if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-821"]="Dombey sat in the corner of the darkened room in the great arm-chair by the bedside, and Son lay tucked up warm in a little basket bedstead, carefully disposed on a low settee immediately in front of the fire and close to it, as if his constitution were analogous to that of a muffin, and it was essential to toast him brown while he was very new.\n\nDombey was about eight-and-forty years of age. Son about eight-and-forty minutes. Dombey was rather bald, rather red, and though a handsome well-made man, too stern and pompous in appearance, to be prepossessing. Son was very bald, and very red, and though (of course) an undeniably fine infant, somewhat crushed and spotty in his general effect, as yet. On the brow of Dombey, Time and his brother Care had set some marks, as on a tree that was to come down in good time—remorseless twins they are for striding through their human forests, notching as they go—while the countenance of Son was crossed with a thousand little creases, which the same deceitful Time would take delight in smoothing out and wearing away with the flat part of his scythe, as a preparation of the surface for his deeper operations.\n\nDombey, exulting in the long-looked-for event, jingled and jingled the heavy gold watch-chain that depended from below his trim blue coat, whereof the buttons sparkled phosphorescently in the feeble rays of the distant fire. Son, with his little fists curled up and clenched, seemed, in his feeble way, to be squaring at existence for having come upon him so unexpectedly.\n\n“The House will once again, Mrs Dombey,” said Mr Dombey, “be not only in name but in fact Dombey and Son;” and he added, in a tone of luxurious satisfaction, with his eyes half-closed as if he were reading the name in a device of flowers, and inhaling their fragrance at the same time; “Dom-bey and Son!”\n\nThe words had such a softening influence, that he appended a term of endearment to Mrs Dombey’s name (though not without some hesitation, as being a man but little used to that form of address): and said, “Mrs Dombey, my—my dear.”\n\nA transient flush of faint surprise overspread the sick lady’s face as she raised her eyes towards him.\n\n“He will be christened Paul, my—Mrs Dombey—of course.”\n\nShe feebly echoed, “Of course,” or rather expressed it by the motion of her lips, and closed her eyes again.\n\n“His father’s name, Mrs Dombey, and his grandfather’s! I wish his grandfather were alive this day! There is some inconvenience in the necessity of writing Junior,” said Mr Dombey, making a fictitious autograph on his knee; “but it is merely of a private and personal complexion. It doesn’t enter into the correspondence of the House. Its signature remains the same.” And again he said “Dombey and Son,” in exactly the same tone as before.\n\nThose three words conveyed the one idea of Mr Dombey’s life. The earth was made for Dombey and Son to trade in, and the sun and moon were made to give them light. Rivers and seas were formed to float their ships; rainbows gave them promise of fair weather; winds blew for or against their enterprises; stars and planets circled in their orbits, to preserve inviolate a system of which they were the centre. Common abbreviations took new meanings in his eyes, and had sole reference to them. A. D. had no concern with Anno Domini, but stood for anno Dombei—and Son.\n\nHe had risen, as his father had before him, in the course of life and death, from Son to Dombey, and for nearly twenty years had been the sole representative of the Firm. Of those years he had been married, ten—married, as some said, to a lady with no heart to give him; whose happiness was in the past, and who was content to bind her broken spirit to the dutiful and meek endurance of the present. Such idle talk was little likely to reach the ears of Mr Dombey, whom it nearly concerned; and probably no one in the world would have received it with such utter incredulity as he, if it had reached him. Dombey and Son had often dealt in hides, but never in hearts. They left that fancy ware to boys and girls, and boarding-schools and books. Mr Dombey would have reasoned: That a matrimonial alliance with himself must, in the nature of things, be gratifying and honourable to any woman of common sense. That the hope of giving birth to a new partner in such a House, could not fail to awaken a glorious and stirring ambition in the breast of the least ambitious of her sex. That Mrs Dombey had entered on that social contract of matrimony: almost necessarily part of a genteel and wealthy station, even without reference to the perpetuation of family Firms: with her eyes fully open to these advantages. That Mrs Dombey had had daily practical knowledge of his position in society. That Mrs Dombey had always sat at the head of his table, and done the honours of his house in a remarkably lady-like and becoming manner. That Mrs Dombey must have been happy. That she couldn’t help it.\n\nOr, at all events, with one drawback. Yes. That he would have allowed. With only one; but that one certainly involving much. With the drawback of hope deferred. That hope deferred, which, (as the Scripture very correctly tells us, Mr Dombey would have added in a patronising way; for his highest distinct idea even of Scripture, if examined, would have been found to be; that as forming part of a general whole, of which Dombey and Son formed another part, it was therefore to be commended and upheld) maketh the heart sick. They had been married ten years, and until this present day on which Mr Dombey sat jingling and jingling his heavy gold watch-chain in the great arm-chair by the side of the bed, had had no issue.\n\n—To speak of; none worth mentioning. There had been a girl some six years before, and the child, who had stolen into the chamber unobserved, was now crouching timidly, in a corner whence she could see her mother’s face. But what was a girl to Dombey and Son! In the capital of the House’s name and dignity, such a child was merely a piece of base coin that couldn’t be invested—a bad Boy—nothing more.\n\nMr Dombey’s cup of satisfaction was so full at this moment, however, that he felt he could afford a drop or two of its contents, even to sprinkle on the dust in the by-path of his little daughter.\n\nSo he said, “Florence, you may go and look at your pretty brother, if you like, I daresay. Don’t touch him!”\n\nThe child glanced keenly at the blue coat and stiff white cravat, which, with a pair of creaking boots and a very loud ticking watch, embodied her idea of a father; but her eyes returned to her mother’s face immediately, and she neither moved nor answered.\n\n“Her insensibility is as proof against a brother as against every thing else,” said Mr Dombey to himself He seemed so confirmed in a previous opinion by the discovery, as to be quite glad of it.”\n\nNext moment, the lady had opened her eyes and seen the child; and the child had run towards her; and, standing on tiptoe, the better to hide her face in her embrace, had clung about her with a desperate affection very much at variance with her years.\n\n“Oh Lord bless me!” said Mr Dombey, rising testily. “A very ill-advised and feverish proceeding this, I am sure. Please to ring there for Miss Florence’s nurse. Really the person should be more care-”\n\n“Wait! I—had better ask Doctor Peps if he’ll have the goodness to step upstairs again perhaps. I’ll go down. I’ll go down. I needn’t beg you,” he added, pausing for a moment at the settee before the fire, “to take particular care of this young gentleman, Mrs ——”\n\n“Blockitt, Sir?” suggested the nurse, a simpering piece of faded gentility, who did not presume to state her name as a fact, but merely offered it as a mild suggestion.\n\n“Of this young gentleman, Mrs Blockitt.”\n\n“No, Sir, indeed. I remember when Miss Florence was born—”\n\n“Ay, ay, ay,” said Mr Dombey, bending over the basket bedstead, and slightly bending his brows at the same time. “Miss Florence was all very well, but this is another matter. This young gentleman has to accomplish a destiny. A destiny, little fellow!” As he thus apostrophised the infant he raised one of his hands to his lips, and kissed it; then, seeming to fear that the action involved some compromise of his dignity, went, awkwardly enough, away.\n\nDoctor Parker Peps, one of the Court Physicians, and a man of immense reputation for assisting at the increase of great families, was walking up and down the drawing-room with his hands behind him, to the unspeakable admiration of the family Surgeon, who had regularly puffed the case for the last six weeks, among all his patients, friends, and acquaintances, as one to which he was in hourly expectation day and night of being summoned, in conjunction with Doctor Parker Pep.\n\n“Well, Sir,” said Doctor Parker Peps in a round, deep, sonorous voice, muffled for the occasion, like the knocker; “do you find that your dear lady is at all roused by your visit?”\n\n“Stimulated as it were?” said the family practitioner faintly: bowing at the same time to the Doctor, as much as to say, “Excuse my putting in a word, but this is a valuable connexion.”\n\nMr Dombey was quite discomfited by the question. He had thought so little of the patient, that he was not in a condition to answer it. He said that it would be a satisfaction to him, if Doctor Parker Peps would walk upstairs again.\n\n“Good! We must not disguise from you, Sir,” said Doctor Parker Peps, “that there is a want of power in Her Grace the Duchess—I beg your pardon; I confound names; I should say, in your amiable lady. That there is a certain degree of languor, and a general absence of elasticity, which we would rather—not—”\n\n“See,” interposed the family practitioner with another inclination of the head.\n\n“Quite so,” said Doctor Parker Peps, “which we would rather not see. It would appear that the system of Lady Cankaby—excuse me: I should say of Mrs Dombey: I confuse the names of cases—”\n\n“So very numerous,” murmured the family practitioner—“can’t be expected I’m sure—quite wonderful if otherwise—Doctor Parker Peps’s West-End practice—”\n\n“Thank you,” said the Doctor, “quite so. It would appear, I was observing, that the system of our patient has sustained a shock, from which it can only hope to rally by a great and strong—”\n\n“And vigorous,” murmured the family practitioner.\n\n“Quite so,” assented the Doctor—“and vigorous effort. Mr Pilkins here, who from his position of medical adviser in this family—no one better qualified to fill that position, I am sure.”\n\n“Oh!” murmured the family practitioner. “‘Praise from Sir Hubert Stanley!’”\n\n“You are good enough,” returned Doctor Parker Peps, “to say so. Mr Pilkins who, from his position, is best acquainted with the patient’s constitution in its normal state (an acquaintance very valuable to us in forming our opinions in these occasions), is of opinion, with me, that Nature must be called upon to make a vigorous effort in this instance; and that if our interesting friend the Countess of Dombey—I beg your pardon; Mrs Dombey—should not be—”\n\n“Able,” said the family practitioner.\n\n“To make,” said Doctor Parker Peps.\n\n“That effort,” said the family practitioner.\n\n“Successfully,” said they both together.\n\n“Then,” added Doctor Parker Peps, alone and very gravely, “a crisis might arise, which we should both sincerely deplore.”\n\nWith that, they stood for a few seconds looking at the ground. Then, on the motion—made in dumb show—of Doctor Parker Peps, they went upstairs; the family practitioner opening the room door for that distinguished professional, and following him out, with most obsequious politeness.\n\nTo record of Mr Dombey that he was not in his way affected by this intelligence, would be to do him an injustice. He was not a man of whom it could properly be said that he was ever startled, or shocked; but he certainly had a sense within him, that if his wife should sicken and decay, he would be very sorry, and that he would find a something gone from among his plate and furniture, and other household possessions, which was well worth the having, and could not be lost without sincere regret. Though it would be a cool, business-like, gentlemanly, self-possessed regret, no doubt.\n\nHis meditations on the subject were soon interrupted, first by the rustling of garments on the staircase, and then by the sudden whisking into the room of a lady rather past the middle age than otherwise but dressed in a very juvenile manner, particularly as to the tightness of her bodice, who, running up to him with a kind of screw in her face and carriage, expressive of suppressed emotion, flung her arms around his neck, and said, in a choking voice,\n\n“My dear Paul! He’s quite a Dombey!”\n\n“Well, well!” returned her brother—for Mr Dombey was her brother—“I think he is like the family. Don’t agitate yourself, Louisa.”\n\n“It’s very foolish of me,” said Louisa, sitting down, and taking out her pocket-handkerchief, “but he’s—he’s such a perfect Dombey!”\n\nMr Dombey coughed.\n\n“It’s so extraordinary,” said Louisa; smiling through her tears, which indeed were not overpowering, “as to be perfectly ridiculous. So completely our family. I never saw anything like it in my life!”\n\n“But what is this about Fanny, herself?” said Mr Dombey. “How is Fanny?”\n\n“My dear Paul,” returned Louisa, “it’s nothing whatever. Take my word, it’s nothing whatever. There is exhaustion, certainly, but nothing like what I underwent myself, either with George or Frederick. An effort is necessary. That’s all. If dear Fanny were a Dombey!—But I daresay she’ll make it; I have no doubt she’ll make it. Knowing it to be required of her, as a duty, of course she’ll make it. My dear Paul, it’s very weak and silly of me, I know, to be so trembly and shaky from head to foot; but I am so very queer that I must ask you for a glass of wine and a morsel of that cake.”\n\nMr Dombey promptly supplied her with these refreshments from a tray on the table.\n\n“I shall not drink my love to you, Paul,” said Louisa: “I shall drink to the little Dombey. Good gracious me!—it’s the most astonishing thing I ever knew in all my days, he’s such a perfect Dombey.”\n\nQuenching this expression of opinion in a short hysterical laugh which terminated in tears, Louisa cast up her eyes, and emptied her glass.\n\n“I know it’s very weak and silly of me,” she repeated, “to be so trembly and shaky from head to foot, and to allow my feelings so completely to get the better of me, but I cannot help it. I thought I should have fallen out of the staircase window as I came down from seeing dear Fanny, and that tiddy ickle sing.” These last words originated in a sudden vivid reminiscence of the baby.\n\nThey were succeeded by a gentle tap at the door.\n\n“Mrs Chick,” said a very bland female voice outside, “how are you now, my dear friend?”\n\n“My dear Paul,” said Louisa in a low voice, as she rose from her seat, “it’s Miss Tox. The kindest creature! I never could have got here without her! Miss Tox, my brother Mr Dombey. Paul, my dear, my very particular friend Miss Tox.”\n\nThe lady thus specially presented, was a long lean figure, wearing such a faded air that she seemed not to have been made in what linen-drapers call “fast colours” originally, and to have, by little and little, washed out. But for this she might have been described as the very pink of general propitiation and politeness. From a long habit of listening admiringly to everything that was said in her presence, and looking at the speakers as if she were mentally engaged in taking off impressions of their images upon her soul, never to part with the same but with life, her head had quite settled on one side. Her hands had contracted a spasmodic habit of raising themselves of their own accord as in involuntary admiration. Her eyes were liable to a similar affection. She had the softest voice that ever was heard; and her nose, stupendously aquiline, had a little knob in the very centre or key-stone of the bridge, whence it tended downwards towards her face, as in an invincible determination never to turn up at anything.\n\nMiss Tox’s dress, though perfectly genteel and good, had a certain character of angularity and scantiness. She was accustomed to wear odd weedy little flowers in her bonnets and caps. Strange grasses were sometimes perceived in her hair; and it was observed by the curious, of all her collars, frills, tuckers, wristbands, and other gossamer articles—indeed of everything she wore which had two ends to it intended to unite—that the two ends were never on good terms, and wouldn’t quite meet without a struggle. She had furry articles for winter wear, as tippets, boas, and muffs, which stood up on end in rampant manner, and were not at all sleek. She was much given to the carrying about of small bags with snaps to them, that went off like little pistols when they were shut up; and when full-dressed, she wore round her neck the barrenest of lockets, representing a fishy old eye, with no approach to speculation in it. These and other appearances of a similar nature, had served to propagate the opinion, that Miss Tox was a lady of what is called a limited independence, which she turned to the best account. Possibly her mincing gait encouraged the belief, and suggested that her clipping a step of ordinary compass into two or three, originated in her habit of making the most of everything.\n\n“I am sure,” said Miss Tox, with a prodigious curtsey, “that to have the honour of being presented to Mr Dombey is a distinction which I have long sought, but very little expected at the present moment. My dear Mrs Chick—may I say Louisa!”\n\nMrs Chick took Miss Tox’s hand in hers, rested the foot of her wine-glass upon it, repressed a tear, and said in a low voice, “God bless you!”\n\n“My dear Louisa then,” said Miss Tox, “my sweet friend, how are you now?”\n\n“Better,” Mrs Chick returned. “Take some wine. You have been almost as anxious as I have been, and must want it, I am sure.”\n\nMr Dombey of course officiated, and also refilled his sister’s glass, which she (looking another way, and unconscious of his intention) held straight and steady the while, and then regarded with great astonishment, saying, “My dear Paul, what have you been doing!”\n\n“Miss Tox, Paul,” pursued Mrs Chick, still retaining her hand, “knowing how much I have been interested in the anticipation of the event of today, and how trembly and shaky I have been from head to foot in expectation of it, has been working at a little gift for Fanny, which I promised to present. Miss Tox is ingenuity itself.”\n\n“My dear Louisa,” said Miss Tox. “Don’t say so.”\n\n“It is only a pincushion for the toilette table, Paul,” resumed his sister; “one of those trifles which are insignificant to your sex in general, as it’s very natural they should be—we have no business to expect they should be otherwise—but to which we attach some interest.”\n\n“Miss Tox is very good,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“And I do say, and will say, and must say,” pursued his sister, pressing the foot of the wine-glass on Miss Tox’s hand, at each of the three clauses, “that Miss Tox has very prettily adapted the sentiment to the occasion. I call ‘Welcome little Dombey’ Poetry, myself!”\n\n“Is that the device?” inquired her brother.\n\n“That is the device,” returned Louisa.\n\n“But do me the justice to remember, my dear Louisa,” said Miss Tox in a tone of low and earnest entreaty, “that nothing but the—I have some difficulty in expressing myself—the dubiousness of the result would have induced me to take so great a liberty: ‘Welcome, Master Dombey,’ would have been much more congenial to my feelings, as I am sure you know. But the uncertainty attendant on angelic strangers, will, I hope, excuse what must otherwise appear an unwarrantable familiarity.” Miss Tox made a graceful bend as she spoke, in favour of Mr Dombey, which that gentleman graciously acknowledged. Even the sort of recognition of Dombey and Son, conveyed in the foregoing conversation, was so palatable to him, that his sister, Mrs Chick—though he affected to consider her a weak good-natured person—had perhaps more influence over him than anybody else.\n\n“My dear Paul,” that lady broke out afresh, after silently contemplating his features for a few moments, “I don’t know whether to laugh or cry when I look at you, I declare, you do so remind me of that dear baby upstairs.”\n\n“Well!” said Mrs Chick, with a sweet smile, “after this, I forgive Fanny everything!”\n\nIt was a declaration in a Christian spirit, and Mrs Chick felt that it did her good. Not that she had anything particular to forgive in her sister-in-law, nor indeed anything at all, except her having married her brother—in itself a species of audacity—and her having, in the course of events, given birth to a girl instead of a boy: which, as Mrs Chick had frequently observed, was not quite what she had expected of her, and was not a pleasant return for all the attention and distinction she had met with.\n\nMr Dombey being hastily summoned out of the room at this moment, the two ladies were left alone together. Miss Tox immediately became spasmodic.\n\n“I knew you would admire my brother. I told you so beforehand, my dear,” said Louisa. Miss Tox’s hands and eyes expressed how much. “And as to his property, my dear!”\n\n“Ah!” said Miss Tox, with deep feeling.\n\n“Im-mense!”\n\n“But his deportment, my dear Louisa!” said Miss Tox. “His presence! His dignity! No portrait that I have ever seen of anyone has been half so replete with those qualities. Something so stately, you know: so uncompromising: so very wide across the chest: so upright! A pecuniary Duke of York, my love, and nothing short of it!” said Miss Tox. “That’s what I should designate him.”\n\n“Why, my dear Paul!” exclaimed his sister, as he returned, “you look quite pale! There’s nothing the matter?”\n\n“I am sorry to say, Louisa, that they tell me that Fanny—”\n\n“Now, my dear Paul,” returned his sister rising, “don’t believe it. Do not allow yourself to receive a turn unnecessarily. Remember of what importance you are to society, and do not allow yourself to be worried by what is so very inconsiderately told you by people who ought to know better. Really I’m surprised at them.”\n\n“I hope I know, Louisa,” said Mr Dombey, stiffly, “how to bear myself before the world.”\n\n“Nobody better, my dear Paul. Nobody half so well. They would be ignorant and base indeed who doubted it.”\n\n“Ignorant and base indeed!” echoed Miss Tox softly.\n\n“But,” pursued Louisa, “if you have any reliance on my experience, Paul, you may rest assured that there is nothing wanting but an effort on Fanny’s part. And that effort,” she continued, taking off her bonnet, and adjusting her cap and gloves, in a business-like manner, “she must be encouraged, and really, if necessary, urged to make. Now, my dear Paul, come upstairs with me.”\n\nMr Dombey, who, besides being generally influenced by his sister for the reason already mentioned, had really faith in her as an experienced and bustling matron, acquiesced; and followed her, at once, to the sick chamber.\n\nThe lady lay upon her bed as he had left her, clasping her little daughter to her breast. The child clung close about her, with the same intensity as before, and never raised her head, or moved her soft cheek from her mother’s face, or looked on those who stood around, or spoke, or moved, or shed a tear.\n\n“Restless without the little girl,” the Doctor whispered Mr Dombey. “We found it best to have her in again.”\n\n“Can nothing be done?” asked Mr Dombey.\n\nThe Doctor shook his head. “We can do no more.”\n\nThe windows stood open, and the twilight was gathering without.\n\nThe scent of the restoratives that had been tried was pungent in the room, but had no fragrance in the dull and languid air the lady breathed.\n\nThere was such a solemn stillness round the bed; and the two medical attendants seemed to look on the impassive form with so much compassion and so little hope, that Mrs Chick was for the moment diverted from her purpose. But presently summoning courage, and what she called presence of mind, she sat down by the bedside, and said in the low precise tone of one who endeavours to awaken a sleeper:\n\n“Fanny! Fanny!”\n\nThere was no sound in answer but the loud ticking of Mr Dombey’s watch and Doctor Parker Peps’s watch, which seemed in the silence to be running a race.\n\n“Fanny, my dear,” said Mrs Chick, with assumed lightness, “here’s Mr Dombey come to see you. Won’t you speak to him? They want to lay your little boy—the baby, Fanny, you know; you have hardly seen him yet, I think—in bed; but they can’t till you rouse yourself a little. Don’t you think it’s time you roused yourself a little? Eh?”\n\nShe bent her ear to the bed, and listened: at the same time looking round at the bystanders, and holding up her finger.\n\n“Eh?” she repeated, “what was it you said, Fanny? I didn’t hear you.”\n\nNo word or sound in answer. Mr Dombey’s watch and Dr Parker Peps’s watch seemed to be racing faster.\n\n“Now, really, Fanny my dear,” said the sister-in-law, altering her position, and speaking less confidently, and more earnestly, in spite of herself, “I shall have to be quite cross with you, if you don’t rouse yourself. It’s necessary for you to make an effort, and perhaps a very great and painful effort which you are not disposed to make; but this is a world of effort you know, Fanny, and we must never yield, when so much depends upon us. Come! Try! I must really scold you if you don’t!”\n\nThe race in the ensuing pause was fierce and furious. The watches seemed to jostle, and to trip each other up.\n\n“Fanny!” said Louisa, glancing round, with a gathering alarm. “Only look at me. Only open your eyes to show me that you hear and understand me; will you? Good Heaven, gentlemen, what is to be done!”\n\nThe two medical attendants exchanged a look across the bed; and the Physician, stooping down, whispered in the child’s ear. Not having understood the purport of his whisper, the little creature turned her perfectly colourless face and deep dark eyes towards him; but without loosening her hold in the least.\n\nThe whisper was repeated.\n\n“Mama!” said the child.\n\nThe little voice, familiar and dearly loved, awakened some show of consciousness, even at that ebb. For a moment, the closed eye lids trembled, and the nostril quivered, and the faintest shadow of a smile was seen.\n\n“Mama!” cried the child sobbing aloud. “Oh dear Mama! oh dear Mama!”\n\nThe Doctor gently brushed the scattered ringlets of the child, aside from the face and mouth of the mother. Alas how calm they lay there; how little breath there was to stir them!\n\nThus, clinging fast to that slight spar within her arms, the mother drifted out upon the dark and unknown sea that rolls round all the world.\n\nCHAPTER II. In which Timely Provision is made for an Emergency that will sometimes arise in the best-regulated Families.\n\nI shall never cease to congratulate myself,” said Mrs Chick,” on having said, when I little thought what was in store for us,—really as if I was inspired by something,—that I forgave poor dear Fanny everything. Whatever happens, that must always be a comfort to me!”\n\nMrs Chick made this impressive observation in the drawing-room, after having descended thither from the inspection of the mantua-makers upstairs, who were busy on the family mourning. She delivered it for the behoof of Mr Chick, who was a stout bald gentleman, with a very large face, and his hands continually in his pockets, and who had a tendency in his nature to whistle and hum tunes, which, sensible of the indecorum of such sounds in a house of grief, he was at some pains to repress at present.\n\n“Don’t you over-exert yourself, Loo,” said Mr Chick, “or you’ll be laid up with spasms, I see. Right tol loor rul! Bless my soul, I forgot! We’re here one day and gone the next!”\n\nMrs Chick contented herself with a glance of reproof, and then proceeded with the thread of her discourse.\n\n“I am sure,” she said, “I hope this heart-rending occurrence will be a warning to all of us, to accustom ourselves to rouse ourselves, and to make efforts in time where they’re required of us. There’s a moral in everything, if we would only avail ourselves of it. It will be our own faults if we lose sight of this one.”\n\nMr Chick invaded the grave silence which ensued on this remark with the singularly inappropriate air of “A cobbler there was;” and checking himself, in some confusion, observed, that it was undoubtedly our own faults if we didn’t improve such melancholy occasions as the present.\n\n“Which might be better improved, I should think, Mr C.,” retorted his helpmate, after a short pause, “than by the introduction, either of the college hornpipe, or the equally unmeaning and unfeeling remark of rump-te-iddity, bow-wow-wow!”—which Mr Chick had indeed indulged in, under his breath, and which Mrs Chick repeated in a tone of withering scorn.\n\n“Merely habit, my dear,” pleaded Mr Chick.\n\n“Nonsense! Habit!” returned his wife. “If you’re a rational being, don’t make such ridiculous excuses. Habit! If I was to get a habit (as you call it) of walking on the ceiling, like the flies, I should hear enough of it, I daresay.”\n\nIt appeared so probable that such a habit might be attended with some degree of notoriety, that Mr Chick didn’t venture to dispute the position.\n\n“Bow-wow-wow!” repeated Mrs Chick with an emphasis of blighting contempt on the last syllable. “More like a professional singer with the hydrophobia, than a man in your station of life!”\n\n“How’s the Baby, Loo?” asked Mr Chick: to change the subject.\n\n“What Baby do you mean?” answered Mrs Chick.\n\n“The poor bereaved little baby,” said Mr Chick. “I don’t know of any other, my dear.”\n\n“You don’t know of any other,” retorted Mrs Chick. “More shame for you, I was going to say.”\n\nMr Chick looked astonished.\n\n“I am sure the morning I have had, with that dining-room downstairs, one mass of babies, no one in their senses would believe.”\n\n“One mass of babies!” repeated Mr Chick, staring with an alarmed expression about him.\n\n“It would have occurred to most men,” said Mrs Chick, “that poor dear Fanny being no more,—those words of mine will always be a balm and comfort to me,” here she dried her eyes; “it becomes necessary to provide a Nurse.”\n\n“Oh! Ah!” said Mr Chick. “Toor-ru!—such is life, I mean. I hope you are suited, my dear.”\n\n“Indeed I am not,” said Mrs Chick; “nor likely to be, so far as I can see, and in the meantime the poor child seems likely to be starved to death. Paul is so very particular—naturally so, of course, having set his whole heart on this one boy—and there are so many objections to everybody that offers, that I don’t see, myself, the least chance of an arrangement. Meanwhile, of course, the child is—”\n\n“Going to the Devil,” said Mr Chick, thoughtfully, “to be sure.”\n\nAdmonished, however, that he had committed himself, by the indignation expressed in Mrs Chick’s countenance at the idea of a Dombey going there; and thinking to atone for his misconduct by a bright suggestion, he added:\n\n“Couldn’t something temporary be done with a teapot?”\n\nIf he had meant to bring the subject prematurely to a close, he could not have done it more effectually. After looking at him for some moments in silent resignation, Mrs Chick said she trusted he hadn’t said it in aggravation, because that would do very little honour to his heart. She trusted he hadn’t said it seriously, because that would do very little honour to his head. As in any case, he couldn’t, however sanguine his disposition, hope to offer a remark that would be a greater outrage on human nature in general, we would beg to leave the discussion at that point.\n\nMrs Chick then walked majestically to the window and peeped through the blind, attracted by the sound of wheels. Mr Chick, finding that his destiny was, for the time, against him, said no more, and walked off. But it was not always thus with Mr Chick. He was often in the ascendant himself, and at those times punished Louisa roundly. In their matrimonial bickerings they were, upon the whole, a well-matched, fairly-balanced, give-and-take couple. It would have been, generally speaking, very difficult to have betted on the winner. Often when Mr Chick seemed beaten, he would suddenly make a start, turn the tables, clatter them about the ears of Mrs Chick, and carry all before him. Being liable himself to similar unlooked for checks from Mrs Chick, their little contests usually possessed a character of uncertainty that was very animating.\n\nMiss Tox had arrived on the wheels just now alluded to, and came running into the room in a breathless condition.\n\n“My dear Louisa,” said Miss Tox, “is the vacancy still unsupplied?”\n\n“You good soul, yes,” said Mrs Chick.\n\n“Then, my dear Louisa,” returned Miss Tox, “I hope and believe—but in one moment, my dear, I’ll introduce the party.”\n\nRunning downstairs again as fast as she had run up, Miss Tox got the party out of the hackney-coach, and soon returned with it under convoy.\n\nIt then appeared that she had used the word, not in its legal or business acceptation, when it merely expresses an individual, but as a noun of multitude, or signifying many: for Miss Tox escorted a plump rosy-cheeked wholesome apple-faced young woman, with an infant in her arms; a younger woman not so plump, but apple-faced also, who led a plump and apple-faced child in each hand; another plump and also apple-faced boy who walked by himself; and finally, a plump and apple-faced man, who carried in his arms another plump and apple-faced boy, whom he stood down on the floor, and admonished, in a husky whisper, to “kitch hold of his brother Johnny.”\n\n“My dear Louisa,” said Miss Tox, “knowing your great anxiety, and wishing to relieve it, I posted off myself to the Queen Charlotte’s Royal Married Females,” which you had forgot, and put the question, Was there anybody there that they thought would suit? No, they said there was not. When they gave me that answer, I do assure you, my dear, I was almost driven to despair on your account. But it did so happen, that one of the Royal Married Females, hearing the inquiry, reminded the matron of another who had gone to her own home, and who, she said, would in all likelihood be most satisfactory. The moment I heard this, and had it corroborated by the matron—excellent references and unimpeachable character—I got the address, my dear, and posted off again.”\n\n“Like the dear good Tox, you are!” said Louisa.\n\n“Not at all,” returned Miss Tox. “Don’t say so. Arriving at the house (the cleanest place, my dear! You might eat your dinner off the floor), I found the whole family sitting at table; and feeling that no account of them could be half so comfortable to you and Mr Dombey as the sight of them all together, I brought them all away. This gentleman,” said Miss Tox, pointing out the apple-faced man, “is the father. Will you have the goodness to come a little forward, Sir?”\n\nThe apple-faced man having sheepishly complied with this request, stood chuckling and grinning in a front row.\n\n“This is his wife, of course,” said Miss Tox, singling out the young woman with the baby. “How do you do, Polly?”\n\n“I’m pretty well, I thank you, Ma’am,” said Polly.\n\nBy way of bringing her out dexterously, Miss Tox had made the inquiry as in condescension to an old acquaintance whom she hadn’t seen for a fortnight or so.\n\n“I’m glad to hear it,” said Miss Tox. “The other young woman is her unmarried sister who lives with them, and would take care of her children. Her name’s Jemima. How do you do, Jemima?”\n\n“I’m pretty well, I thank you, Ma’am,” returned Jemima.\n\n“I’m very glad indeed to hear it,” said Miss Tox. “I hope you’ll keep so. Five children. Youngest six weeks. The fine little boy with the blister on his nose is the eldest. The blister, I believe,” said Miss Tox, looking round upon the family, “is not constitutional, but accidental?”\n\nThe apple-faced man was understood to growl, “Flat iron.”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Sir,” said Miss Tox, “did you—”\n\n“Flat iron,” he repeated.\n\n“Oh yes,” said Miss Tox. “Yes! quite true. I forgot. The little creature, in his mother’s absence, smelt a warm flat iron. You’re quite right, Sir. You were going to have the goodness to inform me, when we arrived at the door that you were by trade a—”\n\n“Stoker,” said the man.\n\n“A choker!” said Miss Tox, quite aghast.\n\n“Stoker,” said the man. “Steam ingine.”\n\n“Oh-h! Yes!” returned Miss Tox, looking thoughtfully at him, and seeming still to have but a very imperfect understanding of his meaning.\n\n“And how do you like it, Sir?”\n\n“Which, Mum?” said the man.\n“That,” replied Miss Tox. “Your trade.”\n\n“Oh! Pretty well, Mum. The ashes sometimes gets in here;” touching his chest: “and makes a man speak gruff, as at the present time. But it is ashes, Mum, not crustiness.”\n\nMiss Tox seemed to be so little enlightened by this reply, as to find a difficulty in pursuing the subject. But Mrs Chick relieved her, by entering into a close private examination of Polly, her children, her marriage certificate, testimonials, and so forth. Polly coming out unscathed from this ordeal, Mrs Chick withdrew with her report to her brother’s room, and as an emphatic comment on it, and corroboration of it, carried the two rosiest little Toodles with her. Toodle being the family name of the apple-faced family.\n\nMr Dombey had remained in his own apartment since the death of his wife, absorbed in visions of the youth, education, and destination of his baby son. Something lay at the bottom of his cool heart, colder and heavier than its ordinary load; but it was more a sense of the child’s loss than his own, awakening within him an almost angry sorrow. That the life and progress on which he built such hopes, should be endangered in the outset by so mean a want; that Dombey and Son should be tottering for a nurse, was a sore humiliation. And yet in his pride and jealousy, he viewed with so much bitterness the thought of being dependent for the very first step towards the accomplishment of his soul’s desire, on a hired serving-woman who would be to the child, for the time, all that even his alliance could have made his own wife, that in every new rejection of a candidate he felt a secret pleasure. The time had now come, however, when he could no longer be divided between these two sets of feelings. The less so, as there seemed to be no flaw in the title of Polly Toodle after his sister had set it forth, with many commendations on the indefatigable friendship of Miss Tox.\n\n“These children look healthy,” said Mr Dombey. “But my God, to think of their some day claiming a sort of relationship to Paul!”\n\n“But what relationship is there!” Louisa began—\n\n“Is there!” echoed Mr Dombey, who had not intended his sister to participate in the thought he had unconsciously expressed. “Is there, did you say, Louisa!”\n\n“Can there be, I mean—”\n\n“Why none,” said Mr Dombey, sternly. “The whole world knows that, I presume. Grief has not made me idiotic, Louisa. Take them away, Louisa! Let me see this woman and her husband.”\n\nMrs Chick bore off the tender pair of Toodles, and presently returned with that tougher couple whose presence her brother had commanded.\n\n“My good woman,” said Mr Dombey, turning round in his easy chair, as one piece, and not as a man with limbs and joints, “I understand you are poor, and wish to earn money by nursing the little boy, my son, who has been so prematurely deprived of what can never be replaced. I have no objection to your adding to the comforts of your family by that means. So far as I can tell, you seem to be a deserving object. But I must impose one or two conditions on you, before you enter my house in that capacity. While you are here, I must stipulate that you are always known as—say as Richards—an ordinary name, and convenient. Have you any objection to be known as Richards? You had better consult your husband.”\n\n“Well?” said Mr Dombey, after a pretty long pause. “What does your husband say to your being called Richards?”\n\nAs the husband did nothing but chuckle and grin, and continually draw his right hand across his mouth, moistening the palm, Mrs Toodle, after nudging him twice or thrice in vain, dropped a curtsey and replied “that perhaps if she was to be called out of her name, it would be considered in the wages.”\n\n“Oh, of course,” said Mr Dombey. “I desire to make it a question of wages, altogether. Now, Richards, if you nurse my bereaved child, I wish you to remember this always. You will receive a liberal stipend in return for the discharge of certain duties, in the performance of which, I wish you to see as little of your family as possible. When those duties cease to be required and rendered, and the stipend ceases to be paid, there is an end of all relations between us. Do you understand me?”\n\nMrs Toodle seemed doubtful about it; and as to Toodle himself, he had evidently no doubt whatever, that he was all abroad.\n\n“You have children of your own,” said Mr Dombey. “It is not at all in this bargain that you need become attached to my child, or that my child need become attached to you. I don’t expect or desire anything of the kind. Quite the reverse. When you go away from here, you will have concluded what is a mere matter of bargain and sale, hiring and letting: and will stay away. The child will cease to remember you; and you will cease, if you please, to remember the child.”\n\nMrs Toodle, with a little more colour in her cheeks than she had had before, said “she hoped she knew her place.”\n\n“I hope you do, Richards,” said Mr Dombey. “I have no doubt you know it very well. Indeed it is so plain and obvious that it could hardly be otherwise. Louisa, my dear, arrange with Richards about money, and let her have it when and how she pleases. Mr what’s-your name, a word with you, if you please!”\n\nThus arrested on the threshold as he was following his wife out of the room, Toodle returned and confronted Mr Dombey alone. He was a strong, loose, round-shouldered, shuffling, shaggy fellow, on whom his clothes sat negligently: with a good deal of hair and whisker, deepened in its natural tint, perhaps by smoke and coal-dust: hard knotty hands: and a square forehead, as coarse in grain as the bark of an oak. A thorough contrast in all respects, to Mr Dombey, who was one of those close-shaved close-cut moneyed gentlemen who are glossy and crisp like new bank-notes, and who seem to be artificially braced and tightened as by the stimulating action of golden showerbaths.\n\n“You have a son, I believe?” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Four on ’em, Sir. Four hims and a her. All alive!”\n\n“Why, it’s as much as you can afford to keep them!” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“I couldn’t hardly afford but one thing in the world less, Sir.”\n\n“What is that?”\n\n“To lose ’em, Sir.”\n\n“Can you read?” asked Mr Dombey.\n\n“Why, not partickk’ller, Sir.”\n\n“Write?”\n\n“With chalk, Sir?”\n\n“With anything?”\n\n“I could make shift to chalk a little bit, I think, if I was put to it,” said Toodle after some reflection.\n\n“And yet,” said Mr Dombey, “you are two or three and thirty, I suppose?”\n\n“Thereabouts, I suppose, Sir,” answered Toodle, after more reflection\n\n“Then why don’t you learn?” asked Mr Dombey.\n\n“So I’m a going to, Sir. One of my little boys is a going to learn me, when he’s old enough, and been to school himself.”\n\n“Well,” said Mr Dombey, after looking at him attentively, and with no great favour, as he stood gazing round the room (principally round the ceiling) and still drawing his hand across and across his mouth. “You heard what I said to your wife just now?”\n\n“Polly heerd it,” said Toodle, jerking his hat over his shoulder in the direction of the door, with an air of perfect confidence in his better half. “It’s all right.”\n\n“But I ask you if you heard it. You did, I suppose, and understood it?” pursued Mr Dombey.\n\n“I heerd it,” said Toodle, “but I don’t know as I understood it rightly Sir, “account of being no scholar, and the words being—ask your pardon—rayther high. But Polly heerd it. It’s all right.”\n\n“As you appear to leave everything to her,” said Mr Dombey, frustrated in his intention of impressing his views still more distinctly on the husband, as the stronger character, “I suppose it is of no use my saying anything to you.”\n\n“Not a bit,” said Toodle. “Polly heerd it. She’s awake, Sir.”\n\n“I won’t detain you any longer then,” returned Mr Dombey, disappointed. “Where have you worked all your life?”\n\n“Mostly underground, Sir, “till I got married. I come to the level then. I’m a going on one of these here railroads when they comes into full play.”\n\nAs he added in one of his hoarse whispers, “We means to bring up little Biler to that line,” Mr Dombey inquired haughtily who little Biler was.\n\n“The eldest on ’em, Sir,” said Toodle, with a smile. “It ain’t a common name. Sermuchser that when he was took to church the gen’lm’n said, it wamm’t a chris’en one, and he couldn’t give it. But we always calls him Biler just the same. For we don’t mean no harm. Not we.”\n\n“Do you mean to say, Man,” inquired Mr Dombey; looking at him with marked displeasure, “that you have called a child after a boiler?”\n\n“No, no, Sir,” returned Toodle, with a tender consideration for his mistake. “I should hope not! No, Sir. Arter a BILER Sir. The Steamingine was a’most as good as a godfather to him, and so we called him Biler, don’t you see!”\n\nAs the last straw breaks the laden camel’s back, this piece of information crushed the sinking spirits of Mr Dombey. He motioned his child’s foster-father to the door, who departed by no means unwillingly: and then turning the key, paced up and down the room in solitary wretchedness.\n\nIt would be harsh, and perhaps not altogether true, to say of him that he felt these rubs and gratings against his pride more keenly than he had felt his wife’s death: but certainly they impressed that event upon him with new force, and communicated to it added weight and bitterness. It was a rude shock to his sense of property in his child, that these people—the mere dust of the earth, as he thought them—should be necessary to him; and it was natural that in proportion as he felt disturbed by it, he should deplore the occurrence which had made them so. For all his starched, impenetrable dignity and composure, he wiped blinding tears from his eyes as he paced up and down his room; and often said, with an emotion of which he would not, for the world, have had a witness, “Poor little fellow!”\n\nIt may have been characteristic of Mr Dombey’s pride, that he pitied himself through the child. Not poor me. Not poor widower, confiding by constraint in the wife of an ignorant Hind who has been working “mostly underground” all his life, and yet at whose door Death had never knocked, and at whose poor table four sons daily sit—but poor little fellow!\n\nThose words being on his lips, it occurred to him—and it is an instance of the strong attraction with which his hopes and fears and all his thoughts were tending to one centre—that a great temptation was being placed in this woman’s way. Her infant was a boy too. Now, would it be possible for her to change them?\n\nThough he was soon satisfied that he had dismissed the idea as romantic and unlikely—though possible, there was no denying—he could not help pursuing it so far as to entertain within himself a picture of what his condition would be, if he should discover such an imposture when he was grown old. Whether a man so situated would be able to pluck away the result of so many years of usage, confidence, and belief, from the impostor, and endow a stranger with it?\n\nBut it was idle speculating thus. It couldn’t happen. In a moment afterwards he determined that it could, but that such women were constantly observed, and had no opportunity given them for the accomplishment of such a design, even when they were so wicked as to entertain it. In another moment, he was remembering how few such cases seemed to have ever happened. In another moment he was wondering whether they ever happened and were not found out.\n\nAs his unusual emotion subsided, these misgivings gradually melted away, though so much of their shadow remained behind, that he was constant in his resolution to look closely after Richards himself, without appearing to do so. Being now in an easier frame of mind, he regarded the woman’s station as rather an advantageous circumstance than otherwise, by placing, in itself, a broad distance between her and the child, and rendering their separation easy and natural. Thence he passed to the contemplation of the future glories of Dombey and Son, and dismissed the memory of his wife, for the time being, with a tributary sigh or two.\n\nMeanwhile terms were ratified and agreed upon between Mrs Chick and Richards, with the assistance of Miss Tox; and Richards being with much ceremony invested with the Dombey baby, as if it were an Order, resigned her own, with many tears and kisses, to Jemima. Glasses of wine were then produced, to sustain the drooping spirits of the family; and Miss Tox, busying herself in dispensing “tastes” to the younger branches, bred them up to their father’s business with such surprising expedition, that she made chokers of four of them in a quarter of a minute.\n\n“You’ll take a glass yourself, Sir, won’t you?” said Miss Tox, as Toodle appeared.\n\n“Thankee, Mum,” said Toodle, “since you are suppressing.”\n\n“And you’re very glad to leave your dear good wife in such a comfortable home, ain’t you, Sir?” said Miss Tox, nodding and winking at him stealthily.\n\n“No, Mum,” said Toodle. “Here’s wishing of her back agin.”\n\nPolly cried more than ever at this. So Mrs Chick, who had her matronly apprehensions that this indulgence in grief might be prejudicial to the little Dombey (“acid, indeed,” she whispered Miss Tox), hastened to the rescue.\n\n“Your little child will thrive charmingly with your sister Jemima, Richards,” said Mrs Chick; “and you have only to make an effort—this is a world of effort, you know, Richards—to be very happy indeed. You have been already measured for your mourning, haven’t you, Richards?”\n\n“Ye—es, Ma’am,” sobbed Polly.\n\n“And it’ll fit beautifully. I know,” said Mrs Chick, “for the same young person has made me many dresses. The very best materials, too!”\n\n“Lor, you’ll be so smart,” said Miss Tox, “that your husband won’t know you; will you, Sir?”\n\n“I should know her,” said Toodle, gruffly, “anyhows and anywheres.”\n\nToodle was evidently not to be bought over.\n\n“As to living, Richards, you know,” pursued Mrs Chick, “why, the very best of everything will be at your disposal. You will order your little dinner every day; and anything you take a fancy to, I’m sure will be as readily provided as if you were a Lady.”\n\n“Yes to be sure!” said Miss Tox, keeping up the ball with great sympathy. “And as to porter!—quite unlimited, will it not, Louisa?”\n\n“Oh, certainly!” returned Mrs Chick in the same tone. “With a little abstinence, you know, my dear, in point of vegetables.”\n\n“And pickles, perhaps,” suggested Miss Tox.\n\n“With such exceptions,” said Louisa, “she’ll consult her choice entirely, and be under no restraint at all, my love.”\n\n“And then, of course, you know,” said Miss Tox, “however fond she is of her own dear little child—and I’m sure, Louisa, you don’t blame her for being fond of it?”\n\n“Oh no!” cried Mrs Chick, benignantly.\n\n“Still,” resumed Miss Tox, “she naturally must be interested in her young charge, and must consider it a privilege to see a little cherub connected with the superior classes, gradually unfolding itself from day to day at one common fountain—is it not so, Louisa?”\n\n“Most undoubtedly!” said Mrs Chick. “You see, my love, she’s already quite contented and comfortable, and means to say goodbye to her sister Jemima and her little pets, and her good honest husband, with a light heart and a smile; don’t she, my dear?”\n\n“Oh yes!” cried Miss Tox. “To be sure she does!”\n\nNotwithstanding which, however, poor Polly embraced them all round in great distress, and coming to her spouse at last, could not make up her mind to part from him, until he gently disengaged himself, at the close of the following allegorical piece of consolation:\n\n“Polly, old “ooman, whatever you do, my darling, hold up your head and fight low. That’s the only rule as I know on, that’ll carry anyone through life. You always have held up your head and fought low, Polly. Do it now, or Bricks is no longer so. God bless you, Polly! Me and J’mima will do your duty by you; and with relating to your’n, hold up your head and fight low, Polly, and you can’t go wrong!”\n\nFortified by this golden secret, Polly finally ran away to avoid any more particular leave-taking between herself and the children. But the stratagem hardly succeeded as well as it deserved; for the smallest boy but one divining her intent, immediately began swarming upstairs after her—if that word of doubtful etymology be admissible—on his arms and legs; while the eldest (known in the family by the name of Biler, in remembrance of the steam engine) beat a demoniacal tattoo with his boots, expressive of grief; in which he was joined by the rest of the family.\n\nA quantity of oranges and halfpence thrust indiscriminately on each young Toodle, checked the first violence of their regret, and the family were speedily transported to their own home, by means of the hackney-coach kept in waiting for that purpose. The children, under the guardianship of Jemima, blocked up the window, and dropped out oranges and halfpence all the way along. Mr Toodle himself preferred to ride behind among the spikes, as being the mode of conveyance to which he was best accustomed.\n\nCHAPTER III. In which Mr Dombey, as a Man and a Father, is seen at the Head of the Home-Department\n\nThe funeral of the deceased lady having been “performed” to the entire satisfaction of the undertaker, as well as of the neighbourhood at large, which is generally disposed to be captious on such a point, and is prone to take offence at any omissions or short-comings in the ceremonies, the various members of Mr Dombey’s household subsided into their several places in the domestic system. That small world, like the great one out of doors, had the capacity of easily forgetting its dead; and when the cook had said she was a quiet-tempered lady, and the house-keeper had said it was the common lot, and the butler had said who’d have thought it, and the housemaid had said she couldn’t hardly believe it, and the footman had said it seemed exactly like a dream, they had quite worn the subject out, and began to think their mourning was wearing rusty too.\n\nOn Richards, who was established upstairs in a state of honourable captivity, the dawn of her new life seemed to break cold and grey. Mr Dombey’s house was a large one, on the shady side of a tall, dark, dreadfully genteel street in the region between Portland Place and Bryanstone Square. It was a corner house, with great wide areas containing cellars frowned upon by barred windows, and leered at by crooked-eyed doors leading to dustbins. It was a house of dismal state, with a circular back to it, containing a whole suite of drawing-rooms looking upon a gravelled yard, where two gaunt trees, with blackened trunks and branches, rattled rather than rustled, their leaves were so smoked-dried. The summer sun was never on the street, but in the morning about breakfast-time, when it came with the water-carts and the old clothes men, and the people with geraniums, and the umbrella-mender, and the man who trilled the little bell of the Dutch clock as he went along. It was soon gone again to return no more that day; and the bands of music and the straggling Punch’s shows going after it, left it a prey to the most dismal of organs, and white mice; with now and then a porcupine, to vary the entertainments; until the butlers whose families were dining out, began to stand at the house-doors in the twilight, and the lamp-lighter made his nightly failure in attempting to brighten up the street with gas.\n\nIt was as blank a house inside as outside. When the funeral was over, Mr Dombey ordered the furniture to be covered up—perhaps to preserve it for the son with whom his plans were all associated—and the rooms to be ungarnished, saving such as he retained for himself on the ground floor. Accordingly, mysterious shapes were made of tables and chairs, heaped together in the middle of rooms, and covered over with great winding-sheets. Bell-handles, window-blinds, and looking-glasses, being papered up in journals, daily and weekly, obtruded fragmentary accounts of deaths and dreadful murders. Every chandelier or lustre, muffled in holland, looked like a monstrous tear depending from the ceiling’s eye. Odours, as from vaults and damp places, came out of the chimneys. The dead and buried lady was awful in a picture-frame of ghastly bandages. Every gust of wind that rose, brought eddying round the corner from the neighbouring mews, some fragments of the straw that had been strewn before the house when she was ill, mildewed remains of which were still cleaving to the neighbourhood: and these, being always drawn by some invisible attraction to the threshold of the dirty house to let immediately opposite, addressed a dismal eloquence to Mr Dombey’s windows.\n\nThe apartments which Mr Dombey reserved for his own inhabiting, were attainable from the hall, and consisted of a sitting-room; a library, which was in fact a dressing-room, so that the smell of hot-pressed paper, vellum, morocco, and Russia leather, contended in it with the smell of divers pairs of boots; and a kind of conservatory or little glass breakfast-room beyond, commanding a prospect of the trees before mentioned, and, generally speaking, of a few prowling cats. These three rooms opened upon one another. In the morning, when Mr Dombey was at his breakfast in one or other of the two first-mentioned of them, as well as in the afternoon when he came home to dinner, a bell was rung for Richards to repair to this glass chamber, and there walk to and fro with her young charge. From the glimpses she caught of Mr Dombey at these times, sitting in the dark distance, looking out towards the infant from among the dark heavy furniture—the house had been inhabited for years by his father, and in many of its appointments was old-fashioned and grim—she began to entertain ideas of him in his solitary state, as if he were a lone prisoner in a cell, or a strange apparition that was not to be accosted or understood. Mr Dombey came to be, in the course of a few days, invested in his own person, to her simple thinking, with all the mystery and gloom of his house. As she walked up and down the glass room, or sat hushing the baby there—which she very often did for hours together, when the dusk was closing in, too—she would sometimes try to pierce the gloom beyond, and make out how he was looking and what he was doing. Sensible that she was plainly to be seen by him, however, she never dared to pry in that direction but very furtively and for a moment at a time. Consequently she made out nothing, and Mr Dombey in his den remained a very shade.\n\nLittle Paul Dombey’s foster-mother had led this life herself, and had carried little Paul through it for some weeks; and had returned upstairs one day from a melancholy saunter through the dreary rooms of state (she never went out without Mrs Chick, who called on fine mornings, usually accompanied by Miss Tox, to take her and Baby for an airing—or in other words, to march them gravely up and down the pavement, like a walking funeral); when, as she was sitting in her own room, the door was slowly and quietly opened, and a dark-eyed little girl looked in.\n\n“It’s Miss Florence come home from her aunt’s, no doubt,” thought Richards, who had never seen the child before. “Hope I see you well, Miss.”\n\n“Is that my brother?” asked the child, pointing to the Baby.\n\n“Yes, my pretty,” answered Richards. “Come and kiss him.”\n\nBut the child, instead of advancing, looked her earnestly in the face, and said:\n\n“What have you done with my Mama?”\n\n“Lord bless the little creeter!” cried Richards, “what a sad question! I done? Nothing, Miss.”\n\n“What have they done with my Mama?” inquired the child, with exactly the same look and manner.\n\n“I never saw such a melting thing in all my life!” said Richards, who naturally substituted for this child one of her own, inquiring for herself in like circumstances. “Come nearer here, my dear Miss! Don’t be afraid of me.”\n\n“I am not afraid of you,” said the child, drawing nearer. “But I want to know what they have done with my Mama.”\n\nHer heart swelled so as she stood before the woman, looking into her eyes, that she was fain to press her little hand upon her breast and hold it there. Yet there was a purpose in the child that prevented both her slender figure and her searching gaze from faltering.\n\n“My darling,” said Richards, “you wear that pretty black frock in remembrance of your Mama.”\n\n“I can remember my Mama,” returned the child, with tears springing to her eyes, “in any frock.”\n\n“But people put on black, to remember people when they’re gone.”\n\n“Where gone?” asked the child.\n\n“Come and sit down by me,” said Richards, “and I’ll tell you a story.”\n\nWith a quick perception that it was intended to relate to what she had asked, little Florence laid aside the bonnet she had held in her hand until now, and sat down on a stool at the Nurse’s feet, looking up into her face.\n\n“Once upon a time,” said Richards, “there was a lady—a very good lady, and her little daughter dearly loved her.”\n\n“A very good lady and her little daughter dearly loved her,” repeated the child.\n\n“Who, when God thought it right that it should be so, was taken ill and died.”\n\nThe child shuddered.\n\n“Died, never to be seen again by anyone on earth, and was buried in the ground where the trees grow.”\n\n“The cold ground?” said the child, shuddering again.\n\n“No! The warm ground,” returned Polly, seizing her advantage, “where the ugly little seeds turn into beautiful flowers, and into grass, and corn, and I don’t know what all besides. Where good people turn into bright angels, and fly away to Heaven!”\n\nThe child, who had dropped her head, raised it again, and sat looking at her intently.\n\n“So; let me see,” said Polly, not a little flurried between this earnest scrutiny, her desire to comfort the child, her sudden success, and her very slight confidence in her own powers. “So, when this lady died, wherever they took her, or wherever they put her, she went to GOD! and she prayed to Him, this lady did,” said Polly, affecting herself beyond measure; being heartily in earnest, “to teach her little daughter to be sure of that in her heart: and to know that she was happy there and loved her still: and to hope and try—Oh, all her life—to meet her there one day, never, never, never to part any more.”\n\n“It was my Mama!” exclaimed the child, springing up, and clasping her round the neck.\n\n“And the child’s heart,” said Polly, drawing her to her breast: “the little daughter’s heart was so full of the truth of this, that even when she heard it from a strange nurse that couldn’t tell it right, but was a poor mother herself and that was all, she found a comfort in it—didn’t feel so lonely—sobbed and cried upon her bosom—took kindly to the baby lying in her lap—and—there, there, there!” said Polly, smoothing the child’s curls and dropping tears upon them. “There, poor dear!”\n\n“Oh well, Miss Floy! And won’t your Pa be angry neither!” cried a quick voice at the door, proceeding from a short, brown, womanly girl of fourteen, with a little snub nose, and black eyes like jet beads. “When it was “tickerlerly given out that you wasn’t to go and worrit the wet nurse.”\n\n“She don’t worry me,” was the surprised rejoinder of Polly. “I am very fond of children.”\n\n“Oh! but begging your pardon, Mrs Richards, that don’t matter, you know,” returned the black-eyed girl, who was so desperately sharp and biting that she seemed to make one’s eyes water. “I may be very fond of pennywinkles, Mrs Richards, but it don’t follow that I’m to have ’em for tea.”\n\n“Well, it don’t matter,” said Polly.\n\n“Oh, thank’ee, Mrs Richards, don’t it!” returned the sharp girl. “Remembering, however, if you’ll be so good, that Miss Floy’s under my charge, and Master Paul’s under your’n.”\n\n“But still we needn’t quarrel,” said Polly.\n\n“Oh no, Mrs Richards,” rejoined Spitfire. “Not at all, I don’t wish it, we needn’t stand upon that footing, Miss Floy being a permanency, Master Paul a temporary.” Spitfire made use of none but comma pauses; shooting out whatever she had to say in one sentence, and in one breath, if possible.\n\n“Miss Florence has just come home, hasn’t she?” asked Polly.\n\n“Yes, Mrs Richards, just come, and here, Miss Floy, before you’ve been in the house a quarter of an hour, you go a smearing your wet face against the expensive mourning that Mrs Richards is a wearing for your Ma!” With this remonstrance, young Spitfire, whose real name was Susan Nipper, detached the child from her new friend by a wrench—as if she were a tooth. But she seemed to do it, more in the excessively sharp exercise of her official functions, than with any deliberate unkindness.\n\n“She’ll be quite happy, now she has come home again,” said Polly, nodding to her with an encouraging smile upon her wholesome face, “and will be so pleased to see her dear Papa tonight.”\n\n“Lork, Mrs Richards!” cried Miss Nipper, taking up her words with a jerk. “Don’t. See her dear Papa indeed! I should like to see her do it!”\n\n“Won’t she then?” asked Polly.\n\n“Lork, Mrs Richards, no, her Pa’s a deal too wrapped up in somebody else, and before there was a somebody else to be wrapped up in she never was a favourite, girls are thrown away in this house, Mrs Richards, I assure you.”\n\nThe child looked quickly from one nurse to the other, as if she understood and felt what was said.\n\n“You surprise me!” cried Polly. “Hasn’t Mr Dombey seen her since—”\n\n“No,” interrupted Susan Nipper. “Not once since, and he hadn’t hardly set his eyes upon her before that for months and months, and I don’t think he’d have known her for his own child if he had met her in the streets, or would know her for his own child if he was to meet her in the streets to-morrow, Mrs Richards, as to me,” said Spitfire, with a giggle, “I doubt if he’s aweer of my existence.”\n\n“Pretty dear!” said Richards; meaning, not Miss Nipper, but the little Florence.\n\n“Oh! there’s a Tartar within a hundred miles of where we’re now in conversation, I can tell you, Mrs Richards, present company always excepted too,” said Susan Nipper; “wish you good morning, Mrs Richards, now Miss Floy, you come along with me, and don’t go hanging back like a naughty wicked child that judgments is no example to, don’t!”\n\nIn spite of being thus adjured, and in spite also of some hauling on the part of Susan Nipper, tending towards the dislocation of her right shoulder, little Florence broke away, and kissed her new friend, affectionately.\n\n“Oh dear! after it was given out so “tickerlerly, that Mrs Richards wasn’t to be made free with!” exclaimed Susan. “Very well, Miss Floy!”\n\n“God bless the sweet thing!” said Richards, “Good-bye, dear!”\n\n“Good-bye!” returned the child. “God bless you! I shall come to see you again soon, and you’ll come to see me? Susan will let us. Won’t you, Susan?”\n\nSpitfire seemed to be in the main a good-natured little body, although a disciple of that school of trainers of the young idea which holds that childhood, like money, must be shaken and rattled and jostled about a good deal to keep it bright. For, being thus appealed to with some endearing gestures and caresses, she folded her small arms and shook her head, and conveyed a relenting expression into her very-wide-open black eyes.\n\n“It ain’t right of you to ask it, Miss Floy, for you know I can’t refuse you, but Mrs Richards and me will see what can be done, if Mrs Richards likes, I may wish, you see, to take a voyage to Chaney, Mrs Richards, but I mayn’t know how to leave the London Docks.”\n\nRichards assented to the proposition.\n\n“This house ain’t so exactly ringing with merry-making,” said Miss Nipper, “that one need be lonelier than one must be. Your Toxes and your Chickses may draw out my two front double teeth, Mrs Richards, but that’s no reason why I need offer ’em the whole set.”\n\nThis proposition was also assented to by Richards, as an obvious one.\n\n“So I’m agreeable, I’m sure,” said Susan Nipper, “to live friendly, Mrs Richards, while Master Paul continues a permanency, if the means can be planned out without going openly against orders, but goodness gracious Miss Floy, you haven’t got your things off yet, you naughty child, you haven’t, come along!”\n\nWith these words, Susan Nipper, in a transport of coercion, made a charge at her young ward, and swept her out of the room.\n\nThe child, in her grief and neglect, was so gentle, so quiet, and uncomplaining; was possessed of so much affection that no one seemed to care to have, and so much sorrowful intelligence that no one seemed to mind or think about the wounding of, that Polly’s heart was sore when she was left alone again. In the simple passage that had taken place between herself and the motherless little girl, her own motherly heart had been touched no less than the child’s; and she felt, as the child did, that there was something of confidence and interest between them from that moment.\n\nNotwithstanding Mr Toodle’s great reliance on Polly, she was perhaps in point of artificial accomplishments very little his superior. She had been good-humouredly working and drudging for her life all her life, and was a sober steady-going person, with matter-of-fact ideas about the butcher and baker, and the division of pence into farthings. But she was a good plain sample of a nature that is ever, in the mass, better, truer, higher, nobler, quicker to feel, and much more constant to retain, all tenderness and pity, self-denial and devotion, than the nature of men. And, perhaps, unlearned as she was, she could have brought a dawning knowledge home to Mr Dombey at that early day, which would not then have struck him in the end like lightning.\n\nBut this is from the purpose. Polly only thought, at that time, of improving on her successful propitiation of Miss Nipper, and devising some means of having little Florence aide her, lawfully, and without rebellion. An opening happened to present itself that very night.\n\nShe had been rung down into the glass room as usual, and had walked about and about it a long time, with the baby in her arms, when, to her great surprise and dismay, Mr Dombey—whom she had seen at first leaning on his elbow at the table, and afterwards walking up and down the middle room, drawing, each time, a little nearer, she thought, to the open folding doors—came out, suddenly, and stopped before her.\n\n“Good evening, Richards.”\n\nJust the same austere, stiff gentleman, as he had appeared to her on that first day. Such a hard-looking gentleman, that she involuntarily dropped her eyes and her curtsey at the same time.\n\n“How is Master Paul, Richards?”\n\n“Quite thriving, Sir, and well.”\n\n“He looks so,” said Mr Dombey, glancing with great interest at the tiny face she uncovered for his observation, and yet affecting to be half careless of it. “They give you everything you want, I hope?”\n\n“Oh yes, thank you, Sir.”\n\nShe suddenly appended such an obvious hesitation to this reply, however, that Mr Dombey, who had turned away; stopped, and turned round again, inquiringly.\n\n“If you please, Sir, the child is very much disposed to take notice of things,” said Richards, with another curtsey, “and—upstairs is a little dull for him, perhaps, Sir.”\n\n“I begged them to take you out for airings, constantly,” said Mr Dombey. “Very well! You shall go out oftener. You’re quite right to mention it.”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Sir,” faltered Polly, “but we go out quite plenty Sir, thank you.”\n\n“What would you have then?” asked Mr Dombey.\n\n“Indeed Sir, I don’t exactly know,” said Polly, “unless—”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“I believe nothing is so good for making children lively and cheerful, Sir, as seeing other children playing about ’em,” observed Polly, taking courage.\n\n“I think I mentioned to you, Richards, when you came here,” said Mr Dombey, with a frown, “that I wished you to see as little of your family as possible.”\n\n“Oh dear yes, Sir, I wasn’t so much as thinking of that.”\n\n“I am glad of it,” said Mr Dombey hastily. “You can continue your walk if you please.”\n\nWith that, he disappeared into his inner room; and Polly had the satisfaction of feeling that he had thoroughly misunderstood her object, and that she had fallen into disgrace without the least advancement of her purpose.\n\nNext night, she found him walking about the conservatory when she came down. As she stopped at the door, checked by this unusual sight, and uncertain whether to advance or retreat, he called her in. His mind was too much set on Dombey and Son, it soon appeared, to admit of his having forgotten her suggestion.\n\n“If you really think that sort of society is good for the child,” he said sharply, as if there had been no interval since she proposed it, “where’s Miss Florence?”\n\n“Nothing could be better than Miss Florence, Sir,” said Polly eagerly, “but I understood from her maid that they were not to—”\n\nMr Dombey rang the bell, and walked till it was answered.\n\n“Tell them always to let Miss Florence be with Richards when she chooses, and go out with her, and so forth. Tell them to let the children be together, when Richards wishes it.”\n\nThe iron was now hot, and Richards striking on it boldly—it was a good cause and she bold in it, though instinctively afraid of Mr Dombey—requested that Miss Florence might be sent down then and there, to make friends with her little brother.\n\nShe feigned to be dandling the child as the servant retired on this errand, but she thought that she saw Mr Dombey’s colour changed; that the expression of his face quite altered; that he turned, hurriedly, as if to gainsay what he had said, or she had said, or both, and was only deterred by very shame.\n\nAnd she was right. The last time he had seen his slighted child, there had been that in the sad embrace between her and her dying mother, which was at once a revelation and a reproach to him. Let him be absorbed as he would in the Son on whom he built such high hopes, he could not forget that closing scene. He could not forget that he had had no part in it. That, at the bottom of its clear depths of tenderness and truth lay those two figures clasped in each other’s arms, while he stood on the bank above them, looking down a mere spectator—not a sharer with them—quite shut out.\n\nUnable to exclude these things from his remembrance, or to keep his mind free from such imperfect shapes of the meaning with which they were fraught, as were able to make themselves visible to him through the mist of his pride, his previous feeling of indifference towards little Florence changed into an uneasiness of an extraordinary kind. Young as she was, and possessing in any eyes but his (and perhaps in his too) even more than the usual amount of childish simplicity and confidence, he almost felt as if she watched and distrusted him. As if she held the clue to something secret in his breast, of the nature of which he was hardly informed himself. As if she had an innate knowledge of one jarring and discordant string within him, and her very breath could sound it.\n\nHis feeling about the child had been negative from her birth. He had never conceived an aversion to her: it had not been worth his while or in his humour. She had never been a positively disagreeable object to him. But now he was ill at ease about her. She troubled his peace. He would have preferred to put her idea aside altogether, if he had known how. Perhaps—who shall decide on such mysteries!—he was afraid that he might come to hate her.\n\nWhen little Florence timidly presented herself, Mr Dombey stopped in his pacing up and down and looked towards her. Had he looked with greater interest and with a father’s eye, he might have read in her keen glance the impulses and fears that made her waver; the passionate desire to run clinging to him, crying, as she hid her face in his embrace, “Oh father, try to love me! there’s no one else!” the dread of a repulse; the fear of being too bold, and of offending him; the pitiable need in which she stood of some assurance and encouragement; and how her overcharged young heart was wandering to find some natural resting-place, for its sorrow and affection.\n\nBut he saw nothing of this. He saw her pause irresolutely at the door and look towards him; and he saw no more.\n\n“Come in,” he said, “come in: what is the child afraid of?”\n\nShe came in; and after glancing round her for a moment with an uncertain air, stood pressing her small hands hard together, close within the door.\n\n“Come here, Florence,” said her father, coldly. “Do you know who I am?”\n\n“Yes, Papa.”\n\n“Have you nothing to say to me?”\n\nThe tears that stood in her eyes as she raised them quickly to his face, were frozen by the expression it wore. She looked down again, and put out her trembling hand.\n\nMr Dombey took it loosely in his own, and stood looking down upon her for a moment, as if he knew as little as the child, what to say or do.\n\n“There! Be a good girl,” he said, patting her on the head, and regarding her as it were by stealth with a disturbed and doubtful look. “Go to Richards! Go!”\n\nHis little daughter hesitated for another instant as though she would have clung about him still, or had some lingering hope that he might raise her in his arms and kiss her. She looked up in his face once more. He thought how like her expression was then, to what it had been when she looked round at the Doctor—that night—and instinctively dropped her hand and turned away.\n\nIt was not difficult to perceive that Florence was at a great disadvantage in her father’s presence. It was not only a constraint upon the child’s mind, but even upon the natural grace and freedom of her actions. As she sported and played about her baby brother that night, her manner was seldom so winning and so pretty as it naturally was, and sometimes when in his pacing to and fro, he came near her (she had, perhaps, for the moment, forgotten him) it changed upon the instant and became forced and embarrassed.\n\nStill, Polly persevered with all the better heart for seeing this; and, judging of Mr Dombey by herself, had great confidence in the mute appeal of poor little Florence’s mourning dress. “It’s hard indeed,” thought Polly, “if he takes only to one little motherless child, when he has another, and that a girl, before his eyes.”\n\nSo, Polly kept her before his eyes, as long as she could, and managed so well with little Paul, as to make it very plain that he was all the livelier for his sister’s company. When it was time to withdraw upstairs again, she would have sent Florence into the inner room to say good-night to her father, but the child was timid and drew back; and when she urged her again, said, spreading her hands before her eyes, as if to shut out her own unworthiness, “Oh no, no! He don’t want me. He don’t want me!”\n\nThe little altercation between them had attracted the notice of Mr Dombey, who inquired from the table where he was sitting at his wine, what the matter was.\n\n“Miss Florence was afraid of interrupting, Sir, if she came in to say good-night,” said Richards.\n\n“It doesn’t matter,” returned Mr Dombey. “You can let her come and go without regarding me.”\n\nThe child shrunk as she listened—and was gone, before her humble friend looked round again.\n\nHowever, Polly triumphed not a little in the success of her well-intentioned scheme, and in the address with which she had brought it to bear: whereof she made a full disclosure to Spitfire when she was once more safely entrenched upstairs. Miss Nipper received that proof of her confidence, as well as the prospect of their free association for the future, rather coldly, and was anything but enthusiastic in her demonstrations of joy.\n\n“I thought you would have been pleased,” said Polly.\n\n“Oh yes, Mrs Richards, I’m very well pleased, thank you,” returned Susan, who had suddenly become so very upright that she seemed to have put an additional bone in her stays.\n\n“You don’t show it,” said Polly.\n\n“Oh! Being only a permanency I couldn’t be expected to show it like a temporary,” said Susan Nipper. “Temporaries carries it all before ’em here, I find, but though there’s a excellent party-wall between this house and the next, I mayn’t exactly like to go to it, Mrs Richards, notwithstanding!”\n\nCHAPTER IV. In which some more First Appearances are made on the Stage of these Adventures\nThough the offices of Dombey and Son were within the liberties of the City of London, and within hearing of Bow Bells, when their clashing voices were not drowned by the uproar in the streets, yet were there hints of adventurous and romantic story to be observed in some of the adjacent objects. Gog and Magog held their state within ten minutes’ walk; the Royal Exchange was close at hand; the Bank of England, with its vaults of gold and silver “down among the dead men” underground, was their magnificent neighbour. Just round the corner stood the rich East India House, teeming with suggestions of precious stuffs and stones, tigers, elephants, howdahs, hookahs, umbrellas, palm trees, palanquins, and gorgeous princes of a brown complexion sitting on carpets, with their slippers very much turned up at the toes. Anywhere in the immediate vicinity there might be seen pictures of ships speeding away full sail to all parts of the world; outfitting warehouses ready to pack off anybody anywhere, fully equipped in half an hour; and little timber midshipmen in obsolete naval uniforms, eternally employed outside the shop doors of nautical Instrument-makers in taking observations of the hackney carriages.\n\nSole master and proprietor of one of these effigies—of that which might be called, familiarly, the woodenest—of that which thrust itself out above the pavement, right leg foremost, with a suavity the least endurable, and had the shoe buckles and flapped waistcoat the least reconcileable to human reason, and bore at its right eye the most offensively disproportionate piece of machinery—sole master and proprietor of that Midshipman, and proud of him too, an elderly gentleman in a Welsh wig had paid house-rent, taxes, rates, and dues, for more years than many a full-grown midshipman of flesh and blood has numbered in his life; and midshipmen who have attained a pretty green old age, have not been wanting in the English Navy.\n\nThe stock-in-trade of this old gentleman comprised chronometers, barometers, telescopes, compasses, charts, maps, sextants, quadrants, and specimens of every kind of instrument used in the working of a ship’s course, or the keeping of a ship’s reckoning, or the prosecuting of a ship’s discoveries. Objects in brass and glass were in his drawers and on his shelves, which none but the initiated could have found the top of, or guessed the use of, or having once examined, could have ever got back again into their mahogany nests without assistance. Everything was jammed into the tightest cases, fitted into the narrowest corners, fenced up behind the most impertinent cushions, and screwed into the acutest angles, to prevent its philosophical composure from being disturbed by the rolling of the sea. Such extraordinary precautions were taken in every instance to save room, and keep the thing compact; and so much practical navigation was fitted, and cushioned, and screwed into every box (whether the box was a mere slab, as some were, or something between a cocked hat and a star-fish, as others were, and those quite mild and modest boxes as compared with others); that the shop itself, partaking of the general infection, seemed almost to become a snug, sea-going, ship-shape concern, wanting only good sea-room, in the event of an unexpected launch, to work its way securely to any desert island in the world.\n\nMany minor incidents in the household life of the Ships’ Instrument-maker who was proud of his little Midshipman, assisted and bore out this fancy. His acquaintance lying chiefly among ship-chandlers and so forth, he had always plenty of the veritable ships’ biscuit on his table. It was familiar with dried meats and tongues, possessing an extraordinary flavour of rope yarn. Pickles were produced upon it, in great wholesale jars, with “dealer in all kinds of Ships’ Provisions” on the label; spirits were set forth in case bottles with no throats. Old prints of ships with alphabetical references to their various mysteries, hung in frames upon the walls; the Tartar Frigate under weigh, was on the plates; outlandish shells, seaweeds, and mosses, decorated the chimney-piece; the little wainscotted back parlour was lighted by a sky-light, like a cabin.\n\nHere he lived too, in skipper-like state, all alone with his nephew Walter: a boy of fourteen who looked quite enough like a midshipman, to carry out the prevailing idea. But there it ended, for Solomon Gills himself (more generally called old Sol) was far from having a maritime appearance. To say nothing of his Welsh wig, which was as plain and stubborn a Welsh wig as ever was worn, and in which he looked like anything but a Rover, he was a slow, quiet-spoken, thoughtful old fellow, with eyes as red as if they had been small suns looking at you through a fog; and a newly-awakened manner, such as he might have acquired by having stared for three or four days successively through every optical instrument in his shop, and suddenly came back to the world again, to find it green. The only change ever known in his outward man, was from a complete suit of coffee-colour cut very square, and ornamented with glaring buttons, to the same suit of coffee-colour minus the inexpressibles, which were then of a pale nankeen. He wore a very precise shirt-frill, and carried a pair of first-rate spectacles on his forehead, and a tremendous chronometer in his fob, rather than doubt which precious possession, he would have believed in a conspiracy against it on part of all the clocks and watches in the City, and even of the very Sun itself. Such as he was, such he had been in the shop and parlour behind the little Midshipman, for years upon years; going regularly aloft to bed every night in a howling garret remote from the lodgers, where, when gentlemen of England who lived below at ease had little or no idea of the state of the weather, it often blew great guns.\n\nIt is half-past five o’clock, and an autumn afternoon, when the reader and Solomon Gills become acquainted. Solomon Gills is in the act of seeing what time it is by the unimpeachable chronometer. The usual daily clearance has been making in the City for an hour or more; and the human tide is still rolling westward. “The streets have thinned,” as Mr Gills says, “very much.” It threatens to be wet tonight. All the weatherglasses in the shop are in low spirits, and the rain already shines upon the cocked hat of the wooden Midshipman.\n\n“Where’s Walter, I wonder!” said Solomon Gills, after he had carefully put up the chronometer again. “Here’s dinner been ready, half an hour, and no Walter!”\n\nTurning round upon his stool behind the counter, Mr Gills looked out among the instruments in the window, to see if his nephew might be crossing the road. No. He was not among the bobbing umbrellas, and he certainly was not the newspaper boy in the oilskin cap who was slowly working his way along the piece of brass outside, writing his name over Mr Gills’s name with his forefinger.\n\n“If I didn’t know he was too fond of me to make a run of it, and go and enter himself aboard ship against my wishes, I should begin to be fidgetty,” said Mr Gills, tapping two or three weather-glasses with his knuckles. “I really should. All in the Downs, eh! Lots of moisture! Well! it’s wanted.”\n\n“I believe,” said Mr Gills, blowing the dust off the glass top of a compass-case, “that you don’t point more direct and due to the back parlour than the boy’s inclination does after all. And the parlour couldn’t bear straighter either. Due north. Not the twentieth part of a point either way.”\n\n“Halloa, Uncle Sol!”\n\n“Halloa, my boy!” cried the Instrument-maker, turning briskly round. “What! you are here, are you?”\n\nA cheerful looking, merry boy, fresh with running home in the rain; fair-faced, bright-eyed, and curly-haired.\n\n“Well, Uncle, how have you got on without me all day? Is dinner ready? I’m so hungry.”\n\n“As to getting on,” said Solomon good-naturedly, “it would be odd if I couldn’t get on without a young dog like you a great deal better than with you. As to dinner being ready, it’s been ready this half hour and waiting for you. As to being hungry, I am!”\n\n“Come along then, Uncle!” cried the boy. “Hurrah for the admiral!”\n\n“Confound the admiral!” returned Solomon Gills. “You mean the Lord Mayor.”\n\n“No I don’t!” cried the boy. “Hurrah for the admiral! Hurrah for the admiral! For-ward!”\n\nAt this word of command, the Welsh wig and its wearer were borne without resistance into the back parlour, as at the head of a boarding party of five hundred men; and Uncle Sol and his nephew were speedily engaged on a fried sole with a prospect of steak to follow.\n\n“The Lord Mayor, Wally,” said Solomon, “for ever! No more admirals. The Lord Mayor’s your admiral.”\n\n“Oh, is he though!” said the boy, shaking his head. “Why, the Sword Bearer’s better than him. He draws his sword sometimes.”\n\n“And a pretty figure he cuts with it for his pains,” returned the Uncle. “Listen to me, Wally, listen to me. Look on the mantelshelf.”\n\n“Why who has cocked my silver mug up there, on a nail?” exclaimed the boy.\n\n“I have,” said his Uncle. “No more mugs now. We must begin to drink out of glasses today, Walter. We are men of business. We belong to the City. We started in life this morning.”\n\n“Well, Uncle,” said the boy, “I’ll drink out of anything you like, so long as I can drink to you. Here’s to you, Uncle Sol, and Hurrah for the—”\n\n“Lord Mayor,” interrupted the old man.\n\n“For the Lord Mayor, Sheriffs, Common Council, and Livery,” said the boy. “Long life to ’em!”\n\nThe uncle nodded his head with great satisfaction. “And now,” he said, “let’s hear something about the Firm.”\n\n“Oh! there’s not much to be told about the Firm, Uncle,” said the boy, plying his knife and fork. “It’s a precious dark set of offices, and in the room where I sit, there’s a high fender, and an iron safe, and some cards about ships that are going to sail, and an almanack, and some desks and stools, and an inkbottle, and some books, and some boxes, and a lot of cobwebs, and in one of ’em, just over my head, a shrivelled-up blue-bottle that looks as if it had hung there ever so long.”\n\n“Nothing else?” said the Uncle.\n\n“No, nothing else, except an old birdcage (I wonder how that ever came there!) and a coal-scuttle.”\n\n“No bankers’ books, or cheque books, or bills, or such tokens of wealth rolling in from day to day?” said old Sol, looking wistfully at his nephew out of the fog that always seemed to hang about him, and laying an unctuous emphasis upon the words.\n\n“Oh yes, plenty of that I suppose,” returned his nephew carelessly; “but all that sort of thing’s in Mr Carker’s room, or Mr Morfin’s, or Mr Dombey’s.”\n\n“Has Mr Dombey been there today?” inquired the Uncle.\n\n“Oh yes! In and out all day.”\n\n“He didn’t take any notice of you, I suppose?”.\n\n“Yes he did. He walked up to my seat,—I wish he wasn’t so solemn and stiff, Uncle,—and said, ‘Oh! you are the son of Mr Gills the Ships’ Instrument-maker.’ ‘Nephew, Sir,’ I said. ‘I said nephew, boy,’ said he. But I could take my oath he said son, Uncle.”\n\n“You’re mistaken I daresay. It’s no matter.”\n\n“No, it’s no matter, but he needn’t have been so sharp, I thought. There was no harm in it though he did say son. Then he told me that you had spoken to him about me, and that he had found me employment in the House accordingly, and that I was expected to be attentive and punctual, and then he went away. I thought he didn’t seem to like me much.”\n\n“You mean, I suppose,” observed the Instrument-maker, “that you didn’t seem to like him much?”\n\n“Well, Uncle,” returned the boy, laughing. “Perhaps so; I never thought of that.”\n\nSolomon looked a little graver as he finished his dinner, and glanced from time to time at the boy’s bright face. When dinner was done, and the cloth was cleared away (the entertainment had been brought from a neighbouring eating-house), he lighted a candle, and went down below into a little cellar, while his nephew, standing on the mouldy staircase, dutifully held the light. After a moment’s groping here and there, he presently returned with a very ancient-looking bottle, covered with dust and dirt.\n\n“Why, Uncle Sol!” said the boy, “what are you about? that’s the wonderful Madeira!—there’s only one more bottle!”\n\nUncle Sol nodded his head, implying that he knew very well what he was about; and having drawn the cork in solemn silence, filled two glasses and set the bottle and a third clean glass on the table.\n\n“You shall drink the other bottle, Wally,” he said, “when you come to good fortune; when you are a thriving, respected, happy man; when the start in life you have made today shall have brought you, as I pray Heaven it may!—to a smooth part of the course you have to run, my child. My love to you!”\n\nSome of the fog that hung about old Sol seemed to have got into his throat; for he spoke huskily. His hand shook too, as he clinked his glass against his nephew’s. But having once got the wine to his lips, he tossed it off like a man, and smacked them afterwards.\n\n“Dear Uncle,” said the boy, affecting to make light of it, while the tears stood in his eyes, “for the honour you have done me, et cetera, et cetera. I shall now beg to propose Mr Solomon Gills with three times three and one cheer more. Hurrah! and you’ll return thanks, Uncle, when we drink the last bottle together; won’t you?”\n\nThey clinked their glasses again; and Walter, who was hoarding his wine, took a sip of it, and held the glass up to his eye with as critical an air as he could possibly assume.\n\nHis Uncle sat looking at him for some time in silence. When their eyes at last met, he began at once to pursue the theme that had occupied his thoughts, aloud, as if he had been speaking all the time.\n\n“You see, Walter,” he said, “in truth this business is merely a habit with me. I am so accustomed to the habit that I could hardly live if I relinquished it: but there’s nothing doing, nothing doing. When that uniform was worn,” pointing out towards the little Midshipman, “then indeed, fortunes were to be made, and were made. But competition, competition—new invention, new invention—alteration, alteration—the world’s gone past me. I hardly know where I am myself, much less where my customers are.”\n\n“Never mind ’em, Uncle!”\n\n“Since you came home from weekly boarding-school at Peckham, for instance—and that’s ten days,” said Solomon, “I don’t remember more than one person that has come into the shop.”\n\n“Two, Uncle, don’t you recollect? There was the man who came to ask for change for a sovereign—”\n\n“That’s the one,” said Solomon.\n\n“Why Uncle! don’t you call the woman anybody, who came to ask the way to Mile-End Turnpike?”\n\n“Oh! it’s true,” said Solomon, “I forgot her. Two persons.”\n\n“To be sure, they didn’t buy anything,” cried the boy.\n\n“No. They didn’t buy anything,” said Solomon, quietly.\n\n“Nor want anything,” cried the boy.\n\n“No. If they had, they’d gone to another shop,” said Solomon, in the same tone.\n\n“But there were two of ’em, Uncle,” cried the boy, as if that were a great triumph. “You said only one.”\n\n“Well, Wally,” resumed the old man, after a short pause: “not being like the Savages who came on Robinson Crusoe’s Island, we can’t live on a man who asks for change for a sovereign, and a woman who inquires the way to Mile-End Turnpike. As I said just now, the world has gone past me. I don’t blame it; but I no longer understand it. Tradesmen are not the same as they used to be, apprentices are not the same, business is not the same, business commodities are not the same. Seven-eighths of my stock is old-fashioned. I am an old-fashioned man in an old-fashioned shop, in a street that is not the same as I remember it. I have fallen behind the time, and am too old to catch it again. Even the noise it makes a long way ahead, confuses me.”\n\nWalter was going to speak, but his Uncle held up his hand.\n\n“Therefore, Wally—therefore it is that I am anxious you should be early in the busy world, and on the world’s track. I am only the ghost of this business—its substance vanished long ago; and when I die, its ghost will be laid. As it is clearly no inheritance for you then, I have thought it best to use for your advantage, almost the only fragment of the old connexion that stands by me, through long habit. Some people suppose me to be wealthy. I wish for your sake they were right. But whatever I leave behind me, or whatever I can give you, you in such a House as Dombey’s are in the road to use well and make the most of. Be diligent, try to like it, my dear boy, work for a steady independence, and be happy!”\n\n“I’ll do everything I can, Uncle, to deserve your affection. Indeed I will,” said the boy, earnestly.\n\n“I know it,” said Solomon. “I am sure of it,” and he applied himself to a second glass of the old Madeira, with increased relish. “As to the Sea,” he pursued, “that’s well enough in fiction, Wally, but it won’t do in fact: it won’t do at all. It’s natural enough that you should think about it, associating it with all these familiar things; but it won’t do, it won’t do.”\n\nSolomon Gills rubbed his hands with an air of stealthy enjoyment, as he talked of the sea, though; and looked on the seafaring objects about him with inexpressible complacency.\n\n“Think of this wine for instance,” said old Sol, “which has been to the East Indies and back, I’m not able to say how often, and has been once round the world. Think of the pitch-dark nights, the roaring winds, and rolling seas:”\n\n“The thunder, lightning, rain, hail, storm of all kinds,” said the boy.\n\n“To be sure,” said Solomon,—“that this wine has passed through. Think what a straining and creaking of timbers and masts: what a whistling and howling of the gale through ropes and rigging:”\n\n“What a clambering aloft of men, vying with each other who shall lie out first upon the yards to furl the icy sails, while the ship rolls and pitches, like mad!” cried his nephew.\n\n“Exactly so,” said Solomon: “has gone on, over the old cask that held this wine. Why, when the Charming Sally went down in the—”\n\n“In the Baltic Sea, in the dead of night; five-and-twenty minutes past twelve when the captain’s watch stopped in his pocket; he lying dead against the main-mast—on the fourteenth of February, seventeen forty-nine!” cried Walter, with great animation.\n\n“Ay, to be sure!” cried old Sol, “quite right! Then, there were five hundred casks of such wine aboard; and all hands (except the first mate, first lieutenant, two seamen, and a lady, in a leaky boat) going to work to stave the casks, got drunk and died drunk, singing ‘Rule Britannia’, when she settled and went down, and ending with one awful scream in chorus.”\n\n“But when the George the Second drove ashore, Uncle, on the coast of Cornwall, in a dismal gale, two hours before daybreak, on the fourth of March, “seventy-one, she had near two hundred horses aboard; and the horses breaking loose down below, early in the gale, and tearing to and fro, and trampling each other to death, made such noises, and set up such human cries, that the crew believing the ship to be full of devils, some of the best men, losing heart and head, went overboard in despair, and only two were left alive, at last, to tell the tale.”\n\n“And when,” said old Sol, “when the Polyphemus—”\n\n“Private West India Trader, burden three hundred and fifty tons, Captain, John Brown of Deptford. Owners, Wiggs and Co.,” cried Walter.\n\n“The same,” said Sol; “when she took fire, four days’ sail with a fair wind out of Jamaica Harbour, in the night—”\n\n“There were two brothers on board,” interposed his nephew, speaking very fast and loud, “and there not being room for both of them in the only boat that wasn’t swamped, neither of them would consent to go, until the elder took the younger by the waist, and flung him in. And then the younger, rising in the boat, cried out, ‘Dear Edward, think of your promised wife at home. I’m only a boy. No one waits at home for me. Leap down into my place!’ and flung himself in the sea!”\n\nThe kindling eye and heightened colour of the boy, who had risen from his seat in the earnestness of what he said and felt, seemed to remind old Sol of something he had forgotten, or that his encircling mist had hitherto shut out. Instead of proceeding with any more anecdotes, as he had evidently intended but a moment before, he gave a short dry cough, and said, “Well! suppose we change the subject.”\n\nThe truth was, that the simple-minded Uncle in his secret attraction towards the marvellous and adventurous—of which he was, in some sort, a distant relation, by his trade—had greatly encouraged the same attraction in the nephew; and that everything that had ever been put before the boy to deter him from a life of adventure, had had the usual unaccountable effect of sharpening his taste for it. This is invariable. It would seem as if there never was a book written, or a story told, expressly with the object of keeping boys on shore, which did not lure and charm them to the ocean, as a matter of course.\n\nBut an addition to the little party now made its appearance, in the shape of a gentleman in a wide suit of blue, with a hook instead of a hand attached to his right wrist; very bushy black eyebrows; and a thick stick in his left hand, covered all over (like his nose) with knobs. He wore a loose black silk handkerchief round his neck, and such a very large coarse shirt collar, that it looked like a small sail. He was evidently the person for whom the spare wine-glass was intended, and evidently knew it; for having taken off his rough outer coat, and hung up, on a particular peg behind the door, such a hard glazed hat as a sympathetic person’s head might ache at the sight of, and which left a red rim round his own forehead as if he had been wearing a tight basin, he brought a chair to where the clean glass was, and sat himself down behind it. He was usually addressed as Captain, this visitor; and had been a pilot, or a skipper, or a privateersman, or all three perhaps; and was a very salt-looking man indeed.\n\nHis face, remarkable for a brown solidity, brightened as he shook hands with Uncle and nephew; but he seemed to be of a laconic disposition, and merely said:\n\n“How goes it?”\n\n“All well,” said Mr Gills, pushing the bottle towards him.\n\nHe took it up, and having surveyed and smelt it, said with extraordinary expression:\n\n“The?”\n\n“The,” returned the Instrument-maker.\n\nUpon that he whistled as he filled his glass, and seemed to think they were making holiday indeed.\n\n“Wal”r!” he said, arranging his hair (which was thin) with his hook, and then pointing it at the Instrument-maker, “Look at him! Love! Honour! And Obey! Overhaul your catechism till you find that passage, and when found turn the leaf down. Success, my boy!”\n\nHe was so perfectly satisfied both with his quotation and his reference to it, that he could not help repeating the words again in a low voice, and saying he had forgotten ’em these forty year.\n\n“But I never wanted two or three words in my life that I didn’t know where to lay my hand upon ’em, Gills,” he observed. “It comes of not wasting language as some do.”\n\nThe reflection perhaps reminded him that he had better, like young Norval’s father, ‘increase his store.’ At any rate he became silent, and remained so, until old Sol went out into the shop to light it up, when he turned to Walter, and said, without any introductory remark:—\n\n“I suppose he could make a clock if he tried?”\n\n“I shouldn’t wonder, Captain Cuttle,” returned the boy.\n\n“And it would go!” said Captain Cuttle, making a species of serpent in the air with his hook. “Lord, how that clock would go!”\n\nFor a moment or two he seemed quite lost in contemplating the pace of this ideal timepiece, and sat looking at the boy as if his face were the dial.\n\n“But he’s chock-full of science,” he observed, waving his hook towards the stock-in-trade. “Look’ye here! Here’s a collection of ’em. Earth, air, or water. It’s all one. Only say where you’ll have it. Up in a balloon? There you are. Down in a bell? There you are. D’ye want to put the North Star in a pair of scales and weigh it? He’ll do it for you.”\n\nIt may be gathered from these remarks that Captain Cuttle’s reverence for the stock of instruments was profound, and that his philosophy knew little or no distinction between trading in it and inventing it.\n\n“Ah!” he said, with a sigh, “it’s a fine thing to understand ’em. And yet it’s a fine thing not to understand ’em. I hardly know which is best. It’s so comfortable to sit here and feel that you might be weighed, measured, magnified, electrified, polarized, played the very devil with: and never know how.”\n\nNothing short of the wonderful Madeira, combined with the occasion (which rendered it desirable to improve and expand Walter’s mind), could have ever loosened his tongue to the extent of giving utterance to this prodigious oration. He seemed quite amazed himself at the manner in which it opened up to view the sources of the taciturn delight he had had in eating Sunday dinners in that parlour for ten years. Becoming a sadder and a wiser man, he mused and held his peace.\n\n“Come!” cried the subject of this admiration, returning. “Before you have your glass of grog, Ned, we must finish the bottle.”\n\n“Stand by!” said Ned, filling his glass. “Give the boy some more.”\n\n“No more, thank’e, Uncle!”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Sol, “a little more. We’ll finish the bottle, to the House, Ned—Walter’s House. Why it may be his House one of these days, in part. Who knows? Sir Richard Whittington married his master’s daughter.”\n\n“‘Turn again Whittington, Lord Mayor of London, and when you are old you will never depart from it,’” interposed the Captain. “Wal”r! Overhaul the book, my lad.”\n\n“And although Mr Dombey hasn’t a daughter,” Sol began.\n\n“Yes, yes, he has, Uncle,” said the boy, reddening and laughing.\n\n“Has he?” cried the old man. “Indeed I think he has too.”\n\n“Oh! I know he has,” said the boy. “Some of ’em were talking about it in the office today. And they do say, Uncle and Captain Cuttle,” lowering his voice, “that he’s taken a dislike to her, and that she’s left, unnoticed, among the servants, and that his mind’s so set all the while upon having his son in the House, that although he’s only a baby now, he is going to have balances struck oftener than formerly, and the books kept closer than they used to be, and has even been seen (when he thought he wasn’t) walking in the Docks, looking at his ships and property and all that, as if he was exulting like, over what he and his son will possess together. That’s what they say. Of course, I don’t know.”\n\n“He knows all about her already, you see,” said the instrument-maker.\n\n“Nonsense, Uncle,” cried the boy, still reddening and laughing, boy-like. “How can I help hearing what they tell me?”\n\n“The son’s a little in our way at present, I’m afraid, Ned,” said the old man, humouring the joke.\n\n“Very much,” said the Captain.\n\n“Nevertheless, we’ll drink him,” pursued Sol. “So, here’s to Dombey and Son.”\n\n“Oh, very well, Uncle,” said the boy, merrily. “Since you have introduced the mention of her, and have connected me with her and have said that I know all about her, I shall make bold to amend the toast. So here’s to Dombey—and Son—and Daughter!”\n\n# CHAPTER V. Paul’s Progress and Christening\n\nLittle Paul, suffering no contamination from the blood of the Toodles, grew stouter and stronger every day. Every day, too, he was more and more ardently cherished by Miss Tox, whose devotion was so far appreciated by Mr Dombey that he began to regard her as a woman of great natural good sense, whose feelings did her credit and deserved encouragement. He was so lavish of this condescension, that he not only bowed to her, in a particular manner, on several occasions, but even entrusted such stately recognitions of her to his sister as “pray tell your friend, Louisa, that she is very good,” or “mention to Miss Tox, Louisa, that I am obliged to her;” specialities which made a deep impression on the lady thus distinguished.\n\nWhether Miss Tox conceived that having been selected by the Fates to welcome the little Dombey before he was born, in Kirby, Beard and Kirby’s Best Mixed Pins, it therefore naturally devolved upon her to greet him with all other forms of welcome in all other early stages of his existence—or whether her overflowing goodness induced her to volunteer into the domestic militia as a substitute in some sort for his deceased Mama—or whether she was conscious of any other motives—are questions which in this stage of the Firm’s history herself only could have solved. Nor have they much bearing on the fact (of which there is no doubt), that Miss Tox’s constancy and zeal were a heavy discouragement to Richards, who lost flesh hourly under her patronage, and was in some danger of being superintended to death.\n\nMiss Tox was often in the habit of assuring Mrs Chick, that nothing could exceed her interest in all connected with the development of that sweet child; and an observer of Miss Tox’s proceedings might have inferred so much without declaratory confirmation. She would preside over the innocent repasts of the young heir, with ineffable satisfaction, almost with an air of joint proprietorship with Richards in the entertainment. At the little ceremonies of the bath and toilette, she assisted with enthusiasm. The administration of infantine doses of physic awakened all the active sympathy of her character; and being on one occasion secreted in a cupboard (whither she had fled in modesty), when Mr Dombey was introduced into the nursery by his sister, to behold his son, in the course of preparation for bed, taking a short walk uphill over Richards’s gown, in a short and airy linen jacket, Miss Tox was so transported beyond the ignorant present as to be unable to refrain from crying out, “Is he not beautiful Mr Dombey! Is he not a Cupid, Sir!” and then almost sinking behind the closet door with confusion and blushes.\n\n“Louisa,” said Mr Dombey, one day, to his sister, “I really think I must present your friend with some little token, on the occasion of Paul’s christening. She has exerted herself so warmly in the child’s behalf from the first, and seems to understand her position so thoroughly (a very rare merit in this world, I am sorry to say), that it would really be agreeable to me to notice her.”\n\nLet it be no detraction from the merits of Miss Tox, to hint that in Mr Dombey’s eyes, as in some others that occasionally see the light, they only achieved that mighty piece of knowledge, the understanding of their own position, who showed a fitting reverence for his. It was not so much their merit that they knew themselves, as that they knew him, and bowed low before him.\n\n“My dear Paul,” returned his sister, “you do Miss Tox but justice, as a man of your penetration was sure, I knew, to do. I believe if there are three words in the English language for which she has a respect amounting almost to veneration, those words are, Dombey and Son.”\n\n“Well,” said Mr Dombey, “I believe it. It does Miss Tox credit.”\n\n“And as to anything in the shape of a token, my dear Paul,” pursued his sister, “all I can say is that anything you give Miss Tox will be hoarded and prized, I am sure, like a relic. But there is a way, my dear Paul, of showing your sense of Miss Tox’s friendliness in a still more flattering and acceptable manner, if you should be so inclined.”\n\n“How is that?” asked Mr Dombey.\n\n“Godfathers, of course,” continued Mrs Chick, “are important in point of connexion and influence.”\n\n“I don’t know why they should be, to my son,” said Mr Dombey, coldly.\n\n“Very true, my dear Paul,” retorted Mrs Chick, with an extraordinary show of animation, to cover the suddenness of her conversion; “and spoken like yourself. I might have expected nothing else from you. I might have known that such would have been your opinion. Perhaps;” here Mrs Chick faltered again, as not quite comfortably feeling her way; “perhaps that is a reason why you might have the less objection to allowing Miss Tox to be godmother to the dear thing, if it were only as deputy and proxy for someone else. That it would be received as a great honour and distinction, Paul, I need not say.”\n\n“Louisa,” said Mr Dombey, after a short pause, “it is not to be supposed—”\n\n“Certainly not,” cried Mrs Chick, hastening to anticipate a refusal, “I never thought it was.”\n\nMr Dombey looked at her impatiently.\n\n“Don’t flurry me, my dear Paul,” said his sister; “for that destroys me. I am far from strong. I have not been quite myself, since poor dear Fanny departed.”\n\nMr Dombey glanced at the pocket-handkerchief which his sister applied to her eyes, and resumed:\n\n“It is not be supposed, I say—”\n“And I say,” murmured Mrs Chick, “that I never thought it was.”\n\n“Good Heaven, Louisa!” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“No, my dear Paul,” she remonstrated with tearful dignity, “I must really be allowed to speak. I am not so clever, or so reasoning, or so eloquent, or so anything, as you are. I know that very well. So much the worse for me. But if they were the last words I had to utter—and last words should be very solemn to you and me, Paul, after poor dear Fanny—I would still say I never thought it was. And what is more,” added Mrs Chick with increased dignity, as if she had withheld her crushing argument until now, “I never did think it was.”\n\nMr Dombey walked to the window and back again.\n\n“It is not to be supposed, Louisa,” he said (Mrs Chick had nailed her colours to the mast, and repeated “I know it isn’t,” but he took no notice of it), “but that there are many persons who, supposing that I recognised any claim at all in such a case, have a claim upon me superior to Miss Tox’s. But I do not. I recognise no such thing. Paul and myself will be able, when the time comes, to hold our own—the House, in other words, will be able to hold its own, and maintain its own, and hand down its own of itself, and without any such common-place aids. The kind of foreign help which people usually seek for their children, I can afford to despise; being above it, I hope. So that Paul’s infancy and childhood pass away well, and I see him becoming qualified without waste of time for the career on which he is destined to enter, I am satisfied. He will make what powerful friends he pleases in after-life, when he is actively maintaining—and extending, if that is possible—the dignity and credit of the Firm. Until then, I am enough for him, perhaps, and all in all. I have no wish that people should step in between us. I would much rather show my sense of the obliging conduct of a deserving person like your friend. Therefore let it be so; and your husband and myself will do well enough for the other sponsors, I daresay.”\n\nIn the course of these remarks, delivered with great majesty and grandeur, Mr Dombey had truly revealed the secret feelings of his breast. An indescribable distrust of anybody stepping in between himself and his son; a haughty dread of having any rival or partner in the boy’s respect and deference; a sharp misgiving, recently acquired, that he was not infallible in his power of bending and binding human wills; as sharp a jealousy of any second check or cross; these were, at that time the master keys of his soul. In all his life, he had never made a friend. His cold and distant nature had neither sought one, nor found one. And now, when that nature concentrated its whole force so strongly on a partial scheme of parental interest and ambition, it seemed as if its icy current, instead of being released by this influence, and running clear and free, had thawed for but an instant to admit its burden, and then frozen with it into one unyielding block.\n\nElevated thus to the godmothership of little Paul, in virtue of her insignificance, Miss Tox was from that hour chosen and appointed to office; and Mr Dombey further signified his pleasure that the ceremony, already long delayed, should take place without further postponement. His sister, who had been far from anticipating so signal a success, withdrew as soon as she could, to communicate it to her best of friends; and Mr Dombey was left alone in his library. He had already laid his hand upon the bellrope to convey his usual summons to Richards, when his eye fell upon a writing-desk, belonging to his deceased wife, which had been taken, among other things, from a cabinet in her chamber. It was not the first time that his eye had lighted on it He carried the key in his pocket; and he brought it to his table and opened it now—having previously locked the room door—with a well-accustomed hand.\n\nFrom beneath a leaf of torn and cancelled scraps of paper, he took one letter that remained entire. Involuntarily holding his breath as he opened this document, and “bating in the stealthy action something of his arrogant demeanour, he sat down, resting his head upon one hand, and read it through.\n\nHe read it slowly and attentively, and with a nice particularity to every syllable. Otherwise than as his great deliberation seemed unnatural, and perhaps the result of an effort equally great, he allowed no sign of emotion to escape him. When he had read it through, he folded and refolded it slowly several times, and tore it carefully into fragments. Checking his hand in the act of throwing these away, he put them in his pocket, as if unwilling to trust them even to the chances of being re-united and deciphered; and instead of ringing, as usual, for little Paul, he sat solitary, all the evening, in his cheerless room.\n\nThere was anything but solitude in the nursery; for there, Mrs Chick and Miss Tox were enjoying a social evening, so much to the disgust of Miss Susan Nipper, that that young lady embraced every opportunity of making wry faces behind the door. Her feelings were so much excited on the occasion, that she found it indispensable to afford them this relief, even without having the comfort of any audience or sympathy whatever. As the knight-errants of old relieved their minds by carving their mistress’s names in deserts, and wildernesses, and other savage places where there was no probability of there ever being anybody to read them, so did Miss Susan Nipper curl her snub nose into drawers and wardrobes, put away winks of disparagement in cupboards, shed derisive squints into stone pitchers, and contradict and call names out in the passage.\n\nThe two interlopers, however, blissfully unconscious of the young lady’s sentiments, saw little Paul safe through all the stages of undressing, airy exercise, supper and bed; and then sat down to tea before the fire. The two children now lay, through the good offices of Polly, in one room; and it was not until the ladies were established at their tea-table that, happening to look towards the little beds, they thought of Florence.\n\n“How sound she sleeps!” said Miss Tox.\n\n“Why, you know, my dear, she takes a great deal of exercise in the course of the day,” returned Mrs Chick, “playing about little Paul so much.”\n\n“She is a curious child,” said Miss Tox.\n\n“My dear,” retorted Mrs Chick, in a low voice: “Her Mama, all over!”\n\n“In-deed!” said Miss Tox. “Ah dear me!”\n\nA tone of most extraordinary compassion Miss Tox said it in, though she had no distinct idea why, except that it was expected of her.\n\n“Florence will never, never, never be a Dombey,” said Mrs Chick, “not if she lives to be a thousand years old.”\n\nMiss Tox elevated her eyebrows, and was again full of commiseration.\n\n“I quite fret and worry myself about her,” said Mrs Chick, with a sigh of modest merit. “I really don’t see what is to become of her when she grows older, or what position she is to take. She don’t gain on her Papa in the least. How can one expect she should, when she is so very unlike a Dombey?”\n\nMiss Tox looked as if she saw no way out of such a cogent argument as that, at all.\n\n“And the child, you see,” said Mrs Chick, in deep confidence, “has poor dear Fanny’s nature. She’ll never make an effort in after-life, I’ll venture to say. Never! She’ll never wind and twine herself about her Papa’s heart like—”\n\n“Like the ivy?” suggested Miss Tox.\n\n“Like the ivy,” Mrs Chick assented. “Never! She’ll never glide and nestle into the bosom of her Papa’s affections like—the—”\n\n“Startled fawn?” suggested Miss Tox.\n\n“Like the startled fawn,” said Mrs Chick. “Never! Poor Fanny! Yet, how I loved her!”\n\n“You must not distress yourself, my dear,” said Miss Tox, in a soothing voice. “Now really! You have too much feeling.”\n\n“We have all our faults,” said Mrs Chick, weeping and shaking her head. “I daresay we have. I never was blind to hers. I never said I was. Far from it. Yet how I loved her!”\n\nWhat a satisfaction it was to Mrs Chick—a common-place piece of folly enough, compared with whom her sister-in-law had been a very angel of womanly intelligence and gentleness—to patronise and be tender to the memory of that lady: in exact pursuance of her conduct to her in her lifetime: and to thoroughly believe herself, and take herself in, and make herself uncommonly comfortable on the strength of her toleration! What a mighty pleasant virtue toleration should be when we are right, to be so very pleasant when we are wrong, and quite unable to demonstrate how we come to be invested with the privilege of exercising it!\n\nMrs Chick was yet drying her eyes and shaking her head, when Richards made bold to caution her that Miss Florence was awake and sitting in her bed. She had risen, as the nurse said, and the lashes of her eyes were wet with tears. But no one saw them glistening save Polly. No one else leant over her, and whispered soothing words to her, or was near enough to hear the flutter of her beating heart.\n\n“Oh! dear nurse!” said the child, looking earnestly up in her face, “let me lie by my brother!”\n\n“Why, my pet?” said Richards.\n\n“Oh! I think he loves me,” cried the child wildly. “Let me lie by him. Pray do!”\n\nMrs Chick interposed with some motherly words about going to sleep like a dear, but Florence repeated her supplication, with a frightened look, and in a voice broken by sobs and tears.\n\n“I’ll not wake him,” she said, covering her face and hanging down her head. “I’ll only touch him with my hand, and go to sleep. Oh, pray, pray, let me lie by my brother tonight, for I believe he’s fond of me!”\n\nRichards took her without a word, and carrying her to the little bed in which the infant was sleeping, laid her down by his side. She crept as near him as she could without disturbing his rest; and stretching out one arm so that it timidly embraced his neck, and hiding her face on the other, over which her damp and scattered hair fell loose, lay motionless.\n\n“Poor little thing,” said Miss Tox; “she has been dreaming, I daresay.”\n\nDreaming, perhaps, of loving tones for ever silent, of loving eyes for ever closed, of loving arms again wound round her, and relaxing in that dream within the dam which no tongue can relate. Seeking, perhaps—in dreams—some natural comfort for a heart, deeply and sorely wounded, though so young a child’s: and finding it, perhaps, in dreams, if not in waking, cold, substantial truth. This trivial incident had so interrupted the current of conversation, that it was difficult of resumption; and Mrs Chick moreover had been so affected by the contemplation of her own tolerant nature, that she was not in spirits. The two friends accordingly soon made an end of their tea, and a servant was despatched to fetch a hackney cabriolet for Miss Tox. Miss Tox had great experience in hackney cabs, and her starting in one was generally a work of time, as she was systematic in the preparatory arrangements.\n\n“Have the goodness, if you please, Towlinson,” said Miss Tox, “first of all, to carry out a pen and ink and take his number legibly.”\n\n“Yes, Miss,” said Towlinson.\n\n“Then, if you please, Towlinson,” said Miss Tox, “have the goodness to turn the cushion. Which,” said Miss Tox apart to Mrs Chick, “is generally damp, my dear.”\n\n“Yes, Miss,” said Towlinson.\n\n“I’ll trouble you also, if you please, Towlinson,” said Miss Tox, “with this card and this shilling. He’s to drive to the card, and is to understand that he will not on any account have more than the shilling.”\n\n“No, Miss,” said Towlinson.\n\n“And—I’m sorry to give you so much trouble, Towlinson,” said Miss Tox, looking at him pensively.\n\n“Not at all, Miss,” said Towlinson.\n\n“Mention to the man, then, if you please, Towlinson,” said Miss Tox, “that the lady’s uncle is a magistrate, and that if he gives her any of his impertinence he will be punished terribly. You can pretend to say that, if you please, Towlinson, in a friendly way, and because you know it was done to another man, who died.”\n\n“Certainly, Miss,” said Towlinson.\n\n“And now good-night to my sweet, sweet, sweet, godson,” said Miss Tox, with a soft shower of kisses at each repetition of the adjective; “and Louisa, my dear friend, promise me to take a little something warm before you go to bed, and not to distress yourself!”\n\nIt was with extreme difficulty that Nipper, the black-eyed, who looked on steadfastly, contained herself at this crisis, and until the subsequent departure of Mrs Chick. But the nursery being at length free of visitors, she made herself some recompense for her late restraint.\n\n“You might keep me in a strait-waistcoat for six weeks,” said Nipper, “and when I got it off I’d only be more aggravated, who ever heard the like of them two Griffins, Mrs Richards?”\n\n“And then to talk of having been dreaming, poor dear!” said Polly.\n\n“Oh you beauties!” cried Susan Nipper, affecting to salute the door by which the ladies had departed. “Never be a Dombey won’t she? It’s to be hoped she won’t, we don’t want any more such, one’s enough.”\n\n“Don’t wake the children, Susan dear,” said Polly.\n\n“I’m very much beholden to you, Mrs Richards,” said Susan, who was not by any means discriminating in her wrath, “and really feel it as a honour to receive your commands, being a black slave and a mulotter. Mrs Richards, if there’s any other orders, you can give me, pray mention ’em.”\n\n“Nonsense; orders,” said Polly.\n\n“Oh! bless your heart, Mrs Richards,” cried Susan, “temporaries always orders permanencies here, didn’t you know that, why wherever was you born, Mrs Richards? But wherever you was born, Mrs Richards,” pursued Spitfire, shaking her head resolutely, “and whenever, and however (which is best known to yourself), you may bear in mind, please, that it’s one thing to give orders, and quite another thing to take ’em. A person may tell a person to dive off a bridge head foremost into five-and-forty feet of water, Mrs Richards, but a person may be very far from diving.”\n\n“There now,” said Polly, “you’re angry because you’re a good little thing, and fond of Miss Florence; and yet you turn round on me, because there’s nobody else.”\n\n“It’s very easy for some to keep their tempers, and be soft-spoken, Mrs Richards,” returned Susan, slightly mollified, “when their child’s made as much of as a prince, and is petted and patted till it wishes its friends further, but when a sweet young pretty innocent, that never ought to have a cross word spoken to or of it, is rundown, the case is very different indeed. My goodness gracious me, Miss Floy, you naughty, sinful child, if you don’t shut your eyes this minute, I’ll call in them hobgoblins that lives in the cock-loft to come and eat you up alive!”\n\nHere Miss Nipper made a horrible lowing, supposed to issue from a conscientious goblin of the bull species, impatient to discharge the severe duty of his position. Having further composed her young charge by covering her head with the bedclothes, and making three or four angry dabs at the pillow, she folded her arms, and screwed up her mouth, and sat looking at the fire for the rest of the evening.\n\nThough little Paul was said, in nursery phrase, “to take a deal of notice for his age,” he took as little notice of all this as of the preparations for his christening on the next day but one; which nevertheless went on about him, as to his personal apparel, and that of his sister and the two nurses, with great activity. Neither did he, on the arrival of the appointed morning, show any sense of its importance; being, on the contrary, unusually inclined to sleep, and unusually inclined to take it ill in his attendants that they dressed him to go out.\n\nIt happened to be an iron-grey autumnal day, with a shrewd east wind blowing—a day in keeping with the proceedings. Mr Dombey represented in himself the wind, the shade, and the autumn of the christening. He stood in his library to receive the company, as hard and cold as the weather; and when he looked out through the glass room, at the trees in the little garden, their brown and yellow leaves came fluttering down, as if he blighted them.\n\nUgh! They were black, cold rooms; and seemed to be in mourning, like the inmates of the house. The books precisely matched as to size, and drawn up in line, like soldiers, looked in their cold, hard, slippery uniforms, as if they had but one idea among them, and that was a freezer. The bookcase, glazed and locked, repudiated all familiarities. Mr Pitt, in bronze, on the top, with no trace of his celestial origin about him, guarded the unattainable treasure like an enchanted Moor. A dusty urn at each high corner, dug up from an ancient tomb, preached desolation and decay, as from two pulpits; and the chimney-glass, reflecting Mr Dombey and his portrait at one blow, seemed fraught with melancholy meditations.\n\nThe stiff and stark fire-irons appeared to claim a nearer relationship than anything else there to Mr Dombey, with his buttoned coat, his white cravat, his heavy gold watch-chain, and his creaking boots. But this was before the arrival of Mr and Mrs Chick, his lawful relatives, who soon presented themselves.\n\n“My dear Paul,” Mrs Chick murmured, as she embraced him, “the beginning, I hope, of many joyful days!”\n\n“Thank you, Louisa,” said Mr Dombey, grimly. “How do you do, Mr John?”\n\n“How do you do, Sir?” said Chick.\n\nHe gave Mr Dombey his hand, as if he feared it might electrify him. Mr Dombey took it as if it were a fish, or seaweed, or some such clammy substance, and immediately returned it to him with exalted politeness.\n\n“Perhaps, Louisa,” said Mr Dombey, slightly turning his head in his cravat, as if it were a socket, “you would have preferred a fire?”\n\n“Oh, my dear Paul, no,” said Mrs Chick, who had much ado to keep her teeth from chattering; “not for me.”\n\n“Mr John,” said Mr Dombey, “you are not sensible of any chill?”\n\nMr John, who had already got both his hands in his pockets over the wrists, and was on the very threshold of that same canine chorus which had given Mrs Chick so much offence on a former occasion, protested that he was perfectly comfortable.\n\nHe added in a low voice, “With my tiddle tol toor rul”—when he was providentially stopped by Towlinson, who announced:\n\n“Miss Tox!”\nAnd enter that fair enslaver, with a blue nose and indescribably frosty face, referable to her being very thinly clad in a maze of fluttering odds and ends, to do honour to the ceremony.\n\n“How do you do, Miss Tox?” said Mr Dombey.\n\nMiss Tox, in the midst of her spreading gauzes, went down altogether like an opera-glass shutting-up; she curtseyed so low, in acknowledgment of Mr Dombey’s advancing a step or two to meet her.\n\n“I can never forget this occasion, Sir,” said Miss Tox, softly. “’Tis impossible. My dear Louisa, I can hardly believe the evidence of my senses.”\n\nIf Miss Tox could believe the evidence of one of her senses, it was a very cold day. That was quite clear. She took an early opportunity of promoting the circulation in the tip of her nose by secretly chafing it with her pocket handkerchief, lest, by its very low temperature, it should disagreeably astonish the baby when she came to kiss it.\n\nThe baby soon appeared, carried in great glory by Richards; while Florence, in custody of that active young constable, Susan Nipper, brought up the rear. Though the whole nursery party were dressed by this time in lighter mourning than at first, there was enough in the appearance of the bereaved children to make the day no brighter. The baby too—it might have been Miss Tox’s nose—began to cry. Thereby, as it happened, preventing Mr Chick from the awkward fulfilment of a very honest purpose he had; which was, to make much of Florence. For this gentleman, insensible to the superior claims of a perfect Dombey (perhaps on account of having the honour to be united to a Dombey himself, and being familiar with excellence), really liked her, and showed that he liked her, and was about to show it in his own way now, when Paul cried, and his helpmate stopped him short—\n\n“Now Florence, child!” said her aunt, briskly, “what are you doing, love? Show yourself to him. Engage his attention, my dear!”\n\nThe atmosphere became or might have become colder and colder, when Mr Dombey stood frigidly watching his little daughter, who, clapping her hands, and standing on tip-toe before the throne of his son and heir, lured him to bend down from his high estate, and look at her. Some honest act of Richards’s may have aided the effect, but he did look down, and held his peace. As his sister hid behind her nurse, he followed her with his eyes; and when she peeped out with a merry cry to him, he sprang up and crowed lustily—laughing outright when she ran in upon him; and seeming to fondle her curls with his tiny hands, while she smothered him with kisses.\n\nWas Mr Dombey pleased to see this? He testified no pleasure by the relaxation of a nerve; but outward tokens of any kind of feeling were unusual with him. If any sunbeam stole into the room to light the children at their play, it never reached his face. He looked on so fixedly and coldly, that the warm light vanished even from the laughing eyes of little Florence, when, at last, they happened to meet his.\n\nIt was a dull, grey, autumn day indeed, and in a minute’s pause and silence that took place, the leaves fell sorrowfully.\n\n“Mr John,” said Mr Dombey, referring to his watch, and assuming his hat and gloves. “Take my sister, if you please: my arm today is Miss Tox’s. You had better go first with Master Paul, Richards. Be very careful.”\n\nIn Mr Dombey’s carriage, Dombey and Son, Miss Tox, Mrs Chick, Richards, and Florence. In a little carriage following it, Susan Nipper and the owner Mr Chick. Susan looking out of window, without intermission, as a relief from the embarrassment of confronting the large face of that gentleman, and thinking whenever anything rattled that he was putting up in paper an appropriate pecuniary compliment for herself.\n\nOnce upon the road to church, Mr Dombey clapped his hands for the amusement of his son. At which instance of parental enthusiasm Miss Tox was enchanted. But exclusive of this incident, the chief difference between the christening party and a party in a mourning coach consisted in the colours of the carriage and horses.\n\nArrived at the church steps, they were received by a portentous beadle. Mr Dombey dismounting first to help the ladies out, and standing near him at the church door, looked like another beadle. A beadle less gorgeous but more dreadful; the beadle of private life; the beadle of our business and our bosoms.\n\nMiss Tox’s hand trembled as she slipped it through Mr Dombey’s arm, and felt herself escorted up the steps, preceded by a cocked hat and a Babylonian collar. It seemed for a moment like that other solemn institution, “Wilt thou have this man, Lucretia?” “Yes, I will.”\n\n“Please to bring the child in quick out of the air there,” whispered the beadle, holding open the inner door of the church.\n\nLittle Paul might have asked with Hamlet “into my grave?” so chill and earthy was the place. The tall, shrouded pulpit and reading desk; the dreary perspective of empty pews stretching away under the galleries, and empty benches mounting to the roof and lost in the shadow of the great grim organ; the dusty matting and cold stone slabs; the grisly free seats in the aisles; and the damp corner by the bell-rope, where the black trestles used for funerals were stowed away, along with some shovels and baskets, and a coil or two of deadly-looking rope; the strange, unusual, uncomfortable smell, and the cadaverous light; were all in unison. It was a cold and dismal scene.\n\n“There’s a wedding just on, Sir,” said the beadle, “but it’ll be over directly, if you’ll walk into the westry here.”\n\nBefore he turned again to lead the way, he gave Mr Dombey a bow and a half smile of recognition, importing that he (the beadle) remembered to have had the pleasure of attending on him when he buried his wife, and hoped he had enjoyed himself since.\n\nThe very wedding looked dismal as they passed in front of the altar. The bride was too old and the bridegroom too young, and a superannuated beau with one eye and an eyeglass stuck in its blank companion, was giving away the lady, while the friends were shivering. In the vestry the fire was smoking; and an over-aged and over-worked and under-paid attorney’s clerk, “making a search,” was running his forefinger down the parchment pages of an immense register (one of a long series of similar volumes) gorged with burials. Over the fireplace was a ground-plan of the vaults underneath the church; and Mr Chick, skimming the literary portion of it aloud, by way of enlivening the company, read the reference to Mrs Dombey’s tomb in full, before he could stop himself.\n\nAfter another cold interval, a wheezy little pew-opener afflicted with an asthma, appropriate to the churchyard, if not to the church, summoned them to the font—a rigid marble basin which seemed to have been playing a churchyard game at cup and ball with its matter of fact pedestal, and to have been just that moment caught on the top of it. Here they waited some little time while the marriage party enrolled themselves; and meanwhile the wheezy little pew-opener—partly in consequence of her infirmity, and partly that the marriage party might not forget her—went about the building coughing like a grampus.\n\nPresently the clerk (the only cheerful-looking object there, and he was an undertaker) came up with a jug of warm water, and said something, as he poured it into the font, about taking the chill off; which millions of gallons boiling hot could not have done for the occasion. Then the clergyman, an amiable and mild-looking young curate, but obviously afraid of the baby, appeared like the principal character in a ghost-story, “a tall figure all in white;” at sight of whom Paul rent the air with his cries, and never left off again till he was taken out black in the face.\n\nEven when that event had happened, to the great relief of everybody, he was heard under the portico, during the rest of the ceremony, now fainter, now louder, now hushed, now bursting forth again with an irrepressible sense of his wrongs. This so distracted the attention of the two ladies, that Mrs Chick was constantly deploying into the centre aisle, to send out messages by the pew-opener, while Miss Tox kept her Prayer-book open at the Gunpowder Plot, and occasionally read responses from that service.\n\nDuring the whole of these proceedings, Mr Dombey remained as impassive and gentlemanly as ever, and perhaps assisted in making it so cold, that the young curate smoked at the mouth as he read. The only time that he unbent his visage in the least, was when the clergyman, in delivering (very unaffectedly and simply) the closing exhortation, relative to the future examination of the child by the sponsors, happened to rest his eye on Mr Chick; and then Mr Dombey might have been seen to express by a majestic look, that he would like to catch him at it.\n\nIt might have been well for Mr Dombey, if he had thought of his own dignity a little less; and had thought of the great origin and purpose of the ceremony in which he took so formal and so stiff a part, a little more. His arrogance contrasted strangely with its history.\n\nWhen it was all over, he again gave his arm to Miss Tox, and conducted her to the vestry, where he informed the clergyman how much pleasure it would have given him to have solicited the honour of his company at dinner, but for the unfortunate state of his household affairs. The register signed, and the fees paid, and the pew-opener (whose cough was very bad again) remembered, and the beadle gratified, and the sexton (who was accidentally on the doorsteps, looking with great interest at the weather) not forgotten, they got into the carriage again, and drove home in the same bleak fellowship.\n\nThere they found Mr Pitt turning up his nose at a cold collation, set forth in a cold pomp of glass and silver, and looking more like a dead dinner lying in state than a social refreshment. On their arrival Miss Tox produced a mug for her godson, and Mr Chick a knife and fork and spoon in a case. Mr Dombey also produced a bracelet for Miss Tox; and, on the receipt of this token, Miss Tox was tenderly affected.\n\n“Mr John,” said Mr Dombey, “will you take the bottom of the table, if you please? What have you got there, Mr John?”\n\n“I have got a cold fillet of veal here, Sir,” replied Mr Chick, rubbing his numbed hands hard together. “What have you got there, Sir?”\n\n“This,” returned Mr Dombey, “is some cold preparation of calf’s head, I think. I see cold fowls—ham—patties—salad—lobster. Miss Tox will do me the honour of taking some wine? Champagne to Miss Tox.”\n\nThere was a toothache in everything. The wine was so bitter cold that it forced a little scream from Miss Tox, which she had great difficulty in turning into a “Hem!” The veal had come from such an airy pantry, that the first taste of it had struck a sensation as of cold lead to Mr Chick’s extremities. Mr Dombey alone remained unmoved. He might have been hung up for sale at a Russian fair as a specimen of a frozen gentleman.\n\nThe prevailing influence was too much even for his sister. She made no effort at flattery or small talk, and directed all her efforts to looking as warm as she could.\n\n“Well, Sir,” said Mr Chick, making a desperate plunge, after a long silence, and filling a glass of sherry; “I shall drink this, if you’ll allow me, Sir, to little Paul.”\n\n“Bless him!” murmured Miss Tox, taking a sip of wine.\n\n“Dear little Dombey!” murmured Mrs Chick.\n\n“Mr John,” said Mr Dombey, with severe gravity, “my son would feel and express himself obliged to you, I have no doubt, if he could appreciate the favour you have done him. He will prove, in time to come, I trust, equal to any responsibility that the obliging disposition of his relations and friends, in private, or the onerous nature of our position, in public, may impose upon him.”\n\nThe tone in which this was said admitting of nothing more, Mr Chick relapsed into low spirits and silence. Not so Miss Tox, who, having listened to Mr Dombey with even a more emphatic attention than usual, and with a more expressive tendency of her head to one side, now leant across the table, and said to Mrs Chick softly:\n\n“Louisa!”\n\n“My dear,” said Mrs Chick.\n\n“Onerous nature of our position in public may—I have forgotten the exact term.”\n\n“Expose him to,” said Mrs Chick.\n\n“Pardon me, my dear,” returned Miss Tox, “I think not. It was more rounded and flowing. Obliging disposition of relations and friends in private, or onerous nature of position in public—may—impose upon him!”\n\n“Impose upon him, to be sure,” said Mrs Chick.\n\nMiss Tox struck her delicate hands together lightly, in triumph; and added, casting up her eyes, “eloquence indeed!”\n\nMr Dombey, in the meanwhile, had issued orders for the attendance of Richards, who now entered curtseying, but without the baby; Paul being asleep after the fatigues of the morning. Mr Dombey, having delivered a glass of wine to this vassal, addressed her in the following words: Miss Tox previously settling her head on one side, and making other little arrangements for engraving them on her heart.\n\n“During the six months or so, Richards, which have seen you an inmate of this house, you have done your duty. Desiring to connect some little service to you with this occasion, I considered how I could best effect that object, and I also advised with my sister, Mrs—”\n\n“Chick,” interposed the gentleman of that name.\n\n“Oh, hush if you please!” said Miss Tox.\n\n“I was about to say to you, Richards,” resumed Mr Dombey, with an appalling glance at Mr John, “that I was further assisted in my decision, by the recollection of a conversation I held with your husband in this room, on the occasion of your being hired, when he disclosed to me the melancholy fact that your family, himself at the head, were sunk and steeped in ignorance.”\n\nRichards quailed under the magnificence of the reproof.\n\n“I am far from being friendly,” pursued Mr Dombey, “to what is called by persons of levelling sentiments, general education. But it is necessary that the inferior classes should continue to be taught to know their position, and to conduct themselves properly. So far I approve of schools. Having the power of nominating a child on the foundation of an ancient establishment, called (from a worshipful company) the Charitable Grinders; where not only is a wholesome education bestowed upon the scholars, but where a dress and badge is likewise provided for them; I have (first communicating, through Mrs Chick, with your family) nominated your eldest son to an existing vacancy; and he has this day, I am informed, assumed the habit. The number of her son, I believe,” said Mr Dombey, turning to his sister and speaking of the child as if he were a hackney-coach, is one hundred and forty-seven. Louisa, you can tell her.”\n\n“One hundred and forty-seven,” said Mrs Chick “The dress, Richards, is a nice, warm, blue baize tailed coat and cap, turned up with orange coloured binding; red worsted stockings; and very strong leather small-clothes. One might wear the articles one’s self,” said Mrs Chick, with enthusiasm, “and be grateful.”\n\n“There, Richards!” said Miss Tox. “Now, indeed, you may be proud. The Charitable Grinders!”\n\n“I am sure I am very much obliged, Sir,” returned Richards faintly, “and take it very kind that you should remember my little ones.” At the same time a vision of Biler as a Charitable Grinder, with his very small legs encased in the serviceable clothing described by Mrs Chick, swam before Richards’s eyes, and made them water.\n\n“I am very glad to see you have so much feeling, Richards,” said Miss Tox.\n\n“It makes one almost hope, it really does,” said Mrs Chick, who prided herself on taking trustful views of human nature, “that there may yet be some faint spark of gratitude and right feeling in the world.”\n\nRichards deferred to these compliments by curtseying and murmuring her thanks; but finding it quite impossible to recover her spirits from the disorder into which they had been thrown by the image of her son in his precocious nether garments, she gradually approached the door and was heartily relieved to escape by it.\n\nSuch temporary indications of a partial thaw that had appeared with her, vanished with her; and the frost set in again, as cold and hard as ever. Mr Chick was twice heard to hum a tune at the bottom of the table, but on both occasions it was a fragment of the Dead March in Saul. The party seemed to get colder and colder, and to be gradually resolving itself into a congealed and solid state, like the collation round which it was assembled. At length Mrs Chick looked at Miss Tox, and Miss Tox returned the look, and they both rose and said it was really time to go. Mr Dombey receiving this announcement with perfect equanimity, they took leave of that gentleman, and presently departed under the protection of Mr Chick; who, when they had turned their backs upon the house and left its master in his usual solitary state, put his hands in his pockets, threw himself back in the carriage, and whistled “With a hey ho chevy!” all through; conveying into his face as he did so, an expression of such gloomy and terrible defiance, that Mrs Chick dared not protest, or in any way molest him.\n\nRichards, though she had little Paul on her lap, could not forget her own first-born. She felt it was ungrateful; but the influence of the day fell even on the Charitable Grinders, and she could hardly help regarding his pewter badge, number one hundred and forty-seven, as, somehow, a part of its formality and sternness. She spoke, too, in the nursery, of his “blessed legs,” and was again troubled by his spectre in uniform.\n\n“I don’t know what I wouldn’t give,” said Polly, “to see the poor little dear before he gets used to ’em.”\n\n“Why, then, I tell you what, Mrs Richards,” retorted Nipper, who had been admitted to her confidence, “see him and make your mind easy.”\n\n“Mr Dombey wouldn’t like it,” said Polly.\n\n“Oh, wouldn’t he, Mrs Richards!” retorted Nipper, “he’d like it very much, I think when he was asked.”\n\n“You wouldn’t ask him, I suppose, at all?” said Polly.\n\n“No, Mrs Richards, quite contrairy,” returned Susan, “and them two inspectors Tox and Chick, not intending to be on duty tomorrow, as I heard ’em say, me and Miss Floy will go along with you tomorrow morning, and welcome, Mrs Richards, if you like, for we may as well walk there as up and down a street, and better too.”\n\nPolly rejected the idea pretty stoutly at first; but by little and little she began to entertain it, as she entertained more and more distinctly the forbidden pictures of her children, and her own home. At length, arguing that there could be no great harm in calling for a moment at the door, she yielded to the Nipper proposition.\n\nThe matter being settled thus, little Paul began to cry most piteously, as if he had a foreboding that no good would come of it.\n\n“What’s the matter with the child?” asked Susan.\n\n“He’s cold, I think,” said Polly, walking with him to and fro, and hushing him.\n\nIt was a bleak autumnal afternoon indeed; and as she walked, and hushed, and, glancing through the dreary windows, pressed the little fellow closer to her breast, the withered leaves came showering down.\n\n# CHAPTER VI. Paul’s Second Deprivation\n\nPolly was beset by so many misgivings in the morning, that but for the incessant promptings of her black-eyed companion, she would have abandoned all thoughts of the expedition, and formally petitioned for leave to see number one hundred and forty-seven, under the awful shadow of Mr Dombey’s roof. But Susan who was personally disposed in favour of the excursion, and who (like Tony Lumpkin), if she could bear the disappointments of other people with tolerable fortitude, could not abide to disappoint herself, threw so many ingenious doubts in the way of this second thought, and stimulated the original intention with so many ingenious arguments, that almost as soon as Mr Dombey’s stately back was turned, and that gentleman was pursuing his daily road towards the City, his unconscious son was on his way to Staggs’s Gardens.\n\nThis euphonious locality was situated in a suburb, known by the inhabitants of Staggs’s Gardens by the name of Camberling Town; a designation which the Strangers’ Map of London, as printed (with a view to pleasant and commodious reference) on pocket handkerchiefs, condenses, with some show of reason, into Camden Town. Hither the two nurses bent their steps, accompanied by their charges; Richards carrying Paul, of course, and Susan leading little Florence by the hand, and giving her such jerks and pokes from time to time, as she considered it wholesome to administer.\n\nThe first shock of a great earthquake had, just at that period, rent the whole neighbourhood to its centre. Traces of its course were visible on every side. Houses were knocked down; streets broken through and stopped; deep pits and trenches dug in the ground; enormous heaps of earth and clay thrown up; buildings that were undermined and shaking, propped by great beams of wood. Here, a chaos of carts, overthrown and jumbled together, lay topsy-turvy at the bottom of a steep unnatural hill; there, confused treasures of iron soaked and rusted in something that had accidentally become a pond. Everywhere were bridges that led nowhere; thoroughfares that were wholly impassable; Babel towers of chimneys, wanting half their height; temporary wooden houses and enclosures, in the most unlikely situations; carcases of ragged tenements, and fragments of unfinished walls and arches, and piles of scaffolding, and wildernesses of bricks, and giant forms of cranes, and tripods straddling above nothing. There were a hundred thousand shapes and substances of incompleteness, wildly mingled out of their places, upside down, burrowing in the earth, aspiring in the air, mouldering in the water, and unintelligible as any dream. Hot springs and fiery eruptions, the usual attendants upon earthquakes, lent their contributions of confusion to the scene. Boiling water hissed and heaved within dilapidated walls; whence, also, the glare and roar of flames came issuing forth; and mounds of ashes blocked up rights of way, and wholly changed the law and custom of the neighbourhood.\n\nIn short, the yet unfinished and unopened Railroad was in progress; and, from the very core of all this dire disorder, trailed smoothly away, upon its mighty course of civilisation and improvement.\n\nBut as yet, the neighbourhood was shy to own the Railroad. One or two bold speculators had projected streets; and one had built a little, but had stopped among the mud and ashes to consider farther of it. A bran-new Tavern, redolent of fresh mortar and size, and fronting nothing at all, had taken for its sign The Railway Arms; but that might be rash enterprise—and then it hoped to sell drink to the workmen. So, the Excavators’ House of Call had sprung up from a beer-shop; and the old-established Ham and Beef Shop had become the Railway Eating House, with a roast leg of pork daily, through interested motives of a similar immediate and popular description. Lodging-house keepers were favourable in like manner; and for the like reasons were not to be trusted. The general belief was very slow. There were frowzy fields, and cow-houses, and dunghills, and dustheaps, and ditches, and gardens, and summer-houses, and carpet-beating grounds, at the very door of the Railway. Little tumuli of oyster shells in the oyster season, and of lobster shells in the lobster season, and of broken crockery and faded cabbage leaves in all seasons, encroached upon its high places. Posts, and rails, and old cautions to trespassers, and backs of mean houses, and patches of wretched vegetation, stared it out of countenance. Nothing was the better for it, or thought of being so. If the miserable waste ground lying near it could have laughed, it would have laughed it to scorn, like many of the miserable neighbours.\n\nStaggs’s Gardens was uncommonly incredulous. It was a little row of houses, with little squalid patches of ground before them, fenced off with old doors, barrel staves, scraps of tarpaulin, and dead bushes; with bottomless tin kettles and exhausted iron fenders, thrust into the gaps. Here, the Staggs’s Gardeners trained scarlet beans, kept fowls and rabbits, erected rotten summer-houses (one was an old boat), dried clothes, and smoked pipes. Some were of opinion that Staggs’s Gardens derived its name from a deceased capitalist, one Mr Staggs, who had built it for his delectation. Others, who had a natural taste for the country, held that it dated from those rural times when the antlered herd, under the familiar denomination of Staggses, had resorted to its shady precincts. Be this as it may, Staggs’s Gardens was regarded by its population as a sacred grove not to be withered by Railroads; and so confident were they generally of its long outliving any such ridiculous inventions, that the master chimney-sweeper at the corner, who was understood to take the lead in the local politics of the Gardens, had publicly declared that on the occasion of the Railroad opening, if ever it did open, two of his boys should ascend the flues of his dwelling, with instructions to hail the failure with derisive cheers from the chimney-pots.\n\nTo this unhallowed spot, the very name of which had hitherto been carefully concealed from Mr Dombey by his sister, was little Paul now borne by Fate and Richards\n\n“That’s my house, Susan,” said Polly, pointing it out.\n\n“Is it, indeed, Mrs Richards?” said Susan, condescendingly.\n\n“And there’s my sister Jemima at the door, I do declare” cried Polly, “with my own sweet precious baby in her arms!”\n\nThe sight added such an extensive pair of wings to Polly’s impatience, that she set off down the Gardens at a run, and bouncing on Jemima, changed babies with her in a twinkling; to the unutterable astonishment of that young damsel, on whom the heir of the Dombeys seemed to have fallen from the clouds.\n\n“Why, Polly!” cried Jemima. “You! what a turn you have given me! who’d have thought it! come along in Polly! How well you do look to be sure! The children will go half wild to see you Polly, that they will.”\n\nThat they did, if one might judge from the noise they made, and the way in which they dashed at Polly and dragged her to a low chair in the chimney corner, where her own honest apple face became immediately the centre of a bunch of smaller pippins, all laying their rosy cheeks close to it, and all evidently the growth of the same tree. As to Polly, she was full as noisy and vehement as the children; and it was not until she was quite out of breath, and her hair was hanging all about her flushed face, and her new christening attire was very much dishevelled, that any pause took place in the confusion. Even then, the smallest Toodle but one remained in her lap, holding on tight with both arms round her neck; while the smallest Toodle but two mounted on the back of the chair, and made desperate efforts, with one leg in the air, to kiss her round the corner.\n\n“Look! there’s a pretty little lady come to see you,” said Polly; “and see how quiet she is! what a beautiful little lady, ain’t she?”\n\nThis reference to Florence, who had been standing by the door not unobservant of what passed, directed the attention of the younger branches towards her; and had likewise the happy effect of leading to the formal recognition of Miss Nipper, who was not quite free from a misgiving that she had been already slighted.\n\n“Oh do come in and sit down a minute, Susan, please,” said Polly. “This is my sister Jemima, this is. Jemima, I don’t know what I should ever do with myself, if it wasn’t for Susan Nipper; I shouldn’t be here now but for her.”\n\n“Oh do sit down, Miss Nipper, if you please,” quoth Jemima.\n\nSusan took the extreme corner of a chair, with a stately and ceremonious aspect.\n\n“I never was so glad to see anybody in all my life; now really I never was, Miss Nipper,” said Jemima.\n\nSusan relaxing, took a little more of the chair, and smiled graciously.\n\n“Do untie your bonnet-strings, and make yourself at home, Miss Nipper, please,” entreated Jemima. “I am afraid it’s a poorer place than you’re used to; but you’ll make allowances, I’m sure.”\n\nThe black-eyed was so softened by this deferential behaviour, that she caught up little Miss Toodle who was running past, and took her to Banbury Cross immediately.\n\n“But where’s my pretty boy?” said Polly. “My poor fellow? I came all this way to see him in his new clothes.”\n\n“Ah what a pity!” cried Jemima. “He’ll break his heart, when he hears his mother has been here. He’s at school, Polly.”\n\n“Gone already!”\n\n“Yes. He went for the first time yesterday, for fear he should lose any learning. But it’s half-holiday, Polly: if you could only stop till he comes home—you and Miss Nipper, leastways,” said Jemima, mindful in good time of the dignity of the black-eyed.\n\n“And how does he look, Jemima, bless him!” faltered Polly.\n\n“Well, really he don’t look so bad as you’d suppose,” returned Jemima.\n\n“Ah!” said Polly, with emotion, “I knew his legs must be too short.”\n\n“His legs is short,” returned Jemima; “especially behind; but they’ll get longer, Polly, every day.”\n\nIt was a slow, prospective kind of consolation; but the cheerfulness and good nature with which it was administered, gave it a value it did not intrinsically possess. After a moment’s silence, Polly asked, in a more sprightly manner:\n\n“And where’s Father, Jemima dear?”—for by that patriarchal appellation, Mr Toodle was generally known in the family.\n\n“There again!” said Jemima. “What a pity! Father took his dinner with him this morning, and isn’t coming home till night. But he’s always talking of you, Polly, and telling the children about you; and is the peaceablest, patientest, best-temperedest soul in the world, as he always was and will be!”\n\n“Thankee, Jemima,” cried the simple Polly; delighted by the speech, and disappointed by the absence.\n\n“Oh you needn’t thank me, Polly,” said her sister, giving her a sounding kiss upon the cheek, and then dancing little Paul cheerfully. “I say the same of you sometimes, and think it too.”\n\nIn spite of the double disappointment, it was impossible to regard in the light of a failure a visit which was greeted with such a reception; so the sisters talked hopefully about family matters, and about Biler, and about all his brothers and sisters: while the black-eyed, having performed several journeys to Banbury Cross and back, took sharp note of the furniture, the Dutch clock, the cupboard, the castle on the mantel-piece with red and green windows in it, susceptible of illumination by a candle-end within; and the pair of small black velvet kittens, each with a lady’s reticule in its mouth; regarded by the Staggs’s Gardeners as prodigies of imitative art. The conversation soon becoming general lest the black-eyed should go off at score and turn sarcastic, that young lady related to Jemima a summary of everything she knew concerning Mr Dombey, his prospects, family, pursuits, and character. Also an exact inventory of her personal wardrobe, and some account of her principal relations and friends. Having relieved her mind of these disclosures, she partook of shrimps and porter, and evinced a disposition to swear eternal friendship.\n\nLittle Florence herself was not behind-hand in improving the occasion; for, being conducted forth by the young Toodles to inspect some toad-stools and other curiosities of the Gardens, she entered with them, heart and soul, on the formation of a temporary breakwater across a small green pool that had collected in a corner. She was still busily engaged in that labour, when sought and found by Susan; who, such was her sense of duty, even under the humanizing influence of shrimps, delivered a moral address to her (punctuated with thumps) on her degenerate nature, while washing her face and hands; and predicted that she would bring the grey hairs of her family in general, with sorrow to the grave. After some delay, occasioned by a pretty long confidential interview above stairs on pecuniary subjects, between Polly and Jemima, an interchange of babies was again effected—for Polly had all this time retained her own child, and Jemima little Paul—and the visitors took leave.\n\nBut first the young Toodles, victims of a pious fraud, were deluded into repairing in a body to a chandler’s shop in the neighbourhood, for the ostensible purpose of spending a penny; and when the coast was quite clear, Polly fled: Jemima calling after her that if they could only go round towards the City Road on their way back, they would be sure to meet little Biler coming from school.\n\n“Do you think that we might make time to go a little round in that direction, Susan?” inquired Polly, when they halted to take breath.\n\n“Why not, Mrs Richards?” returned Susan.\n\n“It’s getting on towards our dinner time you know,” said Polly.\n\nBut lunch had rendered her companion more than indifferent to this grave consideration, so she allowed no weight to it, and they resolved to go “a little round.”\n\nNow, it happened that poor Biler’s life had been, since yesterday morning, rendered weary by the costume of the Charitable Grinders. The youth of the streets could not endure it. No young vagabond could be brought to bear its contemplation for a moment, without throwing himself upon the unoffending wearer, and doing him a mischief. His social existence had been more like that of an early Christian, than an innocent child of the nineteenth century. He had been stoned in the streets. He had been overthrown into gutters; bespattered with mud; violently flattened against posts. Entire strangers to his person had lifted his yellow cap off his head, and cast it to the winds. His legs had not only undergone verbal criticisms and revilings, but had been handled and pinched. That very morning, he had received a perfectly unsolicited black eye on his way to the Grinders’ establishment, and had been punished for it by the master: a superannuated old Grinder of savage disposition, who had been appointed schoolmaster because he didn’t know anything, and wasn’t fit for anything, and for whose cruel cane all chubby little boys had a perfect fascination.\n\nThus it fell out that Biler, on his way home, sought unfrequented paths; and slunk along by narrow passages and back streets, to avoid his tormentors. Being compelled to emerge into the main road, his ill fortune brought him at last where a small party of boys, headed by a ferocious young butcher, were lying in wait for any means of pleasurable excitement that might happen. These, finding a Charitable Grinder in the midst of them—unaccountably delivered over, as it were, into their hands—set up a general yell and rushed upon him.\n\nBut it so fell out likewise, that, at the same time, Polly, looking hopelessly along the road before her, after a good hour’s walk, had said it was no use going any further, when suddenly she saw this sight. She no sooner saw it than, uttering a hasty exclamation, and giving Master Dombey to the black-eyed, she started to the rescue of her unhappy little son.\n\nSurprises, like misfortunes, rarely come alone. The astonished Susan Nipper and her two young charges were rescued by the bystanders from under the very wheels of a passing carriage before they knew what had happened; and at that moment (it was market day) a thundering alarm of “Mad Bull!” was raised.\n\nWith a wild confusion before her, of people running up and down, and shouting, and wheels running over them, and boys fighting, and mad bulls coming up, and the nurse in the midst of all these dangers being torn to pieces, Florence screamed and ran. She ran till she was exhausted, urging Susan to do the same; and then, stopping and wringing her hands as she remembered they had left the other nurse behind, found, with a sensation of terror not to be described, that she was quite alone.\n\n“Susan! Susan!” cried Florence, clapping her hands in the very ecstasy of her alarm. “Oh, where are they? where are they?”\n\n“Where are they?” said an old woman, coming hobbling across as fast as she could from the opposite side of the way. “Why did you run away from ’em?”\n\n“I was frightened,” answered Florence. “I didn’t know what I did. I thought they were with me. Where are they?”\n\nThe old woman took her by the wrist, and said, “I’ll show you.”\n\nShe was a very ugly old woman, with red rims round her eyes, and a mouth that mumbled and chattered of itself when she was not speaking. She was miserably dressed, and carried some skins over her arm. She seemed to have followed Florence some little way at all events, for she had lost her breath; and this made her uglier still, as she stood trying to regain it: working her shrivelled yellow face and throat into all sorts of contortions.\n\nFlorence was afraid of her, and looked, hesitating, up the street, of which she had almost reached the bottom. It was a solitary place—more a back road than a street—and there was no one in it but her-self and the old woman.\n\n“You needn’t be frightened now,” said the old woman, still holding her tight. “Come along with me.”\n\n“I—I don’t know you. What’s your name?” asked Florence.\n\n“Mrs Brown,” said the old woman. “Good Mrs Brown.”\n\n“Are they near here?” asked Florence, beginning to be led away.\n\n“Susan ain’t far off,” said Good Mrs Brown; “and the others are close to her.”\n\n“Is anybody hurt?” cried Florence.\n\n“Not a bit of it,” said Good Mrs Brown.\n\nThe child shed tears of delight on hearing this, and accompanied the old woman willingly; though she could not help glancing at her face as they went along—particularly at that industrious mouth—and wondering whether Bad Mrs Brown, if there were such a person, was at all like her.\n\nThey had not gone far, but had gone by some very uncomfortable places, such as brick-fields and tile-yards, when the old woman turned down a dirty lane, where the mud lay in deep black ruts in the middle of the road. She stopped before a shabby little house, as closely shut up as a house that was full of cracks and crevices could be. Opening the door with a key she took out of her bonnet, she pushed the child before her into a back room, where there was a great heap of rags of different colours lying on the floor; a heap of bones, and a heap of sifted dust or cinders; but there was no furniture at all, and the walls and ceiling were quite black.\n\nThe child became so terrified the she was stricken speechless, and looked as though about to swoon.\n\n“Now don’t be a young mule,” said Good Mrs Brown, reviving her with a shake. “I’m not a going to hurt you. Sit upon the rags.”\n\nFlorence obeyed her, holding out her folded hands, in mute supplication.\n\n“I’m not a going to keep you, even, above an hour,” said Mrs Brown. “D’ye understand what I say?”\n\nThe child answered with great difficulty, “Yes.”\n\n“Then,” said Good Mrs Brown, taking her own seat on the bones, “don’t vex me. If you don’t, I tell you I won’t hurt you. But if you do, I’ll kill you. I could have you killed at any time—even if you was in your own bed at home. Now let’s know who you are, and what you are, and all about it.”\n\nThe old woman’s threats and promises; the dread of giving her offence; and the habit, unusual to a child, but almost natural to Florence now, of being quiet, and repressing what she felt, and feared, and hoped; enabled her to do this bidding, and to tell her little history, or what she knew of it. Mrs Brown listened attentively, until she had finished.\n\n“So your name’s Dombey, eh?” said Mrs Brown.\n\n“I want that pretty frock, Miss Dombey,” said Good Mrs Brown, “and that little bonnet, and a petticoat or two, and anything else you can spare. Come! Take ’em off.”\n\nFlorence obeyed, as fast as her trembling hands would allow; keeping, all the while, a frightened eye on Mrs Brown. When she had divested herself of all the articles of apparel mentioned by that lady, Mrs B. examined them at leisure, and seemed tolerably well satisfied with their quality and value.\n\n“Humph!” she said, running her eyes over the child’s slight figure, “I don’t see anything else—except the shoes. I must have the shoes, Miss Dombey.”\n\nPoor little Florence took them off with equal alacrity, only too glad to have any more means of conciliation about her. The old woman then produced some wretched substitutes from the bottom of the heap of rags, which she turned up for that purpose; together with a girl’s cloak, quite worn out and very old; and the crushed remains of a bonnet that had probably been picked up from some ditch or dunghill. In this dainty raiment, she instructed Florence to dress herself; and as such preparation seemed a prelude to her release, the child complied with increased readiness, if possible.\n\nIn hurriedly putting on the bonnet, if that may be called a bonnet which was more like a pad to carry loads on, she caught it in her hair which grew luxuriantly, and could not immediately disentangle it. Good Mrs Brown whipped out a large pair of scissors, and fell into an unaccountable state of excitement.\n\n“Why couldn’t you let me be!” said Mrs Brown, “when I was contented? You little fool!”\n\n“I beg your pardon. I don’t know what I have done,” panted Florence. “I couldn’t help it.”\n\n“Couldn’t help it!” cried Mrs Brown. “How do you expect I can help it? Why, Lord!” said the old woman, ruffling her curls with a furious pleasure, “anybody but me would have had ’em off, first of all.”\n\nFlorence was so relieved to find that it was only her hair and not her head which Mrs Brown coveted, that she offered no resistance or entreaty, and merely raised her mild eyes towards the face of that good soul.\n\n“If I hadn’t once had a gal of my own—beyond seas now—that was proud of her hair,” said Mrs Brown, “I’d have had every lock of it. She’s far away, she’s far away! Oho! Oho!”\n\nMrs Brown’s was not a melodious cry, but, accompanied with a wild tossing up of her lean arms, it was full of passionate grief, and thrilled to the heart of Florence, whom it frightened more than ever. It had its part, perhaps, in saving her curls; for Mrs Brown, after hovering about her with the scissors for some moments, like a new kind of butterfly, bade her hide them under the bonnet and let no trace of them escape to tempt her. Having accomplished this victory over herself, Mrs Brown resumed her seat on the bones, and smoked a very short black pipe, mowing and mumbling all the time, as if she were eating the stem.\n\nWhen the pipe was smoked out, she gave the child a rabbit-skin to carry, that she might appear the more like her ordinary companion, and told her that she was now going to lead her to a public street whence she could inquire her way to her friends. But she cautioned her, with threats of summary and deadly vengeance in case of disobedience, not to talk to strangers, nor to repair to her own home (which may have been too near for Mrs Brown’s convenience), but to her father’s office in the City; also to wait at the street corner where she would be left, until the clock struck three. These directions Mrs Brown enforced with assurances that there would be potent eyes and ears in her employment cognizant of all she did; and these directions Florence promised faithfully and earnestly to observe.\n\nAt length, Mrs Brown, issuing forth, conducted her changed and ragged little friend through a labyrinth of narrow streets and lanes and alleys, which emerged, after a long time, upon a stable yard, with a gateway at the end, whence the roar of a great thoroughfare made itself audible. Pointing out this gateway, and informing Florence that when the clocks struck three she was to go to the left, Mrs Brown, after making a parting grasp at her hair which seemed involuntary and quite beyond her own control, told her she knew what to do, and bade her go and do it: remembering that she was watched.\n\nWith a lighter heart, but still sore afraid, Florence felt herself released, and tripped off to the corner. When she reached it, she looked back and saw the head of Good Mrs Brown peeping out of the low wooden passage, where she had issued her parting injunctions; likewise the fist of Good Mrs Brown shaking towards her. But though she often looked back afterwards—every minute, at least, in her nervous recollection of the old woman—she could not see her again.\n\nFlorence remained there, looking at the bustle in the street, and more and more bewildered by it; and in the meanwhile the clocks appeared to have made up their minds never to strike three any more. At last the steeples rang out three o’clock; there was one close by, so she couldn’t be mistaken; and—after often looking over her shoulder, and often going a little way, and as often coming back again, lest the all-powerful spies of Mrs Brown should take offence—she hurried off, as fast as she could in her slipshod shoes, holding the rabbit-skin tight in her hand.\n\nAll she knew of her father’s offices was that they belonged to Dombey and Son, and that that was a great power belonging to the City. So she could only ask the way to Dombey and Son’s in the City; and as she generally made inquiry of children—being afraid to ask grown people—she got very little satisfaction indeed. But by dint of asking her way to the City after a while, and dropping the rest of her inquiry for the present, she really did advance, by slow degrees, towards the heart of that great region which is governed by the terrible Lord Mayor.\n\nTired of walking, repulsed and pushed about, stunned by the noise and confusion, anxious for her brother and the nurses, terrified by what she had undergone, and the prospect of encountering her angry father in such an altered state; perplexed and frightened alike by what had passed, and what was passing, and what was yet before her; Florence went upon her weary way with tearful eyes, and once or twice could not help stopping to ease her bursting heart by crying bitterly. But few people noticed her at those times, in the garb she wore: or if they did, believed that she was tutored to excite compassion, and passed on. Florence, too, called to her aid all the firmness and self-reliance of a character that her sad experience had prematurely formed and tried: and keeping the end she had in view steadily before her, steadily pursued it.\n\nIt was full two hours later in the afternoon than when she had started on this strange adventure, when, escaping from the clash and clangour of a narrow street full of carts and waggons, she peeped into a kind of wharf or landing-place upon the river-side, where there were a great many packages, casks, and boxes, strewn about; a large pair of wooden scales; and a little wooden house on wheels, outside of which, looking at the neighbouring masts and boats, a stout man stood whistling, with his pen behind his ear, and his hands in his pockets, as if his day’s work were nearly done.\n\n“Now then!” said this man, happening to turn round. “We haven’t got anything for you, little girl. Be off!”\n\n“If you please, is this the City?” asked the trembling daughter of the Dombeys.\n\n“Ah! It’s the City. You know that well enough, I daresay. Be off! We haven’t got anything for you.”\n\n“I don’t want anything, thank you,” was the timid answer. “Except to know the way to Dombey and Son’s.”\n\nThe man who had been strolling carelessly towards her, seemed surprised by this reply, and looking attentively in her face, rejoined:\n\n“Why, what can you want with Dombey and Son’s?”\n\n“To know the way there, if you please.”\n\nThe man looked at her yet more curiously, and rubbed the back of his head so hard in his wonderment that he knocked his own hat off.\n\n“Joe!” he called to another man—a labourer—as he picked it up and put it on again.\n\n“Joe it is!” said Joe.\n\n“Where’s that young spark of Dombey’s who’s been watching the shipment of them goods?”\n\n“Just gone, by tt’other gate,” said Joe.\n\n“Call him back a minute.”\n\nJoe ran up an archway, bawling as he went, and very soon returned with a blithe-looking boy.\n\n“You’re Dombey’s jockey, ain’t you?” said the first man.\n\n“I’m in Dombey’s House, Mr Clark,” returned the boy.\n\n“Look’ye here, then,” said Mr Clark.\n\nObedient to the indication of Mr Clark’s hand, the boy approached towards Florence, wondering, as well he might, what he had to do with her. But she, who had heard what passed, and who, besides the relief of so suddenly considering herself safe at her journey’s end, felt reassured beyond all measure by his lively youthful face and manner, ran eagerly up to him, leaving one of the slipshod shoes upon the ground and caught his hand in both of hers.\n\n“I am lost, if you please!” said Florence.\n\n“Lost!” cried the boy.\n\n“Yes, I was lost this morning, a long way from here—and I have had my clothes taken away, since—and I am not dressed in my own now—and my name is Florence Dombey, my little brother’s only sister—and, oh dear, dear, take care of me, if you please!” sobbed Florence, giving full vent to the childish feelings she had so long suppressed, and bursting into tears. At the same time her miserable bonnet falling off, her hair came tumbling down about her face: moving to speechless admiration and commiseration, young Walter, nephew of Solomon Gills, Ships’ Instrument-maker in general.\n\nMr Clark stood rapt in amazement: observing under his breath, I never saw such a start on this wharf before. Walter picked up the shoe, and put it on the little foot as the Prince in the story might have fitted Cinderella’s slipper on. He hung the rabbit-skin over his left arm; gave the right to Florence; and felt, not to say like Richard Whittington—that is a tame comparison—but like Saint George of England, with the dragon lying dead before him.\n\n“Don’t cry, Miss Dombey,” said Walter, in a transport of enthusiasm. “What a wonderful thing for me that I am here! You are as safe now as if you were guarded by a whole boat’s crew of picked men from a man-of-war. Oh, don’t cry.”\n\n“I won’t cry any more,” said Florence. “I am only crying for joy.”\n\n“Crying for joy!” thought Walter, “and I’m the cause of it! Come along, Miss Dombey. There’s the other shoe off now! Take mine, Miss Dombey.”\n\n“No, no, no,” said Florence, checking him in the act of impetuously pulling off his own. “These do better. These do very well.”\n\n“Why, to be sure,” said Walter, glancing at her foot, “mine are a mile too large. What am I thinking about! You never could walk in mine! Come along, Miss Dombey. Let me see the villain who will dare molest you now.”\n\nSo Walter, looking immensely fierce, led off Florence, looking very happy; and they went arm-in-arm along the streets, perfectly indifferent to any astonishment that their appearance might or did excite by the way.\n\nIt was growing dark and foggy, and beginning to rain too; but they cared nothing for this: being both wholly absorbed in the late adventures of Florence, which she related with the innocent good faith and confidence of her years, while Walter listened as if, far from the mud and grease of Thames Street, they were rambling alone among the broad leaves and tall trees of some desert island in the tropics—as he very likely fancied, for the time, they were.\n\n“Have we far to go?” asked Florence at last, lilting up her eyes to her companion’s face.\n\n“Ah! By-the-bye,” said Walter, stopping, “let me see; where are we? Oh! I know. But the offices are shut up now, Miss Dombey. There’s nobody there. Mr Dombey has gone home long ago. I suppose we must go home too? or, stay. Suppose I take you to my Uncle’s, where I live—it’s very near here—and go to your house in a coach to tell them you are safe, and bring you back some clothes. Won’t that be best?”\n\n“I think so,” answered Florence. “Don’t you? What do you think?”\n\nAs they stood deliberating in the street, a man passed them, who glanced quickly at Walter as he went by, as if he recognised him; but seeming to correct that first impression, he passed on without stopping.\n\n“Why, I think it’s Mr Carker,” said Walter. “Carker in our House. Not Carker our Manager, Miss Dombey—the other Carker; the Junior—Halloa! Mr Carker!”\n\n“Is that Walter Gay?” said the other, stopping and returning. “I couldn’t believe it, with such a strange companion.”\n\nAs he stood near a lamp, listening with surprise to Walter’s hurried explanation, he presented a remarkable contrast to the two youthful figures arm-in-arm before him. He was not old, but his hair was white; his body was bent, or bowed as if by the weight of some great trouble: and there were deep lines in his worn and melancholy face. The fire of his eyes, the expression of his features, the very voice in which he spoke, were all subdued and quenched, as if the spirit within him lay in ashes. He was respectably, though very plainly dressed, in black; but his clothes, moulded to the general character of his figure, seemed to shrink and abase themselves upon him, and to join in the sorrowful solicitation which the whole man from head to foot expressed, to be left unnoticed, and alone in his humility.\n\nAnd yet his interest in youth and hopefulness was not extinguished with the other embers of his soul, for he watched the boy’s earnest countenance as he spoke with unusual sympathy, though with an inexplicable show of trouble and compassion, which escaped into his looks, however hard he strove to hold it prisoner. When Walter, in conclusion, put to him the question he had put to Florence, he still stood glancing at him with the same expression, as if he had read some fate upon his face, mournfully at variance with its present brightness.\n\n“What do you advise, Mr Carker?” said Walter, smiling. “You always give me good advice, you know, when you do speak to me. That’s not often, though.”\n\n“I think your own idea is the best,” he answered: looking from Florence to Walter, and back again.\n\n“Mr Carker,” said Walter, brightening with a generous thought, “Come! Here’s a chance for you. Go you to Mr Dombey’s, and be the messenger of good news. It may do you some good, Sir. I’ll remain at home. You shall go.”\n\n“I!” returned the other.\n\n“Yes. Why not, Mr Carker?” said the boy.\n\nHe merely shook him by the hand in answer; he seemed in a manner ashamed and afraid even to do that; and bidding him good-night, and advising him to make haste, turned away.\n\n“Come, Miss Dombey,” said Walter, looking after him as they turned away also, “we’ll go to my Uncle’s as quick as we can. Did you ever hear Mr Dombey speak of Mr Carker the Junior, Miss Florence?”\n\n“No,” returned the child, mildly, “I don’t often hear Papa speak.”\n\n“Ah! true! more shame for him,” thought Walter. After a minute’s pause, during which he had been looking down upon the gentle patient little face moving on at his side, he said, “The strangest man, Mr Carker the Junior is, Miss Florence, that ever you heard of. If you could understand what an extraordinary interest he takes in me, and yet how he shuns me and avoids me; and what a low place he holds in our office, and how he is never advanced, and never complains, though year after year he sees young men passed over his head, and though his brother (younger than he is), is our head Manager, you would be as much puzzled about him as I am.”\n\nAs Florence could hardly be expected to understand much about it, Walter bestirred himself with his accustomed boyish animation and restlessness to change the subject; and one of the unfortunate shoes coming off again opportunely, proposed to carry Florence to his uncle’s in his arms. Florence, though very tired, laughingly declined the proposal, lest he should let her fall; and as they were already near the wooden Midshipman, and as Walter went on to cite various precedents, from shipwrecks and other moving accidents, where younger boys than he had triumphantly rescued and carried off older girls than Florence, they were still in full conversation about it when they arrived at the Instrument-maker’s door.\n\n“Holloa, Uncle Sol!” cried Walter, bursting into the shop, and speaking incoherently and out of breath, from that time forth, for the rest of the evening. “Here’s a wonderful adventure! Here’s Mr Dombey’s daughter lost in the streets, and robbed of her clothes by an old witch of a woman—found by me—brought home to our parlour to rest—look here!”\n\n“Good Heaven!” said Uncle Sol, starting back against his favourite compass-case. “It can’t be! Well, I—”\n\n“No, nor anybody else,” said Walter, anticipating the rest. “Nobody would, nobody could, you know. Here! just help me lift the little sofa near the fire, will you, Uncle Sol—take care of the plates—cut some dinner for her, will you, Uncle—throw those shoes under the grate. Miss Florence—put your feet on the fender to dry—how damp they are—here’s an adventure, Uncle, eh?—God bless my soul, how hot I am!”\n\nSolomon Gills was quite as hot, by sympathy, and in excessive bewilderment. He patted Florence’s head, pressed her to eat, pressed her to drink, rubbed the soles of her feet with his pocket-handkerchief heated at the fire, followed his locomotive nephew with his eyes, and ears, and had no clear perception of anything except that he was being constantly knocked against and tumbled over by that excited young gentleman, as he darted about the room attempting to accomplish twenty things at once, and doing nothing at all.\n\n“Here, wait a minute, Uncle,” he continued, catching up a candle, “till I run upstairs, and get another jacket on, and then I’ll be off. I say, Uncle, isn’t this an adventure?”\n\n“My dear boy,” said Solomon, who, with his spectacles on his forehead and the great chronometer in his pocket, was incessantly oscillating between Florence on the sofa, and his nephew in all parts of the parlour, “it’s the most extraordinary—”\n\n“No, but do, Uncle, please—do, Miss Florence—dinner, you know, Uncle.”\n\n“Yes, yes, yes,” cried Solomon, cutting instantly into a leg of mutton, as if he were catering for a giant. “I’ll take care of her, Wally! I understand. Pretty dear! Famished, of course. You go and get ready. Lord bless me! Sir Richard Whittington thrice Lord Mayor of London.”\n\nWalter was not very long in mounting to his lofty garret and descending from it, but in the meantime Florence, overcome by fatigue, had sunk into a doze before the fire. The short interval of quiet, though only a few minutes in duration, enabled Solomon Gills so far to collect his wits as to make some little arrangements for her comfort, and to darken the room, and to screen her from the blaze. Thus, when the boy returned, she was sleeping peacefully.\n\n“That’s capital!” he whispered, giving Solomon such a hug that it squeezed a new expression into his face. “Now I’m off. I’ll just take a crust of bread with me, for I’m very hungry—and don’t wake her, Uncle Sol.”\n\n“No, no,” said Solomon. “Pretty child.”\n\n“Pretty, indeed!” cried Walter. “I never saw such a face, Uncle Sol. Now I’m off.”\n\n“That’s right,” said Solomon, greatly relieved.\n\n“I say, Uncle Sol,” cried Walter, putting his face in at the door.\n\n“Here he is again,” said Solomon.\n\n“How does she look now?”\n\n“Quite happy,” said Solomon.\n\n“That’s famous! now I’m off.”\n\n“I hope you are,” said Solomon to himself.\n\n“I say, Uncle Sol,” cried Walter, reappearing at the door.\n\n“Here he is again!” said Solomon.\n\n“We met Mr Carker the Junior in the street, queerer than ever. He bade me good-bye, but came behind us here—there’s an odd thing!—for when we reached the shop door, I looked round, and saw him going quietly away, like a servant who had seen me home, or a faithful dog. How does she look now, Uncle?”\n\n“Pretty much the same as before, Wally,” replied Uncle Sol.\n\n“That’s right. Now I am off!”\n\nAnd this time he really was: and Solomon Gills, with no appetite for dinner, sat on the opposite side of the fire, watching Florence in her slumber, building a great many airy castles of the most fantastic architecture; and looking, in the dim shade, and in the close vicinity of all the instruments, like a magician disguised in a Welsh wig and a suit of coffee colour, who held the child in an enchanted sleep.\n\nIn the meantime, Walter proceeded towards Mr Dombey’s house at a pace seldom achieved by a hack horse from the stand; and yet with his head out of window every two or three minutes, in impatient remonstrance with the driver. Arriving at his journey’s end, he leaped out, and breathlessly announcing his errand to the servant, followed him straight into the library, we there was a great confusion of tongues, and where Mr Dombey, his sister, and Miss Tox, Richards, and Nipper, were all congregated together.\n\n“Oh! I beg your pardon, Sir,” said Walter, rushing up to him, “but I’m happy to say it’s all right, Sir. Miss Dombey’s found!”\n\nThe boy with his open face, and flowing hair, and sparkling eyes, panting with pleasure and excitement, was wonderfully opposed to Mr Dombey, as he sat confronting him in his library chair.\n\n“I told you, Louisa, that she would certainly be found,” said Mr Dombey, looking slightly over his shoulder at that lady, who wept in company with Miss Tox. “Let the servants know that no further steps are necessary. This boy who brings the information, is young Gay, from the office. How was my daughter found, Sir? I know how she was lost.” Here he looked majestically at Richards. “But how was she found? Who found her?”\n\n“Why, I believe I found Miss Dombey, Sir,” said Walter modestly, “at least I don’t know that I can claim the merit of having exactly found her, Sir, but I was the fortunate instrument of—”\n\n“What do you mean, Sir,” interrupted Mr Dombey, regarding the boy’s evident pride and pleasure in his share of the transaction with an instinctive dislike, “by not having exactly found my daughter, and by being a fortunate instrument? Be plain and coherent, if you please.”\n\nIt was quite out of Walter’s power to be coherent; but he rendered himself as explanatory as he could, in his breathless state, and stated why he had come alone.\n\n“You hear this, girl?” said Mr Dombey sternly to the black-eyed. “Take what is necessary, and return immediately with this young man to fetch Miss Florence home. Gay, you will be rewarded to-morrow.”\n\n“Oh! thank you, Sir,” said Walter. “You are very kind. I’m sure I was not thinking of any reward, Sir.”\n\n“You are a boy,” said Mr Dombey, suddenly and almost fiercely; “and what you think of, or affect to think of, is of little consequence. You have done well, Sir. Don’t undo it. Louisa, please to give the lad some wine.”\n\nMr Dombey’s glance followed Walter Gay with sharp disfavour, as he left the room under the pilotage of Mrs Chick; and it may be that his mind’s eye followed him with no greater relish, as he rode back to his Uncle’s with Miss Susan Nipper.\n\nThere they found that Florence, much refreshed by sleep, had dined, and greatly improved the acquaintance of Solomon Gills, with whom she was on terms of perfect confidence and ease. The black-eyed (who had cried so much that she might now be called the red-eyed, and who was very silent and depressed) caught her in her arms without a word of contradiction or reproach, and made a very hysterical meeting of it. Then converting the parlour, for the nonce, into a private tiring room, she dressed her, with great care, in proper clothes; and presently led her forth, as like a Dombey as her natural disqualifications admitted of her being made.\n\n“Good-night!” said Florence, running up to Solomon. “You have been very good to me.”\n\nOld Sol was quite delighted, and kissed her like her grand-father.\n\n“Good-night, Walter! Good-bye!” said Florence.\n\n“Good-bye!” said Walter, giving both his hands.\n\n“I’ll never forget you,” pursued Florence. “No! indeed I never will. Good-bye, Walter!”\n\nIn the innocence of her grateful heart, the child lifted up her face to his. Walter, bending down his own, raised it again, all red and burning; and looked at Uncle Sol, quite sheepishly.\n\n“Where’s Walter?” “Good-night, Walter!” “Good-bye, Walter!” “Shake hands once more, Walter!” This was still Florence’s cry, after she was shut up with her little maid, in the coach. And when the coach at length moved off, Walter on the door-step gaily returned the waving of her handkerchief, while the wooden Midshipman behind him seemed, like himself, intent upon that coach alone, excluding all the other passing coaches from his observation.\n\nIn good time Mr Dombey’s mansion was gained again, and again there was a noise of tongues in the library. Again, too, the coach was ordered to wait—“for Mrs Richards,” one of Susan’s fellow-servants ominously whispered, as she passed with Florence.\n\nThe entrance of the lost child made a slight sensation, but not much. Mr Dombey, who had never found her, kissed her once upon the forehead, and cautioned her not to run away again, or wander anywhere with treacherous attendants. Mrs Chick stopped in her lamentations on the corruption of human nature, even when beckoned to the paths of virtue by a Charitable Grinder; and received her with a welcome something short of the reception due to none but perfect Dombeys. Miss Tox regulated her feelings by the models before her. Richards, the culprit Richards, alone poured out her heart in broken words of welcome, and bowed herself over the little wandering head as if she really loved it.\n\n“Ah, Richards!” said Mrs Chick, with a sigh. “It would have been much more satisfactory to those who wish to think well of their fellow creatures, and much more becoming in you, if you had shown some proper feeling, in time, for the little child that is now going to be prematurely deprived of its natural nourishment.\n\n“Cut off,” said Miss Tox, in a plaintive whisper, “from one common fountain!”\n\n“If it was my ungrateful case,” said Mrs Chick, solemnly, “and I had your reflections, Richards, I should feel as if the Charitable Grinders’ dress would blight my child, and the education choke him.”\n\nFor the matter of that—but Mrs Chick didn’t know it—he had been pretty well blighted by the dress already; and as to the education, even its retributive effect might be produced in time, for it was a storm of sobs and blows.\n\n“Louisa!” said Mr Dombey. “It is not necessary to prolong these observations. The woman is discharged and paid. You leave this house, Richards, for taking my son—my son,” said Mr Dombey, emphatically repeating these two words, “into haunts and into society which are not to be thought of without a shudder. As to the accident which befel Miss Florence this morning, I regard that as, in one great sense, a happy and fortunate circumstance; inasmuch as, but for that occurrence, I never could have known—and from your own lips too—of what you had been guilty. I think, Louisa, the other nurse, the young person,” here Miss Nipper sobbed aloud, “being so much younger, and necessarily influenced by Paul’s nurse, may remain. Have the goodness to direct that this woman’s coach is paid to”—Mr Dombey stopped and winced—“to Staggs’s Gardens.”\n\nPolly moved towards the door, with Florence holding to her dress, and crying to her in the most pathetic manner not to go away. It was a dagger in the haughty father’s heart, an arrow in his brain, to see how the flesh and blood he could not disown clung to this obscure stranger, and he sitting by. Not that he cared to whom his daughter turned, or from whom turned away. The swift sharp agony struck through him, as he thought of what his son might do.\n\nHis son cried lustily that night, at all events. Sooth to say, poor Paul had better reason for his tears than sons of that age often have, for he had lost his second mother—his first, so far as he knew—by a stroke as sudden as that natural affliction which had darkened the beginning of his life. At the same blow, his sister too, who cried herself to sleep so mournfully, had lost as good and true a friend. But that is quite beside the question. Let us waste no words about it.\n\nCHAPTER VII. A Bird’s-eye Glimpse of Miss Tox’s Dwelling-place: also of the State of Miss Tox’s Affections\n\nMiss Tox inhabited a dark little house that had been squeezed, at some remote period of English History, into a fashionable neighbourhood at the west end of the town, where it stood in the shade like a poor relation of the great street round the corner, coldly looked down upon by mighty mansions. It was not exactly in a court, and it was not exactly in a yard; but it was in the dullest of No-Thoroughfares, rendered anxious and haggard by distant double knocks. The name of this retirement, where grass grew between the chinks in the stone pavement, was Princess’s Place; and in Princess’s Place was Princess’s Chapel, with a tinkling bell, where sometimes as many as five-and-twenty people attended service on a Sunday. The Princess’s Arms was also there, and much resorted to by splendid footmen. A sedan chair was kept inside the railing before the Princess’s Arms, but it had never come out within the memory of man; and on fine mornings, the top of every rail (there were eight-and-forty, as Miss Tox had often counted) was decorated with a pewter-pot.\n\nThere was another private house besides Miss Tox’s in Princess’s Place: not to mention an immense Pair of gates, with an immense pair of lion-headed knockers on them, which were never opened by any chance, and were supposed to constitute a disused entrance to somebody’s stables. Indeed, there was a smack of stabling in the air of Princess’s Place; and Miss Tox’s bedroom (which was at the back) commanded a vista of Mews, where hostlers, at whatever sort of work engaged, were continually accompanying themselves with effervescent noises; and where the most domestic and confidential garments of coachmen and their wives and families, usually hung, like Macbeth’s banners, on the outward walls.\n\nAt this other private house in Princess’s Place, tenanted by a retired butler who had married a housekeeper, apartments were let Furnished, to a single gentleman: to wit, a wooden-featured, blue-faced Major, with his eyes starting out of his head, in whom Miss Tox recognised, as she herself expressed it, “something so truly military;” and between whom and herself, an occasional interchange of newspapers and pamphlets, and such Platonic dalliance, was effected through the medium of a dark servant of the Major’s who Miss Tox was quite content to classify as a “native,” without connecting him with any geographical idea whatever.\n\nPerhaps there never was a smaller entry and staircase, than the entry and staircase of Miss Tox’s house. Perhaps, taken altogether, from top to bottom, it was the most inconvenient little house in England, and the crookedest; but then, Miss Tox said, what a situation! There was very little daylight to be got there in the winter: no sun at the best of times: air was out of the question, and traffic was walled out. Still Miss Tox said, think of the situation! So said the blue-faced Major, whose eyes were starting out of his head: who gloried in Princess’s Place: and who delighted to turn the conversation at his club, whenever he could, to something connected with some of the great people in the great street round the corner, that he might have the satisfaction of saying they were his neighbours.\n\nIn short, with Miss Tox and the blue-faced Major, it was enough for Princess’s Place—as with a very small fragment of society, it is enough for many a little hanger-on of another sort—to be well connected, and to have genteel blood in its veins. It might be poor, mean, shabby, stupid, dull. No matter. The great street round the corner trailed off into Princess’s Place; and that which of High Holborn would have become a choleric word, spoken of Princess’s Place became flat blasphemy.\n\nThe dingy tenement inhabited by Miss Tox was her own; having been devised and bequeathed to her by the deceased owner of the fishy eye in the locket, of whom a miniature portrait, with a powdered head and a pigtail, balanced the kettle-holder on opposite sides of the parlour fireplace. The greater part of the furniture was of the powdered-head and pig-tail period: comprising a plate-warmer, always languishing and sprawling its four attenuated bow legs in somebody’s way; and an obsolete harpsichord, illuminated round the maker’s name with a painted garland of sweet peas. In any part of the house, visitors were usually cognizant of a prevailing mustiness; and in warm weather Miss Tox had been seen apparently writing in sundry chinks and crevices of the wainscoat with the wrong end of a pen dipped in spirits of turpentine.\n\nAlthough Major Bagstock had arrived at what is called in polite literature, the grand meridian of life, and was proceeding on his journey downhill with hardly any throat, and a very rigid pair of jaw-bones, and long-flapped elephantine ears, and his eyes and complexion in the state of artificial excitement already mentioned, he was mightily proud of awakening an interest in Miss Tox, and tickled his vanity with the fiction that she was a splendid woman who had her eye on him. This he had several times hinted at the club: in connexion with little jocularities, of which old Joe Bagstock, old Joey Bagstock, old J. Bagstock, old Josh Bagstock, or so forth, was the perpetual theme: it being, as it were, the Major’s stronghold and donjon-keep of light humour, to be on the most familiar terms with his own name.\n\n“Joey B., Sir,” the Major would say, with a flourish of his walking-stick, “is worth a dozen of you. If you had a few more of the Bagstock breed among you, Sir, you’d be none the worse for it. Old Joe, Sir, needn’t look far for a wife even now, if he was on the look-out; but he’s hard-hearted, Sir, is Joe—he’s tough, Sir, tough, and de-vilish sly!” After such a declaration, wheezing sounds would be heard; and the Major’s blue would deepen into purple, while his eyes strained and started convulsively.\n\nNotwithstanding his very liberal laudation of himself, however, the Major was selfish. It may be doubted whether there ever was a more entirely selfish person at heart; or at stomach is perhaps a better expression, seeing that he was more decidedly endowed with that latter organ than with the former. He had no idea of being overlooked or slighted by anybody; least of all, had he the remotest comprehension of being overlooked and slighted by Miss Tox.\n\nAnd yet, Miss Tox, as it appeared, forgot him—gradually forgot him. She began to forget him soon after her discovery of the Toodle family. She continued to forget him up to the time of the christening. She went on forgetting him with compound interest after that. Something or somebody had superseded him as a source of interest.\n\n“Good morning, Ma’am,” said the Major, meeting Miss Tox in Princess’s Place, some weeks after the changes chronicled in the last chapter.\n\n“Good morning, Sir,” said Miss Tox; very coldly.\n\n“Joe Bagstock, Ma’am,” observed the Major, with his usual gallantry, “has not had the happiness of bowing to you at your window, for a considerable period. Joe has been hardly used, Ma’am. His sun has been behind a cloud.”\n\nMiss Tox inclined her head; but very coldly indeed.\n\n“Joe’s luminary has been out of town, Ma’am, perhaps,” inquired the Major.\n\n“I? out of town? oh no, I have not been out of town,” said Miss Tox. “I have been much engaged lately. My time is nearly all devoted to some very intimate friends. I am afraid I have none to spare, even now. Good morning, Sir!”\n\nAs Miss Tox, with her most fascinating step and carriage, disappeared from Princess’s Place, the Major stood looking after her with a bluer face than ever: muttering and growling some not at all complimentary remarks.\n\n“Why, damme, Sir,” said the Major, rolling his lobster eyes round and round Princess’s Place, and apostrophizing its fragrant air, “six months ago, the woman loved the ground Josh Bagstock walked on. What’s the meaning of it?”\n\nThe Major decided, after some consideration, that it meant mantraps; that it meant plotting and snaring; that Miss Tox was digging pitfalls. “But you won’t catch Joe, Ma’am,” said the Major. “He’s tough, Ma’am, tough, is J.B. Tough, and de-vilish sly!” over which reflection he chuckled for the rest of the day.\n\nBut still, when that day and many other days were gone and past, it seemed that Miss Tox took no heed whatever of the Major, and thought nothing at all about him. She had been wont, once upon a time, to look out at one of her little dark windows by accident, and blushingly return the Major’s greeting; but now, she never gave the Major a chance, and cared nothing at all whether he looked over the way or not. Other changes had come to pass too. The Major, standing in the shade of his own apartment, could make out that an air of greater smartness had recently come over Miss Tox’s house; that a new cage with gilded wires had been provided for the ancient little canary bird; that divers ornaments, cut out of coloured card-boards and paper, seemed to decorate the chimney-piece and tables; that a plant or two had suddenly sprung up in the windows; that Miss Tox occasionally practised on the harpsichord, whose garland of sweet peas was always displayed ostentatiously, crowned with the Copenhagen and Bird Waltzes in a Music\n\n# Book of Miss Tox’s own copying.\n\nOver and above all this, Miss Tox had long been dressed with uncommon care and elegance in slight mourning. But this helped the Major out of his difficulty; and he determined within himself that she had come into a small legacy, and grown proud.\n\nIt was on the very next day after he had eased his mind by arriving at this decision, that the Major, sitting at his breakfast, saw an apparition so tremendous and wonderful in Miss Tox’s little drawing-room, that he remained for some time rooted to his chair; then, rushing into the next room, returned with a double-barrelled opera-glass, through which he surveyed it intently for some minutes.\n\n“It’s a Baby, Sir,” said the Major, shutting up the glass again, “for fifty thousand pounds!”\n\nThe Major couldn’t forget it. He could do nothing but whistle, and stare to that extent, that his eyes, compared with what they now became, had been in former times quite cavernous and sunken. Day after day, two, three, four times a week, this Baby reappeared. The Major continued to stare and whistle. To all other intents and purposes he was alone in Princess’s Place. Miss Tox had ceased to mind what he did. He might have been black as well as blue, and it would have been of no consequence to her.\n\nThe perseverance with which she walked out of Princess’s Place to fetch this baby and its nurse, and walked back with them, and walked home with them again, and continually mounted guard over them; and the perseverance with which she nursed it herself, and fed it, and played with it, and froze its young blood with airs upon the harpsichord, was extraordinary. At about this same period too, she was seized with a passion for looking at a certain bracelet; also with a passion for looking at the moon, of which she would take long observations from her chamber window. But whatever she looked at; sun, moon, stars, or bracelet; she looked no more at the Major. And the Major whistled, and stared, and wondered, and dodged about his room, and could make nothing of it.\n\n“You’ll quite win my brother Paul’s heart, and that’s the truth, my dear,” said Mrs Chick, one day.\n\nMiss Tox turned pale.\n\n“He grows more like Paul every day,” said Mrs Chick.\n\nMiss Tox returned no other reply than by taking the little Paul in her arms, and making his cockade perfectly flat and limp with her caresses.\n\n“His mother, my dear,” said Miss Tox, “whose acquaintance I was to have made through you, does he at all resemble her?”\n\n“Not at all,” returned Louisa\n\n“She was—she was pretty, I believe?” faltered Miss Tox.\n\n“Why, poor dear Fanny was interesting,” said Mrs Chick, after some judicial consideration. “Certainly interesting. She had not that air of commanding superiority which one would somehow expect, almost as a matter of course, to find in my brother’s wife; nor had she that strength and vigour of mind which such a man requires.”\n\nMiss Tox heaved a deep sigh.\n\n“But she was pleasing:” said Mrs Chick: “extremely so. And she meant!—oh, dear, how well poor Fanny meant!”\n\n“You Angel!” cried Miss Tox to little Paul. “You Picture of your own Papa!”\n\nIf the Major could have known how many hopes and ventures, what a multitude of plans and speculations, rested on that baby head; and could have seen them hovering, in all their heterogeneous confusion and disorder, round the puckered cap of the unconscious little Paul; he might have stared indeed. Then would he have recognised, among the crowd, some few ambitious motes and beams belonging to Miss Tox; then would he perhaps have understood the nature of that lady’s faltering investment in the Dombey Firm.\n\nIf the child himself could have awakened in the night, and seen, gathered about his cradle-curtains, faint reflections of the dreams that other people had of him, they might have scared him, with good reason. But he slumbered on, alike unconscious of the kind intentions of Miss Tox, the wonder of the Major, the early sorrows of his sister, and the stern visions of his father; and innocent that any spot of earth contained a Dombey or a Son.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII. Paul’s Further Progress, Growth and Character\n\nBeneath the watching and attentive eyes of Time—so far another Major—Paul’s slumbers gradually changed. More and more light broke in upon them; distincter and distincter dreams disturbed them; an accumulating crowd of objects and impressions swarmed about his rest; and so he passed from babyhood to childhood, and became a talking, walking, wondering Dombey.\n\nOn the downfall and banishment of Richards, the nursery may be said to have been put into commission: as a Public Department is sometimes, when no individual Atlas can be found to support it The Commissioners were, of course, Mrs Chick and Miss Tox: who devoted themselves to their duties with such astonishing ardour that Major Bagstock had every day some new reminder of his being forsaken, while Mr Chick, bereft of domestic supervision, cast himself upon the gay world, dined at clubs and coffee-houses, smelt of smoke on three different occasions, went to the play by himself, and in short, loosened (as Mrs Chick once told him) every social bond, and moral obligation.\n\nYet, in spite of his early promise, all this vigilance and care could not make little Paul a thriving boy. Naturally delicate, perhaps, he pined and wasted after the dismissal of his nurse, and, for a long time, seemed but to wait his opportunity of gliding through their hands, and seeking his lost mother. This dangerous ground in his steeple-chase towards manhood passed, he still found it very rough riding, and was grievously beset by all the obstacles in his course. Every tooth was a break-neck fence, and every pimple in the measles a stone wall to him. He was down in every fit of the hooping-cough, and rolled upon and crushed by a whole field of small diseases, that came trooping on each other’s heels to prevent his getting up again. Some bird of prey got into his throat instead of the thrush; and the very chickens turning ferocious—if they have anything to do with that infant malady to which they lend their name—worried him like tiger-cats.\n\nThe chill of Paul’s christening had struck home, perhaps to some sensitive part of his nature, which could not recover itself in the cold shade of his father; but he was an unfortunate child from that day. Mrs Wickam often said she never see a dear so put upon.\n\nMrs Wickam was a waiter’s wife—which would seem equivalent to being any other man’s widow—whose application for an engagement in Mr Dombey’s service had been favourably considered, on account of the apparent impossibility of her having any followers, or anyone to follow; and who, from within a day or two of Paul’s sharp weaning, had been engaged as his nurse. Mrs Wickam was a meek woman, of a fair complexion, with her eyebrows always elevated, and her head always drooping; who was always ready to pity herself, or to be pitied, or to pity anybody else; and who had a surprising natural gift of viewing all subjects in an utterly forlorn and pitiable light, and bringing dreadful precedents to bear upon them, and deriving the greatest consolation from the exercise of that talent.\n\nIt is hardly necessary to observe, that no touch of this quality ever reached the magnificent knowledge of Mr Dombey. It would have been remarkable, indeed, if any had; when no one in the house—not even Mrs Chick or Miss Tox—dared ever whisper to him that there had, on any one occasion, been the least reason for uneasiness in reference to little Paul. He had settled, within himself, that the child must necessarily pass through a certain routine of minor maladies, and that the sooner he did so the better. If he could have bought him off, or provided a substitute, as in the case of an unlucky drawing for the militia, he would have been glad to do so, on liberal terms. But as this was not feasible, he merely wondered, in his haughty manner, now and then, what Nature meant by it; and comforted himself with the reflection that there was another milestone passed upon the road, and that the great end of the journey lay so much the nearer. For the feeling uppermost in his mind, now and constantly intensifying, and increasing in it as Paul grew older, was impatience. Impatience for the time to come, when his visions of their united consequence and grandeur would be triumphantly realized.\n\nSome philosophers tell us that selfishness is at the root of our best loves and affections. Mr Dombey’s young child was, from the beginning, so distinctly important to him as a part of his own greatness, or (which is the same thing) of the greatness of Dombey and Son, that there is no doubt his parental affection might have been easily traced, like many a goodly superstructure of fair fame, to a very low foundation. But he loved his son with all the love he had. If there were a warm place in his frosty heart, his son occupied it; if its very hard surface could receive the impression of any image, the image of that son was there; though not so much as an infant, or as a boy, but as a grown man—the “Son” of the Firm. Therefore he was impatient to advance into the future, and to hurry over the intervening passages of his history. Therefore he had little or no anxiety about them, in spite of his love; feeling as if the boy had a charmed life, and must become the man with whom he held such constant communication in his thoughts, and for whom he planned and projected, as for an existing reality, every day.\n\nThus Paul grew to be nearly five years old. He was a pretty little fellow; though there was something wan and wistful in his small face, that gave occasion to many significant shakes of Mrs Wickam’s head, and many long-drawn inspirations of Mrs Wickam’s breath. His temper gave abundant promise of being imperious in after-life; and he had as hopeful an apprehension of his own importance, and the rightful subservience of all other things and persons to it, as heart could desire. He was childish and sportive enough at times, and not of a sullen disposition; but he had a strange, old-fashioned, thoughtful way, at other times, of sitting brooding in his miniature arm-chair, when he looked (and talked) like one of those terrible little Beings in the Fairy tales, who, at a hundred and fifty or two hundred years of age, fantastically represent the children for whom they have been substituted. He would frequently be stricken with this precocious mood upstairs in the nursery; and would sometimes lapse into it suddenly, exclaiming that he was tired: even while playing with Florence, or driving Miss Tox in single harness. But at no time did he fall into it so surely, as when, his little chair being carried down into his father’s room, he sat there with him after dinner, by the fire. They were the strangest pair at such a time that ever firelight shone upon. Mr Dombey so erect and solemn, gazing at the glare; his little image, with an old, old face, peering into the red perspective with the fixed and rapt attention of a sage. Mr Dombey entertaining complicated worldly schemes and plans; the little image entertaining Heaven knows what wild fancies, half-formed thoughts, and wandering speculations. Mr Dombey stiff with starch and arrogance; the little image by inheritance, and in unconscious imitation. The two so very much alike, and yet so monstrously contrasted.\n\nOn one of these occasions, when they had both been perfectly quiet for a long time, and Mr Dombey only knew that the child was awake by occasionally glancing at his eye, where the bright fire was sparkling like a jewel, little Paul broke silence thus:\n\n“Papa! what’s money?”\n\nThe abrupt question had such immediate reference to the subject of Mr Dombey’s thoughts, that Mr Dombey was quite disconcerted.\n\n“What is money, Paul?” he answered. “Money?”\n\n“Yes,” said the child, laying his hands upon the elbows of his little chair, and turning the old face up towards Mr Dombey’s; “what is money?”\n\nMr Dombey was in a difficulty. He would have liked to give him some explanation involving the terms circulating-medium, currency, depreciation of currency, paper, bullion, rates of exchange, value of precious metals in the market, and so forth; but looking down at the little chair, and seeing what a long way down it was, he answered: “Gold, and silver, and copper. Guineas, shillings, half-pence. You know what they are?”\n\n“Oh yes, I know what they are,” said Paul. “I don’t mean that, Papa. I mean what’s money after all?”\n\nHeaven and Earth, how old his face was as he turned it up again towards his father’s!\n\n“What is money after all!” said Mr Dombey, backing his chair a little, that he might the better gaze in sheer amazement at the presumptuous atom that propounded such an inquiry.\n\n“I mean, Papa, what can it do?” returned Paul, folding his arms (they were hardly long enough to fold), and looking at the fire, and up at him, and at the fire, and up at him again.\n\nMr Dombey drew his chair back to its former place, and patted him on the head. “You’ll know better by-and-by, my man,” he said. “Money, Paul, can do anything.” He took hold of the little hand, and beat it softly against one of his own, as he said so.\n\nBut Paul got his hand free as soon as he could; and rubbing it gently to and fro on the elbow of his chair, as if his wit were in the palm, and he were sharpening it—and looking at the fire again, as though the fire had been his adviser and prompter—repeated, after a short pause:\n\n“Anything, Papa?”\n\n“Yes. Anything—almost,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Anything means everything, don’t it, Papa?” asked his son: not observing, or possibly not understanding, the qualification.\n\n“It includes it: yes,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Why didn’t money save me my Mama?” returned the child. “It isn’t cruel, is it?”\n\n“Cruel!” said Mr Dombey, settling his neckcloth, and seeming to resent the idea. “No. A good thing can’t be cruel.”\n\n“If it’s a good thing, and can do anything,” said the little fellow, thoughtfully, as he looked back at the fire, “I wonder why it didn’t save me my Mama.”\n\nHe didn’t ask the question of his father this time. Perhaps he had seen, with a child’s quickness, that it had already made his father uncomfortable. But he repeated the thought aloud, as if it were quite an old one to him, and had troubled him very much; and sat with his chin resting on his hand, still cogitating and looking for an explanation in the fire.\n\nMr Dombey having recovered from his surprise, not to say his alarm (for it was the very first occasion on which the child had ever broached the subject of his mother to him, though he had had him sitting by his side, in this same manner, evening after evening), expounded to him how that money, though a very potent spirit, never to be disparaged on any account whatever, could not keep people alive whose time was come to die; and how that we must all die, unfortunately, even in the City, though we were never so rich. But how that money caused us to be honoured, feared, respected, courted, and admired, and made us powerful and glorious in the eyes of all men; and how that it could, very often, even keep off death, for a long time together. How, for example, it had secured to his Mama the services of Mr Pilkins, by which he, Paul, had often profited himself; likewise of the great Doctor Parker Peps, whom he had never known. And how it could do all, that could be done. This, with more to the same purpose, Mr Dombey instilled into the mind of his son, who listened attentively, and seemed to understand the greater part of what was said to him.\n\n“It can’t make me strong and quite well, either, Papa; can it?” asked Paul, after a short silence; rubbing his tiny hands.\n\n“Why, you are strong and quite well,” returned Mr Dombey. “Are you not?”\n\nOh! the age of the face that was turned up again, with an expression, half of melancholy, half of slyness, on it!\n\n“You are as strong and well as such little people usually are? Eh?” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Florence is older than I am, but I’m not as strong and well as Florence, “I know,” returned the child; “and I believe that when Florence was as little as me, she could play a great deal longer at a time without tiring herself. I am so tired sometimes,” said little Paul, warming his hands, and looking in between the bars of the grate, as if some ghostly puppet-show were performing there, “and my bones ache so (Wickam says it’s my bones), that I don’t know what to do.”\n\n“Ay! But that’s at night,” said Mr Dombey, drawing his own chair closer to his son’s, and laying his hand gently on his back; “little people should be tired at night, for then they sleep well.”\n\n“Oh, it’s not at night, Papa,” returned the child, “it’s in the day; and I lie down in Florence’s lap, and she sings to me. At night I dream about such cu-ri-ous things!”\n\nAnd he went on, warming his hands again, and thinking about them, like an old man or a young goblin.\n\nMr Dombey was so astonished, and so uncomfortable, and so perfectly at a loss how to pursue the conversation, that he could only sit looking at his son by the light of the fire, with his hand resting on his back, as if it were detained there by some magnetic attraction. Once he advanced his other hand, and turned the contemplative face towards his own for a moment. But it sought the fire again as soon as he released it; and remained, addressed towards the flickering blaze, until the nurse appeared, to summon him to bed.\n\n“I want Florence to come for me,” said Paul.\n\n“Won’t you come with your poor Nurse Wickam, Master Paul?” inquired that attendant, with great pathos.\n\n“No, I won’t,” replied Paul, composing himself in his arm-chair again, like the master of the house.\n\nInvoking a blessing upon his innocence, Mrs Wickam withdrew, and presently Florence appeared in her stead. The child immediately started up with sudden readiness and animation, and raised towards his father in bidding him good-night, a countenance so much brighter, so much younger, and so much more child-like altogether, that Mr Dombey, while he felt greatly reassured by the change, was quite amazed at it.\n\nAfter they had left the room together, he thought he heard a soft voice singing; and remembering that Paul had said his sister sung to him, he had the curiosity to open the door and listen, and look after them. She was toiling up the great, wide, vacant staircase, with him in her arms; his head was lying on her shoulder, one of his arms thrown negligently round her neck. So they went, toiling up; she singing all the way, and Paul sometimes crooning out a feeble accompaniment. Mr Dombey looked after them until they reached the top of the staircase—not without halting to rest by the way—and passed out of his sight; and then he still stood gazing upwards, until the dull rays of the moon, glimmering in a melancholy manner through the dim skylight, sent him back to his room.\n\nMrs Chick and Miss Tox were convoked in council at dinner next day; and when the cloth was removed, Mr Dombey opened the proceedings by requiring to be informed, without any gloss or reservation, whether there was anything the matter with Paul, and what Mr Pilkins said about him.\n\n“For the child is hardly,” said Mr Dombey, “as stout as I could wish.”\n\n“My dear Paul,” returned Mrs Chick, “with your usual happy discrimination, which I am weak enough to envy you, every time I am in your company; and so I think is Miss Tox.”\n\n“Oh my dear!” said Miss Tox, softly, “how could it be otherwise? Presumptuous as it is to aspire to such a level; still, if the bird of night may—but I’ll not trouble Mr Dombey with the sentiment. It merely relates to the Bulbul.”\n\nMr Dombey bent his head in stately recognition of the Bulbuls as an old-established body.\n\n“With your usual happy discrimination, my dear Paul,” resumed Mrs Chick, “you have hit the point at once. Our darling is altogether as stout as we could wish. The fact is, that his mind is too much for him. His soul is a great deal too large for his frame. I am sure the way in which that dear child talks!” said Mrs Chick, shaking her head; “no one would believe. His expressions, Lucretia, only yesterday upon the subject of Funerals!”\n\n“I am afraid,” said Mr Dombey, interrupting her testily, “that some of those persons upstairs suggest improper subjects to the child. He was speaking to me last night about his—about his Bones,” said Mr Dombey, laying an irritated stress upon the word. “What on earth has anybody to do with the—with the—Bones of my son? He is not a living skeleton, I suppose.”\n\n“Very far from it,” said Mrs Chick, with unspeakable expression.\n\n“I hope so,” returned her brother. “Funerals again! who talks to the child of funerals? We are not undertakers, or mutes, or grave-diggers, I believe.”\n\n“Very far from it,” interposed Mrs Chick, with the same profound expression as before.\n\n“Then who puts such things into his head?” said Mr Dombey. “Really I was quite dismayed and shocked last night. Who puts such things into his head, Louisa?”\n\n“My dear Paul,” said Mrs Chick, after a moment’s silence, “it is of no use inquiring. I do not think, I will tell you candidly that Wickam is a person of very cheerful spirit, or what one would call a—”\n\n“A daughter of Momus,” Miss Tox softly suggested.\n\n“Exactly so,” said Mrs Chick; “but she is exceedingly attentive and useful, and not at all presumptuous; indeed I never saw a more biddable woman. I would say that for her, if I was put upon my trial before a Court of Justice.”\n\n“Well! you are not put upon your trial before a Court of Justice, at present, Louisa,” returned Mr Dombey, chafing, “and therefore it don’t matter.”\n\n“My dear Paul,” said Mrs Chick, in a warning voice, “I must be spoken to kindly, or there is an end of me,” at the same time a premonitory redness developed itself in Mrs Chick’s eyelids which was an invariable sign of rain, unless the weather changed directly.\n\n“I was inquiring, Louisa,” observed Mr Dombey, in an altered voice, and after a decent interval, “about Paul’s health and actual state.”\n\n“If the dear child,” said Mrs Chick, in the tone of one who was summing up what had been previously quite agreed upon, instead of saying it all for the first time, “is a little weakened by that last attack, and is not in quite such vigorous health as we could wish; and if he has some temporary weakness in his system, and does occasionally seem about to lose, for the moment, the use of his—”\n\nMrs Chick was afraid to say limbs, after Mr Dombey’s recent objection to bones, and therefore waited for a suggestion from Miss Tox, who, true to her office, hazarded “members.”\n\n“Members!” repeated Mr Dombey.\n\n“I think the medical gentleman mentioned legs this morning, my dear Louisa, did he not?” said Miss Tox.\n\n“Why, of course he did, my love,” retorted Mrs Chick, mildly reproachful. “How can you ask me? You heard him. I say, if our dear Paul should lose, for the moment, the use of his legs, these are casualties common to many children at his time of life, and not to be prevented by any care or caution. The sooner you understand that, Paul, and admit that, the better. If you have any doubt as to the amount of care, and caution, and affection, and self-sacrifice, that has been bestowed upon little Paul, I should wish to refer the question to your medical attendant, or to any of your dependants in this house. Call Towlinson,” said Mrs Chick, “I believe he has no prejudice in our favour; quite the contrary. I should wish to hear what accusation Towlinson can make!”\n\n“Surely you must know, Louisa,” observed Mr Dombey, “that I don’t question your natural devotion to, and regard for, the future head of my house.”\n\n“I am glad to hear it, Paul,” said Mrs Chick; “but really you are very odd, and sometimes talk very strangely, though without meaning it, I know. If your dear boy’s soul is too much for his body, Paul, you should remember whose fault that is—who he takes after, I mean—and make the best of it. He’s as like his Papa as he can be. People have noticed it in the streets. The very beadle, I am informed, observed it, so long ago as at his christening. He’s a very respectable man, with children of his own. He ought to know.”\n\n“Mr Pilkins saw Paul this morning, I believe?” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Yes, he did,” returned his sister. “Miss Tox and myself were present. Miss Tox and myself are always present. We make a point of it. Mr Pilkins has seen him for some days past, and a very clever man I believe him to be. He says it is nothing to speak of; which I can confirm, if that is any consolation; but he recommended, today, sea-air. Very wisely, Paul, I feel convinced.”\n\n“Sea-air,” repeated Mr Dombey, looking at his sister.\n\n“There is nothing to be made uneasy by, in that,” said Mrs Chick. “My George and Frederick were both ordered sea-air, when they were about his age; and I have been ordered it myself a great many times. I quite agree with you, Paul, that perhaps topics may be incautiously mentioned upstairs before him, which it would be as well for his little mind not to expatiate upon; but I really don’t see how that is to be helped, in the case of a child of his quickness. If he were a common child, there would be nothing in it. I must say I think, with Miss Tox, that a short absence from this house, the air of Brighton, and the bodily and mental training of so judicious a person as Mrs Pipchin for instance—”\n\n“Who is Mrs Pipchin, Louisa?” asked Mr Dombey; aghast at this familiar introduction of a name he had never heard before.\n\n“Mrs Pipchin, my dear Paul,” returned his sister, “is an elderly lady—Miss Tox knows her whole history—who has for some time devoted all the energies of her mind, with the greatest success, to the study and treatment of infancy, and who has been extremely well connected. Her husband broke his heart in—how did you say her husband broke his heart, my dear? I forget the precise circumstances.\n\n“In pumping water out of the Peruvian Mines,” replied Miss Tox.\n\n“Not being a Pumper himself, of course,” said Mrs Chick, glancing at her brother; and it really did seem necessary to offer the explanation, for Miss Tox had spoken of him as if he had died at the handle; “but having invested money in the speculation, which failed. I believe that Mrs Pipchin’s management of children is quite astonishing. I have heard it commended in private circles ever since I was—dear me—how high!” Mrs Chick’s eye wandered about the bookcase near the bust of Mr Pitt, which was about ten feet from the ground.\n\n“Perhaps I should say of Mrs Pipchin, my dear Sir,” observed Miss Tox, with an ingenuous blush, “having been so pointedly referred to, that the encomium which has been passed upon her by your sweet sister is well merited. Many ladies and gentleman, now grown up to be interesting members of society, have been indebted to her care. The humble individual who addresses you was once under her charge. I believe juvenile nobility itself is no stranger to her establishment.”\n\n“Do I understand that this respectable matron keeps an establishment, Miss Tox?” the Mr Dombey, condescendingly.\n\n“Why, I really don’t know,” rejoined that lady, “whether I am justified in calling it so. It is not a Preparatory School by any means. Should I express my meaning,” said Miss Tox, with peculiar sweetness, “if I designated it an infantine Boarding-House of a very select description?”\n\n“On an exceedingly limited and particular scale,” suggested Mrs Chick, with a glance at her brother.\n\n“Oh! Exclusion itself!” said Miss Tox.\n\nThere was something in this. Mrs Pipchin’s husband having broken his heart of the Peruvian mines was good. It had a rich sound. Besides, Mr Dombey was in a state almost amounting to consternation at the idea of Paul remaining where he was one hour after his removal had been recommended by the medical practitioner. It was a stoppage and delay upon the road the child must traverse, slowly at the best, before the goal was reached. Their recommendation of Mrs Pipchin had great weight with him; for he knew that they were jealous of any interference with their charge, and he never for a moment took it into account that they might be solicitous to divide a responsibility, of which he had, as shown just now, his own established views. Broke his heart of the Peruvian mines, mused Mr Dombey. Well! a very respectable way of doing It.\n\n“Supposing we should decide, on to-morrow’s inquiries, to send Paul down to Brighton to this lady, who would go with him?” inquired Mr Dombey, after some reflection.\n\n“I don’t think you could send the child anywhere at present without Florence, my dear Paul,” returned his sister, hesitating. “It’s quite an infatuation with him. He’s very young, you know, and has his fancies.”\n\nMr Dombey turned his head away, and going slowly to the bookcase, and unlocking it, brought back a book to read.\n\n“Anybody else, Louisa?” he said, without looking up, and turning over the leaves.\n\n“Wickam, of course. Wickam would be quite sufficient, I should say,” returned his sister. “Paul being in such hands as Mrs Pipchin’s, you could hardly send anybody who would be a further check upon her. You would go down yourself once a week at least, of course.”\n\n“Of course,” said Mr Dombey; and sat looking at one page for an hour afterwards, without reading one word.\n\nThis celebrated Mrs Pipchin was a marvellous ill-favoured, ill-conditioned old lady, of a stooping figure, with a mottled face, like bad marble, a hook nose, and a hard grey eye, that looked as if it might have been hammered at on an anvil without sustaining any injury. Forty years at least had elapsed since the Peruvian mines had been the death of Mr Pipchin; but his relict still wore black bombazeen, of such a lustreless, deep, dead, sombre shade, that gas itself couldn’t light her up after dark, and her presence was a quencher to any number of candles. She was generally spoken of as “a great manager” of children; and the secret of her management was, to give them everything that they didn’t like, and nothing that they did—which was found to sweeten their dispositions very much. She was such a bitter old lady, that one was tempted to believe there had been some mistake in the application of the Peruvian machinery, and that all her waters of gladness and milk of human kindness, had been pumped out dry, instead of the mines.\n\nThe Castle of this ogress and child-queller was in a steep by-street at Brighton; where the soil was more than usually chalky, flinty, and sterile, and the houses were more than usually brittle and thin; where the small front-gardens had the unaccountable property of producing nothing but marigolds, whatever was sown in them; and where snails were constantly discovered holding on to the street doors, and other public places they were not expected to ornament, with the tenacity of cupping-glasses. In the winter time the air couldn’t be got out of the Castle, and in the summer time it couldn’t be got in. There was such a continual reverberation of wind in it, that it sounded like a great shell, which the inhabitants were obliged to hold to their ears night and day, whether they liked it or no. It was not, naturally, a fresh-smelling house; and in the window of the front parlour, which was never opened, Mrs Pipchin kept a collection of plants in pots, which imparted an earthy flavour of their own to the establishment. However choice examples of their kind, too, these plants were of a kind peculiarly adapted to the embowerment of Mrs Pipchin. There were half-a-dozen specimens of the cactus, writhing round bits of lath, like hairy serpents; another specimen shooting out broad claws, like a green lobster; several creeping vegetables, possessed of sticky and adhesive leaves; and one uncomfortable flower-pot hanging to the ceiling, which appeared to have boiled over, and tickling people underneath with its long green ends, reminded them of spiders—in which Mrs Pipchin’s dwelling was uncommonly prolific, though perhaps it challenged competition still more proudly, in the season, in point of earwigs.\n\nMrs Pipchin’s scale of charges being high, however, to all who could afford to pay, and Mrs Pipchin very seldom sweetening the equable acidity of her nature in favour of anybody, she was held to be an old “lady of remarkable firmness, who was quite scientific in her knowledge of the childish character.” On this reputation, and on the broken heart of Mr Pipchin, she had contrived, taking one year with another, to eke out a tolerable sufficient living since her husband’s demise. Within three days after Mrs Chick’s first allusion to her, this excellent old lady had the satisfaction of anticipating a handsome addition to her current receipts, from the pocket of Mr Dombey; and of receiving Florence and her little brother Paul, as inmates of the Castle.\n\nMrs Chick and Miss Tox, who had brought them down on the previous night (which they all passed at an Hotel), had just driven away from the door, on their journey home again; and Mrs Pipchin, with her back to the fire, stood, reviewing the new-comers, like an old soldier. Mrs Pipchin’s middle-aged niece, her good-natured and devoted slave, but possessing a gaunt and iron-bound aspect, and much afflicted with boils on her nose, was divesting Master Bitherstone of the clean collar he had worn on parade. Miss Pankey, the only other little boarder at present, had that moment been walked off to the Castle Dungeon (an empty apartment at the back, devoted to correctional purposes), for having sniffed thrice, in the presence of visitors.\n\n“Well, Sir,” said Mrs Pipchin to Paul, “how do you think you shall like me?”\n\n“I don’t think I shall like you at all,” replied Paul. “I want to go away. This isn’t my house.”\n\n“No. It’s mine,” retorted Mrs Pipchin.\n\n“It’s a very nasty one,” said Paul.\n\n“There’s a worse place in it than this though,” said Mrs Pipchin, “where we shut up our bad boys.”\n\n“Has he ever been in it?” asked Paul: pointing out Master Bitherstone.\n\nMrs Pipchin nodded assent; and Paul had enough to do, for the rest of that day, in surveying Master Bitherstone from head to foot, and watching all the workings of his countenance, with the interest attaching to a boy of mysterious and terrible experiences.\n\nAt one o’clock there was a dinner, chiefly of the farinaceous and vegetable kind, when Miss Pankey (a mild little blue-eyed morsel of a child, who was shampoo’d every morning, and seemed in danger of being rubbed away, altogether) was led in from captivity by the ogress herself, and instructed that nobody who sniffed before visitors ever went to Heaven. When this great truth had been thoroughly impressed upon her, she was regaled with rice; and subsequently repeated the form of grace established in the Castle, in which there was a special clause, thanking Mrs Pipchin for a good dinner. Mrs Pipchin’s niece, Berinthia, took cold pork. Mrs Pipchin, whose constitution required warm nourishment, made a special repast of mutton-chops, which were brought in hot and hot, between two plates, and smelt very nice.\n\nAs it rained after dinner, and they couldn’t go out walking on the beach, and Mrs Pipchin’s constitution required rest after chops, they went away with Berry (otherwise Berinthia) to the Dungeon; an empty room looking out upon a chalk wall and a water-butt, and made ghastly by a ragged fireplace without any stove in it. Enlivened by company, however, this was the best place after all; for Berry played with them there, and seemed to enjoy a game at romps as much as they did; until Mrs Pipchin knocking angrily at the wall, like the Cock Lane Ghost revived, they left off, and Berry told them stories in a whisper until twilight.\n\nFor tea there was plenty of milk and water, and bread and butter, with a little black tea-pot for Mrs Pipchin and Berry, and buttered toast unlimited for Mrs Pipchin, which was brought in, hot and hot, like the chops. Though Mrs Pipchin got very greasy, outside, over this dish, it didn’t seem to lubricate her internally, at all; for she was as fierce as ever, and the hard grey eye knew no softening.\n\nAfter tea, Berry brought out a little work-box, with the Royal Pavilion on the lid, and fell to working busily; while Mrs Pipchin, having put on her spectacles and opened a great volume bound in green baize, began to nod. And whenever Mrs Pipchin caught herself falling forward into the fire, and woke up, she filliped Master Bitherstone on the nose for nodding too.\n\nAt last it was the children’s bedtime, and after prayers they went to bed. As little Miss Pankey was afraid of sleeping alone in the dark, Mrs Pipchin always made a point of driving her upstairs herself, like a sheep; and it was cheerful to hear Miss Pankey moaning long afterwards, in the least eligible chamber, and Mrs Pipchin now and then going in to shake her. At about half-past nine o’clock the odour of a warm sweet-bread (Mrs Pipchin’s constitution wouldn’t go to sleep without sweet-bread) diversified the prevailing fragrance of the house, which Mrs Wickam said was “a smell of building;” and slumber fell upon the Castle shortly after.\n\nThe breakfast next morning was like the tea over night, except that Mrs Pipchin took her roll instead of toast, and seemed a little more irate when it was over. Master Bitherstone read aloud to the rest a pedigree from Genesis (judiciously selected by Mrs Pipchin), getting over the names with the ease and clearness of a person tumbling up the treadmill. That done, Miss Pankey was borne away to be shampoo’d; and Master Bitherstone to have something else done to him with salt water, from which he always returned very blue and dejected. Paul and Florence went out in the meantime on the beach with Wickam—who was constantly in tears—and at about noon Mrs Pipchin presided over some Early Readings. It being a part of Mrs Pipchin’s system not to encourage a child’s mind to develop and expand itself like a young flower, but to open it by force like an oyster, the moral of these lessons was usually of a violent and stunning character: the hero—a naughty boy—seldom, in the mildest catastrophe, being finished off anything less than a lion, or a bear.\n\nSuch was life at Mrs Pipchin’s. On Saturday Mr Dombey came down; and Florence and Paul would go to his Hotel, and have tea. They passed the whole of Sunday with him, and generally rode out before dinner; and on these occasions Mr Dombey seemed to grow, like Falstaff’s assailants, and instead of being one man in buckram, to become a dozen. Sunday evening was the most melancholy evening in the week; for Mrs Pipchin always made a point of being particularly cross on Sunday nights. Miss Pankey was generally brought back from an aunt’s at Rottingdean, in deep distress; and Master Bitherstone, whose relatives were all in India, and who was required to sit, between the services, in an erect position with his head against the parlour wall, neither moving hand nor foot, suffered so acutely in his young spirits that he once asked Florence, on a Sunday night, if she could give him any idea of the way back to Bengal.\n\nBut it was generally said that Mrs Pipchin was a woman of system with children; and no doubt she was. Certainly the wild ones went home tame enough, after sojourning for a few months beneath her hospitable roof. It was generally said, too, that it was highly creditable of Mrs Pipchin to have devoted herself to this way of life, and to have made such a sacrifice of her feelings, and such a resolute stand against her troubles, when Mr Pipchin broke his heart in the Peruvian mines.\n\nAt this exemplary old lady, Paul would sit staring in his little arm-chair by the fire, for any length of time. He never seemed to know what weariness was, when he was looking fixedly at Mrs Pipchin. He was not fond of her; he was not afraid of her; but in those old, old moods of his, she seemed to have a grotesque attraction for him. There he would sit, looking at her, and warming his hands, and looking at her, until he sometimes quite confounded Mrs Pipchin, Ogress as she was. Once she asked him, when they were alone, what he was thinking about.\n\n“You,” said Paul, without the least reserve.\n\n“And what are you thinking about me?” asked Mrs Pipchin.\n\n“I’m thinking how old you must be,” said Paul.\n\n“You mustn’t say such things as that, young gentleman,” returned the dame. “That’ll never do.”\n\n“Why not?” asked Paul.\n\n“Because it’s not polite,” said Mrs Pipchin, snappishly.\n\n“Not polite?” said Paul.\n\n“No.”\n\n“It’s not polite,” said Paul, innocently, “to eat all the mutton chops and toast”, Wickam says.\n\n“Wickam,” retorted Mrs Pipchin, colouring, “is a wicked, impudent, bold-faced hussy.”\n\n“What’s that?” inquired Paul.\n\n“Never you mind, Sir,” retorted Mrs Pipchin. “Remember the story of the little boy that was gored to death by a mad bull for asking questions.”\n\n“If the bull was mad,” said Paul, “how did he know that the boy had asked questions? Nobody can go and whisper secrets to a mad bull. I don’t believe that story.”\n\n“You don’t believe it, Sir?” repeated Mrs Pipchin, amazed.\n\n“No,” said Paul.\n\n“Not if it should happen to have been a tame bull, you little Infidel?” said Mrs Pipchin.\n\nAs Paul had not considered the subject in that light, and had founded his conclusions on the alleged lunacy of the bull, he allowed himself to be put down for the present. But he sat turning it over in his mind, with such an obvious intention of fixing Mrs Pipchin presently, that even that hardy old lady deemed it prudent to retreat until he should have forgotten the subject.\n\nFrom that time, Mrs Pipchin appeared to have something of the same odd kind of attraction towards Paul, as Paul had towards her. She would make him move his chair to her side of the fire, instead of sitting opposite; and there he would remain in a nook between Mrs Pipchin and the fender, with all the light of his little face absorbed into the black bombazeen drapery, studying every line and wrinkle of her countenance, and peering at the hard grey eye, until Mrs Pipchin was sometimes fain to shut it, on pretence of dozing. Mrs Pipchin had an old black cat, who generally lay coiled upon the centre foot of the fender, purring egotistically, and winking at the fire until the contracted pupils of his eyes were like two notes of admiration. The good old lady might have been—not to record it disrespectfully—a witch, and Paul and the cat her two familiars, as they all sat by the fire together. It would have been quite in keeping with the appearance of the party if they had all sprung up the chimney in a high wind one night, and never been heard of any more.\n\nThis, however, never came to pass. The cat, and Paul, and Mrs Pipchin, were constantly to be found in their usual places after dark; and Paul, eschewing the companionship of Master Bitherstone, went on studying Mrs Pipchin, and the cat, and the fire, night after night, as if they were a book of necromancy, in three volumes.\n\nMrs Wickam put her own construction on Paul’s eccentricities; and being confirmed in her low spirits by a perplexed view of chimneys from the room where she was accustomed to sit, and by the noise of the wind, and by the general dulness (gashliness was Mrs Wickam’s strong expression) of her present life, deduced the most dismal reflections from the foregoing premises. It was a part of Mrs Pipchin’s policy to prevent her own “young hussy”—that was Mrs Pipchin’s generic name for female servant—from communicating with Mrs Wickam: to which end she devoted much of her time to concealing herself behind doors, and springing out on that devoted maiden, whenever she made an approach towards Mrs Wickam’s apartment. But Berry was free to hold what converse she could in that quarter, consistently with the discharge of the multifarious duties at which she toiled incessantly from morning to night; and to Berry Mrs Wickam unburdened her mind.\n\n“What a pretty fellow he is when he’s asleep!” said Berry, stopping to look at Paul in bed, one night when she took up Mrs Wickam’s supper.\n\n“Ah!” sighed Mrs Wickam. “He need be.”\n\n“Why, he’s not ugly when he’s awake,” observed Berry.\n\n“No, Ma’am. Oh, no. No more was my Uncle’s Betsey Jane,” said Mrs Wickam.\n\nBerry looked as if she would like to trace the connexion of ideas between Paul Dombey and Mrs Wickam’s Uncle’s Betsey Jane.\n\n“My Uncle’s wife,” Mrs Wickam went on to say, “died just like his Mama. My Uncle’s child took on just as Master Paul do.”\n\n“Took on! You don’t think he grieves for his Mama, sure?” argued Berry, sitting down on the side of the bed. “He can’t remember anything about her, you know, Mrs Wickam. It’s not possible.”\n\n“No, Ma’am,” said Mrs Wickam “No more did my Uncle’s child. But my Uncle’s child said very strange things sometimes, and looked very strange, and went on very strange, and was very strange altogether. My Uncle’s child made people’s blood run cold, some times, she did!”\n\n“How?” asked Berry.\n\n“I wouldn’t have sat up all night alone with Betsey Jane!” said Mrs Wickam, “not if you’d have put Wickam into business next morning for himself. I couldn’t have done it, Miss Berry.\n\nMiss Berry naturally asked why not? But Mrs Wickam, agreeably to the usage of some ladies in her condition, pursued her own branch of the subject, without any compunction.\n\n“Betsey Jane,” said Mrs Wickam, “was as sweet a child as I could wish to see. I couldn’t wish to see a sweeter. Everything that a child could have in the way of illnesses, Betsey Jane had come through. The cramps was as common to her,” said Mrs Wickam, “as biles is to yourself, Miss Berry.” Miss Berry involuntarily wrinkled her nose.\n\n“But Betsey Jane,” said Mrs Wickam, lowering her voice, and looking round the room, and towards Paul in bed, “had been minded, in her cradle, by her departed mother. I couldn’t say how, nor I couldn’t say when, nor I couldn’t say whether the dear child knew it or not, but Betsey Jane had been watched by her mother, Miss Berry!” and Mrs Wickam, with a very white face, and with watery eyes, and with a tremulous voice, again looked fearfully round the room, and towards Paul in bed.\n\n“Nonsense!” cried Miss Berry—somewhat resentful of the idea.\n\n“You may say nonsense! I ain’t offended, Miss. I hope you may be able to think in your own conscience that it is nonsense; you’ll find your spirits all the better for it in this—you’ll excuse my being so free—in this burying-ground of a place; which is wearing of me down. Master Paul’s a little restless in his sleep. Pat his back, if you please.”\n\n“Of course you think,” said Berry, gently doing what she was asked, “that he has been nursed by his mother, too?”\n\n“Betsey Jane,” returned Mrs Wickam in her most solemn tones, “was put upon as that child has been put upon, and changed as that child has changed. I have seen her sit, often and often, think, think, thinking, like him. I have seen her look, often and often, old, old, old, like him. I have heard her, many a time, talk just like him. I consider that child and Betsey Jane on the same footing entirely, Miss Berry.”\n\n“Is your Uncle’s child alive?” asked Berry.\n\n“Yes, Miss, she is alive,” returned Mrs Wickam with an air of triumph, for it was evident. Miss Berry expected the reverse; “and is married to a silver-chaser. Oh yes, Miss, SHE is alive,” said Mrs Wickam, laying strong stress on her nominative case.\n\nIt being clear that somebody was dead, Mrs Pipchin’s niece inquired who it was.\n\n“I wouldn’t wish to make you uneasy,” returned Mrs Wickam, pursuing her supper. “Don’t ask me.”\n\nThis was the surest way of being asked again. Miss Berry repeated her question, therefore; and after some resistance, and reluctance, Mrs Wickam laid down her knife, and again glancing round the room and at Paul in bed, replied:\n\n“She took fancies to people; whimsical fancies, some of them; others, affections that one might expect to see—only stronger than common. They all died.”\n\nThis was so very unexpected and awful to Mrs Pipchin’s niece, that she sat upright on the hard edge of the bedstead, breathing short, and surveying her informant with looks of undisguised alarm.\n\nMrs Wickam shook her left fore-finger stealthily towards the bed where Florence lay; then turned it upside down, and made several emphatic points at the floor; immediately below which was the parlour in which Mrs Pipchin habitually consumed the toast.\n\n“Remember my words, Miss Berry,” said Mrs Wickam, “and be thankful that Master Paul is not too fond of you. I am, that he’s not too fond of me, I assure you; though there isn’t much to live for—you’ll excuse my being so free—in this jail of a house!”\n\nMiss Berry’s emotion might have led to her patting Paul too hard on the back, or might have produced a cessation of that soothing monotony, but he turned in his bed just now, and, presently awaking, sat up in it with his hair hot and wet from the effects of some childish dream, and asked for Florence.\n\nShe was out of her own bed at the first sound of his voice; and bending over his pillow immediately, sang him to sleep again. Mrs Wickam shaking her head, and letting fall several tears, pointed out the little group to Berry, and turned her eyes up to the ceiling.\n\n“He’s asleep now, my dear,” said Mrs Wickam after a pause, “you’d better go to bed again. Don’t you feel cold?”\n\n“No, nurse,” said Florence, laughing. “Not at all.”\n\n“Ah!” sighed Mrs Wickam, and she shook her head again, expressing to the watchful Berry, “we shall be cold enough, some of us, by and by!”\n\nBerry took the frugal supper-tray, with which Mrs Wickam had by this time done, and bade her good-night.\n\n“Good-night, Miss!” returned Wickam softly. “Good-night! Your aunt is an old lady, Miss Berry, and it’s what you must have looked for, often.”\n\nThis consolatory farewell, Mrs Wickam accompanied with a look of heartfelt anguish; and being left alone with the two children again, and becoming conscious that the wind was blowing mournfully, she indulged in melancholy—that cheapest and most accessible of luxuries—until she was overpowered by slumber.\n\nAlthough the niece of Mrs Pipchin did not expect to find that exemplary dragon prostrate on the hearth-rug when she went downstairs, she was relieved to find her unusually fractious and severe, and with every present appearance of intending to live a long time to be a comfort to all who knew her. Nor had she any symptoms of declining, in the course of the ensuing week, when the constitutional viands still continued to disappear in regular succession, notwithstanding that Paul studied her as attentively as ever, and occupied his usual seat between the black skirts and the fender, with unwavering constancy.\n\nBut as Paul himself was no stronger at the expiration of that time than he had been on his first arrival, though he looked much healthier in the face, a little carriage was got for him, in which he could lie at his ease, with an alphabet and other elementary works of reference, and be wheeled down to the sea-side. Consistent in his odd tastes, the child set aside a ruddy-faced lad who was proposed as the drawer of this carriage, and selected, instead, his grandfather—a weazen, old, crab-faced man, in a suit of battered oilskin, who had got tough and stringy from long pickling in salt water, and who smelt like a weedy sea-beach when the tide is out.\n\nWith this notable attendant to pull him along, and Florence always walking by his side, and the despondent Wickam bringing up the rear, he went down to the margin of the ocean every day; and there he would sit or lie in his carriage for hours together: never so distressed as by the company of children—Florence alone excepted, always.\n\n“Go away, if you please,” he would say to any child who came to bear him company. “Thank you, but I don’t want you.”\n\nSome small voice, near his ear, would ask him how he was, perhaps.\n\n“I am very well, I thank you,” he would answer. “But you had better go and play, if you please.”\n\nThen he would turn his head, and watch the child away, and say to Florence, “We don’t want any others, do we? Kiss me, Floy.”\n\nHe had even a dislike, at such times, to the company of Wickam, and was well pleased when she strolled away, as she generally did, to pick up shells and acquaintances. His favourite spot was quite a lonely one, far away from most loungers; and with Florence sitting by his side at work, or reading to him, or talking to him, and the wind blowing on his face, and the water coming up among the wheels of his bed, he wanted nothing more.\n\n“Floy,” he said one day, “where’s India, where that boy’s friends live?”\n\n“Oh, it’s a long, long distance off,” said Florence, raising her eyes from her work.\n\n“Weeks off?” asked Paul.\n\n“Yes dear. Many weeks’ journey, night and day.”\n\n“If you were in India, Floy,” said Paul, after being silent for a minute, “I should—what is it that Mama did? I forget.”\n\n“Loved me!” answered Florence.\n\n“No, no. Don’t I love you now, Floy? What is it?—Died. If you were in India, I should die, Floy.”\n\nShe hurriedly put her work aside, and laid her head down on his pillow, caressing him. And so would she, she said, if he were there. He would be better soon.\n\n“Oh! I am a great deal better now!” he answered. “I don’t mean that. I mean that I should die of being so sorry and so lonely, Floy!”\n\nAnother time, in the same place, he fell asleep, and slept quietly for a long time. Awaking suddenly, he listened, started up, and sat listening.\n\nFlorence asked him what he thought he heard.\n\n“I want to know what it says,” he answered, looking steadily in her face. “The sea” Floy, what is it that it keeps on saying?”\n\nShe told him that it was only the noise of the rolling waves.\n\n“Yes, yes,” he said. “But I know that they are always saying something. Always the same thing. What place is over there?” He rose up, looking eagerly at the horizon.\n\nShe told him that there was another country opposite, but he said he didn’t mean that: he meant further away—farther away!\n\nVery often afterwards, in the midst of their talk, he would break off, to try to understand what it was that the waves were always saying; and would rise up in his couch to look towards that invisible region, far away.\n\n# CHAPTER IX. In which the Wooden Midshipman gets into Trouble\n\nThat spice of romance and love of the marvellous, of which there was a pretty strong infusion in the nature of young Walter Gay, and which the guardianship of his Uncle, old Solomon Gills, had not very much weakened by the waters of stern practical experience, was the occasion of his attaching an uncommon and delightful interest to the adventure of Florence with Good Mrs Brown. He pampered and cherished it in his memory, especially that part of it with which he had been associated: until it became the spoiled child of his fancy, and took its own way, and did what it liked with it.\n\nThe recollection of those incidents, and his own share in them, may have been made the more captivating, perhaps, by the weekly dreamings of old Sol and Captain Cuttle on Sundays. Hardly a Sunday passed, without mysterious references being made by one or other of those worthy chums to Richard Whittington; and the latter gentleman had even gone so far as to purchase a ballad of considerable antiquity, that had long fluttered among many others, chiefly expressive of maritime sentiments, on a dead wall in the Commercial Road: which poetical performance set forth the courtship and nuptials of a promising young coal-whipper with a certain “lovely Peg,” the accomplished daughter of the master and part-owner of a Newcastle collier. In this stirring legend, Captain Cuttle descried a profound metaphysical bearing on the case of Walter and Florence; and it excited him so much, that on very festive occasions, as birthdays and a few other non-Dominical holidays, he would roar through the whole song in the little back parlour; making an amazing shake on the word Pe-e-eg, with which every verse concluded, in compliment to the heroine of the piece.\n\nBut a frank, free-spirited, open-hearted boy, is not much given to analysing the nature of his own feelings, however strong their hold upon him: and Walter would have found it difficult to decide this point. He had a great affection for the wharf where he had encountered Florence, and for the streets (albeit not enchanting in themselves) by which they had come home. The shoes that had so often tumbled off by the way, he preserved in his own room; and, sitting in the little back parlour of an evening, he had drawn a whole gallery of fancy portraits of Good Mrs Brown. It may be that he became a little smarter in his dress after that memorable occasion; and he certainly liked in his leisure time to walk towards that quarter of the town where Mr Dombey’s house was situated, on the vague chance of passing little Florence in the street. But the sentiment of all this was as boyish and innocent as could be. Florence was very pretty, and it is pleasant to admire a pretty face. Florence was defenceless and weak, and it was a proud thought that he had been able to render her any protection and assistance. Florence was the most grateful little creature in the world, and it was delightful to see her bright gratitude beaming in her face. Florence was neglected and coldly looked upon, and his breast was full of youthful interest for the slighted child in her dull, stately home.\n\nThus it came about that, perhaps some half-a-dozen times in the course of the year, Walter pulled off his hat to Florence in the street, and Florence would stop to shake hands. Mrs Wickam (who, with a characteristic alteration of his name, invariably spoke of him as “Young Graves”) was so well used to this, knowing the story of their acquaintance, that she took no heed of it at all. Miss Nipper, on the other hand, rather looked out for these occasions: her sensitive young heart being secretly propitiated by Walter’s good looks, and inclining to the belief that its sentiments were responded to.\n\nIn this way, Walter, so far from forgetting or losing sight of his acquaintance with Florence, only remembered it better and better. As to its adventurous beginning, and all those little circumstances which gave it a distinctive character and relish, he took them into account, more as a pleasant story very agreeable to his imagination, and not to be dismissed from it, than as a part of any matter of fact with which he was concerned. They set off Florence very much, to his fancy; but not himself. Sometimes he thought (and then he walked very fast) what a grand thing it would have been for him to have been going to sea on the day after that first meeting, and to have gone, and to have done wonders there, and to have stopped away a long time, and to have come back an Admiral of all the colours of the dolphin, or at least a Post-Captain with epaulettes of insupportable brightness, and have married Florence (then a beautiful young woman) in spite of Mr Dombey’s teeth, cravat, and watch-chain, and borne her away to the blue shores of somewhere or other, triumphantly. But these flights of fancy seldom burnished the brass plate of Dombey and Son’s Offices into a tablet of golden hope, or shed a brilliant lustre on their dirty skylights; and when the Captain and Uncle Sol talked about Richard Whittington and masters’ daughters, Walter felt that he understood his true position at Dombey and Son’s, much better than they did.\n\nSo it was that he went on doing what he had to do from day to day, in a cheerful, pains-taking, merry spirit; and saw through the sanguine complexion of Uncle Sol and Captain Cuttle; and yet entertained a thousand indistinct and visionary fancies of his own, to which theirs were work-a-day probabilities. Such was his condition at the Pipchin period, when he looked a little older than of yore, but not much; and was the same light-footed, light-hearted, light-headed lad, as when he charged into the parlour at the head of Uncle Sol and the imaginary boarders, and lighted him to bring up the Madeira.\n\n“Uncle Sol,” said Walter, “I don’t think you’re well. You haven’t eaten any breakfast. I shall bring a doctor to you, if you go on like this.”\n\n“He can’t give me what I want, my boy,” said Uncle Sol. “At least he is in good practice if he can—and then he wouldn’t.”\n\n“What is it, Uncle? Customers?”\n\n“Ay,” returned Solomon, with a sigh. “Customers would do.”\n\n“Confound it, Uncle!” said Walter, putting down his breakfast cup with a clatter, and striking his hand on the table: “when I see the people going up and down the street in shoals all day, and passing and re-passing the shop every minute, by scores, I feel half tempted to rush out, collar somebody, bring him in, and make him buy fifty pounds’ worth of instruments for ready money. What are you looking in at the door for?—” continued Walter, apostrophizing an old gentleman with a powdered head (inaudibly to him of course), who was staring at a ship’s telescope with all his might and main. “That’s no use. I could do that. Come in and buy it!”\n\nThe old gentleman, however, having satiated his curiosity, walked calmly away.\n\n“There he goes!” said Walter. “That’s the way with ’em all. But, Uncle—I say, Uncle Sol”—for the old man was meditating and had not responded to his first appeal. “Don’t be cast down. Don’t be out of spirits, Uncle. When orders do come, they’ll come in such a crowd, you won’t be able to execute ’em.”\n\n“I shall be past executing ’em, whenever they come, my boy,” returned Solomon Gills. “They’ll never come to this shop again, till I am out of t.”\n\n“I say, Uncle! You musn’t really, you know!” urged Walter. “Don’t!”\n\nOld Sol endeavoured to assume a cheery look, and smiled across the little table at him as pleasantly as he could.\n\n“There’s nothing more than usual the matter; is there, Uncle?” said Walter, leaning his elbows on the tea tray, and bending over, to speak the more confidentially and kindly. “Be open with me, Uncle, if there is, and tell me all about it.”\n\n“No, no, no,” returned Old Sol. “More than usual? No, no. What should there be the matter more than usual?”\n\nWalter answered with an incredulous shake of his head. “That’s what I want to know,” he said, “and you ask me! I’ll tell you what, Uncle, when I see you like this, I am quite sorry that I live with you.”\n\nOld Sol opened his eyes involuntarily.\n\n“Yes. Though nobody ever was happier than I am and always have been with you, I am quite sorry that I live with you, when I see you with anything in your mind.”\n\n“I am a little dull at such times, I know,” observed Solomon, meekly rubbing his hands.\n\n“What I mean, Uncle Sol,” pursued Walter, bending over a little more to pat him on the shoulder, “is, that then I feel you ought to have, sitting here and pouring out the tea instead of me, a nice little dumpling of a wife, you know,—a comfortable, capital, cosy old lady, who was just a match for you, and knew how to manage you, and keep you in good heart. Here am I, as loving a nephew as ever was (I am sure I ought to be!) but I am only a nephew, and I can’t be such a companion to you when you’re low and out of sorts as she would have made herself, years ago, though I’m sure I’d give any money if I could cheer you up. And so I say, when I see you with anything on your mind, that I feel quite sorry you haven’t got somebody better about you than a blundering young rough-and-tough boy like me, who has got the will to console you, Uncle, but hasn’t got the way—hasn’t got the way,” repeated Walter, reaching over further yet, to shake his Uncle by the hand.\n\n“Wally, my dear boy,” said Solomon, “if the cosy little old lady had taken her place in this parlour five and forty years ago, I never could have been fonder of her than I am of you.”\n\n“I know that, Uncle Sol,” returned Walter. “Lord bless you, I know that. But you wouldn’t have had the whole weight of any uncomfortable secrets if she had been with you, because she would have known how to relieve you of ’em, and I don’t.”\n\n“Yes, yes, you do,” returned the Instrument-maker.\n\n“Well then, what’s the matter, Uncle Sol?” said Walter, coaxingly. “Come! What’s the matter?”\n\nSolomon Gills persisted that there was nothing the matter; and maintained it so resolutely, that his nephew had no resource but to make a very indifferent imitation of believing him.\n\n“All I can say is, Uncle Sol, that if there is—”\n\n“But there isn’t,” said Solomon.\n\n“Very well,” said Walter. “Then I’ve no more to say; and that’s lucky, for my time’s up for going to business. I shall look in by-and-by when I’m out, to see how you get on, Uncle. And mind, Uncle! I’ll never believe you again, and never tell you anything more about Mr Carker the Junior, if I find out that you have been deceiving me!”\n\nSolomon Gills laughingly defied him to find out anything of the kind; and Walter, revolving in his thoughts all sorts of impracticable ways of making fortunes and placing the wooden Midshipman in a position of independence, betook himself to the offices of Dombey and Son with a heavier countenance than he usually carried there.\n\nThere lived in those days, round the corner—in Bishopsgate Street Without—one Brogley, sworn broker and appraiser, who kept a shop where every description of second-hand furniture was exhibited in the most uncomfortable aspect, and under circumstances and in combinations the most completely foreign to its purpose. Dozens of chairs hooked on to washing-stands, which with difficulty poised themselves on the shoulders of sideboards, which in their turn stood upon the wrong side of dining-tables, gymnastic with their legs upward on the tops of other dining-tables, were among its most reasonable arrangements. A banquet array of dish-covers, wine-glasses, and decanters was generally to be seen, spread forth upon the bosom of a four-post bedstead, for the entertainment of such genial company as half-a-dozen pokers, and a hall lamp. A set of window curtains with no windows belonging to them, would be seen gracefully draping a barricade of chests of drawers, loaded with little jars from chemists’ shops; while a homeless hearthrug severed from its natural companion the fireside, braved the shrewd east wind in its adversity, and trembled in melancholy accord with the shrill complainings of a cabinet piano, wasting away, a string a day, and faintly resounding to the noises of the street in its jangling and distracted brain. Of motionless clocks that never stirred a finger, and seemed as incapable of being successfully wound up, as the pecuniary affairs of their former owners, there was always great choice in Mr Brogley’s shop; and various looking-glasses, accidentally placed at compound interest of reflection and refraction, presented to the eye an eternal perspective of bankruptcy and ruin.\n\nMr Brogley himself was a moist-eyed, pink-complexioned, crisp-haired man, of a bulky figure and an easy temper—for that class of Caius Marius who sits upon the ruins of other people’s Carthages, can keep up his spirits well enough. He had looked in at Solomon’s shop sometimes, to ask a question about articles in Solomon’s way of business; and Walter knew him sufficiently to give him good day when they met in the street. But as that was the extent of the broker’s acquaintance with Solomon Gills also, Walter was not a little surprised when he came back in the course of the forenoon, agreeably to his promise, to find Mr Brogley sitting in the back parlour with his hands in his pockets, and his hat hanging up behind the door.\n\n“Well, Uncle Sol!” said Walter. The old man was sitting ruefully on the opposite side of the table, with his spectacles over his eyes, for a wonder, instead of on his forehead. “How are you now?”\n\nSolomon shook his head, and waved one hand towards the broker, as introducing him.\n\n“Is there anything the matter?” asked Walter, with a catching in his breath.\n\n“No, no. There’s nothing the matter, said Mr Brogley. “Don’t let it put you out of the way.”\n\nWalter looked from the broker to his Uncle in mute amazement.\n\n“The fact is,” said Mr Brogley, “there’s a little payment on a bond debt —three hundred and seventy odd, overdue: and I’m in possession.”\n\n“In possession!” cried Walter, looking round at the shop.\n\n“Ah!” said Mr Brogley, in confidential assent, and nodding his head as if he would urge the advisability of their all being comfortable together. “It’s an execution. That’s what it is. Don’t let it put you out of the way. I come myself, because of keeping it quiet and sociable. You know me. It’s quite private.”\n\n“Uncle Sol!” faltered Walter.\n\n“Wally, my boy,” returned his uncle. “It’s the first time. Such a calamity never happened to me before. I’m an old man to begin.” Pushing up his spectacles again (for they were useless any longer to conceal his emotion), he covered his face with his hand, and sobbed aloud, and his tears fell down upon his coffee-coloured waistcoat.\n\n“Uncle Sol! Pray! oh don’t!” exclaimed Walter, who really felt a thrill of terror in seeing the old man weep. “For God’s sake don’t do that. Mr Brogley, what shall I do?”\n\n“I should recommend you looking up a friend or so,” said Mr Brogley, “and talking it over.”\n\n“To be sure!” cried Walter, catching at anything. “Certainly! Thankee. Captain Cuttle’s the man, Uncle. Wait till I run to Captain Cuttle. Keep your eye upon my Uncle, will you, Mr Brogley, and make him as comfortable as you can while I am gone? Don’t despair, Uncle Sol. Try and keep a good heart, there’s a dear fellow!”\n\nSaying this with great fervour, and disregarding the old man’s broken remonstrances, Walter dashed out of the shop again as hard as he could go; and, having hurried round to the office to excuse himself on the plea of his Uncle’s sudden illness, set off, full speed, for Captain Cuttle’s residence.\n\nEverything seemed altered as he ran along the streets. There were the usual entanglement and noise of carts, drays, omnibuses, waggons, and foot passengers, but the misfortune that had fallen on the wooden Midshipman made it strange and new. Houses and shops were different from what they used to be, and bore Mr Brogley’s warrant on their fronts in large characters. The broker seemed to have got hold of the very churches; for their spires rose into the sky with an unwonted air. Even the sky itself was changed, and had an execution in it plainly.\n\nCaptain Cuttle lived on the brink of a little canal near the India Docks, where there was a swivel bridge which opened now and then to let some wandering monster of a ship come roaming up the street like a stranded leviathan. The gradual change from land to water, on the approach to Captain Cuttle’s lodgings, was curious. It began with the erection of flagstaffs, as appurtenances to public-houses; then came slop-sellers’ shops, with Guernsey shirts, sou’wester hats, and canvas pantaloons, at once the tightest and the loosest of their order, hanging up outside. These were succeeded by anchor and chain-cable forges, where sledgehammers were dinging upon iron all day long. Then came rows of houses, with little vane-surmounted masts uprearing themselves from among the scarlet beans. Then, ditches. Then, pollard willows. Then, more ditches. Then, unaccountable patches of dirty water, hardly to be descried, for the ships that covered them. Then, the air was perfumed with chips; and all other trades were swallowed up in mast, oar, and block-making, and boatbuilding. Then, the ground grew marshy and unsettled. Then, there was nothing to be smelt but rum and sugar. Then, Captain Cuttle’s lodgings—at once a first floor and a top storey, in Brig Place—were close before you.\n\nThe Captain was one of those timber-looking men, suits of oak as well as hearts, whom it is almost impossible for the liveliest imagination to separate from any part of their dress, however insignificant. Accordingly, when Walter knocked at the door, and the Captain instantly poked his head out of one of his little front windows, and hailed him, with the hard glared hat already on it, and the shirt-collar like a sail, and the wide suit of blue, all standing as usual, Walter was as fully persuaded that he was always in that state, as if the Captain had been a bird and those had been his feathers.\n\n“Wal”r, my lad!” said Captain Cuttle. “Stand by and knock again. Hard! It’s washing day.”\n\nWalter, in his impatience, gave a prodigious thump with the knocker.\n\n“Hard it is!” said Captain Cuttle, and immediately drew in his head, as if he expected a squall.\n\nNor was he mistaken: for a widow lady, with her sleeves rolled up to her shoulders, and her arms frothy with soap-suds and smoking with hot water, replied to the summons with startling rapidity. Before she looked at Walter she looked at the knocker, and then, measuring him with her eyes from head to foot, said she wondered he had left any of it.\n\n“Captain Cuttle’s at home, I know,” said Walter with a conciliatory smile.\n\n“Is he?” replied the widow lady. “In-deed!”\n\n“He has just been speaking to me,” said Walter, in breathless explanation.\n\n“Has he?” replied the widow lady. “Then p’raps you’ll give him Mrs MacStinger’s respects, and say that the next time he lowers himself and his lodgings by talking out of the winder she’ll thank him to come down and open the door too.” Mrs MacStinger spoke loud, and listened for any observations that might be offered from the first floor.\n\n“I’ll mention it,” said Walter, “if you’ll have the goodness to let me in, Ma’am.”\n\nFor he was repelled by a wooden fortification extending across the doorway, and put there to prevent the little MacStingers in their moments of recreation from tumbling down the steps.\n\n“A boy that can knock my door down,” said Mrs MacStinger, contemptuously, “can get over that, I should hope!” But Walter, taking this as a permission to enter, and getting over it, Mrs MacStinger immediately demanded whether an Englishwoman’s house was her castle or not; and whether she was to be broke in upon by “raff.” On these subjects her thirst for information was still very importunate, when Walter, having made his way up the little staircase through an artificial fog occasioned by the washing, which covered the banisters with a clammy perspiration, entered Captain Cuttle’s room, and found that gentleman in ambush behind the door.\n\n“Never owed her a penny, Wal”r,” said Captain Cuttle, in a low voice, and with visible marks of trepidation on his countenance. “Done her a world of good turns, and the children too. Vixen at times, though. Whew!”\n\n“I should go away, Captain Cuttle,” said Walter.\n\n“Dursn’t do it, Wal”r,” returned the Captain. “She’d find me out, wherever I went. Sit down. How’s Gills?”\n\nThe Captain was dining (in his hat) off cold loin of mutton, porter, and some smoking hot potatoes, which he had cooked himself, and took out of a little saucepan before the fire as he wanted them. He unscrewed his hook at dinner-time, and screwed a knife into its wooden socket instead, with which he had already begun to peel one of these potatoes for Walter. His rooms were very small, and strongly impregnated with tobacco-smoke, but snug enough: everything being stowed away, as if there were an earthquake regularly every half-hour.\n\n“How’s Gills?” inquired the Captain.\n\nWalter, who had by this time recovered his breath, and lost his spirits—or such temporary spirits as his rapid journey had given him—looked at his questioner for a moment, said “Oh, Captain Cuttle!” and burst into tears.\n\nNo words can describe the Captain’s consternation at this sight. Mrs MacStinger faded into nothing before it. He dropped the potato and the fork—and would have dropped the knife too if he could—and sat gazing at the boy, as if he expected to hear next moment that a gulf had opened in the City, which had swallowed up his old friend, coffee-coloured suit, buttons, chronometer, spectacles, and all.\n\nBut when Walter told him what was really the matter, Captain Cuttle, after a moment’s reflection, started up into full activity. He emptied out of a little tin canister on the top shelf of the cupboard, his whole stock of ready money (amounting to thirteen pounds and half-a-crown), which he transferred to one of the pockets of his square blue coat; further enriched that repository with the contents of his plate chest, consisting of two withered atomies of tea-spoons, and an obsolete pair of knock-knee’d sugar-tongs; pulled up his immense double-cased silver watch from the depths in which it reposed, to assure himself that that valuable was sound and whole; re-attached the hook to his right wrist; and seizing the stick covered over with knobs, bade Walter come along.\n\nRemembering, however, in the midst of his virtuous excitement, that Mrs MacStinger might be lying in wait below, Captain Cuttle hesitated at last, not without glancing at the window, as if he had some thoughts of escaping by that unusual means of egress, rather than encounter his terrible enemy. He decided, however, in favour of stratagem.\n\n“Wal”r,” said the Captain, with a timid wink, “go afore, my lad. Sing out, ‘good-bye, Captain Cuttle,’ when you’re in the passage, and shut the door. Then wait at the corner of the street “till you see me.\n\nThese directions were not issued without a previous knowledge of the enemy’s tactics, for when Walter got downstairs, Mrs MacStinger glided out of the little back kitchen, like an avenging spirit. But not gliding out upon the Captain, as she had expected, she merely made a further allusion to the knocker, and glided in again.\n\nSome five minutes elapsed before Captain Cuttle could summon courage to attempt his escape; for Walter waited so long at the street corner, looking back at the house, before there were any symptoms of the hard glazed hat. At length the Captain burst out of the door with the suddenness of an explosion, and coming towards him at a great pace, and never once looking over his shoulder, pretended, as soon as they were well out of the street, to whistle a tune.\n\n“Uncle much hove down, Wal”r?” inquired the Captain, as they were walking along.\n\n“I am afraid so. If you had seen him this morning, you would never have forgotten it.”\n\n“Walk fast, Wal”r, my lad,” returned the Captain, mending his pace; “and walk the same all the days of your life. Overhaul the catechism for that advice, and keep it!”\n\nThe Captain was too busy with his own thoughts of Solomon Gills, mingled perhaps with some reflections on his late escape from Mrs MacStinger, to offer any further quotations on the way for Walter’s moral improvement They interchanged no other word until they arrived at old Sol’s door, where the unfortunate wooden Midshipman, with his instrument at his eye, seemed to be surveying the whole horizon in search of some friend to help him out of his difficulty.\n\n“Gills!” said the Captain, hurrying into the back parlour, and taking him by the hand quite tenderly. “Lay your head well to the wind, and we’ll fight through it. All you’ve got to do,” said the Captain, with the solemnity of a man who was delivering himself of one of the most precious practical tenets ever discovered by human wisdom, “is to lay your head well to the wind, and we’ll fight through it!”\n\nOld Sol returned the pressure of his hand, and thanked him.\n\nCaptain Cuttle, then, with a gravity suitable to the nature of the occasion, put down upon the table the two tea-spoons and the sugar-tongs, the silver watch, and the ready money; and asked Mr Brogley, the broker, what the damage was.\n\n“Come! What do you make of it?” said Captain Cuttle.\n\n“Why, Lord help you!” returned the broker; “you don’t suppose that property’s of any use, do you?”\n\n“Why not?” inquired the Captain.\n\n“Why? The amount’s three hundred and seventy, odd,” replied the broker.\n\n“Never mind,” returned the Captain, though he was evidently dismayed by the figures: “all’s fish that comes to your net, I suppose?”\n\n“Certainly,” said Mr Brogley. “But sprats ain’t whales, you know.”\n\nThe philosophy of this observation seemed to strike the Captain. He ruminated for a minute; eyeing the broker, meanwhile, as a deep genius; and then called the Instrument-maker aside.\n\n“Gills,” said Captain Cuttle, “what’s the bearings of this business? Who’s the creditor?”\n\n“Hush!” returned the old man. “Come away. Don’t speak before Wally. It’s a matter of security for Wally’s father—an old bond. I’ve paid a good deal of it, Ned, but the times are so bad with me that I can’t do more just now. I’ve foreseen it, but I couldn’t help it. Not a word before Wally, for all the world.”\n\n“You’ve got some money, haven’t you?” whispered the Captain.\n\n“Yes, yes—oh yes—I’ve got some,” returned old Sol, first putting his hands into his empty pockets, and then squeezing his Welsh wig between them, as if he thought he might wring some gold out of it; “but I—the little I have got, isn’t convertible, Ned; it can’t be got at. I have been trying to do something with it for Wally, and I’m old fashioned, and behind the time. It’s here and there, and—and, in short, it’s as good as nowhere,” said the old man, looking in bewilderment about him.\n\nHe had so much the air of a half-witted person who had been hiding his money in a variety of places, and had forgotten where, that the Captain followed his eyes, not without a faint hope that he might remember some few hundred pounds concealed up the chimney, or down in the cellar. But Solomon Gills knew better than that.\n\n“I’m behind the time altogether, my dear Ned,” said Sol, in resigned despair, “a long way. It’s no use my lagging on so far behind it. The stock had better be sold—it’s worth more than this debt—and I had better go and die somewhere, on the balance. I haven’t any energy left. I don’t understand things. This had better be the end of it. Let ’em sell the stock and take him down,” said the old man, pointing feebly to the wooden Midshipman, “and let us both be broken up together.”\n\n“And what d’ye mean to do with Wal”r?” said the Captain. “There, there! Sit ye down, Gills, sit ye down, and let me think o’ this. If I warn’t a man on a small annuity, that was large enough till today, I hadn’t need to think of it. But you only lay your head well to the wind,” said the Captain, again administering that unanswerable piece of consolation, “and you’re all right!”\n\nOld Sol thanked him from his heart, and went and laid it against the back parlour fire-place instead.\n\nCaptain Cuttle walked up and down the shop for some time, cogitating profoundly, and bringing his bushy black eyebrows to bear so heavily on his nose, like clouds setting on a mountain, that Walter was afraid to offer any interruption to the current of his reflections. Mr Brogley, who was averse to being any constraint upon the party, and who had an ingenious cast of mind, went, softly whistling, among the stock; rattling weather-glasses, shaking compasses as if they were physic, catching up keys with loadstones, looking through telescopes, endeavouring to make himself acquainted with the use of the globes, setting parallel rulers astride on to his nose, and amusing himself with other philosophical transactions.\n\n“Wal”r!” said the Captain at last. “I’ve got it.”\n\n“Have you, Captain Cuttle?” cried Walter, with great animation.\n\n“Come this way, my lad,” said the Captain. “The stock’s the security. I’m another. Your governor’s the man to advance money.”\n\n“Mr Dombey!” faltered Walter.\n\nThe Captain nodded gravely. “Look at him,” he said. “Look at Gills. If they was to sell off these things now, he’d die of it. You know he would. We mustn’t leave a stone unturned—and there’s a stone for you.”\n\n“A stone!—Mr Dombey!” faltered Walter.\n\n“You run round to the office, first of all, and see if he’s there,” said Captain Cuttle, clapping him on the back. “Quick!”\n\nWalter felt he must not dispute the command—a glance at his Uncle would have determined him if he had felt otherwise—and disappeared to execute it. He soon returned, out of breath, to say that Mr Dombey was not there. It was Saturday, and he had gone to Brighton.\n\n“I tell you what, Wal”r!” said the Captain, who seemed to have prepared himself for this contingency in his absence. “We’ll go to Brighton. I’ll back you, my boy. I’ll back you, Wal”r. We’ll go to Brighton by the afternoon’s coach.”\n\nIf the application must be made to Mr Dombey at all, which was awful to think of, Walter felt that he would rather prefer it alone and unassisted, than backed by the personal influence of Captain Cuttle, to which he hardly thought Mr Dombey would attach much weight. But as the Captain appeared to be of quite another opinion, and was bent upon it, and as his friendship was too zealous and serious to be trifled with by one so much younger than himself, he forbore to hint the least objection. Cuttle, therefore, taking a hurried leave of Solomon Gills, and returning the ready money, the teaspoons, the sugar-tongs, and the silver watch, to his pocket—with a view, as Walter thought, with horror, to making a gorgeous impression on Mr Dombey—bore him off to the coach-office, without a minute’s delay, and repeatedly assured him, on the road, that he would stick by him to the last.\n\n# CHAPTER X. Containing the Sequel of the Midshipman’s Disaster\n\nMajor Bagstock, after long and frequent observation of Paul, across Princess’s Place, through his double-barrelled opera-glass; and after receiving many minute reports, daily, weekly, and monthly, on that subject, from the native who kept himself in constant communication with Miss Tox’s maid for that purpose; came to the conclusion that Dombey, Sir, was a man to be known, and that J. B. was the boy to make his acquaintance.\n\nMiss Tox, however, maintaining her reserved behaviour, and frigidly declining to understand the Major whenever he called (which he often did) on any little fishing excursion connected with this project, the Major, in spite of his constitutional toughness and slyness, was fain to leave the accomplishment of his desire in some measure to chance, “which,” as he was used to observe with chuckles at his club, “has been fifty to one in favour of Joey B., Sir, ever since his elder brother died of Yellow Jack in the West Indies.”\n\nIt was some time coming to his aid in the present instance, but it befriended him at last. When the dark servant, with full particulars, reported Miss Tox absent on Brighton service, the Major was suddenly touched with affectionate reminiscences of his friend Bill Bitherstone of Bengal, who had written to ask him, if he ever went that way, to bestow a call upon his only son. But when the same dark servant reported Paul at Mrs Pipchin’s, and the Major, referring to the letter favoured by Master Bitherstone on his arrival in England—to which he had never had the least idea of paying any attention—saw the opening that presented itself, he was made so rabid by the gout, with which he happened to be then laid up, that he threw a footstool at the dark servant in return for his intelligence, and swore he would be the death of the rascal before he had done with him: which the dark servant was more than half disposed to believe.\n\nAt length the Major being released from his fit, went one Saturday growling down to Brighton, with the native behind him; apostrophizing Miss Tox all the way, and gloating over the prospect of carrying by storm the distinguished friend to whom she attached so much mystery, and for whom she had deserted him.\n\n“Would you, Ma’am, would you!” said the Major, straining with vindictiveness, and swelling every already swollen vein in his head. “Would you give Joey B. the go-by, Ma’am? Not yet, Ma’am, not yet! Damme, not yet, Sir. Joe is awake, Ma’am. Bagstock is alive, Sir. J. B. knows a move or two, Ma’am. Josh has his weather-eye open, Sir. You’ll find him tough, Ma’am. Tough, Sir, tough is Joseph. Tough, and de-vilish sly!”\n\nAnd very tough indeed Master Bitherstone found him, when he took that young gentleman out for a walk. But the Major, with his complexion like a Stilton cheese, and his eyes like a prawn’s, went roving about, perfectly indifferent to Master Bitherstone’s amusement, and dragging Master Bitherstone along, while he looked about him high and low, for Mr Dombey and his children.\n\nIn good time the Major, previously instructed by Mrs Pipchin, spied out Paul and Florence, and bore down upon them; there being a stately gentleman (Mr Dombey, doubtless) in their company. Charging with Master Bitherstone into the very heart of the little squadron, it fell out, of course, that Master Bitherstone spoke to his fellow-sufferers. Upon that the Major stopped to notice and admire them; remembered with amazement that he had seen and spoken to them at his friend Miss Tox’s in Princess’s Place; opined that Paul was a devilish fine fellow, and his own little friend; inquired if he remembered Joey B. the Major; and finally, with a sudden recollection of the conventionalities of life, turned and apologised to Mr Dombey.\n\n“But my little friend here, Sir,” said the Major, “makes a boy of me again: An old soldier, Sir—Major Bagstock, at your service—is not ashamed to confess it.” Here the Major lifted his hat. “Damme, Sir,” cried the Major with sudden warmth, “I envy you.” Then he recollected himself, and added, “Excuse my freedom.”\n\nMr Dombey begged he wouldn’t mention it.\n\n“An old campaigner, Sir,” said the Major, “a smoke-dried, sun-burnt, used-up, invalided old dog of a Major, Sir, was not afraid of being condemned for his whim by a man like Mr Dombey. I have the honour of addressing Mr Dombey, I believe?”\n\n“I am the present unworthy representative of that name, Major,” returned Mr Dombey.\n\n“By G—, Sir!” said the Major, “it’s a great name. It’s a name, Sir,” said the Major firmly, as if he defied Mr Dombey to contradict him, and would feel it his painful duty to bully him if he did, “that is known and honoured in the British possessions abroad. It is a name, Sir, that a man is proud to recognise. There is nothing adulatory in Joseph Bagstock, Sir. His Royal Highness the Duke of York observed on more than one occasion, ‘there is no adulation in Joey. He is a plain old soldier is Joe. He is tough to a fault is Joseph:’ but it’s a great name, Sir. By the Lord, it’s a great name!” said the Major, solemnly.\n\n“You are good enough to rate it higher than it deserves, perhaps, Major,” returned Mr Dombey.\n\n“No, Sir,” said the Major, in a severe tone. No, Mr Dombey, let us understand each other. That is not the Bagstock vein, Sir. You don’t know Joseph B. He is a blunt old blade is Josh. No flattery in him, Sir. Nothing like it.”\n\nMr Dombey inclined his head, and said he believed him to be in earnest, and that his high opinion was gratifying.\n\n“My little friend here, Sir,” croaked the Major, looking as amiably as he could, on Paul, “will certify for Joseph Bagstock that he is a thorough-going, down-right, plain-spoken, old Trump, Sir, and nothing more. That boy, Sir,” said the Major in a lower tone, “will live in history. That boy, Sir, is not a common production. Take care of him, Mr Dombey.”\n\nMr Dombey seemed to intimate that he would endeavour to do so.\n\n“Here is a boy here, Sir,” pursued the Major, confidentially, and giving him a thrust with his cane. “Son of Bitherstone of Bengal. Bill Bitherstone formerly of ours. That boy’s father and myself, Sir, were sworn friends. Wherever you went, Sir, you heard of nothing but Bill Bitherstone and Joe Bagstock. Am I blind to that boy’s defects? By no means. He’s a fool, Sir.”\n\nMr Dombey glanced at the libelled Master Bitherstone, of whom he knew at least as much as the Major did, and said, in quite a complacent manner, “Really?”\n\n“That is what he is, sir,” said the Major. “He’s a fool. Joe Bagstock never minces matters. The son of my old friend Bill Bitherstone, of Bengal, is a born fool, Sir.” Here the Major laughed till he was almost black. “My little friend is destined for a public school, I presume, Mr Dombey?” said the Major when he had recovered.\n\n“I am not quite decided,” returned Mr Dombey. “I think not. He is delicate.”\n\n“If he’s delicate, Sir,” said the Major, “you are right. None but the tough fellows could live through it, Sir, at Sandhurst. We put each other to the torture there, Sir. We roasted the new fellows at a slow fire, and hung ’em out of a three pair of stairs window, with their heads downwards. Joseph Bagstock, Sir, was held out of the window by the heels of his boots, for thirteen minutes by the college clock.”\n\nThe Major might have appealed to his countenance in corroboration of this story. It certainly looked as if he had hung out a little too long.\n\n“But it made us what we were, Sir,” said the Major, settling his shirt frill. “We were iron, Sir, and it forged us. Are you remaining here, Mr Dombey?”\n\n“I generally come down once a week, Major,” returned that gentleman. “I stay at the Bedford.”\n\n“I shall have the honour of calling at the Bedford, Sir, if you’ll permit me,” said the Major. “Joey B., Sir, is not in general a calling man, but Mr Dombey’s is not a common name. I am much indebted to my little friend, Sir, for the honour of this introduction.”\n\nMr Dombey made a very gracious reply; and Major Bagstock, having patted Paul on the head, and said of Florence that her eyes would play the Devil with the youngsters before long—“and the oldsters too, Sir, if you come to that,” added the Major, chuckling very much—stirred up Master Bitherstone with his walking-stick, and departed with that young gentleman, at a kind of half-trot; rolling his head and coughing with great dignity, as he staggered away, with his legs very wide asunder.\n\nIn fulfilment of his promise, the Major afterwards called on Mr Dombey; and Mr Dombey, having referred to the army list, afterwards called on the Major. Then the Major called at Mr Dombey’s house in town; and came down again, in the same coach as Mr Dombey. In short, Mr Dombey and the Major got on uncommonly well together, and uncommonly fast: and Mr Dombey observed of the Major, to his sister, that besides being quite a military man he was really something more, as he had a very admirable idea of the importance of things unconnected with his own profession.\n\nAt length Mr Dombey, bringing down Miss Tox and Mrs Chick to see the children, and finding the Major again at Brighton, invited him to dinner at the Bedford, and complimented Miss Tox highly, beforehand, on her neighbour and acquaintance.\n\n“My dearest Louisa,” said Miss Tox to Mrs Chick, when they were alone together, on the morning of the appointed day, “if I should seem at all reserved to Major Bagstock, or under any constraint with him, promise me not to notice it.”\n\n“My dear Lucretia,” returned Mrs Chick, “what mystery is involved in this remarkable request? I must insist upon knowing.”\n\n“Since you are resolved to extort a confession from me, Louisa,” said Miss Tox instantly, “I have no alternative but to confide to you that the Major has been particular.”\n\n“Particular!” repeated Mrs Chick.\n\n“The Major has long been very particular indeed, my love, in his attentions,” said Miss Tox, “occasionally they have been so very marked, that my position has been one of no common difficulty.”\n\n“Is he in good circumstances?” inquired Mrs Chick.\n\n“I have every reason to believe, my dear—indeed I may say I know,” returned Miss Tox, “that he is wealthy. He is truly military, and full of anecdote. I have been informed that his valour, when he was in active service, knew no bounds. I am told that he did all sorts of things in the Peninsula, with every description of fire-arm; and in the East and West Indies, my love, I really couldn’t undertake to say what he did not do.”\n\n“Very creditable to him indeed,” said Mrs Chick, “extremely so; and you have given him no encouragement, my dear?”\n\n“If I were to say, Louisa,” replied Miss Tox, with every demonstration of making an effort that rent her soul, “that I never encouraged Major Bagstock slightly, I should not do justice to the friendship which exists between you and me. It is, perhaps, hardly in the nature of woman to receive such attentions as the Major once lavished upon myself without betraying some sense of obligation. But that is past—long past. Between the Major and me there is now a yawning chasm, and I will not feign to give encouragement, Louisa, where I cannot give my heart. My affections,” said Miss Tox—“but, Louisa, this is madness!” and departed from the room.\n\nAll this Mrs Chick communicated to her brother before dinner: and it by no means indisposed Mr Dombey to receive the Major with unwonted cordiality. The Major, for his part, was in a state of plethoric satisfaction that knew no bounds: and he coughed, and choked, and chuckled, and gasped, and swelled, until the waiters seemed positively afraid of him.\n\n“Your family monopolises Joe’s light, Sir,” said the Major, when he had saluted Miss Tox. “Joe lives in darkness. Princess’s Place is changed into Kamschatka in the winter time. There is no ray of sun, Sir, for Joey B., now.”\n\n“Miss Tox is good enough to take a great deal of interest in Paul, Major,” returned Mr Dombey on behalf of that blushing virgin.\n\n“Damme Sir,” said the Major, “I’m jealous of my little friend. I’m pining away Sir. The Bagstock breed is degenerating in the forsaken person of old Joe.” And the Major, becoming bluer and bluer and puffing his cheeks further and further over the stiff ridge of his tight cravat, stared at Miss Tox, until his eyes seemed as if he were at that moment being overdone before the slow fire at the military college.\n\nNotwithstanding the palpitation of the heart which these allusions occasioned her, they were anything but disagreeable to Miss Tox, as they enabled her to be extremely interesting, and to manifest an occasional incoherence and distraction which she was not at all unwilling to display. The Major gave her abundant opportunities of exhibiting this emotion: being profuse in his complaints, at dinner, of her desertion of him and Princess’s Place: and as he appeared to derive great enjoyment from making them, they all got on very well.\n\nNone the worse on account of the Major taking charge of the whole conversation, and showing as great an appetite in that respect as in regard of the various dainties on the table, among which he may be almost said to have wallowed: greatly to the aggravation of his inflammatory tendencies. Mr Dombey’s habitual silence and reserve yielding readily to this usurpation, the Major felt that he was coming out and shining: and in the flow of spirits thus engendered, rang such an infinite number of new changes on his own name that he quite astonished himself. In a word, they were all very well pleased. The Major was considered to possess an inexhaustible fund of conversation; and when he took a late farewell, after a long rubber, Mr Dombey again complimented the blushing Miss Tox on her neighbour and acquaintance.\n\nBut all the way home to his own hotel, the Major incessantly said to himself, and of himself, “Sly, Sir—sly, Sir—de-vil-ish sly!” And when he got there, sat down in a chair, and fell into a silent fit of laughter, with which he was sometimes seized, and which was always particularly awful. It held him so long on this occasion that the dark servant, who stood watching him at a distance, but dared not for his life approach, twice or thrice gave him over for lost. His whole form, but especially his face and head, dilated beyond all former experience; and presented to the dark man’s view, nothing but a heaving mass of indigo. At length he burst into a violent paroxysm of coughing, and when that was a little better burst into such ejaculations as the following:\n\n“Would you, Ma’am, would you? Mrs Dombey, eh, Ma’am? I think not, Ma’am. Not while Joe B. can put a spoke in your wheel, Ma’am. J. B.“s even with you now, Ma’am. He isn’t altogether bowled out, yet, Sir, isn’t Bagstock. She’s deep, Sir, deep, but Josh is deeper. Wide awake is old Joe—broad awake, and staring, Sir!” There was no doubt of this last assertion being true, and to a very fearful extent; as it continued to be during the greater part of that night, which the Major chiefly passed in similar exclamations, diversified with fits of coughing and choking that startled the whole house.\n\nIt was on the day after this occasion (being Sunday) when, as Mr Dombey, Mrs Chick, and Miss Tox were sitting at breakfast, still eulogising the Major, Florence came running in: her face suffused with a bright colour, and her eyes sparkling joyfully: and cried,\n\n“Papa! Papa! Here’s Walter! and he won’t come in.”\n\n“Who?” cried Mr Dombey. “What does she mean? What is this?”\n\n“Walter, Papa!” said Florence timidly; sensible of having approached the presence with too much familiarity. “Who found me when I was lost.”\n\n“Does she mean young Gay, Louisa?” inquired Mr Dombey, knitting his brows. “Really, this child’s manners have become very boisterous. She cannot mean young Gay, I think. See what it is, will you?”\n\nMrs Chick hurried into the passage, and returned with the information that it was young Gay, accompanied by a very strange-looking person; and that young Gay said he would not take the liberty of coming in, hearing Mr Dombey was at breakfast, but would wait until Mr Dombey should signify that he might approach.\n\n“Tell the boy to come in now,” said Mr Dombey. “Now, Gay, what is the matter? Who sent you down here? Was there nobody else to come?”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Sir,” returned Walter. “I have not been sent. I have been so bold as to come on my own account, which I hope you’ll pardon when I mention the cause.\n\nBut Mr Dombey, without attending to what he said, was looking impatiently on either side of him (as if he were a pillar in his way) at some object behind.\n\n“What’s that?” said Mr Dombey. “Who is that? I think you have made some mistake in the door, Sir.”\n\n“Oh, I’m very sorry to intrude with anyone, Sir,” cried Walter, hastily: “but this is—this is Captain Cuttle, Sir.”\n\n“Wal”r, my lad,” observed the Captain in a deep voice: “stand by!”\n\nAt the same time the Captain, coming a little further in, brought out his wide suit of blue, his conspicuous shirt-collar, and his knobby nose in full relief, and stood bowing to Mr Dombey, and waving his hook politely to the ladies, with the hard glazed hat in his one hand, and a red equator round his head which it had newly imprinted there.\n\nMr Dombey regarded this phenomenon with amazement and indignation, and seemed by his looks to appeal to Mrs Chick and Miss Tox against it. Little Paul, who had come in after Florence, backed towards Miss Tox as the Captain waved his hook, and stood on the defensive.\n\n“Now, Gay,” said Mr Dombey. “What have you got to say to me?”\n\nAgain the Captain observed, as a general opening of the conversation that could not fail to propitiate all parties, “Wal”r, standby!”\n\n“I am afraid, Sir,” began Walter, trembling, and looking down at the ground, “that I take a very great liberty in coming—indeed, I am sure I do. I should hardly have had the courage to ask to see you, Sir, even after coming down, I am afraid, if I had not overtaken Miss Dombey, and—”\n\n“Well!” said Mr Dombey, following his eyes as he glanced at the attentive Florence, and frowning unconsciously as she encouraged him with a smile. “Go on, if you please.”\n\n“Ay, ay,” observed the Captain, considering it incumbent on him, as a point of good breeding, to support Mr Dombey. “Well said! Go on, Wal”r.”\n\nCaptain Cuttle ought to have been withered by the look which Mr Dombey bestowed upon him in acknowledgment of his patronage. But quite innocent of this, he closed one eye in reply, and gave Mr Dombey to understand, by certain significant motions of his hook, that Walter was a little bashful at first, and might be expected to come out shortly.\n\n“It is entirely a private and personal matter, that has brought me here, Sir,” continued Walter, faltering, “and Captain Cuttle—”\n\n“Here!” interposed the Captain, as an assurance that he was at hand, and might be relied upon.\n\n“Who is a very old friend of my poor Uncle’s, and a most excellent man, Sir,” pursued Walter, raising his eyes with a look of entreaty in the Captain’s behalf, “was so good as to offer to come with me, which I could hardly refuse.”\n\n“No, no, no;” observed the Captain complacently. “Of course not. No call for refusing. Go on, Wal”r.”\n\n“And therefore, Sir,” said Walter, venturing to meet Mr Dombey’s eye, and proceeding with better courage in the very desperation of the case, now that there was no avoiding it, “therefore I have come, with him, Sir, to say that my poor old Uncle is in very great affliction and distress. That, through the gradual loss of his business, and not being able to make a payment, the apprehension of which has weighed very heavily upon his mind, months and months, as indeed I know, Sir, he has an execution in his house, and is in danger of losing all he has, and breaking his heart. And that if you would, in your kindness, and in your old knowledge of him as a respectable man, do anything to help him out of his difficulty, Sir, we never could thank you enough for it.”\n\nWalter’s eyes filled with tears as he spoke; and so did those of Florence. Her father saw them glistening, though he appeared to look at Walter only.\n\n“It is a very large sum, Sir,” said Walter. “More than three hundred pounds. My Uncle is quite beaten down by his misfortune, it lies so heavy on him; and is quite unable to do anything for his own relief. He doesn’t even know yet, that I have come to speak to you. You would wish me to say, Sir,” added Walter, after a moment’s hesitation, “exactly what it is I want. I really don’t know, Sir. There is my Uncle’s stock, on which I believe I may say, confidently, there are no other demands, and there is Captain Cuttle, who would wish to be security too. I—I hardly like to mention,” said Walter, “such earnings as mine; but if you would allow them—accumulate—payment—advance—Uncle—frugal, honourable, old man.” Walter trailed off, through these broken sentences, into silence: and stood with downcast head, before his employer.\n\nConsidering this a favourable moment for the display of the valuables, Captain Cuttle advanced to the table; and clearing a space among the breakfast-cups at Mr Dombey’s elbow, produced the silver watch, the ready money, the teaspoons, and the sugar-tongs; and piling them up into a heap that they might look as precious as possible, delivered himself of these words:\n\n“Half a loaf’s better than no bread, and the same remark holds good with crumbs. There’s a few. Annuity of one hundred pound premium also ready to be made over. If there is a man chock full of science in the world, it’s old Sol Gills. If there is a lad of promise—one flowing,” added the Captain, in one of his happy quotations, “with milk and honey—it’s his nevy!”\n\nThe Captain then withdrew to his former place, where he stood arranging his scattered locks with the air of a man who had given the finishing touch to a difficult performance.\n\nWhen Walter ceased to speak, Mr Dombey’s eyes were attracted to little Paul, who, seeing his sister hanging down her head and silently weeping in her commiseration for the distress she had heard described, went over to her, and tried to comfort her: looking at Walter and his father as he did so, with a very expressive face. After the momentary distraction of Captain Cuttle’s address, which he regarded with lofty indifference, Mr Dombey again turned his eyes upon his son, and sat steadily regarding the child, for some moments, in silence.\n\n“What was this debt contracted for?” asked Mr Dombey, at length. “Who is the creditor?”\n\n“He don’t know,” replied the Captain, putting his hand on Walter’s shoulder. “I do. It came of helping a man that’s dead now, and that’s cost my friend Gills many a hundred pound already. More particulars in private, if agreeable.”\n\n“People who have enough to do to hold their own way,” said Mr Dombey, unobservant of the Captain’s mysterious signs behind Walter, and still looking at his son, “had better be content with their own obligations and difficulties, and not increase them by engaging for other men. It is an act of dishonesty and presumption, too,” said Mr Dombey, sternly; “great presumption; for the wealthy could do no more. Paul, come here!”\n\nThe child obeyed: and Mr Dombey took him on his knee.\n\n“If you had money now—” said Mr Dombey. “Look at me!”\n\nPaul, whose eyes had wandered to his sister, and to Walter, looked his father in the face.\n\n“If you had money now,” said Mr Dombey; “as much money as young Gay has talked about; what would you do?”\n\n“Give it to his old Uncle,” returned Paul.\n\n“Lend it to his old Uncle, eh?” retorted Mr Dombey. “Well! When you are old enough, you know, you will share my money, and we shall use it together.”\n\n“Dombey and Son,” interrupted Paul, who had been tutored early in the phrase.\n\n“Dombey and Son,” repeated his father. “Would you like to begin to be Dombey and Son, now, and lend this money to young Gay’s Uncle?”\n\n“Oh! if you please, Papa!” said Paul: “and so would Florence.”\n\n“Girls,” said Mr Dombey, “have nothing to do with Dombey and Son. Would you like it?”\n\n“Yes, Papa, yes!”\n\n“Then you shall do it,” returned his father. “And you see, Paul,” he added, dropping his voice, “how powerful money is, and how anxious people are to get it. Young Gay comes all this way to beg for money, and you, who are so grand and great, having got it, are going to let him have it, as a great favour and obligation.”\n\nPaul turned up the old face for a moment, in which there was a sharp understanding of the reference conveyed in these words: but it was a young and childish face immediately afterwards, when he slipped down from his father’s knee, and ran to tell Florence not to cry any more, for he was going to let young Gay have the money.\n\nMr Dombey then turned to a side-table, and wrote a note and sealed it. During the interval, Paul and Florence whispered to Walter, and Captain Cuttle beamed on the three, with such aspiring and ineffably presumptuous thoughts as Mr Dombey never could have believed in. The note being finished, Mr Dombey turned round to his former place, and held it out to Walter.\n\n“Give that,” he said, “the first thing to-morrow morning, to Mr Carker. He will immediately take care that one of my people releases your Uncle from his present position, by paying the amount at issue; and that such arrangements are made for its repayment as may be consistent with your Uncle’s circumstances. You will consider that this is done for you by Master Paul.”\n\nWalter, in the emotion of holding in his hand the means of releasing his good Uncle from his trouble, would have endeavoured to express something of his gratitude and joy. But Mr Dombey stopped him short.\n\n“You will consider that it is done,” he repeated, “by Master Paul. I have explained that to him, and he understands it. I wish no more to be said.”\n\nAs he motioned towards the door, Walter could only bow his head and retire. Miss Tox, seeing that the Captain appeared about to do the same, interposed.\n\n“My dear Sir,” she said, addressing Mr Dombey, at whose munificence both she and Mrs Chick were shedding tears copiously; “I think you have overlooked something. Pardon me, Mr Dombey, I think, in the nobility of your character, and its exalted scope, you have omitted a matter of detail.”\n\n“Indeed, Miss Tox!” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“The gentleman with the—Instrument,” pursued Miss Tox, glancing at Captain Cuttle, “has left upon the table, at your elbow—”\n\n“Good Heaven!” said Mr Dombey, sweeping the Captain’s property from him, as if it were so much crumb indeed. “Take these things away. I am obliged to you, Miss Tox; it is like your usual discretion. Have the goodness to take these things away, Sir!”\n\nCaptain Cuttle felt he had no alternative but to comply. But he was so much struck by the magnanimity of Mr Dombey, in refusing treasures lying heaped up to his hand, that when he had deposited the teaspoons and sugar-tongs in one pocket, and the ready money in another, and had lowered the great watch down slowly into its proper vault, he could not refrain from seizing that gentleman’s right hand in his own solitary left, and while he held it open with his powerful fingers, bringing the hook down upon its palm in a transport of admiration. At this touch of warm feeling and cold iron, Mr Dombey shivered all over.\n\nCaptain Cuttle then kissed his hook to the ladies several times, with great elegance and gallantry; and having taken a particular leave of Paul and Florence, accompanied Walter out of the room. Florence was running after them in the earnestness of her heart, to send some message to old Sol, when Mr Dombey called her back, and bade her stay where she was.\n\n“Will you never be a Dombey, my dear child!” said Mrs Chick, with pathetic reproachfulness.\n\n“Dear aunt,” said Florence. “Don’t be angry with me. I am so thankful to Papa!”\n\nShe would have run and thrown her arms about his neck if she had dared; but as she did not dare, she glanced with thankful eyes towards him, as he sat musing; sometimes bestowing an uneasy glance on her, but, for the most part, watching Paul, who walked about the room with the new-blown dignity of having let young Gay have the money.\n\nAnd young Gay—Walter—what of him?\n\nHe was overjoyed to purge the old man’s hearth from bailiffs and brokers, and to hurry back to his Uncle with the good tidings. He was overjoyed to have it all arranged and settled next day before noon; and to sit down at evening in the little back parlour with old Sol and Captain Cuttle; and to see the Instrument-maker already reviving, and hopeful for the future, and feeling that the wooden Midshipman was his own again. But without the least impeachment of his gratitude to Mr Dombey, it must be confessed that Walter was humbled and cast down. It is when our budding hopes are nipped beyond recovery by some rough wind, that we are the most disposed to picture to ourselves what flowers they might have borne, if they had flourished; and now, when Walter found himself cut off from that great Dombey height, by the depth of a new and terrible tumble, and felt that all his old wild fancies had been scattered to the winds in the fall, he began to suspect that they might have led him on to harmless visions of aspiring to Florence in the remote distance of time.\n\nThe Captain viewed the subject in quite a different light. He appeared to entertain a belief that the interview at which he had assisted was so very satisfactory and encouraging, as to be only a step or two removed from a regular betrothal of Florence to Walter; and that the late transaction had immensely forwarded, if not thoroughly established, the Whittingtonian hopes. Stimulated by this conviction, and by the improvement in the spirits of his old friend, and by his own consequent gaiety, he even attempted, in favouring them with the ballad of “Lovely Peg” for the third time in one evening, to make an extemporaneous substitution of the name “Florence;” but finding this difficult, on account of the word Peg invariably rhyming to leg (in which personal beauty the original was described as having excelled all competitors), he hit upon the happy thought of changing it to Fle-e-eg; which he accordingly did, with an archness almost supernatural, and a voice quite vociferous, notwithstanding that the time was close at hand when he must seek the abode of the dreadful Mrs MacStinger.\n\nThat same evening the Major was diffuse at his club, on the subject of his friend Dombey in the City. “Damme, Sir,” said the Major, “he’s a prince, is my friend Dombey in the City. I tell you what, Sir. If you had a few more men among you like old Joe Bagstock and my friend Dombey in the City, Sir, you’d do!”\n\n# CHAPTER XI. Paul’s Introduction to a New Scene\n\nMrs Pipchin’s constitution was made of such hard metal, in spite of its liability to the fleshly weaknesses of standing in need of repose after chops, and of requiring to be coaxed to sleep by the soporific agency of sweet-breads, that it utterly set at naught the predictions of Mrs Wickam, and showed no symptoms of decline. Yet, as Paul’s rapt interest in the old lady continued unbated, Mrs Wickam would not budge an inch from the position she had taken up. Fortifying and entrenching herself on the strong ground of her Uncle’s Betsey Jane, she advised Miss Berry, as a friend, to prepare herself for the worst; and forewarned her that her aunt might, at any time, be expected to go off suddenly, like a powder-mill.\n\n“I hope, Miss Berry,” Mrs Wickam would observe, “that you’ll come into whatever little property there may be to leave. You deserve it, I am sure, for yours is a trying life. Though there don’t seem much worth coming into—you’ll excuse my being so open—in this dismal den.”\n\nPoor Berry took it all in good part, and drudged and slaved away as usual; perfectly convinced that Mrs Pipchin was one of the most meritorious persons in the world, and making every day innumerable sacrifices of herself upon the altar of that noble old woman. But all these immolations of Berry were somehow carried to the credit of Mrs Pipchin by Mrs Pipchin’s friends and admirers; and were made to harmonise with, and carry out, that melancholy fact of the deceased Mr Pipchin having broken his heart in the Peruvian mines.\n\nFor example, there was an honest grocer and general dealer in the retail line of business, between whom and Mrs Pipchin there was a small memorandum book, with a greasy red cover, perpetually in question, and concerning which divers secret councils and conferences were continually being held between the parties to that register, on the mat in the passage, and with closed doors in the parlour. Nor were there wanting dark hints from Master Bitherstone (whose temper had been made revengeful by the solar heats of India acting on his blood), of balances unsettled, and of a failure, on one occasion within his memory, in the supply of moist sugar at tea-time. This grocer being a bachelor and not a man who looked upon the surface for beauty, had once made honourable offers for the hand of Berry, which Mrs Pipchin had, with contumely and scorn, rejected. Everybody said how laudable this was in Mrs Pipchin, relict of a man who had died of the Peruvian mines; and what a staunch, high, independent spirit the old lady had. But nobody said anything about poor Berry, who cried for six weeks (being soundly rated by her good aunt all the time), and lapsed into a state of hopeless spinsterhood.\n\n“Berry’s very fond of you, ain’t she?” Paul once asked Mrs Pipchin when they were sitting by the fire with the cat.\n\n“Yes,” said Mrs Pipchin.\n\n“Why?” asked Paul.\n\n“Why!” returned the disconcerted old lady. “How can you ask such things, Sir! why are you fond of your sister Florence?”\n\n“Because she’s very good,” said Paul. “There’s nobody like Florence.”\n\n“Well!” retorted Mrs Pipchin, shortly, “and there’s nobody like me, I suppose.”\n\n“Ain’t there really though?” asked Paul, leaning forward in his chair, and looking at her very hard.\n\n“No,” said the old lady.\n\n“I am glad of that,” observed Paul, rubbing his hands thoughtfully. “That’s a very good thing.”\n\nMrs Pipchin didn’t dare to ask him why, lest she should receive some perfectly annihilating answer. But as a compensation to her wounded feelings, she harassed Master Bitherstone to that extent until bed-time, that he began that very night to make arrangements for an overland return to India, by secreting from his supper a quarter of a round of bread and a fragment of moist Dutch cheese, as the beginning of a stock of provision to support him on the voyage.\n\nMrs Pipchin had kept watch and ward over little Paul and his sister for nearly twelve months. They had been home twice, but only for a few days; and had been constant in their weekly visits to Mr Dombey at the hotel. By little and little Paul had grown stronger, and had become able to dispense with his carriage; though he still looked thin and delicate; and still remained the same old, quiet, dreamy child that he had been when first consigned to Mrs Pipchin’s care. One Saturday afternoon, at dusk, great consternation was occasioned in the Castle by the unlooked-for announcement of Mr Dombey as a visitor to Mrs Pipchin. The population of the parlour was immediately swept upstairs as on the wings of a whirlwind, and after much slamming of bedroom doors, and trampling overhead, and some knocking about of Master Bitherstone by Mrs Pipchin, as a relief to the perturbation of her spirits, the black bombazeen garments of the worthy old lady darkened the audience-chamber where Mr Dombey was contemplating the vacant arm-chair of his son and heir.\n\n“Mrs Pipchin,” said Mr Dombey, “How do you do?”\n\n“Thank you, Sir,” said Mrs Pipchin, “I am pretty well, considering.”\n\nMrs Pipchin always used that form of words. It meant, considering her virtues, sacrifices, and so forth.\n\n“I can’t expect, Sir, to be very well,” said Mrs Pipchin, taking a chair and fetching her breath; “but such health as I have, I am grateful for.”\n\nMr Dombey inclined his head with the satisfied air of a patron, who felt that this was the sort of thing for which he paid so much a quarter. After a moment’s silence he went on to say:\n\n“Mrs Pipchin, I have taken the liberty of calling, to consult you in reference to my son. I have had it in my mind to do so for some time past; but have deferred it from time to time, in order that his health might be thoroughly re-established. You have no misgivings on that subject, Mrs Pipchin?”\n\n“Brighton has proved very beneficial, Sir,” returned Mrs Pipchin. “Very beneficial, indeed.”\n\n“I purpose,” said Mr Dombey, “his remaining at Brighton.”\n\nMrs Pipchin rubbed her hands, and bent her grey eyes on the fire.\n\n“But,” pursued Mr Dombey, stretching out his forefinger, “but possibly that he should now make a change, and lead a different kind of life here. In short, Mrs Pipchin, that is the object of my visit. My son is getting on, Mrs Pipchin. Really, he is getting on.”\n\nThere was something melancholy in the triumphant air with which Mr Dombey said this. It showed how long Paul’s childish life had been to him, and how his hopes were set upon a later stage of his existence. Pity may appear a strange word to connect with anyone so haughty and so cold, and yet he seemed a worthy subject for it at that moment.\n\n“Six years old!” said Mr Dombey, settling his neckcloth—perhaps to hide an irrepressible smile that rather seemed to strike upon the surface of his face and glance away, as finding no resting-place, than to play there for an instant. “Dear me, six will be changed to sixteen, before we have time to look about us.”\n\n“Ten years,” croaked the unsympathetic Pipchin, with a frosty glistening of her hard grey eye, and a dreary shaking of her bent head, “is a long time.”\n\n“It depends on circumstances, returned Mr Dombey; “at all events, Mrs Pipchin, my son is six years old, and there is no doubt, I fear, that in his studies he is behind many children of his age—or his youth,” said Mr Dombey, quickly answering what he mistrusted was a shrewd twinkle of the frosty eye, “his youth is a more appropriate expression. Now, Mrs Pipchin, instead of being behind his peers, my son ought to be before them; far before them. There is an eminence ready for him to mount upon. There is nothing of chance or doubt in the course before my son. His way in life was clear and prepared, and marked out before he existed. The education of such a young gentleman must not be delayed. It must not be left imperfect. It must be very steadily and seriously undertaken, Mrs Pipchin.”\n\n“Well, Sir,” said Mrs Pipchin, “I can say nothing to the contrary.”\n\n“I was quite sure, Mrs Pipchin,” returned Mr Dombey, approvingly, “that a person of your good sense could not, and would not.”\n\n“There is a great deal of nonsense—and worse—talked about young people not being pressed too hard at first, and being tempted on, and all the rest of it, Sir,” said Mrs Pipchin, impatiently rubbing her hooked nose. “It never was thought of in my time, and it has no business to be thought of now. My opinion is ‘keep ’em at it’.”\n\n“My good madam,” returned Mr Dombey, “you have not acquired your reputation undeservedly; and I beg you to believe, Mrs Pipchin, that I am more than satisfied with your excellent system of management, and shall have the greatest pleasure in commending it whenever my poor commendation—” Mr Dombey’s loftiness when he affected to disparage his own importance, passed all bounds—“can be of any service. I have been thinking of Doctor Blimber’s, Mrs Pipchin.”\n\n“My neighbour, Sir?” said Mrs Pipchin. “I believe the Doctor’s is an excellent establishment. I’ve heard that it’s very strictly conducted, and there is nothing but learning going on from morning to night.”\n\n“And it’s very expensive,” added Mr Dombey.\n\n“And it’s very expensive, Sir,” returned Mrs Pipchin, catching at the fact, as if in omitting that, she had omitted one of its leading merits.\n\n“I have had some communication with the Doctor, Mrs Pipchin,” said Mr Dombey, hitching his chair anxiously a little nearer to the fire, “and he does not consider Paul at all too young for his purpose. He mentioned several instances of boys in Greek at about the same age. If I have any little uneasiness in my own mind, Mrs Pipchin, on the subject of this change, it is not on that head. My son not having known a mother has gradually concentrated much—too much—of his childish affection on his sister. Whether their separation—” Mr Dombey said no more, but sat silent.\n\n“Hoity-toity!” exclaimed Mrs Pipchin, shaking out her black bombazeen skirts, and plucking up all the ogress within her. “If she don’t like it, Mr Dombey, she must be taught to lump it.” The good lady apologised immediately afterwards for using so common a figure of speech, but said (and truly) that that was the way she reasoned with ’em.\n\nMr Dombey waited until Mrs Pipchin had done bridling and shaking her head, and frowning down a legion of Bitherstones and Pankeys; and then said quietly, but correctively, “He, my good madam, he.”\n\nMrs Pipchin’s system would have applied very much the same mode of cure to any uneasiness on the part of Paul, too; but as the hard grey eye was sharp enough to see that the recipe, however Mr Dombey might admit its efficacy in the case of the daughter, was not a sovereign remedy for the son, she argued the point; and contended that change, and new society, and the different form of life he would lead at Doctor Blimber’s, and the studies he would have to master, would very soon prove sufficient alienations. As this chimed in with Mr Dombey’s own hope and belief, it gave that gentleman a still higher opinion of Mrs Pipchin’s understanding; and as Mrs Pipchin, at the same time, bewailed the loss of her dear little friend (which was not an overwhelming shock to her, as she had long expected it, and had not looked, in the beginning, for his remaining with her longer than three months), he formed an equally good opinion of Mrs Pipchin’s disinterestedness. It was plain that he had given the subject anxious consideration, for he had formed a plan, which he announced to the ogress, of sending Paul to the Doctor’s as a weekly boarder for the first half year, during which time Florence would remain at the Castle, that she might receive her brother there, on Saturdays. This would wean him by degrees, Mr Dombey said; possibly with a recollection of his not having been weaned by degrees on a former occasion.\n\nMr Dombey finished the interview by expressing his hope that Mrs Pipchin would still remain in office as general superintendent and overseer of his son, pending his studies at Brighton; and having kissed Paul, and shaken hands with Florence, and beheld Master Bitherstone in his collar of state, and made Miss Pankey cry by patting her on the head (in which region she was uncommonly tender, on account of a habit Mrs Pipchin had of sounding it with her knuckles, like a cask), he withdrew to his hotel and dinner: resolved that Paul, now that he was getting so old and well, should begin a vigorous course of education forthwith, to qualify him for the position in which he was to shine; and that Doctor Blimber should take him in hand immediately.\n\nWhenever a young gentleman was taken in hand by Doctor Blimber, he might consider himself sure of a pretty tight squeeze. The Doctor only undertook the charge of ten young gentlemen, but he had, always ready, a supply of learning for a hundred, on the lowest estimate; and it was at once the business and delight of his life to gorge the unhappy ten with it.\n\nIn fact, Doctor Blimber’s establishment was a great hot-house, in which there was a forcing apparatus incessantly at work. All the boys blew before their time. Mental green-peas were produced at Christmas, and intellectual asparagus all the year round. Mathematical gooseberries (very sour ones too) were common at untimely seasons, and from mere sprouts of bushes, under Doctor Blimber’s cultivation. Every description of Greek and Latin vegetable was got off the driest twigs of boys, under the frostiest circumstances. Nature was of no consequence at all. No matter what a young gentleman was intended to bear, Doctor Blimber made him bear to pattern, somehow or other.\n\nThis was all very pleasant and ingenious, but the system of forcing was attended with its usual disadvantages. There was not the right taste about the premature productions, and they didn’t keep well. Moreover, one young gentleman, with a swollen nose and an excessively large head (the oldest of the ten who had “gone through” everything), suddenly left off blowing one day, and remained in the establishment a mere stalk. And people did say that the Doctor had rather overdone it with young Toots, and that when he began to have whiskers he left off having brains.\n\nThere young Toots was, at any rate; possessed of the gruffest of voices and the shrillest of minds; sticking ornamental pins into his shirt, and keeping a ring in his waistcoat pocket to put on his little finger by stealth, when the pupils went out walking; constantly falling in love by sight with nurserymaids, who had no idea of his existence; and looking at the gas-lighted world over the little iron bars in the left-hand corner window of the front three pairs of stairs, after bed-time, like a greatly overgrown cherub who had sat up aloft much too long.\n\nThe Doctor was a portly gentleman in a suit of black, with strings at his knees, and stockings below them. He had a bald head, highly polished; a deep voice; and a chin so very double, that it was a wonder how he ever managed to shave into the creases. He had likewise a pair of little eyes that were always half shut up, and a mouth that was always half expanded into a grin, as if he had, that moment, posed a boy, and were waiting to convict him from his own lips. Insomuch, that when the Doctor put his right hand into the breast of his coat, and with his other hand behind him, and a scarcely perceptible wag of his head, made the commonest observation to a nervous stranger, it was like a sentiment from the sphynx, and settled his business.\n\nThe Doctor’s was a mighty fine house, fronting the sea. Not a joyful style of house within, but quite the contrary. Sad-coloured curtains, whose proportions were spare and lean, hid themselves despondently behind the windows. The tables and chairs were put away in rows, like figures in a sum; fires were so rarely lighted in the rooms of ceremony, that they felt like wells, and a visitor represented the bucket; the dining-room seemed the last place in the world where any eating or drinking was likely to occur; there was no sound through all the house but the ticking of a great clock in the hall, which made itself audible in the very garrets; and sometimes a dull cooing of young gentlemen at their lessons, like the murmurings of an assemblage of melancholy pigeons.\n\nMiss Blimber, too, although a slim and graceful maid, did no soft violence to the gravity of the house. There was no light nonsense about Miss Blimber. She kept her hair short and crisp, and wore spectacles. She was dry and sandy with working in the graves of deceased languages. None of your live languages for Miss Blimber. They must be dead—stone dead—and then Miss Blimber dug them up like a Ghoul.\n\nMrs Blimber, her Mama, was not learned herself, but she pretended to be, and that did quite as well. She said at evening parties, that if she could have known Cicero, she thought she could have died contented. It was the steady joy of her life to see the Doctor’s young gentlemen go out walking, unlike all other young gentlemen, in the largest possible shirt-collars, and the stiffest possible cravats. It was so classical, she said.\n\nAs to Mr Feeder, B.A., Doctor Blimber’s assistant, he was a kind of human barrel-organ, with a little list of tunes at which he was continually working, over and over again, without any variation. He might have been fitted up with a change of barrels, perhaps, in early life, if his destiny had been favourable; but it had not been; and he had only one, with which, in a monotonous round, it was his occupation to bewilder the young ideas of Doctor Blimber’s young gentlemen. The young gentlemen were prematurely full of carking anxieties. They knew no rest from the pursuit of stony-hearted verbs, savage noun-substantives, inflexible syntactic passages, and ghosts of exercises that appeared to them in their dreams. Under the forcing system, a young gentleman usually took leave of his spirits in three weeks. He had all the cares of the world on his head in three months. He conceived bitter sentiments against his parents or guardians in four; he was an old misanthrope, in five; envied Curtius that blessed refuge in the earth, in six; and at the end of the first twelvemonth had arrived at the conclusion, from which he never afterwards departed, that all the fancies of the poets, and lessons of the sages, were a mere collection of words and grammar, and had no other meaning in the world.\n\nBut he went on blow, blow, blowing, in the Doctor’s hothouse, all the time; and the Doctor’s glory and reputation were great, when he took his wintry growth home to his relations and friends.\n\nUpon the Doctor’s door-steps one day, Paul stood with a fluttering heart, and with his small right hand in his father’s. His other hand was locked in that of Florence. How tight the tiny pressure of that one; and how loose and cold the other!\n\nMrs Pipchin hovered behind the victim, with her sable plumage and her hooked beak, like a bird of ill-omen. She was out of breath—for Mr Dombey, full of great thoughts, had walked fast—and she croaked hoarsely as she waited for the opening of the door.\n\n“Now, Paul,” said Mr Dombey, exultingly. “This is the way indeed to be Dombey and Son, and have money. You are almost a man already.”\n\n“Almost,” returned the child.\n\nEven his childish agitation could not master the sly and quaint yet touching look, with which he accompanied the reply.\n\nIt brought a vague expression of dissatisfaction into Mr Dombey’s face; but the door being opened, it was quickly gone.\n\n“Doctor Blimber is at home, I believe?” said Mr Dombey.\n\nThe man said yes; and as they passed in, looked at Paul as if he were a little mouse, and the house were a trap. He was a weak-eyed young man, with the first faint streaks or early dawn of a grin on his countenance. It was mere imbecility; but Mrs Pipchin took it into her head that it was impudence, and made a snap at him directly.\n\n“How dare you laugh behind the gentleman’s back?” said Mrs Pipchin. “And what do you take me for?”\n\n“I ain’t a laughing at nobody, and I’m sure I don’t take you for nothing, Ma’am,” returned the young man, in consternation.\n\n“A pack of idle dogs!” said Mrs Pipchin, “only fit to be turnspits. Go and tell your master that Mr Dombey’s here, or it’ll be worse for you!”\n\nThe weak-eyed young man went, very meekly, to discharge himself of this commission; and soon came back to invite them to the Doctor’s study.\n\n“You’re laughing again, Sir,” said Mrs Pipchin, when it came to her turn, bringing up the rear, to pass him in the hall.\n\n“I ain’t,” returned the young man, grievously oppressed. “I never see such a thing as this!”\n\n“What is the matter, Mrs Pipchin?” said Mr Dombey, looking round. “Softly! Pray!”\n\nMrs Pipchin, in her deference, merely muttered at the young man as she passed on, and said, “Oh! he was a precious fellow”—leaving the young man, who was all meekness and incapacity, affected even to tears by the incident. But Mrs Pipchin had a way of falling foul of all meek people; and her friends said who could wonder at it, after the Peruvian mines!\n\nThe Doctor was sitting in his portentous study, with a globe at each knee, books all round him, Homer over the door, and Minerva on the mantel-shelf. “And how do you do, Sir?” he said to Mr Dombey, “and how is my little friend?” Grave as an organ was the Doctor’s speech; and when he ceased, the great clock in the hall seemed (to Paul at least) to take him up, and to go on saying, “how, is, my, lit, tle, friend? how, is, my, lit, tle, friend?” over and over and over again.\n\nThe little friend being something too small to be seen at all from where the Doctor sat, over the books on his table, the Doctor made several futile attempts to get a view of him round the legs; which Mr Dombey perceiving, relieved the Doctor from his embarrassment by taking Paul up in his arms, and sitting him on another little table, over against the Doctor, in the middle of the room.\n\n“Ha!” said the Doctor, leaning back in his chair with his hand in his breast. “Now I see my little friend. How do you do, my little friend?”\n\nThe clock in the hall wouldn’t subscribe to this alteration in the form of words, but continued to repeat how, is, my, lit, tle, friend? how, is, my, lit, tle, friend?”\n\n“Very well, I thank you, Sir,” returned Paul, answering the clock quite as much as the Doctor.\n\n“Ha!” said Doctor Blimber. “Shall we make a man of him?”\n\n“Do you hear, Paul?” added Mr Dombey; Paul being silent.\n\n“Shall we make a man of him?” repeated the Doctor.\n\n“I had rather be a child,” replied Paul.\n\n“Indeed!” said the Doctor. “Why?”\n\nThe child sat on the table looking at him, with a curious expression of suppressed emotion in his face, and beating one hand proudly on his knee as if he had the rising tears beneath it, and crushed them. But his other hand strayed a little way the while, a little farther—farther from him yet—until it lighted on the neck of Florence. “This is why,” it seemed to say, and then the steady look was broken up and gone; the working lip was loosened; and the tears came streaming forth.\n\n“Mrs Pipchin,” said his father, in a querulous manner, “I am really very sorry to see this.”\n\n“Come away from him, do, Miss Dombey,” quoth the matron.\n\n“Never mind,” said the Doctor, blandly nodding his head, to keep Mrs Pipchin back. “Never mind; we shall substitute new cares and new impressions, Mr Dombey, very shortly. You would still wish my little friend to acquire—”\n\n“Everything, if you please, Doctor,” returned Mr Dombey, firmly.\n\n“Yes,” said the Doctor, who, with his half-shut eyes, and his usual smile, seemed to survey Paul with the sort of interest that might attach to some choice little animal he was going to stuff. “Yes, exactly. Ha! We shall impart a great variety of information to our little friend, and bring him quickly forward, I daresay. I daresay. Quite a virgin soil, I believe you said, Mr Dombey?”\n\n“Except some ordinary preparation at home, and from this lady,” replied Mr Dombey, introducing Mrs Pipchin, who instantly communicated a rigidity to her whole muscular system, and snorted defiance beforehand, in case the Doctor should disparage her; “except so far, Paul has, as yet, applied himself to no studies at all.”\n\nDoctor Blimber inclined his head, in gentle tolerance of such insignificant poaching as Mrs Pipchin’s, and said he was glad to hear it. It was much more satisfactory, he observed, rubbing his hands, to begin at the foundation. And again he leered at Paul, as if he would have liked to tackle him with the Greek alphabet, on the spot.\n\n“That circumstance, indeed, Doctor Blimber,” pursued Mr Dombey, glancing at his little son, “and the interview I have already had the pleasure of holding with you, renders any further explanation, and consequently, any further intrusion on your valuable time, so unnecessary, that—”\n\n“Now, Miss Dombey!” said the acid Pipchin.\n\n“Permit me,” said the Doctor, “one moment. Allow me to present Mrs Blimber and my daughter; who will be associated with the domestic life of our young Pilgrim to Parnassus Mrs Blimber,” for the lady, who had perhaps been in waiting, opportunely entered, followed by her daughter, that fair Sexton in spectacles, “Mr Dombey. My daughter Cornelia, Mr Dombey. Mr Dombey, my love,” pursued the Doctor, turning to his wife, “is so confiding as to—do you see our little friend?”\n\nMrs Blimber, in an excess of politeness, of which Mr Dombey was the object, apparently did not, for she was backing against the little friend, and very much endangering his position on the table. But, on this hint, she turned to admire his classical and intellectual lineaments, and turning again to Mr Dombey, said, with a sigh, that she envied his dear son.\n\n“Like a bee, Sir,” said Mrs Blimber, with uplifted eyes, “about to plunge into a garden of the choicest flowers, and sip the sweets for the first time Virgil, Horace, Ovid, Terence, Plautus, Cicero. What a world of honey have we here. It may appear remarkable, Mr Dombey, in one who is a wife—the wife of such a husband—”\n\n“Hush, hush,” said Doctor Blimber. “Fie for shame.”\n\n“Mr Dombey will forgive the partiality of a wife,” said Mrs Blimber, with an engaging smile.\n\nMr Dombey answered “Not at all:” applying those words, it is to be presumed, to the partiality, and not to the forgiveness.\n\n“And it may seem remarkable in one who is a mother also,” resumed Mrs Blimber.\n\n“And such a mother,” observed Mr Dombey, bowing with some confused idea of being complimentary to Cornelia.\n\n“But really,” pursued Mrs Blimber, “I think if I could have known Cicero, and been his friend, and talked with him in his retirement at Tusculum (beau-ti-ful Tusculum!), I could have died contented.”\n\nA learned enthusiasm is so very contagious, that Mr Dombey half believed this was exactly his case; and even Mrs Pipchin, who was not, as we have seen, of an accommodating disposition generally, gave utterance to a little sound between a groan and a sigh, as if she would have said that nobody but Cicero could have proved a lasting consolation under that failure of the Peruvian Mines, but that he indeed would have been a very Davy-lamp of refuge.\n\nCornelia looked at Mr Dombey through her spectacles, as if she would have liked to crack a few quotations with him from the authority in question. But this design, if she entertained it, was frustrated by a knock at the room-door.\n\n“Who is that?” said the Doctor. “Oh! Come in, Toots; come in. Mr Dombey, Sir.” Toots bowed. “Quite a coincidence!” said Doctor Blimber. “Here we have the beginning and the end. Alpha and Omega. Our head boy, Mr Dombey.”\n\nThe Doctor might have called him their head and shoulders boy, for he was at least that much taller than any of the rest. He blushed very much at finding himself among strangers, and chuckled aloud.\n\n“An addition to our little Portico, Toots,” said the Doctor; “Mr Dombey’s son.”\n\nYoung Toots blushed again; and finding, from a solemn silence which prevailed, that he was expected to say something, said to Paul, “How are you?” in a voice so deep, and a manner so sheepish, that if a lamb had roared it couldn’t have been more surprising.\n\n“Ask Mr Feeder, if you please, Toots,” said the Doctor, “to prepare a few introductory volumes for Mr Dombey’s son, and to allot him a convenient seat for study. My dear, I believe Mr Dombey has not seen the dormitories.”\n\n“If Mr Dombey will walk upstairs,” said Mrs Blimber, “I shall be more than proud to show him the dominions of the drowsy god.”\n\nWith that, Mrs Blimber, who was a lady of great suavity, and a wiry figure, and who wore a cap composed of sky-blue materials, proceeded upstairs with Mr Dombey and Cornelia; Mrs Pipchin following, and looking out sharp for her enemy the footman.\n\nWhile they were gone, Paul sat upon the table, holding Florence by the hand, and glancing timidly from the Doctor round and round the room, while the Doctor, leaning back in his chair, with his hand in his breast as usual, held a book from him at arm’s length, and read. There was something very awful in this manner of reading. It was such a determined, unimpassioned, inflexible, cold-blooded way of going to work. It left the Doctor’s countenance exposed to view; and when the Doctor smiled suspiciously at his author, or knit his brows, or shook his head and made wry faces at him, as much as to say, “Don’t tell me, Sir; I know better,” it was terrific.\n\nToots, too, had no business to be outside the door, ostentatiously examining the wheels in his watch, and counting his half-crowns. But that didn’t last long; for Doctor Blimber, happening to change the position of his tight plump legs, as if he were going to get up, Toots swiftly vanished, and appeared no more.\n\nMr Dombey and his conductress were soon heard coming downstairs again, talking all the way; and presently they re-entered the Doctor’s study.\n\n“I hope, Mr Dombey,” said the Doctor, laying down his book, “that the arrangements meet your approval.”\n\n“They are excellent, Sir,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Very fair, indeed,” said Mrs Pipchin, in a low voice; never disposed to give too much encouragement.\n\n“Mrs Pipchin,” said Mr Dombey, wheeling round, “will, with your permission, Doctor and Mrs Blimber, visit Paul now and then.”\n\n“Whenever Mrs Pipchin pleases,” observed the Doctor.\n\n“Always happy to see her,” said Mrs Blimber.\n\n“I think,” said Mr Dombey, “I have given all the trouble I need, and may take my leave. Paul, my child,” he went close to him, as he sat upon the table. “Good-bye.”\n\n“Good-bye, Papa.”\n\nThe limp and careless little hand that Mr Dombey took in his, was singularly out of keeping with the wistful face. But he had no part in its sorrowful expression. It was not addressed to him. No, no. To Florence—all to Florence.\n\nIf Mr Dombey in his insolence of wealth, had ever made an enemy, hard to appease and cruelly vindictive in his hate, even such an enemy might have received the pang that wrung his proud heart then, as compensation for his injury.\n\nHe bent down, over his boy, and kissed him. If his sight were dimmed as he did so, by something that for a moment blurred the little face, and made it indistinct to him, his mental vision may have been, for that short time, the clearer perhaps.\n\n“I shall see you soon, Paul. You are free on Saturdays and Sundays, you know.”\n\n“Yes, Papa,” returned Paul: looking at his sister. “On Saturdays and Sundays.”\n\n“And you’ll try and learn a great deal here, and be a clever man,” said Mr Dombey; “won’t you?”\n\n“I’ll try,” returned the child, wearily.\n\n“And you’ll soon be grown up now!” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Oh! very soon!” replied the child. Once more the old, old look passed rapidly across his features like a strange light. It fell on Mrs Pipchin, and extinguished itself in her black dress. That excellent ogress stepped forward to take leave and to bear off Florence, which she had long been thirsting to do. The move on her part roused Mr Dombey, whose eyes were fixed on Paul. After patting him on the head, and pressing his small hand again, he took leave of Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, and Miss Blimber, with his usual polite frigidity, and walked out of the study.\n\nDespite his entreaty that they would not think of stirring, Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, and Miss Blimber all pressed forward to attend him to the hall; and thus Mrs Pipchin got into a state of entanglement with Miss Blimber and the Doctor, and was crowded out of the study before she could clutch Florence. To which happy accident Paul stood afterwards indebted for the dear remembrance, that Florence ran back to throw her arms round his neck, and that hers was the last face in the doorway: turned towards him with a smile of encouragement, the brighter for the tears through which it beamed.\n\nIt made his childish bosom heave and swell when it was gone; and sent the globes, the books, blind Homer and Minerva, swimming round the room. But they stopped, all of a sudden; and then he heard the loud clock in the hall still gravely inquiring “how, is, my, lit, tle, friend? how, is, my, lit, tle, friend?” as it had done before.\n\nHe sat, with folded hands, upon his pedestal, silently listening. But he might have answered “weary, weary! very lonely, very sad!” And there, with an aching void in his young heart, and all outside so cold, and bare, and strange, Paul sat as if he had taken life unfurnished, and the upholsterer were never coming.\n\n# CHAPTER XII. Paul’s Education\n\nAfter the lapse of some minutes, which appeared an immense time to little Paul Dombey on the table, Doctor Blimber came back. The Doctor’s walk was stately, and calculated to impress the juvenile mind with solemn feelings. It was a sort of march; but when the Doctor put out his right foot, he gravely turned upon his axis, with a semi-circular sweep towards the left; and when he put out his left foot, he turned in the same manner towards the right. So that he seemed, at every stride he took, to look about him as though he were saying, “Can anybody have the goodness to indicate any subject, in any direction, on which I am uninformed? I rather think not.”\n\nMrs Blimber and Miss Blimber came back in the Doctor’s company; and the Doctor, lifting his new pupil off the table, delivered him over to Miss Blimber.\n\n“Cornelia,” said the Doctor, “Dombey will be your charge at first. Bring him on, Cornelia, bring him on.”\n\nMiss Blimber received her young ward from the Doctor’s hands; and Paul, feeling that the spectacles were surveying him, cast down his eyes.\n\n“How old are you, Dombey?” said Miss Blimber.\n\n“Six,” answered Paul, wondering, as he stole a glance at the young lady, why her hair didn’t grow long like Florence’s, and why she was like a boy.\n\n“How much do you know of your Latin Grammar, Dombey?” said Miss Blimber.\n\n“None of it,” answered Paul. Feeling that the answer was a shock to Miss Blimber’s sensibility, he looked up at the three faces that were looking down at him, and said:\n\n“I haven’t been well. I have been a weak child. I couldn’t learn a Latin Grammar when I was out, every day, with old Glubb. I wish you’d tell old Glubb to come and see me, if you please.”\n\n“What a dreadfully low name” said Mrs Blimber. “Unclassical to a degree! Who is the monster, child?”\n\n“What monster?” inquired Paul.\n\n“Glubb,” said Mrs Blimber, with a great disrelish.\n\n“He’s no more a monster than you are,” returned Paul.\n\n“What!” cried the Doctor, in a terrible voice. “Ay, ay, ay? Aha! What’s that?”\n\nPaul was dreadfully frightened; but still he made a stand for the absent Glubb, though he did it trembling.\n\n“He’s a very nice old man, Ma’am,” he said. “He used to draw my couch. He knows all about the deep sea, and the fish that are in it, and the great monsters that come and lie on rocks in the sun, and dive into the water again when they’re startled, blowing and splashing so, that they can be heard for miles. There are some creatures, said Paul, warming with his subject, “I don’t know how many yards long, and I forget their names, but Florence knows, that pretend to be in distress; and when a man goes near them, out of compassion, they open their great jaws, and attack him. But all he has got to do,” said Paul, boldly tendering this information to the very Doctor himself, “is to keep on turning as he runs away, and then, as they turn slowly, because they are so long, and can’t bend, he’s sure to beat them. And though old Glubb don’t know why the sea should make me think of my Mama that’s dead, or what it is that it is always saying—always saying! he knows a great deal about it. And I wish,” the child concluded, with a sudden falling of his countenance, and failing in his animation, as he looked like one forlorn, upon the three strange faces, “that you’d let old Glubb come here to see me, for I know him very well, and he knows me.”\n\n“Ha!” said the Doctor, shaking his head; “this is bad, but study will do much.”\n\nMrs Blimber opined, with something like a shiver, that he was an unaccountable child; and, allowing for the difference of visage, looked at him pretty much as Mrs Pipchin had been used to do.\n\n“Take him round the house, Cornelia,” said the Doctor, “and familiarise him with his new sphere. Go with that young lady, Dombey.”\n\nDombey obeyed; giving his hand to the abstruse Cornelia, and looking at her sideways, with timid curiosity, as they went away together. For her spectacles, by reason of the glistening of the glasses, made her so mysterious, that he didn’t know where she was looking, and was not indeed quite sure that she had any eyes at all behind them.\n\nCornelia took him first to the schoolroom, which was situated at the back of the hall, and was approached through two baize doors, which deadened and muffled the young gentlemen’s voices. Here, there were eight young gentlemen in various stages of mental prostration, all very hard at work, and very grave indeed. Toots, as an old hand, had a desk to himself in one corner: and a magnificent man, of immense age, he looked, in Paul’s young eyes, behind it.\n\nMr Feeder, B.A., who sat at another little desk, had his Virgil stop on, and was slowly grinding that tune to four young gentlemen. Of the remaining four, two, who grasped their foreheads convulsively, were engaged in solving mathematical problems; one with his face like a dirty window, from much crying, was endeavouring to flounder through a hopeless number of lines before dinner; and one sat looking at his task in stony stupefaction and despair—which it seemed had been his condition ever since breakfast time.\n\nThe appearance of a new boy did not create the sensation that might have been expected. Mr Feeder, B.A. (who was in the habit of shaving his head for coolness, and had nothing but little bristles on it), gave him a bony hand, and told him he was glad to see him—which Paul would have been very glad to have told him, if he could have done so with the least sincerity. Then Paul, instructed by Cornelia, shook hands with the four young gentlemen at Mr Feeder’s desk; then with the two young gentlemen at work on the problems, who were very feverish; then with the young gentleman at work against time, who was very inky; and lastly with the young gentleman in a state of stupefaction, who was flabby and quite cold.\n\nPaul having been already introduced to Toots, that pupil merely chuckled and breathed hard, as his custom was, and pursued the occupation in which he was engaged. It was not a severe one; for on account of his having “gone through” so much (in more senses than one), and also of his having, as before hinted, left off blowing in his prime, Toots now had licence to pursue his own course of study: which was chiefly to write long letters to himself from persons of distinction, adds “P. Toots, Esquire, Brighton, Sussex,” and to preserve them in his desk with great care.\n\nThese ceremonies passed, Cornelia led Paul upstairs to the top of the house; which was rather a slow journey, on account of Paul being obliged to land both feet on every stair, before he mounted another. But they reached their journey’s end at last; and there, in a front room, looking over the wild sea, Cornelia showed him a nice little bed with white hangings, close to the window, on which there was already beautifully written on a card in round text—down strokes very thick, and up strokes very fine—DOMBEY; while two other little bedsteads in the same room were announced, through like means, as respectively appertaining unto BRIGGS and TOZER.\n\nJust as they got downstairs again into the hall, Paul saw the weak-eyed young man who had given that mortal offence to Mrs Pipchin, suddenly seize a very large drumstick, and fly at a gong that was hanging up, as if he had gone mad, or wanted vengeance. Instead of receiving warning, however, or being instantly taken into custody, the young man left off unchecked, after having made a dreadful noise. Then Cornelia Blimber said to Dombey that dinner would be ready in a quarter of an hour, and perhaps he had better go into the schoolroom among his “friends.”\n\nSo Dombey, deferentially passing the great clock which was still as anxious as ever to know how he found himself, opened the schoolroom door a very little way, and strayed in like a lost boy: shutting it after him with some difficulty. His friends were all dispersed about the room except the stony friend, who remained immoveable. Mr Feeder was stretching himself in his grey gown, as if, regardless of expense, he were resolved to pull the sleeves off.\n\n“Heigh ho hum!” cried Mr Feeder, shaking himself like a cart-horse. “Oh dear me, dear me! Ya-a-a-ah!”\n\nPaul was quite alarmed by Mr Feeder’s yawning; it was done on such a great scale, and he was so terribly in earnest. All the boys too (Toots excepted) seemed knocked up, and were getting ready for dinner—some newly tying their neckcloths, which were very stiff indeed; and others washing their hands or brushing their hair, in an adjoining ante-chamber—as if they didn’t think they should enjoy it at all.\n\nYoung Toots who was ready beforehand, and had therefore nothing to do, and had leisure to bestow upon Paul, said, with heavy good nature:\n\n“Sit down, Dombey.”\n\n“Thank you, Sir,” said Paul.\n\nHis endeavouring to hoist himself on to a very high window-seat, and his slipping down again, appeared to prepare Toots’s mind for the reception of a discovery.\n\n“You’re a very small chap;” said Mr Toots.\n\n“Yes, Sir, I’m small,” returned Paul. “Thank you, Sir.”\n\nFor Toots had lifted him into the seat, and done it kindly too.\n\n“Who’s your tailor?” inquired Toots, after looking at him for some moments.\n\n“It’s a woman that has made my clothes as yet,” said Paul. “My sister’s dressmaker.”\n\n“My tailor’s Burgess and Co.,” said Toots. “Fash’nable. But very dear.”\n\nPaul had wit enough to shake his head, as if he would have said it was easy to see that; and indeed he thought so.\n\n“Your father’s regularly rich, ain’t he?” inquired Mr Toots.\n\n“Yes, Sir,” said Paul. “He’s Dombey and Son.”\n\n“And which?” demanded Toots.\n\n“And Son, Sir,” replied Paul.\n\nMr Toots made one or two attempts, in a low voice, to fix the Firm in his mind; but not quite succeeding, said he would get Paul to mention the name again to-morrow morning, as it was rather important. And indeed he purposed nothing less than writing himself a private and confidential letter from Dombey and Son immediately.\n\nBy this time the other pupils (always excepting the stony boy) gathered round. They were polite, but pale; and spoke low; and they were so depressed in their spirits, that in comparison with the general tone of that company, Master Bitherstone was a perfect Miller, or complete Jest\n\n# Book.” And yet he had a sense of injury upon him, too, had Bitherstone.\n\n“You sleep in my room, don’t you?” asked a solemn young gentleman, whose shirt-collar curled up the lobes of his ears.\n\n“Master Briggs?” inquired Paul.\n\n“Tozer,” said the young gentleman.\n\nPaul answered yes; and Tozer pointing out the stony pupil, said that was Briggs. Paul had already felt certain that it must be either Briggs or Tozer, though he didn’t know why.\n\n“Is yours a strong constitution?” inquired Tozer.\n\nPaul said he thought not. Tozer replied that he thought not also, judging from Paul’s looks, and that it was a pity, for it need be. He then asked Paul if he were going to begin with Cornelia; and on Paul saying “yes,” all the young gentlemen (Briggs excepted) gave a low groan.\n\nIt was drowned in the tintinnabulation of the gong, which sounding again with great fury, there was a general move towards the dining-room; still excepting Briggs the stony boy, who remained where he was, and as he was; and on its way to whom Paul presently encountered a round of bread, genteelly served on a plate and napkin, and with a silver fork lying crosswise on the top of it. Doctor Blimber was already in his place in the dining-room, at the top of the table, with Miss Blimber and Mrs Blimber on either side of him. Mr Feeder in a black coat was at the bottom. Paul’s chair was next to Miss Blimber; but it being found, when he sat in it, that his eyebrows were not much above the level of the table-cloth, some books were brought in from the Doctor’s study, on which he was elevated, and on which he always sat from that time— carrying them in and out himself on after occasions, like a little elephant and castle.\n\nGrace having been said by the Doctor, dinner began. There was some nice soup; also roast meat, boiled meat, vegetables, pie, and cheese. Every young gentleman had a massive silver fork, and a napkin; and all the arrangements were stately and handsome. In particular, there was a butler in a blue coat and bright buttons, who gave quite a winey flavour to the table beer; he poured it out so superbly.\n\nNobody spoke, unless spoken to, except Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, and Miss Blimber, who conversed occasionally. Whenever a young gentleman was not actually engaged with his knife and fork or spoon, his eye, with an irresistible attraction, sought the eye of Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, or Miss Blimber, and modestly rested there. Toots appeared to be the only exception to this rule. He sat next Mr Feeder on Paul’s side of the table, and frequently looked behind and before the intervening boys to catch a glimpse of Paul.\n\nOnly once during dinner was there any conversation that included the young gentlemen. It happened at the epoch of the cheese, when the Doctor, having taken a glass of port wine, and hemmed twice or thrice, said:\n\n“It is remarkable, Mr Feeder, that the Romans—”\n\nAt the mention of this terrible people, their implacable enemies, every young gentleman fastened his gaze upon the Doctor, with an assumption of the deepest interest. One of the number who happened to be drinking, and who caught the Doctor’s eye glaring at him through the side of his tumbler, left off so hastily that he was convulsed for some moments, and in the sequel ruined Doctor Blimber’s point.\n\n“It is remarkable, Mr Feeder,” said the Doctor, beginning again slowly, “that the Romans, in those gorgeous and profuse entertainments of which we read in the days of the Emperors, when luxury had attained a height unknown before or since, and when whole provinces were ravaged to supply the splendid means of one Imperial Banquet—”\n\nHere the offender, who had been swelling and straining, and waiting in vain for a full stop, broke out violently.\n\n“Johnson,” said Mr Feeder, in a low reproachful voice, “take some water.”\n\nThe Doctor, looking very stern, made a pause until the water was brought, and then resumed:\n\n“And when, Mr Feeder—”\n\nBut Mr Feeder, who saw that Johnson must break out again, and who knew that the Doctor would never come to a period before the young gentlemen until he had finished all he meant to say, couldn’t keep his eye off Johnson; and thus was caught in the fact of not looking at the Doctor, who consequently stopped.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Sir,” said Mr Feeder, reddening. “I beg your pardon, Doctor Blimber.”\n\n“And when,” said the Doctor, raising his voice, “when, Sir, as we read, and have no reason to doubt—incredible as it may appear to the vulgar—of our time—the brother of Vitellius prepared for him a feast, in which were served, of fish, two thousand dishes—”\n\n“Take some water, Johnson—dishes, Sir,” said Mr Feeder.\n\n“Of various sorts of fowl, five thousand dishes.”\n\n“Or try a crust of bread,” said Mr Feeder.\n\n“And one dish,” pursued Doctor Blimber, raising his voice still higher as he looked all round the table, “called, from its enormous dimensions, the Shield of Minerva, and made, among other costly ingredients, of the brains of pheasants—”\n\n“Ow, ow, ow!” (from Johnson.)\n\n“Woodcocks—”\n\n“Ow, ow, ow!”\n\n“The sounds of the fish called scari—”\n\n“You’ll burst some vessel in your head,” said Mr Feeder. “You had better let it come.”\n\n“And the spawn of the lamprey, brought from the Carpathian Sea,” pursued the Doctor, in his severest voice; “when we read of costly entertainments such as these, and still remember, that we have a Titus—”\n\n“What would be your mother’s feelings if you died of apoplexy!” said Mr Feeder.\n\n“A Domitian—”\n“And you’re blue, you know,” said Mr Feeder.\n\n“A Nero, a Tiberius, a Caligula, a Heliogabalus, and many more, pursued the Doctor; “it is, Mr Feeder—if you are doing me the honour to attend—remarkable; VERY remarkable, Sir—”\n\nBut Johnson, unable to suppress it any longer, burst at that moment into such an overwhelming fit of coughing, that although both his immediate neighbours thumped him on the back, and Mr Feeder himself held a glass of water to his lips, and the butler walked him up and down several times between his own chair and the sideboard, like a sentry, it was a full five minutes before he was moderately composed. Then there was a profound silence.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said Doctor Blimber, “rise for Grace! Cornelia, lift Dombey down”—nothing of whom but his scalp was accordingly seen above the tablecloth. “Johnson will repeat to me tomorrow morning before breakfast, without book, and from the Greek Testament, the first chapter of the Epistle of Saint Paul to the Ephesians. We will resume our studies, Mr Feeder, in half-an-hour.”\n\nThe young gentlemen bowed and withdrew. Mr Feeder did likewise. During the half-hour, the young gentlemen, broken into pairs, loitered arm-in-arm up and down a small piece of ground behind the house, or endeavoured to kindle a spark of animation in the breast of Briggs. But nothing happened so vulgar as play. Punctually at the appointed time, the gong was sounded, and the studies, under the joint auspices of Doctor Blimber and Mr Feeder, were resumed.\n\nAs the Olympic game of lounging up and down had been cut shorter than usual that day, on Johnson’s account, they all went out for a walk before tea. Even Briggs (though he hadn’t begun yet) partook of this dissipation; in the enjoyment of which he looked over the cliff two or three times darkly. Doctor Blimber accompanied them; and Paul had the honour of being taken in tow by the Doctor himself: a distinguished state of things, in which he looked very little and feeble.\n\nTea was served in a style no less polite than the dinner; and after tea, the young gentlemen rising and bowing as before, withdrew to fetch up the unfinished tasks of that day, or to get up the already looming tasks of to-morrow. In the meantime Mr Feeder withdrew to his own room; and Paul sat in a corner wondering whether Florence was thinking of him, and what they were all about at Mrs Pipchin’s.\n\nMr Toots, who had been detained by an important letter from the Duke of Wellington, found Paul out after a time; and having looked at him for a long while, as before, inquired if he was fond of waistcoats.\n\nPaul said “Yes, Sir.”\n\n“So am I,” said Toots.\n\nNo word more spoke Toots that night; but he stood looking at Paul as if he liked him; and as there was company in that, and Paul was not inclined to talk, it answered his purpose better than conversation.\n\nAt eight o’clock or so, the gong sounded again for prayers in the dining-room, where the butler afterwards presided over a side-table, on which bread and cheese and beer were spread for such young gentlemen as desired to partake of those refreshments. The ceremonies concluded by the Doctor’s saying, “Gentlemen, we will resume our studies at seven to-morrow;” and then, for the first time, Paul saw Cornelia Blimber’s eye, and saw that it was upon him. When the Doctor had said these words, “Gentlemen, we will resume our studies at seven tomorrow,” the pupils bowed again, and went to bed.\n\nIn the confidence of their own room upstairs, Briggs said his head ached ready to split, and that he should wish himself dead if it wasn’t for his mother, and a blackbird he had at home. Tozer didn’t say much, but he sighed a good deal, and told Paul to look out, for his turn would come to-morrow. After uttering those prophetic words, he undressed himself moodily, and got into bed. Briggs was in his bed too, and Paul in his bed too, before the weak-eyed young man appeared to take away the candle, when he wished them good-night and pleasant dreams. But his benevolent wishes were in vain, as far as Briggs and Tozer were concerned; for Paul, who lay awake for a long while, and often woke afterwards, found that Briggs was ridden by his lesson as a nightmare: and that Tozer, whose mind was affected in his sleep by similar causes, in a minor degree talked unknown tongues, or scraps of Greek and Latin—it was all one to Paul—which, in the silence of night, had an inexpressibly wicked and guilty effect.\n\nPaul had sunk into a sweet sleep, and dreamed that he was walking hand in hand with Florence through beautiful gardens, when they came to a large sunflower which suddenly expanded itself into a gong, and began to sound. Opening his eyes, he found that it was a dark, windy morning, with a drizzling rain: and that the real gong was giving dreadful note of preparation, down in the hall.\n\nSo he got up directly, and found Briggs with hardly any eyes, for nightmare and grief had made his face puffy, putting his boots on: while Tozer stood shivering and rubbing his shoulders in a very bad humour. Poor Paul couldn’t dress himself easily, not being used to it, and asked them if they would have the goodness to tie some strings for him; but as Briggs merely said “Bother!” and Tozer, “Oh yes!” he went down when he was otherwise ready, to the next storey, where he saw a pretty young woman in leather gloves, cleaning a stove. The young woman seemed surprised at his appearance, and asked him where his mother was. When Paul told her she was dead, she took her gloves off, and did what he wanted; and furthermore rubbed his hands to warm them; and gave him a kiss; and told him whenever he wanted anything of that sort—meaning in the dressing way—to ask for “Melia; which Paul, thanking her very much, said he certainly would. He then proceeded softly on his journey downstairs, towards the room in which the young gentlemen resumed their studies, when, passing by a door that stood ajar, a voice from within cried, “Is that Dombey?” On Paul replying, “Yes, Ma’am:” for he knew the voice to be Miss Blimber’s: Miss Blimber said, “Come in, Dombey.” And in he went.\n\nMiss Blimber presented exactly the appearance she had presented yesterday, except that she wore a shawl. Her little light curls were as crisp as ever, and she had already her spectacles on, which made Paul wonder whether she went to bed in them. She had a cool little sitting-room of her own up there, with some books in it, and no fire But Miss Blimber was never cold, and never sleepy.\n\nNow, Dombey,” said Miss Blimber, “I am going out for a constitutional.”\n\nPaul wondered what that was, and why she didn’t send the footman out to get it in such unfavourable weather. But he made no observation on the subject: his attention being devoted to a little pile of new books, on which Miss Blimber appeared to have been recently engaged.\n\n“These are yours, Dombey,” said Miss Blimber.\n\n“All of ’em, Ma’am?” said Paul.\n\n“Yes,” returned Miss Blimber; “and Mr Feeder will look you out some more very soon, if you are as studious as I expect you will be, Dombey.”\n\n“Thank you, Ma’am,” said Paul.\n\n“I am going out for a constitutional,” resumed Miss Blimber; “and while I am gone, that is to say in the interval between this and breakfast, Dombey, I wish you to read over what I have marked in these books, and to tell me if you quite understand what you have got to learn. Don’t lose time, Dombey, for you have none to spare, but take them downstairs, and begin directly.”\n\n“Yes, Ma’am,” answered Paul.\n\nThere were so many of them, that although Paul put one hand under the bottom book and his other hand and his chin on the top book, and hugged them all closely, the middle book slipped out before he reached the door, and then they all tumbled down on the floor. Miss Blimber said, “Oh, Dombey, Dombey, this is really very careless!” and piled them up afresh for him; and this time, by dint of balancing them with great nicety, Paul got out of the room, and down a few stairs before two of them escaped again. But he held the rest so tight, that he only left one more on the first floor, and one in the passage; and when he had got the main body down into the schoolroom, he set off upstairs again to collect the stragglers. Having at last amassed the whole library, and climbed into his place, he fell to work, encouraged by a remark from Tozer to the effect that he “was in for it now;” which was the only interruption he received till breakfast time. At that meal, for which he had no appetite, everything was quite as solemn and genteel as at the others; and when it was finished, he followed Miss Blimber upstairs.\n\n“Now, Dombey,” said Miss Blimber. “How have you got on with those books?”\n\nThey comprised a little English, and a deal of Latin—names of things, declensions of articles and substantives, exercises thereon, and preliminary rules—a trifle of orthography, a glance at ancient history, a wink or two at modern ditto, a few tables, two or three weights and measures, and a little general information. When poor Paul had spelt out number two, he found he had no idea of number one; fragments whereof afterwards obtruded themselves into number three, which slided into number four, which grafted itself on to number two. So that whether twenty Romuluses made a Remus, or hic haec hoc was troy weight, or a verb always agreed with an ancient Briton, or three times four was Taurus a bull, were open questions with him.\n\n“Oh, Dombey, Dombey!” said Miss Blimber, “this is very shocking.”\n\n“If you please,” said Paul, “I think if I might sometimes talk a little to old Glubb, I should be able to do better.”\n\n“Nonsense, Dombey,” said Miss Blimber. “I couldn’t hear of it. This is not the place for Glubbs of any kind. You must take the books down, I suppose, Dombey, one by one, and perfect yourself in the day’s instalment of subject A, before you turn at all to subject B. I am sorry to say, Dombey, that your education appears to have been very much neglected.”\n\n“So Papa says,” returned Paul; “but I told you—I have been a weak child. Florence knows I have. So does Wickam.”\n\n“Who is Wickam?” asked Miss Blimber.\n\n“She has been my nurse,” Paul answered.\n\n“I must beg you not to mention Wickam to me, then,” said Miss Blimber. “I couldn’t allow it”.\n\n“You asked me who she was,” said Paul.\n\n“Very well,” returned Miss Blimber; “but this is all very different indeed from anything of that sort, Dombey, and I couldn’t think of permitting it. As to having been weak, you must begin to be strong. And now take away the top book, if you please, Dombey, and return when you are master of the theme.”\n\nMiss Blimber expressed her opinions on the subject of Paul’s uninstructed state with a gloomy delight, as if she had expected this result, and were glad to find that they must be in constant communication. Paul withdrew with the top task, as he was told, and laboured away at it, down below: sometimes remembering every word of it, and sometimes forgetting it all, and everything else besides: until at last he ventured upstairs again to repeat the lesson, when it was nearly all driven out of his head before he began, by Miss Blimber’s shutting up the book, and saying, “Go on, Dombey!” a proceeding so suggestive of the knowledge inside of her, that Paul looked upon the young lady with consternation, as a kind of learned Guy Fawkes, or artificial Bogle, stuffed full of scholastic straw.\n\nHe acquitted himself very well, nevertheless; and Miss Blimber, commending him as giving promise of getting on fast, immediately provided him with subject B; from which he passed to C, and even D before dinner. It was hard work, resuming his studies, soon after dinner; and he felt giddy and confused and drowsy and dull. But all the other young gentlemen had similar sensations, and were obliged to resume their studies too, if there were any comfort in that. It was a wonder that the great clock in the hall, instead of being constant to its first inquiry, never said, “Gentlemen, we will now resume our studies,” for that phrase was often enough repeated in its neighbourhood. The studies went round like a mighty wheel, and the young gentlemen were always stretched upon it.\n\nAfter tea there were exercises again, and preparations for next day by candlelight. And in due course there was bed; where, but for that resumption of the studies which took place in dreams, were rest and sweet forgetfulness.\n\nOh Saturdays! Oh happy Saturdays, when Florence always came at noon, and never would, in any weather, stay away, though Mrs Pipchin snarled and growled, and worried her bitterly. Those Saturdays were Sabbaths for at least two little Christians among all the Jews, and did the holy Sabbath work of strengthening and knitting up a brother’s and a sister’s love.\n\nNot even Sunday nights—the heavy Sunday nights, whose shadow darkened the first waking burst of light on Sunday mornings—could mar those precious Saturdays. Whether it was the great sea-shore, where they sat, and strolled together; or whether it was only Mrs Pipchin’s dull back room, in which she sang to him so softly, with his drowsy head upon her arm; Paul never cared. It was Florence. That was all he thought of. So, on Sunday nights, when the Doctor’s dark door stood agape to swallow him up for another week, the time was come for taking leave of Florence; no one else.\n\nMrs Wickam had been drafted home to the house in town, and Miss Nipper, now a smart young woman, had come down. To many a single combat with Mrs Pipchin, did Miss Nipper gallantly devote herself, and if ever Mrs Pipchin in all her life had found her match, she had found it now. Miss Nipper threw away the scabbard the first morning she arose in Mrs Pipchin’s house. She asked and gave no quarter. She said it must be war, and war it was; and Mrs Pipchin lived from that time in the midst of surprises, harassings, and defiances, and skirmishing attacks that came bouncing in upon her from the passage, even in unguarded moments of chops, and carried desolation to her very toast.\n\nMiss Nipper had returned one Sunday night with Florence, from walking back with Paul to the Doctor’s, when Florence took from her bosom a little piece of paper, on which she had pencilled down some words.\n\n“See here, Susan,” she said. “These are the names of the little books that Paul brings home to do those long exercises with, when he is so tired. I copied them last night while he was writing.”\n\n“Don’t show ’em to me, Miss Floy, if you please,” returned Nipper, “I’d as soon see Mrs Pipchin.”\n\n“I want you to buy them for me, Susan, if you will, tomorrow morning. I have money enough,” said Florence.\n\n“Why, goodness gracious me, Miss Floy,” returned Miss Nipper, “how can you talk like that, when you have books upon books already, and masterses and mississes a teaching of you everything continual, though my belief is that your Pa, Miss Dombey, never would have learnt you nothing, never would have thought of it, unless you’d asked him—when he couldn’t well refuse; but giving consent when asked, and offering when unasked, Miss, is quite two things; I may not have my objections to a young man’s keeping company with me, and when he puts the question, may say ‘yes,’ but that’s not saying ‘would you be so kind as like me.’”\n\n“But you can buy me the books, Susan; and you will, when you know why I want them.”\n\n“Well, Miss, and why do you want ’em?” replied Nipper; adding, in a lower voice, “If it was to fling at Mrs Pipchin’s head, I’d buy a cart-load.”\n\n“Paul has a great deal too much to do, Susan,” said Florence, “I am sure of it.”\n\n“And well you may be, Miss,” returned her maid, “and make your mind quite easy that the willing dear is worked and worked away. If those is Latin legs,” exclaimed Miss Nipper, with strong feeling—in allusion to Paul’s; “give me English ones.”\n\n“I am afraid he feels lonely and lost at Doctor Blimber’s, Susan,” pursued Florence, turning away her face.\n\n“Ah,” said Miss Nipper, with great sharpness, “Oh, them ‘Blimbers’”\n\n“Don’t blame anyone,” said Florence. “It’s a mistake.”\n\n“I say nothing about blame, Miss,” cried Miss Nipper, “for I know that you object, but I may wish, Miss, that the family was set to work to make new roads, and that Miss Blimber went in front and had the pickaxe.”\n\nAfter this speech, Miss Nipper, who was perfectly serious, wiped her eyes.\n\n“I think I could perhaps give Paul some help, Susan, if I had these books,” said Florence, “and make the coming week a little easier to him. At least I want to try. So buy them for me, dear, and I will never forget how kind it was of you to do it!”\n\nIt must have been a harder heart than Susan Nipper’s that could have rejected the little purse Florence held out with these words, or the gentle look of entreaty with which she seconded her petition. Susan put the purse in her pocket without reply, and trotted out at once upon her errand.\n\nThe books were not easy to procure; and the answer at several shops was, either that they were just out of them, or that they never kept them, or that they had had a great many last month, or that they expected a great many next week But Susan was not easily baffled in such an enterprise; and having entrapped a white-haired youth, in a black calico apron, from a library where she was known, to accompany her in her quest, she led him such a life in going up and down, that he exerted himself to the utmost, if it were only to get rid of her; and finally enabled her to return home in triumph.\n\nWith these treasures then, after her own daily lessons were over, Florence sat down at night to track Paul’s footsteps through the thorny ways of learning; and being possessed of a naturally quick and sound capacity, and taught by that most wonderful of masters, love, it was not long before she gained upon Paul’s heels, and caught and passed him.\n\nNot a word of this was breathed to Mrs Pipchin: but many a night when they were all in bed, and when Miss Nipper, with her hair in papers and herself asleep in some uncomfortable attitude, reposed unconscious by her side; and when the chinking ashes in the grate were cold and grey; and when the candles were burnt down and guttering out;—Florence tried so hard to be a substitute for one small Dombey, that her fortitude and perseverance might have almost won her a free right to bear the name herself.\n\nAnd high was her reward, when one Saturday evening, as little Paul was sitting down as usual to “resume his studies,” she sat down by his side, and showed him all that was so rough, made smooth, and all that was so dark, made clear and plain, before him. It was nothing but a startled look in Paul’s wan face—a flush—a smile—and then a close embrace—but God knows how her heart leapt up at this rich payment for her trouble.\n\n“Oh, Floy!” cried her brother, “how I love you! How I love you, Floy!”\n\n“And I you, dear!”\n\n“Oh! I am sure of that, Floy.”\n\nHe said no more about it, but all that evening sat close by her, very quiet; and in the night he called out from his little room within hers, three or four times, that he loved her.\n\nRegularly, after that, Florence was prepared to sit down with Paul on Saturday night, and patiently assist him through so much as they could anticipate together of his next week’s work. The cheering thought that he was labouring on where Florence had just toiled before him, would, of itself, have been a stimulant to Paul in the perpetual resumption of his studies; but coupled with the actual lightening of his load, consequent on this assistance, it saved him, possibly, from sinking underneath the burden which the fair Cornelia Blimber piled upon his back.\n\nIt was not that Miss Blimber meant to be too hard upon him, or that Doctor Blimber meant to bear too heavily on the young gentlemen in general. Cornelia merely held the faith in which she had been bred; and the Doctor, in some partial confusion of his ideas, regarded the young gentlemen as if they were all Doctors, and were born grown up. Comforted by the applause of the young gentlemen’s nearest relations, and urged on by their blind vanity and ill-considered haste, it would have been strange if Doctor Blimber had discovered his mistake, or trimmed his swelling sails to any other tack.\n\nThus in the case of Paul. When Doctor Blimber said he made great progress and was naturally clever, Mr Dombey was more bent than ever on his being forced and crammed. In the case of Briggs, when Doctor Blimber reported that he did not make great progress yet, and was not naturally clever, Briggs senior was inexorable in the same purpose. In short, however high and false the temperature at which the Doctor kept his hothouse, the owners of the plants were always ready to lend a helping hand at the bellows, and to stir the fire.\n\nSuch spirits as he had in the outset, Paul soon lost of course. But he retained all that was strange, and old, and thoughtful in his character: and under circumstances so favourable to the development of those tendencies, became even more strange, and old, and thoughtful, than before.\n\nThe only difference was, that he kept his character to himself. He grew more thoughtful and reserved, every day; and had no such curiosity in any living member of the Doctor’s household, as he had had in Mrs Pipchin. He loved to be alone; and in those short intervals when he was not occupied with his books, liked nothing so well as wandering about the house by himself, or sitting on the stairs, listening to the great clock in the hall. He was intimate with all the paperhanging in the house; saw things that no one else saw in the patterns; found out miniature tigers and lions running up the bedroom walls, and squinting faces leering in the squares and diamonds of the floor-cloth.\n\nThe solitary child lived on, surrounded by this arabesque work of his musing fancy, and no one understood him. Mrs Blimber thought him “odd,” and sometimes the servants said among themselves that little Dombey “moped;” but that was all.\n\nUnless young Toots had some idea on the subject, to the expression of which he was wholly unequal. Ideas, like ghosts (according to the common notion of ghosts), must be spoken to a little before they will explain themselves; and Toots had long left off asking any questions of his own mind. Some mist there may have been, issuing from that leaden casket, his cranium, which, if it could have taken shape and form, would have become a genie; but it could not; and it only so far followed the example of the smoke in the Arabian story, as to roll out in a thick cloud, and there hang and hover. But it left a little figure visible upon a lonely shore, and Toots was always staring at it.\n\n“How are you?” he would say to Paul, fifty times a day. “Quite well, Sir, thank you,” Paul would answer. “Shake hands,” would be Toots’s next advance.\n\nWhich Paul, of course, would immediately do. Mr Toots generally said again, after a long interval of staring and hard breathing, “How are you?” To which Paul again replied, “Quite well, Sir, thank you.”\n\nOne evening Mr Toots was sitting at his desk, oppressed by correspondence, when a great purpose seemed to flash upon him. He laid down his pen, and went off to seek Paul, whom he found at last, after a long search, looking through the window of his little bedroom.\n\n“I say!” cried Toots, speaking the moment he entered the room, lest he should forget it; “what do you think about?”\n\n“Oh! I think about a great many things,” replied Paul.\n\n“Do you, though?” said Toots, appearing to consider that fact in itself surprising. “If you had to die,” said Paul, looking up into his face—Mr Toots started, and seemed much disturbed.\n\n“Don’t you think you would rather die on a moonlight night, when the sky was quite clear, and the wind blowing, as it did last night?”\n\nMr Toots said, looking doubtfully at Paul, and shaking his head, that he didn’t know about that.\n\n“Not blowing, at least,” said Paul, “but sounding in the air like the sea sounds in the shells. It was a beautiful night. When I had listened to the water for a long time, I got up and looked out. There was a boat over there, in the full light of the moon; a boat with a sail.”\n\nThe child looked at him so steadfastly, and spoke so earnestly, that Mr Toots, feeling himself called upon to say something about this boat, said, “Smugglers.” But with an impartial remembrance of there being two sides to every question, he added, “or Preventive.”\n\n“A boat with a sail,” repeated Paul, “in the full light of the moon. The sail like an arm, all silver. It went away into the distance, and what do you think it seemed to do as it moved with the waves?”\n\n“Pitch,” said Mr Toots.\n\n“It seemed to beckon,” said the child, “to beckon me to come!—There she is! There she is!”\n\nToots was almost beside himself with dismay at this sudden exclamation, after what had gone before, and cried “Who?”\n\n“My sister Florence!” cried Paul, “looking up here, and waving her hand. She sees me—she sees me! Good-night, dear, good-night, good-night.”\n\nHis quick transition to a state of unbounded pleasure, as he stood at his window, kissing and clapping his hands: and the way in which the light retreated from his features as she passed out of his view, and left a patient melancholy on the little face: were too remarkable wholly to escape even Toots’s notice. Their interview being interrupted at this moment by a visit from Mrs Pipchin, who usually brought her black skirts to bear upon Paul just before dusk, once or twice a week, Toots had no opportunity of improving the occasion: but it left so marked an impression on his mind that he twice returned, after having exchanged the usual salutations, to ask Mrs Pipchin how she did. This the irascible old lady conceived to be a deeply devised and long-meditated insult, originating in the diabolical invention of the weak-eyed young man downstairs, against whom she accordingly lodged a formal complaint with Doctor Blimber that very night; who mentioned to the young man that if he ever did it again, he should be obliged to part with him.\n\nThe evenings being longer now, Paul stole up to his window every evening to look out for Florence. She always passed and repassed at a certain time, until she saw him; and their mutual recognition was a gleam of sunshine in Paul’s daily life. Often after dark, one other figure walked alone before the Doctor’s house. He rarely joined them on the Saturdays now. He could not bear it. He would rather come unrecognised, and look up at the windows where his son was qualifying for a man; and wait, and watch, and plan, and hope.\n\nOh! could he but have seen, or seen as others did, the slight spare boy above, watching the waves and clouds at twilight, with his earnest eyes, and breasting the window of his solitary cage when birds flew by, as if he would have emulated them, and soared away!\n\n# CHAPTER XIII. Shipping Intelligence and Office Business\n\nMr Dombey’s offices were in a court where there was an old-established stall of choice fruit at the corner: where perambulating merchants, of both sexes, offered for sale at any time between the hours of ten and five, slippers, pocket-books, sponges, dogs’ collars, and Windsor soap; and sometimes a pointer or an oil-painting.\n\nThe pointer always came that way, with a view to the Stock Exchange, where a sporting taste (originating generally in bets of new hats) is much in vogue. The other commodities were addressed to the general public; but they were never offered by the vendors to Mr Dombey. When he appeared, the dealers in those wares fell off respectfully. The principal slipper and dogs’ collar man—who considered himself a public character, and whose portrait was screwed on to an artist’s door in Cheapside—threw up his forefinger to the brim of his hat as Mr Dombey went by. The ticket-porter, if he were not absent on a job, always ran officiously before, to open Mr Dombey’s office door as wide as possible, and hold it open, with his hat off, while he entered.\n\nThe clerks within were not a whit behind-hand in their demonstrations of respect. A solemn hush prevailed, as Mr Dombey passed through the outer office. The wit of the Counting-House became in a moment as mute as the row of leathern fire-buckets hanging up behind him. Such vapid and flat daylight as filtered through the ground-glass windows and skylights, leaving a black sediment upon the panes, showed the books and papers, and the figures bending over them, enveloped in a studious gloom, and as much abstracted in appearance, from the world without, as if they were assembled at the bottom of the sea; while a mouldy little strong room in the obscure perspective, where a shaded lamp was always burning, might have represented the cavern of some ocean monster, looking on with a red eye at these mysteries of the deep.\n\nWhen Perch the messenger, whose place was on a little bracket, like a timepiece, saw Mr Dombey come in—or rather when he felt that he was coming, for he had usually an instinctive sense of his approach—he hurried into Mr Dombey’s room, stirred the fire, carried fresh coals from the bowels of the coal-box, hung the newspaper to air upon the fender, put the chair ready, and the screen in its place, and was round upon his heel on the instant of Mr Dombey’s entrance, to take his great-coat and hat, and hang them up. Then Perch took the newspaper, and gave it a turn or two in his hands before the fire, and laid it, deferentially, at Mr Dombey’s elbow. And so little objection had Perch to being deferential in the last degree, that if he might have laid himself at Mr Dombey’s feet, or might have called him by some such title as used to be bestowed upon the Caliph Haroun Alraschid, he would have been all the better pleased.\n\nAs this honour would have been an innovation and an experiment, Perch was fain to content himself by expressing as well as he could, in his manner, You are the light of my Eyes. You are the Breath of my Soul. You are the commander of the Faithful Perch! With this imperfect happiness to cheer him, he would shut the door softly, walk away on tiptoe, and leave his great chief to be stared at, through a dome-shaped window in the leads, by ugly chimney-pots and backs of houses, and especially by the bold window of a hair-cutting saloon on a first floor, where a waxen effigy, bald as a Mussulman in the morning, and covered, after eleven o’clock in the day, with luxuriant hair and whiskers in the latest Christian fashion, showed him the wrong side of its head for ever.\n\nBetween Mr Dombey and the common world, as it was accessible through the medium of the outer office—to which Mr Dombey’s presence in his own room may be said to have struck like damp, or cold air—there were two degrees of descent. Mr Carker in his own office was the first step; Mr Morfin, in his own office, was the second. Each of these gentlemen occupied a little chamber like a bath-room, opening from the passage outside Mr Dombey’s door. Mr Carker, as Grand Vizier, inhabited the room that was nearest to the Sultan. Mr Morfin, as an officer of inferior state, inhabited the room that was nearest to the clerks.\n\nThe gentleman last mentioned was a cheerful-looking, hazel-eyed elderly bachelor: gravely attired, as to his upper man, in black; and as to his legs, in pepper-and-salt colour. His dark hair was just touched here and there with specks of gray, as though the tread of Time had splashed it; and his whiskers were already white. He had a mighty respect for Mr Dombey, and rendered him due homage; but as he was of a genial temper himself, and never wholly at his ease in that stately presence, he was disquieted by no jealousy of the many conferences enjoyed by Mr Carker, and felt a secret satisfaction in having duties to discharge, which rarely exposed him to be singled out for such distinction. He was a great musical amateur in his way—after business; and had a paternal affection for his violoncello, which was once in every week transported from Islington, his place of abode, to a certain club-room hard by the Bank, where quartettes of the most tormenting and excruciating nature were executed every Wednesday evening by a private party.\n\nMr Carker was a gentleman thirty-eight or forty years old, of a florid complexion, and with two unbroken rows of glistening teeth, whose regularity and whiteness were quite distressing. It was impossible to escape the observation of them, for he showed them whenever he spoke; and bore so wide a smile upon his countenance (a smile, however, very rarely, indeed, extending beyond his mouth), that there was something in it like the snarl of a cat. He affected a stiff white cravat, after the example of his principal, and was always closely buttoned up and tightly dressed. His manner towards Mr Dombey was deeply conceived and perfectly expressed. He was familiar with him, in the very extremity of his sense of the distance between them. “Mr Dombey, to a man in your position from a man in mine, there is no show of subservience compatible with the transaction of business between us, that I should think sufficient. I frankly tell you, Sir, I give it up altogether. I feel that I could not satisfy my own mind; and Heaven knows, Mr Dombey, you can afford to dispense with the endeavour.” If he had carried these words about with him printed on a placard, and had constantly offered it to Mr Dombey’s perusal on the breast of his coat, he could not have been more explicit than he was.\n\nThis was Carker the Manager. Mr Carker the Junior, Walter’s friend, was his brother; two or three years older than he, but widely removed in station. The younger brother’s post was on the top of the official ladder; the elder brother’s at the bottom. The elder brother never gained a stave, or raised his foot to mount one. Young men passed above his head, and rose and rose; but he was always at the bottom. He was quite resigned to occupy that low condition: never complained of it: and certainly never hoped to escape from it.\n\n“How do you do this morning?” said Mr Carker the Manager, entering Mr Dombey’s room soon after his arrival one day: with a bundle of papers in his hand.\n\n“How do you do, Carker?” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Coolish!” observed Carker, stirring the fire.\n\n“Rather,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Any news of the young gentleman who is so important to us all?” asked Carker, with his whole regiment of teeth on parade.\n\n“Yes—not direct news—I hear he’s very well,” said Mr Dombey. Who had come from Brighton over-night. But no one knew It.\n\n“Very well, and becoming a great scholar, no doubt?” observed the Manager.\n\n“I hope so,” returned Mr Dombey.\n\n“Egad!” said Mr Carker, shaking his head, “Time flies!”\n\n“I think so, sometimes,” returned Mr Dombey, glancing at his newspaper.\n\n“Oh! You! You have no reason to think so,” observed Carker. “One who sits on such an elevation as yours, and can sit there, unmoved, in all seasons—hasn’t much reason to know anything about the flight of time. It’s men like myself, who are low down and are not superior in circumstances, and who inherit new masters in the course of Time, that have cause to look about us. I shall have a rising sun to worship, soon.”\n\n“Time enough, time enough, Carker!” said Mr Dombey, rising from his chair, and standing with his back to the fire. “Have you anything there for me?”\n\n“I don’t know that I need trouble you,” returned Carker, turning over the papers in his hand. “You have a committee today at three, you know.”\n\n“And one at three, three-quarters,” added Mr Dombey.\n\n“Catch you forgetting anything!” exclaimed Carker, still turning over his papers. “If Mr Paul inherits your memory, he’ll be a troublesome customer in the House. One of you is enough.”\n\n“You have an accurate memory of your own,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Oh! I!” returned the manager. “It’s the only capital of a man like me.”\n\nMr Dombey did not look less pompous or at all displeased, as he stood leaning against the chimney-piece, surveying his (of course unconscious) clerk, from head to foot. The stiffness and nicety of Mr Carker’s dress, and a certain arrogance of manner, either natural to him or imitated from a pattern not far off, gave great additional effect to his humility. He seemed a man who would contend against the power that vanquished him, if he could, but who was utterly borne down by the greatness and superiority of Mr Dombey.\n\n“Is Morfin here?” asked Mr Dombey after a short pause, during which Mr Carker had been fluttering his papers, and muttering little abstracts of their contents to himself.\n\n“Morfin’s here,” he answered, looking up with his widest and almost sudden smile; “humming musical recollections—of his last night’s quartette party, I suppose—through the walls between us, and driving me half mad. I wish he’d make a bonfire of his violoncello, and burn his music-books in it.”\n\n“You respect nobody, Carker, I think,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“No?” inquired Carker, with another wide and most feline show of his teeth. “Well! Not many people, I believe. I wouldn’t answer perhaps,” he murmured, as if he were only thinking it, “for more than one.”\n\nA dangerous quality, if real; and a not less dangerous one, if feigned. But Mr Dombey hardly seemed to think so, as he still stood with his back to the fire, drawn up to his full height, and looking at his head-clerk with a dignified composure, in which there seemed to lurk a stronger latent sense of power than usual.\n\n“Talking of Morfin,” resumed Mr Carker, taking out one paper from the rest, “he reports a junior dead in the agency at Barbados, and proposes to reserve a passage in the Son and Heir—she’ll sail in a month or so—for the successor. You don’t care who goes, I suppose? We have nobody of that sort here.”\n\nMr Dombey shook his head with supreme indifference.\n\n“It’s no very precious appointment,” observed Mr Carker, taking up a pen, with which to endorse a memorandum on the back of the paper. “I hope he may bestow it on some orphan nephew of a musical friend. It may perhaps stop his fiddle-playing, if he has a gift that way. Who’s that? Come in!”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Mr Carker. I didn’t know you were here, Sir,” answered Walter; appearing with some letters in his hand, unopened, and newly arrived. “Mr Carker the junior, Sir—”\n\nAt the mention of this name, Mr Carker the Manager was or affected to be, touched to the quick with shame and humiliation. He cast his eyes full on Mr Dombey with an altered and apologetic look, abased them on the ground, and remained for a moment without speaking.\n\n“I thought, Sir,” he said suddenly and angrily, turning on Walter, “that you had been before requested not to drag Mr Carker the Junior into your conversation.”\n\n“I beg your pardon,” returned Walter. “I was only going to say that Mr Carker the Junior had told me he believed you were gone out, or I should not have knocked at the door when you were engaged with Mr Dombey. These are letters for Mr Dombey, Sir.”\n\n“Very well, Sir,” returned Mr Carker the Manager, plucking them sharply from his hand. “Go about your business.”\n\nBut in taking them with so little ceremony, Mr Carker dropped one on the floor, and did not see what he had done; neither did Mr Dombey observe the letter lying near his feet. Walter hesitated for a moment, thinking that one or other of them would notice it; but finding that neither did, he stopped, came back, picked it up, and laid it himself on Mr Dombey’s desk. The letters were post-letters; and it happened that the one in question was Mrs Pipchin’s regular report, directed as usual—for Mrs Pipchin was but an indifferent penwoman—by Florence. Mr Dombey, having his attention silently called to this letter by Walter, started, and looked fiercely at him, as if he believed that he had purposely selected it from all the rest.\n\n“You can leave the room, Sir!” said Mr Dombey, haughtily.\n\nHe crushed the letter in his hand; and having watched Walter out at the door, put it in his pocket without breaking the seal.\n\n“These continual references to Mr Carker the Junior,” Mr Carker the Manager began, as soon as they were alone, “are, to a man in my position, uttered before one in yours, so unspeakably distressing—”\n\n“Nonsense, Carker,” Mr Dombey interrupted. “You are too sensitive.”\n\n“I am sensitive,” he returned. “If one in your position could by any possibility imagine yourself in my place: which you cannot: you would be so too.”\n\nAs Mr Dombey’s thoughts were evidently pursuing some other subject, his discreet ally broke off here, and stood with his teeth ready to present to him, when he should look up.\n\n“You want somebody to send to the West Indies, you were saying,” observed Mr Dombey, hurriedly.\n\n“Yes,” replied Carker.\n\n“Send young Gay.”\n\n“Good, very good indeed. Nothing easier,” said Mr Carker, without any show of surprise, and taking up the pen to re-endorse the letter, as coolly as he had done before. “‘Send young Gay.’”\n\n“Call him back,” said Mr Dombey.\n\nMr Carker was quick to do so, and Walter was quick to return.\n\n“Gay,” said Mr Dombey, turning a little to look at him over his shoulder. “Here is a—”\n\n“An opening,” said Mr Carker, with his mouth stretched to the utmost.\n\n“In the West Indies. At Barbados. I am going to send you,” said Mr Dombey, scorning to embellish the bare truth, “to fill a junior situation in the counting-house at Barbados. Let your Uncle know from me, that I have chosen you to go to the West Indies.”\n\nWalter’s breath was so completely taken away by his astonishment, that he could hardly find enough for the repetition of the words “West Indies.”\n\n“Somebody must go,” said Mr Dombey, “and you are young and healthy, and your Uncle’s circumstances are not good. Tell your Uncle that you are appointed. You will not go yet. There will be an interval of a month—or two perhaps.”\n\n“Shall I remain there, Sir?” inquired Walter.\n\n“Will you remain there, Sir!” repeated Mr Dombey, turning a little more round towards him. “What do you mean? What does he mean, Carker?”\n\n“Live there, Sir,” faltered Walter.\n\n“Certainly,” returned Mr Dombey.\n\nWalter bowed.\n\n“That’s all,” said Mr Dombey, resuming his letters. “You will explain to him in good time about the usual outfit and so forth, Carker, of course. He needn’t wait, Carker.”\n\n“You needn’t wait, Gay,” observed Mr Carker: bare to the gums.\n\n“Unless,” said Mr Dombey, stopping in his reading without looking off the letter, and seeming to listen. “Unless he has anything to say.”\n\n“No, Sir,” returned Walter, agitated and confused, and almost stunned, as an infinite variety of pictures presented themselves to his mind; among which Captain Cuttle, in his glazed hat, transfixed with astonishment at Mrs MacStinger’s, and his uncle bemoaning his loss in the little back parlour, held prominent places. “I hardly know—I—I am much obliged, Sir.”\n\n“He needn’t wait, Carker,” said Mr Dombey.\n\nAnd as Mr Carker again echoed the words, and also collected his papers as if he were going away too, Walter felt that his lingering any longer would be an unpardonable intrusion—especially as he had nothing to say—and therefore walked out quite confounded.\n\nGoing along the passage, with the mingled consciousness and helplessness of a dream, he heard Mr Dombey’s door shut again, as Mr Carker came out: and immediately afterwards that gentleman called to him.\n\n“Bring your friend Mr Carker the Junior to my room, Sir, if you please.”\n\nWalter went to the outer office and apprised Mr Carker the Junior of his errand, who accordingly came out from behind a partition where he sat alone in one corner, and returned with him to the room of Mr Carker the Manager.\n\nThat gentleman was standing with his back to the fire, and his hands under his coat-tails, looking over his white cravat, as unpromisingly as Mr Dombey himself could have looked. He received them without any change in his attitude or softening of his harsh and black expression: merely signing to Walter to close the door.\n\n“John Carker,” said the Manager, when this was done, turning suddenly upon his brother, with his two rows of teeth bristling as if he would have bitten him, “what is the league between you and this young man, in virtue of which I am haunted and hunted by the mention of your name? Is it not enough for you, John Carker, that I am your near relation, and can’t detach myself from that—”\n\n“Say disgrace, James,” interposed the other in a low voice, finding that he stammered for a word. “You mean it, and have reason, say disgrace.”\n\n“From that disgrace,” assented his brother with keen emphasis, “but is the fact to be blurted out and trumpeted, and proclaimed continually in the presence of the very House! In moments of confidence too? Do you think your name is calculated to harmonise in this place with trust and confidence, John Carker?”\n\n“No,” returned the other. “No, James. God knows I have no such thought.”\n\n“What is your thought, then?” said his brother, “and why do you thrust yourself in my way? Haven’t you injured me enough already?”\n\n“I have never injured you, James, wilfully.”\n\n“You are my brother,” said the Manager. “That’s injury enough.”\n\n“I wish I could undo it, James.”\n\n“I wish you could and would.”\n\nDuring this conversation, Walter had looked from one brother to the other, with pain and amazement. He who was the Senior in years, and Junior in the House, stood, with his eyes cast upon the ground, and his head bowed, humbly listening to the reproaches of the other. Though these were rendered very bitter by the tone and look with which they were accompanied, and by the presence of Walter whom they so much surprised and shocked, he entered no other protest against them than by slightly raising his right hand in a deprecatory manner, as if he would have said, “Spare me!” So, had they been blows, and he a brave man, under strong constraint, and weakened by bodily suffering, he might have stood before the executioner.\n\nGenerous and quick in all his emotions, and regarding himself as the innocent occasion of these taunts, Walter now struck in, with all the earnestness he felt.\n\n“Mr Carker,” he said, addressing himself to the Manager. “Indeed, indeed, this is my fault solely. In a kind of heedlessness, for which I cannot blame myself enough, I have, I have no doubt, mentioned Mr Carker the Junior much oftener than was necessary; and have allowed his name sometimes to slip through my lips, when it was against your expressed wish. But it has been my own mistake, Sir. We have never exchanged one word upon the subject—very few, indeed, on any subject. And it has not been,” added Walter, after a moment’s pause, “all heedlessness on my part, Sir; for I have felt an interest in Mr Carker ever since I have been here, and have hardly been able to help speaking of him sometimes, when I have thought of him so much!”\n\nWalter said this from his soul, and with the very breath of honour. For he looked upon the bowed head, and the downcast eyes, and upraised hand, and thought, “I have felt it; and why should I not avow it in behalf of this unfriended, broken man!”\n\nMr Carker the Manager looked at him, as he spoke, and when he had finished speaking, with a smile that seemed to divide his face into two parts.\n\n“You are an excitable youth, Gay,” he said; “and should endeavour to cool down a little now, for it would be unwise to encourage feverish predispositions. Be as cool as you can, Gay. Be as cool as you can. You might have asked Mr John Carker himself (if you have not done so) whether he claims to be, or is, an object of such strong interest.”\n\n“James, do me justice,” said his brother. “I have claimed nothing; and I claim nothing. Believe me, on my—”\n\n“Honour?” said his brother, with another smile, as he warmed himself before the fire.\n\n“On my Me—on my fallen life!” returned the other, in the same low voice, but with a deeper stress on his words than he had yet seemed capable of giving them. “Believe me, I have held myself aloof, and kept alone. This has been unsought by me. I have avoided him and everyone.\n\n“Indeed, you have avoided me, Mr Carker,” said Walter, with the tears rising to his eyes; so true was his compassion. “I know it, to my disappointment and regret. When I first came here, and ever since, I am sure I have tried to be as much your friend, as one of my age could presume to be; but it has been of no use.\n\n“And observe,” said the Manager, taking him up quickly, “it will be of still less use, Gay, if you persist in forcing Mr John Carker’s name on people’s attention. That is not the way to befriend Mr John Carker. Ask him if he thinks it is.”\n\n“It is no service to me,” said the brother. “It only leads to such a conversation as the present, which I need not say I could have well spared. No one can be a better friend to me:” he spoke here very distinctly, as if he would impress it upon Walter: “than in forgetting me, and leaving me to go my way, unquestioned and unnoticed.”\n\n“Your memory not being retentive, Gay, of what you are told by others,” said Mr Carker the Manager, warming himself with great and increased satisfaction, “I thought it well that you should be told this from the best authority,” nodding towards his brother. “You are not likely to forget it now, I hope. That’s all, Gay. You can go.”\n\nWalter passed out at the door, and was about to close it after him, when, hearing the voices of the brothers again, and also the mention of his own name, he stood irresolutely, with his hand upon the lock, and the door ajar, uncertain whether to return or go away. In this position he could not help overhearing what followed.\n\n“Think of me more leniently, if you can, James,” said John Carker, “when I tell you I have had—how could I help having, with my history, written here”—striking himself upon the breast—“my whole heart awakened by my observation of that boy, Walter Gay. I saw in him when he first came here, almost my other self.”\n\n“Your other self!” repeated the Manager, disdainfully.\n\n“Not as I am, but as I was when I first came here too; as sanguine, giddy, youthful, inexperienced; flushed with the same restless and adventurous fancies; and full of the same qualities, fraught with the same capacity of leading on to good or evil.”\n\n“I hope not,” said his brother, with some hidden and sarcastic meaning in his tone.\n\n“You strike me sharply; and your hand is steady, and your thrust is very deep,” returned the other, speaking (or so Walter thought) as if some cruel weapon actually stabbed him as he spoke. “I imagined all this when he was a boy. I believed it. It was a truth to me. I saw him lightly walking on the edge of an unseen gulf where so many others walk with equal gaiety, and from which—”\n\n“The old excuse,” interrupted his brother, as he stirred the fire. “So many. Go on. Say, so many fall.”\n\n“From which ONE traveller fell,” returned the other, “who set forward, on his way, a boy like him, and missed his footing more and more, and slipped a little and a little lower; and went on stumbling still, until he fell headlong and found himself below a shattered man. Think what I suffered, when I watched that boy.”\n\n“You have only yourself to thank for it,” returned the brother.\n\n“Only myself,” he assented with a sigh. “I don’t seek to divide the blame or shame.”\n\n“You have divided the shame,” James Carker muttered through his teeth. And, through so many and such close teeth, he could mutter well.\n\n“Ah, James,” returned his brother, speaking for the first time in an accent of reproach, and seeming, by the sound of his voice, to have covered his face with his hands, “I have been, since then, a useful foil to you. You have trodden on me freely in your climbing up. Don’t spurn me with your heel!”\n\nA silence ensued. After a time, Mr Carker the Manager was heard rustling among his papers, as if he had resolved to bring the interview to a conclusion. At the same time his brother withdrew nearer to the door.\n\n“That’s all,” he said. “I watched him with such trembling and such fear, as was some little punishment to me, until he passed the place where I first fell; and then, though I had been his father, I believe I never could have thanked God more devoutly. I didn’t dare to warn him, and advise him; but if I had seen direct cause, I would have shown him my example. I was afraid to be seen speaking with him, lest it should be thought I did him harm, and tempted him to evil, and corrupted him: or lest I really should. There may be such contagion in me; I don’t know. Piece out my history, in connexion with young Walter Gay, and what he has made me feel; and think of me more leniently, James, if you can.”\n\nWith these words he came out to where Walter was standing. He turned a little paler when he saw him there, and paler yet when Walter caught him by the hand, and said in a whisper:\n\n“Mr Carker, pray let me thank you! Let me say how much I feel for you! How sorry I am, to have been the unhappy cause of all this! How I almost look upon you now as my protector and guardian! How very, very much, I feel obliged to you and pity you!” said Walter, squeezing both his hands, and hardly knowing, in his agitation, what he did or said.\n\nMr Morfin’s room being close at hand and empty, and the door wide open, they moved thither by one accord: the passage being seldom free from someone passing to or fro. When they were there, and Walter saw in Mr Carker’s face some traces of the emotion within, he almost felt as if he had never seen the face before; it was so greatly changed.\n\n“Walter,” he said, laying his hand on his shoulder. “I am far removed from you, and may I ever be. Do you know what I am?”\n\n“What you are!” appeared to hang on Walter’s lips, as he regarded him attentively.\n\n“It was begun,” said Carker, “before my twenty-first birthday—led up to, long before, but not begun till near that time. I had robbed them when I came of age. I robbed them afterwards. Before my twenty-second birthday, it was all found out; and then, Walter, from all men’s society, I died.”\n\nAgain his last few words hung trembling upon Walter’s lips, but he could neither utter them, nor any of his own.\n\n“The House was very good to me. May Heaven reward the old man for his forbearance! This one, too, his son, who was then newly in the Firm, where I had held great trust! I was called into that room which is now his—I have never entered it since—and came out, what you know me. For many years I sat in my present seat, alone as now, but then a known and recognised example to the rest. They were all merciful to me, and I lived. Time has altered that part of my poor expiation; and I think, except the three heads of the House, there is no one here who knows my story rightly. Before the little boy grows up, and has it told to him, my corner may be vacant. I would rather that it might be so! This is the only change to me since that day, when I left all youth, and hope, and good men’s company, behind me in that room. God bless you, Walter! Keep you, and all dear to you, in honesty, or strike them dead!”\n\nSome recollection of his trembling from head to foot, as if with excessive cold, and of his bursting into tears, was all that Walter could add to this, when he tried to recall exactly what had passed between them.\n\nWhen Walter saw him next, he was bending over his desk in his old silent, drooping, humbled way. Then, observing him at his work, and feeling how resolved he evidently was that no further intercourse should arise between them, and thinking again and again on all he had seen and heard that morning in so short a time, in connexion with the history of both the Carkers, Walter could hardly believe that he was under orders for the West Indies, and would soon be lost to Uncle Sol, and Captain Cuttle, and to glimpses few and far between of Florence Dombey—no, he meant Paul—and to all he loved, and liked, and looked for, in his daily life.\n\nBut it was true, and the news had already penetrated to the outer office; for while he sat with a heavy heart, pondering on these things, and resting his head upon his arm, Perch the messenger, descending from his mahogany bracket, and jogging his elbow, begged his pardon, but wished to say in his ear, Did he think he could arrange to send home to England a jar of preserved Ginger, cheap, for Mrs Perch’s own eating, in the course of her recovery from her next confinement?\n\nCHAPTER XIV. Paul grows more and more Old-fashioned, and goes Home for the Holidays\nWhen the Midsummer vacation approached, no indecent manifestations of joy were exhibited by the leaden-eyed young gentlemen assembled at Doctor Blimber’s. Any such violent expression as “breaking up,” would have been quite inapplicable to that polite establishment. The young gentlemen oozed away, semi-annually, to their own homes; but they never broke up. They would have scorned the action.\n\nTozer, who was constantly galled and tormented by a starched white cambric neckerchief, which he wore at the express desire of Mrs Tozer, his parent, who, designing him for the Church, was of opinion that he couldn’t be in that forward state of preparation too soon—Tozer said, indeed, that choosing between two evils, he thought he would rather stay where he was, than go home. However inconsistent this declaration might appear with that passage in Tozer’s Essay on the subject, wherein he had observed “that the thoughts of home and all its recollections, awakened in his mind the most pleasing emotions of anticipation and delight,” and had also likened himself to a Roman General, flushed with a recent victory over the Iceni, or laden with Carthaginian spoil, advancing within a few hours’ march of the Capitol, presupposed, for the purposes of the simile, to be the dwelling-place of Mrs Tozer, still it was very sincerely made. For it seemed that Tozer had a dreadful Uncle, who not only volunteered examinations of him, in the holidays, on abstruse points, but twisted innocent events and things, and wrenched them to the same fell purpose. So that if this Uncle took him to the Play, or, on a similar pretence of kindness, carried him to see a Giant, or a Dwarf, or a Conjuror, or anything, Tozer knew he had read up some classical allusion to the subject beforehand, and was thrown into a state of mortal apprehension: not foreseeing where he might break out, or what authority he might not quote against him.\n\nAs to Briggs, his father made no show of artifice about it. He never would leave him alone. So numerous and severe were the mental trials of that unfortunate youth in vacation time, that the friends of the family (then resident near Bayswater, London) seldom approached the ornamental piece of water in Kensington Gardens, without a vague expectation of seeing Master Briggs’s hat floating on the surface, and an unfinished exercise lying on the bank. Briggs, therefore, was not at all sanguine on the subject of holidays; and these two sharers of little Paul’s bedroom were so fair a sample of the young gentlemen in general, that the most elastic among them contemplated the arrival of those festive periods with genteel resignation.\n\nIt was far otherwise with little Paul. The end of these first holidays was to witness his separation from Florence, but who ever looked forward to the end of holidays whose beginning was not yet come! Not Paul, assuredly. As the happy time drew near, the lions and tigers climbing up the bedroom walls became quite tame and frolicsome. The grim sly faces in the squares and diamonds of the floor-cloth, relaxed and peeped out at him with less wicked eyes. The grave old clock had more of personal interest in the tone of its formal inquiry; and the restless sea went rolling on all night, to the sounding of a melancholy strain—yet it was pleasant too—that rose and fell with the waves, and rocked him, as it were, to sleep.\n\nMr Feeder, B.A., seemed to think that he, too, would enjoy the holidays very much. Mr Toots projected a life of holidays from that time forth; for, as he regularly informed Paul every day, it was his “last half” at Doctor Blimber’s, and he was going to begin to come into his property directly.\n\nIt was perfectly understood between Paul and Mr Toots, that they were intimate friends, notwithstanding their distance in point of years and station. As the vacation approached, and Mr Toots breathed harder and stared oftener in Paul’s society, than he had done before, Paul knew that he meant he was sorry they were going to lose sight of each other, and felt very much obliged to him for his patronage and good opinion.\nIt was even understood by Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, and Miss Blimber, as well as by the young gentlemen in general, that Toots had somehow constituted himself protector and guardian of Dombey, and the circumstance became so notorious, even to Mrs Pipchin, that the good old creature cherished feelings of bitterness and jealousy against Toots; and, in the sanctuary of her own home, repeatedly denounced him as a “chuckle-headed noodle.” Whereas the innocent Toots had no more idea of awakening Mrs Pipchin’s wrath, than he had of any other definite possibility or proposition. On the contrary, he was disposed to consider her rather a remarkable character, with many points of interest about her. For this reason he smiled on her with so much urbanity, and asked her how she did, so often, in the course of her visits to little Paul, that at last she one night told him plainly, she wasn’t used to it, whatever he might think; and she could not, and she would not bear it, either from himself or any other puppy then existing: at which unexpected acknowledgment of his civilities, Mr Toots was so alarmed that he secreted himself in a retired spot until she had gone. Nor did he ever again face the doughty Mrs Pipchin, under Doctor Blimber’s roof.\n\nThey were within two or three weeks of the holidays, when, one day, Cornelia Blimber called Paul into her room, and said, “Dombey, I am going to send home your analysis.”\n\n“Thank you, Ma’am,” returned Paul.\n\n“You know what I mean, do you, Dombey?” inquired Miss Blimber, looking hard at him, through the spectacles.\n\n“No, Ma’am,” said Paul.\n\n“Dombey, Dombey,” said Miss Blimber, “I begin to be afraid you are a sad boy. When you don’t know the meaning of an expression, why don’t you seek for information?”\n\n“Mrs Pipchin told me I wasn’t to ask questions,” returned Paul.\n\n“I must beg you not to mention Mrs Pipchin to me, on any account, Dombey,” returned Miss Blimber. “I couldn’t think of allowing it. The course of study here, is very far removed from anything of that sort. A repetition of such allusions would make it necessary for me to request to hear, without a mistake, before breakfast-time to-morrow morning, from Verbum personale down to simillimia cygno.”\n\n“I didn’t mean, Ma’am—” began little Paul.\n\n“I must trouble you not to tell me that you didn’t mean, if you please, Dombey,” said Miss Blimber, who preserved an awful politeness in her admonitions. “That is a line of argument I couldn’t dream of permitting.”\n\nPaul felt it safest to say nothing at all, so he only looked at Miss Blimber’s spectacles. Miss Blimber having shaken her head at him gravely, referred to a paper lying before her.\n\n“‘Analysis of the character of P. Dombey.’ If my recollection serves me,” said Miss Blimber breaking off, “the word analysis as opposed to synthesis, is thus defined by Walker. ‘The resolution of an object, whether of the senses or of the intellect, into its first elements.’ As opposed to synthesis, you observe. Now you know what analysis is, Dombey.”\n\nDombey didn’t seem to be absolutely blinded by the light let in upon his intellect, but he made Miss Blimber a little bow.\n\n“‘Analysis,’” resumed Miss Blimber, casting her eye over the paper, “‘of the character of P. Dombey.’ I find that the natural capacity of Dombey is extremely good; and that his general disposition to study may be stated in an equal ratio. Thus, taking eight as our standard and highest number, I find these qualities in Dombey stated each at six three-fourths!”\n\nMiss Blimber paused to see how Paul received this news. Being undecided whether six three-fourths meant six pounds fifteen, or sixpence three farthings, or six foot three, or three quarters past six, or six somethings that he hadn’t learnt yet, with three unknown something elses over, Paul rubbed his hands and looked straight at Miss Blimber. It happened to answer as well as anything else he could have done; and Cornelia proceeded.\n\n“‘Violence two. Selfishness two. Inclination to low company, as evinced in the case of a person named Glubb, originally seven, but since reduced. Gentlemanly demeanour four, and improving with advancing years.’ Now what I particularly wish to call your attention to, Dombey, is the general observation at the close of this analysis.”\n\nPaul set himself to follow it with great care.\n\n“‘It may be generally observed of Dombey,’” said Miss Blimber, reading in a loud voice, and at every second word directing her spectacles towards the little figure before her: “‘that his abilities and inclinations are good, and that he has made as much progress as under the circumstances could have been expected. But it is to be lamented of this young gentleman that he is singular (what is usually termed old-fashioned) in his character and conduct, and that, without presenting anything in either which distinctly calls for reprobation, he is often very unlike other young gentlemen of his age and social position.’ Now, Dombey,” said Miss Blimber, laying down the paper, “do you understand that?”\n\n“I think I do, Ma’am,” said Paul.\n\n“This analysis, you see, Dombey,” Miss Blimber continued, “is going to be sent home to your respected parent. It will naturally be very painful to him to find that you are singular in your character and conduct. It is naturally painful to us; for we can’t like you, you know, Dombey, as well as we could wish.”\n\nShe touched the child upon a tender point. He had secretly become more and more solicitous from day to day, as the time of his departure drew more near, that all the house should like him. From some hidden reason, very imperfectly understood by himself—if understood at all—he felt a gradually increasing impulse of affection, towards almost everything and everybody in the place. He could not bear to think that they would be quite indifferent to him when he was gone. He wanted them to remember him kindly; and he had made it his business even to conciliate a great hoarse shaggy dog, chained up at the back of the house, who had previously been the terror of his life: that even he might miss him when he was no longer there.\n\nLittle thinking that in this, he only showed again the difference between himself and his compeers, poor tiny Paul set it forth to Miss Blimber as well as he could, and begged her, in despite of the official analysis, to have the goodness to try and like him. To Mrs Blimber, who had joined them, he preferred the same petition: and when that lady could not forbear, even in his presence, from giving utterance to her often-repeated opinion, that he was an odd child, Paul told her that he was sure she was quite right; that he thought it must be his bones, but he didn’t know; and that he hoped she would overlook it, for he was fond of them all.\n\n“Not so fond,” said Paul, with a mixture of timidity and perfect frankness, which was one of the most peculiar and most engaging qualities of the child, “not so fond as I am of Florence, of course; that could never be. You couldn’t expect that, could you, Ma’am?”\n\n“Oh! the old-fashioned little soul!” cried Mrs Blimber, in a whisper.\n\n“But I like everybody here very much,” pursued Paul, “and I should grieve to go away, and think that anyone was glad that I was gone, or didn’t care.”\n\nMrs Blimber was now quite sure that Paul was the oddest child in the world; and when she told the Doctor what had passed, the Doctor did not controvert his wife’s opinion. But he said, as he had said before, when Paul first came, that study would do much; and he also said, as he had said on that occasion, “Bring him on, Cornelia! Bring him on!”\n\nCornelia had always brought him on as vigorously as she could; and Paul had had a hard life of it. But over and above the getting through his tasks, he had long had another purpose always present to him, and to which he still held fast. It was, to be a gentle, useful, quiet little fellow, always striving to secure the love and attachment of the rest; and though he was yet often to be seen at his old post on the stairs, or watching the waves and clouds from his solitary window, he was oftener found, too, among the other boys, modestly rendering them some little voluntary service. Thus it came to pass, that even among those rigid and absorbed young anchorites, who mortified themselves beneath the roof of Doctor Blimber, Paul was an object of general interest; a fragile little plaything that they all liked, and that no one would have thought of treating roughly. But he could not change his nature, or rewrite the analysis; and so they all agreed that Dombey was old-fashioned.\n\nThere were some immunities, however, attaching to the character enjoyed by no one else. They could have better spared a newer-fashioned child, and that alone was much. When the others only bowed to Doctor Blimber and family on retiring for the night, Paul would stretch out his morsel of a hand, and boldly shake the Doctor’s; also Mrs Blimber’s; also Cornelia’s. If anybody was to be begged off from impending punishment, Paul was always the delegate. The weak-eyed young man himself had once consulted him, in reference to a little breakage of glass and china. And it was darkly rumoured that the butler, regarding him with favour such as that stern man had never shown before to mortal boy, had sometimes mingled porter with his table-beer to make him strong.\n\nOver and above these extensive privileges, Paul had free right of entry to Mr Feeder’s room, from which apartment he had twice led Mr Toots into the open air in a state of faintness, consequent on an unsuccessful attempt to smoke a very blunt cigar: one of a bundle which that young gentleman had covertly purchased on the shingle from a most desperate smuggler, who had acknowledged, in confidence, that two hundred pounds was the price set upon his head, dead or alive, by the Custom House. It was a snug room, Mr Feeder’s, with his bed in another little room inside of it; and a flute, which Mr Feeder couldn’t play yet, but was going to make a point of learning, he said, hanging up over the fireplace. There were some books in it, too, and a fishing-rod; for Mr Feeder said he should certainly make a point of learning to fish, when he could find time. Mr Feeder had amassed, with similar intentions, a beautiful little curly secondhand key-bugle, a chess-board and men, a Spanish Grammar, a set of sketching materials, and a pair of boxing-gloves. The art of self-defence Mr Feeder said he should undoubtedly make a point of learning, as he considered it the duty of every man to do; for it might lead to the protection of a female in distress.\n\nBut Mr Feeder’s great possession was a large green jar of snuff, which Mr Toots had brought down as a present, at the close of the last vacation; and for which he had paid a high price, having been the genuine property of the Prince Regent. Neither Mr Toots nor Mr Feeder could partake of this or any other snuff, even in the most stinted and moderate degree, without being seized with convulsions of sneezing. Nevertheless it was their great delight to moisten a box-full with cold tea, stir it up on a piece of parchment with a paper-knife, and devote themselves to its consumption then and there. In the course of which cramming of their noses, they endured surprising torments with the constancy of martyrs: and, drinking table-beer at intervals, felt all the glories of dissipation.\n\nTo little Paul sitting silent in their company, and by the side of his chief patron, Mr Toots, there was a dread charm in these reckless occasions: and when Mr Feeder spoke of the dark mysteries of London, and told Mr Toots that he was going to observe it himself closely in all its ramifications in the approaching holidays, and for that purpose had made arrangements to board with two old maiden ladies at Peckham, Paul regarded him as if he were the hero of some book of travels or wild adventure, and was almost afraid of such a slashing person.\n\nGoing into this room one evening, when the holidays were very near, Paul found Mr Feeder filling up the blanks in some printed letters, while some others, already filled up and strewn before him, were being folded and sealed by Mr Toots. Mr Feeder said, “Aha, Dombey, there you are, are you?”—for they were always kind to him, and glad to see him—and then said, tossing one of the letters towards him, “And there you are, too, Dombey. That’s yours.”\n\n“Mine, Sir?” said Paul.\n\n“Your invitation,” returned Mr Feeder.\n\nPaul, looking at it, found, in copper-plate print, with the exception of his own name and the date, which were in Mr Feeder’s penmanship, that Doctor and Mrs Blimber requested the pleasure of Mr P. Dombey’s company at an early party on Wednesday Evening the Seventeenth Instant; and that the hour was half-past seven o’clock; and that the object was Quadrilles. Mr Toots also showed him, by holding up a companion sheet of paper, that Doctor and Mrs Blimber requested the pleasure of Mr Toots’s company at an early party on Wednesday Evening the Seventeenth Instant, when the hour was half-past seven o’clock, and when the object was Quadrilles. He also found, on glancing at the table where Mr Feeder sat, that the pleasure of Mr Briggs’s company, and of Mr Tozer’s company, and of every young gentleman’s company, was requested by Doctor and Mrs Blimber on the same genteel Occasion.\n\nMr Feeder then told him, to his great joy, that his sister was invited, and that it was a half-yearly event, and that, as the holidays began that day, he could go away with his sister after the party, if he liked, which Paul interrupted him to say he would like, very much. Mr Feeder then gave him to understand that he would be expected to inform Doctor and Mrs Blimber, in superfine small-hand, that Mr P. Dombey would be happy to have the honour of waiting on them, in accordance with their polite invitation. Lastly, Mr Feeder said, he had better not refer to the festive occasion, in the hearing of Doctor and Mrs Blimber; as these preliminaries, and the whole of the arrangements, were conducted on principles of classicality and high breeding; and that Doctor and Mrs Blimber on the one hand, and the young gentlemen on the other, were supposed, in their scholastic capacities, not to have the least idea of what was in the wind.\n\nPaul thanked Mr Feeder for these hints, and pocketing his invitation, sat down on a stool by the side of Mr Toots, as usual. But Paul’s head, which had long been ailing more or less, and was sometimes very heavy and painful, felt so uneasy that night, that he was obliged to support it on his hand. And yet it dropped so, that by little and little it sunk on Mr Toots’s knee, and rested there, as if it had no care to be ever lifted up again.\n\nThat was no reason why he should be deaf; but he must have been, he thought, for, by and by, he heard Mr Feeder calling in his ear, and gently shaking him to rouse his attention. And when he raised his head, quite scared, and looked about him, he found that Doctor Blimber had come into the room; and that the window was open, and that his forehead was wet with sprinkled water; though how all this had been done without his knowledge, was very curious indeed.\n\n“Ah! Come, come! That’s well! How is my little friend now?” said Doctor Blimber, encouragingly.\n\n“Oh, quite well, thank you, Sir,” said Paul.\n\nBut there seemed to be something the matter with the floor, for he couldn’t stand upon it steadily; and with the walls too, for they were inclined to turn round and round, and could only be stopped by being looked at very hard indeed. Mr Toots’s head had the appearance of being at once bigger and farther off than was quite natural; and when he took Paul in his arms, to carry him upstairs, Paul observed with astonishment that the door was in quite a different place from that in which he had expected to find it, and almost thought, at first, that Mr Toots was going to walk straight up the chimney.\n\nIt was very kind of Mr Toots to carry him to the top of the house so tenderly; and Paul told him that it was. But Mr Toots said he would do a great deal more than that, if he could; and indeed he did more as it was: for he helped Paul to undress, and helped him to bed, in the kindest manner possible, and then sat down by the bedside and chuckled very much; while Mr Feeder, B.A., leaning over the bottom of the bedstead, set all the little bristles on his head bolt upright with his bony hands, and then made believe to spar at Paul with great science, on account of his being all right again, which was so uncommonly facetious, and kind too in Mr Feeder, that Paul, not being able to make up his mind whether it was best to laugh or cry at him, did both at once.\n\nHow Mr Toots melted away, and Mr Feeder changed into Mrs Pipchin, Paul never thought of asking; neither was he at all curious to know; but when he saw Mrs Pipchin standing at the bottom of the bed, instead of Mr Feeder, he cried out, “Mrs Pipchin, don’t tell Florence!”\n\n“Don’t tell Florence what, my little Paul?” said Mrs Pipchin, coming round to the bedside, and sitting down in the chair.\n\n“About me,” said Paul.\n\n“No, no,” said Mrs Pipchin.\n\n“What do you think I mean to do when I grow up, Mrs Pipchin?” inquired Paul, turning his face towards her on his pillow, and resting his chin wistfully on his folded hands.\n\nMrs Pipchin couldn’t guess.\n\n“I mean,” said Paul, “to put my money all together in one Bank, never try to get any more, go away into the country with my darling Florence, have a beautiful garden, fields, and woods, and live there with her all my life!”\n\n“Indeed!” cried Mrs Pipchin.\n\n“Yes,” said Paul. “That’s what I mean to do, when I—” He stopped, and pondered for a moment.\n\nMrs Pipchin’s grey eye scanned his thoughtful face.\n\n“If I grow up,” said Paul. Then he went on immediately to tell Mrs Pipchin all about the party, about Florence’s invitation, about the pride he would have in the admiration that would be felt for her by all the boys, about their being so kind to him and fond of him, about his being so fond of them, and about his being so glad of it. Then he told Mrs Pipchin about the analysis, and about his being certainly old-fashioned, and took Mrs Pipchin’s opinion on that point, and whether she knew why it was, and what it meant. Mrs Pipchin denied the fact altogether, as the shortest way of getting out of the difficulty; but Paul was far from satisfied with that reply, and looked so searchingly at Mrs Pipchin for a truer answer, that she was obliged to get up and look out of the window to avoid his eyes.\n\nThere was a certain calm Apothecary, who attended at the establishment when any of the young gentlemen were ill, and somehow he got into the room and appeared at the bedside, with Mrs Blimber. How they came there, or how long they had been there, Paul didn’t know; but when he saw them, he sat up in bed, and answered all the Apothecary’s questions at full length, and whispered to him that Florence was not to know anything about it, if he pleased, and that he had set his mind upon her coming to the party. He was very chatty with the Apothecary, and they parted excellent friends. Lying down again with his eyes shut, he heard the Apothecary say, out of the room and quite a long way off—or he dreamed it—that there was a want of vital power (what was that, Paul wondered!) and great constitutional weakness. That as the little fellow had set his heart on parting with his school-mates on the seventeenth, it would be better to indulge the fancy if he grew no worse. That he was glad to hear from Mrs Pipchin, that the little fellow would go to his friends in London on the eighteenth. That he would write to Mr Dombey, when he should have gained a better knowledge of the case, and before that day. That there was no immediate cause for—what? Paul lost that word. And that the little fellow had a fine mind, but was an old-fashioned boy.\n\nWhat old fashion could that be, Paul wondered with a palpitating heart, that was so visibly expressed in him; so plainly seen by so many people!\n\nHe could neither make it out, nor trouble himself long with the effort. Mrs Pipchin was again beside him, if she had ever been away (he thought she had gone out with the Doctor, but it was all a dream perhaps), and presently a bottle and glass got into her hands magically, and she poured out the contents for him. After that, he had some real good jelly, which Mrs Blimber brought to him herself; and then he was so well, that Mrs Pipchin went home, at his urgent solicitation, and Briggs and Tozer came to bed. Poor Briggs grumbled terribly about his own analysis, which could hardly have discomposed him more if it had been a chemical process; but he was very good to Paul, and so was Tozer, and so were all the rest, for they every one looked in before going to bed, and said, “How are you now, Dombey?” “Cheer up, little Dombey!” and so forth. After Briggs had got into bed, he lay awake for a long time, still bemoaning his analysis, and saying he knew it was all wrong, and they couldn’t have analysed a murderer worse, and—how would Doctor Blimber like it if his pocket-money depended on it? It was very easy, Briggs said, to make a galley-slave of a boy all the half-year, and then score him up idle; and to crib two dinners a-week out of his board, and then score him up greedy; but that wasn’t going to be submitted to, he believed, was it? Oh! Ah!\n\nBefore the weak-eyed young man performed on the gong next morning, he came upstairs to Paul and told him he was to lie still, which Paul very gladly did. Mrs Pipchin reappeared a little before the Apothecary, and a little after the good young woman whom Paul had seen cleaning the stove on that first morning (how long ago it seemed now!) had brought him his breakfast. There was another consultation a long way off, or else Paul dreamed it again; and then the Apothecary, coming back with Doctor and Mrs Blimber, said:\n\n“Yes, I think, Doctor Blimber, we may release this young gentleman from his books just now; the vacation being so very near at hand.”\n\n“By all means,” said Doctor Blimber. “My love, you will inform Cornelia, if you please.”\n\n“Assuredly,” said Mrs Blimber.\n\nThe Apothecary bending down, looked closely into Paul’s eyes, and felt his head, and his pulse, and his heart, with so much interest and care, that Paul said, “Thank you, Sir.”\n\n“Our little friend,” observed Doctor Blimber, “has never complained.”\n\n“Oh no!” replied the Apothecary. “He was not likely to complain.”\n\n“You find him greatly better?” said Doctor Blimber.\n\n“Oh! he is greatly better, Sir,” returned the Apothecary.\n\nPaul had begun to speculate, in his own odd way, on the subject that might occupy the Apothecary’s mind just at that moment; so musingly had he answered the two questions of Doctor Blimber. But the Apothecary happening to meet his little patient’s eyes, as the latter set off on that mental expedition, and coming instantly out of his abstraction with a cheerful smile, Paul smiled in return and abandoned it.\n\nHe lay in bed all that day, dozing and dreaming, and looking at Mr Toots; but got up on the next, and went downstairs. Lo and behold, there was something the matter with the great clock; and a workman on a pair of steps had taken its face off, and was poking instruments into the works by the light of a candle! This was a great event for Paul, who sat down on the bottom stair, and watched the operation attentively: now and then glancing at the clock face, leaning all askew, against the wall hard by, and feeling a little confused by a suspicion that it was ogling him.\n\nThe workman on the steps was very civil; and as he said, when he observed Paul, “How do you do, Sir?” Paul got into conversation with him, and told him he hadn’t been quite well lately. The ice being thus broken, Paul asked him a multitude of questions about chimes and clocks: as, whether people watched up in the lonely church steeples by night to make them strike, and how the bells were rung when people died, and whether those were different bells from wedding bells, or only sounded dismal in the fancies of the living. Finding that his new acquaintance was not very well informed on the subject of the Curfew Bell of ancient days, Paul gave him an account of that institution; and also asked him, as a practical man, what he thought about King Alfred’s idea of measuring time by the burning of candles; to which the workman replied, that he thought it would be the ruin of the clock trade if it was to come up again. In fine, Paul looked on, until the clock had quite recovered its familiar aspect, and resumed its sedate inquiry; when the workman, putting away his tools in a long basket, bade him good day, and went away. Though not before he had whispered something, on the door-mat, to the footman, in which there was the phrase “old-fashioned”—for Paul heard it.\n\nWhat could that old fashion be, that seemed to make the people sorry! What could it be!\n\nHaving nothing to learn now, he thought of this frequently; though not so often as he might have done, if he had had fewer things to think of. But he had a great many; and was always thinking, all day long.\n\nFirst, there was Florence coming to the party. Florence would see that the boys were fond of him; and that would make her happy. This was his great theme. Let Florence once be sure that they were gentle and good to him, and that he had become a little favourite among them, and then she would always think of the time he had passed there, without being very sorry. Florence might be all the happier too for that, perhaps, when he came back.\n\nWhen he came back! Fifty times a day, his noiseless little feet went up the stairs to his own room, as he collected every book, and scrap, and trifle that belonged to him, and put them all together there, down to the minutest thing, for taking home! There was no shade of coming back on little Paul; no preparation for it, or other reference to it, grew out of anything he thought or did, except this slight one in connexion with his sister. On the contrary, he had to think of everything familiar to him, in his contemplative moods and in his wanderings about the house, as being to be parted with; and hence the many things he had to think of, all day long.\n\nHe had to peep into those rooms upstairs, and think how solitary they would be when he was gone, and wonder through how many silent days, weeks, months, and years, they would continue just as grave and undisturbed. He had to think—would any other child (old-fashioned, like himself) stray there at any time, to whom the same grotesque distortions of pattern and furniture would manifest themselves; and would anybody tell that boy of little Dombey, who had been there once?\nHe had to think of a portrait on the stairs, which always looked earnestly after him as he went away, eyeing it over his shoulder; and which, when he passed it in the company of anyone, still seemed to gaze at him, and not at his companion. He had much to think of, in association with a print that hung up in another place, where, in the centre of a wondering group, one figure that he knew, a figure with a light about its head—benignant, mild, and merciful—stood pointing upward.\n\nAt his own bedroom window, there were crowds of thoughts that mixed with these, and came on, one upon another, like the rolling waves. Where those wild birds lived, that were always hovering out at sea in troubled weather; where the clouds rose and first began; whence the wind issued on its rushing flight, and where it stopped; whether the spot where he and Florence had so often sat, and watched, and talked about these things, could ever be exactly as it used to be without them; whether it could ever be the same to Florence, if he were in some distant place, and she were sitting there alone.\n\nHe had to think, too, of Mr Toots, and Mr Feeder, B.A., of all the boys; and of Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, and Miss Blimber; of home, and of his aunt and Miss Tox; of his father; Dombey and Son, Walter with the poor old Uncle who had got the money he wanted, and that gruff-voiced Captain with the iron hand. Besides all this, he had a number of little visits to pay, in the course of the day; to the schoolroom, to Doctor Blimber’s study, to Mrs Blimber’s private apartment, to Miss Blimber’s, and to the dog. For he was free of the whole house now, to range it as he chose; and, in his desire to part with everybody on affectionate terms, he attended, in his way, to them all. Sometimes he found places in books for Briggs, who was always losing them; sometimes he looked up words in dictionaries for other young gentlemen who were in extremity; sometimes he held skeins of silk for Mrs Blimber to wind; sometimes he put Cornelia’s desk to rights; sometimes he would even creep into the Doctor’s study, and, sitting on the carpet near his learned feet, turn the globes softly, and go round the world, or take a flight among the far-off stars.\n\nIn those days immediately before the holidays, in short, when the other young gentlemen were labouring for dear life through a general resumption of the studies of the whole half-year, Paul was such a privileged pupil as had never been seen in that house before. He could hardly believe it himself; but his liberty lasted from hour to hour, and from day to day; and little Dombey was caressed by everyone. Doctor Blimber was so particular about him, that he requested Johnson to retire from the dinner-table one day, for having thoughtlessly spoken to him as “poor little Dombey;” which Paul thought rather hard and severe, though he had flushed at the moment, and wondered why Johnson should pity him. It was the more questionable justice, Paul thought, in the Doctor, from his having certainly overheard that great authority give his assent on the previous evening, to the proposition (stated by Mrs Blimber) that poor dear little Dombey was more old-fashioned than ever. And now it was that Paul began to think it must surely be old-fashioned to be very thin, and light, and easily tired, and soon disposed to lie down anywhere and rest; for he couldn’t help feeling that these were more and more his habits every day.\n\nAt last the party-day arrived; and Doctor Blimber said at breakfast, “Gentlemen, we will resume our studies on the twenty-fifth of next month.” Mr Toots immediately threw off his allegiance, and put on his ring: and mentioning the Doctor in casual conversation shortly afterwards, spoke of him as “Blimber”! This act of freedom inspired the older pupils with admiration and envy; but the younger spirits were appalled, and seemed to marvel that no beam fell down and crushed him.\nNot the least allusion was made to the ceremonies of the evening, either at breakfast or at dinner; but there was a bustle in the house all day, and in the course of his perambulations, Paul made acquaintance with various strange benches and candlesticks, and met a harp in a green greatcoat standing on the landing outside the drawing-room door. There was something queer, too, about Mrs Blimber’s head at dinner-time, as if she had screwed her hair up too tight; and though Miss Blimber showed a graceful bunch of plaited hair on each temple, she seemed to have her own little curls in paper underneath, and in a play-bill too; for Paul read “Theatre Royal” over one of her sparkling spectacles, and “Brighton” over the other.\n\nThere was a grand array of white waistcoats and cravats in the young gentlemen’s bedrooms as evening approached; and such a smell of singed hair, that Doctor Blimber sent up the footman with his compliments, and wished to know if the house was on fire. But it was only the hairdresser curling the young gentlemen, and over-heating his tongs in the ardour of business.\n\nWhen Paul was dressed—which was very soon done, for he felt unwell and drowsy, and was not able to stand about it very long—he went down into the drawing-room; where he found Doctor Blimber pacing up and down the room full dressed, but with a dignified and unconcerned demeanour, as if he thought it barely possible that one or two people might drop in by and by. Shortly afterwards, Mrs Blimber appeared, looking lovely, Paul thought; and attired in such a number of skirts that it was quite an excursion to walk round her. Miss Blimber came down soon after her Mama; a little squeezed in appearance, but very charming.\n\nMr Toots and Mr Feeder were the next arrivals. Each of these gentlemen brought his hat in his hand, as if he lived somewhere else; and when they were announced by the butler, Doctor Blimber said, “Ay, ay, ay! God bless my soul!” and seemed extremely glad to see them. Mr Toots was one blaze of jewellery and buttons; and he felt the circumstance so strongly, that when he had shaken hands with the Doctor, and had bowed to Mrs Blimber and Miss Blimber, he took Paul aside, and said, “What do you think of this, Dombey?”\n\nBut notwithstanding this modest confidence in himself, Mr Toots appeared to be involved in a good deal of uncertainty whether, on the whole, it was judicious to button the bottom button of his waistcoat, and whether, on a calm revision of all the circumstances, it was best to wear his waistbands turned up or turned down. Observing that Mr Feeder’s were turned up, Mr Toots turned his up; but the waistbands of the next arrival being turned down, Mr Toots turned his down. The differences in point of waistcoat-buttoning, not only at the bottom, but at the top too, became so numerous and complicated as the arrivals thickened, that Mr Toots was continually fingering that article of dress, as if he were performing on some instrument; and appeared to find the incessant execution it demanded, quite bewildering.\n\nAll the young gentlemen, tightly cravatted, curled, and pumped, and with their best hats in their hands, having been at different times announced and introduced, Mr Baps, the dancing-master, came, accompanied by Mrs Baps, to whom Mrs Blimber was extremely kind and condescending. Mr Baps was a very grave gentleman, with a slow and measured manner of speaking; and before he had stood under the lamp five minutes, he began to talk to Toots (who had been silently comparing pumps with him) about what you were to do with your raw materials when they came into your ports in return for your drain of gold. Mr Toots, to whom the question seemed perplexing, suggested “Cook ’em.” But Mr Baps did not appear to think that would do.\n\nPaul now slipped away from the cushioned corner of a sofa, which had been his post of observation, and went downstairs into the tea-room to be ready for Florence, whom he had not seen for nearly a fortnight, as he had remained at Doctor Blimber’s on the previous Saturday and Sunday, lest he should take cold. Presently she came: looking so beautiful in her simple ball dress, with her fresh flowers in her hand, that when she knelt down on the ground to take Paul round the neck and kiss him (for there was no one there, but his friend and another young woman waiting to serve out the tea), he could hardly make up his mind to let her go again, or to take away her bright and loving eyes from his face.\n\n“But what is the matter, Floy?” asked Paul, almost sure that he saw a tear there.\n\n“Nothing, darling; nothing,” returned Florence.\n\nPaul touched her cheek gently with his finger—and it was a tear! “Why, Floy!” said he.\n\n“We’ll go home together, and I’ll nurse you, love,” said Florence.\n\n“Nurse me!” echoed Paul.\n\nPaul couldn’t understand what that had to do with it, nor why the two young women looked on so seriously, nor why Florence turned away her face for a moment, and then turned it back, lighted up again with smiles.\n\n“Floy,” said Paul, holding a ringlet of her dark hair in his hand. “Tell me, dear, Do you think I have grown old-fashioned?”\n\nHis sister laughed, and fondled him, and told him “No.”\n\n“Because I know they say so,” returned Paul, “and I want to know what they mean, Floy.”\n\nBut a loud double knock coming at the door, and Florence hurrying to the table, there was no more said between them. Paul wondered again when he saw his friend whisper to Florence, as if she were comforting her; but a new arrival put that out of his head speedily.\n\nIt was Sir Barnet Skettles, Lady Skettles, and Master Skettles. Master Skettles was to be a new boy after the vacation, and Fame had been busy, in Mr Feeder’s room, with his father, who was in the House of Commons, and of whom Mr Feeder had said that when he did catch the Speaker’s eye (which he had been expected to do for three or four years), it was anticipated that he would rather touch up the Radicals.\n\n“And what room is this now, for instance?” said Lady Skettles to Paul’s friend, “Melia.\n\n“Doctor Blimber’s study, Ma’am,” was the reply.\n\nLady Skettles took a panoramic survey of it through her glass, and said to Sir Barnet Skettles, with a nod of approval, “Very good.” Sir Barnet assented, but Master Skettles looked suspicious and doubtful.\n\n“And this little creature, now,” said Lady Skettles, turning to Paul. “Is he one of the—”\n\n“Young gentlemen, Ma’am; yes, Ma’am,” said Paul’s friend.\n\n“And what is your name, my pale child?” said Lady Skettles.\n\n“Dombey,” answered Paul.\n\nSir Barnet Skettles immediately interposed, and said that he had had the honour of meeting Paul’s father at a public dinner, and that he hoped he was very well. Then Paul heard him say to Lady Skettles, “City—very rich—most respectable—Doctor mentioned it.” And then he said to Paul, “Will you tell your good Papa that Sir Barnet Skettles rejoiced to hear that he was very well, and sent him his best compliments?”\n\n“Yes, Sir,” answered Paul.\n\n“That is my brave boy,” said Sir Barnet Skettles. “Barnet,” to Master Skettles, who was revenging himself for the studies to come, on the plum-cake, “this is a young gentleman you ought to know. This is a young gentleman you may know, Barnet,” said Sir Barnet Skettles, with an emphasis on the permission.\n\n“What eyes! What hair! What a lovely face!” exclaimed Lady Skettles softly, as she looked at Florence through her glass.\n\n“My sister,” said Paul, presenting her.\n\nThe satisfaction of the Skettleses was now complete. And as Lady Skettles had conceived, at first sight, a liking for Paul, they all went upstairs together: Sir Barnet Skettles taking care of Florence, and young Barnet following.\n\nYoung Barnet did not remain long in the background after they had reached the drawing-room, for Dr Blimber had him out in no time, dancing with Florence. He did not appear to Paul to be particularly happy, or particularly anything but sulky, or to care much what he was about; but as Paul heard Lady Skettles say to Mrs Blimber, while she beat time with her fan, that her dear boy was evidently smitten to death by that angel of a child, Miss Dombey, it would seem that Skettles Junior was in a state of bliss, without showing it.\n\nLittle Paul thought it a singular coincidence that nobody had occupied his place among the pillows; and that when he came into the room again, they should all make way for him to go back to it, remembering it was his. Nobody stood before him either, when they observed that he liked to see Florence dancing, but they left the space in front quite clear, so that he might follow her with his eyes. They were so kind, too, even the strangers, of whom there were soon a great many, that they came and spoke to him every now and then, and asked him how he was, and if his head ached, and whether he was tired. He was very much obliged to them for all their kindness and attention, and reclining propped up in his corner, with Mrs Blimber and Lady Skettles on the same sofa, and Florence coming and sitting by his side as soon as every dance was ended, he looked on very happily indeed.\n\nFlorence would have sat by him all night, and would not have danced at all of her own accord, but Paul made her, by telling her how much it pleased him. And he told her the truth, too; for his small heart swelled, and his face glowed, when he saw how much they all admired her, and how she was the beautiful little rosebud of the room.\n\nFrom his nest among the pillows, Paul could see and hear almost everything that passed as if the whole were being done for his amusement. Among other little incidents that he observed, he observed Mr Baps the dancing-master get into conversation with Sir Barnet Skettles, and very soon ask him, as he had asked Mr Toots, what you were to do with your raw materials, when they came into your ports in return for your drain of gold—which was such a mystery to Paul that he was quite desirous to know what ought to be done with them. Sir Barnet Skettles had much to say upon the question, and said it; but it did not appear to solve the question, for Mr Baps retorted, Yes, but supposing Russia stepped in with her tallows; which struck Sir Barnet almost dumb, for he could only shake his head after that, and say, Why then you must fall back upon your cottons, he supposed.\n\nSir Barnet Skettles looked after Mr Baps when he went to cheer up Mrs Baps (who, being quite deserted, was pretending to look over the music-book of the gentleman who played the harp), as if he thought him a remarkable kind of man; and shortly afterwards he said so in those words to Doctor Blimber, and inquired if he might take the liberty of asking who he was, and whether he had ever been in the Board of Trade. Doctor Blimber answered no, he believed not; and that in fact he was a Professor of—”\n\n“Of something connected with statistics, I’ll swear?” observed Sir Barnet Skettles.\n\n“Why no, Sir Barnet,” replied Doctor Blimber, rubbing his chin. “No, not exactly.”\n\n“Figures of some sort, I would venture a bet,” said Sir Barnet Skettles.\n\n“Why yes,” said Doctor Blimber, yes, but not of that sort. Mr Baps is a very worthy sort of man, Sir Barnet, and—in fact he’s our Professor of dancing.”\n\nPaul was amazed to see that this piece of information quite altered Sir Barnet Skettles’s opinion of Mr Baps, and that Sir Barnet flew into a perfect rage, and glowered at Mr Baps over on the other side of the room. He even went so far as to D— Mr Baps to Lady Skettles, in telling her what had happened, and to say that it was like his most con-sum-mate and con-foun-ded impudence.\n\nThere was another thing that Paul observed. Mr Feeder, after imbibing several custard-cups of negus, began to enjoy himself. The dancing in general was ceremonious, and the music rather solemn—a little like church music in fact—but after the custard-cups, Mr Feeder told Mr Toots that he was going to throw a little spirit into the thing. After that, Mr Feeder not only began to dance as if he meant dancing and nothing else, but secretly to stimulate the music to perform wild tunes. Further, he became particular in his attentions to the ladies; and dancing with Miss Blimber, whispered to her—whispered to her!—though not so softly but that Paul heard him say this remarkable poetry,\n\n“Had I a heart for falsehood framed, I ne’er could injure You!”\n\nThis, Paul heard him repeat to four young ladies, in succession. Well might Mr Feeder say to Mr Toots, that he was afraid he should be the worse for it to-morrow!\n\nMrs Blimber was a little alarmed by this—comparatively speaking—profligate behaviour; and especially by the alteration in the character of the music, which, beginning to comprehend low melodies that were popular in the streets, might not unnaturally be supposed to give offence to Lady Skettles. But Lady Skettles was so very kind as to beg Mrs Blimber not to mention it; and to receive her explanation that Mr Feeder’s spirits sometimes betrayed him into excesses on these occasions, with the greatest courtesy and politeness; observing, that he seemed a very nice sort of person for his situation, and that she particularly liked the unassuming style of his hair—which (as already hinted) was about a quarter of an inch long.\n\nOnce, when there was a pause in the dancing, Lady Skettles told Paul that he seemed very fond of music. Paul replied, that he was; and if she was too, she ought to hear his sister, Florence, sing. Lady Skettles presently discovered that she was dying with anxiety to have that gratification; and though Florence was at first very much frightened at being asked to sing before so many people, and begged earnestly to be excused, yet, on Paul calling her to him, and saying, “Do, Floy! Please! For me, my dear!” she went straight to the piano, and began. When they all drew a little away, that Paul might see her; and when he saw her sitting there all alone, so young, and good, and beautiful, and kind to him; and heard her thrilling voice, so natural and sweet, and such a golden link between him and all his life’s love and happiness, rising out of the silence; he turned his face away, and hid his tears. Not, as he told them when they spoke to him, not that the music was too plaintive or too sorrowful, but it was so dear to him.\n\nThey all loved Florence. How could they help it! Paul had known beforehand that they must and would; and sitting in his cushioned corner, with calmly folded hands; and one leg loosely doubled under him, few would have thought what triumph and delight expanded his childish bosom while he watched her, or what a sweet tranquillity he felt. Lavish encomiums on “Dombey’s sister” reached his ears from all the boys: admiration of the self-possessed and modest little beauty was on every lip: reports of her intelligence and accomplishments floated past him, constantly; and, as if borne in upon the air of the summer night, there was a half intelligible sentiment diffused around, referring to Florence and himself, and breathing sympathy for both, that soothed and touched him.\n\nHe did not know why. For all that the child observed, and felt, and thought, that night—the present and the absent; what was then and what had been—were blended like the colours in the rainbow, or in the plumage of rich birds when the sun is shining on them, or in the softening sky when the same sun is setting. The many things he had had to think of lately, passed before him in the music; not as claiming his attention over again, or as likely evermore to occupy it, but as peacefully disposed of and gone. A solitary window, gazed through years ago, looked out upon an ocean, miles and miles away; upon its waters, fancies, busy with him only yesterday, were hushed and lulled to rest like broken waves. The same mysterious murmur he had wondered at, when lying on his couch upon the beach, he thought he still heard sounding through his sister’s song, and through the hum of voices, and the tread of feet, and having some part in the faces flitting by, and even in the heavy gentleness of Mr Toots, who frequently came up to shake him by the hand. Through the universal kindness he still thought he heard it, speaking to him; and even his old-fashioned reputation seemed to be allied to it, he knew not how. Thus little Paul sat musing, listening, looking on, and dreaming; and was very happy.\n\nUntil the time arrived for taking leave: and then, indeed, there was a sensation in the party. Sir Barnet Skettles brought up Skettles Junior to shake hands with him, and asked him if he would remember to tell his good Papa, with his best compliments, that he, Sir Barnet Skettles, had said he hoped the two young gentlemen would become intimately acquainted. Lady Skettles kissed him, and patted his hair upon his brow, and held him in her arms; and even Mrs Baps—poor Mrs Baps! Paul was glad of that—came over from beside the music-book of the gentleman who played the harp, and took leave of him quite as heartily as anybody in the room.\n\n“Good-bye, Doctor Blimber,” said Paul, stretching out his hand.\n\n“Good-bye, my little friend,” returned the Doctor.\n\n“I’m very much obliged to you, Sir,” said Paul, looking innocently up into his awful face. “Ask them to take care of Diogenes, if you please.”\n\nDiogenes was the dog: who had never in his life received a friend into his confidence, before Paul. The Doctor promised that every attention should be paid to Diogenes in Paul’s absence, and Paul having again thanked him, and shaken hands with him, bade adieu to Mrs Blimber and Cornelia with such heartfelt earnestness that Mrs Blimber forgot from that moment to mention Cicero to Lady Skettles, though she had fully intended it all the evening. Cornelia, taking both Paul’s hands in hers, said, “Dombey, Dombey, you have always been my favourite pupil. God bless you!” And it showed, Paul thought, how easily one might do injustice to a person; for Miss Blimber meant it—though she was a Forcer—and felt it.\n\nA buzz then went round among the young gentlemen, of “Dombey’s going!” “Little Dombey’s going!” and there was a general move after Paul and Florence down the staircase and into the hall, in which the whole Blimber family were included. Such a circumstance, Mr Feeder said aloud, as had never happened in the case of any former young gentleman within his experience; but it would be difficult to say if this were\n\nsober fact or custard-cups. The servants, with the butler at their head, had all an interest in seeing Little Dombey go; and even the weak-eyed young man, taking out his books and trunks to the coach that was to carry him and Florence to Mrs Pipchin’s for the night, melted visibly.\n\nNot even the influence of the softer passion on the young gentlemen—and they all, to a boy, doted on Florence—could restrain them from taking quite a noisy leave of Paul; waving hats after him, pressing downstairs to shake hands with him, crying individually “Dombey, don’t forget me!” and indulging in many such ebullitions of feeling, uncommon among those young Chesterfields. Paul whispered Florence, as she wrapped him up before the door was opened, Did she hear them? Would she ever forget it? Was she glad to know it? And a lively delight was in his eyes as he spoke to her.\n\nOnce, for a last look, he turned and gazed upon the faces thus addressed to him, surprised to see how shining and how bright, and numerous they were, and how they were all piled and heaped up, as faces are at crowded theatres. They swam before him as he looked, like faces in an agitated glass; and next moment he was in the dark coach outside, holding close to Florence. From that time, whenever he thought of Doctor Blimber’s, it came back as he had seen it in this last view; and it never seemed to be a real place again, but always a dream, full of eyes.\n\nThis was not quite the last of Doctor Blimber’s, however. There was something else. There was Mr Toots. Who, unexpectedly letting down one of the coach-windows, and looking in, said, with a most egregious chuckle, “Is Dombey there?” and immediately put it up again, without waiting for an answer. Nor was this quite the last of Mr Toots, even; for before the coachman could drive off, he as suddenly let down the other window, and looking in with a precisely similar chuckle, said in a precisely similar tone of voice, “Is Dombey there?” and disappeared precisely as before.\n\nHow Florence laughed! Paul often remembered it, and laughed himself whenever he did so.\n\nBut there was much, soon afterwards—next day, and after that—which Paul could only recollect confusedly. As, why they stayed at Mrs Pipchin’s days and nights, instead of going home; why he lay in bed, with Florence sitting by his side; whether that had been his father in the room, or only a tall shadow on the wall; whether he had heard his doctor say, of someone, that if they had removed him before the occasion on which he had built up fancies, strong in proportion to his own weakness, it was very possible he might have pined away.\n\nHe could not even remember whether he had often said to Florence, “Oh Floy, take me home, and never leave me!” but he thought he had. He fancied sometimes he had heard himself repeating, “Take me home, Floy! take me home!”\n\nBut he could remember, when he got home, and was carried up the well-remembered stairs, that there had been the rumbling of a coach for many hours together, while he lay upon the seat, with Florence still beside him, and old Mrs Pipchin sitting opposite. He remembered his old bed too, when they laid him down in it: his aunt, Miss Tox, and Susan: but there was something else, and recent too, that still perplexed him.\n\n“I want to speak to Florence, if you please,” he said. “To Florence by herself, for a moment!”\n\nShe bent down over him, and the others stood away.\n\n“Floy, my pet, wasn’t that Papa in the hall, when they brought me from the coach?”\n\n“Yes, dear.”\n\n“He didn’t cry, and go into his room, Floy, did he, when he saw me coming in?”\n\nFlorence shook her head, and pressed her lips against his cheek.\n\n“I’m very glad he didn’t cry,” said little Paul. “I thought he did. Don’t tell them that I asked.”\n\nCHAPTER XV. Amazing Artfulness of Captain Cuttle, and a new Pursuit for Walter Gay\nWalter could not, for several days, decide what to do in the Barbados business; and even cherished some faint hope that Mr Dombey might not have meant what he had said, or that he might change his mind, and tell him he was not to go. But as nothing occurred to give this idea (which was sufficiently improbable in itself) any touch of confirmation, and as time was slipping by, and he had none to lose, he felt that he must act, without hesitating any longer.\n\nWalter’s chief difficulty was, how to break the change in his affairs to Uncle Sol, to whom he was sensible it would be a terrible blow. He had the greater difficulty in dashing Uncle Sol’s spirits with such an astounding piece of intelligence, because they had lately recovered very much, and the old man had become so cheerful, that the little back parlour was itself again. Uncle Sol had paid the first appointed portion of the debt to Mr Dombey, and was hopeful of working his way through the rest; and to cast him down afresh, when he had sprung up so manfully from his troubles, was a very distressing necessity.\n\nYet it would never do to run away from him. He must know of it beforehand; and how to tell him was the point. As to the question of going or not going, Walter did not consider that he had any power of choice in the matter. Mr Dombey had truly told him that he was young, and that his Uncle’s circumstances were not good; and Mr Dombey had plainly expressed, in the glance with which he had accompanied that reminder, that if he declined to go he might stay at home if he chose, but not in his counting-house. His Uncle and he lay under a great obligation to Mr Dombey, which was of Walter’s own soliciting. He might have begun in secret to despair of ever winning that gentleman’s favour, and might have thought that he was now and then disposed to put a slight upon him, which was hardly just. But what would have been duty without that, was still duty with it—or Walter thought so—and duty must be done.\n\nWhen Mr Dombey had looked at him, and told him he was young, and that his Uncle’s circumstances were not good, there had been an expression of disdain in his face; a contemptuous and disparaging assumption that he would be quite content to live idly on a reduced old man, which stung the boy’s generous soul. Determined to assure Mr Dombey, in so far as it was possible to give him the assurance without expressing it in words, that indeed he mistook his nature, Walter had been anxious to show even more cheerfulness and activity after the West Indian interview than he had shown before: if that were possible, in one of his quick and zealous disposition. He was too young and inexperienced to think, that possibly this very quality in him was not agreeable to Mr Dombey, and that it was no stepping-stone to his good opinion to be elastic and hopeful of pleasing under the shadow of his powerful displeasure, whether it were right or wrong. But it may have been—it may have been—that the great man thought himself defied in this new exposition of an honest spirit, and purposed to bring it down.\n\n“Well! at last and at least, Uncle Sol must be told,” thought Walter, with a sigh. And as Walter was apprehensive that his voice might perhaps quaver a little, and that his countenance might not be quite as hopeful as he could wish it to be, if he told the old man himself, and saw the first effects of his communication on his wrinkled face, he resolved to avail himself of the services of that powerful mediator, Captain Cuttle. Sunday coming round, he set off therefore, after breakfast, once more to beat up Captain Cuttle’s quarters.\n\nIt was not unpleasant to remember, on the way thither, that Mrs MacStinger resorted to a great distance every Sunday morning, to attend the ministry of the Reverend Melchisedech Howler, who, having been one day discharged from the West India Docks on a false suspicion (got up expressly against him by the general enemy) of screwing gimlets into puncheons, and applying his lips to the orifice, had announced the destruction of the world for that day two years, at ten in the morning, and opened a front parlour for the reception of ladies and gentlemen of the Ranting persuasion, upon whom, on the first occasion of their assemblage, the admonitions of the Reverend Melchisedech had produced so powerful an effect, that, in their rapturous performance of a sacred jig, which closed the service, the whole flock broke through into a kitchen below, and disabled a mangle belonging to one of the fold.\n\nThis the Captain, in a moment of uncommon conviviality, had confided to Walter and his Uncle, between the repetitions of lovely Peg, on the night when Brogley the broker was paid out. The Captain himself was punctual in his attendance at a church in his own neighbourhood, which hoisted the Union Jack every Sunday morning; and where he was good enough—the lawful beadle being infirm—to keep an eye upon the boys, over whom he exercised great power, in virtue of his mysterious hook. Knowing the regularity of the Captain’s habits, Walter made all the haste he could, that he might anticipate his going out; and he made such good speed, that he had the pleasure, on turning into Brig Place, to behold the broad blue coat and waistcoat hanging out of the Captain’s open window, to air in the sun.\n\nIt appeared incredible that the coat and waistcoat could be seen by mortal eyes without the Captain; but he certainly was not in them, otherwise his legs—the houses in Brig Place not being lofty—would have obstructed the street door, which was perfectly clear. Quite wondering at this discovery, Walter gave a single knock.\n\n“Stinger,” he distinctly heard the Captain say, up in his room, as if that were no business of his. Therefore Walter gave two knocks.\n\n“Cuttle,” he heard the Captain say upon that; and immediately afterwards the Captain, in his clean shirt and braces, with his neckerchief hanging loosely round his throat like a coil of rope, and his glazed hat on, appeared at the window, leaning out over the broad blue coat and waistcoat.\n\n“Wal”r!” cried the Captain, looking down upon him in amazement.\n\n“Ay, ay, Captain Cuttle,” returned Walter, “only me”\n\n“What’s the matter, my lad?” inquired the Captain, with great concern. “Gills an’t been and sprung nothing again?”\n\n“No, no,” said Walter. “My Uncle’s all right, Captain Cuttle.”\n\nThe Captain expressed his gratification, and said he would come down below and open the door, which he did.\n\n“Though you’re early, Wal”r,” said the Captain, eyeing him still doubtfully, when they got upstairs:\n\n“Why, the fact is, Captain Cuttle,” said Walter, sitting down, “I was afraid you would have gone out, and I want to benefit by your friendly counsel.”\n\n“So you shall,” said the Captain; “what’ll you take?”\n\n“I want to take your opinion, Captain Cuttle,” returned Walter, smiling. “That’s the only thing for me.”\n\n“Come on then,” said the Captain. “With a will, my lad!”\n\nWalter related to him what had happened; and the difficulty in which he felt respecting his Uncle, and the relief it would be to him if Captain Cuttle, in his kindness, would help him to smooth it away; Captain Cuttle’s infinite consternation and astonishment at the prospect unfolded to him, gradually swallowing that gentleman up, until it left his face quite vacant, and the suit of blue, the glazed hat, and the hook, apparently without an owner.\n\n“You see, Captain Cuttle,” pursued Walter, “for myself, I am young, as Mr Dombey said, and not to be considered. I am to fight my way through the world, I know; but there are two points I was thinking, as I came along, that I should be very particular about, in respect to my Uncle. I don’t mean to say that I deserve to be the pride and delight of his life—you believe me, I know—but I am. Now, don’t you think I am?”\n\nThe Captain seemed to make an endeavour to rise from the depths of his astonishment, and get back to his face; but the effort being ineffectual, the glazed hat merely nodded with a mute, unutterable meaning.\n\n“If I live and have my health,” said Walter, “and I am not afraid of that, still, when I leave England I can hardly hope to see my Uncle again. He is old, Captain Cuttle; and besides, his life is a life of custom—”\n\n“Steady, Wal”r! Of a want of custom?” said the Captain, suddenly reappearing.\n\n“Too true,” returned Walter, shaking his head: “but I meant a life of habit, Captain Cuttle—that sort of custom. And if (as you very truly said, I am sure) he would have died the sooner for the loss of the stock, and all those objects to which he has been accustomed for so many years, don’t you think he might die a little sooner for the loss of—”\n\n“Of his Nevy,” interposed the Captain. “Right!”\n\n“Well then,” said Walter, trying to speak gaily, “we must do our best to make him believe that the separation is but a temporary one, after all; but as I know better, or dread that I know better, Captain Cuttle, and as I have so many reasons for regarding him with affection, and duty, and honour, I am afraid I should make but a very poor hand at that, if I tried to persuade him of it. That’s my great reason for wishing you to break it out to him; and that’s the first point.”\n\n“Keep her off a point or so!” observed the Captain, in a contemplative voice.\n\n“What did you say, Captain Cuttle?” inquired Walter.\n\n“Stand by!” returned the Captain, thoughtfully.\n\nWalter paused to ascertain if the Captain had any particular information to add to this, but as he said no more, went on.\n\n“Now, the second point, Captain Cuttle. I am sorry to say, I am not a favourite with Mr Dombey. I have always tried to do my best, and I have always done it; but he does not like me. He can’t help his likings and dislikings, perhaps. I say nothing of that. I only say that I am certain he does not like me. He does not send me to this post as a good one; he disclaims to represent it as being better than it is; and I doubt very much if it will ever lead me to advancement in the House—whether it does not, on the contrary, dispose of me for ever, and put me out of the way. Now, we must say nothing of this to my Uncle, Captain Cuttle, but must make it out to be as favourable and promising as we can; and when I tell you what it really is, I only do so, that in case any means should ever arise of lending me a hand, so far off, I may have one friend at home who knows my real situation.\n\n“Wal”r, my boy,” replied the Captain, “in the Proverbs of Solomon you will find the following words, ‘May we never want a friend in need, nor a bottle to give him!’ When found, make a note of.”\n\nHere the Captain stretched out his hand to Walter, with an air of downright good faith that spoke volumes; at the same time repeating (for he felt proud of the accuracy and pointed application of his quotation), “When found, make a note of.”\n\n“Captain Cuttle,” said Walter, taking the immense fist extended to him by the Captain in both his hands, which it completely filled, next to my Uncle Sol, I love you. There is no one on earth in whom I can more safely trust, I am sure. As to the mere going away, Captain Cuttle, I don’t care for that; why should I care for that! If I were free to seek my own fortune—if I were free to go as a common sailor—if I were free to venture on my own account to the farthest end of the world—I would gladly go! I would have gladly gone, years ago, and taken my chance of what might come of it. But it was against my Uncle’s wishes, and against the plans he had formed for me; and there was an end of that. But what I feel, Captain Cuttle, is that we have been a little mistaken all along, and that, so far as any improvement in my prospects is concerned, I am no better off now than I was when I first entered Dombey’s House—perhaps a little worse, for the House may have been kindly inclined towards me then, and it certainly is not now.”\n\n“Turn again, Whittington,” muttered the disconsolate Captain, after looking at Walter for some time.\n\n“Ay,” replied Walter, laughing, “and turn a great many times, too, Captain Cuttle, I’m afraid, before such fortune as his ever turns up again. Not that I complain,” he added, in his lively, animated, energetic way. “I have nothing to complain of. I am provided for. I can live. When I leave my Uncle, I leave him to you; and I can leave him to no one better, Captain Cuttle. I haven’t told you all this because I despair, not I; it’s to convince you that I can’t pick and choose in Dombey’s House, and that where I am sent, there I must go, and what I am offered, that I must take. It’s better for my Uncle that I should be sent away; for Mr Dombey is a valuable friend to him, as he proved himself, you know when, Captain Cuttle; and I am persuaded he won’t be less valuable when he hasn’t me there, every day, to awaken his dislike. So hurrah for the West Indies, Captain Cuttle! How does that tune go that the sailors sing?\n\n“For the Port of Barbados, Boys! Cheerily! Leaving old England behind us, Boys! Cheerily!” Here the Captain roared in chorus— “Oh cheerily, cheerily! Oh cheer-i-ly!”\n\nThe last line reaching the quick ears of an ardent skipper not quite sober, who lodged opposite, and who instantly sprung out of bed, threw up his window, and joined in, across the street, at the top of his voice, produced a fine effect. When it was impossible to sustain the concluding note any longer, the skipper bellowed forth a terrific “ahoy!” intended in part as a friendly greeting, and in part to show that he was not at all breathed. That done, he shut down his window, and went to bed again.\n\n“And now, Captain Cuttle,” said Walter, handing him the blue coat and waistcoat, and bustling very much, “if you’ll come and break the news to Uncle Sol (which he ought to have known, days upon days ago, by rights), I’ll leave you at the door, you know, and walk about until the afternoon.”\n\nThe Captain, however, scarcely appeared to relish the commission, or to be by any means confident of his powers of executing it. He had arranged the future life and adventures of Walter so very differently, and so entirely to his own satisfaction; he had felicitated himself so often on the sagacity and foresight displayed in that arrangement, and had found it so complete and perfect in all its parts; that to suffer it to go to pieces all at once, and even to assist in breaking it up, required a great effort of his resolution. The Captain, too, found it difficult to unload his old ideas upon the subject, and to take a perfectly new cargo on board, with that rapidity which the circumstances required, or without jumbling and confounding the two. Consequently, instead of putting on his coat and waistcoat with anything like the impetuosity that could alone have kept pace with Walter’s mood, he declined to invest himself with those garments at all at present; and informed Walter that on such a serious matter, he must be allowed to “bite his nails a bit”.\n\n“It’s an old habit of mine, Wal”r,” said the Captain, “any time these fifty year. When you see Ned Cuttle bite his nails, Wal”r, then you may know that Ned Cuttle’s aground.”\n\nThereupon the Captain put his iron hook between his teeth, as if it were a hand; and with an air of wisdom and profundity that was the very concentration and sublimation of all philosophical reflection and grave inquiry, applied himself to the consideration of the subject in its various branches.\n\n“There’s a friend of mine,” murmured the Captain, in an absent manner, “but he’s at present coasting round to Whitby, that would deliver such an opinion on this subject, or any other that could be named, as would give Parliament six and beat ’em. Been knocked overboard, that man,” said the Captain, “twice, and none the worse for it. Was beat in his apprenticeship, for three weeks (off and on), about the head with a ring-bolt. And yet a clearer-minded man don’t walk.”\n\nIn spite of his respect for Captain Cuttle, Walter could not help inwardly rejoicing at the absence of this sage, and devoutly hoping that his limpid intellect might not be brought to bear on his difficulties until they were quite settled.\n\n“If you was to take and show that man the buoy at the Nore,” said Captain Cuttle in the same tone, “and ask him his opinion of it, Wal”r, he’d give you an opinion that was no more like that buoy than your Uncle’s buttons are. There ain’t a man that walks—certainly not on two legs—that can come near him. Not near him!”\n\n“What’s his name, Captain Cuttle?” inquired Walter, determined to be interested in the Captain’s friend.\n\n“His name’s Bunsby,” said the Captain. “But Lord, it might be anything for the matter of that, with such a mind as his!”\n\nThe exact idea which the Captain attached to this concluding piece of praise, he did not further elucidate; neither did Walter seek to draw it forth. For on his beginning to review, with the vivacity natural to himself and to his situation, the leading points in his own affairs, he soon discovered that the Captain had relapsed into his former profound state of mind; and that while he eyed him steadfastly from beneath his bushy eyebrows, he evidently neither saw nor heard him, but remained immersed in cogitation.\n\nIn fact, Captain Cuttle was labouring with such great designs, that far from being aground, he soon got off into the deepest of water, and could find no bottom to his penetration. By degrees it became perfectly plain to the Captain that there was some mistake here; that it was undoubtedly much more likely to be Walter’s mistake than his; that if there were really any West India scheme afoot, it was a very different one from what Walter, who was young and rash, supposed; and could only be some new device for making his fortune with unusual celerity. “Or if there should be any little hitch between ’em,” thought the Captain, meaning between Walter and Mr Dombey, “it only wants a word in season from a friend of both parties, to set it right and smooth, and make all taut again.” Captain Cuttle’s deduction from these considerations was, that as he already enjoyed the pleasure of knowing Mr Dombey, from having spent a very agreeable half-hour in his company at Brighton (on the morning when they borrowed the money); and that, as a couple of men of the world, who understood each other, and were mutually disposed to make things comfortable, could easily arrange any little difficulty of this sort, and come at the real facts; the friendly thing for him to do would be, without saying anything about it to Walter at present, just to step up to Mr Dombey’s house—say to the servant “Would ye be so good, my lad, as report Cap’en Cuttle here?”—meet Mr Dombey in a confidential spirit—hook him by the button-hole—talk it over—make it all right—and come away triumphant!\n\nAs these reflections presented themselves to the Captain’s mind, and by slow degrees assumed this shape and form, his visage cleared like a doubtful morning when it gives place to a bright noon. His eyebrows, which had been in the highest degree portentous, smoothed their rugged bristling aspect, and became serene; his eyes, which had been nearly closed in the severity of his mental exercise, opened freely; a smile which had been at first but three specks—one at the right-hand corner of his mouth, and one at the corner of each eye—gradually overspread his whole face, and, rippling up into his forehead, lifted the glazed hat: as if that too had been aground with Captain Cuttle, and were now, like him, happily afloat again.\n\nFinally, the Captain left off biting his nails, and said, “Now, Wal”r, my boy, you may help me on with them slops.” By which the Captain meant his coat and waistcoat.\n\nWalter little imagined why the Captain was so particular in the arrangement of his cravat, as to twist the pendent ends into a sort of pigtail, and pass them through a massive gold ring with a picture of a tomb upon it, and a neat iron railing, and a tree, in memory of some deceased friend. Nor why the Captain pulled up his shirt-collar to the utmost limits allowed by the Irish linen below, and by so doing decorated himself with a complete pair of blinkers; nor why he changed his shoes, and put on an unparalleled pair of ankle-jacks, which he only wore on extraordinary occasions. The Captain being at length attired to his own complete satisfaction, and having glanced at himself from head to foot in a shaving-glass which he removed from a nail for that purpose, took up his knotted stick, and said he was ready.\n\nThe Captain’s walk was more complacent than usual when they got out into the street; but this Walter supposed to be the effect of the ankle-jacks, and took little heed of. Before they had gone very far, they encountered a woman selling flowers; when the Captain stopping short, as if struck by a happy idea, made a purchase of the largest bundle in her basket: a most glorious nosegay, fan-shaped, some two feet and a half round, and composed of all the jolliest-looking flowers that blow.\n\nArmed with this little token which he designed for Mr Dombey, Captain Cuttle walked on with Walter until they reached the Instrument-maker’s door, before which they both paused.\n\n“You’re going in?” said Walter.\n\n“Yes,” returned the Captain, who felt that Walter must be got rid of before he proceeded any further, and that he had better time his projected visit somewhat later in the day.\n\n“And you won’t forget anything?”\n\n“No,” returned the Captain.\n\n“I’ll go upon my walk at once,” said Walter, “and then I shall be out of the way, Captain Cuttle.”\n\n“Take a good long “un, my lad!” replied the Captain, calling after him. Walter waved his hand in assent, and went his way.\n\nHis way was nowhere in particular; but he thought he would go out into the fields, where he could reflect upon the unknown life before him, and resting under some tree, ponder quietly. He knew no better fields than those near Hampstead, and no better means of getting at them than by passing Mr Dombey’s house.\n\nIt was as stately and as dark as ever, when he went by and glanced up at its frowning front. The blinds were all pulled down, but the upper windows stood wide open, and the pleasant air stirring those curtains and waving them to and fro was the only sign of animation in the whole exterior. Walter walked softly as he passed, and was glad when he had left the house a door or two behind.\n\nHe looked back then; with the interest he had always felt for the place since the adventure of the lost child, years ago; and looked especially at those upper windows. While he was thus engaged, a chariot drove to the door, and a portly gentleman in black, with a heavy watch-chain, alighted, and went in. When he afterwards remembered this gentleman and his equipage together, Walter had no doubt he was a physician; and then he wondered who was ill; but the discovery did not occur to him until he had walked some distance, thinking listlessly of other things.\n\nThough still, of what the house had suggested to him; for Walter pleased himself with thinking that perhaps the time might come, when the beautiful child who was his old friend and had always been so grateful to him and so glad to see him since, might interest her brother in his behalf and influence his fortunes for the better. He liked to imagine this—more, at that moment, for the pleasure of imagining her continued remembrance of him, than for any worldly profit he might gain: but another and more sober fancy whispered to him that if he were alive then, he would be beyond the sea and forgotten; she married, rich, proud, happy. There was no more reason why she should remember him with any interest in such an altered state of things, than any plaything she ever had. No, not so much.\n\nYet Walter so idealised the pretty child whom he had found wandering in the rough streets, and so identified her with her innocent gratitude of that night and the simplicity and truth of its expression, that he blushed for himself as a libeller when he argued that she could ever grow proud. On the other hand, his meditations were of that fantastic order that it seemed hardly less libellous in him to imagine her grown a woman: to think of her as anything but the same artless, gentle, winning little creature, that she had been in the days of Good Mrs Brown. In a word, Walter found out that to reason with himself about Florence at all, was to become very unreasonable indeed; and that he could do no better than preserve her image in his mind as something precious, unattainable, unchangeable, and indefinite—indefinite in all but its power of giving him pleasure, and restraining him like an angel’s hand from anything unworthy.\n\nIt was a long stroll in the fields that Walter took that day, listening to the birds, and the Sunday bells, and the softened murmur of the town—breathing sweet scents; glancing sometimes at the dim horizon beyond which his voyage and his place of destination lay; then looking round on the green English grass and the home landscape. But he hardly once thought, even of going away, distinctly; and seemed to put off reflection idly, from hour to hour, and from minute to minute, while he yet went on reflecting all the time.\n\nWalter had left the fields behind him, and was plodding homeward in the same abstracted mood, when he heard a shout from a man, and then a woman’s voice calling to him loudly by name. Turning quickly in his surprise, he saw that a hackney-coach, going in the contrary direction, had stopped at no great distance; that the coachman was looking back from his box and making signals to him with his whip; and that a young woman inside was leaning out of the window, and beckoning with immense energy. Running up to this coach, he found that the young woman was Miss Nipper, and that Miss Nipper was in such a flutter as to be almost beside herself.\n\n“Staggs’s Gardens, Mr Walter!” said Miss Nipper; “if you please, oh do!”\n\n“Eh?” cried Walter; “what is the matter?”\n\n“Oh, Mr Walter, Staggs’s Gardens, if you please!” said Susan.\n\n“There!” cried the coachman, appealing to Walter, with a sort of exalting despair; “that’s the way the young lady’s been a goin’ on for up’ards of a mortal hour, and me continivally backing out of no thoroughfares, where she would drive up. I’ve had a many fares in this coach, first and last, but never such a fare as her.”\n\n“Do you want to go to Staggs’s Gardens, Susan?” inquired Walter.\n\n“Ah! She wants to go there! WHERE IS IT?” growled the coachman.\n\n“I don’t know where it is!” exclaimed Susan, wildly. “Mr Walter, I was there once myself, along with Miss Floy and our poor darling Master Paul, on the very day when you found Miss Floy in the City, for we lost her coming home, Mrs Richards and me, and a mad bull, and Mrs Richards’s eldest, and though I went there afterwards, I can’t remember where it is, I think it’s sunk into the ground. Oh, Mr Walter, don’t desert me, Staggs’s Gardens, if you please! Miss Floy’s darling—all our darlings—little, meek, meek Master Paul! Oh Mr Walter!”\n\n“Good God!” cried Walter. “Is he very ill?”\n\n“The pretty flower!” cried Susan, wringing her hands, “has took the fancy that he’d like to see his old nurse, and I’ve come to bring her to his bedside, Mrs Staggs, of Polly Toodle’s Gardens, someone pray!”\n\nGreatly moved by what he heard, and catching Susan’s earnestness immediately, Walter, now that he understood the nature of her errand, dashed into it with such ardour that the coachman had enough to do to follow closely as he ran before, inquiring here and there and everywhere, the way to Staggs’s Gardens.\n\nThere was no such place as Staggs’s Gardens. It had vanished from the earth. Where the old rotten summer-houses once had stood, palaces now reared their heads, and granite columns of gigantic girth opened a vista to the railway world beyond. The miserable waste ground, where the refuse-matter had been heaped of yore, was swallowed up and gone; and in its frowsy stead were tiers of warehouses, crammed with rich goods and costly merchandise. The old by-streets now swarmed with passengers and vehicles of every kind: the new streets that had stopped disheartened in the mud and waggon-ruts, formed towns within themselves, originating wholesome comforts and conveniences belonging to themselves, and never tried nor thought of until they sprung into existence. Bridges that had led to nothing, led to villas, gardens, churches, healthy public walks. The carcasses of houses, and beginnings of new thoroughfares, had started off upon the line at steam’s own speed, and shot away into the country in a monster train.\n\nAs to the neighbourhood which had hesitated to acknowledge the railroad in its straggling days, that had grown wise and penitent, as any Christian might in such a case, and now boasted of its powerful and prosperous relation. There were railway patterns in its drapers’ shops, and railway journals in the windows of its newsmen. There were railway hotels, office-houses, lodging-houses, boarding-houses; railway plans, maps, views, wrappers, bottles, sandwich-boxes, and time-tables; railway hackney-coach and stands; railway omnibuses, railway streets and buildings, railway hangers-on and parasites, and flatterers out of all calculation. There was even railway time observed in clocks, as if the sun itself had given in. Among the vanquished was the master chimney-sweeper, whilom incredulous at Staggs’s Gardens, who now lived in a stuccoed house three stories high, and gave himself out, with golden flourishes upon a varnished board, as contractor for the cleansing of railway chimneys by machinery.\n\nTo and from the heart of this great change, all day and night, throbbing currents rushed and returned incessantly like its life’s blood. Crowds of people and mountains of goods, departing and arriving scores upon scores of times in every four-and-twenty hours, produced a fermentation in the place that was always in action. The very houses seemed disposed to pack up and take trips. Wonderful Members of Parliament, who, little more than twenty years before, had made themselves merry with the wild railroad theories of engineers, and given them the liveliest rubs in cross-examination, went down into the north with their watches in their hands, and sent on messages before by the electric telegraph, to say that they were coming. Night and day the conquering engines rumbled at their distant work, or, advancing smoothly to their journey’s end, and gliding like tame dragons into the allotted corners grooved out to the inch for their reception, stood bubbling and trembling there, making the walls quake, as if they were dilating with the secret knowledge of great powers yet unsuspected in them, and strong purposes not yet achieved.\n\nBut Staggs’s Gardens had been cut up root and branch. Oh woe the day when “not a rood of English ground”—laid out in Staggs’s Gardens—is secure!\n\nAt last, after much fruitless inquiry, Walter, followed by the coach and Susan, found a man who had once resided in that vanished land, and who was no other than the master sweep before referred to, grown stout, and knocking a double knock at his own door. He knowed Toodle, he said, well. Belonged to the Railroad, didn’t he?\n\n“Yes sir, yes!” cried Susan Nipper from the coach window.\n\nWhere did he live now? hastily inquired Walter.\n\nHe lived in the Company’s own Buildings, second turning to the right, down the yard, cross over, and take the second on the right again. It was number eleven; they couldn’t mistake it; but if they did, they had only to ask for Toodle, Engine Fireman, and any one would show them which was his house. At this unexpected stroke of success Susan Nipper dismounted from the coach with all speed, took Walter’s arm, and set off at a breathless pace on foot; leaving the coach there to await their return.\n\n“Has the little boy been long ill, Susan?” inquired Walter, as they hurried on.\n\n“Ailing for a deal of time, but no one knew how much,” said Susan; adding, with excessive sharpness, “Oh, them Blimbers!”\n\n“Blimbers?” echoed Walter.\n\n“I couldn’t forgive myself at such a time as this, Mr Walter,” said Susan, “and when there’s so much serious distress to think about, if I rested hard on anyone, especially on them that little darling Paul speaks well of, but I may wish that the family was set to work in a stony soil to make new roads, and that Miss Blimber went in front, and had the pickaxe!”\n\nMiss Nipper then took breath, and went on faster than before, as if this extraordinary aspiration had relieved her. Walter, who had by this time no breath of his own to spare, hurried along without asking any more questions; and they soon, in their impatience, burst in at a little door and came into a clean parlour full of children.\n\n“Where’s Mrs Richards?” exclaimed Susan Nipper, looking round. “Oh Mrs Richards, Mrs Richards, come along with me, my dear creetur!”\n\n“Why, if it ain’t Susan!” cried Polly, rising with her honest face and motherly figure from among the group, in great surprise.\n\n“Yes, Mrs Richards, it’s me,” said Susan, “and I wish it wasn’t, though I may not seem to flatter when I say so, but little Master Paul is very ill, and told his Pa today that he would like to see the face of his old nurse, and him and Miss Floy hope you’ll come along with me—and Mr Walter, Mrs Richards—forgetting what is past, and do a kindness to the sweet dear that is withering away. Oh, Mrs Richards, withering away!” Susan Nipper crying, Polly shed tears to see her, and to hear what she had said; and all the children gathered round (including numbers of new babies); and Mr Toodle, who had just come home from Birmingham, and was eating his dinner out of a basin, laid down his knife and fork, and put on his wife’s bonnet and shawl for her, which were hanging up behind the door; then tapped her on the back; and said, with more fatherly feeling than eloquence, “Polly! cut away!”\n\nSo they got back to the coach, long before the coachman expected them; and Walter, putting Susan and Mrs Richards inside, took his seat on the box himself that there might be no more mistakes, and deposited them safely in the hall of Mr Dombey’s house—where, by the bye, he saw a mighty nosegay lying, which reminded him of the one Captain Cuttle had purchased in his company that morning. He would have lingered to know more of the young invalid, or waited any length of time to see if he could render the least service; but, painfully sensible that such conduct would be looked upon by Mr Dombey as presumptuous and forward, he turned slowly, sadly, anxiously, away.\n\nHe had not gone five minutes’ walk from the door, when a man came running after him, and begged him to return. Walter retraced his steps as quickly as he could, and entered the gloomy house with a sorrowful foreboding.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI. What the Waves were always saying\n\nPaul had never risen from his little bed. He lay there, listening to the noises in the street, quite tranquilly; not caring much how the time went, but watching it and watching everything about him with observing eyes.\n\nWhen the sunbeams struck into his room through the rustling blinds, and quivered on the opposite wall like golden water, he knew that evening was coming on, and that the sky was red and beautiful. As the reflection died away, and a gloom went creeping up the wall, he watched it deepen, deepen, deepen, into night. Then he thought how the long streets were dotted with lamps, and how the peaceful stars were shining overhead. His fancy had a strange tendency to wander to the river, which he knew was flowing through the great city; and now he thought how black it was, and how deep it would look, reflecting the hosts of stars—and more than all, how steadily it rolled away to meet the sea.\n\nAs it grew later in the night, and footsteps in the street became so rare that he could hear them coming, count them as they passed, and lose them in the hollow distance, he would lie and watch the many-coloured ring about the candle, and wait patiently for day. His only trouble was, the swift and rapid river. He felt forced, sometimes, to try to stop it—to stem it with his childish hands—or choke its way with sand—and when he saw it coming on, resistless, he cried out! But a word from Florence, who was always at his side, restored him to himself; and leaning his poor head upon her breast, he told Floy of his dream, and smiled.\n\nWhen day began to dawn again, he watched for the sun; and when its cheerful light began to sparkle in the room, he pictured to himself—pictured! he saw—the high church towers rising up into the morning sky, the town reviving, waking, starting into life once more, the river glistening as it rolled (but rolling fast as ever), and the country bright with dew. Familiar sounds and cries came by degrees into the street below; the servants in the house were roused and busy; faces looked in at the door, and voices asked his attendants softly how he was. Paul always answered for himself, “I am better. I am a great deal better, thank you! Tell Papa so!”\n\nBy little and little, he got tired of the bustle of the day, the noise of carriages and carts, and people passing and repassing; and would fall asleep, or be troubled with a restless and uneasy sense again—the child could hardly tell whether this were in his sleeping or his waking moments—of that rushing river. “Why, will it never stop, Floy?” he would sometimes ask her. “It is bearing me away, I think!”\n\nBut Floy could always soothe and reassure him; and it was his daily delight to make her lay her head down on his pillow, and take some rest.\n\n“You are always watching me, Floy, let me watch you, now!” They would prop him up with cushions in a corner of his bed, and there he would recline the while she lay beside him: bending forward oftentimes to kiss her, and whispering to those who were near that she was tired, and how she had sat up so many nights beside him.\n\nThus, the flush of the day, in its heat and light, would gradually decline; and again the golden water would be dancing on the wall.\n\nHe was visited by as many as three grave doctors—they used to assemble downstairs, and come up together—and the room was so quiet, and Paul was so observant of them (though he never asked of anybody what they said), that he even knew the difference in the sound of their watches. But his interest centred in Sir Parker Peps, who always took his seat on the side of the bed. For Paul had heard them say long ago, that that gentleman had been with his Mama when she clasped Florence in her arms, and died. And he could not forget it, now. He liked him for it. He was not afraid.\n\nThe people round him changed as unaccountably as on that first night at Doctor Blimber’s—except Florence; Florence never changed—and what had been Sir Parker Peps, was now his father, sitting with his head upon his hand. Old Mrs Pipchin dozing in an easy chair, often changed to Miss Tox, or his aunt; and Paul was quite content to shut his eyes again, and see what happened next, without emotion. But this figure with its head upon its hand returned so often, and remained so long, and sat so still and solemn, never speaking, never being spoken to, and rarely lifting up its face, that Paul began to wonder languidly, if it were real; and in the night-time saw it sitting there, with fear.\n\n“Floy!” he said. “What is that?”\n\n“Where, dearest?”\n\n“There! at the bottom of the bed.”\n\n“There’s nothing there, except Papa!”\n\nThe figure lifted up its head, and rose, and coming to the bedside, said: “My own boy! Don’t you know me?”\n\nPaul looked it in the face, and thought, was this his father? But the face so altered to his thinking, thrilled while he gazed, as if it were in pain; and before he could reach out both his hands to take it between them, and draw it towards him, the figure turned away quickly from the little bed, and went out at the door.\n\nPaul looked at Florence with a fluttering heart, but he knew what she was going to say, and stopped her with his face against her lips. The next time he observed the figure sitting at the bottom of the bed, he called to it.\n\n“Don’t be sorry for me, dear Papa! Indeed I am quite happy!”\n\nHis father coming and bending down to him—which he did quickly, and without first pausing by the bedside—Paul held him round the neck, and repeated those words to him several times, and very earnestly; and Paul never saw him in his room again at any time, whether it were day or night, but he called out, “Don’t be sorry for me! Indeed I am quite happy!” This was the beginning of his always saying in the morning that he was a great deal better, and that they were to tell his father so.\n\nHow many times the golden water danced upon the wall; how many nights the dark, dark river rolled towards the sea in spite of him; Paul never counted, never sought to know. If their kindness, or his sense of it, could have increased, they were more kind, and he more grateful every day; but whether they were many days or few, appeared of little moment now, to the gentle boy.\n\nOne night he had been thinking of his mother, and her picture in the drawing-room downstairs, and thought she must have loved sweet Florence better than his father did, to have held her in her arms when she felt that she was dying—for even he, her brother, who had such dear love for her, could have no greater wish than that. The train of thought suggested to him to inquire if he had ever seen his mother? for he could not remember whether they had told him, yes or no, the river running very fast, and confusing his mind.\n\n“Floy, did I ever see Mama?”\n\n“No, darling, why?”\n\n“Did I ever see any kind face, like Mama’s, looking at me when I was a baby, Floy?”\n\nHe asked, incredulously, as if he had some vision of a face before him.\n\n“Oh yes, dear!”\n\n“Whose, Floy?”\n\n“Your old nurse’s. Often.”\n\n“And where is my old nurse?” said Paul. “Is she dead too? Floy, are we all dead, except you?”\n\nThere was a hurry in the room, for an instant—longer, perhaps; but it seemed no more—then all was still again; and Florence, with her face quite colourless, but smiling, held his head upon her arm. Her arm trembled very much.\n\n“Show me that old nurse, Floy, if you please!”\n\n“She is not here, darling. She shall come to-morrow.”\n\n“Thank you, Floy!”\n\nPaul closed his eyes with those words, and fell asleep. When he awoke, the sun was high, and the broad day was clear and warm. He lay a little, looking at the windows, which were open, and the curtains rustling in the air, and waving to and fro: then he said, “Floy, is it tomorrow? Is she come?”\n\nSomeone seemed to go in quest of her. Perhaps it was Susan. Paul thought he heard her telling him when he had closed his eyes again, that she would soon be back; but he did not open them to see. She kept her word—perhaps she had never been away—but the next thing that happened was a noise of footsteps on the stairs, and then Paul woke—woke mind and body—and sat upright in his bed. He saw them now about him. There was no grey mist before them, as there had been sometimes in the night. He knew them every one, and called them by their names.\n\n“And who is this? Is this my old nurse?” said the child, regarding with a radiant smile, a figure coming in.\n\nYes, yes. No other stranger would have shed those tears at sight of him, and called him her dear boy, her pretty boy, her own poor blighted child. No other woman would have stooped down by his bed, and taken up his wasted hand, and put it to her lips and breast, as one who had some right to fondle it. No other woman would have so forgotten everybody there but him and Floy, and been so full of tenderness and pity.\n\n“Floy! this is a kind good face!” said Paul. “I am glad to see it again. Don’t go away, old nurse! Stay here.”\n\nHis senses were all quickened, and he heard a name he knew.\n\n“Who was that, who said ‘Walter’?” he asked, looking round. “Someone said Walter. Is he here? I should like to see him very much.”\n\nNobody replied directly; but his father soon said to Susan, “Call him back, then: let him come up!” Alter a short pause of expectation, during which he looked with smiling interest and wonder, on his nurse, and saw that she had not forgotten Floy, Walter was brought into the room. His open face and manner, and his cheerful eyes, had always made him a favourite with Paul; and when Paul saw him, he stretched Out his hand, and said “Good-bye!”\n\n“Good-bye, my child!” said Mrs Pipchin, hurrying to his bed’s head. “Not good-bye?”\n\nFor an instant, Paul looked at her with the wistful face with which he had so often gazed upon her in his corner by the fire. “Yes,” he said placidly, “good-bye! Walter dear, good-bye!”—turning his head to where he stood, and putting out his hand again. “Where is Papa?”\n\nHe felt his father’s breath upon his cheek, before the words had parted from his lips.\n\n“Remember Walter, dear Papa,” he whispered, looking in his face. “Remember Walter. I was fond of Walter!” The feeble hand waved in the air, as if it cried “good-bye!” to Walter once again.\n\n“Now lay me down,” he said, “and, Floy, come close to me, and let me see you!”\n\nSister and brother wound their arms around each other, and the golden light came streaming in, and fell upon them, locked together.\n\n“How fast the river runs, between its green banks and the rushes, Floy! But it’s very near the sea. I hear the waves! They always said so!”\n\nPresently he told her the motion of the boat upon the stream was lulling him to rest. How green the banks were now, how bright the flowers growing on them, and how tall the rushes! Now the boat was out at sea, but gliding smoothly on. And now there was a shore before him. Who stood on the bank?—\n\nHe put his hands together, as he had been used to do at his prayers. He did not remove his arms to do it; but they saw him fold them so, behind her neck.\n\n“Mama is like you, Floy. I know her by the face! But tell them that the print upon the stairs at school is not divine enough. The light about the head is shining on me as I go!”\n\nThe golden ripple on the wall came back again, and nothing else stirred in the room. The old, old fashion! The fashion that came in with our first garments, and will last unchanged until our race has run its course, and the wide firmament is rolled up like a scroll. The old, old fashion—Death!\n\nOh thank GOD, all who see it, for that older fashion yet, of Immortality! And look upon us, angels of young children, with regards not quite estranged, when the swift river bears us to the ocean!\n\n“Dear me, dear me! To think,” said Miss Tox, bursting out afresh that night, as if her heart were broken, “that Dombey and Son should be a Daughter after all!”\n\n# CHAPTER XVII. Captain Cuttle does a little Business for the Young People\n\nCaptain Cuttle, in the exercise of that surprising talent for deep-laid and unfathomable scheming, with which (as is not unusual in men of transparent simplicity) he sincerely believed himself to be endowed by nature, had gone to Mr Dombey’s house on the eventful Sunday, winking all the way as a vent for his superfluous sagacity, and had presented himself in the full lustre of the ankle-jacks before the eyes of Towlinson. Hearing from that individual, to his great concern, of the impending calamity, Captain Cuttle, in his delicacy, sheered off again confounded; merely handing in the nosegay as a small mark of his solicitude, and leaving his respectful compliments for the family in general, which he accompanied with an expression of his hope that they would lay their heads well to the wind under existing circumstances, and a friendly intimation that he would “look up again” to-morrow.\n\nThe Captain’s compliments were never heard of any more. The Captain’s nosegay, after lying in the hall all night, was swept into the dust-bin next morning; and the Captain’s sly arrangement, involved in one catastrophe with greater hopes and loftier designs, was crushed to pieces. So, when an avalanche bears down a mountain-forest, twigs and bushes suffer with the trees, and all perish together.\n\nWhen Walter returned home on the Sunday evening from his long walk, and its memorable close, he was too much occupied at first by the tidings he had to give them, and by the emotions naturally awakened in his breast by the scene through which he had passed, to observe either that his Uncle was evidently unacquainted with the intelligence the Captain had undertaken to impart, or that the Captain made signals with his hook, warning him to avoid the subject. Not that the Captain’s signals were calculated to have proved very comprehensible, however attentively observed; for, like those Chinese sages who are said in their conferences to write certain learned words in the air that are wholly impossible of pronunciation, the Captain made such waves and flourishes as nobody without a previous knowledge of his mystery, would have been at all likely to understand.\n\nCaptain Cuttle, however, becoming cognisant of what had happened, relinquished these attempts, as he perceived the slender chance that now existed of his being able to obtain a little easy chat with Mr Dombey before the period of Walter’s departure. But in admitting to himself, with a disappointed and crestfallen countenance, that Sol Gills must be told, and that Walter must go—taking the case for the present as he found it, and not having it enlightened or improved beforehand by the knowing management of a friend—the Captain still felt an unabated confidence that he, Ned Cuttle, was the man for Mr Dombey; and that, to set Walter’s fortunes quite square, nothing was wanted but that they two should come together. For the Captain never could forget how well he and Mr Dombey had got on at Brighton; with what nicety each of them had put in a word when it was wanted; how exactly they had taken one another’s measure; nor how Ned Cuttle had pointed out that resources in the first extremity, and had brought the interview to the desired termination. On all these grounds the Captain soothed himself with thinking that though Ned Cuttle was forced by the pressure of events to “stand by” almost useless for the present, Ned would fetch up with a wet sail in good time, and carry all before him.\n\nUnder the influence of this good-natured delusion, Captain Cuttle even went so far as to revolve in his own bosom, while he sat looking at Walter and listening with a tear on his shirt-collar to what he related, whether it might not be at once genteel and politic to give Mr Dombey a verbal invitation, whenever they should meet, to come and cut his mutton in Brig Place on some day of his own naming, and enter on the question of his young friend’s prospects over a social glass. But the uncertain temper of Mrs MacStinger, and the possibility of her setting up her rest in the passage during such an entertainment, and there delivering some homily of an uncomplimentary nature, operated as a check on the Captain’s hospitable thoughts, and rendered him timid of giving them encouragement.\n\nOne fact was quite clear to the Captain, as Walter, sitting thoughtfully over his untasted dinner, dwelt on all that had happened; namely, that however Walter’s modesty might stand in the way of his perceiving it himself, he was, as one might say, a member of Mr Dombey’s family. He had been, in his own person, connected with the incident he so pathetically described; he had been by name remembered and commended in close association with it; and his fortunes must have a particular interest in his employer’s eyes. If the Captain had any lurking doubt whatever of his own conclusions, he had not the least doubt that they were good conclusions for the peace of mind of the Instrument-maker. Therefore he availed himself of so favourable a moment for breaking the West Indian intelligence to his friend, as a piece of extraordinary preferment; declaring that for his part he would freely give a hundred thousand pounds (if he had it) for Walter’s gain in the long-run, and that he had no doubt such an investment would yield a handsome premium.\n\nSolomon Gills was at first stunned by the communication, which fell upon the little back-parlour like a thunderbolt, and tore up the hearth savagely. But the Captain flashed such golden prospects before his dim sight: hinted so mysteriously at Whittingtonian consequences; laid such emphasis on what Walter had just now told them: and appealed to it so confidently as a corroboration of his predictions, and a great advance towards the realisation of the romantic legend of Lovely Peg: that he bewildered the old man. Walter, for his part, feigned to be so full of hope and ardour, and so sure of coming home again soon, and backed up the Captain with such expressive shakings of his head and rubbings of his hands, that Solomon, looking first at him then at Captain Cuttle, began to think he ought to be transported with joy.\n\n“But I’m behind the time, you understand,” he observed in apology, passing his hand nervously down the whole row of bright buttons on his coat, and then up again, as if they were beads and he were telling them twice over: “and I would rather have my dear boy here. It’s an old-fashioned notion, I daresay. He was always fond of the sea He’s”—and he looked wistfully at Walter—“he’s glad to go.”\n\n“Uncle Sol!” cried Walter, quickly, “if you say that, I won’t go. No, Captain Cuttle, I won’t. If my Uncle thinks I could be glad to leave him, though I was going to be made Governor of all the Islands in the West Indies, that’s enough. I’m a fixture.”\n\n“Wal”r, my lad,” said the Captain. “Steady! Sol Gills, take an observation of your nevy.”\n\nFollowing with his eyes the majestic action of the Captain’s hook, the old man looked at Walter.\n\n“Here is a certain craft,” said the Captain, with a magnificent sense of the allegory into which he was soaring, “a-going to put out on a certain voyage. What name is wrote upon that craft indelibly? Is it The Gay? or,” said the Captain, raising his voice as much as to say, observe the point of this, “is it The Gills?”\n\n“Ned,” said the old man, drawing Walter to his side, and taking his arm tenderly through his, “I know. I know. Of course I know that Wally considers me more than himself always. That’s in my mind. When I say he is glad to go, I mean I hope he is. Eh? look you, Ned and you too, Wally, my dear, this is new and unexpected to me; and I’m afraid my being behind the time, and poor, is at the bottom of it. Is it really good fortune for him, do you tell me, now?” said the old man, looking anxiously from one to the other. “Really and truly? Is it? I can reconcile myself to almost anything that advances Wally, but I won’t have Wally putting himself at any disadvantage for me, or keeping anything from me. You, Ned Cuttle!” said the old man, fastening on the Captain, to the manifest confusion of that diplomatist; “are you dealing plainly by your old friend? Speak out, Ned Cuttle. Is there anything behind? Ought he to go? How do you know it first, and why?”\n\nAs it was a contest of affection and self-denial, Walter struck in with infinite effect, to the Captain’s relief; and between them they tolerably reconciled old Sol Gills, by continued talking, to the project; or rather so confused him, that nothing, not even the pain of separation, was distinctly clear to his mind.\n\nHe had not much time to balance the matter; for on the very next day, Walter received from Mr Carker the Manager, the necessary credentials for his passage and outfit, together with the information that the Son and Heir would sail in a fortnight, or within a day or two afterwards at latest. In the hurry of preparation: which Walter purposely enhanced as much as possible: the old man lost what little self-possession he ever had; and so the time of departure drew on rapidly.\n\nThe Captain, who did not fail to make himself acquainted with all that passed, through inquiries of Walter from day to day, found the time still tending on towards his going away, without any occasion offering itself, or seeming likely to offer itself, for a better understanding of his position. It was after much consideration of this fact, and much pondering over such an unfortunate combination of circumstances, that a bright idea occurred to the Captain. Suppose he made a call on Mr Carker, and tried to find out from him how the land really lay!\n\nCaptain Cuttle liked this idea very much. It came upon him in a moment of inspiration, as he was smoking an early pipe in Brig Place after breakfast; and it was worthy of the tobacco. It would quiet his conscience, which was an honest one, and was made a little uneasy by what Walter had confided to him, and what Sol Gills had said; and it would be a deep, shrewd act of friendship. He would sound Mr Carker carefully, and say much or little, just as he read that gentleman’s character, and discovered that they got on well together or the reverse.\n\nAccordingly, without the fear of Walter before his eyes (who he knew was at home packing), Captain Cuttle again assumed his ankle-jacks and mourning brooch, and issued forth on this second expedition. He purchased no propitiatory nosegay on the present occasion, as he was going to a place of business; but he put a small sunflower in his button-hole to give himself an agreeable relish of the country; and with this, and the knobby stick, and the glazed hat, bore down upon the offices of Dombey and Son.\n\nAfter taking a glass of warm rum-and-water at a tavern close by, to collect his thoughts, the Captain made a rush down the court, lest its good effects should evaporate, and appeared suddenly to Mr Perch.\n\n“Matey,” said the Captain, in persuasive accents. “One of your Governors is named Carker.”\n\nMr Perch admitted it; but gave him to understand, as in official duty bound, that all his Governors were engaged, and never expected to be disengaged any more.\n\n“Look’ee here, mate,” said the Captain in his ear; “my name’s Cap’en Cuttle.”\n\nThe Captain would have hooked Perch gently to him, but Mr Perch eluded the attempt; not so much in design, as in starting at the sudden thought that such a weapon unexpectedly exhibited to Mrs Perch might, in her then condition, be destructive to that lady’s hopes.\n\n“If you’ll be so good as just report Cap’en Cuttle here, when you get a chance,” said the Captain, “I’ll wait.”\n\nSaying which, the Captain took his seat on Mr Perch’s bracket, and drawing out his handkerchief from the crown of the glazed hat which he jammed between his knees (without injury to its shape, for nothing human could bend it), rubbed his head well all over, and appeared refreshed. He subsequently arranged his hair with his hook, and sat looking round the office, contemplating the clerks with a serene respect.\n\nThe Captain’s equanimity was so impenetrable, and he was altogether so mysterious a being, that Perch the messenger was daunted.\n\n“What name was it you said?” asked Mr Perch, bending down over him as he sat on the bracket.\n\n“Cap’en,” in a deep hoarse whisper.\n\n“Yes,” said Mr Perch, keeping time with his head.\n\n“Cuttle.”\n\n“Oh!” said Mr Perch, in the same tone, for he caught it, and couldn’t help it; the Captain, in his diplomacy, was so impressive. “I’ll see if he’s disengaged now. I don’t know. Perhaps he may be for a minute.”\n\n“Ay, ay, my lad, I won’t detain him longer than a minute,” said the Captain, nodding with all the weighty importance that he felt within him. Perch, soon returning, said, “Will Captain Cuttle walk this way?”\n\nMr Carker the Manager, standing on the hearth-rug before the empty fireplace, which was ornamented with a castellated sheet of brown paper, looked at the Captain as he came in, with no very special encouragement.\n\n“Mr Carker?” said Captain Cuttle.\n\n“I believe so,” said Mr Carker, showing all his teeth.\n\nThe Captain liked his answering with a smile; it looked pleasant. “You see,” began the Captain, rolling his eyes slowly round the little room, and taking in as much of it as his shirt-collar permitted; “I’m a seafaring man myself, Mr Carker, and Wal”r, as is on your books here, is almost a son of mine.”\n\n“Walter Gay?” said Mr Carker, showing all his teeth again.\n\n“Wal”r Gay it is,” replied the Captain, “right!” The Captain’s manner expressed a warm approval of Mr Carker’s quickness of perception. “I’m a intimate friend of his and his Uncle’s. Perhaps,” said the Captain, “you may have heard your head Governor mention my name?—Captain Cuttle.”\n\n“No!” said Mr Carker, with a still wider demonstration than before.\n\n“Well,” resumed the Captain, “I’ve the pleasure of his acquaintance. I waited upon him down on the Sussex coast there, with my young friend Wal”r, when—in short, when there was a little accommodation wanted.” The Captain nodded his head in a manner that was at once comfortable, easy, and expressive. “You remember, I daresay?”\n\n“I think,” said Mr Carker, “I had the honour of arranging the business.”\n\n“To be sure!” returned the Captain. “Right again! you had. Now I’ve took the liberty of coming here—\n\n“Won’t you sit down?” said Mr Carker, smiling.\n\n“Thank’ee,” returned the Captain, availing himself of the offer. “A man does get more way upon himself, perhaps, in his conversation, when he sits down. Won’t you take a cheer yourself?”\n\n“No thank you,” said the Manager, standing, perhaps from the force of winter habit, with his back against the chimney-piece, and looking down upon the Captain with an eye in every tooth and gum. “You have taken the liberty, you were going to say—though it’s none—”\n\n“Thank’ee kindly, my lad,” returned the Captain: “of coming here, on account of my friend Wal”r. Sol Gills, his Uncle, is a man of science, and in science he may be considered a clipper; but he ain’t what I should altogether call a able seaman—not man of practice. Wal”r is as trim a lad as ever stepped; but he’s a little down by the head in one respect, and that is, modesty. Now what I should wish to put to you,” said the Captain, lowering his voice, and speaking in a kind of confidential growl, “in a friendly way, entirely between you and me, and for my own private reckoning, “till your head Governor has wore round a bit, and I can come alongside of him, is this.—Is everything right and comfortable here, and is Wal”r out’ard bound with a pretty fair wind?”\n\n“What do you think now, Captain Cuttle?” returned Carker, gathering up his skirts and settling himself in his position. “You are a practical man; what do you think?”\n\nThe acuteness and the significance of the Captain’s eye as he cocked it in reply, no words short of those unutterable Chinese words before referred to could describe.\n\n“Come!” said the Captain, unspeakably encouraged, “what do you say? Am I right or wrong?”\n\nSo much had the Captain expressed in his eye, emboldened and incited by Mr Carker’s smiling urbanity, that he felt himself in as fair a condition to put the question, as if he had expressed his sentiments with the utmost elaboration.\n\n“Right,” said Mr Carker, “I have no doubt.”\n\n“Out’ard bound with fair weather, then, I say,” cried Captain Cuttle.\n\nMr Carker smiled assent.\n\n“Wind right astarn, and plenty of it,” pursued the Captain.\n\nMr Carker smiled assent again.\n\n“Ay, ay!” said Captain Cuttle, greatly relieved and pleased. “I know’d how she headed, well enough; I told Wal”r so. Thank’ee, thank’ee.”\n\n“Gay has brilliant prospects,” observed Mr Carker, stretching his mouth wider yet: “all the world before him.”\n\n“All the world and his wife too, as the saying is,” returned the delighted Captain.\n\nAt the word “wife” (which he had uttered without design), the Captain stopped, cocked his eye again, and putting the glazed hat on the top of the knobby stick, gave it a twirl, and looked sideways at his always smiling friend.\n\n“I’d bet a gill of old Jamaica,” said the Captain, eyeing him attentively, “that I know what you’re a smiling at.”\n\nMr Carker took his cue, and smiled the more.\n\n“It goes no farther?” said the Captain, making a poke at the door with the knobby stick to assure himself that it was shut.\n\n“Not an inch,” said Mr Carker.\n\n“You’re thinking of a capital F perhaps?” said the Captain.\n\nMr Carker didn’t deny it.\n\n“Anything about a L,” said the Captain, “or a O?”\n\nMr Carker still smiled.\n\n“Am I right, again?” inquired the Captain in a whisper, with the scarlet circle on his forehead swelling in his triumphant joy.\n\nMr Carker, in reply, still smiling, and now nodding assent, Captain Cuttle rose and squeezed him by the hand, assuring him, warmly, that they were on the same tack, and that as for him (Cuttle) he had laid his course that way all along. “He know’d her first,” said the Captain, with all the secrecy and gravity that the subject demanded, “in an uncommon manner—you remember his finding her in the street when she was a’most a babby—he has liked her ever since, and she him, as much as two youngsters can. We’ve always said, Sol Gills and me, that they was cut out for each other.”\n\nA cat, or a monkey, or a hyena, or a death’s-head, could not have shown the Captain more teeth at one time, than Mr Carker showed him at this period of their interview.\n\n“There’s a general indraught that way,” observed the happy Captain. “Wind and water sets in that direction, you see. Look at his being present t’other day!”\n\n“Most favourable to his hopes,” said Mr Carker.\n\n“Look at his being towed along in the wake of that day!” pursued the Captain. “Why what can cut him adrift now?”\n\n“Nothing,” replied Mr Carker.\n\n“You’re right again,” returned the Captain, giving his hand another squeeze. “Nothing it is. So! steady! There’s a son gone: pretty little creetur. Ain’t there?”\n\n“Yes, there’s a son gone,” said the acquiescent Carker.\n\n“Pass the word, and there’s another ready for you,” quoth the Captain. “Nevy of a scientific Uncle! Nevy of Sol Gills! Wal”r! Wal”r, as is already in your business! And”—said the Captain, rising gradually to a quotation he was preparing for a final burst, “who—comes from Sol Gills’s daily, to your business, and your buzzums.”\n\nThe Captain’s complacency as he gently jogged Mr Carker with his elbow, on concluding each of the foregoing short sentences, could be surpassed by nothing but the exultation with which he fell back and eyed him when he had finished this brilliant display of eloquence and sagacity; his great blue waistcoat heaving with the throes of such a masterpiece, and his nose in a state of violent inflammation from the same cause.\n\n“Am I right?” said the Captain.\n\n“Captain Cuttle,” said Mr Carker, bending down at the knees, for a moment, in an odd manner, as if he were falling together to hug the whole of himself at once, “your views in reference to Walter Gay are thoroughly and accurately right. I understand that we speak together in confidence.\n\n“Honour!” interposed the Captain. “Not a word.”\n\n“To him or anyone?” pursued the Manager.\n\nCaptain Cuttle frowned and shook his head.\n\n“But merely for your own satisfaction and guidance—and guidance, of course,” repeated Mr Carker, “with a view to your future proceedings.”\n\n“Thank’ee kindly, I am sure,” said the Captain, listening with great attention.\n\n“I have no hesitation in saying, that’s the fact. You have hit the probabilities exactly.”\n\n“And with regard to your head Governor,” said the Captain, “why an interview had better come about nat’ral between us. There’s time enough.”\n\nMr Carker, with his mouth from ear to ear, repeated, “Time enough.” Not articulating the words, but bowing his head affably, and forming them with his tongue and lips.\n\n“And as I know—it’s what I always said—that Wal”r’s in a way to make his fortune,” said the Captain.\n\n“To make his fortune,” Mr Carker repeated, in the same dumb manner.\n\n“And as Wal”r’s going on this little voyage is, as I may say, in his day’s work, and a part of his general expectations here,” said the Captain.\n\n“Of his general expectations here,” assented Mr Carker, dumbly as before.\n\n“Why, so long as I know that,” pursued the Captain, “there’s no hurry, and my mind’s at ease.\n\nMr Carker still blandly assenting in the same voiceless manner, Captain Cuttle was strongly confirmed in his opinion that he was one of the most agreeable men he had ever met, and that even Mr Dombey might improve himself on such a model. With great heartiness, therefore, the Captain once again extended his enormous hand (not unlike an old block in colour), and gave him a grip that left upon his smoother flesh a proof impression of the chinks and crevices with which the Captain’s palm was liberally tattooed.\n\n“Farewell!” said the Captain. “I ain’t a man of many words, but I take it very kind of you to be so friendly, and above-board. You’ll excuse me if I’ve been at all intruding, will you?” said the Captain.\n\n“Not at all,” returned the other.\n\n“Thank’ee. My berth ain’t very roomy,” said the Captain, turning back again, “but it’s tolerably snug; and if you was to find yourself near Brig Place, number nine, at any time—will you make a note of it?—and would come upstairs, without minding what was said by the person at the door, I should be proud to see you.\n\nWith that hospitable invitation, the Captain said “Good day!” and walked out and shut the door; leaving Mr Carker still reclining against the chimney-piece. In whose sly look and watchful manner; in whose false mouth, stretched but not laughing; in whose spotless cravat and very whiskers; even in whose silent passing of his soft hand over his white linen and his smooth face; there was something desperately cat-like.\n\nThe unconscious Captain walked out in a state of self-glorification that imparted quite a new cut to the broad blue suit. “Stand by, Ned!” said the Captain to himself. “You’ve done a little business for the youngsters today, my lad!”\n\nIn his exultation, and in his familiarity, present and prospective, with the House, the Captain, when he reached the outer office, could not refrain from rallying Mr Perch a little, and asking him whether he thought everybody was still engaged. But not to be bitter on a man who had done his duty, the Captain whispered in his ear, that if he felt disposed for a glass of rum-and-water, and would follow, he would be happy to bestow the same upon him.\n\nBefore leaving the premises, the Captain, somewhat to the astonishment of the clerks, looked round from a central point of view, and took a general survey of the officers part and parcel of a project in which his young friend was nearly interested. The strong-room excited his especial admiration; but, that he might not appear too particular, he limited himself to an approving glance, and, with a graceful recognition of the clerks as a body, that was full of politeness and patronage, passed out into the court. Being promptly joined by Mr Perch, he conveyed that gentleman to the tavern, and fulfilled his pledge—hastily, for Perch’s time was precious.\n\n“I’ll give you for a toast,” said the Captain, “Wal”r!”\n\n“Who?” submitted Mr Perch.\n\n“Wal”r!” repeated the Captain, in a voice of thunder.\n\nMr Perch, who seemed to remember having heard in infancy that there was once a poet of that name, made no objection; but he was much astonished at the Captain’s coming into the City to propose a poet; indeed, if he had proposed to put a poet’s statue up—say Shakespeare’s for example—in a civic thoroughfare, he could hardly have done a greater outrage to Mr Perch’s experience. On the whole, he was such a mysterious and incomprehensible character, that Mr Perch decided not to mention him to Mrs Perch at all, in case of giving rise to any disagreeable consequences.\n\nMysterious and incomprehensible, the Captain, with that lively sense upon him of having done a little business for the youngsters, remained all day, even to his most intimate friends; and but that Walter attributed his winks and grins, and other such pantomimic reliefs of himself, to his satisfaction in the success of their innocent deception upon old Sol Gills, he would assuredly have betrayed himself before night. As it was, however, he kept his own secret; and went home late from the Instrument-maker’s house, wearing the glazed hat so much on one side, and carrying such a beaming expression in his eyes, that Mrs MacStinger (who might have been brought up at Doctor Blimber’s, she was such a Roman matron) fortified herself, at the first glimpse of him, behind the open street door, and refused to come out to the contemplation of her blessed infants, until he was securely lodged in his own room.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII. Father and Daughter\n\nThere is a hush through Mr Dombey’s house. Servants gliding up and down stairs rustle, but make no sound of footsteps. They talk together constantly, and sit long at meals, making much of their meat and drink, and enjoying themselves after a grim unholy fashion. Mrs Wickam, with her eyes suffused with tears, relates melancholy anecdotes; and tells them how she always said at Mrs Pipchin’s that it would be so, and takes more table-ale than usual, and is very sorry but sociable. Cook’s state of mind is similar. She promises a little fry for supper, and struggles about equally against her feelings and the onions. Towlinson begins to think there’s a fate in it, and wants to know if anybody can tell him of any good that ever came of living in a corner house. It seems to all of them as having happened a long time ago; though yet the child lies, calm and beautiful, upon his little bed.\n\nAfter dark there come some visitors—noiseless visitors, with shoes of felt—who have been there before; and with them comes that bed of rest which is so strange a one for infant sleepers. All this time, the bereaved father has not been seen even by his attendant; for he sits in an inner corner of his own dark room when anyone is there, and never seems to move at other times, except to pace it to and fro. But in the morning it is whispered among the household that he was heard to go upstairs in the dead night, and that he stayed there—in the room—until the sun was shining.\n\nAt the offices in the City, the ground-glass windows are made more dim by shutters; and while the lighted lamps upon the desks are half extinguished by the day that wanders in, the day is half extinguished by the lamps, and an unusual gloom prevails. There is not much business done. The clerks are indisposed to work; and they make assignations to eat chops in the afternoon, and go up the river. Perch, the messenger, stays long upon his errands; and finds himself in bars of public-houses, invited thither by friends, and holding forth on the uncertainty of human affairs. He goes home to Ball’s Pond earlier in the evening than usual, and treats Mrs Perch to a veal cutlet and Scotch ale. Mr Carker the Manager treats no one; neither is he treated; but alone in his own room he shows his teeth all day; and it would seem that there is something gone from Mr Carker’s path—some obstacle removed—which clears his way before him.\n\nNow the rosy children living opposite to Mr Dombey’s house, peep from their nursery windows down into the street; for there are four black horses at his door, with feathers on their heads; and feathers tremble on the carriage that they draw; and these, and an array of men with scarves and staves, attract a crowd. The juggler who was going to twirl the basin, puts his loose coat on again over his fine dress; and his trudging wife, one-sided with her heavy baby in her arms, loiters to see the company come out. But closer to her dingy breast she presses her baby, when the burden that is so easily carried is borne forth; and the youngest of the rosy children at the high window opposite, needs no restraining hand to check her in her glee, when, pointing with her dimpled finger, she looks into her nurse’s face, and asks “What’s that?”\n\nAnd now, among the knot of servants dressed in mourning, and the weeping women, Mr Dombey passes through the hall to the other carriage that is waiting to receive him. He is not “brought down,” these observers think, by sorrow and distress of mind. His walk is as erect, his bearing is as stiff as ever it has been. He hides his face behind no handkerchief, and looks before him. But that his face is something sunk and rigid, and is pale, it bears the same expression as of old. He takes his place within the carriage, and three other gentlemen follow. Then the grand funeral moves slowly down the street. The feathers are yet nodding in the distance, when the juggler has the basin spinning on a cane, and has the same crowd to admire it. But the juggler’s wife is less alert than usual with the money-box, for a child’s burial has set her thinking that perhaps the baby underneath her shabby shawl may not grow up to be a man, and wear a sky-blue fillet round his head, and salmon-coloured worsted drawers, and tumble in the mud.\n\nThe feathers wind their gloomy way along the streets, and come within the sound of a church bell. In this same church, the pretty boy received all that will soon be left of him on earth—a name. All of him that is dead, they lay there, near the perishable substance of his mother. It is well. Their ashes lie where Florence in her walks—oh lonely, lonely walks!—may pass them any day.\n\nThe service over, and the clergyman withdrawn, Mr Dombey looks round, demanding in a low voice, whether the person who has been requested to attend to receive instructions for the tablet, is there?\n\nSomeone comes forward, and says “Yes.”\n\nMr Dombey intimates where he would have it placed; and shows him, with his hand upon the wall, the shape and size; and how it is to follow the memorial to the mother. Then, with his pencil, he writes out the inscription, and gives it to him: adding, “I wish to have it done at once.\n\n“It shall be done immediately, Sir.”\n\n“There is really nothing to inscribe but name and age, you see.”\n\nThe man bows, glancing at the paper, but appears to hesitate. Mr Dombey not observing his hesitation, turns away, and leads towards the porch.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Sir;” a touch falls gently on his mourning cloak; “but as you wish it done immediately, and it may be put in hand when I get back—”\n\n“Well?”\n“Will you be so good as read it over again? I think there’s a mistake.”\n\n“Where?”\n\nThe statuary gives him back the paper, and points out, with his pocket rule, the words, “beloved and only child.”\n\n“It should be, ‘son,’ I think, Sir?”\n\n“You are right. Of course. Make the correction.”\n\nThe father, with a hastier step, pursues his way to the coach. When the other three, who follow closely, take their seats, his face is hidden for the first time—shaded by his cloak. Nor do they see it any more that day. He alights first, and passes immediately into his own room. The other mourners (who are only Mr Chick, and two of the medical attendants) proceed upstairs to the drawing-room, to be received by Mrs Chick and Miss Tox. And what the face is, in the shut-up chamber underneath: or what the thoughts are: what the heart is, what the contest or the suffering: no one knows.\n\nThe chief thing that they know, below stairs, in the kitchen, is that “it seems like Sunday.” They can hardly persuade themselves but that there is something unbecoming, if not wicked, in the conduct of the people out of doors, who pursue their ordinary occupations, and wear their everyday attire. It is quite a novelty to have the blinds up, and the shutters open; and they make themselves dismally comfortable over bottles of wine, which are freely broached as on a festival. They are much inclined to moralise. Mr Towlinson proposes with a sigh, “Amendment to us all!” for which, as Cook says with another sigh, “There’s room enough, God knows.” In the evening, Mrs Chick and Miss Tox take to needlework again. In the evening also, Mr Towlinson goes out to take the air, accompanied by the housemaid, who has not yet tried her mourning bonnet. They are very tender to each other at dusky street-corners, and Towlinson has visions of leading an altered and blameless existence as a serious greengrocer in Oxford Market.\n\nThere is sounder sleep and deeper rest in Mr Dombey’s house tonight, than there has been for many nights. The morning sun awakens the old household, settled down once more in their old ways. The rosy children opposite run past with hoops. There is a splendid wedding in the church. The juggler’s wife is active with the money-box in another quarter of the town. The mason sings and whistles as he chips out P-A-U-L in the marble slab before him.\n\nAnd can it be that in a world so full and busy, the loss of one weak creature makes a void in any heart, so wide and deep that nothing but the width and depth of vast eternity can fill it up! Florence, in her innocent affliction, might have answered, “Oh my brother, oh my dearly loved and loving brother! Only friend and companion of my slighted childhood! Could any less idea shed the light already dawning on your early grave, or give birth to the softened sorrow that is springing into life beneath this rain of tears!”\n\n“My dear child,” said Mrs Chick, who held it as a duty incumbent on her, to improve the occasion, “when you are as old as I am—”\n\n“Which will be the prime of life,” observed Miss Tox.\n\n“You will then,” pursued Mrs Chick, gently squeezing Miss Tox’s hand in acknowledgment of her friendly remark, “you will then know that all grief is unavailing, and that it is our duty to submit.”\n\n“I will try, dear aunt I do try,” answered Florence, sobbing.\n\n“I am glad to hear it,” said Mrs Chick, “because; my love, as our dear Miss Tox—of whose sound sense and excellent judgment, there cannot possibly be two opinions—”\n\n“My dear Louisa, I shall really be proud, soon,” said Miss Tox.\n\n“—will tell you, and confirm by her experience,” pursued Mrs Chick, “we are called upon on all occasions to make an effort It is required of us. If any—my dear,” turning to Miss Tox, “I want a word. Mis—Mis-”\n\n“Demeanour?” suggested Miss Tox.\n\n“No, no, no,” said Mrs Chic “How can you! Goodness me, it’s on, the end of my tongue. Mis-”\n\n“Placed affection?” suggested Miss Tox, timidly.\n\n“Good gracious, Lucretia!” returned Mrs Chick “How very monstrous! Misanthrope, is the word I want. The idea! Misplaced affection! I say, if any misanthrope were to put, in my presence, the question ‘Why were we born?’ I should reply, ‘To make an effort’.”\n\n“Very good indeed,” said Miss Tox, much impressed by the originality of the sentiment “Very good.”\n\n“Unhappily,” pursued Mrs Chick, “we have a warning under our own eyes. We have but too much reason to suppose, my dear child, that if an effort had been made in time, in this family, a train of the most trying and distressing circumstances might have been avoided. Nothing shall ever persuade me,” observed the good matron, with a resolute air, “but that if that effort had been made by poor dear Fanny, the poor dear darling child would at least have had a stronger constitution.”\n\nMrs Chick abandoned herself to her feelings for half a moment; but, as a practical illustration of her doctrine, brought herself up short, in the middle of a sob, and went on again.\n\n“Therefore, Florence, pray let us see that you have some strength of mind, and do not selfishly aggravate the distress in which your poor Papa is plunged.”\n\n“Dear aunt!” said Florence, kneeling quickly down before her, that she might the better and more earnestly look into her face. “Tell me more about Papa. Pray tell me about him! Is he quite heartbroken?”\n\nMiss Tox was of a tender nature, and there was something in this appeal that moved her very much. Whether she saw it in a succession, on the part of the neglected child, to the affectionate concern so often expressed by her dead brother—or a love that sought to twine itself about the heart that had loved him, and that could not bear to be shut out from sympathy with such a sorrow, in such sad community of love and grief—or whether she only recognised the earnest and devoted spirit which, although discarded and repulsed, was wrung with tenderness long unreturned, and in the waste and solitude of this bereavement cried to him to seek a comfort in it, and to give some, by some small response—whatever may have been her understanding of it, it moved Miss Tox. For the moment she forgot the majesty of Mrs Chick, and, patting Florence hastily on the cheek, turned aside and suffered the tears to gush from her eyes, without waiting for a lead from that wise matron.\n\nMrs Chick herself lost, for a moment, the presence of mind on which she so much prided herself; and remained mute, looking on the beautiful young face that had so long, so steadily, and patiently, been turned towards the little bed. But recovering her voice—which was synonymous with her presence of mind, indeed they were one and the same thing—she replied with dignity:\n\n“Florence, my dear child, your poor Papa is peculiar at times; and to question me about him, is to question me upon a subject which I really do not pretend to understand. I believe I have as much influence with your Papa as anybody has. Still, all I can say is, that he has said very little to me; and that I have only seen him once or twice for a minute at a time, and indeed have hardly seen him then, for his room has been dark. I have said to your Papa, ‘Paul!’—that is the exact expression I used—‘Paul! why do you not take something stimulating?’ Your Papa’s reply has always been, ‘Louisa, have the goodness to leave me. I want nothing. I am better by myself.’ If I was to be put upon my oath to-morrow, Lucretia, before a magistrate,” said Mrs Chick, “I have no doubt I could venture to swear to those identical words.”\n\nMiss Tox expressed her admiration by saying, “My Louisa is ever methodical!”\n\n“In short, Florence,” resumed her aunt, “literally nothing has passed between your poor Papa and myself, until today; when I mentioned to your Papa that Sir Barnet and Lady Skettles had written exceedingly kind notes—our sweet boy! Lady Skettles loved him like a—where’s my pocket handkerchief?”\n\nMiss Tox produced one.\n\n“Exceedingly kind notes, proposing that you should visit them for change of scene. Mentioning to your Papa that I thought Miss Tox and myself might now go home (in which he quite agreed), I inquired if he had any objection to your accepting this invitation. He said, ‘No, Louisa, not the least!’”\n\nFlorence raised her tearful eye.\n\n“At the same time, if you would prefer staying here, Florence, to paying this visit at present, or to going home with me—”\n\n“I should much prefer it, aunt,” was the faint rejoinder.\n\n“Why then, child,” said Mrs Chick, “you can. It’s a strange choice, I must say. But you always were strange. Anybody else at your time of life, and after what has passed—my dear Miss Tox, I have lost my pocket handkerchief again—would be glad to leave here, one would suppose.”\n\n“I should not like to feel,” said Florence, “as if the house was avoided. I should not like to think that the—his—the rooms upstairs were quite empty and dreary, aunt. I would rather stay here, for the present. Oh my brother! oh my brother!”\n\nIt was a natural emotion, not to be suppressed; and it would make way even between the fingers of the hands with which she covered up her face. The overcharged and heavy-laden breast must some times have that vent, or the poor wounded solitary heart within it would have fluttered like a bird with broken wings, and sunk down in the dust.\n\n“Well, child!” said Mrs Chick, after a pause “I wouldn’t on any account say anything unkind to you, and that I’m sure you know. You will remain here, then, and do exactly as you like. No one will interfere with you, Florence, or wish to interfere with you, I’m sure.”\n\nFlorence shook her head in sad assent.\n\n“I had no sooner begun to advise your poor Papa that he really ought to seek some distraction and restoration in a temporary change,” said Mrs Chick, “than he told me he had already formed the intention of going into the country for a short time. I’m sure I hope he’ll go very soon. He can’t go too soon. But I suppose there are some arrangements connected with his private papers and so forth, consequent on the affliction that has tried us all so much—I can’t think what’s become of mine: Lucretia, lend me yours, my dear—that may occupy him for one or two evenings in his own room. Your Papa’s a Dombey, child, if ever there was one,” said Mrs Chick, drying both her eyes at once with great care on opposite corners of Miss Tox’s handkerchief “He’ll make an effort. There’s no fear of him.”\n\n“Is there nothing, aunt,” said Florence, trembling, “I might do to—”\n\n“Lord, my dear child,” interposed Mrs Chick, hastily, “what are you talking about? If your Papa said to Me—I have given you his exact words, ‘Louisa, I want nothing; I am better by myself’—what do you think he’d say to you? You mustn’t show yourself to him, child. Don’t dream of such a thing.”\n\n“Aunt,” said Florence, “I will go and lie down on my bed.”\n\nMrs Chick approved of this resolution, and dismissed her with a kiss. But Miss Tox, on a faint pretence of looking for the mislaid handkerchief, went upstairs after her; and tried in a few stolen minutes to comfort her, in spite of great discouragement from Susan Nipper. For Miss Nipper, in her burning zeal, disparaged Miss Tox as a crocodile; yet her sympathy seemed genuine, and had at least the vantage-ground of disinterestedness—there was little favour to be won by it.\n\nAnd was there no one nearer and dearer than Susan, to uphold the striving heart in its anguish? Was there no other neck to clasp; no other face to turn to? no one else to say a soothing word to such deep sorrow? Was Florence so alone in the bleak world that nothing else remained to her? Nothing. Stricken motherless and brotherless at once—for in the loss of little Paul, that first and greatest loss fell heavily upon her—this was the only help she had. Oh, who can tell how much she needed help at first!\n\nAt first, when the house subsided into its accustomed course, and they had all gone away, except the servants, and her father shut up in his own rooms, Florence could do nothing but weep, and wander up and down, and sometimes, in a sudden pang of desolate remembrance, fly to her own chamber, wring her hands, lay her face down on her bed, and know no consolation: nothing but the bitterness and cruelty of grief. This commonly ensued upon the recognition of some spot or object very tenderly associated with him; and it made the miserable house, at first, a place of agony.\n\nBut it is not in the nature of pure love to burn so fiercely and unkindly long. The flame that in its grosser composition has the taint of earth may prey upon the breast that gives it shelter; but the fire from heaven is as gentle in the heart, as when it rested on the heads of the assembled twelve, and showed each man his brother, brightened and unhurt. The image conjured up, there soon returned the placid face, the softened voice, the loving looks, the quiet trustfulness and peace; and Florence, though she wept still, wept more tranquilly, and courted the remembrance.\n\nIt was not very long before the golden water, dancing on the wall, in the old place, at the old serene time, had her calm eye fixed upon it as it ebbed away. It was not very long before that room again knew her, often; sitting there alone, as patient and as mild as when she had watched beside the little bed. When any sharp sense of its being empty smote upon her, she could kneel beside it, and pray GOD—it was the pouring out of her full heart—to let one angel love her and remember her.\n\nIt was not very long before, in the midst of the dismal house so wide and dreary, her low voice in the twilight, slowly and stopping sometimes, touched the old air to which he had so often listened, with his drooping head upon her arm. And after that, and when it was quite dark, a little strain of music trembled in the room: so softly played and sung, that it was more like the mournful recollection of what she had done at his request on that last night, than the reality repeated. But it was repeated, often—very often, in the shadowy solitude; and broken murmurs of the strain still trembled on the keys, when the sweet voice was hushed in tears.\n\nThus she gained heart to look upon the work with which her fingers had been busy by his side on the sea-shore; and thus it was not very long before she took to it again—with something of a human love for it, as if it had been sentient and had known him; and, sitting in a window, near her mother’s picture, in the unused room so long deserted, wore away the thoughtful hours.\n\nWhy did the dark eyes turn so often from this work to where the rosy children lived? They were not immediately suggestive of her loss; for they were all girls: four little sisters. But they were motherless like her—and had a father.\n\nIt was easy to know when he had gone out and was expected home, for the elder child was always dressed and waiting for him at the drawing-room window, or on the balcony; and when he appeared, her expectant face lighted up with joy, while the others at the high window, and always on the watch too, clapped their hands, and drummed them on the sill, and called to him. The elder child would come down to the hall, and put her hand in his, and lead him up the stairs; and Florence would see her afterwards sitting by his side, or on his knee, or hanging coaxingly about his neck and talking to him: and though they were always gay together, he would often watch her face as if he thought her like her mother that was dead. Florence would sometimes look no more at this, and bursting into tears would hide behind the curtain as if she were frightened, or would hurry from the window. Yet she could not help returning; and her work would soon fall unheeded from her hands again.\n\nIt was the house that had been empty, years ago. It had remained so for a long time. At last, and while she had been away from home, this family had taken it; and it was repaired and newly painted; and there were birds and flowers about it; and it looked very different from its old self. But she never thought of the house. The children and their father were all in all.\n\nWhen he had dined, she could see them, through the open windows, go down with their governess or nurse, and cluster round the table; and in the still summer weather, the sound of their childish voices and clear laughter would come ringing across the street, into the drooping air of the room in which she sat. Then they would climb and clamber upstairs with him, and romp about him on the sofa, or group themselves at his knee, a very nosegay of little faces, while he seemed to tell them some story. Or they would come running out into the balcony; and then Florence would hide herself quickly, lest it should check them in their joy, to see her in her black dress, sitting there alone.\n\nThe elder child remained with her father when the rest had gone away, and made his tea for him—happy little house-keeper she was then!—and sat conversing with him, sometimes at the window, sometimes in the room, until the candles came. He made her his companion, though she was some years younger than Florence; and she could be as staid and pleasantly demure, with her little book or work-box, as a woman. When they had candles, Florence from her own dark room was not afraid to look again. But when the time came for the child to say “Good-night, Papa,” and go to bed, Florence would sob and tremble as she raised her face to him, and could look no more.\n\nThough still she would turn, again and again, before going to bed herself from the simple air that had lulled him to rest so often, long ago, and from the other low soft broken strain of music, back to that house. But that she ever thought of it, or watched it, was a secret which she kept within her own young breast.\n\nAnd did that breast of Florence—Florence, so ingenuous and true—so worthy of the love that he had borne her, and had whispered in his last faint words—whose guileless heart was mirrored in the beauty of her face, and breathed in every accent of her gentle voice—did that young breast hold any other secret? Yes. One more.\n\nWhen no one in the house was stirring, and the lights were all extinguished, she would softly leave her own room, and with noiseless feet descend the staircase, and approach her father’s door. Against it, scarcely breathing, she would rest her face and head, and press her lips, in the yearning of her love. She crouched upon the cold stone floor outside it, every night, to listen even for his breath; and in her one absorbing wish to be allowed to show him some affection, to be a consolation to him, to win him over to the endurance of some tenderness from her, his solitary child, she would have knelt down at his feet, if she had dared, in humble supplication.\n\nNo one knew it. No one thought of it. The door was ever closed, and he shut up within. He went out once or twice, and it was said in the house that he was very soon going on his country journey; but he lived in those rooms, and lived alone, and never saw her, or inquired for her. Perhaps he did not even know that she was in the house.\n\nOne day, about a week after the funeral, Florence was sitting at her work, when Susan appeared, with a face half laughing and half crying, to announce a visitor.\n\n“A visitor! To me, Susan!” said Florence, looking up in astonishment.\n\n“Well, it is a wonder, ain’t it now, Miss Floy?” said Susan; “but I wish you had a many visitors, I do, indeed, for you’d be all the better for it, and it’s my opinion that the sooner you and me goes even to them old Skettleses, Miss, the better for both, I may not wish to live in crowds, Miss Floy, but still I’m not a oyster.”\n\nTo do Miss Nipper justice, she spoke more for her young mistress than herself; and her face showed it.\n\n“But the visitor, Susan,” said Florence.\n\nSusan, with an hysterical explosion that was as much a laugh as a sob, and as much a sob as a laugh, answered,\n\n“Mr Toots!”\n\nThe smile that appeared on Florence’s face passed from it in a moment, and her eyes filled with tears. But at any rate it was a smile, and that gave great satisfaction to Miss Nipper.\n\n“My own feelings exactly, Miss Floy,” said Susan, putting her apron to her eyes, and shaking her head. “Immediately I see that Innocent in the Hall, Miss Floy, I burst out laughing first, and then I choked.”\n\nSusan Nipper involuntarily proceeded to do the like again on the spot. In the meantime Mr Toots, who had come upstairs after her, all unconscious of the effect he produced, announced himself with his knuckles on the door, and walked in very briskly.\n\n“How d’ye do, Miss Dombey?” said Mr Toots. “I’m very well, I thank you; how are you?”\n\nMr Toots—than whom there were few better fellows in the world, though there may have been one or two brighter spirits—had laboriously invented this long burst of discourse with the view of relieving the feelings both of Florence and himself. But finding that he had run through his property, as it were, in an injudicious manner, by squandering the whole before taking a chair, or before Florence had uttered a word, or before he had well got in at the door, he deemed it advisable to begin again.\n\n“How d’ye do, Miss Dombey?” said Mr Toots. “I’m very well, I thank you; how are you?”\n\nFlorence gave him her hand, and said she was very well.\n\n“I’m very well indeed,” said Mr Toots, taking a chair. “Very well indeed, I am. I don’t remember,” said Mr Toots, after reflecting a little, “that I was ever better, thank you.”\n\n“It’s very kind of you to come,” said Florence, taking up her work, “I am very glad to see you.”\n\nMr Toots responded with a chuckle. Thinking that might be too lively, he corrected it with a sigh. Thinking that might be too melancholy, he corrected it with a chuckle. Not thoroughly pleasing himself with either mode of reply, he breathed hard.\n\n“You were very kind to my dear brother,” said Florence, obeying her own natural impulse to relieve him by saying so. “He often talked to me about you.”\n\n“Oh it’s of no consequence,” said Mr Toots hastily. “Warm, ain’t it?”\n\n“It is beautiful weather,” replied Florence.\n\n“It agrees with me!” said Mr Toots. “I don’t think I ever was so well as I find myself at present, I’m obliged to you.\n\nAfter stating this curious and unexpected fact, Mr Toots fell into a deep well of silence.\n\n“You have left Dr Blimber’s, I think?” said Florence, trying to help him out.\n\n“I should hope so,” returned Mr Toots. And tumbled in again.\n\nHe remained at the bottom, apparently drowned, for at least ten minutes. At the expiration of that period, he suddenly floated, and said,\n\n“Well! Good morning, Miss Dombey.”\n\n“Are you going?” asked Florence, rising.\n\n“I don’t know, though. No, not just at present,” said Mr Toots, sitting down again, most unexpectedly. “The fact is—I say, Miss Dombey!”\n\n“Don’t be afraid to speak to me,” said Florence, with a quiet smile, “I should be very glad if you would talk about my brother.”\n\n“Would you, though?” retorted Mr Toots, with sympathy in every fibre of his otherwise expressionless face. “Poor Dombey! I’m sure I never thought that Burgess and Co.—fashionable tailors (but very dear), that we used to talk about—would make this suit of clothes for such a purpose.” Mr Toots was dressed in mourning. “Poor Dombey! I say! Miss Dombey!” blubbered Toots.\n\n“Yes,” said Florence.\n\n“There’s a friend he took to very much at last. I thought you’d like to have him, perhaps, as a sort of keepsake. You remember his remembering Diogenes?”\n\n“Oh yes! oh yes” cried Florence.\n\n“Poor Dombey! So do I,” said Mr Toots.\n\nMr Toots, seeing Florence in tears, had great difficulty in getting beyond this point, and had nearly tumbled into the well again. But a chuckle saved him on the brink.\n\n“I say,” he proceeded, “Miss Dombey! I could have had him stolen for ten shillings, if they hadn’t given him up: and I would: but they were glad to get rid of him, I think. If you’d like to have him, he’s at the door. I brought him on purpose for you. He ain’t a lady’s dog, you know,” said Mr Toots, “but you won’t mind that, will you?”\n\nIn fact, Diogenes was at that moment, as they presently ascertained from looking down into the street, staring through the window of a hackney cabriolet, into which, for conveyance to that spot, he had been ensnared, on a false pretence of rats among the straw. Sooth to say, he was as unlike a lady’s dog as might be; and in his gruff anxiety to get out, presented an appearance sufficiently unpromising, as he gave short yelps out of one side of his mouth, and overbalancing himself by the intensity of every one of those efforts, tumbled down into the straw, and then sprung panting up again, putting out his tongue, as if he had come express to a Dispensary to be examined for his health.\n\nBut though Diogenes was as ridiculous a dog as one would meet with on a summer’s day; a blundering, ill-favoured, clumsy, bullet-headed dog, continually acting on a wrong idea that there was an enemy in the neighbourhood, whom it was meritorious to bark at; and though he was far from good-tempered, and certainly was not clever, and had hair all over his eyes, and a comic nose, and an inconsistent tail, and a gruff voice; he was dearer to Florence, in virtue of that parting remembrance of him, and that request that he might be taken care of, than the most valuable and beautiful of his kind. So dear, indeed, was this same ugly Diogenes, and so welcome to her, that she took the jewelled hand of Mr Toots and kissed it in her gratitude. And when Diogenes, released, came tearing up the stairs and bouncing into the room (such a business as there was, first, to get him out of the cabriolet!), dived under all the furniture, and wound a long iron chain, that dangled from his neck, round legs of chairs and tables, and then tugged at it until his eyes became unnaturally visible, in consequence of their nearly starting out of his head; and when he growled at Mr Toots, who affected familiarity; and went pell-mell at Towlinson, morally convinced that he was the enemy whom he had barked at round the corner all his life and had never seen yet; Florence was as pleased with him as if he had been a miracle of discretion.\n\nMr Toots was so overjoyed by the success of his present, and was so delighted to see Florence bending down over Diogenes, smoothing his coarse back with her little delicate hand—Diogenes graciously allowing it from the first moment of their acquaintance—that he felt it difficult to take leave, and would, no doubt, have been a much longer time in making up his mind to do so, if he had not been assisted by Diogenes himself, who suddenly took it into his head to bay Mr Toots, and to make short runs at him with his mouth open. Not exactly seeing his way to the end of these demonstrations, and sensible that they placed the pantaloons constructed by the art of Burgess and Co. in jeopardy, Mr Toots, with chuckles, lapsed out at the door: by which, after looking in again two or three times, without any object at all, and being on each occasion greeted with a fresh run from Diogenes, he finally took himself off and got away.\n\n“Come, then, Di! Dear Di! Make friends with your new mistress. Let us love each other, Di!” said Florence, fondling his shaggy head. And Di, the rough and gruff, as if his hairy hide were pervious to the tear that dropped upon it, and his dog’s heart melted as it fell, put his nose up to her face, and swore fidelity.\n\nDiogenes the man did not speak plainer to Alexander the Great than Diogenes the dog spoke to Florence. He subscribed to the offer of his little mistress cheerfully, and devoted himself to her service. A banquet was immediately provided for him in a corner; and when he had eaten and drunk his fill, he went to the window where Florence was sitting, looking on, rose up on his hind legs, with his awkward fore paws on her shoulders, licked her face and hands, nestled his great head against her heart, and wagged his tail till he was tired. Finally, Diogenes coiled himself up at her feet and went to sleep.\n\nAlthough Miss Nipper was nervous in regard of dogs, and felt it necessary to come into the room with her skirts carefully collected about her, as if she were crossing a brook on stepping-stones; also to utter little screams and stand up on chairs when Diogenes stretched himself, she was in her own manner affected by the kindness of Mr Toots, and could not see Florence so alive to the attachment and society of this rude friend of little Paul’s, without some mental comments thereupon that brought the water to her eyes. Mr Dombey, as a part of her reflections, may have been, in the association of ideas, connected with the dog; but, at any rate, after observing Diogenes and his mistress all the evening, and after exerting herself with much good-will to provide Diogenes a bed in an ante-chamber outside his mistress’s door, she said hurriedly to Florence, before leaving her for the night:\n\n“Your Pa’s a going off, Miss Floy, tomorrow morning.”\n\n“To-morrow morning, Susan?”\n\n“Yes, Miss; that’s the orders. Early.”\n\n“Do you know,” asked Florence, without looking at her, “where Papa is going, Susan?”\n\n“Not exactly, Miss. He’s going to meet that precious Major first, and I must say if I was acquainted with any Major myself (which Heavens forbid), it shouldn’t be a blue one!”\n\n“Hush, Susan!” urged Florence gently.\n\n“Well, Miss Floy,” returned Miss Nipper, who was full of burning indignation, and minded her stops even less than usual. “I can’t help it, blue he is, and while I was a Christian, although humble, I would have natural-coloured friends, or none.”\n\nIt appeared from what she added and had gleaned downstairs, that Mrs Chick had proposed the Major for Mr Dombey’s companion, and that Mr Dombey, after some hesitation, had invited him.\n\n“Talk of him being a change, indeed!” observed Miss Nipper to herself with boundless contempt. “If he’s a change, give me a constancy.”\n\n“Good-night, Susan,” said Florence.\n\n“Good-night, my darling dear Miss Floy.”\n\nHer tone of commiseration smote the chord so often roughly touched, but never listened to while she or anyone looked on. Florence left alone, laid her head upon her hand, and pressing the other over her swelling heart, held free communication with her sorrows.\n\nIt was a wet night; and the melancholy rain fell pattering and dropping with a weary sound. A sluggish wind was blowing, and went moaning round the house, as if it were in pain or grief. A shrill noise quivered through the trees. While she sat weeping, it grew late, and dreary midnight tolled out from the steeples.\n\nFlorence was little more than a child in years—not yet fourteen—and the loneliness and gloom of such an hour in the great house where Death had lately made its own tremendous devastation, might have set an older fancy brooding on vague terrors. But her innocent imagination was too full of one theme to admit them. Nothing wandered in her thoughts but love—a wandering love, indeed, and castaway—but turning always to her father.\n\nThere was nothing in the dropping of the rain, the moaning of the wind, the shuddering of the trees, the striking of the solemn clocks, that shook this one thought, or diminished its interest. Her recollections of the dear dead boy—and they were never absent—were itself, the same thing. And oh, to be shut out: to be so lost: never to have looked into her father’s face or touched him, since that hour!\n\nShe could not go to bed, poor child, and never had gone yet, since then, without making her nightly pilgrimage to his door. It would have been a strange sad sight, to see her now, stealing lightly down the stairs through the thick gloom, and stopping at it with a beating heart, and blinded eyes, and hair that fell down loosely and unthought of; and touching it outside with her wet cheek. But the night covered it, and no one knew.\n\nThe moment that she touched the door on this night, Florence found that it was open. For the first time it stood open, though by but a hair’s-breadth: and there was a light within. The first impulse of the timid child—and she yielded to it—was to retire swiftly. Her next, to go back, and to enter; and this second impulse held her in irresolution on the staircase.\n\nIn its standing open, even by so much as that chink, there seemed to be hope. There was encouragement in seeing a ray of light from within, stealing through the dark stern doorway, and falling in a thread upon the marble floor. She turned back, hardly knowing what she did, but urged on by the love within her, and the trial they had undergone together, but not shared: and with her hands a little raised and trembling, glided in.\n\nHer father sat at his old table in the middle room. He had been arranging some papers, and destroying others, and the latter lay in fragile ruins before him. The rain dripped heavily upon the glass panes in the outer room, where he had so often watched poor Paul, a baby; and the low complainings of the wind were heard without.\n\nBut not by him. He sat with his eyes fixed on the table, so immersed in thought, that a far heavier tread than the light foot of his child could make, might have failed to rouse him. His face was turned towards her. By the waning lamp, and at that haggard hour, it looked worn and dejected; and in the utter loneliness surrounding him, there was an appeal to Florence that struck home.\n\n“Papa! Papa! speak to me, dear Papa!”\n\nHe started at her voice, and leaped up from his seat. She was close before him with extended arms, but he fell back.\n\n“What is the matter?” he said, sternly. “Why do you come here? What has frightened you?”\n\nIf anything had frightened her, it was the face he turned upon her. The glowing love within the breast of his young daughter froze before it, and she stood and looked at him as if stricken into stone.\n\nThere was not one touch of tenderness or pity in it. There was not one gleam of interest, parental recognition, or relenting in it. There was a change in it, but not of that kind. The old indifference and cold constraint had given place to something: what, she never thought and did not dare to think, and yet she felt it in its force, and knew it well without a name: that as it looked upon her, seemed to cast a shadow on her head.\n\nDid he see before him the successful rival of his son, in health and life? Did he look upon his own successful rival in that son’s affection? Did a mad jealousy and withered pride, poison sweet remembrances that should have endeared and made her precious to him? Could it be possible that it was gall to him to look upon her in her beauty and her promise: thinking of his infant boy!\n\nFlorence had no such thoughts. But love is quick to know when it is spurned and hopeless: and hope died out of hers, as she stood looking in her father’s face.\n\n“I ask you, Florence, are you frightened? Is there anything the matter, that you come here?”\n\n“I came, Papa—”\n\n“Against my wishes. Why?”\n\nShe saw he knew why: it was written broadly on his face: and dropped her head upon her hands with one prolonged low cry.\n\nLet him remember it in that room, years to come. It has faded from the air, before he breaks the silence. It may pass as quickly from his brain, as he believes, but it is there. Let him remember it in that room, years to come!\n\nHe took her by the arm. His hand was cold, and loose, and scarcely closed upon her.\n\n“You are tired, I daresay,” he said, taking up the light, and leading her towards the door, “and want rest. We all want rest. Go, Florence. You have been dreaming.”\n\nThe dream she had had, was over then, God help her! and she felt that it could never more come back.\n\n“I will remain here to light you up the stairs. The whole house is yours above there,” said her father, slowly. “You are its mistress now. Good-night!”\n\nStill covering her face, she sobbed, and answered “Good-night, dear Papa,” and silently ascended. Once she looked back as if she would have returned to him, but for fear. It was a momentary thought, too hopeless to encourage; and her father stood there with the light—hard, unresponsive, motionless—until the fluttering dress of his fair child was lost in the darkness.\n\nLet him remember it in that room, years to come. The rain that falls upon the roof: the wind that mourns outside the door: may have foreknowledge in their melancholy sound. Let him remember it in that room, years to come!\n\nThe last time he had watched her, from the same place, winding up those stairs, she had had her brother in her arms. It did not move his heart towards her now, it steeled it: but he went into his room, and locked his door, and sat down in his chair, and cried for his lost boy.\n\nDiogenes was broad awake upon his post, and waiting for his little mistress.\n\n“Oh, Di! Oh, dear Di! Love me for his sake!”\n\nDiogenes already loved her for her own, and didn’t care how much he showed it. So he made himself vastly ridiculous by performing a variety of uncouth bounces in the ante-chamber, and concluded, when poor Florence was at last asleep, and dreaming of the rosy children opposite, by scratching open her bedroom door: rolling up his bed into a pillow: lying down on the boards, at the full length of his tether, with his head towards her: and looking lazily at her, upside down, out of the tops of his eyes, until from winking and winking he fell asleep himself, and dreamed, with gruff barks, of his enemy.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX. Walter goes away\n\nThe wooden Midshipman at the Instrument-maker’s door, like the hard-hearted little Midshipman he was, remained supremely indifferent to Walter’s going away, even when the very last day of his sojourn in the back parlour was on the decline. With his quadrant at his round black knob of an eye, and his figure in its old attitude of indomitable alacrity, the Midshipman displayed his elfin small-clothes to the best advantage, and, absorbed in scientific pursuits, had no sympathy with worldly concerns. He was so far the creature of circumstances, that a dry day covered him with dust, and a misty day peppered him with little bits of soot, and a wet day brightened up his tarnished uniform for the moment, and a very hot day blistered him; but otherwise he was a callous, obdurate, conceited Midshipman, intent on his own discoveries, and caring as little for what went on about him, terrestrially, as Archimedes at the taking of Syracuse.\n\nSuch a Midshipman he seemed to be, at least, in the then position of domestic affairs. Walter eyed him kindly many a time in passing in and out; and poor old Sol, when Walter was not there, would come and lean against the doorpost, resting his weary wig as near the shoe-buckles of the guardian genius of his trade and shop as he could. But no fierce idol with a mouth from ear to ear, and a murderous visage made of parrot’s feathers, was ever more indifferent to the appeals of its savage votaries, than was the Midshipman to these marks of attachment.\n\nWalter’s heart felt heavy as he looked round his old bedroom, up among the parapets and chimney-pots, and thought that one more night already darkening would close his acquaintance with it, perhaps for ever. Dismantled of his little stock of books and pictures, it looked coldly and reproachfully on him for his desertion, and had already a foreshadowing upon it of its coming strangeness. “A few hours more,” thought Walter, “and no dream I ever had here when I was a schoolboy will be so little mine as this old room. The dream may come back in my sleep, and I may return waking to this place, it may be: but the dream at least will serve no other master, and the room may have a score, and every one of them may change, neglect, misuse it.”\n\nBut his Uncle was not to be left alone in the little back parlour, where he was then sitting by himself; for Captain Cuttle, considerate in his roughness, stayed away against his will, purposely that they should have some talk together unobserved: so Walter, newly returned home from his last day’s bustle, descended briskly, to bear him company.\n\n“Uncle,” he said gaily, laying his hand upon the old man’s shoulder, “what shall I send you home from Barbados?”\n\n“Hope, my dear Wally. Hope that we shall meet again, on this side of the grave. Send me as much of that as you can.”\n\n“So I will, Uncle: I have enough and to spare, and I’ll not be chary of it! And as to lively turtles, and limes for Captain Cuttle’s punch, and preserves for you on Sundays, and all that sort of thing, why I’ll send you ship-loads, Uncle: when I’m rich enough.”\n\nOld Sol wiped his spectacles, and faintly smiled.\n\n“That’s right, Uncle!” cried Walter, merrily, and clapping him half a dozen times more upon the shoulder. “You cheer up me! I’ll cheer up you! We’ll be as gay as larks to-morrow morning, Uncle, and we’ll fly as high! As to my anticipations, they are singing out of sight now.”\n\n“Wally, my dear boy,” returned the old man, “I’ll do my best, I’ll do my best.”\n\n“And your best, Uncle,” said Walter, with his pleasant laugh, “is the best best that I know. You’ll not forget what you’re to send me, Uncle?”\n\n“No, Wally, no,” replied the old man; “everything I hear about Miss Dombey, now that she is left alone, poor lamb, I’ll write. I fear it won’t be much though, Wally.”\n\n“Why, I’ll tell you what, Uncle,” said Walter, after a moment’s hesitation, “I have just been up there.”\n\n“Ay, ay, ay?” murmured the old man, raising his eyebrows, and his spectacles with them.\n\n“Not to see her,” said Walter, “though I could have seen her, I daresay, if I had asked, Mr Dombey being out of town: but to say a parting word to Susan. I thought I might venture to do that, you know, under the circumstances, and remembering when I saw Miss Dombey last.”\n\n“Yes, my boy, yes,” replied his Uncle, rousing himself from a temporary abstraction.\n\n“So I saw her,” pursued Walter, “Susan, I mean: and I told her I was off and away to-morrow. And I said, Uncle, that you had always had an interest in Miss Dombey since that night when she was here, and always wished her well and happy, and always would be proud and glad to serve her in the least: I thought I might say that, you know, under the circumstances. Don’t you think so?”\n\n“Yes, my boy, yes,” replied his Uncle, in the tone as before.\n\n“And I added,” pursued Walter, “that if she—Susan, I mean—could ever let you know, either through herself, or Mrs Richards, or anybody else who might be coming this way, that Miss Dombey was well and happy, you would take it very kindly, and would write so much to me, and I should take it very kindly too. There! Upon my word, Uncle,” said Walter, “I scarcely slept all last night through thinking of doing this; and could not make up my mind when I was out, whether to do it or not; and yet I am sure it is the true feeling of my heart, and I should have been quite miserable afterwards if I had not relieved it.”\n\nHis honest voice and manner corroborated what he said, and quite established its ingenuousness.\n\n“So, if you ever see her, Uncle,” said Walter, “I mean Miss Dombey now—and perhaps you may, who knows!—tell her how much I felt for her; how much I used to think of her when I was here; how I spoke of her, with the tears in my eyes, Uncle, on this last night before I went away. Tell her that I said I never could forget her gentle manner, or her beautiful face, or her sweet kind disposition that was better than all. And as I didn’t take them from a woman’s feet, or a young lady’s: only a little innocent child’s,” said Walter: “tell her, if you don’t mind, Uncle, that I kept those shoes—she’ll remember how often they fell off, that night—and took them away with me as a remembrance!”\n\nThey were at that very moment going out at the door in one of Walter’s trunks. A porter carrying off his baggage on a truck for shipment at the docks on board the Son and Heir, had got possession of them; and wheeled them away under the very eye of the insensible Midshipman before their owner had well finished speaking.\n\nBut that ancient mariner might have been excused his insensibility to the treasure as it rolled away. For, under his eye at the same moment, accurately within his range of observation, coming full into the sphere of his startled and intensely wide-awake look-out, were Florence and Susan Nipper: Florence looking up into his face half timidly, and receiving the whole shock of his wooden ogling!\n\nMore than this, they passed into the shop, and passed in at the parlour door before they were observed by anybody but the Midshipman. And Walter, having his back to the door, would have known nothing of their apparition even then, but for seeing his Uncle spring out of his own chair, and nearly tumble over another.\n\n“Why, Uncle!” exclaimed Walter. “What’s the matter?”\n\nOld Solomon replied, “Miss Dombey!”\n\n“Is it possible?” cried Walter, looking round and starting up in his turn. “Here!”\n\nWhy, It was so possible and so actual, that, while the words were on his lips, Florence hurried past him; took Uncle Sol’s snuff-coloured lapels, one in each hand; kissed him on the cheek; and turning, gave her hand to Walter with a simple truth and earnestness that was her own, and no one else’s in the world!\n\n“Going away, Walter?” said Florence.\n\n“Yes, Miss Dombey,” he replied, but not so hopefully as he endeavoured: “I have a voyage before me.”\n\n“And your Uncle,” said Florence, looking back at Solomon. “He is sorry you are going, I am sure. Ah! I see he is! Dear Walter, I am very sorry too.”\n\n“Goodness knows,” exclaimed Miss Nipper, “there’s a many we could spare instead, if numbers is a object, Mrs Pipchin as a overseer would come cheap at her weight in gold, and if a knowledge of black slavery should be required, them Blimbers is the very people for the sitiwation.”\n\nWith that Miss Nipper untied her bonnet strings, and after looking vacantly for some moments into a little black teapot that was set forth with the usual homely service on the table, shook her head and a tin canister, and began unasked to make the tea.\n\nIn the meantime Florence had turned again to the Instrument-maker, who was as full of admiration as surprise. “So grown!” said old Sol. “So improved! And yet not altered! Just the same!”\n\n“Indeed!” said Florence.\n\n“Ye—yes,” returned old Sol, rubbing his hands slowly, and considering the matter half aloud, as something pensive in the bright eyes looking at him arrested his attention. “Yes, that expression was in the younger face, too!”\n\n“You remember me,” said Florence with a smile, “and what a little creature I was then?”\n\n“My dear young lady,” returned the Instrument-maker, “how could I forget you, often as I have thought of you and heard of you since! At the very moment, indeed, when you came in, Wally was talking about you to me, and leaving messages for you, and—”\n\n“Was he?” said Florence. “Thank you, Walter! Oh thank you, Walter! I was afraid you might be going away and hardly thinking of me;” and again she gave him her little hand so freely and so faithfully that Walter held it for some moments in his own, and could not bear to let it go.\n\nYet Walter did not hold it as he might have held it once, nor did its touch awaken those old day-dreams of his boyhood that had floated past him sometimes even lately, and confused him with their indistinct and broken shapes. The purity and innocence of her endearing manner, and its perfect trustfulness, and the undisguised regard for him that lay so deeply seated in her constant eyes, and glowed upon her fair face through the smile that shaded—for alas! it was a smile too sad to brighten—it, were not of their romantic race. They brought back to his thoughts the early death-bed he had seen her tending, and the love the child had borne her; and on the wings of such remembrances she seemed to rise up, far above his idle fancies, into clearer and serener air.\n\n“I—I am afraid I must call you Walter’s Uncle, Sir,” said Florence to the old man, “if you’ll let me.”\n\n“My dear young lady,” cried old Sol. “Let you! Good gracious!”\n\n“We always knew you by that name, and talked of you,” said Florence, glancing round, and sighing gently. “The nice old parlour! Just the same! How well I recollect it!”\n\nOld Sol looked first at her, then at his nephew, and then rubbed his hands, and rubbed his spectacles, and said below his breath, “Ah! time, time, time!”\n\nThere was a short silence; during which Susan Nipper skilfully impounded two extra cups and saucers from the cupboard, and awaited the drawing of the tea with a thoughtful air.\n\n“I want to tell Walter’s Uncle,” said Florence, laying her hand timidly upon the old man’s as it rested on the table, to bespeak his attention, “something that I am anxious about. He is going to be left alone, and if he will allow me—not to take Walter’s place, for that I couldn’t do, but to be his true friend and help him if I ever can while Walter is away, I shall be very much obliged to him indeed. Will you? May I, Walter’s Uncle?”\n\nThe Instrument-maker, without speaking, put her hand to his lips, and Susan Nipper, leaning back with her arms crossed, in the chair of presidency into which she had voted herself, bit one end of her bonnet strings, and heaved a gentle sigh as she looked up at the skylight.\n\n“You will let me come to see you,” said Florence, “when I can; and you will tell me everything about yourself and Walter; and you will have no secrets from Susan when she comes and I do not, but will confide in us, and trust us, and rely upon us. And you’ll try to let us be a comfort to you? Will you, Walter’s Uncle?”\n\nThe sweet face looking into his, the gentle pleading eyes, the soft voice, and the light touch on his arm made the more winning by a child’s respect and honour for his age, that gave to all an air of graceful doubt and modest hesitation—these, and her natural earnestness, so overcame the poor old Instrument-maker, that he only answered:\n\n“Wally! say a word for me, my dear. I’m very grateful.”\n\n“No, Walter,” returned Florence with her quiet smile. “Say nothing for him, if you please. I understand him very well, and we must learn to talk together without you, dear Walter.”\n\nThe regretful tone in which she said these latter words, touched Walter more than all the rest.\n\n“Miss Florence,” he replied, with an effort to recover the cheerful manner he had preserved while talking with his Uncle, “I know no more than my Uncle, what to say in acknowledgment of such kindness, I am sure. But what could I say, after all, if I had the power of talking for an hour, except that it is like you?”\n\nSusan Nipper began upon a new part of her bonnet string, and nodded at the skylight, in approval of the sentiment expressed.\n\n“Oh! but, Walter,” said Florence, “there is something that I wish to say to you before you go away, and you must call me Florence, if you please, and not speak like a stranger.”\n\n“Like a stranger!” returned Walter, “No. I couldn’t speak so. I am sure, at least, I couldn’t feel like one.”\n\n“Ay, but that is not enough, and is not what I mean. For, Walter,” added Florence, bursting into tears, “he liked you very much, and said before he died that he was fond of you, and said ‘Remember Walter!’ and if you’ll be a brother to me, Walter, now that he is gone and I have none on earth, I’ll be your sister all my life, and think of you like one wherever we may be! This is what I wished to say, dear Walter, but I cannot say it as I would, because my heart is full.”\n\nAnd in its fulness and its sweet simplicity, she held out both her hands to him. Walter taking them, stooped down and touched the tearful face that neither shrunk nor turned away, nor reddened as he did so, but looked up at him with confidence and truth. In that one moment, every shadow of doubt or agitation passed away from Walter’s soul. It seemed to him that he responded to her innocent appeal, beside the dead child’s bed: and, in the solemn presence he had seen there, pledged himself to cherish and protect her very image, in his banishment, with brotherly regard; to garner up her simple faith, inviolate; and hold himself degraded if he breathed upon it any thought that was not in her own breast when she gave it to him.\n\nSusan Nipper, who had bitten both her bonnet strings at once, and imparted a great deal of private emotion to the skylight, during this transaction, now changed the subject by inquiring who took milk and who took sugar; and being enlightened on these points, poured out the tea. They all four gathered socially about the little table, and took tea under that young lady’s active superintendence; and the presence of Florence in the back parlour, brightened the Tartar frigate on the wall.\n\nHalf an hour ago Walter, for his life, would have hardly called her by her name. But he could do so now when she entreated him. He could think of her being there, without a lurking misgiving that it would have been better if she had not come. He could calmly think how beautiful she was, how full of promise, what a home some happy man would find in such a heart one day. He could reflect upon his own place in that heart, with pride; and with a brave determination, if not to deserve it—he still thought that far above him—never to deserve it less.\n\nSome fairy influence must surely have hovered round the hands of Susan Nipper when she made the tea, engendering the tranquil air that reigned in the back parlour during its discussion. Some counter-influence must surely have hovered round the hands of Uncle Sol’s chronometer, and moved them faster than the Tartar frigate ever went before the wind. Be this as it may, the visitors had a coach in waiting at a quiet corner not far off; and the chronometer, on being incidentally referred to, gave such a positive opinion that it had been waiting a long time, that it was impossible to doubt the fact, especially when stated on such unimpeachable authority. If Uncle Sol had been going to be hanged by his own time, he never would have allowed that the chronometer was too fast, by the least fraction of a second.\n\nFlorence at parting recapitulated to the old man all that she had said before, and bound him to their compact. Uncle Sol attended her lovingly to the legs of the wooden Midshipman, and there resigned her to Walter, who was ready to escort her and Susan Nipper to the coach.\n\n“Walter,” said Florence by the way, “I have been afraid to ask before your Uncle. Do you think you will be absent very long?”\n\n“Indeed,” said Walter, “I don’t know. I fear so. Mr Dombey signified as much, I thought, when he appointed me.”\n\n“Is it a favour, Walter?” inquired Florence, after a moment’s hesitation, and looking anxiously in his face.\n\n“The appointment?” returned Walter.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nWalter would have given anything to have answered in the affirmative, but his face answered before his lips could, and Florence was too attentive to it not to understand its reply.\n\n“I am afraid you have scarcely been a favourite with Papa,” she said, timidly.\n\n“There is no reason,” replied Walter, smiling, “why I should be.”\n\n“No reason, Walter!”\n\n“There was no reason,” said Walter, understanding what she meant. “There are many people employed in the House. Between Mr Dombey and a young man like me, there’s a wide space of separation. If I do my duty, I do what I ought, and do no more than all the rest.”\n\nHad Florence any misgiving of which she was hardly conscious: any misgiving that had sprung into an indistinct and undefined existence since that recent night when she had gone down to her father’s room: that Walter’s accidental interest in her, and early knowledge of her, might have involved him in that powerful displeasure and dislike? Had Walter any such idea, or any sudden thought that it was in her mind at that moment? Neither of them hinted at it. Neither of them spoke at all, for some short time. Susan, walking on the other side of Walter, eyed them both sharply; and certainly Miss Nipper’s thoughts travelled in that direction, and very confidently too.\n\n“You may come back very soon,” said Florence, “perhaps, Walter.”\n\n“I may come back,” said Walter, “an old man, and find you an old lady. But I hope for better things.”\n\n“Papa,” said Florence, after a moment, “will—will recover from his grief, and—speak more freely to me one day, perhaps; and if he should, I will tell him how much I wish to see you back again, and ask him to recall you for my sake.”\n\nThere was a touching modulation in these words about her father, that Walter understood too well.\n\nThe coach being close at hand, he would have left her without speaking, for now he felt what parting was; but Florence held his hand when she was seated, and then he found there was a little packet in her own.\n\n“Walter,” she said, looking full upon him with her affectionate eyes, “like you, I hope for better things. I will pray for them, and believe that they will arrive. I made this little gift for Paul. Pray take it with my love, and do not look at it until you are gone away. And now, God bless you, Walter! never forget me. You are my brother, dear!”\n\nHe was glad that Susan Nipper came between them, or he might have left her with a sorrowful remembrance of him. He was glad too that she did not look out of the coach again, but waved the little hand to him instead, as long as he could see it.\n\nIn spite of her request, he could not help opening the packet that night when he went to bed. It was a little purse: and there was money in it.\n\nBright rose the sun next morning, from his absence in strange countries and up rose Walter with it to receive the Captain, who was already at the door: having turned out earlier than was necessary, in order to get under weigh while Mrs MacStinger was still slumbering. The Captain pretended to be in tip-top spirits, and brought a very smoky tongue in one of the pockets of the broad blue coat for breakfast.\n\n“And, Wal”r,” said the Captain, when they took their seats at table, if your Uncle’s the man I think him, he’ll bring out the last bottle of the Madeira on the present occasion.”\n\n“No, no, Ned,” returned the old man. “No! That shall be opened when Walter comes home again.”\n\n“Well said!” cried the Captain. “Hear him!”\n\n“There it lies,” said Sol Gills, “down in the little cellar, covered with dirt and cobwebs. There may be dirt and cobwebs over you and me perhaps, Ned, before it sees the light.”\n\n“Hear him!” cried the Captain. “Good morality! Wal”r, my lad. Train up a fig-tree in the way it should go, and when you are old sit under the shade on it. Overhaul the—Well,” said the Captain on second thoughts, “I ain’t quite certain where that’s to be found, but when found, make a note of. Sol Gills, heave ahead again!”\n\n“But there or somewhere, it shall lie, Ned, until Wally comes back to claim it,” said the old man. “That’s all I meant to say.”\n\n“And well said too,” returned the Captain; “and if we three don’t crack that bottle in company, I’ll give you two leave to.”\n\nNotwithstanding the Captain’s excessive joviality, he made but a poor hand at the smoky tongue, though he tried very hard, when anybody looked at him, to appear as if he were eating with a vast appetite. He was terribly afraid, likewise, of being left alone with either Uncle or nephew; appearing to consider that his only chance of safety as to keeping up appearances, was in there being always three together. This terror on the part of the Captain, reduced him to such ingenious evasions as running to the door, when Solomon went to put his coat on, under pretence of having seen an extraordinary hackney-coach pass: and darting out into the road when Walter went upstairs to take leave of the lodgers, on a feint of smelling fire in a neighbouring chimney. These artifices Captain Cuttle deemed inscrutable by any uninspired observer.\n\nWalter was coming down from his parting expedition upstairs, and was crossing the shop to go back to the little parlour, when he saw a faded face he knew, looking in at the door, and darted towards it.\n\n“Mr Carker!” cried Walter, pressing the hand of John Carker the Junior. “Pray come in! This is kind of you, to be here so early to say good-bye to me. You knew how glad it would make me to shake hands with you, once, before going away. I cannot say how glad I am to have this opportunity. Pray come in.”\n\n“It is not likely that we may ever meet again, Walter,” returned the other, gently resisting his invitation, “and I am glad of this opportunity too. I may venture to speak to you, and to take you by the hand, on the eve of separation. I shall not have to resist your frank approaches, Walter, any more.”\n\nThere was a melancholy in his smile as he said it, that showed he had found some company and friendship for his thoughts even in that.\n\n“Ah, Mr Carker!” returned Walter. “Why did you resist them? You could have done me nothing but good, I am very sure.”\n\nHe shook his head. “If there were any good,” he said, “I could do on this earth, I would do it, Walter, for you. The sight of you from day to day, has been at once happiness and remorse to me. But the pleasure has outweighed the pain. I know that, now, by knowing what I lose.”\n\n“Come in, Mr Carker, and make acquaintance with my good old Uncle,” urged Walter. “I have often talked to him about you, and he will be glad to tell you all he hears from me. I have not,” said Walter, noticing his hesitation, and speaking with embarrassment himself: “I have not told him anything about our last conversation, Mr Carker; not even him, believe me.\n\nThe grey Junior pressed his hand, and tears rose in his eyes.\n\n“If I ever make acquaintance with him, Walter,” he returned, “it will be that I may hear tidings of you. Rely on my not wronging your forbearance and consideration. It would be to wrong it, not to tell him all the truth, before I sought a word of confidence from him. But I have no friend or acquaintance except you: and even for your sake, am little likely to make any.”\n\n“I wish,” said Walter, “you had suffered me to be your friend indeed. I always wished it, Mr Carker, as you know; but never half so much as now, when we are going to part.”\n\n“It is enough,” replied the other, “that you have been the friend of my own breast, and that when I have avoided you most, my heart inclined the most towards you, and was fullest of you. Walter, good-bye!”\n\n“Good-bye, Mr Carker. Heaven be with you, Sir!” cried Walter with emotion.\n\n“If,” said the other, retaining his hand while he spoke; “if when you come back, you miss me from my old corner, and should hear from anyone where I am lying, come and look upon my grave. Think that I might have been as honest and as happy as you! And let me think, when I know time is coming on, that some one like my former self may stand there, for a moment, and remember me with pity and forgiveness! Walter, good-bye!”\n\nHis figure crept like a shadow down the bright, sun-lighted street, so cheerful yet so solemn in the early summer morning; and slowly passed away.\n\nThe relentless chronometer at last announced that Walter must turn his back upon the wooden Midshipman: and away they went, himself, his Uncle, and the Captain, in a hackney-coach to a wharf, where they were to take steam-boat for some Reach down the river, the name of which, as the Captain gave it out, was a hopeless mystery to the ears of landsmen. Arrived at this Reach (whither the ship had repaired by last night’s tide), they were boarded by various excited watermen, and among others by a dirty Cyclops of the Captain’s acquaintance, who, with his one eye, had made the Captain out some mile and a half off, and had been exchanging unintelligible roars with him ever since. Becoming the lawful prize of this personage, who was frightfully hoarse and constitutionally in want of shaving, they were all three put aboard the Son and Heir. And the Son and Heir was in a pretty state of confusion, with sails lying all bedraggled on the wet decks, loose ropes tripping people up, men in red shirts running barefoot to and fro, casks blockading every foot of space, and, in the thickest of the fray, a black cook in a black caboose up to his eyes in vegetables and blinded with smoke.\n\nThe Captain immediately drew Walter into a corner, and with a great effort, that made his face very red, pulled up the silver watch, which was so big, and so tight in his pocket, that it came out like a bung.\n\n“Wal”r,” said the Captain, handing it over, and shaking him heartily by the hand, “a parting gift, my lad. Put it back half an hour every morning, and about another quarter towards the arternoon, and it’s a watch that’ll do you credit.”\n\n“Captain Cuttle! I couldn’t think of it!” cried Walter, detaining him, for he was running away. “Pray take it back. I have one already.”\n\n“Then, Wal”r,” said the Captain, suddenly diving into one of his pockets and bringing up the two teaspoons and the sugar-tongs, with which he had armed himself to meet such an objection, “take this here trifle of plate, instead.”\n\n“No, no, I couldn’t indeed!” cried Walter, “a thousand thanks! Don’t throw them away, Captain Cuttle!” for the Captain was about to jerk them overboard. “They’ll be of much more use to you than me. Give me your stick. I have often thought I should like to have it. There! Good-bye, Captain Cuttle! Take care of my Uncle! Uncle Sol, God bless you!”\n\nThey were over the side in the confusion, before Walter caught another glimpse of either; and when he ran up to the stern, and looked after them, he saw his Uncle hanging down his head in the boat, and Captain Cuttle rapping him on the back with the great silver watch (it must have been very painful), and gesticulating hopefully with the teaspoons and sugar-tongs. Catching sight of Walter, Captain Cuttle dropped the property into the bottom of the boat with perfect unconcern, being evidently oblivious of its existence, and pulling off the glazed hat hailed him lustily. The glazed hat made quite a show in the sun with its glistening, and the Captain continued to wave it until he could be seen no longer. Then the confusion on board, which had been rapidly increasing, reached its height; two or three other boats went away with a cheer; the sails shone bright and full above, as Walter watched them spread their surface to the favourable breeze; the water flew in sparkles from the prow; and off upon her voyage went the Son and Heir, as hopefully and trippingly as many another son and heir, gone down, had started on his way before her.\n\nDay after day, old Sol and Captain Cuttle kept her reckoning in the little back parlour and worked out her course, with the chart spread before them on the round table. At night, when old Sol climbed upstairs, so lonely, to the attic where it sometimes blew great guns, he looked up at the stars and listened to the wind, and kept a longer watch than would have fallen to his lot on board the ship. The last bottle of the old Madeira, which had had its cruising days, and known its dangers of the deep, lay silently beneath its dust and cobwebs, in the meanwhile, undisturbed.\n\n# CHAPTER XX. Mr Dombey goes upon a Journey\n\nMr Dombey, Sir,” said Major Bagstock, “Joey” B. is not in general a man of sentiment, for Joseph is tough. But Joe has his feelings, Sir, and when they are awakened—Damme, Mr Dombey,” cried the Major with sudden ferocity, “this is weakness, and I won’t submit to it!”\n\nMajor Bagstock delivered himself of these expressions on receiving Mr Dombey as his guest at the head of his own staircase in Princess’s Place. Mr Dombey had come to breakfast with the Major, previous to their setting forth on their trip; and the ill-starved Native had already undergone a world of misery arising out of the muffins, while, in connexion with the general question of boiled eggs, life was a burden to him.\n\n“It is not for an old soldier of the Bagstock breed,” observed the Major, relapsing into a mild state, “to deliver himself up, a prey to his own emotions; but—damme, Sir,” cried the Major, in another spasm of ferocity, “I condole with you!”\n\nThe Major’s purple visage deepened in its hue, and the Major’s lobster eyes stood out in bolder relief, as he shook Mr Dombey by the hand, imparting to that peaceful action as defiant a character as if it had been the prelude to his immediately boxing Mr Dombey for a thousand pounds a side and the championship of England. With a rotatory motion of his head, and a wheeze very like the cough of a horse, the Major then conducted his visitor to the sitting-room, and there welcomed him (having now composed his feelings) with the freedom and frankness of a travelling companion.\n\n“Dombey,” said the Major, “I’m glad to see you. I’m proud to see you. There are not many men in Europe to whom J. Bagstock would say that—for Josh is blunt. Sir: it’s his nature—but Joey B. is proud to see you, Dombey.”\n\n“Major,” returned Mr Dombey, “you are very obliging.”\n\n“No, Sir,” said the Major, “Devil a bit! That’s not my character. If that had been Joe’s character, Joe might have been, by this time, Lieutenant-General Sir Joseph Bagstock, K.C.B., and might have received you in very different quarters. You don’t know old Joe yet, I find. But this occasion, being special, is a source of pride to me. By the Lord, Sir,” said the Major resolutely, “it’s an honour to me!”\n\nMr Dombey, in his estimation of himself and his money, felt that this was very true, and therefore did not dispute the point. But the instinctive recognition of such a truth by the Major, and his plain avowal of it, were very able. It was a confirmation to Mr Dombey, if he had required any, of his not being mistaken in the Major. It was an assurance to him that his power extended beyond his own immediate sphere; and that the Major, as an officer and a gentleman, had a no less becoming sense of it, than the beadle of the Royal Exchange.\n\nAnd if it were ever consolatory to know this, or the like of this, it was consolatory then, when the impotence of his will, the instability of his hopes, the feebleness of wealth, had been so direfully impressed upon him. What could it do, his boy had asked him. Sometimes, thinking of the baby question, he could hardly forbear inquiring, himself, what could it do indeed: what had it done?\n\nBut these were lonely thoughts, bred late at night in the sullen despondency and gloom of his retirement, and pride easily found its reassurance in many testimonies to the truth, as unimpeachable and precious as the Major’s. Mr Dombey, in his friendlessness, inclined to the Major. It cannot be said that he warmed towards him, but he thawed a little, The Major had had some part—and not too much—in the days by the seaside. He was a man of the world, and knew some great people. He talked much, and told stories; and Mr Dombey was disposed to regard him as a choice spirit who shone in society, and who had not that poisonous ingredient of poverty with which choice spirits in general are too much adulterated. His station was undeniable. Altogether the Major was a creditable companion, well accustomed to a life of leisure, and to such places as that they were about to visit, and having an air of gentlemanly ease about him that mixed well enough with his own City character, and did not compete with it at all. If Mr Dombey had any lingering idea that the Major, as a man accustomed, in the way of his calling, to make light of the ruthless hand that had lately crushed his hopes, might unconsciously impart some useful philosophy to him, and scare away his weak regrets, he hid it from himself, and left it lying at the bottom of his pride, unexamined.\n\n“Where is my scoundrel?” said the Major, looking wrathfully round the room.\n\nThe Native, who had no particular name, but answered to any vituperative epithet, presented himself instantly at the door and ventured to come no nearer.\n\n“You villain!” said the choleric Major, “where’s the breakfast?”\n\nThe dark servant disappeared in search of it, and was quickly heard reascending the stairs in such a tremulous state, that the plates and dishes on the tray he carried, trembling sympathetically as he came, rattled again, all the way up.\n\n“Dombey,” said the Major, glancing at the Native as he arranged the table, and encouraging him with an awful shake of his fist when he upset a spoon, “here is a devilled grill, a savoury pie, a dish of kidneys, and so forth. Pray sit down. Old Joe can give you nothing but camp fare, you see.”\n\n“Very excellent fare, Major,” replied his guest; and not in mere politeness either; for the Major always took the best possible care of himself, and indeed ate rather more of rich meats than was good for him, insomuch that his Imperial complexion was mainly referred by the faculty to that circumstance.\n\n“You have been looking over the way, Sir,” observed the Major. “Have you seen our friend?”\n\n“You mean Miss Tox,” retorted Mr Dombey. “No.”\n\n“Charming woman, Sir,” said the Major, with a fat laugh rising in his short throat, and nearly suffocating him.\n\n“Miss Tox is a very good sort of person, I believe,” replied Mr Dombey.\n\nThe haughty coldness of the reply seemed to afford Major Bagstock infinite delight. He swelled and swelled, exceedingly: and even laid down his knife and fork for a moment, to rub his hands.\n\n“Old Joe, Sir,” said the Major, “was a bit of a favourite in that quarter once. But Joe has had his day. J. Bagstock is extinguished—outrivalled—floored, Sir.”\n\n“I should have supposed,” Mr Dombey replied, “that the lady’s day for favourites was over: but perhaps you are jesting, Major.”\n\n“Perhaps you are jesting, Dombey?” was the Major’s rejoinder.\n\nThere never was a more unlikely possibility. It was so clearly expressed in Mr Dombey’s face, that the Major apologised.\n\n“I beg your pardon,” he said. “I see you are in earnest. I tell you what, Dombey.” The Major paused in his eating, and looked mysteriously indignant. “That’s a de-vilish ambitious woman, Sir.”\n\nMr Dombey said “Indeed?” with frigid indifference: mingled perhaps with some contemptuous incredulity as to Miss Tox having the presumption to harbour such a superior quality.\n\n“That woman, Sir,” said the Major, “is, in her way, a Lucifer. Joey B. has had his day, Sir, but he keeps his eyes. He sees, does Joe. His Royal Highness the late Duke of York observed of Joey, at a levee, that he saw.”\n\nThe Major accompanied this with such a look, and, between eating, drinking, hot tea, devilled grill, muffins, and meaning, was altogether so swollen and inflamed about the head, that even Mr Dombey showed some anxiety for him.\n\n“That ridiculous old spectacle, Sir,” pursued the Major, “aspires. She aspires sky-high, Sir. Matrimonially, Dombey.”\n\n“I am sorry for her,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Don’t say that, Dombey,” returned the Major in a warning voice.\n\n“Why should I not, Major?” said Mr Dombey.\n\nThe Major gave no answer but the horse’s cough, and went on eating vigorously.\n\n“She has taken an interest in your household,” said the Major, stopping short again, “and has been a frequent visitor at your house for some time now.”\n\n“Yes,” replied Mr Dombey with great stateliness, “Miss Tox was originally received there, at the time of Mrs Dombey’s death, as a friend of my sister’s; and being a well-behaved person, and showing a liking for the poor infant, she was permitted—may I say encouraged—to repeat her visits with my sister, and gradually to occupy a kind of footing of familiarity in the family. I have,” said Mr Dombey, in the tone of a man who was making a great and valuable concession, “I have a respect for Miss Tox. She has been so obliging as to render many little services in my house: trifling and insignificant services perhaps, Major, but not to be disparaged on that account: and I hope I have had the good fortune to be enabled to acknowledge them by such attention and notice as it has been in my power to bestow. I hold myself indebted to Miss Tox, Major,” added Mr Dombey, with a slight wave of his hand, “for the pleasure of your acquaintance.”\n\n“Dombey,” said the Major, warmly: “no! No, Sir! Joseph Bagstock can never permit that assertion to pass uncontradicted. Your knowledge of old Joe, Sir, such as he is, and old Joe’s knowledge of you, Sir, had its origin in a noble fellow, Sir—in a great creature, Sir. Dombey!” said the Major, with a struggle which it was not very difficult to parade, his whole life being a struggle against all kinds of apoplectic symptoms, “we knew each other through your boy.”\n\nMr Dombey seemed touched, as it is not improbable the Major designed he should be, by this allusion. He looked down and sighed: and the Major, rousing himself fiercely, again said, in reference to the state of mind into which he felt himself in danger of falling, that this was weakness, and nothing should induce him to submit to it.\n\n“Our friend had a remote connexion with that event,” said the Major, “and all the credit that belongs to her, J. B. is willing to give her, Sir. Notwithstanding which, Ma’am,” he added, raising his eyes from his plate, and casting them across Princess’s Place, to where Miss Tox was at that moment visible at her window watering her flowers, “you’re a scheming jade, Ma’am, and your ambition is a piece of monstrous impudence. If it only made yourself ridiculous, Ma’am,” said the Major, rolling his head at the unconscious Miss Tox, while his starting eyes appeared to make a leap towards her, “you might do that to your heart’s content, Ma’am, without any objection, I assure you, on the part of Bagstock.” Here the Major laughed frightfully up in the tips of his ears and in the veins of his head. “But when, Ma’am,” said the Major, “you compromise other people, and generous, unsuspicious people too, as a repayment for their condescension, you stir the blood of old Joe in his body.”\n\n“Major,” said Mr Dombey, reddening, “I hope you do not hint at anything so absurd on the part of Miss Tox as—”\n\n“Dombey,” returned the Major, “I hint at nothing. But Joey B. has lived in the world, Sir: lived in the world with his eyes open, Sir, and his ears cocked: and Joe tells you, Dombey, that there’s a devilish artful and ambitious woman over the way.”\n\nMr Dombey involuntarily glanced over the way; and an angry glance he sent in that direction, too.\n\n“That’s all on such a subject that shall pass the lips of Joseph Bagstock,” said the Major firmly. “Joe is not a tale-bearer, but there are times when he must speak, when he will speak!—confound your arts, Ma’am,” cried the Major, again apostrophising his fair neighbour, with great ire,—“when the provocation is too strong to admit of his remaining silent.”\n\nThe emotion of this outbreak threw the Major into a paroxysm of horse’s coughs, which held him for a long time. On recovering he added:\n\n“And now, Dombey, as you have invited Joe—old Joe, who has no other merit, Sir, but that he is tough and hearty—to be your guest and guide at Leamington, command him in any way you please, and he is wholly yours. I don’t know, Sir,” said the Major, wagging his double chin with a jocose air, “what it is you people see in Joe to make you hold him in such great request, all of you; but this I know, Sir, that if he wasn’t pretty tough, and obstinate in his refusals, you’d kill him among you with your invitations and so forth, in double-quick time.”\n\nMr Dombey, in a few words, expressed his sense of the preference he received over those other distinguished members of society who were clamouring for the possession of Major Bagstock. But the Major cut him short by giving him to understand that he followed his own inclinations, and that they had risen up in a body and said with one accord, “J. B., Dombey is the man for you to choose as a friend.”\n\nThe Major being by this time in a state of repletion, with essence of savoury pie oozing out at the corners of his eyes, and devilled grill and kidneys tightening his cravat: and the time moreover approaching for the departure of the railway train to Birmingham, by which they were to leave town: the Native got him into his great-coat with immense difficulty, and buttoned him up until his face looked staring and gasping, over the top of that garment, as if he were in a barrel. The Native then handed him separately, and with a decent interval between each supply, his washleather gloves, his thick stick, and his hat; which latter article the Major wore with a rakish air on one side of his head, by way of toning down his remarkable visage. The Native had previously packed, in all possible and impossible parts of Mr Dombey’s chariot, which was in waiting, an unusual quantity of carpet-bags and small portmanteaus, no less apoplectic in appearance than the Major himself: and having filled his own pockets with Seltzer water, East India sherry, sandwiches, shawls, telescopes, maps, and newspapers, any or all of which light baggage the Major might require at any instant of the journey, he announced that everything was ready. To complete the equipment of this unfortunate foreigner (currently believed to be a prince in his own country), when he took his seat in the rumble by the side of Mr Towlinson, a pile of the Major’s cloaks and great-coats was hurled upon him by the landlord, who aimed at him from the pavement with those great missiles like a Titan, and so covered him up, that he proceeded, in a living tomb, to the railroad station.\n\nBut before the carriage moved away, and while the Native was in the act of sepulture, Miss Tox appearing at her window, waved a lilywhite handkerchief. Mr Dombey received this parting salutation very coldly—very coldly even for him—and honouring her with the slightest possible inclination of his head, leaned back in the carriage with a very discontented look. His marked behaviour seemed to afford the Major (who was all politeness in his recognition of Miss Tox) unbounded satisfaction; and he sat for a long time afterwards, leering, and choking, like an over-fed Mephistopheles.\n\nDuring the bustle of preparation at the railway, Mr Dombey and the Major walked up and down the platform side by side; the former taciturn and gloomy, and the latter entertaining him, or entertaining himself, with a variety of anecdotes and reminiscences, in most of which Joe Bagstock was the principal performer. Neither of the two observed that in the course of these walks, they attracted the attention of a working man who was standing near the engine, and who touched his hat every time they passed; for Mr Dombey habitually looked over the vulgar herd, not at them; and the Major was looking, at the time, into the core of one of his stories. At length, however, this man stepped before them as they turned round, and pulling his hat off, and keeping it off, ducked his head to Mr Dombey.\n\n“Beg your pardon, Sir,” said the man, “but I hope you’re a doin’ pretty well, Sir.”\n\nHe was dressed in a canvas suit abundantly besmeared with coal-dust and oil, and had cinders in his whiskers, and a smell of half-slaked ashes all over him. He was not a bad-looking fellow, nor even what could be fairly called a dirty-looking fellow, in spite of this; and, in short, he was Mr Toodle, professionally clothed.\n\n“I shall have the honour of stokin’ of you down, Sir,” said Mr Toodle. “Beg your pardon, Sir.—I hope you find yourself a coming round?”\n\nMr Dombey looked at him, in return for his tone of interest, as if a man like that would make his very eyesight dirty.\n\n“’Scuse the liberty, Sir,” said Toodle, seeing he was not clearly remembered, “but my wife Polly, as was called Richards in your family—”\n\nA change in Mr Dombey’s face, which seemed to express recollection of him, and so it did, but it expressed in a much stronger degree an angry sense of humiliation, stopped Mr Toodle short.\n\n“Your wife wants money, I suppose,” said Mr Dombey, putting his hand in his pocket, and speaking (but that he always did) haughtily.\n\n“No thank’ee, Sir,” returned Toodle, “I can’t say she does. I don’t.”\n\nMr Dombey was stopped short now in his turn: and awkwardly: with his hand in his pocket.\n\n“No, Sir,” said Toodle, turning his oilskin cap round and round; “we’re a doin’ pretty well, Sir; we haven’t no cause to complain in the worldly way, Sir. We’ve had four more since then, Sir, but we rubs on.”\n\nMr Dombey would have rubbed on to his own carriage, though in so doing he had rubbed the stoker underneath the wheels; but his attention was arrested by something in connexion with the cap still going slowly round and round in the man’s hand.\n\n“We lost one babby,” observed Toodle, “there’s no denyin’.”\n\n“Lately,” added Mr Dombey, looking at the cap.\n\n“No, Sir, up’ard of three years ago, but all the rest is hearty. And in the matter o readin’, Sir,” said Toodle, ducking again, as if to remind Mr Dombey of what had passed between them on that subject long ago, “them boys o’ mine, they learned me, among ’em, arter all. They’ve made a wery tolerable scholar of me, Sir, them boys.”\n\n“Come, Major!” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Beg your pardon, Sir,” resumed Toodle, taking a step before them and deferentially stopping them again, still cap in hand: “I wouldn’t have troubled you with such a pint except as a way of gettin’ in the name of my son Biler—christened Robin—him as you was so good as to make a Charitable Grinder on.”\n\n“Well, man,” said Mr Dombey in his severest manner. “What about him?”\n\n“Why, Sir,” returned Toodle, shaking his head with a face of great anxiety and distress, “I’m forced to say, Sir, that he’s gone wrong.”\n\n“He has gone wrong, has he?” said Mr Dombey, with a hard kind of satisfaction.\n\n“He has fell into bad company, you see, genelmen,” pursued the father, looking wistfully at both, and evidently taking the Major into the conversation with the hope of having his sympathy. “He has got into bad ways. God send he may come to again, genelmen, but he’s on the wrong track now! You could hardly be off hearing of it somehow, Sir,” said Toodle, again addressing Mr Dombey individually; “and it’s better I should out and say my boy’s gone rather wrong. Polly’s dreadful down about it, genelmen,” said Toodle with the same dejected look, and another appeal to the Major.\n\n“A son of this man’s whom I caused to be educated, Major,” said Mr Dombey, giving him his arm. “The usual return!”\n\n“Take advice from plain old Joe, and never educate that sort of people, Sir,” returned the Major. “Damme, Sir, it never does! It always fails!”\n\nThe simple father was beginning to submit that he hoped his son, the quondam Grinder, huffed and cuffed, and flogged and badged, and taught, as parrots are, by a brute jobbed into his place of schoolmaster with as much fitness for it as a hound, might not have been educated on quite a right plan in some undiscovered respect, when Mr Dombey angrily repeating “The usual return!” led the Major away. And the Major being heavy to hoist into Mr Dombey’s carriage, elevated in mid-air, and having to stop and swear that he would flay the Native alive, and break every bone in his skin, and visit other physical torments upon him, every time he couldn’t get his foot on the step, and fell back on that dark exile, had barely time before they started to repeat hoarsely that it would never do: that it always failed: and that if he were to educate “his own vagabond,” he would certainly be hanged.\n\nMr Dombey assented bitterly; but there was something more in his bitterness, and in his moody way of falling back in the carriage, and looking with knitted brows at the changing objects without, than the failure of that noble educational system administered by the Grinders’ Company. He had seen upon the man’s rough cap a piece of new crape, and he had assured himself, from his manner and his answers, that he wore it for his son.\n\nSo! from high to low, at home or abroad, from Florence in his great house to the coarse churl who was feeding the fire then smoking before them, everyone set up some claim or other to a share in his dead boy, and was a bidder against him! Could he ever forget how that woman had wept over his pillow, and called him her own child! or how he, waking from his sleep, had asked for her, and had raised himself in his bed and brightened when she came in!\n\nTo think of this presumptuous raker among coals and ashes going on before there, with his sign of mourning! To think that he dared to enter, even by a common show like that, into the trial and disappointment of a proud gentleman’s secret heart! To think that this lost child, who was to have divided with him his riches, and his projects, and his power, and allied with whom he was to have shut out all the world as with a double door of gold, should have let in such a herd to insult him with their knowledge of his defeated hopes, and their boasts of claiming community of feeling with himself, so far removed: if not of having crept into the place wherein he would have lorded it, alone!\n\nHe found no pleasure or relief in the journey. Tortured by these thoughts he carried monotony with him, through the rushing landscape, and hurried headlong, not through a rich and varied country, but a wilderness of blighted plans and gnawing jealousies. The very speed at which the train was whirled along, mocked the swift course of the young life that had been borne away so steadily and so inexorably to its foredoomed end. The power that forced itself upon its iron way—its own—defiant of all paths and roads, piercing through the heart of every obstacle, and dragging living creatures of all classes, ages, and degrees behind it, was a type of the triumphant monster, Death.\n\nAway, with a shriek, and a roar, and a rattle, from the town, burrowing among the dwellings of men and making the streets hum, flashing out into the meadows for a moment, mining in through the damp earth, booming on in darkness and heavy air, bursting out again into the sunny day so bright and wide; away, with a shriek, and a roar, and a rattle, through the fields, through the woods, through the corn, through the hay, through the chalk, through the mould, through the clay, through the rock, among objects close at hand and almost in the grasp, ever flying from the traveller, and a deceitful distance ever moving slowly within him: like as in the track of the remorseless monster, Death!\n\nThrough the hollow, on the height, by the heath, by the orchard, by the park, by the garden, over the canal, across the river, where the sheep are feeding, where the mill is going, where the barge is floating, where the dead are lying, where the factory is smoking, where the stream is running, where the village clusters, where the great cathedral rises, where the bleak moor lies, and the wild breeze smooths or ruffles it at its inconstant will; away, with a shriek, and a roar, and a rattle, and no trace to leave behind but dust and vapour: like as in the track of the remorseless monster, Death!\n\nBreasting the wind and light, the shower and sunshine, away, and still away, it rolls and roars, fierce and rapid, smooth and certain, and great works and massive bridges crossing up above, fall like a beam of shadow an inch broad, upon the eye, and then are lost. Away, and still away, onward and onward ever: glimpses of cottage-homes, of houses, mansions, rich estates, of husbandry and handicraft, of people, of old roads and paths that look deserted, small, and insignificant as they are left behind: and so they do, and what else is there but such glimpses, in the track of the indomitable monster, Death!\n\nAway, with a shriek, and a roar, and a rattle, plunging down into the earth again, and working on in such a storm of energy and perseverance, that amidst the darkness and whirlwind the motion seems reversed, and to tend furiously backward, until a ray of light upon the wet wall shows its surface flying past like a fierce stream. Away once more into the day, and through the day, with a shrill yell of exultation, roaring, rattling, tearing on, spurning everything with its dark breath, sometimes pausing for a minute where a crowd of faces are, that in a minute more are not; sometimes lapping water greedily, and before the spout at which it drinks has ceased to drip upon the ground, shrieking, roaring, rattling through the purple distance!\n\nLouder and louder yet, it shrieks and cries as it comes tearing on resistless to the goal: and now its way, still like the way of Death, is strewn with ashes thickly. Everything around is blackened. There are dark pools of water, muddy lanes, and miserable habitations far below. There are jagged walls and falling houses close at hand, and through the battered roofs and broken windows, wretched rooms are seen, where want and fever hide themselves in many wretched shapes, while smoke and crowded gables, and distorted chimneys, and deformity of brick and mortar penning up deformity of mind and body, choke the murky distance. As Mr Dombey looks out of his carriage window, it is never in his thoughts that the monster who has brought him there has let the light of day in on these things: not made or caused them. It was the journey’s fitting end, and might have been the end of everything; it was so ruinous and dreary.\n\nSo, pursuing the one course of thought, he had the one relentless monster still before him. All things looked black, and cold, and deadly upon him, and he on them. He found a likeness to his misfortune everywhere. There was a remorseless triumph going on about him, and it galled and stung him in his pride and jealousy, whatever form it took: though most of all when it divided with him the love and memory of his lost boy.\n\nThere was a face—he had looked upon it, on the previous night, and it on him with eyes that read his soul, though they were dim with tears, and hidden soon behind two quivering hands—that often had attended him in fancy, on this ride. He had seen it, with the expression of last night, timidly pleading to him. It was not reproachful, but there was something of doubt, almost of hopeful incredulity in it, which, as he once more saw that fade away into a desolate certainty of his dislike, was like reproach. It was a trouble to him to think of this face of Florence.\n\nBecause he felt any new compunction towards it? No. Because the feeling it awakened in him—of which he had had some old foreshadowing in older times—was full-formed now, and spoke out plainly, moving him too much, and threatening to grow too strong for his composure. Because the face was abroad, in the expression of defeat and persecution that seemed to encircle him like the air. Because it barbed the arrow of that cruel and remorseless enemy on which his thoughts so ran, and put into its grasp a double-handed sword. Because he knew full well, in his own breast, as he stood there, tinging the scene of transition before him with the morbid colours of his own mind, and making it a ruin and a picture of decay, instead of hopeful change, and promise of better things, that life had quite as much to do with his complainings as death. One child was gone, and one child left. Why was the object of his hope removed instead of her?\n\nThe sweet, calm, gentle presence in his fancy, moved him to no reflection but that. She had been unwelcome to him from the first; she was an aggravation of his bitterness now. If his son had been his only child, and the same blow had fallen on him, it would have been heavy to bear; but infinitely lighter than now, when it might have fallen on her (whom he could have lost, or he believed it, without a pang), and had not. Her loving and innocent face rising before him, had no softening or winning influence. He rejected the angel, and took up with the tormenting spirit crouching in his bosom. Her patience, goodness, youth, devotion, love, were as so many atoms in the ashes upon which he set his heel. He saw her image in the blight and blackness all around him, not irradiating but deepening the gloom. More than once upon this journey, and now again as he stood pondering at this journey’s end, tracing figures in the dust with his stick, the thought came into his mind, what was there he could interpose between himself and it?\n\nThe Major, who had been blowing and panting all the way down, like another engine, and whose eye had often wandered from his newspaper to leer at the prospect, as if there were a procession of discomfited Miss Toxes pouring out in the smoke of the train, and flying away over the fields to hide themselves in any place of refuge, aroused his friends by informing him that the post-horses were harnessed and the carriage ready.\n\n“Dombey,” said the Major, rapping him on the arm with his cane, “don’t be thoughtful. It’s a bad habit, Old Joe, Sir, wouldn’t be as tough as you see him, if he had ever encouraged it. You are too great a man, Dombey, to be thoughtful. In your position, Sir, you’re far above that kind of thing.”\n\nThe Major even in his friendly remonstrances, thus consulting the dignity and honour of Mr Dombey, and showing a lively sense of their importance, Mr Dombey felt more than ever disposed to defer to a gentleman possessing so much good sense and such a well-regulated mind; accordingly he made an effort to listen to the Major’s stories, as they trotted along the turnpike road; and the Major, finding both the pace and the road a great deal better adapted to his conversational powers than the mode of travelling they had just relinquished, came out of his entertainment.\n\nBut still the Major, blunt and tough as he was, and as he so very often said he was, administered some palatable catering to his companion’s appetite. He related, or rather suffered it to escape him, accidentally, and as one might say, grudgingly and against his will, how there was great curiosity and excitement at the club, in regard of his friend Dombey. How he was suffocated with questions, Sir. How old Joe Bagstock was a greater man than ever, there, on the strength of Dombey. How they said, “Bagstock, your friend Dombey now, what is the view he takes of such and such a question? Though, by the Rood, Sir,” said the Major, with a broad stare, “how they discovered that J. B. ever came to know you, is a mystery!”\n\nIn this flow of spirits and conversation, only interrupted by his usual plethoric symptoms, and by intervals of lunch, and from time to time by some violent assault upon the Native, who wore a pair of ear-rings in his dark-brown ears, and on whom his European clothes sat with an outlandish impossibility of adjustment—being, of their own accord, and without any reference to the tailor’s art, long where they ought to be short, short where they ought to be long, tight where they ought to be loose, and loose where they ought to be tight—and to which he imparted a new grace, whenever the Major attacked him, by shrinking into them like a shrivelled nut, or a cold monkey—in this flow of spirits and conversation, the Major continued all day: so that when evening came on, and found them trotting through the green and leafy road near Leamington, the Major’s voice, what with talking and eating and chuckling and choking, appeared to be in the box under the rumble, or in some neighbouring hay-stack. Nor did the Major improve it at the Royal Hotel, where rooms and dinner had been ordered, and where he so oppressed his organs of speech by eating and drinking, that when he retired to bed he had no voice at all, except to cough with, and could only make himself intelligible to the dark servant by gasping at him.\n\nHe not only rose next morning, however, like a giant refreshed, but conducted himself, at breakfast like a giant refreshing. At this meal they arranged their daily habits. The Major was to take the responsibility of ordering everything to eat and drink; and they were to have a late breakfast together every morning, and a late dinner together every day. Mr Dombey would prefer remaining in his own room, or walking in the country by himself, on that first day of their sojourn at Leamington; but next morning he would be happy to accompany the Major to the Pump-room, and about the town. So they parted until dinner-time. Mr Dombey retired to nurse his wholesome thoughts in his own way. The Major, attended by the Native carrying a camp-stool, a great-coat, and an umbrella, swaggered up and down through all the public places: looking into subscription books to find out who was there, looking up old ladies by whom he was much admired, reporting J. B. tougher than ever, and puffing his rich friend Dombey wherever he went. There never was a man who stood by a friend more staunchly than the Major, when in puffing him, he puffed himself.\n\nIt was surprising how much new conversation the Major had to let off at dinner-time, and what occasion he gave Mr Dombey to admire his social qualities. At breakfast next morning, he knew the contents of the latest newspapers received; and mentioned several subjects in connexion with them, on which his opinion had recently been sought by persons of such power and might, that they were only to be obscurely hinted at. Mr Dombey, who had been so long shut up within himself, and who had rarely, at any time, overstepped the enchanted circle within which the operations of Dombey and Son were conducted, began to think this an improvement on his solitary life; and in place of excusing himself for another day, as he had thought of doing when alone, walked out with the Major arm-in-arm.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI. New Faces\n\nThe MAJOR, more blue-faced and staring—more over-ripe, as it were, than ever—and giving vent, every now and then, to one of the horse’s coughs, not so much of necessity as in a spontaneous explosion of importance, walked arm-in-arm with Mr Dombey up the sunny side of the way, with his cheeks swelling over his tight stock, his legs majestically wide apart, and his great head wagging from side to side, as if he were remonstrating within himself for being such a captivating object. They had not walked many yards, before the Major encountered somebody he knew, nor many yards farther before the Major encountered somebody else he knew, but he merely shook his fingers at them as he passed, and led Mr Dombey on: pointing out the localities as they went, and enlivening the walk with any current scandal suggested by them.\n\nIn this manner the Major and Mr Dombey were walking arm-in-arm, much to their own satisfaction, when they beheld advancing towards them, a wheeled chair, in which a lady was seated, indolently steering her carriage by a kind of rudder in front, while it was propelled by some unseen power in the rear. Although the lady was not young, she was very blooming in the face—quite rosy—and her dress and attitude were perfectly juvenile. Walking by the side of the chair, and carrying her gossamer parasol with a proud and weary air, as if so great an effort must be soon abandoned and the parasol dropped, sauntered a much younger lady, very handsome, very haughty, very wilful, who tossed her head and drooped her eyelids, as though, if there were anything in all the world worth looking into, save a mirror, it certainly was not the earth or sky.\n\n“Why, what the devil have we here, Sir!” cried the Major, stopping as this little cavalcade drew near.\n\n“My dearest Edith!” drawled the lady in the chair, “Major Bagstock!”\n\nThe Major no sooner heard the voice, than he relinquished Mr Dombey’s arm, darted forward, took the hand of the lady in the chair and pressed it to his lips. With no less gallantry, the Major folded both his gloves upon his heart, and bowed low to the other lady. And now, the chair having stopped, the motive power became visible in the shape of a flushed page pushing behind, who seemed to have in part outgrown and in part out-pushed his strength, for when he stood upright he was tall, and wan, and thin, and his plight appeared the more forlorn from his having injured the shape of his hat, by butting at the carriage with his head to urge it forward, as is sometimes done by elephants in Oriental countries.\n\n“Joe Bagstock,” said the Major to both ladies, “is a proud and happy man for the rest of his life.”\n\n“You false creature!” said the old lady in the chair, insipidly. “Where do you come from? I can’t bear you.”\n\n“Then suffer old Joe to present a friend, Ma’am,” said the Major, promptly, “as a reason for being tolerated. Mr Dombey, Mrs Skewton.” The lady in the chair was gracious. “Mr Dombey, Mrs Granger.” The lady with the parasol was faintly conscious of Mr Dombey’s taking off his hat, and bowing low. “I am delighted, Sir,” said the Major, “to have this opportunity.”\n\nThe Major seemed in earnest, for he looked at all the three, and leered in his ugliest manner.\n\n“Mrs Skewton, Dombey,” said the Major, “makes havoc in the heart of old Josh.”\n\nMr Dombey signified that he didn’t wonder at it.\n\n“You perfidious goblin,” said the lady in the chair, “have done! How long have you been here, bad man?”\n\n“One day,” replied the Major.\n\n“And can you be a day, or even a minute,” returned the lady, slightly settling her false curls and false eyebrows with her fan, and showing her false teeth, set off by her false complexion, “in the garden of what’s-its-name.”\n\n“Eden, I suppose, Mama,” interrupted the younger lady, scornfully.\n\n“My dear Edith,” said the other, “I cannot help it. I never can remember those frightful names—without having your whole Soul and Being inspired by the sight of Nature; by the perfume,” said Mrs Skewton, rustling a handkerchief that was faint and sickly with essences, “of her artless breath, you creature!”\n\nThe discrepancy between Mrs Skewton’s fresh enthusiasm of words, and forlornly faded manner, was hardly less observable than that between her age, which was about seventy, and her dress, which would have been youthful for twenty-seven. Her attitude in the wheeled chair (which she never varied) was one in which she had been taken in a barouche, some fifty years before, by a then fashionable artist who had appended to his published sketch the name of Cleopatra: in consequence of a discovery made by the critics of the time, that it bore an exact resemblance to that Princess as she reclined on board her galley. Mrs Skewton was a beauty then, and bucks threw wine-glasses over their heads by dozens in her honour. The beauty and the barouche had both passed away, but she still preserved the attitude, and for this reason expressly, maintained the wheeled chair and the butting page: there being nothing whatever, except the attitude, to prevent her from walking.\n\n“Mr Dombey is devoted to Nature, I trust?” said Mrs Skewton, settling her diamond brooch. And by the way, she chiefly lived upon the reputation of some diamonds, and her family connexions.\n\n“My friend Dombey, Ma’am,” returned the Major, “may be devoted to her in secret, but a man who is paramount in the greatest city in the universe—”\n\n“No one can be a stranger,” said Mrs Skewton, “to Mr Dombey’s immense influence.”\n\nAs Mr Dombey acknowledged the compliment with a bend of his head, the younger lady glancing at him, met his eyes.\n\n“You reside here, Madam?” said Mr Dombey, addressing her.\n\n“No, we have been to a great many places. To Harrogate and Scarborough, and into Devonshire. We have been visiting, and resting here and there. Mama likes change.”\n\n“Edith of course does not,” said Mrs Skewton, with a ghastly archness.\n\n“I have not found that there is any change in such places,” was the answer, delivered with supreme indifference.\n\n“They libel me. There is only one change, Mr Dombey,” observed Mrs Skewton, with a mincing sigh, “for which I really care, and that I fear I shall never be permitted to enjoy. People cannot spare one. But seclusion and contemplation are my what-his-name—”\n\n“If you mean Paradise, Mama, you had better say so, to render yourself intelligible,” said the younger lady.\n\n“My dearest Edith,” returned Mrs Skewton, “you know that I am wholly dependent upon you for those odious names. I assure you, Mr Dombey, Nature intended me for an Arcadian. I am thrown away in society. Cows are my passion. What I have ever sighed for, has been to retreat to a Swiss farm, and live entirely surrounded by cows—and china.”\n\nThis curious association of objects, suggesting a remembrance of the celebrated bull who got by mistake into a crockery shop, was received with perfect gravity by Mr Dombey, who intimated his opinion that Nature was, no doubt, a very respectable institution.\n\n“What I want,” drawled Mrs Skewton, pinching her shrivelled throat, “is heart.” It was frightfully true in one sense, if not in that in which she used the phrase. “What I want, is frankness, confidence, less conventionality, and freer play of soul. We are so dreadfully artificial.”\n\nWe were, indeed.\n\n“In short,” said Mrs Skewton, “I want Nature everywhere. It would be so extremely charming.”\n\n“Nature is inviting us away now, Mama, if you are ready,” said the younger lady, curling her handsome lip. At this hint, the wan page, who had been surveying the party over the top of the chair, vanished behind it, as if the ground had swallowed him up.\n\n“Stop a moment, Withers!” said Mrs Skewton, as the chair began to move; calling to the page with all the languid dignity with which she had called in days of yore to a coachman with a wig, cauliflower nosegay, and silk stockings. “Where are you staying, abomination?”\n\nThe Major was staying at the Royal Hotel, with his friend Dombey.\n\n“You may come and see us any evening when you are good,” lisped Mrs Skewton. “If Mr Dombey will honour us, we shall be happy. Withers, go on!”\n\nThe Major again pressed to his blue lips the tips of the fingers that were disposed on the ledge of the wheeled chair with careful carelessness, after the Cleopatra model: and Mr Dombey bowed. The elder lady honoured them both with a very gracious smile and a girlish wave of her hand; the younger lady with the very slightest inclination of her head that common courtesy allowed.\n\nThe last glimpse of the wrinkled face of the mother, with that patched colour on it which the sun made infinitely more haggard and dismal than any want of colour could have been, and of the proud beauty of the daughter with her graceful figure and erect deportment, engendered such an involuntary disposition on the part of both the Major and Mr Dombey to look after them, that they both turned at the same moment. The Page, nearly as much aslant as his own shadow, was toiling after the chair, uphill, like a slow battering-ram; the top of Cleopatra’s bonnet was fluttering in exactly the same corner to the inch as before; and the Beauty, loitering by herself a little in advance, expressed in all her elegant form, from head to foot, the same supreme disregard of everything and everybody.\n\n“I tell you what, Sir,” said the Major, as they resumed their walk again. “If Joe Bagstock were a younger man, there’s not a woman in the world whom he’d prefer for Mrs Bagstock to that woman. By George, Sir!” said the Major, “she’s superb!”\n\n“Do you mean the daughter?” inquired Mr Dombey.\n\n“Is Joey B. a turnip, Dombey,” said the Major, “that he should mean the mother?”\n\n“You were complimentary to the mother,” returned Mr Dombey.\n\n“An ancient flame, Sir,” chuckled Major Bagstock. “Devilish ancient. I humour her.”\n\n“She impresses me as being perfectly genteel,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Genteel, Sir,” said the Major, stopping short, and staring in his companion’s face. “The Honourable Mrs Skewton, Sir, is sister to the late Lord Feenix, and aunt to the present Lord. The family are not wealthy—they’re poor, indeed—and she lives upon a small jointure; but if you come to blood, Sir!” The Major gave a flourish with his stick and walked on again, in despair of being able to say what you came to, if you came to that.\n\n“You addressed the daughter, I observed,” said Mr Dombey, after a short pause, “as Mrs Granger.”\n\n“Edith Skewton, Sir,” returned the Major, stopping short again, and punching a mark in the ground with his cane, to represent her, “married (at eighteen) Granger of Ours;” whom the Major indicated by another punch. “Granger, Sir,” said the Major, tapping the last ideal portrait, and rolling his head emphatically, “was Colonel of Ours; a de-vilish handsome fellow, Sir, of forty-one. He died, Sir, in the second year of his marriage.” The Major ran the representative of the deceased Granger through and through the body with his walking-stick, and went on again, carrying his stick over his shoulder.\n\n“How long is this ago?” asked Mr Dombey, making another halt.\n\n“Edith Granger, Sir,” replied the Major, shutting one eye, putting his head on one side, passing his cane into his left hand, and smoothing his shirt-frill with his right, “is, at this present time, not quite thirty. And damme, Sir,” said the Major, shouldering his stick once more, and walking on again, “she’s a peerless woman!”\n\n“Was there any family?” asked Mr Dombey presently.\n\n“Yes, Sir,” said the Major. “There was a boy.”\n\nMr Dombey’s eyes sought the ground, and a shade came over his face.\n\n“Who was drowned, Sir,” pursued the Major. “When a child of four or five years old.”\n\n“Indeed?” said Mr Dombey, raising his head.\n\n“By the upsetting of a boat in which his nurse had no business to have put him,” said the Major. “That’s his history. Edith Granger is Edith Granger still; but if tough old Joey B., Sir, were a little younger and a little richer, the name of that immortal paragon should be Bagstock.”\n\nThe Major heaved his shoulders, and his cheeks, and laughed more like an over-fed Mephistopheles than ever, as he said the words.\n\n“Provided the lady made no objection, I suppose?” said Mr Dombey coldly.\n\n“By Gad, Sir,” said the Major, “the Bagstock breed are not accustomed to that sort of obstacle. Though it’s true enough that Edith might have married twenty times, but for being proud, Sir, proud.”\n\nMr Dombey seemed, by his face, to think no worse of her for that.\n\n“It’s a great quality after all,” said the Major. “By the Lord, it’s a high quality! Dombey! You are proud yourself, and your friend, Old Joe, respects you for it, Sir.”\n\nWith this tribute to the character of his ally, which seemed to be wrung from him by the force of circumstances and the irresistible tendency of their conversation, the Major closed the subject, and glided into a general exposition of the extent to which he had been beloved and doted on by splendid women and brilliant creatures.\n\nOn the next day but one, Mr Dombey and the Major encountered the Honourable Mrs Skewton and her daughter in the Pump-room; on the day after, they met them again very near the place where they had met them first. After meeting them thus, three or four times in all, it became a point of mere civility to old acquaintances that the Major should go there one evening. Mr Dombey had not originally intended to pay visits, but on the Major announcing this intention, he said he would have the pleasure of accompanying him. So the Major told the Native to go round before dinner, and say, with his and Mr Dombey’s compliments, that they would have the honour of visiting the ladies that same evening, if the ladies were alone. In answer to which message, the Native brought back a very small note with a very large quantity of scent about it, indited by the Honourable Mrs Skewton to Major Bagstock, and briefly saying, “You are a shocking bear and I have a great mind not to forgive you, but if you are very good indeed,” which was underlined, “you may come. Compliments (in which Edith unites) to Mr Dombey.”\n\nThe Honourable Mrs Skewton and her daughter, Mrs Granger, resided, while at Leamington, in lodgings that were fashionable enough and dear enough, but rather limited in point of space and conveniences; so that the Honourable Mrs Skewton, being in bed, had her feet in the window and her head in the fireplace, while the Honourable Mrs Skewton’s maid was quartered in a closet within the drawing-room, so extremely small, that, to avoid developing the whole of its accommodations, she was obliged to writhe in and out of the door like a beautiful serpent. Withers, the wan page, slept out of the house immediately under the tiles at a neighbouring milk-shop; and the wheeled chair, which was the stone of that young Sisyphus, passed the night in a shed belonging to the same dairy, where new-laid eggs were produced by the poultry connected with the establishment, who roosted on a broken donkey-cart, persuaded, to all appearance, that it grew there, and was a species of tree.\n\nMr Dombey and the Major found Mrs Skewton arranged, as Cleopatra, among the cushions of a sofa: very airily dressed; and certainly not resembling Shakespeare’s Cleopatra, whom age could not wither. On their way upstairs they had heard the sound of a harp, but it had ceased on their being announced, and Edith now stood beside it handsomer and haughtier than ever. It was a remarkable characteristic of this lady’s beauty that it appeared to vaunt and assert itself without her aid, and against her will. She knew that she was beautiful: it was impossible that it could be otherwise: but she seemed with her own pride to defy her very self.\n\nWhether she held cheap attractions that could only call forth admiration that was worthless to her, or whether she designed to render them more precious to admirers by this usage of them, those to whom they were precious seldom paused to consider.\n\n“I hope, Mrs Granger,” said Mr Dombey, advancing a step towards her, “we are not the cause of your ceasing to play?”\n\n“You! oh no!”\n“Why do you not go on then, my dearest Edith?” said Cleopatra.\n\n“I left off as I began—of my own fancy.”\n\nThe exquisite indifference of her manner in saying this: an indifference quite removed from dulness or insensibility, for it was pointed with proud purpose: was well set off by the carelessness with which she drew her hand across the strings, and came from that part of the room.\n\n“Do you know, Mr Dombey,” said her languishing mother, playing with a hand-screen, “that occasionally my dearest Edith and myself actually almost differ—”\n\n“Not quite, sometimes, Mama?” said Edith.\n\n“Oh never quite, my darling! Fie, fie, it would break my heart,” returned her mother, making a faint attempt to pat her with the screen, which Edith made no movement to meet, “—about these old conventionalities of manner that are observed in little things? Why are we not more natural? Dear me! With all those yearnings, and gushings, and impulsive throbbings that we have implanted in our souls, and which are so very charming, why are we not more natural?”\n\nMr Dombey said it was very true, very true.\n\n“We could be more natural I suppose if we tried?” said Mrs Skewton.\n\nMr Dombey thought it possible.\n\n“Devil a bit, Ma’am,” said the Major. “We couldn’t afford it. Unless the world was peopled with J.B.“s—tough and blunt old Joes, Ma’am, plain red herrings with hard roes, Sir—we couldn’t afford it. It wouldn’t do.”\n\n“You naughty Infidel,” said Mrs Skewton, “be mute.”\n\n“Cleopatra commands,” returned the Major, kissing his hand, “and Antony Bagstock obeys.”\n\n“The man has no sensitiveness,” said Mrs Skewton, cruelly holding up the hand-screen so as to shut the Major out. “No sympathy. And what do we live for but sympathy! What else is so extremely charming! Without that gleam of sunshine on our cold cold earth,” said Mrs Skewton, arranging her lace tucker, and complacently observing the effect of her bare lean arm, looking upward from the wrist, “how could we possibly bear it? In short, obdurate man!” glancing at the Major, round the screen, “I would have my world all heart; and Faith is so excessively charming, that I won’t allow you to disturb it, do you hear?”\n\nThe Major replied that it was hard in Cleopatra to require the world to be all heart, and yet to appropriate to herself the hearts of all the world; which obliged Cleopatra to remind him that flattery was insupportable to her, and that if he had the boldness to address her in that strain any more, she would positively send him home.\n\nWithers the Wan, at this period, handing round the tea, Mr Dombey again addressed himself to Edith.\n\n“There is not much company here, it would seem?” said Mr Dombey, in his own portentous gentlemanly way.\n\n“I believe not. We see none.”\n\n“Why really,” observed Mrs Skewton from her couch, “there are no people here just now with whom we care to associate.”\n\n“They have not enough heart,” said Edith, with a smile. The very twilight of a smile: so singularly were its light and darkness blended.\n\n“My dearest Edith rallies me, you see!” said her mother, shaking her head: which shook a little of itself sometimes, as if the palsy twinkled now and then in opposition to the diamonds. “Wicked one!”\n\n“You have been here before, if I am not mistaken?” said Mr Dombey. Still to Edith.\n\n“Oh, several times. I think we have been everywhere.”\n\n“A beautiful country!”\n\n“I suppose it is. Everybody says so.”\n\n“Your cousin Feenix raves about it, Edith,” interposed her mother from her couch.\n\nThe daughter slightly turned her graceful head, and raising her eyebrows by a hair’s-breadth, as if her cousin Feenix were of all the mortal world the least to be regarded, turned her eyes again towards Mr Dombey.\n\n“I hope, for the credit of my good taste, that I am tired of the neighbourhood,” she said.\n\n“You have almost reason to be, Madam,” he replied, glancing at a variety of landscape drawings, of which he had already recognised several as representing neighbouring points of view, and which were strewn abundantly about the room, “if these beautiful productions are from your hand.”\n\nShe gave him no reply, but sat in a disdainful beauty, quite amazing.\n\n“Have they that interest?” said Mr Dombey. “Are they yours?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And you play, I already know.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And sing?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nShe answered all these questions with a strange reluctance; and with that remarkable air of opposition to herself, already noticed as belonging to her beauty. Yet she was not embarrassed, but wholly self-possessed. Neither did she seem to wish to avoid the conversation, for she addressed her face, and—so far as she could—her manner also, to him; and continued to do so, when he was silent.\n\n“You have many resources against weariness at least,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Whatever their efficiency may be,” she returned, “you know them all now. I have no more.”\n\n“May I hope to prove them all?” said Mr Dombey, with solemn gallantry, laying down a drawing he had held, and motioning towards the harp.\n\n“Oh certainly! If you desire it!”\n\nShe rose as she spoke, and crossing by her mother’s couch, and directing a stately look towards her, which was instantaneous in its duration, but inclusive (if anyone had seen it) of a multitude of expressions, among which that of the twilight smile, without the smile itself, overshadowed all the rest, went out of the room.\n\nThe Major, who was quite forgiven by this time, had wheeled a little table up to Cleopatra, and was sitting down to play picquet with her. Mr Dombey, not knowing the game, sat down to watch them for his edification until Edith should return.\n\n“We are going to have some music, Mr Dombey, I hope?” said Cleopatra.\n\n“Mrs Granger has been kind enough to promise so,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Ah! That’s very nice. Do you propose, Major?”\n\n“No, Ma’am,” said the Major. “Couldn’t do it.”\n\n“You’re a barbarous being,” replied the lady, “and my hand’s destroyed. You are fond of music, Mr Dombey?”\n\n“Eminently so,” was Mr Dombey’s answer.\n\n“Yes. It’s very nice,” said Cleopatra, looking at her cards. “So much heart in it—undeveloped recollections of a previous state of existence—and all that—which is so truly charming. Do you know,” simpered Cleopatra, reversing the knave of clubs, who had come into her game with his heels uppermost, “that if anything could tempt me to put a period to my life, it would be curiosity to find out what it’s all about, and what it means; there are so many provoking mysteries, really, that are hidden from us. Major, you to play!”\n\nThe Major played; and Mr Dombey, looking on for his instruction, would soon have been in a state of dire confusion, but that he gave no attention to the game whatever, and sat wondering instead when Edith would come back.\n\nShe came at last, and sat down to her harp, and Mr Dombey rose and stood beside her, listening. He had little taste for music, and no knowledge of the strain she played, but he saw her bending over it, and perhaps he heard among the sounding strings some distant music of his own, that tamed the monster of the iron road, and made it less inexorable.\n\nCleopatra had a sharp eye, verily, at picquet. It glistened like a bird’s, and did not fix itself upon the game, but pierced the room from end to end, and gleamed on harp, performer, listener, everything.\n\nWhen the haughty beauty had concluded, she arose, and receiving Mr Dombey’s thanks and compliments in exactly the same manner as before, went with scarcely any pause to the piano, and began there.\n\nEdith Granger, any song but that! Edith Granger, you are very handsome, and your touch upon the keys is brilliant, and your voice is deep and rich; but not the air that his neglected daughter sang to his dead son!\n\nAlas, he knows it not; and if he did, what air of hers would stir him, rigid man! Sleep, lonely Florence, sleep! Peace in thy dreams, although the night has turned dark, and the clouds are gathering, and threaten to discharge themselves in hail!\n\n# CHAPTER XXII. A Trifle of Management by Mr Carker the Manager\n\nMr Carker the Manager sat at his desk, smooth and soft as usual, reading those letters which were reserved for him to open, backing them occasionally with such memoranda and references as their business purport required, and parcelling them out into little heaps for distribution through the several departments of the House. The post had come in heavy that morning, and Mr Carker the Manager had a good deal to do.\n\nThe general action of a man so engaged—pausing to look over a bundle of papers in his hand, dealing them round in various portions, taking up another bundle and examining its contents with knitted brows and pursed-out lips—dealing, and sorting, and pondering by turns—would easily suggest some whimsical resemblance to a player at cards. The face of Mr Carker the Manager was in good keeping with such a fancy. It was the face of a man who studied his play, warily: who made himself master of all the strong and weak points of the game: who registered the cards in his mind as they fell about him, knew exactly what was on them, what they missed, and what they made: who was crafty to find out what the other players held, and who never betrayed his own hand.\n\nThe letters were in various languages, but Mr Carker the Manager read them all. If there had been anything in the offices of Dombey and Son that he could read, there would have been a card wanting in the pack. He read almost at a glance, and made combinations of one letter with another and one business with another as he went on, adding new matter to the heaps—much as a man would know the cards at sight, and work out their combinations in his mind after they were turned. Something too deep for a partner, and much too deep for an adversary, Mr Carker the Manager sat in the rays of the sun that came down slanting on him through the skylight, playing his game alone.\n\nAnd although it is not among the instincts wild or domestic of the cat tribe to play at cards, feline from sole to crown was Mr Carker the Manager, as he basked in the strip of summer-light and warmth that shone upon his table and the ground as if they were a crooked dial-plate, and himself the only figure on it. With hair and whiskers deficient in colour at all times, but feebler than common in the rich sunshine, and more like the coat of a sandy tortoise-shell cat; with long nails, nicely pared and sharpened; with a natural antipathy to any speck of dirt, which made him pause sometimes and watch the falling motes of dust, and rub them off his smooth white hand or glossy linen: Mr Carker the Manager, sly of manner, sharp of tooth, soft of foot, watchful of eye, oily of tongue, cruel of heart, nice of habit, sat with a dainty steadfastness and patience at his work, as if he were waiting at a mouse’s hole.\n\nAt length the letters were disposed of, excepting one which he reserved for a particular audience. Having locked the more confidential correspondence in a drawer, Mr Carker the Manager rang his bell.\n\n“Why do you answer it?” was his reception of his brother.\n\n“The messenger is out, and I am the next,” was the submissive reply.\n\n“You are the next?” muttered the Manager. “Yes! Creditable to me! There!”\n\nPointing to the heaps of opened letters, he turned disdainfully away, in his elbow-chair, and broke the seal of that one which he held in his hand.\n\n“I am sorry to trouble you, James,” said the brother, gathering them up, “but—”\n\n“Oh! you have something to say. I knew that. Well?”\n\nMr Carker the Manager did not raise his eyes or turn them on his brother, but kept them on his letter, though without opening it.\n\n“Well?” he repeated sharply.\n\n“I am uneasy about Harriet.”\n\n“Harriet who? what Harriet? I know nobody of that name.”\n\n“She is not well, and has changed very much of late.”\n\n“She changed very much, a great many years ago,” replied the Manager; “and that is all I have to say.\n\n“I think if you would hear me—\n\n“Why should I hear you, Brother John?” returned the Manager, laying a sarcastic emphasis on those two words, and throwing up his head, but not lifting his eyes. “I tell you, Harriet Carker made her choice many years ago between her two brothers. She may repent it, but she must abide by it.”\n\n“Don’t mistake me. I do not say she does repent it. It would be black ingratitude in me to hint at such a thing,” returned the other. “Though believe me, James, I am as sorry for her sacrifice as you.”\n\n“As I?” exclaimed the Manager. “As I?”\n\n“As sorry for her choice—for what you call her choice—as you are angry at it,” said the Junior.\n\n“Angry?” repeated the other, with a wide show of his teeth.\n\n“Displeased. Whatever word you like best. You know my meaning. There is no offence in my intention.”\n\n“There is offence in everything you do,” replied his brother, glancing at him with a sudden scowl, which in a moment gave place to a wider smile than the last. “Carry those papers away, if you please. I am busy.\n\nHis politeness was so much more cutting than his wrath, that the Junior went to the door. But stopping at it, and looking round, he said:\n\n“When Harriet tried in vain to plead for me with you, on your first just indignation, and my first disgrace; and when she left you, James, to follow my broken fortunes, and devote herself, in her mistaken affection, to a ruined brother, because without her he had no one, and was lost; she was young and pretty. I think if you could see her now—if you would go and see her—she would move your admiration and compassion.”\n\nThe Manager inclined his head, and showed his teeth, as who should say, in answer to some careless small-talk, “Dear me! Is that the case?” but said never a word.\n\n“We thought in those days: you and I both: that she would marry young, and lead a happy and light-hearted life,” pursued the other. “Oh if you knew how cheerfully she cast those hopes away; how cheerfully she has gone forward on the path she took, and never once looked back; you never could say again that her name was strange in your ears. Never!”\n\nAgain the Manager inclined his head and showed his teeth, and seemed to say, “Remarkable indeed! You quite surprise me!” And again he uttered never a word.\n\n“May I go on?” said John Carker, mildly.\n\n“On your way?” replied his smiling brother. “If you will have the goodness.”\n\nJohn Carker, with a sigh, was passing slowly out at the door, when his brother’s voice detained him for a moment on the threshold.\n\n“If she has gone, and goes, her own way cheerfully,” he said, throwing the still unfolded letter on his desk, and putting his hands firmly in his pockets, “you may tell her that I go as cheerfully on mine. If she has never once looked back, you may tell her that I have, sometimes, to recall her taking part with you, and that my resolution is no easier to wear away;” he smiled very sweetly here; “than marble.”\n\n“I tell her nothing of you. We never speak about you. Once a year, on your birthday, Harriet says always, ‘Let us remember James by name, and wish him happy,’ but we say no more.”\n\n“Tell it then, if you please,” returned the other, “to yourself. You can’t repeat it too often, as a lesson to you to avoid the subject in speaking to me. I know no Harriet Carker. There is no such person. You may have a sister; make much of her. I have none.”\n\nMr Carker the Manager took up the letter again, and waved it with a smile of mock courtesy towards the door. Unfolding it as his brother withdrew, and looking darkly after him as he left the room, he once more turned round in his elbow-chair, and applied himself to a diligent perusal of its contents.\n\nIt was in the writing of his great chief, Mr Dombey, and dated from Leamington. Though he was a quick reader of all other letters, Mr Carker read this slowly; weighing the words as he went, and bringing every tooth in his head to bear upon them. When he had read it through once, he turned it over again, and picked out these passages. “I find myself benefited by the change, and am not yet inclined to name any time for my return.” “I wish, Carker, you would arrange to come down once and see me here, and let me know how things are going on, in person.” “I omitted to speak to you about young Gay. If not gone per Son and Heir, or if Son and Heir still lying in the Docks, appoint some other young man and keep him in the City for the present. I am not decided.” “Now that’s unfortunate!” said Mr Carker the Manager, expanding his mouth, as if it were made of India-rubber: “for he’s far away.”\n\nStill that passage, which was in a postscript, attracted his attention and his teeth, once more.\n\n“I think,” he said, “my good friend Captain Cuttle mentioned something about being towed along in the wake of that day. What a pity he’s so far away!”\n\nHe refolded the letter, and was sitting trifling with it, standing it long-wise and broad-wise on his table, and turning it over and over on all sides—doing pretty much the same thing, perhaps, by its contents—when Mr Perch the messenger knocked softly at the door, and coming in on tiptoe, bending his body at every step as if it were the delight of his life to bow, laid some papers on the table.\n\n“Would you please to be engaged, Sir?” asked Mr Perch, rubbing his hands, and deferentially putting his head on one side, like a man who felt he had no business to hold it up in such a presence, and would keep it as much out of the way as possible.\n\n“Who wants me?”\n\n“Why, Sir,” said Mr Perch, in a soft voice, “really nobody, Sir, to speak of at present. Mr Gills the Ship’s Instrument-maker, Sir, has looked in, about a little matter of payment, he says: but I mentioned to him, Sir, that you was engaged several deep; several deep.”\n\nMr Perch coughed once behind his hand, and waited for further orders.\n\n“Anybody else?”\n\n“Well, Sir,” said Mr Perch, “I wouldn’t of my own self take the liberty of mentioning, Sir, that there was anybody else; but that same young lad that was here yesterday, Sir, and last week, has been hanging about the place; and it looks, Sir,” added Mr Perch, stopping to shut the door, “dreadful unbusiness-like to see him whistling to the sparrows down the court, and making of ’em answer him.”\n\n“You said he wanted something to do, didn’t you, Perch?” asked Mr Carker, leaning back in his chair and looking at that officer.\n\n“Why, Sir,” said Mr Perch, coughing behind his hand again, “his expression certainly were that he was in wants of a sitiwation, and that he considered something might be done for him about the Docks, being used to fishing with a rod and line: but—” Mr Perch shook his head very dubiously indeed.\n\n“What does he say when he comes?” asked Mr Carker.\n\n“Indeed, Sir,” said Mr Perch, coughing another cough behind his hand, which was always his resource as an expression of humility when nothing else occurred to him, “his observation generally air that he would humbly wish to see one of the gentlemen, and that he wants to earn a living. But you see, Sir,” added Perch, dropping his voice to a whisper, and turning, in the inviolable nature of his confidence, to give the door a thrust with his hand and knee, as if that would shut it any more when it was shut already, “it’s hardly to be bore, Sir, that a common lad like that should come a prowling here, and saying that his mother nursed our House’s young gentleman, and that he hopes our House will give him a chance on that account. I am sure, Sir,” observed Mr Perch, “that although Mrs Perch was at that time nursing as thriving a little girl, Sir, as we’ve ever took the liberty of adding to our family, I wouldn’t have made so free as drop a hint of her being capable of imparting nourishment, not if it was never so!”\n\nMr Carker grinned at him like a shark, but in an absent, thoughtful manner.\n\n“Whether,” submitted Mr Perch, after a short silence, and another cough, “it mightn’t be best for me to tell him, that if he was seen here any more he would be given into custody; and to keep to it! With respect to bodily fear,” said Mr Perch, “I’m so timid, myself, by nature, Sir, and my nerves is so unstrung by Mrs Perch’s state, that I could take my affidavit easy.”\n\n“Let me see this fellow, Perch,” said Mr Carker. “Bring him in!”\n\n“Yes, Sir. Begging your pardon, Sir,” said Mr Perch, hesitating at the door, “he’s rough, Sir, in appearance.”\n\n“Never mind. If he’s there, bring him in. I’ll see Mr Gills directly. Ask him to wait.”\n\nMr Perch bowed; and shutting the door, as precisely and carefully as if he were not coming back for a week, went on his quest among the sparrows in the court. While he was gone, Mr Carker assumed his favourite attitude before the fire-place, and stood looking at the door; presenting, with his under lip tucked into the smile that showed his whole row of upper teeth, a singularly crouching apace.\n\nThe messenger was not long in returning, followed by a pair of heavy boots that came bumping along the passage like boxes. With the unceremonious words “Come along with you!”—a very unusual form of introduction from his lips—Mr Perch then ushered into the presence a strong-built lad of fifteen, with a round red face, a round sleek head, round black eyes, round limbs, and round body, who, to carry out the general rotundity of his appearance, had a round hat in his hand, without a particle of brim to it.\n\nObedient to a nod from Mr Carker, Perch had no sooner confronted the visitor with that gentleman than he withdrew. The moment they were face to face alone, Mr Carker, without a word of preparation, took him by the throat, and shook him until his head seemed loose upon his shoulders.\n\nThe boy, who in the midst of his astonishment could not help staring wildly at the gentleman with so many white teeth who was choking him, and at the office walls, as though determined, if he were choked, that his last look should be at the mysteries for his intrusion into which he was paying such a severe penalty, at last contrived to utter—\n\n“Come, Sir! You let me alone, will you!”\n\n“Let you alone!” said Mr Carker. “What! I have got you, have I?” There was no doubt of that, and tightly too. “You dog,” said Mr Carker, through his set jaws, “I’ll strangle you!”\n\nBiler whimpered, would he though? oh no he wouldn’t—and what was he doing of—and why didn’t he strangle some—body of his own size and not him: but Biler was quelled by the extraordinary nature of his reception, and, as his head became stationary, and he looked the gentleman in the face, or rather in the teeth, and saw him snarling at him, he so far forgot his manhood as to cry.\n\n“I haven’t done nothing to you, Sir,” said Biler, otherwise Rob, otherwise Grinder, and always Toodle.\n\n“You young scoundrel!” replied Mr Carker, slowly releasing him, and moving back a step into his favourite position. “What do you mean by daring to come here?”\n\n“I didn’t mean no harm, Sir,” whimpered Rob, putting one hand to his throat, and the knuckles of the other to his eyes. “I’ll never come again, Sir. I only wanted work.”\n\n“Work, young Cain that you are!” repeated Mr Carker, eyeing him narrowly. “Ain’t you the idlest vagabond in London?”\n\nThe impeachment, while it much affected Mr Toodle Junior, attached to his character so justly, that he could not say a word in denial. He stood looking at the gentleman, therefore, with a frightened, self-convicted, and remorseful air. As to his looking at him, it may be observed that he was fascinated by Mr Carker, and never took his round eyes off him for an instant.\n\n“Ain’t you a thief?” said Mr Carker, with his hands behind him in his pockets.\n\n“No, sir,” pleaded Rob.\n\n“You are!” said Mr Carker.\n\n“I ain’t indeed, Sir,” whimpered Rob. “I never did such a thing as thieve, Sir, if you’ll believe me. I know I’ve been a going wrong, Sir, ever since I took to bird-catching and walking-matching. I’m sure a cove might think,” said Mr Toodle Junior, with a burst of penitence, “that singing birds was innocent company, but nobody knows what harm is in them little creeturs and what they brings you down to.”\n\nThey seemed to have brought him down to a velveteen jacket and trousers very much the worse for wear, a particularly small red waistcoat like a gorget, an interval of blue check, and the hat before mentioned.\n\n“I ain’t been home twenty times since them birds got their will of me,” said Rob, “and that’s ten months. How can I go home when everybody’s miserable to see me! I wonder,” said Biler, blubbering outright, and smearing his eyes with his coat-cuff, “that I haven’t been and drownded myself over and over again.”\n\nAll of which, including his expression of surprise at not having achieved this last scarce performance, the boy said, just as if the teeth of Mr Carker drew it out of him, and he had no power of concealing anything with that battery of attraction in full play.\n\n“You’re a nice young gentleman!” said Mr Carker, shaking his head at him. “There’s hemp-seed sown for you, my fine fellow!”\n\n“I’m sure, Sir,” returned the wretched Biler, blubbering again, and again having recourse to his coat-cuff: “I shouldn’t care, sometimes, if it was growed too. My misfortunes all began in wagging, Sir; but what could I do, exceptin’ wag?”\n\n“Excepting what?” said Mr Carker.\n\n“Wag, Sir. Wagging from school.”\n\n“Do you mean pretending to go there, and not going?” said Mr Carker.\n\n“Yes, Sir, that’s wagging, Sir,” returned the quondam Grinder, much affected. “I was chivied through the streets, Sir, when I went there, and pounded when I got there. So I wagged, and hid myself, and that began it.”\n\n“And you mean to tell me,” said Mr Carker, taking him by the throat again, holding him out at arm’s-length, and surveying him in silence for some moments, “that you want a place, do you?”\n\n“I should be thankful to be tried, Sir,” returned Toodle Junior, faintly.\n\nMr Carker the Manager pushed him backward into a corner—the boy submitting quietly, hardly venturing to breathe, and never once removing his eyes from his face—and rang the bell.\n\n“Tell Mr Gills to come here.”\n\nMr Perch was too deferential to express surprise or recognition of the figure in the corner: and Uncle Sol appeared immediately.\n\n“Mr Gills!” said Carker, with a smile, “sit down. How do you do? You continue to enjoy your health, I hope?”\n\n“Thank you, Sir,” returned Uncle Sol, taking out his pocket-book, and handing over some notes as he spoke. “Nothing ails me in body but old age. Twenty-five, Sir.”\n\n“You are as punctual and exact, Mr Gills,” replied the smiling Manager, taking a paper from one of his many drawers, and making an endorsement on it, while Uncle Sol looked over him, “as one of your own chronometers. Quite right.”\n\n“The Son and Heir has not been spoken, I find by the list, Sir,” said Uncle Sol, with a slight addition to the usual tremor in his voice.\n\n“The Son and Heir has not been spoken,” returned Carker. “There seems to have been tempestuous weather, Mr Gills, and she has probably been driven out of her course.”\n\n“She is safe, I trust in Heaven!” said old Sol.\n\n“She is safe, I trust in Heaven!” assented Mr Carker in that voiceless manner of his: which made the observant young Toodle tremble again. “Mr Gills,” he added aloud, throwing himself back in his chair, “you must miss your nephew very much?”\n\nUncle Sol, standing by him, shook his head and heaved a deep sigh.\n\n“Mr Gills,” said Carker, with his soft hand playing round his mouth, and looking up into the Instrument-maker’s face, “it would be company to you to have a young fellow in your shop just now, and it would be obliging me if you would give one house-room for the present. No, to be sure,” he added quickly, in anticipation of what the old man was going to say, “there’s not much business doing there, I know; but you can make him clean the place out, polish up the instruments; drudge, Mr Gills. That’s the lad!”\n\nSol Gills pulled down his spectacles from his forehead to his eyes, and looked at Toodle Junior standing upright in the corner: his head presenting the appearance (which it always did) of having been newly drawn out of a bucket of cold water; his small waistcoat rising and falling quickly in the play of his emotions; and his eyes intently fixed on Mr Carker, without the least reference to his proposed master.\n\n“Will you give him house-room, Mr Gills?” said the Manager.\n\nOld Sol, without being quite enthusiastic on the subject, replied that he was glad of any opportunity, however slight, to oblige Mr Carker, whose wish on such a point was a command: and that the wooden Midshipman would consider himself happy to receive in his berth any visitor of Mr Carker’s selecting.\n\nMr Carker bared himself to the tops and bottoms of his gums: making the watchful Toodle Junior tremble more and more: and acknowledged the Instrument-maker’s politeness in his most affable manner.\n\n“I’ll dispose of him so, then, Mr Gills,” he answered, rising, and shaking the old man by the hand, “until I make up my mind what to do with him, and what he deserves. As I consider myself responsible for him, Mr Gills,” here he smiled a wide smile at Rob, who shook before it: “I shall be glad if you’ll look sharply after him, and report his behaviour to me. I’ll ask a question or two of his parents as I ride home this afternoon—respectable people—to confirm some particulars in his own account of himself; and that done, Mr Gills, I’ll send him round to you to-morrow morning. Goodbye!”\n\nHis smile at parting was so full of teeth, that it confused old Sol, and made him vaguely uncomfortable. He went home, thinking of raging seas, foundering ships, drowning men, an ancient bottle of Madeira never brought to light, and other dismal matters.\n\n“Now, boy!” said Mr Carker, putting his hand on young Toodle’s shoulder, and bringing him out into the middle of the room. “You have heard me?”\n\nRob said, “Yes, Sir.”\n\n“Perhaps you understand,” pursued his patron, “that if you ever deceive or play tricks with me, you had better have drowned yourself, indeed, once for all, before you came here?”\n\nThere was nothing in any branch of mental acquisition that Rob seemed to understand better than that.\n\n“If you have lied to me,” said Mr Carker, “in anything, never come in my way again. If not, you may let me find you waiting for me somewhere near your mother’s house this afternoon. I shall leave this at five o’clock, and ride there on horseback. Now, give me the address.”\n\nRob repeated it slowly, as Mr Carker wrote it down. Rob even spelt it over a second time, letter by letter, as if he thought that the omission of a dot or scratch would lead to his destruction. Mr Carker then handed him out of the room; and Rob, keeping his round eyes fixed upon his patron to the last, vanished for the time being.\n\nMr Carker the Manager did a great deal of business in the course of the day, and bestowed his teeth upon a great many people. In the office, in the court, in the street, and on “Change, they glistened and bristled to a terrible extent. Five o’clock arriving, and with it Mr Carker’s bay horse, they got on horseback, and went gleaming up Cheapside.\n\nAs no one can easily ride fast, even if inclined to do so, through the press and throng of the City at that hour, and as Mr Carker was not inclined, he went leisurely along, picking his way among the carts and carriages, avoiding whenever he could the wetter and more dirty places in the over-watered road, and taking infinite pains to keep himself and his steed clean. Glancing at the passersby while he was thus ambling on his way, he suddenly encountered the round eyes of the sleek-headed Rob intently fixed upon his face as if they had never been taken off, while the boy himself, with a pocket-handkerchief twisted up like a speckled eel and girded round his waist, made a very conspicuous demonstration of being prepared to attend upon him, at whatever pace he might think proper to go.\n\nThis attention, however flattering, being one of an unusual kind, and attracting some notice from the other passengers, Mr Carker took advantage of a clearer thoroughfare and a cleaner road, and broke into a trot. Rob immediately did the same. Mr Carker presently tried a canter; Rob was still in attendance. Then a short gallop; it was all one to the boy. Whenever Mr Carker turned his eyes to that side of the road, he still saw Toodle Junior holding his course, apparently without distress, and working himself along by the elbows after the most approved manner of professional gentlemen who get over the ground for wagers.\n\nRidiculous as this attendance was, it was a sign of an influence established over the boy, and therefore Mr Carker, affecting not to notice it, rode away into the neighbourhood of Mr Toodle’s house. On his slackening his pace here, Rob appeared before him to point out the turnings; and when he called to a man at a neighbouring gateway to hold his horse, pending his visit to the buildings that had succeeded Staggs’s Gardens, Rob dutifully held the stirrup, while the Manager dismounted.\n\n“Now, Sir,” said Mr Carker, taking him by the shoulder, “come along!”\n\nThe prodigal son was evidently nervous of visiting the parental abode; but Mr Carker pushing him on before, he had nothing for it but to open the right door, and suffer himself to be walked into the midst of his brothers and sisters, mustered in overwhelming force round the family tea-table. At sight of the prodigal in the grasp of a stranger, these tender relations united in a general howl, which smote upon the prodigal’s breast so sharply when he saw his mother stand up among them, pale and trembling, with the baby in her arms, that he lent his own voice to the chorus.\n\nNothing doubting now that the stranger, if not Mr Ketch in person, was one of that company, the whole of the young family wailed the louder, while its more infantine members, unable to control the transports of emotion appertaining to their time of life, threw themselves on their backs like young birds when terrified by a hawk, and kicked violently. At length, poor Polly making herself audible, said, with quivering lips, “Oh Rob, my poor boy, what have you done at last!”\n\n“Nothing, mother,” cried Rob, in a piteous voice, “ask the gentleman!”\n\n“Don’t be alarmed,” said Mr Carker, “I want to do him good.”\n\nAt this announcement, Polly, who had not cried yet, began to do so. The elder Toodles, who appeared to have been meditating a rescue, unclenched their fists. The younger Toodles clustered round their mother’s gown, and peeped from under their own chubby arms at their desperado brother and his unknown friend. Everybody blessed the gentleman with the beautiful teeth, who wanted to do good.\n\n“This fellow,” said Mr Carker to Polly, giving him a gentle shake, “is your son, eh, Ma’am?”\n\n“Yes, Sir,” sobbed Polly, with a curtsey; “yes, Sir.”\n\n“A bad son, I am afraid?” said Mr Carker.\n\n“Never a bad son to me, Sir,” returned Polly.\n“To whom then?” demanded Mr Carker.\n\n“He has been a little wild, Sir,” returned Polly, checking the baby, who was making convulsive efforts with his arms and legs to launch himself on Biler, through the ambient air, “and has gone with wrong companions: but I hope he has seen the misery of that, Sir, and will do well again.”\n\nMr Carker looked at Polly, and the clean room, and the clean children, and the simple Toodle face, combined of father and mother, that was reflected and repeated everywhere about him—and seemed to have achieved the real purpose of his visit.\n\n“Your husband, I take it, is not at home?” he said.\n\n“No, Sir,” replied Polly. “He’s down the line at present.”\n\nThe prodigal Rob seemed very much relieved to hear it: though still in the absorption of all his faculties in his patron, he hardly took his eyes from Mr Carker’s face, unless for a moment at a time to steal a sorrowful glance at his mother.\n\n“Then,” said Mr Carker, “I’ll tell you how I have stumbled on this boy of yours, and who I am, and what I am going to do for him.”\n\nThis Mr Carker did, in his own way; saying that he at first intended to have accumulated nameless terrors on his presumptuous head, for coming to the whereabout of Dombey and Son. That he had relented, in consideration of his youth, his professed contrition, and his friends. That he was afraid he took a rash step in doing anything for the boy, and one that might expose him to the censure of the prudent; but that he did it of himself and for himself, and risked the consequences single-handed; and that his mother’s past connexion with Mr Dombey’s family had nothing to do with it, and that Mr Dombey had nothing to do with it, but that he, Mr Carker, was the be-all and the end-all of this business. Taking great credit to himself for his goodness, and receiving no less from all the family then present, Mr Carker signified, indirectly but still pretty plainly, that Rob’s implicit fidelity, attachment, and devotion, were for evermore his due, and the least homage he could receive. And with this great truth Rob himself was so impressed, that, standing gazing on his patron with tears rolling down his cheeks, he nodded his shiny head until it seemed almost as loose as it had done under the same patron’s hands that morning.\n\nPolly, who had passed Heaven knows how many sleepless nights on account of this her dissipated firstborn, and had not seen him for weeks and weeks, could have almost kneeled to Mr Carker the Manager, as to a Good Spirit—in spite of his teeth. But Mr Carker rising to depart, she only thanked him with her mother’s prayers and blessings; thanks so rich when paid out of the Heart’s mint, especially for any service Mr Carker had rendered, that he might have given back a large amount of change, and yet been overpaid.\n\nAs that gentleman made his way among the crowding children to the door, Rob retreated on his mother, and took her and the baby in the same repentant hug.\n\n“I’ll try hard, dear mother, now. Upon my soul I will!” said Rob.\n\n“Oh do, my dear boy! I am sure you will, for our sakes and your own!” cried Polly, kissing him. “But you’re coming back to speak to me, when you have seen the gentleman away?”\n\n“I don’t know, mother.” Rob hesitated, and looked down. “Father—when’s he coming home?”\n\n“Not till two o’clock to-morrow morning.”\n\n“I’ll come back, mother dear!” cried Rob. And passing through the shrill cry of his brothers and sisters in reception of this promise, he followed Mr Carker out.\n\n“What!” said Mr Carker, who had heard this. “You have a bad father, have you?”\n\n“No, Sir!” returned Rob, amazed. “There ain’t a better nor a kinder father going, than mine is.”\n\n“Why don’t you want to see him then?” inquired his patron.\n\n“There’s such a difference between a father and a mother, Sir,” said Rob, after faltering for a moment. “He couldn’t hardly believe yet that I was doing to do better—though I know he’d try to—but a mother—she always believes what’s good, Sir; at least, I know my mother does, God bless her!”\n\nMr Carker’s mouth expanded, but he said no more until he was mounted on his horse, and had dismissed the man who held it, when, looking down from the saddle steadily into the attentive and watchful face of the boy, he said:\n\n“You’ll come to me tomorrow morning, and you shall be shown where that old gentleman lives; that old gentleman who was with me this morning; where you are going, as you heard me say.”\n\n“Yes, Sir,” returned Rob.\n\n“I have a great interest in that old gentleman, and in serving him, you serve me, boy, do you understand? Well,” he added, interrupting him, for he saw his round face brighten when he was told that: “I see you do. I want to know all about that old gentleman, and how he goes on from day to day—for I am anxious to be of service to him—and especially who comes there to see him. Do you understand?”\n\nRob nodded his steadfast face, and said “Yes, Sir,” again.\n\n“I should like to know that he has friends who are attentive to him, and that they don’t desert him—for he lives very much alone now, poor fellow; but that they are fond of him, and of his nephew who has gone abroad. There is a very young lady who may perhaps come to see him. I want particularly to know all about her.”\n\n“I’ll take care, Sir,” said the boy.\n\n“And take care,” returned his patron, bending forward to advance his grinning face closer to the boy’s, and pat him on the shoulder with the handle of his whip: “take care you talk about affairs of mine to nobody but me.”\n\n“To nobody in the world, Sir,” replied Rob, shaking his head.\n\n“Neither there,” said Mr Carker, pointing to the place they had just left, “nor anywhere else. I’ll try how true and grateful you can be. I’ll prove you!” Making this, by his display of teeth and by the action of his head, as much a threat as a promise, he turned from Rob’s eyes, which were nailed upon him as if he had won the boy by a charm, body and soul, and rode away. But again becoming conscious, after trotting a short distance, that his devoted henchman, girt as before, was yielding him the same attendance, to the great amusement of sundry spectators, he reined up, and ordered him off. To ensure his obedience, he turned in the saddle and watched him as he retired. It was curious to see that even then Rob could not keep his eyes wholly averted from his patron’s face, but, constantly turning and turning again to look after him, involved himself in a tempest of buffetings and jostlings from the other passengers in the street: of which, in the pursuit of the one paramount idea, he was perfectly heedless.\n\nMr Carker the Manager rode on at a foot-pace, with the easy air of one who had performed all the business of the day in a satisfactory manner, and got it comfortably off his mind. Complacent and affable as man could be, Mr Carker picked his way along the streets and hummed a soft tune as he went. He seemed to purr, he was so glad.\n\nAnd in some sort, Mr Carker, in his fancy, basked upon a hearth too. Coiled up snugly at certain feet, he was ready for a spring, Or for a tear, or for a scratch, or for a velvet touch, as the humour took him and occasion served. Was there any bird in a cage, that came in for a share of his regards?\n\n“A very young lady!” thought Mr Carker the Manager, through his song. “Ay! when I saw her last, she was a little child. With dark eyes and hair, I recollect, and a good face; a very good face! I daresay she’s pretty.”\n\nMore affable and pleasant yet, and humming his song until his many teeth vibrated to it, Mr Carker picked his way along, and turned at last into the shady street where Mr Dombey’s house stood. He had been so busy, winding webs round good faces, and obscuring them with meshes, that he hardly thought of being at this point of his ride, until, glancing down the cold perspective of tall houses, he reined in his horse quickly within a few yards of the door. But to explain why Mr Carker reined in his horse quickly, and what he looked at in no small surprise, a few digressive words are necessary.\n\nMr Toots, emancipated from the Blimber thraldom and coming into the possession of a certain portion of his worldly wealth, “which,” as he had been wont, during his last half-year’s probation, to communicate to Mr Feeder every evening as a new discovery, “the executors couldn’t keep him out of” had applied himself with great diligence, to the science of Life. Fired with a noble emulation to pursue a brilliant and distinguished career, Mr Toots had furnished a choice set of apartments; had established among them a sporting bower, embellished with the portraits of winning horses, in which he took no particle of interest; and a divan, which made him poorly. In this delicious abode, Mr Toots devoted himself to the cultivation of those gentle arts which refine and humanise existence, his chief instructor in which was an interesting character called the Game Chicken, who was always to be heard of at the bar of the Black Badger, wore a shaggy white great-coat in the warmest weather, and knocked Mr Toots about the head three times a week, for the small consideration of ten and six per visit.\n\nThe Game Chicken, who was quite the Apollo of Mr Toots’s Pantheon, had introduced to him a marker who taught billiards, a Life Guard who taught fencing, a jobmaster who taught riding, a Cornish gentleman who was up to anything in the athletic line, and two or three other friends connected no less intimately with the fine arts. Under whose auspices Mr Toots could hardly fail to improve apace, and under whose tuition he went to work.\n\nBut however it came about, it came to pass, even while these gentlemen had the gloss of novelty upon them, that Mr Toots felt, he didn’t know how, unsettled and uneasy. There were husks in his corn, that even Game Chickens couldn’t peck up; gloomy giants in his leisure, that even Game Chickens couldn’t knock down. Nothing seemed to do Mr Toots so much good as incessantly leaving cards at Mr Dombey’s door. No taxgatherer in the British Dominions—that wide-spread territory on which the sun never sets, and where the tax-gatherer never goes to bed—was more regular and persevering in his calls than Mr Toots.\n\nMr Toots never went upstairs; and always performed the same ceremonies, richly dressed for the purpose, at the hall door.\n\n“Oh! Good morning!” would be Mr Toots’s first remark to the servant. “For Mr Dombey,” would be Mr Toots’s next remark, as he handed in a card. “For Miss Dombey,” would be his next, as he handed in another.\n\nMr Toots would then turn round as if to go away; but the man knew him by this time, and knew he wouldn’t.\n\n“Oh, I beg your pardon,” Mr Toots would say, as if a thought had suddenly descended on him. “Is the young woman at home?”\n\nThe man would rather think she was, but wouldn’t quite know. Then he would ring a bell that rang upstairs, and would look up the staircase, and would say, yes, she was at home, and was coming down. Then Miss Nipper would appear, and the man would retire.\n\n“Oh! How de do?” Mr Toots would say, with a chuckle and a blush.\n\nSusan would thank him, and say she was very well.\n\n“How’s Diogenes going on?” would be Mr Toots’s second interrogation.\n\nVery well indeed. Miss Florence was fonder and fonder of him every day. Mr Toots was sure to hail this with a burst of chuckles, like the opening of a bottle of some effervescent beverage.\n\n“Miss Florence is quite well, Sir,” Susan would add.\n\n“Oh, it’s of no consequence, thank’ee,” was the invariable reply of Mr Toots; and when he had said so, he always went away very fast.\n\nNow it is certain that Mr Toots had a filmy something in his mind, which led him to conclude that if he could aspire successfully in the fulness of time, to the hand of Florence, he would be fortunate and blest. It is certain that Mr Toots, by some remote and roundabout road, had got to that point, and that there he made a stand. His heart was wounded; he was touched; he was in love. He had made a desperate attempt, one night, and had sat up all night for the purpose, to write an acrostic on Florence, which affected him to tears in the conception. But he never proceeded in the execution further than the words “For when I gaze,”—the flow of imagination in which he had previously written down the initial letters of the other seven lines, deserting him at that point.\n\nBeyond devising that very artful and politic measure of leaving a card for Mr Dombey daily, the brain of Mr Toots had not worked much in reference to the subject that held his feelings prisoner. But deep consideration at length assured Mr Toots that an important step to gain, was, the conciliation of Miss Susan Nipper, preparatory to giving her some inkling of his state of mind.\n\nA little light and playful gallantry towards this lady seemed the means to employ in that early chapter of the history, for winning her to his interests. Not being able quite to make up his mind about it, he consulted the Chicken—without taking that gentleman into his confidence; merely informing him that a friend in Yorkshire had written to him (Mr Toots) for his opinion on such a question. The Chicken replying that his opinion always was, “Go in and win,” and further, “When your man’s before you and your work cut out, go in and do it,” Mr Toots considered this a figurative way of supporting his own view of the case, and heroically resolved to kiss Miss Nipper next day.\n\nUpon the next day, therefore, Mr Toots, putting into requisition some of the greatest marvels that Burgess and Co. had ever turned out, went off to Mr Dombey’s upon this design. But his heart failed him so much as he approached the scene of action, that, although he arrived on the ground at three o’clock in the afternoon, it was six before he knocked at the door.\n\nEverything happened as usual, down to the point where Susan said her young mistress was well, and Mr Toots said it was of no consequence. To her amazement, Mr Toots, instead of going off, like a rocket, after that observation, lingered and chuckled.\n\n“Perhaps you’d like to walk upstairs, Sir!” said Susan.\n\n“Well, I think I will come in!” said Mr Toots.\n\nBut instead of walking upstairs, the bold Toots made an awkward plunge at Susan when the door was shut, and embracing that fair creature, kissed her on the cheek.\n\n“Go along with you!” cried Susan, “or Ill tear your eyes out.”\n\n“Just another!” said Mr Toots.\n\n“Go along with you!” exclaimed Susan, giving him a push “Innocents like you, too! Who’ll begin next? Go along, Sir!”\n\nSusan was not in any serious strait, for she could hardly speak for laughing; but Diogenes, on the staircase, hearing a rustling against the wall, and a shuffling of feet, and seeing through the banisters that there was some contention going on, and foreign invasion in the house, formed a different opinion, dashed down to the rescue, and in the twinkling of an eye had Mr Toots by the leg.\n\nSusan screamed, laughed, opened the street-door, and ran downstairs; the bold Toots tumbled staggering out into the street, with Diogenes holding on to one leg of his pantaloons, as if Burgess and Co. were his cooks, and had provided that dainty morsel for his holiday entertainment; Diogenes shaken off, rolled over and over in the dust, got up again, whirled round the giddy Toots and snapped at him: and all this turmoil Mr Carker, reigning up his horse and sitting a little at a distance, saw to his amazement, issue from the stately house of Mr Dombey.\n\nMr Carker remained watching the discomfited Toots, when Diogenes was called in, and the door shut: and while that gentleman, taking refuge in a doorway near at hand, bound up the torn leg of his pantaloons with a costly silk handkerchief that had formed part of his expensive outfit for the advent.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Sir,” said Mr Carker, riding up, with his most propitiatory smile. “I hope you are not hurt?”\n\n“Oh no, thank you,” replied Mr Toots, raising his flushed face, “it’s of no consequence” Mr Toots would have signified, if he could, that he liked it very much.\n\n“If the dog’s teeth have entered the leg, Sir—” began Carker, with a display of his own.\n\n“No, thank you,” said Mr Toots, “it’s all quite right. It’s very comfortable, thank you.”\n\n“I have the pleasure of knowing Mr Dombey,” observed Carker.\n\n“Have you though?” rejoined the blushing Took\n\n“And you will allow me, perhaps, to apologise, in his absence,” said Mr Carker, taking off his hat, “for such a misadventure, and to wonder how it can possibly have happened.”\n\nMr Toots is so much gratified by this politeness, and the lucky chance of making friends with a friend of Mr Dombey, that he pulls out his card-case which he never loses an opportunity of using, and hands his name and address to Mr Carker: who responds to that courtesy by giving him his own, and with that they part.\n\nAs Mr Carker picks his way so softly past the house, looking up at the windows, and trying to make out the pensive face behind the curtain looking at the children opposite, the rough head of Diogenes came clambering up close by it, and the dog, regardless of all soothing, barks and growls, and makes at him from that height, as if he would spring down and tear him limb from limb.\n\nWell spoken, Di, so near your Mistress! Another, and another with your head up, your eyes flashing, and your vexed mouth worrying itself, for want of him! Another, as he picks his way along! You have a good scent, Di,—cats, boy, cats!\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII. Florence solitary, and the Midshipman mysterious\n\nFlorence lived alone in the great dreary house, and day succeeded day, and still she lived alone; and the blank walls looked down upon her with a vacant stare, as if they had a Gorgon-like mind to stare her youth and beauty into stone.\n\nNo magic dwelling-place in magic story, shut up in the heart of a thick wood, was ever more solitary and deserted to the fancy, than was her father’s mansion in its grim reality, as it stood lowering on the street: always by night, when lights were shining from neighbouring windows, a blot upon its scanty brightness; always by day, a frown upon its never-smiling face.\n\nThere were not two dragon sentries keeping ward before the gate of this above, as in magic legend are usually found on duty over the wronged innocence imprisoned; but besides a glowering visage, with its thin lips parted wickedly, that surveyed all comers from above the archway of the door, there was a monstrous fantasy of rusty iron, curling and twisting like a petrifaction of an arbour over threshold, budding in spikes and corkscrew points, and bearing, one on either side, two ominous extinguishers, that seemed to say, “Who enter here, leave light behind!” There were no talismanic characters engraven on the portal, but the house was now so neglected in appearance, that boys chalked the railings and the pavement—particularly round the corner where the side wall was—and drew ghosts on the stable door; and being sometimes driven off by Mr Towlinson, made portraits of him, in return, with his ears growing out horizontally from under his hat. Noise ceased to be, within the shadow of the roof. The brass band that came into the street once a week, in the morning, never brayed a note in at those windows; but all such company, down to a poor little piping organ of weak intellect, with an imbecile party of automaton dancers, waltzing in and out at folding-doors, fell off from it with one accord, and shunned it as a hopeless place.\n\nThe spell upon it was more wasting than the spell that used to set enchanted houses sleeping once upon a time, but left their waking freshness unimpaired.\n\nThe passive desolation of disuse was everywhere silently manifest about it. Within doors, curtains, drooping heavily, lost their old folds and shapes, and hung like cumbrous palls. Hecatombs of furniture, still piled and covered up, shrunk like imprisoned and forgotten men, and changed insensibly. Mirrors were dim as with the breath of years. Patterns of carpets faded and became perplexed and faint, like the memory of those years’ trifling incidents. Boards, starting at unwonted footsteps, creaked and shook. Keys rusted in the locks of doors. Damp started on the walls, and as the stains came out, the pictures seemed to go in and secrete themselves. Mildew and mould began to lurk in closets. Fungus trees grew in corners of the cellars. Dust accumulated, nobody knew whence nor how; spiders, moths, and grubs were heard of every day. An exploratory blackbeetle now and then was found immovable upon the stairs, or in an upper room, as wondering how he got there. Rats began to squeak and scuffle in the night time, through dark galleries they mined behind the panelling.\n\nThe dreary magnificence of the state rooms, seen imperfectly by the doubtful light admitted through closed shutters, would have answered well enough for an enchanted abode. Such as the tarnished paws of gilded lions, stealthily put out from beneath their wrappers; the marble lineaments of busts on pedestals, fearfully revealing themselves through veils; the clocks that never told the time, or, if wound up by any chance, told it wrong, and struck unearthly numbers, which are not upon the dial; the accidental tinklings among the pendant lustres, more startling than alarm-bells; the softened sounds and laggard air that made their way among these objects, and a phantom crowd of others, shrouded and hooded, and made spectral of shape. But, besides, there was the great staircase, where the lord of the place so rarely set his foot, and by which his little child had gone up to Heaven. There were other staircases and passages where no one went for weeks together; there were two closed rooms associated with dead members of the family, and with whispered recollections of them; and to all the house but Florence, there was a gentle figure moving through the solitude and gloom, that gave to every lifeless thing a touch of present human interest and wonder.\n\nFor Florence lived alone in the deserted house, and day succeeded day, and still she lived alone, and the cold walls looked down upon her with a vacant stare, as if they had a Gorgon-like mind to stare her youth and beauty into stone.\n\nThe grass began to grow upon the roof, and in the crevices of the basement paving. A scaly crumbling vegetation sprouted round the window-sills. Fragments of mortar lost their hold upon the insides of the unused chimneys, and came dropping down. The two trees with the smoky trunks were blighted high up, and the withered branches domineered above the leaves, Through the whole building white had turned yellow, yellow nearly black; and since the time when the poor lady died, it had slowly become a dark gap in the long monotonous street.\n\nBut Florence bloomed there, like the king’s fair daughter in the story. Her books, her music, and her daily teachers, were her only real companions, Susan Nipper and Diogenes excepted: of whom the former, in her attendance on the studies of her young mistress, began to grow quite learned herself, while the latter, softened possibly by the same influences, would lay his head upon the window-ledge, and placidly open and shut his eyes upon the street, all through a summer morning; sometimes pricking up his head to look with great significance after some noisy dog in a cart, who was barking his way along, and sometimes, with an exasperated and unaccountable recollection of his supposed enemy in the neighbourhood, rushing to the door, whence, after a deafening disturbance, he would come jogging back with a ridiculous complacency that belonged to him, and lay his jaw upon the window-ledge again, with the air of a dog who had done a public service.\n\nSo Florence lived in her wilderness of a home, within the circle of her innocent pursuits and thoughts, and nothing harmed her. She could go down to her father’s rooms now, and think of him, and suffer her loving heart humbly to approach him, without fear of repulse. She could look upon the objects that had surrounded him in his sorrow, and could nestle near his chair, and not dread the glance that she so well remembered. She could render him such little tokens of her duty and service, as putting everything in order for him with her own hands, binding little nosegays for table, changing them as one by one they withered and he did not come back, preparing something for him every day, and leaving some timid mark of her presence near his usual seat. Today, it was a little painted stand for his watch; tomorrow she would be afraid to leave it, and would substitute some other trifle of her making not so likely to attract his eye. Waking in the night, perhaps, she would tremble at the thought of his coming home and angrily rejecting it, and would hurry down with slippered feet and quickly beating heart, and bring it away. At another time, she would only lay her face upon his desk, and leave a kiss there, and a tear.\n\nStill no one knew of this. Unless the household found it out when she was not there—and they all held Mr Dombey’s rooms in awe—it was as deep a secret in her breast as what had gone before it. Florence stole into those rooms at twilight, early in the morning, and at times when meals were served downstairs. And although they were in every nook the better and the brighter for her care, she entered and passed out as quietly as any sunbeam, opting that she left her light behind.\n\nShadowy company attended Florence up and down the echoing house, and sat with her in the dismantled rooms. As if her life were an enchanted vision, there arose out of her solitude ministering thoughts, that made it fanciful and unreal. She imagined so often what her life would have been if her father could have loved her and she had been a favourite child, that sometimes, for the moment, she almost believed it was so, and, borne on by the current of that pensive fiction, seemed to remember how they had watched her brother in his grave together; how they had freely shared his heart between them; how they were united in the dear remembrance of him; how they often spoke about him yet; and her kind father, looking at her gently, told her of their common hope and trust in God. At other times she pictured to herself her mother yet alive. And oh the happiness of falling on her neck, and clinging to her with the love and confidence of all her soul! And oh the desolation of the solitary house again, with evening coming on, and no one there!\n\nBut there was one thought, scarcely shaped out to herself, yet fervent and strong within her, that upheld Florence when she strove and filled her true young heart, so sorely tried, with constancy of purpose. Into her mind, as into all others contending with the great affliction of our mortal nature, there had stolen solemn wonderings and hopes, arising in the dim world beyond the present life, and murmuring, like faint music, of recognition in the far-off land between her brother and her mother: of some present consciousness in both of her: some love and commiseration for her: and some knowledge of her as she went her way upon the earth. It was a soothing consolation to Florence to give shelter to these thoughts, until one day—it was soon after she had last seen her father in his own room, late at night—the fancy came upon her, that, in weeping for his alienated heart, she might stir the spirits of the dead against him. Wild, weak, childish, as it may have been to think so, and to tremble at the half-formed thought, it was the impulse of her loving nature; and from that hour Florence strove against the cruel wound in her breast, and tried to think of him whose hand had made it, only with hope.\n\nHer father did not know—she held to it from that time—how much she loved him. She was very young, and had no mother, and had never learned, by some fault or misfortune, how to express to him that she loved him. She would be patient, and would try to gain that art in time, and win him to a better knowledge of his only child.\n\nThis became the purpose of her life. The morning sun shone down upon the faded house, and found the resolution bright and fresh within the bosom of its solitary mistress, Through all the duties of the day, it animated her; for Florence hoped that the more she knew, and the more accomplished she became, the more glad he would be when he came to know and like her. Sometimes she wondered, with a swelling heart and rising tear, whether she was proficient enough in anything to surprise him when they should become companions. Sometimes she tried to think if there were any kind of knowledge that would bespeak his interest more readily than another. Always: at her books, her music, and her work: in her morning walks, and in her nightly prayers: she had her engrossing aim in view. Strange study for a child, to learn the road to a hard parent’s heart!\n\nThere were many careless loungers through the street, as the summer evening deepened into night, who glanced across the road at the sombre house, and saw the youthful figure at the window, such a contrast to it, looking upward at the stars as they began to shine, who would have slept the worse if they had known on what design she mused so steadfastly. The reputation of the mansion as a haunted house, would not have been the gayer with some humble dwellers elsewhere, who were struck by its external gloom in passing and repassing on their daily avocations, and so named it, if they could have read its story in the darkening face. But Florence held her sacred purpose, unsuspected and unaided: and studied only how to bring her father to the understanding that she loved him, and made no appeal against him in any wandering thought.\n\nThus Florence lived alone in the deserted house, and day succeeded day, and still she lived alone, and the monotonous walls looked down upon her with a stare, as if they had a Gorgon-like intent to stare her youth and beauty into stone.\n\nSusan Nipper stood opposite to her young mistress one morning, as she folded and sealed a note she had been writing: and showed in her looks an approving knowledge of its contents.\n\n“Better late than never, dear Miss Floy,” said Susan, “and I do say, that even a visit to them old Skettleses will be a Godsend.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV. The Study of a Loving Heart\n\n“It is very good of Sir Barnet and Lady Skettles, Susan,” returned Florence, with a mild correction of that young lady’s familiar mention of the family in question, “to repeat their invitation so kindly.”\n\nMiss Nipper, who was perhaps the most thoroughgoing partisan on the face of the earth, and who carried her partisanship into all matters great or small, and perpetually waged war with it against society, screwed up her lips and shook her head, as a protest against any recognition of disinterestedness in the Skettleses, and a plea in bar that they would have valuable consideration for their kindness, in the company of Florence.\n\n“They know what they’re about, if ever people did,” murmured Miss Nipper, drawing in her breath “oh! trust them Skettleses for that!”\n\n“I am not very anxious to go to Fulham, Susan, I confess,” said Florence thoughtfully: “but it will be right to go. I think it will be better.”\n\n“Much better,” interposed Susan, with another emphatic shake of her head.\n\n“And so,” said Florence, “though I would prefer to have gone when there was no one there, instead of in this vacation time, when it seems there are some young people staying in the house, I have thankfully said yes.”\n\n“For which I say, Miss Floy, Oh be joyful!” returned Susan, “Ah! h—h!”\n\nThis last ejaculation, with which Miss Nipper frequently wound up a sentence, at about that epoch of time, was supposed below the level of the hall to have a general reference to Mr Dombey, and to be expressive of a yearning in Miss Nipper to favour that gentleman with a piece of her mind. But she never explained it; and it had, in consequence, the charm of mystery, in addition to the advantage of the sharpest expression.\n\n“How long it is before we have any news of Walter, Susan!” observed Florence, after a moment’s silence.\n\n“Long indeed, Miss Floy!” replied her maid. “And Perch said, when he came just now to see for letters—but what signifies what he says!” exclaimed Susan, reddening and breaking off. “Much he knows about it!”\n\nFlorence raised her eyes quickly, and a flush overspread her face.\n\n“If I hadn’t,” said Susan Nipper, evidently struggling with some latent anxiety and alarm, and looking full at her young mistress, while endeavouring to work herself into a state of resentment with the unoffending Mr Perch’s image, “if I hadn’t more manliness than that insipidest of his sex, I’d never take pride in my hair again, but turn it up behind my ears, and wear coarse caps, without a bit of border, until death released me from my insignificance. I may not be a Amazon, Miss Floy, and wouldn’t so demean myself by such disfigurement, but anyways I’m not a giver up, I hope.”\n\n“Give up! What?” cried Florence, with a face of terror.\n\n“Why, nothing, Miss,” said Susan. “Good gracious, nothing! It’s only that wet curl-paper of a man, Perch, that anyone might almost make away with, with a touch, and really it would be a blessed event for all parties if someone would take pity on him, and would have the goodness!”\n\n“Does he give up the ship, Susan?” inquired Florence, very pale.\n\n“No, Miss,” returned Susan, “I should like to see him make so bold as do it to my face! No, Miss, but he goes on about some bothering ginger that Mr Walter was to send to Mrs Perch, and shakes his dismal head, and says he hopes it may be coming; anyhow, he says, it can’t come now in time for the intended occasion, but may do for next, which really,” said Miss Nipper, with aggravated scorn, “puts me out of patience with the man, for though I can bear a great deal, I am not a camel, neither am I,” added Susan, after a moment’s consideration, “if I know myself, a dromedary neither.”\n\n“What else does he say, Susan?” inquired Florence, earnestly. “Won’t you tell me?”\n\n“As if I wouldn’t tell you anything, Miss Floy, and everything!” said Susan. “Why, nothing Miss, he says that there begins to be a general talk about the ship, and that they have never had a ship on that voyage half so long unheard of, and that the Captain’s wife was at the office yesterday, and seemed a little put out about it, but anyone could say that, we knew nearly that before.”\n\n“I must visit Walter’s uncle,” said Florence, hurriedly, “before I leave home. I will go and see him this morning. Let us walk there, directly, Susan.”\n\nMiss Nipper having nothing to urge against the proposal, but being perfectly acquiescent, they were soon equipped, and in the streets, and on their way towards the little Midshipman.\n\nThe state of mind in which poor Walter had gone to Captain Cuttle’s, on the day when Brogley the broker came into possession, and when there seemed to him to be an execution in the very steeples, was pretty much the same as that in which Florence now took her way to Uncle Sol’s; with this difference, that Florence suffered the added pain of thinking that she had been, perhaps, the innocent occasion of involving Walter in peril, and all to whom he was dear, herself included, in an agony of suspense. For the rest, uncertainty and danger seemed written upon everything. The weathercocks on spires and housetops were mysterious with hints of stormy wind, and pointed, like so many ghostly fingers, out to dangerous seas, where fragments of great wrecks were drifting, perhaps, and helpless men were rocked upon them into a sleep as deep as the unfathomable waters. When Florence came into the City, and passed gentlemen who were talking together, she dreaded to hear them speaking of the ship, and saying it was lost. Pictures and prints of vessels fighting with the rolling waves filled her with alarm. The smoke and clouds, though moving gently, moved too fast for her apprehensions, and made her fear there was a tempest blowing at that moment on the ocean.\nSusan Nipper may or may not have been affected similarly, but having her attention much engaged in struggles with boys, whenever there was any press of people—for, between that grade of human kind and herself, there was some natural animosity that invariably broke out, whenever they came together—it would seem that she had not much leisure on the road for intellectual operations.\n\nArriving in good time abreast of the wooden Midshipman on the opposite side of the way, and waiting for an opportunity to cross the street, they were a little surprised at first to see, at the Instrument-maker’s door, a round-headed lad, with his chubby face addressed towards the sky, who, as they looked at him, suddenly thrust into his capacious mouth two fingers of each hand, and with the assistance of that machinery whistled, with astonishing shrillness, to some pigeons at a considerable elevation in the air.\n\n“Mrs Richards’s eldest, Miss!” said Susan, “and the worrit of Mrs Richards’s life!”\n\nAs Polly had been to tell Florence of the resuscitated prospects of her son and heir, Florence was prepared for the meeting: so, a favourable moment presenting itself, they both hastened across, without any further contemplation of Mrs Richards’s bane. That sporting character, unconscious of their approach, again whistled with his utmost might, and then yelled in a rapture of excitement, “Strays! Whoo-oop! Strays!” which identification had such an effect upon the conscience-stricken pigeons, that instead of going direct to some town in the North of England, as appeared to have been their original intention, they began to wheel and falter; whereupon Mrs Richards’s first born pierced them with another whistle, and again yelled, in a voice that rose above the turmoil of the street, “Strays! Whoo-oop! Strays!”\n\nFrom this transport, he was abruptly recalled to terrestrial objects, by a poke from Miss Nipper, which sent him into the shop.\n\n“Is this the way you show your penitence, when Mrs Richards has been fretting for you months and months?” said Susan, following the poke. “Where’s Mr Gills?”\n\nRob, who smoothed his first rebellious glance at Miss Nipper when he saw Florence following, put his knuckles to his hair, in honour of the latter, and said to the former, that Mr Gills was out.”\n\n“Fetch him home,” said Miss Nipper, with authority, “and say that my young lady’s here.”\n\n“I don’t know where he’s gone,” said Rob.\n\n“Is that your penitence?” cried Susan, with stinging sharpness.\n\n“Why how can I go and fetch him when I don’t know where to go?” whimpered the baited Rob. “How can you be so unreasonable?”\n\n“Did Mr Gills say when he should be home?” asked Florence.\n\n“Yes, Miss,” replied Rob, with another application of his knuckles to his hair. “He said he should be home early in the afternoon; in about a couple of hours from now, Miss.”\n\n“Is he very anxious about his nephew?” inquired Susan.\n\n“Yes, Miss,” returned Rob, preferring to address himself to Florence and slighting Nipper; “I should say he was, very much so. He ain’t indoors, Miss, not a quarter of an hour together. He can’t settle in one place five minutes. He goes about, like a—just like a stray,” said Rob, stooping to get a glimpse of the pigeons through the window, and checking himself, with his fingers half-way to his mouth, on the verge of another whistle.\n\n“Do you know a friend of Mr Gills, called Captain Cuttle?” inquired Florence, after a moment’s reflection.\n\n“Him with a hook, Miss?” rejoined Rob, with an illustrative twist of his left hand. Yes, Miss. He was here the day before yesterday.”\n\n“Has he not been here since?” asked Susan.\n\n“No, Miss,” returned Rob, still addressing his reply to Florence.\n\n“Perhaps Walter’s Uncle has gone there, Susan,” observed Florence, turning to her.\n\n“To Captain Cuttle’s, Miss?” interposed Rob; “no, he’s not gone there, Miss. Because he left particular word that if Captain Cuttle called, I should tell him how surprised he was, not to have seen him yesterday, and should make him stop till he came back.”\n\n“Do you know where Captain Cuttle lives?” asked Florence.\n\nRob replied in the affirmative, and turning to a greasy parchment book on the shop desk, read the address aloud.\n\nFlorence again turned to her maid and took counsel with her in a low voice, while Rob the round-eyed, mindful of his patron’s secret charge, looked on and listened. Florence proposed that they could go to Captain Cuttle’s house; hear from his own lips, what he thought of the absence of any tidings of the Son and Heir; and bring him, if they could, to comfort Uncle Sol. Susan at first objected slightly, on the score of distance; but a hackney-coach being mentioned by her mistress, withdrew that opposition, and gave in her assent. There were some minutes of discussion between them before they came to this conclusion, during which the staring Rob paid close attention to both speakers, and inclined his ear to each by turns, as if he were appointed arbitrator of the argument.\n\nIn time, Rob was despatched for a coach, the visitors keeping shop meanwhile; and when he brought it, they got into it, leaving word for Uncle Sol that they would be sure to call again, on their way back. Rob having stared after the coach until it was as invisible as the pigeons had now become, sat down behind the desk with a most assiduous demeanour; and in order that he might forget nothing of what had transpired, made notes of it on various small scraps of paper, with a vast expenditure of ink. There was no danger of these documents betraying anything, if accidentally lost; for long before a word was dry, it became as profound a mystery to Rob, as if he had had no part whatever in its production.\n\nWhile he was yet busy with these labours, the hackney-coach, after encountering unheard-of difficulties from swivel-bridges, soft roads, impassable canals, caravans of casks, settlements of scarlet-beans and little wash-houses, and many such obstacles abounding in that country, stopped at the corner of Brig Place. Alighting here, Florence and Susan Nipper walked down the street, and sought out the abode of Captain Cuttle.\n\nIt happened by evil chance to be one of Mrs MacStinger’s great cleaning days. On these occasions, Mrs MacStinger was knocked up by the policeman at a quarter before three in the morning, and rarely succumbed before twelve o’clock next night. The chief object of this institution appeared to be, that Mrs MacStinger should move all the furniture into the back garden at early dawn, walk about the house in pattens all day, and move the furniture back again after dark. These ceremonies greatly fluttered those doves the young MacStingers, who were not only unable at such times to find any resting-place for the soles of their feet, but generally came in for a good deal of pecking from the maternal bird during the progress of the solemnities.\n\nAt the moment when Florence and Susan Nipper presented themselves at Mrs MacStinger’s door, that worthy but redoubtable female was in the act of conveying Alexander MacStinger, aged two years and three months, along the passage, for forcible deposition in a sitting posture on the street pavement: Alexander being black in the face with holding his breath after punishment, and a cool paving-stone being usually found to act as a powerful restorative in such cases.\n\nThe feelings of Mrs MacStinger, as a woman and a mother, were outraged by the look of pity for Alexander which she observed on Florence’s face. Therefore, Mrs MacStinger asserting those finest emotions of our nature, in preference to weakly gratifying her curiosity, shook and buffeted Alexander both before and during the application of the paving-stone, and took no further notice of the strangers.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Ma’am,” said Florence, when the child had found his breath again, and was using it. “Is this Captain Cuttle’s house?”\n\n“No,” said Mrs MacStinger.\n\n“Not Number Nine?” asked Florence, hesitating.\n\n“Who said it wasn’t Number Nine?” said Mrs MacStinger.\n\nSusan Nipper instantly struck in, and begged to inquire what Mrs MacStinger meant by that, and if she knew whom she was talking to.\n\nMrs MacStinger in retort, looked at her all over. “What do you want with Captain Cuttle, I should wish to know?” said Mrs MacStinger.\n\n“Should you? Then I’m sorry that you won’t be satisfied,” returned Miss Nipper.\n\n“Hush, Susan! If you please!” said Florence. “Perhaps you can have the goodness to tell us where Captain Cuttle lives, Ma’am as he don’t live here.”\n\n“Who says he don’t live here?” retorted the implacable MacStinger. “I said it wasn’t Cap’en Cuttle’s house—and it ain’t his house—and forbid it, that it ever should be his house—for Cap’en Cuttle don’t know how to keep a house—and don’t deserve to have a house—it’s my house—and when I let the upper floor to Cap’en Cuttle, oh I do a thankless thing, and cast pearls before swine!”\n\nMrs MacStinger pitched her voice for the upper windows in offering these remarks, and cracked off each clause sharply by itself as if from a rifle possessing an infinity of barrels. After the last shot, the Captain’s voice was heard to say, in feeble remonstrance from his own room, “Steady below!”\n\n“Since you want Cap’en Cuttle, there he is!” said Mrs MacStinger, with an angry motion of her hand. On Florence making bold to enter, without any more parley, and on Susan following, Mrs MacStinger recommenced her pedestrian exercise in pattens, and Alexander MacStinger (still on the paving-stone), who had stopped in his crying to attend to the conversation, began to wail again, entertaining himself during that dismal performance, which was quite mechanical, with a general survey of the prospect, terminating in the hackney-coach.\n\nThe Captain in his own apartment was sitting with his hands in his pockets and his legs drawn up under his chair, on a very small desolate island, lying about midway in an ocean of soap and water. The Captain’s windows had been cleaned, the walls had been cleaned, the stove had been cleaned, and everything the stove excepted, was wet, and shining with soft soap and sand: the smell of which dry-saltery impregnated the air. In the midst of the dreary scene, the Captain, cast away upon his island, looked round on the waste of waters with a rueful countenance, and seemed waiting for some friendly bark to come that way, and take him off.\n\nBut when the Captain, directing his forlorn visage towards the door, saw Florence appear with her maid, no words can describe his astonishment. Mrs MacStinger’s eloquence having rendered all other sounds but imperfectly distinguishable, he had looked for no rarer visitor than the potboy or the milkman; wherefore, when Florence appeared, and coming to the confines of the island, put her hand in his, the Captain stood up, aghast, as if he supposed her, for the moment, to be some young member of the Flying Dutchman’s family.\n\nInstantly recovering his self-possession, however, the Captain’s first care was to place her on dry land, which he happily accomplished, with one motion of his arm. Issuing forth, then, upon the main, Captain Cuttle took Miss Nipper round the waist, and bore her to the island also. Captain Cuttle, then, with great respect and admiration, raised the hand of Florence to his lips, and standing off a little (for the island was not large enough for three), beamed on her from the soap and water like a new description of Triton.\n\n“You are amazed to see us, I am sure,” said Florence, with a smile.\n\nThe inexpressibly gratified Captain kissed his hook in reply, and growled, as if a choice and delicate compliment were included in the words, “Stand by! Stand by!”\n\n“But I couldn’t rest,” said Florence, “without coming to ask you what you think about dear Walter—who is my brother now—and whether there is anything to fear, and whether you will not go and console his poor Uncle every day, until we have some intelligence of him?”\n\nAt these words Captain Cuttle, as by an involuntary gesture, clapped his hand to his head, on which the hard glazed hat was not, and looked discomfited.\n\n“Have you any fears for Walter’s safety?” inquired Florence, from whose face the Captain (so enraptured he was with it) could not take his eyes: while she, in her turn, looked earnestly at him, to be assured of the sincerity of his reply.\n\n“No, Heart’s-delight,” said Captain Cuttle, “I am not afeard. Wal”r is a lad as’ll go through a deal o’ hard weather. Wal”r is a lad as’ll bring as much success to that “ere brig as a lad is capable on. Wal”r,” said the Captain, his eyes glistening with the praise of his young friend, and his hook raised to announce a beautiful quotation, “is what you may call a out’ard and visible sign of an in’ard and spirited grasp, and when found make a note of.”\n\nFlorence, who did not quite understand this, though the Captain evidently thought it full of meaning, and highly satisfactory, mildly looked to him for something more.\n\n“I am not afeard, my Heart’s-delight,” resumed the Captain, “There’s been most uncommon bad weather in them latitudes, there’s no denyin’, and they have drove and drove and been beat off, may be t’other side the world. But the ship’s a good ship, and the lad’s a good lad; and it ain’t easy, thank the Lord,” the Captain made a little bow, “to break up hearts of oak, whether they’re in brigs or buzzums. Here we have ’em both ways, which is bringing it up with a round turn, and so I ain’t a bit afeard as yet.”\n\n“As yet?” repeated Florence.\n\n“Not a bit,” returned the Captain, kissing his iron hand; “and afore I begin to be, my Hearts-delight, Wal”r will have wrote home from the island, or from some port or another, and made all taut and ship-shape.” And with regard to old Sol Gills, here the Captain became solemn, “who I’ll stand by, and not desert until death do us part, and when the stormy winds do blow, do blow, do blow—overhaul the Catechism,” said the Captain parenthetically, “and there you’ll find them expressions—if it would console Sol Gills to have the opinion of a seafaring man as has got a mind equal to any undertaking that he puts it alongside of, and as was all but smashed in his “prenticeship, and of which the name is Bunsby, that “ere man shall give him such an opinion in his own parlour as’ll stun him. Ah!” said Captain Cuttle, vauntingly, “as much as if he’d gone and knocked his head again a door!”\n\n“Let us take this gentleman to see him, and let us hear what he says,” cried Florence. “Will you go with us now? We have a coach here.”\n\nAgain the Captain clapped his hand to his head, on which the hard glazed hat was not, and looked discomfited. But at this instant a most remarkable phenomenon occurred. The door opening, without any note of preparation, and apparently of itself, the hard glazed hat in question skimmed into the room like a bird, and alighted heavily at the Captain’s feet. The door then shut as violently as it had opened, and nothing ensued in explanation of the prodigy.\n\nCaptain Cuttle picked up his hat, and having turned it over with a look of interest and welcome, began to polish it on his sleeve. While doing so, the Captain eyed his visitors intently, and said in a low voice,\n“You see I should have bore down on Sol Gills yesterday, and this morning, but she—she took it away and kept it. That’s the long and short of the subject.”\n\n“Who did, for goodness sake?” asked Susan Nipper.\n\n“The lady of the house, my dear,” returned the Captain, in a gruff whisper, and making signals of secrecy. “We had some words about the swabbing of these here planks, and she—In short,” said the Captain, eyeing the door, and relieving himself with a long breath, “she stopped my liberty.”\n\n“Oh! I wish she had me to deal with!” said Susan, reddening with the energy of the wish. “I’d stop her!”\n\n“Would you, do you, my dear?” rejoined the Captain, shaking his head doubtfully, but regarding the desperate courage of the fair aspirant with obvious admiration. “I don’t know. It’s difficult navigation. She’s very hard to carry on with, my dear. You never can tell how she’ll head, you see. She’s full one minute, and round upon you next. And when she in a tartar,” said the Captain, with the perspiration breaking out upon his forehead. There was nothing but a whistle emphatic enough for the conclusion of the sentence, so the Captain whistled tremulously. After which he again shook his head, and recurring to his admiration of Miss Nipper’s devoted bravery, timidly repeated, “Would you, do you think, my dear?”\n\nSusan only replied with a bridling smile, but that was so very full of defiance, that there is no knowing how long Captain Cuttle might have stood entranced in its contemplation, if Florence in her anxiety had not again proposed their immediately resorting to the oracular Bunsby. Thus reminded of his duty, Captain Cuttle put on the glazed hat firmly, took up another knobby stick, with which he had supplied the place of that one given to Walter, and offering his arm to Florence, prepared to cut his way through the enemy.\n\nIt turned out, however, that Mrs MacStinger had already changed her course, and that she headed, as the Captain had remarked she often did, in quite a new direction. For when they got downstairs, they found that exemplary woman beating the mats on the doorsteps, with Alexander, still upon the paving-stone, dimly looming through a fog of dust; and so absorbed was Mrs MacStinger in her household occupation, that when Captain Cuttle and his visitors passed, she beat the harder, and neither by word nor gesture showed any consciousness of their vicinity. The Captain was so well pleased with this easy escape—although the effect of the door-mats on him was like a copious administration of snuff, and made him sneeze until the tears ran down his face—that he could hardly believe his good fortune; but more than once, between the door and the hackney-coach, looked over his shoulder, with an obvious apprehension of Mrs MacStinger’s giving chase yet.\n\nHowever, they got to the corner of Brig Place without any molestation from that terrible fire-ship; and the Captain mounting the coach-box—for his gallantry would not allow him to ride inside with the ladies, though besought to do so—piloted the driver on his course for Captain Bunsby’s vessel, which was called the Cautious Clara, and was lying hard by Ratcliffe.\n\nArrived at the wharf off which this great commander’s ship was jammed in among some five hundred companions, whose tangled rigging looked like monstrous cobwebs half swept down, Captain Cuttle appeared at the coach-window, and invited Florence and Miss Nipper to accompany him on board; observing that Bunsby was to the last degree soft-hearted in respect of ladies, and that nothing would so much tend to bring his expansive intellect into a state of harmony as their presentation to the Cautious Clara.\n\nFlorence readily consented; and the Captain, taking her little hand in his prodigious palm, led her, with a mixed expression of patronage, paternity, pride, and ceremony, that was pleasant to see, over several very dirty decks, until, coming to the Clara, they found that cautious craft (which lay outside the tier) with her gangway removed, and half-a-dozen feet of river interposed between herself and her nearest neighbour. It appeared, from Captain Cuttle’s explanation, that the great Bunsby, like himself, was cruelly treated by his landlady, and that when her usage of him for the time being was so hard that he could bear it no longer, he set this gulf between them as a last resource.\n\n“Clara a-hoy!” cried the Captain, putting a hand to each side of his mouth.\n\n“A-hoy!” cried a boy, like the Captain’s echo, tumbling up from below.\n\n“Bunsby aboard?” cried the Captain, hailing the boy in a stentorian voice, as if he were half-a-mile off instead of two yards.\n\n“Ay, ay!” cried the boy, in the same tone.\n\nThe boy then shoved out a plank to Captain Cuttle, who adjusted it carefully, and led Florence across: returning presently for Miss Nipper. So they stood upon the deck of the Cautious Clara, in whose standing rigging, divers fluttering articles of dress were curing, in company with a few tongues and some mackerel.\n\nImmediately there appeared, coming slowly up above the bulk-head of the cabin, another bulk-head—human, and very large—with one stationary eye in the mahogany face, and one revolving one, on the principle of some lighthouses. This head was decorated with shaggy hair, like oakum, which had no governing inclination towards the north, east, west, or south, but inclined to all four quarters of the compass, and to every point upon it. The head was followed by a perfect desert of chin, and by a shirt-collar and neckerchief, and by a dreadnought pilot-coat, and by a pair of dreadnought pilot-trousers, whereof the waistband was so very broad and high, that it became a succedaneum for a waistcoat: being ornamented near the wearer’s breastbone with some massive wooden buttons, like backgammon men. As the lower portions of these pantaloons became revealed, Bunsby stood confessed; his hands in their pockets, which were of vast size; and his gaze directed, not to Captain Cuttle or the ladies, but the mast-head.\n\nThe profound appearance of this philosopher, who was bulky and strong, and on whose extremely red face an expression of taciturnity sat enthroned, not inconsistent with his character, in which that quality was proudly conspicuous, almost daunted Captain Cuttle, though on familiar terms with him. Whispering to Florence that Bunsby had never in his life expressed surprise, and was considered not to know what it meant, the Captain watched him as he eyed his mast-head, and afterwards swept the horizon; and when the revolving eye seemed to be coming round in his direction, said:\n\n“Bunsby, my lad, how fares it?”\n\nA deep, gruff, husky utterance, which seemed to have no connexion with Bunsby, and certainly had not the least effect upon his face, replied, “Ay, ay, shipmet, how goes it?” At the same time Bunsby’s right hand and arm, emerging from a pocket, shook the Captain’s, and went back again.\n\n“Bunsby,” said the Captain, striking home at once, “here you are; a man of mind, and a man as can give an opinion. Here’s a young lady as wants to take that opinion, in regard of my friend Wal”r; likewise my t’other friend, Sol Gills, which is a character for you to come within hail of, being a man of science, which is the mother of invention, and knows no law. Bunsby, will you wear, to oblige me, and come along with us?”\n\nThe great commander, who seemed by expression of his visage to be always on the look-out for something in the extremest distance, and to have no ocular knowledge of anything within ten miles, made no reply whatever.\n\n“Here is a man,” said the Captain, addressing himself to his fair auditors, and indicating the commander with his outstretched hook, “that has fell down, more than any man alive; that has had more accidents happen to his own self than the Seamen’s Hospital to all hands; that took as many spars and bars and bolts about the outside of his head when he was young, as you’d want a order for on Chatham-yard to build a pleasure yacht with; and yet that his opinions in that way, it’s my belief, for there ain’t nothing like ’em afloat or ashore.”\n\nThe stolid commander appeared by a very slight vibration in his elbows, to express some satisfaction in this encomium; but if his face had been as distant as his gaze was, it could hardly have enlightened the beholders less in reference to anything that was passing in his thoughts.\n\n“Shipmet,” said Bunsby, all of a sudden, and stooping down to look out under some interposing spar, “what’ll the ladies drink?”\n\nCaptain Cuttle, whose delicacy was shocked by such an inquiry in connection with Florence, drew the sage aside, and seeming to explain in his ear, accompanied him below; where, that he might not take offence, the Captain drank a dram himself, which Florence and Susan, glancing down the open skylight, saw the sage, with difficulty finding room for himself between his berth and a very little brass fireplace, serve out for self and friend. They soon reappeared on deck, and Captain Cuttle, triumphing in the success of his enterprise, conducted Florence back to the coach, while Bunsby followed, escorting Miss Nipper, whom he hugged upon the way (much to that young lady’s indignation) with his pilot-coated arm, like a blue bear.\n\nThe Captain put his oracle inside, and gloried so much in having secured him, and having got that mind into a hackney-coach, that he could not refrain from often peeping in at Florence through the little window behind the driver, and testifying his delight in smiles, and also in taps upon his forehead, to hint to her that the brain of Bunsby was hard at it. In the meantime, Bunsby, still hugging Miss Nipper (for his friend, the Captain, had not exaggerated the softness of his heart), uniformly preserved his gravity of deportment, and showed no other consciousness of her or anything.\n\nUncle Sol, who had come home, received them at the door, and ushered them immediately into the little back parlour: strangely altered by the absence of Walter. On the table, and about the room, were the charts and maps on which the heavy-hearted Instrument-maker had again and again tracked the missing vessel across the sea, and on which, with a pair of compasses that he still had in his hand, he had been measuring, a minute before, how far she must have driven, to have driven here or there: and trying to demonstrate that a long time must elapse before hope was exhausted.\n\n“Whether she can have run,” said Uncle Sol, looking wistfully over the chart; “but no, that’s almost impossible or whether she can have been forced by stress of weather,—but that’s not reasonably likely. Or whether there is any hope she so far changed her course as—but even I can hardly hope that!” With such broken suggestions, poor old Uncle Sol roamed over the great sheet before him, and could not find a speck of hopeful probability in it large enough to set one small point of the compasses upon.\n\nFlorence saw immediately—it would have been difficult to help seeing—that there was a singular, indescribable change in the old man, and that while his manner was far more restless and unsettled than usual, there was yet a curious, contradictory decision in it, that perplexed her very much. She fancied once that he spoke wildly, and at random; for on her saying she regretted not to have seen him when she had been there before that morning, he at first replied that he had been to see her, and directly afterwards seemed to wish to recall that answer.\n\n“You have been to see me?” said Florence. “Today?”\n\n“Yes, my dear young lady,” returned Uncle Sol, looking at her and away from her in a confused manner. “I wished to see you with my own eyes, and to hear you with my own ears, once more before—” There he stopped.\n\n“Before when? Before what?” said Florence, putting her hand upon his arm.\n\n“Did I say ‘before?’” replied old Sol. “If I did, I must have meant before we should have news of my dear boy.”\n\n“You are not well,” said Florence, tenderly. “You have been so very anxious I am sure you are not well.”\n\n“I am as well,” returned the old man, shutting up his right hand, and holding it out to show her: “as well and firm as any man at my time of life can hope to be. See! It’s steady. Is its master not as capable of resolution and fortitude as many a younger man? I think so. We shall see.”\n\nThere was that in his manner more than in his words, though they remained with her too, which impressed Florence so much, that she would have confided her uneasiness to Captain Cuttle at that moment, if the Captain had not seized that moment for expounding the state of circumstance, on which the opinion of the sagacious Bunsby was requested, and entreating that profound authority to deliver the same.\n\nBunsby, whose eye continued to be addressed to somewhere about the half-way house between London and Gravesend, two or three times put out his rough right arm, as seeking to wind it for inspiration round the fair form of Miss Nipper; but that young female having withdrawn herself, in displeasure, to the opposite side of the table, the soft heart of the Commander of the Cautious Clara met with no response to its impulses. After sundry failures in this wise, the Commander, addressing himself to nobody, thus spake; or rather the voice within him said of its own accord, and quite independent of himself, as if he were possessed by a gruff spirit:\n\n“My name’s Jack Bunsby!”\n\n“He was christened John,” cried the delighted Captain Cuttle. “Hear him!”\n\n“And what I says,” pursued the voice, after some deliberation, “I stands to.”\n\nThe Captain, with Florence on his arm, nodded at the auditory, and seemed to say, “Now he’s coming out. This is what I meant when I brought him.”\n\n“Whereby,” proceeded the voice, “why not? If so, what odds? Can any man say otherwise? No. Awast then!”\n\nWhen it had pursued its train of argument to this point, the voice stopped, and rested. It then proceeded very slowly, thus:\n\n“Do I believe that this here Son and Heir’s gone down, my lads? Mayhap. Do I say so? Which? If a skipper stands out by Sen’ George’s Channel, making for the Downs, what’s right ahead of him? The Goodwins. He isn’t forced to run upon the Goodwins, but he may. The bearings of this observation lays in the application on it. That ain’t no part of my duty. Awast then, keep a bright look-out for’ard, and good luck to you!”\n\nThe voice here went out of the back parlour and into the street, taking the Commander of the Cautious Clara with it, and accompanying him on board again with all convenient expedition, where he immediately turned in, and refreshed his mind with a nap.\n\nThe students of the sage’s precepts, left to their own application of his wisdom—upon a principle which was the main leg of the Bunsby tripod, as it is perchance of some other oracular stools—looked upon one another in a little uncertainty; while Rob the Grinder, who had taken the innocent freedom of peering in, and listening, through the skylight in the roof, came softly down from the leads, in a state of very dense confusion. Captain Cuttle, however, whose admiration of Bunsby was, if possible, enhanced by the splendid manner in which he had justified his reputation and come through this solemn reference, proceeded to explain that Bunsby meant nothing but confidence; that Bunsby had no misgivings; and that such an opinion as that man had given, coming from such a mind as his, was Hope’s own anchor, with good roads to cast it in. Florence endeavoured to believe that the Captain was right; but the Nipper, with her arms tight folded, shook her head in resolute denial, and had no more trust in Bunsby than in Mr Perch himself.\n\nThe philosopher seemed to have left Uncle Sol pretty much where he had found him, for he still went roaming about the watery world, compasses in hand, and discovering no rest for them. It was in pursuance of a whisper in his ear from Florence, while the old man was absorbed in this pursuit, that Captain Cuttle laid his heavy hand upon his shoulder.\n\n“What cheer, Sol Gills?” cried the Captain, heartily.\n\n“But so-so, Ned,” returned the Instrument-maker. “I have been remembering, all this afternoon, that on the very day when my boy entered Dombey’s House, and came home late to dinner, sitting just there where you stand, we talked of storm and shipwreck, and I could hardly turn him from the subject.”\n\nBut meeting the eyes of Florence, which were fixed with earnest scrutiny upon his face, the old man stopped and smiled.\n\n“Stand by, old friend!” cried the Captain. “Look alive! I tell you what, Sol Gills; arter I’ve convoyed Heart’s-delight safe home,” here the Captain kissed his hook to Florence, “I’ll come back and take you in tow for the rest of this blessed day. You’ll come and eat your dinner along with me, Sol, somewheres or another.”\n\n“Not today, Ned!” said the old man quickly, and appearing to be unaccountably startled by the proposition. “Not today. I couldn’t do it!”\n\n“Why not?” returned the Captain, gazing at him in astonishment.\n\n“I—I have so much to do. I—I mean to think of, and arrange. I couldn’t do it, Ned, indeed. I must go out again, and be alone, and turn my mind to many things today.”\n\nThe Captain looked at the Instrument-maker, and looked at Florence, and again at the Instrument-maker. “To-morrow, then,” he suggested, at last.\n\n“Yes, yes. To-morrow,” said the old man. “Think of me to-morrow. Say to-morrow.”\n\n“I shall come here early, mind, Sol Gills,” stipulated the Captain.\n\n“Yes, yes. The first thing tomorrow morning,” said old Sol; “and now good-bye, Ned Cuttle, and God bless you!”\n\nSqueezing both the Captain’s hands, with uncommon fervour, as he said it, the old man turned to Florence, folded hers in his own, and put them to his lips; then hurried her out to the coach with very singular precipitation. Altogether, he made such an effect on Captain Cuttle that the Captain lingered behind, and instructed Rob to be particularly gentle and attentive to his master until the morning: which injunction he strengthened with the payment of one shilling down, and the promise of another sixpence before noon next day. This kind office performed, Captain Cuttle, who considered himself the natural and lawful body-guard of Florence, mounted the box with a mighty sense of his trust, and escorted her home. At parting, he assured her that he would stand by Sol Gills, close and true; and once again inquired of Susan Nipper, unable to forget her gallant words in reference to Mrs MacStinger, “Would you, do you think my dear, though?”\n\nWhen the desolate house had closed upon the two, the Captain’s thoughts reverted to the old Instrument-maker, and he felt uncomfortable. Therefore, instead of going home, he walked up and down the street several times, and, eking out his leisure until evening, dined late at a certain angular little tavern in the City, with a public parlour like a wedge, to which glazed hats much resorted. The Captain’s principal intention was to pass Sol Gills’s, after dark, and look in through the window: which he did, The parlour door stood open, and he could see his old friend writing busily and steadily at the table within, while the little Midshipman, already sheltered from the night dews, watched him from the counter; under which Rob the Grinder made his own bed, preparatory to shutting the shop. Reassured by the tranquillity that reigned within the precincts of the wooden mariner, the Captain headed for Brig Place, resolving to weigh anchor betimes in the morning.\n\nSir Barnet and Lady Skettles, very good people, resided in a pretty villa at Fulham, on the banks of the Thames; which was one of the most desirable residences in the world when a rowing-match happened to be going past, but had its little inconveniences at other times, among which may be enumerated the occasional appearance of the river in the drawing-room, and the contemporaneous disappearance of the lawn and shrubbery.\n\nSir Barnet Skettles expressed his personal consequence chiefly through an antique gold snuffbox, and a ponderous silk pocket-kerchief, which he had an imposing manner of drawing out of his pocket like a banner and using with both hands at once. Sir Barnet’s object in life was constantly to extend the range of his acquaintance. Like a heavy body dropped into water—not to disparage so worthy a gentleman by the comparison—it was in the nature of things that Sir Barnet must spread an ever widening circle about him, until there was no room left. Or, like a sound in air, the vibration of which, according to the speculation of an ingenious modern philosopher, may go on travelling for ever through the interminable fields of space, nothing but coming to the end of his moral tether could stop Sir Barnet Skettles in his voyage of discovery through the social system.\n\nSir Barnet was proud of making people acquainted with people. He liked the thing for its own sake, and it advanced his favourite object too. For example, if Sir Barnet had the good fortune to get hold of a law recruit, or a country gentleman, and ensnared him to his hospitable villa, Sir Barnet would say to him, on the morning after his arrival, “Now, my dear Sir, is there anybody you would like to know? Who is there you would wish to meet? Do you take any interest in writing people, or in painting or sculpturing people, or in acting people, or in anything of that sort?” Possibly the patient answered yes, and mentioned somebody, of whom Sir Barnet had no more personal knowledge than of Ptolemy the Great. Sir Barnet replied, that nothing on earth was easier, as he knew him very well: immediately called on the aforesaid somebody, left his card, wrote a short note,—“My dear Sir—penalty of your eminent position—friend at my house naturally desirous—Lady Skettles and myself participate—trust that genius being superior to ceremonies, you will do us the distinguished favour of giving us the pleasure,” etc, etc.—and so killed a brace of birds with one stone, dead as door-nails.\n\nWith the snuff-box and banner in full force, Sir Barnet Skettles propounded his usual inquiry to Florence on the first morning of her visit. When Florence thanked him, and said there was no one in particular whom she desired to see, it was natural she should think with a pang, of poor lost Walter. When Sir Barnet Skettles, urging his kind offer, said, “My dear Miss Dombey, are you sure you can remember no one whom your good Papa—to whom I beg you present the best compliments of myself and Lady Skettles when you write—might wish you to know?” it was natural, perhaps, that her poor head should droop a little, and that her voice should tremble as it softly answered in the negative.\n\nSkettles Junior, much stiffened as to his cravat, and sobered down as to his spirits, was at home for the holidays, and appeared to feel himself aggrieved by the solicitude of his excellent mother that he should be attentive to Florence. Another and a deeper injury under which the soul of young Barnet chafed, was the company of Dr and Mrs Blimber, who had been invited on a visit to the paternal roof-tree, and of whom the young gentleman often said he would have preferred their passing the vacation at Jericho.\n\n“Is there anybody you can suggest now, Doctor Blimber?” said Sir Barnet Skettles, turning to that gentleman.\n\n“You are very kind, Sir Barnet,” returned Doctor Blimber. “Really I am not aware that there is, in particular. I like to know my fellow-men in general, Sir Barnet. What does Terence say? Anyone who is the parent of a son is interesting to me.”\n\n“Has Mrs Blimber any wish to see any remarkable person?” asked Sir Barnet, courteously.\n\nMrs Blimber replied, with a sweet smile and a shake of her sky-blue cap, that if Sir Barnet could have made her known to Cicero, she would have troubled him; but such an introduction not being feasible, and she already enjoying the friendship of himself and his amiable lady, and possessing with the Doctor her husband their joint confidence in regard to their dear son—here young Barnet was observed to curl his nose—she asked no more.\n\nSir Barnet was fain, under these circumstances, to content himself for the time with the company assembled. Florence was glad of that; for she had a study to pursue among them, and it lay too near her heart, and was too precious and momentous, to yield to any other interest.\n\nThere were some children staying in the house. Children who were as frank and happy with fathers and with mothers as those rosy faces opposite home. Children who had no restraint upon their love, and freely showed it. Florence sought to learn their secret; sought to find out what it was she had missed; what simple art they knew, and she knew not; how she could be taught by them to show her father that she loved him, and to win his love again.\n\nMany a day did Florence thoughtfully observe these children. On many a bright morning did she leave her bed when the glorious sun rose, and walking up and down upon the river’s bank, before anyone in the house was stirring, look up at the windows of their rooms, and think of them, asleep, so gently tended and affectionately thought of. Florence would feel more lonely then, than in the great house all alone; and would think sometimes that she was better there than here, and that there was greater peace in hiding herself than in mingling with others of her age, and finding how unlike them all she was. But attentive to her study, though it touched her to the quick at every little leaf she turned in the hard book, Florence remained among them, and tried, with patient hope, to gain the knowledge that she wearied for.\n\nAh! how to gain it! how to know the charm in its beginning! There were daughters here, who rose up in the morning, and lay down to rest at night, possessed of fathers’ hearts already. They had no repulse to overcome, no coldness to dread, no frown to smooth away. As the morning advanced, and the windows opened one by one, and the dew began to dry upon the flowers and youthful feet began to move upon the lawn, Florence, glancing round at the bright faces, thought what was there she could learn from these children? It was too late to learn from them; each could approach her father fearlessly, and put up her lips to meet the ready kiss, and wind her arm about the neck that bent down to caress her. She could not begin by being so bold. Oh! could it be that there was less and less hope as she studied more and more!\n\nShe remembered well, that even the old woman who had robbed her when a little child—whose image and whose house, and all she had said and done, were stamped upon her recollection, with the enduring sharpness of a fearful impression made at that early period of life—had spoken fondly of her daughter, and how terribly even she had cried out in the pain of hopeless separation from her child. But her own mother, she would think again, when she recalled this, had loved her well. Then, sometimes, when her thoughts reverted swiftly to the void between herself and her father, Florence would tremble, and the tears would start upon her face, as she pictured to herself her mother living on, and coming also to dislike her, because of her wanting the unknown grace that should conciliate that father naturally, and had never done so from her cradle. She knew that this imagination did wrong to her mother’s memory, and had no truth in it, or base to rest upon; and yet she tried so hard to justify him, and to find the whole blame in herself, that she could not resist its passing, like a wild cloud, through the distance of her mind.\n\nThere came among the other visitors, soon after Florence, one beautiful girl, three or four years younger than she, who was an orphan child, and who was accompanied by her aunt, a grey-haired lady, who spoke much to Florence, and who greatly liked (but that they all did) to hear her sing of an evening, and would always sit near her at that time, with motherly interest. They had only been two days in the house, when Florence, being in an arbour in the garden one warm morning, musingly observant of a youthful group upon the turf, through some intervening boughs,—and wreathing flowers for the head of one little creature among them who was the pet and plaything of the rest, heard this same lady and her niece, in pacing up and down a sheltered nook close by, speak of herself.\n\n“Is Florence an orphan like me, aunt?” said the child.\n\n“No, my love. She has no mother, but her father is living.”\n\n“Is she in mourning for her poor Mama, now?” inquired the child quickly.\n\n“No; for her only brother.”\n\n“Has she no other brother?”\n\n“None.”\n\n“No sister?”\n“None,”\n\n“I am very, very sorry!” said the little girl\n\nAs they stopped soon afterwards to watch some boats, and had been silent in the meantime, Florence, who had risen when she heard her name, and had gathered up her flowers to go and meet them, that they might know of her being within hearing, resumed her seat and work, expecting to hear no more; but the conversation recommenced next moment.\n\n“Florence is a favourite with everyone here, and deserves to be, I am sure,” said the child, earnestly. “Where is her Papa?”\n\nThe aunt replied, after a moment’s pause, that she did not know. Her tone of voice arrested Florence, who had started from her seat again; and held her fastened to the spot, with her work hastily caught up to her bosom, and her two hands saving it from being scattered on the ground.\n\n“He is in England, I hope, aunt?” said the child.\n\n“I believe so. Yes; I know he is, indeed.”\n\n“Has he ever been here?”\n\n“I believe not. No.”\n\n“Is he coming here to see her?”\n\n“I believe not.”\n\n“Is he lame, or blind, or ill, aunt?” asked the child.\n\nThe flowers that Florence held to her breast began to fall when she heard those words, so wonderingly spoke. She held them closer; and her face hung down upon them.\n\n“Kate,” said the lady, after another moment of silence, “I will tell you the whole truth about Florence as I have heard it, and believe it to be. Tell no one else, my dear, because it may be little known here, and your doing so would give her pain.”\n\n“I never will!” exclaimed the child.\n\n“I know you never will,” returned the lady. “I can trust you as myself. I fear then, Kate, that Florence’s father cares little for her, very seldom sees her, never was kind to her in her life, and now quite shuns her and avoids her. She would love him dearly if he would suffer her, but he will not—though for no fault of hers; and she is greatly to be loved and pitied by all gentle hearts.”\n\nMore of the flowers that Florence held fell scattering on the ground; those that remained were wet, but not with dew; and her face dropped upon her laden hands.\n\n“Poor Florence! Dear, good Florence!” cried the child.\n\n“Do you know why I have told you this, Kate?” said the lady.\n\n“That I may be very kind to her, and take great care to try to please her. Is that the reason, aunt?”\n\n“Partly,” said the lady, “but not all. Though we see her so cheerful; with a pleasant smile for everyone; ready to oblige us all, and bearing her part in every amusement here: she can hardly be quite happy, do you think she can, Kate?”\n\n“I am afraid not,” said the little girl.\n\n“And you can understand,” pursued the lady, “why her observation of children who have parents who are fond of them, and proud of them—like many here, just now—should make her sorrowful in secret?”\n\n“Yes, dear aunt,” said the child, “I understand that very well. Poor Florence!”\n\nMore flowers strayed upon the ground, and those she yet held to her breast trembled as if a wintry wind were rustling them.\n\n“My Kate,” said the lady, whose voice was serious, but very calm and sweet, and had so impressed Florence from the first moment of her hearing it, “of all the youthful people here, you are her natural and harmless friend; you have not the innocent means, that happier children have—”\n\n“There are none happier, aunt!” exclaimed the child, who seemed to cling about her.\n\n“—As other children have, dear Kate, of reminding her of her misfortune. Therefore I would have you, when you try to be her little friend, try all the more for that, and feel that the bereavement you sustained—thank Heaven! before you knew its weight—gives you claim and hold upon poor Florence.”\n\n“But I am not without a parent’s love, aunt, and I never have been,” said the child, “with you.”\n\n“However that may be, my dear,” returned the lady, “your misfortune is a lighter one than Florence’s; for not an orphan in the wide world can be so deserted as the child who is an outcast from a living parent’s love.”\n\nThe flowers were scattered on the ground like dust; the empty hands were spread upon the face; and orphaned Florence, shrinking down upon the ground, wept long and bitterly.\n\nBut true of heart and resolute in her good purpose, Florence held to it as her dying mother held by her upon the day that gave Paul life. He did not know how much she loved him. However long the time in coming, and however slow the interval, she must try to bring that knowledge to her father’s heart one day or other. Meantime she must be careful in no thoughtless word, or look, or burst of feeling awakened by any chance circumstance, to complain against him, or to give occasion for these whispers to his prejudice.\n\nEven in the response she made the orphan child, to whom she was attracted strongly, and whom she had such occasion to remember, Florence was mindful of him. If she singled her out too plainly (Florence thought) from among the rest, she would confirm—in one mind certainly: perhaps in more—the belief that he was cruel and unnatural. Her own delight was no set-off to this. What she had overheard was a reason, not for soothing herself, but for saving him; and Florence did it, in pursuance of the study of her heart.\n\nShe did so always. If a book were read aloud, and there were anything in the story that pointed at an unkind father, she was in pain for their application of it to him; not for herself. So with any trifle of an interlude that was acted, or picture that was shown, or game that was played, among them. The occasions for such tenderness towards him were so many, that her mind misgave her often, it would indeed be better to go back to the old house, and live again within the shadow of its dull walls, undisturbed. How few who saw sweet Florence, in her spring of womanhood, the modest little queen of those small revels, imagined what a load of sacred care lay heavy in her breast! How few of those who stiffened in her father’s freezing atmosphere, suspected what a heap of fiery coals was piled upon his head!\n\nFlorence pursued her study patiently, and, failing to acquire the secret of the nameless grace she sought, among the youthful company who were assembled in the house, often walked out alone, in the early morning, among the children of the poor. But still she found them all too far advanced to learn from. They had won their household places long ago, and did not stand without, as she did, with a bar across the door.\n\nThere was one man whom she several times observed at work very early, and often with a girl of about her own age seated near him. He was a very poor man, who seemed to have no regular employment, but now went roaming about the banks of the river when the tide was low, looking out for bits and scraps in the mud; and now worked at the unpromising little patch of garden-ground before his cottage; and now tinkered up a miserable old boat that belonged to him; or did some job of that kind for a neighbour, as chance occurred. Whatever the man’s labour, the girl was never employed; but sat, when she was with him, in a listless, moping state, and idle.\n\nFlorence had often wished to speak to this man; yet she had never taken courage to do so, as he made no movement towards her. But one morning when she happened to come upon him suddenly, from a by-path among some pollard willows which terminated in the little shelving piece of stony ground that lay between his dwelling and the water, where he was bending over a fire he had made to caulk the old boat which was lying bottom upwards, close by, he raised his head at the sound of her footstep, and gave her Good morning.\n\n“Good morning,” said Florence, approaching nearer, “you are at work early.”\n\n“I’d be glad to be often at work earlier, Miss, if I had work to do.”\n\n“Is it so hard to get?” asked Florence.\n\n“I find it so,” replied the man.\n\nFlorence glanced to where the girl was sitting, drawn together, with her elbows on her knees, and her chin on her hands, and said:\n\n“Is that your daughter?”\n\nHe raised his head quickly, and looking towards the girl with a brightened face, nodded to her, and said “Yes,” Florence looked towards her too, and gave her a kind salutation; the girl muttered something in return, ungraciously and sullenly.\n\n“Is she in want of employment also?” said Florence.\n\nThe man shook his head. “No, Miss,” he said. “I work for both,”\n\n“Are there only you two, then?” inquired Florence.\n\n“Only us two,” said the man. “Her mother has been dead these ten year. Martha!” (he lifted up his head again, and whistled to her) “won’t you say a word to the pretty young lady?”\n\nThe girl made an impatient gesture with her cowering shoulders, and turned her head another way. Ugly, misshapen, peevish, ill-conditioned, ragged, dirty—but beloved! Oh yes! Florence had seen her father’s look towards her, and she knew whose look it had no likeness to.\n\n“I’m afraid she’s worse this morning, my poor girl!” said the man, suspending his work, and contemplating his ill-favoured child, with a compassion that was the more tender for being rougher.\n\n“She is ill, then!” said Florence.\n\nThe man drew a deep sigh. “I don’t believe my Martha’s had five short days’ good health,” he answered, looking at her still, “in as many long years.”\n\n“Ay! and more than that, John,” said a neighbour, who had come down to help him with the boat.\n\n“More than that, you say, do you?” cried the other, pushing back his battered hat, and drawing his hand across his forehead. “Very like. It seems a long, long time.”\n\n“And the more the time,” pursued the neighbour, “the more you’ve favoured and humoured her, John, till she’s got to be a burden to herself, and everybody else.”\n\n“Not to me,” said her father, falling to his work. “Not to me.”\n\nFlorence could feel—who better?—how truly he spoke. She drew a little closer to him, and would have been glad to touch his rugged hand, and thank him for his goodness to the miserable object that he looked upon with eyes so different from any other man’s.\n\n“Who would favour my poor girl—to call it favouring—if I didn’t?” said the father.\n\n“Ay, ay,” cried the neighbour. “In reason, John. But you! You rob yourself to give to her. You bind yourself hand and foot on her account. You make your life miserable along of her. And what does she care! You don’t believe she knows it?”\n\nThe father lifted up his head again, and whistled to her. Martha made the same impatient gesture with her crouching shoulders, in reply; and he was glad and happy.\n\n“Only for that, Miss,” said the neighbour, with a smile, in which there was more of secret sympathy than he expressed; “only to get that, he never lets her out of his sight!”\n\n“Because the day’ll come, and has been coming a long while,” observed the other, bending low over his work, “when to get half as much from that unfort’nate child of mine—to get the trembling of a finger, or the waving of a hair—would be to raise the dead.”\n\nFlorence softly put some money near his hand on the old boat, and left him.\n\nAnd now Florence began to think, if she were to fall ill, if she were to fade like her dear brother, would he then know that she had loved him; would she then grow dear to him; would he come to her bedside, when she was weak and dim of sight, and take her into his embrace, and cancel all the past? Would he so forgive her, in that changed condition, for not having been able to lay open her childish heart to him, as to make it easy to relate with what emotions she had gone out of his room that night; what she had meant to say if she had had the courage; and how she had endeavoured, afterwards, to learn the way she never knew in infancy?\n\nYes, she thought if she were dying, he would relent. She thought, that if she lay, serene and not unwilling to depart, upon the bed that was curtained round with recollections of their darling boy, he would be touched home, and would say, “Dear Florence, live for me, and we will love each other as we might have done, and be as happy as we might have been these many years!” She thought that if she heard such words from him, and had her arms clasped round him, she could answer with a smile, “It is too late for anything but this; I never could be happier, dear father!” and so leave him, with a blessing on her lips.\n\nThe golden water she remembered on the wall, appeared to Florence, in the light of such reflections, only as a current flowing on to rest, and to a region where the dear ones, gone before, were waiting, hand in hand; and often when she looked upon the darker river rippling at her feet, she thought with awful wonder, but not terror, of that river which her brother had so often said was bearing him away.\n\nThe father and his sick daughter were yet fresh in Florence’s mind, and, indeed, that incident was not a week old, when Sir Barnet and his lady going out walking in the lanes one afternoon, proposed to her to bear them company. Florence readily consenting, Lady Skettles ordered out young Barnet as a matter of course. For nothing delighted Lady Skettles so much, as beholding her eldest son with Florence on his arm.\n\nBarnet, to say the truth, appeared to entertain an opposite sentiment on the subject, and on such occasions frequently expressed himself audibly, though indefinitely, in reference to “a parcel of girls.” As it was not easy to ruffle her sweet temper, however, Florence generally reconciled the young gentleman to his fate after a few minutes, and they strolled on amicably: Lady Skettles and Sir Barnet following, in a state of perfect complacency and high gratification.\n\nThis was the order of procedure on the afternoon in question; and Florence had almost succeeded in overruling the present objections of Skettles Junior to his destiny, when a gentleman on horseback came riding by, looked at them earnestly as he passed, drew in his rein, wheeled round, and came riding back again, hat in hand.\n\nThe gentleman had looked particularly at Florence; and when the little party stopped, on his riding back, he bowed to her, before saluting Sir Barnet and his lady. Florence had no remembrance of having ever seen him, but she started involuntarily when he came near her, and drew back.\n\n“My horse is perfectly quiet, I assure you,” said the gentleman.\n\nIt was not that, but something in the gentleman himself—Florence could not have said what—that made her recoil as if she had been stung.\n\n“I have the honour to address Miss Dombey, I believe?” said the gentleman, with a most persuasive smile. On Florence inclining her head, he added, “My name is Carker. I can hardly hope to be remembered by Miss Dombey, except by name. Carker.”\n\nFlorence, sensible of a strange inclination to shiver, though the day was hot, presented him to her host and hostess; by whom he was very graciously received.\n\n“I beg pardon,” said Mr Carker, “a thousand times! But I am going down tomorrow morning to Mr Dombey, at Leamington, and if Miss Dombey can entrust me with any commission, need I say how very happy I shall be?”\n\nSir Barnet immediately divining that Florence would desire to write a letter to her father, proposed to return, and besought Mr Carker to come home and dine in his riding gear. Mr Carker had the misfortune to be engaged to dinner, but if Miss Dombey wished to write, nothing would delight him more than to accompany them back, and to be her faithful slave in waiting as long as she pleased. As he said this with his widest smile, and bent down close to her to pat his horse’s neck, Florence meeting his eyes, saw, rather than heard him say, “There is no news of the ship!”\n\nConfused, frightened, shrinking from him, and not even sure that he had said those words, for he seemed to have shown them to her in some extraordinary manner through his smile, instead of uttering them, Florence faintly said that she was obliged to him, but she would not write; she had nothing to say.\n\n“Nothing to send, Miss Dombey?” said the man of teeth.\n\n“Nothing,” said Florence, “but my—but my dear love—if you please.”\n\nDisturbed as Florence was, she raised her eyes to his face with an imploring and expressive look, that plainly besought him, if he knew—which he as plainly did—that any message between her and her father was an uncommon charge, but that one most of all, to spare her. Mr Carker smiled and bowed low, and being charged by Sir Barnet with the best compliments of himself and Lady Skettles, took his leave, and rode away: leaving a favourable impression on that worthy couple. Florence was seized with such a shudder as he went, that Sir Barnet, adopting the popular superstition, supposed somebody was passing over her grave. Mr Carker turning a corner, on the instant, looked back, and bowed, and disappeared, as if he rode off to the churchyard straight, to do it.\n\n# CHAPTER XXV. Strange News of Uncle Sol\n\nCaptain Cuttle, though no sluggard, did not turn out so early on the morning after he had seen Sol Gills, through the shop-window, writing in the parlour, with the Midshipman upon the counter, and Rob the Grinder making up his bed below it, but that the clocks struck six as he raised himself on his elbow, and took a survey of his little chamber. The Captain’s eyes must have done severe duty, if he usually opened them as wide on awaking as he did that morning; and were but roughly rewarded for their vigilance, if he generally rubbed them half as hard. But the occasion was no common one, for Rob the Grinder had certainly never stood in the doorway of Captain Cuttle’s room before, and in it he stood then, panting at the Captain, with a flushed and touzled air of Bed about him, that greatly heightened both his colour and expression.\n\n“Holloa!” roared the Captain. “What’s the matter?”\n\nBefore Rob could stammer a word in answer, Captain Cuttle turned out, all in a heap, and covered the boy’s mouth with his hand.\n\n“Steady, my lad,” said the Captain, “don’t ye speak a word to me as yet!”\n\nThe Captain, looking at his visitor in great consternation, gently shouldered him into the next room, after laying this injunction upon him; and disappearing for a few moments, forthwith returned in the blue suit. Holding up his hand in token of the injunction not yet being taken off, Captain Cuttle walked up to the cupboard, and poured himself out a dram; a counterpart of which he handed to the messenger. The Captain then stood himself up in a corner, against the wall, as if to forestall the possibility of being knocked backwards by the communication that was to be made to him; and having swallowed his liquor, with his eyes fixed on the messenger, and his face as pale as his face could be, requested him to “heave ahead.”\n\n“Do you mean, tell you, Captain?” asked Rob, who had been greatly impressed by these precautions.\n\n“Ay!” said the Captain.\n\n“Well, Sir,” said Rob, “I ain’t got much to tell. But look here!”\n\nRob produced a bundle of keys. The Captain surveyed them, remained in his corner, and surveyed the messenger.\n\n“And look here!” pursued Rob.\n\nThe boy produced a sealed packet, which Captain Cuttle stared at as he had stared at the keys.\n\n“When I woke this morning, Captain,” said Rob, “which was about a quarter after five, I found these on my pillow. The shop-door was unbolted and unlocked, and Mr Gills gone.”\n\n“Gone!” roared the Captain.\n\n“Flowed, Sir,” returned Rob.\n\nThe Captain’s voice was so tremendous, and he came out of his corner with such way on him, that Rob retreated before him into another corner: holding out the keys and packet, to prevent himself from being run down.\n\n“‘For Captain Cuttle,’ Sir,” cried Rob, “is on the keys, and on the packet too. Upon my word and honour, Captain Cuttle, I don’t know anything more about it. I wish I may die if I do! Here’s a sitiwation for a lad that’s just got a sitiwation,” cried the unfortunate Grinder, screwing his cuff into his face: “his master bolted with his place, and him blamed for it!”\n\nThese lamentations had reference to Captain Cuttle’s gaze, or rather glare, which was full of vague suspicions, threatenings, and denunciations. Taking the proffered packet from his hand, the Captain opened it and read as follows:—\n\n“‘My dear Ned Cuttle. Enclosed is my will!’” The Captain turned it over, with a doubtful look—“\"and Testament’—Where’s the Testament?” said the Captain, instantly impeaching the ill-fated Grinder. “What have you done with that, my lad?”\n\n“I never see it,” whimpered Rob. “Don’t keep on suspecting an innocent lad, Captain. I never touched the Testament.”\n\nCaptain Cuttle shook his head, implying that somebody must be made answerable for it; and gravely proceeded:\n\n“‘Which don’t break open for a year, or until you have decisive intelligence of my dear Walter, who is dear to you, Ned, too, I am sure.’” The Captain paused and shook his head in some emotion; then, as a re-establishment of his dignity in this trying position, looked with exceeding sternness at the Grinder. “‘If you should never hear of me, or see me more, Ned, remember an old friend as he will remember you to the last—kindly; and at least until the period I have mentioned has expired, keep a home in the old place for Walter. There are no debts, the loan from Dombey’s House is paid off and all my keys I send with this. Keep this quiet, and make no inquiry for me; it is useless. So no more, dear Ned, from your true friend, Solomon Gills.’” The Captain took a long breath, and then read these words written below: “‘The boy Rob, well recommended, as I told you, from Dombey’s House. If all else should come to the hammer, take care, Ned, of the little Midshipman.’”\n\nTo convey to posterity any idea of the manner in which the Captain, after turning this letter over and over, and reading it a score of times, sat down in his chair, and held a court-martial on the subject in his own mind, would require the united genius of all the great men, who, discarding their own untoward days, have determined to go down to posterity, and have never got there. At first the Captain was too much confounded and distressed to think of anything but the letter itself; and even when his thoughts began to glance upon the various attendant facts, they might, perhaps, as well have occupied themselves with their former theme, for any light they reflected on them. In this state of mind, Captain Cuttle having the Grinder before the court, and no one else, found it a great relief to decide, generally, that he was an object of suspicion: which the Captain so clearly expressed in his visage, that Rob remonstrated.\n\n“Oh, don’t, Captain!” cried the Grinder. “I wonder how you can! what have I done to be looked at, like that?”\n\n“My lad,” said Captain Cuttle, “don’t you sing out afore you’re hurt. And don’t you commit yourself, whatever you do.”\n\n“I haven’t been and committed nothing, Captain!” answered Rob.\n\n“Keep her free, then,” said the Captain, impressively, “and ride easy.”\n\nWith a deep sense of the responsibility imposed upon him, and the necessity of thoroughly fathoming this mysterious affair as became a man in his relations with the parties, Captain Cuttle resolved to go down and examine the premises, and to keep the Grinder with him. Considering that youth as under arrest at present, the Captain was in some doubt whether it might not be expedient to handcuff him, or tie his ankles together, or attach a weight to his legs; but not being clear as to the legality of such formalities, the Captain decided merely to hold him by the shoulder all the way, and knock him down if he made any objection.\n\nHowever, he made none, and consequently got to the Instrument-maker’s house without being placed under any more stringent restraint. As the shutters were not yet taken down, the Captain’s first care was to have the shop opened; and when the daylight was freely admitted, he proceeded, with its aid, to further investigation.\n\nThe Captain’s first care was to establish himself in a chair in the shop, as President of the solemn tribunal that was sitting within him; and to require Rob to lie down in his bed under the counter, show exactly where he discovered the keys and packet when he awoke, how he found the door when he went to try it, how he started off to Brig Place—cautiously preventing the latter imitation from being carried farther than the threshold—and so on to the end of the chapter. When all this had been done several times, the Captain shook his head and seemed to think the matter had a bad look.\n\nNext, the Captain, with some indistinct idea of finding a body, instituted a strict search over the whole house; groping in the cellars with a lighted candle, thrusting his hook behind doors, bringing his head into violent contact with beams, and covering himself with cobwebs. Mounting up to the old man’s bed-room, they found that he had not been in bed on the previous night, but had merely lain down on the coverlet, as was evident from the impression yet remaining there.\n\n“And I think, Captain,” said Rob, looking round the room, “that when Mr Gills was going in and out so often, these last few days, he was taking little things away, piecemeal, not to attract attention.”\n\n“Ay!” said the Captain, mysteriously. “Why so, my lad?”\n\n“Why,” returned Rob, looking about, “I don’t see his shaving tackle. Nor his brushes, Captain. Nor no shirts. Nor yet his shoes.”\n\nAs each of these articles was mentioned, Captain Cuttle took particular notice of the corresponding department of the Grinder, lest he should appear to have been in recent use, or should prove to be in present possession thereof. But Rob had no occasion to shave, was not brushed, and wore the clothes he had on for a long time past, beyond all possibility of a mistake.\n\n“And what should you say,” said the Captain—“not committing yourself—about his time of sheering off? Hey?”\n\n“Why, I think, Captain,” returned Rob, “that he must have gone pretty soon after I began to snore.”\n\n“What o’clock was that?” said the Captain, prepared to be very particular about the exact time.\n\n“How can I tell, Captain!” answered Rob. “I only know that I’m a heavy sleeper at first, and a light one towards morning; and if Mr Gills had come through the shop near daybreak, though ever so much on tiptoe, I’m pretty sure I should have heard him shut the door at all events.”\n\nOn mature consideration of this evidence, Captain Cuttle began to think that the Instrument-maker must have vanished of his own accord; to which logical conclusion he was assisted by the letter addressed to himself, which, as being undeniably in the old man’s handwriting, would seem, with no great forcing, to bear the construction, that he arranged of his own will to go, and so went. The Captain had next to consider where and why? and as there was no way whatsoever that he saw to the solution of the first difficulty, he confined his meditations to the second.\n\nRemembering the old man’s curious manner, and the farewell he had taken of him; unaccountably fervent at the time, but quite intelligible now: a terrible apprehension strengthened on the Captain, that, overpowered by his anxieties and regrets for Walter, he had been driven to commit suicide. Unequal to the wear and tear of daily life, as he had often professed himself to be, and shaken as he no doubt was by the uncertainty and deferred hope he had undergone, it seemed no violently strained misgiving, but only too probable.\n\nFree from debt, and with no fear for his personal liberty, or the seizure of his goods, what else but such a state of madness could have hurried him away alone and secretly? As to his carrying some apparel with him, if he had really done so—and they were not even sure of that—he might have done so, the Captain argued, to prevent inquiry, to distract attention from his probable fate, or to ease the very mind that was now revolving all these possibilities. Such, reduced into plain language, and condensed within a small compass, was the final result and substance of Captain Cuttle’s deliberations: which took a long time to arrive at this pass, and were, like some more public deliberations, very discursive and disorderly.\n\nDejected and despondent in the extreme, Captain Cuttle felt it just to release Rob from the arrest in which he had placed him, and to enlarge him, subject to a kind of honourable inspection which he still resolved to exercise; and having hired a man, from Brogley the Broker, to sit in the shop during their absence, the Captain, taking Rob with him, issued forth upon a dismal quest after the mortal remains of Solomon Gills.\n\nNot a station-house, or bone-house, or work-house in the metropolis escaped a visitation from the hard glazed hat. Along the wharves, among the shipping on the bank-side, up the river, down the river, here, there, everywhere, it went gleaming where men were thickest, like the hero’s helmet in an epic battle. For a whole week the Captain read of all the found and missing people in all the newspapers and handbills, and went forth on expeditions at all hours of the day to identify Solomon Gills, in poor little ship-boys who had fallen overboard, and in tall foreigners with dark beards who had taken poison—“to make sure,” Captain Cuttle said, “that it wam’t him.” It is a sure thing that it never was, and that the good Captain had no other satisfaction.\n\nCaptain Cuttle at last abandoned these attempts as hopeless, and set himself to consider what was to be done next. After several new perusals of his poor friend’s letter, he considered that the maintenance of “a home in the old place for Walter” was the primary duty imposed upon him. Therefore, the Captain’s decision was, that he would keep house on the premises of Solomon Gills himself, and would go into the instrument-business, and see what came of it.\n\nBut as this step involved the relinquishment of his apartments at Mrs MacStinger’s, and he knew that resolute woman would never hear of his deserting them, the Captain took the desperate determination of running away.\n\n“Now, look ye here, my lad,” said the Captain to Rob, when he had matured this notable scheme, “to-morrow, I shan’t be found in this here roadstead till night—not till arter midnight p’rhaps. But you keep watch till you hear me knock, and the moment you do, turn-to, and open the door.”\n\n“Very good, Captain,” said Rob.\n\n“You’ll continue to be rated on these here books,” pursued the Captain condescendingly, “and I don’t say but what you may get promotion, if you and me should pull together with a will. But the moment you hear me knock to-morrow night, whatever time it is, turn-to and show yourself smart with the door.”\n\n“I’ll be sure to do it, Captain,” replied Rob.\n\n“Because you understand,” resumed the Captain, coming back again to enforce this charge upon his mind, “there may be, for anything I can say, a chase; and I might be took while I was waiting, if you didn’t show yourself smart with the door.”\n\nRob again assured the Captain that he would be prompt and wakeful; and the Captain having made this prudent arrangement, went home to Mrs MacStinger’s for the last time.\n\nThe sense the Captain had of its being the last time, and of the awful purpose hidden beneath his blue waistcoat, inspired him with such a mortal dread of Mrs MacStinger, that the sound of that lady’s foot downstairs at any time of the day, was sufficient to throw him into a fit of trembling. It fell out, too, that Mrs MacStinger was in a charming temper—mild and placid as a house—lamb; and Captain Cuttle’s conscience suffered terrible twinges, when she came up to inquire if she could cook him nothing for his dinner.\n\n“A nice small kidney-pudding now, Cap’en Cuttle,” said his landlady: “or a sheep’s heart. Don’t mind my trouble.”\n\n“No thank’ee, Ma’am,” returned the Captain.\n\n“Have a roast fowl,” said Mrs MacStinger, “with a bit of weal stuffing and some egg sauce. Come, Cap’en Cuttle! Give yourself a little treat!”\n\n“No thank’ee, Ma’am,” returned the Captain very humbly.\n\n“I’m sure you’re out of sorts, and want to be stimulated,” said Mrs MacStinger. “Why not have, for once in a way, a bottle of sherry wine?”\n\n“Well, Ma’am,” rejoined the Captain, “if you’d be so good as take a glass or two, I think I would try that. Would you do me the favour, Ma’am,” said the Captain, torn to pieces by his conscience, “to accept a quarter’s rent ahead?”\n\n“And why so, Cap’en Cuttle?” retorted Mrs MacStinger—sharply, as the Captain thought.\n\nThe Captain was frightened to dead “If you would Ma’am,” he said with submission, “it would oblige me. I can’t keep my money very well. It pays itself out. I should take it kind if you’d comply.”\n\n“Well, Cap’en Cuttle,” said the unconscious MacStinger, rubbing her hands, “you can do as you please. It’s not for me, with my family, to refuse, no more than it is to ask.”\n\n“And would you, Ma’am,” said the Captain, taking down the tin canister in which he kept his cash, from the top shelf of the cupboard, “be so good as offer eighteen-pence a-piece to the little family all round? If you could make it convenient, Ma’am, to pass the word presently for them children to come for’ard, in a body, I should be glad to see ’em.”\n\nThese innocent MacStingers were so many daggers to the Captain’s breast, when they appeared in a swarm, and tore at him with the confiding trustfulness he so little deserved. The eye of Alexander MacStinger, who had been his favourite, was insupportable to the Captain; the voice of Juliana MacStinger, who was the picture of her mother, made a coward of him.\n\nCaptain Cuttle kept up appearances, nevertheless, tolerably well, and for an hour or two was very hardly used and roughly handled by the young MacStingers: who in their childish frolics, did a little damage also to the glazed hat, by sitting in it, two at a time, as in a nest, and drumming on the inside of the crown with their shoes. At length the Captain sorrowfully dismissed them: taking leave of these cherubs with the poignant remorse and grief of a man who was going to execution.\n\nIn the silence of night, the Captain packed up his heavier property in a chest, which he locked, intending to leave it there, in all probability for ever, but on the forlorn chance of one day finding a man sufficiently bold and desperate to come and ask for it. Of his lighter necessaries, the Captain made a bundle; and disposed his plate about his person, ready for flight. At the hour of midnight, when Brig Place was buried in slumber, and Mrs MacStinger was lulled in sweet oblivion, with her infants around her, the guilty Captain, stealing down on tiptoe, in the dark, opened the door, closed it softly after him, and took to his heels.\n\nPursued by the image of Mrs MacStinger springing out of bed, and, regardless of costume, following and bringing him back; pursued also by a consciousness of his enormous crime; Captain Cuttle held on at a great pace, and allowed no grass to grow under his feet, between Brig Place and the Instrument-maker’s door. It opened when he knocked—for Rob was on the watch—and when it was bolted and locked behind him, Captain Cuttle felt comparatively safe.\n\n“Whew!” cried the Captain, looking round him. “It’s a breather!”\n\n“Nothing the matter, is there, Captain?” cried the gaping Rob.\n\n“No, no!” said Captain Cuttle, after changing colour, and listening to a passing footstep in the street. “But mind ye, my lad; if any lady, except either of them two as you see t’other day, ever comes and asks for Cap’en Cuttle, be sure to report no person of that name known, nor never heard of here; observe them orders, will you?”\n\n“I’ll take care, Captain,” returned Rob.\n\n“You might say—if you liked,” hesitated the Captain, “that you’d read in the paper that a Cap’en of that name was gone to Australia, emigrating, along with a whole ship’s complement of people as had all swore never to come back no more.”\n\nRob nodded his understanding of these instructions; and Captain Cuttle promising to make a man of him, if he obeyed orders, dismissed him, yawning, to his bed under the counter, and went aloft to the chamber of Solomon Gills.\n\nWhat the Captain suffered next day, whenever a bonnet passed, or how often he darted out of the shop to elude imaginary MacStingers, and sought safety in the attic, cannot be told. But to avoid the fatigues attendant on this means of self-preservation, the Captain curtained the glass door of communication between the shop and parlour, on the inside; fitted a key to it from the bunch that had been sent to him; and cut a small hole of espial in the wall. The advantage of this fortification is obvious. On a bonnet appearing, the Captain instantly slipped into his garrison, locked himself up, and took a secret observation of the enemy. Finding it a false alarm, the Captain instantly slipped out again. And the bonnets in the street were so very numerous, and alarms were so inseparable from their appearance, that the Captain was almost incessantly slipping in and out all day long.\n\nCaptain Cuttle found time, however, in the midst of this fatiguing service to inspect the stock; in connexion with which he had the general idea (very laborious to Rob) that too much friction could not be bestowed upon it, and that it could not be made too bright. He also ticketed a few attractive-looking articles at a venture, at prices ranging from ten shillings to fifty pounds, and exposed them in the window to the great astonishment of the public.\n\nAfter effecting these improvements, Captain Cuttle, surrounded by the instruments, began to feel scientific: and looked up at the stars at night, through the skylight, when he was smoking his pipe in the little back parlour before going to bed, as if he had established a kind of property in them. As a tradesman in the City, too, he began to have an interest in the Lord Mayor, and the Sheriffs, and in Public Companies; and felt bound to read the quotations of the Funds every day, though he was unable to make out, on any principle of navigation, what the figures meant, and could have very well dispensed with the fractions. Florence, the Captain waited on, with his strange news of Uncle Sol, immediately after taking possession of the Midshipman; but she was away from home. So the Captain sat himself down in his altered station of life, with no company but Rob the Grinder; and losing count of time, as men do when great changes come upon them, thought musingly of Walter, and of Solomon Gills, and even of Mrs MacStinger herself, as among the things that had been.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVI. Shadows of the Past and Future\n\nYour most obedient, Sir,” said the Major. “Damme, Sir, a friend of my friend Dombey’s is a friend of mine, and I’m glad to see you!”\n\n“I am infinitely obliged, Carker,” explained Mr Dombey, “to Major Bagstock, for his company and conversation. Major Bagstock has rendered me great service, Carker.”\n\nMr Carker the Manager, hat in hand, just arrived at Leamington, and just introduced to the Major, showed the Major his whole double range of teeth, and trusted he might take the liberty of thanking him with all his heart for having effected so great an Improvement in Mr Dombey’s looks and spirits.\n\n“By Gad, Sir,” said the Major, in reply, “there are no thanks due to me, for it’s a give and take affair. A great creature like our friend Dombey, Sir,” said the Major, lowering his voice, but not lowering it so much as to render it inaudible to that gentleman, “cannot help improving and exalting his friends. He strengthens and invigorates a man, Sir, does Dombey, in his moral nature.”\n\nMr Carker snapped at the expression. In his moral nature. Exactly. The very words he had been on the point of suggesting.\n\n“But when my friend Dombey, Sir,” added the Major, “talks to you of Major Bagstock, I must crave leave to set him and you right. He means plain Joe, Sir—Joey B.—Josh. Bagstock—Joseph—rough and tough Old J., Sir. At your service.”\n\nMr Carker’s excessively friendly inclinations towards the Major, and Mr Carker’s admiration of his roughness, toughness, and plainness, gleamed out of every tooth in Mr Carker’s head.\n\n“And now, Sir,” said the Major, “you and Dombey have the devil’s own amount of business to talk over.”\n\n“By no means, Major,” observed Mr Dombey.\n\n“Dombey,” said the Major, defiantly, “I know better; a man of your mark—the Colossus of commerce—is not to be interrupted. Your moments are precious. We shall meet at dinner-time. In the interval, old Joseph will be scarce. The dinner-hour is a sharp seven, Mr Carker.”\n\nWith that, the Major, greatly swollen as to his face, withdrew; but immediately putting in his head at the door again, said:\n\n“I beg your pardon. Dombey, have you any message to ’em?”\n\nMr Dombey in some embarrassment, and not without a glance at the courteous keeper of his business confidence, entrusted the Major with his compliments.\n\n“By the Lord, Sir,” said the Major, “you must make it something warmer than that, or old Joe will be far from welcome.”\n\n“Regards then, if you will, Major,” returned Mr Dombey.\n\n“Damme, Sir,” said the Major, shaking his shoulders and his great cheeks jocularly: “make it something warmer than that.”\n\n“What you please, then, Major,” observed Mr Dombey.\n\n“Our friend is sly, Sir, sly, Sir, de-vilish sly,” said the Major, staring round the door at Carker. “So is Bagstock.” But stopping in the midst of a chuckle, and drawing himself up to his full height, the Major solemnly exclaimed, as he struck himself on the chest, “Dombey! I envy your feelings. God bless you!” and withdrew.\n\n“You must have found the gentleman a great resource,” said Carker, following him with his teeth.\n\n“Very great indeed,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“He has friends here, no doubt,” pursued Carker. “I perceive, from what he has said, that you go into society here. Do you know,” smiling horribly, “I am so very glad that you go into society!”\n\nMr Dombey acknowledged this display of interest on the part of his second in command, by twirling his watch-chain, and slightly moving his head.\n\n“You were formed for society,” said Carker. “Of all the men I know, you are the best adapted, by nature and by position, for society. Do you know I have been frequently amazed that you should have held it at arm’s length so long!”\n\n“I have had my reasons, Carker. I have been alone, and indifferent to it. But you have great social qualifications yourself, and are the more likely to have been surprised.”\n\n“Oh! I!” returned the other, with ready self-disparagement. “It’s quite another matter in the case of a man like me. I don’t come into comparison with you.”\n\nMr Dombey put his hand to his neckcloth, settled his chin in it, coughed, and stood looking at his faithful friend and servant for a few moments in silence.\n\n“I shall have the pleasure, Carker,” said Mr Dombey at length: making as if he swallowed something a little too large for his throat: “to present you to my—to the Major’s friends. Highly agreeable people.”\n\n“Ladies among them, I presume?” insinuated the smooth Manager.\n\n“They are all—that is to say, they are both—ladies,” replied Mr Dombey.\n\n“Only two?” smiled Carker.\n\n“They are only two. I have confined my visits to their residence, and have made no other acquaintance here.”\n\n“Sisters, perhaps?” quoth Carker.\n\n“Mother and daughter,” replied Mr Dombey.\n\nAs Mr Dombey dropped his eyes, and adjusted his neckcloth again, the smiling face of Mr Carker the Manager became in a moment, and without any stage of transition, transformed into a most intent and frowning face, scanning his closely, and with an ugly sneer. As Mr Dombey raised his eyes, it changed back, no less quickly, to its old expression, and showed him every gum of which it stood possessed.\n\n“You are very kind,” said Carker, “I shall be delighted to know them. Speaking of daughters, I have seen Miss Dombey.”\n\nThere was a sudden rush of blood to Mr Dombey’s face.\n\n“I took the liberty of waiting on her,” said Carker, “to inquire if she could charge me with any little commission. I am not so fortunate as to be the bearer of any but her—but her dear love.”\n\nWolf’s face that it was then, with even the hot tongue revealing itself through the stretched mouth, as the eyes encountered Mr Dombey’s!\n\n“What business intelligence is there?” inquired the latter gentleman, after a silence, during which Mr Carker had produced some memoranda and other papers.\n\n“There is very little,” returned Carker. “Upon the whole we have not had our usual good fortune of late, but that is of little moment to you. At Lloyd’s, they give up the Son and Heir for lost. Well, she was insured, from her keel to her masthead.”\n\n“Carker,” said Mr Dombey, taking a chair near him, “I cannot say that young man, Gay, ever impressed me favourably—”\n\n“Nor me,” interposed the Manager.\n\n“—But I wish,” said Mr Dombey, without heeding the interruption, “he had never gone on board that ship. I wish he had never been sent out.\n\n“It is a pity you didn’t say so, in good time, is it not?” retorted Carker, coolly. “However, I think it’s all for the best. I really, think it’s all for the best. Did I mention that there was something like a little confidence between Miss Dombey and myself?”\n\n“No,” said Mr Dombey, sternly.\n\n“I have no doubt,” returned Mr Carker, after an impressive pause, “that wherever Gay is, he is much better where he is, than at home here. If I were, or could be, in your place, I should be satisfied of that. I am quite satisfied of it myself. Miss Dombey is confiding and young—perhaps hardly proud enough, for your daughter—if she have a fault. Not that that is much though, I am sure. Will you check these balances with me?”\n\nMr Dombey leaned back in his chair, instead of bending over the papers that were laid before him, and looked the Manager steadily in the face. The Manager, with his eyelids slightly raised, affected to be glancing at his figures, and to await the leisure of his principal. He showed that he affected this, as if from great delicacy, and with a design to spare Mr Dombey’s feelings; and the latter, as he looked at him, was cognizant of his intended consideration, and felt that but for it, this confidential Carker would have said a great deal more, which he, Mr Dombey, was too proud to ask for. It was his way in business, often. Little by little, Mr Dombey’s gaze relaxed, and his attention became diverted to the papers before him; but while busy with the occupation they afforded him, he frequently stopped, and looked at Mr Carker again. Whenever he did so, Mr Carker was demonstrative, as before, in his delicacy, and impressed it on his great chief more and more.\n\nWhile they were thus engaged; and under the skilful culture of the Manager, angry thoughts in reference to poor Florence brooded and bred in Mr Dombey’s breast, usurping the place of the cold dislike that generally reigned there; Major Bagstock, much admired by the old ladies of Leamington, and followed by the Native, carrying the usual amount of light baggage, straddled along the shady side of the way, to make a morning call on Mrs Skewton. It being midday when the Major reached the bower of Cleopatra, he had the good fortune to find his Princess on her usual sofa, languishing over a cup of coffee, with the room so darkened and shaded for her more luxurious repose, that Withers, who was in attendance on her, loomed like a phantom page.\n\n“What insupportable creature is this, coming in?” said Mrs Skewton, “I cannot hear it. Go away, whoever you are!”\n\n“You have not the heart to banish J. B., Ma’am!” said the Major halting midway, to remonstrate, with his cane over his shoulder.\n\n“Oh it’s you, is it? On second thoughts, you may enter,” observed Cleopatra.\n\nThe Major entered accordingly, and advancing to the sofa pressed her charming hand to his lips.\n\n“Sit down,” said Cleopatra, listlessly waving her fan, “a long way off. Don’t come too near me, for I am frightfully faint and sensitive this morning, and you smell of the Sun. You are absolutely tropical.”\n\n“By George, Ma’am,” said the Major, “the time has been when Joseph Bagstock has been grilled and blistered by the Sun; then time was, when he was forced, Ma’am, into such full blow, by high hothouse heat in the West Indies, that he was known as the Flower. A man never heard of Bagstock, Ma’am, in those days; he heard of the Flower—the Flower of Ours. The Flower may have faded, more or less, Ma’am,” observed the Major, dropping into a much nearer chair than had been indicated by his cruel Divinity, “but it is a tough plant yet, and constant as the evergreen.”\n\nHere the Major, under cover of the dark room, shut up one eye, rolled his head like a Harlequin, and, in his great self-satisfaction, perhaps went nearer to the confines of apoplexy than he had ever gone before.\n\n“Where is Mrs Granger?” inquired Cleopatra of her page.\n\nWithers believed she was in her own room.\n\n“Very well,” said Mrs Skewton. “Go away, and shut the door. I am engaged.”\n\nAs Withers disappeared, Mrs Skewton turned her head languidly towards the Major, without otherwise moving, and asked him how his friend was.\n\n“Dombey, Ma’am,” returned the Major, with a facetious gurgling in his throat, “is as well as a man in his condition can be. His condition is a desperate one, Ma’am. He is touched, is Dombey! Touched!” cried the Major. “He is bayonetted through the body.”\n\nCleopatra cast a sharp look at the Major, that contrasted forcibly with the affected drawl in which she presently said:\n\n“Major Bagstock, although I know but little of the world,—nor can I really regret my experience, for I fear it is a false place, full of withering conventionalities: where Nature is but little regarded, and where the music of the heart, and the gushing of the soul, and all that sort of thing, which is so truly poetical, is seldom heard,—I cannot misunderstand your meaning. There is an allusion to Edith—to my extremely dear child,” said Mrs Skewton, tracing the outline of her eyebrows with her forefinger, “in your words, to which the tenderest of chords vibrates excessively.”\n\n“Bluntness, Ma’am,” returned the Major, “has ever been the characteristic of the Bagstock breed. You are right. Joe admits it.”\n\n“And that allusion,” pursued Cleopatra, “would involve one of the most—if not positively the most—touching, and thrilling, and sacred emotions of which our sadly-fallen nature is susceptible, I conceive.”\n\nThe Major laid his hand upon his lips, and wafted a kiss to Cleopatra, as if to identify the emotion in question.\n\n“I feel that I am weak. I feel that I am wanting in that energy, which should sustain a Mama: not to say a parent: on such a subject,” said Mrs Skewton, trimming her lips with the laced edge of her pocket-handkerchief; “but I can hardly approach a topic so excessively momentous to my dearest Edith without a feeling of faintness. Nevertheless, bad man, as you have boldly remarked upon it, and as it has occasioned me great anguish:” Mrs Skewton touched her left side with her fan: “I will not shrink from my duty.”\n\nThe Major, under cover of the dimness, swelled, and swelled, and rolled his purple face about, and winked his lobster eye, until he fell into a fit of wheezing, which obliged him to rise and take a turn or two about the room, before his fair friend could proceed.\n\n“Mr Dombey,” said Mrs Skewton, when she at length resumed, “was obliging enough, now many weeks ago, to do us the honour of visiting us here; in company, my dear Major, with yourself. I acknowledge—let me be open—that it is my failing to be the creature of impulse, and to wear my heart as it were, outside. I know my failing full well. My enemy cannot know it better. But I am not penitent; I would rather not be frozen by the heartless world, and am content to bear this imputation justly.”\n\nMrs Skewton arranged her tucker, pinched her wiry throat to give it a soft surface, and went on, with great complacency.\n\n“It gave me (my dearest Edith too, I am sure) infinite pleasure to receive Mr Dombey. As a friend of yours, my dear Major, we were naturally disposed to be prepossessed in his favour; and I fancied that I observed an amount of Heart in Mr Dombey, that was excessively refreshing.”\n\n“There is devilish little heart in Dombey now, Ma’am,” said the Major.\n\n“Wretched man!” cried Mrs Skewton, looking at him languidly, “pray be silent.”\n\n“J. B. is dumb, Ma’am,” said the Major.\n\n“Mr Dombey,” pursued Cleopatra, smoothing the rosy hue upon her cheeks, “accordingly repeated his visit; and possibly finding some attraction in the simplicity and primitiveness of our tastes—for there is always a charm in nature—it is so very sweet—became one of our little circle every evening. Little did I think of the awful responsibility into which I plunged when I encouraged Mr Dombey—to”—\n\n“To beat up these quarters, Ma’am,” suggested Major Bagstock.\n\n“Coarse person!” said Mrs Skewton, “you anticipate my meaning, though in odious language.”\n\nHere Mrs Skewton rested her elbow on the little table at her side, and suffering her wrist to droop in what she considered a graceful and becoming manner, dangled her fan to and fro, and lazily admired her hand while speaking.\n\n“The agony I have endured,” she said mincingly, “as the truth has by degrees dawned upon me, has been too exceedingly terrific to dilate upon. My whole existence is bound up in my sweetest Edith; and to see her change from day to day—my beautiful pet, who has positively garnered up her heart since the death of that most delightful creature, Granger—is the most affecting thing in the world.”\n\nMrs Skewton’s world was not a very trying one, if one might judge of it by the influence of its most affecting circumstance upon her; but this by the way.\n\n“Edith,” simpered Mrs Skewton, “who is the perfect pearl of my life, is said to resemble me. I believe we are alike.”\n\n“There is one man in the world who never will admit that anyone resembles you, Ma’am,” said the Major; “and that man’s name is Old Joe Bagstock.”\n\nCleopatra made as if she would brain the flatterer with her fan, but relenting, smiled upon him and proceeded:\n\n“If my charming girl inherits any advantages from me, wicked one!”: the Major was the wicked one: “she inherits also my foolish nature. She has great force of character—mine has been said to be immense, though I don’t believe it—but once moved, she is susceptible and sensitive to the last extent. What are my feelings when I see her pining! They destroy me.\n\nThe Major advancing his double chin, and pursing up his blue lips into a soothing expression, affected the profoundest sympathy.\n\n“The confidence,” said Mrs Skewton, “that has subsisted between us—the free development of soul, and openness of sentiment—is touching to think of. We have been more like sisters than Mama and child.”\n\n“J. B.“s own sentiment,” observed the Major, “expressed by J. B. fifty thousand times!”\n\n“Do not interrupt, rude man!” said Cleopatra. “What are my feelings, then, when I find that there is one subject avoided by us! That there is a what’s-his-name—a gulf—opened between us. That my own artless Edith is changed to me! They are of the most poignant description, of course.”\n\nThe Major left his chair, and took one nearer to the little table.\n\n“From day to day I see this, my dear Major,” proceeded Mrs Skewton. “From day to day I feel this. From hour to hour I reproach myself for that excess of faith and trustfulness which has led to such distressing consequences; and almost from minute to minute, I hope that Mr Dombey may explain himself, and relieve the torture I undergo, which is extremely wearing. But nothing happens, my dear Major; I am the slave of remorse—take care of the coffee-cup: you are so very awkward—my darling Edith is an altered being; and I really don’t see what is to be done, or what good creature I can advise with.”\n\nMajor Bagstock, encouraged perhaps by the softened and confidential tone into which Mrs Skewton, after several times lapsing into it for a moment, seemed now to have subsided for good, stretched out his hand across the little table, and said with a leer,\n\n“Advise with Joe, Ma’am.”\n\n“Then, you aggravating monster,” said Cleopatra, giving one hand to the Major, and tapping his knuckles with her fan, which she held in the other: “why don’t you talk to me? you know what I mean. Why don’t you tell me something to the purpose?”\n\nThe Major laughed, and kissed the hand she had bestowed upon him, and laughed again immensely.\n\n“Is there as much Heart in Mr Dombey as I gave him credit for?” languished Cleopatra tenderly. “Do you think he is in earnest, my dear Major? Would you recommend his being spoken to, or his being left alone? Now tell me, like a dear man, what would you advise.”\n\n“Shall we marry him to Edith Granger, Ma’am?” chuckled the Major, hoarsely.\n\n“Mysterious creature!” returned Cleopatra, bringing her fan to bear upon the Major’s nose. “How can we marry him?”\n\n“Shall we marry him to Edith Granger, Ma’am, I say?” chuckled the Major again.\n\nMrs Skewton returned no answer in words, but smiled upon the Major with so much archness and vivacity, that that gallant officer considering himself challenged, would have imprinted a kiss on her exceedingly red lips, but for her interposing the fan with a very winning and juvenile dexterity. It might have been in modesty; it might have been in apprehension of some danger to their bloom.\n\n“Dombey, Ma’am,” said the Major, “is a great catch.”\n\n“Oh, mercenary wretch!” cried Cleopatra, with a little shriek, “I am shocked.”\n\n“And Dombey, Ma’am,” pursued the Major, thrusting forward his head, and distending his eyes, “is in earnest. Joseph says it; Bagstock knows it; J. B. keeps him to the mark. Leave Dombey to himself, Ma’am. Dombey is safe, Ma’am. Do as you have done; do no more; and trust to J. B. for the end.”\n\n“You really think so, my dear Major?” returned Cleopatra, who had eyed him very cautiously, and very searchingly, in spite of her listless bearing.\n\n“Sure of it, Ma’am,” rejoined the Major. “Cleopatra the peerless, and her Antony Bagstock, will often speak of this, triumphantly, when sharing the elegance and wealth of Edith Dombey’s establishment. Dombey’s right-hand man, Ma’am,” said the Major, stopping abruptly in a chuckle, and becoming serious, “has arrived.”\n\n“This morning?” said Cleopatra.\n\n“This morning, Ma’am,” returned the Major. “And Dombey’s anxiety for his arrival, Ma’am, is to be referred—take J. B.“s word for this; for Joe is devilish sly”—the Major tapped his nose, and screwed up one of his eyes tight: which did not enhance his native beauty—“to his desire that what is in the wind should become known to him” without Dombey’s telling and consulting him. For Dombey is as proud, Ma’am,” said the Major, “as Lucifer.”\n\n“A charming quality,” lisped Mrs Skewton; “reminding one of dearest Edith.”\n\n“Well, Ma’am,” said the Major. “I have thrown out hints already, and the right-hand man understands ’em; and I’ll throw out more, before the day is done. Dombey projected this morning a ride to Warwick Castle, and to Kenilworth, to-morrow, to be preceded by a breakfast with us. I undertook the delivery of this invitation. Will you honour us so far, Ma’am?” said the Major, swelling with shortness of breath and slyness, as he produced a note, addressed to the Honourable Mrs Skewton, by favour of Major Bagstock, wherein hers ever faithfully, Paul Dombey, besought her and her amiable and accomplished daughter to consent to the proposed excursion; and in a postscript unto which, the same ever faithfully Paul Dombey entreated to be recalled to the remembrance of Mrs Granger.\n\n“Hush!” said Cleopatra, suddenly, “Edith!”\n\nThe loving mother can scarcely be described as resuming her insipid and affected air when she made this exclamation; for she had never cast it off; nor was it likely that she ever would or could, in any other place than in the grave. But hurriedly dismissing whatever shadow of earnestness, or faint confession of a purpose, laudable or wicked, that her face, or voice, or manner: had, for the moment, betrayed, she lounged upon the couch, her most insipid and most languid self again, as Edith entered the room.\n\nEdith, so beautiful and stately, but so cold and so repelling. Who, slightly acknowledging the presence of Major Bagstock, and directing a keen glance at her mother, drew back from a window, and sat down there, looking out.\n\n“My dearest Edith,” said Mrs Skewton, “where on earth have you been? I have wanted you, my love, most sadly.”\n\n“You said you were engaged, and I stayed away,” she answered, without turning her head.\n\n“It was cruel to Old Joe, Ma’am,” said the Major in his gallantry.\n\n“It was very cruel, I know,” she said, still looking out—and said with such calm disdain, that the Major was discomfited, and could think of nothing in reply.\n\n“Major Bagstock, my darling Edith,” drawled her mother, “who is generally the most useless and disagreeable creature in the world: as you know—”\n\n“It is surely not worthwhile, Mama,” said Edith, looking round, “to observe these forms of speech. We are quite alone. We know each other.”\n\nThe quiet scorn that sat upon her handsome face—a scorn that evidently lighted on herself, no less than them—was so intense and deep, that her mother’s simper, for the instant, though of a hardy constitution, drooped before it.\n\n“My darling girl,” she began again.\n\n“Not woman yet?” said Edith, with a smile.\n\n“How very odd you are today, my dear! Pray let me say, my love, that Major Bagstock has brought the kindest of notes from Mr Dombey, proposing that we should breakfast with him to-morrow, and ride to Warwick and Kenilworth. Will you go, Edith?”\n\n“Will I go!” she repeated, turning very red, and breathing quickly as she looked round at her mother.\n\n“I knew you would, my own, observed the latter carelessly. “It is, as you say, quite a form to ask. Here is Mr Dombey’s letter, Edith.”\n\n“Thank you. I have no desire to read it,” was her answer.\n\n“Then perhaps I had better answer it myself,” said Mrs Skewton, “though I had thought of asking you to be my secretary, darling.” As Edith made no movement, and no answer, Mrs Skewton begged the Major to wheel her little table nearer, and to set open the desk it contained, and to take out pen and paper for her; all which congenial offices of gallantry the Major discharged, with much submission and devotion.\n\n“Your regards, Edith, my dear?” said Mrs Skewton, pausing, pen in hand, at the postscript.\n\n“What you will, Mama,” she answered, without turning her head, and with supreme indifference.\n\nMrs Skewton wrote what she would, without seeking for any more explicit directions, and handed her letter to the Major, who receiving it as a precious charge, made a show of laying it near his heart, but was fain to put it in the pocket of his pantaloons on account of the insecurity of his waistcoat. The Major then took a very polished and chivalrous farewell of both ladies, which the elder one acknowledged in her usual manner, while the younger, sitting with her face addressed to the window, bent her head so slightly that it would have been a greater compliment to the Major to have made no sign at all, and to have left him to infer that he had not been heard or thought of.\n\n“As to alteration in her, Sir,” mused the Major on his way back; on which expedition—the afternoon being sunny and hot—he ordered the Native and the light baggage to the front, and walked in the shadow of that expatriated prince: “as to alteration, Sir, and pining, and so forth, that won’t go down with Joseph Bagstock, None of that, Sir. It won’t do here. But as to there being something of a division between ’em—or a gulf as the mother calls it—damme, Sir, that seems true enough. And it’s odd enough! Well, Sir!” panted the Major, “Edith Granger and Dombey are well matched; let ’em fight it out! Bagstock backs the winner!”\n\nThe Major, by saying these latter words aloud, in the vigour of his thoughts, caused the unhappy Native to stop, and turn round, in the belief that he was personally addressed. Exasperated to the last degree by this act of insubordination, the Major (though he was swelling with enjoyment of his own humour), at the moment of its occurrence instantly thrust his cane among the Native’s ribs, and continued to stir him up, at short intervals, all the way to the hotel.\n\nNor was the Major less exasperated as he dressed for dinner, during which operation the dark servant underwent the pelting of a shower of miscellaneous objects, varying in size from a boot to a hairbrush, and including everything that came within his master’s reach. For the Major plumed himself on having the Native in a perfect state of drill, and visited the least departure from strict discipline with this kind of fatigue duty. Add to this, that he maintained the Native about his person as a counter-irritant against the gout, and all other vexations, mental as well as bodily; and the Native would appear to have earned his pay—which was not large.\n\nAt length, the Major having disposed of all the missiles that were convenient to his hand, and having called the Native so many new names as must have given him great occasion to marvel at the resources of the English language, submitted to have his cravat put on; and being dressed, and finding himself in a brisk flow of spirits after this exercise, went downstairs to enliven “Dombey” and his right-hand man.\n\nDombey was not yet in the room, but the right-hand man was there, and his dental treasures were, as usual, ready for the Major.\n\n“Well, Sir!” said the Major. “How have you passed the time since I had the happiness of meeting you? Have you walked at all?”\n\n“A saunter of barely half an hour’s duration,” returned Carker. “We have been so much occupied.”\n\n“Business, eh?” said the Major.\n\n“A variety of little matters necessary to be gone through,” replied Carker. “But do you know—this is quite unusual with me, educated in a distrustful school, and who am not generally disposed to be communicative,” he said, breaking off, and speaking in a charming tone of frankness—“but I feel quite confidential with you, Major Bagstock.”\n\n“You do me honour, Sir,” returned the Major. “You may be.”\n\n“Do you know, then,” pursued Carker, “that I have not found my friend—our friend, I ought rather to call him—”\n\n“Meaning Dombey, Sir?” cried the Major. “You see me, Mr Carker, standing here! J. B.?”\n\nHe was puffy enough to see, and blue enough; and Mr Carker intimated the he had that pleasure.\n\n“Then you see a man, Sir, who would go through fire and water to serve Dombey,” returned Major Bagstock.\n\nMr Carker smiled, and said he was sure of it. “Do you know, Major,” he proceeded: “to resume where I left off: that I have not found our friend so attentive to business today, as usual?”\n\n“No?” observed the delighted Major.\n\n“I have found him a little abstracted, and with his attention disposed to wander,” said Carker.\n\n“By Jove, Sir,” cried the Major, “there’s a lady in the case.”\n\n“Indeed, I begin to believe there really is,” returned Carker; “I thought you might be jesting when you seemed to hint at it; for I know you military men”—\n\nThe Major gave the horse’s cough, and shook his head and shoulders, as much as to say, “Well! we are gay dogs, there’s no denying.” He then seized Mr Carker by the button-hole, and with starting eyes whispered in his ear, that she was a woman of extraordinary charms, Sir. That she was a young widow, Sir. That she was of a fine family, Sir. That Dombey was over head and ears in love with her, Sir, and that it would be a good match on both sides; for she had beauty, blood, and talent, and Dombey had fortune; and what more could any couple have? Hearing Mr Dombey’s footsteps without, the Major cut himself short by saying, that Mr Carker would see her tomorrow morning, and would judge for himself; and between his mental excitement, and the exertion of saying all this in wheezy whispers, the Major sat gurgling in the throat and watering at the eyes, until dinner was ready.\n\nThe Major, like some other noble animals, exhibited himself to great advantage at feeding-time. On this occasion, he shone resplendent at one end of the table, supported by the milder lustre of Mr Dombey at the other; while Carker on one side lent his ray to either light, or suffered it to merge into both, as occasion arose.\n\nDuring the first course or two, the Major was usually grave; for the Native, in obedience to general orders, secretly issued, collected every sauce and cruet round him, and gave him a great deal to do, in taking out the stoppers, and mixing up the contents in his plate. Besides which, the Native had private zests and flavours on a side-table, with which the Major daily scorched himself; to say nothing of strange machines out of which he spirited unknown liquids into the Major’s drink. But on this occasion, Major Bagstock, even amidst these many occupations, found time to be social; and his sociality consisted in excessive slyness for the behoof of Mr Carker, and the betrayal of Mr Dombey’s state of mind.\n\n“Dombey,” said the Major, “you don’t eat; what’s the matter?”\n\n“Thank you,” returned the gentleman, “I am doing very well; I have no great appetite today.”\n\n“Why, Dombey, what’s become of it?” asked the Major. “Where’s it gone? You haven’t left it with our friends, I’ll swear, for I can answer for their having none today at luncheon. I can answer for one of ’em, at least: I won’t say which.”\n\nThen the Major winked at Carker, and became so frightfully sly, that his dark attendant was obliged to pat him on the back, without orders, or he would probably have disappeared under the table.\n\nIn a later stage of the dinner: that is to say, when the Native stood at the Major’s elbow ready to serve the first bottle of champagne: the Major became still slyer.\n\n“Fill this to the brim, you scoundrel,” said the Major, holding up his glass. “Fill Mr Carker’s to the brim too. And Mr Dombey’s too. By Gad, gentlemen,” said the Major, winking at his new friend, while Mr Dombey looked into his plate with a conscious air, “we’ll consecrate this glass of wine to a Divinity whom Joe is proud to know, and at a distance humbly and reverently to admire. Edith,” said the Major, “is her name; angelic Edith!”\n\n“To angelic Edith!” cried the smiling Carker.\n\n“Edith, by all means,” said Mr Dombey.\n\nThe entrance of the waiters with new dishes caused the Major to be slyer yet, but in a more serious vein. “For though among ourselves, Joe Bagstock mingles jest and earnest on this subject, Sir,” said the Major, laying his finger on his lips, and speaking half apart to Carker, “he holds that name too sacred to be made the property of these fellows, or of any fellows. Not a word, Sir, while they are here!”\n\nThis was respectful and becoming on the Major’s part, and Mr Dombey plainly felt it so. Although embarrassed in his own frigid way, by the Major’s allusions, Mr Dombey had no objection to such rallying, it was clear, but rather courted it. Perhaps the Major had been pretty near the truth, when he had divined that morning that the great man who was too haughty formally to consult with, or confide in his prime minister, on such a matter, yet wished him to be fully possessed of it. Let this be how it may, he often glanced at Mr Carker while the Major plied his light artillery, and seemed watchful of its effect upon him.\n\nBut the Major, having secured an attentive listener, and a smiler who had not his match in all the world—“in short, a devilish intelligent and able fellow,” as he often afterwards declared—was not going to let him off with a little slyness personal to Mr Dombey. Therefore, on the removal of the cloth, the Major developed himself as a choice spirit in the broader and more comprehensive range of narrating regimental stories, and cracking regimental jokes, which he did with such prodigal exuberance, that Carker was (or feigned to be) quite exhausted with laughter and admiration: while Mr Dombey looked on over his starched cravat, like the Major’s proprietor, or like a stately showman who was glad to see his bear dancing well.\n\nWhen the Major was too hoarse with meat and drink, and the display of his social powers, to render himself intelligible any longer, they adjourned to coffee. After which, the Major inquired of Mr Carker the Manager, with little apparent hope of an answer in the affirmative, if he played picquet.\n\n“Yes, I play picquet a little,” said Mr Carker.\n\n“Backgammon, perhaps?” observed the Major, hesitating.\n\n“Yes, I play backgammon a little too,” replied the man of teeth.\n\n“Carker plays at all games, I believe,” said Mr Dombey, laying himself on a sofa like a man of wood, without a hinge or a joint in him; “and plays them well.”\n\nIn sooth, he played the two in question, to such perfection, that the Major was astonished, and asked him, at random, if he played chess.\n\n“Yes, I play chess a little,” answered Carker. “I have sometimes played, and won a game—it’s a mere trick—without seeing the board.”\n\n“By Gad, Sir!” said the Major, staring, “you are a contrast to Dombey, who plays nothing.”\n\n“Oh! He!” returned the Manager. “He has never had occasion to acquire such little arts. To men like me, they are sometimes useful. As at present, Major Bagstock, when they enable me to take a hand with you.”\n\nIt might be only the false mouth, so smooth and wide; and yet there seemed to lurk beneath the humility and subserviency of this short speech, a something like a snarl; and, for a moment, one might have thought that the white teeth were prone to bite the hand they fawned upon. But the Major thought nothing about it; and Mr Dombey lay meditating with his eyes half shut, during the whole of the play, which lasted until bed-time.\n\nBy that time, Mr Carker, though the winner, had mounted high into the Major’s good opinion, insomuch that when he left the Major at his own room before going to bed, the Major as a special attention, sent the Native—who always rested on a mattress spread upon the ground at his master’s door—along the gallery, to light him to his room in state.\n\nThere was a faint blur on the surface of the mirror in Mr Carker’s chamber, and its reflection was, perhaps, a false one. But it showed, that night, the image of a man, who saw, in his fancy, a crowd of people slumbering on the ground at his feet, like the poor Native at his master’s door: who picked his way among them: looking down, maliciously enough: but trod upon no upturned face—as yet.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVII. Deeper Shadows\n\nMr Carker the Manager rose with the lark, and went out, walking in the summer day. His meditations—and he meditated with contracted brows while he strolled along—hardly seemed to soar as high as the lark, or to mount in that direction; rather they kept close to their nest upon the earth, and looked about, among the dust and worms. But there was not a bird in the air, singing unseen, farther beyond the reach of human eye than Mr Carker’s thoughts. He had his face so perfectly under control, that few could say more, in distinct terms, of its expression, than that it smiled or that it pondered. It pondered now, intently. As the lark rose higher, he sank deeper in thought. As the lark poured out her melody clearer and stronger, he fell into a graver and profounder silence. At length, when the lark came headlong down, with an accumulating stream of song, and dropped among the green wheat near him, rippling in the breath of the morning like a river, he sprang up from his reverie, and looked round with a sudden smile, as courteous and as soft as if he had had numerous observers to propitiate; nor did he relapse, after being thus awakened; but clearing his face, like one who bethought himself that it might otherwise wrinkle and tell tales, went smiling on, as if for practice.\n\nPerhaps with an eye to first impressions, Mr Carker was very carefully and trimly dressed, that morning. Though always somewhat formal, in his dress, in imitation of the great man whom he served, he stopped short of the extent of Mr Dombey’s stiffness: at once perhaps because he knew it to be ludicrous, and because in doing so he found another means of expressing his sense of the difference and distance between them. Some people quoted him indeed, in this respect, as a pointed commentary, and not a flattering one, on his icy patron—but the world is prone to misconstruction, and Mr Carker was not accountable for its bad propensity.\n\nClean and florid: with his light complexion, fading as it were, in the sun, and his dainty step enhancing the softness of the turf: Mr Carker the Manager strolled about meadows, and green lanes, and glided among avenues of trees, until it was time to return to breakfast. Taking a nearer way back, Mr Carker pursued it, airing his teeth, and said aloud as he did so, “Now to see the second Mrs Dombey!”\n\nHe had strolled beyond the town, and re-entered it by a pleasant walk, where there was a deep shade of leafy trees, and where there were a few benches here and there for those who chose to rest. It not being a place of general resort at any hour, and wearing at that time of the still morning the air of being quite deserted and retired, Mr Carker had it, or thought he had it, all to himself. So, with the whim of an idle man, to whom there yet remained twenty minutes for reaching a destination easily able in ten, Mr Carker threaded the great boles of the trees, and went passing in and out, before this one and behind that, weaving a chain of footsteps on the dewy ground.\n\nBut he found he was mistaken in supposing there was no one in the grove, for as he softly rounded the trunk of one large tree, on which the obdurate bark was knotted and overlapped like the hide of a rhinoceros or some kindred monster of the ancient days before the Flood, he saw an unexpected figure sitting on a bench near at hand, about which, in another moment, he would have wound the chain he was making.\n\nIt was that of a lady, elegantly dressed and very handsome, whose dark proud eyes were fixed upon the ground, and in whom some passion or struggle was raging. For as she sat looking down, she held a corner of her under lip within her mouth, her bosom heaved, her nostril quivered, her head trembled, indignant tears were on her cheek, and her foot was set upon the moss as though she would have crushed it into nothing. And yet almost the self-same glance that showed him this, showed him the self-same lady rising with a scornful air of weariness and lassitude, and turning away with nothing expressed in face or figure but careless beauty and imperious disdain.\n\nA withered and very ugly old woman, dressed not so much like a gipsy as like any of that medley race of vagabonds who tramp about the country, begging, and stealing, and tinkering, and weaving rushes, by turns, or all together, had been observing the lady, too; for, as she rose, this second figure strangely confronting the first, scrambled up from the ground—out of it, it almost appeared—and stood in the way.\n\n“Let me tell your fortune, my pretty lady,” said the old woman, munching with her jaws, as if the Death’s Head beneath her yellow skin were impatient to get out.\n\n“I can tell it for myself,” was the reply.\n\n“Ay, ay, pretty lady; but not right. You didn’t tell it right when you were sitting there. I see you! Give me a piece of silver, pretty lady, and I’ll tell your fortune true. There’s riches, pretty lady, in your face.”\n\n“I know,” returned the lady, passing her with a dark smile, and a proud step. “I knew it before.\n\n“What! You won’t give me nothing?” cried the old woman. “You won’t give me nothing to tell your fortune, pretty lady? How much will you give me to tell it, then? Give me something, or I’ll call it after you!” croaked the old woman, passionately.\n\nMr Carker, whom the lady was about to pass close, slinking against his tree as she crossed to gain the path, advanced so as to meet her, and pulling off his hat as she went by, bade the old woman hold her peace. The lady acknowledged his interference with an inclination of the head, and went her way.\n\n“You give me something then, or I’ll call it after her!” screamed the old woman, throwing up her arms, and pressing forward against his outstretched hand. “Or come,” she added, dropping her voice suddenly, looking at him earnestly, and seeming in a moment to forget the object of her wrath, “give me something, or I’ll call it after you!”\n\n“After me, old lady!” returned the Manager, putting his hand in his pocket.\n\n“Yes,” said the woman, steadfast in her scrutiny, and holding out her shrivelled hand. “I know!”\n\n“What do you know?” demanded Carker, throwing her a shilling. “Do you know who the handsome lady is?”\n\nMunching like that sailor’s wife of yore, who had chestnuts in her lap, and scowling like the witch who asked for some in vain, the old woman picked the shilling up, and going backwards, like a crab, or like a heap of crabs: for her alternately expanding and contracting hands might have represented two of that species, and her creeping face, some half-a-dozen more: crouched on the veinous root of an old tree, pulled out a short black pipe from within the crown of her bonnet, lighted it with a match, and smoked in silence, looking fixedly at her questioner.\n\nMr Carker laughed, and turned upon his heel.\n\n“Good!” said the old woman. “One child dead, and one child living: one wife dead, and one wife coming. Go and meet her!”\n\nIn spite of himself, the Manager looked round again, and stopped. The old woman, who had not removed her pipe, and was munching and mumbling while she smoked, as if in conversation with an invisible familiar, pointed with her finger in the direction he was going, and laughed.\n\n“What was that you said, Bedlamite?” he demanded.\n\nThe woman mumbled, and chattered, and smoked, and still pointed before him; but remained silent Muttering a farewell that was not complimentary, Mr Carker pursued his way; but as he turned out of that place, and looked over his shoulder at the root of the old tree, he could yet see the finger pointing before him, and thought he heard the woman screaming, “Go and meet her!”\n\nPreparations for a choice repast were completed, he found, at the hotel; and Mr Dombey, and the Major, and the breakfast, were awaiting the ladies. Individual constitution has much to do with the development of such facts, no doubt; but in this case, appetite carried it hollow over the tender passion; Mr Dombey being very cool and collected, and the Major fretting and fuming in a state of violent heat and irritation. At length the door was thrown open by the Native, and, after a pause, occupied by her languishing along the gallery, a very blooming, but not very youthful lady, appeared.\n\n“My dear Mr Dombey,” said the lady, “I am afraid we are late, but Edith has been out already looking for a favourable point of view for a sketch, and kept me waiting for her. Falsest of Majors,” giving him her little finger, “how do you do?”\n\n“Mrs Skewton,” said Mr Dombey, “let me gratify my friend Carker:” Mr Dombey unconsciously emphasised the word friend, as saying ‘no really; I do allow him to take credit for that distinction:’ “by presenting him to you. You have heard me mention Mr Carker.”\n\n“I am charmed, I am sure,” said Mrs Skewton, graciously.\n\nMr Carker was charmed, of course. Would he have been more charmed on Mr Dombey’s behalf, if Mrs Skewton had been (as he at first supposed her) the Edith whom they had toasted overnight?\n\n“Why, where, for Heaven’s sake, is Edith?” exclaimed Mrs Skewton, looking round. “Still at the door, giving Withers orders about the mounting of those drawings! My dear Mr Dombey, will you have the kindness”—\n\nMr Dombey was already gone to seek her. Next moment he returned, bearing on his arm the same elegantly dressed and very handsome lady whom Mr Carker had encountered underneath the trees.\n\n“Carker—” began Mr Dombey. But their recognition of each other was so manifest, that Mr Dombey stopped surprised.\n\n“I am obliged to the gentleman,” said Edith, with a stately bend, “for sparing me some annoyance from an importunate beggar just now.”\n\n“I am obliged to my good fortune,” said Mr Carker, bowing low, “for the opportunity of rendering so slight a service to one whose servant I am proud to be.”\n\nAs her eye rested on him for an instant, and then lighted on the ground, he saw in its bright and searching glance a suspicion that he had not come up at the moment of his interference, but had secretly observed her sooner. As he saw that, she saw in his eye that her distrust was not without foundation.\n\n“Really,” cried Mrs Skewton, who had taken this opportunity of inspecting Mr Carker through her glass, and satisfying herself (as she lisped audibly to the Major) that he was all heart; “really now, this is one of the most enchanting coincidences that I ever heard of. The idea! My dearest Edith, there is such an obvious destiny in it, that really one might almost be induced to cross one’s arms upon one’s frock, and say, like those wicked Turks, there is no What’s-his-name but Thingummy, and What-you-may-call-it is his prophet!”\n\nEdith designed no revision of this extraordinary quotation from the Koran, but Mr Dombey felt it necessary to offer a few polite remarks.\n\n“It gives me great pleasure,” said Mr Dombey, with cumbrous gallantry, “that a gentleman so nearly connected with myself as Carker is, should have had the honour and happiness of rendering the least assistance to Mrs Granger.” Mr Dombey bowed to her. “But it gives me some pain, and it occasions me to be really envious of Carker;” he unconsciously laid stress on these words, as sensible that they must appear to involve a very surprising proposition; “envious of Carker, that I had not that honour and that happiness myself.” Mr Dombey bowed again. Edith, saving for a curl of her lip, was motionless.\n\n“By the Lord, Sir,” cried the Major, bursting into speech at sight of the waiter, who was come to announce breakfast, “it’s an extraordinary thing to me that no one can have the honour and happiness of shooting all such beggars through the head without being brought to book for it. But here’s an arm for Mrs Granger if she’ll do J. B. the honour to accept it; and the greatest service Joe can render you, Ma’am, just now, is, to lead you into table!”\n\nWith this, the Major gave his arm to Edith; Mr Dombey led the way with Mrs Skewton; Mr Carker went last, smiling on the party.\n\n“I am quite rejoiced, Mr Carker,” said the lady-mother, at breakfast, after another approving survey of him through her glass, “that you have timed your visit so happily, as to go with us today. It is the most enchanting expedition!”\n\n“Any expedition would be enchanting in such society,” returned Carker; “but I believe it is, in itself, full of interest.”\n\n“Oh!” cried Mrs Skewton, with a faded little scream of rapture, “the Castle is charming!—associations of the Middle Ages—and all that—which is so truly exquisite. Don’t you dote upon the Middle Ages, Mr Carker?”\n\n“Very much, indeed,” said Mr Carker.\n\n“Such charming times!” cried Cleopatra. “So full of faith! So vigorous and forcible! So picturesque! So perfectly removed from commonplace! Oh dear! If they would only leave us a little more of the poetry of existence in these terrible days!”\n\nMrs Skewton was looking sharp after Mr Dombey all the time she said this, who was looking at Edith: who was listening, but who never lifted up her eyes.\n\n“We are dreadfully real, Mr Carker,” said Mrs Skewton; “are we not?”\n\nFew people had less reason to complain of their reality than Cleopatra, who had as much that was false about her as could well go to the composition of anybody with a real individual existence. But Mr Carker commiserated our reality nevertheless, and agreed that we were very hardly used in that regard.\n\n“Pictures at the Castle, quite divine!” said Cleopatra. “I hope you dote upon pictures?”\n\n“I assure you, Mrs Skewton,” said Mr Dombey, with solemn encouragement of his Manager, “that Carker has a very good taste for pictures; quite a natural power of appreciating them. He is a very creditable artist himself. He will be delighted, I am sure, with Mrs Granger’s taste and skill.”\n\n“Damme, Sir!” cried Major Bagstock, “my opinion is, that you’re the admirable Carker, and can do anything.”\n\n“Oh!” smiled Carker, with humility, “you are much too sanguine, Major Bagstock. I can do very little. But Mr Dombey is so generous in his estimation of any trivial accomplishment a man like myself may find it almost necessary to acquire, and to which, in his very different sphere, he is far superior, that—” Mr Carker shrugged his shoulders, deprecating further praise, and said no more.\n\nAll this time, Edith never raised her eyes, unless to glance towards her mother when that lady’s fervent spirit shone forth in words. But as Carker ceased, she looked at Mr Dombey for a moment. For a moment only; but with a transient gleam of scornful wonder on her face, not lost on one observer, who was smiling round the board.\n\nMr Dombey caught the dark eyelash in its descent, and took the opportunity of arresting it.\n\n“You have been to Warwick often, unfortunately?” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Several times.”\n\n“The visit will be tedious to you, I am afraid.”\n\n“Oh no; not at all.”\n\n“Ah! You are like your cousin Feenix, my dearest Edith,” said Mrs Skewton. “He has been to Warwick Castle fifty times, if he has been there once; yet if he came to Leamington to-morrow—I wish he would, dear angel!—he would make his fifty-second visit next day.”\n\n“We are all enthusiastic, are we not, Mama?” said Edith, with a cold smile.\n\n“Too much so, for our peace, perhaps, my dear,” returned her mother; “but we won’t complain. Our own emotions are our recompense. If, as your cousin Feenix says, the sword wears out the what’s-its-name—”\n\n“The scabbard, perhaps,” said Edith.\n\n“Exactly—a little too fast, it is because it is bright and glowing, you know, my dearest love.”\n\nMrs Skewton heaved a gentle sigh, supposed to cast a shadow on the surface of that dagger of lath, whereof her susceptible bosom was the sheath: and leaning her head on one side, in the Cleopatra manner, looked with pensive affection on her darling child.\n\nEdith had turned her face towards Mr Dombey when he first addressed her, and had remained in that attitude, while speaking to her mother, and while her mother spoke to her, as though offering him her attention, if he had anything more to say. There was something in the manner of this simple courtesy: almost defiant, and giving it the character of being rendered on compulsion, or as a matter of traffic to which she was a reluctant party again not lost upon that same observer who was smiling round the board. It set him thinking of her as he had first seen her, when she had believed herself to be alone among the trees.\n\nMr Dombey having nothing else to say, proposed—the breakfast being now finished, and the Major gorged, like any Boa Constrictor—that they should start. A barouche being in waiting, according to the orders of that gentleman, the two ladies, the Major and himself, took their seats in it; the Native and the wan page mounted the box, Mr Towlinson being left behind; and Mr Carker, on horseback, brought up the rear.\n\nMr Carker cantered behind the carriage at the distance of a hundred yards or so, and watched it, during all the ride, as if he were a cat, indeed, and its four occupants, mice. Whether he looked to one side of the road, or to the other—over distant landscape, with its smooth undulations, wind-mills, corn, grass, bean fields, wild-flowers, farm-yards, hayricks, and the spire among the wood—or upwards in the sunny air, where butterflies were sporting round his head, and birds were pouring out their songs—or downward, where the shadows of the branches interlaced, and made a trembling carpet on the road—or onward, where the overhanging trees formed aisles and arches, dim with the softened light that steeped through leaves—one corner of his eye was ever on the formal head of Mr Dombey, addressed towards him, and the feather in the bonnet, drooping so neglectfully and scornfully between them; much as he had seen the haughty eyelids droop; not least so, when the face met that now fronting it. Once, and once only, did his wary glance release these objects; and that was, when a leap over a low hedge, and a gallop across a field, enabled him to anticipate the carriage coming by the road, and to be standing ready, at the journey’s end, to hand the ladies out. Then, and but then, he met her glance for an instant in her first surprise; but when he touched her, in alighting, with his soft white hand, it overlooked him altogether as before.\n\nMrs Skewton was bent on taking charge of Mr Carker herself, and showing him the beauties of the Castle. She was determined to have his arm, and the Major’s too. It would do that incorrigible creature: who was the most barbarous infidel in point of poetry: good to be in such company. This chance arrangement left Mr Dombey at liberty to escort Edith: which he did: stalking before them through the apartments with a gentlemanly solemnity.\n\n“Those darling byegone times, Mr Carker,” said Cleopatra, “with their delicious fortresses, and their dear old dungeons, and their delightful places of torture, and their romantic vengeances, and their picturesque assaults and sieges, and everything that makes life truly charming! How dreadfully we have degenerated!”\n\n“Yes, we have fallen off deplorably,” said Mr Carker.\n\nThe peculiarity of their conversation was, that Mrs Skewton, in spite of her ecstasies, and Mr Carker, in spite of his urbanity, were both intent on watching Mr Dombey and Edith. With all their conversational endowments, they spoke somewhat distractedly, and at random, in consequence.\n\n“We have no Faith left, positively,” said Mrs Skewton, advancing her shrivelled ear; for Mr Dombey was saying something to Edith. “We have no Faith in the dear old Barons, who were the most delightful creatures—or in the dear old Priests, who were the most warlike of men—or even in the days of that inestimable Queen Bess, upon the wall there, which were so extremely golden. Dear creature! She was all Heart And that charming father of hers! I hope you dote on Harry the Eighth!”\n\n“I admire him very much,” said Carker.\n\n“So bluff!” cried Mrs Skewton, “wasn’t he? So burly. So truly English. Such a picture, too, he makes, with his dear little peepy eyes, and his benevolent chin!”\n\n“Ah, Ma’am!” said Carker, stopping short; “but if you speak of pictures, there’s a composition! What gallery in the world can produce the counterpart of that?”\n\nAs the smiling gentleman thus spake, he pointed through a doorway to where Mr Dombey and Edith were standing alone in the centre of another room.\n\nThey were not interchanging a word or a look. Standing together, arm in arm, they had the appearance of being more divided than if seas had rolled between them. There was a difference even in the pride of the two, that removed them farther from each other, than if one had been the proudest and the other the humblest specimen of humanity in all creation. He, self-important, unbending, formal, austere. She, lovely and graceful, in an uncommon degree, but totally regardless of herself and him and everything around, and spurning her own attractions with her haughty brow and lip, as if they were a badge or livery she hated. So unmatched were they, and opposed, so forced and linked together by a chain which adverse hazard and mischance had forged: that fancy might have imagined the pictures on the walls around them, startled by the unnatural conjunction, and observant of it in their several expressions. Grim knights and warriors looked scowling on them. A churchman, with his hand upraised, denounced the mockery of such a couple coming to God’s altar. Quiet waters in landscapes, with the sun reflected in their depths, asked, if better means of escape were not at hand, was there no drowning left? Ruins cried, “Look here, and see what We are, wedded to uncongenial Time!” Animals, opposed by nature, worried one another, as a moral to them. Loves and Cupids took to flight afraid, and Martyrdom had no such torment in its painted history of suffering.\n\nNevertheless, Mrs Skewton was so charmed by the sight to which Mr Carker invoked her attention, that she could not refrain from saying, half aloud, how sweet, how very full of soul it was! Edith, overhearing, looked round, and flushed indignant scarlet to her hair.\n\n“My dearest Edith knows I was admiring her!” said Cleopatra, tapping her, almost timidly, on the back with her parasol. “Sweet pet!”\n\nAgain Mr Carker saw the strife he had witnessed so unexpectedly among the trees. Again he saw the haughty languor and indifference come over it, and hide it like a cloud.\n\nShe did not raise her eyes to him; but with a slight peremptory motion of them, seemed to bid her mother come near. Mrs Skewton thought it expedient to understand the hint, and advancing quickly, with her two cavaliers, kept near her daughter from that time.\n\nMr Carker now, having nothing to distract his attention, began to discourse upon the pictures and to select the best, and point them out to Mr Dombey: speaking with his usual familiar recognition of Mr Dombey’s greatness, and rendering homage by adjusting his eye-glass for him, or finding out the right place in his catalogue, or holding his stick, or the like. These services did not so much originate with Mr Carker, in truth, as with Mr Dombey himself, who was apt to assert his chieftainship by saying, with subdued authority, and in an easy way—for him—“Here, Carker, have the goodness to assist me, will you?” which the smiling gentleman always did with pleasure.\n\nThey made the tour of the pictures, the walls, crow’s nest, and so forth; and as they were still one little party, and the Major was rather in the shade: being sleepy during the process of digestion: Mr Carker became communicative and agreeable. At first, he addressed himself for the most part to Mrs Skewton; but as that sensitive lady was in such ecstasies with the works of art, after the first quarter of an hour, that she could do nothing but yawn (they were such perfect inspirations, she observed as a reason for that mark of rapture), he transferred his attentions to Mr Dombey. Mr Dombey said little beyond an occasional “Very true, Carker,” or “Indeed, Carker,” but he tacitly encouraged Carker to proceed, and inwardly approved of his behaviour very much: deeming it as well that somebody should talk, and thinking that his remarks, which were, as one might say, a branch of the parent establishment, might amuse Mrs Granger. Mr Carker, who possessed an excellent discretion, never took the liberty of addressing that lady, direct; but she seemed to listen, though she never looked at him; and once or twice, when he was emphatic in his peculiar humility, the twilight smile stole over her face, not as a light, but as a deep black shadow.\n\nWarwick Castle being at length pretty well exhausted, and the Major very much so: to say nothing of Mrs Skewton, whose peculiar demonstrations of delight had become very frequent Indeed: the carriage was again put in requisition, and they rode to several admired points of view in the neighbourhood. Mr Dombey ceremoniously observed of one of these, that a sketch, however slight, from the fair hand of Mrs Granger, would be a remembrance to him of that agreeable day: though he wanted no artificial remembrance, he was sure (here Mr Dombey made another of his bows), which he must always highly value. Withers the lean having Edith’s sketch-book under his arm, was immediately called upon by Mrs Skewton to produce the same: and the carriage stopped, that Edith might make the drawing, which Mr Dombey was to put away among his treasures.\n\n“But I am afraid I trouble you too much,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“By no means. Where would you wish it taken from?” she answered, turning to him with the same enforced attention as before.\n\nMr Dombey, with another bow, which cracked the starch in his cravat, would beg to leave that to the Artist.\n\n“I would rather you chose for yourself,” said Edith.\n\n“Suppose then,” said Mr Dombey, “we say from here. It appears a good spot for the purpose, or—Carker, what do you think?”\n\nThere happened to be in the foreground, at some little distance, a grove of trees, not unlike that in which Mr Carker had made his chain of footsteps in the morning, and with a seat under one tree, greatly resembling, in the general character of its situation, the point where his chain had broken.\n\n“Might I venture to suggest to Mrs Granger,” said Carker, “that that is an interesting—almost a curious—point of view?”\n\nShe followed the direction of his riding-whip with her eyes, and raised them quickly to his face. It was the second glance they had exchanged since their introduction; and would have been exactly like the first, but that its expression was plainer.\n\n“Will you like that?” said Edith to Mr Dombey.\n\n“I shall be charmed,” said Mr Dombey to Edith.\n\nTherefore the carriage was driven to the spot where Mr Dombey was to be charmed; and Edith, without moving from her seat, and opening her sketch-book with her usual proud indifference, began to sketch.\n\n“My pencils are all pointless,” she said, stopping and turning them over.\n\n“Pray allow me,” said Mr Dombey. “Or Carker will do it better, as he understands these things. Carker, have the goodness to see to these pencils for Mrs Granger.”\n\nMr Carker rode up close to the carriage-door on Mrs Granger’s side, and letting the rein fall on his horse’s neck, took the pencils from her hand with a smile and a bow, and sat in the saddle leisurely mending them. Having done so, he begged to be allowed to hold them, and to hand them to her as they were required; and thus Mr Carker, with many commendations of Mrs Granger’s extraordinary skill—especially in trees—remained—close at her side, looking over the drawing as she made it. Mr Dombey in the meantime stood bolt upright in the carriage like a highly respectable ghost, looking on too; while Cleopatra and the Major dallied as two ancient doves might do.\n\n“Are you satisfied with that, or shall I finish it a little more?” said Edith, showing the sketch to Mr Dombey.\n\nMr Dombey begged that it might not be touched; it was perfection.\n\n“It is most extraordinary,” said Carker, bringing every one of his red gums to bear upon his praise. “I was not prepared for anything so beautiful, and so unusual altogether.”\n\nThis might have applied to the sketcher no less than to the sketch; but Mr Carker’s manner was openness itself—not as to his mouth alone, but as to his whole spirit. So it continued to be while the drawing was laid aside for Mr Dombey, and while the sketching materials were put up; then he handed in the pencils (which were received with a distant acknowledgment of his help, but without a look), and tightening his rein, fell back, and followed the carriage again.\n\nThinking, perhaps, as he rode, that even this trivial sketch had been made and delivered to its owner, as if it had been bargained for and bought. Thinking, perhaps, that although she had assented with such perfect readiness to his request, her haughty face, bent over the drawing, or glancing at the distant objects represented in it, had been the face of a proud woman, engaged in a sordid and miserable transaction. Thinking, perhaps, of such things: but smiling certainly, and while he seemed to look about him freely, in enjoyment of the air and exercise, keeping always that sharp corner of his eye upon the carriage.\n\nA stroll among the haunted ruins of Kenilworth, and more rides to more points of view: most of which, Mrs Skewton reminded Mr Dombey, Edith had already sketched, as he had seen in looking over her drawings: brought the day’s expedition to a close. Mrs Skewton and Edith were driven to their own lodgings; Mr Carker was graciously invited by Cleopatra to return thither with Mr Dombey and the Major, in the evening, to hear some of Edith’s music; and the three gentlemen repaired to their hotel to dinner.\n\nThe dinner was the counterpart of yesterday’s, except that the Major was twenty-four hours more triumphant and less mysterious. Edith was toasted again. Mr Dombey was again agreeably embarrassed. And Mr Carker was full of interest and praise.\n\nThere were no other visitors at Mrs Skewton’s. Edith’s drawings were strewn about the room, a little more abundantly than usual perhaps; and Withers, the wan page, handed round a little stronger tea. The harp was there; the piano was there; and Edith sang and played. But even the music was played by Edith to Mr Dombey’s order, as it were, in the same uncompromising way. As thus.\n\n“Edith, my dearest love,” said Mrs Skewton, half an hour after tea, “Mr Dombey is dying to hear you, I know.”\n\n“Mr Dombey has life enough left to say so for himself, Mama, I have no doubt.”\n\n“I shall be immensely obliged,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“What do you wish?”\n\n“Piano?” hesitated Mr Dombey.\n\n“Whatever you please. You have only to choose.”\n\nAccordingly, she began with the piano. It was the same with the harp; the same with her singing; the same with the selection of the pieces that she sang and played. Such frigid and constrained, yet prompt and pointed acquiescence with the wishes he imposed upon her, and on no one else, was sufficiently remarkable to penetrate through all the mysteries of picquet, and impress itself on Mr Carker’s keen attention. Nor did he lose sight of the fact that Mr Dombey was evidently proud of his power, and liked to show it.\n\nNevertheless, Mr Carker played so well—some games with the Major, and some with Cleopatra, whose vigilance of eye in respect of Mr Dombey and Edith no lynx could have surpassed—that he even heightened his position in the lady-mother’s good graces; and when on taking leave he regretted that he would be obliged to return to London next morning, Cleopatra trusted: community of feeling not being met with every day: that it was far from being the last time they would meet.\n\n“I hope so,” said Mr Carker, with an expressive look at the couple in the distance, as he drew towards the door, following the Major. “I think so.”\n\nMr Dombey, who had taken a stately leave of Edith, bent, or made some approach to a bend, over Cleopatra’s couch, and said, in a low voice:\n\n“I have requested Mrs Granger’s permission to call on her to-morrow morning—for a purpose—and she has appointed twelve o’clock. May I hope to have the pleasure of finding you at home, Madam, afterwards?”\n\nCleopatra was so much fluttered and moved, by hearing this, of course, incomprehensible speech, that she could only shut her eyes, and shake her head, and give Mr Dombey her hand; which Mr Dombey, not exactly knowing what to do with, dropped.\n\n“Dombey, come along!” cried the Major, looking in at the door. “Damme, Sir, old Joe has a great mind to propose an alteration in the name of the Royal Hotel, and that it should be called the Three Jolly Bachelors, in honour of ourselves and Carker.” With this, the Major slapped Mr Dombey on the back, and winking over his shoulder at the ladies, with a frightful tendency of blood to the head, carried him off.\n\nMrs Skewton reposed on her sofa, and Edith sat apart, by her harp, in silence. The mother, trifling with her fan, looked stealthily at the daughter more than once, but the daughter, brooding gloomily with downcast eyes, was not to be disturbed.\n\nThus they remained for a long hour, without a word, until Mrs Skewton’s maid appeared, according to custom, to prepare her gradually for night. At night, she should have been a skeleton, with dart and hour-glass, rather than a woman, this attendant; for her touch was as the touch of Death. The painted object shrivelled underneath her hand; the form collapsed, the hair dropped off, the arched dark eyebrows changed to scanty tufts of grey; the pale lips shrunk, the skin became cadaverous and loose; an old, worn, yellow, nodding woman, with red eyes, alone remained in Cleopatra’s place, huddled up, like a slovenly bundle, in a greasy flannel gown.\n\nThe very voice was changed, as it addressed Edith, when they were alone again.\n\n“Why don’t you tell me,” it said sharply, “that he is coming here to-morrow by appointment?”\n\n“Because you know it,” returned Edith, “Mother.”\n\nThe mocking emphasis she laid on that one word!\n\n“You know he has bought me,” she resumed. “Or that he will, to-morrow. He has considered of his bargain; he has shown it to his friend; he is even rather proud of it; he thinks that it will suit him, and may be had sufficiently cheap; and he will buy to-morrow. God, that I have lived for this, and that I feel it!”\n\nCompress into one handsome face the conscious self-abasement, and the burning indignation of a hundred women, strong in passion and in pride; and there it hid itself with two white shuddering arms.\n\n“What do you mean?” returned the angry mother. “Haven’t you from a child—”\n\n“A child!” said Edith, looking at her, “when was I a child? What childhood did you ever leave to me? I was a woman—artful, designing, mercenary, laying snares for men—before I knew myself, or you, or even understood the base and wretched aim of every new display I learnt You gave birth to a woman. Look upon her. She is in her pride tonight.”\n\nAnd as she spoke, she struck her hand upon her beautiful bosom, as though she would have beaten down herself.\n\n“Look at me,” she said, “who have never known what it is to have an honest heart, and love. Look at me, taught to scheme and plot when children play; and married in my youth—an old age of design—to one for whom I had no feeling but indifference. Look at me, whom he left a widow, dying before his inheritance descended to him—a judgment on you! well deserved!—and tell me what has been my life for ten years since.”\n\n“We have been making every effort to endeavour to secure to you a good establishment,” rejoined her mother. “That has been your life. And now you have got it.”\n\n“There is no slave in a market: there is no horse in a fair: so shown and offered and examined and paraded, Mother, as I have been, for ten shameful years,” cried Edith, with a burning brow, and the same bitter emphasis on the one word. “Is it not so? Have I been made the bye-word of all kinds of men? Have fools, have profligates, have boys, have dotards, dangled after me, and one by one rejected me, and fallen off, because you were too plain with all your cunning: yes, and too true, with all those false pretences: until we have almost come to be notorious? The licence of look and touch,” she said, with flashing eyes, “have I submitted to it, in half the places of resort upon the map of England? Have I been hawked and vended here and there, until the last grain of self-respect is dead within me, and I loathe myself? Has been my late childhood? I had none before. Do not tell me that I had, tonight of all nights in my life!”\n\n“You might have been well married,” said her mother, “twenty times at least, Edith, if you had given encouragement enough.”\n\n“No! Who takes me, refuse that I am, and as I well deserve to be,” she answered, raising her head, and trembling in her energy of shame and stormy pride, “shall take me, as this man does, with no art of mine put forth to lure him. He sees me at the auction, and he thinks it well to buy me. Let him! When he came to view me—perhaps to bid—he required to see the roll of my accomplishments. I gave it to him. When he would have me show one of them, to justify his purchase to his men, I require of him to say which he demands, and I exhibit it. I will do no more. He makes the purchase of his own will, and with his own sense of its worth, and the power of his money; and I hope it may never disappoint him. I have not vaunted and pressed the bargain; neither have you, so far as I have been able to prevent you.\n\n“You talk strangely tonight, Edith, to your own Mother.”\n\n“It seems so to me; stranger to me than you,” said Edith. “But my education was completed long ago. I am too old now, and have fallen too low, by degrees, to take a new course, and to stop yours, and to help myself. The germ of all that purifies a woman’s breast, and makes it true and good, has never stirred in mine, and I have nothing else to sustain me when I despise myself.” There had been a touching sadness in her voice, but it was gone, when she went on to say, with a curled lip, “So, as we are genteel and poor, I am content that we should be made rich by these means; all I say is, I have kept the only purpose I have had the strength to form—I had almost said the power, with you at my side, Mother—and have not tempted this man on.”\n\n“This man! You speak,” said her mother, “as if you hated him.”\n\n“And you thought I loved him, did you not?” she answered, stopping on her way across the room, and looking round. “Shall I tell you,” she continued, with her eyes fixed on her mother, “who already knows us thoroughly, and reads us right, and before whom I have even less of self-respect or confidence than before my own inward self; being so much degraded by his knowledge of me?”\n\n“This is an attack, I suppose,” returned her mother coldly, “on poor, unfortunate what’s-his-name—Mr Carker! Your want of self-respect and confidence, my dear, in reference to that person (who is very agreeable, it strikes me), is not likely to have much effect on your establishment. Why do you look at me so hard? Are you ill?”\n\nEdith suddenly let fall her face, as if it had been stung, and while she pressed her hands upon it, a terrible tremble crept over her whole frame. It was quickly gone; and with her usual step, she passed out of the room.\n\nThe maid who should have been a skeleton, then reappeared, and giving one arm to her mistress, who appeared to have taken off her manner with her charms, and to have put on paralysis with her flannel gown, collected the ashes of Cleopatra, and carried them away in the other, ready for tomorrow’s revivification.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII. Alterations\n\nSo the day has come at length, Susan,” said Florence to the excellent Nipper, “when we are going back to our quiet home!”\n\nSusan drew in her breath with an amount of expression not easily described, further relieving her feelings with a smart cough, answered, “Very quiet indeed, Miss Floy, no doubt. Excessive so.”\n\n“When I was a child,” said Florence, thoughtfully, and after musing for some moments, “did you ever see that gentleman who has taken the trouble to ride down here to speak to me, now three times—three times, I think, Susan?”\n\n“Three times, Miss,” returned the Nipper. “Once when you was out a walking with them Sket—”\n\nFlorence gently looked at her, and Miss Nipper checked herself.\n\n“With Sir Barnet and his lady, I mean to say, Miss, and the young gentleman. And two evenings since then.”\n\n“When I was a child, and when company used to come to visit Papa, did you ever see that gentleman at home, Susan?” asked Florence.\n\n“Well, Miss,” returned her maid, after considering, “I really couldn’t say I ever did. When your poor dear Ma died, Miss Floy, I was very new in the family, you see, and my element:” the Nipper bridled, as opining that her merits had been always designedly extinguished by Mr Dombey: “was the floor below the attics.”\n\n“To be sure,” said Florence, still thoughtfully; “you are not likely to have known who came to the house. I quite forgot.”\n\n“Not, Miss, but what we talked about the family and visitors,” said Susan, “and but what I heard much said, although the nurse before Mrs Richards make unpleasant remarks when I was in company, and hint at little Pitchers, but that could only be attributed, poor thing,” observed Susan, with composed forbearance, “to habits of intoxication, for which she was required to leave, and did.”\n\nFlorence, who was seated at her chamber window, with her face resting on her hand, sat looking out, and hardly seemed to hear what Susan said, she was so lost in thought.\n\n“At all events, Miss,” said Susan, “I remember very well that this same gentleman, Mr Carker, was almost, if not quite, as great a gentleman with your Papa then, as he is now. It used to be said in the house then, Miss, that he was at the head of all your Pa’s affairs in the City, and managed the whole, and that your Pa minded him more than anybody, which, begging your pardon, Miss Floy, he might easy do, for he never minded anybody else. I knew that, Pitcher as I might have been.”\n\nSusan Nipper, with an injured remembrance of the nurse before Mrs Richards, emphasised “Pitcher” strongly.\n\n“And that Mr Carker has not fallen off, Miss,” she pursued, “but has stood his ground, and kept his credit with your Pa, I know from what is always said among our people by that Perch, whenever he comes to the house; and though he’s the weakest weed in the world, Miss Floy, and no one can have a moment’s patience with the man, he knows what goes on in the City tolerable well, and says that your Pa does nothing without Mr Carker, and leaves all to Mr Carker, and acts according to Mr Carker, and has Mr Carker always at his elbow, and I do believe that he believes (that washiest of Perches!) that after your Pa, the Emperor of India is the child unborn to Mr Carker.”\n\nNot a word of this was lost on Florence, who, with an awakened interest in Susan’s speech, no longer gazed abstractedly on the prospect without, but looked at her, and listened with attention.\n\n“Yes, Susan,” she said, when that young lady had concluded. “He is in Papa’s confidence, and is his friend, I am sure.”\n\nFlorence’s mind ran high on this theme, and had done for some days. Mr Carker, in the two visits with which he had followed up his first one, had assumed a confidence between himself and her—a right on his part to be mysterious and stealthy, in telling her that the ship was still unheard of—a kind of mildly restrained power and authority over her—that made her wonder, and caused her great uneasiness. She had no means of repelling it, or of freeing herself from the web he was gradually winding about her; for that would have required some art and knowledge of the world, opposed to such address as his; and Florence had none. True, he had said no more to her than that there was no news of the ship, and that he feared the worst; but how he came to know that she was interested in the ship, and why he had the right to signify his knowledge to her, so insidiously and darkly, troubled Florence very much.\n\nThis conduct on the part of Mr Carker, and her habit of often considering it with wonder and uneasiness, began to invest him with an uncomfortable fascination in Florence’s thoughts. A more distinct remembrance of his features, voice, and manner: which she sometimes courted, as a means of reducing him to the level of a real personage, capable of exerting no greater charm over her than another: did not remove the vague impression. And yet he never frowned, or looked upon her with an air of dislike or animosity, but was always smiling and serene.\n\nAgain, Florence, in pursuit of her strong purpose with reference to her father, and her steady resolution to believe that she was herself unwittingly to blame for their so cold and distant relations, would recall to mind that this gentleman was his confidential friend, and would think, with an anxious heart, could her struggling tendency to dislike and fear him be a part of that misfortune in her, which had turned her father’s love adrift, and left her so alone? She dreaded that it might be; sometimes believed it was: then she resolved that she would try to conquer this wrong feeling; persuaded herself that she was honoured and encouraged by the notice of her father’s friend; and hoped that patient observation of him and trust in him would lead her bleeding feet along that stony road which ended in her father’s heart.\n\nThus, with no one to advise her—for she could advise with no one without seeming to complain against him—gentle Florence tossed on an uneasy sea of doubt and hope; and Mr Carker, like a scaly monster of the deep, swam down below, and kept his shining eye upon her.\n\nFlorence had a new reason in all this for wishing to be at home again. Her lonely life was better suited to her course of timid hope and doubt; and she feared sometimes, that in her absence she might miss some hopeful chance of testifying her affection for her father. Heaven knows, she might have set her mind at rest, poor child! on this last point; but her slighted love was fluttering within her, and, even in her sleep, it flew away in dreams, and nestled, like a wandering bird come home, upon her father’s neck.\n\nOf Walter she thought often. Ah! how often, when the night was gloomy, and the wind was blowing round the house! But hope was strong in her breast. It is so difficult for the young and ardent, even with such experience as hers, to imagine youth and ardour quenched like a weak flame, and the bright day of life merging into night, at noon, that hope was strong yet. Her tears fell frequently for Walter’s sufferings; but rarely for his supposed death, and never long.\n\nShe had written to the old Instrument-maker, but had received no answer to her note: which indeed required none. Thus matters stood with Florence on the morning when she was going home, gladly, to her old secluded life.\n\nDoctor and Mrs Blimber, accompanied (much against his will) by their valued charge, Master Barnet, were already gone back to Brighton, where that young gentleman and his fellow-pilgrims to Parnassus were then, no doubt, in the continual resumption of their studies. The holiday time was past and over; most of the juvenile guests at the villa had taken their departure; and Florence’s long visit was come to an end.\n\nThere was one guest, however, albeit not resident within the house, who had been very constant in his attentions to the family, and who still remained devoted to them. This was Mr Toots, who after renewing, some weeks ago, the acquaintance he had had the happiness of forming with Skettles Junior, on the night when he burst the Blimberian bonds and soared into freedom with his ring on, called regularly every other day, and left a perfect pack of cards at the hall-door; so many indeed, that the ceremony was quite a deal on the part of Mr Toots, and a hand at whist on the part of the servant.\n\nMr Toots, likewise, with the bold and happy idea of preventing the family from forgetting him (but there is reason to suppose that this expedient originated in the teeming brain of the Chicken), had established a six-oared cutter, manned by aquatic friends of the Chicken’s and steered by that illustrious character in person, who wore a bright red fireman’s coat for the purpose, and concealed the perpetual black eye with which he was afflicted, beneath a green shade. Previous to the institution of this equipage, Mr Toots sounded the Chicken on a hypothetical case, as, supposing the Chicken to be enamoured of a young lady named Mary, and to have conceived the intention of starting a boat of his own, what would he call that boat? The Chicken replied, with divers strong asseverations, that he would either christen it Poll or The Chicken’s Delight. Improving on this idea, Mr Toots, after deep study and the exercise of much invention, resolved to call his boat The Toots’s Joy, as a delicate compliment to Florence, of which no man knowing the parties, could possibly miss the appreciation.\n\nStretched on a crimson cushion in his gallant bark, with his shoes in the air, Mr Toots, in the exercise of his project, had come up the river, day after day, and week after week, and had flitted to and fro, near Sir Barnet’s garden, and had caused his crew to cut across and across the river at sharp angles, for his better exhibition to any lookers-out from Sir Barnet’s windows, and had had such evolutions performed by the Toots’s Joy as had filled all the neighbouring part of the water-side with astonishment. But whenever he saw anyone in Sir Barnet’s garden on the brink of the river, Mr Toots always feigned to be passing there, by a combination of coincidences of the most singular and unlikely description.\n\n“How are you, Toots?” Sir Barnet would say, waving his hand from the lawn, while the artful Chicken steered close in shore.\n\n“How de do, Sir Barnet?” Mr Toots would answer, “What a surprising thing that I should see you here!”\n\nMr Toots, in his sagacity, always said this, as if, instead of that being Sir Barnet’s house, it were some deserted edifice on the banks of the Nile, or Ganges.\n\n“I never was so surprised!” Mr Toots would exclaim.—“Is Miss Dombey there?”\n\nWhereupon Florence would appear, perhaps.\n\n“Oh, Diogenes is quite well, Miss Dombey,” Toots would cry. “I called to ask this morning.”\n\n“Thank you very much!” the pleasant voice of Florence would reply.\n\n“Won’t you come ashore, Toots?” Sir Barnet would say then. “Come! you’re in no hurry. Come and see us.”\n\n“Oh, it’s of no consequence, thank you!” Mr Toots would blushingly rejoin. “I thought Miss Dombey might like to know, that’s all. Good-bye!” And poor Mr Toots, who was dying to accept the invitation, but hadn’t the courage to do it, signed to the Chicken, with an aching heart, and away went the Joy, cleaving the water like an arrow.\n\nThe Joy was lying in a state of extraordinary splendour, at the garden steps, on the morning of Florence’s departure. When she went downstairs to take leave, after her talk with Susan, she found Mr Toots awaiting her in the drawing-room.\n\n“Oh, how de do, Miss Dombey?” said the stricken Toots, always dreadfully disconcerted when the desire of his heart was gained, and he was speaking to her; “thank you, I’m very well indeed, I hope you’re the same, so was Diogenes yesterday.”\n\n“You are very kind,” said Florence.\n\n“Thank you, it’s of no consequence,” retorted Mr Toots. “I thought perhaps you wouldn’t mind, in this fine weather, coming home by water, Miss Dombey. There’s plenty of room in the boat for your maid.”\n\n“I am very much obliged to you,” said Florence, hesitating. “I really am—but I would rather not.”\n\n“Oh, it’s of no consequence,” retorted Mr Toots. “Good morning.”\n\n“Won’t you wait and see Lady Skettles?” asked Florence, kindly.\n\n“Oh no, thank you,” returned Mr Toots, “it’s of no consequence at all.”\n\nSo shy was Mr Toots on such occasions, and so flurried! But Lady Skettles entering at the moment, Mr Toots was suddenly seized with a passion for asking her how she did, and hoping she was very well; nor could Mr Toots by any possibility leave off shaking hands with her, until Sir Barnet appeared: to whom he immediately clung with the tenacity of desperation.\n\n“We are losing, today, Toots,” said Sir Barnet, turning towards Florence, “the light of our house, I assure you”\n\n“Oh, it’s of no conseq—I mean yes, to be sure,” faltered the embarrassed Mr Toots. “Good morning!”\n\nNotwithstanding the emphatic nature of this farewell, Mr Toots, instead of going away, stood leering about him, vacantly. Florence, to relieve him, bade adieu, with many thanks, to Lady Skettles, and gave her arm to Sir Barnet.\n\n“May I beg of you, my dear Miss Dombey,” said her host, as he conducted her to the carriage, “to present my best compliments to your dear Papa?”\n\nIt was distressing to Florence to receive the commission, for she felt as if she were imposing on Sir Barnet by allowing him to believe that a kindness rendered to her, was rendered to her father. As she could not explain, however, she bowed her head and thanked him; and again she thought that the dull home, free from such embarrassments, and such reminders of her sorrow, was her natural and best retreat.\n\nSuch of her late friends and companions as were yet remaining at the villa, came running from within, and from the garden, to say good-bye. They were all attached to her, and very earnest in taking leave of her. Even the household were sorry for her going, and the servants came nodding and curtseying round the carriage door. As Florence looked round on the kind faces, and saw among them those of Sir Barnet and his lady, and of Mr Toots, who was chuckling and staring at her from a distance, she was reminded of the night when Paul and she had come from Doctor Blimber’s: and when the carriage drove away, her face was wet with tears.\n\nSorrowful tears, but tears of consolation, too; for all the softer memories connected with the dull old house to which she was returning made it dear to her, as they rose up. How long it seemed since she had wandered through the silent rooms: since she had last crept, softly and afraid, into those her father occupied: since she had felt the solemn but yet soothing influence of the beloved dead in every action of her daily life! This new farewell reminded her, besides, of her parting with poor Walter: of his looks and words that night: and of the gracious blending she had noticed in him, of tenderness for those he left behind, with courage and high spirit. His little history was associated with the old house too, and gave it a new claim and hold upon her heart.\n\nEven Susan Nipper softened towards the home of so many years, as they were on their way towards it. Gloomy as it was, and rigid justice as she rendered to its gloom, she forgave it a great deal. “I shall be glad to see it again, I don’t deny, Miss,” said the Nipper. “There ain’t much in it to boast of, but I wouldn’t have it burnt or pulled down, neither!”\n\n“You’ll be glad to go through the old rooms, won’t you, Susan?” said Florence, smiling.\n\n“Well, Miss,” returned the Nipper, softening more and more towards the house, as they approached it nearer, “I won’t deny but what I shall, though I shall hate ’em again, to-morrow, very likely.”\n\nFlorence felt that, for her, there was greater peace within it than elsewhere. It was better and easier to keep her secret shut up there, among the tall dark walls, than to carry it abroad into the light, and try to hide it from a crowd of happy eyes. It was better to pursue the study of her loving heart, alone, and find no new discouragements in loving hearts about her. It was easier to hope, and pray, and love on, all uncared for, yet with constancy and patience, in the tranquil sanctuary of such remembrances: although it mouldered, rusted, and decayed about her: than in a new scene, let its gaiety be what it would. She welcomed back her old enchanted dream of life, and longed for the old dark door to close upon her, once again.\n\nFull of such thoughts, they turned into the long and sombre street. Florence was not on that side of the carriage which was nearest to her home, and as the distance lessened between them and it, she looked out of her window for the children over the way.\n\nShe was thus engaged, when an exclamation from Susan caused her to turn quickly round.\n\n“Why, Gracious me!” cried Susan, breathless, “where’s our house!”\n\n“Our house!” said Florence.\n\nSusan, drawing in her head from the window, thrust it out again, drew it in again as the carriage stopped, and stared at her mistress in amazement.\n\nThere was a labyrinth of scaffolding raised all round the house, from the basement to the roof. Loads of bricks and stones, and heaps of mortar, and piles of wood, blocked up half the width and length of the broad street at the side. Ladders were raised against the walls; labourers were climbing up and down; men were at work upon the steps of the scaffolding; painters and decorators were busy inside; great rolls of ornamental paper were being delivered from a cart at the door; an upholsterer’s waggon also stopped the way; no furniture was to be seen through the gaping and broken windows in any of the rooms; nothing but workmen, and the implements of their several trades, swarming from the kitchens to the garrets. Inside and outside alike: bricklayers, painters, carpenters, masons: hammer, hod, brush, pickaxe, saw, and trowel: all at work together, in full chorus!\n\nFlorence descended from the coach, half doubting if it were, or could be the right house, until she recognised Towlinson, with a sun-burnt face, standing at the door to receive her.\n\n“There is nothing the matter?” inquired Florence.\n\n“Oh no, Miss.”\n\n“There are great alterations going on.”\n\n“Yes, Miss, great alterations,” said Towlinson.\n\nFlorence passed him as if she were in a dream, and hurried upstairs. The garish light was in the long-darkened drawing-room and there were steps and platforms, and men in paper caps, in the high places. Her mother’s picture was gone with the rest of the moveables, and on the mark where it had been, was scrawled in chalk, “this room in panel. Green and gold.” The staircase was a labyrinth of posts and planks like the outside of the house, and a whole Olympus of plumbers and glaziers was reclining in various attitudes, on the skylight. Her own room was not yet touched within, but there were beams and boards raised against it without, baulking the daylight. She went up swiftly to that other bedroom, where the little bed was; and a dark giant of a man with a pipe in his mouth, and his head tied up in a pocket-handkerchief, was staring in at the window.\n\nIt was here that Susan Nipper, who had been in quest of Florence, found her, and said, would she go downstairs to her Papa, who wished to speak to her.\n\n“At home! and wishing to speak to me!” cried Florence, trembling.\n\nSusan, who was infinitely more distraught than Florence herself, repeated her errand; and Florence, pale and agitated, hurried down again, without a moment’s hesitation. She thought upon the way down, would she dare to kiss him? The longing of her heart resolved her, and she thought she would.\n\nHer father might have heard that heart beat, when it came into his presence. One instant, and it would have beat against his breast.\n\nBut he was not alone. There were two ladies there; and Florence stopped. Striving so hard with her emotion, that if her brute friend Di had not burst in and overwhelmed her with his caresses as a welcome home—at which one of the ladies gave a little scream, and that diverted her attention from herself—she would have swooned upon the floor.\n\n“Florence,” said her father, putting out his hand: so stiffly that it held her off: “how do you do?”\n\nFlorence took the hand between her own, and putting it timidly to her lips, yielded to its withdrawal. It touched the door in shutting it, with quite as much endearment as it had touched her.\n\n“What dog is that?” said Mr Dombey, displeased.\n\n“It is a dog, Papa—from Brighton.”\n\n“Well!” said Mr Dombey; and a cloud passed over his face, for he understood her.\n\n“He is very good-tempered,” said Florence, addressing herself with her natural grace and sweetness to the two lady strangers. “He is only glad to see me. Pray forgive him.”\n\nShe saw in the glance they interchanged, that the lady who had screamed, and who was seated, was old; and that the other lady, who stood near her Papa, was very beautiful, and of an elegant figure.\n\n“Mrs Skewton,” said her father, turning to the first, and holding out his hand, “this is my daughter Florence.”\n\n“Charming, I am sure,” observed the lady, putting up her glass. “So natural! My darling Florence, you must kiss me, if you please.”\n\nFlorence having done so, turned towards the other lady, by whom her father stood waiting.\n\n“Edith,” said Mr Dombey, “this is my daughter Florence. Florence, this lady will soon be your Mama.”\n\nFlorence started, and looked up at the beautiful face in a conflict of emotions, among which the tears that name awakened, struggled for a moment with surprise, interest, admiration, and an indefinable sort of fear. Then she cried out, “Oh, Papa, may you be happy! may you be very, very happy all your life!” and then fell weeping on the lady’s bosom.\n\nThere was a short silence. The beautiful lady, who at first had seemed to hesitate whether or no she should advance to Florence, held her to her breast, and pressed the hand with which she clasped her, close about her waist, as if to reassure her and comfort her. Not one word passed the lady’s lips. She bent her head down over Florence, and she kissed her on the cheek, but she said no word.\n\n“Shall we go on through the rooms,” said Mr Dombey, “and see how our workmen are doing? Pray allow me, my dear madam.”\n\nHe said this in offering his arm to Mrs Skewton, who had been looking at Florence through her glass, as though picturing to herself what she might be made, by the infusion—from her own copious storehouse, no doubt—of a little more Heart and Nature. Florence was still sobbing on the lady’s breast, and holding to her, when Mr Dombey was heard to say from the Conservatory:\n\n“Let us ask Edith. Dear me, where is she?”\n\n“Edith, my dear!” cried Mrs Skewton, “where are you? Looking for Mr Dombey somewhere, I know. We are here, my love.”\n\nThe beautiful lady released her hold of Florence, and pressing her lips once more upon her face, withdrew hurriedly, and joined them. Florence remained standing in the same place: happy, sorry, joyful, and in tears, she knew not how, or how long, but all at once: when her new Mama came back, and took her in her arms again.\n\n“Florence,” said the lady, hurriedly, and looking into her face with great earnestness. “You will not begin by hating me?”\n\n“By hating you, Mama?” cried Florence, winding her arm round her neck, and returning the look.\n\n“Hush! Begin by thinking well of me,” said the beautiful lady. “Begin by believing that I will try to make you happy, and that I am prepared to love you, Florence. Good-bye. We shall meet again soon. Good-bye! Don’t stay here, now.”\n\nAgain she pressed her to her breast she had spoken in a rapid manner, but firmly—and Florence saw her rejoin them in the other room.\n\nAnd now Florence began to hope that she would learn from her new and beautiful Mama, how to gain her father’s love; and in her sleep that night, in her lost old home, her own Mama smiled radiantly upon the hope, and blessed it. Dreaming Florence!\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX. The Opening of the Eyes of Mrs Chick\n\nMiss Tox, all unconscious of any such rare appearances in connexion with Mr Dombey’s house, as scaffoldings and ladders, and men with their heads tied up in pocket-handkerchiefs, glaring in at the windows like flying genii or strange birds,—having breakfasted one morning at about this eventful period of time, on her customary viands; to wit, one French roll rasped, one egg new laid (or warranted to be), and one little pot of tea, wherein was infused one little silver scoopful of that herb on behalf of Miss Tox, and one little silver scoopful on behalf of the teapot—a flight of fancy in which good housekeepers delight; went upstairs to set forth the bird waltz on the harpsichord, to water and arrange the plants, to dust the nick-nacks, and, according to her daily custom, to make her little drawing-room the garland of Princess’s Place.\n\nMiss Tox endued herself with a pair of ancient gloves, like dead leaves, in which she was accustomed to perform these avocations—hidden from human sight at other times in a table drawer—and went methodically to work; beginning with the bird waltz; passing, by a natural association of ideas, to her bird—a very high-shouldered canary, stricken in years, and much rumpled, but a piercing singer, as Princess’s Place well knew; taking, next in order, the little china ornaments, paper fly-cages, and so forth; and coming round, in good time, to the plants, which generally required to be snipped here and there with a pair of scissors, for some botanical reason that was very powerful with Miss Tox.\n\nMiss Tox was slow in coming to the plants, this morning. The weather was warm, the wind southerly; and there was a sigh of the summer-time in Princess’s Place, that turned Miss Tox’s thoughts upon the country. The pot-boy attached to the Princess’s Arms had come out with a can and trickled water, in a flowering pattern, all over Princess’s Place, and it gave the weedy ground a fresh scent—quite a growing scent, Miss Tox said. There was a tiny blink of sun peeping in from the great street round the corner, and the smoky sparrows hopped over it and back again, brightening as they passed: or bathed in it, like a stream, and became glorified sparrows, unconnected with chimneys. Legends in praise of Ginger-Beer, with pictorial representations of thirsty customers submerged in the effervescence, or stunned by the flying corks, were conspicuous in the window of the Princess’s Arms. They were making late hay, somewhere out of town; and though the fragrance had a long way to come, and many counter fragrances to contend with among the dwellings of the poor (may God reward the worthy gentlemen who stickle for the Plague as part and parcel of the wisdom of our ancestors, and who do their little best to keep those dwellings miserable!), yet it was wafted faintly into Princess’s Place, whispering of Nature and her wholesome air, as such things will, even unto prisoners and captives, and those who are desolate and oppressed, in very spite of aldermen and knights to boot: at whose sage nod—and how they nod!—the rolling world stands still!\n\nMiss Tox sat down upon the window-seat, and thought of her good Papa deceased—Mr Tox, of the Customs Department of the public service; and of her childhood, passed at a seaport, among a considerable quantity of cold tar, and some rusticity. She fell into a softened remembrance of meadows, in old time, gleaming with buttercups, like so many inverted firmaments of golden stars; and how she had made chains of dandelion-stalks for youthful vowers of eternal constancy, dressed chiefly in nankeen; and how soon those fetters had withered and broken.\n\nSitting on the window-seat, and looking out upon the sparrows and the blink of sun, Miss Tox thought likewise of her good Mama deceased—sister to the owner of the powdered head and pigtail—of her virtues and her rheumatism. And when a man with bulgy legs, and a rough voice, and a heavy basket on his head that crushed his hat into a mere black muffin, came crying flowers down Princess’s Place, making his timid little roots of daisies shudder in the vibration of every yell he gave, as though he had been an ogre, hawking little children, summer recollections were so strong upon Miss Tox, that she shook her head, and murmured she would be comparatively old before she knew it—which seemed likely.\n\nIn her pensive mood, Miss Tox’s thoughts went wandering on Mr Dombey’s track; probably because the Major had returned home to his lodgings opposite, and had just bowed to her from his window. What other reason could Miss Tox have for connecting Mr Dombey with her summer days and dandelion fetters? Was he more cheerful? thought Miss Tox. Was he reconciled to the decrees of fate? Would he ever marry again? and if yes, whom? What sort of person now!\n\nA flush—it was warm weather—overspread Miss Tox’s face, as, while entertaining these meditations, she turned her head, and was surprised by the reflection of her thoughtful image in the chimney-glass. Another flush succeeded when she saw a little carriage drive into Princess’s Place, and make straight for her own door. Miss Tox arose, took up her scissors hastily, and so coming, at last, to the plants, was very busy with them when Mrs Chick entered the room.\n\n“How is my sweetest friend!” exclaimed Miss Tox, with open arms.\n\nA little stateliness was mingled with Miss Tox’s sweetest friend’s demeanour, but she kissed Miss Tox, and said, “Lucretia, thank you, I am pretty well. I hope you are the same. Hem!”\n\nMrs Chick was labouring under a peculiar little monosyllabic cough; a sort of primer, or easy introduction to the art of coughing.\n\n“You call very early, and how kind that is, my dear!” pursued Miss Tox. “Now, have you breakfasted?”\n\n“Thank you, Lucretia,” said Mrs Chick, “I have. I took an early breakfast”—the good lady seemed curious on the subject of Princess’s Place, and looked all round it as she spoke—“with my brother, who has come home.”\n\n“He is better, I trust, my love,” faltered Miss Tox.\n\n“He is greatly better, thank you. Hem!”\n\n“My dear Louisa must be careful of that cough” remarked Miss Tox.\n\n“It’s nothing,” returned Mrs Chick. “It’s merely change of weather. We must expect change.”\n\n“Of weather?” asked Miss Tox, in her simplicity.\n\n“Of everything,” returned Mrs Chick. “Of course we must. It’s a world of change. Anyone would surprise me very much, Lucretia, and would greatly alter my opinion of their understanding, if they attempted to contradict or evade what is so perfectly evident. Change!” exclaimed Mrs Chick, with severe philosophy. “Why, my gracious me, what is there that does not change! even the silkworm, who I am sure might be supposed not to trouble itself about such subjects, changes into all sorts of unexpected things continually.”\n\n“My Louisa,” said the mild Miss Tox, “is ever happy in her illustrations.”\n\n“You are so kind, Lucretia,” returned Mrs Chick, a little softened, “as to say so, and to think so, I believe. I hope neither of us may ever have any cause to lessen our opinion of the other, Lucretia.”\n\n“I am sure of it,” returned Miss Tox.\n\nMrs Chick coughed as before, and drew lines on the carpet with the ivory end of her parasol. Miss Tox, who had experience of her fair friend, and knew that under the pressure of any slight fatigue or vexation she was prone to a discursive kind of irritability, availed herself of the pause, to change the subject.\n\n“Pardon me, my dear Louisa,” said Miss Tox, “but have I caught sight of the manly form of Mr Chick in the carriage?”\n\n“He is there,” said Mrs Chick, “but pray leave him there. He has his newspaper, and would be quite contented for the next two hours. Go on with your flowers, Lucretia, and allow me to sit here and rest.”\n\n“My Louisa knows,” observed Miss Tox, “that between friends like ourselves, any approach to ceremony would be out of the question. Therefore—” Therefore Miss Tox finished the sentence, not in words but action; and putting on her gloves again, which she had taken off, and arming herself once more with her scissors, began to snip and clip among the leaves with microscopic industry.\n\n“Florence has returned home also,” said Mrs Chick, after sitting silent for some time, with her head on one side, and her parasol sketching on the floor; “and really Florence is a great deal too old now, to continue to lead that solitary life to which she has been accustomed. Of course she is. There can be no doubt about it. I should have very little respect, indeed, for anybody who could advocate a different opinion. Whatever my wishes might be, I could not respect them. We cannot command our feelings to such an extent as that.”\n\nMiss Tox assented, without being particular as to the intelligibility of the proposition.\n\n“If she’s a strange girl,” said Mrs Chick, “and if my brother Paul cannot feel perfectly comfortable in her society, after all the sad things that have happened, and all the terrible disappointments that have been undergone, then, what is the reply? That he must make an effort. That he is bound to make an effort. We have always been a family remarkable for effort. Paul is at the head of the family; almost the only representative of it left—for what am I—I am of no consequence—”\n\n“My dearest love,” remonstrated Miss Tox.\n\nMrs Chick dried her eyes, which were, for the moment, overflowing; and proceeded:\n\n“And consequently he is more than ever bound to make an effort. And though his having done so, comes upon me with a sort of shock—for mine is a very weak and foolish nature; which is anything but a blessing I am sure; I often wish my heart was a marble slab, or a paving-stone—”\n\n“My sweet Louisa,” remonstrated Miss Tox again.\n\n“Still, it is a triumph to me to know that he is so true to himself, and to his name of Dombey; although, of course, I always knew he would be. I only hope,” said Mrs Chick, after a pause, “that she may be worthy of the name too.”\n\nMiss Tox filled a little green watering-pot from a jug, and happening to look up when she had done so, was so surprised by the amount of expression Mrs Chick had conveyed into her face, and was bestowing upon her, that she put the little watering-pot on the table for the present, and sat down near it.\n\n“My dear Louisa,” said Miss Tox, “will it be the least satisfaction to you, if I venture to observe in reference to that remark, that I, as a humble individual, think your sweet niece in every way most promising?”\n\n“What do you mean, Lucretia?” returned Mrs Chick, with increased stateliness of manner. “To what remark of mine, my dear, do you refer?”\n\n“Her being worthy of her name, my love,” replied Miss Tox.\n\n“If,” said Mrs Chick, with solemn patience, “I have not expressed myself with clearness, Lucretia, the fault of course is mine. There is, perhaps, no reason why I should express myself at all, except the intimacy that has subsisted between us, and which I very much hope, Lucretia—confidently hope—nothing will occur to disturb. Because, why should I do anything else? There is no reason; it would be absurd. But I wish to express myself clearly, Lucretia; and therefore to go back to that remark, I must beg to say that it was not intended to relate to Florence, in any way.”\n\n“Indeed!” returned Miss Tox.\n\n“No,” said Mrs Chick shortly and decisively.\n\n“Pardon me, my dear,” rejoined her meek friend; “but I cannot have understood it. I fear I am dull.”\n\nMrs Chick looked round the room and over the way; at the plants, at the bird, at the watering-pot, at almost everything within view, except Miss Tox; and finally dropping her glance upon Miss Tox, for a moment, on its way to the ground, said, looking meanwhile with elevated eyebrows at the carpet:\n\n“When I speak, Lucretia, of her being worthy of the name, I speak of my brother Paul’s second wife. I believe I have already said, in effect, if not in the very words I now use, that it is his intention to marry a second wife.”\n\nMiss Tox left her seat in a hurry, and returned to her plants; clipping among the stems and leaves, with as little favour as a barber working at so many pauper heads of hair.\n\n“Whether she will be fully sensible of the distinction conferred upon her,” said Mrs Chick, in a lofty tone, “is quite another question. I hope she may be. We are bound to think well of one another in this world, and I hope she may be. I have not been advised with myself. If I had been advised with, I have no doubt my advice would have been cavalierly received, and therefore it is infinitely better as it is. I much prefer it as it is.”\n\nMiss Tox, with head bent down, still clipped among the plants. Mrs Chick, with energetic shakings of her own head from time to time, continued to hold forth, as if in defiance of somebody.\n\n“If my brother Paul had consulted with me, which he sometimes does—or rather, sometimes used to do; for he will naturally do that no more now, and this is a circumstance which I regard as a relief from responsibility,” said Mrs Chick, hysterically, “for I thank Heaven I am not jealous—” here Mrs Chick again shed tears: “if my brother Paul had come to me, and had said, ‘Louisa, what kind of qualities would you advise me to look out for, in a wife?’ I should certainly have answered, ‘Paul, you must have family, you must have beauty, you must have dignity, you must have connexion.’ Those are the words I should have used. You might have led me to the block immediately afterwards,” said Mrs Chick, as if that consequence were highly probable, “but I should have used them. I should have said, ‘Paul! You to marry a second time without family! You to marry without beauty! You to marry without dignity! You to marry without connexion! There is nobody in the world, not mad, who could dream of daring to entertain such a preposterous idea!’”\n\nMiss Tox stopped clipping; and with her head among the plants, listened attentively. Perhaps Miss Tox thought there was hope in this exordium, and the warmth of Mrs Chick.\n\n“I should have adopted this course of argument,” pursued the discreet lady, “because I trust I am not a fool. I make no claim to be considered a person of superior intellect—though I believe some people have been extraordinary enough to consider me so; one so little humoured as I am, would very soon be disabused of any such notion; but I trust I am not a downright fool. And to tell ME,” said Mrs Chick with ineffable disdain, “that my brother Paul Dombey could ever contemplate the possibility of uniting himself to anybody—I don’t care who”—she was more sharp and emphatic in that short clause than in any other part of her discourse—“not possessing these requisites, would be to insult what understanding I have got, as much as if I was to be told that I was born and bred an elephant, which I may be told next,” said Mrs Chick, with resignation. “It wouldn’t surprise me at all. I expect it.”\n\nIn the moment’s silence that ensued, Miss Tox’s scissors gave a feeble clip or two; but Miss Tox’s face was still invisible, and Miss Tox’s morning gown was agitated. Mrs Chick looked sideways at her, through the intervening plants, and went on to say, in a tone of bland conviction, and as one dwelling on a point of fact that hardly required to be stated:\n\n“Therefore, of course my brother Paul has done what was to be expected of him, and what anybody might have foreseen he would do, if he entered the marriage state again. I confess it takes me rather by surprise, however gratifying; because when Paul went out of town I had no idea at all that he would form any attachment out of town, and he certainly had no attachment when he left here. However, it seems to be extremely desirable in every point of view. I have no doubt the mother is a most genteel and elegant creature, and I have no right whatever to dispute the policy of her living with them: which is Paul’s affair, not mine—and as to Paul’s choice, herself, I have only seen her picture yet, but that is beautiful indeed. Her name is beautiful too,” said Mrs Chick, shaking her head with energy, and arranging herself in her chair; “Edith is at once uncommon, as it strikes me, and distinguished. Consequently, Lucretia, I have no doubt you will be happy to hear that the marriage is to take place immediately—of course, you will:” great emphasis again: “and that you are delighted with this change in the condition of my brother, who has shown you a great deal of pleasant attention at various times.”\n\nMiss Tox made no verbal answer, but took up the little watering-pot with a trembling hand, and looked vacantly round as if considering what article of furniture would be improved by the contents. The room door opening at this crisis of Miss Tox’s feelings, she started, laughed aloud, and fell into the arms of the person entering; happily insensible alike of Mrs Chick’s indignant countenance and of the Major at his window over the way, who had his double-barrelled eye-glass in full action, and whose face and figure were dilated with Mephistophelean joy.\n\nNot so the expatriated Native, amazed supporter of Miss Tox’s swooning form, who, coming straight upstairs, with a polite inquiry touching Miss Tox’s health (in exact pursuance of the Major’s malicious instructions), had accidentally arrived in the very nick of time to catch the delicate burden in his arms, and to receive the contents of the little watering-pot in his shoe; both of which circumstances, coupled with his consciousness of being closely watched by the wrathful Major, who had threatened the usual penalty in regard of every bone in his skin in case of any failure, combined to render him a moving spectacle of mental and bodily distress.\n\nFor some moments, this afflicted foreigner remained clasping Miss Tox to his heart, with an energy of action in remarkable opposition to his disconcerted face, while that poor lady trickled slowly down upon him the very last sprinklings of the little watering-pot, as if he were a delicate exotic (which indeed he was), and might be almost expected to blow while the gentle rain descended. Mrs Chick, at length recovering sufficient presence of mind to interpose, commanded him to drop Miss Tox upon the sofa and withdraw; and the exile promptly obeying, she applied herself to promote Miss Tox’s recovery.\n\nBut none of that gentle concern which usually characterises the daughters of Eve in their tending of each other; none of that freemasonry in fainting, by which they are generally bound together in a mysterious bond of sisterhood; was visible in Mrs Chick’s demeanour. Rather like the executioner who restores the victim to sensation previous to proceeding with the torture (or was wont to do so, in the good old times for which all true men wear perpetual mourning), did Mrs Chick administer the smelling-bottle, the slapping on the hands, the dashing of cold water on the face, and the other proved remedies. And when, at length, Miss Tox opened her eyes, and gradually became restored to animation and consciousness, Mrs Chick drew off as from a criminal, and reversing the precedent of the murdered king of Denmark, regarded her more in anger than in sorrow.”\n\n“Lucretia!” said Mrs Chick “I will not attempt to disguise what I feel. My eyes are opened, all at once. I wouldn’t have believed this, if a Saint had told it to me.”\n\n“I am foolish to give way to faintness,” Miss Tox faltered. “I shall be better presently.”\n\n“You will be better presently, Lucretia!” repeated Mrs Chick, with exceeding scorn. “Do you suppose I am blind? Do you imagine I am in my second childhood? No, Lucretia! I am obliged to you!”\n\nMiss Tox directed an imploring, helpless kind of look towards her friend, and put her handkerchief before her face.\n\n“If anyone had told me this yesterday,” said Mrs Chick, with majesty, “or even half-an-hour ago, I should have been tempted, I almost believe, to strike them to the earth. Lucretia Tox, my eyes are opened to you all at once. The scales:” here Mrs Chick cast down an imaginary pair, such as are commonly used in grocers” shops: “have fallen from my sight. The blindness of my confidence is past, Lucretia. It has been abused and played, upon, and evasion is quite out of the question now, I assure you.”\n\n“Oh! to what do you allude so cruelly, my love?” asked Miss Tox, through her tears.\n\n“Lucretia,” said Mrs Chick, “ask your own heart. I must entreat you not to address me by any such familiar term as you have just used, if you please. I have some self-respect left, though you may think otherwise.”\n\n“Oh, Louisa!” cried Miss Tox. “How can you speak to me like that?”\n\n“How can I speak to you like that?” retorted Mrs Chick, who, in default of having any particular argument to sustain herself upon, relied principally on such repetitions for her most withering effects. “Like that! You may well say like that, indeed!”\n\nMiss Tox sobbed pitifully.\n\n“The idea!” said Mrs Chick, “of your having basked at my brother’s fireside, like a serpent, and wound yourself, through me, almost into his confidence, Lucretia, that you might, in secret, entertain designs upon him, and dare to aspire to contemplate the possibility of his uniting himself to you! Why, it is an idea,” said Mrs Chick, with sarcastic dignity, “the absurdity of which almost relieves its treachery.”\n\n“Pray, Louisa,” urged Miss Tox, “do not say such dreadful things.”\n\n“Dreadful things!” repeated Mrs Chick. “Dreadful things! Is it not a fact, Lucretia, that you have just now been unable to command your feelings even before me, whose eyes you had so completely closed?”\n\n“I have made no complaint,” sobbed Miss Tox. “I have said nothing. If I have been a little overpowered by your news, Louisa, and have ever had any lingering thought that Mr Dombey was inclined to be particular towards me, surely you will not condemn me.”\n\n“She is going to say,” said Mrs Chick, addressing herself to the whole of the furniture, in a comprehensive glance of resignation and appeal, “She is going to say—I know it—that I have encouraged her!”\n\n“I don’t wish to exchange reproaches, dear Louisa,” sobbed Miss Tox. “Nor do I wish to complain. But, in my own defence—”\n\n“Yes,” cried Mrs Chick, looking round the room with a prophetic smile, “that’s what she’s going to say. I knew it. You had better say it. Say it openly! Be open, Lucretia Tox,” said Mrs Chick, with desperate sternness, “whatever you are.”\n\n“In my own defence,” faltered Miss Tox, “and only in my own defence against your unkind words, my dear Louisa, I would merely ask you if you haven’t often favoured such a fancy, and even said it might happen, for anything we could tell?”\n\n“There is a point,” said Mrs Chick, rising, not as if she were going to stop at the floor, but as if she were about to soar up, high, into her native skies, “beyond which endurance becomes ridiculous, if not culpable. I can bear much; but not too much. What spell was on me when I came into this house this day, I don’t know; but I had a presentiment—a dark presentiment,” said Mrs Chick, with a shiver, “that something was going to happen. Well may I have had that foreboding, Lucretia, when my confidence of many years is destroyed in an instant, when my eyes are opened all at once, and when I find you revealed in your true colours. Lucretia, I have been mistaken in you. It is better for us both that this subject should end here. I wish you well, and I shall ever wish you well. But, as an individual who desires to be true to herself in her own poor position, whatever that position may be, or may not be—and as the sister of my brother—and as the sister-in-law of my brother’s wife—and as a connexion by marriage of my brother’s wife’s mother—may I be permitted to add, as a Dombey?—I can wish you nothing else but good morning.”\n\nThese words, delivered with cutting suavity, tempered and chastened by a lofty air of moral rectitude, carried the speaker to the door. There she inclined her head in a ghostly and statue-like manner, and so withdrew to her carriage, to seek comfort and consolation in the arms of Mr Chick, her lord.\n\nFiguratively speaking, that is to say; for the arms of Mr Chick were full of his newspaper. Neither did that gentleman address his eyes towards his wife otherwise than by stealth. Neither did he offer any consolation whatever. In short, he sat reading, and humming fag ends of tunes, and sometimes glancing furtively at her without delivering himself of a word, good, bad, or indifferent.\n\nIn the meantime Mrs Chick sat swelling and bridling, and tossing her head, as if she were still repeating that solemn formula of farewell to Lucretia Tox. At length, she said aloud, “Oh the extent to which her eyes had been opened that day!”\n\n“To which your eyes have been opened, my dear!” repeated Mr Chick.\n\n“Oh, don’t talk to me!” said Mrs Chic “if you can bear to see me in this state, and not ask me what the matter is, you had better hold your tongue for ever.”\n\n“What is the matter, my dear?” asked Mr Chick\n\n“To think,” said Mrs Chick, in a state of soliloquy, “that she should ever have conceived the base idea of connecting herself with our family by a marriage with Paul! To think that when she was playing at horses with that dear child who is now in his grave—I never liked it at the time—she should have been hiding such a double-faced design! I wonder she was never afraid that something would happen to her. She is fortunate if nothing does.”\n\n“I really thought, my dear,” said Mr Chick slowly, after rubbing the bridge of his nose for some time with his newspaper, “that you had gone on the same tack yourself, all along, until this morning; and had thought it would be a convenient thing enough, if it could have been brought about.”\n\nMrs Chick instantly burst into tears, and told Mr Chick that if he wished to trample upon her with his boots, he had better do It.\n\n“But with Lucretia Tox I have done,” said Mrs Chick, after abandoning herself to her feelings for some minutes, to Mr Chick’s great terror. “I can bear to resign Paul’s confidence in favour of one who, I hope and trust, may be deserving of it, and with whom he has a perfect right to replace poor Fanny if he chooses; I can bear to be informed, in Paul’s cool manner, of such a change in his plans, and never to be consulted until all is settled and determined; but deceit I can not bear, and with Lucretia Tox I have done. It is better as it is,” said Mrs Chick, piously; “much better. It would have been a long time before I could have accommodated myself comfortably with her, after this; and I really don’t know, as Paul is going to be very grand, and these are people of condition, that she would have been quite presentable, and might not have compromised myself. There’s a providence in everything; everything works for the best; I have been tried today but on the whole I do not regret it.”\n\nIn which Christian spirit, Mrs Chick dried her eyes and smoothed her lap, and sat as became a person calm under a great wrong. Mr Chick feeling his unworthiness no doubt, took an early opportunity of being set down at a street corner and walking away whistling, with his shoulders very much raised, and his hands in his pockets.\n\nWhile poor excommunicated Miss Tox, who, if she were a fawner and toad-eater, was at least an honest and a constant one, and had ever borne a faithful friendship towards her impeacher and had been truly absorbed and swallowed up in devotion to the magnificence of Mr Dombey—while poor excommunicated Miss Tox watered her plants with her tears, and felt that it was winter in Princess’s Place.\n\n# CHAPTER XXX. The interval before the Marriage\n\nAlthough the enchanted house was no more, and the working world had broken into it, and was hammering and crashing and tramping up and down stairs all day long keeping Diogenes in an incessant paroxysm of barking, from sunrise to sunset—evidently convinced that his enemy had got the better of him at last, and was then sacking the premises in triumphant defiance—there was, at first, no other great change in the method of Florence’s life. At night, when the workpeople went away, the house was dreary and deserted again; and Florence, listening to their voices echoing through the hall and staircase as they departed, pictured to herself the cheerful homes to which they were returning, and the children who were waiting for them, and was glad to think that they were merry and well pleased to go.\n\nShe welcomed back the evening silence as an old friend, but it came now with an altered face, and looked more kindly on her. Fresh hope was in it. The beautiful lady who had soothed and carressed her, in the very room in which her heart had been so wrung, was a spirit of promise to her. Soft shadows of the bright life dawning, when her father’s affection should be gradually won, and all, or much should be restored, of what she had lost on the dark day when a mother’s love had faded with a mother’s last breath on her cheek, moved about her in the twilight and were welcome company. Peeping at the rosy children her neighbours, it was a new and precious sensation to think that they might soon speak together and know each other; when she would not fear, as of old, to show herself before them, lest they should be grieved to see her in her black dress sitting there alone!\n\nIn her thoughts of her new mother, and in the love and trust overflowing her pure heart towards her, Florence loved her own dead mother more and more. She had no fear of setting up a rival in her breast. The new flower sprang from the deep-planted and long-cherished root, she knew. Every gentle word that had fallen from the lips of the beautiful lady, sounded to Florence like an echo of the voice long hushed and silent. How could she love that memory less for living tenderness, when it was her memory of all parental tenderness and love!\n\nFlorence was, one day, sitting reading in her room, and thinking of the lady and her promised visit soon—for her book turned on a kindred subject—when, raising her eyes, she saw her standing in the doorway.\n\n“Mama!” cried Florence, joyfully meeting her. “Come again!”\n\n“Not Mama yet,” returned the lady, with a serious smile, as she encircled Florence’s neck with her arm.\n\n“But very soon to be,” cried Florence.\n\n“Very soon now, Florence: very soon.”\n\nEdith bent her head a little, so as to press the blooming cheek of Florence against her own, and for some few moments remained thus silent. There was something so very tender in her manner, that Florence was even more sensible of it than on the first occasion of their meeting.\n\nShe led Florence to a chair beside her, and sat down: Florence looking in her face, quite wondering at its beauty, and willingly leaving her hand in hers.\n\n“Have you been alone, Florence, since I was here last?”\n\n“Oh yes!” smiled Florence, hastily.\n\nShe hesitated and cast down her eyes; for her new Mama was very earnest in her look, and the look was intently and thoughtfully fixed upon her face.\n\n“I—I—am used to be alone,” said Florence. “I don’t mind it at all. Di and I pass whole days together, sometimes.” Florence might have said, whole weeks and months.\n\n“Is Di your maid, love?”\n\n“My dog, Mama,” said Florence, laughing. “Susan is my maid.”\n\n“And these are your rooms,” said Edith, looking round. “I was not shown these rooms the other day. We must have them improved, Florence. They shall be made the prettiest in the house.”\n\n“If I might change them, Mama,” returned Florence; “there is one upstairs I should like much better.”\n\n“Is this not high enough, dear girl?” asked Edith, smiling.\n\n“The other was my brother’s room,” said Florence, “and I am very fond of it. I would have spoken to Papa about it when I came home, and found the workmen here, and everything changing; but—”\n\nFlorence dropped her eyes, lest the same look should make her falter again.\n\n“but I was afraid it might distress him; and as you said you would be here again soon, Mama, and are the mistress of everything, I determined to take courage and ask you.”\n\nEdith sat looking at her, with her brilliant eyes intent upon her face, until Florence raising her own, she, in her turn, withdrew her gaze, and turned it on the ground. It was then that Florence thought how different this lady’s beauty was, from what she had supposed. She had thought it of a proud and lofty kind; yet her manner was so subdued and gentle, that if she had been of Florence’s own age and character, it scarcely could have invited confidence more.\n\nExcept when a constrained and singular reserve crept over her; and then she seemed (but Florence hardly understood this, though she could not choose but notice it, and think about it) as if she were humbled before Florence, and ill at ease. When she had said that she was not her Mama yet, and when Florence had called her the mistress of everything there, this change in her was quick and startling; and now, while the eyes of Florence rested on her face, she sat as though she would have shrunk and hidden from her, rather than as one about to love and cherish her, in right of such a near connexion.\n\nShe gave Florence her ready promise, about her new room, and said she would give directions about it herself. She then asked some questions concerning poor Paul; and when they had sat in conversation for some time, told Florence she had come to take her to her own home.\n\n“We have come to London now, my mother and I,” said Edith, “and you shall stay with us until I am married. I wish that we should know and trust each other, Florence.”\n\n“You are very kind to me,” said Florence, “dear Mama. How much I thank you!”\n\n“Let me say now, for it may be the best opportunity,” continued Edith, looking round to see that they were quite alone, and speaking in a lower voice, “that when I am married, and have gone away for some weeks, I shall be easier at heart if you will come home here. No matter who invites you to stay elsewhere. Come home here. It is better to be alone than—what I would say is,” she added, checking herself, “that I know well you are best at home, dear Florence.”\n\n“I will come home on the very day, Mama”\n\n“Do so. I rely on that promise. Now, prepare to come with me, dear girl. You will find me downstairs when you are ready.”\n\nSlowly and thoughtfully did Edith wander alone through the mansion of which she was so soon to be the lady: and little heed took she of all the elegance and splendour it began to display. The same indomitable haughtiness of soul, the same proud scorn expressed in eye and lip, the same fierce beauty, only tamed by a sense of its own little worth, and of the little worth of everything around it, went through the grand saloons and halls, that had got loose among the shady trees, and raged and rent themselves. The mimic roses on the walls and floors were set round with sharp thorns, that tore her breast; in every scrap of gold so dazzling to the eye, she saw some hateful atom of her purchase-money; the broad high mirrors showed her, at full length, a woman with a noble quality yet dwelling in her nature, who was too false to her better self, and too debased and lost, to save herself. She believed that all this was so plain, more or less, to all eyes, that she had no resource or power of self-assertion but in pride: and with this pride, which tortured her own heart night and day, she fought her fate out, braved it, and defied it.\n\nWas this the woman whom Florence—an innocent girl, strong only in her earnestness and simple truth—could so impress and quell, that by her side she was another creature, with her tempest of passion hushed, and her very pride itself subdued? Was this the woman who now sat beside her in a carriage, with her arms entwined, and who, while she courted and entreated her to love and trust her, drew her fair head to nestle on her breast, and would have laid down life to shield it from wrong or harm?\n\nOh, Edith! it were well to die, indeed, at such a time! Better and happier far, perhaps, to die so, Edith, than to live on to the end!\n\nThe Honourable Mrs Skewton, who was thinking of anything rather than of such sentiments—for, like many genteel persons who have existed at various times, she set her face against death altogether, and objected to the mention of any such low and levelling upstart—had borrowed a house in Brook Street, Grosvenor Square, from a stately relative (one of the Feenix brood), who was out of town, and who did not object to lending it, in the handsomest manner, for nuptial purposes, as the loan implied his final release and acquittance from all further loans and gifts to Mrs Skewton and her daughter. It being necessary for the credit of the family to make a handsome appearance at such a time, Mrs Skewton, with the assistance of an accommodating tradesman resident in the parish of Mary-le-bone, who lent out all sorts of articles to the nobility and gentry, from a service of plate to an army of footmen, clapped into this house a silver-headed butler (who was charged extra on that account, as having the appearance of an ancient family retainer), two very tall young men in livery, and a select staff of kitchen-servants; so that a legend arose, downstairs, that Withers the page, released at once from his numerous household duties, and from the propulsion of the wheeled-chair (inconsistent with the metropolis), had been several times observed to rub his eyes and pinch his limbs, as if he misdoubted his having overslept himself at the Leamington milkman’s, and being still in a celestial dream. A variety of requisites in plate and china being also conveyed to the same establishment from the same convenient source, with several miscellaneous articles, including a neat chariot and a pair of bays, Mrs Skewton cushioned herself on the principal sofa, in the Cleopatra attitude, and held her court in fair state.\n\n“And how,” said Mrs Skewton, on the entrance of her daughter and her charge, “is my charming Florence? You must come and kiss me, Florence, if you please, my love.”\n\nFlorence was timidly stooping to pick out a place in the white part of Mrs Skewton’s face, when that lady presented her ear, and relieved her of her difficulty.\n\n“Edith, my dear,” said Mrs Skewton, “positively, I—stand a little more in the light, my sweetest Florence, for a moment.”\n\nFlorence blushingly complied.\n\n“You don’t remember, dearest Edith,” said her mother, “what you were when you were about the same age as our exceedingly precious Florence, or a few years younger?”\n\n“I have long forgotten, mother.”\n\n“For positively, my dear,” said Mrs Skewton, “I do think that I see a decided resemblance to what you were then, in our extremely fascinating young friend. And it shows,” said Mrs Skewton, in a lower voice, which conveyed her opinion that Florence was in a very unfinished state, “what cultivation will do.”\n\n“It does, indeed,” was Edith’s stern reply.\n\nHer mother eyed her sharply for a moment, and feeling herself on unsafe ground, said, as a diversion:\n\n“My charming Florence, you must come and kiss me once more, if you please, my love.”\n\nFlorence complied, of course, and again imprinted her lips on Mrs Skewton’s ear.\n\n“And you have heard, no doubt, my darling pet,” said Mrs Skewton, detaining her hand, “that your Papa, whom we all perfectly adore and dote upon, is to be married to my dearest Edith this day week.”\n\n“I knew it would be very soon,” returned Florence, “but not exactly when.”\n\n“My darling Edith,” urged her mother, gaily, “is it possible you have not told Florence?”\n\n“Why should I tell Florence?” she returned, so suddenly and harshly, that Florence could scarcely believe it was the same voice.\n\nMrs Skewton then told Florence, as another and safer diversion, that her father was coming to dinner, and that he would no doubt be charmingly surprised to see her; as he had spoken last night of dressing in the City, and had known nothing of Edith’s design, the execution of which, according to Mrs Skewton’s expectation, would throw him into a perfect ecstasy. Florence was troubled to hear this; and her distress became so keen, as the dinner-hour approached, that if she had known how to frame an entreaty to be suffered to return home, without involving her father in her explanation, she would have hurried back on foot, bareheaded, breathless, and alone, rather than incur the risk of meeting his displeasure.\n\nAs the time drew nearer, she could hardly breathe. She dared not approach a window, lest he should see her from the street. She dared not go upstairs to hide her emotion, lest, in passing out at the door, she should meet him unexpectedly; besides which dread, she felt as though she never could come back again if she were summoned to his presence. In this conflict of fears; she was sitting by Cleopatra’s couch, endeavouring to understand and to reply to the bald discourse of that lady, when she heard his foot upon the stair.\n\n“I hear him now!” cried Florence, starting. “He is coming!”\n\nCleopatra, who in her juvenility was always playfully disposed, and who in her self-engrossment did not trouble herself about the nature of this agitation, pushed Florence behind her couch, and dropped a shawl over her, preparatory to giving Mr Dombey a rapture of surprise. It was so quickly done, that in a moment Florence heard his awful step in the room.\n\nHe saluted his intended mother-in-law, and his intended bride. The strange sound of his voice thrilled through the whole frame of his child.\n\n“My dear Dombey,” said Cleopatra, “come here and tell me how your pretty Florence is.”\n\n“Florence is very well,” said Mr Dombey, advancing towards the couch.\n\n“At home?”\n\n“At home,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“My dear Dombey,” returned Cleopatra, with bewitching vivacity; “now are you sure you are not deceiving me? I don’t know what my dearest Edith will say to me when I make such a declaration, but upon my honour I am afraid you are the falsest of men, my dear Dombey.”\n\nThough he had been; and had been detected on the spot, in the most enormous falsehood that was ever said or done; he could hardly have been more disconcerted than he was, when Mrs Skewton plucked the shawl away, and Florence, pale and trembling, rose before him like a ghost. He had not yet recovered his presence of mind, when Florence had run up to him, clasped her hands round his neck, kissed his face, and hurried out of the room. He looked round as if to refer the matter to somebody else, but Edith had gone after Florence, instantly.\n\n“Now, confess, my dear Dombey,” said Mrs Skewton, giving him her hand, “that you never were more surprised and pleased in your life.”\n\n“I never was more surprised,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Nor pleased, my dearest Dombey?” returned Mrs Skewton, holding up her fan.\n\n“I—yes, I am exceedingly glad to meet Florence here,” said Mr Dombey. He appeared to consider gravely about it for a moment, and then said, more decidedly, “Yes, I really am very glad indeed to meet Florence here.”\n\n“You wonder how she comes here?” said Mrs Skewton, “don’t you?”\n\n“Edith, perhaps—” suggested Mr Dombey.\n\n“Ah! wicked guesser!” replied Cleopatra, shaking her head. “Ah! cunning, cunning man! One shouldn’t tell these things; your sex, my dear Dombey, are so vain, and so apt to abuse our weakness; but you know my open soul—very well; immediately.”\n\nThis was addressed to one of the very tall young men who announced dinner.\n\n“But Edith, my dear Dombey,” she continued in a whisper, “when she cannot have you near her—and as I tell her, she cannot expect that always—will at least have near her something or somebody belonging to you. Well, how extremely natural that is! And in this spirit, nothing would keep her from riding off today to fetch our darling Florence. Well, how excessively charming that is!”\n\nAs she waited for an answer, Mr Dombey answered, “Eminently so.”\n\n“Bless you, my dear Dombey, for that proof of heart!” cried Cleopatra, squeezing his hand. “But I am growing too serious! Take me downstairs, like an angel, and let us see what these people intend to give us for dinner. Bless you, dear Dombey!”\n\nCleopatra skipping off her couch with tolerable briskness, after the last benediction, Mr Dombey took her arm in his and led her ceremoniously downstairs; one of the very tall young men on hire, whose organ of veneration was imperfectly developed, thrusting his tongue into his cheek, for the entertainment of the other very tall young man on hire, as the couple turned into the dining-room.\n\nFlorence and Edith were already there, and sitting side by side. Florence would have risen when her father entered, to resign her chair to him; but Edith openly put her hand upon her arm, and Mr Dombey took an opposite place at the round table.\n\nThe conversation was almost entirely sustained by Mrs Skewton. Florence hardly dared to raise her eyes, lest they should reveal the traces of tears; far less dared to speak; and Edith never uttered one word, unless in answer to a question. Verily, Cleopatra worked hard, for the establishment that was so nearly clutched; and verily it should have been a rich one to reward her!\n\n“And so your preparations are nearly finished at last, my dear Dombey?” said Cleopatra, when the dessert was put upon the table, and the silver-headed butler had withdrawn. “Even the lawyers” preparations!”\n\n“Yes, madam,” replied Mr Dombey; “the deed of settlement, the professional gentlemen inform me, is now ready, and as I was mentioning to you, Edith has only to do us the favour to suggest her own time for its execution.”\n\nEdith sat like a handsome statue; as cold, as silent, and as still.\n\n“My dearest love,” said Cleopatra, “do you hear what Mr Dombey says? Ah, my dear Dombey!” aside to that gentleman, “how her absence, as the time approaches, reminds me of the days, when that most agreeable of creatures, her Papa, was in your situation!”\n\n“I have nothing to suggest. It shall be when you please,” said Edith, scarcely looking over the table at Mr Dombey.\n\n“To-morrow?” suggested Mr Dombey.\n\n“If you please.”\n\n“Or would next day,” said Mr Dombey, “suit your engagements better?”\n\n“I have no engagements. I am always at your disposal. Let it be when you like.”\n\n“No engagements, my dear Edith!” remonstrated her mother, “when you are in a most terrible state of flurry all day long, and have a thousand and one appointments with all sorts of trades-people!”\n\n“They are of your making,” returned Edith, turning on her with a slight contraction of her brow. “You and Mr Dombey can arrange between you.”\n\n“Very true indeed, my love, and most considerate of you!” said Cleopatra. “My darling Florence, you must really come and kiss me once more, if you please, my dear!”\n\nSingular coincidence, that these gushes of interest in Florence hurried Cleopatra away from almost every dialogue in which Edith had a share, however trifling! Florence had certainly never undergone so much embracing, and perhaps had never been, unconsciously, so useful in her life.\n\nMr Dombey was far from quarrelling, in his own breast, with the manner of his beautiful betrothed. He had that good reason for sympathy with haughtiness and coldness, which is found in a fellow-feeling. It flattered him to think how these deferred to him, in Edith’s case, and seemed to have no will apart from his. It flattered him to picture to himself, this proud and stately woman doing the honours of his house, and chilling his guests after his own manner. The dignity of Dombey and Son would be heightened and maintained, indeed, in such hands.\n\nSo thought Mr Dombey, when he was left alone at the dining-table, and mused upon his past and future fortunes: finding no uncongeniality in an air of scant and gloomy state that pervaded the room, in colour a dark brown, with black hatchments of pictures blotching the walls, and twenty-four black chairs, with almost as many nails in them as so many coffins, waiting like mutes, upon the threshold of the Turkey carpet; and two exhausted negroes holding up two withered branches of candelabra on the sideboard, and a musty smell prevailing as if the ashes of ten thousand dinners were entombed in the sarcophagus below it. The owner of the house lived much abroad; the air of England seldom agreed long with a member of the Feenix family; and the room had gradually put itself into deeper and still deeper mourning for him, until it was become so funereal as to want nothing but a body in it to be quite complete.\n\nNo bad representation of the body, for the nonce, in his unbending form, if not in his attitude, Mr Dombey looked down into the cold depths of the dead sea of mahogany on which the fruit dishes and decanters lay at anchor: as if the subjects of his thoughts were rising towards the surface one by one, and plunging down again. Edith was there in all her majesty of brow and figure; and close to her came Florence, with her timid head turned to him, as it had been, for an instant, when she left the room; and Edith’s eyes upon her, and Edith’s hand put out protectingly. A little figure in a low arm-chair came springing next into the light, and looked upon him wonderingly, with its bright eyes and its old-young face, gleaming as in the flickering of an evening fire. Again came Florence close upon it, and absorbed his whole attention. Whether as a fore-doomed difficulty and disappointment to him; whether as a rival who had crossed him in his way, and might again; whether as his child, of whom, in his successful wooing, he could stoop to think as claiming, at such a time, to be no more estranged; or whether as a hint to him that the mere appearance of caring for his own blood should be maintained in his new relations; he best knew. Indifferently well, perhaps, at best; for marriage company and marriage altars, and ambitious scenes—still blotted here and there with Florence—always Florence—turned up so fast, and so confusedly, that he rose, and went upstairs to escape them.\n\nIt was quite late at night before candles were brought; for at present they made Mrs Skewton’s head ache, she complained; and in the meantime Florence and Mrs Skewton talked together (Cleopatra being very anxious to keep her close to herself), or Florence touched the piano softly for Mrs Skewton’s delight; to make no mention of a few occasions in the course of the evening, when that affectionate lady was impelled to solicit another kiss, and which always happened after Edith had said anything. They were not many, however, for Edith sat apart by an open window during the whole time (in spite of her mother’s fears that she would take cold), and remained there until Mr Dombey took leave. He was serenely gracious to Florence when he did so; and Florence went to bed in a room within Edith’s, so happy and hopeful, that she thought of her late self as if it were some other poor deserted girl who was to be pitied for her sorrow; and in her pity, sobbed herself to sleep.\n\nThe week fled fast. There were drives to milliners, dressmakers, jewellers, lawyers, florists, pastry-cooks; and Florence was always of the party. Florence was to go to the wedding. Florence was to cast off her mourning, and to wear a brilliant dress on the occasion. The milliner’s intentions on the subject of this dress—the milliner was a Frenchwoman, and greatly resembled Mrs Skewton—were so chaste and elegant, that Mrs Skewton bespoke one like it for herself. The milliner said it would become her to admiration, and that all the world would take her for the young lady’s sister.\n\nThe week fled faster. Edith looked at nothing and cared for nothing. Her rich dresses came home, and were tried on, and were loudly commended by Mrs Skewton and the milliners, and were put away without a word from her. Mrs Skewton made their plans for every day, and executed them. Sometimes Edith sat in the carriage when they went to make purchases; sometimes, when it was absolutely necessary, she went into the shops. But Mrs Skewton conducted the whole business, whatever it happened to be; and Edith looked on as uninterested and with as much apparent indifference as if she had no concern in it. Florence might perhaps have thought she was haughty and listless, but that she was never so to her. So Florence quenched her wonder in her gratitude whenever it broke out, and soon subdued it.\n\nThe week fled faster. It had nearly winged its flight away. The last night of the week, the night before the marriage, was come. In the dark room—for Mrs Skewton’s head was no better yet, though she expected to recover permanently to-morrow—were that lady, Edith, and Mr Dombey. Edith was at her open window looking out into the street; Mr Dombey and Cleopatra were talking softly on the sofa. It was growing late; and Florence, being fatigued, had gone to bed.\n\n“My dear Dombey,” said Cleopatra, “you will leave me Florence to-morrow, when you deprive me of my sweetest Edith.”\n\nMr Dombey said he would, with pleasure.\n\n“To have her about me, here, while you are both at Paris, and to think at her age, I am assisting in the formation of her mind, my dear Dombey,” said Cleopatra, “will be a perfect balm to me in the extremely shattered state to which I shall be reduced.”\n\nEdith turned her head suddenly. Her listless manner was exchanged, in a moment, to one of burning interest, and, unseen in the darkness, she attended closely to their conversation.\n\nMr Dombey would be delighted to leave Florence in such admirable guardianship.\n\n“My dear Dombey,” returned Cleopatra, “a thousand thanks for your good opinion. I feared you were going, with malice aforethought, as the dreadful lawyers say—those horrid prosers!—to condemn me to utter solitude.”\n\n“Why do me so great an injustice, my dear madam?” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Because my charming Florence tells me so positively she must go home tomorrow, returned Cleopatra, that I began to be afraid, my dearest Dombey, you were quite a Bashaw.”\n\n“I assure you, madam!” said Mr Dombey, “I have laid no commands on Florence; and if I had, there are no commands like your wish.”\n\n“My dear Dombey,” replied Cleopatra, what a courtier you are! Though I’ll not say so, either; for courtiers have no heart, and yours pervades your farming life and character. And are you really going so early, my dear Dombey!”\n\nOh, indeed! it was late, and Mr Dombey feared he must.\n\n“Is this a fact, or is it all a dream!” lisped Cleopatra. “Can I believe, my dearest Dombey, that you are coming back tomorrow morning to deprive me of my sweet companion; my own Edith!”\n\nMr Dombey, who was accustomed to take things literally, reminded Mrs Skewton that they were to meet first at the church.\n\n“The pang,” said Mrs Skewton, “of consigning a child, even to you, my dear Dombey, is one of the most excruciating imaginable, and combined with a naturally delicate constitution, and the extreme stupidity of the pastry-cook who has undertaken the breakfast, is almost too much for my poor strength. But I shall rally, my dear Dombey, in the morning; do not fear for me, or be uneasy on my account. Heaven bless you! My dearest Edith!” she cried archly. “Somebody is going, pet.”\n\nEdith, who had turned her head again towards the window, and whose interest in their conversation had ceased, rose up in her place, but made no advance towards him, and said nothing. Mr Dombey, with a lofty gallantry adapted to his dignity and the occasion, betook his creaking boots towards her, put her hand to his lips, said, “Tomorrow morning I shall have the happiness of claiming this hand as Mrs Dombey’s,” and bowed himself solemnly out.\n\nMrs Skewton rang for candles as soon as the house-door had closed upon him. With the candles appeared her maid, with the juvenile dress that was to delude the world to-morrow. The dress had savage retribution in it, as such dresses ever have, and made her infinitely older and more hideous than her greasy flannel gown. But Mrs Skewton tried it on with mincing satisfaction; smirked at her cadaverous self in the glass, as she thought of its killing effect upon the Major; and suffering her maid to take it off again, and to prepare her for repose, tumbled into ruins like a house of painted cards.\n\nAll this time, Edith remained at the dark window looking out into the street. When she and her mother were at last left alone, she moved from it for the first time that evening, and came opposite to her. The yawning, shaking, peevish figure of the mother, with her eyes raised to confront the proud erect form of the daughter, whose glance of fire was bent downward upon her, had a conscious air upon it, that no levity or temper could conceal.\n\n“I am tired to death,” said she. “You can’t be trusted for a moment. You are worse than a child. Child! No child would be half so obstinate and undutiful.”\n\n“Listen to me, mother,” returned Edith, passing these words by with a scorn that would not descend to trifle with them. “You must remain alone here until I return.”\n\n“Must remain alone here, Edith, until you return!” repeated her mother.\n\n“Or in that name upon which I shall call to-morrow to witness what I do, so falsely: and so shamefully, I swear I will refuse the hand of this man in the church. If I do not, may I fall dead upon the pavement!”\n\nThe mother answered with a look of quick alarm, in no degree diminished by the look she met.\n\n“It is enough,” said Edith, steadily, “that we are what we are. I will have no youth and truth dragged down to my level. I will have no guileless nature undermined, corrupted, and perverted, to amuse the leisure of a world of mothers. You know my meaning. Florence must go home.”\n\n“You are an idiot, Edith,” cried her angry mother. “Do you expect there can ever be peace for you in that house, till she is married, and away?”\n\n“Ask me, or ask yourself, if I ever expect peace in that house,” said her daughter, “and you know the answer.”\n\n“And am I to be told tonight, after all my pains and labour, and when you are going, through me, to be rendered independent,” her mother almost shrieked in her passion, while her palsied head shook like a leaf, “that there is corruption and contagion in me, and that I am not fit company for a girl! What are you, pray? What are you?”\n\n“I have put the question to myself,” said Edith, ashy pale, and pointing to the window, “more than once when I have been sitting there, and something in the faded likeness of my sex has wandered past outside; and God knows I have met with my reply. Oh mother, mother, if you had but left me to my natural heart when I too was a girl—a younger girl than Florence—how different I might have been!”\n\nSensible that any show of anger was useless here, her mother restrained herself, and fell a whimpering, and bewailed that she had lived too long, and that her only child had cast her off, and that duty towards parents was forgotten in these evil days, and that she had heard unnatural taunts, and cared for life no longer.\n\n“If one is to go on living through continual scenes like this,” she whined, “I am sure it would be much better for me to think of some means of putting an end to my existence. Oh! The idea of your being my daughter, Edith, and addressing me in such a strain!”\n\n“Between us, mother,” returned Edith, mournfully, “the time for mutual reproaches is past.”\n\n“Then why do you revive it?” whimpered her mother. “You know that you are lacerating me in the cruellest manner. You know how sensitive I am to unkindness. At such a moment, too, when I have so much to think of, and am naturally anxious to appear to the best advantage! I wonder at you, Edith. To make your mother a fright upon your wedding-day!”\n\nEdith bent the same fixed look upon her, as she sobbed and rubbed her eyes; and said in the same low steady voice, which had neither risen nor fallen since she first addressed her, “I have said that Florence must go home.”\n\n“Let her go!” cried the afflicted and affrighted parent, hastily. “I am sure I am willing she should go. What is the girl to me?”\n\n“She is so much to me, that rather than communicate, or suffer to be communicated to her, one grain of the evil that is in my breast, mother, I would renounce you, as I would (if you gave me cause) renounce him in the church to-morrow,” replied Edith. “Leave her alone. She shall not, while I can interpose, be tampered with and tainted by the lessons I have learned. This is no hard condition on this bitter night.”\n\n“If you had proposed it in a filial manner, Edith,” whined her mother, “perhaps not; very likely not. But such extremely cutting words—”\n\n“They are past and at an end between us now,” said Edith. “Take your own way, mother; share as you please in what you have gained; spend, enjoy, make much of it; and be as happy as you will. The object of our lives is won. Henceforth let us wear it silently. My lips are closed upon the past from this hour. I forgive you your part in to-morrow’s wickedness. May God forgive my own!”\n\nWithout a tremor in her voice, or frame, and passing onward with a foot that set itself upon the neck of every soft emotion, she bade her mother good-night, and repaired to her own room.\n\nBut not to rest; for there was no rest in the tumult of her agitation when alone to and fro, and to and fro, and to and fro again, five hundred times, among the splendid preparations for her adornment on the morrow; with her dark hair shaken down, her dark eyes flashing with a raging light, her broad white bosom red with the cruel grasp of the relentless hand with which she spurned it from her, pacing up and down with an averted head, as if she would avoid the sight of her own fair person, and divorce herself from its companionship. Thus, in the dead time of the night before her bridal, Edith Granger wrestled with her unquiet spirit, tearless, friendless, silent, proud, and uncomplaining.\n\nAt length it happened that she touched the open door which led into the room where Florence lay.\n\nShe started, stopped, and looked in.\n\nA light was burning there, and showed her Florence in her bloom of innocence and beauty, fast asleep. Edith held her breath, and felt herself drawn on towards her.\n\nDrawn nearer, nearer, nearer yet; at last, drawn so near, that stooping down, she pressed her lips to the gentle hand that lay outside the bed, and put it softly to her neck. Its touch was like the prophet’s rod of old upon the rock. Her tears sprung forth beneath it, as she sunk upon her knees, and laid her aching head and streaming hair upon the pillow by its side.\n\nThus Edith Granger passed the night before her bridal. Thus the sun found her on her bridal morning.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI. The Wedding\n\nDawn with its passionless blank face, steals shivering to the church beneath which lies the dust of little Paul and his mother, and looks in at the windows. It is cold and dark. Night crouches yet, upon the pavement, and broods, sombre and heavy, in nooks and corners of the building. The steeple-clock, perched up above the houses, emerging from beneath another of the countless ripples in the tide of time that regularly roll and break on the eternal shore, is greyly visible, like a stone beacon, recording how the sea flows on; but within doors, dawn, at first, can only peep at night, and see that it is there.\n\nHovering feebly round the church, and looking in, dawn moans and weeps for its short reign, and its tears trickle on the window-glass, and the trees against the church-wall bow their heads, and wring their many hands in sympathy. Night, growing pale before it, gradually fades out of the church, but lingers in the vaults below, and sits upon the coffins. And now comes bright day, burnishing the steeple-clock, and reddening the spire, and drying up the tears of dawn, and stifling its complaining; and the dawn, following the night, and chasing it from its last refuge, shrinks into the vaults itself and hides, with a frightened face, among the dead, until night returns, refreshed, to drive it out.\n\nAnd now, the mice, who have been busier with the prayer-books than their proper owners, and with the hassocks, more worn by their little teeth than by human knees, hide their bright eyes in their holes, and gather close together in affright at the resounding clashing of the church-door. For the beadle, that man of power, comes early this morning with the sexton; and Mrs Miff, the wheezy little pew-opener—a mighty dry old lady, sparely dressed, with not an inch of fulness anywhere about her—is also here, and has been waiting at the church-gate half-an-hour, as her place is, for the beadle.\n\nA vinegary face has Mrs Miff, and a mortified bonnet, and eke a thirsty soul for sixpences and shillings. Beckoning to stray people to come into pews, has given Mrs Miff an air of mystery; and there is reservation in the eye of Mrs Miff, as always knowing of a softer seat, but having her suspicions of the fee. There is no such fact as Mr Miff, nor has there been, these twenty years, and Mrs Miff would rather not allude to him. He held some bad opinions, it would seem, about free seats; and though Mrs Miff hopes he may be gone upwards, she couldn’t positively undertake to say so.\n\nBusy is Mrs Miff this morning at the church-door, beating and dusting the altar-cloth, the carpet, and the cushions; and much has Mrs Miff to say, about the wedding they are going to have. Mrs Miff is told, that the new furniture and alterations in the house cost full five thousand pound if they cost a penny; and Mrs Miff has heard, upon the best authority, that the lady hasn’t got a sixpence wherewithal to bless herself. Mrs Miff remembers, like wise, as if it had happened yesterday, the first wife’s funeral, and then the christening, and then the other funeral; and Mrs Miff says, by-the-by she’ll soap-and-water that “ere tablet presently, against the company arrive. Mr Sownds the Beadle, who is sitting in the sun upon the church steps all this time (and seldom does anything else, except, in cold weather, sitting by the fire), approves of Mrs Miff’s discourse, and asks if Mrs Miff has heard it said, that the lady is uncommon handsome? The information Mrs Miff has received, being of this nature, Mr Sownds the Beadle, who, though orthodox and corpulent, is still an admirer of female beauty, observes, with unction, yes, he hears she is a spanker—an expression that seems somewhat forcible to Mrs Miff, or would, from any lips but those of Mr Sownds the Beadle.\n\nIn Mr Dombey’s house, at this same time, there is great stir and bustle, more especially among the women: not one of whom has had a wink of sleep since four o’clock, and all of whom were fully dressed before six. Mr Towlinson is an object of greater consideration than usual to the housemaid, and the cook says at breakfast time that one wedding makes many, which the housemaid can’t believe, and don’t think true at all. Mr Towlinson reserves his sentiments on this question; being rendered something gloomy by the engagement of a foreigner with whiskers (Mr Towlinson is whiskerless himself), who has been hired to accompany the happy pair to Paris, and who is busy packing the new chariot. In respect of this personage, Mr Towlinson admits, presently, that he never knew of any good that ever come of foreigners; and being charged by the ladies with prejudice, says, look at Bonaparte who was at the head of ’em, and see what he was always up to! Which the housemaid says is very true.\n\nThe pastry-cook is hard at work in the funereal room in Brook Street, and the very tall young men are busy looking on. One of the very tall young men already smells of sherry, and his eyes have a tendency to become fixed in his head, and to stare at objects without seeing them. The very tall young man is conscious of this failing in himself; and informs his comrade that it’s his “exciseman.” The very tall young man would say excitement, but his speech is hazy.\n\nThe men who play the bells have got scent of the marriage; and the marrow-bones and cleavers too; and a brass band too. The first, are practising in a back settlement near Battlebridge; the second, put themselves in communication, through their chief, with Mr Towlinson, to whom they offer terms to be bought off; and the third, in the person of an artful trombone, lurks and dodges round the corner, waiting for some traitor tradesman to reveal the place and hour of breakfast, for a bribe. Expectation and excitement extend further yet, and take a wider range. From Balls Pond, Mr Perch brings Mrs Perch to spend the day with Mr Dombey’s servants, and accompany them, surreptitiously, to see the wedding. In Mr Toots’s lodgings, Mr Toots attires himself as if he were at least the Bridegroom; determined to behold the spectacle in splendour from a secret corner of the gallery, and thither to convey the Chicken: for it is Mr Toots’s desperate intent to point out Florence to the Chicken, then and there, and openly to say, “Now, Chicken, I will not deceive you any longer; the friend I have sometimes mentioned to you is myself; Miss Dombey is the object of my passion; what are your opinions, Chicken, in this state of things, and what, on the spot, do you advise? The so-much-to-be-astonished Chicken, in the meanwhile, dips his beak into a tankard of strong beer, in Mr Toots’s kitchen, and pecks up two pounds of beefsteaks. In Princess’s Place, Miss Tox is up and doing; for she too, though in sore distress, is resolved to put a shilling in the hands of Mrs Miff, and see the ceremony which has a cruel fascination for her, from some lonely corner. The quarters of the wooden Midshipman are all alive; for Captain Cuttle, in his ankle-jacks and with a huge shirt-collar, is seated at his breakfast, listening to Rob the Grinder as he reads the marriage service to him beforehand, under orders, to the end that the Captain may perfectly understand the solemnity he is about to witness: for which purpose, the Captain gravely lays injunctions on his chaplain, from time to time, to “put about,” or to “overhaul that “ere article again,” or to stick to his own duty, and leave the Amens to him, the Captain; one of which he repeats, whenever a pause is made by Rob the Grinder, with sonorous satisfaction.\n\nBesides all this, and much more, twenty nursery-maids in Mr Dombey’s street alone, have promised twenty families of little women, whose instinctive interest in nuptials dates from their cradles, that they shall go and see the marriage. Truly, Mr Sownds the Beadle has good reason to feel himself in office, as he suns his portly figure on the church steps, waiting for the marriage hour. Truly, Mrs Miff has cause to pounce on an unlucky dwarf child, with a giant baby, who peeps in at the porch, and drive her forth with indignation!\n\nCousin Feenix has come over from abroad, expressly to attend the marriage. Cousin Feenix was a man about town, forty years ago; but he is still so juvenile in figure and in manner, and so well got up, that strangers are amazed when they discover latent wrinkles in his lordship’s face, and crows’ feet in his eyes: and first observe him, not exactly certain when he walks across a room, of going quite straight to where he wants to go. But Cousin Feenix, getting up at half-past seven o’clock or so, is quite another thing from Cousin Feenix got up; and very dim, indeed, he looks, while being shaved at Long’s Hotel, in Bond Street.\n\nMr Dombey leaves his dressing-room, amidst a general whisking away of the women on the staircase, who disperse in all directions, with a great rustling of skirts, except Mrs Perch, who, being (but that she always is) in an interesting situation, is not nimble, and is obliged to face him, and is ready to sink with confusion as she curtesys;—may Heaven avert all evil consequences from the house of Perch! Mr Dombey walks up to the drawing-room, to bide his time. Gorgeous are Mr Dombey’s new blue coat, fawn-coloured pantaloons, and lilac waistcoat; and a whisper goes about the house, that Mr Dombey’s hair is curled.\n\nA double knock announces the arrival of the Major, who is gorgeous too, and wears a whole geranium in his button-hole, and has his hair curled tight and crisp, as well the Native knows.\n\n“Dombey!” says the Major, putting out both hands, “how are you?”\n\n“Major,” says Mr Dombey, “how are You?”\n\n“By Jove, Sir,” says the Major, “Joey B. is in such case this morning, Sir,”—and here he hits himself hard upon the breast—“In such case this morning, Sir, that, damme, Dombey, he has half a mind to make a double marriage of it, Sir, and take the mother.”\n\nMr Dombey smiles; but faintly, even for him; for Mr Dombey feels that he is going to be related to the mother, and that, under those circumstances, she is not to be joked about.\n\n“Dombey,” says the Major, seeing this, “I give you joy. I congratulate you, Dombey. By the Lord, Sir,” says the Major, “you are more to be envied, this day, than any man in England!”\n\nHere again Mr Dombey’s assent is qualified; because he is going to confer a great distinction on a lady; and, no doubt, she is to be envied most.\n\n“As to Edith Granger, Sir,” pursues the Major, “there is not a woman in all Europe but might—and would, Sir, you will allow Bagstock to add—and would—give her ears, and her earrings, too, to be in Edith Granger’s place.”\n\n“You are good enough to say so, Major,” says Mr Dombey.\n\n“Dombey,” returns the Major, “you know it. Let us have no false delicacy. You know it. Do you know it, or do you not, Dombey?” says the Major, almost in a passion.\n\n“Oh, really, Major—”\n\n“Damme, Sir,” retorts the Major, “do you know that fact, or do you not? Dombey! Is old Joe your friend? Are we on that footing of unreserved intimacy, Dombey, that may justify a man—a blunt old Joseph B., Sir—in speaking out; or am I to take open order, Dombey, and to keep my distance, and to stand on forms?”\n\n“My dear Major Bagstock,” says Mr Dombey, with a gratified air, “you are quite warm.”\n\n“By Gad, Sir,” says the Major, “I am warm. Joseph B. does not deny it, Dombey. He is warm. This is an occasion, Sir, that calls forth all the honest sympathies remaining in an old, infernal, battered, used-up, invalided, J. B. carcase. And I tell you what, Dombey—at such a time a man must blurt out what he feels, or put a muzzle on; and Joseph Bagstock tells you to your face, Dombey, as he tells his club behind your back, that he never will be muzzled when Paul Dombey is in question. Now, damme, Sir,” concludes the Major, with great firmness, “what do you make of that?”\n\n“Major,” says Mr Dombey, “I assure you that I am really obliged to you. I had no idea of checking your too partial friendship.”\n\n“Not too partial, Sir!” exclaims the choleric Major. “Dombey, I deny it.”\n\n“Your friendship I will say then,” pursues Mr Dombey, “on any account. Nor can I forget, Major, on such an occasion as the present, how much I am indebted to it.”\n\n“Dombey,” says the Major, with appropriate action, “that is the hand of Joseph Bagstock: of plain old Joey B., Sir, if you like that better! That is the hand, of which His Royal Highness the late Duke of York, did me the honour to observe, Sir, to His Royal Highness the late Duke of Kent, that it was the hand of Josh: a rough and tough, and possibly an up-to-snuff, old vagabond. Dombey, may the present moment be the least unhappy of our lives. God bless you!”\n\nNow enters Mr Carker, gorgeous likewise, and smiling like a wedding-guest indeed. He can scarcely let Mr Dombey’s hand go, he is so congratulatory; and he shakes the Major’s hand so heartily at the same time, that his voice shakes too, in accord with his arms, as it comes sliding from between his teeth.\n\n“The very day is auspicious,” says Mr Carker. “The brightest and most genial weather! I hope I am not a moment late?”\n\n“Punctual to your time, Sir,” says the Major.\n\n“I am rejoiced, I am sure,” says Mr Carker. “I was afraid I might be a few seconds after the appointed time, for I was delayed by a procession of waggons; and I took the liberty of riding round to Brook Street”—this to Mr Dombey—“to leave a few poor rarities of flowers for Mrs Dombey. A man in my position, and so distinguished as to be invited here, is proud to offer some homage in acknowledgment of his vassalage: and as I have no doubt Mrs Dombey is overwhelmed with what is costly and magnificent;” with a strange glance at his patron; “I hope the very poverty of my offering, may find favour for it.”\n\n“Mrs Dombey, that is to be,” returns Mr Dombey, condescendingly, “will be very sensible of your attention, Carker, I am sure.”\n\n“And if she is to be Mrs Dombey this morning, Sir,” says the Major, putting down his coffee-cup, and looking at his watch, “it’s high time we were off!”\n\nForth, in a barouche, ride Mr Dombey, Major Bagstock, and Mr Carker, to the church. Mr Sownds the Beadle has long risen from the steps, and is in waiting with his cocked hat in his hand. Mrs Miff curtseys and proposes chairs in the vestry. Mr Dombey prefers remaining in the church. As he looks up at the organ, Miss Tox in the gallery shrinks behind the fat leg of a cherubim on a monument, with cheeks like a young Wind. Captain Cuttle, on the contrary, stands up and waves his hook, in token of welcome and encouragement. Mr Toots informs the Chicken, behind his hand, that the middle gentleman, he in the fawn-coloured pantaloons, is the father of his love. The Chicken hoarsely whispers Mr Toots that he’s as stiff a cove as ever he see, but that it is within the resources of Science to double him up, with one blow in the waistcoat.\n\nMr Sownds and Mrs Miff are eyeing Mr Dombey from a little distance, when the noise of approaching wheels is heard, and Mr Sownds goes out. Mrs Miff, meeting Mr Dombey’s eye as it is withdrawn from the presumptuous maniac upstairs, who salutes him with so much urbanity, drops a curtsey, and informs him that she believes his “good lady” is come. Then there is a crowding and a whispering at the door, and the good lady enters, with a haughty step.\n\nThere is no sign upon her face, of last night’s suffering; there is no trace in her manner, of the woman on the bended knees, reposing her wild head, in beautiful abandonment, upon the pillow of the sleeping girl. That girl, all gentle and lovely, is at her side—a striking contrast to her own disdainful and defiant figure, standing there, composed, erect, inscrutable of will, resplendent and majestic in the zenith of its charms, yet beating down, and treading on, the admiration that it challenges.\n\nThere is a pause while Mr Sownds the Beadle glides into the vestry for the clergyman and clerk. At this juncture, Mrs Skewton speaks to Mr Dombey: more distinctly and emphatically than her custom is, and moving at the same time, close to Edith.\n\n“My dear Dombey,” said the good Mama, “I fear I must relinquish darling Florence after all, and suffer her to go home, as she herself proposed. After my loss of today, my dear Dombey, I feel I shall not have spirits, even for her society.”\n\n“Had she not better stay with you?” returns the Bridegroom.\n\n“I think not, my dear Dombey. No, I think not. I shall be better alone. Besides, my dearest Edith will be her natural and constant guardian when you return, and I had better not encroach upon her trust, perhaps. She might be jealous. Eh, dear Edith?”\n\nThe affectionate Mama presses her daughter’s arm, as she says this; perhaps entreating her attention earnestly.\n\n“To be serious, my dear Dombey,” she resumes, “I will relinquish our dear child, and not inflict my gloom upon her. We have settled that, just now. She fully understands, dear Dombey. Edith, my dear,—she fully understands.”\n\nAgain, the good mother presses her daughter’s arm. Mr Dombey offers no additional remonstrance; for the clergyman and clerk appear; and Mrs Miff, and Mr Sownds the Beadle, group the party in their proper places at the altar rails.\n\nThe sun is shining down, upon the golden letters of the ten commandments. Why does the Bride’s eye read them, one by one? Which one of all the ten appears the plainest to her in the glare of light? False Gods; murder; theft; the honour that she owes her mother;—which is it that appears to leave the wall, and printing itself in glowing letters, on her book!\n\n“Who giveth this woman to be married to this man?”\n\nCousin Feenix does that. He has come from Baden-Baden on purpose. “Confound it,” Cousin Feenix says—good-natured creature, Cousin Feenix—“when we do get a rich City fellow into the family, let us show him some attention; let us do something for him.”\n\n“I give this woman to be married to this man,” saith Cousin Feenix therefore. Cousin Feenix, meaning to go in a straight line, but turning off sideways by reason of his wilful legs, gives the wrong woman to be married to this man, at first—to wit, a brides—maid of some condition, distantly connected with the family, and ten years Mrs Skewton’s junior —but Mrs Miff, interposing her mortified bonnet, dexterously turns him back, and runs him, as on castors, full at the “good lady:” whom Cousin Feenix giveth to married to this man accordingly.\n\nAnd will they in the sight of heaven—?\n\nAy, that they will: Mr Dombey says he will. And what says Edith? She will.\n\nSo, from that day forward, for better for worse, for richer for poorer, in sickness and in health, to love and to cherish, till death do them part, they plight their troth to one another, and are married.\n\nIn a firm, free hand, the Bride subscribes her name in the register, when they adjourn to the vestry. “There ain’t a many ladies come here,” Mrs Miff says with a curtsey—to look at Mrs Miff, at such a season, is to make her mortified bonnet go down with a dip—“writes their names like this good lady!” Mr Sownds the Beadle thinks it is a truly spanking signature, and worthy of the writer—this, however, between himself and conscience.\n\nFlorence signs too, but unapplauded, for her hand shakes. All the party sign; Cousin Feenix last; who puts his noble name into a wrong place, and enrols himself as having been born that morning.\n\nThe Major now salutes the Bride right gallantly, and carries out that branch of military tactics in reference to all the ladies: notwithstanding Mrs Skewton’s being extremely hard to kiss, and squeaking shrilly in the sacred edifice. The example is followed by Cousin Feenix and even by Mr Dombey. Lastly, Mr Carker, with his white teeth glistening, approaches Edith, more as if he meant to bite her, than to taste the sweets that linger on her lips.\n\nThere is a glow upon her proud cheek, and a flashing in her eyes, that may be meant to stay him; but it does not, for he salutes her as the rest have done, and wishes her all happiness.\n\n“If wishes,” says he in a low voice, “are not superfluous, applied to such a union.”\n\n“I thank you, Sir,” she answers, with a curled lip, and a heaving bosom.\n\nBut, does Edith feel still, as on the night when she knew that Mr Dombey would return to offer his alliance, that Carker knows her thoroughly, and reads her right, and that she is more degraded by his knowledge of her, than by aught else? Is it for this reason that her haughtiness shrinks beneath his smile, like snow within the hands that grasps it firmly, and that her imperious glance droops in meeting his, and seeks the ground?\n\n“I am proud to see,” said Mr Carker, with a servile stooping of his neck, which the revelations making by his eyes and teeth proclaim to be a lie, “I am proud to see that my humble offering is graced by Mrs Dombey’s hand, and permitted to hold so favoured a place in so joyful an occasion.”\n\nThough she bends her head, in answer, there is something in the momentary action of her hand, as if she would crush the flowers it holds, and fling them, with contempt, upon the ground. But, she puts the hand through the arm of her new husband, who has been standing near, conversing with the Major, and is proud again, and motionless, and silent.\n\nThe carriages are once more at the church door. Mr Dombey, with his bride upon his arm, conducts her through the twenty families of little women who are on the steps, and every one of whom remembers the fashion and the colour of her every article of dress from that moment, and reproduces it on her doll, who is for ever being married. Cleopatra and Cousin Feenix enter the same carriage. The Major hands into a second carriage, Florence, and the bridesmaid who so narrowly escaped being given away by mistake, and then enters it himself, and is followed by Mr Carker. Horses prance and caper; coachmen and footmen shine in fluttering favours, flowers, and new-made liveries. Away they dash and rattle through the streets; and as they pass along, a thousand heads are turned to look at them, and a thousand sober moralists revenge themselves for not being married too, that morning, by reflecting that these people little think such happiness can’t last.\n\nMiss Tox emerges from behind the cherubim’s leg, when all is quiet, and comes slowly down from the gallery. Miss Tox’s eyes are red, and her pocket-handkerchief is damp. She is wounded, but not exasperated, and she hopes they may be happy. She quite admits to herself the beauty of the bride, and her own comparatively feeble and faded attractions; but the stately image of Mr Dombey in his lilac waistcoat, and his fawn-coloured pantaloons, is present to her mind, and Miss Tox weeps afresh, behind her veil, on her way home to Princess’s Place. Captain Cuttle, having joined in all the amens and responses, with a devout growl, feels much improved by his religious exercises; and in a peaceful frame of mind pervades the body of the church, glazed hat in hand, and reads the tablet to the memory of little Paul. The gallant Mr Toots, attended by the faithful Chicken, leaves the building in torments of love. The Chicken is as yet unable to elaborate a scheme for winning Florence, but his first idea has gained possession of him, and he thinks the doubling up of Mr Dombey would be a move in the right direction. Mr Dombey’s servants come out of their hiding-places, and prepare to rush to Brook Street, when they are delayed by symptoms of indisposition on the part of Mrs Perch, who entreats a glass of water, and becomes alarming; Mrs Perch gets better soon, however, and is borne away; and Mrs Miff, and Mr Sownds the Beadle, sit upon the steps to count what they have gained by the affair, and talk it over, while the sexton tolls a funeral.\n\nNow, the carriages arrive at the Bride’s residence, and the players on the bells begin to jingle, and the band strikes up, and Mr Punch, that model of connubial bliss, salutes his wife. Now, the people run, and push, and press round in a gaping throng, while Mr Dombey, leading Mrs Dombey by the hand, advances solemnly into the Feenix Halls. Now, the rest of the wedding party alight, and enter after them. And why does Mr Carker, passing through the people to the hall-door, think of the old woman who called to him in the Grove that morning? Or why does Florence, as she passes, think, with a tremble, of her childhood, when she was lost, and of the visage of Good Mrs Brown?\n\nNow, there are more congratulations on this happiest of days, and more company, though not much; and now they leave the drawing-room, and range themselves at table in the dark-brown dining-room, which no confectioner can brighten up, let him garnish the exhausted negroes with as many flowers and love-knots as he will.\n\nThe pastry-cook has done his duty like a man, though, and a rich breakfast is set forth. Mr and Mrs Chick have joined the party, among others. Mrs Chick admires that Edith should be, by nature, such a perfect Dombey; and is affable and confidential to Mrs Skewton, whose mind is relieved of a great load, and who takes her share of the champagne. The very tall young man who suffered from excitement early, is better; but a vague sentiment of repentance has seized upon him, and he hates the other very tall young man, and wrests dishes from him by violence, and takes a grim delight in disobliging the company. The company are cool and calm, and do not outrage the black hatchments of pictures looking down upon them, by any excess of mirth. Cousin Feenix and the Major are the gayest there; but Mr Carker has a smile for the whole table. He has an especial smile for the Bride, who very, very seldom meets it.\n\nCousin Feenix rises, when the company have breakfasted, and the servants have left the room; and wonderfully young he looks, with his white wristbands almost covering his hands (otherwise rather bony), and the bloom of the champagne in his cheeks.\n\n“Upon my honour,” says Cousin Feenix, “although it’s an unusual sort of thing in a private gentleman’s house, I must beg leave to call upon you to drink what is usually called a—in fact a toast.”\n\nThe Major very hoarsely indicates his approval. Mr Carker, bending his head forward over the table in the direction of Cousin Feenix, smiles and nods a great many times.\n\n“A—in fact it’s not a—” Cousin Feenix beginning again, thus, comes to a dead stop.\n\n“Hear, hear!” says the Major, in a tone of conviction.\n\nMr Carker softly claps his hands, and bending forward over the table again, smiles and nods a great many more times than before, as if he were particularly struck by this last observation, and desired personally to express his sense of the good it has done.\n\n“It is,” says Cousin Feenix, “an occasion in fact, when the general usages of life may be a little departed from, without impropriety; and although I never was an orator in my life, and when I was in the House of Commons, and had the honour of seconding the address, was—in fact, was laid up for a fortnight with the consciousness of failure—”\n\nThe Major and Mr Carker are so much delighted by this fragment of personal history, that Cousin Feenix laughs, and addressing them individually, goes on to say:\n\n“And in point of fact, when I was devilish ill—still, you know, I feel that a duty devolves upon me. And when a duty devolves upon an Englishman, he is bound to get out of it, in my opinion, in the best way he can. Well! our family has had the gratification, today, of connecting itself, in the person of my lovely and accomplished relative, whom I now see—in point of fact, present—”\n\nHere there is general applause.\n\n“Present,” repeats Cousin Feenix, feeling that it is a neat point which will bear repetition,—“with one who—that is to say, with a man, at whom the finger of scorn can never—in fact, with my honourable friend Dombey, if he will allow me to call him so.”\n\nCousin Feenix bows to Mr Dombey; Mr Dombey solemnly returns the bow; everybody is more or less gratified and affected by this extraordinary, and perhaps unprecedented, appeal to the feelings.\n\n“I have not,” says Cousin Feenix, “enjoyed those opportunities which I could have desired, of cultivating the acquaintance of my friend Dombey, and studying those qualities which do equal honour to his head, and, in point of fact, to his heart; for it has been my misfortune to be, as we used to say in my time in the House of Commons, when it was not the custom to allude to the Lords, and when the order of parliamentary proceedings was perhaps better observed than it is now—to be in—in point of fact,” says Cousin Feenix, cherishing his joke, with great slyness, and finally bringing it out with a jerk, ‘“in another place!’”\n\nThe Major falls into convulsions, and is recovered with difficulty.\n\n“But I know sufficient of my friend Dombey,” resumes Cousin Feenix in a graver tone, as if he had suddenly become a sadder and wiser man, “to know that he is, in point of fact, what may be emphatically called a—a merchant—a British merchant—and a—and a man. And although I have been resident abroad, for some years (it would give me great pleasure to receive my friend Dombey, and everybody here, at Baden-Baden, and to have an opportunity of making ’em known to the Grand Duke), still I know enough, I flatter myself, of my lovely and accomplished relative, to know that she possesses every requisite to make a man happy, and that her marriage with my friend Dombey is one of inclination and affection on both sides.”\n\nMany smiles and nods from Mr Carker.\n\n“Therefore,” says Cousin Feenix, “I congratulate the family of which I am a member, on the acquisition of my friend Dombey. I congratulate my friend Dombey on his union with my lovely and accomplished relative who possesses every requisite to make a man happy; and I take the liberty of calling on you all, in point of fact, to congratulate both my friend Dombey and my lovely and accomplished relative, on the present occasion.”\n\nThe speech of Cousin Feenix is received with great applause, and Mr Dombey returns thanks on behalf of himself and Mrs Dombey. J. B. shortly afterwards proposes Mrs Skewton. The breakfast languishes when that is done, the violated hatchments are avenged, and Edith rises to assume her travelling dress.\n\nAll the servants in the meantime, have been breakfasting below. Champagne has grown too common among them to be mentioned, and roast fowls, raised pies, and lobster-salad, have become mere drugs. The very tall young man has recovered his spirits, and again alludes to the exciseman. His comrade’s eye begins to emulate his own, and he, too, stares at objects without taking cognizance thereof. There is a general redness in the faces of the ladies; in the face of Mrs Perch particularly, who is joyous and beaming, and lifted so far above the cares of life, that if she were asked just now to direct a wayfarer to Ball’s Pond, where her own cares lodge, she would have some difficulty in recalling the way. Mr Towlinson has proposed the happy pair; to which the silver-headed butler has responded neatly, and with emotion; for he half begins to think he is an old retainer of the family, and that he is bound to be affected by these changes. The whole party, and especially the ladies, are very frolicsome. Mr Dombey’s cook, who generally takes the lead in society, has said, it is impossible to settle down after this, and why not go, in a party, to the play? Everybody (Mrs Perch included) has agreed to this; even the Native, who is tigerish in his drink, and who alarms the ladies (Mrs Perch particularly) by the rolling of his eyes. One of the very tall young men has even proposed a ball after the play, and it presents itself to no one (Mrs Perch included) in the light of an impossibility. Words have arisen between the housemaid and Mr Towlinson; she, on the authority of an old saw, asserting marriages to be made in Heaven: he, affecting to trace the manufacture elsewhere; he, supposing that she says so, because she thinks of being married her own self: she, saying, Lord forbid, at any rate, that she should ever marry him. To calm these flying taunts, the silver-headed butler rises to propose the health of Mr Towlinson, whom to know is to esteem, and to esteem is to wish well settled in life with the object of his choice, wherever (here the silver-headed butler eyes the housemaid) she may be. Mr Towlinson returns thanks in a speech replete with feeling, of which the peroration turns on foreigners, regarding whom he says they may find favour, sometimes, with weak and inconstant intellects that can be led away by hair, but all he hopes, is, he may never hear of no foreigner never boning nothing out of no travelling chariot. The eye of Mr Towlinson is so severe and so expressive here, that the housemaid is turning hysterical, when she and all the rest, roused by the intelligence that the Bride is going away, hurry upstairs to witness her departure.\n\nThe chariot is at the door; the Bride is descending to the hall, where Mr Dombey waits for her. Florence is ready on the staircase to depart too; and Miss Nipper, who has held a middle state between the parlour and the kitchen, is prepared to accompany her. As Edith appears, Florence hastens towards her, to bid her farewell.\n\nIs Edith cold, that she should tremble! Is there anything unnatural or unwholesome in the touch of Florence, that the beautiful form recedes and contracts, as if it could not bear it! Is there so much hurry in this going away, that Edith, with a wave of her hand, sweeps on, and is gone!\n\nMrs Skewton, overpowered by her feelings as a mother, sinks on her sofa in the Cleopatra attitude, when the clatter of the chariot wheels is lost, and sheds several tears. The Major, coming with the rest of the company from table, endeavours to comfort her; but she will not be comforted on any terms, and so the Major takes his leave. Cousin Feenix takes his leave, and Mr Carker takes his leave. The guests all go away. Cleopatra, left alone, feels a little giddy from her strong emotion, and falls asleep.\n\nGiddiness prevails below stairs too. The very tall young man whose excitement came on so soon, appears to have his head glued to the table in the pantry, and cannot be detached from it. A violent revulsion has taken place in the spirits of Mrs Perch, who is low on account of Mr Perch, and tells cook that she fears he is not so much attached to his home, as he used to be, when they were only nine in family. Mr Towlinson has a singing in his ears and a large wheel going round and round inside his head. The housemaid wishes it wasn’t wicked to wish that one was dead.\n\nThere is a general delusion likewise, in these lower regions, on the subject of time; everybody conceiving that it ought to be, at the earliest, ten o’clock at night, whereas it is not yet three in the afternoon. A shadowy idea of wickedness committed, haunts every individual in the party; and each one secretly thinks the other a companion in guilt, whom it would be agreeable to avoid. No man or woman has the hardihood to hint at the projected visit to the play. Anyone reviving the notion of the ball, would be scouted as a malignant idiot.\n\nMrs Skewton sleeps upstairs, two hours afterwards, and naps are not yet over in the kitchen. The hatchments in the dining-room look down on crumbs, dirty plates, spillings of wine, half-thawed ice, stale discoloured heel-taps, scraps of lobster, drumsticks of fowls, and pensive jellies, gradually resolving themselves into a lukewarm gummy soup. The marriage is, by this time, almost as denuded of its show and garnish as the breakfast. Mr Dombey’s servants moralise so much about it, and are so repentant over their early tea, at home, that by eight o’clock or so, they settle down into confirmed seriousness; and Mr Perch, arriving at that time from the City, fresh and jocular, with a white waistcoat and a comic song, ready to spend the evening, and prepared for any amount of dissipation, is amazed to find himself coldly received, and Mrs Perch but poorly, and to have the pleasing duty of escorting that lady home by the next omnibus.\n\nNight closes in. Florence, having rambled through the handsome house, from room to room, seeks her own chamber, where the care of Edith has surrounded her with luxuries and comforts; and divesting herself of her handsome dress, puts on her old simple mourning for dear Paul, and sits down to read, with Diogenes winking and blinking on the ground beside her. But Florence cannot read tonight. The house seems strange and new, and there are loud echoes in it. There is a shadow on her heart: she knows not why or what: but it is heavy. Florence shuts her book, and gruff Diogenes, who takes that for a signal, puts his paws upon her lap, and rubs his ears against her caressing hands. But Florence cannot see him plainly, in a little time, for there is a mist between her eyes and him, and her dead brother and dead mother shine in it like angels. Walter, too, poor wandering shipwrecked boy, oh, where is he?\n\nThe Major don’t know; that’s for certain; and don’t care. The Major, having choked and slumbered, all the afternoon, has taken a late dinner at his club, and now sits over his pint of wine, driving a modest young man, with a fresh-coloured face, at the next table (who would give a handsome sum to be able to rise and go away, but cannot do it) to the verge of madness, by anecdotes of Bagstock, Sir, at Dombey’s wedding, and Old Joe’s devilish gentle manly friend, Lord Feenix. While Cousin Feenix, who ought to be at Long’s, and in bed, finds himself, instead, at a gaming-table, where his wilful legs have taken him, perhaps, in his own despite.\n\nNight, like a giant, fills the church, from pavement to roof, and holds dominion through the silent hours. Pale dawn again comes peeping through the windows: and, giving place to day, sees night withdraw into the vaults, and follows it, and drives it out, and hides among the dead. The timid mice again cower close together, when the great door clashes, and Mr Sownds and Mrs Miff treading the circle of their daily lives, unbroken as a marriage ring, come in. Again, the cocked hat and the mortified bonnet stand in the background at the marriage hour; and again this man taketh this woman, and this woman taketh this man, on the solemn terms:\n\n“To have and to hold, from this day forward, for better for worse, for richer for poorer, in sickness and in health, to love and to cherish, until death do them part.”\n\nThe very words that Mr Carker rides into town repeating, with his mouth stretched to the utmost, as he picks his dainty way.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII. The Wooden Midshipman goes to Pieces\n\nHonest Captain Cuttle, as the weeks flew over him in his fortified retreat, by no means abated any of his prudent provisions against surprise, because of the non-appearance of the enemy. The Captain argued that his present security was too profound and wonderful to endure much longer; he knew that when the wind stood in a fair quarter, the weathercock was seldom nailed there; and he was too well acquainted with the determined and dauntless character of Mrs MacStinger, to doubt that that heroic woman had devoted herself to the task of his discovery and capture. Trembling beneath the weight of these reasons, Captain Cuttle lived a very close and retired life; seldom stirring abroad until after dark; venturing even then only into the obscurest streets; never going forth at all on Sundays; and both within and without the walls of his retreat, avoiding bonnets, as if they were worn by raging lions.\n\nThe Captain never dreamed that in the event of his being pounced upon by Mrs MacStinger, in his walks, it would be possible to offer resistance. He felt that it could not be done. He saw himself, in his mind’s eye, put meekly in a hackney-coach, and carried off to his old lodgings. He foresaw that, once immured there, he was a lost man: his hat gone; Mrs MacStinger watchful of him day and night; reproaches heaped upon his head, before the infant family; himself the guilty object of suspicion and distrust; an ogre in the children’s eyes, and in their mother’s a detected traitor.\n\nA violent perspiration, and a lowness of spirits, always came over the Captain as this gloomy picture presented itself to his imagination. It generally did so previous to his stealing out of doors at night for air and exercise. Sensible of the risk he ran, the Captain took leave of Rob, at those times, with the solemnity which became a man who might never return: exhorting him, in the event of his (the Captain’s) being lost sight of, for a time, to tread in the paths of virtue, and keep the brazen instruments well polished.\n\nBut not to throw away a chance; and to secure to himself a means, in case of the worst, of holding communication with the external world; Captain Cuttle soon conceived the happy idea of teaching Rob the Grinder some secret signal, by which that adherent might make his presence and fidelity known to his commander, in the hour of adversity. After much cogitation, the Captain decided in favour of instructing him to whistle the marine melody, “Oh cheerily, cheerily!” and Rob the Grinder attaining a point as near perfection in that accomplishment as a landsman could hope to reach, the Captain impressed these mysterious instructions on his mind:\n\n“Now, my lad, stand by! If ever I’m took—”\n\n“Took, Captain!” interposed Rob, with his round eyes wide open.\n\n“Ah!” said Captain Cuttle darkly, “if ever I goes away, meaning to come back to supper, and don’t come within hail again, twenty-four hours arter my loss, go you to Brig Place and whistle that “ere tune near my old moorings—not as if you was a meaning of it, you understand, but as if you’d drifted there, promiscuous. If I answer in that tune, you sheer off, my lad, and come back four-and-twenty hours arterwards; if I answer in another tune, do you stand off and on, and wait till I throw out further signals. Do you understand them orders, now?”\n\n“What am I to stand off and on of, Captain?” inquired Rob. “The horse-road?”\n\n“Here’s a smart lad for you!” cried the Captain eyeing him sternly, “as don’t know his own native alphabet! Go away a bit and come back again alternate—d’ye understand that?”\n\n“Yes, Captain,” said Rob.\n\n“Very good my lad, then,” said the Captain, relenting. “Do it!”\n\nThat he might do it the better, Captain Cuttle sometimes condescended, of an evening after the shop was shut, to rehearse this scene: retiring into the parlour for the purpose, as into the lodgings of a supposititious MacStinger, and carefully observing the behaviour of his ally, from the hole of espial he had cut in the wall. Rob the Grinder discharged himself of his duty with so much exactness and judgment, when thus put to the proof, that the Captain presented him, at divers times, with seven sixpences, in token of satisfaction; and gradually felt stealing over his spirit the resignation of a man who had made provision for the worst, and taken every reasonable precaution against an unrelenting fate.\n\nNevertheless, the Captain did not tempt ill-fortune, by being a whit more venturesome than before. Though he considered it a point of good breeding in himself, as a general friend of the family, to attend Mr Dombey’s wedding (of which he had heard from Mr Perch), and to show that gentleman a pleasant and approving countenance from the gallery, he had repaired to the church in a hackney cabriolet with both windows up; and might have scrupled even to make that venture, in his dread of Mrs MacStinger, but that the lady’s attendance on the ministry of the Reverend Melchisedech rendered it peculiarly unlikely that she would be found in communion with the Establishment.\n\nThe Captain got safe home again, and fell into the ordinary routine of his new life, without encountering any more direct alarm from the enemy, than was suggested to him by the daily bonnets in the street. But other subjects began to lay heavy on the Captain’s mind. Walter’s ship was still unheard of. No news came of old Sol Gills. Florence did not even know of the old man’s disappearance, and Captain Cuttle had not the heart to tell her. Indeed the Captain, as his own hopes of the generous, handsome, gallant-hearted youth, whom he had loved, according to his rough manner, from a child, began to fade, and faded more and more from day to day, shrunk with instinctive pain from the thought of exchanging a word with Florence. If he had had good news to carry to her, the honest Captain would have braved the newly decorated house and splendid furniture—though these, connected with the lady he had seen at church, were awful to him—and made his way into her presence. With a dark horizon gathering around their common hopes, however, that darkened every hour, the Captain almost felt as if he were a new misfortune and affliction to her; and was scarcely less afraid of a visit from Florence, than from Mrs MacStinger herself.\n\nIt was a chill dark autumn evening, and Captain Cuttle had ordered a fire to be kindled in the little back parlour, now more than ever like the cabin of a ship. The rain fell fast, and the wind blew hard; and straying out on the house-top by that stormy bedroom of his old friend, to take an observation of the weather, the Captain’s heart died within him, when he saw how wild and desolate it was. Not that he associated the weather of that time with poor Walter’s destiny, or doubted that if Providence had doomed him to be lost and shipwrecked, it was over, long ago; but that beneath an outward influence, quite distinct from the subject-matter of his thoughts, the Captain’s spirits sank, and his hopes turned pale, as those of wiser men had often done before him, and will often do again.\n\nCaptain Cuttle, addressing his face to the sharp wind and slanting rain, looked up at the heavy scud that was flying fast over the wilderness of house-tops, and looked for something cheery there in vain. The prospect near at hand was no better. In sundry tea-chests and other rough boxes at his feet, the pigeons of Rob the Grinder were cooing like so many dismal breezes getting up. A crazy weathercock of a midshipman, with a telescope at his eye, once visible from the street, but long bricked out, creaked and complained upon his rusty pivot as the shrill blast spun him round and round, and sported with him cruelly. Upon the Captain’s coarse blue vest the cold raindrops started like steel beads; and he could hardly maintain himself aslant against the stiff Nor’-Wester that came pressing against him, importunate to topple him over the parapet, and throw him on the pavement below. If there were any Hope alive that evening, the Captain thought, as he held his hat on, it certainly kept house, and wasn’t out of doors; so the Captain, shaking his head in a despondent manner, went in to look for it.\n\nCaptain Cuttle descended slowly to the little back parlour, and, seated in his accustomed chair, looked for it in the fire; but it was not there, though the fire was bright. He took out his tobacco-box and pipe, and composing himself to smoke, looked for it in the red glow from the bowl, and in the wreaths of vapour that curled upward from his lips; but there was not so much as an atom of the rust of Hope’s anchor in either. He tried a glass of grog; but melancholy truth was at the bottom of that well, and he couldn’t finish it. He made a turn or two in the shop, and looked for Hope among the instruments; but they obstinately worked out reckonings for the missing ship, in spite of any opposition he could offer, that ended at the bottom of the lone sea.\n\nThe wind still rushing, and the rain still pattering, against the closed shutters, the Captain brought to before the wooden Midshipman upon the counter, and thought, as he dried the little officer’s uniform with his sleeve, how many years the Midshipman had seen, during which few changes—hardly any—had transpired among his ship’s company; how the changes had come all together, one day, as it might be; and of what a sweeping kind they were. Here was the little society of the back parlour broken up, and scattered far and wide. Here was no audience for Lovely Peg, even if there had been anybody to sing it, which there was not; for the Captain was as morally certain that nobody but he could execute that ballad, as he was that he had not the spirit, under existing circumstances, to attempt it. There was no bright face of “Wal”r” in the house;—here the Captain transferred his sleeve for a moment from the Midshipman’s uniform to his own cheek;—the familiar wig and buttons of Sol Gills were a vision of the past; Richard Whittington was knocked on the head; and every plan and project in connexion with the Midshipman, lay drifting, without mast or rudder, on the waste of waters.\n\nAs the Captain, with a dejected face, stood revolving these thoughts, and polishing the Midshipman, partly in the tenderness of old acquaintance, and partly in the absence of his mind, a knocking at the shop-door communicated a frightful start to the frame of Rob the Grinder, seated on the counter, whose large eyes had been intently fixed on the Captain’s face, and who had been debating within himself, for the five hundredth time, whether the Captain could have done a murder, that he had such an evil conscience, and was always running away.\n\n“What’s that?” said Captain Cuttle, softly.\n\n“Somebody’s knuckles, Captain,” answered Rob the Grinder.\n\nThe Captain, with an abashed and guilty air, immediately walked on tiptoe to the little parlour and locked himself in. Rob, opening the door, would have parleyed with the visitor on the threshold if the visitor had come in female guise; but the figure being of the male sex, and Rob’s orders only applying to women, Rob held the door open and allowed it to enter: which it did very quickly, glad to get out of the driving rain.\n\n“A job for Burgess and Co. at any rate,” said the visitor, looking over his shoulder compassionately at his own legs, which were very wet and covered with splashes. “Oh, how-de-do, Mr Gills?”\n\nThe salutation was addressed to the Captain, now emerging from the back parlour with a most transparent and utterly futile affectation of coming out by accidence.\n\n“Thankee,” the gentleman went on to say in the same breath; “I’m very well indeed, myself, I’m much obliged to you. My name is Toots,—Mister Toots.”\n\nThe Captain remembered to have seen this young gentleman at the wedding, and made him a bow. Mr Toots replied with a chuckle; and being embarrassed, as he generally was, breathed hard, shook hands with the Captain for a long time, and then falling on Rob the Grinder, in the absence of any other resource, shook hands with him in a most affectionate and cordial manner.\n\n“I say! I should like to speak a word to you, Mr Gills, if you please,” said Toots at length, with surprising presence of mind. “I say! Miss D.O.M. you know!”\n\nThe Captain, with responsive gravity and mystery, immediately waved his hook towards the little parlour, whither Mr Toots followed him.\n\n“Oh! I beg your pardon though,” said Mr Toots, looking up in the Captain’s face as he sat down in a chair by the fire, which the Captain placed for him; “you don’t happen to know the Chicken at all; do you, Mr Gills?”\n\n“The Chicken?” said the Captain.\n\n“The Game Chicken,” said Mr Toots.\n\nThe Captain shaking his head, Mr Toots explained that the man alluded to was the celebrated public character who had covered himself and his country with glory in his contest with the Nobby Shropshire One; but this piece of information did not appear to enlighten the Captain very much.\n\n“Because he’s outside: that’s all,” said Mr Toots. “But it’s of no consequence; he won’t get very wet, perhaps.”\n\n“I can pass the word for him in a moment,” said the Captain.\n\n“Well, if you would have the goodness to let him sit in the shop with your young man,” chuckled Mr Toots, “I should be glad; because, you know, he’s easily offended, and the damp’s rather bad for his stamina. I’ll call him in, Mr Gills.”\n\nWith that, Mr Toots repairing to the shop-door, sent a peculiar whistle into the night, which produced a stoical gentleman in a shaggy white great-coat and a flat-brimmed hat, with very short hair, a broken nose, and a considerable tract of bare and sterile country behind each ear.\n\n“Sit down, Chicken,” said Mr Toots.\n\nThe compliant Chicken spat out some small pieces of straw on which he was regaling himself, and took in a fresh supply from a reserve he carried in his hand.\n\n“There ain’t no drain of nothing short handy, is there?” said the Chicken, generally. “This here sluicing night is hard lines to a man as lives on his condition.”\n\nCaptain Cuttle proffered a glass of rum, which the Chicken, throwing back his head, emptied into himself, as into a cask, after proposing the brief sentiment, “Towards us!” Mr Toots and the Captain returning then to the parlour, and taking their seats before the fire, Mr Toots began:\n\n“Mr Gills—”\n\n“Awast!” said the Captain. “My name’s Cuttle.”\n\nMr Toots looked greatly disconcerted, while the Captain proceeded gravely.\n\n“Cap’en Cuttle is my name, and England is my nation, this here is my dwelling-place, and blessed be creation—Job,” said the Captain, as an index to his authority.\n\n“Oh! I couldn’t see Mr Gills, could I?” said Mr Toots; “because—”\n\n“If you could see Sol Gills, young gen’l’m’n,” said the Captain, impressively, and laying his heavy hand on Mr Toots’s knee, “old Sol, mind you—with your own eyes—as you sit there—you’d be welcomer to me, than a wind astern, to a ship becalmed. But you can’t see Sol Gills. And why can’t you see Sol Gills?” said the Captain, apprised by the face of Mr Toots that he was making a profound impression on that gentleman’s mind. “Because he’s inwisible.”\n\nMr Toots in his agitation was going to reply that it was of no consequence at all. But he corrected himself, and said, “Lor bless me!”\n\n“That there man,” said the Captain, “has left me in charge here by a piece of writing, but though he was a’most as good as my sworn brother, I know no more where he’s gone, or why he’s gone; if so be to seek his nevy, or if so be along of being not quite settled in his mind; than you do. One morning at daybreak, he went over the side,” said the Captain, “without a splash, without a ripple I have looked for that man high and low, and never set eyes, nor ears, nor nothing else, upon him from that hour.”\n\n“But, good Gracious, Miss Dombey don’t know—” Mr Toots began.\n\n“Why, I ask you, as a feeling heart,” said the Captain, dropping his voice, “why should she know? why should she be made to know, until such time as there wam’t any help for it? She took to old Sol Gills, did that sweet creetur, with a kindness, with a affability, with a—what’s the good of saying so? you know her.”\n\n“I should hope so,” chuckled Mr Toots, with a conscious blush that suffused his whole countenance.\n\n“And you come here from her?” said the Captain.\n\n“I should think so,” chuckled Mr Toots.\n\n“Then all I need observe, is,” said the Captain, “that you know a angel, and are chartered a angel.”\n\nMr Toots instantly seized the Captain’s hand, and requested the favour of his friendship.\n\n“Upon my word and honour,” said Mr Toots, earnestly, “I should be very much obliged to you if you’d improve my acquaintance. I should like to know you, Captain, very much. I really am in want of a friend, I am. Little Dombey was my friend at old Blimber’s, and would have been now, if he’d have lived. The Chicken,” said Mr Toots, in a forlorn whisper, “is very well—admirable in his way—the sharpest man perhaps in the world; there’s not a move he isn’t up to, everybody says so—but I don’t know—he’s not everything. So she is an angel, Captain. If there is an angel anywhere, it’s Miss Dombey. That’s what I’ve always said. Really though, you know,” said Mr Toots, “I should be very much obliged to you if you’d cultivate my acquaintance.”\n\nCaptain Cuttle received this proposal in a polite manner, but still without committing himself to its acceptance; merely observing, “Ay, ay, my lad. We shall see, we shall see;” and reminding Mr Toots of his immediate mission, by inquiring to what he was indebted for the honour of that visit.\n\n“Why the fact is,” replied Mr Toots, “that it’s the young woman I come from. Not Miss Dombey—Susan, you know.\n\nThe Captain nodded his head once, with a grave expression of face indicative of his regarding that young woman with serious respect.\n\n“And I’ll tell you how it happens,” said Mr Toots. “You know, I go and call sometimes, on Miss Dombey. I don’t go there on purpose, you know, but I happen to be in the neighbourhood very often; and when I find myself there, why—why I call.”\n\n“Nat’rally,” observed the Captain.\n\n“Yes,” said Mr Toots. “I called this afternoon. Upon my word and honour, I don’t think it’s possible to form an idea of the angel Miss Dombey was this afternoon.”\n\nThe Captain answered with a jerk of his head, implying that it might not be easy to some people, but was quite so to him.\n\n“As I was coming out,” said Mr Toots, “the young woman, in the most unexpected manner, took me into the pantry.”\n\nThe Captain seemed, for the moment, to object to this proceeding; and leaning back in his chair, looked at Mr Toots with a distrustful, if not threatening visage.\n\n“Where she brought out,” said Mr Toots, “this newspaper. She told me that she had kept it from Miss Dombey all day, on account of something that was in it, about somebody that she and Dombey used to know; and then she read the passage to me. Very well. Then she said—wait a minute; what was it she said, though!”\n\nMr Toots, endeavouring to concentrate his mental powers on this question, unintentionally fixed the Captain’s eye, and was so much discomposed by its stern expression, that his difficulty in resuming the thread of his subject was enhanced to a painful extent.\n\n“Oh!” said Mr Toots after long consideration. “Oh, ah! Yes! She said that she hoped there was a bare possibility that it mightn’t be true; and that as she couldn’t very well come out herself, without surprising Miss Dombey, would I go down to Mr Solomon Gills the Instrument-maker’s in this street, who was the party’s Uncle, and ask whether he believed it was true, or had heard anything else in the City. She said, if he couldn’t speak to me, no doubt Captain Cuttle could. By the bye!” said Mr Toots, as the discovery flashed upon him, “you, you know!”\n\nThe Captain glanced at the newspaper in Mr Toots’s hand, and breathed short and hurriedly.\n\n“Well,” pursued Mr Toots, “the reason why I’m rather late is, because I went up as far as Finchley first, to get some uncommonly fine chickweed that grows there, for Miss Dombey’s bird. But I came on here, directly afterwards. You’ve seen the paper, I suppose?”\n\nThe Captain, who had become cautious of reading the news, lest he should find himself advertised at full length by Mrs MacStinger, shook his head.\n\n“Shall I read the passage to you?” inquired Mr Toots.\n\nThe Captain making a sign in the affirmative, Mr Toots read as follows, from the Shipping Intelligence:\n\n“‘Southampton. The barque Defiance, Henry James, Commander, arrived in this port today, with a cargo of sugar, coffee, and rum, reports that being becalmed on the sixth day of her passage home from Jamaica, in’—in such and such a latitude, you know,” said Mr Toots, after making a feeble dash at the figures, and tumbling over them.\n\n“Ay!” cried the Captain, striking his clenched hand on the table. “Heave ahead, my lad!”\n\n“—latitude,” repeated Mr Toots, with a startled glance at the Captain, “and longitude so-and-so,—‘the look-out observed, half an hour before sunset, some fragments of a wreck, drifting at about the distance of a mile. The weather being clear, and the barque making no way, a boat was hoisted out, with orders to inspect the same, when they were found to consist of sundry large spars, and a part of the main rigging of an English brig, of about five hundred tons burden, together with a portion of the stem on which the words and letters “Son and H-” were yet plainly legible. No vestige of any dead body was to be seen upon the floating fragments. Log of the Defiance states, that a breeze springing up in the night, the wreck was seen no more. There can be no doubt that all surmises as to the fate of the missing vessel, the Son and Heir, port of London, bound for Barbados, are now set at rest for ever; that she broke up in the last hurricane; and that every soul on board perished.’”\n\nCaptain Cuttle, like all mankind, little knew how much hope had survived within him under discouragement, until he felt its death-shock. During the reading of the paragraph, and for a minute or two afterwards, he sat with his gaze fixed on the modest Mr Toots, like a man entranced; then, suddenly rising, and putting on his glazed hat, which, in his visitor’s honour, he had laid upon the table, the Captain turned his back, and bent his head down on the little chimneypiece.\n\n“Oh” upon my word and honour,” cried Mr Toots, whose tender heart was moved by the Captain’s unexpected distress, “this is a most wretched sort of affair this world is! Somebody’s always dying, or going and doing something uncomfortable in it. I’m sure I never should have looked forward so much, to coming into my property, if I had known this. I never saw such a world. It’s a great deal worse than Blimber’s.”\n\nCaptain Cuttle, without altering his position, signed to Mr Toots not to mind him; and presently turned round, with his glazed hat thrust back upon his ears, and his hand composing and smoothing his brown face.\n\n“Wal”r, my dear lad,” said the Captain, “farewell! Wal”r my child, my boy, and man, I loved you! He warn’t my flesh and blood,” said the Captain, looking at the fire—“I ain’t got none—but something of what a father feels when he loses a son, I feel in losing Wal”r. For why?” said the Captain. “Because it ain’t one loss, but a round dozen. Where’s that there young school-boy with the rosy face and curly hair, that used to be as merry in this here parlour, come round every week, as a piece of music? Gone down with Wal”r. Where’s that there fresh lad, that nothing couldn’t tire nor put out, and that sparkled up and blushed so, when we joked him about Heart’s Delight, that he was beautiful to look at? Gone down with Wal”r. Where’s that there man’s spirit, all afire, that wouldn’t see the old man hove down for a minute, and cared nothing for itself? Gone down with Wal”r. It ain’t one Wal”r. There was a dozen Wal”rs that I know’d and loved, all holding round his neck when he went down, and they’re a-holding round mine now!”\n\nMr Toots sat silent: folding and refolding the newspaper as small as possible upon his knee.\n\n“And Sol Gills,” said the Captain, gazing at the fire, “poor nevyless old Sol, where are you got to! you was left in charge of me; his last words was, ‘Take care of my Uncle!’ What came over you, Sol, when you went and gave the go-bye to Ned Cuttle; and what am I to put in my accounts that he’s a looking down upon, respecting you! Sol Gills, Sol Gills!” said the Captain, shaking his head slowly, “catch sight of that there newspaper, away from home, with no one as know’d Wal”r by, to say a word; and broadside to you broach, and down you pitch, head foremost!”\n\nDrawing a heavy sigh, the Captain turned to Mr Toots, and roused himself to a sustained consciousness of that gentleman’s presence.\n\n“My lad,” said the Captain, “you must tell the young woman honestly that this here fatal news is too correct. They don’t romance, you see, on such pints. It’s entered on the ship’s log, and that’s the truest book as a man can write. To-morrow morning,” said the Captain, “I’ll step out and make inquiries; but they’ll lead to no good. They can’t do it. If you’ll give me a look-in in the forenoon, you shall know what I have heerd; but tell the young woman from Cap’en Cuttle, that it’s over. Over!” And the Captain, hooking off his glazed hat, pulled his handkerchief out of the crown, wiped his grizzled head despairingly, and tossed the handkerchief in again, with the indifference of deep dejection.\n\n“Oh! I assure you,” said Mr Toots, “really I am dreadfully sorry. Upon my word I am, though I wasn’t acquainted with the party. Do you think Miss Dombey will be very much affected, Captain Gills—I mean Mr Cuttle?”\n\n“Why, Lord love you,” returned the Captain, with something of compassion for Mr Toots’s innocence. “When she warn’t no higher than that, they were as fond of one another as two young doves.”\n\n“Were they though!” said Mr Toots, with a considerably lengthened face.\n\n“They were made for one another,” said the Captain, mournfully; “but what signifies that now!”\n\n“Upon my word and honour,” cried Mr Toots, blurting out his words through a singular combination of awkward chuckles and emotion, “I’m even more sorry than I was before. You know, Captain Gills, I—I positively adore Miss Dombey;—I—I am perfectly sore with loving her;” the burst with which this confession forced itself out of the unhappy Mr Toots, bespoke the vehemence of his feelings; “but what would be the good of my regarding her in this manner, if I wasn’t truly sorry for her feeling pain, whatever was the cause of it. Mine ain’t a selfish affection, you know,” said Mr Toots, in the confidence engendered by his having been a witness of the Captain’s tenderness. “It’s the sort of thing with me, Captain Gills, that if I could be run over—or—or trampled upon—or—or thrown off a very high place-or anything of that sort—for Miss Dombey’s sake, it would be the most delightful thing that could happen to me.”\n\nAll this, Mr Toots said in a suppressed voice, to prevent its reaching the jealous ears of the Chicken, who objected to the softer emotions; which effort of restraint, coupled with the intensity of his feelings, made him red to the tips of his ears, and caused him to present such an affecting spectacle of disinterested love to the eyes of Captain Cuttle, that the good Captain patted him consolingly on the back, and bade him cheer up.\n\n“Thankee, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, “it’s kind of you, in the midst of your own troubles, to say so. I’m very much obliged to you. As I said before, I really want a friend, and should be glad to have your acquaintance. Although I am very well off,” said Mr Toots, with energy, “you can’t think what a miserable Beast I am. The hollow crowd, you know, when they see me with the Chicken, and characters of distinction like that, suppose me to be happy; but I’m wretched. I suffer for Miss Dombey, Captain Gills. I can’t get through my meals; I have no pleasure in my tailor; I often cry when I’m alone. I assure you it’ll be a satisfaction to me to come back to-morrow, or to come back fifty times.”\n\nMr Toots, with these words, shook the Captain’s hand; and disguising such traces of his agitation as could be disguised on so short a notice, before the Chicken’s penetrating glance, rejoined that eminent gentleman in the shop. The Chicken, who was apt to be jealous of his ascendancy, eyed Captain Cuttle with anything but favour as he took leave of Mr Toots, but followed his patron without being otherwise demonstrative of his ill-will: leaving the Captain oppressed with sorrow; and Rob the Grinder elevated with joy, on account of having had the honour of staring for nearly half an hour at the conqueror of the Nobby Shropshire One.\n\nLong after Rob was fast asleep in his bed under the counter, the Captain sat looking at the fire; and long after there was no fire to look at, the Captain sat gazing on the rusty bars, with unavailing thoughts of Walter and old Sol crowding through his mind. Retirement to the stormy chamber at the top of the house brought no rest with it; and the Captain rose up in the morning, sorrowful and unrefreshed.\n\nAs soon as the City offices were opened, the Captain issued forth to the counting-house of Dombey and Son. But there was no opening of the Midshipman’s windows that morning. Rob the Grinder, by the Captain’s orders, left the shutters closed, and the house was as a house of death.\n\nIt chanced that Mr Carker was entering the office, as Captain Cuttle arrived at the door. Receiving the Manager’s benison gravely and silently, Captain Cuttle made bold to accompany him into his own room.\n\n“Well, Captain Cuttle,” said Mr Carker, taking up his usual position before the fireplace, and keeping on his hat, “this is a bad business.”\n\n“You have received the news as was in print yesterday, Sir?” said the Captain.\n\n“Yes,” said Mr Carker, “we have received it! It was accurately stated. The underwriters suffer a considerable loss. We are very sorry. No help! Such is life!”\n\nMr Carker pared his nails delicately with a penknife, and smiled at the Captain, who was standing by the door looking at him.\n\n“I excessively regret poor Gay,” said Carker, “and the crew. I understand there were some of our very best men among ’em. It always happens so. Many men with families too. A comfort to reflect that poor Gay had no family, Captain Cuttle!”\n\nThe Captain stood rubbing his chin, and looking at the Manager. The Manager glanced at the unopened letters lying on his desk, and took up the newspaper.\n\n“Is there anything I can do for you, Captain Cuttle?” he asked looking off it, with a smiling and expressive glance at the door.\n\n“I wish you could set my mind at rest, Sir, on something it’s uneasy about,” returned the Captain.\n\n“Ay!” exclaimed the Manager, “what’s that? Come, Captain Cuttle, I must trouble you to be quick, if you please. I am much engaged.”\n\n“Lookee here, Sir,” said the Captain, advancing a step. “Afore my friend Wal”r went on this here disastrous voyage—”\n\n“Come, come, Captain Cuttle,” interposed the smiling Manager, “don’t talk about disastrous voyages in that way. We have nothing to do with disastrous voyages here, my good fellow. You must have begun very early on your day’s allowance, Captain, if you don’t remember that there are hazards in all voyages, whether by sea or land. You are not made uneasy by the supposition that young what’s-his-name was lost in bad weather that was got up against him in these offices—are you? Fie, Captain! Sleep, and soda-water, are the best cures for such uneasiness as that.”\n\n“My lad,” returned the Captain, slowly—“you are a’most a lad to me, and so I don’t ask your pardon for that slip of a word,—if you find any pleasure in this here sport, you ain’t the gentleman I took you for. And if you ain’t the gentleman I took you for, may be my mind has call to be uneasy. Now this is what it is, Mr Carker.—Afore that poor lad went away, according to orders, he told me that he warn’t a going away for his own good, or for promotion, he know’d. It was my belief that he was wrong, and I told him so, and I come here, your head governor being absent, to ask a question or two of you in a civil way, for my own satisfaction. Them questions you answered—free. Now it’ll ease my mind to know, when all is over, as it is, and when what can’t be cured must be endoored—for which, as a scholar, you’ll overhaul the book it’s in, and thereof make a note—to know once more, in a word, that I warn’t mistaken; that I warn’t back’ard in my duty when I didn’t tell the old man what Wal”r told me; and that the wind was truly in his sail, when he highsted of it for Barbados Harbour. Mr Carker,” said the Captain, in the goodness of his nature, “when I was here last, we was very pleasant together. If I ain’t been altogether so pleasant myself this morning, on account of this poor lad, and if I have chafed again any observation of yours that I might have fended off, my name is Ed’ard Cuttle, and I ask your pardon.”\n\n“Captain Cuttle,” returned the Manager, with all possible politeness, “I must ask you to do me a favour.”\n\n“And what is it, Sir?” inquired the Captain.\n\n“To have the goodness to walk off, if you please,” rejoined the Manager, stretching forth his arm, “and to carry your jargon somewhere else.”\n\nEvery knob in the Captain’s face turned white with astonishment and indignation; even the red rim on his forehead faded, like a rainbow among the gathering clouds.\n\n“I tell you what, Captain Cuttle,” said the Manager, shaking his forefinger at him, and showing him all his teeth, but still amiably smiling, “I was much too lenient with you when you came here before. You belong to an artful and audacious set of people. In my desire to save young what’s-his-name from being kicked out of this place, neck and crop, my good Captain, I tolerated you; but for once, and only once. Now, go, my friend!”\n\nThe Captain was absolutely rooted to the ground, and speechless—\n\n“Go,” said the good-humoured Manager, gathering up his skirts, and standing astride upon the hearth-rug, “like a sensible fellow, and let us have no turning out, or any such violent measures. If Mr Dombey were here, Captain, you might be obliged to leave in a more ignominious manner, possibly. I merely say, Go!”\n\nThe Captain, laying his ponderous hand upon his chest, to assist himself in fetching a deep breath, looked at Mr Carker from head to foot, and looked round the little room, as if he did not clearly understand where he was, or in what company.\n\n“You are deep, Captain Cuttle,” pursued Carker, with the easy and vivacious frankness of a man of the world who knew the world too well to be ruffled by any discovery of misdoing, when it did not immediately concern himself, “but you are not quite out of soundings, either—neither you nor your absent friend, Captain. What have you done with your absent friend, hey?”\n\nAgain the Captain laid his hand upon his chest. After drawing another deep breath, he conjured himself to “stand by!” But in a whisper.\n\n“You hatch nice little plots, and hold nice little councils, and make nice little appointments, and receive nice little visitors, too, Captain, hey?” said Carker, bending his brows upon him, without showing his teeth any the less: “but it’s a bold measure to come here afterwards. Not like your discretion! You conspirators, and hiders, and runners-away, should know better than that. Will you oblige me by going?”\n\n“My lad,” gasped the Captain, in a choked and trembling voice, and with a curious action going on in the ponderous fist; “there’s a many words I could wish to say to you, but I don’t rightly know where they’re stowed just at present. My young friend, Wal”r, was drownded only last night, according to my reckoning, and it puts me out, you see. But you and me will come alongside o’one another again, my lad,” said the Captain, holding up his hook, “if we live.”\n\n“It will be anything but shrewd in you, my good fellow, if we do,” returned the Manager, with the same frankness; “for you may rely, I give you fair warning, upon my detecting and exposing you. I don’t pretend to be a more moral man than my neighbours, my good Captain; but the confidence of this House, or of any member of this House, is not to be abused and undermined while I have eyes and ears. Good day!” said Mr Carker, nodding his head.\n\nCaptain Cuttle, looking at him steadily (Mr Carker looked full as steadily at the Captain), went out of the office and left him standing astride before the fire, as calm and pleasant as if there were no more spots upon his soul than on his pure white linen, and his smooth sleek skin.\n\nThe Captain glanced, in passing through the outer counting-house, at the desk where he knew poor Walter had been used to sit, now occupied by another young boy, with a face almost as fresh and hopeful as his on the day when they tapped the famous last bottle but one of the old Madeira, in the little back parlour. The nation of ideas, thus awakened, did the Captain a great deal of good; it softened him in the very height of his anger, and brought the tears into his eyes.\n\nArrived at the wooden Midshipman’s again, and sitting down in a corner of the dark shop, the Captain’s indignation, strong as it was, could make no head against his grief. Passion seemed not only to do wrong and violence to the memory of the dead, but to be infected by death, and to droop and decline beside it. All the living knaves and liars in the world, were nothing to the honesty and truth of one dead friend.\n\nThe only thing the honest Captain made out clearly, in this state of mind, besides the loss of Walter, was, that with him almost the whole world of Captain Cuttle had been drowned. If he reproached himself sometimes, and keenly too, for having ever connived at Walter’s innocent deceit, he thought at least as often of the Mr Carker whom no sea could ever render up; and the Mr Dombey, whom he now began to perceive was as far beyond human recall; and the “Heart’s Delight,” with whom he must never foregather again; and the Lovely Peg, that teak-built and trim ballad, that had gone ashore upon a rock, and split into mere planks and beams of rhyme. The Captain sat in the dark shop, thinking of these things, to the entire exclusion of his own injury; and looking with as sad an eye upon the ground, as if in contemplation of their actual fragments, as they floated past.\n\nBut the Captain was not unmindful, for all that, of such decent and rest observances in memory of poor Walter, as he felt within his power. Rousing himself, and rousing Rob the Grinder (who in the unnatural twilight was fast asleep), the Captain sallied forth with his attendant at his heels, and the door-key in his pocket, and repairing to one of those convenient slop-selling establishments of which there is abundant choice at the eastern end of London, purchased on the spot two suits of mourning—one for Rob the Grinder, which was immensely too small, and one for himself, which was immensely too large. He also provided Rob with a species of hat, greatly to be admired for its symmetry and usefulness, as well as for a happy blending of the mariner with the coal-heaver; which is usually termed a sou’wester; and which was something of a novelty in connexion with the instrument business. In their several garments, which the vendor declared to be such a miracle in point of fit as nothing but a rare combination of fortuitous circumstances ever brought about, and the fashion of which was unparalleled within the memory of the oldest inhabitant, the Captain and Grinder immediately arrayed themselves: presenting a spectacle fraught with wonder to all who beheld it.\n\nIn this altered form, the Captain received Mr Toots. “I’m took aback, my lad, at present,” said the Captain, “and will only confirm that there ill news. Tell the young woman to break it gentle to the young lady, and for neither of ’em never to think of me no more—“special, mind you, that is—though I will think of them, when night comes on a hurricane and seas is mountains rowling, for which overhaul your Doctor Watts, brother, and when found make a note on.”\n\nThe Captain reserved, until some fitter time, the consideration of Mr Toots’s offer of friendship, and thus dismissed him. Captain Cuttle’s spirits were so low, in truth, that he half determined, that day, to take no further precautions against surprise from Mrs MacStinger, but to abandon himself recklessly to chance, and be indifferent to what might happen. As evening came on, he fell into a better frame of mind, however; and spoke much of Walter to Rob the Grinder, whose attention and fidelity he likewise incidentally commended. Rob did not blush to hear the Captain earnest in his praises, but sat staring at him, and affecting to snivel with sympathy, and making a feint of being virtuous, and treasuring up every word he said (like a young spy as he was) with very promising deceit.\n\nWhen Rob had turned in, and was fast asleep, the Captain trimmed the candle, put on his spectacles—he had felt it appropriate to take to spectacles on entering into the Instrument Trade, though his eyes were like a hawk’s—and opened the prayer-book at the Burial Service. And reading softly to himself, in the little back parlour, and stopping now and then to wipe his eyes, the Captain, in a true and simple spirit, committed Walter’s body to the deep.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII. Contrasts\n\nTurn we our eyes upon two homes; not lying side by side, but wide apart, though both within easy range and reach of the great city of London.\n\nThe first is situated in the green and wooded country near Norwood. It is not a mansion; it is of no pretensions as to size; but it is beautifully arranged, and tastefully kept. The lawn, the soft, smooth slope, the flower-garden, the clumps of trees where graceful forms of ash and willow are not wanting, the conservatory, the rustic verandah with sweet-smelling creeping plants entwined about the pillars, the simple exterior of the house, the well-ordered offices, though all upon the diminutive scale proper to a mere cottage, bespeak an amount of elegant comfort within, that might serve for a palace. This indication is not without warrant; for, within, it is a house of refinement and luxury. Rich colours, excellently blended, meet the eye at every turn; in the furniture—its proportions admirably devised to suit the shapes and sizes of the small rooms; on the walls; upon the floors; tingeing and subduing the light that comes in through the odd glass doors and windows here and there. There are a few choice prints and pictures too; in quaint nooks and recesses there is no want of books; and there are games of skill and chance set forth on tables—fantastic chessmen, dice, backgammon, cards, and billiards.\n\nAnd yet amidst this opulence of comfort, there is something in the general air that is not well. Is it that the carpets and the cushions are too soft and noiseless, so that those who move or repose among them seem to act by stealth? Is it that the prints and pictures do not commemorate great thoughts or deeds, or render nature in the Poetry of landscape, hall, or hut, but are of one voluptuous cast—mere shows of form and colour—and no more? Is it that the books have all their gold outside, and that the titles of the greater part qualify them to be companions of the prints and pictures? Is it that the completeness and the beauty of the place are here and there belied by an affectation of humility, in some unimportant and inexpensive regard, which is as false as the face of the too truly painted portrait hanging yonder, or its original at breakfast in his easy chair below it? Or is it that, with the daily breath of that original and master of all here, there issues forth some subtle portion of himself, which gives a vague expression of himself to everything about him?\n\nIt is Mr Carker the Manager who sits in the easy chair. A gaudy parrot in a burnished cage upon the table tears at the wires with her beak, and goes walking, upside down, in its dome-top, shaking her house and screeching; but Mr Carker is indifferent to the bird, and looks with a musing smile at a picture on the opposite wall.\n\n“A most extraordinary accidental likeness, certainly,” says he.\n\nPerhaps it is a Juno; perhaps a Potiphar’s Wife”; perhaps some scornful Nymph—according as the Picture Dealers found the market, when they christened it. It is the figure of a woman, supremely handsome, who, turning away, but with her face addressed to the spectator, flashes her proud glance upon him.\n\nIt is like Edith.\n\nWith a passing gesture of his hand at the picture—what! a menace? No; yet something like it. A wave as of triumph? No; yet more like that. An insolent salute wafted from his lips? No; yet like that too—he resumes his breakfast, and calls to the chafing and imprisoned bird, who coming down into a pendant gilded hoop within the cage, like a great wedding-ring, swings in it, for his delight.\n\nThe second home is on the other side of London, near to where the busy great north road of bygone days is silent and almost deserted, except by wayfarers who toil along on foot. It is a poor small house, barely and sparely furnished, but very clean; and there is even an attempt to decorate it, shown in the homely flowers trained about the porch and in the narrow garden. The neighbourhood in which it stands has as little of the country to recommend it, as it has of the town. It is neither of the town nor country. The former, like the giant in his travelling boots, has made a stride and passed it, and has set his brick-and-mortar heel a long way in advance; but the intermediate space between the giant’s feet, as yet, is only blighted country, and not town; and, here, among a few tall chimneys belching smoke all day and night, and among the brick-fields and the lanes where turf is cut, and where the fences tumble down, and where the dusty nettles grow, and where a scrap or two of hedge may yet be seen, and where the bird-catcher still comes occasionally, though he swears every time to come no more—this second home is to be found.”\n\nShe who inhabits it, is she who left the first in her devotion to an outcast brother. She withdrew from that home its redeeming spirit, and from its master’s breast his solitary angel: but though his liking for her is gone, after this ungrateful slight as he considers it; and though he abandons her altogether in return, an old idea of her is not quite forgotten even by him. Let her flower-garden, in which he never sets his foot, but which is yet maintained, among all his costly alterations, as if she had quitted it but yesterday, bear witness!\n\nHarriet Carker has changed since then, and on her beauty there has fallen a heavier shade than Time of his unassisted self can cast, all-potent as he is—the shadow of anxiety and sorrow, and the daily struggle of a poor existence. But it is beauty still; and still a gentle, quiet, and retiring beauty that must be sought out, for it cannot vaunt itself; if it could, it would be what it is, no more.\n\nYes. This slight, small, patient figure, neatly dressed in homely stuffs, and indicating nothing but the dull, household virtues, that have so little in common with the received idea of heroism and greatness, unless, indeed, any ray of them should shine through the lives of the great ones of the earth, when it becomes a constellation and is tracked in Heaven straightway—this slight, small, patient figure, leaning on the man still young but worn and grey, is she, his sister, who, of all the world, went over to him in his shame and put her hand in his, and with a sweet composure and determination, led him hopefully upon his barren way.\n\n“It is early, John,” she said. “Why do you go so early?”\n\n“Not many minutes earlier than usual, Harriet. If I have the time to spare, I should like, I think—it’s a fancy—to walk once by the house where I took leave of him.”\n\n“I wish I had ever seen or known him, John.”\n\n“It is better as it is, my dear, remembering his fate.”\n\n“But I could not regret it more, though I had known him. Is not your sorrow mine? And if I had, perhaps you would feel that I was a better companion to you in speaking about him, than I may seem now.”\n\n“My dearest sister! Is there anything within the range of rejoicing or regret, in which I am not sure of your companionship?”\n\n“I hope you think not, John, for surely there is nothing!”\n\n“How could you be better to me, or nearer to me then, than you are in this, or anything?” said her brother. “I feel that you did know him, Harriet, and that you shared my feelings towards him.”\n\nShe drew the hand which had been resting on his shoulder, round his neck, and answered, with some hesitation:\n\n“No, not quite.”\n“True, true!” he said; “you think I might have done him no harm if I had allowed myself to know him better?”\n\n“Think! I know it.”\n\n“Designedly, Heaven knows I would not,” he replied, shaking his head mournfully; “but his reputation was too precious to be perilled by such association. Whether you share that knowledge, or do not, my dear—”\n\n“I do not,” she said quietly.\n\n“It is still the truth, Harriet, and my mind is lighter when I think of him for that which made it so much heavier then.” He checked himself in his tone of melancholy, and smiled upon her as he said “Good-bye!”\n\n“Good-bye, dear John! In the evening, at the old time and place, I shall meet you as usual on your way home. Good-bye.”\n\nThe cordial face she lifted up to his to kiss him, was his home, his life, his universe, and yet it was a portion of his punishment and grief; for in the cloud he saw upon it—though serene and calm as any radiant cloud at sunset—and in the constancy and devotion of her life, and in the sacrifice she had made of ease, enjoyment, and hope, he saw the bitter fruits of his old crime, for ever ripe and fresh.\n\nShe stood at the door looking after him, with her hands loosely clasped in each other, as he made his way over the frowzy and uneven patch of ground which lay before their house, which had once (and not long ago) been a pleasant meadow, and was now a very waste, with a disorderly crop of beginnings of mean houses, rising out of the rubbish, as if they had been unskilfully sown there. Whenever he looked back—as once or twice he did—her cordial face shone like a light upon his heart; but when he plodded on his way, and saw her not, the tears were in her eyes as she stood watching him.\n\nHer pensive form was not long idle at the door. There was daily duty to discharge, and daily work to do—for such commonplace spirits that are not heroic, often work hard with their hands—and Harriet was soon busy with her household tasks. These discharged, and the poor house made quite neat and orderly, she counted her little stock of money, with an anxious face, and went out thoughtfully to buy some necessaries for their table, planning and conniving, as she went, how to save. So sordid are the lives of such low natures, who are not only not heroic to their valets and waiting-women, but have neither valets nor waiting-women to be heroic to withal!\n\nWhile she was absent, and there was no one in the house, there approached it by a different way from that the brother had taken, a gentleman, a very little past his prime of life perhaps, but of a healthy florid hue, an upright presence, and a bright clear aspect, that was gracious and good-humoured. His eyebrows were still black, and so was much of his hair; the sprinkling of grey observable among the latter, graced the former very much, and showed his broad frank brow and honest eyes to great advantage.\n\nAfter knocking once at the door, and obtaining no response, this gentleman sat down on a bench in the little porch to wait. A certain skilful action of his fingers as he hummed some bars, and beat time on the seat beside him, seemed to denote the musician; and the extraordinary satisfaction he derived from humming something very slow and long, which had no recognisable tune, seemed to denote that he was a scientific one.\n\nThe gentleman was still twirling a theme, which seemed to go round and round and round, and in and in and in, and to involve itself like a corkscrew twirled upon a table, without getting any nearer to anything, when Harriet appeared returning. He rose up as she advanced, and stood with his head uncovered.\n\n“You are come again, Sir!” she said, faltering.\n\n“I take that liberty,” he answered. “May I ask for five minutes of your leisure?”\n\nAfter a moment’s hesitation, she opened the door, and gave him admission to the little parlour. The gentleman sat down there, drew his chair to the table over against her, and said, in a voice that perfectly corresponded to his appearance, and with a simplicity that was very engaging:\n\n“Miss Harriet, you cannot be proud. You signified to me, when I called t’other morning, that you were. Pardon me if I say that I looked into your face while you spoke, and that it contradicted you. I look into it again,” he added, laying his hand gently on her arm, for an instant, “and it contradicts you more and more.”\n\nShe was somewhat confused and agitated, and could make no ready answer.\n\n“It is the mirror of truth,” said her visitor, “and gentleness. Excuse my trusting to it, and returning.”\n\nHis manner of saying these words, divested them entirely of the character of compliments. It was so plain, grave, unaffected, and sincere, that she bent her head, as if at once to thank him, and acknowledge his sincerity.\n\n“The disparity between our ages,” said the gentleman, “and the plainness of my purpose, empower me, I am glad to think, to speak my mind. That is my mind; and so you see me for the second time.”\n\n“There is a kind of pride, Sir,” she returned, after a moment’s silence, “or what may be supposed to be pride, which is mere duty. I hope I cherish no other.”\n\n“For yourself,” he said.\n\n“For myself.”\n“But—pardon me—” suggested the gentleman. “For your brother John?”\n\n“Proud of his love, I am,” said Harriet, looking full upon her visitor, and changing her manner on the instant—not that it was less composed and quiet, but that there was a deep impassioned earnestness in it that made the very tremble in her voice a part of her firmness, “and proud of him. Sir, you who strangely know the story of his life, and repeated it to me when you were here last—”\n\n“Merely to make my way into your confidence,” interposed the gentleman. “For heaven’s sake, don’t suppose—”\n\n“I am sure,” she said, “you revived it, in my hearing, with a kind and good purpose. I am quite sure of it.”\n\n“I thank you,” returned her visitor, pressing her hand hastily. “I am much obliged to you. You do me justice, I assure you. You were going to say, that I, who know the story of John Carker’s life—”\n\n“May think it pride in me,” she continued, “when I say that I am proud of him! I am. You know the time was, when I was not—when I could not be—but that is past. The humility of many years, the uncomplaining expiation, the true repentance, the terrible regret, the pain I know he has even in my affection, which he thinks has cost me dear, though Heaven knows I am happy, but for his sorrow I—oh, Sir, after what I have seen, let me conjure you, if you are in any place of power, and are ever wronged, never, for any wrong, inflict a punishment that cannot be recalled; while there is a GOD above us to work changes in the hearts He made.”\n\n“Your brother is an altered man,” returned the gentleman, compassionately. “I assure you I don’t doubt it.”\n\n“He was an altered man when he did wrong,” said Harriet. “He is an altered man again, and is his true self now, believe me, Sir.”\n\n“But we go on,” said her visitor, rubbing his forehead, in an absent manner, with his hand, and then drumming thoughtfully on the table, “we go on in our clockwork routine, from day to day, and can’t make out, or follow, these changes. They—they’re a metaphysical sort of thing. We—we haven’t leisure for it. We—we haven’t courage. They’re not taught at schools or colleges, and we don’t know how to set about it. In short, we are so d——d business-like,” said the gentleman, walking to the window, and back, and sitting down again, in a state of extreme dissatisfaction and vexation.\n\n“I am sure,” said the gentleman, rubbing his forehead again; and drumming on the table as before, “I have good reason to believe that a jog-trot life, the same from day to day, would reconcile one to anything. One don’t see anything, one don’t hear anything, one don’t know anything; that’s the fact. We go on taking everything for granted, and so we go on, until whatever we do, good, bad, or indifferent, we do from habit. Habit is all I shall have to report, when I am called upon to plead to my conscience, on my death-bed. ‘Habit,’ says I; ‘I was deaf, dumb, blind, and paralytic, to a million things, from habit.’ ‘Very business-like indeed, Mr What’s-your-name,’ says Conscience, ‘but it won’t do here!’”\n\nThe gentleman got up and walked to the window again and back: seriously uneasy, though giving his uneasiness this peculiar expression.\n\n“Miss Harriet,” he said, resuming his chair, “I wish you would let me serve you. Look at me; I ought to look honest, for I know I am so, at present. Do I?”\n\n“Yes,” she answered with a smile.\n\n“I believe every word you have said,” he returned. “I am full of self-reproach that I might have known this and seen this, and known you and seen you, any time these dozen years, and that I never have. I hardly know how I ever got here—creature that I am, not only of my own habit, but of other people’s! But having done so, let me do something. I ask it in all honour and respect. You inspire me with both, in the highest degree. Let me do something.”\n\n“We are contented, Sir.”\n\n“No, no, not quite,” returned the gentleman. “I think not quite. There are some little comforts that might smooth your life, and his. And his!” he repeated, fancying that had made some impression on her. “I have been in the habit of thinking that there was nothing wanting to be done for him; that it was all settled and over; in short, of not thinking at all about it. I am different now. Let me do something for him. You too,” said the visitor, with careful delicacy, “have need to watch your health closely, for his sake, and I fear it fails.”\n\n“Whoever you may be, Sir,” answered Harriet, raising her eyes to his face, “I am deeply grateful to you. I feel certain that in all you say, you have no object in the world but kindness to us. But years have passed since we began this life; and to take from my brother any part of what has so endeared him to me, and so proved his better resolution—any fragment of the merit of his unassisted, obscure, and forgotten reparation—would be to diminish the comfort it will be to him and me, when that time comes to each of us, of which you spoke just now. I thank you better with these tears than any words. Believe it, pray.”\n\nThe gentleman was moved, and put the hand she held out, to his lips, much as a tender father might kiss the hand of a dutiful child. But more reverently.\n\n“If the day should ever come,” said Harriet, “when he is restored, in part, to the position he lost—”\n\n“Restored!” cried the gentleman, quickly. “How can that be hoped for? In whose hands does the power of any restoration lie? It is no mistake of mine, surely, to suppose that his having gained the priceless blessing of his life, is one cause of the animosity shown to him by his brother.”\n\n“You touch upon a subject that is never breathed between us; not even between us,” said Harriet.\n\n“I beg your forgiveness,” said the visitor. “I should have known it. I entreat you to forget that I have done so, inadvertently. And now, as I dare urge no more—as I am not sure that I have a right to do so—though Heaven knows, even that doubt may be habit,” said the gentleman, rubbing his head, as despondently as before, “let me; though a stranger, yet no stranger; ask two favours.”\n\n“What are they?” she inquired.\n\n“The first, that if you should see cause to change your resolution, you will suffer me to be as your right hand. My name shall then be at your service; it is useless now, and always insignificant.”\n\n“Our choice of friends,” she answered, smiling faintly, “is not so great, that I need any time for consideration. I can promise that.”\n\n“The second, that you will allow me sometimes, say every Monday morning, at nine o’clock—habit again—I must be businesslike,” said the gentleman, with a whimsical inclination to quarrel with himself on that head, “in walking past, to see you at the door or window. I don’t ask to come in, as your brother will be gone out at that hour. I don’t ask to speak to you. I merely ask to see, for the satisfaction of my own mind, that you are well, and without intrusion to remind you, by the sight of me, that you have a friend—an elderly friend, grey-haired already, and fast growing greyer—whom you may ever command.”\n\nThe cordial face looked up in his; confided in it; and promised.\n\n“I understand, as before,” said the gentleman, rising, “that you purpose not to mention my visit to John Carker, lest he should be at all distressed by my acquaintance with his history. I am glad of it, for it is out of the ordinary course of things, and—habit again!” said the gentleman, checking himself impatiently, “as if there were no better course than the ordinary course!”\n\nWith that he turned to go, and walking, bareheaded, to the outside of the little porch, took leave of her with such a happy mixture of unconstrained respect and unaffected interest, as no breeding could have taught, no truth mistrusted, and nothing but a pure and single heart expressed.\n\nMany half-forgotten emotions were awakened in the sister’s mind by this visit. It was so very long since any other visitor had crossed their threshold; it was so very long since any voice of apathy had made sad music in her ears; that the stranger’s figure remained present to her, hours afterwards, when she sat at the window, plying her needle; and his words seemed newly spoken, again and again. He had touched the spring that opened her whole life; and if she lost him for a short space, it was only among the many shapes of the one great recollection of which that life was made.\n\nMusing and working by turns; now constraining herself to be steady at her needle for a long time together, and now letting her work fall, unregarded, on her lap, and straying wheresoever her busier thoughts led, Harriet Carker found the hours glide by her, and the day steal on. The morning, which had been bright and clear, gradually became overcast; a sharp wind set in; the rain fell heavily; and a dark mist drooping over the distant town, hid it from the view.\n\nShe often looked with compassion, at such a time, upon the stragglers who came wandering into London, by the great highway hard by, and who, footsore and weary, and gazing fearfully at the huge town before them, as if foreboding that their misery there would be but as a drop of water in the sea, or as a grain of sea-sand on the shore, went shrinking on, cowering before the angry weather, and looking as if the very elements rejected them. Day after day, such travellers crept past, but always, as she thought, in one direction—always towards the town. Swallowed up in one phase or other of its immensity, towards which they seemed impelled by a desperate fascination, they never returned. Food for the hospitals, the churchyards, the prisons, the river, fever, madness, vice, and death,—they passed on to the monster, roaring in the distance, and were lost.\n\nThe chill wind was howling, and the rain was falling, and the day was darkening moodily, when Harriet, raising her eyes from the work on which she had long since been engaged with unremitting constancy, saw one of these travellers approaching.\n\nA woman. A solitary woman of some thirty years of age; tall; well-formed; handsome; miserably dressed; the soil of many country roads in varied weather—dust, chalk, clay, gravel—clotted on her grey cloak by the streaming wet; no bonnet on her head, nothing to defend her rich black hair from the rain, but a torn handkerchief; with the fluttering ends of which, and with her hair, the wind blinded her so that she often stopped to push them back, and look upon the way she was going.\n\nShe was in the act of doing so, when Harriet observed her. As her hands, parting on her sunburnt forehead, swept across her face, and threw aside the hindrances that encroached upon it, there was a reckless and regardless beauty in it: a dauntless and depraved indifference to more than weather: a carelessness of what was cast upon her bare head from Heaven or earth: that, coupled with her misery and loneliness, touched the heart of her fellow-woman. She thought of all that was perverted and debased within her, no less than without: of modest graces of the mind, hardened and steeled, like these attractions of the person; of the many gifts of the Creator flung to the winds like the wild hair; of all the beautiful ruin upon which the storm was beating and the night was coming.\n\nThinking of this, she did not turn away with a delicate indignation—too many of her own compassionate and tender sex too often do—but pitied her.\n\nHer fallen sister came on, looking far before her, trying with her eager eyes to pierce the mist in which the city was enshrouded, and glancing, now and then, from side to side, with the bewildered—and uncertain aspect of a stranger. Though her tread was bold and courageous, she was fatigued, and after a moment of irresolution,—sat down upon a heap of stones; seeking no shelter from the rain, but letting it rain on her as it would.\n\nShe was now opposite the house; raising her head after resting it for a moment on both hands, her eyes met those of Harriet.\n\nIn a moment, Harriet was at the door; and the other, rising from her seat at her beck, came slowly, and with no conciliatory look, towards her.\n\n“Why do you rest in the rain?” said Harriet, gently.\n\n“Because I have no other resting-place,” was the reply.\n\n“But there are many places of shelter near here. This,” referring to the little porch, “is better than where you were. You are very welcome to rest here.”\n\nThe wanderer looked at her, in doubt and surprise, but without any expression of thankfulness; and sitting down, and taking off one of her worn shoes to beat out the fragments of stone and dust that were inside, showed that her foot was cut and bleeding.\n\nHarriet uttering an expression of pity, the traveller looked up with a contemptuous and incredulous smile.\n\n“Why, what’s a torn foot to such as me?” she said. “And what’s a torn foot in such as me, to such as you?”\n\n“Come in and wash it,” answered Harriet, mildly, “and let me give you something to bind it up.”\n\nThe woman caught her arm, and drawing it before her own eyes, hid them against it, and wept. Not like a woman, but like a stern man surprised into that weakness; with a violent heaving of her breast, and struggle for recovery, that showed how unusual the emotion was with her.\n\nShe submitted to be led into the house, and, evidently more in gratitude than in any care for herself, washed and bound the injured place. Harriet then put before her fragments of her own frugal dinner, and when she had eaten of them, though sparingly, besought her, before resuming her road (which she showed her anxiety to do), to dry her clothes before the fire. Again, more in gratitude than with any evidence of concern in her own behalf, she sat down in front of it, and unbinding the handkerchief about her head, and letting her thick wet hair fall down below her waist, sat drying it with the palms of her hands, and looking at the blaze.\n\n“I daresay you are thinking,” she said, lifting her head suddenly, “that I used to be handsome, once. I believe I was—I know I was—Look here!”\n\nShe held up her hair roughly with both hands; seizing it as if she would have torn it out; then, threw it down again, and flung it back as though it were a heap of serpents.\n\n“Are you a stranger in this place?” asked Harriet.\n\n“A stranger!” she returned, stopping between each short reply, and looking at the fire. “Yes. Ten or a dozen years a stranger. I have had no almanack where I have been. Ten or a dozen years. I don’t know this part. It’s much altered since I went away.”\n\n“Have you been far?”\n\n“Very far. Months upon months over the sea, and far away even then. I have been where convicts go,” she added, looking full upon her entertainer. “I have been one myself.”\n\n“Heaven help you and forgive you!” was the gentle answer.\n\n“Ah! Heaven help me and forgive me!” she returned, nodding her head at the fire. “If man would help some of us a little more, God would forgive us all the sooner perhaps.”\n\nBut she was softened by the earnest manner, and the cordial face so full of mildness and so free from judgment, of her, and said, less hardily:\n\n“We may be about the same age, you and me. If I am older, it is not above a year or two. Oh think of that!”\n\nShe opened her arms, as though the exhibition of her outward form would show the moral wretch she was; and letting them drop at her sides, hung down her head.\n\n“There is nothing we may not hope to repair; it is never too late to amend,” said Harriet. “You are penitent?”\n\n“No,” she answered. “I am not! I can’t be. I am no such thing. Why should I be penitent, and all the world go free? They talk to me of my penitence. Who’s penitent for the wrongs that have been done to me?”\n\nShe rose up, bound her handkerchief about her head, and turned to move away.\n\n“Where are you going?” said Harriet.\n\n“Yonder,” she answered, pointing with her hand. “To London.”\n\n“Have you any home to go to?”\n\n“I think I have a mother. She’s as much a mother, as her dwelling is a home,” she answered with a bitter laugh.\n\n“Take this,” cried Harriet, putting money in her hand. “Try to do well. It is very little, but for one day it may keep you from harm.”\n\n“Are you married?” said the other, faintly, as she took it.\n\n“No. I live here with my brother. We have not much to spare, or I would give you more.”\n\n“Will you let me kiss you?”\n\nSeeing no scorn or repugnance in her face, the object of her charity bent over her as she asked the question, and pressed her lips against her cheek. Once more she caught her arm, and covered her eyes with it; and then was gone.\n\nGone into the deepening night, and howling wind, and pelting rain; urging her way on towards the mist-enshrouded city where the blurred lights gleamed; and with her black hair, and disordered head-gear, fluttering round her reckless face.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIV. Another Mother and Daughter\n\nIn an ugly and dark room, an old woman, ugly and dark too, sat listening to the wind and rain, and crouching over a meagre fire. More constant to the last-named occupation than the first, she never changed her attitude, unless, when any stray drops of rain fell hissing on the smouldering embers, to raise her head with an awakened attention to the whistling and pattering outside, and gradually to let it fall again lower and lower and lower as she sunk into a brooding state of thought, in which the noises of the night were as indistinctly regarded as is the monotonous rolling of a sea by one who sits in contemplation on its shore.\n\nThere was no light in the room save that which the fire afforded. Glaring sullenly from time to time like the eye of a fierce beast half asleep, it revealed no objects that needed to be jealous of a better display. A heap of rags, a heap of bones, a wretched bed, two or three mutilated chairs or stools, the black walls and blacker ceiling, were all its winking brightness shone upon. As the old woman, with a gigantic and distorted image of herself thrown half upon the wall behind her, half upon the roof above, sat bending over the few loose bricks within which it was pent, on the damp hearth of the chimney—for there was no stove—she looked as if she were watching at some witch’s altar for a favourable token; and but that the movement of her chattering jaws and trembling chin was too frequent and too fast for the slow flickering of the fire, it would have seemed an illusion wrought by the light, as it came and went, upon a face as motionless as the form to which it belonged.\n\nIf Florence could have stood within the room and looked upon the original of the shadow thrown upon the wall and roof as it cowered thus over the fire, a glance might have sufficed to recall the figure of Good Mrs Brown; notwithstanding that her childish recollection of that terrible old woman was as grotesque and exaggerated a presentment of the truth, perhaps, as the shadow on the wall. But Florence was not there to look on; and Good Mrs Brown remained unrecognised, and sat staring at her fire, unobserved.\n\nAttracted by a louder sputtering than usual, as the rain came hissing down the chimney in a little stream, the old woman raised her head, impatiently, to listen afresh. And this time she did not drop it again; for there was a hand upon the door, and a footstep in the room.\n\n“Who’s that?” she said, looking over her shoulder.\n\n“One who brings you news, was the answer, in a woman’s voice.\n\n“News? Where from?”\n\n“From abroad.”\n\n“From beyond seas?” cried the old woman, starting up.\n\n“Ay, from beyond seas.”\n\nThe old woman raked the fire together, hurriedly, and going close to her visitor who had entered, and shut the door, and who now stood in the middle of the room, put her hand upon the drenched cloak, and turned the unresisting figure, so as to have it in the full light of the fire. She did not find what she had expected, whatever that might be; for she let the cloak go again, and uttered a querulous cry of disappointment and misery.\n\n“What is the matter?” asked her visitor.\n\n“Oho! Oho!” cried the old woman, turning her face upward, with a terrible howl.\n\n“What is the matter?” asked the visitor again.\n\n“It’s not my gal!” cried the old woman, tossing up her arms, and clasping her hands above her head. “Where’s my Alice? Where’s my handsome daughter? They’ve been the death of her!”\n\n“They’ve not been the death of her yet, if your name’s Marwood,” said the visitor.\n\n“Have you seen my gal, then?” cried the old woman. “Has she wrote to me?”\n\n“She said you couldn’t read,” returned the other.\n\n“No more I can!” exclaimed the old woman, wringing her hands.\n\n“Have you no light here?” said the other, looking round the room.\n\nThe old woman, mumbling and shaking her head, and muttering to herself about her handsome daughter, brought a candle from a cupboard in the corner, and thrusting it into the fire with a trembling hand, lighted it with some difficulty and set it on the table. Its dirty wick burnt dimly at first, being choked in its own grease; and when the bleared eyes and failing sight of the old woman could distinguish anything by its light, her visitor was sitting with her arms folded, her eyes turned downwards, and a handkerchief she had worn upon her head lying on the table by her side.\n\n“She sent to me by word of mouth then, my gal, Alice?” mumbled the old woman, after waiting for some moments. “What did she say?”\n\n“Look,” returned the visitor.\n\nThe old woman repeated the word in a scared uncertain way; and, shading her eyes, looked at the speaker, round the room, and at the speaker once again.\n\n“Alice said look again, mother;” and the speaker fixed her eyes upon her.\n\nAgain the old woman looked round the room, and at her visitor, and round the room once more. Hastily seizing the candle, and rising from her seat, she held it to the visitor’s face, uttered a loud cry, set down the light, and fell upon her neck!\n\n“It’s my gal! It’s my Alice! It’s my handsome daughter, living and come back!” screamed the old woman, rocking herself to and fro upon the breast that coldly suffered her embrace. “It’s my gal! It’s my Alice! It’s my handsome daughter, living and come back!” she screamed again, dropping on the floor before her, clasping her knees, laying her head against them, and still rocking herself to and fro with every frantic demonstration of which her vitality was capable.\n\n“Yes, mother,” returned Alice, stooping forward for a moment and kissing her, but endeavouring, even in the act, to disengage herself from her embrace. “I am here, at last. Let go, mother; let go. Get up, and sit in your chair. What good does this do?”\n\n“She’s come back harder than she went!” cried the mother, looking up in her face, and still holding to her knees. “She don’t care for me! after all these years, and all the wretched life I’ve led!”\n\n“Why, mother!” said Alice, shaking her ragged skirts to detach the old woman from them: “there are two sides to that. There have been years for me as well as you, and there has been wretchedness for me as well as you. Get up, get up!”\n\nHer mother rose, and cried, and wrung her hands, and stood at a little distance gazing on her. Then she took the candle again, and going round her, surveyed her from head to foot, making a low moaning all the time. Then she put the candle down, resumed her chair, and beating her hands together to a kind of weary tune, and rolling herself from side to side, continued moaning and wailing to herself.\n\nAlice got up, took off her wet cloak, and laid it aside. That done, she sat down as before, and with her arms folded, and her eyes gazing at the fire, remained silently listening with a contemptuous face to her old mother’s inarticulate complainings.\n\n“Did you expect to see me return as youthful as I went away, mother?” she said at length, turning her eyes upon the old woman. “Did you think a foreign life, like mine, was good for good looks? One would believe so, to hear you!”\n\n“It ain’t that!” cried the mother. “She knows it!”\n\n“What is it then?” returned the daughter. “It had best be something that don’t last, mother, or my way out is easier than my way in.”\n\n“Hear that!” exclaimed the mother. “After all these years she threatens to desert me in the moment of her coming back again!”\n\n“I tell you, mother, for the second time, there have been years for me as well as you,” said Alice. “Come back harder? Of course I have come back harder. What else did you expect?”\n\n“Harder to me! To her own dear mother!” cried the old woman\n\n“I don’t know who began to harden me, if my own dear mother didn’t,” she returned, sitting with her folded arms, and knitted brows, and compressed lips as if she were bent on excluding, by force, every softer feeling from her breast. “Listen, mother, to a word or two. If we understand each other now, we shall not fall out any more, perhaps. I went away a girl, and have come back a woman. I went away undutiful enough, and have come back no better, you may swear. But have you been very dutiful to me?”\n\n“I!” cried the old woman. “To my gal! A mother dutiful to her own child!”\n\n“It sounds unnatural, don’t it?” returned the daughter, looking coldly on her with her stern, regardless, hardy, beautiful face; “but I have thought of it sometimes, in the course of my lone years, till I have got used to it. I have heard some talk about duty first and last; but it has always been of my duty to other people. I have wondered now and then—to pass away the time—whether no one ever owed any duty to me.”\n\nHer mother sat mowing, and mumbling, and shaking her head, but whether angrily or remorsefully, or in denial, or only in her physical infirmity, did not appear.\n\n“There was a child called Alice Marwood,” said the daughter, with a laugh, and looking down at herself in terrible derision of herself, “born, among poverty and neglect, and nursed in it. Nobody taught her, nobody stepped forward to help her, nobody cared for her.”\n\n“Nobody!” echoed the mother, pointing to herself, and striking her breast.\n\n“The only care she knew,” returned the daughter, “was to be beaten, and stinted, and abused sometimes; and she might have done better without that. She lived in homes like this, and in the streets, with a crowd of little wretches like herself; and yet she brought good looks out of this childhood. So much the worse for her. She had better have been hunted and worried to death for ugliness.”\n\n“Go on! go on!” exclaimed the mother.\n\n“I am going on,” returned the daughter. “There was a girl called Alice Marwood. She was handsome. She was taught too late, and taught all wrong. She was too well cared for, too well trained, too well helped on, too much looked after. You were very fond of her—you were better off then. What came to that girl comes to thousands every year. It was only ruin, and she was born to it.”\n\n“After all these years!” whined the old woman. “My gal begins with this.”\n\n“She’ll soon have ended,” said the daughter. “There was a criminal called Alice Marwood—a girl still, but deserted and an outcast. And she was tried, and she was sentenced. And lord, how the gentlemen in the Court talked about it! and how grave the judge was on her duty, and on her having perverted the gifts of nature—as if he didn’t know better than anybody there, that they had been made curses to her!—and how he preached about the strong arm of the Law—so very strong to save her, when she was an innocent and helpless little wretch!—and how solemn and religious it all was! I have thought of that, many times since, to be sure!”\n\nShe folded her arms tightly on her breast, and laughed in a tone that made the howl of the old woman musical.\n\n“So Alice Marwood was transported, mother,” she pursued, “and was sent to learn her duty, where there was twenty times less duty, and more wickedness, and wrong, and infamy, than here. And Alice Marwood is come back a woman. Such a woman as she ought to be, after all this. In good time, there will be more solemnity, and more fine talk, and more strong arm, most likely, and there will be an end of her; but the gentlemen needn’t be afraid of being thrown out of work. There’s crowds of little wretches, boy and girl, growing up in any of the streets they live in, that’ll keep them to it till they’ve made their fortunes.”\n\nThe old woman leaned her elbows on the table, and resting her face upon her two hands, made a show of being in great distress—or really was, perhaps.\n\n“There! I have done, mother,” said the daughter, with a motion of her head, as if in dismissal of the subject. “I have said enough. Don’t let you and I talk of being dutiful, whatever we do. Your childhood was like mine, I suppose. So much the worse for both of us. I don’t want to blame you, or to defend myself; why should I? That’s all over long ago. But I am a woman—not a girl, now—and you and I needn’t make a show of our history, like the gentlemen in the Court. We know all about it, well enough.”\n\nLost and degraded as she was, there was a beauty in her, both of face and form, which, even in its worst expression, could not but be recognised as such by anyone regarding her with the least attention. As she subsided into silence, and her face which had been harshly agitated, quieted down; while her dark eyes, fixed upon the fire, exchanged the reckless light that had animated them, for one that was softened by something like sorrow; there shone through all her wayworn misery and fatigue, a ray of the departed radiance of the fallen angel.\n\nHer mother, after watching her for some time without speaking, ventured to steal her withered hand a little nearer to her across the table; and finding that she permitted this, to touch her face, and smooth her hair. With the feeling, as it seemed, that the old woman was at least sincere in this show of interest, Alice made no movement to check her; so, advancing by degrees, she bound up her daughter’s hair afresh, took off her wet shoes, if they deserved the name, spread something dry upon her shoulders, and hovered humbly about her, muttering to herself, as she recognised her old features and expression more and more.\n\n“You are very poor, mother, I see,” said Alice, looking round, when she had sat thus for some time.\n\n“Bitter poor, my deary,” replied the old woman.\n\nShe admired her daughter, and was afraid of her. Perhaps her admiration, such as it was, had originated long ago, when she first found anything that was beautiful appearing in the midst of the squalid fight of her existence. Perhaps her fear was referable, in some sort, to the retrospect she had so lately heard. Be this as it might, she stood, submissively and deferentially, before her child, and inclined her head, as if in a pitiful entreaty to be spared any further reproach.\n\n“How have you lived?”\n\n“By begging, my deary.\n\n“And pilfering, mother?”\n\n“Sometimes, Ally—in a very small way. I am old and timid. I have taken trifles from children now and then, my deary, but not often. I have tramped about the country, pet, and I know what I know. I have watched.”\n\n“Watched?” returned the daughter, looking at her.\n\n“I have hung about a family, my deary,” said the mother, even more humbly and submissively than before.\n\n“What family?”\n\n“Hush, darling. Don’t be angry with me. I did it for the love of you. In memory of my poor gal beyond seas.” She put out her hand deprecatingly, and drawing it back again, laid it on her lips.\n\n“Years ago, my deary,” she pursued, glancing timidly at the attentive and stern face opposed to her, “I came across his little child, by chance.”\n\n“Whose child?”\n\n“Not his, Alice deary; don’t look at me like that; not his. How could it be his? You know he has none.”\n\n“Whose then?” returned the daughter. “You said his.”\n\n“Hush, Ally; you frighten me, deary. Mr Dombey’s—only Mr Dombey’s. Since then, darling, I have seen them often. I have seen him.”\n\nIn uttering this last word, the old woman shrunk and recoiled, as if with sudden fear that her daughter would strike her. But though the daughter’s face was fixed upon her, and expressed the most vehement passion, she remained still: except that she clenched her arms tighter and tighter within each other, on her bosom, as if to restrain them by that means from doing an injury to herself, or someone else, in the blind fury of the wrath that suddenly possessed her.\n\n“Little he thought who I was!” said the old woman, shaking her clenched hand.\n\n“And little he cared!” muttered her daughter, between her teeth.\n\n“But there we were, said the old woman, “face to face. I spoke to him, and he spoke to me. I sat and watched him as he went away down a long grove of trees: and at every step he took, I cursed him soul and body.”\n\n“He will thrive in spite of that,” returned the daughter disdainfully.\n\n“Ay, he is thriving,” said the mother.\n\nShe held her peace; for the face and form before her were unshaped by rage. It seemed as if the bosom would burst with the emotions that strove within it. The effort that constrained and held it pent up, was no less formidable than the rage itself: no less bespeaking the violent and dangerous character of the woman who made it. But it succeeded, and she asked, after a silence:\n\n“Is he married?”\n\n“No, deary,” said the mother.\n\n“Going to be?”\n\n“Not that I know of, deary. But his master and friend is married. Oh, we may give him joy! We may give ’em all joy!” cried the old woman, hugging herself with her lean arms in her exultation. “Nothing but joy to us will come of that marriage. Mind me!”\n\nThe daughter looked at her for an explanation.\n\n“But you are wet and tired; hungry and thirsty,” said the old woman, hobbling to the cupboard; “and there’s little here, and little”—diving down into her pocket, and jingling a few half—pence on the table—“little here. Have you any money, Alice, deary?”\n\nThe covetous, sharp, eager face, with which she asked the question and looked on, as her daughter took out of her bosom the little gift she had so lately received, told almost as much of the history of this parent and child as the child herself had told in words.\n\n“Is that all?” said the mother.\n\n“I have no more. I should not have this, but for charity.”\n\n“But for charity, eh, deary?” said the old woman, bending greedily over the table to look at the money, which she appeared distrustful of her daughter’s still retaining in her hand, and gazing on. “Humph! six and six is twelve, and six eighteen—so—we must make the most of it. I’ll go buy something to eat and drink.”\n\nWith greater alacrity than might have been expected in one of her appearance—for age and misery seemed to have made her as decrepit as ugly—she began to occupy her trembling hands in tying an old bonnet on her head, and folding a torn shawl about herself: still eyeing the money in her daughter’s hand, with the same sharp desire.\n\n“What joy is to come to us of this marriage, mother?” asked the daughter. “You have not told me that.”\n\n“The joy,” she replied, attiring herself, with fumbling fingers, “of no love at all, and much pride and hate, my deary. The joy of confusion and strife among ’em, proud as they are, and of danger—danger, Alice!”\n\n“What danger?”\n\n“I have seen what I have seen. I know what I know!” chuckled the mother. “Let some look to it. Let some be upon their guard. My gal may keep good company yet!”\n\nThen, seeing that in the wondering earnestness with which her daughter regarded her, her hand involuntarily closed upon the money, the old woman made more speed to secure it, and hurriedly added, “but I’ll go buy something; I’ll go buy something.”\n\nAs she stood with her hand stretched out before her daughter, her daughter, glancing again at the money, put it to her lips before parting with it.\n\n“What, Ally! Do you kiss it?” chuckled the old woman. “That’s like me—I often do. Oh, it’s so good to us!” squeezing her own tarnished halfpence up to her bag of a throat, “so good to us in everything but not coming in heaps!”\n\n“I kiss it, mother,” said the daughter, “or I did then—I don’t know that I ever did before—for the giver’s sake.”\n\n“The giver, eh, deary?” retorted the old woman, whose dimmed eyes glistened as she took it. “Ay! I’ll kiss it for the giver’s sake, too, when the giver can make it go farther. But I’ll go spend it, deary. I’ll be back directly.”\n\n“You seem to say you know a great deal, mother,” said the daughter, following her to the door with her eyes. “You have grown very wise since we parted.”\n\n“Know!” croaked the old woman, coming back a step or two, “I know more than you think I know more than he thinks, deary, as I’ll tell you by and bye. I know all.”\n\nThe daughter smiled incredulously.\n\n“I know of his brother, Alice,” said the old woman, stretching out her neck with a leer of malice absolutely frightful, “who might have been where you have been—for stealing money—and who lives with his sister, over yonder, by the north road out of London.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“By the north road out of London, deary. You shall see the house if you like. It ain’t much to boast of, genteel as his own is. No, no, no,” cried the old woman, shaking her head and laughing; for her daughter had started up, “not now; it’s too far off; it’s by the milestone, where the stones are heaped;—to-morrow, deary, if it’s fine, and you are in the humour. But I’ll go spend—”\n\n“Stop!” and the daughter flung herself upon her, with her former passion raging like a fire. “The sister is a fair-faced Devil, with brown hair?”\n\nThe old woman, amazed and terrified, nodded her head.\n\n“I see the shadow of him in her face! It’s a red house standing by itself. Before the door there is a small green porch.”\n\nAgain the old woman nodded.\n\n“In which I sat today! Give me back the money.”\n\n“Alice! Deary!”\n\n“Give me back the money, or you’ll be hurt.”\n\nShe forced it from the old woman’s hand as she spoke, and utterly indifferent to her complainings and entreaties, threw on the garments she had taken off, and hurried out, with headlong speed.\n\nThe mother followed, limping after her as she could, and expostulating with no more effect upon her than upon the wind and rain and darkness that encompassed them. Obdurate and fierce in her own purpose, and indifferent to all besides, the daughter defied the weather and the distance, as if she had known no travel or fatigue, and made for the house where she had been relieved. After some quarter of an hour’s walking, the old woman, spent and out of breath, ventured to hold by her skirts; but she ventured no more, and they travelled on in silence through the wet and gloom. If the mother now and then uttered a word of complaint, she stifled it lest her daughter should break away from her and leave her behind; and the daughter was dumb.\n\nIt was within an hour or so of midnight, when they left the regular streets behind them, and entered on the deeper gloom of that neutral ground where the house was situated. The town lay in the distance, lurid and lowering; the bleak wind howled over the open space; all around was black, wild, desolate.\n\n“This is a fit place for me!” said the daughter, stopping to look back. “I thought so, when I was here before, today.”\n\n“Alice, my deary,” cried the mother, pulling her gently by the skirt. “Alice!”\n\n“What now, mother?”\n\n“Don’t give the money back, my darling; please don’t. We can’t afford it. We want supper, deary. Money is money, whoever gives it. Say what you will, but keep the money.”\n\n“See there!” was all the daughter’s answer. “That is the house I mean. Is that it?”\n\nThe old woman nodded in the affirmative; and a few more paces brought them to the threshold. There was the light of fire and candle in the room where Alice had sat to dry her clothes; and on her knocking at the door, John Carker appeared from that room.\n\nHe was surprised to see such visitors at such an hour, and asked Alice what she wanted.\n\n“I want your sister,” she said. “The woman who gave me money today.”\n\nAt the sound of her raised voice, Harriet came out.\n\n“Oh!” said Alice. “You are here! Do you remember me?”\n\n“Yes,” she answered, wondering.\n\nThe face that had humbled itself before her, looked on her now with such invincible hatred and defiance; and the hand that had gently touched her arm, was clenched with such a show of evil purpose, as if it would gladly strangle her; that she drew close to her brother for protection.\n\n“That I could speak with you, and not know you! That I could come near you, and not feel what blood was running in your veins, by the tingling of my own!” said Alice, with a menacing gesture.\n\n“What do you mean? What have I done?”\n\n“Done!” returned the other. “You have sat me by your fire; you have given me food and money; you have bestowed your compassion on me! You! whose name I spit upon!”\n\nThe old woman, with a malevolence that made her ugliness quite awful, shook her withered hand at the brother and sister in confirmation of her daughter, but plucked her by the skirts again, nevertheless, imploring her to keep the money.\n\n“If I dropped a tear upon your hand, may it wither it up! If I spoke a gentle word in your hearing, may it deafen you! If I touched you with my lips, may the touch be poison to you! A curse upon this roof that gave me shelter! Sorrow and shame upon your head! Ruin upon all belonging to you!”\n\nAs she said the words, she threw the money down upon the ground, and spurned it with her foot.\n\n“I tread it in the dust: I wouldn’t take it if it paved my way to Heaven! I would the bleeding foot that brought me here today, had rotted off, before it led me to your house!”\n\nHarriet, pale and trembling, restrained her brother, and suffered her to go on uninterrupted.\n\n“It was well that I should be pitied and forgiven by you, or anyone of your name, in the first hour of my return! It was well that you should act the kind good lady to me! I’ll thank you when I die; I’ll pray for you, and all your race, you may be sure!”\n\nWith a fierce action of her hand, as if she sprinkled hatred on the ground, and with it devoted those who were standing there to destruction, she looked up once at the black sky, and strode out into the wild night.\n\nThe mother, who had plucked at her skirts again and again in vain, and had eyed the money lying on the threshold with an absorbing greed that seemed to concentrate her faculties upon it, would have prowled about, until the house was dark, and then groped in the mire on the chance of repossessing herself of it. But the daughter drew her away, and they set forth, straight, on their return to their dwelling; the old woman whimpering and bemoaning their loss upon the road, and fretfully bewailing, as openly as she dared, the undutiful conduct of her handsome girl in depriving her of a supper, on the very first night of their reunion.\n\nSupperless to bed she went, saving for a few coarse fragments; and those she sat mumbling and munching over a scrap of fire, long after her undutiful daughter lay asleep.\n\nWere this miserable mother, and this miserable daughter, only the reduction to their lowest grade, of certain social vices sometimes prevailing higher up? In this round world of many circles within circles, do we make a weary journey from the high grade to the low, to find at last that they lie close together, that the two extremes touch, and that our journey’s end is but our starting-place? Allowing for great difference of stuff and texture, was the pattern of this woof repeated among gentle blood at all?\n\nSay, Edith Dombey! And Cleopatra, best of mothers, let us have your testimony!\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV. The Happy Pair\n\nThe dark blot on the street is gone. Mr Dombey’s mansion, if it be a gap among the other houses any longer, is only so because it is not to be vied with in its brightness, and haughtily casts them off. The saying is, that home is home, be it never so homely. If it hold good in the opposite contingency, and home is home be it never so stately, what an altar to the Household Gods is raised up here!\n\nLights are sparkling in the windows this evening, and the ruddy glow of fires is warm and bright upon the hangings and soft carpets, and the dinner waits to be served, and the dinner-table is handsomely set forth, though only for four persons, and the side board is cumbrous with plate. It is the first time that the house has been arranged for occupation since its late changes, and the happy pair are looked for every minute.\n\nOnly second to the wedding morning, in the interest and expectation it engenders among the household, is this evening of the coming home. Mrs Perch is in the kitchen taking tea; and has made the tour of the establishment, and priced the silks and damasks by the yard, and exhausted every interjection in the dictionary and out of it expressive of admiration and wonder. The upholsterer’s foreman, who has left his hat, with a pocket-handkerchief in it, both smelling strongly of varnish, under a chair in the hall, lurks about the house, gazing upwards at the cornices, and downward at the carpets, and occasionally, in a silent transport of enjoyment, taking a rule out of his pocket, and skirmishingly measuring expensive objects, with unutterable feelings. Cook is in high spirits, and says give her a place where there’s plenty of company (as she’ll bet you sixpence there will be now), for she is of a lively disposition, and she always was from a child, and she don’t mind who knows it; which sentiment elicits from the breast of Mrs Perch a responsive murmur of support and approbation. All the housemaid hopes is, happiness for ’em—but marriage is a lottery, and the more she thinks about it, the more she feels the independence and the safety of a single life. Mr Towlinson is saturnine and grim, and says that’s his opinion too, and give him War besides, and down with the French—for this young man has a general impression that every foreigner is a Frenchman, and must be by the laws of nature.\n\nAt each new sound of wheels, they all stop, whatever they are saying, and listen; and more than once there is a general starting up and a cry of “Here they are!” But here they are not yet; and Cook begins to mourn over the dinner, which has been put back twice, and the upholsterer’s foreman still goes lurking about the rooms, undisturbed in his blissful reverie!\n\nFlorence is ready to receive her father and her new Mama. Whether the emotions that are throbbing in her breast originate in pleasure or in pain, she hardly knows. But the fluttering heart sends added colour to her cheeks, and brightness to her eyes; and they say downstairs, drawing their heads together—for they always speak softly when they speak of her—how beautiful Miss Florence looks tonight, and what a sweet young lady she has grown, poor dear! A pause succeeds; and then Cook, feeling, as president, that her sentiments are waited for, wonders whether—and there stops. The housemaid wonders too, and so does Mrs Perch, who has the happy social faculty of always wondering when other people wonder, without being at all particular what she wonders at. Mr Towlinson, who now descries an opportunity of bringing down the spirits of the ladies to his own level, says wait and see; he wishes some people were well out of this. Cook leads a sigh then, and a murmur of “Ah, it’s a strange world, it is indeed!” and when it has gone round the table, adds persuasively, “but Miss Florence can’t well be the worse for any change, Tom.” Mr Towlinson’s rejoinder, pregnant with frightful meaning, is “Oh, can’t she though!” and sensible that a mere man can scarcely be more prophetic, or improve upon that, he holds his peace.\n\nMrs Skewton, prepared to greet her darling daughter and dear son-in-law with open arms, is appropriately attired for that purpose in a very youthful costume, with short sleeves. At present, however, her ripe charms are blooming in the shade of her own apartments, whence she had not emerged since she took possession of them a few hours ago, and where she is fast growing fretful, on account of the postponement of dinner. The maid who ought to be a skeleton, but is in truth a buxom damsel, is, on the other hand, in a most amiable state: considering her quarterly stipend much safer than heretofore, and foreseeing a great improvement in her board and lodging.\n\nWhere are the happy pair, for whom this brave home is waiting? Do steam, tide, wind, and horses, all abate their speed, to linger on such happiness? Does the swarm of loves and graces hovering about them retard their progress by its numbers? Are there so many flowers in their happy path, that they can scarcely move along, without entanglement in thornless roses, and sweetest briar?\n\nThey are here at last! The noise of wheels is heard, grows louder, and a carriage drives up to the door! A thundering knock from the obnoxious foreigner anticipates the rush of Mr Towlinson and party to open it; and Mr Dombey and his bride alight, and walk in arm in arm.\n\n“My sweetest Edith!” cries an agitated voice upon the stairs. “My dearest Dombey!” and the short sleeves wreath themselves about the happy couple in turn, and embrace them.\n\nFlorence had come down to the hall too, but did not advance: reserving her timid welcome until these nearer and dearer transports should subside. But the eyes of Edith sought her out, upon the threshold; and dismissing her sensitive parent with a slight kiss on the cheek, she hurried on to Florence and embraced her.\n\n“How do you do, Florence?” said Mr Dombey, putting out his hand.\n\nAs Florence, trembling, raised it to her lips, she met his glance. The look was cold and distant enough, but it stirred her heart to think that she observed in it something more of interest than he had ever shown before. It even expressed a kind of faint surprise, and not a disagreeable surprise, at sight of her. She dared not raise her eyes to his any more; but she felt that he looked at her once again, and not less favourably. Oh what a thrill of joy shot through her, awakened by even this intangible and baseless confirmation of her hope that she would learn to win him, through her new and beautiful Mama!\n\n“You will not be long dressing, Mrs Dombey, I presume?” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“I shall be ready immediately.”\n\n“Let them send up dinner in a quarter of an hour.”\n\nWith that Mr Dombey stalked away to his own dressing-room, and Mrs Dombey went upstairs to hers. Mrs Skewton and Florence repaired to the drawing-room, where that excellent mother considered it incumbent on her to shed a few irrepressible tears, supposed to be forced from her by her daughter’s felicity; and which she was still drying, very gingerly, with a laced corner of her pocket-handkerchief, when her son-in-law appeared.\n\n“And how, my dearest Dombey, did you find that delightfullest of cities, Paris?” she asked, subduing her emotion.\n\n“It was cold,” returned Mr Dombey.\n\n“Gay as ever,” said Mrs Skewton, “of course.\n\n“Not particularly. I thought it dull,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Fie, my dearest Dombey!” archly; “dull!”\n\n“It made that impression upon me, Madam,” said Mr Dombey, with grave politeness. “I believe Mrs Dombey found it dull too. She mentioned once or twice that she thought it so.”\n\n“Why, you naughty girl!” cried Mrs Skewton, rallying her dear child, who now entered, “what dreadfully heretical things have you been saying about Paris?”\n\nEdith raised her eyebrows with an air of weariness; and passing the folding-doors which were thrown open to display the suite of rooms in their new and handsome garniture, and barely glancing at them as she passed, sat down by Florence.\n\n“My dear Dombey,” said Mrs Skewton, “how charmingly these people have carried out every idea that we hinted. They have made a perfect palace of the house, positively.”\n\n“It is handsome,” said Mr Dombey, looking round. “I directed that no expense should be spared; and all that money could do, has been done, I believe.”\n\n“And what can it not do, dear Dombey?” observed Cleopatra.\n\n“It is powerful, Madam,” said Mr Dombey.\n\nHe looked in his solemn way towards his wife, but not a word said she.\n\n“I hope, Mrs Dombey,” addressing her after a moment’s silence, with especial distinctness; “that these alterations meet with your approval?”\n\n“They are as handsome as they can be,” she returned, with haughty carelessness. “They should be so, of course. And I suppose they are.”\n\nAn expression of scorn was habitual to the proud face, and seemed inseparable from it; but the contempt with which it received any appeal to admiration, respect, or consideration on the ground of his riches, no matter how slight or ordinary in itself, was a new and different expression, unequalled in intensity by any other of which it was capable. Whether Mr Dombey, wrapped in his own greatness, was at all aware of this, or no, there had not been wanting opportunities already for his complete enlightenment; and at that moment it might have been effected by the one glance of the dark eye that lighted on him, after it had rapidly and scornfully surveyed the theme of his self-glorification. He might have read in that one glance that nothing that his wealth could do, though it were increased ten thousand fold, could win him for its own sake, one look of softened recognition from the defiant woman, linked to him, but arrayed with her whole soul against him. He might have read in that one glance that even for its sordid and mercenary influence upon herself, she spurned it, while she claimed its utmost power as her right, her bargain—as the base and worthless recompense for which she had become his wife. He might have read in it that, ever baring her own head for the lightning of her own contempt and pride to strike, the most innocent allusion to the power of his riches degraded her anew, sunk her deeper in her own respect, and made the blight and waste within her more complete.\n\nBut dinner was announced, and Mr Dombey led down Cleopatra; Edith and his daughter following. Sweeping past the gold and silver demonstration on the sideboard as if it were heaped-up dirt, and deigning to bestow no look upon the elegancies around her, she took her place at his board for the first time, and sat, like a statue, at the feast.\n\nMr Dombey, being a good deal in the statue way himself, was well enough pleased to see his handsome wife immovable and proud and cold. Her deportment being always elegant and graceful, this as a general behaviour was agreeable and congenial to him. Presiding, therefore, with his accustomed dignity, and not at all reflecting on his wife by any warmth or hilarity of his own, he performed his share of the honours of the table with a cool satisfaction; and the installation dinner, though not regarded downstairs as a great success, or very promising beginning, passed off, above, in a sufficiently polite, genteel, and frosty manner.\n\nSoon after tea, Mrs Skewton, who affected to be quite overcome and worn out by her emotions of happiness, arising in the contemplation of her dear child united to the man of her heart, but who, there is reason to suppose, found this family party somewhat dull, as she yawned for one hour continually behind her fan, retired to bed. Edith, also, silently withdrew and came back no more. Thus, it happened that Florence, who had been upstairs to have some conversation with Diogenes, returning to the drawing-room with her little work-basket, found no one there but her father, who was walking to and fro, in dreary magnificence.\n\n“I beg your pardon. Shall I go away, Papa?” said Florence faintly, hesitating at the door.\n\n“No,” returned Mr Dombey, looking round over his shoulder; “you can come and go here, Florence, as you please. This is not my private room.”\n\nFlorence entered, and sat down at a distant little table with her work: finding herself for the first time in her life—for the very first time within her memory from her infancy to that hour—alone with her father, as his companion. She, his natural companion, his only child, who in her lonely life and grief had known the suffering of a breaking heart; who, in her rejected love, had never breathed his name to God at night, but with a tearful blessing, heavier on him than a curse; who had prayed to die young, so she might only die in his arms; who had, all through, repaid the agony of slight and coldness, and dislike, with patient unexacting love, excusing him, and pleading for him, like his better angel!\n\nShe trembled, and her eyes were dim. His figure seemed to grow in height and bulk before her as he paced the room: now it was all blurred and indistinct; now clear again, and plain; and now she seemed to think that this had happened, just the same, a multitude of years ago. She yearned towards him, and yet shrunk from his approach. Unnatural emotion in a child, innocent of wrong! Unnatural the hand that had directed the sharp plough, which furrowed up her gentle nature for the sowing of its seeds!\n\nBent upon not distressing or offending him by her distress, Florence controlled herself, and sat quietly at her work. After a few more turns across and across the room, he left off pacing it; and withdrawing into a shadowy corner at some distance, where there was an easy chair, covered his head with a handkerchief, and composed himself to sleep.\n\nIt was enough for Florence to sit there watching him; turning her eyes towards his chair from time to time; watching him with her thoughts, when her face was intent upon her work; and sorrowfully glad to think that he could sleep, while she was there, and that he was not made restless by her strange and long-forbidden presence.\n\nWhat would have been her thoughts if she had known that he was steadily regarding her; that the veil upon his face, by accident or by design, was so adjusted that his sight was free, and that it never wandered from her face an instant. That when she looked towards him, in the obscure dark corner, her speaking eyes, more earnest and pathetic in their voiceless speech than all the orators of all the world, and impeaching him more nearly in their mute address, met his, and did not know it! That when she bent her head again over her work, he drew his breath more easily, but with the same attention looked upon her still—upon her white brow and her falling hair, and busy hands; and once attracted, seemed to have no power to turn his eyes away!\n\nAnd what were his thoughts meanwhile? With what emotions did he prolong the attentive gaze covertly directed on his unknown daughter? Was there reproach to him in the quiet figure and the mild eyes? Had he begun to feel her disregarded claims and did they touch him home at last, and waken him to some sense of his cruel injustice?\n\nThere are yielding moments in the lives of the sternest and harshest men, though such men often keep their secret well. The sight of her in her beauty, almost changed into a woman without his knowledge, may have struck out some such moments even in his life of pride. Some passing thought that he had had a happy home within his reach—had had a household spirit bending at his feet—had overlooked it in his stiffnecked sullen arrogance, and wandered away and lost himself, may have engendered them. Some simple eloquence distinctly heard, though only uttered in her eyes, unconscious that he read them as “By the death-beds I have tended, by the childhood I have suffered, by our meeting in this dreary house at midnight, by the cry wrung from me in the anguish of my heart, oh, father, turn to me and seek a refuge in my love before it is too late!” may have arrested them. Meaner and lower thoughts, as that his dead boy was now superseded by new ties, and he could forgive the having been supplanted in his affection, may have occasioned them. The mere association of her as an ornament, with all the ornament and pomp about him, may have been sufficient. But as he looked, he softened to her, more and more. As he looked, she became blended with the child he had loved, and he could hardly separate the two. As he looked, he saw her for an instant by a clearer and a brighter light, not bending over that child’s pillow as his rival—monstrous thought—but as the spirit of his home, and in the action tending himself no less, as he sat once more with his bowed-down head upon his hand at the foot of the little bed. He felt inclined to speak to her, and call her to him. The words “Florence, come here!” were rising to his lips—but slowly and with difficulty, they were so very strange—when they were checked and stifled by a footstep on the stair.\n\nIt was his wife’s. She had exchanged her dinner dress for a loose robe, and unbound her hair, which fell freely about her neck. But this was not the change in her that startled him.\n\n“Florence, dear,” she said, “I have been looking for you everywhere.”\n\nAs she sat down by the side of Florence, she stooped and kissed her hand. He hardly knew his wife. She was so changed. It was not merely that her smile was new to him—though that he had never seen; but her manner, the tone of her voice, the light of her eyes, the interest, and confidence, and winning wish to please, expressed in all-this was not Edith.\n\n“Softly, dear Mama. Papa is asleep.”\n\nIt was Edith now. She looked towards the corner where he was, and he knew that face and manner very well.\n\n“I scarcely thought you could be here, Florence.”\n\nAgain, how altered and how softened, in an instant!\n\n“I left here early,” pursued Edith, “purposely to sit upstairs and talk with you. But, going to your room, I found my bird was flown, and I have been waiting there ever since, expecting its return.\n\nIf it had been a bird, indeed, she could not have taken it more tenderly and gently to her breast, than she did Florence.\n\n“Come, dear!”\n\n“Papa will not expect to find me, I suppose, when he wakes,” hesitated Florence.\n\n“Do you think he will, Florence?” said Edith, looking full upon her.\n\nFlorence drooped her head, and rose, and put up her work-basket. Edith drew her hand through her arm, and they went out of the room like sisters. Her very step was different and new to him, Mr Dombey thought, as his eyes followed her to the door.\n\nHe sat in his shadowy corner so long, that the church clocks struck the hour three times before he moved that night. All that while his face was still intent upon the spot where Florence had been seated. The room grew darker, as the candles waned and went out; but a darkness gathered on his face, exceeding any that the night could cast, and rested there.\n\nFlorence and Edith, seated before the fire in the remote room where little Paul had died, talked together for a long time. Diogenes, who was of the party, had at first objected to the admission of Edith, and, even in deference to his mistress’s wish, had only permitted it under growling protest. But, emerging by little and little from the ante-room, whither he had retired in dudgeon, he soon appeared to comprehend, that with the most amiable intentions he had made one of those mistakes which will occasionally arise in the best-regulated dogs’ minds; as a friendly apology for which he stuck himself up on end between the two, in a very hot place in front of the fire, and sat panting at it, with his tongue out, and a most imbecile expression of countenance, listening to the conversation.\n\nIt turned, at first, on Florence’s books and favourite pursuits, and on the manner in which she had beguiled the interval since the marriage. The last theme opened up to her a subject which lay very near her heart, and she said, with the tears starting to her eyes:\n\n“Oh, Mama! I have had a great sorrow since that day.”\n\n“You a great sorrow, Florence!”\n\n“Yes. Poor Walter is drowned.”\n\nFlorence spread her hands before her face, and wept with all her heart. Many as were the secret tears which Walter’s fate had cost her, they flowed yet, when she thought or spoke of him.\n\n“But tell me, dear,” said Edith, soothing her. “Who was Walter? What was he to you?”\n\n“He was my brother, Mama. After dear Paul died, we said we would be brother and sister. I had known him a long time—from a little child. He knew Paul, who liked him very much; Paul said, almost at the last, ‘Take care of Walter, dear Papa! I was fond of him!’ Walter had been brought in to see him, and was there then—in this room.”\n\n“And did he take care of Walter?” inquired Edith, sternly.\n\n“Papa? He appointed him to go abroad. He was drowned in shipwreck on his voyage,” said Florence, sobbing.\n\n“Does he know that he is dead?” asked Edith.\n\n“I cannot tell, Mama. I have no means of knowing. Dear Mama!” cried Florence, clinging to her as for help, and hiding her face upon her bosom, “I know that you have seen—”\n\n“Stay! Stop, Florence.” Edith turned so pale, and spoke so earnestly, that Florence did not need her restraining hand upon her lips. “Tell me all about Walter first; let me understand this history all through.”\n\nFlorence related it, and everything belonging to it, even down to the friendship of Mr Toots, of whom she could hardly speak in her distress without a tearful smile, although she was deeply grateful to him. When she had concluded her account, to the whole of which Edith, holding her hand, listened with close attention, and when a silence had succeeded, Edith said:\n\n“What is it that you know I have seen, Florence?”\n\n“That I am not,” said Florence, with the same mute appeal, and the same quick concealment of her face as before, “that I am not a favourite child, Mama. I never have been. I have never known how to be. I have missed the way, and had no one to show it to me. Oh, let me learn from you how to become dearer to Papa Teach me! you, who can so well!” and clinging closer to her, with some broken fervent words of gratitude and endearment, Florence, relieved of her sad secret, wept long, but not as painfully as of yore, within the encircling arms of her new mother.\n\nPale even to her lips, and with a face that strove for composure until its proud beauty was as fixed as death, Edith looked down upon the weeping girl, and once kissed her. Then gradually disengaging herself, and putting Florence away, she said, stately, and quiet as a marble image, and in a voice that deepened as she spoke, but had no other token of emotion in it:\n\n“Florence, you do not know me! Heaven forbid that you should learn from me!”\n\n“Not learn from you?” repeated Florence, in surprise.\n\n“That I should teach you how to love, or be loved, Heaven forbid!” said Edith. “If you could teach me, that were better; but it is too late. You are dear to me, Florence. I did not think that anything could ever be so dear to me, as you are in this little time.”\n\nShe saw that Florence would have spoken here, so checked her with her hand, and went on.\n\n“I will be your true friend always. I will cherish you, as much, if not as well as anyone in this world could. You may trust in me—I know it and I say it, dear,—with the whole confidence even of your pure heart. There are hosts of women whom he might have married, better and truer in all other respects than I am, Florence; but there is not one who could come here, his wife, whose heart could beat with greater truth to you than mine does.”\n\n“I know it, dear Mama!” cried Florence. “From that first most happy day I have known it.”\n\n“Most happy day!” Edith seemed to repeat the words involuntarily, and went on. “Though the merit is not mine, for I thought little of you until I saw you, let the undeserved reward be mine in your trust and love. And in this—in this, Florence; on the first night of my taking up my abode here; I am led on as it is best I should be, to say it for the first and last time.”\n\nFlorence, without knowing why, felt almost afraid to hear her proceed, but kept her eyes riveted on the beautiful face so fixed upon her own.\n\n“Never seek to find in me,” said Edith, laying her hand upon her breast, “what is not here. Never if you can help it, Florence, fall off from me because it is not here. Little by little you will know me better, and the time will come when you will know me, as I know myself. Then, be as lenient to me as you can, and do not turn to bitterness the only sweet remembrance I shall have.”\n\nThe tears that were visible in her eyes as she kept them fixed on Florence, showed that the composed face was but as a handsome mask; but she preserved it, and continued:\n\n“I have seen what you say, and know how true it is. But believe me—you will soon, if you cannot now—there is no one on this earth less qualified to set it right or help you, Florence, than I. Never ask me why, or speak to me about it or of my husband, more. There should be, so far, a division, and a silence between us two, like the grave itself.”\n\nShe sat for some time silent; Florence scarcely venturing to breathe meanwhile, as dim and imperfect shadows of the truth, and all its daily consequences, chased each other through her terrified, yet incredulous imagination. Almost as soon as she had ceased to speak, Edith’s face began to subside from its set composure to that quieter and more relenting aspect, which it usually wore when she and Florence were alone together. She shaded it, after this change, with her hands; and when she arose, and with an affectionate embrace bade Florence good-night, went quickly, and without looking round.\n\nBut when Florence was in bed, and the room was dark except for the glow of the fire, Edith returned, and saying that she could not sleep, and that her dressing-room was lonely, drew a chair upon the hearth, and watched the embers as they died away. Florence watched them too from her bed, until they, and the noble figure before them, crowned with its flowing hair, and in its thoughtful eyes reflecting back their light, became confused and indistinct, and finally were lost in slumber.\n\nIn her sleep, however, Florence could not lose an undefined impression of what had so recently passed. It formed the subject of her dreams, and haunted her; now in one shape, now in another; but always oppressively; and with a sense of fear. She dreamed of seeking her father in wildernesses, of following his track up fearful heights, and down into deep mines and caverns; of being charged with something that would release him from extraordinary suffering—she knew not what, or why—yet never being able to attain the goal and set him free. Then she saw him dead, upon that very bed, and in that very room, and knew that he had never loved her to the last, and fell upon his cold breast, passionately weeping. Then a prospect opened, and a river flowed, and a plaintive voice she knew, cried, “It is running on, Floy! It has never stopped! You are moving with it!” And she saw him at a distance stretching out his arms towards her, while a figure such as Walter’s used to be, stood near him, awfully serene and still. In every vision, Edith came and went, sometimes to her joy, sometimes to her sorrow, until they were alone upon the brink of a dark grave, and Edith pointing down, she looked and saw—what!—another Edith lying at the bottom.\n\nIn the terror of this dream, she cried out and awoke, she thought. A soft voice seemed to whisper in her ear, “Florence, dear Florence, it is nothing but a dream!” and stretching out her arms, she returned the caress of her new Mama, who then went out at the door in the light of the grey morning. In a moment, Florence sat up wondering whether this had really taken place or not; but she was only certain that it was grey morning indeed, and that the blackened ashes of the fire were on the hearth, and that she was alone.\n\nSo passed the night on which the happy pair came home.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVI. Housewarming\n\nMany succeeding days passed in like manner; except that there were numerous visits received and paid, and that Mrs Skewton held little levees in her own apartments, at which Major Bagstock was a frequent attendant, and that Florence encountered no second look from her father, although she saw him every day. Nor had she much communication in words with her new Mama, who was imperious and proud to all the house but her—Florence could not but observe that—and who, although she always sent for her or went to her when she came home from visiting, and would always go into her room at night, before retiring to rest, however late the hour, and never lost an opportunity of being with her, was often her silent and thoughtful companion for a long time together.\n\nFlorence, who had hoped for so much from this marriage, could not help sometimes comparing the bright house with the faded dreary place out of which it had arisen, and wondering when, in any shape, it would begin to be a home; for that it was no home then, for anyone, though everything went on luxuriously and regularly, she had always a secret misgiving. Many an hour of sorrowful reflection by day and night, and many a tear of blighted hope, Florence bestowed upon the assurance her new Mama had given her so strongly, that there was no one on the earth more powerless than herself to teach her how to win her father’s heart. And soon Florence began to think—resolved to think would be the truer phrase—that as no one knew so well, how hopeless of being subdued or changed her father’s coldness to her was, so she had given her this warning, and forbidden the subject in very compassion. Unselfish here, as in her every act and fancy, Florence preferred to bear the pain of this new wound, rather than encourage any faint foreshadowings of the truth as it concerned her father; tender of him, even in her wandering thoughts. As for his home, she hoped it would become a better one, when its state of novelty and transition should be over; and for herself, thought little and lamented less.\n\nIf none of the new family were particularly at home in private, it was resolved that Mrs Dombey at least should be at home in public, without delay. A series of entertainments in celebration of the late nuptials, and in cultivation of society, were arranged, chiefly by Mr Dombey and Mrs Skewton; and it was settled that the festive proceedings should commence by Mrs Dombey’s being at home upon a certain evening, and by Mr and Mrs Dombey’s requesting the honour of the company of a great many incongruous people to dinner on the same day.\n\nAccordingly, Mr Dombey produced a list of sundry eastern magnates who were to be bidden to this feast on his behalf; to which Mrs Skewton, acting for her dearest child, who was haughtily careless on the subject, subjoined a western list, comprising Cousin Feenix, not yet returned to Baden-Baden, greatly to the detriment of his personal estate; and a variety of moths of various degrees and ages, who had, at various times, fluttered round the light of her fair daughter, or herself, without any lasting injury to their wings. Florence was enrolled as a member of the dinner-party, by Edith’s command—elicited by a moment’s doubt and hesitation on the part of Mrs Skewton; and Florence, with a wondering heart, and with a quick instinctive sense of everything that grated on her father in the least, took her silent share in the proceedings of the day.\n\nThe proceedings commenced by Mr Dombey, in a cravat of extraordinary height and stiffness, walking restlessly about the drawing-room until the hour appointed for dinner; punctual to which, an East India Director, of immense wealth, in a waistcoat apparently constructed in serviceable deal by some plain carpenter, but really engendered in the tailor’s art, and composed of the material called nankeen, arrived and was received by Mr Dombey alone. The next stage of the proceedings was Mr Dombey’s sending his compliments to Mrs Dombey, with a correct statement of the time; and the next, the East India Director’s falling prostrate, in a conversational point of view, and as Mr Dombey was not the man to pick him up, staring at the fire until rescue appeared in the shape of Mrs Skewton; whom the director, as a pleasant start in life for the evening, mistook for Mrs Dombey, and greeted with enthusiasm.\n\nThe next arrival was a Bank Director, reputed to be able to buy up anything—human Nature generally, if he should take it in his head to influence the money market in that direction—but who was a wonderfully modest-spoken man, almost boastfully so, and mentioned his “little place” at Kingston-upon-Thames, and its just being barely equal to giving Dombey a bed and a chop, if he would come and visit it. Ladies, he said, it was not for a man who lived in his quiet way to take upon himself to invite—but if Mrs Skewton and her daughter, Mrs Dombey, should ever find themselves in that direction, and would do him the honour to look at a little bit of a shrubbery they would find there, and a poor little flower-bed or so, and a humble apology for a pinery, and two or three little attempts of that sort without any pretension, they would distinguish him very much. Carrying out his character, this gentleman was very plainly dressed, in a wisp of cambric for a neckcloth, big shoes, a coat that was too loose for him, and a pair of trousers that were too spare; and mention being made of the Opera by Mrs Skewton, he said he very seldom went there, for he couldn’t afford it. It seemed greatly to delight and exhilarate him to say so: and he beamed on his audience afterwards, with his hands in his pockets, and excessive satisfaction twinkling in his eyes.\n\nNow Mrs Dombey appeared, beautiful and proud, and as disdainful and defiant of them all as if the bridal wreath upon her head had been a garland of steel spikes put on to force concession from her which she would die sooner than yield. With her was Florence. When they entered together, the shadow of the night of the return again darkened Mr Dombey’s face. But unobserved; for Florence did not venture to raise her eyes to his, and Edith’s indifference was too supreme to take the least heed of him.\n\nThe arrivals quickly became numerous. More directors, chairmen of public companies, elderly ladies carrying burdens on their heads for full dress, Cousin Feenix, Major Bagstock, friends of Mrs Skewton, with the same bright bloom on their complexion, and very precious necklaces on very withered necks. Among these, a young lady of sixty-five, remarkably coolly dressed as to her back and shoulders, who spoke with an engaging lisp, and whose eyelids wouldn’t keep up well, without a great deal of trouble on her part, and whose manners had that indefinable charm which so frequently attaches to the giddiness of youth. As the greater part of Mr Dombey’s list were disposed to be taciturn, and the greater part of Mrs Dombey’s list were disposed to be talkative, and there was no sympathy between them, Mrs Dombey’s list, by magnetic agreement, entered into a bond of union against Mr Dombey’s list, who, wandering about the rooms in a desolate manner, or seeking refuge in corners, entangled themselves with company coming in, and became barricaded behind sofas, and had doors opened smartly from without against their heads, and underwent every sort of discomfiture.\n\nWhen dinner was announced, Mr Dombey took down an old lady like a crimson velvet pincushion stuffed with bank notes, who might have been the identical old lady of Threadneedle Street, she was so rich, and looked so unaccommodating; Cousin Feenix took down Mrs Dombey; Major Bagstock took down Mrs Skewton; the young thing with the shoulders was bestowed, as an extinguisher, upon the East India Director; and the remaining ladies were left on view in the drawing-room by the remaining gentlemen, until a forlorn hope volunteered to conduct them downstairs, and those brave spirits with their captives blocked up the dining-room door, shutting out seven mild men in the stony-hearted hall. When all the rest were got in and were seated, one of these mild men still appeared, in smiling confusion, totally destitute and unprovided for, and, escorted by the butler, made the complete circuit of the table twice before his chair could be found, which it finally was, on Mrs Dombey’s left hand; after which the mild man never held up his head again.\n\nNow, the spacious dining-room, with the company seated round the glittering table, busy with their glittering spoons, and knives and forks, and plates, might have been taken for a grown-up exposition of Tom Tiddler’s ground, where children pick up gold and silver. Mr Dombey, as Tiddler, looked his character to admiration; and the long plateau of precious metal frosted, separating him from Mrs Dombey, whereon frosted Cupids offered scentless flowers to each of them, was allegorical to see.\n\nCousin Feenix was in great force, and looked astonishingly young. But he was sometimes thoughtless in his good humour—his memory occasionally wandering like his legs—and on this occasion caused the company to shudder. It happened thus. The young lady with the back, who regarded Cousin Feenix with sentiments of tenderness, had entrapped the East India Director into leading her to the chair next him; in return for which good office, she immediately abandoned the Director, who, being shaded on the other side by a gloomy black velvet hat surmounting a bony and speechless female with a fan, yielded to a depression of spirits and withdrew into himself. Cousin Feenix and the young lady were very lively and humorous, and the young lady laughed so much at something Cousin Feenix related to her, that Major Bagstock begged leave to inquire on behalf of Mrs Skewton (they were sitting opposite, a little lower down), whether that might not be considered public property.\n\n“Why, upon my life,” said Cousin Feenix, “there’s nothing in it; it really is not worth repeating: in point of fact, it’s merely an anecdote of Jack Adams. I dare say my friend Dombey;” for the general attention was concentrated on Cousin Feenix; “may remember Jack Adams, Jack Adams, not Joe; that was his brother. Jack—little Jack—man with a cast in his eye, and slight impediment in his speech—man who sat for somebody’s borough. We used to call him in my parliamentary time W. P. Adams, in consequence of his being Warming Pan for a young fellow who was in his minority. Perhaps my friend Dombey may have known the man?”\n\nMr Dombey, who was as likely to have known Guy Fawkes, replied in the negative. But one of the seven mild men unexpectedly leaped into distinction, by saying he had known him, and adding—“always wore Hessian boots!”\n\n“Exactly,” said Cousin Feenix, bending forward to see the mild man, and smile encouragement at him down the table. “That was Jack. Joe wore—”\n\n“Tops!” cried the mild man, rising in public estimation every Instant.\n\n“Of course,” said Cousin Feenix, “you were intimate with em?”\n\n“I knew them both,” said the mild man. With whom Mr Dombey immediately took wine.\n\n“Devilish good fellow, Jack!” said Cousin Feenix, again bending forward, and smiling.\n\n“Excellent,” returned the mild man, becoming bold on his success. “One of the best fellows I ever knew.”\n\n“No doubt you have heard the story?” said Cousin Feenix.\n\n“I shall know,” replied the bold mild man, “when I have heard your Ludship tell it.” With that, he leaned back in his chair and smiled at the ceiling, as knowing it by heart, and being already tickled.\n\n“In point of fact, it’s nothing of a story in itself,” said Cousin Feenix, addressing the table with a smile, and a gay shake of his head, “and not worth a word of preface. But it’s illustrative of the neatness of Jack’s humour. The fact is, that Jack was invited down to a marriage—which I think took place in Berkshire?”\n\n“Shropshire,” said the bold mild man, finding himself appealed to.\n\n“Was it? Well! In point of fact it might have been in any shire,” said Cousin Feenix. “So my friend being invited down to this marriage in Anyshire,” with a pleasant sense of the readiness of this joke, “goes. Just as some of us, having had the honour of being invited to the marriage of my lovely and accomplished relative with my friend Dombey, didn’t require to be asked twice, and were devilish glad to be present on so interesting an occasion.—Goes—Jack goes. Now, this marriage was, in point of fact, the marriage of an uncommonly fine girl with a man for whom she didn’t care a button, but whom she accepted on account of his property, which was immense. When Jack returned to town, after the nuptials, a man he knew, meeting him in the lobby of the House of Commons, says, ‘Well, Jack, how are the ill-matched couple?’ ‘Ill-matched,’ says Jack ‘Not at all. It’s a perfectly and equal transaction. She is regularly bought, and you may take your oath he is as regularly sold!’”\n\nIn his full enjoyment of this culminating point of his story, the shudder, which had gone all round the table like an electric spark, struck Cousin Feenix, and he stopped. Not a smile occasioned by the only general topic of conversation broached that day, appeared on any face. A profound silence ensued; and the wretched mild man, who had been as innocent of any real foreknowledge of the story as the child unborn, had the exquisite misery of reading in every eye that he was regarded as the prime mover of the mischief.\n\nMr Dombey’s face was not a changeful one, and being cast in its mould of state that day, showed little other apprehension of the story, if any, than that which he expressed when he said solemnly, amidst the silence, that it was “Very good.” There was a rapid glance from Edith towards Florence, but otherwise she remained, externally, impassive and unconscious.\n\nThrough the various stages of rich meats and wines, continual gold and silver, dainties of earth, air, fire, and water, heaped-up fruits, and that unnecessary article in Mr Dombey’s banquets—ice—the dinner slowly made its way: the later stages being achieved to the sonorous music of incessant double knocks, announcing the arrival of visitors, whose portion of the feast was limited to the smell thereof. When Mrs Dombey rose, it was a sight to see her lord, with stiff throat and erect head, hold the door open for the withdrawal of the ladies; and to see how she swept past him with his daughter on her arm.\n\nMr Dombey was a grave sight, behind the decanters, in a state of dignity; and the East India Director was a forlorn sight near the unoccupied end of the table, in a state of solitude; and the Major was a military sight, relating stories of the Duke of York to six of the seven mild men (the ambitious one was utterly quenched); and the Bank Director was a lowly sight, making a plan of his little attempt at a pinery, with dessert-knives, for a group of admirers; and Cousin Feenix was a thoughtful sight, as he smoothed his long wristbands and stealthily adjusted his wig. But all these sights were of short duration, being speedily broken up by coffee, and the desertion of the room.\n\nThere was a throng in the state-rooms upstairs, increasing every minute; but still Mr Dombey’s list of visitors appeared to have some native impossibility of amalgamation with Mrs Dombey’s list, and no one could have doubted which was which. The single exception to this rule perhaps was Mr Carker, who now smiled among the company, and who, as he stood in the circle that was gathered about Mrs Dombey—watchful of her, of them, his chief, Cleopatra and the Major, Florence, and everything around—appeared at ease with both divisions of guests, and not marked as exclusively belonging to either.\n\nFlorence had a dread of him, which made his presence in the room a nightmare to her. She could not avoid the recollection of it, for her eyes were drawn towards him every now and then, by an attraction of dislike and distrust that she could not resist. Yet her thoughts were busy with other things; for as she sat apart—not unadmired or unsought, but in the gentleness of her quiet spirit—she felt how little part her father had in what was going on, and saw, with pain, how ill at ease he seemed to be, and how little regarded he was as he lingered about near the door, for those visitors whom he wished to distinguish with particular attention, and took them up to introduce them to his wife, who received them with proud coldness, but showed no interest or wish to please, and never, after the bare ceremony of reception, in consultation of his wishes, or in welcome of his friends, opened her lips. It was not the less perplexing or painful to Florence, that she who acted thus, treated her so kindly and with such loving consideration, that it almost seemed an ungrateful return on her part even to know of what was passing before her eyes.\n\nHappy Florence would have been, might she have ventured to bear her father company, by so much as a look; and happy Florence was, in little suspecting the main cause of his uneasiness. But afraid of seeming to know that he was placed at any disadvantage, lest he should be resentful of that knowledge; and divided between her impulse towards him, and her grateful affection for Edith; she scarcely dared to raise her eyes towards either. Anxious and unhappy for them both, the thought stole on her through the crowd, that it might have been better for them if this noise of tongues and tread of feet had never come there,—if the old dulness and decay had never been replaced by novelty and splendour,—if the neglected child had found no friend in Edith, but had lived her solitary life, unpitied and forgotten.\n\nMrs Chick had some such thoughts too, but they were not so quietly developed in her mind. This good matron had been outraged in the first instance by not receiving an invitation to dinner. That blow partially recovered, she had gone to a vast expense to make such a figure before Mrs Dombey at home, as should dazzle the senses of that lady, and heap mortification, mountains high, on the head of Mrs Skewton.\n\n“But I am made,” said Mrs Chick to Mr Chick, “of no more account than Florence! Who takes the smallest notice of me? No one!”\n\n“No one, my dear,” assented Mr Chick, who was seated by the side of Mrs Chick against the wall, and could console himself, even there, by softly whistling.\n\n“Does it at all appear as if I was wanted here?” exclaimed Mrs Chick, with flashing eyes.\n\n“No, my dear, I don’t think it does,” said Mr Chick.\n\n“Paul’s mad!” said Mrs Chick.\n\nMr Chick whistled.\n\n“Unless you are a monster, which I sometimes think you are,” said Mrs Chick with candour, “don’t sit there humming tunes. How anyone with the most distant feelings of a man, can see that mother-in-law of Paul’s, dressed as she is, going on like that, with Major Bagstock, for whom, among other precious things, we are indebted to your Lucretia Tox.”\n\n“My Lucretia Tox, my dear!” said Mr Chick, astounded.\n\n“Yes,” retorted Mrs Chick, with great severity, “your Lucretia Tox—I say how anybody can see that mother-in-law of Paul’s, and that haughty wife of Paul’s, and these indecent old frights with their backs and shoulders, and in short this at home generally, and hum—” on which word Mrs Chick laid a scornful emphasis that made Mr Chick start, “is, I thank Heaven, a mystery to me!”\n\nMr Chick screwed his mouth into a form irreconcilable with humming or whistling, and looked very contemplative.\n\n“But I hope I know what is due to myself,” said Mrs Chick, swelling with indignation, “though Paul has forgotten what is due to me. I am not going to sit here, a member of this family, to be taken no notice of. I am not the dirt under Mrs Dombey’s feet, yet—not quite yet,” said Mrs Chick, as if she expected to become so, about the day after to-morrow. “And I shall go. I will not say (whatever I may think) that this affair has been got up solely to degrade and insult me. I shall merely go. I shall not be missed!”\n\nMrs Chick rose erect with these words, and took the arm of Mr Chick, who escorted her from the room, after half an hour’s shady sojourn there. And it is due to her penetration to observe that she certainly was not missed at all.\n\nBut she was not the only indignant guest; for Mr Dombey’s list (still constantly in difficulties) were, as a body, indignant with Mrs Dombey’s list, for looking at them through eyeglasses, and audibly wondering who all those people were; while Mrs Dombey’s list complained of weariness, and the young thing with the shoulders, deprived of the attentions of that gay youth Cousin Feenix (who went away from the dinner-table), confidentially alleged to thirty or forty friends that she was bored to death. All the old ladies with the burdens on their heads, had greater or less cause of complaint against Mr Dombey; and the Directors and Chairmen coincided in thinking that if Dombey must marry, he had better have married somebody nearer his own age, not quite so handsome, and a little better off. The general opinion among this class of gentlemen was, that it was a weak thing in Dombey, and he’d live to repent it. Hardly anybody there, except the mild men, stayed, or went away, without considering himself or herself neglected and aggrieved by Mr Dombey or Mrs Dombey; and the speechless female in the black velvet hat was found to have been stricken mute, because the lady in the crimson velvet had been handed down before her. The nature even of the mild men got corrupted, either from their curdling it with too much lemonade, or from the general inoculation that prevailed; and they made sarcastic jokes to one another, and whispered disparagement on stairs and in bye-places. The general dissatisfaction and discomfort so diffused itself, that the assembled footmen in the hall were as well acquainted with it as the company above. Nay, the very linkmen outside got hold of it, and compared the party to a funeral out of mourning, with none of the company remembered in the will.\n\nAt last, the guests were all gone, and the linkmen too; and the street, crowded so long with carriages, was clear; and the dying lights showed no one in the rooms, but Mr Dombey and Mr Carker, who were talking together apart, and Mrs Dombey and her mother: the former seated on an ottoman; the latter reclining in the Cleopatra attitude, awaiting the arrival of her maid. Mr Dombey having finished his communication to Carker, the latter advanced obsequiously to take leave.\n\n“I trust,” he said, “that the fatigues of this delightful evening will not inconvenience Mrs Dombey to-morrow.”\n\n“Mrs Dombey,” said Mr Dombey, advancing, “has sufficiently spared herself fatigue, to relieve you from any anxiety of that kind. I regret to say, Mrs Dombey, that I could have wished you had fatigued yourself a little more on this occasion.\n\nShe looked at him with a supercilious glance, that it seemed not worth her while to protract, and turned away her eyes without speaking.\n\n“I am sorry, Madam,” said Mr Dombey, “that you should not have thought it your duty—”\n\nShe looked at him again.\n\n“Your duty, Madam,” pursued Mr Dombey, “to have received my friends with a little more deference. Some of those whom you have been pleased to slight tonight in a very marked manner, Mrs Dombey, confer a distinction upon you, I must tell you, in any visit they pay you.”\n\n“Do you know that there is someone here?” she returned, now looking at him steadily.\n\n“No! Carker! I beg that you do not. I insist that you do not,” cried Mr Dombey, stopping that noiseless gentleman in his withdrawal. “Mr Carker, Madam, as you know, possesses my confidence. He is as well acquainted as myself with the subject on which I speak. I beg to tell you, for your information, Mrs Dombey, that I consider these wealthy and important persons confer a distinction upon me:” and Mr Dombey drew himself up, as having now rendered them of the highest possible importance.\n\n“I ask you,” she repeated, bending her disdainful, steady gaze upon him, “do you know that there is someone here, Sir?”\n\n“I must entreat,” said Mr Carker, stepping forward, “I must beg, I must demand, to be released. Slight and unimportant as this difference is—”\n\nMrs Skewton, who had been intent upon her daughter’s face, took him up here.\n\n“My sweetest Edith,” she said, “and my dearest Dombey; our excellent friend Mr Carker, for so I am sure I ought to mention him—”\n\nMr Carker murmured, “Too much honour.”\n\n“—has used the very words that were in my mind, and that I have been dying, these ages, for an opportunity of introducing. Slight and unimportant! My sweetest Edith, and my dearest Dombey, do we not know that any difference between you two—No, Flowers; not now.”\n\nFlowers was the maid, who, finding gentlemen present, retreated with precipitation.\n\n“That any difference between you two,” resumed Mrs Skewton, “with the Heart you possess in common, and the excessively charming bond of feeling that there is between you, must be slight and unimportant? What words could better define the fact? None. Therefore I am glad to take this slight occasion—this trifling occasion, that is so replete with Nature, and your individual characters, and all that—so truly calculated to bring the tears into a parent’s eyes—to say that I attach no importance to them in the least, except as developing these minor elements of Soul; and that, unlike most Mamas-in-law (that odious phrase, dear Dombey!) as they have been represented to me to exist in this I fear too artificial world, I never shall attempt to interpose between you, at such a time, and never can much regret, after all, such little flashes of the torch of What’s-his-name—not Cupid, but the other delightful creature.”\n\nThere was a sharpness in the good mother’s glance at both her children as she spoke, that may have been expressive of a direct and well-considered purpose hidden between these rambling words. That purpose, providently to detach herself in the beginning from all the clankings of their chain that were to come, and to shelter herself with the fiction of her innocent belief in their mutual affection, and their adaptation to each other.\n\n“I have pointed out to Mrs Dombey,” said Mr Dombey, in his most stately manner, “that in her conduct thus early in our married life, to which I object, and which, I request, may be corrected. Carker,” with a nod of dismissal, “good-night to you!”\n\nMr Carker bowed to the imperious form of the Bride, whose sparkling eye was fixed upon her husband; and stopping at Cleopatra’s couch on his way out, raised to his lips the hand she graciously extended to him, in lowly and admiring homage.\n\nIf his handsome wife had reproached him, or even changed countenance, or broken the silence in which she remained, by one word, now that they were alone (for Cleopatra made off with all speed), Mr Dombey would have been equal to some assertion of his case against her. But the intense, unutterable, withering scorn, with which, after looking upon him, she dropped her eyes, as if he were too worthless and indifferent to her to be challenged with a syllable—the ineffable disdain and haughtiness in which she sat before him—the cold inflexible resolve with which her every feature seemed to bear him down, and put him by—these, he had no resource against; and he left her, with her whole overbearing beauty concentrated on despising him.\n\nWas he coward enough to watch her, an hour afterwards, on the old well staircase, where he had once seen Florence in the moonlight, toiling up with Paul? Or was he in the dark by accident, when, looking up, he saw her coming, with a light, from the room where Florence lay, and marked again the face so changed, which he could not subdue?\n\nBut it could never alter as his own did. It never, in its uttermost pride and passion, knew the shadow that had fallen on his, in the dark corner, on the night of the return; and often since; and which deepened on it now, as he looked up.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII. More Warnings than One\n\nFlorence, Edith, and Mrs Skewton were together next day, and the carriage was waiting at the door to take them out. For Cleopatra had her galley again now, and Withers, no longer the wan, stood upright in a pigeon-breasted jacket and military trousers, behind her wheel-less chair at dinner-time and butted no more. The hair of Withers was radiant with pomatum, in these days of down, and he wore kid gloves and smelt of the water of Cologne.\n\nThey were assembled in Cleopatra’s room. The Serpent of old Nile (not to mention her disrespectfully) was reposing on her sofa, sipping her morning chocolate at three o’clock in the afternoon, and Flowers the Maid was fastening on her youthful cuffs and frills, and performing a kind of private coronation ceremony on her, with a peach-coloured velvet bonnet; the artificial roses in which nodded to uncommon advantage, as the palsy trifled with them, like a breeze.\n\n“I think I am a little nervous this morning, Flowers,” said Mrs Skewton. “My hand quite shakes.”\n\n“You were the life of the party last night, Ma’am, you know,” returned Flowers, “and you suffer for it today, you see.”\n\nEdith, who had beckoned Florence to the window, and was looking out, with her back turned on the toilet of her esteemed mother, suddenly withdrew from it, as if it had lightened.\n\n“My darling child,” cried Cleopatra, languidly, “you are not nervous? Don’t tell me, my dear Edith, that you, so enviably self-possessed, are beginning to be a martyr too, like your unfortunately constituted mother! Withers, someone at the door.”\n\n“Card, Ma’am,” said Withers, taking it towards Mrs Dombey.\n\n“I am going out,” she said without looking at it.\n\n“My dear love,” drawled Mrs Skewton, “how very odd to send that message without seeing the name! Bring it here, Withers. Dear me, my love; Mr Carker, too! That very sensible person!”\n\n“I am going out,” repeated Edith, in so imperious a tone that Withers, going to the door, imperiously informed the servant who was waiting, “Mrs Dombey is going out. Get along with you,” and shut it on him.\n\nBut the servant came back after a short absence, and whispered to Withers again, who once more, and not very willingly, presented himself before Mrs Dombey.\n\n“If you please, Ma’am, Mr Carker sends his respectful compliments, and begs you would spare him one minute, if you could—for business, Ma’am, if you please.”\n\n“Really, my love,” said Mrs Skewton in her mildest manner; for her daughter’s face was threatening; “if you would allow me to offer a word, I should recommend—”\n\n“Show him this way,” said Edith. As Withers disappeared to execute the command, she added, frowning on her mother, “As he comes at your recommendation, let him come to your room.”\n\n“May I—shall I go away?” asked Florence, hurriedly.\n\nEdith nodded yes, but on her way to the door Florence met the visitor coming in. With the same disagreeable mixture of familiarity and forbearance, with which he had first addressed her, he addressed her now in his softest manner—hoped she was quite well—needed not to ask, with such looks to anticipate the answer—had scarcely had the honour to know her, last night, she was so greatly changed—and held the door open for her to pass out; with a secret sense of power in her shrinking from him, that all the deference and politeness of his manner could not quite conceal.\n\nHe then bowed himself for a moment over Mrs Skewton’s condescending hand, and lastly bowed to Edith. Coldly returning his salute without looking at him, and neither seating herself nor inviting him to be seated, she waited for him to speak.\n\nEntrenched in her pride and power, and with all the obduracy of her spirit summoned about her, still her old conviction that she and her mother had been known by this man in their worst colours, from their first acquaintance; that every degradation she had suffered in her own eyes was as plain to him as to herself; that he read her life as though it were a vile book, and fluttered the leaves before her in slight looks and tones of voice which no one else could detect; weakened and undermined her. Proudly as she opposed herself to him, with her commanding face exacting his humility, her disdainful lip repulsing him, her bosom angry at his intrusion, and the dark lashes of her eyes sullenly veiling their light, that no ray of it might shine upon him—and submissively as he stood before her, with an entreating injured manner, but with complete submission to her will—she knew, in her own soul, that the cases were reversed, and that the triumph and superiority were his, and that he knew it full well.\n\n“I have presumed,” said Mr Carker, “to solicit an interview, and I have ventured to describe it as being one of business, because—”\n\n“Perhaps you are charged by Mr Dombey with some message of reproof,” said Edith “You possess Mr Dombey’s confidence in such an unusual degree, Sir, that you would scarcely surprise me if that were your business.”\n\n“I have no message to the lady who sheds a lustre upon his name,” said Mr Carker. “But I entreat that lady, on my own behalf, to be just to a very humble claimant for justice at her hands—a mere dependant of Mr Dombey’s—which is a position of humility; and to reflect upon my perfect helplessness last night, and the impossibility of my avoiding the share that was forced upon me in a very painful occasion.”\n\n“My dearest Edith,” hinted Cleopatra in a low voice, as she held her eye-glass aside, “really very charming of Mr What’s-his-name. And full of heart!”\n\n“For I do,” said Mr Carker, appealing to Mrs Skewton with a look of grateful deference,—“I do venture to call it a painful occasion, though merely because it was so to me, who had the misfortune to be present. So slight a difference, as between the principals—between those who love each other with disinterested devotion, and would make any sacrifice of self in such a cause—is nothing. As Mrs Skewton herself expressed, with so much truth and feeling last night, it is nothing.”\n\nEdith could not look at him, but she said after a few moments.\n\n“And your business, Sir—”\n\n“Edith, my pet,” said Mrs Skewton, “all this time Mr Carker is standing! My dear Mr Carker, take a seat, I beg.”\n\nHe offered no reply to the mother, but fixed his eyes on the proud daughter, as though he would only be bidden by her, and was resolved to be bidden by her. Edith, in spite of herself, sat down, and slightly motioned with her hand to him to be seated too. No action could be colder, haughtier, more insolent in its air of supremacy and disrespect, but she had struggled against even that concession ineffectually, and it was wrested from her. That was enough! Mr Carker sat down.\n\n“May I be allowed, Madam,” said Carker, turning his white teeth on Mrs Skewton like a light—“a lady of your excellent sense and quick feeling will give me credit, for good reason, I am sure—to address what I have to say, to Mrs Dombey, and to leave her to impart it to you who are her best and dearest friend—next to Mr Dombey?”\n\nMrs Skewton would have retired, but Edith stopped her. Edith would have stopped him too, and indignantly ordered him to speak openly or not at all, but that he said, in a low Voice—“Miss Florence—the young lady who has just left the room—”\n\nEdith suffered him to proceed. She looked at him now. As he bent forward, to be nearer, with the utmost show of delicacy and respect, and with his teeth persuasively arrayed, in a self-depreciating smile, she felt as if she could have struck him dead.\n\n“Miss Florence’s position,” he began, “has been an unfortunate one. I have a difficulty in alluding to it to you, whose attachment to her father is naturally watchful and jealous of every word that applies to him.” Always distinct and soft in speech, no language could describe the extent of his distinctness and softness, when he said these words, or came to any others of a similar import. “But, as one who is devoted to Mr Dombey in his different way, and whose life is passed in admiration of Mr Dombey’s character, may I say, without offence to your tenderness as a wife, that Miss Florence has unhappily been neglected—by her father. May I say by her father?”\n\nEdith replied, “I know it.”\n\n“You know it!” said Mr Carker, with a great appearance of relief. “It removes a mountain from my breast. May I hope you know how the neglect originated; in what an amiable phase of Mr Dombey’s pride—character I mean?”\n\n“You may pass that by, Sir,” she returned, “and come the sooner to the end of what you have to say.”\n\n“Indeed, I am sensible, Madam,” replied Carker,—“trust me, I am deeply sensible, that Mr Dombey can require no justification in anything to you. But, kindly judge of my breast by your own, and you will forgive my interest in him, if in its excess, it goes at all astray.”\n\nWhat a stab to her proud heart, to sit there, face to face with him, and have him tendering her false oath at the altar again and again for her acceptance, and pressing it upon her like the dregs of a sickening cup she could not own her loathing of, or turn away from! How shame, remorse, and passion raged within her, when, upright and majestic in her beauty before him, she knew that in her spirit she was down at his feet!\n\n“Miss Florence,” said Carker, “left to the care—if one may call it care—of servants and mercenary people, in every way her inferiors, necessarily wanted some guide and compass in her younger days, and, naturally, for want of them, has been indiscreet, and has in some degree forgotten her station. There was some folly about one Walter, a common lad, who is fortunately dead now: and some very undesirable association, I regret to say, with certain coasting sailors, of anything but good repute, and a runaway old bankrupt.”\n\n“I have heard the circumstances, Sir,” said Edith, flashing her disdainful glance upon him, “and I know that you pervert them. You may not know it. I hope so.”\n\n“Pardon me,” said Mr Carker, “I believe that nobody knows them so well as I. Your generous and ardent nature, Madam—the same nature which is so nobly imperative in vindication of your beloved and honoured husband, and which has blessed him as even his merits deserve—I must respect, defer to, bow before. But, as regards the circumstances, which is indeed the business I presumed to solicit your attention to, I can have no doubt, since, in the execution of my trust as Mr Dombey’s confidential—I presume to say—friend, I have fully ascertained them. In my execution of that trust; in my deep concern, which you can so well understand, for everything relating to him, intensified, if you will (for I fear I labour under your displeasure), by the lower motive of desire to prove my diligence, and make myself the more acceptable; I have long pursued these circumstances by myself and trustworthy instruments, and have innumerable and most minute proofs.”\n\nShe raised her eyes no higher than his mouth, but she saw the means of mischief vaunted in every tooth it contained.\n\n“Pardon me, Madam,” he continued, “if in my perplexity, I presume to take counsel with you, and to consult your pleasure. I think I have observed that you are greatly interested in Miss Florence?”\n\nWhat was there in her he had not observed, and did not know? Humbled and yet maddened by the thought, in every new presentment of it, however faint, she pressed her teeth upon her quivering lip to force composure on it, and distantly inclined her head in reply.\n\n“This interest, Madam—so touching an evidence of everything associated with Mr Dombey being dear to you—induces me to pause before I make him acquainted with these circumstances, which, as yet, he does not know. It so shakes me, if I may make the confession, in my allegiance, that on the intimation of the least desire to that effect from you, I would suppress them.”\n\nEdith raised her head quickly, and starting back, bent her dark glance upon him. He met it with his blandest and most deferential smile, and went on.\n\n“You say that as I describe them, they are perverted. I fear not—I fear not: but let us assume that they are. The uneasiness I have for some time felt on the subject, arises in this: that the mere circumstance of such association often repeated, on the part of Miss Florence, however innocently and confidingly, would be conclusive with Mr Dombey, already predisposed against her, and would lead him to take some step (I know he has occasionally contemplated it) of separation and alienation of her from his home. Madam, bear with me, and remember my intercourse with Mr Dombey, and my knowledge of him, and my reverence for him, almost from childhood, when I say that if he has a fault, it is a lofty stubbornness, rooted in that noble pride and sense of power which belong to him, and which we must all defer to; which is not assailable like the obstinacy of other characters; and which grows upon itself from day to day, and year to year.”\n\nShe bent her glance upon him still; but, look as steadfast as she would, her haughty nostrils dilated, and her breath came somewhat deeper, and her lip would slightly curl, as he described that in his patron to which they must all bow down. He saw it; and though his expression did not change, she knew he saw it.\n\n“Even so slight an incident as last night’s,” he said, “if I might refer to it once more, would serve to illustrate my meaning, better than a greater one. Dombey and Son know neither time, nor place, nor season, but bear them all down. But I rejoice in its occurrence, for it has opened the way for me to approach Mrs Dombey with this subject today, even if it has entailed upon me the penalty of her temporary displeasure. Madam, in the midst of my uneasiness and apprehension on this subject, I was summoned by Mr Dombey to Leamington. There I saw you. There I could not help knowing what relation you would shortly occupy towards him—to his enduring happiness and yours. There I resolved to await the time of your establishment at home here, and to do as I have now done. I have, at heart, no fear that I shall be wanting in my duty to Mr Dombey, if I bury what I know in your breast; for where there is but one heart and mind between two persons—as in such a marriage—one almost represents the other. I can acquit my conscience therefore, almost equally, by confidence, on such a theme, in you or him. For the reasons I have mentioned I would select you. May I aspire to the distinction of believing that my confidence is accepted, and that I am relieved from my responsibility?”\n\nHe long remembered the look she gave him—who could see it, and forget it?—and the struggle that ensued within her. At last she said:\n\n“I accept it, Sir You will please to consider this matter at an end, and that it goes no farther.”\n\nHe bowed low, and rose. She rose too, and he took leave with all humility. But Withers, meeting him on the stairs, stood amazed at the beauty of his teeth, and at his brilliant smile; and as he rode away upon his white-legged horse, the people took him for a dentist, such was the dazzling show he made. The people took her, when she rode out in her carriage presently, for a great lady, as happy as she was rich and fine. But they had not seen her, just before, in her own room with no one by; and they had not heard her utterance of the three words, “Oh Florence, Florence!”\n\nMrs Skewton, reposing on her sofa, and sipping her chocolate, had heard nothing but the low word business, for which she had a mortal aversion, insomuch that she had long banished it from her vocabulary, and had gone nigh, in a charming manner and with an immense amount of heart, to say nothing of soul, to ruin divers milliners and others in consequence. Therefore Mrs Skewton asked no questions, and showed no curiosity. Indeed, the peach-velvet bonnet gave her sufficient occupation out of doors; for being perched on the back of her head, and the day being rather windy, it was frantic to escape from Mrs Skewton’s company, and would be coaxed into no sort of compromise. When the carriage was closed, and the wind shut out, the palsy played among the artificial roses again like an almshouse-full of superannuated zephyrs; and altogether Mrs Skewton had enough to do, and got on but indifferently.\n\nShe got on no better towards night; for when Mrs Dombey, in her dressing-room, had been dressed and waiting for her half an hour, and Mr Dombey, in the drawing-room, had paraded himself into a state of solemn fretfulness (they were all three going out to dinner), Flowers the Maid appeared with a pale face to Mrs Dombey, saying:\n\n“If you please, Ma’am, I beg your pardon, but I can’t do nothing with Missis!”\n\n“What do you mean?” asked Edith.\n\n“Well, Ma’am,” replied the frightened maid, “I hardly know. She’s making faces!”\n\nEdith hurried with her to her mother’s room. Cleopatra was arrayed in full dress, with the diamonds, short sleeves, rouge, curls, teeth, and other juvenility all complete; but Paralysis was not to be deceived, had known her for the object of its errand, and had struck her at her glass, where she lay like a horrible doll that had tumbled down.\n\nThey took her to pieces in very shame, and put the little of her that was real on a bed. Doctors were sent for, and soon came. Powerful remedies were resorted to; opinions given that she would rally from this shock, but would not survive another; and there she lay speechless, and staring at the ceiling, for days; sometimes making inarticulate sounds in answer to such questions as did she know who were present, and the like: sometimes giving no reply either by sign or gesture, or in her unwinking eyes.\n\nAt length she began to recover consciousness, and in some degree the power of motion, though not yet of speech. One day the use of her right hand returned; and showing it to her maid who was in attendance on her, and appearing very uneasy in her mind, she made signs for a pencil and some paper. This the maid immediately provided, thinking she was going to make a will, or write some last request; and Mrs Dombey being from home, the maid awaited the result with solemn feelings.\n\nAfter much painful scrawling and erasing, and putting in of wrong characters, which seemed to tumble out of the pencil of their own accord, the old woman produced this document:\n\n“Rose-coloured curtains.”\n\nThe maid being perfectly transfixed, and with tolerable reason, Cleopatra amended the manuscript by adding two words more, when it stood thus:\n\n“Rose-coloured curtains for doctors.”\n\nThe maid now perceived remotely that she wished these articles to be provided for the better presentation of her complexion to the faculty; and as those in the house who knew her best, had no doubt of the correctness of this opinion, which she was soon able to establish for herself, the rose-coloured curtains were added to her bed, and she mended with increased rapidity from that hour. She was soon able to sit up, in curls and a laced cap and nightgown, and to have a little artificial bloom dropped into the hollow caverns of her cheeks.\n\nIt was a tremendous sight to see this old woman in her finery leering and mincing at Death, and playing off her youthful tricks upon him as if he had been the Major; but an alteration in her mind that ensued on the paralytic stroke was fraught with as much matter for reflection, and was quite as ghastly.\n\nWhether the weakening of her intellect made her more cunning and false than before, or whether it confused her between what she had assumed to be and what she really had been, or whether it had awakened any glimmering of remorse, which could neither struggle into light nor get back into total darkness, or whether, in the jumble of her faculties, a combination of these effects had been shaken up, which is perhaps the more likely supposition, the result was this:—That she became hugely exacting in respect of Edith’s affection and gratitude and attention to her; highly laudatory of herself as a most inestimable parent; and very jealous of having any rival in Edith’s regard. Further, in place of remembering that compact made between them for an avoidance of the subject, she constantly alluded to her daughter’s marriage as a proof of her being an incomparable mother; and all this, with the weakness and peevishness of such a state, always serving for a sarcastic commentary on her levity and youthfulness.\n\n“Where is Mrs Dombey?” she would say to her maid.\n\n“Gone out, Ma’am.”\n\n“Gone out! Does she go out to shun her Mama, Flowers?”\n\n“La bless you, no, Ma’am. Mrs Dombey has only gone out for a ride with Miss Florence.”\n\n“Miss Florence. Who’s Miss Florence? Don’t tell me about Miss Florence. What’s Miss Florence to her, compared to me?”\n\nThe apposite display of the diamonds, or the peach-velvet bonnet (she sat in the bonnet to receive visitors, weeks before she could stir out of doors), or the dressing of her up in some gaud or other, usually stopped the tears that began to flow hereabouts; and she would remain in a complacent state until Edith came to see her; when, at a glance of the proud face, she would relapse again.\n\n“Well, I am sure, Edith!” she would cry, shaking her head.\n\n“What is the matter, mother?”\n\n“Matter! I really don’t know what is the matter. The world is coming to such an artificial and ungrateful state, that I begin to think there’s no Heart—or anything of that sort—left in it, positively. Withers is more a child to me than you are. He attends to me much more than my own daughter. I almost wish I didn’t look so young—and all that kind of thing—and then perhaps I should be more considered.”\n\n“What would you have, mother?”\n\n“Oh, a great deal, Edith,” impatiently.\n\n“Is there anything you want that you have not? It is your own fault if there be.”\n\n“My own fault!” beginning to whimper. “The parent I have been to you, Edith: making you a companion from your cradle! And when you neglect me, and have no more natural affection for me than if I was a stranger—not a twentieth part of the affection that you have for Florence—but I am only your mother, and should corrupt her in a day!—you reproach me with its being my own fault.”\n\n“Mother, mother, I reproach you with nothing. Why will you always dwell on this?”\n\n“Isn’t it natural that I should dwell on this, when I am all affection and sensitiveness, and am wounded in the cruellest way, whenever you look at me?”\n\n“I do not mean to wound you, mother. Have you no remembrance of what has been said between us? Let the Past rest.”\n\n“Yes, rest! And let gratitude to me rest; and let affection for me rest; and let me rest in my out-of-the-way room, with no society and no attention, while you find new relations to make much of, who have no earthly claim upon you! Good gracious, Edith, do you know what an elegant establishment you are at the head of?”\n\n“Yes. Hush!”\n\n“And that gentlemanly creature, Dombey? Do you know that you are married to him, Edith, and that you have a settlement and a position, and a carriage, and I don’t know what?”\n\n“Indeed, I know it, mother; well.”\n\n“As you would have had with that delightful good soul—what did they call him?—Granger—if he hadn’t died. And who have you to thank for all this, Edith?”\n\n“You, mother; you.”\n\n“Then put your arms round my neck, and kiss me; and show me, Edith, that you know there never was a better Mama than I have been to you. And don’t let me become a perfect fright with teasing and wearing myself at your ingratitude, or when I’m out again in society no soul will know me, not even that hateful animal, the Major.”\n\nBut, sometimes, when Edith went nearer to her, and bending down her stately head, put her cold cheek to hers, the mother would draw back as If she were afraid of her, and would fall into a fit of trembling, and cry out that there was a wandering in her wits. And sometimes she would entreat her, with humility, to sit down on the chair beside her bed, and would look at her (as she sat there brooding) with a face that even the rose-coloured curtains could not make otherwise than scared and wild.\n\nThe rose-coloured curtains blushed, in course of time, on Cleopatra’s bodily recovery, and on her dress—more juvenile than ever, to repair the ravages of illness—and on the rouge, and on the teeth, and on the curls, and on the diamonds, and the short sleeves, and the whole wardrobe of the doll that had tumbled down before the mirror. They blushed, too, now and then, upon an indistinctness in her speech which she turned off with a girlish giggle, and on an occasional failing in her memory, that had no rule in it, but came and went fantastically, as if in mockery of her fantastic self.\n\nBut they never blushed upon a change in the new manner of her thought and speech towards her daughter. And though that daughter often came within their influence, they never blushed upon her loveliness irradiated by a smile, or softened by the light of filial love, in its stern beauty.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVIII. Miss Tox improves an Old Acquaintance\n\nThe forlorn Miss Tox, abandoned by her friend Louisa Chick, and bereft of Mr Dombey’s countenance—for no delicate pair of wedding cards, united by a silver thread, graced the chimney-glass in Princess’s Place, or the harpsichord, or any of those little posts of display which Lucretia reserved for holiday occupation—became depressed in her spirits, and suffered much from melancholy. For a time the Bird Waltz was unheard in Princess’s Place, the plants were neglected, and dust collected on the miniature of Miss Tox’s ancestor with the powdered head and pigtail.\n\nMiss Tox, however, was not of an age or of a disposition long to abandon herself to unavailing regrets. Only two notes of the harpsichord were dumb from disuse when the Bird Waltz again warbled and trilled in the crooked drawing-room: only one slip of geranium fell a victim to imperfect nursing, before she was gardening at her green baskets again, regularly every morning; the powdered-headed ancestor had not been under a cloud for more than six weeks, when Miss Tox breathed on his benignant visage, and polished him up with a piece of wash-leather.\n\nStill, Miss Tox was lonely, and at a loss. Her attachments, however ludicrously shown, were real and strong; and she was, as she expressed it, “deeply hurt by the unmerited contumely she had met with from Louisa.” But there was no such thing as anger in Miss Tox’s composition. If she had ambled on through life, in her soft spoken way, without any opinions, she had, at least, got so far without any harsh passions. The mere sight of Louisa Chick in the street one day, at a considerable distance, so overpowered her milky nature, that she was fain to seek immediate refuge in a pastrycook’s, and there, in a musty little back room usually devoted to the consumption of soups, and pervaded by an ox-tail atmosphere, relieve her feelings by weeping plentifully.\n\nAgainst Mr Dombey Miss Tox hardly felt that she had any reason of complaint. Her sense of that gentleman’s magnificence was such, that once removed from him, she felt as if her distance always had been immeasurable, and as if he had greatly condescended in tolerating her at all. No wife could be too handsome or too stately for him, according to Miss Tox’s sincere opinion. It was perfectly natural that in looking for one, he should look high. Miss Tox with tears laid down this proposition, and fully admitted it, twenty times a day. She never recalled the lofty manner in which Mr Dombey had made her subservient to his convenience and caprices, and had graciously permitted her to be one of the nurses of his little son. She only thought, in her own words, “that she had passed a great many happy hours in that house, which she must ever remember with gratification, and that she could never cease to regard Mr Dombey as one of the most impressive and dignified of men.”\n\nCut off, however, from the implacable Louisa, and being shy of the Major (whom she viewed with some distrust now), Miss Tox found it very irksome to know nothing of what was going on in Mr Dombey’s establishment. And as she really had got into the habit of considering Dombey and Son as the pivot on which the world in general turned, she resolved, rather than be ignorant of intelligence which so strongly interested her, to cultivate her old acquaintance, Mrs Richards, who she knew, since her last memorable appearance before Mr Dombey, was in the habit of sometimes holding communication with his servants. Perhaps Miss Tox, in seeking out the Toodle family, had the tender motive hidden in her breast of having somebody to whom she could talk about Mr Dombey, no matter how humble that somebody might be.\n\nAt all events, towards the Toodle habitation Miss Tox directed her steps one evening, what time Mr Toodle, cindery and swart, was refreshing himself with tea, in the bosom of his family. Mr Toodle had only three stages of existence. He was either taking refreshment in the bosom just mentioned, or he was tearing through the country at from twenty-five to fifty miles an hour, or he was sleeping after his fatigues. He was always in a whirlwind or a calm, and a peaceable, contented, easy-going man Mr Toodle was in either state, who seemed to have made over all his own inheritance of fuming and fretting to the engines with which he was connected, which panted, and gasped, and chafed, and wore themselves out, in a most unsparing manner, while Mr Toodle led a mild and equable life.\n\n“Polly, my gal,” said Mr Toodle, with a young Toodle on each knee, and two more making tea for him, and plenty more scattered about—Mr Toodle was never out of children, but always kept a good supply on hand—“you ain’t seen our Biler lately, have you?”\n\n“No,” replied Polly, “but he’s almost certain to look in tonight. It’s his right evening, and he’s very regular.”\n\n“I suppose,” said Mr Toodle, relishing his meal infinitely, “as our Biler is a doin’ now about as well as a boy can do, eh, Polly?”\n\n“Oh! he’s a doing beautiful!” responded Polly.\n\n“He ain’t got to be at all secret-like—has he, Polly?” inquired Mr Toodle.\n\n“No!” said Mrs Toodle, plumply.\n\n“I’m glad he ain’t got to be at all secret-like, Polly,” observed Mr Toodle in his slow and measured way, and shovelling in his bread and butter with a clasp knife, as if he were stoking himself, “because that don’t look well; do it, Polly?”\n\n“Why, of course it don’t, father. How can you ask!”\n\n“You see, my boys and gals,” said Mr Toodle, looking round upon his family, “wotever you’re up to in a honest way, it’s my opinion as you can’t do better than be open. If you find yourselves in cuttings or in tunnels, don’t you play no secret games. Keep your whistles going, and let’s know where you are.”\n\nThe rising Toodles set up a shrill murmur, expressive of their resolution to profit by the paternal advice.\n\n“But what makes you say this along of Rob, father?” asked his wife, anxiously.\n\n“Polly, old “ooman,” said Mr Toodle, “I don’t know as I said it partickler along o’ Rob, I’m sure. I starts light with Rob only; I comes to a branch; I takes on what I finds there; and a whole train of ideas gets coupled on to him, afore I knows where I am, or where they comes from. What a Junction a man’s thoughts is,” said Mr Toodle, “to-be-sure!”\n\nThis profound reflection Mr Toodle washed down with a pint mug of tea, and proceeded to solidify with a great weight of bread and butter; charging his young daughters meanwhile, to keep plenty of hot water in the pot, as he was uncommon dry, and should take the indefinite quantity of “a sight of mugs,” before his thirst was appeased.\n\nIn satisfying himself, however, Mr Toodle was not regardless of the younger branches about him, who, although they had made their own evening repast, were on the look-out for irregular morsels, as possessing a relish. These he distributed now and then to the expectant circle, by holding out great wedges of bread and butter, to be bitten at by the family in lawful succession, and by serving out small doses of tea in like manner with a spoon; which snacks had such a relish in the mouths of these young Toodles, that, after partaking of the same, they performed private dances of ecstasy among themselves, and stood on one leg apiece, and hopped, and indulged in other saltatory tokens of gladness. These vents for their excitement found, they gradually closed about Mr Toodle again, and eyed him hard as he got through more bread and butter and tea; affecting, however, to have no further expectations of their own in reference to those viands, but to be conversing on foreign subjects, and whispering confidentially.\n\nMr Toodle, in the midst of this family group, and setting an awful example to his children in the way of appetite, was conveying the two young Toodles on his knees to Birmingham by special engine, and was contemplating the rest over a barrier of bread and butter, when Rob the Grinder, in his sou’wester hat and mourning slops, presented himself, and was received with a general rush of brothers and sisters.\n\n“Well, mother!” said Rob, dutifully kissing her; “how are you, mother?”\n\n“There’s my boy!” cried Polly, giving him a hug and a pat on the back. “Secret! Bless you, father, not he!”\n\nThis was intended for Mr Toodle’s private edification, but Rob the Grinder, whose withers were not unwrung, caught the words as they were spoken.\n\n“What! father’s been a saying something more again me, has he?” cried the injured innocent. “Oh, what a hard thing it is that when a cove has once gone a little wrong, a cove’s own father should be always a throwing it in his face behind his back! It’s enough,” cried Rob, resorting to his coat-cuff in anguish of spirit, “to make a cove go and do something, out of spite!”\n\n“My poor boy!” cried Polly, “father didn’t mean anything.”\n\n“If father didn’t mean anything,” blubbered the injured Grinder, “why did he go and say anything, mother? Nobody thinks half so bad of me as my own father does. What a unnatural thing! I wish somebody’d take and chop my head off. Father wouldn’t mind doing it, I believe, and I’d much rather he did that than t’other.”\n\nAt these desperate words all the young Toodles shrieked; a pathetic effect, which the Grinder improved by ironically adjuring them not to cry for him, for they ought to hate him, they ought, if they was good boys and girls; and this so touched the youngest Toodle but one, who was easily moved, that it touched him not only in his spirit but in his wind too; making him so purple that Mr Toodle in consternation carried him out to the water-butt, and would have put him under the tap, but for his being recovered by the sight of that instrument.\n\nMatters having reached this point, Mr Toodle explained, and the virtuous feelings of his son being thereby calmed, they shook hands, and harmony reigned again.\n\n“Will you do as I do, Biler, my boy?” inquired his father, returning to his tea with new strength.\n\n“No, thank’ee, father. Master and I had tea together.”\n\n“And how is master, Rob?” said Polly.\n\n“Well, I don’t know, mother; not much to boast on. There ain’t no bis’ness done, you see. He don’t know anything about it—the Cap’en don’t. There was a man come into the shop this very day, and says, ‘I want a so-and-so,’ he says—some hard name or another. ‘A which?’ says the Cap’en. ‘A so-and-so,’ says the man. ‘Brother,’ says the Cap’en, ‘will you take a observation round the shop.’ ‘Well,’ says the man, ‘I’ve done.’ ‘Do you see wot you want?’ says the Cap’en ‘No, I don’t,’ says the man. ‘Do you know it wen you do see it?’ says the Cap’en. ‘No, I don’t,’ says the man. ‘Why, then I tell you wot, my lad,’ says the Cap’en, ‘you’d better go back and ask wot it’s like, outside, for no more don’t I!’”\n\n“That ain’t the way to make money, though, is it?” said Polly.\n\n“Money, mother! He’ll never make money. He has such ways as I never see. He ain’t a bad master though, I’ll say that for him. But that ain’t much to me, for I don’t think I shall stop with him long.”\n\n“Not stop in your place, Rob!” cried his mother; while Mr Toodle opened his eyes.\n\n“Not in that place, p’raps,” returned the Grinder, with a wink. “I shouldn’t wonder—friends at court you know—but never you mind, mother, just now; I’m all right, that’s all.”\n\nThe indisputable proof afforded in these hints, and in the Grinder’s mysterious manner, of his not being subject to that failing which Mr Toodle had, by implication, attributed to him, might have led to a renewal of his wrongs, and of the sensation in the family, but for the opportune arrival of another visitor, who, to Polly’s great surprise, appeared at the door, smiling patronage and friendship on all there.\n\n“How do you do, Mrs Richards?” said Miss Tox. “I have come to see you. May I come in?”\n\nThe cheery face of Mrs Richards shone with a hospitable reply, and Miss Tox, accepting the proffered chair, and gracefully recognising Mr Toodle on her way to it, untied her bonnet strings, and said that in the first place she must beg the dear children, one and all, to come and kiss her.\n\nThe ill-starred youngest Toodle but one, who would appear, from the frequency of his domestic troubles, to have been born under an unlucky planet, was prevented from performing his part in this general salutation by having fixed the sou’wester hat (with which he had been previously trifling) deep on his head, hind side before, and being unable to get it off again; which accident presenting to his terrified imagination a dismal picture of his passing the rest of his days in darkness, and in hopeless seclusion from his friends and family, caused him to struggle with great violence, and to utter suffocating cries. Being released, his face was discovered to be very hot, and red, and damp; and Miss Tox took him on her lap, much exhausted.\n\n“You have almost forgotten me, Sir, I daresay,” said Miss Tox to Mr Toodle.\n\n“No, Ma’am, no,” said Toodle. “But we’ve all on us got a little older since then.”\n\n“And how do you find yourself, Sir?” inquired Miss Tox, blandly.\n\n“Hearty, Ma’am, thank’ee,” replied Toodle. “How do you find yourself, Ma’am? Do the rheumaticks keep off pretty well, Ma’am? We must all expect to grow into ’em, as we gets on.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Miss Tox. “I have not felt any inconvenience from that disorder yet.”\n\n“You’re wery fortunate, Ma’am,” returned Mr Toodle. “Many people at your time of life, Ma’am, is martyrs to it. There was my mother—” But catching his wife’s eye here, Mr Toodle judiciously buried the rest in another mug of tea.\n\n“You never mean to say, Mrs Richards,” cried Miss Tox, looking at Rob, “that that is your—”\n\n“Eldest, Ma’am,” said Polly. “Yes, indeed, it is. That’s the little fellow, Ma’am, that was the innocent cause of so much.”\n\n“This here, Ma’am,” said Toodle, “is him with the short legs—and they was,” said Mr Toodle, with a touch of poetry in his tone, “unusual short for leathers—as Mr Dombey made a Grinder on.”\n\nThe recollection almost overpowered Miss Tox. The subject of it had a peculiar interest for her directly. She asked him to shake hands, and congratulated his mother on his frank, ingenuous face. Rob, overhearing her, called up a look, to justify the eulogium, but it was hardly the right look.\n\n“And now, Mrs Richards,” said Miss Tox,—“and you too, Sir,” addressing Toodle—“I’ll tell you, plainly and truly, what I have come here for. You may be aware, Mrs Richards—and, possibly, you may be aware too, Sir—that a little distance has interposed itself between me and some of my friends, and that where I used to visit a good deal, I do not visit now.”\n\nPolly, who, with a woman’s tact, understood this at once, expressed as much in a little look. Mr Toodle, who had not the faintest idea of what Miss Tox was talking about, expressed that also, in a stare.\n\n“Of course,” said Miss Tox, “how our little coolness has arisen is of no moment, and does not require to be discussed. It is sufficient for me to say, that I have the greatest possible respect for, and interest in, Mr Dombey;” Miss Tox’s voice faltered; “and everything that relates to him.”\n\nMr Toodle, enlightened, shook his head, and said he had heerd it said, and, for his own part, he did think, as Mr Dombey was a difficult subject.\n\n“Pray don’t say so, Sir, if you please,” returned Miss Tox. “Let me entreat you not to say so, Sir, either now, or at any future time. Such observations cannot but be very painful to me; and to a gentleman, whose mind is constituted as, I am quite sure, yours is, can afford no permanent satisfaction.”\n\nMr Toodle, who had not entertained the least doubt of offering a remark that would be received with acquiescence, was greatly confounded.\n\n“All that I wish to say, Mrs Richards,” resumed Miss Tox,—“and I address myself to you too, Sir,—is this. That any intelligence of the proceedings of the family, of the welfare of the family, of the health of the family, that reaches you, will be always most acceptable to me. That I shall be always very glad to chat with Mrs Richards about the family, and about old time. And as Mrs Richards and I never had the least difference (though I could wish now that we had been better acquainted, but I have no one but myself to blame for that), I hope she will not object to our being very good friends now, and to my coming backwards and forwards here, when I like, without being a stranger. Now, I really hope, Mrs Richards,” said Miss Tox, earnestly, “that you will take this, as I mean it, like a good-humoured creature, as you always were.”\n\nPolly was gratified, and showed it. Mr Toodle didn’t know whether he was gratified or not, and preserved a stolid calmness.\n\n“You see, Mrs Richards,” said Miss Tox—“and I hope you see too, Sir—there are many little ways in which I can be slightly useful to you, if you will make no stranger of me; and in which I shall be delighted to be so. For instance, I can teach your children something. I shall bring a few little books, if you’ll allow me, and some work, and of an evening now and then, they’ll learn—dear me, they’ll learn a great deal, I trust, and be a credit to their teacher.”\n\nMr Toodle, who had a great respect for learning, jerked his head approvingly at his wife, and moistened his hands with dawning satisfaction.\n\n“Then, not being a stranger, I shall be in nobody’s way,” said Miss Tox, “and everything will go on just as if I were not here. Mrs Richards will do her mending, or her ironing, or her nursing, whatever it is, without minding me: and you’ll smoke your pipe, too, if you’re so disposed, Sir, won’t you?”\n\n“Thank’ee, Mum,” said Mr Toodle. “Yes; I’ll take my bit of backer.”\n\n“Very good of you to say so, Sir,” rejoined Miss Tox, “and I really do assure you now, unfeignedly, that it will be a great comfort to me, and that whatever good I may be fortunate enough to do the children, you will more than pay back to me, if you’ll enter into this little bargain comfortably, and easily, and good-naturedly, without another word about it.”\n\nThe bargain was ratified on the spot; and Miss Tox found herself so much at home already, that without delay she instituted a preliminary examination of the children all round—which Mr Toodle much admired—and booked their ages, names, and acquirements, on a piece of paper. This ceremony, and a little attendant gossip, prolonged the time until after their usual hour of going to bed, and detained Miss Tox at the Toodle fireside until it was too late for her to walk home alone. The gallant Grinder, however, being still there, politely offered to attend her to her own door; and as it was something to Miss Tox to be seen home by a youth whom Mr Dombey had first inducted into those manly garments which are rarely mentioned by name, she very readily accepted the proposal.\n\nAfter shaking hands with Mr Toodle and Polly, and kissing all the children, Miss Tox left the house, therefore, with unlimited popularity, and carrying away with her so light a heart that it might have given Mrs Chick offence if that good lady could have weighed it.\n\nRob the Grinder, in his modesty, would have walked behind, but Miss Tox desired him to keep beside her, for conversational purposes; and, as she afterwards expressed it to his mother, “drew him out,” upon the road.\n\nHe drew out so bright, and clear, and shining, that Miss Tox was charmed with him. The more Miss Tox drew him out, the finer he came—like wire. There never was a better or more promising youth—a more affectionate, steady, prudent, sober, honest, meek, candid young man—than Rob drew out, that night.\n\n“I am quite glad,” said Miss Tox, arrived at her own door, “to know you. I hope you’ll consider me your friend, and that you’ll come and see me as often as you like. Do you keep a money-box?”\n\n“Yes, Ma’am,” returned Rob; “I’m saving up, against I’ve got enough to put in the Bank, Ma’am.\n\n“Very laudable indeed,” said Miss Tox. “I’m glad to hear it. Put this half-crown into it, if you please.”\n\n“Oh thank you, Ma’am,” replied Rob, “but really I couldn’t think of depriving you.”\n\n“I commend your independent spirit,” said Miss Tox, “but it’s no deprivation, I assure you. I shall be offended if you don’t take it, as a mark of my good-will. Good-night, Robin.”\n\n“Good-night, Ma’am,” said Rob, “and thank you!”\n\nWho ran sniggering off to get change, and tossed it away with a pieman. But they never taught honour at the Grinders’ School, where the system that prevailed was particularly strong in the engendering of hypocrisy. Insomuch, that many of the friends and masters of past Grinders said, if this were what came of education for the common people, let us have none. Some more rational said, let us have a better one. But the governing powers of the Grinders’ Company were always ready for them, by picking out a few boys who had turned out well in spite of the system, and roundly asserting that they could have only turned out well because of it. Which settled the business of those objectors out of hand, and established the glory of the Grinders’ Institution.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIX. Further Adventures of Captain Edward Cuttle, Mariner\n\nTime, sure of foot and strong of will, had so pressed onward, that the year enjoined by the old Instrument-maker, as the term during which his friend should refrain from opening the sealed packet accompanying the letter he had left for him, was now nearly expired, and Captain Cuttle began to look at it, of an evening, with feelings of mystery and uneasiness.\n\nThe Captain, in his honour, would as soon have thought of opening the parcel one hour before the expiration of the term, as he would have thought of opening himself, to study his own anatomy. He merely brought it out, at a certain stage of his first evening pipe, laid it on the table, and sat gazing at the outside of it, through the smoke, in silent gravity, for two or three hours at a spell. Sometimes, when he had contemplated it thus for a pretty long while, the Captain would hitch his chair, by degrees, farther and farther off, as if to get beyond the range of its fascination; but if this were his design, he never succeeded: for even when he was brought up by the parlour wall, the packet still attracted him; or if his eyes, in thoughtful wandering, roved to the ceiling or the fire, its image immediately followed, and posted itself conspicuously among the coals, or took up an advantageous position on the whitewash.\n\nIn respect of Heart’s Delight, the Captain’s parental and admiration knew no change. But since his last interview with Mr Carker, Captain Cuttle had come to entertain doubts whether his former intervention in behalf of that young lady and his dear boy Wal”r, had proved altogether so favourable as he could have wished, and as he at the time believed. The Captain was troubled with a serious misgiving that he had done more harm than good, in short; and in his remorse and modesty he made the best atonement he could think of, by putting himself out of the way of doing any harm to anyone, and, as it were, throwing himself overboard for a dangerous person.\n\nSelf-buried, therefore, among the instruments, the Captain never went near Mr Dombey’s house, or reported himself in any way to Florence or Miss Nipper. He even severed himself from Mr Perch, on the occasion of his next visit, by dryly informing that gentleman, that he thanked him for his company, but had cut himself adrift from all such acquaintance, as he didn’t know what magazine he mightn’t blow up, without meaning of it. In this self-imposed retirement, the Captain passed whole days and weeks without interchanging a word with anyone but Rob the Grinder, whom he esteemed as a pattern of disinterested attachment and fidelity. In this retirement, the Captain, gazing at the packet of an evening, would sit smoking, and thinking of Florence and poor Walter, until they both seemed to his homely fancy to be dead, and to have passed away into eternal youth, the beautiful and innocent children of his first remembrance.\n\nThe Captain did not, however, in his musings, neglect his own improvement, or the mental culture of Rob the Grinder. That young man was generally required to read out of some book to the Captain, for one hour, every evening; and as the Captain implicitly believed that all books were true, he accumulated, by this means, many remarkable facts. On Sunday nights, the Captain always read for himself, before going to bed, a certain Divine Sermon once delivered on a Mount; and although he was accustomed to quote the text, without book, after his own manner, he appeared to read it with as reverent an understanding of its heavenly spirit, as if he had got it all by heart in Greek, and had been able to write any number of fierce theological disquisitions on its every phrase.\n\nRob the Grinder, whose reverence for the inspired writings, under the admirable system of the Grinders’ School, had been developed by a perpetual bruising of his intellectual shins against all the proper names of all the tribes of Judah, and by the monotonous repetition of hard verses, especially by way of punishment, and by the parading of him at six years old in leather breeches, three times a Sunday, very high up, in a very hot church, with a great organ buzzing against his drowsy head, like an exceedingly busy bee—Rob the Grinder made a mighty show of being edified when the Captain ceased to read, and generally yawned and nodded while the reading was in progress. The latter fact being never so much as suspected by the good Captain.\n\nCaptain Cuttle, also, as a man of business; took to keeping books. In these he entered observations on the weather, and on the currents of the waggons and other vehicles: which he observed, in that quarter, to set westward in the morning and during the greater part of the day, and eastward towards the evening. Two or three stragglers appearing in one week, who “spoke him”—so the Captain entered it—on the subject of spectacles, and who, without positively purchasing, said they would look in again, the Captain decided that the business was improving, and made an entry in the day-book to that effect: the wind then blowing (which he first recorded) pretty fresh, west and by north; having changed in the night.\n\nOne of the Captain’s chief difficulties was Mr Toots, who called frequently, and who without saying much seemed to have an idea that the little back parlour was an eligible room to chuckle in, as he would sit and avail himself of its accommodations in that regard by the half-hour together, without at all advancing in intimacy with the Captain. The Captain, rendered cautious by his late experience, was unable quite to satisfy his mind whether Mr Toots was the mild subject he appeared to be, or was a profoundly artful and dissimulating hypocrite. His frequent reference to Miss Dombey was suspicious; but the Captain had a secret kindness for Mr Toots’s apparent reliance on him, and forbore to decide against him for the present; merely eyeing him, with a sagacity not to be described, whenever he approached the subject that was nearest to his heart.\n\n“Captain Gills,” blurted out Mr Toots, one day all at once, as his manner was, “do you think you could think favourably of that proposition of mine, and give me the pleasure of your acquaintance?”\n\n“Why, I tell you what it is, my lad,” replied the Captain, who had at length concluded on a course of action; “I’ve been turning that there, over.”\n\n“Captain Gills, it’s very kind of you,” retorted Mr Toots. “I’m much obliged to you. Upon my word and honour, Captain Gills, it would be a charity to give me the pleasure of your acquaintance. It really would.”\n\n“You see, brother,” argued the Captain slowly, “I don’t know you.”\n\n“But you never can know me, Captain Gills,” replied Mr Toots, steadfast to his point, “if you don’t give me the pleasure of your acquaintance.”\n\nThe Captain seemed struck by the originality and power of this remark, and looked at Mr Toots as if he thought there was a great deal more in him than he had expected.\n\n“Well said, my lad,” observed the Captain, nodding his head thoughtfully; “and true. Now look’ee here: You’ve made some observations to me, which gives me to understand as you admire a certain sweet creetur. Hey?”\n\n“Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, gesticulating violently with the hand in which he held his hat, “Admiration is not the word. Upon my honour, you have no conception what my feelings are. If I could be dyed black, and made Miss Dombey’s slave, I should consider it a compliment. If, at the sacrifice of all my property, I could get transmigrated into Miss Dombey’s dog—I—I really think I should never leave off wagging my tail. I should be so perfectly happy, Captain Gills!”\n\nMr Toots said it with watery eyes, and pressed his hat against his bosom with deep emotion.\n\n“My lad,” returned the Captain, moved to compassion, “if you’re in arnest—”\n\n“Captain Gills,” cried Mr Toots, “I’m in such a state of mind, and am so dreadfully in earnest, that if I could swear to it upon a hot piece of iron, or a live coal, or melted lead, or burning sealing-wax, Or anything of that sort, I should be glad to hurt myself, as a relief to my feelings.” And Mr Toots looked hurriedly about the room, as if for some sufficiently painful means of accomplishing his dread purpose.\n\nThe Captain pushed his glazed hat back upon his head, stroked his face down with his heavy hand—making his nose more mottled in the process—and planting himself before Mr Toots, and hooking him by the lapel of his coat, addressed him in these words, while Mr Toots looked up into his face, with much attention and some wonder.\n\n“If you’re in arnest, you see, my lad,” said the Captain, “you’re a object of clemency, and clemency is the brightest jewel in the crown of a Briton’s head, for which you’ll overhaul the constitution as laid down in Rule Britannia, and, when found, that is the charter as them garden angels was a singing of, so many times over. Stand by! This here proposal o’ you’rn takes me a little aback. And why? Because I holds my own only, you understand, in these here waters, and haven’t got no consort, and may be don’t wish for none. Steady! You hailed me first, along of a certain young lady, as you was chartered by. Now if you and me is to keep one another’s company at all, that there young creetur’s name must never be named nor referred to. I don’t know what harm mayn’t have been done by naming of it too free, afore now, and thereby I brings up short. D’ye make me out pretty clear, brother?”\n\n“Well, you’ll excuse me, Captain Gills,” replied Mr Toots, “if I don’t quite follow you sometimes. But upon my word I—it’s a hard thing, Captain Gills, not to be able to mention Miss Dombey. I really have got such a dreadful load here!”—Mr Toots pathetically touched his shirt-front with both hands—“that I feel night and day, exactly as if somebody was sitting upon me.”\n\n“Them,” said the Captain, “is the terms I offer. If they’re hard upon you, brother, as mayhap they are, give ’em a wide berth, sheer off, and part company cheerily!”\n\n“Captain Gills,” returned Mr Toots, “I hardly know how it is, but after what you told me when I came here, for the first time, I—I feel that I’d rather think about Miss Dombey in your society than talk about her in almost anybody else’s. Therefore, Captain Gills, if you’ll give me the pleasure of your acquaintance, I shall be very happy to accept it on your own conditions. I wish to be honourable, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, holding back his extended hand for a moment, “and therefore I am obliged to say that I can not help thinking about Miss Dombey. It’s impossible for me to make a promise not to think about her.”\n\n“My lad,” said the Captain, whose opinion of Mr Toots was much improved by this candid avowal, “a man’s thoughts is like the winds, and nobody can’t answer for ’em for certain, any length of time together. Is it a treaty as to words?”\n\n“As to words, Captain Gills,” returned Mr Toots, “I think I can bind myself.”\n\nMr Toots gave Captain Cuttle his hand upon it, then and there; and the Captain with a pleasant and gracious show of condescension, bestowed his acquaintance upon him formally. Mr Toots seemed much relieved and gladdened by the acquisition, and chuckled rapturously during the remainder of his visit. The Captain, for his part, was not ill pleased to occupy that position of patronage, and was exceedingly well satisfied by his own prudence and foresight.\n\nBut rich as Captain Cuttle was in the latter quality, he received a surprise that same evening from a no less ingenuous and simple youth, than Rob the Grinder. That artless lad, drinking tea at the same table, and bending meekly over his cup and saucer, having taken sidelong observations of his master for some time, who was reading the newspaper with great difficulty, but much dignity, through his glasses, broke silence by saying—\n\n“Oh! I beg your pardon, Captain, but you mayn’t be in want of any pigeons, may you, Sir?”\n\n“No, my lad,” replied the Captain.\n\n“Because I was wishing to dispose of mine, Captain,” said Rob.\n\n“Ay, ay?” cried the Captain, lifting up his bushy eyebrows a little.\n\n“Yes; I’m going, Captain, if you please,” said Rob.\n\n“Going? Where are you going?” asked the Captain, looking round at him over the glasses.\n\n“What? didn’t you know that I was going to leave you, Captain?” asked Rob, with a sneaking smile.\n\nThe Captain put down the paper, took off his spectacles, and brought his eyes to bear on the deserter.\n\n“Oh yes, Captain, I am going to give you warning. I thought you’d have known that beforehand, perhaps,” said Rob, rubbing his hands, and getting up. “If you could be so good as provide yourself soon, Captain, it would be a great convenience to me. You couldn’t provide yourself by to-morrow morning, I am afraid, Captain: could you, do you think?”\n\n“And you’re a going to desert your colours, are you, my lad?” said the Captain, after a long examination of his face.\n\n“Oh, it’s very hard upon a cove, Captain,” cried the tender Rob, injured and indignant in a moment, “that he can’t give lawful warning, without being frowned at in that way, and called a deserter. You haven’t any right to call a poor cove names, Captain. It ain’t because I’m a servant and you’re a master, that you’re to go and libel me. What wrong have I done? Come, Captain, let me know what my crime is, will you?”\n\nThe stricken Grinder wept, and put his coat-cuff in his eye.\n\n“Come, Captain,” cried the injured youth, “give my crime a name! What have I been and done? Have I stolen any of the property? have I set the house a-fire? If I have, why don’t you give me in charge, and try it? But to take away the character of a lad that’s been a good servant to you, because he can’t afford to stand in his own light for your good, what a injury it is, and what a bad return for faithful service! This is the way young coves is spiled and drove wrong. I wonder at you, Captain, I do.”\n\nAll of which the Grinder howled forth in a lachrymose whine, and backing carefully towards the door.\n\n“And so you’ve got another berth, have you, my lad?” said the Captain, eyeing him intently.\n\n“Yes, Captain, since you put it in that shape, I have got another berth,” cried Rob, backing more and more; “a better berth than I’ve got here, and one where I don’t so much as want your good word, Captain, which is fort’nate for me, after all the dirt you’ve throw’d at me, because I’m poor, and can’t afford to stand in my own light for your good. Yes, I have got another berth; and if it wasn’t for leaving you unprovided, Captain, I’d go to it now, sooner than I’d take them names from you, because I’m poor, and can’t afford to stand in my own light for your good. Why do you reproach me for being poor, and not standing in my own light for your good, Captain? How can you so demean yourself?”\n\n“Look ye here, my boy,” replied the peaceful Captain. “Don’t you pay out no more of them words.”\n\n“Well, then, don’t you pay in no more of your words, Captain,” retorted the roused innocent, getting louder in his whine, and backing into the shop. “I’d sooner you took my blood than my character.”\n\n“Because,” pursued the Captain calmly, “you have heerd, may be, of such a thing as a rope’s end.”\n\n“Oh, have I though, Captain?” cried the taunting Grinder. “No I haven’t. I never heerd of any such a article!”\n\n“Well,” said the Captain, “it’s my belief as you’ll know more about it pretty soon, if you don’t keep a bright look-out. I can read your signals, my lad. You may go.”\n\n“Oh! I may go at once, may I, Captain?” cried Rob, exulting in his success. “But mind! I never asked to go at once, Captain. You are not to take away my character again, because you send me off of your own accord. And you’re not to stop any of my wages, Captain!”\n\nHis employer settled the last point by producing the tin canister and telling the Grinder’s money out in full upon the table. Rob, snivelling and sobbing, and grievously wounded in his feelings, took up the pieces one by one, with a sob and a snivel for each, and tied them up separately in knots in his pockethandkerchief; then he ascended to the roof of the house and filled his hat and pockets with pigeons; then, came down to his bed under the counter and made up his bundle, snivelling and sobbing louder, as if he were cut to the heart by old associations; then he whined, “Good-night, Captain. I leave you without malice!” and then, going out upon the door-step, pulled the little Midshipman’s nose as a parting indignity, and went away down the street grinning triumphantly.\n\nThe Captain, left to himself, resumed his perusal of the news as if nothing unusual or unexpected had taken place, and went reading on with the greatest assiduity. But never a word did Captain Cuttle understand, though he read a vast number, for Rob the Grinder was scampering up one column and down another all through the newspaper.\n\nIt is doubtful whether the worthy Captain had ever felt himself quite abandoned until now; but now, old Sol Gills, Walter, and Heart’s Delight were lost to him indeed, and now Mr Carker deceived and jeered him cruelly. They were all represented in the false Rob, to whom he had held forth many a time on the recollections that were warm within him; he had believed in the false Rob, and had been glad to believe in him; he had made a companion of him as the last of the old ship’s company; he had taken the command of the little Midshipman with him at his right hand; he had meant to do his duty by him, and had felt almost as kindly towards the boy as if they had been shipwrecked and cast upon a desert place together. And now, that the false Rob had brought distrust, treachery, and meanness into the very parlour, which was a kind of sacred place, Captain Cuttle felt as if the parlour might have gone down next, and not surprised him much by its sinking, or given him any very great concern.\n\nTherefore Captain Cuttle read the newspaper with profound attention and no comprehension, and therefore Captain Cuttle said nothing whatever about Rob to himself, or admitted to himself that he was thinking about him, or would recognise in the most distant manner that Rob had anything to do with his feeling as lonely as Robinson Crusoe.\n\nIn the same composed, business-like way, the Captain stepped over to Leadenhall Market in the dusk, and effected an arrangement with a private watchman on duty there, to come and put up and take down the shutters of the wooden Midshipman every night and morning. He then called in at the eating-house to diminish by one half the daily rations theretofore supplied to the Midshipman, and at the public-house to stop the traitor’s beer. “My young man,” said the Captain, in explanation to the young lady at the bar, “my young man having bettered himself, Miss.” Lastly, the Captain resolved to take possession of the bed under the counter, and to turn in there o’ nights instead of upstairs, as sole guardian of the property.\n\nFrom this bed Captain Cuttle daily rose thenceforth, and clapped on his glazed hat at six o’clock in the morning, with the solitary air of Crusoe finishing his toilet with his goat-skin cap; and although his fears of a visitation from the savage tribe, MacStinger, were somewhat cooled, as similar apprehensions on the part of that lone mariner used to be by the lapse of a long interval without any symptoms of the cannibals, he still observed a regular routine of defensive operations, and never encountered a bonnet without previous survey from his castle of retreat. In the meantime (during which he received no call from Mr Toots, who wrote to say he was out of town) his own voice began to have a strange sound in his ears; and he acquired such habits of profound meditation from much polishing and stowing away of the stock, and from much sitting behind the counter reading, or looking out of window, that the red rim made on his forehead by the hard glazed hat, sometimes ached again with excess of reflection.\n\nThe year being now expired, Captain Cuttle deemed it expedient to open the packet; but as he had always designed doing this in the presence of Rob the Grinder, who had brought it to him, and as he had an idea that it would be regular and ship-shape to open it in the presence of somebody, he was sadly put to it for want of a witness. In this difficulty, he hailed one day with unusual delight the announcement in the Shipping Intelligence of the arrival of the Cautious Clara, Captain John Bunsby, from a coasting voyage; and to that philosopher immediately dispatched a letter by post, enjoining inviolable secrecy as to his place of residence, and requesting to be favoured with an early visit, in the evening season.\n\nBunsby, who was one of those sages who act upon conviction, took some days to get the conviction thoroughly into his mind, that he had received a letter to this effect. But when he had grappled with the fact, and mastered it, he promptly sent his boy with the message, “He’s a coming tonight.” Who being instructed to deliver those words and disappear, fulfilled his mission like a tarry spirit, charged with a mysterious warning.\n\nThe Captain, well pleased to receive it, made preparation of pipes and rum and water, and awaited his visitor in the back parlour. At the hour of eight, a deep lowing, as of a nautical Bull, outside the shop-door, succeeded by the knocking of a stick on the panel, announced to the listening ear of Captain Cuttle, that Bunsby was alongside; whom he instantly admitted, shaggy and loose, and with his stolid mahogany visage, as usual, appearing to have no consciousness of anything before it, but to be attentively observing something that was taking place in quite another part of the world.\n\n“Bunsby,” said the Captain, grasping him by the hand, “what cheer, my lad, what cheer?”\n\n“Shipmet,” replied the voice within Bunsby, unaccompanied by any sign on the part of the Commander himself, “hearty, hearty.”\n\n“Bunsby!” said the Captain, rendering irrepressible homage to his genius, “here you are! a man as can give an opinion as is brighter than di’monds—and give me the lad with the tarry trousers as shines to me like di’monds bright, for which you’ll overhaul the Stanfell’s Budget, and when found make a note. Here you are, a man as gave an opinion in this here very place, that has come true, every letter on it,” which the Captain sincerely believed.\n\n“Ay, ay?” growled Bunsby.\n\n“Every letter,” said the Captain.\n\n“For why?” growled Bunsby, looking at his friend for the first time. “Which way? If so, why not? Therefore.” With these oracular words—they seemed almost to make the Captain giddy; they launched him upon such a sea of speculation and conjecture—the sage submitted to be helped off with his pilot-coat, and accompanied his friend into the back parlour, where his hand presently alighted on the rum-bottle, from which he brewed a stiff glass of grog; and presently afterwards on a pipe, which he filled, lighted, and began to smoke.\n\nCaptain Cuttle, imitating his visitor in the matter of these particulars, though the rapt and imperturbable manner of the great Commander was far above his powers, sat in the opposite corner of the fireside, observing him respectfully, and as if he waited for some encouragement or expression of curiosity on Bunsby’s part which should lead him to his own affairs. But as the mahogany philosopher gave no evidence of being sentient of anything but warmth and tobacco, except once, when taking his pipe from his lips to make room for his glass, he incidentally remarked with exceeding gruffness, that his name was Jack Bunsby—a declaration that presented but small opening for conversation—the Captain bespeaking his attention in a short complimentary exordium, narrated the whole history of Uncle Sol’s departure, with the change it had produced in his own life and fortunes; and concluded by placing the packet on the table.\n\nAfter a long pause, Mr Bunsby nodded his head.\n\n“Open?” said the Captain.\n\nBunsby nodded again.\n\nThe Captain accordingly broke the seal, and disclosed to view two folded papers, of which he severally read the endorsements, thus: “Last Will and Testament of Solomon Gills.” “Letter for Ned Cuttle.”\n\nBunsby, with his eye on the coast of Greenland, seemed to listen for the contents. The Captain therefore hemmed to clear his throat, and read the letter aloud.\n\n“‘My dear Ned Cuttle. When I left home for the West Indies’—”\n\nHere the Captain stopped, and looked hard at Bunsby, who looked fixedly at the coast of Greenland.\n\n“—‘in forlorn search of intelligence of my dear boy, I knew that if you were acquainted with my design, you would thwart it, or accompany me; and therefore I kept it secret. If you ever read this letter, Ned, I am likely to be dead. You will easily forgive an old friend’s folly then, and will feel for the restlessness and uncertainty in which he wandered away on such a wild voyage. So no more of that. I have little hope that my poor boy will ever read these words, or gladden your eyes with the sight of his frank face any more.’ No, no; no more,” said Captain Cuttle, sorrowfully meditating; “no more. There he lays, all his days—”\n\nMr Bunsby, who had a musical ear, suddenly bellowed, “In the Bays of Biscay, O!” which so affected the good Captain, as an appropriate tribute to departed worth, that he shook him by the hand in acknowledgment, and was fain to wipe his eyes.\n\n“Well, well!” said the Captain with a sigh, as the Lament of Bunsby ceased to ring and vibrate in the skylight. “Affliction sore, long time he bore, and let us overhaul the wollume, and there find it.”\n\n“Physicians,” observed Bunsby, “was in vain.”\n\n“Ay, ay, to be sure,” said the Captain, “what’s the good o’ them in two or three hundred fathoms o’ water!” Then, returning to the letter, he read on:—“\"But if he should be by, when it is opened;’” the Captain involuntarily looked round, and shook his head; “‘or should know of it at any other time;’” the Captain shook his head again; “‘my blessing on him! In case the accompanying paper is not legally written, it matters very little, for there is no one interested but you and he, and my plain wish is, that if he is living he should have what little there may be, and if (as I fear) otherwise, that you should have it, Ned. You will respect my wish, I know. God bless you for it, and for all your friendliness besides, to Solomon Gills.’ Bunsby!” said the Captain, appealing to him solemnly, “what do you make of this? There you sit, a man as has had his head broke from infancy up’ards, and has got a new opinion into it at every seam as has been opened. Now, what do you make o’ this?”\n\n“If so be,” returned Bunsby, with unusual promptitude, “as he’s dead, my opinion is he won’t come back no more. If so be as he’s alive, my opinion is he will. Do I say he will? No. Why not? Because the bearings of this obserwation lays in the application on it.”\n\n“Bunsby!” said Captain Cuttle, who would seem to have estimated the value of his distinguished friend’s opinions in proportion to the immensity of the difficulty he experienced in making anything out of them; “Bunsby,” said the Captain, quite confounded by admiration, “you carry a weight of mind easy, as would swamp one of my tonnage soon. But in regard o’ this here will, I don’t mean to take no steps towards the property—Lord forbid!—except to keep it for a more rightful owner; and I hope yet as the rightful owner, Sol Gills, is living and’ll come back, strange as it is that he ain’t forwarded no dispatches. Now, what is your opinion, Bunsby, as to stowing of these here papers away again, and marking outside as they was opened, such a day, in the presence of John Bunsby and Ed’ard Cuttle?”\n\nBunsby, descrying no objection, on the coast of Greenland or elsewhere, to this proposal, it was carried into execution; and that great man, bringing his eye into the present for a moment, affixed his sign-manual to the cover, totally abstaining, with characteristic modesty, from the use of capital letters. Captain Cuttle, having attached his own left-handed signature, and locked up the packet in the iron safe, entreated his guest to mix another glass and smoke another pipe; and doing the like himself, fell a musing over the fire on the possible fortunes of the poor old Instrument-maker.\n\nAnd now a surprise occurred, so overwhelming and terrific that Captain Cuttle, unsupported by the presence of Bunsby, must have sunk beneath it, and been a lost man from that fatal hour.\n\nHow the Captain, even in the satisfaction of admitting such a guest, could have only shut the door, and not locked it, of which negligence he was undoubtedly guilty, is one of those questions that must for ever remain mere points of speculation, or vague charges against destiny. But by that unlocked door, at this quiet moment, did the fell MacStinger dash into the parlour, bringing Alexander MacStinger in her parental arms, and confusion and vengeance (not to mention Juliana MacStinger, and the sweet child’s brother, Charles MacStinger, popularly known about the scenes of his youthful sports, as Chowley) in her train. She came so swiftly and so silently, like a rushing air from the neighbourhood of the East India Docks, that Captain Cuttle found himself in the very act of sitting looking at her, before the calm face with which he had been meditating, changed to one of horror and dismay.\n\nBut the moment Captain Cuttle understood the full extent of his misfortune, self-preservation dictated an attempt at flight. Darting at the little door which opened from the parlour on the steep little range of cellar-steps, the Captain made a rush, head-foremost, at the latter, like a man indifferent to bruises and contusions, who only sought to hide himself in the bowels of the earth. In this gallant effort he would probably have succeeded, but for the affectionate dispositions of Juliana and Chowley, who pinning him by the legs—one of those dear children holding on to each—claimed him as their friend, with lamentable cries. In the meantime, Mrs MacStinger, who never entered upon any action of importance without previously inverting Alexander MacStinger, to bring him within the range of a brisk battery of slaps, and then sitting him down to cool as the reader first beheld him, performed that solemn rite, as if on this occasion it were a sacrifice to the Furies; and having deposited the victim on the floor, made at the Captain with a strength of purpose that appeared to threaten scratches to the interposing Bunsby.\n\nThe cries of the two elder MacStingers, and the wailing of young Alexander, who may be said to have passed a piebald childhood, forasmuch as he was black in the face during one half of that fairy period of existence, combined to make this visitation the more awful. But when silence reigned again, and the Captain, in a violent perspiration, stood meekly looking at Mrs MacStinger, its terrors were at their height.\n\n“Oh, Cap’en Cuttle, Cap’en Cuttle!” said Mrs MacStinger, making her chin rigid, and shaking it in unison with what, but for the weakness of her sex, might be described as her fist. “Oh, Cap’en Cuttle, Cap’en Cuttle, do you dare to look me in the face, and not be struck down in the berth!”\n\nThe Captain, who looked anything but daring, feebly muttered “Stand by!”\n\n“Oh I was a weak and trusting Fool when I took you under my roof, Cap’en Cuttle, I was!” cried Mrs MacStinger. “To think of the benefits I’ve showered on that man, and the way in which I brought my children up to love and honour him as if he was a father to ’em, when there ain’t a housekeeper, no nor a lodger in our street, don’t know that I lost money by that man, and by his guzzlings and his muzzlings”—Mrs MacStinger used the last word for the joint sake of alliteration and aggravation, rather than for the expression of any idea—“and when they cried out one and all, shame upon him for putting upon an industrious woman, up early and late for the good of her young family, and keeping her poor place so clean that a individual might have ate his dinner, yes, and his tea too, if he was so disposed, off any one of the floors or stairs, in spite of all his guzzlings and his muzzlings, such was the care and pains bestowed upon him!”\n\nMrs MacStinger stopped to fetch her breath; and her face flushed with triumph in this second happy introduction of Captain Cuttle’s muzzlings.\n\n“And he runs awa-a-a-y!” cried Mrs MacStinger, with a lengthening out of the last syllable that made the unfortunate Captain regard himself as the meanest of men; “and keeps away a twelve-month! From a woman! Such is his conscience! He hasn’t the courage to meet her hi-i-igh;” long syllable again; “but steals away, like a fellon. Why, if that baby of mine,” said Mrs MacStinger, with sudden rapidity, “was to offer to go and steal away, I’d do my duty as a mother by him, till he was covered with wales!”\n\nThe young Alexander, interpreting this into a positive promise, to be shortly redeemed, tumbled over with fear and grief, and lay upon the floor, exhibiting the soles of his shoes and making such a deafening outcry, that Mrs MacStinger found it necessary to take him up in her arms, where she quieted him, ever and anon, as he broke out again, by a shake that seemed enough to loosen his teeth.\n\n“A pretty sort of a man is Cap’en Cuttle,” said Mrs MacStinger, with a sharp stress on the first syllable of the Captain’s name, “to take on for—and to lose sleep for—and to faint along of—and to think dead forsooth—and to go up and down the blessed town like a madwoman, asking questions after! Oh, a pretty sort of a man! Ha ha ha ha! He’s worth all that trouble and distress of mind, and much more. That’s nothing, bless you! Ha ha ha ha! Cap’en Cuttle,” said Mrs MacStinger, with severe reaction in her voice and manner, “I wish to know if you’re a-coming home.”\n\nThe frightened Captain looked into his hat, as if he saw nothing for it but to put it on, and give himself up.\n\n“Cap’en Cuttle,” repeated Mrs MacStinger, in the same determined manner, “I wish to know if you’re a-coming home, Sir.”\n\nThe Captain seemed quite ready to go, but faintly suggested something to the effect of “not making so much noise about it.”\n\n“Ay, ay, ay,” said Bunsby, in a soothing tone. “Awast, my lass, awast!”\n\n“And who may you be, if you please!” retorted Mrs MacStinger, with chaste loftiness. “Did you ever lodge at Number Nine, Brig Place, Sir? My memory may be bad, but not with me, I think. There was a Mrs Jollson lived at Number Nine before me, and perhaps you’re mistaking me for her. That is my only ways of accounting for your familiarity, Sir.”\n\n“Come, come, my lass, awast, awast!” said Bunsby.\n\nCaptain Cuttle could hardly believe it, even of this great man, though he saw it done with his waking eyes; but Bunsby, advancing boldly, put his shaggy blue arm round Mrs MacStinger, and so softened her by his magic way of doing it, and by these few words—he said no more—that she melted into tears, after looking upon him for a few moments, and observed that a child might conquer her now, she was so low in her courage.\n\nSpeechless and utterly amazed, the Captain saw him gradually persuade this inexorable woman into the shop, return for rum and water and a candle, take them to her, and pacify her without appearing to utter one word. Presently he looked in with his pilot-coat on, and said, “Cuttle, I’m a-going to act as convoy home;” and Captain Cuttle, more to his confusion than if he had been put in irons himself, for safe transport to Brig Place, saw the family pacifically filing off, with Mrs MacStinger at their head. He had scarcely time to take down his canister, and stealthily convey some money into the hands of Juliana MacStinger, his former favourite, and Chowley, who had the claim upon him that he was naturally of a maritime build, before the Midshipman was abandoned by them all; and Bunsby whispering that he’d carry on smart, and hail Ned Cuttle again before he went aboard, shut the door upon himself, as the last member of the party.\n\nSome uneasy ideas that he must be walking in his sleep, or that he had been troubled with phantoms, and not a family of flesh and blood, beset the Captain at first, when he went back to the little parlour, and found himself alone. Illimitable faith in, and immeasurable admiration of, the Commander of the Cautious Clara, succeeded, and threw the Captain into a wondering trance.\n\nStill, as time wore on, and Bunsby failed to reappear, the Captain began to entertain uncomfortable doubts of another kind. Whether Bunsby had been artfully decoyed to Brig Place, and was there detained in safe custody as hostage for his friend; in which case it would become the Captain, as a man of honour, to release him, by the sacrifice of his own liberty. Whether he had been attacked and defeated by Mrs MacStinger, and was ashamed to show himself after his discomfiture. Whether Mrs MacStinger, thinking better of it, in the uncertainty of her temper, had turned back to board the Midshipman again, and Bunsby, pretending to conduct her by a short cut, was endeavouring to lose the family amid the wilds and savage places of the City. Above all, what it would behove him, Captain Cuttle, to do, in case of his hearing no more, either of the MacStingers or of Bunsby, which, in these wonderful and unforeseen conjunctions of events, might possibly happen.\n\nHe debated all this until he was tired; and still no Bunsby. He made up his bed under the counter, all ready for turning in; and still no Bunsby. At length, when the Captain had given him up, for that night at least, and had begun to undress, the sound of approaching wheels was heard, and, stopping at the door, was succeeded by Bunsby’s hail.\n\nThe Captain trembled to think that Mrs MacStinger was not to be got rid of, and had been brought back in a coach.\n\nBut no. Bunsby was accompanied by nothing but a large box, which he hauled into the shop with his own hands, and as soon as he had hauled in, sat upon. Captain Cuttle knew it for the chest he had left at Mrs MacStinger’s house, and looking, candle in hand, at Bunsby more attentively, believed that he was three sheets in the wind, or, in plain words, drunk. It was difficult, however, to be sure of this; the Commander having no trace of expression in his face when sober.\n\n“Cuttle,” said the Commander, getting off the chest, and opening the lid, “are these here your traps?”\n\nCaptain Cuttle looked in and identified his property.\n\n“Done pretty taut and trim, hey, shipmet?” said Bunsby.\n\nThe grateful and bewildered Captain grasped him by the hand, and was launching into a reply expressive of his astonished feelings, when Bunsby disengaged himself by a jerk of his wrist, and seemed to make an effort to wink with his revolving eye, the only effect of which attempt, in his condition, was nearly to over-balance him. He then abruptly opened the door, and shot away to rejoin the Cautious Clara with all speed—supposed to be his invariable custom, whenever he considered he had made a point.\n\nAs it was not his humour to be often sought, Captain Cuttle decided not to go or send to him next day, or until he should make his gracious pleasure known in such wise, or failing that, until some little time should have lapsed. The Captain, therefore, renewed his solitary life next morning, and thought profoundly, many mornings, noons, and nights, of old Sol Gills, and Bunsby’s sentiments concerning him, and the hopes there were of his return. Much of such thinking strengthened Captain Cuttle’s hopes; and he humoured them and himself by watching for the Instrument-maker at the door—as he ventured to do now, in his strange liberty—and setting his chair in its place, and arranging the little parlour as it used to be, in case he should come home unexpectedly. He likewise, in his thoughtfulness, took down a certain little miniature of Walter as a schoolboy, from its accustomed nail, lest it should shock the old man on his return. The Captain had his presentiments, too, sometimes, that he would come on such a day; and one particular Sunday, even ordered a double allowance of dinner, he was so sanguine. But come, old Solomon did not; and still the neighbours noticed how the seafaring man in the glazed hat, stood at the shop-door of an evening, looking up and down the street.\n\n# CHAPTER XL. Domestic Relations\n\nIt was not in the nature of things that a man of Mr Dombey’s mood, opposed to such a spirit as he had raised against himself, should be softened in the imperious asperity of his temper; or that the cold hard armour of pride in which he lived encased, should be made more flexible by constant collision with haughty scorn and defiance. It is the curse of such a nature—it is a main part of the heavy retribution on itself it bears within itself—that while deference and concession swell its evil qualities, and are the food it grows upon, resistance and a questioning of its exacting claims, foster it too, no less. The evil that is in it finds equally its means of growth and propagation in opposites. It draws support and life from sweets and bitters; bowed down before, or unacknowledged, it still enslaves the breast in which it has its throne; and, worshipped or rejected, is as hard a master as the Devil in dark fables.\n\nTowards his first wife, Mr Dombey, in his cold and lofty arrogance, had borne himself like the removed Being he almost conceived himself to be. He had been “Mr Dombey” with her when she first saw him, and he was “Mr Dombey” when she died. He had asserted his greatness during their whole married life, and she had meekly recognised it. He had kept his distant seat of state on the top of his throne, and she her humble station on its lowest step; and much good it had done him, so to live in solitary bondage to his one idea. He had imagined that the proud character of his second wife would have been added to his own—would have merged into it, and exalted his greatness. He had pictured himself haughtier than ever, with Edith’s haughtiness subservient to his. He had never entertained the possibility of its arraying itself against him. And now, when he found it rising in his path at every step and turn of his daily life, fixing its cold, defiant, and contemptuous face upon him, this pride of his, instead of withering, or hanging down its head beneath the shock, put forth new shoots, became more concentrated and intense, more gloomy, sullen, irksome, and unyielding, than it had ever been before.\n\nWho wears such armour, too, bears with him ever another heavy retribution. It is of proof against conciliation, love, and confidence; against all gentle sympathy from without, all trust, all tenderness, all soft emotion; but to deep stabs in the self-love, it is as vulnerable as the bare breast to steel; and such tormenting festers rankle there, as follow on no other wounds, no, though dealt with the mailed hand of Pride itself, on weaker pride, disarmed and thrown down.\n\nSuch wounds were his. He felt them sharply, in the solitude of his old rooms; whither he now began often to retire again, and pass long solitary hours. It seemed his fate to be ever proud and powerful; ever humbled and powerless where he would be most strong. Who seemed fated to work out that doom?\n\nWho? Who was it who could win his wife as she had won his boy? Who was it who had shown him that new victory, as he sat in the dark corner? Who was it whose least word did what his utmost means could not? Who was it who, unaided by his love, regard or notice, thrived and grew beautiful when those so aided died? Who could it be, but the same child at whom he had often glanced uneasily in her motherless infancy, with a kind of dread, lest he might come to hate her; and of whom his foreboding was fulfilled, for he DID hate her in his heart?\n\nYes, and he would have it hatred, and he made it hatred, though some sparkles of the light in which she had appeared before him on the memorable night of his return home with his Bride, occasionally hung about her still. He knew now that she was beautiful; he did not dispute that she was graceful and winning, and that in the bright dawn of her womanhood she had come upon him, a surprise. But he turned even this against her. In his sullen and unwholesome brooding, the unhappy man, with a dull perception of his alienation from all hearts, and a vague yearning for what he had all his life repelled, made a distorted picture of his rights and wrongs, and justified himself with it against her. The worthier she promised to be of him, the greater claim he was disposed to antedate upon her duty and submission. When had she ever shown him duty and submission? Did she grace his life—or Edith’s? Had her attractions been manifested first to him—or Edith? Why, he and she had never been, from her birth, like father and child! They had always been estranged. She had crossed him every way and everywhere. She was leagued against him now. Her very beauty softened natures that were obdurate to him, and insulted him with an unnatural triumph.\n\nIt may have been that in all this there were mutterings of an awakened feeling in his breast, however selfishly aroused by his position of disadvantage, in comparison with what she might have made his life. But he silenced the distant thunder with the rolling of his sea of pride. He would bear nothing but his pride. And in his pride, a heap of inconsistency, and misery, and self-inflicted torment, he hated her.\n\nTo the moody, stubborn, sullen demon, that possessed him, his wife opposed her different pride in its full force. They never could have led a happy life together; but nothing could have made it more unhappy, than the wilful and determined warfare of such elements. His pride was set upon maintaining his magnificent supremacy, and forcing recognition of it from her. She would have been racked to death, and turned but her haughty glance of calm inflexible disdain upon him, to the last. Such recognition from Edith! He little knew through what a storm and struggle she had been driven onward to the crowning honour of his hand. He little knew how much she thought she had conceded, when she suffered him to call her wife.\n\nMr Dombey was resolved to show her that he was supreme. There must be no will but his. Proud he desired that she should be, but she must be proud for, not against him. As he sat alone, hardening, he would often hear her go out and come home, treading the round of London life with no more heed of his liking or disliking, pleasure or displeasure, than if he had been her groom. Her cold supreme indifference—his own unquestioned attribute usurped—stung him more than any other kind of treatment could have done; and he determined to bend her to his magnificent and stately will.\n\nHe had been long communing with these thoughts, when one night he sought her in her own apartment, after he had heard her return home late. She was alone, in her brilliant dress, and had but that moment come from her mother’s room. Her face was melancholy and pensive, when he came upon her; but it marked him at the door; for, glancing at the mirror before it, he saw immediately, as in a picture-frame, the knitted brow, and darkened beauty that he knew so well.\n\n“Mrs Dombey,” he said, entering, “I must beg leave to have a few words with you.”\n\n“To-morrow,” she replied.\n\n“There is no time like the present, Madam,” he returned. “You mistake your position. I am used to choose my own times; not to have them chosen for me. I think you scarcely understand who and what I am, Mrs Dombey.”\n\n“I think,” she answered, “that I understand you very well.”\n\nShe looked upon him as she said so, and folding her white arms, sparkling with gold and gems, upon her swelling breast, turned away her eyes.\n\nIf she had been less handsome, and less stately in her cold composure, she might not have had the power of impressing him with the sense of disadvantage that penetrated through his utmost pride. But she had the power, and he felt it keenly. He glanced round the room: saw how the splendid means of personal adornment, and the luxuries of dress, were scattered here and there, and disregarded; not in mere caprice and carelessness (or so he thought), but in a steadfast haughty disregard of costly things: and felt it more and more. Chaplets of flowers, plumes of feathers, jewels, laces, silks and satins; look where he would, he saw riches, despised, poured out, and made of no account. The very diamonds—a marriage gift—that rose and fell impatiently upon her bosom, seemed to pant to break the chain that clasped them round her neck, and roll down on the floor where she might tread upon them.\n\nHe felt his disadvantage, and he showed it. Solemn and strange among this wealth of colour and voluptuous glitter, strange and constrained towards its haughty mistress, whose repellent beauty it repeated, and presented all around him, as in so many fragments of a mirror, he was conscious of embarrassment and awkwardness. Nothing that ministered to her disdainful self-possession could fail to gall him. Galled and irritated with himself, he sat down, and went on, in no improved humour:\n\n“Mrs Dombey, it is very necessary that there should be some understanding arrived at between us. Your conduct does not please me, Madam.”\n\nShe merely glanced at him again, and again averted her eyes; but she might have spoken for an hour, and expressed less.\n\n“I repeat, Mrs Dombey, does not please me. I have already taken occasion to request that it may be corrected. I now insist upon it.”\n\n“You chose a fitting occasion for your first remonstrance, Sir, and you adopt a fitting manner, and a fitting word for your second. You insist! To me!”\n\n“Madam,” said Mr Dombey, with his most offensive air of state, “I have made you my wife. You bear my name. You are associated with my position and my reputation. I will not say that the world in general may be disposed to think you honoured by that association; but I will say that I am accustomed to ‘insist,’ to my connexions and dependents.”\n\n“Which may you be pleased to consider me? she asked.\n\n“Possibly I may think that my wife should partake—or does partake, and cannot help herself—of both characters, Mrs Dombey.”\n\nShe bent her eyes upon him steadily, and set her trembling lips. He saw her bosom throb, and saw her face flush and turn white. All this he could know, and did: but he could not know that one word was whispering in the deep recesses of her heart, to keep her quiet; and that the word was Florence.\n\nBlind idiot, rushing to a precipice! He thought she stood in awe of him.\n\n“You are too expensive, Madam,” said Mr Dombey. “You are extravagant. You waste a great deal of money—or what would be a great deal in the pockets of most gentlemen—in cultivating a kind of society that is useless to me, and, indeed, that upon the whole is disagreeable to me. I have to insist upon a total change in all these respects. I know that in the novelty of possessing a tithe of such means as Fortune has placed at your disposal, ladies are apt to run into a sudden extreme. There has been more than enough of that extreme. I beg that Mrs Granger’s very different experiences may now come to the instruction of Mrs Dombey.”\n\nStill the fixed look, the trembling lips, the throbbing breast, the face now crimson and now white; and still the deep whisper Florence, Florence, speaking to her in the beating of her heart.\n\nHis insolence of self-importance dilated as he saw this alteration in her. Swollen no less by her past scorn of him, and his so recent feeling of disadvantage, than by her present submission (as he took it to be), it became too mighty for his breast, and burst all bounds. Why, who could long resist his lofty will and pleasure! He had resolved to conquer her, and look here!\n\n“You will further please, Madam,” said Mr Dombey, in a tone of sovereign command, “to understand distinctly, that I am to be deferred to and obeyed. That I must have a positive show and confession of deference before the world, Madam. I am used to this. I require it as my right. In short I will have it. I consider it no unreasonable return for the worldly advancement that has befallen you; and I believe nobody will be surprised, either at its being required from you, or at your making it.—To Me—To Me!” he added, with emphasis.\n\nNo word from her. No change in her. Her eyes upon him.\n\n“I have learnt from your mother, Mrs Dombey,” said Mr Dombey, with magisterial importance, “what no doubt you know, namely, that Brighton is recommended for her health. Mr Carker has been so good.”\n\nShe changed suddenly. Her face and bosom glowed as if the red light of an angry sunset had been flung upon them. Not unobservant of the change, and putting his own interpretation upon it, Mr Dombey resumed:\n\n“Mr Carker has been so good as to go down and secure a house there, for a time. On the return of the establishment to London, I shall take such steps for its better management as I consider necessary. One of these, will be the engagement at Brighton (if it is to be effected), of a very respectable reduced person there, a Mrs Pipchin, formerly employed in a situation of trust in my family, to act as housekeeper. An establishment like this, presided over but nominally, Mrs Dombey, requires a competent head.”\n\nShe had changed her attitude before he arrived at these words, and now sat—still looking at him fixedly—turning a bracelet round and round upon her arm; not winding it about with a light, womanly touch, but pressing and dragging it over the smooth skin, until the white limb showed a bar of red.\n\n“I observed,” said Mr Dombey—“and this concludes what I deem it necessary to say to you at present, Mrs Dombey—I observed a moment ago, Madam, that my allusion to Mr Carker was received in a peculiar manner. On the occasion of my happening to point out to you, before that confidential agent, the objection I had to your mode of receiving my visitors, you were pleased to object to his presence. You will have to get the better of that objection, Madam, and to accustom yourself to it very probably on many similar occasions; unless you adopt the remedy which is in your own hands, of giving me no cause of complaint. Mr Carker,” said Mr Dombey, who, after the emotion he had just seen, set great store by this means of reducing his proud wife, and who was perhaps sufficiently willing to exhibit his power to that gentleman in a new and triumphant aspect, “Mr Carker being in my confidence, Mrs Dombey, may very well be in yours to such an extent. I hope, Mrs Dombey,” he continued, after a few moments, during which, in his increasing haughtiness, he had improved on his idea, “I may not find it necessary ever to entrust Mr Carker with any message of objection or remonstrance to you; but as it would be derogatory to my position and reputation to be frequently holding trivial disputes with a lady upon whom I have conferred the highest distinction that it is in my power to bestow, I shall not scruple to avail myself of his services if I see occasion.”\n\n“And now,” he thought, rising in his moral magnificence, and rising a stiffer and more impenetrable man than ever, “she knows me and my resolution.”\n\nThe hand that had so pressed the bracelet was laid heavily upon her breast, but she looked at him still, with an unaltered face, and said in a low voice:\n\n“Wait! For God’s sake! I must speak to you.”\n\nWhy did she not, and what was the inward struggle that rendered her incapable of doing so, for minutes, while, in the strong constraint she put upon her face, it was as fixed as any statue’s—looking upon him with neither yielding nor unyielding, liking nor hatred, pride not humility: nothing but a searching gaze?\n\n“Did I ever tempt you to seek my hand? Did I ever use any art to win you? Was I ever more conciliating to you when you pursued me, than I have been since our marriage? Was I ever other to you than I am?”\n\n“It is wholly unnecessary, Madam,” said Mr Dombey, “to enter upon such discussions.”\n\n“Did you think I loved you? Did you know I did not? Did you ever care, Man! for my heart, or propose to yourself to win the worthless thing? Was there any poor pretence of any in our bargain? Upon your side, or on mine?”\n\n“These questions,” said Mr Dombey, “are all wide of the purpose, Madam.”\n\nShe moved between him and the door to prevent his going away, and drawing her majestic figure to its height, looked steadily upon him still.\n\n“You answer each of them. You answer me before I speak, I see. How can you help it; you who know the miserable truth as well as I? Now, tell me. If I loved you to devotion, could I do more than render up my whole will and being to you, as you have just demanded? If my heart were pure and all untried, and you its idol, could you ask more; could you have more?”\n\n“Possibly not, Madam,” he returned coolly.\n\n“You know how different I am. You see me looking on you now, and you can read the warmth of passion for you that is breathing in my face.” Not a curl of the proud lip, not a flash of the dark eye, nothing but the same intent and searching look, accompanied these words. “You know my general history. You have spoken of my mother. Do you think you can degrade, or bend or break, me to submission and obedience?”\n\nMr Dombey smiled, as he might have smiled at an inquiry whether he thought he could raise ten thousand pounds.\n\n“If there is anything unusual here,” she said, with a slight motion of her hand before her brow, which did not for a moment flinch from its immovable and otherwise expressionless gaze, “as I know there are unusual feelings here,” raising the hand she pressed upon her bosom, and heavily returning it, “consider that there is no common meaning in the appeal I am going to make you. Yes, for I am going;” she said it as in prompt reply to something in his face; “to appeal to you.”\n\nMr Dombey, with a slightly condescending bend of his chin that rustled and crackled his stiff cravat, sat down on a sofa that was near him, to hear the appeal.\n\n“If you can believe that I am of such a nature now,”—he fancied he saw tears glistening in her eyes, and he thought, complacently, that he had forced them from her, though none fell on her cheek, and she regarded him as steadily as ever,—“as would make what I now say almost incredible to myself, said to any man who had become my husband, but, above all, said to you, you may, perhaps, attach the greater weight to it. In the dark end to which we are tending, and may come, we shall not involve ourselves alone (that might not be much) but others.”\n\nOthers! He knew at whom that word pointed, and frowned heavily.\n\n“I speak to you for the sake of others. Also your own sake; and for mine. Since our marriage, you have been arrogant to me; and I have repaid you in kind. You have shown to me and everyone around us, every day and hour, that you think I am graced and distinguished by your alliance. I do not think so, and have shown that too. It seems you do not understand, or (so far as your power can go) intend that each of us shall take a separate course; and you expect from me instead, a homage you will never have.”\n\nAlthough her face was still the same, there was emphatic confirmation of this “Never” in the very breath she drew.\n\n“I feel no tenderness towards you; that you know. You would care nothing for it, if I did or could. I know as well that you feel none towards me. But we are linked together; and in the knot that ties us, as I have said, others are bound up. We must both die; we are both connected with the dead already, each by a little child. Let us forbear.”\n\nMr Dombey took a long respiration, as if he would have said, Oh! was this all!\n\n“There is no wealth,” she went on, turning paler as she watched him, while her eyes grew yet more lustrous in their earnestness, “that could buy these words of me, and the meaning that belongs to them. Once cast away as idle breath, no wealth or power can bring them back. I mean them; I have weighed them; and I will be true to what I undertake. If you will promise to forbear on your part, I will promise to forbear on mine. We are a most unhappy pair, in whom, from different causes, every sentiment that blesses marriage, or justifies it, is rooted out; but in the course of time, some friendship, or some fitness for each other, may arise between us. I will try to hope so, if you will make the endeavour too; and I will look forward to a better and a happier use of age than I have made of youth or prime.”\n\nThroughout she had spoken in a low plain voice, that neither rose nor fell; ceasing, she dropped the hand with which she had enforced herself to be so passionless and distinct, but not the eyes with which she had so steadily observed him.\n\n“Madam,” said Mr Dombey, with his utmost dignity, “I cannot entertain any proposal of this extraordinary nature.”\n\nShe looked at him yet, without the least change.\n\n“I cannot,” said Mr Dombey, rising as he spoke, “consent to temporise or treat with you, Mrs Dombey, upon a subject as to which you are in possession of my opinions and expectations. I have stated my ultimatum, Madam, and have only to request your very serious attention to it.”\n\nTo see the face change to its old expression, deepened in intensity! To see the eyes droop as from some mean and odious object! To see the lighting of the haughty brow! To see scorn, anger, indignation, and abhorrence starting into sight, and the pale blank earnestness vanish like a mist! He could not choose but look, although he looked to his dismay.\n\n“Go, Sir!” she said, pointing with an imperious hand towards the door. “Our first and last confidence is at an end. Nothing can make us stranger to each other than we are henceforth.”\n\n“I shall take my rightful course, Madam,” said Mr Dombey, “undeterred, you may be sure, by any general declamation.”\n\nShe turned her back upon him, and, without reply, sat down before her glass.\n\n“I place my reliance on your improved sense of duty, and more correct feeling, and better reflection, Madam,” said Mr Dombey.\n\nShe answered not one word. He saw no more expression of any heed of him, in the mirror, than if he had been an unseen spider on the wall, or beetle on the floor, or rather, than if he had been the one or other, seen and crushed when she last turned from him, and forgotten among the ignominious and dead vermin of the ground.\n\nHe looked back, as he went out at the door, upon the well-lighted and luxurious room, the beautiful and glittering objects everywhere displayed, the shape of Edith in its rich dress seated before her glass, and the face of Edith as the glass presented it to him; and betook himself to his old chamber of cogitation, carrying away with him a vivid picture in his mind of all these things, and a rambling and unaccountable speculation (such as sometimes comes into a man’s head) how they would all look when he saw them next.\n\nFor the rest, Mr Dombey was very taciturn, and very dignified, and very confident of carrying out his purpose; and remained so.\n\nHe did not design accompanying the family to Brighton; but he graciously informed Cleopatra at breakfast, on the morning of departure, which arrived a day or two afterwards, that he might be expected down, soon. There was no time to be lost in getting Cleopatra to any place recommended as being salutary; for, indeed, she seemed upon the wane, and turning of the earth, earthy.\n\nWithout having undergone any decided second attack of her malady, the old woman seemed to have crawled backward in her recovery from the first. She was more lean and shrunken, more uncertain in her imbecility, and made stranger confusions in her mind and memory. Among other symptoms of this last affliction, she fell into the habit of confounding the names of her two sons-in-law, the living and the deceased; and in general called Mr Dombey, either “Grangeby,” or “Domber,” or indifferently, both.\n\nBut she was youthful, very youthful still; and in her youthfulness appeared at breakfast, before going away, in a new bonnet made express, and a travelling robe that was embroidered and braided like an old baby’s. It was not easy to put her into a fly-away bonnet now, or to keep the bonnet in its place on the back of her poor nodding head, when it was got on. In this instance, it had not only the extraneous effect of being always on one side, but of being perpetually tapped on the crown by Flowers the maid, who attended in the background during breakfast to perform that duty.\n\n“Now, my dearest Grangeby,” said Mrs Skewton, “you must posively prom,” she cut some of her words short, and cut out others altogether, “come down very soon.”\n\n“I said just now, Madam,” returned Mr Dombey, loudly and laboriously, “that I am coming in a day or two.”\n\n“Bless you, Domber!”\n\nHere the Major, who was come to take leave of the ladies, and who was staring through his apoplectic eyes at Mrs Skewton’s face with the disinterested composure of an immortal being, said:\n\n“Begad, Ma’am, you don’t ask old Joe to come!”\n\n“Sterious wretch, who’s he?” lisped Cleopatra. But a tap on the bonnet from Flowers seeming to jog her memory, she added, “Oh! You mean yourself, you naughty creature!”\n\n“Devilish queer, Sir,” whispered the Major to Mr Dombey. “Bad case. Never did wrap up enough;” the Major being buttoned to the chin. “Why who should J. B. mean by Joe, but old Joe Bagstock—Joseph—your slave—Joe, Ma’am? Here! Here’s the man! Here are the Bagstock bellows, Ma’am!” cried the Major, striking himself a sounding blow on the chest.\n\n“My dearest Edith—Grangeby—it’s most trordinry thing,” said Cleopatra, pettishly, “that Major—”\n\n“Bagstock! J. B.!” cried the Major, seeing that she faltered for his name.\n\n“Well, it don’t matter,” said Cleopatra. “Edith, my love, you know I never could remember names—what was it? oh!—most trordinry thing that so many people want to come down to see me. I’m not going for long. I’m coming back. Surely they can wait, till I come back!”\n\nCleopatra looked all round the table as she said it, and appeared very uneasy.\n\n“I won’t have visitors—really don’t want visitors,” she said; “little repose—and all that sort of thing—is what I quire. No odious brutes must proach me till I’ve shaken off this numbness;” and in a grisly resumption of her coquettish ways, she made a dab at the Major with her fan, but overset Mr Dombey’s breakfast cup instead, which was in quite a different direction.\n\nThen she called for Withers, and charged him to see particularly that word was left about some trivial alterations in her room, which must be all made before she came back, and which must be set about immediately, as there was no saying how soon she might come back; for she had a great many engagements, and all sorts of people to call upon. Withers received these directions with becoming deference, and gave his guarantee for their execution; but when he withdrew a pace or two behind her, it appeared as if he couldn’t help looking strangely at the Major, who couldn’t help looking strangely at Mr Dombey, who couldn’t help looking strangely at Cleopatra, who couldn’t help nodding her bonnet over one eye, and rattling her knife and fork upon her plate in using them, as if she were playing castanets.\n\nEdith alone never lifted her eyes to any face at the table, and never seemed dismayed by anything her mother said or did. She listened to her disjointed talk, or at least, turned her head towards her when addressed; replied in a few low words when necessary; and sometimes stopped her when she was rambling, or brought her thoughts back with a monosyllable, to the point from which they had strayed. The mother, however unsteady in other things, was constant in this—that she was always observant of her. She would look at the beautiful face, in its marble stillness and severity, now with a kind of fearful admiration; now in a giggling foolish effort to move it to a smile; now with capricious tears and jealous shakings of her head, as imagining herself neglected by it; always with an attraction towards it, that never fluctuated like her other ideas, but had constant possession of her. From Edith she would sometimes look at Florence, and back again at Edith, in a manner that was wild enough; and sometimes she would try to look elsewhere, as if to escape from her daughter’s face; but back to it she seemed forced to come, although it never sought hers unless sought, or troubled her with one single glance.\n\nThe breakfast concluded, Mrs Skewton, affecting to lean girlishly upon the Major’s arm, but heavily supported on the other side by Flowers the maid, and propped up behind by Withers the page, was conducted to the carriage, which was to take her, Florence, and Edith to Brighton.\n\n“And is Joseph absolutely banished?” said the Major, thrusting in his purple face over the steps. “Damme, Ma’am, is Cleopatra so hard-hearted as to forbid her faithful Antony Bagstock to approach the presence?”\n\n“Go along!” said Cleopatra, “I can’t bear you. You shall see me when I come back, if you are very good.”\n\n“Tell Joseph, he may live in hope, Ma’am,” said the Major; “or he’ll die in despair.”\n\nCleopatra shuddered, and leaned back. “Edith, my dear,” she said. “Tell him—”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Such dreadful words,” said Cleopatra. “He uses such dreadful words!”\n\nEdith signed to him to retire, gave the word to go on, and left the objectionable Major to Mr Dombey. To whom he returned, whistling.\n\n“I’ll tell you what, Sir,” said the Major, with his hands behind him, and his legs very wide asunder, “a fair friend of ours has removed to Queer Street.”\n\n“What do you mean, Major?” inquired Mr Dombey.\n\n“I mean to say, Dombey,” returned the Major, “that you’ll soon be an orphan-in-law.”\n\nMr Dombey appeared to relish this waggish description of himself so very little, that the Major wound up with the horse’s cough, as an expression of gravity.\n\n“Damme, Sir,” said the Major, “there is no use in disguising a fact. Joe is blunt, Sir. That’s his nature. If you take old Josh at all, you take him as you find him; and a devilish rusty, old rasper, of a close-toothed, J. B. file, you do find him. Dombey,” said the Major, “your wife’s mother is on the move, Sir.”\n\n“I fear,” returned Mr Dombey, with much philosophy, “that Mrs Skewton is shaken.”\n\n“Shaken, Dombey!” said the Major. “Smashed!”\n\n“Change, however,” pursued Mr Dombey, “and attention, may do much yet.”\n\n“Don’t believe it, Sir,” returned the Major. “Damme, Sir, she never wrapped up enough. If a man don’t wrap up,” said the Major, taking in another button of his buff waistcoat, “he has nothing to fall back upon. But some people will die. They will do it. Damme, they will. They’re obstinate. I tell you what, Dombey, it may not be ornamental; it may not be refined; it may be rough and tough; but a little of the genuine old English Bagstock stamina, Sir, would do all the good in the world to the human breed.”\n\nAfter imparting this precious piece of information, the Major, who was certainly true-blue, whatever other endowments he may have had or wanted, coming within the “genuine old English” classification, which has never been exactly ascertained, took his lobster-eyes and his apoplexy to the club, and choked there all day.\n\nCleopatra, at one time fretful, at another self-complacent, sometimes awake, sometimes asleep, and at all times juvenile, reached Brighton the same night, fell to pieces as usual, and was put away in bed; where a gloomy fancy might have pictured a more potent skeleton than the maid, who should have been one, watching at the rose-coloured curtains, which were carried down to shed their bloom upon her.\n\nIt was settled in high council of medical authority that she should take a carriage airing every day, and that it was important she should get out every day, and walk if she could. Edith was ready to attend her—always ready to attend her, with the same mechanical attention and immovable beauty—and they drove out alone; for Edith had an uneasiness in the presence of Florence, now that her mother was worse, and told Florence, with a kiss, that she would rather they two went alone.\n\nMrs Skewton, on one particular day, was in the irresolute, exacting, jealous temper that had developed itself on her recovery from her first attack. After sitting silent in the carriage watching Edith for some time, she took her hand and kissed it passionately. The hand was neither given nor withdrawn, but simply yielded to her raising of it, and being released, dropped down again, almost as if it were insensible. At this she began to whimper and moan, and say what a mother she had been, and how she was forgotten! This she continued to do at capricious intervals, even when they had alighted: when she herself was halting along with the joint support of Withers and a stick, and Edith was walking by her side, and the carriage slowly following at a little distance.\n\nIt was a bleak, lowering, windy day, and they were out upon the Downs with nothing but a bare sweep of land between them and the sky. The mother, with a querulous satisfaction in the monotony of her complaint, was still repeating it in a low voice from time to time, and the proud form of her daughter moved beside her slowly, when there came advancing over a dark ridge before them, two other figures, which in the distance, were so like an exaggerated imitation of their own, that Edith stopped.\n\nAlmost as she stopped, the two figures stopped; and that one which to Edith’s thinking was like a distorted shadow of her mother, spoke to the other, earnestly, and with a pointing hand towards them. That one seemed inclined to turn back, but the other, in which Edith recognised enough that was like herself to strike her with an unusual feeling, not quite free from fear, came on; and then they came on together.\n\nThe greater part of this observation, she made while walking towards them, for her stoppage had been momentary. Nearer observation showed her that they were poorly dressed, as wanderers about the country; that the younger woman carried knitted work or some such goods for sale; and that the old one toiled on empty-handed.\n\nAnd yet, however far removed she was in dress, in dignity, in beauty, Edith could not but compare the younger woman with herself, still. It may have been that she saw upon her face some traces which she knew were lingering in her own soul, if not yet written on that index; but, as the woman came on, returning her gaze, fixing her shining eyes upon her, undoubtedly presenting something of her own air and stature, and appearing to reciprocate her own thoughts, she felt a chill creep over her, as if the day were darkening, and the wind were colder.\n\nThey had now come up. The old woman, holding out her hand importunately, stopped to beg of Mrs Skewton. The younger one stopped too, and she and Edith looked in one another’s eyes.\n\n“What is it that you have to sell?” said Edith.\n\n“Only this,” returned the woman, holding out her wares, without looking at them. “I sold myself long ago.”\n\n“My Lady, don’t believe her,” croaked the old woman to Mrs Skewton; “don’t believe what she says. She loves to talk like that. She’s my handsome and undutiful daughter. She gives me nothing but reproaches, my Lady, for all I have done for her. Look at her now, my Lady, how she turns upon her poor old mother with her looks.”\n\nAs Mrs Skewton drew her purse out with a trembling hand, and eagerly fumbled for some money, which the other old woman greedily watched for—their heads all but touching, in their hurry and decrepitude—Edith interposed:\n\n“I have seen you,” addressing the old woman, “before.”\n\n“Yes, my Lady,” with a curtsey. “Down in Warwickshire. The morning among the trees. When you wouldn’t give me nothing. But the gentleman, he give me something! Oh, bless him, bless him!” mumbled the old woman, holding up her skinny hand, and grinning frightfully at her daughter.\n\n“It’s of no use attempting to stay me, Edith!” said Mrs Skewton, angrily anticipating an objection from her. “You know nothing about it. I won’t be dissuaded. I am sure this is an excellent woman, and a good mother.”\n\n“Yes, my Lady, yes,” chattered the old woman, holding out her avaricious hand. “Thankee, my Lady. Lord bless you, my Lady. Sixpence more, my pretty Lady, as a good mother yourself.”\n\n“And treated undutifully enough, too, my good old creature, sometimes, I assure you,” said Mrs Skewton, whimpering. “There! Shake hands with me. You’re a very good old creature—full of what’s-his-name—and all that. You’re all affection and et cetera, ain’t you?”\n\n“Oh, yes, my Lady!”\n\n“Yes, I’m sure you are; and so’s that gentlemanly creature Grangeby. I must really shake hands with you again. And now you can go, you know; and I hope,” addressing the daughter, “that you’ll show more gratitude, and natural what’s-its-name, and all the rest of it—but I never remember names—for there never was a better mother than the good old creature’s been to you. Come, Edith!”\n\nAs the ruin of Cleopatra tottered off whimpering, and wiping its eyes with a gingerly remembrance of rouge in their neighbourhood, the old woman hobbled another way, mumbling and counting her money. Not one word more, nor one other gesture, had been exchanged between Edith and the younger woman, but neither had removed her eyes from the other for a moment. They had remained confronted until now, when Edith, as awakening from a dream, passed slowly on.\n\n“You’re a handsome woman,” muttered her shadow, looking after her; “but good looks won’t save us. And you’re a proud woman; but pride won’t save us. We had need to know each other when we meet again!”\n\n# CHAPTER XLI. New Voices in the Waves\n\nAll is going on as it was wont. The waves are hoarse with repetition of their mystery; the dust lies piled upon the shore; the sea-birds soar and hover; the winds and clouds go forth upon their trackless flight; the white arms beckon, in the moonlight, to the invisible country far away.\n\nWith a tender melancholy pleasure, Florence finds herself again on the old ground so sadly trodden, yet so happily, and thinks of him in the quiet place, where he and she have many and many a time conversed together, with the water welling up about his couch. And now, as she sits pensive there, she hears in the wild low murmur of the sea, his little story told again, his very words repeated; and finds that all her life and hopes, and griefs, since—in the solitary house, and in the pageant it has changed to—have a portion in the burden of the marvellous song.\n\nAnd gentle Mr Toots, who wanders at a distance, looking wistfully towards the figure that he dotes upon, and has followed there, but cannot in his delicacy disturb at such a time, likewise hears the requiem of little Dombey on the waters, rising and falling in the lulls of their eternal madrigal in praise of Florence. Yes! and he faintly understands, poor Mr Toots, that they are saying something of a time when he was sensible of being brighter and not addle-brained; and the tears rising in his eyes when he fears that he is dull and stupid now, and good for little but to be laughed at, diminish his satisfaction in their soothing reminder that he is relieved from present responsibility to the Chicken, by the absence of that game head of poultry in the country, training (at Toots’s cost) for his great mill with the Larkey Boy.\n\nBut Mr Toots takes courage, when they whisper a kind thought to him; and by slow degrees and with many indecisive stoppages on the way, approaches Florence. Stammering and blushing, Mr Toots affects amazement when he comes near her, and says (having followed close on the carriage in which she travelled, every inch of the way from London, loving even to be choked by the dust of its wheels) that he never was so surprised in all his life.\n\n“And you’ve brought Diogenes, too, Miss Dombey!” says Mr Toots, thrilled through and through by the touch of the small hand so pleasantly and frankly given him.\n\nNo doubt Diogenes is there, and no doubt Mr Toots has reason to observe him, for he comes straightway at Mr Toots’s legs, and tumbles over himself in the desperation with which he makes at him, like a very dog of Montargis. But he is checked by his sweet mistress.\n\n“Down, Di, down. Don’t you remember who first made us friends, Di? For shame!”\n\nOh! Well may Di lay his loving cheek against her hand, and run off, and run back, and run round her, barking, and run headlong at anybody coming by, to show his devotion. Mr Toots would run headlong at anybody, too. A military gentleman goes past, and Mr Toots would like nothing better than to run at him, full tilt.\n\n“Diogenes is quite in his native air, isn’t he, Miss Dombey?” says Mr Toots.\n\nFlorence assents, with a grateful smile.\n\n“Miss Dombey,” says Mr Toots, “beg your pardon, but if you would like to walk to Blimber’s, I—I’m going there.”\n\nFlorence puts her arm in that of Mr Toots without a word, and they walk away together, with Diogenes going on before. Mr Toots’s legs shake under him; and though he is splendidly dressed, he feels misfits, and sees wrinkles, in the masterpieces of Burgess and Co., and wishes he had put on that brightest pair of boots.\n\nDoctor Blimber’s house, outside, has as scholastic and studious an air as ever; and up there is the window where she used to look for the pale face, and where the pale face brightened when it saw her, and the wasted little hand waved kisses as she passed. The door is opened by the same weak-eyed young man, whose imbecility of grin at sight of Mr Toots is feebleness of character personified. They are shown into the Doctor’s study, where blind Homer and Minerva give them audience as of yore, to the sober ticking of the great clock in the hall; and where the globes stand still in their accustomed places, as if the world were stationary too, and nothing in it ever perished in obedience to the universal law, that, while it keeps it on the roll, calls everything to earth.\n\nAnd here is Doctor Blimber, with his learned legs; and here is Mrs Blimber, with her sky-blue cap; and here Cornelia, with her sandy little row of curls, and her bright spectacles, still working like a sexton in the graves of languages. Here is the table upon which he sat forlorn and strange, the “new boy” of the school; and hither comes the distant cooing of the old boys, at their old lives in the old room on the old principle!\n\n“Toots,” says Doctor Blimber, “I am very glad to see you, Toots.”\n\nMr Toots chuckles in reply.\n\n“Also to see you, Toots, in such good company,” says Doctor Blimber.\n\nMr Toots, with a scarlet visage, explains that he has met Miss Dombey by accident, and that Miss Dombey wishing, like himself, to see the old place, they have come together.\n\n“You will like,” says Doctor Blimber, “to step among our young friends, Miss Dombey, no doubt. All fellow-students of yours, Toots, once. I think we have no new disciples in our little portico, my dear,” says Doctor Blimber to Cornelia, “since Mr Toots left us.”\n\n“Except Bitherstone,” returns Cornelia.\n\n“Ay, truly,” says the Doctor. “Bitherstone is new to Mr Toots.”\n\nNew to Florence, too, almost; for, in the schoolroom, Bitherstone—no longer Master Bitherstone of Mrs Pipchin’s—shows in collars and a neckcloth, and wears a watch. But Bitherstone, born beneath some Bengal star of ill-omen, is extremely inky; and his Lexicon has got so dropsical from constant reference, that it won’t shut, and yawns as if it really could not bear to be so bothered. So does Bitherstone its master, forced at Doctor Blimber’s highest pressure; but in the yawn of Bitherstone there is malice and snarl, and he has been heard to say that he wishes he could catch “old Blimber” in India. He’d precious soon find himself carried up the country by a few of his (Bitherstone’s) Coolies, and handed over to the Thugs; he can tell him that.\n\nBriggs is still grinding in the mill of knowledge; and Tozer, too; and Johnson, too; and all the rest; the older pupils being principally engaged in forgetting, with prodigious labour, everything they knew when they were younger. All are as polite and as pale as ever; and among them, Mr Feeder, B.A., with his bony hand and bristly head, is still hard at it; with his Herodotus stop on just at present, and his other barrels on a shelf behind him.\n\nA mighty sensation is created, even among these grave young gentlemen, by a visit from the emancipated Toots; who is regarded with a kind of awe, as one who has passed the Rubicon, and is pledged never to come back, and concerning the cut of whose clothes, and fashion of whose jewellery, whispers go about, behind hands; the bilious Bitherstone, who is not of Mr Toots’s time, affecting to despise the latter to the smaller boys, and saying he knows better, and that he should like to see him coming that sort of thing in Bengal, where his mother had got an emerald belonging to him that was taken out of the footstool of a Rajah. Come now!\n\nBewildering emotions are awakened also by the sight of Florence, with whom every young gentleman immediately falls in love, again; except, as aforesaid, the bilious Bitherstone, who declines to do so, out of contradiction. Black jealousies of Mr Toots arise, and Briggs is of opinion that he ain’t so very old after all. But this disparaging insinuation is speedily made nought by Mr Toots saying aloud to Mr Feeder, B.A., “How are you, Feeder?” and asking him to come and dine with him today at the Bedford; in right of which feats he might set up as Old Parr, if he chose, unquestioned.\n\nThere is much shaking of hands, and much bowing, and a great desire on the part of each young gentleman to take Toots down in Miss Dombey’s good graces; and then, Mr Toots having bestowed a chuckle on his old desk, Florence and he withdraw with Mrs Blimber and Cornelia; and Doctor Blimber is heard to observe behind them as he comes out last, and shuts the door, “Gentlemen, we will now resume our studies,” For that and little else is what the Doctor hears the sea say, or has heard it saying all his life.\n\nFlorence then steals away and goes upstairs to the old bedroom with Mrs Blimber and Cornelia; Mr Toots, who feels that neither he nor anybody else is wanted there, stands talking to the Doctor at the study-door, or rather hearing the Doctor talk to him, and wondering how he ever thought the study a great sanctuary, and the Doctor, with his round turned legs, like a clerical pianoforte, an awful man. Florence soon comes down and takes leave; Mr Toots takes leave; and Diogenes, who has been worrying the weak-eyed young man pitilessly all the time, shoots out at the door, and barks a glad defiance down the cliff; while Melia, and another of the Doctor’s female domestics, looks out of an upper window, laughing “at that there Toots,” and saying of Miss Dombey, “But really though, now—ain’t she like her brother, only prettier?”\n\nMr Toots, who saw when Florence came down that there were tears upon her face, is desperately anxious and uneasy, and at first fears that he did wrong in proposing the visit. But he is soon relieved by her saying she is very glad to have been there again, and by her talking quite cheerfully about it all, as they walked on by the sea. What with the voices there, and her sweet voice, when they come near Mr Dombey’s house, and Mr Toots must leave her, he is so enslaved that he has not a scrap of free-will left; when she gives him her hand at parting, he cannot let it go.\n\n“Miss Dombey, I beg your pardon,” says Mr Toots, in a sad fluster, “but if you would allow me to—to—”\n\nThe smiling and unconscious look of Florence brings him to a dead stop.\n\n“If you would allow me to—if you would not consider it a liberty, Miss Dombey, if I was to—without any encouragement at all, if I was to hope, you know,” says Mr Toots.\n\nFlorence looks at him inquiringly.\n\n“Miss Dombey,” says Mr Toots, who feels that he is in for it now, “I really am in that state of adoration of you that I don’t know what to do with myself. I am the most deplorable wretch. If it wasn’t at the corner of the Square at present, I should go down on my knees, and beg and entreat of you, without any encouragement at all, just to let me hope that I may—may think it possible that you—”\n\n“Oh, if you please, don’t!” cries Florence, for the moment quite alarmed and distressed. “Oh, pray don’t, Mr Toots. Stop, if you please. Don’t say any more. As a kindness and a favour to me, don’t.”\n\nMr Toots is dreadfully abashed, and his mouth opens.\n\n“You have been so good to me,” says Florence, “I am so grateful to you, I have such reason to like you for being a kind friend to me, and I do like you so much;” and here the ingenuous face smiles upon him with the pleasantest look of honesty in the world; “that I am sure you are only going to say good-bye!”\n\n“Certainly, Miss Dombey,” says Mr Toots, “I—I—that’s exactly what I mean. It’s of no consequence.”\n\n“Good-bye!” cries Florence.\n\n“Good-bye, Miss Dombey!” stammers Mr Toots. “I hope you won’t think anything about it. It’s—it’s of no consequence, thank you. It’s not of the least consequence in the world.”\n\nPoor Mr Toots goes home to his hotel in a state of desperation, locks himself into his bedroom, flings himself upon his bed, and lies there for a long time; as if it were of the greatest consequence, nevertheless. But Mr Feeder, B.A., is coming to dinner, which happens well for Mr Toots, or there is no knowing when he might get up again. Mr Toots is obliged to get up to receive him, and to give him hospitable entertainment.\n\nAnd the generous influence of that social virtue, hospitality (to make no mention of wine and good cheer), opens Mr Toots’s heart, and warms him to conversation. He does not tell Mr Feeder, B.A., what passed at the corner of the Square; but when Mr Feeder asks him “When it is to come off?” Mr Toots replies, “that there are certain subjects”—which brings Mr Feeder down a peg or two immediately. Mr Toots adds, that he don’t know what right Blimber had to notice his being in Miss Dombey’s company, and that if he thought he meant impudence by it, he’d have him out, Doctor or no Doctor; but he supposes its only his ignorance. Mr Feeder says he has no doubt of it.\n\nMr Feeder, however, as an intimate friend, is not excluded from the subject. Mr Toots merely requires that it should be mentioned mysteriously, and with feeling. After a few glasses of wine, he gives Miss Dombey’s health, observing, “Feeder, you have no idea of the sentiments with which I propose that toast.” Mr Feeder replies, “Oh, yes, I have, my dear Toots; and greatly they redound to your honour, old boy.” Mr Feeder is then agitated by friendship, and shakes hands; and says, if ever Toots wants a brother, he knows where to find him, either by post or parcel. Mr Feeder like-wise says, that if he may advise, he would recommend Mr Toots to learn the guitar, or, at least the flute; for women like music, when you are paying your addresses to ’em, and he has found the advantage of it himself.\n\nThis brings Mr Feeder, B.A., to the confession that he has his eye upon Cornelia Blimber. He informs Mr Toots that he don’t object to spectacles, and that if the Doctor were to do the handsome thing and give up the business, why, there they are—provided for. He says it’s his opinion that when a man has made a handsome sum by his business, he is bound to give it up; and that Cornelia would be an assistance in it which any man might be proud of. Mr Toots replies by launching wildly out into Miss Dombey’s praises, and by insinuations that sometimes he thinks he should like to blow his brains out. Mr Feeder strongly urges that it would be a rash attempt, and shows him, as a reconcilement to existence, Cornelia’s portrait, spectacles and all.\n\nThus these quiet spirits pass the evening; and when it has yielded place to night, Mr Toots walks home with Mr Feeder, and parts with him at Doctor Blimber’s door. But Mr Feeder only goes up the steps, and when Mr Toots is gone, comes down again, to stroll upon the beach alone, and think about his prospects. Mr Feeder plainly hears the waves informing him, as he loiters along, that Doctor Blimber will give up the business; and he feels a soft romantic pleasure in looking at the outside of the house, and thinking that the Doctor will first paint it, and put it into thorough repair.\n\nMr Toots is likewise roaming up and down, outside the casket that contains his jewel; and in a deplorable condition of mind, and not unsuspected by the police, gazes at a window where he sees a light, and which he has no doubt is Florence’s. But it is not, for that is Mrs Skewton’s room; and while Florence, sleeping in another chamber, dreams lovingly, in the midst of the old scenes, and their old associations live again, the figure which in grim reality is substituted for the patient boy’s on the same theatre, once more to connect it—but how differently!—with decay and death, is stretched there, wakeful and complaining. Ugly and haggard it lies upon its bed of unrest; and by it, in the terror of her unimpassioned loveliness—for it has terror in the sufferer’s failing eyes—sits Edith. What do the waves say, in the stillness of the night, to them?\n\n“Edith, what is that stone arm raised to strike me? Don’t you see it?”\n\n“There is nothing, mother, but your fancy.”\n\n“But my fancy! Everything is my fancy. Look! Is it possible that you don’t see it?”\n\n“Indeed, mother, there is nothing. Should I sit unmoved, if there were any such thing there?”\n\n“Unmoved?” looking wildly at her—“it’s gone now—and why are you so unmoved? That is not my fancy, Edith. It turns me cold to see you sitting at my side.”\n\n“I am sorry, mother.”\n\n“Sorry! You seem always sorry. But it is not for me!”\n\nWith that, she cries; and tossing her restless head from side to side upon her pillow, runs on about neglect, and the mother she has been, and the mother the good old creature was, whom they met, and the cold return the daughters of such mothers make. In the midst of her incoherence, she stops, looks at her daughter, cries out that her wits are going, and hides her face upon the bed.\n\nEdith, in compassion, bends over her and speaks to her. The sick old woman clutches her round the neck, and says, with a look of horror,\n\n“Edith! we are going home soon; going back. You mean that I shall go home again?”\n\n“Yes, mother, yes.”\n\n“And what he said—what’s-his-name, I never could remember names—Major—that dreadful word, when we came away—it’s not true? Edith!” with a shriek and a stare, “it’s not that that is the matter with me.”\n\nNight after night, the lights burn in the window, and the figure lies upon the bed, and Edith sits beside it, and the restless waves are calling to them both the whole night long. Night after night, the waves are hoarse with repetition of their mystery; the dust lies piled upon the shore; the sea-birds soar and hover; the winds and clouds are on their trackless flight; the white arms beckon, in the moonlight, to the invisible country far away.\n\nAnd still the sick old woman looks into the corner, where the stone arm—part of a figure of some tomb, she says—is raised to strike her. At last it falls; and then a dumb old woman lies upon the bed, and she is crooked and shrunk up, and half of her is dead.\n\nSuch is the figure, painted and patched for the sun to mock, that is drawn slowly through the crowd from day to day; looking, as it goes, for the good old creature who was such a mother, and making mouths as it peers among the crowd in vain. Such is the figure that is often wheeled down to the margin of the sea, and stationed there; but on which no wind can blow freshness, and for which the murmur of the ocean has no soothing word. She lies and listens to it by the hour; but its speech is dark and gloomy to her, and a dread is on her face, and when her eyes wander over the expanse, they see but a broad stretch of desolation between earth and heaven.\n\nFlorence she seldom sees, and when she does, is angry with and mows at. Edith is beside her always, and keeps Florence away; and Florence, in her bed at night, trembles at the thought of death in such a shape, and often wakes and listens, thinking it has come. No one attends on her but Edith. It is better that few eyes should see her; and her daughter watches alone by the bedside.\n\nA shadow even on that shadowed face, a sharpening even of the sharpened features, and a thickening of the veil before the eyes into a pall that shuts out the dim world, is come. Her wandering hands upon the coverlet join feebly palm to palm, and move towards her daughter; and a voice not like hers, not like any voice that speaks our mortal language—says, “For I nursed you!”\n\nEdith, without a tear, kneels down to bring her voice closer to the sinking head, and answers:\n\n“Mother, can you hear me?”\n\nStaring wide, she tries to nod in answer.\n\n“Can you recollect the night before I married?”\n\nThe head is motionless, but it expresses somehow that she does.\n\n“I told you then that I forgave your part in it, and prayed God to forgive my own. I told you that time past was at an end between us. I say so now, again. Kiss me, mother.”\n\nEdith touches the white lips, and for a moment all is still. A moment afterwards, her mother, with her girlish laugh, and the skeleton of the Cleopatra manner, rises in her bed.\n\nDraw the rose-coloured curtains. There is something else upon its flight besides the wind and clouds. Draw the rose-coloured curtains close!\n\nIntelligence of the event is sent to Mr Dombey in town, who waits upon Cousin Feenix (not yet able to make up his mind for Baden-Baden), who has just received it too. A good-natured creature like Cousin Feenix is the very man for a marriage or a funeral, and his position in the family renders it right that he should be consulted.\n\n“Dombey,” said Cousin Feenix, “upon my soul, I am very much shocked to see you on such a melancholy occasion. My poor aunt! She was a devilish lively woman.”\n\nMr Dombey replies, “Very much so.”\n\n“And made up,” says Cousin Feenix, “really young, you know, considering. I am sure, on the day of your marriage, I thought she was good for another twenty years. In point of fact, I said so to a man at Brooks’s—little Billy Joper—you know him, no doubt—man with a glass in his eye?”\n\nMr Dombey bows a negative. “In reference to the obsequies,” he hints, “whether there is any suggestion—”\n\n“Well, upon my life,” says Cousin Feenix, stroking his chin, which he has just enough of hand below his wristbands to do; “I really don’t know. There’s a Mausoleum down at my place, in the park, but I’m afraid it’s in bad repair, and, in point of fact, in a devil of a state. But for being a little out at elbows, I should have had it put to rights; but I believe the people come and make pic-nic parties there inside the iron railings.”\n\nMr Dombey is clear that this won’t do.\n\n“There’s an uncommon good church in the village,” says Cousin Feenix, thoughtfully; “pure specimen of the Anglo-Norman style, and admirably well sketched too by Lady Jane Finchbury—woman with tight stays—but they’ve spoilt it with whitewash, I understand, and it’s a long journey.”\n\n“Perhaps Brighton itself,” Mr Dombey suggests.\n\n“Upon my honour, Dombey, I don’t think we could do better,” says Cousin Feenix. “It’s on the spot, you see, and a very cheerful place.”\n\n“And when,” hints Mr Dombey, “would it be convenient?”\n\n“I shall make a point,” says Cousin Feenix, “of pledging myself for any day you think best. I shall have great pleasure (melancholy pleasure, of course) in following my poor aunt to the confines of the—in point of fact, to the grave,” says Cousin Feenix, failing in the other turn of speech.\n\n“Would Monday do for leaving town?” says Mr Dombey.\n\n“Monday would suit me to perfection,” replies Cousin Feenix. Therefore Mr Dombey arranges to take Cousin Feenix down on that day, and presently takes his leave, attended to the stairs by Cousin Feenix, who says, at parting, “I’m really excessively sorry, Dombey, that you should have so much trouble about it;” to which Mr Dombey answers, “Not at all.”\n\nAt the appointed time, Cousin Feenix and Mr Dombey meet, and go down to Brighton, and representing, in their two selves, all the other mourners for the deceased lady’s loss, attend her remains to their place of rest. Cousin Feenix, sitting in the mourning-coach, recognises innumerable acquaintances on the road, but takes no other notice of them, in decorum, than checking them off aloud, as they go by, for Mr Dombey’s information, as “Tom Johnson. Man with cork leg, from White’s. What, are you here, Tommy? Foley on a blood mare. The Smalder girls”—and so forth. At the ceremony Cousin Feenix is depressed, observing, that these are the occasions to make a man think, in point of fact, that he is getting shaky; and his eyes are really moistened, when it is over. But he soon recovers; and so do the rest of Mrs Skewton’s relatives and friends, of whom the Major continually tells the club that she never did wrap up enough; while the young lady with the back, who has so much trouble with her eyelids, says, with a little scream, that she must have been enormously old, and that she died of all kinds of horrors, and you mustn’t mention it.\n\nSo Edith’s mother lies unmentioned of her dear friends, who are deaf to the waves that are hoarse with repetition of their mystery, and blind to the dust that is piled upon the shore, and to the white arms that are beckoning, in the moonlight, to the invisible country far away. But all goes on, as it was wont, upon the margin of the unknown sea; and Edith standing there alone, and listening to its waves, has dank weed cast up at her feet, to strew her path in life withal.\n\n# CHAPTER XLII. Confidential and Accidental\n\nAttired no more in Captain Cuttle’s sable slops and sou’-wester hat, but dressed in a substantial suit of brown livery, which, while it affected to be a very sober and demure livery indeed, was really as self-satisfied and confident a one as tailor need desire to make, Rob the Grinder, thus transformed as to his outer man, and all regardless within of the Captain and the Midshipman, except when he devoted a few minutes of his leisure time to crowing over those inseparable worthies, and recalling, with much applauding music from that brazen instrument, his conscience, the triumphant manner in which he had disembarrassed himself of their company, now served his patron, Mr Carker. Inmate of Mr Carker’s house, and serving about his person, Rob kept his round eyes on the white teeth with fear and trembling, and felt that he had need to open them wider than ever.\n\nHe could not have quaked more, through his whole being, before the teeth, though he had come into the service of some powerful enchanter, and they had been his strongest spells. The boy had a sense of power and authority in this patron of his that engrossed his whole attention and exacted his most implicit submission and obedience. He hardly considered himself safe in thinking about him when he was absent, lest he should feel himself immediately taken by the throat again, as on the morning when he first became bound to him, and should see every one of the teeth finding him out, and taxing him with every fancy of his mind. Face to face with him, Rob had no more doubt that Mr Carker read his secret thoughts, or that he could read them by the least exertion of his will if he were so inclined, than he had that Mr Carker saw him when he looked at him. The ascendancy was so complete, and held him in such enthralment, that, hardly daring to think at all, but with his mind filled with a constantly dilating impression of his patron’s irresistible command over him, and power of doing anything with him, he would stand watching his pleasure, and trying to anticipate his orders, in a state of mental suspension, as to all other things.\n\nRob had not informed himself perhaps—in his then state of mind it would have been an act of no common temerity to inquire—whether he yielded so completely to this influence in any part, because he had floating suspicions of his patron’s being a master of certain treacherous arts in which he had himself been a poor scholar at the Grinders’ School. But certainly Rob admired him, as well as feared him. Mr Carker, perhaps, was better acquainted with the sources of his power, which lost nothing by his management of it.\n\nOn the very night when he left the Captain’s service, Rob, after disposing of his pigeons, and even making a bad bargain in his hurry, had gone straight down to Mr Carker’s house, and hotly presented himself before his new master with a glowing face that seemed to expect commendation.\n\n“What, scapegrace!” said Mr Carker, glancing at his bundle “Have you left your situation and come to me?”\n\n“Oh if you please, Sir,” faltered Rob, “you said, you know, when I come here last—”\n\n“I said,” returned Mr Carker, “what did I say?”\n\n“If you please, Sir, you didn’t say nothing at all, Sir,” returned Rob, warned by the manner of this inquiry, and very much disconcerted.\n\nHis patron looked at him with a wide display of gums, and shaking his forefinger, observed:\n\n“You’ll come to an evil end, my vagabond friend, I foresee. There’s ruin in store for you.\n\n“Oh if you please, don’t, Sir!” cried Rob, with his legs trembling under him. “I’m sure, Sir, I only want to work for you, Sir, and to wait upon you, Sir, and to do faithful whatever I’m bid, Sir.”\n\n“You had better do faithfully whatever you are bid,” returned his patron, “if you have anything to do with me.”\n\n“Yes, I know that, Sir,” pleaded the submissive Rob; “I’m sure of that, Sir. If you’ll only be so good as try me, Sir! And if ever you find me out, Sir, doing anything against your wishes, I give you leave to kill me.”\n\n“You dog!” said Mr Carker, leaning back in his chair, and smiling at him serenely. “That’s nothing to what I’d do to you, if you tried to deceive me.”\n\n“Yes, Sir,” replied the abject Grinder, “I’m sure you would be down upon me dreadful, Sir. I wouldn’t attempt for to go and do it, Sir, not if I was bribed with golden guineas.”\n\nThoroughly checked in his expectations of commendation, the crestfallen Grinder stood looking at his patron, and vainly endeavouring not to look at him, with the uneasiness which a cur will often manifest in a similar situation.\n\n“So you have left your old service, and come here to ask me to take you into mine, eh?” said Mr Carker.\n\n“Yes, if you please, Sir,” returned Rob, who, in doing so, had acted on his patron’s own instructions, but dared not justify himself by the least insinuation to that effect.\n\n“Well!” said Mr Carker. “You know me, boy?”\n\n“Please, Sir, yes, Sir,” returned Rob, tumbling with his hat, and still fixed by Mr Carker’s eye, and fruitlessly endeavouring to unfix himself.\n\nMr Carker nodded. “Take care, then!”\n\nRob expressed in a number of short bows his lively understanding of this caution, and was bowing himself back to the door, greatly relieved by the prospect of getting on the outside of it, when his patron stopped him.\n\n“Halloa!” he cried, calling him roughly back. “You have been—shut that door.”\n\nRob obeyed as if his life had depended on his alacrity.\n\n“You have been used to eaves-dropping. Do you know what that means?”\n\n“Listening, Sir?” Rob hazarded, after some embarrassed reflection.\n\nHis patron nodded. “And watching, and so forth.”\n\n“I wouldn’t do such a thing here, Sir,” answered Rob; “upon my word and honour, I wouldn’t, Sir, I wish I may die if I would, Sir, for anything that could be promised to me. I should consider it is as much as all the world was worth, to offer to do such a thing, unless I was ordered, Sir.”\n\n“You had better not” You have been used, too, to babbling and tattling,” said his patron with perfect coolness. “Beware of that here, or you’re a lost rascal,” and he smiled again, and again cautioned him with his forefinger.\n\nThe Grinder’s breath came short and thick with consternation. He tried to protest the purity of his intentions, but could only stare at the smiling gentleman in a stupor of submission, with which the smiling gentleman seemed well enough satisfied, for he ordered him downstairs, after observing him for some moments in silence, and gave him to understand that he was retained in his employment.\n\nThis was the manner of Rob the Grinder’s engagement by Mr Carker, and his awe-stricken devotion to that gentleman had strengthened and increased, if possible, with every minute of his service.\n\nIt was a service of some months’ duration, when early one morning, Rob opened the garden gate to Mr Dombey, who was come to breakfast with his master, by appointment. At the same moment his master himself came, hurrying forth to receive the distinguished guest, and give him welcome with all his teeth.\n\n“I never thought,” said Carker, when he had assisted him to alight from his horse, “to see you here, I’m sure. This is an extraordinary day in my calendar. No occasion is very special to a man like you, who may do anything; but to a man like me, the case is widely different.”\n\n“You have a tasteful place here, Carker,” said Mr Dombey, condescending to stop upon the lawn, to look about him.\n\n“You can afford to say so,” returned Carker. “Thank you.”\n\n“Indeed,” said Mr Dombey, in his lofty patronage, “anyone might say so. As far as it goes, it is a very commodious and well-arranged place—quite elegant.”\n\n“As far as it goes, truly,” returned Carker, with an air of disparagement. “It wants that qualification. Well! we have said enough about it; and though you can afford to praise it, I thank you nonetheless. Will you walk in?”\n\nMr Dombey, entering the house, noticed, as he had reason to do, the complete arrangement of the rooms, and the numerous contrivances for comfort and effect that abounded there. Mr Carker, in his ostentation of humility, received this notice with a deferential smile, and said he understood its delicate meaning, and appreciated it, but in truth the cottage was good enough for one in his position—better, perhaps, than such a man should occupy, poor as it was.\n\n“But perhaps to you, who are so far removed, it really does look better than it is,” he said, with his false mouth distended to its fullest stretch. “Just as monarchs imagine attractions in the lives of beggars.”\n\nHe directed a sharp glance and a sharp smile at Mr Dombey as he spoke, and a sharper glance, and a sharper smile yet, when Mr Dombey, drawing himself up before the fire, in the attitude so often copied by his second in command, looked round at the pictures on the walls. Cursorily as his cold eye wandered over them, Carker’s keen glance accompanied his, and kept pace with his, marking exactly where it went, and what it saw. As it rested on one picture in particular, Carker hardly seemed to breathe, his sidelong scrutiny was so cat-like and vigilant, but the eye of his great chief passed from that, as from the others, and appeared no more impressed by it than by the rest.\n\nCarker looked at it—it was the picture that resembled Edith—as if it were a living thing; and with a wicked, silent laugh upon his face, that seemed in part addressed to it, though it was all derisive of the great man standing so unconscious beside him. Breakfast was soon set upon the table; and, inviting Mr Dombey to a chair which had its back towards this picture, he took his own seat opposite to it as usual.\n\nMr Dombey was even graver than it was his custom to be, and quite silent. The parrot, swinging in the gilded hoop within her gaudy cage, attempted in vain to attract notice, for Carker was too observant of his visitor to heed her; and the visitor, abstracted in meditation, looked fixedly, not to say sullenly, over his stiff neckcloth, without raising his eyes from the table-cloth. As to Rob, who was in attendance, all his faculties and energies were so locked up in observation of his master, that he scarcely ventured to give shelter to the thought that the visitor was the great gentleman before whom he had been carried as a certificate of the family health, in his childhood, and to whom he had been indebted for his leather smalls.\n\n“Allow me,” said Carker suddenly, “to ask how Mrs Dombey is?”\n\nHe leaned forward obsequiously, as he made the inquiry, with his chin resting on his hand; and at the same time his eyes went up to the picture, as if he said to it, “Now, see, how I will lead him on!”\n\nMr Dombey reddened as he answered:\n\n“Mrs Dombey is quite well. You remind me, Carker, of some conversation that I wish to have with you.”\n\n“Robin, you can leave us,” said his master, at whose mild tones Robin started and disappeared, with his eyes fixed on his patron to the last. “You don’t remember that boy, of course?” he added, when the enmeshed Grinder was gone.\n\n“No,” said Mr Dombey, with magnificent indifference.\n\n“Not likely that a man like you would. Hardly possible,” murmured Carker. “But he is one of that family from whom you took a nurse. Perhaps you may remember having generously charged yourself with his education?”\n\n“Is it that boy?” said Mr Dombey, with a frown. “He does little credit to his education, I believe.”\n\n“Why, he is a young rip, I am afraid,” returned Carker, with a shrug. “He bears that character. But the truth is, I took him into my service because, being able to get no other employment, he conceived (had been taught at home, I daresay) that he had some sort of claim upon you, and was constantly trying to dog your heels with his petition. And although my defined and recognised connexion with your affairs is merely of a business character, still I have that spontaneous interest in everything belonging to you, that—”\n\nHe stopped again, as if to discover whether he had led Mr Dombey far enough yet. And again, with his chin resting on his hand, he leered at the picture.\n\n“Carker,” said Mr Dombey, “I am sensible that you do not limit your—”\n\n“Service,” suggested his smiling entertainer.\n\n“No; I prefer to say your regard,” observed Mr Dombey; very sensible, as he said so, that he was paying him a handsome and flattering compliment, “to our mere business relations. Your consideration for my feelings, hopes, and disappointments, in the little instance you have just now mentioned, is an example in point. I am obliged to you, Carker.”\n\nMr Carker bent his head slowly, and very softly rubbed his hands, as if he were afraid by any action to disturb the current of Mr Dombey’s confidence.\n\n“Your allusion to it is opportune,” said Mr Dombey, after a little hesitation; “for it prepares the way to what I was beginning to say to you, and reminds me that that involves no absolutely new relations between us, although it may involve more personal confidence on my part than I have hitherto—”\n\n“Distinguished me with,” suggested Carker, bending his head again: “I will not say to you how honoured I am; for a man like you well knows how much honour he has in his power to bestow at pleasure.”\n\n“Mrs Dombey and myself,” said Mr Dombey, passing this compliment with august self-denial, “are not quite agreed upon some points. We do not appear to understand each other yet. Mrs Dombey has something to learn.”\n\n“Mrs Dombey is distinguished by many rare attractions; and has been accustomed, no doubt, to receive much adulation,” said the smooth, sleek watcher of his slightest look and tone. “But where there is affection, duty, and respect, any little mistakes engendered by such causes are soon set right.”\n\nMr Dombey’s thoughts instinctively flew back to the face that had looked at him in his wife’s dressing-room when an imperious hand was stretched towards the door; and remembering the affection, duty, and respect, expressed in it, he felt the blood rush to his own face quite as plainly as the watchful eyes upon him saw it there.\n\n“Mrs Dombey and myself,” he went on to say, “had some discussion, before Mrs Skewton’s death, upon the causes of my dissatisfaction; of which you will have formed a general understanding from having been a witness of what passed between Mrs Dombey and myself on the evening when you were at our—at my house.”\n\n“When I so much regretted being present,” said the smiling Carker. “Proud as a man in my position necessarily must be of your familiar notice—though I give you no credit for it; you may do anything you please without losing caste—and honoured as I was by an early presentation to Mrs Dombey, before she was made eminent by bearing your name, I almost regretted that night, I assure you, that I had been the object of such especial good fortune.”\n\nThat any man could, under any possible circumstances, regret the being distinguished by his condescension and patronage, was a moral phenomenon which Mr Dombey could not comprehend. He therefore responded, with a considerable accession of dignity. “Indeed! And why, Carker?”\n\n“I fear,” returned the confidential agent, “that Mrs Dombey, never very much disposed to regard me with favourable interest—one in my position could not expect that, from a lady naturally proud, and whose pride becomes her so well—may not easily forgive my innocent part in that conversation. Your displeasure is no light matter, you must remember; and to be visited with it before a third party—”\n\n“Carker,” said Mr Dombey, arrogantly; “I presume that I am the first consideration?”\n\n“Oh! Can there be a doubt about it?” replied the other, with the impatience of a man admitting a notorious and incontrovertible fact.\n\n“Mrs Dombey becomes a secondary consideration, when we are both in question, I imagine,” said Mr Dombey. “Is that so?”\n\n“Is it so?” returned Carker. “Do you know better than anyone, that you have no need to ask?”\n\n“Then I hope, Carker,” said Mr Dombey, “that your regret in the acquisition of Mrs Dombey’s displeasure, may be almost counterbalanced by your satisfaction in retaining my confidence and good opinion.”\n\n“I have the misfortune, I find,” returned Carker, “to have incurred that displeasure. Mrs Dombey has expressed it to you?”\n\n“Mrs Dombey has expressed various opinions,” said Mr Dombey, with majestic coldness and indifference, “in which I do not participate, and which I am not inclined to discuss, or to recall. I made Mrs Dombey acquainted, some time since, as I have already told you, with certain points of domestic deference and submission on which I felt it necessary to insist. I failed to convince Mrs Dombey of the expediency of her immediately altering her conduct in those respects, with a view to her own peace and welfare, and my dignity; and I informed Mrs Dombey that if I should find it necessary to object or remonstrate again, I should express my opinion to her through yourself, my confidential agent.”\n\nBlended with the look that Carker bent upon him, was a devilish look at the picture over his head, that struck upon it like a flash of lightning.\n\n“Now, Carker,” said Mr Dombey, “I do not hesitate to say to you that I will carry my point. I am not to be trifled with. Mrs Dombey must understand that my will is law, and that I cannot allow of one exception to the whole rule of my life. You will have the goodness to undertake this charge, which, coming from me, is not unacceptable to you, I hope, whatever regret you may politely profess—for which I am obliged to you on behalf of Mrs Dombey; and you will have the goodness, I am persuaded, to discharge it as exactly as any other commission.”\n\n“You know,” said Mr Carker, “that you have only to command me.”\n\n“I know,” said Mr Dombey, with a majestic indication of assent, “that I have only to command you. It is necessary that I should proceed in this. Mrs Dombey is a lady undoubtedly highly qualified, in many respects, to—”\n\n“To do credit even to your choice,” suggested Carker, with a yawning show of teeth.\n\n“Yes; if you please to adopt that form of words,” said Mr Dombey, in his tone of state; “and at present I do not conceive that Mrs Dombey does that credit to it, to which it is entitled. There is a principle of opposition in Mrs Dombey that must be eradicated; that must be overcome: Mrs Dombey does not appear to understand,” said Mr Dombey, forcibly, “that the idea of opposition to Me is monstrous and absurd.”\n\n“We, in the City, know you better,” replied Carker, with a smile from ear to ear.\n\n“You know me better,” said Mr Dombey. “I hope so. Though, indeed, I am bound to do Mrs Dombey the justice of saying, however inconsistent it may seem with her subsequent conduct (which remains unchanged), that on my expressing my disapprobation and determination to her, with some severity, on the occasion to which I have referred, my admonition appeared to produce a very powerful effect.” Mr Dombey delivered himself of those words with most portentous stateliness. “I wish you to have the goodness, then, to inform Mrs Dombey, Carker, from me, that I must recall our former conversation to her remembrance, in some surprise that it has not yet had its effect. That I must insist upon her regulating her conduct by the injunctions laid upon her in that conversation. That I am not satisfied with her conduct. That I am greatly dissatisfied with it. And that I shall be under the very disagreeable necessity of making you the bearer of yet more unwelcome and explicit communications, if she has not the good sense and the proper feeling to adapt herself to my wishes, as the first Mrs Dombey did, and, I believe I may add, as any other lady in her place would.”\n\n“The first Mrs Dombey lived very happily,” said Carker.\n\n“The first Mrs Dombey had great good sense,” said Mr Dombey, in a gentlemanly toleration of the dead, “and very correct feeling.”\n\n“Is Miss Dombey like her mother, do you think?” said Carker.\n\nSwiftly and darkly, Mr Dombey’s face changed. His confidential agent eyed it keenly.\n\n“I have approached a painful subject,” he said, in a soft regretful tone of voice, irreconcilable with his eager eye. “Pray forgive me. I forget these chains of association in the interest I have. Pray forgive me.”\n\nBut for all he said, his eager eye scanned Mr Dombey’s downcast face none the less closely; and then it shot a strange triumphant look at the picture, as appealing to it to bear witness how he led him on again, and what was coming.\n\n“Carker,” said Mr Dombey, looking here and there upon the table, and saying in a somewhat altered and more hurried voice, and with a paler lip, “there is no occasion for apology. You mistake. The association is with the matter in hand, and not with any recollection, as you suppose. I do not approve of Mrs Dombey’s behaviour towards my daughter.”\n\n“Pardon me,” said Mr Carker, “I don’t quite understand.”\n\n“Understand then,” returned Mr Dombey, “that you may make that—that you will make that, if you please—matter of direct objection from me to Mrs Dombey. You will please to tell her that her show of devotion for my daughter is disagreeable to me. It is likely to be noticed. It is likely to induce people to contrast Mrs Dombey in her relation towards my daughter, with Mrs Dombey in her relation towards myself. You will have the goodness to let Mrs Dombey know, plainly, that I object to it; and that I expect her to defer, immediately, to my objection. Mrs Dombey may be in earnest, or she may be pursuing a whim, or she may be opposing me; but I object to it in any case, and in every case. If Mrs Dombey is in earnest, so much the less reluctant should she be to desist; for she will not serve my daughter by any such display. If my wife has any superfluous gentleness, and duty over and above her proper submission to me, she may bestow them where she pleases, perhaps; but I will have submission first!—Carker,” said Mr Dombey, checking the unusual emotion with which he had spoken, and falling into a tone more like that in which he was accustomed to assert his greatness, “you will have the goodness not to omit or slur this point, but to consider it a very important part of your instructions.”\n\nMr Carker bowed his head, and rising from the table, and standing thoughtfully before the fire, with his hand to his smooth chin, looked down at Mr Dombey with the evil slyness of some monkish carving, half human and half brute; or like a leering face on an old water-spout. Mr Dombey, recovering his composure by degrees, or cooling his emotion in his sense of having taken a high position, sat gradually stiffening again, and looking at the parrot as she swung to and fro, in her great wedding ring.\n\n“I beg your pardon,” said Carker, after a silence, suddenly resuming his chair, and drawing it opposite to Mr Dombey’s, “but let me understand. Mrs Dombey is aware of the probability of your making me the organ of your displeasure?”\n\n“Yes,” replied Mr Dombey. “I have said so.”\n\n“Yes,” rejoined Carker, quickly; “but why?”\n\n“Why!” Mr Dombey repeated, not without hesitation. “Because I told her.”\n\n“Ay,” replied Carker. “But why did you tell her? You see,” he continued with a smile, and softly laying his velvet hand, as a cat might have laid its sheathed claws, on Mr Dombey’s arm; “if I perfectly understand what is in your mind, I am so much more likely to be useful, and to have the happiness of being effectually employed. I think I do understand. I have not the honour of Mrs Dombey’s good opinion. In my position, I have no reason to expect it; but I take the fact to be, that I have not got it?”\n\n“Possibly not,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Consequently,” pursued Carker, “your making the communications to Mrs Dombey through me, is sure to be particularly unpalatable to that lady?”\n\n“It appears to me,” said Mr Dombey, with haughty reserve, and yet with some embarrassment, “that Mrs Dombey’s views upon the subject form no part of it as it presents itself to you and me, Carker. But it may be so.”\n\n“And—pardon me—do I misconceive you,” said Carker, “when I think you descry in this, a likely means of humbling Mrs Dombey’s pride—I use the word as expressive of a quality which, kept within due bounds, adorns and graces a lady so distinguished for her beauty and accomplishments—and, not to say of punishing her, but of reducing her to the submission you so naturally and justly require?”\n\n“I am not accustomed, Carker, as you know,” said Mr Dombey, “to give such close reasons for any course of conduct I think proper to adopt, but I will gainsay nothing of this. If you have any objection to found upon it, that is indeed another thing, and the mere statement that you have one will be sufficient. But I have not supposed, I confess, that any confidence I could entrust to you, would be likely to degrade you—”\n\n“Oh! I degraded!” exclaimed Carker. “In your service!”\n\n“—or to place you,” pursued Mr Dombey, “in a false position.”\n\n“I in a false position!” exclaimed Carker. “I shall be proud—delighted—to execute your trust. I could have wished, I own, to have given the lady at whose feet I would lay my humble duty and devotion—for is she not your wife!—no new cause of dislike; but a wish from you is, of course, paramount to every other consideration on earth. Besides, when Mrs Dombey is converted from these little errors of judgment, incidental, I would presume to say, to the novelty of her situation, I shall hope that she will perceive in the slight part I take, only a grain—my removed and different sphere gives room for little more—of the respect for you, and sacrifice of all considerations to you, of which it will be her pleasure and privilege to garner up a great store every day.”\n\nMr Dombey seemed, at the moment, again to see her with her hand stretched out towards the door, and again to hear through the mild speech of his confidential agent an echo of the words, “Nothing can make us stranger to each other than we are henceforth!” But he shook off the fancy, and did not shake in his resolution, and said, “Certainly, no doubt.”\n\n“There is nothing more,” quoth Carker, drawing his chair back to its old place—for they had taken little breakfast as yet—and pausing for an answer before he sat down.\n\n“Nothing,” said Mr Dombey, “but this. You will be good enough to observe, Carker, that no message to Mrs Dombey with which you are or may be charged, admits of reply. You will be good enough to bring me no reply. Mrs Dombey is informed that it does not become me to temporise or treat upon any matter that is at issue between us, and that what I say is final.”\n\nMr Carker signified his understanding of these credentials, and they fell to breakfast with what appetite they might. The Grinder also, in due time reappeared, keeping his eyes upon his master without a moment’s respite, and passing the time in a reverie of worshipful tenor. Breakfast concluded, Mr Dombey’s horse was ordered out again, and Mr Carker mounting his own, they rode off for the City together.\n\nMr Carker was in capital spirits, and talked much. Mr Dombey received his conversation with the sovereign air of a man who had a right to be talked to, and occasionally condescended to throw in a few words to carry on the conversation. So they rode on characteristically enough. But Mr Dombey, in his dignity, rode with very long stirrups, and a very loose rein, and very rarely deigned to look down to see where his horse went. In consequence of which it happened that Mr Dombey’s horse, while going at a round trot, stumbled on some loose stones, threw him, rolled over him, and lashing out with his iron-shod feet, in his struggles to get up, kicked him.\n\nMr Carker, quick of eye, steady of hand, and a good horseman, was afoot, and had the struggling animal upon his legs and by the bridle, in a moment. Otherwise that morning’s confidence would have been Mr Dombey’s last. Yet even with the flush and hurry of this action red upon him, he bent over his prostrate chief with every tooth disclosed, and muttered as he stooped down, “I have given good cause of offence to Mrs Dombey now, if she knew it!”\n\nMr Dombey being insensible, and bleeding from the head and face, was carried by certain menders of the road, under Carker’s direction, to the nearest public-house, which was not far off, and where he was soon attended by divers surgeons, who arrived in quick succession from all parts, and who seemed to come by some mysterious instinct, as vultures are said to gather about a camel who dies in the desert. After being at some pains to restore him to consciousness, these gentlemen examined into the nature of his injuries. One surgeon who lived hard by was strong for a compound fracture of the leg, which was the landlord’s opinion also; but two surgeons who lived at a distance, and were only in that neighbourhood by accident, combated this opinion so disinterestedly, that it was decided at last that the patient, though severely cut and bruised, had broken no bones but a lesser rib or so, and might be carefully taken home before night. His injuries being dressed and bandaged, which was a long operation, and he at length left to repose, Mr Carker mounted his horse again, and rode away to carry the intelligence home.\n\nCrafty and cruel as his face was at the best of times, though it was a sufficiently fair face as to form and regularity of feature, it was at its worst when he set forth on this errand; animated by the craft and cruelty of thoughts within him, suggestions of remote possibility rather than of design or plot, that made him ride as if he hunted men and women. Drawing rein at length, and slackening in his speed, as he came into the more public roads, he checked his white-legged horse into picking his way along as usual, and hid himself beneath his sleek, hushed, crouched manner, and his ivory smile, as he best could.\n\nHe rode direct to Mr Dombey’s house, alighted at the door, and begged to see Mrs Dombey on an affair of importance. The servant who showed him to Mr Dombey’s own room, soon returned to say that it was not Mrs Dombey’s hour for receiving visitors, and that he begged pardon for not having mentioned it before.\n\nMr Carker, who was quite prepared for a cold reception, wrote upon a card that he must take the liberty of pressing for an interview, and that he would not be so bold as to do so, for the second time (this he underlined), if he were not equally sure of the occasion being sufficient for his justification. After a trifling delay, Mrs Dombey’s maid appeared, and conducted him to a morning room upstairs, where Edith and Florence were together.\n\nHe had never thought Edith half so beautiful before. Much as he admired the graces of her face and form, and freshly as they dwelt within his sensual remembrance, he had never thought her half so beautiful.\n\nHer glance fell haughtily upon him in the doorway; but he looked at Florence—though only in the act of bending his head, as he came in—with some irrepressible expression of the new power he held; and it was his triumph to see the glance droop and falter, and to see that Edith half rose up to receive him.\n\nHe was very sorry, he was deeply grieved; he couldn’t say with what unwillingness he came to prepare her for the intelligence of a very slight accident. He entreated Mrs Dombey to compose herself. Upon his sacred word of honour, there was no cause of alarm. But Mr Dombey—\n\nFlorence uttered a sudden cry. He did not look at her, but at Edith. Edith composed and reassured her. She uttered no cry of distress. No, no.\n\nMr Dombey had met with an accident in riding. His horse had slipped, and he had been thrown.\n\nFlorence wildly exclaimed that he was badly hurt; that he was killed!\n\nNo. Upon his honour, Mr Dombey, though stunned at first, was soon recovered, and though certainly hurt was in no kind of danger. If this were not the truth, he, the distressed intruder, never could have had the courage to present himself before Mrs Dombey. It was the truth indeed, he solemnly assured her.\n\nAll this he said as if he were answering Edith, and not Florence, and with his eyes and his smile fastened on Edith.\n\nHe then went on to tell her where Mr Dombey was lying, and to request that a carriage might be placed at his disposal to bring him home.\n\n“Mama,” faltered Florence in tears, “if I might venture to go!”\n\nMr Carker, having his eyes on Edith when he heard these words, gave her a secret look and slightly shook his head. He saw how she battled with herself before she answered him with her handsome eyes, but he wrested the answer from her—he showed her that he would have it, or that he would speak and cut Florence to the heart—and she gave it to him. As he had looked at the picture in the morning, so he looked at her afterwards, when she turned her eyes away.\n\n“I am directed to request,” he said, “that the new housekeeper—Mrs Pipchin, I think, is the name—”\n\nNothing escaped him. He saw, in an instant, that she was another slight of Mr Dombey’s on his wife.\n\n“—may be informed that Mr Dombey wishes to have his bed prepared in his own apartments downstairs, as he prefers those rooms to any other. I shall return to Mr Dombey almost immediately. That every possible attention has been paid to his comfort, and that he is the object of every possible solicitude, I need not assure you, Madam. Let me again say, there is no cause for the least alarm. Even you may be quite at ease, believe me.”\n\nHe bowed himself out, with his extremest show of deference and conciliation; and having returned to Mr Dombey’s room, and there arranged for a carriage being sent after him to the City, mounted his horse again, and rode slowly thither. He was very thoughtful as he went along, and very thoughtful there, and very thoughtful in the carriage on his way back to the place where Mr Dombey had been left. It was only when sitting by that gentleman’s couch that he was quite himself again, and conscious of his teeth.\n\nAbout the time of twilight, Mr Dombey, grievously afflicted with aches and pains, was helped into his carriage, and propped with cloaks and pillows on one side of it, while his confidential agent bore him company upon the other. As he was not to be shaken, they moved at little more than a foot pace; and hence it was quite dark when he was brought home. Mrs Pipchin, bitter and grim, and not oblivious of the Peruvian mines, as the establishment in general had good reason to know, received him at the door, and freshened the domestics with several little sprinklings of wordy vinegar, while they assisted in conveying him to his room. Mr Carker remained in attendance until he was safe in bed, and then, as he declined to receive any female visitor, but the excellent Ogress who presided over his household, waited on Mrs Dombey once more, with his report on her lord’s condition.\n\nHe again found Edith alone with Florence, and he again addressed the whole of his soothing speech to Edith, as if she were a prey to the liveliest and most affectionate anxieties. So earnest he was in his respectful sympathy, that on taking leave, he ventured—with one more glance towards Florence at the moment—to take her hand, and bending over it, to touch it with his lips.\n\nEdith did not withdraw the hand, nor did she strike his fair face with it, despite the flush upon her cheek, the bright light in her eyes, and the dilation of her whole form. But when she was alone in her own room, she struck it on the marble chimney-shelf, so that, at one blow, it was bruised, and bled; and held it from her, near the shining fire, as if she could have thrust it in and burned it.\n\nFar into the night she sat alone, by the sinking blaze, in dark and threatening beauty, watching the murky shadows looming on the wall, as if her thoughts were tangible, and cast them there. Whatever shapes of outrage and affront, and black foreshadowings of things that might happen, flickered, indistinct and giant-like, before her, one resented figure marshalled them against her. And that figure was her husband.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIII. The Watches of the Night\n\nFlorence, long since awakened from her dream, mournfully observed the estrangement between her father and Edith, and saw it widen more and more, and knew that there was greater bitterness between them every day. Each day’s added knowledge deepened the shade upon her love and hope, roused up the old sorrow that had slumbered for a little time, and made it even heavier to bear than it had been before.\n\nIt had been hard—how hard may none but Florence ever know!—to have the natural affection of a true and earnest nature turned to agony; and slight, or stern repulse, substituted for the tenderest protection and the dearest care. It had been hard to feel in her deep heart what she had felt, and never know the happiness of one touch of response. But it was much more hard to be compelled to doubt either her father or Edith, so affectionate and dear to her, and to think of her love for each of them, by turns, with fear, distrust, and wonder.\n\nYet Florence now began to do so; and the doing of it was a task imposed upon her by the very purity of her soul, as one she could not fly from. She saw her father cold and obdurate to Edith, as to her; hard, inflexible, unyielding. Could it be, she asked herself with starting tears, that her own dear mother had been made unhappy by such treatment, and had pined away and died? Then she would think how proud and stately Edith was to everyone but her, with what disdain she treated him, how distantly she kept apart from him, and what she had said on the night when they came home; and quickly it would come on Florence, almost as a crime, that she loved one who was set in opposition to her father, and that her father knowing of it, must think of her in his solitary room as the unnatural child who added this wrong to the old fault, so much wept for, of never having won his fatherly affection from her birth. The next kind word from Edith, the next kind glance, would shake these thoughts again, and make them seem like black ingratitude; for who but she had cheered the drooping heart of Florence, so lonely and so hurt, and been its best of comforters! Thus, with her gentle nature yearning to them both, feeling for the misery of both, and whispering doubts of her own duty to both, Florence in her wider and expanded love, and by the side of Edith, endured more than when she had hoarded up her undivided secret in the mournful house, and her beautiful Mama had never dawned upon it.\n\nOne exquisite unhappiness that would have far outweighed this, Florence was spared. She never had the least suspicion that Edith by her tenderness for her widened the separation from her father, or gave him new cause of dislike. If Florence had conceived the possibility of such an effect being wrought by such a cause, what grief she would have felt, what sacrifice she would have tried to make, poor loving girl, how fast and sure her quiet passage might have been beneath it to the presence of that higher Father who does not reject his children’s love, or spurn their tried and broken hearts, Heaven knows! But it was otherwise, and that was well.\n\nNo word was ever spoken between Florence and Edith now, on these subjects. Edith had said there ought to be between them, in that wise, a division and a silence like the grave itself: and Florence felt she was right.\n\nIn this state of affairs her father was brought home, suffering and disabled; and gloomily retired to his own rooms, where he was tended by servants, not approached by Edith, and had no friend or companion but Mr Carker, who withdrew near midnight.\n\n“And nice company he is, Miss Floy,” said Susan Nipper. “Oh, he’s a precious piece of goods! If ever he wants a character don’t let him come to me whatever he does, that’s all I tell him.”\n\n“Dear Susan,” urged Florence, “don’t!”\n\n“Oh, it’s very well to say ‘don’t’ Miss Floy,” returned the Nipper, much exasperated; “but raly begging your pardon we’re coming to such passes that it turns all the blood in a person’s body into pins and needles, with their pints all ways. Don’t mistake me, Miss Floy, I don’t mean nothing again your ma-in-law who has always treated me as a lady should though she is rather high I must say not that I have any right to object to that particular, but when we come to Mrs Pipchinses and having them put over us and keeping guard at your Pa’s door like crocodiles (only make us thankful that they lay no eggs!) we are a growing too outrageous!”\n\n“Papa thinks well of Mrs Pipchin, Susan,” returned Florence, “and has a right to choose his housekeeper, you know. Pray don’t!”\n\n“Well Miss Floy,” returned the Nipper, “when you say don’t, I never do I hope but Mrs Pipchin acts like early gooseberries upon me Miss, and nothing less.”\n\nSusan was unusually emphatic and destitute of punctuation in her discourse on this night, which was the night of Mr Dombey’s being brought home, because, having been sent downstairs by Florence to inquire after him, she had been obliged to deliver her message to her mortal enemy Mrs Pipchin; who, without carrying it in to Mr Dombey, had taken upon herself to return what Miss Nipper called a huffish answer, on her own responsibility. This, Susan Nipper construed into presumption on the part of that exemplary sufferer by the Peruvian mines, and a deed of disparagement upon her young lady, that was not to be forgiven; and so far her emphatic state was special. But she had been in a condition of greatly increased suspicion and distrust, ever since the marriage; for, like most persons of her quality of mind, who form a strong and sincere attachment to one in the different station which Florence occupied, Susan was very jealous, and her jealousy naturally attached to Edith, who divided her old empire, and came between them. Proud and glad as Susan Nipper truly was, that her young mistress should be advanced towards her proper place in the scene of her old neglect, and that she should have her father’s handsome wife for her companion and protectress, she could not relinquish any part of her own dominion to the handsome wife, without a grudge and a vague feeling of ill-will, for which she did not fail to find a disinterested justification in her sharp perception of the pride and passion of the lady’s character. From the background to which she had necessarily retired somewhat, since the marriage, Miss Nipper looked on, therefore, at domestic affairs in general, with a resolute conviction that no good would come of Mrs Dombey: always being very careful to publish on all possible occasions, that she had nothing to say against her.\n\n“Susan,” said Florence, who was sitting thoughtfully at her table, “it is very late. I shall want nothing more tonight.”\n\n“Ah, Miss Floy!” returned the Nipper, “I’m sure I often wish for them old times when I sat up with you hours later than this and fell asleep through being tired out when you was as broad awake as spectacles, but you’ve ma’s-in-law to come and sit with you now Miss Floy and I’m thankful for it I’m sure. I’ve not a word to say against ’em.”\n\n“I shall not forget who was my old companion when I had none, Susan,” returned Florence, gently, “never!” And looking up, she put her arm round the neck of her humble friend, drew her face down to hers, and bidding her good-night, kissed it; which so mollified Miss Nipper, that she fell a sobbing.\n\n“Now my dear Miss Floy,” said Susan, “let me go downstairs again and see how your Pa is, I know you’re wretched about him, do let me go downstairs again and knock at his door my own self.”\n\n“No,” said Florence, “go to bed. We shall hear more in the morning. I will inquire myself in the morning. Mama has been down, I daresay;” Florence blushed, for she had no such hope; “or is there now, perhaps. Good-night!”\n\nSusan was too much softened to express her private opinion on the probability of Mrs Dombey’s being in attendance on her husband, and silently withdrew. Florence left alone, soon hid her head upon her hands as she had often done in other days, and did not restrain the tears from coursing down her face. The misery of this domestic discord and unhappiness; the withered hope she cherished now, if hope it could be called, of ever being taken to her father’s heart; her doubts and fears between the two; the yearning of her innocent breast to both; the heavy disappointment and regret of such an end as this, to what had been a vision of bright hope and promise to her; all crowded on her mind and made her tears flow fast. Her mother and her brother dead, her father unmoved towards her, Edith opposed to him and casting him away, but loving her, and loved by her, it seemed as if her affection could never prosper, rest where it would. That weak thought was soon hushed, but the thoughts in which it had arisen were too true and strong to be dismissed with it; and they made the night desolate.\n\nAmong such reflections there rose up, as there had risen up all day, the image of her father, wounded and in pain, alone in his own room, untended by those who should be nearest to him, and passing the tardy hours in lonely suffering. A frightened thought which made her start and clasp her hands—though it was not a new one in her mind—that he might die, and never see her or pronounce her name, thrilled her whole frame. In her agitation she thought, and trembled while she thought, of once more stealing downstairs, and venturing to his door.\n\nShe listened at her own. The house was quiet, and all the lights were out. It was a long, long time, she thought, since she used to make her nightly pilgrimages to his door! It was a long, long time, she tried to think, since she had entered his room at midnight, and he had led her back to the stair-foot!\n\nWith the same child’s heart within her, as of old: even with the child’s sweet timid eyes and clustering hair: Florence, as strange to her father in her early maiden bloom, as in her nursery time, crept down the staircase listening as she went, and drew near to his room. No one was stirring in the house. The door was partly open to admit air; and all was so still within, that she could hear the burning of the fire, and count the ticking of the clock that stood upon the chimney-piece.\n\nShe looked in. In that room, the housekeeper wrapped in a blanket was fast asleep in an easy chair before the fire. The doors between it and the next were partly closed, and a screen was drawn before them; but there was a light there, and it shone upon the cornice of his bed. All was so very still that she could hear from his breathing that he was asleep. This gave her courage to pass round the screen, and look into his chamber.\n\nIt was as great a start to come upon his sleeping face as if she had not expected to see it. Florence stood arrested on the spot, and if he had awakened then, must have remained there.\n\nThere was a cut upon his forehead, and they had been wetting his hair, which lay bedabbled and entangled on the pillow. One of his arms, resting outside the bed, was bandaged up, and he was very white. But it was not this, that after the first quick glance, and first assurance of his sleeping quietly, held Florence rooted to the ground. It was something very different from this, and more than this, that made him look so solemn in her eye.\n\nShe had never seen his face in all her life, but there had been upon it—or she fancied so—some disturbing consciousness of her. She had never seen his face in all her life, but hope had sunk within her, and her timid glance had dropped before its stern, unloving, and repelling harshness. As she looked upon it now, she saw it, for the first time, free from the cloud that had darkened her childhood. Calm, tranquil night was reigning in its stead. He might have gone to sleep, for anything she saw there, blessing her.\n\nAwake, unkind father! Awake, now, sullen man! The time is flitting by; the hour is coming with an angry tread. Awake!\n\nThere was no change upon his face; and as she watched it, awfully, its motionless response recalled the faces that were gone. So they looked, so would he; so she, his weeping child, who should say when! so all the world of love and hatred and indifference around them! When that time should come, it would not be the heavier to him, for this that she was going to do; and it might fall something lighter upon her.\n\nShe stole close to the bed, and drawing in her breath, bent down, and softly kissed him on the face, and laid her own for one brief moment by its side, and put the arm, with which she dared not touch him, round about him on the pillow.\n\nAwake, doomed man, while she is near! The time is flitting by; the hour is coming with an angry tread; its foot is in the house. Awake!\n\nIn her mind, she prayed to God to bless her father, and to soften him towards her, if it might be so; and if not, to forgive him if he was wrong, and pardon her the prayer which almost seemed impiety. And doing so, and looking back at him with blinded eyes, and stealing timidly away, passed out of his room, and crossed the other, and was gone.\n\nHe may sleep on now. He may sleep on while he may. But let him look for that slight figure when he wakes, and find it near him when the hour is come!\n\nSad and grieving was the heart of Florence, as she crept upstairs. The quiet house had grown more dismal since she came down. The sleep she had been looking on, in the dead of night, had the solemnity to her of death and life in one. The secrecy and silence of her own proceeding made the night secret, silent, and oppressive. She felt unwilling, almost unable, to go on to her own chamber; and turning into the drawing-rooms, where the clouded moon was shining through the blinds, looked out into the empty streets.\n\nThe wind was blowing drearily. The lamps looked pale, and shook as if they were cold. There was a distant glimmer of something that was not quite darkness, rather than of light, in the sky; and foreboding night was shivering and restless, as the dying are who make a troubled end. Florence remembered how, as a watcher, by a sick-bed, she had noted this bleak time, and felt its influence, as if in some hidden natural antipathy to it; and now it was very, very gloomy.\n\nHer Mama had not come to her room that night, which was one cause of her having sat late out of her bed. In her general uneasiness, no less than in her ardent longing to have somebody to speak to, and to break the spell of gloom and silence, Florence directed her steps towards the chamber where she slept.\n\nThe door was not fastened within, and yielded smoothly to her hesitating hand. She was surprised to find a bright light burning; still more surprised, on looking in, to see that her Mama, but partially undressed, was sitting near the ashes of the fire, which had crumbled and dropped away. Her eyes were intently bent upon the air; and in their light, and in her face, and in her form, and in the grasp with which she held the elbows of her chair as if about to start up, Florence saw such fierce emotion that it terrified her.\n\n“Mama!” she cried, “what is the matter?”\n\nEdith started; looking at her with such a strange dread in her face, that Florence was more frightened than before.\n\n“Mama!” said Florence, hurriedly advancing. “Dear Mama! what is the matter?”\n\n“I have not been well,” said Edith, shaking, and still looking at her in the same strange way. “I have had bad dreams, my love.”\n\n“And not yet been to bed, Mama?”\n\n“No,” she returned. “Half-waking dreams.”\n\nHer features gradually softened; and suffering Florence to come closer to her, within her embrace, she said in a tender manner, “But what does my bird do here? What does my bird do here?”\n\n“I have been uneasy, Mama, in not seeing you tonight, and in not knowing how Papa was; and I—”\n\nFlorence stopped there, and said no more.\n\n“Is it late?” asked Edith, fondly putting back the curls that mingled with her own dark hair, and strayed upon her face.\n\n“Very late. Near day.”\n\n“Near day!” she repeated in surprise.\n\n“Dear Mama, what have you done to your hand?” said Florence.\n\nEdith drew it suddenly away, and, for a moment, looked at her with the same strange dread (there was a sort of wild avoidance in it) as before; but she presently said, “Nothing, nothing. A blow.” And then she said, “My Florence!” and then her bosom heaved, and she was weeping passionately.\n\n“Mama!” said Florence. “Oh Mama, what can I do, what should I do, to make us happier? Is there anything?”\n\n“Nothing,” she replied.\n\n“Are you sure of that? Can it never be? If I speak now of what is in my thoughts, in spite of what we have agreed,” said Florence, “you will not blame me, will you?”\n\n“It is useless,” she replied, “useless. I have told you, dear, that I have had bad dreams. Nothing can change them, or prevent them coming back.”\n\n“I do not understand,” said Florence, gazing on her agitated face which seemed to darken as she looked.\n\n“I have dreamed,” said Edith in a low voice, “of a pride that is all powerless for good, all powerful for evil; of a pride that has been galled and goaded, through many shameful years, and has never recoiled except upon itself; a pride that has debased its owner with the consciousness of deep humiliation, and never helped its owner boldly to resent it or avoid it, or to say, ‘This shall not be!’ a pride that, rightly guided, might have led perhaps to better things, but which, misdirected and perverted, like all else belonging to the same possessor, has been self-contempt, mere hardihood and ruin.”\n\nShe neither looked nor spoke to Florence now, but went on as if she were alone.\n\n“I have dreamed,” she said, “of such indifference and callousness, arising from this self-contempt; this wretched, inefficient, miserable pride; that it has gone on with listless steps even to the altar, yielding to the old, familiar, beckoning finger,—oh mother, oh mother!—while it spurned it; and willing to be hateful to itself for once and for all, rather than to be stung daily in some new form. Mean, poor thing!”\n\nAnd now with gathering and darkening emotion, she looked as she had looked when Florence entered.\n\n“And I have dreamed,” she said, “that in a first late effort to achieve a purpose, it has been trodden on, and trodden down by a base foot, but turns and looks upon him. I have dreamed that it is wounded, hunted, set upon by dogs, but that it stands at bay, and will not yield; no, that it cannot if it would; but that it is urged on to hate.”\n\nHer clenched hand tightened on the trembling arm she had in hers, and as she looked down on the alarmed and wondering face, frown subsided. “Oh Florence!” she said, “I think I have been nearly mad tonight!” and humbled her proud head upon her neck and wept again.\n\n“Don’t leave me! be near me! I have no hope but in you!” These words she said a score of times.\n\nSoon she grew calmer, and was full of pity for the tears of Florence, and for her waking at such untimely hours. And the day now dawning, Edith folded her in her arms and laid her down upon her bed, and, not lying down herself, sat by her, and bade her try to sleep.\n\n“For you are weary, dearest, and unhappy, and should rest.”\n\n“I am indeed unhappy, dear Mama, tonight,” said Florence. “But you are weary and unhappy, too.”\n\n“Not when you lie asleep so near me, sweet.”\n\nThey kissed each other, and Florence, worn out, gradually fell into a gentle slumber; but as her eyes closed on the face beside her, it was so sad to think upon the face downstairs, that her hand drew closer to Edith for some comfort; yet, even in the act, it faltered, lest it should be deserting him. So, in her sleep, she tried to reconcile the two together, and to show them that she loved them both, but could not do it, and her waking grief was part of her dreams.\n\nEdith, sitting by, looked down at the dark eyelashes lying wet on the flushed cheeks, and looked with gentleness and pity, for she knew the truth. But no sleep hung upon her own eyes. As the day came on she still sat watching and waking, with the placid hand in hers, and sometimes whispered, as she looked at the hushed face, “Be near me, Florence. I have no hope but in you!”\n\n# CHAPTER XLIV. A Separation\n\nWith the day, though not so early as the sun, uprose Miss Susan Nipper. There was a heaviness in this young maiden’s exceedingly sharp black eyes, that abated somewhat of their sparkling, and suggested—which was not their usual character—the possibility of their being sometimes shut. There was likewise a swollen look about them, as if they had been crying over-night. But the Nipper, so far from being cast down, was singularly brisk and bold, and all her energies appeared to be braced up for some great feat. This was noticeable even in her dress, which was much more tight and trim than usual; and in occasional twitches of her head as she went about the house, which were mightily expressive of determination.\n\nIn a word, she had formed a determination, and an aspiring one: it being nothing less than this—to penetrate to Mr Dombey’s presence, and have speech of that gentleman alone. “I have often said I would,” she remarked, in a threatening manner, to herself, that morning, with many twitches of her head, “and now I will!”\n\nSpurring herself on to the accomplishment of this desperate design, with a sharpness that was peculiar to herself, Susan Nipper haunted the hall and staircase during the whole forenoon, without finding a favourable opportunity for the assault. Not at all baffled by this discomfiture, which indeed had a stimulating effect, and put her on her mettle, she diminished nothing of her vigilance; and at last discovered, towards evening, that her sworn foe Mrs Pipchin, under pretence of having sat up all night, was dozing in her own room, and that Mr Dombey was lying on his sofa, unattended.\n\nWith a twitch—not of her head merely, this time, but of her whole self—the Nipper went on tiptoe to Mr Dombey’s door, and knocked. “Come in!” said Mr Dombey. Susan encouraged herself with a final twitch, and went in.\n\nMr Dombey, who was eyeing the fire, gave an amazed look at his visitor, and raised himself a little on his arm. The Nipper dropped a curtsey.\n\n“What do you want?” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“If you please, Sir, I wish to speak to you,” said Susan.\n\nMr Dombey moved his lips as if he were repeating the words, but he seemed so lost in astonishment at the presumption of the young woman as to be incapable of giving them utterance.\n\n“I have been in your service, Sir,” said Susan Nipper, with her usual rapidity, “now twelve “year a waiting on Miss Floy my own young lady who couldn’t speak plain when I first come here and I was old in this house when Mrs Richards was new, I may not be Meethosalem, but I am not a child in arms.”\n\nMr Dombey, raised upon his arm and looking at her, offered no comment on this preparatory statement of fact.\n\n“There never was a dearer or a blesseder young lady than is my young lady, Sir,” said Susan, “and I ought to know a great deal better than some for I have seen her in her grief and I have seen her in her joy (there’s not been much of it) and I have seen her with her brother and I have seen her in her loneliness and some have never seen her, and I say to some and all—I do!” and here the black-eyed shook her head, and slightly stamped her foot; “that she’s the blessedest and dearest angel is Miss Floy that ever drew the breath of life, the more that I was torn to pieces Sir the more I’d say it though I may not be a Fox’s Martyr.”\n\nMr Dombey turned yet paler than his fall had made him, with indignation and astonishment; and kept his eyes upon the speaker as if he accused them, and his ears too, of playing him false.\n\n“No one could be anything but true and faithful to Miss Floy, Sir,” pursued Susan, “and I take no merit for my service of twelve year, for I love her—yes, I say to some and all I do!”—and here the black-eyed shook her head again, and slightly stamped her foot again, and checked a sob; “but true and faithful service gives me right to speak I hope, and speak I must and will now, right or wrong.”\n\n“What do you mean, woman?” said Mr Dombey, glaring at her. “How do you dare?”\n\n“What I mean, Sir, is to speak respectful and without offence, but out, and how I dare I know not but I do!” said Susan. “Oh! you don’t know my young lady Sir you don’t indeed, you’d never know so little of her, if you did.”\n\nMr Dombey, in a fury, put his hand out for the bell-rope; but there was no bell-rope on that side of the fire, and he could not rise and cross to the other without assistance. The quick eye of the Nipper detected his helplessness immediately, and now, as she afterwards observed, she felt she had got him.\n\n“Miss Floy,” said Susan Nipper, “is the most devoted and most patient and most dutiful and beautiful of daughters, there ain’t no gentleman, no Sir, though as great and rich as all the greatest and richest of England put together, but might be proud of her and would and ought. If he knew her value right, he’d rather lose his greatness and his fortune piece by piece and beg his way in rags from door to door, I say to some and all, he would!” cried Susan Nipper, bursting into tears, “than bring the sorrow on her tender heart that I have seen it suffer in this house!”\n\n“Woman,” cried Mr Dombey, “leave the room.”\n\n“Begging your pardon, not even if I am to leave the situation, Sir,” replied the steadfast Nipper, “in which I have been so many years and seen so much—although I hope you’d never have the heart to send me from Miss Floy for such a cause—will I go now till I have said the rest, I may not be a Indian widow Sir and I am not and I would not so become but if I once made up my mind to burn myself alive, I’d do it! And I’ve made my mind up to go on.”\n\nWhich was rendered no less clear by the expression of Susan Nipper’s countenance, than by her words.\n\n“There ain’t a person in your service, Sir,” pursued the black-eyed, “that has always stood more in awe of you than me and you may think how true it is when I make so bold as say that I have hundreds and hundreds of times thought of speaking to you and never been able to make my mind up to it till last night, but last night decided of me.”\n\nMr Dombey, in a paroxysm of rage, made another grasp at the bell-rope that was not there, and, in its absence, pulled his hair rather than nothing.\n\n“I have seen,” said Susan Nipper, “Miss Floy strive and strive when nothing but a child so sweet and patient that the best of women might have copied from her, I’ve seen her sitting nights together half the night through to help her delicate brother with his learning, I’ve seen her helping him and watching him at other times—some well know when—I’ve seen her, with no encouragement and no help, grow up to be a lady, thank God! that is the grace and pride of every company she goes in, and I’ve always seen her cruelly neglected and keenly feeling of it—I say to some and all, I have!—and never said one word, but ordering one’s self lowly and reverently towards one’s betters, is not to be a worshipper of graven images, and I will and must speak!”\n\n“Is there anybody there?” cried Mr Dombey, calling out. “Where are the men? where are the women? Is there no one there?”\n\n“I left my dear young lady out of bed late last night,” said Susan, nothing checked, “and I knew why, for you was ill Sir and she didn’t know how ill and that was enough to make her wretched as I saw it did. I may not be a Peacock; but I have my eyes—and I sat up a little in my own room thinking she might be lonesome and might want me, and I saw her steal downstairs and come to this door as if it was a guilty thing to look at her own Pa, and then steal back again and go into them lonely drawing-rooms, a-crying so, that I could hardly bear to hear it. I can not bear to hear it,” said Susan Nipper, wiping her black eyes, and fixing them undauntingly on Mr Dombey’s infuriated face. “It’s not the first time I have heard it, not by many and many a time you don’t know your own daughter, Sir, you don’t know what you’re doing, Sir, I say to some and all,” cried Susan Nipper, in a final burst, “that it’s a sinful shame!”\n\n“Why, hoity toity!” cried the voice of Mrs Pipchin, as the black bombazeen garments of that fair Peruvian Miner swept into the room. “What’s this, indeed?”\n\nSusan favoured Mrs Pipchin with a look she had invented expressly for her when they first became acquainted, and resigned the reply to Mr Dombey.\n\n“What’s this?” repeated Mr Dombey, almost foaming. “What’s this, Madam? You who are at the head of this household, and bound to keep it in order, have reason to inquire. Do you know this woman?”\n\n“I know very little good of her, Sir,” croaked Mrs Pipchin. “How dare you come here, you hussy? Go along with you!”\n\nBut the inflexible Nipper, merely honouring Mrs Pipchin with another look, remained.\n\n“Do you call it managing this establishment, Madam,” said Mr Dombey, “to leave a person like this at liberty to come and talk to me! A gentleman—in his own house—in his own room—assailed with the impertinences of women-servants!”\n\n“Well, Sir,” returned Mrs Pipchin, with vengeance in her hard grey eye, “I exceedingly deplore it; nothing can be more irregular; nothing can be more out of all bounds and reason; but I regret to say, Sir, that this young woman is quite beyond control. She has been spoiled by Miss Dombey, and is amenable to nobody. You know you’re not,” said Mrs Pipchin, sharply, and shaking her head at Susan Nipper. “For shame, you hussy! Go along with you!”\n\n“If you find people in my service who are not to be controlled, Mrs Pipchin,” said Mr Dombey, turning back towards the fire, “you know what to do with them, I presume. You know what you are here for? Take her away!”\n\n“Sir, I know what to do,” retorted Mrs Pipchin, “and of course shall do it. Susan Nipper,” snapping her up particularly short, “a month’s warning from this hour.”\n\n“Oh indeed!” cried Susan, loftily.\n\n“Yes,” returned Mrs Pipchin, “and don’t smile at me, you minx, or I’ll know the reason why! Go along with you this minute!”\n\n“I intend to go this minute, you may rely upon it,” said the voluble Nipper. “I have been in this house waiting on my young lady a dozen year and I won’t stop in it one hour under notice from a person owning to the name of Pipchin trust me, Mrs P.”\n\n“A good riddance of bad rubbish!” said that wrathful old lady. “Get along with you, or I’ll have you carried out!”\n\n“My comfort is,” said Susan, looking back at Mr Dombey, “that I have told a piece of truth this day which ought to have been told long before and can’t be told too often or too plain and that no amount of Pipchinses—I hope the number of ’em mayn’t be great” (here Mrs Pipchin uttered a very sharp “Go along with you!” and Miss Nipper repeated the look) “can unsay what I have said, though they gave a whole year full of warnings beginning at ten o’clock in the forenoon and never leaving off till twelve at night and died of the exhaustion which would be a Jubilee!”\n\nWith these words, Miss Nipper preceded her foe out of the room; and walking upstairs to her own apartments in great state, to the choking exasperation of the ireful Pipchin, sat down among her boxes and began to cry.\n\nFrom this soft mood she was soon aroused, with a very wholesome and refreshing effect, by the voice of Mrs Pipchin outside the door.\n\n“Does that bold-faced slut,” said the fell Pipchin, “intend to take her warning, or does she not?”\n\nMiss Nipper replied from within that the person described did not inhabit that part of the house, but that her name was Pipchin, and she was to be found in the housekeeper’s room.\n\n“You saucy baggage!” retorted Mrs Pipchin, rattling at the handle of the door. “Go along with you this minute. Pack up your things directly! How dare you talk in this way to a gentle-woman who has seen better days?”\n\nTo which Miss Nipper rejoined from her castle, that she pitied the better days that had seen Mrs Pipchin; and that for her part she considered the worst days in the year to be about that lady’s mark, except that they were much too good for her.\n\n“But you needn’t trouble yourself to make a noise at my door,” said Susan Nipper, “nor to contaminate the key-hole with your eye, I’m packing up and going you may take your affidavit.”\n\nThe Dowager expressed her lively satisfaction at this intelligence, and with some general opinions upon young hussies as a race, and especially upon their demerits after being spoiled by Miss Dombey, withdrew to prepare the Nipper’s wages. Susan then bestirred herself to get her trunks in order, that she might take an immediate and dignified departure; sobbing heartily all the time, as she thought of Florence.\n\nThe object of her regret was not long in coming to her, for the news soon spread over the house that Susan Nipper had had a disturbance with Mrs Pipchin, and that they had both appealed to Mr Dombey, and that there had been an unprecedented piece of work in Mr Dombey’s room, and that Susan was going. The latter part of this confused rumour, Florence found to be so correct, that Susan had locked the last trunk and was sitting upon it with her bonnet on, when she came into her room.\n\n“Susan!” cried Florence. “Going to leave me! You!”\n\n“Oh for goodness gracious sake, Miss Floy,” said Susan, sobbing, “don’t speak a word to me or I shall demean myself before them Pi-i-pchinses, and I wouldn’t have ’em see me cry Miss Floy for worlds!”\n\n“Susan!” said Florence. “My dear girl, my old friend! What shall I do without you! Can you bear to go away so?”\n\n“No-n-o-o, my darling dear Miss Floy, I can’t indeed,” sobbed Susan. “But it can’t be helped, I’ve done my duty, Miss, I have indeed. It’s no fault of mine. I am quite resigned. I couldn’t stay my month or I could never leave you then my darling and I must at last as well as at first, don’t speak to me Miss Floy, for though I’m pretty firm I’m not a marble doorpost, my own dear.”\n\n“What is it? Why is it?” said Florence, “Won’t you tell me?” For Susan was shaking her head.\n\n“No-n-no, my darling,” returned Susan. “Don’t ask me, for I mustn’t, and whatever you do don’t put in a word for me to stop, for it couldn’t be and you’d only wrong yourself, and so God bless you my own precious and forgive me any harm I have done, or any temper I have showed in all these many years!”\n\nWith which entreaty, very heartily delivered, Susan hugged her mistress in her arms.\n\n“My darling there’s a many that may come to serve you and be glad to serve you and who’ll serve you well and true,” said Susan, “but there can’t be one who’ll serve you so affectionate as me or love you half as dearly, that’s my comfort. Go-ood-bye, sweet Miss Floy!”\n\n“Where will you go, Susan?” asked her weeping mistress.\n\n“I’ve got a brother down in the country Miss—a farmer in Essex,” said the heart-broken Nipper, “that keeps ever so many co-o-ows and pigs and I shall go down there by the coach and sto-op with him, and don’t mind me, for I’ve got money in the Savings Banks my dear, and needn’t take another service just yet, which I couldn’t, couldn’t, couldn’t do, my heart’s own mistress!” Susan finished with a burst of sorrow, which was opportunely broken by the voice of Mrs Pipchin talking downstairs; on hearing which, she dried her red and swollen eyes, and made a melancholy feint of calling jauntily to Mr Towlinson to fetch a cab and carry down her boxes.\n\nFlorence, pale and hurried and distressed, but withheld from useless interference even here, by her dread of causing any new division between her father and his wife (whose stern, indignant face had been a warning to her a few moments since), and by her apprehension of being in some way unconsciously connected already with the dismissal of her old servant and friend, followed, weeping, downstairs to Edith’s dressing-room, whither Susan betook herself to make her parting curtsey.\n\n“Now, here’s the cab, and here’s the boxes, get along with you, do!” said Mrs Pipchin, presenting herself at the same moment. “I beg your pardon, Ma’am, but Mr Dombey’s orders are imperative.”\n\nEdith, sitting under the hands of her maid—she was going out to dinner—preserved her haughty face, and took not the least notice.\n\n“There’s your money,” said Mrs Pipchin, who, in pursuance of her system, and in recollection of the Mines, was accustomed to rout the servants about, as she had routed her young Brighton boarders; to the everlasting acidulation of Master Bitherstone, “and the sooner this house sees your back the better.”\n\nSusan had no spirits even for the look that belonged to Ma Pipchin by right; so she dropped her curtsey to Mrs Dombey (who inclined her head without one word, and whose eye avoided everyone but Florence), and gave one last parting hug to her young mistress, and received her parting embrace in return. Poor Susan’s face at this crisis, in the intensity of her feelings and the determined suffocation of her sobs, lest one should become audible and be a triumph to Mrs Pipchin, presented a series of the most extraordinary physiognomical phenomena ever witnessed.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Miss, I’m sure,” said Towlinson, outside the door with the boxes, addressing Florence, “but Mr Toots is in the drawing-room, and sends his compliments, and begs to know how Diogenes and Master is.”\n\nQuick as thought, Florence glided out and hastened downstairs, where Mr Toots, in the most splendid vestments, was breathing very hard with doubt and agitation on the subject of her coming.\n\n“Oh, how de do, Miss Dombey,” said Mr Toots, “God bless my soul!”\n\nThis last ejaculation was occasioned by Mr Toots’s deep concern at the distress he saw in Florence’s face; which caused him to stop short in a fit of chuckles, and become an image of despair.\n\n“Dear Mr Toots,” said Florence, “you are so friendly to me, and so honest, that I am sure I may ask a favour of you.”\n\n“Miss Dombey,” returned Mr Toots, “if you’ll only name one, you’ll—you’ll give me an appetite. To which,” said Mr Toots, with some sentiment, “I have long been a stranger.”\n\n“Susan, who is an old friend of mine, the oldest friend I have,” said Florence, “is about to leave here suddenly, and quite alone, poor girl. She is going home, a little way into the country. Might I ask you to take care of her until she is in the coach?”\n\n“Miss Dombey,” returned Mr Toots, “you really do me an honour and a kindness. This proof of your confidence, after the manner in which I was Beast enough to conduct myself at Brighton—”\n\n“Yes,” said Florence, hurriedly—“no—don’t think of that. Then would you have the kindness to—to go? and to be ready to meet her when she comes out? Thank you a thousand times! You ease my mind so much. She doesn’t seem so desolate. You cannot think how grateful I feel to you, or what a good friend I am sure you are!” and Florence in her earnestness thanked him again and again; and Mr Toots, in his earnestness, hurried away—but backwards, that he might lose no glimpse of her.\n\nFlorence had not the courage to go out, when she saw poor Susan in the hall, with Mrs Pipchin driving her forth, and Diogenes jumping about her, and terrifying Mrs Pipchin to the last degree by making snaps at her bombazeen skirts, and howling with anguish at the sound of her voice—for the good duenna was the dearest and most cherished aversion of his breast. But she saw Susan shake hands with the servants all round, and turn once to look at her old home; and she saw Diogenes bound out after the cab, and want to follow it, and testify an impossibility of conviction that he had no longer any property in the fare; and the door was shut, and the hurry over, and her tears flowed fast for the loss of an old friend, whom no one could replace. No one. No one.\n\nMr Toots, like the leal and trusty soul he was, stopped the cabriolet in a twinkling, and told Susan Nipper of his commission, at which she cried more than before.\n\n“Upon my soul and body!” said Mr Toots, taking his seat beside her. “I feel for you. Upon my word and honour I think you can hardly know your own feelings better than I imagine them. I can conceive nothing more dreadful than to have to leave Miss Dombey.”\n\nSusan abandoned herself to her grief now, and it really was touching to see her.\n\n“I say,” said Mr Toots, “now, don’t! at least I mean now do, you know!”\n\n“Do what, Mr Toots!” cried Susan.\n\n“Why, come home to my place, and have some dinner before you start,” said Mr Toots. “My cook’s a most respectable woman—one of the most motherly people I ever saw—and she’ll be delighted to make you comfortable. Her son,” said Mr Toots, as an additional recommendation, “was educated in the Bluecoat School, and blown up in a powder-mill.”\n\nSusan accepting this kind offer, Mr Toots conducted her to his dwelling, where they were received by the Matron in question who fully justified his character of her, and by the Chicken who at first supposed, on seeing a lady in the vehicle, that Mr Dombey had been doubled up, agreeably to his old recommendation, and Miss Dombey abducted. This gentleman awakened in Miss Nipper some considerable astonishment; for, having been defeated by the Larkey Boy, his visage was in a state of such great dilapidation, as to be hardly presentable in society with comfort to the beholders. The Chicken himself attributed this punishment to his having had the misfortune to get into Chancery early in the proceedings, when he was severely fibbed by the Larkey one, and heavily grassed. But it appeared from the published records of that great contest that the Larkey Boy had had it all his own way from the beginning, and that the Chicken had been tapped, and bunged, and had received pepper, and had been made groggy, and had come up piping, and had endured a complication of similar strange inconveniences, until he had been gone into and finished.\n\nAfter a good repast, and much hospitality, Susan set out for the coach-office in another cabriolet, with Mr Toots inside, as before, and the Chicken on the box, who, whatever distinction he conferred on the little party by the moral weight and heroism of his character, was scarcely ornamental to it, physically speaking, on account of his plasters; which were numerous. But the Chicken had registered a vow, in secret, that he would never leave Mr Toots (who was secretly pining to get rid of him), for any less consideration than the good-will and fixtures of a public-house; and being ambitious to go into that line, and drink himself to death as soon as possible, he felt it his cue to make his company unacceptable.\n\nThe night-coach by which Susan was to go, was on the point of departure. Mr Toots having put her inside, lingered by the window, irresolutely, until the driver was about to mount; when, standing on the step, and putting in a face that by the light of the lamp was anxious and confused, he said abruptly:\n\n“I say, Susan! Miss Dombey, you know—”\n\n“Yes, Sir.”\n\n“Do you think she could—you know—eh?”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Mr Toots,” said Susan, “but I don’t hear you.”\n\n“Do you think she could be brought, you know—not exactly at once, but in time—in a long time—to—to love me, you know? There!” said poor Mr Toots.\n\n“Oh dear no!” returned Susan, shaking her head. “I should say, never. Never!”\n\n“Thank’ee!” said Mr Toots. “It’s of no consequence. Good-night. It’s of no consequence, thank’ee!”\n\n# CHAPTER XLV. The Trusty Agent\n\nEdith went out alone that day, and returned home early. It was but a few minutes after ten o’clock, when her carriage rolled along the street in which she lived.\n\nThere was the same enforced composure on her face, that there had been when she was dressing; and the wreath upon her head encircled the same cold and steady brow. But it would have been better to have seen its leaves and flowers reft into fragments by her passionate hand, or rendered shapeless by the fitful searches of a throbbing and bewildered brain for any resting-place, than adorning such tranquillity. So obdurate, so unapproachable, so unrelenting, one would have thought that nothing could soften such a woman’s nature, and that everything in life had hardened it.\n\nArrived at her own door, she was alighting, when some one coming quietly from the hall, and standing bareheaded, offered her his arm. The servant being thrust aside, she had no choice but to touch it; and she then knew whose arm it was.\n\n“How is your patient, Sir?” she asked, with a curled lip.\n\n“He is better,” returned Carker. “He is doing very well. I have left him for the night.”\n\nShe bent her head, and was passing up the staircase, when he followed and said, speaking at the bottom:\n\n“Madam! May I beg the favour of a minute’s audience?”\n\nShe stopped and turned her eyes back “It is an unseasonable time, Sir, and I am fatigued. Is your business urgent?”\n\n“It is very urgent, returned Carker. “As I am so fortunate as to have met you, let me press my petition.”\n\nShe looked down for a moment at his glistening mouth; and he looked up at her, standing above him in her stately dress, and thought, again, how beautiful she was.\n\n“Where is Miss Dombey?” she asked the servant, aloud.\n\n“In the morning room, Ma’am.”\n\n“Show the way there!” Turning her eyes again on the attentive gentleman at the bottom of the stairs, and informing him with a slight motion of her head, that he was at liberty to follow, she passed on.\n\n“I beg your pardon! Madam! Mrs Dombey!” cried the soft and nimble Carker, at her side in a moment. “May I be permitted to entreat that Miss Dombey is not present?”\n\nShe confronted him, with a quick look, but with the same self-possession and steadiness.\n\n“I would spare Miss Dombey,” said Carker, in a low voice, “the knowledge of what I have to say. At least, Madam, I would leave it to you to decide whether she shall know of it or not. I owe that to you. It is my bounden duty to you. After our former interview, it would be monstrous in me if I did otherwise.”\n\nShe slowly withdrew her eyes from his face, and turning to the servant, said, “Some other room.” He led the way to a drawing-room, which he speedily lighted up and then left them. While he remained, not a word was spoken. Edith enthroned herself upon a couch by the fire; and Mr Carker, with his hat in his hand and his eyes bent upon the carpet, stood before her, at some little distance.\n\n“Before I hear you, Sir,” said Edith, when the door was closed, “I wish you to hear me.”\n\n“To be addressed by Mrs Dombey,” he returned, “even in accents of unmerited reproach, is an honour I so greatly esteem, that although I were not her servant in all things, I should defer to such a wish, most readily.”\n\n“If you are charged by the man whom you have just now left, Sir;” Mr Carker raised his eyes, as if he were going to counterfeit surprise, but she met them, and stopped him, if such were his intention; “with any message to me, do not attempt to deliver it, for I will not receive it. I need scarcely ask you if you are come on such an errand. I have expected you some time.”\n\n“It is my misfortune,” he replied, “to be here, wholly against my will, for such a purpose. Allow me to say that I am here for two purposes. That is one.”\n\n“That one, Sir,” she returned, “is ended. Or, if you return to it—”\n\n“Can Mrs Dombey believe,” said Carker, coming nearer, “that I would return to it in the face of her prohibition? Is it possible that Mrs Dombey, having no regard to my unfortunate position, is so determined to consider me inseparable from my instructor as to do me great and wilful injustice?”\n\n“Sir,” returned Edith, bending her dark gaze full upon him, and speaking with a rising passion that inflated her proud nostril and her swelling neck, and stirred the delicate white down upon a robe she wore, thrown loosely over shoulders that could bear its snowy neighbourhood, “Why do you present yourself to me, as you have done, and speak to me of love and duty to my husband, and pretend to think that I am happily married, and that I honour him? How dare you venture so to affront me, when you know—I do not know better, Sir: I have seen it in your every glance, and heard it in your every word—that in place of affection between us there is aversion and contempt, and that I despise him hardly less than I despise myself for being his! Injustice! If I had done justice to the torment you have made me feel, and to my sense of the insult you have put upon me, I should have slain you!”\n\nShe had asked him why he did this. Had she not been blinded by her pride and wrath, and self-humiliation,—which she was, fiercely as she bent her gaze upon him,—she would have seen the answer in his face. To bring her to this declaration.\n\nShe saw it not, and cared not whether it was there or no. She saw only the indignities and struggles she had undergone and had to undergo, and was writhing under them. As she sat looking fixedly at them, rather than at him, she plucked the feathers from a pinion of some rare and beautiful bird, which hung from her wrist by a golden thread, to serve her as a fan, and rained them on the ground.\n\nHe did not shrink beneath her gaze, but stood, until such outward signs of her anger as had escaped her control subsided, with the air of a man who had his sufficient reply in reserve and would presently deliver it. And he then spoke, looking straight into her kindling eyes.\n\n“Madam,” he said, “I know, and knew before today, that I have found no favour with you; and I knew why. Yes. I knew why. You have spoken so openly to me; I am so relieved by the possession of your confidence—”\n\n“Confidence!” she repeated, with disdain.\n\nHe passed it over.\n\n“—that I will make no pretence of concealment. I did see from the first, that there was no affection on your part for Mr Dombey—how could it possibly exist between such different subjects? And I have seen, since, that stronger feelings than indifference have been engendered in your breast—how could that possibly be otherwise, either, circumstanced as you have been? But was it for me to presume to avow this knowledge to you in so many words?”\n\n“Was it for you, Sir,” she replied, “to feign that other belief, and audaciously to thrust it on me day by day?”\n\n“Madam, it was,” he eagerly retorted. “If I had done less, if I had done anything but that, I should not be speaking to you thus; and I foresaw—who could better foresee, for who has had greater experience of Mr Dombey than myself?—that unless your character should prove to be as yielding and obedient as that of his first submissive lady, which I did not believe—”\n\nA haughty smile gave him reason to observe that he might repeat this.\n\n“I say, which I did not believe,—the time was likely to come, when such an understanding as we have now arrived at, would be serviceable.”\n\n“Serviceable to whom, Sir?” she demanded scornfully.\n\n“To you. I will not add to myself, as warning me to refrain even from that limited commendation of Mr Dombey, in which I can honestly indulge, in order that I may not have the misfortune of saying anything distasteful to one whose aversion and contempt,” with great expression, “are so keen.”\n\n“Is it honest in you, Sir,” said Edith, “to confess to your ‘limited commendation,’ and to speak in that tone of disparagement, even of him: being his chief counsellor and flatterer!”\n\n“Counsellor,—yes,” said Carker. “Flatterer,—no. A little reservation I fear I must confess to. But our interest and convenience commonly oblige many of us to make professions that we cannot feel. We have partnerships of interest and convenience, friendships of interest and convenience, dealings of interest and convenience, marriages of interest and convenience, every day.”\n\nShe bit her blood-red lip; but without wavering in the dark, stern watch she kept upon him.\n\n“Madam,” said Mr Carker, sitting down in a chair that was near her, with an air of the most profound and most considerate respect, “why should I hesitate now, being altogether devoted to your service, to speak plainly? It was natural that a lady, endowed as you are, should think it feasible to change her husband’s character in some respects, and mould him to a better form.”\n\n“It was not natural to me, Sir,” she rejoined. “I had never any expectation or intention of that kind.”\n\nThe proud undaunted face showed him it was resolute to wear no mask he offered, but was set upon a reckless disclosure of itself, indifferent to any aspect in which it might present itself to such as he.\n\n“At least it was natural,” he resumed, “that you should deem it quite possible to live with Mr Dombey as his wife, at once without submitting to him, and without coming into such violent collision with him. But, Madam, you did not know Mr Dombey (as you have since ascertained), when you thought that. You did not know how exacting and how proud he is, or how he is, if I may say so, the slave of his own greatness, and goes yoked to his own triumphal car like a beast of burden, with no idea on earth but that it is behind him and is to be drawn on, over everything and through everything.”\n\nHis teeth gleamed through his malicious relish of this conceit, as he went on talking:\n\n“Mr Dombey is really capable of no more true consideration for you, Madam, than for me. The comparison is an extreme one; I intend it to be so; but quite just. Mr Dombey, in the plenitude of his power, asked me—I had it from his own lips yesterday morning—to be his go-between to you, because he knows I am not agreeable to you, and because he intends that I shall be a punishment for your contumacy; and besides that, because he really does consider, that I, his paid servant, am an ambassador whom it is derogatory to the dignity—not of the lady to whom I have the happiness of speaking; she has no existence in his mind—but of his wife, a part of himself, to receive. You may imagine how regardless of me, how obtuse to the possibility of my having any individual sentiment or opinion he is, when he tells me, openly, that I am so employed. You know how perfectly indifferent to your feelings he is, when he threatens you with such a messenger. As you, of course, have not forgotten that he did.”\n\nShe watched him still attentively. But he watched her too; and he saw that this indication of a knowledge on his part, of something that had passed between herself and her husband, rankled and smarted in her haughty breast, like a poisoned arrow.\n\n“I do not recall all this to widen the breach between yourself and Mr Dombey, Madam—Heaven forbid! what would it profit me?—but as an example of the hopelessness of impressing Mr Dombey with a sense that anybody is to be considered when he is in question. We who are about him, have, in our various positions, done our part, I daresay, to confirm him in his way of thinking; but if we had not done so, others would—or they would not have been about him; and it has always been, from the beginning, the very staple of his life. Mr Dombey has had to deal, in short, with none but submissive and dependent persons, who have bowed the knee, and bent the neck, before him. He has never known what it is to have angry pride and strong resentment opposed to him.”\n\n“But he will know it now!” she seemed to say; though her lips did not part, nor her eyes falter. He saw the soft down tremble once again, and he saw her lay the plumage of the beautiful bird against her bosom for a moment; and he unfolded one more ring of the coil into which he had gathered himself.\n\n“Mr Dombey, though a most honourable gentleman,” he said, “is so prone to pervert even facts to his own view, when he is at all opposed, in consequence of the warp in his mind, that he—can I give a better instance than this!—he sincerely believes (you will excuse the folly of what I am about to say; it not being mine) that his severe expression of opinion to his present wife, on a certain special occasion she may remember, before the lamented death of Mrs Skewton, produced a withering effect, and for the moment quite subdued her!”\n\nEdith laughed. How harshly and unmusically need not be described. It is enough that he was glad to hear her.\n\n“Madam,” he resumed, “I have done with this. Your own opinions are so strong, and, I am persuaded, so unalterable,” he repeated those words slowly and with great emphasis, “that I am almost afraid to incur your displeasure anew, when I say that in spite of these defects and my full knowledge of them, I have become habituated to Mr Dombey, and esteem him. But when I say so, it is not, believe me, for the mere sake of vaunting a feeling that is so utterly at variance with your own, and for which you can have no sympathy”—oh how distinct and plain and emphasized this was!—“but to give you an assurance of the zeal with which, in this unhappy matter, I am yours, and the indignation with which I regard the part I am to fill!”\n\nShe sat as if she were afraid to take her eyes from his face.\n\nAnd now to unwind the last ring of the coil!\n\n“It is growing late,” said Carker, after a pause, “and you are, as you said, fatigued. But the second object of this interview, I must not forget. I must recommend you, I must entreat you in the most earnest manner, for sufficient reasons that I have, to be cautious in your demonstrations of regard for Miss Dombey.”\n\n“Cautious! What do you mean?”\n\n“To be careful how you exhibit too much affection for that young lady.”\n\n“Too much affection, Sir!” said Edith, knitting her broad brow and rising. “Who judges my affection, or measures it out? You?”\n\n“It is not I who do so.” He was, or feigned to be, perplexed.\n\n“Who then?”\n\n“Can you not guess who then?”\n\n“I do not choose to guess,” she answered.\n\n“Madam,” he said after a little hesitation; meantime they had been, and still were, regarding each other as before; “I am in a difficulty here. You have told me you will receive no message, and you have forbidden me to return to that subject; but the two subjects are so closely entwined, I find, that unless you will accept this vague caution from one who has now the honour to possess your confidence, though the way to it has been through your displeasure, I must violate the injunction you have laid upon me.”\n\n“You know that you are free to do so, Sir,” said Edith. “Do it.”\n\nSo pale, so trembling, so impassioned! He had not miscalculated the effect then!\n\n“His instructions were,” he said, in a low voice, “that I should inform you that your demeanour towards Miss Dombey is not agreeable to him. That it suggests comparisons to him which are not favourable to himself. That he desires it may be wholly changed; and that if you are in earnest, he is confident it will be; for your continued show of affection will not benefit its object.”\n\n“That is a threat,” she said.\n\n“That is a threat,” he answered, in his voiceless manner of assent: adding aloud, “but not directed against you.”\n\nProud, erect, and dignified, as she stood confronting him; and looking through him as she did, with her full bright flashing eye; and smiling, as she was, with scorn and bitterness; she sunk as if the ground had dropped beneath her, and in an instant would have fallen on the floor, but that he caught her in his arms. As instantaneously she threw him off, the moment that he touched her, and, drawing back, confronted him again, immoveable, with her hand stretched out.\n\n“Please to leave me. Say no more tonight.”\n\n“I feel the urgency of this,” said Mr Carker, “because it is impossible to say what unforeseen consequences might arise, or how soon, from your being unacquainted with his state of mind. I understand Miss Dombey is concerned, now, at the dismissal of her old servant, which is likely to have been a minor consequence in itself. You don’t blame me for requesting that Miss Dombey might not be present. May I hope so?”\n\n“I do not. Please to leave me, Sir.”\n\n“I knew that your regard for the young lady, which is very sincere and strong, I am well persuaded, would render it a great unhappiness to you, ever to be a prey to the reflection that you had injured her position and ruined her future hopes,” said Carker hurriedly, but eagerly.\n\n“No more tonight. Leave me, if you please.”\n\n“I shall be here constantly in my attendance upon him, and in the transaction of business matters. You will allow me to see you again, and to consult what should be done, and learn your wishes?”\n\nShe motioned him towards the door.\n\n“I cannot even decide whether to tell him I have spoken to you yet; or to lead him to suppose that I have deferred doing so, for want of opportunity, or for any other reason. It will be necessary that you should enable me to consult with you very soon.”\n\n“At any time but now,” she answered.\n\n“You will understand, when I wish to see you, that Miss Dombey is not to be present; and that I seek an interview as one who has the happiness to possess your confidence, and who comes to render you every assistance in his power, and, perhaps, on many occasions, to ward off evil from her?”\n\nLooking at him still with the same apparent dread of releasing him for a moment from the influence of her steady gaze, whatever that might be, she answered, “Yes!” and once more bade him go.\n\nHe bowed, as if in compliance; but turning back, when he had nearly reached the door, said:\n\n“I am forgiven, and have explained my fault. May I—for Miss Dombey’s sake, and for my own—take your hand before I go?”\n\nShe gave him the gloved hand she had maimed last night. He took it in one of his, and kissed it, and withdrew. And when he had closed the door, he waved the hand with which he had taken hers, and thrust it in his breast.\n\nEdith saw no one that night, but locked her door, and kept herself alone.\n\nShe did not weep; she showed no greater agitation, outwardly, than when she was riding home. She laid as proud a head upon her pillow as she had borne in her carriage; and her prayer ran thus:\n\n“May this man be a liar! For if he has spoken truth, she is lost to me, and I have no hope left!”\n\nThis man, meanwhile, went home musing to bed, thinking, with a dainty pleasure, how imperious her passion was, how she had sat before him in her beauty, with the dark eyes that had never turned away but once; how the white down had fluttered; how the bird’s feathers had been strewn upon the ground.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVI. Recognizant and Reflective\n\nAmong sundry minor alterations in Mr Carker’s life and habits that began to take place at this time, none was more remarkable than the extraordinary diligence with which he applied himself to business, and the closeness with which he investigated every detail that the affairs of the House laid open to him. Always active and penetrating in such matters, his lynx-eyed vigilance now increased twenty-fold. Not only did his weary watch keep pace with every present point that every day presented to him in some new form, but in the midst of these engrossing occupations he found leisure—that is, he made it—to review the past transactions of the Firm, and his share in them, during a long series of years. Frequently when the clerks were all gone, the offices dark and empty, and all similar places of business shut up, Mr Carker, with the whole anatomy of the iron room laid bare before him, would explore the mysteries of books and papers, with the patient progress of a man who was dissecting the minutest nerves and fibres of his subject. Perch, the messenger, who usually remained on these occasions, to entertain himself with the perusal of the Price Current by the light of one candle, or to doze over the fire in the outer office, at the imminent risk every moment of diving head foremost into the coal-box, could not withhold the tribute of his admiration from this zealous conduct, although it much contracted his domestic enjoyments; and again, and again, expatiated to Mrs Perch (now nursing twins) on the industry and acuteness of their managing gentleman in the City.\n\nThe same increased and sharp attention that Mr Carker bestowed on the business of the House, he applied to his own personal affairs. Though not a partner in the concern—a distinction hitherto reserved solely to inheritors of the great name of Dombey—he was in the receipt of some percentage on its dealings; and, participating in all its facilities for the employment of money to advantage, was considered, by the minnows among the tritons of the East, a rich man. It began to be said, among these shrewd observers, that Jem Carker, of Dombey’s, was looking about him to see what he was worth; and that he was calling in his money at a good time, like the long-headed fellow he was; and bets were even offered on the Stock Exchange that Jem was going to marry a rich widow.\n\nYet these cares did not in the least interfere with Mr Carker’s watching of his chief, or with his cleanness, neatness, sleekness, or any cat-like quality he possessed. It was not so much that there was a change in him, in reference to any of his habits, as that the whole man was intensified. Everything that had been observable in him before, was observable now, but with a greater amount of concentration. He did each single thing, as if he did nothing else—a pretty certain indication in a man of that range of ability and purpose, that he is doing something which sharpens and keeps alive his keenest powers.\n\nThe only decided alteration in him was, that as he rode to and fro along the streets, he would fall into deep fits of musing, like that in which he had come away from Mr Dombey’s house, on the morning of that gentleman’s disaster. At such times, he would keep clear of the obstacles in his way, mechanically; and would appear to see and hear nothing until arrival at his destination, or some sudden chance or effort roused him.\n\nWalking his white-legged horse thus, to the counting-house of Dombey and Son one day, he was as unconscious of the observation of two pairs of women’s eyes, as of the fascinated orbs of Rob the Grinder, who, in waiting a street’s length from the appointed place, as a demonstration of punctuality, vainly touched and retouched his hat to attract attention, and trotted along on foot, by his master’s side, prepared to hold his stirrup when he should alight.\n\n“See where he goes!” cried one of these two women, an old creature, who stretched out her shrivelled arm to point him out to her companion, a young woman, who stood close beside her, withdrawn like herself into a gateway.\n\nMrs Brown’s daughter looked out, at this bidding on the part of Mrs Brown; and there were wrath and vengeance in her face.\n\n“I never thought to look at him again,” she said, in a low voice; “but it’s well I should, perhaps. I see. I see!”\n\n“Not changed!” said the old woman, with a look of eager malice.\n\n“He changed!” returned the other. “What for? What has he suffered? There is change enough for twenty in me. Isn’t that enough?”\n\n“See where he goes!” muttered the old woman, watching her daughter with her red eyes; “so easy and so trim a-horseback, while we are in the mud.”\n\n“And of it,” said her daughter impatiently. “We are mud, underneath his horse’s feet. What should we be?”\n\nIn the intentness with which she looked after him again, she made a hasty gesture with her hand when the old woman began to reply, as if her view could be obstructed by mere sound. Her mother watching her, and not him, remained silent; until her kindling glance subsided, and she drew a long breath, as if in the relief of his being gone.\n\n“Deary!” said the old woman then. “Alice! Handsome gall Ally!” She gently shook her sleeve to arouse her attention. “Will you let him go like that, when you can wring money from him? Why, it’s a wickedness, my daughter.”\n\n“Haven’t I told you, that I will not have money from him?” she returned. “And don’t you yet believe me? Did I take his sister’s money? Would I touch a penny, if I knew it, that had gone through his white hands—unless it was, indeed, that I could poison it, and send it back to him? Peace, mother, and come away.”\n\n“And him so rich?” murmured the old woman. “And us so poor!”\n\n“Poor in not being able to pay him any of the harm we owe him,” returned her daughter. “Let him give me that sort of riches, and I’ll take them from him, and use them. Come away. Its no good looking at his horse. Come away, mother!”\n\nBut the old woman, for whom the spectacle of Rob the Grinder returning down the street, leading the riderless horse, appeared to have some extraneous interest that it did not possess in itself, surveyed that young man with the utmost earnestness; and seeming to have whatever doubts she entertained, resolved as he drew nearer, glanced at her daughter with brightened eyes and with her finger on her lip, and emerging from the gateway at the moment of his passing, touched him on the shoulder.\n\n“Why, where’s my sprightly Rob been, all this time!” she said, as he turned round.\n\nThe sprightly Rob, whose sprightliness was very much diminished by the salutation, looked exceedingly dismayed, and said, with the water rising in his eyes:\n\n“Oh! why can’t you leave a poor cove alone, Misses Brown, when he’s getting an honest livelihood and conducting himself respectable? What do you come and deprive a cove of his character for, by talking to him in the streets, when he’s taking his master’s horse to a honest stable—a horse you’d go and sell for cats’ and dogs’ meat if you had your way! Why, I thought,” said the Grinder, producing his concluding remark as if it were the climax of all his injuries, “that you was dead long ago!”\n\n“This is the way,” cried the old woman, appealing to her daughter, “that he talks to me, who knew him weeks and months together, my deary, and have stood his friend many and many a time among the pigeon-fancying tramps and bird-catchers.”\n\n“Let the birds be, will you, Misses Brown?” retorted Rob, in a tone of the acutest anguish. “I think a cove had better have to do with lions than them little creeturs, for they’re always flying back in your face when you least expect it. Well, how d’ye do and what do you want?” These polite inquiries the Grinder uttered, as it were under protest, and with great exasperation and vindictiveness.\n\n“Hark how he speaks to an old friend, my deary!” said Mrs Brown, again appealing to her daughter. “But there’s some of his old friends not so patient as me. If I was to tell some that he knows, and has spotted and cheated with, where to find him—”\n\n“Will you hold your tongue, Misses Brown?” interrupted the miserable Grinder, glancing quickly round, as though he expected to see his master’s teeth shining at his elbow. “What do you take a pleasure in ruining a cove for? At your time of life too! when you ought to be thinking of a variety of things!”\n\n“What a gallant horse!” said the old woman, patting the animal’s neck.\n\n“Let him alone, will you, Misses Brown?” cried Rob, pushing away her hand. “You’re enough to drive a penitent cove mad!”\n\n“Why, what hurt do I do him, child?” returned the old woman.\n\n“Hurt?” said Rob. “He’s got a master that would find it out if he was touched with a straw.” And he blew upon the place where the old woman’s hand had rested for a moment, and smoothed it gently with his finger, as if he seriously believed what he said.\n\nThe old woman looking back to mumble and mouth at her daughter, who followed, kept close to Rob’s heels as he walked on with the bridle in his hand; and pursued the conversation.\n\n“A good place, Rob, eh?” said she. “You’re in luck, my child.”\n\n“Oh don’t talk about luck, Misses Brown,” returned the wretched Grinder, facing round and stopping. “If you’d never come, or if you’d go away, then indeed a cove might be considered tolerable lucky. Can’t you go along, Misses Brown, and not foller me!” blubbered Rob, with sudden defiance. “If the young woman’s a friend of yours, why don’t she take you away, instead of letting you make yourself so disgraceful!”\n\n“What!” croaked the old woman, putting her face close to his, with a malevolent grin upon it that puckered up the loose skin down in her very throat. “Do you deny your old chum! Have you lurked to my house fifty times, and slept sound in a corner when you had no other bed but the paving-stones, and do you talk to me like this! Have I bought and sold with you, and helped you in my way of business, schoolboy, sneak, and what not, and do you tell me to go along? Could I raise a crowd of old company about you to-morrow morning, that would follow you to ruin like copies of your own shadow, and do you turn on me with your bold looks! I’ll go. Come, Alice.”\n\n“Stop, Misses Brown!” cried the distracted Grinder. “What are you doing of? Don’t put yourself in a passion! Don’t let her go, if you please. I haven’t meant any offence. I said ‘how d’ye do,’ at first, didn’t I? But you wouldn’t answer. How you do? Besides,” said Rob piteously, “look here! How can a cove stand talking in the street with his master’s prad a-wanting to be took to be rubbed down, and his master up to every individgle thing that happens!”\n\nThe old woman made a show of being partially appeased, but shook her head, and mouthed and muttered still.\n\n“Come along to the stables, and have a glass of something that’s good for you, Misses Brown, can’t you?” said Rob, “instead of going on, like that, which is no good to you, nor anybody else. Come along with her, will you be so kind?” said Rob. “I’m sure I’m delighted to see her, if it wasn’t for the horse!”\n\nWith this apology, Rob turned away, a rueful picture of despair, and walked his charge down a bye street. The old woman, mouthing at her daughter, followed close upon him. The daughter followed.\n\nTurning into a silent little square or court-yard that had a great church tower rising above it, and a packer’s warehouse, and a bottle-maker’s warehouse, for its places of business, Rob the Grinder delivered the white-legged horse to the hostler of a quaint stable at the corner; and inviting Mrs Brown and her daughter to seat themselves upon a stone bench at the gate of that establishment, soon reappeared from a neighbouring public-house with a pewter measure and a glass.\n\n“Here’s master—Mr Carker, child!” said the old woman, slowly, as her sentiment before drinking. “Lord bless him!”\n\n“Why, I didn’t tell you who he was,” observed Rob, with staring eyes.\n\n“We know him by sight,” said Mrs Brown, whose working mouth and nodding head stopped for the moment, in the fixedness of her attention. “We saw him pass this morning, afore he got off his horse; when you were ready to take it.”\n\n“Ay, ay,” returned Rob, appearing to wish that his readiness had carried him to any other place.—“What’s the matter with her? Won’t she drink?”\n\nThis inquiry had reference to Alice, who, folded in her cloak, sat a little apart, profoundly inattentive to his offer of the replenished glass.\n\nThe old woman shook her head. “Don’t mind her,” she said; “she’s a strange creetur, if you know’d her, Rob. But Mr Carker—”\n\n“Hush!” said Rob, glancing cautiously up at the packer’s, and at the bottle-maker’s, as if, from any one of the tiers of warehouses, Mr Carker might be looking down. “Softly.”\n\n“Why, he ain’t here!” cried Mrs Brown.\n\n“I don’t know that,” muttered Rob, whose glance even wandered to the church tower, as if he might be there, with a supernatural power of hearing.\n\n“Good master?” inquired Mrs Brown.\n\nRob nodded; and added, in a low voice, “precious sharp.”\n\n“Lives out of town, don’t he, lovey?” said the old woman.\n\n“When he’s at home,” returned Rob; “but we don’t live at home just now.”\n\n“Where then?” asked the old woman.\n\n“Lodgings; up near Mr Dombey’s,” returned Rob.\n\nThe younger woman fixed her eyes so searchingly upon him, and so suddenly, that Rob was quite confounded, and offered the glass again, but with no more effect upon her than before.\n\n“Mr Dombey—you and I used to talk about him, sometimes, you know,” said Rob to Mrs Brown. “You used to get me to talk about him.”\n\nThe old woman nodded.\n\n“Well, Mr Dombey, he’s had a fall from his horse,” said Rob, unwillingly; “and my master has to be up there, more than usual, either with him, or Mrs Dombey, or some of ’em; and so we’ve come to town.”\n\n“Are they good friends, lovey?” asked the old woman.\n\n“Who?” retorted Rob.\n“He and she?”\n\n“What, Mr and Mrs Dombey?” said Rob. “How should I know!”\n\n“Not them—Master and Mrs Dombey, chick,” replied the old woman, coaxingly.\n\n“I don’t know,” said Rob, looking round him again. “I suppose so. How curious you are, Misses Brown! Least said, soonest mended.”\n\n“Why there’s no harm in it!” exclaimed the old woman, with a laugh, and a clap of her hands. “Sprightly Rob, has grown tame since he has been well off! There’s no harm in it.”\n\n“No, there’s no harm in it, I know,” returned Rob, with the same distrustful glance at the packer’s and the bottle-maker’s, and the church; “but blabbing, if it’s only about the number of buttons on my master’s coat, won’t do. I tell you it won’t do with him. A cove had better drown himself. He says so. I shouldn’t have so much as told you what his name was, if you hadn’t known it. Talk about somebody else.”\n\nAs Rob took another cautious survey of the yard, the old woman made a secret motion to her daughter. It was momentary, but the daughter, with a slight look of intelligence, withdrew her eyes from the boy’s face, and sat folded in her cloak as before.\n\n“Rob, lovey!” said the old woman, beckoning him to the other end of the bench. “You were always a pet and favourite of mine. Now, weren’t you? Don’t you know you were?”\n\n“Yes, Misses Brown,” replied the Grinder, with a very bad grace.\n\n“And you could leave me!” said the old woman, flinging her arms about his neck. “You could go away, and grow almost out of knowledge, and never come to tell your poor old friend how fortunate you were, proud lad! Oho, Oho!”\n\n“Oh here’s a dreadful go for a cove that’s got a master wide awake in the neighbourhood!” exclaimed the wretched Grinder. “To be howled over like this here!”\n\n“Won’t you come and see me, Robby?” cried Mrs Brown. “Oho, won’t you ever come and see me?”\n\n“Yes, I tell you! Yes, I will!” returned the Grinder.\n\n“That’s my own Rob! That’s my lovey!” said Mrs Brown, drying the tears upon her shrivelled face, and giving him a tender squeeze. “At the old place, Rob?”\n\n“Yes,” replied the Grinder.\n\n“Soon, Robby dear?” cried Mrs Brown; “and often?”\n\n“Yes. Yes. Yes,” replied Rob. “I will indeed, upon my soul and body.”\n\n“And then,” said Mrs Brown, with her arms uplifted towards the sky, and her head thrown back and shaking, “if he’s true to his word, I’ll never come a-near him though I know where he is, and never breathe a syllable about him! Never!”\n\nThis ejaculation seemed a drop of comfort to the miserable Grinder, who shook Mrs Brown by the hand upon it, and implored her with tears in his eyes, to leave a cove and not destroy his prospects. Mrs Brown, with another fond embrace, assented; but in the act of following her daughter, turned back, with her finger stealthily raised, and asked in a hoarse whisper for some money.\n\n“A shilling, dear!” she said, with her eager avaricious face, “or sixpence! For old acquaintance sake. I’m so poor. And my handsome gal”—looking over her shoulder—“she’s my gal, Rob—half starves me.”\n\nBut as the reluctant Grinder put it in her hand, her daughter, coming quietly back, caught the hand in hers, and twisted out the coin.\n\n“What,” she said, “mother! always money! money from the first, and to the last. Do you mind so little what I said but now? Here. Take it!”\n\nThe old woman uttered a moan as the money was restored, but without in any other way opposing its restoration, hobbled at her daughter’s side out of the yard, and along the by-street upon which it opened. The astonished and dismayed Rob staring after them, saw that they stopped, and fell to earnest conversation very soon; and more than once observed a darkly threatening action of the younger woman’s hand (obviously having reference to someone of whom they spoke), and a crooning feeble imitation of it on the part of Mrs Brown, that made him earnestly hope he might not be the subject of their discourse.\n\nWith the present consolation that they were gone, and with the prospective comfort that Mrs Brown could not live for ever, and was not likely to live long to trouble him, the Grinder, not otherwise regretting his misdeeds than as they were attended with such disagreeable incidental consequences, composed his ruffled features to a more serene expression by thinking of the admirable manner in which he had disposed of Captain Cuttle (a reflection that seldom failed to put him in a flow of spirits), and went to the Dombey Counting House to receive his master’s orders.\n\nThere his master, so subtle and vigilant of eye, that Rob quaked before him, more than half expecting to be taxed with Mrs Brown, gave him the usual morning’s box of papers for Mr Dombey, and a note for Mrs Dombey: merely nodding his head as an enjoinder to be careful, and to use dispatch—a mysterious admonition, fraught in the Grinder’s imagination with dismal warnings and threats; and more powerful with him than any words.\n\nAlone again, in his own room, Mr Carker applied himself to work, and worked all day. He saw many visitors; overlooked a number of documents; went in and out, to and from, sundry places of mercantile resort; and indulged in no more abstraction until the day’s business was done. But, when the usual clearance of papers from his table was made at last, he fell into his thoughtful mood once more.\n\nHe was standing in his accustomed place and attitude, with his eyes intently fixed upon the ground, when his brother entered to bring back some letters that had been taken out in the course of the day. He put them quietly on the table, and was going immediately, when Mr Carker the Manager, whose eyes had rested on him, on his entrance, as if they had all this time had him for the subject of their contemplation, instead of the office-floor, said:\n\n“Well, John Carker, and what brings you here?”\n\nHis brother pointed to the letters, and was again withdrawing.\n\n“I wonder,” said the Manager, “that you can come and go, without inquiring how our master is”.\n\n“We had word this morning in the Counting House, that Mr Dombey was doing well,” replied his brother.\n\n“You are such a meek fellow,” said the Manager, with a smile,—“but you have grown so, in the course of years—that if any harm came to him, you’d be miserable, I dare swear now.”\n\n“I should be truly sorry, James,” returned the other.\n\n“He would be sorry!” said the Manager, pointing at him, as if there were some other person present to whom he was appealing. “He would be truly sorry! This brother of mine! This junior of the place, this slighted piece of lumber, pushed aside with his face to the wall, like a rotten picture, and left so, for Heaven knows how many years he’s all gratitude and respect, and devotion too, he would have me believe!”\n\n“I would have you believe nothing, James,” returned the other. “Be as just to me as you would to any other man below you. You ask a question, and I answer it.”\n\n“And have you nothing, Spaniel,” said the Manager, with unusual irascibility, “to complain of in him? No proud treatment to resent, no insolence, no foolery of state, no exaction of any sort! What the devil! are you man or mouse?”\n\n“It would be strange if any two persons could be together for so many years, especially as superior and inferior, without each having something to complain of in the other—as he thought, at all events,” replied John Carker. “But apart from my history here—”\n\n“His history here!” exclaimed the Manager. “Why, there it is. The very fact that makes him an extreme case, puts him out of the whole chapter! Well?”\n\n“Apart from that, which, as you hint, gives me a reason to be thankful that I alone (happily for all the rest) possess, surely there is no one in the House who would not say and feel at least as much. You do not think that anybody here would be indifferent to a mischance or misfortune happening to the head of the House, or anything than truly sorry for it?”\n\n“You have good reason to be bound to him too!” said the Manager, contemptuously. “Why, don’t you believe that you are kept here, as a cheap example, and a famous instance of the clemency of Dombey and Son, redounding to the credit of the illustrious House?”\n\n“No,” replied his brother, mildly, “I have long believed that I am kept here for more kind and disinterested reasons.”\n\n“But you were going,” said the Manager, with the snarl of a tiger-cat, “to recite some Christian precept, I observed.”\n\n“Nay, James,” returned the other, “though the tie of brotherhood between us has been long broken and thrown away—”\n\n“Who broke it, good Sir?” said the Manager.\n\n“I, by my misconduct. I do not charge it upon you.”\n\nThe Manager replied, with that mute action of his bristling mouth, “Oh, you don’t charge it upon me!” and bade him go on.\n\n“I say, though there is not that tie between us, do not, I entreat, assail me with unnecessary taunts, or misinterpret what I say, or would say. I was only going to suggest to you that it would be a mistake to suppose that it is only you, who have been selected here, above all others, for advancement, confidence and distinction (selected, in the beginning, I know, for your great ability and trustfulness), and who communicate more freely with Mr Dombey than anyone, and stand, it may be said, on equal terms with him, and have been favoured and enriched by him—that it would be a mistake to suppose that it is only you who are tender of his welfare and reputation. There is no one in the House, from yourself down to the lowest, I sincerely believe, who does not participate in that feeling.”\n\n“You lie!” said the Manager, red with sudden anger. “You’re a hypocrite, John Carker, and you lie.”\n\n“James!” cried the other, flushing in his turn. “What do you mean by these insulting words? Why do you so basely use them to me, unprovoked?”\n\n“I tell you,” said the Manager, “that your hypocrisy and meekness—that all the hypocrisy and meekness of this place—is not worth that to me,” snapping his thumb and finger, “and that I see through it as if it were air! There is not a man employed here, standing between myself and the lowest in place (of whom you are very considerate, and with reason, for he is not far off), who wouldn’t be glad at heart to see his master humbled: who does not hate him, secretly: who does not wish him evil rather than good: and who would not turn upon him, if he had the power and boldness. The nearer to his favour, the nearer to his insolence; the closer to him, the farther from him. That’s the creed here!”\n\n“I don’t know,” said his brother, whose roused feelings had soon yielded to surprise, “who may have abused your ear with such representations; or why you have chosen to try me, rather than another. But that you have been trying me, and tampering with me, I am now sure. You have a different manner and a different aspect from any that I ever saw in you. I will only say to you, once more, you are deceived.”\n\n“I know I am,” said the Manager. “I have told you so.”\n\n“Not by me,” returned his brother. “By your informant, if you have one. If not, by your own thoughts and suspicions.”\n\n“I have no suspicions,” said the Manager. “Mine are certainties. You pusillanimous, abject, cringing dogs! All making the same show, all canting the same story, all whining the same professions, all harbouring the same transparent secret.”\n\nHis brother withdrew, without saying more, and shut the door as he concluded. Mr Carker the Manager drew a chair close before the fire, and fell to beating the coals softly with the poker.\n\n“The faint-hearted, fawning knaves,” he muttered, with his two shining rows of teeth laid bare. “There’s not one among them, who wouldn’t feign to be so shocked and outraged—! Bah! There’s not one among them, but if he had at once the power, and the wit and daring to use it, would scatter Dombey’s pride and lay it low, as ruthlessly as I rake out these ashes.”\n\nAs he broke them up and strewed them in the grate, he looked on with a thoughtful smile at what he was doing. “Without the same queen beckoner too!” he added presently; “and there is pride there, not to be forgotten—witness our own acquaintance!” With that he fell into a deeper reverie, and sat pondering over the blackening grate, until he rose up like a man who had been absorbed in a book, and looking round him took his hat and gloves, went to where his horse was waiting, mounted, and rode away through the lighted streets, for it was evening.\n\nHe rode near Mr Dombey’s house; and falling into a walk as he approached it, looked up at the windows The window where he had once seen Florence sitting with her dog attracted his attention first, though there was no light in it; but he smiled as he carried his eyes up the tall front of the house, and seemed to leave that object superciliously behind.\n\n“Time was,” he said, “when it was well to watch even your rising little star, and know in what quarter there were clouds, to shadow you if needful. But a planet has arisen, and you are lost in its light.”\n\nHe turned the white-legged horse round the street corner, and sought one shining window from among those at the back of the house. Associated with it was a certain stately presence, a gloved hand, the remembrance how the feathers of a beautiful bird’s wing had been showered down upon the floor, and how the light white down upon a robe had stirred and rustled, as in the rising of a distant storm. These were the things he carried with him as he turned away again, and rode through the darkening and deserted Parks at a quick rate.\n\nIn fatal truth, these were associated with a woman, a proud woman, who hated him, but who by slow and sure degrees had been led on by his craft, and her pride and resentment, to endure his company, and little by little to receive him as one who had the privilege to talk to her of her own defiant disregard of her own husband, and her abandonment of high consideration for herself. They were associated with a woman who hated him deeply, and who knew him, and who mistrusted him because she knew him, and because he knew her; but who fed her fierce resentment by suffering him to draw nearer and yet nearer to her every day, in spite of the hate she cherished for him. In spite of it! For that very reason; since in its depths, too far down for her threatening eye to pierce, though she could see into them dimly, lay the dark retaliation, whose faintest shadow seen once and shuddered at, and never seen again, would have been sufficient stain upon her soul.\n\nDid the phantom of such a woman flit about him on his ride; true to the reality, and obvious to him?\n\nYes. He saw her in his mind, exactly as she was. She bore him company with her pride, resentment, hatred, all as plain to him as her beauty; with nothing plainer to him than her hatred of him. He saw her sometimes haughty and repellent at his side, and some times down among his horse’s feet, fallen and in the dust. But he always saw her as she was, without disguise, and watched her on the dangerous way that she was going.\n\nAnd when his ride was over, and he was newly dressed, and came into the light of her bright room with his bent head, soft voice, and soothing smile, he saw her yet as plainly. He even suspected the mystery of the gloved hand, and held it all the longer in his own for that suspicion. Upon the dangerous way that she was going, he was, still; and not a footprint did she mark upon it, but he set his own there, straight.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII. The Thunderbolt\n\nThe barrier between Mr Dombey and his wife was not weakened by time. Ill-assorted couple, unhappy in themselves and in each other, bound together by no tie but the manacle that joined their fettered hands, and straining that so harshly, in their shrinking asunder, that it wore and chafed to the bone, Time, consoler of affliction and softener of anger, could do nothing to help them. Their pride, however different in kind and object, was equal in degree; and, in their flinty opposition, struck out fire between them which might smoulder or might blaze, as circumstances were, but burned up everything within their mutual reach, and made their marriage way a road of ashes.\n\nLet us be just to him. In the monstrous delusion of his life, swelling with every grain of sand that shifted in its glass, he urged her on, he little thought to what, or considered how; but still his feeling towards her, such as it was, remained as at first. She had the grand demerit of unaccountably putting herself in opposition to the recognition of his vast importance, and to the acknowledgment of her complete submission to it, and so far it was necessary to correct and reduce her; but otherwise he still considered her, in his cold way, a lady capable of doing honour, if she would, to his choice and name, and of reflecting credit on his proprietorship.\n\nNow, she, with all her might of passionate and proud resentment, bent her dark glance from day to day, and hour to hour—from that night in her own chamber, when she had sat gazing at the shadows on the wall, to the deeper night fast coming—upon one figure directing a crowd of humiliations and exasperations against her; and that figure, still her husband’s.\n\nWas Mr Dombey’s master-vice, that ruled him so inexorably, an unnatural characteristic? It might be worthwhile, sometimes, to inquire what Nature is, and how men work to change her, and whether, in the enforced distortions so produced, it is not natural to be unnatural. Coop any son or daughter of our mighty mother within narrow range, and bind the prisoner to one idea, and foster it by servile worship of it on the part of the few timid or designing people standing round, and what is Nature to the willing captive who has never risen up upon the wings of a free mind—drooping and useless soon—to see her in her comprehensive truth!\n\nAlas! are there so few things in the world, about us, most unnatural, and yet most natural in being so? Hear the magistrate or judge admonish the unnatural outcasts of society; unnatural in brutal habits, unnatural in want of decency, unnatural in losing and confounding all distinctions between good and evil; unnatural in ignorance, in vice, in recklessness, in contumacy, in mind, in looks, in everything. But follow the good clergyman or doctor, who, with his life imperilled at every breath he draws, goes down into their dens, lying within the echoes of our carriage wheels and daily tread upon the pavement stones. Look round upon the world of odious sights—millions of immortal creatures have no other world on earth—at the lightest mention of which humanity revolts, and dainty delicacy living in the next street, stops her ears, and lisps “I don’t believe it!” Breathe the polluted air, foul with every impurity that is poisonous to health and life; and have every sense, conferred upon our race for its delight and happiness, offended, sickened and disgusted, and made a channel by which misery and death alone can enter. Vainly attempt to think of any simple plant, or flower, or wholesome weed, that, set in this foetid bed, could have its natural growth, or put its little leaves off to the sun as GOD designed it. And then, calling up some ghastly child, with stunted form and wicked face, hold forth on its unnatural sinfulness, and lament its being, so early, far away from Heaven—but think a little of its having been conceived, and born and bred, in Hell!\n\nThose who study the physical sciences, and bring them to bear upon the health of Man, tell us that if the noxious particles that rise from vitiated air were palpable to the sight, we should see them lowering in a dense black cloud above such haunts, and rolling slowly on to corrupt the better portions of a town. But if the moral pestilence that rises with them, and in the eternal laws of our Nature, is inseparable from them, could be made discernible too, how terrible the revelation! Then should we see depravity, impiety, drunkenness, theft, murder, and a long train of nameless sins against the natural affections and repulsions of mankind, overhanging the devoted spots, and creeping on, to blight the innocent and spread contagion among the pure. Then should we see how the same poisoned fountains that flow into our hospitals and lazar-houses, inundate the jails, and make the convict-ships swim deep, and roll across the seas, and over-run vast continents with crime. Then should we stand appalled to know, that where we generate disease to strike our children down and entail itself on unborn generations, there also we breed, by the same certain process, infancy that knows no innocence, youth without modesty or shame, maturity that is mature in nothing but in suffering and guilt, blasted old age that is a scandal on the form we bear, unnatural humanity! When we shall gather grapes from thorns, and figs from thistles; when fields of grain shall spring up from the offal in the bye-ways of our wicked cities, and roses bloom in the fat churchyards that they cherish; then we may look for natural humanity, and find it growing from such seed.\n\nOh for a good spirit who would take the house-tops off, with a more potent and benignant hand than the lame demon in the tale, and show a Christian people what dark shapes issue from amidst their homes, to swell the retinue of the Destroying Angel as he moves forth among them! For only one night’s view of the pale phantoms rising from the scenes of our too-long neglect; and from the thick and sullen air where Vice and Fever propagate together, raining the tremendous social retributions which are ever pouring down, and ever coming thicker! Bright and blest the morning that should rise on such a night: for men, delayed no more by stumbling-blocks of their own making, which are but specks of dust upon the path between them and eternity, would then apply themselves, like creatures of one common origin, owing one duty to the Father of one family, and tending to one common end, to make the world a better place!\n\nNot the less bright and blest would that day be for rousing some who never have looked out upon the world of human life around them, to a knowledge of their own relation to it, and for making them acquainted with a perversion of nature in their own contracted sympathies and estimates; as great, and yet as natural in its development when once begun, as the lowest degradation known.\n\nBut no such day had ever dawned on Mr Dombey, or his wife; and the course of each was taken.\n\nThrough six months that ensued upon his accident, they held the same relations one towards the other. A marble rock could not have stood more obdurately in his way than she; and no chilled spring, lying uncheered by any ray of light in the depths of a deep cave, could be more sullen or more cold than he.\n\nThe hope that had fluttered within her when the promise of her new home dawned, was quite gone from the heart of Florence now. That home was nearly two years old; and even the patient trust that was in her, could not survive the daily blight of such experience. If she had any lingering fancy in the nature of hope left, that Edith and her father might be happier together, in some distant time, she had none, now, that her father would ever love her. The little interval in which she had imagined that she saw some small relenting in him, was forgotten in the long remembrance of his coldness since and before, or only remembered as a sorrowful delusion.\n\nFlorence loved him still, but, by degrees, had come to love him rather as some dear one who had been, or who might have been, than as the hard reality before her eyes. Something of the softened sadness with which she loved the memory of little Paul, or of her mother, seemed to enter now into her thoughts of him, and to make them, as it were, a dear remembrance. Whether it was that he was dead to her, and that partly for this reason, partly for his share in those old objects of her affection, and partly for the long association of him with hopes that were withered and tendernesses he had frozen, she could not have told; but the father whom she loved began to be a vague and dreamy idea to her: hardly more substantially connected with her real life, than the image she would sometimes conjure up, of her dear brother yet alive, and growing to be a man, who would protect and cherish her.\n\nThe change, if it may be called one, had stolen on her like the change from childhood to womanhood, and had come with it. Florence was almost seventeen, when, in her lonely musings, she was conscious of these thoughts.\n\nShe was often alone now, for the old association between her and her Mama was greatly changed. At the time of her father’s accident, and when he was lying in his room downstairs, Florence had first observed that Edith avoided her. Wounded and shocked, and yet unable to reconcile this with her affection when they did meet, she sought her in her own room at night, once more.\n\n“Mama,” said Florence, stealing softly to her side, “have I offended you?”\n\nEdith answered “No.”\n\n“I must have done something,” said Florence. “Tell me what it is. You have changed your manner to me, dear Mama. I cannot say how instantly I feel the least change; for I love you with my whole heart.”\n\n“As I do you,” said Edith. “Ah, Florence, believe me never more than now!”\n\n“Why do you go away from me so often, and keep away?” asked Florence. “And why do you sometimes look so strangely on me, dear Mama? You do so, do you not?”\n\nEdith signified assent with her dark eyes.\n\n“Why?” returned Florence imploringly. “Tell me why, that I may know how to please you better; and tell me this shall not be so any more.”\n\n“My Florence,” answered Edith, taking the hand that embraced her neck, and looking into the eyes that looked into hers so lovingly, as Florence knelt upon the ground before her; “why it is, I cannot tell you. It is neither for me to say, nor you to hear; but that it is, and that it must be, I know. Should I do it if I did not?”\n\n“Are we to be estranged, Mama?” asked Florence, gazing at her like one frightened.\n\nEdith’s silent lips formed “Yes.”\n\nFlorence looked at her with increasing fear and wonder, until she could see her no more through the blinding tears that ran down her face.\n\n“Florence! my life!” said Edith, hurriedly, “listen to me. I cannot bear to see this grief. Be calmer. You see that I am composed, and is it nothing to me?”\n\nShe resumed her steady voice and manner as she said the latter words, and added presently:\n\n“Not wholly estranged. Partially: and only that, in appearance, Florence, for in my own breast I am still the same to you, and ever will be. But what I do is not done for myself.”\n\n“Is it for me, Mama?” asked Florence.\n\n“It is enough,” said Edith, after a pause, “to know what it is; why, matters little. Dear Florence, it is better—it is necessary—it must be—that our association should be less frequent. The confidence there has been between us must be broken off.”\n\n“When?” cried Florence. “Oh, Mama, when?”\n\n“Now,” said Edith.\n\n“For all time to come?” asked Florence.\n\n“I do not say that,” answered Edith. “I do not know that. Nor will I say that companionship between us is, at the best, an ill-assorted and unholy union, of which I might have known no good could come. My way here has been through paths that you will never tread, and my way henceforth may lie—God knows—I do not see it—”\n\nHer voice died away into silence; and she sat, looking at Florence, and almost shrinking from her, with the same strange dread and wild avoidance that Florence had noticed once before. The same dark pride and rage succeeded, sweeping over her form and features like an angry chord across the strings of a wild harp. But no softness or humility ensued on that. She did not lay her head down now, and weep, and say that she had no hope but in Florence. She held it up as if she were a beautiful Medusa, looking on him, face to face, to strike him dead. Yes, and she would have done it, if she had had the charm.\n\n“Mama,” said Florence, anxiously, “there is a change in you, in more than what you say to me, which alarms me. Let me stay with you a little.”\n\n“No,” said Edith, “no, dearest. I am best left alone now, and I do best to keep apart from you, of all else. Ask me no questions, but believe that what I am when I seem fickle or capricious to you, I am not of my own will, or for myself. Believe, though we are stranger to each other than we have been, that I am unchanged to you within. Forgive me for having ever darkened your dark home—I am a shadow on it, I know well—and let us never speak of this again.”\n\n“Mama,” sobbed Florence, “we are not to part?”\n\n“We do this that we may not part,” said Edith. “Ask no more. Go, Florence! My love and my remorse go with you!”\n\nShe embraced her, and dismissed her; and as Florence passed out of her room, Edith looked on the retiring figure, as if her good angel went out in that form, and left her to the haughty and indignant passions that now claimed her for their own, and set their seal upon her brow.\n\nFrom that hour, Florence and she were, as they had been, no more. For days together, they would seldom meet, except at table, and when Mr Dombey was present. Then Edith, imperious, inflexible, and silent, never looked at her. Whenever Mr Carker was of the party, as he often was, during the progress of Mr Dombey’s recovery, and afterwards, Edith held herself more removed from her, and was more distant towards her, than at other times. Yet she and Florence never encountered, when there was no one by, but she would embrace her as affectionately as of old, though not with the same relenting of her proud aspect; and often, when she had been out late, she would steal up to Florence’s room, as she had been used to do, in the dark, and whisper “Good-night,” on her pillow. When unconscious, in her slumber, of such visits, Florence would sometimes awake, as from a dream of those words, softly spoken, and would seem to feel the touch of lips upon her face. But less and less often as the months went on.\n\nAnd now the void in Florence’s own heart began again, indeed, to make a solitude around her. As the image of the father whom she loved had insensibly become a mere abstraction, so Edith, following the fate of all the rest about whom her affections had entwined themselves, was fleeting, fading, growing paler in the distance, every day. Little by little, she receded from Florence, like the retiring ghost of what she had been; little by little, the chasm between them widened and seemed deeper; little by little, all the power of earnestness and tenderness she had shown, was frozen up in the bold, angry hardihood with which she stood, upon the brink of a deep precipice unseen by Florence, daring to look down.\n\nThere was but one consideration to set against the heavy loss of Edith, and though it was slight comfort to her burdened heart, she tried to think it some relief. No longer divided between her affection and duty to the two, Florence could love both and do no injustice to either. As shadows of her fond imagination, she could give them equal place in her own bosom, and wrong them with no doubts.\n\nSo she tried to do. At times, and often too, wondering speculations on the cause of this change in Edith, would obtrude themselves upon her mind and frighten her; but in the calm of its abandonment once more to silent grief and loneliness, it was not a curious mind. Florence had only to remember that her star of promise was clouded in the general gloom that hung upon the house, and to weep and be resigned.\n\nThus living, in a dream wherein the overflowing love of her young heart expended itself on airy forms, and in a real world where she had experienced little but the rolling back of that strong tide upon itself, Florence grew to be seventeen. Timid and retiring as her solitary life had made her, it had not embittered her sweet temper, or her earnest nature. A child in innocent simplicity; a woman in her modest self-reliance, and her deep intensity of feeling; both child and woman seemed at once expressed in her face and fragile delicacy of shape, and gracefully to mingle there;—as if the spring should be unwilling to depart when summer came, and sought to blend the earlier beauties of the flowers with their bloom. But in her thrilling voice, in her calm eyes, sometimes in a sage ethereal light that seemed to rest upon her head, and always in a certain pensive air upon her beauty, there was an expression, such as had been seen in the dead boy; and the council in the Servants’ Hall whispered so among themselves, and shook their heads, and ate and drank the more, in a closer bond of good-fellowship.\n\nThis observant body had plenty to say of Mr and Mrs Dombey, and of Mr Carker, who appeared to be a mediator between them, and who came and went as if he were trying to make peace, but never could. They all deplored the uncomfortable state of affairs, and all agreed that Mrs Pipchin (whose unpopularity was not to be surpassed) had some hand in it; but, upon the whole, it was agreeable to have so good a subject for a rallying point, and they made a great deal of it, and enjoyed themselves very much.\n\nThe general visitors who came to the house, and those among whom Mr and Mrs Dombey visited, thought it a pretty equal match, as to haughtiness, at all events, and thought nothing more about it. The young lady with the back did not appear for some time after Mrs Skewton’s death; observing to some particular friends, with her usual engaging little scream, that she couldn’t separate the family from a notion of tombstones, and horrors of that sort; but when she did come, she saw nothing wrong, except Mr Dombey’s wearing a bunch of gold seals to his watch, which shocked her very much, as an exploded superstition. This youthful fascinator considered a daughter-in-law objectionable in principle; otherwise, she had nothing to say against Florence, but that she sadly wanted “style”—which might mean back, perhaps. Many, who only came to the house on state occasions, hardly knew who Florence was, and said, going home, “Indeed, was that Miss Dombey, in the corner? Very pretty, but a little delicate and thoughtful in appearance!”\n\nNone the less so, certainly, for her life of the last six months. Florence took her seat at the dinner-table, on the day before the second anniversary of her father’s marriage to Edith (Mrs Skewton had been lying stricken with paralysis when the first came round), with an uneasiness, amounting to dread. She had no other warrant for it, than the occasion, the expression of her father’s face, in the hasty glance she caught of it, and the presence of Mr Carker, which, always unpleasant to her, was more so on this day, than she had ever felt it before.\n\nEdith was richly dressed, for she and Mr Dombey were engaged in the evening to some large assembly, and the dinner-hour that day was late. She did not appear until they were seated at table, when Mr Carker rose and led her to her chair. Beautiful and lustrous as she was, there was that in her face and air which seemed to separate her hopelessly from Florence, and from everyone, for ever more. And yet, for an instant, Florence saw a beam of kindness in her eyes, when they were turned on her, that made the distance to which she had withdrawn herself a greater cause of sorrow and regret than ever.\n\nThere was very little said at dinner. Florence heard her father speak to Mr Carker sometimes on business matters, and heard him softly reply, but she paid little attention to what they said, and only wished the dinner at an end. When the dessert was placed upon the table, and they were left alone, with no servant in attendance, Mr Dombey, who had been several times clearing his throat in a manner that augured no good, said:\n\n“Mrs Dombey, you know, I suppose, that I have instructed the housekeeper that there will be some company to dinner here to-morrow.”\n\n“I do not dine at home,” she answered.\n\n“Not a large party,” pursued Mr Dombey, with an indifferent assumption of not having heard her; “merely some twelve or fourteen. My sister, Major Bagstock, and some others whom you know but slightly.”\n\n“I do not dine at home,” she repeated.\n\n“However doubtful reason I may have, Mrs Dombey,” said Mr Dombey, still going majestically on, as if she had not spoken, “to hold the occasion in very pleasant remembrance just now, there are appearances in these things which must be maintained before the world. If you have no respect for yourself, Mrs Dombey—”\n\n“I have none,” she said.\n\n“Madam,” cried Mr Dombey, striking his hand upon the table, “hear me if you please. I say, if you have no respect for yourself—”\n\n“And I say I have none,” she answered.\n\nHe looked at her; but the face she showed him in return would not have changed, if death itself had looked.\n\n“Carker,” said Mr Dombey, turning more quietly to that gentleman, “as you have been my medium of communication with Mrs Dombey on former occasions, and as I choose to preserve the decencies of life, so far as I am individually concerned, I will trouble you to have the goodness to inform Mrs Dombey that if she has no respect for herself, I have some respect for myself, and therefore insist on my arrangements for to-morrow.”\n\n“Tell your sovereign master, Sir,” said Edith, “that I will take leave to speak to him on this subject by-and-bye, and that I will speak to him alone.”\n\n“Mr Carker, Madam,” said her husband, “being in possession of the reason which obliges me to refuse you that privilege, shall be absolved from the delivery of any such message.” He saw her eyes move, while he spoke, and followed them with his own.\n\n“Your daughter is present, Sir,” said Edith.\n\n“My daughter will remain present,” said Mr Dombey.\n\nFlorence, who had risen, sat down again, hiding her face in her hands, and trembling.\n\n“My daughter, Madam”—began Mr Dombey.\n\nBut Edith stopped him, in a voice which, although not raised in the least, was so clear, emphatic, and distinct, that it might have been heard in a whirlwind.\n\n“I tell you I will speak to you alone,” she said. “If you are not mad, heed what I say.”\n\n“I have authority to speak to you, Madam,” returned her husband, “when and where I please; and it is my pleasure to speak here and now.”\n\nShe rose up as if to leave the room; but sat down again, and looking at him with all outward composure, said, in the same voice:\n\n“You shall!”\n\n“I must tell you first, that there is a threatening appearance in your manner, Madam,” said Mr Dombey, “which does not become you.”\n\nShe laughed. The shaken diamonds in her hair started and trembled. There are fables of precious stones that would turn pale, their wearer being in danger. Had these been such, their imprisoned rays of light would have taken flight that moment, and they would have been as dull as lead.\n\nCarker listened, with his eyes cast down.\n\n“As to my daughter, Madam,” said Mr Dombey, resuming the thread of his discourse, “it is by no means inconsistent with her duty to me, that she should know what conduct to avoid. At present you are a very strong example to her of this kind, and I hope she may profit by it.”\n\n“I would not stop you now,” returned his wife, immoveable in eye, and voice, and attitude; “I would not rise and go away, and save you the utterance of one word, if the room were burning.”\n\nMr Dombey moved his head, as if in a sarcastic acknowledgment of the attention, and resumed. But not with so much self-possession as before; for Edith’s quick uneasiness in reference to Florence, and Edith’s indifference to him and his censure, chafed and galled him like a stiffening wound.\n\n“Mrs Dombey,” said he, “it may not be inconsistent with my daughter’s improvement to know how very much to be lamented, and how necessary to be corrected, a stubborn disposition is, especially when it is indulged in—unthankfully indulged in, I will add—after the gratification of ambition and interest. Both of which, I believe, had some share in inducing you to occupy your present station at this board.”\n\n“No! I would not rise, and go away, and save you the utterance of one word,” she repeated, exactly as before, “if the room were burning.”\n\n“It may be natural enough, Mrs Dombey,” he pursued, “that you should be uneasy in the presence of any auditors of these disagreeable truths; though why”—he could not hide his real feeling here, or keep his eyes from glancing gloomily at Florence—“why anyone can give them greater force and point than myself, whom they so nearly concern, I do not pretend to understand. It may be natural enough that you should object to hear, in anybody’s presence, that there is a rebellious principle within you which you cannot curb too soon; which you must curb, Mrs Dombey; and which, I regret to say, I remember to have seen manifested—with some doubt and displeasure, on more than one occasion before our marriage—towards your deceased mother. But you have the remedy in your own hands. I by no means forgot, when I began, that my daughter was present, Mrs Dombey. I beg you will not forget, to-morrow, that there are several persons present; and that, with some regard to appearances, you will receive your company in a becoming manner.”\n\n“So it is not enough,” said Edith, “that you know what has passed between yourself and me; it is not enough that you can look here,” pointing at Carker, who still listened, with his eyes cast down, “and be reminded of the affronts you have put upon me; it is not enough that you can look here,” pointing to Florence with a hand that slightly trembled for the first and only time, “and think of what you have done, and of the ingenious agony, daily, hourly, constant, you have made me feel in doing it; it is not enough that this day, of all others in the year, is memorable to me for a struggle (well-deserved, but not conceivable by such as you) in which I wish I had died! You add to all this, do you, the last crowning meanness of making her a witness of the depth to which I have fallen; when you know that you have made me sacrifice to her peace, the only gentle feeling and interest of my life, when you know that for her sake, I would now if I could—but I can not, my soul recoils from you too much—submit myself wholly to your will, and be the meekest vassal that you have!”\n\nThis was not the way to minister to Mr Dombey’s greatness. The old feeling was roused by what she said, into a stronger and fiercer existence than it had ever had. Again, his neglected child, at this rough passage of his life, put forth by even this rebellious woman, as powerful where he was powerless, and everything where he was nothing!\n\nHe turned on Florence, as if it were she who had spoken, and bade her leave the room. Florence with her covered face obeyed, trembling and weeping as she went.\n\n“I understand, Madam,” said Mr Dombey, with an angry flush of triumph, “the spirit of opposition that turned your affections in that channel, but they have been met, Mrs Dombey; they have been met, and turned back!”\n\n“The worse for you!” she answered, with her voice and manner still unchanged. “Ay!” for he turned sharply when she said so, “what is the worse for me, is twenty million times the worse for you. Heed that, if you heed nothing else.”\n\nThe arch of diamonds spanning her dark hair, flashed and glittered like a starry bridge. There was no warning in them, or they would have turned as dull and dim as tarnished honour. Carker still sat and listened, with his eyes cast down.\n\n“Mrs Dombey,” said Mr Dombey, resuming as much as he could of his arrogant composure, “you will not conciliate me, or turn me from any purpose, by this course of conduct.”\n\n“It is the only true although it is a faint expression of what is within me,” she replied. “But if I thought it would conciliate you, I would repress it, if it were repressible by any human effort. I will do nothing that you ask.”\n\n“I am not accustomed to ask, Mrs Dombey,” he observed; “I direct.”\n\n“I will hold no place in your house to-morrow, or on any recurrence of to-morrow. I will be exhibited to no one, as the refractory slave you purchased, such a time. If I kept my marriage day, I would keep it as a day of shame. Self-respect! appearances before the world! what are these to me? You have done all you can to make them nothing to me, and they are nothing.”\n\n“Carker,” said Mr Dombey, speaking with knitted brows, and after a moment’s consideration, “Mrs Dombey is so forgetful of herself and me in all this, and places me in a position so unsuited to my character, that I must bring this state of matters to a close.”\n\n“Release me, then,” said Edith, immoveable in voice, in look, and bearing, as she had been throughout, “from the chain by which I am bound. Let me go.”\n\n“Madam?” exclaimed Mr Dombey.\n\n“Loose me. Set me free!”\n\n“Madam?” he repeated, “Mrs Dombey?”\n\n“Tell him,” said Edith, addressing her proud face to Carker, “that I wish for a separation between us. That there had better be one. That I recommend it to him. Tell him it may take place on his own terms—his wealth is nothing to me—but that it cannot be too soon.”\n\n“Good Heaven, Mrs Dombey!” said her husband, with supreme amazement, “do you imagine it possible that I could ever listen to such a proposition? Do you know who I am, Madam? Do you know what I represent? Did you ever hear of Dombey and Son? People to say that Mr Dombey—Mr Dombey!—was separated from his wife! Common people to talk of Mr Dombey and his domestic affairs! Do you seriously think, Mrs Dombey, that I would permit my name to be banded about in such connexion? Pooh, pooh, Madam! Fie for shame! You’re absurd.” Mr Dombey absolutely laughed.\n\nBut not as she did. She had better have been dead than laugh as she did, in reply, with her intent look fixed upon him. He had better have been dead, than sitting there, in his magnificence, to hear her.\n\n“No, Mrs Dombey,” he resumed. “No, Madam. There is no possibility of separation between you and me, and therefore I the more advise you to be awakened to a sense of duty. And, Carker, as I was about to say to you—”\n\nMr Carker, who had sat and listened all this time, now raised his eyes, in which there was a bright unusual light.\n\n“—As I was about to say to you,” resumed Mr Dombey, “I must beg you, now that matters have come to this, to inform Mrs Dombey, that it is not the rule of my life to allow myself to be thwarted by anybody—anybody, Carker—or to suffer anybody to be paraded as a stronger motive for obedience in those who owe obedience to me than I am my self. The mention that has been made of my daughter, and the use that is made of my daughter, in opposition to me, are unnatural. Whether my daughter is in actual concert with Mrs Dombey, I do not know, and do not care; but after what Mrs Dombey has said today, and my daughter has heard today, I beg you to make known to Mrs Dombey, that if she continues to make this house the scene of contention it has become, I shall consider my daughter responsible in some degree, on that lady’s own avowal, and shall visit her with my severe displeasure. Mrs Dombey has asked ‘whether it is not enough,’ that she had done this and that. You will please to answer no, it is not enough.”\n\n“A moment!” cried Carker, interposing, “permit me! painful as my position is, at the best, and unusually painful in seeming to entertain a different opinion from you,” addressing Mr Dombey, “I must ask, had you not better reconsider the question of a separation. I know how incompatible it appears with your high public position, and I know how determined you are when you give Mrs Dombey to understand”—the light in his eyes fell upon her as he separated his words each from each, with the distinctness of so many bells—“that nothing but death can ever part you. Nothing else. But when you consider that Mrs Dombey, by living in this house, and making it as you have said, a scene of contention, not only has her part in that contention, but compromises Miss Dombey every day (for I know how determined you are), will you not relieve her from a continual irritation of spirit, and a continual sense of being unjust to another, almost intolerable? Does this not seem like—I do not say it is—sacrificing Mrs Dombey to the preservation of your preeminent and unassailable position?”\n\nAgain the light in his eyes fell upon her, as she stood looking at her husband: now with an extraordinary and awful smile upon her face.\n\n“Carker,” returned Mr Dombey, with a supercilious frown, and in a tone that was intended to be final, “you mistake your position in offering advice to me on such a point, and you mistake me (I am surprised to find) in the character of your advice. I have no more to say.”\n\n“Perhaps,” said Carker, with an unusual and indefinable taunt in his air, “you mistook my position, when you honoured me with the negotiations in which I have been engaged here”—with a motion of his hand towards Mrs Dombey.\n\n“Not at all, Sir, not at all,” returned the other haughtily. “You were employed—”\n\n“Being an inferior person, for the humiliation of Mrs Dombey. I forgot. Oh, yes, it was expressly understood!” said Carker. “I beg your pardon!”\n\nAs he bent his head to Mr Dombey, with an air of deference that accorded ill with his words, though they were humbly spoken, he moved it round towards her, and kept his watching eyes that way.\n\nShe had better have turned hideous and dropped dead, than have stood up with such a smile upon her face, in such a fallen spirit’s majesty of scorn and beauty. She lifted her hand to the tiara of bright jewels radiant on her head, and, plucking it off with a force that dragged and strained her rich black hair with heedless cruelty, and brought it tumbling wildly on her shoulders, cast the gems upon the ground. From each arm, she unclasped a diamond bracelet, flung it down, and trod upon the glittering heap. Without a word, without a shadow on the fire of her bright eye, without abatement of her awful smile, she looked on Mr Dombey to the last, in moving to the door; and left him.\n\nFlorence had heard enough before quitting the room, to know that Edith loved her yet; that she had suffered for her sake; and that she had kept her sacrifices quiet, lest they should trouble her peace. She did not want to speak to her of this—she could not, remembering to whom she was opposed—but she wished, in one silent and affectionate embrace, to assure her that she felt it all, and thanked her.\n\nHer father went out alone, that evening, and Florence issuing from her own chamber soon afterwards, went about the house in search of Edith, but unavailingly. She was in her own rooms, where Florence had long ceased to go, and did not dare to venture now, lest she should unconsciously engender new trouble. Still Florence hoping to meet her before going to bed, changed from room to room, and wandered through the house so splendid and so dreary, without remaining anywhere.\n\nShe was crossing a gallery of communication that opened at some little distance on the staircase, and was only lighted on great occasions, when she saw, through the opening, which was an arch, the figure of a man coming down some few stairs opposite. Instinctively apprehensive of her father, whom she supposed it was, she stopped, in the dark, gazing through the arch into the light. But it was Mr Carker coming down alone, and looking over the railing into the hall. No bell was rung to announce his departure, and no servant was in attendance. He went down quietly, opened the door for himself, glided out, and shut it softly after him.\n\nHer invincible repugnance to this man, and perhaps the stealthy act of watching anyone, which, even under such innocent circumstances, is in a manner guilty and oppressive, made Florence shake from head to foot. Her blood seemed to run cold. As soon as she could—for at first she felt an insurmountable dread of moving—she went quickly to her own room and locked her door; but even then, shut in with her dog beside her, felt a chill sensation of horror, as if there were danger brooding somewhere near her.\n\nIt invaded her dreams and disturbed the whole night. Rising in the morning, unrefreshed, and with a heavy recollection of the domestic unhappiness of the preceding day, she sought Edith again in all the rooms, and did so, from time to time, all the morning. But she remained in her own chamber, and Florence saw nothing of her. Learning, however, that the projected dinner at home was put off, Florence thought it likely that she would go out in the evening to fulfil the engagement she had spoken of; and resolved to try and meet her, then, upon the staircase.\n\nWhen the evening had set in, she heard, from the room in which she sat on purpose, a footstep on the stairs that she thought to be Edith’s. Hurrying out, and up towards her room, Florence met her immediately, coming down alone.\n\nWhat was Florence’s affright and wonder when, at sight of her, with her tearful face, and outstretched arms, Edith recoiled and shrieked!\n\n“Don’t come near me!” she cried. “Keep away! Let me go by!”\n\n“Mama!” said Florence.\n\n“Don’t call me by that name! Don’t speak to me! Don’t look at me!—Florence!” shrinking back, as Florence moved a step towards her, “don’t touch me!”\n\nAs Florence stood transfixed before the haggard face and staring eyes, she noted, as in a dream, that Edith spread her hands over them, and shuddering through all her form, and crouching down against the wall, crawled by her like some lower animal, sprang up, and fled away.\n\nFlorence dropped upon the stairs in a swoon; and was found there by Mrs Pipchin, she supposed. She knew nothing more, until she found herself lying on her own bed, with Mrs Pipchin and some servants standing round her.\n\n“Where is Mama?” was her first question.\n\n“Gone out to dinner,” said Mrs Pipchin.\n\n“And Papa?”\n\n“Mr Dombey is in his own room, Miss Dombey,” said Mrs Pipchin, “and the best thing you can do, is to take off your things and go to bed this minute.” This was the sagacious woman’s remedy for all complaints, particularly lowness of spirits, and inability to sleep; for which offences, many young victims in the days of the Brighton Castle had been committed to bed at ten o’clock in the morning.\n\nWithout promising obedience, but on the plea of desiring to be very quiet, Florence disengaged herself, as soon as she could, from the ministration of Mrs Pipchin and her attendants. Left alone, she thought of what had happened on the staircase, at first in doubt of its reality; then with tears; then with an indescribable and terrible alarm, like that she had felt the night before.\n\nShe determined not to go to bed until Edith returned, and if she could not speak to her, at least to be sure that she was safe at home. What indistinct and shadowy dread moved Florence to this resolution, she did not know, and did not dare to think. She only knew that until Edith came back, there was no repose for her aching head or throbbing heart.\n\nThe evening deepened into night; midnight came; no Edith.\n\nFlorence could not read, or rest a moment. She paced her own room, opened the door and paced the staircase-gallery outside, looked out of window on the night, listened to the wind blowing and the rain falling, sat down and watched the faces in the fire, got up and watched the moon flying like a storm-driven ship through the sea of clouds.\n\nAll the house was gone to bed, except two servants who were waiting the return of their mistress, downstairs.\n\nOne o’clock. The carriages that rumbled in the distance, turned away, or stopped short, or went past; the silence gradually deepened, and was more and more rarely broken, save by a rush of wind or sweep of rain. Two o’clock. No Edith!\n\nFlorence, more agitated, paced her room; and paced the gallery outside; and looked out at the night, blurred and wavy with the raindrops on the glass, and the tears in her own eyes; and looked up at the hurry in the sky, so different from the repose below, and yet so tranquil and solitary. Three o’clock! There was a terror in every ash that dropped out of the fire. No Edith yet.\n\nMore and more agitated, Florence paced her room, and paced the gallery, and looked out at the moon with a new fancy of her likeness to a pale fugitive hurrying away and hiding her guilty face. Four struck! Five! No Edith yet.\n\nBut now there was some cautious stir in the house; and Florence found that Mrs Pipchin had been awakened by one of those who sat up, had risen and had gone down to her father’s door. Stealing lower down the stairs, and observing what passed, she saw her father come out in his morning gown, and start when he was told his wife had not come home. He dispatched a messenger to the stables to inquire whether the coachman was there; and while the man was gone, dressed himself very hurriedly.\n\nThe man came back, in great haste, bringing the coachman with him, who said he had been at home and in bed, since ten o’clock. He had driven his mistress to her old house in Brook Street, where she had been met by Mr Carker—\n\nFlorence stood upon the very spot where she had seen him coming down. Again she shivered with the nameless terror of that sight, and had hardly steadiness enough to hear and understand what followed.\n\n—Who had told him, the man went on to say, that his mistress would not want the carriage to go home in; and had dismissed him.\n\nShe saw her father turn white in the face, and heard him ask in a quick, trembling voice, for Mrs Dombey’s maid. The whole house was roused; for she was there, in a moment, very pale too, and speaking incoherently.\n\nShe said she had dressed her mistress early—full two hours before she went out—and had been told, as she often was, that she would not be wanted at night. She had just come from her mistress’s rooms, but—\n\n“But what! what was it?” Florence heard her father demand like a madman.\n\n“But the inner dressing-room was locked and the key gone.”\n\nHer father seized a candle that was flaming on the ground—someone had put it down there, and forgotten it—and came running upstairs with such fury, that Florence, in her fear, had hardly time to fly before him. She heard him striking in the door, as she ran on, with her hands widely spread, and her hair streaming, and her face like a distracted person’s, back to her own room.\n\nWhen the door yielded, and he rushed in, what did he see there? No one knew. But thrown down in a costly mass upon the ground, was every ornament she had had, since she had been his wife; every dress she had worn; and everything she had possessed. This was the room in which he had seen, in yonder mirror, the proud face discard him. This was the room in which he had wondered, idly, how these things would look when he should see them next!\n\nHeaping them back into the drawers, and locking them up in a rage of haste, he saw some papers on the table. The deed of settlement he had executed on their marriage, and a letter. He read that she was gone. He read that he was dishonoured. He read that she had fled, upon her shameful wedding-day, with the man whom he had chosen for her humiliation; and he tore out of the room, and out of the house, with a frantic idea of finding her yet, at the place to which she had been taken, and beating all trace of beauty out of the triumphant face with his bare hand.\n\nFlorence, not knowing what she did, put on a shawl and bonnet, in a dream of running through the streets until she found Edith, and then clasping her in her arms, to save and bring her back. But when she hurried out upon the staircase, and saw the frightened servants going up and down with lights, and whispering together, and falling away from her father as he passed down, she awoke to a sense of her own powerlessness; and hiding in one of the great rooms that had been made gorgeous for this, felt as if her heart would burst with grief.\n\nCompassion for her father was the first distinct emotion that made head against the flood of sorrow which overwhelmed her. Her constant nature turned to him in his distress, as fervently and faithfully, as if, in his prosperity, he had been the embodiment of that idea which had gradually become so faint and dim. Although she did not know, otherwise than through the suggestions of a shapeless fear, the full extent of his calamity, he stood before her, wronged and deserted; and again her yearning love impelled her to his side.\n\nHe was not long away; for Florence was yet weeping in the great room and nourishing these thoughts, when she heard him come back. He ordered the servants to set about their ordinary occupations, and went into his own apartment, where he trod so heavily that she could hear him walking up and down from end to end.\n\nYielding at once to the impulse of her affection, timid at all other times, but bold in its truth to him in his adversity, and undaunted by past repulse, Florence, dressed as she was, hurried downstairs. As she set her light foot in the hall, he came out of his room. She hastened towards him unchecked, with her arms stretched out, and crying “Oh dear, dear Papa!” as if she would have clasped him round the neck.\n\nAnd so she would have done. But in his frenzy, he lifted up his cruel arm, and struck her, crosswise, with that heaviness, that she tottered on the marble floor; and as he dealt the blow, he told her what Edith was, and bade her follow her, since they had always been in league.\n\nShe did not sink down at his feet; she did not shut out the sight of him with her trembling hands; she did not weep; she did not utter one word of reproach. But she looked at him, and a cry of desolation issued from her heart. For as she looked, she saw him murdering that fond idea to which she had held in spite of him. She saw his cruelty, neglect, and hatred dominant above it, and stamping it down. She saw she had no father upon earth, and ran out, orphaned, from his house.\n\nRan out of his house. A moment, and her hand was on the lock, the cry was on her lips, his face was there, made paler by the yellow candles hastily put down and guttering away, and by the daylight coming in above the door. Another moment, and the close darkness of the shut-up house (forgotten to be opened, though it was long since day) yielded to the unexpected glare and freedom of the morning; and Florence, with her head bent down to hide her agony of tears, was in the streets.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII. The Flight of Florence\n\nIn the wildness of her sorrow, shame, and terror, the forlorn girl hurried through the sunshine of a bright morning, as if it were the darkness of a winter night. Wringing her hands and weeping bitterly, insensible to everything but the deep wound in her breast, stunned by the loss of all she loved, left like the sole survivor on a lonely shore from the wreck of a great vessel, she fled without a thought, without a hope, without a purpose, but to fly somewhere anywhere.\n\nThe cheerful vista of the long street, burnished by the morning light, the sight of the blue sky and airy clouds, the vigorous freshness of the day, so flushed and rosy in its conquest of the night, awakened no responsive feelings in her so hurt bosom. Somewhere, anywhere, to hide her head! somewhere, anywhere, for refuge, never more to look upon the place from which she fled!\n\nBut there were people going to and fro; there were opening shops, and servants at the doors of houses; there was the rising clash and roar of the day’s struggle. Florence saw surprise and curiosity in the faces flitting past her; saw long shadows coming back upon the pavement; and heard voices that were strange to her asking her where she went, and what the matter was; and though these frightened her the more at first, and made her hurry on the faster, they did her the good service of recalling her in some degree to herself, and reminding her of the necessity of greater composure.\n\nWhere to go? Still somewhere, anywhere! still going on; but where! She thought of the only other time she had been lost in the wild wilderness of London—though not lost as now—and went that way. To the home of Walter’s Uncle.\n\nChecking her sobs, and drying her swollen eyes, and endeavouring to calm the agitation of her manner, so as to avoid attracting notice, Florence, resolving to keep to the more quiet streets as long as she could, was going on more quietly herself, when a familiar little shadow darted past upon the sunny pavement, stopped short, wheeled about, came close to her, made off again, bounded round and round her, and Diogenes, panting for breath, and yet making the street ring with his glad bark, was at her feet.\n\n“Oh, Di! oh, dear, true, faithful Di, how did you come here? How could I ever leave you, Di, who would never leave me?”\n\nFlorence bent down on the pavement, and laid his rough, old, loving, foolish head against her breast, and they got up together, and went on together; Di more off the ground than on it, endeavouring to kiss his mistress flying, tumbling over and getting up again without the least concern, dashing at big dogs in a jocose defiance of his species, terrifying with touches of his nose young housemaids who were cleaning doorsteps, and continually stopping, in the midst of a thousand extravagances, to look back at Florence, and bark until all the dogs within hearing answered, and all the dogs who could come out, came out to stare at him.\n\nWith this last adherent, Florence hurried away in the advancing morning, and the strengthening sunshine, to the City. The roar soon grew more loud, the passengers more numerous, the shops more busy, until she was carried onward in a stream of life setting that way, and flowing, indifferently, past marts and mansions, prisons, churches, market-places, wealth, poverty, good, and evil, like the broad river side by side with it, awakened from its dreams of rushes, willows, and green moss, and rolling on, turbid and troubled, among the works and cares of men, to the deep sea.\n\nAt length the quarters of the little Midshipman arose in view. Nearer yet, and the little Midshipman himself was seen upon his post, intent as ever on his observations. Nearer yet, and the door stood open, inviting her to enter. Florence, who had again quickened her pace, as she approached the end of her journey, ran across the road (closely followed by Diogenes, whom the bustle had somewhat confused), ran in, and sank upon the threshold of the well-remembered little parlour.\n\nThe Captain, in his glazed hat, was standing over the fire, making his morning’s cocoa, with that elegant trifle, his watch, upon the chimney-piece, for easy reference during the progress of the cookery. Hearing a footstep and the rustle of a dress, the Captain turned with a palpitating remembrance of the dreadful Mrs MacStinger, at the instant when Florence made a motion with her hand towards him, reeled, and fell upon the floor.\n\nThe Captain, pale as Florence, pale in the very knobs upon his face, raised her like a baby, and laid her on the same old sofa upon which she had slumbered long ago.\n\n“It’s Heart’s Delight!” said the Captain, looking intently in her face. “It’s the sweet creetur grow’d a woman!”\n\nCaptain Cuttle was so respectful of her, and had such a reverence for her, in this new character, that he would not have held her in his arms, while she was unconscious, for a thousand pounds.\n\n“My Heart’s Delight!” said the Captain, withdrawing to a little distance, with the greatest alarm and sympathy depicted on his countenance. “If you can hail Ned Cuttle with a finger, do it!”\n\nBut Florence did not stir.\n\n“My Heart’s Delight!” said the trembling Captain. “For the sake of Wal”r drownded in the briny deep, turn to, and histe up something or another, if able!”\n\nFinding her insensible to this impressive adjuration also, Captain Cuttle snatched from his breakfast-table a basin of cold water, and sprinkled some upon her face. Yielding to the urgency of the case, the Captain then, using his immense hand with extraordinary gentleness, relieved her of her bonnet, moistened her lips and forehead, put back her hair, covered her feet with his own coat which he pulled off for the purpose, patted her hand—so small in his, that he was struck with wonder when he touched it—and seeing that her eyelids quivered, and that her lips began to move, continued these restorative applications with a better heart.\n\n“Cheerily,” said the Captain. “Cheerily! Stand by, my pretty one, stand by! There! You’re better now. Steady’s the word, and steady it is. Keep her so! Drink a little drop o’ this here,” said the Captain. “There you are! What cheer now, my pretty, what cheer now?”\n\nAt this stage of her recovery, Captain Cuttle, with an imperfect association of a Watch with a Physician’s treatment of a patient, took his own down from the mantel-shelf, and holding it out on his hook, and taking Florence’s hand in his, looked steadily from one to the other, as expecting the dial to do something.\n\n“What cheer, my pretty?” said the Captain. “What cheer now? You’ve done her some good, my lad, I believe,” said the Captain, under his breath, and throwing an approving glance upon his watch. “Put you back half-an-hour every morning, and about another quarter towards the arternoon, and you’re a watch as can be ekalled by few and excelled by none. What cheer, my lady lass!”\n\n“Captain Cuttle! Is it you?” exclaimed Florence, raising herself a little.\n\n“Yes, yes, my lady lass,” said the Captain, hastily deciding in his own mind upon the superior elegance of that form of address, as the most courtly he could think of.\n\n“Is Walter’s Uncle here?” asked Florence.\n\n“Here, pretty?” returned the Captain. “He ain’t been here this many a long day. He ain’t been heerd on, since he sheered off arter poor Wal”r. But,” said the Captain, as a quotation, “Though lost to sight, to memory dear, and England, Home, and Beauty!”\n\n“Do you live here?” asked Florence.\n\n“Yes, my lady lass,” returned the Captain.\n\n“Oh, Captain Cuttle!” cried Florence, putting her hands together, and speaking wildly. “Save me! keep me here! Let no one know where I am! I’ll tell you what has happened by-and-by, when I can. I have no one in the world to go to. Do not send me away!”\n\n“Send you away, my lady lass!” exclaimed the Captain. “You, my Heart’s Delight! Stay a bit! We’ll put up this here deadlight, and take a double turn on the key!”\n\nWith these words, the Captain, using his one hand and his hook with the greatest dexterity, got out the shutter of the door, put it up, made it all fast, and locked the door itself.\n\nWhen he came back to the side of Florence, she took his hand, and kissed it. The helplessness of the action, the appeal it made to him, the confidence it expressed, the unspeakable sorrow in her face, the pain of mind she had too plainly suffered, and was suffering then, his knowledge of her past history, her present lonely, worn, and unprotected appearance, all so rushed upon the good Captain together, that he fairly overflowed with compassion and gentleness.\n\n“My lady lass,” said the Captain, polishing the bridge of his nose with his arm until it shone like burnished copper, “don’t you say a word to Ed’ard Cuttle, until such times as you finds yourself a riding smooth and easy; which won’t be today, nor yet to-morrow. And as to giving of you up, or reporting where you are, yes verily, and by God’s help, so I won’t, Church catechism, make a note on!”\n\nThis the Captain said, reference and all, in one breath, and with much solemnity; taking off his hat at “yes verily,” and putting it on again, when he had quite concluded.\n\nFlorence could do but one thing more to thank him, and to show him how she trusted in him; and she did it. Clinging to this rough creature as the last asylum of her bleeding heart, she laid her head upon his honest shoulder, and clasped him round his neck, and would have kneeled down to bless him, but that he divined her purpose, and held her up like a true man.\n\n“Steady!” said the Captain. “Steady! You’re too weak to stand, you see, my pretty, and must lie down here again. There, there!” To see the Captain lift her on the sofa, and cover her with his coat, would have been worth a hundred state sights. “And now,” said the Captain, “you must take some breakfast, lady lass, and the dog shall have some too. And arter that you shall go aloft to old Sol Gills’s room, and fall asleep there, like a angel.”\n\nCaptain Cuttle patted Diogenes when he made allusion to him, and Diogenes met that overture graciously, half-way. During the administration of the restoratives he had clearly been in two minds whether to fly at the Captain or to offer him his friendship; and he had expressed that conflict of feeling by alternate waggings of his tail, and displays of his teeth, with now and then a growl or so. But by this time, his doubts were all removed. It was plain that he considered the Captain one of the most amiable of men, and a man whom it was an honour to a dog to know.\n\nIn evidence of these convictions, Diogenes attended on the Captain while he made some tea and toast, and showed a lively interest in his housekeeping. But it was in vain for the kind Captain to make such preparations for Florence, who sorely tried to do some honour to them, but could touch nothing, and could only weep and weep again.\n\n“Well, well!” said the compassionate Captain, “arter turning in, my Heart’s Delight, you’ll get more way upon you. Now, I’ll serve out your allowance, my lad.” To Diogenes. “And you shall keep guard on your mistress aloft.”\n\nDiogenes, however, although he had been eyeing his intended breakfast with a watering mouth and glistening eyes, instead of falling to, ravenously, when it was put before him, pricked up his ears, darted to the shop-door, and barked there furiously: burrowing with his head at the bottom, as if he were bent on mining his way out.\n\n“Can there be anybody there!” asked Florence, in alarm.\n\n“No, my lady lass,” returned the Captain. “Who’d stay there, without making any noise! Keep up a good heart, pretty. It’s only people going by.”\n\nBut for all that, Diogenes barked and barked, and burrowed and burrowed, with pertinacious fury; and whenever he stopped to listen, appeared to receive some new conviction into his mind, for he set to, barking and burrowing again, a dozen times. Even when he was persuaded to return to his breakfast, he came jogging back to it, with a very doubtful air; and was off again, in another paroxysm, before touching a morsel.\n\n“If there should be someone listening and watching,” whispered Florence. “Someone who saw me come—who followed me, perhaps.”\n\n“It ain’t the young woman, lady lass, is it?” said the Captain, taken with a bright idea.\n\n“Susan?” said Florence, shaking her head. “Ah no! Susan has been gone from me a long time.”\n\n“Not deserted, I hope?” said the Captain. “Don’t say that that there young woman’s run, my pretty!”\n\n“Oh, no, no!” cried Florence. “She is one of the truest hearts in the world!”\n\nThe Captain was greatly relieved by this reply, and expressed his satisfaction by taking off his hard glazed hat, and dabbing his head all over with his handkerchief, rolled up like a ball, observing several times, with infinite complacency, and with a beaming countenance, that he know’d it.\n\n“So you’re quiet now, are you, brother?” said the Captain to Diogenes. “There warn’t nobody there, my lady lass, bless you!”\n\nDiogenes was not so sure of that. The door still had an attraction for him at intervals; and he went snuffing about it, and growling to himself, unable to forget the subject. This incident, coupled with the Captain’s observation of Florence’s fatigue and faintness, decided him to prepare Sol Gills’s chamber as a place of retirement for her immediately. He therefore hastily betook himself to the top of the house, and made the best arrangement of it that his imagination and his means suggested.\n\nIt was very clean already; and the Captain, being an orderly man, and accustomed to make things ship-shape, converted the bed into a couch, by covering it all over with a clean white drapery. By a similar contrivance, the Captain converted the little dressing-table into a species of altar, on which he set forth two silver teaspoons, a flower-pot, a telescope, his celebrated watch, a pocket-comb, and a song-book, as a small collection of rarities, that made a choice appearance. Having darkened the window, and straightened the pieces of carpet on the floor, the Captain surveyed these preparations with great delight, and descended to the little parlour again, to bring Florence to her bower.\n\nNothing would induce the Captain to believe that it was possible for Florence to walk upstairs. If he could have got the idea into his head, he would have considered it an outrageous breach of hospitality to allow her to do so. Florence was too weak to dispute the point, and the Captain carried her up out of hand, laid her down, and covered her with a great watch-coat.\n\n“My lady lass!” said the Captain, “you’re as safe here as if you was at the top of St Paul’s Cathedral, with the ladder cast off. Sleep is what you want, afore all other things, and may you be able to show yourself smart with that there balsam for the still small woice of a wounded mind! When there’s anything you want, my Heart’s Delight, as this here humble house or town can offer, pass the word to Ed’ard Cuttle, as’ll stand off and on outside that door, and that there man will wibrate with joy.” The Captain concluded by kissing the hand that Florence stretched out to him, with the chivalry of any old knight-errant, and walking on tiptoe out of the room.\n\nDescending to the little parlour, Captain Cuttle, after holding a hasty council with himself, decided to open the shop-door for a few minutes, and satisfy himself that now, at all events, there was no one loitering about it. Accordingly he set it open, and stood upon the threshold, keeping a bright look-out, and sweeping the whole street with his spectacles.\n\n“How de do, Captain Gills?” said a voice beside him. The Captain, looking down, found that he had been boarded by Mr Toots while sweeping the horizon.\n\n“How are, you, my lad?” replied the Captain.\n\n“Well, I’m pretty well, thank’ee, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots. “You know I’m never quite what I could wish to be, now. I don’t expect that I ever shall be any more.”\n\nMr Toots never approached any nearer than this to the great theme of his life, when in conversation with Captain Cuttle, on account of the agreement between them.\n\n“Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, “if I could have the pleasure of a word with you, it’s—it’s rather particular.”\n\n“Why, you see, my lad,” replied the Captain, leading the way into the parlour, “I ain’t what you may call exactly free this morning; and therefore if you can clap on a bit, I should take it kindly.”\n\n“Certainly, Captain Gills,” replied Mr Toots, who seldom had any notion of the Captain’s meaning. “To clap on, is exactly what I could wish to do. Naturally.”\n\n“If so be, my lad,” returned the Captain. “Do it!”\n\nThe Captain was so impressed by the possession of his tremendous secret—by the fact of Miss Dombey being at that moment under his roof, while the innocent and unconscious Toots sat opposite to him—that a perspiration broke out on his forehead, and he found it impossible, while slowly drying the same, glazed hat in hand, to keep his eyes off Mr Toots’s face. Mr Toots, who himself appeared to have some secret reasons for being in a nervous state, was so unspeakably disconcerted by the Captain’s stare, that after looking at him vacantly for some time in silence, and shifting uneasily on his chair, he said:\n\n“I beg your pardon, Captain Gills, but you don’t happen to see anything particular in me, do you?”\n\n“No, my lad,” returned the Captain. “No.”\n\n“Because you know,” said Mr Toots with a chuckle, “I know I’m wasting away. You needn’t at all mind alluding to that. I—I should like it. Burgess and Co. have altered my measure, I’m in that state of thinness. It’s a gratification to me. I—I’m glad of it. I—I’d a great deal rather go into a decline, if I could. I’m a mere brute you know, grazing upon the surface of the earth, Captain Gills.”\n\nThe more Mr Toots went on in this way, the more the Captain was weighed down by his secret, and stared at him. What with this cause of uneasiness, and his desire to get rid of Mr Toots, the Captain was in such a scared and strange condition, indeed, that if he had been in conversation with a ghost, he could hardly have evinced greater discomposure.\n\n“But I was going to say, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots. “Happening to be this way early this morning—to tell you the truth, I was coming to breakfast with you. As to sleep, you know, I never sleep now. I might be a Watchman, except that I don’t get any pay, and he’s got nothing on his mind.”\n\n“Carry on, my lad!” said the Captain, in an admonitory voice.\n\n“Certainly, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots. “Perfectly true! Happening to be this way early this morning (an hour or so ago), and finding the door shut—”\n\n“What! were you waiting there, brother?” demanded the Captain.\n\n“Not at all, Captain Gills,” returned Mr Toots. “I didn’t stop a moment. I thought you were out. But the person said—by the bye, you don’t keep a dog, you, Captain Gills?”\n\nThe Captain shook his head.\n\n“To be sure,” said Mr Toots, “that’s exactly what I said. I knew you didn’t. There is a dog, Captain Gills, connected with—but excuse me. That’s forbidden ground.”\n\nThe Captain stared at Mr Toots until he seemed to swell to twice his natural size; and again the perspiration broke out on the Captain’s forehead, when he thought of Diogenes taking it into his head to come down and make a third in the parlour.\n\n“The person said,” continued Mr Toots, “that he had heard a dog barking in the shop: which I knew couldn’t be, and I told him so. But he was as positive as if he had seen the dog.”\n\n“What person, my lad?” inquired the Captain.\n\n“Why, you see there it is, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, with a perceptible increase in the nervousness of his manner. “It’s not for me to say what may have taken place, or what may not have taken place. Indeed, I don’t know. I get mixed up with all sorts of things that I don’t quite understand, and I think there’s something rather weak in my—in my head, in short.”\n\nThe Captain nodded his own, as a mark of assent.\n\n“But the person said, as we were walking away,” continued Mr Toots, “that you knew what, under existing circumstances, might occur—he said ‘might,’ very strongly—and that if you were requested to prepare yourself, you would, no doubt, come prepared.”\n\n“Person, my lad” the Captain repeated.\n\n“I don’t know what person, I’m sure, Captain Gills,” replied Mr Toots, “I haven’t the least idea. But coming to the door, I found him waiting there; and he said was I coming back again, and I said yes; and he said did I know you, and I said, yes, I had the pleasure of your acquaintance—you had given me the pleasure of your acquaintance, after some persuasion; and he said, if that was the case, would I say to you what I have said, about existing circumstances and coming prepared, and as soon as ever I saw you, would I ask you to step round the corner, if it was only for one minute, on most important business, to Mr Brogley’s the Broker’s. Now, I tell you what, Captain Gills—whatever it is, I am convinced it’s very important; and if you like to step round, now, I’ll wait here till you come back.”\n\nThe Captain, divided between his fear of compromising Florence in some way by not going, and his horror of leaving Mr Toots in possession of the house with a chance of finding out the secret, was a spectacle of mental disturbance that even Mr Toots could not be blind to. But that young gentleman, considering his nautical friend as merely in a state of preparation for the interview he was going to have, was quite satisfied, and did not review his own discreet conduct without chuckle.\n\nAt length the Captain decided, as the lesser of two evils, to run round to Brogley’s the Broker’s: previously locking the door that communicated with the upper part of the house, and putting the key in his pocket. “If so be,” said the Captain to Mr Toots, with not a little shame and hesitation, “as you’ll excuse my doing of it, brother.”\n\n“Captain Gills,” returned Mr Toots, “whatever you do, is satisfactory to me.”\n\nThe Captain thanked him heartily, and promising to come back in less than five minutes, went out in quest of the person who had entrusted Mr Toots with this mysterious message. Poor Mr Toots, left to himself, lay down upon the sofa, little thinking who had reclined there last, and, gazing up at the skylight and resigning himself to visions of Miss Dombey, lost all heed of time and place.\n\nIt was as well that he did so; for although the Captain was not gone long, he was gone much longer than he had proposed. When he came back, he was very pale indeed, and greatly agitated, and even looked as if he had been shedding tears. He seemed to have lost the faculty of speech, until he had been to the cupboard and taken a dram of rum from the case-bottle, when he fetched a deep breath, and sat down in a chair with his hand before his face.\n\n“Captain Gills,” said Toots, kindly, “I hope and trust there’s nothing wrong?”\n\n“Thank’ee, my lad, not a bit,” said the Captain. “Quite contrairy.”\n\n“You have the appearance of being overcome, Captain Gills,” observed Mr Toots.\n\n“Why, my lad, I am took aback,” the Captain admitted. “I am.”\n\n“Is there anything I can do, Captain Gills?” inquired Mr Toots. “If there is, make use of me.”\n\nThe Captain removed his hand from his face, looked at him with a remarkable expression of pity and tenderness, and took him by the hand, and shook it hard.\n\n“No, thank’ee,” said the Captain. “Nothing. Only I’ll take it as a favour if you’ll part company for the present. I believe, brother,” wringing his hand again, “that, after Wal”r, and on a different model, you’re as good a lad as ever stepped.”\n\n“Upon my word and honour, Captain Gills,” returned Mr Toots, giving the Captain’s hand a preliminary slap before shaking it again, “it’s delightful to me to possess your good opinion. Thank’ee.”\n\n“And bear a hand and cheer up,” said the Captain, patting him on the back. “What! There’s more than one sweet creetur in the world!”\n\n“Not to me, Captain Gills,” replied Mr Toots gravely. “Not to me, I assure you. The state of my feelings towards Miss Dombey is of that unspeakable description, that my heart is a desert island, and she lives in it alone. I’m getting more used up every day, and I’m proud to be so. If you could see my legs when I take my boots off, you’d form some idea of what unrequited affection is. I have been prescribed bark, but I don’t take it, for I don’t wish to have any tone whatever given to my constitution. I’d rather not. This, however, is forbidden ground. Captain Gills, goodbye!”\n\nCaptain Cuttle cordially reciprocating the warmth of Mr Toots’s farewell, locked the door behind him, and shaking his head with the same remarkable expression of pity and tenderness as he had regarded him with before, went up to see if Florence wanted him.\n\nThere was an entire change in the Captain’s face as he went upstairs. He wiped his eyes with his handkerchief, and he polished the bridge of his nose with his sleeve as he had done already that morning, but his face was absolutely changed. Now, he might have been thought supremely happy; now, he might have been thought sad; but the kind of gravity that sat upon his features was quite new to them, and was as great an improvement to them as if they had undergone some sublimating process.\n\nHe knocked softly, with his hook, at Florence’s door, twice or thrice; but, receiving no answer, ventured first to peep in, and then to enter: emboldened to take the latter step, perhaps, by the familiar recognition of Diogenes, who, stretched upon the ground by the side of her couch, wagged his tail, and winked his eyes at the Captain, without being at the trouble of getting up.\n\nShe was sleeping heavily, and moaning in her sleep; and Captain Cuttle, with a perfect awe of her youth, and beauty, and her sorrow, raised her head, and adjusted the coat that covered her, where it had fallen off, and darkened the window a little more that she might sleep on, and crept out again, and took his post of watch upon the stairs. All this, with a touch and tread as light as Florence’s own.\n\nLong may it remain in this mixed world a point not easy of decision, which is the more beautiful evidence of the Almighty’s goodness—the delicate fingers that are formed for sensitiveness and sympathy of touch, and made to minister to pain and grief, or the rough hard Captain Cuttle hand, that the heart teaches, guides, and softens in a moment!\n\nFlorence slept upon her couch, forgetful of her homelessness and orphanage, and Captain Cuttle watched upon the stairs. A louder sob or moan than usual, brought him sometimes to her door; but by degrees she slept more peacefully, and the Captain’s watch was undisturbed.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIX. The Midshipman makes a Discovery\n\nIt was long before Florence awoke. The day was in its prime, the day was in its wane, and still, uneasy in mind and body, she slept on; unconscious of her strange bed, of the noise and turmoil in the street, and of the light that shone outside the shaded window. Perfect unconsciousness of what had happened in the home that existed no more, even the deep slumber of exhaustion could not produce. Some undefined and mournful recollection of it, dozing uneasily but never sleeping, pervaded all her rest. A dull sorrow, like a half-lulled sense of pain, was always present to her; and her pale cheek was oftener wet with tears than the honest Captain, softly putting in his head from time to time at the half-closed door, could have desired to see it.\n\nThe sun was getting low in the west, and, glancing out of a red mist, pierced with its rays opposite loopholes and pieces of fretwork in the spires of city churches, as if with golden arrows that struck through and through them—and far away athwart the river and its flat banks, it was gleaming like a path of fire—and out at sea it was irradiating sails of ships—and, looked towards, from quiet churchyards, upon hill-tops in the country, it was steeping distant prospects in a flush and glow that seemed to mingle earth and sky together in one glorious suffusion—when Florence, opening her heavy eyes, lay at first, looking without interest or recognition at the unfamiliar walls around her, and listening in the same regardless manner to the noises in the street. But presently she started up upon her couch, gazed round with a surprised and vacant look, and recollected all.\n\n“My pretty,” said the Captain, knocking at the door, “what cheer?”\n\n“Dear friend,” cried Florence, hurrying to him, “is it you?”\n\nThe Captain felt so much pride in the name, and was so pleased by the gleam of pleasure in her face, when she saw him, that he kissed his hook, by way of reply, in speechless gratification.\n\n“What cheer, bright di’mond?” said the Captain.\n\n“I have surely slept very long,” returned Florence. “When did I come here? Yesterday?”\n\n“This here blessed day, my lady lass,” replied the Captain.\n\n“Has there been no night? Is it still day?” asked Florence.\n\n“Getting on for evening now, my pretty,” said the Captain, drawing back the curtain of the window. “See!”\n\nFlorence, with her hand upon the Captain’s arm, so sorrowful and timid, and the Captain with his rough face and burly figure, so quietly protective of her, stood in the rosy light of the bright evening sky, without saying a word. However strange the form of speech into which he might have fashioned the feeling, if he had had to give it utterance, the Captain felt, as sensibly as the most eloquent of men could have done, that there was something in the tranquil time and in its softened beauty that would make the wounded heart of Florence overflow; and that it was better that such tears should have their way. So not a word spake Captain Cuttle. But when he felt his arm clasped closer, and when he felt the lonely head come nearer to it, and lay itself against his homely coarse blue sleeve, he pressed it gently with his rugged hand, and understood it, and was understood.\n\n“Better now, my pretty!” said the Captain. “Cheerily, cheerily, I’ll go down below, and get some dinner ready. Will you come down of your own self, arterwards, pretty, or shall Ed’ard Cuttle come and fetch you?”\n\nAs Florence assured him that she was quite able to walk downstairs, the Captain, though evidently doubtful of his own hospitality in permitting it, left her to do so, and immediately set about roasting a fowl at the fire in the little parlour. To achieve his cookery with the greater skill, he pulled off his coat, tucked up his wristbands, and put on his glazed hat, without which assistant he never applied himself to any nice or difficult undertaking.\n\nAfter cooling her aching head and burning face in the fresh water which the Captain’s care had provided for her while she slept, Florence went to the little mirror to bind up her disordered hair. Then she knew—in a moment, for she shunned it instantly, that on her breast there was the darkening mark of an angry hand.\n\nHer tears burst forth afresh at the sight; she was ashamed and afraid of it; but it moved her to no anger against him. Homeless and fatherless, she forgave him everything; hardly thought that she had need to forgive him, or that she did; but she fled from the idea of him as she had fled from the reality, and he was utterly gone and lost. There was no such Being in the world.\n\nWhat to do, or where to live, Florence—poor, inexperienced girl!—could not yet consider. She had indistinct dreams of finding, a long way off, some little sisters to instruct, who would be gentle with her, and to whom, under some feigned name, she might attach herself, and who would grow up in their happy home, and marry, and be good to their old governess, and perhaps entrust her, in time, with the education of their own daughters. And she thought how strange and sorrowful it would be, thus to become a grey-haired woman, carrying her secret to the grave, when Florence Dombey was forgotten. But it was all dim and clouded to her now. She only knew that she had no Father upon earth, and she said so, many times, with her suppliant head hidden from all, but her Father who was in Heaven.\n\nHer little stock of money amounted to but a few guineas. With a part of this, it would be necessary to buy some clothes, for she had none but those she wore. She was too desolate to think how soon her money would be gone—too much a child in worldly matters to be greatly troubled on that score yet, even if her other trouble had been less. She tried to calm her thoughts and stay her tears; to quiet the hurry in her throbbing head, and bring herself to believe that what had happened were but the events of a few hours ago, instead of weeks or months, as they appeared; and went down to her kind protector.\n\nThe Captain had spread the cloth with great care, and was making some egg-sauce in a little saucepan: basting the fowl from time to time during the process with a strong interest, as it turned and browned on a string before the fire. Having propped Florence up with cushions on the sofa, which was already wheeled into a warm corner for her greater comfort, the Captain pursued his cooking with extraordinary skill, making hot gravy in a second little saucepan, boiling a handful of potatoes in a third, never forgetting the egg-sauce in the first, and making an impartial round of basting and stirring with the most useful of spoons every minute. Besides these cares, the Captain had to keep his eye on a diminutive frying-pan, in which some sausages were hissing and bubbling in a most musical manner; and there was never such a radiant cook as the Captain looked, in the height and heat of these functions: it being impossible to say whether his face or his glazed hat shone the brighter.\n\nThe dinner being at length quite ready, Captain Cuttle dished and served it up, with no less dexterity than he had cooked it. He then dressed for dinner, by taking off his glazed hat and putting on his coat. That done, he wheeled the table close against Florence on the sofa, said grace, unscrewed his hook, screwed his fork into its place, and did the honours of the table.\n\n“My lady lass,” said the Captain, “cheer up, and try to eat a deal. Stand by, my deary! Liver wing it is. Sarse it is. Sassage it is. And potato!” all which the Captain ranged symmetrically on a plate, and pouring hot gravy on the whole with the useful spoon, set before his cherished guest.\n\n“The whole row o’ dead lights is up, for’ard, lady lass,” observed the Captain, encouragingly, “and everythink is made snug. Try and pick a bit, my pretty. If Wal”r was here—”\n\n“Ah! If I had him for my brother now!” cried Florence.\n\n“Don’t! don’t take on, my pretty!” said the Captain, “awast, to obleege me! He was your nat’ral born friend like, warn’t he, Pet?”\n\nFlorence had no words to answer with. She only said, “Oh, dear, dear Paul! oh, Walter!”\n\n“The wery planks she walked on,” murmured the Captain, looking at her drooping face, “was as high esteemed by Wal”r, as the water brooks is by the hart which never rejices! I see him now, the wery day as he was rated on them Dombey books, a speaking of her with his face a glistening with doo—leastways with his modest sentiments—like a new blowed rose, at dinner. Well, well! If our poor Wal”r was here, my lady lass—or if he could be—for he’s drownded, ain’t he?”\n\nFlorence shook her head.\n\n“Yes, yes; drownded,” said the Captain, soothingly; “as I was saying, if he could be here he’d beg and pray of you, my precious, to pick a leetle bit, with a look-out for your own sweet health. Whereby, hold your own, my lady lass, as if it was for Wal”r’s sake, and lay your pretty head to the wind.”\n\nFlorence essayed to eat a morsel, for the Captain’s pleasure. The Captain, meanwhile, who seemed to have quite forgotten his own dinner, laid down his knife and fork, and drew his chair to the sofa.\n\n“Wal”r was a trim lad, warn’t he, precious?” said the Captain, after sitting for some time silently rubbing his chin, with his eyes fixed upon her, “and a brave lad, and a good lad?”\n\nFlorence tearfully assented.\n\n“And he’s drownded, Beauty, ain’t he?” said the Captain, in a soothing voice.\n\nFlorence could not but assent again.\n\n“He was older than you, my lady lass,” pursued the Captain, “but you was like two children together, at first; wam’t you?”\n\nFlorence answered “Yes.”\n\n“And Wal”r’s drownded,” said the Captain. “Ain’t he?”\n\nThe repetition of this inquiry was a curious source of consolation, but it seemed to be one to Captain Cuttle, for he came back to it again and again. Florence, fain to push from her her untasted dinner, and to lie back on her sofa, gave him her hand, feeling that she had disappointed him, though truly wishing to have pleased him after all his trouble, but he held it in his own (which shook as he held it), and appearing to have quite forgotten all about the dinner and her want of appetite, went on growling at intervals, in a ruminating tone of sympathy, “Poor Wal”r. Ay, ay! Drownded. Ain’t he?” And always waited for her answer, in which the great point of these singular reflections appeared to consist.\n\nThe fowl and sausages were cold, and the gravy and the egg-sauce stagnant, before the Captain remembered that they were on the board, and fell to with the assistance of Diogenes, whose united efforts quickly dispatched the banquet. The Captain’s delight and wonder at the quiet housewifery of Florence in assisting to clear the table, arrange the parlour, and sweep up the hearth—only to be equalled by the fervency of his protest when she began to assist him—were gradually raised to that degree, that at last he could not choose but do nothing himself, and stand looking at her as if she were some Fairy, daintily performing these offices for him; the red rim on his forehead glowing again, in his unspeakable admiration.\n\nBut when Florence, taking down his pipe from the mantel-shelf gave it into his hand, and entreated him to smoke it, the good Captain was so bewildered by her attention that he held it as if he had never held a pipe, in all his life. Likewise, when Florence, looking into the little cupboard, took out the case-bottle and mixed a perfect glass of grog for him, unasked, and set it at his elbow, his ruddy nose turned pale, he felt himself so graced and honoured. When he had filled his pipe in an absolute reverie of satisfaction, Florence lighted it for him—the Captain having no power to object, or to prevent her—and resuming her place on the old sofa, looked at him with a smile so loving and so grateful, a smile that showed him so plainly how her forlorn heart turned to him, as her face did, through grief, that the smoke of the pipe got into the Captain’s throat and made him cough, and got into the Captain’s eyes, and made them blink and water.\n\nThe manner in which the Captain tried to make believe that the cause of these effects lay hidden in the pipe itself, and the way in which he looked into the bowl for it, and not finding it there, pretended to blow it out of the stem, was wonderfully pleasant. The pipe soon getting into better condition, he fell into that state of repose becoming a good smoker; but sat with his eyes fixed on Florence, and, with a beaming placidity not to be described, and stopping every now and then to discharge a little cloud from his lips, slowly puffed it forth, as if it were a scroll coming out of his mouth, bearing the legend “Poor Wal”r, ay, ay. Drownded, ain’t he?” after which he would resume his smoking with infinite gentleness.\n\nUnlike as they were externally—and there could scarcely be a more decided contrast than between Florence in her delicate youth and beauty, and Captain Cuttle with his knobby face, his great broad weather-beaten person, and his gruff voice—in simple innocence of the world’s ways and the world’s perplexities and dangers, they were nearly on a level. No child could have surpassed Captain Cuttle in inexperience of everything but wind and weather; in simplicity, credulity, and generous trustfulness. Faith, hope, and charity, shared his whole nature among them. An odd sort of romance, perfectly unimaginative, yet perfectly unreal, and subject to no considerations of worldly prudence or practicability, was the only partner they had in his character. As the Captain sat, and smoked, and looked at Florence, God knows what impossible pictures, in which she was the principal figure, presented themselves to his mind. Equally vague and uncertain, though not so sanguine, were her own thoughts of the life before her; and even as her tears made prismatic colours in the light she gazed at, so, through her new and heavy grief, she already saw a rainbow faintly shining in the far-off sky. A wandering princess and a good monster in a storybook might have sat by the fireside, and talked as Captain Cuttle and poor Florence talked—and not have looked very much unlike them.\n\nThe Captain was not troubled with the faintest idea of any difficulty in retaining Florence, or of any responsibility thereby incurred. Having put up the shutters and locked the door, he was quite satisfied on this head. If she had been a Ward in Chancery, it would have made no difference at all to Captain Cuttle. He was the last man in the world to be troubled by any such considerations.\n\nSo the Captain smoked his pipe very comfortably, and Florence and he meditated after their own manner. When the pipe was out, they had some tea; and then Florence entreated him to take her to some neighbouring shop, where she could buy the few necessaries she immediately wanted. It being quite dark, the Captain consented: peeping carefully out first, as he had been wont to do in his time of hiding from Mrs MacStinger; and arming himself with his large stick, in case of an appeal to arms being rendered necessary by any unforeseen circumstance.\n\nThe pride Captain Cuttle had, in giving his arm to Florence, and escorting her some two or three hundred yards, keeping a bright look-out all the time, and attracting the attention of everyone who passed them, by his great vigilance and numerous precautions, was extreme. Arrived at the shop, the Captain felt it a point of delicacy to retire during the making of the purchases, as they were to consist of wearing apparel; but he previously deposited his tin canister on the counter, and informing the young lady of the establishment that it contained fourteen pound two, requested her, in case that amount of property should not be sufficient to defray the expenses of his niece’s little outfit—at the word “niece,” he bestowed a most significant look on Florence, accompanied with pantomime, expressive of sagacity and mystery—to have the goodness to “sing out,” and he would make up the difference from his pocket. Casually consulting his big watch, as a deep means of dazzling the establishment, and impressing it with a sense of property, the Captain then kissed his hook to his niece, and retired outside the window, where it was a choice sight to see his great face looking in from time to time, among the silks and ribbons, with an obvious misgiving that Florence had been spirited away by a back door.\n\n“Dear Captain Cuttle,” said Florence, when she came out with a parcel, the size of which greatly disappointed the Captain, who had expected to see a porter following with a bale of goods, “I don’t want this money, indeed. I have not spent any of it. I have money of my own.”\n\n“My lady lass,” returned the baffled Captain, looking straight down the street before them, “take care on it for me, will you be so good, till such time as I ask ye for it?”\n\n“May I put it back in its usual place,” said Florence, “and keep it there?”\n\nThe Captain was not at all gratified by this proposal, but he answered, “Ay, ay, put it anywheres, my lady lass, so long as you know where to find it again. It ain’t o’ no use to me,” said the Captain. “I wonder I haven’t chucked it away afore now.\n\nThe Captain was quite disheartened for the moment, but he revived at the first touch of Florence’s arm, and they returned with the same precautions as they had come; the Captain opening the door of the little Midshipman’s berth, and diving in, with a suddenness which his great practice only could have taught him. During Florence’s slumber in the morning, he had engaged the daughter of an elderly lady who usually sat under a blue umbrella in Leadenhall Market, selling poultry, to come and put her room in order, and render her any little services she required; and this damsel now appearing, Florence found everything about her as convenient and orderly, if not as handsome, as in the terrible dream she had once called Home.\n\nWhen they were alone again, the Captain insisted on her eating a slice of dry toast, and drinking a glass of spiced negus (which he made to perfection); and, encouraging her with every kind word and inconsequential quotation he could possibly think of, led her upstairs to her bedroom. But he too had something on his mind, and was not easy in his manner.\n\n“Good-night, dear heart,” said Captain Cuttle to her at her chamber-door.\n\nFlorence raised her lips to his face, and kissed him.\n\nAt any other time the Captain would have been overbalanced by such a token of her affection and gratitude; but now, although he was very sensible of it, he looked in her face with even more uneasiness than he had testified before, and seemed unwilling to leave her.\n\n“Poor Wal”r!” said the Captain.\n\n“Poor, poor Walter!” sighed Florence.\n\n“Drownded, ain’t he?” said the Captain.\n\nFlorence shook her head, and sighed.\n\n“Good-night, my lady lass!” said Captain Cuttle, putting out his hand.\n\n“God bless you, dear, kind friend!”\n\nBut the Captain lingered still.\n\n“Is anything the matter, dear Captain Cuttle?” said Florence, easily alarmed in her then state of mind. “Have you anything to tell me?”\n\n“To tell you, lady lass!” replied the Captain, meeting her eyes in confusion. “No, no; what should I have to tell you, pretty! You don’t expect as I’ve got anything good to tell you, sure?”\n\n“No!” said Florence, shaking her head.\n\nThe Captain looked at her wistfully, and repeated “No,”— still lingering, and still showing embarrassment.\n\n“Poor Wal”r!” said the Captain. “My Wal”r, as I used to call you! Old Sol Gills’s nevy! Welcome to all as knowed you, as the flowers in May! Where are you got to, brave boy? Drownded, ain’t he?”\n\nConcluding his apostrophe with this abrupt appeal to Florence, the Captain bade her good-night, and descended the stairs, while Florence remained at the top, holding the candle out to light him down. He was lost in the obscurity, and, judging from the sound of his receding footsteps, was in the act of turning into the little parlour, when his head and shoulders unexpectedly emerged again, as from the deep, apparently for no other purpose than to repeat, “Drownded, ain’t he, pretty?” For when he had said that in a tone of tender condolence, he disappeared.\n\nFlorence was very sorry that she should unwittingly, though naturally, have awakened these associations in the mind of her protector, by taking refuge there; and sitting down before the little table where the Captain had arranged the telescope and song-book, and those other rarities, thought of Walter, and of all that was connected with him in the past, until she could have almost wished to lie down on her bed and fade away. But in her lonely yearning to the dead whom she had loved, no thought of home—no possibility of going back—no presentation of it as yet existing, or as sheltering her father—once entered her thoughts. She had seen the murder done. In the last lingering natural aspect in which she had cherished him through so much, he had been torn out of her heart, defaced, and slain. The thought of it was so appalling to her, that she covered her eyes, and shrunk trembling from the least remembrance of the deed, or of the cruel hand that did it. If her fond heart could have held his image after that, it must have broken; but it could not; and the void was filled with a wild dread that fled from all confronting with its shattered fragments—with such a dread as could have risen out of nothing but the depths of such a love, so wronged.\n\nShe dared not look into the glass; for the sight of the darkening mark upon her bosom made her afraid of herself, as if she bore about her something wicked. She covered it up, with a hasty, faltering hand, and in the dark; and laid her weary head down, weeping.\n\nThe Captain did not go to bed for a long time. He walked to and fro in the shop and in the little parlour, for a full hour, and, appearing to have composed himself by that exercise, sat down with a grave and thoughtful face, and read out of a Prayer-book the forms of prayer appointed to be used at sea. These were not easily disposed of; the good Captain being a mighty slow, gruff reader, and frequently stopping at a hard word to give himself such encouragement as “Now, my lad! With a will!” or, “Steady, Ed’ard Cuttle, steady!” which had a great effect in helping him out of any difficulty. Moreover, his spectacles greatly interfered with his powers of vision. But notwithstanding these drawbacks, the Captain, being heartily in earnest, read the service to the very last line, and with genuine feeling too; and approving of it very much when he had done, turned in, under the counter (but not before he had been upstairs, and listened at Florence’s door), with a serene breast, and a most benevolent visage.\n\nThe Captain turned out several times in the course of the night, to assure himself that his charge was resting quietly; and once, at daybreak, found that she was awake: for she called to know if it were he, on hearing footsteps near her door.\n\n“Yes, my lady lass,” replied the Captain, in a growling whisper. “Are you all right, di’mond?”\n\nFlorence thanked him, and said “Yes.”\n\nThe Captain could not lose so favourable an opportunity of applying his mouth to the keyhole, and calling through it, like a hoarse breeze, “Poor Wal”r! Drownded, ain’t he?” after which he withdrew, and turning in again, slept till seven o’clock.\n\nNor was he free from his uneasy and embarrassed manner all that day; though Florence, being busy with her needle in the little parlour, was more calm and tranquil than she had been on the day preceding. Almost always when she raised her eyes from her work, she observed the captain looking at her, and thoughtfully stroking his chin; and he so often hitched his arm-chair close to her, as if he were going to say something very confidential, and hitched it away again, as not being able to make up his mind how to begin, that in the course of the day he cruised completely round the parlour in that frail bark, and more than once went ashore against the wainscot or the closet door, in a very distressed condition.\n\nIt was not until the twilight that Captain Cuttle, fairly dropping anchor, at last, by the side of Florence, began to talk at all connectedly. But when the light of the fire was shining on the walls and ceiling of the little room, and on the tea-board and the cups and saucers that were ranged upon the table, and on her calm face turned towards the flame, and reflecting it in the tears that filled her eyes, the Captain broke a long silence thus:\n\n“You never was at sea, my own?”\n“No,” replied Florence.\n\n“Ay,” said the Captain, reverentially; “it’s a almighty element. There’s wonders in the deep, my pretty. Think on it when the winds is roaring and the waves is rowling. Think on it when the stormy nights is so pitch dark,” said the Captain, solemnly holding up his hook, “as you can’t see your hand afore you, excepting when the wiwid lightning reweals the same; and when you drive, drive, drive through the storm and dark, as if you was a driving, head on, to the world without end, evermore, amen, and when found making a note of. Them’s the times, my beauty, when a man may say to his messmate (previously a overhauling of the wollume), ‘A stiff nor’wester’s blowing, Bill; hark, don’t you hear it roar now! Lord help ’em, how I pitys all unhappy folks ashore now!’” Which quotation, as particularly applicable to the terrors of the ocean, the Captain delivered in a most impressive manner, concluding with a sonorous “Stand by!”\n\n“Were you ever in a dreadful storm?” asked Florence.\n\n“Why ay, my lady lass, I’ve seen my share of bad weather,” said the Captain, tremulously wiping his head, “and I’ve had my share of knocking about; but—but it ain’t of myself as I was a meaning to speak. Our dear boy,” drawing closer to her, “Wal”r, darling, as was drownded.”\n\nThe Captain spoke in such a trembling voice, and looked at Florence with a face so pale and agitated, that she clung to his hand in affright.\n\n“Your face is changed,” cried Florence. “You are altered in a moment. What is it? Dear Captain Cuttle, it turns me cold to see you!”\n\n“What! Lady lass,” returned the Captain, supporting her with his hand, “don’t be took aback. No, no! All’s well, all’s well, my dear. As I was a saying—Wal”r—he’s—he’s drownded. Ain’t he?”\n\nFlorence looked at him intently; her colour came and went; and she laid her hand upon her breast.\n\n“There’s perils and dangers on the deep, my beauty,” said the Captain; “and over many a brave ship, and many and many a bould heart, the secret waters has closed up, and never told no tales. But there’s escapes upon the deep, too, and sometimes one man out of a score,—ah! maybe out of a hundred, pretty,—has been saved by the mercy of God, and come home after being given over for dead, and told of all hands lost. I—I know a story, Heart’s Delight,” stammered the Captain, “o’ this natur, as was told to me once; and being on this here tack, and you and me sitting alone by the fire, maybe you’d like to hear me tell it. Would you, deary?”\n\nFlorence, trembling with an agitation which she could not control or understand, involuntarily followed his glance, which went behind her into the shop, where a lamp was burning. The instant that she turned her head, the Captain sprung out of his chair, and interposed his hand.\n\n“There’s nothing there, my beauty,” said the Captain. “Don’t look there.”\n\n“Why not?” asked Florence.\n\nThe Captain murmured something about its being dull that way, and about the fire being cheerful. He drew the door ajar, which had been standing open until now, and resumed his seat. Florence followed him with her eyes, and looked intently in his face.\n\n“The story was about a ship, my lady lass,” began the Captain, “as sailed out of the Port of London, with a fair wind and in fair weather, bound for—don’t be took aback, my lady lass, she was only out’ard bound, pretty, only out’ard bound!”\n\nThe expression on Florence’s face alarmed the Captain, who was himself very hot and flurried, and showed scarcely less agitation than she did.\n\n“Shall I go on, Beauty?” said the Captain.\n\n“Yes, yes, pray!” cried Florence.\n\nThe Captain made a gulp as if to get down something that was sticking in his throat, and nervously proceeded:\n\n“That there unfort’nate ship met with such foul weather, out at sea, as don’t blow once in twenty year, my darling. There was hurricanes ashore as tore up forests and blowed down towns, and there was gales at sea in them latitudes, as not the stoutest wessel ever launched could live in. Day arter day that there unfort’nate ship behaved noble, I’m told, and did her duty brave, my pretty, but at one blow a’most her bulwarks was stove in, her masts and rudder carved away, her best man swept overboard, and she left to the mercy of the storm as had no mercy but blowed harder and harder yet, while the waves dashed over her, and beat her in, and every time they come a thundering at her, broke her like a shell. Every black spot in every mountain of water that rolled away was a bit o’ the ship’s life or a living man, and so she went to pieces, Beauty, and no grass will never grow upon the graves of them as manned that ship.”\n\n“They were not all lost!” cried Florence. “Some were saved!—Was one?”\n\n“Aboard o’ that there unfort’nate wessel,” said the Captain, rising from his chair, and clenching his hand with prodigious energy and exultation, “was a lad, a gallant lad—as I’ve heerd tell—that had loved, when he was a boy, to read and talk about brave actions in shipwrecks—I’ve heerd him! I’ve heerd him!—and he remembered of ’em in his hour of need; for when the stoutest and oldest hands was hove down, he was firm and cheery. It warn’t the want of objects to like and love ashore that gave him courage, it was his nat’ral mind. I’ve seen it in his face, when he was no more than a child—ay, many a time!—and when I thought it nothing but his good looks, bless him!”\n\n“And was he saved!” cried Florence. “Was he saved!”\n\n“That brave lad,” said the Captain,—“look at me, pretty! Don’t look round—”\n\nFlorence had hardly power to repeat, “Why not?”\n\n“Because there’s nothing there, my deary,” said the Captain. “Don’t be took aback, pretty creetur! Don’t, for the sake of Wal”r, as was dear to all on us! That there lad,” said the Captain, “arter working with the best, and standing by the faint-hearted, and never making no complaint nor sign of fear, and keeping up a spirit in all hands that made ’em honour him as if he’d been a admiral—that lad, along with the second-mate and one seaman, was left, of all the beatin’ hearts that went aboard that ship, the only living creeturs—lashed to a fragment of the wreck, and driftin’ on the stormy sea.”\n\n“Were they saved?” cried Florence.\n\n“Days and nights they drifted on them endless waters,” said the Captain, “until at last—No! Don’t look that way, pretty!—a sail bore down upon ’em, and they was, by the Lord’s mercy, took aboard: two living and one dead.”\n\n“Which of them was dead?” cried Florence.\n\n“Not the lad I speak on,” said the Captain.\n\n“Thank God! oh thank God!”\n\n“Amen!” returned the Captain hurriedly. “Don’t be took aback! A minute more, my lady lass! with a good heart!—aboard that ship, they went a long voyage, right away across the chart (for there warn’t no touching nowhere), and on that voyage the seaman as was picked up with him died. But he was spared, and—”\n\nThe Captain, without knowing what he did, had cut a slice of bread from the loaf, and put it on his hook (which was his usual toasting-fork), on which he now held it to the fire; looking behind Florence with great emotion in his face, and suffering the bread to blaze and burn like fuel.\n\n“Was spared,” repeated Florence, “and—?”\n\n“And come home in that ship,” said the Captain, still looking in the same direction, “and—don’t be frightened, pretty—and landed; and one morning come cautiously to his own door to take a obserwation, knowing that his friends would think him drownded, when he sheered off at the unexpected—”\n\n“At the unexpected barking of a dog?” cried Florence, quickly.\n\n“Yes,” roared the Captain. “Steady, darling! courage! Don’t look round yet. See there! upon the wall!”\n\nThere was the shadow of a man upon the wall close to her. She started up, looked round, and with a piercing cry, saw Walter Gay behind her!\n\nShe had no thought of him but as a brother, a brother rescued from the grave; a shipwrecked brother saved and at her side; and rushed into his arms. In all the world, he seemed to be her hope, her comfort, refuge, natural protector. “Take care of Walter, I was fond of Walter!” The dear remembrance of the plaintive voice that said so, rushed upon her soul, like music in the night. “Oh welcome home, dear Walter! Welcome to this stricken breast!” She felt the words, although she could not utter them, and held him in her pure embrace.\n\nCaptain Cuttle, in a fit of delirium, attempted to wipe his head with the blackened toast upon his hook: and finding it an uncongenial substance for the purpose, put it into the crown of his glazed hat, put the glazed hat on with some difficulty, essayed to sing a verse of Lovely Peg, broke down at the first word, and retired into the shop, whence he presently came back express, with a face all flushed and besmeared, and the starch completely taken out of his shirt-collar, to say these words:\n\n“Wal”r, my lad, here is a little bit of property as I should wish to make over, jintly!”\n\nThe Captain hastily produced the big watch, the teaspoons, the sugar-tongs, and the canister, and laying them on the table, swept them with his great hand into Walter’s hat; but in handing that singular strong box to Walter, he was so overcome again, that he was fain to make another retreat into the shop, and absent himself for a longer space of time than on his first retirement.\n\nBut Walter sought him out, and brought him back; and then the Captain’s great apprehension was, that Florence would suffer from this new shock. He felt it so earnestly, that he turned quite rational, and positively interdicted any further allusion to Walter’s adventures for some days to come. Captain Cuttle then became sufficiently composed to relieve himself of the toast in his hat, and to take his place at the tea-board; but finding Walter’s grasp upon his shoulder, on one side, and Florence whispering her tearful congratulations on the other, the Captain suddenly bolted again, and was missing for a good ten minutes.\n\nBut never in all his life had the Captain’s face so shone and glistened, as when, at last, he sat stationary at the tea-board, looking from Florence to Walter, and from Walter to Florence. Nor was this effect produced or at all heightened by the immense quantity of polishing he had administered to his face with his coat-sleeve during the last half-hour. It was solely the effect of his internal emotions. There was a glory and delight within the Captain that spread itself over his whole visage, and made a perfect illumination there.\n\nThe pride with which the Captain looked upon the bronzed cheek and the courageous eyes of his recovered boy; with which he saw the generous fervour of his youth, and all its frank and hopeful qualities, shining once more, in the fresh, wholesome manner, and the ardent face, would have kindled something of this light in his countenance. The admiration and sympathy with which he turned his eyes on Florence, whose beauty, grace, and innocence could have won no truer or more zealous champion than himself, would have had an equal influence upon him. But the fulness of the glow he shed around him could only have been engendered in his contemplation of the two together, and in all the fancies springing out of that association, that came sparkling and beaming into his head, and danced about it.\n\nHow they talked of poor old Uncle Sol, and dwelt on every little circumstance relating to his disappearance; how their joy was moderated by the old man’s absence and by the misfortunes of Florence; how they released Diogenes, whom the Captain had decoyed upstairs some time before, lest he should bark again; the Captain, though he was in one continual flutter, and made many more short plunges into the shop, fully comprehended. But he no more dreamed that Walter looked on Florence, as it were, from a new and far-off place; that while his eyes often sought the lovely face, they seldom met its open glance of sisterly affection, but withdrew themselves when hers were raised towards him; than he believed that it was Walter’s ghost who sat beside him. He saw them together in their youth and beauty, and he knew the story of their younger days, and he had no inch of room beneath his great blue waistcoat for anything save admiration of such a pair, and gratitude for their being reunited.\n\nThey sat thus, until it grew late. The Captain would have been content to sit so for a week. But Walter rose, to take leave for the night.\n\n“Going, Walter!” said Florence. “Where?”\n\n“He slings his hammock for the present, lady lass,” said Captain Cuttle, “round at Brogley’s. Within hail, Heart’s Delight.”\n\n“I am the cause of your going away, Walter,” said Florence. “There is a houseless sister in your place.”\n\n“Dear Miss Dombey,” replied Walter, hesitating—“if it is not too bold to call you so!—”\n\n“Walter!” she exclaimed, surprised.\n\n“—If anything could make me happier in being allowed to see and speak to you, would it not be the discovery that I had any means on earth of doing you a moment’s service! Where would I not go, what would I not do, for your sake?”\n\nShe smiled, and called him brother.\n\n“You are so changed,” said Walter—\n\n“I changed!” she interrupted.\n\n“—To me,” said Walter, softly, as if he were thinking aloud, “changed to me. I left you such a child, and find you—oh! something so different—”\n\n“But your sister, Walter. You have not forgotten what we promised to each other, when we parted?”\n\n“Forgotten!” But he said no more.\n\n“And if you had—if suffering and danger had driven it from your thoughts—which it has not—you would remember it now, Walter, when you find me poor and abandoned, with no home but this, and no friends but the two who hear me speak!”\n\n“I would! Heaven knows I would!” said Walter.\n\n“Oh, Walter,” exclaimed Florence, through her sobs and tears. “Dear brother! Show me some way through the world—some humble path that I may take alone, and labour in, and sometimes think of you as one who will protect and care for me as for a sister! Oh, help me, Walter, for I need help so much!”\n\n“Miss Dombey! Florence! I would die to help you. But your friends are proud and rich. Your father—”\n\n“No, no! Walter!” She shrieked, and put her hands up to her head, in an attitude of terror that transfixed him where he stood. “Don’t say that word!”\n\nHe never, from that hour, forgot the voice and look with which she stopped him at the name. He felt that if he were to live a hundred years, he never could forget it.\n\nSomewhere—anywhere—but never home! All past, all gone, all lost, and broken up! The whole history of her untold slight and suffering was in the cry and look; and he felt he never could forget it, and he never did.\n\nShe laid her gentle face upon the Captain’s shoulder, and related how and why she had fled. If every sorrowing tear she shed in doing so, had been a curse upon the head of him she never named or blamed, it would have been better for him, Walter thought, with awe, than to be renounced out of such a strength and might of love.\n\n“There, precious!” said the Captain, when she ceased; and deep attention the Captain had paid to her while she spoke; listening, with his glazed hat all awry and his mouth wide open. “Awast, awast, my eyes! Wal”r, dear lad, sheer off for tonight, and leave the pretty one to me!”\n\nWalter took her hand in both of his, and put it to his lips, and kissed it. He knew now that she was, indeed, a homeless wandering fugitive; but, richer to him so, than in all the wealth and pride of her right station, she seemed farther off than even on the height that had made him giddy in his boyish dreams.\n\nCaptain Cuttle, perplexed by no such meditations, guarded Florence to her room, and watched at intervals upon the charmed ground outside her door—for such it truly was to him—until he felt sufficiently easy in his mind about her, to turn in under the counter. On abandoning his watch for that purpose, he could not help calling once, rapturously, through the keyhole, “Drownded. Ain’t he, pretty?”—or, when he got downstairs, making another trial at that verse of Lovely Peg. But it stuck in his throat somehow, and he could make nothing of it; so he went to bed, and dreamed that old Sol Gills was married to Mrs MacStinger, and kept prisoner by that lady in a secret chamber on a short allowance of victuals.\n\n# CHAPTER L. Mr Toots’s Complaint\n\nThere was an empty room above-stairs at the wooden Midshipman’s, which, in days of yore, had been Walter’s bedroom. Walter, rousing up the Captain betimes in the morning, proposed that they should carry thither such furniture out of the little parlour as would grace it best, so that Florence might take possession of it when she rose. As nothing could be more agreeable to Captain Cuttle than making himself very red and short of breath in such a cause, he turned to (as he himself said) with a will; and, in a couple of hours, this garret was transformed into a species of land-cabin, adorned with all the choicest moveables out of the parlour, inclusive even of the Tartar frigate, which the Captain hung up over the chimney-piece with such extreme delight, that he could do nothing for half-an-hour afterwards but walk backward from it, lost in admiration.\n\nThe Captain could be induced by no persuasion of Walter’s to wind up the big watch, or to take back the canister, or to touch the sugar-tongs and teaspoons. “No, no, my lad;” was the Captain’s invariable reply to any solicitation of the kind, “I’ve made that there little property over, jintly.” These words he repeated with great unction and gravity, evidently believing that they had the virtue of an Act of Parliament, and that unless he committed himself by some new admission of ownership, no flaw could be found in such a form of conveyance.\n\nIt was an advantage of the new arrangement, that besides the greater seclusion it afforded Florence, it admitted of the Midshipman being restored to his usual post of observation, and also of the shop shutters being taken down. The latter ceremony, however little importance the unconscious Captain attached to it, was not wholly superfluous; for, on the previous day, so much excitement had been occasioned in the neighbourhood, by the shutters remaining unopened, that the Instrument-maker’s house had been honoured with an unusual share of public observation, and had been intently stared at from the opposite side of the way, by groups of hungry gazers, at any time between sunrise and sunset. The idlers and vagabonds had been particularly interested in the Captain’s fate; constantly grovelling in the mud to apply their eyes to the cellar-grating, under the shop-window, and delighting their imaginations with the fancy that they could see a piece of his coat as he hung in a corner; though this settlement of him was stoutly disputed by an opposite faction, who were of opinion that he lay murdered with a hammer, on the stairs. It was not without exciting some discontent, therefore, that the subject of these rumours was seen early in the morning standing at his shop-door as hale and hearty as if nothing had happened; and the beadle of that quarter, a man of an ambitious character, who had expected to have the distinction of being present at the breaking open of the door, and of giving evidence in full uniform before the coroner, went so far as to say to an opposite neighbour, that the chap in the glazed hat had better not try it on there—without more particularly mentioning what—and further, that he, the beadle, would keep his eye upon him.\n\n“Captain Cuttle,” said Walter, musing, when they stood resting from their labours at the shop-door, looking down the old familiar street; it being still early in the morning; “nothing at all of Uncle Sol, in all that time!”\n\n“Nothing at all, my lad,” replied the Captain, shaking his head.\n\n“Gone in search of me, dear, kind old man,” said Walter: “yet never write to you! But why not? He says, in effect, in this packet that you gave me,” taking the paper from his pocket, which had been opened in the presence of the enlightened Bunsby, “that if you never hear from him before opening it, you may believe him dead. Heaven forbid! But you would have heard of him, even if he were dead! Someone would have written, surely, by his desire, if he could not; and have said, ‘on such a day, there died in my house,’ or ‘under my care,’ or so forth, ‘Mr Solomon Gills of London, who left this last remembrance and this last request to you’.”\n\nThe Captain, who had never climbed to such a clear height of probability before, was greatly impressed by the wide prospect it opened, and answered, with a thoughtful shake of his head, “Well said, my lad; wery well said.”\n\n“I have been thinking of this, or, at least,” said Walter, colouring, “I have been thinking of one thing and another, all through a sleepless night, and I cannot believe, Captain Cuttle, but that my Uncle Sol (Lord bless him!) is alive, and will return. I don’t so much wonder at his going away, because, leaving out of consideration that spice of the marvellous which was always in his character, and his great affection for me, before which every other consideration of his life became nothing, as no one ought to know so well as I who had the best of fathers in him,”—Walter’s voice was indistinct and husky here, and he looked away, along the street,—“leaving that out of consideration, I say, I have often read and heard of people who, having some near and dear relative, who was supposed to be shipwrecked at sea, have gone down to live on that part of the sea-shore where any tidings of the missing ship might be expected to arrive, though only an hour or two sooner than elsewhere, or have even gone upon her track to the place whither she was bound, as if their going would create intelligence. I think I should do such a thing myself, as soon as another, or sooner than many, perhaps. But why my Uncle shouldn’t write to you, when he so clearly intended to do so, or how he should die abroad, and you not know it through some other hand, I cannot make out.”\n\nCaptain Cuttle observed, with a shake of his head, that Jack Bunsby himself hadn’t made it out, and that he was a man as could give a pretty taut opinion too.\n\n“If my Uncle had been a heedless young man, likely to be entrapped by jovial company to some drinking-place, where he was to be got rid of for the sake of what money he might have about him,” said Walter; “or if he had been a reckless sailor, going ashore with two or three months’ pay in his pocket, I could understand his disappearing, and leaving no trace behind. But, being what he was—and is, I hope—I can’t believe it.”\n\n“Wal”r, my lad,” inquired the Captain, wistfully eyeing him as he pondered and pondered, “what do you make of it, then?”\n\n“Captain Cuttle,” returned Walter, “I don’t know what to make of it. I suppose he never has written! There is no doubt about that?”\n\n“If so be as Sol Gills wrote, my lad,” replied the Captain, argumentatively, “where’s his dispatch?”\n\n“Say that he entrusted it to some private hand,” suggested Walter, “and that it has been forgotten, or carelessly thrown aside, or lost. Even that is more probable to me, than the other event. In short, I not only cannot bear to contemplate that other event, Captain Cuttle, but I can’t, and won’t.”\n\n“Hope, you see, Wal”r,” said the Captain, sagely, “Hope. It’s that as animates you. Hope is a buoy, for which you overhaul your Little Warbler, sentimental diwision, but Lord, my lad, like any other buoy, it only floats; it can’t be steered nowhere. Along with the figure-head of Hope,” said the Captain, “there’s a anchor; but what’s the good of my having a anchor, if I can’t find no bottom to let it go in?”\n\nCaptain Cuttle said this rather in his character of a sagacious citizen and householder, bound to impart a morsel from his stores of wisdom to an inexperienced youth, than in his own proper person. Indeed, his face was quite luminous as he spoke, with new hope, caught from Walter; and he appropriately concluded by slapping him on the back; and saying, with enthusiasm, “Hooroar, my lad! Indiwidually, I’m o’ your opinion.”\n\nWalter, with his cheerful laugh, returned the salutation, and said:\n\n“Only one word more about my Uncle at present, Captain Cuttle. I suppose it is impossible that he can have written in the ordinary course—by mail packet, or ship letter, you understand—”\n\n“Ay, ay, my lad,” said the Captain approvingly.\n\n“—And that you have missed the letter, anyhow?”\n\n“Why, Wal”r,” said the Captain, turning his eyes upon him with a faint approach to a severe expression, “ain’t I been on the look-out for any tidings of that man o’ science, old Sol Gills, your Uncle, day and night, ever since I lost him? Ain’t my heart been heavy and watchful always, along of him and you? Sleeping and waking, ain’t I been upon my post, and wouldn’t I scorn to quit it while this here Midshipman held together!”\n\n“Yes, Captain Cuttle,” replied Walter, grasping his hand, “I know you would, and I know how faithful and earnest all you say and feel is. I am sure of it. You don’t doubt that I am as sure of it as I am that my foot is again upon this door-step, or that I again have hold of this true hand. Do you?”\n\n“No, no, Wal”r,” returned the Captain, with his beaming\n\n“I’ll hazard no more conjectures,” said Walter, fervently shaking the hard hand of the Captain, who shook his with no less goodwill. “All I will add is, Heaven forbid that I should touch my Uncle’s possessions, Captain Cuttle! Everything that he left here, shall remain in the care of the truest of stewards and kindest of men—and if his name is not Cuttle, he has no name! Now, best of friends, about—Miss Dombey.”\n\nThere was a change in Walter’s manner, as he came to these two words; and when he uttered them, all his confidence and cheerfulness appeared to have deserted him.\n\n“I thought, before Miss Dombey stopped me when I spoke of her father last night,” said Walter, “—you remember how?”\n\nThe Captain well remembered, and shook his head.\n\n“I thought,” said Walter, “before that, that we had but one hard duty to perform, and that it was, to prevail upon her to communicate with her friends, and to return home.”\n\nThe Captain muttered a feeble “Awast!” or a “Stand by!” or something or other, equally pertinent to the occasion; but it was rendered so extremely feeble by the total discomfiture with which he received this announcement, that what it was, is mere matter of conjecture.\n\n“But,” said Walter, “that is over. I think so, no longer. I would sooner be put back again upon that piece of wreck, on which I have so often floated, since my preservation, in my dreams, and there left to drift, and drive, and die!”\n\n“Hooroar, my lad!” exclaimed the Captain, in a burst of uncontrollable satisfaction. “Hooroar! hooroar! hooroar!”\n\n“To think that she, so young, so good, and beautiful,” said Walter, “so delicately brought up, and born to such a different fortune, should strive with the rough world! But we have seen the gulf that cuts off all behind her, though no one but herself can know how deep it is; and there is no return.”\n\nCaptain Cuttle, without quite understanding this, greatly approved of it, and observed in a tone of strong corroboration, that the wind was quite abaft.\n\n“She ought not to be alone here; ought she, Captain Cuttle?” said Walter, anxiously.\n\n“Well, my lad,” replied the Captain, after a little sagacious consideration. “I don’t know. You being here to keep her company, you see, and you two being jintly—”\n\n“Dear Captain Cuttle!” remonstrated Walter. “I being here! Miss Dombey, in her guileless innocent heart, regards me as her adopted brother; but what would the guile and guilt of my heart be, if I pretended to believe that I had any right to approach her, familiarly, in that character—if I pretended to forget that I am bound, in honour, not to do it?”\n\n“Wal”r, my lad,” hinted the Captain, with some revival of his discomfiture, “ain’t there no other character as—”\n\n“Oh!” returned Walter, “would you have me die in her esteem—in such esteem as hers—and put a veil between myself and her angel’s face for ever, by taking advantage of her being here for refuge, so trusting and so unprotected, to endeavour to exalt myself into her lover? What do I say? There is no one in the world who would be more opposed to me if I could do so, than you.”\n\n“Wal”r, my lad,” said the Captain, drooping more and more, “prowiding as there is any just cause or impediment why two persons should not be jined together in the house of bondage, for which you’ll overhaul the place and make a note, I hope I should declare it as promised and wowed in the banns. So there ain’t no other character; ain’t there, my lad?”\n\nWalter briskly waved his hand in the negative.\n\n“Well, my lad,” growled the Captain slowly, “I won’t deny but what I find myself wery much down by the head, along o’ this here, or but what I’ve gone clean about. But as to Lady lass, Wal”r, mind you, wot’s respect and duty to her, is respect and duty in my articles, howsumever disapinting; and therefore I follows in your wake, my lad, and feel as you are, no doubt, acting up to yourself. And there ain’t no other character, ain’t there?” said the Captain, musing over the ruins of his fallen castle, with a very despondent face.\n\n“Now, Captain Cuttle,” said Walter, starting a fresh point with a gayer air, to cheer the Captain up—but nothing could do that; he was too much concerned—“I think we should exert ourselves to find someone who would be a proper attendant for Miss Dombey while she remains here, and who may be trusted. None of her relations may. It’s clear Miss Dombey feels that they are all subservient to her father. What has become of Susan?”\n\n“The young woman?” returned the Captain. “It’s my belief as she was sent away again the will of Heart’s Delight. I made a signal for her when Lady lass first come, and she rated of her wery high, and said she had been gone a long time.”\n\n“Then,” said Walter, “do you ask Miss Dombey where she’s gone, and we’ll try to find her. The morning’s getting on, and Miss Dombey will soon be rising. You are her best friend. Wait for her upstairs, and leave me to take care of all down here.”\n\nThe Captain, very crest-fallen indeed, echoed the sigh with which Walter said this, and complied. Florence was delighted with her new room, anxious to see Walter, and overjoyed at the prospect of greeting her old friend Susan. But Florence could not say where Susan was gone, except that it was in Essex, and no one could say, she remembered, unless it were Mr Toots.\n\nWith this information the melancholy Captain returned to Walter, and gave him to understand that Mr Toots was the young gentleman whom he had encountered on the door-step, and that he was a friend of his, and that he was a young gentleman of property, and that he hopelessly adored Miss Dombey. The Captain also related how the intelligence of Walter’s supposed fate had first made him acquainted with Mr Toots, and how there was solemn treaty and compact between them, that Mr Toots should be mute upon the subject of his love.\n\nThe question then was, whether Florence could trust Mr Toots; and Florence saying, with a smile, “Oh, yes, with her whole heart!” it became important to find out where Mr Toots lived. This, Florence didn’t know, and the Captain had forgotten; and the Captain was telling Walter, in the little parlour, that Mr Toots was sure to be there soon, when in came Mr Toots himself.\n\n“Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, rushing into the parlour without any ceremony, “I’m in a state of mind bordering on distraction!”\n\nMr Toots had discharged those words, as from a mortar, before he observed Walter, whom he recognised with what may be described as a chuckle of misery.\n\n“You’ll excuse me, Sir,” said Mr Toots, holding his forehead, “but I’m at present in that state that my brain is going, if not gone, and anything approaching to politeness in an individual so situated would be a hollow mockery. Captain Gills, I beg to request the favour of a private interview.”\n\n“Why, Brother,” returned the Captain, taking him by the hand, “you are the man as we was on the look-out for.”\n\n“Oh, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, “what a look-out that must be, of which I am the object! I haven’t dared to shave, I’m in that rash state. I haven’t had my clothes brushed. My hair is matted together. I told the Chicken that if he offered to clean my boots, I’d stretch him a Corpse before me!”\n\nAll these indications of a disordered mind were verified in Mr Toots’s appearance, which was wild and savage.\n\n“See here, Brother,” said the Captain. “This here’s old Sol Gills’s nevy Wal”r. Him as was supposed to have perished at sea.”\n\nMr Toots took his hand from his forehead, and stared at Walter.\n\n“Good gracious me!” stammered Mr Toots. “What a complication of misery! How-de-do? I—I—I’m afraid you must have got very wet. Captain Gills, will you allow me a word in the shop?”\n\nHe took the Captain by the coat, and going out with him whispered:\n\n“That then, Captain Gills, is the party you spoke of, when you said that he and Miss Dombey were made for one another?”\n\n“Why, ay, my lad,” replied the disconsolate Captain; “I was of that mind once.”\n\n“And at this time!” exclaimed Mr Toots, with his hand to his forehead again. “Of all others!—a hated rival! At least, he ain’t a hated rival,” said Mr Toots, stopping short, on second thoughts, and taking away his hand; “what should I hate him for? No. If my affection has been truly disinterested, Captain Gills, let me prove it now!”\n\nMr Toots shot back abruptly into the parlour, and said, wringing Walter by the hand:\n\n“How-de-do? I hope you didn’t take any cold. I—I shall be very glad if you’ll give me the pleasure of your acquaintance. I wish you many happy returns of the day. Upon my word and honour,” said Mr Toots, warming as he became better acquainted with Walter’s face and figure, “I’m very glad to see you!”\n\n“Thank you, heartily,” said Walter. “I couldn’t desire a more genuine and genial welcome.”\n\n“Couldn’t you, though?” said Mr Toots, still shaking his hand. “It’s very kind of you. I’m much obliged to you. How-de-do? I hope you left everybody quite well over the—that is, upon the—I mean wherever you came from last, you know.”\n\nAll these good wishes, and better intentions, Walter responded to manfully.\n\n“Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, “I should wish to be strictly honourable; but I trust I may be allowed now, to allude to a certain subject that—”\n\n“Ay, ay, my lad,” returned the Captain. “Freely, freely.”\n\n“Then, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, “and Lieutenant Walters—are you aware that the most dreadful circumstances have been happening at Mr Dombey’s house, and that Miss Dombey herself has left her father, who, in my opinion,” said Mr Toots, with great excitement, “is a Brute, that it would be a flattery to call a—a marble monument, or a bird of prey,—and that she is not to be found, and has gone no one knows where?”\n\n“May I ask how you heard this?” inquired Walter.\n\n“Lieutenant Walters,” said Mr Toots, who had arrived at that appellation by a process peculiar to himself; probably by jumbling up his Christian name with the seafaring profession, and supposing some relationship between him and the Captain, which would extend, as a matter of course, to their titles; “Lieutenant Walters, I can have no objection to make a straightforward reply. The fact is, that feeling extremely interested in everything that relates to Miss Dombey—not for any selfish reason, Lieutenant Walters, for I am well aware that the most able thing I could do for all parties would be to put an end to my existence, which can only be regarded as an inconvenience—I have been in the habit of bestowing a trifle now and then upon a footman; a most respectable young man, of the name of Towlinson, who has lived in the family some time; and Towlinson informed me, yesterday evening, that this was the state of things. Since which, Captain Gills—and Lieutenant Walters—I have been perfectly frantic, and have been lying down on the sofa all night, the Ruin you behold.”\n\n“Mr Toots,” said Walter, “I am happy to be able to relieve your mind. Pray calm yourself. Miss Dombey is safe and well.”\n\n“Sir!” cried Mr Toots, starting from his chair and shaking hands with him anew, “the relief is so excessive, and unspeakable, that if you were to tell me now that Miss Dombey was married even, I could smile. Yes, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, appealing to him, “upon my soul and body, I really think, whatever I might do to myself immediately afterwards, that I could smile, I am so relieved.”\n\n“It will be a greater relief and delight still, to such a generous mind as yours,” said Walter, not at all slow in returning his greeting, “to find that you can render service to Miss Dombey. Captain Cuttle, will you have the kindness to take Mr Toots upstairs?”\n\nThe Captain beckoned to Mr Toots, who followed him with a bewildered countenance, and, ascending to the top of the house, was introduced, without a word of preparation from his conductor, into Florence’s new retreat.\n\nPoor Mr Toots’s amazement and pleasure at sight of her were such, that they could find a vent in nothing but extravagance. He ran up to her, seized her hand, kissed it, dropped it, seized it again, fell upon one knee, shed tears, chuckled, and was quite regardless of his danger of being pinned by Diogenes, who, inspired by the belief that there was something hostile to his mistress in these demonstrations, worked round and round him, as if only undecided at what particular point to go in for the assault, but quite resolved to do him a fearful mischief.\n\n“Oh Di, you bad, forgetful dog! Dear Mr Toots, I am so rejoiced to see you!”\n\n“Thankee,” said Mr Toots, “I am pretty well, I’m much obliged to you, Miss Dombey. I hope all the family are the same.”\n\nMr Toots said this without the least notion of what he was talking about, and sat down on a chair, staring at Florence with the liveliest contention of delight and despair going on in his face that any face could exhibit.\n\n“Captain Gills and Lieutenant Walters have mentioned, Miss Dombey,” gasped Mr Toots, “that I can do you some service. If I could by any means wash out the remembrance of that day at Brighton, when I conducted myself—much more like a Parricide than a person of independent property,” said Mr Toots, with severe self-accusation, “I should sink into the silent tomb with a gleam of joy.”\n\n“Pray, Mr Toots,” said Florence, “do not wish me to forget anything in our acquaintance. I never can, believe me. You have been far too kind and good to me always.”\n\n“Miss Dombey,” returned Mr Toots, “your consideration for my feelings is a part of your angelic character. Thank you a thousand times. It’s of no consequence at all.”\n\n“What we thought of asking you,” said Florence, “is, whether you remember where Susan, whom you were so kind as to accompany to the coach-office when she left me, is to be found.”\n\n“Why I do not certainly, Miss Dombey,” said Mr Toots, after a little consideration, “remember the exact name of the place that was on the coach; and I do recollect that she said she was not going to stop there, but was going farther on. But, Miss Dombey, if your object is to find her, and to have her here, myself and the Chicken will produce her with every dispatch that devotion on my part, and great intelligence on the Chicken’s, can ensure.”\n\nMr Toots was so manifestly delighted and revived by the prospect of being useful, and the disinterested sincerity of his devotion was so unquestionable, that it would have been cruel to refuse him. Florence, with an instinctive delicacy, forbore to urge the least obstacle, though she did not forbear to overpower him with thanks; and Mr Toots proudly took the commission upon himself for immediate execution.\n\n“Miss Dombey,” said Mr Toots, touching her proffered hand, with a pang of hopeless love visibly shooting through him, and flashing out in his face, “Good-bye! Allow me to take the liberty of saying, that your misfortunes make me perfectly wretched, and that you may trust me, next to Captain Gills himself. I am quite aware, Miss Dombey, of my own deficiencies—they’re not of the least consequence, thank you—but I am entirely to be relied upon, I do assure you, Miss Dombey.”\n\nWith that Mr Toots came out of the room, again accompanied by the Captain, who, standing at a little distance, holding his hat under his arm and arranging his scattered locks with his hook, had been a not uninterested witness of what passed. And when the door closed behind them, the light of Mr Toots’s life was darkly clouded again.\n\n“Captain Gills,” said that gentleman, stopping near the bottom of the stairs, and turning round, “to tell you the truth, I am not in a frame of mind at the present moment, in which I could see Lieutenant Walters with that entirely friendly feeling towards him that I should wish to harbour in my breast. We cannot always command our feelings, Captain Gills, and I should take it as a particular favour if you’d let me out at the private door.”\n\n“Brother,” returned the Captain, “you shall shape your own course. Wotever course you take, is plain and seamanlike, I’m wery sure.”\n\n“Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, “you’re extremely kind. Your good opinion is a consolation to me. There is one thing,” said Mr Toots, standing in the passage, behind the half-opened door, “that I hope you’ll bear in mind, Captain Gills, and that I should wish Lieutenant Walters to be made acquainted with. I have quite come into my property now, you know, and—and I don’t know what to do with it. If I could be at all useful in a pecuniary point of view, I should glide into the silent tomb with ease and smoothness.”\n\nMr Toots said no more, but slipped out quietly and shut the door upon himself, to cut the Captain off from any reply.\n\nFlorence thought of this good creature, long after he had left her, with mingled emotions of pain and pleasure. He was so honest and warm-hearted, that to see him again and be assured of his truth to her in her distress, was a joy and comfort beyond all price; but for that very reason, it was so affecting to think that she caused him a moment’s unhappiness, or ruffled, by a breath, the harmless current of his life, that her eyes filled with tears, and her bosom overflowed with pity. Captain Cuttle, in his different way, thought much of Mr Toots too; and so did Walter; and when the evening came, and they were all sitting together in Florence’s new room, Walter praised him in a most impassioned manner, and told Florence what he had said on leaving the house, with every graceful setting-off in the way of comment and appreciation that his own honesty and sympathy could surround it with.\n\nMr Toots did not return upon the next day, or the next, or for several days; and in the meanwhile Florence, without any new alarm, lived like a quiet bird in a cage, at the top of the old Instrument-maker’s house. But Florence drooped and hung her head more and more plainly, as the days went on; and the expression that had been seen in the face of the dead child, was often turned to the sky from her high window, as if it sought his angel out, on the bright shore of which he had spoken: lying on his little bed.\n\nFlorence had been weak and delicate of late, and the agitation she had undergone was not without its influences on her health. But it was no bodily illness that affected her now. She was distressed in mind; and the cause of her distress was Walter.\n\nInterested in her, anxious for her, proud and glad to serve her, and showing all this with the enthusiasm and ardour of his character, Florence saw that he avoided her. All the long day through, he seldom approached her room. If she asked for him, he came, again for the moment as earnest and as bright as she remembered him when she was a lost child in the staring streets; but he soon became constrained—her quick affection was too watchful not to know it—and uneasy, and soon left her. Unsought, he never came, all day, between the morning and the night. When the evening closed in, he was always there, and that was her happiest time, for then she half believed that the old Walter of her childhood was not changed. But, even then, some trivial word, look, or circumstance would show her that there was an indefinable division between them which could not be passed.\n\nAnd she could not but see that these revealings of a great alteration in Walter manifested themselves in despite of his utmost efforts to hide them. In his consideration for her, she thought, and in the earnestness of his desire to spare her any wound from his kind hand, he resorted to innumerable little artifices and disguises. So much the more did Florence feel the greatness of the alteration in him; so much the oftener did she weep at this estrangement of her brother.\n\nThe good Captain—her untiring, tender, ever zealous friend—saw it, too, Florence thought, and it pained him. He was less cheerful and hopeful than he had been at first, and would steal looks at her and Walter, by turns, when they were all three together of an evening, with quite a sad face.\n\nFlorence resolved, at last, to speak to Walter. She believed she knew now what the cause of his estrangement was, and she thought it would be a relief to her full heart, and would set him more at ease, if she told him she had found it out, and quite submitted to it, and did not reproach him.\n\nIt was on a certain Sunday afternoon, that Florence took this resolution. The faithful Captain, in an amazing shirt-collar, was sitting by her, reading with his spectacles on, and she asked him where Walter was.\n\n“I think he’s down below, my lady lass,” returned the Captain.\n\n“I should like to speak to him,” said Florence, rising hurriedly as if to go downstairs.\n\n“I’ll rouse him up here, Beauty,” said the Captain, “in a trice.”\n\nThereupon the Captain, with much alacrity, shouldered his book—for he made it a point of duty to read none but very large books on a Sunday, as having a more staid appearance: and had bargained, years ago, for a prodigious volume at a book-stall, five lines of which utterly confounded him at any time, insomuch that he had not yet ascertained of what subject it treated—and withdrew. Walter soon appeared.\n\n“Captain Cuttle tells me, Miss Dombey,” he eagerly began on coming in—but stopped when he saw her face.\n\n“You are not so well today. You look distressed. You have been weeping.”\n\nHe spoke so kindly, and with such a fervent tremor in his voice, that the tears gushed into her eyes at the sound of his words.\n\n“Walter,” said Florence, gently, “I am not quite well, and I have been weeping. I want to speak to you.”\n\nHe sat down opposite to her, looking at her beautiful and innocent face; and his own turned pale, and his lips trembled.\n\n“You said, upon the night when I knew that you were saved—and oh! dear Walter, what I felt that night, and what I hoped!—”\n\nHe put his trembling hand upon the table between them, and sat looking at her.\n\n“—that I was changed. I was surprised to hear you say so, but I understand, now, that I am. Don’t be angry with me, Walter. I was too much overjoyed to think of it, then.”\n\nShe seemed a child to him again. It was the ingenuous, confiding, loving child he saw and heard. Not the dear woman, at whose feet he would have laid the riches of the earth.\n\n“You remember the last time I saw you, Walter, before you went away?”\n\nHe put his hand into his breast, and took out a little purse.\n\n“I have always worn it round my neck! If I had gone down in the deep, it would have been with me at the bottom of the sea.”\n\n“And you will wear it still, Walter, for my old sake?”\n\n“Until I die!”\n\nShe laid her hand on his, as fearlessly and simply, as if not a day had intervened since she gave him the little token of remembrance.\n\n“I am glad of that. I shall be always glad to think so, Walter. Do you recollect that a thought of this change seemed to come into our minds at the same time that evening, when we were talking together?”\n\n“No!” he answered, in a wondering tone.\n\n“Yes, Walter. I had been the means of injuring your hopes and prospects even then. I feared to think so, then, but I know it now. If you were able, then, in your generosity, to hide from me that you knew it too, you cannot do so now, although you try as generously as before. You do. I thank you for it, Walter, deeply, truly; but you cannot succeed. You have suffered too much in your own hardships, and in those of your dearest relation, quite to overlook the innocent cause of all the peril and affliction that has befallen you. You cannot quite forget me in that character, and we can be brother and sister no longer. But, dear Walter, do not think that I complain of you in this. I might have known it—ought to have known it—but forgot it in my joy. All I hope is that you may think of me less irksomely when this feeling is no more a secret one; and all I ask is, Walter, in the name of the poor child who was your sister once, that you will not struggle with yourself, and pain yourself, for my sake, now that I know all!”\n\nWalter had looked upon her while she said this, with a face so full of wonder and amazement, that it had room for nothing else. Now he caught up the hand that touched his, so entreatingly, and held it between his own.\n\n“Oh, Miss Dombey,” he said, “is it possible that while I have been suffering so much, in striving with my sense of what is due to you, and must be rendered to you, I have made you suffer what your words disclose to me? Never, never, before Heaven, have I thought of you but as the single, bright, pure, blessed recollection of my boyhood and my youth. Never have I from the first, and never shall I to the last, regard your part in my life, but as something sacred, never to be lightly thought of, never to be esteemed enough, never, until death, to be forgotten. Again to see you look, and hear you speak, as you did on that night when we parted, is happiness to me that there are no words to utter; and to be loved and trusted as your brother, is the next gift I could receive and prize!”\n\n“Walter,” said Florence, looking at him earnestly, but with a changing face, “what is that which is due to me, and must be rendered to me, at the sacrifice of all this?”\n\n“Respect,” said Walter, in a low tone. “Reverence.”\n\nThe colour dawned in her face, and she timidly and thoughtfully withdrew her hand; still looking at him with unabated earnestness.\n\n“I have not a brother’s right,” said Walter. “I have not a brother’s claim. I left a child. I find a woman.”\n\nThe colour overspread her face. She made a gesture as if of entreaty that he would say no more, and her face dropped upon her hands.\n\nThey were both silent for a time; she weeping.\n\n“I owe it to a heart so trusting, pure, and good,” said Walter, “even to tear myself from it, though I rend my own. How dare I say it is my sister’s!”\n\nShe was weeping still.\n\n“If you had been happy; surrounded as you should be by loving and admiring friends, and by all that makes the station you were born to enviable,” said Walter; “and if you had called me brother, then, in your affectionate remembrance of the past, I could have answered to the name from my distant place, with no inward assurance that I wronged your spotless truth by doing so. But here—and now!”\n\n“Oh thank you, thank you, Walter! Forgive my having wronged you so much. I had no one to advise me. I am quite alone.”\n\n“Florence!” said Walter, passionately. “I am hurried on to say, what I thought, but a few moments ago, nothing could have forced from my lips. If I had been prosperous; if I had any means or hope of being one day able to restore you to a station near your own; I would have told you that there was one name you might bestow upon—me—a right above all others, to protect and cherish you—that I was worthy of in nothing but the love and honour that I bore you, and in my whole heart being yours. I would have told you that it was the only claim that you could give me to defend and guard you, which I dare accept and dare assert; but that if I had that right, I would regard it as a trust so precious and so priceless, that the undivided truth and fervour of my life would poorly acknowledge its worth.”\n\nThe head was still bent down, the tears still falling, and the bosom swelling with its sobs.\n\n“Dear Florence! Dearest Florence! whom I called so in my thoughts before I could consider how presumptuous and wild it was. One last time let me call you by your own dear name, and touch this gentle hand in token of your sisterly forgetfulness of what I have said.”\n\nShe raised her head, and spoke to him with such a solemn sweetness in her eyes; with such a calm, bright, placid smile shining on him through her tears; with such a low, soft tremble in her frame and voice; that the innermost chords of his heart were touched, and his sight was dim as he listened.\n\n“No, Walter, I cannot forget it. I would not forget it, for the world. Are you—are you very poor?”\n\n“I am but a wanderer,” said Walter, “making voyages to live, across the sea. That is my calling now.”\n\n“Are you soon going away again, Walter?”\n\n“Very soon.”\n\nShe sat looking at him for a moment; then timidly put her trembling hand in his.\n\n“If you will take me for your wife, Walter, I will love you dearly. If you will let me go with you, Walter, I will go to the world’s end without fear. I can give up nothing for you—I have nothing to resign, and no one to forsake; but all my love and life shall be devoted to you, and with my last breath I will breathe your name to God if I have sense and memory left.”\n\nHe caught her to his heart, and laid her cheek against his own, and now, no more repulsed, no more forlorn, she wept indeed, upon the breast of her dear lover.\n\nBlessed Sunday Bells, ringing so tranquilly in their entranced and happy ears! Blessed Sunday peace and quiet, harmonising with the calmness in their souls, and making holy air around them! Blessed twilight stealing on, and shading her so soothingly and gravely, as she falls asleep, like a hushed child, upon the bosom she has clung to!\n\nOh load of love and trustfulness that lies to lightly there! Ay, look down on the closed eyes, Walter, with a proudly tender gaze; for in all the wide wide world they seek but thee now—only thee!\n\nThe Captain remained in the little parlour until it was quite dark. He took the chair on which Walter had been sitting, and looked up at the skylight, until the day, by little and little, faded away, and the stars peeped down. He lighted a candle, lighted a pipe, smoked it out, and wondered what on earth was going on upstairs, and why they didn’t call him to tea.\n\nFlorence came to his side while he was in the height of his wonderment.\n\n“Ay! lady lass!” cried the Captain. “Why, you and Wal”r have had a long spell o’ talk, my beauty.”\n\nFlorence put her little hand round one of the great buttons of his coat, and said, looking down into his face:\n\n“Dear Captain, I want to tell you something, if you please.\n\nThe Captain raised his head pretty smartly, to hear what it was. Catching by this means a more distinct view of Florence, he pushed back his chair, and himself with it, as far as they could go.\n\n“What! Heart’s Delight!” cried the Captain, suddenly elated, “Is it that?”\n\n“Yes!” said Florence, eagerly.\n\n“Wal”r! Husband! THAT?” roared the Captain, tossing up his glazed hat into the skylight.\n\n“Yes!” cried Florence, laughing and crying together.\n\nThe Captain immediately hugged her; and then, picking up the glazed hat and putting it on, drew her arm through his, and conducted her upstairs again; where he felt that the great joke of his life was now to be made.\n\n“What, Wal”r my lad!” said the Captain, looking in at the door, with his face like an amiable warming-pan. “So there ain’t NO other character, ain’t there?”\n\nHe had like to have suffocated himself with this pleasantry, which he repeated at least forty times during tea; polishing his radiant face with the sleeve of his coat, and dabbing his head all over with his pocket-handkerchief, in the intervals. But he was not without a graver source of enjoyment to fall back upon, when so disposed, for he was repeatedly heard to say in an undertone, as he looked with ineffable delight at Walter and Florence:\n\n“Ed’ard Cuttle, my lad, you never shaped a better course in your life, than when you made that there little property over, jintly!”\n\n# CHAPTER LI. Mr Dombey and the World\n\nWhat is the proud man doing, while the days go by? Does he ever think of his daughter, or wonder where she is gone? Does he suppose she has come home, and is leading her old life in the weary house? No one can answer for him. He has never uttered her name, since. His household dread him too much to approach a subject on which he is resolutely dumb; and the only person who dares question him, he silences immediately.\n\n“My dear Paul!” murmurs his sister, sidling into the room, on the day of Florence’s departure, “your wife! that upstart woman! Is it possible that what I hear confusedly, is true, and that this is her return for your unparalleled devotion to her; extending, I am sure, even to the sacrifice of your own relations, to her caprices and haughtiness? My poor brother!”\n\nWith this speech feelingly reminiscent of her not having been asked to dinner on the day of the first party, Mrs Chick makes great use of her pocket-handkerchief, and falls on Mr Dombey’s neck. But Mr Dombey frigidly lifts her off, and hands her to a chair.\n\n“I thank you, Louisa,” he says, “for this mark of your affection; but desire that our conversation may refer to any other subject. When I bewail my fate, Louisa, or express myself as being in want of consolation, you can offer it, if you will have the goodness.”\n\n“My dear Paul,” rejoins his sister, with her handkerchief to her face, and shaking her head, “I know your great spirit, and will say no more upon a theme so painful and revolting;” on the heads of which two adjectives, Mrs Chick visits scathing indignation; “but pray let me ask you—though I dread to hear something that will shock and distress me—that unfortunate child Florence—”\n\n“Louisa!” says her brother, sternly, “silence! Not another word of this!”\n\nMrs Chick can only shake her head, and use her handkerchief, and moan over degenerate Dombeys, who are no Dombeys. But whether Florence has been inculpated in the flight of Edith, or has followed her, or has done too much, or too little, or anything, or nothing, she has not the least idea.\n\nHe goes on, without deviation, keeping his thoughts and feelings close within his own breast, and imparting them to no one. He makes no search for his daughter. He may think that she is with his sister, or that she is under his own roof. He may think of her constantly, or he may never think about her. It is all one for any sign he makes.\n\nBut this is sure; he does not think that he has lost her. He has no suspicion of the truth. He has lived too long shut up in his towering supremacy, seeing her, a patient gentle creature, in the path below it, to have any fear of that. Shaken as he is by his disgrace, he is not yet humbled to the level earth. The root is broad and deep, and in the course of years its fibres have spread out and gathered nourishment from everything around it. The tree is struck, but not down.\n\nThough he hide the world within him from the world without—which he believes has but one purpose for the time, and that, to watch him eagerly wherever he goes—he cannot hide those rebel traces of it, which escape in hollow eyes and cheeks, a haggard forehead, and a moody, brooding air. Impenetrable as before, he is still an altered man; and, proud as ever, he is humbled, or those marks would not be there.\n\nThe world. What the world thinks of him, how it looks at him, what it sees in him, and what it says—this is the haunting demon of his mind. It is everywhere where he is; and, worse than that, it is everywhere where he is not. It comes out with him among his servants, and yet he leaves it whispering behind; he sees it pointing after him in the street; it is waiting for him in his counting-house; it leers over the shoulders of rich men among the merchants; it goes beckoning and babbling among the crowd; it always anticipates him, in every place; and is always busiest, he knows, when he has gone away. When he is shut up in his room at night, it is in his house, outside it, audible in footsteps on the pavement, visible in print upon the table, steaming to and fro on railroads and in ships; restless and busy everywhere, with nothing else but him.\n\nIt is not a phantom of his imagination. It is as active in other people’s minds as in his. Witness Cousin Feenix, who comes from Baden-Baden, purposely to talk to him. Witness Major Bagstock, who accompanies Cousin Feenix on that friendly mission.\n\nMr Dombey receives them with his usual dignity, and stands erect, in his old attitude, before the fire. He feels that the world is looking at him out of their eyes. That it is in the stare of the pictures. That Mr Pitt, upon the bookcase, represents it. That there are eyes in its own map, hanging on the wall.\n\n“An unusually cold spring,” says Mr Dombey—to deceive the world.\n\n“Damme, Sir,” says the Major, in the warmth of friendship, “Joseph Bagstock is a bad hand at a counterfeit. If you want to hold your friends off, Dombey, and to give them the cold shoulder, J. B. is not the man for your purpose. Joe is rough and tough, Sir; blunt, Sir, blunt, is Joe. His Royal Highness the late Duke of York did me the honour to say, deservedly or undeservedly—never mind that—‘If there is a man in the service on whom I can depend for coming to the point, that man is Joe—Joe Bagstock.’”\n\nMr Dombey intimates his acquiescence.\n\n“Now, Dombey,” says the Major, “I am a man of the world. Our friend Feenix—if I may presume to—”\n\n“Honoured, I am sure,” says Cousin Feenix.\n\n“—is,” proceeds the Major, with a wag of his head, “also a man of the world. Dombey, you are a man of the world. Now, when three men of the world meet together, and are friends—as I believe—” again appealing to Cousin Feenix.\n\n“I am sure,” says Cousin Feenix, “most friendly.”\n\n“—and are friends,” resumes the Major, “Old Joe’s opinion is (I may be wrong), that the opinion of the world on any particular subject, is very easily got at.”\n\n“Undoubtedly,” says Cousin Feenix. “In point of fact, it’s quite a self-evident sort of thing. I am extremely anxious, Major, that my friend Dombey should hear me express my very great astonishment and regret, that my lovely and accomplished relative, who was possessed of every qualification to make a man happy, should have so far forgotten what was due to—in point of fact, to the world—as to commit herself in such a very extraordinary manner. I have been in a devilish state of depression ever since; and said indeed to Long Saxby last night—man of six foot ten, with whom my friend Dombey is probably acquainted—that it had upset me in a confounded way, and made me bilious. It induces a man to reflect, this kind of fatal catastrophe,” says Cousin Feenix, “that events do occur in quite a providential manner; for if my Aunt had been living at the time, I think the effect upon a devilish lively woman like herself, would have been prostration, and that she would have fallen, in point of fact, a victim.”\n\n“Now, Dombey!—” says the Major, resuming his discourse with great energy.\n\n“I beg your pardon,” interposes Cousin Feenix. “Allow me another word. My friend Dombey will permit me to say, that if any circumstance could have added to the most infernal state of pain in which I find myself on this occasion, it would be the natural amazement of the world at my lovely and accomplished relative (as I must still beg leave to call her) being supposed to have so committed herself with a person—man with white teeth, in point of fact—of very inferior station to her husband. But while I must, rather peremptorily, request my friend Dombey not to criminate my lovely and accomplished relative until her criminality is perfectly established, I beg to assure my friend Dombey that the family I represent, and which is now almost extinct (devilish sad reflection for a man), will interpose no obstacle in his way, and will be happy to assent to any honourable course of proceeding, with a view to the future, that he may point out. I trust my friend Dombey will give me credit for the intentions by which I am animated in this very melancholy affair, and—a—in point of fact, I am not aware that I need trouble my friend Dombey with any further observations.”\n\nMr Dombey bows, without raising his eyes, and is silent.\n\n“Now, Dombey,” says the Major, “our friend Feenix having, with an amount of eloquence that Old Joe B. has never heard surpassed—no, by the Lord, Sir! never!”—says the Major, very blue, indeed, and grasping his cane in the middle—“stated the case as regards the lady, I shall presume upon our friendship, Dombey, to offer a word on another aspect of it. Sir,” says the Major, with the horse’s cough, “the world in these things has opinions, which must be satisfied.”\n\n“I know it,” rejoins Mr Dombey.\n\n“Of course you know it, Dombey,” says the Major, “Damme, Sir, I know you know it. A man of your calibre is not likely to be ignorant of it.”\n\n“I hope not,” replies Mr Dombey.\n\n“Dombey!” says the Major, “you will guess the rest. I speak out—prematurely, perhaps—because the Bagstock breed have always spoke out. Little, Sir, have they ever got by doing it; but it’s in the Bagstock blood. A shot is to be taken at this man. You have J. B. at your elbow. He claims the name of friend. God bless you!”\n\n“Major,” returns Mr Dombey, “I am obliged. I shall put myself in your hands when the time comes. The time not being come, I have forborne to speak to you.”\n\n“Where is the fellow, Dombey?” inquires the Major, after gasping and looking at him, for a minute.\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“Any intelligence of him?” asks the Major.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Dombey, I am rejoiced to hear it,” says the Major. “I congratulate you.”\n\n“You will excuse—even you, Major,” replies Mr Dombey, “my entering into any further detail at present. The intelligence is of a singular kind, and singularly obtained. It may turn out to be valueless; it may turn out to be true; I cannot say at present. My explanation must stop here.”\n\nAlthough this is but a dry reply to the Major’s purple enthusiasm, the Major receives it graciously, and is delighted to think that the world has such a fair prospect of soon receiving its due. Cousin Feenix is then presented with his meed of acknowledgment by the husband of his lovely and accomplished relative, and Cousin Feenix and Major Bagstock retire, leaving that husband to the world again, and to ponder at leisure on their representation of its state of mind concerning his affairs, and on its just and reasonable expectations.\n\nBut who sits in the housekeeper’s room, shedding tears, and talking to Mrs Pipchin in a low tone, with uplifted hands? It is a lady with her face concealed in a very close black bonnet, which appears not to belong to her. It is Miss Tox, who has borrowed this disguise from her servant, and comes from Princess’s Place, thus secretly, to revive her old acquaintance with Mrs Pipchin, in order to get certain information of the state of Mr Dombey.\n\n“How does he bear it, my dear creature?” asks Miss Tox.\n\n“Well,” says Mrs Pipchin, in her snappish way, “he’s pretty much as usual.”\n\n“Externally,” suggests Miss Tox “But what he feels within!”\n\nMrs Pipchin’s hard grey eye looks doubtful as she answers, in three distinct jerks, “Ah! Perhaps. I suppose so.”\n\n“To tell you my mind, Lucretia,” says Mrs Pipchin; she still calls Miss Tox Lucretia, on account of having made her first experiments in the child-quelling line of business on that lady, when an unfortunate and weazen little girl of tender years; “to tell you my mind, Lucretia, I think it’s a good riddance. I don’t want any of your brazen faces here, myself!”\n\n“Brazen indeed! Well may you say brazen, Mrs Pipchin!” returned Miss Tox. “To leave him! Such a noble figure of a man!” And here Miss Tox is overcome.\n\n“I don’t know about noble, I’m sure,” observes Mrs Pipchin; irascibly rubbing her nose. “But I know this—that when people meet with trials, they must bear ’em. Hoity, toity! I have had enough to bear myself, in my time! What a fuss there is! She’s gone, and well got rid of. Nobody wants her back, I should think!”\n\nThis hint of the Peruvian Mines, causes Miss Tox to rise to go away; when Mrs Pipchin rings the bell for Towlinson to show her out, Mr Towlinson, not having seen Miss Tox for ages, grins, and hopes she’s well; observing that he didn’t know her at first, in that bonnet.\n\n“Pretty well, Towlinson, I thank you,” says Miss Tox. “I beg you’ll have the goodness, when you happen to see me here, not to mention it. My visits are merely to Mrs Pipchin.”\n\n“Very good, Miss,” says Towlinson.\n\n“Shocking circumstances occur, Towlinson,” says Miss Tox.\n\n“Very much so indeed, Miss,” rejoins Towlinson.\n\n“I hope, Towlinson,” says Miss Tox, who, in her instruction of the Toodle family, has acquired an admonitorial tone, and a habit of improving passing occasions, “that what has happened here, will be a warning to you, Towlinson.”\n\n“Thank you, Miss, I’m sure,” says Towlinson.\n\nHe appears to be falling into a consideration of the manner in which this warning ought to operate in his particular case, when the vinegary Mrs Pipchin, suddenly stirring him up with a “What are you doing? Why don’t you show the lady to the door?” he ushers Miss Tox forth. As she passes Mr Dombey’s room, she shrinks into the inmost depths of the black bonnet, and walks, on tip-toe; and there is not another atom in the world which haunts him so, that feels such sorrow and solicitude about him, as Miss Tox takes out under the black bonnet into the street, and tries to carry home shadowed it from the newly-lighted lamps.\n\nBut Miss Tox is not a part of Mr Dombey’s world. She comes back every evening at dusk; adding clogs and an umbrella to the bonnet on wet nights; and bears the grins of Towlinson, and the huffs and rebuffs of Mrs Pipchin, and all to ask how he does, and how he bears his misfortune: but she has nothing to do with Mr Dombey’s world. Exacting and harassing as ever, it goes on without her; and she, a by no means bright or particular star, moves in her little orbit in the corner of another system, and knows it quite well, and comes, and cries, and goes away, and is satisfied. Verily Miss Tox is easier of satisfaction than the world that troubles Mr Dombey so much!\n\nAt the Counting House, the clerks discuss the great disaster in all its lights and shades, but chiefly wonder who will get Mr Carker’s place. They are generally of opinion that it will be shorn of some of its emoluments, and made uncomfortable by newly-devised checks and restrictions; and those who are beyond all hope of it are quite sure they would rather not have it, and don’t at all envy the person for whom it may prove to be reserved. Nothing like the prevailing sensation has existed in the Counting House since Mr Dombey’s little son died; but all such excitements there take a social, not to say a jovial turn, and lead to the cultivation of good fellowship. A reconciliation is established on this propitious occasion between the acknowledged wit of the Counting House and an aspiring rival, with whom he has been at deadly feud for months; and a little dinner being proposed, in commemoration of their happily restored amity, takes place at a neighbouring tavern; the wit in the chair; the rival acting as Vice-President. The orations following the removal of the cloth are opened by the Chair, who says, Gentlemen, he can’t disguise from himself that this is not a time for private dissensions. Recent occurrences to which he need not more particularly allude, but which have not been altogether without notice in some Sunday Papers, and in a daily paper which he need not name (here every other member of the company names it in an audible murmur), have caused him to reflect; and he feels that for him and Robinson to have any personal differences at such a moment, would be for ever to deny that good feeling in the general cause, for which he has reason to think and hope that the gentlemen in Dombey’s House have always been distinguished. Robinson replies to this like a man and a brother; and one gentleman who has been in the office three years, under continual notice to quit on account of lapses in his arithmetic, appears in a perfectly new light, suddenly bursting out with a thrilling speech, in which he says, May their respected chief never again know the desolation which has fallen on his hearth! and says a great variety of things, beginning with “May he never again,” which are received with thunders of applause. In short, a most delightful evening is passed, only interrupted by a difference between two juniors, who, quarrelling about the probable amount of Mr Carker’s late receipts per annum, defy each other with decanters, and are taken out greatly excited. Soda water is in general request at the office next day, and most of the party deem the bill an imposition.\n\nAs to Perch, the messenger, he is in a fair way of being ruined for life. He finds himself again constantly in bars of public-houses, being treated and lying dreadfully. It appears that he met everybody concerned in the late transaction, everywhere, and said to them, “Sir,” or “Madam,” as the case was, “why do you look so pale?” at which each shuddered from head to foot, and said, “Oh, Perch!” and ran away. Either the consciousness of these enormities, or the reaction consequent on liquor, reduces Mr Perch to an extreme state of low spirits at that hour of the evening when he usually seeks consolation in the society of Mrs Perch at Balls Pond; and Mrs Perch frets a good deal, for she fears his confidence in woman is shaken now, and that he half expects on coming home at night to find her gone off with some Viscount—“which,” as she observes to an intimate female friend, “is what these wretches in the form of woman have to answer for, Mrs P. It ain’t the harm they do themselves so much as what they reflect upon us, Ma’am; and I see it in Perch’s eye.”\n\nMr Dombey’s servants are becoming, at the same time, quite dissipated, and unfit for other service. They have hot suppers every night, and “talk it over” with smoking drinks upon the board. Mr Towlinson is always maudlin after half-past ten, and frequently begs to know whether he didn’t say that no good would ever come of living in a corner house? They whisper about Miss Florence, and wonder where she is; but agree that if Mr Dombey don’t know, Mrs Dombey does. This brings them to the latter, of whom Cook says, She had a stately way though, hadn’t she? But she was too high! They all agree that she was too high, and Mr Towlinson’s old flame, the housemaid (who is very virtuous), entreats that you will never talk to her any more about people who hold their heads up, as if the ground wasn’t good enough for ’em.\n\nEverything that is said and done about it, except by Mr Dombey, is done in chorus. Mr Dombey and the world are alone together.\n\n# CHAPTER LII. Secret Intelligence\n\nGood Mrs Brown and her daughter Alice kept silent company together, in their own dwelling. It was early in the evening, and late in the spring. But a few days had elapsed since Mr Dombey had told Major Bagstock of his singular intelligence, singularly obtained, which might turn out to be valueless, and might turn out to be true; and the world was not satisfied yet.\n\nThe mother and daughter sat for a long time without interchanging a word: almost without motion. The old woman’s face was shrewdly anxious and expectant; that of her daughter was expectant too, but in a less sharp degree, and sometimes it darkened, as if with gathering disappointment and incredulity. The old woman, without heeding these changes in its expression, though her eyes were often turned towards it, sat mumbling and munching, and listening confidently.\n\nTheir abode, though poor and miserable, was not so utterly wretched as in the days when only Good Mrs Brown inhabited it. Some few attempts at cleanliness and order were manifest, though made in a reckless, gipsy way, that might have connected them, at a glance, with the younger woman. The shades of evening thickened and deepened as the two kept silence, until the blackened walls were nearly lost in the prevailing gloom.\n\nThen Alice broke the silence which had lasted so long, and said:\n\n“You may give him up, mother. He’ll not come here.”\n\n“Death give him up!” returned the old woman, impatiently. “He will come here.”\n\n“We shall see,” said Alice.\n\n“We shall see him,” returned her mother.\n\n“And doomsday,” said the daughter.\n\n“You think I’m in my second childhood, I know!” croaked the old woman. “That’s the respect and duty that I get from my own gal, but I’m wiser than you take me for. He’ll come. T’other day when I touched his coat in the street, he looked round as if I was a toad. But Lord, to see him when I said their names, and asked him if he’d like to find out where they was!”\n\n“Was it so angry?” asked her daughter, roused to interest in a moment.\n\n“Angry? ask if it was bloody. That’s more like the word. Angry? Ha, ha! To call that only angry!” said the old woman, hobbling to the cupboard, and lighting a candle, which displayed the workings of her mouth to ugly advantage, as she brought it to the table. “I might as well call your face only angry, when you think or talk about ’em.”\n\nIt was something different from that, truly, as she sat as still as a crouched tigress, with her kindling eyes.\n\n“Hark!” said the old woman, triumphantly. “I hear a step coming. It’s not the tread of anyone that lives about here, or comes this way often. We don’t walk like that. We should grow proud on such neighbours! Do you hear him?”\n\n“I believe you are right, mother,” replied Alice, in a low voice. “Peace! open the door.”\n\nAs she drew herself within her shawl, and gathered it about her, the old woman complied; and peering out, and beckoning, gave admission to Mr Dombey, who stopped when he had set his foot within the door, and looked distrustfully around.\n\n“It’s a poor place for a great gentleman like your worship,” said the old woman, curtseying and chattering. “I told you so, but there’s no harm in it.”\n\n“Who is that?” asked Mr Dombey, looking at her companion.\n\n“That’s my handsome daughter,” said the old woman. “Your worship won’t mind her. She knows all about it.”\n\nA shadow fell upon his face not less expressive than if he had groaned aloud, “Who does not know all about it!” but he looked at her steadily, and she, without any acknowledgment of his presence, looked at him. The shadow on his face was darker when he turned his glance away from her; and even then it wandered back again, furtively, as if he were haunted by her bold eyes, and some remembrance they inspired.\n\n“Woman,” said Mr Dombey to the old witch who was chuckling and leering close at his elbow, and who, when he turned to address her, pointed stealthily at her daughter, and rubbed her hands, and pointed again, “Woman! I believe that I am weak and forgetful of my station in coming here, but you know why I come, and what you offered when you stopped me in the street the other day. What is it that you have to tell me concerning what I want to know; and how does it happen that I can find voluntary intelligence in a hovel like this,” with a disdainful glance about him, “when I have exerted my power and means to obtain it in vain? I do not think,” he said, after a moment’s pause, during which he had observed her, sternly, “that you are so audacious as to mean to trifle with me, or endeavour to impose upon me. But if you have that purpose, you had better stop on the threshold of your scheme. My humour is not a trifling one, and my acknowledgment will be severe.”\n\n“Oh a proud, hard gentleman!” chuckled the old woman, shaking her head, and rubbing her shrivelled hands, “oh hard, hard, hard! But your worship shall see with your own eyes and hear with your own ears; not with ours—and if your worship’s put upon their track, you won’t mind paying something for it, will you, honourable deary?”\n\n“Money,” returned Mr Dombey, apparently relieved, and assured by this inquiry, “will bring about unlikely things, I know. It may turn even means as unexpected and unpromising as these, to account. Yes. For any reliable information I receive, I will pay. But I must have the information first, and judge for myself of its value.”\n\n“Do you know nothing more powerful than money?” asked the younger woman, without rising, or altering her attitude.\n\n“Not here, I should imagine,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“You should know of something that is more powerful elsewhere, as I judge,” she returned. “Do you know nothing of a woman’s anger?”\n\n“You have a saucy tongue, Jade,” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Not usually,” she answered, without any show of emotion: “I speak to you now, that you may understand us better, and rely more on us. A woman’s anger is pretty much the same here, as in your fine house. I am angry. I have been so, many years. I have as good cause for my anger as you have for yours, and its object is the same man.”\n\nHe started, in spite of himself, and looked at her with astonishment.\n\n“Yes,” she said, with a kind of laugh. “Wide as the distance may seem between us, it is so. How it is so, is no matter; that is my story, and I keep my story to myself. I would bring you and him together, because I have a rage against him. My mother there, is avaricious and poor; and she would sell any tidings she could glean, or anything, or anybody, for money. It is fair enough, perhaps, that you should pay her some, if she can help you to what you want to know. But that is not my motive. I have told you what mine is, and it would be as strong and all-sufficient with me if you haggled and bargained with her for a sixpence. I have done. My saucy tongue says no more, if you wait here till sunrise tomorrow.”\n\nThe old woman, who had shown great uneasiness during this speech, which had a tendency to depreciate her expected gains, pulled Mr Dombey softly by the sleeve, and whispered to him not to mind her. He glared at them both, by turns, with a haggard look, and said, in a deeper voice than was usual with him:\n\n“Go on—what do you know?”\n\n“Oh, not so fast, your worship! we must wait for someone,” answered the old woman. “It’s to be got from someone else—wormed out—screwed and twisted from him.”\n\n“What do you mean?” said Mr Dombey.\n\n“Patience,” she croaked, laying her hand, like a claw, upon his arm. “Patience. I’ll get at it. I know I can! If he was to hold it back from me,” said Good Mrs Brown, crooking her ten fingers, “I’d tear it out of him!”\n\nMr Dombey followed her with his eyes as she hobbled to the door, and looked out again: and then his glance sought her daughter; but she remained impassive, silent, and regardless of him.\n\n“Do you tell me, woman,” he said, when the bent figure of Mrs Brown came back, shaking its head and chattering to itself, “that there is another person expected here?”\n\n“Yes!” said the old woman, looking up into his face, and nodding.\n\n“From whom you are to exact the intelligence that is to be useful to me?”\n\n“Yes,” said the old woman, nodding again.\n\n“A stranger?”\n\n“Chut!” said the old woman, with a shrill laugh. “What signifies! Well, well; no. No stranger to your worship. But he won’t see you. He’d be afraid of you, and wouldn’t talk. You’ll stand behind that door, and judge him for yourself. We don’t ask to be believed on trust What! Your worship doubts the room behind the door? Oh the suspicion of you rich gentlefolks! Look at it, then.”\n\nHer sharp eye had detected an involuntary expression of this feeling on his part, which was not unreasonable under the circumstances. In satisfaction of it she now took the candle to the door she spoke of. Mr Dombey looked in; assured himself that it was an empty, crazy room; and signed to her to put the light back in its place.\n\n“How long,” he asked, “before this person comes?”\n\n“Not long,” she answered. “Would your worship sit down for a few odd minutes?”\n\nHe made no answer; but began pacing the room with an irresolute air, as if he were undecided whether to remain or depart, and as if he had some quarrel with himself for being there at all. But soon his tread grew slower and heavier, and his face more sternly thoughtful; as the object with which he had come, fixed itself in his mind, and dilated there again.\n\nWhile he thus walked up and down with his eyes on the ground, Mrs Brown, in the chair from which she had risen to receive him, sat listening anew. The monotony of his step, or the uncertainty of age, made her so slow of hearing, that a footfall without had sounded in her daughter’s ears for some moments, and she had looked up hastily to warn her mother of its approach, before the old woman was roused by it. But then she started from her seat, and whispering “Here he is!” hurried her visitor to his place of observation, and put a bottle and glass upon the table, with such alacrity, as to be ready to fling her arms round the neck of Rob the Grinder on his appearance at the door.\n\n“And here’s my bonny boy,” cried Mrs Brown, “at last!—oho, oho! You’re like my own son, Robby!”\n\n“Oh! Misses Brown!” remonstrated the Grinder. “Don’t! Can’t you be fond of a cove without squeedging and throttling of him? Take care of the birdcage in my hand, will you?”\n\n“Thinks of a birdcage, afore me!” cried the old woman, apostrophizing the ceiling. “Me that feels more than a mother for him!”\n\n“Well, I’m sure I’m very much obliged to you, Misses Brown,” said the unfortunate youth, greatly aggravated; “but you’re so jealous of a cove. I’m very fond of you myself, and all that, of course; but I don’t smother you, do I, Misses Brown?”\n\nHe looked and spoke as if he would have been far from objecting to do so, however, on a favourable occasion.\n\n“And to talk about birdcages, too!” whimpered the Grinder. “As if that was a crime! Why, look’ee here! Do you know who this belongs to?”\n\n“To Master, dear?” said the old woman with a grin.\n\n“Ah!” replied the Grinder, lifting a large cage tied up in a wrapper, on the table, and untying it with his teeth and hands. “It’s our parrot, this is.”\n\n“Mr Carker’s parrot, Rob?”\n\n“Will you hold your tongue, Misses Brown?” returned the goaded Grinder. “What do you go naming names for? I’m blest,” said Rob, pulling his hair with both hands in the exasperation of his feelings, “if she ain’t enough to make a cove run wild!”\n\n“What! Do you snub me, thankless boy!” cried the old woman, with ready vehemence.\n\n“Good gracious, Misses Brown, no!” returned the Grinder, with tears in his eyes. “Was there ever such a—! Don’t I dote upon you, Misses Brown?”\n\n“Do you, sweet Rob? Do you truly, chickabiddy?” With that, Mrs Brown held him in her fond embrace once more; and did not release him until he had made several violent and ineffectual struggles with his legs, and his hair was standing on end all over his head.\n\n“Oh!” returned the Grinder, “what a thing it is to be perfectly pitched into with affection like this here. I wish she was—How have you been, Misses Brown?”\n\n“Ah! Not here since this night week!” said the old woman, contemplating him with a look of reproach.\n\n“Good gracious, Misses Brown,” returned the Grinder, “I said tonight’s a week, that I’d come tonight, didn’t I? And here I am. How you do go on! I wish you’d be a little rational, Misses Brown. I’m hoarse with saying things in my defence, and my very face is shiny with being hugged!” He rubbed it hard with his sleeve, as if to remove the tender polish in question.\n\n“Drink a little drop to comfort you, my Robin,” said the old woman, filling the glass from the bottle and giving it to him.\n\n“Thank’ee, Misses Brown,” returned the Grinder. “Here’s your health. And long may you—et ceterer.” Which, to judge from the expression of his face, did not include any very choice blessings. “And here’s her health,” said the Grinder, glancing at Alice, who sat with her eyes fixed, as it seemed to him, on the wall behind him, but in reality on Mr Dombey’s face at the door, “and wishing her the same and many of ’em!”\n\nHe drained the glass to these two sentiments, and set it down.\n\n“Well, I say, Misses Brown!” he proceeded. “To go on a little rational now. You’re a judge of birds, and up to their ways, as I know to my cost.”\n\n“Cost!” repeated Mrs Brown.\n\n“Satisfaction, I mean,” returned the Grinder. “How you do take up a cove, Misses Brown! You’ve put it all out of my head again.”\n\n“Judge of birds, Robby,” suggested the old woman.\n\n“Ah!” said the Grinder. “Well, I’ve got to take care of this parrot—certain things being sold, and a certain establishment broke up—and as I don’t want no notice took at present, I wish you’d attend to her for a week or so, and give her board and lodging, will you? If I must come backwards and forwards,” mused the Grinder with a dejected face, “I may as well have something to come for.”\n\n“Something to come for?” screamed the old woman.\n\n“Besides you, I mean, Misses Brown,” returned the craven Rob. “Not that I want any inducement but yourself, Misses Brown, I’m sure. Don’t begin again, for goodness’ sake.”\n\n“He don’t care for me! He don’t care for me, as I care for him!” cried Mrs Brown, lifting up her skinny hands. “But I’ll take care of his bird.”\n\n“Take good care of it too, you know, Mrs Brown,” said Rob, shaking his head. “If you was so much as to stroke its feathers once the wrong way, I believe it would be found out.”\n\n“Ah, so sharp as that, Rob?” said Mrs Brown, quickly.\n\n“Sharp, Misses Brown!” repeated Rob. “But this is not to be talked about.”\n\nChecking himself abruptly, and not without a fearful glance across the room, Rob filled the glass again, and having slowly emptied it, shook his head, and began to draw his fingers across and across the wires of the parrot’s cage by way of a diversion from the dangerous theme that had just been broached.\n\nThe old woman eyed him slily, and hitching her chair nearer his, and looking in at the parrot, who came down from the gilded dome at her call, said:\n\n“Out of place now, Robby?”\n\n“Never you mind, Misses Brown,” returned the Grinder, shortly.\n\n“Board wages, perhaps, Rob?” said Mrs Brown.\n\n“Pretty Polly!” said the Grinder.\n\nThe old woman darted a glance at him that might have warned him to consider his ears in danger, but it was his turn to look in at the parrot now, and however expressive his imagination may have made her angry scowl, it was unseen by his bodily eyes.\n\n“I wonder Master didn’t take you with him, Rob,” said the old woman, in a wheedling voice, but with increased malignity of aspect.\n\nRob was so absorbed in contemplation of the parrot, and in trolling his forefinger on the wires, that he made no answer.\n\nThe old woman had her clutch within a hair’s breadth of his shock of hair as it stooped over the table; but she restrained her fingers, and said, in a voice that choked with its efforts to be coaxing:\n\n“Robby, my child.”\n\n“Well, Misses Brown,” returned the Grinder.\n\n“I say I wonder Master didn’t take you with him, dear.”\n\n“Never you mind, Misses Brown,” returned the Grinder.\n\nMrs Brown instantly directed the clutch of her right hand at his hair, and the clutch of her left hand at his throat, and held on to the object of her fond affection with such extraordinary fury, that his face began to blacken in a moment.\n\n“Misses Brown!” exclaimed the Grinder, “let go, will you? What are you doing of? Help, young woman! Misses Brow—Brow—!”\n\nThe young woman, however, equally unmoved by his direct appeal to her, and by his inarticulate utterance, remained quite neutral, until, after struggling with his assailant into a corner, Rob disengaged himself, and stood there panting and fenced in by his own elbows, while the old woman, panting too, and stamping with rage and eagerness, appeared to be collecting her energies for another swoop upon him. At this crisis Alice interposed her voice, but not in the Grinder’s favour, by saying,\n\n“Well done, mother. Tear him to pieces!”\n\n“What, young woman!” blubbered Rob; “are you against me too? What have I been and done? What am I to be tore to pieces for, I should like to know? Why do you take and choke a cove who has never done you any harm, neither of you? Call yourselves females, too!” said the frightened and afflicted Grinder, with his coat-cuff at his eye. “I’m surprised at you! Where’s your feminine tenderness?”\n\n“You thankless dog!” gasped Mrs Brown. “You impudent insulting dog!”\n\n“What have I been and done to go and give you offence, Misses Brown?” retorted the fearful Rob. “You was very much attached to me a minute ago.”\n\n“To cut me off with his short answers and his sulky words,” said the old woman. “Me! Because I happen to be curious to have a little bit of gossip about Master and the lady, to dare to play at fast and loose with me! But I’ll talk to you no more, my lad. Now go!”\n\n“I’m sure, Misses Brown,” returned the abject Grinder, “I never insiniwated that I wished to go. Don’t talk like that, Misses Brown, if you please.”\n\n“I won’t talk at all,” said Mrs Brown, with an action of her crooked fingers that made him shrink into half his natural compass in the corner. “Not another word with him shall pass my lips. He’s an ungrateful hound. I cast him off. Now let him go! And I’ll slip those after him that shall talk too much; that won’t be shook away; that’ll hang to him like leeches, and slink arter him like foxes. What! He knows ’em. He knows his old games and his old ways. If he’s forgotten ’em, they’ll soon remind him. Now let him go, and see how he’ll do Master’s business, and keep Master’s secrets, with such company always following him up and down. Ha, ha, ha! He’ll find ’em a different sort from you and me, Ally; Close as he is with you and me. Now let him go, now let him go!”\n\nThe old woman, to the unspeakable dismay of the Grinder, walked her twisted figure round and round, in a ring of some four feet in diameter, constantly repeating these words, and shaking her fist above her head, and working her mouth about.\n\n“Misses Brown,” pleaded Rob, coming a little out of his corner, “I’m sure you wouldn’t injure a cove, on second thoughts, and in cold blood, would you?”\n\n“Don’t talk to me,” said Mrs Brown, still wrathfully pursuing her circle. “Now let him go, now let him go!”\n\n“Misses Brown,” urged the tormented Grinder, “I didn’t mean to—Oh, what a thing it is for a cove to get into such a line as this!—I was only careful of talking, Misses Brown, because I always am, on account of his being up to everything; but I might have known it wouldn’t have gone any further. I’m sure I’m quite agreeable,” with a wretched face, “for any little bit of gossip, Misses Brown. Don’t go on like this, if you please. Oh, couldn’t you have the goodness to put in a word for a miserable cove, here?” said the Grinder, appealing in desperation to the daughter.\n\n“Come, mother, you hear what he says,” she interposed, in her stern voice, and with an impatient action of her head; “try him once more, and if you fall out with him again, ruin him, if you like, and have done with him.”\n\nMrs Brown, moved as it seemed by this very tender exhortation, presently began to howl; and softening by degrees, took the apologetic Grinder to her arms, who embraced her with a face of unutterable woe, and like a victim as he was, resumed his former seat, close by the side of his venerable friend, whom he suffered, not without much constrained sweetness of countenance, combating very expressive physiognomical revelations of an opposite character to draw his arm through hers, and keep it there.\n\n“And how’s Master, deary dear?” said Mrs Brown, when, sitting in this amicable posture, they had pledged each other.\n\n“Hush! If you’d be so good, Misses Brown, as to speak a little lower,” Rob implored. “Why, he’s pretty well, thank’ee, I suppose.”\n\n“You’re not out of place, Robby?” said Mrs Brown, in a wheedling tone.\n\n“Why, I’m not exactly out of place, nor in,” faltered Rob. “I—I’m still in pay, Misses Brown.”\n\n“And nothing to do, Rob?”\n\n“Nothing particular to do just now, Misses Brown, but to—keep my eyes open,” said the Grinder, rolling them in a forlorn way.\n\n“Master abroad, Rob?”\n\n“Oh, for goodness’ sake, Misses Brown, couldn’t you gossip with a cove about anything else?” cried the Grinder, in a burst of despair.\n\nThe impetuous Mrs Brown rising directly, the tortured Grinder detained her, stammering “Ye-es, Misses Brown, I believe he’s abroad. What’s she staring at?” he added, in allusion to the daughter, whose eyes were fixed upon the face that now again looked out behind.\n\n“Don’t mind her, lad,” said the old woman, holding him closer to prevent his turning round. “It’s her way—her way. Tell me, Rob. Did you ever see the lady, deary?”\n\n“Oh, Misses Brown, what lady?” cried the Grinder in a tone of piteous supplication.\n\n“What lady?” she retorted. “The lady; Mrs Dombey.”\n\n“Yes, I believe I see her once,” replied Rob.\n\n“The night she went away, Robby, eh?” said the old woman in his ear, and taking note of every change in his face. “Aha! I know it was that night.”\n\n“Well, if you know it was that night, you know, Misses Brown,” replied Rob, “it’s no use putting pinchers into a cove to make him say so.\n\n“Where did they go that night, Rob? Straight away? How did they go? Where did you see her? Did she laugh? Did she cry? Tell me all about it,” cried the old hag, holding him closer yet, patting the hand that was drawn through his arm against her other hand, and searching every line in his face with her bleared eyes. “Come! Begin! I want to be told all about it. What, Rob, boy! You and me can keep a secret together, eh? We’ve done so before now. Where did they go first, Rob?”\n\nThe wretched Grinder made a gasp, and a pause.\n\n“Are you dumb?” said the old woman, angrily.\n\n“Lord, Misses Brown, no! You expect a cove to be a flash of lightning. I wish I was the electric fluency,” muttered the bewildered Grinder. “I’d have a shock at somebody, that would settle their business.”\n\n“What do you say?” asked the old woman, with a grin.\n\n“I’m wishing my love to you, Misses Brown,” returned the false Rob, seeking consolation in the glass. “Where did they go to first was it? Him and her, do you mean?”\n\n“Ah!” said the old woman, eagerly. “Them two.”\n\n“Why, they didn’t go nowhere—not together, I mean,” answered Rob.\n\nThe old woman looked at him, as though she had a strong impulse upon her to make another clutch at his head and throat, but was restrained by a certain dogged mystery in his face.\n\n“That was the art of it,” said the reluctant Grinder; “that’s the way nobody saw ’em go, or has been able to say how they did go. They went different ways, I tell you Misses Brown.”\n\n“Ay, ay, ay! To meet at an appointed place,” chuckled the old woman, after a moment’s silent and keen scrutiny of his face.\n\n“Why, if they weren’t a going to meet somewhere, I suppose they might as well have stayed at home, mightn’t they, Brown?” returned the unwilling Grinder.\n\n“Well, Rob? Well?” said the old woman, drawing his arm yet tighter through her own, as if, in her eagerness, she were afraid of his slipping away.\n\n“What, haven’t we talked enough yet, Misses Brown?” returned the Grinder, who, between his sense of injury, his sense of liquor, and his sense of being on the rack, had become so lachrymose, that at almost every answer he scooped his coats into one or other of his eyes, and uttered an unavailing whine of remonstrance. “Did she laugh that night, was it? Didn’t you ask if she laughed, Misses Brown?”\n\n“Or cried?” added the old woman, nodding assent.\n\n“Neither,” said the Grinder. “She kept as steady when she and me—oh, I see you will have it out of me, Misses Brown! But take your solemn oath now, that you’ll never tell anybody.”\n\nThis Mrs Brown very readily did: being naturally Jesuitical; and having no other intention in the matter than that her concealed visitor should hear for himself.\n\n“She kept as steady, then, when she and me went down to Southampton,” said the Grinder, “as a image. In the morning she was just the same, Misses Brown. And when she went away in the packet before daylight, by herself—me pretending to be her servant, and seeing her safe aboard—she was just the same. Now, are you contented, Misses Brown?”\n\n“No, Rob. Not yet,” answered Mrs Brown, decisively.\n\n“Oh, here’s a woman for you!” cried the unfortunate Rob, in an outburst of feeble lamentation over his own helplessness. “What did you wish to know next, Misses Brown?”\n\n“What became of Master? Where did he go?” she inquired, still holding him tight, and looking close into his face, with her sharp eyes.\n\n“Upon my soul, I don’t know, Misses Brown,” answered Rob. “Upon my soul I don’t know what he did, nor where he went, nor anything about him I only know what he said to me as a caution to hold my tongue, when we parted; and I tell you this, Misses Brown, as a friend, that sooner than ever repeat a word of what we’re saying now, you had better take and shoot yourself, or shut yourself up in this house, and set it a-fire, for there’s nothing he wouldn’t do, to be revenged upon you. You don’t know him half as well as I do, Misses Brown. You’re never safe from him, I tell you.”\n\n“Haven’t I taken an oath,” retorted the old woman, “and won’t I keep it?”\n\n“Well, I’m sure I hope you will, Misses Brown,” returned Rob, somewhat doubtfully, and not without a latent threatening in his manner. “For your own sake, quite as much as mine.”\n\nHe looked at her as he gave her this friendly caution, and emphasized it with a nodding of his head; but finding it uncomfortable to encounter the yellow face with its grotesque action, and the ferret eyes with their keen old wintry gaze, so close to his own, he looked down uneasily and sat skulking in his chair, as if he were trying to bring himself to a sullen declaration that he would answer no more questions. The old woman, still holding him as before, took this opportunity of raising the forefinger of her right hand, in the air, as a stealthy signal to the concealed observer to give particular attention to what was about to follow.\n\n“Rob,” she said, in her most coaxing tone.\n\n“Good gracious, Misses Brown, what’s the matter now?” returned the exasperated Grinder.\n\n“Rob! where did the lady and Master appoint to meet?”\n\nRob shuffled more and more, and looked up and looked down, and bit his thumb, and dried it on his waistcoat, and finally said, eyeing his tormentor askance, “How should I know, Misses Brown?”\n\nThe old woman held up her finger again, as before, and replying, “Come, lad! It’s no use leading me to that, and there leaving me. I want to know” waited for his answer. Rob, after a discomfited pause, suddenly broke out with, “How can I pronounce the names of foreign places, Mrs Brown? What an unreasonable woman you are!”\n\n“But you have heard it said, Robby,” she retorted firmly, “and you know what it sounded like. Come!”\n\n“I never heard it said, Misses Brown,” returned the Grinder.\n\n“Then,” retorted the old woman quickly, “you have seen it written, and you can spell it.”\n\nRob, with a petulant exclamation between laughing and crying—for he was penetrated with some admiration of Mrs Brown’s cunning, even through this persecution—after some reluctant fumbling in his waistcoat pocket, produced from it a little piece of chalk. The old woman’s eyes sparkled when she saw it between his thumb and finger, and hastily clearing a space on the deal table, that he might write the word there, she once more made her signal with a shaking hand.\n\n“Now I tell you beforehand what it is, Misses Brown,” said Rob, “it’s no use asking me anything else. I won’t answer anything else; I can’t. How long it was to be before they met, or whose plan it was that they was to go away alone, I don’t know no more than you do. I don’t know any more about it. If I was to tell you how I found out this word, you’d believe that. Shall I tell you, Misses Brown?”\n\n“Yes, Rob.”\n\n“Well then, Misses Brown. The way—now you won’t ask any more, you know?” said Rob, turning his eyes, which were now fast getting drowsy and stupid, upon her.\n\n“Not another word,” said Mrs Brown.\n\n“Well then, the way was this. When a certain person left the lady with me, he put a piece of paper with a direction written on it in the lady’s hand, saying it was in case she should forget. She wasn’t afraid of forgetting, for she tore it up as soon as his back was turned, and when I put up the carriage steps, I shook out one of the pieces—she sprinkled the rest out of the window, I suppose, for there was none there afterwards, though I looked for ’em. There was only one word on it, and that was this, if you must and will know. But remember! You’re upon your oath, Misses Brown!”\n\nMrs Brown knew that, she said. Rob, having nothing more to say, began to chalk, slowly and laboriously, on the table.\n\n“‘D,’” the old woman read aloud, when he had formed the letter.\n\n“Will you hold your tongue, Misses Brown?” he exclaimed, covering it with his hand, and turning impatiently upon her. “I won’t have it read out. Be quiet, will you!”\n\n“Then write large, Rob,” she returned, repeating her secret signal; “for my eyes are not good, even at print.”\n\nMuttering to himself, and returning to his work with an ill will, Rob went on with the word. As he bent his head down, the person for whose information he so unconsciously laboured, moved from the door behind him to within a short stride of his shoulder, and looked eagerly towards the creeping track of his hand upon the table. At the same time, Alice, from her opposite chair, watched it narrowly as it shaped the letters, and repeated each one on her lips as he made it, without articulating it aloud. At the end of every letter her eyes and Mr Dombey’s met, as if each of them sought to be confirmed by the other; and thus they both spelt D.I.J.O.N.\n\n“There!” said the Grinder, moistening the palm of his hand hastily, to obliterate the word; and not content with smearing it out, rubbing and planing all trace of it away with his coat-sleeve, until the very colour of the chalk was gone from the table. “Now, I hope you’re contented, Misses Brown!”\n\nThe old woman, in token of her being so, released his arm and patted his back; and the Grinder, overcome with mortification, cross-examination, and liquor, folded his arms on the table, laid his head upon them, and fell asleep.\n\nNot until he had been heavily asleep some time, and was snoring roundly, did the old woman turn towards the door where Mr Dombey stood concealed, and beckon him to come through the room, and pass out. Even then, she hovered over Rob, ready to blind him with her hands, or strike his head down, if he should raise it while the secret step was crossing to the door. But though her glance took sharp cognizance of the sleeper, it was sharp too for the waking man; and when he touched her hand with his, and in spite of all his caution, made a chinking, golden sound, it was as bright and greedy as a raven’s.\n\nThe daughter’s dark gaze followed him to the door, and noted well how pale he was, and how his hurried tread indicated that the least delay was an insupportable restraint upon him, and how he was burning to be active and away. As he closed the door behind him, she looked round at her mother. The old woman trotted to her; opened her hand to show what was within; and, tightly closing it again in her jealousy and avarice, whispered:\n\n“What will he do, Ally?”\n\n“Mischief,” said the daughter.\n\n“Murder?” asked the old woman.\n\n“He’s a madman, in his wounded pride, and may do that, for anything we can say, or he either.”\n\nHer glance was brighter than her mother’s, and the fire that shone in it was fiercer; but her face was colourless, even to her lips.\n\nThey said no more, but sat apart; the mother communing with her money; the daughter with her thoughts; the glance of each, shining in the gloom of the feebly lighted room. Rob slept and snored. The disregarded parrot only was in action. It twisted and pulled at the wires of its cage, with its crooked beak, and crawled up to the dome, and along its roof like a fly, and down again head foremost, and shook, and bit, and rattled at every slender bar, as if it knew its master’s danger, and was wild to force a passage out, and fly away to warn him of it.\n\n# CHAPTER LIII. More Intelligence\n\nThere were two of the traitor’s own blood—his renounced brother and sister—on whom the weight of his guilt rested almost more heavily, at this time, than on the man whom he had so deeply injured. Prying and tormenting as the world was, it did Mr Dombey the service of nerving him to pursuit and revenge. It roused his passion, stung his pride, twisted the one idea of his life into a new shape, and made some gratification of his wrath, the object into which his whole intellectual existence resolved itself. All the stubbornness and implacability of his nature, all its hard impenetrable quality, all its gloom and moroseness, all its exaggerated sense of personal importance, all its jealous disposition to resent the least flaw in the ample recognition of his importance by others, set this way like many streams united into one, and bore him on upon their tide. The most impetuously passionate and violently impulsive of mankind would have been a milder enemy to encounter than the sullen Mr Dombey wrought to this. A wild beast would have been easier turned or soothed than the grave gentleman without a wrinkle in his starched cravat.\n\nBut the very intensity of his purpose became almost a substitute for action in it. While he was yet uninformed of the traitor’s retreat, it served to divert his mind from his own calamity, and to entertain it with another prospect. The brother and sister of his false favourite had no such relief; everything in their history, past and present, gave his delinquency a more afflicting meaning to them.\n\nThe sister may have sometimes sadly thought that if she had remained with him, the companion and friend she had been once, he might have escaped the crime into which he had fallen. If she ever thought so, it was still without regret for what she had done, without the least doubt of her duty, without any pricing or enhancing of her self-devotion. But when this possibility presented itself to the erring and repentant brother, as it sometimes did, it smote upon his heart with such a keen, reproachful touch as he could hardly bear. No idea of retort upon his cruel brother came into his mind. New accusation of himself, fresh inward lamentings over his own unworthiness, and the ruin in which it was at once his consolation and his self-reproach that he did not stand alone, were the sole kind of reflections to which the discovery gave rise in him.\n\nIt was on the very same day whose evening set upon the last chapter, and when Mr Dombey’s world was busiest with the elopement of his wife, that the window of the room in which the brother and sister sat at their early breakfast, was darkened by the unexpected shadow of a man coming to the little porch: which man was Perch the Messenger.\n\n“I’ve stepped over from Balls Pond at a early hour,” said Mr Perch, confidentially looking in at the room door, and stopping on the mat to wipe his shoes all round, which had no mud upon them, “agreeable to my instructions last night. They was, to be sure and bring a note to you, Mr Carker, before you went out in the morning. I should have been here a good hour and a half ago,” said Mr Perch, meekly, “but for the state of health of Mrs P., who I thought I should have lost in the night, I do assure you, five distinct times.”\n\n“Is your wife so ill?” asked Harriet.\n\n“Why, you see,” said Mr Perch, first turning round to shut the door carefully, “she takes what has happened in our House so much to heart, Miss. Her nerves is so very delicate, you see, and soon unstrung. Not but what the strongest nerves had good need to be shook, I’m sure. You feel it very much yourself, no doubts.”\n\nHarriet repressed a sigh, and glanced at her brother.\n\n“I’m sure I feel it myself, in my humble way,” Mr Perch went on to say, with a shake of his head, “in a manner I couldn’t have believed if I hadn’t been called upon to undergo. It has almost the effect of drink upon me. I literally feels every morning as if I had been taking more than was good for me over-night.”\n\nMr Perch’s appearance corroborated this recital of his symptoms. There was an air of feverish lassitude about it, that seemed referable to drams; and, which, in fact, might no doubt have been traced to those numerous discoveries of himself in the bars of public-houses, being treated and questioned, which he was in the daily habit of making.\n\n“Therefore I can judge,” said Mr Perch, shaking his head and speaking in a silvery murmur, “of the feelings of such as is at all peculiarly sitiwated in this most painful rewelation.”\n\nHere Mr Perch waited to be confided in; and receiving no confidence, coughed behind his hand. This leading to nothing, he coughed behind his hat; and that leading to nothing, he put his hat on the ground and sought in his breast pocket for the letter.\n\n“If I rightly recollect, there was no answer,” said Mr Perch, with an affable smile; “but perhaps you’ll be so good as cast your eye over it, Sir.”\n\nJohn Carker broke the seal, which was Mr Dombey’s, and possessing himself of the contents, which were very brief, replied, “No. No answer is expected.”\n\n“Then I shall wish you good morning, Miss,” said Perch, taking a step toward the door, and hoping, I’m sure, that you’ll not permit yourself to be more reduced in mind than you can help, by the late painful rewelation. The Papers,” said Mr Perch, taking two steps back again, and comprehensively addressing both the brother and sister in a whisper of increased mystery, “is more eager for news of it than you’d suppose possible. One of the Sunday ones, in a blue cloak and a white hat, that had previously offered for to bribe me—need I say with what success?—was dodging about our court last night as late as twenty minutes after eight o’clock. I see him myself, with his eye at the counting-house keyhole, which being patent is impervious. Another one,” said Mr Perch, “with military frogs, is in the parlour of the King’s Arms all the blessed day. I happened, last week, to let a little obserwation fall there, and next morning, which was Sunday, I see it worked up in print, in a most surprising manner.”\n\nMr Perch resorted to his breast pocket, as if to produce the paragraph but receiving no encouragement, pulled out his beaver gloves, picked up his hat, and took his leave; and before it was high noon, Mr Perch had related to several select audiences at the King’s Arms and elsewhere, how Miss Carker, bursting into tears, had caught him by both hands, and said, “Oh! dear dear Perch, the sight of you is all the comfort I have left!” and how Mr John Carker had said, in an awful voice, “Perch, I disown him. Never let me hear him mentioned as a brother more!”\n\n“Dear John,” said Harriet, when they were left alone, and had remained silent for some few moments. “There are bad tidings in that letter.”\n\n“Yes. But nothing unexpected,” he replied. “I saw the writer yesterday.”\n\n“The writer?”\n\n“Mr Dombey. He passed twice through the Counting House while I was there. I had been able to avoid him before, but of course could not hope to do that long. I know how natural it was that he should regard my presence as something offensive; I felt it must be so, myself.”\n\n“He did not say so?”\n\n“No; he said nothing: but I saw that his glance rested on me for a moment, and I was prepared for what would happen—for what has happened. I am dismissed!”\n\nShe looked as little shocked and as hopeful as she could, but it was distressing news, for many reasons.\n\n“‘I need not tell you,’” said John Carker, reading the letter, “‘why your name would henceforth have an unnatural sound, in however remote a connexion with mine, or why the daily sight of anyone who bears it, would be unendurable to me. I have to notify the cessation of all engagements between us, from this date, and to request that no renewal of any communication with me, or my establishment, be ever attempted by you.’—Enclosed is an equivalent in money to a generously long notice, and this is my discharge. Heaven knows, Harriet, it is a lenient and considerate one, when we remember all!”\n\n“If it be lenient and considerate to punish you at all, John, for the misdeed of another,” she replied gently, “yes.”\n\n“We have been an ill-omened race to him,” said John Carker. “He has reason to shrink from the sound of our name, and to think that there is something cursed and wicked in our blood. I should almost think it too, Harriet, but for you.”\n\n“Brother, don’t speak like this. If you have any special reason, as you say you have, and think you have—though I say, No!—to love me, spare me the hearing of such wild mad words!”\n\nHe covered his face with both his hands; but soon permitted her, coming near him, to take one in her own.\n\n“After so many years, this parting is a melancholy thing, I know,” said his sister, “and the cause of it is dreadful to us both. We have to live, too, and must look about us for the means. Well, well! We can do so, undismayed. It is our pride, not our trouble, to strive, John, and to strive together!”\n\nA smile played on her lips, as she kissed his cheek, and entreated him to be of good cheer.\n\n“Oh, dearest sister! Tied, of your own noble will, to a ruined man! whose reputation is blighted; who has no friend himself, and has driven every friend of yours away!”\n\n“John!” she laid her hand hastily upon his lips, “for my sake! In remembrance of our long companionship!” He was silent “Now, let me tell you, dear,” quietly sitting by his side, “I have, as you have, expected this; and when I have been thinking of it, and fearing that it would happen, and preparing myself for it, as well as I could, I have resolved to tell you, if it should be so, that I have kept a secret from you, and that we have a friend.”\n\n“What’s our friend’s name, Harriet?” he answered with a sorrowful smile.\n\n“Indeed, I don’t know, but he once made a very earnest protestation to me of his friendship and his wish to serve us: and to this day I believe him.”\n\n“Harriet!” exclaimed her wondering brother, “where does this friend live?”\n\n“Neither do I know that,” she returned. “But he knows us both, and our history—all our little history, John. That is the reason why, at his own suggestion, I have kept the secret of his coming, here, from you, lest his acquaintance with it should distress you.”\n\n“Here! Has he been here, Harriet?”\n\n“Here, in this room. Once.”\n\n“What kind of man?”\n\n“Not young. ‘Grey-headed,’ as he said, ‘and fast growing greyer.’ But generous, and frank, and good, I am sure.”\n\n“And only seen once, Harriet?”\n\n“In this room only once,” said his sister, with the slightest and most transient glow upon her cheek; “but when here, he entreated me to suffer him to see me once a week as he passed by, in token of our being well, and continuing to need nothing at his hands. For I told him, when he proffered us any service he could render—which was the object of his visit—that we needed nothing.”\n\n“And once a week—”\n\n“Once every week since then, and always on the same day, and at the same hour, he his gone past; always on foot; always going in the same direction—towards London; and never pausing longer than to bow to me, and wave his hand cheerfully, as a kind guardian might. He made that promise when he proposed these curious interviews, and has kept it so faithfully and pleasantly, that if I ever felt any trifling uneasiness about them in the beginning (which I don’t think I did, John; his manner was so plain and true) it very soon vanished, and left me quite glad when the day was coming. Last Monday—the first since this terrible event—he did not go by; and I have wondered whether his absence can have been in any way connected with what has happened.”\n\n“How?” inquired her brother.\n\n“I don’t know how. I have only speculated on the coincidence; I have not tried to account for it. I feel sure he will return. When he does, dear John, let me tell him that I have at last spoken to you, and let me bring you together. He will certainly help us to a new livelihood. His entreaty was that he might do something to smooth my life and yours; and I gave him my promise that if we ever wanted a friend, I would remember him. Then his name was to be no secret.”\n\n“Harriet,” said her brother, who had listened with close attention, “describe this gentleman to me. I surely ought to know one who knows me so well.”\n\nHis sister painted, as vividly as she could, the features, stature, and dress of her visitor; but John Carker, either from having no knowledge of the original, or from some fault in her description, or from some abstraction of his thoughts as he walked to and fro, pondering, could not recognise the portrait she presented to him.\n\nHowever, it was agreed between them that he should see the original when he next appeared. This concluded, the sister applied herself, with a less anxious breast, to her domestic occupations; and the grey-haired man, late Junior of Dombey’s, devoted the first day of his unwonted liberty to working in the garden.\n\nIt was quite late at night, and the brother was reading aloud while the sister plied her needle, when they were interrupted by a knocking at the door. In the atmosphere of vague anxiety and dread that lowered about them in connexion with their fugitive brother, this sound, unusual there, became almost alarming. The brother going to the door, the sister sat and listened timidly. Someone spoke to him, and he replied and seemed surprised; and after a few words, the two approached together.\n\n“Harriet,” said her brother, lighting in their late visitor, and speaking in a low voice, “Mr Morfin—the gentleman so long in Dombey’s House with James.”\n\nHis sister started back, as if a ghost had entered. In the doorway stood the unknown friend, with the dark hair sprinkled with grey, the ruddy face, the broad clear brow, and hazel eyes, whose secret she had kept so long!\n\n“John!” she said, half-breathless. “It is the gentleman I told you of, today!”\n\n“The gentleman, Miss Harriet,” said the visitor, coming in—for he had stopped a moment in the doorway—“is greatly relieved to hear you say that: he has been devising ways and means, all the way here, of explaining himself, and has been satisfied with none. Mr John, I am not quite a stranger here. You were stricken with astonishment when you saw me at your door just now. I observe you are more astonished at present. Well! That’s reasonable enough under existing circumstances. If we were not such creatures of habit as we are, we shouldn’t have reason to be astonished half so often.”\n\nBy this time, he had greeted Harriet with that able mingling of cordiality and respect which she recollected so well, and had sat down near her, pulled off his gloves, and thrown them into his hat upon the table.\n\n“There’s nothing astonishing,” he said, “in my having conceived a desire to see your sister, Mr John, or in my having gratified it in my own way. As to the regularity of my visits since (which she may have mentioned to you), there is nothing extraordinary in that. They soon grew into a habit; and we are creatures of habit—creatures of habit!”\n\nPutting his hands into his pockets, and leaning back in his chair, he looked at the brother and sister as if it were interesting to him to see them together; and went on to say, with a kind of irritable thoughtfulness: “It’s this same habit that confirms some of us, who are capable of better things, in Lucifer’s own pride and stubbornness—that confirms and deepens others of us in villainy—more of us in indifference —that hardens us from day to day, according to the temper of our clay, like images, and leaves us as susceptible as images to new impressions and convictions. You shall judge of its influence on me, John. For more years than I need name, I had my small, and exactly defined share, in the management of Dombey’s House, and saw your brother (who has proved himself a scoundrel! Your sister will forgive my being obliged to mention it) extending and extending his influence, until the business and its owner were his football; and saw you toiling at your obscure desk every day; and was quite content to be as little troubled as I might be, out of my own strip of duty, and to let everything about me go on, day by day, unquestioned, like a great machine—that was its habit and mine—and to take it all for granted, and consider it all right. My Wednesday nights came regularly round, our quartette parties came regularly off, my violoncello was in good tune, and there was nothing wrong in my world—or if anything not much—or little or much, it was no affair of mine.”\n\n“I can answer for your being more respected and beloved during all that time than anybody in the House, Sir,” said John Carker.\n\n“Pooh! Good-natured and easy enough, I daresay,” returned the other, “a habit I had. It suited the Manager; it suited the man he managed: it suited me best of all. I did what was allotted to me to do, made no court to either of them, and was glad to occupy a station in which none was required. So I should have gone on till now, but that my room had a thin wall. You can tell your sister that it was divided from the Manager’s room by a wainscot partition.”\n\n“They were adjoining rooms; had been one, Perhaps, originally; and were separated, as Mr Morfin says,” said her brother, looking back to him for the resumption of his explanation.\n\n“I have whistled, hummed tunes, gone accurately through the whole of Beethoven’s Sonata in B, to let him know that I was within hearing,” said Mr Morfin; “but he never heeded me. It happened seldom enough that I was within hearing of anything of a private nature, certainly. But when I was, and couldn’t otherwise avoid knowing something of it, I walked out. I walked out once, John, during a conversation between two brothers, to which, in the beginning, young Walter Gay was a party. But I overheard some of it before I left the room. You remember it sufficiently, perhaps, to tell your sister what its nature was?”\n\n“It referred, Harriet,” said her brother in a low voice, “to the past, and to our relative positions in the House.”\n\n“Its matter was not new to me, but was presented in a new aspect. It shook me in my habit—the habit of nine-tenths of the world—of believing that all was right about me, because I was used to it,” said their visitor; “and induced me to recall the history of the two brothers, and to ponder on it. I think it was almost the first time in my life when I fell into this train of reflection—how will many things that are familiar, and quite matters of course to us now, look, when we come to see them from that new and distant point of view which we must all take up, one day or other? I was something less good-natured, as the phrase goes, after that morning, less easy and complacent altogether.”\n\nHe sat for a minute or so, drumming with one hand on the table; and resumed in a hurry, as if he were anxious to get rid of his confession.\n\n“Before I knew what to do, or whether I could do anything, there was a second conversation between the same two brothers, in which their sister was mentioned. I had no scruples of conscience in suffering all the waifs and strays of that conversation to float to me as freely as they would. I considered them mine by right. After that, I came here to see the sister for myself. The first time I stopped at the garden gate, I made a pretext of inquiring into the character of a poor neighbour; but I wandered out of that tract, and I think Miss Harriet mistrusted me. The second time I asked leave to come in; came in; and said what I wished to say. Your sister showed me reasons which I dared not dispute, for receiving no assistance from me then; but I established a means of communication between us, which remained unbroken until within these few days, when I was prevented, by important matters that have lately devolved upon me, from maintaining them.”\n\n“How little I have suspected this,” said John Carker, “when I have seen you every day, Sir! If Harriet could have guessed your name—”\n\n“Why, to tell you the truth, John,” interposed the visitor, “I kept it to myself for two reasons. I don’t know that the first might have been binding alone; but one has no business to take credit for good intentions, and I made up my mind, at all events, not to disclose myself until I should be able to do you some real service or other. My second reason was, that I always hoped there might be some lingering possibility of your brother’s relenting towards you both; and in that case, I felt that where there was the chance of a man of his suspicious, watchful character, discovering that you had been secretly befriended by me, there was the chance of a new and fatal cause of division. I resolved, to be sure, at the risk of turning his displeasure against myself—which would have been no matter—to watch my opportunity of serving you with the head of the House; but the distractions of death, courtship, marriage, and domestic unhappiness, have left us no head but your brother for this long, long time. And it would have been better for us,” said the visitor, dropping his voice, “to have been a lifeless trunk.”\n\nHe seemed conscious that these latter words had escaped him against his will, and stretching out a hand to the brother, and a hand to the sister, continued:\n\n“All I could desire to say, and more, I have now said. All I mean goes beyond words, as I hope you understand and believe. The time has come, John—though most unfortunately and unhappily come—when I may help you without interfering with that redeeming struggle, which has lasted through so many years; since you were discharged from it today by no act of your own. It is late; I need say no more tonight. You will guard the treasure you have here, without advice or reminder from me.”\n\nWith these words he rose to go.\n\n“But go you first, John,” he said goodhumouredly, “with a light, without saying what you want to say, whatever that may be;” John Carker’s heart was full, and he would have relieved it in speech, if he could; “and let me have a word with your sister. We have talked alone before, and in this room too; though it looks more natural with you here.”\n\nFollowing him out with his eyes, he turned kindly to Harriet, and said in a lower voice, and with an altered and graver manner:\n\n“You wish to ask me something of the man whose sister it is your misfortune to be.”\n\n“I dread to ask,” said Harriet.\n\n“You have looked so earnestly at me more than once,” rejoined the visitor, “that I think I can divine your question. Has he taken money? Is it that?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“He has not.”\n\n“I thank Heaven!” said Harriet. “For the sake of John.”\n\n“That he has abused his trust in many ways,” said Mr Morfin; “that he has oftener dealt and speculated to advantage for himself, than for the House he represented; that he has led the House on, to prodigious ventures, often resulting in enormous losses; that he has always pampered the vanity and ambition of his employer, when it was his duty to have held them in check, and shown, as it was in his power to do, to what they tended here or there; will not, perhaps, surprise you now. Undertakings have been entered on, to swell the reputation of the House for vast resources, and to exhibit it in magnificent contrast to other merchants’ Houses, of which it requires a steady head to contemplate the possibly—a few disastrous changes of affairs might render them the probably—ruinous consequences. In the midst of the many transactions of the House, in most parts of the world: a great labyrinth of which only he has held the clue: he has had the opportunity, and he seems to have used it, of keeping the various results afloat, when ascertained, and substituting estimates and generalities for facts. But latterly—you follow me, Miss Harriet?”\n\n“Perfectly, perfectly,” she answered, with her frightened face fixed on his. “Pray tell me all the worst at once.”\n\n“Latterly, he appears to have devoted the greatest pains to making these results so plain and clear, that reference to the private books enables one to grasp them, numerous and varying as they are, with extraordinary ease. As if he had resolved to show his employer at one broad view what has been brought upon him by ministration to his ruling passion! That it has been his constant practice to minister to that passion basely, and to flatter it corruptly, is indubitable. In that, his criminality, as it is connected with the affairs of the House, chiefly consists.”\n\n“One other word before you leave me, dear Sir,” said Harriet. “There is no danger in all this?”\n\n“How danger?” he returned, with a little hesitation.\n\n“To the credit of the House?”\n\n“I cannot help answering you plainly, and trusting you completely,” said Mr Morfin, after a moment’s survey of her face.\n\n“You may. Indeed you may!”\n\n“I am sure I may. Danger to the House’s credit? No; none There may be difficulty, greater or less difficulty, but no danger, unless—unless, indeed—the head of the House, unable to bring his mind to the reduction of its enterprises, and positively refusing to believe that it is, or can be, in any position but the position in which he has always represented it to himself, should urge it beyond its strength. Then it would totter.”\n\n“But there is no apprehension of that?” said Harriet.\n\n“There shall be no half-confidence,” he replied, shaking her hand, “between us. Mr Dombey is unapproachable by anyone, and his state of mind is haughty, rash, unreasonable, and ungovernable, now. But he is disturbed and agitated now beyond all common bounds, and it may pass. You now know all, both worst and best. No more tonight, and good-night!”\n\nWith that he kissed her hand, and, passing out to the door where her brother stood awaiting his coming, put him cheerfully aside when he essayed to speak; told him that, as they would see each other soon and often, he might speak at another time, if he would, but there was no leisure for it then; and went away at a round pace, in order that no word of gratitude might follow him.\n\nThe brother and sister sat conversing by the fireside, until it was almost day; made sleepless by this glimpse of the new world that opened before them, and feeling like two people shipwrecked long ago, upon a solitary coast, to whom a ship had come at last, when they were old in resignation, and had lost all thought of any other home. But another and different kind of disquietude kept them waking too. The darkness out of which this light had broken on them gathered around; and the shadow of their guilty brother was in the house where his foot had never trod.\n\nNor was it to be driven out, nor did it fade before the sun. Next morning it was there; at noon; at night Darkest and most distinct at night, as is now to be told.\n\nJohn Carker had gone out, in pursuance of a letter of appointment from their friend, and Harriet was left in the house alone. She had been alone some hours. A dull, grave evening, and a deepening twilight, were not favourable to the removal of the oppression on her spirits. The idea of this brother, long unseen and unknown, flitted about her in frightful shapes. He was dead, dying, calling to her, staring at her, frowning on her. The pictures in her mind were so obtrusive and exact that, as the twilight deepened, she dreaded to raise her head and look at the dark corners of the room, lest his wraith, the offspring of her excited imagination, should be waiting there, to startle her. Once she had such a fancy of his being in the next room, hiding—though she knew quite well what a distempered fancy it was, and had no belief in it—that she forced herself to go there, for her own conviction. But in vain. The room resumed its shadowy terrors, the moment she left it; and she had no more power to divest herself of these vague impressions of dread, than if they had been stone giants, rooted in the solid earth.\n\nIt was almost dark, and she was sitting near the window, with her head upon her hand, looking down, when, sensible of a sudden increase in the gloom of the apartment, she raised her eyes, and uttered an involuntary cry. Close to the glass, a pale scared face gazed in; vacantly, for an instant, as searching for an object; then the eyes rested on herself, and lighted up.\n\n“Let me in! Let me in! I want to speak to you!” and the hand rattled on the glass.\n\nShe recognised immediately the woman with the long dark hair, to whom she had given warmth, food, and shelter, one wet night. Naturally afraid of her, remembering her violent behaviour, Harriet, retreating a little from the window, stood undecided and alarmed.\n\n“Let me in! Let me speak to you! I am thankful—quiet—humble—anything you like. But let me speak to you.”\n\nThe vehement manner of the entreaty, the earnest expression of the face, the trembling of the two hands that were raised imploringly, a certain dread and terror in the voice akin to her own condition at the moment, prevailed with Harriet. She hastened to the door and opened it.\n\n“May I come in, or shall I speak here?” said the woman, catching at her hand.\n\n“What is it that you want? What is it that you have to say?”\n\n“Not much, but let me say it out, or I shall never say it. I am tempted now to go away. There seem to be hands dragging me from the door. Let me come in, if you can trust me for this once!”\n\nHer energy again prevailed, and they passed into the firelight of the little kitchen, where she had before sat, and ate, and dried her clothes.\n\n“Sit there,” said Alice, kneeling down beside her, “and look at me. You remember me?”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“You remember what I told you I had been, and where I came from, ragged and lame, with the fierce wind and weather beating on my head?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You know how I came back that night, and threw your money in the dirt, and you and your race. Now, see me here, upon my knees. Am I less earnest now, than I was then?”\n\n“If what you ask,” said Harriet, gently, “is forgiveness—”\n\n“But it’s not!” returned the other, with a proud, fierce look “What I ask is to be believed. Now you shall judge if I am worthy of belief, both as I was, and as I am.”\n\nStill upon her knees, and with her eyes upon the fire, and the fire shining on her ruined beauty and her wild black hair, one long tress of which she pulled over her shoulder, and wound about her hand, and thoughtfully bit and tore while speaking, she went on:\n\n“When I was young and pretty, and this,” plucking contemptuously at the hair she held, “was only handled delicately, and couldn’t be admired enough, my mother, who had not been very mindful of me as a child, found out my merits, and was fond of me, and proud of me. She was covetous and poor, and thought to make a sort of property of me. No great lady ever thought that of a daughter yet, I’m sure, or acted as if she did—it’s never done, we all know—and that shows that the only instances of mothers bringing up their daughters wrong, and evil coming of it, are among such miserable folks as us.”\n\nLooking at the fire, as if she were forgetful, for the moment, of having any auditor, she continued in a dreamy way, as she wound the long tress of hair tight round and round her hand.\n\n“What came of that, I needn’t say. Wretched marriages don’t come of such things, in our degree; only wretchedness and ruin. Wretchedness and ruin came on me—came on me.”\n\nRaising her eyes swiftly from their moody gaze upon the fire, to Harriet’s face, she said:\n\n“I am wasting time, and there is none to spare; yet if I hadn’t thought of all, I shouldn’t be here now. Wretchedness and ruin came on me, I say. I was made a short-lived toy, and flung aside more cruelly and carelessly than even such things are. By whose hand do you think?”\n\n“Why do you ask me?” said Harriet.\n\n“Why do you tremble?” rejoined Alice, with an eager look. “His usage made a Devil of me. I sunk in wretchedness and ruin, lower and lower yet. I was concerned in a robbery—in every part of it but the gains—and was found out, and sent to be tried, without a friend, without a penny. Though I was but a girl, I would have gone to Death, sooner than ask him for a word, if a word of his could have saved me. I would! To any death that could have been invented. But my mother, covetous always, sent to him in my name, told the true story of my case, and humbly prayed and petitioned for a small last gift—for not so many pounds as I have fingers on this hand. Who was it, do you think, who snapped his fingers at me in my misery, lying, as he believed, at his feet, and left me without even this poor sign of remembrance; well satisfied that I should be sent abroad, beyond the reach of farther trouble to him, and should die, and rot there? Who was this, do you think?”\n\n“Why do you ask me?” repeated Harriet.\n\n“Why do you tremble?” said Alice, laying her hand upon her arm, and looking in her face, “but that the answer is on your lips! It was your brother James.”\n\nHarriet trembled more and more, but did not avert her eyes from the eager look that rested on them.\n\n“When I knew you were his sister—which was on that night—I came back, weary and lame, to spurn your gift. I felt that night as if I could have travelled, weary and lame, over the whole world, to stab him, if I could have found him in a lonely place with no one near. Do you believe that I was earnest in all that?”\n\n“I do! Good Heaven, why are you come again?”\n\n“Since then,” said Alice, with the same grasp of her arm, and the same look in her face, “I have seen him! I have followed him with my eyes. In the broad day. If any spark of my resentment slumbered in my bosom, it sprung into a blaze when my eyes rested on him. You know he has wronged a proud man, and made him his deadly enemy. What if I had given information of him to that man?”\n\n“Information!” repeated Harriet.\n\n“What if I had found out one who knew your brother’s secret; who knew the manner of his flight, who knew where he and the companion of his flight were gone? What if I had made him utter all his knowledge, word by word, before his enemy, concealed to hear it? What if I had sat by at the time, looking into this enemy’s face, and seeing it change till it was scarcely human? What if I had seen him rush away, mad, in pursuit? What if I knew, now, that he was on his road, more fiend than man, and must, in so many hours, come up with him?”\n\n“Remove your hand!” said Harriet, recoiling. “Go away! Your touch is dreadful to me!”\n\n“I have done this,” pursued the other, with her eager look, regardless of the interruption. “Do I speak and look as if I really had? Do you believe what I am saying?”\n\n“I fear I must. Let my arm go!”\n\n“Not yet. A moment more. You can think what my revengeful purpose must have been, to last so long, and urge me to do this?”\n\n“Dreadful!” said Harriet.\n\n“Then when you see me now,” said Alice hoarsely, “here again, kneeling quietly on the ground, with my touch upon your arm, with my eyes upon your face, you may believe that there is no common earnestness in what I say, and that no common struggle has been battling in my breast. I am ashamed to speak the words, but I relent. I despise myself; I have fought with myself all day, and all last night; but I relent towards him without reason, and wish to repair what I have done, if it is possible. I wouldn’t have them come together while his pursuer is so blind and headlong. If you had seen him as he went out last night, you would know the danger better.”\n\n“How can it be prevented? What can I do?” cried Harriet.\n\n“All night long,” pursued the other, hurriedly, “I had dreams of him—and yet I didn’t sleep—in his blood. All day, I have had him near me.”\n\n“What can I do?” cried Harriet, shuddering at these words.\n\n“If there is anyone who’ll write, or send, or go to him, let them lose no time. He is at Dijon. Do you know the name, and where it is?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Warn him that the man he has made his enemy is in a frenzy, and that he doesn’t know him if he makes light of his approach. Tell him that he is on the road—I know he is!—and hurrying on. Urge him to get away while there is time—if there is time—and not to meet him yet. A month or so will make years of difference. Let them not encounter, through me. Anywhere but there! Any time but now! Let his foe follow him, and find him for himself, but not through me! There is enough upon my head without.”\n\nThe fire ceased to be reflected in her jet black hair, uplifted face, and eager eyes; her hand was gone from Harriet’s arm; and the place where she had been was empty.\n\n# CHAPTER LIV. The Fugitives\n\nTea-time, an hour short of midnight; the place, a French apartment, comprising some half-dozen rooms;—a dull cold hall or corridor, a dining-room, a drawing-room, a bed-room, and an inner drawingroom, or boudoir, smaller and more retired than the rest. All these shut in by one large pair of doors on the main staircase, but each room provided with two or three pairs of doors of its own, establishing several means of communication with the remaining portion of the apartment, or with certain small passages within the wall, leading, as is not unusual in such houses, to some back stairs with an obscure outlet below. The whole situated on the first floor of so large an Hotel, that it did not absorb one entire row of windows upon one side of the square court-yard in the centre, upon which the whole four sides of the mansion looked.\n\nAn air of splendour, sufficiently faded to be melancholy, and sufficiently dazzling to clog and embarrass the details of life with a show of state, reigned in these rooms. The walls and ceilings were gilded and painted; the floors were waxed and polished; crimson drapery hung in festoons from window, door, and mirror; and candelabra, gnarled and intertwisted like the branches of trees, or horns of animals, stuck out from the panels of the wall. But in the day-time, when the lattice-blinds (now closely shut) were opened, and the light let in, traces were discernible among this finery, of wear and tear and dust, of sun and damp and smoke, and lengthened intervals of want of use and habitation, when such shows and toys of life seem sensitive like life, and waste as men shut up in prison do. Even night, and clusters of burning candles, could not wholly efface them, though the general glitter threw them in the shade.\n\nThe glitter of bright tapers, and their reflection in looking-glasses, scraps of gilding and gay colours, were confined, on this night, to one room—that smaller room within the rest, just now enumerated. Seen from the hall, where a lamp was feebly burning, through the dark perspective of open doors, it looked as shining and precious as a gem. In the heart of its radiance sat a beautiful woman—Edith.\n\nShe was alone. The same defiant, scornful woman still. The cheek a little worn, the eye a little larger in appearance, and more lustrous, but the haughty bearing just the same. No shame upon her brow; no late repentance bending her disdainful neck. Imperious and stately yet, and yet regardless of herself and of all else, she sat with her dark eyes cast down, waiting for someone.\n\nNo book, no work, no occupation of any kind but her own thought, beguiled the tardy time. Some purpose, strong enough to fill up any pause, possessed her. With her lips pressed together, and quivering if for a moment she released them from her control; with her nostril inflated; her hands clasped in one another; and her purpose swelling in her breast; she sat, and waited.\n\nAt the sound of a key in the outer door, and a footstep in the hall, she started up, and cried “Who’s that?” The answer was in French, and two men came in with jingling trays, to make preparation for supper.\n\n“Who had bade them to do so?” she asked.\n\n“Monsieur had commanded it, when it was his pleasure to take the apartment. Monsieur had said, when he stayed there for an hour, en route, and left the letter for Madame—Madame had received it surely?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“A thousand pardons! The sudden apprehension that it might have been forgotten had struck him;” a bald man, with a large beard from a neighbouring restaurant; “with despair! Monsieur had said that supper was to be ready at that hour: also that he had forewarned Madame of the commands he had given, in his letter. Monsieur had done the Golden Head the honour to request that the supper should be choice and delicate. Monsieur would find that his confidence in the Golden Head was not misplaced.”\n\nEdith said no more, but looked on thoughtfully while they prepared the table for two persons, and set the wine upon it. She arose before they had finished, and taking a lamp, passed into the bed-chamber and into the drawing-room, where she hurriedly but narrowly examined all the doors; particularly one in the former room that opened on the passage in the wall. From this she took the key, and put it on the outer side. She then came back.\n\nThe men—the second of whom was a dark, bilious subject, in a jacket, close shaved, and with a black head of hair close cropped—had completed their preparation of the table, and were standing looking at it. He who had spoken before, inquired whether Madame thought it would be long before Monsieur arrived?\n\n“She couldn’t say. It was all one.”\n\n“Pardon! There was the supper! It should be eaten on the instant. Monsieur (who spoke French like an Angel—or a Frenchman—it was all the same) had spoken with great emphasis of his punctuality. But the English nation had so grand a genius for punctuality. Ah! what noise! Great Heaven, here was Monsieur. Behold him!”\n\nIn effect, Monsieur, admitted by the other of the two, came, with his gleaming teeth, through the dark rooms, like a mouth; and arriving in that sanctuary of light and colour, a figure at full length, embraced Madame, and addressed her in the French tongue as his charming wife.\n\n“My God! Madame is going to faint. Madame is overcome with joy!” The bald man with the beard observed it, and cried out.\n\nMadame had only shrunk and shivered. Before the words were spoken, she was standing with her hand upon the velvet back of a great chair; her figure drawn up to its full height, and her face immoveable.\n\n“Francois has flown over to the Golden Head for supper. He flies on these occasions like an angel or a bird. The baggage of Monsieur is in his room. All is arranged. The supper will be here this moment.” These facts the bald man notified with bows and smiles, and presently the supper came.\n\nThe hot dishes were on a chafing-dish; the cold already set forth, with the change of service on a sideboard. Monsieur was satisfied with this arrangement. The supper table being small, it pleased him very well. Let them set the chafing-dish upon the floor, and go. He would remove the dishes with his own hands.\n\n“Pardon!” said the bald man, politely. “It was impossible!”\n\nMonsieur was of another opinion. He required no further attendance that night.\n\n“But Madame—” the bald man hinted.\n\n“Madame,” replied Monsieur, “had her own maid. It was enough.”\n\n“A million pardons! No! Madame had no maid!”\n\n“I came here alone,” said Edith “It was my choice to do so. I am well used to travelling; I want no attendance. They need send nobody to me.\n\nMonsieur accordingly, persevering in his first proposed impossibility, proceeded to follow the two attendants to the outer door, and secure it after them for the night. The bald man turning round to bow, as he went out, observed that Madame still stood with her hand upon the velvet back of the great chair, and that her face was quite regardless of him, though she was looking straight before her.\n\nAs the sound of Carker’s fastening the door resounded through the intermediate rooms, and seemed to come hushed and stilled into that last distant one, the sound of the Cathedral clock striking twelve mingled with it, in Edith’s ears. She heard him pause, as if he heard it too and listened; and then came back towards her, laying a long train of footsteps through the silence, and shutting all the doors behind him as he came along. Her hand, for a moment, left the velvet chair to bring a knife within her reach upon the table; then she stood as she had stood before.\n\n“How strange to come here by yourself, my love!” he said as he entered.\n\n“What?” she returned.\n\nHer tone was so harsh; the quick turn of her head so fierce; her attitude so repellent; and her frown so black; that he stood, with the lamp in his hand, looking at her, as if she had struck him motionless.\n\n“I say,” he at length repeated, putting down the lamp, and smiling his most courtly smile, “how strange to come here alone! It was unnecessary caution surely, and might have defeated itself. You were to have engaged an attendant at Havre or Rouen, and have had abundance of time for the purpose, though you had been the most capricious and difficult (as you are the most beautiful, my love) of women.”\n\nHer eyes gleamed strangely on him, but she stood with her hand resting on the chair, and said not a word.\n\n“I have never,” resumed Carker, “seen you look so handsome, as you do tonight. Even the picture I have carried in my mind during this cruel probation, and which I have contemplated night and day, is exceeded by the reality.”\n\nNot a word. Not a look Her eyes completely hidden by their drooping lashes, but her head held up.\n\n“Hard, unrelenting terms they were!” said Carker, with a smile, “but they are all fulfilled and passed, and make the present more delicious and more safe. Sicily shall be the place of our retreat. In the idlest and easiest part of the world, my soul, we’ll both seek compensation for old slavery.”\n\nHe was coming gaily towards her, when, in an instant, she caught the knife up from the table, and started one pace back.\n\n“Stand still!” she said, “or I shall murder you!”\n\nThe sudden change in her, the towering fury and intense abhorrence sparkling in her eyes and lighting up her brow, made him stop as if a fire had stopped him.\n\n“Stand still!” she said, “come no nearer me, upon your life!”\n\nThey both stood looking at each other. Rage and astonishment were in his face, but he controlled them, and said lightly,\n\n“Come, come! Tush, we are alone, and out of everybody’s sight and hearing. Do you think to frighten me with these tricks of virtue?”\n\n“Do you think to frighten me,” she answered fiercely, “from any purpose that I have, and any course I am resolved upon, by reminding me of the solitude of this place, and there being no help near? Me, who am here alone, designedly? If I feared you, should I not have avoided you? If I feared you, should I be here, in the dead of night, telling you to your face what I am going to tell?”\n\n“And what is that,” he said, “you handsome shrew? Handsomer so, than any other woman in her best humour?”\n\n“I tell you nothing,” she returned, until you go back to that chair—except this, once again—Don’t come near me! Not a step nearer. I tell you, if you do, as Heaven sees us, I shall murder you!”\n\n“Do you mistake me for your husband?” he retorted, with a grin.\n\nDisdaining to reply, she stretched her arm out, pointing to the chair. He bit his lip, frowned, laughed, and sat down in it, with a baffled, irresolute, impatient air, he was unable to conceal; and biting his nail nervously, and looking at her sideways, with bitter discomfiture, even while he feigned to be amused by her caprice.\n\nShe put the knife down upon the table, and touching her bosom with her hand, said:\n\n“I have something lying here that is no love trinket, and sooner than endure your touch once more, I would use it on you—and you know it, while I speak—with less reluctance than I would on any other creeping thing that lives.”\n\nHe affected to laugh jestingly, and entreated her to act her play out quickly, for the supper was growing cold. But the secret look with which he regarded her, was more sullen and lowering, and he struck his foot once upon the floor with a muttered oath.\n\n“How many times,” said Edith, bending her darkest glance upon him, “has your bold knavery assailed me with outrage and insult? How many times in your smooth manner, and mocking words and looks, have I been twitted with my courtship and my marriage? How many times have you laid bare my wound of love for that sweet, injured girl and lacerated it? How often have you fanned the fire on which, for two years, I have writhed; and tempted me to take a desperate revenge, when it has most tortured me?”\n\n“I have no doubt, Ma’am,” he replied, “that you have kept a good account, and that it’s pretty accurate. Come, Edith. To your husband, poor wretch, this was well enough—”\n\n“Why, if,” she said, surveying him with a haughty contempt and disgust, that he shrunk under, let him brave it as he would, “if all my other reasons for despising him could have been blown away like feathers, his having you for his counsellor and favourite, would have almost been enough to hold their place.”\n\n“Is that a reason why you have run away with me?” he asked her, tauntingly.\n\n“Yes, and why we are face to face for the last time. Wretch! We meet tonight, and part tonight. For not one moment after I have ceased to speak, will I stay here!”\n\nHe turned upon her with his ugliest look, and gripped the table with his hand; but neither rose, nor otherwise answered or threatened her.\n\n“I am a woman,” she said, confronting him steadfastly, “who from her childhood has been shamed and steeled. I have been offered and rejected, put up and appraised, until my very soul has sickened. I have not had an accomplishment or grace that might have been a resource to me, but it has been paraded and vended to enhance my value, as if the common crier had called it through the streets. My poor, proud friends, have looked on and approved; and every tie between us has been deadened in my breast. There is not one of them for whom I care, as I could care for a pet dog. I stand alone in the world, remembering well what a hollow world it has been to me, and what a hollow part of it I have been myself. You know this, and you know that my fame with it is worthless to me.”\n\n“Yes; I imagined that,” he said.\n\n“And calculated on it,” she rejoined, “and so pursued me. Grown too indifferent for any opposition but indifference, to the daily working of the hands that had moulded me to this; and knowing that my marriage would at least prevent their hawking of me up and down; I suffered myself to be sold, as infamously as any woman with a halter round her neck is sold in any market-place. You know that.”\n\n“Yes,” he said, showing all his teeth “I know that.”\n\n“And calculated on it,” she rejoined once more, “and so pursued me. From my marriage day, I found myself exposed to such new shame—to such solicitation and pursuit (expressed as clearly as if it had been written in the coarsest words, and thrust into my hand at every turn) from one mean villain, that I felt as if I had never known humiliation till that time. This shame my husband fixed upon me; hemmed me round with, himself; steeped me in, with his own hands, and of his own act, repeated hundreds of times. And thus—forced by the two from every point of rest I had—forced by the two to yield up the last retreat of love and gentleness within me, or to be a new misfortune on its innocent object—driven from each to each, and beset by one when I escaped the other—my anger rose almost to distraction against both. I do not know against which it rose higher—the master or the man!”\n\nHe watched her closely, as she stood before him in the very triumph of her indignant beauty. She was resolute, he saw; undauntable; with no more fear of him than of a worm.\n\n“What should I say of honour or of chastity to you!” she went on. “What meaning would it have to you; what meaning would it have from me! But if I tell you that the lightest touch of your hand makes my blood cold with antipathy; that from the hour when I first saw and hated you, to now, when my instinctive repugnance is enhanced by every minute’s knowledge of you I have since had, you have been a loathsome creature to me which has not its like on earth; how then?”\n\nHe answered with a faint laugh, “Ay! How then, my queen?”\n\n“On that night, when, emboldened by the scene you had assisted at, you dared come to my room and speak to me,” she said, “what passed?”\n\nHe shrugged his shoulders, and laughed\n\n“What passed?” she said.\n\n“Your memory is so distinct,” he said, “that I have no doubt you can recall it.”\n\n“I can,” she said. “Hear it! Proposing then, this flight—not this flight, but the flight you thought it—you told me that in the having given you that meeting, and leaving you to be discovered there, if you so thought fit; and in the having suffered you to be alone with me many times before,—and having made the opportunities, you said,—and in the having openly avowed to you that I had no feeling for my husband but aversion, and no care for myself—I was lost; I had given you the power to traduce my name; and I lived, in virtuous reputation, at the pleasure of your breath.”\n\n“All stratagems in love—-” he interrupted, smiling. “The old adage—”\n\n“On that night,” said Edith, “and then, the struggle that I long had had with something that was not respect for my good fame—that was I know not what—perhaps the clinging to that last retreat—was ended. On that night, and then, I turned from everything but passion and resentment. I struck a blow that laid your lofty master in the dust, and set you there, before me, looking at me now, and knowing what I mean.”\n\nHe sprung up from his chair with a great oath. She put her hand into her bosom, and not a finger trembled, not a hair upon her head was stirred. He stood still: she too: the table and chair between them.\n\n“When I forget that this man put his lips to mine that night, and held me in his arms as he has done again tonight,” said Edith, pointing at him; “when I forget the taint of his kiss upon my cheek—the cheek that Florence would have laid her guiltless face against—when I forget my meeting with her, while that taint was hot upon me, and in what a flood the knowledge rushed upon me when I saw her, that in releasing her from the persecution I had caused by my love, I brought a shame and degradation on her name through mine, and in all time to come should be the solitary figure representing in her mind her first avoidance of a guilty creature—then, Husband, from whom I stand divorced henceforth, I will forget these last two years, and undo what I have done, and undeceive you!”\n\nHer flashing eyes, uplifted for a moment, lighted again on Carker, and she held some letters out in her left hand.\n\n“See these!” she said, contemptuously. “You have addressed these to me in the false name you go by; one here, some elsewhere on my road. The seals are unbroken. Take them back!”\n\nShe crunched them in her hand, and tossed them to his feet. And as she looked upon him now, a smile was on her face.\n\n“We meet and part tonight,” she said. “You have fallen on Sicilian days and sensual rest, too soon. You might have cajoled, and fawned, and played your traitor’s part, a little longer, and grown richer. You purchase your voluptuous retirement dear!”\n\n“Edith!” he retorted, menacing her with his hand. “Sit down! Have done with this! What devil possesses you?”\n\n“Their name is Legion,” she replied, uprearing her proud form as if she would have crushed him; “you and your master have raised them in a fruitful house, and they shall tear you both. False to him, false to his innocent child, false every way and everywhere, go forth and boast of me, and gnash your teeth, for once, to know that you are lying!”\n\nHe stood before her, muttering and menacing, and scowling round as if for something that would help him to conquer her; but with the same indomitable spirit she opposed him, without faltering.\n\n“In every vaunt you make,” she said, “I have my triumph. I single out in you the meanest man I know, the parasite and tool of the proud tyrant, that his wound may go the deeper, and may rankle more. Boast, and revenge me on him! You know how you came here tonight; you know how you stand cowering there; you see yourself in colours quite as despicable, if not as odious, as those in which I see you. Boast then, and revenge me on yourself.”\n\nThe foam was on his lips; the wet stood on his forehead. If she would have faltered once for only one half-moment, he would have pinioned her; but she was as firm as rock, and her searching eyes never left him.\n\n“We don’t part so,” he said. “Do you think I am drivelling, to let you go in your mad temper?”\n\n“Do you think,” she answered, “that I am to be stayed?”\n\n“I’ll try, my dear,” he said with a ferocious gesture of his head.\n\n“God’s mercy on you, if you try by coming near me!” she replied.\n\n“And what,” he said, “if there are none of these same boasts and vaunts on my part? What if I were to turn too? Come!” and his teeth fairly shone again. “We must make a treaty of this, or I may take some unexpected course. Sit down, sit down!”\n\n“Too late!” she cried, with eyes that seemed to sparkle fire. “I have thrown my fame and good name to the winds! I have resolved to bear the shame that will attach to me—resolved to know that it attaches falsely—that you know it too—and that he does not, never can, and never shall. I’ll die, and make no sign. For this, I am here alone with you, at the dead of night. For this, I have met you here, in a false name, as your wife. For this, I have been seen here by those men, and left here. Nothing can save you now.”\n\nHe would have sold his soul to root her, in her beauty, to the floor, and make her arms drop at her sides, and have her at his mercy. But he could not look at her, and not be afraid of her. He saw a strength within her that was resistless. He saw that she was desperate, and that her unquenchable hatred of him would stop at nothing. His eyes followed the hand that was put with such rugged uncongenial purpose into her white bosom, and he thought that if it struck at him, and failed, it would strike there, just as soon.\n\nHe did not venture, therefore, to advance towards her; but the door by which he had entered was behind him, and he stepped back to lock it.\n\n“Lastly, take my warning! Look to yourself!” she said, and smiled again. “You have been betrayed, as all betrayers are. It has been made known that you are in this place, or were to be, or have been. If I live, I saw my husband in a carriage in the street tonight!”\n\n“Strumpet, it’s false!” cried Carker.\n\nAt the moment, the bell rang loudly in the hall. He turned white, as she held her hand up like an enchantress, at whose invocation the sound had come.\n\n“Hark! do you hear it?”\n\nHe set his back against the door; for he saw a change in her, and fancied she was coming on to pass him. But, in a moment, she was gone through the opposite doors communicating with the bed-chamber, and they shut upon her.\n\nOnce turned, once changed in her inflexible unyielding look, he felt that he could cope with her. He thought a sudden terror, occasioned by this night-alarm, had subdued her; not the less readily, for her overwrought condition. Throwing open the doors, he followed, almost instantly.\n\nBut the room was dark; and as she made no answer to his call, he was fain to go back for the lamp. He held it up, and looked round, everywhere, expecting to see her crouching in some corner; but the room was empty. So, into the drawing-room and dining-room he went, in succession, with the uncertain steps of a man in a strange place; looking fearfully about, and prying behind screens and couches; but she was not there. No, nor in the hall, which was so bare that he could see that, at a glance.\n\nAll this time, the ringing at the bell was constantly renewed, and those without were beating at the door. He put his lamp down at a distance, and going near it, listened. There were several voices talking together: at least two of them in English; and though the door was thick, and there was great confusion, he knew one of these too well to doubt whose voice it was.\n\nHe took up his lamp again, and came back quickly through all the rooms, stopping as he quitted each, and looking round for her, with the light raised above his head. He was standing thus in the bed-chamber, when the door, leading to the little passage in the wall, caught his eye. He went to it, and found it fastened on the other side; but she had dropped a veil in going through, and shut it in the door.\n\nAll this time the people on the stairs were ringing at the bell, and knocking with their hands and feet.\n\nHe was not a coward: but these sounds; what had gone before; the strangeness of the place, which had confused him, even in his return from the hall; the frustration of his schemes (for, strange to say, he would have been much bolder, if they had succeeded); the unseasonable time; the recollection of having no one near to whom he could appeal for any friendly office; above all, the sudden sense, which made even his heart beat like lead, that the man whose confidence he had outraged, and whom he had so treacherously deceived, was there to recognise and challenge him with his mask plucked off his face; struck a panic through him. He tried the door in which the veil was shut, but couldn’t force it. He opened one of the windows, and looked down through the lattice of the blind, into the court-yard; but it was a high leap, and the stones were pitiless.\n\nThe ringing and knocking still continuing—his panic too—he went back to the door in the bed-chamber, and with some new efforts, each more stubborn than the last, wrenched it open. Seeing the little staircase not far off, and feeling the night-air coming up, he stole back for his hat and coat, made the door as secure after him as he could, crept down lamp in hand, extinguished it on seeing the street, and having put it in a corner, went out where the stars were shining.\n\n# CHAPTER LV. Rob the Grinder loses his Place\n\nThe Porter at the iron gate which shut the court-yard from the street, had left the little wicket of his house open, and was gone away; no doubt to mingle in the distant noise at the door of the great staircase. Lifting the latch softly, Carker crept out, and shutting the jangling gate after him with as little noise as possible, hurried off.\n\nIn the fever of his mortification and unavailing rage, the panic that had seized upon him mastered him completely. It rose to such a height that he would have blindly encountered almost any risk, rather than meet the man of whom, two hours ago, he had been utterly regardless. His fierce arrival, which he had never expected; the sound of his voice; their having been so near a meeting, face to face; he would have braved out this, after the first momentary shock of alarm, and would have put as bold a front upon his guilt as any villain. But the springing of his mine upon himself, seemed to have rent and shivered all his hardihood and self-reliance. Spurned like any reptile; entrapped and mocked; turned upon, and trodden down by the proud woman whose mind he had slowly poisoned, as he thought, until she had sunk into the mere creature of his pleasure; undeceived in his deceit, and with his fox’s hide stripped off, he sneaked away, abashed, degraded, and afraid.\n\nSome other terror came upon him quite removed from this of being pursued, suddenly, like an electric shock, as he was creeping through the streets Some visionary terror, unintelligible and inexplicable, associated with a trembling of the ground,—a rush and sweep of something through the air, like Death upon the wing. He shrunk, as if to let the thing go by. It was not gone, it never had been there, yet what a startling horror it had left behind.\n\nHe raised his wicked face so full of trouble, to the night sky, where the stars, so full of peace, were shining on him as they had been when he first stole out into the air; and stopped to think what he should do. The dread of being hunted in a strange remote place, where the laws might not protect him—the novelty of the feeling that it was strange and remote, originating in his being left alone so suddenly amid the ruins of his plans—his greater dread of seeking refuge now, in Italy or in Sicily, where men might be hired to assassinate him, he thought, at any dark street corner—the waywardness of guilt and fear—perhaps some sympathy of action with the turning back of all his schemes—impelled him to turn back too, and go to England.\n\n“I am safer there, in any case. If I should not decide,” he thought, “to give this fool a meeting, I am less likely to be traced there, than abroad here, now. And if I should (this cursed fit being over), at least I shall not be alone, without a soul to speak to, or advise with, or stand by me. I shall not be run in upon and worried like a rat.”\n\nHe muttered Edith’s name, and clenched his hand. As he crept along, in the shadow of the massive buildings, he set his teeth, and muttered dreadful imprecations on her head, and looked from side to side, as if in search of her. Thus, he stole on to the gate of an inn-yard. The people were a-bed; but his ringing at the bell soon produced a man with a lantern, in company with whom he was presently in a dim coach-house, bargaining for the hire of an old phaeton, to Paris.\n\nThe bargain was a short one; and the horses were soon sent for. Leaving word that the carriage was to follow him when they came, he stole away again, beyond the town, past the old ramparts, out on the open road, which seemed to glide away along the dark plain, like a stream.\n\nWhither did it flow? What was the end of it? As he paused, with some such suggestion within him, looking over the gloomy flat where the slender trees marked out the way, again that flight of Death came rushing up, again went on, impetuous and resistless, again was nothing but a horror in his mind, dark as the scene and undefined as its remotest verge.\n\nThere was no wind; there was no passing shadow on the deep shade of the night; there was no noise. The city lay behind him, lighted here and there, and starry worlds were hidden by the masonry of spire and roof that hardly made out any shapes against the sky. Dark and lonely distance lay around him everywhere, and the clocks were faintly striking two.\n\nHe went forward for what appeared a long time, and a long way; often stopping to listen. At last the ringing of horses’ bells greeted his anxious ears. Now softer, and now louder, now inaudible, now ringing very slowly over bad ground, now brisk and merry, it came on; until with a loud shouting and lashing, a shadowy postillion muffled to the eyes, checked his four struggling horses at his side.\n\n“Who goes there! Monsieur?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Monsieur has walked a long way in the dark midnight.”\n\n“No matter. Everyone to his task. Were there any other horses ordered at the Post-house?”\n\n“A thousand devils!—and pardons! other horses? at this hour? No.”\n\n“Listen, my friend. I am much hurried. Let us see how fast we can travel! The faster, the more money there will be to drink. Off we go then! Quick!”\n\n“Halloa! whoop! Halloa! Hi!” Away, at a gallop, over the black landscape, scattering the dust and dirt like spray!\n\nThe clatter and commotion echoed to the hurry and discordance of the fugitive’s ideas. Nothing clear without, and nothing clear within. Objects flitting past, merging into one another, dimly descried, confusedly lost sight of, gone! Beyond the changing scraps of fence and cottage immediately upon the road, a lowering waste. Beyond the shifting images that rose up in his mind and vanished as they showed themselves, a black expanse of dread and rage and baffled villainy. Occasionally, a sigh of mountain air came from the distant Jura, fading along the plain. Sometimes that rush which was so furious and horrible, again came sweeping through his fancy, passed away, and left a chill upon his blood.\n\nThe lamps, gleaming on the medley of horses’ heads, jumbled with the shadowy driver, and the fluttering of his cloak, made a thousand indistinct shapes, answering to his thoughts. Shadows of familiar people, stooping at their desks and books, in their remembered attitudes; strange apparitions of the man whom he was flying from, or of Edith; repetitions in the ringing bells and rolling wheels, of words that had been spoken; confusions of time and place, making last night a month ago, a month ago last night—home now distant beyond hope, now instantly accessible; commotion, discord, hurry, darkness, and confusion in his mind, and all around him.—Hallo! Hi! away at a gallop over the black landscape; dust and dirt flying like spray, the smoking horses snorting and plunging as if each of them were ridden by a demon, away in a frantic triumph on the dark road—whither?\n\nAgain the nameless shock comes speeding up, and as it passes, the bells ring in his ears “whither?” The wheels roar in his ears “whither?” All the noise and rattle shapes itself into that cry. The lights and shadows dance upon the horses’ heads like imps. No stopping now: no slackening! On, on! Away with him upon the dark road wildly!\n\nHe could not think to any purpose. He could not separate one subject of reflection from another, sufficiently to dwell upon it, by itself, for a minute at a time. The crash of his project for the gaining of a voluptuous compensation for past restraint; the overthrow of his treachery to one who had been true and generous to him, but whose least proud word and look he had treasured up, at interest, for years—for false and subtle men will always secretly despise and dislike the object upon which they fawn and always resent the payment and receipt of homage that they know to be worthless; these were the themes uppermost in his mind. A lurking rage against the woman who had so entrapped him and avenged herself was always there; crude and misshapen schemes of retaliation upon her, floated in his brain; but nothing was distinct. A hurry and contradiction pervaded all his thoughts. Even while he was so busy with this fevered, ineffectual thinking, his one constant idea was, that he would postpone reflection until some indefinite time.\n\nThen, the old days before the second marriage rose up in his remembrance. He thought how jealous he had been of the boy, how jealous he had been of the girl, how artfully he had kept intruders at a distance, and drawn a circle round his dupe that none but himself should cross; and then he thought, had he done all this to be flying now, like a scared thief, from only the poor dupe?\n\nHe could have laid hands upon himself for his cowardice, but it was the very shadow of his defeat, and could not be separated from it. To have his confidence in his own knavery so shattered at a blow—to be within his own knowledge such a miserable tool—was like being paralysed. With an impotent ferocity he raged at Edith, and hated Mr Dombey and hated himself, but still he fled, and could do nothing else.\n\nAgain and again he listened for the sound of wheels behind. Again and again his fancy heard it, coming on louder and louder. At last he was so persuaded of this, that he cried out, “Stop” preferring even the loss of ground to such uncertainty.\n\nThe word soon brought carriage, horses, driver, all in a heap together, across the road.\n\n“The devil!” cried the driver, looking over his shoulder, “what’s the matter?”\n\n“Hark! What’s that?”\n\n“What?”\n\n“That noise?”\n\n“Ah Heaven, be quiet, cursed brigand!” to a horse who shook his bells “What noise?”\n\n“Behind. Is it not another carriage at a gallop? There! what’s that?”\n\n“Miscreant with a Pig’s head, stand still!” to another horse, who bit another, who frightened the other two, who plunged and backed. “There is nothing coming.”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“No, nothing but the day yonder.”\n\n“You are right, I think. I hear nothing now, indeed. Go on!”\n\nThe entangled equipage, half hidden in the reeking cloud from the horses, goes on slowly at first, for the driver, checked unnecessarily in his progress, sulkily takes out a pocket-knife, and puts a new lash to his whip. Then “Hallo, whoop! Hallo, hi!” Away once more, savagely.\n\nAnd now the stars faded, and the day glimmered, and standing in the carriage, looking back, he could discern the track by which he had come, and see that there was no traveller within view, on all the heavy expanse. And soon it was broad day, and the sun began to shine on cornfields and vineyards; and solitary labourers, risen from little temporary huts by heaps of stones upon the road, were, here and there, at work repairing the highway, or eating bread. By and by, there were peasants going to their daily labour, or to market, or lounging at the doors of poor cottages, gazing idly at him as he passed. And then there was a postyard, ankle-deep in mud, with steaming dunghills and vast outhouses half ruined; and looking on this dainty prospect, an immense, old, shadeless, glaring, stone chateau, with half its windows blinded, and green damp crawling lazily over it, from the balustraded terrace to the taper tips of the extinguishers upon the turrets.\n\nGathered up moodily in a corner of the carriage, and only intent on going fast—except when he stood up, for a mile together, and looked back; which he would do whenever there was a piece of open country—he went on, still postponing thought indefinitely, and still always tormented with thinking to no purpose.\n\nShame, disappointment, and discomfiture gnawed at his heart; a constant apprehension of being overtaken, or met—for he was groundlessly afraid even of travellers, who came towards him by the way he was going—oppressed him heavily. The same intolerable awe and dread that had come upon him in the night, returned unweakened in the day. The monotonous ringing of the bells and tramping of the horses; the monotony of his anxiety, and useless rage; the monotonous wheel of fear, regret, and passion, he kept turning round and round; made the journey like a vision, in which nothing was quite real but his own torment.\n\nIt was a vision of long roads, that stretched away to an horizon, always receding and never gained; of ill-paved towns, up hill and down, where faces came to dark doors and ill-glazed windows, and where rows of mudbespattered cows and oxen were tied up for sale in the long narrow streets, butting and lowing, and receiving blows on their blunt heads from bludgeons that might have beaten them in; of bridges, crosses, churches, postyards, new horses being put in against their wills, and the horses of the last stage reeking, panting, and laying their drooping heads together dolefully at stable doors; of little cemeteries with black crosses settled sideways in the graves, and withered wreaths upon them dropping away; again of long, long roads, dragging themselves out, up hill and down, to the treacherous horizon.\n\nOf morning, noon, and sunset; night, and the rising of an early moon. Of long roads temporarily left behind, and a rough pavement reached; of battering and clattering over it, and looking up, among house-roofs, at a great church-tower; of getting out and eating hastily, and drinking draughts of wine that had no cheering influence; of coming forth afoot, among a host of beggars—blind men with quivering eyelids, led by old women holding candles to their faces; idiot girls; the lame, the epileptic, and the palsied—of passing through the clamour, and looking from his seat at the upturned countenances and outstretched hands, with a hurried dread of recognising some pursuer pressing forward—of galloping away again, upon the long, long road, gathered up, dull and stunned, in his corner, or rising to see where the moon shone faintly on a patch of the same endless road miles away, or looking back to see who followed.\n\nOf never sleeping, but sometimes dozing with unclosed eyes, and springing up with a start, and a reply aloud to an imaginary voice. Of cursing himself for being there, for having fled, for having let her go, for not having confronted and defied him. Of having a deadly quarrel with the whole world, but chiefly with himself. Of blighting everything with his black mood as he was carried on and away.\n\nIt was a fevered vision of things past and present all confounded together; of his life and journey blended into one. Of being madly hurried somewhere, whither he must go. Of old scenes starting up among the novelties through which he travelled. Of musing and brooding over what was past and distant, and seeming to take no notice of the actual objects he encountered, but with a wearisome exhausting consciousness of being bewildered by them, and having their images all crowded in his hot brain after they were gone.\n\nA vision of change upon change, and still the same monotony of bells and wheels, and horses’ feet, and no rest. Of town and country, postyards, horses, drivers, hill and valley, light and darkness, road and pavement, height and hollow, wet weather and dry, and still the same monotony of bells and wheels, and horses’ feet, and no rest. A vision of tending on at last, towards the distant capital, by busier roads, and sweeping round, by old cathedrals, and dashing through small towns and villages, less thinly scattered on the road than formerly, and sitting shrouded in his corner, with his cloak up to his face, as people passing by looked at him.\n\nOf rolling on and on, always postponing thought, and always racked with thinking; of being unable to reckon up the hours he had been upon the road, or to comprehend the points of time and place in his journey. Of being parched and giddy, and half mad. Of pressing on, in spite of all, as if he could not stop, and coming into Paris, where the turbid river held its swift course undisturbed, between two brawling streams of life and motion.\n\nA troubled vision, then, of bridges, quays, interminable streets; of wine-shops, water-carriers, great crowds of people, soldiers, coaches, military drums, arcades. Of the monotony of bells and wheels and horses’ feet being at length lost in the universal din and uproar. Of the gradual subsidence of that noise as he passed out in another carriage by a different barrier from that by which he had entered. Of the restoration, as he travelled on towards the seacoast, of the monotony of bells and wheels, and horses’ feet, and no rest.\n\nOf sunset once again, and nightfall. Of long roads again, and dead of night, and feeble lights in windows by the roadside; and still the old monotony of bells and wheels, and horses’ feet, and no rest. Of dawn, and daybreak, and the rising of the sun. Of tolling slowly up a hill, and feeling on its top the fresh sea-breeze; and seeing the morning light upon the edges of the distant waves. Of coming down into a harbour when the tide was at its full, and seeing fishing-boats float on, and glad women and children waiting for them. Of nets and seamen’s clothes spread out to dry upon the shore; of busy sailors, and their voices high among ships’ masts and rigging; of the buoyancy and brightness of the water, and the universal sparkling.\n\nOf receding from the coast, and looking back upon it from the deck when it was a haze upon the water, with here and there a little opening of bright land where the Sun struck. Of the swell, and flash, and murmur of the calm sea. Of another grey line on the ocean, on the vessel’s track, fast growing clearer and higher. Of cliffs and buildings, and a windmill, and a church, becoming more and more visible upon it. Of steaming on at last into smooth water, and mooring to a pier whence groups of people looked down, greeting friends on board. Of disembarking, passing among them quickly, shunning every one; and of being at last again in England.\n\nHe had thought, in his dream, of going down into a remote country-place he knew, and lying quiet there, while he secretly informed himself of what transpired, and determined how to act, Still in the same stunned condition, he remembered a certain station on the railway, where he would have to branch off to his place of destination, and where there was a quiet Inn. Here, he indistinctly resolved to tarry and rest.\n\nWith this purpose he slunk into a railway carriage as quickly as he could, and lying there wrapped in his cloak as if he were asleep, was soon borne far away from the sea, and deep into the inland green. Arrived at his destination he looked out, and surveyed it carefully. He was not mistaken in his impression of the place. It was a retired spot, on the borders of a little wood. Only one house, newly-built or altered for the purpose, stood there, surrounded by its neat garden; the small town that was nearest, was some miles away. Here he alighted then; and going straight into the tavern, unobserved by anyone, secured two rooms upstairs communicating with each other, and sufficiently retired.\n\nHis object was to rest, and recover the command of himself, and the balance of his mind. Imbecile discomfiture and rage—so that, as he walked about his room, he ground his teeth—had complete possession of him. His thoughts, not to be stopped or directed, still wandered where they would, and dragged him after them. He was stupefied, and he was wearied to death.\n\nBut, as if there were a curse upon him that he should never rest again, his drowsy senses would not lose their consciousness. He had no more influence with them, in this regard, than if they had been another man’s. It was not that they forced him to take note of present sounds and objects, but that they would not be diverted from the whole hurried vision of his journey. It was constantly before him all at once. She stood there, with her dark disdainful eyes again upon him; and he was riding on nevertheless, through town and country, light and darkness, wet weather and dry, over road and pavement, hill and valley, height and hollow, jaded and scared by the monotony of bells and wheels, and horses’ feet, and no rest.\n\n“What day is this?” he asked of the waiter, who was making preparations for his dinner.\n\n“Day, Sir?”\n\n“Is it Wednesday?”\n“Wednesday, Sir? No, Sir. Thursday, Sir.”\n\n“I forgot. How goes the time? My watch is unwound.”\n\n“Wants a few minutes of five o’clock, Sir. Been travelling a long time, Sir, perhaps?”\n\n“By rail, Sir?”\n“Yes”\n\n“Very confusing, Sir. Not much in the habit of travelling by rail myself, Sir, but gentlemen frequently say so.”\n\n“Do many gentlemen come here?\n\n“Pretty well, Sir, in general. Nobody here at present. Rather slack just now, Sir. Everything is slack, Sir.”\n\nHe made no answer; but had risen into a sitting posture on the sofa where he had been lying, and leaned forward with an arm on each knee, staring at the ground. He could not master his own attention for a minute together. It rushed away where it would, but it never, for an instant, lost itself in sleep.\n\nHe drank a quantity of wine after dinner, in vain. No such artificial means would bring sleep to his eyes. His thoughts, more incoherent, dragged him more unmercifully after them—as if a wretch, condemned to such expiation, were drawn at the heels of wild horses. No oblivion, and no rest.\n\nHow long he sat, drinking and brooding, and being dragged in imagination hither and thither, no one could have told less correctly than he. But he knew that he had been sitting a long time by candle-light, when he started up and listened, in a sudden terror.\n\nFor now, indeed, it was no fancy. The ground shook, the house rattled, the fierce impetuous rush was in the air! He felt it come up, and go darting by; and even when he had hurried to the window, and saw what it was, he stood, shrinking from it, as if it were not safe to look.\n\nA curse upon the fiery devil, thundering along so smoothly, tracked through the distant valley by a glare of light and lurid smoke, and gone! He felt as if he had been plucked out of its path, and saved from being torn asunder. It made him shrink and shudder even now, when its faintest hum was hushed, and when the lines of iron road he could trace in the moonlight, running to a point, were as empty and as silent as a desert.\n\nUnable to rest, and irresistibly attracted—or he thought so—to this road, he went out, and lounged on the brink of it, marking the way the train had gone, by the yet smoking cinders that were lying in its track. After a lounge of some half hour in the direction by which it had disappeared, he turned and walked the other way—still keeping to the brink of the road—past the inn garden, and a long way down; looking curiously at the bridges, signals, lamps, and wondering when another Devil would come by.\n\nA trembling of the ground, and quick vibration in his ears; a distant shriek; a dull light advancing, quickly changed to two red eyes, and a fierce fire, dropping glowing coals; an irresistible bearing on of a great roaring and dilating mass; a high wind, and a rattle—another come and gone, and he holding to a gate, as if to save himself!\n\nHe waited for another, and for another. He walked back to his former point, and back again to that, and still, through the wearisome vision of his journey, looked for these approaching monsters. He loitered about the station, waiting until one should stay to call there; and when one did, and was detached for water, he stood parallel with it, watching its heavy wheels and brazen front, and thinking what a cruel power and might it had. Ugh! To see the great wheels slowly turning, and to think of being run down and crushed!\n\nDisordered with wine and want of rest—that want which nothing, although he was so weary, would appease—these ideas and objects assumed a diseased importance in his thoughts. When he went back to his room, which was not until near midnight, they still haunted him, and he sat listening for the coming of another.\n\nSo in his bed, whither he repaired with no hope of sleep. He still lay listening; and when he felt the trembling and vibration, got up and went to the window, to watch (as he could from its position) the dull light changing to the two red eyes, and the fierce fire dropping glowing coals, and the rush of the giant as it fled past, and the track of glare and smoke along the valley. Then he would glance in the direction by which he intended to depart at sunrise, as there was no rest for him there; and would lie down again, to be troubled by the vision of his journey, and the old monotony of bells and wheels and horses’ feet, until another came. This lasted all night. So far from resuming the mastery of himself, he seemed, if possible, to lose it more and more, as the night crept on. When the dawn appeared, he was still tormented with thinking, still postponing thought until he should be in a better state; the past, present, and future all floated confusedly before him, and he had lost all power of looking steadily at any one of them.\n\n“At what time,” he asked the man who had waited on him over-night, now entering with a candle, “do I leave here, did you say?”\n\n“About a quarter after four, Sir. Express comes through at four, Sir.—It don’t stop.”\n\nHe passed his hand across his throbbing head, and looked at his watch. Nearly half-past three.\n\n“Nobody going with you, Sir, probably,” observed the man. “Two gentlemen here, Sir, but they’re waiting for the train to London.”\n\n“I thought you said there was nobody here,” said Carker, turning upon him with the ghost of his old smile, when he was angry or suspicious.\n\n“Not then, sir. Two gentlemen came in the night by the short train that stops here, Sir. Warm water, Sir?”\n\n“No; and take away the candle. There’s day enough for me.”\n\nHaving thrown himself upon the bed, half-dressed he was at the window as the man left the room. The cold light of morning had succeeded to night and there was already, in the sky, the red suffusion of the coming sun. He bathed his head and face with water—there was no cooling influence in it for him—hurriedly put on his clothes, paid what he owed, and went out.\n\nThe air struck chill and comfortless as it breathed upon him. There was a heavy dew; and, hot as he was, it made him shiver. After a glance at the place where he had walked last night, and at the signal-lights burning in the morning, and bereft of their significance, he turned to where the sun was rising, and beheld it, in its glory, as it broke upon the scene.\n\nSo awful, so transcendent in its beauty, so divinely solemn. As he cast his faded eyes upon it, where it rose, tranquil and serene, unmoved by all the wrong and wickedness on which its beams had shone since the beginning of the world, who shall say that some weak sense of virtue upon Earth, and its in Heaven, did not manifest itself, even to him? If ever he remembered sister or brother with a touch of tenderness and remorse, who shall say it was not then?\n\nHe needed some such touch then. Death was on him. He was marked off—the living world, and going down into his grave.\n\nHe paid the money for his journey to the country-place he had thought of; and was walking to and fro, alone, looking along the lines of iron, across the valley in one direction, and towards a dark bridge near at hand in the other; when, turning in his walk, where it was bounded by one end of the wooden stage on which he paced up and down, he saw the man from whom he had fled, emerging from the door by which he himself had entered. And their eyes met.\n\nIn the quick unsteadiness of the surprise, he staggered, and slipped on to the road below him. But recovering his feet immediately, he stepped back a pace or two upon that road, to interpose some wider space between them, and looked at his pursuer, breathing short and quick.\n\nHe heard a shout—another—saw the face change from its vindictive passion to a faint sickness and terror—felt the earth tremble—knew in a moment that the rush was come—uttered a shriek—looked round—saw the red eyes, bleared and dim, in the daylight, close upon him—was beaten down, caught up, and whirled away upon a jagged mill, that spun him round and round, and struck him limb from limb, and licked his stream of life up with its fiery heat, and cast his mutilated fragments in the air.\n\nWhen the traveller, who had been recognised, recovered from a swoon, he saw them bringing from a distance something covered, that lay heavy and still, upon a board, between four men, and saw that others drove some dogs away that sniffed upon the road, and soaked his blood up, with a train of ashes.\n\n# CHAPTER LVI. Several People delighted, and the Game Chicken disgusted\n\nThe Midshipman was all alive. Mr Toots and Susan had arrived at last. Susan had run upstairs like a young woman bereft of her senses, and Mr Toots and the Chicken had gone into the Parlour.\n\n“Oh my own pretty darling sweet Miss Floy!” cried the Nipper, running into Florence’s room, “to think that it should come to this and I should find you here my own dear dove with nobody to wait upon you and no home to call your own but never never will I go away again Miss Floy for though I may not gather moss I’m not a rolling stone nor is my heart a stone or else it wouldn’t bust as it is busting now oh dear oh dear!”\n\nPouring out these words, without the faintest indication of a stop, of any sort, Miss Nipper, on her knees beside her mistress, hugged her close.\n\n“Oh love!” cried Susan, “I know all that’s past I know it all my tender pet and I’m a choking give me air!”\n\n“Susan, dear good Susan!” said Florence.\n\n“Oh bless her! I that was her little maid when she was a little child! and is she really, really truly going to be married?” exclaimed Susan, in a burst of pain and pleasure, pride and grief, and Heaven knows how many other conflicting feelings.\n\n“Who told you so?” said Florence.\n\n“Oh gracious me! that innocentest creetur Toots,” returned Susan hysterically. “I knew he must be right my dear, because he took on so. He’s the devotedest and innocentest infant! And is my darling,” pursued Susan, with another close embrace and burst of tears, “really really going to be married!”\n\nThe mixture of compassion, pleasure, tenderness, protection, and regret with which the Nipper constantly recurred to this subject, and at every such once, raised her head to look in the young face and kiss it, and then laid her head again upon her mistress’s shoulder, caressing her and sobbing, was as womanly and good a thing, in its way, as ever was seen in the world.\n\n“There, there!” said the soothing voice of Florence presently. “Now you’re quite yourself, dear Susan!”\n\nMiss Nipper, sitting down upon the floor, at her mistress’s feet, laughing and sobbing, holding her pocket-handkerchief to her eyes with one hand, and patting Diogenes with the other as he licked her face, confessed to being more composed, and laughed and cried a little more in proof of it.\n\n“I-I-I never did see such a creetur as that Toots,” said Susan, “in all my born days never!”\n\n“So kind,” suggested Florence.\n\n“And so comic!” Susan sobbed. “The way he’s been going on inside with me with that disrespectable Chicken on the box!”\n\n“About what, Susan?” inquired Florence, timidly.\n\n“Oh about Lieutenant Walters, and Captain Gills, and you my dear Miss Floy, and the silent tomb,” said Susan.\n\n“The silent tomb!” repeated Florence.\n\n“He says,” here Susan burst into a violent hysterical laugh, “that he’ll go down into it now immediately and quite comfortable, but bless your heart my dear Miss Floy he won’t, he’s a great deal too happy in seeing other people happy for that, he may not be a Solomon,” pursued the Nipper, with her usual volubility, “nor do I say he is but this I do say a less selfish human creature human nature never knew!”\n\nMiss Nipper being still hysterical, laughed immoderately after making this energetic declaration, and then informed Florence that he was waiting below to see her; which would be a rich repayment for the trouble he had had in his late expedition.\n\nFlorence entreated Susan to beg of Mr Toots as a favour that she might have the pleasure of thanking him for his kindness; and Susan, in a few moments, produced that young gentleman, still very much dishevelled in appearance, and stammering exceedingly.\n\n“Miss Dombey,” said Mr Toots. “To be again permitted to—to—gaze—at least, not to gaze, but—I don’t exactly know what I was going to say, but it’s of no consequence.”\n\n“I have to thank you so often,” returned Florence, giving him both her hands, with all her innocent gratitude beaming in her face, “that I have no words left, and don’t know how to do it.”\n\n“Miss Dombey,” said Mr Toots, in an awful voice, “if it was possible that you could, consistently with your angelic nature, Curse me, you would—if I may be allowed to say so—floor me infinitely less, than by these undeserved expressions of kindness Their effect upon me—is—but,” said Mr Toots, abruptly, “this is a digression, and of no consequence at all.”\n\nAs there seemed to be no means of replying to this, but by thanking him again, Florence thanked him again.\n\n“I could wish,” said Mr Toots, “to take this opportunity, Miss Dombey, if I might, of entering into a word of explanation. I should have had the pleasure of—of returning with Susan at an earlier period; but, in the first place, we didn’t know the name of the relation to whose house she had gone, and, in the second, as she had left that relation’s and gone to another at a distance, I think that scarcely anything short of the sagacity of the Chicken, would have found her out in the time.”\n\nFlorence was sure of it.\n\n“This, however,” said Mr Toots, “is not the point. The company of Susan has been, I assure you, Miss Dombey, a consolation and satisfaction to me, in my state of mind, more easily conceived than described. The journey has been its own reward. That, however, still, is not the point. Miss Dombey, I have before observed that I know I am not what is considered a quick person. I am perfectly aware of that. I don’t think anybody could be better acquainted with his own—if it was not too strong an expression, I should say with the thickness of his own head—than myself. But, Miss Dombey, I do, notwithstanding, perceive the state of—of things—with Lieutenant Walters. Whatever agony that state of things may have caused me (which is of no consequence at all), I am bound to say, that Lieutenant Walters is a person who appears to be worthy of the blessing that has fallen on his—on his brow. May he wear it long, and appreciate it, as a very different, and very unworthy individual, that it is of no consequence to name, would have done! That, however, still, is not the point. Miss Dombey, Captain Gills is a friend of mine; and during the interval that is now elapsing, I believe it would afford Captain Gills pleasure to see me occasionally coming backwards and forwards here. It would afford me pleasure so to come. But I cannot forget that I once committed myself, fatally, at the corner of the Square at Brighton; and if my presence will be, in the least degree, unpleasant to you, I only ask you to name it to me now, and assure you that I shall perfectly understand you. I shall not consider it at all unkind, and shall only be too delighted and happy to be honoured with your confidence.”\n\n“Mr Toots,” returned Florence, “if you, who are so old and true a friend of mine, were to stay away from this house now, you would make me very unhappy. It can never, never, give me any feeling but pleasure to see you.\n\n“Miss Dombey,” said Mr Toots, taking out his pocket-handkerchief, “if I shed a tear, it is a tear of joy. It is of no consequence, and I am very much obliged to you. I may be allowed to remark, after what you have so kindly said, that it is not my intention to neglect my person any longer.”\n\nFlorence received this intimation with the prettiest expression of perplexity possible.\n\n“I mean,” said Mr Toots, “that I shall consider it my duty as a fellow-creature generally, until I am claimed by the silent tomb, to make the best of myself, and to—to have my boots as brightly polished, as—as—circumstances will admit of. This is the last time, Miss Dombey, of my intruding any observation of a private and personal nature. I thank you very much indeed. If I am not, in a general way, as sensible as my friends could wish me to be, or as I could wish myself, I really am, upon my word and honour, particularly sensible of what is considerate and kind. I feel,” said Mr Toots, in an impassioned tone, “as if I could express my feelings, at the present moment, in a most remarkable manner, if—if—I could only get a start.”\n\nAppearing not to get it, after waiting a minute or two to see if it would come, Mr Toots took a hasty leave, and went below to seek the Captain, whom he found in the shop.\n\n“Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, “what is now to take place between us, takes place under the sacred seal of confidence. It is the sequel, Captain Gills, of what has taken place between myself and Miss Dombey, upstairs.”\n\n“Alow and aloft, eh, my lad?” murmured the Captain.\n\n“Exactly so, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, whose fervour of acquiescence was greatly heightened by his entire ignorance of the Captain’s meaning. “Miss Dombey, I believe, Captain Gills, is to be shortly united to Lieutenant Walters?”\n\n“Why, ay, my lad. We’re all shipmets here,—Wal”r and sweet—heart will be jined together in the house of bondage, as soon as the askings is over,” whispered Captain Cuttle, in his ear.\n\n“The askings, Captain Gills!” repeated Mr Toots.\n\n“In the church, down yonder,” said the Captain, pointing his thumb over his shoulder.\n\n“Oh! Yes!” returned Mr Toots.\n\n“And then,” said the Captain, in his hoarse whisper, and tapping Mr Toots on the chest with the back of his hand, and falling from him with a look of infinite admiration, “what follers? That there pretty creetur, as delicately brought up as a foreign bird, goes away upon the roaring main with Wal”r on a woyage to China!”\n\n“Lord, Captain Gills!” said Mr Toots.\n\n“Ay!” nodded the Captain. “The ship as took him up, when he was wrecked in the hurricane that had drove her clean out of her course, was a China trader, and Wal”r made the woyage, and got into favour, aboard and ashore—being as smart and good a lad as ever stepped—and so, the supercargo dying at Canton, he got made (having acted as clerk afore), and now he’s supercargo aboard another ship, same owners. And so, you see,” repeated the Captain, thoughtfully, “the pretty creetur goes away upon the roaring main with Wal”r, on a woyage to China.”\n\nMr Toots and Captain Cuttle heaved a sigh in concert. “What then?” said the Captain. “She loves him true. He loves her true. Them as should have loved and tended of her, treated of her like the beasts as perish. When she, cast out of home, come here to me, and dropped upon them planks, her wownded heart was broke. I know it. I, Ed’ard Cuttle, see it. There’s nowt but true, kind, steady love, as can ever piece it up again. If so be I didn’t know that, and didn’t know as Wal”r was her true love, brother, and she his, I’d have these here blue arms and legs chopped off, afore I’d let her go. But I know it, and what then! Why, then, I say, Heaven go with ’em both, and so it will! Amen!”\n\n“Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, “let me have the pleasure of shaking hands. You’ve a way of saying things, that gives me an agreeable warmth, all up my back. I say Amen. You are aware, Captain Gills, that I, too, have adored Miss Dombey.”\n\n“Cheer up!” said the Captain, laying his hand on Mr Toots’s shoulder. “Stand by, boy!”\n\n“It is my intention, Captain Gills,” returned the spirited Mr Toots, “to cheer up. Also to standby, as much as possible. When the silent tomb shall yawn, Captain Gills, I shall be ready for burial; not before. But not being certain, just at present, of my power over myself, what I wish to say to you, and what I shall take it as a particular favour if you will mention to Lieutenant Walters, is as follows.”\n\n“Is as follers,” echoed the Captain. “Steady!”\n\n“Miss Dombey being so inexpressably kind,” continued Mr Toots with watery eyes, “as to say that my presence is the reverse of disagreeable to her, and you and everybody here being no less forbearing and tolerant towards one who—who certainly,” said Mr Toots, with momentary dejection, “would appear to have been born by mistake, I shall come backwards and forwards of an evening, during the short time we can all be together. But what I ask is this. If, at any moment, I find that I cannot endure the contemplation of Lieutenant Walters’s bliss, and should rush out, I hope, Captain Gills, that you and he will both consider it as my misfortune and not my fault, or the want of inward conflict. That you’ll feel convinced I bear no malice to any living creature-least of all to Lieutenant Walters himself—and that you’ll casually remark that I have gone out for a walk, or probably to see what o’clock it is by the Royal Exchange. Captain Gills, if you could enter into this arrangement, and could answer for Lieutenant Walters, it would be a relief to my feelings that I should think cheap at the sacrifice of a considerable portion of my property.”\n\n“My lad,” returned the Captain, “say no more. There ain’t a colour you can run up, as won’t be made out, and answered to, by Wal”r and self.”\n\n“Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots, “my mind is greatly relieved. I wish to preserve the good opinion of all here. I—I—mean well, upon my honour, however badly I may show it. You know,” said Mr Toots, “it’s as exactly as Burgess and Co. wished to oblige a customer with a most extraordinary pair of trousers, and could not cut out what they had in their minds.”\n\nWith this apposite illustration, of which he seemed a little Proud, Mr Toots gave Captain Cuttle his blessing and departed.\n\nThe honest Captain, with his Heart’s Delight in the house, and Susan tending her, was a beaming and a happy man. As the days flew by, he grew more beaming and more happy, every day. After some conferences with Susan (for whose wisdom the Captain had a profound respect, and whose valiant precipitation of herself on Mrs MacStinger he could never forget), he proposed to Florence that the daughter of the elderly lady who usually sat under the blue umbrella in Leadenhall Market, should, for prudential reasons and considerations of privacy, be superseded in the temporary discharge of the household duties, by someone who was not unknown to them, and in whom they could safely confide. Susan, being present, then named, in furtherance of a suggestion she had previously offered to the Captain, Mrs Richards. Florence brightened at the name. And Susan, setting off that very afternoon to the Toodle domicile, to sound Mrs Richards, returned in triumph the same evening, accompanied by the identical rosy-cheeked apple-faced Polly, whose demonstrations, when brought into Florence’s presence, were hardly less affectionate than those of Susan Nipper herself.\n\nThis piece of generalship accomplished; from which the Captain derived uncommon satisfaction, as he did, indeed, from everything else that was done, whatever it happened to be; Florence had next to prepare Susan for their approaching separation. This was a much more difficult task, as Miss Nipper was of a resolute disposition, and had fully made up her mind that she had come back never to be parted from her old mistress any more.\n\n“As to wages dear Miss Floy,” she said, “you wouldn’t hint and wrong me so as think of naming them, for I’ve put money by and wouldn’t sell my love and duty at a time like this even if the Savings’ Banks and me were total strangers or the Banks were broke to pieces, but you’ve never been without me darling from the time your poor dear Ma was took away, and though I’m nothing to be boasted of you’re used to me and oh my own dear mistress through so many years don’t think of going anywhere without me, for it mustn’t and can’t be!”\n\n“Dear Susan, I am going on a long, long voyage.”\n\n“Well Miss Floy, and what of that? the more you’ll want me. Lengths of voyages ain’t an object in my eyes, thank God!” said the impetuous Susan Nipper.\n\n“But, Susan, I am going with Walter, and I would go with Walter anywhere—everywhere! Walter is poor, and I am very poor, and I must learn, now, both to help myself, and help him.”\n\n“Dear Miss Floy!” cried Susan, bursting out afresh, and shaking her head violently, “it’s nothing new to you to help yourself and others too and be the patientest and truest of noble hearts, but let me talk to Mr Walter Gay and settle it with him, for suffer you to go away across the world alone I cannot, and I won’t.”\n\n“Alone, Susan?” returned Florence. “Alone? and Walter taking me with him!” Ah, what a bright, amazed, enraptured smile was on her face!—He should have seen it. “I am sure you will not speak to Walter if I ask you not,” she added tenderly; “and pray don’t, dear.”\n\nSusan sobbed “Why not, Miss Floy?”\n\n“Because,” said Florence, “I am going to be his wife, to give him up my whole heart, and to live with him and die with him. He might think, if you said to him what you have said to me, that I am afraid of what is before me, or that you have some cause to be afraid for me. Why, Susan, dear, I love him!”\n\nMiss Nipper was so much affected by the quiet fervour of these words, and the simple, heartfelt, all-pervading earnestness expressed in them, and making the speaker’s face more beautiful and pure than ever, that she could only cling to her again, crying. Was her little mistress really, really going to be married, and pitying, caressing, and protecting her, as she had done before.\n\nBut the Nipper, though susceptible of womanly weaknesses, was almost as capable of putting constraint upon herself as of attacking the redoubtable MacStinger. From that time, she never returned to the subject, but was always cheerful, active, bustling, and hopeful. She did, indeed, inform Mr Toots privately, that she was only “keeping up” for the time, and that when it was all over, and Miss Dombey was gone, she might be expected to become a spectacle distressful; and Mr Toots did also express that it was his case too, and that they would mingle their tears together; but she never otherwise indulged her private feelings in the presence of Florence or within the precincts of the Midshipman.\n\nLimited and plain as Florence’s wardrobe was—what a contrast to that prepared for the last marriage in which she had taken part!—there was a good deal to do in getting it ready, and Susan Nipper worked away at her side, all day, with the concentrated zeal of fifty sempstresses. The wonderful contributions Captain Cuttle would have made to this branch of the outfit, if he had been permitted—as pink parasols, tinted silk stockings, blue shoes, and other articles no less necessary on shipboard—would occupy some space in the recital. He was induced, however, by various fraudulent representations, to limit his contributions to a work-box and dressing case, of each of which he purchased the very largest specimen that could be got for money. For ten days or a fortnight afterwards, he generally sat, during the greater part of the day, gazing at these boxes; divided between extreme admiration of them, and dejected misgivings that they were not gorgeous enough, and frequently diving out into the street to purchase some wild article that he deemed necessary to their completeness. But his master-stroke was, the bearing of them both off, suddenly, one morning, and getting the two words FLORENCE GAY engraved upon a brass heart inlaid over the lid of each. After this, he smoked four pipes successively in the little parlour by himself, and was discovered chuckling, at the expiration of as many hours.\n\nWalter was busy and away all day, but came there every morning early to see Florence, and always passed the evening with her. Florence never left her high rooms but to steal downstairs to wait for him when it was his time to come, or, sheltered by his proud, encircling arm, to bear him company to the door again, and sometimes peep into the street. In the twilight they were always together. Oh blessed time! Oh wandering heart at rest! Oh deep, exhaustless, mighty well of love, in which so much was sunk!\n\nThe cruel mark was on her bosom yet. It rose against her father with the breath she drew, it lay between her and her lover when he pressed her to his heart. But she forgot it. In the beating of that heart for her, and in the beating of her own for him, all harsher music was unheard, all stern unloving hearts forgotten. Fragile and delicate she was, but with a might of love within her that could, and did, create a world to fly to, and to rest in, out of his one image.\n\nHow often did the great house, and the old days, come before her in the twilight time, when she was sheltered by the arm, so proud, so fond, and, creeping closer to him, shrunk within it at the recollection! How often, from remembering the night when she went down to that room and met the never-to-be forgotten look, did she raise her eyes to those that watched her with such loving earnestness, and weep with happiness in such a refuge! The more she clung to it, the more the dear dead child was in her thoughts: but as if the last time she had seen her father, had been when he was sleeping and she kissed his face, she always left him so, and never, in her fancy, passed that hour.\n\n“Walter, dear,” said Florence, one evening, when it was almost dark. “Do you know what I have been thinking today?”\n\n“Thinking how the time is flying on, and how soon we shall be upon the sea, sweet Florence?”\n\n“I don’t mean that, Walter, though I think of that too. I have been thinking what a charge I am to you.”\n\n“A precious, sacred charge, dear heart! Why, I think that sometimes.”\n\n“You are laughing, Walter. I know that’s much more in your thoughts than mine. But I mean a cost.\n\n“A cost, my own?”\n\n“In money, dear. All these preparations that Susan and I are so busy with—I have been able to purchase very little for myself. You were poor before. But how much poorer I shall make you, Walter!”\n\n“And how much richer, Florence!”\n\nFlorence laughed, and shook her head.\n\n“Besides,” said Walter, “long ago—before I went to sea—I had a little purse presented to me, dearest, which had money in it.”\n\n“Ah!” returned Florence, laughing sorrowfully, “very little! very little, Walter! But, you must not think,” and here she laid her light hand on his shoulder, and looked into his face, “that I regret to be this burden on you. No, dear love, I am glad of it. I am happy in it. I wouldn’t have it otherwise for all the world!”\n\n“Nor I, indeed, dear Florence.”\n\n“Ay! but, Walter, you can never feel it as I do. I am so proud of you! It makes my heart swell with such delight to know that those who speak of you must say you married a poor disowned girl, who had taken shelter here; who had no other home, no other friends; who had nothing—nothing! Oh, Walter, if I could have brought you millions, I never could have been so happy for your sake, as I am!”\n\n“And you, dear Florence? are you nothing?” he returned.\n\n“No, nothing, Walter. Nothing but your wife.” The light hand stole about his neck, and the voice came nearer—nearer. “I am nothing any more, that is not you. I have no earthly hope any more, that is not you. I have nothing dear to me any more, that is not you.”\n\nOh! well might Mr Toots leave the little company that evening, and twice go out to correct his watch by the Royal Exchange, and once to keep an appointment with a banker which he suddenly remembered, and once to take a little turn to Aldgate Pump and back!\n\nBut before he went upon these expeditions, or indeed before he came, and before lights were brought, Walter said:\n\n“Florence, love, the lading of our ship is nearly finished, and probably on the very day of our marriage she will drop down the river. Shall we go away that morning, and stay in Kent until we go on board at Gravesend within a week?”\n\n“If you please, Walter. I shall be happy anywhere. But—”\n\n“Yes, my life?”\n\n“You know,” said Florence, “that we shall have no marriage party, and that nobody will distinguish us by our dress from other people. As we leave the same day, will you—will you take me somewhere that morning, Walter—early—before we go to church?”\n\nWalter seemed to understand her, as so true a lover so truly loved should, and confirmed his ready promise with a kiss—with more than one perhaps, or two or three, or five or six; and in the grave, peaceful evening, Florence was very happy.\n\nThen into the quiet room came Susan Nipper and the candles; shortly afterwards, the tea, the Captain, and the excursive Mr Toots, who, as above mentioned, was frequently on the move afterwards, and passed but a restless evening. This, however, was not his habit: for he generally got on very well, by dint of playing at cribbage with the Captain under the advice and guidance of Miss Nipper, and distracting his mind with the calculations incidental to the game; which he found to be a very effectual means of utterly confounding himself.\n\nThe Captain’s visage on these occasions presented one of the finest examples of combination and succession of expression ever observed. His instinctive delicacy and his chivalrous feeling towards Florence, taught him that it was not a time for any boisterous jollity, or violent display of satisfaction; floating reminiscences of Lovely Peg, on the other hand, were constantly struggling for a vent, and urging the Captain to commit himself by some irreparable demonstration. Anon, his admiration of Florence and Walter—well-matched, truly, and full of grace and interest in their youth, and love, and good looks, as they sat apart—would take such complete possession of him, that he would lay down his cards, and beam upon them, dabbing his head all over with his pocket-handkerchief; until warned, perhaps, by the sudden rushing forth of Mr Toots, that he had unconsciously been very instrumental, indeed, in making that gentleman miserable. This reflection would make the Captain profoundly melancholy, until the return of Mr Toots; when he would fall to his cards again, with many side winks and nods, and polite waves of his hook at Miss Nipper, importing that he wasn’t going to do so any more. The state that ensued on this, was, perhaps, his best; for then, endeavouring to discharge all expression from his face, he would sit staring round the room, with all these expressions conveyed into it at once, and each wrestling with the other. Delighted admiration of Florence and Walter always overthrew the rest, and remained victorious and undisguised, unless Mr Toots made another rush into the air, and then the Captain would sit, like a remorseful culprit, until he came back again, occasionally calling upon himself, in a low reproachful voice, to “Stand by!” or growling some remonstrance to “Ed’ard Cuttle, my lad,” on the want of caution observable in his behaviour.\n\nOne of Mr Toots’s hardest trials, however, was of his own seeking. On the approach of the Sunday which was to witness the last of those askings in church of which the Captain had spoken, Mr Toots thus stated his feelings to Susan Nipper.\n\n“Susan,” said Mr Toots, “I am drawn towards the building. The words which cut me off from Miss Dombey for ever, will strike upon my ears like a knell you know, but upon my word and honour, I feel that I must hear them. Therefore,” said Mr Toots, “will you accompany me to-morrow, to the sacred edifice?”\n\nMiss Nipper expressed her readiness to do so, if that would be any satisfaction to Mr Toots, but besought him to abandon his idea of going.\n\n“Susan,” returned Mr Toots, with much solemnity, “before my whiskers began to be observed by anybody but myself, I adored Miss Dombey. While yet a victim to the thraldom of Blimber, I adored Miss Dombey. When I could no longer be kept out of my property, in a legal point of view, and—and accordingly came into it—I adored Miss Dombey. The banns which consign her to Lieutenant Walters, and me to—to Gloom, you know,” said Mr Toots, after hesitating for a strong expression, “may be dreadful, will be dreadful; but I feel that I should wish to hear them spoken. I feel that I should wish to know that the ground was certainly cut from under me, and that I hadn’t a hope to cherish, or a—or a leg, in short, to—to go upon.”\n\nSusan Nipper could only commiserate Mr Toots’s unfortunate condition, and agree, under these circumstances, to accompany him; which she did next morning.\n\nThe church Walter had chosen for the purpose, was a mouldy old church in a yard, hemmed in by a labyrinth of back streets and courts, with a little burying-ground round it, and itself buried in a kind of vault, formed by the neighbouring houses, and paved with echoing stones. It was a great dim, shabby pile, with high old oaken pews, among which about a score of people lost themselves every Sunday; while the clergyman’s voice drowsily resounded through the emptiness, and the organ rumbled and rolled as if the church had got the colic, for want of a congregation to keep the wind and damp out. But so far was this city church from languishing for the company of other churches, that spires were clustered round it, as the masts of shipping cluster on the river. It would have been hard to count them from its steeple-top, they were so many. In almost every yard and blind-place near, there was a church. The confusion of bells when Susan and Mr Toots betook themselves towards it on the Sunday morning, was deafening. There were twenty churches close together, clamouring for people to come in.\n\nThe two stray sheep in question were penned by a beadle in a commodious pew, and, being early, sat for some time counting the congregation, listening to the disappointed bell high up in the tower, or looking at a shabby little old man in the porch behind the screen, who was ringing the same, like the Bull in Cock Robin, with his foot in a stirrup. Mr Toots, after a lengthened survey of the large books on the reading-desk, whispered Miss Nipper that he wondered where the banns were kept, but that young lady merely shook her head and frowned; repelling for the time all approaches of a temporal nature.\n\nMr Toots, however, appearing unable to keep his thoughts from the banns, was evidently looking out for them during the whole preliminary portion of the service. As the time for reading them approached, the poor young gentleman manifested great anxiety and trepidation, which was not diminished by the unexpected apparition of the Captain in the front row of the gallery. When the clerk handed up a list to the clergyman, Mr Toots, being then seated, held on by the seat of the pew; but when the names of Walter Gay and Florence Dombey were read aloud as being in the third and last stage of that association, he was so entirley conquered by his feelings as to rush from the church without his hat, followed by the beadle and pew-opener, and two gentlemen of the medical profession, who happened to be present; of whom the first-named presently returned for that article, informing Miss Nipper in a whisper that she was not to make herself uneasy about the gentleman, as the gentleman said his indisposition was of no consequence.\n\nMiss Nipper, feeling that the eyes of that integral portion of Europe which lost itself weekly among the high-backed pews, were upon her, would have been sufficient embarrassed by this incident, though it had terminated here; the more so, as the Captain in the front row of the gallery, was in a state of unmitigated consciousness which could hardly fail to express to the congregation that he had some mysterious connection with it. But the extreme restlessness of Mr Toots painfully increased and protracted the delicacy of her situation. That young gentleman, incapable, in his state of mind, of remaining alone in the churchyard, a prey to solitary meditation, and also desirous, no doubt, of testifying his respect for the offices he had in some measure interrupted, suddenly returned—not coming back to the pew, but stationing himself on a free seat in the aisle, between two elderly females who were in the habit of receiving their portion of a weekly dole of bread then set forth on a shelf in the porch. In this conjunction Mr Toots remained, greatly disturbing the congregation, who felt it impossible to avoid looking at him, until his feelings overcame him again, when he departed silently and suddenly. Not venturing to trust himself in the church any more, and yet wishing to have some social participation in what was going on there, Mr Toots was, after this, seen from time to time, looking in, with a lorn aspect, at one or other of the windows; and as there were several windows accessible to him from without, and as his restlessness was very great, it not only became difficult to conceive at which window he would appear next, but likewise became necessary, as it were, for the whole congregation to speculate upon the chances of the different windows, during the comparative leisure afforded them by the sermon. Mr Toots’s movements in the churchyard were so eccentric, that he seemed generally to defeat all calculation, and to appear, like the conjuror’s figure, where he was least expected; and the effect of these mysterious presentations was much increased by its being difficult to him to see in, and easy to everybody else to see out: which occasioned his remaining, every time, longer than might have been expected, with his face close to the glass, until he all at once became aware that all eyes were upon him, and vanished.\n\nThese proceedings on the part of Mr Toots, and the strong individual consciousness of them that was exhibited by the Captain, rendered Miss Nipper’s position so responsible a one, that she was mightily relieved by the conclusion of the service; and was hardly so affable to Mr Toots as usual, when he informed her and the Captain, on the way back, that now he was sure he had no hope, you know, he felt more comfortable—at least not exactly more comfortable, but more comfortably and completely miserable.\n\nSwiftly now, indeed, the time flew by until it was the evening before the day appointed for the marriage. They were all assembled in the upper room at the Midshipman’s, and had no fear of interruption; for there were no lodgers in the house now, and the Midshipman had it all to himself. They were grave and quiet in the prospect of to-morrow, but moderately cheerful too. Florence, with Walter close beside her, was finishing a little piece of work intended as a parting gift to the Captain. The Captain was playing cribbage with Mr Toots. Mr Toots was taking counsel as to his hand, of Susan Nipper. Miss Nipper was giving it, with all due secrecy and circumspection. Diogenes was listening, and occasionally breaking out into a gruff half-smothered fragment of a bark, of which he afterwards seemed half-ashamed, as if he doubted having any reason for it.\n\n“Steady, steady!” said the Captain to Diogenes, “what’s amiss with you? You don’t seem easy in your mind tonight, my boy!”\n\nDiogenes wagged his tail, but pricked up his ears immediately afterwards, and gave utterance to another fragment of a bark; for which he apologised to the Captain, by again wagging his tail.\n\n“It’s my opinion, Di,” said the Captain, looking thoughtfully at his cards, and stroking his chin with his hook, “as you have your doubts of Mrs Richards; but if you’re the animal I take you to be, you’ll think better o’ that; for her looks is her commission. Now, Brother:” to Mr Toots: “if so be as you’re ready, heave ahead.”\n\nThe Captain spoke with all composure and attention to the game, but suddenly his cards dropped out of his hand, his mouth and eyes opened wide, his legs drew themselves up and stuck out in front of his chair, and he sat staring at the door with blank amazement. Looking round upon the company, and seeing that none of them observed him or the cause of his astonishment, the Captain recovered himself with a great gasp, struck the table a tremendous blow, cried in a stentorian roar, “Sol Gills ahoy!” and tumbled into the arms of a weather-beaten pea-coat that had come with Polly into the room.\n\nIn another moment, Walter was in the arms of the weather-beaten pea-coat. In another moment, Florence was in the arms of the weather-beaten pea-coat. In another moment, Captain Cuttle had embraced Mrs Richards and Miss Nipper, and was violently shaking hands with Mr Toots, exclaiming, as he waved his hook above his head, “Hooroar, my lad, hooroar!” To which Mr Toots, wholly at a loss to account for these proceedings, replied with great politeness, “Certainly, Captain Gills, whatever you think proper!”\n\nThe weather-beaten pea-coat, and a no less weather-beaten cap and comforter belonging to it, turned from the Captain and from Florence back to Walter, and sounds came from the weather-beaten pea-coat, cap, and comforter, as of an old man sobbing underneath them; while the shaggy sleeves clasped Walter tight. During this pause, there was an universal silence, and the Captain polished his nose with great diligence. But when the pea-coat, cap, and comforter lifted themselves up again, Florence gently moved towards them; and she and Walter taking them off, disclosed the old Instrument-maker, a little thinner and more careworn than of old, in his old Welsh wig and his old coffee-coloured coat and basket buttons, with his old infallible chronometer ticking away in his pocket.\n\n“Chock full o’ science,” said the radiant Captain, “as ever he was! Sol Gills, Sol Gills, what have you been up to, for this many a long day, my ould boy?”\n\n“I’m half blind, Ned,” said the old man, “and almost deaf and dumb with joy.”\n\n“His wery woice,” said the Captain, looking round with an exultation to which even his face could hardly render justice—“his wery woice as chock full o’ science as ever it was! Sol Gills, lay to, my lad, upon your own wines and fig-trees like a taut ould patriark as you are, and overhaul them there adwentures o’ yourn, in your own formilior woice. “Tis the woice,” said the Captain, impressively, and announcing a quotation with his hook, “of the sluggard, I heerd him complain, you have woke me too soon, I must slumber again. Scatter his ene-mies, and make ’em fall!”\n\nThe Captain sat down with the air of a man who had happily expressed the feeling of everybody present, and immediately rose again to present Mr Toots, who was much disconcerted by the arrival of anybody, appearing to prefer a claim to the name of Gills.\n\n“Although,” stammered Mr Toots, “I had not the pleasure of your acquaintance, Sir, before you were—you were—”\n\n“Lost to sight, to memory dear,” suggested the Captain, in a low voice.\n\n“Exactly so, Captain Gills!” assented Mr Toots. “Although I had not the pleasure of your acquaintance, Mr—Mr Sols,” said Toots, hitting on that name in the inspiration of a bright idea, “before that happened, I have the greatest pleasure, I assure you, in—you know, in knowing you. I hope,” said Mr Toots, “that you’re as well as can be expected.”\n\nWith these courteous words, Mr Toots sat down blushing and chuckling.\nThe old Instrument-maker, seated in a corner between Walter and Florence, and nodding at Polly, who was looking on, all smiles and delight, answered the Captain thus:\n\n“Ned Cuttle, my dear boy, although I have heard something of the changes of events here, from my pleasant friend there—what a pleasant face she has to be sure, to welcome a wanderer home!” said the old man, breaking off, and rubbing his hands in his old dreamy way.\n\n“Hear him!” cried the Captain gravely. “’Tis woman as seduces all mankind. For which,” aside to Mr Toots, “you’ll overhaul your Adam and Eve, brother.”\n\n“I shall make a point of doing so, Captain Gills,” said Mr Toots.\n\n“Although I have heard something of the changes of events, from her,” resumed the Instrument-maker, taking his old spectacles from his pocket, and putting them on his forehead in his old manner, “they are so great and unexpected, and I am so overpowered by the sight of my dear boy, and by the,”—glancing at the downcast eyes of Florence, and not attempting to finish the sentence—“that I—I can’t say much tonight. But my dear Ned Cuttle, why didn’t you write?”\n\nThe astonishment depicted in the Captain’s features positively frightened Mr Toots, whose eyes were quite fixed by it, so that he could not withdraw them from his face.\n\n“Write!” echoed the Captain. “Write, Sol Gills?”\n\n“Ay,” said the old man, “either to Barbados, or Jamaica, or Demerara, that was what I asked.”\n\n“What you asked, Sol Gills?” repeated the Captain.\n\n“Ay,” said the old man. “Don’t you know, Ned? Sure you have not forgotten? Every time I wrote to you.”\n\nThe Captain took off his glazed hat, hung it on his hook, and smoothing his hair from behind with his hand, sat gazing at the group around him: a perfect image of wondering resignation.\n\n“You don’t appear to understand me, Ned!” observed old Sol.\n\n“Sol Gills,” returned the Captain, after staring at him and the rest for a long time, without speaking, “I’m gone about and adrift. Pay out a word or two respecting them adwenturs, will you! Can’t I bring up, nohows? Nohows?” said the Captain, ruminating, and staring all round.\n\n“You know, Ned,” said Sol Gills, “why I left here. Did you open my packet, Ned?”\n\n“Why, ay, ay,” said the Captain. “To be sure, I opened the packet.”\n\n“And read it?” said the old man.\n\n“And read it,” answered the Captain, eyeing him attentively, and proceeding to quote it from memory. “‘My dear Ned Cuttle, when I left home for the West Indies in forlorn search of intelligence of my dear-’ There he sits! There’s Wal”r!” said the Captain, as if he were relieved by getting hold of anything that was real and indisputable.\n\n“Well, Ned. Now attend a moment!” said the old man. “When I wrote first—that was from Barbados—I said that though you would receive that letter long before the year was out, I should be glad if you would open the packet, as it explained the reason of my going away. Very good, Ned. When I wrote the second, third, and perhaps the fourth times—that was from Jamaica—I said I was in just the same state, couldn’t rest, and couldn’t come away from that part of the world, without knowing that my boy was lost or saved. When I wrote next—that, I think, was from Demerara, wasn’t it?”\n\n“That he thinks was from Demerara, warn’t it!” said the Captain, looking hopelessly round.\n\n“—I said,” proceeded old Sol, “that still there was no certain information got yet. That I found many captains and others, in that part of the world, who had known me for years, and who assisted me with a passage here and there, and for whom I was able, now and then, to do a little in return, in my own craft. That everyone was sorry for me, and seemed to take a sort of interest in my wanderings; and that I began to think it would be my fate to cruise about in search of tidings of my boy, until I died.”\n\n“Began to think as how he was a scientific Flying Dutchman!” said the Captain, as before, and with great seriousness.\n\n“But when the news come one day, Ned,—that was to Barbados, after I got back there,—that a China trader home’ard bound had been spoke, that had my boy aboard, then, Ned, I took passage in the next ship and came home; arrived at home tonight to find it true, thank God!” said the old man, devoutly.\n\nThe Captain, after bowing his head with great reverence, stared all round the circle, beginning with Mr Toots, and ending with the Instrument-maker; then gravely said:\n\n“Sol Gills! The observation as I’m a-going to make is calc’lated to blow every stitch of sail as you can carry, clean out of the bolt-ropes, and bring you on your beam ends with a lurch. Not one of them letters was ever delivered to Ed’ard Cuttle. Not one o’ them letters,” repeated the Captain, to make his declaration the more solemn and impressive, “was ever delivered unto Ed’ard Cuttle, Mariner, of England, as lives at home at ease, and doth improve each shining hour!”\n\n“And posted by my own hand! And directed by my own hand, Number nine Brig Place!” exclaimed old Sol.\n\nThe colour all went out of the Captain’s face and all came back again in a glow.\n\n“What do you mean, Sol Gills, my friend, by Number nine Brig Place?” inquired the Captain.\n\n“Mean? Your lodgings, Ned,” returned the old man. “Mrs What’s-her-name! I shall forget my own name next, but I am behind the present time—I always was, you recollect—and very much confused. Mrs—”\n\n“Sol Gills!” said the Captain, as if he were putting the most improbable case in the world, “it ain’t the name of MacStinger as you’re a trying to remember?”\n\n“Of course it is!” exclaimed the Instrument-maker. “To be sure Ned. Mrs MacStinger!”\n\nCaptain Cuttle, whose eyes were now as wide open as they would be, and the knobs upon whose face were perfectly luminous, gave a long shrill whistle of a most melancholy sound, and stood gazing at everybody in a state of speechlessness.\n\n“Overhaul that there again, Sol Gills, will you be so kind?” he said at last.\n\n“All these letters,” returned Uncle Sol, beating time with the forefinger of his right hand upon the palm of his left, with a steadiness and distinctness that might have done honour, even to the infallible chronometer in his pocket, “I posted with my own hand, and directed with my own hand, to Captain Cuttle, at Mrs MacStinger’s, Number nine Brig Place.”\n\nThe Captain took his glazed hat off his hook, looked into it, put it on, and sat down.\n\n“Why, friends all,” said the Captain, staring round in the last state of discomfiture, “I cut and run from there!”\n\n“And no one knew where you were gone, Captain Cuttle?” cried Walter hastily.\n\n“Bless your heart, Wal”r,” said the Captain, shaking his head, “she’d never have allowed o’ my coming to take charge o’ this here property. Nothing could be done but cut and run. Lord love you, Wal”r!” said the Captain, “you’ve only seen her in a calm! But see her when her angry passions rise—and make a note on!”\n\n“I’d give it her!” remarked the Nipper, softly.\n\n“Would you, do you think, my dear?” returned the Captain, with feeble admiration. “Well, my dear, it does you credit. But there ain’t no wild animal I wouldn’t sooner face myself. I only got my chest away by means of a friend as nobody’s a match for. It was no good sending any letter there. She wouldn’t take in any letter, bless you,” said the Captain, “under them circumstances! Why, you could hardly make it worth a man’s while to be the postman!”\n\n“Then it’s pretty clear, Captain Cuttle, that all of us, and you and Uncle Sol especially,” said Walter, “may thank Mrs MacStinger for no small anxiety.”\n\nThe general obligation in this wise to the determined relict of the late Mr MacStinger, was so apparent, that the Captain did not contest the point; but being in some measure ashamed of his position, though nobody dwelt upon the subject, and Walter especially avoided it, remembering the last conversation he and the Captain had held together respecting it, he remained under a cloud for nearly five minutes—an extraordinary period for him when that sun, his face, broke out once more, shining on all beholders with extraordinary brilliancy; and he fell into a fit of shaking hands with everybody over and over again.\n\nAt an early hour, but not before Uncle Sol and Walter had questioned each other at some length about their voyages and dangers, they all, except Walter, vacated Florence’s room, and went down to the parlour. Here they were soon afterwards joined by Walter, who told them Florence was a little sorrowful and heavy-hearted, and had gone to bed. Though they could not have disturbed her with their voices down there, they all spoke in a whisper after this: and each, in his different way, felt very lovingly and gently towards Walter’s fair young bride: and a long explanation there was of everything relating to her, for the satisfaction of Uncle Sol; and very sensible Mr Toots was of the delicacy with which Walter made his name and services important, and his presence necessary to their little council.\n\n“Mr Toots,” said Walter, on parting with him at the house door, “we shall see each other to-morrow morning?”\n\n“Lieutenant Walters,” returned Mr Toots, grasping his hand fervently, “I shall certainly be present.”\n\n“This is the last night we shall meet for a long time—the last night we may ever meet,” said Walter. “Such a noble heart as yours, must feel, I think, when another heart is bound to it. I hope you know that I am very grateful to you?”\n\n“Walters,” replied Mr Toots, quite touched, “I should be glad to feel that you had reason to be so.”\n\n“Florence,” said Walter, “on this last night of her bearing her own name, has made me promise—it was only just now, when you left us together—that I would tell you—with her dear love—”\n\nMr Toots laid his hand upon the doorpost, and his eyes upon his hand.\n\n“—With her dear love,” said Walter, “that she can never have a friend whom she will value above you. That the recollection of your true consideration for her always, can never be forgotten by her. That she remembers you in her prayers tonight, and hopes that you will think of her when she is far away. Shall I say anything for you?”\n\n“Say, Walter,” replied Mr Toots indistinctly, “that I shall think of her every day, but never without feeling happy to know that she is married to the man she loves, and who loves her. Say, if you please, that I am sure her husband deserves her—even her!—and that I am glad of her choice.”\n\nMr Toots got more distinct as he came to these last words, and raising his eyes from the doorpost, said them stoutly. He then shook Walter’s hand again with a fervour that Walter was not slow to return and started homeward.\n\nMr Toots was accompanied by the Chicken, whom he had of late brought with him every evening, and left in the shop, with an idea that unforeseen circumstances might arise from without, in which the prowess of that distinguished character would be of service to the Midshipman. The Chicken did not appear to be in a particularly good humour on this occasion. Either the gas-lamps were treacherous, or he cocked his eye in a hideous manner, and likewise distorted his nose, when Mr Toots, crossing the road, looked back over his shoulder at the room where Florence slept. On the road home, he was more demonstrative of aggressive intentions against the other foot-passengers, than comported with a professor of the peaceful art of self-defence. Arrived at home, instead of leaving Mr Toots in his apartments when he had escorted him thither, he remained before him weighing his white hat in both hands by the brim, and twitching his head and nose (both of which had been many times broken, and but indifferently repaired), with an air of decided disrespect.\n\nHis patron being much engaged with his own thoughts, did not observe this for some time, nor indeed until the Chicken, determined not to be overlooked, had made divers clicking sounds with his tongue and teeth, to attract attention.\n\n“Now, Master,” said the Chicken, doggedly, when he, at length, caught Mr Toots’s eye, “I want to know whether this here gammon is to finish it, or whether you’re a going in to win?”\n\n“Chicken,” returned Mr Toots, “explain yourself.”\n\n“Why then, here’s all about it, Master,” said the Chicken. “I ain’t a cove to chuck a word away. Here’s wot it is. Are any on ’em to be doubled up?”\n\nWhen the Chicken put this question he dropped his hat, made a dodge and a feint with his left hand, hit a supposed enemy a violent blow with his right, shook his head smartly, and recovered himself.\n\n“Come, Master,” said the Chicken. “Is it to be gammon or pluck? Which?”\n\n“Chicken,” returned Mr Toots, “your expressions are coarse, and your meaning is obscure.”\n\n“Why, then, I tell you what, Master,” said the Chicken. “This is where it is. It’s mean.”\n\n“What is mean, Chicken?” asked Mr Toots.\n\n“It is,” said the Chicken, with a frightful corrugation of his broken nose. “There! Now, Master! Wot! When you could go and blow on this here match to the stiff’un;” by which depreciatory appellation it has been since supposed that the Game One intended to signify Mr Dombey; “and when you could knock the winner and all the kit of ’em dead out o’ wind and time, are you going to give in? To give in?” said the Chicken, with contemptuous emphasis. “Wy, it’s mean!”\n\n“Chicken,” said Mr Toots, severely, “you’re a perfect Vulture! Your sentiments are atrocious.”\n\n“My sentiments is Game and Fancy, Master,” returned the Chicken. “That’s wot my sentiments is. I can’t abear a meanness. I’m afore the public, I’m to be heerd on at the bar of the Little Helephant, and no Gov’ner o’ mine mustn’t go and do what’s mean. Wy, it’s mean,” said the Chicken, with increased expression. “That’s where it is. It’s mean.”\n\n“Chicken,” said Mr Toots, “you disgust me.”\n\n“Master,” returned the Chicken, putting on his hat, “there’s a pair on us, then. Come! Here’s a offer! You’ve spoke to me more than once”t or twice’t about the public line. Never mind! Give me a fi’typunnote to-morrow, and let me go.”\n\n“Chicken,” returned Mr Toots, “after the odious sentiments you have expressed, I shall be glad to part on such terms.”\n\n“Done then,” said the Chicken. “It’s a bargain. This here conduct of yourn won’t suit my book, Master. Wy, it’s mean,” said the Chicken; who seemed equally unable to get beyond that point, and to stop short of it. “That’s where it is; it’s mean!”\n\nSo Mr Toots and the Chicken agreed to part on this incompatibility of moral perception; and Mr Toots lying down to sleep, dreamed happily of Florence, who had thought of him as her friend upon the last night of her maiden life, and who had sent him her dear love.\n\n# CHAPTER LVII. Another Wedding\n\nMr Sownds the beadle, and Mrs Miff the pew-opener, are early at their posts in the fine church where Mr Dombey was married. A yellow-faced old gentleman from India, is going to take unto himself a young wife this morning, and six carriages full of company are expected, and Mrs Miff has been informed that the yellow-faced old gentleman could pave the road to church with diamonds and hardly miss them.\n\nThe nuptial benediction is to be a superior one, proceeding from a very reverend, a dean, and the lady is to be given away, as an extraordinary present, by somebody who comes express from the Horse Guards.\n\nMrs Miff is more intolerant of common people this morning, than she generally is; and she has always strong opinions on that subject, for it is associated with free sittings. Mrs Miff is not a student of political economy (she thinks the science is connected with dissenters; “Baptists or Wesleyans, or some o’ them,” she says), but she can never understand what business your common folks have to be married. “Drat ’em,” says Mrs Miff “you read the same things over ’em and instead of sovereigns get sixpences!”\n\nMr Sownds the beadle is more liberal than Mrs Miff—but then he is not a pew-opener. “It must be done, Ma’am,” he says. “We must marry ’em. We must have our national schools to walk at the head of, and we must have our standing armies. We must marry ’em, Ma’am,” says Mr Sownds, “and keep the country going.”\n\nMr Sownds is sitting on the steps and Mrs Miff is dusting in the church, when a young couple, plainly dressed, come in. The mortified bonnet of Mrs Miff is sharply turned towards them, for she espies in this early visit indications of a runaway match. But they don’t want to be married—“Only,” says the gentleman, “to walk round the church.” And as he slips a genteel compliment into the palm of Mrs Miff, her vinegary face relaxes, and her mortified bonnet and her spare dry figure dip and crackle.\n\nMrs Miff resumes her dusting and plumps up her cushions—for the yellow-faced old gentleman is reported to have tender knees—but keeps her glazed, pew-opening eye on the young couple who are walking round the church. “Ahem,” coughs Mrs Miff whose cough is drier than the hay in any hassock in her charge, “you’ll come to us one of these mornings, my dears, unless I’m much mistaken!”\n\nThey are looking at a tablet on the wall, erected to the memory of someone dead. They are a long way off from Mrs Miff, but Mrs Miff can see with half an eye how she is leaning on his arm, and how his head is bent down over her. “Well, well,” says Mrs Miff, “you might do worse. For you’re a tidy pair!”\n\nThere is nothing personal in Mrs Miff’s remark. She merely speaks of stock-in-trade. She is hardly more curious in couples than in coffins. She is such a spare, straight, dry old lady—such a pew of a woman—that you should find as many individual sympathies in a chip. Mr Sownds, now, who is fleshy, and has scarlet in his coat, is of a different temperament. He says, as they stand upon the steps watching the young couple away, that she has a pretty figure, hasn’t she, and as well as he could see (for she held her head down coming out), an uncommon pretty face. “Altogether, Mrs Miff,” says Mr Sownds with a relish, “she is what you may call a rose-bud.”\n\nMrs Miff assents with a spare nod of her mortified bonnet; but approves of this so little, that she inwardly resolves she wouldn’t be the wife of Mr Sownds for any money he could give her, Beadle as he is.\n\nAnd what are the young couple saying as they leave the church, and go out at the gate?\n\n“Dear Walter, thank you! I can go away, now, happy.”\n\n“And when we come back, Florence, we will come and see his grave again.”\n\nFlorence lifts her eyes, so bright with tears, to his kind face; and clasps her disengaged hand on that other modest little hand which clasps his arm.\n\n“It is very early, Walter, and the streets are almost empty yet. Let us walk.”\n\n“But you will be so tired, my love.”\n\n“Oh no! I was very tired the first time that we ever walked together, but I shall not be so today.”\n\nAnd thus—not much changed—she, as innocent and earnest-hearted—he, as frank, as hopeful, and more proud of her—Florence and Walter, on their bridal morning, walk through the streets together.\n\nNot even in that childish walk of long ago, were they so far removed from all the world about them as today. The childish feet of long ago, did not tread such enchanted ground as theirs do now. The confidence and love of children may be given many times, and will spring up in many places; but the woman’s heart of Florence, with its undivided treasure, can be yielded only once, and under slight or change, can only droop and die.\n\nThey take the streets that are the quietest, and do not go near that in which her old home stands. It is a fair, warm summer morning, and the sun shines on them, as they walk towards the darkening mist that overspreads the City. Riches are uncovering in shops; jewels, gold, and silver flash in the goldsmith’s sunny windows; and great houses cast a stately shade upon them as they pass. But through the light, and through the shade, they go on lovingly together, lost to everything around; thinking of no other riches, and no prouder home, than they have now in one another.\n\nGradually they come into the darker, narrower streets, where the sun, now yellow, and now red, is seen through the mist, only at street corners, and in small open spaces where there is a tree, or one of the innumerable churches, or a paved way and a flight of steps, or a curious little patch of garden, or a burying-ground, where the few tombs and tombstones are almost black. Lovingly and trustfully, through all the narrow yards and alleys and the shady streets, Florence goes, clinging to his arm, to be his wife.\n\nHer heart beats quicker now, for Walter tells her that their church is very near. They pass a few great stacks of warehouses, with waggons at the doors, and busy carmen stopping up the way—but Florence does not see or hear them—and then the air is quiet, and the day is darkened, and she is trembling in a church which has a strange smell like a cellar.\n\nThe shabby little old man, ringer of the disappointed bell, is standing in the porch, and has put his hat in the font—for he is quite at home there, being sexton. He ushers them into an old brown, panelled, dusty vestry, like a corner-cupboard with the shelves taken out; where the wormy registers diffuse a smell like faded snuff, which has set the tearful Nipper sneezing.\n\nYouthful, and how beautiful, the young bride looks, in this old dusty place, with no kindred object near her but her husband. There is a dusty old clerk, who keeps a sort of evaporated news shop underneath an archway opposite, behind a perfect fortification of posts. There is a dusty old pew-opener who only keeps herself, and finds that quite enough to do. There is a dusty old beadle (these are Mr Toots’s beadle and pew-opener of last Sunday), who has something to do with a Worshipful Company who have got a Hall in the next yard, with a stained-glass window in it that no mortal ever saw. There are dusty wooden ledges and cornices poked in and out over the altar, and over the screen and round the gallery, and over the inscription about what the Master and Wardens of the Worshipful Company did in one thousand six hundred and ninety-four. There are dusty old sounding-boards over the pulpit and reading-desk, looking like lids to be let down on the officiating ministers in case of their giving offence. There is every possible provision for the accommodation of dust, except in the churchyard, where the facilities in that respect are very limited.\n\nThe Captain, Uncle Sol, and Mr Toots are come; the clergyman is putting on his surplice in the vestry, while the clerk walks round him, blowing the dust off it; and the bride and bridegroom stand before the altar. There is no bridesmaid, unless Susan Nipper is one; and no better father than Captain Cuttle. A man with a wooden leg, chewing a faint apple and carrying a blue bag in has hand, looks in to see what is going on; but finding it nothing entertaining, stumps off again, and pegs his way among the echoes out of doors.\n\nNo gracious ray of light is seen to fall on Florence, kneeling at the altar with her timid head bowed down. The morning luminary is built out, and don’t shine there. There is a meagre tree outside, where the sparrows are chirping a little; and there is a blackbird in an eyelet-hole of sun in a dyer’s garret, over against the window, who whistles loudly whilst the service is performing; and there is the man with the wooden leg stumping away. The amens of the dusty clerk appear, like Macbeth’s, to stick in his throat a little; but Captain Cuttle helps him out, and does it with so much goodwill that he interpolates three entirely new responses of that word, never introduced into the service before.\n\nThey are married, and have signed their names in one of the old sneezy registers, and the clergyman’s surplice is restored to the dust, and the clergyman is gone home. In a dark corner of the dark church, Florence has turned to Susan Nipper, and is weeping in her arms. Mr Toots’s eyes are red. The Captain lubricates his nose. Uncle Sol has pulled down his spectacles from his forehead, and walked out to the door.\n\n“God bless you, Susan; dearest Susan! If you ever can bear witness to the love I have for Walter, and the reason that I have to love him, do it for his sake. Good-bye! Good-bye!”\n\nThey have thought it better not to go back to the Midshipman, but to part so; a coach is waiting for them, near at hand.\n\nMiss Nipper cannot speak; she only sobs and chokes, and hugs her mistress. Mr Toots advances, urges her to cheer up, and takes charge of her. Florence gives him her hand—gives him, in the fulness of her heart, her lips—kisses Uncle Sol, and Captain Cuttle, and is borne away by her young husband.\n\nBut Susan cannot bear that Florence should go away with a mournful recollection of her. She had meant to be so different, that she reproaches herself bitterly. Intent on making one last effort to redeem her character, she breaks from Mr Toots and runs away to find the coach, and show a parting smile. The Captain, divining her object, sets off after her; for he feels it his duty also to dismiss them with a cheer, if possible. Uncle Sol and Mr Toots are left behind together, outside the church, to wait for them.\n\nThe coach is gone, but the street is steep, and narrow, and blocked up, and Susan can see it at a stand-still in the distance, she is sure. Captain Cuttle follows her as she flies down the hill, and waves his glazed hat as a general signal, which may attract the right coach and which may not.\n\nSusan outstrips the Captain, and comes up with it. She looks in at the window, sees Walter, with the gentle face beside him, and claps her hands and screams:\n\n“Miss Floy, my darling! look at me! We are all so happy now, dear! One more good-bye, my precious, one more!”\n\nHow Susan does it, she don’t know, but she reaches to the window, kisses her, and has her arms about her neck, in a moment.\n\n“We are all so—so happy now, my dear Miss Floy!” says Susan, with a suspicious catching in her breath. “You, you won’t be angry with me now. Now will you?”\n\n“Angry, Susan!”\n\n“No, no; I am sure you won’t. I say you won’t, my pet, my dearest!” exclaims Susan; “and here’s the Captain too—your friend the Captain, you know—to say good-bye once more!”\n\n“Hooroar, my Heart’s Delight!” vociferates the Captain, with a countenance of strong emotion. “Hooroar, Wal”r my lad. Hooroar! Hooroar!”\n\nWhat with the young husband at one window, and the young wife at the other; the Captain hanging on at this door, and Susan Nipper holding fast by that; the coach obliged to go on whether it will or no, and all the other carts and coaches turbulent because it hesitates; there never was so much confusion on four wheels. But Susan Nipper gallantly maintains her point. She keeps a smiling face upon her mistress, smiling through her tears, until the last. Even when she is left behind, the Captain continues to appear and disappear at the door, crying “Hooroar, my lad! Hooroar, my Heart’s Delight!” with his shirt-collar in a violent state of agitation, until it is hopeless to attempt to keep up with the coach any longer. Finally, when the coach is gone, Susan Nipper, being rejoined by the Captain, falls into a state of insensibility, and is taken into a baker’s shop to recover.\n\nUncle Sol and Mr Toots wait patiently in the churchyard, sitting on the coping-stone of the railings, until Captain Cuttle and Susan come back. Neither being at all desirous to speak, or to be spoken to, they are excellent company, and quite satisfied. When they all arrive again at the little Midshipman, and sit down to breakfast, nobody can touch a morsel. Captain Cuttle makes a feint of being voracious about toast, but gives it up as a swindle. Mr Toots says, after breakfast, he will come back in the evening; and goes wandering about the town all day, with a vague sensation upon him as if he hadn’t been to bed for a fortnight.\n\nThere is a strange charm in the house, and in the room, in which they have been used to be together, and out of which so much is gone. It aggravates, and yet it soothes, the sorrow of the separation. Mr Toots tells Susan Nipper when he comes at night, that he hasn’t been so wretched all day long, and yet he likes it. He confides in Susan Nipper, being alone with her, and tells her what his feelings were when she gave him that candid opinion as to the probability of Miss Dombey’s ever loving him. In the vein of confidence engendered by these common recollections, and their tears, Mr Toots proposes that they shall go out together, and buy something for supper. Miss Nipper assenting, they buy a good many little things; and, with the aid of Mrs Richards, set the supper out quite showily before the Captain and old Sol came home.\n\nThe Captain and old Sol have been on board the ship, and have established Di there, and have seen the chests put aboard. They have much to tell about the popularity of Walter, and the comforts he will have about him, and the quiet way in which it seems he has been working early and late, to make his cabin what the Captain calls “a picter,” to surprise his little wife. “A admiral’s cabin, mind you,” says the Captain, “ain’t more trim.”\n\nBut one of the Captain’s chief delights is, that he knows the big watch, and the sugar-tongs, and tea-spoons, are on board: and again and again he murmurs to himself, “Ed’ard Cuttle, my lad, you never shaped a better course in your life than when you made that there little property over jintly. You see how the land bore, Ed’ard,” says the Captain, “and it does you credit, my lad.”\n\nThe old Instrument-maker is more distraught and misty than he used to be, and takes the marriage and the parting very much to heart. But he is greatly comforted by having his old ally, Ned Cuttle, at his side; and he sits down to supper with a grateful and contented face.\n\n“My boy has been preserved and thrives,” says old Sol Gills, rubbing his hands. “What right have I to be otherwise than thankful and happy!”\n\nThe Captain, who has not yet taken his seat at the table, but who has been fidgeting about for some time, and now stands hesitating in his place, looks doubtfully at Mr Gills, and says:\n\n“Sol! There’s the last bottle of the old Madeira down below. Would you wish to have it up tonight, my boy, and drink to Wal”r and his wife?”\n\nThe Instrument-maker, looking wistfully at the Captain, puts his hand into the breast-pocket of his coffee-coloured coat, brings forth his pocket-book, and takes a letter out.\n\n“To Mr Dombey,” says the old man. “From Walter. To be sent in three weeks’ time. I’ll read it.”\n\n“‘Sir. I am married to your daughter. She is gone with me upon a distant voyage. To be devoted to her is to have no claim on her or you, but God knows that I am.\n\n“‘Why, loving her beyond all earthly things, I have yet, without remorse, united her to the uncertainties and dangers of my life, I will not say to you. You know why, and you are her father.\n\n“‘Do not reproach her. She has never reproached you.\n\n“‘I do not think or hope that you will ever forgive me. There is nothing I expect less. But if an hour should come when it will comfort you to believe that Florence has someone ever near her, the great charge of whose life is to cancel her remembrance of past sorrow, I solemnly assure you, you may, in that hour, rest in that belief.’”\n\nSolomon puts back the letter carefully in his pocket-book, and puts back his pocket-book in his coat.\n\n“We won’t drink the last bottle of the old Madeira yet, Ned,” says the old man thoughtfully. “Not yet.\n\n“Not yet,” assents the Captain. “No. Not yet.”\n\nSusan and Mr Toots are of the same opinion. After a silence they all sit down to supper, and drink to the young husband and wife in something else; and the last bottle of the old Madeira still remains among its dust and cobwebs, undisturbed.\n\nA few days have elapsed, and a stately ship is out at sea, spreading its white wings to the favouring wind.\n\nUpon the deck, image to the roughest man on board of something that is graceful, beautiful, and harmless—something that it is good and pleasant to have there, and that should make the voyage prosperous—is Florence. It is night, and she and Walter sit alone, watching the solemn path of light upon the sea between them and the moon.\n\nAt length she cannot see it plainly, for the tears that fill her eyes; and then she lays her head down on his breast, and puts her arms around his neck, saying, “Oh Walter, dearest love, I am so happy!”\n\nHer husband holds her to his heart, and they are very quiet, and the stately ship goes on serenely.\n\n“As I hear the sea,” says Florence, “and sit watching it, it brings so many days into my mind. It makes me think so much—”\n\n“Of Paul, my love. I know it does.”\n\nOf Paul and Walter. And the voices in the waves are always whispering to Florence, in their ceaseless murmuring, of love—of love, eternal and illimitable, not bounded by the confines of this world, or by the end of time, but ranging still, beyond the sea, beyond the sky, to the invisible country far away!\n\n# CHAPTER LVIII. After a Lapse\n\nThe sea had ebbed and flowed, through a whole year. Through a whole year, the winds and clouds had come and gone; the ceaseless work of Time had been performed, in storm and sunshine. Through a whole year, the tides of human chance and change had set in their allotted courses. Through a whole year, the famous House of Dombey and Son had fought a fight for life, against cross accidents, doubtful rumours, unsuccessful ventures, unpropitious times, and most of all, against the infatuation of its head, who would not contract its enterprises by a hair’s breadth, and would not listen to a word of warning that the ship he strained so hard against the storm, was weak, and could not bear it.\n\nThe year was out, and the great House was down.\n\nOne summer afternoon; a year, wanting some odd days, after the marriage in the City church; there was a buzz and whisper upon “Change of a great failure. A certain cold proud man, well known there, was not there, nor was he represented there. Next day it was noised abroad that Dombey and Son had stopped, and next night there was a List of Bankrupts published, headed by that name.\n\nThe world was very busy now, in sooth, and had a deal to say. It was an innocently credulous and a much ill-used world. It was a world in which there was no other sort of bankruptcy whatever. There were no conspicuous people in it, trading far and wide on rotten banks of religion, patriotism, virtue, honour. There was no amount worth mentioning of mere paper in circulation, on which anybody lived pretty handsomely, promising to pay great sums of goodness with no effects. There were no shortcomings anywhere, in anything but money. The world was very angry indeed; and the people especially, who, in a worse world, might have been supposed to be apt traders themselves in shows and pretences, were observed to be mightily indignant.\n\nHere was a new inducement to dissipation, presented to that sport of circumstances, Mr Perch the Messenger! It was apparently the fate of Mr Perch to be always waking up, and finding himself famous. He had but yesterday, as one might say, subsided into private life from the celebrity of the elopement and the events that followed it; and now he was made a more important man than ever, by the bankruptcy. Gliding from his bracket in the outer office where he now sat, watching the strange faces of accountants and others, who quickly superseded nearly all the old clerks, Mr Perch had but to show himself in the court outside, or, at farthest, in the bar of the King’s Arms, to be asked a multitude of questions, almost certain to include that interesting question, what would he take to drink? Then would Mr Perch descant upon the hours of acute uneasiness he and Mrs Perch had suffered out at Balls Pond, when they first suspected “things was going wrong.” Then would Mr Perch relate to gaping listeners, in a low voice, as if the corpse of the deceased House were lying unburied in the next room, how Mrs Perch had first come to surmise that things was going wrong by hearing him (Perch) moaning in his sleep, “twelve and ninepence in the pound, twelve and ninepence in the pound!” Which act of somnambulism he supposed to have originated in the impression made upon him by the change in Mr Dombey’s face. Then would he inform them how he had once said, “Might I make so bold as ask, Sir, are you unhappy in your mind?” and how Mr Dombey had replied, “My faithful Perch—but no, it cannot be!” and with that had struck his hand upon his forehead, and said, “Leave me, Perch!” Then, in short, would Mr Perch, a victim to his position, tell all manner of lies; affecting himself to tears by those that were of a moving nature, and really believing that the inventions of yesterday had, on repetition, a sort of truth about them today.\n\nMr Perch always closed these conferences by meekly remarking, that, of course, whatever his suspicions might have been (as if he had ever had any!) it wasn’t for him to betray his trust, was it? Which sentiment (there never being any creditors present) was received as doing great honour to his feelings. Thus, he generally brought away a soothed conscience and left an agreeable impression behind him, when he returned to his bracket: again to sit watching the strange faces of the accountants and others, making so free with the great mysteries, the Books; or now and then to go on tiptoe into Mr Dombey’s empty room, and stir the fire; or to take an airing at the door, and have a little more doleful chat with any straggler whom he knew; or to propitiate, with various small attentions, the head accountant: from whom Mr Perch had expectations of a messengership in a Fire Office, when the affairs of the House should be wound up.\n\nTo Major Bagstock, the bankruptcy was quite a calamity. The Major was not a sympathetic character—his attention being wholly concentrated on J. B.—nor was he a man subject to lively emotions, except in the physical regards of gasping and choking. But he had so paraded his friend Dombey at the club; had so flourished him at the heads of the members in general, and so put them down by continual assertion of his riches; that the club, being but human, was delighted to retort upon the Major, by asking him, with a show of great concern, whether this tremendous smash had been at all expected, and how his friend Dombey bore it. To such questions, the Major, waxing very purple, would reply that it was a bad world, Sir, altogether; that Joey knew a thing or two, but had been done, Sir, done like an infant; that if you had foretold this, Sir, to J. Bagstock, when he went abroad with Dombey and was chasing that vagabond up and down France, J. Bagstock would have pooh-pooh’d you—would have pooh-pooh’d you, Sir, by the Lord! That Joe had been deceived, Sir, taken in, hoodwinked, blindfolded, but was broad awake again and staring; insomuch, Sir, that if Joe’s father were to rise up from the grave to-morrow, he wouldn’t trust the old blade with a penny piece, but would tell him that his son Josh was too old a soldier to be done again, Sir. That he was a suspicious, crabbed, cranky, used-up, J. B. infidel, Sir; and that if it were consistent with the dignity of a rough and tough old Major, of the old school, who had had the honour of being personally known to, and commended by, their late Royal Highnesses the Dukes of Kent and York, to retire to a tub and live in it, by Gad! Sir, he’d have a tub in Pall Mall to-morrow, to show his contempt for mankind!\n\nOf all this, and many variations of the same tune, the Major would deliver himself with so many apoplectic symptoms, such rollings of his head, and such violent growls of ill usage and resentment, that the younger members of the club surmised he had invested money in his friend Dombey’s House, and lost it; though the older soldiers and deeper dogs, who knew Joe better, wouldn’t hear of such a thing. The unfortunate Native, expressing no opinion, suffered dreadfully; not merely in his moral feelings, which were regularly fusilladed by the Major every hour in the day, and riddled through and through, but in his sensitiveness to bodily knocks and bumps, which was kept continually on the stretch. For six entire weeks after the bankruptcy, this miserable foreigner lived in a rainy season of boot-jacks and brushes.\n\nMrs Chick had three ideas upon the subject of the terrible reverse. The first was that she could not understand it. The second, that her brother had not made an effort. The third, that if she had been invited to dinner on the day of that first party, it never would have happened; and that she had said so, at the time.\n\nNobody’s opinion stayed the misfortune, lightened it, or made it heavier. It was understood that the affairs of the House were to be wound up as they best could be; that Mr Dombey freely resigned everything he had, and asked for no favour from anyone. That any resumption of the business was out of the question, as he would listen to no friendly negotiation having that compromise in view; that he had relinquished every post of trust or distinction he had held, as a man respected among merchants; that he was dying, according to some; that he was going melancholy mad, according to others; that he was a broken man, according to all.\n\nThe clerks dispersed after holding a little dinner of condolence among themselves, which was enlivened by comic singing, and went off admirably. Some took places abroad, and some engaged in other Houses at home; some looked up relations in the country, for whom they suddenly remembered they had a particular affection; and some advertised for employment in the newspapers. Mr Perch alone remained of all the late establishment, sitting on his bracket looking at the accountants, or starting off it, to propitiate the head accountant, who was to get him into the Fire Office. The Counting House soon got to be dirty and neglected. The principal slipper and dogs’ collar seller, at the corner of the court, would have doubted the propriety of throwing up his forefinger to the brim of his hat, any more, if Mr Dombey had appeared there now; and the ticket porter, with his hands under his white apron, moralised good sound morality about ambition, which (he observed) was not, in his opinion, made to rhyme to perdition, for nothing.\n\nMr Morfin, the hazel-eyed bachelor, with the hair and whiskers sprinkled with grey, was perhaps the only person within the atmosphere of the House—its head, of course, excepted—who was heartily and deeply affected by the disaster that had befallen it. He had treated Mr Dombey with due respect and deference through many years, but he had never disguised his natural character, or meanly truckled to him, or pampered his master passion for the advancement of his own purposes. He had, therefore, no self-disrespect to avenge; no long-tightened springs to release with a quick recoil. He worked early and late to unravel whatever was complicated or difficult in the records of the transactions of the House; was always in attendance to explain whatever required explanation; sat in his old room sometimes very late at night, studying points by his mastery of which he could spare Mr Dombey the pain of being personally referred to; and then would go home to Islington, and calm his mind by producing the most dismal and forlorn sounds out of his violoncello before going to bed.\n\nHe was solacing himself with this melodious grumbler one evening, and, having been much dispirited by the proceedings of the day, was scraping consolation out of its deepest notes, when his landlady (who was fortunately deaf, and had no other consciousness of these performances than a sensation of something rumbling in her bones) announced a lady.\n\n“In mourning,” she said.\n\nThe violoncello stopped immediately; and the performer, laying it on the sofa with great tenderness and care, made a sign that the lady was to come in. He followed directly, and met Harriet Carker on the stair.\n\n“Alone!” he said, “and John here this morning! Is there anything the matter, my dear? But no,” he added, “your face tells quite another story.”\n\n“I am afraid it is a selfish revelation that you see there, then,” she answered.\n\n“It is a very pleasant one,” said he; “and, if selfish, a novelty too, worth seeing in you. But I don’t believe that.”\n\nHe had placed a chair for her by this time, and sat down opposite; the violoncello lying snugly on the sofa between them.\n\n“You will not be surprised at my coming alone, or at John’s not having told you I was coming,” said Harriet; “and you will believe that, when I tell you why I have come. May I do so now?”\n\n“You can do nothing better.”\n\n“You were not busy?”\n\nHe pointed to the violoncello lying on the sofa, and said “I have been, all day. Here’s my witness. I have been confiding all my cares to it. I wish I had none but my own to tell.”\n\n“Is the House at an end?” said Harriet, earnestly.\n\n“Completely at an end.”\n\n“Will it never be resumed?”\n\n“Never.”\n\nThe bright expression of her face was not overshadowed as her lips silently repeated the word. He seemed to observe this with some little involuntary surprise: and said again:\n\n“Never. You remember what I told you. It has been, all along, impossible to convince him; impossible to reason with him; sometimes, impossible even to approach him. The worst has happened; and the House has fallen, never to be built up any more.”\n\n“And Mr Dombey, is he personally ruined?”\n\n“Ruined.”\n\n“Will he have no private fortune left? Nothing?”\n\nA certain eagerness in her voice, and something that was almost joyful in her look, seemed to surprise him more and more; to disappoint him too, and jar discordantly against his own emotions. He drummed with the fingers of one hand on the table, looking wistfully at her, and shaking his head, said, after a pause:\n\n“The extent of Mr Dombey’s resources is not accurately within my knowledge; but though they are doubtless very large, his obligations are enormous. He is a gentleman of high honour and integrity. Any man in his position could, and many a man in his position would, have saved himself, by making terms which would have very slightly, almost insensibly, increased the losses of those who had had dealings with him, and left him a remnant to live upon. But he is resolved on payment to the last farthing of his means. His own words are, that they will clear, or nearly clear, the House, and that no one can lose much. Ah, Miss Harriet, it would do us no harm to remember oftener than we do, that vices are sometimes only virtues carried to excess! His pride shows well in this.”\n\nShe heard him with little or no change in her expression, and with a divided attention that showed her to be busy with something in her own mind. When he was silent, she asked him hurriedly:\n\n“Have you seen him lately?”\n\n“No one sees him. When this crisis of his affairs renders it necessary for him to come out of his house, he comes out for the occasion, and again goes home, and shuts himself up, and will see no one. He has written me a letter, acknowledging our past connexion in higher terms than it deserved, and parting from me. I am delicate of obtruding myself upon him now, never having had much intercourse with him in better times; but I have tried to do so. I have written, gone there, entreated. Quite in vain.”\n\nHe watched her, as in the hope that she would testify some greater concern than she had yet shown; and spoke gravely and feelingly, as if to impress her the more; but there was no change in her.\n\n“Well, well, Miss Harriet,” he said, with a disappointed air, “this is not to the purpose. You have not come here to hear this. Some other and pleasanter theme is in your mind. Let it be in mine, too, and we shall talk upon more equal terms. Come!”\n\n“No, it is the same theme,” returned Harriet, with frank and quick surprise. “Is it not likely that it should be? Is it not natural that John and I should have been thinking and speaking very much of late of these great changes? Mr Dombey, whom he served so many years—you know upon what terms—reduced, as you describe; and we quite rich!”\n\nGood, true face, as that face of hers was, and pleasant as it had been to him, Mr Morfin, the hazel-eyed bachelor, since the first time he had ever looked upon it, it pleased him less at that moment, lighted with a ray of exultation, than it had ever pleased him before.\n\n“I need not remind you,” said Harriet, casting down her eyes upon her black dress, “through what means our circumstances changed. You have not forgotten that our brother James, upon that dreadful day, left no will, no relations but ourselves.”\n\nThe face was pleasanter to him now, though it was pale and melancholy, than it had been a moment since. He seemed to breathe more cheerily.\n\n“You know,” she said, “our history, the history of both my brothers, in connexion with the unfortunate, unhappy gentleman, of whom you have spoken so truly. You know how few our wants are—John’s and mine—and what little use we have for money, after the life we have led together for so many years; and now that he is earning an income that is ample for us, through your kindness. You are not unprepared to hear what favour I have come to ask of you?”\n\n“I hardly know. I was, a minute ago. Now, I think, I am not.”\n\n“Of my dead brother I say nothing. If the dead know what we do—but you understand me. Of my living brother I could say much; but what need I say more, than that this act of duty, in which I have come to ask your indispensable assistance, is his own, and that he cannot rest until it is performed!”\n\nShe raised her eyes again; and the light of exultation in her face began to appear beautiful, in the observant eyes that watched her.\n\n“Dear Sir,” she went on to say, “it must be done very quietly and secretly. Your experience and knowledge will point out a way of doing it. Mr Dombey may, perhaps, be led to believe that it is something saved, unexpectedly, from the wreck of his fortunes; or that it is a voluntary tribute to his honourable and upright character, from some of those with whom he has had great dealings; or that it is some old lost debt repaid. There must be many ways of doing it. I know you will choose the best. The favour I have come to ask is, that you will do it for us in your own kind, generous, considerate manner. That you will never speak of it to John, whose chief happiness in this act of restitution is to do it secretly, unknown, and unapproved of: that only a very small part of the inheritance may be reserved to us, until Mr Dombey shall have possessed the interest of the rest for the remainder of his life; that you will keep our secret, faithfully—but that I am sure you will; and that, from this time, it may seldom be whispered, even between you and me, but may live in my thoughts only as a new reason for thankfulness to Heaven, and joy and pride in my brother.”\n\nSuch a look of exultation there may be on Angels’ faces when the one repentant sinner enters Heaven, among ninety-nine just men. It was not dimmed or tarnished by the joyful tears that filled her eyes, but was the brighter for them.\n\n“My dear Harriet,” said Mr Morfin, after a silence, “I was not prepared for this. Do I understand you that you wish to make your own part in the inheritance available for your good purpose, as well as John’s?”\n\n“Oh, yes,” she returned “When we have shared everything together for so long a time, and have had no care, hope, or purpose apart, could I bear to be excluded from my share in this? May I not urge a claim to be my brother’s partner and companion to the last?”\n\n“Heaven forbid that I should dispute it!” he replied.\n\n“We may rely on your friendly help?” she said. “I knew we might!”\n\n“I should be a worse man than,—than I hope I am, or would willingly believe myself, if I could not give you that assurance from my heart and soul. You may, implicitly. Upon my honour, I will keep your secret. And if it should be found that Mr Dombey is so reduced as I fear he will be, acting on a determination that there seem to be no means of influencing, I will assist you to accomplish the design, on which you and John are jointly resolved.”\n\nShe gave him her hand, and thanked him with a cordial, happy face.\n\n“Harriet,” he said, detaining it in his. “To speak to you of the worth of any sacrifice that you can make now—above all, of any sacrifice of mere money—would be idle and presumptuous. To put before you any appeal to reconsider your purpose or to set narrow limits to it, would be, I feel, not less so. I have no right to mar the great end of a great history, by any obtrusion of my own weak self. I have every right to bend my head before what you confide to me, satisfied that it comes from a higher and better source of inspiration than my poor worldly knowledge. I will say only this: I am your faithful steward; and I would rather be so, and your chosen friend, than I would be anybody in the world, except yourself.”\n\nShe thanked him again, cordially, and wished him good-night.\n\n“Are you going home?” he said. “Let me go with you.”\n\n“Not tonight. I am not going home now; I have a visit to make alone. Will you come to-morrow?”\n\n“Well, well,” said he, “I’ll come to-morrow. In the meantime, I’ll think of this, and how we can best proceed. And perhaps I’ll think of it, dear Harriet, and—and—think of me a little in connexion with it.”\n\nHe handed her down to a coach she had in waiting at the door; and if his landlady had not been deaf, she would have heard him muttering as he went back upstairs, when the coach had driven off, that we were creatures of habit, and it was a sorrowful habit to be an old bachelor.\n\nThe violoncello lying on the sofa between the two chairs, he took it up, without putting away the vacant chair, and sat droning on it, and slowly shaking his head at the vacant chair, for a long, long time. The expression he communicated to the instrument at first, though monstrously pathetic and bland, was nothing to the expression he communicated to his own face, and bestowed upon the empty chair: which was so sincere, that he was obliged to have recourse to Captain Cuttle’s remedy more than once, and to rub his face with his sleeve. By degrees, however, the violoncello, in unison with his own frame of mind, glided melodiously into the Harmonious Blacksmith, which he played over and over again, until his ruddy and serene face gleamed like true metal on the anvil of a veritable blacksmith. In fine, the violoncello and the empty chair were the companions of his bachelorhood until nearly midnight; and when he took his supper, the violoncello set up on end in the sofa corner, big with the latent harmony of a whole foundry full of harmonious blacksmiths, seemed to ogle the empty chair out of its crooked eyes, with unutterable intelligence.\n\nWhen Harriet left the house, the driver of her hired coach, taking a course that was evidently no new one to him, went in and out by bye-ways, through that part of the suburbs, until he arrived at some open ground, where there were a few quiet little old houses standing among gardens. At the garden-gate of one of these he stopped, and Harriet alighted.\n\nHer gentle ringing at the bell was responded to by a dolorous-looking woman, of light complexion, with raised eyebrows, and head drooping on one side, who curtseyed at sight of her, and conducted her across the garden to the house.\n\n“How is your patient, nurse, tonight?” said Harriet.\n\n“In a poor way, Miss, I am afraid. Oh how she do remind me, sometimes, of my Uncle’s Betsey Jane!” returned the woman of the light complexion, in a sort of doleful rapture.\n\n“In what respect?” asked Harriet.\n\n“Miss, in all respects,” replied the other, “except that she’s grown up, and Betsey Jane, when at death’s door, was but a child.”\n\n“But you have told me she recovered,” observed Harriet mildly; “so there is the more reason for hope, Mrs Wickam.”\n\n“Ah, Miss, hope is an excellent thing for such as has the spirits to bear it!” said Mrs Wickam, shaking her head. “My own spirits is not equal to it, but I don’t owe it any grudge. I envys them that is so blest!”\n\n“You should try to be more cheerful,” remarked Harriet.\n\n“Thank you, Miss, I’m sure,” said Mrs Wickam grimly. “If I was so inclined, the loneliness of this situation—you’ll excuse my speaking so free—would put it out of my power, in four and twenty hours; but I ain’t at all. I’d rather not. The little spirits that I ever had, I was bereaved of at Brighton some few years ago, and I think I feel myself the better for it.”\n\nIn truth, this was the very Mrs Wickam who had superseded Mrs Richards as the nurse of little Paul, and who considered herself to have gained the loss in question, under the roof of the amiable Pipchin. The excellent and thoughtful old system, hallowed by long prescription, which has usually picked out from the rest of mankind the most dreary and uncomfortable people that could possibly be laid hold of, to act as instructors of youth, finger-posts to the virtues, matrons, monitors, attendants on sick beds, and the like, had established Mrs Wickam in very good business as a nurse, and had led to her serious qualities being particularly commended by an admiring and numerous connexion.\n\nMrs Wickam, with her eyebrows elevated, and her head on one side, lighted the way upstairs to a clean, neat chamber, opening on another chamber dimly lighted, where there was a bed. In the first room, an old woman sat mechanically staring out at the open window, on the darkness. In the second, stretched upon the bed, lay the shadow of a figure that had spurned the wind and rain, one wintry night; hardly to be recognised now, but by the long black hair that showed so very black against the colourless face, and all the white things about it.\n\nOh, the strong eyes, and the weak frame! The eyes that turned so eagerly and brightly to the door when Harriet came in; the feeble head that could not raise itself, and moved so slowly round upon its pillow!\n\n“Alice!” said the visitor’s mild voice, “am I late tonight?”\n\n“You always seem late, but are always early.”\n\nHarriet had sat down by the bedside now, and put her hand upon the thin hand lying there.\n\n“You are better?”\n\nMrs Wickam, standing at the foot of the bed, like a disconsolate spectre, most decidedly and forcibly shook her head to negative this position.\n\n“It matters very little!” said Alice, with a faint smile. “Better or worse today, is but a day’s difference—perhaps not so much.”\n\nMrs Wickam, as a serious character, expressed her approval with a groan; and having made some cold dabs at the bottom of the bedclothes, as feeling for the patient’s feet and expecting to find them stony; went clinking among the medicine bottles on the table, as who should say, “while we are here, let us repeat the mixture as before.”\n\n“No,” said Alice, whispering to her visitor, “evil courses, and remorse, travel, want, and weather, storm within, and storm without, have worn my life away. It will not last much longer.\n\nShe drew the hand up as she spoke, and laid her face against it.\n\n“I lie here, sometimes, thinking I should like to live until I had had a little time to show you how grateful I could be! It is a weakness, and soon passes. Better for you as it is. Better for me!”\n\nHow different her hold upon the hand, from what it had been when she took it by the fireside on the bleak winter evening! Scorn, rage, defiance, recklessness, look here! This is the end.\n\nMrs Wickam having clinked sufficiently among the bottles, now produced the mixture. Mrs Wickam looked hard at her patient in the act of drinking, screwed her mouth up tight, her eyebrows also, and shook her head, expressing that tortures shouldn’t make her say it was a hopeless case. Mrs Wickam then sprinkled a little cooling-stuff about the room, with the air of a female grave-digger, who was strewing ashes on ashes, dust on dust—for she was a serious character—and withdrew to partake of certain funeral baked meats downstairs.\n\n“How long is it,” asked Alice, “since I went to you and told you what I had done, and when you were advised it was too late for anyone to follow?”\n\n“It is a year and more,” said Harriet.\n\n“A year and more,” said Alice, thoughtfully intent upon her face. “Months upon months since you brought me here!”\n\nHarriet answered “Yes.”\n\n“Brought me here, by force of gentleness and kindness. Me!” said Alice, shrinking with her face behind her hand, “and made me human by woman’s looks and words, and angel’s deeds!”\n\nHarriet bending over her, composed and soothed her. By and bye, Alice lying as before, with the hand against her face, asked to have her mother called.\n\nHarriet called to her more than once, but the old woman was so absorbed looking out at the open window on the darkness, that she did not hear. It was not until Harriet went to her and touched her, that she rose up, and came.\n\n“Mother,” said Alice, taking the hand again, and fixing her lustrous eyes lovingly upon her visitor, while she merely addressed a motion of her finger to the old woman, “tell her what you know.”\n\n“Tonight, my deary?”\n\n“Ay, mother,” answered Alice, faintly and solemnly, “tonight!”\n\nThe old woman, whose wits appeared disorderly by alarm, remorse, or grief, came creeping along the side of the bed, opposite to that on which Harriet sat; and kneeling down, so as to bring her withered face upon a level with the coverlet, and stretching out her hand, so as to touch her daughter’s arm, began:\n\n“My handsome gal—”\n\nHeaven, what a cry was that, with which she stopped there, gazing at the poor form lying on the bed!\n\n“Changed, long ago, mother! Withered, long ago,” said Alice, without looking at her. “Don’t grieve for that now.”\n\n“—My daughter,” faltered the old woman, “my gal who’ll soon get better, and shame ’em all with her good looks.”\n\nAlice smiled mournfully at Harriet, and fondled her hand a little closer, but said nothing.\n\n“Who’ll soon get better, I say,” repeated the old woman, menacing the vacant air with her shrivelled fist, “and who’ll shame ’em all with her good looks—she will. I say she will! she shall!”—as if she were in passionate contention with some unseen opponent at the bedside, who contradicted her—“my daughter has been turned away from, and cast out, but she could boast relationship to proud folks too, if she chose. Ah! To proud folks! There’s relationship without your clergy and your wedding rings—they may make it, but they can’t break it—and my daughter’s well related. Show me Mrs Dombey, and I’ll show you my Alice’s first cousin.”\n\nHarriet glanced from the old woman to the lustrous eyes intent upon her face, and derived corroboration from them.\n\n“What!” cried the old woman, her nodding head bridling with a ghastly vanity. “Though I am old and ugly now,—much older by life and habit than years though,—I was once as young as any. Ah! as pretty too, as many! I was a fresh country wench in my time, darling,” stretching out her arm to Harriet, across the bed, “and looked it, too. Down in my country, Mrs Dombey’s father and his brother were the gayest gentlemen and the best-liked that came a visiting from London—they have long been dead, though! Lord, Lord, this long while! The brother, who was my Ally’s father, longest of the two.”\n\nShe raised her head a little, and peered at her daughter’s face; as if from the remembrance of her own youth, she had flown to the remembrance of her child’s. Then, suddenly, she laid her face down on the bed, and shut her head up in her hands and arms.\n\n“They were as like,” said the old woman, without looking up, as you could see two brothers, so near an age—there wasn’t much more than a year between them, as I recollect—and if you could have seen my gal, as I have seen her once, side by side with the other’s daughter, you’d have seen, for all the difference of dress and life, that they were like each other. Oh! is the likeness gone, and is it my gal—only my gal—that’s to change so!”\n\n“We shall all change, mother, in our turn,” said Alice.\n\n“Turn!” cried the old woman, “but why not hers as soon as my gal’s! The mother must have changed—she looked as old as me, and full as wrinkled through her paint—but she was handsome. What have I done, I, what have I done worse than her, that only my gal is to lie there fading!”\n\nWith another of those wild cries, she went running out into the room from which she had come; but immediately, in her uncertain mood, returned, and creeping up to Harriet, said:\n\n“That’s what Alice bade me tell you, deary. That’s all. I found it out when I began to ask who she was, and all about her, away in Warwickshire there, one summer-time. Such relations was no good to me, then. They wouldn’t have owned me, and had nothing to give me. I should have asked ’em, maybe, for a little money, afterwards, if it hadn’t been for my Alice; she’d a’most have killed me, if I had, I think. She was as proud as t’other in her way,” said the old woman, touching the face of her daughter fearfully, and withdrawing her hand, “for all she’s so quiet now; but she’ll shame ’em with her good looks yet. Ha, ha! She’ll shame ’em, will my handsome daughter!”\n\nHer laugh, as she retreated, was worse than her cry; worse than the burst of imbecile lamentation in which it ended; worse than the doting air with which she sat down in her old seat, and stared out at the darkness.\n\nThe eyes of Alice had all this time been fixed on Harriet, whose hand she had never released. She said now:\n\n“I have felt, lying here, that I should like you to know this. It might explain, I have thought, something that used to help to harden me. I had heard so much, in my wrongdoing, of my neglected duty, that I took up with the belief that duty had not been done to me, and that as the seed was sown, the harvest grew. I somehow made it out that when ladies had bad homes and mothers, they went wrong in their way, too; but that their way was not so foul a one as mine, and they had need to bless God for it. That is all past. It is like a dream, now, which I cannot quite remember or understand. It has been more and more like a dream, every day, since you began to sit here, and to read to me. I only tell it you, as I can recollect it. Will you read to me a little more?”\n\nHarriet was withdrawing her hand to open the book, when Alice detained it for a moment.\n\n“You will not forget my mother? I forgive her, if I have any cause. I know that she forgives me, and is sorry in her heart. You will not forget her?”\n\n“Never, Alice!”\n“A moment yet. Lay your head so, dear, that as you read I may see the words in your kind face.”\n\nHarriet complied and read—read the eternal book for all the weary, and the heavy-laden; for all the wretched, fallen, and neglected of this earth—read the blessed history, in which the blind lame palsied beggar, the criminal, the woman stained with shame, the shunned of all our dainty clay, has each a portion, that no human pride, indifference, or sophistry, through all the ages that this world shall last, can take away, or by the thousandth atom of a grain reduce—read the ministry of Him who, through the round of human life, and all its hopes and griefs, from birth to death, from infancy to age, had sweet compassion for, and interest in, its every scene and stage, its every suffering and sorrow.\n\n“I shall come,” said Harriet, when she shut the book, “very early in the morning.”\n\nThe lustrous eyes, yet fixed upon her face, closed for a moment, then opened; and Alice kissed and blest her.\n\nThe same eyes followed her to the door; and in their light, and on the tranquil face, there was a smile when it was closed.\n\nThey never turned away. She laid her hand upon her breast, murmuring the sacred name that had been read to her; and life passed from her face, like light removed.\n\nNothing lay there, any longer, but the ruin of the mortal house on which the rain had beaten, and the black hair that had fluttered in the wintry wind.\n\n# CHAPTER LIX. Retribution\n\nChanges have come again upon the great house in the long dull street, once the scene of Florence’s childhood and loneliness. It is a great house still, proof against wind and weather, without breaches in the roof, or shattered windows, or dilapidated walls; but it is a ruin none the less, and the rats fly from it.\n\nMr Towlinson and company are, at first, incredulous in respect of the shapeless rumours that they hear. Cook says our people’s credit ain’t so easy shook as that comes to, thank God; and Mr Towlinson expects to hear it reported next, that the Bank of England’s a-going to break, or the jewels in the Tower to be sold up. But, next come the Gazette, and Mr Perch; and Mr Perch brings Mrs Perch to talk it over in the kitchen, and to spend a pleasant evening.\n\nAs soon as there is no doubt about it, Mr Towlinson’s main anxiety is that the failure should be a good round one—not less than a hundred thousand pound. Mr Perch don’t think himself that a hundred thousand pound will nearly cover it. The women, led by Mrs Perch and Cook, often repeat “a hun-dred thou-sand pound!” with awful satisfaction—as if handling the words were like handling the money; and the housemaid, who has her eye on Mr Towlinson, wishes she had only a hundredth part of the sum to bestow on the man of her choice. Mr Towlinson, still mindful of his old wrong, opines that a foreigner would hardly know what to do with so much money, unless he spent it on his whiskers; which bitter sarcasm causes the housemaid to withdraw in tears.\n\nBut not to remain long absent; for Cook, who has the reputation of being extremely good-hearted, says, whatever they do, let ’em stand by one another now, Towlinson, for there’s no telling how soon they may be divided. They have been in that house (says Cook) through a funeral, a wedding, and a running-away; and let it not be said that they couldn’t agree among themselves at such a time as the present. Mrs Perch is immensely affected by this moving address, and openly remarks that Cook is an angel. Mr Towlinson replies to Cook, far be it from him to stand in the way of that good feeling which he could wish to see; and adjourning in quest of the housemaid, and presently returning with that young lady on his arm, informs the kitchen that foreigners is only his fun, and that him and Anne have now resolved to take one another for better for worse, and to settle in Oxford Market in the general greengrocery and herb and leech line, where your kind favours is particular requested. This announcement is received with acclamation; and Mrs Perch, projecting her soul into futurity, says, “girls,” in Cook’s ear, in a solemn whisper.\n\nMisfortune in the family without feasting, in these lower regions, couldn’t be. Therefore Cook tosses up a hot dish or two for supper, and Mr Towlinson compounds a lobster salad to be devoted to the same hospitable purpose. Even Mrs Pipchin, agitated by the occasion, rings her bell, and sends down word that she requests to have that little bit of sweetbread that was left, warmed up for her supper, and sent to her on a tray with about a quarter of a tumbler-full of mulled sherry; for she feels poorly.\n\nThere is a little talk about Mr Dombey, but very little. It is chiefly speculation as to how long he has known that this was going to happen. Cook says shrewdly, “Oh a long time, bless you! Take your oath of that.” And reference being made to Mr Perch, he confirms her view of the case. Somebody wonders what he’ll do, and whether he’ll go out in any situation. Mr Towlinson thinks not, and hints at a refuge in one of them genteel almshouses of the better kind. “Ah, where he’ll have his little garden, you know,” says Cook plaintively, “and bring up sweet peas in the spring.” “Exactly so,” says Mr Towlinson, “and be one of the Brethren of something or another.” “We are all brethren,” says Mrs Perch, in a pause of her drink. “Except the sisters,” says Mr Perch. “How are the mighty fallen!” remarks Cook. “Pride shall have a fall, and it always was and will be so!” observes the housemaid.\n\nIt is wonderful how good they feel, in making these reflections; and what a Christian unanimity they are sensible of, in bearing the common shock with resignation. There is only one interruption to this excellent state of mind, which is occasioned by a young kitchen-maid of inferior rank—in black stockings—who, having sat with her mouth open for a long time, unexpectedly discharges from it words to this effect, “Suppose the wages shouldn’t be paid!” The company sit for a moment speechless; but Cook recovering first, turns upon the young woman, and requests to know how she dares insult the family, whose bread she eats, by such a dishonest supposition, and whether she thinks that anybody, with a scrap of honour left, could deprive poor servants of their pittance? “Because if that is your religious feelings, Mary Daws,” says Cook warmly, “I don’t know where you mean to go to.”\n\nMr Towlinson don’t know either; nor anybody; and the young kitchen-maid, appearing not to know exactly, herself, and scouted by the general voice, is covered with confusion, as with a garment.\n\nAfter a few days, strange people begin to call at the house, and to make appointments with one another in the dining-room, as if they lived there. Especially, there is a gentleman, of a Mosaic Arabian cast of countenance, with a very massive watch-guard, who whistles in the drawing-room, and, while he is waiting for the other gentleman, who always has pen and ink in his pocket, asks Mr Towlinson (by the easy name of “Old Cock,”) if he happens to know what the figure of them crimson and gold hangings might have been, when new bought. The callers and appointments in the dining-room become more numerous every day, and every gentleman seems to have pen and ink in his pocket, and to have some occasion to use it. At last it is said that there is going to be a Sale; and then more people arrive, with pen and ink in their pockets, commanding a detachment of men with carpet caps, who immediately begin to pull up the carpets, and knock the furniture about, and to print off thousands of impressions of their shoes upon the hall and staircase.\n\nThe council downstairs are in full conclave all this time, and, having nothing to do, perform perfect feats of eating. At length, they are one day summoned in a body to Mrs Pipchin’s room, and thus addressed by the fair Peruvian:\n\n“Your master’s in difficulties,” says Mrs Pipchin, tartly. “You know that, I suppose?”\n\nMr Towlinson, as spokesman, admits a general knowledge of the fact.\n\n“And you’re all on the look-out for yourselves, I warrant you,” says Mrs Pipchin, shaking her head at them.\n\nA shrill voice from the rear exclaims, “No more than yourself!”\n\n“That’s your opinion, Mrs Impudence, is it?” says the ireful Pipchin, looking with a fiery eye over the intermediate heads.\n\n“Yes, Mrs Pipchin, it is,” replies Cook, advancing. “And what then, pray?”\n\n“Why, then you may go as soon as you like,” says Mrs Pipchin. “The sooner the better; and I hope I shall never see your face again.”\n\nWith this the doughty Pipchin produces a canvas bag; and tells her wages out to that day, and a month beyond it; and clutches the money tight, until a receipt for the same is duly signed, to the last upstroke; when she grudgingly lets it go. This form of proceeding Mrs Pipchin repeats with every member of the household, until all are paid.\n\n“Now those that choose, can go about their business,” says Mrs Pipchin, “and those that choose can stay here on board wages for a week or so, and make themselves useful. Except,” says the inflammable Pipchin, “that slut of a cook, who’ll go immediately.”\n\n“That,” says Cook, “she certainly will! I wish you good day, Mrs Pipchin, and sincerely wish I could compliment you on the sweetness of your appearance!”\n\n“Get along with you,” says Mrs Pipchin, stamping her foot.\n\nCook sails off with an air of beneficent dignity, highly exasperating to Mrs Pipchin, and is shortly joined below stairs by the rest of the confederation.\n\nMr Towlinson then says that, in the first place, he would beg to propose a little snack of something to eat; and over that snack would desire to offer a suggestion which he thinks will meet the position in which they find themselves. The refreshment being produced, and very heartily partaken of, Mr Towlinson’s suggestion is, in effect, that Cook is going, and that if we are not true to ourselves, nobody will be true to us. That they have lived in that house a long time, and exerted themselves very much to be sociable together. (At this, Cook says, with emotion, “Hear, hear!” and Mrs Perch, who is there again, and full to the throat, sheds tears.) And that he thinks, at the present time, the feeling ought to be “Go one, go all!” The housemaid is much affected by this generous sentiment, and warmly seconds it. Cook says she feels it’s right, and only hopes it’s not done as a compliment to her, but from a sense of duty. Mr Towlinson replies, from a sense of duty; and that now he is driven to express his opinions, he will openly say, that he does not think it over-respectable to remain in a house where Sales and such-like are carrying forwards. The housemaid is sure of it; and relates, in confirmation, that a strange man, in a carpet cap, offered, this very morning, to kiss her on the stairs. Hereupon, Mr Towlinson is starting from his chair, to seek and “smash” the offender; when he is laid hold on by the ladies, who beseech him to calm himself, and to reflect that it is easier and wiser to leave the scene of such indecencies at once. Mrs Perch, presenting the case in a new light, even shows that delicacy towards Mr Dombey, shut up in his own rooms, imperatively demands precipitate retreat. “For what,” says the good woman, “must his feelings be, if he was to come upon any of the poor servants that he once deceived into thinking him immensely rich!” Cook is so struck by this moral consideration, that Mrs Perch improves it with several pious axioms, original and selected. It becomes a clear case that they must all go. Boxes are packed, cabs fetched, and at dusk that evening there is not one member of the party left.\n\nThe house stands, large and weather-proof, in the long dull street; but it is a ruin, and the rats fly from it.\n\nThe men in the carpet caps go on tumbling the furniture about; and the gentlemen with the pens and ink make out inventories of it, and sit upon pieces of furniture never made to be sat upon, and eat bread and cheese from the public-house on other pieces of furniture never made to be eaten on, and seem to have a delight in appropriating precious articles to strange uses. Chaotic combinations of furniture also take place. Mattresses and bedding appear in the dining-room; the glass and china get into the conservatory; the great dinner service is set out in heaps on the long divan in the large drawing-room; and the stair-wires, made into fasces, decorate the marble chimneypieces. Finally, a rug, with a printed bill upon it, is hung out from the balcony; and a similar appendage graces either side of the hall door.\n\nThen, all day long, there is a retinue of mouldy gigs and chaise-carts in the street; and herds of shabby vampires, Jew and Christian, over-run the house, sounding the plate-glass mirrors with their knuckles, striking discordant octaves on the Grand Piano, drawing wet forefingers over the pictures, breathing on the blades of the best dinner-knives, punching the squabs of chairs and sofas with their dirty fists, touzling the feather beds, opening and shutting all the drawers, balancing the silver spoons and forks, looking into the very threads of the drapery and linen, and disparaging everything. There is not a secret place in the whole house. Fluffy and snuffy strangers stare into the kitchen-range as curiously as into the attic clothes-press. Stout men with napless hats on, look out of the bedroom windows, and cut jokes with friends in the street. Quiet, calculating spirits withdraw into the dressing-rooms with catalogues, and make marginal notes thereon, with stumps of pencils. Two brokers invade the very fire-escape, and take a panoramic survey of the neighbourhood from the top of the house. The swarm and buzz, and going up and down, endure for days. The Capital Modern Household Furniture, &c., is on view.\n\nThen there is a palisade of tables made in the best drawing-room; and on the capital, french-polished, extending, telescopic range of Spanish mahogany dining-tables with turned legs, the pulpit of the Auctioneer is erected; and the herds of shabby vampires, Jew and Christian, the strangers fluffy and snuffy, and the stout men with the napless hats, congregate about it and sit upon everything within reach, mantel-pieces included, and begin to bid. Hot, humming, and dusty are the rooms all day; and—high above the heat, hum, and dust—the head and shoulders, voice and hammer, of the Auctioneer, are ever at work. The men in the carpet caps get flustered and vicious with tumbling the Lots about, and still the Lots are going, going, gone; still coming on. Sometimes there is joking and a general roar. This lasts all day and three days following. The Capital Modern Household Furniture, &c., is on sale.\n\nThen the mouldy gigs and chaise-carts reappear; and with them come spring-vans and waggons, and an army of porters with knots. All day long, the men with carpet caps are screwing at screw-drivers and bed-winches, or staggering by the dozen together on the staircase under heavy burdens, or upheaving perfect rocks of Spanish mahogany, best rose-wood, or plate-glass, into the gigs and chaise-carts, vans and waggons. All sorts of vehicles of burden are in attendance, from a tilted waggon to a wheelbarrow. Poor Paul’s little bedstead is carried off in a donkey-tandem. For nearly a whole week, the Capital Modern Household Furniture, & c., is in course of removal.\n\nAt last it is all gone. Nothing is left about the house but scattered leaves of catalogues, littered scraps of straw and hay, and a battery of pewter pots behind the hall-door. The men with the carpet-caps gather up their screw-drivers and bed-winches into bags, shoulder them, and walk off. One of the pen-and-ink gentlemen goes over the house as a last attention; sticking up bills in the windows respecting the lease of this desirable family mansion, and shutting the shutters. At length he follows the men with the carpet caps. None of the invaders remain. The house is a ruin, and the rats fly from it.\n\nMrs Pipchin’s apartments, together with those locked rooms on the ground-floor where the window-blinds are drawn down close, have been spared the general devastation. Mrs Pipchin has remained austere and stony during the proceedings, in her own room; or has occasionally looked in at the sale to see what the goods are fetching, and to bid for one particular easy chair. Mrs Pipchin has been the highest bidder for the easy chair, and sits upon her property when Mrs Chick comes to see her.\n\n“How is my brother, Mrs Pipchin?” says Mrs Chick.\n\n“I don’t know any more than the deuce,” says Mrs Pipchin. “He never does me the honour to speak to me. He has his meat and drink put in the next room to his own; and what he takes, he comes out and takes when there’s nobody there. It’s no use asking me. I know no more about him than the man in the south who burnt his mouth by eating cold plum porridge.”\n\nThis the acrimonious Pipchin says with a flounce.\n\n“But good gracious me!” cries Mrs Chick blandly. “How long is this to last! If my brother will not make an effort, Mrs Pipchin, what is to become of him? I am sure I should have thought he had seen enough of the consequences of not making an effort, by this time, to be warned against that fatal error.”\n\n“Hoity toity!” says Mrs Pipchin, rubbing her nose. “There’s a great fuss, I think, about it. It ain’t so wonderful a case. People have had misfortunes before now, and been obliged to part with their furniture. I’m sure I have!”\n\n“My brother,” pursues Mrs Chick profoundly, “is so peculiar—so strange a man. He is the most peculiar man I ever saw. Would anyone believe that when he received news of the marriage and emigration of that unnatural child—it’s a comfort to me, now, to remember that I always said there was something extraordinary about that child: but nobody minds me—would anybody believe, I say, that he should then turn round upon me and say he had supposed, from my manner, that she had come to my house? Why, my gracious! And would anybody believe that when I merely say to him, ‘Paul, I may be very foolish, and I have no doubt I am, but I cannot understand how your affairs can have got into this state,’ he should actually fly at me, and request that I will come to see him no more until he asks me! Why, my goodness!”\n\n“Ah!” says Mrs Pipchin. “It’s a pity he hadn’t a little more to do with mines. They’d have tried his temper for him.”\n\n“And what,” resumes Mrs Chick, quite regardless of Mrs Pipchin’s observations, “is it to end in? That’s what I want to know. What does my brother mean to do? He must do something. It’s of no use remaining shut up in his own rooms. Business won’t come to him. No. He must go to it. Then why don’t he go? He knows where to go, I suppose, having been a man of business all his life. Very good. Then why not go there?”\n\nMrs Chick, after forging this powerful chain of reasoning, remains silent for a minute to admire it.\n\n“Besides,” says the discreet lady, with an argumentative air, “who ever heard of such obstinacy as his staying shut up here through all these dreadful disagreeables? It’s not as if there was no place for him to go to. Of course he could have come to our house. He knows he is at home there, I suppose? Mr Chick has perfectly bored about it, and I said with my own lips, ‘Why surely, Paul, you don’t imagine that because your affairs have got into this state, you are the less at home to such near relatives as ourselves? You don’t imagine that we are like the rest of the world?’ But no; here he stays all through, and here he is. Why, good gracious me, suppose the house was to be let! What would he do then? He couldn’t remain here then. If he attempted to do so, there would be an ejectment, an action for Doe, and all sorts of things; and then he must go. Then why not go at first instead of at last? And that brings me back to what I said just now, and I naturally ask what is to be the end of it?”\n\n“I know what’s to be the end of it, as far as I am concerned,” replies Mrs Pipchin, “and that’s enough for me. I’m going to take myself off in a jiffy.”\n\n“In a which, Mrs Pipchin,” says Mrs Chick.\n\n“In a jiffy,” retorts Mrs Pipchin sharply.\n\n“Ah, well! really I can’t blame you, Mrs Pipchin,” says Mrs Chick, with frankness.\n\n“It would be pretty much the same to me, if you could,” replies the sardonic Pipchin. “At any rate I’m going. I can’t stop here. I should be dead in a week. I had to cook my own pork chop yesterday, and I’m not used to it. My constitution will be giving way next. Besides, I had a very fair connexion at Brighton when I came here—little Pankey’s folks alone were worth a good eighty pounds a-year to me—and I can’t afford to throw it away. I’ve written to my niece, and she expects me by this time.”\n\n“Have you spoken to my brother?” inquires Mrs Chick\n\n“Oh, yes, it’s very easy to say speak to him,” retorts Mrs Pipchin. “How is it done? I called out to him yesterday, that I was no use here, and that he had better let me send for Mrs Richards. He grunted something or other that meant yes, and I sent. Grunt indeed! If he had been Mr Pipchin, he’d have had some reason to grunt. Yah! I’ve no patience with it!”\n\nHere this exemplary female, who has pumped up so much fortitude and virtue from the depths of the Peruvian mines, rises from her cushioned property to see Mrs Chick to the door. Mrs Chick, deploring to the last the peculiar character of her brother, noiselessly retires, much occupied with her own sagacity and clearness of head.\n\nIn the dusk of the evening Mr Toodle, being off duty, arrives with Polly and a box, and leaves them, with a sounding kiss, in the hall of the empty house, the retired character of which affects Mr Toodle’s spirits strongly.\n\n“I tell you what, Polly, me dear,” says Mr Toodle, “being now an ingine-driver, and well to do in the world, I shouldn’t allow of your coming here, to be made dull-like, if it warn’t for favours past. But favours past, Polly, is never to be forgot. To them which is in adversity, besides, your face is a cord’l. So let’s have another kiss on it, my dear. You wish no better than to do a right act, I know; and my views is, that it’s right and dutiful to do this. Good-night, Polly!”\n\nMrs Pipchin by this time looms dark in her black bombazeen skirts, black bonnet, and shawl; and has her personal property packed up; and has her chair (late a favourite chair of Mr Dombey’s and the dead bargain of the sale) ready near the street door; and is only waiting for a fly-van, going tonight to Brighton on private service, which is to call for her, by private contract, and convey her home.\n\nPresently it comes. Mrs Pipchin’s wardrobe being handed in and stowed away, Mrs Pipchin’s chair is next handed in, and placed in a convenient corner among certain trusses of hay; it being the intention of the amiable woman to occupy the chair during her journey. Mrs Pipchin herself is next handed in, and grimly takes her seat. There is a snaky gleam in her hard grey eye, as of anticipated rounds of buttered toast, relays of hot chops, worryings and quellings of young children, sharp snappings at poor Berry, and all the other delights of her Ogress’s castle. Mrs Pipchin almost laughs as the fly-van drives off, and she composes her black bombazeen skirts, and settles herself among the cushions of her easy chair.\n\nThe house is such a ruin that the rats have fled, and there is not one left.\n\nBut Polly, though alone in the deserted mansion—for there is no companionship in the shut-up rooms in which its late master hides his head—is not alone long. It is night; and she is sitting at work in the housekeeper’s room, trying to forget what a lonely house it is, and what a history belongs to it; when there is a knock at the hall door, as loud sounding as any knock can be, striking into such an empty place. Opening it, she returns across the echoing hall, accompanied by a female figure in a close black bonnet. It is Miss Tox, and Miss Tox’s eyes are red.\n\n“Oh, Polly,” says Miss Tox, “when I looked in to have a little lesson with the children just now, I got the message that you left for me; and as soon as I could recover my spirits at all, I came on after you. Is there no one here but you?”\n\n“Ah! not a soul,” says Polly.\n\n“Have you seen him?” whispers Miss Tox.\n\n“Bless you,” returns Polly, “no; he has not been seen this many a day. They tell me he never leaves his room.”\n\n“Is he said to be ill?” inquires Miss Tox.\n\n“No, Ma’am, not that I know of,” returns Polly, “except in his mind. He must be very bad there, poor gentleman!”\n\nMiss Tox’s sympathy is such that she can scarcely speak. She is no chicken, but she has not grown tough with age and celibacy. Her heart is very tender, her compassion very genuine, her homage very real. Beneath the locket with the fishy eye in it, Miss Tox bears better qualities than many a less whimsical outside; such qualities as will outlive, by many courses of the sun, the best outsides and brightest husks that fall in the harvest of the great reaper.\n\nIt is long before Miss Tox goes away, and before Polly, with a candle flaring on the blank stairs, looks after her, for company, down the street, and feels unwilling to go back into the dreary house, and jar its emptiness with the heavy fastenings of the door, and glide away to bed. But all this Polly does; and in the morning sets in one of those darkened rooms such matters as she has been advised to prepare, and then retires and enters them no more until next morning at the same hour. There are bells there, but they never ring; and though she can sometimes hear a footfall going to and fro, it never comes out.\n\nMiss Tox returns early in the day. It then begins to be Miss Tox’s occupation to prepare little dainties—or what are such to her—to be carried into these rooms next morning. She derives so much satisfaction from the pursuit, that she enters on it regularly from that time; and brings daily in her little basket, various choice condiments selected from the scanty stores of the deceased owner of the powdered head and pigtail. She likewise brings, in sheets of curl-paper, morsels of cold meats, tongues of sheep, halves of fowls, for her own dinner; and sharing these collations with Polly, passes the greater part of her time in the ruined house that the rats have fled from: hiding, in a fright at every sound, stealing in and out like a criminal; only desiring to be true to the fallen object of her admiration, unknown to him, unknown to all the world but one poor simple woman.\n\nThe Major knows it; but no one is the wiser for that, though the Major is much the merrier. The Major, in a fit of curiosity, has charged the Native to watch the house sometimes, and find out what becomes of Dombey. The Native has reported Miss Tox’s fidelity, and the Major has nearly choked himself dead with laughter. He is permanently bluer from that hour, and constantly wheezes to himself, his lobster eyes starting out of his head, “Damme, Sir, the woman’s a born idiot!”\n\nAnd the ruined man. How does he pass the hours, alone?\n\n“Let him remember it in that room, years to come!” He did remember it. It was heavy on his mind now; heavier than all the rest.\n\n“Let him remember it in that room, years to come! The rain that falls upon the roof, the wind that mourns outside the door, may have foreknowledge in their melancholy sound. Let him remember it in that room, years to come!”\n\nHe did remember it. In the miserable night he thought of it; in the dreary day, the wretched dawn, the ghostly, memory-haunted twilight. He did remember it. In agony, in sorrow, in remorse, in despair! “Papa! Papa! Speak to me, dear Papa!” He heard the words again, and saw the face. He saw it fall upon the trembling hands, and heard the one prolonged low cry go upward.\n\nHe was fallen, never to be raised up any more. For the night of his worldly ruin there was no to-morrow’s sun; for the stain of his domestic shame there was no purification; nothing, thank Heaven, could bring his dead child back to life. But that which he might have made so different in all the Past—which might have made the Past itself so different, though this he hardly thought of now—that which was his own work, that which he could so easily have wrought into a blessing, and had set himself so steadily for years to form into a curse: that was the sharp grief of his soul.\n\nOh! He did remember it! The rain that fell upon the roof, the wind that mourned outside the door that night, had had foreknowledge in their melancholy sound. He knew, now, what he had done. He knew, now, that he had called down that upon his head, which bowed it lower than the heaviest stroke of fortune. He knew, now, what it was to be rejected and deserted; now, when every loving blossom he had withered in his innocent daughter’s heart was snowing down in ashes on him.\n\nHe thought of her, as she had been that night when he and his bride came home. He thought of her as she had been, in all the home-events of the abandoned house. He thought, now, that of all around him, she alone had never changed. His boy had faded into dust, his proud wife had sunk into a polluted creature, his flatterer and friend had been transformed into the worst of villains, his riches had melted away, the very walls that sheltered him looked on him as a stranger; she alone had turned the same mild gentle look upon him always. Yes, to the latest and the last. She had never changed to him—nor had he ever changed to her—and she was lost.\n\nAs, one by one, they fell away before his mind—his baby—hope, his wife, his friend, his fortune—oh how the mist, through which he had seen her, cleared, and showed him her true self! Oh, how much better than this that he had loved her as he had his boy, and lost her as he had his boy, and laid them in their early grave together!\n\nIn his pride—for he was proud yet—he let the world go from him freely. As it fell away, he shook it off. Whether he imagined its face as expressing pity for him, or indifference to him, he shunned it alike. It was in the same degree to be avoided, in either aspect. He had no idea of any one companion in his misery, but the one he had driven away. What he would have said to her, or what consolation submitted to receive from her, he never pictured to himself. But he always knew she would have been true to him, if he had suffered her. He always knew she would have loved him better now, than at any other time; he was as certain that it was in her nature, as he was that there was a sky above him; and he sat thinking so, in his loneliness, from hour to hour. Day after day uttered this speech; night after night showed him this knowledge.\n\nIt began, beyond all doubt (however slow it advanced for some time), in the receipt of her young husband’s letter, and the certainty that she was gone. And yet—so proud he was in his ruin, or so reminiscent of her only as something that might have been his, but was lost beyond redemption—that if he could have heard her voice in an adjoining room, he would not have gone to her. If he could have seen her in the street, and she had done no more than look at him as she had been used to look, he would have passed on with his old cold unforgiving face, and not addressed her, or relaxed it, though his heart should have broken soon afterwards. However turbulent his thoughts, or harsh his anger had been, at first, concerning her marriage, or her husband, that was all past now. He chiefly thought of what might have been, and what was not. What was, was all summed up in this: that she was lost, and he bowed down with sorrow and remorse.\n\nAnd now he felt that he had had two children born to him in that house, and that between him and the bare wide empty walls there was a tie, mournful, but hard to rend asunder, connected with a double childhood, and a double loss. He had thought to leave the house—knowing he must go, not knowing whither—upon the evening of the day on which this feeling first struck root in his breast; but he resolved to stay another night, and in the night to ramble through the rooms once more.\n\nHe came out of his solitude when it was the dead of night, and with a candle in his hand went softly up the stairs. Of all the footmarks there, making them as common as the common street, there was not one, he thought, but had seemed at the time to set itself upon his brain while he had kept close, listening. He looked at their number, and their hurry, and contention—foot treading foot out, and upward track and downward jostling one another—and thought, with absolute dread and wonder, how much he must have suffered during that trial, and what a changed man he had cause to be. He thought, besides, oh was there, somewhere in the world, a light footstep that might have worn out in a moment half those marks!—and bent his head, and wept as he went up.\n\nHe almost saw it, going on before. He stopped, looking up towards the skylight; and a figure, childish itself, but carrying a child, and singing as it went, seemed to be there again. Anon, it was the same figure, alone, stopping for an instant, with suspended breath; the bright hair clustering loosely round its tearful face; and looking back at him.\n\nHe wandered through the rooms: lately so luxurious; now so bare and dismal and so changed, apparently, even in their shape and size. The press of footsteps was as thick here; and the same consideration of the suffering he had had, perplexed and terrified him. He began to fear that all this intricacy in his brain would drive him mad; and that his thoughts already lost coherence as the footprints did, and were pieced on to one another, with the same trackless involutions, and varieties of indistinct shapes.\n\nHe did not so much as know in which of these rooms she had lived, when she was alone. He was glad to leave them, and go wandering higher up. Abundance of associations were here, connected with his false wife, his false friend and servant, his false grounds of pride; but he put them all by now, and only recalled miserably, weakly, fondly, his two children.\n\nEverywhere, the footsteps! They had had no respect for the old room high up, where the little bed had been; he could hardly find a clear space there, to throw himself down, on the floor, against the wall, poor broken man, and let his tears flow as they would. He had shed so many tears here, long ago, that he was less ashamed of his weakness in this place than in any other—perhaps, with that consciousness, had made excuses to himself for coming here. Here, with stooping shoulders, and his chin dropped on his breast, he had come. Here, thrown upon the bare boards, in the dead of night, he wept, alone—a proud man, even then; who, if a kind hand could have been stretched out, or a kind face could have looked in, would have risen up, and turned away, and gone down to his cell.\n\nWhen the day broke he was shut up in his rooms again. He had meant to go away today, but clung to this tie in the house as the last and only thing left to him. He would go to-morrow. To-morrow came. He would go to-morrow. Every night, within the knowledge of no human creature, he came forth, and wandered through the despoiled house like a ghost. Many a morning when the day broke, his altered face, drooping behind the closed blind in his window, imperfectly transparent to the light as yet, pondered on the loss of his two children. It was one child no more. He reunited them in his thoughts, and they were never asunder. Oh, that he could have united them in his past love, and in death, and that one had not been so much worse than dead!\n\nStrong mental agitation and disturbance was no novelty to him, even before his late sufferings. It never is, to obstinate and sullen natures; for they struggle hard to be such. Ground, long undermined, will often fall down in a moment; what was undermined here in so many ways, weakened, and crumbled, little by little, more and more, as the hand moved on the dial.\n\nAt last he began to think he need not go at all. He might yet give up what his creditors had spared him (that they had not spared him more, was his own act), and only sever the tie between him and the ruined house, by severing that other link—\n\nIt was then that his footfall was audible in the late housekeeper’s room, as he walked to and fro; but not audible in its true meaning, or it would have had an appalling sound.\n\nThe world was very busy and restless about him. He became aware of that again. It was whispering and babbling. It was never quiet. This, and the intricacy and complication of the footsteps, harassed him to death. Objects began to take a bleared and russet colour in his eyes. Dombey and Son was no more—his children no more. This must be thought of, well, to-morrow.\n\nHe thought of it to-morrow; and sitting thinking in his chair, saw in the glass, from time to time, this picture:\n\nA spectral, haggard, wasted likeness of himself, brooded and brooded over the empty fireplace. Now it lifted up its head, examining the lines and hollows in its face; now hung it down again, and brooded afresh. Now it rose and walked about; now passed into the next room, and came back with something from the dressing-table in its breast. Now, it was looking at the bottom of the door, and thinking.\n\n—Hush! what?\n\nIt was thinking that if blood were to trickle that way, and to leak out into the hall, it must be a long time going so far. It would move so stealthily and slowly, creeping on, with here a lazy little pool, and there a start, and then another little pool, that a desperately wounded man could only be discovered through its means, either dead or dying. When it had thought of this a long while, it got up again, and walked to and fro with its hand in its breast. He glanced at it occasionally, very curious to watch its motions, and he marked how wicked and murderous that hand looked.\n\nNow it was thinking again! What was it thinking?\n\nWhether they would tread in the blood when it crept so far, and carry it about the house among those many prints of feet, or even out into the street.\n\nIt sat down, with its eyes upon the empty fireplace, and as it lost itself in thought there shone into the room a gleam of light; a ray of sun. It was quite unmindful, and sat thinking. Suddenly it rose, with a terrible face, and that guilty hand grasping what was in its breast. Then it was arrested by a cry—a wild, loud, piercing, loving, rapturous cry—and he only saw his own reflection in the glass, and at his knees, his daughter!\n\nYes. His daughter! Look at her! Look here! Down upon the ground, clinging to him, calling to him, folding her hands, praying to him.\n\n“Papa! Dearest Papa! Pardon me, forgive me! I have come back to ask forgiveness on my knees. I never can be happy more, without it!”\n\nUnchanged still. Of all the world, unchanged. Raising the same face to his, as on that miserable night. Asking his forgiveness!\n\n“Dear Papa, oh don’t look strangely on me! I never meant to leave you. I never thought of it, before or afterwards. I was frightened when I went away, and could not think. Papa, dear, I am changed. I am penitent. I know my fault. I know my duty better now. Papa, don’t cast me off, or I shall die!”\n\nHe tottered to his chair. He felt her draw his arms about her neck; he felt her put her own round his; he felt her kisses on his face; he felt her wet cheek laid against his own; he felt—oh, how deeply!—all that he had done.\n\nUpon the breast that he had bruised, against the heart that he had almost broken, she laid his face, now covered with his hands, and said, sobbing:\n\n“Papa, love, I am a mother. I have a child who will soon call Walter by the name by which I call you. When it was born, and when I knew how much I loved it, I knew what I had done in leaving you. Forgive me, dear Papa! oh say God bless me, and my little child!”\n\nHe would have said it, if he could. He would have raised his hands and besought her for pardon, but she caught them in her own, and put them down, hurriedly.\n\n“My little child was born at sea, Papa I prayed to God (and so did Walter for me) to spare me, that I might come home. The moment I could land, I came back to you. Never let us be parted any more, Papa. Never let us be parted any more!”\n\nHis head, now grey, was encircled by her arm; and he groaned to think that never, never, had it rested so before.\n\n“You will come home with me, Papa, and see my baby. A boy, Papa. His name is Paul. I think—I hope—he’s like—”\n\nHer tears stopped her.\n\n“Dear Papa, for the sake of my child, for the sake of the name we have given him, for my sake, pardon Walter. He is so kind and tender to me. I am so happy with him. It was not his fault that we were married. It was mine. I loved him so much.”\n\nShe clung closer to him, more endearing and more earnest.\n\n“He is the darling of my heart, Papa I would die for him. He will love and honour you as I will. We will teach our little child to love and honour you; and we will tell him, when he can understand, that you had a son of that name once, and that he died, and you were very sorry; but that he is gone to Heaven, where we all hope to see him when our time for resting comes. Kiss me, Papa, as a promise that you will be reconciled to Walter—to my dearest husband—to the father of the little child who taught me to come back, Papa Who taught me to come back!”\n\nAs she clung closer to him, in another burst of tears, he kissed her on her lips, and, lifting up his eyes, said, “Oh my God, forgive me, for I need it very much!”\n\nWith that he dropped his head again, lamenting over and caressing her, and there was not a sound in all the house for a long, long time; they remaining clasped in one another’s arms, in the glorious sunshine that had crept in with Florence.\n\nHe dressed himself for going out, with a docile submission to her entreaty; and walking with a feeble gait, and looking back, with a tremble, at the room in which he had been so long shut up, and where he had seen the picture in the glass, passed out with her into the hall. Florence, hardly glancing round her, lest she should remind him freshly of their last parting—for their feet were on the very stones where he had struck her in his madness—and keeping close to him, with her eyes upon his face, and his arm about her, led him out to a coach that was waiting at the door, and carried him away.\n\nThen, Miss Tox and Polly came out of their concealment, and exulted tearfully. And then they packed his clothes, and books, and so forth, with great care; and consigned them in due course to certain persons sent by Florence, in the evening, to fetch them. And then they took a last cup of tea in the lonely house.\n\n“And so Dombey and Son, as I observed upon a certain sad occasion,” said Miss Tox, winding up a host of recollections, “is indeed a daughter, Polly, after all.”\n\n“And a good one!” exclaimed Polly.\n\n“You are right,” said Miss Tox; “and it’s a credit to you, Polly, that you were always her friend when she was a little child. You were her friend long before I was, Polly,” said Miss Tox; “and you’re a good creature. Robin!”\n\nMiss Tox addressed herself to a bullet-headed young man, who appeared to be in but indifferent circumstances, and in depressed spirits, and who was sitting in a remote corner. Rising, he disclosed to view the form and features of the Grinder.\n\n“Robin,” said Miss Tox, “I have just observed to your mother, as you may have heard, that she is a good creature.”\n\n“And so she is, Miss,” quoth the Grinder, with some feeling.\n\n“Very well, Robin,” said Miss Tox, “I am glad to hear you say so. Now, Robin, as I am going to give you a trial, at your urgent request, as my domestic, with a view to your restoration to respectability, I will take this impressive occasion of remarking that I hope you will never forget that you have, and have always had, a good mother, and that you will endeavour so to conduct yourself as to be a comfort to her.”\n\n“Upon my soul I will, Miss,” returned the Grinder. “I have come through a good deal, and my intentions is now as straightfor’ard, Miss, as a cove’s—”\n\n“I must get you to break yourself of that word, Robin, if you please,” interposed Miss Tox, politely.\n\n“If you please, Miss, as a chap’s—”\n\n“Thankee, Robin, no,” returned Miss Tox, “I should prefer individual.”\n\n“As a indiwiddle’s—,” said the Grinder.\n\n“Much better,” remarked Miss Tox, complacently; “infinitely more expressive!”\n\n“—can be,” pursued Rob. “If I hadn’t been and got made a Grinder on, Miss and Mother, which was a most unfortunate circumstance for a young co—indiwiddle—”\n\n“Very good indeed,” observed Miss Tox, approvingly.\n\n“—and if I hadn’t been led away by birds, and then fallen into a bad service,” said the Grinder, “I hope I might have done better. But it’s never too late for a—”\n\n“Indi—” suggested Miss Tox.\n\n“—widdle,” said the Grinder, “to mend; and I hope to mend, Miss, with your kind trial; and wishing, Mother, my love to father, and brothers and sisters, and saying of it.”\n\n“I am very glad indeed to hear it,” observed Miss Tox. “Will you take a little bread and butter, and a cup of tea, before we go, Robin?”\n\n“Thankee, Miss,” returned the Grinder; who immediately began to use his own personal grinders in a most remarkable manner, as if he had been on very short allowance for a considerable period.\n\nMiss Tox, being, in good time, bonneted and shawled, and Polly too, Rob hugged his mother, and followed his new mistress away; so much to the hopeful admiration of Polly, that something in her eyes made luminous rings round the gas-lamps as she looked after him. Polly then put out her light, locked the house-door, delivered the key at an agent’s hard by, and went home as fast as she could go; rejoicing in the shrill delight that her unexpected arrival would occasion there. The great house, dumb as to all that had been suffered in it, and the changes it had witnessed, stood frowning like a dark mute on the street; baulking any nearer inquiries with the staring announcement that the lease of this desirable Family Mansion was to be disposed of.\n\n# CHAPTER LX. Chiefly Matrimonial\n\nThe grand half-yearly festival holden by Doctor and Mrs Blimber, on which occasion they requested the pleasure of the company of every young gentleman pursuing his studies in that genteel establishment, at an early party, when the hour was half-past seven o’clock, and when the object was quadrilles, had duly taken place, about this time; and the young gentlemen, with no unbecoming demonstrations of levity, had betaken themselves, in a state of scholastic repletion, to their own homes. Mr Skettles had repaired abroad, permanently to grace the establishment of his father Sir Barnet Skettles, whose popular manners had obtained him a diplomatic appointment, the honours of which were discharged by himself and Lady Skettles, to the satisfaction even of their own countrymen and countrywomen: which was considered almost miraculous. Mr Tozer, now a young man of lofty stature, in Wellington boots, was so extremely full of antiquity as to be nearly on a par with a genuine ancient Roman in his knowledge of English: a triumph that affected his good parents with the tenderest emotions, and caused the father and mother of Mr Briggs (whose learning, like ill-arranged luggage, was so tightly packed that he couldn’t get at anything he wanted) to hide their diminished heads. The fruit laboriously gathered from the tree of knowledge by this latter young gentleman, in fact, had been subjected to so much pressure, that it had become a kind of intellectual Norfolk Biffin, and had nothing of its original form or flavour remaining. Master Bitherstone now, on whom the forcing system had the happier and not uncommon effect of leaving no impression whatever, when the forcing apparatus ceased to work, was in a much more comfortable plight; and being then on shipboard, bound for Bengal, found himself forgetting, with such admirable rapidity, that it was doubtful whether his declensions of noun-substantives would hold out to the end of the voyage.\n\nWhen Doctor Blimber, in pursuance of the usual course, would have said to the young gentlemen, on the morning of the party, “Gentlemen, we will resume our studies on the twenty-fifth of next month,” he departed from the usual course, and said, “Gentlemen, when our friend Cincinnatus retired to his farm, he did not present to the senate any Roman who he sought to nominate as his successor. But there is a Roman here,” said Doctor Blimber, laying his hand on the shoulder of Mr Feeder, B.A., “adolescens imprimis gravis et doctus, gentlemen, whom I, a retiring Cincinnatus, wish to present to my little senate, as their future Dictator. Gentlemen, we will resume our studies on the twenty-fifth of next month, under the auspices of Mr Feeder, B.A.” At this (which Doctor Blimber had previously called upon all the parents, and urbanely explained), the young gentlemen cheered; and Mr Tozer, on behalf of the rest, instantly presented the Doctor with a silver inkstand, in a speech containing very little of the mother-tongue, but fifteen quotations from the Latin, and seven from the Greek, which moved the younger of the young gentlemen to discontent and envy: they remarking, “Oh, ah. It was all very well for old Tozer, but they didn’t subscribe money for old Tozer to show off with, they supposed; did they? What business was it of old Tozer’s more than anybody else’s? It wasn’t his inkstand. Why couldn’t he leave the boys’ property alone?” and murmuring other expressions of their dissatisfaction, which seemed to find a greater relief in calling him old Tozer, than in any other available vent.\n\nNot a word had been said to the young gentlemen, nor a hint dropped, of anything like a contemplated marriage between Mr Feeder, B.A., and the fair Cornelia Blimber. Doctor Blimber, especially, seemed to take pains to look as if nothing would surprise him more; but it was perfectly well known to all the young gentlemen nevertheless, and when they departed for the society of their relations and friends, they took leave of Mr Feeder with awe.\n\nMr Feeder’s most romantic visions were fulfilled. The Doctor had determined to paint the house outside, and put it in thorough repair; and to give up the business, and to give up Cornelia. The painting and repairing began upon the very day of the young gentlemen’s departure, and now behold! the wedding morning was come, and Cornelia, in a new pair of spectacles, was waiting to be led to the hymeneal altar.\n\nThe Doctor with his learned legs, and Mrs Blimber in a lilac bonnet, and Mr Feeder, B.A., with his long knuckles and his bristly head of hair, and Mr Feeder’s brother, the Reverend Alfred Feeder, M.A., who was to perform the ceremony, were all assembled in the drawing-room, and Cornelia with her orange-flowers and bridesmaids had just come down, and looked, as of old, a little squeezed in appearance, but very charming, when the door opened, and the weak-eyed young man, in a loud voice, made the following proclamation:\n\nUpon which there entered Mr Toots, grown extremely stout, and on his arm a lady very handsomely and becomingly dressed, with very bright black eyes.\n\n“Mrs Blimber,” said Mr Toots, “allow me to present my wife.”\n\nMrs Blimber was delighted to receive her. Mrs Blimber was a little condescending, but extremely kind.\n\n“And as you’ve known me for a long time, you know,” said Mr Toots, “let me assure you that she is one of the most remarkable women that ever lived.”\n\n“My dear!” remonstrated Mrs Toots.\n\n“Upon my word and honour she is,” said Mr Toots. “I—I assure you, Mrs Blimber, she’s a most extraordinary woman.”\n\nMrs Toots laughed merrily, and Mrs Blimber led her to Cornelia. Mr Toots having paid his respects in that direction and having saluted his old preceptor, who said, in allusion to his conjugal state, “Well, Toots, well, Toots! So you are one of us, are you, Toots?”—retired with Mr Feeder, B.A., into a window.\n\nMr Feeder, B.A., being in great spirits, made a spar at Mr Toots, and tapped him skilfully with the back of his hand on the breastbone.\n\n“Well, old Buck!” said Mr Feeder with a laugh. “Well! Here we are! Taken in and done for. Eh?”\n\n“Feeder,” returned Mr Toots. “I give you joy. If you’re as—as—as perfectly blissful in a matrimonial life, as I am myself, you’ll have nothing to desire.”\n\n“I don’t forget my old friends, you see,” said Mr Feeder. “I ask em to my wedding, Toots.”\n\n“Feeder,” replied Mr Toots gravely, “the fact is, that there were several circumstances which prevented me from communicating with you until after my marriage had been solemnised. In the first place, I had made a perfect brute of myself to you, on the subject of Miss Dombey; and I felt that if you were asked to any wedding of mine, you would naturally expect that it was with Miss Dombey, which involved explanations, that upon my word and honour, at that crisis, would have knocked me completely over. In the second place, our wedding was strictly private; there being nobody present but one friend of myself and Mrs Toots’s, who is a Captain in—I don’t exactly know in what,” said Mr Toots, “but it’s of no consequence. I hope, Feeder, that in writing a statement of what had occurred before Mrs Toots and myself went abroad upon our foreign tour, I fully discharged the offices of friendship.”\n\n“Toots, my boy,” said Mr Feeder, shaking his hands, “I was joking.”\n\n“And now, Feeder,” said Mr Toots, “I should be glad to know what you think of my union.”\n\n“Capital!” returned Mr Feeder.\n\n“You think it’s capital, do you, Feeder?” said Mr Toots solemnly. “Then how capital must it be to Me! For you can never know what an extraordinary woman that is.”\n\nMr Feeder was willing to take it for granted. But Mr Toots shook his head, and wouldn’t hear of that being possible.\n\n“You see,” said Mr Toots, “what I wanted in a wife was—in short, was sense. Money, Feeder, I had. Sense I—I had not, particularly.”\n\nMr Feeder murmured, “Oh, yes, you had, Toots!” But Mr Toots said:\n\n“No, Feeder, I had not. Why should I disguise it? I had not. I knew that sense was There,” said Mr Toots, stretching out his hand towards his wife, “in perfect heaps. I had no relation to object or be offended, on the score of station; for I had no relation. I have never had anybody belonging to me but my guardian, and him, Feeder, I have always considered as a Pirate and a Corsair. Therefore, you know it was not likely,” said Mr Toots, “that I should take his opinion.”\n\n“No,” said Mr Feeder.\n\n“Accordingly,” resumed Mr Toots, “I acted on my own. Bright was the day on which I did so! Feeder! Nobody but myself can tell what the capacity of that woman’s mind is. If ever the Rights of Women, and all that kind of thing, are properly attended to, it will be through her powerful intellect—Susan, my dear!” said Mr Toots, looking abruptly out of the windows “pray do not exert yourself!”\n\n“My dear,” said Mrs Toots, “I was only talking.”\n\n“But, my love,” said Mr Toots, “pray do not exert yourself. You really must be careful. Do not, my dear Susan, exert yourself. She’s so easily excited,” said Mr Toots, apart to Mrs Blimber, “and then she forgets the medical man altogether.”\n\nMrs Blimber was impressing on Mrs Toots the necessity of caution, when Mr Feeder, B.A., offered her his arm, and led her down to the carriages that were waiting to go to church. Doctor Blimber escorted Mrs Toots. Mr Toots escorted the fair bride, around whose lambent spectacles two gauzy little bridesmaids fluttered like moths. Mr Feeder’s brother, Mr Alfred Feeder, M.A., had already gone on, in advance, to assume his official functions.\n\nThe ceremony was performed in an admirable manner. Cornelia, with her crisp little curls, “went in,” as the Chicken might have said, with great composure; and Doctor Blimber gave her away, like a man who had quite made up his mind to it. The gauzy little bridesmaids appeared to suffer most. Mrs Blimber was affected, but gently so; and told the Reverend Mr Alfred Feeder, M.A., on the way home, that if she could only have seen Cicero in his retirement at Tusculum, she would not have had a wish, now, ungratified.\n\nThere was a breakfast afterwards, limited to the same small party; at which the spirits of Mr Feeder, B.A., were tremendous, and so communicated themselves to Mrs Toots that Mr Toots was several times heard to observe, across the table, “My dear Susan, don’t exert yourself!” The best of it was, that Mr Toots felt it incumbent on him to make a speech; and in spite of a whole code of telegraphic dissuasions from Mrs Toots, appeared on his legs for the first time in his life.\n\n“I really,” said Mr Toots, “in this house, where whatever was done to me in the way of—of any mental confusion sometimes—which is of no consequence and I impute to nobody—I was always treated like one of Doctor Blimber’s family, and had a desk to myself for a considerable period—can—not—allow—my friend Feeder to be—”\n\nMrs Toots suggested “married.”\n\n“It may not be inappropriate to the occasion, or altogether uninteresting,” said Mr Toots with a delighted face, “to observe that my wife is a most extraordinary woman, and would do this much better than myself—allow my friend Feeder to be married—especially to—”\n\nMrs Toots suggested “to Miss Blimber.”\n\n“To Mrs Feeder, my love!” said Mr Toots, in a subdued tone of private discussion: ‘“whom God hath joined,’ you know, ‘let no man’—don’t you know? I cannot allow my friend Feeder to be married—especially to Mrs Feeder—without proposing their—their—Toasts; and may,” said Mr Toots, fixing his eyes on his wife, as if for inspiration in a high flight, “may the torch of Hymen be the beacon of joy, and may the flowers we have this day strewed in their path, be the—the banishers of—of gloom!”\n\nDoctor Blimber, who had a taste for metaphor, was pleased with this, and said, “Very good, Toots! Very well said, indeed, Toots!” and nodded his head and patted his hands. Mr Feeder made in reply, a comic speech chequered with sentiment. Mr Alfred Feeder, M.A., was afterwards very happy on Doctor and Mrs Blimber; Mr Feeder, B.A., scarcely less so, on the gauzy little bridesmaids. Doctor Blimber then, in a sonorous voice, delivered a few thoughts in the pastoral style, relative to the rushes among which it was the intention of himself and Mrs Blimber to dwell, and the bee that would hum around their cot. Shortly after which, as the Doctor’s eyes were twinkling in a remarkable manner, and his son-in-law had already observed that time was made for slaves, and had inquired whether Mrs Toots sang, the discreet Mrs Blimber dissolved the sitting, and sent Cornelia away, very cool and comfortable, in a post-chaise, with the man of her heart.\n\nMr and Mrs Toots withdrew to the Bedford (Mrs Toots had been there before in old times, under her maiden name of Nipper), and there found a letter, which it took Mr Toots such an enormous time to read, that Mrs Toots was frightened.\n\n“My dear Susan,” said Mr Toots, “fright is worse than exertion. Pray be calm!”\n\n“Who is it from?” asked Mrs Toots.\n\n“Why, my love,” said Mr Toots, “it’s from Captain Gills. Do not excite yourself. Walters and Miss Dombey are expected home!”\n\n“My dear,” said Mrs Toots, raising herself quickly from the sofa, very pale, “don’t try to deceive me, for it’s no use, they’re come home—I see it plainly in your face!”\n\n“She’s a most extraordinary woman!” exclaimed Mr Toots, in rapturous admiration. “You’re perfectly right, my love, they have come home. Miss Dombey has seen her father, and they are reconciled!”\n\n“Reconciled!” cried Mrs Toots, clapping her hands.\n\n“My dear,” said Mr Toots; “pray do not exert yourself. Do remember the medical man! Captain Gills says—at least he don’t say, but I imagine, from what I can make out, he means—that Miss Dombey has brought her unfortunate father away from his old house, to one where she and Walters are living; that he is lying very ill there—supposed to be dying; and that she attends upon him night and day.”\n\nMrs Toots began to cry quite bitterly.\n\n“My dearest Susan,” replied Mr Toots, “do, do, if you possibly can, remember the medical man! If you can’t, it’s of no consequence—but do endeavour to!”\n\nHis wife, with her old manner suddenly restored, so pathetically entreated him to take her to her precious pet, her little mistress, her own darling, and the like, that Mr Toots, whose sympathy and admiration were of the strongest kind, consented from his very heart of hearts; and they agreed to depart immediately, and present themselves in answer to the Captain’s letter.\n\nNow some hidden sympathies of things, or some coincidences, had that day brought the Captain himself (toward whom Mr and Mrs Toots were soon journeying) into the flowery train of wedlock; not as a principal, but as an accessory. It happened accidentally, and thus:\n\nThe Captain, having seen Florence and her baby for a moment, to his unbounded content, and having had a long talk with Walter, turned out for a walk; feeling it necessary to have some solitary meditation on the changes of human affairs, and to shake his glazed hat profoundly over the fall of Mr Dombey, for whom the generosity and simplicity of his nature were awakened in a lively manner. The Captain would have been very low, indeed, on the unhappy gentleman’s account, but for the recollection of the baby; which afforded him such intense satisfaction whenever it arose, that he laughed aloud as he went along the street, and, indeed, more than once, in a sudden impulse of joy, threw up his glazed hat and caught it again; much to the amazement of the spectators. The rapid alternations of light and shade to which these two conflicting subjects of reflection exposed the Captain, were so very trying to his spirits, that he felt a long walk necessary to his composure; and as there is a great deal in the influence of harmonious associations, he chose, for the scene of this walk, his old neighbourhood, down among the mast, oar, and block makers, ship-biscuit bakers, coal-whippers, pitch-kettles, sailors, canals, docks, swing-bridges, and other soothing objects.\n\nThese peaceful scenes, and particularly the region of Limehouse Hole and thereabouts, were so influential in calming the Captain, that he walked on with restored tranquillity, and was, in fact, regaling himself, under his breath, with the ballad of Lovely Peg, when, on turning a corner, he was suddenly transfixed and rendered speechless by a triumphant procession that he beheld advancing towards him.\n\nThis awful demonstration was headed by that determined woman Mrs MacStinger, who, preserving a countenance of inexorable resolution, and wearing conspicuously attached to her obdurate bosom a stupendous watch and appendages, which the Captain recognised at a glance as the property of Bunsby, conducted under her arm no other than that sagacious mariner; he, with the distraught and melancholy visage of a captive borne into a foreign land, meekly resigning himself to her will. Behind them appeared the young MacStingers, in a body, exulting. Behind them, two ladies of a terrible and steadfast aspect, leading between them a short gentleman in a tall hat, who likewise exulted. In the wake, appeared Bunsby’s boy, bearing umbrellas. The whole were in good marching order; and a dreadful smartness that pervaded the party would have sufficiently announced, if the intrepid countenances of the ladies had been wanting, that it was a procession of sacrifice, and that the victim was Bunsby.\n\nThe first impulse of the Captain was to run away. This also appeared to be the first impulse of Bunsby, hopeless as its execution must have proved. But a cry of recognition proceeding from the party, and Alexander MacStinger running up to the Captain with open arms, the Captain struck.\n\n“Well, Cap’en Cuttle!” said Mrs MacStinger. “This is indeed a meeting! I bear no malice now, Cap’en Cuttle—you needn’t fear that I’m a going to cast any reflections. I hope to go to the altar in another spirit.” Here Mrs MacStinger paused, and drawing herself up, and inflating her bosom with a long breath, said, in allusion to the victim, “My “usband, Cap’en Cuttle!”\n\nThe abject Bunsby looked neither to the right nor to the left, nor at his bride, nor at his friend, but straight before him at nothing. The Captain putting out his hand, Bunsby put out his; but, in answer to the Captain’s greeting, spake no word.\n\n“Cap’en Cuttle,” said Mrs MacStinger, “if you would wish to heal up past animosities, and to see the last of your friend, my “usband, as a single person, we should be “appy of your company to chapel. Here is a lady here,” said Mrs MacStinger, turning round to the more intrepid of the two, “my bridesmaid, that will be glad of your protection, Cap’en Cuttle.”\n\nThe short gentleman in the tall hat, who it appeared was the husband of the other lady, and who evidently exulted at the reduction of a fellow creature to his own condition, gave place at this, and resigned the lady to Captain Cuttle. The lady immediately seized him, and, observing that there was no time to lose, gave the word, in a strong voice, to advance.\n\nThe Captain’s concern for his friend, not unmingled, at first, with some concern for himself—for a shadowy terror that he might be married by violence, possessed him, until his knowledge of the service came to his relief, and remembering the legal obligation of saying, “I will,” he felt himself personally safe so long as he resolved, if asked any question, distinctly to reply “I won’t”—threw him into a profuse perspiration; and rendered him, for a time, insensible to the movements of the procession, of which he now formed a feature, and to the conversation of his fair companion. But as he became less agitated, he learnt from this lady that she was the widow of a Mr Bokum, who had held an employment in the Custom House; that she was the dearest friend of Mrs MacStinger, whom she considered a pattern for her sex; that she had often heard of the Captain, and now hoped he had repented of his past life; that she trusted Mr Bunsby knew what a blessing he had gained, but that she feared men seldom did know what such blessings were, until they had lost them; with more to the same purpose.\n\nAll this time, the Captain could not but observe that Mrs Bokum kept her eyes steadily on the bridegroom, and that whenever they came near a court or other narrow turning which appeared favourable for flight, she was on the alert to cut him off if he attempted escape. The other lady, too, as well as her husband, the short gentleman with the tall hat, were plainly on guard, according to a preconcerted plan; and the wretched man was so secured by Mrs MacStinger, that any effort at self-preservation by flight was rendered futile. This, indeed, was apparent to the mere populace, who expressed their perception of the fact by jeers and cries; to all of which, the dread MacStinger was inflexibly indifferent, while Bunsby himself appeared in a state of unconsciousness.\n\nThe Captain made many attempts to accost the philosopher, if only in a monosyllable or a signal; but always failed, in consequence of the vigilance of the guard, and the difficulty, at all times peculiar to Bunsby’s constitution, of having his attention aroused by any outward and visible sign whatever. Thus they approached the chapel, a neat whitewashed edifice, recently engaged by the Reverend Melchisedech Howler, who had consented, on very urgent solicitation, to give the world another two years of existence, but had informed his followers that, then, it must positively go.\n\nWhile the Reverend Melchisedech was offering up some extemporary orisons, the Captain found an opportunity of growling in the bridegroom’s ear:\n\n“What cheer, my lad, what cheer?”\n\nTo which Bunsby replied, with a forgetfulness of the Reverend Melchisedech, which nothing but his desperate circumstances could have excused:\n\n“D——d bad,”\n\n“Jack Bunsby,” whispered the Captain, “do you do this here, of your own free will?”\n\nMr Bunsby answered “No.”\n\n“Why do you do it, then, my lad?” inquired the Captain, not unnaturally.\n\nBunsby, still looking, and always looking with an immovable countenance, at the opposite side of the world, made no reply.\n\n“Why not sheer off?” said the Captain. “Eh?” whispered Bunsby, with a momentary gleam of hope.\n\n“Sheer off,” said the Captain.\n\n“Where’s the good?” retorted the forlorn sage. “She’d capter me agen.”\n\n“Try!” replied the Captain. “Cheer up! Come! Now’s your time. Sheer off, Jack Bunsby!”\n\nJack Bunsby, however, instead of profiting by the advice, said in a doleful whisper:\n\n“It all began in that there chest o’ yourn. Why did I ever conwoy her into port that night?”\n\n“My lad,” faltered the Captain, “I thought as you had come over her; not as she had come over you. A man as has got such opinions as you have!”\n\nMr Bunsby merely uttered a suppressed groan.\n\n“Come!” said the Captain, nudging him with his elbow, “now’s your time! Sheer off! I’ll cover your retreat. The time’s a flying. Bunsby! It’s for liberty. Will you once?”\n\nBunsby was immovable.\n\n“Bunsby!” whispered the Captain, “will you twice?”\n\nBunsby wouldn’t twice.\n\n“Bunsby!” urged the Captain, “it’s for liberty; will you three times? Now or never!”\n\nBunsby didn’t then, and didn’t ever; for Mrs MacStinger immediately afterwards married him.\n\nOne of the most frightful circumstances of the ceremony to the Captain, was the deadly interest exhibited therein by Juliana MacStinger; and the fatal concentration of her faculties, with which that promising child, already the image of her parent, observed the whole proceedings. The Captain saw in this a succession of man-traps stretching out infinitely; a series of ages of oppression and coercion, through which the seafaring line was doomed. It was a more memorable sight than the unflinching steadiness of Mrs Bokum and the other lady, the exultation of the short gentleman in the tall hat, or even the fell inflexibility of Mrs MacStinger. The Master MacStingers understood little of what was going on, and cared less; being chiefly engaged, during the ceremony, in treading on one another’s half-boots; but the contrast afforded by those wretched infants only set off and adorned the precocious woman in Juliana. Another year or two, the Captain thought, and to lodge where that child was, would be destruction.\n\nThe ceremony was concluded by a general spring of the young family on Mr Bunsby, whom they hailed by the endearing name of father, and from whom they solicited half-pence. These gushes of affection over, the procession was about to issue forth again, when it was delayed for some little time by an unexpected transport on the part of Alexander MacStinger. That dear child, it seemed, connecting a chapel with tombstones, when it was entered for any purpose apart from the ordinary religious exercises, could not be persuaded but that his mother was now to be decently interred, and lost to him for ever. In the anguish of this conviction, he screamed with astonishing force, and turned black in the face. However touching these marks of a tender disposition were to his mother, it was not in the character of that remarkable woman to permit her recognition of them to degenerate into weakness. Therefore, after vainly endeavouring to convince his reason by shakes, pokes, bawlings-out, and similar applications to his head, she led him into the air, and tried another method; which was manifested to the marriage party by a quick succession of sharp sounds, resembling applause, and subsequently, by their seeing Alexander in contact with the coolest paving-stone in the court, greatly flushed, and loudly lamenting.\n\nThe procession being then in a condition to form itself once more, and repair to Brig Place, where a marriage feast was in readiness, returned as it had come; not without the receipt, by Bunsby, of many humorous congratulations from the populace on his recently-acquired happiness. The Captain accompanied it as far as the house-door, but, being made uneasy by the gentler manner of Mrs Bokum, who, now that she was relieved from her engrossing duty—for the watchfulness and alacrity of the ladies sensibly diminished when the bridegroom was safely married—had greater leisure to show an interest in his behalf, there left it and the captive; faintly pleading an appointment, and promising to return presently. The Captain had another cause for uneasiness, in remorsefully reflecting that he had been the first means of Bunsby’s entrapment, though certainly without intending it, and through his unbounded faith in the resources of that philosopher.\n\nTo go back to old Sol Gills at the wooden Midshipman’s, and not first go round to ask how Mr Dombey was—albeit the house where he lay was out of London, and away on the borders of a fresh heath—was quite out of the Captain’s course. So he got a lift when he was tired, and made out the journey gaily.\n\nThe blinds were pulled down, and the house so quiet, that the Captain was almost afraid to knock; but listening at the door, he heard low voices within, very near it, and, knocking softly, was admitted by Mr Toots. Mr Toots and his wife had, in fact, just arrived there; having been at the Midshipman’s to seek him, and having there obtained the address.\n\nThey were not so recently arrived, but that Mrs Toots had caught the baby from somebody, taken it in her arms, and sat down on the stairs, hugging and fondling it. Florence was stooping down beside her; and no one could have said which Mrs Toots was hugging and fondling most, the mother or the child, or which was the tenderer, Florence of Mrs Toots, or Mrs Toots of her, or both of the baby; it was such a little group of love and agitation.\n\n“And is your Pa very ill, my darling dear Miss Floy?” asked Susan.\n\n“He is very, very ill,” said Florence. “But, Susan, dear, you must not speak to me as you used to speak. And what’s this?” said Florence, touching her clothes, in amazement. “Your old dress, dear? Your old cap, curls, and all?”\n\nSusan burst into tears, and showered kisses on the little hand that had touched her so wonderingly.\n\n“My dear Miss Dombey,” said Mr Toots, stepping forward, “I’ll explain. She’s the most extraordinary woman. There are not many to equal her! She has always said—she said before we were married, and has said to this day—that whenever you came home, she’d come to you in no dress but the dress she used to serve you in, for fear she might seem strange to you, and you might like her less. I admire the dress myself,” said Mr Toots, “of all things. I adore her in it! My dear Miss Dombey, she’ll be your maid again, your nurse, all that she ever was, and more. There’s no change in her. But, Susan, my dear,” said Mr Toots, who had spoken with great feeling and high admiration, “all I ask is, that you’ll remember the medical man, and not exert yourself too much!”\n\n# CHAPTER LXI. Relenting\n\nFlorence had need of help. Her father’s need of it was sore, and made the aid of her old friend invaluable. Death stood at his pillow. A shade, already, of what he had been, shattered in mind, and perilously sick in body, he laid his weary head down on the bed his daughter’s hands prepared for him, and had never raised it since.\n\nShe was always with him. He knew her, generally; though, in the wandering of his brain, he often confused the circumstances under which he spoke to her. Thus he would address her, sometimes, as if his boy were newly dead; and would tell her, that although he had said nothing of her ministering at the little bedside, yet he had seen it—he had seen it; and then would hide his face and sob, and put out his worn hand. Sometimes he would ask her for herself. “Where is Florence?” “I am here, Papa, I am here.” “I don’t know her!” he would cry. “We have been parted so long, that I don’t know her!” and then a staring dread would be upon him, until she could soothe his perturbation; and recall the tears she tried so hard, at other times, to dry.\n\nHe rambled through the scenes of his old pursuits—through many where Florence lost him as she listened—sometimes for hours. He would repeat that childish question, “What is money?” and ponder on it, and think about it, and reason with himself, more or less connectedly, for a good answer; as if it had never been proposed to him until that moment. He would go on with a musing repetition of the title of his old firm twenty thousand times, and at every one of them, would turn his head upon his pillow. He would count his children—one—two—stop, and go back, and begin again in the same way.\n\nBut this was when his mind was in its most distracted state. In all the other phases of its illness, and in those to which it was most constant, it always turned on Florence. What he would oftenest do was this: he would recall that night he had so recently remembered, the night on which she came down to his room, and would imagine that his heart smote him, and that he went out after her, and up the stairs to seek her. Then, confounding that time with the later days of the many footsteps, he would be amazed at their number, and begin to count them as he followed her. Here, of a sudden, was a bloody footstep going on among the others; and after it there began to be, at intervals, doors standing open, through which certain terrible pictures were seen, in mirrors, of haggard men, concealing something in their breasts. Still, among the many footsteps and the bloody footsteps here and there, was the step of Florence. Still she was going on before. Still the restless mind went, following and counting, ever farther, ever higher, as to the summit of a mighty tower that it took years to climb.\n\nOne day he inquired if that were not Susan who had spoken a long while ago.\n\nFlorence said “Yes, dear Papa;” and asked him would he like to see her?\n\nHe said “very much.” And Susan, with no little trepidation, showed herself at his bedside.\n\nIt seemed a great relief to him. He begged her not to go; to understand that he forgave her what she had said; and that she was to stay. Florence and he were very different now, he said, and very happy. Let her look at this! He meant his drawing the gentle head down to his pillow, and laying it beside him.\n\nHe remained like this for days and weeks. At length, lying, the faint feeble semblance of a man, upon his bed, and speaking in a voice so low that they could only hear him by listening very near to his lips, he became quiet. It was dimly pleasant to him now, to lie there, with the window open, looking out at the summer sky and the trees: and, in the evening, at the sunset. To watch the shadows of the clouds and leaves, and seem to feel a sympathy with shadows. It was natural that he should. To him, life and the world were nothing else.\n\nHe began to show now that he thought of Florence’s fatigue: and often taxed his weakness to whisper to her, “Go and walk, my dearest, in the sweet air. Go to your good husband!” One time when Walter was in his room, he beckoned him to come near, and to stoop down; and pressing his hand, whispered an assurance to him that he knew he could trust him with his child when he was dead.\n\nIt chanced one evening, towards sunset, when Florence and Walter were sitting in his room together, as he liked to see them, that Florence, having her baby in her arms, began in a low voice to sing to the little fellow, and sang the old tune she had so often sung to the dead child: He could not bear it at the time; he held up his trembling hand, imploring her to stop; but next day he asked her to repeat it, and to do so often of an evening: which she did. He listening, with his face turned away.\n\nFlorence was sitting on a certain time by his window, with her work-basket between her and her old attendant, who was still her faithful companion. He had fallen into a doze. It was a beautiful evening, with two hours of light to come yet; and the tranquillity and quiet made Florence very thoughtful. She was lost to everything for the moment, but the occasion when the so altered figure on the bed had first presented her to her beautiful Mama; when a touch from Walter leaning on the back of her chair, made her start.\n\n“My dear,” said Walter, “there is someone downstairs who wishes to speak to you.”\n\nShe fancied Walter looked grave, and asked him if anything had happened.\n\n“No, no, my love!” said Walter. “I have seen the gentleman myself, and spoken with him. Nothing has happened. Will you come?”\n\nFlorence put her arm through his; and confiding her father to the black-eyed Mrs Toots, who sat as brisk and smart at her work as black-eyed woman could, accompanied her husband downstairs. In the pleasant little parlour opening on the garden, sat a gentleman, who rose to advance towards her when she came in, but turned off, by reason of some peculiarity in his legs, and was only stopped by the table.\n\nFlorence then remembered Cousin Feenix, whom she had not at first recognised in the shade of the leaves. Cousin Feenix took her hand, and congratulated her upon her marriage.\n\n“I could have wished, I am sure,” said Cousin Feenix, sitting down as Florence sat, “to have had an earlier opportunity of offering my congratulations; but, in point of fact, so many painful occurrences have happened, treading, as a man may say, on one another’s heels, that I have been in a devil of a state myself, and perfectly unfit for every description of society. The only description of society I have kept, has been my own; and it certainly is anything but flattering to a man’s good opinion of his own sources, to know that, in point of fact, he has the capacity of boring himself to a perfectly unlimited extent.”\n\nFlorence divined, from some indefinable constraint and anxiety in this gentleman’s manner—which was always a gentleman’s, in spite of the harmless little eccentricities that attached to it—and from Walter’s manner no less, that something more immediately tending to some object was to follow this.\n\n“I have been mentioning to my friend Mr Gay, if I may be allowed to have the honour of calling him so,” said Cousin Feenix, “that I am rejoiced to hear that my friend Dombey is very decidedly mending. I trust my friend Dombey will not allow his mind to be too much preyed upon, by any mere loss of fortune. I cannot say that I have ever experienced any very great loss of fortune myself: never having had, in point of fact, any great amount of fortune to lose. But as much as I could lose, I have lost; and I don’t find that I particularly care about it. I know my friend Dombey to be a devilish honourable man; and it’s calculated to console my friend Dombey very much, to know, that this is the universal sentiment. Even Tommy Screwzer,—a man of an extremely bilious habit, with whom my friend Gay is probably acquainted—cannot say a syllable in disputation of the fact.”\n\nFlorence felt, more than ever, that there was something to come; and looked earnestly for it. So earnestly, that Cousin Feenix answered, as if she had spoken.\n\n“The fact is,” said Cousin Feenix, “that my friend Gay and myself have been discussing the propriety of entreating a favour at your hands; and that I have the consent of my friend Gay—who has met me in an exceedingly kind and open manner, for which I am very much indebted to him—to solicit it. I am sensible that so amiable a lady as the lovely and accomplished daughter of my friend Dombey will not require much urging; but I am happy to know, that I am supported by my friend Gay’s influence and approval. As in my parliamentary time, when a man had a motion to make of any sort—which happened seldom in those days, for we were kept very tight in hand, the leaders on both sides being regular Martinets, which was a devilish good thing for the rank and file, like myself, and prevented our exposing ourselves continually, as a great many of us had a feverish anxiety to do—as, in my parliamentary time, I was about to say, when a man had leave to let off any little private popgun, it was always considered a great point for him to say that he had the happiness of believing that his sentiments were not without an echo in the breast of Mr Pitt; the pilot, in point of fact, who had weathered the storm. Upon which, a devilish large number of fellows immediately cheered, and put him in spirits. Though the fact is, that these fellows, being under orders to cheer most excessively whenever Mr Pitt’s name was mentioned, became so proficient that it always woke ’em. And they were so entirely innocent of what was going on, otherwise, that it used to be commonly said by Conversation Brown—four-bottle man at the Treasury Board, with whom the father of my friend Gay was probably acquainted, for it was before my friend Gay’s time—that if a man had risen in his place, and said that he regretted to inform the house that there was an Honourable Member in the last stage of convulsions in the Lobby, and that the Honourable Member’s name was Pitt, the approbation would have been vociferous.”\n\nThis postponement of the point, put Florence in a flutter; and she looked from Cousin Feenix to Walter, in increasing agitation.\n\n“My love,” said Walter, “there is nothing the matter.”\n\n“There is nothing the matter, upon my honour,” said Cousin Feenix; “and I am deeply distressed at being the means of causing you a moment’s uneasiness. I beg to assure you that there is nothing the matter. The favour that I have to ask is, simply—but it really does seem so exceedingly singular, that I should be in the last degree obliged to my friend Gay if he would have the goodness to break the—in point of fact, the ice,” said Cousin Feenix.\n\nWalter thus appealed to, and appealed to no less in the look that Florence turned towards him, said:\n\n“My dearest, it is no more than this. That you will ride to London with this gentleman, whom you know.”\n\n“And my friend Gay, also—I beg your pardon!” interrupted Cousin Feenix.\n\n“—And with me—and make a visit somewhere.”\n\n“To whom?” asked Florence, looking from one to the other.\n\n“If I might entreat,” said Cousin Feenix, “that you would not press for an answer to that question, I would venture to take the liberty of making the request.”\n\n“Do you know, Walter?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And think it right?”\n\n“Yes. Only because I am sure that you would too. Though there may be reasons I very well understand, which make it better that nothing more should be said beforehand.”\n\n“If Papa is still asleep, or can spare me if he is awake, I will go immediately,” said Florence. And rising quietly, and glancing at them with a look that was a little alarmed but perfectly confiding, left the room.\n\nWhen she came back, ready to bear them company, they were talking together, gravely, at the window; and Florence could not but wonder what the topic was, that had made them so well acquainted in so short a time. She did not wonder at the look of pride and love with which her husband broke off as she entered; for she never saw him, but that rested on her.\n\n“I will leave,” said Cousin Feenix, “a card for my friend Dombey, sincerely trusting that he will pick up health and strength with every returning hour. And I hope my friend Dombey will do me the favour to consider me a man who has a devilish warm admiration of his character, as, in point of fact, a British merchant and a devilish upright gentleman. My place in the country is in a most confounded state of dilapidation, but if my friend Dombey should require a change of air, and would take up his quarters there, he would find it a remarkably healthy spot—as it need be, for it’s amazingly dull. If my friend Dombey suffers from bodily weakness, and would allow me to recommend what has frequently done myself good, as a man who has been extremely queer at times, and who lived pretty freely in the days when men lived very freely, I should say, let it be in point of fact the yolk of an egg, beat up with sugar and nutmeg, in a glass of sherry, and taken in the morning with a slice of dry toast. Jackson, who kept the boxing-rooms in Bond Street—man of very superior qualifications, with whose reputation my friend Gay is no doubt acquainted—used to mention that in training for the ring they substituted rum for sherry. I should recommend sherry in this case, on account of my friend Dombey being in an invalided condition; which might occasion rum to fly—in point of fact to his head—and throw him into a devil of a state.”\n\nOf all this, Cousin Feenix delivered himself with an obviously nervous and discomposed air. Then, giving his arm to Florence, and putting the strongest possible constraint upon his wilful legs, which seemed determined to go out into the garden, he led her to the door, and handed her into a carriage that was ready for her reception.\n\nWalter entered after him, and they drove away.\n\nTheir ride was six or eight miles long. When they drove through certain dull and stately streets, lying westward in London, it was growing dusk. Florence had, by this time, put her hand in Walter’s; and was looking very earnestly, and with increasing agitation, into every new street into which they turned.\n\nWhen the carriage stopped, at last, before that house in Brook Street, where her father’s unhappy marriage had been celebrated, Florence said, “Walter, what is this? Who is here?” Walter cheering her, and not replying, she glanced up at the house-front, and saw that all the windows were shut, as if it were uninhabited. Cousin Feenix had by this time alighted, and was offering his hand.\n\n“Are you not coming, Walter?”\n\n“No, I will remain here. Don’t tremble there is nothing to fear, dearest Florence.”\n\n“I know that, Walter, with you so near. I am sure of that, but—”\n\nThe door was softly opened, without any knock, and Cousin Feenix led her out of the summer evening air into the close dull house. More sombre and brown than ever, it seemed to have been shut up from the wedding-day, and to have hoarded darkness and sadness ever since.\n\nFlorence ascended the dusky staircase, trembling; and stopped, with her conductor, at the drawing-room door. He opened it, without speaking, and signed an entreaty to her to advance into the inner room, while he remained there. Florence, after hesitating an instant, complied.\n\nSitting by the window at a table, where she seemed to have been writing or drawing, was a lady, whose head, turned away towards the dying light, was resting on her hand. Florence advancing, doubtfully, all at once stood still, as if she had lost the power of motion. The lady turned her head.\n\n“Great Heaven!” she said, “what is this?”\n\n“No, no!” cried Florence, shrinking back as she rose up and putting out her hands to keep her off. “Mama!”\n\nThey stood looking at each other. Passion and pride had worn it, but it was the face of Edith, and beautiful and stately yet. It was the face of Florence, and through all the terrified avoidance it expressed, there was pity in it, sorrow, a grateful tender memory. On each face, wonder and fear were painted vividly; each so still and silent, looking at the other over the black gulf of the irrevocable past.\n\nFlorence was the first to change. Bursting into tears, she said from her full heart, “Oh, Mama, Mama! why do we meet like this? Why were you ever kind to me when there was no one else, that we should meet like this?”\n\nEdith stood before her, dumb and motionless. Her eyes were fixed upon her face.\n\n“I dare not think of that,” said Florence, “I am come from Papa’s sick bed. We are never asunder now; we never shall be” any more. If you would have me ask his pardon, I will do it, Mama. I am almost sure he will grant it now, if I ask him. May Heaven grant it to you, too, and comfort you!”\n\nShe answered not a word.\n\n“Walter—I am married to him, and we have a son,” said Florence, timidly—“is at the door, and has brought me here. I will tell him that you are repentant; that you are changed,” said Florence, looking mournfully upon her; “and he will speak to Papa with me, I know. Is there anything but this that I can do?”\n\nEdith, breaking her silence, without moving eye or limb, answered slowly:\n\n“The stain upon your name, upon your husband’s, on your child’s. Will that ever be forgiven, Florence?”\n\n“Will it ever be, Mama? It is! Freely, freely, both by Walter and by me. If that is any consolation to you, there is nothing that you may believe more certainly. You do not—you do not,” faltered Florence, “speak of Papa; but I am sure you wish that I should ask him for his forgiveness. I am sure you do.”\n\nShe answered not a word.\n\n“I will!” said Florence. “I will bring it you, if you will let me; and then, perhaps, we may take leave of each other, more like what we used to be to one another. I have not,” said Florence very gently, and drawing nearer to her, “I have not shrunk back from you, Mama, because I fear you, or because I dread to be disgraced by you. I only wish to do my duty to Papa. I am very dear to him, and he is very dear to me. But I never can forget that you were very good to me. Oh, pray to Heaven,” cried Florence, falling on her bosom, “pray to Heaven, Mama, to forgive you all this sin and shame, and to forgive me if I cannot help doing this (if it is wrong), when I remember what you used to be!”\n\nEdith, as if she fell beneath her touch, sunk down on her knees, and caught her round the neck.\n\n“Florence!” she cried. “My better angel! Before I am mad again, before my stubbornness comes back and strikes me dumb, believe me, upon my soul I am innocent!”\n\n“Mama!”\n\n“Guilty of much! Guilty of that which sets a waste between us evermore. Guilty of what must separate me, through the whole remainder of my life, from purity and innocence—from you, of all the earth. Guilty of a blind and passionate resentment, of which I do not, cannot, will not, even now, repent; but not guilty with that dead man. Before God!”\n\nUpon her knees upon the ground, she held up both her hands, and swore it.\n\n“Florence!” she said, “purest and best of natures,—whom I love—who might have changed me long ago, and did for a time work some change even in the woman that I am,—believe me, I am innocent of that; and once more, on my desolate heart, let me lay this dear head, for the last time!”\n\nShe was moved and weeping. Had she been oftener thus in older days, she had been happier now.\n\n“There is nothing else in all the world,” she said, “that would have wrung denial from me. No love, no hatred, no hope, no threat. I said that I would die, and make no sign. I could have done so, and I would, if we had never met, Florence.”\n\n“I trust,” said Cousin Feenix, ambling in at the door, and speaking, half in the room, and half out of it, “that my lovely and accomplished relative will excuse my having, by a little stratagem, effected this meeting. I cannot say that I was, at first, wholly incredulous as to the possibility of my lovely and accomplished relative having, very unfortunately, committed herself with the deceased person with white teeth; because in point of fact, one does see, in this world—which is remarkable for devilish strange arrangements, and for being decidedly the most unintelligible thing within a man’s experience—very odd conjunctions of that sort. But as I mentioned to my friend Dombey, I could not admit the criminality of my lovely and accomplished relative until it was perfectly established. And feeling, when the deceased person was, in point of fact, destroyed in a devilish horrible manner, that her position was a very painful one—and feeling besides that our family had been a little to blame in not paying more attention to her, and that we are a careless family—and also that my aunt, though a devilish lively woman, had perhaps not been the very best of mothers—I took the liberty of seeking her in France, and offering her such protection as a man very much out at elbows could offer. Upon which occasion, my lovely and accomplished relative did me the honour to express that she believed I was, in my way, a devilish good sort of fellow; and that therefore she put herself under my protection. Which in point of fact I understood to be a kind thing on the part of my lovely and accomplished relative, as I am getting extremely shaky, and have derived great comfort from her solicitude.”\n\nEdith, who had taken Florence to a sofa, made a gesture with her hand as if she would have begged him to say no more.\n\n“My lovely and accomplished relative,” resumed Cousin Feenix, still ambling about at the door, “will excuse me, if, for her satisfaction, and my own, and that of my friend Dombey, whose lovely and accomplished daughter we so much admire, I complete the thread of my observations. She will remember that, from the first, she and I never alluded to the subject of her elopement. My impression, certainly, has always been, that there was a mystery in the affair which she could explain if so inclined. But my lovely and accomplished relative being a devilish resolute woman, I knew that she was not, in point of fact, to be trifled with, and therefore did not involve myself in any discussions. But, observing lately, that her accessible point did appear to be a very strong description of tenderness for the daughter of my friend Dombey, it occurred to me that if I could bring about a meeting, unexpected on both sides, it might lead to beneficial results. Therefore, we being in London, in the present private way, before going to the South of Italy, there to establish ourselves, in point of fact, until we go to our long homes, which is a devilish disagreeable reflection for a man, I applied myself to the discovery of the residence of my friend Gay—handsome man of an uncommonly frank disposition, who is probably known to my lovely and accomplished relative—and had the happiness of bringing his amiable wife to the present place. And now,” said Cousin Feenix, with a real and genuine earnestness shining through the levity of his manner and his slipshod speech, “I do conjure my relative, not to stop half way, but to set right, as far as she can, whatever she has done wrong—not for the honour of her family, not for her own fame, not for any of those considerations which unfortunate circumstances have induced her to regard as hollow, and in point of fact, as approaching to humbug—but because it is wrong, and not right.”\n\nCousin Feenix’s legs consented to take him away after this; and leaving them alone together, he shut the door.\n\nEdith remained silent for some minutes, with Florence sitting close beside her. Then she took from her bosom a sealed paper.\n\n“I debated with myself a long time,” she said in a low voice, “whether to write this at all, in case of dying suddenly or by accident, and feeling the want of it upon me. I have deliberated, ever since, when and how to destroy it. Take it, Florence. The truth is written in it.”\n\n“Is it for Papa?” asked Florence.\n\n“It is for whom you will,” she answered. “It is given to you, and is obtained by you. He never could have had it otherwise.”\n\nAgain they sat silent, in the deepening darkness.\n\n“Mama,” said Florence, “he has lost his fortune; he has been at the point of death; he may not recover, even now. Is there any word that I shall say to him from you?”\n\n“Did you tell me,” asked Edith, “that you were very dear to him?”\n\n“Yes!” said Florence, in a thrilling voice.\n\n“Tell him I am sorry that we ever met.”\n\n“No more?” said Florence after a pause.\n\n“Tell him, if he asks, that I do not repent of what I have done—not yet—for if it were to do again to-morrow, I should do it. But if he is a changed man—-”\n\nShe stopped. There was something in the silent touch of Florence’s hand that stopped her.\n\n“—But that being a changed man, he knows, now, it would never be. Tell him I wish it never had been.”\n\n“May I say,” said Florence, “that you grieved to hear of the afflictions he has suffered?”\n\n“Not,” she replied, “if they have taught him that his daughter is very dear to him. He will not grieve for them himself, one day, if they have brought that lesson, Florence.”\n\n“You wish well to him, and would have him happy. I am sure you would!” said Florence. “Oh! let me be able, if I have the occasion at some future time, to say so?”\n\nEdith sat with her dark eyes gazing steadfastly before her, and did not reply until Florence had repeated her entreaty; when she drew her hand within her arm, and said, with the same thoughtful gaze upon the night outside:\n\n“Tell him that if, in his own present, he can find any reason to compassionate my past, I sent word that I asked him to do so. Tell him that if, in his own present, he can find a reason to think less bitterly of me, I asked him to do so. Tell him, that, dead as we are to one another, never more to meet on this side of eternity, he knows there is one feeling in common between us now, that there never was before.”\n\nHer sternness seemed to yield, and there were tears in her dark eyes.\n\n“I trust myself to that,” she said, “for his better thoughts of me, and mine of him. When he loves his Florence most, he will hate me least. When he is most proud and happy in her and her children, he will be most repentant of his own part in the dark vision of our married life. At that time, I will be repentant too—let him know it then—and think that when I thought so much of all the causes that had made me what I was, I needed to have allowed more for the causes that had made him what he was. I will try, then, to forgive him his share of blame. Let him try to forgive me mine!”\n\n“Oh Mama!” said Florence. “How it lightens my heart, even in such a strange meeting and parting, to hear this!”\n\n“Strange words in my own ears,” said Edith, “and foreign to the sound of my own voice! But even if I had been the wretched creature I have given him occasion to believe me, I think I could have said them still, hearing that you and he were very dear to one another. Let him, when you are dearest, ever feel that he is most forbearing in his thoughts of me—that I am most forbearing in my thoughts of him! Those are the last words I send him! Now, goodbye, my life!”\n\nShe clasped her in her arms, and seemed to pour out all her woman’s soul of love and tenderness at once.\n\n“This kiss for your child! These kisses for a blessing on your head! My own dear Florence, my sweet girl, farewell!”\n\n“To meet again!” cried Florence.\n\n“Never again! Never again! When you leave me in this dark room, think that you have left me in the grave. Remember only that I was once, and that I loved you!”\n\nAnd Florence left her, seeing her face no more, but accompanied by her embraces and caresses to the last.\n\nCousin Feenix met her at the door, and took her down to Walter in the dingy dining room, upon whose shoulder she laid her head weeping.\n\n“I am devilish sorry,” said Cousin Feenix, lifting his wristbands to his eyes in the simplest manner possible, and without the least concealment, “that the lovely and accomplished daughter of my friend Dombey and amiable wife of my friend Gay, should have had her sensitive nature so very much distressed and cut up by the interview which is just concluded. But I hope and trust I have acted for the best, and that my honourable friend Dombey will find his mind relieved by the disclosures which have taken place. I exceedingly lament that my friend Dombey should have got himself, in point of fact, into the devil’s own state of conglomeration by an alliance with our family; but am strongly of opinion that if it hadn’t been for the infernal scoundrel Barker—man with white teeth—everything would have gone on pretty smoothly. In regard to my relative who does me the honour to have formed an uncommonly good opinion of myself, I can assure the amiable wife of my friend Gay, that she may rely on my being, in point of fact, a father to her. And in regard to the changes of human life, and the extraordinary manner in which we are perpetually conducting ourselves, all I can say is, with my friend Shakespeare—man who wasn’t for an age but for all time, and with whom my friend Gay is no doubt acquainted—that its like the shadow of a dream.”\n\n# CHAPTER LXII. Final\n\nA bottle that has been long excluded from the light of day, and is hoary with dust and cobwebs, has been brought into the sunshine; and the golden wine within it sheds a lustre on the table.\n\nIt is the last bottle of the old Madiera.\n\n“You are quite right, Mr Gills,” says Mr Dombey. “This is a very rare and most delicious wine.”\n\nThe Captain, who is of the party, beams with joy. There is a very halo of delight round his glowing forehead.\n\n“We always promised ourselves, Sir,” observes Mr Gills,” Ned and myself, I mean—”\n\nMr Dombey nods at the Captain, who shines more and more with speechless gratification.\n\n“—that we would drink this, one day or other, to Walter safe at home: though such a home we never thought of. If you don’t object to our old whim, Sir, let us devote this first glass to Walter and his wife.”\n\n“To Walter and his wife!” says Mr Dombey. “Florence, my child”—and turns to kiss her.\n\n“To Walter and his wife!” says Mr Toots.\n\n“To Wal”r and his wife!” exclaims the Captain. “Hooroar!” and the Captain exhibiting a strong desire to clink his glass against some other glass, Mr Dombey, with a ready hand, holds out his. The others follow; and there is a blithe and merry ringing, as of a little peal of marriage bells.\n\nOther buried wine grows older, as the old Madeira did in its time; and dust and cobwebs thicken on the bottles.\n\nMr Dombey is a white-haired gentleman, whose face bears heavy marks of care and suffering; but they are traces of a storm that has passed on for ever, and left a clear evening in its track.\n\nAmbitious projects trouble him no more. His only pride is in his daughter and her husband. He has a silent, thoughtful, quiet manner, and is always with his daughter. Miss Tox is not infrequently of the family party, and is quite devoted to it, and a great favourite. Her admiration of her once stately patron is, and has been ever since the morning of her shock in Princess’s Place, platonic, but not weakened in the least.\n\nNothing has drifted to him from the wreck of his fortunes, but a certain annual sum that comes he knows not how, with an earnest entreaty that he will not seek to discover, and with the assurance that it is a debt, and an act of reparation. He has consulted with his old clerk about this, who is clear it may be honourably accepted, and has no doubt it arises out of some forgotten transaction in the times of the old House.\n\nThat hazel-eyed bachelor, a bachelor no more, is married now, and to the sister of the grey-haired Junior. He visits his old chief sometimes, but seldom. There is a reason in the greyhaired Junior’s history, and yet a stronger reason in his name, why he should keep retired from his old employer; and as he lives with his sister and her husband, they participate in that retirement. Walter sees them sometimes—Florence too—and the pleasant house resounds with profound duets arranged for the Piano-Forte and Violoncello, and with the labours of Harmonious Blacksmiths.\n\nAnd how goes the wooden Midshipman in these changed days? Why, here he still is, right leg foremost, hard at work upon the hackney coaches, and more on the alert than ever, being newly painted from his cocked hat to his buckled shoes; and up above him, in golden characters, these names shine refulgent, GILLS AND CUTTLE.\n\nNot another stroke of business does the Midshipman achieve beyond his usual easy trade. But they do say, in a circuit of some half-mile round the blue umbrella in Leadenhall Market, that some of Mr Gills’s old investments are coming out wonderfully well; and that instead of being behind the time in those respects, as he supposed, he was, in truth, a little before it, and had to wait the fulness of the time and the design. The whisper is that Mr Gills’s money has begun to turn itself, and that it is turning itself over and over pretty briskly. Certain it is that, standing at his shop-door, in his coffee-coloured suit, with his chronometer in his pocket, and his spectacles on his forehead, he don’t appear to break his heart at customers not coming, but looks very jovial and contented, though full as misty as of yore.\n\nAs to his partner, Captain Cuttle, there is a fiction of a business in the Captain’s mind which is better than any reality. The Captain is as satisfied of the Midshipman’s importance to the commerce and navigation of the country, as he could possibly be, if no ship left the Port of London without the Midshipman’s assistance. His delight in his own name over the door, is inexhaustible. He crosses the street, twenty times a day, to look at it from the other side of the way; and invariably says, on these occasions, “Ed’ard Cuttle, my lad, if your mother could ha’ know’d as you would ever be a man o’ science, the good old creetur would ha’ been took aback in-deed!”\n\nBut here is Mr Toots descending on the Midshipman with violent rapidity, and Mr Toots’s face is very red as he bursts into the little parlour.\n\n“Captain Gills,” says Mr Toots, “and Mr Sols, I am happy to inform you that Mrs Toots has had an increase to her family.”\n\n“And it does her credit!” cries the Captain.\n\n“I give you joy, Mr Toots!” says old Sol.\n\n“Thank’ee,” chuckles Mr Toots, “I’m very much obliged to you. I knew that you’d be glad to hear, and so I came down myself. We’re positively getting on, you know. There’s Florence, and Susan, and now here’s another little stranger.”\n\n“A female stranger?” inquires the Captain.\n\n“Yes, Captain Gills,” says Mr Toots, “and I’m glad of it. The oftener we can repeat that most extraordinary woman, my opinion is, the better!”\n\n“Stand by!” says the Captain, turning to the old case-bottle with no throat—for it is evening, and the Midshipman’s usual moderate provision of pipes and glasses is on the board. “Here’s to her, and may she have ever so many more!”\n\n“Thank’ee, Captain Gills,” says the delighted Mr Toots. “I echo the sentiment. If you’ll allow me, as my so doing cannot be unpleasant to anybody, under the circumstances, I think I’ll take a pipe.”\n\nMr Toots begins to smoke, accordingly, and in the openness of his heart is very loquacious.\n\n“Of all the remarkable instances that that delightful woman has given of her excellent sense, Captain Gills and Mr Sols,” said Mr Toots, “I think none is more remarkable than the perfection with which she has understood my devotion to Miss Dombey.”\n\nBoth his auditors assent.\n\n“Because you know,” says Mr Toots, “I have never changed my sentiments towards Miss Dombey. They are the same as ever. She is the same bright vision to me, at present, that she was before I made Walters’s acquaintance. When Mrs Toots and myself first began to talk of—in short, of the tender passion, you know, Captain Gills.”\n\n“Ay, ay, my lad,” says the Captain, “as makes us all slue round—for which you’ll overhaul the book—”\n\n“I shall certainly do so, Captain Gills,” says Mr Toots, with great earnestness; “when we first began to mention such subjects, I explained that I was what you may call a Blighted Flower, you know.”\n\nThe Captain approves of this figure greatly; and murmurs that no flower as blows, is like the rose.\n\n“But Lord bless me,” pursues Mr Toots, “she was as entirely conscious of the state of my feelings as I was myself. There was nothing I could tell her. She was the only person who could have stood between me and the silent Tomb, and she did it, in a manner to command my everlasting admiration. She knows that there’s nobody in the world I look up to, as I do to Miss Dombey. Knows that there’s nothing on earth I wouldn’t do for Miss Dombey. She knows that I consider Miss Dombey the most beautiful, the most amiable, the most angelic of her sex. What is her observation upon that? The perfection of sense. ‘My dear, you’re right. I think so too.’”\n\n“And so do I!” says the Captain.\n\n“So do I,” says Sol Gills.\n\n“Then,” resumes Mr Toots, after some contemplative pulling at his pipe, during which his visage has expressed the most contented reflection, “what an observant woman my wife is! What sagacity she possesses! What remarks she makes! It was only last night, when we were sitting in the enjoyment of connubial bliss—which, upon my word and honour, is a feeble term to express my feelings in the society of my wife—that she said how remarkable it was to consider the present position of our friend Walters. ‘Here,’ observes my wife, ‘he is, released from sea-going, after that first long voyage with his young bride’—as you know he was, Mr Sols.”\n\n“Quite true,” says the old Instrument-maker, rubbing his hands.\n\n“‘Here he is,’ says my wife, ‘released from that, immediately; appointed by the same establishment to a post of great trust and confidence at home; showing himself again worthy; mounting up the ladder with the greatest expedition; beloved by everybody; assisted by his uncle at the very best possible time of his fortunes’—which I think is the case, Mr Sols? My wife is always correct.”\n\n“Why yes, yes—some of our lost ships, freighted with gold, have come home, truly,” returns old Sol, laughing. “Small craft, Mr Toots, but serviceable to my boy!”\n\n“Exactly so,” says Mr Toots. “You’ll never find my wife wrong. ‘Here he is,’ says that most remarkable woman, ‘so situated,—and what follows? What follows?’ observed Mrs Toots. Now pray remark, Captain Gills, and Mr Sols, the depth of my wife’s penetration. ‘Why that, under the very eye of Mr Dombey, there is a foundation going on, upon which a—an Edifice;’ that was Mrs Toots’s word,” says Mr Toots exultingly, ‘“is gradually rising, perhaps to equal, perhaps excel, that of which he was once the head, and the small beginnings of which (a common fault, but a bad one, Mrs Toots said) escaped his memory. Thus,’ said my wife, ‘from his daughter, after all, another Dombey and Son will ascend’—no ‘rise;’ that was Mrs Toots’s word—‘triumphant!’”\n\nMr Toots, with the assistance of his pipe—which he is extremely glad to devote to oratorical purposes, as its proper use affects him with a very uncomfortable sensation—does such grand justice to this prophetic sentence of his wife’s, that the Captain, throwing away his glazed hat in a state of the greatest excitement, cries:\n\n“Sol Gills, you man of science and my ould pardner, what did I tell Wal”r to overhaul on that there night when he first took to business? Was it this here quotation, ‘Turn again Whittington, Lord Mayor of London, and when you are old you will never depart from it.’ Was it them words, Sol Gills?”\n\n“It certainly was, Ned,” replied the old Instrument-maker. “I remember well.”\n\n“Then I tell you what,” says the Captain, leaning back in his chair, and composing his chest for a prodigious roar. “I’ll give you Lovely Peg right through; and stand by, both on you, for the chorus!”\n\nBuried wine grows older, as the old Madeira did, in its time; and dust and cobwebs thicken on the bottles.\n\nAutumn days are shining, and on the sea-beach there are often a young lady, and a white-haired gentleman. With them, or near them, are two children: boy and girl. And an old dog is generally in their company.\n\nThe white-haired gentleman walks with the little boy, talks with him, helps him in his play, attends upon him, watches him as if he were the object of his life. If he be thoughtful, the white-haired gentleman is thoughtful too; and sometimes when the child is sitting by his side, and looks up in his face, asking him questions, he takes the tiny hand in his, and holding it, forgets to answer. Then the child says:\n\n“What, grandpa! Am I so like my poor little Uncle again?”\n\n“Yes, Paul. But he was weak, and you are very strong.”\n\n“Oh yes, I am very strong.”\n\n“And he lay on a little bed beside the sea, and you can run about.”\n\nAnd so they range away again, busily, for the white-haired gentleman likes best to see the child free and stirring; and as they go about together, the story of the bond between them goes about, and follows them.\n\nBut no one, except Florence, knows the measure of the white-haired gentleman’s affection for the girl. That story never goes about. The child herself almost wonders at a certain secrecy he keeps in it. He hoards her in his heart. He cannot bear to see a cloud upon her face. He cannot bear to see her sit apart. He fancies that she feels a slight, when there is none. He steals away to look at her, in her sleep. It pleases him to have her come, and wake him in the morning. He is fondest of her and most loving to her, when there is no creature by. The child says then, sometimes:\n\n“Dear grandpapa, why do you cry when you kiss me?”\n\nHe only answers, “Little Florence! little Florence!” and smooths away the curls that shade her earnest eyes.\n\nThe voices in the waves speak low to him of Florence, day and night—plainest when he, his blooming daughter, and her husband, beside them in the evening, or sit at an open window, listening to their roar. They speak to him of Florence and his altered heart; of Florence and their ceaseless murmuring to her of the love, eternal and illimitable, extending still, beyond the sea, beyond the sky, to the invisible country far away.\n\nNever from the mighty sea may voices rise too late, to come between us and the unseen region on the other shore! Better, far better, that they whispered of that region in our childish ears, and the swift river hurried us away!\n\nI cannot forego my usual opportunity of saying farewell to my readers in this greeting-place, though I have only to acknowledge the unbounded warmth and earnestness of their sympathy in every stage of the journey we have just concluded.\n\nIf any of them have felt a sorrow in one of the principal incidents on which this fiction turns, I hope it may be a sorrow of that sort which endears the sharers in it, one to another. This is not unselfish in me. I may claim to have felt it, at least as much as anybody else; and I would fain be remembered kindly for my part in the experience.\n\nDEVONSHIRE TERRACE, Twenty-Fourth March, 1848.\n\nI make so bold as to believe that the faculty (or the habit) of correctly observing the characters of men, is a rare one. I have not even found, within my experience, that the faculty (or the habit) of correctly observing so much as the faces of men, is a general one by any means. The two commonest mistakes in judgement that I suppose to arise from the former default, are, the confounding of shyness with arrogance—a very common mistake indeed—and the not understanding that an obstinate nature exists in a perpetual struggle with itself.\n\nMr Dombey undergoes no violent change, either in this book, or in real life. A sense of his injustice is within him, all along. The more he represses it, the more unjust he necessarily is. Internal shame and external circumstances may bring the contest to a close in a week, or a day; but, it has been a contest for years, and is only fought out after a long balance of victory.\n\nI began this book by the Lake of Geneva, and went on with it for some months in France, before pursuing it in England. The association between the writing and the place of writing is so curiously strong in my mind, that at this day, although I know, in my fancy, every stair in the little midshipman’s house, and could swear to every pew in the church in which Florence was married, or to every young gentleman’s bedstead in Doctor Blimber’s establishment, I yet confusedly imagine Captain Cuttle as secluding himself from Mrs MacStinger among the mountains of Switzerland. Similarly, when I am reminded by any chance of what it was that the waves were always saying, my remembrance wanders for a whole winter night about the streets of Paris—as I restlessly did with a heavy heart, on the night when I had written the chapter in which my little friend and I parted company.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-821"]="돔비는 어두컴컴한 방 한쪽 구석, 침대 곁의 커다란 안락의자에 앉아 있었고, 아들은 따뜻하게 감싸인 채 자그마한 바구니 침대에 누워 있었다. 그 침대는 난롯불 바로 앞에, 그것도 아주 가까이 낮은 긴 의자 위에 조심스럽게 놓여 있었는데, 마치 그의 체질이 머핀과 비슷해서 갓 만들어졌을 때 노릇노릇하게 구워 두는 것이 꼭 필요하기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n돔비는 나이가 마흔여덟쯤이었다. 아들은 태어난 지 마흔여덟 분쯤 되었다. 돔비는 다소 머리가 벗어졌고, 얼굴이 다소 붉었으며, 잘생기고 체격 좋은 사내였지만 인상이 지나치게 엄격하고 위엄을 부리는 탓에 호감을 주지는 못했다. 아들은 머리가 아주 벗어졌고, 얼굴이 아주 붉었으며, 비록 (당연히) 흠잡을 데 없이 잘생긴 갓난아이였으나 전체적인 인상은 아직 어딘가 우그러지고 얼룩덜룩했다. 돔비의 이마에는 세월과 그의 형제인 근심이 몇 개의 자국을 새겨 놓았는데, 마치 때가 되면 베어 넘길 나무에 표시를 해 둔 것 같았다—그 무자비한 쌍둥이는 인간이라는 숲을 성큼성큼 누비며 지나가는 길마다 표를 새기는 법이다—한편 아들의 얼굴에는 천 개의 자잘한 주름이 가로질러 있었으니, 그 똑같이 교활한 세월이 자기 낫의 평평한 면으로 그 주름들을 펴고 닳게 하는 일을, 더 깊은 작업을 위한 표면 손질로 즐겨 하게 될 것이었다.\n\n돔비는 오래 기다려 온 일이 이루어진 것에 우쭐하여, 단정한 푸른 외투 아래 늘어진 묵직한 금시곗줄을 짤랑짤랑 흔들고 또 흔들었으며, 그 외투의 단추들은 멀리 있는 난롯불의 희미한 빛 속에서 인광처럼 반짝였다. 아들은 작은 주먹을 꼭 말아 쥔 채, 자기 식의 가냘픈 방식으로, 자기에게 이토록 뜻밖에 닥쳐온 존재라는 것을 향해 권투 자세라도 취하는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n\"우리 회사는 다시 한번, 돔비 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"이름뿐 아니라 실제로도 돔비와 아들이 될 거요.\" 그러고는 흐뭇하기 그지없는 어조로, 마치 꽃으로 꾸민 도안 속에서 그 이름을 읽으며 동시에 그 향기를 들이마시기라도 하듯 눈을 반쯤 감고 덧붙였다. \"돔—비와 아들!\"\n\n그 말이 어찌나 마음을 누그러뜨렸던지, 그는 돔비 부인의 이름 뒤에 애정을 담은 호칭을 덧붙였다(다만 그런 호칭에 거의 익숙지 않은 사람인지라 약간의 망설임이 없지는 않았다). 그러고는 말했다, \"돔비 부인, 내—내 사랑.\"\n\n병든 부인은 그를 향해 눈을 들어 올리며, 옅은 놀라움의 빛이 얼굴에 잠시 스쳐 지나갔다.\n\n\"이 아이는 폴이라고 세례를 받게 될 거요, 내—돔비 부인—물론이지.\"\n\n부인은 가냘프게 \"물론이죠\"라고 따라 했다. 아니, 오히려 입술의 움직임으로 그렇게 나타내고는 다시 눈을 감았다.\n\n\"제 아비의 이름이고, 제 할아비의 이름이오, 돔비 부인! 그 애 할아비가 오늘 살아 있었더라면! '주니어'라고 써야 하는 번거로움이 좀 있긴 하지만,\" 돔비 씨는 무릎 위에 가상의 서명을 그어 보이며 말했다, \"그건 그저 사적이고 개인적인 성격일 뿐이오. 회사의 거래 서신에는 들어가지 않소. 그 서명은 똑같이 유지되지.\" 그러고는 다시 한번, 앞서와 똑같은 어조로 \"돔비와 아들\"이라고 말했다.\n\n그 세 단어는 돔비 씨의 생애에서 단 하나의 관념을 담고 있었다. 대지는 돔비와 아들이 장사를 하라고 만들어졌고, 해와 달은 그들에게 빛을 주라고 만들어졌다. 강과 바다는 그들의 배를 띄우라고 형성되었으며, 무지개는 그들에게 맑은 날씨를 약속해 주었고, 바람은 그들의 사업에 유리하게 혹은 불리하게 불었으며, 별과 행성들은 그들이 중심이 되는 체계를 침범당하지 않게 보존하려고 제 궤도를 돌았다. 흔한 약자들도 그의 눈에는 새로운 의미를 띠었고 오로지 그들과만 관련되었다. A. D.는 안노 도미니(주님의 해)와는 아무 상관이 없었고, 안노 돔베이—와 아들을 뜻했다.\n\n그는 자기 아버지가 전에 그랬던 것처럼, 삶과 죽음의 과정을 거치며 '아들'에서 '돔비'로 올라섰고, 거의 이십 년 동안 회사의 유일한 대표자였다. 그 세월 가운데 십 년을 결혼한 몸으로 지냈다—어떤 이들의 말로는, 그에게 줄 마음을 갖지 못한 부인과 결혼했다고 했다. 그녀의 행복은 과거에 있었고, 그녀는 상처 입은 영혼을 현재의 순종적이고 온순한 인내에 매어 두는 데 만족했다고 했다. 그런 부질없는 소문이 정작 그것과 가장 깊이 관련된 돔비 씨의 귀에 들어갈 리는 거의 없었다. 또 설령 그것이 그에게 닿았다 하더라도, 세상에 그만큼 완전히 믿지 않을 사람도 아마 없었을 것이다. 돔비와 아들은 가죽은 자주 거래했어도, 마음은 결코 거래한 적이 없었다. 그런 공상 같은 물건은 소년 소녀들에게, 그리고 기숙학교와 책들에게 맡겨 두었다. 돔비 씨라면 이렇게 따졌을 것이다. 자기와의 혼인 결합은, 사물의 본성상, 상식을 갖춘 여자라면 누구에게나 흡족하고 영예로운 일임에 틀림없다고. 그런 회사의 새 동업자를 낳을 수 있다는 희망은, 자기 성별 가운데 가장 야심 없는 여자의 가슴속에서조차 영광스럽고 가슴 벅찬 야심을 일깨우지 않을 수 없다고. 돔비 부인은 혼인이라는 그 사회적 계약에—집안 회사를 영속시키는 문제를 차치하고서라도 품위 있고 부유한 신분에는 거의 필연적으로 따르는 그 계약에—이런 이점들을 두 눈 똑똑히 뜨고 들어왔다고. 돔비 부인은 사교계에서 자기의 지위를 날마다 실질적으로 알고 지냈다고. 돔비 부인은 늘 그의 식탁 상석에 앉아, 그의 집안의 접대를 매우 귀부인답고 보기 좋은 태도로 해 왔다고. 돔비 부인은 행복했음에 틀림없다고. 행복하지 않을 수 없었으리라고.\n\n또는, 어쨌든 한 가지 결함만 빼고는. 그렇다. 그 점은 그도 인정했을 것이다. 단 하나뿐이지만, 그 하나에는 확실히 많은 것이 걸려 있었다. 바로 이루어지기를 미루어 온 희망이라는 결함. 그 미루어진 희망은 (성경이 매우 정확하게 일러 주듯이, 라고 돔비 씨는 생색내는 투로 덧붙였을 것이다. 그가 성경에 대해 가진 가장 분명한 관념을 따져 보면, 돔비와 아들이 또 다른 한 부분을 이루는 어떤 전체의 한 부분을 형성하는 것이므로, 그러니 칭송하고 떠받들 만하다는 것이었으니까) 마음을 병들게 하는 법이다. 그들은 결혼한 지 십 년이 되었지만, 돔비 씨가 침대 곁의 커다란 안락의자에 앉아 묵직한 금시곗줄을 짤랑짤랑 흔들고 있는 바로 오늘에 이르도록, 자식이 없었다.\n\n—말할 만한 자식 말이다. 거론할 가치가 있는 자식이 없었다. 한 육 년쯤 전에 딸아이가 하나 있었는데, 아무도 모르게 방 안으로 살그머니 들어온 그 아이는 지금 어미의 얼굴이 보이는 구석에 겁먹은 듯 웅크리고 있었다. 그러나 돔비와 아들에게 딸이란 무엇이란 말인가! 회사의 이름과 위엄이라는 자본 안에서 그런 아이란 그저 투자할 수 없는 한 닢의 위조 동전—나쁜 사내아이—그 이상도 이하도 아니었다.\n\n그러나 이 순간 돔비 씨의 만족이라는 잔은 어찌나 가득 차 있었던지, 그 내용물을 한두 방울쯤은, 어린 딸의 샛길에 깔린 먼지 위에 뿌려 줄 정도로는 여유가 있다고 느꼈다.\n\n그래서 그는 말했다, \"플로렌스, 보고 싶으면 가서 네 예쁜 동생을 봐도 좋다, 그래. 손은 대지 말고!\"\n\n아이는 그 푸른 외투와 빳빳한 흰 크라바트를 날카롭게 흘끗 보았다. 그것은 삐걱거리는 부츠 한 켤레와 아주 큰 소리로 똑딱이는 시계와 더불어, 아이가 아버지에 대해 품은 관념을 이루는 것이었다. 그러나 아이의 눈은 곧바로 어미의 얼굴로 되돌아갔고, 아이는 움직이지도 대답하지도 않았다.\n\n\"저 애의 무감각함은 다른 모든 것에 그러하듯 동생에게도 끄떡없군,\" 돔비 씨는 속으로 중얼거렸다. 이 발견으로 그는 전부터 품어 온 생각이 확증된 듯하여, 오히려 그 사실이 꽤 흡족한 모양이었다.\n\n다음 순간, 부인이 눈을 떴고 아이를 보았다. 아이는 어미에게로 달려갔다. 그리고 발끝으로 서서, 어미의 품에 얼굴을 더 잘 묻으려는 듯, 제 나이와는 도무지 어울리지 않는 필사적인 애정으로 어미에게 매달렸다.\n\n\"오, 맙소사!\" 돔비 씨가 짜증스럽게 일어서며 말했다. \"이건 정말이지 매우 경솔하고 신경을 자극하는 짓이오. 저기 종을 울려서 플로렌스 아가씨의 유모를 좀 부르시오. 정말이지 사람이 좀 더 조심을—\"\n\n\"잠깐! 차라리—펩스 박사에게 다시 한번 위층으로 올라와 주시지 않겠느냐고 부탁드리는 게 낫겠소. 내가 내려가겠소. 내려가겠소. 굳이 부탁할 것도 없겠지만,\" 그는 난롯불 앞 긴 의자 곁에서 잠시 멈춰 서서 덧붙였다, \"이 어린 신사분을 각별히 잘 보살펴 주시오, 그—부인.\"\n\n\"블로킷이옵니다, 나리?\" 유모가 조심스레 일러 주었다. 빛바랜 점잖음을 지닌, 실없이 웃는 여인이었는데, 자기 이름을 사실로서 단언하려 들지는 않고 그저 부드러운 제안처럼 내놓았다.\n\n\"이 어린 신사분을 말이오, 블로킷 부인.\"\n\n\"네, 나리, 여부가 있겠습니까. 플로렌스 아가씨가 태어나셨을 때를 기억하옵니다—\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그래,\" 돔비 씨가 바구니 침대 위로 몸을 굽히며, 동시에 살짝 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"플로렌스 아가씨는 다 좋았소, 그러나 이건 다른 문제요. 이 어린 신사분에게는 이루어야 할 운명이 있소. 운명이란다, 꼬마야!\" 이렇게 갓난아이에게 일장 연설을 하면서 그는 한 손을 입술로 가져가 입을 맞추었다. 그러더니 그 행동이 자기 위엄을 다소 손상시키는 것은 아닐까 두려워하는 듯, 어색하기 짝이 없는 걸음으로 자리를 떴다.\n\n궁정 시의(侍醫) 가운데 한 사람이자 명문가의 출산을 돕는 일로 막대한 명성을 떨치는 파커 펩스 박사가, 두 손을 뒤로 한 채 응접실을 오락가락 거닐고 있었다. 그 모습은 집안 주치의의 말할 수 없는 감탄을 자아냈는데, 이 주치의는 지난 육 주 동안 자기 환자, 친구, 지인들 사이에서 이 일을 두고, 자기가 파커 펩스 박사와 함께 밤낮으로 시시각각 호출되기를 고대하고 있는 건이라고 줄곧 떠벌려 온 터였다.\n\n\"자, 그래,\" 파커 펩스 박사가 둥글고 깊고 우렁찬 목소리로, 이번 경우에 맞게 문고리처럼 소리를 죽여 말했다, \"선생의 방문으로 댁의 사랑스러운 부인께서 조금이라도 기운을 차리신 듯합니까?\"\n\n\"말하자면 자극을 받으셨는지요?\" 집안 주치의가 가냘프게 말했다. 동시에 박사에게 절을 했는데, 그 절은 마치 \"한마디 거드는 무례를 용서하십시오, 그러나 이건 귀중한 인연이라서요\"라고 말하는 듯했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 그 질문에 완전히 당황했다. 그는 환자에 대해 너무도 생각해 둔 것이 없어서 대답할 처지가 아니었다. 그는 파커 펩스 박사가 다시 위층으로 올라가 주신다면 자기로서는 흡족하겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"좋습니다! 선생께 숨길 수는 없는 일입니다만,\" 파커 펩스 박사가 말했다, \"공작부인 마님께 기력이 부족하시어—실례했습니다. 제가 이름을 혼동했군요. 댁의 상냥하신 부인께 그렇다는 말씀입니다. 어느 정도의 무기력함과, 전반적인 탄력의 부재가 있어서, 저희로서는 차라리—없었으면—\"\n\n\"하는 바입니다,\" 집안 주치의가 다시 한번 고개를 숙이며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다,\" 파커 펩스 박사가 말했다, \"저희로서는 차라리 보고 싶지 않은 것이지요. 캥커비 부인의 체계가—실례했습니다. 돔비 부인이라 해야지요. 제가 증례들의 이름을 혼동해서—\"\n\n\"하도 많으시니,\" 집안 주치의가 중얼거렸다—\"그러시는 것도 무리가 아니지요—오히려 안 그러시면 놀라운 일입니다—파커 펩스 박사님 같은 웨스트엔드 진료라면—\"\n\n\"고맙습니다,\" 박사가 말했다, \"바로 그렇습니다. 제가 말씀드리려던 것은, 우리 환자분의 체계가 충격을 받은 듯하다는 것입니다. 그 충격에서 회복하려면 오직 크고 강력한—\"\n\n\"그리고 활기찬,\" 집안 주치의가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다,\" 박사가 동의했다—\"그리고 활기찬 노력을 바랄 수밖에 없습니다. 여기 계신 필킨스 씨는, 이 댁의 의학 자문이라는 위치로 보아—그 위치를 채우기에 이분보다 더 적임자는 없으리라 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"오!\" 집안 주치의가 중얼거렸다. \"'휴버트 스탠리 경의 칭찬이로군!'\"\n\n\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니,\" 파커 펩스 박사가 응수했다, \"황송합니다. 필킨스 씨는, 그 위치로 보아, 정상 상태에서의 환자분의 체질을 가장 잘 알고 계시니(이런 경우 저희가 견해를 세우는 데 매우 귀중한 지식이지요), 저와 마찬가지로, 이번 경우에는 자연이 활기찬 노력을 발휘해 주어야 한다는 의견이십니다. 그리고 만일 우리의 흥미로운 벗, 돔비 백작부인께서—정말 실례했습니다, 돔비 부인께서—그러지—\"\n\n\"못하신다면,\" 집안 주치의가 말했다.\n\n\"발휘하지,\" 파커 펩스 박사가 말했다.\n\n\"그 노력을,\" 집안 주치의가 말했다.\n\n\"성공적으로,\" 둘이 함께 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 파커 펩스 박사가 혼자서, 매우 엄숙하게 덧붙였다, \"우리 두 사람 모두가 진심으로 애통해할 위기가 닥칠 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n그 말과 함께, 둘은 몇 초 동안 바닥을 내려다보며 서 있었다. 그러다가 파커 펩스 박사가—말없이 몸짓으로—신호하자, 둘은 위층으로 올라갔다. 집안 주치의가 그 저명한 동업자를 위해 방문을 열어 주고, 더없이 알랑거리는 정중함으로 그 뒤를 따라 나갔다.\n\n돔비 씨가 이 소식에 자기 나름으로는 동요하지 않았다고 기록한다면, 그것은 그에게 부당한 일이 될 것이다. 그는 언제 놀라거나 충격받은 적이 있다고 함부로 말할 수 있는 사람은 아니었다. 그러나 그는 분명, 만일 아내가 병들어 시들어 간다면 자기는 무척 안타까울 것이며, 자기의 은식기며 가구며 그 밖의 살림살이 가운데서 가질 만한 가치가 충분했던 어떤 것이 사라져 버린 것을, 진심 어린 애석함 없이는 잃을 수 없는 어떤 것이 사라져 버린 것을 느끼게 되리라는 정도의 감각은 마음속에 지니고 있었다. 다만 그것은 의심할 바 없이 냉정하고 사무적이며 신사답고 침착한 애석함일 터였다.\n\n이 문제에 대한 그의 사색은 곧 중단되었다. 처음에는 계단에서 옷자락 스치는 소리가 났고, 다음에는 한 부인이 방 안으로 휙 들이닥쳤다. 중년을 다소 넘긴 편이었으나 옷차림은 매우 젊은이답게, 특히 몸통을 꽉 조인 것이 그러했는데, 억눌린 감정이 드러나는 듯 얼굴과 거동이 비틀어진 채 그에게 달려와서는, 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안고, 목멘 소리로 말했다,\n\n\"오, 폴! 영락없는 돔비 집안이구나!\"\n\n\"자, 자!\" 그녀의 오라비가—돔비 씨는 그녀의 오라비였다—응수했다. \"내 생각에도 집안을 닮은 것 같소. 그렇게 흥분하지 마시오, 루이자.\"\n\n\"내가 너무 어리석지,\" 루이자가 자리에 앉아 손수건을 꺼내며 말했다, \"하지만 그 애는—그 애는 정말이지 완벽한 돔비란 말이야!\"\n\n돔비 씨가 헛기침을 했다.\n\n\"정말 놀라워,\" 루이자가 눈물 사이로 미소 지으며 말했다. 사실 그 눈물이 그리 주체할 수 없는 정도는 아니었다. \"어처구니없을 만큼 놀랍다니까. 완전히 우리 집안이야. 나는 평생 이런 건 본 적이 없어!\"\n\n\"그런데 패니는, 패니 본인은 어떻소?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"패니는 어떻소?\"\n\n\"오, 폴,\" 루이자가 응수했다, \"아무것도 아니야. 내 말 믿어, 정말 아무것도 아니야. 기진맥진하긴 했지, 물론. 하지만 내가 조지나 프레더릭 때 겪은 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니야. 노력이 필요할 뿐이야. 그게 다라고. 사랑스러운 패니가 돔비 사람이기만 했어도!—하지만 분명 해낼 거야. 틀림없이 해낼 거야. 그게 자기 의무로 요구되는 일임을 아니까, 당연히 해낼 거야. 오, 폴, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 이렇게 떨고 흔들리는 게 정말 약하고 어리석은 짓이라는 거 나도 알아. 하지만 너무 이상하게 떨려서, 포도주 한 잔하고 저 케이크 한 조각만 부탁해야겠어.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 즉시 탁자 위 쟁반에서 그 다과를 가져다주었다.\n\n\"네 건강을 위해 마시지는 않겠어, 폴,\" 루이자가 말했다. \"꼬마 돔비를 위해 마실 거야. 어머나 세상에!—내 평생 알아 온 것 중에 가장 놀라운 일이야, 그 애가 어쩜 그렇게 완벽한 돔비인지.\"\n\n이 의견의 표명을 짤막한 히스테릭한 웃음으로 삼켰는데, 그 웃음은 끝내 눈물로 변했고, 루이자는 눈을 치켜뜨며 잔을 비웠다.\n\n\"이렇게 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 흔들리고, 내 감정에 완전히 사로잡히는 게 정말 약하고 어리석은 짓이라는 거 나도 알아,\" 그녀가 되풀이했다, \"하지만 어쩔 수가 없어. 사랑스러운 패니랑 그 쪼끄만 아가를 보고 내려오다가 계단 창문 밖으로 떨어질 뻔했다니까.\" 이 마지막 말은 갓난아이에 대한 갑작스럽고 생생한 회상에서 비롯된 것이었다.\n\n그 말 뒤를 이어 문에서 부드러운 노크 소리가 났다.\n\n\"칙 부인,\" 바깥에서 매우 상냥한 여자 목소리가 말했다, \"이제 좀 어떠세요, 내 소중한 친구?\"\n\n\"오, 폴,\" 루이자가 자리에서 일어서며 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"톡스 양이야. 더없이 친절한 사람이지! 그녀가 없었으면 여기까지 오지도 못했을 거야! 톡스 양, 이쪽은 제 오라비 돔비 씨예요. 폴, 이쪽은 내 아주 각별한 친구 톡스 양이야.\"\n\n이렇게 특별히 소개된 부인은 길쭉하고 마른 체격으로, 어찌나 빛바랜 분위기를 풍기는지 본디 포목상들이 말하는 \"물 안 빠지는 색\"으로 지어진 것이 아니라, 조금씩 조금씩 색이 빠져 버린 것처럼 보였다. 이 점만 아니라면 그녀는 일반적인 비위 맞추기와 정중함의 더할 나위 없는 본보기라고 묘사될 만했다. 자기 앞에서 하는 모든 말을 감탄하며 경청하고, 말하는 이들을 마치 그 모습을 마음속으로 영혼에 새겨 두어 죽기 전에는 결코 떼어 놓지 않으려는 듯 바라보는 오랜 습관 탓에, 그녀의 머리는 완전히 한쪽으로 기울어 있었다. 그녀의 두 손은 저도 모르는 감탄을 표하듯 제멋대로 들어 올려지는 경련성 습관이 들어 있었다. 그녀의 두 눈도 비슷한 증세에 걸리기 쉬웠다. 그녀는 일찍이 들어 본 적 없을 만큼 부드러운 목소리를 지녔으며, 엄청나게 매부리진 그녀의 코는 콧대의 한가운데, 곧 그 마룻돌 격인 자리에 작은 혹이 하나 있어, 거기서부터 얼굴 쪽으로 처져 내려가 있었으니, 마치 무슨 일에도 결코 콧대를 치켜올리지 않겠다는 굽힐 수 없는 결의를 보이는 듯했다.\n\n톡스 양의 옷차림은 더없이 점잖고 좋았으나, 어딘가 모나고 옹색한 데가 있었다. 그녀는 보닛과 모자에 시들시들한 자그마한 꽃들을 즐겨 꽂았다. 때로는 그녀의 머리카락 사이에서 기이한 풀잎들이 눈에 띄기도 했다. 그리고 호기심 많은 이들은 그녀의 깃, 주름 장식, 가슴 장식, 소맷부리, 그 밖의 온갖 얇디얇은 물건들에 대해—사실 그녀가 걸친 것 가운데 양쪽 끝을 맞붙이게 되어 있는 것은 무엇이든—그 양쪽 끝이 결코 사이가 좋은 법이 없어, 한바탕 씨름을 하지 않고서는 도무지 만나려 들지 않는다는 것을 알아챘다. 그녀는 겨울에 걸칠 모피 물건들을 가지고 있었으니, 어깨걸이며 보아며 토시 같은 것들이었는데, 그것들은 사납게 곤두서 있어 도무지 매끈하지가 않았다. 그녀는 똑딱이가 달린 작은 가방들을 들고 다니기를 무척 좋아했는데, 그 가방들은 닫힐 때면 자그마한 권총처럼 탁 소리를 냈다. 그리고 성장(盛裝)을 할 때면, 목에 더없이 빈약한 로켓을 걸었는데, 거기에는 생기라곤 조금도 비치지 않는 물고기 눈 같은 늙은 눈알 하나가 새겨져 있었다. 이런 것들과 그와 비슷한 부류의 다른 모습들로 인해, 톡스 양은 이른바 제한된 자립 수단을 지닌 부인이며, 그것을 최대한 잘 활용한다는 견해가 퍼지게 되었다. 어쩌면 그녀의 종종걸음 또한 그 믿음을 부추겨, 보통 보폭의 한 걸음을 두세 걸음으로 잘게 나누는 것이 무엇이든 최대한 알뜰히 쓰는 그녀의 습관에서 비롯된 것임을 짐작하게 했을지 모른다.\n\n\"제가 확실히 말씀드리건대,\" 톡스 양이 어마어마하게 큰 절을 하며 말했다, \"돔비 씨께 소개되는 영예를 누리는 것은 오래도록 바라 왔으면서도 지금 이 순간 도무지 기대하지 못한 영광이옵니다. 사랑하는 칙 부인—루이자라고 불러도 될까요!\"\n\n칙 부인은 톡스 양의 손을 자기 손으로 잡아, 그 위에 포도주 잔의 발을 얹고는, 눈물을 억누르며 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"하느님의 축복이 함께하길!\"\n\n\"그럼 사랑하는 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"내 다정한 친구, 이제 좀 어떠세요?\"\n\n\"좀 나아요,\" 칙 부인이 응수했다. \"포도주 좀 들어요. 당신도 나만큼이나 마음 졸였을 테니, 분명 필요할 거예요.\"\n\n물론 돔비 씨가 거들었고, 누이의 잔도 다시 채워 주었는데, 누이는 (다른 데를 보느라 그의 의도를 알아채지 못한 채) 그동안 잔을 곧고 흔들림 없이 들고 있다가, 이윽고 크게 놀란 표정으로 그것을 바라보며 말했다, \"오, 폴, 도대체 무슨 짓을 한 거야!\"\n\n\"톡스 양은, 폴,\" 칙 부인이 여전히 그녀의 손을 잡은 채 말을 이었다, \"내가 오늘 있을 일을 얼마나 마음 졸이며 기대해 왔는지, 또 그것을 기다리며 머리끝부터 발끝까지 얼마나 떨고 흔들렸는지를 알고서, 패니에게 줄 작은 선물을 만들어 왔어. 내가 전해 주겠다고 약속했지. 톡스 양은 그야말로 손재주 그 자체라니까.\"\n\n\"오, 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"그런 말씀 마세요.\"\n\n\"화장대에 둘 바늘꽂이일 뿐이야, 폴,\" 그의 누이가 말을 이었다. \"당신네 남자들에게는 대체로 하찮은 그런 자질구레한 물건이지—그러는 게 지극히 당연하고, 우리도 달리 기대할 처지는 아니지만—그래도 우리 여자들은 거기에 어느 정도 의미를 두지.\"\n\n\"톡스 양은 참 친절하시군요,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 내가 말하건대, 또 말할 것이며, 또 말하지 않을 수 없는데,\" 그의 누이가 세 구절마다 한 번씩 톡스 양의 손 위에 포도주 잔의 발을 누르며 말을 이었다, \"톡스 양이 이 자리에 어울리게 글귀를 참으로 곱게 붙여 놓았어. 나는 '환영해요, 꼬마 돔비'를 시(詩)라고 부르겠어!\"\n\n\"그게 그 도안이오?\" 그녀의 오라비가 물었다.\n\n\"그게 그 도안이야,\" 루이자가 응수했다.\n\n\"하지만 부디 공정하게 헤아려 주세요, 사랑하는 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 낮고 간절한 애원의 어조로 말했다, \"오직—제 자신을 표현하기가 좀 어렵습니다만—결과가 불확실하다는 점 때문에 제가 이토록 큰 무례를 무릅쓰게 된 것이라고요. '환영합니다, 돔비 도련님'이 제 마음에는 훨씬 더 어울렸을 거라는 걸 분명 아실 거예요. 하지만 천사 같은 손님에게 따르는 불확실함이, 그러지 않았더라면 분에 넘치는 친근함으로 보였을 것을 부디 용서해 주리라 믿습니다.\" 톡스 양은 이렇게 말하며 돔비 씨를 향해 우아하게 몸을 굽혔고, 그 신사는 정중하게 그것을 받아 주었다. 앞서의 대화 속에 담긴 돔비와 아들에 대한 그런 종류의 인정조차 그에게는 무척 흡족했던 터라, 비록 그가 누이 칙 부인을 마음 약하고 사람 좋은 여자쯤으로 여기는 척했지만, 어쩌면 그녀는 세상 누구보다도 그에게 더 큰 영향력을 지니고 있었는지 모른다.\n\n\"오, 폴,\" 그 부인이 몇 분 동안 말없이 그의 이목구비를 바라보다가 다시 입을 열었다, \"너를 보고 있으면 웃어야 할지 울어야 할지 모르겠어, 정말이지, 위층의 그 사랑스러운 아가가 어찌나 떠오르는지.\"\n\n\"자!\" 칙 부인이 달콤한 미소를 지으며 말했다, \"이렇게 된 이상, 나는 패니의 모든 걸 용서하겠어!\"\n\n그것은 기독교 정신에서 나온 선언이었고, 칙 부인은 그 말이 자기에게 흐뭇함을 안긴다고 느꼈다. 그렇다고 올케에게 딱히 용서할 무엇이 있었던 것은 아니었다. 아니, 사실 아무것도 없었다. 다만 자기 오라비와 결혼한 것—그 자체가 일종의 무엄함이었다—과, 일이 흘러가는 가운데 사내아이가 아닌 계집아이를 낳은 것을 빼면 말이다. 그 일에 대해서 칙 부인은 자주, 자기가 올케에게 기대했던 바와는 사뭇 다른 일이며, 올케가 받아 온 온갖 배려와 융숭한 대접에 대한 흐뭇한 보답은 못 된다고 한마디씩 해 온 터였다.\n\n이때 돔비 씨가 황급히 방 밖으로 불려 나가서, 두 부인만 함께 남게 되었다. 톡스 양은 즉시 경련하듯 들떴다.\n\n\"당신이 내 오라비를 흠모하게 될 줄 알았어요. 미리 말했잖아요, 그렇죠,\" 루이자가 말했다. 톡스 양의 두 손과 두 눈이 얼마나 그러한지를 표현했다. \"게다가 그이의 재산으로 말하자면!\"\n\n\"아!\" 톡스 양이 깊은 감정을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"어—마어마하지!\"\n\n\"하지만 그분의 그 거동이라니, 사랑하는 루이자!\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"그 위풍이라니! 그 위엄이라니! 제가 여태껏 본 어느 누구의 초상화도 그런 자질이 절반도 담겨 있지 않았어요. 어찌나 당당한지: 어찌나 타협이라곤 모르는지: 가슴이 어찌나 떡 벌어졌는지: 어찌나 꼿꼿한지! 돈으로 치면 요크 공작이세요, 그래요, 그에 못지않으세요!\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"저 같으면 그렇게 일컫겠어요.\"\n\n\"아니, 폴!\" 그가 돌아오자 누이가 외쳤다, \"안색이 아주 창백하잖아! 무슨 일 있는 건 아니지?\"\n\n\"안타깝게도 말이오, 루이자, 사람들 말이 패니가—\"\n\n\"자, 폴,\" 누이가 일어서며 응수했다, \"그런 말 믿지 마. 공연히 마음 흔들리지 말고. 네가 사회에 얼마나 중요한 존재인지를 생각하고, 더 잘 알아야 마땅한 사람들이 분별없이 떠벌리는 말에 휘둘리지 마. 정말이지 그 사람들이 어이가 없네.\"\n\n\"내 처신은 나도 안다고 생각하오, 루이자,\" 돔비 씨가 뻣뻣하게 말했다, \"세상 앞에서 어떻게 처신해야 하는지를.\"\n\n\"누구보다도 잘 알지, 폴. 누구도 너만큼 못해. 그걸 의심하는 자가 있다면 정말 무지하고 천한 자일 거야.\"\n\n\"정말 무지하고 천한 자고말고요!\" 톡스 양이 부드럽게 따라 말했다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 루이자가 말을 이었다, \"내 경험을 조금이라도 믿는다면, 폴, 부족한 건 패니 쪽의 노력 하나뿐이라고 확신해도 좋아. 그리고 그 노력을,\" 그녀는 사무적인 태도로 보닛을 벗고 모자와 장갑을 매만지며 말을 이었다, \"패니가 발휘하도록 북돋아 주어야 하고, 또 정말 필요하다면, 다그쳐서라도 발휘하게 해야 해. 자, 폴, 나랑 같이 위층으로 가자.\"\n\n돔비 씨는, 앞서 말한 이유로 평소에도 누이에게 영향을 받기도 했거니와, 경험 많고 부산한 부인이라며 그녀를 정말로 신뢰하기도 했던 터라, 그 말에 따랐다. 그러고는 즉시 그녀를 따라 병실로 갔다.\n\n부인은 그가 떠날 때 그대로 침대에 누워, 어린 딸을 가슴에 꼭 끌어안고 있었다. 아이는 아까와 똑같은 강렬함으로 어미에게 바싹 매달려 있었고, 고개를 들지도, 부드러운 뺨을 어미의 얼굴에서 떼지도, 둘러선 이들을 쳐다보지도, 말하지도, 움직이지도, 눈물 한 방울 흘리지도 않았다.\n\n\"그 어린 딸이 없으면 부인께서 불안해하셔서,\" 박사가 돔비 씨에게 속삭였다. \"다시 들여보내는 게 좋겠다고 판단했습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 방도가 없겠소?\" 돔비 씨가 물었다.\n\n박사가 고개를 저었다. \"더는 손쓸 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n창문들은 열려 있었고, 바깥에는 땅거미가 내려앉고 있었다.\n\n시도해 본 강심제들의 냄새가 방 안에 코를 찌르도록 배어 있었으나, 부인이 들이쉬는 흐릿하고 나른한 공기 속에서는 아무런 향기도 풍기지 않았다.\n\n침대 둘레에는 어찌나 엄숙한 정적이 감돌았는지, 또 두 의사가 그 미동도 없는 형체를 어찌나 큰 연민과 어찌나 적은 희망으로 바라보았는지, 칙 부인은 한순간 자기 목적을 잊고 말았다. 그러나 이내 용기를 그러모으고, 스스로 침착함이라 부르는 것을 그러모아, 침대 곁에 앉아, 잠든 이를 깨우려는 사람의 낮고 또박또박한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"패니! 패니!\"\n\n대답으로는 아무 소리도 없었다. 다만 돔비 씨의 시계와 파커 펩스 박사의 시계가 크게 똑딱이는 소리뿐이었는데, 그 정적 속에서 두 시계는 경주라도 벌이는 듯했다.\n\n\"패니, 얘,\" 칙 부인이 짐짓 경쾌한 척하며 말했다, \"여기 돔비 씨가 너를 보러 오셨어. 말 좀 안 할래? 사람들이 네 어린 아들을—아가 말이야, 패니, 알지? 아직 거의 보지도 못했잖아—침대에 뉘려고 하는데, 네가 좀 정신을 차려야 그럴 수가 있어. 이제 좀 정신을 차릴 때가 됐다고 생각하지 않니? 응?\"\n\n그녀는 침대 쪽으로 귀를 기울이고 들었다. 동시에 둘러선 이들을 돌아보며 손가락을 들어 보였다.\n\n\"응?\" 그녀가 되풀이했다, \"뭐라고 했어, 패니? 못 들었어.\"\n\n대답으로는 한마디 말도, 아무 소리도 없었다. 돔비 씨의 시계와 파커 펩스 박사의 시계가 더 빨리 내달리는 듯했다.\n\n\"자, 정말이지, 패니야,\" 올케가 자세를 바꾸며, 자기도 모르게 덜 자신 있는, 그러나 더 간절한 어조로 말했다, \"네가 정신을 차리지 않으면 너한테 정말 화를 내야 할지도 몰라. 너는 노력을 해야 해, 어쩌면 네가 내키지 않는 아주 크고 고통스러운 노력을 말이야. 하지만 이건 노력의 세상이잖니, 패니, 우리에게 이토록 많은 게 걸려 있을 때는 결코 굴해서는 안 돼. 자! 해 봐! 안 그러면 정말로 야단칠 거야!\"\n\n뒤이은 침묵 속의 경주는 사납고 맹렬했다. 두 시계는 서로 밀치고 서로 발을 거는 듯했다.\n\n\"패니!\" 루이자가 점점 커지는 불안으로 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"나만 봐. 내 말을 듣고 알아듣는다는 걸 보여 주게 눈만 떠 줘, 응? 맙소사, 선생님들, 어떻게 해야 하나요!\"\n\n두 의사가 침대 너머로 눈빛을 주고받았다. 그리고 시의가 몸을 굽혀 아이의 귀에 속삭였다. 그 속삭임의 뜻을 알아듣지 못한 채, 그 자그마한 것은 핏기라고는 전혀 없는 얼굴과 깊고 검은 눈을 그에게로 돌렸으나, 끌어안은 손은 조금도 늦추지 않았다.\n\n속삭임이 되풀이되었다.\n\n\"엄마!\" 아이가 말했다.\n\n그 자그마하고, 익숙하고, 더없이 사랑스러운 목소리가, 그토록 기력이 다한 와중에도 얼마간의 의식의 기미를 일깨웠다. 잠시, 감긴 눈꺼풀이 떨렸고, 콧방울이 실룩였으며, 더없이 희미한 미소의 그림자가 어렸다.\n\n\"엄마!\" 아이가 소리 내어 흐느끼며 외쳤다. \"오, 사랑하는 엄마! 오, 사랑하는 엄마!\"\n\n박사가 아이의 흩어진 곱슬머리를 어미의 얼굴과 입에서 부드럽게 쓸어 넘겼다. 아, 그것들은 거기 얼마나 고요히 누워 있었는지. 그것들을 흔들 숨결이 얼마나 가냘프게밖에 남아 있지 않았는지!\n\n이렇게, 제 두 팔 안에 놓인 그 가냘픈 부유물을 꼭 붙든 채, 어미는 온 세상을 굽이도는 그 어둡고 알 수 없는 바다 위로 떠밀려 나갔다.\n\n가장 잘 다스려진 가정에서도 때때로 일어나기 마련인 비상사태에 대비하여 시의적절한 대비가 이루어지는 장.\n\n\"나는 평생 두고두고 스스로를 대견하게 여길 거예요,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"앞으로 우리에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지 까맣게 모르던 그때—정말 무언가에 영감이라도 받은 사람처럼—가엾고 사랑스러운 패니에게 모든 걸 다 용서한다고 말해 둔 일을요. 무슨 일이 벌어지든, 그건 언제까지나 내게 위안이 될 거예요!\"\n\n칙 부인은 이 인상적인 소견을 응접실에서 토로했다. 위층에서 가족 상복을 짓느라 바쁜 양재사들을 둘러보고 내려온 뒤였다. 그녀는 이 말을 칙 씨를 위해 들려준 것이었는데, 칙 씨는 살집 좋고 머리가 벗어진 신사로 얼굴이 매우 컸고 두 손은 늘 주머니에 찔러 넣고 있었으며, 천성적으로 휘파람을 불고 콧노래를 흥얼거리는 버릇이 있었으나, 슬픔의 집에서 그런 소리가 무례하다는 것을 의식하여 지금은 그것을 억누르느라 애를 좀 쓰고 있었다.\n\n\"너무 무리하지 마시오, 루.\" 칙 씨가 말했다, \"안 그러면 경련으로 드러눕게 될 거요, 내 보아하니. 라잇 톨 루르 룰! 아이고 맙소사, 깜빡했군! 우리는 오늘 여기 있다가 내일이면 가 버리는 신세인데!\"\n\n칙 부인은 나무라는 눈길 한 번으로 만족하고는, 다시 이야기의 갈피를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"이 가슴 미어지는 일이 우리 모두에게 경종이 되었으면 해요. 평소에 스스로를 분발시키는 데 익숙해지고, 우리에게 요구되는 노력을 제때 기울이도록 말이에요. 모든 일에는 교훈이 담겨 있지요, 우리가 그것을 받아들이기만 한다면요. 이 교훈을 놓쳐 버린다면 그건 순전히 우리 잘못일 거예요.\"\n\n칙 씨는 이 말에 뒤따른 무거운 침묵을 \"구두장이가 하나 있었네\"라는, 도무지 어울리지 않는 가락으로 침범했다. 그러더니 좀 당황한 듯 스스로를 멈추고는, 지금 같은 이런 우울한 때를 우리가 유익하게 활용하지 못한다면 그건 분명 우리 잘못이라고 한마디 덧붙였다.\n\n\"제 생각엔, 칙 씨,\" 그의 반려가 잠깐 멈췄다가 응수했다, \"대학 호른파이프 가락을 끌어들이거나, 그에 못지않게 뜻도 없고 정도 없는 럼-트-이디티, 바우-와우-와우 같은 소리를 늘어놓는 것보다는, 더 유익하게 활용할 방도가 있을 텐데요!\"—과연 칙 씨가 입속으로 그런 가락을 흥얼거린 터였고, 칙 부인은 그것을 사람을 시들게 할 만한 경멸의 어조로 되뇌었다.\n\n\"그저 버릇일 뿐이오, 여보.\" 칙 씨가 변명했다.\n\n\"무슨 소리예요! 버릇이라뇨!\" 그의 아내가 응수했다. \"당신이 이성을 가진 존재라면, 그런 우스꽝스러운 변명은 하지 마세요. 버릇이라니! 만약 내가 파리처럼 천장을 걸어 다니는 (당신 말마따나) 버릇을 들이면, 아마 그 소리를 실컷 듣게 될 테죠.\"\n\n그런 버릇이라면 어느 정도 악명을 떨치게 될 법도 했으므로, 칙 씨는 감히 그 견해에 토를 달지 못했다.\n\n\"바우-와우-와우!\" 칙 부인이 마지막 음절에 사람을 옴짝달싹 못 하게 하는 경멸을 실어 되뇌었다. \"당신 신분의 사람이라기보다는, 공수병에 걸린 직업 가수에 더 가깝지 뭐예요!\"\n\n\"아기는 어떻소, 루?\" 칙 씨가 화제를 돌리려고 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 아기를 말하는 거예요?\" 칙 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"가엾게도 어미를 여읜 그 어린 아기 말이오,\" 칙 씨가 말했다. \"내가 아는 아기는 그 애밖에 없는데, 여보.\"\n\n\"당신이 아는 아기가 그 애밖에 없다고요,\" 칙 부인이 응수했다. \"더 부끄러운 일이라고 말하려던 참이에요.\"\n\n칙 씨는 어리둥절한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"정말이지 오늘 아침에 내가 겪은 일은, 저 아래층 식당이 온통 아기들 천지가 되어서—제정신인 사람이라면 아무도 믿지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"온통 아기들 천지라니!\" 칙 씨가 놀란 표정으로 주위를 둘러보며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"웬만한 사람이라면 떠올렸을 거예요,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"가엾고 사랑스러운 패니가 이제 세상에 없으니—이 말은 언제까지나 내게 위안이고 위로가 될 거예요\"—여기서 그녀는 눈물을 닦았다—\"유모를 구하는 일이 꼭 필요해졌다는 걸요.\"\n\n\"오! 아!\" 칙 씨가 말했다. \"투르-루!—그게 인생이라는 거지, 내 말은. 마땅한 사람을 구했길 바라오, 여보.\"\n\n\"천만에요,\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"구할 가망도 없어요, 내가 보기엔. 그러는 사이에 가엾은 아이는 굶어 죽을 판이고요. 폴은 워낙 까다로워서—하기야 당연하지요, 이 아들 하나에 온 마음을 쏟았으니—나서는 사람마다 트집거리가 어찌나 많은지, 내가 보기엔 일이 성사될 가망이 도무지 없어요. 그러는 동안 아이는, 물론—\"\n\n\"악마한테로 가고 있겠지,\" 칙 씨가 생각에 잠겨 말했다, \"그렇고말고.\"\n\n그러나 돔비 집안 사람이 그런 데로 간다는 생각에 칙 부인의 얼굴에 떠오른 분노를 보고 자기가 실언했음을 깨달은 칙 씨는, 멋진 제안 하나로 자기 실수를 만회하려는 생각에 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"찻주전자로 임시변통 뭐라도 할 수 없을까?\"\n\n화제를 너무 일찍 끝내 버리려고 작정했다 한들, 이보다 더 효과적으로 그럴 수는 없었을 것이다. 칙 부인은 말없이 체념한 채 그를 몇 초 동안 바라보고 나서, 부디 당신이 화를 돋우려고 그 말을 한 건 아니길 바란다고, 만약 그렇다면 당신 마음씨에 그리 명예로운 일이 못 될 테니까 하고 말했다. 또 진심으로 한 말이 아니길 바란다고, 만약 그렇다면 당신 머리에 그리 명예로운 일이 못 될 테니까 하고 덧붙였다. 어느 쪽이든 간에, 당신이 아무리 낙천적인 기질이라 한들 인간 본성 전반에 대해 이보다 더한 모욕이 될 만한 말을 내놓기를 바랄 수는 없을 테니, 이쯤에서 논의를 그만두기로 했으면 좋겠다고 했다.\n\n그러고 나서 칙 부인은 바퀴 소리에 이끌려 위풍당당하게 창가로 걸어가 블라인드 사이로 내다보았다. 칙 씨는 당분간 운명이 자기 편이 아님을 알고 더 말하지 않고 자리를 떴다. 그러나 칙 씨가 늘 이런 식인 것은 아니었다. 그도 종종 기세를 잡는 때가 있었고, 그럴 때면 루이자를 호되게 혼내 주었다. 부부 싸움에서 두 사람은 대체로 잘 어울리는, 꽤 균형 잡힌, 주고받는 한 쌍이었다. 대개는 누가 이길지 점치기가 매우 어려웠다. 칙 씨가 패한 듯 보일 때면 종종 그는 별안간 분발하여 형세를 뒤집고, 그 형세를 칙 부인의 귀에 대고 요란하게 휘둘러서 단번에 판을 휩쓸곤 했다. 칙 씨 자신도 칙 부인에게서 그와 비슷한 뜻밖의 제동을 당하기 일쑤였으므로, 두 사람의 자그마한 다툼은 대개 매우 생기 넘치는 불확실성의 성격을 띠었다.\n\n방금 언급한 그 바퀴를 타고 막 도착한 톡스 양이 숨을 헐떡이며 방 안으로 뛰어 들어왔다.\n\n\"사랑하는 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"그 빈자리는 아직 못 채웠나요?\"\n\n\"착한 분, 그래요, 아직이에요,\" 칙 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 사랑하는 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 응수했다, \"내 바라고 믿건대—아니, 잠깐만요, 당장 그 사람을 소개할게요.\"\n\n올라올 때만큼이나 빠르게 다시 아래층으로 뛰어 내려간 톡스 양은, 그 사람을 삯마차에서 내리게 하고는, 곧 그 일행을 거느리고 돌아왔다.\n\n그제야 드러난 사실인즉, 톡스 양은 그 단어를 법률이나 사무에서 쓰는 의미, 곧 한 개인을 뜻하는 뜻으로 쓴 게 아니라, 집합 명사, 곧 여럿을 가리키는 뜻으로 쓴 것이었다. 톡스 양이 거느리고 온 것은, 통통하고 볼이 발그레하며 건강하고 사과 같은 얼굴의 젊은 여자로 품에 갓난아이를 안고 있었고, 그만큼 통통하지는 않으나 역시 사과 같은 얼굴의 더 젊은 여자가 양손에 통통하고 사과 같은 얼굴의 아이를 하나씩 잡고 있었으며, 또 다른 통통하고 사과 같은 얼굴의 사내아이가 혼자 걸어왔고, 마지막으로 통통하고 사과 같은 얼굴의 사내가 또 다른 통통하고 사과 같은 얼굴의 사내아이를 품에 안고 와서 바닥에 내려놓고는, 쉰 목소리로 속삭이며 \"형 조니를 꼭 붙잡고 있으라\"고 타일렀다.\n\n\"사랑하는 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"당신이 크게 걱정하고 있는 걸 알고, 그 걱정을 덜어 주고 싶어서, 내가 직접 퀸 샬럿 왕립 기혼 부인회로 달려갔어요—당신이 깜빡한 그곳 말이에요—그리고 물었지요, 거기에 적합하다고 생각되는 사람이 있느냐고요. 없다고들 하더군요. 그 대답을 들었을 때, 정말이지 여보, 당신을 생각하면 거의 절망에 빠질 지경이었어요. 그런데 마침 그 왕립 기혼 부인 하나가 그 문의를 듣고는, 자기 집으로 돌아간 또 다른 사람을 사감에게 일러 주면서, 그 사람이라면 십중팔구 가장 만족스러울 거라고 하지 뭐예요. 그 말을 듣자마자, 그리고 사감에게서—훌륭한 추천에다 흠잡을 데 없는 인품이라는—확인을 받자마자, 나는 주소를 얻어서, 여보, 다시 달려간 거예요.\"\n\n\"역시 착하고 다정한 톡스 양이라니까요!\" 루이자가 말했다.\n\n\"천만에요,\" 톡스 양이 응수했다. \"그런 말 마세요. 그 집에 도착해 보니(어찌나 깨끗한지, 여보! 바닥에 음식을 놓고 먹어도 될 정도였어요), 온 가족이 식탁에 둘러앉아 있더군요. 그래서 이 사람들에 대한 어떤 설명도 다 함께 모인 모습을 직접 보는 것의 절반만큼도 당신과 돔비 씨께 든든하지 못하리라 싶어서, 다 같이 데려온 거예요. 이 사람이,\" 톡스 양이 사과 같은 얼굴의 사내를 가리키며 말했다, \"아버지예요. 좀 앞으로 나와 주시겠어요, 선생?\"\n\n사과 같은 얼굴의 사내는 멋쩍게 그 청에 응하여, 킬킬 웃으며 앞줄에 서서 이를 드러내고 씩 웃었다.\n\n\"이쪽은 물론 그의 아내고요,\" 톡스 양이 갓난아이를 안은 젊은 여자를 가리키며 말했다. \"잘 지냈어요, 폴리?\"\n\n\"덕분에 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다, 마님,\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n폴리를 자연스럽게 끌어내려고, 톡스 양은 마치 한 보름쯤 못 본 오랜 지인을 대하듯 너그럽게 물은 것이었다.\n\n\"그렇다니 다행이에요,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"저쪽 젊은 여자는 그 사람의 미혼인 여동생인데, 함께 살면서 아이들을 돌봐 줄 거예요. 이름은 제미마예요. 잘 지냈어요, 제미마?\"\n\n\"덕분에 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다, 마님,\" 제미마가 응수했다.\n\n\"정말이지 그렇다니 무척 다행이에요,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"계속 그렇게 지내길 바라요. 아이가 다섯이에요. 막내가 생후 육 주 됐고요. 코에 물집이 잡힌 저 예쁜 사내아이가 맏이예요. 저 물집은 내 생각에,\" 톡스 양이 가족들을 둘러보며 말했다, \"체질적인 게 아니라 사고로 생긴 거지요?\"\n\n사과 같은 얼굴의 사내가 \"다리미요\" 하고 으르렁거린 것으로 짐작되었다.\n\n\"미안하지만, 선생,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"방금 뭐라고—\"\n\n\"다리미요,\" 그가 되풀이했다.\n\n\"오, 그렇군요,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"그래요! 정말 그렇겠네요. 깜빡했어요. 그 어린것이 엄마가 없는 사이에 뜨거운 다리미 냄새를 맡은 거군요. 맞는 말씀이에요, 선생. 우리가 문 앞에 도착했을 때, 친절하게도 당신이 직업이 뭐라고 일러 주려던 참이었지요—\"\n\n\"화부입니다,\" 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"교살자라니!\" 톡스 양이 화들짝 놀라 말했다.\n\n\"화부요,\" 사내가 말했다. \"증기 기관 말입니다.\"\n\n\"오-호! 그래요!\" 톡스 양이 그를 생각에 잠겨 바라보며, 여전히 그 뜻을 매우 어렴풋이밖에 이해하지 못한 듯 응수했다.\n\"그래, 그 일이 마음에 드세요, 선생?\"\n\n\"뭐가 말입니까, 마님?\" 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"그거 말이에요,\" 톡스 양이 대답했다. \"당신 직업 말이에요.\"\n\n\"오! 그럭저럭 괜찮습니다, 마님. 재가 가끔 이 안으로 들어와서요.\" 그가 가슴을 가리켰다. \"그래서 지금처럼 사람 목소리가 거칠어집니다. 하지만 그건 재 때문이지, 성미가 거칠어서가 아닙니다, 마님.\"\n\n톡스 양은 이 대답으로도 깨달은 바가 너무 적어서 그 화제를 이어 가기가 어려운 듯했다. 그러나 칙 부인이 폴리와 그 아이들, 혼인 증명서, 추천서 등을 꼼꼼히 사적으로 검토하기 시작하면서 그녀를 구해 주었다. 폴리가 이 시련에서 흠 없이 통과하자, 칙 부인은 그 결과 보고를 들고 자기 오라비의 방으로 물러갔는데, 그것에 대한 강조 어린 논평이자 뒷받침으로, 가장 발그레한 투들 가의 어린것 둘을 데리고 갔다. 투들은 그 사과 같은 얼굴의 가족의 성이었다.\n\n돔비 씨는 아내가 죽은 뒤로 자기 방에 틀어박혀, 어린 아들의 소년기와 교육과 진로에 대한 환상에 잠겨 있었다. 그의 차가운 마음 밑바닥에는, 평소의 짐보다 더 차갑고 무거운 무언가가 가라앉아 있었다. 그러나 그것은 자기 자신의 상실감이라기보다 아이가 잃은 것에 대한 감각이었고, 그것이 그의 안에서 거의 분노에 가까운 슬픔을 불러일으켰다. 자기가 그토록 큰 희망을 걸어 둔 생명과 그 앞날이, 출발부터 이토록 하찮은 결핍 때문에 위태로워진다는 것, 돔비와 아들이 유모 하나를 구하지 못해 비틀거린다는 것은 쓰라린 굴욕이었다. 그러면서도 그는 자존심과 질투심에 사로잡혀, 자기 영혼의 소망을 이루는 그 첫걸음을 떼는 데에서부터 고용된 하녀에게 의존해야 한다는 생각을—그 여자가 당분간 아이에게, 자기와의 결혼으로 자기 아내가 될 수 있었던 그 모든 것이 되어 줄 거라는 생각을—어찌나 씁쓸하게 바라보았던지, 후보자가 하나씩 거절될 때마다 은근한 쾌감을 느낄 정도였다. 그러나 이제는 더 이상 이 두 갈래 감정 사이에서 갈팡질팡할 수 없는 때가 왔다. 더욱이 그의 누이가 톡스 양의 지칠 줄 모르는 우정을 거듭 칭찬하며 폴리 투들의 자격을 늘어놓은 뒤로, 그 자격에는 아무런 흠도 없어 보였으니 더욱 그러했다.\n\n\"이 아이들은 건강해 보이는군,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"그러나 맙소사, 이 아이들이 언젠가 폴과 일종의 친척 관계를 주장하게 될 거라고 생각하면!\"\n\n\"하지만 무슨 친척 관계가 있겠어요!\" 루이자가 입을 떼었다—\n\n\"있겠느냐고!\" 돔비 씨가 따라 말했다. 그는 자기도 모르게 입 밖에 낸 그 생각에 누이가 끼어들 줄은 생각지 못한 터였다. \"있겠느냐고 했나, 루이자?\"\n\n\"내 말은, 있을 수 있겠—\"\n\n\"아무 관계도 없지,\" 돔비 씨가 매섭게 말했다. \"온 세상이 그걸 알 거다, 짐작건대. 슬픔이 나를 백치로 만든 건 아니야, 루이자. 이 아이들을 데리고 나가, 루이자! 이 여자와 그 남편을 만나 봐야겠다.\"\n\n칙 부인은 그 어린 투들 한 쌍을 데리고 나갔다가, 이내 오라비가 들라고 한 그 더 억센 부부를 데리고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"이보게, 착한 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 안락의자에서 팔다리와 관절을 가진 사람이 아니라 한 덩어리처럼 통째로 몸을 돌리며 말했다, \"듣자 하니 자네는 가난하여, 내 아들인 이 어린 사내아이를 젖을 먹여 키워서 돈을 벌고 싶다고 하더군. 이 아이는 무엇으로도 결코 대신할 수 없는 것을 너무 일찍 잃었네. 자네가 그렇게 해서 자네 가족의 생계에 보탬을 하겠다는 데에 나는 이의가 없네. 내가 보기엔 자네는 그럴 자격이 있는 사람 같군. 그러나 자네가 그 자격으로 내 집에 들어오기 전에, 한두 가지 조건을 두어야겠네. 자네가 여기 있는 동안에는, 자네가 늘—그러니까 리처즈라는 이름으로 불려야 한다는 점을 못 박아 두겠네. 흔하고 편리한 이름이지. 리처즈라고 불리는 데에 이의가 있나? 남편과 상의해 보는 게 낫겠군.\"\n\n\"어떤가?\" 돔비 씨가 꽤 오랜 침묵 끝에 말했다. \"자네가 리처즈라고 불리는 것에 대해 남편은 뭐라고 하나?\"\n\n남편이라는 자는 그저 킬킬 웃으며 이를 드러내고 씩 웃을 뿐, 줄곧 오른손으로 입을 쓱쓱 문지르며 손바닥을 적시기만 했으므로, 투들 부인은 그를 두세 번 헛되이 쿡쿡 찔러 본 뒤에, 무릎을 살짝 굽혀 인사하고는 \"혹시 제 이름이 아닌 다른 이름으로 불린다면, 그것이 품삯에 반영될지\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 물론이지,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"이건 전적으로 품삯의 문제로 삼고 싶네. 자, 리처즈, 자네가 어미 잃은 내 아이에게 젖을 먹인다면, 늘 이 점을 명심해 주게. 자네는 일정한 의무를 다하는 대가로 넉넉한 급료를 받게 될 텐데, 그 의무를 수행하는 동안 자네 가족과는 되도록 적게 만나기를 바라네. 그 의무가 더 이상 요구되지도 행해지지도 않고, 급료가 더 이상 지급되지 않게 되면, 우리 사이의 모든 관계는 끝나는 거네. 내 말 알아듣겠나?\"\n\n투들 부인은 그것을 미심쩍어하는 듯했다. 투들 본인으로 말하자면, 자기가 완전히 갈피를 못 잡고 있다는 데에는 분명 한 점의 의심도 없어 보였다.\n\n\"자네는 자네 자식이 있지,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"이 계약에는, 자네가 내 아이에게 정을 붙인다거나 내 아이가 자네에게 정을 붙인다거나 하는 일은 전혀 들어 있지 않네. 나는 그런 종류의 것은 기대하지도 바라지도 않네. 오히려 그 반대야. 자네가 여기서 떠날 때면, 자네는 그저 사고팔고, 빌리고 빌려주는 거래를 마친 것일 뿐이니, 그대로 떠나 있게. 아이는 자네를 더 이상 기억하지 않게 될 거고, 자네도, 괜찮다면, 아이를 더 이상 기억하지 않게 될 걸세.\"\n\n투들 부인은 전보다 볼에 약간 더 핏기가 돈 채로, \"제 분수는 안다고 생각합니다\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"안다니 다행이군, 리처즈,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"자네가 그걸 아주 잘 알고 있으리라 의심치 않네. 사실 그건 너무도 명백하고 뻔한 일이라, 달리 될 수가 없겠지. 루이자, 얘야, 리처즈와 돈에 관해 매듭짓고, 본인이 원하는 때에 원하는 방식으로 받게 해 주거라. 이름이 뭐라고 했나—자네, 잠깐 할 말이 있네!\"\n\n아내를 따라 방을 나서려던 차에 문턱에서 이렇게 멈춰 세워진 투들은, 되돌아와 홀로 돔비 씨와 마주 섰다. 그는 건장하고 헐렁하며 어깨가 둥글고 발을 끌며 걷는 텁수룩한 사내로, 옷이 몸에 아무렇게나 걸쳐져 있었다. 머리와 구레나룻이 무성했는데, 그 본래 색조는 아마 매연과 석탄 먼지로 더 짙어진 듯했다. 손은 단단하고 옹이졌으며, 이마는 떡갈나무 껍질만큼이나 결이 거칠고 네모졌다. 모든 면에서 돔비 씨와는 완전한 대조였으니, 돔비 씨는 새 지폐처럼 반들반들하고 빳빳하며, 황금 샤워의 자극적인 작용으로 인위적으로 조여지고 팽팽해진 듯 보이는, 말끔히 면도하고 단정하게 차려입은 돈 많은 신사 가운데 하나였다.\n\n\"자네에겐 아들이 있다지?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"넷입니다, 나리. 사내가 넷에 계집이 하나죠. 모두 살아 있고요!\"\n\n\"이런, 그 아이들을 먹여 살리는 게 자네 힘에 부치겠군!\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"세상에서 딱 한 가지 일만 빼면, 그보다 더 힘에 부칠 일이 없겠습죠, 나리.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\"\n\n\"그 애들을 잃는 일입죠, 나리.\"\n\n\"글은 읽을 줄 아나?\" 돔비 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"뭐, 그리 잘은 못 합니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"쓸 줄은?\"\n\n\"분필로 말입니까요, 나리?\"\n\n\"무엇으로든?\"\n\n\"어쩔 수 없이 시켜야 한다면, 분필로 조금쯤은 끄적일 수 있을 것 같습니다요,\" 투들이 잠시 생각하고서 말했다.\n\n\"그런데도,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"자네 나이가 서른두셋쯤 됐을 텐데?\"\n\n\"그쯤 됐을 겁니다요, 나리,\" 투들이 좀 더 생각하고서 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 왜 배우지 않나?\" 돔비 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"이제 배우려고요, 나리. 제 어린 아들놈 하나가 충분히 자라서 제 스스로 학교를 다니게 되면, 저를 가르쳐 줄 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 돔비 씨가 그를 유심히, 그다지 호의 없이 바라보며 말했다. 그동안 투들은 방 안을(주로 천장을) 둘러보며 여전히 손으로 입을 쓱쓱 문지르고 있었다. \"방금 내가 자네 아내에게 한 말을 들었나?\"\n\n\"폴리가 들었습죠,\" 투들이 자기 더 나은 반쪽에 대한 완전한 신뢰를 담아, 모자를 어깨 너머 문 쪽으로 휙 젖히며 말했다. \"다 괜찮습니다요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 묻는 건 자네가 들었느냐는 거네. 들었겠지, 짐작건대, 그리고 알아들었겠지?\" 돔비 씨가 캐물었다.\n\n\"듣긴 들었습죠,\" 투들이 말했다, \"하지만 제대로 알아들었는지는 모르겠습니다요, 나리, 제가 배운 게 없는 데다 말씀이—실례지만—좀 어려워서요. 하지만 폴리가 들었습죠. 다 괜찮습니다요.\"\n\n\"자네가 만사를 아내에게 맡기는 듯하니,\" 돔비 씨가, 더 강한 성격인 남편에게 자기 견해를 한층 더 또렷이 새겨 두려던 의도가 좌절되어 말했다, \"자네에게 무슨 말을 해 봤자 소용없겠군.\"\n\n\"전혀 소용없습죠,\" 투들이 말했다. \"폴리가 들었습죠. 그 사람은 빈틈없으니까요, 나리.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 더 붙잡지 않겠네,\" 돔비 씨가 실망하여 응수했다. \"자네는 평생 어디서 일했나?\"\n\n\"결혼하기 전까진 대체로 땅속에서요, 나리. 그러고 나서 지상으로 올라왔습죠. 이 철도라는 게 본격적으로 돌아가게 되면, 그중 하나에 들어갈 작정입니다요.\"\n\n그러고는 쉰 목소리의 속삭임으로 \"우리는 어린 바일러를 그 길로 키울 작정입니다요\" 하고 덧붙이자, 돔비 씨는 거만하게 어린 바일러가 누구냐고 물었다.\n\n\"맏이입니다요, 나리,\" 투들이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"흔한 이름은 아니지요. 어찌나 그런지, 그 애를 교회에 데려갔을 때 목사님이 그건 세례명이 못 되니 줄 수 없다고 하시더군요. 하지만 우리는 그래도 변함없이 바일러라고 부릅니다요. 나쁜 뜻은 없으니까요. 우리는요.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 돔비 씨가 그를 뚜렷한 불쾌감을 담아 바라보며 물었다, \"자네 말은, 아이 이름을 보일러(보일러)를 따서 지었다는 건가?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 아뇨, 나리,\" 투들이 자기 오해를 너그럽게 헤아리며 응수했다. \"그럴 리가요! 아닙니다, 나리. 바-일러를 따서요, 나리. 그 증기 기관이 그 애한테는 거의 대부나 다름없어서, 그래서 바일러라고 부른 겁니다요, 아시겠어요!\"\n\n마지막 지푸라기 한 올이 짐 실은 낙타의 등을 부러뜨리듯, 이 한마디 정보는 가라앉아 가던 돔비 씨의 기력을 짓이겨 버렸다. 그는 아이의 양부에게 문 쪽으로 가라는 손짓을 했고, 그자는 조금도 마지못한 기색 없이 떠났다. 그러고 나서 돔비 씨는 열쇠를 돌려 문을 잠그고, 홀로 비참한 심정으로 방 안을 오락가락 거닐었다.\n\n그가 이런 마찰과 자존심의 긁힘을, 아내의 죽음을 느낀 것보다 더 절절히 느꼈다고 말한다면, 그것은 가혹할 뿐 아니라 어쩌면 전적으로 옳지도 않을 것이다. 그러나 그것들이 그 죽음이라는 사건을 그에게 새로운 힘으로 각인시키고, 거기에 한층 더한 무게와 쓰라림을 더해 준 것만은 분명했다. 자기 아이에 대한 소유 감각에 그것은 거친 충격이었다—그가 보기에 한낱 땅의 티끌 같은 이 사람들이 자기에게 꼭 필요한 존재라는 것이. 그러니 그것에 마음이 흔들리는 만큼, 그런 필요를 만들어 낸 그 사건을 한탄하는 것도 자연스러운 일이었다. 그 빳빳하고 꿰뚫을 수 없는 위엄과 침착함에도 불구하고, 그는 방 안을 오락가락 거니는 동안 앞을 가리는 눈물을 닦았다. 그리고 세상 무엇과도 바꾸지 않을 만큼 결코 누구에게도 보이고 싶지 않은 격정에 사로잡혀, 자주 \"가엾은 어린것!\" 하고 중얼거렸다.\n\n그가 아이를 통해 자기 자신을 가엾이 여겼다는 것은, 어쩌면 돔비 씨의 자존심을 잘 보여 주는 일이었을 것이다. 가엾은 나, 가 아니었다. 평생 \"대체로 땅속에서\" 일해 왔지만 죽음이 그 집 문을 한 번도 두드린 적 없고, 그 초라한 식탁에 매일 네 아들이 둘러앉는, 무지한 막일꾼의 아내에게 어쩔 수 없이 의지하게 된 가엾은 홀아비, 가 아니었다—바로 가엾은 어린것이었다.\n\n그 말이 입가에 맴돌던 차에, 문득 그에게 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다—그것은 그의 희망과 두려움과 온갖 생각이 하나의 중심으로 쏠려 가던 강한 이끌림을 보여 주는 한 예였는데—이 여자 앞에 커다란 유혹이 놓여 있다는 생각이었다. 그녀의 갓난아이도 사내아이였다. 자, 그녀가 두 아이를 바꿔치기하는 것이 가능하지 않을까?\n\n그는 곧, 그 생각이 공상적이며 일어날 법하지 않다고 여겨 떨쳐 버리긴 했지만—하기야 가능성을 부인할 수는 없었다—그래도 그것을 끝까지 따라가 보지 않을 수 없어서, 만약 자기가 늙은 뒤에 그런 속임수를 알아차린다면 자기 처지가 어떨지 마음속으로 그려 보았다. 그런 처지에 놓인 사람이, 그토록 오랜 세월에 걸쳐 길들고 신뢰하고 믿어 온 그 결실을, 사기꾼에게서 빼앗아 낯선 자에게 물려줄 수 있을까?\n\n그러나 이렇게 헤아려 보는 것은 부질없는 일이었다. 그런 일은 일어날 수 없었다. 그러나 잠시 뒤에 그는, 일어날 수도 있지만, 그런 여자들은 늘 감시당하므로 설령 그런 음흉한 계략을 품을 만큼 사악하다 한들 그것을 실행할 기회가 주어지지 않는다고 단정했다. 또 잠시 뒤에는, 그런 사례가 여태껏 일어난 적이 거의 없는 듯하다는 사실을 떠올렸다. 또 잠시 뒤에는, 그런 일이 실제로 일어났는데도 발각되지 않은 것은 아닐까 하고 자문했다.\n\n그 평소답지 않은 격정이 가라앉으면서 이런 불안도 차츰 녹아 사라졌지만, 그 그림자가 워낙 많이 뒤에 남은 탓에, 그는 티 내지 않으면서 자기가 직접 리처즈를 면밀히 살펴보겠다는 결심만은 굳게 지켰다. 이제 한결 편안한 마음이 된 그는, 그 여자의 신분이 오히려 유리한 사정이라고 보게 되었다. 그 신분 자체가 그녀와 아이 사이에 넓은 거리를 두어, 둘의 이별을 쉽고 자연스럽게 만들어 주기 때문이었다. 거기서부터 그는 돔비와 아들의 미래의 영광을 그려 보는 데로 넘어갔고, 당분간은 아내의 기억을, 추모의 한숨 한두 번과 함께 떨쳐 버렸다.\n\n그러는 사이에 톡스 양의 도움을 받아 칙 부인과 리처즈 사이에 조건이 비준되고 합의되었다. 그리고 리처즈는 마치 무슨 훈장이라도 받듯이 대단히 격식을 갖추어 돔비 가의 갓난아이를 떠맡았고, 자기 아이는 숱한 눈물과 입맞춤과 함께 제미마에게 넘겼다. 그러고 나서 가족의 처진 기운을 북돋우려고 포도주 잔들이 나왔다. 톡스 양은 어린 가지들에게 \"맛보기\"를 나누어 주느라 분주했는데, 어찌나 놀랍도록 신속하게 그들을 그 아버지의 직업으로 길러 냈던지, 십오 초 만에 그중 넷을 교살자로 만들어 버렸다.\n\n\"선생도 한 잔 드시겠죠, 그렇죠?\" 투들이 나타나자 톡스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다요, 마님,\" 투들이 말했다, \"권하시니 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"그리고 사랑하는 착한 아내를 이렇게 편안한 집에 두고 가게 되어 무척 기쁘시죠, 그렇죠, 선생?\" 톡스 양이 그에게 고개를 끄덕이고 슬그머니 눈짓하며 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 마님,\" 투들이 말했다. \"도로 데려가고 싶을 뿐입니다요.\"\n\n폴리는 이 말에 그 어느 때보다 더 울었다. 그래서 칙 부인은, 이런 슬픔에 빠지는 것이 어린 돔비에게 해로울지 모른다는 주부다운 걱정에서(\"그야말로 산성이 되니까요\"라고 그녀는 톡스 양에게 속삭였다), 서둘러 구원에 나섰다.\n\n\"자네 어린 아이는 자네 동생 제미마 손에서 더없이 잘 자랄 거예요, 리처즈,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"자네는 그저 노력만 하면—세상은 노력이잖아요, 알다시피, 리처즈—정말로 아주 행복해질 거예요. 상복 치수는 이미 쟀지요, 그렇죠, 리처즈?\"\n\n\"네—네, 마님,\" 폴리가 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"그리고 그게 아주 멋지게 맞을 거예요. 내가 알지요,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"그 똑같은 젊은 사람이 내 옷도 여러 벌 지어 줬으니까요. 더구나 최고의 옷감으로요!\"\n\n\"어머, 어찌나 맵시가 날는지,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"남편이 자넬 못 알아보겠어요. 그렇죠, 선생?\"\n\n\"저는 알아봅니다요,\" 투들이 퉁명스레 말했다, \"어떤 모습이든 어디서든요.\"\n\n투들은 분명 매수당할 사람이 아니었다.\n\n\"먹고 지내는 거로 말하면, 리처즈, 알다시피,\" 칙 부인이 이어 말했다, \"무엇이든 가장 좋은 것이 자네 마음대로 차려질 거예요. 매일 자기 끼니를 주문하면 되고, 무엇이든 자네가 먹고 싶은 게 있으면, 마치 자네가 귀부인이라도 되는 양 척척 마련될 거예요, 틀림없이.\"\n\n\"그래요, 물론이지요!\" 톡스 양이 깊은 동정심으로 맞장구를 치며 말했다. \"그리고 흑맥주로 말하면!—전혀 제한이 없잖아요, 그렇죠, 루이자?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요!\" 칙 부인이 같은 어조로 응수했다. \"다만 채소는, 알다시피, 여보, 조금 절제하고요.\"\n\n\"그리고 어쩌면 절임류도요,\" 톡스 양이 거들었다.\n\n\"그런 예외만 빼면,\" 루이자가 말했다, \"본인이 전적으로 자기 취향대로 고르면 되고, 아무 제약도 없을 거예요, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그리고 또, 물론, 알다시피,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"이 사람이 제 사랑스러운 어린 아이를 아무리 끔찍이 여긴다 해도—그야 분명, 루이자, 자기 아이를 끔찍이 여긴다고 자네가 나무라진 않겠지요?\"\n\n\"오, 천만에요!\" 칙 부인이 인자하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래도,\" 톡스 양이 다시 말했다, \"이 사람은 자연히 자기가 맡은 어린것에게 관심을 갖게 될 테고, 상류 계급과 연을 맺은 어린 천사가 하나의 공동 샘에서 날마다 조금씩 피어나는 걸 보는 게 특권이라고 여기게 될 거예요—그렇지 않겠어요, 루이자?\"\n\n\"의심할 여지 없이 그럼요!\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"보다시피, 얘야, 이 사람은 벌써 아주 만족하고 편안해져서, 가벼운 마음으로 미소 띤 채 동생 제미마와 어린 귀염둥이들과 착하고 정직한 남편에게 작별 인사를 하려는 참이잖아요. 그렇죠, 자네?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요!\" 톡스 양이 외쳤다. \"물론 그렇고말고요!\"\n\n그렇지만 그럼에도 불구하고, 가엾은 폴리는 큰 고통 속에서 모두를 차례로 끌어안았고, 마지막으로 남편에게 다가가서는 그와 헤어질 결심이 도무지 서지 않았다. 결국 남편이 다음과 같은 비유적인 위로의 말을 마치며 부드럽게 그녀를 떼어 놓고 나서야 헤어졌다.\n\n\"폴리, 여보, 무슨 일이 있든, 내 사랑, 고개는 들고 낮게 싸우라고. 그게 사람을 살면서 헤쳐 나가게 해 주는, 내가 아는 유일한 규칙이야. 당신은 늘 고개를 들고 낮게 싸워 왔지, 폴리. 지금도 그렇게 해, 안 그러면 브릭스도 더는 안 그럴 테니까. 신의 가호가 있기를, 폴리! 나랑 제미마가 당신 몫의 의무를 대신해 줄게. 그리고 당신 일에 관해선, 고개를 들고 낮게 싸우라고, 폴리, 그러면 절대 그르칠 일이 없어!\"\n\n이 황금 같은 비결에 힘을 얻은 폴리는, 마침내 자기와 아이들 사이에 더 구구한 작별이 벌어지는 것을 피하려고 달아나듯 빠져나갔다. 그러나 그 책략은 받아 마땅한 만큼 잘 들어맞지는 못했다. 막내 바로 위의 사내아이가 그녀의 의도를 알아채고는, 곧장 팔다리로 기어 그녀를 쫓아 계단을 위로 떼 지어 올라가기 시작했으니—그 어원이 미심쩍은 표현이 허용된다면 말이다—한편 맏이(증기 기관을 기념하여 집안에서 바일러라 불리는)는 슬픔을 드러내며 부츠로 악마 같은 북장단을 두드려 댔고, 거기에 나머지 식구들도 가세했다.\n\n오렌지와 반 페니짜리 동전을 어린 투들들 하나하나에게 닥치는 대로 안겨 주자, 그들의 격렬한 서러움도 일단 누그러졌다. 그리고 그 목적으로 대기시켜 둔 삯마차에 실려, 식구들은 곧 자기네 집으로 옮겨졌다. 아이들은 제미마의 보호 아래 창문을 틀어막고서, 길을 가는 내내 오렌지와 반 페니짜리 동전을 떨어뜨렸다. 투들 씨 본인은, 그것이 자기가 가장 익숙한 이동 방식인지라, 뒤쪽의 쇠창살 사이에 앉아 가는 편을 택했다.\n\n돔비 씨가 한 사람의 인간이자 아버지로서, 가정 부서의 수장으로 모습을 드러내는 장\n\n고인이 된 부인의 장례식이 장의사를 더없이 만족시키며 \"치러졌고\", 또한 이런 일에는 흔히 트집을 잡기 일쑤이며 의식의 빠진 점이나 부족한 점에 곧잘 기분 상하기 쉬운 인근 사람들 모두를 두루 만족시키며 치러진 뒤, 돔비 씨 집안의 여러 식구들은 가정이라는 체계 속 저마다의 자리로 가라앉아 들어갔다. 바깥세상이라는 큰 세계가 그러하듯, 그 작은 세계 역시 죽은 이를 쉽게 잊어버리는 능력을 지니고 있었다. 요리사가 조용한 성품의 부인이셨다고 말하고, 가정부가 그게 사람의 공통된 운명이라고 말하고, 집사가 누가 그럴 줄 알았겠느냐고 말하고, 하녀가 도무지 믿기지가 않는다고 말하고, 하인이 영락없이 꿈만 같다고 말하고 나자, 이들은 그 화제를 완전히 닳아 없애 버렸고, 자기들의 상복 또한 이제 낡아빠져 가고 있다는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n위층에 명예로운 포로 신세로 자리 잡은 리처즈에게는, 새로운 삶의 여명이 차갑고 잿빛으로 밝아 오는 듯했다. 돔비 씨의 집은 큰 집으로, 포틀랜드 플레이스와 브라이언스톤 스퀘어 사이 지역에 있는, 키 높고 어둡고 지독하게 점잖은 거리의 응달 쪽에 있었다. 모퉁이 집이었는데, 넓디넓은 앞마당이 딸려 있어 그 안에는 창살 박힌 창문들이 노려보고, 쓰레기통으로 이어지는 비뚤어진 눈매의 문들이 흘끔거리는 지하 저장고들이 있었다. 음울한 위세를 풍기는 집으로, 뒤쪽이 둥글게 돌아간 형태였고, 거기에는 자갈 깔린 안마당을 굽어보는 일련의 응접실 전체가 들어 있었다. 그 안마당에는 줄기와 가지가 시커멓게 그을린 앙상한 나무 두 그루가 서 있었는데, 잎이 어찌나 연기에 그을려 말라붙었던지 살랑거린다기보다 달그락거렸다. 여름 햇살이 그 거리에 드는 일은 결코 없었다. 다만 아침 식사 무렵, 물수레와 헌옷 장수와 제라늄 파는 사람들과 우산 수선공과, 그리고 길을 가며 네덜란드제 시계의 작은 종을 딸랑딸랑 울리는 사내와 함께 햇살이 찾아올 때를 빼고는. 그러나 그 햇살도 이내 사라져 그날은 다시 돌아오지 않았다. 그리고 그 뒤를 따라가던 악대며 띄엄띄엄 이어지는 펀치 인형극이 떠나고 나면, 거리는 더없이 음산한 손풍금과 흰 쥐들의 먹잇감으로 남겨졌다. 이따금 여흥에 변화를 주려는 듯 호저가 한 마리 나타나기도 했다. 그러다가 가족이 외식하러 나간 집사들이 땅거미 속에서 현관문 앞에 서 있기 시작하고, 가스등 켜는 사람이 거리를 가스 불빛으로 환히 밝히려다 밤마다 실패하는 시각이 올 때까지 그러했다.\n\n집은 바깥만큼이나 안도 휑뎅그렁했다. 장례가 끝나자 돔비 씨는 가구에 덮개를 씌우라고—아마도 자기의 모든 계획이 결부된 아들을 위해 보존해 두려는 것이었으리라—그리고 자기가 일층에서 쓰려고 남겨 둔 방들을 제외한 모든 방의 장식을 거두어 내라고 명했다. 그리하여 탁자와 의자들은 방 한가운데 무더기로 쌓인 채 커다란 수의 같은 천으로 덮여 신비로운 형상을 이루었다. 종 손잡이며 창문 블라인드며 거울들은 일간지와 주간지로 발라 싸여, 죽음과 끔찍한 살인 사건에 관한 단편적인 기사들을 단편적으로 들이밀었다. 모든 샹들리에며 장식 조명은 무명천에 싸여, 천장의 눈에서 늘어진 한 방울의 괴물 같은 눈물처럼 보였다. 굴뚝에서는 지하 납골당이나 축축한 곳에서 나는 듯한 냄새가 새어 나왔다. 죽어 묻힌 부인은 섬뜩한 붕대로 된 액자 틀 속에서 무시무시했다. 바람이 한 차례 일 때마다 이웃 마구간 골목 모퉁이를 돌아 소용돌이치며, 부인이 앓을 때 집 앞에 깔아 두었던 짚 부스러기를 실어 왔는데, 곰팡이 슨 그 잔해는 여전히 인근에 들러붙어 있었다. 그리고 그것들은 언제나 어떤 보이지 않는 인력에 이끌려, 바로 맞은편 즉시 세놓는다는 더러운 집의 문지방으로 끌려가서는, 돔비 씨의 창문들을 향해 음울한 웅변을 늘어놓았다.\n\n돔비 씨가 자기 거처로 남겨 둔 방들은 현관에서 들어갈 수 있었는데, 거실 하나와 서재 하나가 있었다. 서재라고는 하나 실은 옷 갈아입는 방이어서, 다리미질한 종이와 모조 양피지와 모로코가죽과 러시아가죽의 냄새가 그 안에서 여러 켤레의 부츠 냄새와 다투었다. 그리고 그 너머에는 일종의 온실, 또는 작은 유리 아침식사용 방이 있었는데, 앞서 말한 나무들과, 대체로 어슬렁거리는 고양이 몇 마리가 내다보이는 전망을 갖추고 있었다. 이 세 방은 서로 통해 있었다. 아침에 돔비 씨가 앞서 말한 두 방 가운데 어느 한 곳에서 아침을 들 때, 또 오후에 그가 만찬을 들러 집에 돌아왔을 때, 종이 울려 리처즈를 이 유리방으로 불러들였고, 거기서 그녀는 어린 도련님을 안고 이리저리 거닐었다. 이런 때에 어두컴컴한 저편에 앉아, 어둡고 묵직한 가구들 사이에서 갓난아이 쪽을 바라보는 돔비 씨를 언뜻언뜻 보게 되면서—그 집은 그의 부친이 여러 해 살았던 집이라 많은 비품들이 구식이고 음산했다—리처즈는 그 외로운 처지의 그에 대해, 마치 그가 감방에 홀로 갇힌 죄수이거나, 말을 걸 수도 이해할 수도 없는 기이한 유령이기라도 한 듯한 생각을 품기 시작했다. 며칠이 지나자 돔비 씨는, 그녀의 소박한 생각 속에서, 그 자신의 일신에 그 집의 온갖 신비와 음울함을 두른 존재가 되었다. 유리방을 이리저리 거닐거나, 거기서 갓난아이를 어르며 앉아 있을 때—땅거미가 질 무렵에도 종종 몇 시간씩 그렇게 하곤 했는데—그녀는 이따금 저편의 어둠을 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰며, 그가 어떤 표정을 짓고 있는지, 무엇을 하고 있는지 알아내려 했다. 그러나 자기가 그에게 훤히 보인다는 것을 알고 있었기에, 그쪽을 엿보려는 시도는 결코 감히 하지 못했고, 다만 아주 몰래, 한 번에 한순간씩만 곁눈질했을 뿐이다. 따라서 그녀는 아무것도 알아내지 못했고, 자기 소굴 속의 돔비 씨는 여전히 하나의 그림자에 머물러 있었다.\n\n어린 폴 돔비의 유모는 몇 주 동안 이런 생활을 몸소 해 왔고, 어린 폴을 안고 그 생활을 헤쳐 왔다. 그러던 어느 날 그녀는 음산한 위세의 방들을 거니는 우울한 산책을 마치고 위층으로 돌아온 참이었다(그녀는 칙 부인 없이는 결코 외출하지 않았는데, 칙 부인은 날 좋은 아침이면, 대개 톡스 양을 대동하고 찾아와 그녀와 아기를 바람 쐬러 데리고 나갔다—다시 말해, 걸어가는 장례 행렬처럼 보도를 따라 엄숙하게 이리저리 행진시켰다). 그녀가 자기 방에 앉아 있는데, 문이 천천히 그리고 조용히 열리더니, 검은 눈의 어린 소녀가 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"이모님 댁에서 돌아오신 플로렌스 아가씨인 게 분명하군,\" 그 아이를 한 번도 본 적 없는 리처즈는 생각했다. \"안녕하셨어요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"저게 제 동생인가요?\" 아이가 아기를 가리키며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 예쁜 아가씨,\" 리처즈가 대답했다. \"이리 와서 입맞춰 주세요.\"\n\n그러나 아이는 다가오기는커녕 그녀의 얼굴을 진지하게 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"우리 엄마를 어떻게 한 거예요?\"\n\n\"세상에 가엾은 것 같으니!\" 리처즈가 외쳤다. \"어쩜 그렇게 슬픈 질문을! 제가요? 아무것도 안 했답니다, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"우리 엄마를 어떻게 한 거예요?\" 아이가 똑같은 표정과 똑같은 태도로 다시 물었다.\n\n\"내 평생 이렇게 가슴 아픈 일은 본 적이 없네!\" 리처즈가 말했다. 그녀는 자연스레 이 아이 자리에 자기 아이 하나를 놓고, 같은 처지에서 자기를 찾아 묻는 모습을 떠올렸던 것이다. \"이리 가까이 와요, 가엾은 아가씨! 저를 무서워하지 마세요.\"\n\n\"전 당신이 무섭지 않아요,\" 아이가 가까이 다가오며 말했다. \"하지만 우리 엄마를 어떻게 했는지 알고 싶어요.\"\n\n그 여인 앞에 서서 그녀의 눈을 들여다보는 동안, 아이의 가슴이 어찌나 벅차올랐던지, 아이는 작은 손을 제 가슴에 갖다 대어 거기 누르고 있어야만 했다. 그러나 아이에게는 어떤 결연한 뜻이 있어서, 그 가냘픈 몸도, 캐묻는 듯한 눈길도 흔들리지 않게 해 주었다.\n\n\"우리 아가야,\" 리처즈가 말했다, \"그렇게 예쁜 검은 옷을 입은 건 엄마를 기억하기 위해서랍니다.\"\n\n\"전 우리 엄마를 기억할 수 있어요,\" 아이가 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 대답했다, \"어떤 옷을 입어도요.\"\n\n\"하지만 사람들은 떠나간 이들을 기억하려고 검은 옷을 입는답니다.\"\n\n\"어디로 떠났는데요?\" 아이가 물었다.\n\n\"이리 와서 제 곁에 앉아요,\" 리처즈가 말했다, \"그럼 이야기를 하나 들려드릴게요.\"\n\n자기가 물어본 것에 관한 이야기일 거라고 재빨리 알아챈 어린 플로렌스는, 그때까지 손에 들고 있던 보닛을 옆에 내려놓고, 유모의 발치 걸상에 앉아 그녀의 얼굴을 올려다보았다.\n\n\"옛날 옛적에,\" 리처즈가 말했다, \"한 부인이 있었어요—아주 착한 부인이었는데, 그 어린 딸이 부인을 무척이나 사랑했답니다.\"\n\n\"아주 착한 부인이었는데 그 어린 딸이 부인을 무척이나 사랑했어요,\" 아이가 따라 했다.\n\n\"그런데 하느님께서 그렇게 되는 게 옳다고 생각하셨을 때, 부인은 병에 걸려 돌아가셨답니다.\"\n\n아이가 몸서리쳤다.\n\n\"돌아가셔서, 이 세상 누구에게도 다시는 보이지 않게 되었고, 나무들이 자라는 땅에 묻혔어요.\"\n\n\"차가운 땅에요?\" 아이가 또 한 번 몸서리치며 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요! 따뜻한 땅이랍니다,\" 폴리가 기회를 붙잡으며 대답했다, \"못생긴 작은 씨앗들이 아름다운 꽃으로, 그리고 풀로, 곡식으로, 그밖에 뭐든 다 되는 그런 땅이지요. 착한 사람들이 빛나는 천사로 변해서 하늘나라로 날아가는 곳이랍니다!\"\n\n고개를 떨구고 있던 아이가 다시 고개를 들고는, 앉아서 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"자, 그러니까,\" 폴리가 말했다. 이 진지한 응시와, 아이를 위로하려는 마음과, 뜻밖의 성공과, 자기 능력에 대한 미미하기 짝이 없는 자신감 사이에서 적잖이 당황한 채였다. \"그래서 이 부인이 돌아가셨을 때, 사람들이 부인을 어디로 모셔 갔든, 어디에 모셨든, 부인은 하느님께로 갔답니다! 그리고 이 부인은 하느님께 기도드렸어요,\" 폴리가 진심으로 간절했던 터라 한없이 감격에 겨워 말했다, \"어린 딸이 그것을 마음속에 확실히 믿도록 가르쳐 달라고요. 그리고 엄마가 거기서 행복하며 여전히 딸을 사랑하고 있다는 걸 딸이 알게 해 달라고요. 그리고 언젠가 그곳에서 엄마를 만나, 다시는, 다시는, 다시는 헤어지지 않게 되기를—오, 평생토록—바라고 또 애쓰게 해 달라고요.\"\n\n\"우리 엄마였어요!\" 아이가 벌떡 일어나 그녀의 목을 끌어안으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 그 아이의 마음은,\" 폴리가 아이를 자기 가슴으로 끌어당기며 말했다, \"그 어린 딸의 마음은 이 진실로 어찌나 가득 찼던지, 그 이야기를 제대로 들려줄 줄도 모르고 그저 자신도 가엾은 한 어머니일 뿐인 낯선 유모에게서 들었을 때조차, 거기서 위안을 찾았답니다—그렇게 외롭지 않게 느껴졌고요—그 유모의 가슴에 기대어 흐느껴 울었지요—제 무릎에 누운 갓난아기에게 다정하게 마음을 주었어요—그리고—그래, 그래, 그래!\" 폴리가 아이의 곱슬머리를 쓰다듬으며 그 위에 눈물을 떨구며 말했다. \"그래, 가엾은 것!\"\n\n\"어머나, 플로이 아가씨! 아버님께서 또 노여워하시지 않겠어요!\" 문가에서 빠른 목소리가 외쳤다. 작달막하고 까무잡잡하며 어른티 나는 열네 살 소녀의 목소리였는데, 들창코에다 흑옥 구슬 같은 검은 눈을 하고 있었다. \"유모를 귀찮게 하러 가면 안 된다고 그렇게 특별히 일러두었는데.\"\n\n\"전혀 귀찮지 않은데요,\" 폴리가 놀라 대꾸했다. \"전 아이들을 무척 좋아한답니다.\"\n\n\"어머! 그래도 죄송한 말씀이지만, 리처즈 부인, 그건 상관없는 일이잖아요,\" 검은 눈의 소녀가 대답했다. 어찌나 지독하게 날카롭고 톡 쏘는지 듣는 이의 눈에서 눈물이 핑 돌 정도였다. \"제가 경단고둥을 무척 좋아할 수도 있지요, 리처즈 부인, 그렇다고 차 마실 때마다 그걸 먹어야 한다는 법은 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 상관없어요,\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 고맙기도 해라, 리처즈 부인, 상관없으시다니!\" 날카로운 소녀가 대꾸했다. \"그래도 죄송하지만 기억해 주세요. 플로이 아가씨는 제 담당이고, 폴 도련님은 부인 담당이라는 걸요.\"\n\n\"그래도 우리가 다툴 필요는 없잖아요,\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"어머 그럼요, 리처즈 부인,\" 스핏파이어가 응수했다. \"전혀요, 저도 그러길 바라지 않아요, 그렇게 따지고 들 것까진 없죠, 플로이 아가씨는 붙박이고 폴 도련님은 임시니까요.\" 스핏파이어는 쉼표만큼의 멈춤밖에 쓰지 않았다. 할 말이 무엇이든 가능하면 한 문장에, 한 호흡에 쏘아 내뱉었다.\n\n\"플로렌스 아가씨가 막 집에 돌아오신 거죠, 그렇죠?\" 폴리가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 리처즈 부인, 막 오셨어요, 그런데 플로이 아가씨, 집에 들어온 지 십오 분도 안 됐는데, 부인이 아가씨 어머님 때문에 입고 계신 그 비싼 상복에다 젖은 얼굴을 비벼 대고 계시네요!\" 이렇게 나무라면서, 본명이 수전 닙퍼인 어린 스핏파이어는 아이를 새 친구에게서 홱 떼어 냈다—마치 이를 뽑아내듯이. 그러나 그것은 어떤 의도적인 매정함에서라기보다는, 자기 직무를 지나치게 날카롭게 수행하느라 그런 것 같았다.\n\n\"이제 다시 집에 돌아오셨으니 아주 행복해지실 거예요,\" 폴리가 자기 건강한 얼굴에 격려의 미소를 띠며 그녀에게 고개를 끄덕여 보였다, \"그리고 오늘 밤 사랑하는 아버님을 뵙게 되면 무척 기뻐하시겠지요.\"\n\n\"세상에, 리처즈 부인!\" 닙퍼 양이 그 말을 홱 받아채며 외쳤다. \"그러지 마세요. 사랑하는 아버님이라니요! 아가씨가 그러시는 꼴을 한번 보고 싶네요!\"\n\n\"그럼 안 그러신다고요?\" 폴리가 물었다.\n\n\"세상에, 리처즈 부인, 그럼요, 아가씨 아버님은 딴 사람한테 너무 푹 빠져 계신 데다, 푹 빠질 딴 사람이 생기기 전에도 아가씨는 결코 귀염받은 적이 없어요. 이 집에선 여자아이들은 내버려져요, 리처즈 부인, 정말이라니까요.\"\n\n아이는 한 유모에게서 다른 유모에게로 재빨리 눈길을 옮겼다. 마치 오가는 말을 알아듣고 느끼기라도 하는 듯이.\n\n\"놀랍네요!\" 폴리가 외쳤다. \"돔비 씨께서 그 후로 아가씨를 못 보셨어요—\"\n\n\"못 보셨죠,\" 수전 닙퍼가 가로막았다. \"그 후로 한 번도요, 그리고 그 전에도 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 아가씨한테 거의 눈길도 안 주셨어요, 제 생각엔 길거리에서 마주쳤어도 자기 자식인 줄 못 알아보셨을 거예요, 내일 길거리에서 마주친대도 자기 자식인 줄 못 알아보실걸요, 리처즈 부인, 저로 말하자면,\" 스핏파이어가 키득 웃으며 말했다, \"제가 이 세상에 있는 줄이나 아실지 의심스럽다니까요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 것!\" 리처즈가 말했다. 닙퍼 양이 아니라 어린 플로렌스를 두고 한 말이었다.\n\n\"어머! 지금 우리가 이야기 나누는 데서 백 마일 안에 폭군 하나가 있다는 거, 제가 말씀드릴 수 있어요, 리처즈 부인, 물론 여기 계신 분들은 언제나 예외고요,\" 수전 닙퍼가 말했다. \"그럼 안녕히 계세요, 리처즈 부인, 자 플로이 아가씨, 저랑 같이 가요, 그리고 본보기로 삼아도 소용없는 못되고 고약한 아이처럼 그렇게 뒤로 빼지 마세요, 어서요!\"\n\n이렇게 다그침을 받고, 또 수전 닙퍼가 오른쪽 어깨가 빠지도록 잡아당기는데도 불구하고, 어린 플로렌스는 뿌리치고 나와 새 친구에게 애정 어린 입맞춤을 했다.\n\n\"어머나! 리처즈 부인을 함부로 대하면 안 된다고 그렇게 특별히 일러두었는데!\" 수전이 외쳤다. \"아주 좋아요, 플로이 아가씨!\"\n\n\"하느님께서 이 사랑스러운 아이를 축복하시길!\" 리처즈가 말했다. \"잘 가요, 아가야!\"\n\n\"안녕히 계세요!\" 아이가 대답했다. \"하느님이 당신을 축복하시길! 곧 또 보러 올게요, 당신도 저를 보러 올 거죠? 수전이 그렇게 해 줄 거예요. 그렇지, 수전?\"\n\n스핏파이어는, 어린 사고력을 기르는 사람들 가운데 어린 시절이란 돈처럼 반짝반짝하게 유지하려면 많이 흔들고 달그락거리고 떠밀어 대야 한다고 믿는 그런 학파의 제자이긴 했지만, 대체로 마음씨 고운 작은 사람인 듯했다. 이렇게 몇 가지 다정한 몸짓과 어루만짐으로 호소를 받자, 그녀는 작은 두 팔을 팔짱 끼고 고개를 흔들며, 한껏 크게 뜬 검은 눈에 누그러지는 빛을 띠었던 것이다.\n\n\"아가씨가 그걸 청하시는 건 옳지 않아요, 플로이 아가씨, 제가 아가씨한테 거절을 못 한다는 걸 아시면서, 하지만 리처즈 부인이 좋으시다면 리처즈 부인이랑 저랑 어떻게 할 수 있을지 알아볼게요, 보세요, 저야 차이나로 항해를 떠나고 싶을 수도 있지만, 런던 부두를 어떻게 벗어나야 할지 모를 수도 있는 거잖아요, 리처즈 부인.\"\n\n리처즈는 그 말에 동의했다.\n\n\"이 집이 그렇게까지 흥겨운 놀이로 떠들썩한 것도 아니니,\" 닙퍼 양이 말했다, \"굳이 필요 이상으로 외로울 까닭은 없죠. 댁의 톡스 양이며 칙 부인이며 하는 분들이 제 앞니 두 개를 뽑아 갈 수는 있어도, 그렇다고 제가 한 벌 다 내드려야 할 이유는 없으니까요, 리처즈 부인.\"\n\n이 말 역시 리처즈는 당연한 것으로 받아들여 동의했다.\n\n\"그러니 저야 기꺼이,\" 수전 닙퍼가 말했다, \"폴 도련님이 붙박이로 계시는 동안에는 다정하게 지낼 마음이에요, 리처즈 부인, 대놓고 분부를 어기지 않으면서 방도를 짜낼 수만 있다면요, 그런데 아이고 세상에 플로이 아가씨, 아직 옷도 안 벗으셨네요, 이 말 안 듣는 아이 같으니, 아직도, 어서 와요!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 수전 닙퍼는 강제의 발작에 휩싸여 어린 담당 아가씨에게 돌격하더니 아이를 휩쓸 듯 방 밖으로 데리고 나갔다.\n\n슬픔과 방치 속에서도 아이는 어찌나 온순하고, 어찌나 조용하며, 불평이 없던지. 또 아무도 가지려 하지 않는 듯한 애정을 그토록 많이 지녔고, 아무도 그것이 상처받든 말든 마음 쓰거나 생각조차 하지 않는 듯한 슬픈 분별력을 그토록 많이 지녔던지, 다시 혼자 남게 되자 폴리의 마음이 쓰라렸다. 자기와 그 어미 없는 어린 소녀 사이에 오간 그 소박한 일을 통해, 폴리 자신의 어머니다운 마음도 아이의 마음 못지않게 감동받았던 것이다. 그리고 그녀는 아이가 그렇게 느끼듯이, 그 순간부터 둘 사이에 어떤 신뢰와 관심이 자리 잡았음을 느꼈다.\n\n투들 씨가 폴리를 크게 신뢰하긴 했지만, 인위적인 교양이라는 점에서 보자면 그녀는 어쩌면 그보다 별로 나을 것이 없었다. 그녀는 평생 살아가기 위해 싹싹하게 일하고 고되게 애써 왔으며, 푸줏간과 빵집에 관한, 그리고 페니를 파딩으로 나누는 일에 관한 실제적인 생각을 지닌, 진지하고 착실한 사람이었다. 그러나 그녀는, 모든 다정함과 연민, 자기희생과 헌신을 한결 더 잘 느끼고, 그것을 훨씬 더 한결같이 간직하는 점에서, 통틀어 보면 언제나 남자의 천성보다 더 낫고, 더 진실하며, 더 높고, 더 고귀한 어떤 천성의 훌륭하고 소박한 본보기였다. 그리고 어쩌면, 못 배운 처지였음에도, 그녀는 바로 그 이른 날에 어떤 동트는 깨달음을 돔비 씨에게 가져다줄 수도 있었으리라—그랬더라면 그것이 끝내 벼락처럼 그를 내리치지는 않았을 그런 깨달음을.\n\n그러나 이것은 본래 이야기에서 벗어난 말이다. 그때 폴리가 생각한 것은 다만, 닙퍼 양을 성공적으로 달랜 일을 더 발전시키는 것과, 어린 플로렌스가 적법하게, 반항을 사지 않고 자기를 돕게 할 어떤 방도를 궁리하는 것뿐이었다. 그런 기회가 바로 그날 밤 마침 찾아왔다.\n\n여느 때처럼 종이 울려 유리방으로 불려 내려간 그녀는, 아기를 품에 안고 그곳을 오래도록 이리저리 거닐고 있었다. 그러던 차에, 그녀가 처음에는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 있다가, 나중에는 가운데 방을 오락가락 거닐며 그때마다 열린 접이문 쪽으로 조금씩 더 가까이 다가오는 듯하던 돔비 씨가—그녀를 몹시 놀라고 당황하게 하며—갑자기 나오더니 그녀 앞에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 리처즈.\"\n\n첫날 그녀에게 비쳤던 것과 똑같은, 근엄하고 뻣뻣한 신사였다. 어찌나 험상궂은 인상의 신사였던지, 그녀는 저도 모르게 눈길을 떨구는 동시에 절을 했다.\n\n\"폴 도련님은 어떻소, 리처즈?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 자라고 계세요, 나리, 건강하시고요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 보이는군,\" 돔비 씨가 그녀가 살펴보라고 들춰 보인 자그마한 얼굴을 큰 관심으로 흘끗 보면서, 그러면서도 그것에 반쯤 무심한 척하며 말했다. \"필요한 건 다 챙겨 주는 모양이지?\"\n\n\"네, 그럼요, 감사합니다, 나리.\"\n\n그런데 그녀가 갑자기 이 대답에 너무도 뻔한 망설임을 덧붙이는 바람에, 이미 돌아섰던 돔비 씨가 걸음을 멈추고 다시 돌아서며 캐묻는 듯한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"외람되오나, 나리, 도련님이 사물에 무척 주의를 기울이는 성미라서요,\" 리처즈가 다시 한번 절하며 말했다, \"그런데—위층이 도련님에게는 좀 따분할지도 모르겠어요, 나리.\"\n\n\"항상 자네를 데리고 바람 쐬러 나가라고 일러두었는데,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"좋아! 더 자주 나가도록 하게. 그 점을 말해 준 건 아주 잘한 일이야.\"\n\n\"송구하옵니다, 나리,\" 폴리가 우물거렸다, \"그래도 저희는 충분히 자주 나가고 있어요, 나리, 감사합니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 무엇을 바라는 건가?\" 돔비 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"실은 나리, 저도 정확히는 모르겠어요,\" 폴리가 말했다, \"다만—\"\n\n\"다만?\"\n\n\"아이들을 생기 있고 명랑하게 만드는 데는, 다른 아이들이 곁에서 노는 걸 보는 것만 한 게 없는 듯해요, 나리,\" 폴리가 용기를 내어 말했다.\n\n\"자네가 여기 왔을 때 내가 일러두었던 것 같은데, 리처즈,\" 돔비 씨가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다, \"자네 가족과는 되도록 적게 만나기를 바란다고 말이야.\"\n\n\"어머나 그럼요, 나리, 그걸 두고 한 말은 결코 아니었어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다니 다행이군,\" 돔비 씨가 서둘러 말했다. \"원한다면 산책을 계속하게.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 안쪽 방으로 사라졌다. 그리고 폴리는, 그가 자기 의도를 완전히 잘못 알아들었다는 것과, 자기 목적은 조금도 진전시키지 못한 채 미움만 샀다는 것을 느끼는 만족을 누렸다.\n\n이튿날 밤, 그녀가 내려가 보니 그가 온실을 거닐고 있었다. 이 흔치 않은 광경에 멈칫하여 문가에 멈춰 선 채, 나아가야 할지 물러서야 할지 망설이고 있는데, 그가 그녀를 안으로 불러들였다. 그의 마음은, 곧 드러났듯이, 너무도 돔비와 아들에 쏠려 있어서 그녀의 제안을 잊을 여지가 없었던 것이다.\n\n\"그런 또래들과 어울리는 게 정말 아이에게 좋다고 생각한다면,\" 그가, 마치 그녀가 그것을 제안한 이후로 시간이 전혀 흐르지 않았다는 듯, 날카롭게 말했다, \"플로렌스 아가씨는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"플로렌스 아가씨보다 더 좋은 분은 없을 거예요, 나리,\" 폴리가 간절히 말했다, \"그런데 아가씨의 시중드는 아이한테서 듣기로는 그래선 안 된다고—\"\n\n돔비 씨는 종을 울리고, 그것에 응답이 올 때까지 거닐었다.\n\n\"플로렌스 아가씨가 원할 때면 언제든 리처즈와 함께 있고, 함께 외출하고, 그렇게 하도록 늘 일러두게. 리처즈가 바랄 때면 아이들이 함께 있도록 하라고 일러두게.\"\n\n이제 쇠가 달구어졌고, 리처즈는 그것을 과감히 내리쳤다—옳은 일이었기에 그녀는 그 일에 담대했다, 비록 본능적으로 돔비 씨가 두렵기는 했지만—해서 플로렌스 아가씨를 지금 당장 이리로 내려보내, 어린 동생과 친해지게 해 달라고 청했다.\n\n하인이 이 심부름을 하러 물러가는 동안, 그녀는 아기를 어르는 척했다. 그러나 그녀는 돔비 씨의 안색이 변하는 것을 보았다고 생각했다. 그의 얼굴 표정이 완전히 달라지는 것을, 그가 자기가 한 말을, 또는 그녀가 한 말을, 또는 둘 다를 번복하려는 듯 황급히 돌아서다가, 오직 부끄러움 때문에 그만두는 것을 보았다고 생각했다.\n\n그녀의 생각은 옳았다. 자기가 박대한 아이를 마지막으로 보았을 때, 아이와 죽어 가는 어미 사이의 그 슬픈 포옹 속에는, 그에게 하나의 계시인 동시에 하나의 책망이 되는 무언가가 있었다. 그가 그토록 큰 희망을 거는 아들에게 아무리 몰두한다 한들, 그는 그 마지막 장면을 잊을 수 없었다. 자기가 거기서 아무 몫도 차지하지 못했다는 것을 잊을 수 없었다. 그 다정함과 진실의 맑은 심연 밑바닥에는 서로의 품에 꼭 안긴 두 사람의 모습이 놓여 있었고, 한편 그는 그 위쪽 둑에 서서, 한낱 구경꾼으로—그들과 함께하는 자가 아니라—아래를 내려다보며, 완전히 차단되어 있었다는 것을.\n\n이런 것들을 기억에서 몰아내지도, 또 그것들이 잔뜩 품고 있던 의미의 불완전한 형상들—그의 자만이라는 안개를 뚫고 그에게 보이게 될 만큼의 형상들—로부터 마음을 자유롭게 지키지도 못한 채, 어린 플로렌스를 향한 그의 이전의 무관심한 감정은 비상한 종류의 불안으로 바뀌었다. 그녀는 그토록 어렸고, 그의 눈을 제외한 누구의 눈에나(어쩌면 그의 눈에도) 보통 아이보다도 더 많은 어린아이다운 순진함과 신뢰를 지니고 있었음에도, 그는 거의 그녀가 자기를 지켜보며 의심하는 것처럼 느꼈다. 마치 그녀가, 그 자신조차 거의 알지 못하는 어떤 성질의, 그의 가슴속 비밀스러운 무언가의 실마리를 쥐고 있는 것처럼. 마치 그녀가 그의 내면에 있는 어떤 거슬리고 불협화한 현을 타고난 듯이 알고 있어서, 그녀의 숨결만으로도 그것을 울릴 수 있는 것처럼.\n\n아이에 대한 그의 감정은 그녀가 태어난 때부터 부정적인 것이었다. 그가 그녀에게 혐오를 품은 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그럴 만한 가치도, 그럴 기분도 그에게는 없었으니까. 그녀가 그에게 적극적으로 불쾌한 대상이었던 적도 없었다. 그러나 이제 그는 그녀를 두고 마음이 편치 않았다. 그녀가 그의 평온을 어지럽혔다. 방법만 알았더라면 그는 차라리 그녀에 대한 생각 자체를 아예 치워 버리고 싶었으리라. 어쩌면—이런 신비를 누가 판가름할 수 있으랴!—그는 자기가 그녀를 미워하게 될까 봐 두려웠는지도 모른다.\n\n어린 플로렌스가 머뭇머뭇 모습을 드러내자, 돔비 씨는 왔다 갔다 하던 걸음을 멈추고 그녀 쪽을 바라보았다. 만일 그가 더 큰 관심을 가지고, 아버지의 눈으로 바라보았더라면, 그녀의 그 간절한 눈길 속에서, 그녀를 망설이게 하는 충동과 두려움을 읽어 냈을지도 모른다. 그의 품에 얼굴을 묻으며 매달려, \"오 아버지, 절 사랑하려고 애써 주세요! 제겐 아무도 없어요!\" 하고 울부짖으며 그에게 달려가 매달리고 싶은 열렬한 갈망을. 퇴짜 맞을지 모른다는 두려움을. 너무 대담했다가 그의 기분을 상하게 할까 하는 겁을. 어떤 다짐과 격려가 절실히 필요한 그 가련한 처지를. 그리고 그녀의 벅찬 어린 가슴이 제 슬픔과 애정을 위해 어떤 자연스러운 안식처를 찾아 헤매고 있다는 것을.\n\n그러나 그는 이 가운데 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그는 그녀가 문가에서 우유부단하게 멈춰 서서 자기 쪽을 바라보는 것을 보았다. 그리고 그 이상은 아무것도 보지 못했다.\n\n\"들어오너라,\" 그가 말했다, \"들어와, 이 아이는 뭘 그리 무서워하는 거냐?\"\n\n그녀가 들어왔다. 그러고는 잠시 막연한 표정으로 주위를 둘러본 뒤, 작은 두 손을 꼭 맞잡은 채, 문 바로 안쪽에 서 있었다.\n\n\"이리 오너라, 플로렌스,\" 아버지가 차갑게 말했다. \"내가 누군지 아느냐?\"\n\n\"네, 아빠.\"\n\n\"내게 할 말이 없느냐?\"\n\n빠르게 그의 얼굴로 들어 올린 그녀의 눈에 고였던 눈물은, 그 얼굴이 띤 표정에 얼어붙고 말았다. 그녀는 다시 아래를 내려다보고, 떨리는 손을 내밀었다.\n\n돔비 씨는 그것을 제 손에 헐겁게 잡고, 잠시 그녀를 내려다보고 서 있었다. 마치 무슨 말을 하거나 어떻게 해야 할지를 그 아이만큼이나 모르는 듯이.\n\n\"자! 착하게 굴어라,\" 그가 그녀의 머리를 토닥이며, 어딘가 어수선하고 미심쩍은 눈길로 슬그머니 그녀를 살피듯 바라보며 말했다. \"리처즈에게 가거라! 가!\"\n\n그의 어린 딸은 한순간 더 머뭇거렸다. 마치 여전히 그에게 매달리고 싶기라도 한 듯, 또는 그가 자기를 두 팔로 안아 올려 입맞춰 주리라는 한 가닥 미련이 남아 있기라도 한 듯이. 그녀는 다시 한번 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그는 그때 그녀의 표정이, 그날 밤 박사 쪽을 돌아보았을 때의 표정과 어찌나 닮았던가를 떠올렸고—저도 모르게 그녀의 손을 놓으며 돌아섰다.\n\n플로렌스가 아버지 앞에서 몹시 불리한 처지에 있다는 것은 알아채기 어렵지 않았다. 그것은 아이의 마음을 옥죌 뿐 아니라, 그녀의 동작이 지닌 타고난 우아함과 자유로움마저 옥죄었다. 그날 밤 어린 동생 곁에서 까불며 노는 동안에도, 그녀의 태도는 본래의 그토록 사랑스럽고 어여쁜 모습인 적이 드물었고, 이따금 그가 왔다 갔다 거닐다가 그녀 가까이 다가오면(아마 그 순간 그녀는 그를 잊고 있었으리라) 그녀의 태도는 순식간에 변하여 부자연스럽고 어색해졌다.\n\n그래도 폴리는 이것을 보며 한층 더 의욕을 내어 꾸준히 애썼다. 그리고 돔비 씨를 제 마음에 비추어 헤아리며, 가엾은 어린 플로렌스의 상복이 보내는 무언의 호소에 큰 기대를 걸었다. \"정말이지 야박한 일이지,\" 폴리는 생각했다, \"눈앞에 또 다른 어미 없는 아이가, 그것도 딸이 하나 더 있는데, 한 아이한테만 마음을 준다면.\"\n\n그래서 폴리는 될 수 있는 한 오래도록 그녀를 그의 눈앞에 두었고, 어린 폴을 어찌나 잘 다루었던지, 도련님이 누이가 곁에 있어서 한결 더 생기 넘친다는 것을 아주 분명하게 드러내 보였다. 다시 위층으로 물러갈 때가 되자, 그녀는 플로렌스를 안쪽 방으로 들여보내 아버지에게 잘 자라는 인사를 드리게 하려 했으나, 아이는 겁을 내며 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 그리고 그녀가 다시 권하자, 아이는 자신의 못남을 가려 버리려는 듯 두 손을 펴서 눈앞을 가리며 말했다, \"오 안 돼요, 안 돼요! 아빠는 절 원하지 않아요. 절 원하지 않아요!\"\n\n둘 사이의 그 자그마한 실랑이가 돔비 씨의 주의를 끌었고, 포도주를 들며 앉아 있던 탁자에서 그는 무슨 일이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"플로렌스 아가씨가, 잘 자라는 인사를 드리러 들어오면 방해가 될까 봐 겁내셨어요, 나리,\" 리처즈가 말했다.\n\n\"상관없네,\" 돔비 씨가 대답했다. \"나는 신경 쓰지 말고 아가씨가 오가게 두게.\"\n\n그 말을 들으며 아이는 움츠러들었다—그리고 그녀의 변변찮은 친구가 다시 돌아보았을 때는 이미 가 버린 뒤였다.\n\n그러나 폴리는 자기의 선의에서 비롯된 계획이 성공한 것과, 그것을 이루어 낸 수완에 적잖이 우쭐했다. 그래서 다시 위층 안전한 진지에 자리 잡자, 그것을 스핏파이어에게 죄다 털어놓았다. 닙퍼 양은 그런 신뢰의 증표도, 또 앞으로 둘이 자유로이 어울리게 되리라는 전망도 다소 냉랭하게 받아들였고, 기쁨을 드러내는 데는 전혀 열의가 없었다.\n\n\"좋아할 줄 알았는데,\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"어머 그럼요, 리처즈 부인, 아주 좋아요, 고맙습니다,\" 수전이 대답했다. 그녀는 갑자기 어찌나 꼿꼿해졌던지 코르셋에 뼈대를 하나 더 끼워 넣은 것 같았다.\n\n\"그런 티가 안 나는데요,\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"어머! 저야 한낱 붙박이일 뿐이니, 임시처럼 티를 내길 바랄 순 없죠,\" 수전 닙퍼가 말했다. \"여기선 임시들이 다 차지하더군요, 제가 보니까요, 하지만 이 집이랑 옆집 사이에 훌륭한 칸막이벽이 있다 해도, 그렇다고 제가 굳이 거길 넘어가고 싶은 건 아니에요, 리처즈 부인, 그래도 말이죠!\"\n\n돔비와 아들의 사무소는 런던 시(市)의 자치 구역 안에, 거리의 소란에 그 부딪치는 소리가 묻히지 않을 때면 보 교회의 종소리가 들리는 범위 안에 자리하고 있었지만, 그래도 그 근방의 어떤 사물들 속에는 모험과 낭만이 깃든 이야기의 암시가 엿보였다. 고그와 마고그가 걸어서 십 분 거리 안에 위풍당당하게 서 있었고, 왕립 거래소가 바로 곁에 있었으며, 지하의 금고 속에 금과 은을 \"죽은 자들 틈에\" 묻어 둔 영국 은행이 그들의 으리으리한 이웃이었다. 모퉁이를 막 돌면 부유한 동인도 회사 건물이 서 있었는데, 귀한 천과 보석, 호랑이, 코끼리, 코끼리 등에 얹는 가마, 물담뱃대, 양산, 야자수, 가마, 그리고 양탄자 위에 발끝이 한껏 위로 말려 올라간 슬리퍼를 신고 앉아 있는 갈색 피부의 호화로운 왕자들에 관한 온갖 상상을 불러일으켰다. 바로 그 근방 어디서든 돛을 활짝 펴고 세계 곳곳으로 질주해 가는 배 그림이며, 누구든 어디로든 반 시간 안에 완전한 채비를 갖춰 떠나보낼 준비가 된 출항 용품 창고며, 항해 기구 제작자의 가게 문 밖에서 한물간 해군 제복 차림으로 영원히 삯마차들을 관측하는 일에 매달려 있는 자그마한 나무 견습 사관 인형을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n이 인형들 가운데 하나의—허물없이 말하자면 가장 나무토막 같다고 할 만한, 보도 위로 오른쪽 다리를 앞으로 내밀며 도무지 참아 줄 수 없는 상냥함으로 제 몸을 쑥 내밀고 있는, 그리고 인간의 이성과는 도무지 화해할 수 없는 구두 죔쇠와 날개처럼 펄럭이는 조끼를 달고 있으며, 오른쪽 눈에는 비위 상할 만큼 어울리지 않게 큰 기계 장치를 달고 있는—그 견습 사관 인형의 유일한 주인이자 소유주로서, 그를 자랑스러워하기까지 하는 한 노신사가 있었다. 그는 웨일스 가발을 쓴 채로, 살아 있는 다 자란 견습 사관이 평생 헤아린 햇수보다도 더 오랜 세월 동안 집세며 세금이며 부과금을 꼬박꼬박 치러 왔는데, 영국 해군에는 제법 노련한 고령에 이른 견습 사관도 없지 않았다.\n\n이 노신사가 거래하는 물품에는 크로노미터, 기압계, 망원경, 나침반, 해도, 지도, 육분의, 사분의, 그리고 배의 항로를 잡거나 배의 위치를 계산하거나 배의 탐사를 수행하는 데 쓰이는 온갖 종류의 기구 견본이 들어 있었다. 그의 서랍과 선반에는 놋쇠와 유리로 된 물건들이 놓여 있었는데, 그것들은 통달한 사람이 아니고서는 어디가 위쪽인지 찾을 수도, 무슨 쓰임새인지 짐작할 수도 없었으며, 한번 꺼내어 살펴본 다음에는 도움 없이는 결코 그 마호가니 둥지로 도로 집어넣을 수도 없는 것들이었다. 모든 물건이 더없이 빠듯한 상자 속에 끼워지고, 가장 좁은 구석에 맞춰지고, 가장 주제넘은 쿠션 뒤에 둘러쳐지고, 가장 날카로운 각도로 비틀려 박혀 있었으니, 바다의 흔들림으로 그 철학적인 평정이 흐트러지지 않게 하려는 것이었다. 자리를 아끼고 물건을 빈틈없이 챙기려고 어느 것에나 그토록 비상한 주의가 기울여졌으며, 어찌나 많은 실용 항해술이 (어떤 것은 그저 평평한 판 모양이었고, 어떤 것은 삼각모와 불가사리의 중간쯤 되는 모양이었는데, 그것들조차 다른 것들에 비하면 아주 점잖고 얌전한 상자였다) 상자마다 빠짐없이 맞춰지고 쿠션에 싸이고 비틀려 박혀 있었던지, 그 가게 자체가 그 보편적인 전염에 물든 듯 거의 아늑하고 항해에 알맞은 배 모양의 살림처럼 보였으니, 뜻밖에 진수하게 되는 경우 넉넉한 바다 공간만 있다면 세계 어느 무인도까지든 무사히 헤쳐 나갈 수 있을 것만 같았다.\n\n자기의 자그마한 견습 사관 인형을 자랑스러워하던 그 선박 기구 제작자의 가정생활에서 일어난 자잘한 사건들이 이 공상을 거들고 뒷받침해 주었다. 그의 교제는 주로 선구상(船具商) 따위들 사이에 이루어진 터라, 그의 식탁에는 늘 진짜 선박용 비스킷이 넉넉히 놓여 있었다. 그것은 밧줄 가닥 같은 묘한 풍미를 지닌 말린 고기와 우설(牛舌)에 익숙해 있었다. 식탁 위에는 \"온갖 종류의 선박용 식료품 거래상\"이라는 상표가 붙은 커다란 도매용 항아리에 든 절임이 차려졌고, 술은 목이 없는 사각 병에 담겨 나왔다. 벽에는 갖가지 신비한 부분에 알파벳 표시를 붙인 옛 배 그림들이 액자에 걸려 있었으며, 접시에는 출항 중인 타타르 프리깃함이 그려져 있었고, 벽난로 선반은 진기한 조개껍데기와 해초와 이끼로 장식되어 있었으며, 판자를 두른 자그마한 뒷거실은 선실처럼 천장 채광창으로 빛이 들었다.\n\n여기서 그는 또한 선장 같은 위세로, 조카 월터와 단둘이서만 살았다. 월터는 열네 살 소년으로, 그 흔한 인상을 뒷받침할 만큼 견습 사관과 꽤나 닮아 보였다. 그러나 닮은 것은 거기까지였으니, 솔로몬 길스 자신은 (대개 늙은 솔이라 불렸다) 바닷사람다운 풍모와는 거리가 멀었다. 일찍이 누가 썼던 것 중에서도 가장 수수하고 고집스러운 웨일스 가발이라 할 만한 그 가발—그것을 쓰면 그는 도무지 바다의 떠돌이로는 보이지 않았다—은 말할 것도 없거니와, 그는 안개를 뚫고 그대를 들여다보는 작은 두 개의 태양처럼 새빨간 눈을 한, 굼뜨고 말수 적고 생각 많은 노인이었다. 마치 사나흘을 연거푸 자기 가게의 모든 광학 기구를 들여다보다가 갑자기 세상으로 되돌아와 그것이 푸르른 것을 발견하기라도 한 듯한, 막 잠에서 깬 듯한 태도가 있었다. 그의 겉모습에서 일어난 유일한 변화라고 알려진 것은, 매우 각지게 재단되고 번쩍이는 단추로 장식된 커피색 정장 한 벌에서, 바지만 옅은 황색 무명천으로 바뀐 똑같은 커피색 정장으로 바뀐 것뿐이었다. 그는 매우 단정한 셔츠 주름 장식을 달았고, 이마에는 일급 안경 한 벌을 얹었으며, 회중시계 주머니에는 엄청난 크로노미터를 지니고 다녔는데, 그 귀중한 소유물을 의심하느니 차라리 시내의 온갖 괘종시계며 회중시계가, 그리고 심지어 태양 그 자체까지도 그것에 맞서 음모를 꾸미고 있다고 믿어 버릴 그런 사람이었다. 그는 이런 사람이었고, 또 이러한 모습으로 그 자그마한 견습 사관 인형 뒤편의 가게와 거실에서 여러 해 동안 지내 왔으며, 밤마다 어김없이 세입자들과는 멀리 떨어진 바람 윙윙대는 다락방으로 올라가 잠을 잤다. 그 다락방에서는, 아래층에서 편안히 사는 영국 신사들이 날씨가 어떤지 거의 짐작도 못 할 때에도, 흔히 바람이 대포처럼 거세게 몰아쳤다.\n\n독자와 솔로몬 길스가 알게 되는 때는 어느 가을날 오후 다섯 시 반이다. 솔로몬 길스는 그 흠잡을 데 없는 크로노미터로 몇 시인지 보고 있는 참이다. 시내에서는 으레 있는 하루의 퇴근 행렬이 한 시간 남짓 이어져 왔고, 인파는 여전히 서쪽으로 밀려가고 있다. \"거리가 많이 한산해졌군.\" 길스 씨가 말하는 대로다. 오늘 밤은 비가 올 듯하다. 가게의 모든 기압계가 풀이 죽어 있고, 빗방울이 벌써 나무 견습 사관의 삼각모 위에서 반짝인다.\n\n\"월터가 어디 있는지 모르겠군!\" 솔로몬 길스가 크로노미터를 다시 조심스레 넣어 두고는 말했다. \"저녁이 차려진 지 반 시간이나 됐는데, 월터는 코빼기도 안 보이니!\"\n\n계산대 뒤 걸상 위에서 몸을 돌려, 길스 씨는 진열창의 기구들 너머로 내다보며 조카가 길을 건너오는 중은 아닌지 살폈다. 아니었다. 그는 흔들거리는 우산들 사이에도 없었고, 가게 밖 놋쇠판을 따라 천천히 나아가며 집게손가락으로 길스 씨의 이름 위에 제 이름을 쓰고 있는, 기름먹인 모자를 쓴 신문팔이 소년도 분명 아니었다.\n\n\"저 녀석이 날 너무 좋아해서 줄행랑을 치거나 내 뜻을 거슬러 배에 올라 입대해 버리지는 않으리라는 걸 몰랐다면, 안절부절못하기 시작했을 텐데.\" 길스 씨가 손가락 마디로 기압계 두세 개를 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"정말 그랬을 거야. 다운스 해역 일대가 온통! 습기가 잔뜩이야! 뭐! 필요한 거지.\"\n\n\"내 생각에는,\" 길스 씨가 나침반 함의 유리 뚜껑에서 먼지를 후 불며 말했다, \"결국 저 녀석의 마음이 뒷거실 쪽을 가리키는 것만큼 너도 그렇게 똑바로 정확하게 가리키지는 못할 게다. 그리고 거실 또한 그보다 더 곧게 향할 수는 없지. 정북이야. 어느 쪽으로도 한 점의 이십분의 일도 빗나가지 않는다고.\"\n\n\"안녕하세요, 솔 삼촌!\"\n\n\"오, 어서 오너라, 얘야!\" 기구 제작자가 활기차게 몸을 돌리며 외쳤다. \"아니! 너 와 있었구나, 그렇지?\"\n\n쾌활해 보이는 명랑한 소년이었다. 빗속을 달려온 터라 생기가 돌았고, 얼굴이 희고, 눈이 반짝이며, 머리가 곱슬곱슬했다.\n\n\"자, 삼촌, 하루 종일 저 없이 어떻게 지내셨어요? 저녁 다 됐어요? 배가 무척 고파요.\"\n\n\"지내는 거로 말하자면,\" 솔로몬이 사람 좋게 말했다, \"너 같은 강아지 녀석이 없어도, 있을 때보다 훨씬 잘 지내지 못한다면 그게 더 이상한 일이지. 저녁이 다 됐냐 하면, 반 시간 전부터 다 돼서 너를 기다리고 있었다. 배가 고프냐 하면, 나야말로 고프단다!\"\n\n\"그럼 어서 가요, 삼촌!\" 소년이 외쳤다. \"제독 만세!\"\n\n\"제독은 무슨!\" 솔로몬 길스가 응수했다. \"시장님을 말하는 거겠지.\"\n\n\"아니에요!\" 소년이 외쳤다. \"제독 만세! 제독 만세! 앞으로—옷!\"\n\n이 호령 한마디에, 웨일스 가발과 그 주인은, 마치 오백 명의 적함 돌격대 선두에 서기라도 한 듯, 저항할 새도 없이 뒷거실로 떠밀려 갔다. 그리하여 솔 삼촌과 조카는 곧 튀긴 가자미 요리에 몰두하게 되었고, 뒤이어 스테이크가 나올 전망이었다.\n\n\"시장님 말이다, 월리,\" 솔로몬이 말했다, \"영원하시기를! 제독은 이제 그만. 시장님이 네 제독이시다.\"\n\n\"오, 정말 그래요?\" 소년이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"그래도 검(劍) 의장(儀仗)관이 그분보다 나아요. 그분은 가끔 검을 뽑잖아요.\"\n\n\"그러느라 애써 봤자 꼴이 우습기만 하지.\" 삼촌이 응수했다. \"내 말 좀 들어 봐라, 월리, 들어 봐. 저 벽난로 선반을 봐.\"\n\n\"아니 누가 제 은잔을 저 위에다, 못에다 걸어 놨어요?\" 소년이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"내가 그랬다.\" 삼촌이 말했다. \"이제 잔(머그)은 그만이야. 오늘부터 우리는 유리잔으로 마셔야 한다, 월터. 우리는 사업하는 사람이야. 시(市)에 속한 사람들이고. 우리는 오늘 아침 인생의 첫발을 내디뎠지.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 삼촌.\" 소년이 말했다. \"삼촌을 위해 마실 수만 있다면, 무엇으로든 마시겠어요. 삼촌을 위하여, 솔 삼촌, 그리고—만세—\"\n\n\"시장님.\" 노인이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"시장님과, 주(州) 장관님들과, 시 참사회와, 동업조합을 위하여.\" 소년이 말했다. \"그분들 만수무강을!\"\n\n삼촌은 매우 흡족해하며 고개를 끄덕였다. \"자, 이제,\" 그가 말했다, \"그 회사 이야기를 좀 들어 보자.\"\n\n\"오! 회사에 대해서는 별로 할 얘기가 없어요, 삼촌.\" 소년이 나이프와 포크를 놀리며 말했다. \"엄청나게 어두침침한 사무소예요. 그리고 제가 앉아 있는 방에는 높은 난로 가리개가 있고, 철제 금고가 있고, 출항할 배들에 관한 카드가 좀 붙어 있고, 달력이 있고, 책상과 걸상이 몇 개 있고, 잉크병이 하나 있고, 책이 좀 있고, 상자가 몇 개 있고, 거미줄이 잔뜩 쳐져 있어요. 그리고 그중 하나에는, 바로 제 머리 위에, 쪼그라든 쉬파리가 한 마리 걸려 있는데, 아주 오래전부터 거기 매달려 있던 것처럼 보여요.\"\n\n\"그것뿐이냐?\" 삼촌이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그것뿐이에요. 낡은 새장 하나만 빼고요 (그게 어떻게 거기 들어왔는지 모르겠어요!) 그리고 석탄통 하나도요.\"\n\n\"날마다 굴러 들어오는 부의 징표 같은, 은행 장부나 수표책이나 어음 같은 건 없더냐?\" 늙은 솔이, 늘 자기 주위에 드리워진 듯한 그 안개 너머로 조카를 그윽이 바라보며, 그 말들에 진하게 힘을 주어 물었다.\n\n\"오, 네, 그런 건 잔뜩 있겠죠, 아마.\" 조카가 무심하게 응수했다. \"하지만 그런 것들은 죄다 카커 씨 방이나, 모핀 씨 방이나, 돔비 씨 방에 있어요.\"\n\n\"오늘 돔비 씨가 거기 왔더냐?\" 삼촌이 물었다.\n\n\"오, 네! 하루 종일 들락날락하셨어요.\"\n\n\"너한테는 별로 눈길을 주지 않았겠지, 아마?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 주셨어요. 제 자리로 걸어오셔서—그분이 그렇게 엄숙하고 뻣뻣하지만 않으셨으면 좋겠어요, 삼촌—이러시더라고요. '오! 네가 선박 기구 제작자 길스 씨의 아들이로구나.' '조카입니다, 나리.' 제가 말했죠. '내가 조카라고 했다, 얘야.' 그분이 그러셨어요. 하지만 그분이 아들이라고 하셨다는 데 제 목을 걸 수 있어요, 삼촌.\"\n\n\"네가 잘못 들은 모양이다. 그런 건 아무래도 좋아.\"\n\n\"네, 아무래도 좋지만, 그렇게 쌀쌀맞게 안 하셔도 됐을 텐데 싶었어요. 아들이라고 하셨다 해도 해될 게 없잖아요. 그러고 나서 삼촌이 저에 관해 그분께 말씀하셨고, 그래서 그분이 회사에 제 일자리를 마련해 주셨다고, 그리고 제가 성실하고 시간을 잘 지킬 것을 기대한다고 하시고는, 가 버리셨어요. 그분은 절 별로 좋아하시지 않는 것 같았어요.\"\n\n\"내 짐작에 네 말은,\" 기구 제작자가 말했다, \"네가 그분을 별로 좋아하지 않는 것처럼 보였다는 뜻이겠지?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 삼촌.\" 소년이 웃으며 응수했다. \"아마 그럴지도요. 그건 미처 생각 못 했네요.\"\n\n솔로몬은 저녁을 마치면서 조금 더 진지한 표정이 되어, 이따금 소년의 환한 얼굴을 흘끗거렸다. 저녁이 끝나고 식탁보가 치워지자 (음식은 이웃 식당에서 가져온 것이었다), 그는 촛불을 켜 들고 자그마한 지하 저장고로 내려갔고, 그동안 조카는 곰팡내 나는 계단에 서서 충실히 불을 비춰 주었다. 여기저기 잠시 더듬은 끝에, 그는 곧 먼지와 때를 뒤집어쓴 아주 오래되어 보이는 병 하나를 들고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아니, 솔 삼촌!\" 소년이 말했다, \"뭐 하시는 거예요? 그건 그 굉장한 마데이라잖아요!—이제 한 병밖에 안 남았는데!\"\n\n솔 삼촌은 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 아주 잘 안다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러고는 엄숙한 침묵 속에 코르크 마개를 뽑아, 잔 두 개를 채우고 병과 깨끗한 세 번째 잔을 식탁에 놓았다.\n\n\"나머지 한 병은 네가 마시거라, 월리.\" 그가 말했다, \"네게 좋은 운이 찾아왔을 때, 네가 번창하고 존경받는 행복한 사람이 되었을 때, 네가 오늘 내디딘 인생의 첫발이 너를—하늘이 그러기를 비노라!—네가 달려야 할 경주에서 평탄한 구간으로 데려다주었을 때 말이다, 얘야. 너를 위하여 마시마!\"\n\n늙은 솔을 둘러싸고 있던 안개의 일부가 그의 목구멍으로 들어가 버린 듯, 그는 쉰 목소리로 말했다. 조카의 잔에 제 잔을 부딪칠 때 그의 손도 떨렸다. 그러나 일단 술을 입에 대자, 그는 사내답게 단숨에 들이켜고는 입맛을 쩝쩝 다셨다.\n\n\"사랑하는 삼촌,\" 소년이 눈에 눈물이 고인 채, 짐짓 대수롭지 않은 척하며 말했다, \"저에게 베풀어 주신 영광에 대하여, 기타 등등, 기타 등등. 이제 저는 외람되이 솔로몬 길스 씨를 위하여 세 번씩 세 번 그리고 한 번 더 환호할 것을 제안합니다. 만세! 그리고 삼촌도 답례 인사를 해 주셔야 해요, 우리가 마지막 병을 함께 마실 때 말이에요. 그러실 거죠?\"\n\n두 사람은 다시 잔을 부딪쳤고, 술을 아껴 두려던 월터는 한 모금 홀짝이고는, 자기가 지을 수 있는 최대한 까다로운 태도로 잔을 눈높이까지 들어 올렸다.\n\n삼촌은 한동안 말없이 그를 바라보고 앉아 있었다. 마침내 두 사람의 눈이 마주치자, 그는 곧바로, 마치 내내 말해 오던 것처럼, 소리 내어 자기 머릿속을 차지하고 있던 화제를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"보아라, 월터,\" 그가 말했다, \"사실 이 장사는 나한테는 그저 습관일 뿐이란다. 이 습관에 어찌나 익숙해졌는지, 그걸 놓아 버리면 살아가기조차 힘들 정도지. 하지만 되는 일이 없어, 되는 일이 없다고. 저 제복이 입혀지던 시절에는,\" 자그마한 견습 사관 인형 쪽을 가리키며, \"그땐 정말이지 한밑천 잡을 수 있었고, 또 잡았지. 하지만 경쟁, 경쟁—새로운 발명, 새로운 발명—변화, 변화—세상이 날 앞질러 가 버렸어. 나는 내가 어디 있는지조차 거의 모르겠으니, 내 손님들이 어디 있는지는 더더욱 모르지.\"\n\n\"손님들 따위 신경 쓰지 마세요, 삼촌!\"\n\n\"이를테면 네가 페컴의 주(週) 단위 기숙학교에서 집에 돌아온 뒤로—그게 열흘 됐는데,\" 솔로몬이 말했다, \"가게에 들어온 사람이라곤 한 사람밖에 기억이 안 나는구나.\"\n\n\"두 사람이에요, 삼촌, 기억 안 나세요? 1소버린을 잔돈으로 바꿔 달라고 물으러 온 남자가 있었잖아요—\"\n\n\"그게 그 한 사람이지.\" 솔로몬이 말했다.\n\n\"아니 삼촌! 마일엔드 통행료 징수소로 가는 길을 물으러 온 여자는 사람으로 안 치세요?\"\n\n\"오! 맞다.\" 솔로몬이 말했다, \"그 여자를 깜박했구나. 두 사람이다.\"\n\n\"하긴 그 사람들은 아무것도 안 샀죠.\" 소년이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래. 아무것도 안 샀지.\" 솔로몬이 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"뭘 원하지도 않았고요.\" 소년이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래. 원했다면 다른 가게로 갔을 테지.\" 솔로몬이 같은 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 두 사람이었어요, 삼촌.\" 소년이, 마치 그것이 대단한 승리라도 되는 양 외쳤다. \"삼촌은 한 사람뿐이라고 하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 월리,\" 노인이 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 다시 말했다. \"우리는 로빈슨 크루소의 섬에 찾아온 야만인들과는 다르니, 1소버린을 잔돈으로 바꿔 달라는 남자와 마일엔드 통행료 징수소 가는 길을 묻는 여자에 기대어 살 수는 없지. 방금 말했듯이, 세상이 나를 앞질러 가 버렸어. 세상을 탓하지는 않아. 하지만 나는 더 이상 세상을 이해하지 못한다. 상인들도 예전 같지 않고, 견습공들도 예전 같지 않고, 장사도 예전 같지 않고, 장사하는 물건들도 예전 같지 않아. 내 재고의 팔분의 칠은 구식이지. 나는 구식 거리에서—내가 기억하는 그 거리와는 같지 않은 거리에서—구식 가게를 하는 구식 사람이야. 나는 시대에 뒤처졌고, 다시 따라잡기엔 너무 늙었어. 저 멀리 앞에서 시대가 내는 소리조차 나를 어리둥절하게 만든단다.\"\n\n월터가 무어라 말하려 했지만, 삼촌이 손을 들어 막았다.\n\n\"그래서, 월리—그래서 나는 네가 일찌감치 분주한 세상에 들어가, 세상의 궤도에 오르기를 바라는 거란다. 나는 이 장사의 유령일 뿐이야—그 알맹이는 오래전에 사라져 버렸지. 그리고 내가 죽으면 그 유령도 잠들 게다. 그러니 이게 네게 물려줄 유산이 못 됨이 명백하니, 나는 오랜 습관 덕에 그나마 내게 남아 있는, 옛 인연의 거의 유일한 조각을 너를 위해 쓰는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 생각했단다. 어떤 이들은 나를 부유하다고 짐작하지. 너를 위해서라면 그들 말이 맞기를 바란다. 하지만 내가 무엇을 남기든, 또 무엇을 네게 줄 수 있든, 너는 돔비 씨네 같은 회사에서 그것을 잘 활용하고 최대한으로 살릴 길에 들어선 거야. 부지런히 일하고, 그 일을 좋아하려고 애쓰고, 안정된 자립을 위해 일하고, 그리고 행복하거라, 얘야!\"\n\n\"삼촌의 애정에 보답할 수 있도록 제가 할 수 있는 건 다 하겠어요. 정말로요.\" 소년이 간절하게 말했다.\n\n\"안다.\" 솔로몬이 말했다. \"확신한다.\" 그러고는 더욱 흡족한 마음으로 그 오래된 마데이라 두 잔째에 매달렸다. \"바다로 말하자면,\" 그가 말을 이었다, \"그건 소설 속에서야 그럴듯하지, 월리, 하지만 현실에서는 안 될 일이야. 도무지 안 될 일이지. 네가 그걸 이 모든 친숙한 물건들과 결부시켜 생각하는 거야 당연한 일이다만, 안 될 일이야, 안 될 일이지.\"\n\n그러면서도 솔로몬 길스는 바다 이야기를 할 때면 은근히 즐기는 기색으로 두 손을 비볐고, 주위의 항해 관련 물건들을 형언할 수 없는 흐뭇함으로 바라보았다.\n\n\"예를 들어 이 술을 생각해 보렴.\" 늙은 솔이 말했다, \"이건 내가 몇 번이나 그랬는지 말할 수도 없을 만큼 동인도까지 갔다가 돌아왔고, 한 번은 세계를 일주하기까지 했지. 칠흑같이 어두운 밤들, 포효하는 바람, 출렁이는 바다를 생각해 보아라.\"\n\n\"천둥, 번개, 비, 우박, 온갖 종류의 폭풍이요.\" 소년이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇고말고,\" 솔로몬이 말했다, \"이 술이 그 모든 걸 헤치고 지나왔다는 거야. 목재며 돛대가 얼마나 삐걱대고 끼익거렸을지, 밧줄과 삭구 사이로 강풍이 얼마나 휘파람 불며 울부짖었을지 생각해 보렴.\"\n\n\"미친 듯이 배가 흔들리고 곤두박질하는 와중에, 누가 먼저 활대 위로 기어 나가 얼어붙은 돛을 걷느냐 서로 다투며 사람들이 돛대 위로 기어오르는 걸요!\" 조카가 외쳤다.\n\n\"바로 그렇지,\" 솔로몬이 말했다, \"그 모든 일이, 이 술을 담았던 그 오래된 통 위에서 벌어졌다는 거야. 그래, 매혹의 샐리호가 가라앉았을 때—\"\n\n\"발트해에서, 한밤중에 말이죠. 선장의 회중시계가 그의 주머니 속에서 멈춘 게 열두 시 이십오 분, 선장은 큰 돛대에 기댄 채 죽어 있었고요—1749년 2월 14일이었어요!\" 월터가 잔뜩 신이 나서 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 그렇고말고!\" 늙은 솔이 외쳤다, \"아주 정확해! 그때 배에는 그런 술이 오백 통이나 실려 있었지. 그리고 (일등 항해사, 일등 사관, 선원 둘, 그리고 물 새는 보트에 탄 여인 하나만 빼고) 모든 선원이 술통을 부수려 달려들었다가, 곤드레만드레 취해서는 '룰 브리타니아'를 부르며 취한 채로 죽어 갔고, 배가 가라앉아 물속으로 잠길 때, 일제히 끔찍한 비명 하나로 끝을 맺었지.\"\n\n* 룰 브리타니아: 영국을 찬양하는 애국 가요.\n\"하지만 조지 2세호가, 삼촌, 음울한 강풍 속에서 콘월 해안에 좌초했을 때, 그게 날 밝기 두 시간 전, 1771년 3월 4일이었는데, 그 배엔 말이 이백 마리 가까이 실려 있었어요. 그런데 강풍이 일자마자 아래쪽에서 말들이 풀려나 이리저리 날뛰며 서로 짓밟아 죽이는 바람에, 어찌나 요란한 소리를 내고 사람처럼 울부짖었던지, 선원들은 배가 악마로 가득 찼다고 믿었고, 가장 뛰어난 사람들 몇몇이 정신이 나가 절망 속에 바다로 뛰어들었으며, 결국 살아남아 이 이야기를 전한 사람은 단 둘뿐이었어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 언제냐 하면,\" 늙은 솔이 말했다, \"폴리페모스호가 말이다—\"\n\n\"버든 삼백오십 톤짜리 개인 서인도 무역선이죠. 선장은 뎁트퍼드의 존 브라운. 선주는 위그스 상회였고요.\" 월터가 외쳤다.\n\n\"바로 그거다.\" 솔이 말했다, \"그 배가 자메이카 항을 떠나 순풍을 타고 나흘 항해한 끝에, 한밤중에 불이 났을 때—\"\n\n\"배에는 형제 둘이 타고 있었어요.\" 조카가 매우 빠르고 큰 소리로 끼어들었다, \"그리고 물에 잠기지 않은 단 한 척의 보트에 두 사람이 다 탈 자리가 없자, 둘 중 누구도 가려 하지 않다가, 형이 동생의 허리를 붙잡아 보트 안으로 던져 넣었어요. 그러자 동생이 보트에서 일어나 외쳤죠. '사랑하는 에드워드 형, 집에서 형을 기다리는 약혼녀를 생각해. 난 그냥 소년일 뿐이야. 집에서 날 기다리는 사람은 아무도 없어. 내 자리로 뛰어 내려와!' 그러고는 바다로 몸을 던졌어요!\"\n\n자기가 말하고 느끼는 바에 대한 간절함으로 자리에서 일어선 소년의, 불타오르는 눈빛과 짙어진 혈색이, 늙은 솔에게 자기가 잊고 있던 무언가를, 혹은 자기를 둘러싼 안개가 지금껏 가로막아 온 무언가를 일깨우는 듯했다. 바로 한순간 전까지만 해도 분명히 그러려고 했던 것처럼 일화를 더 이어 가는 대신, 그는 짧고 메마른 기침을 한 번 하고는 말했다. \"자! 화제를 좀 바꿔 보는 게 어떻겠니.\"\n\n사실인즉, 단순한 마음을 지닌 그 삼촌은 자기 장사로 인해 어떤 의미에서는 먼 친척뻘이라 할 수 있는 그 경이롭고 모험적인 것에 은근히 끌리는 마음 때문에, 조카에게도 그와 똑같은 끌림을 크게 부추겨 온 터였다. 그리하여 소년이 모험적인 삶에 빠지지 않게 하려고 그 앞에 놓아 둔 모든 것이, 도리어 그것에 대한 소년의 취향을 날카롭게 벼리는, 늘 그렇듯 설명할 수 없는 효과를 내고 만 것이었다. 이건 변함없는 법칙이다. 소년들을 뭍에 붙들어 두려는 명백한 목적으로 쓰인 책이나 들려준 이야기치고, 도리어 그들을 당연한 일인 양 바다로 꾀어내고 매혹하지 않은 것은 단 한 번도 없었던 것만 같다.\n\n그런데 이제 그 작은 모임에 한 사람이 더 모습을 드러냈으니, 헐렁한 푸른 정장 차림에, 오른쪽 손목에는 손 대신 갈고리가 달린 신사였다. 매우 텁수룩한 검은 눈썹에, 왼손에는 (그의 코처럼) 온통 혹투성이인 굵은 지팡이를 들고 있었다. 목에는 헐렁한 검은 비단 손수건을 둘렀고, 어찌나 큼지막하고 거친 셔츠 깃을 달았는지 마치 작은 돛처럼 보였다. 그는 분명 여분의 술잔이 마련된 바로 그 사람이었고, 또 자기도 그걸 분명히 알고 있었다. 그는 거친 겉외투를 벗어, 문 뒤의 특정한 못에다 걸었는데—그것은 보기만 해도 마음 약한 사람은 머리가 지끈거릴 만큼 딱딱하게 광택 나는 모자였으며, 꽉 끼는 대야라도 쓰고 있었던 것처럼 그의 이마에 붉은 자국을 남겨 놓았다—그러고는 의자를 깨끗한 잔이 있는 곳으로 가져다 그 뒤에 자리 잡고 앉았다. 이 방문객은 으레 선장이라 불렸으며, 도선사였거나, 선장이었거나, 사략선원이었거나, 어쩌면 그 셋 다였을 것이고, 정말이지 짠 바닷물에 절은 듯한 사내였다.\n\n갈색의 단단함이 두드러지는 그의 얼굴은, 삼촌과 조카와 악수를 나누며 환하게 밝아졌다. 그러나 그는 과묵한 기질인 듯, 그저 이렇게만 말했다.\n\n\"어찌 지내시오?\"\n\n\"다들 잘 있소.\" 길스 씨가 그에게 병을 밀어 주며 말했다.\n\n그는 그것을 집어 들고, 찬찬히 살펴보고 냄새를 맡고는, 유난스러운 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"그건가?\"\n\n\"그거요.\" 기구 제작자가 대답했다.\n\n그 말에 그는 잔을 채우며 휘파람을 불었고, 정말로 잔칫날이라도 맞은 듯 여기는 듯했다.\n\n\"월—터!\" 그가 갈고리로 (숱이 적은) 머리카락을 매만지고는, 그것으로 기구 제작자를 가리키며 말했다, \"저분을 보거라! 사랑하라! 공경하라! 그리고 순종하라! 그 구절을 찾을 때까지 네 교리문답서를 샅샅이 뒤져서, 찾거든 그 쪽 귀퉁이를 접어 두렴. 성공을 비네, 얘야!\"\n\n그는 자기가 인용한 말과 그것을 들먹인 방식 둘 다에 어찌나 완벽하게 만족했던지, 그 말을 낮은 목소리로 다시 한번 되뇌지 않을 수 없었고, 자기가 그 말들을 사십 년 동안이나 잊고 있었다고 했다.\n\n\"하지만 살면서 내가 두세 마디 말이 필요할 때 그걸 어디서 끌어다 댈지 몰랐던 적은 한 번도 없었네, 길스.\" 그가 말했다. \"그건 다 어떤 자들처럼 말을 허투루 낭비하지 않은 덕이지.\"\n\n이런 생각을 하다 보니 어쩌면, 젊은 노발의 아버지처럼, 자기도 '재산을 늘리는' 편이 낫겠다는 생각이 떠올랐는지도 모른다. 아무튼 그는 입을 다물었고, 늙은 솔이 가게에 불을 밝히려고 밖으로 나갈 때까지 줄곧 잠자코 있었다. 솔이 나가자 그는 월터에게로 돌아서서, 아무런 서두도 없이 말했다.\n* 젊은 노발: 18세기 비극 「더글러스」에 나오는 인물로, 그의 아버지가 검소함으로 재산을 늘렸다는 대사를 빗댄 것.\n\n\"내 짐작에 저분이 마음만 먹으면 시계도 만들 수 있겠지?\"\n\n\"그러셔도 놀랍지 않을 거예요, 커틀 선장님.\" 소년이 응수했다.\n\n\"그리고 그게 잘 갈 거야!\" 커틀 선장이 갈고리로 허공에 일종의 뱀 모양을 그리며 말했다. \"이런, 그 시계가 얼마나 잘 가겠나!\"\n\n잠시 동안 그는 그 가상의 시계가 가는 속도를 곰곰이 떠올리는 데 완전히 빠져든 듯, 마치 소년의 얼굴이 시계 문자판이라도 되는 양 그를 바라보고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 저분은 학식이 그득하시지.\" 그가 재고품 쪽으로 갈고리를 휘저으며 말했다. \"여길 봐! 여기 그것들이 한가득 모여 있잖나. 땅이든, 하늘이든, 물이든. 다 매한가지야. 어디서 가질지 말하기만 하면 돼. 기구를 타고 하늘 위로? 자, 됐네. 종 모양 잠수기를 타고 물속 아래로? 자, 됐어. 북극성을 저울 한 쌍에 올려 무게를 달고 싶나? 저분이 자네 대신 해 주실 걸세.\"\n\n이런 말들로 미루어, 그 기구 재고품에 대한 커틀 선장의 경외심이 깊었으며, 그의 철학으로는 그것을 거래하는 일과 발명하는 일 사이에 거의, 혹은 전혀 구별이 없었음을 짐작할 수 있다.\n\n\"아!\" 그가 한숨지으며 말했다, \"그것들을 이해한다는 건 멋진 일이지. 하지만 또 그것들을 이해하지 못한다는 것도 멋진 일이야. 어느 쪽이 더 나은지 도무지 모르겠네. 이렇게 여기 앉아서, 내가 무게가 재어지고, 치수가 측정되고, 확대되고, 전기를 띠고, 편광되고, 흠씬 농락당할 수도 있는데, 어떻게 그렇게 되는지는 전혀 모른다고 느끼는 게 어찌나 마음 편한지.\"\n\n그 굉장한 마데이라가, 이 상황과 (월터의 마음을 길러 주고 넓혀 줄 만하다는 점에서) 어우러지지 않았다면, 결코 그의 혀가 이런 엄청난 일장 연설을 토해 낼 만큼 풀어지는 일은 없었을 것이다. 그가 십 년 동안 그 거실에서 일요일 저녁을 먹으며 누려 온 그 과묵한 즐거움의 원천이, 그 연설로 환히 드러나는 모양새에 그 자신조차 자못 놀라는 듯했다. 한층 슬프고도 현명해진 사람이 되어, 그는 사색에 잠긴 채 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"자!\" 이 감탄의 대상이 돌아오며 외쳤다. \"자네가 그로그주 한 잔을 들기 전에, 네드, 우리는 이 병을 비워야 하네.\"\n\n\"준비됐네!\" 네드가 자기 잔을 채우며 말했다. \"얘한테도 좀 더 따라 주게.\"\n\n\"전 그만이에요, 고맙습니다, 삼촌!\"\n\n\"아니다, 아니야.\" 솔이 말했다, \"조금만 더. 우리는 이 병을 비워 회사를 위해 건배하자꾸나, 네드—월터의 회사 말이다. 아니, 언젠가는 이게 부분적으로나마 저 애의 회사가 될지도 모르지. 누가 알겠나? 리처드 휘팅턴 경도 자기 주인의 딸과 결혼했잖나.\"\n\n\"'돌아오너라 휘팅턴, 런던 시장이여, 그리고 늙거든 결코 거기서 떠나지 말지어다.'\" 선장이 끼어들었다. \"월—터! 그 책을 샅샅이 뒤져 봐라, 얘야.\"\n\n* 휘팅턴: 가난한 소년에서 런던 시장이 되었다는 전설의 인물 딕 휘팅턴.\n\n\"그리고 돔비 씨한테는 딸이 없지만—\" 솔이 운을 뗐다.\n\n\"아뇨, 있어요, 삼촌.\" 소년이 얼굴을 붉히며 웃었다.\n\n\"있다고?\" 노인이 외쳤다. \"정말이지 내 생각에도 있는 것 같구나.\"\n\"오! 분명히 있어요.\" 소년이 말했다. \"오늘 사무소에서 몇 사람이 그 얘기를 하더라고요. 그리고 사람들이 그러는데요, 삼촌, 그리고 커틀 선장님,\" 목소리를 낮추며, \"그분이 그 딸을 싫어하게 되셨대요. 그래서 딸은 하인들 틈에서 거들떠보지도 않은 채 내버려져 있고, 그분 마음은 온통 회사에 아들을 들이는 데만 쏠려 있어서, 비록 아들이 아직 갓난아기일 뿐인데도, 예전보다 더 자주 결산을 하게 하고, 장부도 예전보다 더 꼼꼼히 관리하게 하시고, 게다가 (아무도 안 본다고 여기실 때) 부두를 거닐며 자기 배들과 재산과 그런 걸 둘러보시는 게 목격되기까지 했대요. 마치 자기와 아들이 함께 소유하게 될 것을 두고 의기양양해하시는 것처럼요. 사람들이 그렇게 말해요. 물론 저야 모르지만요.\"\n\n\"그분은 벌써 그 애에 관해 죄다 알고 계신 게로구나, 봐라.\" 기구 제작자가 말했다.\n\n\"말도 안 돼요, 삼촌.\" 소년이, 여전히 소년답게 얼굴을 붉히며 웃으며 외쳤다. \"사람들이 들려주는 걸 제가 안 들을 수가 있나요?\"\n\n\"그 아들이 지금 우리에게는 좀 거치적거리는군, 안 그런가, 네드.\" 노인이 농을 맞장구치며 말했다.\n\n\"아주 그렇지.\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇긴 해도, 우리 그 아이를 위해 마시세.\" 솔이 말을 이었다. \"자, 돔비와 아들을 위하여.\"\n\n\"오, 좋아요, 삼촌.\" 소년이 흥겹게 말했다. \"삼촌이 그 딸 이야기를 꺼내셨고, 저를 그 딸과 엮으셨고, 또 제가 그 딸에 관해 죄다 안다고 하셨으니, 외람되이 그 건배사를 고쳐 보겠어요. 그러니, 돔비—와 아들—그리고 딸을 위하여!\"\n\n# CHAPTER V. Paul’s Progress and Christening\n\n어린 폴은 투들 집안의 피로 인해 조금도 더럽혀지지 않은 채, 날마다 더 튼튼하고 건강하게 자랐다. 또한 날마다 톡스 양에게 점점 더 뜨겁게 사랑받았는데, 그녀의 헌신을 돔비 씨가 어느 정도 알아주게 되어 그녀를 타고난 양식이 뛰어난 여인, 그 감정이 본인에게 영예가 되고 격려받을 만한 여인으로 여기기 시작했다. 그는 이런 겸양을 어찌나 아낌없이 베풀었던지, 여러 차례에 걸쳐 특별한 태도로 그녀에게 절을 했을 뿐 아니라, 누이를 통해 이런 위엄 있는 인사까지 전했으니, 이를테면 \"루이자, 네 친구에게 그녀가 참 착하다고 전해 주려무나\"라거나 \"루이자, 톡스 양에게 내가 그녀에게 신세를 졌다고 전해 다오\" 하는 식이었다. 이런 특별한 대우들은 그렇게 두드러진 영예를 입은 그 부인에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다.\n\n톡스 양은 어린 돔비가 태어나기도 전에 커비, 비어드, 앤드 커비표 최상급 혼합 핀으로써 그를 맞이하도록 운명의 여신들에게 선택받았으니, 그러므로 그의 존재의 다른 모든 초기 단계마다 다른 모든 형태의 환대로 그를 맞이하는 일이 자연히 자신에게 떨어진 것이라 여겼는지—아니면 넘쳐흐르는 선량함에 이끌려 그의 돌아가신 어머니를 어느 정도 대신하는 자로서 집안의 의용군에 자원한 것인지—아니면 다른 어떤 동기를 스스로 의식하고 있었는지—이런 물음들은 회사 역사의 이 단계에서는 오직 그녀 자신만이 풀 수 있는 것이었다. 또한 이 물음들은, 톡스 양의 변함없음과 열성이 리처즈에게 무거운 낙담을 안겨 주었다는 사실(이는 의심의 여지가 없다)과도 별 상관이 없었으니, 리처즈는 그 후원 아래 시시각각 살이 빠져 갔고, 감독을 받다가 죽음에 이를 위험마저 어느 정도 있었다.\n\n톡스 양은 종종 칙 부인에게, 저 사랑스러운 아이의 성장과 관련된 모든 일에 자신이 품은 관심은 그 무엇으로도 능가할 수 없을 것이라고 단언하곤 했다. 그리고 톡스 양의 행동을 지켜본 사람이라면 그런 단언적 확인이 없더라도 그만큼은 미루어 짐작할 수 있었을 것이다. 그녀는 형언할 수 없는 만족감으로, 거의 리처즈와 그 잔치를 공동으로 소유한 듯한 태도로, 어린 상속자의 순진무구한 식사를 주재하곤 했다. 목욕과 몸단장이라는 자그마한 의식에서도 그녀는 열성적으로 거들었다. 갓난아이에게 약을 먹이는 일은 그녀의 성품에 깃든 온갖 적극적인 동정심을 일깨웠다. 그리고 한번은 돔비 씨가 누이의 안내로 아들을 보려고 육아실에 들어왔을 때—마침 폴이 잠자리에 들 채비를 하는 중에, 짧고 시원한 리넨 윗도리만 걸친 채 리처즈의 가운 위로 비탈을 오르듯 짧은 산책을 하고 있을 무렵—톡스 양은 (얌전을 떨며 달아나 들어간) 벽장 안에 몸을 숨기고 있었는데, 무지한 현재를 넘어서 어찌나 황홀경에 빠졌던지 \"어쩜 이렇게 예쁠까요, 돔비 씨! 큐피드가 따로 없네요, 나리!\" 하고 소리치지 않을 수 없었고, 그러고는 당황과 홍조에 휩싸여 벽장 문 뒤로 거의 주저앉을 뻔했다.\n\n\"루이자,\" 어느 날 돔비 씨가 누이에게 말했다, \"내 생각엔 폴의 세례를 기념해서 네 친구에게 자그마한 기념품이라도 하나 줘야 할 것 같구나. 그 여자가 처음부터 그 아이를 위해 그토록 따뜻하게 애써 왔고, 또 자기 처지를 그토록 철저히 이해하고 있는 듯하니(유감스럽게도 이 세상에서 매우 드문 미덕이지), 그녀를 좀 알아주는 일이 내게도 정말 흐뭇한 일이 될 거다.\"\n\n돔비 씨의 눈에는—이따금 세상 빛을 보는 다른 몇몇 눈에도 그렇듯—자기 처지를 이해한다는 그 대단한 지혜의 경지에 이른 자란, 오로지 그의 처지에 합당한 경의를 표하는 자들뿐이었다는 점을 슬쩍 일러둔다 해도, 그것이 톡스 양의 공로를 깎아내리는 일이 되지는 않을 것이다. 그들의 공로는 자기 자신을 안다는 데 있다기보다, 그를 알고 그 앞에 깊이 머리를 숙인다는 데 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"폴 오라버니,\" 누이가 응수했다, \"톡스 양에게 정당한 평가를 내리시는군요. 오라버니처럼 통찰력 있는 분이라면 분명 그러시리라 제가 알고 있었지요. 만일 영어에 그녀가 거의 숭배에 가까운 존경을 품은 단어가 세 개 있다면, 그 단어는 바로 돔비와 아들일 거예요.\"\n\n\"음,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"나도 그렇게 믿는다. 톡스 양의 명예가 되는 일이지.\"\n\n\"그리고 기념품 같은 것에 관해서라면, 폴 오라버니,\" 누이가 말을 이었다, \"오라버니가 톡스 양에게 무엇을 주든 그것은 분명 성유물처럼 소중히 간직되고 귀히 여겨질 거예요. 하지만 폴 오라버니, 만일 그럴 마음이 있으시다면, 톡스 양의 다정함에 대한 오라버니의 마음을 더욱 기쁘게 하고 받아들여질 만한 방식으로 보여 주는 길이 있긴 해요.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇이냐?\" 돔비 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"대부모는, 물론,\" 칙 부인이 말을 이었다, \"인연과 영향력이라는 면에서 중요하지요.\"\n\n\"내 아들에게 그게 왜 중요한지 모르겠구나,\" 돔비 씨가 차갑게 말했다.\n\n\"지당하신 말씀이에요, 폴 오라버니,\" 칙 부인이 갑작스러운 변심을 감추려고 유난히 활기를 부리며 응수했다. \"오라버니다운 말씀이세요. 다른 말씀을 기대할 수가 없지요. 그런 의견을 가지셨으리라는 걸 진작 알았어야 했는데. 어쩌면,\" 여기서 칙 부인은 그다지 편치 않은 마음으로 길을 더듬듯 다시 머뭇거렸다, \"어쩌면 그게 바로, 톡스 양을 그 귀여운 아이의 대모로 삼는 데 오라버니가 덜 반대하실 만한 이유일지도 모르겠어요. 그저 다른 누군가의 대리인이자 대행자로서 말이지요. 그것이 크나큰 영예이자 영광으로 받아들여지리라는 건, 폴, 굳이 말할 필요도 없지요.\"\n\n\"루이자,\" 돔비 씨가 잠시 침묵한 뒤 말했다, \"설마 그렇게 생각하는 건—\"\n\n\"물론 아니에요,\" 거절을 미리 막으려 서두르며 칙 부인이 외쳤다, \"저는 한 번도 그렇게 생각한 적 없어요.\"\n\n돔비 씨가 짜증스럽게 누이를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저를 당황하게 하지 마세요, 폴 오라버니,\" 누이가 말했다, \"그러면 저는 무너지고 말아요. 저는 도무지 튼튼하질 못해요. 가엾은 패니가 떠난 뒤로 제정신이 아니랍니다.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 누이가 두 눈에 갖다 대는 손수건을 흘끗 보고는 말을 이었다.\n\"설마 그렇게 생각하지는 않겠지, 라고 내가 말하는 거다—\"\n\n\"그리고 저는,\" 칙 부인이 중얼거렸다, \"한 번도 그렇게 생각한 적 없다고 말씀드리는 거예요.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 루이자!\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 폴 오라버니,\" 그녀가 눈물 어린 위엄을 지키며 항변했다, \"저도 정말 말 좀 하게 해 주세요. 저는 오라버니만큼 영리하지도, 사리에 밝지도, 언변이 좋지도, 그 무엇도 못해요. 저도 잘 알아요. 그래서 더 딱한 거지요. 하지만 이게 제가 내뱉을 마지막 말이라 해도—그리고 가엾은 패니가 떠난 마당에, 폴, 마지막 말이란 오라버니와 제게 무척 엄숙한 것이어야 하는데—저는 여전히 한 번도 그렇게 생각한 적 없다고 말하겠어요. 더구나,\" 칙 부인이, 마치 결정적인 논거를 지금까지 아껴 두기라도 했다는 듯 한층 위엄을 더하며 덧붙였다, \"저는 진짜로 단 한 번도 그렇게 생각한 적이 없어요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 창가로 걸어갔다가 다시 돌아왔다.\n\n\"설마 그렇게 생각하지는 않겠지, 루이자,\" 그가 말했다(칙 부인은 자기 깃발을 돛대에 못 박아 걸고는 \"그렇지 않다는 거 알아요\"라고 되풀이했으나, 그는 거기에 아랑곳하지 않았다), \"이런 경우에 내가 누구의 권리든 조금이라도 인정한다고 친다면, 톡스 양보다 더 우선하는 권리를 내게 주장할 사람이 많이 있으리라는 점은 부정할 수 없겠지. 하지만 나는 인정하지 않아. 그런 건 전혀 인정하지 않는다. 폴과 나는, 때가 오면, 스스로를 지켜낼 수 있을 거야—다시 말해, 우리 회사는 스스로를 지키고, 스스로를 유지하고, 그런 흔해 빠진 도움 없이도 스스로의 힘으로 대를 이어 물려줄 수 있을 거다. 사람들이 흔히 제 자식들을 위해 구하는 그런 외부의 도움 따위는 나는 능히 경멸할 수 있어. 나는 그런 것 위에 있는 사람이니까. 그러니 폴의 유아기와 어린 시절이 잘 흘러가서, 그 애가 들어서도록 정해진 그 길을 향해 시간 낭비 없이 자질을 갖추어 가는 모습을 내가 보게 된다면, 나는 만족이야. 그 애가 훗날 인생에서, 회사의 위엄과 신용을 적극적으로 지켜 나가며—가능하다면 넓혀 나가며—마음 내키는 대로 권세 있는 친구들을 사귀게 되겠지. 그때까지는 나 하나로 그 애에게 충분하고, 어쩌면 전부일 게다. 나는 사람들이 우리 사이에 끼어드는 걸 원치 않아. 차라리 네 친구처럼 마땅한 사람의 호의로운 행실에 대한 내 마음을 표하는 편이 훨씬 낫지. 그러니 그렇게 하자꾸나. 그리고 네 남편과 내가 나머지 대부 노릇은 충분히 잘 해낼 수 있을 게다.\"\n\n대단한 위엄과 장중함으로 늘어놓은 이 말들 가운데, 돔비 씨는 진실로 자기 가슴속에 숨은 감정을 드러냈다. 자신과 아들 사이에 누가 끼어드는 것에 대한 형언할 수 없는 불신, 그 아이의 존경과 순종을 두고 어떤 경쟁자나 동업자라도 두게 될까 하는 오만한 두려움, 인간의 의지를 굽히고 묶는 자신의 힘이 결코 틀림없는 것은 아닐지도 모른다는, 근래에 새로 생긴 날카로운 의구심, 두 번째 견제나 방해라도 있을세라 그만큼 날카로운 질투—이런 것들이 그 무렵 그의 영혼을 여는 으뜸 열쇠였다. 그는 평생 한 번도 벗을 사귄 적이 없었다. 차갑고 거리를 두는 그의 천성은 벗을 구한 적도, 찾은 적도 없었다. 그리고 지금, 그 천성이 부성애의 관심과 야심이라는 한쪽으로 치우친 계획에 온 힘을 그토록 강하게 쏟아붓자, 마치 그 얼음 같은 물줄기가 이 영향으로 풀려나 맑고 자유로이 흐르기는커녕, 그 짐을 받아들이려고 한순간 녹았다가 다시 그 짐과 함께 얼어붙어 굽힐 줄 모르는 한 덩어리가 된 듯했다.\n\n이리하여 톡스 양은 자신의 보잘것없음 덕분에 어린 폴의 대모 자리로 격상되어, 그 시각부터 그 직책에 선택되고 임명되었다. 그리고 돔비 씨는 더 나아가, 이미 오래 미뤄진 그 의식을 더는 연기하지 말고 거행해야겠다는 뜻을 밝혔다. 이토록 눈에 띄는 성공은 전혀 예상치 못했던 그의 누이는 가능한 한 빨리 물러나, 그 둘도 없는 벗에게 이 소식을 전했다. 그리고 돔비 씨는 서재에 혼자 남았다. 그는 리처즈를 부르려고 평소처럼 종 줄에 손을 가져갔는데, 그때 그의 눈이 죽은 아내의 것이던 글쓰기용 책상에 가닿았다. 그것은 다른 물건들과 함께 그녀의 방 장식장에서 꺼내온 것이었다. 그의 눈이 거기에 머문 것은 처음이 아니었다. 그는 주머니에 그 열쇠를 지니고 다녔다. 그는 그것을 탁자로 가져와, 미리 방문을 잠근 다음, 익숙한 손길로 열었다.\n\n찢기고 줄 그어진 종이 부스러기들이 쌓인 한 겹 아래에서, 그는 온전히 남은 편지 한 통을 꺼냈다. 이 문서를 펼치며 저도 모르게 숨을 죽이고, 그 은밀한 동작 속에서 평소의 오만한 태도를 다소 누그러뜨린 채, 그는 한 손으로 머리를 받치고 앉아 그것을 끝까지 읽었다.\n\n그는 천천히, 주의 깊게, 음절 하나하나에 세심한 정성을 기울여 읽었다. 그토록 신중한 태도가 부자연스러워 보이고, 어쩌면 그만큼이나 큰 노력의 결과로 보였다는 점 말고는, 그는 어떤 감정의 기색도 새어 나오게 두지 않았다. 다 읽고 나자 그는 그것을 천천히 여러 번 접고 또 접더니, 조심스럽게 조각조각 찢었다. 그것들을 내던지려던 손을 멈추고는, 다시 맞춰져 해독될지도 모를 운에마저 맡기기 싫다는 듯 주머니에 넣었다. 그리고 평소처럼 어린 폴을 불러오라고 종을 울리는 대신, 그는 그 음울한 방에서 저녁 내내 홀로 앉아 있었다.\n\n그러나 육아실은 외로움과는 거리가 멀었다. 그곳에서는 칙 부인과 톡스 양이 즐거운 저녁을 보내고 있었는데, 수전 니퍼 양은 그것이 어찌나 못마땅했던지 기회만 있으면 문 뒤에서 얼굴을 찡그렸다. 그녀는 이 일로 감정이 어찌나 격해졌던지, 청중이나 동조자의 위안 하나 없이도 그렇게라도 감정을 풀어내지 않고는 견딜 수 없었다. 옛 편력 기사들이 사막이며 황야며, 그것을 읽어 줄 사람이 있을 가망이라곤 없는 그 밖의 야만스러운 곳에 사랑하는 여인의 이름을 새기며 마음을 달랬듯이, 수전 니퍼 양도 서랍과 옷장에 대고 들창코를 찡그리고, 벽장 안에 멸시의 눈짓을 감추어 두고, 돌 항아리에 비웃는 곁눈질을 흘려 넣고, 복도에 나가 대들고 욕을 퍼부었다.\n\n그러나 이 두 침입자는 그 처녀의 심정 따위는 더없이 행복하게도 까맣게 모른 채, 어린 폴이 옷 벗기, 시원한 운동, 저녁 식사, 잠자리에 이르는 온갖 단계를 무사히 거치는 것을 지켜보았고, 그런 다음 난롯불 앞에 앉아 차를 마셨다. 두 아이는 이제 폴리의 주선으로 한 방에 누워 있었는데, 두 부인이 찻상에 자리를 잡고 나서야 비로소 우연히 자그마한 침대들 쪽을 바라보다가 플로렌스를 떠올렸다.\n\n\"참 곤히도 자네!\" 톡스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 글쎄, 얘야, 저 애는 하루 종일 운동을 어찌나 많이 하는지,\" 칙 부인이 응수했다, \"어린 폴 곁에서 그렇게 노니 말이야.\"\n\n\"신기한 아이예요,\" 톡스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"얘,\" 칙 부인이 낮은 목소리로 응수했다, \"제 어미를 쏙 빼닮았다니까!\"\n\n\"저-런!\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"어머나 가엾어라!\"\n\n톡스 양은 더없이 유별난 연민의 어조로 그렇게 말했는데, 사실은 그것이 자기에게 기대되는 반응이라는 것 말고는 왜 그래야 하는지 뚜렷한 생각도 없었다.\n\n\"플로렌스는 결코, 결코, 결코 돔비가 되지 못할 거야,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"천 살까지 산다 해도 안 될 거야.\"\n\n톡스 양은 눈썹을 치켜올리며 다시금 가엾은 마음에 가득 찼다.\n\n\"나는 저 애 때문에 정말이지 속을 끓이고 마음을 졸인다니까,\" 겸손한 미덕의 한숨을 쉬며 칙 부인이 말했다. \"저 애가 나이 들면 대체 어떻게 될지, 어떤 처지를 차지하게 될지 정말 모르겠어. 제 아빠한테 조금도 환심을 못 사는걸. 저렇게 돔비를 안 닮았으니, 어떻게 그러기를 바라겠어?\"\n\n톡스 양은 그토록 설득력 있는 논거에서 빠져나갈 길이라곤 도무지 보이지 않는다는 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"그리고 저 애는, 알다시피,\" 칙 부인이 깊은 속내를 털어놓듯 말했다, \"가엾은 패니의 천성을 그대로 물려받았어. 장담컨대 저 애는 훗날 인생에서 결코 노력이란 걸 하지 않을 거야. 결코! 저 애는 결코 제 아빠의 마음을 칭칭 감고 휘감지 못할 거야, 마치—\"\n\n\"담쟁이덩굴처럼요?\" 톡스 양이 거들었다.\n\n\"담쟁이덩굴처럼,\" 칙 부인이 수긍했다. \"결코! 저 애는 결코 제 아빠의 애정의 품 안으로 사뿐히 미끄러져 들어가 안기지 못할 거야, 마치—그—\"\n\n\"놀란 새끼 사슴처럼요?\" 톡스 양이 거들었다.\n\n\"놀란 새끼 사슴처럼,\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"결코! 가엾은 패니! 그래도, 내가 그 애를 얼마나 사랑했던지!\"\n\n\"너무 괴로워하지 마세요, 얘,\" 톡스 양이 달래는 목소리로 말했다. \"정말이지! 당신은 정이 너무 깊어요.\"\n\n\"우리 모두 결점이야 있지,\" 칙 부인이 울며 고개를 저었다. \"분명 우리도 그렇겠지. 나는 한 번도 그 애의 결점에 눈감은 적 없어. 그런 적 없다고 말한 적도 없고. 천만에. 그래도 내가 그 애를 얼마나 사랑했던지!\"\n\n칙 부인에게는—여성다운 지성과 온화함의 천사라 할 만했던 올케에 비하면 그저 흔해 빠진 어리석음 덩어리에 지나지 않았던 그녀에게는—그 부인의 기억을 너그럽게 굽어보며 다정을 베푸는 일이, 그것도 생전에 그 부인을 대하던 행실 그대로, 그리하여 자기 자신을 철저히 믿고 자기 자신을 감쪽같이 속이며, 그 관용을 빌미로 유난히 흐뭇해하는 일이 어찌나 흡족했던지! 우리가 옳을 때조차 그토록 유쾌한 관용이라는 미덕이, 우리가 틀렸을 때도 그렇게나 유쾌하고, 더구나 우리가 어떻게 그것을 행사할 특권을 갖게 되었는지 도무지 증명할 수 없을 때조차 그렇다니, 관용이란 얼마나 굉장히 유쾌한 미덕인가!\n\n칙 부인이 아직 눈물을 훔치며 고개를 젓고 있을 때, 리처즈가 용기를 내어 플로렌스 아가씨가 잠에서 깨어 침대에 일어나 앉아 있다고 알렸다. 유모의 말로는, 아이가 일어났는데 속눈썹이 눈물로 젖어 있더라는 것이었다. 그러나 그 눈물이 반짝이는 것을 본 사람은 폴리 말고는 아무도 없었다. 그 위로 몸을 굽혀 달래는 말을 속삭여 준 사람도, 두근거리는 그 심장의 떨림을 들을 만큼 가까이 다가간 사람도 달리 없었다.\n\n\"오! 유모 아줌마!\" 아이가 그녀의 얼굴을 진지하게 올려다보며 말했다, \"동생 옆에서 자게 해 주세요!\"\n\n\"왜 그러니, 우리 아가?\" 리처즈가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 동생은 나를 사랑하는 것 같아요,\" 아이가 격하게 외쳤다. \"동생 옆에서 자게 해 주세요. 제발요!\"\n\n칙 부인이 착한 아이답게 자야지 하는 어미다운 말로 끼어들었으나, 플로렌스는 겁먹은 표정으로, 흐느낌과 눈물에 끊기는 목소리로 애원을 거듭했다.\n\n\"깨우지 않을게요,\" 아이가 얼굴을 가리고 고개를 떨구며 말했다. \"그냥 손으로만 만지고 잘게요. 오, 제발, 제발, 오늘 밤만 동생 옆에서 자게 해 주세요, 동생은 나를 좋아하는 것 같단 말이에요!\"\n\n리처즈는 아무 말 없이 아이를 안아, 갓난아이가 자고 있는 자그마한 침대로 데려가 그 곁에 뉘었다. 아이는 동생의 잠을 방해하지 않을 만큼 최대한 가까이 다가가 기어들었다. 그러고는 한 팔을 뻗어 머뭇거리듯 동생의 목을 감싸 안고, 다른 팔에 얼굴을 묻은 채—그 위로 축축하고 흐트러진 머리카락이 풀어져 흘러내렸다—꼼짝 않고 누워 있었다.\n\n\"가엾은 것,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"꿈을 꾸었나 봐요.\"\n\n꿈을 꾸었던 것이리라, 어쩌면, 영원히 잠잠해진 다정한 목소리를, 영원히 감겨 버린 다정한 눈을, 다시 한번 자기를 감싸 안았다가 그 꿈속에서, 어떤 혀로도 풀어낼 수 없는 그 둑 안에서 스르르 풀려 가는 다정한 팔을. 어쩌면 찾고 있었으리라—꿈속에서—그토록 어린 아이의 것이건만 깊고도 아프게 상처 입은 마음을 위한 어떤 자연스러운 위안을. 그리고 어쩌면 찾아냈으리라, 꿈속에서, 깨어 있을 때가 아니라면, 차갑고 실체 있는 진실을. 이 사소한 사건이 대화의 흐름을 어찌나 끊어 놓았던지 다시 잇기가 어려웠다. 게다가 칙 부인은 자기 자신의 관대한 천성을 곱씹다 보니 너무도 감동에 젖어 기운이 나질 않았다. 그리하여 두 벗은 곧 차를 끝마쳤고, 하인 하나가 톡스 양을 위해 삯마차를 부르러 보내졌다. 톡스 양은 삯마차에 관한 한 경험이 풍부해서, 그것을 타고 출발하는 일은 으레 시간이 걸리는 작업이었는데, 사전 준비 절차에서 늘 체계적이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"미안하지만, 타울린슨,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"무엇보다 먼저, 펜과 잉크를 가지고 나가서 마차 번호를 또렷이 적어 주겠어요?\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨,\" 타울린슨이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고, 미안하지만, 타울린슨,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"방석을 좀 뒤집어 주겠어요. 그건,\" 톡스 양이 칙 부인에게 따로 말했다, \"대개 축축하답니다, 얘.\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨,\" 타울린슨이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 또 폐를 끼쳐서 미안하지만, 타울린슨,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"이 명함하고 이 실링을 좀 부탁해요. 마부더러 명함에 적힌 곳으로 몰고 가라고, 그리고 무슨 일이 있어도 이 실링보다 더는 못 받는다는 걸 알아두라고 해 줘요.\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨,\" 타울린슨이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고—이렇게 폐를 많이 끼쳐 미안해요, 타울린슨,\" 톡스 양이 그를 수심에 잠긴 듯 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"천만에요, 아가씨,\" 타울린슨이 말했다.\n\n\"그러면, 미안하지만, 타울린슨,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"마부에게 이 부인의 삼촌이 치안판사라는 걸, 그리고 만일 이 부인에게 조금이라도 무례하게 굴면 끔찍하게 벌을 받을 거라는 걸 일러둬요. 미안하지만 그렇게 말해 주면 돼요, 타울린슨, 친근한 투로, 그리고 또 다른 어떤 남자한테 그렇게 했더니 그 사람이 죽었다는 걸 당신이 알고 있어서 그러는 것처럼 말이에요.\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 아가씨,\" 타울린슨이 말했다.\n\n\"자 그럼 이제 잘 자거라, 내 사랑스럽고, 사랑스럽고, 사랑스러운 대자야,\" 톡스 양이 형용사를 되풀이할 때마다 부드러운 입맞춤을 한바탕 퍼부으며 말했다. \"그리고 루이자, 내 소중한 벗이여, 자기 전에 따뜻한 걸 좀 들고, 너무 괴로워하지 않겠다고 약속해 줘요!\"\n\n검은 눈의 니퍼는 이 고비에, 그리고 그 뒤 칙 부인이 떠날 때까지, 줄곧 꼼짝 않고 지켜보면서 자신을 억누르느라 더없이 애를 먹었다. 그러나 마침내 육아실에 손님이 없어지자, 그녀는 조금 전 억눌렀던 것에 대한 보상을 스스로에게 톡톡히 베풀었다.\n\n\"나를 육 주 동안 구속복에 가둬 둔다 해도,\" 니퍼가 말했다, \"그걸 벗고 나면 더 화만 돋울걸요, 저 두 그리핀* 같은 인간들을 본 적이 있어요, 리처즈 부인?\"\n* 그리핀: 사자 몸에 독수리 머리와 날개를 단 전설 속 괴수. 여기서는 못마땅한 두 부인을 빗댄 말.\n\n\"게다가 꿈을 꾼 거라느니, 가엾은 우리 아가!\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 잘들 노시는군!\" 수전 니퍼가 두 부인이 떠난 문을 향해 인사하는 시늉을 하며 외쳤다. \"결코 돔비가 되지 못할 거라고요? 안 되기를 바라야지요, 우린 더는 그런 사람 필요 없으니까, 하나로 충분하다고요.\"\n\n\"애들 깨우지 마요, 수전,\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"리처즈 부인, 정말 황송하기 그지없네요,\" 수전이 말했는데, 그녀는 도무지 화풀이 대상을 가리는 법이 없었다, \"검둥이 노예에 흑백 혼혈인 주제에 부인의 분부를 받드는 것을 영광으로 여긴답니다. 리처즈 부인, 다른 분부가 또 있으면 어서 말씀하시지요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말도 안 되는 소리, 분부라니,\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 어쩜 이렇게 마음씨도 고우실까, 리처즈 부인,\" 수전이 외쳤다, \"이 집에선 임시직이 항상 정규직한테 분부를 내린답니다, 그것도 몰랐어요, 아니 대체 어디서 태어나신 거예요, 리처즈 부인? 하지만 어디서 태어났든, 리처즈 부인,\" 스핏파이어*가 단호히 고개를 저으며 말을 이었다, \"그리고 언제, 어떻게 태어났든(그건 부인 자신이 제일 잘 알겠지요만), 부디 명심하세요, 분부를 내리는 것과 그걸 받드는 것은 전혀 별개의 일이라는 걸요. 어떤 사람이 다른 사람한테 다리에서 머리부터 거꾸로 사십오 피트 물속으로 뛰어들라고 시킬 순 있어요, 리처즈 부인, 하지만 그 사람이 뛰어드는 것과는 거리가 한참 멀 수도 있다고요.\"\n* 스핏파이어: '성깔 있는 사람'을 뜻하는 별명. 여기서는 수전 니퍼를 가리킨다.\n\n\"이것 봐요,\" 폴리가 말했다, \"당신은 착한 사람인 데다 플로렌스 아가씨를 좋아하니까 화가 난 거예요. 그런데 달리 화풀이할 사람이 없으니 나한테 돌아서는 거고요.\"\n\n\"어떤 사람들은 성질 참기도, 부드럽게 말하기도 쉽지요, 리처즈 부인,\" 수전이 조금 누그러져 응수했다, \"제 아이가 왕자님처럼 떠받들어지고, 제 곁 사람들이 차라리 멀리 가 버렸으면 싶을 만큼 쓰다듬어지고 어루만져질 땐 말이에요. 하지만 모진 말 한마디 듣지도, 들어서도 안 될 사랑스럽고 어리고 예쁘고 죄 없는 아이가 깎아내려질 땐, 사정이 정말 영 딴판이지요. 어머나 세상에, 플로이 아가씨, 이 못되고 나쁜 아이야, 당장 눈 안 감으면, 다락에 사는 도깨비들을 불러다가 산 채로 잡아먹게 할 테야!\"\n\n여기서 니퍼 양은 자기 직책의 엄중한 의무를 어서 다하지 못해 안달이 난 어떤 양심적인 황소 도깨비에게서 나옴직한 무시무시한 울음소리를 흉내 냈다. 그러고는 이불로 어린 아가씨의 머리를 덮고 베개를 서너 번 성난 손길로 두들겨 어린 보호 대상을 더 진정시킨 뒤, 팔짱을 끼고 입을 꼭 다문 채, 저녁 내내 난롯불을 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n어린 폴은 육아실 식 표현으로 \"제 나이치고 꽤 눈치가 빠르다\"는 말을 들었지만, 정작 이 모든 일에 대해서는 그다음다음 날의 세례식 준비에 무관심하듯 별 관심을 두지 않았다. 그럼에도 그 준비는, 그의 옷차림이며 누이의 옷차림이며 두 유모의 옷차림에 이르기까지, 그를 둘러싸고 매우 활기차게 진행되었다. 또한 그는 정해진 날 아침이 밝아 와도 그날의 중요성을 조금도 알아차리는 기색이 없었으니, 오히려 유난히 잠이 쏟아지고, 외출 차림으로 옷을 입히는 시중꾼들에게 유난히 못마땅해할 뿐이었다.\n\n마침 무쇠처럼 잿빛인 가을날이었고, 날카로운 동풍이 불고 있었다—이날의 행사와 잘 어울리는 날씨였다. 돔비 씨는 그 자신이 이 세례식의 바람이요, 그늘이요, 가을이었다. 그는 날씨만큼이나 굳고 차갑게 서재에 서서 손님들을 맞이했다. 유리로 된 방을 통해 작은 정원의 나무들을 내다보자, 갈색과 노란색 잎들이 마치 그가 시들게 하기라도 한 듯 팔랑팔랑 떨어져 내렸다.\n\n윽! 검고 차가운 방들이었다. 마치 이 집 식구들처럼 상복을 입고 있는 듯했다. 책들은 크기가 정확히 맞춰져, 병사들처럼 줄지어 늘어선 채, 그 차갑고 딱딱하고 매끈한 제복 차림으로, 마치 자기들끼리 단 하나의 관념만을 품고 있는 듯, 그리고 그것이 냉동기인 듯 보였다. 유리를 끼우고 자물쇠를 채운 책장은 일체의 친밀함을 거부했다. 청동상이 된 피트 씨*는 그 천상의 기원이라곤 흔적도 남기지 않은 채 맨 꼭대기에서, 마법에 걸린 무어인처럼 그 가닿을 수 없는 보물을 지키고 있었다. 높다란 모퉁이마다 놓인, 옛 무덤에서 파낸 먼지투성이 항아리 하나하나가 두 개의 설교단에서처럼 황폐와 쇠락을 설교했다. 그리고 돔비 씨와 그의 초상화를 단번에 비추는 벽난로 거울은 우울한 명상으로 가득 찬 듯했다.\n\n* 피트 씨: 윌리엄 피트(영국 총리)의 흉상을 말한다.\n뻣뻣하고 앙상한 난로 연장들은, 단추 채운 외투에 흰 크라바트, 묵직한 금시곗줄, 삐걱거리는 부츠를 갖춘 돔비 씨와, 그곳의 다른 무엇보다 더 가까운 친족 관계를 주장하는 듯 보였다. 그러나 이는 그의 합법적 친척인 칙 부부가 도착하기 전의 일이었고, 그들은 곧 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"폴 오라버니,\" 칙 부인이 그를 끌어안으며 속삭였다, \"수많은 기쁜 날들의 시작이 되기를 바라요!\"\n\n\"고맙다, 루이자,\" 돔비 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"잘 지냈나, 존?\"\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 처남?\" 칙이 말했다.\n\n그는 마치 감전이라도 될까 두려운 듯 돔비 씨에게 손을 내밀었다. 돔비 씨는 그것을 마치 물고기나 해초, 또는 그 비슷한 물컹한 물건이라도 되는 양 받아 들고는, 곧바로 한껏 정중하게 그에게 돌려주었다.\n\n\"루이자,\" 돔비 씨가 마치 크라바트가 받침대라도 되는 양 그 안에서 고개를 살짝 돌리며 말했다, \"혹시 불을 피우는 편이 좋았겠느냐?\"\n\n\"오, 폴 오라버니, 아니에요,\" 이를 딱딱 부딪치지 않으려고 무진 애를 쓰며 칙 부인이 말했다, \"저는 괜찮아요.\"\n\n\"존,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"자넨 한기가 느껴지지 않나?\"\n\n존은 이미 두 손을 손목까지 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채, 전에 칙 부인을 그토록 거슬리게 했던 바로 그 개 짖는 듯한 합창의 문턱에 막 서 있던 참이었는데, 자기는 더없이 편안하다고 단언했다.\n\n그는 낮은 목소리로 \"내 티들 톨 투르 룰\" 하고 덧붙였다—그때 마침 다행스럽게도 타울린슨이 가로막으며 알렸다.\n\"톡스 양 드십니다!\"\n\n그러자 그 어여쁜 매혹자가 들어섰으니, 푸르스름한 코에 형언할 수 없이 얼어붙은 얼굴이었다. 이는 그녀가 의식에 예를 표하느라 펄럭이는 자투리들의 미로 속에 아주 얇게 차려입은 탓이었다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 톡스 양?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n톡스 양은 펼쳐진 거즈 천 한가운데서 마치 오페라글라스가 접히듯 그대로 폭 내려앉았다. 그녀를 맞으려 돔비 씨가 한두 걸음 다가서자, 그것에 답하느라 어찌나 낮게 무릎을 굽혀 인사했던지.\n\n\"저는 결코 이날을 잊지 못할 거예요, 나리,\" 톡스 양이 부드럽게 말했다. \"도무지 불가능하지요. 사랑하는 루이자, 내 오감의 증거를 믿기조차 어렵네요.\"\n\n만일 톡스 양이 자기 오감 중 하나의 증거를 믿을 수 있었다면, 그날은 무척 추운 날이었다. 그것만은 아주 분명했다. 그녀는 일찌감치 기회를 잡아, 코끝의 혈액 순환을 돕고자 손수건으로 남몰래 코를 문질렀으니, 아기에게 입을 맞출 때 그 너무도 낮은 온도로 아기를 불쾌하게 놀라게 할까 봐서였다.\n\n아기는 곧 모습을 드러냈는데, 리처즈가 한껏 위풍당당하게 안고 나왔다. 한편 플로렌스는 저 민첩한 어린 순경 수전 니퍼의 호위를 받으며 뒤를 따랐다. 육아실 일행은 이때쯤 처음보다는 옅은 상복 차림이었으나, 그래도 부모 잃은 아이들의 모습에는 그날을 조금도 더 밝게 해 줄 수 없게 만드는 무언가가 충분히 깃들어 있었다. 아기도 또한—어쩌면 톡스 양의 코 때문이었는지—울기 시작했다. 마침 그 바람에, 칙 씨가 자기 나름의 매우 정직한 뜻을 어색하게 실행에 옮기는 일을 막았으니, 그 뜻이란 플로렌스를 귀여워해 주려는 것이었다. 이 신사는, 완벽한 돔비의 우월한 권리에는 둔감했으나(어쩌면 자기 자신이 돔비 집안과 결합하는 영예를 누린 까닭에, 그 빼어남에 익숙해진 탓이었으리라), 진심으로 그 아이를 좋아했고, 또 좋아한다는 것을 드러냈으며, 지금 막 제 나름의 방식으로 그것을 드러내려던 참이었다. 그런데 폴이 울음을 터뜨렸고, 그의 조력자가 그를 다급히 가로막았다—\n\n\"자 플로렌스, 얘야!\" 그녀의 고모가 활기차게 말했다, \"뭐 하고 있니, 아가? 동생한테 네 모습을 보여 주렴. 동생의 관심을 끌어 봐, 얘야!\"\n\n돔비 씨가 자기 어린 딸을 차갑게 지켜보며 서 있는 동안, 분위기는 점점 더 차가워졌거나, 그럴 뻔했다. 딸아이는 손뼉을 치며 발끝으로 서서, 아들이자 상속자인 동생의 옥좌 앞에서, 그 높은 지위에서 몸을 굽혀 자기를 봐 달라고 동생을 꾀었다. 리처즈의 어떤 정직한 행동이 그 효과를 거들었는지도 모르나, 아무튼 동생은 정말로 아래를 내려다보았고, 잠자코 있었다. 누이가 유모 뒤로 숨자, 동생은 눈으로 그녀를 좇았다. 그리고 그녀가 동생에게 즐겁게 소리치며 빼꼼 내다보자, 동생은 벌떡 일어나 우렁차게 까르륵 소리를 냈고—그녀가 자기에게 달려들자 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸으며—그녀가 입맞춤으로 동생을 뒤덮는 동안, 자그마한 손으로 그녀의 곱슬머리를 어루만지는 듯했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 이 광경을 보고 기뻤을까? 그는 신경 하나 풀리는 것으로도 기쁨을 드러내지 않았다. 하지만 어떤 종류의 감정이든 그것을 겉으로 드러내는 일은 그에게 드문 일이었다. 설령 어떤 햇살이 방 안으로 슬며시 들어와 노는 아이들을 비추었다 한들, 그것은 결코 그의 얼굴에 가닿지 못했다. 그가 어찌나 뚫어지게, 또 차갑게 바라보았던지, 마침내 어린 플로렌스의 웃는 눈이 우연히 그의 눈과 마주쳤을 때, 그 따뜻한 빛마저 사라져 버렸다.\n\n과연 흐리고 잿빛이 도는 가을날이었다. 그리고 잠시 말이 끊기고 침묵이 흐르는 동안, 잎들이 서글피 떨어져 내렸다.\n\n\"존,\" 돔비 씨가 시계를 보며 모자와 장갑을 갖추고 말했다. \"미안하지만 내 누이를 부탁하네. 오늘 내 팔은 톡스 양의 것이라서. 리처즈, 자네가 폴 도련님을 모시고 먼저 가는 게 좋겠어. 아주 조심하게.\"\n\n돔비 씨의 마차에는 돔비와 아들, 톡스 양, 칙 부인, 리처즈, 그리고 플로렌스가 탔다. 그 뒤를 따르는 작은 마차에는 수전 니퍼와 그 마차의 주인 격인 칙 씨가 탔다. 수전은 저 신사의 커다란 얼굴을 마주하는 거북함을 면하려고 쉴 새 없이 창밖을 내다보며, 무언가 달그락거리는 소리가 날 때마다 그가 자기에게 줄 적당한 금전적 사례를 종이에 싸고 있는 거라고 생각했다.\n\n교회로 가는 길에 한 번, 돔비 씨는 아들을 즐겁게 해 주려고 손뼉을 쳤다. 이런 부성애의 발로에 톡스 양은 황홀해했다. 그러나 이 일을 빼면, 세례식 일행과 상가 마차에 탄 일행 사이의 주된 차이라곤 마차와 말의 빛깔뿐이었다.\n\n교회 계단에 다다르자, 그들은 위풍당당한 교구지기의 영접을 받았다. 돔비 씨는 부인들을 내리는 것을 돕고자 먼저 마차에서 내려, 교회 문 가까이 그의 곁에 서니, 또 다른 교구지기처럼 보였다. 덜 호화롭지만 더 무시무시한 교구지기, 사생활의 교구지기, 우리 일과 우리 가슴속의 교구지기 말이다.\n\n톡스 양은 자기 손을 돔비 씨의 팔에 슬며시 끼고, 삼각모와 바빌론식 옷깃을 앞세워 계단을 인도받아 오르는 자신을 느끼며, 손을 떨었다. 그 순간 그것은 마치 또 다른 엄숙한 제도와도 같았다, \"그대는 이 남자를 맞이하겠소, 루크레티아?\" \"예, 그러겠어요.\"\n\n\"바람 부는 그곳에서 어서 아이를 안으로 데려오십시오,\" 교구지기가 교회 안쪽 문을 열어 잡으며 속삭였다.\n\n어린 폴은 햄릿처럼 \"내 무덤 속으로?\"* 하고 물을 법도 했으니, 그곳은 그토록 한기 가득하고 흙냄새가 났다. 천으로 가린 높다란 설교단과 낭독대, 회랑 아래로 멀리 뻗은 빈 신도석들의 음울한 풍경, 지붕까지 솟아올라 거대하고 음산한 오르간의 그늘 속으로 사라지는 빈 의자들, 먼지투성이 깔개와 차가운 돌판들, 측랑의 으스스한 무료 좌석들, 그리고 종 줄 옆 축축한 모퉁이—거기엔 장례에 쓰는 검은 받침대들이 삽 몇 자루, 바구니 몇 개, 죽음을 연상시키는 밧줄 한두 사리와 함께 치워져 있었다—그 낯설고 유별나고 불쾌한 냄새, 그리고 시체 같은 빛까지, 이 모든 것이 한 가락으로 어우러져 있었다. 그것은 춥고 음울한 광경이었다.\n* \"내 무덤 속으로?\": 셰익스피어 『햄릿』에서 따온 구절.\n\n\"방금 결혼식이 하나 진행 중입니다, 나리,\" 교구지기가 말했다, \"하지만 곧 끝날 테니, 이쪽 제의실로 들어와 계시면 됩니다.\"\n\n다시 앞장서 길을 안내하려 돌아서기 전에, 그는 돔비 씨에게 절을 하고 알아본다는 듯 반쯤 미소를 지었으니, 그 뜻인즉 자기(교구지기)가 그분이 부인을 묻을 때 모시는 기쁨을 누렸던 것을 기억하고 있으며, 그 후로 즐겁게 지내셨기를 바란다는 것이었다.\n\n제단 앞을 지날 때 그 결혼식조차 음울해 보였다. 신부는 너무 늙었고 신랑은 너무 젊었으며, 한쪽 눈은 멀어 그 텅 빈 눈에 안경알을 박은, 한물간 멋쟁이가 신부를 넘겨주고 있었고, 하객들은 떨고 있었다. 제의실에서는 난롯불이 연기를 피우고 있었고, 너무 늙고 너무 혹사당하고 너무 박봉인 변호사 사무원 하나가 \"기록을 뒤지며\", 매장 기록으로 가득 찬 어마어마한 등록부(비슷한 책들이 길게 이어진 가운데 한 권)의 양피지 책장을 집게손가락으로 짚어 내려가고 있었다. 벽난로 위에는 교회 아래 지하 납골당의 평면도가 걸려 있었는데, 칙 씨가 일행의 흥을 돋우려고 그 글자 부분을 소리 내어 훑어보다가, 미처 멈추기도 전에 돔비 부인의 무덤에 관한 항목을 빠짐없이 읽어 버리고 말았다.\n\n또 한 차례 추운 막간이 지난 뒤, 교회보다는 차라리 묘지에나 어울릴 천식을 앓는, 쌕쌕거리는 자그마한 좌석 안내인이 그들을 세례반으로 불렀다. 그것은 뻣뻣한 대리석 대야로, 무미건조한 받침대를 짝 삼아 컵앤볼* 놀이를 하다가 마침 그 위에 막 얹힌 채로 붙잡히기라도 한 듯 보였다. 여기서 그들은 결혼식 일행이 등록을 마치는 동안 잠시 기다렸다. 그리고 그동안 쌕쌕거리는 자그마한 좌석 안내인은—일부는 그 병약함 탓이고, 일부는 결혼식 일행이 자기를 잊지 않게 하려는 셈으로—고래처럼 기침을 해 대며 건물 안을 돌아다녔다.\n\n* 컵앤볼: 막대 끝의 컵에 실로 매단 공을 받아 올리는 놀이.\n이윽고 사무원(그곳에서 유일하게 쾌활해 보이는 존재였는데, 정작 장의사였다)이 따뜻한 물 한 주전자를 들고 와서, 그것을 세례반에 부으며 한기를 가시게 하는 거라는 둥 무어라 말했으나, 그것은 끓는 물 수백만 갤런으로도 이번 경우에는 해낼 수 없는 일이었다. 그러고 나서 성직자가, 상냥하고 온화해 보이는 젊은 부목사였으나 보아하니 아기를 무서워하는 기색이 역력했는데, 마치 유령 이야기의 주인공처럼 \"온통 흰옷을 입은 키 큰 형상\"으로 나타났다. 그 모습을 보자 폴은 울음으로 허공을 찢었고, 시퍼렇게 질린 얼굴로 들려 나갈 때까지 한 번도 그치지 않았다.\n\n모두에게 크나큰 안도를 안기며 그 일이 벌어진 뒤에도, 의식이 진행되는 내내 그가 현관 아래에서 우는 소리가 들려왔으니, 이제는 잦아들었다가, 이제는 커졌다가, 이제는 잠잠해졌다가, 다시금 억누를 수 없는 억울함을 토하며 터져 나오곤 했다. 이것이 두 부인의 주의를 어찌나 산만하게 했던지, 칙 부인은 좌석 안내인을 통해 전갈을 내보내려고 끊임없이 가운뎃 통로로 진출했고, 톡스 양은 자기 기도서를 화약 음모 사건* 부분에 펼쳐 놓은 채 이따금 그 예배문에서 응답 구절을 읽곤 했다.\n\n* 화약 음모 사건: 1605년의 사건을 기념하는 예배가 영국 기도서에 실려 있었다.\n이 모든 절차가 진행되는 동안, 돔비 씨는 여느 때처럼 냉담하고 신사다운 태도를 유지했으며, 어쩌면 그 덕에 어찌나 추워졌던지 젊은 부목사가 글을 읽는 동안 입에서 김이 새어 나올 정도였다. 그가 조금이라도 얼굴을 풀었던 단 한 번은, 성직자가 (매우 꾸밈없고 소박하게) 대부모가 장차 그 아이를 가르쳐 시험할 일에 관한 마무리 권면을 전하던 중, 마침 칙 씨에게 눈길을 멈추었을 때였다. 그때 돔비 씨는, 그가 그 일을 게을리하다가는 단단히 혼쭐을 내 주겠다는 뜻을 위엄 어린 눈빛으로 드러내는 듯 보였다.\n\n돔비 씨가 자기 위엄에 대해서는 조금 덜 생각하고, 자기가 그토록 격식 차리고 뻣뻣하게 한몫 끼어든 그 의식의 위대한 기원과 목적에 대해서는 조금 더 생각했더라면, 그에게 더 좋았을지도 모른다. 그의 오만함은 그 의식의 내력과 묘하게 대조되었다.\n\n모든 것이 끝나자, 그는 다시 톡스 양에게 팔을 내주어 제의실로 인도했고, 거기서 성직자에게, 만찬에 모시는 영예를 청할 수 있었다면 자기로서는 얼마나 기뻤겠느냐만, 안타깝게도 집안 형편이 그러하지 못하다고 알렸다. 등록부에 서명을 마치고, 사례비를 치르고, 좌석 안내인(기침이 또 무척 심해진)을 챙기고, 교구지기를 흡족하게 해 주고, 묘지기(마침 우연히 문간 계단에 서서 날씨를 자못 흥미롭게 살피고 있던)도 잊지 않은 다음, 그들은 다시 마차에 올라 똑같이 황량한 동행 속에 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n집에 도착하니 피트 씨*가 차가운 냉찬 앞에서 코를 치켜올리고 있었는데, 그 냉찬은 유리그릇과 은그릇의 싸늘한 위세 속에 차려져, 정겨운 다과라기보다는 죽은 만찬이 빈소에 안치된 꼴에 가까웠다. 그들이 도착하자 톡스 양은 대자에게 줄 머그잔을 내놓았고, 칙 씨는 갑에 든 나이프와 포크와 숟가락을 내놓았다. 돔비 씨도 톡스 양에게 줄 팔찌를 내놓았는데, 이 선물을 받자 톡스 양은 가슴 뭉클하게 감격했다.\n* 피트 씨: 앞서 언급한 청동 흉상을 가리킨다.\n\n\"존,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"미안하지만 식탁 끝자리에 좀 앉아 주겠나? 자네 앞에 놓인 건 뭔가, 존?\"\n\n\"여긴 송아지 등심 냉채가 있습니다, 처남,\" 칙 씨가 곱은 두 손을 세게 비비며 대답했다. \"거긴 뭐가 있습니까, 처남?\"\n\n\"이건,\" 돔비 씨가 응수했다, \"송아지 머리 냉채인 것 같군. 냉계며—햄이며—파이며—샐러드며—바닷가재가 보이는군. 톡스 양, 포도주를 좀 드시는 영광을 베풀어 주시겠소? 톡스 양께 샴페인을.\"\n\n모든 것에 이가 시렸다. 포도주는 어찌나 쓰리도록 차가웠던지 톡스 양에게서 작은 비명을 끌어냈고, 그녀는 그것을 \"에헴!\" 소리로 바꾸느라 무진 애를 먹었다. 송아지 고기는 어찌나 바람 잘 통하는 식료품 저장실에서 나왔던지, 칙 씨는 그 첫맛에 사지에 차가운 납이 닿는 듯한 감각을 느꼈다. 오직 돔비 씨만이 꿈쩍하지 않았다. 그는 러시아 시장에 얼어붙은 신사의 표본으로 내걸려 팔려도 좋을 법했다.\n\n이 압도적인 기운은 그의 누이에게마저 너무 버거웠다. 그녀는 아첨이나 잡담을 시도하지도 않은 채, 가능한 한 따뜻해 보이려는 데에만 온 힘을 쏟았다.\n\n\"자, 처남,\" 칙 씨가 긴 침묵 끝에 필사적으로 뛰어들 듯, 셰리주 한 잔을 채우며 말했다, \"괜찮으시다면, 처남, 이 술을 어린 폴을 위해 마시겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아가에게 축복을!\" 톡스 양이 포도주를 한 모금 마시며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"사랑스러운 어린 돔비!\" 칙 부인이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"존,\" 돔비 씨가 엄숙하기 그지없는 진중함으로 말했다, \"내 아들이 자네가 베푼 호의를 헤아릴 수만 있다면, 의심할 바 없이 자네에게 고마움을 느끼고 또 그렇게 표현하겠지. 그 애는 장차, 사적으로는 친척과 벗들의 호의로운 성품이, 또 공적으로는 우리 처지의 막중한 본성이 그에게 지울지도 모를 그 어떤 책임에도 능히 부응하리라고, 나는 믿네.\"\n\n이렇게 말하는 어조가 더 이상 무엇도 덧붙일 여지를 주지 않았으므로, 칙 씨는 다시금 풀죽은 침묵에 빠졌다. 그러나 톡스 양은 그렇지 않았으니, 평소보다 한층 더 힘주어 주의를 기울여, 또 한층 더 표나게 고개를 한쪽으로 기울인 채 돔비 씨의 말을 듣고 있던 그녀는, 이제 탁자 너머로 몸을 기울여 칙 부인에게 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"루이자!\"\n\n\"왜 그래, 얘?\" 칙 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"공적으로 우리 처지의 막중한 본성이—그 정확한 말이 기억이 안 나네.\"\n\n\"그에게 노출시킬지도,\" 칙 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"미안하지만, 얘,\" 톡스 양이 응수했다, \"아닌 것 같아. 더 둥글고 유려했어. 사적으로는 친척과 벗들의 호의로운 성품이, 또 공적으로는 처지의 막중한 본성이—그에게—지울지도!\"\n\n\"그에게 지울지도, 그렇지,\" 칙 부인이 말했다.\n\n톡스 양은 의기양양하여 가냘픈 두 손을 가볍게 마주쳤다. 그러고는 눈을 치켜뜨며 덧붙였다, \"참으로 명연설이야!\"\n\n그러는 동안 돔비 씨는 리처즈를 들이라는 명을 내려 두었고, 그녀가 이제 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 들어왔으나 아기는 데려오지 않았다. 폴은 아침의 피로 끝에 잠들어 있었던 것이다. 돔비 씨는 이 가신에게 포도주 한 잔을 건넨 다음, 다음과 같은 말로 그녀에게 일렀다. 톡스 양은 그에 앞서 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고, 그 말을 가슴에 새기려는 자잘한 채비들을 갖추었다.\n\n\"리처즈, 네가 이 집의 식구로 지낸 지난 여섯 달가량, 너는 네 의무를 다했다. 이 기회에 너에게 자그마한 보답을 결부시키고자, 나는 그 목적을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 이룰 수 있을까 궁리했고, 또 내 누이 부인과도—\"\n\n\"칙입니다,\" 그 이름을 가진 신사가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"아, 부디 좀 조용히 해요!\" 톡스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"리처즈, 내가 너에게 말하려던 것은,\" 돔비 씨가 존을 향해 섬뜩한 눈길을 던지며 말을 이었다, \"내 결정에 한 가지 더 보탬이 된 것이 있었으니, 네가 고용될 때 이 방에서 네 남편과 나누었던 대화의 기억이다. 그때 그는 네 가족이, 그 자신을 우두머리로 하여, 무지에 빠져 푹 절어 있다는 그 서글픈 사실을 내게 털어놓았지.\"\n\n리처즈는 그 장엄한 질책 앞에 움츠러들었다.\n\n\"나는, 평등주의적 정서를 지닌 자들이 보편 교육이라 부르는 것과는 거리가 먼 사람이다,\" 돔비 씨가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 하층 계급은 자기 처지를 알고 처신을 바르게 하도록 계속 가르침을 받을 필요가 있지. 그런 한에서 나는 학교를 찬성한다. 어느 유서 깊은 자선기관—(어느 명예로운 동업조합의 이름을 따서) 자선 연마공이라 불리는 곳—에 아이 하나를 추천할 권한이 내게 있는데, 그곳에서는 학생들에게 건전한 교육을 베풀 뿐 아니라 옷과 표장까지 마련해 준다. 나는 (먼저 칙 부인을 통해 네 가족과 연락을 취한 뒤) 네 맏아들을 빈자리에 추천했고, 들으니 그 애가 오늘 그 복장을 갖추어 입었다는구나. 그 애의 번호가, 아마,\" 돔비 씨가 누이 쪽으로 돌아서며, 마치 그 아이가 삯마차이기라도 한 듯 말했다, \"백사십칠번이지. 루이자, 네가 알려 주려무나.\"\n\n\"백사십칠번이에요,\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"그 복장은, 리처즈, 멋지고 따뜻한 파란색 나사 연미복에 모자인데, 주황색 가두리로 접어 올렸고, 빨간 털 양말에, 아주 튼튼한 가죽 반바지란다. 그런 물건이라면 자기가 직접 입어도,\" 칙 부인이 열을 올리며 말했다, \"고마워할 만하지.\"\n\n\"자, 리처즈!\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"이제 정말 자랑스러워해도 되겠어요. 자선 연마공이라니!\"\n\n\"정말 너무나 감사드립니다, 나리,\" 리처즈가 가냘프게 응수했다, \"제 어린것들을 기억해 주셔서 더없이 고마우신 일이고요.\" 그와 동시에, 빌러*가 자선 연마공이 되어, 칙 부인이 묘사한 그 실용적인 옷에 그 자그마한 다리를 끼워 넣은 모습이 리처즈의 눈앞에 어른거려, 두 눈에 눈물이 고였다.\n* 빌러: 리처즈의 맏아들 로빈의 별명.\n\n\"네가 그렇게 정이 깊은 걸 보니 정말 기쁘구나, 리처즈,\" 톡스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"이러면 거의 희망이 생겨요, 정말로요,\" 인간 본성을 신뢰의 눈으로 바라보는 것을 자랑으로 여기는 칙 부인이 말했다, \"이 세상에 아직도 감사와 올바른 정서의 희미한 불씨나마 남아 있을지 모른다는 희망이요.\"\n\n리처즈는 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고 감사의 말을 중얼거리며 이런 칭찬들에 예를 표했다. 그러나 조숙한 아랫도리 차림을 한 아들의 모습에 휘저어진 마음의 혼란에서 도무지 기운을 되찾을 수 없자, 차츰 문 쪽으로 다가가더니 그리로 빠져나가게 되어 진심으로 안도했다.\n\n그녀와 함께 나타났던, 부분적인 해빙의 그런 일시적 기미는 그녀와 함께 사라졌다. 그리고 다시금 서리가 내려앉았으니, 여전히 차갑고 단단했다. 칙 씨가 식탁 끝자리에서 곡조를 흥얼거리는 것이 두 번 들렸으나, 두 번 다 「사울」에 나오는 「장송 행진곡」\\의 한 토막이었다. 일행은 점점 더 차가워져 가는 듯했고, 자기들이 둘러앉은 냉찬처럼 점점 응고되어 단단한 상태로 굳어 가는 듯했다. 마침내 칙 부인이 톡스 양을 바라보았고, 톡스 양도 그 눈길을 되받았으며, 둘 다 일어서서 정말 갈 시간이 되었다고 말했다. 돔비 씨가 이 통고를 완벽히 평정한 태도로 받아들이자, 그들은 그 신사에게 작별을 고하고, 이윽고 칙 씨의 보호 아래 떠났다. 칙 씨는, 그들이 그 집에 등을 돌리고 그 주인을 여느 때의 외로운 처지에 남겨 두고 나서자, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 마차에 몸을 푹 젖힌 채 「헤이 호 체비!」\\를 처음부터 끝까지 휘파람으로 불었다. 그러면서 그 얼굴에는 어찌나 음울하고 무시무시한 반항의 표정을 떠올렸던지, 칙 부인은 감히 항의하거나 어떤 식으로든 그를 건드리지 못했다.\n* 「사울」의 「장송 행진곡」: 헨델의 오라토리오 「사울」에 나오는 곡. * 「헤이 호 체비!」: 당시의 사냥 노래.\n\n리처즈는 어린 폴을 무릎에 안고 있으면서도 자기 맏이를 잊을 수 없었다. 그것이 배은망덕한 일이라는 것을 그녀도 느꼈다. 그러나 그날의 기운은 자선 연마공들에게마저 드리워졌고, 그녀는 백사십칠번이라 새겨진 그의 백랍 표장이 어쩐지 그 격식과 엄격함의 일부인 양 여겨지는 것을 도무지 떨칠 수 없었다. 그녀는 또한 육아실에서 그의 \"복받은 다리\"에 관해 이야기하다가, 제복 차림을 한 그의 환영에 다시금 시달렸다.\n\n\"무엇이든 못 내줄 게 없어요,\" 폴리가 말했다, \"그 가엾은 어린것이 그 옷에 익숙해지기 전에 한 번만 볼 수 있다면요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그럼, 내 말 들어 봐요, 리처즈 부인,\" 그녀의 속내를 듣게 된 니퍼가 응수했다, \"가서 보고 마음 편히 가지세요.\"\n\n\"돔비 씨가 좋아하지 않을 거예요,\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그분이 안 좋아하시겠어요, 리처즈 부인!\" 니퍼가 응수했다, \"제 생각엔 여쭤보면 무척 좋아하실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 당신이 그분께 여쭤보진 않겠죠, 설마?\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"아니죠, 리처즈 부인, 정반대예요,\" 수전이 응수했다, \"그리고 저 두 감독관 톡스랑 칙은 내일은 근무할 생각이 없다는데, 그렇게들 말하는 걸 제가 들었거든요, 그러니 저랑 플로이 아가씨가 내일 아침 부인이랑 함께 갈게요, 그럼 좋잖아요, 리처즈 부인, 부인만 괜찮으시면요, 거리를 이리저리 오르내리느니 거기까지 걸어가는 게 차라리 낫고, 더 나은 일이니까요.\"\n\n폴리는 처음에는 그 생각을 제법 단호히 물리쳤다. 그러나 자기 아이들과 자기 집의 금단의 그림들을 점점 더 또렷이 머릿속에 그리게 되면서, 조금씩 그 생각을 받아들이기 시작했다. 마침내 그녀는, 잠깐 문 앞에 들르는 것쯤이야 큰 해가 될 리 없다고 따져 보고는, 니퍼의 제안에 굴복했다.\n\n이렇게 일이 정해지자, 어린 폴이 어찌나 가엾게 울기 시작했던지, 마치 거기서 좋은 일이라곤 생기지 않으리라는 예감이라도 든 듯했다.\n\n\"아기가 왜 그러는 거예요?\" 수전이 물었다.\n\n\"추운 것 같아요,\" 폴리가 아기를 안고 이리저리 거닐며 어르며 말했다.\n\n과연 쌀쌀한 가을 오후였다. 그리고 그녀가 거닐고, 어르고, 음울한 창문 너머를 흘끗 내다보며 그 어린것을 자기 가슴에 더 바짝 끌어안는 동안, 시든 잎들이 우수수 쏟아져 내렸다.\n\n# 제2장\n\n폴리는 아침이 되자 너무도 많은 불안에 시달려서, 검은 눈의 동행이 끊임없이 부추기지 않았더라면 그 나들이에 대한 생각을 모두 접고, 돔비 씨의 지붕이라는 무시무시한 그늘 아래에서 백사십칠 번지를 보러 가게 해 달라고 정식으로 청을 넣었을 것이다. 그러나 수전은 그 소풍에 개인적으로 마음이 쏠려 있었고, (토니 럼킨처럼) 남의 실망은 그럭저럭 의연하게 견딜 수 있을지언정 자기 자신을 실망시키는 일만큼은 도무지 참지 못하는 성미여서, 이 두 번째 생각에 갖가지 교묘한 의문을 던져 놓고 본래의 의도를 갖가지 교묘한 논변으로 부추긴 끝에, 돔비 씨의 위엄 있는 등이 돌아서고 그 신사가 시티를 향해 날마다의 길을 좇아가기가 무섭게, 그의 영문 모르는 아들은 스택스 가든으로 가는 길에 올라 있었다.\n\n이 듣기 좋은 지명의 동네는 어느 교외에 있었는데, 스택스 가든 주민들은 그곳을 캠벌링 타운이라 불렀다. 이 명칭을 (기분 좋고 편리하게 참조하라는 취지로) 손수건에 인쇄한 「외지인용 런던 지도」는 나름의 근거를 내세워 캠든 타운으로 줄여 놓았다. 두 유모는 맡은 아이들을 데리고 이리로 발걸음을 옮겼다. 물론 리처즈가 폴을 안고, 수전이 어린 플로렌스의 손을 이끌면서, 때때로 건강에 좋다고 여겨질 만큼 아이를 홱홱 잡아당기고 쿡쿡 찔러 주었다.\n\n바로 그 무렵 큰 지진의 첫 충격이 동네 전체를 한복판까지 갈라놓았다. 그 진로의 자취가 사방에 뚜렷이 드러나 있었다. 집들은 무너졌고, 거리는 뚫리다 막혔으며, 땅에는 깊은 구덩이와 도랑이 파였고, 거대한 흙더미와 진흙더미가 솟아올랐으며, 밑이 파헤쳐져 흔들리는 건물들은 굵은 목재 들보로 떠받쳐져 있었다. 여기에는 뒤집히고 뒤엉킨 수레들이 가파르고 부자연스러운 언덕 밑바닥에 거꾸로 처박혀 있었고, 저기에는 우연히 못이 되어 버린 어떤 곳에서 쇠붙이의 어수선한 보물들이 물에 젖어 녹슬어 가고 있었다. 어디에나 아무 데로도 이어지지 않는 다리가 있었고, 전혀 통행할 수 없는 큰길이 있었으며, 높이가 절반밖에 안 되는 바벨탑 같은 굴뚝들과, 도무지 어울리지 않는 자리에 들어선 임시 목조 가옥과 울타리, 다 해진 셋집의 잔해, 미완성 벽과 아치의 파편, 비계 더미, 벽돌의 황야, 거대한 형체의 기중기들, 그리고 아무것도 없는 허공 위에 다리를 벌리고 선 삼각대들이 있었다. 미완성의 형상과 실체가 십만 가지나 되어, 제자리를 벗어나 마구 뒤섞이고, 거꾸로 뒤집히고, 땅속을 파고들고, 허공을 향해 솟구치고, 물속에서 썩어 가며, 그 어떤 꿈처럼이나 알아볼 수 없었다. 지진에 으레 따라붙는 온천과 화염의 분출이 이 광경에 혼란의 몫을 보태었다. 허물어진 벽 안에서 끓는 물이 쉭쉭거리며 솟구쳤고, 그곳에서 또한 불길의 섬광과 굉음이 뿜어 나왔으며, 잿더미가 통행로를 막아 동네의 법도와 관습을 송두리째 바꾸어 놓았다.\n\n요컨대, 아직 완공되지도 개통되지도 않은 철도가 공사 중이었던 것이다. 그리고 이 끔찍한 무질서의 한복판으로부터, 문명과 발전의 그 웅대한 행로를 따라 매끄럽게 뻗어 나가고 있었다.\n\n그러나 아직 동네는 그 철도를 인정하기를 꺼렸다. 대담한 투기꾼 한둘이 거리를 기획했고, 한 사람은 조금 짓다가 진흙과 잿더미 속에 멈추어 서서 좀 더 궁리해 보는 중이었다. 갓 바른 회반죽과 아교풀 냄새가 물씬 풍기는, 정면이 아무것도 마주하지 않은 신축 선술집 한 채가 「철도 정거장」이라는 간판을 내걸었는데, 그것은 경솔한 모험일 수도 있었으나, 어쨌든 인부들에게 술을 팔 수 있기를 바랐던 것이다. 그리하여 한 맥줏집에서 「굴착 인부 집결소」가 생겨났고, 오래전부터 자리 잡은 「햄·소고기 가게」는 「철도 식당」이 되어, 비슷하게 당장 눈앞의 인기에 영합하는 속셈으로 날마다 돼지 다리 구이를 내놓았다. 하숙집 주인들도 마찬가지로 호의적이었으나, 같은 이유로 신뢰할 수 없는 자들이었다. 사람들의 일반적인 믿음은 매우 더뎠다. 철도 바로 문턱에 곰팡내 나는 들판과 외양간과 거름더미와 쓰레기더미와 도랑과 텃밭과 정자와 양탄자 터는 마당이 있었다. 굴 철에는 굴 껍데기, 바닷가재 철에는 바닷가재 껍데기, 그리고 사철 내내 깨진 그릇과 시든 양배추 잎이 작은 무덤처럼 쌓여 철도의 높은 자리를 잠식했다. 기둥과 난간, 침입자에게 경고하는 낡은 푯말, 초라한 집들의 뒷면, 비참하게 자란 식물의 무더기들이 철도를 무안하게 노려보았다. 철도 덕에 나아진 것은 아무것도 없었고, 나아지리라 생각하는 이도 없었다. 만일 그 곁에 놓인 비참한 황무지가 웃을 수 있었더라면, 비참한 이웃들 가운데 많은 이들처럼, 그것을 비웃었을 것이다.\n\n스택스 가든은 유달리 믿지 않았다. 그것은 작은 집들이 한 줄로 늘어선 곳으로, 집 앞에는 초라한 땅뙈기가 조금씩 있었고, 낡은 문짝과 통널과 방수포 조각과 죽은 덤불로 울타리를 둘렀으며, 그 틈새에는 밑 빠진 양철 주전자와 다 닳은 무쇠 난로받이를 쑤셔 넣어 두었다. 여기서 스택스 가든의 정원사들은 빨간 강낭콩을 길렀고, 닭과 토끼를 쳤으며, 썩어 가는 정자(그중 하나는 낡은 배였다)를 세우고, 빨래를 말리고, 담배를 피웠다. 어떤 이들은 스택스 가든이라는 이름이 죽은 자본가, 곧 자기 즐길 거리로 그곳을 지었다는 어느 스택스 씨에게서 비롯되었다고 보았다. 또 시골에 대한 타고난 취향을 지닌 다른 이들은, 뿔 달린 무리가 스택스라는 친숙한 이름으로 그 그늘진 경내를 즐겨 찾던 그 전원의 시절로 거슬러 올라간다고 여겼다. 그것이야 어찌 되었든, 스택스 가든은 그 주민들에게 철도 따위로는 시들지 않을 신성한 숲으로 여겨졌다. 또 그곳이 그런 우스꽝스러운 발명품보다 오래 살아남으리라 다들 어찌나 확신했던지, 그 가든의 지역 정치를 주도하는 것으로 알려진 길모퉁이의 굴뚝 청소부 우두머리는, 만일 철도가 개통되는 일이 있다면, 그 개통일에 자기 두 아들을 자기 집 굴뚝으로 올려 보내, 굴뚝 갓머리에서 조롱의 환호로 그 실패를 맞이하라고 일러두겠노라 공언한 터였다.\n\n이제껏 그 이름조차 누이의 손에 의해 돔비 씨에게서 조심스레 감추어져 온 이 불경한 곳으로, 어린 폴은 이제 운명과 리처즈에게 안겨 실려 왔다.\n\n\"저기가 우리 집이에요, 수전,\" 폴리가 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"정말요, 리처즈 부인?\" 수전이 짐짓 거들먹거리며 말했다.\n\n\"어머나, 문간에 우리 제미마가 있네,\" 폴리가 외쳤다, \"내 사랑스럽고 귀한 아기를 품에 안고서!\"\n\n그 광경에 폴리의 조바심에 어찌나 큰 날개가 한 쌍 돋았던지, 그녀는 가든을 따라 달음박질치며 내려가 제미마에게 와락 안기더니, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 아기를 맞바꾸어 버렸다. 그 젊은 처녀는 형언할 수 없이 놀랐는데, 돔비가의 상속자가 그녀에게 마치 구름에서 떨어진 것만 같았던 것이다.\n\n\"어머, 폴리!\" 제미마가 외쳤다. \"언니가! 깜짝 놀랐잖아! 누가 생각이나 했겠어! 어서 들어와, 폴리! 정말이지 얼굴 좋아 보이네! 애들이 언니를 보면 반쯤 미쳐 날뛸 거야, 폴리, 정말로 그럴걸.\"\n\n아이들이 내는 소리와 폴리에게 달려들어 굴뚝 모퉁이의 나지막한 의자로 끌고 가던 모양새로 미루어 보건대, 정말로 그랬다. 그 의자에서 폴리의 정직한 사과 같은 얼굴은 이내 더 작은 사과 알들의 한가운데가 되었으니, 모두가 발그레한 볼을 거기에 바짝 갖다 대었고, 모두가 분명히 같은 나무에서 자란 열매들이었다. 폴리로 말하자면, 아이들 못지않게 시끌벅적하고 격렬했다. 그러다 마침내 숨이 완전히 차오르고, 머리카락이 발갛게 달아오른 얼굴 주위로 온통 흘러내리고, 새로 마련한 세례식 옷차림이 몹시 헝클어지고 나서야, 그 소란에 잠시 짬이 생겼다. 그때조차도 막내에서 둘째인 투들이 그녀의 무릎에 남아 두 팔로 그 목을 꼭 끌어안고 있었고, 막내에서 셋째인 투들은 의자 등받이에 올라타 한쪽 다리를 허공에 든 채 모퉁이로 돌아 그녀에게 입 맞추려고 필사의 노력을 기울였다.\n\n\"봐! 너희를 보러 예쁜 꼬마 아가씨가 오셨단다,\" 폴리가 말했다. \"그리고 얼마나 얌전한지 좀 보렴! 정말 아름다운 꼬마 아가씨지, 그렇지 않니?\"\n\n플로렌스에 대한 이 언급은—그 자리에서 벌어진 일을 눈여겨보지 않은 것은 아닌 채 문간에 서 있던 그 아이에게로—어린 가지들의 주의를 돌렸을 뿐 아니라, 이미 자기가 푸대접을 받았다는 불안에서 그리 자유롭지 못했던 니퍼 양을 정식으로 알아보게 하는 행복한 효과까지 가져왔다.\n\n\"아 들어와서 잠깐 앉아요, 수전, 제발,\" 폴리가 말했다. \"이쪽이 우리 동생 제미마예요. 이 사람이요. 제미마, 수전 니퍼 양이 없었으면 내가 나 자신을 어떻게 했을지 모르겠어. 이 사람 아니었으면 지금 여기 오지도 못했을 거야.\"\n\n\"아 앉으세요, 니퍼 양, 제발,\" 제미마가 말했다.\n\n수전은 위엄 있고 격식 차린 표정으로 의자의 맨 끝 모퉁이에 걸터앉았다.\n\n\"내 평생 누굴 보고 이렇게 반가웠던 적이 없어요. 정말이지, 정말 없었어요, 니퍼 양,\" 제미마가 말했다.\n\n수전은 조금 누그러져서 의자를 조금 더 차지하고는, 우아하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"보닛 끈을 풀고 편히 계세요, 니퍼 양, 제발,\" 제미마가 간청했다. \"익숙하신 데보다 누추한 곳이라 걱정이네요. 하지만 분명 너그러이 봐주시겠지요.\"\n\n이 깍듯한 태도에 검은 눈은 어찌나 누그러졌던지, 마침 뛰어 지나가던 어린 투들 양을 와락 붙잡아 곧장 「밴버리 십자가」 놀이를 시켜 주었다.\n\n\"그런데 우리 예쁜 사내아이는 어디 있니?\" 폴리가 말했다. \"내 가엾은 녀석은? 새 옷 입은 모습을 보려고 이 먼 길을 왔는데.\"\n\n\"아 어쩌면 좋아!\" 제미마가 외쳤다. \"엄마가 여기 왔었다는 걸 들으면 그 애 가슴이 미어질 거야. 학교에 갔어, 폴리.\"\n\n\"벌써 갔다고!\"\n\n\"응. 배움을 조금이라도 놓칠까 봐 어제 처음으로 갔거든. 하지만 반공일이야, 폴리. 그 애가 집에 올 때까지만 머물 수 있다면—언니랑 니퍼 양은 적어도 말이야,\" 제미마가 검은 눈의 위신을 때맞춰 헤아리며 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 그 애 모습이 어때, 제미마, 그 애 좀 봐 줘!\" 폴리가 더듬거리며 물었다.\n\n\"음, 정말이지 언니가 짐작하는 만큼 나빠 보이진 않아,\" 제미마가 대답했다.\n\n\"아!\" 폴리가 감격하여 말했다. \"다리가 너무 짧을 게 틀림없다고 생각했어.\"\n\n\"다리가 짧긴 해,\" 제미마가 대답했다. \"특히 뒤쪽이 그래. 하지만 날마다 길어질 거야, 폴리.\"\n\n그것은 더디고 막연한 위로였다. 그러나 그것을 베푸는 명랑함과 선량함이, 본디 지니지 못한 값어치를 거기에 더해 주었다. 잠시 침묵한 뒤, 폴리가 좀 더 활기찬 투로 물었다.\n\n\"그런데 아버지는 어디 계셔, 제미마 얘?\"—투들 씨는 집안에서 대개 이 가장다운 호칭으로 통했으니까.\n\n\"또 그러네!\" 제미마가 말했다. \"어쩌면 좋아! 아버지는 오늘 아침 도시락을 싸 가셨거든, 그래서 밤이 되어야 오셔. 하지만 늘 언니 이야기를 하고, 애들한테 언니 얘기를 들려주신다니까. 그리고 세상에서 제일 온순하고, 제일 참을성 있고, 제일 너그러운 분이야. 늘 그러셨고 앞으로도 그러실 거야!\"\n\n\"고마워, 제미마,\" 순박한 폴리가 외쳤다. 그 말에 기뻐하면서도, 만나지 못한 데 실망하면서.\n\n\"아 고마워할 거 없어, 폴리,\" 동생이 그녀의 볼에 쪽 소리 나게 입을 맞추고는, 어린 폴을 즐겁게 어르며 말했다. \"나도 가끔 언니 얘기를 똑같이 하는걸. 그리고 진심으로 그렇게 생각하고.\"\n\n이중의 실망에도 불구하고, 이런 환대로 맞이된 방문을 실패로 여기기란 불가능했다. 그리하여 자매는 집안일에 대해, 바일러에 대해, 그 애의 형제자매 모두에 대해 희망찬 이야기를 나누었다. 한편 검은 눈은 「밴버리 십자가」까지 몇 차례 다녀오고는, 가구며 네덜란드제 시계며 찬장이며 벽난로 선반 위의 성(城)—그 안에 빨강과 초록 창문이 있어 안쪽에 양초 토막을 넣으면 불을 밝힐 수 있었다—이며, 저마다 입에 부인용 손가방을 문 한 쌍의 작은 검은 벨벳 새끼 고양이를—스택스 가든의 정원사들이 모방 예술의 경이로 여기던 그것을—날카롭게 살폈다. 검은 눈이 함부로 빈정거리는 쪽으로 내달릴까 하여 대화는 이내 다 함께 나누는 것이 되었고, 그 젊은 아가씨는 제미마에게 자기가 돔비 씨와 그의 장래, 가족, 일, 성격에 관해 아는 모든 것을 요약해 들려주었다. 또한 자기의 개인 의상 목록을 정확히 일러 주고, 자기의 주요 친척과 벗들에 대해서도 얼마간 설명했다. 이런 폭로로 마음의 짐을 던 뒤, 그녀는 새우와 흑맥주를 들고는 영원한 우정을 맹세할 기색을 내비쳤다.\n\n어린 플로렌스 자신도 이 기회를 활용하는 데 뒤처지지 않았다. 어린 투들들의 안내로 가든의 독버섯이며 그 밖의 진기한 것들을 구경하러 나갔다가, 그 애들과 함께 마음과 영혼을 다해, 한쪽 구석에 고인 작은 초록빛 웅덩이를 가로지르는 임시 방파제 쌓기에 착수했던 것이다. 그녀가 여전히 그 작업에 분주히 몰두해 있을 때, 수전이 찾아내었다. 새우의 인간미 어린 영향 아래에서조차 의무감이 어찌나 투철했던지, 수전은 그녀의 얼굴과 손을 씻기면서 그 타락한 천성을 두고 (퍽퍽 때리는 것으로 구두점을 찍어 가며) 일장 훈계를 늘어놓았고, 그녀가 온 집안 식구의 흰머리를 슬픔과 함께 무덤으로 데려가고 말 것이라 예언했다. 폴리와 제미마가 위층에서 금전 문제에 관해 꽤 긴 은밀한 면담을 나누느라 얼마간 지체된 뒤—그동안 폴리는 줄곧 자기 아이를, 제미마는 어린 폴을 안고 있었으니—다시 아기를 맞바꾸었고, 방문객들은 작별을 고했다.\n\n그러나 우선 어린 투들들은 경건한 속임수의 희생양이 되어, 표면상 동전 한 푼 쓰러 간다는 명목으로, 다 함께 떼 지어 동네의 잡화점으로 가도록 유인되었다. 그리하여 사방이 완전히 트이자 폴리는 달아났다. 제미마는 돌아가는 길에 시티 로드 쪽으로 빙 돌아갈 수만 있다면, 학교에서 돌아오는 어린 바일러를 분명 만날 거라고 뒤에서 외쳐 댔다.\n\n\"우리가 시간을 내서 그쪽으로 좀 돌아갈 수 있을까요, 수전?\" 폴리가 숨을 돌리려 멈춰 섰을 때 물었다.\n\n\"안 될 게 뭐 있어요, 리처즈 부인?\" 수전이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"우리 점심때가 다가오고 있잖아요, 아시다시피,\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n그러나 점심 요기가 그 동행으로 하여금 이 중대한 고려에 도무지 무관심하게 만들어 놓았던 터라, 수전은 거기에 아무런 무게도 두지 않았고, 두 사람은 \"조금 돌아가기로\" 결정했다.\n\n그런데, 가엾은 바일러의 삶은 어제 아침 이래로 「자선 연마공」의 복장 때문에 고달파져 있었다. 거리의 청소년들은 그것을 참아 내지 못했다. 어떤 어린 부랑아도 그것을 한순간 바라보고 견딜 수 없었고, 그러면 그 죄 없는 착용자에게 달려들어 해코지를 해 댔다. 그의 사회적 존재는 십구 세기의 죄 없는 아이라기보다는 초기 기독교인의 그것에 더 가까웠다. 그는 거리에서 돌팔매질을 당했다. 시궁창에 처박혔고, 진흙을 뒤집어썼으며, 기둥에 거칠게 짓눌렸다. 그를 전혀 모르는 낯선 자들이 그의 노란 모자를 머리에서 벗겨 바람에 날려 버렸다. 그의 다리는 말로 비판받고 욕먹었을 뿐 아니라, 만져지고 꼬집혔다. 바로 그날 아침에는 연마공 학교로 가는 길에 전혀 청하지도 않은 눈탱이를 하나 얻어맞았는데, 그 일로 교장에게 벌을 받기까지 했다. 그 교장은 사나운 기질의 늙은 퇴역 연마공으로, 아무것도 아는 게 없고 무엇에도 쓸모가 없다는 이유로 교장에 임명된 자였으며, 그의 잔인한 회초리에 토실토실한 사내아이들은 하나같이 완전히 매료되어 있었다.\n\n이리하여 바일러는 집으로 돌아가는 길에 사람 다니지 않는 길을 찾았고, 자기를 괴롭히는 자들을 피하려고 좁은 통로와 뒷골목을 따라 슬그머니 빠져나갔다. 큰길로 나설 수밖에 없게 되자, 불운이 그를 마침내, 사나운 젊은 백정을 우두머리로 한 한 무리의 사내아이들이 무슨 신나는 흥밋거리든 생기기를 노리며 매복해 있던 곳으로 이끌었다. 이들은 자기네 한복판에서, 말하자면 영문 모르게 자기네 손아귀에 떨어진 「자선 연마공」을 발견하자, 일제히 함성을 지르며 그에게 달려들었다.\n\n그러나 또한 마침 그때, 폴리가 족히 한 시간을 걸은 끝에 앞쪽 길을 절망적으로 살펴보며 이제 더 가 봤자 소용없다고 막 말하던 참에, 별안간 이 광경을 보게 되었다. 그것을 보자마자 그녀는 다급한 외침을 내지르며 돔비 도련님을 검은 눈에게 떠넘기고는, 자기의 불행한 어린 아들을 구하러 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n뜻밖의 일이란, 불행처럼, 좀처럼 홀로 오지 않는 법이다. 깜짝 놀란 수전 니퍼와 그녀가 맡은 두 아이는, 영문도 모르는 사이에, 지나가던 마차 바퀴 바로 밑에서 구경꾼들에게 구조되었다. 그리고 바로 그 순간(장날이었다) \"미친 소다!\" 하는 우레 같은 경보가 일었다.\n\n사람들이 이리저리 내달리며 고함치고, 바퀴들이 그들을 깔아뭉개고, 사내아이들이 싸우고, 미친 소가 들이닥치고, 이 모든 위험의 한복판에서 유모가 갈가리 찢기는 어지러운 혼란을 눈앞에 두고서, 플로렌스는 비명을 지르며 달아났다. 그녀는 지쳐 쓰러질 때까지 달렸고, 수전에게도 그러라고 다그쳤다. 그러다가 멈춰 서서, 다른 유모를 뒤에 두고 왔다는 것을 떠올리며 두 손을 비틀던 차에, 형언할 수 없는 공포의 감각과 더불어, 자기가 완전히 홀로라는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"수전! 수전!\" 플로렌스가 공포의 절정에서 두 손을 마주치며 외쳤다. \"오, 어디 있어요? 다들 어디 있어요?\"\n\n\"다들 어디 있느냐고?\" 한 늙은 여자가 길 건너편에서 할 수 있는 한 빨리 절뚝거리며 다가오면서 말했다. \"왜 그 사람들한테서 도망쳤어?\"\n\n\"무서웠어요,\" 플로렌스가 대답했다. \"제가 뭘 한 건지도 몰랐어요. 다들 저랑 같이 있는 줄 알았어요. 어디 있어요?\"\n\n늙은 여자는 그녀의 손목을 붙잡고 말했다, \"내가 보여 주마.\"\n\n그녀는 매우 못생긴 늙은 여자로, 눈가에 붉은 테가 둘려 있었고, 말을 하지 않을 때면 입이 저절로 우물거리며 떠들어 댔다. 그녀는 비참한 행색이었고, 팔에 가죽 몇 장을 걸치고 있었다. 어쨌든 플로렌스를 얼마간 뒤따라온 모양이었으니, 숨이 차 있었던 것이다. 그래서 그녀는 숨을 돌리려 애쓰며 서 있는 동안 더더욱 못생겨 보였다. 쪼글쪼글한 누런 얼굴과 목을 온갖 모양으로 일그러뜨리면서.\n\n플로렌스는 그녀가 무서웠고, 망설이며, 거의 끝까지 다다른 그 거리 위쪽을 올려다보았다. 그곳은 인적 없는 곳—거리라기보다는 뒷길에 가까운 곳—이었고, 그곳에는 그녀 자신과 늙은 여자 말고는 아무도 없었다.\n\n\"이제 무서워할 거 없어,\" 늙은 여자가 여전히 그녀를 꽉 붙든 채 말했다. \"나랑 같이 가자.\"\n\n\"저—저는 할머니를 몰라요. 이름이 뭐예요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"브라운 부인이란다,\" 늙은 여자가 말했다. \"착한 브라운 부인이지.\"\n\n\"다들 이 근처에 있어요?\" 플로렌스가 끌려가기 시작하면서 물었다.\n\n\"수전은 멀지 않은 데 있어,\" 착한 브라운 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 다른 사람들은 그 옆에 바짝 붙어 있고.\"\n\n\"다친 사람은 없어요?\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"하나도 없지,\" 착한 브라운 부인이 말했다.\n\n아이는 이 말을 듣고 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리며 늙은 여자를 기꺼이 따라갔다. 그러면서도 가는 동안 그 얼굴을—특히 그 부지런히 움직이는 입을—흘끔거리며, 만일 나쁜 브라운 부인이라는 사람이 있다면 이 사람과 조금이라도 닮았을까 궁금해하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n멀리 가지는 않았으나, 벽돌 굽는 들판이며 기와 마당 같은 매우 불쾌한 곳들을 지나, 늙은 여자는 어느 더러운 골목으로 꺾어 들었는데, 그곳은 길 한복판에 진흙이 깊고 검은 바큇자국을 이루며 깔려 있었다. 그녀는 초라한 작은 집 앞에 멈춰 섰는데, 그 집은 갈라진 틈과 균열로 가득한 집이 닫힐 수 있는 한 빈틈없이 닫혀 있었다. 보닛에서 꺼낸 열쇠로 문을 열고, 그녀는 아이를 떠밀어 안쪽 방으로 들여보냈다. 그곳에는 갖가지 색깔의 누더기 더미가 바닥에 잔뜩 쌓여 있었고, 뼈 무더기와 체로 거른 먼지나 잿가루 더미가 있었으나, 가구라고는 전혀 없었으며, 벽과 천장이 온통 새까맸다.\n\n아이는 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지 말문이 막혀 버렸고, 금방이라도 까무러칠 것처럼 보였다.\n\n\"자 어린 노새처럼 굴지 마,\" 착한 브라운 부인이 한 차례 흔들어 아이를 정신 차리게 하며 말했다. \"널 해치진 않을 거야. 누더기 위에 앉아.\"\n\n플로렌스는 그 말에 따랐고, 두 손을 모아 내밀며 말없이 애원했다.\n\n\"한 시간 넘게 널 붙잡아 두지도 않을 거야,\" 브라운 부인이 말했다. \"내 말 알아듣겠어?\"\n\n아이는 몹시 힘겹게 \"네\"라고 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼,\" 착한 브라운 부인이 뼈 무더기 위에 자기 자리를 잡고 앉으며 말했다, \"날 성가시게 하지 마. 그러지만 않으면, 내 말하건대, 널 해치지 않을 거야. 하지만 성가시게 굴면, 널 죽여 버릴 테니까. 난 언제든 너를 죽게 만들 수 있어—네가 집에서 네 침대에 누워 있다 해도 말이야. 자 이제 네가 누군지, 뭐 하는 애인지, 그 모든 걸 좀 알아보자.\"\n\n늙은 여자의 위협과 약속, 그녀의 비위를 거스를까 하는 두려움, 그리고 아이에게는 드물지만 이제 플로렌스에게는 거의 천성이 되어 버린 습성—조용히 있으면서, 자기가 느끼고 두려워하고 바라는 것을 억누르는 습성—이 그 분부를 따르게 하고, 자기의 짧은 내력을, 아니 자기가 아는 만큼을 이야기하게 해 주었다. 브라운 부인은 아이가 다 마칠 때까지 주의 깊게 들었다.\n\n\"그래 네 이름이 돔비라고, 어?\" 브라운 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"저 예쁜 원피스가 갖고 싶구나, 돔비 아가씨,\" 착한 브라운 부인이 말했다, \"그리고 저 작은 보닛하고, 페티코트 한두 장하고, 또 네가 내줄 수 있는 건 뭐든. 자! 벗어.\"\n\n플로렌스는 떨리는 손이 허락하는 한 빨리 따랐다. 그러는 내내 겁먹은 눈을 브라운 부인에게서 떼지 못한 채로. 그 부인이 말한 의류를 죄다 벗어 내자, 브라운 부인은 그것들을 느긋이 살펴보더니, 그 품질과 값어치에 그럭저럭 흡족해하는 듯했다.\n\n\"흠!\" 그녀가 아이의 가냘픈 몸을 눈으로 훑으며 말했다, \"신발 말고는 별다른 게 안 보이는구나. 신발도 가져야겠다, 돔비 아가씨.\"\n\n가엾은 어린 플로렌스는 똑같이 선뜻 그것을 벗었으니, 자기 곁에 비위를 맞출 수단이 더 생긴 것이 그저 기쁘기만 했다. 그러자 늙은 여자는 그러려고 뒤집어엎은 누더기 더미 바닥에서 형편없는 대용품 몇 가지를 꺼내 왔다. 다 닳아 빠지고 몹시 낡은 소녀용 망토와, 아마도 어느 도랑이나 거름더미에서 주워 왔을 짓이겨진 보닛 잔해와 함께였다. 이 우아한 의복을 입으라고 그녀는 플로렌스에게 일렀고, 그런 준비가 풀려날 전조처럼 보였기에 아이는, 가능하다면, 한층 더 선뜻 따랐다.\n\n서둘러 보닛을 쓰다가—짐을 머리에 일 때 받치는 깔개에 더 가까운 것을 보닛이라 부를 수 있다면—그것이 풍성하게 자란 머리카락에 걸렸는데, 좀처럼 즉시 풀 수가 없었다. 착한 브라운 부인은 큼직한 가위를 휙 꺼내 들더니, 영문 모를 흥분 상태에 빠졌다.\n\n\"왜 가만히 못 놔둬!\" 브라운 부인이 말했다, \"내가 만족하고 있었는데! 이 어린 바보야!\"\n\n\"죄송해요. 제가 뭘 잘못했는지 모르겠어요,\" 플로렌스가 헐떡이며 말했다. \"어쩔 수가 없었어요.\"\n\n\"어쩔 수 없었다고!\" 브라운 부인이 외쳤다. \"그럼 내가 어떻게 참을 수 있겠니? 아니, 세상에!\" 늙은 여자가 사나운 즐거움에 차 아이의 곱슬머리를 헝클어뜨리며 말했다, \"나 말고 다른 사람이었으면 진작에 죄다 잘라 버렸을 거야.\"\n\n플로렌스는 브라운 부인이 탐낸 것이 자기 머리일 뿐 자기 목숨이 아니라는 것을 알고 어찌나 안심했던지, 아무 저항도 애원도 하지 않고, 그저 온순한 눈을 그 착한 사람의 얼굴을 향해 들어 올릴 따름이었다.\n\n\"나도 한때 내 딸이 있었지—지금은 바다 건너에 있지만—제 머리카락을 자랑스러워하던 애였지,\" 브라운 부인이 말했다, \"그 애만 아니었으면 한 올도 남김없이 잘라 버렸을 거야. 그 애는 멀리 가 있어, 멀리 가 있다고! 오호! 오호!\"\n\n브라운 부인의 울음은 가락 좋은 것이 아니었으나, 비쩍 마른 두 팔을 거칠게 치켜드는 것과 어우러져, 격정적인 비탄으로 가득했고, 플로렌스의 가슴까지 떨리게 했으니, 아이는 어느 때보다 더 겁에 질렸다. 어쩌면 그것이 그녀의 곱슬머리를 구하는 데 한몫했는지도 모른다. 브라운 부인은 가위를 들고서 한동안 그녀의 주위를, 마치 새로운 종류의 나비처럼 맴돌더니, 머리카락을 보닛 밑에 감추고 한 올도 빠져나와 자기를 유혹하는 일이 없게 하라고 일렀던 것이다. 자기 자신에 대한 이 승리를 거두고 나서, 브라운 부인은 다시 뼈 무더기 위 자기 자리에 앉아, 매우 짧은 검은 담뱃대를 피웠는데, 줄곧 무언가를 씹어 대고 우물거리는 것이 마치 담뱃대 자루를 먹고 있는 것만 같았다.\n\n담뱃대를 다 피우자, 그녀는 아이에게 토끼 가죽을 하나 들려 주어, 늘 데리고 다니는 자기 동행처럼 더 보이게 했다. 그러고는 이제 사람들이 다니는 거리로 데려가, 거기서 벗들에게 가는 길을 물어볼 수 있게 해 주겠다고 말했다. 그러나 만일 말을 듣지 않으면 즉결로 치명적인 복수를 하겠다는 위협을 곁들여, 낯선 이들에게 말을 걸지 말 것이며, (브라운 부인의 편의에는 너무 가까웠을지도 모를) 제 집으로 가지 말고, 시티에 있는 아버지의 사무소로 가라고 단단히 일렀다. 또 자기가 두고 갈 길모퉁이에서 세 시를 칠 때까지 기다리라고도 했다. 이 지시들을, 브라운 부인은 자기가 부리는 막강한 눈과 귀가 아이가 하는 모든 일을 다 알고 있을 거라는 다짐으로써 다그쳤다. 그리고 이 지시들을 플로렌스는 충실하고 간절하게 지키겠노라 약속했다.\n\n마침내 브라운 부인은 밖으로 나서, 변장하고 누더기를 걸친 자기 어린 벗을 좁은 거리와 골목과 뒷길의 미로 사이로 데리고 갔는데, 한참 만에 어느 마구간 마당으로 빠져나왔다. 그 끝에 대문이 하나 있었고, 거기서 큰 한길의 굉음이 들려왔다. 이 대문을 가리켜 보이며, 시계가 세 시를 치면 왼쪽으로 가라고 일러 준 뒤, 브라운 부인은 도무지 자기 뜻과는 상관없이 저절로 그러는 듯 마지막으로 아이의 머리카락을 한 번 움켜쥐고는, 무엇을 해야 할지 알 테니 가서 그대로 하라며, 감시당하고 있음을 잊지 말라고 일렀다.\n\n한결 가벼워진 마음으로, 그러나 여전히 몹시 두려운 채, 플로렌스는 자기가 풀려난 것을 깨닫고 모퉁이로 종종걸음 쳐 갔다. 그곳에 다다라 뒤를 돌아보니, 착한 브라운 부인이 마지막 분부를 내리던 그 나지막한 나무 통로 밖으로 머리를 내밀고 있는 것이 보였다. 마찬가지로 착한 브라운 부인이 자기를 향해 주먹을 흔들고 있는 것도 보였다. 그러나 그 뒤로 자주 뒤를 돌아보았어도—늙은 여자에 대한 불안한 기억 속에서 적어도 일 분에 한 번씩은 돌아보았어도—다시는 그녀를 볼 수 없었다.\n\n플로렌스는 그곳에 머물러, 거리의 북적임을 바라보며 점점 더 어리둥절해했다. 그러는 사이 시계들은 다시는 세 시를 치지 않기로 작정한 듯했다. 마침내 첨탑들이 세 시를 알렸다. 바로 가까이에 하나가 있었으므로 잘못 들었을 리 없었다. 그리하여—자꾸 어깨너머로 돌아보고, 자꾸 조금 가다가, 그만큼 자꾸 다시 돌아오기를 거듭한 끝에, 브라운 부인의 그 전능한 첩자들이 비위 상하지 않도록—그녀는 헐렁한 신발로 낼 수 있는 한 빨리, 토끼 가죽을 손에 꼭 쥔 채, 서둘러 떠났다.\n\n아버지의 사무소에 대해 그녀가 아는 것이라고는, 그것이 돔비와 아들의 것이라는 것, 그리고 그것이 시티에 속한 큰 권세라는 것뿐이었다. 그래서 그녀는 시티에 있는 돔비와 아들로 가는 길만을 물을 수 있었다. 그리고 대개—어른들에게는 묻기가 무서워—아이들에게 길을 물었던 까닭에, 정말이지 신통한 답을 거의 얻지 못했다. 그러나 얼마 뒤로는 시티로 가는 길을 묻고, 나머지 물음은 당분간 접어 둔 덕분에, 그녀는 정말로, 더디게나마, 무시무시한 시장 각하가 다스리는 그 거대한 구역의 심장부를 향해 나아가고 있었다.\n\n걷느라 지치고, 떠밀리고 채이고, 소음과 혼란에 얼떨떨하고, 남동생과 유모들 걱정에, 자기가 겪은 일에 겁먹고, 이토록 달라진 행색으로 화난 아버지와 마주칠 일에 두려워하며, 지나간 일과 지금 벌어지는 일과 앞으로 닥칠 일에 한결같이 어리둥절하고 겁에 질려서, 플로렌스는 눈물 어린 눈으로 고단한 길을 갔고, 한두 번은 터질 듯한 가슴을 달래려고 쓰라리게 울며 멈춰 서지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그런 때에 그녀가 입은 옷차림 때문에 그녀를 눈여겨보는 사람은 거의 없었다. 설령 눈여겨본다 해도, 동정을 사도록 길들여진 아이려니 여기고 그냥 지나쳤다. 플로렌스 또한, 자기의 슬픈 경험이 일찍이 빚어내고 시험해 둔 성품의 온갖 굳건함과 자립심을 불러내었다. 그리고 자기가 마음에 둔 목표를 한결같이 앞에 두고서, 한결같이 그것을 좇았다.\n\n그녀가 이 기이한 모험에 나선 때보다 오후로 족히 두 시간이 더 흐른 뒤, 수레와 짐마차로 가득한 좁은 거리의 충돌과 소음에서 빠져나와, 그녀는 강가의 어느 부두인지 하역장인지로 살며시 들여다보았다. 그곳에는 짐 꾸러미와 통과 상자가 잔뜩 흩어져 있었고, 큰 나무 저울 한 쌍이 있었으며, 바퀴 달린 작은 나무 오두막이 있었는데, 그 바깥에서 한 건장한 사내가 이웃한 돛대와 배들을 바라보며 휘파람을 불고 있었다. 펜을 귀 뒤에 꽂고 두 손을 주머니에 넣은 채, 마치 그날 일이 거의 끝났다는 듯이.\n\n\"이봐!\" 이 사내가 마침 돌아서며 말했다. \"여기엔 너한테 줄 게 아무것도 없다, 꼬마 아가씨. 저리 가!\"\n\n\"저, 여기가 시티인가요?\" 떨고 있는 돔비가의 딸이 물었다.\n\n\"아! 시티지. 너도 잘 알 텐데, 분명. 저리 가! 너한테 줄 건 아무것도 없어.\"\n\n\"아무것도 바라지 않아요, 고맙습니다,\" 하는 것이 그 수줍은 대답이었다. \"다만 돔비와 아들로 가는 길을 알고 싶어서요.\"\n\n무심히 그녀 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어오던 사내는 이 대답에 놀란 듯했고, 그녀의 얼굴을 유심히 들여다보며 되받았다.\n\n\"아니, 돔비와 아들에 무슨 볼일이 있는데?\"\n\n\"그리로 가는 길을 알고 싶어요, 부탁이에요.\"\n\n사내는 더욱 호기심 어린 눈으로 그녀를 보았고, 어찌나 세게 뒤통수를 긁었던지 제 모자가 떨어져 버렸다.\n\n\"조!\" 그는 그것을 주워 다시 쓰면서 다른 사내—한 인부—를 불렀다.\n\n\"조 여기 있소!\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"저 짐 싣는 거 지켜보던 돔비네 그 젊은 풋내기 어디 갔나?\"\n\n\"방금 저쪽 문으로 갔는데요,\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐 다시 불러 와.\"\n\n조는 아치 통로를 뛰어 올라가며 큰 소리로 외쳤고, 이내 쾌활해 보이는 소년 하나를 데리고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"네가 돔비네 심부름꾼이지, 안 그래?\" 처음의 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"돔비 상회에서 일합니다, 클라크 씨,\" 소년이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 이쪽을 좀 봐,\" 클라크 씨가 말했다.\n\n클라크 씨의 손짓이 가리키는 대로, 소년은 플로렌스 쪽으로 다가왔다. 자기가 이 아이와 무슨 상관이 있는지 어리둥절해하면서. 그러나 오간 이야기를 들었던 데다, 자기 여정의 끝에 별안간 안전하다는 안도감에 더해, 그의 생기 넘치는 앳된 얼굴과 거동에 더할 나위 없이 안심한 그녀는, 헐렁한 신발 한 짝을 땅바닥에 떨어뜨린 채 그에게 한달음에 달려가, 두 손으로 그의 손을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"저 길을 잃었어요, 제발!\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"길을 잃었다고!\" 소년이 외쳤다.\n\n\"네, 오늘 아침에 여기서 한참 먼 데서 길을 잃었어요—그러고 나서 제 옷을 빼앗겼고요—지금 이건 제 옷이 아니에요—제 이름은 플로렌스 돔비예요, 제 어린 남동생의 단 하나뿐인 누나예요—그리고, 오 제발, 제발, 저를 좀 보살펴 주세요, 제발!\" 플로렌스가 그토록 오래 억눌러 온 어린아이다운 감정을 한껏 터뜨리며, 울음을 터뜨리며 흐느꼈다. 동시에 그 비참한 보닛이 벗겨지면서 머리카락이 얼굴 주위로 와르르 쏟아져 내렸으니, 솔로몬 길스—일반 선박 계기 제작자—의 조카인 젊은 월터를 말없는 경탄과 연민으로 감동시켰다.\n\n클라크 씨는 놀라움에 사로잡혀 서 있었다. 이 부두에서 이런 깜짝 놀랄 일은 처음 본다고 그는 나직이 중얼거렸다. 월터는 신발을 집어 들어, 이야기 속의 왕자가 신데렐라의 구두를 신겼을 법한 모양으로 그 작은 발에 신겨 주었다. 그는 토끼 가죽을 왼팔에 걸치고, 오른팔을 플로렌스에게 내주었다. 그리고 리처드 휘팅턴 같다기보다는—그건 너무 밋밋한 비유다—오히려 발치에 용을 쓰러뜨려 둔 잉글랜드의 성 게오르기우스가 된 기분이었다.\n\n\"울지 마세요, 돔비 아가씨,\" 월터가 열정에 들떠 말했다. \"제가 여기 있다는 게 저한테는 얼마나 놀라운 일인지! 이제 아가씨는 군함에서 뽑힌 일등 수병으로 이루어진 보트 한 척의 호위를 받는 것만큼이나 안전해요. 오, 울지 마세요.\"\n\n\"이젠 안 울게요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"그냥 기뻐서 우는 거예요.\"\n\n\"기뻐서 운다니!\" 월터는 생각했다. \"그리고 그 까닭이 나라니!\" \"어서 가요, 돔비 아가씨. 이제 다른 신발도 벗겨졌네요! 제 걸 신으세요, 돔비 아가씨.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 안 돼요,\" 플로렌스가 그가 다급히 제 신발을 벗으려 하는 것을 말리며 말했다. \"이게 더 나아요. 이걸로 충분해요.\"\n\n\"하긴 그렇네요,\" 월터가 그녀의 발을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"제 건 일 마일은 더 크니까요. 내가 무슨 생각을 한 거지! 제 걸 신고는 도저히 못 걸으실 텐데! 어서 가요, 돔비 아가씨. 이제 누가 감히 아가씨를 괴롭히는지 어디 한번 봅시다.\"\n\n그리하여 월터는 한껏 사나운 표정을 지으며, 무척 행복해 보이는 플로렌스를 데리고 길을 나섰다. 그들은 팔짱을 끼고 거리를 따라 걸었으니, 그들의 모습이 길에서 일으킬 법한, 또는 실제로 일으킨 그 어떤 놀라움에도 도무지 아랑곳하지 않았다.\n\n날은 어둡고 안개가 끼었으며, 비까지 내리기 시작했다. 그러나 그들은 이런 데에는 조금도 개의치 않았다. 둘 다 플로렌스가 방금 겪은 모험에 온전히 빠져 있었으니, 그녀는 제 나이답게 순진한 진심과 신뢰로 그 일을 이야기했고, 월터는, 템스가의 진흙과 기름때에서 멀리 떨어져, 마치 둘이서 어느 열대의 무인도, 그 너른 잎사귀와 키 큰 나무들 사이를 단둘이 거니는 양 귀 기울였다—실로 그 순간 그는 둘이서 그러고 있다고 곧잘 상상했을 것이다.\n\n\"멀리 가야 하나요?\" 마침내 플로렌스가 눈을 들어 동행의 얼굴을 보며 물었다.\n\n\"아! 그러고 보니,\" 월터가 멈춰 서며 말했다, \"가만, 우리가 어디쯤이지? 아! 알았다. 그런데 사무소는 지금 문을 닫았어요, 돔비 아가씨. 거기엔 아무도 없어요. 돔비 씨는 한참 전에 댁으로 가셨을 거고요. 우리도 댁으로 가야겠지요? 아니, 잠깐만요. 제가 사는 우리 삼촌 댁으로 아가씨를 모시면 어떨까요—여기서 아주 가깝거든요—그리고 마차로 아가씨 댁에 가서 안전하다고 알리고, 옷을 좀 가져다드릴게요. 그게 제일 낫지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같아요,\" 플로렌스가 대답했다. \"안 그래요? 어떻게 생각하세요?\"\n\n그들이 거리에 서서 궁리하고 있을 때, 한 사내가 그들 곁을 지나가면서 월터를 알아본 듯 휙 흘끗 보았으나, 그 첫인상을 바로잡으려는 듯, 멈추지 않고 지나가 버렸다.\n\n\"어, 카커 씨인 것 같은데,\" 월터가 말했다. \"우리 상회의 카커요. 우리 지배인 카커가 아니에요, 돔비 아가씨—다른 카커, 동생 쪽이에요—이봐요! 카커 씨!\"\n\n\"월터 게이인가?\" 그 다른 사람이 멈춰 서서 되돌아오며 말했다. \"이런 묘한 동행이 있어서, 도무지 믿을 수가 없었네.\"\n\n가로등 곁에 서서 월터의 다급한 설명을 놀라움에 차 듣고 있는 그는, 자기 앞에서 팔짱을 낀 두 앳된 모습과는 놀랍도록 대조를 이루었다. 그는 늙지는 않았으나 머리가 하얬다. 몸은 굽었거나, 마치 무슨 큰 근심의 무게에 짓눌린 듯 휘어 있었다. 그리고 지치고 우울한 얼굴에는 깊은 주름이 패여 있었다. 그의 눈빛도, 이목구비의 표정도, 말하는 목소리마저도, 모두 가라앉고 꺼져 있었으니, 마치 그 안의 영혼이 잿더미가 되어 누워 있는 것만 같았다. 그는 점잖게, 그러나 매우 수수하게, 검은 옷을 입고 있었다. 그러나 그의 옷은 그 몸의 전반적인 됨됨이에 맞춰 빚어진 듯, 그의 몸 위에서 움츠러들고 비굴해지며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그 사람 전체가 드러내는 그 슬픈 간청—눈에 띄지 않게, 자기의 비천함 속에 홀로 내버려 두어 달라는 간청—에 동참하는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n그럼에도 젊음과 희망에 대한 그의 관심은 그 영혼의 다른 잿불과 함께 꺼지지는 않았으니, 소년이 말하는 동안 그는 남다른 공감으로 그 진지한 얼굴을 지켜보았다. 다만, 아무리 가두어 두려 애써도 표정으로 새어 나오는, 영문 모를 근심과 연민의 기색을 띠고서였다. 월터가 끝으로, 플로렌스에게 던졌던 그 질문을 그에게 던졌을 때에도, 그는 여전히 그 똑같은 표정으로 소년을 흘끔거리며 서 있었으니, 마치 그 얼굴에서, 지금의 그 환함과는 슬프게도 어긋나는 어떤 운명을 읽어 낸 것만 같았다.\n\n\"어떻게 하면 좋을까요, 카커 씨?\" 월터가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신은 저한테 늘 좋은 조언을 해 주시잖아요, 말을 걸어 주실 때면요. 자주는 아니지만요.\"\n\n\"네 생각이 제일 나은 것 같구나,\" 그가 대답했다. 플로렌스에게서 월터에게로, 다시 그 반대로 시선을 옮기면서.\n\n\"카커 씨,\" 월터가 너그러운 생각에 환해지며 말했다, \"자! 당신한테 기회가 왔어요. 당신이 돔비 씨 댁으로 가서, 좋은 소식을 전하는 심부름꾼이 되세요. 그러면 당신한테 좀 도움이 될지도 몰라요, 선생님. 저는 집에 남을게요. 당신이 가세요.\"\n\n\"내가!\" 그 다른 사람이 되받았다.\n\n\"네. 안 될 게 뭐 있어요, 카커 씨?\" 소년이 말했다.\n\n그는 대답 대신 그저 소년의 손을 흔들어 잡을 뿐이었다. 그조차도 어쩐지 부끄럽고 두려워하는 듯했다. 그리고 잘 자라고, 서두르라고 이른 뒤, 돌아서 가 버렸다.\n\n\"가요, 돔비 아가씨,\" 그들도 돌아서면서 월터가 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며 말했다, \"되도록 빨리 우리 삼촌 댁으로 가요. 돔비 씨가 동생 카커 씨에 대해 말씀하시는 걸 들어 보신 적 있어요, 플로렌스 아가씨?\"\n\n\"아니요,\" 아이가 온순하게 대답했다, \"저는 아빠가 말씀하시는 걸 자주 듣지 못해요.\"\n\n\"아! 그렇겠네! 그건 그분이 부끄러워하셔야 할 일인데,\" 월터는 생각했다. 잠시 말이 없던 동안 그는 제 곁에서 걸어가는 그 온순하고 참을성 있는 작은 얼굴을 내려다보고 있다가, 이윽고 말했다, \"동생 카커 씨는 플로렌스 아가씨가 들어 본 사람 중에 가장 이상한 분이에요. 그분이 저한테 얼마나 유별난 관심을 두시는지, 그러면서도 어떻게 저를 멀리하고 피하시는지, 또 우리 사무소에서 얼마나 낮은 자리에 계신지, 어떻게 한 번도 승진하지 못하시는지, 해가 가도 젊은 사람들이 자기 머리 위로 올라가는 걸 보면서도, 또 그분의 동생이(그분보다 어린데) 우리 수석 지배인인데도, 한 번도 불평하지 않으시는지를 아신다면, 저만큼이나 그분에 대해 어리둥절하실 거예요.\"\n\n플로렌스가 그것에 대해 많이 이해하리라고는 도무지 기대할 수 없었으므로, 월터는 평소의 소년다운 활기와 부산함으로 화제를 바꾸려 애썼다. 그러던 차에 마침 그 불운한 신발 한 짝이 또 벗겨지자, 플로렌스를 안고 삼촌 댁까지 데려가겠다고 했다. 플로렌스는 몹시 지쳤음에도, 그가 자기를 떨어뜨릴까 봐 웃으며 그 제안을 사양했다. 그리고 그들은 어느새 그 나무 해군 사관후보생 가까이에 와 있었고, 월터가 난파라든가 그 밖의 가슴 뭉클한 사고들에서, 자기보다 더 어린 소년들이 플로렌스보다 더 나이 든 소녀들을 보란 듯이 구해 내어 데리고 갔던 갖가지 선례를 계속 들먹이고 있던 까닭에, 계기 제작자의 문 앞에 다다랐을 때에도 둘은 여전히 그 일을 두고 한창 이야기를 나누고 있었다.\n\n\"이봐요, 솔 삼촌!\" 월터가 가게로 뛰어들며 외쳤는데, 그때부터 그날 저녁 내내, 숨을 헐떡이며 두서없이 말했다. \"굉장한 모험이 있었어요! 여기 돔비 씨의 따님이 거리에서 길을 잃고, 마녀 같은 늙은 여자한테 옷을 빼앗겼는데—제가 찾아내서—쉬시라고 우리 응접실로 모셔 왔어요—여기 좀 보세요!\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 솔 삼촌이 자기가 아끼는 나침반 상자에 등을 부딪치며 뒷걸음질 쳤다. \"그럴 리가! 아니, 나는—\"\n\n\"네, 다른 누구라도 그럴 거예요,\" 월터가 나머지 말을 앞질러 말했다. \"아무도 못 믿을 거고, 못 믿겠죠, 아시잖아요. 자! 작은 소파를 난롯가로 옮기는 것 좀 도와주세요, 솔 삼촌—접시들 조심하시고요—이 아가씨한테 저녁거리 좀 썰어 주실래요, 삼촌—저 신발은 쇠살대 밑으로 던져 두세요. 플로렌스 아가씨—발을 난로받이에 올려놓고 말리세요—얼마나 축축한지—굉장한 모험이죠, 삼촌, 그렇죠?—세상에, 나 정말 덥다!\"\n\n솔로몬 길스도 덩달아 똑같이 더웠고, 또 어쩔 줄 모르게 어리둥절했다. 그는 플로렌스의 머리를 토닥이고, 먹으라고 권하고, 마시라고 권하고, 난롯불에 데운 손수건으로 그녀의 발바닥을 문질러 주었으며, 끊임없이 움직이는 조카를 눈과 귀로 좇았으나, 한 번에 스무 가지 일을 해내려고 방 안을 휙휙 날아다니면서도 정작 아무것도 못 하는 그 들뜬 젊은 신사에게 줄곧 부딪치고 채여 넘어진다는 것 말고는 아무것도 또렷이 알아채지 못했다.\n\n\"자, 잠깐만요, 삼촌,\" 그가 양초를 집어 들며 말을 이었다, \"제가 위층에 뛰어 올라가 다른 웃옷으로 갈아입고 올 동안만요. 그러고 나서 떠날게요. 참, 삼촌, 이거 모험 아니에요?\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 솔로몬이 말했다. 안경을 이마에 얹고 그 큰 정밀시계를 주머니에 넣은 채, 소파 위의 플로렌스와 응접실 곳곳에 있는 조카 사이를 쉴 새 없이 오가면서, \"이건 정말이지 가장 기이한—\"\n\n\"아니, 그러지 마시고, 삼촌, 제발—그러세요, 플로렌스 아가씨—저녁이요, 아시잖아요, 삼촌.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그래,\" 솔로몬이 외치며, 마치 거인에게 차려 주기라도 하는 양 곧장 양고기 다리를 썰어 들어갔다. \"내가 이 애를 돌보마, 월리! 알겠다. 예쁜 것! 당연히 굶주렸겠지. 너는 가서 채비하렴. 맙소사! 런던 시장을 세 번이나 지낸 리처드 휘팅턴 경이라니.\"\n\n월터가 자기의 높다란 다락방으로 올라갔다 내려오는 데에는 그리 오래 걸리지 않았으나, 그사이에 플로렌스는 피로에 못 이겨 난롯가에서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작했다. 그 짧은 고요의 짬은, 비록 단 몇 분에 지나지 않았어도, 솔로몬 길스가 정신을 가다듬어 그녀가 편하도록 자그마한 채비를 좀 하고, 방을 어둡게 하고, 불꽃에서 그녀를 가려 주기에는 충분했다. 그리하여 소년이 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 평온하게 잠들어 있었다.\n\n\"잘됐다!\" 그가 속삭이며, 솔로몬을 어찌나 꽉 끌어안았던지 그 얼굴에 새로운 표정이 짜내어졌다. \"이제 떠날게요. 빵 한 조각만 들고 갈게요, 무척 배가 고프거든요—그리고 이 애를 깨우지 마세요, 솔 삼촌.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래,\" 솔로몬이 말했다. \"예쁜 아이로구나.\"\n\n\"예쁘다마다요!\" 월터가 외쳤다. \"저런 얼굴은 처음 봐요, 솔 삼촌. 이제 떠날게요.\"\n\n\"그게 좋겠다,\" 솔로몬이 크게 안도하며 말했다.\n\n\"참, 솔 삼촌,\" 월터가 문틈으로 얼굴을 들이밀며 외쳤다.\n\n\"또 왔네,\" 솔로몬이 말했다.\n\n\"이 애 지금 모습이 어때요?\"\n\n\"아주 행복해 보이는구나,\" 솔로몬이 말했다.\n\n\"그거 좋네요! 이제 떠날게요.\"\n\n\"그러길 바란다,\" 솔로몬이 혼잣말했다.\n\n\"참, 솔 삼촌,\" 월터가 다시 문간에 나타나며 외쳤다.\n\n\"또 왔네!\" 솔로몬이 말했다.\n\n\"거리에서 동생 카커 씨를 만났는데, 어느 때보다 더 이상했어요. 저한테 작별 인사를 하고는, 우리 뒤를 따라 여기까지 왔지 뭐예요—참 묘한 일이죠!—우리가 가게 문에 다다랐을 때 제가 돌아보니, 그분이 조용히 가 버리는 게 보였거든요, 마치 저를 집까지 바래다준 하인처럼, 아니면 충직한 개처럼요. 이 애 지금 모습이 어때요, 삼촌?\"\n\n\"아까랑 거의 똑같다, 월리,\" 솔 삼촌이 대답했다.\n\n\"그거 좋네요. 이제 정말 떠날게요!\"\n\n그리고 이번에는 정말로 떠났다. 솔로몬 길스는 저녁 먹을 생각도 없이 난로 맞은편에 앉아, 잠든 플로렌스를 지켜보며, 더없이 환상적인 건축양식의 허황된 누각을 잔뜩 지어 올렸다. 그리고 그 어슴푸레한 그늘 속에서, 그 모든 계기들에 둘러싸여, 마치 웨일스풍 가발과 커피색 양복으로 변장한 마법사가 아이를 마법의 잠에 빠뜨려 둔 것처럼 보였다.\n\n그러는 사이, 월터는 삯마차 정거장의 삯말로는 좀처럼 내기 힘든 속도로 돔비 씨의 저택을 향해 나아갔다. 그러면서도 이삼 분마다 창밖으로 머리를 내밀어, 조바심을 내며 마부를 나무라곤 했다. 여정의 끝에 다다르자 그는 펄쩍 뛰어내려, 숨을 헐떡이며 하인에게 용건을 알리고는, 그 뒤를 곧장 따라 서재로 들어갔다. 거기서는 떠드는 소리로 한바탕 어지러웠는데, 돔비 씨와 그의 누이, 그리고 톡스 양과 리처즈와 니퍼가 모두 한데 모여 있었다.\n\n\"오! 실례합니다, 선생님,\" 월터가 그에게 달려가며 말했다, \"그렇지만 다 잘 풀렸다는 걸 기쁘게 말씀드립니다, 선생님. 돔비 아가씨를 찾았습니다!\"\n\n트인 얼굴에 머리카락을 휘날리고 눈을 반짝이며, 기쁨과 흥분으로 헐떡이는 그 소년은, 서재의 의자에 앉아 그를 마주한 돔비 씨와 놀랍도록 대조를 이루었다.\n\n\"내가 말했지, 루이자, 그 애는 분명 찾을 거라고,\" 돔비 씨가 톡스 양과 더불어 울고 있는 그 부인을 어깨너머로 슬쩍 보며 말했다. \"더 이상 손쓸 필요가 없다고 하인들에게 알려라. 이 소식을 가져온 이 소년은 사무소의 젊은 게이로군. 내 딸을 어떻게 찾았소, 선생? 어쩌다 잃어버렸는지는 알고 있소만.\" 여기서 그는 위엄 있게 리처즈를 노려보았다. \"그런데 어떻게 찾았소? 누가 찾았소?\"\n\n\"음, 제가 돔비 아가씨를 찾은 것 같습니다, 선생님,\" 월터가 겸손하게 말했다, \"적어도 제가 꼭 찾았다고 그 공을 내세울 수 있을지는 모르겠습니다만, 선생님, 제가 운 좋은 도구가 되어—\"\n\n\"무슨 말이오, 선생,\" 돔비 씨가 끼어들었다. 이 일에서 자기 몫에 대해 소년이 뚜렷이 내비치는 자부심과 기쁨을 본능적인 반감으로 바라보며, \"내 딸을 꼭 찾은 것은 아니라느니, 운 좋은 도구가 되었다느니 하는 게? 분명하고 조리 있게 말하시오, 부탁이오.\"\n\n조리 있게 말하기란 월터의 능력을 한참 벗어나는 일이었으나, 그는 숨 가쁜 처지에서나마 할 수 있는 한 또렷이 설명했고, 자기가 왜 혼자 왔는지를 밝혔다.\n\n\"이거 들었느냐, 얘?\" 돔비 씨가 검은 눈에게 엄하게 말했다. \"필요한 것을 챙겨서, 이 젊은이와 함께 당장 돌아가 플로렌스 아가씨를 집으로 데려와라. 게이, 자네에게는 내일 상을 내리겠네.\"\n\n\"오! 고맙습니다, 선생님,\" 월터가 말했다. \"정말 친절하십니다. 저는 정말이지 어떤 상도 생각하지 않았습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"자네는 소년이야,\" 돔비 씨가 별안간, 거의 사납게 말했다. \"그리고 자네가 무얼 생각하든, 혹은 생각하는 척하든, 그건 별 대수롭지 않은 일이지. 자네는 잘했네, 선생. 그걸 망치지 마시게. 루이자, 이 아이에게 포도주를 좀 주시오.\"\n\n돔비 씨의 시선은 칙 부인의 안내를 받아 방을 나서는 월터 게이를 날카로운 못마땅함으로 좇았다. 그리고 월터가 수전 니퍼 양과 함께 삼촌 댁으로 마차를 타고 돌아갈 때에도, 그의 마음의 눈이 그를 더 흐뭇한 마음으로 좇았던 것은 아닐 터였다.\n\n거기서 그들은, 잠으로 한껏 기운을 차린 플로렌스가 저녁을 먹고서, 솔로몬 길스와의 사이가 부쩍 가까워져, 그와 더없이 미덥고 편안한 사이가 되어 있는 것을 보았다. 검은 눈은(어찌나 많이 울었던지 이제 붉은 눈이라 불릴 만했고, 매우 말이 없고 풀이 죽어 있었다) 한마디 반박이나 책망도 없이 그녀를 와락 품에 안았고, 무척 히스테릭한 상봉을 벌였다. 그러고는 잠깐 동안 그 응접실을 사사로운 분장실로 삼아, 매우 정성스레 그녀에게 제대로 된 옷을 입혔다. 이윽고, 타고난 결격 사유가 허락하는 한 최대한 돔비답게 꾸민 모습으로 그녀를 데리고 나섰다.\n\n\"안녕히 계세요!\" 플로렌스가 솔로몬에게 달려가며 말했다. \"정말 잘해 주셨어요.\"\n\n늙은 솔은 무척 기뻐하며, 마치 손녀에게 하듯 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"안녕, 월터! 잘 있어요!\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"잘 가요!\" 월터가 두 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"당신을 절대 잊지 않을게요,\" 플로렌스가 말을 이었다. \"아니요! 정말로 절대 안 잊을 거예요. 잘 있어요, 월터!\"\n\n고마운 마음의 천진함 속에서, 아이는 얼굴을 들어 그를 향했다. 월터는 제 얼굴을 숙였다가, 온통 빨갛게 달아오른 채 다시 들고는, 무척 쑥스러워하며 솔 삼촌을 보았다.\n\n\"월터는 어디 있죠?\" \"잘 있어요, 월터!\" \"잘 가요, 월터!\" \"한 번만 더 악수해요, 월터!\" 작은 하녀와 함께 마차에 갇히고 나서도, 이것이 여전히 플로렌스의 외침이었다. 그리고 마차가 마침내 떠나갈 때, 문간 계단에 선 월터는 그녀가 흔드는 손수건에 쾌활하게 답했다. 한편 그 뒤의 나무 해군 사관후보생은, 그 자신처럼, 그 마차 하나에만 온 신경을 쏟으며 지나가는 다른 모든 마차는 시야에서 제쳐 놓는 듯했다.\n\n이윽고 돔비 씨의 저택에 다시 다다랐고, 서재에서는 또다시 떠드는 소리가 일었다. 마차에는 또다시 기다리라는 분부가 내려졌다—\"리처즈 부인을 위해서\"라고, 수전의 동료 하인 하나가 플로렌스와 함께 지나가는 그녀를 보고 불길하게 속삭였다.\n\n잃어버렸던 아이의 등장은 약간의 동요를 일으켰으나, 그리 대단하지는 않았다. 그 애를 한 번도 찾아 나선 적 없는 돔비 씨는 그 이마에 한 번 입을 맞추고는, 다시는 달아나지 말 것이며 믿지 못할 시중꾼들과 어디로든 떠돌지 말라고 일렀다. 칙 부인은, 「자선 연마공」이 미덕의 길로 손짓해 부른다 해도, 인간 본성의 타락을 두고 늘어놓던 한탄을 멈추었다. 그리고 완전한 돔비 사람에게만 마땅한 환대에는 어딘가 못 미치는 환영으로 그 애를 맞이했다. 톡스 양은 자기 앞에 놓인 본보기에 맞춰 자기 감정을 조절했다. 리처즈, 그 죄인 리처즈만이 홀로 끊긴 환영의 말들로 마음을 쏟아 내었고, 마치 정말로 그 애를 사랑하기라도 하는 양 그 떠돌다 온 작은 머리 위로 몸을 숙였다.\n\n\"아, 리처즈!\" 칙 부인이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"이제 막 천성의 양분을 너무 일찍 빼앗기게 될 그 어린아이를 위해, 자네가 진작에 어떤 합당한 정을 보였더라면, 자기 동포를 좋게 생각하고 싶어 하는 이들에게 훨씬 더 흡족하고, 또 자네에게도 훨씬 더 어울리는 일이었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"끊겨 버렸어요,\" 톡스 양이 애처로운 속삭임으로 말했다, \"하나의 공통된 샘에서!\"\n\n\"만일 그게 내 배은망덕한 처지였다면,\" 칙 부인이 엄숙하게 말했다, \"그리고 내가 자네 같은 생각을 한다면, 리처즈, 나는 「자선 연마공」의 옷이 내 아이를 시들게 하고, 그 교육이 내 아이의 목을 조를 것만 같은 기분일 거야.\"\n\n사실인즉—그러나 칙 부인은 몰랐지만—그 애는 그 옷 때문에 이미 꽤나 시들어 있었다. 그리고 교육으로 말하자면, 그 응보의 효과조차 머지않아 나타날 만했으니, 그것은 흐느낌과 매질의 폭풍이었으니까.\n\n\"루이자!\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"이런 이야기를 길게 늘어놓을 필요는 없소. 저 여자는 해고되고 삯도 치러졌소. 너는 이 집을 떠나라, 리처즈, 내 아들을—내 아들을,\" 돔비 씨가 이 두 단어를 힘주어 되풀이하며 말했다, \"몸서리 없이는 생각조차 할 수 없는 소굴과 무리 속으로 데려간 죄로 말이다. 오늘 아침 플로렌스 아가씨에게 닥친 사고로 말하자면, 나는 그것을, 어떤 큰 의미에서, 다행스럽고 운 좋은 일로 여기네. 왜냐하면 그 일이 없었더라면, 나는 결코—그것도 네 입으로—네가 무슨 잘못을 저질렀는지 알 수 없었을 테니까. 내 생각에는, 루이자, 다른 유모, 저 젊은 사람은,\" 여기서 니퍼 양이 큰 소리로 흐느꼈다, \"워낙 훨씬 어리고, 폴의 유모에게 부득이 영향을 받았을 테니, 남아도 좋겠소. 부디 이 여자의 마차 삯을—\" 돔비 씨가 말을 멈추고 움찔했다—\"스택스 가든까지로 지불하도록 일러 주시오.\"\n\n폴리는 문 쪽으로 움직였고, 플로렌스가 그 옷자락에 매달려 가지 말라고 더없이 애처롭게 울며 매달렸다. 자기가 부정할 수 없는 피붙이가, 자기는 곁에 앉아 있는데도, 이 보잘것없는 낯선 이에게 그토록 매달리는 것을 보는 일은, 그 오만한 아버지의 가슴에 꽂히는 비수요, 그의 머릿속에 박히는 화살이었다. 자기 딸이 누구에게로 향하든, 또 누구에게서 등을 돌리든, 그가 마음 쓴 것은 아니었다. 그 빠르고 날카로운 고통이 그를 꿰뚫고 지나간 것은, 자기 아들이라면 어떻게 할까 하는 생각이 들었기 때문이었다.\n\n어쨌든 그의 아들은 그날 밤 목청껏 울었다. 솔직히 말하자면, 가엾은 폴은 그 나이의 아이들이 흔히 그러는 것보다 더 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어 눈물을 흘렸다. 자기의 두 번째 어머니를—그가 아는 한 첫 번째 어머니를—제 삶의 시작을 어둡게 했던 그 천부의 비통만큼이나 갑작스러운 일격으로 잃어버렸으니까. 그 똑같은 타격으로, 그토록 구슬프게 울다 잠든 그 누나 또한 그만큼이나 착하고 진실한 벗을 잃었다. 그러나 그것은 지금 이야기와는 사뭇 동떨어진 문제다. 그것에 대해서는 말을 낭비하지 말기로 하자.\n\n톡스 양의 거처를 새의 눈으로 내려다본 풍경, 그리고 톡스 양의 애정 상태에 대하여\n\n톡스 양은 영국 역사의 어느 머나먼 시기에 도시 서쪽 끝의 멋들어진 동네 안으로 비집고 들어와 끼인, 어둑한 작은 집에 살았다. 그 집은 모퉁이를 돌면 나오는 큰 거리의 가난한 친척처럼 그늘 속에 서서, 위풍당당한 저택들에게 냉랭하게 내려다보였다. 그곳은 딱히 골목 안이라고도 할 수 없고 딱히 마당 안이라고도 할 수 없었으나, 막다른 길 가운데서도 가장 따분한 막다른 길에 있어, 멀리서 들려오는 이중 노크 소리에 불안하고 초췌해져 있었다. 돌포장의 틈새마다 풀이 돋아나는 이 은거지의 이름은 프린세스 플레이스였다. 그리고 프린세스 플레이스에는 댕그랑거리는 종이 달린 프린세스 채플이 있었는데, 일요일이면 많을 때는 스물다섯 명이나 되는 사람들이 예배에 참석하기도 했다. 프린세스 암스도 그곳에 있었는데, 화려한 하인들이 자주 드나드는 곳이었다. 프린세스 암스 앞 난간 안에는 가마 하나가 놓여 있었으나, 사람들의 기억으로는 그것이 한 번도 밖으로 나온 적이 없었다. 그리고 화창한 아침이면 난간의 꼭대기마다(톡스 양이 여러 번 세어 본 바로는 마흔여덟 개였다) 백랍 단지가 하나씩 장식처럼 얹혀 있었다.\n\n프린세스 플레이스에는 톡스 양의 집 외에도 다른 개인 주택이 한 채 더 있었다. 어쩌다가도 결코 열리는 법이 없는, 사자 머리 모양의 거대한 노커 한 쌍이 달린 거대한 대문 한 쌍은 말할 것도 없거니와—그 대문은 누군가의 마구간으로 통하던, 지금은 쓰이지 않는 입구일 것으로 짐작되었다. 사실 프린세스 플레이스의 공기에는 마구간 냄새가 살짝 배어 있었다. 그리고 (뒤쪽에 있던) 톡스 양의 침실에서는 마구간 골목이 한눈에 내다보였는데, 그곳에서는 마부들이 무슨 일을 하고 있든 끊임없이 부글거리는 소리를 입에서 내며 일했고, 마부와 그 아내와 가족들의 더없이 사사롭고 은밀한 옷가지들이 으레 맥베스의 군기처럼 바깥벽에 널려 있었다.\n\n프린세스 플레이스의 이 다른 개인 주택은, 가정부와 결혼한 은퇴한 집사가 세 들어 사는 곳이었는데, 가구가 딸린 방들이 독신 신사 한 사람에게 세놓여 있었다. 즉, 나무토막 같은 이목구비에 푸르죽죽한 얼굴을 하고 눈알이 머리에서 튀어나올 듯한 소령이었는데, 톡스 양은 그에게서, 그녀 자신의 표현을 빌리자면 \"정말이지 군인다운 무언가\"를 알아보았다. 그리고 그녀와 그 사이에는, 이따금 신문과 소책자를 주고받는 일과 그런 플라톤식의 정담이, 소령의 검은 하인을 매개로 하여 이루어졌다. 톡스 양은 그 하인을 어떤 지리적 관념과도 연결 짓지 않은 채 그저 \"원주민\"으로 분류해 두는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n톡스 양의 집 현관과 계단보다 더 작은 현관과 계단은 아마 없었을 것이다. 아마 위에서 아래까지 통틀어, 그것은 영국에서 가장 불편하고 가장 비뚤어진 작은 집이었을 것이다. 그러나 그래도 톡스 양은 말했다, 이 위치가 어떤가! 겨울에는 그곳에서 햇빛을 거의 받을 수 없었다. 형편이 가장 좋을 때조차 해가 들지 않았다. 바람은 말할 것도 없었고, 오가는 행인은 담벼락에 막혀 있었다. 그래도 톡스 양은 말했다, 이 위치를 생각해 보라니까! 눈알이 머리에서 튀어나올 듯한 푸르죽죽한 얼굴의 소령도 똑같이 말했다. 그는 프린세스 플레이스를 자랑스러워했고, 클럽에서 기회만 닿으면 화제를 모퉁이를 돌면 나오는 큰 거리의 위대한 인물들과 관련된 무언가로 돌리기를 좋아했으니, 그들이 자기 이웃이라고 말할 수 있는 만족을 누리기 위해서였다.\n\n요컨대 톡스 양과 푸르죽죽한 얼굴의 소령에게는, 프린세스 플레이스가—사회의 아주 작은 한 조각에게, 또 다른 종류의 보잘것없는 부류 중 많은 식객들에게 그러하듯이—좋은 연줄을 지니고 그 혈관에 점잖은 피가 흐른다는 것으로 족했다. 가난하든, 초라하든, 누추하든, 어리석든, 따분하든 상관없었다. 모퉁이를 돌면 나오는 큰 거리가 프린세스 플레이스로 슬며시 이어졌고, 하이 홀번에서라면 발끈하는 욕설이 되었을 말도 프린세스 플레이스를 두고 한다면 노골적인 신성모독이 되었다.\n\n톡스 양이 사는 그 칙칙한 셋집은 그녀 자신의 것이었으니, 로켓 속 생선 같은 눈의 고인이 된 주인이 그녀에게 유언으로 물려준 것이었다. 그 주인의 분 바른 머리에 땋은 머리를 늘어뜨린 작은 초상화가, 응접실 벽난로의 양쪽 끝에서 주전자 받침과 균형을 이루고 있었다. 가구의 대부분은 분 바른 머리와 땋은 머리 시대의 것이었으니, 늘 비실비실하게 가느다란 네 활 모양 다리를 누군가의 길목에 떡 벌리고 있는 접시 보온기와, 제작자의 이름 둘레에 스위트피 화환이 그려진 한물간 하프시코드가 그중에 있었다. 집안 어느 구석에서든 방문객들은 으레 떠도는 곰팡내를 알아챘다. 그리고 따뜻한 날씨면, 톡스 양이 테레빈유에 적신 펜의 잘못된 끝으로 벽판의 온갖 틈과 갈라진 곳에 무언가를 쓰는 듯한 모습이 목격되곤 했다.\n\n배그스톡 소령은 고상한 문학에서 이른바 인생의 한복판이라 일컫는 지점에 다다라, 목이라곤 거의 없고 턱뼈는 매우 뻣뻣하며 코끼리처럼 길게 늘어진 귀를 단 채, 그리고 두 눈과 안색은 앞서 언급한 인위적으로 흥분한 상태로, 내리막 여정을 걸어 내려가는 중이었지만, 톡스 양의 관심을 일깨운 것을 어마어마하게 자랑스러워했고, 그녀가 자기에게 눈독을 들이는 멋진 여인이라는 허구로 자기 허영심을 간질였다. 이를 그는 클럽에서 여러 번 넌지시 비쳤으니, 늙은 조 배그스톡, 늙은 조이 배그스톡, 늙은 J. 배그스톡, 늙은 조시 배그스톡 따위가 영원한 주제가 되는 자질구레한 농담과 곁들여서였다. 말하자면 자기 자신의 이름과 더없이 허물없는 사이가 되는 것이야말로, 소령의 경쾌한 익살의 아성이요 본성루였던 것이다.\n\n\"조이 B.는 말이오, 선생,\" 소령은 지팡이를 한 번 휘두르며 말하곤 했다, \"당신 같은 사람 열둘 몫은 하지요. 당신네 가운데 배그스톡 혈통이 몇 명만 더 있었더라면, 선생, 당신네한테 조금도 손해 될 게 없었을 거요. 늙은 조는 말이오, 선생, 마음만 먹는다면 지금이라도 아내 구하려고 멀리 찾아다닐 필요가 없소. 하지만 그는 무정한 사람이오, 선생, 조라는 사람은 말이오—그는 질기지요, 선생, 질기고, 지독히도 교활하다오!\" 이런 선언 뒤에는 쌕쌕거리는 소리가 들리곤 했다. 그리고 소령의 푸른빛은 자줏빛으로 짙어졌고, 그동안 두 눈은 발작하듯 부릅떠지며 튀어나왔다.\n\n그러나 자기 자신을 그토록 후하게 칭송했음에도, 소령은 이기적이었다. 마음으로 보아 그보다 더 완전히 이기적인 사람이 일찍이 있었을지 의심스러울 정도였다. 아니, 마음이라기보다는 위장이라고 하는 편이 더 적절할 터인데, 그가 분명 전자의 기관보다는 후자의 기관을 훨씬 더 두둑이 타고났기 때문이다. 그는 누군가에게 무시당하거나 홀대받는다는 생각 따위는 하지 못했다. 더구나 톡스 양에게 무시당하고 홀대받는다는 것은 추호도 이해할 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n그런데 보아하니, 톡스 양은 그를 잊고 있었다—차츰차츰 그를 잊어 갔다. 그녀는 투들 가족을 발견한 직후부터 그를 잊기 시작했다. 세례식 무렵까지 줄곧 그를 잊어 갔다. 그 후로는 복리로 불어나듯 더더욱 그를 잊어 갔다. 무언가 혹은 누군가가 관심거리로서 그의 자리를 대신했던 것이다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 부인,\" 지난 장에 기록된 변화들이 있은 지 몇 주 뒤에, 프린세스 플레이스에서 톡스 양과 마주친 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 선생님,\" 톡스 양은 매우 쌀쌀맞게 말했다.\n\n\"조 배그스톡은 말입니다, 부인,\" 소령은 여느 때의 정중함으로 말했다, \"꽤 오랫동안 부인의 창가에서 부인께 인사 올리는 행복을 누리지 못했습니다. 조는 박대를 당했지요, 부인. 그의 태양이 구름 뒤에 가려 있었습니다.\"\n\n톡스 양은 고개를 숙였다. 그러나 정말이지 매우 쌀쌀맞게.\n\n\"조의 빛나는 별께서 어쩌면 도시를 떠나 계셨던 게 아닌지요, 부인,\" 소령이 물었다.\n\n\"제가요? 도시를 떠나요? 오 아니에요, 저는 도시를 떠난 적이 없는걸요,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"요즘 매우 바빴답니다. 제 시간은 거의 다 어떤 아주 가까운 벗들에게 바쳐지고 있어요. 지금도 여유가 통 없을까 걱정이네요. 안녕히 가세요, 선생님!\"\n\n톡스 양이 더없이 매혹적인 걸음걸이와 자태로 프린세스 플레이스에서 사라지자, 소령은 그 어느 때보다 더 푸르죽죽해진 얼굴로 그녀의 뒷모습을 바라보고 서서, 도무지 칭찬이라고는 할 수 없는 말들을 중얼중얼 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"아니, 빌어먹을, 선생,\" 소령은 가재 같은 두 눈알을 프린세스 플레이스 이리저리로 굴리며, 그 향긋한 공기를 향해 외쳤다, \"여섯 달 전만 해도, 저 여자는 조시 배그스톡이 밟고 다니는 땅까지 사랑했단 말이야. 이게 대체 무슨 영문이지?\"\n\n소령은 한참 생각한 끝에 그것이 덫을 뜻한다고, 음모와 올가미를 뜻한다고, 톡스 양이 함정을 파고 있는 것이라고 결론지었다. \"하지만 부인, 당신은 조를 잡지 못할 거요,\" 소령이 말했다. \"그는 질기지요, 부인, J.B.는 질겨요. 질기고, 지독히도 교활하다오!\" 그는 이 생각을 곱씹으며 그날 하루 내내 낄낄거렸다.\n\n그러나 그래도, 그날과 다른 여러 날이 지나고 흘러가도, 톡스 양은 소령에게 전혀 아랑곳하지 않는 듯했고, 그에 대해서는 도무지 아무 생각도 하지 않는 듯했다. 한때 그녀는 어쩌다 자기의 작고 어두운 창문 중 하나로 밖을 내다보다가, 얼굴을 붉히며 소령의 인사에 답하곤 했다. 그러나 이제는 소령에게 그럴 기회를 도무지 주지 않았고, 그가 길 건너편을 쳐다보든 말든 전혀 개의치 않았다. 다른 변화들도 일어났다. 소령은 자기 방 그늘 속에 서서, 최근 톡스 양의 집에 한결 산뜻한 기운이 감돌게 되었음을 알아챌 수 있었다. 늙은 작은 카나리아를 위해 금박 입힌 철사로 만든 새 새장이 마련되었고, 색색의 마분지와 종이를 오려 만든 갖가지 장식물이 벽난로 선반과 탁자들을 꾸미는 듯했으며, 화초 한두 포기가 창가에 불쑥 돋아났고, 톡스 양이 이따금 하프시코드를 연주했는데, 그 스위트피 화환은 코펜하겐 왈츠와 버드 왈츠가 실린 악보로 머리에 관을 쓴 듯 늘 보란 듯이 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n# 제3장\n\n토록스 양이 직접 베껴 적은 책이었다.\n이 모든 것에 더하여, 토록스 양은 오래전부터 보기 드물게 정성스럽고 우아하게, 가벼운 상복 차림을 하고 있었다. 그러나 이것이 소령을 곤혹스러움에서 건져 주었다. 그는 속으로, 그녀가 작은 유산을 물려받아 거만해진 것이라고 단정해 버렸다.\n\n이런 결론에 이르러 마음의 짐을 던 바로 그 이튿날, 소령은 아침 식사 자리에 앉아 있다가, 토록스 양의 자그마한 응접실에서 어찌나 어마어마하고 놀라운 환영이 보였던지, 한동안 의자에 못 박힌 듯 앉아 있었다. 그러더니 옆방으로 달려들어가 쌍안식 오페라 망원경을 들고 돌아와서는, 그것을 통해 몇 분 동안 그 환영을 뚫어지게 살폈다.\n\n\"아기야, 이런, 오만 파운드를 걸고,\" 소령은 다시 망원경을 접으며 말했다, \"갓난아기란 말이야!\"\n\n소령은 그 일을 잊을 수가 없었다. 그는 휘파람을 불고 멍하니 바라보는 것밖에는 아무것도 할 수 없었는데, 그 정도가 어찌나 심했던지 지금 그렇게 변한 두 눈에 비하면 예전의 그의 눈은 차라리 움푹 꺼지고 들어가 있었다고 할 만했다. 날이면 날마다, 한 주에 두 번, 세 번, 네 번씩 이 아기가 다시 나타났다. 소령은 여전히 멍하니 바라보며 휘파람을 불었다. 그 밖의 모든 면에서 그는 프린세스 플레이스에 홀로 있는 셈이었다. 토록스 양은 그가 무엇을 하든 더는 신경 쓰지 않았다. 그가 푸른빛이 아니라 검은빛이었다 한들, 그녀에게는 아무런 상관이 없었을 것이다.\n\n* 푸른빛: 원문 'blue'는 우울함을 뜻하는 동시에 소령 이름 'Bagstock'을 떠올리게 하는 말장난으로, 검은빛(black)과 대비된다.\n그녀가 이 아기와 그 유모를 데리러 프린세스 플레이스를 나서고, 그들과 함께 걷고, 다시 그들을 집까지 바래다주고, 끊임없이 그들을 지키며 보초를 서던 그 끈기란, 그리고 직접 아기를 돌보고, 먹이고, 놀아 주고, 하프시코드로 곡조를 들려주어 그 어린 핏줄을 얼어붙게 만들던 그 끈기란 실로 대단한 것이었다. 또한 거의 같은 무렵, 그녀는 어떤 팔찌 하나를 바라보는 일에 열을 올렸고, 달을 바라보는 일에도 열을 올려서, 제 침실 창문에서 오래도록 달을 관찰하곤 했다. 그러나 그녀가 무엇을 바라보든—해든, 달이든, 별이든, 팔찌든—소령만큼은 더 이상 바라보지 않았다. 그리고 소령은 휘파람을 불고, 멍하니 바라보고, 의아해하고, 제 방 안을 이리저리 서성거렸으나, 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"자네는 정말이지 우리 오라버니 폴의 마음을 완전히 사로잡을 거야, 그건 틀림없어, 자네,\" 어느 날 칙 부인이 말했다.\n\n토록스 양의 얼굴이 창백해졌다.\n\n\"날이 갈수록 폴을 더 닮아 가는구나,\" 칙 부인이 말했다.\n\n토록스 양은 어린 폴을 두 팔에 안고서, 쓰다듬느라 그 아이의 모자 장식을 완전히 납작하고 흐물흐물하게 만들어 놓는 것 말고는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"이 아이의 어머니 말인데요, 자네,\" 토록스 양이 말했다, \"내가 자네를 통해 알게 되었어야 할 분 말이에요, 이 아이가 그분을 조금이라도 닮았나요?\"\n\n\"전혀요,\" 루이자가 응수했다.\n\n\"그분은—그분은 예뻤다고들 하던데요?\" 토록스 양이 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 가엾고 사랑스러운 패니는 매력적인 사람이었어요,\" 칙 부인이 한참 신중히 따져 본 뒤에 말했다. \"분명 매력적이었지요. 어쩐지 거의 당연하게, 우리 오라버니의 아내라면 으레 갖추었으리라 기대하게 되는 그런 군림하는 듯한 위엄은 없었어요. 또 그런 남자에게 필요한 그런 정신의 힘과 기백도 없었고요.\"\n\n토록스 양이 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"하지만 사람을 기쁘게 하는 데가 있었어요.\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"더할 나위 없이요. 게다가 마음씨는—오, 세상에, 가엾은 패니는 얼마나 마음씨가 고왔는지!\"\n\n\"이 천사 같으니!\" 토록스 양이 어린 폴에게 외쳤다. \"네 아빠를 꼭 빼닮은 그림 같은 아이!\"\n\n만일 소령이, 얼마나 많은 희망과 모험이, 얼마나 숱한 계획과 기대가 그 아기의 머리 위에 걸려 있는지를 알 수 있었더라면, 그리고 그것들이 온갖 잡다한 혼란과 무질서 속에서, 아무것도 모르는 어린 폴의 주름진 모자 둘레를 맴도는 모습을 볼 수 있었더라면, 그는 정말이지 눈이 휘둥그레졌을 것이다. 그랬다면 그는 그 무리 가운데서 토록스 양에게 속한 야심에 찬 티끌과 들보 몇 개를 알아보았을 것이고, 그랬다면 아마도 돔비 회사에 대한 그 부인의 머뭇거리는 투자의 본질을 이해했을 것이다.\n* 티끌과 들보: 「마태복음」 7장의 \"남의 눈 속 티끌과 제 눈 속 들보\" 비유를 빗댄 표현.\n\n만일 그 아이 자신이 한밤중에 깨어나, 제 요람 휘장 둘레에 모여든, 다른 사람들이 저를 두고 꾸는 꿈의 희미한 잔영들을 보았더라면, 그것들은 마땅히 그를 겁에 질리게 했을지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 잠을 자고 있었으니, 토록스 양의 친절한 의도도, 소령의 의아함도, 누이의 이른 슬픔도, 아버지의 준엄한 환상도 똑같이 알지 못한 채, 그리고 이 땅 어느 한 귀퉁이에 돔비나 아들이라는 것이 존재한다는 사실조차 모르는 채, 천진하게 잠들어 있었다.\n\n# 제4장\n\n시간이라는—말하자면 또 하나의 소령이라 할—그 지켜보고 주의 깊은 눈길 아래, 폴의 잠은 차츰 변해 갔다. 점점 더 많은 빛이 그 잠 속으로 새어 들었고, 점점 더 또렷한 꿈들이 그것을 흔들어 놓았다. 갈수록 쌓여 가는 사물과 인상들의 무리가 그의 휴식 주위로 몰려들었다. 그렇게 그는 갓난아기 시절에서 어린아이 시절로 넘어갔고, 말하고 걷고 신기해하는 돔비가 되었다.\n\n리처즈가 쫓겨나 추방된 뒤로, 육아실은 위원회 관리 체제에 들어갔다고 할 만했다. 어떤 공공 부서가 그것을 떠받칠 만한 개개인의 아틀라스를 찾지 못할 때 더러 그러하듯이 말이다. 그 위원들은 물론 칙 부인과 톡스 양이었고, 둘은 어찌나 놀라운 열성으로 자기들 임무에 몰두했던지, 백스톡 소령은 날마다 자기가 버림받았다는 새로운 사실을 새삼 떠올리게 되었으며, 한편 가정의 감독을 잃은 칙 씨는 화려한 사교계에 몸을 던져 클럽과 커피하우스에서 식사하고, 세 번에 걸쳐 담배 냄새를 풍기고, 혼자 연극을 보러 가고, 요컨대 (칙 부인이 한번 그에게 말했듯이) 온갖 사회적 결속과 도덕적 의무를 느슨하게 풀어 놓았다.\n\n그러나 어릴 적의 그 모든 장래성에도 불구하고, 이 모든 경계와 보살핌으로도 어린 폴을 무럭무럭 자라는 아이로 만들지는 못했다. 아마도 본디 허약한 체질이었던지, 그는 유모가 쫓겨난 뒤로 시들고 야위어 갔고, 오랫동안 그저 사람들의 손 사이로 미끄러져 빠져나가 잃어버린 어미를 찾아 떠날 기회만 기다리는 듯이 보였다. 어른으로 향하는 그 장애물 경주에서 이 위험한 구간을 지나고서도, 그는 여전히 무척 험한 길을 달려야 했고, 가는 길의 온갖 장애물에 시달리며 괴로움을 겪었다. 이가 하나 날 때마다 그것은 목이 부러질 만큼 위험한 울타리였고, 홍역으로 돋는 발진 하나하나가 그에게는 돌담이었다. 백일해 발작이 일 때마다 그는 쓰러졌고, 그가 다시 일어나지 못하도록 줄줄이 꼬리를 물고 몰려오는 자잘한 질병들의 한 무리에게 짓밟히고 깔려 으스러졌다. 개똥지빠귀 대신 어떤 맹금류가 그의 목구멍 속으로 들어왔고, 병아리들조차—그 새끼 새들이 이름을 빌려준 그 유아병과 무슨 관련이 있다면—사납게 변해서 호랑이 살쾡이처럼 그를 괴롭혔다.\n* 개똥지빠귀(thrush)는 영어로 구내염(아구창)을 뜻하기도 하고, 병아리(chicken)는 수두(chicken-pox)를 가리키는 말장난이다.\n\n폴의 세례식 때의 그 냉기가, 어쩌면 그의 본성 가운데 어떤 예민한 부분을 깊숙이 찔렀던 것인지도 모른다. 그 부분은 아버지의 차가운 그늘 속에서 도무지 회복될 수 없었다. 어쨌든 그날 이후로 그는 불운한 아이였다. 위컴 부인은 그토록 시달림을 받는 귀한 아이는 본 적이 없다고 곧잘 말했다.\n\n위컴 부인은 어느 식당 종업원의 아내였는데—이는 다른 어느 사내의 미망인이나 다를 바 없다는 뜻인 듯했다—돔비 씨 댁에서 일하게 해 달라는 그녀의 청원은, 그녀에게 따라다닐 정인(情人)이나 따를 사람이 있을 가능성이 도무지 없어 보인다는 이유로 호의적으로 받아들여졌다. 그녀는 폴이 모질게 젖을 뗀 지 하루 이틀 만에 그의 유모로 고용되었다. 위컴 부인은 온순한 여인으로, 살결이 희고, 눈썹은 늘 치켜올라가 있었으며, 고개는 늘 수그리고 있었다. 그녀는 자기 자신을 가엾이 여기거나, 남에게 가엾이 여겨지거나, 다른 누군가를 가엾이 여길 채비가 늘 되어 있었다. 또 모든 일을 더없이 처량하고 가련한 빛으로 바라보고, 거기에 무시무시한 선례들을 끌어다 붙이며, 그 재주를 발휘하는 데서 더없는 위안을 얻는 놀라운 천성의 재능을 지니고 있었다.\n\n이런 기질의 기미가 돔비 씨의 그 장엄한 인식에는 단 한 차례도 닿지 않았다는 것은 굳이 짚을 필요도 없겠다. 만일 조금이라도 닿았더라면 그것이야말로 정말 놀라운 일이었으리라. 집안에서—심지어 칙 부인이나 톡스 양조차도—어린 폴과 관련하여 단 한 번이라도 조금이나마 불안해할 이유가 있었다는 것을 그에게 감히 속삭인 사람이 아무도 없었으니 말이다. 그는 마음속으로, 아이가 반드시 어떤 사소한 질병들의 일정한 통과 의례를 거쳐야 하며, 그 일을 빨리 치를수록 좋다고 단정해 두었다. 만일 민병대에 뽑히는 운 나쁜 제비를 뽑았을 때처럼 그 일을 돈을 주고 면하거나 대리인을 세울 수 있었다면, 그는 후한 조건으로 기꺼이 그렇게 했을 것이다. 그러나 그것이 가능하지 않았으므로, 그는 그저 이따금 거만한 태도로 자연이 도대체 무슨 뜻으로 이러는가 의아해할 따름이었다. 그리고 길 위에서 이정표 하나를 또 지났으니 여정의 그 위대한 종착점이 그만큼 더 가까워졌다는 생각으로 스스로를 위로했다. 지금 그의 마음에서 가장 위에 자리한 감정, 그리고 폴이 자랄수록 끊임없이 강해지고 커져 가는 감정은 조바심이었기 때문이다. 그들 부자가 한데 어우러진 위세와 위대함에 대한 자신의 환상이 의기양양하게 실현될 그날이 오기를 바라는 조바심.\n\n어떤 철학자들은 이기심이야말로 우리의 가장 좋은 사랑과 애정의 뿌리라고 우리에게 일러 준다. 돔비 씨의 어린 아들은 처음부터 그에게, 자기 자신의 위대함의—혹은 (같은 말이지만) 돔비와 아들의 위대함의—한 부분으로서 너무도 분명하게 중요했으므로, 그의 부정(父情) 또한 빛나는 명성을 자랑하는 숱한 훌륭한 상부구조들과 마찬가지로 매우 천한 토대까지 손쉽게 거슬러 올라가 더듬어 볼 수 있었으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 그러나 그는 자기가 가진 사랑을 다해 아들을 사랑했다. 그의 서리 낀 마음에 따뜻한 자리가 한 군데라도 있었다면, 그 자리는 아들이 차지했다. 그 더없이 단단한 표면이 어떤 형상이라도 새겨 받을 수 있었다면, 그곳에는 그 아들의 형상이 있었다. 다만 그것은 갓난아기로서나 소년으로서가 아니라, 다 자란 어른—회사의 '아들'—로서의 형상이었다. 그러므로 그는 미래로 나아가, 그 사이에 끼인 자기 인생의 대목들을 서둘러 지나가고 싶어 안달이었다. 그러므로 그는 사랑에도 불구하고 그 대목들에 대해 걱정이 거의, 아니 전혀 없었다. 마치 그 아이가 마법에 걸린 목숨을 지녀, 자기가 생각 속에서 그토록 끊임없이 교감하고, 또 이미 존재하는 현실인 양 날마다 계획하고 구상하는 그 어른이 되고야 말 것처럼 느꼈으니까.\n\n이렇게 폴은 자라서 거의 다섯 살이 되었다. 그는 예쁘장한 꼬마였다. 다만 그 자그마한 얼굴에는 어딘가 핏기 없고 수심에 잠긴 데가 있어서, 위컴 부인이 의미심장하게 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 길게 한숨을 들이쉬게 만드는 빌미가 되곤 했다. 그의 성미는 훗날 자라서 거만해지리라는 것을 충분히 약속했고, 그는 자기 자신의 중요함과, 그 앞에 다른 모든 사물과 사람이 마땅히 복종해야 한다는 것에 대해, 더 바랄 나위 없을 만큼 야무진 자각을 지니고 있었다. 그는 이따금 충분히 어린아이답고 장난기가 넘쳤으며 시무룩한 기질도 아니었다. 그러나 어떤 때는 묘하고 예스럽고 생각에 잠긴 태도로, 자기의 자그마한 안락의자에 앉아 골똘히 생각에 빠지곤 했는데, 그럴 때면 그는 동화 속에 나오는 그 무서운 작은 존재들 가운데 하나처럼 보이고 또 그렇게 말했다. 백오십 살이나 이백 살쯤 된 그 존재들은, 자기들이 대신 들어앉은 그 아이들을 기괴하게 흉내 내며 나타나는 법이다. 그는 위층 육아실에서 곧잘 이런 조숙한 기분에 사로잡히곤 했다. 그리고 때로는, 플로렌스와 놀고 있을 때나 톡스 양을 외바퀴 마차 끌듯 끌고 다닐 때조차도, 별안간 그 기분에 빠져들어 자기가 피곤하다고 외치곤 했다. 그러나 그가 그 기분에 그토록 확실하게 빠져드는 때는, 자기의 작은 의자가 아버지의 방으로 옮겨져 내려가, 식사 후 난롯가에서 아버지와 함께 거기 앉아 있을 때만 한 적이 없었다. 그런 때 두 사람은 난롯불이 일찍이 비춘 적이 없을 만큼 기이한 한 쌍이었다. 돔비 씨는 너무나 꼿꼿하고 근엄하게 그 불빛을 응시하고, 그의 작은 분신은 늙디늙은 얼굴로 그 붉게 펼쳐진 광경을 현인의 고정되고 넋 나간 주의력으로 들여다보았다. 돔비 씨는 복잡한 세속의 계획과 구상을 품고 있었고, 그 작은 분신은 하느님만이 아실 어떤 거친 공상과 채 다 영글지 못한 생각과 떠도는 사색을 품고 있었다. 돔비 씨는 풀 먹인 옷과 오만함으로 뻣뻣했고, 그 작은 분신은 물려받은 천성으로, 또 자기도 모르는 흉내로 뻣뻣했다. 둘은 그토록 똑 닮았으면서도, 또 그토록 어처구니없이 대조를 이루었다.\n\n그런 때 가운데 한번은, 둘 다 오랫동안 완전히 조용히 있어서, 돔비 씨는 이따금 아이의 눈을—거기에는 밝은 난롯불이 보석처럼 반짝이고 있었다—흘끗 보고서야 아이가 깨어 있다는 것을 알 수 있었는데, 그때 어린 폴이 이렇게 침묵을 깨뜨렸다.\n\n\"아빠! 돈이 뭐예요?\"\n\n그 느닷없는 질문은 돔비 씨가 생각하던 바로 그 주제와 너무나 직접 맞닿아 있어서, 돔비 씨는 완전히 당황하고 말았다.\n\n\"돈이 무엇이냐고, 폴?\" 그가 대답했다. \"돈?\"\n\n\"네,\" 아이가 자기 작은 의자의 팔걸이에 두 손을 얹고, 늙은 얼굴을 돔비 씨 쪽으로 치켜들며 말했다. \"돈이 뭐예요?\"\n\n돔비 씨는 난처해졌다. 그는 유통 매개, 통화, 통화 가치의 하락, 지폐, 지금(地金), 환율, 시장에서의 귀금속 가치, 따위의 용어가 들어간 어떤 설명을 해 주고 싶었다. 그러나 그 작은 의자를 내려다보고, 그것이 얼마나 까마득히 아래에 있는가를 보고는, 이렇게 대답했다. \"금하고, 은하고, 구리란다. 기니, 실링, 반 페니. 그게 뭔지 알지?\"\n\n\"네, 그럼요, 그게 뭔지 알아요,\" 폴이 말했다. \"그걸 말하는 게 아니에요, 아빠. 그래도 돈이 결국 뭐냐는 거예요.\"\n\n천지신명이여, 아이가 그 얼굴을 다시 아버지 쪽으로 치켜들었을 때 그 얼굴은 어찌 그리 늙어 보이던지!\n\n\"돈이 결국 무엇이냐니!\" 돔비 씨는 의자를 약간 뒤로 빼며 말했다. 그런 질문을 던지는 그 건방진 티끌 같은 존재를 더 잘, 그저 어이가 없어 바라보기 위해서였다.\n\n\"제 말은요, 아빠, 돈이 뭘 할 수 있냐는 거예요,\" 폴이 두 팔을 (팔짱을 끼기엔 거의 길이가 모자랐지만) 끼고서, 난롯불을 보았다가, 아버지를 올려다보았다가, 다시 난롯불을 보았다가, 또 아버지를 올려다보며 대꾸했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 의자를 본디 자리로 다시 끌어당기고는, 아이의 머리를 토닥였다. \"차차 더 잘 알게 될 거다, 우리 아들,\" 그가 말했다. \"돈은 말이다, 폴, 무엇이든 다 할 수 있단다.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 그 작은 손을 잡아, 자기 한 손에 대고 부드럽게 톡톡 쳤다.\n\n그러나 폴은 되도록 빨리 손을 빼내고는, 마치 자기 재치가 손바닥에 들어 있어 그것을 갈기라도 하는 듯, 자기 의자의 팔걸이에 손을 가만히 이리저리 문질렀다—그리고 다시 난롯불을 바라보았는데, 마치 그 불이 자기의 조언자이자 길잡이라도 되는 듯했다—그러더니 잠깐 뜸을 들였다가 되물었다.\n\n\"무엇이든요, 아빠?\"\n\n\"그래. 무엇이든—거의,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"무엇이든이라는 건 모든 거라는 뜻이죠, 그렇죠, 아빠?\" 아들이 물었다. 그 단서를 알아차리지 못했거나, 어쩌면 이해하지 못한 채로.\n\n\"모든 걸 포함하지, 그래,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 왜 돈은 우리 엄마를 살려 주지 않았어요?\" 아이가 되물었다. \"돈은 잔인하지 않잖아요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"잔인하다고!\" 돔비 씨는 목도리를 매만지며, 그런 생각에 발끈하는 듯이 말했다. \"아니다. 좋은 것은 잔인할 수가 없지.\"\n\n\"그게 좋은 거고, 또 무엇이든 할 수 있다면,\" 그 꼬마가 다시 난롯불을 돌아보며 생각에 잠겨 말했다, \"왜 우리 엄마를 살려 주지 않았는지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n이번에는 아버지에게 그 질문을 던진 것이 아니었다. 어쩌면 그 질문이 이미 아버지를 불편하게 만들었다는 것을, 아이 특유의 눈치 빠름으로 알아차렸는지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 그 생각을, 마치 그것이 자기에게는 아주 오래된 것이고 또 자기를 무척 괴롭혀 온 것인 양 소리 내어 되풀이했다. 그리고 턱을 손에 괸 채, 여전히 골똘히 생각하며 난롯불 속에서 어떤 해답을 찾는 듯이 앉아 있었다.\n\n돔비 씨는 놀람에서—아니, 거의 경악이라 해도 좋을 것에서(아이가 이런 식으로 자기 곁에 저녁마다 앉아 있었음에도, 어머니라는 주제를 그에게 꺼낸 것은 이번이 그야말로 처음이었으니까)—정신을 차리고는, 아이에게 이렇게 설명해 주었다. 돈이라는 것은 비록 매우 강력한 정령이어서 무슨 이유로도 결코 깎아내릴 것이 못 되지만, 죽을 때가 된 사람들을 살아 있게 할 수는 없다고. 그리고 우리는 안타깝게도 누구나 다 죽어야 하는데, 아무리 부유하더라도, 심지어 시티 안에서도 그렇다고. 그러나 돈은 우리가 존경받고, 두려움의 대상이 되고, 우러름을 받고, 떠받들리고, 칭송받게 해 주며, 모든 사람의 눈에 우리를 강력하고 영예롭게 만들어 준다고. 그리고 돈은 종종 죽음마저도, 그것도 꽤 오랫동안, 물리칠 수 있다고. 예를 들어, 돈이 그의 엄마에게 필킨스 씨의 진료를 보장해 주었으며, 그 덕을 폴 자신도 곧잘 보았고, 또한 폴이 한 번도 본 적 없는 저 위대한 파커 펩스 박사의 진료도 마찬가지였다고. 그리고 돈은 무릇 할 수 있는 일이라면 다 해낼 수 있다고. 이런 이야기를, 같은 취지의 더 많은 말과 함께, 돔비 씨는 아들의 마음에 불어넣어 주었고, 아이는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울이며 자기에게 하는 말의 대부분을 이해하는 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"돈이 저를 튼튼하고 아주 건강하게 만들어 줄 수도 없죠, 아빠? 그렇죠?\" 폴이 잠시 잠잠히 있다가, 자그마한 두 손을 비비며 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 너는 튼튼하고 아주 건강한걸,\" 돔비 씨가 대꾸했다. \"그렇지 않으냐?\"\n\n오! 다시 치켜든 그 얼굴은, 절반은 우울하고 절반은 능청스러운 표정을 띤 채로, 어찌 그리 늙어 보이던지!\n\n\"너는 너만 한 꼬마들이 으레 그렇듯 튼튼하고 건강하지? 응?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"플로렌스는 저보다 나이가 많은데, 저는 플로렌스만큼 튼튼하고 건강하지 못해요, 저도 알아요,\" 아이가 대꾸했다. \"그리고 플로렌스가 저만큼 어렸을 때는, 지치지 않고 훨씬 더 오래 놀 수 있었을 거예요. 저는 가끔 너무 피곤해요,\" 어린 폴이 두 손을 녹이며, 마치 그 안에서 무슨 유령 인형극이라도 벌어지고 있는 듯이 난로 살창 사이를 들여다보면서 말했다, \"그리고 뼈가 너무 아파서요(위컴이 그러는데 제 뼈래요), 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"그래! 하지만 그건 밤이지,\" 돔비 씨가 자기 의자를 아들의 의자에 더 가까이 끌어당기고, 한 손을 아이의 등에 가만히 얹으며 말했다. \"꼬마들은 밤이면 피곤한 게 당연하지, 그래야 잠을 잘 자니까.\"\n\n\"아, 밤이 아니에요, 아빠,\" 아이가 대꾸했다. \"낮이에요. 그러면 저는 플로렌스 무릎에 누워요, 그럼 플로렌스가 노래를 불러 줘요. 밤에는 정말 이-상-한 것들을 꿈꿔요!\"\n\n그러고 그는 다시 두 손을 녹이며, 그 손을 두고 골똘히 생각에 잠겼는데, 마치 노인이나 어린 도깨비 같았다.\n\n돔비 씨는 너무도 놀랍고, 너무도 불편하고, 어떻게 대화를 이어 가야 할지 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수가 없어서, 그저 한 손을 아이의 등에 얹은 채—마치 어떤 자력에 이끌려 거기 붙들려 있는 것처럼—난롯불 빛에 아들을 바라보고 앉아 있을 수밖에 없었다. 한번은 그가 다른 한 손을 내밀어, 그 사색에 잠긴 얼굴을 잠시 자기 쪽으로 돌렸다. 그러나 그가 놓아 주자마자 그 얼굴은 다시 난롯불을 찾았고, 유모가 그를 잠자리로 부르러 나타날 때까지, 깜박이는 불꽃 쪽을 향한 채로 있었다.\n\n\"플로렌스가 저를 데리러 왔으면 좋겠어요,\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 유모 위컴이랑 가지 않으시려고요, 폴 도련님?\" 그 시중꾼이 매우 애처로운 투로 물었다.\n\n\"안 가요,\" 폴이 마치 이 집의 주인이라도 되는 양 다시 안락의자에 자세를 고쳐 앉으며 대꾸했다.\n\n위컴 부인은 그의 순진함에 축복을 빌어 주고 물러갔고, 이내 그 대신 플로렌스가 나타났다. 아이는 곧바로 갑작스러운 채비와 생기로 벌떡 일어나, 아버지에게 잘 자라고 인사하며 그를 향해 얼굴을 들어 올렸는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 훨씬 더 환하고, 훨씬 더 어려 보이고, 전체적으로 훨씬 더 어린아이다웠던지, 돔비 씨는 그 변화에 크게 안도하면서도 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n둘이 함께 방을 나간 뒤, 그는 부드러운 목소리로 노래하는 소리가 들리는 듯했다. 그리고 폴이 누이가 자기에게 노래를 불러 준다고 했던 말이 떠올라, 호기심에 문을 열고 귀를 기울이며 둘의 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 누이는 그 크고 널찍하고 텅 빈 계단을, 아이를 품에 안고 힘겹게 올라가고 있었다. 아이의 머리는 누이의 어깨에 얹혀 있었고, 한쪽 팔은 무심한 듯 그녀의 목에 둘려 있었다. 그렇게 둘은 힘겹게 올라갔다. 누이는 내내 노래를 불렀고, 폴은 이따금 가냘프게 흥얼거리며 화음을 보탰다. 돔비 씨는 둘이 계단 꼭대기에 이를 때까지—도중에 쉬려고 멈춰 서기도 하면서—그 뒷모습을 바라보았고, 둘이 그의 시야에서 사라진 뒤에도, 흐릿한 채광창을 통해 우울하게 가물거리는 달의 희미한 빛이 그를 자기 방으로 돌려보낼 때까지, 여전히 위쪽을 올려다보며 서 있었다.\n\n이튿날 저녁 식사 때 칙 부인과 톡스 양이 회의에 소집되었다. 그리고 식탁보가 걷히자, 돔비 씨가 절차를 시작하며, 폴에게 무슨 문제가 있는지, 그리고 필킨스 씨가 아이에 대해 무어라 했는지를, 아무런 꾸밈이나 숨김 없이 알려 달라고 요구했다.\n\n\"왜냐하면 그 아이가,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"내가 바라는 만큼 튼튼하지가 않아서 말이오.\"\n\n\"폴, 너는,\" 칙 부인이 대꾸했다, \"여느 때처럼 그 행복한 분별력으로—그건 너와 함께 있을 때마다 내가 부럽다 못해 약해질 지경인데—그리고 톡스 양도 그렇게 생각하리라 믿어.\"\n\n\"오, 부인!\" 톡스 양이 나직이 말했다, \"어찌 안 그러겠어요? 그런 경지를 우러러보려는 것은 외람된 일이지만, 그래도 밤의 새가 감히—아니, 돔비 씨께 그런 감상으로 폐를 끼치진 않겠어요. 그저 불불에 관한 거랍니다.\"\n\n* 불불(Bulbul): 페르시아 시에서 장미를 사모하는 새, 곧 나이팅게일을 뜻한다.\n\n돔비 씨는 불불이라는 것을 오래도록 자리 잡은 유서 깊은 집단인 양 여기며, 위엄 있게 고개를 끄덕여 그 존재를 인정했다.\n\"여느 때처럼 그 행복한 분별력으로, 폴,\" 칙 부인이 말을 이었다, \"너는 단번에 핵심을 짚었어. 우리 귀염둥이는 전체적으로 우리가 바랄 만큼 튼튼해. 사실은, 그 애 정신이 그 애에게 너무 벅찬 거야. 영혼이 그 애 몸에 비해 너무나 큰 거지. 그 귀여운 아이가 말하는 품이라니!\" 칙 부인이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"아무도 안 믿을 거야. 글쎄, 루크리셔, 바로 어제 그 애가 장례식이라는 주제에 대해 한 말이라니!\"\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 돔비 씨가 짜증스럽게 누이의 말을 가로막으며 말했다, \"위층에 있는 사람들 가운데 누군가가 아이에게 부적절한 주제를 들이미는 것 같소. 그 애가 어젯밤 나한테 자기—자기 뼈에 대해 이야기를 하더란 말이오,\" 돔비 씨가 그 말에 짜증스러운 힘을 주며 말했다. \"도대체 누가 내 아들의—그—뼈와 무슨 상관이 있단 말이오? 그 애가 살아 있는 해골은 아니지 않소, 설마.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요,\" 칙 부인이 형언할 수 없는 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"그러길 바라오,\" 그녀의 오라비가 대꾸했다. \"또 장례식이라니! 누가 아이한테 장례식 이야기를 한단 말이오? 우리가 장의사도, 장례 곡꾼도, 무덤 파는 사람도 아니지 않소.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요,\" 칙 부인이 아까와 똑같은 깊은 표정으로 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그럼 누가 그런 것들을 그 애 머릿속에 넣어 준단 말이오?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"정말이지 어젯밤엔 나도 꽤나 당황하고 놀랐소. 누가 그런 것들을 그 애 머릿속에 넣어 주느냐고, 루이자?\"\n\n\"폴,\" 칙 부인이 잠시 잠잠히 있다가 말했다, \"물어봐야 소용없어. 내 솔직히 말하지만, 나는 위컴이 아주 명랑한 기질을 지닌 사람이라거나, 이른바—\"\n\"모무스의 딸이라고는,\" 톡스 양이 나직이 거들었다.\n\n* 모무스(Momus): 그리스 신화의 조롱과 비난, 풍자의 신.\n\n\"바로 그래,\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 그 여자는 더없이 세심하고 쓸모가 있고, 또 전혀 주제넘지가 않아. 정말이지 그렇게 고분고분한 여자는 본 적이 없어. 내가 법정에 서서 재판을 받게 되더라도 그 점만은 그 여자를 위해 말해 주겠어.\"\n\n\"흥! 너는 지금 법정에 서서 재판을 받는 게 아니다, 루이자,\" 돔비 씨가 발끈하며 대꾸했다, \"그러니 그건 상관없는 일이야.\"\n\n\"폴,\" 칙 부인이 경고하는 목소리로 말했다, \"나한테는 다정하게 말해 줘야 해, 안 그러면 나는 끝장이야,\" 그와 동시에 칙 부인의 눈꺼풀에는 비를 예고하는 붉은 기운이 번지기 시작했는데, 그것은 날씨가 곧장 바뀌지 않는 한 어김없이 비가 온다는 징조였다.\n\n\"내가 물은 건, 루이자,\" 돔비 씨가 어조를 바꾸어, 그리고 점잖이 한 박자 쉬고서 말했다, \"폴의 건강과 지금의 실제 상태에 대해서였소.\"\n\n\"우리 귀여운 아이가,\" 칙 부인이, 마치 모든 것을 처음 이야기하는 게 아니라 이미 다 합의된 바를 요약하는 사람의 어조로 말했다, \"그 지난번 발작으로 조금 약해졌고, 우리가 바랄 만큼 그렇게 정정하지는 못하다면, 그리고 그 애 체질에 일시적인 허약함이 좀 있어서, 이따금 잠깐씩 자기—자기—그—을 못 쓰게 될 것처럼 보인다면—\"\n\n칙 부인은 돔비 씨가 방금 뼈에 대해 반발한 터라 사지(四肢)라는 말을 하기가 겁이 나서, 톡스 양의 도움말을 기다렸다. 톡스 양은 제 소임에 충실하게 \"지체(肢體)\"라는 말을 슬쩍 던졌다.\n\n\"지체라니!\" 돔비 씨가 되받았다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 그 의학 선생이 다리라고 한 것 같은데, 루이자, 안 그래요?\" 톡스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 당연히 그랬지, 얘,\" 칙 부인이 부드럽게 나무라듯 받아쳤다. \"어떻게 나한테 그걸 물어? 너도 들었잖니. 내 말은, 우리 귀여운 폴이 잠깐씩 다리를 못 쓰게 된다 하더라도, 이런 건 그 또래의 많은 아이들에게 흔한 사고이고, 그 어떤 보살핌이나 조심으로도 막을 수 없는 일이라는 거야. 그걸 빨리 알아들을수록, 폴, 그리고 그걸 인정할수록 좋아. 만일 어린 폴에게 쏟아부은 보살핌과 조심과 애정과 자기희생의 양에 대해 조금이라도 의심이 든다면, 그 문제는 네 주치의에게나, 아니면 이 집안의 어느 아랫사람에게라도 한번 물어보고 싶구나. 타울린슨을 불러 봐,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"그 사람은 우리한테 좋은 편견 같은 건 없는 줄로 아는데, 오히려 정반대지. 타울린슨이 무슨 비난을 할 수 있는지 한번 들어 보고 싶어!\"\n\n\"분명 너도 알 텐데, 루이자,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"나는 내 집안의 미래 가장에 대한 너의 타고난 헌신과 애정을 의심하는 게 아니야.\"\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘구나, 폴,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"하지만 정말이지 너는 참 별나고, 가끔 아주 이상하게 말을 한다니까, 본심은 아니라는 거 나도 알지만 말이야. 만일 네 귀여운 아들의 영혼이 그 애 몸에 비해 너무 크다면, 폴, 그게 누구 탓인지를—그러니까 그 애가 누구를 닮았는지를—기억하고, 그걸 좋게 받아들여야 해. 그 애는 아빠를 더할 나위 없이 빼닮았어. 길거리에서도 사람들이 알아본다니까. 내가 듣기로는, 세례식 때 벌써 그 교구 직원조차도 그걸 알아봤대. 그 사람은 아주 점잖은 사람이고, 자기 자식들도 있어. 알 만한 사람이지.\"\n\n\"필킨스 씨가 오늘 아침에 폴을 보았다지요?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 봤어,\" 누이가 대꾸했다. \"톡스 양과 나도 함께 있었어. 톡스 양과 나는 늘 함께 있지. 우리는 그걸 원칙으로 삼고 있으니까. 필킨스 씨는 며칠째 그 애를 봐 왔는데, 내 보기엔 아주 영리한 사람이야. 그 사람 말로는 이렇다 할 게 없대. 그게 조금이라도 위안이 된다면, 나도 그 말을 확인해 줄 수 있어. 그런데 오늘은 바닷바람을 권했어. 아주 현명한 처사야, 폴, 나는 확신해.\"\n\n\"바닷바람,\" 돔비 씨가 누이를 보며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"거기엔 불안해할 게 하나도 없어,\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"우리 집 조지랑 프레더릭도 둘 다 그 또래 무렵에 바닷바람을 처방받았고, 나도 여러 번 처방받았는걸. 폴, 나도 네 말에 전적으로 동의해, 어쩌면 위층에서 그 애 앞에서 부주의하게 입에 올린 화제들이 있어서, 그 애의 어린 마음이 그것을 곱씹지 않는 편이 나았을 거라는 점에 말이야. 하지만 그 애처럼 영민한 아이의 경우엔 그걸 어떻게 막을 수 있는지 정말 모르겠어. 그 애가 평범한 아이라면야 아무 문제도 없겠지. 나도 톡스 양과 같은 생각인데, 이 집을 잠시 떠나 있는 것과, 브라이턴의 공기와, 이를테면 핍친 부인처럼 그토록 분별 있는 사람의 신체적·정신적 훈련이라면—\"\n\n\"핍친 부인이 누구야, 루이자?\" 돔비 씨가, 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 이름을 이렇게 허물없이 들이미는 데에 어이없어하며 물었다.\n\n\"핍친 부인은, 폴,\" 누이가 대꾸했다, \"나이 지긋한 부인인데—톡스 양이 그 사람 내력을 다 알아—얼마 전부터 자기 정신의 온 힘을 다 쏟아, 더없이 큰 성공을 거두며 유아 연구와 보육에 힘써 온 사람이고, 인맥도 아주 좋았어. 그 사람 남편은 가슴이 찢어져 죽었는데—얘, 그 남편이 어쩌다 가슴이 찢어져 죽었다고 했더라? 정확한 사정을 잊어버렸네.\"\n\n\"페루 광산에서 물을 퍼내다가요,\" 톡스 양이 대답했다.\n\n\"물론 본인이 직접 물을 펐다는 건 아니고,\" 칙 부인이 오라비를 흘끗 보며 말했다. 정말이지 그 설명을 덧붙일 필요가 있어 보였는데, 톡스 양이 마치 그가 펌프 손잡이를 잡은 채 죽기라도 한 것처럼 말했기 때문이다. \"그 사업에 돈을 투자했는데, 그게 실패한 거야. 핍친 부인의 아이 다루는 솜씨는 정말 놀라운가 봐. 내가—저런—이만했을 때부터 사람들이 사적인 자리에서 그걸 칭찬하는 걸 들어 왔으니까!\" 칙 부인의 눈은 피트 씨 흉상 옆 책장 언저리를 헤맸는데, 그것은 바닥에서 십 피트쯤 되는 높이였다.\n\n\"핍친 부인에 대해 제가 한 말씀 드리는 게 좋을 듯합니다, 선생님,\" 톡스 양이 순진한 듯 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다, \"이렇게 콕 집어 거론되었으니 말입니다. 선생님의 다정하신 누이께서 그분에게 보내신 찬사는 마땅한 것입니다. 지금은 다 자라 사회의 흥미로운 일원이 된 숱한 신사 숙녀들이 그분의 보살핌에 신세를 졌지요. 지금 선생님께 말씀드리는 이 미천한 사람도 한때 그분의 보호 아래 있었답니다. 또 제가 알기로는 어린 귀족들조차 그분의 시설에 낯설지 않다고 해요.\"\n\n\"그 점잖은 부인이 시설을 운영한다는 말이오, 톡스 양?\" 돔비 씨가 짐짓 너그러이 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 정말이지 모르겠어요,\" 그 부인이 대꾸했다, \"제가 그것을 그렇게 불러도 될지 말이에요. 결코 예비학교 같은 건 아니에요. 제 뜻을 표현하자면,\" 톡스 양이 유달리 다정하게 말했다, \"그것을 아주 엄선된 부류의 유아 기숙관이라고 일컬으면 될까요?\"\n\n\"지극히 한정되고 특별한 규모로 말이지요,\" 칙 부인이 오라비를 흘끗 보며 거들었다.\n\n\"오! 배타성 그 자체죠!\" 톡스 양이 말했다.\n\n여기엔 무언가가 있었다. 핍친 부인의 남편이 페루 광산 때문에 가슴이 찢어져 죽었다는 것은 좋았다. 그것은 풍성한 울림이 있었다. 게다가 돔비 씨는, 의사가 옮기라고 권한 마당에 폴이 한 시간이라도 더 지금 있는 곳에 머문다는 생각에 거의 경악에 가까운 상태였다. 그것은, 잘해 봐야 더디게 갈 수밖에 없는 그 종착점에 닿기까지 아이가 가로질러야 할 그 길 위에서의 멈춤이요 지연이었다. 그들이 핍친 부인을 추천한 것은 그에게 큰 무게가 있었다. 두 사람이 자기들이 맡은 아이에 대한 그 어떤 간섭도 시샘한다는 것을 그는 알았기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 방금 보여 주었듯이 자기 나름의 확고한 견해를 가진 그 책임을, 두 사람이 어쩌면 나누어 지고 싶어 할지도 모른다는 것은 그는 한순간도 염두에 두지 않았다. 페루 광산 때문에 가슴이 찢어져 죽었다, 돔비 씨는 곰곰이 생각했다. 흠! 참으로 점잖게 죽는 방식이로군.\n\n\"내일 알아본 끝에, 폴을 브라이턴의 그 부인에게 내려보내기로 결정한다면, 누가 그 애와 함께 가야 하지?\" 돔비 씨가 한참 생각한 끝에 물었다.\n\n\"지금으로선 플로렌스 없이는 그 애를 어디로도 보낼 수 없을 거야, 폴,\" 누이가 머뭇거리며 대꾸했다. \"그 애가 누이한테 아주 푹 빠져 있거든. 알다시피 그 애는 무척 어리고, 또 제 나름의 변덕이 있으니까.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 고개를 돌리고, 천천히 책장으로 가서 자물쇠를 열고는, 읽을 책 한 권을 가지고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"또 누가 있겠나, 루이자?\" 그가 고개도 들지 않고, 책장을 넘기며 말했다.\n\n\"위컴은 당연하고. 위컴이면 충분할 거야, 내 보기엔,\" 누이가 대꾸했다. \"폴이 핍친 부인 같은 분의 손에 맡겨지는 마당에, 그 부인을 한층 더 견제할 만한 사람은 보내려야 보내기 어려울 거야. 너야 물론 적어도 일주일에 한 번은 직접 내려가 볼 테고.\"\n\n\"물론이지,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. 그러고는 그 뒤로 한 시간 동안, 한 글자도 읽지 않으면서 한 쪽만 들여다보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n이 명성 자자한 핍친 부인은 놀랍도록 못생기고 심술궂은 노부인으로, 허리가 구부정하고, 질 나쁜 대리석처럼 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴에, 갈고리 같은 코와, 모루 위에서 망치질을 당해도 끄떡없을 듯한 단단한 잿빛 눈을 지니고 있었다. 페루 광산이 핍친 씨의 죽음이 된 지 적어도 사십 년은 흘렀건만, 그 미망인은 여태도 검은 봄버진을 입고 다녔는데, 그 빛깔이 어찌나 윤기 없고 짙고 죽은 듯 침침했던지, 어두워진 뒤엔 가스등으로도 그녀를 환히 밝힐 수가 없었고, 그녀가 있으면 아무리 많은 촛불도 꺼지는 듯했다. 사람들은 흔히 그녀를 아이들의 \"대단한 관리자\"라고 일컬었는데, 그 관리법의 비결은, 아이들이 싫어하는 것은 무엇이든 다 주고, 좋아하는 것은 아무것도 주지 않는 것이었다—그러면 아이들의 성정이 매우 누그러지는 것으로 드러났다. 그녀가 어찌나 신랄한 노부인이었던지, 페루의 그 펌프 기계가 잘못 쓰인 게 아닐까, 광산 대신 그녀의 모든 기쁨의 샘물과 인정의 젖이 죄다 바싹 퍼내져 버린 게 아닐까 믿고 싶어질 지경이었다.\n* 봄버진(bombazeen): 비단과 모직을 섞어 짠 직물로, 흔히 상복에 쓰였다.\n\n이 식인귀 같은, 아이 잡는 노파의 성채는 브라이턴의 어느 가파른 골목길에 있었다. 그곳은 흙이 유달리 백악질에 부싯돌투성이고 메말랐으며, 집들은 유달리 잘 부서지고 얇았다. 그곳의 자그마한 앞뜰들은 무엇을 심든 메리골드 말고는 아무것도 피우지 못하는 영문 모를 성질을 지녔고, 그곳에서는 달팽이들이 거리로 난 문이며, 도무지 장식할 거라고 기대되지 않는 다른 공공장소들에 부항단지처럼 끈질기게 들러붙어 있는 것이 늘 발견되곤 했다. 겨울철엔 성채에서 공기를 빼낼 수가 없었고, 여름철엔 공기를 들일 수가 없었다. 그 안에서 바람이 어찌나 끊임없이 메아리쳤던지, 마치 커다란 소라 껍데기 같아서, 거주자들은 좋든 싫든 밤낮으로 그것을 귀에 대고 있을 수밖에 없었다. 본디 향긋한 냄새가 나는 집은 아니었다. 그리고 한 번도 열리지 않는 앞쪽 거실 창가에 핍친 부인은 화분에 심은 식물들을 모아 두었는데, 그것들이 자기들 나름의 흙냄새를 그 시설에 보태 주었다. 게다가 그 종류로는 더없이 빼어난 표본들이었음에도, 이 식물들은 핍친 부인을 두르기에 유달리 어울리는 종류였다. 거기엔 선인장 표본이 대여섯 점 있었는데, 털북숭이 뱀들처럼 가느다란 나무오리를 휘감고 비틀려 있었다. 또 다른 표본 하나는 초록빛 바닷가재처럼 넓적한 집게발을 뻗치고 있었다. 끈적끈적 들러붙는 잎을 지닌 덩굴 식물도 여럿 있었고, 천장에 매달린 보기 흉한 화분이 하나 있었는데, 그것은 마치 끓어 넘친 것처럼 보였으며, 길게 늘어진 초록빛 끝자락으로 그 아래를 지나는 사람들을 간질여 거미를 떠올리게 했다—거미로 말하자면 핍친 부인의 거처가 유난히 풍성하게 길러 냈으나, 어쩌면 철이 되면 집게벌레라는 점에서 한층 더 자랑스레 경쟁을 걸었을지도 모른다.\n\n그렇지만 핍친 부인의 청구 요금 기준은, 낼 형편이 되는 모든 이에게 높았고, 또 핍친 부인은 누구를 위해서든 자기 본성의 한결같은 신맛을 좀체 누그러뜨리는 법이 없었으므로, 그녀는 \"어린아이의 성격에 관한 지식이 가히 과학적인, 의지가 놀랍도록 굳은 노부인\"으로 통했다. 이런 평판과, 핍친 씨의 찢어진 가슴 덕분에, 그녀는 남편이 죽은 이래로 이 해 저 해 따져 가며 그럭저럭 넉넉한 생계를 꾸려 올 수 있었다. 칙 부인이 처음 그녀를 입에 올린 지 사흘 만에, 이 훌륭한 노부인은 돔비 씨의 주머니에서 자기의 현 수입에 짭짤한 보탬이 더해지리라 미리 내다보는 흐뭇함을 누렸고, 플로렌스와 그녀의 어린 동생 폴을 성채의 식구로 맞아들이게 되었다.\n\n전날 밤 둘을 데리고 내려온(다들 한 호텔에서 그 밤을 보냈다) 칙 부인과 톡스 양은 막 문 앞에서 마차를 몰아 다시 집으로 가는 길에 올랐다. 그리고 핍친 부인은 난로를 등진 채 서서, 늙은 병사처럼 새로 온 아이들을 살피고 있었다. 핍친 부인의 중년 조카딸은, 마음씨 곱고 헌신적인 그녀의 노예였으나 비쩍 마르고 쇠틀에 죈 듯한 인상에 코에 뾰루지가 잔뜩 나서 무척 시달리는 사람이었는데, 비더스톤 도련님이 행진할 때 차고 있던 깨끗한 깃을 벗기고 있었다. 지금으로선 단 하나뿐인 또 다른 어린 기숙생 팽키 양은, 손님들 앞에서 세 번 코를 훌쩍였다는 죄로, 방금 성채 지하 감옥(뒤편에 있는, 교정 목적에 바쳐진 빈방)으로 끌려간 참이었다.\n\n\"자, 도련님,\" 핍친 부인이 폴에게 말했다, \"내가 어떨 것 같은가? 마음에 들 것 같은가?\"\n\n\"아주머니가 전혀 마음에 안 들 것 같아요,\" 폴이 대꾸했다. \"저는 가고 싶어요. 여긴 우리 집이 아니에요.\"\n\n\"아무렴. 여긴 내 집이지,\" 핍친 부인이 받아쳤다.\n\n\"아주 고약한 집이에요,\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 이 집엔 여기보다 더 나쁜 데도 있단다,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다, \"우리가 나쁜 아이들을 가둬 두는 데 말이야.\"\n\n\"쟤도 거기 들어가 봤어요?\" 폴이 비더스톤 도련님을 가리키며 물었다.\n\n핍친 부인이 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였고, 그날 남은 시간 동안 폴은, 비더스톤 도련님을 머리끝부터 발끝까지 뜯어보고, 그 얼굴에 떠오르는 온갖 움직임을 지켜보는 일로 분주했다. 신비롭고 끔찍한 경험을 한 소년에게 따라붙기 마련인 그 흥미를 느끼면서 말이다.\n\n한 시가 되자 저녁 식사가 나왔는데, 주로 곡물과 채소 종류였다. 그때 팽키 양(아침마다 마사지를 받아서, 통째로 문질러 닳아 없어질 위험에 처한 듯한, 온순하고 자그마한 파란 눈의 한 조각 아이였다)이 그 식인귀 노파에게 직접 이끌려 갇혀 있던 데서 나왔고, 손님들 앞에서 코를 훌쩍이는 자는 누구도 천국에 가지 못한다는 가르침을 받았다. 이 위대한 진리가 그녀에게 톡톡히 새겨지자, 그녀에게는 쌀밥이 상으로 주어졌다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 성채에서 정해진 형식의 감사 기도를 읊었는데, 거기엔 맛있는 저녁을 주신 핍친 부인에게 감사하는 특별 조항이 들어 있었다. 핍친 부인의 조카딸 베린시아는 식은 돼지고기를 먹었다. 핍친 부인은, 체질상 따뜻한 영양이 필요하다 하여, 양고기 갈비로 특식을 차렸는데, 그것은 접시 두 장 사이에 끼워 뜨겁고 뜨겁게 들어왔고 아주 맛있는 냄새가 났다.\n\n식사 후에 비가 와서 바닷가로 산책을 나갈 수도 없었고, 또 갈비를 먹은 핍친 부인의 체질이 휴식을 요했으므로, 아이들은 베리(다른 말로 베린시아)와 함께 지하 감옥으로 갔다. 그곳은 백악 벽과 빗물통이 내다보이는 빈방이었고, 안에 난로 한 짝 없는 너덜너덜한 벽난로 자리 때문에 으스스했다. 그러나 사람들이 있어 흥겨워지니 그곳은 결국 가장 좋은 곳이었다. 베리가 거기서 아이들과 놀아 주었고, 또 아이들 못지않게 뒹굴며 노는 놀이를 즐기는 듯 보였으니까. 그러다 핍친 부인이 되살아난 콕 레인 유령처럼 화가 나서 벽을 두드리자, 아이들은 놀이를 멈추었고, 베리는 땅거미가 질 때까지 속삭이는 목소리로 이야기를 들려주었다.\n* 콕 레인 유령(Cock Lane Ghost): 18세기 런던에서 화제가 된 유명한 유령 소동.\n\n차로는 물 탄 우유와, 버터 바른 빵이 넉넉히 나왔고, 핍친 부인과 베리를 위한 자그마한 검은 찻주전자와, 핍친 부인을 위한 무제한의 버터 바른 토스트가 있었는데, 그 토스트는 갈비처럼 뜨겁고 뜨겁게 들어왔다. 핍친 부인은 이 음식으로 겉은 기름투성이가 되었지만, 그것이 그녀의 속을 조금도 매끄럽게 해 주지는 못하는 듯했다. 그녀는 여전히 사나웠고, 그 단단한 잿빛 눈은 누그러질 줄을 몰랐다.\n\n차를 마신 뒤, 베리는 뚜껑에 왕실 별궁이 그려진 자그마한 반짇고리를 꺼내 부지런히 바느질에 매달렸다. 한편 핍친 부인은 안경을 쓰고 초록색 베이즈 천으로 장정된 큰 책을 펼쳐 들고는 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작했다. 그리고 핍친 부인은 자기가 난롯불 쪽으로 앞으로 고꾸라지려는 것을 알아채고 깨어날 때마다, 비더스톤 도련님도 꾸벅거린다는 이유로 그의 코를 손가락으로 튕겼다.\n\n마침내 아이들이 잘 시간이 되었고, 기도를 마친 뒤 다들 잠자리에 들었다. 어린 팽키 양은 어둠 속에서 혼자 자는 것을 무서워했으므로, 핍친 부인은 늘 양 몰듯이 그녀를 직접 위층으로 몰아넣는 것을 원칙으로 삼았다. 그리고 그 뒤로 한참 동안 가장 살기 나쁜 방에서 팽키 양이 끙끙대는 소리와, 핍친 부인이 이따금 들어가 그녀를 흔들어 대는 소리를 듣자니 흥겨웠다. 아홉 시 반쯤이 되자, 따뜻한 스위트브레드(핍친 부인의 체질은 스위트브레드 없이는 잠들지 않았다)의 냄새가, 집안에 감도는 그 향기—위컴 부인이 \"집 짓는 냄새\"라고 한—에 변주를 더했다. 그러고 얼마 지나지 않아 성채에는 잠이 내려앉았다.\n* 스위트브레드(sweet-bread): 송아지나 양의 흉선이나 췌장으로 만든 요리.\n\n이튿날 아침 식사는 지난밤의 차와 비슷했다. 다만 핍친 부인이 토스트 대신 둥근 빵을 먹었고, 식사가 끝나자 좀 더 성을 내는 듯했다는 점만 달랐다. 비더스톤 도련님이 나머지 아이들에게 「창세기」에서 뽑은 족보를(핍친 부인이 분별 있게 골라 준 것이었다) 소리 내어 읽었는데, 마치 발판 디딤틀을 굴러 올라가는 사람처럼 쉽고도 또렷하게 이름들을 척척 넘겼다. 그것이 끝나자 팽키 양은 마사지를 받으러 끌려갔고, 비더스톤 도련님은 소금물로 무언가 다른 처치를 받으러 끌려갔는데, 거기서 돌아올 때면 그는 늘 시퍼렇고 풀이 죽어 있었다. 그동안 폴과 플로렌스는 위컴과 함께 바닷가로 나갔는데—위컴은 줄곧 눈물바람이었다—정오쯤 되면 핍친 부인이 몇몇 조기 독서 시간을 주재했다. 어린아이의 마음을 어린 꽃처럼 펼치고 자라나게 북돋우는 것이 아니라, 굴 까듯이 억지로 비틀어 여는 것이 핍친 부인의 방침이었던 까닭에, 이 수업들의 교훈은 으레 난폭하고 충격적인 성격을 띠었다. 주인공—못된 사내아이—은, 아무리 온건한 결말이라도, 적어도 사자나 곰에게 끝장나는 것이 보통이었다.\n\n핍친 부인의 집에서의 생활이란 이런 것이었다. 토요일이면 돔비 씨가 내려왔다. 그러면 플로렌스와 폴은 그의 호텔로 가서 차를 마셨다. 둘은 일요일 하루를 온전히 그와 함께 보냈고, 대개는 저녁 식사 전에 마차를 타고 나가곤 했다. 그런 때 돔비 씨는 폴스타프를 습격한 자들처럼 점점 불어나서, 거친 삼베옷을 입은 한 사람이 아니라 열두 사람쯤 되는 듯했다. 일요일 저녁은 한 주 가운데 가장 우울한 저녁이었다. 핍친 부인이 일요일 밤이면 늘 유달리 심술궂게 구는 것을 원칙으로 삼았기 때문이다. 팽키 양은 보통 로팅딘의 고모 집에서 깊은 시름에 잠긴 채 돌아왔고, 친척이 모두 인도에 있는 비더스톤 도련님은, 예배와 예배 사이에 손발 하나 까딱 않고 머리를 거실 벽에 댄 채 꼿꼿한 자세로 앉아 있어야 했는데, 그의 어린 마음이 어찌나 사무치게 괴로웠던지, 한번은 일요일 밤에 플로렌스에게 벵골로 돌아가는 길을 조금이라도 일러 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물은 적도 있었다.\n* 폴스타프를 습격한 자들: 셰익스피어 「헨리 4세」에서 폴스타프가 강도 둘에게 습격당했다고 허풍을 떨면서, 이야기할 때마다 적의 수가 점점 불어났던 대목을 가리킨다.\n\n그러나 사람들은 으레, 핍친 부인이 아이들을 다루는 데 체계가 잡힌 여자라고 말했다. 의심할 바 없이 그랬다. 분명 그 거친 아이들도 몇 달 동안 그녀의 따뜻한 지붕 아래 머물고 나면 충분히 길든 채로 집에 돌아갔다. 또 사람들은 으레, 핍친 씨가 페루 광산에서 가슴이 찢어져 죽었을 때, 핍친 부인이 이렇게 사는 방식에 자신을 바치고, 그토록 자기 감정을 희생하며, 그토록 자기 고난에 맞서 의연히 버텨 온 것은 참으로 칭찬할 만한 일이라고 말했다.\n\n이 본보기 같은 노부인을, 폴은 난롯가의 자기 자그마한 안락의자에 앉아 한없이 빤히 바라보곤 했다. 그는 핍친 부인을 뚫어지게 바라볼 때면 피로라는 것을 도무지 모르는 듯했다. 그는 그녀를 좋아하지도 않았고, 그녀를 무서워하지도 않았다. 그러나 그 늙디늙은 기분에 잠겨 있을 때면, 그녀가 그에게 어딘가 기괴한 끌림을 지닌 듯했다. 그는 거기 앉아, 그녀를 바라보고, 두 손을 녹이고, 또 그녀를 바라보았는데, 그러다 보면 식인귀 같은 핍친 부인조차 이따금 적잖이 당황할 지경이었다. 한번은 둘만 있을 때 그녀가 그에게 무슨 생각을 하고 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아주머니요,\" 폴이 조금도 거리낌 없이 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 나에 대해 무슨 생각을 하는데?\" 핍친 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"아주머니가 도대체 몇 살일까 생각하고 있어요,\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n\"그런 말은 하면 못쓴단다, 도련님,\" 그 노부인이 대꾸했다. \"그러면 절대 안 돼.\"\n\n\"왜요?\" 폴이 물었다.\n\n\"무례한 거니까,\" 핍친 부인이 톡 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"무례한 거예요?\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"양고기 갈비랑 토스트를 다 먹어 치우는 게 무례한 거래요,\" 폴이 천진하게 말했다, \"위컴이 그러던데요.\"\n\n\"위컴은,\" 핍친 부인이 얼굴을 붉히며 받아쳤다, \"사악하고, 뻔뻔하고, 낯짝 두꺼운 계집이야.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐예요?\" 폴이 물었다.\n\n\"도련님은 알 거 없어요,\" 핍친 부인이 받아쳤다. \"질문을 했다고 미친 황소한테 뿔에 받혀 죽은 어린 사내아이 이야기를 기억하렴.\"\n\n\"황소가 미쳤다면,\" 폴이 말했다, \"그 사내아이가 질문을 했다는 걸 어떻게 알았어요? 미친 황소한테 가서 비밀을 속삭여 줄 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없잖아요. 저는 그 이야기 안 믿어요.\"\n\n\"그걸 안 믿는다고, 도련님?\" 핍친 부인이 어이없어하며 되받았다.\n\n\"네,\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n\"그게 혹시 길든 황소였더라도 안 믿겠다는 거냐, 이 꼬마 이교도야?\" 핍친 부인이 말했다.\n\n폴은 그 문제를 그런 각도에서 생각해 본 적이 없었고, 자기 결론을 황소가 미쳤다는 그 주장에 근거해 세웠던 터라, 지금으로선 잠자코 물러나 주기로 했다. 그러나 그는 머잖아 핍친 부인을 꼼짝 못 하게 만들 작정이 너무나 뻔히 드러나는 태도로, 그 일을 마음속에서 곱씹으며 앉아 있었기에, 그 강단 있는 노부인조차 그가 그 주제를 잊을 때까지 일단 물러나 있는 편이 현명하겠다고 여겼다.\n\n그때부터 핍친 부인은, 폴이 그녀에게 그러하듯, 폴에게 무언가 그 비슷한 묘한 종류의 끌림을 느끼는 듯했다. 그녀는 그에게, 난로 맞은편에 앉는 대신 의자를 자기 쪽으로 옮기게 했다. 그러면 그는 핍친 부인과 난로 울 사이의 한구석에 자리 잡고서, 자기 작은 얼굴의 빛을 죄다 그 검은 봄버진 옷자락에 빨아들인 채, 그녀의 얼굴 구석구석의 선과 주름을 살피고, 그 단단한 잿빛 눈을 들여다보곤 했으니, 핍친 부인은 이따금 조는 척하며 눈을 감을 수밖에 없을 지경이었다. 핍친 부인에게는 늙은 검은 고양이가 한 마리 있었는데, 보통은 난로 울의 한가운데 발에 똬리를 틀고 누워, 자기밖에 모르는 듯 가르랑거리며 불을 향해 눈을 깜박이곤 했다. 그러다 보면 그 오므라든 눈동자가 마치 느낌표 두 개 같았다. 그렇게 셋이 다 함께 난롯가에 앉아 있노라면, 그 착한 노부인은—무례하게 적어 두려는 것은 아니지만—마녀였을지도 모르고, 폴과 고양이는 그녀의 두 부리는 마귀였을지도 몰랐다. 만일 어느 날 밤 거센 바람이 불 때 셋이 다 함께 굴뚝으로 솟구쳐 올라가 다시는 소식이 끊겼다 한들, 그 일행의 모습과 더없이 잘 어울렸으리라.\n\n그러나 그런 일은 끝내 일어나지 않았다. 고양이와, 폴과, 핍친 부인은, 날이 어두워지면 늘 그들의 평소 자리에서 변함없이 찾아볼 수 있었다. 그리고 폴은 비더스톤 도련님과 어울리기를 마다하고, 밤마다 핍친 부인과, 고양이와, 난롯불을, 마치 그것들이 세 권으로 된 마법서라도 되는 양 줄곧 연구해 나갔다.\n\n위컴 부인은 폴의 별난 행동을 자기 나름으로 해석했다. 그리고 자기가 늘 앉아 있곤 하던 방에서 보이는 어지러운 굴뚝들의 광경과, 바람 소리와, 지금 자기 생활의 전반적인 따분함(위컴 부인의 힘준 표현으로는 '소름 끼침'이었다)으로 우울한 기분이 더욱 굳어진 그녀는, 앞서 말한 전제들로부터 더없이 음울한 사색을 끌어냈다. 자기네 \"어린 계집\"이—이것이 여자 하인을 가리키는 핍친 부인의 통칭이었다—위컴 부인과 통하는 것을 막는 것이 핍친 부인의 방침 가운데 하나였다. 그 목적을 위해 그녀는 자기 시간의 많은 부분을, 문 뒤에 숨어 있다가 그 가엾은 처녀가 위컴 부인의 방 쪽으로 다가갈 때마다 튀어나오는 데에 바쳤다. 그러나 베리는, 아침부터 밤까지 쉴 새 없이 매달려 일하는 그 잡다한 임무를 다하는 것과 어긋나지 않는 한, 그 방면에서 나눌 수 있는 만큼의 이야기는 자유로이 나눌 수 있었다. 그리하여 위컴 부인은 베리에게 자기 속내를 털어놓곤 했다.\n\n\"잠들었을 땐 정말 예쁜 아이네요!\" 어느 날 밤 위컴 부인의 야식을 들고 올라간 베리가, 침대에 누운 폴을 보려고 걸음을 멈추며 말했다.\n\n\"아!\" 위컴 부인이 한숨지었다. \"그래야지요.\"\n\n\"아니, 깨어 있어도 못생기지 않았는걸요,\" 베리가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요, 아가씨. 오, 그럼요. 우리 외삼촌네 베치 제인도 안 그랬어요,\" 위컴 부인이 말했다.\n\n베리는 폴 돔비와 위컴 부인의 외삼촌네 베치 제인 사이에 무슨 생각의 연결 고리가 있는지 짚어 보고 싶다는 표정이었다.\n\n\"우리 외삼촌네 안사람이,\" 위컴 부인이 말을 이었다, \"그 애 엄마처럼 꼭 그렇게 죽었어요. 외삼촌네 아이도 폴 도련님처럼 꼭 그렇게 시름시름했지요.\"\n\n\"시름시름했다니! 설마 저 애가 엄마를 그리워한다는 건 아니죠?\" 베리가 침대 가에 앉으며 따지듯 말했다. \"엄마에 대해선 아무것도 기억 못 할 거 아니에요, 위컴 부인. 그럴 리가 없어요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 아가씨,\" 위컴 부인이 말했다. \"우리 외삼촌네 아이도 그랬어요. 그래도 외삼촌네 아이는 가끔 아주 이상한 말을 했고, 아주 이상하게 굴었고, 아주 이상하게 행동했고, 전체적으로 아주 이상했지요. 외삼촌네 아이는 가끔 사람들 피를 얼어붙게 했답니다, 정말이지요!\"\n\n\"어떻게요?\" 베리가 물었다.\n\n\"저라면 베치 제인이랑 밤새 단둘이 깨어 앉아 있지는 않았을 거예요!\" 위컴 부인이 말했다, \"다음 날 아침에 위컴을 제 사업이라도 차려 준다 해도요. 저는 그렇게는 못 했을 거예요, 베리 아가씨.\"\n\n베리 양은 당연히 왜 못 하느냐고 물었다. 그러나 위컴 부인은, 그녀 같은 처지의 일부 여인네들의 버릇대로, 아무런 거리낌도 없이 자기 나름의 이야기 가지를 좇아 나갔다.\n\n\"베치 제인은,\" 위컴 부인이 말했다, \"제가 보고 싶다 할 만큼 사랑스러운 아이였어요. 그보다 더 사랑스러운 아이는 보고 싶어도 못 봤을 거예요. 아이가 앓을 수 있는 병이란 병은 베치 제인이 다 거쳐 갔지요. 그 애한테는 경련이,\" 위컴 부인이 말했다, \"아가씨한테 뾰루지가 흔한 것만큼이나 흔했답니다, 베리 아가씨.\" 베리 양은 자기도 모르게 코를 찡그렸다.\n\n\"그런데 베치 제인은,\" 위컴 부인이 목소리를 낮추고, 방 안을 둘러보고, 침대에 누운 폴 쪽을 바라보며 말했다, \"요람에 있을 때, 돌아가신 제 어머니가 그 애를 돌봤어요. 어떻게 그랬는지도 모르겠고, 언제 그랬는지도 모르겠고, 그 귀여운 아이가 그걸 알았는지 어쨌는지도 모르겠지만, 베치 제인은 제 어머니의 보살핌을 받았답니다, 베리 아가씨!\" 그러고 위컴 부인은, 새하얗게 질린 얼굴과 눈물 어린 눈과 떨리는 목소리로, 다시 한번 겁먹은 듯 방 안을 둘러보고, 침대에 누운 폴 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n\"말도 안 돼요!\" 베리 양이—그 생각에 다소 발끈하여—외쳤다.\n\n\"말도 안 된다고 하셔도 좋아요! 저는 기분 안 상해요, 아가씨. 양심으로 그게 말도 안 된다고 생각하실 수 있으면 좋겠네요. 그러면 이런 데서—이렇게 자유롭게 말하는 걸 용서하세요—이런 무덤 같은 데서 마음이 한결 나으실 거예요. 저는 여기서 진이 다 빠지고 있답니다. 폴 도련님이 자면서 좀 뒤척이네요. 등 좀 토닥여 주세요, 부탁이에요.\"\n\n\"그럼 부인 생각엔,\" 베리가 시키는 대로 살며시 토닥이며 말했다, \"이 애도 제 어머니의 보살핌을 받았다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"베치 제인은,\" 위컴 부인이 더없이 엄숙한 어조로 대꾸했다, \"이 아이가 시달려 온 것처럼 시달렸고, 이 아이가 변해 온 것처럼 변했어요. 저는 그 애가, 이 애처럼, 생각하고, 생각하고, 또 생각하며 앉아 있는 걸 몇 번이고 몇 번이고 봤어요. 그 애가, 이 애처럼, 늙고, 늙고, 또 늙어 보이는 걸 몇 번이고 몇 번이고 봤어요. 그 애가, 이 애랑 꼭 같이 말하는 걸 여러 번 들었어요. 저는 이 아이랑 베치 제인이 완전히 똑같은 처지라고 봐요, 베리 아가씨.\"\n\n\"부인 외삼촌네 아이는 살아 있어요?\" 베리가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아가씨, 살아 있어요,\" 위컴 부인이 의기양양한 기색으로 대꾸했다. 베리 양이 그 반대를 예상했던 것이 분명했기 때문이다. \"그리고 은세공장이한테 시집갔지요. 오, 그럼요, 아가씨, 그 애는 살아 있어요,\" 위컴 부인이 주어에 힘을 잔뜩 실어 말했다.\n\n누군가가 죽었다는 것이 분명해지자, 핍친 부인의 조카딸은 그게 누구냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아가씨를 불안하게 만들고 싶진 않아요,\" 위컴 부인이 야식을 들며 대꾸했다. \"묻지 마세요.\"\n\n이것이야말로 다시 묻게 만드는 가장 확실한 방법이었다. 그래서 베리 양은 자기 질문을 되풀이했고, 위컴 부인은 얼마간 버티고 망설인 끝에 칼을 내려놓고는, 다시 방 안과 침대에 누운 폴을 흘끗 보며 대답했다.\n\n\"그 애는 사람들한테 정을 붙였어요. 그중엔 변덕스러운 정도 있었고, 또 어떤 건 으레 볼 법한 그런 애정이었지요—다만 보통보다 더 강했을 뿐. 그런데 그 사람들이 다 죽었어요.\"\n\n이것이 핍친 부인의 조카딸에게는 너무도 뜻밖이고 너무도 섬뜩했던지, 그녀는 침대틀의 딱딱한 모서리에 꼿꼿이 앉아, 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며, 노골적인 경악의 눈빛으로 자기에게 이야기를 들려주는 그 여자를 바라보았다.\n\n위컴 부인은 왼쪽 집게손가락을 플로렌스가 누워 있는 침대 쪽으로 슬그머니 흔들어 보였다. 그러더니 그것을 거꾸로 뒤집어, 바닥을 향해 힘주어 여러 번 가리켰다. 그 바로 아래가, 핍친 부인이 으레 토스트를 먹어 치우는 그 거실이었다.\n\n\"제 말 명심하세요, 베리 아가씨,\" 위컴 부인이 말했다, \"그리고 폴 도련님이 아가씨를 너무 좋아하지 않는 걸 고맙게 여기세요. 저는, 그 애가 저를 너무 좋아하지 않는 게 고마워요, 정말이에요. 하긴 이런 데서—이렇게 자유롭게 말하는 걸 용서하세요—이런 감옥 같은 집에서 살아갈 낙이라곤 별로 없지만요!\"\n\n베리 양의 동요가 그녀로 하여금 폴의 등을 너무 세게 토닥이게 했을 수도 있고, 아니면 그 마음을 달래 주던 단조로운 손길을 멈추게 했을 수도 있었다. 그러나 바로 그때 폴이 잠자리에서 몸을 뒤척이더니, 곧 잠에서 깨어 침대에 일어나 앉았다. 그의 머리카락은 무슨 어린아이다운 꿈의 여파로 뜨겁고 축축했고, 그는 플로렌스를 찾았다.\n\n플로렌스는 그의 목소리가 들리자마자 자기 침대에서 일어나, 곧장 그의 베개 위로 몸을 굽히고는, 노래를 불러 그를 다시 잠들게 했다. 위컴 부인은 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 눈물을 몇 방울 떨구며, 그 자그마한 무리를 베리에게 가리켜 보이고는, 눈을 천장으로 치떴다.\n\n\"이제 잠들었어요, 아가씨,\" 위컴 부인이 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했다, \"아가씨도 다시 잠자리에 드시는 게 좋겠어요. 춥지 않으세요?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 유모,\" 플로렌스가 웃으며 말했다. \"전혀요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 위컴 부인이 한숨지었다. 그리고 다시 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 지켜보는 베리에게 이렇게 말하는 듯했다, \"머잖아 우리 가운데 누군가는 충분히 차가워질 거예요!\"\n\n베리는 위컴 부인이 이제 다 비운 그 검소한 야식 쟁반을 집어 들고, 그녀에게 잘 자라고 인사했다.\n\n\"안녕히 주무세요, 아가씨!\" 위컴이 나직이 대꾸했다. \"안녕히요! 아가씨 고모님은 연로한 분이시니까, 베리 아가씨, 그건 아가씨도 진작부터 각오하고 계셨을 일이잖아요.\"\n\n이 위로의 작별 인사에 위컴 부인은 진심에서 우러난 비통한 표정을 곁들였다. 그리고 다시 두 아이와 단둘이 남아, 바람이 구슬프게 불고 있다는 것을 깨닫자, 그녀는 우울—그 가장 값싸고 가장 손쉬운 사치—에 잠겨 들었고, 마침내 잠에 압도되고 말았다.\n\n핍친 부인의 조카딸은 아래층으로 내려가면서 그 본보기 같은 용이 난롯가 깔개 위에 엎어져 있으리라고 기대하지는 않았으나, 그녀가 유난히 까다롭고 모질며, 또 아는 모든 이에게 위안이 되고자 오래오래 살아갈 작정이라는 기색이 지금 온통 드러나 있는 것을 보고는 안도했다. 또 그녀에게는 이어지는 한 주 동안에도 쇠약해질 기미라곤 전혀 없었으니, 그 체질을 위한 음식들은 여전히 차례차례 규칙적으로 사라져 갔고, 한편 폴은 여느 때처럼 열심히 그녀를 연구하며, 그 검은 치맛자락과 난로 울 사이의 평소 자리를 변함없는 한결같음으로 차지하고 있었다.\n\n그러나 그 무렵이 다 지나도록 폴 자신은, 얼굴빛은 훨씬 건강해 보였으나, 처음 도착했을 때보다 조금도 더 튼튼해지지 않았으므로, 그를 위해 자그마한 손수레가 마련되었다. 그 안에서 그는 알파벳판이며 그 밖의 기초 참고서들을 곁에 두고 편안히 누워, 바닷가로 끌려 내려갈 수 있었다. 자기의 별난 취향에 한결같던 아이는, 이 수레를 끌 사람으로 제안된 혈색 좋은 사내아이는 마다하고, 그 대신 그 아이의 할아버지를 골랐다. 쪼글쪼글하고 늙은, 게처럼 생긴 사내였는데, 너덜너덜한 기름먹인 옷을 입고 있었으며, 짠물에 오래 절여진 탓에 질기고 힘줄이 도드라졌고, 썰물 때 해초가 깔린 바닷가 같은 냄새가 났다.\n\n이 별난 시중꾼이 그를 끌고, 플로렌스는 늘 그의 곁에서 걷고, 풀이 죽은 위컴은 맨 뒤를 따르는 가운데, 그는 날마다 바다의 가장자리로 내려갔다. 그리고 그곳에서 그는 몇 시간씩 한꺼번에 자기 수레에 앉거나 누워 있곤 했다. 다른 아이들이 함께 있을 때만큼 그를 괴롭게 하는 것은 없었다—언제나 플로렌스만은 예외였지만.\n\n\"제발 저리 가 줘,\" 그는 자기와 어울리려고 다가오는 아이에게라면 누구에게든 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"고맙지만, 너는 필요 없어.\"\n\n이따금 그의 귓전에서 어떤 작은 목소리가, 그에게 안녕하냐고 물었을지도 모른다.\n\n\"난 아주 잘 있어, 고마워,\" 그는 이렇게 대답하곤 했다. \"그래도 제발 가서 노는 게 좋을 거야.\"\n\n그러고는 고개를 돌려, 그 아이가 멀어지는 것을 지켜보다가, 플로렌스에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"우리는 다른 애들은 필요 없지, 그렇지? 뽀뽀해 줘, 플로이.\"\n\n그런 때면 그는 위컴이 함께 있는 것조차 싫어해서, 그녀가 으레 그러듯 조개껍데기며 아는 사람을 주우러 어슬렁어슬렁 가 버리면 무척 좋아했다. 그가 가장 좋아하는 자리는 한적하기 그지없는 곳으로, 거니는 사람들 대부분에게서 멀리 떨어져 있었다. 그리고 플로렌스가 곁에 앉아 바느질을 하거나, 그에게 책을 읽어 주거나, 그에게 말을 걸어 주고, 바람이 그의 얼굴에 불어오고, 물이 그의 침대 바퀴 사이로 밀려들어 오면, 그는 더 바랄 것이 없었다.\n\n\"플로이,\" 어느 날 그가 말했다, \"그 애 친척들이 사는 인도는 어디 있어?\"\n\n\"오, 아주아주 먼 데 있어,\" 플로렌스가 바느질하던 데서 눈을 들며 말했다.\n\n\"몇 주나 걸리는 데?\" 폴이 물었다.\n\n\"응, 얘. 밤낮으로 몇 주나 걸리는 여정이야.\"\n\n\"플로이, 네가 인도에 있으면,\" 폴이 잠깐 잠잠히 있다가 말했다, \"나는—엄마가 한 게 뭐였지? 잊어버렸어.\"\n\n\"날 사랑하셨지!\" 플로렌스가 대답했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니. 나도 지금 너를 사랑하잖아, 플로이? 그게 뭐였지?—돌아가신 거. 네가 인도에 있으면, 나는 죽을 거야, 플로이.\"\n\n플로렌스는 황급히 바느질감을 옆으로 치우고, 그의 베개 위에 머리를 누이며 그를 어루만졌다. 그리고 자기도, 만일 그가 거기 있다면 그럴 거라고 말했다. 그는 곧 나아질 거라고.\n\n\"오! 난 지금도 훨씬 나은걸!\" 그가 대답했다. \"그런 뜻이 아니야. 나는 너무 슬프고 너무 외로워서 죽을 거라는 거야, 플로이!\"\n\n또 한번은, 같은 자리에서, 그가 잠이 들어 오랫동안 고요히 잤다. 그러다 별안간 잠에서 깨어, 귀를 기울이고, 벌떡 일어나, 앉아서 귀를 기울였다.\n\n플로렌스는 그에게 무슨 소리가 들리는 것 같으냐고 물었다.\n\n\"그게 뭐라고 하는지 알고 싶어,\" 그가 그녀의 얼굴을 가만히 들여다보며 대답했다. \"바다 말이야, 플로이, 저게 자꾸 뭐라고 하는 거지?\"\n\n플로렌스는 그것이 그저 굽이치는 파도 소리일 뿐이라고 일러 주었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래,\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 나는 저 파도가 늘 무언가를 말하고 있다는 걸 알아. 늘 똑같은 걸 말이야. 저기 저 너머엔 어떤 데가 있어?\" 그는 일어나서, 수평선을 간절히 바라보았다.\n\n플로렌스는 맞은편에 또 다른 나라가 있다고 일러 주었으나, 그는 그걸 말하는 게 아니라고 했다. 더 멀리—더 멀리 말이야!\n\n그 뒤로도 아주 자주, 둘이 이야기를 나누다가도, 그는 문득 말을 멈추고, 그 파도가 늘 무어라고 하는 것인지를 헤아려 보려 했다. 그리고 자기 침상에서 일어나, 저 멀리 보이지 않는 그 나라 쪽을 바라보곤 했다.\n\n# CHAPTER IX. In which the Wooden Midshipman gets into Trouble\n\n젊은 월터 게이의 천성에는 낭만에 대한 기질과 경이로운 것을 사랑하는 마음이 꽤 강하게 배어 있었는데, 그의 외삼촌인 노(老) 솔로몬 길스의 보살핌도 냉정하고 실질적인 경험이라는 물로 그것을 그리 크게 약화시키지는 못했다. 바로 그 기질 때문에 그는 플로렌스가 착한 브라운 부인과 겪은 그 모험에 남다르고 즐거운 흥미를 붙이게 되었다. 그는 그 일을 기억 속에서 애지중지 키우고 보듬었는데, 특히 자기가 관여했던 부분이 그러했다. 마침내 그것은 그의 공상이 떠받드는 응석받이 아이가 되어, 제멋대로 굴며 마음껏 그 공상을 가지고 놀았다.\n\n그 사건들과 거기서 자신이 맡았던 역할에 대한 회상은, 어쩌면 일요일마다 노(老) 솔과 커틀 선장이 함께 펼치는 주간(週間) 공상 덕분에 더욱 매혹적인 것이 되었는지도 모른다. 그 훌륭한 단짝들 가운데 어느 한 사람이 리처드 휘팅턴을 신비롭게 언급하지 않고 지나가는 일요일은 거의 없었다. 그중 후자인 신사는 한술 더 떠, 상업로(路)의 어느 허물어진 담벼락에 주로 바다와 관련된 정서를 담은 수많은 다른 노래들 사이에서 오랫동안 펄럭이던, 상당히 오래된 노래 한 편을 사들이기까지 했다. 그 시적 작품은 유망한 젊은 석탄 하역부가 어느 \"사랑스러운 페그\", 곧 뉴캐슬 석탄선의 선장 겸 공동 선주의 재주 많은 딸과 벌인 구애와 혼인을 노래한 것이었다. 이 가슴 설레는 전설 속에서 커틀 선장은 월터와 플로렌스의 경우에 들어맞는 심오한 형이상학적 의미를 발견했다. 그것이 어찌나 그를 들뜨게 했던지, 생일이나 그 밖의 몇몇 비(非)주일 휴일 같은 아주 떠들썩한 날이면, 그는 작은 뒷방에서 그 노래 전체를 우렁차게 불러 젖혔고, 작품의 여주인공에 대한 경의로, 매 절(節)이 끝맺는 페에에에그라는 말에서 놀라운 떨림을 넣곤 했다.\n\n그러나 솔직하고 자유로운 정신을 지닌, 마음이 활짝 열린 소년이란, 제 감정이 아무리 자기를 강하게 붙잡고 있다 한들, 그 감정의 본질을 분석하는 데는 그리 능하지 않은 법이다. 월터로서도 이 점을 판단하기는 어려웠으리라. 그는 플로렌스를 만났던 그 부두에 큰 애정을 품었고, (그 거리들 자체가 매혹적이지는 않았으나) 그들이 집으로 돌아올 때 지나온 거리들에도 애정을 품었다. 그 길에서 그토록 자주 벗겨졌던 신발을 그는 자기 방에 간직해 두었다. 그리고 저녁이면 작은 뒷방에 앉아, 착한 브라운 부인의 상상 초상화를 화랑이라도 차릴 만큼 잔뜩 그려 댔다. 어쩌면 그 잊지 못할 사건 이후로 그는 옷차림에 조금 더 멋을 부리게 되었는지도 모른다. 또 그가 한가한 시간이면 돔비 씨의 저택이 자리한 동네 쪽으로 걷기를, 거리에서 어린 플로렌스를 스칠지도 모른다는 막연한 기대로 즐겨 했던 것도 분명하다. 그러나 이 모든 것의 정서는 더할 나위 없이 소년답고 순수했다. 플로렌스는 무척 예뻤고, 예쁜 얼굴을 흠모하는 것은 즐거운 일이었다. 플로렌스는 무방비하고 연약했으며, 자기가 그녀에게 얼마간 보호와 도움을 줄 수 있었다는 것은 자랑스러운 생각이었다. 플로렌스는 세상에서 가장 고마워할 줄 아는 작은 존재였고, 그 환한 감사가 얼굴에 빛나는 모습을 보는 것은 기쁜 일이었다. 플로렌스는 방치되고 냉대받는 처지였으며, 그 음울하고 위엄 서린 집안에서 푸대접받는 아이를 향해 그의 가슴은 젊은이다운 관심으로 가득했다.\n\n그리하여 한 해에 어쩌면 대여섯 번쯤, 월터는 거리에서 플로렌스에게 모자를 벗어 인사했고, 플로렌스는 멈춰 서서 악수를 나누곤 했다. 위컴 부인은(그의 이름을 제 식대로 바꾸어 한결같이 \"청년 그레이브스\"라 불렀는데) 두 사람이 알게 된 사연을 알고 있던 터라 이런 일에 어찌나 익숙했던지, 전혀 개의치 않았다. 반면에 니퍼 양은 오히려 그런 기회를 노렸으니, 그녀의 예민한 젊은 가슴이 월터의 잘생긴 외모에 은근히 호감을 품고서, 제 감정이 화답받고 있다는 믿음 쪽으로 기울었기 때문이다.\n\n이런 식으로, 월터는 플로렌스와의 친분을 잊거나 시야에서 놓치기는커녕, 오히려 점점 더 또렷이 기억할 따름이었다. 그 모험적인 시작과, 거기에 독특한 성격과 운치를 더해 준 자잘한 정황들에 대해서는, 자기가 얽혀 있는 어떤 사실의 일부로서가 아니라, 자기 상상에 무척 잘 맞아 떨쳐 버릴 수 없는 즐거운 이야기로서 더 많이 헤아렸다. 그 정황들은 그의 공상 속에서 플로렌스를 한껏 돋보이게 했으나, 정작 자신은 그렇지 못했다. 때로 그는 (그러고 나면 무척 빠르게 걸었는데) 첫 만남 다음 날에 자기가 바다로 나가게 되었더라면, 그래서 정말로 떠나, 거기서 놀라운 일들을 해내고, 오랫동안 멀리 머물다가, 돌고래의 온갖 빛깔을 두른 제독으로, 적어도 견딜 수 없을 만큼 번쩍이는 견장을 단 전대장(全大將)으로 돌아와서, 돔비 씨의 이빨이며 크라바트며 시곗줄 따위에 아랑곳없이 (그때쯤 아리따운 처녀가 되어 있을) 플로렌스와 혼인하여, 어딘가의 푸른 해안으로 그녀를 의기양양하게 데려갔더라면 얼마나 멋진 일이었을까 하고 생각하곤 했다. 그러나 이런 공상의 비상이 돔비와 아들 회사 사무소의 놋쇠 명패를 황금빛 희망의 명판으로 광나게 닦아 주거나, 그 더러운 채광창에 찬란한 광채를 드리워 주는 일은 좀처럼 없었다. 그리고 선장과 솔 외삼촌이 리처드 휘팅턴이며 주인의 딸들 이야기를 늘어놓을 때면, 월터는 돔비와 아들 회사에서의 자기 진짜 처지를 그들보다 훨씬 잘 알고 있다고 느꼈다.\n\n그렇게 그는 날마다 해야 할 일을, 명랑하고 부지런하며 즐거운 마음으로 해 나갔다. 그리고 솔 외삼촌과 커틀 선장의 낙천적인 기질을 꿰뚫어 보면서도, 한편으로는 자기 나름의 막연하고 몽환적인 공상을 수도 없이 품었으니, 그것에 비하면 그들의 공상은 그저 일상적인 개연성에 지나지 않았다. 피프친 시절의 그의 형편이 이러했는데, 그 무렵 그는 예전보다 조금 나이 들어 보이긴 했으나 그리 크게는 아니었고, 솔 외삼촌과 가상의 하숙인들을 거느린 채 거실로 돌격해 들어가 마데이라 술을 가져오라고 외삼촌의 발치를 비춰 주던 그때와 똑같이, 발걸음 가볍고 마음 가볍고 머릿속 가벼운 청년이었다.\n\n\"솔 외삼촌,\" 월터가 말했다, \"어디 편찮으신 것 같아요. 아침도 통 안 드셨잖아요. 이러시면 제가 의사를 모셔 올 거예요.\"\n\n\"의사라도 내가 원하는 걸 줄 수는 없단다, 얘야,\" 솔 외삼촌이 말했다. \"줄 수 있다면야 용한 의사겠지—그렇대도 안 주려 들겠지만.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데요, 외삼촌? 손님이요?\"\n\n\"그래,\" 솔로몬이 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다. \"손님이라면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n\"제기랄, 외삼촌!\" 월터가 아침 찻잔을 달그락 내려놓고 손으로 탁자를 치며 말했다. \"하루 종일 사람들이 떼 지어 거리를 오르락내리락하고, 시시각각 수십 명씩 가게 앞을 지나가고 또 지나가는 걸 보고 있노라면, 저는 당장 밖으로 뛰쳐나가 아무나 멱살을 잡아 끌고 들어와서, 현금으로 오십 파운드어치 기구를 사게 만들고 싶은 충동이 반쯤은 든다니까요. 거기 문간에서 뭘 들여다보시는 거요?—\" 월터는 가루를 뿌린 머리를 한 노신사를 향해 (물론 그에게는 들리지 않게) 한마디 던지며 말을 이었는데, 그 노인은 온 힘을 다해 선박용 망원경을 뚫어져라 보고 있었다. \"그래 봤자 소용없소. 그런 거야 나도 할 수 있지. 들어와서 사시지 그래요!\"\n\n그러나 노신사는 호기심을 다 채우고는 태연히 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n\"저것 봐요!\" 월터가 말했다. \"다들 저 모양이라니까요. 하지만 외삼촌—이봐요, 솔 외삼촌\"—노인이 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 채 그의 첫 호소에 응답하지 않았던 것이다. \"낙심하지 마세요. 기운 빠지지 마세요, 외삼촌. 주문이 들어오기 시작하면, 어찌나 한꺼번에 몰려들지 다 처리하지도 못하실걸요.\"\n\n\"주문이 들어올 때쯤이면 나는 그걸 처리할 처지가 못 될 게다, 얘야,\" 솔로몬 길스가 대답했다. \"내가 이 가게에서 나가기 전엔 주문이 다시는 안 들어올 테니.\"\n\n\"이봐요, 외삼촌! 정말 그러시면 안 돼요, 아시잖아요!\" 월터가 다그쳤다. \"그러지 마세요!\"\n\n노 솔은 애써 명랑한 표정을 지어 보이려 하면서, 작은 탁자 너머로 그에게 최대한 다정하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"평소보다 더한 무슨 일이 있는 건 아니죠, 외삼촌?\" 월터가 찻쟁반에 팔꿈치를 괴고 몸을 기울여, 한층 은밀하고 다정하게 말했다. \"혹시 그렇다면 저한테 솔직히 털어놓으세요, 외삼촌, 무슨 일인지 다 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니다,\" 노 솔이 대답했다. \"평소보다 더한 일? 아니, 아니야. 평소보다 더한 무슨 일이 있겠니?\"\n\n월터는 못 믿겠다는 듯 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"그게 바로 제가 알고 싶은 거예요,\" 그가 말했다, \"그런데 도리어 저한테 물으시다니요! 제가 말씀드릴게요, 외삼촌, 외삼촌이 이러시는 걸 보면, 외삼촌이랑 같이 사는 게 정말 미안해져요.\"\n\n노 솔은 자기도 모르게 눈을 휘둥그레 떴다.\n\n\"네. 외삼촌이랑 함께 지내는 것보다 더 행복했던 사람은 없을 만큼, 저는 늘 행복했지만요, 외삼촌이 마음에 뭔가 담아 두고 있는 걸 보면, 외삼촌이랑 같이 사는 게 정말 미안해져요.\"\n\n\"그럴 때면 내가 좀 시무룩해지는 거 나도 안다,\" 솔로몬이 온순하게 두 손을 비비며 말했다.\n\n\"제 말씀은요, 솔 외삼촌,\" 월터가 조금 더 몸을 기울여 그의 어깨를 토닥이며 이어 갔다, \"그럴 때면 저 대신 여기 앉아 차를 따라 줄, 동글동글하고 자그마한 아내가 외삼촌한테 있어야 마땅하다는 거예요—편안하고 더없이 좋고 아늑한 노부인 말이에요, 외삼촌한테 딱 어울리고, 외삼촌을 어떻게 다뤄야 할지 알고, 외삼촌 기운을 북돋아 줄 줄 아는 그런 분이요. 여기 저로 말하면, 어느 조카 못지않게 외삼촌을 사랑하긴 하지만요 (당연히 그래야죠!) 그래도 저는 그저 조카일 뿐이라, 외삼촌이 가라앉고 기분이 안 좋을 때, 그분이 오래전에 그래 주었을 만큼 좋은 동무가 되어 드리지를 못해요, 외삼촌 기운을 북돋아 드릴 수만 있다면 무슨 돈이라도 다 내놓을 텐데 말이에요. 그래서 저는, 외삼촌이 마음에 뭔가 담아 두고 있는 걸 볼 때면, 저처럼 어설프고 거칠고 미숙한 풋내기 소년보다 더 나은 누군가가 외삼촌 곁에 없는 게 정말 안타깝다는 거예요. 저는 외삼촌을 위로하려는 마음은 있어도, 그 방법을 모르거든요—방법을 몰라요,\" 월터는 외삼촌의 손을 잡으려고 더욱더 몸을 뻗으며 되풀이했다.\n\n\"월리, 사랑하는 얘야,\" 솔로몬이 말했다, \"그 아늑한 자그마한 노부인이 마흔다섯 해 전에 이 거실에 자리 잡았다 한들, 내가 지금 너를 아끼는 것만큼 그녀를 더 아낄 수는 없었을 게다.\"\n\n\"저도 알아요, 솔 외삼촌,\" 월터가 대답했다. \"정말이지 저도 알아요. 하지만 그분이 곁에 있었다면, 외삼촌이 불편한 비밀이라는 짐 전부를 혼자 짊어지지는 않으셨을 거예요, 그분은 그 짐을 덜어 드릴 줄 알았을 테니까요, 저는 모르지만요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야, 너도 안다,\" 기구 제조공이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 도대체 무슨 일이세요, 솔 외삼촌?\" 월터가 어르듯이 말했다. \"자요! 무슨 일이세요?\"\n\n솔로몬 길스는 아무 일도 없다고 우겼고, 어찌나 단호히 버티던지, 그의 조카로서는 믿는 시늉을 영 어설프게나마 해 보는 수밖에 도리가 없었다.\n\n\"제가 드릴 말씀은요, 솔 외삼촌, 만약 무슨 일이 있다면—\"\n\n\"하지만 아무 일도 없대도,\" 솔로몬이 말했다.\n\n\"좋아요,\" 월터가 말했다. \"그럼 더는 드릴 말씀이 없네요. 그게 다행이에요, 일하러 갈 시간이 됐으니까요. 나갔다가 이따 들러서, 어떻게 지내시는지 볼게요, 외삼촌. 그리고 명심하세요, 외삼촌! 만약 외삼촌이 저를 속이고 있었다는 걸 알아내면, 다시는 외삼촌 말을 안 믿을 거고, 카커 주니어 씨에 관한 얘기도 다시는 안 해 드릴 거예요!\"\n\n솔로몬 길스는 웃으며 그런 건 어디 알아낼 테면 알아내 보라고 받아쳤다. 그리고 월터는, 한밑천 잡아 그 나무 사관후보생을 떳떳한 독립의 처지에 올려놓을 온갖 비현실적인 방법들을 머릿속에서 굴리며, 평소보다 한층 무거운 얼굴로 돔비와 아들 회사 사무소로 향했다.\n\n그 시절 모퉁이를 돌면—비숍스게이트 스트리트 위다우트에—브로글리라는 자가 살았는데, 공인 중개인 겸 감정인이었다. 그는 가게를 하나 꾸렸는데, 거기에는 온갖 종류의 중고 가구가 더없이 거북한 모양새로, 그 본래 용도와는 가장 완전히 동떨어진 상황과 조합으로 진열되어 있었다. 의자 수십 개가 세면대에 걸려 있고, 그 세면대는 간신히 찬장 어깨 위에서 균형을 잡고 있으며, 그 찬장은 다시 식탁의 뒤집힌 쪽에 올라서 있고, 또 그 식탁은 다리를 위로 쳐든 채 다른 식탁 위에서 곡예를 부리고 있는 것이, 그 가게에서 가장 합리적인 배치 축에 들었다. 접시 덮개며 포도주잔이며 디캔터를 잔칫상처럼 늘어놓은 것은, 부지깽이 대여섯 개와 현관 등(燈) 같은 정겨운 손님들을 대접하느라, 흔히 네 기둥 침대의 한복판에 펼쳐져 있는 광경으로 볼 수 있었다. 딸린 창문도 없는 창문 커튼 한 벌이, 약방에서 가져온 작은 단지들을 잔뜩 인 서랍장 바리케이드를 우아하게 휘감고 있는가 하면, 한편으로는 천연의 짝인 난롯가에서 떨어져 나와 갈 곳 없어진 난로 깔개가, 그 역경 속에서 매서운 동풍에 맞서며, 하루에 현 하나씩 닳아 가다가 그 어지럽고 산만한 머릿속으로 거리의 소음에 희미하게 울려 대는 어느 캐비닛 피아노의 날카로운 푸념에 우울하게 장단을 맞추어 떨고 있었다. 손가락 하나 까딱 않고, 옛 주인들의 금전 사정만큼이나 도무지 제대로 태엽을 감을 수 없을 듯 보이는, 멈춰 선 시계들은 브로글리 씨의 가게에 늘 풍성하게 구비되어 있었다. 또 반사와 굴절의 복리(複利)로 우연히 마주 놓인 여러 거울들은, 보는 이의 눈에 파산과 몰락의 영원한 원근법을 펼쳐 보였다.\n\n브로글리 씨 본인은 눈이 촉촉하고 안색이 발그레하며 머리가 곱슬곱슬한 사람으로, 덩치가 큼직하고 성미가 느긋했다—남의 카르타고 폐허 위에 걸터앉는 부류의 카이우스 마리우스라면, 능히 기운을 잃지 않고 지낼 수 있는 법이니까. 그는 이따금 솔로몬의 가게에 들러 솔로몬의 업종에 속하는 물품에 관해 한두 가지 물어보곤 했다. 그래서 월터도 거리에서 그와 마주치면 인사를 건넬 만큼은 그를 알고 있었다. 그러나 그 중개인이 솔로몬 길스와 알고 지내는 정도 역시 그것이 전부였던지라, 월터가 약속대로 오전 중에 돌아왔을 때 브로글리 씨가 뒷방에 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 앉아 있고, 그의 모자가 문 뒤에 걸려 있는 것을 보고는 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n\"어, 솔 외삼촌!\" 월터가 말했다. 노인은 탁자 맞은편에 침울하게 앉아 있었는데, 웬일로 안경을 이마가 아니라 눈에 걸치고 있었다. \"이제 좀 어떠세요?\"\n\n솔로몬은 고개를 저으며, 한 손으로 중개인 쪽을 가리켜 그를 소개하듯 했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있는 거예요?\" 월터가 숨이 멎는 듯한 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 아니오. 아무 일도 없어요,\" 브로글리 씨가 말했다. \"이 일로 너무 마음 쓰지 마시오.\"\n\n월터는 중개인에게서 외삼촌에게로, 말없이 어리둥절한 눈길을 옮겼다.\n\n\"실은 말이오,\" 브로글리 씨가 말했다, \"증서 채무에 대한 소액 변제 건이 좀 있는데—삼백칠십몇 파운드가 연체되어서, 내가 점유하고 있는 거요.\"\n\n\"점유라니요!\" 월터가 가게를 휘둘러보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇소!\" 브로글리 씨가 은밀히 동의하듯 말하며, 우리 모두 다 같이 사이좋게 지내는 편이 좋겠다고 권하기라도 하는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다. \"차압이오. 그게 그거요. 너무 마음 쓰지 마시오. 조용하고 사근사근하게 처리하려고 내가 직접 온 거요. 나를 알잖소. 아주 사적인 일이오.\"\n\n\"솔 외삼촌!\" 월터가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"월리, 얘야,\" 외삼촌이 대답했다. \"이런 건 처음이란다. 이런 재앙은 내게 한 번도 닥친 적이 없었어. 시작하기엔 나는 늙은이로구나.\" 그는 (이제 감정을 숨기는 데 더는 쓸모가 없어진) 안경을 다시 이마로 밀어 올리고는, 한 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 소리 내어 흐느꼈고, 그의 눈물이 커피색 조끼 위로 떨어졌다.\n\n\"솔 외삼촌! 제발! 오, 그러지 마세요!\" 월터가 외쳤는데, 노인이 우는 것을 보고는 정말이지 섬뜩한 공포를 느꼈던 것이다. \"제발 그러지 마세요. 브로글리 씨, 저는 어떡하면 좋죠?\"\n\n\"친구분이라도 한두 분 찾아가서,\" 브로글리 씨가 말했다, \"의논해 보시길 권하겠소.\"\n\n\"맞아요!\" 월터가 지푸라기라도 잡듯 외쳤다. \"그래요! 고마워요. 커틀 선장님이 적임자예요, 외삼촌. 제가 커틀 선장님께 달려갔다 올 때까지 기다리세요. 제가 없는 동안 우리 외삼촌 좀 지켜봐 주시고, 최대한 편안하게 해 드릴 수 있죠, 브로글리 씨? 절망하지 마세요, 솔 외삼촌. 부디 마음 굳게 먹어 보세요, 우리 좋은 외삼촌!\"\n\n월터는 이 말을 무척 열렬하게 내뱉으며, 노인의 띄엄띄엄한 만류에도 아랑곳없이, 다시 있는 힘껏 가게에서 뛰쳐나갔다. 그리고 사무소로 부랴부랴 돌아가 외삼촌이 갑자기 편찮으시다는 핑계로 양해를 구한 다음, 전속력으로 커틀 선장의 거처를 향해 떠났다.\n\n거리를 달려가는 동안 모든 것이 달라 보였다. 손수레며 짐마차며 합승마차며 사륜마차며 보행자들이 평소처럼 뒤엉켜 시끄러웠지만, 그 나무 사관후보생에게 닥친 불운이 그 모든 것을 낯설고 새롭게 만들었다. 집과 가게들도 예전과 달라져, 정면에 브로글리 씨의 영장이 큼직한 글자로 붙어 있는 듯했다. 그 중개인은 교회들마저 손아귀에 넣은 것 같았으니, 교회 첨탑들이 어딘가 낯선 기운을 띤 채 하늘로 솟아 있었던 것이다. 하늘 자체도 달라져, 그 안에 차압이 들어 있는 것이 역력했다.\n\n커틀 선장은 인디아 부두 근처의 작은 운하 가장자리에 살았는데, 거기에는 회전교(回轉橋)가 하나 있어, 이따금 다리를 열어 헤매던 괴물 같은 배 한 척이 좌초된 거대한 바다 짐승처럼 거리 위로 어슬렁어슬렁 거슬러 올라오게 해 주었다. 커틀 선장의 숙소로 다가갈수록 육지에서 물로 점차 변해 가는 모습은 진기했다. 그것은 술집의 부속물인 양 깃대들이 세워지는 것으로 시작되었다. 다음으로는 헌옷 장수들의 가게가 나왔는데, 거지 셔츠며 방수 모자며, 같은 부류 중에서도 가장 꽉 끼면서 동시에 가장 헐렁한 범포 바지가 바깥에 걸려 있었다. 그다음으로는 닻과 사슬 케이블을 만드는 대장간들이 이어져, 거기서는 큰 쇠망치가 하루 종일 쇠를 땅땅 두드려 댔다. 그다음으로는 집들이 늘어서 있었는데, 진홍빛 강낭콩 사이로 작은 풍향계 단 돛대들이 솟아 있었다. 그다음은 도랑들. 그다음은 가지 친 버드나무들. 그다음은 또 도랑들. 그다음은 까닭 모를, 더러운 물웅덩이들이 여기저기 있었는데, 그 위를 뒤덮은 배들 때문에 거의 분간하기 어려울 지경이었다. 그다음으로는 대팻밥 냄새가 공기에 배어 있었고, 다른 모든 업종이 돛대, 노, 도르래 제작과 배 만드는 일에 삼켜져 버렸다. 그다음으로는 땅이 질척하고 불안정해졌다. 그다음으로는 럼주와 설탕 말고는 아무 냄새도 나지 않았다. 그다음으로는 커틀 선장의 숙소가—브릭 플레이스에 있는, 이층이자 동시에 꼭대기 층인 그 방이—바로 코앞에 있었다.\n\n선장은 목재처럼 생긴 사람들 부류에 속했는데, 마음씨뿐 아니라 옷차림까지 참나무로 한 벌 지어 입은 듯한 그런 사람들이라, 아무리 생기 넘치는 상상력으로도 그를 옷의 어느 한 부분과—그것이 아무리 하찮은 것일지라도—떼어 놓기란 거의 불가능했다. 그래서 월터가 문을 두드리자 선장이 즉시 자기의 작은 앞창 하나로 머리를 쑥 내밀고 그를 불렀을 때, 그 머리에 어느새 단단한 광택 모자가 얹혀 있고, 셔츠 깃이 돛처럼 서 있으며, 헐렁한 푸른 한 벌이 평소처럼 죄다 갖추어져 있는 것을 보고는, 월터는 선장이 늘 그런 상태로 있다고, 마치 선장이 새이고 그것들이 그의 깃털이기라도 한 양 완전히 믿어 버렸다.\n\n\"월터, 얘야!\" 커틀 선장이 말했다. \"대기하고서 다시 두드려 봐라. 세게! 오늘이 빨래하는 날이거든.\"\n\n월터는 조급한 마음에 노커를 어마어마하게 쾅 내리쳤다.\n\n\"세게 두드렸군!\" 커틀 선장이 말하더니, 마치 돌풍이라도 예상하는 듯 즉시 머리를 안으로 끌어들였다.\n\n그의 예상은 빗나가지 않았다. 소매를 어깨까지 걷어 올리고, 두 팔에 비누 거품이 일고 뜨거운 물 김이 모락모락 피어오르는 어느 과부가, 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 잽싸게 그 부름에 응답해 나왔던 것이다. 그 여인은 월터를 보기에 앞서 노커를 보고는, 이어 그를 머리부터 발끝까지 눈으로 가늠하더니, 노커가 남아나기는 했는지 모르겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"커틀 선장님 댁에 계신 거 알아요,\" 월터가 비위를 맞추려는 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래요?\" 과부가 응수했다. \"그-래요!\"\n\n\"방금 저한테 말씀하셨는걸요,\" 월터가 숨을 헐떡이며 설명했다.\n\n\"그러셨어요?\" 과부가 응수했다. \"그럼 아마 그분께 맥스팅어 부인이 안부 여쭙더라고 전해 주시고, 다음번에 또 창밖으로 떠들어 대서 당신 체면이랑 당신 하숙집 격을 떨어뜨릴 양이면, 친절히 좀 내려와서 문도 직접 열어 주시라고 말씀드려 줘요.\" 맥스팅어 부인은 큰 소리로 말하면서, 이층에서 무슨 한마디가 나오나 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"전해 드릴게요,\" 월터가 말했다, \"들어가게 해 주시면요, 부인.\"\n\n문간을 가로질러 친 나무 방벽 때문에 그가 막혀 있었던 것인데, 그것은 어린 맥스팅어들이 노는 동안 계단 아래로 굴러떨어지지 않게 거기 세워 둔 것이었다.\n\n\"우리 집 문짝을 다 부숴 놓을 수 있는 애라면,\" 맥스팅어 부인이 경멸하듯 말했다, \"저쯤은 넘어올 수 있겠지요, 안 그래요!\" 그러나 월터가 이것을 들어와도 좋다는 허락으로 받아들여 그것을 넘어서자, 맥스팅어 부인은 곧장 영국 여인의 집은 그녀의 성(城)인지 아닌지, 그리고 자기가 \"잡것들\"한테 불쑥 침입당해도 되는 건지 따져 물었다. 월터가 빨래 때문에 생겨난 인공 안개를 뚫고—그 안개는 난간을 끈적끈적한 땀으로 뒤덮고 있었다—작은 계단을 올라가 커틀 선장의 방에 들어서서, 그 신사가 문 뒤에 매복해 있는 것을 발견했을 때에도, 이 문제들에 관한 그녀의 갈증은 여전히 끈질기게 정보를 갈구하고 있었다.\n\n\"저 여자한테 단돈 한 푼 빚진 적 없다, 월터,\" 커틀 선장이 낮은 목소리로, 얼굴에 두려운 기색을 역력히 드러내며 말했다. \"오히려 저 여자한테, 그리고 애들한테도 숱하게 좋은 일을 해 줬지. 그래도 가끔 표독스러운 여편네라니까. 휘유!\"\n\n\"저 같으면 떠나 버리겠어요, 커틀 선장님,\" 월터가 말했다.\n\n\"감히 그러지를 못한다, 월터,\" 선장이 대답했다. \"내가 어디로 가든 저 여자가 기어이 찾아낼 거야. 앉아라. 길스는 좀 어떠냐?\"\n\n선장은 (모자를 쓴 채로) 식은 양고기 허릿살, 흑맥주, 그리고 김이 모락모락 나는 감자 몇 개로 식사를 하고 있었는데, 그 감자는 자기가 직접 삶아 필요할 때마다 난롯불 앞 작은 냄비에서 꺼내 먹는 것이었다. 그는 식사 때면 자기 갈고리를 비틀어 빼고, 그 나무 소켓에 대신 칼을 비틀어 끼웠는데, 이미 그 칼로 월터에게 줄 감자 하나를 까기 시작한 참이었다. 그의 방은 무척 비좁았고 담배 연기가 짙게 배어 있었지만, 그런대로 아늑했다. 마치 삼십 분마다 정기적으로 지진이라도 나는 양, 모든 것이 차곡차곡 치워져 있었다.\n\n\"길스는 좀 어떠냐?\" 선장이 물었다.\n\n이때쯤이면 숨을 돌렸으나 기운은—다급한 여정이 잠시 불어넣어 준 그 일시적인 기운마저—잃어버린 월터는, 묻는 이를 잠시 바라보다가, \"오, 커틀 선장님!\" 하더니 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n이 광경에 선장이 얼마나 경악했는지는 말로 다 표현할 수 없다. 맥스팅어 부인 따위는 그 앞에서 흔적도 없이 사라져 버렸다. 그는 감자와 포크를 떨어뜨렸고—할 수만 있었다면 칼도 떨어뜨렸으리라—소년을 빤히 바라보며 앉아 있었으니, 마치 다음 순간 시(市)에 심연이 열려 자기 옛 친구를, 커피색 양복이며 단추며 정밀시계며 안경이며 죄다 한꺼번에 삼켜 버렸다는 소식이라도 들으려는 듯했다.\n\n그러나 월터가 정말로 무슨 일인지 그에게 이야기하자, 커틀 선장은 잠시 생각하고 나서 벌떡 일어나 본격적으로 움직이기 시작했다. 그는 찬장 맨 위 선반에 있던 작은 양철통을 비워, 자기의 현금 재산 전부를(십삼 파운드 반 크라운에 달했다) 꺼내어 네모난 푸른 외투의 호주머니 하나에 옮겨 넣었다. 또 시들어 빠진 찻숟가락 두 개의 잔해와 한물간 안짱다리 설탕 집게 한 벌로 이루어진 은제품 상자의 내용물로 그 호주머니를 한층 더 불렸다. 그러고는 깊숙이 묻혀 잠들어 있던 자기의 거대한 이중 케이스 은시계를 끄집어내어, 그 귀중품이 멀쩡하고 온전한지 확인했다. 갈고리를 다시 오른쪽 손목에 부착하고, 옹이가 잔뜩 덮인 지팡이를 움켜쥔 다음, 월터에게 가자고 일렀다.\n\n그러나 그 의로운 흥분의 와중에도, 맥스팅어 부인이 아래층에서 매복해 기다리고 있을지 모른다는 데에 생각이 미치자, 선장은 마침내 머뭇거렸다. 그러면서 창문을 흘끔거렸는데, 그 무서운 적과 맞닥뜨리느니 차라리 그 유별난 출구로 빠져나갈까 하는 생각을 얼마간 품은 듯했다. 그러나 그는 결국 책략 쪽으로 마음을 정했다.\n\n\"월터,\" 선장이 겁먹은 듯 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다. \"네가 앞장서라, 얘야. 복도에 나가거든 '안녕히 계세요, 커틀 선장님' 하고 소리쳐 외친 다음 문을 닫아. 그러고는 거리 모퉁이에서 내가 보일 때까지 기다려라.\"\n\n이런 지시는 적의 전술을 미리 알고서 내린 것이었다. 월터가 아래층으로 내려가자, 맥스팅어 부인이 복수의 정령처럼 작은 뒷부엌에서 스르르 미끄러져 나왔던 것이다. 그러나 자기가 예상했던 대로 선장에게 미끄러져 나온 게 아닌지라, 그녀는 노커에 관한 언급을 한 번 더 덧붙이고는 다시 스르르 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n커틀 선장이 탈출을 감행할 용기를 그러모으기까지는 오 분쯤 걸렸다. 그동안 월터는 거리 모퉁이에서, 그 단단한 광택 모자가 나타날 기미가 보이기를 기다리며 집 쪽을 돌아보며 그토록 오래 서 있었다. 마침내 선장은 폭발하듯 별안간 문을 박차고 나와, 어깨너머로 단 한 번도 돌아보지 않은 채 빠른 걸음으로 그에게 다가왔고, 거리를 완전히 벗어나자마자 휘파람으로 곡조를 부는 척했다.\n\n\"외삼촌이 많이 가라앉으셨더냐, 월터?\" 함께 걸으며 선장이 물었다.\n\n\"그런 것 같아요. 오늘 아침 그 모습을 보셨다면, 결코 잊지 못하셨을 거예요.\"\n\n\"빨리 걸어라, 월터, 얘야,\" 선장이 발걸음을 재촉하며 대답했다, \"그리고 평생 그렇게 걸어라. 그 충고는 교리문답서를 샅샅이 뒤져서라도 찾아 명심해 두려무나!\"\n\n선장은 솔로몬 길스에 대한 제 생각으로—거기에 어쩌면 방금 맥스팅어 부인에게서 벗어난 데 대한 얼마간의 회상이 뒤섞여 있었으리라—너무 바빠서, 가는 길에 월터의 도덕적 향상을 위한 인용구를 더 내놓지는 못했다. 두 사람은 노 솔의 집 문 앞에 이를 때까지 더는 한마디도 주고받지 않았다. 거기서는 그 불운한 나무 사관후보생이 기구를 눈에 댄 채, 곤경에서 자기를 구해 줄 어떤 친구라도 찾으려는 듯 온 지평선을 살피고 있는 것 같았다.\n\n\"길스!\" 선장이 뒷방으로 황급히 들어가 자못 다정하게 그의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"고개를 단단히 바람 쪽으로 향하게나, 그러면 우리가 이 난관을 뚫고 나갈 거야. 자네가 할 일은,\" 선장은 인간의 지혜가 여태껏 발견한 가장 귀중한 실천 신조 가운데 하나를 토해 내는 사람의 엄숙함으로 말했다, \"그저 고개를 단단히 바람 쪽으로 향하는 것뿐이야, 그러면 우리가 이 난관을 뚫고 나갈 테니까!\"\n\n노 솔은 그의 손길에 마주 힘을 주며 고마워했다.\n\n그러고 나서 커틀 선장은, 사안의 성격에 걸맞은 엄숙함으로, 찻숟가락 두 개와 설탕 집게, 은시계, 그리고 현금을 탁자 위에 내려놓고는, 중개인 브로글리 씨에게 손해액이 얼마냐고 물었다.\n\n\"자! 얼마로 보시오?\" 커틀 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"원, 이거 참!\" 중개인이 대답했다. \"설마 그 재산이 무슨 쓸모가 있다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"왜 아니라는 거요?\" 선장이 물었다.\n\n\"왜냐고요? 금액이 삼백칠십몇 파운드란 말이오,\" 중개인이 대답했다.\n\n\"상관없소,\" 선장이 응수했다. 다만 그 숫자에 적잖이 당황한 기색이 역력했다. \"당신 그물에 걸려드는 거라면 뭐든 다 고기 아니겠소?\"\n\n\"물론이지요,\" 브로글리 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 정어리가 고래는 아니잖소, 아시다시피.\"\n\n이 말의 철학이 선장의 마음을 친 모양이었다. 그는 잠시 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨, 그동안 중개인을 심오한 천재라도 되는 양 바라보았다. 그러더니 기구 제조공을 한쪽으로 불러냈다.\n\n\"길스,\" 커틀 선장이 말했다, \"이 일의 정황이 어떤 건가? 채권자가 누구야?\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 노인이 대답했다. \"이리 와. 월리 앞에서는 말하지 말게. 월리 아버지 보증 건이야—오래된 증서지. 내가 그동안 많이 갚았네, 네드, 하지만 형편이 너무 어려워서 지금 당장은 더 어쩔 수가 없어. 이렇게 될 줄 짐작은 했지만, 막을 도리가 없었지. 무슨 일이 있어도 월리 앞에서는 한마디도 말게.\"\n\n\"돈이 좀 있지 않은가?\" 선장이 속삭였다.\n\n\"있어, 있고말고—오 그럼—좀 있지,\" 노 솔이 대답하며, 먼저 텅 빈 호주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣었다가, 이어 마치 거기서 금이라도 짜낼 수 있다고 여기는 듯 두 손 사이에 자기 웨일스 가발을 쥐어짰다. \"하지만 내가—가진 그 얼마 안 되는 돈은 현금으로 바꿀 수가 없네, 네드. 손댈 수가 없는 거야. 그걸 월리를 위해 뭔가 해 보려고 애써 왔는데, 나는 구식이고 시대에 뒤떨어졌어. 여기저기 흩어져 있고, 그러니까—그러니까, 한마디로 없는 거나 마찬가지야,\" 노인이 어쩔 줄 모르고 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n그는 제 돈을 여기저기 갖가지 곳에 숨겨 두었다가 어디에 두었는지 잊어버린, 반쯤 얼이 빠진 사람 같은 분위기를 어찌나 짙게 풍기던지, 선장은 그의 눈길을 따라가며, 혹시 굴뚝 위나 지하실 아래에 감춰 둔 몇백 파운드라도 노인이 기억해 내지 않을까 하는 희미한 기대를 품지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 솔로몬 길스에게는 그럴 만한 게 없다는 걸 본인이 잘 알았다.\n\n\"나는 시대에 완전히 뒤떨어졌어, 사랑하는 네드,\" 솔이 체념한 절망 속에서 말했다, \"한참이나 말이야. 이렇게까지 멀찍이 뒤처져 비척대는 건 아무 소용이 없어. 차라리 재고를 파는 게 낫겠어—이 빚보다는 값이 더 나가니까—그리고 나는 그 차액을 들고 어디 가서 죽는 게 낫겠지. 내겐 남은 기력이 없어. 세상일을 모르겠고. 차라리 이걸로 끝을 보는 게 나아. 재고를 팔게 하고, 저 친구도 끌어내리라고 하게,\" 노인이 나무 사관후보생을 가리키며 힘없이 말했다, \"우리 둘 다 함께 해체되게 두라고.\"\n\n\"그럼 월터는 어떻게 할 작정인가?\" 선장이 말했다. \"자, 자! 앉게, 길스, 앉아서, 내가 이 일을 좀 생각해 보게 해 줘. 내가 오늘까지는 넉넉했던 쥐꼬리만 한 연금이나 받는 사람이 아니었다면, 이런 걸 생각할 필요도 없었을 텐데. 하지만 자네는 그저 고개를 단단히 바람 쪽으로 향하기만 하게,\" 선장이 그 반박할 수 없는 위로의 말을 다시 한번 일러 주며 말했다, \"그러면 다 잘될 거야!\"\n\n노 솔은 진심으로 그에게 고마워하고는, 가서 그 고개를 뒷방 난로 벽에 기대었다.\n\n커틀 선장은 한동안 가게 안을 이리저리 거닐며 깊이 골똘히 궁리했는데, 마치 산 위에 구름이 내려앉듯 그 텁수룩한 검은 눈썹을 코 위로 어찌나 무겁게 모았던지, 월터는 그 사색의 흐름에 감히 끼어들어 방해할 엄두를 내지 못했다. 일행에게 조금이라도 거북함을 주기 싫어하고, 또 기발한 데가 있는 성미의 브로글리 씨는, 나직이 휘파람을 불며 재고 사이를 돌아다녔다. 기압계를 덜그럭거리고, 나침반을 마치 물약 흔들 듯 흔들어 대고, 자석 달린 열쇠들을 집어 들고, 망원경을 들여다보고, 지구의(地球儀)의 용도에 정통해 보려고 애쓰고, 평행자를 코에 걸터 얹어 보고, 그 밖의 다른 철학적 행위들로 제 흥을 돋우면서 말이다.\n\n\"월터!\" 마침내 선장이 말했다. \"방법을 찾았다.\"\n\n\"찾으셨어요, 커틀 선장님?\" 월터가 무척 활기를 띠며 외쳤다.\n\n\"이리 와 봐라, 얘야,\" 선장이 말했다. \"재고가 담보야. 나도 또 하나의 담보고. 돈을 내줄 사람은 네 사장님이지.\"\n\n\"돔비 씨라고요!\" 월터가 더듬거렸다.\n\n선장이 엄숙하게 고개를 끄덕였다. \"저 양반을 봐라,\" 그가 말했다. \"길스를 봐. 지금 저 사람들이 이 물건들을 다 팔아 치운다면, 저 양반은 그 때문에 죽고 말 거야. 너도 그럴 거라는 걸 알지. 우리는 어떤 수단도 빠뜨려선 안 돼—그리고 저기 너한테 한 가지 수단이 있는 거다.\"\n\n\"수단이라뇨!—돔비 씨가요!\" 월터가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"우선 사무소로 달려가서, 그분이 거기 계신지 보고 와라,\" 커틀 선장이 그의 등을 탁 치며 말했다. \"어서!\"\n\n월터는 그 명령에 토를 달아서는 안 된다고 느꼈고—설령 달리 느꼈다 하더라도 외삼촌을 한번 흘끗 보았으면 마음을 굳혔으리라—그것을 이행하러 사라졌다. 그는 곧 숨을 헐떡이며 돌아와, 돔비 씨가 거기 안 계시다고 말했다. 토요일이라, 그분은 브라이턴에 가셨다는 것이었다.\n\n\"내가 너한테 말해 주마, 월터!\" 선장이 말했는데, 그는 월터가 없는 동안 이런 만일의 사태에 대비해 둔 듯했다. \"우리가 브라이턴으로 가는 거야. 내가 너를 후원해 주마, 얘야. 내가 너를 후원해 줄게, 월터. 오후 마차로 브라이턴에 가는 거다.\"\n\n도무지 생각만 해도 끔찍한 일이지만, 굳이 돔비 씨에게 청을 넣어야 한다면, 월터로서는 커틀 선장의 개인적 영향력을 등에 업기보다—그런 것에 돔비 씨가 별 무게를 두리라고는 도무지 생각되지 않았으니—차라리 혼자 아무 도움 없이 청하는 편이 더 낫다고 느꼈다. 그러나 선장은 전혀 다른 의견인 데다 거기에 단단히 마음을 굳히고 있었고, 또 그의 우정이 자기보다 한참 어린 사람이 함부로 가볍게 여길 수 없을 만큼 열렬하고 진지했던 까닭에, 월터는 조금의 이의도 비치지 않았다. 그리하여 커틀은 솔로몬 길스에게 황급히 작별을 고하고, 현금이며 찻숟가락이며 설탕 집게며 은시계를 다시 호주머니에 집어넣고는—월터가 섬뜩하게 짐작하기로는, 돔비 씨에게 으리으리한 인상을 주려는 속셈이었다—단 일 분도 지체 없이 그를 마차 사무소로 데려갔고, 길에서 거듭, 끝까지 그의 곁을 지키겠노라고 다짐했다.\n\n# CHAPTER X. Containing the Sequel of the Midshipman’s Disaster\n\n배그스톡 소령은 프린세스 플레이스 건너편에서 쌍안 오페라글라스로 폴을 오래도록 자주 관찰한 끝에, 그리고 그 목적으로 톡스 양의 하녀와 끊임없이 연락을 주고받아 온 원주민 하인에게서 그 일에 관해 날마다, 주마다, 달마다 시시콜콜한 보고를 받은 끝에, 돔비라는 사람은 알아 둘 만한 인물이며, 그와 친분을 트기에 J. B.야말로 적임자라는 결론에 이르렀다.\n\n그러나 톡스 양은 줄곧 조심스러운 태도를 지키며, 소령이 이 계획과 관련하여 슬쩍 떠보려고 찾아올 때마다(그는 자주 찾아왔다) 무슨 말인지 모르겠다는 듯 쌀쌀맞게 시치미를 떼었으므로, 소령은 타고난 끈질김과 교활함에도 불구하고 자기 소원의 성취를 어느 정도 운에 맡길 수밖에 없었다. \"그 운이라는 것이,\" 그가 클럽에서 낄낄거리며 곧잘 하던 말마따나, \"이 조이 B.에게는, 형님이 서인도제도에서 황열병으로 죽은 이래로, 줄곧 오십 대 일로 유리하게 작용해 왔다 이 말씀이오.\"\n\n이번 경우에는 그 운이 그를 도우러 오기까지 다소 시간이 걸렸지만, 결국에는 그의 편이 되어 주었다. 검은 하인이 자세한 사정과 함께 톡스 양이 브라이턴 일로 자리를 비웠다고 보고하자, 소령은 문득 벵골에 있는 자기 친구 빌 비더스톤에 대한 애틋한 회상에 사로잡혔다. 그 친구는 혹시 그쪽으로 가게 되거든 자기 외아들을 한번 들여다봐 달라고 편지로 부탁해 온 적이 있었던 것이다. 그런데 그 똑같은 검은 하인이 폴이 핍친 부인 댁에 있다고 보고하자, 소령은—비더스톤 도련님이 영국에 도착했을 때 가져온, 그러나 그가 조금이라도 신경 쓸 생각은 추호도 없었던 그 편지를 떠올리며—눈앞에 펼쳐진 기회를 알아보았다. 그러자 마침 통풍으로 몸져누워 있던 그는 어찌나 광분했던지, 그 소식을 전한 대가로 검은 하인에게 발판을 집어 던지고는, 이 일이 끝나기 전에 그 악당 놈을 죽여 버리고 말겠다고 욕설을 퍼부었다. 검은 하인은 그 말을 반쯤은 진심으로 믿어 두는 편이었다.\n\n마침내 발작에서 풀려난 소령은 어느 토요일, 뒤에 원주민 하인을 거느린 채 으르렁거리며 브라이턴으로 내려갔다. 가는 내내 톡스 양에게 욕을 퍼부으면서, 그녀가 그토록 신비에 싸 두고 또 그를 위해 자기를 버리기까지 한 그 고귀한 친구라는 자를 기습적으로 함락시킬 전망에 흐뭇해했다.\n\n\"어디 한번, 부인, 어디 한번!\" 소령이 복수심으로 안간힘을 쓰며, 이미 부풀어 오른 머리의 핏줄을 모조리 더 부풀리며 말했다. \"이 조이 B.를 따돌려 보시겠다 이 말씀이오, 부인? 아직은 안 되지, 부인, 아직은 안 돼! 빌어먹을, 아직은 안 되고말고. 조는 멀쩡히 깨어 있소, 부인. 배그스톡은 시퍼렇게 살아 있고. J. B.는 수 한두 가지는 알고 있단 말이오, 부인. 조시는 빈틈없이 망을 보고 있소. 부인은 그가 만만치 않다는 걸 알게 될 거요. 만만치 않지, 조지프는 만만치 않은 사람이오. 만만치 않고, 게다가 지독히도 교활하지!\"\n\n그리고 비더스톤 도련님은 소령이 그 어린 신사를 산책에 데리고 나갔을 때, 그가 과연 지독히도 만만치 않은 사람임을 알게 되었다. 그러나 소령은 스틸턴 치즈 같은 안색에 새우 같은 눈을 하고서, 비더스톤 도련님이 재미있어하든 말든 전혀 아랑곳하지 않은 채 이리저리 쏘다니며, 위아래로 사방을 두리번거리며 돔비 씨와 그 아이들을 찾느라 비더스톤 도련님을 질질 끌고 다녔다.\n\n때맞춰 소령은, 미리 핍친 부인에게서 귀띔을 받아 둔 터라, 폴과 플로렌스를 발견하고 그들에게로 들이닥쳤다. 그들과 함께 위엄 있는 신사 한 분(분명 돔비 씨였다)이 있었다. 비더스톤 도련님을 앞세우고 그 작은 무리의 한복판으로 돌격해 들어가니, 당연한 일로 비더스톤 도련님이 같은 처지의 동무들에게 말을 걸게 되었다. 그러자 소령은 걸음을 멈추고 그 아이들을 알은체하며 감탄하더니, 프린세스 플레이스의 친구 톡스 양 댁에서 그들을 보고 말을 나눈 적이 있다는 것을 깜짝 놀란 듯 떠올렸다. 그러고는 폴이 지독히도 멋진 녀석이며 자기의 어린 친구라고 단언하고, 소령인 이 조이 B.를 기억하느냐고 물었다. 그러더니 마침내, 세상의 격식이라는 것이 문득 떠오른 듯, 돔비 씨를 향해 돌아서서 사과했다.\n\n\"하지만 여기 이 어린 친구가 말입니다, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"저를 다시 소년으로 만들어 놓는군요. 늙은 군인이—저는 배그스톡 소령이라 합니다, 잘 부탁드립니다—그걸 고백하기를 부끄러워하지 않습니다.\" 여기서 소령은 모자를 벗어 들었다. \"빌어먹을, 선생,\" 소령이 갑작스러운 열기를 띠며 외쳤다, \"저는 선생이 부럽습니다.\" 그러더니 정신을 차리고 덧붙였다. \"제 무례를 용서하십시오.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 그런 말씀은 마시라고 청했다.\n\n\"고참병이,\" 소령이 말했다, \"연기에 그을리고 햇볕에 그을린, 다 닳아빠진, 폐물이 다 된 늙은 개 같은 소령이 말입니다, 선생, 돔비 씨 같은 분에게 제 변덕을 두고 책망을 받을까 두려워하지는 않습니다. 제가 지금 돔비 씨를 마주하고 있는 영광을 누리는 것이라 믿습니다만?\"\n\n\"제가 그 이름의, 지금으로서는, 변변치 못한 대표자올시다, 소령,\" 돔비 씨가 응수했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 선생!\" 소령이 말했다, \"그건 위대한 이름입니다. 그건,\" 소령이 마치 돔비 씨더러 반박해 보라고 도전하듯, 그리고 만약 반박한다면 그를 윽박지르는 것이 자기의 괴로운 의무라고 여기기라도 하듯 단호하게 말했다, \"선생, 해외의 영국 영토에서 익히 알려지고 존경받는 이름입니다. 사람이 알아본다는 것만으로도 자랑스러워할 만한 이름이지요. 이 조지프 배그스톡에게는 아첨이라는 것이 없습니다, 선생. 요크 공작 전하께서도 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번 말씀하셨지요, '조이에게는 아첨이라는 게 없다. 조는 꾸밈없는 늙은 군인이다. 조지프는 흠이라 할 만큼 만만치 않은 사람이다'라고요. 하지만 그건 위대한 이름입니다, 선생. 맹세코, 위대한 이름이지요!\" 소령이 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"아마도 소령께서는 그 이름을 분에 넘치게 높이 쳐 주시는군요,\" 돔비 씨가 응수했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 선생,\" 소령이 준엄한 어조로 말했다. \"아닙니다, 돔비 씨, 우리 서로를 분명히 알고 지냅시다. 그건 배그스톡의 본색이 아닙니다, 선생. 선생께서는 이 조지프 B.를 모르십니다. 조시는 무뚝뚝한 늙은 칼날 같은 사람입니다. 그에게는 아첨이라는 게 없습니다, 선생. 그런 건 조금도 없지요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 고개를 숙이고는, 그가 진심인 것으로 믿으며 그처럼 높이 평가해 주니 흐뭇하다고 말했다.\n\n\"여기 이 어린 친구가 말입니다, 선생,\" 소령이 폴을 향해 최대한 상냥한 표정을 지어 보이며 쉰 목소리로 말했다, \"이 조지프 배그스톡이 철두철미하고, 솔직담백하고, 거침없이 말하는 늙은 호인이라는 것을, 그 이상도 이하도 아니라는 것을 증언해 줄 겁니다, 선생. 저 아이는요, 선생,\" 소령이 목소리를 낮추어 말했다, \"역사에 길이 남을 겁니다. 저 아이는 흔해 빠진 인물이 아닙니다. 잘 보살피십시오, 돔비 씨.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 그러도록 애써 보겠다는 뜻을 넌지시 비치는 듯했다.\n\n\"여기 또 한 녀석이 있습니다, 선생,\" 소령이 은밀하게 말을 이으며, 지팡이로 그 아이를 쿡 찔렀다. \"벵골의 비더스톤의 아들입지요. 빌 비더스톤은 한때 우리 부대에 있었습니다. 저 아이의 아비와 저는, 선생, 맹세한 친구 사이였습니다. 어딜 가든, 선생, 빌 비더스톤과 조 배그스톡 이야기밖에 들리지 않았지요. 제가 저 아이의 결점을 못 본 척하느냐고요? 천만에요. 저 녀석은 바보입니다, 선생.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 적어도 소령만큼은 그에 대해 알고 있는, 그 비방당한 비더스톤 도련님을 흘끗 보고는, 자못 흡족한 태도로 말했다. \"정말입니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다. \"저 녀석은 바보예요. 이 조 배그스톡은 절대 말을 빙빙 돌리지 않습니다. 제 옛 친구, 벵골의 빌 비더스톤의 아들놈은 타고난 바보랍니다, 선생.\" 여기서 소령은 얼굴이 거의 검어지도록 웃어 댔다. \"제 어린 친구는 퍼블릭스쿨로 보낼 작정이시겠지요, 돔비 씨?\" 소령이 정신을 차리고 나서 말했다.\n\n\"아직 확실히 정하지는 못했습니다,\" 돔비 씨가 응수했다. \"안 보낼 것 같습니다. 이 아이는 몸이 약하거든요.\"\n\n\"몸이 약하다면, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"옳은 판단이십니다. 샌드허스트에서는 만만치 않은 녀석들만 버텨 낼 수 있습니다, 선생. 거기서 우리는 서로를 고문했지요, 선생. 새로 들어온 녀석들을 약한 불에 천천히 구웠고, 머리를 거꾸로 한 채 삼층 창문 밖으로 매달아 놓았습니다. 이 조지프 배그스톡도, 선생, 부츠 뒤축에 매달려 학교 시계로 십삼 분 동안 창밖에 걸려 있었답니다.\"\n\n소령은 이 이야기의 입증을 위해 자기 얼굴을 증거로 내세울 수도 있었을 것이다. 확실히 그 얼굴은 그가 좀 지나치게 오래 매달려 있었던 것처럼 보였으니까.\n\n\"하지만 그것이 우리를 지금의 우리로 만들었지요, 선생,\" 소령이 셔츠 주름 장식을 매만지며 말했다. \"우리는 무쇠였고, 그것이 우리를 단련시켰습니다. 여기 계속 머무르십니까, 돔비 씨?\"\n\n\"보통 일주일에 한 번씩 내려옵니다, 소령,\" 그 신사가 응수했다. \"베드퍼드에 묵습니다.\"\n\n\"허락하신다면, 베드퍼드로 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리고 싶습니다, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다. \"이 조이 B.는, 선생, 대체로 남을 찾아다니는 사람이 아닙니다만, 돔비 씨의 이름은 흔한 이름이 아니니까요. 이렇게 소개받는 영광을 누리게 해 준 제 어린 친구에게 큰 신세를 졌습니다, 선생.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 매우 정중하게 답례했다. 그리고 배그스톡 소령은 폴의 머리를 토닥이고, 플로렌스를 두고는 그 눈이 머지않아 젊은이들을 애먹일 거라고—\"나이 든 사람들도 마찬가지지요, 그건 그렇고요, 선생,\" 하고 소령이 한껏 낄낄거리며 덧붙였다—말한 뒤, 지팡이로 비더스톤 도련님을 쿡 찔러 일으켜 그 어린 신사를 데리고, 일종의 반쯤 종종걸음으로 떠났다. 다리를 한껏 벌린 채 비틀거리며 멀어지는 동안, 머리를 흔들고 자못 위엄 있게 기침을 해 댔다.\n\n소령은 자기 약속을 지켜, 나중에 돔비 씨를 찾아갔다. 그리고 돔비 씨는 육군 명부를 확인해 본 뒤, 나중에 소령을 찾아갔다. 그러고는 소령이 시내의 돔비 씨 집으로 찾아갔다가, 돔비 씨와 같은 마차를 타고 다시 내려왔다. 요컨대 돔비 씨와 소령은 유난히 잘, 그리고 유난히 빠르게 가까워졌다. 그리고 돔비 씨는 누이에게 소령을 두고, 그는 꽤나 군인다운 사람일 뿐만 아니라 실로 그 이상의 무언가가 있는데, 자기 직업과 무관한 일들의 중요성에 대해서도 매우 훌륭한 식견을 지녔기 때문이라고 평했다.\n\n마침내 돔비 씨는 톡스 양과 칙 부인을 데리고 아이들을 보러 내려왔다가, 소령이 또 브라이턴에 있는 것을 알고는, 그를 베드퍼드의 만찬에 초대하고, 그에 앞서 톡스 양에게 그녀의 이웃이자 지인을 두고 높이 칭찬했다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 루이자,\" 약속한 날 아침, 두 사람만 함께 있을 때 톡스 양이 칙 부인에게 말했다. \"혹시 내가 배그스톡 소령에게 조금이라도 서먹하게 굴거나 어딘가 거북해하는 것처럼 보이거든, 부디 모르는 척해 주겠다고 약속해 줘요.\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 루크리셔,\" 칙 부인이 응수했다. \"이 별난 부탁에 무슨 비밀이 숨어 있는 거예요? 꼭 알아야겠어요.\"\n\n\"기어이 나한테서 고백을 짜내려 작정했다면, 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 곧바로 말했다, \"소령이 각별했다는 걸 털어놓는 수밖에 없겠네요.\"\n\n\"각별했다고요!\" 칙 부인이 따라 했다.\n\n\"소령은 그 관심에 있어서 오래도록 정말 각별했어요, 자기,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"이따금 그게 어찌나 노골적이던지, 내 처지가 여간 곤란하지 않았다니까요.\"\n\n\"그 사람 형편은 괜찮은가요?\" 칙 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"내가 믿을 만한 근거는 충분해요, 자기—사실 안다고 해도 될 만큼요,\" 톡스 양이 응수했다, \"그가 부유하다는 걸요. 그는 진정한 군인이고, 일화도 무궁무진해요. 현역으로 복무할 때 그 용맹함이 끝이 없었다고 들었어요. 이베리아 반도에서는 온갖 종류의 화기를 가지고 별의별 일을 다 했다고 하고요, 자기, 동인도와 서인도에서는 정말이지, 그가 안 한 일이 뭔지 내가 다 말할 수 없을 정도예요.\"\n\n\"정말 그 사람에게는 명예로운 일이네요,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"지극히 그렇고말고요. 그래서 자기는 그에게 아무 기대도 갖게 하지 않았어요?\"\n\n\"내가 만약, 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 영혼을 쥐어짜는 듯한 안간힘을 온통 내보이며 대답했다, \"배그스톡 소령에게 조금도 기대를 갖게 하지 않았다고 말한다면, 그건 자기와 나 사이에 존재하는 우정에 떳떳치 못한 일이 될 거예요. 한때 소령이 나한테 쏟아부은 그런 관심을, 어떤 의무감도 내비치지 않고 받아넘기는 건 아마 여자의 본성상 거의 불가능한 일이겠지요. 하지만 그건 다 지난 일이에요—먼 옛날 일이지요. 소령과 나 사이에는 이제 입을 떡 벌린 깊은 골이 가로놓여 있고, 나는 내 마음을 줄 수 없는 곳에서 기대를 갖게 하는 척하지는 않을 거예요, 루이자. 내 애정은요,\" 톡스 양이 말했다—\"하지만, 루이자, 이건 미친 짓이에요!\" 그러고는 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n칙 부인은 이 모든 것을 만찬 전에 자기 오라비에게 전했다. 그것은 돔비 씨가 평소답지 않은 따뜻함으로 소령을 맞이하는 데 결코 방해가 되지 않았다. 소령은 소령대로, 끝을 모르는 충만한 만족의 상태에 빠져 있었다. 그는 기침을 하고, 컥컥거리고, 낄낄거리고, 헐떡이고, 부풀어 올라서, 급기야 급사들이 그를 정말로 무서워하는 듯이 보일 지경이었다.\n\n\"댁의 가족이 조의 햇빛을 독차지하고 있습니다, 선생,\" 소령이 톡스 양에게 인사를 마치고 나서 말했다. \"조는 어둠 속에 살고 있지요. 프린세스 플레이스는 겨울철에 캄차카로 변해 버렸습니다. 이제 이 조이 B.에게는 햇살 한 줄기 들지 않아요, 선생.\"\n\n\"톡스 양은 친절하게도 폴에게 무척 관심을 가져 주십니다, 소령,\" 돔비 씨가 얼굴을 붉히는 그 처녀를 대신해 응수했다.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"저는 제 어린 친구가 질투 납니다. 저는 시들어 가고 있어요, 선생. 배그스톡 가문은 이 버림받은 늙은 조라는 인물 속에서 퇴락하고 있습니다.\" 그러고는 소령은 점점 더 시퍼레지고, 꽉 조인 크라바트의 빳빳한 등성이 위로 두 볼을 점점 더 부풀리며, 마치 그 순간 군사학교의 약한 불에 지나치게 구워지고 있기라도 한 것처럼 눈이 튀어나올 지경으로 톡스 양을 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n이런 암시들이 그녀에게 가슴 두근거림을 일으키기는 했지만, 그것들은 톡스 양에게 결코 불쾌하지 않았다. 덕분에 그녀는 매우 흥미로운 사람이 될 수 있었고, 이따금 종잡을 수 없고 정신없어하는 모습을 내보일 수 있었는데, 그녀로서는 그런 모습을 드러내는 것이 전혀 싫지 않았던 것이다. 소령은 그녀에게 이런 감정을 드러낼 기회를 듬뿍 마련해 주었다. 만찬 내내 그가 자기와 프린세스 플레이스를 저버렸다며 늘어놓는 불평이 끊이지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 그런 불평을 늘어놓는 데서 큰 즐거움을 얻는 듯이 보였으므로, 자리는 다들 매우 화기애애하게 흘러갔다.\n\n소령이 대화 전체를 도맡아, 식탁 위의 갖가지 진미에 대해서 그러했던 것 못지않게 그 방면에서도 왕성한 식욕을 보였다고 해서 분위기가 나빠지지는 않았다. 그 진미들로 말하자면 그가 거의 뒹굴다시피 했다고 해도 좋을 정도였으니, 그의 염증성 체질을 크게 악화시켰다. 돔비 씨의 평소 과묵함과 조심성이 이런 침범에 선뜻 자리를 내어 주자, 소령은 자기가 두각을 드러내며 빛나고 있다고 느꼈다. 그리하여 솟구치는 기운에 자기 이름을 가지고 어찌나 끝없이 새로운 변주를 울려 대던지, 스스로도 깜짝 놀랄 지경이었다. 한마디로, 다들 매우 흡족해했다. 소령은 무궁무진한 화제의 보고를 지닌 사람으로 여겨졌다. 그리고 그가 긴 카드놀이 한 판을 마친 뒤 늦게야 작별을 고하자, 돔비 씨는 다시 한번 얼굴을 붉히는 톡스 양에게 그녀의 이웃이자 지인을 두고 칭찬을 건넸다.\n\n그러나 자기 호텔로 돌아오는 내내, 소령은 자기 자신을 향해, 또 자기 자신에 대해 끊임없이 중얼거렸다, \"교활하지, 선생—교활해, 선생—지독히도 교활해!\" 그리고 그곳에 도착해서는 의자에 앉아, 이따금 그를 사로잡곤 하는 소리 없는 웃음 발작에 빠져들었는데, 그것은 언제나 유난히 무시무시했다. 이번에는 그 발작이 어찌나 오래 그를 붙들고 놓아주지 않던지, 멀찍이 서서 그를 지켜보면서도 죽었다 깨어나도 감히 다가가지는 못하던 검은 하인이 두세 번이나 그가 영영 끝장났다고 단념할 정도였다. 그의 온몸이, 특히 얼굴과 머리가, 일찍이 없던 정도로 부풀어 올라, 검은 하인의 눈에는 그저 출렁이는 쪽빛 덩어리 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 마침내 그는 격렬한 기침 발작을 터뜨렸고, 그것이 좀 가라앉자 다음과 같은 외침들을 쏟아냈다.\n\n\"어디 한번, 부인, 어디 한번 해 보시지? 돔비 부인이라, 응, 부인? 어림없지, 부인. 이 조 B.가 부인의 수레바퀴에 막대기 하나 꽂아 넣을 수 있는 한은 안 되고말고, 부인. 이 J. B.가 지금 부인과 비긴 셈이오, 부인. 이 배그스톡이 아직 완전히 나가떨어진 건 아니란 말씀이오, 선생. 부인은 속이 깊지, 선생, 깊고말고, 하지만 이 조시는 더 깊단 말이오. 이 늙은 조는 멀쩡히 깨어 있소—활짝 깨어, 눈을 부릅뜨고 있다 이 말씀이오, 선생!\" 이 마지막 단언이 진실이라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었고, 그것도 매우 무시무시한 정도로 그러했다. 그 밤의 대부분 동안 그것이 계속되었으니, 소령은 그 밤을 주로 비슷한 외침들로 보냈는데, 그 사이사이에 온 집안을 놀라게 하는 기침과 컥컥거리는 발작이 끼어들었다.\n\n이 일이 있은 다음 날(일요일이었다), 돔비 씨와 칙 부인과 톡스 양이 아침 식탁에 앉아 여전히 소령을 추켜세우고 있을 때, 플로렌스가 뛰어들어 왔다. 얼굴은 발그레하게 상기되고 눈은 기쁨으로 반짝이며, 그 애가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아빠! 아빠! 월터가 왔어요! 그런데 안 들어오려고 해요.\"\n\n\"누구라고?\" 돔비 씨가 외쳤다. \"저 애가 무슨 말을 하는 거지? 이게 무슨 일이야?\"\n\n\"월터요, 아빠!\" 플로렌스가 너무 허물없이 그 앞에 다가왔음을 깨닫고는 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"제가 길을 잃었을 때 저를 찾아 준 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"저 애가 게이라는 젊은이를 말하는 거요, 루이자?\" 돔비 씨가 미간을 찌푸리며 물었다. \"정말이지, 이 아이 행실이 아주 거칠어졌군. 게이라는 젊은이를 말하는 건 아닐 텐데. 무슨 일인지 좀 알아봐 주겠소?\"\n\n칙 부인이 서둘러 복도로 나갔다가, 그것이 게이라는 젊은이이며 매우 괴상하게 생긴 사람과 함께 왔다는 소식을 가지고 돌아왔다. 그리고 게이라는 젊은이가 말하기를, 돔비 씨가 아침 식사 중이라는 말을 듣고는 함부로 들어가는 무례를 범하지 않겠으며, 돔비 씨가 들어와도 좋다는 뜻을 비칠 때까지 기다리겠다고 했다는 것이었다.\n\n\"이제 그 애더러 들어오라고 하시오,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"자, 게이, 무슨 일인가? 누가 자네를 여기로 보냈나? 달리 올 사람이 없었나?\"\n\n\"송구합니다, 선생님,\" 월터가 응수했다. \"저는 보내져서 온 것이 아닙니다. 외람되게도 제 발로 온 것인데, 그 까닭을 말씀드리면 용서해 주시리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n그러나 돔비 씨는 그의 말에 귀를 기울이지 않고, 마치 그가 길을 가로막은 기둥이라도 되는 양 그의 양옆으로, 뒤에 있는 어떤 물체를 못마땅하다는 듯 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"저게 뭔가?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"저 사람은 누구지? 자네가 문을 잘못 찾아온 모양이군, 선생.\"\n\n\"오, 누군가와 함께 끼어들어 송구합니다, 선생님,\" 월터가 황급히 외쳤다. \"하지만 이분은—이분은 커틀 선장이십니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"월터, 이 사람아,\" 선장이 굵은 목소리로 일렀다. \"버티고 서 있게!\"\n\n이와 동시에 선장은 조금 더 안으로 들어서며, 그 헐렁한 푸른 옷과 눈에 띄는 셔츠 깃과 울퉁불퉁한 코를 환히 드러내고는, 돔비 씨에게 절을 하고 갈고리를 정중히 흔들어 부인들에게 인사했다. 한 손에는 딱딱하고 번들거리는 모자를 들었고, 머리에는 그 모자가 방금 새겨 놓은 붉은 적도가 한 바퀴 둘러져 있었다.\n\n돔비 씨는 이 진기한 광경을 놀라움과 분개로 바라보며, 그 표정으로 칙 부인과 톡스 양에게 항의를 호소하는 듯했다. 플로렌스를 뒤따라 들어온 어린 폴은, 선장이 갈고리를 흔들자 톡스 양 쪽으로 물러서며 방어 자세를 취했다.\n\n\"자, 게이,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"나한테 할 말이 무엇인가?\"\n\n선장은 다시 한번, 모두를 무마하지 않을 수 없는 대화의 전반적인 서두로서 일렀다. \"월터, 버티고 서 있게!\"\n\n\"송구합니다만, 선생님,\" 월터가 떨면서, 바닥을 내려다보며 입을 열었다, \"제가 이렇게 찾아온 것이 매우 큰 무례인 줄 압니다—정말이지 그런 줄 압니다. 여기까지 내려온 뒤에도, 만약 제가 돔비 아가씨를 마주치지 않았다면, 감히 선생님을 뵙겠다고 청할 용기조차 내지 못했을 것입니다, 그리고—\"\n\n\"음!\" 돔비 씨가, 월터가 주의 깊게 듣고 있는 플로렌스를 흘끗 보는 데 따라 시선을 옮기며, 그 애가 미소로 그를 격려하자 자기도 모르게 인상을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"계속하게, 어서.\"\n\n\"옳지, 옳소,\" 선장이, 돔비 씨를 거드는 것이 예법상 자기 도리라고 여기고는 일렀다. \"잘 말했어! 계속하게, 월터.\"\n\n커틀 선장은 자기를 후원하는 데 대한 사례로 돔비 씨가 그에게 던진 그 눈길에 위축되어 시들어 마땅했을 것이다. 그러나 그런 줄은 전혀 모른 채, 그는 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보이고, 갈고리로 모종의 의미심장한 동작을 해 가며, 월터가 처음에는 좀 수줍어하지만 곧 본색을 드러낼 거라는 뜻을 돔비 씨에게 알렸다.\n\n\"제가 여기 온 것은 전적으로 사사롭고 개인적인 일 때문입니다, 선생님,\" 월터가 더듬거리며 말을 이었다, \"그리고 커틀 선장님께서—\"\n\n\"여기 있네!\" 선장이, 자기가 곁에 있으며 믿어도 좋다는 보증으로서 끼어들었다.\n\n\"이분은 제 가엾은 삼촌의 아주 오랜 친구이시고, 더없이 훌륭하신 분인데요, 선생님,\" 월터가 선장을 위하여 간청하는 눈빛으로 눈을 들며 말을 이었다, \"친절하게도 저와 함께 와 주시겠다고 하셨고, 저로서는 차마 거절할 수가 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니지,\" 선장이 흐뭇하게 일렀다. \"거절할 거야 없지. 거절할 까닭이 없고말고. 계속하게, 월터.\"\n\n\"그래서요, 선생님,\" 월터가 용기를 내어 돔비 씨의 눈을 마주하고는, 이제 피할 수 없게 된 처지의 그 절박함 속에서 한결 나은 용기로 말을 이어 갔다, \"그래서 제가 이분과 함께 찾아온 것은, 제 가엾은 늙은 삼촌이 매우 큰 고난과 곤경에 처해 있다는 말씀을 드리기 위해서입니다. 장사가 차츰 기울고, 갚아야 할 돈을 마련하지 못하게 되어—그 걱정이 여러 달 내내 삼촌의 마음을 무겁게 짓눌러 왔는데요, 정말이지 제가 아는 사실입니다, 선생님—집에 차압이 들어와, 가진 것을 모조리 잃고 가슴이 무너질 위기에 놓여 있습니다. 그러니 만약 선생님께서 친절을 베푸시어, 또 삼촌을 존경할 만한 사람으로 오래 알아 오신 정으로, 그 어려움에서 벗어나도록 무엇이든 도와주신다면, 선생님, 저희로서는 아무리 감사를 드려도 모자랄 것입니다.\"\n\n월터는 말을 하면서 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다. 플로렌스의 눈에도 그러했다. 그 애 아버지는 월터만 바라보는 척했지만, 그 눈물이 반짝이는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"매우 큰 액수입니다, 선생님,\" 월터가 말했다. \"삼백 파운드가 넘습니다. 제 삼촌은 그 불운에 완전히 짓눌려 버렸습니다, 그게 어찌나 무겁게 짓누르는지요. 그래서 자기 자신을 구하기 위해 아무것도 할 수 없는 형편입니다. 제가 선생님께 말씀드리러 온 것조차 아직 모르고 계십니다. 제가 정확히 무엇을 바라는지 말해 주기를 원하시겠지요, 선생님,\" 월터가 잠시 망설인 뒤에 덧붙였다, \"저도 정말 잘 모르겠습니다, 선생님. 제 삼촌의 재고품이 있는데, 거기에는 다른 청구가 없다고 자신 있게 말씀드릴 수 있을 것 같습니다, 그리고 여기 커틀 선장님께서도 보증을 서 주시기를 바라고 계십니다. 저는—제 입으로 말씀드리기는 좀 무엇하지만,\" 월터가 말했다, \"저 같은 사람의 벌이라도, 만약 선생님께서 허락하신다면—쌓아서—상환—선불—삼촌은—검소하고, 정직한, 늙은 분이십니다.\" 월터는 이렇게 끊어진 문장들을 헤매다가 침묵 속으로 잦아들었고, 고용주 앞에서 고개를 떨군 채 서 있었다.\n\n이때가 그 귀중품들을 내보이기에 좋은 순간이라 여기고, 커틀 선장은 식탁으로 다가갔다. 그리고 돔비 씨의 팔꿈치 곁의 아침 식사용 찻잔들 사이로 빈자리를 마련하고는, 은시계와 현금과 찻숟가락과 각설탕 집게를 꺼내 놓았다. 그러고는 그것들을 최대한 값져 보이게 한 무더기로 쌓아 올리고서, 다음과 같은 말을 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"빵 반 덩이가 빵 없는 것보다 낫지요, 그 말은 빵부스러기에도 똑같이 들어맞습니다. 여기 몇 점 있습니다. 연금 백 파운드짜리 증서도 양도할 채비가 되어 있고요. 만약 세상에 학식이 가득 찬 사람이 있다면, 그건 이 늙은 솔 길스올시다. 만약 장래가 촉망되는 젊은이가 있다면—젖과 꿀이,\" 선장이 그 절묘한 인용 가운데 하나를 끼워 넣으며 덧붙였다, \"흐르는 젊은이가 있다면, 그건 바로 저 아이의 조카랍니다!\"\n\n그러고 나서 선장은 마치 어려운 공연에 마무리 손질을 끝낸 사람 같은 태도로 흐트러진 머리칼을 매만지며, 원래 서 있던 자리로 물러났다.\n\n월터가 말을 그치자, 돔비 씨의 시선이 어린 폴에게로 쏠렸다. 폴은 누이가 고개를 떨군 채 방금 들은 곤경에 대한 가엾은 마음으로 소리 없이 울고 있는 것을 보고는, 누이에게 다가가 위로하려 애쓰며, 그러면서 매우 의미심장한 얼굴로 월터와 자기 아버지를 바라보고 있었다. 커틀 선장의 연설로 잠시 산만해졌다가—그는 그것을 거만하게 무관심한 태도로 대했다—돔비 씨는 다시 아들에게로 눈길을 돌려, 한동안 말없이 그 아이를 물끄러미 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"이 빚은 무엇 때문에 진 것인가?\" 돔비 씨가 마침내 물었다. \"채권자는 누구인가?\"\n\n\"이 친구는 모릅니다,\" 선장이 월터의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 대답했다. \"제가 압니다. 지금은 죽고 없는 사람을 도와주다가 생긴 빚인데, 그 일로 제 친구 길스가 이미 수백 파운드를 날렸지요. 괜찮으시다면 자세한 사정은 따로 말씀드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"제 앞가림을 해 나가기에도 벅찬 사람들은,\" 돔비 씨가, 월터 뒤에서 선장이 보내는 알 수 없는 신호들은 알아채지 못한 채 여전히 아들을 바라보며 말했다, \"남의 빚을 떠맡아 그것을 늘릴 게 아니라, 자기 자신의 채무와 어려움에 만족하는 편이 낫지. 그건 또한 부정직하고 주제넘은 짓이야,\" 돔비 씨가 준엄하게 말했다. \"큰 주제넘음이지. 그런 일은 부유한 자나 할 수 있는 거니까. 폴, 이리 오너라!\"\n\n아이는 시키는 대로 했다. 돔비 씨는 그 아이를 무릎에 앉혔다.\n\n\"네가 지금 돈이 있다면—\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"날 보아라!\"\n\n폴은, 누이에게로, 또 월터에게로 헤매던 눈길로, 아버지의 얼굴을 마주 보았다.\n\n\"네가 지금 돈이 있다면,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"저 게이라는 젊은이가 말한 만큼의 돈이 있다면, 너는 어떻게 하겠느냐?\"\n\n\"그 늙은 삼촌에게 주겠어요,\" 폴이 응수했다.\n\n\"그 늙은 삼촌에게 빌려주겠다는 거지, 응?\" 돔비 씨가 받아넘겼다. \"그래! 네가 다 크면, 알다시피, 너는 내 돈을 함께 나누어 갖게 될 것이고, 우리는 그걸 함께 쓰게 될 게다.\"\n\n\"돔비와 아들이요,\" 일찍부터 그 문구를 익혀 온 폴이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"돔비와 아들이지,\" 그 애 아버지가 따라 했다. \"이제부터 돔비와 아들이 되어, 저 게이라는 젊은이의 삼촌에게 이 돈을 빌려주는 일을 시작해 보고 싶으냐?\"\n\n\"오! 그래 주신다면요, 아빠!\" 폴이 말했다. \"그리고 플로렌스도 그러고 싶어 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"여자아이들은,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"돔비와 아들과는 아무 상관이 없단다. 너는 그러고 싶으냐?\"\n\n\"네, 아빠, 네!\"\n\n\"그러면 네가 그렇게 하려무나,\" 그 애 아버지가 응수했다. \"그리고 너도 알겠지, 폴,\" 그가 목소리를 낮추며 덧붙였다, \"돈이 얼마나 힘이 센지, 그리고 사람들이 그걸 얻으려고 얼마나 안달하는지를 말이다. 저 게이라는 젊은이는 돈을 구걸하러 이 먼 길을 왔고, 그토록 대단하고 위대한 너는, 그 돈을 가졌으니, 큰 호의이자 은혜로서 그가 그것을 갖도록 해 주려는 거란다.\"\n\n폴은 잠시 그 늙은이 같은 얼굴을 들었는데, 거기에는 이 말 속에 담긴 암시에 대한 예리한 이해가 어려 있었다. 그러나 곧이어, 그 애가 아버지의 무릎에서 미끄러져 내려와 플로렌스에게 더는 울지 말라고, 게이라는 젊은이가 돈을 갖도록 해 줄 거라고 일러 주러 달려갈 때는, 그것은 어리고 천진한 아이의 얼굴이었다.\n\n그러고 나서 돔비 씨는 옆 탁자로 돌아서서 쪽지 한 장을 써서 봉했다. 그동안 폴과 플로렌스는 월터에게 소곤거렸고, 커틀 선장은 돔비 씨로서는 도저히 믿을 수 없을 만큼 야심차고 이루 말할 수 없이 주제넘은 생각에 잠겨 그 세 사람을 향해 흐뭇하게 미소 지었다. 쪽지를 다 쓰고 나서, 돔비 씨는 원래 자리로 돌아서며 그것을 월터에게 내밀었다.\n\n\"이것을,\" 그가 말했다, \"내일 아침 제일 먼저 카커 씨에게 전하게. 그가 즉시 손을 써서, 내 사람 가운데 하나가 문제의 금액을 지불하여 자네 삼촌을 지금의 처지에서 풀어 줄 것이고, 또 자네 삼촌의 형편에 맞도록 그 상환에 관한 합의가 이루어지도록 할 걸세. 이 일은 폴 도련님이 자네를 위해 해 준 것임을 명심하게.\"\n\n월터는 자기의 선량한 삼촌을 곤경에서 구해 낼 수단을 손에 쥔 감격에 겨워, 자기의 감사와 기쁨을 어떻게든 표현하려 했다. 그러나 돔비 씨가 그를 막았다.\n\n\"이 일은 폴 도련님이 해 준 것임을 명심하게,\" 그가 되풀이했다. \"내가 그 애에게 그렇게 설명했고, 그 애도 그렇게 이해하고 있네. 더는 아무 말도 듣고 싶지 않네.\"\n\n그가 문 쪽으로 손짓하자, 월터는 그저 고개를 숙이고 물러날 수밖에 없었다. 톡스 양은 선장도 똑같이 하려는 듯이 보이자, 끼어들었다.\n\n\"선생님,\" 그녀가, 그 후한 인심에 자기와 칙 부인이 함께 눈물을 펑펑 흘리고 있던 돔비 씨를 향해 말했다, \"무언가 빠뜨리신 것 같습니다. 용서하세요, 돔비 씨, 제 생각에는, 선생님의 고귀한 성품과 그 드높은 도량 가운데서, 사소한 한 가지를 빠뜨리신 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 톡스 양!\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"저—기구를 가지신 신사분이,\" 톡스 양이 커틀 선장을 흘끗 보며 말을 이었다, \"선생님 팔꿈치 곁 탁자 위에 놓아두고 가신 것이—\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 돔비 씨가, 마치 그것들이 정말로 빵부스러기라도 되는 양 선장의 소지품을 자기에게서 휙 쓸어내며 말했다. \"이것들을 치우시오. 고맙소, 톡스 양. 평소의 그 사려 깊음다우시오. 부디 이것들을 좀 치워 주시오, 선생!\"\n\n커틀 선장은 따르는 것 외에 달리 도리가 없다고 느꼈다. 그러나 손이 닿는 데 수북이 쌓여 있는 보물을 마다하는 돔비 씨의 그 도량에 어찌나 깊이 감동했던지, 찻숟가락과 각설탕 집게를 한쪽 주머니에, 현금을 다른 쪽 주머니에 넣고, 그 커다란 시계를 제 격납고로 천천히 내려 넣고 나서는, 자기의 외짝 왼손으로 그 신사의 오른손을 덥석 붙잡지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 억센 손가락으로 손바닥을 펴서 잡은 채, 감격에 겨워 그 위로 갈고리를 내리찍었다. 이 따뜻한 감정과 차디찬 쇠붙이의 접촉에, 돔비 씨는 온몸을 부르르 떨었다.\n\n그러고 나서 커틀 선장은 매우 우아하고 정중한 태도로 부인들을 향해 여러 차례 갈고리에 입을 맞추어 인사했다. 그러고는 폴과 플로렌스에게 각별히 작별을 고한 뒤, 월터를 데리고 방을 나갔다. 플로렌스가 진심에서 우러난 마음으로 늙은 솔에게 무슨 말을 전하려고 그들을 뒤쫓아 달려가려는데, 돔비 씨가 그 애를 다시 불러 그 자리에 있으라고 일렀다.\n\n\"넌 도무지 돔비답게 되지 않으려는 거니, 얘야!\" 칙 부인이 애처롭게 나무라듯 말했다.\n\n\"고모,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"저한테 화내지 마세요. 저는 아빠가 너무 고마워서 그래요!\"\n\n그 애는, 감히 그럴 수만 있었다면, 달려가 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안았을 것이다. 그러나 감히 그러지 못했으므로, 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있는 그를 향해 감사 어린 눈길을 보냈다. 그는 이따금 그 애에게 불편한 눈길을 던졌지만, 대개는 폴을 지켜보고 있었다. 폴은 게이라는 젊은이가 돈을 갖도록 해 주었다는 새로 갓 피어난 위엄을 띠고 방 안을 거닐고 있었다.\n\n그러면 게이라는 젊은이는—월터는—그는 어찌 되었을까?\n\n그는 늙은이의 화롯가에서 집행관과 중개인들을 몰아내게 된 것에, 그리고 그 기쁜 소식을 가지고 삼촌에게 서둘러 돌아갈 수 있게 된 것에 뛸 듯이 기뻤다. 다음 날 정오 전에 모든 일이 매듭지어지고 정리된 것에, 그리고 저녁이면 작은 뒷거실에 늙은 솔과 커틀 선장과 함께 앉아, 그 기구 제작자가 벌써 생기를 되찾아 앞날에 희망을 품고, 나무로 된 항해사 견습생이 다시 자기 것이 되었음을 느끼는 모습을 보게 된 것에 뛸 듯이 기뻤다. 그러나 돔비 씨에 대한 그의 감사를 조금도 의심할 바 없으면서도, 월터가 풀이 죽고 의기소침해졌다는 것은 인정해야 한다. 우리가 갓 피어나려던 희망이 어떤 거친 바람에 회복할 수 없을 만큼 꺾여 버렸을 때, 만약 그것이 무성히 자랐더라면 어떤 꽃을 피웠을지 그려 보는 데 가장 마음이 기우는 법이다. 그리고 이제, 월터는 새롭고 끔찍한 추락의 깊이만큼 그 위대한 돔비의 높이로부터 동떨어져 있음을 깨닫고, 자기의 그 모든 옛 부질없는 공상이 그 추락 속에서 바람에 흩어져 버렸음을 느끼면서, 그 공상들이 자기를 이끌어, 까마득히 먼 훗날 플로렌스를 사모해 보겠다는 무해한 환상으로 데려갔을지도 모른다는 것을 어렴풋이 의심하기 시작했다.\n\n선장은 이 일을 전혀 다른 시각으로 보았다. 그는 자기가 거든 그 면담이 매우 흡족하고 고무적이어서, 플로렌스를 월터에게 정식으로 약혼시키는 데서 한두 걸음밖에 떨어져 있지 않다고 믿는 듯했다. 또 이번 일이 그 휘팅턴식 희망을 완전히 굳히지는 못했더라도 엄청나게 진척시켰다고 여기는 듯했다. 이런 확신에, 그리고 자기 옛 친구의 기운이 살아난 것에, 또 그로 인한 자기 자신의 흥겨움에 들떠, 그는 심지어, 그날 저녁 세 번째로 그들에게 \"어여쁜 페그\"라는 발라드를 들려주는 자리에서, 즉흥적으로 \"플로렌스\"라는 이름으로 바꿔 부르려고 시도하기까지 했다. 그러나 페그라는 단어가 으레 레그(다리)와 운이 맞는지라(원래 노래의 주인공은 그 신체적 아름다움에 있어 모든 경쟁자를 능가했다고 묘사되어 있었다) 이것이 어렵다는 것을 알고, 그것을 플레-에-에그로 바꾸는 절묘한 묘안을 떠올렸다. 그리하여 그는, 머지않아 그 무시무시한 맥스팅거 부인의 거처를 찾아가야 할 시각이 코앞에 다가와 있었음에도 불구하고, 거의 초자연적이라 할 만한 익살과 자못 우렁찬 목소리로 그렇게 불러 댔다.\n\n바로 그날 저녁, 소령은 자기 클럽에서 시내의 친구 돔비를 화제로 장광설을 늘어놓았다. \"빌어먹을, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"그는 왕족이라니까요, 시내의 내 친구 돔비는요. 내 말 잘 들어 보시오, 선생. 만약 당신들 가운데 이 늙은 조 배그스톡이나 시내의 내 친구 돔비 같은 사람이 몇 명만 더 있었더라면, 선생, 제법 쓸 만했을 거란 말이오!\"\n\n# CHAPTER XI. Paul’s Introduction to a New Scene\n\n핍친 부인의 체질은, 갈비 요리를 먹은 뒤 휴식을 필요로 하고 췌장 요리라는 수면제의 도움 없이는 잠을 청하기 어려운 육체적 약점이 있기는 했으나, 그토록 단단한 금속으로 빚어진 터라 위컴 부인의 예언을 깡그리 무시한 채 쇠약의 기미라고는 도무지 보이지 않았다. 그럼에도 폴이 그 노부인에게 품은 넋 나간 듯한 관심이 조금도 수그러들지 않았으므로, 위컴 부인은 자기가 잡은 입장에서 한 치도 물러서지 않았다. 자기 삼촌의 벳시 제인이라는 든든한 발판 위에 자기를 무장하고 진지를 구축한 채, 그녀는 친구로서 베리 아가씨에게 최악의 경우에 대비하라고 충고했으며, 이모가 화약 공장처럼 언제든 별안간 터져 버릴지 모른다고 미리 일러두었다.\n\n\"바라건대, 베리 아가씨,\" 위컴 부인은 이렇게 말하곤 했다, \"남길 만한 소소한 재산이 무엇이든 있다면 그게 아가씨 차지가 되었으면 해요. 아가씨야말로 그럴 자격이 충분하지요, 정말이지 고된 삶을 사니까요. 하긴 차지할 만한 게 그리 많아 보이지는 않지만—이렇게 터놓고 말하는 걸 용서해요—이 음침한 굴속에서는요.\"\n\n가엾은 베리는 그 모든 말을 좋게 받아들이며 여느 때처럼 죽도록 일하고 시중을 들었다. 핍친 부인이 세상에서 가장 갸륵한 사람 가운데 하나라고 철석같이 믿으며, 날마다 그 고귀한 노파의 제단에 자기 자신을 무수히 제물로 바쳤다. 그러나 베리의 이 모든 희생은 어쩐 영문인지 핍친 부인의 친구들과 찬미자들에 의해 죄다 핍친 부인의 공적으로 돌아갔으며, 고인이 된 핍친 씨가 페루의 광산에서 상심하여 죽었다는 그 우울한 사실과 어우러져 그것을 떠받치는 데 쓰였다.\n\n이를테면 소매업을 하는 정직한 식료품상 겸 잡화상이 하나 있었는데, 그와 핍친 부인 사이에는 기름때 묻은 붉은 표지의 자그마한 외상 장부 한 권이 끊임없이 문제가 되었고, 그 장부에 얽힌 당사자들 사이에서는 복도의 깔개 위에서, 또 응접실에서 문을 닫아건 채 갖가지 은밀한 협의와 회담이 줄곧 벌어지곤 했다. 또 비더스톤 도련님(인도의 뙤약볕이 피에 작용한 탓에 성미가 앙심을 품게 된 아이였다)이 정산되지 않은 잔액에 관해서, 그리고 자기 기억으로 한 번은 차 마시는 시간에 흑설탕 공급에 차질이 빚어졌던 일에 관해서 음울한 암시를 흘리는 일도 없지 않았다. 이 식료품상은 독신이었고 겉모습에서 아름다움을 찾는 사람이 아닌지라 한때 베리에게 정식으로 청혼을 한 적이 있었는데, 핍친 부인은 그것을 모욕과 경멸로써 물리쳤다. 모두가 페루 광산에서 죽은 사람의 미망인인 핍친 부인의 그 처사가 얼마나 칭찬할 만한 일이며, 그 노부인이 얼마나 굳세고 고결하며 독립적인 기개를 지녔는지 입을 모았다. 그러나 여섯 주 동안 울었던(그동안 내내 갸륵한 이모에게 호되게 야단을 맞았다) 가엾은 베리에 대해서는, 그리고 절망적인 노처녀 신세로 떨어진 그녀에 대해서는 아무도 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"베리가 너를 무척 좋아하지, 그렇지 않니?\" 폴이 한번은 고양이와 함께 난롯가에 앉아 있을 때 핍친 부인에게 물었다.\n\n\"그래,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"왜요?\" 폴이 물었다.\n\n\"왜라니!\" 당황한 노부인이 되받았다. \"어떻게 그런 걸 물어보니, 도련님! 너는 왜 네 누나 플로렌스를 좋아하지?\"\n\n\"누나는 아주 착하니까요,\" 폴이 말했다. \"플로렌스 같은 사람은 없어요.\"\n\n\"흥!\" 핍친 부인이 퉁명스레 쏘아붙였다, \"그럼 나 같은 사람도 없을 테지, 아마.\"\n\n\"근데 정말 없어요?\" 폴이 의자에서 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 그녀를 아주 빤히 바라보면서 물었다.\n\n\"없지,\" 노부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그거 다행이에요,\" 폴이 생각에 잠긴 듯 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"그거 아주 잘된 일이에요.\"\n\n핍친 부인은 어째서냐고 차마 묻지 못했다. 무언가 완전히 절멸시켜 버릴 듯한 대답이 돌아올까 두려웠던 것이다. 그러나 상처받은 감정에 대한 보상으로, 그녀는 잠자리에 들 시간까지 비더스톤 도련님을 어찌나 들볶았던지, 그 아이는 바로 그날 밤부터 인도로 육로를 통해 돌아갈 채비를 하기 시작했다. 항해 동안 자기를 지탱해 줄 식량을 비축하는 첫걸음으로, 저녁 식사에서 빵 한 덩이의 사분의 일과 눅눅한 네덜란드 치즈 한 조각을 빼돌려 감춘 것이다.\n\n핍친 부인은 어린 폴과 그 누이를 거의 열두 달 가까이 지켜보며 돌봐 왔다. 그들은 두 번 집에 다녀왔으나 며칠씩에 불과했고, 호텔에 머무는 돔비 씨를 매주 빠짐없이 찾아뵙곤 했다. 조금씩 조금씩 폴은 튼튼해져 갔고 더는 유모차가 없어도 될 만큼 되었다. 그래도 여전히 야위고 가냘파 보였으며, 처음 핍친 부인의 손에 맡겨졌을 때와 똑같이, 예나 다름없는 그 조용하고 꿈꾸는 듯한 아이로 남아 있었다. 어느 토요일 오후 해 질 무렵, 돔비 씨가 핍친 부인을 찾아왔다는 뜻밖의 통보로 '성'에 큰 소동이 일었다. 응접실에 있던 사람들은 즉각 회오리바람의 날개에라도 실린 듯 위층으로 휩쓸려 올라갔고, 침실 문들을 요란하게 쾅쾅 닫고 머리 위에서 우당탕거린 다음, 핍친 부인이 동요한 마음을 달래느라 비더스톤 도련님을 한 차례 쥐어박고 나서, 그 갸륵한 노부인의 검은 봄버진 옷자락이 돔비 씨가 자기 아들이자 후계자의 빈 안락의자를 바라보고 있던 접견실을 어둡게 드리웠다.\n* 봄버진: 견·모 혼방의 검은 상복용 직물.\n\n\"핍친 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"안녕하시오?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다, \"그럭저럭 잘 지내고 있습니다, 형편을 생각하면요.\"\n\n핍친 부인은 늘 그런 식으로 말했다. 그 말은 자기의 미덕이며 희생이며 그런 것들을 생각하면, 이라는 뜻이었다.\n\n\"제가 아주 잘 지내기를 바랄 수야 없지요, 나리,\" 핍친 부인이 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 숨을 고르고 말했다. \"그래도 이만한 건강이나마 감사히 여깁니다.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 분기마다 그만한 돈을 치르는 것이 바로 이런 종류의 것을 위함이라고 느끼는 후원자의 흡족한 태도로 고개를 끄덕였다. 잠시 침묵한 뒤 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"핍친 부인, 제 아들에 관해 의논드리려고 실례를 무릅쓰고 찾아왔습니다. 한동안 그럴 생각을 품고 있었으나, 그 애의 건강이 완전히 회복되도록 하기 위해 차일피일 미루어 왔지요. 그 점에 대해서는 부인께서도 우려가 없으시지요, 핍친 부인?\"\n\n\"브라이턴은 무척 이로운 것으로 드러났습니다, 나리,\" 핍친 부인이 대답했다. \"정말이지 무척 이로웠지요.\"\n\n\"저는,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"그 애를 브라이턴에 계속 두려고 합니다.\"\n\n핍친 부인은 두 손을 비비며 잿빛 눈을 난롯불에 떨구었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 돔비 씨가 집게손가락을 쭉 뻗으며 말을 이었다, \"하지만 이제는 그 애가 변화를 꾀하여, 이곳에서 지금과는 다른 종류의 생활을 해 나가는 편이 좋을 듯합니다. 요컨대, 핍친 부인, 그것이 제가 찾아온 목적입니다. 제 아들이 자라고 있습니다, 핍친 부인. 정말로, 자라고 있어요.\"\n\n돔비 씨가 이 말을 하는 그 의기양양한 태도에는 어딘가 우울한 데가 있었다. 그것은 폴의 어린 시절이 그에게 얼마나 길게 느껴졌는지를, 그리고 그의 희망이 그 아이의 생애의 더 나중 단계에 얼마나 쏠려 있는지를 드러냈다. 연민이란 이토록 오만하고 차가운 사람과 결부시키기에 묘한 말로 보일지 모르나, 그 순간만큼은 그가 연민을 받을 만한 대상으로 보였다.\n\n\"여섯 살이라!\" 돔비 씨가 자기 목깃을 매만지며 말했다—아마도 억누를 수 없는 미소를 감추기 위함이었으리라. 그 미소는 그의 얼굴 표면에 잠시 머무는 것이라기보다는, 머물 곳을 찾지 못해 표면을 스치고 비껴가는 듯했다. \"이런, 우리가 미처 둘러볼 새도 없이 여섯이 열여섯으로 바뀌어 버리겠군요.\"\n\n\"십 년은,\" 동정심이라고는 없는 핍친 부인이 그 단단한 잿빛 눈을 서리처럼 번득이며, 굽은 머리를 음울하게 가로저으며 갈라진 소리로 말했다, \"긴 세월이지요.\"\n\n\"그건 사정에 따라 다르지요,\" 돔비 씨가 되받았다. \"어쨌든, 핍친 부인, 제 아들은 여섯 살이고, 유감스럽게도 학업에서 또래의 많은 아이들에게—아니, 동년배의 많은 아이들에게,\" 돔비 씨는 서리 같은 눈에서 날카로운 반짝임을 보았다고 의심한 것에 얼른 대응하여 말을 고쳤다, \"동년배라는 표현이 더 적절하겠군요. 그러니까, 핍친 부인, 제 아들은 또래보다 뒤처질 것이 아니라 앞서야 합니다. 한참 앞서야지요. 그 애가 올라설 높은 자리가 마련되어 있습니다. 제 아들 앞에 놓인 길에는 우연이나 의심의 여지가 없습니다. 그 애의 인생행로는 그 애가 태어나기도 전에 이미 명확하게 정해지고 닦여 있었습니다. 그런 어린 신사의 교육은 미뤄져서는 안 됩니다. 불완전하게 남겨져서도 안 되고요. 매우 꾸준하고 진지하게 착수해야 합니다, 핍친 부인.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 나리,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다, \"그에 반대되는 말씀은 제가 드릴 게 없습니다.\"\n\n\"부인 같은 양식 있는 분이라면 그러실 수도, 그러실 마음도 없으리라 확신했습니다, 핍친 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 흡족해하며 응수했다.\n\n\"어린아이들을 처음부터 너무 몰아붙여서는 안 된다느니, 살살 꾀어 가며 가르쳐야 한다느니, 어쩌니저쩌니 하는 헛소리가—더 고약한 소리들이—얼마나 많이들 떠도는지요, 나리,\" 핍친 부인이 매부리코를 짜증스레 문지르며 말했다. \"제 시절에는 그런 생각은 입에 올린 적도 없었고, 지금도 그래서는 안 됩니다. 제 의견은 '쉴 새 없이 시켜라'입니다.\"\n\n\"훌륭하신 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 되받았다, \"부인께서는 그 명성을 거저 얻으신 것이 아니로군요. 그리고 핍친 부인, 부디 믿어 주십시오, 저는 부인의 그 탁월한 관리 방식에 더할 나위 없이 만족하며, 저의 변변찮은 칭찬이라도—\" 자기 중요성을 짐짓 깎아내릴 때의 돔비 씨의 거만함은 도를 넘었다—\"무슨 보탬이 될 수 있는 자리라면 언제든 기꺼이 그것을 추천할 것입니다. 저는 블림버 박사의 학교를 생각해 보고 있었습니다, 핍친 부인.\"\n\n\"제 이웃 말씀이군요, 나리?\" 핍친 부인이 말했다. \"그 박사의 학교는 훌륭한 곳으로 알고 있습니다. 무척 엄격하게 운영되고, 아침부터 밤까지 오로지 공부만 한다고 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"게다가 학비가 무척 비싸지요,\" 돔비 씨가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"게다가 학비가 무척 비싸고요, 나리,\" 핍친 부인이 그 사실을 냉큼 받아 되받았다. 마치 그것을 빠뜨린다면 그 학교의 으뜸가는 장점 가운데 하나를 빠뜨리는 셈이라는 듯이.\n\n\"제가 박사와 몇 차례 연락을 주고받았습니다, 핍친 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 의자를 난롯불 쪽으로 조바심치듯 조금 더 끌어당기며 말했다, \"그런데 그분은 폴이 자기 목적에 비추어 너무 어리다고는 전혀 여기지 않으십니다. 거의 같은 나이에 그리스어를 배운 소년들의 사례를 여럿 들어 주셨지요. 이번 변화에 대해 제 마음속에 자그마한 불안이 하나 있다면, 핍친 부인, 그것은 그 점에 관한 것이 아닙니다. 제 아들은 어머니를 알지 못한 탓에 점차 자기의 어린 애정을 너무 많이—지나치게 많이—누이에게 쏟아 왔습니다. 그 둘을 떼어 놓는 것이—\" 돔비 씨는 더 말하지 않고 잠자코 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"원, 별소리를!\" 핍친 부인이 검은 봄버진 치맛자락을 펄럭이며, 자기 안의 식인귀 같은 기질을 한껏 끌어올리며 외쳤다. \"그 애가 그걸 싫어한다면, 돔비 씨, 참고 견디도록 가르쳐야지요.\" 갸륵한 부인은 그렇게 상스러운 표현을 쓴 것을 곧바로 사과했으나, 자기는 아이들을 그런 식으로 타이른다고 (사실대로) 말했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 핍친 부인이 고개를 빳빳이 세웠다 가로저었다 하며 비더스톤이며 팽키 무리를 잔뜩 노려보아 굴복시키는 동작을 마칠 때까지 기다렸다가, 조용히, 그러나 바로잡듯 말했다. \"그 애 말입니다, 훌륭하신 부인, 그 애요.\" [* 핍친 부인이 누이 플로렌스를 가리켜 'she'라 했으나, 돔비 씨는 아들 폴을 두고 한 말임을 정정한 것이다.]\n\n핍친 부인의 방식이라면 폴 쪽의 어떤 불안에 대해서도 거의 똑같은 치료법을 적용했을 터였다. 그러나 그 단단한 잿빛 눈은, 돔비 씨가 딸의 경우에는 그 처방의 효험을 인정할지언정 아들에게는 그것이 만병통치약이 못 된다는 것을 알아챌 만큼 날카로웠으므로, 그녀는 그 점을 따져 들었다. 그러면서 변화와, 새로운 교제와, 블림버 박사의 학교에서 그가 영위할 다른 형태의 생활과, 그가 익혀야 할 학업이 머지않아 충분한 이별의 계기가 되어 줄 것이라고 주장했다. 이것이 돔비 씨 자신의 희망이자 믿음과 맞아떨어졌으므로, 그 신사는 핍친 부인의 식견을 한층 더 높이 평가하게 되었다. 그리고 핍친 부인이 동시에 사랑스러운 어린 친구를 잃게 됨을 애통해했으므로(그것이 그녀에게 그리 큰 충격은 아니었으니, 오래전부터 예상해 온 일인 데다 애초에 그가 석 달 넘게 자기 곁에 머무르리라고는 기대하지도 않았던 까닭이다), 그는 핍친 부인의 사심 없음에 대해서도 똑같이 좋은 평가를 내렸다. 그가 이 문제를 노심초사하며 숙고했음은 분명했으니, 한 가지 계획을 세워 그 식인귀에게 알린 것이다. 곧, 처음 반년 동안은 폴을 박사의 학교에 주간 기숙생으로 보내고, 그동안 플로렌스는 '성'에 남아 토요일마다 그곳에서 동생을 맞이하게 한다는 것이었다. 이렇게 하면 그 애를 차츰차츰 떼어 놓을 수 있으리라고 돔비 씨는 말했다—아마도 예전에 그 애가 차츰차츰 젖을 떼지 못했던 일을 떠올렸는지도 모른다.\n\n돔비 씨는 폴이 브라이턴에서 학업을 이어 가는 동안에도 핍친 부인이 아들의 총괄 감독자이자 관리자로서 계속 그 직책에 머물러 주기를 바란다는 뜻을 표하는 것으로 회견을 마무리했다. 그리고 폴에게 입을 맞추고, 플로렌스와 악수하고, 위풍당당한 옷깃을 두른 비더스톤 도련님을 둘러본 다음, 팽키 아가씨의 머리를 토닥여 그 아이를 울리고(이 부위는 핍친 부인이 손가락 마디로 술통 두드리듯 톡톡 쳐 보는 버릇이 있는 탓에 유난히 아픈 곳이었다) 호텔과 만찬을 향해 물러갔다. 이제 그토록 나이도 들고 건강도 좋아진 폴이 곧장 활기찬 교육 과정을 시작하여, 그가 빛을 발하게 될 그 지위에 걸맞은 자격을 갖추도록 해야 하며, 블림버 박사가 당장 그를 맡아야 한다고 굳게 결심하면서.\n\n블림버 박사에게 맡겨진 어린 신사라면 누구든 꽤나 단단히 쥐어짜이리라 각오하는 편이 좋았다. 박사는 어린 신사를 열 명만 맡았으나, 아무리 적게 잡아도 백 명분의 학식을 늘 비축해 두고 있었다. 그리고 그 불행한 열 명에게 그것을 잔뜩 처넣어 주는 것이야말로 그의 평생의 사업이자 기쁨이었다.\n\n사실 블림버 박사의 학교는 거대한 온실로, 그 안에서는 촉성 장치가 쉴 새 없이 돌아가고 있었다. 모든 소년들이 제철보다 일찍 꽃을 피웠다. 정신의 풋완두가 크리스마스에 산출되었고, 지성의 아스파라거스가 일 년 내내 산출되었다. 수학의 구스베리(그것도 아주 신 것)가 블림버 박사의 재배 아래 철 아닌 때에, 그저 덤불의 새순에 불과한 것에서 흔히 열렸다. 온갖 종류의 그리스어와 라틴어 채소가, 더없이 서리 내린 환경 속에서, 소년이라는 바싹 마른 잔가지에서 거둬졌다. 자연 따위는 전혀 중요하지 않았다. 어떤 어린 신사가 무엇을 맺도록 타고났든, 블림버 박사는 어떻게든 그를 정해진 본보기대로 맺게 만들었다.\n\n이 모든 것이 무척 흐뭇하고 기발했으나, 촉성 재배의 방식에는 으레 따르기 마련인 폐단이 뒤따랐다. 그 설익은 산물들에는 제대로 된 맛이 없었고, 오래 보존되지도 않았다. 게다가 코가 부어오르고 머리가 지나치게 큰 어린 신사 하나(모든 것을 '다 거쳐 낸' 열 명 가운데 가장 나이 든 아이였다)는 어느 날 갑자기 꽃 피우기를 그만두더니 학교 안에 한낱 줄기로만 남고 말았다. 그리고 사람들은 박사가 어린 투츠에게 다소 도를 넘게 했다고, 그래서 그가 구레나룻이 나기 시작할 무렵에는 머리 쓰기를 그만두었다고들 수군거렸다.\n\n어쨌든 어린 투츠는 거기 있었다. 더없이 굵직한 목소리와 더없이 새된 머리를 지닌 채, 셔츠에 장식 핀을 꽂고, 학생들이 산책하러 나갈 때 몰래 새끼손가락에 끼려고 조끼 주머니에 반지를 하나 넣어 두고, 자기 존재를 알 리도 없는 보모들에게 보기만 하면 끊임없이 반하며, 취침 시간 뒤에는 정면 삼층 계단 왼쪽 구석 창문의 작은 쇠창살 너머로 가스등 켜진 세상을 내다보았는데, 그 모습이 마치 높은 데 너무 오래 앉아 있어 지나치게 웃자란 거대한 천사 같았다.\n\n박사는 검은 정장을 차려입은 풍채 좋은 신사로, 무릎께에 끈이 달려 있고 그 아래로 양말을 신고 있었다. 그는 반들반들 윤이 나는 대머리에, 깊은 목소리에, 턱이 어찌나 겹졌는지 그 주름 사이를 대체 어떻게 면도하는지 신기할 지경이었다. 또한 늘 반쯤 감겨 있는 작은 눈 한 쌍과, 늘 반쯤 히죽거림으로 벌어진 입을 지니고 있었는데, 마치 방금 어떤 소년에게 질문을 던져 놓고 그 입에서 나온 말로 그를 잡아내려고 기다리는 듯한 모양새였다. 그리하여 박사가 오른손을 외투 가슴팍에 찔러 넣고, 다른 손은 등 뒤로 한 채, 머리를 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 까딱이며, 긴장한 낯선 이에게 더없이 평범한 말을 건넬 때면, 그것이 마치 스핑크스의 잠언처럼 들려 상대를 옴짝달싹 못 하게 만들었다.\n\n박사의 집은 바다를 마주한 으리으리한 저택이었다. 안은 즐거운 양식이 아니라 오히려 정반대였다. 빛바랜 색의 커튼들은, 그 폭이 인색하고 빈약하여, 풀이 죽은 채 창문 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 있었다. 탁자와 의자들은 마치 셈에 늘어선 숫자처럼 줄지어 치워져 있었다. 격식 차린 방들에는 난롯불이 어찌나 드물게 지펴졌던지 우물처럼 느껴졌고, 손님은 두레박 노릇을 하는 격이었다. 식당은 세상에서 먹고 마시는 일이 일어날 법한 가장 마지막 장소처럼 보였다. 온 집 안을 통틀어 현관의 커다란 시계가 째깍대는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 없었는데, 그 소리는 다락방에까지 들렸다. 그리고 이따금 어린 신사들이 공부하며 내는 둔탁한 구구 소리가, 마치 우울한 비둘기 떼가 모여 웅얼대는 소리처럼 들려왔다.\n\n블림버 양 또한, 비록 날씬하고 우아한 처녀였으나, 그 집의 엄숙함을 부드럽게 해치는 일이라고는 없었다. 블림버 양에게는 가벼운 객쩍음이라고는 없었다. 그녀는 머리를 짧고 부스스하게 유지했으며 안경을 썼다. 죽은 언어들의 무덤을 파헤치는 일로 메마르고 까칠해진 사람이었다. 블림버 양에게 살아 있는 언어 따위는 어림없었다. 언어는 죽어야 했다—완전히 죽어야 했다—그래야 블림버 양이 시체 도굴꾼처럼 그것들을 파냈다.\n\n그녀의 어머니인 블림버 부인은 자신이 학식 있는 사람은 아니었으나 그런 척했고, 그것으로 충분히 통했다. 그녀는 저녁 모임에서, 만일 키케로를 알 수 있었더라면 만족하며 죽을 수 있었으리라 생각한다고 말하곤 했다. 박사의 어린 신사들이 다른 모든 소년들과는 달리 되도록 큼직한 셔츠 깃과 되도록 빳빳한 크라바트를 두르고 산책하러 나가는 모습을 보는 것이 그녀의 한결같은 기쁨이었다. 그게 어찌나 고전적인지 모른다고 그녀는 말했다.\n\n블림버 박사의 조교인 피더 학사로 말하자면, 그는 일종의 인간 손풍금 같은 존재로, 변주라고는 전혀 없이 곡목 몇 가지가 적힌 짧은 목록을 끊임없이 거듭거듭 연주하고 있었다. 운명이 그의 편이었더라면 어쩌면 어린 시절에 다른 곡통으로 교체할 수 있게 장치되었을지도 모르나, 그렇지 못했다. 그에게는 단 하나의 곡통밖에 없었고, 그것으로 단조로운 회전을 거듭하며 블림버 박사네 어린 신사들의 어린 머리를 어리둥절하게 만드는 것이 그의 일이었다. 어린 신사들은 일찌감치 갉아먹는 듯한 근심으로 가득 차 있었다. 그들은 돌처럼 매정한 동사며, 사나운 명사며, 굽힐 줄 모르는 구문 단락이며, 꿈속에까지 나타나는 연습 문제의 유령들을 쫓느라 한시도 쉬지 못했다. 그 촉성 재배의 방식 아래에서, 어린 신사는 보통 삼 주 만에 기운을 작별했다. 석 달이면 세상의 온갖 근심을 머리에 짊어졌다. 넉 달이면 부모나 후견인에 대해 쓰디쓴 감정을 품게 되었고, 다섯 달이면 늙은 염세가가 되었으며, 여섯 달이면 땅속 그 복된 피난처를 차지한 쿠르티우스를 부러워했다. 그리고 첫 일 년이 끝날 무렵에는 그 뒤로 결코 벗어나지 못할 결론에 다다랐으니, 곧 시인들의 온갖 공상이며 현인들의 가르침이란 한낱 낱말과 문법의 모음에 불과하며 세상에 다른 뜻이라고는 없다는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그러는 내내 그는 박사의 온실 안에서 줄곧 꽃을 피우고 또 피웠다. 그리고 그가 그 겨울 동안 자란 결실을 친척과 친구들에게로 가져갈 때면 박사의 영광과 명성은 드높았다.\n* 쿠르티우스: 로마 전설 속 마르쿠스 쿠르티우스. 갈라진 땅속 심연에 스스로 뛰어들어 나라를 구한 청년.\n\n어느 날 박사네 집 현관 계단 위에, 폴은 두근거리는 가슴으로, 자그마한 오른손을 아버지의 손에 맡긴 채 서 있었다. 그의 다른 손은 플로렌스의 손에 꼭 쥐여 있었다. 한쪽의 그 자그마한 쥐임은 얼마나 꼭 죄어 있었으며, 다른 한쪽은 얼마나 느슨하고 차가웠던지!\n\n핍친 부인이 그 검은 깃털과 매부리 같은 부리를 하고서, 흉조처럼 그 제물 뒤를 맴돌았다. 그녀는 숨이 차 있었다—돔비 씨가 큰 생각들로 가득 차 빠르게 걸어온 탓이다—그리고 문이 열리기를 기다리며 쉰 소리로 갈갈댔다.\n\n\"자, 폴,\" 돔비 씨가 의기양양하게 말했다. \"이것이야말로 돔비와 아들이 되어 돈을 벌어들이는 길이란다. 너는 벌써 거의 어엿한 사내가 다 되었어.\"\n\n\"거의요,\" 아이가 되받았다.\n\n그 어린아이다운 동요조차도, 그 대답에 곁들인 그 능청맞고 별스러우면서도 가슴 뭉클한 표정을 누르지는 못했다.\n\n그것은 돔비 씨의 얼굴에 막연한 불만의 빛을 떠오르게 했으나, 문이 열리는 바람에 그 빛은 이내 사라졌다.\n\n\"블림버 박사 댁에 계시지요, 아마?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n하인은 그렇다고 답했다. 그러고는 그들이 들어설 때, 마치 폴이 자그마한 생쥐이고 이 집은 덫이기라도 한 듯 그를 바라보았다. 그는 눈이 약한 젊은이로, 그 얼굴에는 막 동트는 새벽의 첫 희미한 줄기처럼 히죽거림의 기미가 어려 있었다. 그것은 한낱 멍청함이었으나, 핍친 부인은 그것을 건방진 짓이라고 단정하고 즉각 그를 향해 딱 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"어떻게 감히 신사분 등 뒤에서 웃는 게냐?\" 핍친 부인이 말했다. \"그래, 나를 뭘로 보는 거냐?\"\n\n\"저는 아무도 비웃지 않았고, 정말이지 마님을 무엇으로도 본 적이 없습니다요,\" 젊은이가 당황하여 되받았다.\n\n\"빈둥대는 개 같은 것들 같으니!\" 핍친 부인이 말했다, \"고작 개 쳇바퀴나 돌릴 주제에. 가서 네 주인에게 돔비 씨가 오셨다고 일러라, 안 그러면 네 신세가 더 고약해질 테니!\"\n\n* 개 쳇바퀴(turnspit): 옛날 부엌에서 개를 쳇바퀴에 넣어 돌리며 고기 굽는 꼬챙이를 돌리게 한 데서 온 천한 일거리.\n\n눈이 약한 젊은이는 무척 고분고분하게 그 심부름을 하러 갔고, 이내 돌아와 그들을 박사의 서재로 안내했다.\n\n\"또 웃는구나, 이놈,\" 핍친 부인이 맨 뒤를 따라가며 현관에서 그의 곁을 지나칠 차례가 되자 말했다.\n\n\"안 웃었습니다요,\" 젊은이가 몹시 짓눌린 채 되받았다. \"이런 일은 난생처음 봅니다요!\"\n\n\"무슨 일이오, 핍친 부인?\" 돔비 씨가 돌아보며 말했다. \"조용히! 제발요!\"\n핍친 부인은 그 공손함을 지키느라, 지나가면서 그저 젊은이를 향해 중얼거리며 \"오! 참으로 귀하신 몸이지\"라고만 했다—그리고 온통 고분고분함과 무능함 덩어리인 그 젊은이를, 그 일로 눈물이 다 날 지경으로 만들어 놓고 가 버렸다. 그러나 핍친 부인에게는 온순한 사람들에게는 죄다 시비를 거는 버릇이 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 친구들은, 페루 광산 일을 겪었으니 그게 뭐 놀랄 일이냐고들 했다!\n\n박사는 그 위압적인 서재에 앉아 있었는데, 양 무릎께에 지구의가 하나씩 놓이고, 사방에 책이 둘러 있고, 문 위에는 호메로스가, 벽난로 선반 위에는 미네르바가 있었다. \"그래, 안녕하시오, 나리?\" 그가 돔비 씨에게 말했다, \"그래, 우리 어린 친구는 어떤가?\" 박사의 말투는 오르간처럼 장중했다. 그가 말을 멈추자, 현관의 큰 시계가 (적어도 폴에게는) 그 말을 이어받아 \"어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는? 어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는?\" 하고 거듭거듭, 한없이 되뇌는 듯했다.\n\n어린 친구는 너무 자그마하여 박사가 앉은 자리에서는 탁자 위 책 너머로 도무지 보이지가 않았으므로, 박사는 책상다리 옆으로 돌아 그를 보려고 몇 차례 헛된 시도를 했다. 이를 알아챈 돔비 씨가, 폴을 팔로 안아 올려 방 한가운데 박사 맞은편의 또 다른 자그마한 탁자 위에 앉혀 줌으로써 박사를 그 난처함에서 구해 주었다.\n\n\"하!\" 박사가 손을 가슴팍에 찌른 채 의자에 등을 기대며 말했다. \"이제 우리 어린 친구가 보이는군. 안녕한가, 우리 어린 친구?\"\n\n현관의 시계는 이렇게 말의 형태가 바뀐 것에는 동의하지 않은 채, 여전히 \"어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는? 어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는?\" 하고 되뇌기를 그치지 않았다.\n\n\"아주 잘 지냅니다, 고맙습니다, 선생님,\" 폴이 되받았다. 박사 못지않게 그 시계에게도 대답을 하면서.\n\n\"하!\" 블림버 박사가 말했다. \"이 아이를 어엿한 사내로 만들어 볼까요?\"\n\n\"듣고 있니, 폴?\" 돔비 씨가 덧붙였다. 폴이 잠자코 있었던 까닭이다.\n\n\"이 아이를 어엿한 사내로 만들어 볼까요?\" 박사가 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"저는 어린아이로 있는 게 더 좋아요,\" 폴이 대답했다.\n\n\"허!\" 박사가 말했다. \"어째서지?\"\n\n아이는 탁자 위에 앉아 그를 바라보았는데, 그 얼굴에는 억눌린 감정이 묘하게 어려 있었고, 한 손으로는 마치 그 아래에 솟구치는 눈물이 있어 그것을 으스러뜨리기라도 하는 듯 자기 무릎을 자랑스레 두드렸다. 그러나 그러는 동안 다른 손은 조금씩, 조금 더 멀리—그에게서 더더욱 멀리—더듬어 가더니, 마침내 플로렌스의 목에 가 닿았다. \"이게 그 까닭이에요,\" 하고 그 손이 말하는 듯했다. 그러더니 그 차분한 표정이 무너져 사라지고, 떨리는 입술이 풀리며, 눈물이 줄줄 쏟아져 나왔다.\n\n\"핍친 부인,\" 그의 아버지가 짜증스러운 투로 말했다, \"이런 모습을 보게 되어 정말이지 무척 유감입니다.\"\n\n\"그 애한테서 떨어져요, 어서요, 돔비 아가씨,\" 그 노부인이 말했다.\n\n\"괜찮습니다,\" 박사가 핍친 부인을 막느라 부드럽게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"괜찮아요. 머지않아 새로운 근심거리와 새로운 인상으로 채워 줄 테니까요, 돔비 씨. 그래도 우리 어린 친구가 익히기를 여전히 바라시는 것은—\"\n\n\"전부입니다, 부디, 박사님,\" 돔비 씨가 단호하게 되받았다.\n\n\"네,\" 박사가 말했다. 반쯤 감긴 눈과 여느 때의 미소로, 마치 자기가 박제로 만들려는 어떤 진귀한 작은 짐승을 살펴보는 듯한 흥미를 띠고 폴을 훑어보면서. \"네, 바로 그렇지요. 하! 우리 어린 친구에게 아주 다양한 지식을 전수하여, 분명 빠르게 진보시킬 것입니다. 분명히요. 완전히 처녀지라고 하셨지요, 돔비 씨, 아마?\"\n\n\"집에서, 그리고 이 부인에게서 받은 평범한 준비를 빼면 그렇습니다,\" 돔비 씨가 핍친 부인을 소개하며 대답했다. 그러자 그녀는 즉각 온몸의 근육을 뻣뻣하게 굳히고는, 박사가 자기를 깎아내릴 경우에 대비하여 미리부터 콧방귀로 도전장을 내밀었다. \"그 정도를 빼면, 폴은 아직껏 어떤 학업에도 손을 댄 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n블림버 박사는 핍친 부인이 한 정도의 그 하찮은 밀렵을 너그러이 봐주는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕이고는, 그 말을 들으니 기쁘다고 했다. 두 손을 비비며 그가 말하기를, 기초부터 시작하는 것이 훨씬 만족스럽다는 것이었다. 그러고는 다시 폴을 곁눈질했는데, 마치 그 자리에서 당장 그리스어 알파벳으로 그를 붙들고 늘어지고 싶기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n\"실은 그 사정만으로도, 블림버 박사님,\" 돔비 씨가 자기 어린 아들을 흘낏 보며 말을 이었다, \"그리고 제가 이미 박사님과 나누는 기쁨을 누렸던 그 회견만으로도, 더 이상의 설명이, 따라서 박사님의 귀중한 시간에 대한 더 이상의 침범이 너무도 불필요해진지라—\"\n\n\"자, 돔비 아가씨!\" 시큼한 핍친 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐만요,\" 박사가 말했다, \"잠시만요. 제 아내와 딸을 소개하게 해 주십시오. 파르나소스를 향한 우리 어린 순례자의 가정생활에 함께하게 될 사람들입니다. 블림버 부인이오,\" 마침 대기하고 있었던 듯한 그 부인이 때맞춰 들어섰고, 그 뒤로 안경 쓴 그 어여쁜 묘지기인 딸이 따라 들어왔다, \"돔비 씨일세. 제 딸 코닐리아입니다, 돔비 씨. 돔비 씨는, 여보,\" 박사가 아내 쪽으로 돌아서며 말을 이었다, \"이렇게도 우리를 믿어 주셔서—우리 어린 친구가 보이시오?\"\n\n블림버 부인은 돔비 씨를 향한 지나친 공손함에 빠져 있던 나머지, 어린 친구가 보이지 않았던 모양이다. 그녀가 어린 친구 쪽으로 뒷걸음질을 치는 바람에 탁자 위 그의 자리가 몹시 위태로워졌으니 말이다. 그러나 그 귀띔에 그녀는 돌아서서 그의 고전적이고 지적인 이목구비를 감탄하며 바라보았고, 다시 돔비 씨 쪽으로 돌아서더니, 한숨을 쉬며 그 사랑스러운 아드님이 부럽다고 말했다.\n\n\"꼭 한 마리 벌 같답니다, 나리,\" 블림버 부인이 눈을 치켜뜨며 말했다, \"더없이 빼어난 꽃들이 핀 정원으로 막 뛰어들어, 그 단꿀을 처음으로 맛보려는 벌이지요—베르길리우스, 호라티우스, 오비디우스, 테렌티우스, 플라우투스, 키케로. 여기 얼마나 꿀이 넘쳐 나는 세계가 펼쳐져 있는지요. 아내 된 자가—그것도 이런 남편을 둔 아내가—이런 말을 하는 게 기이하게 보일는지 모르나—\"\n\n\"쉿, 쉿,\" 블림버 박사가 말했다. \"원, 부끄럽지도 않소.\"\n\n\"돔비 씨께서야 아내의 편애를 용서해 주시겠지요,\" 블림버 부인이 사람을 끄는 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 \"천만에요\"라고 답했다. 짐작건대 그 말은 용서가 아니라 편애 쪽에 갖다 붙인 것이리라.\n\n\"그리고 어미 된 자가 하는 말로도 기이하게 보일는지 모르나,\" 블림버 부인이 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그것도 이런 어머니께서요,\" 돔비 씨가 코닐리아에게 무언가 어렴풋이 찬사를 보낸다는 생각으로 고개를 숙이며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 정말이지,\" 블림버 부인이 말을 이었다, \"만일 제가 키케로를 알 수 있었더라면, 그의 벗이 되어 투스쿨룸의 그 은거지에서 그와 이야기를 나눌 수 있었더라면(아—름다운 투스쿨룸!), 저는 만족하며 죽을 수 있었으리라 생각합니다.\"\n\n학문에 대한 열광이란 어찌나 전염성이 강한지, 돔비 씨는 자기 경우도 바로 그러하다고 반쯤 믿게 되었다. 그리고 앞서 보았듯 대체로 너그러운 성미가 못 되는 핍친 부인조차도, 신음과 한숨 사이의 자그마한 소리를 토해 냈는데, 마치 페루 광산의 그 실패 앞에서 키케로 말고는 그 누구도 변치 않는 위안이 되어 줄 수 없었으리라고, 정녕 그라면 피난처의 데이비 안전등 같은 존재가 되었으리라고 말하려는 듯했다.\n* 데이비 안전등: 험프리 데이비가 발명한 광산용 안전등. 갱내의 폭발을 막아 주는 빛이라는 뜻.\n\n코닐리아는 안경 너머로 돔비 씨를 바라보았는데, 마치 그 화제의 권위자인 키케로에서 인용구 몇 마디를 그와 주고받고 싶기라도 한 듯했다. 그러나 그녀가 그런 의도를 품었다 하더라도, 그것은 방문을 두드리는 노크 소리에 가로막혀 좌절되고 말았다.\n\n\"누구지?\" 박사가 말했다. \"오! 들어오게, 투츠. 들어와. 돔비 씨일세.\" 투츠가 절을 했다. \"참으로 묘한 우연이로군요!\" 블림버 박사가 말했다. \"여기 시작과 끝이 다 모였습니다. 알파와 오메가지요. 우리 학교의 수석 학생입니다, 돔비 씨.\"\n\n박사는 그를 머리와 어깨까지 큰 학생이라 불러도 좋았을 것이다. 그가 적어도 그만큼은 다른 누구보다도 키가 컸기 때문이다. 그는 낯선 이들 틈에 끼인 것을 알고 몹시 얼굴을 붉히며 소리 내어 킥킥거렸다.\n\n\"우리 작은 학당에 한 명이 더 늘었네, 투츠,\" 박사가 말했다. \"돔비 씨의 아드님일세.\"\n\n어린 투츠는 다시 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그러고는 좌중에 감도는 엄숙한 침묵으로 미루어 자기가 무언가 말을 해야 할 처지임을 알아채고, 폴에게 \"안녕?\" 하고 말했는데, 그 목소리가 어찌나 굵고 그 태도가 어찌나 머쓱하던지, 양 한 마리가 으르렁댔다 해도 그보다 더 놀랍지는 않았을 것이다.\n\n\"피더 선생에게 좀 일러 주게, 투츠,\" 박사가 말했다, \"돔비 씨 아드님을 위해 입문용 책을 몇 권 마련하고, 공부하기 편한 자리를 하나 배정해 달라고 말일세. 여보, 돔비 씨께서 아직 기숙사를 못 보신 것 같소.\"\n\n\"돔비 씨께서 위층으로 올라와 주신다면,\" 블림버 부인이 말했다, \"잠의 신이 다스리는 영토를 보여 드리게 되어 더없는 영광이겠습니다.\"\n\n그 말과 함께, 매우 상냥하고 깡마른 몸매에, 하늘색 천으로 지은 모자를 쓴 블림버 부인이 돔비 씨와 코닐리아를 데리고 위층으로 올라갔다. 핍친 부인이 그 뒤를 따르며, 자기 원수인 그 하인을 매섭게 살피고 다녔다.\n\n그들이 나가 있는 동안, 폴은 탁자 위에 앉아 플로렌스의 손을 잡은 채, 박사에게서 방 안 구석구석으로 겁먹은 듯 눈길을 옮겨 다녔다. 한편 박사는 여느 때처럼 손을 가슴팍에 찌른 채 의자에 등을 기대고, 책을 팔 길이만큼 멀찍이 들고서 읽었다. 그 책 읽는 방식에는 어딘가 무척 무시무시한 데가 있었다. 그토록 단호하고, 무덤덤하고, 굽힐 줄 모르고, 냉혈한 같은 식의 일 처리였다. 그 방식은 박사의 얼굴을 고스란히 드러내 보였다. 그리하여 박사가 자기 저자를 향해 미심쩍은 듯 미소 짓거나, 미간을 찌푸리거나, 고개를 가로저으며 \"그런 소리 마시오, 선생; 나는 더 잘 안다오\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯 그를 향해 얼굴을 찡그릴 때면, 그 광경은 가공할 만했다.\n\n투츠 역시 문밖에 머물며 보란 듯이 자기 회중시계의 톱니바퀴를 들여다보고 자기 반 크라운짜리 동전들을 세고 있을 까닭이 없었다. 그러나 그것도 오래가지 못했다. 블림버 박사가 마침 일어서려는 듯 그 통통하게 죈 다리의 자세를 바꾸자, 투츠는 잽싸게 사라져 다시는 나타나지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n이내 돔비 씨와 그 안내자가 줄곧 이야기를 나누며 다시 아래층으로 내려오는 소리가 들렸고, 곧이어 그들은 박사의 서재로 다시 들어섰다.\n\n\"바라건대, 돔비 씨,\" 박사가 책을 내려놓으며 말했다, \"이 조처들이 마음에 드셨으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"더할 나위 없습니다, 박사님,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지 꽤 괜찮군요,\" 핍친 부인이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 결코 지나친 격려를 보낼 마음은 없는 그녀였다.\n\n\"핍친 부인이,\" 돔비 씨가 휙 돌아서며 말했다, \"박사님과 블림버 부인의 허락을 얻어, 이따금 폴을 찾아오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"핍친 부인께서 원하실 때면 언제든지요,\" 박사가 말했다.\n\n\"언제든 반갑게 맞이하겠습니다,\" 블림버 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"제 생각에는,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"끼쳐야 할 폐는 다 끼친 듯하니, 이만 작별하는 게 좋겠군요. 폴, 내 아들,\" 그는 탁자 위에 앉아 있는 아이에게 가까이 다가갔다. \"잘 있거라.\"\n\n\"안녕히 가세요, 아빠.\"\n\n돔비 씨가 자기 손에 쥔 그 맥없고 무심한 자그마한 손은, 그 애틋한 얼굴과는 유난히도 어울리지 않았다. 그러나 그 슬픈 표정에 그는 아무런 몫이 없었다. 그것은 그를 향한 것이 아니었다. 아니, 아니었다. 플로렌스를 향한 것이었다—온통 플로렌스를 향한 것이었다.\n\n만일 돔비 씨가 그 부의 오만함으로 인해, 좀처럼 누그러지지 않고 그 증오에 있어 잔인하게 앙심을 품는 어떤 원수를 만든 적이 있었다면, 그런 원수조차도 그 순간 그의 오만한 가슴을 쥐어짠 그 고통을, 자기가 입은 상처에 대한 보상으로 흡족히 받아들였을 것이다.\n\n그는 자기 아이 위로 몸을 굽혀 입을 맞추었다. 그렇게 하는 동안 그의 시야가, 잠시 그 자그마한 얼굴을 흐릿하게 가려 또렷이 보이지 않게 만든 무언가로 인해 침침해졌다면, 그 짧은 동안만은 어쩌면 그의 마음의 시야가 도리어 더 맑았는지도 모른다.\n\n\"곧 보자꾸나, 폴. 알다시피 토요일과 일요일에는 자유로우니까.\"\n\n\"네, 아빠,\" 폴이 누이를 바라보며 되받았다. \"토요일과 일요일에요.\"\n\n\"그리고 여기서 많은 걸 배우려고 애써서, 영리한 사내가 되어야지,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"그러겠지?\"\n\n\"애써 볼게요,\" 아이가 지친 듯 되받았다.\n\n\"그러면 너도 이제 곧 어른이 되겠구나!\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 아주 곧이요!\" 아이가 대답했다. 다시 한번 그 오래되고 오래된 표정이 야릇한 빛처럼 그의 이목구비를 빠르게 스쳐 갔다. 그것은 핍친 부인에게 내려앉았다가, 그녀의 검은 옷 속에서 꺼지고 말았다. 그 갸륵한 식인귀는 작별을 고하고 플로렌스를 데려가려고 앞으로 나섰는데, 그것은 그녀가 오래도록 갈망해 온 일이었다. 그녀의 그 움직임이, 폴에게 눈길을 고정하고 있던 돔비 씨를 일깨웠다. 그는 폴의 머리를 토닥이고 그 자그마한 손을 다시 한번 꼭 쥔 다음, 여느 때의 그 정중한 냉랭함으로 블림버 박사와 블림버 부인과 블림버 양에게 작별을 고하고 서재를 걸어 나갔다.\n\n자기를 배웅하느라 자리에서 일어설 생각일랑 하지 말아 달라는 그의 간청에도 불구하고, 블림버 박사와 블림버 부인과 블림버 양은 모두 앞다투어 그를 현관까지 배웅하러 나섰다. 그리하여 핍친 부인은 블림버 양과 박사 틈에 얽혀 들고 말았고, 미처 플로렌스를 붙잡기도 전에 서재 밖으로 떠밀려 나갔다. 폴은 그 다행스러운 우연 덕분에, 플로렌스가 그의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안으려고 도로 달려왔던 것, 그리고 문간에서 마지막으로 보인 얼굴이 그녀의 얼굴이었던 것을, 그 소중한 추억으로 두고두고 간직하게 되었다. 그 얼굴은 그를 향해, 눈물 사이로 빛나기에 더더욱 환한 격려의 미소를 띤 채 돌려져 있었다.\n\n그 얼굴이 사라지자 그의 어린 가슴은 들먹이며 부풀어 올랐다. 그리고 지구의들과 책들과 눈먼 호메로스와 미네르바가 방 안을 빙글빙글 헤엄치게 만들었다. 그러나 그것들은 별안간 멈추었다. 그러고 나서 그는 현관의 그 큰 시계가 여전히 장중하게 \"어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는? 어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는?\" 하고 전처럼 묻고 있는 소리를 들었다.\n\n그는 두 손을 모은 채 자기 받침대 위에 앉아 잠자코 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 그는 이렇게 대답할 수도 있었으리라. \"지쳤어요, 지쳤어! 너무 외로워요, 너무 슬퍼요!\" 그리고 거기, 어린 가슴속에 쑤시는 듯한 공허함을 안고서, 바깥의 모든 것이 그토록 차갑고, 헐벗고, 낯선 가운데, 폴은 마치 가구 하나 없이 텅 빈 삶을 떠맡았는데 가구장이는 영영 오지 않을 사람처럼 앉아 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XII. Paul’s Education\n\n탁자 위에 앉은 어린 폴 돔비에게는 한없이 긴 시간처럼 느껴진 몇 분이 흐른 뒤, 블림버 박사가 돌아왔다. 박사의 걸음걸이는 위엄이 있었고, 어린 마음에 엄숙한 감정을 새겨 넣도록 계산된 것이었다. 그것은 일종의 행진이었다. 다만 박사가 오른발을 내디딜 때면 엄숙하게 자기 축을 중심으로 왼쪽을 향해 반원을 그리며 돌았고, 왼발을 내디딜 때면 같은 식으로 오른쪽을 향해 돌았다. 그래서 그는 한 걸음 한 걸음 옮길 때마다 주위를 두루 둘러보는 것 같았으니, 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"어느 방향으로든, 내가 알지 못하는 주제를 누구든 친절히 일러 줄 수 있겠는가? 아무래도 없을 것 같군.\"\n\n블림버 부인과 블림버 양이 박사를 따라 함께 들어왔다. 그리고 박사는 자기의 새 제자를 탁자에서 들어 내려 블림버 양에게 넘겨주었다.\n\n\"코닐리아,\" 박사가 말했다, \"돔비는 처음에 네가 맡도록 해라. 가르쳐 놓거라, 코닐리아, 가르쳐 놓아.\"\n\n블림버 양은 자기의 어린 피후견인을 박사의 손에서 받았다. 폴은 그 안경이 자기를 살펴보고 있음을 느끼고 눈을 내리깔았다.\n\n\"몇 살이니, 돔비?\" 블림버 양이 말했다.\n\n\"여섯 살이에요,\" 폴이 대답하며, 그 아가씨를 슬쩍 훔쳐보고는, 어째서 그녀의 머리는 플로렌스처럼 길게 자라지 않았는지, 어째서 그녀가 사내아이 같은지 의아해했다.\n\n\"라틴어 문법은 얼마나 아니, 돔비?\" 블림버 양이 말했다.\n\n\"하나도 몰라요,\" 폴이 대답했다. 그 대답이 블림버 양의 감수성에 충격을 주었음을 느끼고, 그는 자기를 내려다보고 있는 세 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 몸이 좋지 않았거든요. 저는 허약한 아이였어요. 매일 밖에서 늙은 글럽 할아버지랑 같이 있을 때는 라틴어 문법을 배울 수가 없었어요. 괜찮으시면, 늙은 글럽 할아버지더러 저를 보러 와 달라고 해 주셨으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 끔찍하게 천한 이름이람,\" 블림버 부인이 말했다. \"고전과는 거리가 멀어도 한참 멀구나! 그 괴물 같은 자가 누구냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"무슨 괴물이요?\" 폴이 물었다.\n\n\"글럽 말이다,\" 블림버 부인이 몹시 못마땅한 투로 말했다.\n\n\"그 할아버지는 부인보다 더 괴물도 아니에요,\" 폴이 응수했다.\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 박사가 무시무시한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"어, 어, 어? 아하! 그게 무슨 소리냐?\"\n\n폴은 끔찍이 겁이 났다. 그래도 그는 자리에 없는 글럽을 위해 버티고 섰다. 떨면서이긴 했지만.\n\n\"그분은 아주 좋은 할아버지예요, 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"제 침상을 끌어 주곤 하셨어요. 깊은 바다에 대해서도, 그 안에 사는 물고기에 대해서도, 햇볕을 쬐러 바위에 나와 누워 있다가 놀라면 다시 물속으로 뛰어드는, 그러면서 어찌나 콧김을 내뿜고 물보라를 일으키는지 몇 마일 밖에서도 들리는 커다란 괴물들에 대해서도 다 아세요. 어떤 생물들은요,\" 폴이 자기 화제에 신이 나서 말했다, \"몇 야드나 되는지 모를 만큼 길고, 이름은 잊어버렸지만 플로렌스는 알아요, 그것들이 괴로운 척하다가, 어떤 사람이 가엾어서 가까이 다가가면 커다란 아가리를 벌리고 덤벼들어요. 하지만 그럴 때 해야 할 일은요,\" 폴이 다른 누구도 아닌 박사 본인에게 이 정보를 대담하게 내놓으며 말했다, \"도망가면서 계속 방향을 트는 거예요. 그러면 그것들은 몸이 너무 길어서 굽힐 수가 없으니까 천천히 돌게 되고, 그래서 분명히 그것들을 따돌릴 수 있어요. 그리고 늙은 글럽 할아버지는 어째서 바다가 저를 돌아가신 엄마 생각이 나게 하는지, 또 바다가 늘 무어라고 하는 그 말이—늘 무어라고 하는데!—무엇인지는 모르시지만, 바다에 대해 아주 많이 아세요. 그래서 저는요,\" 아이는 갑자기 얼굴빛이 흐려지고 생기가 꺾이며, 그 낯선 세 얼굴을 마치 버림받은 사람처럼 올려다보면서 말을 맺었다, \"늙은 글럽 할아버지가 여기 와서 저를 봐 주게 해 주셨으면 좋겠어요. 저는 그분을 아주 잘 알고, 그분도 저를 아시니까요.\"\n\n\"하!\" 박사가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"이건 좋지 않군, 그러나 공부가 많은 것을 해 줄 게다.\"\n\n블림버 부인은 몸을 살짝 떠는 듯한 기색으로, 그 아이가 영문을 알 수 없는 아이라고 단언했다. 그러고는 얼굴 생김새의 차이를 감안하면, 펩친 부인이 늘 그를 바라보던 것과 거의 똑같은 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"집 안을 한 바퀴 데리고 다니거라, 코닐리아,\" 박사가 말했다, \"그래서 이 아이를 자기의 새 영역에 익숙해지게 하여라. 저 아가씨와 함께 가거라, 돔비.\"\n\n돔비는 따랐다. 난해한 코닐리아에게 손을 내맡기고, 함께 걸어가는 동안 머뭇거리는 호기심으로 그녀를 곁눈질하면서. 그녀의 안경은 알의 번들거림 때문에 그녀를 어찌나 신비롭게 만드는지, 그는 그녀가 어디를 보고 있는지 알 수가 없었고, 정말이지 그 뒤에 눈이 있기는 한 것인지조차 영 확신이 서지 않았다.\n\n코닐리아는 우선 그를 교실로 데려갔다. 교실은 현관홀 뒤쪽에 자리 잡고 있었고, 어린 신사들의 목소리를 죽이고 누그러뜨리는 베이즈 천을 댄 문 두 짝을 거쳐 들어가게 되어 있었다. 그곳에는 정신적으로 갖가지 단계의 쇠진에 빠진 여덟 명의 어린 신사들이 있었는데, 다들 무척 열심히 매달려 있었고 정말이지 무척 엄숙했다. 고참인 투츠는 한쪽 구석에 자기만의 책상을 차지하고 있었다. 그 뒤에 앉은 그는, 폴의 어린 눈에는 어마어마하게 나이 많은 당당한 사내로 보였다.\n\n다른 작은 책상에 앉은 피더 학사는 자기의 베르길리우스 음전을 잡아당겨 놓고, 그 가락을 네 명의 어린 신사들에게 천천히 갈아 내고 있었다. 나머지 넷 가운데 둘은 발작하듯 이마를 움켜쥔 채 수학 문제를 푸는 데 몰두해 있었고, 하나는 하도 많이 울어 얼굴이 더러운 창문처럼 된 채 저녁 식사 전까지 가망 없이 많은 행을 어떻게든 헤쳐 나가려 애쓰고 있었으며, 하나는 돌처럼 굳은 망연자실과 절망에 빠져 자기 과제를 바라보며 앉아 있었는데—그 모습은 아침 식사 시간 이후로 줄곧 그의 상태였던 것 같았다.\nBitherstone.\n\n새로 온 아이의 등장은 기대했을 법한 소동을 전혀 일으키지 않았다. 피더 선생은(시원하라고 머리를 박박 미는 버릇이 있어서 잔털만 까칠하게 남아 있었다, 문학사 학위 소지자였다) 그에게 뼈마디 굵은 손을 내밀며 만나서 반갑다고 말했다—폴로서는 조금이라도 진심을 담아 그렇게 말할 수만 있었다면 자기도 그 말을 무척 기쁘게 했을 것이었다. 그런 다음 폴은 코닐리아의 지시에 따라 피더 선생의 책상에 있는 네 명의 어린 신사들과 악수했고, 그다음에는 문제 풀이에 열중하느라 무척 열이 오른 두 어린 신사와, 그다음에는 시간에 쫓겨 작업하느라 잉크투성이가 된 어린 신사와, 마지막으로 멍한 상태에 빠진 어린 신사와 악수했는데, 그 아이는 흐물흐물하고 아주 차가웠다.\n\n폴은 이미 투츠에게 소개된 터라, 그 학생은 그저 평소 버릇대로 낄낄거리며 거친 숨을 내쉬고는 자기가 하던 일을 계속했다. 그것은 고된 일은 아니었다. 그가 (한 가지 이상의 의미에서) 너무 많은 것을 \"거쳐 온\" 데다, 앞서 넌지시 비쳤듯이 한창때에 성장을 멈춘 까닭에, 투츠는 이제 제 나름의 공부를 추구해도 좋다는 허락을 받았던 것이다. 그 공부란 주로 명망 있는 인사들로부터 자기 자신에게 긴 편지를 쓰고 \"브라이턴, 서식스, P. 투츠 귀하\"라고 덧붙인 다음, 그것을 책상 속에 아주 소중히 간직하는 일이었다.\n\n이 의례가 다 끝나자, 코닐리아는 폴을 데리고 집 꼭대기까지 위층으로 올라갔다. 폴이 다음 계단을 오르기 전에 매번 두 발을 한 계단에 디뎌야 했던 탓에 다소 더딘 여정이었다. 그래도 마침내 그들은 여정의 끝에 다다랐다. 그리고 거기, 거친 바다를 내려다보는 앞쪽 방에서, 코닐리아는 그에게 흰 휘장이 드리워진 자그마하고 아담한 침대를 보여 주었는데, 창문 바로 곁에 놓인 그 침대에는 이미 카드에 둥근 정자체로 아름답게 쓰인 글씨가—내려긋는 획은 아주 굵게, 올려긋는 획은 아주 가늘게—돔비라고 적혀 있었다. 한편 같은 방에 있는 다른 두 개의 작은 침대는 같은 방식으로 각각 브릭스와 토저에게 속한 것임을 알리고 있었다.\n\n그들이 다시 아래층 현관에 내려서자마자, 폴은 핍친 부인에게 그토록 치명적인 모욕을 안겨 주었던 눈이 약한 젊은이가 갑자기 아주 큰 북채를 움켜쥐고는, 미치기라도 했거나 복수라도 하려는 듯 매달려 있는 징을 향해 달려드는 것을 보았다. 그러나 그 젊은이는 경고를 받지도, 당장 붙들려 가지도 않았고, 무시무시한 소리를 한바탕 내고 나서 제지당하는 일 없이 그만두었다. 그러자 코닐리아 블림버가 돔비에게 십오 분 후면 저녁 식사가 준비될 테니 어쩌면 교실로 들어가 자기 \"친구들\" 사이에 끼어 있는 편이 좋겠다고 말했다.\n\n그리하여 돔비는, 여전히 그의 안부를 궁금해하던 그 큰 시계 곁을 정중하게 지나, 교실 문을 아주 조금만 열고는 길 잃은 아이처럼 안으로 들어섰다. 그러고는 다소 애를 써서 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 그의 친구들은 돌 같은 친구만 빼고 모두 방 안 여기저기 흩어져 있었고, 그 돌 같은 친구는 꿈쩍도 않고 그대로 있었다. 피더 선생은 회색 가운을 입은 채 기지개를 켜고 있었는데, 마치 비용 따위는 아랑곳 않고 소매를 통째로 잡아 뜯기로 작정한 것 같았다.\n\n\"하이 호 흠!\" 피더 선생이 짐마차 끄는 말처럼 몸을 부르르 떨며 외쳤다. \"아이고 맙소사, 맙소사! 야아아아!\"\n\n폴은 피더 선생의 하품에 적잖이 놀랐다. 어찌나 큰 규모로 했는지, 또 어찌나 끔찍하게 진지하던지. 다른 아이들도(투츠만 빼고) 죄다 녹초가 된 듯했고, 저녁 식사를 준비하고 있었다—어떤 아이들은 새로 목도리를 매고 있었는데 그게 정말이지 아주 빳빳했고, 또 어떤 아이들은 옆에 딸린 대기실에서 손을 씻거나 머리를 빗고 있었다—마치 식사를 조금도 즐기지 못할 거라고 여기는 것처럼.\n\n미리 준비를 끝내서 할 일이 없던, 그래서 폴에게 시간을 내어 줄 여유가 있던 어린 투츠가 묵직한 호의를 담아 말했다.\n\n\"앉아, 돔비.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선배님,\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n폴이 아주 높은 창가 자리에 기어 올라가려고 애쓰다가 다시 미끄러져 내려오는 모습은, 투츠의 머릿속에 어떤 발견을 받아들일 준비를 시킨 듯했다.\n\n\"너 참 조그만 녀석이구나,\" 투츠가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 선배님, 저는 작아요,\" 폴이 대꾸했다. \"고맙습니다, 선배님.\"\n\n투츠가 그를 들어 자리에 올려놓아 주었는데, 그것도 친절하게 해 주었기 때문이었다.\n\n\"네 재단사는 누구지?\" 투츠가 잠시 그를 바라본 후 물었다.\n\n\"지금까지 제 옷을 만든 건 어떤 여자분이에요,\" 폴이 말했다. \"제 누나의 양재사예요.\"\n\n\"내 재단사는 버지스 상회야,\" 투츠가 말했다. \"고급이지. 하지만 아주 비싸.\"\n\n폴은 그쯤은 보면 안다는 듯 고개를 끄덕일 정도의 재치는 있었다. 그리고 실제로 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n\"네 아버지는 어김없이 부자지, 그렇지 않아?\" 투츠가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 선배님,\" 폴이 말했다. \"저희 아버지는 돔비와 아들이에요.\"\n\n\"그리고 뭐라고?\" 투츠가 따져 물었다.\n\n\"그리고 아들이요, 선배님,\" 폴이 대답했다.\n\n투츠는 낮은 목소리로 그 회사 이름을 머릿속에 새겨 두려고 한두 번 애썼지만, 영 잘되지 않자 내일 아침에 폴에게 그 이름을 다시 한번 말해 달라고 해야겠다고 말했다. 다소 중요한 일이기 때문이라는 것이었다. 사실 그가 마음먹은 것은 다름 아니라 곧장 돔비와 아들로부터 자기 자신에게 사적이고 비밀스러운 편지 한 통을 쓰는 일이었다.\n\n이 무렵 다른 학생들이(언제나 돌 같은 아이만 빼고) 둘러 모였다. 그들은 예의 바르되 창백했고, 낮은 소리로 말했으며, 어찌나 풀이 죽어 있던지, 그 무리의 전반적인 분위기에 견주면 비더스톤 도련님은 완벽한 밀러* 거나 완전한 농담 책이라 할 만했다. 그렇지만 비더스톤도 나름대로 억울하다는 감정을 품고 있기는 했다.\n* 밀러: 당대에 널리 읽힌 농담집 「조 밀러의 농담집」을 빗댄 표현으로, 유쾌하고 익살스러운 사람을 뜻한다.\n\n# Book.” And yet he had a sense of injury upon him, too, had Bitherstone.\n\n책.” 그런데도 비더스톤은 자기 안에 어떤 억울함 같은 것을 품고 있기도 했다.\n“너, 내 방에서 같이 자지, 그렇지?” 셔츠 깃이 귓불 끝까지 말려 올라간, 근엄한 어린 신사가 물었다.\n\n“브릭스 도련님인가요?” 폴이 물었다.\n\n“토저야,” 그 어린 신사가 말했다.\n\n폴이 그렇다고 대답하자, 토저는 그 돌처럼 굳은 학생을 가리키며 저 애가 브릭스라고 했다. 폴은 이미, 왜인지는 몰라도 브릭스 아니면 토저일 것이 틀림없다고 확신하던 참이었다.\n\n“넌 체질이 튼튼하니?” 토저가 물었다.\n\n폴은 아닌 것 같다고 말했다. 토저는 폴의 생김새로 보아 자기 생각에도 아닌 것 같다고, 그건 안된 일이라고, 튼튼할 필요가 있는 것이니까 하고 대답했다. 그러고는 폴에게 코닐리아부터 시작할 거냐고 물었고, 폴이 “응” 하고 대답하자, 어린 신사들은 (브릭스만 빼고) 모두 낮게 신음 소리를 냈다.\n\n그 소리는 징의 땡땡거림에 묻혀 버렸다. 징이 다시 한번 무시무시하게 울리자, 다들 식당 쪽으로 우르르 움직였다. 다만 돌처럼 굳은 소년 브릭스만은 여전히 있던 자리에, 있던 그대로 남아 있었다. 그에게로 가는 길에 폴은 곧 둥근 빵 하나와 마주쳤는데, 그것은 접시와 냅킨에 점잖게 담겨 있었고 그 위에는 은포크 하나가 가로로 놓여 있었다. 블림버 박사는 이미 식당의 식탁 상석 제자리에 앉아 있었고, 양옆으로 블림버 양과 블림버 부인이 자리했다. 검은 외투를 입은 피더 씨는 말석에 있었다. 폴의 의자는 블림버 양의 옆자리였다. 그러나 그가 거기 앉고 보니 눈썹이 식탁보 높이보다 그리 높지 않다는 것이 드러나, 박사의 서재에서 책 몇 권을 가져와 그 위에 그를 올려 앉혔다. 그때부터 그는 늘 그 위에 앉았으며—나중에는 직접 그 책들을 들고 드나들었으니, 마치 자그마한 코끼리와 망루 같았다.\n\n박사가 식전 기도를 올린 뒤, 식사가 시작되었다. 맛있는 수프가 있었고, 구운 고기, 삶은 고기, 채소, 파이, 치즈도 있었다. 어린 신사들은 저마다 묵직한 은포크와 냅킨을 가졌고, 차림새는 모두 위엄 있고 훌륭했다. 특히 푸른 외투에 번쩍이는 단추를 단 집사가 있었는데, 그가 어찌나 근사하게 따라 주는지 식탁용 맥주에 제법 포도주 같은 풍미가 감돌았다.\n\n블림버 박사와 블림버 부인, 블림버 양만이 이따금 이야기를 나눌 뿐, 아무도 말을 걸어오지 않으면 입을 열지 않았다. 어린 신사는 누구든 칼이나 포크나 숟가락에 실제로 매달려 있지 않을 때면, 그 눈이 거역할 수 없는 인력에 끌려 블림버 박사나 블림버 부인이나 블림버 양의 눈을 찾았고, 거기에 얌전히 머물렀다. 투츠만이 이 규칙의 유일한 예외인 듯했다. 그는 폴이 앉은 쪽 식탁에서 피더 씨 옆자리에 앉아, 사이에 끼인 소년들의 앞뒤로 자주 고개를 빼며 폴을 한 번이라도 보려 했다.\n\n식사하는 동안 어린 신사들이 끼어든 대화는 단 한 번뿐이었다. 그것은 치즈가 나올 무렵에 일어났다. 박사가 포트와인을 한 잔 들고 두세 번 헛기침을 하더니 말했다.\n\n“피더 군, 참으로 주목할 만한 일이오. 로마인들이—”\n\n이 무시무시한 종족, 자기들의 가차 없는 원수의 이름이 나오자, 어린 신사들은 저마다 더없이 깊은 흥미를 가장하며 박사에게 시선을 고정했다. 그중 한 명이 마침 음료를 마시고 있다가 텀블러 옆면 너머로 자기를 노려보는 박사의 눈을 마주치고는 너무 급히 마시기를 멈춘 탓에 한동안 발작을 일으켰고, 결국 블림버 박사의 말머리를 망쳐 놓고 말았다.\n\n“참으로 주목할 만한 일이오, 피더 군,” 박사가 천천히 다시 시작했다. “로마인들이 말이오, 황제들의 시대에 관해 우리가 읽는 그 화려하고 사치스러운 향연들에서—사치가 그 전에도 그 후에도 알려진 적 없는 경지에 이르렀던 때, 단 한 번의 황실 연회에 쓸 그 휘황한 자재를 마련하느라 온 속주들이 약탈당하던 때—”\n\n여기서 그 말썽꾸러기, 그동안 부풀어 오르며 안간힘을 쓰고 마침표를 헛되이 기다리던 그자가 격렬하게 터져 나왔다.\n\n“존슨,” 피더 씨가 낮고 나무라는 목소리로 말했다, “물 좀 마시게.”\n\n박사는 매우 근엄한 표정으로 물이 올 때까지 잠시 멈추었다가 다시 이었다.\n\n“그리고 말이오, 피더 군—”\n\n그러나 피더 씨는 존슨이 또 터져 나올 것이 분명하다는 걸 알았고, 박사가 하려는 말을 모두 마치기 전에는 결코 어린 신사들 앞에서 한 마디 끝맺지 않으리라는 것도 알았기에, 존슨에게서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다. 그렇게 그는 박사를 보고 있지 않은 현장을 들켰고, 그 바람에 박사는 또 멈추었다.\n\n“죄송합니다, 선생님,” 피더 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. “죄송합니다, 블림버 박사님.”\n\n“그리고 말이오,” 박사가 목소리를 높이며 말했다, “선생, 우리가 읽기로—또 의심할 이유도 없거니와, 우리 시대의 속된 자들에게는 믿기 어려운 일로 보일지언정—비텔리우스의 형이 그를 위해 한 잔치를 마련했는데, 거기에 생선 요리만 이천 접시가 차려졌고—”\n\n“물 좀 마시게, 존슨—접시들 말이오, 선생,” 피더 씨가 말했다.\n\n“온갖 종류의 새 요리가 오천 접시였소.”\n\n“아니면 빵 껍질이라도 좀 베어 물든가,” 피더 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 한 접시는,” 블림버 박사가 식탁을 빙 둘러보며 목소리를 한층 더 높여 계속했다, “그 어마어마한 크기 때문에 미네르바의 방패라 불렸으며, 그 밖의 값비싼 재료들 가운데 꿩의 골—”\n\n“욱, 욱, 욱!” (존슨에게서.)\n\n“멧도요—”\n\n“욱, 욱, 욱!”\n\n“스카리라 불리는 물고기의—”\n\n“자네 머릿속 혈관이 터지겠네,” 피더 씨가 말했다. “차라리 그냥 터뜨리는 게 낫겠어.”\n\n“그리고 카르파티아 해에서 가져온 칠성장어의 알로 만들어졌소,” 박사가 더없이 준엄한 목소리로 계속했다. “이런 값비싼 향연들을 읽으면서도, 우리에게 티투스 같은 자가 있었음을 여전히 떠올린다면—”\n\n“자네가 졸중으로 죽으면 자네 어머니 심정이 어떻겠나!” 피더 씨가 말했다.\n“도미티아누스 같은 자—”\n\n“게다가 자네 얼굴이 새파래졌어, 알겠나,” 피더 씨가 말했다.\n\n“네로, 티베리우스, 칼리굴라, 헬리오가발루스, 그 밖에도 숱한 자들이 있었으니,” 박사가 계속했다. “그것은, 피더 군—만일 자네가 송구스럽게도 내 말을 듣고 있다면—주목할 만하오. 참으로 주목할 만한 일이오, 선생—”\n\n그러나 존슨은 더는 억누를 수가 없어, 바로 그 순간 감당 못 할 기침 발작으로 터져 나오고 말았으니, 양옆의 두 친구가 등을 두드려 주고, 피더 씨가 친히 물잔을 입에 대 주고, 집사가 보초처럼 그를 제 의자와 찬장 사이로 여러 번 걸려 주었음에도, 어지간히 진정되기까지 꼬박 오 분이 걸렸다. 그러고 나서는 깊은 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n“여러분,” 블림버 박사가 말했다, “기도를 위해 일어서시오! 코닐리아, 돔비를 내려놓아라.”—그래서 식탁보 위로는 그의 정수리밖에 보이지 않게 되었다. “존슨은 내일 아침 식사 전에 책을 보지 않고, 그리스어 신약성서로 사도 바울이 에베소인에게 보낸 편지 제1장을 내게 외워 들려주게. 피더 군, 우리는 삼십 분 후에 공부를 다시 시작하겠소.”\n\n어린 신사들이 절을 하고 물러갔다. 피더 씨도 그렇게 했다. 그 삼십 분 동안 어린 신사들은 둘씩 짝을 지어 팔짱을 끼고 집 뒤의 자그마한 마당을 이리저리 거닐거나, 브릭스의 가슴속에 한 점 생기의 불꽃이라도 지펴 보려 애썼다. 그러나 놀이처럼 속된 일은 결코 일어나지 않았다. 정해진 시간에 어김없이 징이 울렸고, 블림버 박사와 피더 씨의 합동 주재 아래 공부가 다시 시작되었다.\n\n그날은 존슨의 일 때문에 이리저리 거니는 그 올림픽 경기가 평소보다 짧게 잘려 버린 탓에, 그들은 모두 차 마시기 전에 산책을 나갔다. 브릭스마저 (아직 시작도 안 했으면서) 이 방탕에 끼었는데, 그 즐거움 가운데서 그는 두세 번 벼랑 너머를 음울하게 내려다보았다. 블림버 박사가 그들을 따라나섰고, 폴은 박사 본인에게 직접 손을 잡혀 끌려가는 영예를 누렸다. 대단한 상황이었지만, 그 속에서 그는 무척 작고 가냘파 보였다.\n\n차는 식사 못지않게 점잖은 방식으로 차려졌다. 차를 마신 뒤 어린 신사들은 앞서처럼 일어나 절을 하고 물러가서는, 그날 끝내지 못한 과제를 마저 하거나, 이미 어렴풋이 다가오는 내일의 과제를 준비했다. 그동안 피더 씨는 자기 방으로 물러갔고, 폴은 한쪽 구석에 앉아 플로렌스가 자기를 생각하고 있을까, 핍친 부인 댁에서는 다들 무얼 하고 있을까 궁금해했다.\n\n웰링턴 공작에게서 온 중요한 편지에 붙들려 있던 투츠 씨가 얼마 후 폴을 찾아냈다. 그는 전처럼 한참 동안 폴을 바라보더니, 조끼를 좋아하느냐고 물었다.\n\n폴은 “네, 좋아해요” 하고 말했다.\n\n“나도 그래,” 투츠가 말했다.\n\n투츠는 그날 밤 그 이상은 한 마디도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그는 폴이 마음에 든다는 듯 폴을 바라보며 서 있었고, 거기에 동무가 되어 주는 무엇이 있었기에, 그리고 폴도 말할 기분이 아니었기에, 그것은 대화보다 폴의 뜻에 더 잘 맞았다.\n\n여덟 시쯤 식당에서 기도를 위해 또 한번 징이 울렸다. 그 뒤 집사가 곁상을 주재했는데, 거기에는 그런 다과를 들고 싶어 하는 어린 신사들을 위해 빵과 치즈와 맥주가 차려져 있었다. 의식은 박사의 “여러분, 우리는 내일 일곱 시에 공부를 다시 시작하겠소”라는 말로 끝났다. 그리고 그때 처음으로 폴은 코닐리아 블림버의 눈을 보았고, 그 눈이 자기에게 향해 있음을 보았다. 박사가 “여러분, 우리는 내일 일곱 시에 공부를 다시 시작하겠소”라고 말하자, 학생들은 다시 절을 하고 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n위층 자기들 방의 은밀함 속에서, 브릭스는 머리가 쪼개질 듯이 아프다고, 어머니와 집에 두고 온 검은지빠귀 한 마리만 아니었다면 차라리 죽고 싶다고 말했다. 토저는 별말은 하지 않았으나 한숨을 잔뜩 내쉬며, 내일은 네 차례가 올 테니 조심하라고 폴에게 일렀다. 그 예언 같은 말을 내뱉은 뒤, 그는 침울하게 옷을 벗고 침대로 들어갔다. 눈이 약한 젊은이가 촛불을 가지러 나타나 잘 자고 좋은 꿈 꾸라고 인사할 무렵에는, 브릭스도 침대에, 폴도 침대에 들어가 있었다. 그러나 그의 자비로운 소원은 브릭스와 토저에 관한 한 헛된 것이었으니, 오래도록 잠 못 이루다가 그 뒤에도 자주 깬 폴이 보니, 브릭스는 자기 수업에 악몽처럼 짓눌려 있었고, 잠결에 비슷한 이유로 정신이 시달린 토저는 그보다 덜한 정도로 알 수 없는 말—그리스어와 라틴어 토막들—을 중얼거렸는데, 폴에게는 그게 다 그게 그거였지만, 밤의 정적 속에서 그것은 말할 수 없이 사악하고 죄스러운 느낌을 자아냈다.\n\n폴은 달콤한 잠에 빠져들어, 플로렌스와 손을 잡고 아름다운 정원들을 거니는 꿈을 꾸었는데, 그들이 커다란 해바라기 한 송이에 이르자 그것이 별안간 징으로 부풀어 오르더니 울리기 시작했다. 눈을 떠 보니, 어둡고 바람 부는 아침이었고 가랑비가 보슬보슬 내리고 있었다. 그리고 진짜 징이 아래층 현관에서 무시무시한 준비의 신호를 울리고 있었다.\n\n그래서 그는 곧장 일어났는데, 보니 악몽과 슬픔으로 얼굴이 퉁퉁 부어 눈이 거의 보이지 않는 브릭스가 부츠를 신고 있었고, 토저는 부들부들 떨며 어깨를 문지르면서 몹시 기분이 좋지 않은 채 서 있었다. 가엾은 폴은 익숙지 않아서 옷을 쉽게 입지 못했다. 그래서 끈 몇 개를 좀 매어 주는 친절을 베풀어 주지 않겠느냐고 물었지만, 브릭스는 그저 “귀찮게!”라고만 하고 토저는 “오 그래!”라고만 하기에, 그는 그 밖의 채비를 마친 뒤 아래층으로 내려갔다. 한 층 아래에서 그는 가죽 장갑을 낀 예쁜 젊은 여자가 난로를 닦고 있는 것을 보았다. 그 젊은 여자는 그가 나타난 것에 놀란 듯, 어머니가 어디 계시냐고 물었다. 폴이 어머니는 돌아가셨다고 하자, 그녀는 장갑을 벗고 그가 바라던 일을 해 주었다. 게다가 그의 손을 비벼 따뜻하게 데워 주고, 입을 맞추어 주고는, 그런 종류의 일이—옷 입는 쪽으로—필요할 때면 언제든 ‘밀리아’를 찾으라고 일러 주었다. 폴은 그녀에게 무척 고마워하며 꼭 그러겠다고 말했다. 그러고는 어린 신사들이 공부를 다시 시작하는 방을 향해 살그머니 아래층 길을 계속 내려가던 중, 빠끔히 열린 문 하나를 지나는데 안에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다. “돔비니?” 폴이 “네, 부인” 하고 대답하자—그 목소리가 블림버 양의 것임을 알았기에—블림버 양이 말했다. “들어오너라, 돔비.” 그래서 그는 들어갔다.\n\n블림버 양은 숄을 두른 것만 빼면 어제와 똑같은 모습을 하고 있었다. 그녀의 자그마한 밝은색 곱슬머리는 여전히 빳빳했고, 벌써 안경을 쓰고 있어서 폴은 그녀가 안경을 쓴 채 잠자리에 드는 것은 아닐까 궁금해졌다. 그녀는 그 위층에 자기만의 서늘한 작은 거실을 가지고 있었는데, 거기에는 책 몇 권이 있었고 난롯불은 없었다. 그러나 블림버 양은 결코 춥지도, 결코 졸리지도 않았다.\n\n“자, 돔비,” 블림버 양이 말했다, “나는 건강 산책을 나갈 거란다.”\n\n폴은 그게 뭔지, 또 이렇게 궂은 날씨에 왜 하인을 시켜 받아 오게 하지 않는지 궁금했다. 그러나 그는 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그의 주의는 블림버 양이 최근에 매달려 있었던 듯한 새 책 한 무더기에 쏠려 있었다.\n\n“이건 네 거야, 돔비,” 블림버 양이 말했다.\n\n“전부 다요, 부인?” 폴이 말했다.\n\n“그래,” 블림버 양이 대답했다, “그리고 네가 내 기대대로 공부에 열심이라면, 돔비, 피더 씨가 곧 더 골라 줄 거란다.”\n\n“고맙습니다, 부인,” 폴이 말했다.\n\n“나는 건강 산책을 나갈 거야,” 블림버 양이 다시 말했다. “그리고 내가 나가 있는 동안, 그러니까 지금부터 아침 식사까지의 사이에, 돔비, 네가 이 책들에서 내가 표시해 둔 것을 읽어 보고, 네가 배워야 할 것을 충분히 이해했는지 내게 말해 주었으면 한다. 시간을 허비하지 마라, 돔비. 너에게는 남는 시간이 없으니까. 책을 아래층으로 가지고 내려가서 곧장 시작하렴.”\n\n“네, 부인,” 폴이 대답했다.\n\n책이 어찌나 많았던지, 폴이 한 손은 맨 밑의 책 아래에 받치고 다른 한 손과 턱은 맨 위의 책에 얹고 그 전부를 바짝 끌어안았는데도, 문에 닿기 전에 가운데 책이 미끄러져 빠지더니 죄다 바닥으로 와르르 쏟아졌다. 블림버 양은 “오, 돔비, 돔비, 정말 너무 조심성이 없구나!” 하고는 그를 위해 책을 다시 쌓아 주었다. 이번에는 매우 정교하게 균형을 잡은 덕에 폴은 방에서 나가 계단을 몇 칸 내려갔으나, 그러다 두 권이 또 빠져나갔다. 그래도 나머지는 어찌나 꽉 붙들었던지, 이 층에 한 권을, 복도에 한 권을 더 떨어뜨렸을 뿐이었다. 그리고 본대(本隊)를 교실까지 내려놓고 나서, 그는 낙오병들을 거두러 다시 위층으로 올라갔다. 마침내 장서 전부를 모아 자기 자리로 기어 올라간 그는, 토저가 “이제 너도 꼼짝없이 걸려들었군” 하고 한 말에 격려를 받으며 일에 착수했다. 그 말이 아침 식사 시간까지 그가 받은 유일한 방해였다. 그 식사 때—그에게는 입맛이 전혀 없었지만—모든 것이 다른 끼니들과 똑같이 엄숙하고 점잖았다. 식사가 끝나자, 그는 블림버 양을 따라 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n“자, 돔비,” 블림버 양이 말했다. “그 책들은 어떻게 되어 가고 있니?”\n\n그 책들에는 영어가 조금, 라틴어가 잔뜩 들어 있었다—사물의 이름들, 관사와 명사의 격변화, 그에 관한 연습 문제와 예비 규칙들—철자법 약간, 고대사 한 번 훑기, 근대사 한두 번 곁눈질하기, 표 몇 개, 도량형 두세 가지, 그리고 일반 상식 약간. 가엾은 폴이 둘째 항목을 더듬더듬 익히고 나니, 첫째 항목은 도무지 모르겠는 것이었다. 그리고 그 첫째 항목의 조각들이 나중에 셋째 항목으로 비집고 들어왔고, 셋째 항목은 넷째 항목으로 미끄러졌으며, 넷째 항목은 다시 둘째 항목에 접붙었다. 그래서 로물루스 스무 명이 모이면 레무스 하나가 되는지, ‘히크 하이크 호크’가 트로이 중량인지, 동사가 늘 고대 브리튼인과 일치하는지, 사 곱하기 삼이 황소 타우루스인지 하는 것들이 그에게는 모두 미해결의 문제로 남았다.\n\n“오, 돔비, 돔비!” 블림버 양이 말했다, “이건 정말 형편없구나.”\n\n“괜찮으시다면,” 폴이 말했다, “제 생각엔 이따금 늙은 글럽 아저씨하고 조금씩 이야기를 나눌 수 있다면, 더 잘할 수 있을 것 같아요.”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리, 돔비,” 블림버 양이 말했다. “그런 건 들어 줄 수 없어. 여긴 어떤 종류든 글럽 같은 자가 있을 자리가 아니야. 책을 한 권씩 가지고 내려가야 할 게다, 돔비. 그리고 B 과목으로 넘어가기 전에 우선 그날 분량의 A 과목을 완벽히 익혀야 해. 유감스럽지만, 돔비, 네 교육이 너무 많이 소홀히 되어 온 것 같구나.”\n\n“아빠도 그렇게 말씀하세요,” 폴이 대답했다. “하지만 제가 말씀드렸잖아요—저는 허약한 아이였어요. 플로렌스도 그걸 알아요. 위컴도 알고요.”\n\n“위컴이 누구지?” 블림버 양이 물었다.\n\n“제 유모였어요,” 폴이 대답했다.\n\n“그렇다면 내게 위컴 이야기는 꺼내지 말아 주었으면 한다,” 블림버 양이 말했다. “그건 허용할 수 없어.”\n\n“부인이 누구냐고 물으셨잖아요,” 폴이 말했다.\n\n“좋아,” 블림버 양이 대답했다. “하지만 이건 그런 따위와는 정말이지 전혀 다른 거야, 돔비. 그리고 나는 그런 걸 허락할 생각이 없다. 허약했던 것으로 말하면, 이제는 튼튼해지기 시작해야지. 자, 이제 맨 위의 책을 가져가렴, 돔비. 그리고 그 주제를 완전히 터득하거든 돌아오너라.”\n\n블림버 양은 폴의 무지한 상태에 대해 자기 의견을 음울한 기쁨으로 표명했는데, 마치 이런 결과를 예상하고 있었고, 그래서 둘이 끊임없이 소통해야만 하리라는 것을 알게 되어 기뻐하는 듯했다. 폴은 시킨 대로 맨 위의 과제를 가지고 물러나, 아래층에서 그것에 매달려 끙끙댔다. 어떤 때는 한 마디 한 마디 다 기억하다가도, 어떤 때는 그것을 전부, 또 그 밖의 모든 것까지 다 잊어버렸다. 그러다 마침내 그 수업을 외워 보려고 다시 위층으로 올라갔는데, 시작하기도 전에 블림버 양이 책을 탁 덮고 “계속해 봐, 돔비!” 하고 말하는 바람에 머릿속의 것이 거의 다 쫓겨 나가 버렸다. 그것은 그녀 안에 든 지식을 너무도 잘 암시하는 행동이어서, 폴은 그 젊은 숙녀를 일종의 박학한 가이 포크스, 혹은 학문적인 짚으로 가득 채워진 인조 허수아비 같은 존재로 여기며 경악하여 바라보았다.\n\n* 가이 포크스: 1605년 영국 의회 폭파 음모(화약 음모 사건)의 가담자로, 해마다 그의 모형 인형을 만들어 태우는 풍습이 있었다.\n그래도 그는 매우 훌륭히 해냈고, 블림버 양은 빠르게 발전할 가능성을 보여 준다고 그를 칭찬하며 즉시 B 과목을 내주었다. 거기서 그는 저녁 식사 전에 C로, 심지어 D로까지 넘어갔다. 식사 후 얼마 지나지 않아 공부를 다시 시작하는 것은 고된 일이었다. 그는 어지럽고 혼란스럽고 졸리고 멍했다. 그러나 다른 어린 신사들도 모두 비슷한 느낌이었고, 그들 역시 공부를 다시 시작해야만 했으니, 거기에 무슨 위안이라도 있다면 말이다. 현관의 그 커다란 시계가 처음의 그 물음에만 한결같지 않고 “여러분, 이제 공부를 다시 시작합시다”라고 말하지 않은 것이 신기할 정도였다. 그 어구가 그 근방에서 충분히 자주 되풀이되었으니까. 공부는 거대한 바퀴처럼 빙빙 돌았고, 어린 신사들은 늘 그 위에 늘여 매달려 있었다.\n\n차를 마신 뒤에는 또 연습 문제와, 촛불 아래의 내일 준비가 있었다. 그리고 차례가 되어 잠자리가 있었으니, 그곳에서는 꿈속에서 일어나는 그 공부의 재개만 아니라면, 안식과 달콤한 망각이 있었다.\n\n오 토요일이여! 오 행복한 토요일이여, 그날이면 플로렌스는 늘 정오에 왔고, 핍친 부인이 으르렁대고 투덜대며 모질게 괴롭혀도, 어떤 날씨에도 결코 오지 않는 법이 없었다. 그 토요일들은 그 모든 유대인 가운데 적어도 두 어린 그리스도인에게는 안식일이었으며, 오누이의 사랑을 굳건히 하고 단단히 엮어 주는 거룩한 안식일의 일을 해 주었다.\n\n일요일 밤들조차—그 무거운 일요일 밤들, 그 그림자가 일요일 아침에 처음 깨어나며 터져 나오는 빛을 어둡게 했던 그 밤들조차—그 소중한 토요일들을 망칠 수는 없었다. 그들이 함께 앉고 거닐던 그 드넓은 바닷가였든, 아니면 그녀가 졸린 그의 머리를 자기 팔에 기대게 하고 그토록 부드럽게 노래를 불러 주던 핍친 부인의 칙칙한 뒷방일 뿐이었든, 폴은 결코 개의치 않았다. 그것은 플로렌스였다. 그가 생각하는 것은 그뿐이었다. 그래서 일요일 밤, 박사의 어두운 문이 그를 또 한 주일 동안 삼키려고 입을 벌리고 서 있을 때, 작별할 시간이 온 것은 플로렌스였다. 다른 누구도 아니었다.\n\n위컴 부인은 시내의 집으로 다시 차출되어 갔고, 이제 맵시 있는 젊은 여자가 된 니퍼 양이 내려와 있었다. 니퍼 양은 핍친 부인과의 숱한 일대일 결투에 용감히 자신을 바쳤고, 만일 핍친 부인이 평생 단 한 번이라도 자기와 맞먹는 적수를 만난 적이 있다면, 바로 이제 그를 만난 것이었다. 니퍼 양은 핍친 부인 댁에서 처음 일어난 그 아침에 칼집을 내던져 버렸다. 그녀는 자비를 구하지도 베풀지도 않았다. 그녀는 이것은 전쟁이어야 한다고 했고, 정말 전쟁이었다. 그때부터 핍친 부인은 기습과 시달림과 도전, 그리고 복도에서 튀어 들어오는 소규모 공격들 한가운데서 살았으니, 그 공격은 양고기 토막을 먹는 무방비한 순간에마저 들이닥쳐 그녀의 토스트에까지 황폐함을 안겨 주었다.\n\n어느 일요일 밤 니퍼 양은 폴을 박사 댁까지 다시 데려다주고 플로렌스와 함께 돌아온 참이었는데, 그때 플로렌스가 가슴에서 작은 종잇조각을 꺼냈다. 거기에는 그녀가 연필로 적어 둔 몇 마디 말이 있었다.\n\n“이거 봐, 수전,” 그녀가 말했다. “이건 폴이 그렇게 지칠 때 그 긴 연습 문제를 하려고 집에 가져오는 작은 책들의 이름이야. 어젯밤에 걔가 쓰고 있는 동안 베껴 둔 거란다.”\n\n“제발 그건 제게 보여 주지 마세요, 플로이 아가씨,” 니퍼가 대답했다. “그럴 바엔 핍친 부인을 보는 게 낫겠어요.”\n\n“네가 이걸 나 대신 사다 줬으면 해, 수전, 괜찮다면 내일 아침에. 나 돈은 충분히 있어,” 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n“아니, 세상에 맙소사, 플로이 아가씨,” 니퍼 양이 대답했다, “책에 책이 또 있고, 선생님이며 사모님이며 온갖 걸 끊임없이 가르치는데 어떻게 그런 말씀을 하세요. 하기야 제 생각엔, 돔비 아가씨, 아가씨 아버님은 아가씨가 청하기 전엔 아무것도 가르치려 하지 않았을 거예요, 생각조차 안 하셨을 거고요—청하면 차마 거절은 못 하셨겠지만요. 하지만 청할 때 승낙하는 것과 청하지 않아도 먼저 내미는 것은, 아가씨, 전혀 다른 두 가지예요. 어떤 청년이 저랑 사귀는 걸 제가 마다하지 않을 수도 있고, 그 사람이 청혼하면 ‘예’라고 할 수도 있지만, 그건 ‘부디 저를 좋아해 주시겠어요’라고 말하는 거랑은 다르잖아요.”\n\n“하지만 넌 그 책들을 사다 줄 수 있잖아, 수전. 그리고 내가 왜 그게 필요한지 알면, 너도 사다 줄 거야.”\n\n“그럼요, 아가씨, 그런데 그건 왜 필요하신데요?” 니퍼가 대답하고는 더 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. “그게 핍친 부인 머리통에 던지려는 거라면, 한 수레라도 사 오겠어요.”\n\n“폴은 할 일이 너무 많아, 수전,” 플로렌스가 말했다. “나 확신해.”\n\n“정말 그러실 만해요, 아가씨,” 그녀의 하녀가 대답했다. “그 착한 도련님이 일에 시달리고 또 시달린다고 마음 푹 놓고 믿으셔도 돼요. 만약 저게 라틴어식 다리라면,” 폴의 다리를 빗대어 니퍼 양이 격한 감정으로 외쳤다, “전 영국식 다리를 갖겠어요.”\n\n“폴이 블림버 박사 댁에서 외롭고 막막함을 느끼는 것 같아 걱정이야, 수전,” 플로렌스가 얼굴을 돌리며 계속했다.\n\n“아,” 니퍼 양이 무척 날카롭게 말했다, “오, 그 ‘블림버’들이라니!”\n\n“아무도 탓하지 마,” 플로렌스가 말했다. “이건 잘못된 일이야.”\n\n“탓에 대해선 아무 말도 안 할게요, 아가씨,” 니퍼 양이 외쳤다. “아가씨가 싫어하시는 걸 아니까요. 하지만 아가씨, 저는 바랄 수는 있잖아요. 그 집안 식구들이 새 길을 닦는 일에 부려졌으면, 그리고 블림버 양이 앞장서서 곡괭이를 들었으면 하고요.”\n\n이 말을 마친 뒤, 더없이 진지했던 니퍼 양은 눈가를 닦았다.\n\n“이 책들이 있으면 어쩌면 내가 폴을 좀 도와줄 수 있을 것 같아, 수전,” 플로렌스가 말했다, “그리고 다가오는 한 주를 걔한테 조금이라도 수월하게 해 줄 수 있을 것 같아. 적어도 해 보고 싶어. 그러니 사다 줘, 응. 네가 해 준 게 얼마나 고마운 일인지 결코 잊지 않을게!”\n\n플로렌스가 이 말과 함께 내미는 작은 지갑을, 혹은 자기 청을 거드는 그 부드러운 애원의 눈빛을 거절할 수 있는 마음이라면, 수전 니퍼의 마음보다 더 모진 것이어야 했으리라. 수전은 아무 대답 없이 지갑을 주머니에 넣고, 곧장 심부름을 떠나 종종걸음으로 나갔다.\n\n그 책들은 구하기가 쉽지 않았다. 여러 가게에서 돌아온 대답은, 마침 다 떨어졌다거나, 원래 그런 건 두지 않는다거나, 지난달에 잔뜩 들여놓았었다거나, 다음 주에 잔뜩 들어올 예정이라는 것이었다. 그러나 수전은 그런 일에서 쉽게 좌절할 사람이 아니었다. 그래서 자기가 잘 아는 어느 도서 대여점에서 검은 옥양목 앞치마를 두른, 머리가 흰 젊은이 하나를 꾀어내어 함께 탐색에 나서게 했는데, 그를 이리저리 끌고 다니며 어찌나 들볶았던지, 그는 오로지 그녀에게서 벗어나기 위해서라도 온 힘을 다했고, 마침내 그녀가 의기양양하게 집으로 돌아갈 수 있게 해 주었다.\n\n그리하여 이 보물들을 가지고, 플로렌스는 자기 자신의 그날 공부가 끝난 뒤 밤이면 자리에 앉아, 가시밭 같은 배움의 길을 따라 폴의 발자취를 더듬어 나갔다. 그리고 타고난 빠르고 건실한 능력을 갖춘 데다, 그 모든 스승 가운데 가장 놀라운 스승인 사랑에게 가르침을 받았으니, 오래지 않아 그녀는 폴의 발꿈치를 따라잡았고, 그를 따라잡아 앞질렀다.\n\n이 일은 핍친 부인에게는 한마디도 새어 나가지 않았다. 그러나 다들 잠자리에 들고, 머리에 종이를 만 니퍼 양이 어느 불편한 자세로 잠든 채 그녀 곁에서 무심히 쉬고 있을 때, 그리고 난로 안의 짤랑이는 잿불이 차갑게 식어 잿빛이 되고, 촛불이 다 타들어 가 녹아 흘러내릴 때—플로렌스는 한 명의 작은 돔비를 대신하려고 너무도 애써서, 그 의연함과 끈기만으로도 그 이름을 스스로 떳떳이 지닐 자격을 거의 얻어 낼 만했던 숱한 밤이 있었다.\n\n그리고 그녀의 보답은 컸다. 어느 토요일 저녁, 어린 폴이 여느 때처럼 “공부를 다시 시작하려고” 자리에 앉았을 때, 그녀가 그 곁에 앉아, 그토록 거칠던 모든 것이 매끄러워지고, 그토록 어둡던 모든 것이 또렷하고 분명해진 것을 그의 앞에 펼쳐 보여 준 것이다. 그것은 그저 폴의 핼쑥한 얼굴에 떠오른 놀란 표정—발그레함—미소—그리고 이어지는 꼭 껴안음뿐이었으나, 자기 수고에 대한 이 풍성한 보답에 그녀의 가슴이 얼마나 뛰어올랐는지는 하느님만이 아실 일이었다.\n\n“오, 플로이!” 그녀의 남동생이 외쳤다, “내가 누나를 얼마나 사랑하는지! 내가 누나를 얼마나 사랑하는지 몰라, 플로이!”\n\n“나도 너를 사랑해, 얘야!”\n\n“오! 그건 나도 알아, 플로이.”\n\n그는 그것에 대해 더는 말하지 않았으나, 그날 저녁 내내 그녀 곁에 바싹 붙어 아주 조용히 앉아 있었다. 그리고 밤이 되자 그녀의 방 안에 딸린 자기 작은 방에서 세 번이나 네 번, 누나를 사랑한다고 외쳤다.\n\n그 뒤로 줄곧, 플로렌스는 토요일 밤이면 폴과 함께 앉아, 둘이서 함께 미리 헤아릴 수 있는 만큼 그의 다음 주 공부를 참을성 있게 도울 채비를 했다. 자기가 지금 끙끙대는 곳을 플로렌스가 바로 앞서 애써 지나갔다는 흐뭇한 생각만으로도, 끝없이 공부를 다시 시작하는 폴에게는 그 자체로 자극이 되었을 터였다. 그러나 그 도움에 따라 짐이 실제로 가벼워진 것과 더불어, 그 생각은, 아리따운 코닐리아 블림버가 그의 등에 쌓아 올린 짐 밑에 그가 짓눌려 가라앉는 것을 어쩌면 막아 주었다.\n\n그것은 블림버 양이 그를 너무 모질게 대하려 했기 때문도, 블림버 박사가 어린 신사들 일반을 너무 호되게 짓누르려 했기 때문도 아니었다. 코닐리아는 그저 자기가 자라며 배운 신조를 굳게 지킬 뿐이었다. 그리고 박사는 생각이 다소 어수선하게 뒤섞여 있어서, 어린 신사들을 마치 그들이 모두 박사이고, 또 다 자란 어른으로 태어나기라도 한 양 여겼다. 어린 신사들의 가장 가까운 친척들의 갈채에 흐뭇해하고, 그들의 맹목적인 허영과 분별없는 조급함에 부추김을 받았으니, 블림버 박사가 자기 잘못을 깨닫거나, 부풀어 오른 돛을 다른 방향으로 손질했다면 그것이 도리어 이상한 일이었을 것이다.\n\n폴의 경우가 바로 그러했다. 블림버 박사가 그가 큰 진보를 이루었으며 천성이 영리하다고 말하자, 돔비 씨는 그 어느 때보다 그를 더 밀어붙이고 더 채워 넣어야 한다는 데 골몰했다. 브릭스의 경우, 블림버 박사가 아직 큰 진보를 이루지 못했으며 천성이 영리하지 않다고 보고하자, 브릭스 씨 어른은 똑같은 목적에 대해 추호도 흔들림이 없었다. 요컨대, 박사가 자기 온실을 아무리 높고 거짓된 온도로 유지하든, 그 화초들의 주인들은 언제나 풀무질을 거들고 불을 더 돋우려 기꺼이 나섰다.\n\n처음에 폴이 가졌던 만큼의 기운은 물론 그도 곧 잃어버렸다. 그러나 그 성품 가운데 기묘하고, 노숙하고, 사색적인 면은 모두 그대로 간직했다. 그리고 그런 경향이 자라나기에 그토록 알맞은 상황 아래서, 그는 전보다 한층 더 기묘하고, 노숙하고, 사색적인 아이가 되었다.\n\n다만 한 가지 다른 점이라면, 그가 자기 성품을 혼자만 간직했다는 것이었다. 그는 날마다 더 사색에 잠기고 더 말수가 줄었다. 그리고 박사네 집안의 그 어떤 살아 있는 식구에 대해서도, 핍친 부인에게 가졌던 만큼의 호기심을 갖지 않았다. 그는 혼자 있기를 좋아했다. 그리고 책에 매달리지 않은 그 짧은 틈이면, 혼자 집 안을 이리저리 거닐거나, 계단에 앉아 현관의 그 커다란 시계에 귀 기울이는 것만큼 좋아하는 일이 없었다. 그는 집 안의 모든 벽지와 친했다. 그 무늬 속에서 다른 누구도 못 보는 것들을 보았다. 침실 벽을 타고 달리는 자그마한 호랑이며 사자를 찾아냈고, 바닥 깔개의 네모와 마름모 무늬 속에서 곁눈질하며 히죽이는 얼굴들을 발견했다.\n\n그 외로운 아이는 자기 사색의 공상이 빚어낸 이 아라베스크 같은 무늬에 둘러싸여 살아갔고, 아무도 그를 이해하지 못했다. 블림버 부인은 그를 “별나다”고 여겼고, 하인들은 이따금 자기들끼리 어린 돔비가 “우울해한다”고 말했다. 그러나 그것이 전부였다.\n\n어린 투츠가 이 일에 대해 어떤 생각을 품고 있었던 것이 아니라면 말이다. 다만 그는 그것을 표현할 능력이 전혀 없었다. 생각이라는 것은 유령처럼 (유령에 대한 흔한 관념을 따르자면) 먼저 조금 말을 걸어 주어야 비로소 자기를 설명하는 법인데, 투츠는 오래전에 자기 마음에 어떤 물음을 던지는 일을 그만두었다. 그의 두개골이라는 그 납빛 궤짝에서 어떤 안개가 피어 나왔을지도 모르고, 그것이 만일 모양과 형태를 갖출 수 있었다면 정령이 되었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그러지 못했다. 그것은 그저, 두꺼운 구름이 되어 굽이쳐 나와서는 거기 걸려 맴도는 정도로만 아라비아 이야기 속 연기의 본을 따랐을 뿐이다. 그러나 그것은 한 외로운 바닷가에 자그마한 형상 하나를 어렴풋이 남겨 놓았고, 투츠는 늘 그것을 빤히 바라보고 있었다.\n\n“잘 있었니?” 그는 하루에도 쉰 번씩 폴에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. “네, 아주 잘 지냈어요, 고맙습니다.” 폴이 대답하곤 했다. “악수하자,” 그것이 투츠의 다음 진전이었다.\n\n물론 폴은 즉시 그렇게 했다. 투츠 씨는 한참 빤히 바라보며 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉰 끝에, 으레 다시 “잘 있었니?” 하고 말했다. 그러면 폴은 다시 “네, 아주 잘 지냈어요, 고맙습니다” 하고 대답했다.\n\n어느 날 저녁 투츠 씨가 편지에 짓눌려 책상 앞에 앉아 있을 때, 어떤 큰 결심이 그에게 번뜩 떠오른 듯했다. 그는 펜을 내려놓고 폴을 찾으러 갔는데, 한참을 뒤진 끝에 마침내 그가 자기 작은 침실 창문 밖을 내다보고 있는 것을 발견했다.\n\n“있잖아!” 투츠가 방에 들어서는 순간, 잊어버릴까 봐 곧바로 말했다. “넌 무슨 생각을 하니?”\n\n“오! 저는 아주 많은 것들을 생각해요,” 폴이 대답했다.\n\n“그래, 정말?” 투츠가 그 사실 자체를 놀랍게 여기는 듯이 말했다. “만약에 죽어야 한다면 말이에요,” 폴이 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다—투츠 씨는 흠칫 놀라며 몹시 동요한 듯 보였다.\n\n“달빛 비치는 밤에, 하늘이 아주 맑고 바람이, 어젯밤처럼 불 때 죽는 게 더 낫다고 생각하지 않으세요?”\n\n투츠 씨는 미심쩍은 듯 폴을 바라보며 고개를 저었고, 그건 잘 모르겠다고 말했다.\n\n“바람이 불기보다는,” 폴이 말했다, “공기 속에서 소리가 나는 거예요, 조개껍데기 속에서 바다 소리가 나듯이요. 아름다운 밤이었어요. 한참 동안 물소리를 듣고 나서 일어나 밖을 내다봤어요. 저기 저편에, 달빛이 가득한 가운데 배 한 척이 있었어요. 돛을 단 배요.”\n\n그 아이가 어찌나 한결같이 그를 바라보며 어찌나 진지하게 말하는지, 투츠 씨는 이 배에 대해 무언가 말해야 할 것 같은 의무감을 느껴 “밀수꾼들이로군” 하고 말했다. 그러나 모든 문제에는 양면이 있다는 것을 공정하게 떠올리고는 “아니면 단속선이거나”라고 덧붙였다.\n\n“돛을 단 배요,” 폴이 되풀이했다, “달빛이 가득한 가운데. 돛이 마치 팔 같았어요, 온통 은빛으로. 그게 저 멀리로 멀어져 갔는데, 그게 물결을 따라 움직이면서 무얼 하는 것 같았는지 아세요?”\n\n“출렁였겠지,” 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n“손짓하는 것 같았어요,” 그 아이가 말했다, “나더러 오라고 손짓하는 것 같았어요!—저기 누나가 있어요! 저기 누나가 있어요!”\n\n이 갑작스러운 외침에, 앞서 있었던 일들 끝에 나온 것이라, 투츠는 경악하여 거의 제정신이 아니게 되어 “누가?” 하고 외쳤다.\n\n“우리 누나 플로렌스요!” 폴이 외쳤다, “여기 위를 올려다보면서 손을 흔들고 있어요. 누나가 날 봐요—날 본다고요! 잘 자, 누나, 잘 자, 잘 자.”\n\n순식간에 끝없는 기쁨의 상태로 옮겨 가서, 창가에 서서 입을 맞추고 손뼉을 치는 그 모습, 그리고 그녀가 시야에서 사라지자 빛이 그의 얼굴에서 물러나며 그 작은 얼굴에 참을성 있는 우수를 남겨 놓는 그 모양은, 투츠의 눈마저 온전히 비껴가기에는 너무도 두드러진 것이었다. 마침 이 순간 핍친 부인의 방문으로 그들의 만남이 중단되었는데—그녀는 보통 일주일에 한두 번, 해질 무렵 직전에 자기 검은 치마를 끌고 폴에게 들이닥쳤다—그래서 투츠는 이 기회를 이용할 여지가 없었다. 그러나 그것은 그의 마음에 너무도 뚜렷한 인상을 남겨, 그는 으레 하는 인사를 나눈 뒤에 두 번이나 되돌아와 핍친 부인에게 안녕하시냐고 물었다. 이 성마른 노부인은 그것을, 아래층의 그 눈 약한 젊은이의 악마적인 발상에서 비롯된, 깊이 꾸며지고 오래 궁리된 모욕이라 여겼다. 그래서 그녀는 바로 그날 밤 블림버 박사에게 정식으로 항의를 제기했고, 박사는 그 젊은이에게 만약 다시 한번 그런 짓을 하면 그를 내보낼 수밖에 없을 거라고 일러두었다.\n\n이제 저녁이 길어졌으므로, 폴은 저녁마다 자기 창가로 살그머니 올라가 플로렌스를 내다보았다. 그녀는 그가 자기를 볼 때까지 늘 정해진 시간에 오갔다. 그리고 그들이 서로를 알아보는 것은 폴의 하루하루에 비치는 한 줄기 햇살이었다. 어둠이 깔린 뒤에도 또 다른 한 형상이 박사의 집 앞을 홀로 거니는 일이 잦았다. 그는 이제 토요일에는 좀처럼 그들과 함께하지 않았다. 그는 그것을 견딜 수가 없었다. 그는 차라리 아무도 모르게 와서, 자기 아들이 어엿한 사내가 되어 가고 있는 그 창문들을 올려다보며, 기다리고, 지켜보고, 계획하고, 바라곤 했다.\n\n오! 그가 만일 볼 수만 있었더라면, 혹은 다른 이들이 보듯 볼 수만 있었더라면—저 위의 그 가냘프고 여윈 소년이, 황혼녘에 진지한 두 눈으로 물결과 구름을 바라보고, 새들이 날아 지날 때면 마치 그들을 흉내 내어 훨훨 날아가 버리고 싶다는 듯 자기 외로운 새장의 창문에 가슴을 들이대고 있는 그 모습을!\n\n# CHAPTER XIII. Shipping Intelligence and Office Business\n\n돔비 씨의 사무소는 어느 안마당에 자리하고 있었는데, 그 모퉁이에는 좋은 과일을 파는 오래된 노점이 하나 있었다. 그곳에서는 남녀를 막론한 떠돌이 장사꾼들이 열 시부터 다섯 시 사이 아무 때나, 슬리퍼며 수첩이며 해면이며 개 목줄이며 윈저 비누를 팔겠다고 내놓았고, 더러는 포인터 사냥개나 유화 한 점을 내놓기도 했다.\n\n포인터는 늘 그쪽으로 끌려왔는데, 증권거래소를 노린 것이었다. 그곳에서는 (대개 새 모자를 건 내기에서 비롯된) 사냥 취미가 한창 유행이었다. 다른 물건들은 일반 대중을 상대로 한 것이었으나, 장사꾼들이 그것들을 돔비 씨에게 들이미는 일은 결코 없었다. 그가 나타나면 그런 잡화를 다루는 자들은 공손히 물러섰다. 슬리퍼와 개 목줄을 파는 우두머리 격 사내는—그는 스스로를 공인이라 여겼고, 그의 초상은 칩사이드의 어느 화가 문에 나사로 박혀 있었다—돔비 씨가 지나갈 때면 집게손가락을 들어 모자챙에 갖다 댔다. 표찰을 단 짐꾼은, 일하러 나가 자리를 비운 게 아니라면, 언제나 부산스레 앞서 달려가 돔비 씨의 사무소 문을 한껏 활짝 열어젖히고는, 모자를 벗은 채 그가 들어가는 동안 그 문을 잡고 서 있었다.\n\n안에 있는 서기들도 공경의 표시에서는 조금도 뒤지지 않았다. 돔비 씨가 바깥 사무실을 가로질러 지나갈 때면 엄숙한 정적이 감돌았다. 회계실의 익살은 그 즉시, 그의 등 뒤에 걸려 있는 가죽 소화통 한 줄처럼 말없이 잠잠해졌다. 간유리 창문과 채광창을 통해 걸러져 들어와 유리판에 검은 침전물을 남기는, 그 김빠지고 밋밋한 햇빛은, 장부와 서류들, 그리고 그 위로 몸을 굽힌 형체들을 비추었는데, 그들은 골똘한 어스름에 감싸인 채 마치 바다 밑바닥에 모여 있기라도 한 듯 바깥세상에서 동떨어진 모습이었다. 한편 어둑한 저편 구석에 곰팡내 나는 작은 금고실에서는 갓을 씌운 등불이 늘 타고 있었는데, 그것은 마치 어느 대양 괴물의 동굴 같아서, 붉은 눈으로 이 심해의 신비를 지켜보는 듯했다.\n\n전령 퍼치는—그의 자리는 마치 괘종시계처럼 작은 받침 선반 위에 있었다—돔비 씨가 들어오는 것을 보면, 아니 오히려 그가 오고 있음을 느끼면(그는 대개 본능적인 감각으로 그가 다가오는 것을 알아챘다), 돔비 씨의 방으로 부리나케 들어가 불을 헤집고, 석탄통 깊은 데서 새 석탄을 가져오고, 신문을 난로 울에 걸어 데우고, 의자를 갖춰 놓고, 가리개를 제자리에 두었다가, 돔비 씨가 들어서는 바로 그 순간에 발꿈치를 빙 돌려 그의 외투와 모자를 받아 걸었다. 그러고 나서 퍼치는 신문을 집어 들고 불 앞에서 손으로 한두 번 뒤집은 뒤, 공손하게 돔비 씨의 팔꿈치 곁에 놓았다. 그리고 퍼치는 더없이 극진하게 공손한 태도를 취하는 데 조금도 거리낌이 없어서, 만일 돔비 씨의 발치에 자기 몸을 엎드릴 수 있었다면, 혹은 칼리프 하룬 알라시드에게 바쳐지곤 했던 그런 칭호로 그를 부를 수 있었다면, 그는 더더욱 기뻐했을 것이다.\n\n이런 영예는 새로운 시도이자 실험이 될 터였으므로, 퍼치는 자기 태도로써 할 수 있는 한 잘 표현하는 것으로 만족할 수밖에 없었다. 당신은 제 눈의 빛이옵니다. 당신은 제 영혼의 숨결이옵니다. 당신은 충실한 퍼치의 사령관이시옵니다! 이런 불완전한 행복으로 마음을 달래며, 그는 문을 살며시 닫고 발끝으로 걸어 나가, 위대한 우두머리를 남겨 두었다—납지붕에 난 둥근 돔 모양 창문을 통해 흉한 굴뚝갓들과 집들의 뒤편이, 특히 이 층에 있는 이발관의 당돌한 창문이 그를 빤히 바라보도록. 그 창문 안에는 밀랍 인형이 하나 있었는데, 아침이면 무슬림처럼 대머리였다가, 한낮 열한 시가 지나면 최신 기독교식 유행에 따라 풍성한 머리카락과 구레나룻을 뒤집어쓴 채, 영원히 자기 머리의 잘못된 쪽을 그에게 보여 주었다.\n\n돔비 씨와, 바깥 사무실을 매개로 닿을 수 있는 보통 세상 사이에는—자기 방 안에 있는 돔비 씨의 존재는 바깥 사무실에 마치 습기나 찬 공기처럼 닿았다고 할 만했다—두 단계의 하강이 있었다. 자기 사무실에 있는 카커 씨가 첫 단계였고, 자기 사무실에 있는 모핀 씨가 둘째 단계였다. 이 두 신사는 저마다 욕실 같은 작은 방을 하나씩 차지했는데, 그 방들은 돔비 씨의 문 바깥 복도에서 통하게 되어 있었다. 카커 씨는 대신(大臣)으로서 술탄에게 가장 가까운 방에 살았다. 모핀 씨는 그보다 낮은 직책의 관리로서 서기들에게 가장 가까운 방에 살았다.\n\n마지막에 언급된 신사는 쾌활해 보이는, 개암빛 눈을 한 중년의 독신남이었다. 윗도리는 검은색으로 점잖게 차려입었고, 다리께는 후추소금빛 색으로 차려입었다. 그의 검은 머리는 여기저기 희끗희끗한 점이 막 박히기 시작했는데, 마치 세월의 발걸음이 그것을 튀겨 놓은 듯했고, 구레나룻은 이미 하얗게 셌다. 그는 돔비 씨를 대단히 존경하여 마땅한 경의를 표했다. 그러나 그 자신이 워낙 싹싹한 기질이었고 그 위엄 있는 존재 앞에서 결코 완전히 마음 편한 법이 없었으므로, 카커 씨가 누리는 수많은 회담을 두고 시샘 같은 것에 시달리지 않았으며, 오히려 그렇게 특별히 지목당해 그런 영예를 받는 일에 좀처럼 노출되지 않는 직무를 맡고 있다는 데서 은근한 만족을 느꼈다. 그는 나름대로 대단한 음악 애호가였다—일과를 마친 뒤에 말이다. 그리고 자기 첼로에 아비 같은 애정을 품고 있었는데, 그 첼로는 매주 한 번씩 그의 거처인 이즐링턴에서 은행 가까이의 어느 클럽 방으로 옮겨졌고, 그곳에서는 사적인 모임이 매주 수요일 저녁마다 더없이 괴롭고 고통스러운 성질의 사중주를 연주하곤 했다.\n\n카커 씨는 서른여덟이나 마흔쯤 된 신사로, 혈색이 불그스레했고, 번쩍이는 이가 두 줄로 빠짐없이 가지런했는데, 그 규칙성과 흰빛이 자못 거북할 정도였다. 그 이를 눈여겨보지 않을 도리가 없었으니, 그가 말할 때마다 그것을 드러냈기 때문이다. 그리고 그는 얼굴에 어찌나 활짝 웃음을 띠고 있던지(다만 그 웃음은 정말이지 좀처럼 입가를 넘어서는 일이 없었다), 거기에는 고양이가 으르렁대는 것 같은 무언가가 있었다. 그는 자기 우두머리를 본떠 빳빳한 흰 크라바트를 즐겨 매었고, 늘 단추를 꼭 채우고 옷을 단단히 차려입었다. 돔비 씨를 대하는 그의 태도는 깊이 고안된 것이었고 완벽하게 표현되어 있었다. 그는 두 사람 사이의 거리감을 극도로 의식하는 가운데 그를 허물없이 대했다. \"돔비 씨, 당신 같은 지위에 계신 분에게 저 같은 위치에 있는 자가 보일 수 있는 굴종의 모습이라면, 우리 사이의 업무 처리에 어울릴 만큼 충분하다고 제가 여길 만한 것은 없습니다. 솔직히 말씀드립니다, 나리, 저는 아예 그것을 포기합니다. 제 스스로의 마음을 만족시킬 수 없으리라 느끼니까요. 그리고 하늘은 아시겠지요, 돔비 씨, 당신께서는 그런 노력 따위 없이도 능히 지내실 만한 분이십니다.\" 만일 그가 이 말들을 벽보에 인쇄해 지니고 다니면서 외투 가슴팍에 붙여 끊임없이 돔비 씨가 읽도록 내밀었다 한들, 지금보다 더 명확할 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n이것이 지배인 카커였다. 동생 카커, 곧 월터의 친구는 그의 형제였다. 그보다 두세 살 위였으나 지위로는 한참 떨어져 있었다. 동생 쪽은 회사 사다리의 꼭대기에 자리했고, 형 쪽은 맨 밑바닥에 자리했다. 형은 결코 한 칸도 올라가지 못했고, 한 칸을 오르려고 발을 들지도 않았다. 젊은 사람들이 그의 머리 위를 지나 오르고 또 올랐다. 그러나 그는 늘 맨 밑바닥에 있었다. 그는 그 낮은 처지를 차지하는 데 완전히 체념해 있었다. 결코 그것을 불평하지 않았고, 거기서 벗어나기를 바라는 일도 분명 결코 없었다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 안녕하십니까?\" 어느 날 돔비 씨가 도착한 직후, 지배인 카커가 손에 서류 뭉치를 든 채 돔비 씨의 방으로 들어서며 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 카커?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"날이 좀 쌀쌀하군요!\" 카커가 불을 헤집으며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇구려,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 모두에게 그토록 중요한 그 어린 신사분에 관해 무슨 소식이라도 있습니까?\" 카커가 자기 이의 전 연대를 사열대에 세우듯 드러내며 물었다.\n\n\"있소—직접적인 소식은 아니지만—아주 잘 지낸다고 들었소,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. 그는 간밤에 브라이턴에서 올라온 터였다. 그러나 그것을 아는 이는 아무도 없었다.\n\n\"아주 잘 지내고, 의심할 바 없이 훌륭한 학자가 되어 가고 있겠지요?\" 지배인이 말했다.\n\n\"그러기를 바라오,\" 돔비 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"저런!\" 카커 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"세월이 빠릅니다!\"\n\n\"이따금 그런 생각이 들기도 하오,\" 돔비 씨가 신문을 흘끗 보며 대답했다.\n\n\"오! 당신께서요! 당신께는 그렇게 생각하실 까닭이 없지요,\" 카커가 말했다. \"당신처럼 그런 높은 자리에 앉아, 어느 철에나 동요 없이 거기 앉아 계실 수 있는 분이라면—세월의 흐름에 대해 무엇을 알 까닭이 별로 없습니다. 저 같은 사람들이지요, 낮은 데 있고 처지가 나을 것도 없으며, 세월이 흐르는 동안 새 주인을 물려받게 되는 그런 자들이야말로, 주위를 살필 이유가 있는 법입니다. 저도 머잖아 떠오르는 해를 떠받들게 되겠지요.\"\n\n\"아직 멀었소, 아직 멀었소, 카커!\" 돔비 씨가 의자에서 일어나 불에 등을 대고 서며 말했다. \"거기 나한테 줄 것이라도 있소?\"\n\n\"굳이 번거롭게 해 드릴 것까진 없을 듯합니다만,\" 카커가 손에 든 서류를 뒤적이며 대답했다. \"오늘 세 시에 위원회가 있으시다는 건 아시지요.\"\n\n\"세 시 사십오 분에도 하나 있고,\" 돔비 씨가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"당신이 무얼 잊는 일이 어디 있겠습니까!\" 카커가 여전히 서류를 뒤적이며 외쳤다. \"폴 도련님이 당신의 기억력을 물려받으신다면, 회사에서 꽤나 골칫거리 손님이 되시겠습니다. 당신 한 분으로도 충분하니까요.\"\n\n\"당신 자신도 정확한 기억력을 지녔잖소,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 제가요!\" 지배인이 대답했다. \"그건 저 같은 사람에게 유일한 자본이니까요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 벽난로 선반에 기댄 채, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 (물론 그 자신은 의식하지 못하는) 서기를 훑어보면서, 거만함이 조금도 덜하지도, 조금도 언짢아하지도 않은 모습이었다. 카커 씨 옷차림의 빳빳함과 단정함, 그리고 그에게 타고난 것이든 멀지 않은 본보기에서 흉내 낸 것이든 어떤 거만한 태도는, 그의 겸손함에 한층 큰 효과를 더했다. 그는 자기를 짓누르는 권력에 맞서 싸울 수 있다면 싸우려 들 사람이로되, 돔비 씨의 위대함과 우월함에 철저히 짓눌려 있는 사람처럼 보였다.\n\n\"모핀은 여기 있소?\" 돔비 씨가 잠시 사이를 둔 뒤 물었다. 그동안 카커 씨는 서류를 팔랑팔랑 넘기며 그 내용의 요점을 혼자 자그맣게 중얼거리고 있었다.\n\n\"모핀은 여기 있습니다,\" 그가 더없이 활짝, 거의 느닷없는 웃음을 띠며 고개를 들어 대답했다. \"음악적 회상을 흥얼거리고 있지요—제 짐작에 간밤 사중주 모임의 회상일 겁니다—우리 사이의 벽 너머로 말입니다, 저를 반쯤 미치게 하면서요. 그 첼로로 모닥불이나 피워서 거기다 악보책을 죄다 태워 버렸으면 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"카커, 당신은 그 누구도 존경하지 않는구려,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"안 그렇습니까?\" 카커가 다시 한번 활짝, 더없이 고양이 같은 모습으로 이를 드러내며 물었다. \"글쎄요! 많지는 않다고 봅니다. 아마 장담할 수는 없겠군요,\" 그가 마치 혼자 생각하는 것처럼 중얼거렸다, \"한 사람 말고는요.\"\n\n위험한 자질이다, 그것이 진짜라면. 그리고 그에 못지않게 위험한 자질이다, 꾸민 것이라 해도. 그러나 돔비 씨는 그렇게 여기는 것 같지 않았으니, 그는 여전히 불에 등을 댄 채 한껏 키를 곧추세우고 서서, 평소보다 더 강한 잠재적 권력의 감각이 숨어 있는 듯한 위엄 있는 침착함으로 수석 서기를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"모핀 이야기가 나왔으니 말입니다만,\" 카커 씨가 나머지에서 서류 한 장을 꺼내며 말을 이었다. \"바베이도스 대리점에서 하급 직원 하나가 죽었다고 보고하면서, 그 후임을 위해 '아들과 후계자호'에 자리 하나를 예약해 두자고 제안합니다—그 배는 한 달쯤 뒤에 출항합니다. 누가 가든 상관없으시지요? 여기엔 그런 부류에 맞는 사람이 없으니까요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 더없이 무관심하게 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그리 대단한 자리는 아닙니다,\" 카커 씨가 펜을 집어 들어 서류 뒷면에 기록을 적으며 말했다. \"그가 그 자리를 어느 음악 친구의 고아가 된 조카한테나 베풀어 주었으면 합니다. 혹 그 아이에게 그런 재주가 있다면, 그러면 바이올린 켜는 일은 그만두게 될지도 모르지요. 거기 누구요? 들어오시오!\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 카커 씨. 여기 계신 줄 몰랐습니다, 나리,\" 월터가 손에 개봉하지 않은, 새로 도착한 편지 몇 통을 들고 나타나며 대답했다. \"동생 카커 씨가요, 나리—\"\n\n이 이름이 거론되자, 지배인 카커 씨는 수치와 굴욕에 정통으로 찔린 듯했다, 아니면 그런 척했다. 그는 달라진, 사죄하는 듯한 눈빛으로 돔비 씨를 똑바로 바라보았다가, 그 눈을 바닥으로 떨구고는 잠시 말없이 서 있었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 말이지,\" 그가 월터에게 홱 돌아서며 느닷없이 성난 목소리로 말했다, \"자네는 전에 동생 카커 씨를 자네 대화에 끌어들이지 말라고 부탁받았던 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"죄송합니다,\" 월터가 대답했다. \"다만 동생 카커 씨가 제게 당신께서 외출하신 것 같다고 하셔서요, 그래서 한 말씀 드리려던 것뿐입니다. 그렇지 않았다면, 당신께서 돔비 씨와 일을 보고 계실 때 문을 두드리지는 않았을 겁니다. 이건 돔비 씨께 온 편지들입니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"좋아,\" 지배인 카커 씨가 그의 손에서 편지들을 홱 낚아채며 대답했다. \"자네 일이나 보러 가게.\"\n\n그런데 그렇게 격식 없이 편지를 받아 드는 통에, 카커 씨는 한 통을 바닥에 떨어뜨렸고, 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지 보지 못했다. 돔비 씨 또한 자기 발치께에 떨어진 편지를 알아채지 못했다. 월터는 두 사람 중 누군가가 그것을 알아차리려니 생각하며 잠시 머뭇거렸다. 그러나 둘 다 그러지 않는 것을 보고는, 멈춰 서서 되돌아와 그것을 주워, 손수 돔비 씨의 책상 위에 놓았다. 그 편지들은 우편으로 온 것이었는데, 공교롭게도 문제의 그 편지는 핍친 부인의 정기 보고서였고, 늘 그렇듯—핍친 부인이 글솜씨가 시원찮은 까닭에—플로렌스가 받는 이를 적은 것이었다. 돔비 씨는 월터가 이 편지에 말없이 자기 주의를 끈 것에 흠칫 놀라, 마치 그가 일부러 나머지 가운데서 그것을 골라낸 것이라 믿기라도 하는 듯, 그를 사납게 노려보았다.\n\n\"방에서 나가도 좋네, 자네!\" 돔비 씨가 거만하게 말했다.\n\n그는 편지를 손안에서 구겼다. 그리고 월터가 문밖으로 나가는 것을 지켜본 뒤, 봉인을 뜯지도 않은 채 그것을 주머니에 집어넣었다.\n\n\"동생 카커 씨를 이렇게 끊임없이 언급하는 것은,\" 둘만 남게 되자마자 지배인 카커 씨가 입을 열었다, \"저 같은 위치에 있는 자에게는, 더구나 당신 같은 분 앞에서 거론될 때는, 말할 수 없이 괴로운 일입니다—\"\n\n\"허튼소리요, 카커,\" 돔비 씨가 가로막았다. \"당신은 너무 예민하오.\"\n\n\"저는 예민합니다,\" 그가 대답했다. \"만일 당신 같은 위치에 계신 분이 어찌어찌하여 제 처지에 자기를 놓고 상상해 보실 수만 있다면—그건 불가능한 일이지만—당신께서도 그러실 겁니다.\"\n\n돔비 씨의 생각이 분명 다른 어떤 주제를 좇고 있었으므로, 그의 분별 있는 동맹자는 여기서 말을 끊고, 그가 고개를 들 때 그에게 내보일 이를 준비한 채 서 있었다.\n\n\"서인도제도로 보낼 사람이 필요하다고 하지 않았소,\" 돔비 씨가 서둘러 말했다.\n\n\"예,\" 카커가 대답했다.\n\n\"게이 그 젊은이를 보내시오.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 정말 아주 좋습니다. 그보다 쉬운 일도 없지요,\" 카커 씨가 조금도 놀라는 기색 없이, 앞서와 똑같이 태연하게 펜을 집어 들어 서류에 다시 기록하며 말했다. \"'게이 젊은이를 보낼 것.'\"\n\n\"그를 다시 부르시오,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n카커 씨가 잽싸게 그렇게 했고, 월터가 잽싸게 돌아왔다.\n\n\"게이,\" 돔비 씨가 어깨 너머로 그를 보려고 몸을 조금 돌리며 말했다. \"여기 한 가지—\"\n\n\"자리가 났네,\" 카커 씨가 입을 한껏 벌리며 말했다.\n\n\"서인도제도에 말일세. 바베이도스에. 자네를 보낼 작정이네,\" 돔비 씨가 있는 그대로의 사실을 꾸미는 것은 천하다는 듯 말했다, \"바베이도스 회계실의 하급 자리를 채우라고 말일세. 내가 자네를 서인도제도로 보내기로 정했다고 자네 삼촌에게 내 말로 전하게.\"\n\n월터는 놀라움에 숨이 완전히 막혀, \"서인도제도\"라는 말을 따라 할 만큼의 숨도 겨우 찾을 수 있었다.\n\n\"누군가는 가야 하네,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"그리고 자네는 젊고 건강하며, 자네 삼촌의 형편은 좋지 않으니까. 자네 삼촌에게 자네가 임명되었다고 전하게. 당장 가는 건 아닐세. 한 달—어쩌면 두 달쯤—간격이 있을 거네.\"\n\n\"제가 거기 머무는 것입니까, 나리?\" 월터가 물었다.\n\n\"자네가 거기 머무느냐고, 자네!\" 돔비 씨가 그를 향해 조금 더 돌아서며 되풀이했다. \"무슨 소린가? 저 친구가 무슨 소리를 하는 건가, 카커?\"\n\n\"거기서 사는 것입니까, 나리,\" 월터가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"물론이지,\" 돔비 씨가 대답했다.\n\n월터는 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"그게 전부일세,\" 돔비 씨가 다시 편지로 눈을 돌리며 말했다. \"물론 자네가 적당한 때에 통상적인 채비며 그런 것들에 대해 그에게 설명해 주게, 카커. 그는 더 기다릴 것 없네, 카커.\"\n\n\"자넨 더 기다릴 것 없네, 게이,\" 카커 씨가 잇몸까지 드러내며 말했다.\n\n\"다만,\" 돔비 씨가 편지에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 읽기를 멈추고, 귀를 기울이는 듯하며 말했다. \"다만 그가 무슨 할 말이 있다면 모르지.\"\n\n\"없습니다, 나리,\" 월터가 대답했다. 그는 동요하고 혼란스러웠으며, 거의 멍해져 있었으니, 무수히 다양한 그림들이 그의 마음속에 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그 가운데서도 번들거리는 모자를 쓴 커틀 선장이 맥스팅어 부인 댁에서 놀라움에 못 박혀 있는 모습, 그리고 삼촌이 작은 뒷방 응접실에서 그를 잃은 슬픔에 잠겨 있는 모습이 두드러진 자리를 차지했다. \"저는 잘 모르겠습니다—저는—대단히 감사합니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"그는 더 기다릴 것 없네, 카커,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n그리고 카커 씨가 다시 그 말을 따라 하고, 자기도 나갈 양으로 서류를 챙기자, 월터는 자기가 더 오래 머무는 것이—더구나 할 말도 없는 마당에—용서받지 못할 무례가 되리라 느꼈다. 그래서 그는 완전히 어리둥절한 채로 걸어 나갔다.\n\n복도를 따라가면서, 꿈을 꿀 때처럼 또렷한 의식과 무력함이 뒤섞인 채로, 그는 카커 씨가 나오면서 돔비 씨의 문이 다시 닫히는 소리를 들었다. 그리고 곧이어 그 신사가 그를 불렀다.\n\n\"괜찮다면 자네 친구 동생 카커 씨를 내 방으로 데려오게, 자네.\"\n\n월터는 바깥 사무실로 가서 동생 카커 씨에게 자기 심부름을 알렸다. 그러자 그는 한쪽 구석에 홀로 앉아 있던 칸막이 뒤에서 나와, 그와 함께 지배인 카커 씨의 방으로 돌아갔다.\n\n그 신사는 불에 등을 대고, 두 손을 외투 자락 밑에 넣은 채, 흰 크라바트 너머로, 돔비 씨 자신이 지을 법한 만큼이나 가망 없는 표정으로 내려다보며 서 있었다. 그는 자세를 조금도 바꾸지도, 그 거칠고 어두운 표정을 누그러뜨리지도 않은 채 그들을 맞이했다. 다만 월터에게 문을 닫으라고 손짓했을 뿐이다.\n\n\"존 카커,\" 그렇게 되자, 지배인이 자기 형제에게 홱 돌아서며 말했다. 마치 그를 물어뜯기라도 할 것처럼 두 줄의 이가 곤두서 있었다. \"당신과 이 젊은이 사이에 무슨 결탁이 있길래, 내가 당신 이름이 거론되는 것에 시달리고 쫓겨다녀야 하는 거요? 존 카커, 내가 당신의 가까운 혈육이고 그것에서 나를 떼어 낼 수 없다는 것만으로는 당신에게 부족하단 말이오, 그—\"\n\n\"치욕이라고 하게, 제임스,\" 상대가 그가 단어를 찾느라 더듬거리는 것을 알아채고 낮은 목소리로 끼어들었다. \"자네는 그걸 말하려는 거고, 그럴 만한 이유도 있지, 치욕이라고 하게.\"\n\n\"그 치욕에서 말이오,\" 그의 형제가 날카롭게 힘주어 동의했다, \"그러나 그 사실이 회사 바로 그 한복판에서 함부로 불쑥 내뱉어지고, 나발 불듯 떠벌려지고, 끊임없이 공표되어야 하겠소? 그것도 비밀스러운 순간에 말이오? 존 카커, 당신은 당신 이름이 이곳에서 신뢰며 믿음과 조화를 이루기에 알맞다고 생각하시오?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 상대가 대답했다. \"아닐세, 제임스. 하느님은 아시겠지만 나는 그런 생각은 하지 않네.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신 생각은 뭐요?\" 그의 형제가 말했다, \"그리고 당신은 왜 내 앞길에 끼어드는 거요? 이미 나를 충분히 해치지 않았소?\"\n\n\"나는 결코 일부러 자네를 해친 적이 없네, 제임스.\"\n\n\"당신은 내 형제요,\" 지배인이 말했다. \"그것만으로도 충분한 해악이지.\"\n\n\"내가 그것을 되돌릴 수 있다면 좋으련만, 제임스.\"\n\n\"당신이 그럴 수 있고 또 그러기를 바라오.\"\n\n이 대화가 오가는 동안, 월터는 고통과 놀라움에 차서 두 형제를 번갈아 바라보았다. 나이로는 형이요 회사에서는 아우인 이는, 눈을 바닥에 떨구고 고개를 숙인 채, 다른 이의 비난을 겸허히 듣고 서 있었다. 그 비난들은 그것에 동반된 어조와 눈빛 때문에, 또 그것에 그토록 놀라고 충격받은 월터의 존재 때문에 몹시 쓰라리게 들렸으나, 그는 다른 항의는 일절 내놓지 않고, 그저 오른손을 살짝 들어 만류하는 듯한 몸짓으로 마치 \"나를 좀 봐주게!\"라고 말하려는 듯할 뿐이었다. 그리하여, 만일 그것이 매질이었고 그가 강한 억압 아래 놓인, 육체적 고통으로 쇠약해진 용감한 사내였더라면, 그는 사형 집행인 앞에 그렇게 서 있었을 것이다.\n\n모든 감정에 있어 너그럽고 빠른 월터는, 자기가 이 조롱의 무고한 빌미라 여기고는, 이제 자기가 느끼는 진심을 다해 끼어들었다.\n\n\"카커 씨,\" 그가 지배인을 향해 말했다. \"정말이지, 정말이지, 이건 오로지 제 잘못입니다. 일종의 경솔함 때문에—제 스스로를 아무리 탓해도 모자랄 그 경솔함 때문에—저는, 틀림없이, 필요 이상으로 훨씬 자주 동생 카커 씨를 언급했습니다. 그리고 때로는 당신께서 분명히 바라지 않으신 데도 그분의 이름이 제 입에서 새어 나오도록 했습니다. 그러나 그건 제 잘못이었습니다, 나리. 우리는 그 일에 관해 단 한마디도 주고받은 적이 없습니다—사실 어떤 주제든 거의 주고받지 않았습니다. 그리고 그것은,\" 월터가 잠시 사이를 두었다가 덧붙였다, \"전부 제 경솔함만은 아니었습니다, 나리. 왜냐하면 저는 여기 온 이래로 줄곧 카커 씨에게 관심을 느껴 왔고, 그분을 그토록 많이 생각하다 보니 이따금 그분에 대해 말하지 않을 수 없었기 때문입니다!\"\n\n월터는 이 말을 영혼으로부터, 명예의 바로 그 숨결로 했다. 그는 숙인 머리와, 내리뜬 눈과, 들어 올린 손을 바라보며 생각했던 것이다. \"나는 그것을 느꼈다. 그러니 이 의지할 데 없고 무너진 사람을 위해, 내가 그것을 밝히지 못할 까닭이 어디 있는가!\"\n\n지배인 카커 씨는 그가 말하는 동안, 그리고 그가 말을 마쳤을 때, 얼굴을 두 쪽으로 갈라 놓는 듯한 웃음을 띠고 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"자네는 흥분 잘하는 젊은이로군, 게이,\" 그가 말했다. \"그러니 이제 좀 가라앉히도록 애써야 하네, 열에 들뜬 기질을 부추기는 건 현명치 못한 일이니까. 되도록 냉정하게, 게이. 되도록 냉정하게. 자네는 존 카커 씨 본인에게 물어볼 수도 있었을 텐데(아직 안 물어봤다면 말이지), 그가 그토록 강한 관심의 대상이라고 주장하는지, 또는 정말 그런 대상인지를 말일세.\"\n\n\"제임스, 나를 공정하게 대해 주게,\" 그의 형제가 말했다. \"나는 아무것도 주장하지 않았고, 아무것도 주장하지 않네. 믿어 주게, 내—\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고?\" 그의 형제가 불을 쬐며 또 한 번 웃음을 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"내 자신을 걸고—내 무너진 인생을 걸고!\" 상대가 똑같이 낮은 목소리로, 그러나 지금껏 그가 낼 수 있어 보이던 것보다 더 깊이 말에 힘을 실어 대답했다. \"믿어 주게, 나는 거리를 두어 왔고, 홀로 지내 왔네. 이건 내가 바란 일이 아니야. 나는 그를, 그리고 모두를 피해 왔네.\n\n\"정말이지, 당신은 저를 피해 오셨습니다, 카커 씨,\" 월터가 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 말했다. 그만큼 그의 연민은 진실했다. \"저는 그것을 압니다, 실망과 안타까움 속에서요. 제가 처음 여기 왔을 때부터, 그리고 그 이후로 줄곧, 저는 분명 제 나이로 감히 그럴 수 있는 만큼 당신의 벗이 되려고 애썼습니다. 그러나 그건 아무 소용이 없었습니다.\n\n\"그리고 명심하게,\" 지배인이 그의 말을 잽싸게 받아 말했다, \"그건 더더욱 소용없는 일이 될 걸세, 게이, 만일 자네가 존 카커 씨의 이름을 사람들의 주의에 끈질기게 들이민다면 말이지. 그건 존 카커 씨를 위하는 길이 아닐세. 그것이 위하는 길인지 그에게 물어보게.\"\n\n\"그건 나를 위하는 일이 아닐세,\" 그의 형제가 말했다. \"그건 그저 지금 같은 대화로 이어질 뿐이고, 그런 건 충분히 없어도 좋았다는 말을 굳이 할 필요도 없네. 그 누구도 내게 더 나은 벗이 될 수는 없어.\" 그는 마치 월터에게 그것을 새겨 주려는 듯, 여기서 아주 또박또박 말했다. \"나를 잊어 주고, 묻지도 주목하지도 않은 채 내 길을 가도록 내버려 두는 것보다 말이지.\"\n\n\"자네는 남이 일러 준 것을 잘 간직하는 기억력은 아니니까, 게이,\" 지배인 카커 씨가 크고 한층 더한 만족 속에 불을 쬐며 말했다, \"자네가 이것을 가장 확실한 권위로부터 듣는 게 좋겠다 싶었네.\" 그러면서 그는 자기 형제를 향해 고갯짓을 했다. \"이제는 잊지 않겠지, 그러기를 바라네. 그게 전부일세, 게이. 자넨 가도 좋네.\"\n\n월터는 문밖으로 나가, 뒤로 문을 닫으려던 참이었다. 그런데 다시 두 형제의 목소리가 들려오고, 또 자기 자신의 이름이 거론되는 것이 들려오자, 그는 손을 자물쇠에 댄 채 문을 빠끔히 열어 둔 채로, 돌아가야 할지 떠나야 할지 마음을 정하지 못하고 머뭇거리며 섰다. 이런 상태에서 그는 뒤이어진 말을 엿듣지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"나를 좀 더 너그럽게 생각해 주게, 그럴 수 있다면, 제임스,\" 존 카커가 말했다, \"내 말하건대, 나는—내 가슴에 새겨진 이런 내력을 지니고서 어찌 안 그럴 수 있었겠나\"—그는 자기 가슴을 치며 말했다—\"저 소년, 월터 게이를 지켜보면서 내 온 마음이 깨어났다네. 그가 처음 여기 왔을 때 나는 그에게서 거의 또 다른 나 자신을 보았어.\"\n\n\"또 다른 자네 자신이라!\" 지배인이 경멸하듯 되풀이했다.\n\n\"지금의 나가 아니라, 나 역시 처음 여기 왔을 때의 나 말일세. 그처럼 혈기왕성하고, 들떠 있고, 젊고, 미숙하며, 그와 똑같은 안절부절못하고 모험심에 찬 공상에 달아올라 있었고, 똑같은 자질로 가득 찬, 선으로도 악으로도 이끌어 갈 똑같은 가능성을 품은 나 말이야.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않기를 바라네,\" 그의 형제가 어조 속에 어떤 감추어진 비꼬는 뜻을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 나를 날카롭게 찌르는군. 그리고 자네 손은 흔들림이 없고, 그 찌름은 매우 깊네,\" 상대가 대답했다. 그는 (적어도 월터가 보기엔) 마치 말하는 동안 어떤 잔인한 무기가 실제로 자기를 찔러 대기라도 하는 듯 말했다. \"나는 그가 소년이었을 때 이 모든 것을 상상했네. 나는 그것을 믿었어. 내게 그것은 진실이었네. 나는 그가, 그토록 많은 다른 이들이 똑같이 들떠서 걷는, 그리고 거기서—보이지 않는 심연의 가장자리를 가뿐히 걷고 있는 것을 보았네.\"\n\n\"늘 하던 그 핑계로군,\" 그의 형제가 불을 헤집으며 가로막았다. \"그토록 많은 이들이라. 계속하게. 그토록 많은 이들이 떨어진다고 말하게.\"\n\n\"거기서 한 나그네가 떨어졌네,\" 상대가 대답했다, \"그도 그와 같은 소년으로 제 길을 떠났다가, 점점 더 발을 헛디뎌, 조금씩 조금씩 더 아래로 미끄러졌고, 머리부터 곤두박질쳐 떨어져 무너진 인간으로 자기 자신을 발견하기까지 계속 비틀거리며 나아갔지. 내가 그 소년을 지켜볼 때 무엇을 견뎠을지 생각해 보게.\"\n\n\"그건 자네가 자초한 일이니 자네 자신을 탓할 수밖에 없지,\" 그의 형제가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"오직 나 자신을,\" 그가 한숨을 쉬며 동의했다. \"나는 그 책망이나 수치를 나누려는 게 아닐세.\"\n\n\"자네는 그 수치를 나눠 가졌지,\" 제임스 카커가 이 사이로 중얼거렸다. 그리고 그토록 많고 그토록 빽빽한 이 사이로, 그는 능히 잘 중얼거릴 수 있었다.\n\n\"아, 제임스,\" 그의 형제가 처음으로 책망의 어조로 말하며, 그 목소리의 울림으로 보아 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 듯 대답했다, \"나는 그 이후로 자네에게 쓸모 있는 받침대가 되어 주었네. 자네는 위로 기어오르면서 나를 마음껏 밟고 디뎠지. 자네 발뒤꿈치로 나를 짓밟지는 말게!\"\n\n침묵이 뒤따랐다. 얼마 뒤, 지배인 카커 씨가 마치 이 만남을 끝맺기로 작정한 듯 서류를 부스럭거리는 소리가 들렸다. 동시에 그의 형제는 문 쪽으로 더 가까이 물러섰다.\n\n\"그게 전부일세,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 그토록 떨면서, 그토록 두려워하면서 그를 지켜보았네—그건 내게 얼마간의 작은 벌이었지—그가 내가 처음 떨어졌던 그 자리를 지나갈 때까지 말이야. 그러고 나서는, 비록 내가 그의 아비였다 한들, 나는 그보다 더 경건하게 하느님께 감사할 수는 없었으리라 믿네. 나는 감히 그에게 경고하고 충고하지 못했네. 그러나 만일 직접적인 까닭을 보았더라면, 나는 그에게 내 본보기를 보여 주었을 걸세. 나는 그와 이야기하는 것이 남의 눈에 띌까 두려웠네—내가 그를 해치고, 악으로 유혹하고, 타락시킨다고 여겨질까 봐, 아니면 정말 내가 그렇게 할까 봐. 내게는 그런 전염성이 있을지도 모르지. 나는 모르네. 내 내력을 젊은 월터 게이와 연관 지어, 그리고 그가 내게 느끼게 한 것과 연결 지어 짜 맞추어 보게. 그리고 나를 좀 더 너그럽게 생각해 주게, 제임스, 그럴 수 있다면.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 월터가 서 있는 곳으로 나왔다. 그는 거기 있는 그를 보고 얼굴이 조금 창백해졌고, 월터가 그의 손을 붙들고 속삭이듯 말하자 더욱 창백해졌다.\n\n\"카커 씨, 부디 제가 당신께 감사드리게 해 주십시오! 제가 당신을 얼마나 안타깝게 여기는지 말하게 해 주십시오! 이 모든 일의 불행한 빌미가 된 것이 제게 얼마나 미안한지를요! 제가 이제 당신을 얼마나 제 보호자요 후견인처럼 여기게 되었는지를요! 제가 당신께 얼마나, 정말이지 얼마나 깊이 은혜를 느끼고 당신을 가엾이 여기는지를요!\" 월터가 그의 두 손을 꼭 움켜쥐며, 동요한 나머지 자기가 무엇을 하고 무슨 말을 하는지도 거의 알지 못한 채 말했다.\n\n모핀 씨의 방이 바로 가까이 있고 비어 있는 데다 문이 활짝 열려 있었으므로, 두 사람은 약속이라도 한 듯 그리로 옮겨 갔다. 복도는 누군가 오가는 일이 좀처럼 끊이지 않았기 때문이다. 그곳에 이르러, 월터가 카커 씨의 얼굴에서 속에 든 감정의 흔적을 보았을 때, 그는 마치 그 얼굴을 전에 본 적이 없는 것 같은 느낌마저 들었다. 그만큼 그것은 크게 변해 있었다.\n\n\"월터,\" 그가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"나는 자네에게서 멀리 떨어져 있고, 앞으로도 늘 그러기를 바라네. 자네는 내가 어떤 사람인지 아는가?\"\n\n\"당신이 어떤 사람인지요!\"라는 말이 월터의 입술에 매달려 있는 듯했다, 그가 그를 주의 깊게 바라보는 동안.\n\n\"그것은 시작되었네,\" 카커가 말했다, \"내 스물한 번째 생일이 되기 전에—훨씬 전부터 그리로 이끌려 갔지만, 그때 가까이 가서야 시작되었지. 나는 성년이 되었을 때 그들의 것을 훔쳤네. 그 후로도 훔쳤어. 내 스물두 번째 생일이 되기 전에 그것이 죄다 발각되었지. 그러고 나서, 월터, 나는 모든 사람들의 사회에서 죽었네.\"\n\n다시 그의 마지막 몇 마디가 월터의 입술에 떨리며 매달렸으나, 그는 그 말도, 자기 자신의 말도 한마디 입 밖에 낼 수 없었다.\n\n\"회사는 내게 무척 너그러웠네. 그 노인의 관용에 하늘이 보답하시기를! 이 사람 또한, 그의 아들 또한, 당시 막 회사에 들어온 사람이었는데—거기서 나는 큰 신임을 받고 있었지! 나는 지금 그의 방이 된 그 방으로 불려 갔네—나는 그 이후로 한 번도 거기 들어간 적이 없네—그리고 자네가 아는 이 모습으로 나왔지. 여러 해 동안 나는 지금의 내 자리에, 지금처럼 홀로 앉아 있었네, 다만 그때는 다른 이들에게 잘 알려지고 인정된 본보기였지. 그들은 모두 내게 자비를 베풀었고, 그래서 나는 살아남았네. 세월이 내 가엾은 속죄의 그 부분을 바꾸어 놓았지. 그래서 내 생각엔, 회사의 세 우두머리를 빼고는, 이곳에 내 사연을 제대로 아는 이는 아무도 없네. 그 어린 사내아이가 자라나 그 이야기를 듣게 되기 전에, 내 구석 자리는 비게 될지도 모르지. 차라리 그렇게 되었으면 싶네! 이것이 그날 이후로 내게 일어난 유일한 변화일세—내가 모든 젊음과 희망과 선한 이들의 벗을 저 방 안에 두고 떠나온 그날 이후로 말이야. 하느님께서 자네에게 복 주시기를, 월터! 자네와, 자네에게 소중한 모든 이를 정직 속에 지켜 주시기를, 그러지 못할 바엔 그들을 쳐서 죽게 하시기를!\"\n\n그가 마치 지독한 추위에 떨듯 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨던 것과, 울음을 터뜨리던 것에 대한 어떤 기억이, 두 사람 사이에 정확히 무슨 일이 오갔는지 떠올리려 애썼을 때 월터가 거기에 보탤 수 있는 전부였다.\n\n월터가 그를 다음에 보았을 때, 그는 예전처럼 말없이, 축 늘어진, 풀죽은 모습으로 자기 책상 위에 몸을 굽히고 있었다. 그러자 그가 일하는 모습을 지켜보며, 그가 분명 두 사람 사이에 더 이상의 교류가 생기지 않도록 단단히 결심했음을 느끼면서, 그리고 그 짧은 시간 동안 그날 아침 두 카커 형제의 내력과 관련해 보고 들은 모든 것을 거듭 되새기면서, 월터는 자기가 서인도제도행 명령을 받았다는 것, 그리고 머잖아 솔 삼촌과 커틀 선장에게서, 또 플로렌스 돔비를—아니, 폴을 말하는 것이었다—드물게 어쩌다 한 번씩 보던 그 모습에서, 그리고 자기가 일상 속에서 사랑하고 좋아하며 기다리던 모든 것에서 떠나가게 되리라는 것을 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다.\n\n그러나 그것은 사실이었고, 그 소식은 이미 바깥 사무실까지 스며들어 있었다. 그가 무거운 마음으로 앉아 이런 일들을 곱씹으며 팔에 머리를 괴고 있을 때, 전령 퍼치가 자기 마호가니 받침 선반에서 내려와 그의 팔꿈치를 슬쩍 건드리며 양해를 구하더니, 귀에 대고 한마디 하고 싶다고 했다. 그가 보기엔, 다음번 산후조리에서 회복하는 동안 퍼치 부인이 직접 먹게끔, 절인 생강 한 단지를 싸게 영국으로 부쳐 줄 수 있도록 손을 좀 써 줄 수 있겠느냐는 것이었다.\n\n한여름 방학이 다가왔을 때, 블림버 박사의 학교에 모인, 눈빛이 흐리멍덩한 어린 신사들 사이에서는 기쁨의 점잖지 못한 표출 같은 것은 조금도 보이지 않았다. \"학교 파(罷)\" 같은 그런 격한 표현은 이 품위 있는 학원에는 전혀 어울리지 않았으리라. 어린 신사들은 반년마다 자기네 집으로 스며 나갔다. 그러나 그들은 결코 학교를 \"파\"하지 않았다. 그런 행동은 경멸했을 것이다.\n\n토저는 빳빳하게 풀먹인 흰 케임브릭 목도리에 늘 쓸리고 시달렸는데, 그것은 그의 부모인 토저 부인의 명확한 바람에 따라 두른 것이었다. 토저 부인은 아들을 성직자로 만들 작정이었던지라, 그렇게 미리 준비된 상태에 일찌감치 들어서는 것이 결코 이르지 않다는 의견이었다. 토저는 사실 두 가지 악 가운데 골라야 한다면 집에 가느니 차라리 여기 남는 편이 낫겠다고 말했다. 이 선언이 그 주제에 관한 토저의 작문 가운데, \"집 생각과 그에 얽힌 온갖 추억은 그의 마음속에 기대와 기쁨이라는 가장 흐뭇한 정서를 일깨운다\"고 적은 대목과, 또 자신을 이세니족을 막 무찌른 승리에 들떴거나 카르타고의 전리품을 잔뜩 짊어진 채 카피톨리누스—이 비유의 목적상 토저 부인의 거처라고 미리 상정해 둔—를 몇 시간의 행군 거리 안까지 진격해 가는 로마 장군에 견준 대목과는 아무리 모순되어 보일지라도, 그래도 그것은 매우 진심에서 나온 말이었다. 왜냐하면 토저에게는 무시무시한 삼촌이 하나 있는 듯했는데, 그 삼촌은 방학 중에 난해한 논점들을 두고 그에게 자청해서 시험을 보일 뿐 아니라, 죄 없는 사건이며 사물들을 비틀고 쥐어짜 같은 흉악한 목적에 갖다 붙였기 때문이다. 그래서 만일 이 삼촌이 그를 연극 구경에 데려가거나, 혹은 비슷하게 친절을 가장한 핑계로 거인이나 난쟁이나 마술사나 그 무엇이든 보러 데려가면, 토저는 삼촌이 그 주제에 관한 고전 인용을 미리 읽고 왔음을 알았기에 죽을 듯한 두려움에 빠지곤 했다. 삼촌이 어디서 그것을 터뜨릴지, 또 자기를 상대로 어떤 전거를 들이댈지 도무지 예측할 수 없었던 까닭이다.\n\n브리그스로 말하자면, 그 아버지는 책략 같은 것을 꾸미는 시늉조차 하지 않았다. 그는 결코 아들을 가만히 내버려두지 않았다. 그 불운한 소년이 방학 동안 겪는 정신적 시련이 어찌나 숱하고 가혹했던지, 그 집안의 친구들은(당시 런던 베이스워터 근처에 살았다) 켄싱턴 가든의 장식용 연못에 다가갈 때면 거의 언제나 막연히, 브리그스 도련님의 모자가 수면에 떠다니고 미처 끝내지 못한 연습 문제가 둑에 놓여 있는 광경을 보게 되지 않을까 하는 기대를 품곤 했다. 그러니 브리그스는 방학이라는 주제에 대해 조금도 낙관적이지 않았다. 그리고 어린 폴의 침실을 함께 쓰는 이 두 동무는 어린 신사들 전반의 매우 적절한 표본이어서, 그들 가운데 가장 탄력 있는 축조차 그 축제 같은 시기의 도래를 점잖은 체념으로 바라보았다.\n\n어린 폴은 사정이 전혀 달랐다. 이 첫 방학이 끝날 무렵이면 그는 플로렌스와 헤어지게 될 터였지만, 아직 시작되지도 않은 방학의 끝을 미리 내다보는 사람이 어디 있겠는가! 폴은 분명 아니었다. 그 행복한 때가 가까워지자 침실 벽을 기어오르던 사자와 호랑이들은 아주 온순하고 장난스러워졌다. 바닥 깔개의 네모와 마름모 무늬 속에 음흉하게 도사린 험상궂은 얼굴들도 누그러져, 한결 덜 사악한 눈으로 그를 빠끔 내다보았다. 근엄한 낡은 시계는 그 격식 차린 물음의 어조에 더 많은 개인적 관심을 담았고, 쉴 새 없는 바다는 밤새도록 굽이쳐 흐르며 구슬픈—그러나 그래도 기분 좋은—가락을 울렸으니, 그 가락은 물결과 더불어 솟았다 가라앉으며 그를 말하자면 흔들어 잠재워 주었다.\n\n문학사 피더 선생도 자기 역시 방학을 무척 즐기게 되리라 여기는 듯했다. 투츠 씨는 그때부터 죽 방학 같은 삶을 살아갈 작정이었다. 그가 날마다 어김없이 폴에게 알려 주었듯이, 이번이 블림버 박사 학교에서의 \"마지막 학기\"였고, 곧장 자기 재산을 물려받기 시작할 참이었기 때문이다.\n\n폴과 투츠 씨 사이에서는, 나이와 신분의 거리에도 불구하고 둘이 친밀한 벗이라는 사실이 완벽하게 양해되어 있었다. 방학이 다가오면서 투츠 씨가 폴과 함께 있을 때 전보다 더 가쁘게 숨을 쉬고 더 자주 멍하니 바라보았는데, 폴은 그것이 둘이 서로 못 보게 되는 것을 그가 아쉬워한다는 뜻임을 알았고, 그의 후원과 호의에 무척 고마움을 느꼈다. 게다가 블림버 박사와 블림버 부인과 블림버 양은 물론 어린 신사들 전반에게도, 투츠가 어쩌다 보니 돔비의 보호자이자 후견인을 자처하고 나섰다는 것이 양해되어 있었고, 그 사정은 피프친 부인에게까지 알려질 만큼 널리 소문이 나서, 그 마음씨 좋은 노파는 투츠에 대해 쓰라림과 질투의 감정을 품게 되었다. 그래서 자기 집이라는 성역 안에서는 그를 \"멍청한 얼간이\"라며 거듭 비난했다. 그러나 순진한 투츠는 피프친 부인의 분노를 살 생각이라곤, 다른 어떤 분명한 가능성이나 명제만큼이나 조금도 없었다. 오히려 그는 그녀를 흥미로운 점이 많은, 꽤 놀라운 인물로 여기는 쪽이었다. 이런 까닭에 그는 그녀가 어린 폴을 방문하는 동안 어찌나 정중하게 미소 지으며 안부를 자주 물었던지, 마침내 어느 날 밤 그녀가 그에게 대놓고 말하기를, 그가 어떻게 생각하든 자기는 그런 데 익숙지 않으며, 그에게서든 당시 살아 있는 그 어떤 풋내기에게서든 그런 것을 참을 수 없고 또 참지 않겠다고 했다. 자신의 정중함에 대한 이 뜻밖의 응답에 투츠 씨는 어찌나 놀랐던지, 그녀가 떠날 때까지 외진 구석에 몸을 숨겼다. 그리고 다시는 블림버 박사의 지붕 아래에서 그 억센 피프친 부인과 맞닥뜨리지 않았다.\n\n방학이 두세 주쯤 남았을 무렵, 어느 날 코닐리어 블림버가 폴을 자기 방으로 불러 말했다. \"돔비, 네 분석표를 집으로 보낼 거란다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생님,\" 폴이 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 말을 하는지 알겠니, 돔비?\" 블림버 양이 안경 너머로 그를 뚫어지게 보며 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 선생님,\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n\"돔비, 돔비,\" 블림버 양이 말했다, \"네가 딱한 아이가 아닐까 슬슬 걱정되는구나. 어떤 표현의 뜻을 모르면, 왜 정보를 구하지 않니?\"\n\n\"피프친 부인이 저더러 질문을 하면 안 된다고 했어요,\" 폴이 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있어도 내게 피프친 부인 얘기를 꺼내지 말아 주기 바란다, 돔비,\" 블림버 양이 응수했다. \"나는 그것을 허용할 생각이 조금도 없어. 여기서 하는 학습 과정은 그런 따위와는 한참 동떨어져 있단다. 그런 언급을 한 번만 더 하면, 내일 아침 식사 전까지 베르붐 페르소날레부터 시밀리마 키그노까지 한 군데도 틀리지 않고 외워 들려 달라고 청할 수밖에 없게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"저는 그런 뜻이—\" 어린 폴이 입을 떼었다.\n\n\"미안하지만 그런 뜻이 아니었다는 말은 하지 말아 줬으면 한다, 돔비,\" 블림버 양이 말했다. 그녀는 훈계를 할 때에도 무시무시한 정중함을 잃지 않았다. \"그건 내가 도저히 용납할 꿈도 못 꿀 논법이야.\"\n\n폴은 아무 말도 하지 않는 편이 가장 안전하겠다 싶어, 그저 블림버 양의 안경만 바라보았다. 블림버 양은 그를 향해 근엄하게 고개를 절레절레 흔든 뒤, 앞에 놓인 종이를 들여다보았다.\n\n\"'P. 돔비의 성격 분석.' 내 기억이 맞다면,\" 블림버 양이 말을 끊으며 말했다, \"분석이라는 낱말은 종합과 대비되어 워커가 이렇게 정의했단다. '감각의 대상이든 지성의 대상이든, 한 대상을 그 최초의 요소들로 분해하는 것.' 종합과 대비되어, 알겠지. 자, 이제 분석이 무엇인지 알겠지, 돔비.\"\n\n돔비는 자기 지성 속으로 쏟아져 들어온 그 빛에 완전히 눈이 멀어 버린 것 같지는 않았으나, 블림버 양에게 살짝 고개를 숙여 보였다.\n\n\"'P. 돔비의 성격 분석,'\" 블림버 양이 종이에 눈길을 던지며 다시 시작했다. \"돔비의 타고난 자질은 매우 우수하며, 학습에 대한 전반적 성향도 그와 동등한 비율로 진술될 수 있음을 본다. 따라서 여덟을 우리의 기준이자 최고 수치로 삼을 때, 돔비에게서 이 자질들은 각각 6과 4분의 3으로 진술됨을 본다!\"\n\n블림버 양은 폴이 이 소식을 어떻게 받아들이는지 보려고 잠시 멈추었다. 6과 4분의 3이 6파운드 15실링을 뜻하는지, 6펜스 3파딩인지, 6피트 3인치인지, 6시 45분인지, 아니면 아직 배우지 못한 무언가 여섯에 알 수 없는 또 다른 무언가 셋이 더해진 것인지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채, 폴은 두 손을 비비며 블림버 양을 똑바로 바라보았다. 그것은 마침 그가 할 수 있는 다른 어떤 행동 못지않게 적절한 반응이 되었고, 코닐리어는 계속했다.\n\n\"'난폭함 둘. 이기심 둘. 글럽이라는 자의 경우에서 드러난 바와 같이 천한 무리와 어울리려는 경향, 처음에는 일곱이었으나 이후 줄어듦. 신사다운 태도 넷, 나이가 들수록 향상되고 있음.' 자, 돔비, 내가 특히 네 주의를 환기하고 싶은 것은 이 분석의 끝에 붙은 전반적 소견이란다.\"\n\n폴은 무척 주의 깊게 그것을 따라가려 했다.\n\n\"'돔비에 관해 전반적으로 다음과 같이 관찰될 수 있다,'\" 블림버 양이 큰 소리로 읽으며, 두 낱말마다 한 번씩 안경을 앞에 선 그 자그마한 형체 쪽으로 향했다. \"'그의 능력과 성향은 양호하며, 처지를 고려할 때 기대할 수 있는 만큼의 진보를 이루었다. 그러나 이 어린 신사에 관해 한탄스러운 바는, 그가 성격과 행실에서 특이하다는(흔히 노숙(老熟)하다고 일컬어지는) 점이며, 그 어느 쪽에서도 분명히 비난을 요할 만한 것을 드러내지 않으면서도, 같은 나이와 사회적 지위의 다른 어린 신사들과 자주 매우 다르다는 점이다.' 자, 돔비,\" 블림버 양이 종이를 내려놓으며 말했다, \"이게 무슨 말인지 알겠니?\"\n\n\"알 것 같아요, 선생님,\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n\"이 분석은, 알겠지, 돔비,\" 블림버 양이 이어 말했다, \"존경하는 네 아버님께 보내질 거란다. 네가 성격과 행실에서 특이하다는 것을 아시면 당연히 무척 마음 아프실 거야. 우리에게도 당연히 마음 아픈 일이고. 왜냐하면 우리는, 알다시피, 돔비, 우리가 바라는 만큼 너를 좋아할 수가 없으니까.\"\n\n그녀는 아이의 여린 곳을 건드렸다. 떠날 때가 점점 가까워질수록, 그는 날이 갈수록 남몰래 더더욱, 온 집안이 자기를 좋아해 주기를 간절히 바라게 되었던 것이다. 자기 자신도 잘 이해하지 못하는—이해한다 해도 매우 불완전하게밖에 이해하지 못하는—어떤 숨은 이유에서, 그는 이곳의 거의 모든 것과 모든 사람을 향해 차츰 커져 가는 애정의 충동을 느꼈다. 자기가 떠나고 나면 그들이 자기에게 아주 무심해지리라는 생각을 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 그들이 자기를 다정하게 기억해 주기를 바랐다. 그래서 그는, 전에는 자기 삶의 공포였던, 집 뒤편에 사슬로 묶인 크고 목쉰 텁수룩한 개까지도 어떻게든 구슬리는 일을 자기 임무로 삼았으니, 자기가 더는 그곳에 없을 때 그 개마저도 자기를 그리워해 주기를 바라서였다.\n\n이 점에서도 자기와 또래들 사이의 차이를 다시 한번 드러낼 뿐이라는 것은 까맣게 모른 채, 가엾고 자그마한 폴은 그것을 블림버 양에게 할 수 있는 한 잘 펼쳐 보였고, 공식 분석표에도 불구하고 부디 자기를 좋아해 보려고 애써 주십사 청했다. 그 자리에 합류한 블림버 부인에게도 그는 같은 부탁을 올렸다. 그리고 그 부인이 그 앞에서까지도, 그가 별난 아이라는 자기가 늘 되풀이하던 의견을 입 밖에 내지 않을 수 없었을 때, 폴은 그녀에게, 부인 말씀이 아주 옳은 게 분명하다고, 자기 생각엔 자기 뼈 때문인 것 같지만 잘은 모르겠다고, 그러나 자기는 모두를 좋아하니 부디 그 점을 너그러이 봐주시기 바란다고 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게까지는 좋아하지 않지만요,\" 폴이, 그 아이의 가장 독특하고 가장 사람을 끄는 자질 가운데 하나인, 수줍음과 완벽한 솔직함이 뒤섞인 투로 말했다, \"당연히 플로렌스만큼 좋아하지는 않지요. 그건 절대 그럴 수 없어요. 그걸 기대하실 수는 없잖아요, 그렇죠, 부인?\"\n\n\"오! 노숙한 어린 영혼 같으니!\" 블림버 부인이 속삭이듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 저는 여기 있는 모두를 무척 좋아해요,\" 폴이 이어 말했다, \"그래서 떠나면서, 제가 가는 걸 누군가가 기뻐한다거나 신경도 안 쓴다고 생각하면 슬플 거예요.\"\n\n블림버 부인은 이제 폴이 세상에서 가장 별난 아이라고 완전히 확신했다. 그리고 박사에게 무슨 일이 있었는지 이야기하자, 박사도 아내의 의견에 이의를 달지 않았다. 다만 그는, 폴이 처음 왔을 때 했던 말처럼, 공부가 많은 것을 해 줄 거라고 했다. 또 그때처럼, \"끌어올려, 코닐리어! 끌어올려!\"라고도 했다.\n\n코닐리어는 늘 할 수 있는 한 힘차게 그를 끌어올려 왔고, 폴은 고된 나날을 보냈다. 그러나 과제를 헤쳐 나가는 일을 넘어, 그에게는 오래도록 늘 마음에 품고 또 여전히 굳게 붙들고 있는 또 다른 목적이 있었다. 그것은 온순하고 쓸모 있고 조용한 꼬마가 되어, 나머지 모두의 사랑과 정을 얻으려 늘 애쓰는 것이었다. 그래서 그는 여전히 계단의 그 옛 자리에 앉아 있거나 외딴 창에서 물결과 구름을 바라보는 모습으로 자주 보이긴 했어도, 다른 아이들 틈에서 겸손하게 자그마한 자발적 봉사를 베푸는 모습으로 더 자주 발견되기도 했다. 그리하여 블림버 박사의 지붕 아래에서 고행하는, 그 엄격하고 골몰에 빠진 어린 은수자들 사이에서조차 폴은 두루 관심의 대상이 되었으니, 모두가 좋아하고 아무도 거칠게 다룰 생각조차 하지 않을, 가냘프고 자그마한 노리개였다. 그러나 그는 자기 본성을 바꿀 수도, 그 분석표를 고쳐 쓸 수도 없었다. 그래서 그들 모두 돔비가 노숙하다는 데 의견을 모았다.\n\n그렇지만 다른 누구도 누리지 못하는, 그 성품에 딸린 몇 가지 특권은 있었다. 그들로서는 더 신식인 아이를 잃는 편이 차라리 나았을 테고, 그것만으로도 대단한 일이었다. 다른 아이들이 밤에 물러가며 블림버 박사와 그 가족에게 그저 고개만 숙일 때, 폴은 자그마한 손을 내밀어 대담하게 박사의 손을, 또 블림버 부인의 손을, 또 코닐리어의 손을 잡고 흔들곤 했다. 닥쳐오는 벌을 면해 달라고 누군가를 위해 빌어야 할 일이 있으면, 언제나 폴이 그 사절이었다. 눈이 약한 그 젊은이조차 한번은 유리잔과 도자기를 조금 깨뜨린 일을 두고 그에게 상의한 적이 있었다. 그리고 어둑한 소문에 따르면, 그 엄한 사내가 어떤 소년에게도 전에는 보인 적 없는 호의로 그를 대하던 집사가, 그를 튼튼하게 해 주려고 이따금 그의 식탁용 약한 맥주에 흑맥주를 섞어 주었다고 했다.\n\n이 광범위한 특권들에 더하여, 폴은 피더 선생의 방에 자유로이 드나들 권리도 있었다. 그 방에서 그는 두 번이나 투츠 씨를, 아주 끝이 뭉툭한 시가를 피우려다 실패한 끝에 정신이 아뜩해진 상태로, 바깥 공기 속으로 부축해 내보낸 적이 있었다. 그 시가는 투츠 씨가 자갈밭에서 어느 더없이 막무가내인 밀수꾼에게 몰래 사들인 한 묶음 가운데 하나였는데, 그 밀수꾼은 은밀히 털어놓기를, 세관이 자기 머리에 산 채로든 죽은 채로든 이백 파운드의 현상금을 걸었다고 했다. 피더 선생의 방은 아늑했고, 그 안쪽의 또 다른 작은 방에 침대가 있었다. 그리고 피더 선생이 아직 불 줄은 모르지만 꼭 배우기로 작정했다고 말하는 플루트가 벽난로 위에 걸려 있었다. 거기엔 책 몇 권과 낚싯대도 있었으니, 피더 선생은 시간이 나면 꼭 낚시를 배우기로 작정하겠다고 말했다. 피더 선생은 비슷한 의도로, 아름답고 작은 곱슬곱슬한 중고 키-뷰글 하나, 체스판과 말들, 스페인어 문법책 한 권, 스케치 도구 한 벌, 그리고 권투 장갑 한 켤레를 모아 두었다. 호신술은, 피더 선생 말로는, 모든 남자가 마땅히 해야 할 의무라 여기므로 반드시 배우기로 작정하겠다고 했으니, 곤경에 빠진 여인을 보호하는 일로 이어질 수도 있기 때문이었다.\n\n그러나 피더 선생의 가장 큰 보물은 코담배가 가득 든 큰 초록색 단지였는데, 그것은 지난 방학이 끝날 무렵 투츠 씨가 선물로 가져온 것이었다. 그는 그것에 비싼 값을 치렀는데, 섭정 왕세자의 진품 소장품이었기 때문이다. 투츠 씨도 피더 선생도 이것이든 다른 어떤 코담배든, 아무리 인색하고 절제된 정도로라도 즐기려 들면 재채기의 발작에 사로잡히지 않을 수 없었다. 그럼에도 그들이 가장 큰 낙으로 삼은 것은, 한 통 가득 찬 코담배를 찬 차로 적셔, 양피지 조각 위에 종이칼로 휘저은 다음, 그 자리에서 곧장 그것을 소비하는 데 몰두하는 일이었다. 그렇게 콧속을 틀어막는 동안 그들은 순교자 같은 의연함으로 놀라운 고통을 견뎠고, 틈틈이 식탁용 약한 맥주를 마셔 가며 방탕의 온갖 영광을 만끽했다.\n\n그들과 함께, 그것도 자기 으뜸 후원자 투츠 씨 곁에 말없이 앉아 있던 어린 폴에게는, 이런 무모한 자리들에 무시무시한 매력이 있었다. 그리고 피더 선생이 런던의 어두운 비밀들을 이야기하며, 투츠 씨에게 다가오는 방학에 자기가 직접 그것을 온갖 갈래로 면밀히 관찰할 작정이고, 그 목적으로 페컴에 사는 두 노처녀 댁에 하숙하기로 약속을 잡아 두었다고 말하자, 폴은 그를 무슨 여행기나 거친 모험담 속 영웅이라도 되는 양 바라보았고, 그토록 대담한 인물 앞에서 거의 두려움마저 느꼈다.\n\n방학이 무척 가까워진 어느 날 저녁 이 방에 들어갔을 때, 폴은 피더 선생이 인쇄된 편지 몇 장의 빈칸을 채워 넣고 있고, 이미 채워져 그의 앞에 흩어진 다른 몇 장은 투츠 씨가 접어 봉인하고 있는 것을 보았다. 피더 선생이 말했다, \"어, 돔비, 너 거기 있었구나, 응?\"—그들은 늘 그에게 친절했고 그를 보면 반가워했다—그러더니 편지 한 장을 그에게 툭 던지며 말했다, \"그리고 너도 거기 있고, 돔비. 그건 네 거야.\"\n\n\"제 거요, 선생님?\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n\"네 초대장이지,\" 피더 선생이 대답했다.\n\n폴이 그것을 들여다보니, 동판 인쇄로—피더 선생의 필체로 된 자기 이름과 날짜만 빼고—블림버 박사 부부가 이달 십칠 일 수요일 저녁에 열리는 이른 모임에 P. 돔비 군이 참석해 주는 기쁨을 청한다는 것, 시각은 일곱 시 반이라는 것, 그리고 그 목적은 카드리유 춤이라는 것이 적혀 있었다. 투츠 씨도 짝이 되는 종이 한 장을 들어 보이며, 블림버 박사 부부가 이달 십칠 일 수요일 저녁에 열리는 이른 모임에 투츠 씨가 참석해 주는 기쁨을 청하며, 시각은 일곱 시 반이고 목적은 카드리유 춤임을 보여 주었다. 피더 선생이 앉아 있는 탁자를 흘긋 보니, 브리그스 군의 참석과 토저 군의 참석과 모든 어린 신사의 참석이 같은 품위 있는 자리를 위해 블림버 박사 부부에 의해 청해지고 있음도 알 수 있었다.\n\n그러자 피더 선생은 그에게, 그를 크게 기쁘게 하는 소식을, 그의 누이도 초대되었다는 것, 그리고 그것이 반년마다 열리는 행사라는 것, 또 방학이 그날부터 시작되므로 원한다면 모임이 끝난 뒤 누이와 함께 떠나도 된다는 것을 일러 주었는데, 폴은 그 말을 가로채며 정말 무척 그러고 싶다고 했다. 그러고 나서 피더 선생은, 그가 더없이 고운 작은 글씨로 블림버 박사 부부께, 정중한 초대에 따라 P. 돔비 군이 그분들을 모시는 영광을 누리게 되어 기쁘겠다고 알려야 함을 일러 주었다. 끝으로 피더 선생은, 블림버 박사 부부가 들리는 데서는 이 잔치 자리를 입에 올리지 않는 편이 낫다고 했다. 이 예비 절차며 모든 준비가 고전적 격식과 고상한 교양의 원칙에 따라 진행되며, 한편으로 블림버 박사 부부와 다른 한편으로 어린 신사들은, 그 학자적 신분상, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 전혀 모르는 것으로 되어 있기 때문이라고 했다.\n\n폴은 이 귀띔에 대해 피더 선생에게 고맙다고 한 뒤, 초대장을 주머니에 넣고 여느 때처럼 투츠 씨 곁의 걸상에 앉았다. 그러나 오래도록 다소간 앓아 왔고 때로 무척 무겁고 아프던 폴의 머리가 그날 밤은 어찌나 거북했던지, 그는 머리를 손으로 받칠 수밖에 없었다. 그런데도 머리가 자꾸 떨궈져, 조금씩 조금씩 투츠 씨의 무릎 위로 내려앉더니, 다시는 들어 올려질 마음이 없다는 듯 거기에 얹혔다.\n\n그것이 그가 귀가 멀어야 할 까닭은 아니었지만, 그는 자기가 귀가 멀었던 게 분명하다고 생각했다. 왜냐하면 얼마 뒤, 피더 선생이 자기 귀에 대고 부르며 정신을 차리게 하려고 부드럽게 흔드는 소리가 들렸기 때문이다. 그리고 잔뜩 겁먹은 채 머리를 들어 둘러보니, 블림버 박사가 방에 들어와 있었고, 창문이 열려 있었으며, 자기 이마가 뿌려진 물로 젖어 있었다. 그런데 이 모든 일이 자기도 모르는 사이에 어떻게 이루어졌는지는 참으로 기이했다.\n\n\"아! 자, 자! 됐다! 우리 꼬마 친구, 지금은 좀 어떤가?\" 블림버 박사가 격려하듯 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아주 좋아요, 고맙습니다, 선생님,\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n그러나 바닥에 무슨 문제가 있는 듯, 그 위에 똑바로 설 수가 없었다. 벽에도 마찬가지로, 자꾸 빙빙 돌려 하는 통에, 아주 호되게 노려보아야만 겨우 멈출 수 있었다. 투츠 씨의 머리는 마땅한 정도보다 동시에 더 크고 더 멀리 있는 것처럼 보였다. 그리고 투츠 씨가 그를 위층으로 데려가려고 팔에 안았을 때, 폴은 문이 자기가 있으리라 예상한 곳과는 전혀 다른 데에 있는 것을 보고 놀랐고, 처음엔 투츠 씨가 굴뚝으로 곧장 걸어 올라가려는 게 아닌가 싶을 정도였다.\n\n투츠 씨가 그를 집 꼭대기까지 그토록 다정하게 안아다 준 것은 무척 친절한 일이었고, 폴은 그에게 그렇다고 말했다. 그러나 투츠 씨는 할 수만 있다면 그보다 훨씬 더한 일도 하겠다고 했다. 그리고 사실 그는 더한 일을 했으니, 더없이 친절한 태도로 폴이 옷을 벗는 것을 돕고 잠자리에 드는 것을 도운 뒤, 침대 곁에 앉아 무척 껄껄거렸다. 한편 문학사 피더 선생은 침대 발치에 몸을 굽혀, 앙상한 두 손으로 폴의 머리에 난 짧은 솜털을 죄다 빳빳이 곤두세우더니, 폴이 다시 멀쩡해진 것을 기리느라 대단한 기교로 그에게 권투 흉내를 내며 시늉을 했다. 그것이 어찌나 유달리 익살스럽고 또 친절했던지, 폴은 그에게 웃어야 할지 울어야 할지 마음을 정하지 못한 채, 둘 다를 한꺼번에 했다.\n\n투츠 씨가 어떻게 사라져 버렸는지, 또 피더 선생이 어떻게 피프친 부인으로 바뀌었는지, 폴은 물어볼 생각조차 하지 않았고, 알고 싶은 마음도 전혀 없었다. 그러나 피더 선생 대신 피프친 부인이 침대 발치에 서 있는 것을 보자 그는 외쳤다, \"피프친 부인, 플로렌스에게는 말하지 마세요!\"\n\n\"플로렌스에게 무얼 말하지 말라는 거니, 우리 꼬마 폴?\" 피프친 부인이 침대 곁으로 돌아와 의자에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"저에 대해서요,\" 폴이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니,\" 피프친 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"제가 자라면 무얼 할 생각인지 아세요, 피프친 부인?\" 폴이 베개 위에서 얼굴을 그녀 쪽으로 돌리고, 깍지 낀 두 손에 턱을 그리운 듯 괴며 물었다.\n\n피프친 부인은 짐작할 수 없었다.\n\n\"저는요,\" 폴이 말했다, \"제 돈을 모두 한 은행에 모아 두고, 더 벌려고 애쓰지 않고, 사랑하는 플로렌스랑 시골로 가서, 아름다운 정원과 들판과 숲을 갖고, 거기서 누나랑 평생 함께 살 거예요!\"\n\n\"저런!\" 피프친 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"네,\" 폴이 말했다. \"제가 그렇게 할 거예요, 제가—\" 그는 말을 멈추고 잠시 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n피프친 부인의 잿빛 눈이 그의 생각에 잠긴 얼굴을 살폈다.\n\n\"제가 자라면요,\" 폴이 말했다. 그러더니 그는 곧바로 이어서, 피프친 부인에게 그 모임에 대해, 플로렌스의 초대에 대해, 모든 아이들이 누나에게 느낄 감탄에 자기가 가질 자랑스러움에 대해, 아이들이 자기에게 그토록 친절하고 자기를 좋아한다는 것에 대해, 자기가 아이들을 그토록 좋아한다는 것에 대해, 그리고 그것이 그토록 기쁘다는 것에 대해 죄다 이야기했다. 그러고는 피프친 부인에게 그 분석에 대해, 자기가 분명 노숙하다는 것에 대해 이야기하고, 그 점에 대한 피프친 부인의 의견을 물었으며, 그녀가 그게 왜 그런지, 무슨 뜻인지 아는지도 물었다. 피프친 부인은 곤란에서 벗어나는 가장 빠른 길로, 그 사실 자체를 통째로 부인했다. 그러나 폴은 그 대답에 도무지 만족하지 못해, 더 진실한 답을 구하느라 피프친 부인을 어찌나 캐묻듯 바라보았던지, 그녀는 그의 눈길을 피하려고 일어나 창밖을 내다볼 수밖에 없었다.\n\n어린 신사들 가운데 누가 아플 때면 그 학원에 와 주는 어느 차분한 약제사가 있었는데, 어쩌다 보니 그가 방으로 들어와 블림버 부인과 함께 침대 곁에 나타났다. 그들이 어떻게 거기 왔는지, 얼마나 오래 거기 있었는지 폴은 몰랐다. 그러나 그들을 보자 그는 침대에서 일어나 앉아, 약제사의 모든 질문에 빠짐없이 대답했고, 괜찮으시다면 플로렌스가 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르게 해 달라고, 자기는 누나가 그 모임에 오는 것에 마음을 굳혔다고 속삭였다. 그는 약제사와 무척 수다스럽게 이야기했고, 둘은 더없이 좋은 벗으로 헤어졌다. 다시 눈을 감고 누워 있을 때, 그는 약제사가 방 밖에서, 아주 멀찍이서—아니면 그가 꿈에서 들었던가—생명력이 부족하고(그게 뭘까, 폴은 궁금했다!) 체질이 매우 허약하다고 말하는 것을 들었다. 그 꼬마가 십칠 일에 학교 동무들과 작별하는 데 마음을 둔 만큼, 더 나빠지지만 않는다면 그 소망을 들어주는 편이 낫겠다고도 했다. 피프친 부인에게서 그 꼬마가 십팔 일에 런던의 가족에게 갈 거라는 말을 들으니 다행이라고도 했다. 사례를 더 잘 파악하게 되면, 그날 이전에 돔비 씨에게 편지를 쓰겠다고도 했다. 당장 무엇무엇 할 까닭은 없다고도 했다—무엇 말인가? 폴은 그 낱말을 놓쳤다. 그리고 그 꼬마는 훌륭한 정신을 지녔으나 노숙한 아이라고도 했다.\n\n대체 무슨 노숙함이기에, 폴은 두근거리는 마음으로 궁금해했다, 자기에게서 그토록 또렷이 드러나고, 그토록 많은 사람의 눈에 그토록 분명히 보이는 것일까!\n\n그는 그것을 도무지 알아낼 수도 없었고, 그 노력으로 오래 자신을 괴롭히지도 못했다. 피프친 부인이—설령 떠난 적이 있었다 해도(그는 그녀가 박사와 함께 나갔다고 생각했지만, 어쩌면 그것도 다 꿈이었는지 모른다)—다시 그의 곁에 와 있었고, 이내 병 하나와 잔 하나가 마법처럼 그녀의 손에 들리더니, 그녀가 그 안의 것을 그에게 따라 주었다. 그러고 나서 그는 블림버 부인이 손수 가져다준 진짜 맛있는 젤리를 좀 먹었다. 그러자 어찌나 좋아졌던지, 피프친 부인은 그의 간곡한 청에 따라 집으로 갔고, 브리그스와 토저가 자러 왔다. 가엾은 브리그스는 자기 분석에 대해 끔찍하게 투덜거렸는데, 설령 그것이 화학 공정이었다 한들 그를 더 심란하게 하지는 못했을 것이다. 그러나 그는 폴에게 무척 잘해 주었고, 토저도 그랬으며, 나머지도 다들 그랬다. 모두 자러 가기 전에 들여다보며 말했기 때문이다, \"지금은 좀 어때, 돔비?\" \"기운 내, 꼬마 돔비!\" 그런 식이었다. 브리그스는 침대에 든 뒤에도 오래도록 잠 못 이룬 채 여전히 자기 분석을 한탄하며, 그게 죄다 틀렸다는 걸 안다고, 살인자를 분석해도 그보다 더 나쁘게는 못 했을 거라고, 그리고—만약 자기 용돈이 거기 달려 있다면 블림버 박사는 그걸 어떻게 받아들이겠느냐고 했다. 한 학기 내내 아이를 갤리선 노예로 부려먹고는 게으르다고 점수를 매기고, 식비에서 일주일에 두 끼씩 빼돌리고는 탐욕스럽다고 점수를 매기는 건 아주 쉬운 일이라고, 브리그스는 말했다. 그러나 그런 건 가만 받아들여질 일이 아니라고 자기는 믿는다는데, 안 그러냐고? 오! 아!\n\n이튿날 아침 눈 약한 젊은이가 징을 울리기 전에, 그는 위층으로 올라와 폴에게 가만히 누워 있으라고 일렀고, 폴은 무척 기꺼이 그렇게 했다. 피프친 부인이 약제사보다 조금 먼저 다시 나타났고, 그 첫날 아침(이제 그것이 얼마나 오래전 일처럼 느껴지는지!) 폴이 난로를 닦는 것을 보았던 그 착한 젊은 여인이 그에게 아침을 가져다준 직후였다. 멀찍이서 또 한 차례 상담이 있었고, 아니면 폴이 그것을 또 꿈꾸었거나 했다. 그러고 나서 약제사가 블림버 박사 부부와 함께 돌아와 말했다.\n\n\"네, 제 생각엔, 블림버 박사님, 지금은 이 어린 신사를 책에서 풀어 주어도 될 듯합니다. 방학이 워낙 코앞에 다가왔으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하지요,\" 블림버 박사가 말했다. \"여보, 괜찮으면 코닐리어에게 알려 주시오.\"\n\n\"여부가 있겠어요,\" 블림버 부인이 말했다.\n\n약제사는 몸을 굽혀, 폴의 두 눈을 가까이 들여다보고, 그의 머리와 맥과 심장을 어찌나 흥미와 정성을 다해 짚어 보았던지, 폴이 말했다, \"고맙습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"우리 꼬마 친구는,\" 블림버 박사가 말했다, \"한 번도 불평한 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇지요!\" 약제사가 대답했다. \"이 아이는 불평할 아이가 아니지요.\"\n\n\"많이 나아진 것 같습니까?\" 블림버 박사가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 많이 나아졌습니다, 박사님,\" 약제사가 대답했다.\n\n폴은 블림버 박사의 두 질문에 약제사가 어찌나 깊이 생각에 잠겨 답했던지, 바로 그 순간 약제사의 마음을 차지하고 있을 만한 화제를 자기 나름의 별난 방식으로 추측하기 시작했다. 그러나 그 어린 환자가 그렇게 마음속 탐험을 떠나려는 찰나, 약제사가 우연히 그의 눈과 마주쳐 명랑한 미소와 함께 곧장 상념에서 빠져나오자, 폴도 마주 미소 짓고는 그 탐험을 그만두었다.\n\n그는 그날 온종일 침대에 누워, 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 꿈꾸며, 투츠 씨를 바라보았다. 그러나 이튿날엔 일어나 아래층으로 내려갔다. 그런데 어럽쇼, 그 큰 시계에 무슨 문제가 생겨, 사다리 위에 올라선 일꾼이 시계 문자판을 떼어 내고는, 촛불 빛으로 기계 속에 도구들을 쑤셔 넣고 있는 것이 아닌가! 이것은 폴에게 큰 사건이어서, 그는 맨 아래 계단에 앉아 그 작업을 주의 깊게 지켜보았다. 이따금 바로 옆 벽에 비스듬히 기대어 놓인 시계 문자판을 흘긋거리며, 그것이 자기에게 추파를 던지고 있는 게 아닐까 하는 의심에 조금 어리둥절해하면서.\n\n사다리 위의 일꾼은 무척 싹싹했다. 그래서 그가 폴을 보고 \"안녕하세요, 도련님?\" 하고 말하자, 폴은 그와 대화에 들어가, 요즘 통 몸이 좋지 않았다고 말했다. 그렇게 서먹함이 깨지자, 폴은 그에게 종소리와 시계에 관해 숱한 질문을 던졌다. 이를테면, 사람들이 종을 치게 하려고 밤에 외딴 교회 첨탑 위에서 망을 보는지, 사람이 죽으면 종을 어떻게 울리는지, 또 그 종은 결혼 종과는 다른 종인지 아니면 그저 살아 있는 사람들의 상상 속에서만 음산하게 들리는 것인지 따위였다. 새 친구가 옛 시절의 통금종에 대해서는 그다지 잘 알지 못함을 알고, 폴은 그에게 그 제도에 대해 설명해 주었다. 또 그에게, 실무에 밝은 사람으로서, 양초가 타는 것으로 시간을 재자던 앨프레드 왕의 생각을 어떻게 보느냐고 물었다. 일꾼은, 만약 그게 다시 유행한다면 시계 장사는 망할 거라고 대답했다. 요컨대 폴은, 시계가 익숙한 모습을 완전히 되찾고 그 점잖은 물음을 다시 시작할 때까지 지켜보았다. 그러자 일꾼은 긴 바구니에 연장을 챙겨 넣고는, 그에게 잘 있으라고 인사하고 떠났다. 다만 그가 떠나기 전, 현관 매트 위에서 하인에게 무언가를 속삭였는데, 그 속에 \"노숙하다\"는 말이 들어 있었다—폴이 들었기 때문이다.\n\n대체 무슨 노숙함이기에, 사람들을 그렇게 안타깝게 만드는 것 같단 말인가! 대체 무엇일까!\n\n이제 배울 것이 없어진지라, 그는 이것을 자주 생각했다. 다만 생각할 거리가 더 적었더라면 했을 만큼 자주는 아니었다. 그러나 그에게는 생각할 거리가 무척 많았고, 그는 온종일 늘 생각에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n먼저, 플로렌스가 모임에 온다는 것이 있었다. 플로렌스는 아이들이 자기를 좋아한다는 것을 보게 될 테고, 그러면 누나는 행복할 것이다. 이것이 그의 큰 주제였다. 플로렌스가 한번, 아이들이 자기에게 다정하고 잘해 주었다는 것, 자기가 그들 사이에서 작은 인기쟁이가 되었다는 것을 확신하게만 된다면, 누나는 자기가 거기서 보낸 시간을 언제나, 그리 슬퍼하지 않고 떠올릴 수 있을 것이다. 어쩌면 그 덕에 누나는, 자기가 돌아왔을 때 한결 더 행복할지도 모른다.\n\n자기가 돌아왔을 때라니! 하루에도 쉰 번씩, 그의 소리 없는 작은 발이 계단을 올라 자기 방으로 갔다. 자기 것인 책이며 종잇조각이며 자질구레한 물건을 죄다 모아, 가장 사소한 것까지도, 집으로 가져가려고 거기에 한데 모아 두면서! 어린 폴에게는 돌아온다는 그림자가 조금도 없었다. 그것에 대한 어떤 준비도, 그것에 대한 다른 어떤 언급도, 그가 생각하거나 행하는 어떤 것에서도 자라나지 않았으니, 다만 누이와 관련된 이 희미한 한 가지만이 예외였다. 오히려 그는, 사색에 잠긴 기분일 때나 집 안을 이리저리 거닐 때면, 자기에게 익숙한 모든 것을 이제 작별할 것으로서 생각해야 했다. 그래서 온종일 생각할 거리가 그토록 많았던 것이다.\n\n그는 위층의 그 방들을 들여다보며, 자기가 떠나면 그곳들이 얼마나 적막할지 생각하고, 또 얼마나 많은 고요한 날과 주와 달과 해 동안 그곳들이 꼭 그렇게 근엄하고 어지럽혀지지 않은 채로 이어질지 궁금해해야 했다. 그는 생각해야 했다—혹시 (자기처럼 노숙한) 또 다른 아이가 언젠가 거기로 흘러들어, 그 똑같이 기괴하게 일그러진 무늬와 가구가 그 아이에게도 모습을 드러낼까. 그리고 한때 거기 있었던 어린 돔비에 대해 그 아이에게 누가 이야기해 줄까. 그는 계단의 어느 초상화에 대해서도 생각해야 했는데, 그것은 그가 떠나갈 때 어깨 너머로 돌아보면 늘 진지하게 그의 뒤를 좇아 바라보았고, 그가 누군가와 함께 그 곁을 지날 때면 여전히 그의 동행이 아니라 그를 응시하는 것 같았다. 그는 또 다른 곳에 걸린 어느 판화와 관련해서도 생각할 거리가 많았으니, 거기에는 경이로워하는 무리의 한가운데에, 그가 아는 한 형상이, 머리에 빛을 두른—자애롭고 온화하고 자비로운—한 형상이 위를 가리키며 서 있었다.\n\n자기 침실 창가에서는, 이런 생각들과 뒤섞여, 굽이치는 물결처럼 잇따라 밀려오는 숱한 상념이 있었다. 거친 날씨면 늘 먼 바다 위를 맴돌던 그 야생의 새들은 어디에 사는지. 구름은 어디서 일어나 처음 시작되는지. 바람은 어디서 나와 그 휘몰아치는 비행을 시작하고, 또 어디서 멈추는지. 자기와 플로렌스가 그토록 자주 함께 앉아, 이런 것들을 바라보고 또 이야기하던 그 자리가, 자기들 없이도 옛날 그대로일 수 있을지. 자기가 어느 먼 곳에 있고 누나가 거기 홀로 앉아 있다면, 플로렌스에게 그곳이 예전과 똑같을 수 있을지.\n\n그는 또 투츠 씨와 문학사 피더 선생과 모든 아이들에 대해서도 생각해야 했다. 또 블림버 박사와 블림버 부인과 블림버 양에 대해서도. 또 집에 대해, 고모와 톡스 양에 대해, 아버지에 대해. 돔비와 아들, 자기가 필요로 하던 돈을 마련한 가엾은 늙은 삼촌과 함께 있는 월터, 그리고 무쇠 갈고리 손을 한 그 걸걸한 목소리의 선장에 대해서도. 이 모든 것에 더하여, 그는 하루를 보내며 자그마한 방문을 여러 차례 해야 했다. 교실로, 블림버 박사의 서재로, 블림버 부인의 개인 거처로, 블림버 양의 방으로, 그리고 그 개에게로. 이제 그는 온 집을 마음대로 누빌 수 있게 되었으므로, 모두와 다정한 사이로 작별하고 싶은 바람에서, 자기 식으로 그들 모두를 살뜰히 챙겼다. 어떤 때는 늘 책 읽던 곳을 잃어버리는 브리그스를 위해 그 자리를 찾아 주었고, 어떤 때는 막다른 처지에 몰린 다른 어린 신사들을 위해 사전에서 낱말을 찾아 주었으며, 어떤 때는 블림버 부인이 실을 감도록 명주 타래를 잡아 주었고, 어떤 때는 코닐리어의 책상을 정돈해 주었다. 어떤 때는 심지어 박사의 서재로 살그머니 들어가, 그 학식 높은 발치의 양탄자에 앉아, 지구의를 부드럽게 돌리며 세계를 한 바퀴 돌거나, 저 먼 별들 사이로 날아오르기도 했다.\n\n요컨대 방학 직전의 그 며칠 동안, 다른 어린 신사들이 한 학기 전체의 학업을 통째로 복습하느라 죽을힘을 다해 애쓰고 있을 때, 폴은 그 집에서 일찍이 본 적 없는 그런 특권을 누리는 학생이었다. 그 자신도 좀처럼 믿기지 않았으나, 그의 자유는 시간이 가도 날이 가도 이어졌고, 꼬마 돔비는 모두에게서 귀여움을 받았다. 블림버 박사는 그를 어찌나 각별히 여겼던지, 어느 날 존슨이 무심코 그를 \"가엾은 꼬마 돔비\"라고 불렀다는 이유로 그에게 식탁에서 물러나라고 명할 정도였다. 폴은 그것이 다소 가혹하고 엄하다고 생각했는데, 비록 그 순간엔 자기도 얼굴이 화끈했고 존슨이 왜 자기를 가엾게 여기는지 의아하긴 했지만 말이다. 더구나 그 처사가 더더욱 의심스러운 정의라고 폴이 여긴 것은, 바로 그 위대한 권위자가 전날 저녁, (블림버 부인이 내놓은) 가엾고 사랑스러운 꼬마 돔비가 그 어느 때보다 노숙해졌다는 명제에 동의하는 것을 자기가 분명히 엿들었기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 폴은, 무척 야위고, 가볍고, 쉬 지치고, 어디든 곧 드러누워 쉬고 싶어지는 것이야말로 틀림없이 노숙한 것이리라 생각하기 시작했다. 날이 갈수록 점점 더 이것들이 자기 버릇이 되어 가고 있음을 느끼지 않을 수 없었던 까닭이다.\n\n마침내 모임 날이 왔다. 블림버 박사가 아침 식사 자리에서 말했다, \"여러분, 우리는 다음 달 이십오 일에 학업을 재개하겠습니다.\" 투츠 씨는 즉시 충성을 벗어던지고 반지를 끼었다. 그리고 잠시 뒤 무심한 대화 중에 박사를 언급하며, 그를 \"블림버\"라고 불렀다! 이 자유의 행위는 나이 든 학생들에게 감탄과 부러움을 불어넣었으나, 어린 영혼들은 질겁하여, 들보 하나 떨어져 그를 짓뭉개지 않는 것이 신기하다는 듯한 표정이었다. 아침 식사 때도 저녁 식사 때도, 그날 밤의 행사에 대한 언급은 조금도 없었다. 그러나 온종일 집안은 부산했고, 폴은 이리저리 거니는 동안 여러 낯선 의자며 촛대와 마주쳤고, 응접실 문 밖 층계참에 초록색 외투를 입고 서 있는 하프 하나와 맞닥뜨렸다. 저녁 식사 때 블림버 부인의 머리도 어딘가 묘했는데, 마치 머리채를 너무 단단히 비틀어 올린 것 같았다. 그리고 블림버 양은 양쪽 관자놀이에 곱게 땋은 머리 다발을 우아하게 드러냈으나, 그 밑에는 종이로 만 자기 곱슬머리를, 그것도 연극 광고지로 만 것을 두른 듯했다. 폴이 그녀의 반짝이는 안경 한쪽 위로는 \"왕립 극장\"을, 다른 한쪽 위로는 \"브라이턴\"을 읽었기 때문이다.\n\n저녁이 다가오자 어린 신사들의 침실에는 흰 조끼와 크라바트가 위풍당당하게 늘어섰고, 머리카락 그을리는 냄새가 어찌나 진동했던지, 블림버 박사가 하인을 올려 보내 인사를 전하며 집에 불이 났는지 알고 싶다고 했다. 그러나 그것은 그저 미용사가 어린 신사들의 머리를 지지면서, 일에 열중한 나머지 인두를 너무 달군 탓이었다.\n\n폴이 옷을 다 입자—몸이 편치 않고 졸렸으며 오래 서 있질 못해 그 일은 아주 금방 끝났다—그는 응접실로 내려갔다. 거기서 그는 블림버 박사가, 정장을 갖춰 입었으되 위엄 있고 무심한 태도로, 마치 한두 사람쯤이야 이따 들를 수도 있겠거니 여기는 듯, 방 안을 오락가락 거니는 것을 보았다. 잠시 뒤 블림버 부인이 나타났는데, 폴이 보기에 사랑스러웠다. 어찌나 많은 치마를 겹쳐 입었는지, 그녀의 둘레를 한 바퀴 도는 것이 가히 한바탕 소풍 같았다. 블림버 양은 어머니에 뒤이어 곧 내려왔는데, 다소 꽉 죄어든 듯한 모습이었으나 무척 매력적이었다.\n\n다음으로 도착한 이는 투츠 씨와 피더 선생이었다. 두 신사는 저마다 모자를 손에 든 채였는데, 마치 어딘가 딴 데 사는 사람들 같았다. 그리고 집사가 그들의 도착을 알리자, 블림버 박사가 \"이런, 이런, 이런! 맙소사!\" 하고는, 그들을 보고 무척 반가워하는 듯했다. 투츠 씨는 온통 보석과 단추로 번쩍였다. 그리고 그는 그 사실을 어찌나 강하게 의식했던지, 박사와 악수를 하고 블림버 부인과 블림버 양에게 절을 한 뒤, 폴을 한쪽으로 데려가 말했다, \"이거 어때, 돔비?\"\n\n그러나 이렇게 자신에 대한 겸손한 확신을 보이면서도, 투츠 씨는 대체로 조끼의 맨 아래 단추를 채우는 것이 현명한지, 또 모든 정황을 차분히 다시 살필 때 허리띠를 위로 접어 올리는 것이 나은지 아래로 내리는 것이 나은지를 두고 적잖은 갈피를 못 잡는 듯했다. 피더 선생의 것이 접어 올려진 것을 보고 투츠 씨는 자기 것도 올렸다. 그러나 다음 도착한 사람의 허리띠가 내려진 것을 보고 투츠 씨는 자기 것도 내렸다. 조끼 단추 채우기를 두고 생기는 차이가, 맨 아래뿐 아니라 맨 위에서도, 손님이 늘어 갈수록 어찌나 숱하고 복잡해졌던지, 투츠 씨는 무슨 악기라도 연주하듯 끊임없이 그 옷가지를 만지작거렸고, 그것이 요구하는 쉴 새 없는 연주에 도무지 정신을 못 차리는 듯했다.\n\n크라바트를 단단히 매고, 머리를 지지고, 에나멜 구두를 신고, 손에 가장 좋은 모자를 든 어린 신사들이 저마다 다른 시각에 도착이 알려지고 소개되고 나자, 무용 선생 뱁스 씨가 뱁스 부인과 함께 왔는데, 블림버 부인은 그 부인에게 무척 친절하고 너그러웠다. 뱁스 씨는 더없이 근엄한 신사로, 말투가 느리고 또박또박했다. 그리고 등불 아래 선 지 오 분도 채 되기 전에, 그는 투츠를 붙들고(투츠는 그때껏 말없이 그와 에나멜 구두를 비교하고 있었다), 금을 유출하여 그 대가로 원자재가 항구로 들어왔을 때 그것을 어떻게 처리해야 하느냐는 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그 질문이 난감해 보였던 투츠 씨는 \"삶아 버리죠\"라고 제안했다. 그러나 뱁스 씨는 그것이 될 거라고는 여기지 않는 듯했다.\n\n폴은 이제 자기 관찰 자리였던 소파의 푹신한 구석에서 슬그머니 빠져나와, 플로렌스를 맞을 준비를 하러 아래층 다과실로 내려갔다. 그는 거의 보름 가까이 누나를 보지 못했는데, 감기에 걸릴까 봐 지난 토요일과 일요일을 블림버 박사 댁에 머물렀기 때문이다. 이내 누나가 왔다. 소박한 무도회 드레스를 입고, 손에 싱싱한 꽃을 든 모습이 어찌나 아름다웠던지, 그녀가 폴의 목을 끌어안고 입 맞추려고 바닥에 무릎을 꿇었을 때(거기에는 그의 친구와, 차를 따라 내려고 기다리던 또 다른 젊은 여인 말고는 아무도 없었으므로), 그는 누나를 다시 놓아 줄 마음도, 자기 얼굴에서 그 밝고 다정한 눈을 떼어 보낼 마음도 좀처럼 먹을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"그런데 무슨 일이야, 플로이?\" 폴이 물었다. 거기에 눈물이 어린 것을 본 것이 거의 확실했던 것이다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니야, 얘. 아무것도 아니야,\" 플로렌스가 대답했다.\n\n폴이 손가락으로 그녀의 뺨을 살며시 만졌다—그것은 눈물이었다! \"어머, 플로이!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 같이 집에 가자, 그러면 내가 너를 간호해 줄게, 아가,\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"나를 간호한다고!\" 폴이 따라 말했다.\n\n폴은 그것이 이 일과 무슨 상관인지, 또 두 젊은 여인이 왜 그렇게 심각하게 바라보는지, 또 플로렌스가 왜 잠시 얼굴을 돌렸다가 다시 미소로 환하게 빛나며 돌아오는지 도무지 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"플로이,\" 폴이 누나의 검은 머리 한 가닥을 손에 쥐며 말했다. \"말해 줘, 누나. 누나는 내가 노숙해졌다고 생각해?\"\n\n누이는 웃으며 그를 어루만지고는 \"아니\"라고 말해 주었다.\n\n\"왜냐하면 사람들이 그렇게 말하거든,\" 폴이 대답했다, \"그게 무슨 뜻인지 알고 싶어, 플로이.\"\n\n그러나 문에서 요란하게 두 번 두드리는 소리가 나고 플로렌스가 급히 탁자로 가는 바람에, 둘 사이에 더는 말이 오가지 않았다. 폴은 자기 친구가 플로렌스에게, 마치 그녀를 달래는 듯 속삭이는 것을 보고 다시 의아해했다. 그러나 새로 도착한 손님이 그것을 금세 그의 머릿속에서 몰아냈다.\n\n도착한 이는 바닛 스케틀스 경과 스케틀스 부인과 스케틀스 도련님이었다. 스케틀스 도련님은 방학 뒤에 새로 입학할 학생이었고, 피더 선생의 방에서는 하원 의원인 그 아버지에 대해 소문이 자자했다. 피더 선생은 그에 대해, 그가 (서너 해 동안 그러리라 기대되어 온) 의장의 눈에 들어 발언권을 얻게 되면, 급진파를 한바탕 손봐 주리라 예상된다고 말한 바 있었다.\n\n\"그런데 이를테면 이 방은 지금 뭐 하는 방이지?\" 스케틀스 부인이 폴의 친구 밀리아에게 말했다.\n\n\"블림버 박사님의 서재입니다, 부인,\" 하는 대답이었다.\n\n스케틀스 부인은 안경을 통해 그 방을 한 바퀴 죽 둘러보더니, 바닛 스케틀스 경에게 흡족하다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다, \"아주 좋군요.\" 바닛 경도 동의했으나, 스케틀스 도련님은 미심쩍고 의심스러운 표정이었다.\n\n\"그런데 이 자그마한 아이는,\" 스케틀스 부인이 폴에게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이 아이도 그—\"\n\n\"어린 신사 가운데 하나입니다, 부인. 네, 부인,\" 폴의 친구가 말했다.\n\n\"네 이름은 무엇이니, 창백한 꼬마야?\" 스케틀스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"돔비예요,\" 폴이 대답했다.\n\n바닛 스케틀스 경이 즉시 끼어들어, 자기가 어느 공식 만찬에서 폴의 아버지를 뵙는 영광을 누렸다고, 그분이 무척 평안하시길 바란다고 했다. 그러고는 폴이 듣자니, 그가 스케틀스 부인에게 말했다, \"시티 사람—굉장한 부자—더없이 점잖은 분—박사가 말해 줬소.\" 그러더니 그는 폴에게 말했다, \"착한 너의 아버님께, 바닛 스케틀스 경이 그분이 무척 평안하시다는 소식을 듣고 기뻐했으며, 진심 어린 안부를 전한다고 말씀드려 주겠니?\"\n\n\"네, 그러죠,\" 폴이 대답했다.\n\n\"용감한 우리 아이로구나,\" 바닛 스케틀스 경이 말했다. \"바닛,\" 다가올 학업에 대한 앙갚음을 자두 케이크에 풀고 있던 스케틀스 도련님에게 그가 말했다, \"이 아이는 네가 알아 두어야 할 어린 신사다. 이 아이는 네가 알고 지내도 좋을 어린 신사야, 바닛,\" 바닛 스케틀스 경은 그 허락에 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"저 눈 좀 봐! 저 머리카락! 저 사랑스러운 얼굴!\" 스케틀스 부인이 안경을 통해 플로렌스를 바라보며 나직이 감탄했다.\n\n\"제 누나예요,\" 폴이 그녀를 소개하며 말했다.\n\n이제 스케틀스 일가의 만족은 완전했다. 그리고 스케틀스 부인이 첫눈에 폴에게 호감을 품었던 터라, 그들은 모두 함께 위층으로 올라갔다. 바닛 스케틀스 경이 플로렌스를 보살피고, 어린 바닛이 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n어린 바닛은 그들이 응접실에 이른 뒤로 그리 오래 뒤편에 머물러 있지 않았으니, 블림버 박사가 그를 곧장 끌어내 플로렌스와 춤을 추게 했기 때문이다. 그는 폴이 보기에 특별히 행복해 보이지도, 뾰로통한 것 말고 무엇이라도 특별해 보이지도, 자기가 무얼 하고 있는지 그다지 신경 쓰는 것 같지도 않았다. 그러나 폴이, 스케틀스 부인이 부채로 박자를 맞추며 블림버 부인에게, 자기 귀한 아들이 저 천사 같은 아이, 돔비 양에게 분명 홀딱 반해 버린 것 같다고 말하는 것을 들으니, 어린 스케틀스는 겉으로 드러내지는 않아도 더없는 행복에 빠져 있는 모양이었다.\n\n어린 폴은 아무도 그 베개들 사이 자기 자리를 차지하지 않았다는 것이, 또 자기가 다시 방에 들어오자 그것이 자기 자리임을 기억하고 모두 그가 거기로 돌아가도록 길을 비켜 준다는 것이 참 묘한 우연이라고 생각했다. 또 그들은 그가 플로렌스가 춤추는 것을 보고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아채자, 그의 앞에 아무도 서지 않고 그 앞 공간을 말끔히 비워 두어, 그가 눈으로 누나를 좇을 수 있게 해 주었다. 그들은 또 어찌나 친절했던지, 이내 무척 많아진 낯선 손님들조차도, 이따금 와서 그에게 말을 건네고, 좀 어떠냐고, 머리가 아프지는 않냐고, 피곤하지는 않냐고 물었다. 그는 그들의 그 모든 친절과 배려가 무척 고마웠다. 그리고 자기 구석에 받쳐져 비스듬히 기댄 채, 같은 소파에 블림버 부인과 스케틀스 부인을 둔 채, 또 춤이 한 곡 끝날 때마다 플로렌스가 와서 곁에 앉아 주는 가운데, 그는 참으로 행복하게 바라보았다.\n\n플로렌스는 밤새 그의 곁에 앉아, 제 뜻으로는 춤을 전혀 추지 않으려 했을 것이다. 그러나 폴이, 그것이 자기를 얼마나 기쁘게 하는지 말해 주며 누나에게 그러게 했다. 그리고 그는 진심을 말한 것이기도 했다. 모두가 누나를 얼마나 흠모하는지, 누나가 그 방의 아름다운 작은 장미 봉오리임을 볼 때면, 그의 작은 가슴이 부풀고 얼굴이 달아올랐기 때문이다.\n\n베개들 사이 자기 둥지에서, 폴은 마치 모든 것이 자기를 즐겁게 해 주려고 펼쳐지기라도 하는 듯, 벌어지는 거의 모든 것을 보고 들을 수 있었다. 그가 눈여겨본 자잘한 일들 가운데, 그는 무용 선생 뱁스 씨가 바닛 스케틀스 경과 대화에 빠져드는 것을, 그리고 곧 그에게도, 투츠 씨에게 물었던 것처럼, 금을 유출하여 그 대가로 원자재가 항구로 들어왔을 때 그것을 어떻게 처리해야 하느냐고 묻는 것을 보았다—그것은 폴에게 너무도 수수께끼여서, 그는 그것들을 대체 어떻게 해야 하는지 무척 알고 싶었다. 바닛 스케틀스 경은 그 문제에 대해 할 말이 많았고, 또 그렇게 말했다. 그러나 그것이 문제를 푸는 것 같지는 않았으니, 뱁스 씨가, 그렇지만 러시아가 자기네 수지(獸脂)를 들고 끼어든다고 가정해 보라고 응수했기 때문이다. 그 말에 바닛 경은 거의 말문이 막혀, 그저 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는, 그렇다면 면화로 물러설 수밖에 없겠지요, 라고 말할 수 있을 따름이었다.\n\n바닛 스케틀스 경은, 뱁스 씨가 (아주 외톨이가 되어, 하프를 켜는 신사의 악보집을 들여다보는 척하던) 뱁스 부인을 위로하러 갔을 때, 그를 마치 비범한 부류의 사람으로 여기는 듯 뒤를 좇아 바라보았다. 그리고 잠시 뒤 그는 블림버 박사에게 바로 그 말을 했고, 외람되지만 그가 누구인지, 또 무역위원회에 몸담은 적이 있는지 물어봐도 되겠느냐고 했다. 블림버 박사는, 아니, 아닐 거라고, 사실 그는 무엇무엇의 교수라고 대답했다—\n\n\"통계에 관련된 무언가겠지요, 장담합니다만?\" 바닛 스케틀스 경이 말했다.\n\n\"그게, 아닙니다, 바닛 경,\" 블림버 박사가 턱을 문지르며 대답했다. \"아니요, 꼭 그렇지는 않습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 종류든 수치에 관한 거겠지요, 내기를 걸어도 좋습니다만,\" 바닛 스케틀스 경이 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 그렇습니다,\" 블림버 박사가 말했다, \"네, 그렇지만 그런 종류는 아닙니다. 뱁스 씨는 무척 훌륭한 부류의 사람이지요, 바닛 경, 그리고—실은 우리 무용 교수입니다.\"\n\n폴은 이 한마디 정보가 뱁스 씨에 대한 바닛 스케틀스 경의 의견을 완전히 바꿔 놓는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 바닛 경은 그만 노발대발하여, 방 저편의 뱁스 씨를 사납게 노려보았다. 그는 심지어, 무슨 일이 있었는지 스케틀스 부인에게 이야기하면서 뱁스 씨를 \"빌어먹을\" 작자라 부르고, 그건 그 더없이 뻔뻔—스럽고 괘씸—한 무례라고까지 했다.\n\n폴이 눈여겨본 일이 또 하나 있었다. 피더 선생이 니거스 술을 커스터드 잔으로 여러 잔 들이켜더니, 흥겨워지기 시작한 것이다. 전반적으로 춤은 격식을 차렸고 음악은 다소 엄숙했다—사실 교회 음악과 좀 비슷했다. 그러나 그 커스터드 잔들을 마신 뒤, 피더 선생은 투츠 씨에게, 자기가 거기에 활기를 좀 불어넣겠다고 했다. 그러고 나서 피더 선생은 정말로 춤만이 목적이라는 듯 춤을 추기 시작했을 뿐 아니라, 음악이 거친 가락을 연주하도록 은밀히 부추기기까지 했다. 더 나아가 그는 숙녀들에게 유난히 살가워졌다. 그리고 블림버 양과 춤추면서, 그녀에게 속삭였다—그녀에게 속삭인 것이다!—그것도 폴이 이 놀라운 시구를 그가 읊는 것을 들을 만큼은 또렷이.\n\n\"거짓을 위해 빚어진 마음을 내가 가졌던들,\n결코 그대를 해칠 수는 없으리!\"\n\n이것을, 폴은 그가 네 명의 젊은 숙녀에게 차례로 되풀이하는 것을 들었다. 피더 선생이 투츠 씨에게, 내일 이 일로 탈이 날까 봐 걱정이라고 말한 것도 무리가 아니었다!\n\n블림버 부인은 이—비교적 말하자면—방탕한 행동에, 특히 음악의 성격이 변한 것에 다소 놀랐다. 거리에서 유행하던 천박한 가락들까지 끌어안기 시작한 그 음악이, 스케틀스 부인의 비위를 거스를지도 모른다고 여기는 것은 자연스러운 일이었다. 그러나 스케틀스 부인은 무척 친절하게도, 블림버 부인에게 그런 건 입에 올리지 말아 달라고 부탁했다. 그리고 피더 선생의 흥이 이런 자리에서 이따금 그를 도를 넘게 만든다는 부인의 해명을, 더없는 정중함과 예의로 받아들이며, 그가 그 처지로서는 무척 좋은 사람 같다고, 또 자기는 특히 그의 그 잘난 체하지 않는 머리 모양이 마음에 든다고 했다—그 머리란 (이미 넌지시 비쳤듯이) 4분의 1인치쯤 길었다.\n\n한번은 춤이 잠시 멈춘 사이, 스케틀스 부인이 폴에게, 너는 음악을 무척 좋아하는 것 같구나, 하고 말했다. 폴은 그렇다고 대답하고는, 부인도 그러시다면 자기 누나 플로렌스가 노래하는 것을 들으셔야 한다고 했다. 스케틀스 부인은 이내, 그 즐거움을 누리고 싶어 애가 탄다는 것을 깨달았다. 그리고 플로렌스는 그토록 많은 사람 앞에서 노래해 달라는 청에 처음엔 무척 겁을 먹고, 부디 양해해 달라고 간곡히 빌었으나, 폴이 자기에게로 불러 \"그러자, 플로이! 제발! 나를 위해서, 얘!\"라고 말하자, 곧장 피아노로 가서 노래를 시작했다. 폴이 누나를 볼 수 있도록 모두 조금씩 비켜서고, 또 그녀가 거기 홀로 앉아 있는 것을—그토록 젊고, 착하고, 아름답고, 자기에게 다정한 그 모습을—그가 보았을 때, 그리고 그토록 자연스럽고 감미로운, 자기와 자기 삶의 모든 사랑과 행복 사이를 잇는 그런 황금의 고리 같은 그녀의 떨리는 목소리가 고요를 뚫고 솟아오르는 것을 들었을 때, 그는 얼굴을 돌리고 눈물을 감추었다. 사람들이 그에게 말을 걸었을 때 그가 그들에게 일러 주었듯이, 음악이 너무 애처롭거나 너무 구슬퍼서가 아니라, 그것이 그에게 너무도 소중해서였다.\n\n그들은 모두 플로렌스를 사랑했다. 어찌 그러지 않을 수 있었겠는가! 폴은 그들이 반드시 그러리라는 것을, 또 그럴 것을 미리 알고 있었다. 그리고 푹신한 구석에 앉아, 두 손을 차분히 모으고, 한쪽 다리는 헐겁게 몸 밑으로 접은 채로, 그가 누나를 지켜보는 동안 그 어린 가슴을 어떤 승리감과 기쁨이 부풀렸는지, 또 그가 얼마나 달콤한 평온을 느꼈는지, 짐작할 이는 거의 없었을 것이다. \"돔비의 누나\"에 대한 아낌없는 찬사가 모든 아이들에게서 그의 귀에 닿았다. 그 침착하고 겸손한 작은 미인에 대한 감탄이 모든 입에 올랐고, 그녀의 지성과 재주에 대한 이야기가 끊임없이 그의 곁을 흘러 지나갔다. 그리고 마치 여름밤의 대기에 실려 오기라도 한 듯, 주위에는 플로렌스와 자기에 관한, 절반쯤 알아들을 만한 어떤 정감이 퍼져 있어, 둘 모두에게 공감을 보내며, 그를 어루만지고 그의 마음을 적셨다.\n\n그는 왜 그런지 알지 못했다. 그날 밤 그 아이가 본 것과 느낀 것과 생각한 모든 것—있는 것과 없는 것, 지금인 것과 지나간 것—이 무지개의 색깔처럼, 혹은 해가 비칠 때 화려한 새들의 깃털처럼, 혹은 그 같은 해가 질 때 부드러워지는 하늘처럼 뒤섞였던 것이다. 요즘 그가 생각해야 했던 그 숱한 것들이 음악 속에서 그의 앞을 지나갔다. 다시 그의 주의를 요구하려는 것도, 앞으로도 영영 그것을 차지할 듯한 것도 아니라, 평온히 마무리되어 떠나간 것으로서. 여러 해 전에 내다보던 외딴 창 하나가, 몇 마일이고 떨어진 바다를 내다보았다. 그 물결 위로, 바로 어제까지도 그에게 분주하던 상념들이, 부서진 물결처럼 잠잠해지고 잦아들어 쉬었다. 바닷가 침상에 누워 있을 때 의아해하던 그 신비로운 속삭임이, 누나의 노래를 뚫고, 사람들의 목소리의 웅성거림과 발걸음 소리를 뚫고 여전히 울려오는 것 같았고, 또 스쳐 지나가는 얼굴들 속에도, 심지어 자주 다가와 그의 손을 잡아 주던 투츠 씨의 그 묵직한 다정함 속에도 어느 정도 깃들어 있는 듯했다. 그 두루 퍼진 친절을 뚫고도, 그것이 여전히 자기에게 말을 거는 것을 듣는 것만 같았다. 그리고 그의 노숙하다는 평판마저도, 어찌 된 영문인지는 몰라도, 그것과 한 가닥으로 이어져 있는 것 같았다. 그렇게 어린 폴은 골똘히 생각하며, 귀 기울이며, 바라보며, 꿈꾸며 앉아 있었다. 그리고 무척 행복했다.\n\n작별할 때가 올 때까지는 그러했다. 그리고 그때가 되자, 과연 모임에 한바탕 술렁임이 일었다. 바닛 스케틀스 경이 어린 스케틀스를 데려와 그와 악수하게 하고는, 착한 너의 아버님께 잊지 말고, 자기, 바닛 스케틀스 경이, 두 어린 신사가 친밀하게 알고 지내게 되길 바란다고 했다는 말을 진심 어린 안부와 함께 전해 주겠느냐고 물었다. 스케틀스 부인은 그에게 입 맞추고, 이마 위로 흘러내린 머리를 쓰다듬어 주고, 그를 품에 안았다. 심지어 뱁스 부인까지도—가엾은 뱁스 부인! 폴은 그것이 기뻤다—하프 켜는 신사의 악보집 곁에서 건너와, 그 방의 누구 못지않게 진심으로 그에게 작별을 고했다.\n\n\"안녕히 계세요, 블림버 박사님,\" 폴이 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"잘 가거라, 우리 꼬마 친구,\" 박사가 대답했다.\n\n\"정말 고맙습니다, 선생님,\" 폴이 그 무시무시한 얼굴을 천진하게 올려다보며 말했다. \"괜찮으시면, 디오게네스를 잘 보살펴 달라고 일러 주세요.\"\n\n디오게네스란 그 개였다. 그 개는 폴이 있기 전까지는 평생 단 한 명의 벗에게도 마음을 터놓은 적이 없었다. 박사는 폴이 없는 동안 디오게네스에게 온갖 보살핌을 베풀겠노라 약속했고, 폴은 그에게 다시 한번 고맙다고 한 뒤 악수를 했다. 그러고는 블림버 부인과 코닐리어에게도 어찌나 진심 어린 간절함으로 작별을 고했던지, 블림버 부인은 그 순간부터 키케로를 스케틀스 부인에게 언급하는 것을 잊고 말았다. 저녁 내내 그럴 작정이 분명했는데도 말이다. 코닐리어는 폴의 두 손을 자기 두 손에 쥐고 말했다, \"돔비, 돔비, 너는 늘 내가 가장 아끼는 학생이었단다. 신의 가호가 있기를!\" 그것은, 한 사람을 얼마나 쉽게 오해할 수 있는지를 보여 주는 것이라고 폴은 생각했다. 블림버 양은—비록 닦달꾼이긴 했어도—진심으로 그 말을 했고, 또 진심으로 그렇게 느꼈으니까.\n\n그러자 어린 신사들 사이에 \"돔비가 간대!\" \"꼬마 돔비가 간대!\" 하는 웅성거림이 돌았고, 폴과 플로렌스를 뒤따라 계단을 내려와 현관으로 향하는 전반적인 움직임이 일었는데, 거기엔 블림버 일가가 모두 끼어 있었다. 그런 일은, 피더 선생이 큰 소리로 말하기를, 자기가 겪은 한 어떤 전임 어린 신사의 경우에도 일어난 적이 없는 일이라고 했다. 그러나 이것이\n\n냉정한 사실이나 커스터드 컵 같은 것은 아니었다. 하인들도, 집사를 우두머리로 하여 모두가 어린 돔비가 떠나는 것을 보는 데 관심을 가졌고, 심지어 눈이 약한 그 젊은이조차 그날 밤 그와 플로렌스를 핍친 부인 댁으로 데려갈 마차에 책과 트렁크를 실어 내면서 눈에 띄게 마음이 녹았다.\n\n그 어린 신사들에게 부드러운 연정이 미치는 영향력조차도—그들은 하나같이, 한 아이도 빠짐없이 플로렌스를 끔찍이 좋아했다—폴에게 자못 떠들썩한 작별을 고하는 것을 막지는 못했다. 그들은 폴의 뒤로 모자를 흔들어 댔고, 아래층으로 우르르 밀려 내려와 그와 악수를 했으며, 저마다 \"돔비, 날 잊지 마!\" 하고 외쳐 댔고, 그 어린 체스터필드들 사이에서는 보기 드문 그런 감정의 분출을 마음껏 쏟아 냈다. 폴은 문이 열리기 전에 플로렌스가 자기를 감싸 줄 때 그녀에게 속삭였다, 저 소리 들었어? 저걸 영영 잊지 않을 거지? 저걸 알게 되어 기뻐? 그녀에게 말하는 그의 눈 속에는 생기 넘치는 기쁨이 어려 있었다.\n\n마지막으로 한 번 보려고, 그는 몸을 돌려 그렇게 자기를 향해 있는 얼굴들을 바라보았는데, 그것들이 어찌나 빛나고 환하며 또 그렇게 수가 많은지, 그리고 붐비는 극장에서 얼굴들이 그러하듯 어찌나 차곡차곡 쌓이고 포개져 있는지를 보고 놀랐다. 그가 바라보는 동안 그것들은 흔들리는 거울 속의 얼굴들처럼 그의 눈앞에서 일렁였다. 그리고 다음 순간 그는 바깥 어두운 마차 안에서, 플로렌스에게 바싹 매달려 있었다. 그때부터 블림버 박사의 학교를 떠올릴 때면 언제나 이 마지막 광경에서 본 모습 그대로 되살아났다. 그리고 그곳은 두 번 다시 실재하는 장소로 여겨지지 않았고, 언제나 눈으로 가득 찬 꿈처럼만 느껴졌다.\n\n그러나 이것이 블림버 박사의 학교에 대한 정말로 마지막은 아니었다. 또 한 가지가 더 있었다. 바로 투츠 씨였다. 그는 뜻밖에도 마차 창문 하나를 내리고 안을 들여다보며, 더없이 엉뚱한 낄낄거림과 함께 \"돔비 거기 있어?\" 하고 말하고는, 대답도 기다리지 않고 곧바로 창문을 도로 올렸다. 그리고 이것조차도 투츠 씨의 정말로 마지막은 아니었다. 마부가 마차를 몰고 떠나기도 전에, 그는 또 그렇게 갑작스럽게 다른 쪽 창문을 내리더니, 꼭 같은 낄낄거림과 함께 안을 들여다보며 꼭 같은 어조로 \"돔비 거기 있어?\" 하고 말하고는, 꼭 앞서처럼 사라져 버렸다.\n\n플로렌스는 어찌나 웃어 댔던지! 폴은 그 일을 자주 떠올렸고, 떠올릴 때마다 스스로도 웃었다.\n\n그러나 그 직후—다음 날, 그리고 그 뒤로—폴이 그저 어렴풋하게만 기억할 수 있는 일들이 많았다. 이를테면, 어째서 집으로 돌아가지 않고 핍친 부인 댁에서 며칠 밤낮을 머물렀는지, 어째서 플로렌스가 곁에 앉아 있는 채로 자기가 침대에 누워 있었는지, 방 안에 있던 것이 정말 자기 아버지였는지 아니면 그저 벽에 비친 키 큰 그림자였는지, 자기 의사가 누군가에 대해, 만약 그가 자신의 약함에 비례하여 강하게 키워 낸 공상들을 품게 된 그 계기가 있기 전에 그를 옮겨 놓았더라면 그가 시들어 죽어 갔을 가능성이 매우 컸으리라고 말하는 것을 자기가 들었는지 하는 것들이.\n\n그는 자기가 플로렌스에게 \"오 플로이, 나 집에 데려다줘, 그리고 날 절대 떠나지 마!\" 하고 자주 말했는지조차 기억할 수 없었다. 그래도 그랬던 것 같다고 생각했다. 때때로 그는 자기가 \"나 집에 데려다줘, 플로이! 집에 데려다줘!\" 하고 되풀이하는 소리를 들었던 것만 같았다.\n\n그러나 집에 돌아와 그 낯익은 계단을 따라 안겨 올라갈 때, 자기가 마차 좌석에 누워 있는 동안—여전히 플로렌스가 곁에 있었고, 늙은 핍친 부인이 맞은편에 앉아 있는 채로—여러 시간을 잇따라 마차가 덜컹거렸던 일은 기억할 수 있었다. 사람들이 자기를 눕혀 준 그 낡은 침대도, 자기 고모와 톡스 양과 수전도 기억났다. 그러나 또 무언가가, 그것도 최근의 무언가가 여전히 그를 어리둥절하게 했다.\n\n\"플로렌스하고 이야기하고 싶어요, 괜찮다면요.\" 그가 말했다. \"플로렌스 혼자만요, 잠깐 동안요!\"\n\n그녀가 그의 위로 몸을 굽혔고, 다른 사람들은 물러섰다.\n\n\"플로이, 우리 귀염둥이, 마차에서 날 데려올 때 현관에 있던 게 아빠였지?\"\n\n\"응, 그래.\"\n\n\"내가 들어오는 걸 보고, 울면서 자기 방으로 들어가시진 않았지, 플로이, 그렇지?\"\n\n플로렌스는 고개를 가로젓고는, 그의 뺨에 입술을 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"아빠가 울지 않으셔서 정말 다행이야.\" 어린 폴이 말했다. \"난 우신 줄 알았어. 내가 물어봤다고 그들한테 말하지 마.\"\n\n월터는 며칠 동안이나 바베이도스 건을 두고 어떻게 해야 할지 마음을 정하지 못했다. 심지어 돔비 씨가 한 말이 진심이 아니었거나, 아니면 마음을 바꾸어 가지 않아도 된다고 말해 줄지 모른다는 가냘픈 희망까지 품었다. 그러나 이 생각에(그 자체로도 충분히 가망 없는 것이었지만) 조금이라도 확신을 더해 줄 어떤 일도 일어나지 않았고, 시간은 흘러가는데 그에게는 허비할 시간이 없었으므로, 그는 더는 망설이지 말고 행동에 옮겨야 한다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n월터의 가장 큰 난관은, 처지의 변화를 솔 삼촌에게 어떻게 알리느냐 하는 것이었다. 그는 그것이 삼촌에게 끔찍한 타격이 되리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 이 놀라운 소식으로 솔 삼촌의 기운을 꺾는 일이 더더욱 어려웠던 까닭은, 최근 들어 삼촌의 기운이 무척 회복되어 그 노인이 어찌나 명랑해졌던지 그 작은 뒤쪽 응접실이 예전 모습을 되찾았기 때문이었다. 솔 삼촌은 돔비 씨에게 진 빚의 첫 약정분을 갚았고, 나머지도 차차 헤쳐 나가리라는 희망에 부풀어 있었다. 그렇게 의연하게 곤경에서 떨쳐 일어선 그를 다시 주저앉히는 일은, 참으로 가슴 아픈 필연이었다.\n\n그렇다고 그를 두고 도망쳐 버릴 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 삼촌은 미리 알고 있어야 했다. 다만 그것을 어떻게 말하느냐가 문제였다. 가느냐 마느냐 하는 문제에 대해서는, 월터는 자기에게 선택권이 있다고 여기지 않았다. 돔비 씨는 그가 젊고 삼촌의 형편이 좋지 못하다고 사실대로 말했다. 그리고 그 일깨움에 곁들인 눈빛으로, 만일 가기를 거절한다면 원한다면 집에 머물러도 좋으나 자기 회계 사무소에는 있을 수 없다고 분명히 표현했다. 삼촌과 그는 돔비 씨에게 큰 은혜를 입고 있었고, 그것은 월터 자신이 청해서 진 빚이었다. 그는 그 신사의 호의를 얻는 일을 마음속으로 단념하기 시작했을지도 모르고, 또 그 신사가 이따금 자기를 가볍게 여기려 든다고 생각했을지도 모르나, 그것은 도무지 공정하다고 할 수 없는 생각이었다. 그러나 그것이 없었더라도 의무였을 일은, 그것이 있어도 여전히 의무였고—적어도 월터는 그렇게 생각했다—의무란 다해야 하는 것이었다.\n\n돔비 씨가 그를 바라보며, 그가 젊고 삼촌의 형편이 좋지 못하다고 말했을 때, 그 얼굴에는 경멸의 빛이 어려 있었다. 그가 형편 줄어든 노인에게 얹혀 빈둥대며 사는 것을 아주 흡족해하리라는, 멸시하고 깔보는 듯한 단정이었으니, 그것이 소년의 너그러운 영혼을 찔렀다. 자기 본성을 오해하고 있는 것이라고 돔비 씨에게, 말로 표현하지 않고도 가능한 한 확신시키려 마음먹은 월터는, 서인도 면담이 있은 뒤로는 그전보다 한층 더 명랑하고 활기차게 보이려 애썼다. 그처럼 재빠르고 열성적인 기질을 지닌 사람에게 그게 가능한 일이라면 말이다. 그는 너무 어리고 미숙해서, 어쩌면 바로 그런 자질이 돔비 씨에게는 마음에 들지 않을 수도 있다는 것, 그리고 그 강력한 불쾌함의 그늘 아래서—그것이 옳든 그르든—여전히 탄력 있고 비위를 맞추려는 희망에 부푼 모습을 보이는 것이 그의 호감을 사는 디딤돌이 되기는커녕 오히려 그렇지 못하다는 것을 미처 생각하지 못했다. 그러나 어쩌면—어쩌면—그 위대한 인물은 정직한 기개를 새로이 드러내는 이런 태도에서 자기가 도전받고 있다고 여기고, 그것을 꺾어 놓으려 마음먹었는지도 모른다.\n\n\"좋아! 어쨌거나 마침내, 적어도 솔 삼촌에게는 말씀드려야 해.\" 월터는 한숨을 쉬며 생각했다. 그리고 만일 자기가 직접 노인에게 말하고 그 주름진 얼굴에 처음으로 떠오르는 반응을 보게 된다면, 어쩌면 목소리가 조금 떨릴지도 모르고 표정도 바라는 만큼 희망차 보이지는 않으리라는 것을 염려하여, 그는 그 막강한 중재자 커틀 선장의 도움을 빌리기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 일요일이 돌아오자, 아침을 먹고 다시 한번 커틀 선장의 거처를 찾아 나섰다.\n\n그곳으로 가는 길에, 맥스팅거 부인이 일요일 아침마다 멀리까지 가서 멜기세덱 하울러 목사의 예배에 참석한다는 것을 떠올리니 나쁘지 않은 기분이었다. 이 목사는 어느 날 서인도 부두에서, 송곳으로 큰 술통에 구멍을 뚫고 그 구멍에 입을 대고 마셨다는 거짓 혐의(전체의 적이 일부러 그를 겨냥해 꾸며 낸 것이었다)로 해고당한 뒤, 이 년 뒤 바로 그날 오전 열 시에 세상이 멸망하리라고 선포하고는, 광신파 신사 숙녀들을 맞이하기 위해 앞쪽 응접실 하나를 열었다. 그들이 처음 모인 자리에서 멜기세덱 목사의 훈계가 어찌나 강력한 효과를 냈던지, 예배를 마무리하는 신성한 춤을 황홀하게 추다가 신도들 전체가 아래층 부엌으로 와르르 무너져 내려 신도 가운데 한 사람의 빨래 짜는 기계를 망가뜨려 놓았다.\n\n이 이야기를 선장은 어느 날 보기 드물게 흥겨운 기분에 젖어, 중개인 브로글리에게 돈을 다 갚던 날 밤, 아름다운 페그를 거듭 부르는 틈틈이 월터와 그의 삼촌에게 털어놓은 것이었다. 선장 자신은 동네에 있는 어느 교회에 어김없이 출석했는데, 그 교회는 일요일 아침마다 유니언 잭을 내걸었고, 그곳에서 그는—적법한 교회지기가 병약했으므로—소년들을 친절하게도 감독해 주었으니, 그 신비로운 갈고리의 위력으로 아이들에게 막강한 권세를 휘둘렀다. 선장의 규칙적인 습관을 아는 월터는, 그가 외출하기 전에 닿으려고 서둘렀고, 어찌나 발걸음을 재게 놀렸던지, 브리그 플레이스로 접어들었을 때 선장의 활짝 열린 창문 밖에 넓적한 푸른 외투와 조끼가 햇볕에 바람 쐬려고 내걸려 있는 것을 보는 기쁨을 누렸다.\n\n선장 없이 그 외투와 조끼만 사람의 눈에 보인다는 것은 도무지 믿기지 않는 일이었다. 그러나 그는 분명 그 옷 속에 들어 있지 않았으니, 만일 들어 있었다면 그의 두 다리가—브리그 플레이스의 집들은 높지 않았으므로—현관문을 가로막았을 텐데, 문은 말끔히 트여 있었다. 이 발견에 못내 의아해하며 월터는 한 번 노크했다.\n\n\"스팅거.\" 월터는 위층 방에서 선장이 마치 자기와는 상관없다는 듯 말하는 소리를 또렷이 들었다. 그래서 월터는 두 번 노크했다.\n\n\"커틀.\" 이어 선장이 그렇게 말하는 소리가 들렸다. 그러더니 곧 선장이, 깨끗한 셔츠에 멜빵을 메고, 밧줄 뭉치처럼 목에 헐겁게 감긴 목도리를 두르고, 번들거리는 모자를 쓴 채 창가에 나타나, 넓적한 푸른 외투와 조끼 너머로 몸을 내밀었다.\n\n\"월터!\" 선장이 놀란 얼굴로 그를 내려다보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"네, 네, 커틀 선장님.\" 월터가 대답했다. \"저뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이냐, 얘야?\" 선장이 몹시 걱정스럽게 물었다. \"길스 영감님이 또 뭔가 일을 당하신 건 아니지?\"\n\n\"아니요, 아니요.\" 월터가 말했다. \"삼촌은 아무 일 없으세요, 커틀 선장님.\"\n\n선장은 흡족함을 표하고는, 아래로 내려가 문을 열어 주겠다고 했고, 정말 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"그래도 이른 시간이구나, 월터.\" 위층으로 올라갔을 때, 선장이 여전히 미심쩍은 눈으로 그를 살피며 말했다.\n\n\"실은요, 커틀 선장님,\" 월터가 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"선장님이 외출하셨을까 봐 걱정돼서요, 선장님의 다정한 조언으로 도움을 좀 받고 싶어서요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그래야지.\" 선장이 말했다. \"뭘 들겠느냐?\"\n\n\"선장님 의견을 듣고 싶다는 거예요, 커틀 선장님.\" 월터가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"저한테 필요한 건 그것뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어디 말해 보거라.\" 선장이 말했다. \"마음껏, 얘야!\"\n\n월터는 그에게 무슨 일이 있었는지를 들려주었다. 삼촌 문제로 자기가 느끼는 어려움과, 커틀 선장이 친절하게도 그 어려움을 누그러뜨리도록 도와준다면 얼마나 마음이 놓일지를 이야기했다. 자기 앞에 펼쳐진 그 전망에 대한 커틀 선장의 한없는 경악과 놀라움이 차츰 그 신사를 통째로 삼켜 버려서, 마침내 그의 얼굴은 멍하니 텅 비고, 푸른 옷과 번들거리는 모자와 갈고리는 임자가 없는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n\"보세요, 커틀 선장님,\" 월터가 말을 이었다. \"저 자신으로 말하자면, 돔비 씨가 말씀하신 대로, 저는 젊고 따질 만한 사람도 못 돼요. 세상을 헤치고 나아가야 한다는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 오는 길에 생각해 보니, 삼촌에 관해서는 제가 꼭 각별히 신경 써야 할 점이 두 가지가 있었어요. 제가 삼촌 인생의 자랑이고 기쁨이 될 만하다는 뜻은 아니에요—제 말 믿으시죠, 알아요—하지만 저는 그래요. 자, 선장님도 그렇게 생각하지 않으세요?\"\n\n선장은 경악의 심연에서 솟아올라 자기 얼굴로 돌아오려고 애쓰는 듯했다. 그러나 그 노력이 헛수고로 끝나자, 번들거리는 모자만이 말없이, 형언할 수 없는 의미를 담아 끄덕였다.\n\n\"제가 살아 있고 건강하다면,\" 월터가 말했다. \"그건 걱정하지 않지만, 그래도 영국을 떠나면 삼촌을 다시 뵐 수 있으리라곤 거의 기대하기 어려워요. 삼촌은 늙으셨어요, 커틀 선장님. 게다가 삼촌의 삶이라는 건 단골손님의 삶이라서—\"\n\n\"잠깐, 월터! 단골손님이 없는 삶 말이냐?\" 선장이 불쑥 다시 나타나며 말했다.\n\n\"너무 맞는 말씀이에요.\" 월터가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"하지만 제 말은 습관의 삶이라는 거예요, 커틀 선장님—그런 종류의 익숙함 말이에요. 그리고 (선장님이 참으로 옳게 말씀하셨듯이, 정말 그래요) 가게며 그 오랜 세월 익숙해진 그 모든 물건을 잃으면 삼촌이 더 일찍 돌아가실 거라면, 또 잃는다면 조금 더 일찍 돌아가시지 않을까요—\"\n\n\"조카를 말이지.\" 선장이 끼어들었다. \"맞아!\"\n\n\"자 그럼,\" 월터가 명랑하게 말하려 애쓰며 말했다. \"어쨌든 우리는 이 이별이 그저 한때의 것이라고 삼촌이 믿도록 최선을 다해야 해요. 하지만 저는 더 잘 알고 있고, 아니 더 잘 알고 있을까 봐 두려운 처지라서요, 커틀 선장님. 또 제가 삼촌을 애정과 의무와 존경의 마음으로 대할 이유가 너무도 많아서, 만일 제가 그 일로 삼촌을 설득하려 든다면 아마 아주 서툴게 굴고 말 거예요. 그게 선장님이 삼촌에게 그 소식을 전해 주셨으면 하는 가장 큰 이유고요, 그게 첫 번째 점이에요.\"\n\n\"키를 한두 포인트 비껴 두게나!\" 선장이 사색에 잠긴 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고 하셨어요, 커틀 선장님?\" 월터가 물었다.\n\n\"대기하라!\" 선장이 깊이 생각하며 대답했다.\n\n월터는 여기에 선장이 덧붙일 특별한 정보가 있는지 알아보려고 잠시 멈추었으나, 더는 말이 없자 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이제 두 번째 점이에요, 커틀 선장님. 유감스럽지만, 저는 돔비 씨의 마음에 드는 사람이 아니에요. 저는 늘 최선을 다하려고 애써 왔고, 늘 그렇게 했어요. 하지만 그분은 저를 좋아하지 않으세요. 어쩌면 그분은 자기가 좋아하고 싫어하는 걸 어쩌지 못하시는지도 모르죠. 그건 아무 말 않겠어요. 다만 제가 확실히 아는 건, 그분이 저를 좋아하지 않는다는 거예요. 그분은 이 자리를 좋은 자리라고 해서 보내시는 게 아니에요. 실제보다 더 나은 것처럼 꾸며 말하기를 거절하시고요. 그리고 이 자리가 과연 저를 회사에서 출세시켜 줄지—오히려 정반대로, 저를 영영 처치해 버리고 비켜나게 하려는 건 아닐지, 저는 무척 의심스러워요. 그런데 이걸 삼촌에게는 한마디도 해서는 안 돼요, 커틀 선장님. 우리는 이걸 될 수 있는 한 좋고 전도유망한 일로 꾸며 내야 해요. 그리고 제가 선장님께 그게 실제로 어떤 자리인지를 말씀드리는 건, 다만 혹시라도 저 멀리 떨어진 제게 손을 보태 줄 무슨 방도가 생길 경우에 대비해서, 제 실제 처지를 아는 친구 한 사람이 고향에 있게 하려는 것뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"월터, 얘야,\" 선장이 대답했다. \"솔로몬의 잠언에는 이런 말이 있단다, '곤경에 빠진 벗이 없게 하시고, 그에게 줄 술병도 떨어지지 않게 하소서!' 찾거든, 적어 두거라.\"\n\n여기서 선장은 더없이 진실한 태도로—그 한마디 한마디가 천 마디의 말을 담고 있었다—월터에게 손을 내밀고, 동시에 (자기 인용의 정확함과 적절한 적용에 자부심을 느끼는지라) 거듭 말했다. \"찾거든, 적어 두거라.\"\n\n\"커틀 선장님,\" 월터가 선장이 내민 그 거대한 주먹을 두 손으로 받쳐 쥐며 말했다. 두 손으로 가득 찰 만큼 큰 주먹이었다. \"솔 삼촌 다음으로, 저는 선장님을 사랑해요. 이 세상에 선장님보다 더 안심하고 믿을 사람은 없다고 확신해요. 그저 떠나는 것 자체에 대해서는, 커틀 선장님, 저는 개의치 않아요. 왜 개의치 않겠어요! 만일 제가 자유로이 제 운을 찾아 나설 수 있다면—자유로이 평범한 선원으로 떠날 수 있다면—자유로이 제 힘으로 세상 끝까지라도 모험을 떠날 수 있다면—저는 기꺼이 가겠어요! 몇 해 전이었더라도 기꺼이 떠나, 무슨 일이 닥치든 운에 맡겼을 거예요. 하지만 그건 삼촌의 뜻에 어긋났고, 삼촌이 저를 위해 세워 둔 계획에도 어긋났어요. 그래서 그걸로 끝이었죠. 하지만 제가 느끼는 건요, 커틀 선장님, 우리가 줄곧 조금 잘못 생각해 왔다는 거예요. 제 앞날이 나아지는 일에 관한 한, 저는 돔비 회사에 처음 들어왔을 때보다 지금 조금도 더 나아진 게 없어요—어쩌면 조금 더 나빠졌는지도 몰라요. 그때는 회사가 저를 호의적으로 여겼을지도 모르지만, 지금은 분명 그렇지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"되돌아가거라, 휘팅턴.\" 한참 동안 월터를 바라보던 선장이 서글프게 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"네,\" 월터가 웃으며 대답했다. \"그것도 아주 여러 번 되돌아가야겠죠, 커틀 선장님. 그 사람의 운 같은 게 또다시 돌아오기 전까지는요. 그렇다고 불평하는 건 아니에요.\" 그는 특유의 활발하고 생기 넘치고 힘찬 태도로 덧붙였다. \"불평할 거리는 하나도 없어요. 저는 먹고살 길이 있어요. 살아갈 수 있고요. 제가 삼촌을 떠나면, 삼촌을 선장님께 맡기는 거예요. 선장님보다 더 나은 분에게는 맡길 수가 없어요, 커틀 선장님. 제가 이 모든 걸 말씀드린 건 절망해서가 아니에요, 저는 절망 같은 거 안 해요. 다만 제가 돔비 회사에서 이것저것 골라잡을 처지가 못 된다는 걸, 보내는 곳이면 가야 하고 주어지는 것이면 받아야 한다는 걸 선장님께 납득시키려는 거예요. 제가 멀리 떠나는 게 삼촌에게는 더 나아요. 돔비 씨는 삼촌에게 귀중한 친구거든요, 언제 그걸 몸소 보여 주셨는지 선장님도 아시잖아요, 커틀 선장님. 그리고 날마다 그분의 미움을 일깨우는 제가 그곳에 없으면, 그분이 덜 귀중한 친구가 되지는 않으리라 저는 확신해요. 그러니 서인도제도 만세예요, 커틀 선장님! 선원들이 부르는 그 노래가 어떻게 되더라?\n\n\"바베이도스 항구를 향하여, 친구들아! 유쾌하게! 정든 영국을 뒤로하고, 친구들아! 유쾌하게!\"\n여기서 선장이 합창으로 우렁차게 받았다— \"오 유쾌하게, 유쾌하게! 오 유쾌하—게!\"\n\n마지막 소절이, 길 건너편에 하숙하던, 영 말짱한 정신은 아닌 어느 혈기왕성한 선장의 예민한 귀에 가닿았다. 그는 즉시 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 창문을 들어 올리고는 길 건너로 목청껏 합세했으니, 멋들어진 효과를 자아냈다. 마지막 음을 더는 끌 수 없게 되자, 그 선장은 무시무시한 \"어이!\" 소리를 내질렀는데, 한편으로는 친근한 인사로, 또 한편으로는 자기가 전혀 숨차지 않다는 것을 보이려는 뜻이었다. 그렇게 하고 나서 그는 창문을 내리고 다시 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n\"자 이제, 커틀 선장님,\" 월터가 푸른 외투와 조끼를 건네주며 부산을 떨었다. \"솔 삼촌에게 가서 소식을 전해 주신다면(원래대로라면 며칠이고 전에 알고 계셨어야 할 일이지만요), 문 앞까지만 모셔다 드리고 저는 오후까지 여기저기 거닐다 올게요.\"\n\n그러나 선장은 그 임무를 도무지 달가워하는 기색이 아니었고, 그것을 해낼 자기 능력에 조금도 자신이 있어 보이지 않았다. 그는 월터의 앞날과 모험을 그것과는 아주 다르게, 그리고 자기 마음에 쏙 들도록 짜 두었던 것이다. 그 구상에 드러난 명민함과 선견지명을 두고 그토록 여러 번 흐뭇해했고, 그것이 구석구석 빈틈없이 완벽하다고 여겨 왔던 터라, 그것이 한꺼번에 산산조각 나도록 내버려 두는 것은, 더구나 그것을 부수는 일에 거드는 것은, 대단한 결단의 노력이 필요했다. 게다가 선장은, 그 일에 대한 묵은 생각을 부려 놓고 정황이 요구하는 그 속도로 전혀 새로운 짐을 실어 들이는 것이, 그것도 둘을 뒤죽박죽 섞고 헷갈리지 않으면서 그렇게 하는 것이 영 어려웠다. 그리하여 그는 월터의 기분에 보조를 맞출 수 있을 만한 그 어떤 성급함과도 거리가 멀게, 외투와 조끼를 걸치기는커녕, 당장은 그 옷들을 도무지 몸에 두르려 들지 않았다. 그러고는 이렇게 중대한 문제에는 \"손톱을 좀 물어뜯도록\" 허락받아야겠다고 월터에게 알렸다.\n\n\"이건 내 오랜 버릇이란다, 월터.\" 선장이 말했다. \"지난 오십 년 동안 줄곧 그랬지. 네드 커틀이 손톱 물어뜯는 걸 보거든, 월터, 그건 네드 커틀이 좌초했다는 걸 알면 된다.\"\n\n그러면서 선장은 마치 그것이 손이기라도 한 양 쇠갈고리를 이로 물고는, 온갖 철학적 성찰과 진지한 탐구의 정수이자 승화라 할 만한 지혜롭고 심오한 태도로, 그 문제를 갈래갈래 따져 보는 데 골몰했다.\n\n\"내 친구가 하나 있는데,\" 선장이 멍하니 중얼거렸다. \"지금은 휘트비 쪽으로 연안을 돌고 있다만, 이 문제든 그 밖에 무슨 문제든 의견을 내놓으라면, 의회를 한 수 접게 하고도 이길 만한 친구야. 그 사람은 바다에 두 번이나 떨어졌었지,\" 선장이 말했다. \"그런데도 멀쩡했어. 견습 시절엔 세 주 동안(이따금) 머리를 고리 볼트로 얻어맞기도 했지. 그런데도 그 친구만큼 머릿속이 맑은 사람은 또 없다니까.\"\n\n커틀 선장을 존경하는 마음에도 불구하고, 월터는 이 현자가 그 자리에 없는 것을 속으로 기뻐하지 않을 수 없었고, 자기 난관이 완전히 정리될 때까지는 그 맑디맑은 지성이 거기에 동원되지 않기를 간절히 바랐다.\n\n\"자네가 만일 그 사람을 데려다 노어의 부표를 보여 주고,\" 커틀 선장이 같은 어조로 말했다. \"그것에 대한 의견을 물으면 말이지, 월터, 그 사람은 자네 삼촌의 단추가 그 부표를 안 닮은 만큼이나 그 부표하고 영 딴판인 의견을 내놓을 거야. 두 다리로 걸어 다니는 사람치고—분명 두 다리로 걸어 다니는 사람치고—그 친구 발끝에라도 미칠 자가 없어. 발끝에도 못 미친다고!\"\n\n\"그분 이름이 뭐예요, 커틀 선장님?\" 선장의 친구에게 관심을 가져 보기로 마음먹은 월터가 물었다.\n\n\"그 사람 이름은 번스비야.\" 선장이 말했다. \"하지만 원, 그만한 머리를 가진 사람이라면 이름 따위야 뭔들 어떻겠느냐!\"\n\n이 마무리 칭찬에 선장이 정확히 어떤 뜻을 담았는지, 그는 더는 설명하지 않았다. 월터도 그것을 끌어내려 들지 않았다. 자기 자신과 자기 처지에 어울리는 그 활기로 제 일의 핵심을 다시 짚어 보기 시작했을 때, 그는 이내 선장이 전처럼 깊은 사색의 상태로 되돌아가 버린 것을 알아챘기 때문이다. 그 덥수룩한 눈썹 아래로 자기를 빤히 바라보면서도, 선장은 그를 보지도 듣지도 못하는 게 분명했고, 그저 골똘한 생각에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n사실 커틀 선장은 어찌나 큰 구상에 골몰했던지, 좌초하기는커녕 이내 가장 깊은 물속으로 떠나가 자기 통찰의 바닥을 도무지 짚을 수가 없었다. 차츰 선장에게는, 여기에 무슨 착오가 있는 게 분명하다는 것, 그것은 자기보다는 십중팔구 월터의 착오일 거라는 것, 만일 정말로 무슨 서인도제도 계획이 진행 중이라면 그것은 젊고 성급한 월터가 짐작하는 것과는 전혀 다른 것이라는 것, 그리고 그것은 보기 드물게 빠른 속도로 그의 재산을 일구어 주려는 어떤 새로운 방안일 수밖에 없다는 것이 더없이 분명해졌다. \"혹시 둘 사이에 무슨 자그마한 차질이라도 있다면,\" 월터와 돔비 씨 사이를 말하는 것이었는데, 선장은 생각했다. \"그저 양쪽 모두의 친구가 제때 한마디 거들어 주면, 바로잡고 매끄럽게 해서 죄다 다시 팽팽하게 만들 수 있지.\" 이런 생각에서 선장이 끌어낸 결론은 이러했다. 자기는 이미 브라이턴에서 돔비 씨와 무척 유쾌한 반 시간을 함께 보내며(돈을 빌리던 날 아침이었다) 그를 아는 즐거움을 누렸으니, 또 서로를 이해하고 서로 일을 원만히 풀어 갈 마음이 있는 두 세상 물정 밝은 사내라면 이 따위 자그마한 어려움쯤은 쉽게 정리하고 진짜 사실에 이를 수 있으니, 자기가 할 우정 어린 일은—지금 당장 월터에게는 그것에 관해 한마디도 않고—그저 돔비 씨 댁으로 슬쩍 올라가—하인에게 \"여보게, 미안하지만 커틀 선장이 왔다고 전해 주겠나?\"라고 일러—허물없는 정으로 돔비 씨를 만나—단춧구멍을 갈고리로 붙들고—그 일을 이야기해—죄다 바로잡고는—의기양양하게 물러나오는 것이었다!\n\n이런 생각들이 선장의 마음속에 떠오르고 천천히 이런 모양과 꼴을 갖추어 가면서, 그의 얼굴은 미심쩍던 아침이 밝은 한낮에 자리를 내주듯 환하게 개었다. 더없이 불길하던 눈썹은 거칠게 곤두선 기색을 누그러뜨리고 평온해졌다. 정신적 노고의 준엄함에 거의 감겨 있던 눈은 시원하게 열렸다. 처음에는 그저 세 점—입 오른쪽 끝에 하나, 양쪽 눈가에 각각 하나—에 지나지 않던 미소가 차츰 온 얼굴로 번져 나가더니, 이마까지 물결쳐 올라가 번들거리는 모자를 들어 올렸다. 마치 그 모자마저도 커틀 선장과 함께 좌초해 있다가 이제 그처럼 다행히도 다시 떠오른 것 같았다.\n\n마침내 선장은 손톱 물어뜯기를 그만두고 말했다. \"자, 월터, 얘야, 그 옷가지 입는 걸 좀 거들어 다오.\" 선장이 말한 옷가지란 외투와 조끼였다.\n\n월터는, 선장이 어째서 그토록 까다롭게 크라바트를 매만지는지—늘어진 양 끝을 일종의 변발처럼 비틀어, 어느 죽은 친구를 기리는 무덤 그림과 말끔한 철책과 나무가 새겨진 묵직한 금반지에 꿰는지—거의 짐작도 하지 못했다. 또 선장이 어째서 셔츠 깃을, 그 아래의 아일랜드제 리넨이 허락하는 한계까지 한껏 추켜올려, 그렇게 함으로써 완벽한 한 쌍의 눈가리개로 자기를 장식하는지도, 또 어째서 신발을 갈아 신고, 특별한 경우에만 신는 둘도 없는 발목 장화 한 켤레를 꿰차는지도 짐작하지 못했다. 마침내 선장은 자기가 완전히 만족할 만큼 차려입고는, 그러려고 못에서 떼어 낸 면도용 거울에 머리끝부터 발끝까지 자기 모습을 한번 비춰 보고 나서, 옹이 진 지팡이를 집어 들며 준비가 됐다고 말했다.\n\n거리로 나섰을 때 선장의 걸음걸이는 여느 때보다 우쭐해 있었으나, 월터는 그것을 발목 장화 탓이려니 여기고 별로 개의치 않았다. 얼마 가지 않아 둘은 꽃 파는 여인과 마주쳤다. 그러자 선장이, 무슨 묘안이라도 떠오른 듯 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 그녀의 바구니에서 가장 큰 다발을 사들였다. 그것은 더없이 화려한 부채꼴 꽃다발로, 둘레가 두 자 반쯤 되었고, 피어나는 꽃들 가운데 가장 흥겨워 보이는 것들로 죄다 엮은 것이었다.\n\n돔비 씨에게 줄 작정으로 이 자그마한 정표를 갖춰 든 채, 커틀 선장은 월터와 함께 걸어 악기 제작자의 문 앞에 이르렀고, 둘은 그 앞에서 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"들어가실 거죠?\" 월터가 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 선장이 대답했다. 그는 더 나아가기 전에 월터를 떼어 놓아야 한다는 것, 그리고 자기가 계획한 방문은 오후 늦게로 시각을 잡는 편이 낫겠다는 것을 느끼고 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 아무것도 잊지 않으실 거죠?\"\n\n\"안 잊지.\" 선장이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 저는 곧장 산책하러 가 볼게요,\" 월터가 말했다. \"그러면 제가 방해되지 않을 테니까요, 커틀 선장님.\"\n\n\"한참 멀리, 오래 다녀오너라, 얘야!\" 선장이 그의 뒤에 대고 외쳤다. 월터는 알겠다는 듯 손을 흔들고 제 길을 갔다.\n\n그의 길은 딱히 정해진 데가 없었다. 다만 그는 들판으로 나가 자기 앞에 놓인 알 수 없는 삶을 곰곰이 생각하고, 어느 나무 아래 쉬며 조용히 사색에 잠겨 볼까 했다. 그는 햄스테드 근처보다 더 좋은 들판을 알지 못했고, 거기에 닿는 데 돔비 씨 댁을 지나는 것보다 더 나은 길도 알지 못했다.\n\n그가 지나가며 그 찌푸린 듯한 정면을 올려다보았을 때, 그 집은 여느 때처럼 으리으리하고 음침했다. 차양은 죄다 내려져 있었으나, 위층 창문들은 활짝 열려 있었고, 그 휘장을 흔들며 이리저리 나부끼게 하는 상쾌한 바람만이 그 외관 전체에서 유일하게 살아 있다는 표시였다. 월터는 지나가며 발소리를 죽였고, 그 집을 한두 집 뒤로하고 나서야 마음이 놓였다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 뒤를 돌아보았다. 여러 해 전 길 잃은 아이의 그 모험이 있은 뒤로 줄곧 그 자리에 품어 온 관심을 안고서, 특히 그 위층 창문들을 바라보았다. 그렇게 바라보고 있는 동안, 마차 한 대가 문 앞으로 들어서더니, 묵직한 시곗줄을 단 검은 옷차림의 풍채 좋은 신사가 내려 안으로 들어갔다. 나중에 그 신사와 그의 마차를 함께 떠올리고서, 월터는 그가 의사임을 의심치 않았다. 그러고는 누가 아픈 걸까 궁금해졌으나, 그 짐작은 다른 일들을 하릴없이 생각하며 한참을 걷고 나서야 떠올랐다.\n\n그래도 여전히, 그 집이 그에게 떠올리게 한 것에 잠겨 있었다. 월터는, 어쩌면 언젠가 때가 오면, 옛 친구이자 그 뒤로 줄곧 그토록 고마워하고 그를 보면 그토록 기뻐하던 그 아름다운 아이가, 자기를 위해 제 오라비의 관심을 끌어 주고 자기 앞날을 더 낫게 좌우해 줄지도 모른다고 생각하며 흐뭇해했다. 그는 이런 상상이 좋았는데—그 순간으로서는, 그것으로 얻을 어떤 세속적인 이득보다도, 그녀가 자기를 줄곧 기억해 준다고 상상하는 즐거움 때문이었다. 그러나 또 하나의 더 차분한 공상이 그에게 속삭였다. 그때쯤 자기가 살아 있다 한들 바다 건너 멀리 잊힌 몸일 테고, 그녀는 결혼해서 부유하고 도도하고 행복할 거라고. 그토록 처지가 달라졌다면, 그녀가 어린 시절 가지고 놀던 어떤 장난감보다 더 깊은 관심으로 자기를 기억할 까닭은 없었다. 아니, 그만큼의 관심조차도 없으리라.\n\n그래도 월터는, 거친 거리를 헤매던 그 어여쁜 아이를 한껏 이상화했고, 그 밤의 천진한 고마움이며 그것을 드러내던 소박함과 진실함과 그녀를 너무도 단단히 하나로 묶어 두었던지라, 그녀가 언젠가 도도해질 수 있다고 따져 보는 자기 자신을, 명예를 헐뜯는 자라며 부끄러워했다. 한편으로 그의 사색은 어찌나 변덕스러운 갈래의 것이었던지, 그녀가 여인으로 자란 모습을 상상하는 것—착한 브라운 부인 시절에 그러했던, 그 한결같이 꾸밈없고 다정하고 사랑스러운 어린 존재 이외의 무엇으로 그녀를 떠올리는 것—조차 그에게는 못지않게 헐뜯는 일처럼 여겨졌다. 한마디로, 월터는 플로렌스에 대해 자기 자신과 따져 보는 일이 무릇 더없이 도리에 어긋난 짓이 되고 만다는 것을 깨달았다. 그리고 그녀의 모습을, 무언가 귀하고 닿을 수 없고 변치 않고 막연한 것으로—그에게 기쁨을 주고 천사의 손길처럼 그를 무엇이든 떳떳지 못한 것에서 붙들어 주는 그 힘만은 막연하지 않은, 그런 것으로—마음속에 간직하는 것보다 더 나은 일은 할 수 없다는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n그날 월터는 들판에서 오래도록 거닐며, 새소리와 일요일의 종소리와 누그러진 도시의 웅성거림에 귀를 기울였고—달콤한 향내를 들이쉬었고, 이따금 항해와 목적지가 놓인 저 희미한 지평선 너머를 바라보다가, 다시 둘레의 푸른 영국 풀밭과 정든 풍경을 둘러보았다. 그러나 그는 떠난다는 것조차도 또렷이 생각해 본 적이 거의 없었고, 시간시간, 분분이 사색을 하릴없이 미루는 듯하면서도, 정작 그러는 내내 줄곧 사색하고 있었다.\n\n월터가 들판을 뒤로하고, 똑같이 멍한 기분에 잠겨 집으로 터덜터덜 돌아오고 있을 때, 한 남자가 고함치는 소리가 들리더니, 이어 한 여자의 목소리가 큰 소리로 그의 이름을 부르는 것이 들렸다. 놀라 재빨리 돌아보니, 반대 방향으로 가던 삯마차 한 대가 멀지 않은 데 멈춰 서 있었고, 마부가 마부석에서 뒤를 돌아보며 채찍으로 그에게 신호를 보내고 있었으며, 안에서는 젊은 여자가 창밖으로 몸을 내밀고 엄청난 기세로 손짓하고 있었다. 그 마차로 달려가 보니, 그 젊은 여자는 니퍼 양이었고, 니퍼 양은 거의 제정신이 아닐 만큼 안절부절못하고 있었다.\n\n\"스태그스 가든이요, 월터 씨!\" 니퍼 양이 말했다. \"제발요, 아, 좀요!\"\n\n\"네?\" 월터가 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이에요?\"\n\n\"아, 월터 씨, 스태그스 가든이요, 제발요!\" 수전이 말했다.\n\n\"이거 보세요!\" 마부가 일종의 의기양양한 절망을 담아 월터에게 하소연하며 외쳤다. \"저 아가씨가 꼬박 한 시간 넘게 저러는 통에, 저는 아가씨가 들어가겠다는 막다른 골목들에서 끊임없이 후진해 빠져나오고 있다니까요. 이 마차에 첫 손님부터 마지막 손님까지 수도 없이 태워 봤지만, 이런 손님은 처음입니다.\"\n\n\"스태그스 가든에 가고 싶은 거예요, 수전?\" 월터가 물었다.\n\n\"아! 거기 가고 싶다잖습니까! 근데 그게 어디냔 말이오?\" 마부가 으르렁댔다.\n\n\"그게 어딘지 모르겠어요!\" 수전이 정신없이 외쳤다. \"월터 씨, 저도 한 번 거기 가 본 적 있어요, 플로이 아가씨랑 우리 가엾고 사랑스러운 폴 도련님이랑요, 바로 그날, 월터 씨가 시내에서 플로이 아가씨를 찾아 주신 그날 말이에요, 우리가 집에 오다가 아가씨를 잃어버렸잖아요, 리처즈 부인이랑 저랑요, 미친 황소랑, 리처즈 부인 맏이까지 있었는데요, 그러고 나서 제가 나중에 거기 가 보긴 했는데, 그게 어딘지 도무지 기억이 안 나요, 땅속으로 꺼져 버린 것 같아요. 아, 월터 씨, 저를 버리지 마세요, 스태그스 가든이요, 제발요! 플로이 아가씨의 사랑둥이—우리 모두의 사랑둥이—여리디여린 폴 도련님요! 아, 월터 씨!\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 월터가 외쳤다. \"그 애가 많이 아파요?\"\n\n\"그 어여쁜 꽃송이가요!\" 수전이 두 손을 쥐어짜며 외쳤다. \"옛 유모를 보고 싶다는 생각이 들었대요, 그래서 제가 그분을 도련님 머리맡으로 모셔 가려고 온 거예요, 폴리 투들의 가든에 사는 스태그스 부인 말이에요, 누구든 좀 제발요!\"\n\n들은 말에 크게 마음이 움직이고, 곧장 수전의 절박함에 휩싸인 월터는, 이제 그녀의 심부름이 어떤 성격의 것인지 알게 되자, 어찌나 열성으로 거기에 뛰어들었던지, 마부는 앞장서 달리며 여기저기 가는 데마다 스태그스 가든으로 가는 길을 물어 대는 그를 바싹 뒤따르느라 진땀을 뺐다.\n\n스태그스 가든이라는 곳은 없었다. 그것은 땅 위에서 사라져 버렸다. 한때 낡고 썩은 정자들이 서 있던 자리에는 이제 궁궐 같은 건물들이 머리를 치켜들었고, 어마어마한 둘레의 화강암 기둥들이 저 너머 철도 세계로 통하는 전망을 열어젖혔다. 옛날 폐기물이 쌓여 있던 그 비참한 황무지는 삼켜져 자취를 감추었고, 그 추레하던 자리에는 값진 물건과 비싼 상품으로 가득 찬 창고들이 층층이 늘어섰다. 옛 골목길에는 이제 온갖 종류의 행인과 탈것이 우글거렸다. 진창과 짐수레 바퀴 자국 속에서 의기소침하게 멈춰 섰던 새 거리들은 그 자체로 하나의 도시를 이루었으니, 거기 생겨나기 전에는 시도된 적도 생각된 적도 없던, 저마다의 건강한 안락과 편의를 만들어 냈다. 아무 데로도 통하지 않던 다리들이 별장으로, 정원으로, 교회로, 건강에 좋은 공중 산책로로 통했다. 집들의 잔해와 새 도로의 시작점들은 증기 그 자체의 속도로 선로 위로 출발해, 괴물 같은 열차가 되어 시골로 쏜살같이 내달았다.\n\n띄엄띄엄 헤매던 시절에 철도를 인정하기를 망설였던 그 동네로 말하자면, 어떤 기독교도라도 그런 경우에 그러하듯 현명하고 뉘우치는 처지가 되어, 이제는 그 강력하고 번창하는 친척을 자랑하고 있었다. 포목점 진열창에는 철도 무늬가, 신문 가게 창문에는 철도 신문이 놓여 있었다. 철도 호텔, 철도 사무소, 철도 셋집, 철도 하숙집이 있었고, 철도 도면, 지도, 풍경화, 포장지, 병, 샌드위치 통, 시간표가 있었고, 철도 삯마차와 마차 정류장이 있었으며, 철도 합승마차, 철도 거리와 건물, 철도에 빌붙어 사는 자들과 기생충들, 셀 수 없이 많은 아첨꾼들이 있었다. 심지어 시계도 철도 시각을 따랐으니, 마치 해마저도 항복한 듯했다. 정복당한 자들 가운데는 굴뚝 청소부 우두머리가 있었는데, 한때 스태그스 가든에서 철도를 도무지 믿지 않던 그가, 이제는 삼 층짜리 회벽 집에 살면서, 니스 칠한 간판에 금빛 장식 글씨로, 기계를 이용한 철도 굴뚝 청소 도급업자라고 자기를 내세우고 있었다.\n\n이 거대한 변화의 심장으로, 또 그곳에서, 밤낮으로 하루 종일, 고동치는 흐름이 마치 그곳의 핏줄처럼 끊임없이 밀려들고 되돌아갔다. 떠나고 도착하기를 스물네 시간마다 수십 번씩 거듭하는 사람들의 무리와 산더미 같은 상품들은, 그곳에 언제나 작동하고 있는 발효를 일으켰다. 집들조차 짐을 꾸려 여행을 떠나고 싶어 하는 것 같았다. 불과 이십여 년 전만 해도 기술자들의 황당한 철도 이론을 비웃고, 반대 신문에서 그들에게 더없이 신랄한 핀잔을 안기던 그 놀라운 국회의원들이, 시계를 손에 든 채 북부로 내려갔고, 자기들이 간다고 알리려 전기 전신으로 미리 전갈을 보냈다. 밤낮으로 정복자인 기관차들은 저 멀리서 제 일을 하며 우르릉거렸고, 또는 매끄럽게 여정의 끝으로 다가와, 자기들을 맞이하려 한 치까지 파 놓은 지정된 모퉁이로 길든 용처럼 미끄러져 들어와서는, 부글거리고 떨며 거기 멈춰 서서 벽을 진동시켰으니, 마치 자기 안에 아직 짐작 못 한 거대한 힘과 아직 이루지 못한 강력한 목적을 은밀히 알고서 그것으로 부풀어 오르는 듯했다.\n\n그러나 스태그스 가든은 뿌리째 가지째 잘려 나갔다. 오, 슬프도다, \"영국 땅 한 평\"도—스태그스 가든에 펼쳐진 그 한 평조차—안전하지 못한 그날이여!\n\n마침내, 헛된 수소문을 많이 한 끝에, 월터는 마차와 수전을 거느린 채, 그 사라진 땅에 한때 살았던 한 남자를 찾아냈다. 그는 다름 아닌 앞서 말한 굴뚝 청소부 우두머리로, 뚱뚱해진 몸으로 제 집 문을 똑똑 두드리고 있었다. 그는 투들을 잘 안다고 말했다. 철도에 다니지 않느냐고 했다.\n\n\"네, 선생님, 맞아요!\" 수전 니퍼가 마차 창문에서 외쳤다.\n\n이제 그가 어디 사느냐고, 월터가 다급히 물었다.\n\n그는 회사 소유 건물에 살았는데, 오른쪽으로 두 번째 모퉁이를 돌아, 마당을 내려가, 가로질러 가서, 다시 오른쪽으로 두 번째 집이었다. 십일 호였으니, 못 알아볼 리가 없지만, 혹시 못 찾으면 그저 기관 화부 투들을 찾기만 하면 누구든 그의 집이 어딘지 가르쳐 줄 거라고 했다. 이 뜻밖의 성공에 수전 니퍼는 부랴부랴 마차에서 내려, 월터의 팔을 붙들고, 숨 가쁜 걸음으로 걸어서 출발했다. 마차는 거기 남겨 두어 그들이 돌아오기를 기다리게 했다.\n\n\"그 어린 도련님이 아픈 지 오래됐어요, 수전?\" 서둘러 가면서 월터가 물었다.\n\n\"오랫동안 시름시름 앓았는데, 얼마나 심한지는 아무도 몰랐어요.\" 수전이 말했다. 그러고는 몹시 날카롭게 덧붙였다. \"아, 그 블림버 사람들!\"\n\n\"블림버요?\" 월터가 따라 물었다.\n\n\"이런 때에 제 자신을 용서할 수가 없을 거예요, 월터 씨,\" 수전이 말했다. \"이렇게 심각한 슬픔을 생각해야 할 때에, 제가 누구한테든 모질게 군다면, 더구나 그 어린 사랑둥이 폴 도련님이 좋게 말하는 사람들한테 그런다면요. 하지만 그 가족이 돌투성이 땅에서 새 길을 닦는 일에 부려지고, 블림버 양이 앞장서서 곡괭이를 들기를 바랄 수는 있겠죠!\"\n\n그러고 나서 니퍼 양은 숨을 돌리고는, 이 별난 소망이 마음을 풀어 주기라도 한 듯, 전보다 더 빠르게 걸어 나갔다. 이때쯤에는 자기 숨조차 가다듬을 여력이 없던 월터는 더는 아무것도 묻지 않고 부지런히 따라갔다. 그리고 둘은 조급한 마음에 곧 자그마한 문을 박차고 들어가, 아이들로 가득 찬 깨끗한 응접실로 들어섰다.\n\n\"리처즈 부인은 어디 계세요?\" 수전 니퍼가 둘레를 둘러보며 외쳤다. \"아, 리처즈 부인, 리처즈 부인, 저랑 같이 가요, 우리 다정한 분!\"\n\n\"아니, 수전 아니야!\" 폴리가 그 무리 가운데서, 그 정직한 얼굴과 자애로운 몸매로 몹시 놀라며 일어섰다.\n\n\"네, 리처즈 부인, 저예요,\" 수전이 말했다. \"차라리 제가 아니었으면 싶긴 한데요, 이런 말 한다고 비위 맞추려는 것처럼 보이진 않겠죠, 하지만 어린 폴 도련님이 많이 아파요, 그래서 오늘 아버님께 옛 유모의 얼굴을 보고 싶다고 했대요. 그래서 도련님이랑 플로이 아가씨가 부디 저랑 같이 가 주셨으면 해요—그리고 월터 씨도요, 리처즈 부인—지난 일은 잊으시고, 시들어 가는 그 어여쁜 분께 친절을 베풀어 주세요. 아, 리처즈 부인, 시들어 가고 있어요!\" 수전 니퍼가 우는 통에, 폴리도 그녀를 보고, 또 그녀가 한 말을 듣고 눈물을 흘렸다. 그리고 아이들이 죄다 둘레로 모여들었고(새로 난 아기들도 여럿 끼어 있었다), 마침 버밍엄에서 막 돌아와 사발에 담긴 저녁을 먹고 있던 투들 씨는 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고, 문 뒤에 걸려 있던 아내의 보닛과 숄을 가져다 그녀에게 입혀 주고는, 등을 톡톡 두드리며, 능변이라기보다는 아버지다운 정으로 말했다. \"폴리! 어서 가!\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 마부가 예상한 것보다 훨씬 일찍 마차로 돌아왔다. 월터는 수전과 리처즈 부인을 안에 태우고, 더는 착오가 없도록 자기는 마부석에 자리를 잡고는, 둘을 돔비 씨 댁 현관까지 무사히 데려다 놓았다—그런데 마침 거기서, 그는 커다란 꽃다발 하나가 놓여 있는 것을 보았으니, 그것은 그날 아침 커틀 선장이 자기와 함께 있을 때 사들인 그 꽃다발을 떠올리게 했다. 그는 어린 환자에 대해 더 알고 싶어 머무르고 싶었고, 아주 조금이라도 도움이 될 수 있을지 보려고 얼마든 기다리고 싶었다. 그러나 그런 행동이 돔비 씨에게는 주제넘고 나서는 짓으로 비치리라는 것을 뼈저리게 의식하고 있었던지라, 그는 천천히, 서글프게, 불안한 마음으로 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n문에서 채 오 분도 걸어가지 않았을 때, 한 남자가 그를 뒤쫓아 달려와 돌아와 달라고 청했다. 월터는 최대한 빨리 발길을 되돌려, 서글픈 예감을 안고 그 음침한 집으로 들어섰다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI. What the Waves were always saying\n\n폴은 그 작은 침대에서 한 번도 일어나지 못했다. 그는 거기 누운 채 거리의 소음에 귀를 기울였는데, 더없이 평온했다. 시간이 어떻게 흘러가든 그다지 마음 쓰지 않으면서도, 시간을 지켜보고 자기 주위의 모든 것을 관찰하는 눈으로 바라보았다.\n\n햇살이 바스락거리는 블라인드를 뚫고 그의 방으로 들이쳐 맞은편 벽 위에서 금빛 물결처럼 일렁일 때면, 그는 저녁이 다가오고 있으며 하늘이 붉고 아름답다는 것을 알았다. 그 반사가 사그라들고 어스름이 벽을 타고 슬그머니 기어오르면, 그는 그것이 깊어지고, 깊어지고, 깊어져 밤이 되는 것을 지켜보았다. 그러면 그는 긴 거리마다 등불이 점점이 박혀 있는 모습과, 머리 위에서 평화로운 별들이 빛나는 모습을 떠올렸다. 그의 공상은 이상하게도 강으로 흘러가는 버릇이 있었는데, 그 강이 이 거대한 도시를 가로질러 흐른다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 그리고 이제 그는 그 강이 얼마나 검은지, 무수한 별을 비추면 얼마나 깊어 보일지를 생각했다—그리고 무엇보다도, 그것이 바다를 만나러 얼마나 한결같이 굽이쳐 흘러가는지를 생각했다.\n\n밤이 더 깊어 거리의 발걸음 소리가 어찌나 드물어지던지 다가오는 소리를 듣고, 지나가는 동안 그 수를 세고, 텅 빈 먼 곳으로 사라지는 것을 들을 수 있게 되면, 그는 누운 채 촛불 둘레의 알록달록한 고리를 지켜보며 참을성 있게 날이 밝기를 기다렸다. 그의 유일한 괴로움은 그 빠르고 거센 강이었다. 그는 때때로 그것을 멈추려고—제 어린 손으로 막아 보려고—혹은 모래로 그 길을 메우려고 애써야 할 것 같은 충동을 느꼈고, 그것이 막을 수 없이 다가오는 것을 보면 비명을 질렀다! 그러나 늘 곁에 있던 플로렌스의 한마디가 그를 제정신으로 되돌려 놓았고, 그러면 그는 가엾은 머리를 그녀의 가슴에 기댄 채 플로이에게 자기 꿈 이야기를 들려주며 미소 지었다.\n\n다시 날이 밝아 오기 시작하면 그는 해를 기다렸다. 그리고 그 명랑한 빛이 방 안에서 반짝이기 시작하면, 그는 마음속으로 그려 보았다—그려 보았다니! 그는 보았다—높은 교회 첨탑들이 아침 하늘로 솟아오르는 모습을, 도시가 되살아나 깨어나며 다시 한번 생기로 화들짝 일어서는 모습을, 강이 굽이치며 반짝이는 모습을(그러나 여전히 빠르게 굽이쳐 흐르는 모습을), 그리고 이슬로 환히 빛나는 들판을. 낯익은 소리와 외침이 차츰 아래 거리로 들려왔다. 집 안의 하인들은 깨어나 분주했고, 얼굴들이 문 안을 들여다보았으며, 목소리들이 그의 시중드는 이들에게 그가 어떠한지 가만히 물었다. 폴은 늘 자기가 직접 대답했다. \"저는 좀 나아졌어요. 훨씬 나아졌어요, 고마워요! 아빠한테도 그렇게 말해 줘요!\"\n\n조금씩, 그는 낮의 부산함에, 마차와 짐수레의 소음에, 오가는 사람들에 지쳐 갔다. 그러면 잠이 들거나, 다시금 그 거센 강에 대한 불안하고 편치 않은 느낌에 시달렸다—그것이 잠든 사이의 일인지 깨어 있는 동안의 일인지 아이로서는 도무지 분간할 수 없었다. \"플로이, 저건 왜 멈추질 않지?\" 그는 가끔 그녀에게 물었다. \"날 떠내려보내는 것 같아!\"\n\n그러나 플로이는 언제나 그를 달래고 안심시킬 수 있었다. 그리고 그녀에게 그의 베개에 머리를 누이고 잠시 쉬게 하는 것이 그의 날마다의 기쁨이었다.\n\n\"넌 늘 날 지켜보잖아, 플로이, 이번엔 내가 널 지켜보게 해 줘!\" 그러면 사람들은 그를 침대 한구석에 쿠션으로 받쳐 일으켜 앉혔고, 그는 그녀가 곁에 누워 있는 동안 거기 기대어 있었다. 이따금 몸을 앞으로 굽혀 그녀에게 입을 맞추고, 가까이 있는 이들에게 그녀가 지쳤다고, 그녀가 자기 곁에서 얼마나 여러 밤을 새웠는지를 속삭이면서.\n\n이렇게 낮의 열기와 빛 속에서 붉게 달아오른 하루가 차츰 기울어 갔다. 그리고 다시 금빛 물결이 벽 위에서 춤을 추곤 했다.\n\n그를 찾아오는 근엄한 의사가 셋이나 되었는데—그들은 아래층에 모였다가 함께 올라오곤 했다—방이 어찌나 조용하고 폴이 그들을 어찌나 유심히 살피던지(다만 그들이 무슨 말을 하는지는 누구에게도 묻지 않았다), 그는 그들의 회중시계 소리의 차이까지 알아챌 정도였다. 그러나 그의 관심은 파커 펩스 경에게 쏠려 있었는데, 그분은 늘 침대 한쪽에 자리를 잡았다. 폴은 오래전에 그들이 하는 말을 들은 적이 있었다. 그분이 자기 엄마가 플로렌스를 품에 안고 숨을 거두던 그 자리에 함께 있었다는 것을. 그리고 그는 이제 그것을 잊을 수가 없었다. 그는 그 일로 그분을 좋아했다. 그는 두렵지 않았다.\n\n그를 둘러싼 사람들은 블림버 박사네에서의 그 첫날 밤처럼 까닭 모르게 바뀌어 갔다—플로렌스만은 예외였다. 플로렌스는 결코 바뀌지 않았다—그리고 파커 펩스 경이었던 것이 이제는 한 손에 머리를 괸 채 앉아 있는 그의 아버지가 되어 있었다. 안락의자에서 졸고 있던 늙은 피프친 부인은 종종 톡스 양으로, 혹은 그의 고모로 바뀌었다. 그래도 폴은 다시 눈을 감고 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어지는지를 아무 감정 없이 바라보는 것으로 흡족했다. 그러나 손에 머리를 괸 이 형상은 어찌나 자주 돌아오고, 어찌나 오래 머물며, 어찌나 가만히 그리고 엄숙하게 앉아, 말하는 일도 없고 누가 말을 거는 일도 없으며, 좀처럼 얼굴을 들지 않던지, 폴은 나른하게 그것이 진짜일까 궁금해하기 시작했다. 그리고 밤이 되면 그것이 거기 앉아 있는 것을 두려움에 차서 바라보았다.\n\n\"플로이!\" 그가 말했다. \"저게 뭐야?\"\n\n\"어디, 우리 아가?\"\n\n\"저기! 침대 발치에.\"\n\n\"저기엔 아무것도 없어, 아빠밖에는!\"\n\n그 형상은 머리를 들고 일어나, 침대맡으로 다가와 말했다. \"내 아들아! 날 모르겠느냐?\"\n\n폴은 그 얼굴을 들여다보며, 이것이 자기 아버지일까 생각했다. 그러나 그가 보기에 너무도 변해 버린 그 얼굴은, 바라보는 동안 마치 고통에 잠긴 듯 떨렸다. 그리고 그가 두 손을 뻗어 그 얼굴을 감싸 쥐고 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기기도 전에, 그 형상은 작은 침대에서 급히 돌아서서 문밖으로 나가 버렸다.\n\n폴은 두근거리는 마음으로 플로렌스를 바라보았으나, 그녀가 무슨 말을 하려는지 알았기에, 자기 얼굴을 그녀의 입술에 갖다 대어 그 말을 막았다. 다음번에 그 형상이 침대 발치에 앉아 있는 것을 보았을 때, 그는 그것을 불렀다.\n\n\"저 때문에 슬퍼하지 마세요, 아빠! 정말 저는 아주 행복해요!\"\n\n그의 아버지가 다가와 그에게 몸을 굽히자—이번에는 침대맡에서 먼저 멈추지 않고 재빨리 그렇게 했다—폴은 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안고, 그 말을 여러 번, 아주 간절하게 되풀이했다. 그리고 그 뒤로 폴은 낮이든 밤이든 방 안에서 그를 볼 때마다 어김없이 외쳤다. \"저 때문에 슬퍼하지 마세요! 정말 저는 아주 행복해요!\" 이것이 그가 아침마다 자기는 훨씬 나아졌으니 아버지께 그렇게 전해 달라고 늘 말하게 된 시초였다.\n\n금빛 물결이 벽 위에서 몇 번이나 춤을 추었는지, 그 어둡고 어두운 강이 그의 뜻과 상관없이 바다를 향해 몇 밤이나 굽이쳐 흘렀는지, 폴은 결코 세지 않았고, 알려고 하지도 않았다. 만일 그들의 다정함이, 혹은 그것을 느끼는 그의 마음이 더 커질 수 있었다면, 그들은 날마다 더 다정했고 그는 날마다 더 고마워했다. 그러나 그것이 여러 날이었는지 며칠 안 되는 날이었는지는, 이제 그 온순한 소년에게는 별로 중요치 않은 듯 보였다.\n\n어느 날 밤 그는 어머니를, 그리고 아래층 응접실에 걸린 그녀의 초상화를 생각하다가, 어머니는 사랑스러운 플로렌스를 아버지보다 더 사랑하셨음에 틀림없다고 생각했다. 자신이 죽어 가고 있음을 느꼈을 때 그 아이를 품에 안으셨으니까—왜냐하면 그토록 그녀를 깊이 사랑하는 오라비인 그조차도, 그보다 더 큰 소망은 가질 수 없었을 테니까. 이 생각의 흐름은 자기가 어머니를 본 적이 있었던가 물어보고 싶은 마음을 불러일으켰다. 그가 어머니를 본 적이 있다고 그들이 말해 주었는지 아닌지를 그는 기억할 수 없었는데, 강이 너무 빠르게 흘러 그의 머리를 어지럽혔기 때문이었다.\n\n\"플로이, 내가 엄마를 본 적이 있어?\"\n\n\"아니, 아가, 왜?\"\n\n\"내가 아기였을 때 엄마 얼굴 같은, 다정한 얼굴이 날 들여다본 적이 있었어, 플로이?\"\n\n그는 마치 어떤 얼굴의 환영이 눈앞에 있기라도 한 듯, 믿기지 않는다는 투로 물었다.\n\n\"오, 그럼, 있었지!\"\n\n\"누구 얼굴인데, 플로이?\"\n\n\"네 옛날 유모. 자주.\"\n\n\"그럼 내 옛날 유모는 어디 있어?\" 폴이 말했다. \"그 사람도 죽었어? 플로이, 우리 모두 죽은 거야, 너만 빼고?\"\n\n방 안이 잠시—어쩌면 좀 더 길었을지도 모르지만, 그래도 그 이상은 아닌 듯했다—술렁였다. 그러고는 다시 모든 것이 고요해졌다. 그리고 플로렌스가, 얼굴은 핏기 하나 없었으나 미소 지으며, 그의 머리를 자기 팔에 받쳐 안았다. 그녀의 팔은 몹시 떨렸다.\n\n\"그 옛날 유모를 보여 줘, 플로이, 부탁이야!\"\n\n\"지금 여기 안 계셔, 아가. 내일 오실 거야.\"\n\n\"고마워, 플로이!\"\n\n폴은 그 말과 함께 눈을 감고 잠이 들었다. 그가 깨어났을 때 해는 높이 떠 있었고, 환한 대낮은 맑고 따뜻했다. 그는 한동안 누워서, 활짝 열린 채 커튼이 바람에 바스락거리며 이리저리 나부끼는 창문을 바라보았다. 그러고는 말했다. \"플로이, 지금이 내일이야? 그분 오셨어?\"\n\n누군가가 그녀를 찾으러 가는 것 같았다. 아마 수전이었을 것이다. 폴이 다시 눈을 감았을 때, 그녀가 곧 돌아오겠다고 그에게 말하는 소리를 들은 것 같았으나, 그는 눈을 떠서 확인하지는 않았다. 그녀는 약속을 지켰다—어쩌면 한 번도 자리를 뜨지 않았는지도 모른다—그러나 다음으로 벌어진 일은 계단을 오르는 발걸음 소리였다. 그러자 폴이 깨어났다—마음도 몸도 깨어났다—그리고 침대에서 똑바로 일어나 앉았다. 그는 이제 자기 주위에 있는 그들이 보였다. 밤에 이따금 그러했던 것처럼 그들 앞을 가린 잿빛 안개는 없었다. 그는 그들을 하나하나 다 알아보았고, 이름을 불렀다.\n\n\"그런데 이분은 누구야? 이분이 내 옛날 유모야?\" 아이가 안으로 들어오는 한 형상을 환한 미소로 바라보며 말했다.\n\n그렇다, 그렇다. 그를 보고 그런 눈물을 흘리며 그를 자기의 귀한 아들, 자기의 예쁜 아들, 자기의 가엾고 시들어 버린 아이라고 부를 다른 낯선 이는 없었을 것이다. 그의 침대맡에 몸을 굽혀, 그의 야윈 손을 들어 올려 입술과 가슴에 갖다 대며, 마치 그 손을 어루만질 어떤 권리라도 가진 사람처럼 굴 다른 여인은 없었을 것이다. 그곳에 있는 모든 이를 그렇게 잊어버리고 오직 그와 플로이만을 생각하며, 그토록 다정함과 연민으로 가득 찰 다른 여인은 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"플로이! 이건 다정하고 좋은 얼굴이야!\" 폴이 말했다. \"다시 보게 되어 기뻐. 가지 마세요, 옛날 유모! 여기 계세요.\"\n\n그의 감각이 온통 또렷해졌고, 그는 자기가 아는 이름 하나를 들었다.\n\n\"방금 '월터'라고 한 사람이 누구야?\" 그가 둘러보며 물었다. \"누가 월터라고 했어. 그가 여기 있어? 그를 정말 보고 싶어.\"\n\n아무도 곧바로 대답하지 않았다. 그러나 그의 아버지가 곧 수전에게 말했다. \"그럼 그 애를 다시 불러라. 올라오라고 해!\" 잠깐의 기대에 찬 침묵이 흐르는 동안, 그는 미소 띤 흥미와 경이로움으로 자기 유모를 바라보았고, 그녀가 플로이를 잊지 않았음을 알아보았다. 이윽고 월터가 방 안으로 데려와졌다. 그의 솔직한 얼굴과 태도, 그리고 그의 명랑한 눈은 늘 그를 폴이 좋아하는 사람으로 만들었다. 그리고 폴은 그를 보자 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"잘 가요!\"\n\n\"잘 가렴, 얘야!\" 피프친 부인이 그의 침대맡으로 황급히 다가오며 말했다. \"잘 가라는 건 아니지?\"\n\n한순간 폴은, 난롯가 자기 구석에서 그토록 자주 그녀를 바라보던 그 애틋한 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보았다. \"맞아요,\" 그가 차분하게 말했다, \"잘 가요! 월터, 잘 가요!\"—그가 서 있는 쪽으로 머리를 돌리고 다시 손을 내밀면서. \"아빠는 어디 있어요?\"\n\n그 말이 입술에서 떨어지기도 전에 그는 자기 뺨에 닿는 아버지의 숨결을 느꼈다.\n\n\"월터를 기억해 주세요, 아빠,\" 그가 아버지의 얼굴을 들여다보며 속삭였다. \"월터를 기억해 주세요. 저는 월터가 좋았어요!\" 그 가냘픈 손이 허공에서 흔들렸다, 마치 월터에게 다시 한번 \"잘 가요!\" 하고 외치기라도 하듯.\n\n\"이제 날 눕혀 줘,\" 그가 말했다, \"그리고, 플로이, 내 곁에 바싹 와서, 너를 보게 해 줘!\"\n\n누이와 동생은 서로의 몸을 두 팔로 휘감았고, 금빛 햇살이 흘러들어 와, 꼭 끌어안은 두 사람 위로 내려앉았다.\n\n\"초록 강둑과 갈대 사이로 강이 얼마나 빠르게 흐르는지, 플로이! 하지만 바다가 아주 가까워. 파도 소리가 들려! 그들이 늘 그랬어!\"\n\n이윽고 그는 그녀에게, 강물 위 배의 흔들림이 자기를 잠재워 쉬게 한다고 말했다. 강둑은 이제 얼마나 푸른지, 그 위에 핀 꽃들은 얼마나 환한지, 갈대는 얼마나 키가 큰지! 이제 배는 바다로 나갔으나, 매끄럽게 미끄러져 나아갔다. 그리고 이제 그의 앞에 바닷가가 펼쳐졌다. 강둑 위에 선 이는 누구일까?—\n\n그는 기도할 때 늘 하던 것처럼 두 손을 모았다. 그는 그렇게 하려고 팔을 떼지 않았다. 그러나 사람들은 그가 그녀의 목 뒤에서 두 손을 그렇게 모으는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"엄마는 너와 닮았어, 플로이. 얼굴을 보니 알겠어! 하지만 사람들한테 전해 줘, 학교 계단에 걸린 그 그림은 충분히 거룩하지 않다고. 머리 둘레의 빛이 내가 가는 길을 비추고 있어!\"\n\n벽 위의 금빛 잔물결이 다시 돌아왔고, 방 안에서는 그 밖에 아무것도 움직이지 않았다. 그 오래되고 오래된 관습이여! 우리가 처음 옷을 입던 때 함께 들어와, 우리 인류가 그 행로를 다하고 넓은 창공이 두루마리처럼 말려 올라갈 때까지 변함없이 이어질 그 관습이여. 그 오래되고 오래된 관습—죽음이여!\n\n오, 그것을 보는 모든 이여, 더욱 오래된 또 하나의 관습, 영생의 관습을 두고 하느님께 감사드릴지어다! 그리고 어린아이들의 천사들이여, 빠른 강물이 우리를 대양으로 실어 갈 때, 아주 멀어지지만은 않은 눈길로 우리를 굽어보소서!\n\n\"어머나 세상에, 어머나 세상에! 생각 좀 해 봐,\" 톡스 양이 그날 밤 마치 가슴이 미어지는 듯 다시 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다, \"돔비와 아들이 결국은 돔비와 딸이었다니 말이야!\"\n\n# 제12장\n\n커틀 선장은 깊고 헤아릴 수 없는 음모를 꾸미는 그 놀라운 재능—투명할 만큼 단순한 사람들에게 흔히 그러하듯, 그도 천성적으로 그 재능을 타고났다고 진심으로 믿고 있던 그 재능—을 발휘하여, 그 운명적인 일요일에 돔비 씨의 집을 찾아갔다. 가는 내내 넘쳐흐르는 슬기로움을 발산하느라 연신 한쪽 눈을 찡긋거리며, 그는 발목까지 오는 반장화의 광채를 온전히 빛내며 타울린슨의 눈앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 그 사람에게서 닥쳐오는 재앙에 관한 소식을 듣고 크게 마음이 쓰인 커틀 선장은, 특유의 섬세함으로 당황한 채 다시 뱃머리를 돌렸다. 그저 자신의 관심의 자그마한 표시로 꽃다발을 건네고, 가족 전체에게 정중한 안부를 남겼는데, 그 안부에는 지금 같은 상황에서 머리를 바람 쪽으로 잘 두기를 바란다는 표현과, 내일 다시 \"올라와 보겠다\"는 다정한 언질이 곁들여졌다.\n\n선장의 안부 인사는 그 뒤로 다시는 누구의 귀에도 들어가지 않았다. 선장의 꽃다발은 밤새 현관에 놓여 있다가 이튿날 아침 쓰레기통으로 쓸려 들어갔다. 그리고 더 큰 희망과 더 드높은 계획과 함께 한 재앙에 휘말린 선장의 은밀한 책략도 산산조각이 났다. 그렇게, 눈사태가 산속의 숲을 덮칠 때 잔가지와 덤불도 큰 나무들과 함께 화를 입고, 모두 함께 스러지는 법이다.\n\n월터가 일요일 저녁에 그 긴 산책과 그 잊지 못할 마무리를 끝내고 집으로 돌아왔을 때, 그는 처음에는 사람들에게 전해야 할 소식과, 자신이 겪고 지나온 그 장면 때문에 가슴속에 자연스레 일어난 감정에 너무 사로잡혀 있어서, 자신의 외삼촌이 선장이 전하기로 한 그 소식을 분명히 모르고 있다는 것도, 또 선장이 갈고리로 신호를 보내며 그 화제를 피하라고 경고하고 있다는 것도 알아차리지 못했다. 하기야 아무리 주의 깊게 살폈더라도 선장의 신호가 그리 알아듣기 쉬웠을 것 같지는 않다. 회담을 할 때 발음이 도무지 불가능한 어떤 학식 있는 글자들을 허공에 쓴다는 그 중국 현인들처럼, 선장은 그의 비법을 미리 알지 못하는 사람이라면 도무지 이해할 법하지 않은 그런 물결무늬와 장식적인 동작들을 만들어 보였던 것이다.\n\n그러나 커틀 선장은 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알아차리게 되자, 월터가 떠나기 전에 돔비 씨와 약간의 편안한 한담을 나눌 수 있을 가능성이 이제 희박하다는 것을 깨닫고 그런 시도들을 그만두었다. 그러나 솔 길스에게 사정을 알려야 하고 월터는 떠나야 한다는 것을—친구의 노련한 솜씨로 미리 사정을 밝히거나 개선해 두지 못한 채, 지금으로서는 닥친 그대로 받아들여야 한다는 것을—실망하고 풀이 죽은 얼굴로 스스로 인정하면서도, 선장은 자기, 네드 커틀이야말로 돔비 씨를 다룰 적임자라는 변함없는 확신을 여전히 품고 있었다. 그리고 월터의 앞날을 완전히 바로잡으려면 오직 그 두 사람이 함께 만나기만 하면 된다고 믿었다. 선장은 자기와 돔비 씨가 브라이턴에서 얼마나 잘 어울렸는지, 필요할 때 둘 다 얼마나 절묘하게 한마디씩 거들었는지, 서로의 됨됨이를 얼마나 정확히 가늠했는지, 그리고 네드 커틀이 절체절명의 순간에 그 해결책을 어떻게 제시하여 그 회담을 바라던 결말로 이끌었는지를 결코 잊을 수 없었던 것이다. 이 모든 근거에서 선장은, 비록 네드 커틀이 사태의 압박에 떠밀려 지금으로서는 거의 쓸모없이 \"대기\"해야 할 처지이긴 하나, 때가 되면 돛을 적셔 단숨에 달려와 모든 것을 휩쓸어 버리리라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로했다.\n\n이 선량한 망상의 영향 아래, 커틀 선장은 월터를 바라보며 그가 들려주는 이야기에 셔츠 깃에 눈물 한 방울을 떨구며 귀를 기울이고 앉아 있는 동안, 마음속으로 이런 궁리까지 했다. 두 사람이 만나게 되면 언제고 돔비 씨에게 자기가 정한 날에 브리그 플레이스로 와서 양고기 한 점을 들고, 다정한 술잔을 앞에 두고 젊은 친구의 앞날 문제를 논의하자고 직접 입으로 청하는 것이, 점잖으면서도 동시에 영리한 일이 아닐까 하는 것이었다. 그러나 맥스팅거 부인의 변덕스러운 성미와, 그런 접대를 하는 동안 그녀가 복도에 진을 치고 앉아 거기서 무언가 칭찬과는 거리가 먼 설교를 늘어놓을 가능성이, 선장의 환대하려는 생각에 제동을 걸어, 그런 생각을 부추기기를 주저하게 만들었다.\n\n월터가 손도 대지 않은 저녁밥을 앞에 두고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 그동안 일어난 모든 일을 곱씹는 동안, 선장에게는 한 가지 사실만은 아주 분명했다. 곧, 월터의 겸손함이 스스로 그것을 깨닫는 데 걸림돌이 될지언정, 그는 말하자면 돔비 씨 가족의 일원이라는 사실이었다. 그가 그토록 애처롭게 묘사한 그 사건에 직접 연루되어 있었고, 그 사건과 긴밀히 연관되어 이름으로 기억되고 칭찬받았으니, 그의 앞날은 고용주의 눈에 특별한 관심거리일 수밖에 없었다. 선장에게 자기 결론에 대해 조금이라도 숨은 의심이 있었다 하더라도, 그 결론이 악기 제조공의 마음의 평안을 위해서는 좋은 결론이라는 데에는 전혀 의심이 없었다. 그래서 그는 이토록 적절한 순간을 틈타 그 서인도 제도 소식을 친구에게 대단한 영전이라도 되는 양 전했다. 자기로서는 (있기만 하다면) 월터의 장기적인 이익을 위해 십만 파운드라도 기꺼이 내놓겠으며, 그런 투자라면 틀림없이 두둑한 이문을 낳으리라고 단언하면서.\n\n솔로몬 길스는 처음에는 그 소식에 망연자실했다. 그 소식은 작은 뒷거실에 벼락처럼 떨어져 난롯가를 사납게 헤집어 놓았던 것이다. 그러나 선장이 그의 흐릿한 눈앞에 그토록 황금빛 전망을 번쩍여 보이고, 휘팅턴 식의 결말을 그토록 신비롭게 암시하고, 월터가 방금 들려준 이야기에 그토록 힘주어 강조하며, 그것을 자기 예언의 입증이자 '어여쁜 페그'라는 낭만적 전설의 실현을 향한 큰 진전이라며 그토록 확신에 차서 끌어다 대니, 노인은 어리둥절해지고 말았다. 월터로 말하자면, 희망과 열정에 가득 찬 척, 곧 다시 집으로 돌아오리라 확신하는 척하며, 머리를 의미심장하게 흔들고 두 손을 비비대며 선장을 거들었으니, 솔로몬은 처음에는 월터를, 다음에는 커틀 선장을 번갈아 바라보며, 자기가 마땅히 기쁨에 겨워해야 하는 게 아닌가 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n\"그렇지만 나는, 알다시피, 시대에 뒤처진 사람이라서,\" 그가 변명하듯 말하며, 외투의 반짝이는 단추들을 한 줄로 죽 따라 초조하게 손을 쓸어내렸다가 다시 쓸어 올렸다. 마치 그것이 묵주 알이고 자기가 그것을 두 번 세고 있기라도 한 것처럼. \"차라리 내 사랑하는 아이를 곁에 두고 싶구나. 구식 생각이겠지, 아마. 저 애는 늘 바다를 좋아했어. 저 애는\"—그러고는 애틋하게 월터를 바라보았다—\"저 애는 가게 되어 기뻐하지.\"\n\n\"솔 삼촌!\" 월터가 다급히 외쳤다. \"그렇게 말씀하시면, 저 안 갈래요. 안 가요, 커틀 선장님, 안 갑니다. 제가 서인도 제도 섬들 전부의 총독이 된다 해도, 삼촌이 제가 삼촌을 두고 떠나는 걸 기뻐할 수 있다고 생각하신다면, 그걸로 충분합니다. 저는 여기 못 박힌 듯 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"월터, 얘야,\" 선장이 말했다. \"침착하게! 솔 길스, 자네 조카를 한번 관찰해 보게.\"\n\n선장의 갈고리가 그리는 위풍당당한 동작을 눈으로 좇으며, 노인은 월터를 바라보았다.\n\n\"여기 어떤 배 한 척이,\" 선장이 자기가 솟아오르고 있는 그 비유에 대한 웅대한 자부심을 품고 말했다, \"어떤 항해를 떠나려 하고 있네. 그 배에 지워지지 않게 쓰인 이름이 무엇인가? '게이' 호인가? 아니면,\" 선장이 이 대목에 주목하라는 듯 목소리를 높여 말했다, \"'길스' 호인가?\"\n\n\"네드,\" 노인이 월터를 자기 곁으로 끌어당기며, 그의 팔을 다정하게 자기 팔에 끼고 말했다, \"나도 알아. 알고말고. 물론 월리가 늘 자기보다 나를 더 위한다는 거 알지. 그게 내 마음에 걸리는 거야. 저 애가 가게 되어 기뻐한다고 내가 말할 때, 그건 그러기를 바란다는 뜻이지. 응? 자, 보게, 네드, 그리고 너도, 월리, 얘야, 이건 나한테 새롭고 뜻밖의 일이야. 그리고 내가 시대에 뒤처지고 가난한 게 이 일의 밑바탕에 있는 게 아닐까 두렵구나. 정말로 저 애한테 좋은 일인가, 자네들 말해 주게, 응?\" 노인이 이쪽저쪽을 근심스레 바라보며 말했다. \"정말로, 진실로? 그런가? 월리한테 도움이 되는 일이라면 나는 거의 무엇에든 마음을 맞출 수 있지만, 월리가 나를 위해 자기를 불리하게 만들거나, 나한테 무얼 숨기는 건 용납 못 해. 자네, 네드 커틀!\" 노인이 선장에게 다그쳐 들자, 그 외교가는 보란 듯이 당황했다. \"오랜 친구를 정직하게 대하고 있는 건가? 솔직히 말해 보게, 네드 커틀. 뒤에 무슨 사정이 있나? 저 애가 마땅히 가야 하나? 자네는 그걸 어떻게 먼저 알았으며, 또 왜인가?\"\n\n그것이 애정과 자기희생의 겨룸이었던 만큼, 월터가 무한한 효과를 발휘하며 끼어들어 선장의 짐을 덜어 주었다. 그리고 둘이서 계속 이야기를 늘어놓아 늙은 솔 길스를 그 계획에 그런대로 수긍시켰다. 아니, 차라리 그를 어찌나 혼란스럽게 만들었던지, 그 무엇도, 심지어 이별의 아픔조차도 그의 마음에 또렷이 잡히지 않게 되었다.\n\n그에게는 그 문제를 저울질할 시간이 그리 많지 않았다. 바로 이튿날 월터가 지배인 카커 씨로부터 항해와 채비에 필요한 증서들과 함께, '아들과 상속자' 호가 보름 후에, 늦어도 그로부터 하루 이틀 안에 출항하리라는 통지를 받았기 때문이다. 월터가 일부러 한껏 부풀린 준비의 분주함 속에서, 노인은 그나마 지니고 있던 얼마 안 되는 침착함마저 잃어버렸다. 그렇게 출항의 때가 빠르게 다가왔다.\n\n날마다 월터에게 캐물어 일어나는 모든 일을 빠짐없이 알아내던 선장은, 자기 처지를 더 잘 이해할 기회가 나타나거나 나타날 듯한 기미도 없이 시간이 자꾸만 그가 떠나는 쪽으로 흘러가는 것을 보았다. 이 사실을 한참 고민하고, 이렇게 불운하게 얽힌 정황을 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 선장에게 한 가지 묘안이 떠올랐다. 카커 씨를 방문해서, 그에게서 사정이 실제로 어떻게 돌아가는지를 알아내 보면 어떨까!\n\n커틀 선장은 이 생각이 무척 마음에 들었다. 그것은 그가 아침 식사 후 브리그 플레이스에서 이른 담배를 피우고 있을 때 영감의 순간에 찾아왔다. 그것은 그 담배에 값할 만한 생각이었다. 그것은 월터가 그에게 털어놓은 것과 솔 길스가 한 말 때문에 다소 불편해진, 그래도 정직한 그의 양심을 가라앉혀 줄 터였다. 그리고 그것은 깊고 빈틈없는 우정의 행위가 될 터였다. 그는 카커 씨를 조심스레 떠보아, 그 신사의 성품을 읽고 둘이 서로 잘 통하는지 아니면 그 반대인지를 알아낸 뒤에, 그에 맞추어 많이 말하든 적게 말하든 할 작정이었다.\n\n그리하여 (집에서 짐을 꾸리고 있는 줄 아는) 월터를 눈앞에 두려워할 일 없이, 커틀 선장은 다시 발목까지 오는 반장화와 상장(喪章) 브로치를 갖추고, 이 두 번째 원정에 나섰다. 이번에는 업무를 보는 곳으로 가는 길이라 비위를 맞추는 꽃다발은 사지 않았다. 다만 시골의 정취를 기분 좋게 곁들이려고 단춧구멍에 작은 해바라기 한 송이를 꽂았다. 그리고 그것과 옹이진 지팡이와 번들거리는 모자를 갖추고서 돔비와 아들 회사의 사무소로 들이닥쳤다.\n\n가까운 선술집에서 따뜻한 럼주와 물을 한 잔 들이켜 생각을 가다듬은 뒤, 선장은 그 좋은 기운이 날아가 버릴까 봐 골목을 한달음에 달려 내려가, 퍼치 씨 앞에 불쑥 나타났다.\n\n\"여보게,\" 선장이 설득하는 어조로 말했다. \"자네 사장님들 중에 카커라는 분이 계시지.\"\n\n퍼치 씨는 그것을 인정했다. 그러나 직무상 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로, 자기 사장님들은 모두 일에 매여 계시고 앞으로도 결코 손이 빌 것 같지 않다는 뜻을 전했다.\n\n\"이보게, 여보게,\" 선장이 그의 귀에 대고 말했다. \"내 이름은 커틀 선장일세.\"\n\n선장이 퍼치를 갈고리로 살며시 끌어당기려 하자, 퍼치 씨는 그 시도를 피했다. 일부러라기보다는, 그런 무기가 퍼치 부인에게 느닷없이 보였다가는 지금 그녀의 처지로는 그녀의 희망을 결딴낼 수도 있겠다는 갑작스러운 생각에 흠칫 놀라서였다.\n\n\"기회가 되거든 여기 커틀 선장이 왔다고 좀 전해 주는 친절을 베풀어 주면,\" 선장이 말했다, \"내 기다리겠네.\"\n\n그러고는 선장은 퍼치 씨의 작은 선반 자리에 앉아, 두 무릎 사이에 끼워 둔 번들거리는 모자의 꼭대기에서 손수건을 끄집어내어(그 모양이 망가질 염려는 없었으니, 인간의 어떤 힘으로도 그것을 구부릴 수 없었던 까닭이다) 머리를 온통 박박 문지르고는, 한결 개운해진 듯했다. 그런 다음 갈고리로 머리를 매만지고 앉아서, 사무실을 둘러보며 사무원들을 평온한 존경의 눈길로 바라보았다.\n\n선장의 평정심은 어찌나 파고들 틈이 없던지, 또 그는 도무지 신비롭기 그지없는 존재였던지라, 사환 퍼치는 기가 질렸다.\n\n\"이름이 뭐라고 하셨지요?\" 퍼치 씨가, 선반에 앉은 선장 위로 몸을 굽히며 물었다.\n\n\"선장일세,\" 깊고 쉰 목소리로 속삭였다.\n\n\"네,\" 퍼치 씨가 고개로 박자를 맞추며 말했다.\n\n\"커틀.\"\n\n\"오!\" 퍼치 씨가 같은 어조로 말했으니, 그가 그것을 옮아 따라 했고 또 어쩔 수가 없었던 것이다. 선장은 그 외교술에서 그토록 위압적이었다. \"지금 손이 비셨는지 알아보겠습니다. 잘 모르겠네요. 잠깐 정도는 되실지도 모르죠.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 여보게, 일 분 이상은 붙들지 않겠네,\" 선장이 자기 속에 품은 묵직한 중대함을 한껏 담아 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. 퍼치가 곧 돌아와서 말했다. \"커틀 선장님, 이쪽으로 오시겠습니까?\"\n\n지배인 카커 씨는 갈색 종이를 성벽 모양으로 오려 장식한 빈 벽난로 앞 난로 깔개 위에 서서, 들어오는 선장을 별로 특별할 것 없는 격려의 표정으로 바라보았다.\n\n\"카커 씨이신가요?\" 커틀 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다만,\" 카커 씨가 이를 모조리 드러내 보이며 말했다.\n\n선장은 그가 미소로 대답하는 것이 마음에 들었다. 그것은 유쾌해 보였다. \"그러니까 말입니다,\" 선장이 작은 방 안을 천천히 눈으로 한 바퀴 굴리며, 셔츠 깃이 허락하는 만큼 방을 둘러보면서 입을 열었다. \"저 자신이 뱃사람이올시다, 카커 씨. 그리고 여기 댁의 장부에 올라 있는 월터로 말하자면, 거의 제 아들이나 다름없지요.\"\n\n\"월터 게이 말입니까?\" 카커 씨가 다시 이를 모조리 드러내 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"월터 게이가 맞습니다,\" 선장이 대답했다, \"옳습니다!\" 선장의 태도는 카커 씨의 눈치 빠름에 대한 따뜻한 찬사를 드러냈다. \"저는 그 애와 그 애 삼촌의 막역한 친구올시다. 어쩌면,\" 선장이 말했다, \"댁의 최고 사장님께서 제 이름을 언급하시는 걸 들으셨을지도 모르겠습니다만—커틀 선장 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아니요!\" 카커 씨가 앞서보다 한층 더 활짝 드러내 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇군요,\" 선장이 다시 말을 이었다, \"저는 그분과 알고 지내는 영광을 누리고 있지요. 제가 젊은 친구 월터와 함께 저 서식스 해안으로 그분을 찾아뵈었습니다, 그때—한마디로, 약간의 편의가 필요했을 때 말입니다.\" 선장은 편안하면서도 느긋하고 또한 의미심장한 태도로 고개를 끄덕였다. \"기억하시겠지요, 아마?\"\n\n\"제 생각에는,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"제가 그 거래를 처리하는 영광을 누렸던 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다!\" 선장이 응수했다. \"또 옳으십니다! 댁이 하셨지요. 자, 제가 외람되게도 여기 찾아온 것은—\"\n\n\"앉으시지요?\" 카커 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다,\" 선장이 그 권유를 받아들이며 응했다. \"사람이 앉아 있으면 이야기에 좀 더 탄력이 붙는 법이지요, 아마. 댁도 의자에 앉지 그러십니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 괜찮습니다,\" 지배인이 말했다. 어쩌면 겨울철 습관의 힘 때문인지, 굴뚝 장식 선반에 등을 기대고 서서, 이와 잇몸 하나하나에 눈이 박힌 듯한 눈길로 선장을 내려다보면서. \"외람되게도, 라고 말씀하시려던 참이군요—하기야 외람될 것 없습니다만—\"\n\n\"친절히 고맙습니다, 여보게,\" 선장이 응수했다. \"제 친구 월터의 일로 여기 찾아왔다는 것이지요. 그 애 삼촌 솔 길스는 학식 있는 사람이고, 학문으로 치자면 쾌속선이라 할 만하지요. 그렇지만 제가 통틀어 유능한 뱃사람이라 부를 만한 사람은 아닙니다—실무에 밝은 사람은 아니지요. 월터는 여태껏 갑판을 밟아 본 누구 못지않게 반듯한 녀석입니다만, 한 가지 점에서 뱃머리가 좀 처져 있는데, 그게 바로 겸손함이지요. 그래서 제가 댁께 여쭙고 싶은 것은,\" 선장이 목소리를 낮추어, 일종의 은밀한 으르렁거림으로 말했다, \"다정한 식으로, 전적으로 댁과 저 둘 사이에서, 그리고 제 개인적인 셈속으로, 댁의 최고 사장님께서 좀 방향을 바꾸셔서 제가 그분 곁에 다가갈 수 있을 때까지는 이렇습니다.—여기 모든 게 옳고 무탈한지요, 그리고 월터는 꽤 순조로운 바람을 받으며 바깥 항로로 떠나는 건지요?\"\n\n\"자, 어떻게 생각하십니까, 커틀 선장님?\" 카커가 옷자락을 추스르고 자세를 고쳐 잡으며 응수했다. \"선장님은 실무에 밝은 분이시니, 어떻게 생각하십니까?\"\n\n선장이 그 대답으로 한쪽 눈을 치켜뜨며 보낸 눈빛의 예리함과 의미심장함은, 앞서 언급한 그 발음 불가능한 중국 글자들만큼 짧고 표현하기 어려운 말이 아니고서는 도무지 묘사할 수 없을 것이었다.\n\n\"자!\" 선장이 이루 말할 수 없이 기운을 얻어 말했다, \"어떻게 보십니까? 제가 옳습니까, 그릅니까?\"\n\n선장은 카커 씨의 미소 띤 정중함에 용기와 자극을 받아 눈빛으로 어찌나 많은 것을 표현했던지, 자기 생각을 더없이 정성껏 말로 풀어놓았을 때나 다름없이 그 질문을 던지기에 충분한 상태라고 느꼈다.\n\n\"옳습니다,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"의심할 바 없지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 좋은 날씨에 바깥 항로로 떠나는 거로군요,\" 커틀 선장이 외쳤다.\n\n카커 씨가 미소로 동의했다.\n\n\"바람은 정통으로 고물 쪽에서, 그것도 넉넉히 불어 주고요,\" 선장이 이어 말했다.\n\n카커 씨가 다시 미소로 동의했다.\n\n\"옳거니, 옳거니!\" 커틀 선장이 크게 안도하고 흐뭇해하며 말했다. \"그 배가 어느 쪽으로 뱃머리를 두는지 진작 잘 알고 있었지요. 월터에게도 그렇게 말했습니다. 고맙습니다, 고맙습니다.\"\n\n\"게이는 빛나는 앞날을 앞두고 있지요,\" 카커 씨가 입을 한층 더 크게 벌리며 말했다, \"온 세상이 그의 것이지요.\"\n\n\"속담대로, 온 세상에 그의 아내까지지요,\" 기뻐하는 선장이 응수했다.\n\n\"아내\"라는 말에(무심코 내뱉은 말이었다) 선장은 멈칫하더니, 다시 한쪽 눈을 치켜뜨고, 번들거리는 모자를 옹이진 지팡이 꼭대기에 얹어 빙그르르 돌리면서, 늘 미소 짓고 있는 친구를 곁눈질로 바라보았다.\n\n\"오래 묵은 자메이카 술 한 잔을 걸지요,\" 선장이 그를 유심히 바라보며 말했다, \"댁이 무얼 보고 미소 짓는지 제가 안다는 데 말입니다.\"\n\n카커 씨는 그 신호를 받아, 더욱 미소 지었다.\n\n\"여기서 더 새 나가지 않겠지요?\" 선장이 옹이진 지팡이로 문을 쿡 찔러 문이 닫혀 있는지 확인하며 말했다.\n\n\"한 치도 안 나갑니다,\" 카커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"혹시 대문자 F를 떠올리고 계신 건 아닙니까?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n카커 씨는 그것을 부인하지 않았다.\n\n\"L에 관한 무언가는요,\" 선장이 말했다, \"아니면 O 같은 것은?\"\n\n카커 씨는 여전히 미소 지었다.\n\n\"제가 또 옳습니까?\" 선장이 속삭이며 물었다. 이마의 진홍빛 둥근 자국이 의기양양한 기쁨에 부풀어 오른 채로.\n\n카커 씨가 그에 답하여 여전히 미소 지으며 이제 고개를 끄덕여 동의하자, 커틀 선장은 자리에서 일어나 그의 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 둘이 같은 항로를 가고 있으며, 자기(커틀)로 말하자면 처음부터 줄곧 그쪽으로 침로를 잡아 왔노라고 따뜻하게 단언했다. \"그 애가 그 아가씨를 처음 알게 된 건,\" 선장이 그 화제가 요구하는 온갖 은밀함과 진중함을 담아 말했다, \"예사롭지 않은 식이었지요—그 애가 아가씨를 거의 갓난아이였을 때 길거리에서 발견한 걸 기억하시지요—그 뒤로 줄곧 그 애는 그 아가씨를 좋아했고, 그 아가씨도 그 애를 좋아했지요, 젊은이 둘이서 그럴 수 있는 만큼 말입니다. 솔 길스와 저는 늘 말해 왔습니다, 그 둘은 서로를 위해 점지된 사이라고요.\"\n\n고양이든 원숭이든 하이에나든 해골이든, 카커 씨가 이 회견의 이 대목에서 선장에게 한꺼번에 드러내 보인 만큼 많은 이를 그에게 드러내 보일 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"전반적으로 물길이 그쪽으로 빨려 들고 있지요,\" 행복한 선장이 말했다. \"바람도 물도 다 그쪽으로 들이친단 말입니다, 보세요. 그 애가 며칠 전 그 자리에 있었던 것만 봐도 그렇지요!\"\n\n\"그의 희망에 더없이 유리하지요,\" 카커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그날의 항적에 그 애가 끌려간 것만 봐도 그래요!\" 선장이 이어 말했다. \"아, 이제 무엇이 그 애를 떨어져 나가게 할 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아무것도 없지요,\" 카커 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"또 옳으십니다,\" 선장이 그의 손을 다시 한번 꼭 쥐며 응수했다. \"아무것도 없지요. 그렇고말고요! 침착하게! 아들이 하나 떠났지요. 예쁘장한 꼬마였는데. 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"네, 아들이 하나 떠났지요,\" 동의하는 카커가 말했다.\n\n\"한마디만 하면, 댁을 위해 또 하나 준비되어 있단 말입니다,\" 선장이 말했다. \"학식 있는 삼촌의 조카! 솔 길스의 조카! 월터! 이미 댁의 사업에 들어와 있는 월터! 그리고\"—선장이 마지막 한 방을 위해 준비해 둔 인용구로 점차 고조되며 말했다, \"솔 길스의 집에서 날마다, 댁의 사업으로, 댁의 품으로—오는 그 애 말이지요.\"\n\n앞의 짧은 문장들을 하나씩 마칠 때마다 팔꿈치로 카커 씨를 슬며시 쿡쿡 찌르던 선장의 흡족함은, 이 빛나는 웅변과 슬기로움의 과시를 끝냈을 때 몸을 뒤로 젖히며 그를 바라보던 그 의기양양함 말고는 무엇으로도 능가할 수 없는 것이었다. 그의 커다란 푸른 조끼는 그런 걸작을 낳는 진통에 들썩였고, 그의 코는 같은 이유로 격렬하게 벌게진 상태였다.\n\n\"제가 옳습니까?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"커틀 선장님,\" 카커 씨가 잠시 묘한 태도로 무릎을 굽히며 말했다. 마치 자기 자신을 한꺼번에 와락 끌어안으려고 몸을 한데 오그리는 듯이. \"월터 게이에 관한 선장님의 견해는 철두철미 정확하게 옳습니다. 우리가 비밀리에 이야기를 나누고 있다는 것으로 알고 있습니다만.\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고요!\" 선장이 끼어들었다. \"한마디도 안 합니다.\"\n\n\"그에게도, 누구에게도요?\" 지배인이 이어 물었다.\n\n커틀 선장은 미간을 찌푸리며 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"다만 오로지 선장님 자신의 만족과 지침을 위해서일 뿐이지요—물론 지침이라 함은,\" 카커 씨가 되풀이했다, \"앞으로의 선장님 행보를 염두에 둔 것이고요.\"\n\n\"친절하시군요, 정말 고맙습니다,\" 선장이 무척 주의 깊게 귀를 기울이며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 사실이라고 말씀드리는 데 주저하지 않겠습니다. 선장님은 가능성을 정확히 짚어 내셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 댁의 최고 사장님에 관해서라면,\" 선장이 말했다, \"그야 우리 사이에 자연스레 만남이 이루어지는 게 낫겠지요. 시간은 넉넉하니까요.\"\n\n카커 씨는 입을 양쪽 귀까지 늘어뜨린 채, \"시간은 넉넉하지요\"라고 되풀이했다. 다만 그 말을 소리 내어 또렷이 하지는 않고, 상냥하게 고개를 숙이며 혀와 입술로 그 말을 빚어 보일 뿐이었다.\n\n\"그리고 제가 알기로는—제가 늘 말해 온 거지만—월터는 한밑천 잡을 길에 들어서 있으니까요,\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"한밑천 잡을 길에 들어서 있지요,\" 카커 씨가 똑같이 소리 없는 식으로 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그리고 월터가 이 작은 항해를 떠나는 것도, 말하자면, 그 애의 일과 가운데 하나이고, 여기서 그 애가 품은 전반적인 기대의 한 부분이니까요,\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"여기서 그가 품은 전반적인 기대의 한 부분이지요,\" 카커 씨가 앞서처럼 소리 없이 수긍했다.\n\n\"그러니, 그것만 알면,\" 선장이 이어 말했다, \"서두를 것 없고, 제 마음도 편안합니다.\"\n\n카커 씨가 여전히 같은 무언의 방식으로 부드럽게 수긍하자, 커틀 선장은 그가 자기가 여태껏 만나 본 사람들 가운데 가장 유쾌한 사람 축에 든다는, 그리고 돔비 씨조차도 그런 본보기를 따라 스스로를 갈고닦으면 좋겠다는 생각을 한층 굳혔다. 그리하여 선장은 매우 진심 어린 마음으로 다시 한번 자신의 거대한 손을(빛깔로 보아 낡은 도르래 토막과 별로 다르지 않은 손이었다) 내밀어, 그를 한 번 꽉 움켜쥐었다. 그 바람에 카커 씨의 매끈한 살갗에는 선장의 손바닥에 넉넉히 새겨진 틈과 갈라진 자국들이 또렷한 자국으로 찍혔다.\n\n\"그럼 안녕히!\" 선장이 말했다. \"저는 말수가 많은 사람은 아닙니다만, 이렇게 다정하고 솔직하게 대해 주시니 무척 고맙게 여깁니다. 제가 조금이라도 폐를 끼쳤다면 용서해 주시겠지요?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"천만에요,\" 상대가 응수했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다. 제 거처는 그리 넓지는 않습니다만,\" 선장이 다시 몸을 돌리며 말했다, \"그런대로 아늑하지요. 그리고 혹시 언제고 브리그 플레이스 9번지 근처에 오시게 되면—적어 두시겠습니까?—문간에 있는 사람이 무슨 말을 하든 개의치 마시고 위층으로 올라와 주신다면, 뵙는 것이 자랑스럽겠습니다.\"\n\n그렇게 환대의 초대를 건네고, 선장은 \"안녕히 계십시오!\"라고 말하고는 밖으로 걸어 나가 문을 닫았다. 카커 씨는 여전히 굴뚝 장식 선반에 기댄 채 남겨 두고서. 그 교활한 눈빛과 경계하는 태도에는, 길게 늘어졌으되 웃지는 않는 그 거짓된 입에는, 흠 하나 없는 크라바트와 다름 아닌 그 구레나룻에는, 심지어 부드러운 손을 자신의 흰 아마포 옷과 매끄러운 얼굴 위로 말없이 쓸어내리는 그 동작에마저, 무언가 지독하게 고양이를 닮은 데가 있었다.\n\n아무것도 모르는 선장은 자기 자랑에 도취된 채로 걸어 나왔는데, 그 도취가 그 폭이 넓은 푸른 양복에 아주 새로운 맵시를 더해 주었다. \"대기, 네드!\" 선장이 혼잣말을 했다. \"오늘 자네 젊은이들을 위해 작은 일 하나 해냈군, 이 친구야!\"\n\n그 의기양양함 속에서, 또 회사와 현재 및 앞으로 가까워질 친분을 생각하니, 선장은 바깥 사무실에 이르렀을 때 퍼치 씨를 조금 놀리지 않을 수 없어서, 아직도 다들 일에 매여 있다고 보느냐고 물었다. 그러나 직분을 다한 사람에게 매정하게 굴고 싶지는 않아, 선장은 그의 귀에 대고, 만일 럼주와 물 한 잔이 당기거든 따라오라고, 기꺼이 한 잔 대접하겠노라고 속삭였다.\n\n그곳을 떠나기 전에 선장은, 사무원들을 다소 놀라게 하면서, 한가운데 자리에서 둘러보며, 자기 젊은 친구가 깊이 관련된 그 계획의 한 부분이자 한 요소인 사무소를 전반적으로 한 차례 둘러보았다. 금고실은 그의 각별한 감탄을 자아냈다. 그러나 너무 유난스러워 보이지 않으려고, 그는 흡족한 눈길 한 번으로 그치고는, 정중함과 너그러움이 가득한 태도로 사무원들 전체에게 우아하게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 골목으로 걸어 나갔다. 곧 퍼치 씨가 합류하자, 그는 그 신사를 선술집으로 데려가 약속을 지켰다—퍼치의 시간이 귀했으므로 서둘러서.\n\n\"건배 구호로,\" 선장이 말했다, \"월터를 들겠네!\"\n\n\"누구요?\" 퍼치 씨가 조심스레 여쭈었다.\n\n\"월터!\" 선장이 우레 같은 목소리로 되풀이했다.\n\n퍼치 씨는 어린 시절에 옛날에 그런 이름의 시인이 한 사람 있었다는 이야기를 들은 적이 있는 것 같아, 이의를 달지 않았다. 그러나 선장이 시인을 위해 건배하자고 시내까지 온 것에는 무척 놀랐다. 실로, 선장이 시인의 동상을—이를테면 셰익스피어의 동상을—시내 큰길에 세우자고 했더라도, 퍼치 씨의 경험에 이보다 더 큰 충격을 주기는 어려웠을 것이다. 통틀어, 선장은 어찌나 신비롭고 종잡을 수 없는 인물이었던지, 퍼치 씨는 행여 무슨 거북한 결과를 빚을세라, 그를 퍼치 부인에게는 아예 언급하지 않기로 마음먹었다.\n\n신비롭고 종잡을 수 없는 선장은, 젊은이들을 위해 작은 일 하나 해냈다는 생생한 자각을 품은 채, 가장 막역한 친구들에게조차 종일 그러한 모습으로 남아 있었다. 그리고 월터가 그의 윙크와 싱글거림과 그 밖의 그런 무언극 같은 기분 발산을, 늙은 솔 길스에게 친 순진한 속임수가 성공한 데 대한 만족 때문이라고 여기지 않았더라면, 그는 분명 밤이 되기 전에 제 속내를 드러내고 말았을 것이다. 그러나 그렇게 여긴 덕분에, 그는 자기 비밀을 지킬 수 있었다. 그리고 악기 제조공의 집에서 밤늦게 집으로 돌아왔는데, 번들거리는 모자를 한쪽으로 어찌나 비뚜름하게 쓰고, 눈에 그토록 환한 표정을 담고 있었던지, 맥스팅거 부인은(그녀가 어찌나 로마의 귀부인 같은 사람이었던지, 블림버 박사 밑에서 자랐다 해도 믿을 정도였다) 그를 처음 흘끗 보자마자 열린 대문 뒤에 진을 치고, 그가 자기 방에 무사히 들어앉을 때까지는 자신의 소중한 아기들을 살피러 나오기를 거부했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII. Father and Daughter\n\n돔비 씨의 집안에는 정적이 감돈다. 하인들이 계단을 오르내리며 옷자락을 스치지만 발소리는 내지 않는다. 그들은 끊임없이 서로 이야기를 나누고, 식사 자리에 오래 앉아, 음식과 음료를 한껏 즐기며, 음울하고 불경한 방식으로 흥겨워한다. 위컴 부인은 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 우울한 일화들을 늘어놓는다. 자기는 핍친 부인 댁에 있을 때부터 늘 이리될 줄 알았다고 말하고, 평소보다 식탁 맥주를 더 마시며, 무척 슬퍼하면서도 어울리기를 즐긴다. 요리사의 심정도 비슷하다. 그녀는 저녁으로 작은 튀김 요리를 약속하고, 자기 감정과 양파에 똑같이 맞서 싸운다. 타울린슨은 여기에 어떤 운명이 깃든 게 아닌가 생각하기 시작하고, 모퉁이 집에 사는 게 누구에게라도 좋은 일이 된 적이 있는지 누가 좀 말해 줄 수 없겠느냐고 한다. 그들 모두에게 그것은 마치 오래전에 일어난 일처럼 느껴진다. 그렇지만 아이는 여전히, 평온하고 아름답게, 제 작은 침대에 누워 있다.\n\n날이 어두워진 뒤, 몇몇 방문객이 온다—발소리 없는 방문객들, 펠트 신을 신은 이들로, 전에도 와 본 적이 있다. 그리고 그들과 함께, 어린 잠든 이들에게는 참으로 낯선 그 안식의 침대가 들어온다. 그 내내, 상심한 아버지의 모습은 시중드는 이에게조차 보이지 않았다. 누군가 방에 있을 때면 그는 어두운 제 방의 안쪽 구석에 앉아 있었고, 다른 때에는 방 안을 이리저리 거니는 것 말고는 결코 움직이지 않는 듯했다. 그러나 아침이 되자, 그가 깊은 밤중에 위층으로 올라가는 소리가 들렸으며, 거기—그 방에—해가 비칠 때까지 머물러 있었다는 말이 집안에 속삭이듯 돈다.\n\n시내의 사무실에서는 간유리 창문이 덧문 때문에 한층 더 흐릿해져 있다. 책상 위에 켜 둔 등불은 안으로 흘러드는 햇빛에 반쯤 꺼져 보이고, 햇빛은 또 그 등불 때문에 반쯤 꺼져 보이며, 평소와 다른 음울함이 감돈다. 업무는 별로 이루어지지 않는다. 서기들은 일할 마음이 내키지 않아, 오후에 갈비 요리를 먹고 강 상류로 놀러 갈 약속을 잡는다. 심부름꾼 퍼치는 심부름을 나가서 오래 머문다. 친구들의 초대를 받아 술집 바에 들어앉아, 인간사의 덧없음에 관해 일장 연설을 늘어놓는 자신을 발견한다. 그는 저녁에 평소보다 일찍 볼스 폰드의 집으로 돌아가, 퍼치 부인에게 송아지 커틀릿과 스카치 에일을 대접한다. 지배인 카커 씨는 누구에게도 한턱내지 않고, 누구에게도 대접받지 않는다. 다만 제 방에 홀로 앉아 온종일 이를 드러내 보일 뿐이다. 그리고 카커 씨의 앞길에서 무언가가—어떤 장애물이 치워져—그의 앞이 훤히 트인 듯하다.\n\n지금 돔비 씨의 집 맞은편에 사는 발그레한 아이들이 육아실 창문에서 거리를 내려다보며 엿본다. 그의 문 앞에 검은 말 네 마리가 머리에 깃털을 단 채 서 있고, 그것들이 끄는 마차 위에서도 깃털이 떨리고 있기 때문이다. 그리고 이것들이, 또 띠와 지팡이를 든 사내들의 행렬이 구경꾼들을 끌어모은다. 대야를 빙빙 돌리려던 곡예사는 고운 의상 위에 헐렁한 외투를 다시 걸친다. 그의 무거운 아기를 품에 안아 한쪽으로 기우뚱한 채 터벅터벅 걷던 아내는, 일행이 나오는 것을 보려고 발길을 멈춘다. 그러나 그토록 쉽게 옮겨지는 그 짐이 밖으로 운반되어 나올 때, 그녀는 칙칙한 가슴팍에 제 아기를 더욱 바싹 끌어안는다. 그리고 맞은편 높은 창가에 있는 발그레한 아이들 중 가장 어린 아이는, 보조개 진 손가락으로 가리키며 유모의 얼굴을 들여다보고 \"저게 뭐예요?\" 하고 물을 때, 그 즐거움을 제지할 어떤 손길도 필요로 하지 않는다.\n\n이제, 상복을 입은 하인 무리와 우는 여인들 사이로, 돔비 씨가 자기를 태우려고 기다리는 다른 마차를 향해 현관을 가로질러 간다. 이 지켜보는 이들이 보기에, 그는 슬픔과 마음의 고통에 \"무너지지\" 않았다. 그의 걸음걸이는 여느 때처럼 꼿꼿하고, 그의 태도는 여느 때처럼 뻣뻣하다. 그는 손수건 뒤로 얼굴을 가리지 않고, 앞을 똑바로 바라본다. 다만 그의 얼굴이 다소 꺼지고 굳었으며 창백하다는 점만 빼면, 예전과 똑같은 표정을 띠고 있다. 그는 마차 안의 자리에 앉고, 다른 신사 세 사람이 뒤따른다. 그러고 나서 성대한 장례 행렬이 거리를 따라 천천히 나아간다. 깃털이 아직 멀리서 끄덕이고 있을 때, 곡예사는 이미 대야를 막대 위에서 돌리고 있고, 그것을 감탄하며 보는 똑같은 구경꾼들을 거느리고 있다. 그러나 곡예사의 아내는 평소만큼 돈통을 들고 민첩하게 굴지 못하는데, 한 아이의 매장이, 어쩌면 자기의 초라한 숄 밑에 든 아기가 어른이 되어, 머리에 하늘색 띠를 두르고, 연어색 털실 바지를 입고, 진창에 뒹굴게 될 만큼 자라지 못할지도 모른다는 생각을 그녀의 마음속에 불러일으켰기 때문이다.\n\n깃털들이 음울한 길을 따라 거리를 누비다가, 교회 종소리가 들리는 곳에 이른다. 바로 이 교회에서, 그 예쁜 사내아이는 머지않아 이 땅에 남게 될 자기의 전부—이름—를 받았다. 죽은 그의 모든 것을, 그들은 그곳, 썩어 없어질 그 어미의 유해 가까이 눕힌다. 잘된 일이다. 그들의 재는, 플로렌스가 산책할 때—오, 외롭고도 외로운 산책!—언제든 그 곁을 지날 수 있는 곳에 놓여 있다.\n\n예배가 끝나고 목사가 물러가자, 돔비 씨는 주위를 둘러보며, 명판에 새길 지시를 받으러 와 달라는 요청을 받은 사람이 와 있는지 낮은 목소리로 묻는다.\n\n누군가 앞으로 나서며 \"네\" 하고 말한다.\n\n돔비 씨는 그것을 어디에 놓고 싶은지 알려 주고, 손을 벽에 댄 채 그 모양과 크기를, 그리고 그것이 어미의 추모비를 어떻게 뒤따르도록 할지를 일러 준다. 그러고 나서 연필로 비문을 적어 그에게 건네며 덧붙인다. \"이걸 곧바로 해 주었으면 하오.\n\n\"즉시 처리하겠습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"보다시피, 새길 것이라곤 이름과 나이밖에 정말 없소.\"\n\n그 사내는 종이를 흘끗 보며 고개를 숙이지만, 망설이는 듯하다. 돔비 씨는 그의 망설임을 알아채지 못한 채 돌아서서 현관 쪽으로 걸어간다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 나리.\" 그의 상복 망토에 가만히 손길이 닿는다. \"곧바로 해 주기를 바라시고, 또 제가 돌아가는 대로 작업에 들어갈 수 있으니—\"\n\"무슨 일이오?\"\n\n\"이걸 한 번 더 읽어 봐 주시겠습니까? 잘못된 데가 있는 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"어디가?\"\n\n석공은 그에게 종이를 돌려주며, 자기 주머니 자로 \"사랑하는 외동딸\"이라는 글자를 가리킨다.\n\n\"이건 '아들'이어야 할 것 같습니다만, 나리?\"\n\n\"자네 말이 맞네. 물론이지. 고치게.\"\n\n아버지는 더 빠른 걸음으로 마차를 향해 길을 재촉한다. 바싹 뒤따르던 다른 세 사람이 자리에 앉을 때, 그의 얼굴은 처음으로 가려진다—망토에 그늘져서. 그리고 그날 그들은 그 얼굴을 더는 보지 못한다. 그는 먼저 마차에서 내려, 곧장 자기 방으로 들어간다. 다른 조문객들(칙 씨와 의사 시중꾼 두 사람뿐이다)은 응접실로 위층에 올라가, 칙 부인과 톡스 양의 맞이를 받는다. 그리고 그 아래 닫힌 방 안에서 그 얼굴이 어떤지, 혹은 어떤 생각들이 오가는지, 그 마음이 어떤지, 그 갈등이나 고통이 어떠한지는 아무도 모른다.\n\n아래층 부엌에서 그들이 무엇보다 잘 아는 것은 \"꼭 일요일 같다\"는 것이다. 그들은 평소의 일과를 이어 가고 일상의 옷을 입은 바깥 사람들의 행동이, 사악하지는 않더라도 어딘가 온당치 못한 데가 있다고 거의 믿어 의심치 않는다. 차양을 걷어 올리고 덧문을 열어 두는 것이 자못 신기한 일이다. 그리고 그들은 마치 축제 때처럼 거리낌 없이 따 마시는 포도주 병을 앞에 두고, 음울하게나마 안락함을 누린다. 그들은 자못 도덕적인 사색에 빠지고 싶어 한다. 타울린슨 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 \"우리 모두의 개과천선을 위하여!\"라고 제안하자, 요리사가 또 한숨을 쉬며 \"하느님도 아시다시피 그럴 여지야 충분하죠\"라고 말한다. 저녁에 칙 부인과 톡스 양은 다시 바느질감을 든다. 또한 저녁에 타울린슨 씨는 바람을 쐬러 나가는데, 아직 자기 상복 보닛을 써 보지 못한 하녀가 함께한다. 그들은 어스름한 길모퉁이에서 서로에게 무척 다정하며, 타울린슨은 옥스퍼드 마켓에서 진중한 청과상이 되어 새사람이 되고 흠 없는 삶을 꾸려 가는 환상에 잠긴다.\n\n오늘 밤 돔비 씨의 집에는, 여러 밤 동안 그랬던 것보다 더 깊은 잠과 더 깊은 안식이 깃든다. 아침 해가 다시 한번 옛 일상으로 돌아간 그 오래된 식솔들을 깨운다. 맞은편의 발그레한 아이들은 굴렁쇠를 굴리며 달려 지나간다. 교회에서는 성대한 결혼식이 열린다. 곡예사의 아내는 동네 다른 구역에서 돈통을 들고 분주하다. 석공은 자기 앞의 대리석판에 P-A-U-L을 새기며 노래하고 휘파람을 분다.\n\n그리고 이토록 가득 차고 분주한 세상에서, 한 가냘픈 존재의 상실이 어느 가슴엔가, 광막한 영원의 넓이와 깊이가 아니고서는 그 무엇으로도 채울 수 없을 만큼 넓고 깊은 빈자리를 남긴다는 것이 과연 있을 수 있는 일일까! 플로렌스라면, 그 순결한 비통 속에서, 이렇게 대답했으리라. \"오 내 동생, 오 내 그토록 사랑하고 사랑받던 동생! 푸대접받던 내 어린 시절의 유일한 벗이자 동무! 그보다 못한 어떤 생각이, 네 이른 무덤 위에 이미 동터 오는 빛을 비출 수 있을까, 혹은 이 눈물의 빗속에서 피어나는 누그러진 슬픔을 낳을 수 있을까!\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 이 기회를 잘 활용하는 것이 자기에게 지워진 의무라 여기는 칙 부인이 말했다, \"네가 내 나이쯤 되면—\"\n\n\"그게 인생의 한창때죠,\" 톡스 양이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그러면 너도,\" 칙 부인이 그 다정한 말에 대한 답례로 톡스 양의 손을 부드럽게 꼭 쥐며 말을 이었다, \"그러면 너도 모든 슬픔이 부질없다는 것을, 그리고 순응하는 것이 우리의 의무라는 것을 알게 될 거다.\"\n\n\"노력할게요, 고모. 정말 노력하고 있어요,\" 플로렌스가 흐느끼며 대답했다.\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘구나,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"왜냐하면, 얘야, 우리의 사랑하는 톡스 양께서도—그분의 건전한 분별력과 탁월한 판단력에 대해서는 두 가지 의견이 있을 수가 없지—\"\n\n\"루이자, 나 정말이지 곧 우쭐해지겠어요,\" 톡스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"—너에게 말해 주고, 또 자기 경험으로 확증해 주시겠지만,\" 칙 부인이 말을 이었다, \"우리는 어떤 경우에든 노력을 하라는 부름을 받는단다. 그게 우리에게 요구되는 일이지. 만약 어떤—얘야,\" 톡스 양을 돌아보며, \"단어가 하나 생각이 안 나요. 인간—인간—\"\n\n\"품행 말인가요?\" 톡스 양이 넌지시 일러 주었다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니에요,\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"어쩜 그래요! 세상에, 혀끝에서 맴도는데. 인간—\"\n\n\"빗나간 애정 말인가요?\" 톡스 양이 머뭇거리며 일러 주었다.\n\n\"맙소사, 루크리셔!\" 칙 부인이 응수했다. \"어쩜 그리 끔찍한 소릴! 인간 혐오자, 그게 내가 찾던 단어예요. 별생각을 다! 빗나간 애정이라니! 내 말은, 만약 어떤 인간 혐오자가 내 앞에서 '우리는 왜 태어났는가?'라는 질문을 던진다면, 나는 '노력하기 위해서요'라고 대답하겠다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"정말 훌륭해요,\" 톡스 양이 그 발상의 독창성에 깊이 감동하여 말했다. \"정말 훌륭해요.\"\n\n\"불행히도,\" 칙 부인이 말을 이었다, \"우리는 바로 우리 눈앞에 하나의 경고를 두고 있단다. 얘야, 만약 이 집안에서 제때에 노력을 기울였더라면, 가장 견디기 힘들고 비통한 일련의 사정들을 피할 수 있었으리라고 생각할 만한 이유가 우리에겐 너무도 많구나. 그 무엇도 나를 설득하지 못할 거다,\" 이 선량한 부인이 단호한 태도로 말했다, \"가엾고 사랑스러운 패니가 그 노력을 기울였더라면, 적어도 가엾고 사랑스러운 그 아이가 더 튼튼한 체질을 타고났으리라는 생각만은 말이다.\"\n\n칙 부인은 잠시 자기 감정에 자신을 내맡겼다. 그러나 자기 학설의 실천적 본보기로서, 흐느낌의 한가운데서 딱 멈추고는, 다시 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그러니 플로렌스, 부디 네가 어느 정도 마음의 굳셈을 지니고 있음을 보여 다오, 그리고 네 가엾은 아버지가 빠져 있는 그 비통함을 이기적으로 더 무겁게 하지는 말아 다오.\"\n\n\"고모!\" 플로렌스가 그녀의 얼굴을 더 잘, 더 간절히 들여다보려고 그 앞에 얼른 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. \"아버지에 대해 더 말해 주세요. 제발 아버지 이야기를 해 주세요! 아버지는 정말 마음이 무너지셨나요?\"\n\n톡스 양은 다정한 성품이었고, 이 호소에는 그녀를 몹시 흔들어 놓는 무언가가 있었다. 푸대접받던 이 아이가, 죽은 오라비가 그토록 자주 드러내던 그 애정 어린 관심을 이어받으려는 것으로 보았든—혹은 그를 사랑했던 그 마음에 자신을 휘감으려 하는, 그리고 그런 슬픔에 대한 공감에서, 그토록 슬픈 사랑과 비탄의 공동체에서 배제되는 것을 견딜 수 없어 하는 사랑으로 보았든—혹은 비록 내쳐지고 거부당했으나, 오래도록 보답받지 못한 다정함으로 쥐어짜인 채, 이 사별의 황량함과 외로움 속에서 그에게, 거기서 위안을 구하라고, 어떤 작은 응답으로 얼마간의 위안을 베풀어 달라고 부르짖던 그 간절하고 헌신적인 영혼을 알아본 것뿐이든—그녀가 그것을 어떻게 이해했든 간에, 그것은 톡스 양을 흔들어 놓았다. 그 순간 그녀는 칙 부인의 위엄도 잊고, 플로렌스의 뺨을 황급히 토닥이고는, 고개를 돌려, 그 현명한 부인의 선례를 기다리지도 않고 두 눈에서 눈물이 솟구치도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n칙 부인 자신도, 그토록 자랑스러워하던 그 침착함을 잠시 잃었다. 그리고 그토록 오래, 그토록 한결같이, 그토록 끈기 있게 그 작은 침대를 향하고 있던 그 아름다운 어린 얼굴을 바라보며, 말없이 있었다. 그러나 목소리를 되찾자—그것은 그녀의 침착함과 동의어였으니, 사실 그 둘은 하나이며 같은 것이었다—그녀는 위엄 있게 대답했다.\n\n\"플로렌스, 얘야, 네 가엾은 아버지는 때때로 별나시단다. 그리고 나에게 그분에 대해 묻는 것은, 내가 정말이지 이해하는 척조차 할 수 없는 주제에 대해 묻는 거란다. 내 생각에 나는 누구 못지않게 네 아버지에게 영향력을 지니고 있어. 그래도 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤, 그분이 나에게 거의 아무 말씀도 하지 않으셨다는 것뿐이야. 그리고 나는 그분을 한두 번, 그것도 한 번에 잠깐씩밖에 못 뵈었고, 사실 그때조차 방이 어두워서 거의 못 뵌 거나 마찬가지란다. 나는 네 아버지께 '폴!'이라고—그게 내가 쓴 정확한 표현이야—'폴! 어째서 뭐든 기운 나는 걸 좀 드시지 않으세요?' 하고 말했단다. 네 아버지의 대답은 늘 '루이자, 부디 나를 좀 내버려 두렴. 나는 아무것도 필요 없다. 혼자 있는 게 낫다'였어. 루크리셔, 내일 치안판사 앞에서 선서를 하라고 한대도,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"나는 분명히 그 토씨 하나 틀림없는 말들을 두고 맹세할 수 있을 거예요.\"\n\n톡스 양은 \"우리 루이자는 언제나 빈틈이 없다니까요!\"라고 말하며 감탄을 표했다.\n\n\"요컨대, 플로렌스,\" 고모가 말을 이었다, \"오늘까지 네 가엾은 아버지와 나 사이에는 말 그대로 아무 일도 없었단다. 오늘에야 내가 네 아버지께 바닛 경과 스케틀스 부인이 더없이 친절한 편지를 보내셨다고 말씀드렸지—우리 귀여운 아이! 스케틀스 부인은 그 애를 마치—내 손수건이 어디 갔지?\"\n\n톡스 양이 하나 꺼내 주었다.\n\n\"더없이 친절한 편지로, 기분 전환 삼아 네가 그분들을 방문하면 어떻겠냐고 제안하셨단다. 톡스 양과 내가 이제 집으로 돌아가도 되겠다고 생각한다고 네 아버지께 말씀드리면서(그분도 전적으로 동의하셨어), 네가 이 초대를 받아들이는 데 무슨 반대가 있으신지 여쭈었지. 그분은 '아니, 루이자, 조금도 없네!' 하고 말씀하셨어.\"\n\n플로렌스가 눈물 어린 눈을 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"그렇지만 만약, 플로렌스, 네가 지금 이 방문을 하거나 나와 함께 집으로 가는 것보다 여기 남는 편을 더 좋아한다면—\"\n\n\"훨씬 더 그러고 싶어요, 고모,\" 가냘픈 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그러면, 얘야,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"그래도 된단다. 참 이상한 선택이라고 말하지 않을 수 없구나. 하지만 너는 늘 이상한 아이였지. 네 나이의 다른 누구라도, 그리고 그런 일을 겪고 나면—얘 톡스 양, 또 손수건을 잃어버렸네요—여기를 떠나게 되어 기뻐할 거라고 다들 생각할 텐데.\"\n\n\"이 집이 기피당하는 것처럼 느껴지는 건 싫어요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"위층의 그—그분의—방들이 텅 비고 쓸쓸하다고 생각하는 것도 싫고요, 고모. 당분간은 여기 있는 편이 더 좋아요. 오 내 동생! 오 내 동생!\"\n\n그것은 억누를 수 없는, 자연스러운 감정이었다. 그리고 그것은 그녀가 얼굴을 가린 두 손의 손가락 사이로조차 비집고 새어 나오려 했다. 그토록 벅차고 무거운 짐을 진 가슴은 이따금 그렇게 터져 나올 곳이 있어야만 했다. 그러지 않으면 그 안에 든 가엾고 상처 입은 외로운 마음이, 날개 부러진 새처럼 파닥이다가 먼지 속으로 가라앉아 버렸을 것이다.\n\n\"자, 얘야!\" 칙 부인이 잠시 사이를 두고 말했다. \"나는 결코 너에게 무정한 말을 할 생각은 추호도 없단다, 그건 너도 분명히 알 거야. 그럼 너는 여기 남아서, 네가 하고 싶은 대로 꼭 그렇게 하렴. 누구도 너를 간섭하지 않을 거고, 간섭하고 싶어 하지도 않을 거야, 분명히.\"\n\n플로렌스는 슬프게 동의하며 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"내가 네 가엾은 아버지께 일시적인 기분 전환으로 얼마간 기분도 풀고 기운도 되찾으셔야 한다고 막 권하기 시작하자마자,\" 칙 부인이 말했다, \"그분은 이미 잠시 시골에 내려갈 작정을 해 두셨다고 말씀하시더구나. 정말이지 어서 빨리 가시면 좋겠어. 빠르면 빠를수록 좋지. 하지만 우리 모두를 그토록 힘들게 한 이 비통한 일에 뒤따라, 개인 서류며 그런 것들과 관련된 정리할 일들이 좀 있어서—내 건 대체 어디로 갔는지 모르겠네. 루크리셔, 네 걸 좀 빌려 줘요, 얘—그분 방에서 한두 저녁쯤 그 일에 매달리실지도 모르겠다. 네 아버지는, 얘야, 둘도 없는 진짜 돔비 사람이란다,\" 칙 부인이 톡스 양의 손수건 양쪽 귀퉁이로 두 눈을 한꺼번에 아주 조심스레 닦으며 말했다. \"그분은 노력하실 거야. 그분에 대해서는 걱정할 게 없단다.\"\n\n\"고모, 제가 무언가—\" 플로렌스가 떨면서 말했다, \"제가 할 수 있는 일이 없을까요—\"\n\n\"맙소사, 얘야,\" 칙 부인이 황급히 끼어들었다, \"무슨 소리니? 네 아버지가 나에게—그분의 정확한 말을 네게 그대로 들려줬잖니, '루이자, 나는 아무것도 필요 없다, 혼자 있는 게 낫다'고—하셨다면, 너에게는 뭐라고 하실 것 같으냐? 너는 그분 앞에 나타나서는 안 된단다, 얘야. 그런 건 꿈도 꾸지 마라.\"\n\n\"고모,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"저는 가서 침대에 좀 누워 있을게요.\"\n\n칙 부인은 이 결심을 옳게 여기고, 입맞춤으로 그녀를 보냈다. 그러나 톡스 양은, 잃어버린 손수건을 찾는다는 어설픈 핑계로, 그녀를 따라 위층으로 올라갔다. 그리고 수전 니퍼의 큰 거부감에도 아랑곳없이, 몇 분 남짓 짬을 내어 그녀를 위로하려 애썼다. 니퍼 양은, 그 불타는 열성으로, 톡스 양을 악어라고 헐뜯었으니 말이다. 그렇지만 그녀의 공감은 진실해 보였고, 적어도 사심 없음이라는 유리한 입지가 있었다—그것으로 얻을 호의라곤 거의 없었으니.\n\n그런데 이 안간힘 쓰는 마음을 그 고뇌 속에서 떠받쳐 줄, 수전보다 더 가깝고 더 소중한 이는 없었던가? 끌어안을 다른 목은 없었던가, 의지할 다른 얼굴은? 그런 깊은 슬픔에 위로의 말 한마디 건넬 다른 누군가는 없었던가? 플로렌스는 이 황량한 세상에서 그토록 외로워, 그녀에게 남은 것이 아무것도 없었던가? 아무것도 없었다. 한꺼번에 어미도 동생도 잃은 채—어린 폴을 잃은 것에서, 그 첫째가는 가장 큰 상실이 그녀에게 무겁게 내려앉았으니—이것이 그녀가 가진 유일한 의지처였다. 오, 처음에 그녀가 얼마나 도움을 필요로 했는지 누가 말로 다 할 수 있으랴!\n\n처음에, 집이 다시 익숙한 일상으로 가라앉고, 하인들만 남고 다들 떠나 버렸으며, 아버지는 자기 방에 틀어박혀 있을 때, 플로렌스는 그저 울고, 이리저리 거니는 것밖에 할 수 없었다. 그리고 때로는 황량한 기억의 갑작스러운 고통에 사로잡혀, 제 방으로 달려가, 두 손을 비틀고, 침대에 얼굴을 묻고는, 아무 위안도 알지 못했다. 슬픔의 쓰라림과 잔혹함밖에는. 이런 일은 대개 그와 매우 다정하게 얽힌 어떤 장소나 물건을 알아본 끝에 뒤따라 일어났다. 그리고 그것은 그 비참한 집을, 처음에는, 고통의 장소로 만들었다.\n\n그러나 순수한 사랑이 그토록 사납고 무정하게 오래 타오르는 것은 그 본성에 맞지 않는다. 그 거친 성분에 흙의 더러움이 섞인 불꽃은 그것을 품어 주는 가슴을 갉아먹을 수 있다. 그러나 하늘에서 내려온 불은, 모여 있던 열두 사람의 머리 위에 내려앉아 저마다에게 제 형제를 환히 밝혀진 모습으로, 상함 없이 보여 주었을 때처럼, 마음속에서도 온화하다. 떠올린 그 모습과 더불어, 곧 평온한 얼굴이, 부드러운 목소리가, 사랑 어린 눈길이, 그 고요한 신뢰와 평화가 되돌아왔다. 그리고 플로렌스는, 여전히 울긴 했으나, 더 평온하게 울었고, 그 기억을 가까이 불러들였다.\n\n오래지 않아, 옛 자리에서, 옛 고요한 시간에, 벽 위에서 일렁이던 금빛 물결을, 그것이 빠져나가며 잦아들 때, 그녀의 차분한 눈이 응시하게 되었다. 오래지 않아 그 방은 다시, 자주 그녀를 맞이하게 되었다. 그녀는 거기 홀로 앉아, 그 작은 침대 곁에서 지켜보던 때처럼 참을성 있고 온순했다. 그것이 비어 있다는 어떤 날카로운 느낌이 그녀를 후려칠 때면, 그녀는 그 곁에 무릎을 꿇고, 하느님께 기도드릴 수 있었다—그것은 그녀의 벅찬 마음이 쏟아져 나오는 것이었다—천사 하나가 자기를 사랑하고 자기를 기억하게 해 달라고.\n\n오래지 않아, 그토록 넓고 쓸쓸한 그 음울한 집 한가운데서, 황혼 녘 그녀의 낮은 목소리가, 더디게, 때로는 멈추면서, 그가 자기 팔에 고개를 떨군 채 그토록 자주 귀 기울이던 그 옛 가락을 어루만지게 되었다. 그리고 그러고 나서, 날이 완전히 어두워졌을 때, 한 가닥 음악이 방 안에서 떨려 왔다. 어찌나 부드럽게 연주되고 노래되었던지, 그것은 그 마지막 밤에 그의 청에 따라 자기가 했던 일을, 슬프게 회상하는 것에 가까웠지, 그 실제를 되풀이하는 것 같지는 않았다. 그러나 그것은 되풀이되었다, 자주—아주 자주, 그 어둑한 고독 속에서. 그리고 그 달콤한 목소리가 눈물 속으로 잦아들 때면, 그 가락의 끊어진 속삭임이 여전히 건반 위에서 떨리고 있었다.\n\n이렇게 하여 그녀는, 바닷가에서 그의 곁에 앉아 자기 손가락이 바삐 매만지던 그 일거리를 바라볼 용기를 얻었다. 그리고 이렇게 하여 오래지 않아 그녀는 다시 그것을 손에 들게 되었다—마치 그것이 감각을 지녀 그를 알기라도 했던 듯, 그것에 대한 어떤 인간적인 사랑을 품고서. 그리고 그토록 오래 버려졌던 그 쓰지 않는 방, 어머니의 초상화 가까운 창가에 앉아, 생각에 잠긴 시간들을 흘려보냈다.\n\n어째서 그 검은 눈은 이 일거리에서 그토록 자주, 발그레한 아이들이 사는 쪽으로 돌아갔을까? 그 아이들이 곧바로 그녀의 상실을 떠올리게 한 것은 아니었다. 그들은 모두 여자아이였으니까. 네 명의 어린 자매였다. 그러나 그들은 그녀처럼 어미가 없었다—그리고 아버지가 있었다.\n\n그가 외출했다가 돌아올 때가 되었음을 알기란 쉬웠다. 큰 아이가 늘 차려입고 응접실 창가나 발코니에서 그를 기다리고 있었기 때문이다. 그가 나타나면, 그 아이의 기대에 찬 얼굴이 기쁨으로 환해졌고, 한편 높은 창가에 있던, 역시 늘 망을 보던 다른 아이들은 손뼉을 치고, 창턱을 두드리고, 그를 부르며 소리쳤다. 큰 아이는 현관으로 내려와, 그의 손에 제 손을 얹고, 그를 위층으로 이끌어 올라갔다. 그러면 플로렌스는 그 뒤에 그 아이가 그의 곁에, 혹은 그의 무릎에 앉아 있거나, 어리광 부리듯 그의 목에 매달려 그에게 이야기하는 모습을 보곤 했다. 그리고 그들은 늘 함께 즐거웠지만, 그는 마치 그 아이가 죽은 제 어미를 닮았다고 여기기라도 하는 듯, 자주 그 아이의 얼굴을 바라보곤 했다. 플로렌스는 때때로 이것을 더는 보지 못하고, 울음을 터뜨리며 마치 겁이라도 먹은 듯 커튼 뒤에 숨거나, 창가에서 황급히 자리를 뜨곤 했다. 그러나 그녀는 다시 돌아오지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그녀의 일거리는 곧 다시, 돌보아지지 못한 채 그녀의 두 손에서 떨어지곤 했다.\n\n그것은 여러 해 전에 비어 있던 그 집이었다. 그 집은 오랫동안 그대로 비어 있었다. 마침내, 그녀가 집을 떠나 있는 동안, 이 가족이 그 집을 들었다. 그리고 그 집은 수리되고 새로 칠해졌으며, 그 주위에는 새와 꽃들이 있었다. 그래서 옛 모습과는 무척 달라 보였다. 그러나 그녀는 그 집은 결코 떠올리지 않았다. 그 아이들과 그들의 아버지가 전부였다.\n\n그가 저녁을 먹고 나면, 그녀는 열린 창문 너머로, 그 아이들이 가정교사나 유모와 함께 내려와 탁자 둘레에 모여드는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그리고 고요한 여름 날씨 속에서, 그 아이들의 어린 목소리와 맑은 웃음소리가 거리를 가로질러 울려와, 그녀가 앉아 있는 방의 가라앉은 공기 속으로 들어오곤 했다. 그러고 나면 그들은 그와 함께 위층으로 기어오르듯 올라가, 소파에서 그를 둘러싸고 뛰놀거나, 그의 무릎가에 옹기종기 모였으니, 자그마한 얼굴들의 영락없는 꽃다발이었다. 그러는 동안 그는 그들에게 무슨 이야기인가를 들려주는 듯했다. 혹은 그들은 발코니로 달려 나오기도 했다. 그러면 플로렌스는, 검은 옷을 입고 거기 홀로 앉아 있는 자기를 보고 그들의 기쁨에 찬물을 끼얹게 될까 봐, 얼른 몸을 숨기곤 했다.\n\n나머지 아이들이 가 버리면 큰 아이는 아버지 곁에 남아 그를 위해 차를 끓였으니—그때 그 아이는 행복한 작은 살림꾼이었다!—그리고 양초가 들어올 때까지, 때로는 창가에서, 때로는 방 안에서, 그와 이야기를 나누며 앉아 있었다. 그는 그 아이를, 비록 플로렌스보다 몇 살 어렸으나, 자기 동무로 삼았다. 그리고 그 아이는 제 작은 책이나 반짇고리를 들고서, 한 사람의 여인만큼이나 침착하고 사랑스럽도록 얌전할 수 있었다. 양초가 들어오면, 플로렌스는 제 어두운 방에서 다시 바라보기를 두려워하지 않았다. 그러나 그 아이가 \"안녕히 주무세요, 아버지\" 하고 말하고 잠자리에 들 시간이 되면, 플로렌스는 그를 향해 얼굴을 들어 올리며 흐느끼고 떨었고, 더는 바라볼 수 없었다.\n\n그래도 그녀는, 자기가 잠자리에 들기 전이면, 오래전 그토록 자주 그를 잠재웠던 그 소박한 가락에서, 그리고 또 다른 낮고 부드러운 그 끊어진 음악의 가락에서, 다시 또다시 그 집으로 눈길을 돌리곤 했다. 그러나 그녀가 그 집을 떠올렸다는 것, 혹은 그 집을 지켜보았다는 것은, 그녀가 제 어린 가슴속에 간직한 비밀이었다.\n\n그런데 플로렌스의 그 가슴은—그토록 천진하고 진실한, 그가 그녀에게 품었던, 마지막 가냘픈 말로 속삭였던 그 사랑을 받을 만한, 그 꾸밈없는 마음이 얼굴의 아름다움에 비치고 부드러운 목소리의 한마디 한마디에 배어 나오는 그 플로렌스의 가슴은—그 어린 가슴은 또 다른 비밀을 품고 있었을까? 그렇다. 하나 더.\n\n집 안의 누구도 움직이지 않고 등불이 모두 꺼졌을 때, 그녀는 가만히 제 방을 나와, 소리 없는 발걸음으로 계단을 내려가, 아버지의 방문에 다가가곤 했다. 그 문에 대고, 숨도 거의 쉬지 못한 채, 그녀는 얼굴과 머리를 기대고, 사랑의 그리움 속에서 입술을 갖다 댔다. 그녀는 밤마다 그 문 밖 차가운 돌바닥에 웅크려, 그의 숨소리만이라도 들으려 했다. 그리고 그에게 어떤 애정이나마 보여 주고, 그에게 위안이 되어 주고, 그의 외로운 자식인 자기에게서 우러난 어떤 다정함을 견뎌 내도록 그를 마음 돌리게 하고 싶다는, 그 하나에 사로잡힌 소원으로, 그녀는 감히 그럴 수만 있었다면, 그의 발치에 무릎 꿇고 겸허히 애원했을 것이다.\n\n아무도 그것을 알지 못했다. 아무도 그것을 생각하지 못했다. 문은 늘 닫혀 있었고, 그는 그 안에 틀어박혀 있었다. 그는 한두 번 외출했고, 집안에서는 그가 머지않아 시골 여행을 떠날 거라는 말이 돌았다. 그러나 그는 그 방들에서 살았고, 홀로 살았으며, 결코 그녀를 보지도, 그녀의 안부를 묻지도 않았다. 어쩌면 그는 그녀가 집에 있다는 것조차 알지 못했을 것이다.\n\n어느 날, 장례가 있은 지 일주일쯤 지났을 때, 플로렌스가 일거리를 하며 앉아 있는데, 수전이 반은 웃고 반은 우는 얼굴로 나타나, 방문객이 왔다고 알렸다.\n\n\"방문객! 나한테, 수전?\" 플로렌스가 깜짝 놀라 고개를 들며 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 신기한 일이지요, 안 그래요, 플로이 아가씨?\" 수전이 말했다. \"하지만 저는 아가씨한테 방문객이 많았으면 좋겠어요, 정말로요. 그래야 아가씨한테 훨씬 좋을 테니까요. 그리고 제 생각엔, 아가씨랑 제가 그 늙은 스케틀스 댁에라도 빨리 갈수록, 우리 둘 다한테 좋을 것 같아요. 저는 사람들 북적이는 데서 살고 싶진 않지만요, 플로이 아가씨, 그래도 저는 굴은 아니잖아요.\"\n\n니퍼 양을 공정하게 평하자면, 그녀는 자기 자신보다 어린 여주인을 위해 더 많이 말한 것이었다. 그리고 그녀의 얼굴이 그것을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"그런데 방문객이라니, 수전,\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n수전은, 웃음이기도 하고 흐느낌이기도 한, 흐느낌이기도 하고 웃음이기도 한 히스테릭한 폭발과 함께, 대답했다,\n\n\"투츠 씨예요!\"\n\n플로렌스의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 미소가 순식간에 사라졌고, 그녀의 두 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다. 그러나 어쨌든 그것은 미소였고, 그것이 니퍼 양에게 큰 만족을 주었다.\n\n\"제 심정도 꼭 그래요, 플로이 아가씨,\" 수전이 앞치마를 눈에 갖다 대며 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"현관에서 그 순진한 양반을 보자마자요, 플로이 아가씨, 저는 먼저 웃음이 터져 나왔다가, 그다음엔 목이 메었지 뭐예요.\"\n\n수전 니퍼는 저도 모르게 그 자리에서 또다시 그러기 시작했다. 그러는 동안 그녀를 따라 위층으로 올라온 투츠 씨는, 자기가 일으킨 효과는 전혀 알지 못한 채, 손가락 마디로 문을 두드려 자기 도착을 알리고, 무척 활기차게 안으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 돔비 아가씨?\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"저는 아주 잘 지냅니다, 고맙습니다. 아가씨는 어떠세요?\"\n\n투츠 씨는—세상에 그보다 나은 사내는 거의 없었으나, 그보다 머리가 잘 도는 사람은 한둘쯤 있었을지 모른다—플로렌스와 자기 둘 다의 심정을 풀어 주고자 이 긴 한바탕의 인사말을 애써 궁리해 낸 터였다. 그러나 의자에 앉기도 전에, 플로렌스가 한마디 하기도 전에, 또 자기가 문 안에 채 들어서기도 전에 그 전부를 탕진해 버려, 말하자면 자기 밑천을 분별없이 다 써 버렸음을 깨닫고는, 다시 시작하는 게 좋겠다고 여겼다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 돔비 아가씨?\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"저는 아주 잘 지냅니다, 고맙습니다. 아가씨는 어떠세요?\"\n\n플로렌스가 그에게 손을 내밀며, 자기는 아주 잘 지낸다고 말했다.\n\n\"저는 정말이지 아주 잘 지냅니다,\" 투츠 씨가 의자에 앉으며 말했다. \"정말 잘 지내요, 그렇습니다. 제 기억으로는,\" 투츠 씨가 잠시 생각한 뒤에 말했다, \"이보다 더 좋았던 적이 없는 것 같습니다, 고맙습니다.\"\n\n\"와 주셔서 정말 고마워요,\" 플로렌스가 일거리를 집어 들며 말했다. \"뵙게 되어 무척 기뻐요.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 킬킬 웃음으로 답했다. 그것이 너무 들떠 보일까 싶어, 그는 한숨으로 바로잡았다. 그것이 너무 우울해 보일까 싶어, 그는 다시 킬킬 웃음으로 바로잡았다. 어느 쪽 대답 방식으로도 자기 자신이 영 흡족하지 않아, 그는 거칠게 숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"제 사랑하는 동생에게 무척 잘 대해 주셨지요,\" 플로렌스가, 그렇게 말함으로써 그의 마음을 풀어 주려는 자기 자신의 자연스러운 충동에 따라 말했다. \"그 애가 자주 당신 얘기를 저에게 했어요.\"\n\n\"오, 그건 아무것도 아닙니다,\" 투츠 씨가 황급히 말했다. \"덥지요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"날씨가 참 좋아요,\" 플로렌스가 대답했다.\n\n\"저한테는 잘 맞아요!\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"지금만큼 좋았던 적이 없는 것 같습니다, 고맙습니다.\n\n이 기묘하고 뜻밖의 사실을 밝힌 뒤, 투츠 씨는 깊은 침묵의 우물 속으로 빠져들었다.\n\n\"블림버 박사님 댁을 떠나셨지요, 아마?\" 플로렌스가 그를 끌어내 보려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"그랬기를 바라야지요,\" 투츠 씨가 대꾸했다. 그러고는 다시 빠져들었다.\n\n그는 적어도 십 분 동안, 보아하니 물에 빠진 채로, 바닥에 머물러 있었다. 그 시간이 다하자, 그는 불쑥 떠올라 말했다,\n\n\"자! 안녕히 계세요, 돔비 아가씨.\"\n\n\"가시려고요?\" 플로렌스가 일어서며 물었다.\n\n\"그래도 모르겠네요. 아니, 지금 당장은 아니에요,\" 투츠 씨가, 더없이 뜻밖으로, 다시 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"사실은—저기, 돔비 아가씨!\"\n\n\"저한테 말씀하시는 걸 두려워하지 마세요,\" 플로렌스가 잔잔한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"동생 얘기를 해 주신다면 저는 무척 기쁠 거예요.\"\n\n\"정말 그러시겠어요?\" 투츠 씨가, 그 밖에는 무표정한 얼굴의 모든 결마다 공감을 담아 응수했다. \"가엾은 돔비! 우리가 늘 얘기하던 버지스 상회—세련된 양복점이지만 무척 비싼—가 이런 용도로 이 양복을 지어 줄 거라곤 정말 한 번도 생각 못 했습니다.\" 투츠 씨는 상복을 입고 있었다. \"가엾은 돔비! 저기! 돔비 아가씨!\" 투츠가 엉엉 울었다.\n\n\"네,\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"그 애가 마지막에 무척 좋아하게 된 친구가 하나 있어요. 어쩌면 아가씨가 그 녀석을, 일종의 기념품처럼, 두고 싶어 하실 것 같아서요. 그 애가 디오게네스를 기억했던 거, 기억하세요?\"\n\n\"오 네! 오 네!\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"가엾은 돔비! 저도 기억합니다,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n투츠 씨는 플로렌스가 눈물짓는 것을 보고, 이 대목을 넘어가기가 무척 어려워, 하마터면 다시 우물에 빠질 뻔했다. 그러나 킬킬 웃음이 그 가장자리에서 그를 구해 주었다.\n\n\"저기요,\" 그가 말을 이었다, \"돔비 아가씨! 그쪽에서 그 녀석을 내주지 않았더라면, 저는 십 실링에 그 녀석을 훔쳐 오게 할 수도 있었어요. 그리고 그렇게 했을 거예요. 하지만 제 생각엔 그쪽도 그 녀석을 떼어 내게 되어 좋아하는 눈치였어요. 그 녀석을 두고 싶으시면, 문 앞에 와 있어요. 아가씨 드리려고 일부러 데려왔거든요. 숙녀에게 어울리는 개는 아니에요, 아시다시피,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"하지만 그건 신경 안 쓰시겠지요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n사실, 디오게네스는 바로 그 순간, 그들이 곧 거리를 내려다보며 확인했듯이, 삯마차의 창문 너머로 노려보고 있었다. 그 마차에는, 그 자리로 실어 오기 위해, 짚 속에 쥐가 있다는 거짓 핑계로 그를 꾀어 넣은 것이었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그는 숙녀에게 어울리는 개와는 더없이 동떨어진 모습이었다. 그리고 밖으로 나가고 싶어 안달하는 그 무뚝뚝한 모습은 어지간히 시원찮아 보였으니, 그는 입 한쪽으로 짧게 캉캉 짖어 대고, 그 한 번 한 번의 안간힘에 너무 힘을 줘서 제 몸의 균형을 잃고 짚 속으로 고꾸라졌다가, 다시 헐떡이며 벌떡 솟구쳐 올라, 마치 건강 검진을 받으러 진료소에 급히 달려오기라도 한 양 혀를 쑥 내밀었다.\n\n그러나 디오게네스가 여름날에 마주칠 수 있는 가장 우스꽝스러운 개였다 해도—덤벙대고, 못생기고, 굼뜨고, 머리통은 총알처럼 둥글고, 근처에 짖어 댈 만한 가치가 있는 적이 있다는 그릇된 생각에 노상 사로잡혀 있는 개였다 해도—그리고 비록 그가 결코 성미가 좋지 않았고, 분명 영리하지도 않았으며, 두 눈을 온통 털로 뒤덮고, 익살맞은 코에, 어딘가 어긋난 꼬리에, 무뚝뚝한 목소리를 지녔다 해도—그는, 그를 두고 작별의 순간에 남긴 그 기억과, 그를 잘 보살펴 달라던 그 부탁 덕분에, 플로렌스에게는 그 종족 가운데 가장 값지고 아름다운 개보다도 더 소중했다. 정말이지 이 못생긴 디오게네스가 어찌나 소중하고, 그녀에게 어찌나 반가웠던지, 그녀는 투츠 씨의 보석 박힌 손을 잡고 고마운 마음에 거기에 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 풀려난 디오게네스가 계단을 마구 뛰어올라 방 안으로 튀어 들어와서는(먼저 그를 삯마차에서 끄집어내느라 어찌나 한바탕 야단이었던지!), 온갖 가구 밑으로 파고들고, 제 목에 달랑거리며 매달린 긴 쇠사슬을 의자며 탁자 다리에 칭칭 감았다가, 두 눈이 머리에서 거의 튀어나올 지경이 되어 부자연스럽게 도드라져 보일 때까지 그것을 잡아당기고, 친한 척하는 투츠 씨에게 으르렁대고, 또 자기가 평생 모퉁이를 돌며 짖어 댔으되 여태 한 번도 본 적 없는 바로 그 적이라고 도덕적으로 확신하며 타울린슨에게 무작정 덤벼들 때, 플로렌스는 마치 그가 분별력의 기적이라도 되는 양 그를 기뻐했다.\n\n투츠 씨는 자기 선물의 성공에 어찌나 기뻤던지, 그리고 플로렌스가 디오게네스 위로 몸을 굽혀, 작고 섬세한 손으로 그의 거친 등을 쓰다듬는 것을—디오게네스는 그들이 안 첫 순간부터 그것을 너그러이 허락했다—보고 어찌나 즐거웠던지, 작별을 고하기가 어렵게 느껴졌다. 그래서, 만약 디오게네스 본인이 거들어 주지 않았더라면, 의심할 바 없이 작별을 결심하는 데 훨씬 더 오랜 시간이 걸렸을 것이다. 디오게네스는 불쑥 투츠 씨를 향해 짖어 대고, 입을 벌린 채 그에게 짧게 달려드는 짓을 머릿속에 떠올린 것이다. 이 시위가 어떻게 끝날지 영 갈피를 잡지 못하고, 또 그것이 버지스 상회의 솜씨로 지어진 바지를 위태롭게 한다는 것을 의식한 투츠 씨는, 킬킬 웃으며 문밖으로 슬그머니 빠져나갔다. 그러고는 아무 용건도 없이 두세 번 다시 안을 들여다보았다가, 그때마다 디오게네스가 새로이 달려드는 통에, 마침내 자리를 떠 가 버렸다.\n\n\"이리 와, 그럼, 디! 사랑스러운 디! 새 여주인하고 친해지렴. 우리 서로 사랑하자, 디!\" 플로렌스가 그의 텁수룩한 머리를 어루만지며 말했다. 그러자 거칠고 무뚝뚝한 디는, 마치 그 텁수룩한 가죽이 거기 떨어진 눈물을 스며들게 하고, 그 눈물이 떨어지자 제 개의 마음이 녹아내리기라도 한 듯, 코를 들어 그녀의 얼굴에 갖다 대고는, 충성을 맹세했다.\n\n사람 디오게네스가 알렉산드로스 대왕에게 한 말도, 개 디오게네스가 플로렌스에게 한 말보다 더 분명하지는 않았다. 그는 작은 여주인의 제안에 기꺼이 동의하고, 그녀를 섬기는 데 헌신했다. 그를 위해 한쪽 구석에 곧바로 잔칫상이 차려졌다. 그리고 그가 배불리 먹고 마신 뒤, 그는 플로렌스가 앉아 바라보고 있는 창가로 가, 뒷다리로 일어서서, 어색한 앞발을 그녀의 어깨에 얹고, 그녀의 얼굴과 손을 핥고, 그 큰 머리를 그녀의 가슴에 파묻고, 지칠 때까지 꼬리를 흔들었다. 마침내, 디오게네스는 그녀의 발치에 몸을 둥글게 말고 잠이 들었다.\n\n니퍼 양은 개에 대해서는 신경이 곤두서서, 마치 징검돌을 밟고 개울을 건너기라도 하듯 치맛자락을 조심스레 그러모은 채 방에 들어와야 한다고 여겼고, 또 디오게네스가 기지개를 켤 때면 작은 비명을 지르며 의자 위로 올라서야 한다고 여겼지만, 그녀는 그녀 나름으로 투츠 씨의 친절에 마음이 움직였다. 그리고 플로렌스가 어린 폴의 이 거친 친구의 애착과 벗함에 그토록 생기를 띠는 것을, 두 눈에 눈물이 차오를 만큼의 어떤 마음속 소회 없이는 차마 볼 수 없었다. 그녀의 그 생각의 한 부분으로, 어쩌면 돔비 씨가, 관념의 연상 속에서, 그 개와 결부되었을지도 모른다. 그러나 어쨌든, 저녁 내내 디오게네스와 그 여주인을 지켜본 뒤, 그리고 여주인의 방문 밖 곁방에 디오게네스의 잠자리를 마련해 주려고 무척 호의를 다해 애쓴 뒤, 그녀는 밤 인사를 하고 떠나기 전에 플로렌스에게 황급히 말했다.\n\n\"아버님이 떠나신대요, 플로이 아가씨, 내일 아침에요.\"\n\n\"내일 아침에, 수전?\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨. 그렇게 분부가 내렸어요. 일찍요.\"\n\n\"혹시,\" 플로렌스가 그녀를 쳐다보지 않은 채 물었다, \"아버지가 어디로 가시는지 아니, 수전?\"\n\n\"정확히는 몰라요, 아가씨. 먼저 그 잘난 소령님을 만나러 가신대요. 그리고 제가 만약 어떤 소령이든 아는 사이라면(하느님 맙소사, 그럴 리야 없겠지만), 그게 푸르딩딩한 소령은 아닐 거라고 말씀드려야겠네요!\"\n\n\"쉿, 수전!\" 플로렌스가 부드럽게 타일렀다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 플로이 아가씨,\" 불타는 분노로 가득 차, 평소보다도 끊어 말하는 데 더 신경 쓰지 않는 니퍼 양이 대꾸했다. \"어쩔 수가 없는 걸요, 그분은 푸르딩딩하다고요. 그리고 제가 비록 보잘것없을지언정 기독교인인 한, 저는 본래 살색 친구들을 두든지, 아니면 아예 없든지 할 거예요.\"\n\n그녀가 덧붙인 말과, 또 아래층에서 주워들은 것으로 미루어 보아, 칙 부인이 소령을 돔비 씨의 동행으로 추천했고, 돔비 씨는 얼마간 망설인 끝에 그를 초대한 모양이었다.\n\n\"그분이 무슨 기분 전환이 된다니, 참!\" 니퍼 양이 끝없는 경멸을 담아 혼잣말했다. \"그분이 변화라면, 차라리 한결같음을 주세요.\"\n\n\"잘 자, 수전,\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"안녕히 주무세요, 우리 사랑하는 플로이 아가씨.\"\n\n그 애처로워하는 어조가, 그토록 자주 거칠게 건드려졌으되 그녀나 누군가가 지켜보는 동안에는 결코 귀 기울여지지 못한 그 심금을 울렸다. 홀로 남은 플로렌스는, 한 손에 머리를 괴고, 다른 손을 부풀어 오르는 가슴에 누른 채, 자기의 슬픔과 거리낌 없이 교감했다.\n\n비 내리는 밤이었다. 그리고 그 우울한 비가 지친 듯한 소리로 후두두 떨어지고 또 떨어졌다. 굼뜬 바람이 불며, 마치 고통이나 비탄에 잠긴 듯, 집 둘레를 신음하며 돌았다. 날카로운 소리가 나무들 사이로 떨며 지나갔다. 그녀가 앉아 우는 동안, 밤이 깊어 갔고, 음울한 자정이 종탑들에서 울려 퍼졌다.\n\n플로렌스는 나이로 보면 아이나 다름없었으니—아직 열네 살도 채 안 되었다—죽음이 최근 제 끔찍한 황폐를 일으킨 그 큰 집에서, 그런 시각의 외로움과 음울함은, 더 나이 든 상상력이라면 막연한 공포에 잠겨 골똘하게 만들었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀의 순결한 상상은 단 하나의 주제로 너무도 가득 차, 그런 것들이 비집고 들 틈이 없었다. 그녀의 생각 속에서 떠도는 것은 오직 사랑뿐이었으니—참으로 떠돌고, 내쫓긴 사랑이었으나—언제나 아버지에게로 향하는 사랑이었다.\n\n비가 떨어지는 소리에도, 바람이 신음하는 소리에도, 나무들이 떠는 소리에도, 엄숙한 시계들이 시각을 알리는 소리에도, 이 한 가지 생각을 흔들거나 그 절실함을 덜어 내는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그 사랑하는 죽은 사내아이에 대한 그녀의 기억은—그리고 그것은 결코 떠난 적이 없었다—그것 자체로, 같은 것이었다. 그리고 오, 밖으로 내쳐져 있다는 것, 그토록 길을 잃고 있다는 것, 그 시각 이래로 아버지의 얼굴을 들여다보거나 그를 만져 본 적이 한 번도 없다는 것!\n\n가엾은 아이는, 그때 이래로, 아버지의 방문으로 가는 밤 순례를 하지 않고는 잠자리에 들 수 없었고, 한 번도 든 적이 없었다. 그녀가 지금, 짙은 어둠 속을 가만히 계단을 내려가, 두근거리는 가슴과, 눈물로 흐려진 눈과, 생각 없이 풀어헤쳐져 흘러내린 머리칼로 그 문 앞에 멈춰 서서, 젖은 뺨으로 그 바깥을 어루만지는 모습을 보았다면, 참으로 기이하고 슬픈 광경이었으리라. 그러나 밤이 그것을 가려 주었고, 아무도 알지 못했다.\n\n이날 밤, 그녀가 그 문에 손을 댄 순간, 플로렌스는 그것이 열려 있음을 발견했다. 처음으로 그것이 열려 있었으니, 비록 머리카락 한 올 너비밖에 안 되었지만. 그리고 안에는 불빛이 있었다. 그 겁 많은 아이의 첫 충동은—그리고 그녀는 그것에 굴복했다—재빨리 물러서는 것이었다. 다음 충동은, 돌아가, 들어서는 것이었다. 그리고 이 두 번째 충동이 그녀를 계단 위에서 결단하지 못한 채 붙들어 두었다.\n\n그것이 그만큼의 틈만큼이라도 열려 있다는 데에, 희망이 깃들어 있는 듯했다. 안에서 새어 나온 한 줄기 불빛이, 그 어둡고 엄숙한 문간을 가로질러 스며 나와, 한 가닥 실처럼 대리석 바닥에 떨어지는 것을 보는 데에 격려가 있었다. 그녀는, 자기가 무엇을 하는지도 잘 모른 채, 그러나 안에 있는 사랑과, 함께 겪었으되 나누지는 못한 그 시련에 떠밀려, 발길을 돌렸다. 그리고 두 손을 약간 들어 올린 채 떨면서, 안으로 미끄러져 들어갔다.\n\n그녀의 아버지는 가운데 방의 그 오래된 탁자에 앉아 있었다. 그는 어떤 서류들은 정리하고 다른 것들은 없애고 있었으며, 후자는 그의 앞에 부서지기 쉬운 잔해로 놓여 있었다. 그가 아기였던 가엾은 폴을 그토록 자주 지켜보던 바깥방에서, 비가 유리창에 무겁게 똑똑 떨어졌다. 그리고 밖에서는 바람의 낮은 하소연이 들려왔다.\n\n그러나 그에게는 들리지 않았다. 그는 두 눈을 탁자에 고정한 채, 생각에 너무도 깊이 잠겨 있어, 제 아이의 가벼운 발걸음보다 훨씬 더 묵직한 발소리라도 그를 깨우지 못했을 것이다. 그의 얼굴은 그녀 쪽을 향하고 있었다. 사그라드는 등불 아래, 그 초췌한 시각에, 그것은 지치고 풀 죽어 보였다. 그리고 그를 둘러싼 그 지독한 외로움 속에, 플로렌스의 가슴을 후벼 파는 어떤 호소가 있었다.\n\n\"아버지! 아버지! 말씀 좀 해 주세요, 사랑하는 아버지!\"\n\n그는 그녀의 목소리에 흠칫 놀라, 자리에서 벌떡 일어섰다. 그녀는 두 팔을 벌린 채 그의 바로 앞에 있었으나, 그는 뒤로 물러섰다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐?\" 그가 매섭게 말했다. \"왜 여기 왔느냐? 무엇이 너를 놀라게 했느냐?\"\n\n만약 무언가가 그녀를 놀라게 했다면, 그것은 그가 그녀에게로 돌린 그 얼굴이었다. 그의 어린 딸의 가슴속에서 타오르던 사랑이 그 앞에서 얼어붙었다. 그리고 그녀는 마치 돌로 굳어 버린 듯, 서서 그를 바라보았다.\n\n거기에는 다정함이나 연민의 기색이 한 점도 없었다. 거기에는 관심이나, 어버이로서의 알아봄이나, 누그러짐의 빛이 한 줄기도 없었다. 거기에는 어떤 변화가 있었으나, 그런 종류의 것은 아니었다. 옛날의 그 무관심과 차가운 거북함이 어떤 것에 자리를 내주었으니—그것이 무엇인지 그녀는 결코 생각하지 못했고, 감히 생각하려 하지도 않았으나, 그래도 그 위력을 느꼈으며, 이름은 붙이지 못해도 그것을 잘 알았다. 그것은, 그녀를 바라볼 때, 그녀의 머리 위에 그늘을 드리우는 듯했다.\n\n그는 제 아들의, 건강과 생명에서의 성공한 경쟁자를 자기 앞에 두고 보았던 것일까? 그 아들의 애정에서의 자기 자신의 성공한 경쟁자를 바라보았던 것일까? 미친 질투와 시들어 버린 자존심이, 마땅히 그녀를 그에게 사랑스럽고 소중하게 만들었어야 할 그 달콤한 기억들을 독으로 물들였던 것일까? 그 어린 아들을 생각하며, 그녀의 아름다움과 그 가능성을 바라보는 것이 그에게는 쓰디쓴 쓸개즙이었다는 것이 과연 있을 수 있는 일일까!\n\n플로렌스에게는 그런 생각이 없었다. 그러나 사랑은 언제 자기가 내쳐지고 가망 없는지를 재빨리 안다. 그리고 그녀의 사랑에서, 그녀가 서서 아버지의 얼굴을 들여다볼 때, 희망이 사그라들었다.\n\n\"내가 묻잖느냐, 플로렌스, 놀랐느냐? 무슨 일이 있어서 여기 온 것이냐?\"\n\n\"저는 왔어요, 아버지—\"\n\n\"내 뜻을 거슬러서. 왜?\"\n\n그녀는 그가 왜인지 알고 있음을 보았다. 그것은 그의 얼굴에 큼지막하게 쓰여 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 한 차례 길고 나직한 외마디 소리와 함께 두 손에 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n그가 그것을 그 방에서 기억하기를, 다가올 세월에. 그가 침묵을 깨기 전에, 그것은 이미 허공에서 사그라들었다. 그것은, 그가 믿는 대로, 그의 머릿속에서도 그만큼 빠르게 지나가 버릴지 모른다. 그러나 그것은 거기 있다. 그가 그것을 그 방에서 기억하기를, 다가올 세월에!\n\n그는 그녀의 팔을 붙들었다. 그의 손은 차갑고, 힘이 없었으며, 그녀를 거의 움켜쥐지 않았다.\n\n\"너는 피곤할 게다, 아마,\" 그가 등불을 집어 들고 그녀를 문 쪽으로 이끌며 말했다, \"그리고 쉬고 싶을 테지. 우리는 모두 쉬고 싶단다. 가거라, 플로렌스. 너는 꿈을 꾸고 있었던 거야.\"\n\n그녀가 꾸었던 꿈은, 그때 끝나 버렸다, 하느님 그녀를 도우소서! 그리고 그녀는 그것이 다시는 결코 돌아올 수 없으리라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"네가 위층으로 올라가도록 불을 비춰 주려고 여기 있겠다. 저 위 온 집이 다 네 것이다,\" 그녀의 아버지가 천천히 말했다. \"이제 네가 그 집의 안주인이야. 잘 자거라!\"\n\n여전히 얼굴을 가린 채, 그녀는 흐느끼며 \"안녕히 주무세요, 사랑하는 아버지\" 하고 대답하고는, 말없이 올라갔다. 한 번 그녀는, 마치 그에게로 돌아가고 싶었으나 두려움 때문에 그러지 못하는 듯, 뒤를 돌아보았다. 그것은 한순간의 생각이었으니, 품기에는 너무도 가망 없었다. 그리고 그녀의 아버지는 등불을 들고 거기 서 있었다—굳고, 응답 없고, 미동도 없이—그 어여쁜 아이의 펄럭이는 옷자락이 어둠 속으로 사라질 때까지.\n\n그가 그것을 그 방에서 기억하기를, 다가올 세월에. 지붕 위로 떨어지는 비도, 문밖에서 슬피 우는 바람도, 그 우울한 소리 속에 미리 아는 바를 품고 있을지 모른다. 그가 그것을 그 방에서 기억하기를, 다가올 세월에!\n\n그가 같은 자리에서 그녀가 그 계단을 올라가는 모습을 마지막으로 지켜보았을 때, 그녀는 동생을 품에 안고 있었다. 그것은 지금 그의 마음을 그녀에게로 움직이지 못했고, 오히려 그것을 무쇠처럼 굳혀 버렸다. 그러나 그는 제 방으로 들어가, 문을 잠그고, 의자에 앉아, 잃어버린 제 아들을 위해 울었다.\n\n디오게네스는 제 자리에서 완전히 깨어, 자기의 작은 여주인을 기다리고 있었다.\n\n\"오, 디! 오, 사랑하는 디! 그 애를 봐서 나를 사랑해 줘!\"\n\n디오게네스는 이미 그녀 자신을 봐서 그녀를 사랑하고 있었고, 그것을 얼마나 드러내든 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그래서 그는 곁방에서 갖가지 볼품없는 도약을 선보이며 한껏 우스꽝스러워졌고, 마침내 가엾은 플로렌스가 끝내 잠들어 맞은편의 발그레한 아이들 꿈을 꿀 때, 그녀의 침실 문을 긁어 열고, 제 잠자리를 베개처럼 둘둘 말아, 줄이 닿는 데까지 한껏 늘어진 채 마룻바닥에 머리를 그녀 쪽으로 두고 누워, 눈 위쪽으로 거꾸로 그녀를 게으르게 올려다보다가, 끔뻑끔뻑하던 끝에 저도 잠이 들어, 무뚝뚝하게 짖어 대며 제 적의 꿈을 꾸는 것으로 마무리했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX. Walter goes away\n\n나무로 깎은 견습 항해사는 기계 제작자의 가게 문 앞에서, 그 매정한 작은 항해사답게, 월터의 출발에 더없이 무심했다. 뒷방에서 머무는 마지막 날조차 저물어 가는데도 말이다. 둥글고 까만 혹 같은 눈에 사분의를 댄 채, 굴복을 모르는 민첩함이라는 예의 그 자세를 취한 항해사는, 자기의 요정 같은 짧은 바지를 한껏 돋보이게 내보이며, 과학적 탐구에 몰두한 나머지 세속적인 일에는 아무런 공감도 없었다. 그가 환경의 피조물인 점이 있다면, 건조한 날에는 먼지를 뒤집어쓰고, 안개 낀 날에는 검댕 조각들이 후추처럼 흩뿌려지며, 비 오는 날에는 빛바랜 제복이 잠시 산뜻해지고, 몹시 더운 날에는 살갗이 부풀어 오른다는 정도였다. 그 밖에는 무정하고 완고하고 자만에 찬 항해사로서, 오로지 자기의 발견에만 골몰할 뿐, 시러큐스가 함락될 때의 아르키메데스가 그러했듯이 자기 둘레에서 지상에 벌어지는 일에는 거의 관심이 없었다.\n\n적어도 당시의 집안 형편 속에서 그는 그런 항해사로 보였다. 월터는 드나들며 여러 번 다정하게 그를 바라보았다. 그리고 월터가 없을 때면 가엾은 솔 노인이 와서 문기둥에 기대어, 자기 장사와 가게의 수호신인 그 인물의 구두 버클에 닿을 만큼 가까이 지친 가발을 기대 쉬곤 했다. 그러나 귀에서 귀까지 입이 찢어지고 앵무새 깃털로 만든 살벌한 얼굴을 한 어떤 사나운 우상도, 자기를 섬기는 야만적인 신도들의 호소에 이 항해사가 이런 애착의 표시들에 보인 무심함보다 더 무심하지는 않았으리라.\n\n월터는 난간과 굴뚝들 사이 높은 곳에 있는 자기의 옛 침실을 둘러보며, 이미 어둑해져 가는 하룻밤이 더 지나면 이 방과의 인연도, 어쩌면 영영, 끝나리라는 생각에 마음이 무거웠다. 자기의 자그마한 책과 그림들이 치워진 그 방은, 자기의 버림받음을 차갑게, 또 나무라듯 바라보았고, 이미 다가올 낯섦의 그림자가 드리워져 있었다. \"몇 시간만 더 지나면,\" 월터는 생각했다. \"내가 학생이던 시절 여기서 꾼 어떤 꿈도 이 낡은 방만큼 내게서 멀어지지는 않으리라. 그 꿈은 잠 속에서 다시 찾아올지도 모르고, 나도 깨어서 이곳으로 돌아올지 모른다. 그럴 수도 있겠지. 하지만 적어도 그 꿈만은 다른 어떤 주인도 섬기지 않을 테고, 이 방은 주인이 수두룩하게 들 테며, 그 하나하나가 방을 바꾸고, 방치하고, 함부로 쓸지도 모른다.\"\n\n그러나 그의 삼촌을 작은 뒷방에 홀로 남겨 둘 수는 없었다. 삼촌은 그때 거기 혼자 앉아 있었던 것이다. 거친 데가 있으면서도 사려 깊은 커틀 선장은, 두 사람이 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 함께 이야기를 나눌 수 있도록, 일부러 자기 뜻을 거슬러 가며 자리를 비워 주었다. 그래서 마지막 날의 분주함을 막 마치고 집에 돌아온 월터는, 삼촌의 말동무가 되어 주려고 경쾌하게 아래층으로 내려왔다.\n\n\"삼촌,\" 그는 노인의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 명랑하게 말했다. \"바베이도스에서 뭘 부쳐 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"희망이지, 우리 월리. 우리가 이승에서 다시 만나리라는 희망 말이다. 그걸 부쳐 줄 수 있는 만큼 많이 부쳐 다오.\"\n\n\"그럴게요, 삼촌. 제겐 차고 넘칠 만큼 있으니, 아낌없이 보내드릴게요! 그리고 펄떡이는 거북이며, 커틀 선장님 펀치에 넣을 라임이며, 삼촌 일요일에 드실 설탕절임이며, 그런 것들이라면, 삼촌, 제가 부자만 되면, 배에 가득가득 실어 보내드릴게요.\"\n\n솔 노인은 안경을 닦으며 희미하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"바로 그거예요, 삼촌!\" 월터가 즐겁게 외치며, 또 대여섯 번 어깨를 토닥였다. \"삼촌이 절 기운 나게 해 주시잖아요! 저도 삼촌을 기운 나게 해 드릴게요! 우린 내일 아침 종달새처럼 명랑할 거예요, 삼촌. 종달새처럼 높이 날아오를 거고요! 제 기대로 말하면, 이젠 보이지 않을 만큼 높이 솟아 노래하고 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"월리, 우리 사랑하는 아이야,\" 노인이 대꾸했다. \"나도 최선을 다하마. 최선을 다하마.\"\n\n\"그리고 삼촌의 최선은,\" 월터가 그 유쾌한 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"제가 아는 최선 중에서도 으뜸가는 최선이지요. 제게 뭘 부쳐 주실지 잊지 않으실 거죠, 삼촌?\"\n\n\"그럼, 월리, 잊지 않으마.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"돔비 아가씨에 대해 듣는 건 뭐든지, 이제 그 가엾은 어린양이 홀로 남겨졌으니, 편지로 써 보내마. 하지만 그리 많지는 않을까 걱정이구나, 월리.\"\n\n\"실은요, 제가 말씀드릴 게 있어요, 삼촌,\" 월터가 잠시 머뭇거리다가 말했다. \"방금 거기 다녀왔거든요.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 이런?\" 노인이 눈썹을, 그리고 그와 함께 안경을 치켜올리며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아가씨를 보러 간 건 아니에요,\" 월터가 말했다. \"물론, 청을 넣었다면 뵐 수도 있었겠지요, 돔비 씨가 시내에 안 계셨으니까요. 그저 수전에게 작별 인사를 하려고요. 형편이 형편이니만큼, 또 제가 돔비 아가씨를 마지막으로 뵈었던 때를 떠올려 보면, 그 정도는 해도 괜찮겠다 싶었거든요.\"\n\n\"그래, 우리 아이야, 그래.\" 노인이 잠시의 멍한 상태에서 정신을 차리며 대답했다.\n\n\"그래서 그녀를 만났어요,\" 월터가 이어 갔다. \"수전 말이에요. 그리고 제가 내일 떠나 멀리 간다고 말했죠. 그리고 삼촌, 제가 이렇게 말했어요. 삼촌은 그날 밤 아가씨가 여기 오신 이래로 늘 돔비 아가씨에게 관심을 두셨고, 늘 잘되고 행복하시기를 바라셨고, 아무리 사소한 일이라도 아가씨를 위해 봉사할 수 있다면 늘 자랑스럽고 기쁘게 여기실 거라고요. 형편이 형편이니, 그 정도는 말해도 되겠다 싶었거든요. 안 그래요, 삼촌? 삼촌도 그렇게 생각하시죠?\"\n\n\"그럼, 우리 아이야, 그래.\" 노인이 아까와 같은 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 제가 이렇게 덧붙였어요,\" 월터가 이어 갔다. \"만약 그녀가—수전 말이에요—혹시라도, 자기를 통해서든, 리처즈 부인을 통해서든, 아니면 이쪽으로 올 만한 누구를 통해서든, 돔비 아가씨가 잘 계시고 행복하시다는 걸 삼촌께 알려 줄 수 있다면, 삼촌이 무척 고맙게 여기시고 제게 그 소식을 편지로 적어 보내 주실 거고, 저 역시 무척 고맙게 여길 거라고요. 자! 정말이지, 삼촌,\" 월터가 말했다. \"어젯밤 내내 이 일을 할까 생각하느라 잠을 거의 못 잤어요. 그러고도 밖에 나가서까지 할지 말지 마음을 정하지 못했죠. 하지만 이게 제 마음에서 우러난 진심임은 분명하고, 만일 그 마음을 풀어 놓지 않았더라면 두고두고 정말 비참했을 거예요.\"\n\n그의 정직한 목소리와 태도가 그가 한 말을 뒷받침해 주었고, 그 진솔함을 온전히 입증해 주었다.\n\n\"그러니, 삼촌, 혹시라도 그녀를 보시거든,\" 월터가 말했다. \"이제는 돔비 아가씨 말이에요—그리고 어쩌면 보시게 될지도 모르죠, 누가 알아요!—제가 아가씨를 얼마나 위했는지, 여기 있을 때 아가씨 생각을 얼마나 자주 했는지, 떠나기 전 이 마지막 밤에 눈에 눈물이 고인 채 아가씨 이야기를 했다고 전해 주세요, 삼촌. 제가 이렇게 말했다고 전해 주세요. 그 부드러운 태도며, 그 아름다운 얼굴이며, 무엇보다 가장 좋았던 그 다정하고 친절한 성품을 결코 잊을 수 없으리라고요. 그리고 제가 그것들을 어떤 여인의 발이나 어떤 아가씨의 발에서 가져온 게 아니라, 그저 천진한 어린아이의 발에서 가져온 거니까요,\" 월터가 말했다. \"삼촌, 괜찮으시다면, 제가 그 신발을 간직했다고 전해 주세요—그날 밤 그게 얼마나 자주 벗겨졌는지 아가씨도 기억하실 거예요—그래서 기념으로 가져갔다고요!\"\n\n바로 그 순간 그 신발은 월터의 짐가방 하나에 담겨 문밖으로 나가고 있었다. 손수레에 그의 짐을 싣고 부두로 가서 '아들이자 후계자호'에 실으려던 짐꾼이 그 가방을 차지하여, 무심한 항해사의 바로 코앞에서 굴려 가 버렸으니, 그 임자가 미처 말을 다 끝내기도 전이었다.\n\n그러나 그 보물이 굴러가는 동안 그 옛 뱃사람이 보인 무심함은 너그러이 봐줄 만했다. 왜냐하면 바로 그 순간 그의 눈 아래, 정확히 그의 관측 범위 안에, 깜짝 놀라 한껏 눈을 부릅뜬 그의 망보는 시야 한복판으로, 플로렌스와 수전 니퍼가 들어왔기 때문이다. 플로렌스는 반쯤 수줍어하며 그의 얼굴을 올려다보고 있었고, 그 나무 인형의 곁눈질 세례를 고스란히 받고 있었다!\n\n그뿐만 아니라, 두 사람은 항해사 말고는 아무에게도 들키지 않은 채 가게로 들어와 응접실 문까지 들어섰다. 그리고 문을 등지고 있던 월터는, 삼촌이 자기 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 다른 의자에 걸려 넘어질 뻔하는 것을 보지 못했더라면, 그때조차 두 사람이 나타난 것을 전혀 몰랐을 터였다.\n\n\"아니, 삼촌!\" 월터가 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이세요?\"\n\n솔로몬 노인이 대답했다. \"돔비 아가씨!\"\n\n\"그럴 리가?\" 월터가 외치며 돌아보고 자기도 벌떡 일어섰다. \"여기에!\"\n\n아니, 그것은 너무도 그럴 법하고 너무도 실제적인 일이어서, 그 말이 그의 입술에 채 머물러 있는 동안 플로렌스는 그의 곁을 지나쳐 가더니, 솔 삼촌의 코담배색 옷깃을 양손에 하나씩 잡고, 그의 뺨에 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 돌아서서, 이 세상에 오직 그녀만의 것이요 다른 누구의 것도 아닌 그 소박한 진실함과 진지함으로, 월터에게 손을 내밀었다!\n\n\"떠나시나요, 월터?\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 돔비 아가씨,\" 그가 대답했으나, 애써 보이려던 만큼 희망에 차 있지는 못했다. \"제 앞에 항해가 놓여 있어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 삼촌께서는,\" 플로렌스가 솔로몬을 돌아보며 말했다. \"당신이 떠나시는 게 분명 섭섭하시겠지요. 아! 정말 그러신 게 보여요! 사랑하는 월터, 저도 무척 섭섭해요.\"\n\n\"맙소사,\" 니퍼 양이 외쳤다. \"숫자가 문제라면 우리가 대신 보내 버려도 될 사람들이 수두룩한걸요, 핍친 부인은 감독으로 쳐서 제 몸무게만큼 금을 줘도 싼 편이고요, 흑인 노예 부리는 지식이 필요하다면 그 블림버네 사람들이야말로 그 자리에 딱 맞는 사람들이지요.\"\n\n그렇게 말하고 니퍼 양은 보닛 끈을 풀었다. 그러고는 탁자 위에 여느 때처럼 소박하게 차려진 차림 가운데 놓인 자그마한 검은 찻주전자를 잠시 멍하니 들여다보더니, 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 양철통도 흔들면서, 청하지도 않았는데 차를 우리기 시작했다.\n\n그동안 플로렌스는 다시 기계 제작자에게로 돌아섰는데, 그는 놀라움만큼이나 감탄으로 가득 차 있었다. \"이렇게 자라셨다니!\" 솔 노인이 말했다. \"이렇게 좋아지셨다니! 그런데도 변치 않으셨어! 꼭 그대로야!\"\n\n\"정말요?\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"그—그래요,\" 솔 노인이 천천히 두 손을 비비며, 자기를 바라보는 그 빛나는 눈 속의 어딘가 수심 어린 기색에 주의가 끌려, 반쯤 소리 내어 그 문제를 곱씹으며 대답했다. \"그래, 그 표정은 그 어린 얼굴에도 있었지!\"\n\n\"절 기억하시는군요,\" 플로렌스가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그리고 그때 제가 얼마나 조그만 아이였는지도요?\"\n\n\"아가씨,\" 기계 제작자가 대답했다. \"어떻게 제가 아가씨를 잊겠습니까, 그 뒤로 그토록 자주 아가씨를 생각하고 또 소식을 들어 왔는데요! 정말이지 아가씨가 들어오시던 바로 그 순간에도, 월리는 제게 아가씨 이야기를 하면서, 아가씨께 전할 말을 남기고 있던 참이었어요, 그리고—\"\n\n\"그랬어요?\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"고마워요, 월터! 오, 고마워요, 월터! 저는 당신이 떠나면서 절 거의 생각하지 않으실까 봐 걱정했거든요.\" 그러고는 다시 그 작은 손을 어찌나 거리낌 없이, 어찌나 신실하게 내밀었던지, 월터는 그 손을 잠시 제 손 안에 쥔 채 차마 놓을 수가 없었다.\n\n그러나 월터는 한때 잡았을 법한 그런 식으로 그 손을 잡은 것이 아니었고, 그 손길도 소년 시절의 그 옛 백일몽들을 일깨우지는 않았다. 그 꿈들은 근래에도 이따금 그의 곁을 떠다니며, 흐릿하고 부서진 형상으로 그를 어지럽히곤 했던 것이다. 그녀의 사랑스러운 태도가 지닌 순수함과 천진함, 그 완벽한 신뢰, 그리고 그녀의 한결같은 눈동자 속에 그토록 깊이 자리 잡고서 미소를 통해 그 고운 얼굴 위로 빛나던—아아! 그 미소는 얼굴을 환하게 밝히기에는 너무 슬픈 미소였으니—그를 향한 꾸밈없는 정, 이런 것들은 그 낭만적인 부류에 속하지 않았다. 그것들은 그녀가 보살피던 그 어린 임종의 자리, 그리고 그 아이가 그녀에게 품었던 사랑을 그의 생각 속으로 되불러 왔다. 그리고 그런 회상의 날개 위에서, 그녀는 그의 부질없는 공상들을 까마득히 넘어, 더 맑고 더 고요한 대기 속으로 떠오르는 듯했다.\n\n\"제가—제가 어르신을 월터의 삼촌이라고 불러야 할까 봐요,\" 플로렌스가 노인에게 말했다. \"허락해 주신다면요.\"\n\n\"아가씨!\" 솔 노인이 외쳤다. \"허락이라뇨! 맙소사!\"\n\n\"우린 늘 어르신을 그 이름으로 알았고, 그렇게 이야기했거든요,\" 플로렌스가 둘레를 둘러보며, 가만히 한숨지으며 말했다. \"정겨운 옛 응접실이네요! 꼭 그대로예요! 제가 얼마나 잘 기억하고 있는지 몰라요!\"\n\n솔 노인은 먼저 그녀를, 다음엔 조카를 바라보았다. 그러고는 두 손을 비비고, 안경을 닦으며, 숨죽인 소리로 말했다. \"아! 세월, 세월, 세월이로다!\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 그동안 수전 니퍼는 찬장에서 여분의 찻잔과 받침 둘을 솜씨 좋게 꺼내 챙겨 두고는, 사색에 잠긴 표정으로 차가 우러나기를 기다렸다.\n\n\"월터의 삼촌께 말씀드리고 싶은 게 있어요,\" 플로렌스가 탁자 위에 놓인 노인의 손에 자기 손을 수줍게 얹어 그의 주의를 끌면서 말했다. \"제가 마음 쓰이는 일이에요. 어르신은 홀로 남으시게 될 텐데, 만약 허락해 주신다면—월터의 자리를 대신하려는 건 아니에요, 그건 제가 할 수 없는 일이니까요—그저 어르신의 참된 벗이 되어, 월터가 떠나 있는 동안 제가 할 수 있는 일이 있다면 어르신을 돕고 싶어요. 그러면 정말로 무척 감사하겠어요. 그래 주시겠어요? 그래도 될까요, 월터의 삼촌?\"\n\n기계 제작자는 아무 말 없이 그녀의 손을 자기 입술로 가져갔고, 수전 니퍼는 스스로 의장 자리로 정해 앉은 의자에 팔짱을 끼고 등을 기댄 채, 보닛 끈 한쪽 끝을 깨물며, 천창을 올려다보면서 가만히 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"제가 찾아뵙는 걸 허락해 주세요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"올 수 있을 때요. 그리고 어르신 자신과 월터에 관한 모든 걸 제게 말씀해 주세요. 그리고 수전이 오고 제가 못 올 때에도 그 애에게 숨기는 일 없이, 우리에게 털어놓으시고, 우리를 믿으시고, 우리에게 기대 주세요. 그리고 우리가 어르신께 위안이 되게 해 주시겠어요? 그래 주시겠어요, 월터의 삼촌?\"\n\n자기 얼굴을 들여다보는 그 사랑스러운 얼굴, 그 부드럽게 간청하는 눈빛, 그 다정한 목소리, 그리고 그의 나이에 대한 한 아이의 존경과 경의가 더해져 더욱 마음을 사로잡는 그 가벼운 팔 위의 손길—그 존경과 경의가 모든 것에 우아한 망설임과 겸손한 머뭇거림의 분위기를 입혔다—이런 것들이, 그리고 그녀의 타고난 진지함이, 가엾은 늙은 기계 제작자를 어찌나 압도했던지, 그는 그저 이렇게 대답할 뿐이었다.\n\n\"월리! 나 대신 한마디 해 다오, 얘야. 정말 고맙구나.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 월터,\" 플로렌스가 그 잔잔한 미소를 띠며 대꾸했다. \"이분을 위해 아무 말도 하지 말아 주세요. 저는 이분을 아주 잘 이해해요. 그리고 우린 당신 없이 함께 이야기하는 법을 익혀야 해요, 사랑하는 월터.\"\n\n이 마지막 말을 하던 그 아쉬워하는 어조가, 다른 무엇보다도 월터의 마음을 더 깊이 울렸다.\n\n\"플로렌스 아가씨,\" 그는 삼촌과 이야기하는 동안 지켜 왔던 그 명랑한 태도를 되찾으려 애쓰며 대답했다. \"이런 친절에 대해 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지, 저도 삼촌만큼이나 알지 못하겠어요, 정말로요. 하지만 한 시간을 떠들 수 있다 한들, 결국 무슨 말을 더 할 수 있겠어요, 그게 꼭 아가씨답다는 말 말고는요?\"\n\n수전 니퍼는 보닛 끈의 새로운 부분을 깨물기 시작하더니, 거기 표현된 그 감정에 동의한다는 듯 천창을 향해 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"오! 하지만, 월터,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"당신이 떠나시기 전에 제가 하고 싶은 말이 있어요. 그리고 부디 절 플로렌스라고 불러 주세요, 낯선 사람처럼 말씀하지 마시고요.\"\n\n\"낯선 사람처럼이라뇨!\" 월터가 대꾸했다. \"안 돼요. 그렇게는 말할 수 없어요. 적어도, 그렇게 느낄 수는 없을 거예요, 분명히요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 그걸로는 충분치 않고, 제가 말하려던 것도 그게 아니에요. 그러니까, 월터,\" 플로렌스가 울음을 터뜨리며 덧붙였다. \"그 애는 당신을 무척 좋아했고, 죽기 전에 당신을 마음에 두었다고 했어요. 그리고 '월터를 기억해!'라고 했어요. 그러니 만약 당신이 제게 오라버니가 되어 주신다면, 월터, 이제 그 애가 떠나고 이 세상에 제겐 형제가 하나도 없으니, 저는 평생 당신의 누이가 되어, 우리가 어디에 있든 당신을 형제처럼 생각할 거예요! 이게 제가 하고 싶었던 말이에요, 사랑하는 월터. 하지만 제 바람처럼 말할 수가 없네요, 가슴이 벅차서요.\"\n\n그리고 그 벅차오름과 그 달콤한 소박함 속에서, 그녀는 두 손을 그에게 내밀었다. 월터는 그 손을 잡고, 몸을 굽혀, 눈물 어린 그 얼굴에 입을 맞추었다. 그가 그러는 동안에도 그 얼굴은 움츠러들지도, 돌아서지도, 붉어지지도 않고, 신뢰와 진실을 담아 그를 올려다보았다. 그 한순간에, 의심이나 동요의 그림자란 그림자는 모조리 월터의 영혼에서 사라졌다. 그에게는 마치 자기가 그 죽은 아이의 침상 곁에서 그녀의 천진한 호소에 응답하는 것만 같았다. 그리고 거기서 보았던 그 엄숙한 임재 앞에서, 자기는 유배지에서도 형제의 정으로 그녀의 그 모습을 소중히 여기고 지켜 주리라, 그녀의 소박한 믿음을 더럽힘 없이 고이 간직하리라, 그리고 그녀가 그것을 자기에게 맡길 때 그녀 자신의 가슴속에 없던 어떤 생각이라도 거기 불어넣는다면 스스로를 천하게 여기리라, 이렇게 다짐했다.\n\n이 일이 오가는 동안 보닛 끈 두 가닥을 한꺼번에 깨물고 천창을 향해 남몰래 잔뜩 감정을 쏟아 내던 수전 니퍼는, 이제 누가 우유를 넣고 누가 설탕을 넣는지 물으며 화제를 돌렸다. 그리고 그 점들에 대해 답을 듣고는, 차를 따랐다. 네 사람은 작은 탁자 둘레에 정답게 모여, 그 아가씨의 활발한 주관 아래 차를 마셨다. 그리고 뒷방에 깃든 플로렌스의 존재가, 벽에 걸린 타타르 전함 그림마저 환하게 밝혀 주었다.\n\n반 시간 전이라면 월터는 죽었다 깨어나도 그녀를 이름으로 부르지 못했으리라. 그러나 이제는 그녀가 청하니 그럴 수 있었다. 그녀가 차라리 오지 않는 편이 나았으리라는 은근한 불안 없이, 그녀가 거기 있다는 사실을 생각할 수 있었다. 그녀가 얼마나 아름다운지, 얼마나 가능성으로 가득한지, 언젠가 어떤 행복한 사내가 그런 마음속에서 어떤 안식처를 찾게 될지를 차분히 생각할 수 있었다. 그 마음속 자기의 자리를 자랑스럽게 헤아릴 수 있었다. 그리고 비록 그럴 자격이 있다고는—그건 여전히 자기로서는 까마득히 높은 일이라 여겼으니—여기지 못할지언정, 적어도 그 자격을 더 잃지는 않으리라는 용감한 결심으로써.\n\n수전 니퍼가 차를 우릴 때 그녀의 손 둘레에 분명 어떤 요정의 기운이 맴돌았으리라, 그것을 마시며 이야기 나누는 동안 뒷방을 다스리던 그 평온한 공기를 자아낸 그 기운이. 솔 삼촌의 정밀 시계 바늘 둘레에도 분명 어떤 반대되는 기운이 맴돌며, 그 바늘들을 타타르 전함이 바람을 받아 나아간 그 어느 때보다도 빠르게 움직였으리라. 어찌 되었든, 방문객들은 멀지 않은 한적한 모퉁이에 마차 한 대를 대기시켜 두고 있었다. 그리고 그 정밀 시계는, 우연히 한번 물어보자, 마차가 오래 기다리고 있노라고 어찌나 단호한 의견을 내놓던지, 그 사실을 의심하기란 불가능했으니, 그토록 흠잡을 데 없는 권위에서 나온 말이고 보면 더욱 그러했다. 만약 솔 삼촌이 자기 시계 시각에 맞추어 교수형에 처해질 처지였다 한들, 그는 그 정밀 시계가 단 한순간이라도 빠르다고는 결코 인정하지 않았을 것이다.\n\n플로렌스는 작별하면서 노인에게 자기가 앞서 한 말을 모두 되풀이하며, 그에게 그들의 약조를 다짐받았다. 솔 삼촌은 다정하게 그녀를 나무 항해사의 다리께까지 배웅하고, 거기서 그녀를 월터에게 넘겨주었다. 월터는 그녀와 수전 니퍼를 마차까지 바래다줄 채비가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"월터,\" 가는 길에 플로렌스가 말했다. \"삼촌 앞에서는 차마 묻기가 두려웠어요. 아주 오래 떠나 계실 것 같으세요?\"\n\n\"실은,\" 월터가 말했다. \"모르겠어요. 그럴까 봐 걱정이에요. 돔비 씨가 절 임명하실 때 그런 뜻을 내비치신 것 같았거든요.\"\n\n\"그게 은혜로운 일인가요, 월터?\" 플로렌스가 잠시 머뭇거리다가, 그의 얼굴을 근심스레 들여다보며 물었다.\n\n\"그 임명이요?\" 월터가 되물었다.\n\n\"네.\"\n\n월터는 그렇다고 대답할 수만 있다면 무엇이든 내주었으련만, 그의 입술이 미처 답하기도 전에 그의 얼굴이 먼저 답해 버렸고, 플로렌스는 너무도 주의 깊게 그것을 보고 있었던지라 그 답을 이해하지 못할 리 없었다.\n\n\"아빠한테 별로 사랑받지 못하셨나 봐요,\" 그녀가 수줍게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 사랑받을,\" 월터가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"까닭이 없는걸요.\"\n\n\"까닭이 없다뇨, 월터!\"\n\n\"까닭이 없었지요,\" 월터가 그녀의 말뜻을 알아채고 말했다. \"회사에는 많은 사람이 고용되어 있어요. 돔비 씨와 저 같은 젊은이 사이에는 까마득한 거리가 있고요. 제가 제 의무를 다한다면, 마땅히 할 일을 하는 것일 뿐, 나머지 모든 사람보다 더 하는 건 아니에요.\"\n\n플로렌스에게는 자기도 거의 의식하지 못하는 어떤 불안이 있었던 걸까. 근래 그녀가 아버지의 방으로 내려갔던 그날 밤 이후로, 흐릿하고 정의할 수 없는 형태로 싹터 버린 어떤 불안이—월터가 우연히 자기에게 보인 관심과, 자기에 대한 이른 시절의 인연이, 혹 그를 그 강력한 노여움과 미움 속으로 끌어들인 게 아닐까 하는 불안이 말이다. 월터에게도 그런 생각이, 혹은 그것이 바로 그 순간 그녀의 마음속에 있다는 어떤 불현듯한 짐작이 있었던 걸까? 둘 중 누구도 그것을 입에 올리지 않았다. 둘 다 잠시 동안 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 월터의 반대편에서 걷던 수전은 두 사람을 날카롭게 살폈다. 그리고 분명 니퍼 양의 생각은 그쪽으로, 그것도 자못 확신을 가지고 흘러가고 있었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 아주 곧 돌아오실지도 몰라요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"그럴지도요, 월터.\"\n\n\"제가 돌아올 때면,\" 월터가 말했다. \"어쩌면 노인이 되어 있고, 아가씨도 할머니가 되어 계실지 모르죠. 하지만 저는 더 나은 일들을 바라요.\"\n\n\"아빠도,\" 플로렌스가 잠시 후에 말했다. \"언젠가는—슬픔에서 회복하시고—제게 좀 더 마음을 터놓고 말씀해 주실지도 몰라요. 그렇게 되면, 제가 당신이 다시 돌아오기를 얼마나 바라는지 말씀드리고, 저를 봐서라도 당신을 다시 불러 달라고 청할게요.\"\n\n아버지에 관한 이 말들 속에는 가슴 저미는 떨림이 깃들어 있었으니, 월터는 그것을 너무도 잘 알아들었다.\n\n마차가 바로 곁에 있어서, 그는 아무 말 없이 그녀와 헤어지려 했다. 이제야 이별이 무엇인지 절감했기 때문이다. 그러나 플로렌스가 자리에 앉으며 그의 손을 잡았고, 그제야 그는 그녀의 손안에 작은 꾸러미가 하나 있음을 알았다.\n\n\"월터,\" 그녀가 그 애정 어린 눈으로 그를 그득히 바라보며 말했다. \"당신처럼, 저도 더 나은 일들을 바라요. 그것들을 위해 기도할 거고, 그것들이 찾아오리라 믿을 거예요. 이 작은 선물은 폴을 위해 만든 거예요. 부디 제 사랑과 함께 받아 주세요, 그리고 떠나기 전까지는 보지 말아 주세요. 그럼, 신의 가호가 함께하기를, 월터! 절 절대 잊지 마세요. 당신은 제 오라버니예요, 그래요!\"\n\n그는 수전 니퍼가 둘 사이에 끼어들어 주어 다행이라 여겼다. 안 그랬더라면 그녀에게 자기에 대한 슬픈 기억을 남겼을지도 모를 일이었다. 그녀가 마차 밖으로 다시 내다보지 않고, 대신 보이지 않을 때까지 그 작은 손을 흔들어 준 것 또한 다행이라 여겼다.\n\n그녀의 부탁에도 불구하고, 그는 그날 밤 잠자리에 들 때 그 꾸러미를 열어 보지 않을 수 없었다. 그것은 작은 지갑이었고, 그 안에는 돈이 들어 있었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 해는 낯선 나라들에 가 있던 자리에서 환히 떠올랐고, 월터도 그와 함께 일어나 선장을 맞았다. 선장은 이미 문 앞에 와 있었으니, 맥스팅거 부인이 아직 곤히 잠들어 있는 동안 출항하려고 필요 이상으로 일찍 나선 것이었다. 선장은 더없이 들뜬 기분인 척하며, 아침거리로 넓은 푸른 외투 주머니 하나에 잔뜩 훈제한 우설을 넣어 가져왔다.\n\n\"그런데, 월—러,\" 선장이 식탁에 앉으며 말했다. \"자네 삼촌이 내가 생각하는 그런 사람이라면, 지금 이런 때 마지막 마데이라 한 병을 꺼내 오실 거야.\"\n\n\"아니, 안 되네, 네드,\" 노인이 대꾸했다. \"안 돼! 그건 월터가 다시 집에 돌아왔을 때 열 거야.\"\n\n\"옳거니!\" 선장이 외쳤다. \"들어 보게나!\"\n\n\"그건 저기 있다네,\" 솔 길스가 말했다. \"저 작은 지하실에, 먼지와 거미줄을 뒤집어쓴 채로. 어쩌면 그게 빛을 보기 전에, 자네와 나 위에 먼지와 거미줄이 덮일지도 모르지, 네드.\"\n\n\"들어 보게나!\" 선장이 외쳤다. \"훌륭한 교훈일세! 월—러, 이 사람아. 무화과나무를 마땅히 갈 길로 가게 기르라, 그러면 늙어서 그 그늘 아래 앉으리라. 펼쳐 보게나, 그—그런데,\" 선장이 다시 생각하고는 말했다. \"그게 어디 나오는 말인지는 도통 모르겠지만, 찾거든 적어 두게나. 솔 길스, 다시 앞으로 나아가게!\"\n\n\"하지만 거기든 어디든, 월리가 돌아와 그걸 찾을 때까지 놔둘 걸세, 네드,\" 노인이 말했다. \"내가 하려던 말은 그게 다라네.\"\n\n\"그것도 잘한 말일세,\" 선장이 대꾸했다. \"그리고 만약 우리 셋이 함께 그 병을 따지 못한다면, 자네 둘이서 따도 좋다고 내가 허락하지.\"\n\n선장의 지나친 쾌활함에도 불구하고, 그는 그 훈제한 우설을 변변히 먹어 내지 못했다. 누가 그를 볼 때면 어마어마한 식욕으로 먹고 있는 양 보이려고 무던히 애를 썼지만 말이다. 또한 그는 삼촌이든 조카든 어느 한쪽과 단둘이 남겨지는 것을 끔찍이도 두려워했다. 마치 겉모습을 지키는 데 있어 자기의 유일한 안전책이란 늘 셋이 함께 있는 것뿐이라고 여기는 듯했다. 선장 쪽의 이 공포가 그를 그런 기발한 회피책들로 몰아넣었으니, 솔로몬이 외투를 입으러 갈 때면 굉장한 삯마차가 지나가는 걸 봤다는 핑계로 문께로 달려가고, 월터가 하숙인들에게 작별 인사를 하러 위층으로 올라갈 때면 이웃 굴뚝에서 불 냄새가 난다는 시늉으로 길로 뛰쳐나가는 식이었다. 커틀 선장은 이런 술책들을 영감을 받지 못한 어떤 관찰자도 꿰뚫어 볼 수 없으리라 여겼다.\n\n월터가 위층으로 작별 인사를 다녀와 내려오는 길에, 작은 응접실로 돌아가려고 가게를 가로지르고 있을 때, 그는 자기가 아는 한 빛바랜 얼굴이 문 안을 들여다보고 있는 것을 보고는 그쪽으로 달려갔다.\n\n\"카커 씨!\" 월터가 동생 존 카커의 손을 꼭 잡으며 외쳤다. \"부디 들어오세요! 이렇게 일찍 절 배웅하러 와 주시다니 친절하시네요. 떠나기 전에 한 번 당신과 악수할 수 있다면 제가 얼마나 기쁠지 아셨던 거죠. 이런 기회를 얻게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지 말로 다 못 하겠어요. 부디 들어오세요.\"\n\n\"우리가 다시 만날 일은 아마 없겠지요, 월터,\" 상대가 그의 청을 부드럽게 뿌리치며 대답했다. \"그래서 나도 이 기회가 기쁘답니다. 헤어지는 마당이니, 자네에게 말을 건네고, 자네 손을 잡아도 되겠지요. 이젠 자네의 그 솔직한 다가옴을 더는 뿌리치지 않아도 되니까요, 월터.\"\n\n그가 그렇게 말할 때 그의 미소에는 어떤 우수가 어려 있었으니, 그것은 그가 바로 그 안에서조차 자기 생각의 길동무와 우정을 얼마간 찾아냈음을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"아, 카커 씨!\" 월터가 대꾸했다. \"왜 그걸 뿌리치셨어요? 제겐 좋은 일만 해 주실 수 있었을 텐데요, 정말 그렇게 믿어요.\"\n\n그는 고개를 저었다. \"만약 이 세상에서 내가 할 수 있는,\" 그가 말했다. \"어떤 선한 일이 있다면, 월터, 자네를 위해 그렇게 했을 거요. 날마다 자네를 보는 것이 내겐 행복인 동시에 회한이었지요. 하지만 그 기쁨이 그 고통보다 무거웠어요. 이제 내가 무엇을 잃는지 알게 되니, 그걸 알겠소.\"\n\n\"들어오세요, 카커 씨, 그리고 제 착하고 늙으신 삼촌과 인사 나누세요,\" 월터가 권했다. \"제가 삼촌께 당신 이야기를 자주 했거든요. 삼촌도 제게서 듣는 소식을 모두 당신께 기꺼이 전해 주실 거예요. 저는,\" 월터가 그의 망설임을 알아채고는, 자기도 당혹스러워하며 말했다. \"삼촌께 우리의 그 마지막 대화에 대해선 아무것도 말하지 않았어요, 카커 씨. 삼촌께조차 말이지요, 믿어 주세요.\"\n\n머리 센 동생은 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었고, 두 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다.\n\n\"내가 만약 언젠가 그분과 인사를 나눈다면, 월터,\" 그가 대답했다. \"그건 자네 소식을 듣기 위해서일 거요. 자네의 그 너그러움과 배려를 저버리지 않으리라 믿어 주오. 그분께 한마디 속내를 청하기 전에 진실을 모두 말씀드리지 않는다면, 그거야말로 그 너그러움을 저버리는 일이 될 테니까요. 하지만 내겐 자네 말고는 벗도 아는 이도 없고, 자네를 봐서라도 누굴 새로 사귈 일은 거의 없을 거요.\"\n\n\"저는,\" 월터가 말했다. \"당신이 절 진정한 벗으로 삼게 해 주셨더라면 했어요. 늘 그러기를 바랐죠, 카커 씨, 아시다시피요. 하지만 우리가 헤어지려는 지금만큼 간절히 바란 적은 없었어요.\"\n\n\"자네가 내 가슴속의 벗이었다는 것,\" 상대가 대답했다. \"그리고 내가 자네를 가장 피했을 때에도 내 마음은 가장 자네에게 기울었고, 자네로 가장 가득 차 있었다는 것, 그것으로 충분하다오. 월터, 잘 가게!\"\n\n\"안녕히 계세요, 카커 씨. 하늘이 당신과 함께하기를, 어르신!\" 월터가 북받쳐 외쳤다.\n\n\"만약,\" 상대가 말하면서 그의 손을 놓지 않고 붙든 채 말했다. \"만약 자네가 돌아왔을 때, 내 그 옛 구석자리에 내가 보이지 않거든, 그리고 누군가에게서 내가 어디 누워 있는지 듣게 되거든, 와서 내 무덤을 한번 들여다봐 주오. 나도 자네처럼 정직하고 행복할 수 있었으리라 생각해 주오! 그리고 내 때가 다가오고 있음을 알게 될 때, 예전의 나 같은 누군가가 거기 잠시 서서, 연민과 용서로 나를 기억해 주리라고, 그렇게 생각하게 해 주오! 월터, 잘 가게!\"\n\n그의 모습은 이른 여름 아침에 그토록 명랑하면서도 그토록 엄숙한, 환한 햇살이 비치는 거리를 따라 그림자처럼 기어가더니, 천천히 사라져 갔다.\n\n마침내 그 매정한 정밀 시계가, 월터는 이제 나무 항해사에게 등을 돌려야 한다고 알려 주었다. 그리하여 그들은 떠났다. 그 자신과 삼촌과 선장이, 삯마차를 타고 어느 부두로 가서, 거기서 강을 따라 내려가 어느 후미진 물길로 가는 증기선을 탈 참이었다. 선장이 일러 준 그 물길의 이름은 뭍사람의 귀에는 도무지 알 길 없는 수수께끼였다. 그 물길에 다다르자(배는 간밤의 밀물을 타고 그리로 옮겨 와 있었다), 흥분한 뱃사공들이 여럿 다가와 그들 곁에 배를 댔는데, 그중에는 선장과 안면이 있는, 더러운 외눈박이 거인 같은 자도 있었다. 그는 외눈 하나로 한 마장 반쯤 떨어진 데서부터 선장을 알아보고는, 그때부터 줄곧 선장과 알아들을 수 없는 고함을 주고받고 있던 터였다. 무섭게 목이 쉬고 천성적으로 면도가 필요해 보이는 이 인물의 정당한 노획물이 되어, 세 사람은 모두 '아들이자 후계자호'에 올랐다. 그리고 '아들이자 후계자호'는 자못 어수선한 상태였으니, 젖은 갑판 위에 돛이란 돛은 죄다 너저분하게 늘어져 있고, 풀린 밧줄들이 사람들의 발을 걸어 넘어뜨리고, 붉은 셔츠를 입은 사내들이 맨발로 이리저리 뛰어다니고, 술통들이 한 발짝의 공간도 남김없이 가로막고 있었으며, 그 난장판의 한복판에서는 검은 취사실의 흑인 요리사가 채소에 눈까지 파묻힌 채 연기에 눈이 멀어 있었다.\n\n선장은 곧장 월터를 한쪽 구석으로 끌고 가더니, 얼굴이 새빨개지도록 안간힘을 써서, 어찌나 크고 주머니에 꼭 끼었던지 마개처럼 쑥 빠져나오는 그 은시계를 끄집어냈다.\n\n\"월—러,\" 선장이 그것을 건네며 그의 손을 진심으로 힘껏 흔들고 말했다. \"작별 선물일세, 이 사람아. 매일 아침마다 반 시간씩 늦춰 놓고, 오후께 가서 또 한 십오 분쯤 늦춰 놓으면, 자네에게 신용을 안겨 줄 시계라네.\"\n\n\"커틀 선장님! 그건 어림없어요!\" 월터가, 달아나려는 그를 붙들며 외쳤다. \"부디 도로 가져가세요. 저는 이미 하나 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 월—러,\" 선장이 느닷없이 주머니 하나로 손을 쑥 집어넣어, 바로 이런 반대에 맞서려고 무장해 둔 찻숟가락 둘과 각설탕 집게를 꺼내 들며 말했다. \"이 사소한 은붙이라도 대신 받게나.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니에요, 정말 못 받아요!\" 월터가 외쳤다. \"천 번 고마워요! 그걸 버리지는 마세요, 커틀 선장님!\"—선장이 그것들을 뱃전 너머로 휙 던지려던 참이었던 것이다.—\"그건 저보다 선장님께 훨씬 더 쓸모가 있을 거예요. 선장님 지팡이를 주세요. 그게 갖고 싶다고 자주 생각했거든요. 자! 안녕히 계세요, 커틀 선장님! 제 삼촌을 잘 보살펴 주세요! 솔 삼촌, 신의 가호가 함께하기를요!\"\n\n월터가 두 사람을 다시 한번 흘끗 보기도 전에, 그들은 어수선한 틈에 뱃전 너머로 내려가 버렸다. 그가 고물로 뛰어가 그들을 뒤따라 바라보았을 때, 삼촌은 작은 배 안에서 고개를 떨구고 있었고, 커틀 선장은 그 커다란 은시계로 삼촌의 등을 톡톡 두드리며(무척 아팠을 것이 분명하다), 찻숟가락과 각설탕 집게를 들고 희망에 차서 손짓을 하고 있었다. 월터가 눈에 들어오자, 커틀 선장은 그 물건들을 그것의 존재 따위는 까맣게 잊은 듯 더없이 무심하게 배 바닥에 떨어뜨리고는, 칠을 한 모자를 벗어 들고 우렁차게 그를 불렀다. 칠을 한 모자가 햇빛 속에서 번쩍번쩍 자못 볼만했고, 선장은 그가 더는 보이지 않을 때까지 계속 그것을 흔들어 댔다. 그러자 배 위의 그 소동은, 빠르게 더해 가더니, 절정에 이르렀다. 다른 작은 배 두세 척이 환호 소리와 함께 떠나갔다. 월터가 지켜보는 가운데 돛들은 그 면을 순풍에 펼치며 머리 위에서 환하고 가득하게 빛났다. 뱃머리에서는 물이 반짝이며 흩날렸다. 그리하여 '아들이자 후계자호'는 항해 길에 올랐으니, 그보다 앞서 침몰해 간 숱한 다른 아들이자 후계자들이 제 길을 떠났던 것처럼, 그토록 희망에 차고 그토록 경쾌하게.\n\n날마다, 솔 노인과 커틀 선장은 작은 뒷방에서 그 배의 위치를 계산하고, 둥근 탁자 위에 해도를 펼쳐 놓고 그 항로를 짚어 나갔다. 밤이면, 솔 노인이 그토록 쓸쓸하게 위층으로, 이따금 대포 소리처럼 바람이 휘몰아치는 다락방으로 올라가서는, 별들을 올려다보고 바람 소리에 귀를 기울이며, 배 위에서 자기 몫으로 떨어졌을 것보다 더 긴 망을 보곤 했다. 그동안, 순항하던 시절을 보내고 바다의 위험들을 겪어 본 그 마지막 오래된 마데이라 한 병은, 먼지와 거미줄 아래 말없이, 건드려지지 않은 채 누워 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XX. Mr Dombey goes upon a Journey\n\n“돔비 씨, 선생,\" 백스톡 소령이 말했다, \"이 '조이' B.는 본래 감상에 젖는 사내가 아니올시다. 조지프는 질긴 사람이거든요. 하지만 조에게도 감정이란 게 있지요, 선생, 그리고 그게 한번 깨어나면—제기랄, 돔비 씨,\" 소령은 별안간 사납게 외쳤다, \"이건 나약함이오, 나는 거기에 굴복하지 않겠소!\"\n\n백스톡 소령이 이런 말을 쏟아 낸 것은, 프린세스 플레이스에 있는 자기 집 계단 꼭대기에서 돔비 씨를 손님으로 맞이하던 참이었다. 돔비 씨는 여행길에 나서기에 앞서 소령과 아침을 함께 먹으러 온 터였다. 그리고 그 박복한 원주민은 머핀을 둘러싼 갖은 비참을 이미 겪은 뒤였고, 삶은 달걀이라는 전반적인 문제와 관련해서는 인생이 그에게 무거운 짐이 되어 있었다.\n\n\"백스톡 혈통의 늙은 군인이 할 짓은 아니지요,\" 소령은 다시 온화한 상태로 돌아오며 말했다, \"제 감정에 사로잡혀 먹잇감이 되어 버리는 일 말이오. 하지만—제기랄, 선생,\" 소령은 또 한 차례 사나운 발작을 일으키며 외쳤다, \"선생께 애도를 표하는 바요!\"\n\n소령의 자줏빛 얼굴은 한층 짙어졌고, 소령의 가재 같은 두 눈은 한결 또렷이 튀어나왔다. 그는 돔비 씨의 손을 잡고 흔들었는데, 그 평화로운 행위에 어찌나 도전적인 성격을 입혔던지, 마치 곧바로 돔비 씨와 한쪽에 천 파운드씩 걸고 영국 챔피언 자리를 두고 권투 시합이라도 벌이기 직전의 서막인 듯했다. 그러고는 머리를 빙빙 돌리고 말의 기침과 아주 흡사한 쌕쌕거리는 소리를 내면서, 소령은 손님을 거실로 안내했고, 거기서 (이제 감정을 가라앉힌 채) 여행 길동무다운 거리낌 없고 솔직한 태도로 그를 맞이했다.\n\n\"돔비,\" 소령이 말했다, \"당신을 만나 기쁘오. 당신을 만나 자랑스럽소. 유럽에 J. 백스톡이 이런 말을 해 줄 사내는 많지 않소—조시는 무뚝뚝한 사람이거든요. 선생, 그게 그의 천성이오—하지만 조이 B.는 당신을 만나 자랑스럽소, 돔비.\"\n\n\"소령,\" 돔비 씨가 응수했다, \"참으로 친절하시군요.\"\n\n\"천만에, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"조금도 그렇지 않소! 그건 내 성품이 아니오. 만약 그게 조의 성품이었다면, 조는 지금쯤 육군 중장 조지프 백스톡 경, 바스 훈장 수훈자가 되어 있었을 테고, 당신을 아주 다른 거처에서 맞이했을 게요. 보아하니 당신은 아직 늙은 조를 잘 모르는 것 같소. 하지만 이번 일은 특별하니, 내게 자랑거리가 되는 것이오. 맹세코, 선생,\" 소령이 단호하게 말했다, \"이건 내게 영광이오!\"\n\n돔비 씨는 자신과 자기 재산에 대한 평가에서, 이것이 매우 사실이라고 느꼈으므로 그 점을 따지고 들지 않았다. 그러나 소령이 그런 진실을 본능적으로 알아보고 솔직히 인정하는 것은 매우 능란한 일이었다. 그것은, 만약 돔비 씨에게 확증이 필요했다면, 자신이 소령을 잘못 보지 않았다는 확증이 되었다. 그것은 자기 권세가 자기 자신의 직접적인 영역 너머까지 미치고 있다는 보증이었고, 또 소령이, 장교이자 신사로서, 왕립 거래소의 수위 못지않게 그 권세를 마땅히 알아보는 감각을 지녔다는 보증이었다.\n\n이런 것, 혹은 이런 것과 비슷한 것을 안다는 게 언제고 위안이 된다면, 바로 그때야말로 위안이 되었다. 자기 의지의 무력함, 자기 희망의 불안정함, 부의 허약함이 그토록 처참하게 그의 가슴에 새겨진 그때였으니. 그게 무슨 소용이 있단 말이오, 하고 자기 아들이 그에게 물었다. 이따금, 그 아이의 그 질문을 떠올릴 때면, 그는 자기 자신도 묻지 않을 수 없었다. 정말이지 그게 무슨 소용이 있었던가. 그게 무엇을 해 주었던가.\n\n그러나 이것들은 고독한 생각이었다. 늦은 밤, 그의 칩거가 빚어내는 침울한 낙담과 어둠 속에서 자라난 생각이었고, 자존심은 소령의 그것만큼이나 흠잡을 데 없고 값진 여러 증언 속에서 손쉽게 다시금 안심을 찾아냈다. 돔비 씨는 벗이 없는 처지에서 소령에게 마음이 기울었다. 그를 향해 따뜻해졌다고는 할 수 없으나, 약간 누그러지기는 했다. 소령은 바닷가에서 보낸 그 나날에 얼마간—그러나 지나치지 않게—한몫을 한 사람이었다. 그는 세상 물정에 밝은 사내였고, 몇몇 대단한 사람들을 알고 있었다. 말이 많았고 이야기를 들려주었다. 그래서 돔비 씨는 그를, 사교계에서 빛을 발하는 선택받은 인물로, 게다가 그런 선택받은 인물들이 흔히 너무 많이 섞여 들이는 가난이라는 독성 성분을 지니지 않은 인물로 여기는 쪽으로 기울었다. 그의 지위는 부인할 수 없는 것이었다. 통틀어 보아 소령은 신뢰할 만한 동행이었다. 여가의 삶에, 그리고 그들이 막 찾아가려는 그런 장소들에 잘 익숙했고, 자신의 도시 상인다운 성품과 충분히 잘 어우러지면서도 그것과 전혀 경합하지 않는, 신사답고 편안한 분위기를 풍기고 있었다. 만약 돔비 씨에게, 소령이 자기 직업의 성질상 최근 자기 희망을 짓밟은 그 무자비한 손길을 가볍게 여기는 데 익숙한 사내인 만큼, 자기도 모르게 자기에게 어떤 쓸모 있는 철학을 전해 주고 자기의 나약한 회한을 쫓아 줄지도 모른다는 미련 같은 생각이 남아 있었다면, 그는 그것을 자기 자신에게마저 감추어 자존심의 밑바닥에 검토하지 않은 채 내버려 두었다.\n\n\"내 그 악당 놈은 어디 있느냐?\" 소령이 방 안을 노여운 눈으로 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n특별한 이름이 없는, 그러나 어떤 욕설이든 갖다 붙이면 거기에 대답하는 그 원주민이 곧장 문간에 나타났으나, 더 가까이는 감히 다가오지 못했다.\n\n\"이 악당아!\" 성마른 소령이 말했다, \"아침은 어디 있느냐?\"\n\n그 검은 하인은 그것을 찾으러 사라졌다가, 곧 계단을 다시 올라오는 소리가 들렸는데, 어찌나 부들부들 떨던지, 그가 들고 오는 쟁반 위의 접시며 그릇들이 그와 한마음으로 떨면서 올라오는 내내 덜그럭덜그럭 다시 부딪쳤다.\n\n\"돔비,\" 소령이 말했다. 그는 원주민이 식탁을 차리는 모습을 흘끗 보면서, 그가 숟가락을 떨어뜨리자 무시무시하게 주먹을 흔들어 그를 다그쳤다. \"여기 매콤한 석쇠구이며, 맛깔난 파이며, 콩팥 요리 한 접시며, 그런 것들이 있소. 자, 앉으시오. 보다시피 늙은 조는 야영지 음식밖에 차려 드릴 게 없소이다.\"\n\n\"아주 훌륭한 음식이오, 소령,\" 손님이 대답했다. 단지 예의로만 한 말은 아니었으니, 소령은 늘 자기 자신을 더없이 잘 돌보았고, 사실 기름진 고기를 몸에 좋을 정도 이상으로 먹어 댔던 까닭에, 그의 황제 같은 안색이 주로 그 사정 탓이라고 의학계에서들 보았던 것이다.\n\n\"건너편을 보고 오셨소, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다. \"우리 친구분을 보셨소?\"\n\n\"톡스 양 말씀이오,\" 돔비 씨가 받아쳤다. \"아니오.\"\n\n\"매혹적인 여인이지요, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다. 살진 웃음이 그의 짧은 목에서 차올라 그를 거의 질식시킬 지경이었다.\n\n\"톡스 양은 매우 좋은 부류의 사람인 것 같소,\" 돔비 씨가 대답했다.\n\n그 거만하고 냉랭한 대답이 백스톡 소령에게는 무한한 기쁨을 안겨 주는 듯했다. 그는 한껏, 자꾸자꾸 부풀어 올랐고, 심지어 잠시 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 두 손을 비비기까지 했다.\n\n\"늙은 조도, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"한때는 저쪽에서 제법 총애받는 사람이었소. 하지만 조의 시절은 갔지요. J. 백스톡은 꺼져 버렸소—밀려났소—나가떨어졌소, 선생.\"\n\n\"내 짐작에는,\" 돔비 씨가 대답했다, \"그 부인의 총애받을 시절이라는 것도 이미 끝났을 것 같소만. 어쩌면 농담을 하시는 거겠지요, 소령.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 당신이 농담을 하시는 거겠지요, 돔비?\" 소령이 응수했다.\n\n이보다 더 있을 법하지 않은 가능성은 없었다. 그것이 돔비 씨의 얼굴에 너무도 분명히 드러나 있었으므로, 소령이 사과를 했다.\n\n\"용서하시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신이 진심이라는 걸 알겠소. 내 한마디 하리다, 돔비.\" 소령은 먹던 것을 멈추고, 알 수 없게 분개하는 표정을 지었다. \"저건 지독히도 야심 많은 여자요, 선생.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 차가운 무관심으로 \"그렇소?\"라고 말했다. 거기에는 아마, 톡스 양이 그런 고상한 자질을 품을 만큼 주제넘은 사람이라는 데 대한 얼마간 경멸 어린 불신도 섞여 있었을 것이다.\n\n\"저 여자는, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"제 나름대로는 루시퍼요. 조이 B.의 시절은 갔소, 선생, 하지만 그는 눈만은 간직하고 있소. 조는 본단 말이오. 작고하신 요크 공작 전하께서도 알현식에서 조이를 두고, 조는 볼 줄 안다고 평하셨소.\"\n\n소령은 이 말과 함께 어찌나 대단한 눈빛을 보였던지, 그리고 먹고 마시고, 뜨거운 차에, 매콤한 석쇠구이에, 머핀에, 의미심장함까지 더해, 머리통이 온통 어찌나 부풀고 벌게졌던지, 돔비 씨조차 그를 좀 걱정하는 기색을 보였다.\n\n\"저 우스꽝스러운 늙은 구경거리는, 선생,\" 소령이 말을 이었다, \"야심을 품고 있소. 하늘 높은 줄 모르고 야심을 품고 있단 말이오, 선생. 혼인의 야심을 말이오, 돔비.\"\n\n\"그 여자가 안됐군요,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 말 마시오, 돔비,\" 소령이 경고하는 어조로 응수했다.\n\n\"어째서 그러면 안 되오, 소령?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n소령은 말의 기침 말고는 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 다시 기운차게 먹기 시작했다.\n\n\"그 여자는 당신네 가정에 관심을 가져 왔소,\" 소령이 다시 뚝 멈추며 말했다, \"그리고 이제 한동안 당신 댁에 자주 드나드는 손님이 되어 왔지요.\"\n\n\"그렇소,\" 돔비 씨가 대단히 위엄 있게 대답했다, \"톡스 양은 본래 돔비 부인이 세상을 떠났을 때, 내 누이의 벗으로서 그곳에 받아들여졌소. 그리고 행실이 바른 사람이고, 그 가엾은 갓난것을 좋아하는 기색을 보였기에, 누이와 더불어 방문을 거듭하는 것이—권장받았다고까지 말할 수 있겠소—허락되었고, 차츰 그 집안에서 일종의 친밀한 발판을 차지하게 되었소. 나는,\" 돔비 씨가, 크고 값진 양보를 베푸는 사람의 어조로 말했다, \"나는 톡스 양을 존중하오. 그 여자는 친절하게도 우리 집에서 자잘한 봉사를 많이 베풀어 주었소. 어쩌면 사소하고 하찮은 봉사일지 모르겠소, 소령, 그러나 그렇다고 해서 깎아내릴 것은 아니지요. 그리고 나는 다행히도 내가 베풀 수 있는 만큼의 배려와 관심으로 그것을 인정해 줄 수 있었기를 바라오. 나는 톡스 양에게 빚을 졌다고 생각하오, 소령,\" 돔비 씨가 손을 가볍게 흔들며 덧붙였다, \"당신과 알게 된 즐거움에 대해서 말이오.\"\n\n\"돔비,\" 소령이 열을 띠며 말했다, \"안 되오! 안 되오, 선생! 조지프 백스톡은 그런 주장이 반박 없이 지나가도록 결코 두고 볼 수 없소. 당신이 늙은 조를, 비록 보잘것없는 사람이나마, 알게 된 것과, 늙은 조가 당신을 알게 된 것은, 선생, 한 고귀한 친구에게서 비롯된 것이오—한 위대한 존재에게서 말이오, 선생. 돔비!\" 소령이 말했다. 그가 그토록 분투하는 시늉을 내기란 그리 어렵지 않았으니, 그의 평생이 온갖 졸중 증세에 맞선 분투였던 까닭이다. \"우리는 당신 아드님을 통해 서로 알게 된 것이오.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 이 언급에 마음이 움직인 듯했고, 소령이 그렇게 만들 작정이었던 것도 아마 사실이었을 것이다. 그는 고개를 떨구고 한숨을 쉬었다. 그러자 소령은 사납게 정신을 가다듬고는, 자기가 빠져들 위험을 느끼는 그 심정 상태를 두고, 이것은 나약함이며 무엇으로도 자기를 거기에 굴복시킬 수 없다고 다시 한번 말했다.\n\n\"우리 친구분은 그 일과 먼 인연이 있었던 거요,\" 소령이 말했다, \"그러니 그 여자에게 돌아갈 공로라면, J. B.는 기꺼이 그 여자에게 돌려줄 용의가 있소, 선생. 그렇긴 하나, 마님,\" 그는 접시에서 눈을 들어, 프린세스 플레이스 건너편으로, 마침 그 순간 창가에서 꽃에 물을 주는 톡스 양이 보이는 곳으로 눈길을 던지며 덧붙였다, \"당신은 음흉한 여우요, 마님, 그리고 당신의 야심이란 가공할 만한 뻔뻔함의 표본이오. 그게 당신 자신만 우스꽝스럽게 만든다면, 마님,\" 소령은 자기를 알 리 없는 톡스 양을 향해 머리를 굴리며 말했고, 그동안 그의 튀어나온 두 눈은 그녀를 향해 뛰어오르는 듯했다, \"당신은 마음껏 그래도 좋소, 마님, 백스톡 쪽에서는 추호도 이의가 없으리라 장담하오.\" 여기서 소령은 귀 끝과 머릿속 혈관까지 동원해 무시무시하게 웃어 댔다. \"하지만 마님,\" 소령이 말했다, \"당신이 다른 사람들을, 그것도 너그럽고 의심할 줄 모르는 사람들을, 그들의 호의에 대한 보답이랍시고 위태롭게 만든다면, 그건 늙은 조의 몸속 피를 끓게 하는 거요.\"\n\n\"소령,\" 돔비 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다, \"톡스 양 쪽에서 무슨 그토록 터무니없는 것을 암시하시는 건 아니겠지요, 이를테면—\"\n\n\"돔비,\" 소령이 응수했다, \"나는 아무것도 암시하지 않소. 하지만 조이 B.는 세상을 살아 본 사람이오, 선생. 눈을 똑바로 뜨고, 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 세상을 살아 본 사람이란 말이오. 그리고 조는 당신에게 말하오, 돔비, 저 건너편에 지독히도 교활하고 야심 많은 여자가 있다고 말이오.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 자기도 모르게 건너편을 흘끗 보았다. 그리고 그 방향으로 노여운 눈길을 또한 던졌다.\n\n\"그런 주제에 관해서라면 조지프 백스톡의 입에서 나올 말은 그게 전부요,\" 소령이 단호하게 말했다. \"조는 고자질쟁이가 아니오, 하지만 말을 해야만 할 때가, 말을 하고야 말 때가 있는 법이오!—당신의 그 잔꾀에 저주가 있기를, 마님,\" 소령이 또다시 자기 어여쁜 이웃을 향해 대단한 노여움으로 부르짖었다, \"도발이 너무 강해서 도저히 침묵을 지키고 있을 수 없을 때 말이오.\"\n\n이 폭발의 격정으로 소령은 말의 기침 발작에 빠져들었고, 그것이 오래도록 그를 붙들었다. 진정이 되자 그는 덧붙였다.\n\n\"자, 이제, 돔비, 당신이 조를—다른 아무 장점도 없고 그저 질기고 기운차다는 것밖에 없는 늙은 조를, 선생—리밍턴에서 당신의 손님이자 안내자로 청한 이상, 무슨 일이든 마음대로 그에게 명하시오, 그러면 그는 온전히 당신의 것이오. 나는 모르겠소, 선생,\" 소령이 익살스러운 기색으로 이중 턱을 흔들며 말했다, \"도대체 당신네 사람들이 조에게서 무엇을 보길래 다들 그를 그렇게 대단히 청하는지 말이오. 하지만 이것만은 아오, 선생, 만약 그가 어지간히 질기지 않고, 또 한사코 거절하지 않았더라면, 당신네들이 초대니 뭐니 하는 것으로 그를 단번에 죽여 버렸으리라는 것을 말이오.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 백스톡 소령을 차지하려고 아우성치는 다른 저명한 사교계 인사들보다 자기가 우선권을 누리게 된 데 대해 몇 마디로 사의를 표했다. 그러나 소령은 그의 말을 잘라, 자기는 자기 마음 가는 대로 따를 뿐이며, 그 마음들이 한 덩어리로 일어나 한목소리로 \"J. B., 돔비야말로 자네가 벗으로 고를 사내일세\"라고 말했다는 것을 그에게 알아듣게 했다.\n\n이쯤에서 소령은 포만 상태에 이르러, 맛깔난 파이의 진액이 눈가에서 배어 나오고, 매콤한 석쇠구이와 콩팥 요리가 그의 크라바트를 죄어 오고 있었다. 게다가 그들이 도시를 떠나 타고 갈 버밍엄행 철도 열차의 출발 시각도 다가오고 있었던지라, 원주민은 엄청난 애를 써서 그를 외투에 끼워 넣고, 단추를 채워 올린 끝에, 마치 그가 통 속에 들어 있기라도 한 양, 그 외투 위로 얼굴이 부릅뜨고 헐떡이는 꼴이 되도록 만들었다. 그런 다음 원주민은 그에게 따로따로, 그것도 매번 적당한 간격을 두고서, 그의 섀미 가죽 장갑과, 그의 굵은 지팡이와, 그의 모자를 건넸다. 소령은 그 마지막 물건을, 자기의 두드러진 얼굴을 좀 누그러뜨리려는 듯, 멋스럽게 머리 한쪽에 비스듬히 얹어 썼다. 원주민은 그에 앞서, 대기 중이던 돔비 씨의 사륜마차의 가능하고 불가능한 온갖 부분에다, 소령 자신만큼이나 졸중에 걸린 듯한 행색의 융단 가방과 작은 여행 가방을 유달리 많이 실어 두었다. 그리고 셀처 광천수며, 동인도산 셰리주며, 샌드위치며, 숄이며, 망원경이며, 지도며, 신문 따위로 자기 주머니를 가득 채웠으니—그 가벼운 짐들 가운데 무엇이든, 혹은 전부든 소령이 여행 중 언제 어느 순간에 필요로 할지 모를 일이었다—그러고 나서 그는 모든 준비가 끝났다고 알렸다. 이 불운한 이방인(제 나라에서는 왕자라는 소문이 도는)이 톨린슨 씨 곁의 뒷좌석에 자리를 잡았을 때, 그의 채비를 완성하기 위해 여관 주인이 소령의 망토며 외투를 한 무더기 그에게 내던졌는데, 주인은 그 거대한 발사체들을 마치 타이탄처럼 보도에서 그를 겨냥해 던져, 그를 어찌나 뒤덮어 버렸던지, 그는 산 채로 무덤에 든 꼴로 철도역까지 갔다.\n\n그러나 마차가 움직이기 전, 원주민이 한창 매장당하고 있는 사이에, 톡스 양이 창가에 나타나 백합처럼 흰 손수건을 흔들었다. 돔비 씨는 이 작별 인사를 매우 냉랭하게—그로서도 매우 냉랭하다 할 만큼—받았고, 머리를 더없이 미미하게 까딱여 그녀를 예우한 뒤, 몹시 못마땅한 표정으로 마차에 등을 기대었다. 그의 그 뚜렷한 태도는 (톡스 양을 알아보는 데 더없이 정중했던) 소령에게 한없는 만족을 안겨 주는 듯했다. 그래서 그는 그 후로도 오랫동안, 너무 많이 먹은 메피스토펠레스처럼 곁눈질을 하고 켁켁거리며 앉아 있었다.\n\n철도역에서 출발 채비로 분주한 동안, 돔비 씨와 소령은 승강장을 나란히 오락가락 거닐었다. 전자는 말이 없고 침울했으며, 후자는 갖가지 일화와 회상으로 그를, 혹은 자기 자신을 즐겁게 했는데, 그 대부분에서 조 백스톡이 주연이었다. 두 사람 다, 이렇게 거니는 동안 그들이 기관차 가까이 서 있던 한 노동자의 주의를 끌고 있다는 것을, 그리고 그 사내가 그들이 지나갈 때마다 모자에 손을 갖다 댄다는 것을 알아채지 못했다. 돔비 씨는 으레 천한 무리를 향해 보는 게 아니라 그들 너머를 보는 사람이었고, 소령은 그때 자기 이야기 가운데 하나의 한복판을 들여다보고 있었던 까닭이다. 그러나 마침내 이 사내가, 그들이 몸을 돌리는 참에 그들 앞으로 나서더니, 모자를 벗어 든 채 그것을 벗어 든 채로 돔비 씨에게 머리를 조아렸다.\n\n\"실례합니다요, 나리,\" 사내가 말했다, \"그저 별고 없으시기를 바랍니다요, 나리.\"\n\n그는 석탄 가루와 기름이 잔뜩 묻은 즈크 작업복을 입고 있었고, 구레나룻에는 탄 부스러기가 박혀 있었으며, 온몸에서 반쯤 꺼진 재 냄새가 풍겼다. 그런데도 그는 못생긴 사내는 아니었고, 공정하게 말하자면 지저분해 보이는 사내라고조차 할 수 없었다. 요컨대 그는 직업복을 차려입은 투들 씨였다.\n\n\"제가 영광스럽게도 나리를 모시고 화부 노릇을 하게 됐습니다요, 나리,\" 투들 씨가 말했다. \"실례합니다요, 나리.—이제 좀 차도가 있으신지요?\"\n\n돔비 씨는 그 관심 어린 어조에 대한 답례로, 마치 저런 사내가 자기의 시력마저 더럽히기라도 할 듯이 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"외람된 말씀입니다만, 나리,\" 자기가 또렷이 기억되고 있지 않음을 알아챈 투들이 말했다, \"나리 댁에서 리처즈로 불리던 제 처 폴리가—\"\n\n돔비 씨의 얼굴에 변화가 일었다. 그를 기억해 낸 것을 나타내는 듯했고 실제로도 그러했으나, 그보다 훨씬 강한 정도로, 굴욕감에서 비롯된 노여움을 나타내는 변화였다. 그 변화가 투들 씨의 말을 뚝 멈추게 했다.\n\n\"자네 처가 돈이 필요한 모양이군,\" 돔비 씨가 주머니에 손을 넣으며, 거만하게 (그러나 그건 늘 그러던 대로였지만) 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다요, 고맙습니다만, 나리,\" 투들이 대답했다, \"그렇다고는 말씀 못 드리겠는뎁쇼. 저도요.\"\n\n이번에는 돔비 씨가 거꾸로 말이 막혔다. 그것도 어색하게. 주머니에 손을 넣은 채로.\n\n\"아닙니다요, 나리,\" 투들이 자기 방수모를 빙글빙글 돌리며 말했다. \"저희는 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다요, 나리. 세상살이 형편으로는 불평할 거리가 없는뎁쇼, 나리. 그 뒤로 넷이나 더 봤습니다요, 나리, 그래도 어떻게든 헤쳐 나가고 있습죠.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 자기 마차 쪽으로 헤쳐 나갈 참이었고, 그러느라 그 화부를 바퀴 밑으로 밀어 깔아뭉갰더라도 개의치 않았을 것이다. 그러나 사내의 손에서 여전히 천천히 빙글빙글 돌아가는 그 모자와 관련된 무언가가 그의 주의를 붙들었다.\n\n\"저희가 갓난것 하나를 잃었습죠,\" 투들이 말했다, \"그건 부정 못 합니다요.\"\n\n\"최근에 말인가,\" 돔비 씨가 모자를 바라보며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아닙니다요, 나리, 삼 년도 더 전입니다요, 하지만 나머지는 다들 튼튼합죠. 그리고 글 읽기 말씀입니다만, 나리,\" 투들이, 오래전 그 문제를 두고 둘 사이에 오갔던 일을 돔비 씨에게 일깨우려는 듯 다시 머리를 조아리며 말했다, \"그 녀석들이, 제 자식들 말입죠, 자기네끼리 결국 저를 가르쳤습니다요. 그 녀석들이 저를 제법 그럴듯한 학자로 만들어 놨습죠, 나리, 그 녀석들이요.\"\n\n\"갑시다, 소령!\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"실례합니다요, 나리,\" 투들이 다시 입을 열어, 그들 앞으로 한 걸음 나서며 여전히 모자를 손에 든 채 공손하게 또 그들을 멈춰 세웠다. \"그저 제 아들 바일러—세례명은 로빈입니다만—나리께서 친절하게도 자선 연마공으로 만들어 주신 그 녀석의 이름을 끄집어내려는 마음이 아니었더라면, 이런 일로 나리를 성가시게 해 드리지는 않았을 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"그래서, 이 사람아,\" 돔비 씨가 더없이 준엄한 태도로 말했다. \"그 애가 어쨌다는 건가?\"\n\n\"그게 말입죠, 나리,\" 투들이 몹시 근심하고 비통해하는 얼굴로 고개를 저으며 대답했다, \"송구하지만 말씀드려야겠습니다요, 나리, 그 녀석이 그릇된 길로 빠졌습니다요.\"\n\n\"그릇된 길로 빠졌다, 그런가?\" 돔비 씨가 어딘가 매정한 만족감을 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"보시다시피, 나쁜 패거리에 끼어들었습죠, 나리들,\" 아비가 두 사람을 애처롭게 바라보며 말을 이었는데, 분명 소령을 대화에 끌어들여 그의 동정을 얻고 싶어 하는 기색이었다. \"나쁜 길에 들어섰습니다요. 부디 다시 제정신을 차렸으면 좋겠습니다만, 나리들, 그 녀석은 지금 그릇된 길로 가고 있습니다요! 나리께서도 어떻게든 그 소식을 안 들으시기는 어려우셨을 겁니다요, 나리,\" 투들이 다시 돔비 씨 한 사람을 향해 말했다. \"그러니 제가 차라리 털어놓고, 제 아이가 좀 그릇되게 빠졌다고 말씀드리는 게 낫겠습죠. 폴리가 그 일로 끔찍이 풀이 죽어 있습니다요, 나리들,\" 투들이 똑같이 의기소침한 표정으로, 또다시 소령에게 호소하며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 교육을 시켜 준 이 사람의 아들이오, 소령,\" 돔비 씨가 그에게 팔을 내밀며 말했다. \"흔해 빠진 보답이지요!\"\n\n\"솔직한 늙은 조의 충고를 들으시오, 그런 부류의 인간들은 절대 교육시키지 마시오, 선생,\" 소령이 응수했다. \"제기랄, 선생, 그건 결코 잘되는 법이 없소! 언제나 실패한단 말이오!\"\n\n이 순박한 아비는, 자기 아들, 그 한때의 연마공이, 핀잔과 주먹질을 당하고, 매질과 표딱지를 달고, 사냥개만큼이나 교사 자리에 어울리는 어떤 무지막지한 자—연줄로 그 자리를 꿰찬 자—한테서 앵무새처럼 배운 것이고 보면, 어쩌면 무언가 알려지지 않은 점에서 아주 옳은 방식으로 교육받지는 못한 것 아니겠느냐고 조심스레 아뢰려던 참이었는데, 그때 돔비 씨가 \"흔해 빠진 보답이지요!\"라고 노엽게 되풀이하며 소령을 데리고 가 버렸다. 그리고 소령은 돔비 씨의 마차 안으로 들어 올리기에 무거운 사람인지라, 공중에 떠올려진 채, 발판에 발을 디디지 못하고 그 검은 유배자 위로 미끄러져 내릴 때마다, 멈춰 서서 저 원주민의 산 가죽을 벗기고, 그 살가죽 속 뼈마디를 죄다 부러뜨리고, 그 밖의 온갖 육체적 고문을 가하겠노라 맹세하지 않을 수 없었다. 그래서 출발하기 전 겨우 짬을 내어, 그건 결코 잘될 리 없으며, 언제나 실패하는 법이고, 만약 자기가 \"제 집의 부랑아 놈\"을 교육시킨다면 틀림없이 그놈이 교수형을 당할 거라고 쉰 목소리로 되풀이했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 씁쓸하게 동의했다. 그러나 그의 그 씁쓸함에는, 그리고 마차에 시무룩이 등을 기댄 채 미간을 찌푸리고 바깥의 스쳐 가는 사물들을 바라보는 그 침울한 태도에는, 연마공 회사가 운영하는 그 고귀한 교육 제도의 실패 이상의 무언가가 있었다. 그는 그 사내의 거친 모자에 새 검정 상장(喪章) 한 조각이 달린 것을 보았고, 사내의 태도와 대답으로 미루어, 그가 그것을 자기 아들을 위해 두르고 있다고 확신했던 것이다.\n\n그렇구나! 위에서나 아래에서나, 집 안에서나 밖에서나, 큰 저택에 있는 플로렌스로부터 지금 그들 앞에서 연기를 피우며 불을 때고 있는 저 거친 촌놈에 이르기까지, 누구나 다 죽은 자기 아들에 대한 몫을 두고 이런저런 권리를 주장하며 자기와 다투어 값을 부르는 입찰자였구나! 그 여자가 자기 베갯머리에서 어떻게 울며 그 애를 제 자식이라 불렀는지, 또 그 애가 잠에서 깨어나 그 여자를 찾으며 침대에서 몸을 일으키고 그 여자가 들어오자 환하게 밝아졌는지, 그가 과연 그것을 잊을 수 있을까!\n\n석탄과 재나 헤집고 다니는 저 주제넘은 자가, 상장 따위를 달고 저기서 앞장서 가다니! 저런 자가, 저런 흔해 빠진 겉치레로나마, 거만한 신사의 은밀한 가슴속 시련과 좌절 속으로 감히 들어오려 하다니! 자기의 재산과 계획과 권세를 함께 나누었어야 할, 그리고 그 애와 손잡고 마치 금으로 된 이중문으로 막아 버리듯 온 세상을 차단했어야 할, 이 잃어버린 아이가, 저런 무리를 안으로 들여, 그의 좌절된 희망을 안다는 사실로 그를 모욕하게 하고, 그토록 동떨어진 자기와 감정의 공동체를 주장한다고 떠벌리게 하다니—설령 자기가 홀로 군림하려 했을 그 자리에 슬그머니 기어든 것까지는 아니라 해도 말이다!\n\n그는 여행에서 아무런 즐거움도 위안도 찾지 못했다. 이런 생각들에 시달리며, 그는 휙휙 스쳐 가는 풍경을 가로질러 단조로움을 함께 지고 갔고, 풍요롭고 다채로운 시골이 아니라 시들어 버린 계획과 갉아먹는 시기심의 황야 속으로 곤두박질치듯 달려갔다. 열차가 휩쓸려 가는 그 속도 자체가, 그토록 꾸준하고도 그토록 가차 없이 예정된 종말로 실려 가 버린 어린 생명의 빠른 행로를 비웃는 듯했다. 제 길—제 것인 그 길—을 따라 억지로 밀고 나아가며, 온갖 길과 도로를 거역하고, 모든 장애물의 심장을 꿰뚫고, 온갖 계층과 연령과 신분의 살아 있는 존재들을 제 뒤에 매달아 끌고 가는 그 힘은, 의기양양한 괴물, 곧 죽음의 한 표상이었다.\n\n비명을 지르고, 으르렁대고, 덜컹거리며, 도시를 벗어나, 사람들의 거처 사이를 파고들어 거리를 윙윙 울리게 하고, 한순간 초원으로 번쩍 튀어나왔다가, 축축한 흙을 뚫고 들어가, 어둠과 무거운 공기 속을 쿵쿵대며 나아가다가, 다시 그토록 밝고 드넓은 햇빛 비치는 대낮으로 터져 나온다. 비명을 지르고, 으르렁대고, 덜컹거리며, 들판을 지나, 숲을 지나, 곡식밭을 지나, 건초밭을 지나, 백악을 지나, 부엽토를 지나, 진흙을 지나, 바위를 지나, 손 닿을 듯 거의 잡힐 듯 가까운 사물들 사이로—그것들은 여행자에게서 끊임없이 달아나고, 한편 기만적인 먼 거리는 그의 안에서 끊임없이 천천히 움직인다. 마치 저 무자비한 괴물, 죽음의 자취에서처럼!\n\n골짜기를 지나, 언덕 위로, 황야 곁으로, 과수원 곁으로, 공원 곁으로, 정원 곁으로, 운하 위를 건너, 강을 가로질러, 양 떼가 풀을 뜯는 곳으로, 물방앗간이 돌아가는 곳으로, 거룻배가 떠 있는 곳으로, 죽은 이들이 누워 있는 곳으로, 공장이 연기를 뿜는 곳으로, 시냇물이 흐르는 곳으로, 마을이 옹기종기 모인 곳으로, 거대한 대성당이 솟은 곳으로, 황량한 황무지가 펼쳐지고 거센 바람이 제멋대로 그것을 매끈하게 펴거나 헝클어뜨리는 곳으로. 비명을 지르고, 으르렁대고, 덜컹거리며, 뒤에 남길 자취라곤 먼지와 수증기밖에 없이. 마치 저 무자비한 괴물, 죽음의 자취에서처럼!\n\n바람과 햇빛을, 소나기와 햇살을 헤치며, 멀리, 또 멀리, 그것은 사납고 빠르게, 매끄럽고 확실하게 구르고 으르렁댄다. 그리고 위로 가로질러 가는 거대한 건축물과 육중한 다리들이 한 치 너비의 그림자 줄기처럼 눈앞에 떨어졌다가 이내 사라진다. 멀리, 또 멀리, 줄곧 앞으로 또 앞으로. 오두막집들, 가옥들, 대저택들, 부유한 영지들, 농경과 수공업, 사람들, 버려진 듯 작고 하찮아 보이며 뒤로 멀어져 가는 옛 길과 오솔길들—그렇게 그것들은 뒤로 멀어진다. 그리고 저 불굴의 괴물, 죽음의 자취 속에 그런 일별들 말고 또 무엇이 있겠는가!\n\n비명을 지르고, 으르렁대고, 덜컹거리며, 다시 땅속으로 곤두박질쳐 들어가, 그토록 격렬한 활력과 끈기의 폭풍 속에서 일해 나가니, 어둠과 회오리바람 속에서 그 움직임이 거꾸로 뒤집혀, 미친 듯이 뒤로 향하는 것처럼 보이다가, 젖은 벽 위에 한 줄기 빛이 비치면서 그 표면이 사나운 물줄기처럼 스쳐 지나가는 것을 드러낸다. 다시 한번 대낮 속으로, 그리고 대낮을 가로질러, 의기양양한 날카로운 비명을 지르며, 으르렁대고 덜컹대며 마구 내달려, 그 검은 숨결로 모든 것을 떨쳐 버리고, 때로는 한 무리의 얼굴들이 있는 곳에서 잠시 멈추었다가—다음 순간이면 그 얼굴들은 거기 없다—때로는 물을 게걸스레 들이켜, 그것이 마시는 주둥이에서 아직 물방울이 땅에 떨어지기를 그치기도 전에, 비명을 지르고, 으르렁대고, 덜컹거리며 자줏빛 먼 곳을 뚫고 나아간다!\n\n점점 더 크게, 그것은 거역할 수 없이 목표를 향해 마구 내달리며 비명을 지르고 울부짖는다. 그리고 이제 그 길은, 여전히 죽음의 길과 같이, 잿더미로 두텁게 뒤덮여 있다. 주위의 모든 것이 시커멓게 그을려 있다. 시커먼 물웅덩이들, 진창길들, 그리고 저 아래 비참한 거처들이 있다. 바로 곁에는 들쭉날쭉한 벽들과 무너져 가는 집들이 있고, 두들겨 맞은 지붕과 깨진 창문 너머로 비참한 방들이 보이는데, 거기서는 궁핍과 열병이 갖가지 비참한 형상으로 몸을 숨기고 있는 한편, 연기와 빽빽이 들어찬 박공들과 일그러진 굴뚝들, 그리고 정신과 육체의 기형을 가두어 두는 벽돌과 회반죽의 기형이 그 칙칙한 먼 곳을 틀어막고 있다. 돔비 씨가 마차 창밖을 내다볼 때, 자기를 이곳으로 데려온 그 괴물이 바로 이런 것들 위로 대낮의 빛을 들여보낸 것이지 그것들을 만들거나 빚어낸 게 아니라는 생각은 결코 그의 머릿속에 떠오르지 않는다. 그곳은 그 여행에 어울리는 종착지였고, 또한 만물의 종착지일 수도 있었다. 그만큼 그곳은 황폐하고 음산했다.\n\n그리하여 그 한 가닥 생각을 좇으면서, 그는 여전히 그 무자비한 괴물 하나를 눈앞에 두고 있었다. 모든 것이 그에게 검고, 차갑고, 죽음 같아 보였고, 그 역시 그것들에게 그러했다. 그는 어디서나 자기 불행과의 닮은꼴을 찾아냈다. 그의 주위에서는 무자비한 승리가 진행되고 있었고, 그것은 어떤 형태를 띠든 그의 자존심과 시기심을 쓰리게 하고 찔러 댔다. 그러나 그 무엇보다도, 그것이 자기와 더불어 죽은 아들의 사랑과 기억을 나누어 가질 때가 가장 그러했다.\n\n한 얼굴이 있었다—그가 전날 밤 들여다보았던, 그리고 그것이, 비록 눈물로 흐려져 있었고 곧 떨리는 두 손 뒤로 가려졌으나, 그의 영혼을 읽어 내는 두 눈으로 그를 들여다보았던 그 얼굴이—이 여정 내내 그의 상상 속에 자주 따라붙었다. 그는 그것을, 어젯밤의 그 표정 그대로, 자기에게 겁먹은 듯 호소하는 모습으로 보았다. 그것은 책망하는 빛은 아니었으나, 거기에는 어떤 망설임이, 거의 희망 어린 불신 같은 것이 깃들어 있었고, 그것이 자기에 대한 그의 혐오라는 황량한 확신으로 사그라드는 것을 그가 다시 한번 보았을 때, 그것은 책망과도 같았다. 이 플로렌스의 얼굴을 생각하는 것은 그에게 괴로움이었다.\n\n그것을 향해 어떤 새로운 양심의 가책을 느꼈기 때문일까? 아니었다. 그 얼굴이 그의 안에 일깨운 감정이—더 오래전 시절에 어떤 옛 전조를 그가 이미 느낀 바 있던 그 감정이—이제 온전히 모습을 갖추고 분명하게 입을 열어, 그를 너무도 흔들어 놓으며, 그의 평정을 압도할 만큼 너무 강해질 듯 위협했기 때문이었다. 그 얼굴이 바깥에, 마치 공기처럼 그를 에워싸는 듯한 패배와 박해의 표정 속에 떠돌고 있었기 때문이었다. 그 얼굴이, 그의 생각이 그토록 줄곧 좇고 있던 그 잔인하고 무자비한 적의 화살에 가시를 박고, 그 손에 양손잡이 칼을 쥐여 주었기 때문이었다. 그가 거기 서서, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 변천의 풍경을 제 마음의 병든 빛깔로 물들이며, 그것을 희망찬 변화나 더 나은 것들의 약속이 아니라 폐허요 쇠락의 그림으로 만들고 있던 그때, 자기 가슴속에서 너무도 잘 알고 있었기 때문이었다—이 한탄들과 관련해서는 죽음 못지않게 삶에도 책임이 있다는 것을. 한 아이는 떠나갔고, 한 아이는 남았다. 어찌하여 그가 그토록 희망을 걸었던 대상이, 그 애 대신 데려가지고 만 것인가?\n\n그의 상상 속의 그 다정하고, 차분하고, 온화한 존재는 그에게 오직 그 생각만을 불러일으켰다. 그 애는 처음부터 그에게 달갑지 않았다. 이제 그 애는 그의 쓰라림을 더욱 악화시키는 존재였다. 만약 자기 아들이 외동이었고 똑같은 타격이 자기에게 떨어졌다면, 그것은 견디기 무거웠으리라. 그러나 그것이 그 애에게 떨어질 수도 있었으나(그 애라면 잃어도—그는 그렇게 믿었다—한 점 아픔 없이 잃을 수 있었으련만) 그러지 않은 지금에 비하면, 한없이 가벼웠으리라. 그의 눈앞에 떠오르는 그 애의 사랑스럽고 순결한 얼굴은 그를 누그러뜨리거나 사로잡는 아무런 힘도 없었다. 그는 그 천사를 물리치고, 자기 가슴속에 웅크린 그 괴롭히는 영을 받아들였다. 그 애의 인내와, 착함과, 젊음과, 헌신과, 사랑은, 그가 발뒤꿈치로 짓밟는 잿더미 속의 무수한 티끌에 지나지 않았다. 그는 자기를 둘러싼 그 시들음과 어둠 속에서 그 애의 형상을 보았으니, 그것은 그 음울함을 환히 밝히는 게 아니라 한층 깊게 했다. 이 여정에서 한 번 이상, 그리고 이제 이 여정의 끝에서 지팡이로 먼지 위에 형상을 그리며 골똘히 서 있는 지금 다시, 한 가지 생각이 그의 마음에 떠올랐다. 자기 자신과 그것 사이에 끼워 넣을 만한 것이 무엇이 있을까?\n\n소령은 또 하나의 기관차처럼 내려오는 내내 씩씩대고 헐떡거렸고, 그의 눈은 신문에서 자주 벗어나 바깥 경치를 곁눈질했는데, 마치 패주한 톡스 양들의 행렬이 열차의 연기 속에서 쏟아져 나와 들판 너머로 날아가 어디든 피난처에 몸을 숨기기라도 하는 양 보였던 것이다—그런 소령이, 역마들이 매여 있고 마차가 준비되었다고 알리며 자기 친구를 일깨웠다.\n\n\"돔비,\" 소령이 지팡이로 그의 팔을 톡톡 치며 말했다, \"그렇게 생각에 잠기지 마시오. 그건 나쁜 버릇이오. 늙은 조도, 선생, 만약 그런 걸 부추긴 적이 있었더라면 보시다시피 이렇게 질긴 사람은 못 됐을 거요. 당신은 너무 대단한 인물이오, 돔비, 생각에 잠겨 있기에는 말이오. 당신의 지위에서는, 선생, 그런 종류의 일 따위는 한참 초월해 있는 거요.\"\n\n이렇게 소령이 그 다정한 충고 속에서조차 돔비 씨의 위엄과 명예를 헤아리고 그 중요성에 대한 생생한 감각을 보여 주니, 돔비 씨는 그토록 풍부한 양식과 그토록 잘 다스려진 정신을 지닌 신사에게 그 어느 때보다도 마음을 기울이고 싶어졌다. 그래서 그는 유료 도로를 따라 빠르게 달리는 동안 소령의 이야기에 귀 기울이려 애를 썼다. 그리고 소령은 그 속도도 그 길도 자기들이 방금 버리고 온 여행 방식보다 자기 화술에 훨씬 잘 맞는다는 것을 알고는, 한껏 흥이 올라 입담을 펼쳤다.\n\n그러나 무뚝뚝하고 질긴 사내인 소령은, 그리고 그 스스로도 그렇게 자주 말하던 대로의 사내인 그는, 그러면서도 자기 길동무의 식욕에 맞는 입맛 당기는 음식을 얼마간 차려 냈다. 그는 자기 친구 돔비를 두고 클럽에 대단한 호기심과 흥분이 일고 있다는 것을, 들려주었다기보다는 오히려 우연인 양, 그리고 말하자면 마지못해 본의 아니게 흘러나오도록 내버려 두었다. 자기가 질문에 어찌나 시달려 숨이 막힐 지경인지, 선생. 거기서는 늙은 조 백스톡이 돔비 덕분에 그 어느 때보다 대단한 인물이 되어 있는지. 사람들이 어떻게 \"백스톡, 자네 친구 돔비 말일세, 이러이러한 문제에 대해 그가 어떤 견해를 갖고 있나?\" 하고 묻는지. \"그렇긴 한데, 십자가에 걸고 맹세컨대, 선생,\" 소령이 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 말했다, \"도대체 J. B.가 어쩌다 당신을 알게 됐는지를 그들이 어떻게 알아냈는지는 수수께끼란 말이오!\"\n\n이렇게 기분이 도도하고 말이 도도하게 흘러가는 가운데, 그것을 가로막는 것이라곤 그의 여느 다혈증 증세와, 이따금의 점심 시간과, 그리고 때때로 원주민에게 가하는 어떤 격렬한 공격뿐이었다. 그 원주민은 짙은 갈색 귀에 귀고리 한 쌍을 달고 있었고, 그의 몸에 걸친 유럽식 옷은 어찌나 괴상하게 맞지 않던지—제멋대로, 그리고 재단술 따위는 아랑곳없이, 짧아야 할 데서 길고, 길어야 할 데서 짧고, 헐거워야 할 데서 죄고, 죄어야 할 데서 헐거웠다—소령이 그를 공격할 때마다 그는 쪼그라든 호두처럼, 혹은 추워하는 원숭이처럼 그 옷 속으로 움츠러들어 거기에 새로운 운치를 더했다—이렇게 기분과 입담이 흘러가는 가운데, 소령은 온종일을 보냈다. 그리하여 저녁이 와서 그들이 리밍턴 가까운 푸르고 잎 무성한 길을 빠르게 달리고 있을 때, 소령의 목소리는, 떠들고 먹고 낄낄대고 켁켁댄 끝에, 뒷좌석 밑의 짐칸에 있거나 어디 근처 건초더미 속에 있는 것처럼 들렸다. 또한 소령은 방과 만찬을 미리 주문해 둔 로열 호텔에서도 자기 목소리를 회복하지 못했으니, 거기서 먹고 마시느라 자기 발성 기관을 어찌나 억눌렀던지, 잠자리에 들 무렵에는 기침할 때 말고는 목소리가 전혀 나오지 않았고, 검은 하인에게 자기 뜻을 알릴 수 있는 방법이라곤 그를 향해 헐떡거리는 것뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 그는 이튿날 아침, 마치 원기를 되찾은 거인처럼 자리에서 일어났을 뿐 아니라, 아침 식사 자리에서는 거인이 원기를 되찾아 가듯 처신했다. 이 식사 자리에서 그들은 매일의 생활 방식을 짰다. 소령이 먹고 마실 모든 것을 주문하는 책임을 맡기로 했다. 그리고 그들은 매일 아침 함께 늦은 아침을, 매일 함께 늦은 만찬을 들기로 했다. 돔비 씨는 리밍턴 체류 첫날만큼은 자기 방에 머물거나 혼자 시골을 거니는 편이 좋겠다고 했다. 그러나 이튿날 아침에는 기꺼이 소령과 더불어 약수터에도, 시내 곳곳에도 함께 가겠노라고 했다. 그래서 그들은 만찬 시간까지 헤어졌다. 돔비 씨는 자기 식대로 자기의 건전한 생각을 가다듬으러 물러갔다. 소령은 야영용 접의자와 외투와 우산을 든 원주민을 거느리고, 온갖 공공장소를 거들먹거리며 오르내렸다. 누가 와 있는지 알아보려고 회원 명부를 들여다보고, 자기를 무척 흠모하는 노부인들을 찾아다니며, J. B.가 그 어느 때보다 질겨졌다고 떠벌리고, 가는 곳마다 자기의 부유한 친구 돔비를 추켜세웠다. 친구를 추켜세우면서 동시에 제 자신을 추켜세울 때만큼, 소령보다 더 충실하게 친구 곁을 지키는 사내는 없었다.\n\n소령이 만찬 시간에 풀어놓을 새로운 이야깃거리를 얼마나 많이 갖고 있었는지, 그리고 돔비 씨에게 그의 사교적 자질을 감탄할 기회를 얼마나 많이 주었는지는 놀라운 일이었다. 이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서 그는 가장 최근에 도착한 신문들의 내용을 꿰고 있었다. 그리고 그것들과 관련된 여러 화제를 언급했는데, 그 화제들에 대해 자기 의견을, 너무도 권세 있고 위세 있는 인물들이 최근에 구해 와서, 그저 어렴풋이 암시하는 수밖에 없다는 식이었다. 그토록 오랫동안 제 안에 틀어박혀 지내 왔고, 어느 때든 돔비와 아들의 사업이 영위되는 그 마법의 동그라미 밖으로 좀처럼 발을 내디딘 적이 없던 돔비 씨는, 이것을 자기 고독한 삶에 견주어 한결 나은 것으로 여기기 시작했다. 그래서 혼자 있을 때 생각했던 것처럼 또 하루를 핑계 대고 빠지는 대신, 소령과 팔짱을 끼고 밖으로 나섰다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI. New Faces\n\n소령은 그 어느 때보다 얼굴이 더 푸르뎅뎅하고 눈을 부라리며—말하자면 그 어느 때보다 더 농익은 모습으로—이따금 그 말[馬] 같은 기침을, 꼭 필요해서라기보다는 자기 중요성이 저절로 터져 나오는 양 한 번씩 뱉어 내면서, 돔비 씨와 팔짱을 끼고 길의 양지바른 쪽을 따라 걸었다. 빳빳한 목깃 위로 두 볼이 불룩 부풀고, 다리는 위엄 있게 한껏 벌리고, 커다란 머리는 이쪽저쪽으로 흔들거렸는데, 마치 자기가 이토록 매혹적인 존재인 것을 두고 속으로 항변이라도 하는 듯했다. 몇 야드 채 걷기도 전에 소령은 아는 사람과 마주쳤고, 다시 몇 야드 더 가기도 전에 또 다른 아는 사람과 마주쳤지만, 그는 지나가면서 그저 손가락을 까딱여 보일 뿐 돔비 씨를 계속 이끌고 갔다. 가는 길에 이런저런 장소를 가리켜 보이고, 그것들이 떠올려 주는 요즘의 추문들로 산책에 흥을 돋우면서.\n\n이런 식으로 소령과 돔비 씨가 서로 매우 흡족해하며 팔짱을 끼고 걷고 있을 때, 두 사람은 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 바퀴 달린 의자 하나를 보았다. 그 안에는 한 부인이 앉아, 앞쪽에 달린 일종의 키 같은 것으로 나른하게 제 수레를 조종하고 있었고, 의자는 뒤편의 보이지 않는 힘에 의해 밀려오고 있었다. 부인은 젊지 않았지만 얼굴이 매우 화사하게 피어 있었고—아주 발그레했으며—옷차림과 자세는 완벽하게 어려 보였다. 그 의자 옆에서, 마치 그토록 대단한 노력은 곧 그만두어 양산을 떨어뜨릴 수밖에 없다는 듯, 자기의 얇디얇은 양산을 자랑스럽고도 지친 태도로 들고 한가로이 거니는 이는 훨씬 젊은 부인이었다. 매우 아름답고, 매우 거만하며, 매우 제멋대로인 그 여인은 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 눈꺼풀을 내리깔았는데, 마치 거울을 빼고 이 세상에 들여다볼 가치가 있는 무언가가 있다 한들 그것이 결코 땅이나 하늘은 아니라는 듯했다.\n\n\"아니, 이거 도대체 누가 오시는 겁니까, 선생!\" 이 작은 행렬이 가까워지자 소령이 걸음을 멈추며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아이참, 내 사랑하는 에디스!\" 의자 안의 부인이 느릿느릿 말했다, \"백스톡 소령님!\"\n\n소령은 그 목소리를 듣자마자 돔비 씨의 팔을 놓아 버리고 앞으로 달려 나가, 의자 안 부인의 손을 잡고 자기 입술에 가져다 댔다. 못지않은 정중함으로 소령은 두 손에 낀 장갑을 가슴에 포개어 얹고, 다른 부인에게도 깊이 절했다. 그러는 사이 의자가 멈추자, 그것을 밀던 동력원이 모습을 드러냈는데, 뒤에서 의자를 밀던 얼굴이 벌게진 시동이었다. 그는 한편으로는 키가 너무 웃자라고 한편으로는 힘이 다 빠진 듯이 보였으니, 똑바로 서니 키가 크고 핼쑥하며 비쩍 말랐고, 그 처지가 한층 처량해 보였던 것은 동방 나라들에서 코끼리들이 더러 그러듯 머리로 수레를 들이받아 앞으로 밀어 댄 탓에 모자의 모양을 망가뜨려 놓은 까닭이었다.\n\n\"조 백스톡은,\" 소령이 두 부인 모두에게 말했다, \"남은 평생 자랑스럽고 행복한 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"이 거짓말쟁이 양반!\" 의자 안의 노부인이 맥없이 말했다. \"어디서 오신 거예요? 정말 못 봐 주겠네.\"\n\n\"그러시면 늙은 조가 친구를 한 사람 소개하게 해 주시지요, 마님,\" 소령이 즉각 말했다, \"그래야 봐주실 명분이 생길 테니까요. 돔비 씨, 이쪽은 스큐턴 부인이십니다.\" 의자 안의 부인이 우아하게 응했다. \"돔비 씨, 이쪽은 그레인저 부인이십니다.\" 양산을 든 부인은 돔비 씨가 모자를 벗고 깊이 절하는 것을 어렴풋이 의식했다. \"이런 기회를 얻게 되어,\" 소령이 말했다, \"참으로 기쁩니다, 선생.\"\n\n소령은 진심인 듯 보였으니, 세 사람을 모두 둘러보며 그 가장 못생긴 방식으로 곁눈질을 했기 때문이다.\n\n\"스큐턴 부인께서는, 돔비,\" 소령이 말했다, \"늙은 조시의 가슴에 큰 파란을 일으키신답니다.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 그럴 만도 하다는 뜻을 비쳤다.\n\n\"이 음흉한 도깨비 양반,\" 의자 안의 부인이 말했다, \"그만하세요! 여기 온 지는 얼마나 됐어요, 못된 양반?\"\n\n\"하루 됐습니다,\" 소령이 대답했다.\n\n\"한데 어떻게 하루를, 아니 단 일 분이라도,\" 부인이 부채로 자기 가짜 곱슬머리와 가짜 눈썹을 살짝 매만지고, 가짜 안색으로 돋보이게 한 가짜 이를 드러내 보이며 응수했다, \"그 뭐라더라 하는 곳의 정원에서 견딜 수가—\"\n\n\"에덴 말씀이시겠죠, 엄마,\" 젊은 부인이 경멸하듯 끼어들었다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 에디스,\" 다른 부인이 말했다, \"어쩔 수가 없구나. 그 끔찍한 이름들을 도무지 기억하질 못하니—자연의 광경에 온 영혼과 존재가 온통 영감으로 차오르지 않고서야, 그 향기에,\" 스큐턴 부인은 향수 때문에 은은하고도 들척지근한 손수건을 부스럭거리며 말했다, \"그 꾸밈없는 숨결의 향기에 말이에요, 이 양반아!\"\n\n스큐턴 부인의 싱싱한 말의 열정과 처량하게 시든 태도 사이의 불일치는, 일흔쯤 되는 그녀의 나이와 스물일곱에게도 어려 보였을 옷차림 사이의 불일치 못지않게 눈에 띄었다. 바퀴 달린 의자 안에서의 그 자세는(그녀는 결코 그것을 바꾸지 않았다) 한 오십 년 전, 당시 유행하던 한 화가가 사륜마차에 탄 그녀의 모습을 그렸을 때 취한 자세였다. 그 화가는 발표한 그 스케치에 '클레오파트라'라는 이름을 붙였는데, 그것이 갤리선 갑판에 비스듬히 기댄 그 여왕의 모습과 똑같이 닮았다는 것을 당대의 비평가들이 발견한 까닭이었다. 그때 스큐턴 부인은 미인이었고, 멋쟁이들은 그녀를 기려 포도주잔을 머리 너머로 수십 개씩 던졌다. 미모도 사륜마차도 둘 다 사라져 버렸지만, 그녀는 여전히 그 자세를 간직했으니, 바로 그 이유 하나만으로 바퀴 달린 의자와 들이받는 시동을 두고 있었던 것이다. 그 자세를 빼고는 그녀가 걷지 못할 이유가 전혀 없었으니 말이다.\n\n\"돔비 씨는 자연에 헌신하시는 분이겠죠?\" 스큐턴 부인이 다이아몬드 브로치를 매만지며 말했다. 그런데 말이 나온 김에 덧붙이자면, 그녀는 주로 어떤 다이아몬드들의 명성과 자기 집안의 인척 관계에 기대어 살아가고 있었다.\n\n\"제 친구 돔비는, 마님,\" 소령이 응수했다, \"남몰래 자연에 헌신할 수는 있겠지요, 그러나 우주에서 가장 위대한 도시에서 으뜸가는 사람으로서—\"\n\n\"돔비 씨의 어마어마한 영향력을,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"모르는 사람은 아무도 없지요.\"\n\n돔비 씨가 고개를 숙여 그 찬사에 답하자, 젊은 부인이 그를 흘끗 보다가 그의 눈과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"부인께서는 이곳에 거주하십니까?\" 돔비 씨가 그녀에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"아뇨, 저희는 여러 곳을 다녔어요. 해러게이트와 스카버러, 그리고 데번셔에도요. 여기저기 방문하고, 머물러 쉬고 했답니다. 엄마가 변화를 좋아하셔서요.\"\n\n\"에디스는 물론 안 그렇지만요,\" 스큐턴 부인이 섬뜩하리만치 천연덕스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"그런 곳들에 무슨 변화가 있다고는 느껴 보지 못했어요,\" 더없이 무심한 어조로 나온 대답이었다.\n\n\"이 애가 저를 모함하네요. 제가 정말로 마음 쓰는 변화는, 돔비 씨, 단 하나뿐이에요,\" 스큐턴 부인이 점잔 빼는 한숨을 쉬며 말했다, \"그런데 그것만은 끝내 누리지 못하게 될까 봐 두렵답니다. 사람들이 저를 놓아주질 않거든요. 하지만 은둔과 명상이야말로 제 뭐라더라 하는—\"\n\n\"낙원을 말씀하시는 거면, 엄마, 알아듣기 쉽게 그냥 그렇게 말씀하시는 편이 낫겠어요,\" 젊은 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 에디스,\" 스큐턴 부인이 응수했다, \"그 끔찍한 이름들은 죄다 너한테 의지하는 거 알잖니. 정말이지, 돔비 씨, 자연은 저를 아르카디아 사람으로 점지했나 봐요. 저는 사교계에서는 버려진 존재예요. 소[牛]가 제 열정이랍니다. 제가 늘 한숨지으며 바라 온 것은, 스위스의 농장으로 물러나 온통 소들에—그리고 도자기에 둘러싸여 사는 거였어요.\"\n\n이 기묘한 사물의 연결은, 실수로 도자기 가게에 들어간 그 유명한 황소의 기억을 떠올리게 했으나, 돔비 씨는 더없이 진지하게 그것을 받아들이며, 자연이란 의심할 바 없이 매우 존경할 만한 제도라는 견해를 내비쳤다.\n\n\"제가 원하는 건,\" 스큐턴 부인이 쪼글쪼글한 목을 꼬집으며 느릿느릿 말했다, \"마음이에요.\" 그녀가 그 말을 쓴 뜻으로는 아닐지언정, 다른 한 가지 뜻으로는 그것은 무섭도록 사실이었다. \"제가 원하는 건 솔직함, 신뢰, 격식의 줄어듦, 그리고 영혼의 더 자유로운 발휘예요. 우리는 정말이지 끔찍하게 인위적이거든요.\"\n\n실로 그러했다.\n\n\"한마디로,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"저는 어디서나 자연을 원해요. 그러면 더없이 매혹적일 텐데.\"\n\n\"자연이 지금 우리를 불러내고 있어요, 엄마, 준비되셨으면요,\" 젊은 부인이 그 아름다운 입술을 비틀며 말했다. 이 암시에 의자 등받이 너머로 일행을 살피고 있던 핼쑥한 시동이, 마치 땅이 그를 집어삼키기라도 한 듯 의자 뒤로 사라졌다.\n\n\"잠깐만, 위더스!\" 의자가 움직이기 시작하자 스큐턴 부인이 시동을 부르며 말했다. 가발과 꽃양배추 모양 꽃다발과 비단 양말을 갖춘 마부를 옛 시절에 부르던 그 나른한 위엄을 고스란히 담은 부름이었다. \"어디 머무시나요, 이 못된 양반?\"\n\n소령은 자기 친구 돔비와 함께 로열 호텔에 머물고 있었다.\n\n\"착하게 구실 때는 언제든 저녁에 저희를 보러 오셔도 좋아요,\" 스큐턴 부인이 혀짤배기소리로 말했다. \"돔비 씨께서 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 저희야 기쁘지요. 위더스, 가자!\"\n\n소령은 클레오파트라 모형을 본떠 조심스러운 무심함으로 바퀴 달린 의자의 가장자리에 얹힌 손가락 끝에 다시 한번 자기 푸른 입술을 갖다 댔고, 돔비 씨는 절을 했다. 손위 부인은 매우 우아한 미소와 소녀 같은 손짓으로 두 사람에게 영광을 베풀었고, 손아래 부인은 통상의 예의가 허락하는 가장 미미한 고갯짓으로 답했다.\n\n어머니의 주름진 얼굴, 그 위에 덧칠한 빛깔이 햇빛 아래서 차라리 빛깔이 없을 때보다 한없이 더 초췌하고 음울해 보이던 그 얼굴과, 우아한 자태와 꼿꼿한 자세를 지닌 딸의 도도한 미모, 그 마지막 모습은 소령과 돔비 씨 양쪽에게 그들을 뒤돌아보고 싶은 충동을 어찌나 무심결에 자아냈던지, 두 사람은 같은 순간에 동시에 돌아보았다. 시동은 거의 제 그림자만큼이나 비스듬히 기운 채, 느릿한 공성추처럼 언덕 위로 의자를 힘겹게 밀어 올리고 있었고, 클레오파트라의 보닛 꼭대기는 조금 전과 정확히 한 치도 다르지 않은 같은 구석에서 나풀거리고 있었으며, 미인은 혼자 조금 앞서 한가로이 거닐면서, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그 우아한 자태 전체로 모든 것과 모든 이를 향한 똑같은 지극한 무관심을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"내 말 좀 들어 보십시오, 선생,\" 두 사람이 다시 걸음을 옮기면서 소령이 말했다. \"조 백스톡이 좀 더 젊은 사람이었다면, 백스톡 부인으로 저 여인보다 더 마음에 둘 여자는 이 세상에 없을 겁니다. 어이쿠, 선생!\" 소령이 말했다, \"참으로 빼어난 여인이지요!\"\n\n\"딸 말이오?\" 돔비 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"조이 비가 순무 대가립니까, 돔비,\" 소령이 말했다, \"어머니를 두고 그런 말을 하게요?\"\n\n\"당신은 어머니에게 찬사를 보냈잖소,\" 돔비 씨가 응수했다.\n\n\"옛 연인이지요, 선생,\" 백스톡 소령이 낄낄 웃었다. \"지독하게 오래된 연인이에요. 제가 그저 비위를 맞춰 드리는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"내게는 더없이 기품 있는 분으로 보이던데,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"기품이라뇨, 선생,\" 소령이 우뚝 멈춰 서서 동행의 얼굴을 빤히 쳐다보며 말했다. \"스큐턴 부인 마님께서는, 선생, 작고하신 피닉스 경의 누이시고, 지금의 피닉스 경의 고모십니다. 그 집안은 부유하진 않아요—사실 가난하지요—그래서 부인은 얼마 안 되는 과부 급여로 살아갑니다. 하지만 혈통을 따지자면, 선생!\" 소령은 지팡이를 한 번 휘두르고는, 혈통을 따지자면 무엇이 나온다는 건지 도무지 말로 표현할 길이 없어 절망하며 다시 걸어 나갔다.\n\n\"내가 보니 당신은 딸을 그레인저 부인이라고 부릅디다,\" 돔비 씨가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했다.\n\n\"에디스 스큐턴이,\" 소령이 다시 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 그녀를 표시하느라 지팡이로 땅에 자국을 콕 찍으며 응수했다, \"(열여덟에) 우리 연대의 그레인저와 결혼했지요.\" 소령은 다시 한번 콕 찍어 그를 나타냈다. \"그레인저는, 선생,\" 소령이 그 마지막 가상의 초상을 톡톡 두드리고 머리를 힘주어 끄덕이며 말했다, \"우리 연대의 대령이었습니다. 마흔하나의 지독하게 잘생긴 사내였지요, 선생. 그는, 선생, 결혼한 지 두 해째에 죽었습니다.\" 소령은 고인이 된 그레인저를 나타내는 표시를 지팡이로 몸통을 푹 찔러 관통시키고는, 지팡이를 어깨에 둘러메고 다시 걸어 나갔다.\n\n\"그게 얼마나 오래전 일이오?\" 돔비 씨가 또 한 번 걸음을 멈추며 물었다.\n\n\"에디스 그레인저는, 선생,\" 소령이 한쪽 눈을 감고 머리를 한쪽으로 기울이며, 지팡이를 왼손으로 옮겨 쥐고 오른손으로는 셔츠의 주름 장식을 매만지며 대답했다, \"지금 이 순간 채 서른이 안 됐습니다. 그리고 제기랄, 선생,\" 소령이 다시 지팡이를 어깨에 둘러메고 다시 걸어 나가며 말했다, \"그녀는 둘도 없는 여인이지요!\"\n\n\"자식은 있었소?\" 이윽고 돔비 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"있었지요, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다. \"사내아이가 하나 있었습니다.\"\n\n돔비 씨의 두 눈이 땅으로 향했고, 얼굴에 그늘이 드리웠다.\n\n\"그 애는 물에 빠져 죽었지요, 선생,\" 소령이 이어 말했다. \"네댓 살 어린아이였을 때요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 돔비 씨가 고개를 들며 말했다.\n\n\"유모가 도무지 태워서는 안 될 배에 그 애를 태웠다가 배가 뒤집어진 거지요,\" 소령이 말했다. \"그게 그 애의 사연입니다. 에디스 그레인저는 여전히 에디스 그레인저예요. 하지만 이 질긴 늙은 조이 비가, 선생, 좀 더 젊고 좀 더 부유했더라면, 저 불멸의 본보기 같은 여인의 이름은 백스톡이 되었을 겁니다.\"\n\n소령은 두 어깨와 두 볼을 들썩이며, 그 말을 하면서 그 어느 때보다도 배불리 먹은 메피스토펠레스처럼 웃었다.\n\n\"부인이 거절하지 않았다는 전제하에서겠지요?\" 돔비 씨가 차갑게 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"백스톡 가문은 그런 종류의 장애물에는 익숙지 않습니다. 하기야 에디스가 도도하지만 않았더라면, 선생, 도도하지만 않았더라면 스무 번은 결혼했을 거라는 게 사실이긴 합니다만.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 그 얼굴로 보아, 그 점 때문에 그녀를 더 나쁘게 여기지는 않는 듯했다.\n\n\"따지고 보면 그것도 대단한 자질이지요,\" 소령이 말했다. \"맙소사, 그건 고귀한 자질입니다! 돔비! 당신도 도도한 분이고, 당신의 친구 늙은 조는 그 점 때문에 당신을 존경하지요, 선생.\"\n\n상황의 힘과 대화의 거스를 수 없는 흐름에 떠밀려 짜내듯 나온, 자기 동맹의 성품에 대한 이 헌사를 끝으로 소령은 그 화제를 마무리하고는, 자기가 그동안 화려한 여인들과 눈부신 미인들에게서 얼마나 사랑받고 떠받들어졌는지에 대한 일반적인 장광설로 슬그머니 빠져들었다.\n\n그다음 다음 날, 돔비 씨와 소령은 펌프룸에서 스큐턴 부인 마님과 그녀의 딸을 마주쳤고, 그 이튿날에는 처음 만났던 바로 그 장소 근처에서 다시 그들과 마주쳤다. 이렇게 통틀어 서너 번 만나고 나니, 소령이 어느 저녁 그곳을 방문하는 것이 옛 지인에 대한 그저 마땅한 예의가 되어 버렸다. 돔비 씨는 본디 방문할 생각이 없었으나, 소령이 이 뜻을 밝히자 자기도 기꺼이 동행하겠다고 말했다. 그래서 소령은 원주민에게 저녁 식사 전에 한 바퀴 돌아 자기와 돔비 씨의 인사를 전하면서, 만일 부인들이 따로 계시다면 바로 그날 저녁 두 부인을 방문하는 영광을 누리고 싶다는 말을 전하라고 했다. 그 전갈에 대한 답으로 원주민은 향수를 잔뜩 뿌린 아주 작은 쪽지 하나를 가지고 돌아왔는데, 스큐턴 부인 마님이 백스톡 소령에게 쓴 것으로, 짤막하게 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"당신은 정말 못된 곰이고 용서하고 싶지 않은 마음이 굴뚝같지만, 정말로 아주 착하게 군다면,\" 이 대목에는 밑줄이 그어져 있었다, \"오셔도 좋아요. 돔비 씨께 인사 전합니다(에디스도 함께요).\"\n\n* 원주민: 소령이 거느린 인도 출신 하인을 가리키는, 당시 식민지 시대의 비하적 호칭.\n스큐턴 부인 마님과 그녀의 딸 그레인저 부인은, 리밍턴에 있는 동안, 충분히 세련되고 충분히 값비싸지만 공간과 편의 면에서는 다소 옹색한 셋방에 머물렀다. 그래서 스큐턴 부인 마님은 침대에 누우면 발이 창문에, 머리가 벽난로에 가 있었고, 한편 스큐턴 부인 마님의 하녀는 응접실 안의 벽장에 거처를 두었는데, 그것이 어찌나 작던지, 그 안의 살림살이를 죄다 펼쳐 보이지 않으려면 마치 아름다운 뱀처럼 문 안팎으로 몸을 비틀며 드나들 수밖에 없었다. 핼쑥한 시동 위더스는 이웃 우유 가게의 기와 바로 밑에서 집 밖 잠을 잤고, 그 젊은 시시포스의 바위인 바퀴 달린 의자는 같은 낙농장에 딸린 헛간에서 밤을 보냈다. 그 헛간에서는 그 가게에 딸린 닭들이 갓 낳은 달걀을 생산했는데, 닭들은 부서진 당나귀 수레 위에 홰를 치고 앉아, 누가 봐도 그것이 거기서 자라난 일종의 나무라고 굳게 믿고 있는 듯했다.\n\n돔비 씨와 소령이 가 보니 스큐턴 부인은 클레오파트라처럼 소파의 쿠션들 사이에 자리 잡고 있었다. 매우 산뜻하게 차려입었으나, 세월도 시들게 하지 못했던 셰익스피어의 클레오파트라와는 분명 닮지 않았다. 위층으로 올라오는 길에 두 사람은 하프 소리를 들었으나, 그들의 방문이 알려지자 그것이 멈췄고, 에디스는 이제 그 어느 때보다도 더 아름답고 더 도도한 모습으로 그 곁에 서 있었다. 이 부인의 미모에는 한 가지 두드러진 특징이 있었으니, 그것은 그녀의 도움 없이, 아니 그녀의 의지를 거슬러 스스로를 뽐내고 내세우는 듯 보인다는 점이었다. 그녀는 자기가 아름답다는 것을 알았다. 달리 어쩔 도리가 없는 일이었다. 그러나 그녀는 자기 자신의 자존심으로 바로 자기 자신에게 도전하는 듯 보였다.\n\n오로지 자기에게는 무가치한 찬탄밖에 불러일으키지 못하는 그 매력들을 그녀가 하찮게 여기는 것인지, 아니면 그것들을 이렇게 다룸으로써 찬미자들에게 더욱 값지게 보이도록 꾀하는 것인지, 그 매력들을 값지게 여기는 이들은 좀처럼 멈춰 서서 헤아려 보는 법이 없었다.\n\n\"바라건대, 그레인저 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 그녀를 향해 한 걸음 다가서며 말했다, \"저희 때문에 연주를 멈추신 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\"댁들 때문이라뇨! 아, 아니에요!\"\n\n\"그럼 어째서 계속하지 않니, 내 사랑하는 에디스?\" 클레오파트라가 말했다.\n\n\"시작했을 때처럼 그만둔 거예요—그저 제 기분에 따라서요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하는 그녀의 더없이 절묘한 무심함—둔함이나 무감각함과는 거리가 먼, 도도한 의도로 날이 선 그 무심함은, 손으로 줄들을 무심히 쓸어내리고는 그 방의 그쪽 자리에서 물러나는 그 무신경함과 잘 어울려 돋보였다.\n\n\"그거 아세요, 돔비 씨,\" 그녀의 나른한 어머니가 손부채를 만지작거리며 말했다, \"이따금 내 사랑하는 에디스와 저는 정말로 거의 의견이 엇갈리곤 한답니다—\"\n\n\"가끔, 아주 엇갈리는 건 아니죠, 엄마?\" 에디스가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 결코 아주 엇갈리는 법은 없지, 내 사랑! 저런, 저런, 그랬다간 내 가슴이 찢어질 거야,\" 그녀의 어머니가 손부채로 딸을 가볍게 토닥이려고 힘없이 시도하며 응수했으나, 에디스는 그것을 맞이하려는 어떤 몸짓도 하지 않았다, \"—사소한 일들에서 지켜지는 그 케케묵은 격식들을 두고서 말이에요. 우리는 어째서 더 자연스럽지 못한 걸까요? 어머나! 우리 영혼 속에 심어진, 더없이 매혹적인 그 모든 동경과 북받침과 충동적인 떨림을 다 지니고 있으면서, 어째서 우리는 더 자연스럽지 못한 걸까요?\"\n\n돔비 씨는 참으로 옳은 말씀이라고, 참으로 옳다고 말했다.\n\n\"우리도 노력하면 더 자연스러울 수 있을 텐데, 안 그래요?\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 그것이 가능하리라고 생각했다.\n\n\"천만의 말씀입니다, 마님,\" 소령이 말했다. \"그럴 형편이 못 됩니다. 세상이 온통 제이 비들로—질기고 무뚝뚝한 늙은 조들로, 마님, 딱딱한 알을 밴 평범한 청어들로 채워지지 않는 한, 선생, 그럴 형편이 못 되지요. 어림없는 일입니다.\"\n\n\"이 못된 이교도 양반,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"잠자코 계세요.\"\n\n\"클레오파트라께서 명하시니,\" 소령이 손에 입을 맞추며 응수했다, \"안토니우스 백스톡은 따를 따름입니다.\"\n\n\"이 사람은 감수성이 없어요,\" 스큐턴 부인이 손부채를 매정하게 들어 올려 소령을 가려 버리며 말했다. \"공감이라곤 없죠. 한데 우리가 공감 말고 무엇을 위해 산답니까! 그 밖에 무엇이 그토록 매혹적이겠어요! 우리의 차디찬 이 땅 위에 비치는 그 한 줄기 햇살이 없다면,\" 스큐턴 부인이 자기 레이스 깃 장식을 매만지면서, 손목에서 위로 치켜든 자기의 드러난 야윈 팔의 효과를 흐뭇하게 살피며 말했다, \"우리가 도대체 그것을 어떻게 견딜 수 있겠어요? 한마디로, 매정한 양반!\" 손부채 너머로 소령을 흘끗 보며, \"저는 제 세상을 온통 마음으로 채우고 싶답니다. 그리고 신의란 어찌나 더없이 매혹적인지, 저는 당신이 그것을 어지럽히도록 두고 보지 않을 거예요, 알겠어요?\"\n\n소령은 세상이 온통 마음으로 채워지기를 요구하면서 동시에 온 세상의 마음을 죄다 자기 것으로 차지하려 드는 것은 클레오파트라로서 가혹한 처사라고 응답했고, 이에 클레오파트라는 아첨이란 자기로서는 참을 수 없는 것이며, 만일 그가 또 한 번 그런 투로 말을 거는 대담함을 보인다면 단연코 그를 집으로 돌려보내겠다는 점을 그에게 일깨워 줄 수밖에 없었다.\n\n이 무렵 핼쑥한 위더스가 차를 돌리고 있을 때, 돔비 씨가 다시 에디스에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"여기에는 어울리는 사람들이 그리 많지 않은 모양이지요?\" 돔비 씨가 자기 특유의 거창하고 신사다운 투로 말했다.\n\n\"없는 것 같아요. 저희는 아무도 만나지 않거든요.\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 스큐턴 부인이 소파에서 한마디 했다, \"지금 여기에는 저희가 어울리고 싶은 사람들이 없답니다.\"\n\n\"마음이 부족한 이들이라서요,\" 에디스가 미소 지으며 말했다. 미소라기보다는 그야말로 미소의 여명 같은 것이었으니, 그 빛과 어둠이 그토록 묘하게 뒤섞여 있었다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 에디스가 저를 놀려 대네요, 보세요!\" 그녀의 어머니가 고개를 흔들며 말했다. 그 고개는 더러 저절로 조금씩 떨렸는데, 마치 다이아몬드들에 맞서 이따금 중풍이 깜빡이기라도 하는 듯했다. \"이 못된 것 같으니!\"\n\n\"내가 틀리지 않았다면, 전에도 이곳에 와 보셨지요?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. 여전히 에디스에게였다.\n\n\"아, 여러 번요. 저희는 안 가 본 데가 없는 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"아름다운 고장이지요!\"\n\n\"그렇겠죠. 다들 그렇게 말하니까요.\"\n\n\"네 사촌 피닉스가 이곳에 푹 빠져 있잖니, 에디스,\" 어머니가 소파에서 끼어들었다.\n\n딸은 그 우아한 고개를 살짝 돌리고, 마치 사촌 피닉스야말로 이 세상에서 가장 마음 쓸 가치가 없는 사람이라는 듯 눈썹을 머리카락 한 올 너비만큼 치켜올렸다가, 다시 눈을 돔비 씨 쪽으로 돌렸다.\n\n\"제 안목의 명예를 위해서라도, 저는 이 일대에 싫증이 났기를 바라요,\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 만도 하시지요, 부인,\" 그가 응수했다. 이미 여러 점이 인근의 경관을 그린 것임을 알아본 갖가지 풍경화들을 흘끗 보며 한 말이었으니, 그것들은 방 안 여기저기에 잔뜩 흩어져 있었다, \"이 아름다운 작품들이 부인의 손에서 나온 것이라면 말이지요.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 자못 놀라우리만치 도도한 미모 그대로 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"그렇게 흥미를 끄는 작품들입니까?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"이게 부인의 작품인가요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그리고 연주도 하시는 건 이미 알았고요.\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"노래도 하시고요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n그녀는 이 모든 물음에 묘한 마지못함으로 대답했다. 그리고 그 미모에 딸린 것으로 이미 언급한 바 있는, 자기 자신에 맞서는 그 두드러진 태도로. 그러나 그녀는 당황하지 않았고, 완전히 침착했다. 또 대화를 피하고 싶어 하는 것 같지도 않았으니, 얼굴을—그리고 할 수 있는 한 태도까지도—그에게로 향하고 있었고, 그가 말이 없을 때도 계속 그러했다.\n\n\"적어도 권태를 막을 방편은 많이 가지고 계시는군요,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그것들이 얼마나 효과가 있든,\" 그녀가 응수했다, \"이제 그것들을 모두 아시게 됐네요. 저한테는 더 이상 없어요.\"\n\n\"그것들을 죄다 시험해 봐도 좋겠습니까?\" 돔비 씨가 엄숙한 정중함으로, 들고 있던 그림 한 장을 내려놓고 하프 쪽을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 물론이죠! 원하신다면요!\"\n\n그녀는 그렇게 말하며 일어서서, 어머니의 소파 곁을 지나가며 그쪽으로 위엄 있는 눈길을 던졌는데, 그 눈길은 한순간 지나가 버렸으나 (누군가 보았더라면) 무수한 표정을 담고 있었고, 그중에서도 미소 자체는 없이 그 미소의 여명만이 다른 모든 표정을 압도하고 있었다. 그러고는 방을 나갔다.\n\n이때쯤이면 완전히 용서받은 소령은 작은 탁자 하나를 클레오파트라에게로 끌어다 놓고, 그녀와 피켓 놀이를 하려고 앉아 있었다. 그 놀이를 모르는 돔비 씨는, 에디스가 돌아올 때까지 견식을 넓힐 겸 두 사람을 지켜보려고 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"우리, 음악을 좀 들을 수 있겠지요, 돔비 씨?\" 클레오파트라가 말했다.\n\n\"그레인저 부인께서 친절히 그러마고 약속해 주셨습니다,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 그거 아주 좋네요. 소령은 어떻게 하시려고요?\"\n\n\"안 합니다, 마님,\" 소령이 말했다. \"못 하지요.\"\n\n\"당신은 야만스러운 존재예요,\" 부인이 응수했다, \"내 패가 다 망가졌잖아요. 음악을 좋아하시나요, 돔비 씨?\"\n\n\"대단히 좋아합니다,\" 돔비 씨의 대답이었다.\n\n\"네. 음악은 참 좋지요,\" 클레오파트라가 자기 패를 보며 말했다. \"그 안에 마음이 가득하잖아요—이전 생의 존재에 대한 채 펼쳐지지 않은 기억들 같은 것 말이에요—그런 거 다요—정말이지 매혹적이지요. 그거 아세요,\" 클레오파트라가 발뒤꿈치가 위로 향한 채 자기 패에 들어와 있던 클럽 잭을 뒤집으며 점잔 빼는 소리로 말했다, \"만약 무언가가 제 삶에 종지부를 찍도록 저를 유혹할 수 있다면, 그건 이 모든 게 다 무엇에 관한 것이고 무슨 뜻인지 알아내고 싶은 호기심일 거예요. 우리에게 감춰진, 정말 약 오르는 신비가 어찌나 많은지요. 소령, 당신 차례예요!\"\n\n소령이 패를 냈다. 그리고 견문을 넓히자고 지켜보던 돔비 씨는 곧 끔찍한 혼란에 빠졌을 테지만, 그는 놀이에는 도무지 주의를 기울이지 않고 그 대신 에디스가 언제 돌아올지 궁금해하며 앉아 있었다.\n\n마침내 그녀가 와서 하프 앞에 앉았고, 돔비 씨는 일어나 그녀 곁에 서서 귀를 기울였다. 그는 음악에 별 취미가 없었고 그녀가 연주하는 곡조도 알지 못했으나, 그녀가 그 위로 몸을 숙이는 것을 보았고, 아마도 그 울리는 줄들 가운데서 어떤 멀리서 들려오는 그 자신만의 음악을 들었으리니, 그것은 그 무쇠 길의 괴물을 길들이고, 그것을 덜 가혹하게 만들어 주는 음악이었다.\n\n클레오파트라는 피켓을 할 때 실로 날카로운 눈을 지니고 있었다. 그 눈은 새의 눈처럼 반짝였고, 놀이에 붙박여 있지 않은 채 방을 이쪽 끝에서 저쪽 끝까지 꿰뚫었으며, 하프와 연주자와 듣는 이, 그 모든 것 위에서 번득였다.\n\n도도한 미인이 연주를 마치자, 그녀는 일어나서 조금 전과 똑같은 태도로 돔비 씨의 감사와 찬사를 받고는, 거의 한 치의 멈춤도 없이 피아노로 가 거기서 연주를 시작했다.\n\n에디스 그레인저, 그 노래만은 안 됩니다! 에디스 그레인저, 당신은 매우 아름답고, 건반 위를 누비는 당신의 손길은 눈부시며, 당신의 목소리는 깊고 풍부합니다. 그러나 그것만은, 그가 거들떠보지 않는 딸이 그의 죽은 아들에게 불러 주던 그 곡조만은 안 됩니다!\n\n아, 그는 그것을 알지 못한다. 또 설령 안다 한들, 그녀의 어떤 곡조가 이 완고한 사내의 마음을 움직이겠는가! 잠들어라, 외로운 플로렌스, 잠들어라! 밤이 어두워지고, 구름이 모여들어 우박을 쏟아부으려 위협한다 해도, 부디 네 꿈속만은 평안하기를!\n\n# CHAPTER XXII. A Trifle of Management by Mr Carker the Manager\n\n지배인 카커 씨가 평소처럼 매끈하고 부드럽게 자기 책상 앞에 앉아, 그가 직접 뜯어보도록 따로 남겨 둔 편지들을 읽고 있었다. 이따금 그 편지의 업무상 취지가 요구하는 메모와 참조 사항을 그 뒷면에 덧붙이기도 하고, 또 회사의 여러 부서로 나눠 보내기 위해 작은 무더기로 분류해 두기도 했다. 그날 아침 우편물이 잔뜩 들어와서, 지배인 카커 씨는 할 일이 꽤 많았다.\n\n이렇게 일하는 사람의 전반적인 동작은—손에 든 한 묶음의 서류를 잠시 멈춰 훑어보고, 그것을 여러 몫으로 나누어 돌리고, 또 다른 묶음을 집어 들어 미간을 찌푸리고 입술을 오므린 채 그 내용을 살피고—나누고, 분류하고, 번갈아 가며 궁리하는 모습은—카드놀이를 하는 사람과의 어떤 기이한 닮음을 손쉽게 떠올리게 했다. 지배인 카커 씨의 얼굴은 그런 상상과 잘 어울렸다. 그것은 자기 패를 조심스럽게 궁리하는 사람의 얼굴이었다. 그 놀이의 강점과 약점을 모조리 제 것으로 만드는 사람, 카드가 자기 주변에 떨어지는 대로 마음속에 기록해 두고, 그 위에 무엇이 있는지, 무엇이 빠졌는지, 무엇을 이루었는지 정확히 아는 사람, 다른 사람들이 무슨 패를 쥐고 있는지 교활하게 알아내면서도 결코 제 패는 드러내지 않는 사람의 얼굴이었다.\n\n편지들은 여러 나라 말로 쓰여 있었지만, 지배인 카커 씨는 그것을 모두 읽어 냈다. 돔비와 아들 회사의 사무실에 그가 읽을 수 있는 것이 무엇이라도 있었다면, 카드 한 벌에서 패 하나가 모자란 셈이 되었을 것이다. 그는 거의 한눈에 읽어 내면서, 일을 진행하는 동안 한 편지를 다른 편지와, 한 거래를 다른 거래와 결합하고, 무더기에 새로운 사안을 더해 갔다—마치 한 사람이 카드를 보자마자 알아채고, 그것이 뒤집힌 뒤 그 조합을 마음속에서 풀어내는 것과 흡사했다. 동업자에게는 너무 깊고, 적수에게는 훨씬 더 깊은 무언가를 품은 채, 지배인 카커 씨는 채광창을 통해 비스듬히 내리쬐는 햇살 속에 앉아, 홀로 자기 놀이를 했다.\n\n비록 카드놀이를 하는 것이 고양이 족속의 야생적이거나 가정적인 본능에 속하지는 않지만, 지배인 카커 씨는 발끝부터 정수리까지 고양이 같았다. 그가 책상과 바닥 위에 비치는 한 줄기 여름 햇빛과 온기 속에서 일광욕을 즐기는 모습은, 마치 그 빛줄기가 비뚤어진 해시계 판이고 그 위의 유일한 형상이 그 자신인 듯했다. 머리카락과 구레나룻은 늘 빛깔이 부족했지만, 짙은 햇살 속에서는 평소보다 더 흐릿해서, 모래빛 얼룩무늬 고양이의 털과 한층 더 비슷해 보였다. 길고 곱게 다듬어 날카롭게 간 손톱에다, 한 점 티끌도 타고난 듯 싫어해서, 때로는 동작을 멈추고 떨어지는 먼지 알갱이를 지켜보다가 그것을 매끈하고 흰 손이나 윤기 나는 아마포에서 닦아 내곤 했다. 거동은 음흉하고, 이는 날카롭고, 발은 부드럽고, 눈은 빈틈없고, 혀는 기름지고, 마음은 잔인하고, 습관은 까다로운 지배인 카커 씨는, 마치 쥐구멍 앞에서 기다리기라도 하듯, 깔끔한 끈기와 인내로 자기 일에 매달려 앉아 있었다.\n\n마침내 편지들이 모두 처리되었는데, 다만 특별한 청중을 위해 따로 남겨 둔 한 통은 예외였다. 좀 더 은밀한 서신들을 서랍에 넣어 잠근 뒤, 지배인 카커 씨는 종을 울렸다.\n\n\"왜 자네가 응하는가?\" 그가 자기 형을 맞이하는 인사였다.\n\n\"심부름꾼이 나가 있어서, 그다음이 나야,\" 순종적인 대답이었다.\n\n\"자네가 그다음이라고?\" 지배인이 중얼거렸다. \"그래! 나로서는 영광스러운 일이군! 자!\"\n\n뜯어 본 편지 무더기를 가리키고는, 그는 안락의자에 앉은 채 경멸하듯 몸을 돌려, 손에 쥐고 있던 그 한 통의 봉인을 뜯었다.\n\n\"성가시게 해서 미안하네, 제임스,\" 형이 그 편지들을 모으며 말했다, \"하지만—\"\n\n\"오! 뭔가 할 말이 있군. 그럴 줄 알았어. 그래서?\"\n\n지배인 카커 씨는 눈을 들거나 형 쪽으로 돌리지 않고, 편지를 펴 보지도 않으면서 거기에 시선을 둔 채로 있었다.\n\n\"그래서?\" 그가 날카롭게 되풀이했다.\n\n\"해리엇이 마음에 걸려서.\"\n\n\"무슨 해리엇? 어떤 해리엇? 난 그런 이름의 사람을 모르네.\"\n\n\"그 애가 건강이 안 좋아, 요즘 들어 아주 많이 변했어.\"\n\n\"걔야 아주 여러 해 전에 무척 많이 변했지,\" 지배인이 응수했다. \"그게 내가 할 말의 전부야.\"\n\n\"한 번만 내 말을 들어 준다면—\"\n\n\"내가 왜 자네 말을 들어야 하지, 존 형?\" 지배인이 그 두 단어에 비꼬는 강세를 실어, 고개를 치켜들면서도 눈은 들지 않은 채 응수했다. \"분명히 말해 두지, 해리엇 카커는 여러 해 전에 자기 두 오라비 사이에서 선택을 했어. 후회할 수는 있겠지만, 그 선택은 감수해야지.\"\n\n\"오해하지 마. 걔가 후회한다고 말하는 게 아니야. 그런 걸 넌지시 비추는 건 내 쪽에서 새까만 배은망덕이지,\" 상대가 응수했다. \"하지만 믿어 줘, 제임스, 나도 너만큼이나 그 애의 희생이 안타까워.\"\n\n\"나만큼?\" 지배인이 외쳤다. \"나만큼이라고?\"\n\n\"그 애의 선택을—네가 그 애의 선택이라 부르는 것을—네가 화내는 만큼이나 나는 안타까워하고 있어,\" 동생이 말했다.\n\n\"화낸다?\" 상대가 이를 한껏 드러내며 되풀이했다.\n\n\"불쾌해한다고. 네가 좋아하는 단어 무엇이든. 내 말뜻은 알잖아. 너를 욕보일 생각은 없어.\"\n\n\"자네가 하는 모든 일에 욕보임이 있지,\" 그의 동생이 별안간 눈살을 찌푸리며 그를 흘끗 보면서 응수했다. 그 찌푸림은 곧 앞서보다 더 넓은 미소로 바뀌었다. \"그 서류들은 가져가 주게. 부디. 난 바쁘니까.\"\n\n그의 정중함은 노여움보다 훨씬 더 베이는 듯해서, 동생은 문 쪽으로 갔다. 그러나 문 앞에서 멈춰 서서 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"네가 처음 정당한 분노를 터뜨리고 내가 처음 망신을 당했을 때, 해리엇이 너에게 나를 변호해 주려고 애썼으나 헛수고였지. 그리고 그 애가 너를 떠나, 제임스, 무너진 내 운명을 따라와서, 잘못된 애정으로 망가진 오라비에게 자신을 바쳤을 때—나에게는 그 애 말고는 아무도 없었고, 길을 잃었으니까—그때 그 애는 젊고 예뻤어. 네가 지금 그 애를 볼 수 있다면—가서 본다면—그 애가 너의 감탄과 연민을 자아낼 거라고 생각해.\"\n\n지배인은 고개를 기울이고 이를 드러냈는데, 마치 무심한 잡담에 대답이라도 하듯, \"저런! 그런가?\" 하는 표정이었으나, 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"그 시절 우리는 생각했지, 너도 나도 둘 다, 그 애가 일찍 결혼해서 행복하고 가뿐한 삶을 살리라고,\" 상대가 말을 이었다. \"오, 그 애가 그 희망들을 얼마나 명랑하게 내던졌는지, 자기가 택한 길을 얼마나 명랑하게 걸어 나아가며 단 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않았는지 네가 안다면, 너는 다시는 그 애 이름이 네 귀에 낯설다고 말할 수 없을 거야. 결코!\"\n\n다시 지배인은 고개를 기울이고 이를 드러내며, \"참으로 놀랍군! 자네가 나를 아주 놀라게 하는걸!\" 하고 말하는 듯했다. 그리고 다시 한마디도 입에 담지 않았다.\n\n\"계속해도 되겠나?\" 존 카커가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"가던 길을?\" 미소 짓는 동생이 응수했다. \"그래 준다면 고맙겠네.\"\n\n존 카커가 한숨을 쉬며 천천히 문밖으로 나서려는데, 동생의 목소리가 그를 문지방 위에 잠시 붙들어 두었다.\n\n\"그 애가 명랑하게 제 길을 떠나갔고 또 그렇게 가고 있다면,\" 그가 아직 펴지 않은 편지를 책상에 던지고 두 손을 주머니에 단단히 찔러 넣으며 말했다, \"내가 내 길을 똑같이 명랑하게 가고 있다고 그 애에게 전하게. 그 애가 단 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않았다면, 나는 가끔 뒤돌아본다고 전하게. 그 애가 자네 편을 들었던 일을 떠올리려고 말이야. 그리고 내 결심은 닳아 없어지기가—\" 여기서 그는 아주 달콤하게 미소 지었다, \"—대리석만큼이나 어렵다고.\"\n\n\"난 자네에 관해서는 그 애에게 아무것도 전하지 않아. 우리는 자네 이야기는 절대 하지 않으니까. 일 년에 한 번, 자네 생일에, 해리엇은 늘 '제임스의 이름을 떠올리고 그가 행복하기를 빌자'고 말하지만, 그 이상은 말하지 않아.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 말을,\" 상대가 응수했다, \"자네 자신에게나 들려주게. 아무리 자주 되풀이해도 모자라지. 나에게 말할 때 그 화제를 피하라는 교훈으로 말이야. 나는 해리엇 카커라는 사람을 몰라. 그런 사람은 없어. 자네에게는 누이가 있을지 모르지. 그 애를 끔찍이 아끼게나. 나에게는 그런 사람 없으니까.\"\n\n지배인 카커 씨는 다시 편지를 집어 들고는, 거짓 정중함의 미소를 띠며 문 쪽으로 그것을 흔들어 보였다. 형이 물러가는 동안 그것을 펴고, 형이 방을 나갈 때 어둡게 그 뒤를 노려본 뒤, 그는 다시 한번 안락의자에서 몸을 돌려, 그 내용을 부지런히 정독하기 시작했다.\n\n그것은 그의 위대한 우두머리인 돔비 씨의 필체였고, 리밍턴에서 부친 것이었다. 다른 모든 편지는 빨리 읽는 그였지만, 카커는 이것은 천천히 읽었다. 진행하면서 단어 하나하나를 저울질하고, 머릿속의 이를 모조리 동원해 거기에 매달렸다. 한 번 끝까지 읽고 나서, 그는 그것을 다시 뒤집어 다음 구절들을 골라냈다. \"나는 이 변화로 효과를 보고 있으며, 아직 돌아갈 시기를 정할 마음이 없소.\" \"카커, 한번 이리로 내려와서 나를 만나, 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 직접 알려 주도록 조처해 주면 좋겠소.\" \"젊은 게이에 관해 자네에게 말하는 걸 빼먹었군. 만일 '아들이자 후계자'호로 떠나지 않았거나, 혹은 그 배가 아직 부두에 정박해 있다면, 다른 젊은이를 하나 임명하고 그자는 당분간 시내에 두도록 하시오. 나는 아직 결정하지 못했소.\" \"이런, 그건 불운한데!\" 지배인 카커 씨가 마치 입이 고무로 만들어진 듯 쩍 벌리며 말했다. \"그자는 멀리 가 버렸으니 말이야.\"\n\n추신에 적힌 그 구절이 다시 한번 그의 주의와 이를 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 그가 말했다, \"내 친애하는 벗 커틀 선장이 그날의 뒷물결에 끌려가는 것에 대해 뭔가 말했던 것 같은데. 그자가 그렇게 멀리 가 버렸다니 안타까운 노릇이군!\"\n\n그는 편지를 다시 접고는, 그것을 가지고 만지작거리며 앉아 있었다. 그것을 책상 위에 세로로도 가로로도 세워 보고, 사방으로 이리저리 뒤집어 보면서—아마 그 내용물에 대해서도 거의 똑같은 짓을 하고 있었을 것이다—그때 심부름꾼 퍼치 씨가 문을 부드럽게 두드리고는, 발끝으로 걸어 들어와, 한 걸음 한 걸음마다 마치 절하는 것이 인생의 낙이라도 되는 듯 몸을 굽히면서, 서류 몇 장을 책상에 내려놓았다.\n\n\"바쁘신 줄로 알아도 되겠습니까, 나리?\" 퍼치 씨가 두 손을 비비며, 마치 이런 분 앞에서 고개를 쳐들 자격이 없다고 느끼는 사람처럼, 그래서 되도록 고개를 멀찍이 비켜 두려는 사람처럼, 공손히 머리를 한쪽으로 기울이며 물었다.\n\n\"누가 나를 찾는가?\"\n\n\"그게 말입니다, 나리,\" 퍼치 씨가 부드러운 목소리로 말했다, \"지금으로선 정말 말씀드릴 만한 사람은 아무도 없습니다, 나리. 선박 계기 제작자 길스 씨가 들렀습니다, 나리, 약간의 대금 문제 때문이라고 합니다만, 제가 그분께 나리께서 몇 겹으로 바쁘시다고, 몇 겹으로 말입니다, 일러 드렸습니다.\"\n\n퍼치 씨는 손등에 대고 한 번 기침을 하고는, 추가 지시를 기다렸다.\n\n\"또 다른 사람은?\"\n\n\"그게요, 나리,\" 퍼치 씨가 말했다, \"제 입으로 감히 다른 누가 있다고 말씀드릴 주제는 못 됩니다만, 어제도 여기 왔던, 그리고 지난주에도 왔던 바로 그 어린 녀석이, 나리, 근처를 어슬렁거리고 있습니다. 그리고, 나리,\" 퍼치 씨가 문을 닫으려고 멈추며 덧붙였다, \"그 녀석이 안마당에서 참새들한테 휘파람을 불고 또 그놈들이 자기한테 대답하게 만드는 꼴을 보면, 영 사업가답지 못해 보입니다.\"\n\n\"그자가 일거리를 원한다고 했다지, 그렇지 않은가, 퍼치?\" 카커 씨가 의자에 기대앉아 그 직원을 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"그게요, 나리,\" 퍼치 씨가 다시 손등에 대고 기침을 하며 말했다, \"그 녀석 말로는 분명히 일자리가 필요하다는 거였고요, 자기가 낚싯대와 낚싯줄로 고기를 잡는 데 익숙하니 부두 쪽에서 무슨 일이 생길 만하다고 여겼다는데요, 하지만—\" 퍼치 씨는 정말이지 매우 미심쩍게 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"올 때마다 뭐라고 하던가?\" 카커 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"실은, 나리,\" 퍼치 씨가 손등에 대고 또 한 번 기침을 했는데, 이는 다른 말이 떠오르지 않을 때 겸손의 표현으로 늘 쓰는 방편이었다, \"그 녀석 말은 대체로, 자기는 황송하오나 신사분들 가운데 한 분을 뵙고 싶다는 거고, 벌어먹고 살고 싶다는 겁니다. 하지만 보십시오, 나리,\" 퍼치가 목소리를 속삭임으로 낮추고는, 그 비밀의 침범할 수 없는 성질대로, 이미 닫힌 문이 더 닫히기라도 할 듯 손과 무릎으로 문을 한 번 더 밀면서 말했다, \"저런 천한 녀석이 여기에 어슬렁거리며 와서, 제 어미가 우리 회사 어린 도련님의 유모였다느니, 그 덕에 우리 회사가 자기한테 기회를 줬으면 한다느니 하는 건, 나리, 정말 참아 주기 어렵습니다. 분명히 말씀드립니다만, 나리,\" 퍼치 씨가 말했다, \"비록 퍼치 부인이 그 무렵에, 우리가 감히 식구로 들인 것 중에 더없이 잘 자라는 어린 계집아이에게 젖을 먹이고 있었습니다만, 그렇다고 제가 감히 그 사람이 젖을 먹일 능력이 있다는 둥 넌지시 비출 만큼 함부로 굴지는 않았을 겁니다, 결코 그러지 않았을 거예요!\"\n\n카커 씨는 상어처럼 그를 보고 씩 웃었으나, 멍하니 생각에 잠긴 듯한 태도였다.\n\n\"혹시,\" 퍼치 씨가 잠시 침묵하고 또 한 번 기침한 뒤 조심스레 의견을 냈다, \"제가 그 녀석한테, 여기서 다시 한 번이라도 눈에 띄면 구금시키겠다고 일러 주고, 또 그대로 지키는 게 상책이 아닐는지요! 몸으로 겪는 두려움으로 말하자면,\" 퍼치 씨가 말했다, \"저 자신이 천성적으로 어찌나 겁이 많은지요, 나리, 게다가 퍼치 부인 상태 때문에 신경이 어찌나 곤두서 있는지, 저는 진술서를 손쉽게 작성할 수 있을 정도입니다.\"\n\n\"그자를 한번 보세, 퍼치,\" 카커 씨가 말했다. \"데려오게!\"\n\n\"네, 나리. 죄송합니다만, 나리,\" 퍼치 씨가 문 앞에서 망설이며 말했다, \"그 녀석은, 나리, 행색이 거칩니다.\"\n\n\"상관없네. 거기 있으면 데려오게. 길스 씨는 곧바로 만나겠네. 기다려 달라고 청하게.\"\n\n퍼치 씨는 절을 했다. 그러고는 일주일 동안 돌아오지 않을 사람처럼 정확하고 조심스럽게 문을 닫고, 안마당의 참새들 사이로 자기 임무를 찾아 나섰다. 그가 나가 있는 동안, 카커 씨는 벽난로 앞에서 그가 좋아하는 자세를 취하고 서서 문을 바라보았다. 아랫입술을 미소 속으로 말아 넣어 윗니를 한 줄 통째로 드러내며, 묘하게 웅크려 노리는 듯한 모습이었다.\n\n심부름꾼은 곧 돌아왔는데, 그 뒤로 무거운 부츠 한 켤레가 마치 상자처럼 복도를 따라 쿵쿵거리며 따라왔다. \"어서 따라와!\"라는 격식 없는 말—그의 입에서는 매우 이례적인 소개 형식이었다—과 함께, 퍼치 씨는 열다섯 살쯤 된 다부진 사내아이를 그 앞으로 안내했다. 둥글고 붉은 얼굴에, 둥글고 매끈한 머리, 둥근 검은 눈, 둥근 사지, 둥근 몸뚱이를 한 그 아이는, 전반적인 둥그스름함을 완성하기라도 하듯, 챙이라곤 한 조각도 없는 둥근 모자를 손에 들고 있었다.\n\n카커 씨의 끄덕임에 순종하여, 퍼치는 방문객을 그 신사 앞에 마주 세우자마자 물러갔다. 두 사람이 단둘이 얼굴을 맞대는 순간, 카커 씨는 한마디 예비도 없이 그의 목덜미를 움켜쥐고, 머리가 어깨 위에서 헐거워질 것 같을 때까지 흔들어 댔다.\n\n그 아이는 놀라움 한가운데서도, 자기 목을 조르고 있는, 그토록 흰 이가 많은 신사를 멍하니 노려보지 않을 수 없었고, 또 사무실 벽들도 노려보았다. 마치 자기가 목 졸려 죽는다면, 그 무단 침입의 대가로 이토록 혹독한 벌을 치르고 있는 그 신비로운 것들에 마지막 시선을 던지겠다고 작정한 듯했다. 그러다 마침내 그는 이렇게 내뱉었다—\n\n\"이봐요, 나리! 좀 놓아주세요, 네!\"\n\n\"놓아 달라고!\" 카커 씨가 말했다. \"뭐! 내가 너를 잡았지, 그렇지?\" 그 점은 의심의 여지가 없었고, 더구나 단단히 잡고 있었다. \"이 개 같은 녀석,\" 카커 씨가 악문 턱 사이로 말했다, \"목을 졸라 죽여 버리겠다!\"\n\n바일러는 흐느끼며, 자기가 뭐 어쨌다고요? 오, 안 그랬어요—대체 자기가 뭘 했다는 거예요—왜 자기 같은 덩치를 골라 졸라 죽이지 않고 자기를 괴롭히느냐는 둥 했지만, 바일러는 자신을 맞이한 그 별난 방식에 기가 꺾여 버렸다. 머리가 가만해지고 그 신사의 얼굴을, 아니 차라리 그 이를 들여다보며 자기를 향해 이를 드러내고 으르렁대는 것을 보자, 그는 사내다움을 잊을 만큼 그만 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"전 나리께 아무 짓도 안 했어요, 나리,\" 바일러가 말했다. 달리 부르면 롭, 또 달리 부르면 그라인더, 그리고 언제나 투들이었다.\n\n\"이 젊은 악당 놈아!\" 카커 씨가 천천히 그를 놓아주고는, 좋아하는 자세로 한 걸음 물러서며 응수했다. \"감히 여기 올 생각을 하다니, 무슨 속셈이냐?\"\n\n\"나쁜 뜻은 없었어요, 나리,\" 롭이 한 손을 목에 대고, 다른 손등을 눈에 갖다 대며 훌쩍였다. \"다시는 안 올게요, 나리. 그냥 일거리가 필요했을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"일거리라니, 이 어린 카인 같은 녀석아!\" 카커 씨가 그를 찬찬히 훑어보며 되풀이했다. \"넌 런던에서 제일가는 게으른 부랑자 아니냐?\"\n\n그 비난은 투들 2세를 무척이나 동요시켰지만, 그의 성격에 너무도 들어맞는 것이라, 그는 부인할 말을 한마디도 하지 못했다. 그래서 그는 겁먹고, 스스로 죄를 인정하며, 뉘우치는 기색으로 그 신사를 바라보며 서 있었다. 그를 바라본다는 것으로 말하자면, 그는 카커 씨에게 홀려 버려서 둥근 눈을 한순간도 그에게서 떼지 못했다는 점을 짚어 둘 만하다.\n\n\"넌 도둑 아니냐?\" 카커 씨가 두 손을 등 뒤 주머니에 넣은 채 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 나리,\" 롭이 항변했다.\n\n\"도둑이지!\" 카커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 아니에요, 나리,\" 롭이 훌쩍였다. \"절 믿어 주신다면, 전 도둑질 같은 건 한 번도 안 했어요, 나리. 새 잡고 도보 시합 다니기 시작하면서부터 제가 빗나가기 시작한 건 압니다, 나리. 사람들은 노래하는 새가 순진한 동무라고 생각할지 모르겠지만,\" 투들 2세가 회개의 격정을 터뜨리며 말했다, \"그 자그마한 미물들 속에 무슨 해악이 들었는지, 또 그놈들이 사람을 어디까지 끌어내리는지는 아무도 모릅니다.\"\n\n그놈들은 그를 몹시 낡아 빠진 골덴 윗옷과 바지, 목가리개처럼 생긴 유난히 작은 빨간 조끼, 사이로 드러나는 파란 체크무늬 천, 그리고 앞서 말한 모자로까지 끌어내린 듯했다.\n\n\"그놈의 새들이 절 제멋대로 다룬 뒤로 집에 들어간 게 스무 번도 안 돼요,\" 롭이 말했다, \"그게 열 달째예요. 다들 절 보면 비참해하는데 어떻게 집에 들어가겠어요! 제 생각엔,\" 바일러가 아예 엉엉 울며 외투 소맷부리로 눈을 문질러 댔다, \"제가 몇 번이고 물에 빠져 죽지 않은 게 신기할 정도라니까요.\"\n\n이 마지막의, 그 좀처럼 해내기 힘든 일을 결국 못 해냈다는 데 대한 놀라움의 표현까지 포함하여, 그 아이는, 마치 카커 씨의 이가 그것을 자기에게서 끌어내기라도 하는 듯, 그 매혹의 포대가 활짝 가동되는 한 무엇 하나 숨길 힘이 없다는 듯이 말해 버렸다.\n\n\"넌 참 훌륭한 도련님이로구나!\" 카커 씨가 그를 향해 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"네 놈을 위해 삼씨가 뿌려져 있다, 이 멋진 녀석아!\"\n\n* 삼씨: 교수형에 쓰는 밧줄(삼)을 만들 씨앗이라는 뜻으로, 교수형에 처해질 운명임을 빗댄 말.\n\"정말이지, 나리,\" 가엾은 바일러가 다시 엉엉 울며 또 외투 소맷부리에 의지하여 응수했다, \"어떤 때는 그게 자라 버려도 상관없을 것 같아요. 제 불운은 전부 학교 빼먹는 데서 시작됐어요, 나리. 하지만 빼먹는 것 말고 제가 뭘 할 수 있었겠어요?\"\n\n\"뭘 말고라고?\" 카커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"빼먹는 거요, 나리. 학교를 빼먹는 거요.\"\n\n\"학교에 가는 척하면서 안 가는 걸 말하는 거냐?\" 카커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리, 그게 빼먹는 거예요, 나리,\" 한때 그라인더였던 아이가 몹시 감동한 듯 응수했다. \"거기 가려고 하면 길거리에서 쫓겨 다녔고요, 나리, 막상 가면 두들겨 맞았어요. 그래서 빼먹고, 숨어 다녔고, 그게 시작이었어요.\"\n\n\"그러니까 너는 나에게,\" 카커 씨가 다시 그의 목덜미를 잡아 팔 길이만큼 멀찍이 들고는 몇 초 동안 말없이 살피며 말했다, \"일자리를 원한다, 이 말이지?\"\n\n\"한번 써 주시면 감사하겠습니다, 나리,\" 투들 2세가 가냘프게 응수했다.\n\n지배인 카커 씨는 그를 한쪽 구석으로 밀어붙였다—그 아이는 숨도 거의 쉬지 못하면서 조용히 순응했고, 단 한 번도 그의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않았다—그러고는 종을 울렸다.\n\n\"길스 씨에게 이리 오시라고 전하게.\"\n\n퍼치 씨는 구석에 있는 그 인물을 보고 놀라움이나 알아본 기색을 내비치기에는 너무 공손했다. 그리고 솔 아저씨가 곧바로 나타났다.\n\n\"길스 씨!\" 카커가 미소 지으며 말했다, \"앉으십시오. 안녕하셨습니까? 여전히 건강하시겠지요?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리,\" 솔 아저씨가 말하면서 수첩을 꺼내, 지폐 몇 장을 건넸다. \"몸은 노쇠한 것 말고는 아무 탈 없습니다. 스물다섯입니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"길스 씨, 당신은 시간에 정확하고 정밀하시군요,\" 미소 짓는 지배인이 자기의 수많은 서랍 가운데 하나에서 서류 한 장을 꺼내 거기에 배서를 하면서—솔 아저씨가 그 위로 들여다보는 동안—응수했다, \"꼭 당신네 정밀시계 중 하나처럼요. 아주 정확합니다.\"\n\n\"명단을 보니 '아들이자 후계자'호의 소식이 전해진 바 없는 것 같습니다만, 나리,\" 솔 아저씨가 평소의 떨림에 약간을 더한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"'아들이자 후계자'호의 소식은 전해진 바 없습니다,\" 카커가 응수했다. \"거센 폭풍우가 있었던 모양이고, 길스 씨, 그 배는 아마 항로에서 밀려 나갔을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하늘에 비옵건대, 그 배가 무사하기를!\" 늙은 솔이 말했다.\n\n\"하늘에 비옵건대, 그 배가 무사하기를!\" 카커 씨가 그 특유의 소리 없는 방식으로 맞장구쳤는데, 그 모습은 눈썰미 있는 어린 투들을 다시 한번 떨게 만들었다. \"길스 씨,\" 그가 의자에 몸을 젖히며 소리 내어 덧붙였다, \"조카가 무척 그리우시겠군요?\"\n\n곁에 서 있던 솔 아저씨는 고개를 가로젓고 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"길스 씨,\" 카커가 부드러운 손으로 자기 입가를 매만지며, 계기 제작자의 얼굴을 올려다보면서 말했다, \"지금 같은 때에 당신 가게에 젊은 친구가 하나 있으면 적적함을 달래 줄 테고, 또 당분간 한 사람에게 거처를 내준다면 나에게도 호의를 베푸시는 게 될 텐데요. 아, 물론,\" 노인이 무슨 말을 하려는지 미리 짐작하고 그가 재빨리 덧붙였다, \"거기에 거래할 일이 많지 않다는 건 압니다. 하지만 그자에게 가게 청소를 시키고, 계기들을 닦게 하고, 막일을 시키면 되지요, 길스 씨. 바로 저 녀석입니다!\"\n\n솔 길스는 안경을 이마에서 눈으로 끌어내려, 구석에 똑바로 서 있는 투들 2세를 보았다. 그 아이의 머리는 (늘 그렇듯) 막 찬물 한 동이에서 끄집어낸 것 같은 모양새였고, 작은 조끼는 감정의 동요 속에서 빠르게 오르내렸으며, 두 눈은 자기의 주인이 될 사람은 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고 카커 씨에게 골똘히 고정되어 있었다.\n\n\"그에게 거처를 내주시겠습니까, 길스 씨?\" 지배인이 말했다.\n\n늙은 솔은 그 문제에 그다지 열성적이지는 않았으나, 아무리 사소한 것이라도 카커 씨에게 호의를 베풀 기회가 생겨 기쁘다고, 그런 문제에서 그분의 바람은 곧 명령이라고 대답했다. 그리고 나무 해군 소위 후보생은 카커 씨가 골라 보낸 손님이라면 누구든 제 선실에 맞아들이는 것을 행복으로 여길 것이라고 했다.\n\n카커 씨는 잇몸 위아래까지 드러냈는데, 그것은 눈썰미 있는 투들 2세를 점점 더 떨게 만들었다. 그러고는 더없이 상냥한 태도로 계기 제작자의 정중함에 화답했다.\n\n\"그럼 그자는 그렇게 처리하지요, 길스 씨,\" 그가 일어서서 노인의 손을 잡고 흔들며 대답했다, \"내가 그자를 어떻게 할지, 또 그자가 무얼 받아 마땅한지 마음을 정할 때까지요. 내가 그자에 대해 책임을 진다고 여기는 만큼, 길스 씨,\" 여기서 그는 롭을 향해 활짝 미소를 지었고, 롭은 그 앞에서 떨었다, \"그자를 빈틈없이 지켜봐 주시고, 그 행실을 나에게 보고해 주시면 고맙겠습니다. 오늘 오후 말을 타고 집으로 가는 길에 그자 부모에게—점잖은 사람들이지요—한두 가지 물어보아, 그자가 자기 자신에 대해 한 이야기 중 몇 가지 세세한 점을 확인하겠습니다. 그게 끝나면, 길스 씨, 내일 아침 그자를 당신께 보내지요. 안녕히 계십시오!\"\n\n작별할 때 그의 미소는 어찌나 이로 가득했던지, 늙은 솔을 어리둥절하게 만들고 어쩐지 막연히 불편하게 했다. 그는 사납게 날뛰는 바다와 침몰하는 배, 물에 빠지는 사람들, 결코 빛을 보지 못한 묵은 마데이라 술 한 병, 그 밖의 음울한 것들을 생각하며 집으로 갔다.\n\n\"자, 얘야!\" 카커 씨가 어린 투들의 어깨에 손을 얹고 방 한가운데로 끌어내며 말했다. \"내 말 들었지?\"\n\n롭이 \"네, 나리\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"아마 알아들었겠지,\" 그의 후원자가 말을 이었다, \"만일 네가 나를 속이거나 나에게 농간을 부린다면, 차라리 이리로 오기 전에 단번에 정말로 물에 빠져 죽었던 편이 나았으리란 걸?\"\n\n지적 습득의 어느 분야에서도, 롭이 그 점보다 더 잘 알아듣는 듯한 것은 없었다.\n\n\"네가 나에게,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"무엇 하나라도 거짓말을 했다면, 다시는 내 앞에 얼씬도 하지 마라. 그렇지 않거든, 오늘 오후 네 어미 집 근처 어딘가에서 나를 기다리고 있도록 해라. 나는 여길 다섯 시에 떠나, 말을 타고 그리로 갈 거다. 자, 주소를 대라.\"\n\n롭은 카커 씨가 받아 적는 동안 그것을 천천히 되뇌었다. 롭은 마치 점 하나, 획 하나라도 빠지면 자기 파멸을 부르기라도 할 듯이, 한 글자 한 글자 두 번이나 또박또박 불러 주기까지 했다. 카커 씨는 그러고 나서 그를 방 밖으로 내보냈다. 롭은 끝까지 둥근 눈을 후원자에게 고정한 채, 당분간 자취를 감추었다.\n\n지배인 카커 씨는 그날 하루 동안 사업을 엄청나게 처리했고, 수많은 사람에게 자기 이를 베풀었다. 사무실에서, 안마당에서, 거리에서, 그리고 거래소에서, 그 이는 무시무시할 정도로 번득이고 곤두섰다. 다섯 시가 되고, 그와 함께 카커 씨의 적갈색 말이 오자, 그들은 말에 올라 칩사이드를 따라 번쩍이며 올라갔다.\n\n그 시각 시내의 혼잡과 인파를 뚫고서는, 설령 그러고 싶다 한들 누구도 쉽게 빨리 달릴 수가 없었고, 또 카커 씨도 그럴 마음이 없었으므로, 그는 짐수레와 마차들 사이로 길을 골라 가며 한가로이 나아갔다. 물을 잔뜩 끼얹은 길에서 더 젖고 더 지저분한 곳은 될 수 있는 한 피하고, 자신과 말을 깨끗이 유지하려고 무진 애를 쓰면서. 이렇게 느릿느릿 길을 가며 행인들을 흘끗거리던 그는, 문득 매끈한 머리의 롭이, 마치 한 번도 떼어 본 적 없다는 듯, 자기 얼굴에 골똘히 고정된 둥근 눈과 마주쳤다. 그 아이 자신은 얼룩무늬 뱀장어처럼 비틀어 꼰 손수건을 허리에 두르고서, 그가 어떤 속도로 가기로 작정하든 시중들 준비가 되어 있음을 아주 눈에 띄게 과시하고 있었다.\n\n이런 시중은, 아무리 우쭐한 것이라 해도, 흔치 않은 종류라 다른 행인들의 눈길을 좀 끄는지라, 카커 씨는 좀 더 트인 길과 좀 더 깨끗한 노면을 틈타 속보로 달리기 시작했다. 롭도 즉시 같이 그랬다. 카커 씨가 곧이어 가벼운 구보를 시도했지만, 롭은 여전히 시중을 들었다. 그러더니 짤막한 질주를 했지만, 그 아이에게는 매한가지였다. 카커 씨가 그쪽 길가로 눈을 돌릴 때마다, 그는 여전히 투들 2세가 별로 힘들어하는 기색도 없이 제 길을 따라가는 것을 보았다. 그 아이는, 내기를 걸고 땅을 누비는 직업적인 신사들의 가장 인정받는 방식대로 두 팔꿈치를 놀려 가며 몸을 앞으로 밀어 나가고 있었다.\n\n이런 시중이 우스꽝스럽긴 했어도, 그것은 그 아이에 대해 어떤 영향력이 확립되었다는 표시였으므로, 카커 씨는 그것을 못 본 척하면서 투들 씨 집 근처로 말을 몰아 갔다. 그가 여기서 속도를 늦추자, 롭이 그 앞에 나타나 길모퉁이들을 일러 주었다. 그리고 그가 스택스의 정원 자리에 들어선 건물들을 방문하는 동안 말을 맡아 달라고 근처 대문에 있는 사내를 불렀을 때, 롭은 충직하게 등자를 잡아 주었고, 그동안 지배인은 말에서 내렸다.\n\n\"자, 가자, 얘야,\" 카커 씨가 그의 어깨를 잡으며 말했다, \"어서!\"\n\n돌아온 탕자는 부모의 거처를 방문하는 것이 분명 긴장되는 모양이었지만, 카커 씨가 그를 앞으로 떠밀자, 그는 별수 없이 옳은 문을 열고, 가족용 차탁 둘레에 압도적인 머릿수로 모여 있는 형제자매들 한가운데로 끌려 들어가는 수밖에 없었다. 낯선 이의 손아귀에 잡힌 탕자를 보자, 이 다정한 혈육들은 한꺼번에 일제히 울부짖었다. 그 울음은, 어머니가 아기를 품에 안은 채 그들 가운데서 창백하게 떨며 일어서는 것을 보았을 때, 탕자의 가슴을 어찌나 날카롭게 후벼 댔던지, 그는 자기 목소리도 그 합창에 보탰다.\n\n이제 그 낯선 이가, 케치 씨 본인은 아닐지언정 그 패거리 중 하나라고 의심해 마지않은 채, 온 어린 식구가 더 크게 울부짖었다. 그중 더 어린 축은 제 나이에 걸맞은 감정의 격발을 가누지 못해, 매에게 놀란 어린 새처럼 벌렁 나자빠져 발을 거세게 버둥거렸다. 마침내 가엾은 폴리가 겨우 목소리를 내어, 떨리는 입술로 말했다, \"오 롭, 내 가엾은 아들아, 이번엔 또 무슨 짓을 저질렀니!\"\n* 케치 씨: 17세기 영국의 악명 높은 사형 집행인 잭 케치(Jack Ketch)에서 유래한 말로, 교수형 집행인을 가리킴.\n\n\"아무 짓도 안 했어요, 엄마,\" 롭이 애처로운 목소리로 외쳤다, \"저 신사분께 여쭤보세요!\"\n\n\"놀라지 마십시오,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"나는 이 아이를 위해 좋은 일을 해 주려는 겁니다.\"\n\n이 말이 나오자, 그때까지 울지 않고 있던 폴리가 울기 시작했다. 구출이라도 궁리하고 있던 듯하던 손위 투들들은 움켜쥐었던 주먹을 폈다. 손아래 투들들은 어머니의 치맛자락에 둘러붙어, 제 통통한 팔 아래로 무모한 형과 그 정체 모를 친구를 훔쳐보았다. 모두가 좋은 일을 해 주려는, 아름다운 이를 가진 그 신사에게 축복을 빌었다.\n\n\"이 녀석이,\" 카커 씨가 폴리에게 말하며 롭을 가볍게 흔들었다, \"댁의 아들이군요, 그렇지요, 부인?\"\n\n\"네, 나리,\" 폴리가 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 흐느꼈다, \"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"못된 아들인 모양인데요?\" 카커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"저에게는 결코 못된 아들이었던 적이 없습니다, 나리,\" 폴리가 응수했다.\n\n\"그럼 누구에게 못됐단 말입니까?\" 카커 씨가 따져 물었다.\n\"이 애가 좀 거칠게 굴긴 했습니다, 나리,\" 폴리가 응수하며, 팔다리로 발작하듯 버둥거려 바일러를 향해 허공으로 몸을 날리려는 아기를 다독였다, \"그리고 나쁜 친구들과 어울렸지요. 하지만 이제 그 비참함을 깨달았으리라 믿습니다, 나리, 그래서 다시 잘 해낼 거예요.\"\n\n카커 씨는 폴리를, 그리고 깨끗한 방을, 깨끗한 아이들을, 또 아버지와 어머니가 한데 어우러진, 자기 주변 어디서나 비치고 되풀이되는 그 순박한 투들의 얼굴을 보았다—그리고 자기 방문의 진짜 목적을 이룬 듯했다.\n\n\"바깥주인은 집에 안 계신 모양이지요?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리,\" 폴리가 대답했다. \"지금은 노선에 나가 있습니다.\"\n\n탕자 롭은 그 말을 듣고 무척 안도하는 기색이었다. 그래도 여전히 온 정신을 자기 후원자에게 빼앗긴 채, 어머니에게 슬픈 눈길을 한 번씩 슬쩍 던지느라 잠깐씩 그러는 것 말고는, 카커 씨의 얼굴에서 거의 눈을 떼지 않았다.\n\n\"그럼,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"내가 어쩌다 댁의 이 아이와 마주치게 됐는지, 또 내가 누구인지, 그리고 이 아이를 위해 무엇을 해 주려는지 말씀드리지요.\"\n\n카커 씨는 이를 자기 나름의 방식으로 풀어놓았다. 처음에는, 돔비와 아들 회사의 소재지까지 찾아온 그 주제넘은 머리통에 이름 모를 공포들을 잔뜩 쌓아 줄 작정이었다고 했다. 그러나 그의 어린 나이와, 그가 입에 담은 회개와, 그의 가족을 생각해서 마음이 누그러졌다고 했다. 이 아이를 위해 무엇이든 해 주는 것이 경솔한 처사일까 봐, 신중한 이들의 비난을 살지도 모를 처사일까 봐 걱정되긴 하나, 자기는 이를 스스로, 자기 자신을 위해 하는 것이며, 그 결과는 혼자서 감수하겠다고 했다. 또 이 아이 어머니가 과거에 돔비 씨 가족과 맺었던 인연은 이 일과 아무 상관이 없고, 돔비 씨도 이 일과 아무 상관이 없으며, 오직 자기, 카커 씨가 이 일의 처음이요 끝이라고 했다. 자신의 선행을 크게 자처하고, 그 자리에 있던 온 가족에게서 그에 못지않은 칭송을 받으며, 카커 씨는 에둘러서, 그러나 여전히 꽤 분명하게, 롭의 절대적인 충실과 애착과 헌신이 영원토록 자기에게 마땅히 돌아올 몫이며, 그것이야말로 자기가 받을 수 있는 최소한의 경의라는 뜻을 내비쳤다. 이 위대한 진실에 롭 자신도 어찌나 깊이 감명을 받았던지, 후원자를 바라보며 두 뺨에 눈물을 줄줄 흘린 채, 그 번들거리는 머리를 끄덕거리는데, 그것이 그날 아침 바로 그 후원자의 손아귀에서 그랬던 것만큼이나 헐거워 보일 지경이었다.\n\n방탕한 맏아들 때문에 하늘만이 아실 만큼 수많은 밤을 뜬눈으로 지새우고, 또 몇 주 몇 주를 그 아이를 보지 못했던 폴리는, 그 이에도 불구하고, 지배인 카커 씨 앞에 마치 선한 정령 앞이라도 되는 듯 거의 무릎이라도 꿇을 뻔했다. 그러나 카커 씨가 떠나려고 일어서자, 그녀는 그저 어미의 기도와 축복으로 그에게 감사할 따름이었다. 마음의 조폐국에서 찍어 낸 그런 감사는, 특히 카커 씨가 베푼 어떤 봉사에 대해서라면 너무도 값진 것이어서, 그는 거스름돈으로 큰 액수를 돌려주고도 여전히 받은 것이 넘쳤을 것이다.\n\n그 신사가 우글거리는 아이들 사이로 문을 향해 길을 헤쳐 가는 동안, 롭은 어머니에게로 물러서서, 어머니와 아기를 한 번에 회개의 포옹으로 끌어안았다.\n\n\"이제 정말 열심히 해 볼게요, 사랑하는 엄마. 맹세코 그럴게요!\" 롭이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그래 다오, 내 아들아! 우리를 위해서, 또 너 자신을 위해서, 꼭 그러리라 믿는다!\" 폴리가 그에게 입맞추며 외쳤다. \"그런데 저 신사분을 배웅하고 나면, 나한테 이야기하러 돌아올 거지?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요, 엄마.\" 롭이 망설이며 고개를 떨구었다. \"아버지는—언제 집에 오세요?\"\n\n\"내일 새벽 두 시나 돼야 온단다.\"\n\n\"돌아올게요, 사랑하는 엄마!\" 롭이 외쳤다. 그리고 이 약속을 맞이하는 형제자매들의 날카로운 환호성을 헤치고, 그는 카커 씨를 따라 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 이 말을 들은 카커 씨가 말했다. \"넌 못된 아버지를 두었느냐?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 나리!\" 롭이 깜짝 놀라 응수했다. \"우리 아버지보다 더 좋고 더 다정한 아버지는 세상에 없어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 아버지를 보고 싶어 하지 않느냐?\" 후원자가 물었다.\n\n\"아버지하고 어머니는 너무 달라서요, 나리,\" 롭이 잠시 머뭇거리다 말했다. \"아버지는 제가 더 잘 해 보려 한다는 걸 아직 거의 믿어 주지 못하실 거예요—믿어 주려고 애는 쓰시겠지만요—하지만 어머니는—어머니는 늘 좋은 것을 믿어 주세요, 나리. 적어도 제 어머니는 그러세요, 신의 가호가 있으시길!\"\n\n카커 씨의 입이 쩍 벌어졌지만, 그는 말 위에 올라타고, 말을 잡아 준 사내를 보낼 때까지 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러고는 안장에서 내려다보며, 주의 깊고 빈틈없이 살피는 그 아이의 얼굴을 똑바로 응시하면서 말했다.\n\n\"내일 아침 나에게 오너라. 그러면 너에게 그 노신사가 사는 곳을 보여 줄 테니. 오늘 아침 나와 함께 있던 그 노신사 말이다. 너도 내가 말하는 걸 들었듯, 네가 갈 곳이다.\"\n\n\"네, 나리,\" 롭이 응수했다.\n\n\"나는 그 노신사에게 큰 관심을 두고 있다. 그러니 그분을 섬기는 것이 곧 나를 섬기는 것이다, 얘야, 알아듣겠느냐? 옳지,\" 그가 말을 가로막으며 덧붙였다. 그 말을 듣자 아이의 둥근 얼굴이 환해지는 것을 보았기 때문이다, \"알아듣는구나. 나는 그 노신사에 관한 모든 것을, 그리고 그분이 날마다 어떻게 지내시는지를 알고 싶다—내가 그분께 도움이 되고 싶어 그러는 것이니까—특히 누가 그분을 보러 거기 오는지를. 알아듣겠느냐?\"\n\n롭은 그 한결같은 얼굴로 끄덕이며, 다시 \"네, 나리\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그분에게 그분을 살뜰히 챙기는 벗들이 있다는 것, 또 그들이 그분을 저버리지 않는다는 것을 알고 싶다—그분은 이제 무척 외롭게 사시거든, 가엾은 양반. 하지만 그들이 그분을, 또 외국으로 떠난 그분의 조카를 좋아한다는 것을 말이다. 어쩌면 아주 젊은 아가씨가 한 분 그분을 보러 올지도 모른다. 나는 특히 그 아가씨에 관한 모든 것을 알고 싶다.\"\n\n\"잘 살피겠습니다, 나리,\" 아이가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 명심해라,\" 후원자가 씩 웃는 얼굴을 아이에게 더 가까이 들이밀며 몸을 앞으로 숙이고, 채찍 손잡이로 그의 어깨를 토닥이며 응수했다, \"내 일에 관해서는 나 말고는 그 누구에게도 발설하지 않도록 명심하란 말이다.\"\n\n\"세상 어느 누구에게도 안 합니다, 나리,\" 롭이 고개를 저으며 대답했다.\n\n\"저기서도 안 되고,\" 카커 씨가 그들이 방금 떠나온 곳을 가리키며 말했다, \"다른 어디서도 안 된다. 네가 얼마나 진실하고 고마워할 줄 아는지 시험해 보겠다. 너를 증명해 보이겠다!\" 이를 드러내고 고개를 놀려, 이를 약속인 동시에 위협으로 만들면서, 그는 마치 마법으로 그 아이의 몸과 영혼을 사로잡기라도 한 듯 자기에게 못 박혀 있는 롭의 눈에서 시선을 돌려, 말을 몰아 떠나갔다. 그러나 잠시 속보로 달린 뒤, 다시금 자기의 충직한 부하가 아까처럼 띠를 두르고 똑같은 시중을 들고 있음을, 그것도 여러 구경꾼을 무척 즐겁게 하면서 그러고 있음을 알아채고는, 고삐를 당겨 그를 쫓아 보냈다. 그가 복종하도록, 그는 안장 위에서 몸을 돌려 그가 물러가는 것을 지켜보았다. 그때조차 롭이 후원자의 얼굴에서 눈을 온전히 돌리지 못하고, 끊임없이 돌아보고 또 돌아보며 그를 뒤좇아 바라보다가, 거리의 다른 행인들에게 떠밀리고 부딪치는 회오리에 휘말리는 광경은 진기했다. 그러나 그 단 하나의 압도적인 생각을 좇느라, 그는 그런 것에 전혀 아랑곳하지 않았다.\n\n지배인 카커 씨는, 그날 하루의 모든 일을 흡족하게 마치고 마음에서 편안히 떨쳐 낸 사람 같은 느긋한 분위기로, 걸음 속도에 맞춰 말을 몰아 나아갔다. 사람으로서 더할 나위 없이 만족스럽고 상냥하게, 카커 씨는 거리를 따라 길을 골라 가며 부드러운 가락을 흥얼거렸다. 어찌나 기뻤던지 그는 고양이처럼 가르랑거리는 듯했다.\n\n그리고 어떤 의미에서, 카커 씨는 상상 속에서 화롯가에서도 일광욕을 즐기고 있었다. 어떤 발치에 아늑하게 몸을 도사린 채, 그는 기분 내키는 대로, 또 기회가 닿는 대로, 덮칠 준비도, 할퀼 준비도, 또는 벨벳 같은 부드러운 손길을 내밀 준비도 되어 있었다. 그의 눈길 한몫을 받게 될 새장 속의 새라도 있었던가?\n\n\"아주 젊은 아가씨라!\" 지배인 카커 씨가 노래 속에서 생각했다. \"그래! 내가 그 아가씨를 마지막으로 봤을 때, 그 애는 자그마한 어린아이였지. 검은 눈에 검은 머리였던 게 기억나는군, 그리고 고운 얼굴이었지. 아주 고운 얼굴! 분명 예쁠 거야.\"\n\n더욱더 상냥하고 즐거워하며, 자기 노래를 흥얼거려 그 수많은 이가 거기에 떨릴 정도로 흥얼대면서, 카커 씨는 길을 골라 가다가, 마침내 돔비 씨의 집이 서 있는 그늘진 거리로 접어들었다. 그는 고운 얼굴들 둘레에 거미줄을 치고 그것을 그물로 가리느라 어찌나 분주했던지, 키 큰 집들의 차가운 원근 풍경을 흘끗 내려다보다가 문에서 몇 야드 떨어진 곳에서 재빨리 말을 멈춰 세우기 전까지는, 자기가 이 길의 이 지점에 와 있다는 것조차 거의 생각지 못하고 있었다. 그러나 카커 씨가 왜 재빨리 말을 멈춰 세웠는지, 또 적잖이 놀라서 무엇을 바라보았는지를 설명하려면, 옆길로 새는 몇 마디가 필요하다.\n\n블림버의 속박에서 풀려나 자기 세속적 재산의 일정 몫을 소유하게 된 투츠 씨는—그 재산은, 그가 마지막 반년의 수습 기간 동안 매일 저녁 새로운 발견이라도 되는 양 피더 씨에게 알리곤 했듯이, \"유언 집행인들이 도저히 그에게 안 줄 수 없는\" 것이었다—삶이라는 학문에 무척이나 부지런히 매달려 왔다. 찬란하고 빼어난 이력을 좇으려는 고귀한 경쟁심에 불타올라, 투츠 씨는 멋들어진 거처 한 벌을 마련했다. 그 안에 사냥용 정자를 차려, 자기는 손톱만큼도 관심 없는 우승마들의 초상으로 장식해 두었고, 또 자기를 거북하게 만드는 긴 소파도 하나 들여놓았다. 이 호화로운 거처에서, 투츠 씨는 삶을 세련되고 인간답게 만드는 그 고상한 기예들을 갈고닦는 데 몰두했는데, 그 분야의 으뜸가는 스승은 게임 치킨이라 불리는 흥미로운 인물이었다. 그 사람은 늘 블랙 배저의 술집에서 소식을 들을 수 있었고, 가장 따뜻한 날씨에도 텁수룩한 흰 외투를 걸쳤으며, 한 번 방문에 십 실링 육 펜스라는 소소한 사례를 받고 일주일에 세 번씩 투츠 씨의 머리를 두들겨 주었다.\n\n투츠 씨의 만신전에서 그야말로 아폴로 격이었던 게임 치킨은 그에게 당구를 가르치는 점수원, 펜싱을 가르치는 근위 기병, 승마를 가르치는 말 임대업자, 운동 분야라면 무엇이든 능한 콘월 출신 신사, 그리고 못지않게 친밀하게 예술과 관련된 두세 명의 다른 친구들을 소개해 주었다. 그들의 후원 아래라면 투츠 씨가 빠르게 실력을 키우지 못할 리 없었고, 그들의 지도 아래 그는 부지런히 정진해 나갔다.\n\n그러나 어떻게 된 노릇인지, 이 신사들이 아직 새것의 광택을 띠고 있는 동안에도, 투츠 씨는 어쩐 영문인지 모르게 마음이 들뜨고 불안해졌다. 그의 곡식 속에는 게임 치킨조차 쪼아 낼 수 없는 쭉정이가 들어 있었고, 그의 여가에는 게임 치킨조차 때려눕힐 수 없는 음울한 거인들이 도사리고 있었다. 돔비 씨의 집 문에 끊임없이 명함을 남기는 일만큼 투츠 씨에게 좋은 약은 없는 듯했다. 해가 결코 지지 않고 세금 징수원이 결코 잠자리에 들지 않는, 그 드넓이 펼쳐진 영토, 영국령 전역의 어떤 세금 징수원도 투츠 씨보다 더 규칙적이고 끈질기게 방문하지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n투츠 씨는 결코 위층으로 올라가지 않았다. 그리고 그 일을 위해 한껏 화려하게 차려입고, 언제나 현관문에서 똑같은 의식을 치렀다.\n\n\"오! 안녕하세요!\" 하는 것이 투츠 씨가 하인에게 건네는 첫마디였다. \"돔비 씨께 드리는 겁니다.\" 하는 것이 명함 한 장을 내밀며 하는 다음 말이었다. \"돔비 아가씨께 드리는 겁니다.\" 하는 것이 또 한 장을 내밀며 하는 그다음 말이었다.\n\n그러고 나면 투츠 씨는 마치 떠나려는 듯 돌아서곤 했다. 그러나 하인은 이제 그를 알아서, 그가 떠나지 않으리란 걸 알았다.\n\n\"오, 실례합니다만,\" 투츠 씨가 마치 문득 어떤 생각이 떠오른 듯 말하곤 했다. \"그 젊은 여인은 집에 있습니까?\"\n\n하인은 있는 것 같기는 한데 잘 모르겠다고 하곤 했다. 그러고는 위층으로 울리는 종을 울리고, 계단 위를 올려다보며, 네, 집에 있고 내려오고 있다고 말하곤 했다. 그러면 니퍼 양이 나타났고, 하인은 물러갔다.\n\n\"오! 안녕하세요?\" 투츠 씨가 킥킥 웃으며 얼굴을 붉히며 말하곤 했다.\n\n수전은 그에게 고맙다고 하며, 자기는 아주 잘 지낸다고 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"디오게네스는 어떻게 지냅니까?\" 하는 것이 투츠 씨의 두 번째 질문이곤 했다.\n\n아주 잘 지낸답니다. 플로렌스 아가씨는 날이 갈수록 그 개를 더더욱 좋아하신답니다. 투츠 씨는 으레 이 말을, 무슨 거품 이는 음료수 병의 마개를 따는 것 같은 한바탕의 킥킥거림으로 반겼다.\n\n\"플로렌스 아가씨는 아주 잘 지내십니다, 나리.\" 수전이 덧붙이곤 했다.\n\n\"오, 별일 아닙니다, 고맙습니다.\" 하는 것이 투츠 씨의 한결같은 대답이었다. 그리고 그 말을 하고 나면, 그는 언제나 아주 빠르게 떠나가곤 했다.\n\n자, 투츠 씨의 마음속에는 어떤 어렴풋한 것이 있어, 만일 때가 무르익었을 때 자기가 플로렌스의 손을 얻으려는 열망을 성공적으로 이룰 수 있다면, 자기는 운 좋고 복받은 사람이 되리라는 결론으로 그를 이끌었음이 분명하다. 투츠 씨가 어떤 멀고 에두른 길을 거쳐 그 지점에 이르렀고, 거기서 발걸음을 멈춰 섰음이 분명하다. 그의 마음은 상처 입었다. 그는 가슴이 동했다. 그는 사랑에 빠졌다. 그는 어느 날 밤, 플로렌스에 관한 이합체시를 쓰겠다는 일념으로 필사적인 시도를 해, 그 일을 위해 밤을 꼬박 새우기도 했는데, 착상하면서 스스로 눈물지을 만큼 감동했다. 그러나 그는 실행에서 \"내가 바라볼 때면\"이라는 말 이상으로는 나아가지 못했다—앞서 나머지 일곱 행의 첫 글자들을 적어 두었던 그 상상의 흐름이, 바로 그 지점에서 그를 저버린 것이다.\n* 이합체시(아크로스틱): 각 행의 첫 글자를 위에서 아래로 이으면 어떤 단어나 이름이 되도록 지은 시.\n\n돔비 씨에게 매일 명함을 남긴다는 그 아주 교묘하고 약삭빠른 방책을 궁리해 낸 것 말고는, 투츠 씨의 두뇌는 자기 감정을 사로잡은 그 주제와 관련해 그다지 많이 작동하지 않았다. 그러나 깊이 숙고한 끝에 투츠 씨는 마침내, 손에 넣어야 할 중요한 한 걸음이란 곧 수전 니퍼 양의 환심을 사는 것이며, 그것이 그녀에게 자기 심중의 일단을 넌지시 알리기 위한 사전 준비라는 확신에 이르렀다.\n\n이 이야기의 그 초기 장에서, 이 숙녀를 자기 편으로 끌어들이기 위해 동원할 수단은, 그녀를 향한 약간의 가볍고 장난기 어린 구애인 듯싶었다. 그것에 대해 도무지 마음을 정하지 못한 그는 치킨에게 의논을—그 신사를 속내까지 끌어들이지는 않고, 그저 요크셔의 한 친구가 그(투츠 씨)에게 그런 문제에 대한 의견을 묻는 편지를 보냈다고만 알리면서—했다. 치킨이, 자기 의견은 언제나 \"들어가서 이겨라\"이며, 더 나아가 \"네 상대가 네 앞에 있고 할 일이 정해졌으면, 들어가서 해치워라\"라고 대답하자, 투츠 씨는 이것을 자기 견해를 비유적으로 뒷받침해 주는 말로 여기고, 이튿날 니퍼 양에게 입맞추리라 영웅적으로 결심했다.\n\n그리하여 이튿날 투츠 씨는, 버지스 상회가 여태껏 만들어 낸 것 중 가장 위대한 경이로운 작품 몇 점을 동원하여, 이 계획을 품고 돔비 씨 댁으로 향했다. 그러나 작전 무대로 다가갈수록 어찌나 가슴이 콩알만 해졌던지, 오후 세 시에 현장에 도착했음에도 막상 문을 두드린 것은 여섯 시였다.\n\n모든 일이 평소처럼 진행되어, 수전이 자기 어린 아가씨는 잘 지내신다고 말하고, 투츠 씨가 별일 아니라고 말하는 지점에 이르렀다. 그런데 그녀가 깜짝 놀랍게도, 투츠 씨는 그 말 뒤에 로켓처럼 떠나 버리는 대신, 머뭇거리며 킥킥거렸다.\n\n\"위층으로 올라오시겠어요, 나리!\" 수전이 말했다.\n\n\"음, 들어가는 게 좋겠군요!\" 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n그러나 위층으로 올라가는 대신, 대담한 투츠는 문이 닫히자 수전에게 어색하게 와락 달려들어, 그 어여쁜 아가씨를 끌어안고 뺨에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"저리 가요!\" 수전이 외쳤다, \"안 그러면 눈을 후벼 파 버릴 테니.\"\n\n\"딱 한 번만 더!\" 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"저리 가라니까요!\" 수전이 그를 밀치며 외쳤다, \"당신처럼 순진한 사람도 참! 다음엔 누가 시작할 작정이람? 가요, 나리!\"\n\n수전은 정말로 곤경에 처한 것은 아니었으니, 웃음 때문에 말도 제대로 하기 어려울 지경이었기 때문이다. 그러나 계단에 있던 디오게네스는 벽에 무언가 스치는 소리와 발을 끄는 소리를 듣고, 난간 살 사이로 무슨 다툼이 벌어지고 있으며 집안에 외적이 침입했음을 보고는, 다른 견해를 품고, 구출하러 쏜살같이 내달아, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 투츠 씨의 다리를 물어 버렸다.\n\n수전은 비명을 지르고, 웃고, 거리로 통하는 문을 열고는 아래층으로 달려 내려갔다. 대담한 투츠는 비틀거리며 거리로 나뒹굴었는데, 디오게네스가 그의 한쪽 바지통을 물고 늘어진 것이, 마치 버지스 상회가 자기 요리사라서 휴일의 흥겨운 식사거리로 그 맛있는 한 조각을 마련해 준 것 같았다. 떨쳐진 디오게네스는 흙먼지 속에 데굴데굴 굴렀다가 다시 일어나, 어질어질해진 투츠 둘레를 빙글빙글 돌며 그에게 덤벼들었다. 그리고 이 모든 소동이, 조금 떨어진 곳에 말을 세우고 앉아 있던 카커 씨의 눈에, 돔비 씨의 위풍당당한 저택에서 쏟아져 나오는 것을 그는 어리둥절하게 지켜보았다.\n\n카커 씨는 풀이 죽은 투츠를 지켜보며 머물러 있었다. 디오게네스가 불려 들어가고 문이 닫힐 때까지, 또 그 신사가 가까운 어느 문간으로 피신하여, 그날의 거사를 위해 마련한 값비싼 차림의 일부였던 호화로운 비단 손수건으로 찢어진 바지통을 동여매는 동안에도.\n\n\"실례합니다, 나리,\" 카커 씨가 더없이 비위 맞추는 미소를 띠며 말을 몰아 다가와 말했다. \"다치지 않으셨기를 바랍니다만?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다, 고맙습니다,\" 투츠 씨가 상기된 얼굴을 들며 응수했다, \"별일 아닙니다.\" 투츠 씨는 할 수만 있었다면, 자기는 그게 무척 마음에 든다는 뜻을 내비치고 싶었을 것이다.\n\n\"개의 이가 다리에 박혔다면, 나리—\" 카커가 자기 이를 드러내 보이며 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"아니요, 고맙습니다,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"다 멀쩡합니다. 아주 편안한걸요, 고맙습니다.\"\n\n\"저는 돔비 씨를 아는 영광을 누리고 있습니다,\" 카커가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그러십니까?\" 얼굴 붉히는 투츠가 응수했다.\n\n\"그리고 어쩌면, 그분이 안 계신 자리에서나마, 이런 변고에 대해 제가 사과드리고,\" 카커 씨가 모자를 벗으며 말했다, \"도대체 어쩌다 이런 일이 일어날 수 있었는지 의아해하는 것을 허락해 주시겠지요.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 이 정중함에, 그리고 돔비 씨의 친구와 벗이 되는 이 다행스러운 기회에 어찌나 흡족했던지, 한 번도 쓸 기회를 놓치는 법이 없는 명함첩을 꺼내, 카커 씨에게 자기 이름과 주소를 건넸다. 카커 씨는 그 호의에 자기 것을 건네는 것으로 화답했고, 그것으로 두 사람은 헤어졌다.\n\n카커 씨가 그토록 부드럽게 그 집을 지나치며, 창문들을 올려다보고, 건너편 아이들을 바라보는 커튼 뒤의 수심 어린 얼굴을 알아보려 애쓰고 있을 때, 디오게네스의 거친 머리가 바로 그 곁으로 기어 올라왔다. 그러더니 그 개는 온갖 달램에도 아랑곳없이, 짖고 으르렁대며 그 높이에서 그에게 덤벼들었는데, 마치 뛰어내려 그를 사지를 찢어발기기라도 할 듯했다.\n\n잘 짖었다, 디, 네 주인 아가씨와 그토록 가까이 있으니! 또 한 번, 그리고 또 한 번, 머리를 곧추세우고, 두 눈을 번득이며, 그를 물어뜯지 못해 안달이 난 입을 분에 못 이겨 우물거리면서! 또 한 번, 그가 길을 골라 지나가는 동안에! 너는 후각이 좋구나, 디—고양이다, 얘야, 고양이!\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII. Florence solitary, and the Midshipman mysterious\n\n플로렌스는 그 크고 음울한 집에서 홀로 살았다. 하루가 또 하루를 이어 갔으나, 그녀는 여전히 홀로 살았다. 텅 빈 벽들은 멍한 눈길로 그녀를 내려다보았는데, 마치 고르곤처럼 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움을 돌로 굳혀 버리려는 심산이라도 품은 듯했다.\n\n마법의 이야기 속, 울창한 숲 한가운데 갇혀 있는 그 어떤 마법의 거처도, 거리를 향해 험상궂게 솟아 있는 그녀 아버지의 저택만큼 상상 속에서조차 외롭고 버려진 곳은 없었으리라. 밤이면 이웃집 창문에서 불빛이 새어 나올 때마다 그 빈약한 밝음 위에 찍힌 하나의 얼룩이었고, 낮이면 결코 미소 짓지 않는 그 얼굴 위에 새겨진 하나의 찌푸림이었다.\n\n마법의 전설 속에서 흔히 부당하게 갇힌 무고한 자를 지키느라 당직을 서는 두 마리 용 파수꾼이 이 집 문 앞을 지키고 있지는 않았다. 그러나 문 위 아치 너머로 모든 방문객을 살피며 가느다란 입술을 사악하게 벌린 험상궂은 형상 말고도, 문지방 위로 정자(亭子)가 돌처럼 굳어 버린 양 휘감기고 비틀린 녹슨 쇠붙이의 괴이한 환상물이 있었으니, 그것은 가시와 나선형 끝으로 싹을 틔우고, 양옆에 하나씩 불길한 촛불 끄개를 달고 있어, \"여기 들어오는 자여, 빛을 버리고 들라!\" 하고 말하는 듯했다. 문에 새겨진 부적 같은 문자는 없었으나, 집의 외양이 이제 어찌나 방치되었던지 아이들이 난간과 보도에—특히 옆벽이 있는 모퉁이 둘레에—분필질을 해 댔고 마구간 문에 유령을 그렸으며, 가끔 타울린슨 씨에게 쫓겨나면 보복으로 그의 초상을 그렸는데, 모자 밑에서 귀가 수평으로 자라난 모습이었다. 지붕의 그늘 안에서는 소리가 사라졌다. 일주일에 한 번 아침이면 거리로 들어오던 금관악대도 그 창문 안으로는 단 한 음도 울리지 않았다. 그뿐 아니라 머리가 둔한 가련하고 자그마한 손풍금 하나에, 접이문 사이로 왈츠를 추며 드나드는 멍청한 자동인형 무용단 패거리에 이르기까지, 그런 모든 떠들썩한 무리가 한결같이 그 집을 떠나, 가망 없는 곳으로 여겨 피해 갔다.\n\n그 집에 걸린 마법은, 옛날 옛적 마법의 집들을 잠들게 했으되 깨어났을 때의 생기는 고스란히 남겨 두던 그 마법보다도 더 사람을 좀먹는 것이었다.\n\n쓰지 않아 생긴 무력한 황폐함이 사방에서 소리 없이 드러나 있었다. 집 안에서는 커튼들이 묵직하게 늘어진 채 옛 주름과 모양을 잃고, 거추장스러운 관(棺) 덮개처럼 걸려 있었다. 여전히 쌓아 올려 천을 덮어 둔 가구의 산더미들이, 갇혀 잊힌 사람들처럼 쪼그라들며 자기도 모르게 변해 갔다. 거울들은 세월의 입김이 서린 듯 뿌옜다. 양탄자의 무늬는 바래어 흐릿하고 희미해졌으니, 그 세월의 사소한 사건들에 대한 기억과도 같았다. 익숙지 않은 발걸음에 놀란 마룻장들이 삐걱거리며 흔들렸다. 열쇠들은 문의 자물쇠 안에서 녹슬었다. 습기가 벽에 번지기 시작했고, 얼룩이 배어 나오자 그림들은 안으로 들어가 제 모습을 감추는 듯했다. 곰팡이와 이끼가 벽장 안에 도사리기 시작했다. 지하실 구석에서는 버섯나무가 자랐다. 어디서 어떻게 생기는지 아무도 모르는 먼지가 쌓여 갔고, 거미와 나방과 굼벵이 이야기가 날마다 들렸다. 이따금 길을 잃고 탐색에 나선 검정딱정벌레가 계단이나 위층 방에서 꼼짝 않고 발견되곤 했는데, 마치 제가 어떻게 거기까지 왔는지 의아해하는 듯했다. 쥐들은 밤이면 끽끽거리며 다투기 시작했으니, 벽판 뒤에 파 놓은 어두운 회랑들을 누비고 다녔다.\n\n닫힌 덧문 사이로 들어오는 흐릿한 빛에 어렴풋이 보이는 큰 응접실들의 음울한 화려함은, 마법에 걸린 거처로 손색이 없었으리라. 가령 천 덮개 밑에서 슬며시 내민 도금 사자들의 빛바랜 발이라든가, 받침대 위 흉상들의 대리석 윤곽이 베일 너머로 무섭게 제 모습을 드러내는 것이라든가, 결코 시간을 알려 주지 않거나 어쩌다 태엽을 감으면 틀린 시간을 알리며 문자반에도 없는 기괴한 숫자를 치는 시계들이라든가, 매달린 샹들리에 사이에서 우연히 짤랑이는 소리가 경종보다 더 사람을 놀라게 하는 것이라든가, 이런 물건들 사이로, 그리고 천에 싸이고 두건이 씌워져 유령 같은 형상이 된 한 무리의 다른 것들 사이로 흘러드는 누그러진 소리와 더딘 공기가 그러했다. 그러나 그 밖에도, 이 저택의 주인이 좀처럼 발을 들이지 않았던, 그리고 그의 어린 자식이 천국으로 올라가던 그 큰 계단이 있었다. 몇 주씩 아무도 다니지 않는 다른 계단과 복도들이 있었고, 죽은 가족들과 결부되어 그들에 대한 속삭이는 추억이 어린 닫힌 방 두 개가 있었다. 그리고 플로렌스를 제외한 온 집 안 사람들에게는, 그 고독과 음울 속을 움직이며 모든 생명 없는 것에 살아 있는 인간의 관심과 경이의 손길을 닿게 하는 한 부드러운 형상이 있었다.\n\n플로렌스는 그 버려진 집에서 홀로 살았고, 하루가 또 하루를 이어 갔으나 여전히 홀로 살았으며, 차가운 벽들은 멍한 눈길로 그녀를 내려다보았는데, 마치 고르곤처럼 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움을 돌로 굳혀 버리려는 심산이라도 품은 듯했다.\n\n지붕 위에, 그리고 지하층 포석의 틈새에 풀이 돋기 시작했다. 비늘처럼 부스러지는 식물이 창턱 둘레에 싹텄다. 쓰지 않는 굴뚝 안쪽에서는 회반죽 조각들이 붙들고 있던 자리를 놓치고 떨어져 내렸다. 그을린 둥치의 나무 두 그루는 높은 곳까지 시들어, 마른 가지들이 잎사귀 위로 거만하게 군림했다. 건물 전체에서 흰색이 누렇게, 누런색이 거의 검게 변했고, 그 가련한 부인이 세상을 떠난 그때 이후로, 그 집은 길고 단조로운 거리 한가운데 어두운 틈으로 서서히 변해 갔다.\n\n그러나 플로렌스는 그곳에서, 이야기 속 임금의 어여쁜 딸처럼 피어났다. 그녀의 책과 음악과 매일 오는 선생들이 그녀의 유일한 진짜 동무였다. 수전 니퍼와 다이오지니즈만은 예외였는데, 전자는 어린 여주인의 공부 시중을 들면서 자기도 제법 박식해지기 시작했고, 후자는 아마도 같은 영향으로 누그러졌는지, 여름날 아침 내내 창턱에 머리를 얹고서 거리를 향해 평온하게 눈을 떴다 감았다 하곤 했다. 때로는 수레에 실려 짖어 대며 지나가는 시끄러운 개를 의미심장하게 바라보려고 고개를 쳐들었고, 때로는 동네에 있다고 여기는 가상의 적에 대한 격앙되고 영문 모를 기억이 떠올라 문으로 달려들었다가, 귀가 먹먹할 만큼 한바탕 소동을 벌인 뒤, 자기 특유의 우스꽝스러운 만족감에 젖어 어슬렁어슬렁 돌아와서는, 공공의 봉사라도 마친 개의 태도로 다시 창턱에 턱을 얹곤 했다.\n\n이렇게 플로렌스는 자기 집이라는 황무지 안에서, 순수한 일과 생각의 울타리 안에서 살았고, 아무것도 그녀를 해치지 못했다. 이제 그녀는 아버지의 방으로 내려가, 그를 생각하고, 사랑하는 마음으로 겸손히 그에게 다가가도 거절당할 두려움이 없었다. 슬픔에 잠긴 그를 둘러쌌던 물건들을 바라볼 수 있었고, 그의 의자 가까이 몸을 기댈 수 있었으며, 그토록 또렷이 기억하는 그 눈길을 두려워하지 않을 수 있었다. 그녀는 그에게 자기 도리와 봉사의 작은 정표들을 바칠 수 있었으니, 제 손으로 그를 위해 모든 것을 정돈하고, 식탁에 놓을 작은 꽃다발을 묶고, 그것들이 하나하나 시들어도 그가 돌아오지 않으면 갈아 두고, 날마다 그를 위해 무언가를 마련하며, 그가 늘 앉던 자리 가까이에 자기가 다녀갔다는 수줍은 흔적을 남기는 일이었다. 오늘은 그의 시계를 올려놓을 작고 색칠한 받침대였고, 내일이면 그것을 그냥 두기가 겁이 나서, 그의 눈에 그리 띌 것 같지 않은 다른 자질구레한 손수 만든 물건으로 바꿔 놓곤 했다. 밤중에 깨어나면, 그가 집에 돌아와 화를 내며 그것을 내쳐 버릴 생각에 떨면서, 슬리퍼 신은 발로 가슴을 두근거리며 서둘러 내려가 그것을 치워 오기도 했다. 또 어떤 때는 그저 그의 책상에 얼굴을 대고, 거기에 입맞춤 하나와 눈물 하나를 남기고 올 뿐이었다.\n\n그래도 아무도 이 일을 알지 못했다. 그녀가 없을 때 식구들이 알아챘다면 모를까—그리고 그들은 모두 돔비 씨의 방을 두려워했다—그것은 그 이전의 일들만큼이나 그녀 가슴속 깊은 비밀이었다. 플로렌스는 해 질 녘, 이른 아침, 그리고 식사가 아래층에서 차려질 때 그 방들로 몰래 들어갔다. 그리고 비록 그 방들이 구석구석 그녀의 보살핌으로 한결 좋아지고 밝아졌어도, 그녀는 한 줄기 햇살처럼 조용히 들어갔다 나왔으니, 다만 자기의 빛을 뒤에 남겨 둔다는 것만이 달랐다.\n\n그림자 같은 동무들이 메아리치는 집 안을 오르내리며 플로렌스를 따라다녔고, 가구가 치워진 방들에서 그녀와 함께 앉아 있었다. 마치 그녀의 삶이 마법에 걸린 환영이기라도 한 듯, 그녀의 고독에서 시중드는 생각들이 일어나, 그 삶을 공상적이고 비현실적인 것으로 만들었다. 아버지가 자기를 사랑할 수 있었더라면, 그리고 자기가 귀여움받는 자식이었더라면 인생이 어떠했을까 너무도 자주 상상한 나머지, 때로는 한순간 거의 그것이 사실이라고 믿기까지 했고, 그 애상에 잠긴 허구의 물결에 실려, 두 사람이 함께 무덤 속 동생을 지켜보았던 일을, 동생의 마음을 서로 거리낌 없이 나누었던 일을, 그를 향한 애틋한 추억 속에서 하나로 맺어졌던 일을, 아직도 자주 그에 대해 이야기하는 일을 기억하는 듯했다. 그리고 다정한 아버지가 그녀를 부드럽게 바라보며, 하느님께 거는 둘의 공통된 소망과 믿음을 이야기해 주는 것을. 또 어떤 때는 어머니가 아직 살아 있는 모습을 그려 보았다. 오, 그 목에 매달려, 온 영혼의 사랑과 신뢰로 어머니에게 안기는 행복이여! 그리고 오, 저녁이 다가오는데 아무도 없는 그 외로운 집의 황량함이 다시금이여!\n\n그러나 그녀 자신에게도 채 형체를 갖추지 못했으나, 그녀 안에서 뜨겁고 강하게 살아, 플로렌스가 애쓸 때 그녀를 떠받치고, 그토록 모질게 시련받은 그 진실한 어린 마음을 한결같은 목적으로 채워 주는 한 가지 생각이 있었다. 우리 죽어야 할 본성의 큰 슬픔과 싸우는 모든 이의 마음에 그러하듯, 그녀의 마음에도 엄숙한 의구와 소망이 스며들어 있었으니, 그것은 현세 너머의 어슴푸레한 세계에서 일어나, 동생과 어머니가 그 머나먼 땅에서 서로를 알아보리라는 것, 둘 다 그녀를 지금도 의식하고 있으리라는 것, 그녀를 향한 어떤 사랑과 연민이 있으리라는 것, 그리고 그녀가 이 땅 위에서 제 갈 길을 갈 때 둘이 그녀를 알아보고 있으리라는 것을, 희미한 음악처럼 속삭여 주었다. 이런 생각들에 깃들 자리를 내어 주는 것은 플로렌스에게 위로가 되는 마음의 안식이었다. 그러던 어느 날—그것은 그녀가 밤늦게 아버지의 방에서 마지막으로 그를 본 직후였다—그녀에게 한 가지 상념이 떠올랐으니, 멀어진 그의 마음 때문에 운다면 죽은 이들의 영혼을 그에게 맞서도록 부추기는 셈이 될지도 모른다는 것이었다. 그렇게 생각하는 것이, 그리고 그 채 형체를 갖추지 못한 상념에 떠는 것이 아무리 터무니없고 약하고 어린아이 같았다 해도, 그것은 그녀의 사랑하는 천성에서 우러난 충동이었다. 그리고 그 순간부터 플로렌스는 가슴속 그 잔인한 상처에 맞서 싸웠고, 그 상처를 낸 손의 주인을 오직 희망으로만 생각하려 애썼다.\n\n아버지는 자기가 그를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알지 못했다—그녀는 그때부터 그렇게 믿었다. 그녀는 매우 어렸고, 어머니가 없었으며, 어떤 잘못이나 불운으로 그를 사랑한다는 것을 그에게 표현하는 법을 끝내 배우지 못했다. 그녀는 참고 기다리며, 머지않아 그 기술을 익혀, 그가 자기의 외동딸을 더 잘 알도록 마음을 돌려놓으리라 했다.\n\n이것이 그녀 삶의 목적이 되었다. 아침 해가 빛바랜 집 위로 비쳐 내리며, 그 외로운 여주인의 가슴속에서 그 결심이 밝고 싱그럽게 살아 있는 것을 발견했다. 그것은 하루의 모든 일과 내내 그녀에게 활기를 불어넣었으니, 플로렌스는 자기가 더 많이 알고 더 많은 것을 갖추게 될수록, 아버지가 자기를 알고 좋아하게 될 때 더 기뻐하리라 바랐기 때문이다. 때로는 가슴이 부풀고 눈물이 차오르며, 두 사람이 동무가 될 때 그를 놀라게 할 만큼 자기가 무엇 하나라도 충분히 능숙한지 자문해 보았다. 때로는 다른 어떤 것보다 그의 관심을 더 쉽게 끌 만한 지식이 있을까 생각해 보았다. 언제나, 책과 음악과 일에서, 아침 산책에서, 밤기도에서, 그녀는 그 골똘한 목표를 눈앞에 두었다. 모진 아버지의 마음에 이르는 길을 배우다니, 어린아이에게는 참으로 기이한 공부였다!\n\n여름 저녁이 깊어 밤이 되어 갈 무렵, 거리를 한가로이 어슬렁거리던 많은 이들이 길 건너 그 음울한 집을 흘끗 바라보고, 창가에 선 그 젊은 모습을—그 집과 그토록 대조되는 모습을—반짝이기 시작하는 별들을 올려다보는 그 모습을 보았다. 그녀가 그토록 한결같이 골몰하는 생각이 무엇인지 알았더라면, 그들은 잠을 더 설쳤을 것이다. 날마다 자기 일로 그 집 앞을 오가다가 그 바깥의 음울함에 충격받아 그 저택을 유령의 집이라 부르던, 어딘가 다른 곳의 가난한 이들도, 어두워지는 그 얼굴에서 사연을 읽어 낼 수 있었더라면, 그 집의 그런 평판을 그리 유쾌하게 여기지는 못했으리라. 그러나 플로렌스는 의심받지도 도움받지도 못한 채 그 거룩한 목적을 간직했고, 오직 어떻게 하면 자기가 그를 사랑한다는 것을 아버지가 깨닫게 할까만을 궁리했으며, 어떤 떠도는 생각 속에서도 그를 원망하지 않았다.\n\n이렇게 플로렌스는 그 버려진 집에서 홀로 살았고, 하루가 또 하루를 이어 갔으나 여전히 홀로 살았으며, 단조로운 벽들은 멍한 눈길로 그녀를 내려다보았는데, 마치 고르곤처럼 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움을 돌로 굳혀 버리려는 심산이라도 품은 듯했다.\n\n어느 날 아침, 플로렌스가 방금 쓴 쪽지를 접어 봉인하고 있을 때, 수전 니퍼가 그 앞에 마주 서서, 그 내용을 흡족하게 안다는 표정을 지어 보였다.\n\n\"늦더라도 안 하느니보다 낫지요, 플로이 아가씨,\" 수전이 말했다. \"그리고 제가 말씀드리는데, 그 늙은 스케틀스 댁에 다녀오는 것만 해도 천만다행이에요.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV. The Study of a Loving Heart\n\n\"바넷 경과 스케틀스 부인이 그렇게 친절히도 초대를 거듭해 주시니 참 고마운 일이지, 수전,\" 플로렌스가 응수하며, 문제의 그 집안을 허물없이 입에 올린 이 처녀를 부드럽게 바로잡았다.\n\n니퍼 양은 아마 이 세상에서 가장 철저한 편파주의자였을 것이며, 크고 작은 모든 일에 그 편파성을 끌어들여, 그것을 무기 삼아 세상과 끊임없이 전쟁을 벌이는 사람이었던지라, 입술을 비죽 오므리고 고개를 저었으니, 스케틀스 댁의 사심 없음을 조금이라도 인정하는 데 항의하고, 그들이 플로렌스를 곁에 둠으로써 그 친절에 대해 톡톡한 보상을 받으리라는 점을 들어 반론을 제기하는 것이었다.\n\n\"세상에 속셈 있는 사람이 있다면 바로 그 사람들이라니까요,\" 니퍼 양이 숨을 들이켜며 중얼거렸다. \"오! 그 점에선 그 스케틀스 댁을 믿으셔도 돼요!\"\n\n\"솔직히 풀럼에 가는 게 그리 내키지는 않아, 수전,\" 플로렌스가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"하지만 가는 게 옳을 거야. 그게 나을 거라고 생각해.\"\n\n\"훨씬 낫고말고요,\" 수전이 또 한 번 단호히 고개를 흔들며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그래서,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"아무도 없을 때 가는 게 더 좋았겠지만, 이렇게 방학 때라서 집에 젊은 사람들 몇이 머물고 있는 것 같은데도, 고맙게 가겠다고 했어.\"\n\n\"그 점에 대해선 저는, 플로이 아가씨, 오 기뻐할 일이다! 하고 말씀드려요,\" 수전이 응수했다. \"아! 흐—흠!\"\n\n니퍼 양이 그 무렵 곧잘 말끝에 붙이던 이 마지막 감탄사는, 현관 아래쪽에서는 대체로 돔비 씨를 두고 하는 말로, 그 신사에게 한바탕 속마음을 들려주고 싶어 하는 니퍼 양의 갈망을 나타내는 것으로 여겨졌다. 그러나 그녀는 결코 그것을 설명하지 않았으니, 그 결과 그 감탄사는 더없이 날카로운 표현이라는 이점에다 신비라는 매력까지 더하게 되었다.\n\n\"월터 소식을 들은 지가 참 오래됐네, 수전!\" 플로렌스가 잠시 침묵한 뒤 말했다.\n\n\"정말 오래됐어요, 플로이 아가씨!\" 하녀가 대답했다. \"그리고 퍼치가 방금 편지 왔나 보러 왔을 때 그러는데—하지만 그 사람이 하는 말이 무슨 소용이람!\" 수전이 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 끊었다. \"그 사람이 뭘 안다고!\"\n\n플로렌스가 재빨리 눈을 들었고, 얼굴에 홍조가 번졌다.\n\n\"제가 만약,\" 수전 니퍼가 어떤 숨은 불안과 경계심과 싸우는 기색이 역력한 채, 죄 없는 퍼치 씨의 모습을 떠올리며 억지로 분개한 상태로 몰아가려 애쓰면서 어린 여주인을 정면으로 바라보며 말했다. \"제가 만약 그 남자들 중에서도 제일 맹탕인 그 작자보다 사내다움이 못하다면, 다시는 제 머리에 자부심 같은 건 안 가질 거예요, 머리를 귀 뒤로 넘겨 묶고, 가장자리 장식 하나 없는 거친 모자나 쓰고 다니다가, 죽음이 저를 이 하찮음에서 풀어 줄 때까지 그렇게 지내겠어요. 제가 아마조네스는 아닐지 몰라요, 플로이 아가씨, 그런 흉한 꼴로 제 몸을 깎아내릴 마음도 없고요, 하지만 어쨌든 저는 쉽게 포기하는 사람은 아니에요, 그렇고말고요.\"\n\n\"포기하다니! 뭘?\" 플로렌스가 겁에 질린 얼굴로 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 아무것도요, 아가씨,\" 수전이 말했다. \"맙소사, 아무것도 아니에요! 그저 그 퍼치라는, 젖은 종이로 만 머리카락 같은 남자 이야기예요, 누구라도 손가락 하나로 거의 없애 버릴 수 있을 것 같은 사람이라서, 누가 그 사람을 가엾이 여겨 좋은 일 좀 해 준다면 정말 모두에게 다 복된 일일 거예요!\"\n\n\"그 사람이 배를 포기하는 거야, 수전?\" 플로렌스가 새파랗게 질려 물었다.\n\n\"아니에요, 아가씨,\" 수전이 응수했다. \"그 사람이 감히 제 면전에서 그러는 꼴을 좀 보고 싶네요! 아니에요, 아가씨, 다만 월터 씨가 퍼치 부인에게 보내 주기로 했다는 그 골치 아픈 생강인지 뭔지를 가지고 계속 떠들면서, 우울하게 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는, 그게 오기를 바란다, 어쨌든 이번 일에는 제때 못 오겠지만 다음번엔 될지도 모른다, 그러는데, 정말이지,\" 니퍼 양이 더욱 경멸을 담아 말했다. \"그 사람 때문에 제 인내심이 다 바닥나요, 저도 어지간한 건 다 참지만, 제가 낙타는 아니거든요, 그리고,\" 잠시 생각한 뒤 수전이 덧붙였다. \"제가 저를 알기로는, 단봉낙타도 아니고요.\"\n\n\"또 무슨 말을 하던, 수전?\" 플로렌스가 간곡히 물었다. \"말해 주지 않을래?\"\n\n\"아가씨한테 무엇이든, 죄다 말씀 안 드릴 리가 있겠어요, 플로이 아가씨!\" 수전이 말했다. \"아니, 아무것도 아니에요, 아가씨, 그 사람 말이, 배에 대해 사람들이 두루 수군대기 시작했다, 그 항로로 다니는 배 중에 이렇게 오래 소식 없는 배는 반만큼도 없었다, 그리고 어제 선장 부인이 사무소에 왔는데 그 일로 좀 마음이 상한 것 같더라, 그러는데, 그거야 누구라도 할 수 있는 말이고, 우리도 거의 그 정도는 진작 알고 있었잖아요.\"\n\n\"집을 떠나기 전에 월터의 삼촌을 찾아뵈어야겠어,\" 플로렌스가 서둘러 말했다. \"오늘 아침에 가서 뵐래. 곧장 걸어서 가자, 수전.\"\n\n니퍼 양은 그 제안에 반대할 거리가 없었고 기꺼이 따랐으므로, 둘은 곧 채비를 마치고 거리로 나서, 자그마한 항해사 인형을 향해 길을 떠났다.\n\n브로글리라는 중개인이 가게를 차지하던 날, 그리고 첨탑 하나하나에서까지 압류가 집행되는 것처럼 느껴지던 그날, 가련한 월터가 커틀 선장에게로 갔을 때의 그 심정은, 지금 플로렌스가 솔 삼촌에게로 향하는 심정과 거의 같았다. 다만 한 가지 차이가 있었으니, 플로렌스는 자기가 어쩌면 월터를 위험에 빠뜨린 무고한 빌미였을지도 모른다는 생각, 그리고 그를 아끼는 모든 이를—자기 자신을 포함해—애타는 불안 속에 몰아넣은 빌미였을지도 모른다는 생각에 더한 고통을 겪었다. 그 밖에는, 불확실함과 위험이 모든 것 위에 쓰여 있는 듯했다. 첨탑과 지붕 위의 풍향계들은 폭풍 같은 바람을 암시하며 신비롭게 움직였고, 수많은 유령의 손가락처럼, 어쩌면 거대한 난파선의 잔해가 떠다니고 무력한 사람들이 그 위에서 흔들리며 헤아릴 수 없는 바닷물만큼 깊은 잠에 빠져드는 위험한 바다를 가리켰다. 플로렌스는 시내로 들어와 함께 이야기하는 신사들 곁을 지날 때, 그들이 그 배 이야기를 하며 배가 침몰했다고 말하는 것을 들을까 두려웠다. 굽이치는 파도와 싸우는 배들을 그린 그림과 판화들이 그녀를 공포로 가득 채웠다. 연기와 구름은 비록 잔잔히 움직였으나 그녀의 불안에 비하면 너무 빨리 움직여, 바로 지금 대양 위에 폭풍이 몰아치고 있는 것은 아닐까 두렵게 했다. 수전 니퍼도 비슷하게 영향을 받았는지는 알 수 없으나, 사람이 좀 몰리는 곳마다 사내아이들과 실랑이하느라 정신이 온통 팔려 있었으니—그 부류의 인간과 그녀 사이에는 마주칠 때마다 어김없이 터져 나오는 어떤 타고난 적의가 있었던 까닭에—길에서 지적인 활동을 할 여유는 별로 없었던 모양이다.\n\n마침 길 건너편 나무 항해사 인형 맞은편에 제때 다다라 길을 건널 기회를 기다리던 두 사람은, 처음에 기구 제작자의 문간에 둥근 머리의 한 소년이 통통한 얼굴을 하늘로 향한 채 서 있는 것을 보고 조금 놀랐다. 두 사람이 그를 바라보고 있는데, 그 소년이 별안간 양손 손가락 두 개씩을 제 널찍한 입속에 쑤셔 넣더니, 그 장치의 힘을 빌려 공중 상당히 높은 곳에 있는 비둘기 몇 마리를 향해 놀랄 만큼 날카롭게 휘파람을 불었다.\n\n\"리처즈 부인 큰아들이에요, 아가씨!\" 수전이 말했다. \"리처즈 부인 인생의 골칫거리고요!\"\n\n폴리가 플로렌스에게 자기 아들이자 후계자의 되살아난 앞날을 이야기해 준 적이 있었으므로, 플로렌스는 이 만남에 대비가 되어 있었다. 그리하여 좋은 기회가 생기자 두 사람은 리처즈 부인의 그 골칫덩이를 더는 눈여겨보지 않고 서둘러 길을 건넜다. 그 스포츠광은 둘이 다가오는 것을 알아채지 못한 채 다시 있는 힘껏 휘파람을 불었고, 이어 흥분에 도취해 \"도망친다! 후—웁! 도망친다!\" 하고 고함쳤다. 이 외침은 양심에 찔린 비둘기들에게 어찌나 효과가 있었던지, 원래 의도했던 듯 잉글랜드 북부의 어느 도시로 곧장 가는 대신 빙글빙글 돌며 머뭇거리기 시작했다. 그러자 리처즈 부인의 맏이는 또 한 번 휘파람으로 그것들을 꿰뚫고는, 거리의 소음을 뚫고 솟구치는 목소리로 다시 외쳤다. \"도망친다! 후—웁! 도망친다!\"\n\n이 황홀경에서 그는 니퍼 양의 쿡 찌름에 의해 갑작스레 지상의 사물로 불려 내려왔고, 그 찌름에 가게 안으로 밀려 들어갔다.\n\n\"리처즈 부인이 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 네 걱정에 애를 태웠는데, 네 뉘우침이라는 게 고작 이거냐?\" 수전이 쿡 찌른 손길을 따라 들어가며 말했다. \"길스 씨는 어디 계셔?\"\n\n플로렌스가 뒤따라 들어오는 것을 본 롭은, 니퍼 양을 향한 처음의 반항적인 눈빛을 누그러뜨리고, 플로렌스에게 경의를 표하느라 주먹을 머리에 갖다 댄 다음, 수전에게 길스 씨는 외출 중이라고 말했다.\n\n\"모셔 와,\" 니퍼 양이 위엄 있게 말했다. \"그리고 우리 아가씨가 와 계신다고 말씀드려.\"\n\n\"어디 가셨는지 모르는데요,\" 롭이 말했다.\n\n\"그게 네 뉘우침이라는 거냐?\" 수전이 쏘아붙이듯 날카롭게 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 어디로 가야 할지도 모르는데 어떻게 모셔 와요?\" 시달린 롭이 칭얼거렸다. \"어떻게 그렇게 막무가내세요?\"\n\n\"길스 씨가 언제 돌아오신다고 하셨니?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아가씨,\" 롭이 또 한 번 주먹을 머리에 갖다 대며 대답했다. \"오후 일찍 돌아오신다고 하셨어요, 지금부터 두 시간쯤 뒤요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"조카 일로 무척 걱정하고 계시니?\" 수전이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아가씨,\" 롭이 플로렌스에게 대답하기를 더 좋아하며 니퍼를 무시한 채 응수했다. \"제가 보기엔 아주 많이 그러세요. 한 번에 십오 분도 집 안에 못 계세요, 아가씨. 한자리에 오 분을 못 붙어 계시고요. 그냥 막—꼭 도망친 놈처럼 돌아다니세요,\" 롭이 창문 너머로 비둘기를 한번 보려고 허리를 굽혔다가, 또 한 번 휘파람을 불려고 손가락을 입까지 반쯤 가져가던 참에 스스로를 멈추며 말했다.\n\n\"길스 씨의 친구분 중에 커틀 선장이라는 분을 아니?\" 플로렌스가 잠시 생각한 뒤 물었다.\n\n\"갈고리 달린 분이요, 아가씨?\" 롭이 왼손을 비틀어 보이며 응수했다. \"네, 아가씨. 그저께 여기 오셨어요.\"\n\n\"그 뒤로는 안 오셨고?\" 수전이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아가씨,\" 롭이 여전히 플로렌스에게 대답하며 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 월터의 삼촌이 거기 가셨는지도 몰라, 수전,\" 플로렌스가 그녀에게 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"커틀 선장 댁에요, 아가씨?\" 롭이 끼어들었다. \"아니에요, 거기 안 가셨어요, 아가씨. 커틀 선장이 들르거든, 어제 못 뵈어서 얼마나 놀랐는지 모른다고 전하고, 돌아오실 때까지 붙들어 두라고 특별히 일러두셨거든요.\"\n\n\"커틀 선장이 어디 사시는지 아니?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n롭은 안다고 대답하고는, 가게 책상 위의 기름때 묻은 양피지 장부를 펼쳐, 주소를 소리 내어 읽었다.\n\n플로렌스는 다시 하녀에게 돌아서서 낮은 목소리로 의논했고, 그동안 둥근 눈의 롭은 후원자의 은밀한 당부를 명심한 채 지켜보고 귀를 기울였다. 플로렌스는 커틀 선장의 집으로 가서, '아들과 후계자' 호의 소식이 없는 것에 대해 그가 어떻게 생각하는지 그의 입으로 직접 듣고, 가능하다면 그를 데려와 솔 삼촌을 위로해 드리자고 제안했다. 수전은 처음에 거리가 멀다는 이유로 약간 반대했으나, 여주인이 삯마차를 언급하자 그 반대를 거두고 동의했다. 이 결론에 이르기까지 둘 사이에 몇 분간 논의가 오갔는데, 그동안 멀뚱히 바라보던 롭은 두 말하는 이에게 바짝 주의를 기울이며 번갈아 한쪽씩 귀를 기울였으니, 마치 그 논쟁의 중재인으로 임명되기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n이윽고 롭이 마차를 부르러 보내졌고, 그동안 손님들이 가게를 봐 주었다. 그가 마차를 가져오자 둘은 그것에 올라타며, 돌아오는 길에 꼭 다시 들르겠다는 말을 솔 삼촌에게 남겼다. 롭은 마차가 이제는 보이지 않게 된 비둘기들만큼이나 시야에서 사라질 때까지 그 뒤를 멀거니 바라보다가, 더없이 부지런한 태도로 책상 뒤에 앉았다. 그러고는 일어난 일을 하나도 잊지 않으려고, 잉크를 엄청나게 소비해 가며 여러 작은 종잇조각에 그것을 적어 두었다. 이 문서들은 행여 잃어버린대도 아무것도 누설할 위험이 없었으니, 글자가 미처 마르기도 전에 롭에게는, 마치 자기가 그것을 만드는 데 아무런 관여도 하지 않은 것처럼 심오한 수수께끼가 되어 버렸기 때문이다.\n\n그가 아직 이 일에 분주한 동안, 삯마차는 회전 다리며 진창길이며 건널 수 없는 운하며 술통을 실은 짐마차 행렬이며 강낭콩 밭이며 자그마한 빨래터며, 그 지방에 흔한 그런 숱한 장애물들로부터 듣도 보도 못한 곤경을 겪은 끝에, 브리그 플레이스 모퉁이에 멈췄다. 여기서 내린 플로렌스와 수전 니퍼는 거리를 걸어 내려가 커틀 선장의 거처를 찾았다.\n\n공교롭게도 그날은 운 나쁘게 맥스팅거 부인의 대청소 날 가운데 하나였다. 이런 날이면 맥스팅거 부인은 새벽 두 시 사십오 분에 순경에게 깨워져, 좀처럼 이튿날 밤 열두 시 전에는 굴복하지 않았다. 이 행사의 주된 목적은, 맥스팅거 부인이 동틀 무렵 모든 가구를 뒷마당으로 옮기고, 온종일 나막신을 신은 채 집 안을 돌아다니다가, 날이 어두워진 뒤 그 가구를 다시 들여놓는 데 있는 듯했다. 이 의식은 어린 맥스팅거 자식들이라는 그 비둘기들을 몹시 떨게 했으니, 그들은 그런 때면 발바닥을 둘 안식처조차 찾지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 이 엄숙한 행사가 진행되는 동안 어미 새에게 적잖이 쪼이기 일쑤였다.\n\n플로렌스와 수전 니퍼가 맥스팅거 부인의 문 앞에 나타난 바로 그 순간, 그 훌륭하나 가공할 만한 여인은 두 살 석 달 된 알렉산더 맥스팅거를 복도를 따라 끌고 가, 거리 보도 위에 앉은 자세로 강제로 내려놓는 중이었다. 알렉산더는 매를 맞은 뒤 숨을 참아 얼굴이 새카매져 있었는데, 이런 경우에는 대개 차가운 포석이 강력한 회복제로 작용한다고 여겨졌기 때문이다.\n\n여자로서, 또 어미로서 맥스팅거 부인의 감정은, 플로렌스의 얼굴에 어린 알렉산더를 향한 연민의 빛을 보고 모욕당했다. 그리하여 맥스팅거 부인은 약하게 호기심이나 채우기보다 우리 본성의 가장 고귀한 그 감정을 내세워, 포석에 앉히기 전과 앉히는 동안에 걸쳐 알렉산더를 흔들고 두들겼으며, 낯선 이들에게는 더 이상 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않았다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 부인,\" 아이가 다시 숨을 찾아 그것을 쓰고 있을 때 플로렌스가 말했다. \"여기가 커틀 선장 댁인가요?\"\n\n\"아니오,\" 맥스팅거 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"9번지가 아닌가요?\" 플로렌스가 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n\"누가 9번지가 아니라고 했소?\" 맥스팅거 부인이 말했다.\n\n수전 니퍼가 즉시 끼어들어, 맥스팅거 부인이 그 말로 무슨 뜻을 말하는 건지, 또 지금 누구에게 말하고 있는지 알기나 하느냐고 물었다.\n\n맥스팅거 부인은 그 대꾸로 그녀를 위아래로 훑어보았다. \"커틀 선장한테 무슨 볼일이오, 그게 알고 싶구려?\" 맥스팅거 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그러세요? 그럼 만족 못 하시게 돼서 안됐네요,\" 니퍼 양이 응수했다.\n\n\"쉿, 수전! 제발!\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"커틀 선장이 여기 안 사신다면, 어디 사시는지 좀 일러 주실 수 있을까요, 부인.\"\n\n\"누가 여기 안 산다고 했소?\" 그 무자비한 맥스팅거가 되받았다. \"내가 여긴 커틀 선장 집이 아니라고 했지—실제로도 그 사람 집이 아니고—그게 그 사람 집이 되는 일은 천만에 없을 거고—커틀 선장은 집을 건사할 줄도 모르고—집을 가질 자격도 없으니—이건 내 집이오—위층을 커틀 선장한테 세준 거지, 오 나는 은혜도 모르는 짓을 하고, 돼지 앞에 진주를 던진 거라오!\"\n\n맥스팅거 부인은 이 말들을 늘어놓으며 목소리를 위층 창문을 향해 던졌고, 한 구절 한 구절을, 마치 무한한 총신을 가진 소총에서 쏘아 대듯 따로따로 날카롭게 터뜨렸다. 마지막 한 방이 끝난 뒤, 선장의 목소리가 자기 방에서 힘없이 항의하듯 \"아래쪽, 진정하시오!\" 하고 말하는 것이 들렸다.\n\n\"커틀 선장을 찾는다니, 저기 있소!\" 맥스팅거 부인이 화가 나서 손을 휘저으며 말했다. 플로렌스가 더 이상 입씨름하지 않고 대담하게 들어서고 수전이 뒤따르자, 맥스팅거 부인은 나막신을 신고 거니는 운동을 다시 시작했다. 그리고 (여전히 포석 위에 있던) 알렉산더 맥스팅거는 그 대화에 귀 기울이느라 울음을 멈추었다가 다시 통곡하기 시작했으며, 그 음울한 공연 내내—그것은 아주 기계적인 것이었다—주위 경치를 두루 둘러보는 것으로 스스로를 달랬으니, 그 시선의 끝은 삯마차였다.\n\n선장은 자기 방에서 두 손을 주머니에 찌르고 두 다리를 의자 밑으로 끌어당긴 채, 비누 거품과 물의 대양 한가운데쯤에 떠 있는 아주 자그마하고 황량한 섬 위에 앉아 있었다. 선장의 창문은 닦여 있었고, 벽도 닦여 있었으며, 난로도 닦여 있었고, 난로를 제외한 모든 것이 젖은 채 무른 비누와 모래로 번들거렸는데, 그 건어물 가게 같은 냄새가 공기에 배어 있었다. 그 음울한 광경 한가운데서, 자기 섬에 표류해 버려진 선장은 침통한 얼굴로 물의 황무지를 둘러보았으니, 마치 다정한 배 한 척이 그쪽으로 와서 자기를 태워 가 주기를 기다리는 듯했다.\n\n그러나 선장이 처량한 얼굴을 문 쪽으로 향하다가, 플로렌스가 하녀와 함께 나타난 것을 보았을 때, 그 놀라움은 무어라 말로 표현할 수가 없었다. 맥스팅거 부인의 웅변이 다른 모든 소리를 어렴풋이밖에 분간할 수 없게 만들어 놓은 터라, 그는 술집 심부름꾼이나 우유 배달부보다 더 귀한 손님은 기대하지 않았다. 그래서 플로렌스가 나타나 섬의 가장자리로 다가와 자기 손에 손을 얹었을 때, 선장은 그녀를 순간 '방황하는 네덜란드인'* 가족의 젊은 일원쯤으로 여기기라도 한 듯 아연실색하여 일어섰다.\n* 방황하는 네덜란드인: 영원히 바다를 떠도는 저주받은 유령선과 그 선장에 관한 항해 전설.\n\n그러나 곧 침착함을 되찾은 선장은, 무엇보다 먼저 그녀를 마른 땅에 올려놓는 일에 마음을 썼고, 팔 한 번 놀려 다행히 그 일을 해냈다. 그러고서 큰 바다로 나아간 커틀 선장은 니퍼 양의 허리를 안아 그녀 또한 섬으로 옮겨다 놓았다. 그런 다음 커틀 선장은 더없는 경의와 감탄을 담아 플로렌스의 손을 들어 입술에 가져갔고, (섬이 세 사람을 두기에는 충분치 않았으므로) 조금 물러서서, 비누 거품과 물속에서 마치 새로운 종류의 트리톤*처럼 그녀를 향해 환하게 웃었다.\n* 트리톤: 그리스 신화의 바다 신으로, 상반신은 사람, 하반신은 물고기 형상.\n\n\"우리를 보고 놀라셨겠지요,\" 플로렌스가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n이루 말할 수 없이 흐뭇해진 선장은 대답 대신 자기 갈고리에 입을 맞추고는, 그 말 속에 정교하고 섬세한 찬사라도 담긴 양 으르렁거렸다. \"대기하라! 대기하라!\"\n\n\"하지만 도무지 가만있을 수가 없었어요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"사랑하는 월터에 대해—이제는 제 오빠인 월터에 대해—선장님이 어떻게 생각하시는지, 무언가 두려워할 일이 있는지, 그리고 그 애 소식을 들을 때까지 매일 그 가엾은 삼촌을 위로하러 가 주시지 않겠는지 여쭤보지 않고는요.\"\n\n이 말에 커틀 선장은 무심결의 몸짓처럼 손을 머리로 가져갔으나, 거기에는 그 단단한 광택 모자가 없었고, 그는 당황한 기색을 보였다.\n\n\"월터의 안전이 조금이라도 걱정되시나요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다. 선장은 (그녀의 얼굴에 그토록 황홀해진 나머지) 그 얼굴에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었고, 그녀는 그녀대로, 그의 대답이 진심인지 확인하려고 그를 간절히 바라보았다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 내 마음의 기쁨이여,\" 커틀 선장이 말했다. \"두렵지 않습니다. 월'터는 모진 풍파를 숱하게 헤쳐 나갈 녀석이지요. 월'터는 한 녀석이 할 수 있는 만큼 그 배에 성공을 가져다줄 녀석입니다. 월'터는,\" 선장이 어린 친구를 칭찬하느라 눈을 반짝이며, 멋진 인용구를 알리려 갈고리를 치켜들고 말했다. \"말하자면 내적이고 기개 있는 파악의, 외적이고 가시적인 표징이라 할 수 있지요, 발견하거든 적어 둘 것이라.\"\n\n플로렌스는 선장이 분명 그 말에 의미가 가득하고 더없이 만족스럽다고 여기는 듯했으나, 도무지 그 뜻을 이해하지 못한 채, 무언가 더 듣고 싶다는 듯 부드럽게 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"두렵지 않습니다, 내 마음의 기쁨이여,\" 선장이 다시 말했다. \"그 위도에서 보기 드물게 사나운 날씨가 있었던 건 부인할 수 없고, 배가 떠밀리고 또 떠밀리고 휩쓸려 나가, 어쩌면 세상 저편까지 갔을지도 모르지요. 하지만 그 배는 좋은 배고, 그 녀석은 좋은 녀석입니다. 그리고 다행히도,\" 선장이 가볍게 머리를 숙였다. \"참나무 같은 단단한 심장은, 그게 쌍돛배에 있든 가슴속에 있든 쉽게 부서지지 않는 법이지요. 여기서는 그게 두 가지 다 해당되니, 그야말로 닻줄을 한 바퀴 단단히 감은 셈이고, 그래서 나는 아직은 조금도 두렵지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"아직은요?\" 플로렌스가 되물었다.\n\n\"조금도요,\" 선장이 자기 쇠손에 입을 맞추며 응수했다. \"그리고 두려워지기 시작하기 전에, 내 마음의 기쁨이여, 월'터가 그 섬에서든 어느 항구에서든 집으로 편지를 보내, 모든 걸 팽팽하게, 배 모양 반듯하게 갖춰 놓을 겁니다.\" 그러고서 늙은 솔 길스에 관해서는, 여기서 선장이 엄숙해졌다. \"그분이라면, 죽음이 우리를 갈라놓을 때까지 곁을 지키고 결코 버리지 않을 겁니다, 그리고 폭풍의 바람이 불고, 불고, 불 때면—교리문답서를 뒤져 보시오,\" 선장이 곁들여 말했다. \"거기에 그런 표현들이 나오니까요—만일 솔 길스에게, 자기가 맞붙는 어떤 일에도 능히 맞먹는 정신을 지녔고, 도제 시절에 거의 박살이 날 뻔했으며, 이름이 번스비라 하는, 한 뱃사람의 견해를 듣는 게 위로가 된다면, 그 사람이 그분의 거실에서 그분이 깜짝 놀랄 만한 견해를 들려줄 겁니다. 아!\" 커틀 선장이 으스대며 말했다. \"마치 가서 문에 머리를 박은 것만큼이나 말이지요!\"\n\n\"그분을 만나러 이 신사분을 모셔 가서, 뭐라고 하시는지 들어 봐요,\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다. \"지금 우리와 함께 가시겠어요? 여기 마차가 있어요.\"\n\n선장은 다시 손을 머리로 가져갔으나 거기엔 그 단단한 광택 모자가 없었고, 당황한 기색을 보였다. 그러나 바로 그 순간, 더없이 기이한 현상이 일어났다. 아무런 예고도 없이, 그리고 보아하니 저절로 문이 열리더니, 문제의 그 단단한 광택 모자가 새처럼 방 안으로 휙 날아들어 선장의 발치에 묵직하게 내려앉았다. 그러고 나서 문은 열렸을 때만큼이나 거칠게 닫혔고, 그 기적을 설명해 줄 어떤 일도 뒤따르지 않았다.\n\n커틀 선장은 모자를 집어 들고, 흥미와 환영의 눈길로 그것을 이리저리 돌려 본 뒤, 소매에 대고 닦기 시작했다. 그러면서 선장은 손님들을 유심히 바라보며 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"보다시피 어제도 오늘 아침에도 솔 길스에게 들이닥쳤어야 했는데, 그 여자가—그 여자가 이걸 가져가서 안 내놓았지요. 그게 이 일의 전말입니다.\"\n\n\"세상에, 누가요?\" 수전 니퍼가 물었다.\n\n\"이 집 안주인이오, 아가씨,\" 선장이 거친 속삭임으로 응수하며 비밀 신호를 보냈다. \"이 마룻장 닦는 일을 두고 몇 마디 주고받았는데, 그 여자가—요컨대,\" 선장이 문을 흘끔거리며 긴 한숨으로 마음을 달래고 말했다. \"내 자유를 막아 버린 거지요.\"\n\n\"오! 그 여자가 저를 상대했으면 좋았을 텐데!\" 수전이 그 소망의 기세로 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"제가 막아 버렸을 거예요!\"\n\n\"그러시겠소, 정말로, 아가씨?\" 선장이 미심쩍게 고개를 저으면서도, 그 아리따운 지망자의 필사적인 용기를 분명한 감탄으로 바라보며 응수했다. \"글쎄요. 어려운 항해입니다. 그 여자는 상대하기가 아주 까다로워요, 아가씨. 어느 쪽으로 뱃머리를 돌릴지 도무지 알 수가 없거든요. 한순간엔 돛을 잔뜩 펴고, 다음 순간엔 휙 돌아서서 덤벼들지요. 그러다 그 여자가 발끈하면,\" 선장이 이마에 땀이 배어 나오며 말했다. 이 문장의 결말로는 휘파람만큼 강조될 만한 것이 없었으므로, 선장은 떨리게 휘파람을 불었다. 그러고 나서 다시 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는, 니퍼 양의 헌신적인 용기에 대한 감탄으로 되돌아가, 소심하게 되풀이했다. \"정말 그러시겠소, 아가씨?\"\n\n수전은 그저 새침하게 미소로만 대답했으나, 그 미소가 어찌나 도전으로 가득했던지, 플로렌스가 안달하며 신탁 같은 번스비에게 즉시 가 보자고 다시 제안하지 않았던들, 커틀 선장이 얼마나 오래 그것을 바라보며 넋을 잃고 서 있었을지 알 수 없는 노릇이었다. 이렇게 자기 할 일을 일깨워진 선장은 광택 모자를 단단히 쓰고, 월터에게 준 그 지팡이를 대신해 마련해 둔 또 다른 옹이투성이 지팡이를 집어 들고는, 플로렌스에게 팔을 내밀어, 적진을 헤치고 나아갈 채비를 했다.\n\n그러나 알고 보니 맥스팅거 부인은 이미 항로를 바꾸어, 선장이 그녀가 곧잘 그런다고 말했던 대로, 전혀 새로운 방향으로 뱃머리를 돌린 상태였다. 일행이 아래층으로 내려가 보니 그 본보기 같은 여인은 현관 계단에서 깔개를 두들기고 있었고, 알렉산더는 여전히 포석 위에서 먼지 안개 속에 어렴풋이 어른거렸다. 맥스팅거 부인은 자기 집안일에 어찌나 몰두해 있던지, 커틀 선장과 손님들이 지나갈 때 오히려 더 세게 두들겨 댈 뿐, 말로도 몸짓으로도 그들이 곁에 있다는 기색을 전혀 내비치지 않았다.\n선장은 이 손쉬운 탈출에 어찌나 흡족했던지—깔개의 효과가 그에게는 코담배를 듬뿍 들이마신 것과 같아서, 얼굴에 눈물이 흐를 만큼 재채기를 해 댔는데도—자기 행운을 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다. 그래서 문과 삯마차 사이에서 한 번 이상 어깨 너머를 돌아보았으니, 맥스팅거 부인이 여태 뒤쫓아 오는 건 아닐까 하는 뚜렷한 우려에서였다.\n\n그러나 일행은 그 무시무시한 화공선(火攻船)에게서 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 브리그 플레이스 모퉁이에 이르렀다. 그리고 선장은—그 기사도 정신이, 안으로 들어와 타라고 간청받았는데도 숙녀들과 함께 마차 안에 타기를 허락하지 않았으므로—마부석에 올라, 번스비 선장의 배가 있는 쪽으로 마부를 인도했으니, 그 배는 '신중한 클라라' 호라 불렸고 래트클리프 바로 곁에 정박해 있었다.\n\n이 위대한 지휘관의 배가 오백 척쯤 되는 동료 배들 사이에 끼여 있는 부두에 다다랐는데, 그 뒤엉킨 삭구들은 반쯤 쓸려 내려간 거대한 거미줄처럼 보였다. 커틀 선장은 마차 창에 모습을 드러내, 플로렌스와 니퍼 양에게 자기와 함께 배에 올라가자고 청했다. 번스비가 숙녀에 관한 한 더없이 마음이 무른 사람이라, 그를 '신중한 클라라' 호로 소개하는 것보다 그 광대한 지성을 조화로운 상태로 이끄는 데 더 좋은 것은 없으리라고 말하면서였다.\n\n플로렌스는 선뜻 응했다. 선장은 그 작은 손을 자기 거대한 손바닥에 쥐고서, 보기에 흐뭇한 후견과 부정(父情)과 자부심과 격식이 뒤섞인 표정으로, 몹시 더러운 갑판 여러 개를 가로질러 그녀를 이끌었다. 마침내 클라라 호에 이르러 보니, 그 신중한 배는 (배들이 늘어선 줄 바깥에 떠 있었는데) 건널 판자가 치워진 채, 가장 가까운 이웃 배와 자기 사이에 강물 여섯 자가량을 두고 있었다. 커틀 선장의 설명으로 미루어 보건대, 위대한 번스비도 그 자신처럼 셋집 안주인에게 모질게 시달리는지라, 안주인의 그때그때 처사가 더는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 가혹해지면 최후의 수단으로 둘 사이에 이런 심연을 두는 것이었다.\n\n\"클라라 호 어어이!\" 선장이 양손을 입 양옆에 대고 외쳤다.\n\n\"어어이!\" 마치 선장의 메아리처럼 한 소년이 아래에서 굴러 올라오며 외쳤다.\n\n\"번스비 배에 있나?\" 선장이 두 자밖에 안 되는 거리인데도 마치 반 마일 떨어진 양 우렁찬 목소리로 그 소년을 향해 외쳤다.\n\n\"예, 예!\" 소년이 같은 어조로 외쳤다.\n\n그러고 나서 소년이 커틀 선장에게 판자를 밀어 내밀었고, 선장은 그것을 조심스레 맞춰 놓고 플로렌스를 건너게 한 다음, 곧 니퍼 양을 데리러 돌아왔다. 그렇게 두 사람은 '신중한 클라라' 호의 갑판 위에 섰는데, 그 고정 삭구에는 갖가지 옷가지가 펄럭이며, 우설 몇 점과 고등어 몇 마리와 더불어 말려지고 있었다.\n\n곧 선실 격벽 위로 또 다른 격벽이—사람의, 그것도 아주 커다란 격벽이—천천히 솟아올랐다. 마호가니빛 얼굴에 한쪽 눈은 고정되어 있고 다른 한쪽 눈은 어떤 등대의 원리처럼 빙글빙글 도는 것이었다. 이 머리는 뱃밥처럼 텁수룩한 머리카락으로 장식되어 있었는데, 그 머리카락은 북이든 동이든 서든 남이든 어느 한 방향으로 쏠리는 법이 없이, 나침반의 네 방위 전부로, 그리고 그 위의 모든 지점으로 뻗쳐 있었다. 머리 뒤로는 완벽한 황무지 같은 턱이 따라 나왔고, 셔츠 깃과 목도리가, 그리고 두꺼운 방수 파일럿 외투가, 또 한 쌍의 두꺼운 방수 파일럿 바지가 따라 나왔으니, 그 바지춤은 어찌나 넓고 높던지 조끼 대용으로 쓰일 지경이라, 입은 이의 복장뼈 가까이에 주사위놀이 말 같은 묵직한 나무 단추 몇 개가 장식으로 달려 있었다. 이 바지의 아래쪽이 드러나자, 비로소 번스비의 전모가 밝혀졌다. 양손은 어마어마하게 큰 주머니에 찌른 채, 그 시선은 커틀 선장이나 숙녀들이 아니라 돛대 꼭대기를 향하고 있었다.\n\n이 철학자는 몸집이 크고 힘이 셌으며, 더없이 붉은 그 얼굴에는 과묵함의 표정이 왕좌에 앉은 듯 자리 잡고 있었으니, 그것은 과묵함이라는 자질이 자랑스럽게 두드러지는 그의 성격과 어긋나지 않았다. 그의 그 심오한 풍모는, 비록 그와 허물없는 사이인 커틀 선장조차 거의 주눅 들게 할 정도였다. 번스비는 평생 단 한 번도 놀라움을 드러낸 적이 없으며 놀라움이 무엇인지조차 모르는 것으로 여겨진다고 플로렌스에게 속삭이며, 선장은 그가 돛대 꼭대기를 바라보다가 이윽고 수평선을 훑는 것을 지켜보았다. 그리고 그 빙글빙글 도는 눈이 자기 쪽으로 돌아오는 듯하자, 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"번스비, 친구, 형편이 어떤가?\"\n\n깊고 거칠고 쉰 목소리가, 번스비와는 아무 연관도 없는 듯하고 그의 얼굴에는 분명 아무런 변화도 일으키지 않은 채 대답했다. \"예, 예, 동료, 어떻게 지내나?\" 동시에 번스비의 오른손과 팔이 주머니에서 나와 선장의 손을 흔들고는 다시 들어갔다.\n\n\"번스비,\" 선장이 단번에 본론을 찔러 말했다. \"여기 자네가 있네, 정신을 갖춘 사람이자 견해를 내놓을 줄 아는 사람이지. 여기 한 젊은 숙녀가 내 친구 월'터에 관해 그 견해를 듣고 싶어 하네. 또 내 다른 친구 솔 길스에 관해서도 그렇고, 그분으로 말하자면 자네가 한번 가까이 사귀어 볼 만한 인물이라, 과학을 하는 사람이거든, 과학은 발명의 어머니요 어떤 법도 모르는 법이지. 번스비, 날 봐서, 침로를 돌려 우리와 함께 가 주겠나?\"\n\n그 위대한 지휘관은, 얼굴 표정으로 보아 늘 더없이 먼 거리의 무언가를 살피느라 십 마일 이내의 무엇에 대해서는 눈으로 아는 바가 전혀 없는 듯했는데, 아무런 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"여기 이 사람은,\" 선장이 그 아리따운 청중을 향해 말하며 내뻗은 갈고리로 그 지휘관을 가리켰다. \"살아 있는 어떤 사람보다도 더 많이 떨어져 본 사람이고, 선원 병원이 모든 환자에게 겪은 것보다 더 많은 사고를 자기 한 몸에 당해 본 사람입니다. 젊었을 적에 머리 바깥쪽에 활대며 가로대며 볼트를 어찌나 많이 맞았던지, 그걸로 채텀 조선소에 유람용 요트 한 척을 짓는 주문을 넣어도 될 만큼이었지요. 그런데도 그 방면에 관한 그의 견해로 말하자면, 바다 위에서든 뭍에서든 그만한 것이 없다, 이게 내 믿음입니다.\"\n\n그 둔중한 지휘관은 팔꿈치를 아주 살짝 떨어 이 찬사에 다소 만족을 표하는 듯했으나, 만일 그의 얼굴이 그의 시선만큼 멀리 떨어져 있었다 한들, 그의 생각 속에 무엇이 오가는지에 대해 보는 이들을 이보다 더 모르게 할 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"동료,\" 번스비가 별안간, 가로지른 활대 밑을 내다보려 허리를 굽히며 말했다. \"숙녀분들은 뭘 마실까?\"\n\n플로렌스와 결부된 그런 물음에 그 섬세함이 충격을 받은 커틀 선장은, 그 현인을 옆으로 데려가 귀에 대고 설명하는 듯하더니, 그를 데리고 아래로 내려갔다. 그곳에서 그가 기분 상하지 않도록 선장은 자기도 한잔 들이켰는데, 플로렌스와 수전이 열린 천창으로 내려다보니, 그 현인이 자기 침상과 아주 자그마한 놋쇠 벽난로 사이에 가까스로 몸 둘 자리를 찾아, 자기와 친구를 위한 술을 따르고 있었다. 둘은 곧 갑판에 다시 나타났고, 자기 계획의 성공에 의기양양해진 커틀 선장은 플로렌스를 마차로 도로 안내했다. 그동안 번스비는 니퍼 양을 호위하며 뒤따랐는데, 가는 길에 파일럿 외투를 입은 그 팔로 그녀를 (그 처녀가 몹시 분개하게도) 마치 푸른 곰처럼 껴안았다.\n\n선장은 자기 신탁자를 안에 태우고는, 그를 확보했다는 것, 그 정신을 삯마차 안에 들여놓았다는 것에 어찌나 의기양양했던지, 마부 뒤의 작은 창으로 플로렌스를 자주 들여다보며 미소로, 또 자기 이마를 톡톡 두드려, 번스비의 두뇌가 한창 그 일에 골몰하고 있다는 것을 그녀에게 넌지시 알리지 않고는 배길 수가 없었다. 그러는 동안 번스비는 여전히 니퍼 양을 껴안은 채(그의 친구 선장이 그의 마음의 무름을 과장한 것은 아니었으므로) 한결같이 진중한 태도를 지켰으며, 그녀에 대해서도 다른 무엇에 대해서도 아무런 의식의 기색을 보이지 않았다.\n\n집에 돌아와 있던 솔 삼촌이 문간에서 그들을 맞아, 즉시 그 자그마한 뒤쪽 거실로 안내했는데, 그곳은 월터의 부재로 묘하게 달라져 있었다. 탁자 위에, 그리고 방 곳곳에, 마음 무거운 기구 제작자가 그 사라진 배를 바다 위로 거듭거듭 좇아 본 해도와 지도들이 펼쳐져 있었다. 그는 아직 손에 쥐고 있는 컴퍼스 한 쌍으로, 바로 조금 전까지, 배가 이리저리 떠밀려 가려면 얼마나 멀리 떠밀렸어야 하는지를 재고 있었으며, 희망이 다하기까지 아직 오랜 시간이 걸릴 수밖에 없음을 입증해 보이려 애쓰던 참이었다.\n\n\"배가 달려갔을까,\" 솔 삼촌이 해도를 애처롭게 들여다보며 말했다. \"아니, 그건 거의 불가능하지. 아니면 날씨가 사나워서 떠밀렸을까—하지만 그것도 그럴듯하지 않아. 아니면 진로를 그만큼 바꿨을 가망이 조금이라도—하지만 나조차 그건 도무지 바라기 어렵구나!\" 이런 토막토막한 추측을 늘어놓으며, 가련한 늙은 솔 삼촌은 눈앞의 그 커다란 종이 위를 헤매었으나, 컴퍼스 한쪽 끝을 댈 만한, 희망적인 개연성이라곤 티끌만큼도 찾아낼 수 없었다.\n\n플로렌스는 이내—못 알아채기가 어려웠을 것이다—그 노인에게 묘한, 말로 표현하기 어려운 변화가 있음을 알아차렸다. 그의 태도가 평소보다 훨씬 더 안절부절못하고 들떠 있으면서도, 그 안에는 묘하게 모순된 단호함이 깃들어 있어, 그녀를 몹시 어리둥절하게 했다. 그녀는 한 번 그가 종잡을 수 없이 마구잡이로 말한다고 여겼으니, 그날 아침 먼저 여기 왔을 때 그를 못 뵈어 아쉬웠다고 그녀가 말하자, 그는 처음에 자기가 그녀를 보러 갔었다고 대답했다가, 곧바로 그 대답을 거두고 싶어 하는 듯했기 때문이다.\n\n\"저를 보러 오셨다고요?\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"오늘요?\"\n\n\"그래, 아가씨,\" 솔 삼촌이 그녀를 보았다 시선을 피했다 하며 혼란스러운 태도로 응수했다. \"한 번 더 내 눈으로 너를 보고, 내 귀로 네 목소리를 듣고 싶었단다, 그—전에—\" 거기서 그는 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"전에 언제요? 무엇 전에요?\" 플로렌스가 그의 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 '전에'라고 했니?\" 늙은 솔이 응수했다. \"그랬다면, 내 사랑하는 아이의 소식을 듣기 전에, 라는 뜻이었을 게다.\"\n\n\"편찮으시군요,\" 플로렌스가 다정하게 말했다. \"너무 노심초사하셔서 분명 편찮으신 거예요.\"\n\n\"나는 괜찮다,\" 노인이 오른손을 꼭 쥐었다가 펴 보이며 응수했다. \"내 나이의 사람이 바랄 수 있는 만큼 멀쩡하고 단단하단다. 보아라! 떨림이 없지. 이 손의 주인이 어지간한 젊은이 못지않게 결단과 인내를 발휘할 수 있지 않겠느냐? 나는 그렇게 생각한다. 두고 보자꾸나.\"\n\n그의 말 속에서보다도 그 태도 속에 무언가가—비록 그 말들 또한 그녀의 마음에 남았지만—플로렌스에게 깊은 인상을 주어, 그녀는 그 순간 자기의 불안을 커틀 선장에게 털어놓았을 것이다. 만일 선장이 바로 그 순간을, 슬기로운 번스비의 견해를 청한 그 사정의 정황을 설명하고, 그 심오한 권위자에게 어서 그 견해를 내려 달라고 간청하는 데 쓰지 않았다면 말이다.\n\n번스비는 그 눈이 여전히 런던과 그레이브젠드 사이 중간쯤 되는 어디께를 향한 채, 두세 번 거친 오른팔을 내뻗어, 영감을 얻으려는 듯 그것을 니퍼 양의 아리따운 자태에 휘감으려 했다. 그러나 그 처녀가 못마땅한 기색으로 탁자 맞은편으로 몸을 빼 버린 탓에, '신중한 클라라' 호 지휘관의 무른 마음은 그 충동에 아무런 응답도 얻지 못했다. 이런 식으로 몇 차례 실패한 뒤, 그 지휘관은 아무에게도 아닌 듯 말하기 시작했으니, 아니, 차라리 그 안의 목소리가 마치 그가 어떤 거친 정령에 씌기라도 한 양, 그와는 전혀 별개로 제멋대로 말했다.\n\n\"내 이름은 잭 번스비요!\"\n\n\"세례명은 존이지요,\" 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르는 커틀 선장이 외쳤다. \"저 말 좀 들어 보시오!\"\n\n\"그리고 내가 말한 건,\" 그 목소리가 잠시 숙고한 뒤 이어 갔다. \"내가 지킨다.\"\n\n선장은 플로렌스를 팔에 낀 채 청중을 향해 고개를 끄덕였으니, 마치 \"이제 슬슬 나오는군. 내가 그를 데려온 게 바로 이 때문이오\"라고 말하는 듯했다.\n\n\"고로,\" 그 목소리가 계속했다. \"왜 아니겠소? 만일 그렇다면, 무슨 차이가 있겠소? 누구든 달리 말할 수 있겠소? 없지. 그러니 잠깐 멈추시오!\"\n\n이 지점까지 논증의 갈래를 좇은 뒤, 목소리는 멈추고 쉬었다. 그러고는 아주 느릿느릿 이렇게 이어 갔다.\n\n\"이 '아들과 후계자' 호가 가라앉았다고 내가 믿느냐, 친구들? 어쩌면. 내가 그렇다고 말하느냐? 어느 쪽이냐? 한 선장이 세인트조지 해협을 끼고 나아가 다운스로 향한다면, 그의 바로 앞에 무엇이 있느냐? 굿윈 모래톱이지. 그가 굿윈에 올라앉도록 강요받는 건 아니지만, 그럴 수도 있지. 이 소견의 방위는 그걸 어떻게 적용하느냐에 달려 있소. 그건 내 직분의 몫이 아니지. 그러니 잠깐 멈추고, 뱃머리 쪽을 환히 살피며, 행운이 함께하기를!\"\n\n여기서 목소리는 뒤쪽 거실을 빠져나가 거리로 나갔으니, '신중한 클라라' 호의 지휘관을 데리고 가서, 더없이 신속하게 그를 다시 배에 태워다 주었고, 거기서 그는 즉시 잠자리에 들어 낮잠으로 정신을 가다듬었다.\n\n그 현인의 가르침을 받든 학생들은—그의 지혜를 스스로 적용하라는, 번스비라는 삼각대의 주된 다리이자 어쩌면 다른 어떤 신탁의 의자에도 해당될 그 원칙에 따라 내버려진 채—약간의 막막함 속에 서로를 바라보았다. 한편 천진난만하게 지붕의 천창으로 들여다보며 엿듣는 자유를 누렸던 분쇄기 롭은, 몹시 짙은 혼란에 빠진 채 지붕에서 살그머니 내려왔다. 그러나 커틀 선장은, 번스비가 자기 명성을 정당화하고 이 엄숙한 자문을 훌륭하게 치러 낸 그 멋진 방식으로 그에 대한 감탄이 가능하다면 더 커진 터라, 번스비의 말은 확신 외에 다른 뜻이 아니다, 번스비에게는 불안 따위 없다, 그런 정신에서 나온 그런 견해야말로 희망 자신의 닻이며, 그것을 내릴 좋은 정박지까지 갖춘 셈이라고 설명하기 시작했다. 플로렌스는 선장이 옳다고 믿으려 애썼으나, 니퍼는 두 팔을 단단히 낀 채 단호히 부정하며 고개를 저었으니, 번스비를 퍼치 씨만큼도 믿지 않았다.\n\n그 철학자는 솔 삼촌을 발견했을 때와 거의 같은 상태로 남겨 둔 듯했으니, 노인은 여전히 컴퍼스를 손에 든 채 그 물의 세계를 헤매며 그것을 내려놓을 안식처를 찾지 못하고 있었다. 노인이 이 일에 몰두해 있는 동안 플로렌스가 귀에 속삭인 데 따라, 커틀 선장이 그의 어깨에 묵직한 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"기운 좀 어떻소, 솔 길스?\" 선장이 진심으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"그저 그렇소, 네드,\" 기구 제작자가 응수했다. \"오후 내내 떠올리고 있었다오, 우리 아이가 돔비 회사에 들어가던 바로 그날, 저녁을 먹으러 늦게 집에 와서, 지금 자네가 서 있는 바로 거기 앉아, 우리가 폭풍과 난파 이야기를 나누었는데, 내가 도무지 그 애를 그 화제에서 돌려놓을 수가 없었던 일을 말이오.\"\n\n그러나 자기 얼굴을 간절한 눈길로 살피는 플로렌스의 눈과 마주치자, 노인은 말을 멈추고 미소 지었다.\n\n\"기운 내시오, 오랜 친구!\" 선장이 외쳤다. \"씩씩하게! 내 말 들으시오, 솔 길스. 내 마음의 기쁨을 무사히 집까지 호송해 드린 다음,\" 여기서 선장이 플로렌스에게 갈고리로 입맞춤을 보냈다. \"다시 돌아와서 이 복된 날의 나머지 동안 당신을 예인해 가겠소. 어디든 함께 가서 저녁을 먹읍시다, 솔.\"\n\n\"오늘은 안 되네, 네드!\" 노인이 그 제안에 영문 모르게 깜짝 놀란 듯, 재빨리 말했다. \"오늘은 안 돼. 그럴 수가 없어!\"\n\n\"왜 안 되오?\" 선장이 놀라 그를 바라보며 응수했다.\n\n\"내가—내가 할 일이 너무 많아서. 내가—생각하고 정리해야 할 게 많아. 그럴 수가 없네, 네드, 정말로. 다시 나가서 혼자 있으면서, 오늘은 여러 가지 일에 마음을 쏟아야 해.\"\n\n선장은 기구 제작자를 보고, 플로렌스를 보고, 다시 기구 제작자를 보았다. \"그럼 내일,\" 마침내 그가 제안했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래. 내일,\" 노인이 말했다. \"내일 나를 생각해 주게. 내일이라고 해 두세.\"\n\n\"내가 일찍 여기로 오겠소, 명심하시오, 솔 길스,\" 선장이 다짐을 두었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래. 내일 아침 제일 먼저,\" 늙은 솔이 말했다. \"자, 그럼 잘 가게, 네드 커틀, 신의 가호가 있기를!\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 노인은 보기 드문 열렬함으로 선장의 두 손을 꼭 쥔 다음, 플로렌스에게 돌아서서 그녀의 두 손을 제 손에 감싸 입술에 갖다 댔다. 그러고는 몹시 유별난 다급함으로 그녀를 서둘러 마차로 데리고 나갔다. 어쨌거나 그가 커틀 선장에게 어찌나 인상을 남겼던지, 선장은 뒤에 남아, 아침까지 주인에게 각별히 다정하고 세심하게 대하라고 롭에게 일렀다. 그리고 그 당부를, 즉석에서 1실링을 지불하고 이튿날 정오 전에 6펜스를 더 주겠다는 약속으로 단단히 못 박았다. 이 친절한 임무를 마치고서, 자기를 플로렌스의 당연하고 정당한 호위병으로 여기는 커틀 선장은 막중한 사명감을 품고 마부석에 올라, 그녀를 집까지 바래다주었다. 헤어질 때 그는 솔 길스를 가까이서, 진실하게 곁을 지키겠노라고 그녀에게 다짐했다. 그리고 맥스팅거 부인에 관한 그 용감한 말을 잊지 못해, 한 번 더 수전 니퍼에게 물었다. \"그래도, 정말 그러시겠소, 아가씨?\"\n\n그 황량한 집이 두 사람을 안으로 들이고 문이 닫히자, 선장의 생각은 늙은 기구 제작자에게로 되돌아갔고, 그는 마음이 편치 않았다. 그래서 집으로 가는 대신 거리를 몇 차례 오르내렸고, 저녁이 될 때까지 짬을 메우느라, 시내의 어느 모난 자그마한 선술집에서 늦은 저녁을 먹었다. 그곳에는 쐐기처럼 생긴 공용 거실이 있었는데, 광택 모자들이 즐겨 드나드는 곳이었다. 선장의 주된 속셈은 날이 어두워진 뒤 솔 길스의 가게 앞을 지나며 창문 안을 들여다보는 것이었고, 실제로 그렇게 했다. 거실 문이 열려 있어, 그는 옛 친구가 안의 탁자에서 바삐, 꾸준히 무언가를 쓰고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 한편 자그마한 항해사 인형은 이미 밤이슬을 피해 들여놓아진 채, 계산대에서 그를 지켜보고 있었으며, 그 계산대 밑에서는 분쇄기 롭이 가게 문을 닫을 채비로 자기 잠자리를 펴고 있었다. 그 나무 뱃사람의 경내를 다스리는 고요함에 안심한 선장은, 아침 일찍 닻을 올리기로 마음먹고 브리그 플레이스를 향해 뱃머리를 돌렸다.\n\n바넷 스케틀스 경과 스케틀스 부인은 매우 선량한 사람들로, 템스 강변 풀럼에 자리한 어여쁜 별장에 살고 있었다. 그곳은 조정 경기가 마침 지나갈 때면 세상에서 가장 탐낼 만한 거처였으나, 다른 때에는 자잘한 불편함이 없지 않았는데, 그 가운데 강물이 이따금 응접실에 모습을 드러내는 일과, 그와 동시에 잔디밭과 관목 숲이 자취를 감추는 일을 꼽을 수 있었다.\n\n바넷 스케틀스 경은 자신의 인물됨을 주로 고풍스러운 금제 코담배갑과 묵직한 비단 손수건으로 드러냈는데, 그는 그 손수건을 마치 깃발처럼 위풍당당하게 주머니에서 꺼내어 양손으로 동시에 다루는 인상적인 태도를 지녔다. 바넷 경의 인생 목표는 끊임없이 자기 교제의 범위를 넓혀 가는 것이었다. 물에 떨어진 무거운 물체처럼—이런 비유로 이토록 훌륭한 신사를 깎아내릴 뜻은 없으나—사물의 본성상 바넷 경은 더 이상 자리가 남지 않을 때까지 자기 둘레로 점점 넓어지는 원을 퍼뜨릴 수밖에 없었다. 혹은 공기 중의 소리처럼, 그 진동이 어느 기발한 현대 철학자의 사색에 따르면 무한한 공간의 들판을 영원토록 여행해 갈 수도 있다는데, 바넷 스케틀스 경 또한 자기 도덕적 밧줄의 끝에 다다르는 것 외에는 그 무엇도 사교계라는 체계를 누비는 그의 발견 항해를 멈출 수 없었다.\n\n바넷 경은 사람과 사람을 알게 해 주는 일에 자부심을 느꼈다. 그는 그 일 자체를 좋아했고, 그것은 그가 가장 아끼는 목표를 진척시키기도 했다. 이를테면, 바넷 경이 운 좋게 법조계 신참이나 시골 신사를 붙잡아 자기의 환대 넘치는 별장으로 꾀어들인다면, 바넷 경은 그가 도착한 다음 날 아침 이렇게 말할 것이었다. \"자, 친애하는 선생, 알고 싶은 분이 있으신가요? 만나 보고 싶은 분이 누구신지요? 혹시 글 쓰는 사람들이나, 그림이나 조각 하는 사람들, 연기하는 사람들, 혹은 그런 부류에 관심이 있으신가요?\" 아마도 그 환자는 그렇다고 답하며 누군가의 이름을 댈 것이고, 바넷 경은 그 사람에 대해 프톨레마이오스 대왕만큼이나 개인적으로 아는 바가 없었을 것이다. 바넷 경은 자기가 그를 아주 잘 안다며 세상에 그보다 더 쉬운 일은 없다고 답하고는, 즉시 앞서 말한 그 사람을 찾아가 명함을 두고 짧은 쪽지를 적었다.—\"친애하는 선생—선생의 저명하신 지위에 따르는 부담이겠지요만—제 집에 머무는 벗이 당연히 간절히 바라는 바이며—스케틀스 부인과 저 또한 함께 청하옵나니—천재란 격식을 초월하는 법이오니, 부디 저희에게 그 기쁨을 베푸시는 빛나는 호의를 주시기 바랍니다,\" 운운—그리하여 돌 하나로 새 두 마리를, 그것도 못대가리만큼 확실하게 잡아 버렸다.\n\n코담배갑과 깃발을 한껏 동원한 채, 바넷 스케틀스 경은 플로렌스가 방문한 첫날 아침에 그녀에게 자기의 평소 그 질문을 던졌다. 플로렌스가 그에게 감사하며 특별히 보고 싶은 사람은 없다고 말했을 때, 그녀가 가엾게 잃어버린 월터를 가슴 저미는 아픔과 함께 떠올린 것은 자연스러운 일이었다. 바넷 스케틀스 경이 친절한 제안을 거듭하며 \"친애하는 돔비 아가씨, 당신의 훌륭하신 아버님께서—편지를 쓰실 때 부디 저와 스케틀스 부인의 안부를 정중히 전해 주십시오—당신이 알기를 바라실 만한 분이 정말로 한 명도 떠오르지 않으신가요?\" 하고 말했을 때, 그녀의 가엾은 고개가 조금 수그러지고, 부드럽게 아니라고 답하는 목소리가 떨린 것도 아마 자연스러운 일이었으리라.\n\n스케틀스 주니어는 크라바트를 잔뜩 빳빳하게 매고 기운은 한결 가라앉은 채 방학을 맞아 집에 와 있었는데, 플로렌스에게 자상하게 굴라는 훌륭한 어머니의 간곡한 당부에 자기가 부당한 대접을 받는 양 느끼는 모양이었다. 어린 바넷의 영혼이 부대끼는 또 다른, 그리고 더 깊은 고통은 블림버 박사 부부의 동행이었으니, 이들은 친가의 지붕 아래 머물도록 초대받은 터였고, 그 젊은 신사는 차라리 그들이 예리코에서 방학을 보냈으면 좋았을 거라고 자주 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"이제 추천하실 만한 분이 있으신가요, 블림버 박사?\" 바넷 스케틀스 경이 그 신사를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"매우 친절하시군요, 바넷 경,\" 블림버 박사가 응수했다. \"사실 딱히 떠오르는 사람은 없습니다. 저는 동료 인간들을 두루 알고 지내는 것을 좋아하지요, 바넷 경. 테렌티우스가 뭐라 했던가요? 아들을 둔 부모라면 누구든 제게는 흥미로운 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"블림버 부인께서는 어떤 주목할 만한 인물을 만나 보고 싶으신지요?\" 바넷 경이 정중하게 물었다.\n\n블림버 부인은 달콤한 미소를 지으며 하늘색 모자를 흔들고는, 바넷 경이 자기를 키케로와 알게 해 주실 수 있었다면 폐를 끼쳤을 테지만, 그런 소개는 가능치 않은 일이고, 자기는 이미 경과 그의 상냥한 부인의 우정을 누리고 있으며, 남편인 박사와 함께 사랑하는 아들에 대한 두 사람의 공동의 신뢰를 지니고 있으니—이 대목에서 어린 바넷이 코를 찡그리는 것이 보였다—더 바랄 것이 없다고 답했다.\n\n이런 사정이고 보니 바넷 경은 당분간 모인 사람들로 만족할 수밖에 없었다. 플로렌스는 그것이 기뻤다. 그녀에게는 그들 가운데서 추구해야 할 연구가 하나 있었는데, 그것은 그녀의 마음에 너무도 가까이 놓여 있었고 너무도 소중하고 중대하여, 다른 어떤 흥미에도 자리를 내줄 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n집에는 머물고 있는 아이들이 몇 있었다. 고향 맞은편의 그 발그레한 얼굴들처럼 아버지와 어머니에게 솔직하고 행복한 아이들이었다. 자기들의 사랑에 아무런 거리낌이 없어 그것을 자유로이 드러내는 아이들이었다. 플로렌스는 그 아이들의 비밀을 배우고자 했다. 자기가 놓쳐 버린 것이 무엇인지, 그 아이들은 알고 자기는 모르는 그 단순한 기술이 무엇인지, 어떻게 하면 그 아이들에게서 배워 아버지에게 그를 사랑한다는 것을 보여 주고 다시 그의 사랑을 얻을 수 있을지 알아내고자 했다.\n\n플로렌스는 여러 날 동안 그 아이들을 골똘히 관찰했다. 여러 번의 환한 아침에, 그녀는 찬란한 해가 떠오를 때 잠자리에서 일어나, 집안의 누구도 깨어나기 전에 강둑을 오르내리며 거닐고는, 그 아이들 방의 창문을 올려다보며 그토록 다정하게 보살핌받고 애틋하게 생각되며 잠들어 있는 그들을 떠올렸다. 그럴 때 플로렌스는 큰 집에 홀로 있을 때보다 더 외로움을 느꼈고, 때로는 여기보다 거기가 더 낫다고, 또래의 다른 아이들과 어울려 자기가 그들 모두와 얼마나 다른지 깨닫는 것보다 자신을 숨기는 편이 더 큰 평화가 있다고 생각하기도 했다. 그러나 자기 연구에 마음을 쏟으며, 그 힘겨운 책의 잎새를 한 장 넘길 때마다 가슴 깊이 사무쳤음에도, 플로렌스는 그들 가운데 머물며 인내하는 희망으로 그토록 갈망하던 그 앎을 얻으려 애썼다.\n\n아! 어떻게 그것을 얻을 것인가! 그 비밀스러운 매력을 그 시작에서부터 어떻게 알 것인가! 여기에는 아침이면 일어나고 밤이면 쉬러 누우면서 이미 아버지의 마음을 차지하고 있는 딸들이 있었다. 그들에게는 극복해야 할 거절도, 두려워할 냉랭함도, 펴 줘야 할 찌푸린 미간도 없었다. 아침이 깊어 가고 창문들이 하나씩 열리며 꽃잎 위의 이슬이 마르기 시작하고 어린 발들이 잔디밭을 거닐기 시작할 때, 플로렌스는 그 환한 얼굴들을 둘러보며 이 아이들에게서 배울 수 있는 것이 무엇일까 생각했다. 그들에게서 배우기에는 너무 늦었다. 저마다 두려움 없이 아버지에게 다가가, 언제든 준비된 입맞춤을 맞으러 입술을 내밀고, 자기를 어루만지려 굽혀 오는 목에 팔을 감을 수 있었다. 그녀는 그토록 대담하게 시작할 수가 없었다. 오! 연구를 거듭할수록 희망이 점점 더 줄어들기만 하는 것은 아닐까!\n\n그녀는 잘 기억하고 있었다. 어린아이였을 적 자기 것을 빼앗아 간 그 노파조차—그 노파의 모습과 그 집과 그녀가 한 말과 행동 모두가 인생의 그 이른 시기에 새겨진 무서운 인상의 지워지지 않는 날카로움으로 기억 속에 찍혀 있었는데—자기 딸을 다정하게 이야기했고, 그 노파마저도 제 아이와의 가망 없는 이별의 고통 속에서 얼마나 끔찍하게 부르짖었던가를. 그러나 자기 어머니는, 이 일을 떠올릴 때면 그녀는 다시 생각했다, 자기를 진심으로 사랑했었다고. 그러다가 때때로, 그녀의 생각이 자기와 아버지 사이의 그 공허로 휙 되돌아갈 때면, 플로렌스는 몸을 떨었고, 어머니가 살아남아 자기를 역시 싫어하게 되는 모습을—그 아버지를 자연스럽게 누그러뜨려야 했으나 요람에서부터 한 번도 그러지 못한, 그 알 수 없는 우아함이 자기에게 결여되어 있기에—마음속에 그려 보며 얼굴에 눈물이 솟구쳤다. 그녀는 이런 상상이 어머니의 추억에 죄를 짓는 일이며, 거기에는 아무런 진실도 없고 기댈 근거도 없다는 것을 알았다. 그럼에도 그녀는 아버지를 정당화하고 모든 잘못을 자기 안에서 찾으려 너무도 안간힘을 쓴 나머지, 그 상상이 거친 구름처럼 자기 마음의 먼 자락을 가로질러 지나가는 것을 막을 수가 없었다.\n\n플로렌스가 온 지 얼마 안 되어 다른 방문객들 가운데 한 아름다운 소녀가 끼어 왔는데, 그녀보다 서너 살 어린 고아 아이로, 백발의 부인인 고모와 함께였다. 그 부인은 플로렌스에게 많은 이야기를 건넸고, 저녁마다 그녀가 노래하는 것을 듣기를 무척 좋아하여(하기야 다들 그랬다), 그럴 때면 늘 어머니 같은 관심으로 그녀 가까이에 앉곤 했다. 집에 머문 지 겨우 이틀 되던 어느 따뜻한 아침, 플로렌스가 정원의 정자에 앉아, 사이를 가로막은 나뭇가지들 너머로 잔디 위의 어린 무리를 골똘히 바라보며—그 무리 가운데서 모두의 귀염둥이이자 놀잇감인 자그마한 아이의 머리에 씌울 꽃 화환을 엮고 있을 때, 바로 그 부인과 조카가 가까운 한적한 구석을 거닐며 그녀에 대해 이야기하는 것을 들었다.\n\n\"플로렌스도 저처럼 고아인가요, 고모?\" 아이가 말했다.\n\n\"아니란다, 얘야. 어머니는 안 계시지만 아버지는 살아 계셔.\"\n\n\"그럼 지금 가엾은 어머니를 위해 상복을 입은 건가요?\" 아이가 재빨리 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 하나밖에 없는 오빠를 위해서란다.\"\n\n\"다른 형제는 없나요?\"\n\n\"없어.\"\n\n\"언니나 여동생은요?\"\n\n\"없단다.\"\n\"정말, 정말 안됐어요!\" 어린 소녀가 말했다.\n\n이내 그들이 배 몇 척을 바라보려 멈춰 서서 그 사이 말이 없어졌을 때, 자기 이름을 듣고 자리에서 일어나 꽃을 챙겨 들고 그들을 마중하러 가서 자기가 그들의 말소리가 들리는 곳에 있음을 알리려던 플로렌스는, 더는 들을 것이 없으리라 여기고 다시 자리에 앉아 일감을 들었다. 그러나 다음 순간 대화가 다시 시작되었다.\n\n\"플로렌스는 여기 모두에게 사랑받아요, 그럴 만하기도 하고요, 정말이에요,\" 아이가 진지하게 말했다. \"그녀의 아버지는 어디 계세요?\"\n\n고모는 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 모르겠다고 답했다. 그 목소리의 어조가, 다시 자리에서 벌떡 일어났던 플로렌스를 붙들어, 일감을 황급히 가슴께로 끌어안고 그것이 땅에 흩어지지 않도록 두 손으로 받친 채 그 자리에 못 박혀 있게 했다.\n\n\"잉글랜드에 계시겠죠, 고모?\" 아이가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 게다. 그래, 정말로 계신단다.\"\n\n\"여기 와 보신 적은 있어요?\"\n\n\"없는 것 같구나. 없어.\"\n\n\"플로렌스를 보러 여기 오실 건가요?\"\n\n\"안 오실 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"다리가 불편하시거나, 눈이 안 보이시거나, 편찮으신가요, 고모?\" 아이가 물었다.\n\n그토록 의아한 듯 던져진 그 말을 들었을 때, 플로렌스가 가슴에 품고 있던 꽃들이 떨어지기 시작했다. 그녀는 꽃을 더 바짝 끌어안았고, 얼굴이 그 위로 수그러졌다.\n\n\"케이트,\" 다시 한순간의 침묵 뒤에 부인이 말했다, \"내가 들은 대로, 그리고 사실이라 믿는 대로, 플로렌스에 대한 모든 진실을 말해 주마. 다른 누구에게도 말하면 안 돼, 얘야, 여기서는 이 일이 거의 알려지지 않았을지 모르고, 네가 말을 옮기면 그녀에게 아픔을 줄 테니까.\"\n\n\"절대로 안 할게요!\" 아이가 외쳤다.\n\n\"네가 절대 그러지 않으리란 걸 안다,\" 부인이 응수했다. \"나는 너를 나 자신처럼 믿을 수 있어. 그렇다면 들어 봐, 케이트, 안타깝게도 플로렌스의 아버지는 그 애를 거의 마음에 두지 않고, 아주 드물게만 보며, 평생 한 번도 다정하게 대한 적이 없고, 지금은 그 애를 완전히 피하고 멀리한단다. 그 애는 아버지가 허락만 한다면 그를 진심으로 사랑할 텐데, 아버지는 그러질 않아—그것도 그 애 잘못이라곤 없는데 말이지. 그러니 그 애는 모든 따뜻한 마음을 가진 사람들에게서 큰 사랑과 연민을 받아 마땅하단다.\"\n\n플로렌스가 쥐고 있던 꽃이 더 많이 땅에 흩어져 떨어졌다. 남은 꽃들은 젖어 있었으나, 이슬 때문은 아니었다. 그리고 그녀의 얼굴이 그 가득 안긴 두 손 위로 떨어졌다.\n\n\"가엾은 플로렌스! 사랑스럽고 착한 플로렌스!\" 아이가 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 왜 이 이야기를 너에게 했는지 아니, 케이트?\" 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"제가 그 애에게 아주 다정하게 대하고, 그 애를 기쁘게 해 주려고 무척 신경 쓰게 하려고요. 그게 이유인가요, 고모?\"\n\n\"일부는 그렇단다,\" 부인이 말했다, \"하지만 전부는 아니야. 우리가 보기엔 그 애가 그토록 명랑하고, 누구에게나 상냥한 미소를 보내고, 우리 모두에게 기꺼이 맞춰 주고, 여기서 모든 놀이에 한몫을 거들지만 말이다. 그래도 그 애가 온전히 행복할 수는 없겠지, 그럴 수 있을 것 같니, 케이트?\"\n\n\"아닐 것 같아요,\" 어린 소녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그러니 이해할 수 있겠지,\" 부인이 이어 말했다, \"자기를 아끼고 자랑스러워하는 부모를 둔 아이들을—지금 여기 많은 아이들처럼—그 애가 지켜보면, 왜 남몰래 슬퍼지는지 말이다.\"\n\n\"네, 고모,\" 아이가 말했다, \"그건 아주 잘 이해해요. 가엾은 플로렌스!\"\n\n더 많은 꽃이 땅에 흩어졌고, 그녀가 아직 가슴에 품고 있던 꽃들은 마치 겨울바람이 그것들을 흔드는 양 떨렸다.\n\n\"내 케이트,\" 진지하지만 매우 차분하고 다정하여, 플로렌스가 처음 들은 순간부터 깊은 인상을 받았던 그 목소리로 부인이 말했다, \"여기 있는 모든 젊은이 가운데서 너야말로 그 애에게 자연스럽고 해롭지 않은 친구란다. 너에게는 더 행복한 아이들이 가진 그 천진한 수단이 없어—\"\n\n\"고모보다 더 행복한 사람은 없어요!\" 아이가 부인에게 매달리는 듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"—다른 아이들이 가진, 그 애에게 자기 불행을 떠올리게 하는 그런 수단 말이다, 사랑하는 케이트. 그래서 나는 네가 그 애의 작은 친구가 되려 할 때, 바로 그 점 때문에 더욱더 애써 주기를, 그리고 네가 겪은 사별이—그 무게를 알기 전에, 다행히도!—가엾은 플로렌스에 대한 너의 권리와 끈이 되어 준다는 것을 느껴 주기를 바란단다.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 부모의 사랑이 없는 게 아니고, 한 번도 없었던 적이 없어요, 고모,\" 아이가 말했다, \"고모가 계시니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다 하더라도, 얘야,\" 부인이 응수했다, \"너의 불행은 플로렌스의 것보다 가볍단다. 살아 있는 부모의 사랑에서 내쳐진 아이만큼 넓은 세상에서 버림받을 수 있는 고아는 없으니까.\"\n\n꽃들은 먼지처럼 땅에 흩어졌다. 비어 버린 두 손은 얼굴을 가렸다. 그리고 고아가 된 플로렌스는 땅바닥에 웅크리며 오래도록 비통하게 울었다.\n\n그러나 마음이 진실되고 자기의 선한 목적에 결연했던 플로렌스는, 폴에게 생명을 준 그날 죽어 가던 어머니가 자기를 붙들었듯이, 그 목적을 굳게 붙들었다. 아버지는 그녀가 자기를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알지 못했다. 그 깨달음이 찾아오기까지 아무리 오랜 시간이 걸리고 그 사이가 아무리 더디다 해도, 그녀는 언젠가는 그 앎을 아버지의 마음에 가닿게 하려 애써야 했다. 그동안 그녀는 어떤 우연한 상황에 깨어난 생각 없는 말 한마디나 표정이나 감정의 분출로도 아버지를 원망하거나, 그를 향한 이런 속삭임에 빌미를 주지 않도록 조심해야 했다.\n\n자기가 강하게 끌렸고 떠올릴 까닭이 그토록 많았던 그 고아 아이에게 응답할 때조차, 플로렌스는 아버지를 마음에 두었다. 만일 자기가 그 아이를 다른 아이들 가운데서 너무 두드러지게 골라낸다면(플로렌스는 생각했다), 아버지가 잔인하고 인정머리 없는 사람이라는 믿음을—적어도 한 사람의 마음속에서, 어쩌면 더 여럿의 마음속에서—굳혀 버릴 것이었다. 자기 자신의 기쁨은 이에 대한 보상이 되지 못했다. 그녀가 엿들은 것은 자기를 위로할 이유가 아니라 아버지를 구할 이유였다. 그래서 플로렌스는, 자기 마음의 연구를 좇아 그렇게 했다.\n\n그녀는 늘 그렇게 했다. 책을 소리 내어 읽을 때 이야기 속에 인정 없는 아버지를 가리키는 대목이 있으면, 그녀는 그들이 그것을 아버지에게 빗댈까 봐 마음 아파했다. 자기 자신을 위해서가 아니었다. 그들 가운데서 공연되는 막간극이나, 보여 주는 그림이나, 벌이는 놀이의 사소한 것에도 마찬가지였다. 아버지를 향해 그런 애틋함을 느낄 일이 너무도 많아서, 그녀의 마음은 차라리 옛집으로 돌아가 그 칙칙한 벽의 그늘 아래서 방해받지 않고 다시 사는 편이 정말 더 나으리라고 자주 흔들렸다. 여인으로 피어나는 봄날을 맞은, 그 자그마한 잔치들의 다소곳한 어린 여왕인 사랑스러운 플로렌스를 본 사람들 가운데, 그 가슴속에 신성한 근심이 얼마나 무겁게 얹혀 있는지 짐작한 이가 얼마나 적었던가! 아버지의 얼어붙은 분위기 속에서 뻣뻣해지던 사람들 가운데, 그의 머리 위에 이글거리는 숯불이 얼마나 수북이 쌓여 있는지 의심한 이가 얼마나 적었던가!\n\n플로렌스는 인내하며 자기 연구를 계속했고, 집에 모인 젊은 무리 가운데서 자기가 찾던 그 이름 없는 우아함의 비밀을 끝내 터득하지 못하자, 이른 아침 종종 홀로 나가 가난한 이들의 아이들 사이를 거닐었다. 그러나 그 아이들 역시 모두 자기가 배우기에는 너무 앞서 나간 것을 알 뿐이었다. 그들은 오래전에 자기 가정의 자리를 얻었고, 그녀처럼 문 앞에 빗장이 가로질린 채 바깥에 서 있지 않았다.\n\n그녀가 여러 번 매우 이른 시각에 일하는 것을 본 한 남자가 있었는데, 그의 곁에는 종종 자기 또래쯤 되는 소녀가 앉아 있었다. 그는 매우 가난한 사람으로 일정한 일자리가 없어 보였는데, 어떤 때는 썰물 때 강둑을 돌아다니며 진흙 속에서 자투리며 부스러기를 찾고, 또 어떤 때는 자기 오두막 앞의 볼품없는 자그마한 텃밭에서 일하고, 또 어떤 때는 자기 소유의 형편없는 낡은 배를 손보거나, 기회가 닿으면 이웃을 위해 그런 종류의 일거리를 해 주었다. 그 남자의 일이 무엇이든 소녀는 한 번도 무언가를 하는 법이 없었고, 그와 함께 있을 때면 멍하니 풀죽은 상태로 빈둥대며 앉아 있었다.\n\n플로렌스는 이 남자에게 말을 걸고 싶었던 적이 여러 번이었으나, 그가 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 기색이 없었기에 한 번도 그럴 용기를 내지 못했다. 그러나 어느 날 아침, 가지치기한 버드나무들 사이로 난 샛길에서—그 길은 그의 거처와 물 사이에 놓인, 자갈 깔린 비탈진 작은 땅뙈기에서 끝났다—갑자기 그와 마주쳤을 때, 마침 그는 바로 옆에 뒤집힌 채 놓인 낡은 배의 틈을 메우려고 피운 불 위로 몸을 굽히고 있었는데, 그녀의 발소리에 고개를 들고 안녕하시냐고 인사를 건넸다.\n\n\"안녕하세요,\" 플로렌스가 더 가까이 다가서며 말했다, \"일찍 일하고 계시네요.\"\n\n\"할 일만 있다면야 더 자주 일찍 일하고 싶지요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"일을 구하기가 그렇게 힘든가요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"제게는 그렇습니다,\" 남자가 답했다.\n\n플로렌스는 소녀가 무릎에 팔꿈치를 얹고 두 손에 턱을 괸 채 몸을 잔뜩 웅크리고 앉아 있는 쪽을 흘끗 보고는 말했다.\n\n\"저 아이가 따님인가요?\"\n\n그는 재빨리 고개를 들어, 환해진 얼굴로 소녀 쪽을 바라보며 고개를 끄덕이고 \"그렇습니다\"라고 말했다. 플로렌스도 소녀 쪽을 바라보며 다정하게 인사를 건넸으나, 소녀는 무뚝뚝하고 시무룩하게 뭔가를 중얼거릴 뿐이었다.\n\n\"저 아이도 일자리가 필요한가요?\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n남자가 고개를 저었다. \"아닙니다, 아가씨,\" 그가 말했다. \"둘 몫을 제가 벌지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 두 분뿐인가요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"저희 둘뿐입니다,\" 남자가 말했다. \"저 애 어미는 죽은 지 십 년이 됐지요. 마사야!\" (그는 다시 고개를 들고 소녀에게 휘파람을 불었다) \"이 예쁜 아가씨께 한마디 안 하련?\"\n\n소녀는 웅크린 어깨로 짜증스러운 몸짓을 하며 고개를 딴 데로 돌렸다. 못생기고, 몸이 비뚤어졌고, 까탈스럽고, 성미가 고약하고, 누더기를 걸쳤고, 더러웠다—그러나 사랑받고 있었다! 오, 그렇고말고! 플로렌스는 소녀를 향한 그 아버지의 눈길을 보았고, 그 눈길이 누구의 눈길과는 닮은 데가 없는지를 알았다.\n\n\"안됐지만 우리 가엾은 애가 오늘 아침에 더 안 좋은가 봅니다!\" 남자가 일손을 멈추고, 거친 만큼 더욱 애틋한 연민으로 못난 자식을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 아프단 말씀이군요!\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n남자는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다. \"우리 마사가 닷새 동안 성한 몸으로 지낸 적이 있는지 모르겠습니다,\" 그가 여전히 소녀를 바라보며 답했다, \"그 긴 세월 햇수만큼이나 되는 동안에요.\"\n\n\"그렇소! 그것도 더 됐지, 존,\" 배를 거들어 주려고 내려온 이웃이 말했다.\n\n\"그것보다 더 됐다고, 그렇게 말하는가?\" 다른 이가 찌그러진 모자를 뒤로 밀고 손으로 이마를 쓸어내리며 외쳤다. \"그럴 만도 하지. 참 길고 긴 세월 같으니.\"\n\n\"그리고 세월이 갈수록,\" 이웃이 말을 이었다, \"자네는 저 애를 점점 더 떠받들고 받아 줘서, 끝내는 저 애가 제 자신에게도, 모두에게도 짐이 되도록 만들어 버렸어, 존.\"\n\n\"내겐 아니야,\" 그녀의 아버지가 다시 일에 몰두하며 말했다. \"내겐 아니야.\"\n\n플로렌스는 그가 얼마나 진실되게 말하는지 느낄 수 있었다—누가 그녀보다 더 잘 알겠는가? 그녀는 그에게 조금 더 가까이 다가가, 그 거친 손을 만지고, 그가 다른 누구의 눈과도 그토록 다른 눈으로 바라보는 그 가엾은 존재에게 베푸는 친절에 감사하고 싶었다.\n\n\"내가 아니면 누가 우리 가엾은 애를 받아 주겠소—그걸 받아 준다고 할 수 있다면 말이오만?\" 아버지가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래,\" 이웃이 외쳤다. \"그것도 정도껏 해야지, 존. 그런데 자네는! 자네는 저 애한테 주려고 제 몸을 깎아 먹는다고. 저 애 때문에 손발을 다 묶어 놓고 사는 거야. 저 애 때문에 자네 인생을 비참하게 만든다고. 그런데 저 애가 그걸 알기나 하나! 자네 정말로 저 애가 그걸 안다고 믿는 거야?\"\n\n아버지는 다시 고개를 들고 소녀에게 휘파람을 불었다. 마사는 그 답으로 웅크린 어깨로 똑같이 짜증스러운 몸짓을 했다. 그러자 그는 기쁘고 행복해했다.\n\n\"바로 그것 때문이랍니다, 아가씨,\" 이웃이, 입 밖으로 드러낸 것보다 더 깊은 은밀한 연민이 담긴 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그것 하나 얻겠다고, 저 사람은 저 애를 한시도 눈에서 떼지 않는답니다!\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 그날이 올 테니까, 그것도 오래전부터 다가오고 있으니까,\" 다른 이가 일감 위로 몸을 낮게 숙이며 말했다, \"내 그 불행한 자식한테서 그 절반만이라도—손가락 하나 떨리는 것이나, 머리카락 한 올 흔들리는 것만이라도—얻으려면 죽은 사람을 되살려 내야 하는 그날이 말이오.\"\n\n플로렌스는 가만히 낡은 배 위, 그의 손 가까이에 돈을 얼마간 놓아두고 그를 떠났다.\n\n이제 플로렌스는 생각하기 시작했다. 만일 자기가 병이 들면, 사랑하는 오빠처럼 시들어 간다면, 아버지는 그제야 자기가 그를 사랑했다는 것을 알까. 그제야 자기를 사랑스럽게 여기게 될까. 자기가 쇠약해지고 눈이 침침해졌을 때 그가 머리맡으로 찾아와 자기를 품에 안고 지난 모든 것을 지워 줄까. 자기가 어린 마음을 그에게 열어 보일 수 없었던 것을, 그렇게 달라진 처지에서 그가 용서해 주어, 그날 밤 자기가 어떤 심정으로 그의 방을 나섰는지, 용기가 있었다면 무슨 말을 하려 했는지, 그리고 그 뒤에 어린 시절 끝내 알지 못했던 그 길을 배우려고 어떻게 애써 왔는지를 쉽게 이야기할 수 있게 해 줄까.\n\n그래, 그녀는 생각했다. 자기가 죽어 간다면 아버지도 마음을 누그러뜨리리라고. 자기가 그들의 사랑하던 소년에 대한 추억으로 둘러쳐진 그 침대 위에 평온히, 떠나기를 마다하지 않는 채 누워 있다면, 아버지는 가슴 깊이 감동받아 이렇게 말하리라고. \"사랑하는 플로렌스, 나를 위해 살아 다오. 그러면 우리는 그렇게 했어야 했던 것처럼 서로 사랑하고, 이 여러 해 동안 그럴 수 있었던 것처럼 행복하게 지내자꾸나!\" 그녀는 생각했다. 만일 그에게서 그런 말을 듣고 두 팔로 그를 끌어안을 수 있다면, 자기는 미소로 답할 수 있으리라고. \"이제 이것 말고는 모든 게 너무 늦었어요. 저는 이보다 더 행복할 수 없어요, 사랑하는 아버지!\" 그러고는 입술에 축복을 담은 채 그를 떠날 수 있으리라고.\n\n그녀가 벽에서 떠올리던 그 황금빛 물은, 그런 상념의 빛 속에서 플로렌스에게, 안식을 향해, 그리고 먼저 떠나간 사랑하는 이들이 손에 손을 맞잡고 기다리는 곳을 향해 흘러가는 물줄기로만 보였다. 그리고 종종 그녀가 자기 발치에서 잔물결치는 더 어두운 강을 바라볼 때면, 오빠가 자기를 실어 가고 있다고 그토록 자주 말하던 그 강을, 경외에 찬 경이로움으로, 그러나 두려움 없이 떠올렸다.\n\n그 아버지와 병든 딸이 아직 플로렌스의 마음에 생생하게 남아 있을 때, 사실 그 일이 있은 지 일주일도 채 안 되었을 때, 바넷 경과 그의 부인이 어느 오후 시골길로 산책을 나가며 그녀에게 동행을 청했다. 플로렌스가 선뜻 응하자, 스케틀스 부인은 으레 그렇듯 어린 바넷도 불러냈다. 스케틀스 부인에게는 맏아들이 플로렌스를 팔에 끼고 가는 것을 보는 일만큼 기쁜 일이 없었기 때문이다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 바넷은 이 일에 대해 정반대의 감정을 품은 듯했고, 그런 자리에서 자주 들릴 만큼, 그러나 막연하게 \"계집애들 한 무리\" 운운하며 속내를 내비쳤다. 그러나 그녀의 다정한 성미를 흐트러뜨리기란 쉽지 않은 일이라, 플로렌스는 대개 몇 분 만에 그 젊은 신사를 제 운명과 화해시켰고, 둘은 사이좋게 거닐었다. 스케틀스 부인과 바넷 경은 더없이 흡족하고 크게 만족스러운 상태로 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n바로 그 오후의 진행 순서가 그러했고, 플로렌스가 자기 운명에 대한 스케틀스 주니어의 현재의 반발을 거의 잠재우는 데 성공했을 무렵, 말을 탄 한 신사가 곁을 지나가다가 지나치면서 그들을 진지하게 바라보더니, 고삐를 당겨 말을 빙 돌려 모자를 손에 든 채 다시 말을 몰고 돌아왔다.\n\n그 신사는 특히 플로렌스를 바라보았다. 그가 말을 몰고 돌아와 작은 일행이 멈춰 섰을 때, 그는 바넷 경과 그의 부인에게 인사하기 전에 먼저 그녀에게 절을 했다. 플로렌스는 그를 본 기억이 전혀 없었으나, 그가 가까이 다가오자 자기도 모르게 흠칫 놀라며 뒤로 물러섰다.\n\n\"제 말은 아주 얌전합니다, 분명히 말씀드리지요,\" 신사가 말했다.\n\n그것 때문이 아니라, 신사 그 자신의 무언가가—플로렌스로서는 무엇이라고 말할 수 없었다—마치 쏘이기라도 한 듯 그녀를 움찔하게 했다.\n\n\"돔비 아가씨를 뵙는 영광을 누리는 것 같습니다만?\" 신사가 더없이 설득력 있는 미소를 띠며 말했다. 플로렌스가 고개를 숙이자 그가 덧붙였다, \"제 이름은 카커입니다. 돔비 아가씨께서 이름 말고 저를 기억해 주시기를 바라기는 어렵겠지요. 카커입니다.\"\n\n날이 더운데도 이상하게 몸이 떨리는 것을 느낀 플로렌스는 그를 자기 주인 내외에게 소개했고, 그는 매우 정중하게 맞아들여졌다.\n\n\"용서해 주십시오,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"천 번이라도요! 다만 제가 내일 아침 리밍턴에 계신 돔비 씨께 내려가는 길인데, 만일 돔비 아가씨께서 제게 무슨 전갈이라도 맡겨 주신다면, 제가 얼마나 기쁠지 굳이 말씀드릴 필요가 있을까요?\"\n\n바넷 경은 플로렌스가 아버지에게 편지를 쓰고 싶어 하리라는 것을 즉시 알아채고는, 돌아가자고 제안하며 카커 씨에게 승마복 차림 그대로 집에 와서 만찬을 들기를 청했다. 카커 씨는 안타깝게도 만찬 약속이 있었으나, 돔비 아가씨가 글을 쓰고자 하신다면 그들을 따라 돌아가 아가씨가 원하시는 만큼 오래도록 충직한 시종이 되어 시중드는 것보다 더 기쁜 일은 없겠다고 했다. 그가 더없이 활짝 웃으며 이 말을 하고는 자기 말의 목을 두드리려 그녀에게 바짝 몸을 굽혔을 때, 플로렌스는 그의 눈과 마주치며, 그가 \"배에 대한 소식은 없습니다!\"라고 말하는 것을 듣기보다는 차라리 보았다.\n\n당황하고 겁에 질려 그에게서 움츠러든 채, 그가 그런 말을 했는지조차 확실치 않은 가운데—그가 그 말을 입 밖에 내는 대신 미소를 통해 무슨 기이한 방식으로 그녀에게 내보인 듯했기에—플로렌스는 그에게 고맙지만 편지는 쓰지 않겠다고, 할 말이 없다고 가냘프게 말했다.\n\n\"보낼 것이 아무것도 없으십니까, 돔비 아가씨?\" 그 이빨의 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"아무것도요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"다만 제—다만 제 깊은 사랑을—괜찮으시다면요.\"\n\n당황한 와중에도 플로렌스는 애원하는 듯한, 그리고 의미심장한 눈빛으로 그의 얼굴을 향해 눈을 들어 올렸는데, 그것은 만일 그가 안다면—그는 분명히 알고 있었다—자기와 아버지 사이의 어떤 전갈이든 흔치 않은 부탁이지만, 그중에서도 특히 그 전갈만큼은 자기를 봐주기를 분명히 간청하는 것이었다. 카커 씨는 미소 지으며 깊이 절했고, 바넷 경으로부터 자기와 스케틀스 부인의 안부를 전해 달라는 부탁을 받고는 작별을 고하고 말을 몰아 떠났다. 그는 그 훌륭한 내외에게 호의적인 인상을 남겼다. 그가 떠날 때 플로렌스는 어찌나 심하게 몸서리를 쳤던지, 바넷 경은 흔한 미신을 따라 누군가가 그녀의 무덤 위를 지나가는 모양이라고 여겼다. 카커 씨는 모퉁이를 돌면서 그 즉시 뒤를 돌아보고 절을 하고는, 마치 그 일을 하러 곧장 교회 묘지로 말을 몰아가기라도 하듯 사라졌다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXV. Strange News of Uncle Sol\n\n커틀 선장은 게으름뱅이는 아니었지만, 솔 길스가 가게 창문 너머로 응접실에서 글을 쓰고 있고, 카운터 위에는 미드십맨이 놓여 있으며, 로브 더 그라인더가 그 아래에서 잠자리를 펴고 있는 모습을 본 그 이튿날 아침에는, 그가 팔꿈치를 짚고 몸을 일으켜 자기 작은 방을 둘러보았을 때 시계가 여섯 시를 칠 만큼 일찍 일어났을 뿐이었다. 만약 선장이 잠에서 깰 때마다 그날 아침처럼 눈을 크게 떴다면, 그의 눈은 혹독한 임무를 수행해 온 셈이었을 것이다. 또 평소에도 그날의 절반만큼이라도 세게 눈을 비볐다면, 그 경계심에 대한 보상은 거칠기 그지없었을 것이다. 그러나 이번 경우는 예사로운 것이 아니었으니, 로브 더 그라인더가 커틀 선장의 방문 앞에 서 있은 적은 분명 한 번도 없었는데, 그가 그때 거기 서서 선장을 향해 헐떡이고 있었고, 그 몸에서는 막 잠자리에서 나온 듯 발갛게 상기되고 헝클어진 기색이 풍겨 그의 혈색과 표정을 한층 도드라지게 했던 것이다.\n\n\"이런!\" 선장이 고함쳤다. \"무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n로브가 미처 한마디 대답을 더듬거리기도 전에, 커틀 선장은 와르르 몸을 굴려 침대에서 나와, 손으로 소년의 입을 막았다.\n\n\"진정해라, 얘야,\" 선장이 말했다, \"아직 나한테 한마디도 하지 마라!\"\n\n선장은 몹시 당황한 얼굴로 손님을 바라보며, 이렇게 명령을 내려 둔 뒤 그를 살며시 옆방으로 떠밀어 넣고는, 잠시 사라졌다가 곧바로 푸른 양복 차림으로 돌아왔다. 아직 명령이 해제되지 않았음을 알리는 표시로 한 손을 들어 올린 채, 커틀 선장은 찬장으로 걸어가 자기 몫의 술을 한 잔 따르고, 똑같은 한 잔을 전령에게 건넸다. 그런 다음 선장은, 자기에게 전해질 소식에 뒤로 자빠질 가능성을 미리 막으려는 듯, 벽에 기대 한쪽 구석에 자리를 잡고 섰다. 그러고는 술을 들이켜고, 전령에게 시선을 못 박은 채, 더는 창백할 수 없을 만큼 창백한 얼굴로, 그에게 \"이제 출항하라\"고 청했다.\n\n\"제 말은, 말씀드리라는 건가요, 선장님?\" 로브가 물었다. 이런 사전 절차에 그는 단단히 위압당해 있었다.\n\n\"그렇다!\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"그게, 선장님,\" 로브가 말했다, \"별로 말씀드릴 게 없어요. 하지만 이걸 보세요!\"\n\n로브가 열쇠 꾸러미를 꺼내 보였다. 선장은 그것을 살펴보고는, 구석에 그대로 선 채, 전령을 살펴보았다.\n\n\"그리고 이것도 보세요!\" 로브가 말을 이었다.\n\n소년은 봉인된 꾸러미를 꺼냈고, 커틀 선장은 열쇠를 노려보았던 것처럼 그것을 노려보았다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 제가 잠에서 깼을 때요, 선장님,\" 로브가 말했다, \"다섯 시 십오 분쯤이었는데요, 베개 위에서 이것들을 발견했어요. 가게 문은 빗장이 풀리고 자물쇠도 열려 있었고, 길스 씨는 가 버리셨어요.\"\n\n\"가 버려!\" 선장이 고함쳤다.\n\n\"떠나 버리셨어요, 선장님,\" 로브가 대답했다.\n\n선장의 목소리가 어찌나 무시무시하고, 또 그가 어찌나 기세 좋게 구석에서 튀어나왔던지, 로브는 그 앞에서 다른 구석으로 물러나며, 깔려 넘어지지 않으려고 열쇠와 꾸러미를 앞으로 내밀었다.\n\n\"'커틀 선장께'라고요, 선장님,\" 로브가 외쳤다, \"열쇠에도 적혀 있고, 꾸러미에도 적혀 있어요. 제 명예를 걸고 맹세컨대, 커틀 선장님, 저는 이 일에 대해 더는 아무것도 몰라요. 거짓이면 죽어도 좋아요! 이제 막 일자리를 얻은 녀석한테 이게 무슨 꼴이람,\" 불운한 그라인더는 소맷부리를 얼굴에 비벼 대며 외쳤다. \"주인은 자리를 박차고 달아나 버리고, 그 책임은 녀석한테 뒤집어씌우다니!\"\n\n이 한탄은 커틀 선장의 시선, 아니 차라리 노려봄을 두고 한 것이었으니, 그 눈길에는 막연한 의심과 위협과 비난이 가득했던 것이다. 선장은 그가 내민 꾸러미를 손에서 받아들어 뜯고는 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n\n\"'친애하는 네드 커틀에게. 동봉한 것은 내 유언장일세!'\" 선장은 미심쩍은 표정으로 그것을 뒤집어 보았다. \"'그리고 유언 증서'라—유언 증서는 어디 있느냐?\" 선장은 말하며, 당장 그 불운한 그라인더를 탄핵했다. \"그걸 어쨌느냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"저는 본 적도 없어요,\" 로브가 흐느꼈다. \"죄 없는 녀석을 계속 의심하지 마세요, 선장님. 저는 유언 증서엔 손도 안 댔어요.\"\n\n커틀 선장은 누군가는 그것에 대해 책임을 져야 한다는 뜻으로 고개를 가로젓고는, 엄숙하게 계속 읽었다.\n\n\"'이것은 일 년 동안, 또는 자네에게도 분명 소중할 내 사랑하는 월터에 대한 결정적인 소식을 들을 때까지는 뜯지 말게, 네드.'\" 선장은 잠시 멈추고 다소 감정에 북받쳐 고개를 가로저었다. 그러더니 이 시련의 처지에서 자기 위엄을 다시 세우려는 듯, 그라인더를 더없이 준엄하게 노려보았다. \"'만약 자네가 나에 대해 다시는 소식을 듣지도, 나를 다시 보지도 못하게 되거든, 네드, 옛 친구가 마지막까지 자네를 다정히 기억하듯, 그를 다정히 기억해 주게. 그리고 적어도 내가 말한 그 기한이 다할 때까지는, 옛 자리에 월터를 위한 보금자리를 지켜 주게. 빚은 없네, 돔비 상사에서 빌린 돈은 다 갚았고, 내 열쇠는 모두 이것과 함께 보내네. 이 일은 조용히 묻어 두고, 나를 찾으려 들지 말게. 부질없는 일이네. 그럼 이만 줄이네, 친애하는 네드, 자네의 진실한 벗, 솔로몬 길스로부터.'\" 선장은 길게 숨을 들이쉬고는, 그 아래에 적힌 다음 글을 읽었다. \"'그 소년 로브는, 전에 말했듯이, 돔비 상사에서 추천받은 잘 보증된 아이일세. 만약 다른 모든 것이 경매에 넘어가게 되거든, 네드, 그 작은 미드십맨을 잘 돌봐 주게.'\"\n\n선장이 이 편지를 몇 번이고 뒤집어 가며 스무 번쯤 읽고 난 뒤, 자기 의자에 주저앉아, 마음속으로 이 문제에 대한 군법 회의를 연 그 태도를 후세에 조금이라도 전하려면, 자기들의 불운한 시대를 내던지고 후세에 길이 남기로 결심했으나 끝내 거기 이르지 못한 모든 위인들의 천재성을 한데 모아야 할 것이다. 처음에 선장은 너무도 어리둥절하고 비통하여 편지 그 자체 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수 없었다. 그리고 그의 생각이 거기 딸린 갖가지 정황을 흘끗거리기 시작했을 때조차, 그것들이 거기에 비추는 빛으로 보자면 차라리 앞서의 주제에나 골몰하는 편이 나았을 정도였다. 이런 심정으로, 커틀 선장은 법정에 그라인더 말고는 아무도 세워 둔 사람이 없었던지라, 대체로 그가 의심의 대상이라고 단정하는 데서 큰 위안을 얻었다. 그 단정을 선장이 어찌나 분명하게 얼굴에 드러냈던지, 로브가 항변했다.\n\n\"오, 그러지 마세요, 선장님!\" 그라인더가 외쳤다. \"어떻게 그러실 수가 있어요! 제가 그런 눈으로 보일 만한 무슨 짓을 했다고요?\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 커틀 선장이 말했다, \"다치기도 전에 비명부터 지르지 마라. 그리고 무슨 일을 하든, 네 입으로 죄를 자백하지 마라.\"\n\n\"저는 아무 짓도 안 했고 자백할 것도 없어요, 선장님!\" 로브가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 배를 막힘없이 두고,\" 선장이 의미심장하게 말했다, \"순탄히 항해해라.\"\n\n자기에게 지워진 책임과, 당사자들과의 관계에 걸맞게 이 수수께끼 같은 사건을 철저히 헤아려야 할 필요를 깊이 느끼며, 커틀 선장은 가게로 내려가 현장을 조사하고, 그라인더를 곁에 붙들어 두기로 결심했다. 지금으로서는 그 젊은이를 체포된 상태로 여겼던 터라, 선장은 그에게 수갑을 채우거나, 발목을 묶어 두거나, 다리에 추를 매다는 편이 상책이 아닐까 다소 망설였다. 그러나 그런 격식들이 적법한지 분명치 않았으므로, 선장은 그저 가는 내내 그의 어깨를 붙들고 있다가, 그가 무슨 반항이라도 하면 때려눕히기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그러나 그는 아무 반항도 하지 않았고, 따라서 더 엄중한 구속을 받지 않고 기구 제작자의 집에 이르렀다. 덧문이 아직 내려져 있지 않았으므로, 선장이 가장 먼저 챙긴 일은 가게를 여는 것이었다. 그리고 햇빛이 거리낌 없이 들어오게 되자, 그는 그 빛의 도움을 받아 한층 더 깊은 조사에 착수했다.\n\n선장이 가장 먼저 챙긴 일은, 자기 안에서 열리고 있는 그 엄숙한 법정의 재판장으로서 가게 안 의자에 자리를 잡는 것이었다. 그리고 로브에게 카운터 밑 잠자리에 누워, 잠에서 깼을 때 열쇠와 꾸러미를 발견한 정확한 위치, 문을 열어 보러 갔을 때 문이 어떻게 되어 있었는지, 어떻게 브리그 플레이스로 출발했는지를—다만 마지막 재연은 문지방 너머로 넘어가지 않도록 조심스레 막으면서—그렇게 처음부터 끝까지 그대로 보여 보라고 요구했다. 이 모든 것을 여러 차례 시킨 끝에, 선장은 고개를 가로저으며 사태가 좋지 않은 낌새라고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n다음으로, 선장은 시체라도 찾아내겠다는 어렴풋한 생각으로, 온 집 안을 샅샅이 뒤지기 시작했다. 불 켠 양초를 들고 지하실을 더듬고, 문 뒤로 갈고리를 찔러 넣고, 들보에 머리를 세게 부딪고, 온몸에 거미줄을 뒤집어썼다. 노인의 침실로 올라가 보니, 그가 간밤에 잠자리에 든 것이 아니라 그저 침대보 위에 누워만 있었음을 알 수 있었는데, 거기 아직 남아 있는 자국을 보면 분명했다.\n\n\"제 생각엔요, 선장님,\" 로브가 방 안을 둘러보며 말했다, \"길스 씨가 요 며칠 그렇게 자주 드나드신 건, 사람들 눈에 띄지 않으려고 자잘한 물건들을 조금씩 빼내신 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇구나!\" 선장이 알 수 없다는 듯 말했다. \"어째서 그러냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"왜냐면요,\" 로브가 둘러보며 대답했다, \"면도 도구가 안 보이거든요. 솔도요, 선장님. 셔츠도 한 장 없고요. 신발도 그렇고요.\"\n\n이 물건들이 하나하나 거론될 때마다, 커틀 선장은 그라인더의 해당 부위를 각별히 눈여겨보았는데, 그것이 최근에 사용된 듯이 보이거나, 지금 그가 그것을 지니고 있는 것으로 드러날까 봐서였다. 그러나 로브는 면도할 일도 없었고, 솔질도 안 한 상태였으며, 입고 있는 옷은 오래전부터 죽 걸치고 다닌 것이어서, 도무지 착각의 여지가 없었다.\n\n\"그래 네 생각엔,\" 선장이 말했다, \"네 입으로 죄를 자백하는 건 아니고 말이다—그분이 줄행랑을 친 시각이 언제쯤일 것 같으냐? 응?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 제 생각엔요, 선장님,\" 로브가 대답했다, \"제가 코를 골기 시작하고 얼마 안 돼서 가셨을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그게 몇 시였느냐?\" 선장이 정확한 시각을 무척 따져 물을 채비를 하며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 어떻게 알겠어요, 선장님!\" 로브가 대답했다. \"저는 처음엔 깊이 잠들었다가 새벽녘엔 얕게 잔다는 것만 알아요. 그러니까 만약 길스 씨가 동틀 무렵에 가게를 가로질러 가셨다면, 아무리 발끝으로 살금살금 걸으셨대도, 적어도 문 닫는 소리만은 분명히 들었을 거라고요.\"\n\n이 증언을 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 커틀 선장은 기구 제작자가 제 발로 사라진 게 틀림없다고 생각하기 시작했다. 이 논리적 결론에 이르는 데는 자기 앞으로 온 편지가 한몫했으니, 그것은 분명 노인의 필적이었던지라, 그가 제 뜻으로 떠나기로 정하고 그래서 떠났다는 해석을 별로 무리 없이 떠받칠 만했던 것이다. 선장이 다음으로 따져 보아야 할 것은 어디로, 그리고 왜 갔느냐였다. 그러나 첫 번째 난제를 풀 길은 도무지 보이지 않았으므로, 그는 두 번째에만 생각을 가두었다.\n\n노인의 묘한 태도와, 그가 자기에게 작별을 고하던 모습을 떠올리니—그때는 까닭 모르게 절절했으나 이제는 충분히 납득되는 것이었다—선장의 마음속에는 끔찍한 우려가 점점 굳어졌다. 노인이 월터에 대한 근심과 회한에 짓눌린 나머지, 자살로 내몰린 것이 아닐까 하는 우려였다. 노인이 입버릇처럼 스스로 일러 왔듯이 일상의 시달림을 견뎌 낼 만한 사람이 못 되었고, 그가 겪어 온 불확실함과 미루어진 희망에 분명 흔들려 있었던 만큼, 그것은 결코 억지로 짜낸 기우가 아니라 오히려 너무도 있을 법한 일로 여겨졌다.\n\n빚도 없고, 신변의 자유나 재산 압류를 두려워할 까닭도 없는데, 그런 광기 어린 상태가 아니고서야 무엇이 그를 홀로, 은밀히 떠나도록 다급하게 내몰았겠는가? 그가 옷가지를 좀 챙겨 갔다는 점으로 말하자면, 정말 그렇게 했다 하더라도—그들은 그것조차 확실치 않았다—사람들이 캐묻지 못하게 하려고, 자기에게 닥칠 운명에서 주의를 돌리려고, 아니면 바로 지금 이 모든 가능성을 곱씹고 있는 그 마음을 달래려고 그랬을 수도 있다고, 선장은 따졌다. 평이한 말로 추려, 좁은 테두리 안에 응축하자면, 그것이 곧 커틀 선장의 숙고가 다다른 최종 결론이자 골자였다. 그 숙고는 이 지경에 이르기까지 오랜 시간이 걸렸고, 어떤 좀 더 공적인 숙고들이 그러하듯, 몹시 산만하고 두서가 없었다.\n\n낙담하고 의기소침하기 이를 데 없는 가운데, 커틀 선장은 자기가 로브를 가두어 둔 체포 상태에서 풀어 주는 것이 옳다고 느꼈다. 그러고는 그를 풀어 주되, 자기가 여전히 행사하기로 마음먹은 일종의 명예로운 감독 아래 두기로 했다. 그리고 자기들이 자리를 비운 동안 가게를 지키도록 중개인 브로글리에게서 사람을 하나 고용한 뒤, 선장은 로브를 데리고 솔로몬 길스의 유해를 찾아 음울한 탐색에 나섰다.\n\n수도의 어느 파출소도, 시체 보관소도, 구빈원도 그 단단하고 번들거리는 모자의 방문을 면치 못했다. 부두를 따라, 강가의 선박들 사이로, 강 상류로, 강 하류로, 여기로, 저기로, 사방으로, 그 모자는 사람이 가장 빽빽한 곳에서, 마치 서사시 전투에 나오는 영웅의 투구처럼 번뜩이며 누볐다. 꼬박 한 주 동안 선장은 온갖 신문과 전단에서 발견된 사람과 실종된 사람에 관한 기사를 모조리 읽고, 하루 중 어느 시각이건 가리지 않고 탐색에 나섰으니, 바다에 빠진 가엾고 어린 선원 소년들에게서, 독약을 삼킨 검은 수염의 키 큰 외국인들에게서 솔로몬 길스를 찾아내려—\"그게 그가 아닌지 확실히 하려고,\" 라고 커틀 선장은 말했다—애쓴 것이었다. 한 번도 그가 아니었다는 것, 그리고 그 선량한 선장이 그 밖에 아무런 위안도 얻지 못했다는 것은 확실하다.\n\n커틀 선장은 마침내 이 시도들을 가망 없는 일로 단념하고, 다음에는 무엇을 해야 할지 곰곰이 따져 보기 시작했다. 가엾은 친구의 편지를 여러 번 새로 정독한 끝에, 그는 \"옛 자리에 월터를 위한 보금자리\"를 지키는 것이 자기에게 지워진 으뜸가는 의무라고 여겼다. 따라서 선장의 결심은, 자기가 직접 솔로몬 길스의 가게에 살림을 차리고, 기구 사업에 뛰어들어, 어떻게 되어 가는지 보겠다는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 이 조치는 맥스팅어 부인 집의 자기 셋방을 내놓는 일을 수반하는데, 그 단호한 여인이 자기가 셋방을 버리고 떠난다는 말을 결코 들으려 하지 않으리라는 것을 알았으므로, 선장은 도망을 친다는 필사적인 결단을 내렸다.\n\n\"자, 잘 들어라, 얘야,\" 이 굉장한 계획을 다 짜낸 뒤 선장이 로브에게 말했다, \"내일 나는 밤이 되기 전엔 이 정박지에서 보이지 않을 거다—어쩌면 자정이 지나서야 올지도 몰라. 하지만 너는 내가 노크하는 소리를 들을 때까지 망을 봐라. 그리고 그 소리가 들리는 순간, 잽싸게 움직여서 문을 열어라.\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다, 선장님,\" 로브가 말했다.\n\n\"너는 이 명부에 계속 등록되어 있을 거다,\" 선장이 거들먹거리며 말을 이었다, \"그리고 너랑 내가 마음을 모아 함께 노를 저어 나간다면, 네가 진급 못 할 거라곤 말 안 하마. 하지만 내일 밤 내가 노크하는 소리를 듣는 순간, 그게 몇 시건, 잽싸게 움직여서 날쌔게 문을 열어 보여라.\"\n\n\"꼭 그렇게 하겠습니다, 선장님,\" 로브가 대답했다.\n\n\"왜냐면 말이다,\" 선장이 이 임무를 그의 마음에 단단히 새겨 두려고 다시 돌아와 말을 이었다, \"내 입으로 장담은 못 하겠다만, 추격이 붙을 수도 있어. 그러니 네가 날쌔게 문을 열어 보여 주지 않으면, 내가 기다리는 사이에 붙잡힐지도 모른단 말이다.\"\n\n로브는 자기가 잽싸고 빈틈없이 깨어 있겠노라고 선장에게 다시금 다짐했다. 그리고 선장은 이런 신중한 채비를 마친 뒤, 마지막으로 맥스팅어 부인의 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n그것이 마지막이라는 자각과, 푸른 조끼 아래 감춰 둔 그 무시무시한 속셈이 선장에게 맥스팅어 부인에 대한 죽을 듯한 공포를 불러일으켜서, 하루 중 어느 때건 아래층에서 그 여인의 발소리가 들리기만 해도 선장은 부들부들 떨기 시작할 지경이었다. 게다가 공교롭게도 맥스팅어 부인은 더없이 좋은 기분이었으니—집에서 기르는 새끼 양처럼 온순하고 차분했다. 그래서 그녀가 올라와 저녁으로 무얼 좀 요리해 드릴까 묻자, 커틀 선장의 양심은 끔찍하게 욱신거리며 쑤셔 댔다.\n\n\"맛 좋은 콩팥 푸딩이라도 조금 어떠세요, 커틀 선장님,\" 하숙집 안주인이 말했다. \"아니면 양 염통이라도요. 제 수고는 마음 쓰지 마시고요.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 고맙소만, 부인,\" 선장이 대답했다.\n\n\"통닭구이라도 드세요,\" 맥스팅어 부인이 말했다, \"송아지 고기 소를 좀 넣고 달걀 소스를 곁들여서요. 자요, 커틀 선장님! 한번쯤 호사를 좀 누리시라고요!\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 고맙소만, 부인,\" 선장이 아주 풀 죽은 채로 대답했다.\n\n\"분명히 기분이 안 좋으셔서 기운을 좀 돋워야 할 것 같은데요,\" 맥스팅어 부인이 말했다. \"어쩌다 한 번쯤, 셰리 와인 한 병 드시지 그러세요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 부인,\" 선장이 응수했다, \"부인께서 한두 잔 드실 만큼 친절을 베풀어 주신다면야, 저도 한번 해 볼까 합니다. 부인, 제게 부탁 하나 들어주시겠습니까,\" 선장이 양심에 갈가리 찢긴 채 말했다, \"한 분기 치 방세를 미리 받아 주시겠는지요?\"\n\n\"아니, 어째서요, 커틀 선장님?\" 맥스팅어 부인이—선장이 느끼기엔 날카롭게—되받았다.\n\n선장은 사색이 되도록 겁먹었다. \"그래 주신다면요, 부인,\" 그가 고분고분 말했다, \"제겐 큰 신세지요. 제가 돈을 잘 간수를 못 해서요. 자꾸 새어 나가 버린단 말입니다. 들어주신다면 고맙겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 커틀 선장님,\" 영문도 모르는 맥스팅어 부인이 두 손을 비비며 말했다, \"선장님 좋으실 대로 하세요. 식구가 딸린 저로서야, 청하는 것도 그렇지만, 마다할 일도 아니지요.\"\n\n\"그리고 부인,\" 선장이 찬장 맨 위 선반에서 돈을 보관해 두는 양철통을 내리며 말했다, \"저 딸린 식구들한테 골고루 한 사람당 일 실링 육 펜스씩 나눠 주는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠습니까? 형편이 되신다면, 부인, 지금 그 애들한테 다 같이 앞으로 나오라고 말씀을 전해 주시면, 제가 그 애들을 보면 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n이 천진한 맥스팅어네 아이들이 떼 지어 나타나, 자기가 그토록 받을 자격 없는 그 믿음과 신뢰를 다해 그에게 매달리자, 그들은 선장의 가슴을 찌르는 숱한 비수가 되었다. 자기가 가장 아끼던 알렉산더 맥스팅어의 눈은 선장으로서는 차마 견딜 수 없었고, 제 어미를 빼다 박은 줄리아나 맥스팅어의 목소리는 그를 비겁자로 만들었다.\n\n그래도 커틀 선장은 그런대로 잘 겉모습을 꾸몄고, 한두 시간 동안 어린 맥스팅어네 아이들에게 몹시 시달리며 거칠게 다루어졌다. 그 아이들은 어린애다운 장난으로 번들거리는 모자에도 약간 손상을 입혔으니, 둥지 속에 들어앉듯 한 번에 둘씩 그 안에 들어앉아, 모자 꼭대기 안쪽을 신발로 둥둥 두드려 댄 것이다. 마침내 선장은 슬픔에 잠겨 아이들을 물러가게 했으니, 처형장으로 끌려가는 사람 같은 사무치는 회한과 비탄으로 이 천사 같은 아이들에게 작별을 고했다.\n\n밤의 적막 속에서, 선장은 무거운 짐들을 궤짝에 챙겨 넣고 자물쇠를 채웠는데, 십중팔구 영영 거기 놔두고 갈 작정이었으나, 언젠가 그것을 찾으러 와서 달라고 할 만큼 대담하고 무모한 사람이 나타날지 모른다는 가망 없는 요행에 기대 둔 것이었다. 가벼운 필수품들로는 선장이 보따리를 하나 꾸렸고, 도주에 대비해 자기 식기를 몸에 챙겨 둘렀다. 자정 무렵, 브리그 플레이스가 잠 속에 파묻히고 맥스팅어 부인이 아이들에게 둘러싸여 달콤한 망각 속에 잠들었을 때, 죄지은 선장은 어둠 속에서 발끝으로 살금살금 내려가, 문을 열고, 등 뒤로 살며시 닫고는, 줄행랑을 쳤다.\n\n맥스팅어 부인이 침대에서 벌떡 일어나, 옷차림 따위 아랑곳없이 뒤쫓아 와 그를 도로 끌고 가는 형상에 쫓기고, 또 자기가 저지른 엄청난 죄의 의식에도 쫓기며, 커틀 선장은 빠른 걸음을 늦추지 않고, 브리그 플레이스와 기구 제작자의 문 사이에서 발밑에 풀 한 포기 자랄 틈도 주지 않았다. 그가 노크하자 문이 열렸으니—로브가 망을 보고 있었던 것이다—그리고 등 뒤로 문에 빗장이 걸리고 자물쇠가 채워지자, 커틀 선장은 비교적 안전하다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"휴!\" 선장이 주위를 둘러보며 외쳤다. \"한숨 돌리겠구나!\"\n\n\"별일 없으신 거죠, 선장님?\" 입을 헤벌린 로브가 외쳤다.\n\n\"없다, 없어!\" 커틀 선장이 안색이 변했다가, 거리에서 지나가는 발소리에 귀를 기울인 끝에 말했다. \"하지만 잘 들어 둬라, 얘야. 만약 어떤 부인이든—네가 요전 날 본 그 두 사람을 빼고 말이다—와서 커틀 선장을 찾거든, 그런 이름을 가진 사람은 알지도 못하고, 여기서 들어 본 적도 없다고 꼭 그렇게 둘러대라. 그 명령을 잘 지키겠지, 알겠냐?\"\n\n\"명심하겠습니다, 선장님,\" 로브가 대답했다.\n\n\"네가 원한다면 이렇게 말해도 좋다,\" 선장이 머뭇거리며 말했다, \"그런 이름의 선장이 호주로, 다시는 돌아오지 않겠다고 다들 맹세한 한 배 가득한 사람들과 함께 이민을 떠났다고 신문에서 읽었다고 말이다.\"\n\n로브는 이 지시를 알아들었다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였다. 그리고 선장은 명령을 잘 따르면 그를 어엿한 사내로 만들어 주겠노라 약속한 뒤, 하품을 하는 그를 카운터 밑 잠자리로 물러가게 하고, 자기는 솔로몬 길스의 방으로 올라갔다.\n\n이튿날 보닛이 지나갈 때마다 선장이 얼마나 시달렸는지, 혹은 상상 속의 맥스팅어들을 피하려고 얼마나 자주 가게 밖으로 뛰쳐나가 다락에서 안전을 구했는지는 이루 다 말할 수가 없다. 그러나 이런 자기 보호 수단에 따르는 고단함을 피하고자, 선장은 가게와 응접실 사이의 유리문에 안쪽으로 커튼을 쳤다. 그리고 자기에게 보내진 꾸러미 속 열쇠로 그 문에 맞는 열쇠 하나를 골라 끼우고, 벽에 작은 정찰용 구멍을 뚫었다. 이 요새화의 이점은 명백하다. 보닛이 나타나면 선장은 즉각 자기 요새로 미끄러져 들어가, 안에서 문을 잠그고, 적을 몰래 살폈다. 거짓 경보임을 알게 되면 선장은 즉각 다시 미끄러져 나왔다. 그런데 거리의 보닛이 어찌나 많았던지, 그리고 그것이 나타날 때마다 경보가 어찌나 따라붙던지, 선장은 하루 종일 거의 쉴 새 없이 들락날락하는 형편이었다.\n\n그래도 커틀 선장은 이 고단한 임무 와중에 짬을 내어 재고를 점검했는데, 그와 관련해서 그는 (로브에게는 몹시 고된 일이었으나) 아무리 문질러 닦아도 지나치지 않고, 아무리 반짝반짝하게 만들어도 모자란다는 식의 전반적인 생각을 품고 있었다. 또한 그는 그럴듯해 보이는 물건 몇 가지에 운수 삼아 십 실링에서 오십 파운드에 이르는 값을 매겨 가격표를 붙이고는, 진열창에 내놓아 사람들을 크게 놀라게 했다.\n\n이런 개선을 이뤄 낸 뒤, 커틀 선장은 기구들에 둘러싸인 채 학구적인 기분이 들기 시작했다. 그래서 잠자리에 들기 전 작은 뒷방에서 파이프 담배를 피우며 밤이면 천창 너머로 별을 올려다보았는데, 마치 그것들에 대해 일종의 소유권이라도 세워 둔 듯한 태도였다. 또 시내의 상인으로서, 그는 시장과 주 장관들과 공기업들에 관심을 갖기 시작했다. 그리고 항해술의 어떤 원리로도 그 숫자들이 무엇을 뜻하는지 도무지 알아낼 수 없었고, 분수 따위는 없는 편이 차라리 나았겠지만, 날마다 채권 시세를 읽어야 한다는 의무감을 느꼈다. 플로렌스에게는, 미드십맨을 차지하자마자 곧바로, 선장이 솔 삼촌에 관한 이상한 소식을 들고 찾아갔다. 그러나 그녀는 집에 없었다. 그래서 선장은 로브 더 그라인더 말고는 아무 벗도 없이, 바뀐 삶의 처지에 주저앉았다. 그리고 큰 변화가 닥칠 때 사람들이 으레 그러하듯 시간 가는 줄을 잊고서, 월터와 솔로몬 길스를, 심지어 맥스팅어 부인까지도, 모두 흘러간 옛일 가운데 하나인 양 골똘히 떠올렸다.\n\n# 제21장\n\n“삼가 인사 올립니다, 선생.\" 소령이 말했다. \"이런, 선생, 내 친구 돔비의 친구라면 곧 내 친구이지요. 만나 뵙게 되어 반갑소이다!\"\n\n\"카커, 나는 한없이 고맙게 여기고 있네,\" 돔비 씨가 설명했다, \"배그스톡 소령께서 함께해 주시고 말벗이 되어 주신 데 대해서 말일세. 배그스톡 소령은 내게 큰 도움을 주셨어, 카커.\"\n\n지배인 카커 씨는 막 리밍턴에 도착하여 막 소령에게 소개를 받은 참이었는데, 모자를 손에 든 채 소령에게 자신의 두 줄로 늘어선 치아를 남김없이 드러내 보이며, 돔비 씨의 안색과 기분을 이토록 크게 호전시켜 주신 데 대하여 진심으로 감사를 드리는 무례를 허락해 주시기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 선생,\" 소령이 대답했다, \"내게 감사할 것은 없소이다. 이건 주고받는 일이니까. 우리 친구 돔비처럼 위대한 인물이라면, 선생,\" 소령은 목소리를 낮추었으나, 그 신사에게 들리지 않을 만큼 낮추지는 않으면서 말했다, \"친구들을 향상시키고 드높여 주지 않을 수 없는 법이오. 돔비는 사람을 강하게 하고 활기차게 해 줍니다, 선생, 그 사람의 도덕적 본성에 있어서 말이오.\"\n\n카커 씨는 그 표현에 덥석 달려들었다. 그 사람의 도덕적 본성에 있어서. 바로 그것이었다. 그가 막 제안하려던 바로 그 말이었다.\n\n\"하지만 내 친구 돔비가, 선생,\" 소령이 덧붙였다, \"선생에게 배그스톡 소령에 대해 이야기하거든, 그를 그리고 선생을 바로잡아 드리는 것을 허락받아야겠소이다. 그는 그저 평범한 조를 뜻하는 거요, 선생—조이 비—조시 배그스톡—조지프—거칠고 억센 늙은 제이, 선생. 마음껏 부려 주시오.\"\n\n소령을 향한 카커 씨의 지나치도록 다정한 호의와, 소령의 거칠고 억세고 소탈함에 대한 카커 씨의 감탄이, 카커 씨 머릿속에 박힌 이 하나하나에서 번뜩였다.\n\n\"자 그럼, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"선생과 돔비는 의논할 일이 산더미처럼 쌓여 있겠구려.\"\n\n\"천만의 말씀입니다, 소령,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"돔비,\" 소령이 단호하게 말했다, \"내가 더 잘 알지. 자네 같은 거물—상업계의 거인—을 방해해서는 안 되지. 자네의 시간은 귀중하니까. 우리는 만찬 시간에 만나기로 하세. 그동안 늙은 조지프는 모습을 감추겠네. 만찬 시간은 정확히 일곱 시요, 카커 씨.\"\n\n그 말과 함께, 얼굴이 잔뜩 부풀어 오른 소령은 물러갔다. 그러나 곧바로 문에 머리를 다시 들이밀고는 말했다.\n\n\"실례하네. 돔비, 그분들께 전할 말이라도 있는가?\"\n\n돔비 씨는 다소 당황한 채, 자기 사업상의 비밀을 정중히 지켜 주는 사람을 흘끗 보지 않을 수 없으면서, 소령에게 자신의 안부 인사를 부탁했다.\n\n\"맹세코, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"그보다는 좀 더 따뜻한 말로 해 주어야 하네. 안 그러면 늙은 조는 도무지 환영받지 못할 테니.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 안부 인사를, 소령께서 좋으시다면,\" 돔비 씨가 응수했다.\n\n\"이런, 선생,\" 소령이 익살스럽게 어깨와 커다란 양 볼을 흔들며 말했다, \"그보다는 좀 더 따뜻하게 해 주게.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 소령께서 좋으실 대로 하시지요,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 친구는 능청맞단 말이야, 선생, 능청맞아, 지독하게 능청맞아,\" 소령이 문 너머로 카커를 빤히 쳐다보며 말했다. \"배그스톡도 그렇지만.\" 그러나 낄낄거리던 도중에 멈추고는, 몸을 한껏 곧추세우면서, 소령은 자기 가슴을 치며 엄숙하게 외쳤다, \"돔비! 나는 자네의 심정이 부럽네. 신의 가호가 있기를!\" 그러고는 물러갔다.\n\n\"그 신사분이 자네에게 큰 의지가 되었던 모양이군요,\" 카커가 그를 이로 좇으며 말했다.\n\n\"정말 크게 그랬다네,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"여기 친구들이 있는 게 틀림없겠지요,\" 카커가 이었다. \"그분이 한 말로 미루어 보니, 자네가 여기서 사교 모임에 나가고 있다는 걸 알겠습니다. 아십니까,\" 끔찍하게 미소 지으며, \"자네가 사교 모임에 나간다니 저는 정말이지 기쁩니다!\"\n\n돔비 씨는 자기 부관이 보인 이 관심의 표시를 시곗줄을 빙빙 돌리고 고개를 살짝 끄덕이는 것으로 받아들였다.\n\n\"자네는 사교계를 위해 태어난 사람입니다,\" 카커가 말했다. \"제가 아는 모든 사람 가운데, 자네야말로 천성으로 보나 지위로 보나 사교계에 가장 잘 어울리는 분이지요. 아십니까, 저는 자네가 그토록 오랫동안 사교계를 멀찌감치 떼어 놓고 지냈다는 데 자주 놀라곤 했습니다!\"\n\n\"내게는 그럴 이유가 있었네, 카커. 나는 혼자였고, 그런 데 무관심했지. 하지만 자네야말로 사교적 자질이 대단하니, 그만큼 더 놀랄 만하지 않은가.\"\n\n\"오! 제가요!\" 상대가 선뜻 자기를 낮추며 응수했다. \"저 같은 사람의 경우는 전혀 다른 문제입니다. 저는 자네와 비교가 되지 않지요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 손을 크라바트에 가져가 그 안에 턱을 묻고는 기침을 하더니, 잠시 말없이 자기의 충실한 벗이자 종복을 바라보며 서 있었다.\n\n\"내가 기쁘게도 자네를, 카커,\" 마침내 돔비 씨가 목구멍에 다소 큰 것을 삼키는 시늉을 하며 말했다, \"내—그 소령의 친구분들께 소개해 줄 수 있을 걸세. 무척 유쾌한 분들이지.\"\n\n\"그분들 가운데 부인들도 계시겠지요?\" 매끄러운 지배인이 슬쩍 떠보았다.\n\n\"그분들은 다—그러니까, 두 분 다—부인들이라네,\" 돔비 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"두 분뿐입니까?\" 카커가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"두 분뿐일세. 나는 그분들의 거처로만 방문을 한정했고, 여기서 다른 사람과는 사귀지 않았네.\"\n\n\"자매분일까요?\" 카커가 물었다.\n\n\"모녀라네,\" 돔비 씨가 대답했다.\n\n돔비 씨가 눈을 내리깔고 다시 크라바트를 매만지는 동안, 지배인 카커 씨의 미소 띤 얼굴은 순식간에, 아무런 중간 단계도 없이, 더없이 골똘하고 찌푸린 얼굴로 바뀌어, 추한 비웃음을 머금은 채 그를 빈틈없이 살폈다. 돔비 씨가 눈을 들자, 그 얼굴은 그에 못지않게 빠르게 본래의 표정으로 되돌아가, 그에게 자기가 가진 잇몸을 하나도 빠짐없이 드러내 보였다.\n\n\"무척 친절하십니다,\" 카커가 말했다, \"그분들을 알게 되어 기쁘겠습니다. 따님 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 저는 돔비 양을 뵈었습니다.\"\n\n돔비 씨의 얼굴로 별안간 피가 솟구쳤다.\n\n\"제가 무례를 무릅쓰고 그분을 찾아갔습니다,\" 카커가 말했다, \"혹시 제게 자그마한 심부름이라도 맡기실 게 있는지 여쭤보려고요. 다만 저는 그분의—그분의 다정한 안부 말고는 아무것도 전할 만큼 운이 좋지는 못했습니다.\"\n\n그때 그것은 늑대의 얼굴이었으니, 그 눈이 돔비 씨의 눈과 마주치자 벌어진 입을 통해 뜨거운 혀까지 드러내 보이는 것이었다!\n\n\"무슨 사업상의 소식이라도 있는가?\" 후자의 신사가 한동안 침묵한 뒤에 물었다. 그동안 카커 씨는 몇 가지 비망록과 다른 서류들을 꺼내 놓았다.\n\n\"별로 없습니다,\" 카커가 대답했다. \"전반적으로 요즘 들어 평소만큼 운이 따르지는 않았습니다만, 그건 자네에게 그리 대수로운 일은 아니지요. 로이드사에서는 '아들이자 후계자호'를 침몰한 것으로 처리했습니다. 뭐, 그 배는 용골에서 돛대 꼭대기까지 보험에 들어 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"카커,\" 돔비 씨가 그의 곁에 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 말했다, \"그 게이라는 젊은이가 내게 좋은 인상을 준 적이 있다고는 말할 수 없네만—\"\n\n\"제게도 그렇습니다,\" 지배인이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"—하지만 나는,\" 돔비 씨가 그 끼어듦에 아랑곳하지 않고 말했다, \"그가 그 배에 결코 오르지 않았더라면 하고 바라네. 그가 결코 파견되지 않았더라면 하고 말일세.\"\n\n\"진작에 그렇게 말씀하시지 않은 게 안타깝지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 카커가 차분하게 응수했다. \"하지만 저는 그게 다 잘된 일이라 생각합니다. 정말이지, 다 잘된 일이라 생각해요. 제가 말씀드렸던가요, 돔비 양과 저 사이에 자그마한 신뢰 같은 게 생겼다는 걸요?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 돔비 씨가 엄하게 말했다.\n\n\"저는 의심치 않습니다,\" 카커 씨가 인상적으로 뜸을 들인 뒤에 응수했다, \"게이가 어디에 있든, 그가 있는 그곳이 이곳 본가보다는 훨씬 낫다는 것을요. 만일 제가 자네 입장이라면, 또 그럴 수 있다면, 저는 그 점에 만족할 것입니다. 저 스스로는 그 점에 아주 만족합니다. 돔비 양은 사람을 잘 믿고 어립니다—어쩌면 자네의 따님치고는 자존심이 그리 충분치 못하다고 할까요—흠을 잡자면 말이지요. 하긴 그것도 별것 아닙니다만, 분명히요. 이 잔액들을 저와 함께 대조해 보시겠습니까?\"\n\n돔비 씨는 자기 앞에 펼쳐진 서류 위로 몸을 굽히는 대신, 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 지배인의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다. 지배인은 눈꺼풀을 살짝 치켜뜬 채, 자기 숫자들을 들여다보며 상관이 여유가 날 때까지 기다리는 척했다. 그는 마치 대단히 섬세한 배려에서, 또 돔비 씨의 감정을 다치지 않게 하려는 의도에서 그러는 것처럼 그런 척하는 모습을 내보였다. 그리고 후자는, 그를 바라보면서, 그의 의도적인 배려를 알아차렸고, 그것만 아니었다면 이 비밀스러운 카커가 훨씬 더 많은 말을 했으리라는 것을, 그러나 돔비 씨 자신은 너무 자존심이 강해 차마 그것을 청하지 못하리라는 것을 느꼈다. 사업에서 그것은 흔히 그의 방식이었다. 차츰차츰 돔비 씨의 시선이 누그러졌고, 그의 주의는 앞에 놓인 서류 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다. 그러나 그 서류가 안겨 준 일에 몰두하면서도, 그는 자주 멈추고는 다시 카커 씨를 바라보았다. 그가 그럴 때마다, 카커 씨는 앞서처럼 자기의 섬세함을 한껏 드러내 보였고, 그것을 자기의 위대한 상관에게 점점 더 깊이 각인시켰다.\n\n이렇게 둘이 일에 몰두해 있는 동안, 그리고 지배인의 능숙한 가꿈 아래, 가엾은 플로렌스에 관한 노여운 생각들이 돔비 씨의 가슴속에서 들끓고 자라나, 평소 그곳을 지배하던 차가운 혐오의 자리를 빼앗아 가는 동안, 리밍턴의 노부인들에게 크게 흠모받는 배그스톡 소령은, 평소만큼의 가벼운 짐을 든 원주민을 거느린 채, 길의 그늘진 쪽을 따라 다리를 벌려 성큼성큼 걸으며, 스큐턴 부인에게 아침 방문을 하러 갔다. 소령이 클레오파트라의 정자에 다다른 것이 정오 무렵이었던지라, 그는 운 좋게도 자기의 공주가 평소의 소파에 앉아, 커피 한 잔을 앞에 두고 나른히 늘어져 있는 것을 보게 되었다. 방은 그녀의 한층 호사스러운 휴식을 위해 어찌나 어둡고 그늘지게 해 놓았던지, 그녀의 시중을 들고 있던 위더스는 유령 같은 시동처럼 어렴풋이 보였다.\n\n\"들어오는 이 못 참을 인간은 누구지?\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"도저히 견딜 수가 없어. 누구든 간에 저리 가!\"\n\n\"제이 비를 내쫓을 만큼 매정하지는 않으시겠지요, 마님!\" 소령이 길 한가운데서 멈추어, 지팡이를 어깨에 걸친 채 항변했다.\n\n\"오 당신이군요? 다시 생각해 보니, 들어와도 좋겠어요,\" 클레오파트라가 말했다.\n\n소령은 그에 따라 들어가서는, 소파로 다가가 그녀의 매혹적인 손을 입술에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"앉으세요,\" 클레오파트라가 나른히 부채를 흔들며 말했다, \"멀찌감치요. 너무 가까이 오지 말아요. 오늘 아침 나는 끔찍하게 기운이 없고 예민한데, 당신한테서는 햇볕 냄새가 나니까요. 완전히 열대 같다고요.\"\n\n\"이런, 마님,\" 소령이 말했다, \"조지프 배그스톡이 햇볕에 구워지고 부르터졌던 시절이 있었지요. 마님, 그가 서인도제도의 무더운 온실 같은 열기에 떠밀려 활짝 만개하던 시절도 있었답니다. 그래서 그는 '꽃'이라 불렸지요. 그 시절엔 아무도 배그스톡 이야기를 들어 보지 못했어요, 마님. 사람들은 '꽃' 이야기를 들었지요—우리 연대의 꽃 말이오. 그 꽃은 다소간 시들었을지도 모릅니다, 마님,\" 소령이 자기의 무자비한 여신이 가리킨 자리보다 훨씬 더 가까운 의자에 털썩 앉으며 말했다, \"하지만 여전히 질긴 식물이고, 상록수처럼 한결같지요.\"\n\n여기서 소령은 어두운 방을 은폐물 삼아 한쪽 눈을 감고, 할리퀸처럼 머리를 굴리면서, 자기만족이 어찌나 컸던지 어쩌면 이제껏 가 본 어느 때보다도 졸중의 경계에 더 가까이 다가갔는지도 모른다.\n\n\"그레인저 부인은 어디 있느냐?\" 클레오파트라가 시동에게 물었다.\n\n위더스는 그녀가 자기 방에 있는 것 같다고 했다.\n\n\"좋아,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다. \"저리 가서, 문을 닫아라. 손님 중이니까.\"\n\n위더스가 사라지자, 스큐턴 부인은 달리 움직이지는 않은 채 나른히 소령 쪽으로 고개를 돌리고는, 그의 친구가 어떻게 지내는지 물었다.\n\n\"돔비 말입니다, 마님,\" 소령이 목구멍에서 익살스럽게 그르렁거리며 응수했다, \"그 처지에 놓인 사람치고는 그만하면 됐지요. 그의 처지가 절박하답니다, 마님. 그는 가슴을 찔렸어요, 돔비가 말입니다! 찔렸지요!\" 소령이 외쳤다. \"몸뚱이를 총검에 꿰뚫린 거랍니다.\"\n\n클레오파트라는 소령에게 날카로운 눈길을 던졌는데, 그것은 그녀가 이내 짐짓 늘어뜨려 말한 말투와는 극명히 대조되었다.\n\n\"배그스톡 소령, 비록 내가 세상에 대해 아는 것이 거의 없고—내 그 경험을 정말이지 후회하지도 않아요, 세상이란 시들어 가는 인습들로 가득한 거짓된 곳이 아닌가 싶으니까요. 거기서는 자연이 거의 존중받지 못하고, 마음의 음악이며 영혼의 솟구침이며, 그토록 참되게 시적인 그 모든 것들이 좀처럼 들리지 않잖아요—그래도 나는 당신의 뜻을 오해할 수는 없어요. 당신의 말 속에는 에디스에 대한—나의 더없이 사랑스러운 아이에 대한 암시가 있군요,\" 스큐턴 부인이 집게손가락으로 자기 눈썹의 윤곽을 따라 그리며 말했다, \"그 말에 가장 여린 마음의 현이 몹시 떨리는군요.\"\n\n\"무뚝뚝함은, 마님,\" 소령이 응수했다, \"언제나 배그스톡 가문의 특성이었지요. 옳은 말씀이오. 조도 인정합니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 암시는,\" 클레오파트라가 이었다, \"우리의 슬프게 타락한 본성이 느낄 수 있는 가장—단정코 가장이라고는 못할지라도—가슴 저미고, 짜릿하며, 신성한 감정들 가운데 하나를 포함하는 것이라고 나는 생각해요.\"\n\n소령은 손을 입술에 갖다 대고는, 마치 문제의 그 감정이 무엇인지 확인이라도 하듯, 클레오파트라에게 입맞춤을 날려 보냈다.\n\n\"나는 내가 약하다는 걸 느껴요. 이런 문제에 있어서 엄마를—부모라고는 못 하더라도—지탱해 주어야 할 그 기력이 내게 부족하다는 걸 느낀답니다,\" 스큐턴 부인이 손수건의 레이스 가장자리로 입술을 다듬으며 말했다, \"하지만 나의 가장 소중한 에디스에게 그토록 지극히 중대한 화제에는, 아찔한 느낌 없이는 도무지 다가갈 수가 없군요. 그렇지만, 못된 양반, 당신이 그것을 대담하게 입에 올린 데다, 그것이 내게 큰 고통을 안겨 주었으니,\" 스큐턴 부인이 부채로 자기 왼쪽 옆구리를 짚었다, \"나는 내 의무에서 물러서지 않겠어요.\"\n\n소령은 어둑함을 은폐물 삼아, 부풀고 또 부풀어, 자줏빛 얼굴을 이리저리 굴리며, 가재 같은 눈을 끔벅거리다가, 끝내 쌕쌕거리는 발작에 빠져들었으니, 그의 어여쁜 벗이 말을 이어 가기 전에 일어나 방 안을 한두 바퀴 돌지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"돔비 씨는,\" 스큐턴 부인이 마침내 다시 입을 열며 말했다, \"이제 여러 주 전에, 친절하게도 여기 우리를 찾아 주시는 영광을 베푸셨지요. 나의 친애하는 소령, 당신과 함께 말이에요. 내 인정하겠어요—솔직히 털어놓겠어요—내 결점이 충동의 산물이라는 것을, 그리고 말하자면 마음을 바깥에 내놓고 다닌다는 것을 말이에요. 나는 내 결점을 아주 잘 알아요. 내 적이라 한들 그보다 더 잘 알지는 못할 거예요. 하지만 나는 뉘우치지 않아요. 차라리 매정한 세상에 얼어붙지 않는 편이 낫고, 이런 비난을 마땅히 감수하는 데 만족한답니다.\"\n\n스큐턴 부인은 가슴 장식을 매만지고, 거칠고 마른 목을 꼬집어 부드러운 표면을 만들고는, 크게 흡족해하며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"돔비 씨를 맞이하는 것은 내게(나의 가장 소중한 에디스에게도, 분명히) 한없는 기쁨이었어요. 나의 친애하는 소령, 당신의 친구로서, 우리는 자연히 그분에게 호의를 품게 되었지요. 그리고 나는 돔비 씨에게서 어떤 마음의 깊이를 보았다고 여겼는데, 그것이 더없이 신선하게 느껴졌답니다.\"\n\n\"이제 돔비에게 마음이라곤 지독하게 적게 남아 있지요, 마님,\" 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 양반!\" 스큐턴 부인이 나른히 그를 바라보며 외쳤다, \"제발 좀 잠자코 계세요.\"\n\n\"제이 비는 입을 다물지요, 마님,\" 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"돔비 씨는,\" 클레오파트라가 자기 볼의 장밋빛을 매만지며 이었다, \"그에 따라 다시 방문을 하셨지요. 그리고 어쩌면 우리 취향의 소박함과 꾸밈없음에 무언가 끌림을 느끼셨는지—자연에는 언제나 매력이 있으니까요. 정말이지 더없이 감미롭지요—매일 저녁 우리의 작은 모임의 일원이 되셨답니다. 내가 돔비 씨를 부추겨서—그분이\"—\n\n\"이곳에 진을 치도록 말이지요, 마님,\" 배그스톡 소령이 거들었다.\n\n\"천박한 양반!\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"비록 가증스러운 표현이긴 하지만, 당신이 내 뜻을 앞질러 말하는군요.\"\n\n여기서 스큐턴 부인은 곁에 놓인 작은 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 자기가 우아하고 어울린다고 여기는 자세로 손목을 늘어뜨린 채, 부채를 이리저리 흔들며, 말을 하는 동안 나른히 자기 손을 감탄하듯 바라보았다.\n\n\"내가 견뎌 온 고뇌란,\" 그녀가 새침하게 말했다, \"진실이 차츰 내게 밝아 옴에 따라, 도저히 자세히 늘어놓을 수 없을 만큼 너무도 끔찍했어요. 내 온 존재는 나의 더없이 사랑스러운 에디스에게 매여 있는데, 그 애가 나날이 변해 가는 것을 보는 것이—그토록 유쾌하던 그레인저가 죽은 뒤로 단정코 제 마음을 꼭꼭 닫아 둔 나의 아름다운 보배가 말이에요—세상에서 가장 가슴 아픈 일이랍니다.\"\n\n스큐턴 부인의 세상이란, 그 가장 가슴 아픈 사정이 그녀에게 미친 영향으로 미루어 판단한다면, 그리 견디기 힘든 세상은 아니었다. 하지만 그건 그렇다 치고.\n\n\"에디스는,\" 스큐턴 부인이 점잔 빼며 말했다, \"내 인생의 완벽한 진주인데, 나를 닮았다고들 해요. 우리가 비슷하긴 한 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"세상에는 그 누구도 마님을 닮았다고는 결코 인정하지 않을 한 사내가 있지요, 마님,\" 소령이 말했다. \"그 사내의 이름은 늙은 조 배그스톡이랍니다.\"\n\n클레오파트라는 부채로 그 아첨꾼의 골통을 후려칠 듯이 굴었으나, 누그러져 그를 향해 미소 짓고는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"내 매력적인 딸이 나에게서 어떤 장점들을 물려받았다면, 짓궂은 양반!\"—그 짓궂은 양반이란 소령이었다—\"그 애는 내 어리석은 천성도 물려받았어요. 그 애는 대단한 성격의 힘을 지녔는데—내 것은 어마어마하다고들 했지만, 나는 믿지 않아요—한번 마음이 흔들리면, 끝 간 데까지 예민하고 민감해진답니다. 그 애가 야위어 가는 것을 볼 때 내 심정이 어떻겠어요! 그건 나를 무너뜨려요.\"\n\n소령은 이중 턱을 앞으로 내밀고, 푸른 입술을 달래는 표정으로 오므리며, 더없이 깊은 동정을 짐짓 꾸며 보였다.\n\n\"우리 사이에 줄곧 있어 온 그 신뢰란,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"영혼의 자유로운 펼침과 감정의 솔직함이란—생각만 해도 가슴 뭉클하답니다. 우리는 엄마와 자식이라기보다는 자매에 가까웠으니까요.\"\n\n\"제이 비 자신의 심정이오,\" 소령이 말했다, \"제이 비가 오만 번도 더 표현한 그 심정이지요!\"\n\n\"끼어들지 말아요, 무례한 양반!\" 클레오파트라가 말했다. \"그렇다면 우리가 피하는 화제가 하나 있다는 걸 알게 됐을 때 내 심정이 어떻겠어요! 우리 사이에 무어라더라—틈이—갈라져 벌어졌다는 걸 알게 됐을 때 말이에요. 나의 그 꾸밈없는 에디스가 내게 변해 버렸다는 걸 알게 됐을 때! 그건 물론 가장 가슴 저미는 종류의 심정이지요.\"\n\n소령은 자기 의자를 떠나, 작은 탁자에 더 가까운 의자로 옮겨 앉았다.\n\n\"나는 나날이 이것을 봐요, 나의 친애하는 소령,\" 스큐턴 부인이 이었다. \"나날이 이것을 느낀답니다. 시시각각 나는 그토록 괴로운 결과를 불러온 그 지나친 믿음과 신뢰를 두고 스스로를 책망해요. 그리고 거의 시시각각, 나는 돔비 씨가 자기 마음을 밝혀, 내가 겪고 있는 이 고문을—정말이지 진을 빼는 이 고문을—덜어 주기를 바란답니다. 하지만 아무 일도 일어나지 않아요, 나의 친애하는 소령. 나는 회한의 노예예요—커피 잔 좀 조심해요. 당신은 어쩜 그렇게 서툴까—나의 사랑스러운 에디스는 딴사람이 되어 버렸고, 나는 정말이지 무엇을 어찌해야 할지, 어떤 마음씨 좋은 이와 의논할 수 있을지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n배그스톡 소령은, 스큐턴 부인이 여러 차례 잠깐씩 빠져들었다가 이제는 아예 가라앉아 버린 듯한 그 누그러지고 은밀한 어조에 어쩌면 고무되어, 작은 탁자 너머로 손을 뻗으며, 곁눈질하면서 말했다,\n\n\"조와 의논하시지요, 마님.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 이 약 올리는 괴물 같으니,\" 클레오파트라가 한 손을 소령에게 내주고, 다른 손에 든 부채로 그의 손가락 마디를 톡톡 치며 말했다, \"왜 내게 말을 안 해 주는 거예요? 내 뜻을 알면서. 왜 뭔가 도움 될 만한 걸 말해 주지 않는 거냐고요?\"\n\n소령은 웃으며, 그녀가 자기에게 내준 손에 입을 맞추고는, 또 한 번 엄청나게 웃었다.\n\n\"돔비 씨에게는 내가 믿었던 만큼 마음이 있을까요?\" 클레오파트라가 다정하게 나른한 목소리로 물었다. \"그분이 진심이라고 생각하세요, 나의 친애하는 소령? 그분에게 말을 건네 보라고 권하시겠어요, 아니면 그냥 내버려 두라고 하시겠어요? 자 이제, 친애하는 양반답게, 어떻게 하면 좋을지 말해 줘요.\"\n\n\"그를 에디스 그레인저와 혼인시켜 드릴까요, 마님?\" 소령이 쉰 목소리로 낄낄거렸다.\n\n\"알 수 없는 양반!\" 클레오파트라가 부채를 소령의 코에 들이대며 응수했다. \"어떻게 우리가 그를 혼인시킨다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"그를 에디스 그레인저와 혼인시켜 드릴까요, 마님, 라고 했지요?\" 소령이 다시 낄낄거렸다.\n\n스큐턴 부인은 말로는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 소령을 향해 어찌나 짓궂고 발랄하게 미소 지었던지, 그 용감한 장교는 자기가 도전을 받았다고 여기고는 그녀의 몹시 붉은 입술에 입맞춤을 찍으려 했을 터인데, 그녀가 자못 사랑스럽고도 젊은이다운 솜씨로 부채를 끼워 넣는 바람에 그러지 못했다. 그것은 정숙함에서였을 수도, 그 입술의 발그레함에 어떤 위험이 닥칠까 염려해서였을 수도 있다.\n\n\"돔비는, 마님,\" 소령이 말했다, \"대단한 신랑감이지요.\"\n\n\"오, 잇속만 챙기는 양반 같으니!\" 클레오파트라가 가볍게 비명을 지르며 외쳤다, \"나는 충격받았어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 돔비는, 마님,\" 소령이 머리를 앞으로 쑥 내밀고 눈을 부릅뜨며 이었다, \"진심입니다. 조지프가 그리 말하고, 배그스톡이 그걸 알며, 제이 비가 그를 그 마음에서 벗어나지 못하게 붙들어 둘 거예요. 돔비는 그냥 내버려 두세요, 마님. 돔비는 안전합니다, 마님. 지금까지 해 오신 대로 하시되, 그 이상은 하지 마시고, 끝맺음은 제이 비에게 맡겨 두시구려.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 생각하세요, 나의 친애하는 소령?\" 클레오파트라가 응수했다. 그녀는 나른한 태도에도 불구하고 무척 신중하게, 무척 면밀하게 그를 살펴보고 있었다.\n\n\"확신합니다, 마님,\" 소령이 응수했다. \"비할 데 없는 클레오파트라와 그녀의 안토니우스 배그스톡은, 에디스 돔비의 저택의 우아함과 부를 함께 누리면서, 이 일을 두고 의기양양하게 자주 이야기하게 될 거예요. 돔비의 오른팔이, 마님,\" 소령이 낄낄거리던 것을 별안간 멈추고 진지해지며 말했다, \"도착했답니다.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침에요?\" 클레오파트라가 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침입니다, 마님,\" 소령이 응수했다. \"그리고 그의 도착에 대한 돔비의 조바심은, 마님, 이렇게 봐야 합니다—제이 비의 말을 믿으세요. 조는 지독하게 능청맞으니까요\"—소령은 자기 코를 톡톡 두드리고 한쪽 눈을 꽉 찡그렸는데, 그것이 그의 타고난 미모를 돋보이게 하지는 못했다—\"그것은, 바람결에 무슨 일이 일어나고 있는지를, 돔비가 굳이 말하거나 의논하지 않고도 그 오른팔이 알아주었으면 하는 그의 바람 때문이지요. 왜냐하면 돔비는, 마님,\" 소령이 말했다, \"루시퍼만큼이나 오만하거든요.\"\n\n\"매력적인 자질이로군요,\" 스큐턴 부인이 혀짤배기소리로 말했다, \"더없이 소중한 에디스를 떠올리게 하네요.\"\n\n\"자 그럼, 마님,\" 소령이 말했다. \"저는 이미 운을 떼어 두었고, 그 오른팔은 그것을 알아듣지요. 그리고 오늘 하루가 가기 전에 더 많은 운을 떼어 둘 거예요. 돔비는 오늘 아침, 내일 워릭 성과 케닐워스로 마차 나들이를 가는 일을 계획했는데, 그에 앞서 우리와 아침 식사를 함께하자는 거랍니다. 제가 이 초대를 전하는 일을 맡았지요. 그 정도로 저희에게 영광을 베풀어 주시겠습니까, 마님?\" 소령이 가쁜 숨과 능청맞음으로 잔뜩 부풀어 오르며 말하고는, 명예로운 스큐턴 부인 앞으로 배그스톡 소령 편에 보낸다는 쪽지 하나를 꺼냈다. 거기에는 영원히 충실한 폴 돔비가, 그녀와 그녀의 상냥하고 교양 있는 따님이 제안된 그 나들이에 응해 주시기를 간청하고 있었으며, 추신에는 같은 영원히 충실한 폴 돔비가, 그레인저 부인의 기억에 자기를 되살려 주시기를 간곡히 청한다고 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"쉿!\" 클레오파트라가 별안간 말했다, \"에디스!\"\n\n이 다정한 어머니가 이렇게 외칠 때 그 무미하고 꾸민 태도를 다시 갖추었다고는 거의 말할 수 없으니, 그녀는 그것을 단 한 번도 벗어 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 또 그녀가 무덤 말고 다른 어떤 곳에서 그것을 벗으려 하거나 벗을 수 있으리라고도 보기 어려웠다. 그러나 잠시 그녀의 얼굴이나 목소리나 태도가 드러냈던 진지함의 그림자나, 가상하든 사악하든 어떤 목적의 희미한 고백을, 그녀는 황급히 떨쳐 버리고는, 에디스가 방에 들어설 때 다시금 더없이 무미하고 더없이 나른한 자기 자신이 되어 긴 의자에 늘어졌다.\n\n에디스, 그토록 아름답고 위엄 있으면서도, 그토록 차갑고 그토록 사람을 밀어내는. 그녀는 배그스톡 소령의 존재를 가볍게 알은체하고, 어머니에게 날카로운 눈길을 던지더니, 창가에서 물러나 그곳에 앉아 바깥을 내다보았다.\n\n\"나의 더없이 소중한 에디스,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"대체 어디 있었던 거니? 얘야, 네가 정말 몹시도 보고 싶었단다.\"\n\n\"손님 중이라고 하셔서, 비켜 있었어요,\" 그녀가 고개도 돌리지 않고 대답했다.\n\n\"그건 늙은 조에게 매정한 일이었지요, 마님,\" 소령이 정중하게 말했다.\n\n\"매정했지요, 알아요,\" 그녀가 여전히 바깥을 내다보며 말했다—그것도 어찌나 차분한 경멸을 담아 말했던지, 소령은 무안해져 무어라 응수할 말을 도무지 떠올리지 못했다.\n\n\"배그스톡 소령은, 나의 사랑스러운 에디스,\" 그녀의 어머니가 늘어지게 말했다, \"흔히는 세상에서 가장 쓸모없고 못마땅한 인간이지만, 너도 알다시피—\"\n\n\"그런 말투를 차릴 필요는 분명 없잖아요, 엄마,\" 에디스가 돌아보며 말했다. \"우리뿐인걸요. 우린 서로를 알고요.\"\n\n그녀의 아름다운 얼굴에 깃든 그 조용한 경멸은—그들 못지않게 자기 자신에게도 분명히 내려앉는 경멸이었으니—어찌나 강렬하고 깊었던지, 그녀 어머니의 점잔 빼는 미소가, 비록 끈질긴 체질의 것이었음에도, 그 순간만은 그 앞에서 풀이 죽었다.\n\n\"나의 사랑스러운 딸아,\" 그녀가 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"아직 여자는 아니고요?\" 에디스가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"오늘따라 너 참 이상하구나, 얘야! 부디 한마디만 하게 해 다오, 얘야, 배그스톡 소령이 돔비 씨에게서 더없이 친절한 쪽지를 가져왔단다. 내일 그분과 아침 식사를 함께하고 워릭과 케닐워스로 마차 나들이를 가자는 제안이야. 가겠니, 에디스?\"\n\n\"제가 가겠느냐고요!\" 그녀가 얼굴이 새빨개지며, 어머니를 돌아보면서 가쁘게 숨을 쉬며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"갈 줄 알았다, 우리 아가,\" 후자가 무심하게 말했다. \"네 말마따나, 묻는 건 순전히 형식이지. 여기 돔비 씨의 편지가 있다, 에디스.\"\n\n\"고마워요. 읽고 싶지 않은걸요,\" 그녀의 대답이었다.\n\n\"그럼 차라리 내가 직접 답장을 쓰는 게 낫겠구나,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"너한테 내 비서가 되어 달라고 할까 했다만, 얘야.\" 에디스가 아무런 움직임도, 아무런 대답도 하지 않자, 스큐턴 부인은 소령에게 자기의 작은 탁자를 더 가까이 밀어 달라고, 그 안에 든 책상을 펼쳐 달라고, 그리고 펜과 종이를 꺼내 달라고 청했다. 이 모든 마음 맞는 정중한 봉사를 소령은 자못 순순히, 헌신적으로 해냈다.\n\n\"네 안부는 어찌할까, 에디스, 얘야?\" 스큐턴 부인이 추신 대목에서 펜을 든 채 멈추며 말했다.\n\n\"엄마 좋으실 대로요,\" 그녀가 고개도 돌리지 않고, 더없이 무심하게 대답했다.\n\n스큐턴 부인은 더 분명한 지시를 구하지 않고 자기 좋을 대로 적은 다음, 편지를 소령에게 건넸다. 소령은 그것을 귀중한 물건처럼 받아 들고는, 가슴 가까이 갖다 대는 시늉을 했으나, 조끼가 미덥지 못한 탓에 어쩔 수 없이 바지 주머니에 넣었다. 그런 다음 소령은 두 부인 모두에게 자못 세련되고 기사다운 작별 인사를 했는데, 손위 부인은 그것을 평소의 태도로 받아들였고, 손아래 부인은, 얼굴을 창 쪽으로 향한 채 앉아, 고개를 어찌나 살짝 숙였던지, 차라리 아무 표시도 하지 않고 소령으로 하여금 그가 들리지도 떠오르지도 않았다고 짐작하게 두는 편이 그에게는 더 큰 예의가 되었을 정도였다.\n\n\"그 여자의 변화로 말할 것 같으면, 선생,\" 소령이 돌아오는 길에 곰곰 생각했다. 이 여정에서—오후가 화창하고 무더운지라—소령은 원주민과 가벼운 짐을 앞세우고, 그 추방된 왕자의 그늘 속에서 걸었다. \"변화니, 야위어 간다느니 하는 따위로 말하자면, 그건 조지프 배그스톡에게는 안 통하지. 그런 건 안 돼, 선생. 여기서는 안 먹혀. 하지만 그 둘 사이에 무언가 갈라짐 같은 게—아니면 그 어미가 부르는 대로 틈 같은 게—있다는 건, 이런, 선생, 그건 충분히 사실로 보이는구먼. 그리고 그것 참 묘하단 말이야! 자 그럼, 선생!\" 소령이 헐떡이며 말했다, \"에디스 그레인저와 돔비는 잘 어울리는 짝이야. 둘이 한판 붙어 보라지! 배그스톡은 이기는 쪽에 걸 테다!\"\n\n소령이 생각에 골몰한 나머지 이 마지막 말들을 큰 소리로 내뱉는 바람에, 가엾은 원주민은 자기더러 하는 말인 줄 알고 걸음을 멈추고 돌아섰다. 이 항명 행위에 극도로 격분한 소령은(비록 자기 익살을 즐기느라 잔뜩 부풀어 올라 있었음에도), 그 일이 벌어진 순간 즉시 지팡이를 원주민의 갈비뼈 사이에 들이밀고는, 호텔에 이르기까지 내내 짧은 간격을 두고 계속 그를 쑤셔 댔다.\n\n소령은 만찬을 위해 옷을 차려입는 동안에도 못지않게 격분해 있었으니, 그 작업이 진행되는 내내 그 검은 하인은 부츠에서 머리빗에 이르기까지 크기가 제각각인, 게다가 주인의 손이 닿는 곳에 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 닥치는 대로 던지는 잡다한 물건들의 우박을 고스란히 맞아야 했다. 소령은 원주민을 완벽한 훈련 상태로 부린다는 데 자부심을 느꼈고, 엄격한 규율에서 조금이라도 벗어나는 일에는 이런 식의 고역 임무를 부과했던 것이다. 게다가 그는 통풍을 비롯해, 육체적인 것은 물론 정신적인 것까지 온갖 골칫거리에 대한 발적제로 삼고자 원주민을 늘 곁에 두고 있었으니, 원주민은 자기 봉급을—그리 많지도 않은 봉급을—제값으로 번 셈이라 할 만했다.\n\n마침내 소령은 손닿는 곳에 있던 던질 만한 것들을 죄다 동내고, 원주민에게 새로운 욕설을 어찌나 많이 퍼부었던지 그 원주민은 영어라는 언어의 자원이 그토록 풍부함에 크게 경탄할 만했으련만, 이윽고 크라바트를 매도록 순순히 몸을 맡겼다. 그리고 옷을 다 차려입고, 이 운동 뒤에 기분이 활기차게 솟아오름을 느끼고는, \"돔비\"와 그의 오른팔에게 흥을 돋우어 주러 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n돔비는 아직 방에 없었으나, 오른팔은 거기 있었고, 그의 치아라는 보물은 여느 때처럼 소령을 맞을 준비가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"자, 선생!\" 소령이 말했다. \"선생을 뵙는 기쁨을 누린 이래로 어떻게 시간을 보내셨소? 산책이라도 좀 하셨소?\"\n\n\"고작 반 시간 남짓 거닐었지요,\" 카커가 응수했다. \"워낙 일이 많아서요.\"\n\n\"사업이오, 응?\" 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"꼭 거쳐야 할 자잘한 일들이 여러 가지 있었지요,\" 카커가 대답했다. \"하지만 아십니까—이건 불신을 가르치는 학교에서 자란 데다 대체로 속마음을 잘 터놓지 않는 저로서는 자못 드문 일입니다만,\" 그가 말을 끊고는, 매혹적인 솔직함의 어조로 말했다, \"소령께는 어쩐지 마음을 다 털어놓고 싶군요, 배그스톡 소령.\"\n\n\"제게 영광을 베푸시는구려, 선생,\" 소령이 응수했다. \"그러셔도 좋소이다.\"\n\n\"그러면 아십니까,\" 카커가 이었다, \"제가 제 친구를—우리 친구라고 불러야겠지요—\"\n\n\"돔비 말이오, 선생?\" 소령이 외쳤다. \"여기 서 있는 저를 보십시오, 카커 씨! 제이 비를?\"\n\n그는 보기에 충분히 부어 있었고, 충분히 푸르렀다. 그래서 카커 씨는 그러한 기쁨을 누리고 있노라고 넌지시 일렀다.\n\n\"그렇다면 선생은, 돔비를 섬기기 위해서라면 물불을 가리지 않을 사내를 보고 계신 거요,\" 배그스톡 소령이 응수했다.\n\n카커 씨는 미소 지으며, 그 점을 확신한다고 말했다. \"아십니까, 소령,\" 그가 이었다, \"하던 말을 다시 잇자면, 제가 오늘 우리 친구가 평소만큼 사업에 마음을 쓰지 않는다는 걸 알게 되었답니다.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 흡족해진 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"그가 좀 정신이 딴 데 가 있고, 주의가 흩어지기 쉬운 상태인 걸 봤어요,\" 카커가 말했다.\n\n\"이런 맙소사, 선생,\" 소령이 외쳤다, \"거기엔 여자가 끼어 있소이다.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 저도 실은 그런 게 아닌가 믿기 시작했습니다,\" 카커가 응수했다. \"소령께서 그걸 넌지시 비치시는 듯했을 때는 농담이신가 했지요. 군인 양반들이란 워낙\"—\n\n소령은 말이 콜록거리는 듯한 그 기침 소리를 내고는, \"허! 우리야 한량들이지, 그건 부정할 수 없지\" 하는 듯이 머리와 어깨를 흔들었다. 그러더니 카커 씨의 단춧구멍을 움켜잡고, 눈을 부릅뜬 채 그의 귀에 대고 속삭이기를, 그 여자는 비범한 매력을 지닌 여인이라고, 선생. 젊은 과부라고, 선생. 명문가 출신이라고, 선생. 돔비가 그녀에게 머리끝까지 푹 빠져 있다고, 선생. 그리고 그것은 양쪽 모두에게 좋은 짝이 될 것이라고. 왜냐하면 그녀에게는 미모와 혈통과 재능이 있고, 돔비에게는 재산이 있으니, 어느 한 쌍인들 그 이상 무엇을 더 바라겠소? 돔비 씨의 발소리가 바깥에서 들려오자, 소령은 카커 씨가 내일 아침이면 그녀를 보고 스스로 판단하게 될 거라고 말하며 말을 뚝 끊었다. 그리고 정신적 흥분과 이 모든 말을 쌕쌕거리는 속삭임으로 해내느라 들인 애 사이에서, 소령은 만찬 준비가 될 때까지 목구멍을 그르렁거리고 눈에서 눈물을 글썽이며 앉아 있었다.\n\n소령은 다른 어떤 고귀한 짐승들이 그러하듯, 먹이 먹는 시간에 자기 모습을 한껏 유리하게 드러내 보였다. 이번 자리에서, 그는 식탁 한쪽 끝에서 휘황하게 빛을 발했고, 다른 쪽 끝에서는 돔비 씨가 한결 부드러운 광채로 그를 뒷받침했다. 한편 카커는 한쪽에서 두 빛에 제 빛을 보태기도 하고, 경우에 따라 그것이 두 빛 모두에 녹아들도록 두기도 했다.\n\n첫 두어 코스가 나오는 동안, 소령은 으레 근엄했다. 원주민이, 은밀히 내려진 일반 명령에 따라, 그의 주위에 온갖 소스와 양념병을 죄다 모아 놓아, 그에게 마개를 빼내고 그 내용물을 접시 위에서 뒤섞느라 할 일을 잔뜩 안겼기 때문이다. 그뿐 아니라, 원주민은 곁탁자에 소령이 날마다 제 입을 데어 가며 즐기는 독특한 풍미와 양념들을 마련해 두었고, 거기에 더해 정체 모를 액체를 소령의 음료에 뿜어 넣는 묘한 기계들도 있었다. 그러나 이번 자리에서 배그스톡 소령은, 이 숱한 일거리 한가운데서도 사교적일 시간을 냈다. 그리고 그의 사교성이란, 카커 씨를 위해 지나치게 능청을 떨고 돔비 씨의 심리 상태를 폭로하는 것으로 이루어졌다.\n\n\"돔비,\" 소령이 말했다, \"자네 안 먹는군. 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"고맙소,\" 그 신사가 응수했다, \"저는 아주 잘 지내고 있습니다. 오늘은 그리 입맛이 없군요.\"\n\n\"아니, 돔비, 그게 어디로 갔나?\" 소령이 물었다. \"어디로 사라졌어? 우리 친구분들에게 두고 온 건 아닐 테지, 내 장담하건대. 오늘 점심에 그분들에게도 입맛이 없었다고 내가 보증할 수 있으니까. 적어도 그 가운데 한 분에 대해서는 보증할 수 있어. 누구라고는 말 안 하겠네만.\"\n\n그러더니 소령은 카커에게 눈을 찡긋했고, 어찌나 무섭도록 능청맞아졌던지, 그의 검은 시중꾼이 명령도 없이 그의 등을 두드려 주지 않았다면 그는 아마 탁자 밑으로 사라져 버렸을 것이다.\n\n만찬이 더 진행된 단계에서—그러니까 원주민이 첫 번째 샴페인 병을 따라 줄 채비를 하고 소령의 팔꿈치 곁에 섰을 때—소령은 한층 더 능청맞아졌다.\n\n\"이걸 가장자리까지 가득 채워라, 이 악당아,\" 소령이 자기 잔을 치켜들며 말했다. \"카커 씨의 잔도 가장자리까지 가득 채우고. 돔비 씨의 잔도. 이런, 신사 여러분,\" 소령이 새 친구에게 눈을 찡긋하며 말했고, 그동안 돔비 씨는 속내를 들킨 듯한 표정으로 자기 접시를 들여다보았다, \"이 포도주 잔을 한 여신께 바칩시다. 조가 그분을 아는 것을 자랑스러워하고, 멀찍이서 겸허하고 경건하게 흠모하는 여신이지요. 에디스,\" 소령이 말했다, \"그것이 그분의 이름이오. 천사 같은 에디스!\"\n\n\"천사 같은 에디스를 위하여!\" 미소 짓는 카커가 외쳤다.\n\n\"좋습니다, 에디스를 위하여,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n새 요리를 든 시중꾼들이 들어오는 바람에 소령은 한층 더 능청맞아졌으나, 더 진지한 어조로 그러했다. \"우리끼리야, 조 배그스톡이 이 화제를 두고 농담과 진담을 섞기는 합니다만, 선생,\" 소령이 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대고 카커에게 반쯤 떨어져서 말했다, \"그는 그 이름을 너무 신성하게 여기는지라 이런 작자들이나 그 어떤 작자들의 입에 올리도록 두지 않지요. 한마디도 안 됩니다, 선생, 저들이 여기 있는 동안은!\"\n\n이것은 소령으로서는 정중하고 어울리는 처신이었고, 돔비 씨도 분명히 그렇게 느꼈다. 소령의 암시에 자기 나름의 그 싸늘한 방식으로 거북해하긴 했어도, 돔비 씨는 그러한 놀림에 반대하지 않았던 것이 분명하며, 오히려 그것을 은근히 바랐다. 어쩌면 소령이, 그날 아침에, 그토록 거만하여 이런 문제를 자기 재상과 격식 차려 의논하거나 속내를 털어놓지는 못하면서도 그가 그 일을 속속들이 알아주기를 바라는 위인이라고 헤아렸을 때, 그는 진실에 자못 가까이 다가갔던 것인지도 모른다. 그것이 어떠하든, 소령이 자기의 가벼운 포격을 퍼붓는 동안 그는 자주 카커 씨를 흘끗거렸고, 그것이 그에게 미치는 효과를 살피는 듯했다.\n\n그러나 소령은, 주의 깊은 청자와, 세상에 둘도 없는 미소꾼을—\"한마디로 지독하게 영리하고 유능한 친구\"라고 그가 훗날 자주 단언했듯이—붙잡아 둔 터라, 그를 돔비 씨에게만 관련된 약간의 능청으로 놓아줄 생각이 없었다. 그리하여 식탁보를 걷어 낸 뒤, 소령은 연대 이야기를 늘어놓고 연대 농담을 터뜨리는, 한층 폭넓고 두루 미치는 영역에서 자기를 빼어난 익살꾼으로 펼쳐 보였는데, 어찌나 헤프도록 흥이 넘쳤던지 카커는 웃음과 감탄에 자못 기진맥진했다(혹은 그런 척했다). 한편 돔비 씨는 빳빳한 크라바트 너머로 그 광경을 바라보았으니, 마치 소령의 소유주처럼, 혹은 자기 곰이 춤을 잘 추는 것을 보고 흐뭇해하는 위엄 있는 흥행사처럼 보였다.\n\n소령이 먹고 마시고 또 사교적 재간을 펼친 통에 목이 너무 쉬어 더는 알아들을 만하게 말할 수 없게 되자, 그들은 커피를 마시러 자리를 옮겼다. 그런 다음 소령은 지배인 카커 씨에게, 긍정의 대답을 들으리라는 기대는 별로 없이, 피켓을 할 줄 아느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"네, 피켓이라면 좀 합니다,\" 카커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"백개먼은, 혹시?\" 소령이 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 백개먼도 좀 합니다,\" 그 치아의 사내가 대답했다.\n\n\"카커는 어떤 놀이든 다 할 줄 알 걸세,\" 돔비 씨가 마치 경첩도 관절도 없는 목각 인형처럼 소파에 몸을 누이며 말했다. \"그것도 잘하지.\"\n\n과연 그는 문제의 그 두 가지를 어찌나 완벽하게 해냈던지 소령이 깜짝 놀라, 무심코 그에게 체스도 둘 줄 아느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"네, 체스도 좀 둡니다,\" 카커가 대답했다. \"더러는 판을 보지 않고도 둬서 한 판 이긴 적도 있지요—그저 잔재주일 뿐이지만요.\"\n\n\"이런 맙소사, 선생!\" 소령이 빤히 쳐다보며 말했다, \"선생은 아무 놀이도 안 하는 돔비와는 대조적이로구려.\"\n\n\"오! 저 사람이요!\" 지배인이 응수했다. \"저분은 그런 자잘한 재간을 익힐 일이 한 번도 없었으니까요. 저 같은 사람에게는 그것들이 더러 쓸모가 있지요. 지금처럼, 배그스톡 소령, 그것들 덕분에 소령과 한 판 둘 수 있게 될 때 말입니다.\"\n\n그것은 단지 그 거짓된 입, 그토록 매끄럽고 널찍한 입 때문이었는지도 모른다. 그런데도 이 짤막한 말의 겸손함과 비굴함 밑에는 무언가 으르렁거림 같은 것이 도사려 있는 듯했고, 한순간 그 하얀 이들이 자기가 아첨하는 그 손을 깨물고 싶어 한다고 여길 만도 했다. 그러나 소령은 그것에 대해 아무 생각도 하지 않았다. 그리고 돔비 씨는, 잠자리에 들 시간까지 이어진 그 놀이 내내, 눈을 반쯤 감은 채 사색에 잠겨 누워 있었다.\n\n그 무렵, 카커 씨는 비록 이긴 쪽이었으나, 소령의 호감 속에 높이 올라가 있었다. 어찌나 그러했던지, 카커 씨가 잠자리에 들기 전에 소령을 그 방에 두고 떠날 때, 소령은 각별한 배려로, 늘 자기 방문 앞 바닥에 깔린 매트리스 위에서 쉬는 원주민을 회랑을 따라 보내, 위풍당당하게 그의 방까지 불을 밝혀 가게 했다.\n\n카커 씨의 침실에 있는 거울의 표면에는 희미한 얼룩이 끼어 있었고, 그 비친 모습은 어쩌면 거짓된 것이었는지도 모른다. 그러나 그 거울은, 그날 밤, 한 사내의 모습을 비추어 주었으니, 그는 제 공상 속에서, 자기 발치 바닥에 잠들어 있는 한 무리의 사람들을—마치 주인 방문 앞의 가엾은 원주민처럼—보고 있었다. 그는 그 사이로 길을 골라 디뎌 나아가며, 자못 악의에 차 내려다보았으나, 위를 향한 어느 얼굴도 밟지는 않았다—아직은.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVII. Deeper Shadows\n\n카커 지배인은 종달새와 함께 일어나, 여름날 아침 산책에 나섰다. 그의 사색은—그는 거니는 동안 미간을 찌푸린 채 사색에 잠겼다—종달새만큼 높이 솟아오르거나 그 방향으로 떠오르는 것 같지는 않았다. 오히려 그것은 땅 위 둥지에 바싹 붙어, 먼지와 벌레들 사이를 둘러보는 듯했다. 그러나 보이지 않는 곳에서 노래하며 인간의 눈이 닿지 못할 만큼 멀리 떠 있는 새도, 카커의 생각보다 더 멀리 가 있지는 못했다. 그는 얼굴을 완벽하게 통제하고 있어서, 그 표정에 대해 분명한 말로 할 수 있는 것이라곤 그저 미소를 짓고 있다거나 깊이 생각에 잠겨 있다는 정도가 고작이었다. 지금 그는 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 종달새가 더 높이 솟아오를수록 그는 생각 속으로 더 깊이 가라앉았다. 종달새가 더 맑고 힘차게 가락을 쏟아낼수록 그는 더 진중하고 깊은 침묵에 빠져들었다. 마침내 종달새가 노래의 물결을 점점 더 쌓아 올리며 곤두박질쳐 내려와, 강물처럼 아침 숨결에 잔물결 일렁이는 그의 곁 푸른 밀밭 사이로 떨어졌을 때, 그는 몽상에서 벌떡 깨어나 갑작스러운 미소를 지으며 주위를 둘러보았는데, 그 미소는 마치 비위를 맞춰야 할 구경꾼이 수없이 둘러서 있기라도 한 듯 정중하고 부드러웠다. 또 그렇게 깨어난 뒤에도 그는 다시 가라앉지 않았다. 오히려 그러지 않으면 얼굴이 주름지며 속을 들킬지도 모른다고 생각한 사람처럼 얼굴을 말끔히 펴고는, 연습이라도 하듯 미소를 지으며 걸음을 이어 갔다.\n\n아마도 첫인상을 의식한 탓이었는지, 카커는 그날 아침 매우 세심하고 단정하게 차려입었다. 그는 자기가 모시는 그 위대한 인물을 흉내 내어 늘 옷차림이 다소 격식을 갖춘 편이었으나, 돔비 씨의 그 빳빳함의 극단에는 미치지 못하게 멈추었다. 그것이 우스꽝스럽다는 것을 알기 때문이기도 했고, 또 그렇게 함으로써 두 사람 사이의 차이와 거리에 대한 자기 인식을 표현하는 또 다른 수단을 찾았기 때문이기도 했다. 실제로 어떤 이들은 이 점에서 그를, 그 얼음장 같은 후원자에 대한 날카롭고도 결코 아첨이 아닌 논평이라고 인용하기도 했다—그러나 세상이란 오해하기 쉬운 법이고, 카커가 세상의 그 나쁜 성향에 책임이 있는 것은 아니었다.\n\n말끔하고 혈색 좋은 모습으로, 햇빛 속에서 마치 옅어지는 듯한 밝은 안색과, 잔디의 부드러움을 한층 돋우는 정갈한 걸음걸이로, 카커 지배인은 초원과 푸른 오솔길을 거닐었고, 나무들이 늘어선 가로수길 사이를 미끄러지듯 지나며, 아침 식사로 돌아갈 시간이 될 때까지 산책했다. 돌아오는 더 가까운 길을 잡아 그 길을 따라가며, 카커는 이를 드러내어 바람을 쐬더니 그렇게 하면서 소리 내어 말했다, \"자, 이제 두 번째 돔비 부인을 만나러 가 볼까!\"\n\n그는 읍내를 벗어나 거닐었다가, 잎이 무성한 나무들이 짙은 그늘을 드리우고, 쉬고 싶은 이들을 위해 여기저기 벤치가 몇 개 놓인 기분 좋은 산책로를 따라 다시 읍내로 들어왔다. 그곳은 어느 시간에도 사람들이 흔히 모이는 장소가 아니었고, 고요한 아침의 그 시각에는 완전히 버려져 외진 분위기를 풍기고 있었으므로, 카커는 그곳을 온통 제 차지로 삼았다, 혹은 그렇게 여겼다. 그래서, 십 분이면 넉넉히 닿을 목적지에 닿는 데 아직 이십 분이 남아 있는 한가한 사내의 변덕으로, 카커는 커다란 나무 둥치들 사이를 누비며, 이 나무 앞으로 저 나무 뒤로 들락날락 지나다니며, 이슬 맺힌 땅 위에 발자국의 사슬을 엮어 갔다.\n\n그러나 그 숲에 아무도 없다고 여긴 것이 착각이었음을 그는 깨달았다. 어느 큰 나무의 둥치를 살그머니 돌아갈 때—그 나무의 거친 껍질은 마치 코뿔소나 대홍수 이전 고대의 어떤 비슷한 괴물의 가죽처럼 옹이지고 겹겹이 포개져 있었는데—그가 막 엮어 가던 그 사슬을 다음 순간 두르게 될 바로 그 부근의 벤치에, 뜻밖의 인물이 앉아 있는 것을 보았기 때문이다.\n\n그것은 한 부인의 모습이었는데, 우아하게 차려입고 매우 아름다웠으며, 그 검고 도도한 두 눈은 땅바닥에 고정되어 있었고, 그 안에서는 어떤 격정이나 갈등이 들끓고 있었다. 앉아서 고개를 숙인 채, 그녀는 아랫입술 한 귀퉁이를 입안으로 깨물고 있었고, 가슴은 들썩였으며, 콧방울은 떨렸고, 고개는 흔들렸으며, 뺨에는 분한 눈물이 어려 있었고, 발은 이끼를 짓밟아 흔적도 없이 으스러뜨리려는 듯 그 위에 놓여 있었다. 그런데도 그에게 이 모습을 보여 준 거의 똑같은 그 눈길이, 바로 그 똑같은 부인이 경멸 어린 권태와 나른함의 기색으로 일어서서, 얼굴이나 자태에 무심한 아름다움과 거만한 멸시 외에는 아무것도 드러내지 않은 채 돌아서는 모습을 그에게 보여 주었다.\n\n말라비틀어지고 몹시 추한 노파 하나가—집시라기보다는 시골을 떠돌며 구걸하고 훔치고 땜질하고 골풀을 엮는 일을 번갈아, 혹은 한꺼번에 하며 돌아다니는 그 잡다한 부랑자 무리의 누구라도 닮은 차림으로—이 부인을 역시 지켜보고 있었던 것이다. 부인이 일어서자, 첫 번째 인물과 기이하게 맞서는 이 두 번째 인물이 땅바닥에서—거의 땅속에서 솟아난 듯—기어올라 와 그 앞을 가로막고 섰기 때문이다.\n\n\"점을 봐 드릴게요, 어여쁜 아씨,\" 노파가, 마치 그 누런 살가죽 밑의 해골 머리가 어서 튀어나오고 싶어 안달이라도 난 듯 턱을 우물거리며 말했다.\n\n\"점이라면 내가 직접 봐도 돼요,\" 하는 것이 대답이었다.\n\n\"아이고, 아이고, 어여쁜 아씨. 하지만 제대로는 못 보지요. 거기 앉아 계실 때도 제대로 못 보셨잖아요. 내가 다 봤다고요! 은전 한 닢만 주시면, 어여쁜 아씨, 점을 제대로 봐 드릴게요. 어여쁜 아씨, 그 얼굴엔 부귀가 깃들어 있어요.\"\n\n\"알아요,\" 부인이 어두운 미소를 띠고 도도한 걸음으로 노파를 지나치며 대꾸했다. \"전부터 알고 있었어요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 아무것도 안 주시겠다고요?\" 노파가 외쳤다. \"점을 봐 줘도 아무것도 안 주시겠다고요, 어여쁜 아씨? 그럼 점을 봐 주면 얼마나 주실 건데요? 뭐라도 좀 주세요, 안 그러면 아씨 뒤에 대고 외쳐 댈 거예요!\" 노파가 격하게 쉰 목소리로 깍깍거렸다.\n\n부인이 막 바싹 지나치려 하던 카커는, 그녀가 길로 들어서려고 가로질러 가는 동안 자기 나무에 몸을 숨기고 있다가, 그녀를 마주하도록 앞으로 나서서, 그녀가 지나갈 때 모자를 벗으며 노파에게 입을 다물라고 일렀다. 부인은 고갯짓으로 그의 개입에 답례하고는 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n\"그럼 당신이 뭐라도 좀 주구려, 안 그러면 저 여자 뒤에 대고 외쳐 댈 테니!\" 노파가 두 팔을 치켜들고, 내민 그의 손에 맞서 앞으로 밀고 들며 비명을 질렀다. \"아니면 이러지,\" 그녀가 갑자기 목소리를 낮추고, 그를 진지하게 바라보며, 순간 분노의 대상을 잊은 듯 덧붙였다, \"뭐라도 좀 주구려, 안 그러면 당신 뒤에 대고 외쳐 댈 테니!\"\n\n\"내 뒤에 대고라고, 할멈!\" 지배인이 주머니에 손을 넣으며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그래,\" 노파가 한결같이 그를 뜯어보며, 쪼그라든 손을 내밀고 말했다. \"내가 다 안다고!\"\n\n\"뭘 안다는 거지?\" 카커가 그녀에게 일 실링을 던져 주며 따져 물었다. \"저 아름다운 부인이 누군지 안단 말인가?\"\n\n옛적에 무릎에 밤을 한가득 담고 우물거리던 그 뱃사람의 아내처럼 입을 우물거리고, 밤을 좀 달라고 청했다가 헛걸음한 그 마녀처럼 험상궂은 표정을 지으며, 노파는 일 실링을 집어 들고, 게처럼, 아니 한 무더기의 게처럼 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 번갈아 펴졌다 오므라드는 그녀의 두 손은 그 종류의 게 두 마리를, 기어가는 듯한 그녀의 얼굴은 또 대여섯 마리를 나타냈을 법했으니. 그녀는 늙은 나무의 울퉁불퉁한 뿌리 위에 웅크려 앉아, 보닛 꼭대기 속에서 짧고 검은 파이프를 꺼내, 성냥으로 불을 붙이고는, 질문한 사내를 뚫어지게 바라보며 말없이 담배를 피웠다.\n\n카커는 웃으며 발꿈치를 돌렸다.\n\n\"좋아!\" 노파가 말했다. \"자식 하나 죽고, 자식 하나 살아 있고. 아내 하나 죽고, 아내 하나 다가오고. 가서 그 여자를 맞으시지!\"\n\n자기도 모르게 지배인은 다시 돌아보며 멈춰 섰다. 파이프를 입에서 떼지 않은 노파는, 담배를 피우면서 보이지 않는 정령과 대화라도 나누듯 우물거리고 중얼거리며, 그가 가던 방향을 손가락으로 가리키고는 웃었다.\n\n\"방금 뭐라고 했나, 미친 할멈?\" 그가 따져 물었다.\n\n여인은 중얼거리고, 재잘대고, 담배를 피우며, 여전히 그의 앞쪽을 가리켰다. 그러나 더는 말이 없었다. 칭찬과는 거리가 먼 작별 인사를 웅얼거리며 카커는 제 갈 길을 갔다. 그러나 그곳을 빠져나오면서 어깨 너머로 그 늙은 나무 뿌리 쪽을 돌아보았을 때, 그는 여전히 자기 앞쪽을 가리키는 손가락을 볼 수 있었고, \"가서 그 여자를 맞으시지!\" 하고 외치는 여인의 소리를 들은 듯했다.\n\n호텔에 가 보니 정성스러운 식사 준비가 끝나 있었고, 돔비 씨와 소령과 아침 식사가 부인들을 기다리고 있었다. 그런 사실들이 어떻게 드러나는지에는 개인의 기질이 큰 몫을 한다는 데 의심의 여지가 없으나, 이번 경우에는 식욕이 다정한 연정을 완전히 압도했다. 돔비 씨는 매우 냉정하고 침착했고, 소령은 격렬한 열기와 짜증 속에서 안달복달하며 부글거리고 있었다. 마침내 그 원주민이 문을 활짝 열었고, 그녀가 회랑을 따라 나른하게 걸어오느라 잠시 뜸을 들인 끝에, 매우 화사하지만 그리 젊지는 않은 한 부인이 나타났다.\n\n\"이보세요, 돔비 씨,\" 부인이 말했다, \"우리가 늦은 것 같아 걱정이군요. 하지만 에디스가 벌써 나가서 스케치하기 좋은 경치를 찾고 있어서, 그 애를 기다리느라 그만. 더없이 거짓된 소령님,\" 그에게 새끼손가락을 내밀며, \"안녕하세요?\"\n\n\"스큐턴 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"제 친구 카커에게 기쁨을 안겨 드리고자 합니다.\" 돔비 씨는 자기도 모르게 친구라는 말에 힘을 주었는데, 마치 '아니, 정말로, 나는 그가 그런 영예를 자처하도록 허락하는 거요'라고 말하는 듯했다. \"이 사람을 부인께 소개해 드림으로써요. 제가 카커 씨를 언급하는 것을 들으셨지요.\"\n\n\"정말이지 황홀하군요,\" 스큐턴 부인이 우아하게 말했다.\n\n물론 카커는 황홀했다. 만일 스큐턴 부인이 (처음에 그가 짐작했던 대로) 그들이 간밤에 건배했던 그 에디스였더라면, 그는 돔비 씨를 위하여 더 황홀해했을까?\n\n\"아니, 도대체, 맙소사, 에디스는 어디 있담?\" 스큐턴 부인이 주위를 둘러보며 외쳤다. \"아직도 문간에서 위더스한테 그 그림들 표구에 대해 지시를 내리고 있다니! 이보세요 돔비 씨, 친절을 좀—\"\n\n돔비 씨는 이미 그녀를 찾으러 나간 뒤였다. 다음 순간 그는, 카커가 나무 아래에서 마주쳤던 바로 그 우아하게 차려입고 매우 아름다운 부인을 팔에 끼고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"카커—\" 돔비 씨가 입을 열었다. 그러나 둘이 서로 알아보는 기색이 어찌나 역력했던지 돔비 씨는 놀라서 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"이분께 신세를 졌어요,\" 에디스가 위엄 있게 고개를 숙이며 말했다, \"방금 끈질긴 거지에게 시달릴 뻔한 걸 면하게 해 주셨거든요.\"\n\n\"제 행운에 신세를 진 셈입니다,\" 카커가 깊이 절하며 말했다, \"그토록 사소한 봉사나마, 기꺼이 그 종이 되고 싶은 분께 해 드릴 기회를 얻었으니 말입니다.\"\n\n그녀의 눈이 한순간 그에게 머물렀다가 땅바닥으로 내려앉을 때, 그는 그 밝고 살피는 듯한 눈길 속에서, 그가 개입한 그 순간에 비로소 다가온 것이 아니라 그보다 먼저 은밀히 그녀를 지켜보고 있었으리라는 의심을 읽었다. 그가 그것을 알아챈 순간, 그녀는 그의 눈에서, 자기 의심이 근거 없는 것이 아니었음을 읽었다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 이 틈을 타 안경 너머로 카커 씨를 살펴보고는, (소령에게 들릴 만큼 혀짧은 소리로 속삭이며) 그가 온통 마음씨뿐인 사람이라고 흡족해하던 스큐턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 이거야말로 내 평생 들어 본 것 중에 가장 매혹적인 우연의 일치 가운데 하나로군요. 어쩜 이럴 수가! 사랑하는 에디스야, 여기엔 너무도 명백한 운명이 깃들어 있어서, 정말이지 사람이 옷 위로 팔짱을 끼고서, 그 못된 터키 사람들처럼, 거시기 말고는 어쩌고가 없으며 무어라더라가 그분의 예언자라고 말하고 싶어질 정도라니까!\"\n\n에디스는 코란에서 따온 이 기이한 인용문을 바로잡으려 들지 않았으나, 돔비 씨는 정중한 말 몇 마디를 건네야 할 필요를 느꼈다.\n\n\"저로서는 대단히 기쁩니다,\" 돔비 씨가 굼뜬 정중함으로 말했다, \"카커처럼 저와 그토록 가까이 연결된 신사분이, 그레인저 부인께 사소하나마 도움을 드리는 영예와 행복을 누리셨다니 말입니다.\" 돔비 씨는 그녀에게 절을 했다. \"허나 그것은 제게 다소의 고통을 주며, 카커를 정말로 부럽게 만드는군요.\" 그는 자기도 모르게 이 말들에 힘을 주었는데, 그것이 매우 놀라운 명제를 담고 있는 듯 보이리라는 것을 의식했기 때문이다. \"그 영예와 그 행복을 제 자신이 누리지 못한 것에 대해, 카커가 부럽다는 말입니다.\" 돔비 씨는 다시 절을 했다. 에디스는 입술을 한 번 비죽인 것 말고는 미동도 없었다.\n\n\"맙소사, 선생,\" 아침 식사를 알리러 온 종업원을 보자 불쑥 입을 연 소령이 외쳤다, \"나로선 참 이상한 일이오, 그런 거지들을 죄다 머리에 총알을 박아 줘도 책잡히지 않을 수 있는 영예와 행복을 아무도 누릴 수 없다니 말이오. 하지만 여기 그레인저 부인을 위한 팔이 있소이다, 부인께서 J. B.에게 이 팔을 받아 주시는 영예를 베푸신다면 말이오. 그리고 부인, 지금 이 조가 부인께 해 드릴 수 있는 가장 큰 봉사는, 부인을 식탁으로 모셔 가는 일이오!\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 소령은 에디스에게 팔을 내밀었다. 돔비 씨는 스큐턴 부인과 함께 앞장섰고, 카커는 일행에게 미소를 지으며 맨 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"정말이지 기쁘답니다, 카커 씨,\" 아침 식사 자리에서, 안경 너머로 그를 다시 한번 흡족하게 살펴본 뒤, 그 어머니 되는 부인이 말했다, \"방문 시기를 어찌나 절묘하게 맞추셨는지, 오늘 우리와 함께 가시게 되었으니 말이에요. 더없이 매혹적인 나들이랍니다!\"\n\n\"이런 모임에서라면 어떤 나들이든 매혹적이겠지요,\" 카커가 대꾸했다, \"허나 제 생각엔 그 자체로도 흥미가 가득하리라 봅니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 스큐턴 부인이 빛바랜 작은 환희의 비명을 지르며 외쳤다, \"그 성(城)은 매혹적이에요!—중세의 정취며—그런 온갖 것이—참으로 절묘하다니까요. 카커 씨도 중세를 끔찍이 사랑하시지 않나요?\"\n\n\"정말이지 무척이나요,\" 카커가 말했다.\n\n\"그 얼마나 매혹적인 시대였던지!\" 클레오파트라가 외쳤다. \"그토록 신앙이 충만하고! 그토록 활기차고 힘찼으며! 그토록 그림 같고! 평범함과는 그토록 완벽하게 동떨어졌으니! 오 이런! 이 끔찍한 시절에 그들이 우리에게 존재의 시정(詩情)을 조금만 더 남겨 주었더라면!\"\n\n스큐턴 부인은 이 말을 하는 내내 돔비 씨를 날카롭게 살피고 있었는데, 그는 에디스를 보고 있었고, 에디스는 듣고는 있었으나 결코 눈을 들지 않았다.\n\n\"우리는 끔찍하게 현실적이에요, 카커 씨,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다. \"그렇지 않나요?\"\n\n자기의 현실성을 불평할 이유가 클레오파트라보다 적은 사람은 거의 없었으니, 실제로 개별적 존재를 지닌 누군가를 구성하는 데 들어갈 수 있는 만큼의 거짓을 그녀는 몸에 지니고 있었다. 그러나 카커는 그럼에도 우리의 현실성을 가엾이 여겼고, 그 점에서 우리가 참으로 가혹한 대접을 받는다는 데 동의했다.\n\n\"그 성의 그림들은 정말이지 거룩하답니다!\" 클레오파트라가 말했다. \"그림을 끔찍이 사랑하시면 좋겠는데요?\"\n\n\"장담하건대, 스큐턴 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 자기 지배인을 엄숙하게 격려하며 말했다, \"카커는 그림에 대해 매우 훌륭한 안목을 지녔습니다. 그림을 감상하는 타고난 능력이 아주 대단하지요. 그 자신이 꽤 평판 있는 화가이기도 하답니다. 그레인저 부인의 안목과 솜씨에 분명 크게 기뻐할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"제기랄, 선생!\" 백스톡 소령이 외쳤다, \"내 의견으론, 당신은 감탄스러운 카커요, 못 하는 게 없는 사람이란 말이오.\"\n\n\"오!\" 카커가 겸손하게 미소 지었다, \"너무 후하게 보시는군요, 백스톡 소령님. 저는 할 줄 아는 게 아주 적습니다. 하지만 돔비 씨께선 저 같은 사람이 거의 부득이하게 익혀야 하는 하찮은 재주에 대해서도 어찌나 후하게 평가해 주시는지요—그리고 그분 자신은 전혀 다른 영역에서 그런 재주를 훨씬 능가하시는데도 말입니다—그러니—\" 카커는 더 이상의 칭찬을 사양하듯 어깨를 으쓱하고는 입을 다물었다.\n\n이 내내 에디스는, 그 어머니의 열렬한 기질이 말로 빛을 발할 때 어머니 쪽을 흘끗 보는 것 말고는, 결코 눈을 들지 않았다. 그러나 카커가 말을 멈추자, 그녀는 잠시 돔비 씨를 바라보았다. 그저 한순간이었으나, 그녀의 얼굴에는 경멸 어린 의아함의 빛이 스쳐 지나갔고, 그것은 식탁을 빙 둘러보며 미소 짓던 한 관찰자의 눈을 벗어나지 못했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 그 검은 속눈썹이 내려앉는 것을 포착하고는, 그것을 붙잡을 기회로 삼았다.\n\n\"안타깝게도 워릭에는 자주 다녀오셨겠지요?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"여러 번요.\"\n\n\"이번 방문이 지루하실까 봐 걱정입니다.\"\n\n\"오 아니에요, 전혀요.\"\n\n\"아! 너는 네 사촌 피닉스를 닮았구나, 사랑하는 에디스야,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다. \"그 애는 워릭 성에 한 번 가 봤다 하면 쉰 번은 가 봤을 거예요. 그러면서도 내일 리밍턴에 온다면—그래 주면 좋으련만, 사랑스러운 천사 같으니!—그다음 날엔 쉰두 번째 방문을 하고 말 거라니까요.\"\n\n\"우린 다들 열정적이잖아요, 그렇지 않아요, 엄마?\" 에디스가 싸늘한 미소를 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"우리 마음의 평안을 위해서는 어쩌면 지나칠 만큼이지, 얘야,\" 그녀의 어머니가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 불평하진 말자꾸나. 우리 자신의 감정이 곧 우리의 보상이니까. 네 사촌 피닉스 말마따나, 칼이 그 뭐라더라를 닳게 한다면—\"\n\n\"칼집이겠지요, 아마,\" 에디스가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그거야—좀 너무 빨리 닳지. 그건 칼이 환하게 빛나며 타오르기 때문이란다, 알겠니, 사랑스러운 내 보물아.\"\n\n스큐턴 부인은 부드러운 한숨을 내쉬었는데, 그것은 그 예민한 가슴이 칼집 노릇을 하는 나무토막 단검의 표면에 그늘을 드리우기라도 하는 듯했다. 그러고는 클레오파트라의 자태로 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고, 수심에 잠긴 애정으로 사랑하는 자식을 바라보았다.\n\n에디스는 돔비 씨가 처음 말을 건넸을 때 그를 향해 얼굴을 돌렸고, 어머니에게 말하는 동안에도, 또 어머니가 그녀에게 말하는 동안에도, 마치 그에게 더 할 말이 있거든 들어 주겠다는 듯 그 자세를 그대로 유지했다. 이 단순한 예의의 태도에는 무언가가 있었으니—거의 도전적이라 할 만하고, 마치 강요에 의해, 혹은 자기가 마지못해 끌려든 어떤 거래의 일환으로 베풀어진다는 성격을 띠게 하는 것이—그것 또한 식탁을 빙 둘러보며 미소 짓던 바로 그 관찰자의 눈을 벗어나지 못했다. 그것은 그로 하여금, 그녀가 나무들 사이에서 홀로 있다고 믿고 있을 때 처음 보았던 그 모습으로 그녀를 떠올리게 했다.\n\n더 할 말이 없는 돔비 씨는—식사가 이제 끝났고 소령은 보아 뱀처럼 잔뜩 포식한 터라—이제 출발하자고 제안했다. 그 신사의 분부에 따라 사륜마차 한 대가 대기하고 있어서, 두 부인과 소령과 그 자신이 그 안에 자리를 잡았다. 원주민과 핏기 없는 시동이 마부석에 올랐고, 타울린슨 씨는 남겨졌다. 그리고 말을 탄 카커가 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n카커는 백 야드쯤 거리를 두고 마차 뒤를 천천히 따라 달리며, 가는 내내 그것을 지켜보았는데, 그 모습은 정말이지 그가 고양이이고 그 안의 네 사람이 생쥐이기라도 한 듯했다. 그가 길 이쪽을 보든 저쪽을 보든—멀리 펼쳐진 풍경, 그 완만한 기복이며 풍차며 곡식이며 풀이며 콩밭이며 들꽃이며 농가 마당이며 건초 더미며 숲 사이로 솟은 첨탑을 보든—아니면 위로, 나비들이 그의 머리 둘레에서 노닐고 새들이 노래를 쏟아 내는 햇살 가득한 공중을 보든—아니면 아래로, 가지들의 그림자가 서로 얽혀 길 위에 일렁이는 양탄자를 만든 곳을 보든—아니면 앞으로, 머리 위로 드리운 나무들이 잎새 사이로 스며 부드러워진 빛으로 어슴푸레한 회랑과 아치를 이룬 곳을 보든—그의 한쪽 눈가는 언제나, 그를 향해 돌려진 돔비 씨의 격식 차린 머리와, 두 사람 사이에서 그토록 무심하고 경멸하듯 늘어진 보닛의 깃털 위에 가 있었다. 그것은 그가 본 그 도도한 눈꺼풀이 내리깔리던 모습과 다를 바 없었으니, 특히 지금 마주한 그 얼굴과 부딪칠 때 더욱 그러했다. 단 한 번, 오직 한 번 그의 경계하는 눈길이 이 대상들을 놓아주었으니, 그것은 낮은 울타리를 뛰어넘고 들판을 가로질러 질주함으로써, 길로 오는 마차를 앞질러 가, 여정의 끝에서 부인들이 내리는 것을 거들 채비를 갖추고 서 있게 되었을 때였다. 그때, 오직 그때, 그녀가 처음 놀라움에 휩싸인 순간 그는 그녀의 눈길을 잠시 마주쳤다. 그러나 그녀가 내릴 때 그의 부드럽고 흰 손으로 그녀를 잡았을 때, 그녀는 이전과 마찬가지로 그를 완전히 못 본 척 지나쳤다.\n\n스큐턴 부인은 자기가 직접 카커를 맡아 성의 아름다움을 보여 주리라 작정했다. 그녀는 그의 팔을, 그리고 소령의 팔도 함께 차지하기로 결심했다. 그것이 그 구제 불능의 작자에게—시정에 관한 한 가장 야만적인 무신론자인 자에게—그런 동행 속에 있는 것이 좋을 터였다. 이 우연한 배치 덕분에 돔비 씨는 에디스를 호위할 자유를 얻었고, 그는 그렇게 했으니, 신사다운 엄숙함으로 여러 방을 가로질러 그녀 앞에서 거드름을 피우며 걸었다.\n\n\"그 사랑스러운 지난날들이여, 카커 씨,\" 클레오파트라가 말했다, \"그 황홀한 요새들과, 그 정겨운 옛 지하 감옥들과, 그 즐거운 고문 장소들과, 그 낭만적인 복수들과, 그 그림 같은 습격과 포위 공격들과, 삶을 진정 매혹적으로 만드는 그 온갖 것들이라니! 우리는 어쩜 이렇게 끔찍하게 타락해 버렸을까요!\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 우리는 한탄스러울 만큼 떨어져 버렸지요,\" 카커가 말했다.\n\n그들 대화의 특이한 점은, 스큐턴 부인이 그 황홀경에도 불구하고, 또 카커가 그 정중함에도 불구하고, 둘 다 돔비 씨와 에디스를 지켜보는 데 골몰해 있었다는 것이다. 온갖 화술의 재능을 갖추고 있으면서도, 그 결과 그들은 다소 산만하게, 또 되는대로 말했다.\n\n\"우리에겐 이제 신앙이라곤 전혀 안 남았어요, 정말이지,\" 스큐턴 부인이 쪼그라든 귀를 앞으로 들이밀며 말했다. 돔비 씨가 에디스에게 뭐라고 말하고 있었던 것이다. \"우리에겐 그 정겨운 옛 남작들에 대한 신앙도 없지요, 더없이 즐거운 자들이었는데—아니면 그 정겨운 옛 사제들에 대해서도요, 더없이 호전적인 사내들이었는데—심지어 저 벽에 걸린 저 더없이 소중한 여왕 베스의 시절, 그토록 더없이 황금 같던 그 시절에 대해서도요. 사랑스러운 분이시지! 온통 마음씨뿐이었답니다. 게다가 그 매혹적인 아버님이라니! 헨리 8세를 끔찍이 사랑하시면 좋겠는데요!\"\n\n\"저는 그를 무척 흠모합니다,\" 카커가 말했다.\n\n\"그토록 우락부락하지요!\" 스큐턴 부인이 외쳤다, \"안 그래요? 그토록 떡 벌어지고. 그토록 진정 영국인답고요. 게다가 그 사랑스러운 작은 눈하며, 그 인자한 턱하며, 어쩜 그렇게 멋진 그림이 되는지요!\"\n\n\"아, 부인!\" 카커가 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"허나 그림을 말씀하신다면, 저기 한 폭의 구도가 있군요! 세상의 어느 화랑이 저것과 견줄 만한 것을 내놓을 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n미소 짓는 신사는 이렇게 말하며, 돔비 씨와 에디스가 다른 방 한가운데 단둘이 서 있는 곳을 문간 너머로 가리켰다.\n\n그들은 말 한마디, 눈길 한 번 주고받지 않고 있었다. 팔짱을 끼고 함께 서 있었으나, 그들 사이에 바다가 굽이쳐 흐르고 있는 것보다 더 동떨어져 보였다. 두 사람의 자존심에는 차이마저 있어서, 그것이 그들을 서로에게서 더욱 멀어지게 했으니, 마치 한쪽은 천지 만물 가운데 가장 거만한 인간 표본이고 다른 한쪽은 가장 비천한 표본이기라도 한 것 같았다. 그는 거드름을 피우고, 굽힐 줄 모르며, 격식을 차리고, 근엄했다. 그녀는 보기 드물 만큼 사랑스럽고 우아했으나, 자기 자신도 그도 주위의 그 무엇도 전혀 아랑곳하지 않았고, 자기 자신의 매력을 도도한 이마와 입술로 마치 자기가 증오하는 표지나 제복이라도 되는 듯 경멸했다. 그들은 그토록 어울리지 않았고 대립되었으며, 불운한 우연과 재앙이 벼려 낸 사슬에 그토록 억지로 함께 묶여 있어서, 그들을 둘러싼 벽의 그림들이 이 부자연스러운 결합에 깜짝 놀라 저마다의 표정으로 그것을 바라보고 있는 듯 상상할 만했다. 험상궂은 기사들과 전사들이 그들을 노려보았다. 손을 치켜든 한 성직자는 이런 한 쌍이 하느님의 제단으로 나아오는 그 우롱을 규탄했다. 풍경화 속의 잔잔한 물은, 깊은 곳에 해를 비추며, 더 나은 도피 수단이 가까이에 없다면 빠져 죽을 곳이라도 남아 있지 않으냐고 물었다. 폐허들은 외쳤다, \"여길 보라, 어울리지 않는 세월에 짝지어진 우리 꼴을 보라!\" 본성상 서로 적대하는 짐승들은 그들에게 교훈이라도 주듯 서로 물어뜯었다. 사랑의 신들과 큐피드들은 겁을 먹고 달아났고, 순교조차도 그 채색된 수난의 역사 속에 이런 고통은 담고 있지 않았다.\n\n그럼에도 스큐턴 부인은 카커 씨가 주의를 기울이라고 청한 그 광경에 어찌나 매혹되었던지, 절반쯤 소리 내어, 그것이 얼마나 아름답고, 얼마나 영혼으로 가득 차 있는지 말하지 않을 수 없었다! 그 말을 엿들은 에디스는 돌아보았고, 머리끝까지 분한 진홍빛으로 발갛게 달아올랐다.\n\n\"사랑하는 에디스는 내가 자기를 흠모하고 있었다는 걸 알 거야!\" 클레오파트라가 거의 머뭇거리듯, 양산으로 그녀의 등을 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"사랑스러운 내 새끼!\"\n\n또다시 카커는 나무들 사이에서 그토록 뜻밖에 목격했던 그 갈등을 보았다. 또다시 그는 그 위에 도도한 나른함과 무심함이 덮여 와, 구름처럼 그것을 가리는 것을 보았다.\n\n그녀는 그에게로 눈을 들지는 않았으나, 가벼우면서도 단호한 눈짓으로 어머니더러 가까이 오라고 명하는 듯했다. 스큐턴 부인은 그 암시를 알아채는 것이 상책이라 여기고, 두 호위 신사를 거느린 채 얼른 다가와, 그때부터 딸 곁에 바싹 붙어 있었다.\n\n이제 주의를 흩뜨릴 것이 없어진 카커는 그림들에 대해 논하기 시작하여, 가장 좋은 것들을 골라 돔비 씨에게 짚어 주었다. 그는 평소처럼 돔비 씨의 위대함을 친근하게 인정하는 투로 말하며, 그를 위해 안경을 맞추어 주거나, 목록에서 알맞은 자리를 찾아 주거나, 그의 지팡이를 들어 주는 따위로 경의를 표했다. 사실 이런 시중들은 카커에게서 비롯되었다기보다 돔비 씨 자신에게서 비롯된 것이었으니, 돔비 씨는 억눌린 권위로, 그로서는 편안한 투로 \"여기, 카커, 좀 거들어 주겠나?\" 하고 말함으로써 자기의 우두머리 됨을 곧잘 내세웠고, 미소 짓는 신사는 늘 기꺼이 그렇게 했던 것이다.\n\n그들은 그림들이며 벽이며 망루 따위를 두루 돌아보았다. 여전히 단출한 일행이었고 소령은 다소 뒷전으로 물러나 있었으므로—소화가 진행되는 동안 졸고 있었던 까닭에—카커는 말이 많아지고 상냥해졌다. 처음에 그는 주로 스큐턴 부인을 상대로 말을 건넸다. 그러나 그 예민한 부인이 미술품들에 어찌나 황홀해했던지, 십오 분이 지나자 하품하는 것밖에는 아무것도 할 수 없게 되어(그것들이 어찌나 완벽한 영감을 주는지, 라고 그녀는 그 황홀의 표시에 대한 핑계로 한마디 했다), 그는 관심을 돔비 씨에게로 옮겼다. 돔비 씨는 이따금 \"지당하네, 카커\" 혹은 \"정말 그렇군, 카커\" 하는 정도 외에는 말이 거의 없었으나, 카커가 계속하도록 은근히 부추겼고, 속으로는 그의 처신을 매우 흡족해했다. 누군가는 말을 하는 편이 낫겠다고 여겼고, 또 말하자면 본점에서 갈라져 나온 한 가지에 불과한 그의 말이 그레인저 부인을 즐겁게 해 줄지도 모른다고 생각했기 때문이다. 빼어난 분별력을 지닌 카커는 결코 그 부인에게 직접 말을 건네는 무례를 범하지 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 한 번도 쳐다보지 않으면서도 귀를 기울이는 듯했고, 한두 번, 그가 그 특유의 겸손을 힘주어 드러낼 때면, 어스름 같은 미소가 그녀의 얼굴 위로 스쳤으니, 그것은 빛이 아니라 짙고 검은 그림자로서였다.\n\n워릭 성을 마침내 거의 다 둘러보고, 소령은 진이 다 빠졌으며—스큐턴 부인은 더 말할 것도 없었으니, 그 특유의 환희의 표현이 실로 매우 잦아져 있었다—마차가 다시 동원되었고, 그들은 인근의 여러 이름난 경승지로 달려갔다. 돔비 씨는 그중 한 곳을 두고 격식을 갖추어, 그레인저 부인의 고운 손으로 그린 스케치라면, 아무리 간략한 것이라도, 이 유쾌한 날의 기념이 되리라고 말했다. 비록 인위적인 기념물 따위는 필요 없으나—그는 확신하건대(여기서 돔비 씨는 또 한 번 절을 했다) 그것을 늘 높이 간직하리라고 했다. 비쩍 마른 위더스가 에디스의 스케치북을 팔에 끼고 있다가, 스큐턴 부인에게서 즉시 그것을 내놓으라는 분부를 받았다. 그리고 에디스가 그림을 그릴 수 있도록 마차가 멈추었으니, 그것을 돔비 씨가 자기 보물들 가운데 간직하기로 한 것이었다.\n\n\"하지만 제가 너무 폐를 끼치는 게 아닐까 걱정입니다,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"천만에요. 어디서 보고 그렸으면 하세요?\" 그녀가 전과 똑같이 억지스러운 주의를 기울이며 그를 향해 돌아서서 대답했다.\n\n돔비 씨는, 크라바트의 풀 먹인 부분이 빠지직 소리를 낼 만큼 또 한 번 절을 하고는, 그것은 화가에게 맡기겠노라고 청했다.\n\n\"당신이 직접 고르시면 좋겠어요,\" 에디스가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"여기서 보고 그리는 게 어떻겠습니까. 그 목적에는 좋은 자리로 보이는데, 아니면—카커, 자넨 어떻게 생각하나?\"\n\n마침 조금 떨어진 전경에 작은 나무 숲이 하나 있었는데, 카커가 아침에 발자국의 사슬을 엮었던 그 숲과 사뭇 비슷했고, 한 나무 아래에 놓인 의자는, 그 위치의 전반적인 성격에 있어 그의 사슬이 끊어졌던 바로 그 지점과 무척이나 닮아 있었다.\n\n\"그레인저 부인께 한 말씀 감히 권해 드려도 될까요,\" 카커가 말했다, \"저곳이 흥미로운—거의 진기하다 할 만한—경치입니다.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 승마 채찍이 가리키는 방향을 눈으로 좇았다가, 재빨리 그의 얼굴로 눈을 들었다. 그것은 소개받은 이후 둘이 주고받은 두 번째 눈길이었고, 그 표정이 더 분명했다는 것만 빼면 첫 번째와 똑같았을 터였다.\n\n\"이게 마음에 드세요?\" 에디스가 돔비 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"황홀하겠습니다,\" 돔비 씨가 에디스에게 말했다.\n\n그리하여 마차는 돔비 씨가 황홀해할 그 지점으로 몰렸고, 에디스는 자리에서 움직이지도 않고 평소의 도도한 무심함으로 스케치북을 펼치더니 그림을 그리기 시작했다.\n\n\"제 연필이 다 뭉툭하네요,\" 그녀가 멈추고 연필들을 뒤적이며 말했다.\n\n\"부디 제게 맡기시지요,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"아니면 카커가 더 잘할 겁니다, 이런 일을 잘 아니까요. 카커, 수고스럽지만 그레인저 부인의 연필들을 좀 봐 드리게.\"\n\n카커는 그레인저 부인 쪽 마차 문에 바싹 다가가 말을 세우고, 고삐를 말의 목덜미에 떨군 채, 미소와 절과 함께 그녀의 손에서 연필들을 받아 들고는, 안장에 앉아 느긋하게 그것들을 깎았다. 그러고는 자기가 그것들을 들고 있다가 필요할 때마다 그녀에게 건네드리게 해 달라고 청했다. 그리하여 카커는, 그레인저 부인의 비범한 솜씨를—특히 나무를 그리는 솜씨를—여러 번 칭송하며, 그녀의 곁에 바싹 붙어, 그녀가 그림 그리는 것을 들여다보고 있었다. 그동안 돔비 씨는 더없이 점잖은 유령처럼 마차 안에 꼿꼿이 서서 역시 지켜보고 있었고, 한편 클레오파트라와 소령은 늙은 비둘기 두 마리가 그러하듯 시시덕거렸다.\n\n\"이걸로 만족하시나요, 아니면 좀 더 마무리할까요?\" 에디스가 돔비 씨에게 스케치를 보여 주며 말했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 손도 대지 말아 달라고 청했다. 그것은 완벽 그 자체였다.\n\n\"참으로 놀랍습니다,\" 카커가 그 붉은 잇몸을 하나도 빠짐없이 동원하여 칭찬하며 말했다. \"이토록 아름답고, 또 전체적으로 이토록 범상치 않은 것은 미처 예상치 못했습니다.\"\n\n이 말은 그림에 못지않게 그림을 그린 사람에게도 해당될 법했다. 그러나 카커 씨의 태도는 솔직함 그 자체였으니—입만이 아니라 그 전 영혼이 그러했다. 그림이 돔비 씨를 위해 한쪽에 치워지고, 스케치 도구들이 챙겨지는 동안에도 그 태도는 그대로였다. 그러고 나서 그는 연필들을 건넸고(그것들은 그의 도움에 대한 데면데면한 인사와 함께, 그러나 눈길 한 번 없이 받아들여졌다), 고삐를 죄어 뒤로 물러나 다시 마차를 따랐다.\n\n말을 달리며 어쩌면 이런 생각을 했을지도 모른다. 이 하찮은 스케치조차도 마치 흥정되어 사들여진 것인 양 그려져 그 주인에게 넘겨졌다고. 어쩌면 이런 생각을 했을지도. 비록 그녀가 그토록 선뜻 그의 청에 응하기는 했으나, 그림 위로 숙인 그 도도한 얼굴, 혹은 그림 속에 담긴 멀리 있는 대상들을 흘끗 보던 그 얼굴은, 추잡하고 비참한 거래에 매여 있는 도도한 여인의 얼굴이었다고. 어쩌면 그런 것들을 생각했을지도. 그러나 분명 미소를 짓고 있었고, 바람과 운동을 즐기며 거리낌 없이 주위를 둘러보는 듯하면서도, 그 날카로운 눈가는 언제나 마차에 두고 있었다.\n\n케닐워스의 유령 깃든 폐허 사이를 거닐고, 더 많은 경승지로 더 많이 말을 달리고—그 대부분은, 스큐턴 부인이 돔비 씨에게 일러 주었듯이, 에디스가 이미 스케치해 둔 것들이어서, 그가 그녀의 그림들을 넘겨 보다가 본 것들이었다—그렇게 그날의 나들이는 막을 내렸다. 스큐턴 부인과 에디스는 마차로 자기네 숙소까지 모셔졌고, 카커는 클레오파트라로부터 저녁에 돔비 씨와 소령과 함께 그곳으로 다시 와서 에디스의 음악을 좀 들으라는 정중한 초대를 받았다. 그리고 세 신사는 만찬을 위해 자기네 호텔로 돌아갔다.\n\n만찬은 어제 것과 판박이였는데, 다만 소령이 스물네 시간만큼 더 의기양양하고 덜 알쏭달쏭했다는 점이 달랐다. 에디스를 위해 다시 건배가 올랐다. 돔비 씨는 다시 기분 좋게 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다. 그리고 카커는 흥미와 찬사로 가득했다.\n\n스큐턴 부인 댁에는 다른 손님이 없었다. 에디스의 그림들이 방 안에 흩어져 있었는데, 어쩌면 평소보다 조금 더 푸짐했다. 그리고 핏기 없는 시동 위더스가 조금 더 진한 차를 돌렸다. 하프가 거기 있었다. 피아노가 거기 있었다. 그리고 에디스는 노래하고 연주했다. 그러나 그 음악조차도 에디스가 돔비 씨의 분부에 따라, 말하자면, 똑같이 한 치도 양보하지 않는 방식으로 연주한 것이었다. 이런 식으로였다.\n\n\"에디스, 사랑하는 내 새끼,\" 차를 마시고 반 시간쯤 지나 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"돔비 씨가 네 연주를 듣고 싶어 죽을 지경이실 거다, 난 알아.\"\n\n\"돔비 씨께서 그 정도는 직접 말씀하실 만큼의 기력은 남아 있으시겠지요, 엄마, 틀림없이요.\"\n\n\"그래 주신다면 한없이 고맙겠습니다,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"무얼 원하세요?\"\n\n\"피아노로?\" 돔비 씨가 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"무엇이든 좋으실 대로요. 그저 고르시기만 하면 돼요.\"\n\n그리하여 그녀는 피아노로 시작했다. 하프도 마찬가지였고, 그녀의 노래도 마찬가지였으며, 그녀가 부르고 연주한 곡목의 선택도 마찬가지였다. 그가 그녀에게만, 다른 누구에게도 아닌 그녀에게만 부과한 소망에 대한 이토록 차갑고 강요된, 그러면서도 즉각적이고 정확한 순응은, 피켓 놀이의 온갖 신경전을 뚫고 들어가 카커 씨의 예리한 주의에 새겨질 만큼 충분히 두드러진 것이었다. 또한 그는 돔비 씨가 자기 권력을 자랑스러워하며 그것을 과시하기를 좋아한다는 사실도 놓치지 않았다.\n\n그럼에도 카커는 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 굴었던지—몇 판은 소령과, 몇 판은 클레오파트라와 했는데, 돔비 씨와 에디스에 관한 그녀의 눈초리의 경계심은 어떤 스라소니도 능가하지 못했을 것이다—그는 심지어 그 어머니 되는 부인의 호감 속에서 자기 입지를 한층 더 높였다. 그리고 작별을 고하면서 내일 아침 런던으로 돌아가야 함을 아쉬워하자, 클레오파트라는—마음이 통하는 사람을 날마다 만나는 것은 아니므로—이것이 결코 마지막 만남이 아니기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n\"저도 그러기를 바랍니다,\" 카커가, 소령을 따라 문 쪽으로 다가가며, 멀찍이 있는 한 쌍을 의미심장하게 바라보며 말했다. \"그러리라 생각합니다.\"\n\n에디스에게 위엄 있게 작별을 고한 돔비 씨는, 클레오파트라의 긴 의자 위로 몸을 굽혔다, 혹은 굽히는 시늉 비슷한 것을 하고는, 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"제가 그레인저 부인께 내일 아침에 찾아뵐 허락을 청했는데요—어떤 목적으로 말입니다—그리고 부인께서 열두 시로 정해 주셨습니다. 그 뒤에 부인을 댁에서 뵐 기쁨을 누릴 수 있기를 바라도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n이 물론 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 말을 듣고 클레오파트라는 어찌나 마음이 들뜨고 동요했던지, 그저 눈을 감고, 고개를 가로젓고, 돔비 씨에게 손을 내미는 수밖에 없었는데, 돔비 씨는 그것을 어찌해야 할지 정확히 알지 못해 그냥 놓아 버렸다.\n\n\"돔비, 어서 갑시다!\" 소령이 문 안을 들여다보며 외쳤다. \"제기랄, 선생, 이 늙은 조는 로열 호텔의 이름을 바꾸자고 제안하고 싶어 죽겠소이다, 우리 자신과 카커를 기리는 뜻으로 '유쾌한 세 독신남'이라고 부르자고 말이오.\" 이렇게 말하며 소령은 돔비 씨의 등을 철썩 치고는, 어깨 너머로 부인들에게 윙크를 했는데, 무시무시하게 피가 머리로 쏠리는 기색으로, 그를 데리고 나가 버렸다.\n\n스큐턴 부인은 소파에 기대 누웠고, 에디스는 떨어져 앉아, 자기 하프 곁에서 말없이 있었다. 어머니는 부채를 만지작거리며 한 번 이상 딸을 몰래 훔쳐보았으나, 눈을 내리깐 채 음울하게 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 딸은 방해할 수 없는 모습이었다.\n\n그렇게 그들은 한 마디 말도 없이 긴 한 시간을 머물러 있었는데, 마침내 스큐턴 부인의 하녀가 관례에 따라, 그녀를 차츰차츰 밤을 위해 채비시키려고 나타났다. 밤이 되면 이 시중드는 여인은 여자라기보다 차라리 화살과 모래시계를 든 해골이 되어 마땅했으니, 그 손길이 죽음의 손길과 같았기 때문이다. 그 손 아래에서 그 채색된 물건은 쪼그라들었다. 형체가 무너지고, 머리카락이 떨어져 나가고, 아치 모양의 검은 눈썹은 듬성듬성한 잿빛 솜털로 변했다. 창백한 입술은 오그라들고, 살갗은 송장 같고 축 늘어졌다. 늙고, 닳고, 누렇고, 꾸벅거리는, 붉은 눈의 여인 하나만이 클레오파트라의 자리에 홀로 남아, 기름때 낀 플란넬 가운을 걸친 채 지저분한 보따리처럼 웅크리고 있었다.\n\n둘만 다시 남았을 때 에디스에게 건네는 그 목소리마저 변해 있었다.\n\n\"왜 나한테 말 안 했니,\" 그것이 날카롭게 말했다, \"그 사람이 내일 약속을 잡고 여기 온다는 걸 말이야?\"\n\n\"엄마는 알고 있으니까요,\" 에디스가 대꾸했다, \"엄마.\"\n\n그 한 단어에 그녀가 실은 그 비웃는 듯한 강세라니!\n\n\"엄마도 알다시피 그 사람은 날 산 거예요,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"아니면 내일 살 거고요. 그 사람은 자기 거래를 곰곰이 따져 봤고, 친구한테 보여 줬으며, 심지어 그걸 꽤 자랑스러워해요. 그 사람은 그게 자기한테 어울리겠다고, 충분히 싼값에 손에 넣을 수 있겠다고 생각해요. 그래서 내일 살 거예요. 하느님, 제가 이러려고 살아왔다니, 그리고 그걸 제가 느끼고 있다니!\"\n\n격정과 자존심으로 강인한 백 명의 여인이 지닌, 의식적인 자기 비하와 타오르는 분노를 한 폭의 아름다운 얼굴 속에 응축해 보라. 그것이 바로 거기, 부들부들 떠는 두 흰 팔 속에 자신을 숨겼다.\n\n\"무슨 소리니?\" 화가 난 어머니가 대꾸했다. \"넌 어릴 적부터—\"\n\n\"어릴 적이라고요!\" 에디스가 그녀를 바라보며 말했다, \"제게 어릴 적이 언제 있었나요? 엄마가 제게 무슨 어린 시절을 남겨 줬어요? 저는 여자였어요—교활하고, 술수에 능하고, 돈만 밝히며, 남자들에게 덫을 놓는—제 자신을, 엄마를, 아니 제가 배운 새로운 재주의 그 천하고 비참한 목적조차 알기도 전에 말이에요. 엄마가 한 여자를 낳았어요. 그 여자를 보세요. 오늘 밤 그 여자는 한껏 도도하군요.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 그녀는 마치 자기 자신을 후려쳐 쓰러뜨리기라도 하려는 듯, 그 아름다운 가슴을 손으로 쳤다.\n\n\"날 봐요,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"정직한 마음을, 그리고 사랑을 가진다는 게 어떤 건지 한 번도 알아 본 적 없는 이 여자를요. 날 봐요, 아이들이 노는 나이에 술수를 부리고 음모를 꾸미도록 배웠고, 젊은 나이에—이미 늙어 빠진 계략 속에서—제겐 무관심 외에 아무 감정도 없는 사람에게 시집간 이 여자를요. 날 봐요, 그 사람이 자기 유산을 물려받기도 전에 죽어 버려 저를 과부로 남긴—엄마에 대한 천벌이지요! 마땅한 벌이고요!—그 여자를요. 그리고 그 이후 십 년간 제 삶이 어땠는지 말해 보세요.\"\n\n\"우리는 너한테 좋은 자리를 마련해 주려고 온갖 애를 써 왔어,\" 그녀의 어머니가 응수했다. \"그게 네 삶이었지. 그리고 이제 넌 그걸 손에 넣었고.\"\n\n\"시장의 어떤 노예도, 장터의 어떤 말도, 제가 그래 왔던 것만큼 이렇게 내보여지고 내놓아지고 살펴지고 행진당하지는 않았어요, 엄마, 십 년이라는 부끄러운 세월 동안 말이에요,\" 에디스가 타오르는 이마로, 그 한 단어에 똑같이 쓰디쓴 강세를 실어 외쳤다. \"그렇지 않아요? 제가 온갖 부류의 남자들의 입방아거리가 되지 않았나요? 바보들이, 난봉꾼들이, 풋내기들이, 망령 든 늙은이들이 제 꽁무니를 쫓아다니다가, 하나씩 하나씩 절 퇴짜 놓고 떨어져 나가지 않았나요. 엄마가 그 온갖 술수를 부리면서도 너무 속이 빤히 들여다보였고, 그 온갖 거짓 핑계를 대면서도 너무 속이 들여다보여서요. 그러다 우리가 거의 악명을 떨치게 될 지경에 이르렀잖아요? 눈으로 더듬고 손으로 만지는 그 방종을,\" 그녀가 번득이는 눈으로 말했다, \"제가 잉글랜드 지도 위 휴양지의 절반에서 다 받아 내지 않았나요? 제 안의 마지막 한 톨의 자존심마저 죽어 버리고, 제가 제 자신을 혐오하게 될 때까지, 제가 여기저기서 호객당하고 팔려 다니지 않았나요? 그게 제 뒤늦은 어린 시절이었나요? 그 전엔 어린 시절이 아예 없었고요. 제게 어린 시절이 있었다는 말은 하지 마세요, 제 평생의 그 어느 밤도 아닌 바로 오늘 밤에는요!\"\n\n\"넌 좋은 곳에 시집갈 수도 있었어,\" 그녀의 어머니가 말했다, \"적어도 스무 번은 말이야, 에디스, 네가 좀 더 부추기기만 했더라면.\"\n\n\"안 돼요! 폐물이나 다름없는 저를, 그리고 제가 마땅히 그래야 할 그런 저를 데려갈 사람은,\" 그녀가 고개를 들고, 부끄러움과 격한 자존심의 격정에 떨며 대답했다, \"이 사람이 그러는 것처럼, 그를 꾀려고 제가 부린 어떤 술수도 없이 저를 데려가야 해요. 그는 경매에서 저를 보고, 저를 사 두는 게 좋겠다고 생각하지요. 그러라죠! 그가 저를 살펴보러—어쩌면 값을 부르러—왔을 때, 제 재주의 목록을 보여 달라고 요구했어요. 저는 그걸 줬어요. 그가 자기 부하들에게 자기 매입을 정당화하려고 그중 하나를 보여 달라고 하면, 저는 그에게 어느 것을 원하는지 말하라고 요구하고, 그러면 그걸 펼쳐 보이지요. 그 이상은 하지 않을 거예요. 그는 자기 뜻대로, 자기 나름의 그 값어치에 대한 판단과 자기 돈의 힘으로 그 매입을 하는 거고요. 그게 그를 결코 실망시키지 않기를 바라요. 저는 그 거래를 떠벌리거나 밀어붙인 적 없어요. 엄마도 안 그랬고요, 제가 막을 수 있는 한에서는요.\"\n\n\"넌 오늘 밤 이상하게 말하는구나, 에디스, 네 친엄마한테 말이다.\"\n\n\"제게도 그렇게 느껴져요. 엄마보다 제게 더 낯설게요,\" 에디스가 말했다. \"하지만 제 교육은 오래전에 끝났어요. 저는 이제 너무 나이 들었고, 차츰차츰 너무 낮게 떨어져 버려서, 새로운 길을 가거나, 엄마의 길을 멈추게 하거나, 제 자신을 도울 수가 없어요. 여자의 가슴을 정화하고 그것을 진실되고 선하게 만드는 모든 것의 싹이, 제 안에서는 한 번도 꿈틀거린 적 없고, 그래서 제 자신을 경멸할 때 저를 지탱해 줄 다른 무엇도 제겐 없어요.\" 그녀의 목소리에는 가슴을 울리는 슬픔이 어려 있었으나, 입술을 비죽이며 말을 이을 때 그것은 사라지고 없었다, \"그러니, 우리가 점잖으면서도 가난하니까, 저는 우리가 이런 수단으로 부유해지는 것에 만족해요. 제가 하고 싶은 말은 오직, 제가 짜낼 힘이 있었던 단 하나의 목적을—거의 능력이라고까지 말할 뻔했네요, 엄마가 제 곁에 있는데도요—지켜 왔다는 것, 그리고 이 사람을 꾀지 않았다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"이 사람이라니! 넌 말하는 게,\" 그녀의 어머니가 말했다, \"마치 그를 미워하기라도 하는 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"그리고 엄만 제가 그를 사랑한다고 생각했죠, 안 그래요?\" 그녀가 방을 가로질러 가던 길을 멈추고 둘러보며 대답했다. \"엄마한테 말해 드릴까요,\" 그녀가 어머니에게 눈을 고정한 채 말을 이었다, \"이미 우리를 속속들이 알고, 우리를 정확히 꿰뚫어 보며, 제 자신의 내면의 자아 앞에서보다도 그 앞에서 제가 자존심도 자신감도 더 적게 느끼는 사람이 누군지요? 그가 저를 너무 잘 알아서 그토록 비참하게 비참해진 까닭에요.\"\n\n\"이건 아마 공격이로구나,\" 그녀의 어머니가 차갑게 응수했다, \"그 가엾고 불운한 그 뭐냐—카커 씨에 대한 말이지! 그 사람에 관한 한(꽤 호감 가는 사람으로 보이던데) 네 자존심이나 자신감의 결핍 따위는, 얘야, 네 혼사에 별 영향을 끼치지는 않을 거다. 왜 날 그렇게 빤히 노려보니? 어디 아픈 거냐?\"\n\n에디스는 마치 무언가에 쏘이기라도 한 듯 갑자기 얼굴을 떨구었고, 두 손으로 얼굴을 누르는 동안, 끔찍한 전율이 온몸으로 기어올랐다. 그것은 금세 사라졌다. 그러고는 평소의 걸음걸이로 그녀는 방을 나섰다.\n\n해골이 되어 마땅했던 그 하녀가 그제야 다시 나타나, 자기 안주인에게—매력과 함께 그 거동도 벗어 버리고, 플란넬 가운과 함께 중풍이라도 걸친 듯한 안주인에게—한쪽 팔을 내주고, 클레오파트라의 잿더미를 그러모아 다른 팔에 안고 데리고 나갔으니, 내일의 소생(蘇生)을 위한 채비였다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII. Alterations\n\n드디어 그날이 왔어요, 수전,\" 플로렌스가 훌륭한 니퍼에게 말했다. \"우리가 그 조용한 집으로 돌아가는 날 말이에요!\"\n\n수전은 쉽게 표현하기 어려운 정도의 의미를 담아 숨을 들이쉬더니, 날카로운 헛기침으로 제 감정을 더 풀어내고 나서 대답했다. \"정말 무척이나 조용하지요, 플로이 아가씨, 의심할 여지가 없어요. 지나치다 싶을 만큼요.\"\n\n\"내가 어렸을 때,\" 플로렌스가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다. \"나한테 말을 하려고 일부러 여기까지 말을 타고 내려오는 수고를 한 그 신사분을, 이제 세 번이나—세 번이었지, 아마, 수전?—본 적이 있니?\"\n\n\"세 번이에요, 아가씨,\" 니퍼가 대답했다. \"한 번은 아가씨가 그 스켓 어쩌고 하는 사람들과 산책을 나가셨을 때—\"\n\n플로렌스가 가만히 그녀를 바라보자, 니퍼 양은 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"바넷 경 내외분과 함께였다는 뜻이에요, 아가씨, 그리고 그 젊은 도련님하고요. 그리고 그 뒤로 두 번의 저녁 때였고요.\"\n\n\"내가 어렸을 때, 그리고 손님들이 아빠를 찾아오곤 했을 때, 그 신사분을 집에서 본 적이 있니, 수전?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 아가씨,\" 하녀가 한참 생각한 끝에 대답했다. \"정말이지 본 적이 있다고는 말씀 못 드리겠어요. 가엾게도 사랑스러운 어머님께서 돌아가셨을 때, 플로이 아가씨, 저는 그 댁에 들어온 지 얼마 안 된 신참이었거든요, 아시잖아요. 그리고 제가 있던 자리는,\" 니퍼는 자기 공로를 돔비 씨가 늘 일부러 묻어 버렸다고 여기는 듯 거드름을 피웠다. \"다락방 바로 아래층이었어요.\"\n\n\"하긴 그렇지,\" 플로렌스가 여전히 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"네가 누가 집에 드나들었는지 알았을 리가 없겠구나. 깜빡했어.\"\n\n\"그렇긴 해도, 아가씨, 저희끼리 그 댁 식구들이며 손님들 얘기를 안 한 건 아니었어요,\" 수전이 말했다. \"그리고 이런저런 말을 많이 들었답니다. 리처즈 부인 전에 있던 유모가, 제가 사람들 틈에 끼어 있으면 듣기 싫은 소리를 하면서 '작은 항아리도 귀가 있다'느니 어쩌니 빈정대긴 했지만요. 하지만 그건 다, 가엾은 사람,\" 수전이 차분히 너그러운 태도로 말했다. \"술버릇 탓으로밖에 볼 수 없는 일이었어요. 그 술버릇 때문에 그만두라는 말을 들었고, 결국 그만뒀으니까요.\"\n\n플로렌스는 침실 창가에 앉아 한 손에 얼굴을 기댄 채 밖을 내다보고 있었는데, 어찌나 생각에 잠겨 있던지 수전이 하는 말이 거의 귀에 들어오지 않는 듯했다.\n\n\"어쨌든 말이에요, 아가씨,\" 수전이 말했다. \"저는 아주 잘 기억해요. 이 카커 씨라는 분이 그때도 지금 못지않게, 아니 거의 그만큼 아가씨 아빠한테 대단한 신사 노릇을 했다는 걸요. 그때 집안에서는 그 양반이 시티에 있는 아빠 일을 죄다 도맡아서 전부 관리하고, 아빠가 누구보다도 그 양반 말을 잘 들으신다는 얘기가 돌았어요. 죄송하지만, 플로이 아가씨, 그거야 어렵지 않은 일이었죠, 아빠는 다른 누구 말도 도통 안 들으셨으니까요. 저는 그걸 알았어요, 제가 '항아리'였을지언정요.\"\n\n수전 니퍼는 리처즈 부인 전에 있던 유모에 대한 상처 입은 기억을 떠올리며 '항아리'라는 말에 힘껏 힘을 주었다.\n\n\"그리고 카커 씨가 떨어져 나가기는커녕, 아가씨,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"자기 자리를 굳건히 지키고 아빠의 신임을 계속 받고 있다는 건, 저 퍼치라는 작자가 이 집에 올 때마다 저희 식구들 사이에서 늘 하는 얘기로 알고 있어요. 그 사람이 세상에서 제일 줏대 없는 잡초 같은 인간이라, 플로이 아가씨, 아무도 그 사람을 한순간도 못 견뎌 하긴 하지만요, 시티에서 무슨 일이 돌아가는지는 꽤 잘 알거든요. 그 사람 말로는, 아가씨 아빠가 카커 씨 없이는 아무것도 안 하시고, 모든 걸 카커 씨한테 맡기시고, 카커 씨가 하라는 대로 하시고, 카커 씨를 늘 곁에 두신다는 거예요. 그래서 제 생각엔 그 사람이(그 더없이 멀건 퍼치가요!) 아가씨 아빠 다음으로는 인도 황제도 카커 씨한테 비하면 아직 태어나지도 않은 갓난애나 다름없다고 믿는 것 같아요.\"\n\n이 말 가운데 한마디도 플로렌스에게서 놓치지 않았으니, 그녀는 수전의 이야기에 흥미가 깨어나, 더 이상 바깥 풍경을 멍하니 바라보지 않고 그녀를 쳐다보며 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"그래, 수전,\" 그 젊은 아가씨가 이야기를 마치자 그녀가 말했다. \"그분은 아빠의 신임을 받고 계셔. 그리고 아빠의 친구야, 분명히.\"\n\n이 화제에 대한 플로렌스의 마음은 며칠째 높이 일렁이고 있었다. 카커 씨는 처음 찾아온 데 이어 두 번 더 방문하면서, 자기와 그녀 사이에 어떤 은밀한 친분이 있는 것처럼—그 배의 소식이 여전히 들리지 않는다고 전하면서 자기 쪽에서 무언가 신비롭고 은근하게 굴 권리가 있는 것처럼—그녀에 대해 일종의 부드럽게 절제된 힘과 권위를 지닌 것처럼 행세했고, 그것은 그녀를 의아하게 만들고 큰 불안을 안겨 주었다. 그녀로서는 그것을 물리치거나, 그가 차츰 그녀 주위에 둘러치는 거미줄에서 빠져나올 방도가 없었다. 그러려면 그의 그런 능란함에 맞설 만한 약간의 기교와 세상 물정에 대한 지식이 필요했을 텐데, 플로렌스에게는 그런 것이 전혀 없었다. 그가 그녀에게 한 말이라곤 배의 소식이 없다는 것, 그리고 최악의 경우를 우려한다는 것뿐이었으나, 그가 어떻게 그녀가 그 배에 마음을 쓰고 있다는 것을 알게 되었는지, 또 무슨 권리로 자기가 그것을 안다는 사실을 그토록 음흉하고 어둡게 그녀에게 내비치는지가 플로렌스를 몹시 괴롭혔다.\n\n카커 씨의 이런 행동, 그리고 그것을 의아함과 불안 속에 거듭 곱씹는 그녀의 버릇은, 플로렌스의 생각 속에서 그를 불편한 매혹으로 둘러싸기 시작했다. 그를 자기에게 다른 누구보다 더한 마력을 행사할 수 없는 실재의 인물 수준으로 끌어내리는 한 방편으로서, 그녀가 이따금 일부러 떠올려 보는 그의 이목구비와 목소리와 태도에 대한 더 또렷한 기억조차도, 그 어렴풋한 인상을 지워 내지는 못했다. 그런데도 그는 결코 눈살을 찌푸리거나 싫어하거나 적의 어린 기색으로 그녀를 바라보는 일이 없었고, 늘 미소 짓고 평온했다.\n\n또한 플로렌스는 아버지에 대해 품은 그 강한 결심을 좇으며, 그리고 두 사람의 그토록 차갑고 서먹한 관계가 자기도 모르게 자기 잘못 때문이라고 굳게 믿으려는 한결같은 결의로, 이 신사가 아버지의 막역한 친구라는 사실을 떠올리고는 불안한 마음으로 생각하곤 했다. 그를 싫어하고 두려워하려는 이 안간힘 같은 성향이, 아버지의 사랑을 떠내려가게 하고 자기를 이토록 외롭게 만든 그 자기 안의 불행의 한 부분은 아닐까? 그녀는 그럴까 봐 두려웠고, 때로는 그렇다고 믿었다. 그러다가는 이 그릇된 감정을 정복해 보리라 마음먹고, 아버지의 친구가 자기를 눈여겨봐 주는 것이 자기에게는 영광이고 격려라고 스스로를 타일렀으며, 그를 참을성 있게 살피고 그를 신뢰하다 보면 자기의 피 흘리는 발걸음이 아버지의 마음으로 이어지는 그 돌투성이 길을 따라 나아갈 수 있으리라 바랐다.\n\n이렇게, 조언을 구할 사람도 하나 없이—그에 대해 불평하는 꼴이 되지 않고서는 누구와도 의논할 수 없었으므로—온순한 플로렌스는 의심과 희망의 불안한 바다 위에서 이리저리 흔들렸다. 그리고 카커 씨는 심해의 비늘 덮인 괴물처럼 그 아래를 헤엄치며, 번뜩이는 눈을 그녀에게서 떼지 않았다.\n\n플로렌스에게는 이 모든 것 속에 다시 집으로 돌아가고 싶은 새로운 이유가 있었다. 그녀의 외로운 생활은 소심한 희망과 의심의 그 행로에 더 잘 어울렸다. 그리고 그녀는 이따금, 자기가 자리를 비운 사이에 아버지에 대한 애정을 드러낼 어떤 희망찬 기회를 놓칠지도 모른다고 두려워했다. 가엾은 아이여, 하늘은 알리라, 그녀가 이 마지막 점에 대해서는 마음을 놓아도 좋았으련만! 그러나 그녀의 푸대접받은 사랑은 그녀 안에서 파닥거리고 있었으니, 잠든 중에도 그것은 꿈속에서 날아올라, 집으로 돌아온 길 잃은 새처럼 아버지의 목에 깃들었다.\n\n월터에 대해서는 자주 생각했다. 아! 밤이 음울하고 바람이 집 둘레로 윙윙 불 때면 얼마나 자주 생각했던가! 그러나 희망은 그녀의 가슴속에서 강했다. 젊고 열정적인 이에게는, 그녀만큼의 경험을 가졌더라도, 젊음과 열정이 가냘픈 불꽃처럼 꺼지고 삶의 환한 대낮이 한낮에 밤으로 잠겨 드는 것을 상상하기란 너무도 어려운 일이어서, 희망은 아직 강했다. 그녀의 눈물은 월터의 고통을 위해 자주 흘렀으나, 그가 죽었으리라는 짐작 때문에 흘린 적은 드물었고, 오래간 적도 결코 없었다.\n\n그녀는 그 늙은 기구 제작자에게 편지를 썼으나 답장을 받지 못했다. 사실 답장이 필요한 편지도 아니었다. 집으로, 그 옛 은둔의 생활로 기쁘게 돌아가던 그 아침의 플로렌스의 사정이 이러했다.\n\n블림버 박사 내외는, (몹시 마지못해하는) 그 소중한 보호 대상인 바넷 도련님을 데리고 이미 브라이턴으로 돌아간 뒤였다. 거기서 그 젊은 도련님과 그의 파르나소스 순례 동무들은 분명 끊임없이 학업을 다시 이어 가고 있을 터였다. 방학 기간은 다 지나가 버렸고, 별장에 머물던 어린 손님들은 대개 떠났으며, 플로렌스의 긴 방문도 끝에 이르렀다.\n\n그러나 비록 집 안에 거주하지는 않았으나, 이 가족에 대한 살뜰한 정성이 한결같고 여전히 그들에게 헌신적인 손님이 하나 있었다. 바로 투츠 씨였다. 몇 주 전, 블림버 식의 속박을 깨뜨리고 반지를 낀 채 자유를 향해 날아오르던 그날 밤에 다행히도 맺게 된 스켓츠 주니어와의 친분을 다시 새롭게 한 이래, 그는 하루걸러 어김없이 찾아와 현관문에 트럼프 한 벌이 통째로 다 될 만큼 명함을 남겨 두고 갔다. 정말이지 어찌나 많았던지, 그 명함 의례는 투츠 씨 쪽에서는 한 벌 떼어 주는 셈이었고, 하인 쪽에서는 휘스트 한 판 칠 만큼이었다.\n\n투츠 씨는 또한, 이 가족이 자기를 잊지 못하게 하겠다는 대담하고도 흐뭇한 발상으로(다만 이 묘책은 치킨의 넘쳐나는 머릿속에서 나온 것이라 짐작할 만한 근거가 있다), 노 여섯 개짜리 커터 한 척을 마련했다. 그 배는 치킨의 물놀이 친구들이 노를 젓고, 그 저명한 인물이 친히 키를 잡았는데, 그는 그 목적을 위해 새빨간 소방수 외투를 걸쳤고, 늘 앓고 있는 멍든 눈은 초록색 차양 아래 가렸다. 이 배를 마련하기에 앞서, 투츠 씨는 치킨에게 가상의 경우를 들어 떠보았다. 가령 치킨이 메리라는 이름의 젊은 아가씨한테 반해서 자기 배를 한 척 마련할 작정이라면, 그 배 이름을 뭐라고 부르겠느냐고 말이다. 치킨은 갖가지 굳센 맹세를 곁들이며, '폴'이라고 짓거나 '치킨의 기쁨'이라고 짓겠다고 대답했다. 이 발상을 더 갈고닦아, 투츠 씨는 깊이 궁리하고 숱하게 머리를 짜낸 끝에 자기 배를 '투츠의 기쁨'이라 부르기로 결정했으니, 이는 플로렌스에게 바치는 섬세한 찬사로서, 두 당사자를 아는 사람이라면 누구도 그 함의를 놓칠 리 없는 것이었다.\n\n진홍색 방석 위에 두 발을 허공에 띄운 채 비스듬히 누워, 투츠 씨는 자기 계획을 수행하느라 멋진 배를 타고 날이면 날마다, 주가 가고 또 주가 가도록 강을 거슬러 올라왔고, 바넷 경의 정원 근처를 이리저리 떠돌았으며, 바넷 경의 창가에서 내다보는 누군가에게 더 잘 보이도록 선원들에게 강을 가로질러 날카로운 각도로 이쪽저쪽 누비게 했으니, '투츠의 기쁨'이 펼친 그런 곡예는 강가의 이웃 동네 전체를 경탄으로 가득 채웠다. 그러나 바넷 경의 정원 강가 가장자리에 누군가가 보이기만 하면, 투츠 씨는 늘 더없이 기묘하고 있을 법하지 않은 우연들이 겹쳐서 마침 그곳을 지나가던 것처럼 시치미를 뗐다.\n\n\"잘 지내시오, 투츠?\" 바넷 경이 잔디밭에서 손을 흔들며 말하곤 했고, 그러면 교활한 치킨은 강기슭 가까이로 배를 몰았다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 바넷 경?\" 투츠 씨가 대답하곤 했다. \"여기서 경을 뵙다니, 이 무슨 놀라운 일입니까!\"\n\n투츠 씨는 그 빈틈없는 머리로, 마치 거기가 바넷 경의 저택이 아니라 나일강이나 갠지스강 강둑의 어느 버려진 건물이기라도 한 듯 늘 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"이렇게 놀란 적이 없습니다!\" 투츠 씨가 외치곤 했다.—\"돔비 아가씨도 거기 계신가요?\"\n\n그러면 플로렌스가, 어쩌면, 나타났다.\n\n\"오, 디오게네스는 아주 잘 있습니다, 돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠가 소리치곤 했다. \"오늘 아침에 들러서 안부를 물었거든요.\"\n\n\"정말 고마워요!\" 플로렌스의 상냥한 목소리가 대답하곤 했다.\n\n\"뭍으로 올라오지 않겠소, 투츠?\" 그러면 바넷 경이 말했다. \"어서! 바쁠 것 없잖소. 와서 우리랑 좀 봅시다.\"\n\n\"오, 별것 아닙니다, 고맙습니다만!\" 투츠 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 대꾸하곤 했다. \"그저 돔비 아가씨가 알고 싶어 하실까 해서요, 그게 다입니다. 안녕히 계십시오!\" 그러고는 그 초대를 받아들이고 싶어 죽을 지경이면서도 그럴 용기가 없는 가엾은 투츠 씨는 쓰라린 마음으로 치킨에게 신호를 보냈고, 그러면 '기쁨'호는 화살처럼 물살을 가르며 멀어져 갔다.\n\n플로렌스가 떠나던 날 아침, '기쁨'호는 정원의 층계참에 더없이 화려한 자태로 떠 있었다. 수전과 이야기를 나눈 뒤 작별 인사를 하러 아래층으로 내려가니, 응접실에서 자기를 기다리고 있는 투츠 씨가 보였다.\n\n\"오, 안녕하십니까, 돔비 아가씨?\" 마음의 소원이 이루어져 그녀에게 말을 건넬 때면 늘 끔찍이도 허둥대는, 그 상사병 든 투츠가 말했다. \"고맙습니다, 저는 아주 잘 지냅니다. 아가씨도 그러시기를 바라고요. 어제 디오게네스도 잘 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"참 친절하시네요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 별것 아닙니다,\" 투츠 씨가 대꾸했다. \"혹시 이렇게 날씨가 좋으니, 돔비 아가씨, 배로 집에 돌아가시는 것도 괜찮지 않으실까 생각했어요. 배에는 하녀 분이 탈 자리도 넉넉하답니다.\"\n\n\"정말 고마워요,\" 플로렌스가 망설이며 말했다. \"정말이에요—하지만 사양할게요.\"\n\n\"오, 별것 아닙니다,\" 투츠 씨가 대꾸했다. \"그럼 안녕히 계십시오.\"\n\n\"기다렸다가 스켓츠 부인을 뵙고 가지 않으실래요?\" 플로렌스가 다정하게 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아닙니다, 고맙습니다,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다. \"그건 정말 아무것도 아닙니다.\"\n\n이런 자리에서 투츠 씨는 어찌나 수줍어하고 또 허둥대던지! 그러나 마침 그때 스켓츠 부인이 들어오자, 투츠 씨는 별안간 부인에게 안부를 묻고 아주 잘 지내시기를 바란다고 말하고 싶은 열정에 사로잡혔다. 그리고 바넷 경이 나타나기 전까지는 도무지 부인의 손을 놓고 악수를 끝낼 줄을 몰랐다. 바넷 경이 나타나자, 그는 곧바로 절박한 자의 끈질김으로 그에게 매달렸다.\n\n\"우리는 오늘,\" 바넷 경이 플로렌스 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"우리 집의 빛을 잃는답니다, 정말이지요, 투츠.\"\n\n\"오, 별것 아—제 말은, 그렇습니다, 정말로요,\" 당황한 투츠 씨가 더듬거렸다. \"안녕히 계십시오!\"\n\n이 작별 인사가 단호하기 짝이 없었음에도, 투츠 씨는 떠나기는커녕 멍하니 주위를 두리번거리며 서 있었다. 플로렌스는 그를 거들어 주려고, 거듭 감사하며 스켓츠 부인에게 작별을 고하고는 바넷 경에게 팔을 내밀었다.\n\n\"청을 하나 드려도 되겠소, 나의 다정한 돔비 아가씨,\" 마차로 그녀를 안내하며 주인이 말했다. \"댁의 다정한 아빠께 내 최고의 안부를 전해 주겠소?\"\n\n이 부탁을 받는 것이 플로렌스에게는 괴로운 일이었다. 자기에게 베풀어진 친절이 곧 아버지에게 베풀어진 것이라고 바넷 경이 믿게 내버려 둠으로써, 그를 속이고 있는 것만 같았기 때문이다. 그러나 사정을 설명할 수 없었으므로, 그녀는 고개를 숙여 그에게 감사했다. 그리고 다시 한번, 그런 거북함도 없고 자기 슬픔을 일깨우는 것도 없는 그 음울한 집이야말로 자기에게 타고난, 가장 좋은 안식처라고 생각했다.\n\n별장에 아직 남아 있던 그녀의 옛 친구들과 벗들이 집 안에서, 또 정원에서 작별 인사를 하러 달려 나왔다. 그들은 모두 그녀를 따랐고, 진심으로 절절하게 그녀와 작별했다. 하인들조차 그녀가 떠나는 것을 아쉬워하며 마차 문 둘레로 와서 고개를 끄덕이고 무릎을 굽혀 절했다. 플로렌스가 그 다정한 얼굴들을 둘러보다가, 그 가운데서 바넷 경 내외분과, 멀찍이서 낄낄거리며 그녀를 빤히 바라보는 투츠 씨의 얼굴을 보았을 때, 그녀는 폴과 함께 블림버 박사 댁에서 돌아오던 그날 밤을 떠올렸다. 그리고 마차가 떠나갈 때, 그녀의 얼굴은 눈물로 젖어 있었다.\n\n서글픈 눈물이었으나, 위안의 눈물이기도 했다. 그녀가 돌아가는 그 음울하고 오래된 집과 얽힌 더 부드러운 기억들이 모두 떠오르면서 그 집을 그녀에게 소중하게 만들었기 때문이다. 그 고요한 방들을 거닐던 때로부터, 아버지가 쓰는 방들 안으로 부드럽게, 두려운 마음으로 마지막으로 살그머니 들어갔던 때로부터, 일상의 모든 행동 속에서 사랑하는 고인의 엄숙하면서도 위안을 주는 영향을 느끼던 때로부터 얼마나 오래된 일처럼 여겨지던지! 이번의 새로운 작별은 더욱이 가엾은 월터와의 헤어짐을 떠올리게 했다. 그날 밤 그의 표정과 말, 그리고 그에게서 보았던, 뒤에 남겨 두고 가는 이들에 대한 다정함이 용기와 드높은 기개와 우아하게 어우러진 모습을 떠올리게 했다. 그의 작은 이야기 또한 그 오래된 집과 결부되어, 그 집에 그녀의 마음에 대한 새로운 권리와 끈을 부여했다.\n\n수전 니퍼조차도, 집으로 향하는 길에서는 그 여러 해의 보금자리를 향해 마음이 누그러졌다. 그 집이 음울했고, 또 그 음울함에 그녀가 가차 없이 정당한 평가를 내리긴 했어도, 그녀는 그 집의 많은 것을 용서했다. \"다시 보면 반가울 거예요, 부정 못 하겠어요, 아가씨,\" 니퍼가 말했다. \"자랑할 거리야 별로 없는 집이지만, 그렇다고 불타거나 헐려 버리는 건 저도 싫네요!\"\n\n\"옛 방들을 둘러보면 반갑겠지, 그렇지 수전?\" 플로렌스가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 아가씨,\" 니퍼가, 집에 가까워질수록 점점 더 마음이 누그러지며 대답했다. \"반갑긴 할 거예요, 부정 안 할게요, 비록 내일이면 또 그 방들이 죄다 싫어질 가능성이 아주 높긴 하지만요.\"\n\n플로렌스는 자기에게 있어서는 다른 어디보다 그 집 안에 더 큰 평화가 있음을 느꼈다. 그 키 높은 어두운 벽들 사이에 자기 비밀을 가두어 두는 편이, 그것을 밝은 데로 들고 나가 행복한 눈들의 무리로부터 감추려 애쓰는 것보다 더 낫고 더 수월했다. 자기 둘레의 사랑하는 마음들 속에서 새로운 낙담을 겪느니, 홀로 자기의 사랑하는 마음을 탐구하는 편이 더 나았다. 새로운 무대가 아무리 즐거운 것이라 한들, 거기서보다는, 비록 자기 둘레에서 곰팡이 피고 녹슬고 썩어 가더라도, 그런 기억들의 고요한 성소 안에서 아무에게도 보살핌받지 못한 채, 그러나 한결같이 참을성 있게 희망하고 기도하고 사랑하기를 이어 가는 편이 더 수월했다. 그녀는 마법에 걸린 듯한 자기의 옛 삶의 꿈을 다시 반겨 맞으며, 그 옛 어두운 문이 자기 위로 다시 한번 닫히기를 갈망했다.\n\n이런 생각들로 가득 찬 채, 그들은 길고 어둑한 거리로 접어들었다. 플로렌스는 집에 가장 가까운 쪽 마차 자리에 앉아 있지 않았으므로, 마차와 집 사이의 거리가 줄어들자 길 건너편 아이들을 보려고 창밖을 내다보았다.\n\n그녀가 그러고 있을 때, 수전의 탄성에 그녀는 재빨리 돌아보았다.\n\n\"어머나, 세상에!\" 수전이 숨을 헐떡이며 외쳤다. \"우리 집이 어디 갔지!\"\n\n\"우리 집이!\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n수전은 창밖에서 머리를 들이밀었다가 다시 내밀고, 마차가 멈춰 서자 다시 들이밀더니, 어리둥절한 눈으로 자기 아가씨를 빤히 바라보았다.\n\n집 둘레로 지하층에서 지붕까지 비계가 미로처럼 둘러쳐져 있었다. 벽돌과 돌을 잔뜩 쌓아 둔 것이며, 회반죽 더미며, 목재 무더기가 옆쪽의 그 넓은 거리의 폭과 길이를 절반이나 가로막고 있었다. 사다리가 벽에 기대 세워져 있었고, 일꾼들이 그 위를 오르락내리락했으며, 사람들이 비계 디딤판 위에서 일하고 있었다. 안에서는 칠장이와 도배공들이 분주했고, 큼직한 장식 벽지 두루마리가 문 앞 수레에서 배달되고 있었으며, 가구 운반 마차 한 대도 길을 막고 있었다. 어느 방을 봐도 휑하니 깨진 창문 너머로는 가구가 하나도 보이지 않았고, 오로지 일꾼들과 그들의 갖가지 연장들만이 부엌에서 다락방까지 우글거렸다. 안이나 밖이나 똑같이, 벽돌공, 칠장이, 목수, 석공—망치, 흙받기, 솔, 곡괭이, 톱, 흙손—이 모두가 한꺼번에, 일제히 합창하듯 일하고 있었다!\n\n플로렌스는 마차에서 내렸는데, 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴로 그녀를 맞으러 문간에 서 있는 타울린슨을 알아보기 전까지는, 여기가 제대로 찾아온 집이 맞는지, 맞을 수 있는지 반신반의했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있는 건 아니지요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아닙니다, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"굉장한 개조가 진행되고 있군요.\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨, 굉장한 개조지요,\" 타울린슨이 말했다.\n\n플로렌스는 꿈속에 있는 듯이 그를 지나쳐 서둘러 위층으로 올라갔다. 오랫동안 어두웠던 응접실에는 야단스러운 빛이 들어차 있었고, 높은 자리에는 디딤대와 발판이 있고 종이 모자를 쓴 사람들이 있었다. 어머니의 초상화는 다른 가재도구들과 함께 사라지고 없었으며, 그것이 걸려 있던 자국 위에는 분필로 \"이 방은 판벽. 초록과 금색\"이라고 휘갈겨져 있었다. 계단은 집 바깥처럼 기둥과 널빤지의 미로였고, 채광창 위에는 배관공과 유리공들의 올림포스 신들이 통째로 갖가지 자세로 비스듬히 누워 있었다. 그녀 자신의 방은 아직 안쪽은 손대지 않았으나, 바깥에는 들보와 판자가 세워져 있어 햇빛을 가로막고 있었다. 그녀는 그 작은 침대가 있던 또 다른 침실로 빠르게 올라갔다. 그런데 입에 파이프를 물고 머리를 손수건으로 동여맨 거인 같은 시커먼 사내 하나가 창문으로 안을 들여다보고 있었다.\n\n플로렌스를 찾아다니던 수전 니퍼가 그녀를 발견한 곳이 바로 여기였다. 수전은, 아가씨더러 아빠께 내려가 보시라고, 아빠가 말씀을 나누고 싶어 하신다고 말했다.\n\n\"집에 계셔! 게다가 나랑 이야기를 하고 싶어 하셔!\" 플로렌스가 떨면서 외쳤다.\n\n플로렌스 자신보다 한없이 더 정신이 산란해진 수전이 자기 심부름을 거듭 전하자, 플로렌스는 창백하게 동요하면서도 한순간의 망설임도 없이 다시 서둘러 내려갔다. 그녀는 내려가는 길에 생각했다. 감히 아빠께 입을 맞춰 봐도 될까? 가슴속 갈망이 그녀의 결심을 굳혔고, 그녀는 그래도 되겠다고 생각했다.\n\n그녀의 심장이 그의 앞에 이르렀을 때, 아버지는 그 고동 소리를 들을 수 있었을지도 모른다. 한순간만 더 있었으면, 그 심장은 그의 가슴에 맞닿아 뛰었을 것이다.\n\n그러나 그는 혼자가 아니었다. 거기에는 두 부인이 있었고, 플로렌스는 멈춰 섰다. 어찌나 안간힘을 다해 감정을 억눌렀던지, 만일 그 짐승 같은 친구 디가 뛰어들어 집에 온 것을 환영하듯 그녀에게 애정 표시를 퍼붓지 않았더라면—그 바람에 부인 가운데 하나가 작게 비명을 질렀고, 그것이 그녀 자신에게서 주의를 돌려놓았는데—그녀는 바닥에 그대로 까무러쳤을 것이다.\n\n\"플로렌스,\" 아버지가 손을 내밀며 말했다. 어찌나 뻣뻣하게 내밀었던지 그것은 그녀를 떼어 놓는 듯했다. \"잘 지냈느냐?\"\n\n플로렌스는 두 손으로 그 손을 받아 들고 머뭇머뭇 입술에 가져다 댔다가, 그 손이 물러나는 대로 놓아주었다. 그 손은 문을 닫으며 그것에 닿았는데, 그녀에게 닿았을 때만큼이나 다정하게 닿았다.\n\n\"저 개는 무슨 개냐?\" 돔비 씨가 못마땅한 듯 말했다.\n\n\"개예요, 아빠—브라이턴에서 온 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래!\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. 그러고는 그녀의 말뜻을 알아차렸기에, 그의 얼굴 위로 구름이 스쳐 지나갔다.\n\n\"성질이 아주 순해요,\" 플로렌스가 타고난 우아함과 다정함으로 두 낯선 부인을 향해 말했다. \"그저 저를 보고 반가워하는 것뿐이에요. 부디 너그러이 봐주세요.\"\n\n그녀는 두 사람이 주고받는 눈길에서, 비명을 지른, 앉아 있는 부인은 나이가 많고, 아빠 가까이에 서 있는 다른 부인은 매우 아름다우며 우아한 자태를 지녔음을 알아보았다.\n\n\"스큐튼 부인,\" 아버지가 첫 번째 부인을 향해 돌아서서 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"이 아이가 제 딸 플로렌스입니다.\"\n\n\"정말 매력적이군요,\" 그 부인이 안경을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"참으로 꾸밈이 없어요! 사랑스러운 플로렌스, 괜찮다면 내게 입을 맞춰 다오.\"\n\n플로렌스는 그렇게 한 뒤, 아버지가 곁에 서서 기다리고 있는 다른 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"이디스,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"이 아이가 제 딸 플로렌스요. 플로렌스, 이 부인이 곧 네 엄마가 되실 거다.\"\n\n플로렌스는 흠칫 놀라며, 갖가지 감정이 뒤섞인 채 그 아름다운 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그 가운데서 그 이름이 일깨운 눈물이 한순간 놀라움과 호기심과 감탄과 형언할 수 없는 종류의 두려움과 다투었다. 그러다 그녀는 외쳤다. \"오, 아빠, 부디 행복하세요! 평생토록 아주, 아주 행복하시기를 바라요!\" 그러더니 그 부인의 가슴에 엎드려 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 처음에는 플로렌스에게 다가갈지 말지 망설이는 듯하던 그 아름다운 부인은, 그녀를 가슴에 끌어안고, 그녀를 감싼 손으로 그녀의 허리를 꼭 죄어 안았다. 마치 그녀를 안심시키고 위로하려는 듯이. 그 부인의 입술에서는 한마디 말도 새어 나오지 않았다. 그녀는 플로렌스 위로 고개를 숙여 그녀의 뺨에 입을 맞추었으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"우리, 방들을 둘러보며,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"일꾼들이 어떻게 하고 있는지 가서 볼까요? 부디 제가 모시지요, 친애하는 부인.\"\n\n그는 안경 너머로 플로렌스를 바라보고 있던 스큐튼 부인에게 팔을 내밀며 이렇게 말했다. 그 부인은, 마치 자기 자신의 풍부한 곳간에서—틀림없이—약간의 마음과 자연스러움을 부어 넣으면 그 아이를 어떤 모습으로 만들 수 있을지 그려 보기라도 하는 듯한 눈빛이었다. 플로렌스는 여전히 그 부인의 가슴에 엎드려 흐느끼며 그녀에게 매달려 있었는데, 그때 온실에서 돔비 씨가 이렇게 말하는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"이디스에게 물어봅시다. 아니, 그 사람이 어디 있지?\"\n\n\"이디스, 얘야!\" 스큐튼 부인이 외쳤다. \"너 어디 있니? 어딘가에서 돔비 씨를 찾고 있구나, 내 다 알지. 우리 여기 있단다, 얘야.\"\n\n그 아름다운 부인은 플로렌스를 안았던 팔을 풀고, 그녀의 얼굴에 다시 한번 입술을 누른 뒤, 서둘러 물러나 그들과 합류했다. 플로렌스는 그 자리에 그대로 서 있었다. 행복하면서도 슬프고, 기쁘면서도 눈물에 젖은 채, 어찌 된 영문인지, 얼마나 오래인지도 모른 채, 그러나 일순간에. 그때 그녀의 새 엄마가 돌아와 다시 그녀를 품에 안았다.\n\n\"플로렌스,\" 그 부인이 서둘러, 그러면서도 더없이 진지하게 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다. \"넌 나를 미워하는 것으로 시작하지는 않을 거지?\"\n\n\"미워하다니요, 엄마?\" 플로렌스가 그녀의 목에 팔을 두르고 그 눈길을 마주 돌려주며 외쳤다.\n\n\"쉿! 나를 좋게 생각하는 것으로 시작하렴,\" 그 아름다운 부인이 말했다. \"내가 너를 행복하게 해 주려 애쓰겠다는 것, 그리고 내가 너를 사랑할 준비가 되어 있다는 것을 믿는 것으로 시작하렴, 플로렌스. 잘 있어. 우리 곧 다시 만나자. 잘 있어! 이제 여기 있지 말고.\"\n\n그녀는 다시 한번 그녀를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안았는데—재빠른 말투였으나, 단호했다—그리고 플로렌스는 그녀가 다른 방에서 그들과 합류하는 것을 지켜보았다.\n\n그리고 이제 플로렌스는 그 새롭고 아름다운 엄마에게서 아버지의 사랑을 얻는 법을 배울 수 있으리라는 희망을 품기 시작했다. 그리고 그날 밤, 잃어버린 옛집에서의 잠 속에서, 그녀 자신의 엄마가 그 희망을 향해 환하게 미소 지으며 축복을 내려 주었다. 꿈꾸는 플로렌스여!\n\n# 제24장\n\n칙 부인의 눈이 뜨이다\n돔비 씨 집과 관련하여 그토록 진귀한 광경들—비계와 사다리, 그리고 머리에 손수건을 동여맨 사내들이 날아다니는 정령이나 기이한 새들처럼 창문 안을 노려보는 광경—이 벌어지고 있는 줄은 전혀 모르는 채, 톡스 양은 이 파란만장한 시기의 어느 날 아침에 늘 먹는 음식으로 식사를 마쳤다. 즉, 겉을 긁어낸 프랑스 빵 한 개, 갓 낳은(혹은 그렇다고 보증된) 달걀 한 개, 그리고 작은 찻주전자 하나였는데, 그 안에는 톡스 양 몫으로 그 찻잎을 작은 은수저로 한 술, 또 찻주전자 몫으로 작은 은수저로 한 술 우려 넣은 것이었다—이는 알뜰한 살림꾼들이 즐기는 공상적 발상이었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 위층으로 올라가, 하프시코드로 새 왈츠를 연주하고, 화초에 물을 주고 손질하며, 자질구레한 장식품의 먼지를 털고, 매일의 습관대로 자신의 작은 응접실을 프린세스 플레이스의 화환으로 만들었다.\n\n톡스 양은 죽은 나뭇잎처럼 보이는 낡은 장갑 한 켤레를 끼었는데, 그 일들을 할 때면 늘 끼던 것으로—다른 때에는 탁자 서랍 속에 사람들 눈에 띄지 않게 숨겨 두었다—그러고는 차근차근 일에 착수했다. 새 왈츠로 시작해서, 자연스러운 연상 작용에 따라 자기 새로 넘어갔는데, 그 새는 어깨가 몹시 솟은, 나이를 먹어 깃이 많이 헝클어진 카나리아였으나, 프린세스 플레이스가 익히 아는 대로 째지게 잘 우는 가수였다. 다음 차례로는 작은 도자기 장식품, 종이로 만든 파리채 같은 것들을 손질하고, 적당한 때가 되자 화초로 돌아왔는데, 그 화초들은 톡스 양에게 매우 설득력 있는 어떤 식물학적 이유로 여기저기 가위로 잘라 주어야 하는 것이 보통이었다.\n\n이날 아침 톡스 양은 화초에 좀처럼 다가가지 못했다. 날씨는 따뜻했고, 바람은 남풍이었으며, 프린세스 플레이스에는 여름철의 한숨 같은 것이 감돌아 톡스 양의 생각을 시골로 돌려놓았다. 프린세스 암스 술집에 딸린 술 따르는 소년이 물통을 들고 나와 프린세스 플레이스 전체에 꽃무늬를 그리며 물을 흩뿌렸는데, 그것이 잡초 무성한 땅에 신선한 향기를—톡스 양의 말로는, 그야말로 무언가 자라나는 향기를—풍겨 주었다. 모퉁이 너머 큰길에서 햇살이 자그맣게 비쳐 들었고, 그을음투성이 참새들이 그 위를 폴짝폴짝 건너갔다 돌아왔다 하면서, 지나칠 때마다 환해졌다. 아니, 그 햇살을 시냇물처럼 멱 감고는, 굴뚝과는 아무 상관 없는 영광스러운 참새가 되었다. 프린세스 암스의 창문에는 진저비어를 찬미하는 문구들이, 목마른 손님들이 거품 속에 잠겨 있거나 날아드는 코르크 마개에 얻어맞아 정신을 잃은 그림과 더불어 눈에 띄게 걸려 있었다. 어딘가 도시 밖에서는 늦건초를 만들고 있었는데, 그 향기가 멀리서 오느라 가난한 이들의 거처 사이에서 수많은 반대편 냄새와 싸워야 했음에도(역병을 우리 조상들의 지혜의 일부로 떠받들며, 그 거처들을 비참하게 유지하려고 제 나름의 보잘것없는 애를 쓰는 갸륵한 양반들에게 하느님께서 갚아 주시기를!), 그래도 그것은 프린세스 플레이스로 희미하게 실려 와, 그런 것들이 으레 그러하듯, 자연과 그 건강한 공기를 속삭여 주었으니—참사회원이며 거기에 기사들까지, 그 현명한 끄덕임에—그것도 어찌나 끄덕여 대는지!—구르는 세상이 멈춰 서는 그자들에게도 아랑곳없이, 갇힌 자들과 사로잡힌 자들에게도, 황폐하고 억눌린 자들에게도 그러했다.\n\n톡스 양은 창가 의자에 앉아, 고인이 된 갸륵한 아버지를—공직의 세관부에 계셨던 톡스 씨를 떠올렸다. 그리고 항구 도시에서 상당량의 차가운 콜타르와 약간의 시골티 속에서 보낸 자기 어린 시절을 떠올렸다. 그녀는 옛 시절의 초원에 대한 부드러운 회상에 잠겨 들었는데, 그 초원은 미나리아재비로 반짝여서, 마치 거꾸로 뒤집힌 황금별 하늘이 수없이 펼쳐진 것 같았다. 그리고 자기가 주로 면포 옷차림을 한 어린 영원한 정절의 맹세자들을 위해 민들레 줄기로 사슬을 엮어 주던 일과, 그 족쇄들이 얼마나 빨리 시들어 끊어졌는지를 떠올렸다.\n\n창가 의자에 앉아 참새들과 비쳐 드는 햇살을 내다보면서, 톡스 양은 또한 고인이 된 갸륵한 어머니를—분 바른 머리에 변발을 한 그자의 누이를—그분의 미덕과 류머티즘을 떠올렸다. 그리고 다리가 불룩하고 목소리가 거친 사내가, 머리에 인 무거운 바구니에 눌려 모자가 그저 검은 머핀처럼 찌부러진 채로, \"꽃 사세요\" 하고 외치며 프린세스 플레이스를 내려왔을 때—그가 내지르는 고함 소리의 진동마다 그의 겁먹은 자그마한 데이지 뿌리들이 부르르 떨었으니, 마치 어린아이들을 팔러 다니는 식인귀라도 되는 것 같았다—그때 여름의 추억이 어찌나 강하게 톡스 양을 사로잡았던지, 그녀는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 자기도 모르는 사이에 비교적 늙어 버릴 거라고 중얼거렸다—그럴 법한 일이었다.\n\n수심에 잠긴 기분 속에서, 톡스 양의 생각은 돔비 씨의 자취를 따라 떠돌아갔다. 아마도 소령이 맞은편 자기 하숙으로 돌아와, 막 자기 창문에서 그녀에게 인사를 건넸기 때문이리라. 그렇지 않고서야 톡스 양이 무슨 이유로 돔비 씨를 자기의 여름날이며 민들레 족쇄와 결부시켰겠는가? 그분은 좀 더 명랑해지셨을까? 톡스 양은 생각했다. 그분은 운명의 명령과 화해하셨을까? 그분은 다시 결혼하실까? 만일 그렇다면, 누구와? 대체 어떤 사람과 말인가!\n\n이런 명상을 즐기던 중에, 그녀가 고개를 돌리다가 벽난로 거울에 비친 자신의 골똘한 모습에 놀랐을 때, 얼굴에 홍조가—날씨가 따뜻한 탓에—번졌다. 작은 마차 한 대가 프린세스 플레이스로 들어와 곧장 자기 집 문을 향해 오는 것을 보았을 때, 또 한 번 홍조가 뒤따랐다. 톡스 양은 일어서서 황급히 가위를 집어 들었고, 그리하여 마침내 화초로 다가가, 칙 부인이 방에 들어왔을 때는 화초를 손질하느라 무척 분주한 척했다.\n\n\"이렇게 더없이 사랑스러운 내 친구는 안녕하신가요!\" 톡스 양이 두 팔을 벌리며 외쳤다.\n\n톡스 양의 더없이 사랑스러운 친구의 거동에는 약간의 위엄이 섞여 있었으나, 그녀는 톡스 양에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"루크리셔, 고마워요, 나는 그럭저럭 잘 지내요. 당신도 그러기를 바라요. 흠!\"\n\n칙 부인은 독특한 단음절의 작은 기침을 앓고 있었다. 일종의 입문서랄까, 기침 기술의 쉬운 길잡이 같은 것이었다.\n\n\"이렇게 일찍 오시다니, 얼마나 다정한지 몰라요, 그래요!\" 톡스 양이 말을 이었다. \"그래, 아침은 드셨나요?\"\n\n\"고마워요, 루크리셔,\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"먹었어요. 일찍 아침을 들었지요\"—이 갸륵한 부인은 프린세스 플레이스에 대해 호기심이 인 듯, 말하는 동안 사방을 둘러보았다—\"집에 돌아온 오라비와 함께요.\"\n\n\"좀 나아지셨으면 좋겠네요, 그렇죠?\" 톡스 양이 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"많이 나아졌어요, 고마워요. 흠!\"\n\n\"우리 루이자는 그 기침을 조심해야 해요.\" 톡스 양이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요,\" 칙 부인이 대꾸했다. \"그저 날씨가 바뀌어서 그래요. 우리는 변화를 각오해야 하지요.\"\n\n\"날씨의요?\" 톡스 양이 천진하게 물었다.\n\n\"모든 것의요,\" 칙 부인이 대꾸했다. \"당연히 그래야지요. 이건 변화의 세상이에요. 누구라도 이렇게 명백한 사실을 반박하거나 회피하려 든다면, 루크리셔, 나는 무척 놀랄 거고, 그 사람의 이해력에 대한 내 평가가 크게 달라질 거예요. 변화라니!\" 칙 부인이 준엄한 철학을 담아 외쳤다. \"아니, 어머나 세상에, 변하지 않는 것이 대체 무어가 있나요! 누에조차도—그런 문제 따위는 신경 쓰지 않을 거라고 다들 짐작할 법한 그 누에조차도—온갖 뜻밖의 것들로 끊임없이 변하잖아요.\"\n\n\"우리 루이자는,\" 온순한 톡스 양이 말했다. \"예를 드는 데 늘 능하시다니까요.\"\n\n\"당신은 어찌나 다정한지요, 루크리셔,\" 칙 부인이 다소 누그러져서 대꾸했다. \"그렇게 말해 주고, 또 정말 그렇게 생각해 주니 말이에요. 우리 둘 중 누구도 서로에 대한 평가를 낮출 이유가 결코 생기지 않기를 바라요, 루크리셔.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그럴 거예요,\" 톡스 양이 대꾸했다.\n\n칙 부인은 앞서처럼 기침을 하고는, 파라솔의 상아 끝으로 카펫에 줄을 그었다. 자기의 어여쁜 친구를 잘 알고, 그녀가 조금이라도 피로하거나 언짢은 압박을 받으면 이리저리 두서없는 짜증으로 빠지기 쉽다는 것을 경험으로 아는 톡스 양은, 이 잠깐의 틈을 이용해 화제를 바꾸었다.\n\n\"미안하지만, 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"혹시 내가 마차 안에서 칙 씨의 늠름한 모습을 본 게 맞나요?\"\n\n\"그이는 거기 있어요,\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 제발 거기 그냥 두세요. 신문을 가지고 있으니, 앞으로 두 시간은 아주 만족스러워할 거예요. 꽃이나 계속 손질하세요, 루크리셔, 나는 여기 앉아서 좀 쉬게 해 주고요.\"\n\n\"우리 루이자도 알다시피,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"우리 같은 친구 사이에서는 격식 비슷한 거라면 무엇이든 어림도 없는 일이지요. 그러니까—\" 그러니까 톡스 양은 그 문장을 말이 아닌 행동으로 끝맺었다. 벗어 두었던 장갑을 다시 끼고, 가위로 한 번 더 무장하고는, 현미경을 들여다보는 듯한 부지런함으로 잎사귀 사이를 싹둑싹둑 잘라 내기 시작했다.\n\n\"플로렌스도 집에 돌아왔어요,\" 한동안 고개를 한쪽으로 기울인 채, 파라솔로 바닥에 그림을 그리며 말없이 앉아 있던 칙 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 정말이지 플로렌스는 이제 너무 컸어요, 늘 익숙해져 있던 그 외톨이 생활을 계속하기에는 말이지요. 물론 그렇고말고요. 그건 의심할 여지가 없어요. 정말이지 나는 다른 의견을 옹호할 수 있는 사람이라면 거의 존경하지 못할 거예요. 내 바람이 어떻든 간에, 나 자신의 바람조차도 존경할 수 없을 거예요. 우리는 감정을 그 정도까지 제멋대로 명령할 수는 없으니까요.\"\n\n톡스 양은 그 명제가 알아들을 만한 것인지 따지지 않고 동의했다.\n\n\"그 애가 이상한 아이고,\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"또 그 모든 슬픈 일들이 벌어지고, 그 모든 끔찍한 실망을 겪은 뒤에, 오라비 폴이 그 애와 함께 있으면서 도무지 편안함을 느낄 수 없다면, 그렇다면, 그 대답은 무엇일까요? 오라비가 노력을 해야 한다는 거죠. 노력해야 할 의무가 있다는 거예요. 우리 집안은 늘 노력으로 유명한 집안이었어요. 폴은 그 집안의 가장이고, 거의 유일하게 남은 대표자예요—나야 뭐겠어요—나는 아무 대수롭지도 않은 사람이지만—\"\n\n\"우리 더없이 사랑스러운 분,\" 톡스 양이 만류했다.\n\n칙 부인은 잠시 넘쳐흐르던 눈을 닦고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그러니까 오라비는 그 어느 때보다도 노력을 해야 할 의무가 있는 거예요. 그리고 오라비가 그렇게 했다는 사실이 나에게는 일종의 충격으로 다가오긴 하지만—나는 워낙 약하고 어리석은 천성이라서요. 그게 결코 축복이 아닌 건 분명하고요. 내 심장이 대리석 판이나 포석이었으면 하고 자주 바란다니까요—\"\n\n\"우리 어여쁜 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 다시 만류했다.\n\n\"그래도, 오라비가 자기 자신에게, 또 돔비라는 이름에 그토록 충실하다는 걸 아는 건 나에게 승리예요. 물론, 나는 늘 오라비가 그러리라는 걸 알고 있었지만요. 다만 바라기로는,\" 칙 부인이 잠시 멈추었다가 말했다. \"그 여자도 그 이름에 어울리는 사람이었으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n톡스 양은 작은 초록색 물뿌리개를 주전자에서 채우고는, 그것을 마친 뒤 우연히 고개를 들었는데, 칙 부인이 자기 얼굴에 담아 자기에게 보내고 있는 표정의 분량에 어찌나 놀랐던지, 그 작은 물뿌리개를 일단 탁자에 내려놓고 그 곁에 앉았다.\n\n\"루이자,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"그 말씀에 관해서, 한낱 보잘것없는 사람으로서 제가 감히 한마디 하자면, 당신의 그 사랑스러운 조카가 모든 면에서 더없이 장래가 촉망된다고 생각하는데요, 혹시 조금이라도 위안이 되실까요?\"\n\n\"무슨 말이에요, 루크리셔?\" 칙 부인이 한층 위엄을 더한 태도로 대꾸했다. \"내 어느 말을 두고 하는 말이지요?\"\n\n\"그 애가 그 이름에 어울린다는 말씀이요,\" 톡스 양이 대답했다.\n\n\"만일,\" 칙 부인이 엄숙한 인내심을 담아 말했다. \"내가 분명히 표현하지 못했다면, 루크리셔, 물론 그건 내 잘못이지요. 어쩌면 우리 사이에 이어져 온 친밀함이 아니라면, 내가 굳이 무어라 표현할 이유 자체가 없을지도 모르지요. 그리고 그 친밀함을, 루크리셔, 나는 진심으로 바라기를—굳게 바라기를—그 무엇도 흩뜨리지 않았으면 해요. 그렇지 않다면, 내가 무엇 때문에 달리 굴겠어요? 이유가 없지요. 터무니없는 일일 테고요. 하지만 나는 분명히 표현하고 싶어요, 루크리셔. 그러니 그 말로 돌아가자면, 그건 어떤 식으로도 플로렌스와 관련된 것이 아니었다고 말해야겠어요.\"\n\n\"정말요!\" 톡스 양이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그래요,\" 칙 부인이 짤막하고 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"미안해요,\" 그 온순한 친구가 응수했다. \"하지만 내가 도무지 이해를 못 했나 봐요. 내가 둔한가 봐요.\"\n\n칙 부인은 방 안과 길 건너편을 둘러보았다. 화초며, 새며, 물뿌리개며, 톡스 양만 빼고 시야에 들어오는 거의 모든 것을 보고는, 마침내 시선이 바닥으로 향하던 길에 잠시 톡스 양에게 떨어뜨렸다가, 눈썹을 치켜올린 채 카펫을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"내가, 루크리셔, 그 여자가 이름에 어울린다고 말할 때, 그건 오라비 폴의 두 번째 아내를 두고 하는 말이에요. 똑같은 말은 아니더라도 사실상 그런 뜻으로, 오라비가 두 번째 아내를 맞을 작정이라는 걸 이미 말한 것 같은데요.\"\n\n톡스 양은 황급히 자리를 떠나 다시 화초로 돌아갔다. 마치 수많은 극빈자들의 머리털을 손질하는 이발사만큼이나 인정사정없이 줄기와 잎사귀 사이를 싹둑싹둑 잘라 댔다.\n\n\"그 여자가 자기에게 베풀어진 영예를 충분히 알아챌 수 있을지,\" 칙 부인이 고고한 어조로 말했다. \"그건 또 별개의 문제지요. 그러기를 바라요. 이 세상에서 우리는 서로를 좋게 생각해야 할 의무가 있으니, 나도 그러기를 바라요. 나와는 상의도 없었어요. 만일 상의했다면, 내 조언은 틀림없이 무례하게 받아들여졌을 테니, 그러니 지금 이대로가 한없이 나아요. 나는 이대로가 훨씬 좋아요.\"\n\n톡스 양은 고개를 숙인 채로 여전히 화초 사이를 잘라 댔다. 칙 부인은 이따금 자기 머리를 힘차게 절레절레 흔들며, 마치 누군가에게 도전이라도 하듯 일장연설을 계속했다.\n\n\"만일 오라비 폴이 나와 의논했다면—이따금 그러기도 하니까요—아니, 차라리, 이따금 그러곤 했지요. 이제 자연히 더는 그러지 않을 테니까요. 그리고 이건 내가 책임에서 벗어나는 일로 여기는 일이에요.\" 칙 부인이 신경질적으로 말했다. \"왜냐하면 다행히도 나는 질투하는 사람이 아니거든요—\" 여기서 칙 부인은 다시 눈물을 흘렸다. \"만일 오라비 폴이 나에게 와서, '루이자, 아내에게서 어떤 종류의 자질을 찾아보라고 충고하겠소?'라고 물었다면, 나는 분명 이렇게 대답했을 거예요. '폴, 가문이 있어야 해요. 미모가 있어야 해요. 품위가 있어야 해요. 연줄이 있어야 해요.' 내가 썼을 말이 바로 그거예요. 그 직후에 나를 단두대로 끌고 갔다 해도,\" 마치 그런 결과가 충분히 있을 법한 일이라는 듯 칙 부인이 말했다. \"나는 그 말을 썼을 거예요. 나는 이렇게 말했을 거예요. '폴! 오라비가 가문도 없는 사람과 재혼을 한다고요! 미모도 없는 사람과 결혼한다고요! 품위도 없는 사람과 결혼한다고요! 연줄도 없는 사람과 결혼한다고요! 이 세상에, 미치지 않고서야, 그런 터무니없는 생각을 감히 품을 꿈조차 꿀 사람은 아무도 없어요!'\"\n\n톡스 양은 자르던 손을 멈추고, 화초 사이로 머리를 숙인 채 귀 기울여 들었다. 어쩌면 톡스 양은 이 서두와 칙 부인의 열기 속에 희망이 있다고 생각했는지도 모른다.\n\n\"내가 이런 논법을 택했을 거예요,\" 신중한 부인이 말을 이었다. \"왜냐하면 나는 바보가 아니라고 믿으니까요. 내가 뛰어난 지성을 지닌 사람이라고 주장하는 건 아니에요—물론 어떤 사람들은 별나게도 나를 그렇게 여겨 왔지만요. 나처럼 응석받이로 자라지 못한 사람이라면 그런 생각은 이내 깨우쳐 버릴 테지만요—그래도 내가 완전히 바보는 아니라고 믿어요. 그리고 나에게,\" 칙 부인이 형언할 수 없는 경멸을 담아 말했다. \"오라비 폴 돔비가 그런 자질을 갖추지 못한—누구든 상관없어요\"—이 짧은 구절에서 그녀는 연설의 다른 어느 부분에서보다 날카롭고 강조하는 어조였다—\"누구든 그런 자와 결합할 가능성을 한순간이라도 떠올릴 수 있다고 말하는 것은, 내가 코끼리로 태어나 코끼리로 길러졌다고 말하는 것만큼이나 내 알량한 이해력을 모욕하는 일이에요. 뭐, 다음번엔 그런 말도 듣겠지요.\" 칙 부인이 체념하듯 말했다. \"나는 조금도 놀라지 않을 거예요. 으레 그러려니 하고 있으니까요.\"\n\n뒤이은 한순간의 침묵 속에서, 톡스 양의 가위가 힘없이 한두 번 싹둑거렸다. 그러나 톡스 양의 얼굴은 여전히 보이지 않았고, 톡스 양의 아침 가운이 들썩이고 있었다. 칙 부인은 사이에 놓인 화초 너머로 그녀를 곁눈질하고는, 부드럽게 확신에 찬 어조로, 굳이 입에 올릴 필요조차 없는 명백한 사실 하나를 곱씹는 사람처럼 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그러니, 물론 오라비 폴은 마땅히 그에게 기대되었을 일을, 또 그가 다시 결혼 상태에 들어선다면 누구라도 예견했을 법한 일을 한 거예요. 솔직히 좀 뜻밖이긴 해요. 아무리 흐뭇한 일이라도 말이에요. 왜냐하면 폴이 시외로 나갈 때, 나는 그이가 시외에서 무슨 애정을 맺으리라고는 전혀 생각도 못 했고, 여기를 떠날 때 그이에게는 분명 어떤 애정도 없었으니까요. 그래도 모든 면에서 더없이 바람직한 일인 것 같아요. 그 어머니라는 분이 더없이 점잖고 우아한 사람일 거라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없고, 그분이 그들과 함께 산다는 방침을 두고 내가 왈가왈부할 권리는 조금도 없어요. 그건 내 일이 아니라 폴의 일이니까요—그리고 폴이 고른 그 여자 본인으로 말하자면, 나는 아직 초상화밖에 못 봤지만, 그건 정말이지 아름다워요. 이름도 아름답고요.\" 칙 부인이 힘차게 머리를 흔들며, 의자에 자세를 고쳐 앉으며 말했다. \"에디스는 내가 보기에 흔치 않으면서도 동시에 기품이 있어요. 그러니, 루크리셔, 당신도 그 결혼이 곧 거행될 거라는 소식을 기쁘게 들을 게 분명해요—물론, 그러겠지요.\" 다시 한번 잔뜩 힘을 주어 말했다. \"그리고 여러 차례 당신에게 많은 유쾌한 호의를 베풀어 온 오라비의 처지에 일어난 이 변화를 당신도 기뻐할 게 분명해요.\"\n\n톡스 양은 말로 대답하지는 않고, 떨리는 손으로 작은 물뿌리개를 집어 들더니, 마치 그 안의 물로 어떤 가구를 더 보기 좋게 만들 수 있을까 궁리하는 사람처럼 멍하니 둘러보았다. 톡스 양의 감정이 이런 고비에 이른 바로 그때 방문이 열렸고, 그녀는 흠칫 놀라 큰 소리로 웃으며, 들어오는 사람의 품에 쓰러졌다. 다행히도 칙 부인의 분개한 얼굴도, 길 건너편 자기 창가의 소령도 알아채지 못한 채였는데, 소령은 쌍안경 안경을 한껏 작동시키며, 메피스토펠레스 같은 환희로 얼굴과 몸뚱이를 한껏 부풀리고 있었다.\n\n쓰러지는 톡스 양의 몸을 떠받친 망연자실한 외국 추방민은 그렇지 못했다. 그는 (소령의 짓궂은 지시를 정확히 따라) 톡스 양의 건강을 정중히 묻기 위해 곧장 위층으로 올라오다가, 마침 딱 그 순간에 우연히 도착하여 그 가냘픈 짐을 두 팔로 받아 들고, 동시에 작은 물뿌리개의 내용물을 제 구두에 받아 내게 되었던 것이다. 이 두 가지 사정이, 혹시라도 실수하면 그의 살갗 속 뼈 하나하나에 늘 하던 벌을 내리겠다고 위협한 노기등등한 소령의 감시를 바짝 받고 있다는 의식과 어우러져, 그를 정신적으로나 육체적으로나 고통받는 움직이는 구경거리로 만들어 놓았다.\n\n이 고통받는 외국인은 몇 순간 동안 톡스 양을 제 가슴에 꼭 끌어안은 채 있었는데, 그 행동의 힘참은 당황한 얼굴과는 놀랄 만큼 대조적이었다. 그러는 동안 그 가엾은 부인은 작은 물뿌리개의 맨 마지막 물방울들을 그의 위로 천천히 흘려보냈으니, 마치 그가 섬세한 외래 식물이라도 되어(사실 그렇기도 했다), 그 부드러운 비가 내리는 동안 거의 꽃이라도 피우리라 기대되는 것 같았다. 마침내 끼어들 만큼 정신을 차린 칙 부인이 그에게 톡스 양을 소파에 내려놓고 물러가라고 명령했고, 그 추방민이 즉각 복종하자, 그녀는 톡스 양을 회복시키는 일에 착수했다.\n\n그러나 이브의 딸들이 서로를 돌볼 때 으레 보이는 그 부드러운 염려는—기절했을 때 그들을 신비로운 자매애의 끈으로 으레 묶어 주는 그 비밀결사의 의리는—칙 부인의 거동에서는 조금도 보이지 않았다. 그보다는, 고문을 진행하기에 앞서 희생자의 감각을 되돌려 놓는 사형 집행인처럼(진정한 사내라면 모두가 영원한 애도의 상복을 입는 그 좋았던 옛 시절에 으레 그러했듯이), 칙 부인은 냄새 맡는 약병이며, 손바닥 때리기며, 얼굴에 찬물 끼얹기며, 그 밖의 입증된 처치들을 시행했다. 그리고 마침내 톡스 양이 눈을 뜨고 차츰 생기와 의식을 되찾았을 때, 칙 부인은 죄인에게서 물러나듯 뒤로 물러나며, 살해당한 덴마크 왕의 선례를 뒤집어, 그녀를 슬픔보다는 노여움으로 바라보았다.\n\n\"루크리셔!\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"내가 느끼는 바를 숨기려 들지 않겠어요. 단번에 내 눈이 뜨였어요. 성인이 나에게 이런 이야기를 해 주었다 해도 나는 믿지 않았을 거예요.\"\n\n\"기절 따위에 굴복하다니 내가 어리석었어요,\" 톡스 양이 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"곧 나아질 거예요.\"\n\n\"곧 나아질 거라고요, 루크리셔!\" 칙 부인이 지독한 경멸을 담아 되풀이했다. \"내가 눈먼 사람인 줄 알아요? 내가 노망이라도 든 줄 알아요? 천만에요, 루크리셔! 신세 한번 잘 졌네요!\"\n\n톡스 양은 애원하는, 어쩔 줄 모르는 눈길을 친구에게 보내고는, 손수건으로 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n\"누군가 어제 나에게 이 이야기를 했다면,\" 칙 부인이 위엄을 담아 말했다. \"아니, 반 시간 전에만 했어도, 나는 거의, 그 사람을 땅바닥에 후려쳐 넘기고 싶은 유혹을 받았을 거예요. 루크리셔 톡스, 당신에 대해 단번에 내 눈이 뜨였어요. 비늘이,\" 여기서 칙 부인은 식료품 가게에서 흔히 쓰는 그런 저울 한 쌍을 떨어뜨리는 시늉을 했다. \"내 시야에서 떨어져 나갔어요. 내 신뢰의 눈멂은 지나갔어요, 루크리셔. 그것은 악용되고 농락당했고, 이제 발뺌은 어림도 없는 일이에요, 정말이지.\"\n\n\"어머! 무얼 그렇게 잔인하게 빗대시는 거예요?\" 톡스 양이 눈물을 흘리며 물었다.\n\n\"루크리셔,\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"당신 자신의 양심에게 물어봐요. 그리고 부탁하건대, 방금 쓴 그런 허물없는 호칭으로 나를 부르지 말아 줘요. 당신은 어떻게 생각할지 몰라도, 나에게는 아직 약간의 자존심이 남아 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"어머나, 루이자!\" 톡스 양이 외쳤다. \"어떻게 나에게 그런 식으로 말할 수 있어요?\"\n\n\"어떻게 당신에게 그런 식으로 말할 수 있느냐고요?\" 칙 부인이 응수했는데, 그녀는 자신을 뒷받침할 딱히 특별한 논거가 없을 때면 가장 신랄한 효과를 내기 위해 주로 이런 되풀이에 기댔다. \"그런 식으로라니! 그래요, 그런 식으로라고 말할 만도 하지요, 정말!\"\n\n톡스 양은 가엾게 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"생각해 봐요!\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"당신이 우리 오라비의 난롯가에 뱀처럼 들러붙어 햇볕을 쬐며, 나를 통해 그이의 신임 속으로 거의 파고들어 갔다는 것을, 그것도 남몰래 그이에게 흑심을 품고, 감히 그이가 당신과 결합할 가능성을 그려 볼 만큼 야심을 부리려고 그랬다는 것을요! 아니, 그건,\" 칙 부인이 빈정대는 위엄을 담아 말했다. \"그 터무니없음이 그 배신을 거의 덜어 줄 지경인 생각이에요.\"\n\n\"제발, 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 호소했다. \"그런 끔찍한 말은 하지 말아요.\"\n\n\"끔찍한 말이라니!\" 칙 부인이 되풀이했다. \"끔찍한 말! 루크리셔, 당신이 방금, 그토록 완전히 눈을 가려 놓았던 나 앞에서조차 감정을 다스리지 못했다는 게 사실이 아닌가요?\"\n\n\"나는 아무 불평도 하지 않았어요,\" 톡스 양이 흐느꼈다. \"아무 말도 하지 않았어요. 만일 내가 당신의 소식에 조금 압도되었고, 돔비 씨가 나에게 각별한 마음을 두는 듯하다는 어떤 미련을 품은 적이 있다 한들, 설마 당신이 나를 나무라지는 않겠지요.\"\n\n\"이 사람이 무슨 말을 하려는지,\" 칙 부인이 체념과 호소가 담긴 포괄적인 눈길로 가구 전체를 향해 말했다. \"이 사람이 무슨 말을 하려는지—나는 알아요—내가 자기를 부추겼다고 말하려는 거예요!\"\n\n\"비난을 주고받고 싶지 않아요, 사랑하는 루이자,\" 톡스 양이 흐느꼈다. \"불평을 하고 싶지도 않고요. 하지만 나 자신을 변호하기 위해서는—\"\n\n\"그래요,\" 칙 부인이 예언자 같은 미소를 지으며 방 안을 둘러보며 외쳤다. \"저게 바로 이 사람이 하려는 말이에요. 내가 알았다니까요. 차라리 말하는 게 낫겠어요. 터놓고 말해 봐요! 솔직해져요, 루크리셔 톡스,\" 칙 부인이 절박한 준엄함을 담아 말했다. \"당신이 어떤 사람이든 간에 말이에요.\"\n\n\"나 자신을 변호하기 위해서,\" 톡스 양이 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"그리고 오직 당신의 매정한 말로부터 나 자신을 변호하기 위해서만, 사랑하는 루이자, 그저 한 가지만 묻고 싶어요. 당신이야말로 그런 공상을 자주 부추기지 않았던가요, 아니, 우리가 알 수 없는 일이니 그런 일이 일어날 수도 있다고 말하기까지 하지 않았던가요?\"\n\n\"한 가지,\" 칙 부인이 일어서며 말했다. 그것은 바닥에서 멈추려는 것이 아니라, 마치 자신이 타고난 하늘로 높이 솟아오르려는 듯한 모습이었다. \"참는 데에도 한도가 있어요, 그 선을 넘으면 참는다는 게 우스꽝스러워지고, 심하면 죄가 되기까지 하지요. 나는 많은 것을 견딜 수 있어요. 하지만 너무 지나친 건 안 돼요. 오늘 이 집에 들어올 때 무슨 마력이 나를 사로잡았는지는 모르겠지만, 나에게는 예감이—어두운 예감이,\" 칙 부인이 몸서리를 치며 말했다. \"무언가 일어나려 한다는 예감이 있었어요. 그런 불길한 예감이 들 만도 했지요, 루크리셔, 수년간의 내 신뢰가 한순간에 무너지고, 단번에 내 눈이 뜨이고, 당신의 본색이 드러난 걸 알게 되었으니 말이에요. 루크리셔, 나는 당신을 잘못 봤어요. 이 이야기는 여기서 끝내는 게 우리 둘 다에게 나아요. 나는 당신이 잘되기를 바라고, 앞으로도 늘 잘되기를 바랄 거예요. 하지만, 자기 자신의 보잘것없는 처지에—그 처지가 무엇이든, 아니든 간에—진실하고자 하는 한 개인으로서, 또 내 오라비의 누이로서, 또 내 오라비 아내의 시누이로서, 또 내 오라비 아내의 어머니의 인척으로서—덧붙이는 걸 허락해 준다면, 돔비 집안의 한 사람으로서—나는 당신에게 그저 안녕히 계시라는 인사밖에는 빌어 줄 게 없어요.\"\n\n도덕적 올바름의 고고한 분위기로 누그러지고 다듬어진 채, 베일 듯한 상냥함으로 내뱉어진 이 말들은, 화자를 문간까지 데려갔다. 거기서 그녀는 유령처럼, 또 조각상처럼 고개를 기울이고는, 자기의 주군인 칙 씨의 품에서 위로와 위안을 구하고자 마차로 물러났다.\n\n비유적으로 말하자면 그렇다는 것이다. 칙 씨의 품은 신문으로 가득 차 있었으니 말이다. 또 이 신사는 자기 아내에게 눈길을 주되 몰래 곁눈질하는 것 말고는 달리 주지 않았다. 또 그는 어떤 위로도 전혀 건네지 않았다. 요컨대 그는 앉아서 신문을 읽고, 노래의 토막토막을 흥얼거리며, 이따금 좋은 말이든 나쁜 말이든 무덤덤한 말이든 한마디도 내뱉지 않고 슬그머니 아내를 흘끔거렸을 뿐이다.\n\n그동안 칙 부인은 잔뜩 부풀어 오른 채, 거드름을 피우며, 머리를 홱홱 젖히고 앉아 있었으니, 마치 루크리셔 톡스에게 그 엄숙한 작별 문구를 아직도 되뇌고 있는 듯했다. 마침내 그녀는 소리 내어 말했다. \"오, 그날 그토록 내 눈이 뜨이게 되다니!\"\n\n\"당신의 눈이 뜨이게 되다니 말이지요, 여보!\" 칙 씨가 되풀이했다.\n\n\"오, 나한테 말 걸지 마세요!\" 칙 부인이 말했다. \"내가 이런 꼴인 걸 보고도 무슨 일이냐고 묻지 못할 사람이라면, 차라리 영원히 입을 다무는 게 나아요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이오, 여보?\" 칙 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"생각해 봐요,\" 칙 부인이 혼잣말하듯이 말했다. \"그 여자가 폴과의 결혼으로 우리 집안과 인연을 맺겠다는 비열한 생각을 다 품다니! 지금은 무덤 속에 있는 그 사랑스러운 아이와 말놀이를 하면서—나는 그때도 그게 영 마음에 들지 않았어요—그런 두 얼굴의 속셈을 숨기고 있었다니! 그 여자에게 무슨 일이 닥치지 않을까 한 번도 두렵지 않았다니 신기하네요. 아무 일도 일어나지 않는다면 운이 좋은 거지요.\"\n\n\"솔직히 말이오, 여보,\" 칙 씨가 신문으로 한참 콧대를 문지르고는 천천히 말했다. \"나는 오늘 아침 전까지는 당신도 줄곧 똑같은 노선을 타고 있었던 줄 알았소. 그 일이 성사될 수만 있다면 그것도 꽤 편리한 일이겠다고 여기고 있었던 줄 알았단 말이오.\"\n\n칙 부인은 즉시 울음을 터뜨리며, 칙 씨에게, 부츠로 자기를 짓밟고 싶거든 차라리 그렇게 하는 게 낫겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 루크리셔 톡스와는 이제 끝이에요,\" 칙 씨를 몹시 떨게 하며 몇 분간 감정에 몸을 내맡긴 끝에 칙 부인이 말했다. \"그럴 자격이 있기를 내가 바라고 또 믿는 어떤 사람을 위해서라면, 폴의 신임을 양보하는 것쯤은 견딜 수 있어요. 폴이 원한다면 가엾은 패니의 자리를 그 사람으로 채울 완벽한 권리가 있는 그 사람을 위해서라면 말이에요. 폴의 그 냉랭한 태도로 자기 계획의 그런 변화를 통고받고, 모든 게 정해지고 결정될 때까지 한 번도 의논받지 못하는 것쯤은 견딜 수 있어요. 하지만 기만은 견딜 수 없고, 그래서 루크리셔 톡스와는 이제 끝이에요. 이대로가 나아요,\" 칙 부인이 경건하게 말했다. \"훨씬 나아요. 이런 일이 있고서 그 여자와 편안하게 잘 어울려 지내려면 한참 걸렸을 거예요. 게다가 폴이 아주 거창해질 참이고, 그쪽은 지체 높은 사람들인데, 그 여자가 과연 사람들 앞에 내놓을 만했을지, 나까지 망신시키지 않았을지 정말 모르겠다니까요. 모든 일에는 섭리가 있어요. 모든 일은 가장 좋은 쪽으로 풀려요. 오늘 나는 시험을 당했지만, 전체적으로 보면 후회하지 않아요.\"\n\n이런 기독교적 정신으로, 칙 부인은 눈물을 닦고 무릎 위를 매만지고는, 큰 부당함 속에서도 평온한 사람답게 앉아 있었다. 칙 씨는 분명 자신의 보잘것없음을 느꼈는지, 일찌감치 어느 길모퉁이에서 내려, 어깨를 잔뜩 추켜올리고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러넣은 채 휘파람을 불며 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n한편 가엾게 파문당한 톡스 양은, 비록 아첨꾼이요 알랑쇠였을지언정, 적어도 정직하고 한결같은 아첨꾼이었으며, 자기를 탄핵한 그 사람에게 늘 충실한 우정을 지녀 왔고, 돔비 씨의 위풍당당함에 대한 헌신에 참으로 빠져들어 송두리째 삼켜져 있었거니와—그 가엾게 파문당한 톡스 양은 눈물로 화초에 물을 주며, 프린세스 플레이스에 겨울이 찾아왔음을 느꼈다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXX. The interval before the Marriage\n\n마법에 걸린 듯하던 그 집은 이제 더는 존재하지 않았고, 일하는 세상이 그 안으로 밀고 들어와 하루 종일 망치질을 하고 부수고 계단을 오르내리며 쿵쿵거렸으니, 디오게네스는 해 뜰 때부터 해 질 때까지 쉴 새 없이 발작하듯 짖어 댔다—제 원수가 마침내 자기를 이겨 먹고는 이제 의기양양하게 도전장을 던지며 집을 약탈하고 있다고 확신하는 게 분명했다—그러나 처음에는 플로렌스의 생활 방식에 그 밖의 큰 변화는 없었다. 밤이 되어 일꾼들이 떠나면, 집은 다시금 음산하고 적막해졌다. 그리고 플로렌스는 그들이 떠나면서 현관과 계단을 통해 메아리치는 목소리에 귀 기울이며, 그들이 돌아갈 정겨운 집들과 그들을 기다리는 아이들을 마음속에 그려 보았고, 그들이 명랑하게, 기꺼이 떠나간다고 생각하니 기뻤다.\n\n플로렌스는 저녁의 고요함을 옛 벗처럼 반겼지만, 이제 그것은 달라진 얼굴로 찾아와 그녀를 한결 다정하게 바라보았다. 그 안에는 새로운 희망이 깃들어 있었다. 그녀의 마음이 그토록 쥐어짜였던 바로 그 방에서 자기를 달래고 어루만져 주었던 그 아름다운 부인은, 그녀에게 약속의 정령과도 같았다. 아버지의 애정을 차츰 얻어 내고, 어머니의 사랑이 어머니의 마지막 숨결과 함께 뺨 위에서 스러져 가던 그 어두운 날에 잃어버린 것을, 전부 혹은 많은 부분 되찾게 될, 동트는 밝은 삶의 부드러운 그림자들이 황혼 속에서 그녀의 둘레를 맴돌며 반가운 벗이 되어 주었다. 이웃의 발그레한 아이들을 살며시 엿보면서, 머지않아 그 아이들과 서로 말을 나누고 알게 될지도 모른다고 생각하는 것은 새롭고 귀한 감각이었다. 그때가 되면 그 아이들이 검은 옷을 입고 홀로 앉아 있는 자기를 보고 슬퍼할까 봐 그들 앞에 나서기를 두려워하던 예전과는 달리, 더는 두려워하지 않으리라!\n\n새 어머니를 생각하면서, 그리고 그분을 향해 순수한 마음에서 흘러넘치는 사랑과 신뢰 속에서, 플로렌스는 세상을 떠난 자기 어머니를 점점 더 사랑하게 되었다. 그녀는 제 가슴속에 경쟁자를 세우는 것이 아닐까 하는 두려움이 없었다. 새 꽃은 깊이 심기고 오래도록 가꾸어 온 그 뿌리에서 돋아난 것임을, 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그 아름다운 부인의 입술에서 떨어진 다정한 말 한 마디 한 마디가 플로렌스에게는 오래전에 잠잠해지고 침묵한 그 목소리의 메아리처럼 들렸다. 그것이 모든 부모의 다정함과 사랑에 대한 그녀의 기억인데, 어찌 살아 있는 다정함 때문에 그 기억을 덜 사랑할 수 있겠는가!\n\n어느 날 플로렌스는 자기 방에서 책을 읽으며 앉아 그 부인과 머지않다고 약속된 방문을 생각하고 있었다—그녀가 읽던 책이 마침 그와 비슷한 주제를 다루고 있었기 때문이다—그런데 눈을 들어 보니, 그 부인이 문간에 서 있는 것이었다.\n\n\"어머니!\" 플로렌스가 기쁘게 맞으며 외쳤다. \"또 오셨네요!\"\n\n\"아직은 어머니가 아니란다.\" 부인이 진지한 미소를 띠며, 한 팔로 플로렌스의 목을 감싸 안으며 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 곧 되실 거잖아요.\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"이제 곧이지, 플로렌스. 아주 곧이야.\"\n\n이디스는 플로렌스의 발그레한 뺨을 제 뺨에 갖다 댈 수 있게끔 고개를 약간 숙였고, 그러고는 몇 분 동안 그렇게 말없이 있었다. 그 태도에 어찌나 다정한 데가 있었던지, 플로렌스는 처음 만났을 때보다도 더 깊이 그것을 느꼈다.\n\n부인은 플로렌스를 제 곁의 의자로 이끌어 앉혔다. 플로렌스는 그 얼굴을 들여다보며 그 아름다움에 못내 경탄하면서, 기꺼이 제 손을 부인의 손안에 맡겨 두었다.\n\n\"플로렌스, 내가 지난번에 다녀간 뒤로 줄곧 혼자 있었니?\"\n\n\"아, 네!\" 플로렌스가 얼른 미소 지었다.\n\n플로렌스는 망설이며 눈을 내리깔았다. 새 어머니의 시선이 무척이나 진지했고, 그 시선이 골똘하고 사려 깊게 자기 얼굴에 박혀 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"저는—저는—혼자 있는 데 익숙해요.\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"전혀 개의치 않아요. 디하고 저는 가끔 온종일 함께 지내기도 해요.\" 플로렌스는 온 몇 주, 온 몇 달이라고 말할 수도 있었으리라.\n\n\"디라는 게 네 하녀니, 얘야?\"\n\n\"제 개예요, 어머니.\" 플로렌스가 웃으며 말했다. \"수전이 제 하녀예요.\"\n\n\"그리고 여기가 네 방이로구나.\" 이디스가 둘러보며 말했다. \"지난번에는 이 방들을 보여 주지 않더구나. 플로렌스, 이 방들을 손봐야겠다. 집에서 가장 예쁜 방으로 만들어 주마.\"\n\n\"이 방들을 바꿀 수 있다면, 어머니,\" 플로렌스가 대답했다, \"위층에 제가 훨씬 더 좋아할 방이 하나 있어요.\"\n\n\"여기는 충분히 높지 않니, 얘야?\" 이디스가 미소 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"그 방은 제 남동생 방이었어요.\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"저는 그 방이 무척 좋아요. 집에 돌아와서 일꾼들이 와 있고 모든 게 바뀌고 있는 걸 보았을 때 아빠께 그 얘기를 드리려 했지만—\"\n\n플로렌스는 그 똑같은 시선이 또다시 자기를 머뭇거리게 할까 봐 눈을 내리깔았다.\n\n\"하지만 아빠가 마음 상하실까 봐 두려웠어요. 그리고 어머니가 곧 다시 오겠다고 하셨고, 또 모든 것의 안주인이시니까, 제가 용기를 내어 어머니께 여쭤 보기로 마음먹었어요.\"\n\n이디스는 그 빛나는 두 눈을 플로렌스의 얼굴에 골똘히 박은 채 그녀를 바라보고 앉아 있다가, 플로렌스가 제 눈을 들자 이번에는 자기가 시선을 거두어 바닥으로 떨어뜨렸다. 그제야 플로렌스는 이 부인의 아름다움이 자기가 짐작했던 것과 얼마나 다른지를 생각했다. 그녀는 그것을 도도하고 고고한 종류의 것이라 여겼었다. 그런데 그 태도가 어찌나 다소곳하고 부드러운지, 만일 이 부인이 플로렌스와 동갑이고 같은 성격이었다 해도 이보다 더 마음을 터놓고 싶게 만들지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n다만 어떤 어색하고 묘한 거리낌이 그녀에게 슬며시 깃들 때만은 예외였다. 그럴 때면 그녀는 (플로렌스로서는 이를 거의 이해하지 못했지만, 알아채고 그에 대해 생각하지 않을 수가 없었다) 마치 플로렌스 앞에서 주눅이 들어 불편해하는 것처럼 보였다. 자기는 아직 어머니가 아니라고 말했을 때, 그리고 플로렌스가 그녀를 거기 모든 것의 안주인이라 불렀을 때, 그녀의 이런 변화는 빠르고 깜짝 놀랄 만한 것이었다. 그리고 지금도, 플로렌스의 눈이 자기 얼굴에 머물러 있는 동안, 그녀는 그토록 가까운 인연을 명분으로 플로렌스를 사랑하고 아끼려는 사람이라기보다는, 오히려 움츠러들어 그녀에게서 숨고 싶어 하는 사람처럼 앉아 있었다.\n\n그녀는 새 방에 대해 플로렌스에게 흔쾌히 약속했고, 그 일은 자기가 직접 지시해 주겠다고 말했다. 그러고는 가엾은 폴에 관해 몇 가지를 물었다. 한동안 대화를 나누며 앉아 있다가, 그녀는 플로렌스를 자기 집으로 데려가려고 왔다고 말했다.\n\n\"이제 어머니와 나는 런던으로 왔단다.\" 이디스가 말했다. \"그러니 내가 혼인할 때까지 우리와 함께 지내자. 플로렌스, 우리가 서로를 알고 믿게 되었으면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n\"제게 정말 잘해 주시네요.\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"친애하는 어머니. 얼마나 감사한지요!\"\n\n\"지금 말해 두마, 이게 가장 좋은 기회일지 모르니.\" 이디스가 둘만 있는지 확인하려고 둘러보고는 더 낮은 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"내가 혼인하고 몇 주 동안 떠나 있게 되면, 네가 이 집으로 돌아와 있어야 마음이 더 놓일 것 같구나. 누가 다른 데 묵으라고 청하든 상관없이, 이 집으로 돌아오너라. 혼자 있는 편이 차라리—내 말은,\" 그녀가 스스로 말을 멈추고 덧붙였다, \"네가 집에 있는 게 가장 좋다는 걸 내가 잘 안다는 거야, 친애하는 플로렌스.\"\n\n\"바로 그날 집으로 돌아올게요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"그래라. 그 약속을 믿으마. 자, 이제 나와 함께 갈 채비를 하렴, 얘야. 준비가 되면 아래층에서 나를 찾으면 된다.\"\n\n이디스는 머지않아 자기가 안주인이 될 그 저택을 홀로 천천히, 생각에 잠겨 거닐었다. 그러면서 그 집이 드러내기 시작한 그 모든 우아함과 화려함에는 거의 눈길도 주지 않았다. 그 길들일 수 없는 영혼의 오만함, 눈과 입술에 어린 그 도도한 경멸, 그 사나운 아름다움이—다만 자기 자신의 보잘것없음과 주위 모든 것의 보잘것없음을 깨달은 마음에 의해서만 누그러진 채—그 웅장한 객실들과 홀들을 누비고 다녔으니, 마치 그늘진 나무들 사이로 풀려나 미쳐 날뛰며 제 몸을 갈가리 찢던 그 무엇과도 같았다. 벽과 바닥의 모조 장미들에는 그녀의 가슴을 찢는 날카로운 가시들이 둘러져 있었고, 눈을 어지럽히는 황금 조각 하나하나에서 그녀는 자기 몸값의 가증스러운 부스러기를 보았다. 넓고 높은 거울들은 본성 안에 아직 고귀한 자질이 머물러 있으나 더 나은 자아에 너무도 거짓되고, 또 너무도 타락하고 길을 잃어 스스로를 구할 수조차 없는 여인의 전신을 그녀에게 비춰 주었다. 그녀는 이 모든 것이, 정도의 차이는 있을지언정, 모든 이의 눈에 그토록 빤히 드러나 있어서, 자기에게는 오만 말고는 어떤 의지처도, 자기를 내세울 힘도 없다고 믿었다. 그리고 밤낮으로 제 마음을 괴롭히는 그 오만으로, 그녀는 제 운명과 끝까지 싸우고, 맞서고, 거역했다.\n\n이 여인이, 순진한 소녀에 지나지 않으며 오직 그 진실함과 소박한 진심에서만 굳센 플로렌스가, 그토록 깊이 감화시키고 압도해서, 그녀 곁에서는 격정의 폭풍이 잠잠해지고 그 오만마저도 길들여진 또 다른 존재가 되게 한 바로 그 여인이란 말인가? 지금 마차 안에서 두 팔을 그녀에게 휘감은 채 그녀 곁에 앉아, 자기를 사랑하고 믿어 달라고 간청하고 졸라 대면서, 그 고운 머리를 끌어다 제 가슴에 기대게 하고, 그것을 어떤 해악이나 위해로부터 지키기 위해서라면 목숨이라도 내놓았을 그 여인이 바로 이 여인이란 말인가?\n\n오, 이디스! 정녕 이런 때에 죽는다면 차라리 나으리라! 어쩌면 끝까지 살아가느니, 이디스, 이렇게 죽는 편이 훨씬 더 낫고 더 행복하리라!\n\n명예로운 스큐턴 부인은 그런 감상과는 전혀 동떨어진 생각을 하고 있었으니—여러 시대에 존재해 온 많은 점잖은 사람들이 그러하듯, 그녀는 죽음이라는 것에 아예 등을 돌렸고, 그런 저속하고 만물을 평준화하는 벼락출세자를 입에 올리는 것조차 못마땅해했다—그리시너 스큐어 광장의 브룩 가에 있는 집 한 채를, 시내에 없는 어느 위엄 있는 친척(피닉스 가문의 한 핏줄)에게서 빌려 왔다. 그 친척은 더없이 후한 태도로 혼례를 위해 집을 빌려 주는 데 반대하지 않았는데, 이 대여가 곧 스큐턴 부인과 그 딸에게 앞으로 일체의 대여나 증여를 더는 하지 않아도 된다는, 그의 최종적인 해방과 면제를 뜻했기 때문이다. 이런 때에 가문의 체면을 위해 번듯한 모양새를 갖출 필요가 있었으므로, 스큐턴 부인은 메릴번 교구에 거주하면서 은제 식기 한 벌에서 하인 한 부대에 이르기까지 온갖 물품을 귀족과 신사 계급에게 빌려 주는 한 편의적인 상인의 도움을 받아, 이 집에 은빛 머리의 집사 한 명(고풍스러운 가문의 늙은 하인 같은 풍채라 하여 그만큼 추가 요금이 붙었다)과 제복 차림의 키 큰 젊은이 둘, 그리고 엄선된 주방 하인들을 들여놓았다. 그래서 아래층에서는 이런 전설이 생겨났다. 시동 위더스가, 그 수많은 집안일과 (대도시에는 어울리지 않는) 바퀴 의자 미는 일에서 단번에 풀려난 나머지, 자기가 리밍턴 우유 장수 집에서 늦잠을 자다가 아직도 천상의 꿈속에 있는 게 아닌가 의심하기라도 하듯, 여러 번 눈을 비비고 팔다리를 꼬집는 모습이 목격되었다는 것이다. 은제품과 도자기의 갖가지 필수품들도 같은 편리한 출처로부터 같은 살림집으로 운반되어 왔고, 그 밖에 말끔한 사륜마차 한 대와 밤색 말 두 필을 포함한 여러 잡다한 물품들도 함께 들여왔으니, 스큐턴 부인은 주된 소파에 클레오파트라 자세로 몸을 기대고 점잖은 위풍 속에서 자기 궁정을 열었다.\n\n\"그래, 우리 매력적인 플로렌스는,\" 딸과 그녀가 맡은 아이가 들어오자 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"어떻게 지내니? 이리 와서 내게 입 맞춰 주렴, 플로렌스, 미안하지만, 얘야.\"\n\n플로렌스가 스큐턴 부인 얼굴의 흰 부분에서 입 맞출 자리를 고르려고 머뭇머뭇 몸을 굽히는데, 그 부인이 귀를 내밀어 그녀의 곤혹스러움을 덜어 주었다.\n\n\"이디스, 얘야,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"정말이지 나는—잠깐만 빛이 좀 더 드는 쪽에 서 보렴, 우리 사랑스러운 플로렌스, 잠깐이면 된다.\"\n\n플로렌스는 얼굴을 붉히며 그 말에 따랐다.\n\n\"기억나지 않니, 사랑하는 이디스,\" 그녀의 어머니가 말했다, \"네가 우리 더없이 소중한 플로렌스만 한 나이였을 때, 아니면 몇 살 더 어렸을 때 어떤 모습이었는지?\"\n\n\"오래전에 잊어버렸어요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 정말로, 얘야,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"우리의 더없이 매혹적인 어린 벗에게서, 그때 네 모습과 뚜렷이 닮은 데가 보이는 것 같거든. 그리고 그건,\" 스큐턴 부인이 더 낮은 목소리로 말했는데, 그 목소리는 플로렌스가 아직 한참 다듬어지지 않은 상태라는 자기 견해를 담고 있었다, \"교양이 무엇을 해낼 수 있는지를 보여 주는 거란다.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇네요.\" 이디스의 매정한 대답이었다.\n\n그녀의 어머니는 잠시 딸을 날카롭게 쳐다보다가, 자기가 위태로운 땅에 서 있음을 느끼고는 화제를 돌리려고 말했다.\n\n\"우리 매력적인 플로렌스, 한 번 더 와서 내게 입 맞춰 주렴, 미안하지만, 얘야.\"\n\n플로렌스는 물론 그 말을 따랐고, 또다시 스큐턴 부인의 귀에 입술을 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"그리고 너도 분명 들었겠지, 우리 귀여운 아가,\" 스큐턴 부인이 그녀의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다, \"우리 모두가 더없이 흠모하고 애지중지하는 네 아빠가, 이날로 일주일 뒤에 내 더없이 사랑하는 이디스와 혼인하시리라는 걸 말이다.\"\n\n\"아주 곧이라는 건 알았어요.\" 플로렌스가 대답했다, \"하지만 정확히 언제인지는 몰랐어요.\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 이디스,\" 그녀의 어머니가 명랑하게 다그쳤다, \"설마 네가 플로렌스에게 말해 주지 않았다는 거니?\"\n\n\"제가 왜 플로렌스에게 말해야 하죠?\" 그녀가 어찌나 갑작스럽고 매몰차게 받아쳤던지, 플로렌스는 그것이 같은 목소리라고는 거의 믿을 수 없었다.\n\n그러자 스큐턴 부인은 또 다른, 더 안전한 화제로 돌리려고, 플로렌스에게 그녀의 아버지가 저녁 식사에 올 것이며, 그녀를 보면 분명 기분 좋게 깜짝 놀랄 거라고 말했다. 그가 어젯밤 시티에서 옷을 차려입겠다고 말한 데다 이디스의 계획을 전혀 모르고 있었으니, 스큐턴 부인의 예상으로는 그 계획의 실행이 그를 완전한 황홀경에 빠뜨리리라는 것이었다. 플로렌스는 이 말을 듣고 괴로웠다. 그리고 저녁 식사 시간이 다가올수록 그 괴로움이 어찌나 사무쳐 갔던지, 만일 자기 설명에 아버지를 끌어들이지 않으면서 집으로 돌아가게 해 달라고 간청하는 방법을 알았더라면, 그의 노여움을 살 위험을 무릅쓰느니 차라리 모자도 쓰지 않고 숨 가쁘게 홀로 걸어서라도 급히 돌아갔으리라.\n\n시간이 가까워질수록 그녀는 거의 숨을 쉴 수 없었다. 그녀는 창가에 다가갈 엄두를 내지 못했으니, 그가 길에서 자기를 볼까 두려웠던 것이다. 위층으로 올라가 자기 감정을 숨길 엄두도 내지 못했으니, 문으로 나가다가 뜻하지 않게 그와 마주칠까 두려웠던 것이다. 게다가 그 두려움에 더하여, 만일 그의 앞으로 불려 가게 된다면 다시는 돌아오지 못할 것만 같은 느낌이 들었다. 이런 두려움의 갈등 속에서, 그녀는 클레오파트라의 긴 의자 곁에 앉아 그 부인의 시시한 이야기를 알아듣고 대답해 보려 애쓰고 있다가, 계단에 울리는 그의 발소리를 들었다.\n\n\"이제 들려요!\" 플로렌스가 화들짝 놀라며 외쳤다. \"오고 계세요!\"\n\n클레오파트라는 그 젊은 척하는 기질로 늘 장난기 어린 태도였고, 또 제 일에만 골몰해 있어서 이 동요가 어떤 성질의 것인지 굳이 신경 쓰지 않은 채, 돔비 씨에게 황홀한 놀라움을 안겨 줄 채비로 플로렌스를 자기 긴 의자 뒤로 밀어 넣고 그 위에 숄을 덮어 버렸다. 어찌나 잽싸게 해치웠던지, 순식간에 플로렌스는 방 안에 울리는 그의 무시무시한 발소리를 들었다.\n\n그는 장차 장모가 될 사람과 장차 신부가 될 사람에게 인사를 건넸다. 그 목소리의 낯선 울림이 그의 아이의 온몸을 전율케 했다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\" 클레오파트라가 말했다, \"이리 와서 당신의 예쁜 플로렌스가 어떻게 지내는지 말해 줘요.\"\n\n\"플로렌스는 아주 잘 지냅니다.\" 돔비 씨가 긴 의자 쪽으로 다가서며 말했다.\n\n\"집에서요?\"\n\n\"집에서요.\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\" 클레오파트라가 사람을 흘리는 활기를 띠며 받았다, \"자, 정말 나를 속이는 게 아니라고 장담할 수 있어요? 내가 이런 단언을 하면 우리 더없이 사랑하는 이디스가 내게 뭐라고 할지 모르겠지만, 명예를 걸고 말하건대 당신이 남자들 가운데 가장 거짓된 사람일까 봐 두렵네요, 내 사랑하는 돔비.\"\n\n설령 그가 정말로 그러했고, 일찍이 입에 담거나 저질러진 적 없는 가장 엄청난 거짓말을 그 자리에서 들켰다 한들, 스큐턴 부인이 숄을 홱 걷어 내고 플로렌스가 창백하게 떨면서 유령처럼 그의 앞에 일어섰을 때만큼 당황하지는 못했을 것이다. 그가 미처 침착을 되찾기도 전에, 플로렌스는 그에게 달려와 두 손을 그의 목에 둘러 감고 그의 얼굴에 입 맞추고는, 황급히 방을 나가 버렸다. 그는 이 일을 누군가 다른 사람에게 물어보기라도 하려는 듯 둘러보았지만, 이디스는 이미 플로렌스를 따라 나가 버린 뒤였다.\n\n\"자, 인정해요, 내 사랑하는 돔비,\" 스큐턴 부인이 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다, \"평생 이보다 더 놀라고 기뻤던 적이 없다고 말이에요.\"\n\n\"이보다 더 놀란 적은 없었습니다.\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"더 기뻤던 적도 없죠, 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비?\" 스큐턴 부인이 부채를 치켜들며 받았다.\n\n\"나는—그렇습니다, 플로렌스를 여기서 만나서 대단히 기쁩니다.\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. 그는 잠시 그것에 대해 진지하게 따져 보는 듯하더니, 한결 단호하게 말했다, \"그래요, 플로렌스를 여기서 만나니 정말이지 무척 기쁩니다.\"\n\n\"저 애가 여기 어떻게 왔는지 궁금하죠?\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"안 그래요?\"\n\n\"아마도 이디스가—\" 돔비 씨가 넌지시 말했다.\n\n\"아! 짓궂은 추측쟁이 같으니!\" 클레오파트라가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"아! 약은, 약은 양반 같으니라고! 이런 건 말하면 안 되는 거예요. 당신네 남자들은, 내 사랑하는 돔비, 어찌나 허영심이 많고 우리 여자들의 약점을 잘도 이용해 먹는지. 하지만 당신은 내 솔직한 영혼을 알잖아요—아주 좋아요, 곧 갑니다.\"\n\n이 말은 저녁 식사가 준비되었음을 알리는 그 키 큰 젊은이들 가운데 하나에게 한 것이었다.\n\n\"하지만 이디스는, 내 사랑하는 돔비,\" 그녀가 속삭이며 말을 이었다, \"당신을 곁에 둘 수 없을 때면—내가 늘 그 애에게 말하듯, 그걸 늘 기대할 수는 없는 노릇이지요—적어도 당신에게 속한 무언가나 누군가를 곁에 두려 한답니다. 자, 그게 얼마나 지극히 자연스러운 일인가요! 그래서 이런 마음으로, 그 무엇도 그 애가 오늘 우리의 사랑스러운 플로렌스를 데리러 마차를 몰고 나가는 걸 막을 수 없었던 거예요. 자, 그게 얼마나 더없이 사랑스러운 일인가요!\"\n\n그녀가 대답을 기다리자, 돔비 씨가 대답했다, \"지극히 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"당신에게 축복을, 내 사랑하는 돔비, 그토록 진심을 보여 주다니!\" 클레오파트라가 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며 외쳤다. \"하지만 내가 너무 심각해지고 있군요! 천사처럼 나를 아래층으로 데려가서, 이 사람들이 저녁으로 우리에게 뭘 내올 작정인지 봅시다. 당신에게 축복을, 사랑하는 돔비!\"\n\n마지막 축복을 마친 뒤, 클레오파트라가 제법 날렵하게 긴 의자에서 폴짝 내려서자, 돔비 씨는 그녀의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼고 격식을 차려 아래층으로 안내했다. 그 두 사람이 식당으로 접어드는데, 고용된 키 큰 젊은이들 가운데 하나, 공경심이라는 기관이 덜 발달한 그자가, 또 다른 고용된 키 큰 젊은이를 즐겁게 해 주려고 혀로 제 볼을 불룩하게 밀어 보였다.\n\n플로렌스와 이디스는 이미 거기 와서 나란히 앉아 있었다. 플로렌스는 아버지가 들어오자 일어나 자기 의자를 그에게 양보하려 했지만, 이디스가 대놓고 그녀의 팔에 손을 얹었고, 돔비 씨는 둥근 식탁의 맞은편 자리에 앉았다.\n\n대화는 거의 전적으로 스큐턴 부인이 이끌었다. 플로렌스는 눈물 자국이 드러날까 봐 감히 눈을 들지도 못했고, 말을 할 엄두는 더더욱 내지 못했다. 그리고 이디스는 질문에 대답할 때를 빼고는 한 마디도 입에 올리지 않았다. 참으로 클레오파트라는, 거의 손아귀에 넣은 그 가문을 위하여 부지런히 애를 썼다. 그리고 참으로 그 가문은, 그녀에게 보답하기에 충분히 풍족한 것이어야 마땅했으리라!\n\n\"그래서 당신의 준비도 이제 거의 다 끝났나요, 내 사랑하는 돔비?\" 후식이 식탁에 올라오고 은빛 머리의 집사가 물러간 뒤, 클레오파트라가 말했다. \"변호사들의 준비까지도요!\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 부인.\" 돔비 씨가 대답했다. \"재산 양도 증서는, 그 전문가 양반들이 알려 주기로, 이제 준비가 되었답니다. 그리고 말씀드렸다시피, 이디스가 그것을 작성할 날짜를 직접 정해 주는 호의만 베풀어 주면 됩니다.\"\n\n이디스는 아름다운 조각상처럼 앉아 있었다. 그만큼 차갑게, 그만큼 말없이, 그만큼 미동도 없이.\n\n\"내 더없이 사랑하는 얘야,\" 클레오파트라가 말했다, \"돔비 씨가 하는 말 들리니? 아, 내 사랑하는 돔비!\" 그 신사에게 곁말로, \"시간이 다가올수록 저 애가 저렇게 멍하니 있는 모습이, 저 애의 더없이 유쾌한 아빠가 당신과 같은 처지에 있던 그 시절을 어찌나 떠오르게 하는지요!\"\n\n\"저는 정할 게 없어요. 당신이 좋으실 때로 하세요.\" 이디스가 식탁 너머 돔비 씨를 거의 쳐다보지도 않고 말했다.\n\n\"내일은요?\" 돔비 씨가 넌지시 말했다.\n\n\"좋으실 대로요.\"\n\n\"아니면 그다음 날이,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"당신의 일정에 더 맞을까요?\"\n\n\"저는 일정이 없어요. 저는 늘 당신 뜻대로 따를 수 있습니다. 당신이 원하실 때로 하세요.\"\n\n\"일정이 없다니, 내 사랑하는 이디스!\" 그녀의 어머니가 항변했다, \"온종일 더없이 정신없이 분주한 상태에다, 온갖 상인들과 천 가지 만 가지 약속이 잡혀 있으면서!\"\n\n\"그건 어머니가 만드신 거예요.\" 이디스가 살짝 미간을 찌푸리며 어머니에게 돌아서서 받았다. \"어머니랑 돔비 씨랑 둘이서 정하시면 되잖아요.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇구나, 얘야, 참으로 사려 깊기도 하지!\" 클레오파트라가 말했다. \"우리 사랑하는 플로렌스, 한 번만 더 와서 내게 입 맞춰 주렴, 미안하지만, 얘야!\"\n\n이상한 우연이지, 플로렌스에 대한 이런 관심의 분출이 클레오파트라를 이디스가 끼어 있는 거의 모든 대화에서, 그것이 아무리 사소한 것이라도, 황급히 빠져나오게 하다니! 플로렌스는 분명 평생 이토록 많은 포옹을 받아 본 적이 없었고, 또 아마 자기도 모르는 사이에 이토록 쓸모 있어진 적도 없었으리라.\n\n돔비 씨는 자기 아름다운 약혼녀의 태도에 대해 마음속으로 조금도 트집을 잡지 않았다. 그는 오만함과 차가움에 공감할 만한 그 충분한 이유를 지니고 있었으니, 그것은 동류의식에서 비롯된 것이었다. 이디스의 경우, 이런 것들이 자기에게 고개를 숙이고 자기와 별개의 의지란 없는 듯이 보인다고 생각하니 우쭐했다. 이 도도하고 위엄 있는 여인이 자기 집안의 접대를 맡아, 자기 방식 그대로 자기 손님들을 싸늘하게 대하는 모습을 상상하니 우쭐했다. 돔비와 아들의 위엄은, 정녕 그런 손에 의해 더욱 드높여지고 지켜질 것이었다.\n\n돔비 씨는 식탁에 혼자 남아, 자기의 지난날과 앞날의 운수를 곰곰이 헤아리며 그렇게 생각했다. 그러면서 그는 그 방을 감도는, 인색하고 음울한 위세의 분위기에서 자기와 어긋나는 데를 조금도 느끼지 못했다. 짙은 갈색을 띤 그 방은, 벽에 검은 문장(紋章) 그림들이 얼룩처럼 박혀 있었고, 검은 의자 스물네 개가, 관(棺)에 박힌 만큼이나 많은 못이 박힌 채, 마치 장례 조객처럼 터키 양탄자의 문턱에서 대기하고 있었다. 그리고 찬장 위에는 지친 흑인상(像) 둘이 시들어 빠진 촛대 가지 둘을 떠받치고 있었고, 마치 만 끼니 식사의 재가 그 아래 석관에 매장되기라도 한 듯 곰팡내가 진동했다. 그 집 주인은 외국에서 지내는 일이 많았다. 잉글랜드의 공기는 피닉스 가문 사람에게 오래도록 잘 맞는 일이 드물었던 것이다. 그래서 그 방은 그를 위해 차츰 더 깊고 더 깊은 상복을 차려입어, 마침내 어찌나 장례식 같아졌던지 완전무결해지는 데에 시신 하나만이 모자랄 뿐이었다.\n\n당장은, 자세는 아닐지언정 그 뻣뻣한 형체로 보아 시신의 표상으로 그리 나쁘지 않은 돔비 씨가, 과일 접시들과 유리병들이 닻을 내리고 있는 그 죽은 마호가니 바다의 차가운 심연을 내려다보았다. 마치 그의 상념의 대상들이 하나씩 하나씩 수면 위로 떠올랐다가 다시 가라앉기라도 하는 듯이. 이디스가 그 이마와 자태의 온갖 위엄을 갖춘 채 거기 있었다. 그리고 그녀 가까이로, 방을 나설 때 한순간 그러했던 것처럼 겁먹은 고개를 그에게 돌린 플로렌스가 다가왔다. 그리고 이디스의 눈이 그녀에게 머물러 있었고, 이디스의 손이 보호하듯 뻗쳐 있었다. 다음으로는 낮은 안락의자에 앉은 자그마한 형체 하나가 빛 속으로 튀어 오르더니, 그 초롱초롱한 눈과 늙은-어린 얼굴로, 마치 저녁 불빛이 일렁이는 가운데서처럼 반짝이며 그를 의아한 듯 바라보았다. 다시 플로렌스가 그것에 바짝 다가와 그의 온 관심을 사로잡았다. 그녀가 그에게 미리 정해진 골칫거리이자 실망거리로서인지, 자기 길을 가로막았고 또다시 가로막을지 모르는 경쟁자로서인지, 혹은 성공적인 구애를 마친 마당에 이런 때에 더는 멀어지지 않기를 요구할 만한 자기 자식으로서 너그러이 생각해 줄 만한 존재로서인지, 아니면 자기의 새로운 관계 속에서 적어도 제 핏줄을 위하는 시늉만이라도 유지해야 한다는 암시로서인지—그것은 그 자신이 가장 잘 알았다. 어쩌면 기껏해야 어중간하게나 알았을 것이다. 혼례 하객들과 혼례 제단들과 야심 찬 장면들이—여전히 여기저기 플로렌스로, 언제나 플로렌스로 얼룩진 채—어찌나 빠르게, 또 어찌나 어지러이 떠오르던지, 그는 그것들에게서 벗어나려고 자리에서 일어나 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n촛불을 들여올 때쯤에는 밤이 꽤 깊어 있었다. 지금은 촛불이 머리를 아프게 한다고 스큐턴 부인이 투덜댔기 때문이다. 그러는 동안 플로렌스와 스큐턴 부인은 함께 이야기를 나누었고(클레오파트라가 그녀를 바짝 자기 곁에 두고 싶어 안달했으므로), 혹은 플로렌스가 스큐턴 부인을 즐겁게 해 주려고 피아노를 가만히 어루만지기도 했다. 그 저녁 내내 그 다정한 부인이 한 번 더 입맞춤을 졸라 대지 않을 수 없었던 몇 번의 경우는 말할 것도 없는데, 그것은 늘 이디스가 무슨 말이든 한 뒤에 일어났다. 그러나 그런 경우가 많지는 않았으니, 이디스가 (감기에 걸릴까 봐 걱정하는 어머니의 우려에도 아랑곳없이) 줄곧 열린 창가에 따로 떨어져 앉아, 돔비 씨가 작별 인사를 할 때까지 거기 머물러 있었기 때문이다. 그는 작별할 때 플로렌스에게 차분하고 정중했다. 그리고 플로렌스는 이디스의 방 안쪽에 딸린 방에서 잠자리에 들었는데, 어찌나 행복하고 희망에 차 있었던지, 자기의 지난 모습을 마치 슬픔 때문에 가엾이 여겨야 할 어떤 다른 가련하고 버림받은 소녀인 양 떠올렸고, 그 가엾음 속에서 흐느끼다 잠들었다.\n\n그 한 주는 빠르게 흘러갔다. 모자 가게며 양장점이며 보석상이며 변호사며 꽃집이며 제과점으로 가는 마차 행차가 있었고, 플로렌스는 늘 그 일행에 끼어 있었다. 플로렌스는 결혼식에 가기로 되어 있었다. 플로렌스는 상복을 벗고 그날을 위해 화려한 드레스를 입기로 되어 있었다. 이 드레스에 관한 모자 장수의 구상은—그 모자 장수는 프랑스 여자였고, 스큐턴 부인과 무척 닮았다—어찌나 고상하고 우아했던지, 스큐턴 부인이 그것과 똑같은 것을 자기 것으로 주문했다. 모자 장수는 그것이 부인에게 감탄스러울 만큼 잘 어울릴 것이며, 온 세상이 그녀를 그 아가씨의 언니로 여길 거라고 말했다.\n\n그 한 주는 더 빠르게 흘러갔다. 이디스는 아무것도 쳐다보지 않았고 아무것도 마음에 두지 않았다. 그녀의 호사스러운 드레스들이 집으로 배달되어 와서, 입어 보았고, 스큐턴 부인과 모자 장수들에게서 요란한 칭찬을 받았고, 그녀에게서는 한 마디 말도 없이 치워졌다. 스큐턴 부인은 날마다의 계획을 세웠고 그것을 실행에 옮겼다. 물건을 사러 갈 때면 이디스는 더러 마차 안에 앉아 있었고, 더러 꼭 필요할 때면 가게 안으로 들어가기도 했다. 그러나 무슨 일이든 그 전체를 처리한 것은 스큐턴 부인이었다. 그리고 이디스는 마치 자기와는 아무 상관도 없다는 듯, 흥미라곤 없이, 겉보기에 그토록 무관심하게 지켜보고만 있었다. 플로렌스는 어쩌면 그녀가 오만하고 무기력하다고 여겼을지도 모르나, 자기에게는 결코 그러는 법이 없었다. 그래서 플로렌스는 의아함이 고개를 들 때마다 그것을 감사함으로 잠재웠고, 곧 가라앉혔다.\n\n그 한 주는 더 빠르게 흘러갔다. 그 주는 거의 날아가 버릴 듯했다. 그 주의 마지막 밤, 곧 혼례 전날 밤이 왔다. 어두운 방 안에는—스큐턴 부인의 머리는 아직 나아지지 않았으나, 내일이면 완전히 회복하리라 기대하고 있었다—그 부인과 이디스와 돔비 씨가 있었다. 이디스는 열린 창가에서 거리를 내다보고 있었고, 돔비 씨와 클레오파트라는 소파에서 나직이 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 밤이 깊어 가고 있었다. 그리고 플로렌스는 지친 나머지 잠자리에 든 뒤였다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\" 클레오파트라가 말했다, \"내일 내 더없이 사랑하는 이디스를 내게서 앗아 갈 때, 플로렌스는 내게 남겨 두고 가겠죠.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 기꺼이 그러겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"두 사람이 다 파리에 가 있는 동안 저 애를 여기 내 곁에 두고서, 저 애 나이에 내가 그 마음을 빚어 내는 일을 거들고 있다고 생각하면, 내 사랑하는 돔비,\" 클레오파트라가 말했다, \"내가 빠지게 될 그 극도로 산산조각 난 상태에 더없는 위안이 될 거예요.\"\n\n이디스가 불쑥 고개를 돌렸다. 그 무기력한 태도가 한순간에 불타는 듯한 관심의 태도로 바뀌었고, 어둠 속에 가려진 채 그녀는 두 사람의 대화에 바짝 귀를 기울였다.\n\n돔비 씨는 플로렌스를 그토록 훌륭한 보호 아래 맡겨 두게 되어 기쁘기 그지없다고 했다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\" 클레오파트라가 받았다, \"그 좋은 평가에 천 번 감사드려요. 나는 당신이, 그 끔찍한 변호사들 말마따나—그 지긋지긋한 수다쟁이들 같으니!—'미리 작심한 악의를 품고서', 나를 완전한 고독에 처하게 하려는 줄 알고 두려웠답니다.\"\n\n\"어찌하여 나를 그토록 부당하게 보십니까, 부인?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 매력적인 플로렌스가 내일 꼭 집에 가야 한다고 어찌나 단호하게 말하던지,\" 클레오파트라가 받았다, \"내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비, 나는 당신이 완전히 폭군인가 보다 하고 두려워지기 시작했거든요.\"\n\n\"분명히 말씀드립니다, 부인!\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"나는 플로렌스에게 아무런 명령도 내린 적이 없습니다. 그리고 설령 내렸다 한들, 부인의 바람만 한 명령은 없지요.\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\" 클레오파트라가 대답했다, \"당신은 어쩌면 그렇게 아첨꾼인지! 하지만 그렇게 말하지는 않겠어요. 아첨꾼에게는 마음이 없는 법인데, 당신의 마음은 당신의 일하는 삶과 인품에 두루 배어 있으니까요. 그래, 정말 이렇게 일찍 가려는 거예요, 내 사랑하는 돔비!\"\n\n아, 그렇다마다요! 늦은 시각이었고, 돔비 씨는 가야만 할 것 같았다.\n\n\"이게 사실인가요, 아니면 다 꿈인가요!\" 클레오파트라가 혀짤배기소리로 말했다. \"믿을 수 있을까요, 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비, 당신이 내일 아침에 다시 와서 내 사랑스러운 벗, 나만의 이디스를 내게서 앗아 가리라는 것을!\"\n\n만사를 곧이곧대로 받아들이는 데 익숙한 돔비 씨는, 스큐턴 부인에게 두 사람이 먼저 교회에서 만나기로 했음을 일깨워 주었다.\n\n\"자식을, 비록 당신에게라 해도, 내 사랑하는 돔비, 내주어야 하는 그 고통은,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"상상할 수 있는 가장 가슴 에이는 일 가운데 하나예요. 게다가 타고나기를 섬약한 체질인 데다, 아침 식사를 맡은 제과점 주인의 극심한 우둔함까지 겹치면, 내 가련한 기력으로는 거의 감당하기 어려울 지경이랍니다. 하지만 아침이 되면 기운을 차릴 거예요, 내 사랑하는 돔비. 나에 대해서는 염려 말고, 나 때문에 마음 쓰지 마세요. 당신에게 하늘의 축복을! 내 더없이 사랑하는 이디스!\" 그녀가 짓궂게 외쳤다. \"누가 가려는구나, 얘야.\"\n\n다시 창문 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸고 두 사람의 대화에 대한 관심이 식어 버린 이디스는, 제자리에서 일어섰지만 그에게로 다가가지도, 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 돔비 씨는 자기 위엄과 그 자리에 걸맞은 고고한 정중함으로, 삐걱거리는 부츠를 끌고 그녀에게로 가서, 그녀의 손을 입술에 갖다 대고는, \"내일 아침이면 이 손을 돔비 부인의 손으로서 차지하는 행복을 누리겠군요\" 하고 말한 뒤, 엄숙하게 절하고 물러났다.\n\n스큐턴 부인은 현관문이 그의 뒤로 닫히자마자 촛불을 가져오라고 종을 울렸다. 촛불과 함께 그녀의 하녀가, 내일 온 세상을 속일 그 젊어 보이는 드레스를 들고 나타났다. 그 드레스에는, 그런 드레스가 으레 그렇듯, 잔인한 응보가 깃들어 있어서, 그녀를 기름때 낀 플란넬 잠옷보다 한없이 더 늙고 더 흉하게 만들었다. 그러나 스큐턴 부인은 새침한 만족감으로 그것을 입어 보았다. 그것이 소령에게 미칠 죽여주는 효과를 떠올리며, 거울 속 자기의 송장 같은 모습을 향해 히죽거렸다. 그러고는 하녀가 다시 그것을 벗기고 잠자리에 들 채비를 해 주게 한 뒤, 그림 그려진 카드로 지은 집처럼 폭삭 무너져 내렸다.\n\n이 모든 동안 이디스는 어두운 창가에 남아 거리를 내다보고 있었다. 그녀와 어머니가 마침내 단둘이 남게 되자, 그녀는 그 저녁 들어 처음으로 창가에서 물러나 어머니 맞은편으로 와 섰다. 하품하고 떨고 짜증스러운 어머니의 형체가, 자기를 마주 보려고 눈을 치켜뜬 채로, 불꽃 같은 시선을 아래로 떨구고 있는 딸의 오만하고 꼿꼿한 자태와 마주하니, 그 위에는 어떤 경박함이나 성마름으로도 감출 수 없는 켕기는 기색이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"나는 죽도록 지쳤다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"너는 한순간도 믿고 맡길 수가 없어. 어린애보다도 못해. 어린애라니! 어떤 어린애도 반만큼이라도 이렇게 고집스럽고 도리를 모르지는 않을 거다.\"\n\n\"제 말 들으세요, 어머니.\" 이디스가, 이런 말들과 시시한 입씨름을 하기에는 격이 떨어진다는 듯한 경멸로 그 말들을 흘려보내며 받았다. \"제가 돌아올 때까지 어머니는 여기 혼자 계셔야 해요.\"\n\n\"내가 돌아올 때까지 여기 혼자 있어야 한다니, 이디스!\" 그녀의 어머니가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"안 그러면, 내일 제가 그토록 거짓되이, 그토록 부끄럽게 행하는 것을 지켜보라고 불러낼 그 이름을 걸고 맹세컨대, 저는 교회에서 이 남자의 손을 거절하겠어요. 그러지 않으면, 제가 포석 위에 쓰러져 죽기를!\"\n\n어머니는 다급한 경악의 눈빛으로 대꾸했고, 그 눈빛은 자기가 마주한 눈빛 때문에 조금도 누그러지지 않았다.\n\n\"우리가 우리 같은 존재라는 것으로 족해요.\" 이디스가 단호하게 말했다. \"저는 어떤 젊음과 진실함도 제 수준으로 끌어내리지 않을 거예요. 어떤 순진무구한 천성도, 어미들로 가득 찬 이 세상의 한가한 소일거리를 위해, 좀먹히고 더럽혀지고 비틀어지게 두지 않겠어요. 제 말뜻을 아시잖아요. 플로렌스는 집으로 가야 해요.\"\n\n\"너는 바보로구나, 이디스.\" 그녀의 화난 어머니가 외쳤다. \"저 애가 시집가서 떠나기 전에는, 저 집에서 너한테 평화란 게 있을 성싶으냐?\"\n\n\"제가 저 집에서 평화를 바라기나 하는지, 저에게든 어머니 자신에게든 물어보세요.\" 딸이 말했다. \"그러면 답을 아실 거예요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 온갖 수고와 노고를 다 들이고, 또 네가 나를 통해 독립된 처지가 되려는 마당에, 오늘 밤 내가 이런 소리를 들어야 한단 말이냐,\" 그녀의 어머니가 격정에 차 거의 비명을 지르다시피 했고, 그러는 동안 그 중풍 든 머리가 나뭇잎처럼 떨렸다, \"내 안에 부패와 전염병이 있어서, 내가 한 소녀에게 어울릴 만한 동무가 못 된다는 소리를! 그럼 도대체 너는 뭐냐? 너는 뭐냔 말이다?\"\n\n\"저는 그 물음을 제 자신에게 던져 봤어요.\" 이디스가, 잿빛으로 창백해진 채 창문을 가리키며 말했다, \"저기 앉아 있을 때, 그리고 빛바랜 내 성(性)의 모습을 한 무언가가 바깥을 지나쳐 갈 때, 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번 던져 봤어요. 그리고 하느님은 아시지요, 제가 어떤 답을 만났는지를. 오 어머니, 어머니, 저도 한낱 소녀였을 때—플로렌스보다 더 어린 소녀였을 때—저를 제 본래의 마음에 그저 내버려 두기만 하셨더라면, 저는 얼마나 달라졌을까요!\"\n\n이 자리에서는 어떤 노여움의 표시도 소용없음을 깨달은 그녀의 어머니는, 스스로를 억누르고 훌쩍훌쩍 울기 시작하더니, 자기가 너무 오래 살았다고, 하나뿐인 자식이 자기를 내쳤다고, 부모에 대한 도리란 이 악한 시절에는 잊혀 버렸다고, 자기가 인륜에 어긋나는 빈정거림을 들었으니 더는 사는 데 미련이 없다고 한탄했다.\n\n\"이런 일을 끊임없이 겪으며 계속 살아가야 한다면,\" 그녀가 칭얼거렸다, \"차라리 내 목숨을 끊을 무슨 방도를 궁리하는 편이 훨씬 나을 거야. 오! 네가 내 딸이라는 생각이, 이디스, 그러고도 내게 그런 투로 말을 하다니!\"\n\n\"우리 사이에는, 어머니,\" 이디스가 애처롭게 받았다, \"서로를 책망할 때는 지났어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 그걸 다시 들춰내는 거냐?\" 그녀의 어머니가 훌쩍였다. \"네가 더없이 잔인하게 나를 갈가리 찢고 있는 걸 너도 알잖니. 내가 매정한 소리에 얼마나 예민한지도 알면서. 게다가 이런 때에, 생각할 게 그토록 많고, 또 당연히 가장 좋은 모습으로 보이고 싶어 안달인 이런 때에! 너한테 기가 막힐 따름이다, 이디스. 네 결혼식 날에 네 어미를 흉물로 만들다니!\"\n\n이디스는, 그녀가 흐느끼며 눈을 비비는 동안, 똑같은 한결같은 시선을 그녀에게 붙박은 채, 처음 말을 건넨 이래로 높아지지도 낮아지지도 않은 그 똑같이 낮고 한결같은 목소리로 말했다. \"플로렌스는 집으로 가야 한다고 제가 말했어요.\"\n\n\"가게 해라!\" 그 고통받고 겁먹은 어미가 황급히 외쳤다. \"당연히 나도 저 애가 가는 게 좋다. 그 계집애가 나한테 뭐라고?\"\n\n\"제게는 너무도 소중한 사람이라, 제 가슴속에 있는 악의 한 톨이라도 그 애에게 전하거나 전해지게 두느니, 어머니, 차라리 저는 어머니를 버리겠어요. 내일 교회에서 (어머니가 제게 빌미를 준다면) 그 사람을 버리겠듯이 말이에요.\" 이디스가 대답했다. \"그 애를 가만히 두세요. 제가 가로막을 수 있는 한, 그 애는 제가 배운 가르침에 농락당하거나 더럽혀지지 않을 거예요. 이건 이 쓰라린 밤에 가혹한 조건이 아니에요.\"\n\n\"네가 이걸 자식다운 태도로 청했더라면, 이디스,\" 그녀의 어머니가 칭얼거렸다, \"어쩌면 안 그랬을지도 모르지. 십중팔구 안 그랬을 거야. 하지만 그토록 극도로 매서운 말들이라니—\"\n\n\"그건 이제 우리 사이에서는 지나갔고 끝난 일이에요.\" 이디스가 말했다. \"어머니 방식대로 하세요. 어머니가 얻은 것을 어머니 마음껏 나누어 가지세요. 쓰고, 누리고, 한껏 즐기시고, 원하시는 만큼 행복하세요. 우리 삶의 목적은 이루어졌어요. 이제부터는 그것을 말없이 지니고 가요. 이 시각부터 제 입술은 지난 일에 대해 닫혔어요. 내일의 악행에서 어머니가 맡으신 몫은 용서할게요. 제 몫은 하느님이 용서해 주시기를!\"\n\n목소리에도 몸에도 떨림 하나 없이, 온갖 여린 감정의 목덜미를 짓밟는 발걸음으로 앞으로 나아가며, 그녀는 어머니에게 안녕히 주무시라 인사하고 자기 방으로 물러났다.\n\n그러나 쉬기 위해서는 아니었다. 혼자가 되니 그 격동의 소란 속에는 안식이라곤 없었던 것이다. 내일 자기를 단장할 그 화려한 준비물들 사이를, 어두운 머리를 풀어 헤친 채, 검은 눈에 사나운 빛을 번득이며, 무자비한 손으로 그것을 제 몸에서 뿌리치느라 그 손아귀의 잔인한 움켜쥠으로 발갛게 된 넓고 흰 가슴으로, 마치 제 고운 몸을 보기조차 싫다는 듯, 또 그 동반에서 자기를 떼어 놓기라도 하려는 듯 고개를 돌린 채로, 오백 번이나 이리저리, 또 이리저리, 또다시 이리저리 거닐었다. 그렇게, 혼례 전날 밤 죽은 듯한 시각에, 이디스 그레인저는 자기의 불안한 영혼과 씨름했으니, 눈물도 없이, 벗도 없이, 말도 없이, 도도하게, 그리고 아무런 하소연도 없이.\n\n마침내 그녀는 플로렌스가 누워 있는 방으로 통하는 열린 문을 만지게 되었다.\n\n그녀는 흠칫 놀라 멈춰 서서, 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n거기에는 등불 하나가 타고 있었고, 순결함과 아름다움이 한창인 플로렌스가 깊이 잠들어 있는 모습을 비춰 주었다. 이디스는 숨을 죽였고, 자기가 그녀에게로 이끌려 가는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n더 가까이, 더 가까이, 더더욱 가까이 이끌려 갔다. 마침내 어찌나 가까이 이끌려 갔던지, 몸을 굽혀, 침대 밖으로 내놓인 그 가녀린 손에 입술을 갖다 대고, 그것을 가만히 제 목으로 가져갔다. 그 손길은 옛적 바위를 친 예언자의 지팡이와도 같았다. 그 아래에서 그녀의 눈물이 솟구쳐 나왔고, 그녀는 무릎을 꿇으며 그 손 곁의 베개 위에 자기의 욱신거리는 머리와 흐르는 머리채를 뉘었다.\n\n그렇게 이디스 그레인저는 혼례 전날 밤을 보냈다. 그렇게 혼례 아침의 해는 그녀를 그 모습으로 맞이했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI. The Wedding\n\n무심하고 텅 빈 얼굴을 한 새벽이 어린 폴과 그 어머니의 유해가 잠든 교회 쪽으로 떨면서 살그머니 다가와, 창문 너머로 안을 들여다본다. 춥고 어둡다. 밤은 아직도 포석 위에 웅크린 채, 건물의 구석구석과 모퉁이에 음울하고 무겁게 도사리고 있다. 집들 위로 우뚝 솟은 첨탑 시계는, 영원한 기슭에 규칙적으로 밀려와 부서지는 시간의 조수, 그 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 물결 가운데 또 하나의 물결 밑에서 솟아나오며, 마치 바다가 어떻게 흘러가는지를 기록하는 돌 봉화처럼 잿빛으로 어렴풋이 보인다. 그러나 문 안쪽에서는, 새벽이 처음에는 그저 밤을 엿볼 수 있을 뿐이고, 밤이 거기 있다는 것을 알아챌 따름이다.\n\n교회 둘레를 힘없이 맴돌며 안을 들여다보면서, 새벽은 제 짧은 통치를 두고 신음하며 운다. 그 눈물이 창유리 위로 흘러내리고, 교회 벽에 기댄 나무들은 머리를 숙인 채 동정하여 그 수많은 손을 비틀어 짠다. 밤은 그 앞에서 점점 창백해지더니, 차츰 교회에서 스러져 나가지만, 아래쪽 납골당에는 머뭇거리며 남아, 관 위에 앉아 있다. 그리고 이제 밝은 낮이 찾아와, 첨탑 시계를 광나게 닦고, 뾰족탑을 붉게 물들이며, 새벽의 눈물을 말리고, 그 하소연을 잠재운다. 그러자 밤을 뒤따라 그 마지막 피난처에서 밤을 쫓아내던 새벽은, 이번에는 제가 납골당 속으로 움츠러들어, 겁먹은 얼굴로 죽은 자들 틈에 숨어, 밤이 새 기운을 얻어 돌아와 저를 몰아낼 때까지 기다린다.\n\n그리고 이제, 기도서를 그 본래 임자들보다 더 부지런히 들쑤셔 댔던, 그리고 사람 무릎보다 제 자그마한 이빨로 더 닳게 한 무릎방석들을 들쑤셔 댔던 쥐들이, 교회 문이 쾅 하고 울리는 소리에 깜짝 놀라, 반짝이는 눈을 제 구멍 속에 감추고 한데 바싹 모여든다. 권세 있는 자, 교회지기가 오늘 아침 일찍 묘지기와 함께 오기 때문이다. 그리고 쌕쌕거리는 자그마한 좌석 안내인 미프 부인—몸 어디에도 한 치의 군더더기 살이라곤 없이, 검소하게 차려입은, 무척이나 메마른 노부인—도 여기 와 있는데, 그녀는 제 직분대로, 교회 문에서 반 시간 동안이나 교회지기를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n미프 부인은 식초처럼 시큼한 얼굴에, 풀이 죽은 보닛을 쓰고 있으며, 게다가 육 펜스와 한 실링짜리 동전에 목마른 영혼을 지녔다. 길 잃은 사람들을 좌석으로 들어오라고 손짓해 부르는 일은 미프 부인에게 어딘가 신비로운 분위기를 주었다. 또한 미프 부인의 눈에는 언제나 더 푹신한 자리를 알고 있으면서도 사례비에 대해서는 미심쩍어하는, 그런 유보의 빛이 어려 있다. 미프 씨라는 사람은 존재하지도 않거니와, 지난 이십 년 동안 존재한 적도 없는데, 미프 부인은 차라리 그 사람 이야기는 꺼내지 않으려 한다. 듣자 하니 그는 무료 좌석에 관하여 좋지 못한 견해를 품고 있었던 모양인데, 미프 부인은 그가 위로 올라갔기를 바라기는 하나, 그렇다고 단언해서 그렇다고 말할 수는 없는 것이다.\n\n오늘 아침 미프 부인은 교회 문에서, 제단보와 양탄자와 방석들을 두드리고 먼지를 털어 내느라 바쁘다. 그리고 미프 부인은 이제 곧 있을 결혼식에 대해 할 말이 많다. 미프 부인이 듣기로는, 그 댁의 새 가구와 개조에 든 비용이 한 푼이라도 들었다면 족히 오천 파운드는 들었다고 하며, 미프 부인은 가장 믿을 만한 소식통으로부터, 그 신붓감 여인에게는 제 한 몸 축복할 만한 육 펜스 한 닢도 없다는 말을 들었다. 미프 부인은 또한, 마치 어제 일이라도 되는 양, 첫 부인의 장례식을, 그다음 세례식을, 또 그다음 다른 장례식을 기억하고 있다. 그리고 미프 부인은, 참 그러고 보니, 손님들이 도착하기 전에 이제 곧 저 명판을 비누와 물로 닦아 두어야겠다고 말한다. 이 내내 교회 계단에 앉아 햇볕을 쬐고 있던(그리고 추운 날씨에 난롯가에 앉는 것 말고는 좀체 다른 일을 하는 법이 없는) 교회지기 사운즈 씨가, 미프 부인의 이야기에 동의하면서, 그 여인이 보기 드물게 미인이라는 말을 미프 부인도 들었느냐고 묻는다. 미프 부인이 받은 정보가 그런 종류의 것인지라, 비록 정통 신앙을 지녔고 살집이 두둑하긴 해도 여전히 여성의 아름다움을 흠모하는 교회지기 사운즈 씨는, 입맛을 다시듯 이렇게 말한다. 그럼요, 그 여인이 끝내주는 미인이라는 소리를 들었지요—이는 미프 부인에게는 다소 노골적으로 들리는 표현인데, 교회지기 사운즈 씨가 아닌 다른 누구의 입에서 나왔더라면 분명 그러했을 것이다.\n\n바로 이 같은 시각, 돔비 씨의 댁에서는 큰 소동과 부산함이 일고 있는데, 특히 여자들 사이에서 그러하다. 그들 가운데 누구도 네 시 이후로는 한숨도 자지 못했고, 모두가 여섯 시도 되기 전에 옷을 다 차려입었다. 타울린슨 씨는 평소보다 더 하녀의 관심거리가 되어 있고, 요리사는 아침 식사 때 결혼식 하나가 여러 결혼식을 부른다고 말하지만, 하녀는 이를 믿지 못하며, 전혀 사실이 아니라고 생각한다. 타울린슨 씨는 이 문제에 대한 제 소감을 아껴 둔다. 그가 다소 침울해진 까닭은, 행복한 부부를 따라 파리로 가도록 고용되어 새 사두마차를 꾸리느라 바쁜, 구레나룻 기른 외국인(타울린슨 씨 자신은 구레나룻이 없다)이 채용되었기 때문이다. 이 인물에 관하여, 타울린슨 씨는 이내, 외국인에게서 좋은 일이 생긴 걸 본 적이 한 번도 없다고 인정한다. 그리고 여자들에게 편견이라고 책망을 듣자, 그 우두머리였던 보나파르트를 보라, 그가 늘 무슨 짓을 꾸몄는지 보라고 말한다. 이에 하녀는 그거 참 옳은 말이라고 한다.\n\n브룩 가의 그 장례식장 같은 방에서는 제과사가 한창 일에 몰두해 있고, 키가 무척 큰 젊은이들이 바삐 그 일을 거들어 본다. 키 큰 젊은이 가운데 하나는 벌써 셰리주 냄새를 풍기고, 그의 눈은 머리 속에 고정되어, 사물을 보면서도 보지 못한 채 멍하니 바라보는 경향이 있다. 그 키 큰 젊은이는 제게 이런 결함이 있음을 의식하고 있어, 동료에게 그건 제 \"주세관\" 탓이라고 일러 준다. 그 키 큰 젊은이는 흥분이라고 말하려는 것이지만, 그의 발음이 흐릿하다.\n\n종을 울리는 사람들이 이 결혼식의 낌새를 알아챘고, 골수뼈와 식칼을 두드리는 패거리도 그러했으며, 금관 악대 또한 그러했다. 첫 번째 패거리는 배틀브리지 근처의 뒷동네에서 연습 중이고, 두 번째 패거리는 제 우두머리를 통해 타울린슨 씨와 연락을 트고는, 손을 떼는 대가로 돈을 받겠다는 조건을 제시한다. 그리고 세 번째 패거리는, 능청맞은 트롬본 연주자의 모습으로, 모퉁이 주위를 어슬렁대며 요리조리 피해 다니면서, 뇌물을 받고 아침 만찬의 장소와 시각을 일러바칠 배신자 상인이 나타나기를 기다린다. 기대와 흥분은 그보다 더 멀리까지 뻗어 나가, 한층 넓은 범위에 미친다. 볼스 폰드에서 퍼치 씨가 퍼치 부인을 데려와, 돔비 씨네 하인들과 함께 하루를 보내며, 몰래 그들을 따라가 결혼식을 구경하게 한다. 투츠 씨의 하숙집에서는, 투츠 씨가 적어도 자기가 신랑이기라도 한 양 차려입는다. 그는 회랑의 은밀한 구석에서 그 장관을 화려하게 지켜보기로, 그리고 그곳으로 치킨을 데려가기로 작정한다. 투츠 씨에게는 필사적인 의도가 있으니, 바로 그때 그 자리에서 치킨에게 플로렌스를 가리켜 보이며, 대놓고 이렇게 말하려는 것이다. \"자, 치킨, 나는 더 이상 자넬 속이지 않겠네. 내가 가끔 자네에게 말하던 그 친구가 바로 나일세. 돔비 아가씨가 내 열정의 대상이라네. 이런 상황에서 자네 의견은 어떤가, 치킨, 그리고 당장 자네는 무얼 권하겠나?\" 한편, 그토록 놀랄 일이 많은 치킨은, 투츠 씨의 부엌에서 독한 맥주가 든 큰 잔에 부리를 처박고, 비프스테이크 두 파운드를 쪼아 먹는다. 프린세스 플레이스에서는 톡스 양이 일어나 분주하다. 그녀 또한, 비록 쓰라린 괴로움 속에 있긴 하나, 미프 부인의 손에 한 실링을 쥐여 주고, 어느 외로운 구석에서, 자기에게는 잔인한 매혹으로 다가오는 그 예식을 지켜보기로 결심했기 때문이다. 나무 항해사 인형이 지키는 거처는 온통 활기에 차 있다. 커틀 선장이, 발목까지 오는 부츠를 신고 커다란 셔츠 깃을 세운 채, 아침 식사 자리에 앉아, 분부에 따라 그라인더 롭이 그에게 결혼식 예문을 미리 읽어 주는 것을 듣고 있기 때문이다. 그가 곧 목격하게 될 그 엄숙한 의식을 선장이 완벽하게 이해할 수 있도록 하려는 것이었다. 이를 위하여 선장은 이따금 제 사제에게 엄숙하게 명을 내려, \"뱃머리를 돌려라\", 혹은 \"그 대목을 한 번 더 점검해 보라\"고 하거나, 아니면 네 본분에나 충실하고 아멘은 자기, 즉 선장에게 맡기라고 이른다. 그라인더 롭이 잠시 멈출 때마다 선장은 그 아멘 가운데 하나를 우렁차게, 흡족한 기색으로 되뇐다.\n\n이 모든 것과 그 밖의 훨씬 더 많은 일에 더하여, 돔비 씨의 거리 하나에서만도 스무 명의 보모가, 요람에 누워 있을 적부터 본능적으로 결혼식에 관심을 두는 자그마한 여자아이들로 이루어진 스무 가족에게, 결혼식을 구경하러 가게 해 주겠노라 약속해 두었다. 참으로, 교회 계단에서 자신의 우람한 몸을 햇볕에 쬐며 결혼식 시각을 기다리는 교회지기 사운즈 씨가 스스로 직무를 수행하고 있다고 느낄 만한 충분한 이유가 있는 것이다. 참으로, 미프 부인이, 거인 같은 갓난아기를 안고 현관에서 안을 기웃거리는 운 나쁜 난쟁이 아이에게 와락 달려들어, 분개하며 그 아이를 쫓아낼 만한 까닭이 있는 것이다!\n\n사촌 피닉스는 이 결혼식에 참석하려고 일부러 외국에서 건너왔다. 사촌 피닉스는 사십 년 전 한량으로 이름을 떨치던 사내였다. 그러나 그는 아직도 몸매며 거동이 어찌나 젊어 보이고, 또 어찌나 잘 차려입었던지, 낯선 이들은 그분 얼굴에 숨은 주름을, 두 눈가의 까마귀 발 같은 잔주름을 발견하고는 깜짝 놀라며, 또 그가 방을 가로질러 걸어갈 때 가고자 하는 곳으로 곧장 가는지 어쩐지 도무지 확신할 수 없다는 것을 처음으로 알아챈다. 그러나 일곱 시 반쯤 막 일어난 사촌 피닉스는 말끔히 단장한 사촌 피닉스와는 전혀 딴판이라, 본드 가의 롱스 호텔에서 면도를 받고 있을 적의 그는 정말이지 무척이나 흐리멍덩해 보인다.\n\n돔비 씨가 화장실에서 나서니, 계단의 여자들이 일제히 휙휙 흩어진다. 그들은 치맛자락을 요란하게 바스락거리며 사방으로 흩어지는데, 다만 퍼치 부인만은—(언제나 그렇긴 하지만) 흥미로운 처지에 놓여 있는지라—민첩하지 못하여, 어쩔 수 없이 그를 마주하게 되고, 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 당혹감에 그만 주저앉을 듯하다. 부디 하늘이 퍼치 가문에 닥칠 온갖 불행을 막아 주시기를! 돔비 씨는 응접실로 올라가 제 때를 기다린다. 돔비 씨의 새 청색 외투며, 담갈색 바지며, 연보라색 조끼가 휘황찬란하다. 그리고 돔비 씨가 머리를 곱슬곱슬 지졌다는 수군거림이 집 안에 떠돈다.\n\n두 번의 노크 소리가 소령의 도착을 알린다. 그 역시 휘황찬란하게 차려입었고, 단춧구멍에 제라늄 한 송이를 통째로 꽂았으며, 머리를 꼬불꼬불 바싹 지졌으니, 토박이 하인이 그것을 잘 알고 있다.\n\n\"돔비!\" 소령이 두 손을 내밀며 말한다, \"그동안 어떻게 지내셨소?\"\n\n\"소령,\" 돔비 씨가 말한다, \"댁은 어떻게 지내셨소?\"\n\n\"맙소사, 선생,\" 소령이 말한다, \"조이 비는 오늘 아침 이런 처지라오, 선생,\"—여기서 그는 제 가슴을 세게 친다—\"오늘 아침 이런 처지라서 말이오, 선생, 빌어먹을, 돔비, 그가 이참에 결혼을 두 쌍으로 하여 그 어머니를 차지할까 하는 마음이 반쯤 들지 뭐요, 선생.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 미소 짓는다. 그러나 그로서도 희미하기 짝이 없는 미소다. 돔비 씨는 자기가 그 어머니와 친척이 될 참이고, 그런 사정에서 그 어머니를 두고 농담을 해서는 안 된다고 느끼기 때문이다.\n\n\"돔비,\" 이를 본 소령이 말한다, \"축하드리오. 경하드리오, 돔비. 맹세컨대, 선생,\" 소령이 말한다, \"오늘만큼은 영국의 그 어떤 사내보다도 댁이 더 부러움을 받을 만하오!\"\n\n여기서 또다시 돔비 씨의 동의에는 단서가 붙는다. 그가 한 부인에게 크나큰 영예를 베풀어 줄 참인 까닭에, 의심할 바 없이 가장 부러움을 받을 사람은 그 부인일 터이기 때문이다.\n\n\"에디스 그레인저 양으로 말하자면, 선생,\" 소령이 말을 잇는다, \"온 유럽을 통틀어 어떤 여인이라도—그러고도 남을 것이오, 선생, 배그스톡이 한마디 덧붙이도록 허락하신다면—그러고도 남을 것이오—에디스 그레인저 양의 자리에 설 수만 있다면 제 두 귀라도, 게다가 귀고리까지도 내놓을 것이오.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말해 주시다니 친절하시구려, 소령,\" 돔비 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"돔비,\" 소령이 응수한다, \"댁도 그걸 알고 있질 않소. 거짓된 점잔은 빼지 맙시다. 댁도 알고 있소. 알고 있소, 아니면 모르고 있소, 돔비?\" 소령이 거의 발끈하며 말한다.\n\n\"이런, 정말이지, 소령—\"\n\n\"빌어먹을, 선생,\" 소령이 받아친다, \"그 사실을 알고 있소, 모르고 있소? 돔비! 늙은 조가 댁의 친구요? 우리가, 한 사내—무뚝뚝한 늙은 조지프 비, 선생—가 거리낌 없이 솔직히 말할 만한 그런 허물없는 친밀한 사이요, 돔비? 아니면 내가 격식을 차려, 거리를 두고, 예법에 매여야겠소, 돔비?\"\n\n\"이보시오, 배그스톡 소령,\" 돔비 씨가 흐뭇한 기색으로 말한다, \"댁은 정말 흥분하셨구려.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 선생,\" 소령이 말한다, \"내가 흥분했소. 조지프 비는 그걸 부인하지 않소, 돔비. 그는 흥분했소. 이건, 선생, 늙고 지긋지긋하고 만신창이가 되고 닳아빠지고 병든 제이 비의 잔해 속에 남아 있는 온갖 진실한 정을 불러일으키는 자리란 말이오. 그리고 내 댁께 한마디 하리다, 돔비—이런 때에 사내라면 제가 느끼는 바를 불쑥 털어놓든지, 아니면 입에 재갈을 물려야 하는 법이오. 그리고 조지프 배그스톡은 댁의 면전에서 말하겠소, 돔비, 댁의 등 뒤에서 제 클럽 사람들에게 말하듯이, 폴 돔비가 화제에 오르는 한 그는 결코 입에 재갈을 물지 않을 것이라고. 자, 빌어먹을, 선생,\" 소령이 자못 단호하게 결론짓는다, \"그 점을 어떻게 생각하시오?\"\n\n\"소령,\" 돔비 씨가 말한다, \"내 진심으로 댁께 신세를 졌소이다. 댁의 지나치게 편향된 우정을 막아설 생각은 추호도 없었소.\"\n\n\"지나치게 편향된 것이 아니오, 선생!\" 성마른 소령이 외친다. \"돔비, 난 그걸 부인하오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 댁의 우정이라고 해 둡시다,\" 돔비 씨가 말을 잇는다, \"어쨌든 말이오. 또한 소령, 오늘 같은 자리에서, 내가 그 우정에 얼마나 큰 빚을 졌는지 잊을 수도 없소.\"\n\n\"돔비,\" 소령이 그에 걸맞은 동작을 곁들여 말한다, \"이것이 조지프 배그스톡의 손이오. 댁이 그편이 더 좋으시다면, 수수한 늙은 조이 비의 손이오, 선생! 이것이 바로, 작고하신 요크 공작 전하께서 친히, 작고하신 켄트 공작 전하께 영광스럽게도 평하시기를, 이건 조시의 손이라, 거칠고 단단하며, 어쩌면 닳고 닳았을지도 모를 늙은 떠돌이의 손이라 하셨던 그 손이오. 돔비, 부디 지금 이 순간이 우리 생애에서 가장 덜 불행한 순간이기를. 신의 가호가 있기를!\"\n\n이제 카커 씨가 들어오는데, 그 역시 휘황찬란하게 차려입었고, 정말로 결혼식 하객답게 미소 짓고 있다. 그는 돔비 씨의 손을 좀처럼 놓지 못할 만큼 축하의 말을 늘어놓는다. 그러면서 동시에 소령의 손을 어찌나 진심으로 흔들어 대는지, 그의 목소리마저 이빨 사이로 미끄러져 나올 때 그 두 팔에 발맞추어 떨려 나온다.\n\n\"날부터가 상서롭구려,\" 카커 씨가 말한다. \"더없이 화창하고 더없이 온화한 날씨라니! 제가 조금도 늦지 않았기를 바랍니다만?\"\n\n\"시간을 꼭 맞추셨소이다, 선생,\" 소령이 말한다.\n\n\"정말 기쁩니다, 진심으로,\" 카커 씨가 말한다. \"약속된 시각보다 몇 초쯤 늦지나 않을까 걱정했지요. 짐마차 행렬에 발이 묶이는 바람에요. 그래서 외람되이 브룩 가로 돌아 말을 달려\"—이 말은 돔비 씨에게 하는 것이다—\"돔비 부인께 변변찮은 꽃 몇 송이를 두고 왔습니다. 저 같은 처지의 사람이, 이 자리에 초대받는 영광을 누릴 만큼 분에 넘치는 대접을 받았으니, 제 종속의 신분을 인정하는 뜻에서 무언가 경의를 표하는 것을 자랑으로 여기지요. 그리고 돔비 부인께서야 의심할 바 없이 값지고 호화로운 것들에 파묻혀 계실 터이니\"—후원자를 향해 묘한 눈길을 던지며—\"제 보잘것없는 선물의 그 보잘것없음이 오히려 호의를 얻기를 바랄 따름입니다.\"\n\n\"머지않아 돔비 부인이 되실 분께서,\" 돔비 씨가 너그러이 응수한다, \"댁의 마음씀에 매우 감동하실 것이오, 카커, 틀림없이.\"\n\n\"그리고 만일 그분이 오늘 아침 돔비 부인이 되실 거라면, 선생,\" 소령이 제 커피잔을 내려놓고 회중시계를 보며 말한다, \"이제 우리가 길을 나설 때가 다 됐소이다!\"\n\n사륜마차에 올라, 돔비 씨와 배그스톡 소령과 카커 씨가 교회로 마차를 달린다. 교회지기 사운즈 씨는 진작 계단에서 일어나, 삼각모를 손에 든 채 대기하고 있다. 미프 부인이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 제의실에 의자를 권한다. 돔비 씨는 교회 안에 그대로 머무는 편을 택한다. 그가 오르간을 올려다보자, 회랑에 있던 톡스 양이 어린 바람 신처럼 부푼 뺨을 한, 기념비 위 천사상의 통통한 다리 뒤로 움츠러든다. 그와 반대로 커틀 선장은 벌떡 일어나, 환영과 격려의 표시로 제 갈고리 손을 흔든다. 투츠 씨는 손으로 입을 가린 채, 가운데 신사, 곧 담갈색 바지를 입은 저분이 제가 연모하는 이의 아버지라고 치킨에게 일러 준다. 치킨은 쉰 목소리로 투츠 씨에게 속삭이기를, 저 양반은 여태껏 본 중에 가장 뻣뻣한 작자이긴 하나, 조끼에 한 방만 먹이면 저 양반을 둘로 접어 버리는 것쯤은 과학의 능력으로 충분하다고 한다.\n\n사운즈 씨와 미프 부인이 조금 떨어진 데서 돔비 씨를 눈여겨보고 있는데, 다가오는 마차 바퀴 소리가 들려오자, 사운즈 씨가 밖으로 나간다. 위층에서 그토록 정중하게 인사를 건네는 그 주제넘은 미치광이에게서 거두어들인 돔비 씨의 눈길과 마주친 미프 부인은, 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 그분의 \"고운 마님\"이 오신 것 같다고 일러 준다. 그러자 문가에 사람들이 몰려들며 수군거리고, 그 고운 마님이 도도한 걸음으로 들어선다.\n\n그녀의 얼굴에는 간밤의 고통의 흔적이 없다. 그 거동에는 잠든 처녀의 베개 위에 아름답게 몸을 내맡긴 채 흐트러진 머리를 누이고 무릎 꿇었던 그 여인의 자취가 조금도 없다. 더없이 온순하고 사랑스러운 그 처녀가 그녀의 곁에 서 있다—거기 침착하게, 꼿꼿하게, 그 의지를 헤아릴 수 없이, 매력의 절정에서 찬란하고 위풍당당하게 서 있으면서도, 제가 불러일으키는 그 찬미를 짓밟고 깔아뭉개는, 그 경멸과 반항으로 가득 찬 자신의 자태와는 두드러진 대조를 이루며.\n\n교회지기 사운즈 씨가 성직자와 서기를 부르러 제의실로 미끄러져 들어간 사이, 잠시 멈춤이 있다. 이 대목에서, 스큐턴 부인이 돔비 씨에게 말을 건넨다. 평소의 버릇보다 더 또렷하고 더 힘주어 말하면서, 동시에 에디스 가까이로 다가선다.\n\n\"이보시오, 돔비,\" 그 자애로운 어머니가 말했다, \"내 아무래도 결국 사랑하는 플로렌스를 놓아주어, 그 애 스스로 청한 대로 집에 돌아가게 해야 할까 보오. 오늘 이렇게 그 애를 잃고 나면, 이보시오 돔비, 그 애와 함께 지낼 기운조차 없을 것 같구려.\"\n\n\"그 애가 댁과 함께 있는 편이 낫지 않겠소?\" 신랑이 응수한다.\n\n\"아닌 것 같소, 이보시오 돔비. 아니, 아닌 것 같소. 나 혼자 있는 편이 나을 게요. 게다가 댁이 돌아오면 내 사랑하는 에디스가 그 애의 당연하고도 한결같은 보호자가 될 테니, 내가 괜히 그 애의 소임을 침범하지 않는 편이 나을 게요, 아마. 그 애가 질투할지도 모르니. 그렇잖니, 사랑하는 에디스?\"\n\n그 애정 어린 어머니는 이렇게 말하며 딸의 팔을 꼭 누른다. 아마도 진지하게 그 주의를 끌려는 듯이.\n\n\"진지하게 말하자면, 이보시오 돔비,\" 그녀가 다시 말을 잇는다, \"내 우리 사랑하는 아이를 놓아주어, 그 애에게 내 우울함을 안기지 않으리다. 방금 우리끼리 그렇게 정했소. 그 애도 잘 알고 있다오, 이보시오 돔비. 에디스, 얘야—그 애도 다 알고 있단다.\"\n\n또다시, 그 자애로운 어머니가 딸의 팔을 꼭 누른다. 돔비 씨는 더는 이의를 달지 않는다. 성직자와 서기가 나타났기 때문이다. 그리고 미프 부인과 교회지기 사운즈 씨가 제단 난간 앞 제자리에 사람들을 늘어세운다.\n\n햇살이 십계명의 금빛 글자 위로 내리쬔다. 어찌하여 신부의 눈은 그것을 하나하나 읽어 내려가는가? 그 열 가지 가운데 어느 것이 눈부신 빛 속에서 그녀에게 가장 또렷하게 떠오르는가? 거짓 신들, 살인, 도둑질, 어머니께 마땅히 드려야 할 공경—그중 어느 것이 벽에서 떨어져 나와, 그녀의 책 위에 이글거리는 글자로 스스로를 새기는 듯한가!\n\n\"누가 이 여인을 이 남자에게 시집보내려 내어 주십니까?\"\n\n사촌 피닉스가 그 일을 한다. 그는 일부러 바덴바덴에서 건너왔다. \"젠장,\" 사촌 피닉스가 말한다—사람 좋은 위인이지, 사촌 피닉스란—\"기왕에 돈 많은 도회지 양반을 집안에 들이게 되었으니, 그자에게 좀 정성을 보이자. 그자를 위해 뭐라도 해 주자.\"\n\n\"내가 이 여인을 이 남자에게 시집보내려 내어 줍니다.\" 그리하여 사촌 피닉스가 말한다. 사촌 피닉스는 곧장 직선으로 가려 했으나, 제멋대로 구는 두 다리 탓에 옆으로 비스듬히 빗나가는 바람에, 처음에는 엉뚱한 여인을 이 남자에게 시집보내려 내어 준다—다름 아니라, 집안과 멀찍이 친척뻘이고 스큐턴 부인보다 열 살은 손아래인, 제법 지체 있는 신부 들러리를 말이다—그러나 미프 부인이 풀 죽은 보닛을 들이밀며, 능숙하게 그를 도로 돌려세워, 마치 바퀴 위에 올려놓은 듯 \"그 고운 마님\" 쪽으로 똑바로 밀어붙인다. 이에 사촌 피닉스는 그 마님을 이 남자에게 시집보내려 내어 준다.\n\n그리고 두 사람은 하늘이 보시는 앞에서—?\n\n암, 그러고말고. 돔비 씨가 그러하겠노라 말한다. 그러면 에디스는 무어라 하는가? 그러하겠노라 한다.\n\n그리하여, 그날 이후로, 좋을 때나 나쁠 때나, 부유할 때나 가난할 때나, 아플 때나 건강할 때나, 죽음이 두 사람을 갈라놓을 때까지, 서로 사랑하고 아끼겠노라, 두 사람은 서로에게 백년가약을 맺고, 부부가 된다.\n\n제의실로 자리를 옮기자, 신부는 굳건하고 거침없는 필치로 결혼 등록부에 제 이름을 적어 넣는다. \"여기 오시는 마님네가 그리 많지는 않지요,\" 미프 부인이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 말한다—이런 자리에서 미프 부인을 본다는 것은 그녀의 풀 죽은 보닛이 까딱 숙여지는 것을 본다는 뜻이다—\"이 고운 마님처럼 이렇게 이름을 쓰시는 분은요!\" 교회지기 사운즈 씨는 그것이 참으로 끝내주는 서명이며, 쓴 이에게 걸맞다고 여긴다—허나 이는 그저 자기 양심에게나 하는 말이다.\n\n플로렌스도 서명하지만, 갈채는 없다. 그녀의 손이 떨리기 때문이다. 일행이 모두 서명한다. 마지막으로 사촌 피닉스가 하는데, 그는 자신의 고귀한 이름을 엉뚱한 칸에 적어 넣어, 마치 자기가 그날 아침에 태어난 것으로 등록해 버린다.\n\n이제 소령이 자못 늠름하게 신부에게 인사를 건네고, 그 군사 전술의 한 갈래를 모든 부인들에게도 적용한다. 스큐턴 부인은 입 맞추기가 여간 까다롭지 않은 데다, 그 성스러운 건물 안에서 날카롭게 꽥꽥 소리를 질러 대는데도 말이다. 그 본보기를 사촌 피닉스가, 심지어 돔비 씨마저 뒤따른다. 마지막으로 카커 씨가, 하얀 이를 번득이며 에디스에게 다가가는데, 그 입술에 어린 단맛을 맛보려는 것이라기보다는, 차라리 그녀를 물어뜯으려는 것 같다.\n\n그녀의 도도한 뺨에는 홍조가 어리고, 두 눈에는 그를 멈춰 세우려는 듯한 번득임이 인다. 그러나 그것은 그를 멈추지 못한다. 그가 다른 이들처럼 그녀에게 인사하며 온갖 행복을 빌어 주기 때문이다.\n\n\"빈다는 것이,\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말한다, \"이런 결합에 갖다 붙이기에 군더더기가 아니라면 말입니다만.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생,\" 그녀가 입술을 비틀고 가슴을 들썩이며 대답한다.\n\n그러나 에디스는, 돔비 씨가 청혼하러 돌아오리라는 것을 알았던 그 밤처럼 여전히 느끼는 것일까? 카커가 자기를 속속들이 알고 있고, 자기를 정확히 꿰뚫어 보며, 그가 자기를 안다는 사실로 인해 다른 그 무엇보다도 더 자기가 비천해진다고? 그를 안다는 사실 때문에 그녀의 도도함이 그의 미소 앞에서, 단단히 움켜쥔 손안의 눈처럼 움츠러들고, 그녀의 거만한 눈길이 그의 눈길과 마주치자 풀이 죽어 땅바닥을 찾는 것은, 바로 그 까닭인가?\n\n\"제 자랑스럽기 그지없습니다,\" 카커 씨가 비굴하게 목을 굽히며 말했는데, 그 눈과 이가 드러내는 바는 그것이 거짓임을 천명하고 있다, \"제 변변찮은 선물이 돔비 부인의 손에 들려 빛을 입고, 이토록 기쁜 자리에서 이토록 영광스러운 자리를 차지하도록 허락받았으니 자랑스럽기 그지없습니다.\"\n\n비록 그녀가 답례로 고개를 숙이긴 하나, 그 손이 순간적으로 움직이는 모양에는, 마치 그 들고 있는 꽃을 짓이겨 경멸하듯 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐 버리려는 듯한 무언가가 있다. 그러나 그녀는 그 손을, 가까이 서서 소령과 이야기를 나누고 있던 새 남편의 팔에 끼고는, 다시금 도도해져, 미동도 없이, 말없이 서 있다.\n\n마차들이 다시 한번 교회 문 앞에 와 있다. 돔비 씨가 신부를 팔에 끼고서, 계단 위에 늘어선 자그마한 여자아이들의 스무 가족 사이로 그녀를 이끌고 지나간다. 그 아이들 하나하나가 그 순간부터 그녀의 옷가지란 옷가지의 모양새와 색깔을 죄다 기억해 두었다가, 영영 시집만 가게 될 제 인형에게 고스란히 본떠 입힌다. 클레오파트라와 사촌 피닉스가 같은 마차에 오른다. 소령은 두 번째 마차에 플로렌스를, 그리고 하마터면 실수로 시집보내질 뻔했던 그 신부 들러리를 태워 주고는, 자기도 그 마차에 오르고, 카커 씨가 그 뒤를 따른다. 말들이 껑충거리고 뛰논다. 마부와 시종들이 펄럭이는 장식 리본과 꽃과 새로 지은 제복으로 번쩍거린다. 그들은 거리를 가로질러 휙휙 내달리며 덜커덩거린다. 그들이 지나갈 때, 천 개의 머리가 그들을 보려 돌아가고, 천 명의 점잖은 도덕가들이, 자기네는 그날 아침 결혼하지 못한 것에 대한 앙갚음으로, 저 사람들은 이런 행복이 오래갈 수 없다는 걸 거의 생각지도 못하리라며 곱씹는다.\n\n모든 것이 잠잠해지자, 톡스 양은 천사상의 다리 뒤에서 모습을 드러내, 회랑에서 천천히 내려온다. 톡스 양의 두 눈은 붉게 충혈되었고, 손수건은 축축하다. 그녀는 상처 입었으나 분개하지는 않았으며, 부디 그들이 행복하기를 바란다. 그녀는 신부의 아름다움을, 그리고 그에 비하면 보잘것없이 빛바랜 자신의 매력을 속으로 순순히 인정한다. 그러나 연보라색 조끼에 담갈색 바지를 입은 돔비 씨의 위풍당당한 모습이 마음속에 떠오르니, 톡스 양은 프린세스 플레이스의 집으로 돌아가는 길에 베일 뒤에서 또다시 운다. 커틀 선장은, 경건한 으르렁거림으로 온갖 아멘과 응답을 함께 외쳤는지라, 제 신앙적 수련으로 한결 나아진 기분이다. 그리하여 평화로운 마음 상태로, 번들거리는 모자를 손에 든 채 교회의 본당을 거닐며, 어린 폴을 기리는 명판을 읽는다. 그 늠름한 투츠 씨는, 충직한 치킨을 거느린 채, 사랑의 고뇌에 시달리며 건물을 떠난다. 치킨은 아직 플로렌스를 손에 넣을 계략을 짜내지 못했으나, 처음에 떠올린 생각이 그를 사로잡고 있어, 그는 돔비 씨를 둘로 접어 버리는 것이 옳은 방향으로 나아가는 한 수가 되리라고 여긴다. 돔비 씨의 하인들이 숨었던 곳에서 나와 브룩 가로 내달릴 채비를 하는데, 퍼치 부인이 몸이 좋지 않은 기색을 보이는 바람에 발이 묶인다. 그녀는 물 한 잔을 청하며 사람들을 놀라게 한다. 그러나 퍼치 부인은 곧 나아져서 부축을 받아 떠나간다. 그리고 미프 부인과 교회지기 사운즈 씨는 계단에 앉아, 이 일로 얼마나 벌었는지 셈하며 그 일을 두고 이야기를 나누고, 그동안 묘지기는 장례를 알리는 조종을 울린다.\n\n이제, 마차들이 신부의 거처에 당도하고, 종을 울리는 사람들이 딸랑딸랑 종을 울리기 시작하며, 악대가 연주를 시작하고, 부부 행복의 본보기인 펀치 씨가 제 아내에게 인사한다. 이제, 사람들이 입을 떡 벌린 무리를 이루어 우르르 몰려와 밀고 떠밀며 둘러싸는 가운데, 돔비 씨가 돔비 부인의 손을 잡아 이끌며 엄숙하게 피닉스 저택으로 들어선다. 이제, 나머지 결혼식 일행이 마차에서 내려, 그들을 뒤따라 들어간다. 그런데 어찌하여 카커 씨는 사람들 사이를 헤치고 현관문 쪽으로 가면서, 그날 아침 그로브에서 자기를 불러 세웠던 그 노파를 떠올리는 걸까? 또 어찌하여 플로렌스는 지나가면서, 길을 잃었던 어린 시절을, 그리고 착한 브라운 부인의 얼굴을 부르르 떨며 떠올리는 걸까?\n\n이제, 이 더없이 행복한 날에 더 많은 축하의 말이 오가고, 그리 많지는 않으나 더 많은 손님들이 모인다. 그리고 이제 그들은 응접실을 나와, 짙은 갈색의 식당에 차려진 식탁에 늘어앉는다. 그 식당은 제과사가 그 시들어 버린 무어인 조각상들을 제아무리 많은 꽃과 사랑의 매듭으로 치장한들, 도무지 밝게 꾸밀 도리가 없는 곳이다.\n\n하기야 제과사는 사내답게 제 소임을 다했고, 푸짐한 아침 만찬이 차려져 있다. 칙 씨 부부도 여러 손님들 틈에 끼어 합류했다. 칙 부인은 에디스가 천성적으로 그토록 완벽한 돔비 사람이라는 것에 감탄해 마지않으며, 큰 짐을 덜어 낸 듯한 스큐턴 부인에게 다정하고도 은근하게 군다. 그 스큐턴 부인은 샴페인을 제 몫만큼 든다. 아까 일찌감치 흥분으로 고생하던 그 키 큰 젊은이는 좀 나아졌다. 그러나 어렴풋한 후회의 감정에 사로잡혀, 그는 다른 키 큰 젊은이를 미워하며, 그에게서 접시들을 우격다짐으로 빼앗고, 손님들의 비위를 거스르는 데서 음울한 즐거움을 느낀다. 손님들은 차분하고 침착하여, 그들을 내려다보고 있는 그림들의 검은 문장(紋章)들을, 지나친 흥취로 모욕하는 법이 없다. 사촌 피닉스와 소령이 거기서 가장 흥겨운 이들이다. 그러나 카커 씨는 식탁 전체를 향한 미소를 머금고 있다. 그는 신부를 위한 각별한 미소를 지니고 있는데, 신부는 그 미소를 정말이지 좀처럼 마주하는 법이 없다.\n\n손님들이 아침 만찬을 마치고 하인들이 방을 나가자, 사촌 피닉스가 일어선다. 하얀 소맷동이 그의 손을(그러잖아도 다소 앙상한 손이지만) 거의 다 덮을 만큼 길고, 두 뺨에 샴페인의 발그레함이 피어올라, 그는 놀랍도록 젊어 보인다.\n\n\"맹세코,\" 사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"비록 이게 한 개인 신사의 댁에서는 흔치 않은 일이긴 하나, 외람되이 여러분께 청하여, 흔히 말하는 그—사실은 건배를 들어 주십사 부탁드려야겠소이다.\"\n\n소령이 자못 쉰 목소리로 그 찬동을 나타낸다. 카커 씨는 사촌 피닉스 쪽으로 식탁 위에 고개를 앞으로 숙이며, 미소 지으며 여러 번 고개를 끄덕인다.\n\n\"그—사실 이게 무슨—\" 사촌 피닉스가 이렇게 다시 시작하더니, 딱 멈추어 버린다.\n\n\"옳소, 옳소!\" 소령이 확신에 찬 어조로 말한다.\n\n카커 씨는 부드럽게 손뼉을 치고는, 다시 식탁 위로 고개를 앞으로 숙이며, 마치 이 마지막 말에 각별히 감동받기라도 한 듯, 그리고 그것이 가져다준 좋은 효과에 대한 제 느낌을 몸소 표하고 싶기라도 한 듯, 아까보다 훨씬 더 여러 번 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕인다.\n\n\"이는,\" 사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"사실 인생의 일반적인 관례를, 무례를 범하지 않고 조금 벗어나도 좋은 그런 자리외다. 그리고 비록 내 평생 한 번도 연설가였던 적이 없고, 하원에 있을 적에, 답사를 재청하는 영광을 누렸을 적에도, 사실은—실패했다는 의식에 사로잡혀 보름이나 드러누워 있었습니다만—\"\n\n소령과 카커 씨가 이 개인적 내력의 한 토막에 어찌나 즐거워하던지, 사촌 피닉스가 웃음을 터뜨리며, 두 사람을 각각 돌아보며 말을 이어 간다.\n\n\"그리고 사실, 내가 지독히 앓아누웠을 적에—그래도, 아시다시피, 나는 내게 어떤 의무가 지워져 있음을 느낍니다. 그리고 한 영국인에게 의무가 지워질 적에는, 내 생각에는, 그가 할 수 있는 한 최선의 방도로 그것을 벗어나야 마땅하지요. 자! 우리 집안은 오늘, 내가 지금 보고 있는—사실, 자리에 와 계신—내 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 친척의 모습으로, 그분과 인연을 맺는 기쁨을 누렸소이다—\"\n\n여기서 두루 박수갈채가 인다.\n\n\"와 계신,\" 사촌 피닉스는, 그것이 거듭 말할 만한 산뜻한 표현이라 느끼며 되뇐다—\"그분과, 한 사람과—다시 말해, 한 사내와 인연을 맺었으니, 그 사내에게는 경멸의 손가락이 결코—사실은, 내 영예로운 벗 돔비, 그를 그렇게 부르도록 허락하신다면 말이오만, 그와 인연을 맺은 것이외다.\"\n\n사촌 피닉스가 돔비 씨에게 절을 한다. 돔비 씨는 엄숙하게 그 절에 답례한다. 모두가, 감정에 호소하는 이 비범한, 어쩌면 전례 없는 이 인사말에 다소간 흐뭇해하며 감동을 받는다.\n\n\"나는,\" 사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"내 벗 돔비와 교분을 쌓고, 그의 머리에—그리고 사실은, 그의 가슴에 똑같이 영예를 안기는 그 자질들을 익히 살필, 그토록 바라 마지않던 기회를 누리지는 못했소이다. 내가, 우리가 하원에 있던 시절에 곧잘 하던 말마따나—상원을 들먹이는 것이 관례가 아니던 시절, 그리고 의회 절차의 질서가 어쩌면 지금보다 더 잘 지켜지던 시절에 하던 말마따나—내가 불운하게도—사실은,\" 사촌 피닉스가 자못 능청맞게 제 농담을 매만지다가, 마침내 불쑥 내뱉으며 말한다, \"'다른 곳에' 있었던 까닭이외다!\"\n\n소령이 발작적으로 웃음을 터뜨리다가, 가까스로 정신을 추스른다.\n\n\"하나 나는 내 벗 돔비에 대해 충분히 알고 있어서,\" 사촌 피닉스가, 마치 별안간 더 슬프고 더 지혜로운 사람이 되기라도 한 듯, 한층 진지한 어조로 다시 말을 잇는다, \"그가, 사실은, 힘주어 일컬어 마땅한 바—한 상인이며—한 영국 상인이며—그리고 한 사내라는 것을 알고 있소이다. 그리고 비록 내가 몇 해 동안 외국에 머물러 살았다고는 하나(내 벗 돔비를, 그리고 여기 계신 모든 분을 바덴바덴에서 맞이하여, 그분들을 대공께 소개해 드릴 기회를 누린다면 더없는 기쁨이겠소이다만), 그래도 나는, 외람되이 자부하건대, 내 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 친척에 대해 충분히 알고 있어서, 그분이 한 사내를 행복하게 해 줄 온갖 자질을 두루 갖추었다는 것, 그리고 그분과 내 벗 돔비의 혼인이 양쪽 모두의 끌림과 애정에서 비롯된 것임을 알고 있소이다.\"\n\n카커 씨가 여러 번 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕인다.\n\n\"하여,\" 사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"나는 내가 그 일원으로 속한 이 집안이 내 벗 돔비를 얻은 것을 경하하는 바이외다. 나는 내 벗 돔비가, 한 사내를 행복하게 해 줄 온갖 자질을 두루 갖춘 내 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 친척과 맺어진 것을 경하하는 바이외다. 그리고 외람되이 여러분 모두에게 청하노니, 사실, 이 자리에서 내 벗 돔비와 내 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 친척, 두 분 모두를 경하해 주시기를 바라는 바이외다.\"\n\n사촌 피닉스의 인사말이 큰 갈채를 받고, 돔비 씨가 자기 자신과 돔비 부인을 대신하여 사례한다. 제이 비가 곧이어 스큐턴 부인을 위한 건배를 제의한다. 그 일이 끝나자 아침 만찬은 시들해지고, 모욕당했던 문장들은 앙갚음을 받으며, 에디스가 여행복으로 갈아입으려 일어선다.\n\n그러는 사이, 모든 하인들은 아래층에서 아침을 먹고 있었다. 그들 사이에서 샴페인은 입에 올리기에는 너무 흔한 것이 되어 버렸고, 통닭구이며, 켜를 올린 파이며, 바닷가재 샐러드도 한낱 흔해 빠진 것이 되어 버렸다. 아까 일찍 고생하던 그 키 큰 젊은이는 기운을 되찾아, 또다시 주세관 이야기를 들먹인다. 그의 동료의 눈도 그를 본떠 가기 시작하여, 그 역시 사물을 보면서도 그것을 알아보지 못한 채 멍하니 바라본다. 부인들의 얼굴에는 두루 붉은 기운이 돈다. 특히 퍼치 부인의 얼굴이 그러한데, 그녀는 기쁨에 들떠 환히 빛나며, 인생의 근심걱정에서 한참 위로 들려 올라가, 만일 지금 누가 그녀에게 제 근심걱정이 깃들어 있는 볼스 폰드로 가는 길을 나그네에게 일러 주라고 청한다면, 그 길을 떠올리는 데 적잖이 애를 먹을 정도다. 타울린슨 씨가 행복한 부부를 위한 건배를 제의했고, 이에 은발의 집사가 깔끔하게, 그리고 감격에 겨워 답했다. 그가 어렴풋이나마 자신이 이 집안의 오랜 가신이라 여기기 시작했고, 이런 변화에 마땅히 감격해야 한다고 여기기 시작했기 때문이다. 일행 전체가, 특히 부인들이 자못 들떠 있다. 사교계에서 으레 앞장서곤 하는 돔비 씨의 요리사가, 이러고 나서 가만히 주저앉아 있을 수는 없으니, 다 함께 패를 지어 연극 구경이라도 가는 게 어떻겠느냐고 말했다. 모두가(퍼치 부인을 포함하여) 이에 동의했다. 술에 취하면 호랑이처럼 사나워져, 부인들을(특히 퍼치 부인을) 두 눈을 부라려 놀라게 하는 토박이 하인마저도 그러했다. 키 큰 젊은이 가운데 하나는 심지어 연극이 끝난 뒤 무도회를 열자고까지 제안했는데, 그것을 (퍼치 부인을 포함하여) 누구도 불가능한 일로 여기지 않았다. 하녀와 타울린슨 씨 사이에 말다툼이 일었다. 그녀는 어느 옛 격언을 근거로 혼인은 하늘이 맺어 주는 것이라 주장했고, 그는 그것이 다른 곳에서 만들어지는 것이라 우기는 척했으며, 그녀가 그렇게 말하는 것은 제 자신이 시집갈 생각을 하기 때문이라고 넘겨짚었다. 그녀는 어쨌든, 자기가 그와 결혼하는 일만은 하느님께서 부디 막아 주시기를 바란다고 말했다. 이 어지러이 오가는 비아냥을 가라앉히고자, 은발의 집사가 일어서서 타울린슨 씨의 건강을 위한 건배를 제의하니, 그를 아는 것은 곧 그를 존경하는 것이요, 그를 존경하는 것은 곧 그가, 그가 택한 사람과 함께—그 사람이 어디에 있든(여기서 은발의 집사가 하녀를 흘끔 본다)—인생에 잘 자리 잡기를 바라는 것이라 하였다. 타울린슨 씨는 감정이 듬뿍 담긴 답사로 사례하는데, 그 결말은 외국인들에 관한 것으로 흘러갔다. 그가 말하기를, 그자들이 때로는, 머리카락에 홀려 넘어가 버리는 줏대 없고 변덕스러운 지각머리에게는 환심을 살지 모르나, 바라는 바는, 어떤 외국인이 어떤 여행용 마차에서 무엇이든 슬쩍 훔쳐 냈다는 소리를 결코 듣지 않는 것이라 하였다. 여기서 타울린슨 씨의 눈이 어찌나 매섭고 또 의미심장하던지, 하녀가 막 신경질을 부리려는 참인데, 신부가 떠난다는 소식에 그녀를 비롯한 다른 모두가 정신이 번쩍 들어, 그 출발을 지켜보려 부랴부랴 위층으로 올라간다.\n\n마차가 문 앞에 와 있다. 신부가 현관으로 내려오는데, 거기서 돔비 씨가 그녀를 기다리고 있다. 플로렌스도 떠날 채비를 하고 계단에 서 있고, 응접실과 부엌 사이의 중간 위치를 지켜 온 니퍼 양이 그녀를 따라나설 채비를 하고 있다. 에디스가 모습을 드러내자, 플로렌스가 작별 인사를 하려고 그녀에게로 서둘러 다가간다.\n\n에디스가 차갑기에 저리 떠는 것일까! 플로렌스의 손길에 무언가 부자연스럽거나 해로운 것이라도 있어, 저 아름다운 자태가 견딜 수 없다는 듯 뒷걸음치며 움츠러드는 것일까! 이 떠남이 그토록 황급한 것이기에, 에디스가 손짓 한 번으로 휙 지나쳐 가 버리고 마는 것일까!\n\n마차 바퀴 소리가 멀어져 사라지자, 어머니로서의 감정에 북받친 스큐턴 부인은 클레오파트라 자세로 소파에 쓰러져 눈물을 몇 방울 흘린다. 소령이 나머지 손님들과 함께 식탁에서 와서 그녀를 달래 보려 애쓰지만, 그녀는 무슨 수를 써도 좀처럼 달래지지 않으니, 그래서 소령은 작별을 고한다. 사촌 피닉스도 작별을 고하고, 카커 씨도 작별을 고한다. 손님들이 모두 떠나간다. 홀로 남겨진 클레오파트라는, 격한 감정에 다소 어질어질해져, 잠이 든다.\n\n아래층에서도 어질어질함이 만연하다. 그토록 일찍 흥분이 도졌던 그 키 큰 젊은이는, 식기실 식탁에 머리를 풀로 붙여 놓은 듯하여, 도무지 거기서 떼어 낼 수가 없다. 퍼치 부인의 기분에는 격렬한 반전이 일어났는데, 그녀는 퍼치 씨 일로 풀이 죽어, 요리사에게 말하기를, 식구가 아홉뿐이던 시절만큼 그이가 이제는 제 집에 정을 붙이지 않는 것 같아 걱정이라고 한다. 타울린슨 씨는 귓속에서 노랫소리가 울리고 머릿속에서 커다란 바퀴 하나가 빙글빙글 돌아간다. 하녀는 누군가가 죽었으면 좋겠다고 바라는 것이 죄가 아니었으면 한다.\n\n이 아래쪽 영역에서는 시간을 두고도 마찬가지로 두루 착각이 일고 있으니, 다들 적어도 밤 열 시는 되었으리라 여기지만, 아직 오후 세 시도 채 되지 않았다. 무언가 못된 짓을 저질렀다는 어렴풋한 생각이 그 일행 한 사람 한 사람을 따라다니고, 저마다 속으로는 상대방을, 가능하면 피하는 편이 좋을 죄의 동무라 여긴다. 그 누구도 연극 구경을 가기로 한 계획을 입에 올릴 만한 뱃심이 없다. 누구든 무도회 이야기를 다시 꺼냈다가는 악의에 찬 멍청이라고 손가락질을 받았을 것이다.\n\n두 시간 뒤, 스큐턴 부인은 위층에서 잠들어 있고, 부엌에서도 낮잠은 아직 끝나지 않았다. 식당의 문장들이, 부스러기며, 더러운 접시며, 흘린 포도주며, 반쯤 녹은 얼음이며, 변색되어 김빠진 술 찌꺼기며, 바닷가재 부스러기며, 닭 다리며, 그리고 점차 미적지근하고 끈적한 수프로 변해 가는 침울한 젤리들을 내려다본다. 결혼식은 이제 아침 만찬이 그러하듯, 그 화려함과 장식을 거의 다 벗어 버렸다. 돔비 씨의 하인들은 그것을 두고 어찌나 도덕을 따져 대는지, 그리고 집에서 이른 저녁 차를 들며 어찌나 뉘우치는지, 여덟 시쯤 되자 굳어진 진지함에 빠져들고 만다. 그리고 그때쯤, 흰 조끼를 입고 익살스러운 노래를 흥얼대며, 도회지에서 산뜻하고 쾌활하게 도착한 퍼치 씨가, 저녁 한때를 보낼 채비로 어떤 흥청거림에도 응할 만반의 준비를 갖추고 왔다가, 자기가 냉랭한 대접을 받는 것을, 그리고 퍼치 부인이 영 시원찮은 것을 알고는 어리둥절해하며, 다음 합승마차 편으로 그 부인을 집까지 바래다주어야 하는 즐거운 소임을 떠맡게 된다.\n\n밤이 깃든다. 플로렌스는 그 으리으리한 저택을 이 방 저 방 거닐다가, 자기 방을 찾는데, 그 방은 에디스의 보살핌으로 온갖 호사와 안락에 둘러싸여 있다. 그녀는 그 고운 옷을 벗고, 사랑하는 폴을 위한 낡고 수수한 상복으로 갈아입고는, 디오게네스가 곁의 바닥에서 눈을 끔벅이며 깜박이는 가운데, 책을 읽으려 자리에 앉는다. 그러나 플로렌스는 오늘 밤 책을 읽지 못한다. 집은 낯설고 새롭게만 보이고, 그 안에는 요란한 메아리가 울린다. 그녀의 가슴에는 그림자가 드리워져 있다. 왜인지 무엇 때문인지는 알지 못하나, 그것은 무겁기만 하다. 플로렌스가 책을 덮자, 무뚝뚝한 디오게네스가 그것을 신호로 알아채고는, 그녀의 무릎에 앞발을 올려놓고, 어루만져 주는 그녀의 손에 제 귀를 비벼 댄다. 그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 플로렌스는 그 개를 또렷이 볼 수가 없으니, 그녀의 두 눈과 그 사이에 안개가 끼어, 그 안개 속에서 죽은 오라비와 죽은 어머니가 천사처럼 빛나기 때문이다. 월터도, 그 가엾고 떠돌다 난파한 소년도, 아, 그는 어디에 있는가?\n\n소령은 모른다. 그건 분명하다. 그리고 관심도 없다. 소령은 오후 내내 숨이 막히고 졸기를 거듭하다가, 클럽에서 늦은 저녁을 들었다. 그리고 이제 포도주 한 파인트를 앞에 놓고 앉아, 옆 식탁에 있는, 혈색 좋은 얼굴을 한 점잖은 젊은이(일어나 자리를 뜰 수만 있다면 상당한 액수라도 내놓고 싶지만 그러지 못하는)를, 돔비의 결혼식에서의 배그스톡, 선생, 이야기며, 늙은 조의 그 지독히도 점잖은 벗 피닉스 경 이야기로, 미칠 지경까지 몰아붙이고 있다. 한편 마땅히 롱스 호텔에서 잠자리에 들어 있어야 할 사촌 피닉스는, 그러기는커녕, 어쩌면 제 본의와는 어긋나게, 제멋대로 구는 두 다리가 그를 데려간 도박장에 가 있다.\n\n밤이, 거인처럼, 교회를 포석에서 지붕까지 가득 채우고, 고요한 시간 내내 그 위에 군림한다. 창백한 새벽이 다시금 창문 너머로 살그머니 들여다본다. 그리고 낮에게 자리를 내주며, 밤이 납골당으로 물러나는 것을 보고, 그것을 뒤따라가 몰아내고는, 죽은 자들 틈에 숨는다. 커다란 문이 쾅 하고 부딪치고, 결혼반지처럼 끊임없이 이어지는 제 일상의 굴레를 밟아 나가는 사운즈 씨와 미프 부인이 들어오자, 겁 많은 쥐들이 또다시 한데 바싹 웅크린다. 또다시, 삼각모와 풀 죽은 보닛이 결혼식 시각에 뒤편에 서 있다. 그리고 또다시 이 남자가 이 여인을 맞이하고, 이 여인이 이 남자를 맞이하니, 그 엄숙한 언약은 이러하다.\n\n\"오늘 이후로, 좋을 때나 나쁠 때나, 부유할 때나 가난할 때나, 아플 때나 건강할 때나, 죽음이 두 사람을 갈라놓을 때까지, 서로 맞이하여 지키며, 사랑하고 아끼겠노라.\"\n\n그것은 바로 카커 씨가, 입을 한껏 벌린 채, 깔끔하게 길을 골라 가며 말을 달려 도회지로 들어가면서 되뇌는 바로 그 말이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII. The Wooden Midshipman goes to Pieces\n\n나무 항해사가 산산조각 나다\n정직한 커틀 선장은 자기 요새화된 은신처에서 몇 주가 휙휙 흘러가는 동안에도, 적이 나타나지 않는다고 해서 기습에 대비한 신중한 방비를 조금도 늦추지 않았다. 선장은 지금 자기가 누리는 안전이 너무도 깊고 신기해서 오래 갈 리가 없다고 따져 보았다. 바람이 순풍 쪽에 서 있을 때라도 풍향계가 거기에 못 박혀 있는 일은 드물다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 또 맥스팅거 부인의 단호하고 두려움 없는 성격을 너무도 잘 알고 있었기에, 그 영웅적인 여인이 자기를 찾아내어 사로잡는 과업에 몸을 바쳤으리라는 것을 의심하지 않았다. 이런 이유들의 무게에 짓눌려 부들부들 떨면서, 커틀 선장은 매우 폐쇄적이고 은둔적인 생활을 했다. 어두워진 다음에야 좀처럼 바깥출입을 하지 않았고, 그때에도 가장 으슥한 거리로만 나섰으며, 일요일에는 아예 한 발짝도 나가지 않았고, 은신처 담장 안에서나 밖에서나 보닛이라면 마치 그것을 성난 사자가 쓰고 있기라도 한 듯 피해 다녔다.\n\n선장은 산책길에 맥스팅거 부인에게 덮쳐졌을 경우 저항을 시도한다는 것은 가능하지도 않은 일이라고 꿈에도 생각지 않았다. 그는 그런 짓은 할 수 없는 일이라고 느꼈다. 마음의 눈으로, 그는 자기가 고분고분하게 삯마차에 태워져 옛 하숙집으로 끌려가는 광경을 보았다. 일단 그곳에 갇히고 나면 자기는 끝장난 사람임을 예견했다. 모자는 빼앗기고, 맥스팅거 부인이 밤낮으로 자기를 감시하며, 어린 자식들 앞에서 비난이 자기 머리 위로 쏟아지고, 자기는 의심과 불신의 죄인이 되며, 아이들 눈에는 도깨비요, 그 어미 눈에는 발각된 배신자가 될 터였다.\n\n이 음울한 그림이 그의 상상 속에 떠오를 때면 언제나 격렬한 식은땀과 의기소침이 선장을 덮쳤다. 그것은 대개 밤에 바람과 운동을 위해 슬그머니 문밖으로 빠져나가기 직전에 그랬다. 자기가 무릅쓰는 위험을 의식하면서, 선장은 그럴 때마다 다시는 돌아오지 못할지도 모르는 사람에게 어울리는 엄숙함으로 롭에게 작별을 고했다. 만일 자기(즉 선장)가 한동안 보이지 않게 되거든, 미덕의 길을 걷고 놋쇠 기구들을 잘 닦아 두라고 신신당부하면서.\n\n그러나 기회를 헛되이 놓치지 않기 위해, 그리고 최악의 경우에 대비하여 바깥세상과 연락을 주고받을 수단을 확보해 두기 위해, 커틀 선장은 곧 그라인더 롭에게 어떤 비밀 신호를 가르친다는 좋은 생각을 떠올렸다. 그 추종자가 역경의 시각에 자기 상관에게 자기 존재와 충성을 알릴 수 있는 신호 말이다. 한참 궁리한 끝에, 선장은 \"오 즐겁게, 즐겁게!\"라는 뱃노래를 휘파람으로 부는 법을 가르치기로 결정했다. 그리하여 그라인더 롭이 뭍사람으로서 바랄 수 있는 한 거의 완벽에 가까운 경지까지 그 재주에 이르자, 선장은 이 신비로운 지시를 그의 머릿속에 새겨 넣었다.\n\n\"자, 얘야, 대기하고 있어라! 혹시라도 내가 잡히면—\"\n\n\"잡히다니요, 선장님!\" 롭이 동그란 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"아!\" 커틀 선장이 음울하게 말했다. \"혹시라도 내가 저녁을 먹으러 돌아올 작정으로 나갔다가, 사라진 지 스물네 시간이 지나도록 다시 부르는 소리가 들리는 거리 안으로 돌아오지 않거든, 너는 브리그 플레이스로 가서 내 옛 정박지 근처에서 그 곡조를 휘파람으로 불어라—작정하고 그러는 것처럼 말고, 알겠느냐, 그저 우연히 거기로 흘러든 것처럼 말이다. 내가 그 곡조로 답하거든, 너는 멀리 떠나거라, 얘야, 그리고 스물네 시간 후에 다시 돌아오너라. 내가 다른 곡조로 답하거든, 멀어졌다 다가왔다 하면서 내가 다음 신호를 내보낼 때까지 기다려라. 자, 이 명령들을 알아들었느냐?\"\n\n\"제가 뭘 멀어졌다 다가왔다 하라는 건가요, 선장님?\" 롭이 물었다. \"찻길을요?\"\n\n\"여기 똘똘한 녀석 한번 보게나!\" 선장이 그를 엄하게 쏘아보며 외쳤다. \"제 모국어 알파벳도 모르다니! 좀 멀어졌다가 다시 돌아왔다가 번갈아 하라는 말이다—그건 알아듣겠느냐?\"\n\n\"네, 선장님,\" 롭이 말했다.\n\n\"아주 좋다 그럼, 얘야,\" 선장이 누그러지며 말했다. \"그렇게 하거라!\"\n\n롭이 그것을 더 잘 해낼 수 있도록, 커틀 선장은 가게 문을 닫은 어느 저녁이면 가끔 이 장면을 예행연습하는 일까지 마다하지 않았다. 그러기 위해 거실로 물러나—마치 가상의 맥스팅거 하숙집으로 들어가듯이—벽에 뚫어 둔 정탐 구멍으로 자기 동맹자의 거동을 주의 깊게 관찰했다. 이렇게 시험에 부쳐졌을 때 그라인더 롭이 어찌나 정확하고 분별 있게 자기 임무를 수행해 내던지, 선장은 만족의 표시로 그에게 여러 번에 걸쳐 도합 6펜스짜리 일곱 닢을 주었다. 그러면서 선장은 차츰, 최악에 대비하고 가차 없는 운명에 맞서 합당한 예방책을 다 강구해 둔 사람의 체념이 자기 마음속에 슬며시 깃드는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n그렇긴 해도 선장은 전보다 조금이라도 더 무모하게 굴어서 불운을 자초하는 짓은 하지 않았다. 비록 그는 집안의 전반적인 벗으로서 돔비 씨의 결혼식에 (그 소식은 퍼치 씨에게서 들었다) 참석하여 회랑에서 그 신사에게 흐뭇하고 흡족해하는 표정을 보여 주는 것이 자기 예의범절상 옳은 일이라고 여겼지만, 양쪽 창문을 다 올린 삯마차를 타고 교회로 갔다. 게다가 맥스팅거 부인에 대한 공포 때문에 그 모험조차 망설였을 법했으나, 그 여인이 멜기세덱 목사의 설교에 다니는 처지였기에 그녀가 국교회 예배에 있을 가능성은 유난히 희박하여, 그나마 마음을 놓을 수 있었다.\n\n선장은 다시 무사히 집으로 돌아왔고, 거리에서 날마다 보이는 보닛이 일러 주는 것 외에는 적으로부터 더 직접적인 경보를 마주치는 일 없이 새 생활의 평범한 일상에 빠져들었다. 그러나 다른 문제들이 선장의 마음을 무겁게 짓누르기 시작했다. 월터의 배는 여전히 소식이 없었다. 늙은 솔 길스에게서도 아무런 기별이 오지 않았다. 플로렌스는 그 노인이 사라진 것조차 알지 못했고, 커틀 선장은 차마 그녀에게 말할 마음이 나지 않았다. 사실 선장은, 자기 식의 투박한 방식으로 어린 시절부터 사랑해 온 그 너그럽고 잘생기고 용감한 마음을 지닌 청년에 대한 자기 희망이 시들기 시작하여, 날이 갈수록 점점 더 시들어 가자, 본능적인 아픔에 움찔하며 플로렌스와 한마디 나눈다는 생각조차 멀리했다. 만일 그녀에게 전할 좋은 소식이 있었더라면, 정직한 선장은 새로 단장한 집과 호화로운 가구들을—교회에서 본 그 부인과 결부되어 그것들이 그에게는 무시무시한 것이었지만—무릅쓰고서라도 그녀 앞으로 나아갔을 것이다. 그러나 그들 공통의 희망 둘레로 어두운 지평선이 몰려들어 시시각각 더 어두워지고 있었기에, 선장은 자기가 그녀에게 새로운 불행이요 고통이기라도 한 양 느껴졌고, 맥스팅거 부인이 찾아오는 것 못지않게 플로렌스가 찾아오는 것을 두려워했다.\n\n쌀쌀하고 어두운 가을 저녁이었고, 커틀 선장은 이제 그 어느 때보다도 배의 선실 같아진 자그마한 뒤채 거실에 불을 지피라고 일러 두었다. 비가 세차게 쏟아지고 바람이 거세게 불었다. 옛 친구의 그 폭풍우 몰아치는 침실 곁 지붕 위로 나가 날씨를 살피던 선장은, 그것이 얼마나 거칠고 황량한지를 보고 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다. 그 시각의 날씨를 가엾은 월터의 운명과 연관 지었다거나, 만일 섭리가 그를 실종되고 난파될 운명에 처하게 했다면 그 일은 이미 오래전에 끝났으리라는 것을 의심해서가 아니었다. 다만 자기 생각의 주제와는 전혀 별개인 어떤 외부의 영향 아래, 선장의 기운은 가라앉았고 그의 희망은 창백해졌다. 그보다 현명한 사람들도 이전에 흔히 그러했고, 앞으로도 흔히 그럴 것처럼.\n\n커틀 선장은 매서운 바람과 비스듬히 들이치는 비에 얼굴을 향한 채, 지붕들의 황야 위로 빠르게 날아가는 묵직한 비구름을 올려다보며 거기서 무언가 기운 나는 것을 찾았지만 헛수고였다. 가까이 있는 광경도 더 나을 것이 없었다. 발치에 놓인 여러 차 상자며 거친 궤짝들 속에서 그라인더 롭의 비둘기들이 마치 음산한 미풍이 일어나는 것처럼 구구거리고 있었다. 망원경을 눈에 댄 채, 한때는 거리에서도 보였으나 오래전에 벽돌로 막혀 버린 풍향계 모양의 낡아빠진 항해사가, 날카로운 돌풍이 그를 빙글빙글 돌리며 잔인하게 가지고 노는 통에 녹슨 축 위에서 삐걱대며 신음했다. 선장의 거친 푸른 조끼 위로 차가운 빗방울들이 강철 구슬처럼 튀어 올랐다. 그는 자기를 난간 너머로 넘어뜨려 아래 포도로 내동댕이치려고 끈질기게 밀어붙이는 뻣뻣한 북서풍에 맞서 몸을 비스듬히 가누기조차 힘들었다. 만일 그날 저녁 살아 있는 희망이라는 것이 어디 있다면, 모자를 누르며 선장은 생각했다, 그것은 분명 집 안에 틀어박혀 있을 뿐 밖에 나와 있지는 않으리라고. 그래서 선장은 풀 죽은 모습으로 고개를 가로저으며 그것을 찾으러 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n커틀 선장은 천천히 자그마한 뒤채 거실로 내려갔고, 늘 앉던 의자에 앉아 난롯불 속에서 그것을 찾았다. 그러나 불은 환히 타올랐어도 거기에는 그것이 없었다. 담배 상자와 파이프를 꺼내 차분히 담배를 피울 채비를 하고는, 대통의 붉은 불빛 속에서, 그리고 입술에서 위로 굽이쳐 오르는 연기 자락 속에서 그것을 찾았다. 그러나 둘 중 어느 쪽에도 희망의 닻에 슨 녹조차 한 점 없었다. 그로그 한 잔을 들이켜 보았지만 그 우물 밑바닥에는 우울한 진실이 깔려 있어서 끝내 다 마시지 못했다. 가게 안을 한두 바퀴 돌면서 기구들 사이에서 희망을 찾았다. 그러나 그것들은 그가 아무리 반대해 보아도 막무가내로 사라진 배에 대한 계산을 풀어 나갔고, 그 계산은 외로운 바다 밑바닥에서 끝나고 말았다.\n\n닫힌 덧문에 여전히 바람이 몰아치고 비가 후두두 두드리는 가운데, 선장은 계산대 위의 나무 항해사 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그러고는 소맷자락으로 그 자그마한 장교의 제복을 닦아 주면서 생각했다. 이 항해사가 그 오랜 세월을 지켜보는 동안 그의 선원들 사이에는 변화가—거의 아무런 변화도—일어나지 않았건만, 그 변화가 어느 날 한꺼번에 닥쳐왔으며, 더구나 얼마나 송두리째 쓸어 가는 변화였는지를. 여기 뒤채 거실의 그 자그마한 모임이 깨어져 사방으로 흩어져 버렸다. 여기 \"사랑스러운 페그\"를 들어 줄 청중이 없었다. 설령 그것을 불러 줄 사람이 있다 한들—그런 사람도 없었지만. 선장 자신밖에는 아무도 그 노래를 불러낼 수 없다고 그는 도덕적으로 확신하다시피 했는데, 그것은 지금 같은 처지에서는 자기에게 그것을 시도할 기운이 없다는 것을 확신하는 것과 마찬가지였다. 집 안에는 \"월\"의 환한 얼굴이 없었다—여기서 선장은 잠시 소맷자락을 항해사의 제복에서 자기 뺨으로 옮겼다—솔 길스의 낯익은 가발과 단추들은 과거의 환영이었고, 리처드 휘팅턴은 머리를 얻어맞아 쓰러졌으며, 항해사와 관련된 온갖 계획과 구상은 돛대도 키도 없이 물의 황야 위를 떠다니고 있었다.\n\n* 리처드 휘팅턴: 가난뱅이에서 런던 시장이 된 입신출세의 전설적 인물로, 월터의 출세에 빗대 자주 거론되던 비유.\n선장이 풀 죽은 얼굴로 이런 생각들을 곱씹으며, 한편으로는 오랜 사귐의 정으로, 또 한편으로는 정신이 딴 데 가 있어서, 항해사를 닦고 서 있을 때, 가게 문을 두드리는 소리가 계산대 위에 앉아 있던 그라인더 롭의 몸뚱이에 무시무시한 깜짝 놀람을 안겨 주었다. 그의 커다란 눈은 줄곧 선장의 얼굴에 골똘히 박혀 있었고, 그는 속으로, 오백 번째쯤, 선장이 무슨 살인이라도 저질렀기에 저토록 양심에 가책을 느끼며 늘 도망치려 하는 것은 아닐까 따져 보던 참이었다.\n\n\"저게 뭐냐?\" 커틀 선장이 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"누가 주먹으로 두드린 겁니다, 선장님,\" 그라인더 롭이 대답했다.\n\n선장은 무안하고 죄지은 듯한 기색으로 즉시 발끝으로 살금살금 자그마한 거실로 걸어가 안에 틀어박혔다. 롭은 문을 열면서, 만일 방문객이 여자 모습으로 왔더라면 문지방에서 담판을 지으려 했을 것이다. 그러나 그 형체가 남자였고, 롭의 명령은 여자에게만 적용되는 것이었으므로, 롭은 문을 열어 둔 채 그것이 들어오도록 했다. 그것은 휘몰아치는 비를 벗어난 것을 반기며 아주 잽싸게 들어왔다.\n\n\"하여간 버지스 상회에 일감 하나는 생기겠군,\" 방문객이 어깨너머로 자기 다리를 안쓰럽게 내려다보며 말했다. 다리는 흠뻑 젖어 흙탕물 자국투성이였다. \"아, 안녕하십니까, 길스 씨?\"\n\n그 인사는 선장을 향한 것이었는데, 선장은 마침 우연히 나오는 척, 더없이 빤히 들여다보이고 전혀 통하지도 않는 시늉을 하며 뒤채 거실에서 나오던 참이었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다,\" 그 신사가 같은 숨에 말을 이었다. \"저야 정말이지 아주 잘 지냅니다, 대단히 감사합니다. 제 이름은 투츠입니다—투츠 씨라고 합니다.\"\n\n선장은 이 젊은 신사를 결혼식에서 본 기억이 나서 그에게 절을 했다. 투츠 씨는 킥킥 웃으며 답했다. 그리고 평소처럼 어색해져서는 거친 숨을 몰아쉬며 한참 동안 선장과 악수를 하더니, 달리 의지할 데가 없는지라 그라인더 롭에게 다가가 더없이 다정하고 친근한 태도로 그와도 악수를 했다.\n\n\"저, 있잖아요! 괜찮으시다면 길스 씨께 한마디 드리고 싶은데요,\" 투츠 씨가 마침내, 놀라운 침착함을 발휘하며 말했다. \"저, 있잖아요! 디.오.엠. 아가씨 말입니다, 아시죠!\"\n\n선장은 그에 화답하듯 엄숙하고 신비스러운 태도로 즉시 자그마한 거실 쪽으로 갈고리를 휘저었고, 투츠 씨가 그 뒤를 따라갔다.\n\n\"아! 그런데 죄송합니다만,\" 투츠 씨가, 선장이 자기를 위해 놓아 준 난롯가 의자에 앉으면서 선장의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다. \"혹시 치킨이라고 전혀 모르시지요, 길스 씨?\"\n\n\"치킨이라뇨?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"게임 치킨 말입니다,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n선장이 고개를 가로젓자, 투츠 씨는 자기가 언급한 사람이 노비 슈롭셔원과의 대결에서 자신과 조국에 영광을 안긴 그 유명한 공인이라고 설명했다. 그러나 이 정보는 선장을 그다지 깨우쳐 주는 것 같지 않았다.\n\n\"왜냐면 그가 밖에 있거든요. 그게 다입니다,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 별일 아닙니다. 아마 그렇게 많이 젖지는 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"제가 금세 그분을 불러올 수 있습니다,\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"음, 그가 댁의 젊은이와 함께 가게에 좀 앉아 있게 해 주신다면,\" 투츠 씨가 킥킥 웃으며 말했다. \"고맙겠습니다. 아시다시피, 그는 쉽게 발끈하는 데다, 습기가 그의 체력에 좀 나쁘거든요. 제가 그를 불러들이겠습니다, 길스 씨.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 투츠 씨는 가게 문으로 가서 밤을 향해 독특한 휘파람을 불어 보냈고, 그러자 텁수룩한 흰 외투에 챙이 납작한 모자를 쓴, 머리가 아주 짧고 코가 부러졌으며 양쪽 귀 뒤로 상당히 넓은 헐벗고 메마른 땅뙈기를 거느린 한 무뚝뚝한 신사가 나타났다.\n\n\"앉게, 치킨,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n고분고분한 치킨은 입에 물고 즐기던 지푸라기 몇 가닥을 뱉어 내고는, 손에 들고 있던 비축분에서 새것 한 줌을 다시 입에 넣었다.\n\n\"여기 짧게라도 한잔 걸칠 거 뭐 없겠수?\" 치킨이 막연히 말했다. \"이렇게 물벼락 치는 밤은 제 컨디션으로 먹고사는 놈한테는 고생길이라우.\"\n\n커틀 선장이 럼주 한 잔을 권하자, 치킨은 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 \"우리를 위하여!\"라는 짤막한 건배사를 던진 뒤, 마치 통 속에 붓듯이 그것을 자기 안으로 비워 넣었다. 그러고 나서 투츠 씨와 선장은 거실로 돌아와 난롯가에 자리를 잡았고, 투츠 씨가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"길스 씨—\"\n\n\"잠깐!\" 선장이 말했다. \"내 이름은 커틀이오.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 몹시 당황한 기색이었고, 선장은 엄숙하게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"커틀 선장이 내 이름이고, 잉글랜드가 내 나라요, 여기가 내 거처라, 창조에 복이 있을진저—욥기,\" 선장이 자기 권위의 근거를 제시하듯 말했다.\n\n\"아! 길스 씨는 못 뵙겠지요, 그렇죠?\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"왜냐면—\"\n\n\"솔 길스를 볼 수만 있다면, 젊은 양반,\" 선장이 묵직한 손을 투츠 씨의 무릎에 얹으며 인상 깊게 말했다. \"늙은 솔을 말이오, 알겠소—당신 두 눈으로—지금 거기 앉아 있는 그대로—그렇다면 당신은 나에게, 바람 한 점 없이 멈춘 배에 불어오는 순풍보다 더 반가운 손님일 거요. 하지만 당신은 솔 길스를 볼 수 없소. 그런데 왜 솔 길스를 볼 수 없느냐?\" 선장이, 투츠 씨의 얼굴을 보고 자기가 그 신사의 마음에 깊은 인상을 새기고 있음을 알아채고서 말했다. \"그가 안 보이게 됐기 때문이오.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 동요한 나머지 그런 건 전혀 상관없다고 대답하려 했다. 그러나 곧 고쳐서, \"이런, 맙소사!\"라고 말했다.\n\n\"바로 저 사람이,\" 선장이 말했다. \"글로 써서 여기를 나한테 맡겨 두고 떠났소. 하지만 그가 거의 내 의형제나 다름없는 사람이었는데도, 그가 어디로 갔는지, 왜 갔는지는 당신만큼이나 나도 도무지 모르겠소. 조카를 찾으러 갔는지, 아니면 마음이 영 진정되지 않아서 그랬는지 말이오. 어느 날 동틀 녘에, 그는 뱃전을 넘어갔소,\" 선장이 말했다. \"물 한 방울 튀기지 않고, 잔물결 하나 없이. 나는 그 사람을 높은 데 낮은 데 다 뒤지며 찾았지만, 그때부터 그를 눈으로도 귀로도, 그 어떤 것으로도 도무지 보지 못했소.\"\n\n\"하지만, 세상에, 돔비 아가씨는 모르시지요—\" 투츠 씨가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"자, 내가 당신한테 묻겠소, 정 많은 사람으로서,\" 선장이 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"왜 그분이 알아야 하오? 어쩔 도리가 없게 될 때까지, 왜 그분에게 알게 만들어야 하오? 그 사랑스러운 분이 늙은 솔 길스를 어찌나 다정하게, 어찌나 상냥하게, 어찌나—이런 말이 다 무슨 소용이오? 당신도 그분을 알지 않소.\"\n\n\"그러길 바랍니다,\" 투츠 씨가, 온 얼굴에 번지는 의식적인 홍조와 함께 킥킥 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 당신은 그분 심부름으로 여기 온 거요?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다고 봐야지요,\" 투츠 씨가 킥킥 웃었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 내가 한마디만 하겠소,\" 선장이 말했다. \"당신은 천사를 알고 있고, 천사의 위임을 받은 사람이오.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 즉시 선장의 손을 붙들고 그와 벗이 되는 영광을 청했다.\n\n\"제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데,\" 투츠 씨가 진지하게 말했다. \"저와의 교분을 두텁게 해 주신다면 정말 대단히 감사하겠습니다. 선장님과 아주 많이 가까워지고 싶어요. 저는 정말이지 친구가 필요합니다, 정말로요. 어린 돔비는 옛날 블림버 댁에서 제 친구였고, 만일 살아 있었더라면 지금도 그랬을 겁니다. 치킨은,\" 투츠 씨가 처량한 속삭임으로 말했다. \"아주 괜찮아요—제 방면에선 훌륭하지요—아마 세상에서 가장 약삭빠른 사람일 겁니다. 그가 모르는 수가 없거든요, 다들 그렇게 말합니다—하지만 글쎄요—그가 전부는 아니거든요. 그러니까 그분은 천사예요, 선장님. 어디엔가 천사가 있다면, 그건 돔비 아가씨입니다. 제가 늘 그렇게 말해 왔지요. 하지만 정말이지, 아시다시피,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"저와의 교분을 길러 주신다면 정말 대단히 감사하겠습니다.\"\n\n커틀 선장은 이 제안을 정중한 태도로 받아들였으나, 그것을 수락하겠다고 확실히 약속하지는 않은 채, 그저 \"그래, 그래, 얘야. 두고 봅시다, 두고 봐요\"라고만 말했다. 그러고는 무슨 일로 이번 방문의 영광을 누리게 되었는지 물어, 투츠 씨에게 그가 온 본래의 용건을 일깨워 주었다.\n\n\"실은 말이지요,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다. \"그 젊은 여자 심부름으로 온 겁니다. 돔비 아가씨가 아니라—수전 말입니다, 아시죠.\"\n\n선장은 한 번 고개를 끄덕였는데, 그 진지한 표정은 자기가 그 젊은 여자를 깊이 존경하고 있음을 나타냈다.\n\n\"어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지 말씀드리지요,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"아시다시피, 저는 가끔 돔비 아가씨를 찾아가 뵙거든요. 일부러 가는 건 아니에요, 아시겠지만, 그저 그 동네에 아주 자주 있게 되는데, 거기 있다 보면, 그러니까—그러니까 들르는 거지요.\"\n\n\"그야 당연한 일이오,\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"네,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"오늘 오후에 들렀어요. 제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데, 오늘 오후 돔비 아가씨가 얼마나 천사 같으셨는지 도무지 상상도 못 하실 겁니다.\"\n\n선장은 고개를 한 번 홱 젖혀 답했는데, 그것은 어떤 사람들에게는 쉽지 않을지 몰라도 자기에게는 충분히 가능한 일이라는 뜻이었다.\n\n\"제가 나오려는데,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"그 젊은 여자가 더없이 뜻밖으로 저를 식료품실로 데려가는 거예요.\"\n\n선장은 잠시 그 처사에 이의가 있는 듯, 의자에 등을 기대며 미심쩍은, 위협적이지는 않다 해도 그런 표정으로 투츠 씨를 바라보았다.\n\n\"거기서 그 여자가,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"이 신문을 꺼내더군요. 거기에 실린 어떤 내용 때문에 하루 종일 그걸 돔비 아가씨에게서 숨겨 두었다고 하면서요. 그 여자와 돔비 아가씨가 전에 알던 누군가에 관한 거라고요. 그러고는 그 대목을 저한테 읽어 주었어요. 아주 좋아요. 그런 다음 그 여자가 말하길—잠깐만요, 뭐라고 했더라!\"\n\n투츠 씨는 이 문제에 자기 정신력을 집중하려 애쓰다가 본의 아니게 선장의 눈에 시선이 박혔고, 그 엄한 표정에 너무도 마음이 흐트러진 나머지, 이야기의 실마리를 다시 잡는 어려움이 괴로울 지경으로 커졌다.\n\n\"아!\" 한참 궁리한 끝에 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"아, 그래! 맞다! 그 여자가 말하길, 그게 사실이 아닐 가능성이 조금이라도 있기를 바란다고 했어요. 그리고 자기는 돔비 아가씨를 놀라게 하지 않고서는 직접 나오기가 어려우니, 제가 이 거리에 있는 기구 제작자 솔로몬 길스 씨—그 당사자의 외삼촌이라는—께 내려가서, 그분이 그게 사실이라고 믿는지, 아니면 시내에서 뭔가 다른 걸 들었는지 여쭤봐 달라고 했어요. 그분이 저한테 말씀하지 못하시면, 틀림없이 커틀 선장님이 해 주실 거라고요. 참 그렇지!\" 투츠 씨가, 그 사실이 퍼뜩 떠오르자 말했다. \"당신, 바로 당신이로군요!\"\n\n선장은 투츠 씨 손에 든 신문을 흘끗 보았고, 숨을 짧고 가쁘게 몰아쉬었다.\n\n\"음,\" 투츠 씨가 말을 이었다. \"제가 좀 늦은 이유는, 먼저 핀칠리까지 올라갔다 왔기 때문이에요. 거기서 자라는 아주 보기 드물게 좋은 별꽃을 좀 구하려고요, 돔비 아가씨 새한테 주려고요. 하지만 그 길로 곧장 여기로 왔어요. 신문은 보셨겠지요?\"\n\n맥스팅거 부인에게 자기에 관한 광고가 길게 실린 것을 발견하게 될까 봐 신문 읽기를 조심하게 된 선장은 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"제가 그 대목을 읽어 드릴까요?\" 투츠 씨가 물었다.\n\n선장이 그러라는 표시를 하자, 투츠 씨가 선박 소식란에서 다음과 같이 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n\"'사우샘프턴. 바크선 디파이언스호, 선장 헨리 제임스, 설탕과 커피와 럼을 싣고 오늘 본 항에 입항, 자메이카에서 귀항하던 항해 엿새째에 바람이 멎어 멈춰 선 곳이'—이러저러한 위도라는데, 아시죠,\" 투츠 씨가 숫자에 한번 힘없이 덤벼들었다가 거기서 고꾸라진 뒤 말했다.\n\n\"이런!\" 선장이 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 탁자를 치며 외쳤다. \"어서 계속 읽어라, 얘야!\"\n\n\"—위도,\" 투츠 씨가 선장을 깜짝 놀란 눈길로 흘끗 보며 되풀이했다. \"그리고 경도 이러저러한 곳에서—'망보던 자가, 해 지기 반 시간 전에, 약 1마일쯤 떨어진 거리에서 어떤 난파선 잔해가 떠다니는 것을 발견했다. 날씨가 맑고 바크선이 나아가지 못하던 터라, 그것을 조사하라는 명령과 함께 보트 한 척을 내렸는데, 그 잔해는 여러 개의 큰 원목과, 적재량 약 5백 톤가량 되는 영국 브리그선의 주삭구 일부, 그리고 \"선과 ㅎ—\"이라는 글자가 아직 또렷이 읽히는 선수재의 일부로 이루어져 있음이 밝혀졌다. 떠다니는 잔해 위에는 어떤 시신의 흔적도 보이지 않았다. 디파이언스호의 항해일지에는, 밤중에 미풍이 일면서 그 난파선 잔해가 더는 보이지 않게 되었다고 기록되어 있다. 런던 항 소속, 바베이도스행이던 실종 선박 선과 상속자호의 운명에 관한 온갖 추측은 이제 영원히 잠재워졌음이 분명하다. 그 배는 지난 허리케인에 부서졌으며, 배에 탄 모든 사람이 목숨을 잃었다.'\"\n\n커틀 선장은, 모든 인간이 그러하듯, 낙담 속에서도 자기 안에 얼마나 많은 희망이 살아남아 있었는지를, 그것의 죽음의 충격을 느끼기 전까지는 거의 알지 못했다. 그 단락을 읽는 동안, 그리고 그 후 한두 분 동안, 그는 마치 넋이 나간 사람처럼 시선을 그 겸손한 투츠 씨에게 고정한 채 앉아 있었다. 그러더니 갑자기 일어나, 손님에 대한 경의로 탁자 위에 놓아 두었던 번들거리는 모자를 쓰고서, 선장은 등을 돌려 자그마한 벽난로 선반 위로 머리를 숙였다.\n\n\"오! 제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데,\" 선장의 뜻밖의 비탄에 다정한 마음이 움직인 투츠 씨가 외쳤다. \"이 세상이라는 건 참으로 비참한 곳이로군요! 늘 누군가는 죽어 가거나, 무슨 거북한 일을 저지르고 있어요. 정말이지 이런 걸 미리 알았더라면, 제가 제 재산을 물려받게 되는 걸 그렇게 고대하지는 않았을 거예요. 이런 세상은 본 적이 없어요. 블림버 댁보다 훨씬 더 못해요.\"\n\n커틀 선장은 자세를 바꾸지 않은 채 투츠 씨에게 자기는 신경 쓰지 말라고 손짓했다. 그러더니 곧 몸을 돌렸는데, 번들거리는 모자는 귀 쪽으로 밀려나 있었고, 손으로는 자기 갈색 얼굴을 진정시키며 쓰다듬고 있었다.\n\n\"월, 내 사랑하는 아이야,\" 선장이 말했다. \"잘 가거라! 월, 내 아이, 내 사내, 내 어른아, 나는 너를 사랑했단다! 그 애는 내 피붙이가 아니었소,\" 선장이 난롯불을 바라보며 말했다. \"나한테는 피붙이라곤 없으니까—하지만 아비가 자식을 잃을 때 느끼는 그 무언가를, 나는 월을 잃으면서 느끼고 있소. 왜냐고?\" 선장이 말했다. \"그건 하나만 잃은 게 아니라, 자그마치 한 다스나 잃었기 때문이오. 발그레한 얼굴에 곱슬머리를 하고, 한 곡조 음악처럼 매주 이 거실에 들러 즐겁게 굴던 그 어린 학생 녀석은 어디 갔소? 월과 함께 가라앉았소. 무엇으로도 지치게 하거나 풀죽게 할 수 없던, '마음의 기쁨' 얘기로 놀려 대면 환히 빛나며 얼굴을 붉히던, 보기에 그렇게도 아름답던 그 싱싱한 청년은 어디 있소? 월과 함께 가라앉았소. 노인이 일 분이라도 짓눌리는 걸 두고 보지 못하던, 자기 자신은 조금도 돌보지 않던, 온통 불타오르던 그 사내의 기상은 어디 있소? 월과 함께 가라앉았소. 그건 월 하나가 아니오. 내가 알고 사랑한 월이 한 다스나 있었는데, 그가 가라앉을 때 모두 그의 목에 매달려 있었고, 지금은 그들이 내 목에 매달려 있소!\"\n\n투츠 씨는 말없이 앉아, 무릎 위에서 신문을 되도록 작게 접고 또 접었다.\n\n\"그리고 솔 길스,\" 선장이 난롯불을 응시하며 말했다. \"가엾게도 조카 잃은 늙은 솔, 자넨 어디로 갔나! 자넨 나한테 맡겨졌었네. 자네 마지막 말은 '내 조카를 돌봐 주게!'였지. 솔, 자네 대체 무슨 마음이 들어서 네드 커틀을 모른 척하고 떠난 건가. 그리고 그 애가 내려다보고 있는 내 장부에, 자네에 관해선 대체 뭐라고 적어 넣어야 한단 말인가! 솔 길스, 솔 길스!\" 선장이 천천히 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"집을 떠나 어딘가에서, 곁에 월을 아는 사람도 없이, 한마디 거들 사람도 없이 저 신문을 보다니. 옆구리로 파도를 정통으로 맞고, 곤두박질치며 머리부터 처박히고 마는구나!\"\n\n무거운 한숨을 내쉬며, 선장은 투츠 씨를 향해 돌아서서, 그 신사가 거기 있다는 것을 다시 또렷이 의식하도록 마음을 추슬렀다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 선장이 말했다. \"그 젊은 여자한테 솔직히 전해 주어야 하오. 이 끔찍한 소식은 너무도 정확하다고. 이런 일에 대해선, 알다시피, 사람들이 꾸며 대지 않거든. 그건 배의 항해일지에 기록된 거고, 항해일지는 사람이 쓸 수 있는 가장 진실한 책이오. 내일 아침,\" 선장이 말했다. \"내가 나가서 알아보겠소. 하지만 좋은 결과로 이어지진 않을 거요. 그럴 리가 없소. 오전 중에 한번 들러 주면, 내가 들은 걸 알려 주리다. 하지만 그 젊은 여자한테는 커틀 선장이 그러더라고 전해 주오, 다 끝났다고. 끝났다고!\" 그러고 선장은 번들거리는 모자를 갈고리로 벗겨 내고, 모자 꼭대기 안에서 손수건을 꺼내, 절망적으로 자기 희끗한 머리를 닦고는, 깊은 의기소침의 무심함으로 손수건을 도로 그 안에 던져 넣었다.\n\n\"오! 정말이지,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"정말로 더없이 마음이 아픕니다. 명예를 걸고 그렇습니다, 비록 제가 그분과 아는 사이는 아니었지만요. 돔비 아가씨가 무척 충격을 받으실까요, 길스 선장님—아니, 커틀 씨 말입니다?\"\n\n\"이런, 자네도 참,\" 선장이 투츠 씨의 순진함에 다소 측은해하며 응수했다. \"그분이 요만할 때부터, 두 사람은 어린 멧비둘기 한 쌍처럼 서로 좋아했다네.\"\n\n\"정말 그랬군요!\" 투츠 씨가 상당히 길어진 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"두 사람은 천생연분이었소,\" 선장이 애절하게 말했다. \"하지만 이제 와서 그게 다 무슨 소용이오!\"\n\n\"제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데,\" 투츠 씨가 어색한 킥킥거림과 격정이 묘하게 뒤섞인 채로 말을 토해 내며 외쳤다. \"아까보다도 더 마음이 아픕니다. 아시다시피, 길스 선장님, 저는—저는 정말로 돔비 아가씨를 흠모합니다—저는—저는 그분을 사랑하느라 완전히 쓰라립니다.\" 이 고백이 불행한 투츠 씨에게서 터져 나오는 그 격렬함은 그의 감정의 맹렬함을 말해 주었다. \"하지만 그 원인이 무엇이든 그분이 고통받는 것에 제가 진정으로 가슴 아파하지 않는다면, 제가 그분을 이런 식으로 우러르는 게 무슨 소용이겠습니까. 제 애정은 이기적인 게 아니거든요, 아시겠지만,\" 투츠 씨가, 선장의 다정함을 목격한 데서 생겨난 신뢰감으로 말했다. \"저는요, 길스 선장님, 만일 제가 돔비 아가씨를 위해서 마차에 치이거나—아니면—아니면 짓밟히거나—아니면—아니면 아주 높은 데서 떨어지거나—뭐 그런 종류의 일을 당할 수 있다면, 그게 저한테 일어날 수 있는 가장 기쁜 일일 것 같은, 그런 사람입니다.\"\n\n이 모든 말을 투츠 씨는 부드러운 감정을 못마땅해하는 치킨의 시샘 어린 귀에 들어가지 않게끔 낮은 목소리로 했다. 그 자제의 노력이 그의 감정의 강렬함과 어우러져 그를 귀 끝까지 빨갛게 만들었고, 커틀 선장의 눈에 그토록 감동적인 사심 없는 사랑의 광경을 펼쳐 보이게 했으니, 마음씨 좋은 선장은 위로하듯 그의 등을 토닥이며 힘내라고 일렀다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"선장님 자신도 괴로우신 와중에 그렇게 말씀해 주시다니 친절하시네요. 정말 대단히 감사합니다. 아까도 말씀드렸지만, 저는 정말로 친구가 필요하고, 선장님과 가까이 지냈으면 좋겠습니다. 제가 형편은 아주 넉넉하지만,\" 투츠 씨가 힘주어 말했다. \"제가 얼마나 비참한 짐승인지 모르실 겁니다. 그 속 빈 군중들은, 아시겠지만, 제가 치킨이나 그런 명사들과 함께 있는 걸 보면 제가 행복한 줄 압니다. 하지만 저는 비참합니다. 저는 돔비 아가씨 때문에 괴롭습니다, 길스 선장님. 밥도 제대로 못 넘기고, 양복 짓는 즐거움도 없고, 혼자 있을 때면 자주 웁니다. 정말이지 내일 다시 오는 것이, 아니 쉰 번을 다시 오는 것이 저에게는 기쁨일 겁니다.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 투츠 씨는 선장의 손을 잡았다. 그리고 치킨의 날카로운 눈길 앞에서 그렇게 짧은 시간에 감출 수 있는 만큼의 동요의 흔적은 감춘 채, 그 저명한 신사가 있는 가게로 돌아갔다. 자기 우위를 시샘하기 쉬운 치킨은, 투츠 씨가 작별을 고하는 동안 커틀 선장을 결코 호의적이라고는 할 수 없는 눈으로 쏘아보았으나, 달리 자기 악감정을 드러내지는 않고 자기 후원자를 따라 나갔다. 그리하여 선장은 슬픔에 짓눌린 채 남았고, 그라인더 롭은 노비 슈롭셔원의 정복자를 거의 반 시간이나 빤히 쳐다보는 영광을 누린 덕에 기쁨에 들떠 남았다.\n\n롭이 계산대 밑 자기 잠자리에서 곤히 잠든 지 한참 후에도, 선장은 앉아서 난롯불을 바라보았다. 그리고 바라볼 불이라곤 없어진 지 한참 후에도, 선장은 녹슨 살창을 응시하며 앉아 있었고, 월터와 늙은 솔에 대한 부질없는 생각들이 그의 마음속으로 몰려들었다. 집 꼭대기 폭풍우 치는 방으로 물러나도 안식은 따라오지 않았고, 선장은 아침에 슬픔에 잠긴 채 개운치 않은 몸으로 일어났다.\n\n시내 사무소들이 문을 열자마자, 선장은 돔비와 아들 회사의 회계실로 나섰다. 그러나 그날 아침 항해사의 창문은 열리지 않았다. 그라인더 롭은 선장의 명령에 따라 덧문을 닫아 두었고, 그 집은 마치 초상집 같았다.\n\n때마침 커틀 선장이 문에 다다랐을 때 카커 씨가 사무소로 들어서고 있었다. 그 지배인의 인사를 엄숙하고 말없이 받은 다음, 커틀 선장은 대담하게도 그를 따라 그의 방으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"자, 커틀 선장,\" 카커 씨가 벽난로 앞 자기 평소 자리를 잡고 모자를 쓴 채로 말했다. \"이거 안된 일이로군요.\"\n\n\"어제 인쇄물에 났던 소식을 받으셨습니까, 선생?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소,\" 카커 씨가 말했다. \"우리도 받았소! 정확하게 보도된 거였소. 보험업자들이 상당한 손실을 입겠지요. 우리도 무척 유감이오. 어쩔 수 없지요! 인생이란 게 그렇소!\"\n\n카커 씨는 주머니칼로 손톱을 섬세하게 다듬으며, 문가에 서서 자기를 바라보고 있는 선장에게 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"가엾은 게이는 정말 안타깝소,\" 카커가 말했다. \"선원들도 그렇고요. 그중에 우리 회사 최고의 일꾼들이 몇 있었다고 들었소. 늘 이런 식이지요. 게다가 식솔을 거느린 자들도 많았고요. 가엾은 게이에게는 식솔이 없었던 게 위안이라면 위안이오, 커틀 선장!\"\n\n선장은 턱을 문지르며 서서 지배인을 바라보았다. 지배인은 책상 위에 놓인 뜯지 않은 편지들을 흘끗 보고는 신문을 집어 들었다.\n\n\"내가 도와줄 일이라도 있소, 커틀 선장?\" 그가 신문에서 눈을 떼며, 미소 띤 의미심장한 눈길로 문 쪽을 보며 물었다.\n\n\"선생, 제 마음이 한 가지 불안해하는 게 있는데, 그걸 좀 가라앉혀 주셨으면 합니다,\" 선장이 응수했다.\n\n\"이런!\" 지배인이 외쳤다. \"그게 뭐요? 자, 커틀 선장, 미안하지만 좀 빨리 말해 주시오. 내가 일이 많이 밀려 있어서요.\"\n\n\"이걸 좀 보십쇼, 선생,\" 선장이 한 발짝 다가서며 말했다. \"제 친구 월이 이번 그 재앙 같은 항해를 떠나기 전에—\"\n\n\"자, 자, 커틀 선장,\" 미소 띤 지배인이 끼어들었다. \"그런 식으로 재앙 같은 항해 운운하지 마시오. 여기서 우리는 재앙 같은 항해와는 아무 상관이 없소, 이 양반아. 바다든 뭍이든 모든 항해에는 위험이 따른다는 걸 기억 못 한다면, 선장, 당신은 오늘치 술 배급을 아주 일찍부터 시작한 게 틀림없소. 그 뭐라더라 하는 젊은이가 이 사무소에서 그에게 불리하게 꾸며진 험한 날씨에 죽은 거라는 억측 때문에 불안한 건 아니겠지요—그렇소? 쳇, 선장! 그런 종류의 불안에는 잠과 소다수가 가장 좋은 약이오.\"\n\n\"이보시오,\" 선장이 천천히 응수했다. \"당신은 나한테는 거의 애송이뻘이니, 그 말이 좀 헛나갔어도 양해를 구하진 않겠소—만일 당신이 이런 장난에서 무슨 재미를 느낀다면, 당신은 내가 생각했던 그런 신사가 아니오. 그리고 만일 당신이 내가 생각했던 그런 신사가 아니라면, 어쩌면 내 마음이 불안해할 만한 까닭이 있는 거요. 자, 그게 뭐냐면 이렇소, 카커 씨—그 가엾은 녀석이 명령대로 떠나기 전에, 그가 나한테 말하길, 자기는 자기 잘되라고 가는 것도, 승진하러 가는 것도 아니라는 걸 알고 있다고 했소. 그건 그 애 생각이 틀린 거라는 게 내 믿음이었고, 그래서 내가 그에게 그렇게 말했소. 그리고 내가 여기로 와서, 당신네 우두머리 총책임자가 안 계셨기에, 내 마음 편하자고 점잖게 당신한테 한두 가지를 물었소. 그 물음들에 당신은—거리낌 없이 답해 주었지요. 자 이제, 모든 게 다 끝나 버린 지금—정말 끝났으니—그리고 고칠 수 없는 것은 견뎌야만 하는 지금—그건, 학식 있는 양반이니, 그 말이 든 책을 찾아보고 거기에 한마디 적어 두시오—내가 틀리지 않았었다는 걸, 월이 나한테 한 말을 노인에게 전하지 않았을 때 내가 내 의무에 소홀했던 게 아니라는 걸, 그리고 그가 바베이도스 항을 향해 돛을 올렸을 때 바람이 정말로 그의 돛에 들어 있었다는 걸, 한 번 더 한마디로 아는 것이 내 마음을 놓이게 할 거요. 카커 씨,\" 선장이 천성의 선량함으로 말했다. \"지난번에 내가 여기 왔을 때, 우리는 아주 유쾌하게 지냈지요. 만일 이 가엾은 녀석 때문에 오늘 아침 내가 그만큼 유쾌하지 못했다면, 그리고 내가 피해 갈 수도 있었을 당신의 어떤 말에 다시 발끈했다면, 내 이름은 에드워드 커틀이오, 그리고 양해를 구하겠소.\"\n\n\"커틀 선장,\" 지배인이 더없이 정중하게 응수했다. \"내 부탁 하나 들어주시오.\"\n\n\"그게 뭡니까, 선생?\" 선장이 물었다.\n\n\"미안하지만 나가 주시오,\" 지배인이 팔을 쭉 뻗으며 응수했다. \"그리고 그 횡설수설은 다른 데 가서 늘어놓으시오.\"\n\n선장 얼굴의 마디마디가 놀라움과 분노로 하얗게 질렸다. 이마의 붉은 테두리마저, 몰려드는 구름 사이의 무지개처럼 빛이 바랬다.\n\n\"내 한마디 하겠소, 커틀 선장,\" 지배인이 그에게 집게손가락을 흔들고 이를 죄다 드러내 보이면서도 여전히 상냥하게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"지난번 당신이 여기 왔을 때 나는 당신한테 너무 너그러웠소. 당신은 교활하고 뻔뻔한 무리에 속한 자요. 그 뭐라더라 하는 젊은이가 이곳에서 목덜미를 잡혀 쫓겨나는 걸 막아 주고 싶은 마음에서, 이 양반아, 내가 당신을 봐준 거요. 하지만 그건 한 번, 단 한 번뿐이오. 자, 가시오, 친구!\"\n\n선장은 완전히 땅에 뿌리박힌 듯 꼼짝 못 하고, 말문이 막혔다—\n\n\"가시오,\" 기분 좋아진 지배인이 옷자락을 추슬러 잡고 난로 깔개 위에 두 다리를 벌리고 서서 말했다. \"분별 있는 사람답게 말이오, 그리고 끌어내는 일이라든가 뭐 그런 난폭한 수단까지 가지는 맙시다. 만일 돔비 씨가 여기 계셨다면, 선장, 당신은 어쩌면 더 망신스러운 꼴로 떠나야 했을지도 모르오. 나는 그저, 가라고 말할 뿐이오!\"\n\n선장은 깊은 숨을 들이쉬는 데 보탬이 되도록 묵직한 손을 가슴에 얹고, 카커 씨를 머리부터 발끝까지 훑어보고는, 자기가 지금 어디에 있는지, 어떤 무리 속에 있는지 도무지 분명히 이해되지 않는 듯 그 자그마한 방을 둘러보았다.\n\n\"속이 깊은 양반이로군, 커틀 선장,\" 카커가, 세상을 너무도 잘 알아서 자기와 직접 관련되지 않는 한 어떤 비행이 드러나도 흐트러지지 않는 세상물정 밝은 사람의 느긋하고 활기찬 솔직함으로 말을 이었다. \"하지만 당신도 수심을 다 벗어난 건 아니오—당신도, 당신의 그 사라진 친구도 말이오, 선장. 그 사라진 친구를 당신은 어떻게 한 거요, 응?\"\n\n다시 선장은 가슴에 손을 얹었다. 또 한 번 깊은 숨을 들이쉰 뒤, 그는 자기 자신에게 \"대기하라!\"고 다짐했다. 그러나 속삭임으로.\n\n\"앙증맞은 음모도 꾸미고, 앙증맞은 회의도 열고, 앙증맞은 약속도 잡고, 앙증맞은 손님도 맞이하지요, 선장, 응?\" 카커가, 이를 여전히 드러낸 채로 그에게 눈썹을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"하지만 그러고 나서 여기로 오다니, 대담한 짓이오. 당신답지 않게 경솔하구려! 당신네 음모꾼들, 숨는 자들, 도망치는 자들은 그보다 잘 알아야 마땅한 법이오. 좀 나가 주시겠소?\"\n\n\"이보게나,\" 선장이 목멘 떨리는 목소리로, 그 묵직한 주먹에 묘한 움직임이 일어나는 채로 헐떡였다. \"자네한테 하고 싶은 말이 많네만, 그것들이 지금 당장은 어디 처박혀 있는지 영 모르겠네. 내 젊은 친구 월이, 내 셈으로는 바로 지난밤에 물에 빠져 죽었으니, 그래서 내가 정신이 없네, 알겠나. 하지만 자네와 나는 다시 한번 나란히 마주 서게 될 걸세, 이 사람아,\" 선장이 갈고리를 치켜들며 말했다. \"우리가 살아 있다면 말이야.\"\n\n\"그렇게 된다면, 이 양반아, 그건 당신한테 결코 영리한 일이 못 될 거요,\" 지배인이 똑같은 솔직함으로 응수했다. \"장담컨대, 분명히 경고하는데, 내가 반드시 당신을 적발하고 폭로할 테니까. 나는 내 이웃들보다 더 도덕적인 사람인 척하지는 않소, 이 양반아. 하지만 내 눈과 귀가 살아 있는 한, 이 회사의, 또는 이 회사 구성원 누구의 신뢰가 악용되고 잠식당하게 두지는 않을 거요. 그럼 안녕히 가시오!\" 카커 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n커틀 선장은 그를 똑바로 바라보며(카커 씨도 꼭 그만큼 똑바로 선장을 바라보았다) 사무실 밖으로 나갔고, 그를 난롯불 앞에 두 다리를 벌리고 선 채로 남겨 두었다. 그는 마치 자기 영혼에도, 자기의 순백 아마포나 매끄럽고 윤기 흐르는 살갗만큼이나 한 점 얼룩도 없다는 듯 태연하고 흐뭇한 모습이었다.\n\n바깥 회계실을 지나면서 선장은, 가엾은 월터가 늘 앉곤 하던 책상을 흘끗 보았는데, 이제 거기는 다른 어린 소년이 차지하고 있었다. 그 얼굴은 자그마한 뒤채 거실에서 옛 마데이라 술 마지막에서 두 번째 그 유명한 병의 마개를 따던 날의 월터 얼굴만큼이나 싱싱하고 희망에 차 있었다. 이렇게 깨어난 한 무리의 생각들은 선장에게 큰 위안이 되었다. 그것은 분노가 한창 치솟은 순간에도 그를 누그러뜨렸고, 그의 눈에 눈물이 차오르게 했다.\n\n다시 나무 항해사에게로 돌아와, 어두운 가게 한구석에 앉으니, 선장의 분노는, 그토록 강했건만, 그의 슬픔에 도무지 맞설 수가 없었다. 격정은 죽은 이의 기억에 부당하고 난폭한 짓을 하는 듯할 뿐 아니라, 죽음에 감염되어 그 곁에서 시들고 사그라드는 것 같았다. 세상의 살아 있는 온갖 악당과 거짓말쟁이들도, 죽은 친구 한 사람의 정직과 진실에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다.\n\n이런 마음 상태에서 정직한 선장이 월터를 잃었다는 것 말고 분명히 깨달은 단 한 가지는, 그와 함께 커틀 선장의 세계가 거의 통째로 물에 빠져 버렸다는 것이었다. 월터의 그 천진한 속임수를 한때나마 묵인했던 것을 두고 가끔, 그것도 통렬하게 자책하긴 했지만, 그는 적어도 그만큼 자주, 어떤 바다도 결코 돌려보내 줄 수 없는 카커 씨를 생각했고, 이제 그도 마찬가지로 인간의 부름이 닿지 않을 만큼 멀리 가 버렸음을 깨닫기 시작한 돔비 씨를 생각했으며, 다시는 함께 어울릴 수 없게 된 \"마음의 기쁨\"을 생각했고, 바위에 좌초하여 운율의 널빤지와 들보로 쪼개져 버린, 티크로 단단히 짜인 그 말끔한 노래 \"사랑스러운 페그\"를 생각했다. 선장은 어두운 가게에 앉아, 자기가 입은 상처는 전혀 안중에 두지 않은 채 이런 것들을 생각했고, 마치 떠내려가는 그 실제 잔해들을 바라보기라도 하듯 슬픈 눈으로 바닥을 내려다보았다.\n\n그러나 그러면서도 선장은, 가엾은 월터를 기리는 점잖고 차분한 추모의 의식을, 자기 힘이 닿는 한 잊지 않았다. 기운을 차리고, 그라인더 롭(이 부자연스러운 황혼 속에서 곤히 잠들어 있던)을 깨워서, 선장은 자기 시종을 뒤에 거느리고 문 열쇠를 주머니에 넣은 채 밖으로 나섰다. 그리고 런던 동쪽 끝에 가면 얼마든지 골라잡을 수 있는 그 편리한 헌옷 가게들 가운데 한 곳으로 가서, 그 자리에서 상복 두 벌을 샀다—하나는 그라인더 롭의 것으로 엄청나게 작았고, 하나는 자기 것으로 엄청나게 컸다. 그는 또 롭에게 모자 비슷한 것을 마련해 주었는데, 균형미와 실용성으로 보나, 뱃사람과 석탄 운반부가 절묘하게 어우러진 점으로 보나 크게 감탄할 만한 것으로, 흔히 사우웨스터라 불리는 것이었다. 그것은 기구 장사와 관련해서는 다소 색다른 물건이었다. 각자의 옷을 차려입으니—파는 자가 단언하기로는, 어쩌다 운 좋은 사정들이 드물게 맞아떨어진 것 외에는 도무지 빚어질 수 없는 맞음새의 기적이요, 그 맵시로 말하자면 가장 나이 든 주민의 기억으로도 견줄 데 없는 것이라 했다—선장과 그라인더는 즉시 그것들을 떨쳐입었고, 그것을 바라보는 모든 이에게 경이로 가득 찬 광경을 연출했다.\n* 사우웨스터: 차양이 뒤로 길게 늘어진, 방수 처리된 뱃사람용 모자.\n\n이렇게 달라진 모습으로 선장은 투츠 씨를 맞이했다. \"지금은 내가 어찌할 바를 모르겠네, 이 사람아,\" 선장이 말했다. \"그러니 그저 그 나쁜 소식을 확실하다고 일러 줄 수밖에. 그 젊은 여자한테 아가씨에게 부드럽게 알리라고 전해 주게. 그리고 둘 다 다시는 나를 생각하지 말라고—특히, 잘 듣게, 그게 중요한데—하지만 밤이 허리케인으로 닥쳐오고 바다가 산처럼 굽이칠 때면 나는 그들을 생각할 걸세. 그건 자네 닥터 와츠를 찾아보게, 형제여, 그리고 찾거든 거기에 한마디 적어 두게.\"\n\n선장은 투츠 씨의 우정 제안에 대한 검토는 좀 더 적당한 때로 미뤄 두고서, 그를 그렇게 돌려보냈다. 사실 커틀 선장의 기운이 어찌나 가라앉았던지, 그날 그는 맥스팅거 부인의 기습에 맞선 더 이상의 예방책은 일절 취하지 않고, 오히려 무모하게 운에 자기 자신을 내맡긴 채 무슨 일이 일어나든 개의치 않기로 거의 마음을 굳히다시피 했다. 그러나 저녁이 다가오자 그는 한결 나은 마음 상태가 되었고, 그라인더 롭에게 월터에 관해 많은 이야기를 했다. 그러면서 곁들여 롭의 주의 깊음과 충실함도 칭찬했다. 롭은 선장이 진지하게 자기를 칭찬하는 것을 듣고도 부끄러워하기는커녕, 그를 빤히 쳐다보며 앉아, 공감하는 척 훌쩍이고, 착한 척 시늉을 하면서, (어린 첩자답게) 그가 하는 말 한마디 한마디를 매우 장래가 촉망되는 교활함으로 차곡차곡 쌓아 두었다.\n\n롭이 잠자리에 들어 곤히 잠들자, 선장은 촛불 심지를 다듬고 안경을 썼다—기구 장사에 들어선 마당에 안경을 쓰는 것이 어울린다고 여겼던 것이다, 비록 그의 눈은 매의 눈 같았지만—그러고는 기도서를 장례 예식 부분에서 펼쳤다. 그리하여 자그마한 뒤채 거실에서 혼자 나직이 읽어 내려가며, 이따금 멈추어 눈물을 훔치면서, 선장은 참되고 소박한 마음으로 월터의 몸을 깊은 바다에 맡겼다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII. Contrasts\n\n우리의 눈을 두 집으로 돌려 보자. 나란히 붙어 있지는 않고 멀리 떨어져 있으나, 둘 다 런던이라는 대도시에서 어렵지 않게 닿고 미칠 수 있는 거리에 있는 집들이다.\n\n첫 번째 집은 노우드 근처의 푸르고 숲이 우거진 시골에 자리하고 있다. 그것은 대저택은 아니며, 크기에 있어 거창함을 내세우지도 않는다. 그러나 아름답게 꾸며져 있고, 품위 있게 관리되고 있다. 잔디밭, 부드럽고 매끄러운 비탈, 화원, 우아한 자태의 물푸레나무와 버드나무가 빠지지 않는 나무 무리, 온실, 향기로운 덩굴식물이 기둥을 휘감은 시골풍 베란다, 소박한 집의 외관, 잘 정돈된 부속 건물들—이 모든 것이 그저 한 채의 오두막에나 어울릴 자그마한 규모이긴 하나, 안에는 궁전에나 쓰일 법한 우아한 안락함이 어느 정도 갖추어져 있음을 말해 준다. 이 짐작은 근거 없는 것이 아니니, 안으로 들어가 보면 그곳은 세련됨과 호사로 가득한 집이다. 훌륭하게 어우러진 풍부한 색채가 어느 쪽으로 눈을 돌려도 마주친다. 작은 방들의 형태와 크기에 꼭 맞게 빼어나게 고안된 가구에서, 벽에서, 바닥에서, 그리고 여기저기 별난 유리문과 창문으로 들어오는 빛에 색을 입히고 그 빛을 누그러뜨리는 데서도. 정선된 판화와 그림도 몇 점 있고, 고풍스러운 구석진 곳과 우묵한 자리에는 책이 부족하지 않으며, 탁자 위에는 기술과 운을 겨루는 놀이들이 펼쳐져 있다—기이한 체스 말들, 주사위, 백개먼, 카드, 그리고 당구.\n\n그러나 이런 안락의 풍요로움 가운데에도, 전체적인 분위기에는 어딘가 온당치 않은 무언가가 있다. 양탄자와 방석이 너무 부드럽고 소리 없는 탓에, 그 사이를 거닐거나 그 위에서 쉬는 이들이 마치 몰래 행동하는 듯 보여서일까? 판화와 그림들이 위대한 사상이나 행적을 기리지도 않고, 풍경이나 저택이나 오두막의 시정(詩情)으로 자연을 담아내지도 않으며, 오로지 관능적인 한 가지 색조로만—그저 형태와 색채의 과시에 불과하고 그 이상은 아닌 것으로만—이루어져서일까? 책들이 전부 겉만 금박을 입혔고, 그 대부분의 제목이 판화와 그림의 동무가 되기에나 알맞아서일까? 이곳의 완전함과 아름다움이 군데군데, 대수롭지 않고 돈도 들지 않는 어떤 면에서 겸손을 가장함으로써 배반당하고 있어서일까—그 가장이 저기 걸려 있는, 너무도 진실되게 그려진 초상화의 얼굴만큼이나, 아니 그 아래 안락의자에 앉아 아침을 들고 있는 그 초상의 실제 인물만큼이나 거짓되어서? 아니면, 이곳의 그 실제 인물이자 이 모든 것의 주인이 날마다 내쉬는 숨결과 더불어, 그 자신의 어떤 미묘한 일부가 흘러나와, 주위의 모든 것에 그 자신을 어렴풋이 드러내고 있어서일까?\n\n안락의자에 앉아 있는 이는 지배인 카커 씨다. 탁자 위의 번쩍이는 새장 안에서 화려한 앵무새 한 마리가 부리로 철사를 물어뜯고, 둥근 천장 꼭대기에서 거꾸로 매달려 걷다가 제 집을 흔들며 꽥꽥거린다. 그러나 카커 씨는 그 새에 무심한 채, 사색에 잠긴 미소를 머금고 맞은편 벽의 그림을 바라본다.\n\n\"참으로 기이한 우연의 닮음이로군, 그래.\" 그가 말한다.\n\n아마도 유노일 것이다. 아마도 \"보디발의 아내\"이거나, 아마도 어떤 경멸 어린 님프일 것이다—그림 상인들이 시장 형편을 보아 그것에 이름을 붙였을 테니까. 그것은 더없이 아름다운 한 여인의 모습으로, 몸은 돌아서 있으나 얼굴은 보는 이를 향한 채, 그에게 거만한 시선을 번뜩이고 있다.\n\n그것은 이디스를 닮았다.\n\n그가 그 그림을 향해 한 손으로 스치듯 몸짓을 한다—저것이 무엇인가! 위협인가? 아니다. 그러나 그것과 비슷한 무엇이다. 승리의 손짓 같은 것인가? 아니다. 그러나 그것에 더 가깝다. 그의 입술에서 띄워 보낸 무례한 인사인가? 아니다. 그러나 그것과도 비슷하다—그는 다시 아침을 들며, 안달하며 갇혀 있는 새를 부른다. 새는 새장 안에 매달린, 커다란 결혼반지 같은 금빛 고리 속으로 내려와 그 안에서 그네를 타며 그를 즐겁게 한다.\n\n두 번째 집은 런던의 반대편에 있는데, 지난날의 분주하던 큰 북부 가도가 이제는 조용하고, 발로 터덜터덜 걸어가는 나그네들 말고는 거의 버려진 곳 가까이에 있다. 그것은 빈약하고 자그마한 집으로, 가구도 변변찮고 듬성듬성하나 매우 깨끗하다. 게다가 현관 둘레와 좁은 정원에 정성껏 가꾼 소박한 꽃들에서 보이듯, 그곳을 꾸미려는 시도마저 엿보인다. 그 집이 서 있는 동네는 도시다운 면이 적은 만큼이나 시골다운 면도 추천할 것이 별로 없다. 그곳은 도시도 아니고 시골도 아니다. 도시는, 여행용 장화를 신은 거인처럼, 성큼 한 걸음을 내디뎌 그곳을 지나쳐 버렸고, 벽돌과 회반죽으로 된 발뒤꿈치를 한참 앞에 디뎌 놓았다. 그러나 그 거인의 두 발 사이에 낀 중간 지대는 아직 황폐해진 시골일 뿐 도시는 아니다. 그리고 이곳, 밤낮으로 종일 연기를 토해 내는 몇 개의 높다란 굴뚝들 사이에, 벽돌 가마터와 잔디를 떠낸 좁은 길들 사이에, 울타리가 무너져 내리고, 먼지 낀 쐐기풀이 자라고, 산울타리 자투리 한두 조각이 아직 보이며, 매번 다시는 오지 않겠노라 맹세하면서도 여전히 이따금 찾아오는 새잡이가 드나드는 그 사이에—이 두 번째 집이 자리하고 있다.\n\n그곳에 사는 여인은, 버림받은 오라비를 향한 헌신으로 첫 번째 집을 떠난 바로 그 여인이다. 그녀는 그 집에서 그곳을 구원하던 영혼을 거두어 갔고, 그 집 주인의 가슴에서 그의 유일한 천사를 거두어 갔다. 그러나 그가 배은망덕한 모욕이라 여기는 그 일이 있은 뒤로 그녀를 향한 그의 애정은 사라지고, 그 보복으로 그가 그녀를 완전히 저버리긴 했어도, 그녀에 대한 옛 정념마저 그에게서 아주 잊힌 것은 아니다. 그가 결코 발을 들이지는 않으면서도, 그의 그 값비싼 온갖 개조 가운데에서도, 마치 그녀가 어제 막 떠나기라도 한 듯 여전히 가꾸어지고 있는 그녀의 화원이 그 증거가 되게 하라!\n\n해리엇 카커는 그때 이후로 변했고, 그녀의 아름다움에는, 세월이 제아무리 전능하다 한들 그 홀로의 힘만으로 드리울 수 있는 것보다 더 무거운 그늘이 내려앉았다—근심과 슬픔의 그늘, 그리고 가난한 삶의 나날의 분투라는 그늘이. 그러나 그것은 여전히 아름다움이며, 여전히 온화하고 조용하며 수줍은 아름다움이어서, 스스로를 자랑할 수 없기에 찾아내야만 하는 아름다움이다. 만일 그것이 자랑할 수 있었다면, 그것은 지금의 그것에 지나지 않았을 것이다.\n\n그렇다. 이 가냘프고, 작고, 참을성 있는 모습은, 소박한 천으로 단정하게 차려입고, 영웅적이거나 위대하다는 통념과는 거의 공통점이 없는 따분한 살림의 미덕 말고는 아무것도 드러내지 않는다—다만 그러한 미덕의 한 줄기 빛이 이 땅의 위인들의 삶을 뚫고 비쳐 들 때, 그것은 하나의 별자리가 되어 곧장 하늘에서 자취가 좇아지긴 하지만 말이다—이 가냘프고, 작고, 참을성 있는 모습이, 아직 젊으나 지치고 머리가 센 한 남자에게 기대어 선 채, 온 세상 가운데 오직 그녀만이 그가 치욕에 빠졌을 때 그에게로 건너가 그의 손에 자기 손을 얹고, 달콤한 침착함과 결연함으로 그 메마른 길을 따라 희망차게 그를 이끌어 온, 그의 누이다.\n\n\"이르네요, 존.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어째서 이렇게 일찍 나가세요?\"\n\n\"평소보다 그리 많이 이른 건 아니야, 해리엇. 시간이 좀 남는다면, 내 생각엔—그저 변덕이지만—그와 작별을 나눈 그 집 앞을 한 번 걸어 보고 싶어서.\"\n\n\"저도 그분을 한 번이라도 뵙거나 알았더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 존.\"\n\n\"이대로가 더 낫단다, 얘야, 그의 운명을 생각하면.\"\n\n\"하지만 그분을 알았다 한들 제가 더 안타까워할 수도 없었을 거예요. 오라버니의 슬픔이 제 슬픔이 아닌가요? 그리고 제가 그분을 알았더라면, 어쩌면 그분에 대해 이야기할 때 제가 지금보다는 오라버니에게 더 나은 동무가 되어 드린다고 느끼셨을지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"내 더없이 사랑하는 누이야! 기쁨이든 회한이든, 그 어떤 것에서든 네가 함께해 주리라는 것을 내가 확신하지 못하는 것이 있겠느냐?\"\n\n\"없다고 생각하시길 바라요, 존. 정말로 아무것도 없으니까요!\"\n\n\"그러니 어떻게 네가 지금 이 일에서, 아니 어떤 일에서든, 지금의 너보다 내게 더 잘해 주거나 더 가까워질 수 있겠느냐?\" 그녀의 오라비가 말했다. \"나는 네가 그를 알았다고, 그리고 그를 향한 내 감정을 함께 나누었다고 느낀단다, 해리엇.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 어깨에 얹혀 있던 손을 그의 목으로 둘러 감으며, 다소 머뭇거리며 대답했다.\n\"아니요, 꼭 그렇지는 않아요.\"\n\n\"맞아, 맞아!\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 나 자신에게 그를 더 잘 알도록 허락했더라면, 내가 그에게 해를 끼치지 않았으리라고 생각하는 거지?\"\n\n\"생각이라뇨! 저는 알아요.\"\n\n\"일부러였다면, 하늘이 알겠지만 나는 결코 그러지 않았을 거다.\" 그가 슬프게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"그러나 그의 평판은 너무도 소중해서, 그런 교제로 위태롭게 할 수는 없었어. 네가 그 생각을 함께하든 안 하든, 얘야—\"\n\n\"저는 함께하지 않아요.\" 그녀가 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"그래도 그건 여전히 진실이란다, 해리엇. 그리고 그때 내 마음을 그토록 무겁게 했던 바로 그 일을 두고 그를 생각하면, 이제 내 마음은 한결 가벼워진다.\" 그는 우울한 어조를 거두고, \"잘 있어!\" 하고 말하며 그녀에게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"잘 다녀오세요, 사랑하는 존! 저녁에, 늘 그 시간 그 자리에서, 평소처럼 돌아오시는 길에 오라버니를 마중할게요. 다녀오세요.\"\n\n그에게 입을 맞추려 들어 올린 그녀의 다정한 얼굴은 그의 집이요, 그의 삶이요, 그의 우주였으나, 동시에 그것은 그가 받는 벌과 슬픔의 한 부분이기도 했다. 그가 그 얼굴 위에서 본 구름 속에서—해 질 녘의 어떤 빛나는 구름만큼이나 고요하고 잔잔한 구름이었지만—그리고 그녀의 삶의 한결같음과 헌신 속에서, 또 그녀가 안락과 즐거움과 희망을 희생한 데서, 그는 자신의 옛 죄가 맺은 쓰디쓴 열매가 영원히 익어 가고 신선하게 맺혀 있는 것을 보았던 것이다.\n\n그가 그들 집 앞에 놓인, 칙칙하고 울퉁불퉁한 땅뙈기를 가로질러 가는 동안, 그녀는 두 손을 헐겁게 맞잡은 채 문가에 서서 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 그 땅은 한때(그리 오래지 않은 옛날) 기분 좋은 풀밭이었으나, 이제는 완전한 황무지가 되어, 누추한 집들이 쓰레기 더미에서 솟아나기 시작한 어수선한 무리를 이루고 있었으니, 마치 거기에 서투르게 씨를 뿌려 놓은 것만 같았다. 그가 뒤돌아볼 때마다—한두 번 그랬다—그녀의 다정한 얼굴은 그의 가슴을 비추는 한 줄기 빛처럼 빛났다. 그러나 그가 다시 터덜터덜 제 길을 가며 그녀를 보지 못할 때면, 그를 지켜보며 서 있는 그녀의 눈에는 눈물이 고였다.\n\n그녀의 시름에 잠긴 모습은 오래도록 문가에서 한가로이 머물러 있지는 않았다. 날마다 해야 할 의무가 있었고, 날마다 해야 할 일이 있었으니—영웅적이지 못한 그런 평범한 영혼들은 흔히 제 손으로 고되게 일하는 법이다—해리엇은 곧 집안일로 분주해졌다. 그 일들을 다 마치고 가난한 집을 아주 말끔하고 정연하게 해 놓은 뒤, 그녀는 근심 어린 얼굴로 얼마 안 되는 돈을 헤아리고는, 식탁에 올릴 필수품 몇 가지를 사러 생각에 잠긴 채 나갔다. 가는 길에 어떻게 아낄지 궁리하고 꾀를 짜내면서. 그런 미천한 천성을 지닌 이들의 삶이란 이토록 구차하니, 그들은 제 하인이나 시녀에게 영웅이 못 될 뿐 아니라, 영웅 노릇을 할 하인도 시녀도 애초에 두지 못한 것이다!\n\n그녀가 집을 비우고 안에 아무도 없는 동안, 오라비가 갔던 길과는 다른 길로 그 집에 한 신사가 다가왔다. 한창때를 어쩌면 조금 지난 듯하나, 건강하게 혈색 좋은 안색에, 꼿꼿한 자태와, 밝고 맑은 풍모를 지녔으니, 그것은 우아하고 쾌활한 인상을 주었다. 그의 눈썹은 아직 검었고 머리카락도 상당 부분이 검었는데, 머리카락 사이로 군데군데 비치는 흰빛이 눈썹을 한결 돋보이게 했고, 그의 넓고 솔직한 이마와 정직한 눈을 크게 살려 주었다.\n\n이 신사는 문을 한 번 두드렸으나 아무 응답이 없자, 자그마한 현관의 긴 의자에 앉아 기다렸다. 그가 몇 소절을 흥얼거리며 옆자리의 좌석을 두드려 박자를 맞출 때, 손가락을 놀리는 어떤 능숙한 동작은 그가 음악가임을 드러내는 듯했다. 그리고 알아챌 만한 곡조라곤 없는, 매우 느리고 긴 무언가를 흥얼거리는 데서 그가 얻는 그 유별난 만족감은, 그가 학식 있는 음악가임을 드러내는 듯했다.\n\n그 신사가 여전히 어떤 선율을 빙빙 돌리고 있을 때—그것은 빙빙 돌고 또 돌고, 안으로 들고 또 들며, 탁자 위에서 돌려진 타래송곳처럼 제 몸을 휘감을 뿐, 어디에도 조금도 가까이 다가가지 못하는 듯했다—해리엇이 돌아오는 모습이 보였다. 그녀가 다가오자 그는 일어서서, 모자를 벗은 채로 섰다.\n\n\"또 오셨군요, 선생님!\" 그녀가 더듬거리며 말했다.\n\n\"외람된 줄 압니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"잠시 오 분만 시간을 내주실 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n잠시 망설인 뒤, 그녀는 문을 열고 그를 자그마한 응접실로 들였다. 신사는 거기 앉아 의자를 끌어다 그녀 맞은편 탁자 곁에 두고, 그의 외모와 완벽하게 어울리는 목소리로, 매우 마음을 끄는 꾸밈없음으로 말했다.\n\n\"해리엇 양, 당신은 오만할 수가 없는 분입니다. 지난번 아침에 제가 찾아왔을 때, 당신은 제게 오만하다고 내비치셨지요. 하지만 용서하십시오, 당신이 말씀하시는 동안 저는 당신의 얼굴을 들여다보았는데, 그 얼굴이 당신의 말을 반박하고 있었습니다. 다시 들여다보아도,\" 그가 그녀의 팔에 잠시 한 손을 부드럽게 얹으며 덧붙였다. \"그 얼굴은 당신의 말을 점점 더 반박하는군요.\"\n\n그녀는 다소 당황하고 동요하여, 선뜻 대답을 하지 못했다.\n\n\"그것은 진실의 거울입니다.\" 그녀의 방문객이 말했다. \"그리고 온화함의 거울이지요. 제가 그것을 믿고 다시 찾아온 것을 용서해 주십시오.\"\n\n이 말을 하는 그의 태도는 그 말에서 칭찬이라는 성격을 완전히 벗겨 냈다. 그것은 너무도 꾸밈없고, 진지하며, 가식 없고, 진실되어서, 그녀는 그에게 감사하는 동시에 그의 진심을 인정하기라도 하듯 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"우리 둘의 나이 차이와,\" 신사가 말했다. \"제 용건의 솔직함이, 제 마음을 그대로 말할 수 있게 해 주니, 다행스럽게 생각합니다. 그것이 제 마음입니다. 그래서 이렇게 두 번째로 저를 보시는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"선생님, 한 가지 종류의 오만이 있어요,\" 그녀가 잠시 침묵한 뒤 대답했다. \"혹은 오만이라 여겨질 만한 것이지만, 그저 의무일 뿐인 것이지요. 저는 그 밖에 다른 오만은 품지 않았기를 바랍니다.\"\n\"당신 자신을 위한 것 말이지요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"제 자신을 위한 것이에요.\"\n\n\"하지만—용서하십시오—\" 신사가 넌지시 말했다. \"당신의 오라버니 존을 위한 것은요?\"\n\n\"그분의 사랑을 자랑스러워한답니다,\" 해리엇이 방문객을 똑바로 바라보며, 순식간에 태도를 바꾸어 말했다—덜 침착하거나 덜 조용해진 것이 아니라, 거기에 깊고 격정적인 진지함이 담겨, 그녀의 떨리는 목소리마저 그녀의 굳건함의 일부가 되었다—\"그리고 그분을 자랑스러워해요. 선생님, 이상하게도 그분의 인생 이야기를 아시고, 지난번에 오셨을 때 그것을 제게 되풀이해 들려주신 분이여—\"\n\n\"그저 당신의 신뢰 속으로 들어가는 길을 찾으려는 것이었을 뿐입니다.\" 신사가 끼어들었다. \"제발, 그렇게 생각지 마십시오—\"\n\n\"분명히,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"선생님은 친절하고 선한 뜻으로 그 이야기를 제 앞에서 되살려 주셨어요. 저는 그것을 확신해요.\"\n\n\"감사합니다.\" 그녀의 방문객이 그녀의 손을 황급히 쥐며 대답했다. \"정말 고맙습니다. 당신은 저를 공정하게 봐 주시는군요, 정말로요. 당신은, 존 카커의 인생 이야기를 아는 제가—라고 말씀하시려던 참이었지요.\"\n\n\"제가 그분을 자랑스러워한다고 말할 때, 그것을 제 오만이라 여기실지도 모른다고요!\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"저는 자랑스러워요. 그렇지 못했던 때가 있었음을 아실 거예요—그럴 수 없었던 때가요—하지만 그건 지나갔어요. 여러 해에 걸친 겸허함, 불평 없는 속죄, 참된 뉘우침, 끔찍한 회한, 제 애정 속에서마저 그분이 느끼는 고통—그분은 그 애정이 제게 큰 대가를 치르게 했다고 여기지만, 하늘은 아실 거예요, 저는 행복하다는 것을, 다만 그분의 슬픔이 아니었다면요—오, 선생님, 제가 보아 온 것을 겪고 나서 간곡히 청하건대, 혹 선생님께서 어떤 권력의 자리에 계시고 언젠가 부당한 일을 당하시더라도, 그 어떤 잘못에 대해서도, 다시는 되돌릴 수 없는 벌은 결코 내리지 마세요. 그분이 지으신 마음에 변화를 일으키실 하느님이 우리 위에 계신 동안에는요.\"\n\n\"당신의 오라버니는 변한 사람입니다.\" 신사가 동정 어린 어조로 대답했다. \"그것을 의심치 않는다고 분명히 말씀드립니다.\"\n\n\"잘못을 저질렀을 때도 그분은 변한 사람이었어요.\" 해리엇이 말했다. \"그분은 다시 변한 사람이 되었고, 이제는 본래의 참된 자신이에요, 믿어 주세요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"하지만 우리는 그저 살아갈 뿐이지요.\" 그녀의 방문객이 한 손으로 멍하니 이마를 문지르더니, 생각에 잠겨 탁자를 두드리며 말했다. \"우리는 날마다 시계태엽 같은 일상 속에서 그저 살아가며, 이런 변화를 알아채지도, 좇아가지도 못합니다. 그것은—그것은 형이상학적인 종류의 일이지요. 우리에겐—우리에겐 그럴 여유가 없습니다. 우리에겐—우리에겐 용기가 없어요. 그런 건 학교나 대학에서 가르쳐 주지도 않고, 우리는 그것을 어떻게 시작해야 할지도 모릅니다. 요컨대, 우리는 지독히도 사무적인 인간들이라서요.\" 신사가 창가로 갔다가 돌아와, 극도의 불만과 짜증에 휩싸인 채 다시 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"분명히,\" 신사가 다시 이마를 문지르며, 앞서처럼 탁자를 두드리며 말했다. \"날마다 똑같은, 터덜터덜 굴러가는 인생이라면 무엇에든 사람을 길들여 버리리라고 믿을 만한 충분한 까닭이 제겐 있습니다. 아무것도 보지 못하고, 아무것도 듣지 못하고, 아무것도 알지 못하지요. 그게 사실입니다. 우리는 모든 걸 당연한 것으로 여기며 그저 살아가고, 그렇게 살아가다 보면, 우리가 무엇을 하든, 선하든 악하든 그저 그렇든, 습관에서 하게 됩니다. 제가 임종의 자리에서 제 양심 앞에 변론하라는 요구를 받을 때, 보고할 것이라고는 습관뿐일 겁니다. '습관 때문이오,' 하고 저는 말하겠지요. '나는 백만 가지 일에 대해 습관 때문에 귀먹고, 벙어리가 되고, 눈멀고, 마비되어 있었소.' 그러면 양심은 이렇게 말하겠지요. '참으로 사무적이시군요, 댁의 이름이 뭐든 간에 양반. 하지만 여기서는 그게 통하지 않아요!'\"\n\n신사는 일어나 다시 창가로 갔다가 돌아왔다. 자기의 불안을 이런 독특한 방식으로 표현하기는 했어도, 진지하게 마음이 편치 않은 것이었다.\n\n\"해리엇 양,\" 그가 다시 의자로 돌아오며 말했다. \"당신께 도움이 되도록 허락해 주셨으면 합니다. 저를 보십시오. 저는 정직해 보일 겁니다. 적어도 지금만큼은 제가 정직하다는 걸 아니까요. 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"당신이 하신 말씀을 한마디 한마디 다 믿습니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"저는 자책으로 가득합니다. 지난 십수 년 동안 언제든 이것을 알고 이것을 볼 수 있었을 텐데, 당신을 알고 당신을 볼 수 있었을 텐데, 한 번도 그러지 못했으니까요. 제가 어떻게 여기까지 왔는지조차 잘 모르겠습니다—저라는 인간은, 제 자신의 습관뿐 아니라 남들의 습관에 매인 피조물이니 말입니다! 하지만 이렇게 온 이상, 무언가 하게 해 주십시오. 온전한 경의와 존중을 담아 청합니다. 당신은 그 두 가지를 제게 더없이 깊이 불러일으키십니다. 무언가 하게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"저희는 만족하고 있어요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 아니에요, 꼭 그렇지는 않지요.\" 신사가 대답했다. \"꼭 그렇지는 않다고 생각합니다. 당신의 삶과 그분의 삶을 한결 편하게 해 줄 자그마한 안락들이 있지요. 그분의 삶도요!\" 그가 그 말이 그녀에게 어떤 인상을 준 듯하다고 여기며 되풀이했다. \"저는 그분을 위해 해 줄 일이라곤 아무것도 없다고, 다 끝나고 결판난 일이라고 여기는 데 익숙해 있었습니다. 요컨대 그것에 대해 아예 생각하지 않는 데 익숙했지요. 이제는 다릅니다. 그분을 위해 무언가 하게 해 주십시오. 당신도요.\" 방문객이 조심스러운 섬세함으로 말했다. \"당신도 그분을 위해 건강을 면밀히 살피셔야 합니다. 한데 제가 보기엔 그 건강이 쇠해 가고 있군요.\"\n\n\"선생님이 어떤 분이시든,\" 해리엇이 그의 얼굴로 눈을 들어 올리며 대답했다. \"저는 선생님께 깊이 감사드려요. 선생님이 하시는 모든 말씀에 저희를 향한 친절 말고는 이 세상 어떤 다른 목적도 없다는 것을 확신해요. 하지만 저희가 이 삶을 시작한 지도 여러 해가 지났어요. 그리고 그분을 제게 그토록 사랑스럽게 만들고, 그분의 더 나은 결심을 그토록 증명해 준 것의 어느 한 부분이라도 제 오라버니에게서 빼앗는 것—그 누구의 도움도 없이, 남모르게, 잊힌 채로 이룬 속죄의 그 공의 한 조각이라도 덜어 내는 것—그것은, 방금 선생님께서 말씀하신 그때가 저희 각자에게 닥쳐올 때, 그분과 제게 위안이 될 그것을 줄어들게 하는 일이 될 거예요. 저는 어떤 말보다 이 눈물로 더 잘 감사드립니다. 부디 그것을 믿어 주세요.\"\n\n신사는 감동하여, 그녀가 내민 손을 입술로 가져갔으니, 자상한 아버지가 효성스러운 자식의 손에 입 맞추는 것과도 같았다. 다만 그보다 더 경건하게.\n\n\"만일 언젠가,\" 해리엇이 말했다. \"그분이 잃었던 지위에 얼마간이라도 회복되는 날이 온다면—\"\n\n\"회복이라뇨!\" 신사가 재빨리 외쳤다. \"어떻게 그것을 바랄 수 있겠습니까? 그 어떤 회복의 권한이 누구의 손에 있단 말입니까? 그분이 자기 인생의 그 값을 매길 수 없는 축복을 얻었다는 것이, 그분의 형이 그분에게 보이는 적의의 한 가지 원인이라고 짐작하는 것이, 분명 제 착각은 아니겠지요.\"\n\n\"우리 사이에서, 우리 둘 사이에서조차 결코 입에 올린 적 없는 일을 건드리시는군요.\" 해리엇이 말했다.\n\n\"용서를 구합니다.\" 방문객이 말했다. \"제가 그것을 알았어야 했는데요. 부디 제가 무심코 그랬던 것을 잊어 주시기를 간청합니다. 그리고 이제, 더는 감히 졸라서는 안 되니—제게 그럴 권리가 있는지도 확신하지 못하니—하기야 하늘은 아시겠지만, 그 의구심마저도 습관일지 모르지요.\" 신사가 앞서처럼 낙심한 듯 머리를 문지르며 말했다. \"이방인이긴 하나 이방인이 아닌 사람으로서, 두 가지 청을 드리게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"무엇인가요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"첫째는, 혹 당신이 결심을 바꿀 까닭이 생긴다면, 저를 당신의 오른팔로 삼아 주시는 겁니다. 그때는 제 이름이 당신께 쓰일 것입니다. 지금은 쓸모없고, 늘 보잘것없는 이름이지만요.\"\n\n\"저희가 벗을 고를 폭이,\" 그녀가 희미하게 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"그리 넓지 않으니, 따로 생각할 시간이 필요치 않아요. 그것은 약속드릴 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"둘째는, 이따금, 이를테면 월요일 아침마다 아홉 시에—또 습관이군요—저는 사무적이어야 하니까요,\" 신사가 그 점을 두고 자신과 다투려는 익살스러운 마음으로 말했다. \"지나가는 길에, 당신을 문가나 창가에서 보게 해 주시는 겁니다. 들어가게 해 달라는 게 아닙니다, 그 시간이면 당신 오라버니는 나가고 없을 테니까요. 당신과 말을 나누게 해 달라는 것도 아닙니다. 저는 그저, 제 마음 한 자락이 편안하도록, 당신이 잘 지내고 있음을 보고 싶을 뿐이고, 거슬리지 않게, 저를 봄으로써 당신께 한 사람의 벗이—이미 머리가 세고 점점 더 빠르게 세어 가는, 나이 든 벗이, 당신이 언제든 부릴 수 있는 그런 벗이 있음을 일깨워 드리고 싶을 뿐입니다.\"\n\n그 다정한 얼굴이 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았고, 그것을 믿었고, 그러겠노라 약속했다.\n\n\"앞서와 마찬가지로,\" 신사가 일어서며 말했다. \"당신은 제 방문을 존 카커에게 말하지 않으시려는 것으로 압니다. 제가 그의 내력을 안다는 것에 그가 조금이라도 마음이 상할까 봐서요. 그것이 다행입니다, 그것이 통상적인 일의 순서에서 벗어난 것이니까요. 또—이게 다 습관이군요!\" 신사가 짜증스럽게 자제하며 말했다. \"통상적인 순서보다 더 나은 순서란 없기라도 한 듯이 말이지요!\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 가려고 돌아서, 모자를 벗은 채로 자그마한 현관 밖으로 걸어 나가, 그 어떤 교양으로도 가르칠 수 없고, 그 어떤 진실됨으로도 의심할 수 없으며, 오로지 순수하고 한결같은 마음만이 드러낼 수 있는, 거리낌 없는 경의와 가식 없는 관심이 어우러진 그 행복한 태도로 그녀에게 작별을 고했다.\n\n이 방문으로 그 누이의 마음속에는 반쯤 잊혔던 숱한 감정이 깨어났다. 다른 어떤 방문객이 그들의 문턱을 넘어온 지가 너무도 오래되었고, 그 어떤 무심함 아닌 목소리가 그녀의 귀에 슬픈 가락을 들려준 지도 너무도 오래되어서, 그 이방인의 모습은 몇 시간이 지나 그녀가 창가에 앉아 바늘을 놀릴 때에도 여전히 그녀 앞에 어른거렸고, 그의 말은 거듭거듭 새로이 들려오는 듯했다. 그는 그녀의 온 삶을 열어 주는 용수철을 건드렸던 것이다. 그래서 그녀가 잠시 그를 놓친다 해도, 그것은 다만 그 삶을 이루어 온 하나의 위대한 회상의 숱한 형상들 가운데서일 뿐이었다.\n\n사색하다가 일하다가를 번갈아 하면서—이제는 오래도록 한결같이 바느질에 마음을 다잡으려 애쓰다가, 이제는 일감이 무릎 위에 떨어지도록 내버려 둔 채 거들떠보지도 않고 더 분주한 생각이 이끄는 대로 헤매면서—해리엇 카커는 시간이 곁을 미끄러져 가고 하루가 슬그머니 흘러가는 것을 깨달았다. 밝고 맑던 아침은 차츰 흐려졌고, 매서운 바람이 일었으며, 비가 세차게 쏟아졌고, 어두운 안개가 멀리 도시 위에 드리워 그것을 시야에서 가려 버렸다.\n\n그녀는 그런 때면 자주, 바로 곁의 큰길을 따라 런던으로 흘러드는 떠돌이들을 동정 어린 눈으로 바라보곤 했다. 발이 부르트고 지친 그들은, 앞에 펼쳐진 거대한 도시를 두려운 듯 바라보며, 마치 그곳에서의 자기네 비참함이 바다에 떨어진 물 한 방울이나 해변의 모래 한 알에 지나지 않으리라 예감하기라도 한 듯, 성난 날씨 앞에 움츠리며 잔뜩 웅크린 채, 마치 만물이 자기네를 거부하기라도 하는 양 위축되어 걸어갔다. 날이면 날마다 그런 나그네들이 곁을 슬며시 지나갔으나, 그녀가 생각하기로는 늘 한 방향으로—언제나 도시를 향해서였다. 어떤 절망적인 매혹에 떠밀리는 듯 보이는 그 도시의 광대함, 그 어느 한 모습에 삼켜진 채, 그들은 결코 돌아오지 않았다. 병원과 묘지와 감옥과 강과 열병과 광기와 악덕과 죽음의 먹이가 되어—그들은 멀리서 으르렁대는 그 괴물을 향해 나아갔고, 그렇게 사라져 갔다.\n\n차디찬 바람이 울부짖고, 비가 내리고, 날이 침울하게 어두워져 갈 무렵, 해리엇이 오래도록 끊임없이 매달려 온 일감에서 눈을 들어 올렸을 때, 그런 나그네들 가운데 하나가 다가오는 것을 보았다.\n\n한 여인이었다. 서른쯤 되어 보이는 외로운 여인으로, 키가 크고, 몸매가 반듯하고, 잘생겼으나, 비참하게 차려입고 있었다. 온갖 날씨 속 숱한 시골길의 흙—먼지, 백악, 진흙, 자갈—이 흘러내리는 빗물에 그녀의 회색 망토 위에 엉겨 붙어 있었다. 머리에는 보닛도 없이, 풍성한 검은 머리칼을 비로부터 막아 줄 것이라고는 찢어진 손수건 하나뿐이었는데, 그 펄럭이는 손수건 자락과 머리칼 때문에 바람이 그녀의 눈을 가려서, 그녀는 그것들을 자꾸 밀어내고 자기가 가는 길을 살피느라 걸음을 멈추곤 했다.\n\n해리엇이 그녀를 보았을 때, 그녀는 마침 그렇게 하고 있던 참이었다. 그녀의 두 손이 햇볕에 그을린 이마 위에서 갈라지며 얼굴을 가로질러 훑어, 얼굴을 가리던 것들을 옆으로 젖혔을 때, 그 얼굴에는 무모하고 거리낌 없는 아름다움이 있었다. 날씨 따위보다 더한 것에도 굴하지 않는 대담하고 타락한 무관심이, 하늘에서든 땅에서든 자기 맨머리 위로 쏟아지는 것에 대한 무신경함이 있었으니, 그것이 그녀의 비참함과 외로움과 어우러져, 같은 여자의 마음을 움직였다. 해리엇은 그 여인의 겉모습 못지않게 그 속에서 비뚤어지고 타락한 모든 것을 떠올렸다. 그 몸의 매력처럼 굳어지고 강철처럼 단단해진 마음의 정숙한 우아함들을, 거친 머리칼처럼 바람에 내던져진 창조주의 숱한 선물들을, 폭풍이 들이치고 밤이 다가오고 있는 그 모든 아름다운 폐허를.\n\n이것을 생각하며, 그녀는 섬세한 분노에 차서 외면하지 않았으니—저 자신도 동정심 많고 다정한 같은 성(性)의 여인들이 너무도 자주 그러하건만—오히려 그녀를 가엾이 여겼다.\n\n타락한 그녀의 자매는 멀리 앞을 바라보며, 도시를 휘감은 안개를 간절한 눈으로 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰면서, 이따금 이쪽저쪽을 곁눈질하며, 낯선 이의 어리둥절하고 미덥지 못한 표정으로 다가왔다. 그녀의 걸음걸이는 대담하고 용감했으나, 그녀는 지쳐 있었고, 잠시 망설인 끝에—돌무더기 위에 주저앉았다. 비를 피할 곳을 찾지도 않은 채, 비가 내리는 대로 자기 위에 내리도록 내버려 두면서.\n\n이제 그녀는 그 집 맞은편에 있었다. 두 손에 머리를 잠시 묻고 쉬다가 고개를 들었을 때, 그녀의 눈이 해리엇의 눈과 마주쳤다.\n\n순식간에 해리엇은 문가로 나왔고, 다른 여인은 그녀의 손짓에 자리에서 일어나, 누그러진 기색이라곤 없이 천천히 그녀에게로 다가왔다.\n\n\"왜 빗속에서 쉬시나요?\" 해리엇이 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"달리 쉴 곳이 없으니까요.\" 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"하지만 이 근처에 비를 피할 곳이 많이 있어요. 여기가,\" 그녀가 자그마한 현관을 가리키며 말했다. \"당신이 있던 데보다는 나아요. 여기서 편히 쉬세요.\"\n\n떠돌이 여인은 의아함과 놀라움에 차서 그녀를 바라보았으나, 고마워하는 기색이라곤 전혀 없었다. 그리고 자리에 앉아, 닳아빠진 신발 한 짝을 벗어 그 안에 든 돌조각과 먼지를 털어 내려다가, 그녀의 발이 찢기고 피 흘리고 있는 것이 드러났다.\n\n해리엇이 가엾다는 말을 내뱉자, 나그네는 경멸하듯 믿기 어렵다는 듯 미소 지으며 올려다보았다.\n\n\"흥, 나 같은 것한테 찢긴 발이 뭐 대수라고요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 나 같은 것의 찢긴 발이, 당신 같은 분한테 뭐 대수겠어요?\"\n\n\"들어와서 씻으세요,\" 해리엇이 부드럽게 대답했다. \"그리고 동여맬 것을 좀 드릴게요.\"\n\n여인은 그녀의 팔을 붙잡아 제 눈앞으로 끌어당기더니, 거기에 눈을 묻고 울었다. 여자처럼이 아니라, 그런 나약함에 불시에 사로잡힌 엄격한 사내처럼, 가슴을 거칠게 들썩이며, 그리고 다시 추스르려 안간힘을 쓰며 울었으니, 그것은 그런 감정이 그녀에게 얼마나 드문 것인지를 보여 주었다.\n\n그녀는 집 안으로 이끌려 들어가는 데에 순순히 따랐고, 분명 제 자신을 위해서라기보다는 감사하는 마음에서, 다친 곳을 씻고 동여맸다. 그러자 해리엇은 자기의 검소한 식사에서 남은 음식 부스러기를 그녀 앞에 내놓았고, 그녀가 비록 조금이긴 하나 그것을 먹고 나자, 길을 다시 떠나기 전에(그녀는 떠나려고 안달하는 기색을 보였다) 불 앞에서 옷을 말리라고 간곡히 청했다. 다시금, 제 자신을 염려하는 기색이라기보다는 감사하는 마음에서, 그녀는 불 앞에 앉아, 머리를 동여맨 손수건을 풀고, 두텁고 젖은 머리칼을 허리 아래까지 늘어뜨린 채, 손바닥으로 그것을 말리며 타오르는 불길을 바라보고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"이런 생각을 하고 계시겠지요,\" 그녀가 불쑥 고개를 들며 말했다. \"내가 한때는 어여뻤겠거니 하고요. 정말 그랬을 거예요—분명 그랬어요—여기 좀 보세요!\"\n\n그녀는 두 손으로 머리칼을 거칠게 쳐들었다. 마치 그것을 잡아 뜯기라도 할 듯 움켜쥐었다가, 다시 그것을 떨어뜨리고는, 마치 그것이 한 무더기 뱀이기라도 한 양 뒤로 홱 내던졌다.\n\n\"이곳이 낯선 곳인가요?\" 해리엇이 물었다.\n\n\"낯선 곳이냐고요!\" 그녀가 짧은 대답마다 사이를 두고 멈추면서, 불을 바라보며 되받았다. \"그래요. 십 년이나 십이 년쯤 낯선 곳이었지요. 내가 있던 데에는 달력이라곤 없었어요. 십 년인지 십이 년인지. 이 동네를 모르겠어요. 내가 떠난 뒤로 많이 변했네요.\"\n\n\"멀리 다녀오셨나요?\"\n\n\"아주 멀리요. 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 바다를 건너서, 거기서도 또 멀리요. 나는 죄수들이 가는 데에 가 있었어요.\" 그녀가 자기를 거두어 준 여인을 똑바로 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"나 자신이 죄수였으니까요.\"\n\n\"하느님께서 당신을 도우시고 용서하시길!\" 부드러운 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아! 하느님께서 나를 도우시고 용서하시길이라!\" 그녀가 불을 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 되받았다. \"사람들이 우리 중 누군가를 조금만 더 도와준다면, 어쩌면 하느님께서 우리 모두를 그만큼 더 빨리 용서해 주실지도 모르지요.\"\n\n그러나 그녀는, 그 진지한 태도와, 온화함으로 가득하고 심판이라곤 없는 그 여인의 다정한 얼굴에 마음이 누그러져, 한결 덜 거칠게 말했다.\n\n\"당신과 나, 우리 둘은 나이가 비슷할 거예요. 내가 더 많다 해도, 한두 살 위는 못 될 거예요. 오, 그걸 생각해 봐요!\"\n\n그녀는, 마치 제 겉모습을 내보이면 자기가 얼마나 도덕적으로 비참한 인간인지가 드러나기라도 할 양 두 팔을 벌렸다가, 그것을 옆구리에 떨어뜨리고 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n\"우리가 고치기를 바라지 못할 것은 아무것도 없어요. 바로잡기에 너무 늦은 때란 결코 없고요.\" 해리엇이 말했다. \"당신은 뉘우치고 있나요?\"\n\n\"아니요,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"안 해요! 못 해요. 그런 거 안 한다고요. 내가 왜 뉘우쳐야 하지요, 온 세상이 멀쩡히 풀려나 있는데? 사람들은 내게 내 뉘우침을 운운하지요. 나한테 저질러진 잘못들은 대체 누가 뉘우친답니까?\"\n\n그녀는 일어나, 머리에 손수건을 동여매고, 떠나려고 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"어디로 가시나요?\" 해리엇이 말했다.\n\n\"저쪽이요,\" 그녀가 손으로 가리키며 대답했다. \"런던으로요.\"\n\n\"갈 만한 집이라도 있나요?\"\n\n\"어머니가 있는 것 같긴 해요. 그 여자가 어머니인 만큼이나, 그 여자가 사는 데도 집이지요.\" 그녀가 쓴웃음을 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"이걸 받으세요,\" 해리엇이 그녀의 손에 돈을 쥐여 주며 외쳤다. \"잘 살아 보려고 애써 봐요. 얼마 안 되지만, 하루쯤은 당신을 화에서 지켜 줄 수 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"결혼하셨나요?\" 다른 여인이 그것을 받으며 가냘프게 말했다.\n\n\"아니요. 저는 여기서 오라버니와 함께 살아요. 저희도 여유가 많지 않아서요, 그렇지 않았다면 더 드렸을 거예요.\"\n\n\"입맞춤해도 될까요?\"\n\n그 얼굴에 경멸이나 혐오가 없음을 보고, 그녀의 자선을 받은 그 여인은 그렇게 물으며 그녀에게로 몸을 굽혀, 그녀의 뺨에 입술을 댔다. 다시 한번 그녀는 그녀의 팔을 붙잡아 거기에 눈을 가렸다. 그러고는 가 버렸다.\n\n짙어 가는 밤과, 울부짖는 바람과, 퍼붓는 비 속으로 가 버렸다. 흐릿한 불빛이 희미하게 빛나는, 안개에 휘감긴 도시를 향해 길을 재촉하면서, 검은 머리칼과 흐트러진 머릿수건을 그 무모한 얼굴 둘레에 펄럭이면서.\n\n# 제29장\n\n추하고 어두운 방 안에, 역시 추하고 어두운 늙은 여인이 앉아 바람과 빗소리에 귀를 기울이며 변변찮은 불 위로 웅크리고 있었다. 앞엣것보다는 뒤엣것에 더 한결같이 매달려, 그녀는 자세를 바꾸는 일이 없었다. 다만 빗방울이 어쩌다 사그라드는 잿불 위로 떨어져 쉭쉭 소리를 낼 때면, 정신이 번쩍 깨어나 바깥의 휘파람 소리와 후두두 떨어지는 소리에 귀 기울이려 고개를 들었다가, 차츰차츰 점점 더 낮게, 더 낮게, 더 낮게 고개를 떨구며 골똘한 상념에 잠겨 들어가곤 했다. 그 상태에서는 밤의 소음들도, 마치 바닷가에 앉아 사색에 잠긴 이에게 단조롭게 굽이치는 바다의 소리가 그러하듯, 흐릿하게만 느껴졌다.\n\n방 안에는 불빛 외에 아무 빛도 없었다. 반쯤 잠든 사나운 짐승의 눈처럼 이따금 음침하게 번득이는 그 빛은, 더 나은 모습을 보여 주려고 애써 가릴 필요가 있는 물건이라곤 아무것도 비추지 않았다. 누더기 한 무더기, 뼈다귀 한 무더기, 초라한 침대, 망가진 의자나 걸상 두세 개, 검은 벽과 그보다 더 검은 천장—그 깜박이는 광채가 비추는 것이라곤 그게 전부였다. 늙은 여인은 자기 자신의 거대하고 일그러진 그림자를 반쯤은 뒤편 벽에, 반쯤은 위쪽 천장에 드리운 채, 불을 가둔 몇 장의 헐거운 벽돌 위로—난로가 없었으므로 굴뚝의 축축한 화덕 위로—몸을 굽히고 앉아 있었는데, 마치 어느 마녀의 제단에서 길조를 기다리며 지켜보는 듯한 모습이었다. 다만 그녀의 달싹이는 턱과 떨리는 아래턱의 움직임이, 더디게 일렁이는 불빛에 비해 너무 잦고 너무 빨랐기에 망정이지, 그렇지 않았다면 그 빛이 왔다 갔다 하며 만들어 낸 환영처럼 보였을 것이다. 그 빛이 비추는 얼굴은, 거기 딸린 형상만큼이나 미동도 없었으니까.\n\n만일 플로렌스가 그 방 안에 서서, 그렇게 불 위로 웅크린 채 벽과 천장에 그림자를 드리운 그 그림자의 실물을 바라볼 수 있었다면, 단 한 번의 눈길로도 착한 브라운 부인의 모습을 떠올리기에 충분했으리라. 비록 그 무서운 늙은 여인에 대한 어린 시절의 기억이, 아마도 벽에 비친 그림자만큼이나 기괴하고 과장되게 진실을 그려 낸 것이었다 해도 말이다. 그러나 플로렌스는 거기서 보고 있지 않았다. 그리하여 착한 브라운 부인은 알아보는 이 없이, 보는 이 없이, 자기 불을 응시하며 앉아 있었다.\n\n빗물이 가느다란 줄기를 이루어 굴뚝을 타고 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 떨어지자 평소보다 요란한 탁탁 소리가 났고, 거기에 이끌려 늙은 여인은 다시 귀 기울이려 짜증스럽게 고개를 들었다. 그리고 이번에는 고개를 다시 떨구지 않았다. 문에 손이 닿았고, 방 안에 발소리가 들렸기 때문이다.\n\n\"거 누구냐?\" 그녀가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"소식을 가져온 사람이오.\" 여자의 목소리로 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"소식? 어디서?\"\n\n\"외지에서.\"\n\n\"바다 건너에서?\" 늙은 여인이 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래요, 바다 건너에서.\"\n\n늙은 여인은 황급히 불을 긁어모으고는, 방 안으로 들어와 문을 닫고 이제 방 한가운데 서 있는 방문객에게 바짝 다가가, 흠뻑 젖은 망토 위에 손을 얹고, 저항하지 않는 그 형체를 돌려 불빛이 온전히 비치도록 했다. 그게 무엇이든 간에, 그녀는 자기가 기대한 것을 찾지 못했다. 망토를 다시 놓아 버리고는, 실망과 비탄의 칭얼대는 비명을 내질렀으니까.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 방문객이 물었다.\n\n\"오호! 오호!\" 늙은 여인이 끔찍한 울부짖음과 함께 얼굴을 위로 쳐들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐고요?\" 방문객이 다시 물었다.\n\n\"내 딸이 아니잖아!\" 늙은 여인이 두 팔을 내던지듯 쳐들고는 머리 위로 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"내 앨리스는 어디 있어? 내 어여쁜 딸은 어디 있어? 그놈들이 그 애를 죽여 버렸어!\"\n\n\"댁 이름이 마우드라면, 그 사람들이 아직 따님을 죽이진 않았소.\" 방문객이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 내 딸을 봤단 말이오?\" 늙은 여인이 외쳤다. \"그 애가 내게 편지를 썼어?\"\n\n\"댁은 글을 못 읽는다고 하던데요.\" 상대가 대답했다.\n\n\"맞아, 나는 못 읽지!\" 늙은 여인이 두 손을 비틀며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"여기 불은 없소?\" 상대가 방 안을 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n늙은 여인은 우물거리며 고개를 흔들고, 어여쁜 딸 이야기를 혼자 중얼거리면서, 구석의 찬장에서 초 하나를 가져와 떨리는 손으로 불 속에 들이밀어 간신히 붙인 뒤 탁자 위에 세웠다. 더러운 심지는 처음엔 제 기름에 막혀 흐릿하게 탔다. 그리고 늙은 여인의 흐린 눈과 침침해진 시력이 그 불빛으로 무언가를 분간할 수 있게 되었을 때, 방문객은 팔짱을 끼고 눈을 내리깐 채 앉아 있었고, 그녀가 머리에 쓰고 있던 손수건은 옆 탁자 위에 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"그럼 그 애가, 내 딸 앨리스가 말로 전갈을 보냈단 말이지?\" 늙은 여인이 잠시 기다린 뒤 우물거렸다. \"그 애가 뭐라고 했소?\"\n\n\"보세요.\" 방문객이 대답했다.\n\n늙은 여인은 겁먹은 듯 불안하게 그 말을 되뇌었다. 그러고는 눈에 그늘을 드리우며, 말한 이를 보고, 방 안을 둘러보고, 다시 한번 말한 이를 보았다.\n\n\"앨리스가 다시 보라고 했어요, 어머니.\" 그러고는 말한 이가 그녀에게 눈길을 고정했다.\n\n다시 늙은 여인은 방 안을 둘러보고, 방문객을 보고, 다시 한번 방 안을 둘러보았다. 황급히 초를 집어 들고 자리에서 일어나, 그것을 방문객의 얼굴에 들이대더니, 큰 비명을 지르고는, 초를 내려놓고, 그녀의 목에 와락 안겼다!\n\n\"내 딸이야! 내 앨리스야! 살아서 돌아온 내 어여쁜 딸이야!\" 늙은 여인이, 그 포옹을 차갑게 견디는 가슴에 매달려 앞뒤로 몸을 흔들며 비명처럼 외쳤다. \"내 딸이야! 내 앨리스야! 살아서 돌아온 내 어여쁜 딸이야!\" 그녀는 다시 비명처럼 외치며, 딸 앞 바닥에 주저앉아 그 무릎을 끌어안고, 거기에 머리를 기대고는, 자기 생명력이 낼 수 있는 온갖 미친 듯한 몸짓으로 여전히 앞뒤로 몸을 흔들었다.\n\n\"네, 어머니.\" 앨리스가, 잠시 몸을 앞으로 숙여 어머니에게 입을 맞추면서도, 그러는 와중에도 그 포옹에서 벗어나려 애쓰며 대답했다. \"마침내 돌아왔어요. 놓으세요, 어머니. 놔요. 일어나서 의자에 앉으세요. 이런다고 뭐가 좋아지나요?\"\n\n\"이 애가 떠날 때보다 더 모질어져서 돌아왔어!\" 어머니가 딸의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 여전히 그 무릎을 붙든 채 외쳤다. \"나 따위는 아랑곳도 안 해! 이 세월이 다 지나도록, 내가 끌어온 그 비참한 삶이 다 지나도록!\"\n\n\"아니, 어머니!\" 앨리스가 누더기 치마를 흔들어 늙은 여인을 떼어 내며 말했다. \"거기엔 양면이 있는 법이에요. 어머니뿐 아니라 내게도 세월이 있었고, 어머니뿐 아니라 내게도 비참함이 있었어요. 일어나세요, 일어나요!\"\n\n어머니는 일어나서 울고, 두 손을 비틀며, 조금 떨어진 거리에 서서 딸을 응시했다. 그러더니 다시 초를 집어 들고 딸 주위를 돌며 머리끝부터 발끝까지 살펴보면서, 내내 나직이 신음했다. 그러고는 초를 내려놓고 다시 의자에 앉아, 두 손을 마주쳐 무슨 지친 가락을 두드리듯 하며 몸을 좌우로 흔들면서, 혼자 신음하고 흐느끼기를 계속했다.\n\n앨리스는 일어나 젖은 망토를 벗어 한쪽에 치워 놓았다. 그러고는 아까처럼 다시 앉아, 팔짱을 끼고 불을 응시하면서, 경멸하는 얼굴로 늙은 어머니의 두서없는 푸념에 말없이 귀 기울이고 있었다.\n\n\"내가 떠날 때처럼 젊은 모습으로 돌아오리라 기대하셨어요, 어머니?\" 마침내 그녀가 늙은 여인에게 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"내가 산 것 같은 외지 생활이 미모에 좋을 거라 생각하셨나요? 어머니 말씀 들으면 누구라도 그렇게 믿겠네!\"\n\n\"그게 아니다!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"이 애도 알면서!\"\n\n\"그럼 뭔데요?\" 딸이 대답했다. \"오래가지 않는 거라야 좋을 거예요, 어머니. 안 그러면 내가 나가는 길이 들어온 길보다 더 쉬울 테니.\"\n\n\"저 말 좀 들어 봐!\" 어머니가 소리쳤다. \"이 세월이 다 지나, 돌아온 바로 그 순간에 나를 버리겠다고 협박을 하다니!\"\n\n\"두 번째로 말씀드리는데요, 어머니, 어머니뿐 아니라 내게도 세월이 있었어요.\" 앨리스가 말했다. \"더 모질어져서 돌아왔다고요? 물론 더 모질어져서 돌아왔죠. 그럼 달리 뭘 기대하셨어요?\"\n\n\"나한테 더 모질다니! 자기 친어미한테!\" 늙은 여인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"내 친어머니가 나를 모질게 만들지 않았다면, 누가 나를 모질게 만들기 시작했는지 나는 모르겠네요.\" 그녀가, 팔짱을 끼고 미간을 찌푸리고 입술을 앙다문 채, 마치 어떤 부드러운 감정이든 힘으로 제 가슴에서 몰아내려 작정한 듯이 앉아서 대답했다. \"어머니, 한두 마디만 들어 보세요. 우리가 지금 서로 이해하게 되면, 어쩌면 다시는 다투지 않을지도 몰라요. 나는 계집애로 떠났다가 여자가 되어 돌아왔어요. 도리를 모르는 채 떠났다가, 나아진 것 없이 돌아왔죠, 그건 맹세해도 좋아요. 그런데 어머니는 내게 도리를 다하셨던가요?\"\n\n\"내가!\" 늙은 여인이 외쳤다. \"내 딸한테! 제 자식한테 도리를 다하는 어미라니!\"\n\n\"부자연스럽게 들리죠, 안 그래요?\" 딸이, 매섭고 무심하고 강인하고 아름다운 얼굴로 그녀를 차갑게 바라보며 대답했다. \"하지만 나는 혼자였던 그 세월 동안 가끔 그 생각을 하다가, 끝내는 익숙해져 버렸어요. 처음부터 끝까지 도리에 관한 이야기는 좀 들어 봤죠. 하지만 그건 늘 내가 남들에게 다해야 할 도리에 관한 거였어요. 나는 가끔, 시간이나 때울 겸, 누구도 내게 다해야 할 도리는 없었던 건가 하고 곰곰 생각했어요.\"\n\n어머니는 입을 우물거리고, 중얼거리고, 고개를 흔들며 앉아 있었으나, 그게 화가 나서인지 후회 때문인지, 부정하는 것인지, 아니면 그저 몸이 쇠약해서인지는 드러나지 않았다.\n\n\"앨리스 마우드라는 아이가 있었어요.\" 딸이 웃으며, 자기 자신을 끔찍하게 비웃듯 제 몸을 내려다보며 말했다. \"가난과 방치 속에서 태어나, 그 속에서 길러졌죠. 아무도 그 애를 가르치지 않았고, 아무도 나서서 도와주지 않았고, 아무도 그 애를 돌보지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"아무도 안 돌봤다고!\" 어머니가 자기 자신을 가리키고 가슴을 치며 따라 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 애가 받은 유일한 보살핌이라곤,\" 딸이 대답했다, \"맞고, 굶주리고, 이따금 욕을 먹는 거였어요. 차라리 그런 게 없는 편이 나았을 거예요. 그 애는 이런 집들에서, 또 거리에서, 자기와 같은 처지의 가엾은 꼬마들 한 무리와 함께 살았어요. 그런데도 이런 어린 시절에서 어여쁜 외모를 가지고 나왔죠. 그러니 더더욱 안된 일이었어요. 차라리 못생겨서 사냥감처럼 시달리다 죽임을 당하는 편이 나았을 거예요.\"\n\n\"계속해! 계속해 봐!\" 어머니가 외쳤다.\n\n\"계속하고 있어요.\" 딸이 대답했다. \"앨리스 마우드라는 처녀가 있었어요. 그 애는 예뻤죠. 너무 늦게, 그리고 죄다 잘못된 방향으로 배웠어요. 너무 잘 보살핌을 받고, 너무 잘 길러지고, 너무 잘 부추겨지고, 너무 많은 손길을 탔죠. 어머니는 그 애를 무척 아끼셨어요—그때는 형편이 좀 나았으니까요. 그 처녀에게 닥친 일은 해마다 수천 명에게 닥치는 일이에요. 그저 파멸이었고, 그 애는 그 파멸을 타고난 거였어요.\"\n\n\"이 세월이 다 지났는데!\" 늙은 여인이 흐느꼈다. \"내 딸이 이런 말로 시작하다니.\"\n\n\"곧 끝낼 거예요.\" 딸이 말했다. \"앨리스 마우드라는 죄인이 있었어요—아직 처녀였지만, 버림받고 내쳐진 처지였죠. 그리고 그 애는 재판을 받았고, 형을 선고받았어요. 아이고, 법정의 그 양반들이 그걸 두고 어찌나 떠들어 대던지! 그 판사는 그 애의 도리에 대해, 그 애가 타고난 천부의 재능을 그르친 데 대해 어찌나 근엄하게 굴던지—마치 그 재능이 그 애에게는 저주가 되어 버렸다는 걸, 그 자리의 누구보다도 자기가 더 잘 알지 못한다는 듯이!—그리고 법의 강한 팔에 대해 어찌나 설교를 하던지—그 애가 죄 없고 의지가지없는 가엾은 어린것이었을 때는 그 강한 팔이 그 애를 구하는 데는 그토록 강하더니!—그리고 그 모든 게 어찌나 엄숙하고 신앙심 깊던지! 정말이지, 그 뒤로 나는 그 생각을 여러 번 했어요!\"\n\n그녀는 두 팔을 가슴에 단단히 모으고는, 늙은 여인의 울부짖음을 음악처럼 들리게 만드는 어조로 웃었다.\n\n\"그래서 앨리스 마우드는 유배되었어요, 어머니.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 자기 도리를 배우라며 보내졌는데, 거기엔 여기보다 도리가 스무 배는 더 적고, 사악함과 그릇됨과 치욕은 더 많았죠. 그리고 앨리스 마우드는 여자가 되어 돌아왔어요. 이 모든 일을 겪고 났으니 마땅히 될 만한 그런 여자가 되어서요. 머지않아 더 엄숙하고, 더 그럴듯한 말이 오가고, 십중팔구 그 강한 팔도 또 등장하겠죠. 그러면 그 애도 끝장날 거예요. 하지만 그 양반들이 일자리를 잃을까 봐 걱정할 필요는 없어요. 그들이 사는 거리 어디에서나, 사내애든 계집애든, 가엾은 꼬마들이 떼를 지어 자라나고 있으니까요. 그 양반들이 재산을 다 모을 때까지 일감을 대 줄 거예요.\"\n\n늙은 여인은 탁자에 두 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻으며 몹시 괴로워하는 시늉을 했다—아니, 어쩌면 정말로 괴로웠는지도 모른다.\n\n\"자! 이제 다 했어요, 어머니.\" 딸이, 그 이야기를 떨쳐 버리듯 고갯짓을 하며 말했다. \"할 만큼 했어요. 우리가 뭘 하든, 어머니랑 나랑 도리를 다한다는 이야기는 하지 맙시다. 어머니의 어린 시절도 내 것과 같았겠죠, 아마. 그러니 우리 둘 다 더더욱 안된 일이고요. 어머니를 탓하거나 내 변명을 하고 싶은 게 아니에요. 그럴 이유가 어디 있겠어요? 그건 다 오래전에 끝난 일이에요. 하지만 나는 이제 여자예요—더는 계집애가 아니라고요—그러니 어머니랑 나랑 법정의 그 양반들처럼 우리 내력을 늘어놓을 필요는 없어요. 그건 우리가 충분히 잘 알고 있으니까요.\"\n\n길을 잃고 타락한 처지였으나, 그녀에게는 얼굴에도 자태에도 아름다움이 있었으니, 그것은 가장 흉한 표정을 지을 때조차 조금이라도 주의 깊게 바라보는 이라면 누구든 아름다움으로 인정하지 않을 수 없는 것이었다. 그녀가 침묵 속으로 가라앉으며, 거칠게 동요했던 얼굴이 잔잔해질 때, 불을 응시하던 그 검은 눈동자가 거기 활기를 불어넣던 무모한 빛을 슬픔 같은 무언가로 누그러진 빛으로 바꿀 때, 길에 지친 그 모든 비참함과 피로를 뚫고, 타락한 천사의 사라진 광채의 한 줄기 빛이 비쳐 들었다.\n\n어머니는 한동안 말없이 그녀를 지켜보다가, 탁자 너머로 시든 손을 그녀 쪽으로 조금 슬그머니 내미는 모험을 감행했다. 그리고 딸이 이를 허락하는 것을 알자, 그 얼굴을 만지고 머리카락을 쓰다듬어 보았다. 적어도 이 관심의 표시만큼은 늙은 여인이 진심인 듯한 느낌이 들었던지, 앨리스는 그녀를 제지하려는 어떤 움직임도 보이지 않았다. 그리하여 어머니는 차츰 다가가 딸의 머리를 새로 묶어 주고, 신발이라 부를 수 있다면 그 젖은 신발을 벗겨 주고, 어깨에 마른 무언가를 둘러 주고는, 딸의 옛 이목구비와 표정을 점점 더 알아보며 혼자 중얼거리면서, 겸손히 그 곁을 맴돌았다.\n\n\"어머니, 보아하니 무척 가난하시네요.\" 한동안 그렇게 앉아 있던 앨리스가 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"지독히 가난하지, 얘야.\" 늙은 여인이 대답했다.\n\n그녀는 딸을 감탄하면서도 딸을 두려워했다. 어쩌면 그 감탄은, 그게 어떤 것이든, 오래전 그녀의 누추한 생존 투쟁 한가운데 처음으로 아름다운 무언가가 나타나는 것을 발견했을 때 비롯된 것이었으리라. 어쩌면 그 두려움은, 어느 정도는, 그녀가 방금 들은 그 회고와 관련된 것이었으리라. 어쨌거나, 그녀는 자식 앞에 순종적이고 공손하게 서서, 더는 책망받지 않게 해 달라고 애처롭게 간청하기라도 하듯 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"어떻게 사셨어요?\"\n\n\"구걸로 살았지, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그리고 좀도둑질로요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"가끔, 앨리—아주 소소하게. 나는 늙었고 겁이 많아. 가끔 아이들한테서 자질구레한 걸 슬쩍하긴 했다, 얘야, 그래도 자주는 아니야. 시골을 떠돌아다녔지, 우리 아가, 그래서 내가 아는 건 알아. 나는 지켜봤어.\"\n\n\"지켜봤다고요?\" 딸이 그녀를 바라보며 되물었다.\n\n\"어떤 집안 둘레를 맴돌았단다, 얘야.\" 어머니가 아까보다 한층 더 겸손하고 순종적으로 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 집안요?\"\n\n\"쉿, 얘야. 화내지 마라. 다 너를 사랑해서 한 일이야. 바다 건너에 있는 내 가엾은 딸을 생각하면서.\" 그녀는 변명하듯 손을 내밀었다가, 다시 거두어들여 입술에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"여러 해 전에, 얘야,\" 그녀가, 자기와 마주한 주의 깊고 매서운 얼굴을 겁먹은 듯 흘끔거리며 말을 이었다, \"우연히 그 사람의 어린 자식을 마주쳤단다.\"\n\n\"누구의 자식이요?\"\n\n\"그 사람 자식은 아니야, 앨리스 얘야. 그렇게 날 쳐다보지 마라. 그 사람 자식이 아니라고. 어떻게 그 사람 자식이겠니? 그 사람한텐 자식이 없는 걸 너도 알잖니.\"\n\n\"그럼 누구의 자식인데요?\" 딸이 대꾸했다. \"어머니가 '그 사람'이라고 하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"쉿, 앨리. 무섭다, 얘야. 돔비 씨—그저 돔비 씨 말이야. 그 뒤로, 우리 아가, 그 사람들을 자주 봤어. 그 사람도 봤단다.\"\n\n이 마지막 말을 입에 올리면서, 늙은 여인은 딸이 자기를 때리기라도 할까 봐 별안간 겁이 난 듯, 움츠러들며 뒷걸음질했다. 그러나 딸의 얼굴은 그녀에게 고정된 채 더없이 격렬한 격정을 드러내면서도, 그녀는 가만히 있었다. 다만 두 팔을 가슴 위로 점점 더 단단히 맞잡았는데, 마치 갑자기 자기를 사로잡은 그 분노의 눈먼 광기 속에서, 자기 자신이나 누군가에게 해를 입히지 못하도록 그렇게 해서 두 팔을 억누르려는 듯했다.\n\n\"내가 누군지는 꿈에도 생각 못 했지!\" 늙은 여인이 맞잡은 손을 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"신경도 안 썼겠죠!\" 딸이 이를 악물고 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"하지만 우리가 거기서,\" 늙은 여인이 말했다, \"얼굴을 맞대고 있었단다. 내가 그 사람한테 말을 걸었고, 그 사람도 내게 말을 했어. 나는 그가 긴 나무숲 길을 따라 내려가는 걸 앉아서 지켜봤지. 그리고 그가 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다, 나는 그 영혼과 육신에 저주를 퍼부었어.\"\n\n\"그래도 그는 잘만 번창할 거예요.\" 딸이 경멸하듯 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그래, 잘 번창하고 있지.\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 입을 다물었다. 자기 앞의 얼굴과 형체가 분노로 일그러져 있었으니까. 마치 그 안에서 다투는 격정들로 가슴이 터져 버릴 것만 같았다. 그것을 억누르고 가두어 두려는 안간힘은 그 분노 자체에 못지않게 무시무시했고, 그것을 행하는 여인의 격렬하고 위험한 성정을 못지않게 드러내 보였다. 그러나 그 안간힘은 성공했고, 그녀는 침묵 끝에 물었다.\n\n\"그는 결혼했어요?\"\n\n\"아니, 얘야.\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"하려고 해요?\"\n\n\"내가 알기론 아니다, 얘야. 하지만 그 사람의 주인이자 친구는 결혼했어. 오, 우리가 그에게 축하를 빌어 줘도 되겠구나! 우리가 그들 모두에게 축하를 빌어 줘도 되겠어!\" 늙은 여인이 의기양양해서 야윈 두 팔로 제 몸을 끌어안으며 외쳤다. \"그 결혼에서 우리에겐 기쁨밖에 올 게 없어. 내 말 명심해라!\"\n\n딸은 설명을 바라며 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그런데 너 젖었고 지쳤구나. 배고프고 목도 마르겠지.\" 늙은 여인이 절뚝거리며 찬장으로 가면서 말했다. \"그런데 여기엔 가진 게 별로 없고, 또 별로\"—주머니 속을 뒤져 반 페니짜리 몇 닢을 탁자 위에 짤랑 떨어뜨리며—\"여기도 별로 없구나. 너 돈 좀 있니, 앨리스 얘야?\"\n\n탐욕스럽고 날카롭고 간절한 그 얼굴로 그녀가 그 질문을 던지며 지켜보는 동안, 딸이 가슴에서 그토록 얼마 전에 받은 그 작은 선물을 꺼내는 모습은, 딸 자신이 말로 들려준 것 못지않게 이 어미와 자식의 내력을 거의 다 말해 주었다.\n\n\"그게 다니?\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"더는 없어요. 적선이 아니었다면 이것도 없었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"적선이 아니었다면, 응, 얘야?\" 늙은 여인이, 딸이 손에 쥔 채 여전히 가지고 있는 게 미덥지 못한 듯, 그 돈을 보려고 탐욕스럽게 탁자 위로 몸을 굽히며 응시한 채 말했다. \"흠! 여섯에 여섯이면 열둘, 거기에 여섯이면 열여덟이라—그러니—우리가 이걸 최대한 알뜰히 써야겠다. 내가 가서 먹고 마실 걸 좀 사 올게.\"\n\n겉모습에서 기대할 법한 것보다 훨씬 재빠른 동작으로—나이와 비참함이 그녀를 추한 만큼이나 쇠약하게 만들어 놓은 듯했으니까—그녀는 떨리는 손을 놀리기 시작해, 낡은 보닛을 머리에 매고 찢어진 숄을 몸에 두르기 시작했다. 그러면서도 여전히 똑같은 날카로운 욕망으로 딸의 손에 든 돈을 곁눈질했다.\n\n\"이 결혼에서 우리에게 무슨 기쁨이 온다는 거예요, 어머니?\" 딸이 물었다. \"그건 말씀 안 하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"기쁨이란,\" 그녀가 더듬거리는 손가락으로 옷을 차려입으며 대답했다, \"사랑이라곤 전혀 없고, 자만과 증오만 그득한 데서 오는 기쁨이지, 얘야. 그들 사이의 혼란과 다툼에서 오는 기쁨, 그렇게 거만한 자들이니까. 그리고 위험에서 오는 기쁨—위험이야, 앨리스!\"\n\n\"무슨 위험요?\"\n\n\"나는 내가 본 걸 봤어. 나는 내가 아는 걸 안다고!\" 어머니가 키득거렸다. \"누군가는 조심해야 할 게야. 누군가는 경계해야지. 내 딸도 아직 좋은 사람들과 어울리게 될지 몰라!\"\n\n그러더니, 딸이 자기를 바라보는 그 의아해하는 진지함 속에서 딸의 손이 저도 모르게 돈을 그러쥐는 것을 보고는, 늙은 여인은 그것을 손에 넣으려 한층 서두르며 황급히 덧붙였다. \"하지만 가서 뭘 좀 사 올게. 가서 뭘 좀 사 올 거야.\"\n\n그녀가 딸 앞에 손을 내민 채 서 있자, 딸은 그 돈을 다시 한번 흘끗 보고는, 그것을 내주기 전에 입술에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"아니, 앨리! 거기다 입을 맞추는 거니?\" 늙은 여인이 키득거렸다. \"꼭 나 같구나—나도 자주 그런단다. 오, 이건 우리한테 참 고마운 거지!\" 그녀는 변색된 제 반 페니 동전들을 자루 같은 목에 꼭 눌러 대며 말했다, \"모든 면에서 우리한테 참 고맙지, 다만 무더기로 들어오지 않는다는 것만 빼면!\"\n\n\"내가 여기 입을 맞춘 건, 어머니,\" 딸이 말했다, \"아니, 그때 입을 맞춘 건—전엔 그래 본 적이 있는지 모르겠지만—이걸 준 사람을 봐서예요.\"\n\n\"준 사람이라, 응, 얘야?\" 늙은 여인이 응수했고, 돈을 받아 들 때 그 흐릿한 눈이 번득였다. \"그래! 준 사람이 이걸 더 멀리 가게 해 줄 수 있을 때면, 나도 준 사람을 봐서 입을 맞춰 주마. 하지만 가서 이걸 쓰고 올게, 얘야. 곧 돌아오마.\"\n\n\"어머니, 무척 많은 걸 아시는 것처럼 말씀하시네요.\" 딸이 눈으로 그녀를 문까지 좇으며 말했다. \"우리가 헤어진 뒤로 무척 영리해지셨어요.\"\n\n\"알다마다!\" 늙은 여인이 한두 걸음 되돌아오며 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"네가 생각하는 것보다 많이 알아, 그가 생각하는 것보다 많이 알지, 얘야, 그건 차차 말해 주마. 나는 다 알아.\"\n\n딸은 못 믿겠다는 듯 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그의 동생에 대해서도 안다, 앨리스.\" 늙은 여인이, 정말로 소름 끼치는 악의의 곁눈질로 목을 길게 빼며 말했다, \"그 동생은 너 있던 곳에 갔을 수도 있는 사람이야—돈을 훔쳐서 말이지—그리고 지금은 제 누이랑 같이 저 너머, 런던을 벗어나는 북쪽 길가에 살고 있지.\"\n\n\"어디라고요?\"\n\n\"런던을 벗어나는 북쪽 길가야, 얘야. 보고 싶으면 그 집을 보여 주마. 자기 형 집처럼 점잖긴 해도, 자랑할 만한 데는 못 돼. 아니, 아니, 안 돼,\" 늙은 여인이, 딸이 벌떡 일어났으므로, 고개를 흔들고 웃으며 외쳤다, \"지금은 안 돼. 너무 멀어. 돌무더기가 쌓여 있는 이정표 옆이야—내일, 얘야, 날이 좋고 네 마음이 내키면. 하지만 나는 가서 이걸 쓰고—\"\n\n\"멈춰요!\" 그러더니 딸이, 아까의 그 격정을 불처럼 타오르게 하며 그녀에게 와락 달려들었다. \"그 누이라는 게 갈색 머리에, 고운 얼굴을 한 악마예요?\"\n\n늙은 여인은 놀라고 겁에 질려 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그 여자 얼굴에서 그의 그림자를 봤어요! 따로 외따로 서 있는 붉은 집이죠. 문 앞에는 작은 초록색 현관이 있고요.\"\n\n다시 늙은 여인이 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"내가 오늘 그 안에 앉아 있었어요! 그 돈 도로 줘요.\"\n\n\"앨리스! 얘야!\"\n\n\"그 돈 도로 줘요, 안 그러면 다칠 거예요.\"\n\n그녀는 말하면서 늙은 여인의 손에서 그것을 빼앗았고, 어머니의 푸념과 애원에는 아랑곳도 않은 채, 벗어 두었던 옷가지를 걸치고는, 쏜살같은 속도로 서둘러 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n어머니는 할 수 있는 한 절뚝거리며 뒤따랐고, 그들을 에워싼 비와 바람과 어둠을 상대로 떠드는 것이나 다름없이, 딸을 상대로 항변해 보았지만 아무 소용이 없었다. 제 목적에 완강하고 사나우며, 그 밖의 모든 것에 무심한 채, 딸은 마치 여행도 피로도 모르는 사람처럼 날씨와 거리를 무릅쓰고, 자기가 도움을 받았던 그 집을 향해 나아갔다. 십오 분쯤 걸은 뒤, 기진맥진해 숨이 가빠진 늙은 여인은 딸의 치맛자락을 붙드는 모험을 감행했다. 그러나 그 이상은 감히 하지 못했고, 그들은 비와 어둠을 뚫고 말없이 나아갔다. 어머니는 이따금 푸념 한마디를 내뱉으면서도, 딸이 떨쳐 버리고 자기를 뒤에 남겨 둘까 봐 그것을 삼켰다. 그리고 딸은 묵묵부답이었다.\n\n자정이 한 시간쯤 남았을 무렵, 그들은 반듯한 거리들을 뒤로하고, 그 집이 자리한 그 중립 지대의 한층 깊은 어둠 속으로 들어섰다. 도시는 멀리, 음산하고 험상궂게 누워 있었고, 황량한 바람이 그 트인 공간 위로 울부짖었으며, 사방은 검고 거칠고 적막했다.\n\n\"여긴 나한테 딱 어울리는 곳이야!\" 딸이 멈춰 서서 뒤돌아보며 말했다. \"오늘 전에 여기 왔을 때도 그렇게 생각했어.\"\n\n\"앨리스, 얘야,\" 어머니가 치맛자락을 살며시 잡아당기며 외쳤다. \"앨리스!\"\n\n\"이번엔 또 뭐예요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"그 돈 돌려주지 마라, 우리 아가. 제발 그러지 마. 우리 형편에 그럴 여유 없어. 우리한텐 저녁거리가 필요해, 얘야. 돈은 돈이지, 누가 주든. 네가 무슨 말을 하든, 그 돈은 그냥 가지고 있어.\"\n\n\"저기 봐요!\" 딸의 대답은 그게 다였다. \"저게 내가 말한 그 집이에요. 저거예요?\"\n\n늙은 여인은 그렇다고 고개를 끄덕였다. 그리고 몇 걸음 더 가자 문턱에 이르렀다. 앨리스가 옷을 말리려 앉아 있었던 그 방에는 난롯불과 촛불의 빛이 있었다. 그녀가 문을 두드리자, 존 카커가 그 방에서 나타났다.\n\n그는 이런 시각에 그런 방문객을 보고 놀라, 앨리스에게 무슨 일이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"당신 누이를 만나고 싶어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"오늘 내게 돈을 준 그 여자요.\"\n\n그녀의 높아진 목소리에, 해리엇이 나왔다.\n\n\"오!\" 앨리스가 말했다. \"당신 여기 있군요! 나를 기억해요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 의아해하며 대답했다.\n\n그녀 앞에서 자신을 낮추었던 그 얼굴이, 이제는 도무지 꺾을 수 없는 증오와 적의로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 팔을 부드럽게 만졌던 그 손은, 마치 기꺼이 그녀의 목을 졸라 버리기라도 하려는 듯, 그토록 사악한 의도를 드러내며 그러쥐어져 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 보호를 구하듯 오라비에게 바싹 다가섰다.\n\n\"내가 당신과 말을 나누면서도 당신을 못 알아봤다니! 내가 당신 곁에 다가가면서도, 내 피가 따끔거리는 걸로 당신 핏줄에 무슨 피가 흐르는지 느끼지 못했다니!\" 앨리스가 위협하는 몸짓으로 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요? 내가 뭘 어쨌다고요?\"\n\n\"어쨌느냐고!\" 상대가 대꾸했다. \"당신은 나를 당신 난롯가에 앉혔어. 내게 먹을 것과 돈을 줬지. 내게 당신의 동정을 베풀었어! 당신이! 내가 이름만 들어도 침을 뱉을 그 당신이!\"\n\n늙은 여인은, 자기 추함을 사뭇 끔찍하게 만드는 악의로, 딸의 말을 확인해 주듯 오라비와 누이를 향해 시든 손을 흔들었지만, 그러면서도 딸의 치맛자락을 다시 잡아당기며 그 돈을 가지고 있으라고 애원했다.\n\n\"내가 당신 손에 눈물 한 방울이라도 떨어뜨렸다면, 그 손이 말라 비틀어지기를! 내가 당신이 듣는 데서 부드러운 말 한마디라도 했다면, 그게 당신 귀를 멀게 하기를! 내가 내 입술로 당신을 만졌다면, 그 닿음이 당신에게 독이 되기를! 내게 거처를 내준 이 지붕에 저주가 있기를! 당신 머리 위에 슬픔과 수치가 있기를! 당신에게 속한 모든 것에 파멸이 있기를!\"\n\n그 말을 하면서, 그녀는 돈을 땅바닥에 내던지고는 발로 걷어차 버렸다.\n\n\"이걸 흙먼지 속에 짓밟아 버리겠어. 이게 천국으로 가는 내 길을 깔아 준다 해도 받지 않을 거야! 오늘 나를 여기로 데려온 이 피 흐르는 발이, 당신 집으로 나를 이끌기 전에 차라리 썩어 문드러져 떨어졌더라면!\"\n\n해리엇은 새파랗게 질려 떨면서도 오라비를 제지하고, 그녀가 끊김 없이 말을 이어 가게 두었다.\n\n\"내가 돌아온 첫 순간에, 당신이든 당신 이름을 가진 누구에게든 동정받고 용서받았다니, 참 잘된 일이야! 당신이 내게 친절하고 착한 귀부인 노릇을 해 줬다니, 참 잘된 일이고! 죽을 때 당신에게 고마워하지. 당신과 당신 일족 모두를 위해 기도해 주겠어, 그건 틀림없으니까!\"\n\n그녀는 마치 땅바닥에 증오를 흩뿌리듯, 그리고 그것으로 거기 서 있는 이들을 파멸에 바치듯, 손을 사납게 내젓고는, 검은 하늘을 한 번 올려다보고, 거친 밤 속으로 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔다.\n\n어머니는 몇 번이고 헛되이 딸의 치맛자락을 잡아당겼고, 문턱에 떨어진 돈을 자기 정신을 온통 거기 쏟아붓는 듯한 탐욕으로 노려보고 있었던지라, 집이 어두워질 때까지 근처를 어슬렁거리다가 그것을 도로 손에 넣을 요량으로 진창을 더듬어 보았으련만. 그러나 딸이 그녀를 끌고 갔고, 둘은 곧장 자기들 거처로 돌아가는 길에 올랐다. 늙은 여인은 길에서 그 손실을 두고 흐느끼며 한탄했고, 재회한 바로 그 첫날 밤에 자기에게서 저녁거리를 앗아 간 어여쁜 딸의 도리 없는 행실을, 감히 드러낼 수 있는 만큼 짜증스럽게 한탄했다.\n\n그녀는 거친 부스러기 몇 점을 빼면 저녁도 못 먹고 잠자리에 들었다. 그리고 도리 없는 딸이 잠든 지 한참이 지나서까지, 자투리 불 위에 앉아 그 부스러기를 우물우물 씹으며 중얼거렸다.\n\n이 비참한 어머니와 이 비참한 딸은, 그저 때로 더 높은 곳에서 횡행하는 어떤 사회적 악덕이 가장 낮은 등급으로 끌어내려진 모습일 뿐일까? 원 안에 또 원이 겹겹이 든 이 둥근 세상에서, 우리는 높은 등급에서 낮은 등급으로 지친 여정을 떠나, 끝내 그 둘이 바싹 붙어 있음을, 양극단이 서로 맞닿아 있음을, 그리고 우리 여정의 끝이 다름 아닌 우리의 출발점임을 알게 되는 것일까? 재료와 결의 큰 차이를 감안하더라도, 이 씨실의 무늬가 고귀한 혈통 사이에서도 되풀이되고 있었던 것은 아닐까?\n\n말해 보라, 이디스 돔비여! 그리고 어머니 중의 으뜸인 클레오파트라여, 그대의 증언을 들려 다오!\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV. The Happy Pair\n\n거리에 드리웠던 어두운 얼룩은 사라졌다. 돔비 씨의 저택은, 설령 다른 집들 사이에서 여전히 하나의 빈틈으로 남아 있다 하더라도, 그것은 오직 그 광채에 있어 견줄 데가 없어 다른 집들을 오만하게 밀쳐 내기 때문일 뿐이다. 흔히들 집은 아무리 초라해도 집이라고 한다. 그 반대의 경우에도 이 말이 들어맞아 집은 아무리 위풍당당해도 집이라면, 여기 가정의 신들을 모실 얼마나 대단한 제단이 세워진 것인가!\n\n오늘 저녁 창문마다 불빛이 반짝이고, 난롯불의 불그레한 광채가 휘장과 부드러운 양탄자 위에 따뜻하고 환하게 비치며, 만찬은 차려지기를 기다리고 있고, 만찬 식탁은 비록 단 네 사람을 위한 것이지만 보기 좋게 차려져 있으며, 식기대는 은그릇으로 묵직하다. 최근의 변화 이후 이 집이 사람이 살 수 있도록 꾸며진 것은 이번이 처음이며, 그 행복한 부부는 시시각각 기다려지고 있다.\n\n집안 식구들 사이에서 자아내는 흥미와 기대에 있어, 결혼식 아침에 버금가는 것이 바로 이 귀가의 저녁이다. 퍼치 부인은 부엌에서 차를 마시고 있다. 그녀는 저택을 한 바퀴 둘러보고, 비단과 다마스크 천을 마(碼) 단위로 값을 매겨 보았으며, 감탄과 경이를 표현할 수 있는 사전 안팎의 감탄사를 죄다 써먹어 버렸다. 가구상의 십장은 모자를—그 안에는 손수건이 들어 있었는데 둘 다 니스 냄새를 강하게 풍겼다—현관 의자 밑에 두고는, 집 안을 어슬렁거리며 위로는 처마 장식을 올려다보고 아래로는 양탄자를 내려다보며, 이따금 말없는 황홀경에 빠져 주머니에서 자를 꺼내, 값나가는 물건들을 형언할 수 없는 감회로 살금살금 재어 보곤 한다. 요리사는 한껏 기분이 들떠서, 사람이 많은 자리에 자기를 두라고 한다(이제 그렇게 될 거라고 육 펜스 내기를 걸 수도 있다면서). 자기는 쾌활한 성미이며, 어릴 적부터 늘 그래 왔고, 누가 알든 상관없다는 것인데, 이 말에 퍼치 부인의 가슴에서는 지지와 찬동의 맞장구치는 중얼거림이 새어 나온다. 가정부가 바라는 것이라곤 그저 두 분의 행복뿐이다—하지만 결혼이란 복권 같은 것이라, 생각하면 생각할수록 그녀는 독신 생활의 독립과 안전을 더욱 절감한다. 타울린슨 씨는 침울하고 무뚝뚝하게, 자기 생각도 그러하다며, 거기에 전쟁이나 한판 붙여 주고 프랑스 놈들이나 쳐부수자고 말한다—이 젊은이는 외국인이라면 누구나 프랑스인이며 자연의 법칙상 그럴 수밖에 없다는 막연한 생각을 품고 있기 때문이다.\n\n바퀴 소리가 새로 날 때마다, 그들은 무슨 말을 하던 중이든 멈추고 귀를 기울인다. 한 번 이상, 다들 벌떡 일어나 \"오셨다!\" 하고 외친다. 그러나 아직 오신 게 아니다. 그래서 요리사는 두 번이나 미뤄진 만찬을 두고 슬퍼하기 시작하고, 가구상의 십장은 여전히 더없이 행복한 몽상에 방해받지 않은 채 방마다 어슬렁거린다!\n\n플로렌스는 아버지와 새어머니를 맞이할 채비를 하고 있다. 가슴속에서 두근거리는 감정이 기쁨에서 비롯된 것인지 고통에서 비롯된 것인지, 그녀는 잘 알지 못한다. 그러나 설레는 마음이 그녀의 뺨에 더한 홍조를, 두 눈에 더한 광채를 보태 준다. 아래층에서는—그녀에 대해 이야기할 때면 그들은 늘 나직이 말하므로—서로 머리를 맞대고, 오늘 밤 플로렌스 아가씨가 얼마나 아름다워 보이는지, 가엾게도 얼마나 사랑스러운 숙녀로 자라셨는지 이야기한다. 잠시 침묵이 흐르고, 그러다 요리사가 좌장(座長)으로서 자기 소감이 기다려지고 있음을 느끼고, ……일지 궁금하다고 운을 떼고는—거기서 멈춘다. 가정부도 궁금해하고, 퍼치 부인도 궁금해하는데, 이 부인은 무엇을 궁금해하는지는 전혀 가리지 않으면서도 남들이 궁금해할 때면 늘 따라서 궁금해하는 행복한 사교적 재능을 지녔다. 이제 부인들의 기분을 자기 수준으로 끌어내릴 기회를 포착한 타울린슨 씨는, 두고 보면 안다고 말한다. 어떤 사람들은 이 일에서 무사히 빠져나왔으면 좋겠다고 한다. 그러자 요리사가 한숨을 앞장세우며 \"아, 참 이상한 세상이야, 정말로!\" 하고 중얼거리고, 그 말이 식탁을 한 바퀴 돌고 나자, 달래는 투로 덧붙인다. \"하지만 플로렌스 아가씨야 무슨 변화가 있다 한들 더 나빠질 리는 없잖아요, 톰.\" 타울린슨 씨의 응수는 무시무시한 의미를 잔뜩 머금은 채 \"오, 과연 그럴까!\"라는 것이며, 한낱 사내가 그보다 더 예언적일 수도, 그 말을 더 보탤 수도 없으리라는 것을 의식하고는, 입을 다문다.\n\n스큐턴 부인은 사랑하는 딸과 사랑하는 사위를 두 팔 벌려 맞이할 채비를 하고서, 그 목적에 걸맞게 매우 젊은이다운 차림에, 소매가 짧은 옷을 입고 있다. 그러나 지금 그녀의 무르익은 매력은 자기 처소의 그늘 속에서 피어나고 있는데, 몇 시간 전 그곳을 차지한 이후로 한 번도 나오지 않았으며, 만찬이 미뤄진 탓에 그곳에서 점점 안달이 나고 있다. 해골처럼 비쩍 말랐어야 마땅하나 실은 통통한 처녀인 하녀는, 반대로 더없이 상냥한 상태이다. 자기 분기별 봉급이 전보다 훨씬 안전하다고 여기며, 숙식이 크게 나아지리라 내다보고 있기 때문이다.\n\n이 멋들어진 집이 기다리고 있는 그 행복한 부부는 대체 어디에 있는가? 증기와 조류와 바람과 말들이, 그런 행복 앞에서 머뭇거리느라 죄다 속력을 늦추기라도 하는가? 그들 주위를 맴도는 사랑과 우아의 무리가 그 수가 너무 많아 진행을 더디게 하는가? 그들의 행복한 길 위에 꽃이 어찌나 많은지, 가시 없는 장미와 더없이 향기로운 들장미에 얽히지 않고서는 좀처럼 나아갈 수 없기라도 한가?\n\n마침내 그들이 왔다! 바퀴 소리가 들리고, 점점 커지더니, 마차 한 대가 현관 앞으로 다가온다! 그 얄미운 외국인이 우레 같은 노크 소리를 울려, 문을 열려고 달려가는 타울린슨 씨 일행보다 한발 앞선다. 돔비 씨와 그의 신부가 내려서, 팔짱을 끼고 안으로 걸어 들어온다.\n\n\"내 더없이 사랑스러운 에디스!\" 계단에서 떨리는 목소리가 외친다. \"내 더없이 사랑스러운 돔비!\" 그러고는 짧은 소매가 그 행복한 부부를 차례로 휘감으며 끌어안는다.\n\n플로렌스도 현관까지 내려와 있었으나, 앞으로 나서지는 않았다. 더 가깝고 더 사랑하는 이들의 격정이 가라앉기를 기다리며 자기의 수줍은 환영을 미뤄 둔 것이었다. 그러나 에디스의 눈은 문턱에서 그녀를 찾아냈다. 그리고 자기의 예민한 어머니를 뺨에 가벼운 입맞춤으로 떼어 내고는, 서둘러 플로렌스에게로 다가가 그녀를 끌어안았다.\n\n\"잘 지냈니, 플로렌스?\" 돔비 씨가 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n플로렌스가 떨면서 그 손을 들어 입술로 가져갈 때, 그녀는 그의 시선과 마주쳤다. 그 눈길은 충분히 차갑고 멀었지만, 그 안에서 그가 전에 보인 적 없는 무언가의 관심 같은 것이 엿보인다고 생각하니 그녀의 마음이 일렁였다. 그것은 심지어 그녀를 보고 일종의 옅은 놀라움을, 그것도 불쾌하지 않은 놀라움을 드러내기까지 했다. 그녀는 더는 감히 그의 눈으로 시선을 들지 못했지만, 그가 다시 한번, 그에 못지않게 호의적으로 자기를 바라보았음을 느꼈다. 오, 그녀가 새롭고 아름다운 어머니를 통해 그의 마음을 얻는 법을 배우게 되리라는 희망이, 비록 만질 수도 없고 근거도 없는 이런 확인에 의해서나마 일깨워졌을 때, 얼마나 큰 기쁨의 전율이 그녀를 꿰뚫고 지나갔던가!\n\n\"옷 갈아입는 데 오래 걸리지는 않으리라 보오, 돔비 부인?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"곧 준비될 거예요.\"\n\n\"십오 분 안에 만찬을 올리라고 하시오.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 돔비 씨는 자기 의상실로 성큼성큼 걸어갔고, 돔비 부인은 자기 방으로 위층에 올라갔다. 스큐턴 부인과 플로렌스는 응접실로 갔는데, 그곳에서 그 훌륭한 어머니는 억누를 수 없는 눈물 몇 방울을 흘리는 것이 자기 도리라 여겼으니, 그 눈물은 딸의 행복에 못 이겨 자아내진 것으로 여겨졌다. 사위가 나타났을 때도 그녀는 여전히 그 눈물을, 손수건의 레이스 달린 모서리로, 매우 조심스럽게 닦아 내고 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비, 그 더없이 즐거운 도시 파리는 어떠하셨나요?\" 그녀가 감정을 가라앉히며 물었다.\n\n\"추웠습니다,\" 돔비 씨가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"여전히 흥겹지요,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"당연히.\"\n\n\"딱히 그렇지도 않았습니다. 따분하더군요,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비!\" 짐짓 장난스럽게. \"따분하다니요!\"\n\n\"제게는 그런 인상이었습니다, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 점잖은 정중함으로 말했다. \"돔비 부인도 그곳을 따분하게 여긴 듯합니다. 한두 번 그렇게 생각한다고 말하더군요.\"\n\n\"아니, 이런 못된 것 같으니!\" 스큐턴 부인이, 이제 막 들어선 사랑하는 딸을 놀려 대며 외쳤다, \"파리에 대해 그렇게 끔찍하게 불경한 말들을 하고 다녔단 말이냐?\"\n\n에디스는 지친 기색으로 눈썹을 치켜올렸다. 그러고는 새롭고 멋진 장식으로 단장한 방들을 펼쳐 보이려고 활짝 열어 둔 접이문을 지나면서, 지나가며 그것들을 흘끗 보는 둥 마는 둥 하고는, 플로렌스 곁에 앉았다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"이 사람들이 우리가 넌지시 일러 준 생각을 하나하나 얼마나 멋지게 구현해 놓았는지요. 이 집을 그야말로 완벽한 궁전으로 만들어 놓았어요, 정말로.\"\n\n\"훌륭합니다,\" 돔비 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"비용을 아끼지 말라고 지시했습니다. 돈으로 할 수 있는 일은 모두 다 했으리라 봅니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 돈으로 못 할 게 무엇이 있겠어요, 사랑하는 돔비?\" 클레오파트라가 말했다.\n\n\"강력한 것이지요, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n그는 엄숙한 태도로 아내 쪽을 바라보았으나, 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"바라건대, 돔비 부인,\" 그가 잠시 침묵한 뒤, 특히 또렷하게 그녀에게 말을 건넸다, \"이 개조가 당신 마음에 드셨으면 하오?\"\n\n\"더 바랄 수 없을 만큼 훌륭하군요,\" 그녀가 오만한 무심함으로 대꾸했다. \"당연히 그래야겠지요. 그리고 그러리라 봅니다.\"\n\n그 도도한 얼굴에는 경멸의 표정이 늘 어려 있어 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 듯 보였다. 그러나 그의 재력을 근거로 한 감탄이나 존경이나 배려의 요구—그 자체로는 아무리 사소하고 평범하든—를 받아들일 때의 그 경멸은, 새롭고 다른 표정이었으니, 그녀가 지을 수 있는 다른 어떤 표정도 그 강렬함에서 견줄 바가 못 되었다. 자기의 위대함에 싸여 있던 돔비 씨가 이를 조금이라도 알아챘는지 어떤지는 모르나, 그가 완전히 깨우칠 만한 기회는 이미 모자라지 않았다. 그리고 바로 그 순간, 자기 자랑의 화제를 재빠르고 경멸스럽게 훑어본 뒤 그에게 떨어진 그 검은 눈의 한 번의 눈길로도 그것은 이루어질 수 있었으리라. 그는 그 한 번의 눈길에서 읽을 수 있었으리라—그의 재력이 만 배로 불어난다 한들, 재력 그 자체를 위해서는, 그에게 매여 있으나 온 영혼을 다해 그에게 맞선 그 반항적인 여인에게서 누그러진 인정의 눈길 하나조차 얻어 낼 수 없으리라는 것을. 그는 그 한 번의 눈길에서 읽을 수 있었으리라—그녀 자신에게 미치는 그 천하고 돈에 매인 영향력마저도 그녀가 멸시하면서, 동시에 그 최대한의 위력을 자기의 권리로, 자기의 거래로—그녀가 그의 아내가 된 대가인 그 비천하고 하찮은 보수로—주장하고 있다는 것을. 그는 그 한 번의 눈길에서 읽을 수 있었으리라—제 머리를 늘 제 경멸과 자존심의 번개에 맞도록 드러내 놓는 그녀에게, 그의 재력의 위세를 향한 더없이 순진무구한 암시조차도 그녀를 새로이 욕보이고, 자기 존중에서 그녀를 더 깊이 가라앉히며, 그녀 내면의 황폐와 폐허를 더욱 완전하게 만든다는 것을.\n\n그러나 만찬이 알려졌고, 돔비 씨가 클레오파트라를 모시고 내려갔다. 에디스와 그의 딸이 뒤따랐다. 그녀는 식기대 위의 금은 과시물을 마치 쌓아 놓은 흙더미라도 되는 양 휙 지나치며, 주위의 우아한 것들에 눈길 한 번 주는 것조차 마다하고는, 처음으로 그의 식탁에 자기 자리를 잡고서, 그 향연에 조각상처럼 앉았다.\n\n돔비 씨는 자신도 조각상 같은 면이 다분한지라, 자기의 아름다운 아내가 꿈쩍 않고 도도하고 차가운 것을 보고 흡족해했다. 그녀의 거동이 늘 우아하고 기품 있는 만큼, 전반적인 처신으로서 이런 태도는 그에게 흐뭇하고 마음에 맞는 것이었다. 따라서 그는 늘 그러던 위엄으로 좌장 노릇을 하면서, 자기 쪽에서 어떤 따스함이나 유쾌함으로도 아내를 비추는 일 없이, 냉정한 만족으로 식탁의 주인 노릇을 했다. 그리하여 이 입주(入住) 만찬은, 비록 아래층에서는 대단한 성공이라거나 매우 전도유망한 시작이라고 여겨지지 않았을지언정, 위층에서는 충분히 정중하고 점잖고 서릿발 같은 분위기로 지나갔다.\n\n차를 마신 직후, 스큐턴 부인은—사랑하는 딸이 자기 마음의 남자와 맺어진 것을 바라보는 데서 솟아난 행복의 감정에 그만 완전히 압도되어 기진맥진한 척했으나, 한 시간 내내 부채 뒤에서 줄곧 하품을 한 것으로 보아 이 가족 모임이 다소 따분하다고 여겼으리라 짐작할 만한 이유가 있다—잠자리에 들었다. 에디스 또한 말없이 물러가서는 다시 돌아오지 않았다. 그리하여, 위층에 올라가 디오게네스와 잠시 이야기를 나누고 온 플로렌스가 자그마한 반짇고리를 들고 응접실로 돌아왔을 때, 그곳에는 음울한 장엄함 속에 이리저리 거닐고 있는 아버지 말고는 아무도 없었다.\n\n\"죄송해요. 저 나갈까요, 아빠?\" 플로렌스가 문가에서 망설이며 가냘프게 말했다.\n\n\"아니다,\" 돔비 씨가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 대꾸했다. \"여기는 네가 마음대로 드나들어도 된다, 플로렌스. 여긴 내 개인 방이 아니니까.\"\n\n플로렌스는 들어와서 일감을 들고 멀찍이 떨어진 작은 탁자에 앉았다. 그녀는 난생처음으로—아주 어린 시절부터 그 순간까지 기억하는 한 그야말로 처음으로—아버지와 단둘이, 그의 동무로서 함께 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그녀, 그의 타고난 동무이자 그의 유일한 자식, 외로운 삶과 슬픔 속에서 가슴이 찢어지는 고통을 겪어 온 아이, 거부당한 사랑 속에서도 밤이면 결코 눈물 어린 축복 없이는 하느님께 그의 이름을 읊조린 적이 없었으니 그 축복은 저주보다도 그에게 더 무거웠던 아이, 오직 그의 품 안에서 죽을 수만 있다면 하고 어려서 죽기를 기도했던 아이, 그 모든 세월 내내 무시와 냉대와 미움의 고통을, 그를 변명하고 그를 두둔하며, 마치 그의 수호천사처럼, 참을성 있고 아무것도 바라지 않는 사랑으로 갚아 온 아이가!\n\n그녀는 떨었고, 두 눈은 흐려졌다. 그가 방 안을 거니는 동안 그의 모습은 그녀 앞에서 키도 덩치도 자라나는 듯했다. 어느 순간엔 온통 흐릿하고 또렷하지 않았다가, 또 어느 순간엔 다시 선명하고 또렷해졌다. 그러다가 지금 이 일이, 꼭 이대로, 수많은 세월 전에 일어났던 것 같다는 생각이 들었다. 그녀는 그를 향해 애틋이 다가가고 싶으면서도, 그가 다가오는 것은 움츠러들며 피했다. 잘못이라곤 모르는 순진한 아이에게는 부자연스러운 감정이었다! 그 부드러운 천성을, 그 씨앗을 뿌리려고 날카로운 쟁기로 갈아엎도록 이끈 그 손이야말로 부자연스러웠다!\n\n플로렌스는 자기 괴로움으로 그를 괴롭히거나 거스르지 않으려 작정하고, 마음을 다잡으며 조용히 일감에 매달려 앉았다. 그는 방을 몇 번 더 이리저리 가로질러 거닐다가, 거니는 것을 멈추었다. 그러고는 안락의자가 놓인, 얼마쯤 떨어진 어둑한 구석으로 물러나, 손수건으로 머리를 덮고 잠을 청하려 자리를 잡았다.\n\n플로렌스에게는 그저 거기 앉아 그를 지켜보는 것으로 족했다. 이따금 그의 의자 쪽으로 눈길을 돌리고, 얼굴은 일감에 골몰해 있을 때면 마음으로 그를 지켜보았다. 그리고 자기가 거기 있는데도 그가 잠들 수 있다는 것, 자기의 낯설고 오랫동안 금지되어 온 존재가 그를 안절부절못하게 만들지 않는다는 것을 생각하니 서글프면서도 기뻤다.\n\n만일 그녀가 알았더라면 그녀의 생각은 어떠했을까—그가 한결같이 그녀를 바라보고 있었다는 것을, 그의 얼굴을 덮은 그 천이, 우연이든 의도이든, 시야가 트이도록 맞추어져 있어, 그의 시선이 한순간도 그녀의 얼굴을 떠난 적이 없었다는 것을. 그녀가 그 어둑하고 컴컴한 구석으로 그를 바라볼 때, 그 어떤 세상의 웅변가들보다도 그 소리 없는 말 속에서 더 간곡하고 더 애처로운, 그리고 그 말 없는 호소로 그를 더 가까이 질책하는 그녀의 그 말하는 듯한 눈이, 그의 눈과 마주쳤건만 정작 그것을 알지 못했다는 것을! 그녀가 다시 일감 위로 고개를 숙였을 때, 그가 한결 편안하게 숨을 내쉬면서도, 여전히 똑같은 주의로 그녀를—그녀의 흰 이마와 흘러내린 머리카락과 바삐 움직이는 두 손을—바라보고 있었으며, 한번 끌린 뒤로는 도무지 눈을 돌릴 힘이 없는 듯했다는 것을!\n\n그러는 동안 그의 생각은 어떠했을까? 그는 어떤 감정으로, 알지 못하는 딸에게 은밀히 던진 그 주의 깊은 응시를 그토록 길게 끌었을까? 그 고요한 자태와 온순한 눈에 그를 향한 책망이 담겨 있었던가? 그가 등한시해 온 그녀의 권리를 느끼기 시작했던가, 그리고 그것이 마침내 그의 마음 깊이 와닿아, 자기의 잔인한 불의에 대한 어떤 자각으로 그를 일깨웠던가?\n\n더없이 엄혹하고 모진 사내들의 삶에도 마음이 누그러지는 순간들이 있는 법이다, 비록 그런 사내들이 흔히 그 비밀을 잘 감추기는 하지만. 자기도 모르는 새 거의 한 사람의 여인으로 변모한 그녀의 아름다운 모습이, 그의 그 오만한 삶 속에서도 그런 순간들을 불러일으켰는지도 모른다. 손 닿는 곳에 행복한 가정을 두고 있었음을—제 발치에 엎드린 가정의 정령을 두고 있었음을—그것을 그 목이 뻣뻣하고 음울한 오만 속에서 못 본 체하고는, 멀리 헤매다가 길을 잃었음을 깨닫는 어떤 스쳐 가는 생각이, 그것들을 자아냈는지도 모른다. 오직 그녀의 눈으로만 말해졌을 뿐, 그가 그것을 읽고 있는 줄도 모르는 채, 또렷이 들려오는 어떤 소박한 웅변이—\"제가 지켜본 임종의 자리들에 걸고, 제가 겪은 어린 시절에 걸고, 이 음울한 집에서 한밤중에 이렇게 만난 것에 걸고, 가슴의 고통 속에서 제게서 짜내어진 그 부르짖음에 걸고, 오, 아버지, 너무 늦기 전에 저를 돌아보시고 제 사랑 안에서 안식처를 찾으세요!\"라고 말하는 그 웅변이—그것들을 붙들어 두었는지도 모른다. 죽은 아들이 이제 새로운 인연들로 대신되었으니, 자기의 애정에서 밀려난 것쯤은 용서할 수 있다는 식의, 더 비루하고 더 낮은 생각들이 그것들을 일으켰는지도 모른다. 그녀를 하나의 장식물로, 자기 주위의 모든 장식과 호사와 연결 짓는 것만으로 충분했는지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 바라보면 바라볼수록 그녀에게 점점 더 마음이 누그러졌다. 바라보고 있노라니, 그녀는 그가 사랑했던 그 아이와 뒤섞여, 그는 좀처럼 그 둘을 갈라 놓을 수가 없었다. 바라보고 있노라니, 그는 한순간 더 맑고 더 환한 빛 속에서 그녀를 보았으니, 그녀가 그 아이의 베갯머리에 몸을 굽힌 모습이—그의 경쟁자로서가 아니라—그 무슨 끔찍한 생각이런가—그의 가정의 정령으로서, 그리고 그 행위 속에서 자기 자신을 못지않게 돌보아 주는 모습으로 보였으니, 그동안 그는 다시금 그 자그마한 침대 발치에서 손에 머리를 떨군 채 앉아 있었다. 그는 그녀에게 말을 걸어, 그녀를 자기 곁으로 부르고 싶은 마음이 들었다. \"플로렌스, 이리 오너라!\"라는 말이 그의 입술로 올라오고 있었다—그러나 더디게, 그리고 어렵사리, 그 말은 너무도 낯설었으니—그러다가 계단 위의 발소리에 막혀 목구멍에서 삼켜졌다.\n\n그의 아내의 발소리였다. 그녀는 만찬용 드레스를 헐렁한 가운으로 갈아입고, 머리를 풀어 목덜미를 따라 자유로이 늘어뜨리고 있었다. 그러나 그를 놀라게 한 것은 이런 변화가 아니었다.\n\n\"플로렌스, 얘야,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"너를 사방으로 찾아다녔단다.\"\n\n플로렌스 곁에 앉으면서 그녀는 몸을 굽혀 그 손에 입을 맞추었다. 그는 자기 아내를 거의 알아보지 못했다. 그녀가 너무도 달라져 있었다. 그것은 단지 그 미소가 그에게 낯설어서만은 아니었으니—하기야 그런 미소는 본 적도 없었지만—그녀의 태도, 목소리의 어조, 두 눈의 빛, 그 모든 것에 깃든 관심과 신뢰와 기쁘게 해 주려는 사랑스러운 바람—이것은 에디스가 아니었다.\n\n\"가만히요, 엄마. 아빠가 주무세요.\"\n\n이제는 에디스였다. 그녀는 그가 있는 구석 쪽을 바라보았고, 그는 그 얼굴과 태도를 아주 잘 알았다.\n\n\"네가 여기 있으리라고는 거의 생각지 못했단다, 플로렌스.\"\n\n다시, 한순간에 얼마나 달라지고 얼마나 부드러워졌는지!\n\n\"여기서 일찍 자리를 떴단다,\" 에디스가 이어 말했다, \"일부러 위층에 올라가 너와 이야기를 나누려고. 그런데 네 방에 가 보니 내 작은 새가 날아가 버렸기에, 그때부터 줄곧 거기서, 새가 돌아오기를 기다리고 있었지.\"\n\n설령 정말로 새였다 한들, 그녀가 플로렌스를 가슴에 안은 것보다 더 다정하고 더 부드럽게 안을 수는 없었으리라.\n\n\"가자, 얘야!\"\n\n\"아빠가 깨어나셨을 때 제가 있을 거라고는 기대하지 않으시겠지요, 아마,\" 플로렌스가 망설이며 말했다.\n\n\"네 생각엔 기대하실 것 같니, 플로렌스?\" 에디스가 그녀를 똑바로 바라보며 말했다.\n\n플로렌스는 고개를 떨구고, 일어서서, 반짇고리를 챙겼다. 에디스가 자기 팔로 그녀의 팔을 끼었고, 둘은 자매처럼 방을 나갔다. 그녀의 발걸음마저도 자기에게는 사뭇 다르고 낯설게 느껴진다고, 돔비 씨는 그녀가 문께로 가는 것을 눈으로 좇으며 생각했다.\n\n그는 그 어둑한 구석에 어찌나 오래 앉아 있었던지, 그날 밤 그가 몸을 움직이기 전에 교회 시계가 세 번이나 시각을 알렸다. 그동안 내내 그의 얼굴은 여전히 플로렌스가 앉아 있던 자리에 골똘히 향해 있었다. 촛불이 사위어 꺼져 가면서 방은 점점 어두워졌다. 그러나 그의 얼굴 위에는, 밤이 드리울 수 있는 그 어떤 어둠보다도 짙은 어둠이 모여들어, 거기 머물렀다.\n\n플로렌스와 에디스는, 어린 폴이 숨을 거둔 그 외딴방에서 난롯불 앞에 앉아, 오래도록 함께 이야기를 나누었다. 그 자리에 함께한 디오게네스는 처음에는 에디스가 들어오는 것을 반대했고, 제 여주인의 바람에 마지못해 따르면서도 으르렁대는 항의 속에서야 그것을 허락했을 뿐이었다. 그러나 골이 나서 물러나 있던 대기실에서 조금씩 조금씩 다시 나오더니, 곧 깨달은 듯했다—더없이 좋은 의도로 한 일이긴 하나, 아무리 잘 길든 개의 마음에도 이따금 생기곤 하는 그런 실수를 하나 저질렀음을. 그 우호적인 사과의 표시로 디오게네스는 난롯불 바로 앞 몹시 뜨거운 자리에서 두 사람 사이에 꼿꼿이 버티고 앉아, 혀를 빼물고 더없이 멍청한 표정을 지은 채, 헐떡거리며 대화에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n이야기는 처음엔 플로렌스의 책과 그녀가 좋아하는 소일거리에 관한 것으로, 그리고 결혼식 이후의 시간을 그녀가 어떻게 보냈는지에 관한 것으로 흘렀다. 마지막 화제는 그녀의 가슴 가장 깊은 곳에 자리한 어떤 일을 열어젖혔고, 그녀는 두 눈에 눈물이 솟구치는 채로 말했다.\n\n\"오, 엄마! 그날 이후로 저는 커다란 슬픔을 겪었어요.\"\n\n\"네가 커다란 슬픔을, 플로렌스!\"\n\n\"네. 가엾은 월터가 물에 빠져 죽었어요.\"\n\n플로렌스는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고, 마음을 다해 흐느껴 울었다. 월터의 운명이 그녀에게 치르게 한 남모를 눈물이 아무리 많았어도, 그를 생각하거나 그에 대해 이야기할 때면 그 눈물은 여전히 흘렀다.\n\n\"하지만 말해 주렴, 얘야,\" 에디스가 그녀를 달래며 말했다. \"월터가 누구였니? 너에게 어떤 사람이었지?\"\n\n\"제 오라버니였어요, 엄마. 사랑하는 폴이 죽은 뒤에, 우리는 오누이가 되자고 했어요. 저는 그를 오래—아주 어릴 적부터 알고 지냈어요. 그는 폴을 알았고, 폴도 그를 무척 좋아했어요. 폴은 거의 마지막에 이렇게 말했어요. '월터를 잘 보살펴 주세요, 사랑하는 아빠! 저는 그를 좋아했어요!' 월터는 폴을 보러 들여보내져서, 그때 거기에—이 방에 있었어요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그분이 월터를 보살폈니?\" 에디스가 매섭게 물었다.\n\n\"아빠가요? 아빠는 그를 외국으로 보내도록 지명하셨어요. 그는 항해 중에 난파를 당해 물에 빠져 죽었어요,\" 플로렌스가 흐느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"그분은 그가 죽은 걸 아시니?\" 에디스가 물었다.\n\n\"모르겠어요, 엄마. 알 길이 없어요. 사랑하는 엄마!\" 플로렌스가 도움을 청하듯 그녀에게 매달리며, 그 가슴에 얼굴을 묻고 외쳤다, \"저는 엄마가 보셨다는 걸 알아요—\"\n\n\"잠깐! 멈추렴, 플로렌스.\" 에디스가 어찌나 창백해지며, 또 어찌나 간곡하게 말했던지, 플로렌스는 자기 입술을 막는 그녀의 손이 필요치 않았다. \"먼저 월터에 대해 다 말해 주렴. 이 이야기를 처음부터 끝까지 다 이해하게 해 주렴.\"\n\n플로렌스는 그 이야기를, 그리고 거기 얽힌 모든 것을 다 들려주었으니, 심지어 투츠 씨의 우정에 이르기까지—비록 그에게 깊이 감사하면서도, 그녀는 슬픔에 잠긴 와중에 눈물 어린 미소 없이는 좀처럼 그에 대해 말할 수가 없었다. 그녀의 손을 잡은 채 그 이야기 전부를 골똘히 귀 기울여 들은 에디스가, 그녀가 이야기를 마치고 나서 침묵이 뒤따랐을 때, 말했다.\n\n\"내가 무엇을 보았다는 거니, 플로렌스?\"\n\n\"제가,\" 플로렌스가 아까와 똑같이 말없이 호소하며, 아까와 똑같이 얼른 얼굴을 가리고 말했다, \"제가 사랑받는 자식이 아니라는 거요, 엄마. 저는 한 번도 그래 본 적이 없어요. 어떻게 하면 되는지도 도무지 알지 못했어요. 저는 그 길을 놓쳤고, 제게 그 길을 일러 줄 사람도 없었어요. 오, 어떻게 하면 아빠에게 더 사랑받을 수 있는지 엄마에게서 배우게 해 주세요. 가르쳐 주세요! 엄마야말로 그토록 잘하실 수 있잖아요!\" 그러고는 더 바싹 그녀에게 매달리며, 감사와 애정의 끊어질 듯한 뜨거운 말 몇 마디와 함께, 그 슬픈 비밀을 덜어 낸 플로렌스는, 자기 새어머니의 감싸 안은 두 팔 안에서, 오래도록, 그러나 예전만큼 괴롭지는 않게 울었다.\n\n입술까지 창백해진 채, 그리고 그 도도한 아름다움이 죽음처럼 굳어질 때까지 평정을 지키려 애쓰는 얼굴로, 에디스는 우는 소녀를 내려다보며, 한 번 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 차츰 몸을 빼내, 플로렌스를 떼어 놓으며, 위엄 있게, 그리고 대리석상처럼 고요하게, 말하는 동안 점점 깊어지되 그 밖에 감정의 기색이라곤 없는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"플로렌스, 너는 나를 모른단다! 네가 나에게서 배우다니, 하늘이 막아 주시기를!\"\n\n\"엄마에게서 배우면 안 된다고요?\" 플로렌스가 놀라서 되물었다.\n\n\"사랑하는 법이나, 사랑받는 법을 내가 네게 가르치다니, 하늘이 막아 주시기를!\" 에디스가 말했다. \"차라리 네가 나를 가르칠 수 있다면, 그게 나으련만. 하지만 이젠 너무 늦었구나. 너는 내게 소중하단다, 플로렌스. 이 짧은 동안에 네가 내게 소중해진 만큼 그 무엇이 내게 그토록 소중해질 수 있으리라고는 생각지 못했어.\"\n\n그녀는 여기서 플로렌스가 무슨 말을 하려는 것을 보고는, 손으로 그것을 막고서, 말을 이었다.\n\n\"나는 언제까지나 너의 참된 벗이 되어 주마. 이 세상 누구 못지않게 너를, 아무리 잘은 못 한다 해도, 그만큼 아끼마. 너는 나를 믿어도 된단다—나는 그것을 알기에 말하는 거야, 얘야—네 순결한 마음의 온전한 믿음으로 그래도 된단다. 다른 모든 점에서 나보다 더 낫고 더 진실한, 그가 결혼할 수도 있었던 여인들이야 수두룩하지, 플로렌스. 하지만 그의 아내로 이곳에 와서, 나만큼 진실하게 너를 향해 두근거리는 마음을 지닐 수 있는 여인은 단 한 사람도 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"저도 알아요, 사랑하는 엄마!\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다. \"그 첫날, 그 더없이 행복했던 날부터 저는 알고 있었어요.\"\n\n\"더없이 행복했던 날이라니!\" 에디스는 그 말을 저도 모르게 되뇌는 듯하다가, 말을 이었다. \"비록 그 공이 내 것은 아니지만—너를 보기 전까지는 너를 거의 마음에 두지 않았으니까—그 받을 자격 없는 보답일랑 너의 믿음과 사랑 속에서 내 것이 되게 해 다오. 그리고 이 일에서는—이 일에서는, 플로렌스, 내가 이곳에 거처를 잡은 첫날 밤이니, 그러는 게 가장 좋을 터이기에, 처음이자 마지막으로 이 말을 하도록 이끌리는구나.\"\n\n플로렌스는 영문도 모른 채, 그녀가 말을 이어 가는 것을 듣기가 거의 두려웠지만, 자기에게 그토록 골똘히 향해 있는 그 아름다운 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 못했다.\n\n\"결코 내게서 찾으려 들지 마라,\" 에디스가 자기 가슴에 손을 얹으며 말했다, \"여기 없는 것을. 결코, 네가 어쩔 수 있는 한, 그것이 여기 없다고 해서 나에게서 멀어지지 마라, 플로렌스. 조금씩 조금씩 너는 나를 더 잘 알게 될 것이고, 내가 나 자신을 아는 것처럼 너도 나를 알게 될 날이 올 거야. 그때, 할 수 있는 한 내게 너그러워 다오. 그리고 내가 갖게 될 단 하나의 달콤한 추억을 쓰디쓴 것으로 만들지 말아 다오.\"\n\n플로렌스에게 눈길을 고정한 채 그 눈에 어린 눈물은, 그 차분한 얼굴이 한낱 아름다운 가면에 지나지 않음을 드러냈다. 그러나 그녀는 그 얼굴을 그대로 지키며, 말을 이었다.\n\n\"나는 네가 말한 것을 보아 왔고, 그것이 얼마나 사실인지도 안단다. 하지만 나를 믿으렴—지금은 못 한다 해도, 너는 곧 믿게 될 거야—이 세상에서 그것을 바로잡거나 너를 도울 자격이 나보다 없는 사람은 없단다, 플로렌스. 결코 그 이유를 내게 묻지 말고, 그것에 대해서도, 내 남편에 대해서도, 더는 내게 말하지 마라. 우리 둘 사이에는, 그만큼은, 무덤 그 자체와도 같은 단절과 침묵이 있어야 한단다.\"\n\n그녀는 한동안 말없이 앉아 있었다. 그동안 플로렌스는 감히 숨도 거의 쉬지 못했으니, 진실의, 그리고 그것이 날마다 가져올 온갖 결과의 어렴풋하고 불완전한 그림자들이, 두려움에 떨면서도 좀처럼 믿기지 않아 하는 그녀의 상상 속에서 서로 꼬리를 물고 뒤쫓았던 것이다. 에디스가 말을 멈추자마자, 그녀의 얼굴은 그 굳은 평정에서, 그녀와 플로렌스가 단둘이 있을 때면 으레 띠곤 하는 그 한결 잔잔하고 한결 누그러진 표정으로 가라앉기 시작했다. 이렇게 표정이 바뀐 뒤, 그녀는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 그러고는 일어서서, 다정한 포옹으로 플로렌스에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건네고는, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 빠르게 나갔다.\n\n그러나 플로렌스가 잠자리에 들고, 난롯불의 잔광 말고는 방이 어두워졌을 때, 에디스가 돌아와, 잠을 이룰 수 없다고, 자기 의상실이 적적하다고 말하며, 난롯가로 의자 하나를 끌어다 놓고, 잦아드는 잉걸불을 지켜보았다. 플로렌스도 자기 침대에서 그 불을 지켜보았으니, 마침내 그 불이며, 그 앞에 앉은 그 고결한 자태가—흘러내린 머리카락을 왕관처럼 두르고, 사색에 잠긴 두 눈으로 그 불빛을 되비추는—흐릿하고 어렴풋해지더니, 끝내 잠 속으로 스러져 갔다.\n\n그러나 잠 속에서도 플로렌스는 그토록 방금 일어난 일의 어떤 막연한 인상을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 그것은 그녀의 꿈의 소재가 되어, 그녀를 따라다녔으니, 어떤 때는 이런 모습으로, 어떤 때는 저런 모습으로, 그러나 언제나 짓누르듯이, 그리고 두려움의 감각과 더불어 그러했다. 그녀는 황야에서 아버지를 찾아 헤매는 꿈을, 무시무시한 높은 곳까지 그의 자취를 좇아 올라가고 또 깊은 광산과 동굴 속으로 내려가는 꿈을, 무언가 그를 비상한 고통에서 풀어 줄 무엇을—그것이 무엇인지도, 왜인지도 모른 채—맡았으면서도, 끝내 그 목표에 다다라 그를 풀어 주지는 못하는 꿈을 꾸었다. 그러다가 그녀는 바로 그 침대 위에서, 바로 그 방에서 그가 죽어 있는 것을 보았고, 그가 끝끝내 자기를 사랑하지 않았음을 알고는, 그 차가운 가슴 위에 엎드려 미친 듯이 울었다. 그러다가 어떤 풍경이 펼쳐지고, 강물이 흘렀으며, 그녀가 아는 어떤 구슬픈 목소리가 외쳤다. \"흘러가고 있어, 플로이! 한 번도 멈춘 적이 없어! 너도 그것과 함께 움직이고 있어!\" 그리고 그녀는 멀리서 그가 자기를 향해 두 팔을 뻗고 있는 것을 보았는데, 그동안 예전의 월터를 닮은 어떤 모습이 그의 곁에, 섬뜩하리만치 고요하고 잔잔하게 서 있었다. 모든 환영 속에서 에디스가 오갔으니, 어떤 때는 그녀를 기쁘게, 어떤 때는 그녀를 슬프게 했고, 마침내 둘은 어두운 무덤가에 단둘이 서게 되었으며, 에디스가 아래를 가리키기에, 그녀가 들여다보니 보였다—무엇이었던가!—또 다른 에디스가 그 바닥에 누워 있는 것이.\n\n이 꿈의 공포 속에서, 그녀는 비명을 지르며 깨어났다고, 그녀는 생각했다. 어떤 부드러운 목소리가 그녀의 귓가에 속삭이는 듯했다. \"플로렌스, 사랑하는 플로렌스, 그건 한낱 꿈일 뿐이란다!\" 그리고 두 팔을 뻗으며, 그녀는 새어머니의 어루만짐에 화답했고, 새어머니는 그러고 나서 잿빛 아침의 빛 속에서 문밖으로 나갔다. 잠시 후, 플로렌스는 일어나 앉아 이 일이 정말로 일어난 것인지 아닌지 의아해했다. 그러나 그녀가 확실히 아는 것이라곤, 정말로 잿빛 아침이라는 것, 그리고 난로 위에 거뭇거뭇 타 버린 잿더미가 놓여 있다는 것, 그리고 자기가 혼자라는 것뿐이었다.\n\n그 행복한 부부가 집으로 돌아온 그날 밤은 이렇게 지나갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVI. Housewarming\n\n여러 날이 그렇게 비슷하게 흘러갔다. 다만 받고 또 갚는 방문이 무수히 많았다는 점, 스큐턴 부인이 자기 거처에서 작은 접견 모임을 열었고 거기에 백스톡 소령이 자주 참석했다는 점, 그리고 플로렌스가 날마다 아버지를 보면서도 두 번 다시 그의 눈길을 받지 못했다는 점은 달랐다. 새어머니와도 말을 많이 주고받지는 못했는데, 그녀는 플로렌스를 제외한 온 집안 사람에게는 거만하고 도도했으나—플로렌스로서는 그 점을 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다—방문에서 돌아오면 늘 플로렌스를 부르러 보내거나 직접 찾아왔고, 밤이면 아무리 늦은 시각이라도 쉬러 들기 전에 언제나 플로렌스의 방에 들렀으며, 함께 있을 기회를 결코 놓치는 법이 없었으나, 종종 오랫동안 말없이 생각에 잠긴 동무가 되곤 했다.\n\n이 결혼에서 그토록 많은 것을 바랐던 플로렌스는, 이따금 이 화려한 집을, 그것이 생겨난 바탕인 그 빛바래고 음울한 곳과 견주어 보지 않을 수 없었고, 도대체 언제쯤이면 이곳이 어떤 모습으로든 가정이 되기 시작할까 궁금해하지 않을 수 없었다. 모든 것이 호사스럽고 규칙적으로 돌아가고 있었음에도, 지금은 그 누구에게도 이곳이 가정이 아니라는 은밀한 불안을 늘 품고 있었기 때문이다. 낮으로 밤으로 슬픔에 잠겨 곱씹는 수많은 시간을, 그리고 시들어 버린 희망의 수많은 눈물을, 플로렌스는 새어머니가 그토록 단호하게 자기에게 못 박아 준 그 말 위에 쏟았다. 곧 아버지의 마음을 얻는 법을 가르쳐 줄 사람으로 치자면 이 세상에서 자기 자신만큼 무력한 이가 없다는 그 말이었다. 그리고 머지않아 플로렌스는 이렇게 생각하기 시작했다—생각하기로 마음먹었다고 하는 편이 더 옳은 표현이리라—자기에 대한 아버지의 냉정함이 누그러지거나 변하리라는 가망이 얼마나 없는지를 그녀만큼 잘 아는 이가 없기에, 바로 그 연민에서 자기에게 그렇게 경고하고 그 화제를 금했던 것이라고. 자기의 모든 행동과 공상에서 그러하듯 여기서도 사심이 없었던 플로렌스는, 아버지에 관한 진실의 어렴풋한 전조를 조금이라도 키우느니, 차라리 이 새로운 상처의 아픔을 견디는 편을 택했다. 떠도는 생각 속에서조차 아버지를 다정히 감쌌던 것이다. 아버지의 집으로 말하자면, 새것이라는 상태와 과도기가 끝나고 나면 더 나은 집이 되리라고 그녀는 바랐다. 그리고 자기 자신에 대해서는 거의 생각하지 않았고, 한탄은 더더욱 하지 않았다.\n\n새 가족 가운데 사적으로 특별히 집에서 편안한 사람이 아무도 없다 하더라도, 적어도 돔비 부인만큼은 지체 없이 공적으로 손님을 맞이하기로 결정되었다. 지난 혼례를 축하하고 사교계를 다지기 위한 일련의 연회가, 주로 돔비 씨와 스큐턴 부인에 의해 마련되었다. 그리하여 축제의 절차는 어느 저녁 돔비 부인이 손님을 맞이하는 것으로 시작하고, 같은 날 돔비 씨 부부가 서로 어울리지 않는 수많은 사람을 만찬에 초대하는 영광을 청하는 것으로 시작하기로 정해졌다.\n\n그리하여 돔비 씨는 자기 몫으로 이 연회에 초청할 동방의 여러 거물들의 명단을 내놓았다. 거기에 스큐턴 부인은, 이 일에 도도하게 무관심한 자기의 더없이 사랑하는 자식을 대신하여, 서쪽 명단을 덧붙였다. 거기에는 아직 바덴바덴으로 돌아가지 않아 자기 재산에 큰 손해를 끼치고 있는 피닉스 사촌과, 여러 시절에 그녀의 아리따운 딸이나 그녀 자신의 불빛 둘레를 날개에 영구적인 손상은 입지 않은 채 펄럭이며 맴돌았던, 갖가지 등급과 나이의 나방 같은 무리가 포함되었다. 플로렌스는 에디스의 명령으로 만찬 손님의 일원으로 이름이 올랐는데—이는 스큐턴 부인 쪽의 한순간 의심과 망설임에서 비롯된 명령이었다—플로렌스는 의아한 마음으로, 그리고 아버지의 신경을 조금이라도 거스르는 모든 것에 대한 빠르고 본능적인 감각으로, 그날의 절차에서 말없이 제 몫을 했다.\n\n절차는 돔비 씨가 유난히 높고 빳빳한 크라바트를 두른 채 만찬 시간으로 정해진 시각까지 응접실을 안절부절못하고 거니는 것으로 시작되었다. 그 시각에 정확히 맞춰, 막대한 재력을 지닌 동인도회사 이사 한 사람이 도착했다. 그는 보아하니 어느 평범한 목수가 쓸 만한 전나무 판자로 짜 맞춘 듯한, 그러나 실은 재단사의 솜씨로 빚어낸, 난킨이라 불리는 천으로 지은 조끼를 입고 있었고, 돔비 씨 혼자서 그를 맞이했다. 다음 단계의 절차는 돔비 씨가 돔비 부인에게 정확한 시각을 알리는 인사말을 보내는 것이었고, 그다음은 동인도회사 이사가 대화라는 관점에서 보면 그만 엎어져 버린 것이었다. 돔비 씨는 그를 일으켜 세워 줄 사람이 아니었으므로, 그는 스큐턴 부인의 모습으로 구원이 나타날 때까지 난롯불만 바라보고 있었다. 그 이사는 저녁을 시작하는 즐거운 출발로서 그녀를 돔비 부인으로 착각하고는 열렬히 인사했다.\n\n다음에 도착한 이는 은행 이사였는데, 그는 무엇이든 사들일 수 있다는—만일 그 방향으로 금융 시장을 움직일 마음만 먹는다면 인간 본성까지도 통째로 사들일 수 있다는—평판을 지닌 사람이었으나, 놀라우리만치 겸손하게 말하는, 거의 자랑하듯 겸손한 사람이었다. 그는 킹스턴어폰템스에 있는 자기의 \"조그만 거처\"를 입에 올리며, 돔비가 와서 방문해 준다면 잠자리 하나와 고기 한 점을 겨우 내줄 수 있을 정도밖에 안 된다고 했다. 부인들로 말하자면, 자기처럼 조용히 사는 사람이 감히 초대를 한다는 것은 가당치 않으나, 만일 스큐턴 부인과 그 따님 돔비 부인이 언젠가 그쪽으로 오게 되어, 거기서 보게 될 자그마한 관목 숲과, 가련하게 작은 화단 몇 개와, 온실이랍시고 내세우기도 민망한 보잘것없는 것과, 별 자랑할 것 없는 그런 자잘한 시도 두세 가지를 보아 주는 영광을 베풀어 준다면, 자기로서는 더없는 영예가 되겠다고 했다. 자기 성격에 걸맞게, 이 신사는 매우 수수하게 차려입었으니, 목수건이랍시고 한 줌의 케임브릭 천을 두르고, 큼직한 신발에, 헐렁한 외투에, 너무 빈약한 바지를 입고 있었다. 스큐턴 부인이 오페라 이야기를 꺼내자, 그는 그곳에 거의 가지 못한다고, 그럴 형편이 못 된다고 말했다. 그렇게 말하는 것이 그를 무척 기쁘게 하고 들뜨게 하는 듯했다. 그러고는 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채, 눈에 지나친 만족이 반짝이는 가운데, 청중을 향해 환하게 웃었다.\n\n이제 돔비 부인이 나타났다. 아름답고 도도하게, 그리고 머리에 쓴 신부의 화관이 마치 죽으면 죽었지 절대 양보하지 않을 굴복을 강요하려고 씌워 놓은 강철 가시 화환이라도 되는 양, 그들 모두를 멸시하고 거역하는 모습이었다. 그녀와 함께 플로렌스가 있었다. 둘이 함께 들어서자, 돌아오던 그 밤의 그림자가 다시 돔비 씨의 얼굴을 어둡게 했다. 그러나 아무도 알아차리지 못했다. 플로렌스는 감히 아버지를 향해 눈을 들지 못했고, 에디스의 무관심은 너무도 지극하여 그를 조금도 안중에 두지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n도착하는 손님들이 곧 많아졌다. 더 많은 이사들, 공공 회사의 회장들, 정장이라며 머리에 짐을 인 노부인들, 피닉스 사촌, 백스톡 소령, 그리고 똑같이 환한 화색을 얼굴에 띠고 몹시 시든 목에 몹시 값진 목걸이를 두른 스큐턴 부인의 친구들. 그 가운데 예순다섯 살의 한 젊은 아가씨가 있었는데, 등과 어깨를 유난히 시원하게 드러낸 차림에, 사람의 마음을 끄는 혀짧은 소리로 말했고, 본인이 어지간히 애쓰지 않으면 눈꺼풀이 도무지 제대로 올라가 있지 않았으며, 젊음의 들뜸에 그토록 흔히 따라붙는 그 형언할 수 없는 매력을 거동에 지니고 있었다. 돔비 씨의 명단에 든 사람들은 대개 과묵한 편이었고, 돔비 부인의 명단에 든 사람들은 대개 말이 많은 편이었으며, 둘 사이에는 아무런 공감이 없었으므로, 돔비 부인의 명단은 자석처럼 끌리는 합의에 따라 돔비 씨의 명단에 맞서는 동맹을 맺었다. 돔비 씨의 명단은 황량하게 방들을 떠돌거나 구석에서 피난처를 찾다가, 들어오는 손님들과 뒤엉키고, 소파 뒤에 갇혀 버리고, 바깥에서 문이 홱 열리며 머리를 얻어맞고, 온갖 낭패를 다 겪었다.\n\n만찬이 알려지자, 돔비 씨는 지폐로 속을 채운 진홍색 벨벳 바늘꽂이 같은 노부인을 이끌고 내려갔다. 그녀는 어찌나 부유하고 또 어찌나 다루기 까다로워 보였던지, 스레드니들 거리의 바로 그 노부인이라 해도 좋을 정도였다. 피닉스 사촌은 돔비 부인을 이끌고 내려갔고, 백스톡 소령은 스큐턴 부인을 이끌고 내려갔으며, 어깨를 드러낸 그 젊은 것은 동인도회사 이사에게 입막음용으로 배정되었다. 그리고 남은 부인들은, 남은 신사들에 의해 응접실에 진열된 채 남겨졌다가, 한 결사대가 자원하여 그들을 아래층으로 안내해 주었다. 그 용맹한 정신의 소유자들은 포로들을 거느린 채 식당 문을 가로막아, 무정한 마음의 현관에 일곱 명의 온순한 사내들을 가두어 버렸다. 나머지 사람들이 다 들어가 자리에 앉았는데도, 이 온순한 사내들 가운데 하나가 여전히 웃음 띤 혼란 속에, 완전히 의지가지없고 자리도 마련되지 않은 채로 나타나서는, 집사의 호위를 받으며 자기 의자를 찾을 때까지 식탁을 두 바퀴나 온전히 돌았다. 마침내 의자는 돔비 부인의 왼편에서 찾아졌고, 그 뒤로 그 온순한 사내는 두 번 다시 고개를 들지 못했다.\n\n이제, 그 널찍한 식당은, 반짝이는 식탁 둘레에 앉아 반짝이는 숟가락과 나이프와 포크와 접시로 분주한 손님들과 더불어, 아이들이 금과 은을 주워 모으는 톰 티들러의 땅을 어른용으로 펼쳐 놓은 것이라 해도 좋을 광경이었다. 티들러 역을 맡은 돔비 씨는 자기 배역에 감탄스러우리만치 잘 어울렸다. 그리고 그와 돔비 부인을 갈라놓은, 서리처럼 뿌옇게 세공한 귀금속의 기다란 장식 받침대—그 위에서 서리처럼 뿌연 큐피드들이 둘에게 각기 향기 없는 꽃을 바치고 있던—는 보기에 우의적이었다.\n\n피닉스 사촌은 기운이 넘쳤고, 놀랍도록 젊어 보였다. 그러나 그는 이따금 그 좋은 기분 속에서 무심했으니—그의 기억이 그의 다리처럼 가끔 갈피를 못 잡고 헤맸으므로—이번에도 좌중을 오싹하게 만들었다. 사정은 이러했다. 등을 드러낸 그 젊은 아가씨는 피닉스 사촌에 대해 애틋한 정을 품고 있어서, 동인도회사 이사를 꾀어 자기를 그의 옆자리로 이끌게 했다. 그 친절에 대한 보답으로, 그녀는 곧장 그 이사를 버려두었고, 다른 한쪽으로는 부채를 든 뼈만 앙상하고 말 없는 여인 위에 음울한 검은 벨벳 모자가 얹혀 그를 가리고 있던 터라, 그 이사는 기운이 가라앉아 제 안으로 움츠러들고 말았다. 피닉스 사촌과 그 젊은 아가씨는 무척 활기차고 익살스러웠는데, 피닉스 사촌이 들려준 어떤 이야기에 젊은 아가씨가 어찌나 크게 웃던지, 백스톡 소령이 스큐턴 부인을 대신하여(둘은 맞은편에, 조금 아래쪽에 앉아 있었다) 그것이 공공의 재산으로 여겨질 수는 없는지 여쭙는 것을 허락해 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"아, 이런, 정말이지,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"별것 아닙니다. 정말 되풀이할 만한 가치가 없어요. 사실 그저 잭 애덤스에 관한 일화일 뿐입니다. 아마 내 친구 돔비는,\" 좌중의 관심이 피닉스 사촌에게 집중되어 있었으므로, \"잭 애덤스를 기억할지도 모르겠소. 조가 아니라 잭 애덤스 말이오. 조는 그 형이었지. 잭—꼬마 잭—한쪽 눈이 사시이고, 말투에 약간 더듬는 데가 있던 사람, 어느 선거구를 대표해 의석에 앉았던 사람 말이오. 내가 의원이던 시절에 우리는 그를 W. P. 애덤스라고 부르곤 했는데, 그가 아직 미성년인 어느 젊은이를 위한 보온 그릇* 노릇을 하고 있었기 때문이오. 어쩌면 내 친구 돔비가 그 사람을 알고 있었을지도 모르겠소.\"\n* 보온 그릇(Warming Pan): 미성년자가 자격을 갖출 때까지 자리를 대신 채워 두는 임시 대리인을 뜻하는 당대의 비유적 표현.\n\n기 포크스를 알고 있었을 가능성이나 매한가지였던 돔비 씨는 모른다고 대답했다. 그러나 일곱 명의 온순한 사내들 가운데 하나가 뜻밖에도 두각을 드러내며, 자기는 그를 알고 있었다고 말하고는 이렇게 덧붙였다—\"늘 헤시안 부츠를 신고 다녔지요!\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소,\" 피닉스 사촌이 그 온순한 사내를 보려고 몸을 앞으로 굽히며, 식탁 너머로 그에게 격려의 미소를 지어 보이며 말했다. \"그게 바로 잭이었소. 조는—\"\n\n\"톱부츠를 신었지요!\" 그 온순한 사내가 매 순간 세간의 평가를 높여 가며 외쳤다.\n\n\"물론이오,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"두 사람과 친하셨던 모양이지요?\"\n\n\"둘 다 알고 있었습니다,\" 그 온순한 사내가 말했다. 돔비 씨는 즉시 그와 함께 술잔을 들었다.\n\n\"기막히게 좋은 친구였지, 잭은!\" 피닉스 사촌이 다시 몸을 앞으로 굽히며 미소 지었다.\n\n\"훌륭한 사람이었지요,\" 그 온순한 사내가 성공에 힘입어 대담해지며 응수했다. \"제가 알던 사람 중에 가장 좋은 친구 가운데 하나였습니다.\"\n\n\"그 이야기는 분명 들으셨겠지요?\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다.\n\n\"각하께서 들려주시는 걸 듣고 나면,\" 대담해진 온순한 사내가 대답했다, \"알게 되겠지요.\" 그 말과 함께 그는 의자에 등을 기대고는, 이미 다 외고 있어 벌써부터 즐거워하는 사람처럼 천장을 향해 미소 지었다.\n\n\"사실 그 자체로는 이렇다 할 이야기도 아닙니다,\" 피닉스 사촌이 미소를 지으며, 머리를 유쾌하게 흔들며 좌중을 향해 말했다, \"서두를 한마디 붙일 가치도 없지요. 다만 잭의 재치가 얼마나 깔끔한지를 잘 보여 주는 이야기랍니다. 사정인즉, 잭이 어느 결혼식에 초대를 받았는데—내 생각엔 버크셔에서 열렸던 것 같소만?\"\n\n\"슈롭셔입니다,\" 대담해진 온순한 사내가 자기에게 묻는 것임을 알아채고 말했다.\n\n\"그랬소? 뭐! 사실 어느 셔에서 열렸든 마찬가지였겠지요,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다. \"그래, 내 친구가 어느셔에서 열리는 이 결혼식에 초대를 받아,\" 이 농담이 잘 떠오른 것에 기분 좋아하며, \"갑니다. 마치 우리 가운데 더러가, 나의 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 친척과 내 친구 돔비의 결혼식에 초대받는 영광을 누리고는, 두 번 청할 것도 없이, 그토록 흥미로운 자리에 참석하게 되어 기막히게 기뻐했던 것처럼 말이오.—갑니다—잭이 갑니다. 그런데 이 결혼이라는 게, 사실은, 보기 드물게 고운 아가씨가, 단추 한 알만큼도 마음에 두지 않는 사내와 하는 결혼이었소. 다만 그 막대한 재산 때문에 그를 받아들인 거였지요. 혼례를 마치고 잭이 런던으로 돌아왔을 때, 그가 아는 사내 하나가 하원의 로비에서 그를 만나 말합니다, '이보게, 잭, 그 어울리지 않는 한 쌍은 어떻던가?' '어울리지 않는다고?' 잭이 말합니다. '천만에. 더없이 완벽하게 대등한 거래였네. 그 여자는 제대로 사들여졌고, 장담하건대 그 사내는 제대로 팔려 나간 게지!'\"\n\n이 이야기의 절정을 한껏 즐기던 차에, 전기 불꽃처럼 식탁을 한 바퀴 돌았던 그 오싹함이 피닉스 사촌에게 닿았고, 그는 말을 멈추었다. 그날 입에 올린 유일한 일반적 화제가 자아낸 미소는 그 어느 얼굴에도 떠오르지 않았다. 깊은 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 그리고 그 이야기를 미리 알았던 것이 갓 태어나지 않은 아기만큼이나 결백했던 그 가엾은 온순한 사내는, 모든 눈동자 속에서 자기가 이 사달의 주모자로 여겨지고 있음을 읽어 내는 더없는 고통을 맛보았다.\n\n돔비 씨의 얼굴은 잘 변하는 얼굴이 아니었고, 그날은 위엄이라는 거푸집에 부어 넣은 채였으므로, 그 이야기에 대해서는 설령 무언가를 느꼈다 한들, 침묵 가운데 엄숙하게 \"참 좋군요\"라고 말했을 때 드러낸 정도 외에는 거의 아무런 기색도 보이지 않았다. 에디스가 플로렌스 쪽으로 재빠른 눈길을 던졌으나, 그 밖에는 겉으로 무표정하고 무심한 채로 있었다.\n\n진귀한 고기와 포도주, 끊임없는 금과 은, 땅과 공기와 불과 물에서 나온 별미들, 수북이 쌓아 올린 과일들, 그리고 돔비 씨의 연회에서는 불필요한 그 물건—얼음—의 여러 단계를 거치며, 만찬은 더디게 나아갔다. 후반 단계는, 손님들의 도착을 알리는 끊임없는 두 번 두드리는 노크 소리의 우렁찬 음악에 맞추어 이루어졌는데, 그 손님들에게 이 연회에서 돌아갈 몫이라곤 그 냄새뿐이었다. 돔비 부인이 일어섰을 때, 그 부군이 빳빳한 목과 꼿꼿한 머리로 부인들이 물러나도록 문을 잡고 서 있던 모습은, 그리고 그녀가 자기 딸을 팔에 끼고 그의 곁을 휙 스쳐 지나가던 모습은, 가히 볼만한 광경이었다.\n\n돔비 씨는 술병들 뒤에서 위엄을 갖춘 채 엄숙한 광경이었고, 동인도회사 이사는 비어 있는 식탁 끄트머리 가까이에서 고독에 잠긴 처량한 광경이었으며, 소령은 일곱 온순한 사내들 가운데 여섯에게 요크 공작의 이야기를 들려주는 군인다운 광경이었고(야심을 품었던 한 명은 완전히 꺼져 버렸다), 은행 이사는 후식용 나이프로 자기의 보잘것없는 온실 시도의 도면을 그려 한 무리의 찬미자들에게 보여 주는 겸손한 광경이었으며, 피닉스 사촌은 긴 소맷부리를 매만지고 슬그머니 가발을 바로잡으며 생각에 잠긴 광경이었다. 그러나 이 모든 광경은 잠깐뿐이었으니, 커피가 나오고 사람들이 방을 비우면서 이내 흩어져 버렸다.\n\n위층의 응접실들에는 사람이 북적였고, 매 순간 그 수가 불어났다. 그러나 여전히 돔비 씨의 손님 명단은 돔비 부인의 명단과 어우러지는 것이 천성적으로 불가능한 무언가가 있는 듯하여, 어느 쪽이 어느 쪽인지를 누구도 의심할 수 없었다. 이 규칙의 유일한 예외라면 아마 카커 씨였을 것이다. 그는 이제 손님들 사이에서 미소 짓고 있었고, 돔비 부인을 둘러싸고 모여든 무리 안에 서서—그녀를, 그들을, 자기 우두머리를, 클레오파트라와 소령을, 플로렌스를, 그리고 주위의 모든 것을 살피며—두 패의 손님 양쪽과 다 편안해 보였고, 어느 한쪽에만 오로지 속한 것으로 두드러지지도 않았다.\n\n플로렌스는 그에 대한 두려움이 있어서, 그가 방에 있다는 것 자체가 그녀에게는 악몽이었다. 그녀는 그것을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었으니, 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 혐오와 불신의 끌림에 이끌려 그녀의 눈이 이따금 그쪽으로 향했기 때문이다. 그렇지만 그녀의 생각은 다른 일들로 분주했다. 한쪽에 떨어져 앉은 채—흠모받지 못한 것도, 청함을 받지 못한 것도 아니었으나, 그 고요한 영혼의 온화함 속에 앉아—그녀는 아버지가 지금 벌어지는 일에 얼마나 발붙일 데가 없는지를 느꼈고, 그가 얼마나 마음 불편해 보이는지를, 그리고 그가 특별한 관심으로 대접하고 싶어 하는 손님들을 맞으려고 문 가까이에서 서성이며 얼마나 무시당하고 있는지를 아프게 지켜보았다. 그는 그 손님들을 데려가 자기 아내에게 소개했는데, 아내는 그들을 도도한 냉랭함으로 맞이할 뿐, 흥미를 보이거나 기쁘게 해 주려는 기색은 없었고, 형식적인 맞이 인사를 마친 다음에는, 남편의 뜻을 헤아리거나 그의 친구들을 환영하느라 입술을 여는 일이 결코 없었다. 그렇게 처신하는 그녀가 정작 플로렌스 자신에게는 그토록 다정하게, 그토록 사랑 어린 배려로 대해 주었기에, 자기 눈앞에서 벌어지는 일을 알고 있는 것조차 거의 배은망덕한 보답처럼 여겨질 지경이었으니, 플로렌스로서는 그 점이 더욱 당혹스럽고 가슴 아팠다.\n\n눈길 하나만큼이라도 감히 아버지의 동무가 되어 줄 수 있었더라면 플로렌스는 행복했으리라. 그리고 그의 불안의 주된 원인을 거의 의심하지 못하고 있다는 점에서 플로렌스는 행복했다. 그러나 그가 어떤 불리한 처지에 놓여 있음을 자기가 안다는 것을 그가 분하게 여길까 두려워, 그것을 아는 듯이 보이는 것이 겁났고, 그를 향한 충동과 에디스를 향한 고마운 애정 사이에서 갈라진 채, 그녀는 어느 쪽으로도 감히 눈을 들지 못했다. 두 사람 다를 위해 마음 졸이고 가슴 아파하던 중에, 사람들의 무리를 뚫고 이런 생각이 그녀에게 슬며시 스며들었다. 어쩌면 이 혀들의 소란과 발들의 디딤 소리가 애초에 이곳에 찾아들지 않았더라면, 옛 침울함과 쇠락함이 새로움과 화려함으로 결코 바뀌지 않았더라면, 그 버림받은 아이가 에디스에게서 아무런 벗도 찾지 못한 채, 가엾이 여겨지지도 기억되지도 못하며 외로운 삶을 살았더라면, 두 사람에게는 차라리 더 나았을지도 모른다는 생각이었다.\n\n칙 부인에게도 그와 비슷한 생각이 들었으나, 그녀의 마음속에서는 그렇게 조용히 펼쳐지지 않았다. 이 선량한 부인은 우선 만찬 초대를 받지 못한 것에 모욕을 느꼈다. 그 타격에서 어느 정도 회복하고 나자, 그녀는 손님을 맞이하는 돔비 부인 앞에서 그 부인의 감각을 어지럽힐 만큼, 그리고 스큐턴 부인의 머리 위에 산더미 같은 굴욕을 쌓아 올릴 만큼의 위용을 갖추려고 막대한 비용을 들였다.\n\n\"하지만 나는,\" 칙 부인이 칙 씨에게 말했다, \"플로렌스만큼도 대접을 못 받는 신세라니까! 누가 나를 조금이라도 거들떠보기나 해? 아무도 안 봐!\"\n\n\"아무도 안 보지, 여보,\" 벽에 기대 칙 부인 곁에 앉아 있던 칙 씨가 맞장구를 쳤다. 그는 거기서나마 가만히 휘파람을 불며 스스로를 위로할 수 있었다.\n\n\"내가 여기 있어 줬으면 하는 기색이 조금이라도 보여?\" 칙 부인이 눈을 번뜩이며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 여보, 그런 것 같지는 않구려,\" 칙 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"폴이 미쳤어!\" 칙 부인이 말했다.\n\n칙 씨가 휘파람을 불었다.\n\n\"당신이 괴물이 아니라면—가끔은 그렇지 싶다만—\" 칙 부인이 솔직하게 말했다, \"거기 앉아서 콧노래나 흥얼대고 있지 좀 마요. 사내로서의 감정이 손톱만큼이라도 있는 사람이라면, 어떻게 폴의 저 장모가, 저런 차림으로, 백스톡 소령이랑 저 모양으로 놀아나는 꼴을 보고 있을 수가 있어? 그 소령이라는 작자도, 다른 귀한 것들과 더불어, 당신의 그 루크리셔 톡스 덕에 우리가 떠안은 거잖아.\"\n\n\"내 루크리셔 톡스라니, 여보!\" 칙 씨가 어안이 벙벙해서 말했다.\n\n\"그래요,\" 칙 부인이 매우 매섭게 받아쳤다, \"당신의 루크리셔 톡스 말이야—그러니까 내 말은, 어떻게 누가 폴의 저 장모를, 그리고 폴의 저 오만한 아내를, 그리고 등이며 어깨며 드러낸 저 꼴사나운 늙은 허깨비들을, 한마디로 대체로 이 집들이 전부를 보고 있으면서 콧노래나 흥—\" 이 말에 칙 부인이 어찌나 경멸 어린 힘을 주었던지 칙 씨가 흠칫 놀랐다, \"흥얼댈 수 있는지, 정말 하느님께 감사하게도, 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없는 노릇이라고!\"\n\n칙 씨는 콧노래나 휘파람과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 모양으로 입을 비틀어 오므리고는, 무척 골똘한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 내가 마땅히 받아야 할 게 무엇인지는 알고 있다고 봐요,\" 칙 부인이 분노로 부풀어 오르며 말했다, \"폴은 나에게 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 잊어버렸지만 말이야. 나는 이 집안의 일원으로서, 여기 앉아 거들떠봐지지도 않는 신세로 있지는 않겠어. 나는 아직 돔비 부인의 발밑에 깔린 흙은 아니라고—아직은 아니지,\" 칙 부인이, 마치 모레쯤이면 그렇게 되리라 예상하는 듯이 말했다. \"그러니 나는 가겠어. 이 자리가 오로지 나를 깎아내리고 모욕하려고 마련된 거라고는 (속으로야 어떻게 생각하든) 말하지 않겠어. 그냥 가겠다고. 아무도 내가 없어진 걸 아쉬워하지도 않을 테니!\"\n\n칙 부인은 이 말과 함께 꼿꼿이 일어나 칙 씨의 팔을 잡았고, 칙 씨는 거기서 반 시간 남짓 음울하게 머문 끝에 그녀를 데리고 방을 나섰다. 그리고 그녀의 통찰력에 마땅히 돌려야 할 말을 하자면, 정말로 그녀가 없어진 것을 아쉬워한 사람은 전혀 없었다는 사실을 적어 두어야겠다.\n\n그러나 분개한 손님이 그녀 하나만은 아니었다. 돔비 씨의 명단은(여전히 끊임없이 곤경에 처해 있던 채) 한 덩어리가 되어 돔비 부인의 명단에 분개하고 있었으니, 저쪽이 외알 안경 너머로 자기들을 쳐다보며 저 사람들이 다 누구냐고 들으라는 듯이 의아해했기 때문이다. 한편 돔비 부인의 명단은 지루하다고 불평했고, 어깨를 드러낸 그 젊은 것은 저 명랑한 젊은이 피닉스 사촌(만찬 식탁을 떠나 가 버렸다)의 관심을 빼앗긴 채, 서른 명인지 마흔 명인지 되는 친구들에게 자기는 지루해 죽을 지경이라고 은밀히 털어놓았다. 머리에 짐을 인 노부인들은 모두 크든 작든 돔비 씨에 대해 불평할 거리가 있었고, 이사들과 회장들은 돔비가 굳이 결혼을 해야 했다면 차라리 제 나이에 좀 더 가깝고, 그렇게까지 잘나지는 않았으며, 형편이 조금 더 나은 사람과 결혼하는 편이 나았으리라는 데에 의견이 일치했다. 이 부류의 신사들 사이에서 일반적인 견해는, 그것이 돔비로서는 우둔한 짓이었으며, 그가 두고두고 후회하며 살게 되리라는 것이었다. 그 자리에 있던 거의 모든 사람이, 온순한 사내들을 빼고는, 머무르든 떠나든 자기가 돔비 씨나 돔비 부인에게 무시당하고 푸대접받았다고 여기지 않은 이가 없었다. 검은 벨벳 모자를 쓴 그 말 없는 여인은, 진홍색 벨벳을 입은 부인이 자기보다 먼저 안내받아 내려갔다는 이유로 그만 벙어리가 되어 버린 것으로 밝혀졌다. 온순한 사내들마저도 그 천성이 변질되고 말았으니, 레모네이드를 너무 많이 마셔 천성이 응어리진 탓인지, 아니면 만연한 전반적인 감염 탓인지, 그들은 서로에게 빈정대는 농담을 던지고, 계단에서며 으슥한 구석에서며 험담을 속삭였다. 전반적인 불만과 불편함이 어찌나 널리 퍼졌던지, 현관에 모여 있던 하인들도 위층의 손님들 못지않게 그것을 환히 알고 있었다. 아니, 바깥에 있던 횃불잡이들까지 그것을 알아채고는, 이 파티를 상복도 입지 않은 장례식에, 그것도 유언장에 한 사람도 기억된 이가 없는 장례식에 빗대었다.\n\n마침내 손님들이 모두 떠났고, 횃불잡이들도 떠났다. 그토록 오래 마차로 붐비던 거리도 한산해졌다. 그리고 꺼져 가는 불빛은 방 안에 아무도 비추지 않았으니, 다만 따로 떨어져 이야기를 나누던 돔비 씨와 카커 씨, 그리고 돔비 부인과 그 어머니뿐이었다. 부인은 오토만에 앉아 있었고, 그 어머니는 시녀가 오기를 기다리며 클레오파트라의 자세로 비스듬히 기대어 있었다. 돔비 씨가 카커에게 할 말을 마치자, 카커는 굽실거리며 작별을 고하러 나아갔다.\n\n\"바라건대,\" 그가 말했다, \"이 즐거운 저녁의 노고가 내일 돔비 부인께 폐가 되지 않기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"돔비 부인은,\" 돔비 씨가 앞으로 나서며 말했다, \"그런 종류의 걱정에서 자네를 놓아 줄 만큼 충분히 노고를 아꼈네. 유감스럽게도, 돔비 부인, 나는 이번 자리에서 당신이 좀 더 수고를 해 주었더라면 좋았으리라 생각하오.\"\n\n그녀는 그를 향해 거만한 눈길을 던졌으나, 그것을 길게 끌 가치도 없다는 듯, 아무 말 없이 눈을 돌려 버렸다.\n\n\"유감입니다, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"당신이 그것을 당신의 도리라고 여기지 않았다는 것이—\"\n\n그녀는 다시 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신의 도리 말이오, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말을 이었다, \"내 친구들을 좀 더 정중하게 맞이하는 것이. 오늘 밤 당신이 아주 유난스럽게 홀대하기를 즐긴 그 사람들 가운데 더러는, 분명히 말해 두건대, 당신을 찾아오는 것만으로도 당신에게 영예를 베푸는 것이오.\"\n\n\"여기 누가 있다는 걸 아시나요?\" 그녀가 이제 그를 똑바로 바라보며 받아쳤다.\n\n\"안 되네! 카커! 부디 그러지 말게. 단단히 이르건대 그러지 말게,\" 돔비 씨가, 소리도 없이 물러나려던 그 신사를 멈춰 세우며 외쳤다. \"카커 씨는, 부인, 당신도 알다시피, 나의 신임을 받는 사람이오. 그는 내가 말하고 있는 이 문제를 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있소. 당신에게 일러두건대, 돔비 부인, 알아 두시오, 나는 이 부유하고 중요한 인사들이 나에게 영예를 베푸는 것이라 여기오.\" 그러고는 돔비 씨가, 이제 그들을 더없이 중요한 존재로 만들어 놓았다는 듯이, 몸을 한껏 곧추세웠다.\n\n\"다시 묻겠어요,\" 그녀가 멸시 어린 흔들림 없는 시선을 그에게 못 박으며 되풀이했다, \"여기 누가 있다는 걸 아시나요, 여보?\"\n\n\"부디 청하건대,\" 카커 씨가 앞으로 나서며 말했다, \"부디 빌건대, 부디 요구하건대, 저를 놓아주십시오. 이 차이가 아무리 사소하고 하찮은 것이라 해도—\"\n\n딸의 얼굴을 골똘히 살피고 있던 스큐턴 부인이 여기서 그 말을 받았다.\n\n\"내 더없이 사랑스러운 에디스,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그리고 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비. 우리의 훌륭한 벗 카커 씨가—분명 그렇게 불러야 마땅하니까—\"\n\n카커 씨가 \"과분한 영광입니다\"라고 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"—내 마음속에 있던 바로 그 말을, 내가 그동안 한없이, 꺼낼 기회를 애타게 바라던 그 말을 해 주었어요. 사소하고 하찮은 것이라고! 내 더없이 사랑스러운 에디스, 그리고 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비, 너희 둘 사이의 어떤 차이라도—아니, 플라워스, 지금은 아니야.\"\n\n플라워스는 시녀였는데, 신사들이 있는 것을 보고는 황급히 물러났다.\n\n\"너희 둘 사이의 어떤 차이라도,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말을 이었다, \"너희가 함께 지닌 그 마음과, 너희 사이에 있는 그 더없이 매혹적인 정의 끈을 생각하면, 사소하고 하찮은 것일 수밖에 없지 않겠니? 그 사실을 이보다 더 잘 일러 줄 말이 어디 있겠어? 없지. 그러니 나는 이 사소한 기회를—이토록 천성과, 너희 각자의 인품과, 그 모든 것으로 가득 찬, 어버이의 눈에 참으로 눈물을 고이게 할 만한 이 하찮은 기회를—기꺼이 빌려 이렇게 말하련다. 나는 그런 것들에 추호도 무게를 두지 않는단다. 다만 영혼의 이런 자잘한 요소들이 드러나는 것으로 볼 뿐이지. 그리고, 이 너무도 가식적인 세상에—유감스럽게도 그렇지—존재한다고 내가 들어 온 대부분의 장모들(그 끔찍한 표현이라니, 사랑하는 돔비!)과는 달리, 나는 그런 때에 결코 너희 사이에 끼어들려 하지 않을 것이며, 그 뭐라더라—큐피드가 아니라, 그 다른 사랑스러운 녀석의 횃불이 그렇게 잠깐씩 번쩍이는 것을, 따지고 보면 그리 크게 안타까워하지도 않을 거야.\"\n\n그 선량한 어머니가 이렇게 말하면서 자기 두 자식을 향해 던진 눈길에는 날카로움이 있었으니, 그것은 이 두서없는 말 속에 감추어진 어떤 직접적이고 잘 궁리된 의도를 드러내는 것이었는지도 모른다. 그 의도란, 앞으로 들이닥칠 그들 사슬의 온갖 절거덕거리는 소리로부터 애초에 자기 자신을 떼어 놓고, 둘의 상호 애정과 서로에 대한 어울림을 결백하게 믿고 있다는 허구로 자기 몸을 감싸는 것이었다.\n\n\"나는 돔비 부인에게 지적해 두었습니다,\" 돔비 씨가 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 말했다, \"우리 결혼 생활의 이토록 이른 시기에 보이는 그 행실에 대해 내가 이의를 제기한다는 것을, 그리고 그것이 시정되기를 내가 요구한다는 것을 말이오. 카커,\" 물러가라는 끄덕임과 함께, \"잘 가게!\"\n\n카커 씨는 그 거만한 신부의 자태를 향해 절을 했는데, 그녀의 반짝이는 눈은 자기 남편에게 못 박혀 있었다. 그는 나가는 길에 클레오파트라의 긴 의자 앞에 멈춰 서서, 그녀가 우아하게 내미는 손을, 낮고도 흠모 어린 경의를 담아 입술로 들어 올렸다.\n\n만일 그의 아름다운 아내가 그를 나무라기라도 했다면, 아니 안색을 바꾸기라도 했다면, 혹은 그녀가 잠겨 있던 그 침묵을, 이제 둘만 남은 마당에(클레오파트라가 부리나케 사라졌으므로) 단 한마디로라도 깨뜨려 주기라도 했다면, 돔비 씨는 그녀에 맞서 자기 주장을 어느 정도 내세울 수 있었으리라. 그러나 그를 한번 바라본 다음, 마치 그가 너무도 하찮고 자기에게 아무래도 좋은 존재라 한 음절로도 따질 가치가 없다는 듯이 눈을 떨어뜨리던 그 강렬하고 형언할 수 없는, 사람을 시들게 하는 경멸—그를 앞에 두고 앉아 있던 그 이루 말할 수 없는 멸시와 오만함—제 얼굴의 모든 이목구비로 그를 짓누르고 밀쳐 내는 듯하던 그 차갑고 굽힐 줄 모르는 결의—이런 것들에 대해 그는 맞설 방도가 없었다. 그리하여 그는, 그를 멸시하는 데에 그 위압적인 아름다움을 온통 집중하고 있는 그녀를 남겨 둔 채 떠났다.\n\n그가 한 시간쯤 뒤에, 일찍이 달빛 속에서 폴을 데리고 힘겹게 올라오던 플로렌스를 본 적이 있는 그 낡은 회랑식 계단에서, 그녀를 몰래 지켜볼 만큼 비겁했던가? 아니면 우연히 어둠 속에 있다가, 눈을 들어, 플로렌스가 누워 있는 방에서 그녀가 등불을 들고 나오는 것을 보고, 그가 끝내 굴복시키지 못한 그토록 달라진 그 얼굴을 다시금 똑똑히 보았던가?\n\n그러나 그 얼굴은 그 자신의 얼굴처럼 변할 수는 없었으리라. 그 얼굴은, 그 극도의 자긍심과 격정 속에서도, 돌아오던 그날 밤 어두운 구석에서 그의 얼굴 위로 내려앉았던 그 그림자를, 그리고 그 뒤로도 자주 내려앉았던 그 그림자를, 그리고 지금 그가 눈을 들어 올렸을 때 그 위로 더욱 짙어진 그 그림자를, 결코 알지 못했다.\n\n# 제32장\n\n이튿날 플로렌스와 이디스, 그리고 스큐턴 부인이 함께 있었고, 마차는 그들을 데리고 나가려고 문 앞에서 기다리고 있었다. 클레오파트라가 이제 다시 자기 유람선을 갖게 되었기 때문이었으니, 더는 핼쑥하지 않은 위더스가 가슴을 비둘기처럼 내민 재킷에 군용 바지를 입고 식사 때 바퀴 없는 그녀의 의자 뒤에 꼿꼿이 서서, 더는 머리로 들이받는 일도 없었다. 위더스의 머리카락은 이 솜털 같은 나날에 포마드로 윤이 났고, 그는 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 오드콜로뉴 향을 풍겼다.\n\n그들은 클레오파트라의 방에 모여 있었다. 오래된 나일강의 뱀이(그분을 무례하게 일컫자는 뜻은 아니다) 소파에 기대어 오후 세 시에 아침 초콜릿을 홀짝이고 있었고, 하녀 플라워스는 그녀에게 젊은이다운 소맷동과 주름 장식을 채워 주며, 복숭아색 벨벳 보닛으로 일종의 사적인 대관식을 거행하고 있었다. 그 보닛에 달린 조화 장미는 미풍에 흔들리듯 마비된 떨림이 희롱하는 통에 유난히 보기 좋게 까딱거렸다.\n\n\"오늘 아침엔 내가 좀 신경이 곤두선 것 같구나, 플라워스,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다. \"손이 영 떨려.\"\n\n\"어젯밤엔 마님이 좌중의 활력이셨잖아요,\" 플라워스가 응수했다, \"그러니 오늘 그 대가를 치르시는 거지요.\"\n\n이디스는 플로렌스를 창가로 손짓해 부른 뒤, 존경스러운 어머니의 화장하는 모습에 등을 돌리고 밖을 내다보고 있었는데, 마치 번개가 친 듯 갑자기 그 자리에서 물러섰다.\n\n\"내 사랑스러운 아가,\" 클레오파트라가 나른하게 외쳤다, \"너까지 신경이 곤두선 건 아니겠지? 내 사랑 이디스, 너처럼 그렇게 부러우리만큼 침착한 아이가, 불행한 체질을 타고난 네 어미처럼 너도 순교자가 되어 가고 있다는 말은 제발 하지 말아 다오! 위더스, 문에 누가 왔다.\"\n\n\"명함입니다, 마님,\" 위더스가 명함을 돔비 부인 쪽으로 가져가며 말했다.\n\n\"나는 나갈 거예요,\" 그녀가 명함을 쳐다보지도 않고 말했다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 스큐턴 부인이 느릿느릿 말했다, \"이름도 보지 않고 그런 전갈을 보내다니 어찌나 이상한지! 이리 가져오너라, 위더스. 어머나 얘야, 카커 씨로구나! 그 아주 분별 있는 분 말이야!\"\n\n\"나는 나갈 거예요,\" 이디스가 어찌나 위압적인 어조로 되풀이했던지, 문으로 가던 위더스도 위압적으로 기다리던 하인에게 일렀다, \"돔비 부인께서는 나가신다. 어서 물러가게나,\" 그러고는 그의 면전에서 문을 닫았다.\n\n그러나 하인은 잠깐 자리를 비웠다가 돌아와 다시 위더스에게 속삭였고, 위더스는 한 번 더, 그것도 그리 내키지 않는 기색으로 돔비 부인 앞에 나섰다.\n\n\"황송하옵니다, 마님, 카커 씨께서 정중한 안부를 전하시며, 가능하시다면 단 일 분만 시간을 내어 주십사 청하옵니다—사업 때문이라고 하옵니다, 마님, 괜찮으시다면요.\"\n\n\"정말이지 얘야,\" 스큐턴 부인이 더없이 부드러운 태도로 말했다. 딸의 얼굴이 험악해지고 있었기 때문이다. \"내가 한마디 거들어도 된다면, 권하건대—\"\n\n\"이리로 모셔라,\" 이디스가 말했다. 위더스가 그 명을 수행하려 사라지자, 그녀는 어머니에게 눈살을 찌푸리며 덧붙였다, \"어머니 권유로 오는 분이니, 어머니 방으로 오시게 하지요.\"\n\n\"제가—제가 나가 있을까요?\" 플로렌스가 황급히 물었다.\n\n이디스가 그렇게 하라고 고개를 끄덕였으나, 문으로 가던 플로렌스는 들어오는 방문객과 마주쳤다. 처음 그녀에게 말을 걸 때와 똑같은, 친근함과 너그러운 자제가 뒤섞인 그 불쾌한 태도로, 그는 이제 더없이 부드러운 모습으로 그녀에게 말을 건넸다—그녀가 아주 건강하기를 바란다고—그런 안색을 보니 대답을 미리 알 수 있어 굳이 물을 것도 없다고—어젯밤에는 그녀를 알아뵙는 영광을 거의 누리지 못했는데, 그토록 크게 달라지셨다고—그러고는 그녀가 지나갈 수 있도록 문을 잡아 주었다. 그녀가 자기 앞에서 움츠러드는 데서 은밀한 권력의 감각을 느끼는 듯했는데, 그것은 그의 온갖 공손함과 정중한 태도로도 끝내 다 감추지 못했다.\n\n그런 다음 그는 스큐턴 부인이 황송하게 내미는 손 위로 잠시 몸을 숙여 절했고, 마지막으로 이디스에게 절했다. 그를 쳐다보지도 않은 채 차갑게 그 인사에 답례한 그녀는, 자리에 앉지도, 그에게 앉기를 권하지도 않은 채, 그가 입을 열기를 기다렸다.\n\n자기의 자존심과 권력 안에 단단히 틀어박혀, 그 정신의 온갖 완강함을 불러 모았음에도, 그녀와 그녀의 어머니가 이 사내에게 처음 알게 된 순간부터 가장 흉한 본색까지 다 들켰다는 오래된 확신이, 그녀가 자기 눈에 겪어 온 모든 굴욕이 자기 자신에게 그러하듯 그에게도 빤히 드러나 있다는 확신이, 그가 마치 그녀의 인생을 비열한 책 한 권 읽듯 읽어 내며, 다른 누구도 알아채지 못할 가벼운 눈짓과 말투로 그 책장을 그녀 앞에서 펄럭여 보인다는 확신이, 그녀를 약하게 하고 무너뜨렸다. 그녀가 자랑스레 그에게 맞서 명령하는 듯한 얼굴로 그의 비굴함을 요구하고, 멸시하는 입술로 그를 물리치고, 가슴이 그의 침입에 분노하고, 그 눈의 검은 속눈썹이 빛이 그에게 한 줄기도 비치지 못하도록 그 빛을 음울하게 가려 보아도—또 그가 애원하는 듯 상처 입은 태도로, 그러나 그녀의 뜻에는 완전히 복종하며 그녀 앞에 순종하듯 서 있어도—그녀는 제 영혼 속에서 알고 있었다, 처지가 뒤바뀌어 있다는 것을, 승리와 우위는 그의 것임을, 그리고 그가 그것을 너무도 잘 알고 있다는 것을.\n\n\"제가 외람되이,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"면담을 청했고, 또 외람되이 그것을 사업상의 일이라 일컬었습니다. 왜냐하면—\"\n\n\"아마 돔비 씨에게서 무슨 책망의 전갈이라도 받아 오셨나 보군요,\" 이디스가 말했다. \"당신은 돔비 씨의 신임을 워낙 유별나게 받고 계시니, 만일 그것이 당신의 용건이라 해도 저로서는 별로 놀랍지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그분의 이름에 광채를 더해 주시는 부인께 전할 전갈 따위는 없습니다,\" 카커 씨가 말했다. \"다만 제 자신을 위하여, 부인의 손에 정의를 구하는 더없이 미천한 청원자에게—한낱 돔비 씨에게 딸린 자에게—그것은 미천한 처지이지요—공정하게 대해 주시기를, 그리고 어젯밤 제가 얼마나 완전히 무력했는지, 또 매우 고통스러운 자리에서 제게 떠넘겨진 몫을 도무지 피할 수 없었음을 헤아려 주시기를 간청합니다.\"\n\n\"내 사랑 이디스,\" 클레오파트라가 안경을 비스듬히 들면서 낮은 목소리로 귀띔했다, \"이름이 뭐라더라 하는 저 양반, 정말이지 매우 매력적이구나. 게다가 마음이 풍부해!\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 저는,\" 카커 씨가 고마운 듯 공손한 눈빛으로 스큐턴 부인에게 호소하며 말했다, \"감히 그것을 고통스러운 자리였다고 부릅니다, 다만 그 자리에 있는 불운을 겪은 제게 그러했기 때문일 뿐입니다. 당사자들 사이의—사심 없는 헌신으로 서로 사랑하며, 그런 일을 위해서라면 어떤 자기희생도 마다하지 않을 두 분 사이의—그토록 사소한 의견 차이는 아무것도 아닙니다. 스큐턴 부인께서 어젯밤 친히 그토록 진실하고 절절하게 말씀하셨듯이, 그것은 아무것도 아닙니다.\"\n\n이디스는 그를 쳐다볼 수 없었으나, 잠시 후 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그래서 용건은 무엇이지요, 선생—\"\n\n\"이디스, 얘야,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"이렇게 내내 카커 씨를 서 계시게 하다니! 카커 씨, 자, 부디 자리에 앉으세요.\"\n\n그는 어머니에게는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 오직 그녀에게서만 권유받기를 바라며, 또 기어이 그녀에게서 권유받고야 말겠다는 듯, 거만한 딸에게 눈길을 고정했다. 이디스는 자기도 모르게 자리에 앉았고, 그에게도 앉으라고 손으로 살짝 신호했다. 그보다 더 차갑고, 더 오만하고, 우월함과 무례함을 풍기는 데 있어 그보다 더 건방진 행동은 없었으나, 그녀는 그 양보조차 하지 않으려 안간힘을 썼지만 헛수고였고, 그것은 그녀에게서 억지로 빼앗긴 것이었다. 그것으로 충분했다! 카커 씨는 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"부인, 허락해 주신다면,\" 카커가 빛을 내듯 스큐턴 부인을 향해 흰 이를 드러내며 말했다—\"부인처럼 빼어난 분별력과 예민한 감수성을 지니신 분이라면, 분명 그럴 만한 이유가 있어서라고 저를 믿어 주시리라 확신합니다—드릴 말씀을 돔비 부인께만 아뢰고, 그것을—돔비 씨 다음으로—그분의 가장 좋고 가장 소중한 벗이신 부인께 전하는 일은 부인께 맡겨도 되겠는지요?\"\n\n스큐턴 부인은 물러가려 했으나, 이디스가 그녀를 붙들었다. 이디스는 그도 막아서서, 떳떳이 말하든지 아예 말하지 말든지 하라고 분연히 명령하려 했으나, 그가 낮은 목소리로 이렇게 말했다—\"플로렌스 아가씨—방금 이 방을 나가신 그 젊은 분 말입니다—\"\n\n이디스는 그가 말을 이어 가도록 내버려 두었다. 이제 그녀는 그를 쳐다보았다. 그가 더 가까이 다가오려고 극진히 정중하고 공손한 태도로, 자기를 낮추는 미소 속에 이를 설득하듯 가지런히 드러내며 몸을 앞으로 굽혔을 때, 그녀는 그를 때려죽일 수도 있을 것 같은 심정이었다.\n\n\"플로렌스 아가씨의 처지는,\" 그가 말문을 열었다, \"불운한 것이었습니다. 아버님에 대한 부인의 애착이 본래 그분에게 닿는 말 한마디 한마디에도 경계하고 민감하실 터이니, 그분 앞에서 이 이야기를 꺼내기가 저로서는 망설여집니다.\" 언제나 또렷하고 부드러운 말씨였으나, 그가 이 말을, 또 그와 비슷한 다른 어떤 말을 할 때의 그 또렷함과 부드러움은 어떤 언어로도 그 정도를 다 표현할 수 없었다. \"그러나 다른 방식으로나마 돔비 씨에게 헌신하는 사람으로서, 또 돔비 씨의 인품을 흠모하며 일생을 보내는 사람으로서, 아내로서의 부인의 다정함을 거스르지 않는 한도에서 말씀드려도 된다면, 플로렌스 아가씨는 불행히도 방치되어 왔습니다—그 아버님에 의해서요. 아버님에 의해서라고 말씀드려도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n이디스가 대답했다, \"알고 있어요.\"\n\n\"알고 계신다고요!\" 카커 씨가 크게 안도하는 기색으로 말했다. \"제 가슴에서 산 하나가 치워지는군요. 그 방치가 어떻게 비롯되었는지도 알고 계시기를 바라도 되겠습니까. 돔비 씨의 자존심—그러니까 인품의—그 사랑스러운 한 단면에서 비롯되었다는 것을요?\"\n\n\"그 대목은 넘어가셔도 됩니다, 선생,\" 그녀가 응수했다, \"하실 말씀의 결론으로 더 빨리 들어가세요.\"\n\n\"실로, 저는 잘 알고 있습니다, 부인,\" 카커가 응수했다, \"믿어 주십시오, 저는 깊이 알고 있습니다, 돔비 씨가 부인 앞에서 그 무엇에 대해서도 변명할 필요가 없다는 것을요. 그러나 부디 부인 자신의 가슴으로 제 가슴을 헤아려 주시면, 그분을 향한 제 관심이, 혹 그 지나침으로 조금 빗나가더라도, 용서해 주실 것입니다.\"\n\n자기 자존심 강한 가슴에 얼마나 깊은 비수였던가, 그와 얼굴을 맞대고 앉아, 그가 제단에서 한 거짓 맹세를 거듭거듭 그녀에게 받아들이라 들이밀며, 그녀가 그 역겨움을 인정할 수도, 외면할 수도 없는 구역질 나는 잔의 찌꺼기처럼 그것을 강요하는 것을 견뎌야 한다는 것은! 그녀가 그 아름다움 속에 꼿꼿하고 위엄 있게 그의 앞에 서 있으면서도, 제 영혼 속에서는 그의 발치에 엎드려 있음을 알았을 때, 그녀 안에서 수치와 회한과 격정이 얼마나 들끓었던가!\n\n\"플로렌스 아가씨는,\" 카커가 말했다, \"모든 면에서 자기보다 못한 하인들과 돈에 움직이는 사람들의 보살핌에—그것을 보살핌이라 부를 수 있다면—맡겨진 채, 어린 시절 필연적으로 무슨 길잡이도 나침반도 없었고, 그래서 당연히 그것이 없는 탓에 경솔하게 굴어 왔으며, 어느 정도는 자기 신분을 잊어버렸습니다. 월터라는 어떤 자에 대한 어리석은 일이 있었는데, 흔해 빠진 청년으로 다행히도 이제는 죽었지요. 그리고, 말씀드리기 송구하오나, 평판이라곤 도무지 좋지 않은 어떤 연안 뱃사람들과, 또 달아난 늙은 파산자와의 아주 바람직하지 못한 교제도 좀 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 사정은 저도 들어 알고 있어요, 선생,\" 이디스가 멸시하는 눈빛을 그에게 번뜩이며 말했다, \"그리고 당신이 그것을 왜곡하고 있다는 것도 알아요. 당신은 모르고 그러는지도 모르지요. 그러기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"용서하십시오,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"저는 그 누구도 저만큼 그 사정을 잘 아는 사람은 없다고 믿습니다. 부인의 너그럽고 열렬한 천성—사랑하고 존경하는 남편을 변호하는 데 그토록 고귀하게 단호하며, 그 미덕에 합당한 만큼 그분을 축복해 온 바로 그 천성—을 저는 존경하고, 따르며, 그 앞에 머리를 숙이지 않을 수 없습니다. 그러나 그 사정에 관해서라면—이것이야말로 제가 외람되이 부인의 주의를 청한 용건입니다만—저는 추호도 의심할 수 없습니다. 왜냐하면 돔비 씨의 비밀스러운—감히 말씀드리건대—벗으로서 맡은 소임을 수행하는 과정에서 그것들을 빠짐없이 확인했기 때문입니다. 그 소임을 수행하면서, 또 그분과 관련된 모든 일에 대한, 부인께서 그토록 잘 헤아리실 저의 깊은 관심으로—굳이 말하자면 제 부지런함을 입증하고 저를 좀 더 받아들여지게 만들려는 더 낮은 동기로 더욱 강해진(부인의 불쾌함을 사고 있는 듯하여 두렵습니다만)—저는 오랫동안 저 자신과 믿을 만한 수단을 통해 이 사정을 추적해 왔고, 헤아릴 수 없이 많고도 더없이 세세한 증거를 가지고 있습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 눈을 그의 입 높이보다 더 높이 들지 않았으나, 그 입에 담긴 이 하나하나에서 해악의 수단이 으스대는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"용서하십시오, 부인,\" 그가 말을 이었다, \"제가 곤혹스러운 나머지, 외람되이 부인과 의논하고 부인의 뜻을 여쭙고자 하더라도요. 제가 보기에 부인께서는 플로렌스 아가씨에게 무척 관심을 두고 계신 듯한데, 제가 그것을 알아챈 것 같습니다만?\"\n\n그가 그녀에게서 알아채지 못한 것이, 알지 못하는 것이 무엇이 있었던가? 그 생각이, 아무리 희미하게라도 새롭게 떠오를 때마다, 굴욕스러우면서도 미칠 것 같아, 그녀는 떨리는 입술 위에 이를 꾹 눌러 억지로 침착을 강요하고는, 쌀쌀맞게 고개를 숙여 답했다.\n\n\"이 관심은요, 부인—돔비 씨와 연관된 모든 것이 부인께 소중하다는 그토록 가슴 뭉클한 증거인 그 관심은—제가 이 사정을, 아직 그분이 모르고 계신 이 사정을 그분께 알려 드리기에 앞서 잠시 멈추게 만듭니다. 고백해도 된다면, 그것이 제 충성심을 어찌나 흔드는지, 부인께서 그러기를 바란다는 기색을 조금이라도 비치시면, 저는 그것들을 묻어 두겠습니다.\"\n\n이디스가 재빨리 고개를 들고, 흠칫 물러나며, 그 검은 눈길을 그에게 쏟았다. 그는 더없이 상냥하고 더없이 공손한 미소로 그것을 받아 내며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"제가 묘사하는 그대로라면 그것은 왜곡된 것이라고 부인께서는 말씀하십니다. 저는 그렇지 않으리라 두렵습니다—그렇지 않으리라 두렵습니다. 그러나 왜곡되었다고 쳐 봅시다. 제가 한동안 이 문제로 느껴 온 불안은 이 점에서 비롯됩니다. 곧, 플로렌스 아가씨 쪽에서 그런 교제를 여러 번 거듭한 그 단순한 정황만으로도, 그것이 아무리 순진하고 거리낌 없는 것이었다 한들, 이미 그녀에게 좋지 않은 마음을 품고 계신 돔비 씨에게는 결정적인 빌미가 될 것이며, 그래서 그분이 그녀를 자기 집에서 갈라놓고 떼어 놓는 어떤 조치를(이따금 그것을 염두에 두셨다는 걸 저는 압니다) 취하시게 만들리라는 것입니다. 부인, 저를 참고 들어 주시고, 제가 돔비 씨와 맺어 온 교분과, 그분에 대한 저의 이해와, 거의 어린 시절부터의 그분에 대한 저의 경외심을 기억해 주십시오. 그분께 흠이 있다면, 그것은 그분께 속한, 또 우리 모두가 따르지 않을 수 없는 그 고귀한 자존심과 권력의 감각에 뿌리내린 드높은 고집입니다. 그것은 다른 사람들의 옹고집처럼 무너뜨릴 수 있는 것이 아니며, 날마다, 해마다 제 스스로 자라나는 것입니다.\"\n\n그녀는 여전히 그에게 눈길을 쏟고 있었으나, 아무리 흔들림 없이 바라보려 해도, 그가 그녀가 모두 머리를 숙여야 한다고 묘사하는 그 후원자의 그것을 묘사할 때, 그녀의 거만한 콧방울이 벌름거렸고, 숨이 어딘가 깊어졌으며, 입술이 살짝 비틀렸다. 그는 그것을 보았다. 그리고 그의 표정은 변하지 않았지만, 그녀는 그가 보았음을 알았다.\n\n\"어젯밤의 일처럼 그토록 사소한 사건조차도,\" 그가 말했다, \"한 번 더 그것을 언급해도 된다면요, 더 큰 사건보다 오히려 제 뜻을 더 잘 보여 줄 것입니다. 돔비와 아들은 때도, 장소도, 철도 가리지 않으며, 그 모두를 짓눌러 버립니다. 그러나 저는 그 일이 일어난 것을 기뻐합니다. 비록 그것이 제게 부인의 일시적인 불쾌라는 벌을 안겨 주었을지언정, 오늘 제가 이 문제로 돔비 부인께 다가갈 길을 열어 주었으니까요. 부인, 이 문제에 대한 불안과 염려가 한창이던 와중에, 저는 돔비 씨의 부름을 받고 리밍턴으로 갔습니다. 거기서 저는 부인을 뵈었습니다. 거기서 저는 부인께서 머지않아 그분과 어떤 관계를 맺으시게 될지—그분과 부인의 변함없는 행복에 어떤 관계를 맺으시게 될지 알지 않을 수 없었습니다. 거기서 저는 부인께서 이곳 가정에 자리 잡으실 때를 기다렸다가, 지금 제가 한 대로 하기로 결심했습니다. 제가 알고 있는 바를 부인의 가슴속에 묻어 둔다 해도, 돔비 씨에 대한 제 의무를 저버리는 것은 아니라고 저는 진심으로 두려워하지 않습니다. 왜냐하면 두 사람 사이에 단 하나의 마음과 정신만이 있는 곳에서는—이런 결혼이 그러하듯이—한쪽이 다른 쪽을 거의 대신하기 때문입니다. 그러므로 이런 주제에서 부인을 신뢰하든 그분을 신뢰하든, 저는 거의 똑같이 제 양심에 떳떳할 수 있습니다. 앞서 말씀드린 이유로 저는 부인을 택하겠습니다. 제 신뢰가 받아들여졌고, 제가 책임에서 벗어났다고 믿어도 되는 그 영광을 감히 바라도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n그는 그녀가 그에게 보낸 그 눈빛을—누가 그것을 보고 잊을 수 있겠는가?—그리고 그녀 안에서 뒤이은 그 갈등을 오래도록 기억했다. 마침내 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"받아들이겠어요, 선생. 이 일은 이것으로 끝난 것으로, 그리고 더는 나아가지 않는 것으로 여겨 주세요.\"\n\n그는 깊이 절하고 일어섰다. 그녀도 일어섰고, 그는 더없이 겸손하게 작별했다. 그러나 계단에서 그와 마주친 위더스는 그의 이의 아름다움과 그 눈부신 미소에 놀라 멍해졌고, 그가 다리가 흰 말을 타고 떠나갈 때, 사람들은 그를 치과 의사로 여겼으니, 그가 보이는 광경이 그토록 눈부셨던 것이다. 잠시 후 그녀가 마차를 타고 나갔을 때, 사람들은 그녀를 부유하고 고운 만큼이나 행복한 귀부인으로 여겼다. 그러나 그들은 바로 그 직전 아무도 곁에 없는 자기 방에서의 그녀를 보지 못했고, 그녀가 내뱉은 세 마디, \"오 플로렌스, 플로렌스!\"를 듣지 못했다.\n\n소파에 기대어 초콜릿을 홀짝이던 스큐턴 부인은 사업이라는 그 낮은 말 한마디 말고는 아무것도 듣지 못했는데, 그녀는 그 말을 끔찍이도 싫어하여 진작에 자기 어휘에서 추방해 버린 터라, 그 결과로—매력적인 태도와 엄청난 마음, 영혼은 말할 것도 없거니와—여러 모자 가게 주인이며 그 밖의 사람들을 망하게 만들 뻔하기까지 했다. 그러므로 스큐턴 부인은 아무것도 묻지 않았고, 아무런 호기심도 보이지 않았다. 사실, 그 복숭아색 벨벳 보닛만으로도 그녀는 바깥에서 충분히 분주했으니, 그것이 머리 뒤쪽에 얹혀 있는 데다 그날따라 바람이 제법 부는 통에, 그것은 스큐턴 부인의 곁을 떠나려고 미친 듯이 발버둥 쳤고, 어떤 식의 타협에도 도무지 구슬려지지 않으려 했다. 마차가 닫히고 바람이 차단되자, 그 마비된 떨림이 다시 조화 장미 사이에서 노쇠한 미풍들로 가득 찬 양로원처럼 희롱했고, 도무지 스큐턴 부인은 할 일이 많았으나 그저 그럭저럭 해 나갈 뿐이었다.\n\n저녁이 되어 가도 그녀는 별로 나아지지 않았으니, 돔비 부인이 화장실에서 단장을 마치고 그녀를 반 시간이나 기다리고, 돔비 씨가 응접실에서 엄숙한 짜증의 상태로 거닐며 시간을 보내고 있을 때(세 사람 모두 만찬에 나가려던 참이었다), 하녀 플라워스가 창백한 얼굴로 돔비 부인 앞에 나타나 말했다.\n\n\"황송하옵니다, 마님, 죄송하지만, 큰 마님을 도무지 어찌할 수가 없어요!\"\n\n\"무슨 말이냐?\" 이디스가 물었다.\n\n\"저어, 마님,\" 겁먹은 하녀가 응수했다, \"저도 잘 모르겠어요. 자꾸 얼굴을 일그러뜨리세요!\"\n\n이디스는 하녀와 함께 어머니의 방으로 서둘러 갔다. 클레오파트라는 정장을 갖추어 입고, 다이아몬드며 짧은 소매며 연지며 곱슬머리며 이며 그 밖의 온갖 젊은이다움까지 완벽하게 차린 채였다. 그러나 중풍은 속아 넘어가지 않았으니, 그녀가 제 임무의 대상임을 진작에 알아보고는 거울 앞에서 그녀를 후려쳤고, 그녀는 그 앞에서 굴러떨어진 끔찍한 인형처럼 쓰러져 있었다.\n\n그들은 수치심 속에서 그녀를 한 조각씩 해체하여, 그녀에게서 진짜인 그 자그마한 부분을 침대에 뉘었다. 의사들이 호출되어 곧 왔다. 강력한 치료제가 동원되었고, 그녀가 이번 충격에서는 회복하겠으나 또 한 번은 견디지 못하리라는 소견이 나왔다. 그렇게 그녀는 며칠 동안 말도 못 한 채 천장을 빤히 응시하며 누워 있었다. 때로는 여기 누가 와 있는지 아느냐는 따위의 물음에 또렷하지 않은 소리를 내어 답했고, 때로는 신호로도 몸짓으로도, 깜박이지 않는 눈으로도 아무 대답을 하지 않았다.\n\n마침내 그녀는 의식을, 그리고 어느 정도 운동 능력을 되찾기 시작했으나, 아직 말하는 능력은 아니었다. 어느 날 오른손의 쓰임이 돌아왔다. 그것을 시중들고 있던 하녀에게 내보이며, 마음속이 몹시 불안한 듯한 기색으로, 그녀는 연필과 종이를 달라고 신호했다. 하녀는 즉시 그것을 마련해 주며, 그녀가 유언장을 쓰거나 무슨 마지막 부탁을 적으려는 줄로 생각했다. 돔비 부인이 외출 중이었으므로, 하녀는 엄숙한 심정으로 그 결과를 기다렸다.\n\n고통스럽게 갈겨쓰고 지우고, 제멋대로 연필에서 굴러떨어지는 듯한 엉뚱한 글자들을 써 넣은 끝에, 노부인은 이런 문서를 만들어 냈다.\n\n\"장밋빛 커튼.\"\n\n하녀가 완전히 얼어붙어 있자, 그것도 그럴 만한 까닭이 충분했으나, 클레오파트라는 두 단어를 더 보태어 원고를 고쳤고, 그러자 그것은 이렇게 되었다.\n\n\"의사들을 위한 장밋빛 커튼.\"\n\n하녀는 이제 어렴풋이나마, 그녀가 의사들에게 자기 안색을 더 좋게 내보이려고 그 물건들을 마련받고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘다. 그리고 그 집에서 그녀를 가장 잘 아는 사람들도 이 의견의 정확함을 의심치 않았으니, 머지않아 그녀 스스로 그것을 입증할 수 있게 되었거니와, 장밋빛 커튼이 그녀의 침대에 더해졌고, 그녀는 그때부터 한층 빠르게 차도를 보였다. 그녀는 곧 곱슬머리에 레이스 달린 모자와 잠옷 차림으로 일어나 앉아, 두 뺨의 움푹 팬 동굴 속에 약간의 인공 홍조를 떨어뜨리게 할 수 있었다.\n\n이 노부인이 화려하게 차려입고 죽음을 향해 추파를 던지며 점잔을 빼고, 마치 그가 소령이라도 되는 양 그에게 자기 젊은 시절의 수작을 부려 대는 광경은 대단한 볼거리였다. 그러나 그 중풍 발작 뒤에 그녀의 정신에 뒤따른 어떤 변화는, 그에 못지않게 곱씹어 볼 거리로 가득했고, 또 그만큼이나 섬뜩했다.\n\n그녀의 지력이 약해진 탓에 그녀가 전보다 더 교활하고 거짓되어진 것인지, 아니면 그녀가 자기가 그런 척해 온 모습과 자기가 정말로 그러했던 모습 사이에서 그녀를 헷갈리게 만든 것인지, 아니면 그것이, 빛 속으로 솟아나지도, 완전한 어둠 속으로 되돌아가지도 못하는 어떤 회한의 가물거림을 일깨운 것인지, 아니면 그녀의 정신 기능들이 뒤죽박죽된 가운데 이 영향들이 한데 뒤섞여 흔들린 것인지—아마 이쪽이 더 그럴듯한 추측일 텐데—그 결과는 이러했다. 곧 그녀는 이디스의 애정과 감사와 자기를 향한 관심에 대해 엄청나게 까다로워졌고, 자기 자신을 더없이 값을 매길 수 없는 어버이라고 한껏 추켜세웠으며, 이디스의 마음에 누가 경쟁자로 끼어드는 것을 몹시 시기했다. 더 나아가, 그 주제를 피하기로 둘 사이에 맺은 그 약속을 기억하기는커녕, 그녀는 끊임없이 딸의 결혼을 자기가 비할 데 없는 어머니라는 증거로 들먹였다. 그리고 이 모든 것은, 그런 상태 특유의 약함과 까탈스러움과 더불어, 늘 그녀 자신의 경박함과 젊은 척하는 태도에 대한 풍자적인 주석 노릇을 했다.\n\n\"돔비 부인은 어디 있느냐?\" 그녀는 하녀에게 묻곤 했다.\n\n\"외출하셨어요, 마님.\"\n\n\"외출했다고! 그 애가 제 어미를 피하려고 나간 게냐, 플라워스?\"\n\n\"어머나 천만에요, 마님. 돔비 부인께서는 그저 플로렌스 아가씨와 함께 마차 산책을 나가셨을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"플로렌스 아가씨라. 플로렌스 아가씨가 누구냐? 나한테 플로렌스 아가씨 얘기는 하지 마라. 플로렌스 아가씨가 나에 비하면 그 애한테 뭐란 말이냐?\"\n\n다이아몬드를 적절히 내보이거나, 복숭아색 벨벳 보닛을(그녀는 바깥출입을 할 수 있게 되기 몇 주 전부터 손님을 맞으려고 그 보닛을 쓰고 앉아 있었다) 내보이거나, 그녀를 무슨 장신구로 치장해 주거나 하면, 이 대목에서 흐르기 시작한 눈물이 대개 멎었다. 그러면 그녀는 이디스가 보러 올 때까지 흐뭇한 상태로 있다가, 그 거만한 얼굴을 한 번 보는 순간 다시 도지곤 했다.\n\n\"원, 정말이지, 이디스!\" 그녀는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 외치곤 했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이세요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이냐고! 정말이지 무슨 일인지 나도 모르겠다. 세상이 어찌나 가식적이고 배은망덕한 꼴이 되어 가는지, 이제는 거기에 마음이라곤—혹은 그런 따위라곤—단 한 톨도 남아 있지 않은 것 같다는 생각이 들기 시작하는구나, 정말로. 위더스가 너보다 더 내 자식 같다니까. 그 애가 내 친딸보다 훨씬 더 나를 살뜰히 챙겨 준단 말이다. 차라리 내가 이렇게 젊어 보이지 않았으면—이런저런 것들도 다 없었으면—싶을 지경이야, 그러면 좀 더 대접받을지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"무엇을 바라세요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"오, 아주 많은 것을 바란다, 이디스,\" 짜증스럽게.\n\n\"가지지 못한 것 중에 바라는 게 있으세요? 만약 있다면 그건 어머니 탓이에요.\"\n\n\"내 탓이라고!\" 훌쩍이기 시작하며. \"내가 너에게 어떤 어버이였는데, 이디스. 너를 요람에서부터 동무로 삼았는데! 그런데 네가 나를 소홀히 하고, 마치 내가 남이라도 되는 양 나한테 자연스러운 애정이라곤 조금도 없으면서—네가 플로렌스에게 품은 애정의 스무 분의 일도 안 되게 하면서—하지만 나는 그저 네 어미일 뿐이고, 그 애를 하루 만에 타락시키기라도 할 사람이지!—나더러 그게 내 탓이라고 나무라는구나.\"\n\n\"어머니, 어머니, 저는 어머니를 아무것도 나무라지 않아요. 어찌하여 늘 이 일을 곱씹으세요?\"\n\n\"내가 온통 애정과 감수성으로 이루어진 사람이고, 네가 나를 쳐다볼 때마다 가장 잔인하게 상처 입는데, 내가 이 일을 곱씹는 게 당연하지 않으냐?\"\n\n\"저는 어머니를 상처 입힐 생각이 없어요, 어머니. 우리 사이에 오갔던 말을 기억 못 하세요? 지난 일은 가만 묻어 두세요.\"\n\n\"그래, 묻어 두라지! 그리고 나에 대한 감사도 묻어 두고, 나에 대한 애정도 묻어 두라지. 그리고 너는 너한테 손톱만큼의 연고도 없는 새 친척들을 찾아내어 떠받드는 동안, 나는 동무도 없고 보살핌도 없이 외진 방구석에 처박혀 묻혀 지내라지! 어쩜 세상에, 이디스, 네가 어떤 우아한 가문의 안주인 자리에 앉아 있는지 알기나 하느냐?\"\n\n\"알아요. 쉿!\"\n\n\"그리고 그 신사다운 양반, 돔비 말이다. 네가 그이와 결혼했고, 너한테 재산 양도와 지위와 마차와, 또 뭔지도 모를 것들까지 다 있다는 걸 알기나 하느냐, 이디스?\"\n\n\"정말이지 알아요, 어머니. 잘 알아요.\"\n\n\"그 어여쁘고 착한 양반—그 사람 이름이 뭐였더라?—그레인저—그 사람이 죽지만 않았던들 너는 그 사람한테서도 그만큼 받았을 테지. 그런데 이 모든 것을 누구한테 고마워해야 하느냐, 이디스?\"\n\n\"어머니께요, 어머니. 어머니께.\"\n\n\"그러면 네 팔을 내 목에 두르고 입을 맞춰 다오. 그리고 보여 다오, 이디스, 내가 너에게 더할 나위 없이 좋은 어미였다는 걸 네가 안다는 걸. 그리고 내가 너의 배은망덕에 안달하고 애태우다가 영락없는 흉물이 되지 않게 해 다오, 아니면 내가 다시 사교계에 나갔을 때 단 한 사람도 나를 못 알아볼 게다, 그 가증스러운 짐승, 소령조차도 말이야.\"\n\n그러나 때때로, 이디스가 그녀에게 더 가까이 다가가, 위엄 있는 고개를 숙여 자기의 차가운 뺨을 그녀의 뺨에 댈 때면, 어머니는 마치 그녀가 두렵기라도 한 듯 뒤로 물러서며, 부들부들 떠는 발작에 빠져, 정신이 오락가락한다고 외치곤 했다. 또 때로는 겸손하게 그녀에게 자기 침대 곁 의자에 앉아 달라고 애원하고는, (그녀가 거기 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있을 때) 장밋빛 커튼으로도 겁에 질리고 사나운 빛을 도무지 가릴 수 없는 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보곤 했다.\n\n장밋빛 커튼은 시간이 흐르며 클레오파트라의 신체적 회복 위에서, 그리고 병이 할퀴고 간 자국을 메우려고 그 어느 때보다 젊은이다워진 그녀의 옷차림 위에서, 또 연지와 이와 곱슬머리와 다이아몬드와 짧은 소매 위에서, 그리고 거울 앞에서 굴러떨어졌던 그 인형의 옷장 전체 위에서 붉게 물들었다. 그것은 또한 이따금, 그녀가 소녀 같은 킥킥 웃음으로 얼버무리는 어눌해진 말씨 위에서, 그리고 아무런 규칙도 없이 변덕스럽게 오락가락하는 가끔씩의 기억의 결함 위에서—마치 그녀의 변덕스러운 자아를 조롱하듯—붉게 물들었다.\n\n그러나 그것은 딸을 향한 그녀의 새로워진 생각과 말투의 변화 위에서는 결코 붉게 물들지 않았다. 그리고 그 딸이 이따금 그것의 영향권 안으로 들어왔어도, 그것은 미소로 환히 빛나거나 자식 된 사랑의 빛으로 부드러워진, 그 준엄한 아름다움 속 그녀의 사랑스러움 위에서는 결코 붉게 물들지 않았다.\n\n# 제33장\n\n톡스 양이 옛 지인과 다시 가까워지다\n쓸쓸한 톡스 양은 친구 루이자 칙에게 버림받고, 돔비 씨의 호의마저 잃은 처지였다—은실로 이어진 우아한 결혼 청첩장 한 쌍이 프린세스 플레이스의 벽난로 거울이나 하프시코드, 혹은 루크리셔가 휴일 소일거리로 남겨 두던 그 자그마한 전시용 자리들 가운데 어느 곳도 장식하지 못했으니—기운이 가라앉고 우울함에 무척 시달렸다. 한동안 프린세스 플레이스에서는 새의 왈츠가 들리지 않았고, 화초들은 방치되었으며, 분칠한 머리에 변발을 한 톡스 양 조상의 세밀화 위에는 먼지가 쌓였다.\n\n그러나 톡스 양은 부질없는 회한에 오래 자신을 내맡길 나이도, 그런 성정도 아니었다. 새의 왈츠가 그 비뚜름한 응접실에서 다시 지저귀며 떨림음을 낼 무렵에는, 쓰지 않아 소리 죽은 하프시코드 건반은 단 두 개뿐이었다. 그녀가 매일 아침 어김없이 초록 바구니 화단을 가꾸기 시작하기 전까지 어설픈 손질의 희생물이 된 제라늄 가지는 단 하나뿐이었다. 분칠한 머리의 조상이 먹구름 아래 놓인 지 여섯 주가 채 못 되었을 때, 톡스 양은 그 인자한 얼굴에 입김을 불어 가죽 헝겊으로 윤이 나도록 닦아 주었다.\n\n그래도 톡스 양은 외로웠고, 어찌할 바를 몰랐다. 그녀의 애정은, 우스꽝스럽게 드러나기는 했어도, 진실하고 강했다. 그래서 그녀는 스스로 표현한 대로 \"루이자에게서 받은 부당한 모욕에 깊이 상처받았다.\" 그러나 톡스 양의 성품에는 분노라는 것이 없었다. 부드럽게 말하는 태도로, 아무런 의견도 없이 인생을 느릿느릿 걸어왔다면, 적어도 그녀는 거친 격정도 없이 여기까지 온 셈이었다. 어느 날 길에서 꽤 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에 있는 루이자 칙을 그저 본 것만으로도 그녀의 우유 같은 천성은 그토록 압도당하여, 그녀는 곧장 어느 제과점으로 피신할 수밖에 없었다. 그러고는 보통 수프를 마시는 데 쓰이는, 소꼬리 냄새가 자욱한 곰팡내 나는 자그마한 뒷방에서, 실컷 눈물을 흘리며 감정을 달랬다.\n\n돔비 씨에 대해서는 톡스 양은 불평할 이유가 있다고는 거의 느끼지 않았다. 그 신사의 위풍에 대한 그녀의 감각이 어찌나 대단했던지, 일단 그에게서 멀어지고 나니, 자기와 그의 거리는 늘 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 멀었던 것처럼, 그리고 그가 자기를 조금이라도 너그러이 봐준 것이 대단한 겸양이었던 것처럼 느껴졌다. 톡스 양의 진심 어린 견해로는, 어떤 아내도 그에게는 지나치게 아름답거나 지나치게 위엄 있을 수 없었다. 그가 아내를 구할 때 눈을 높이 두는 것은 더없이 당연했다. 톡스 양은 눈물을 흘리며 이 명제를 내려놓았고, 하루에도 스무 번씩 그것을 온전히 받아들였다. 돔비 씨가 자기를 자신의 편의와 변덕에 종속시키고, 어린 아들의 유모 가운데 하나가 되도록 은혜로이 허락했던 그 거만한 태도를, 그녀는 결코 떠올리지 않았다. 그녀는 다만 자기 말로 \"그 집에서 무척 행복한 시간을 많이 보냈고, 그것을 늘 흐뭇하게 기억할 수밖에 없으며, 돔비 씨를 가장 인상 깊고 위엄 있는 사람 중 하나로 여기는 마음을 결코 거둘 수 없다\"고만 생각했다.\n\n그러나 가차 없는 루이자에게서 단절되고, 소령에게는 (이제 그를 다소 미심쩍게 보았으므로) 수줍은 처지가 되니, 톡스 양은 돔비 씨네 집안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 전혀 알지 못하는 것이 무척 괴로웠다. 그녀는 정말로 돔비와 아들을 온 세상이 도는 중심축으로 여기는 버릇이 들어 있었으므로, 그토록 강렬하게 자신의 관심을 끄는 소식에 깜깜한 채로 있느니, 옛 지인인 리처즈 부인과 다시 가까이 지내기로 마음먹었다. 그녀는 리처즈 부인이 돔비 씨 앞에 마지막으로 나타났던 그 기억할 만한 일 이후로 때때로 그 집 하인들과 연락을 주고받는 버릇이 있음을 알고 있었다. 어쩌면 툴 가족을 찾아 나선 톡스 양의 가슴속에는, 그 상대가 아무리 보잘것없을지라도 돔비 씨에 관해 이야기를 나눌 누군가가 있었으면 하는 애틋한 동기가 숨어 있었는지도 모른다.\n\n아무튼 어느 날 저녁, 톡스 양은 툴의 거처를 향해 발걸음을 옮겼는데, 그때 마침 석탄재투성이에 검게 그을린 툴 씨는 가족의 품에서 차를 마시며 원기를 회복하고 있었다. 툴 씨의 삶에는 단 세 가지 양상만 있었다. 그는 방금 말한 그 품 안에서 원기를 회복하거나, 시속 이십오 마일에서 오십 마일의 속도로 시골을 가로질러 내달리거나, 아니면 피로를 푼 뒤 잠을 자거나, 셋 중 하나였다. 그는 늘 회오리바람 속에 있거나 고요함 속에 있었으며, 어느 상태에서든 평화롭고 만족스럽고 느긋한 사람이었다. 그는 안달하고 안절부절못하는 자기 몫의 천성을 모조리 자신이 연결된 기관차들에게 넘겨버린 듯했고, 그 기관차들은 가차 없이 헐떡이고 숨차하고 씩씩대며 자신을 닳아 없애는 동안, 정작 툴 씨는 온화하고 한결같은 삶을 살았다.\n\n\"폴리, 여보,\" 양 무릎에 어린 툴 하나씩을 앉히고, 둘은 차를 따라 주고, 나머지 여럿은 여기저기 흩어져 있는 가운데 툴 씨가 말했다—툴 씨에게는 아이가 떨어지는 법이 없었고, 늘 넉넉한 재고를 갖추고 있었다—\"우리 바일러 요새 못 봤지, 응?\"\n\n\"못 봤어요,\" 폴리가 대답했다. \"하지만 오늘 밤엔 거의 틀림없이 들를 거예요. 오늘이 걔가 오는 저녁이고, 워낙 규칙적이니까요.\"\n\n\"내 짐작에는,\" 툴 씨가 더할 나위 없이 식사를 음미하며 말했다, \"우리 바일러가 요새 사내아이로서 할 수 있는 만큼은 잘하고 있는 모양이지, 응, 폴리?\"\n\n\"아유! 아주 훌륭하게 잘하고 있어요!\" 폴리가 대답했다.\n\n\"걔가 통 비밀스럽게 굴거나 그러진 않지—그렇지, 폴리?\" 툴 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"안 그래요!\" 툴 부인이 딱 잘라 말했다.\n\n\"걔가 통 비밀스럽게 굴지 않는다니 다행이군, 폴리,\" 툴 씨가 느릿느릿 신중한 어조로 말하며, 마치 제 몸에 석탄을 퍼 넣기라도 하듯 접칼로 빵과 버터를 떠 넣었다. \"그런 건 보기에 안 좋거든. 안 그래, 폴리?\"\n\n\"아, 당연히 안 좋죠, 여보. 그걸 왜 물어요!\"\n\n\"얘들아 봐라,\" 툴 씨가 가족을 둘러보며 말했다, \"정직한 방식으로 무슨 일을 하든 간에, 내 생각엔 터놓고 하는 것보다 더 나은 건 없단다. 깎아낸 길이나 터널 안에 들어서더라도, 비밀스러운 장난은 하지 마라. 기적을 계속 울리고, 너희가 어디 있는지 알리란 말이다.\"\n\n떠오르는 새싹 같은 툴 아이들은, 아버지의 충고를 따르겠다는 결의를 드러내며 날카로운 웅얼거림을 일으켰다.\n\n\"한데 어째서 그걸 롭한테다 대고 하는 말씀이세요, 여보?\" 아내가 걱정스레 물었다.\n\n\"폴리, 여보,\" 툴 씨가 말했다, \"내가 딱히 롭을 두고 한 말인지는 나도 잘 모르겠어, 정말로. 난 그저 롭에서 가볍게 출발했을 뿐이야. 그러다 분기점에 다다르지. 거기서 마주친 걸 싣고 가는 거야. 그러다 보면 내가 어디 있는지, 그것들이 어디서 왔는지 알기도 전에 온 한 줄의 생각 기차가 거기에 연결돼 버린다고. 사람 생각이란 게 참 대단한 환승역이지,\" 툴 씨가 말했다, \"정말이지!\"\n\n이 심오한 성찰을 툴 씨는 차 한 파인트 잔으로 씻어 내리고는, 묵직한 빵과 버터로 단단히 다졌다. 그러는 한편 어린 딸들에게는 주전자에 뜨거운 물을 넉넉히 채워 두라고 일렀으니, 자기가 유난히 목이 말라서 갈증이 풀리기 전까지 \"잔이 한 무더기\"라는 막연한 양을 들이켤 참이었기 때문이다.\n\n하지만 툴 씨는 제 배를 채우면서도 주변의 어린 가지들을 소홀히 하지 않았다. 그 아이들은 저녁 식사를 이미 마쳤음에도, 별미라며 정해진 분량 밖의 부스러기를 노리고 있었다. 그는 이따금 그 부스러기를 기다리는 무리에게 나눠 주었는데, 커다란 빵과 버터 덩이를 내밀어 정당한 순서대로 가족이 한 입씩 베어 물게 하고, 마찬가지로 숟가락으로 차를 조금씩 떠 주었다. 이 간식들이 어린 툴들의 입에 어찌나 맛있던지, 그것을 맛본 뒤에는 자기들끼리 환희의 사사로운 춤을 추었고, 저마다 한 발로 서서 깡충깡충 뛰며 그 밖에도 갖가지 도약하는 기쁨의 표시를 마음껏 드러냈다. 이렇게 흥분을 발산할 통로를 찾고 나자, 그들은 차츰 다시 툴 씨 주위로 모여들어, 그가 빵과 버터와 차를 더 해치우는 모습을 뚫어지게 지켜보았다. 하지만 그 음식들에 대해 저희 나름의 더 이상의 기대는 없는 척, 다른 화제로 이야기를 나누며 은밀히 속삭이는 척했다.\n\n이 가족 무리 한가운데서, 식욕으로 자식들에게 무시무시한 본보기를 보이며, 툴 씨는 두 무릎에 앉힌 어린 툴 둘을 특별 기관차로 버밍엄까지 실어 나르고 있었고, 빵과 버터의 방벽 너머로 나머지를 바라보고 있었다. 그때 그라인더 롭이 남서풍 모자를 쓰고 상복 작업복 차림으로 모습을 드러냈고, 형제자매들이 우르르 몰려들어 그를 맞이했다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 어머니!\" 롭이 공손히 어머니에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"잘 지내셨어요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"내 아들이로구나!\" 폴리가 그를 꼭 껴안고 등을 토닥이며 외쳤다. \"비밀이라뇨! 천만에요, 여보, 우리 애는 안 그래요!\"\n\n이 말은 툴 씨에게만 들으라고 한 것이었지만, 그라인더 롭은—제 양심에 찔리는 데가 없지 않았으므로—그 말이 떨어지자마자 알아들었다.\n\n\"뭐라고요! 아버지가 또 나에 대해 뭔가 말씀하셨다는 거예요?\" 억울한 죄 없는 사내가 외쳤다. \"아, 한 번 좀 빗나갔다고 해서, 제 친아버지가 등 뒤에서 늘 그걸 면전에 던져대는 게 얼마나 모진 일이람! 정말이지,\" 롭이 비통한 심정으로 외투 소맷자락에 의지하며 외쳤다, \"사람을 홧김에 무슨 일이라도 저지르게 만들기에 충분하다고요!\"\n\n\"가엾은 내 아들!\" 폴리가 외쳤다. \"아버지는 아무 뜻도 없이 하신 말씀이야.\"\n\n\"아버지가 아무 뜻도 없으셨다면,\" 억울한 그라인더가 엉엉 울며 말했다, \"왜 가서 무슨 말씀을 하셨던 거예요, 어머니? 친아버지만큼 저를 나쁘게 여기는 사람은 세상에 절반도 없을 거예요. 이런 인정머리 없는 일이 어디 있어요! 누가 와서 제 목을 싹둑 잘라 줬으면 좋겠네요. 아버지라면 그러는 것도 마다하지 않으실 거예요, 정말로. 차라리 그게 다른 것보단 훨씬 낫겠어요.\"\n\n이 절망적인 말에 어린 툴들이 모두 비명을 질렀다. 그 가련한 효과를 그라인더는, 자기를 위해 울지 말라고, 너희가 착한 아이들이라면 마땅히 나를 미워해야 한다고 비꼬듯 엄숙히 당부하여 더욱 키웠다. 이것이 둘째로 어린 툴—쉽게 마음이 동하는 아이였다—의 마음을 어찌나 흔들었던지, 그 아이의 영혼뿐 아니라 숨통까지 건드려, 얼굴이 새파래지는 바람에 툴 씨가 기겁하여 그를 물통으로 데려가 수도꼭지 아래에 넣으려 했으나, 그 도구를 본 순간 아이가 정신을 차린 덕에 그만두었다.\n\n사태가 이 지경에 이르자 툴 씨가 해명을 했고, 그로써 아들의 갸륵한 감정이 가라앉아, 둘은 악수를 나누었으며, 다시 화목함이 자리 잡았다.\n\n\"나처럼 좀 들겠니, 바일러야?\" 아버지가 새로운 기운으로 차로 돌아가며 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 괜찮아요, 아버지. 주인 어른이랑 같이 차 마셨어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 주인 어른은 어떠시냐, 롭?\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 모르겠어요, 어머니. 그리 자랑할 게 없어요. 보다시피 장사가 안 돼요. 그분은 그런 걸 통 모르세요—선장님 말이에요. 바로 오늘 어떤 사람이 가게에 들어와서는, '이러저러한 걸 주시오' 하더라고요—무슨 어려운 이름이었어요. '뭘?' 하고 선장님이 그러셨죠. '이러저러한 거요' 하고 그 사람이 그랬고요. '여보게,' 하고 선장님이 그러시더니, '가게를 한번 둘러보겠나' 하셨어요. '예,' 하고 그 사람이 '둘러봤소' 하니까, '찾는 게 보이나?' 하고 선장님이 물으셨죠. '아니, 안 보이오' 하고 그 사람이 그러고, '보면 그게 그건 줄 알겠나?' 하고 선장님이 또 물으셨어요. '아니, 모르겠소' 하고 그 사람이 그러니까, '그럼 이보게, 자네한테 한마디 하지,' 하고 선장님이 그러셨어요, '밖에 나가서 그게 어떻게 생겼는지 물어보고 다시 오는 게 낫겠네. 나도 더는 모르니까!' 하시더라고요.\"\n\n\"그래도 그게 돈 버는 방법은 아니잖니, 그렇지?\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"돈요, 어머니! 그분은 절대 돈 못 버실 거예요. 그렇게 별난 방식은 제 평생 본 적이 없어요. 그래도 나쁜 주인은 아니세요, 그건 제가 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 하지만 그게 저한테 큰 의미는 없어요, 그분 밑에 오래 있을 것 같진 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"네 자리를 떠나지는 말아라, 롭!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. 그 사이 툴 씨는 눈을 휘둥그레 떴다.\n\n\"그 자리는 아닐지도 몰라요, 어쩌면,\" 그라인더가 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 대꾸했다. \"이상할 것도 없죠—윗자리에 친구가 있으면 말이에요—하지만 어머니, 지금은 신경 쓰지 마세요. 저 잘 지내고 있어요, 그게 전부예요.\"\n\n이런 암시와 그라인더의 알 수 없는 태도가, 툴 씨가 넌지시 그에게 돌렸던 그 결점에 자기가 빠져 있지 않다는 반박할 수 없는 증거를 보여 주었기에, 자칫 그의 억울함과 가족의 소동이 되살아날 뻔했다. 그러나 마침맞게 또 다른 손님이 도착했는데, 폴리가 무척 놀랍게도, 그 사람은 문간에 나타나 그 자리의 모두에게 자애로운 후원과 우정의 미소를 지어 보였다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 리처즈 부인?\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"댁을 보러 왔답니다. 들어가도 될까요?\"\n\n리처즈 부인의 명랑한 얼굴이 환대의 대답으로 빛났고, 톡스 양은 권하는 의자를 받아들이며 그리로 가는 길에 툴 씨에게 우아하게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 보닛 끈을 풀고는, 우선 사랑스러운 아이들 모두에게, 하나도 빠짐없이 와서 자기에게 입을 맞춰 달라고 청해야겠다고 말했다.\n\n불운한 별 아래 태어난 듯이 보이는—집안에서 곤욕을 자주 치르는 것으로 미루어—둘째로 어린 툴은, 이 전체 인사에서 제 몫을 다하지 못했다. 그가 (앞서 만지작거리던) 남서풍 모자를 머리에 앞뒤를 거꾸로 하여 푹 눌러쓰고는 다시 벗지 못했기 때문이다. 이 사고는 겁에 질린 그의 상상 속에, 남은 평생을 어둠 속에서, 그리고 친구와 가족에게서 가망 없이 격리된 채 보내야 한다는 음울한 그림을 그려 보여, 그로 하여금 격렬하게 몸부림치며 숨 막히는 비명을 지르게 만들었다. 풀려나고 보니 그의 얼굴은 무척 뜨겁고 벌겋고 축축한 것이 드러났고, 톡스 양은 몹시 기진맥진한 그 아이를 자기 무릎에 앉혔다.\n\n\"저를 거의 잊으셨겠지요, 선생님, 아마도요,\" 톡스 양이 툴 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 부인, 아니에요,\" 툴이 말했다. \"하지만 그때 이후로 우리 모두가 조금씩 나이를 먹었지요.\"\n\n\"그래, 어떻게 지내세요, 선생님?\" 톡스 양이 상냥하게 물었다.\n\n\"정정합니다, 부인, 고맙습니다,\" 툴이 대답했다. \"부인께서는 어떻게 지내십니까? 류머티즘은 그런대로 잘 안 오나요, 부인? 우리 모두 나이가 들면 그걸 안고 살게 되리라 각오해야지요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"그 병으로 아직 불편을 느낀 적은 없답니다.\"\n\n\"참 운이 좋으십니다, 부인,\" 툴 씨가 대꾸했다. \"부인 연배의 많은 분들이 그것 때문에 고생하시지요. 우리 어머니도—\" 그러나 여기서 아내의 눈길을 알아챈 툴 씨는, 슬기롭게도 그 나머지 말을 차 한 잔에 묻어 버렸다.\n\n\"설마, 리처즈 부인,\" 톡스 양이 롭을 바라보며 외쳤다, \"저 애가 댁의—\"\n\n\"맏이예요, 부인,\" 폴리가 말했다. \"네, 정말 그렇답니다. 저 애가, 부인, 그 많은 일의 죄 없는 발단이 된 그 꼬마예요.\"\n\n\"이 녀석이요, 부인,\" 툴이 말했다, \"다리가 짧은 그 녀석입니다—그리고 그 다리가,\" 툴 씨가 어조에 시적인 운치를 살짝 담아 말했다, \"가죽바지에 비해 유난히 짧았는데—돔비 씨가 그라인더로 만들어 주신 녀석이지요.\"\n\n그 회상에 톡스 양은 거의 압도당했다. 그 회상의 대상은 그녀에게 단번에 각별한 흥미를 자아냈다. 그녀는 그에게 악수를 청했고, 그의 솔직하고 천진한 얼굴을 두고 어머니에게 축하의 말을 건넸다. 롭은 그 말을 엿듣고는 그 칭찬에 걸맞은 표정을 지어 보였으나, 그것은 도무지 적절한 표정이 아니었다.\n\n\"자, 그럼, 리처즈 부인,\" 톡스 양이 말했다,—\"그리고 선생님도요,\" 그녀가 툴에게 말했다—\"제가 여기 무엇 때문에 왔는지를 솔직하고 진솔하게 말씀드리지요. 리처즈 부인께서는 아실 테고—혹시 선생님께서도 아실지 모르겠지만—저와 제 친구들 가운데 몇몇 사이에 약간의 거리가 생겨서, 예전에는 꽤 자주 드나들던 곳을 지금은 드나들지 않게 되었답니다.\"\n\n여자다운 눈치로 이것을 단번에 알아들은 폴리는, 그만큼을 살짝 눈짓으로 드러냈다. 톡스 양이 무슨 말을 하는지 도무지 짐작도 못 한 툴 씨도, 그만큼을 멍한 눈빛으로 드러냈다.\n\n\"물론,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"우리의 자그마한 냉랭함이 어떻게 생겨났는지는 중요하지 않고, 굳이 따질 필요도 없지요. 제가 돔비 씨를 더할 나위 없이 존경하고, 그분께 더없는 관심을 두고 있다는 것만 말씀드리면 충분하겠어요.\" 톡스 양의 목소리가 떨렸다. \"그리고 그분과 관련된 모든 것에 대해서도요.\"\n\n사정을 알아차린 툴 씨는 고개를 가로저으며, 그런 말을 들은 적이 있다고, 그리고 자기로서도 돔비 씨가 까다로운 양반이라고 생각한다고 말했다.\n\n\"제발 그렇게 말씀하지 말아 주세요, 선생님, 부탁드려요,\" 톡스 양이 대꾸했다. \"지금이든 앞으로 어느 때든, 그런 말씀은 하지 말아 주시길 간청드려요. 그런 말씀은 제게 매우 고통스러울 수밖에 없고, 제가 확신하건대 선생님처럼 마음이 곧은 분께도 결코 두고두고 흡족한 일이 될 수 없으니까요.\"\n\n수긍을 얻으리라 추호도 의심치 않고 한마디 던졌던 툴 씨는, 크게 당황했다.\n\n\"제가 드리고 싶은 말씀은, 리처즈 부인,\" 톡스 양이 다시 말을 이었다,—\"그리고 선생님께도 드리는 말씀이지만—바로 이것이에요. 그 댁의 일이며, 그 댁의 안녕이며, 그 댁의 건강에 관한 어떤 소식이든 두 분께 들려온다면, 제게는 늘 더없이 반가우리라는 것이지요. 리처즈 부인과 그 댁에 관해, 그리고 옛 시절에 관해 이야기를 나누는 것이 저는 늘 무척 기쁠 거예요. 그리고 리처즈 부인과 저 사이에는 조금의 다툼도 없었으니(이제 와서는 우리가 더 가깝게 지냈더라면 하고 바라지만, 그건 저 자신 말고는 탓할 사람이 없지요), 이제 우리가 아주 좋은 친구가 되는 것을, 그리고 제가 내키는 대로 남처럼 느끼지 않고 이리로 드나드는 것을 부인께서 마다하지 않으시기를 바라요. 자, 정말이지 바라건대, 리처즈 부인,\" 톡스 양이 진지하게 말했다, \"이걸 제 뜻 그대로, 늘 그러셨듯이 그 너그러운 마음으로 받아들여 주시기를요.\"\n\n폴리는 흐뭇했고, 그것을 내비쳤다. 툴 씨는 자기가 흐뭇한지 아닌지도 모른 채, 둔중한 침착함을 유지했다.\n\n\"보세요, 리처즈 부인,\" 톡스 양이 말했다—\"그리고 선생님도 알아주셨으면 해요—저를 남처럼 여기지만 않으신다면, 제가 두 분께 자그맣게나마 도움이 될 만한 소소한 방법들이 많이 있고, 저는 기꺼이 그렇게 하고 싶답니다. 이를테면, 댁의 아이들에게 뭔가를 가르칠 수 있어요. 허락해 주신다면 자그마한 책 몇 권과 바느질거리를 가져와서, 저녁에 이따금씩 아이들이 배우면—세상에, 아주 많이 배우게 될 거예요, 분명히요, 그래서 가르친 선생에게 자랑거리가 될 거예요.\"\n\n배움을 무척 존중하던 툴 씨는, 아내를 향해 찬성하듯 고개를 까딱였고, 어렴풋이 차오르는 만족감에 손을 비비며 침을 발랐다.\n\n\"그러면, 남이 아니니까, 저는 누구에게도 걸리적거리지 않을 거예요,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"그리고 모든 일이 제가 여기 없는 것과 똑같이 굴러갈 거예요. 리처즈 부인은 저를 신경 쓰지 않고 깁기나 다림질이나 아이 돌보기나, 뭐든 하던 일을 하시면 되고요. 그리고 선생님도 그러고 싶으시면 파이프 담배를 피우시면 되지요, 그렇죠, 선생님?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 부인,\" 툴 씨가 말했다. \"예, 담배 한 대 피우겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 참 고마워요, 선생님,\" 톡스 양이 응수했다, \"그리고 이제 정말이지 진심으로 장담하건대, 그게 제게는 큰 위안이 될 거예요. 그리고 운 좋게 제가 아이들에게 무슨 좋은 일을 해 주든 간에, 두 분이 이 자그마한 약속을 편안하고 수월하고 너그럽게, 더는 한마디도 보태지 말고 받아들여 주신다면, 그것만으로도 제게 차고 넘치게 갚아 주시는 셈이에요.\"\n\n그 약속은 그 자리에서 비준되었다. 그리고 톡스 양은 이미 어찌나 마음이 편안해졌던지, 지체 없이 아이들을 하나하나 둘러보며 예비 시험을 실시했고—이것을 툴 씨는 무척 감탄해 마지않았다—종이 한 장에 아이들의 나이며 이름이며 익힌 것들을 적어 넣었다. 이 의식과, 그에 딸린 약간의 잡담이 시간을 끌어, 평소 잠자리에 드는 시각을 넘겼고, 톡스 양은 혼자 집까지 걸어가기엔 너무 늦은 시각이 될 때까지 툴네 난롯가에 붙들려 있었다. 그러나 의젓한 그라인더가 아직 그 자리에 있었으므로, 그녀를 댁의 문 앞까지 모셔다 드리겠다고 공손히 제안했다. 그리고 돔비 씨가 처음으로 그 사내다운 옷—좀처럼 이름을 입에 올리지 않는 그 옷—을 입혀 준 청년에게 집까지 바래다받는 것이 톡스 양에게는 적잖이 흐뭇한 일이었으므로, 그녀는 그 제안을 선뜻 받아들였다.\n\n이리하여 톡스 양은 툴 씨와 폴리와 악수를 나누고 아이들에게 모두 입을 맞춘 뒤, 더할 나위 없는 인기를 누리며 그 집을 나섰고, 어찌나 가벼워진 마음을 안고 갔던지, 만일 그 어진 칙 부인이 그것을 저울질해 볼 수 있었더라면 마음이 상했을 법했다.\n\n그라인더 롭은 겸손한 마음에 뒤따라 걷고자 했으나, 톡스 양은 이야기를 나눌 요량으로 그에게 곁에 붙어 걸으라 청했다. 그리고 그녀가 나중에 그의 어머니에게 표현한 대로, 길을 가며 그를 \"끌어내\" 보았다.\n\n그가 어찌나 밝고 또렷하고 반짝이게 끌려 나오던지, 톡스 양은 그에게 매료되었다. 톡스 양이 그를 끌어낼수록, 그는—철사처럼—더 곱게 뽑혀 나왔다. 그날 밤 롭이 끌려 나온 모습보다 더 훌륭하고 더 장래가 촉망되는 청년은—더 다정하고 한결같고 신중하고 진중하고 정직하고 온순하고 솔직한 젊은이는—일찍이 없었다.\n\n\"정말 기쁘네요,\" 톡스 양이 자기 집 문 앞에 이르러 말했다, \"너를 알게 돼서. 나를 친구로 여겨 주었으면 좋겠고, 내키는 대로 자주 나를 보러 와 주었으면 해. 너 저금통 있니?\"\n\n\"네, 있어요, 부인,\" 롭이 대답했다. \"은행에 넣을 만큼 충분히 모일 때까지 저축하고 있어요, 부인.\"\n\n\"정말 기특하구나,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"그 말을 들으니 기쁘다. 괜찮으면 이 반 크라운을 거기 넣으렴.\"\n\n\"아유, 고맙습니다, 부인,\" 롭이 대답했다, \"하지만 정말이지 부인 것을 빼앗을 수는 없어요.\"\n\n\"네 독립심을 칭찬한다,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"하지만 빼앗는 게 아니란다, 정말로. 내 호의의 표시로 받지 않으면 내가 섭섭할 거야. 잘 자거라, 로빈.\"\n\n\"안녕히 주무세요, 부인,\" 롭이 말했다, \"그리고 고맙습니다!\"\n\n그러고는 키득거리며 잔돈을 바꾸러 달려가, 파이 장수에게 그것을 탕진해 버렸다. 그러나 그라인더 학교에서는 명예라는 것을 가르치는 법이 없었고, 거기서 횡행하던 제도는 위선을 길러 내는 데에 유달리 효험이 있었다. 그래서 과거 그라인더 출신들의 친구와 스승들 중 다수가, 평민을 위한 교육이 이런 결과를 낳는 것이라면 차라리 그런 교육은 없는 편이 낫겠다고 말했다. 좀 더 이성적인 이들은, 더 나은 교육을 하자고 말했다. 그러나 그라인더 회사의 운영진은 늘 그들에게 대응할 준비가 되어 있었으니, 그 제도에도 불구하고 잘 자란 소년 몇몇을 골라내어, 그들이 잘 자란 것은 오로지 그 제도 덕분일 수밖에 없다고 딱 잘라 단언하는 식이었다. 이것으로 그 반대자들의 문제는 단번에 처리되었고, 그라인더 학원의 영광은 확립되었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIX. Further Adventures of Captain Edward Cuttle, Mariner\n\n발걸음이 확실하고 의지가 굳센 세월은 어찌나 부지런히 앞으로 밀고 나아갔던지, 늙은 기구 제작자가 자기 친구더러 자기가 남겨 둔 편지에 딸린 봉인된 꾸러미를 열지 말라고 당부한 기한인 일 년이 이제 거의 다 차 가고 있었고, 커틀 선장은 저녁이면 그것을 신비롭고 불안한 심정으로 바라보기 시작했다.\n\n선장은, 그 명예를 걸고, 기한이 다 차기 한 시간 전에 그 꾸러미를 여느니 차라리 자기 자신을 갈라 제 해부 구조를 들여다보겠다고 생각했을 사람이었다. 그는 그저 첫 저녁 파이프를 어느 단계쯤 피울 무렵에 그것을 꺼내 탁자 위에 올려놓고, 한 번에 두세 시간씩 말없이 진중하게 연기 사이로 그 겉면을 응시하며 앉아 있을 뿐이었다. 이따금 꽤 오랫동안 그렇게 그것을 바라보고 나면, 선장은 마치 그 매혹의 사정거리 밖으로 벗어나려는 듯, 자기 의자를 조금씩 점점 더 멀리 끌어당기곤 했다. 그러나 그것이 그의 의도였다면, 그는 결코 성공하지 못했다. 거실 벽에 부딪혀 더는 물러설 수 없게 되었을 때조차 꾸러미는 여전히 그를 끌어당겼으며, 혹은 그의 눈이 생각에 잠겨 천장이나 난롯불로 헤매어 가면, 그것의 형상이 곧바로 따라와 석탄 사이에 눈에 띄게 자리 잡거나 회칠한 벽 위에 유리한 위치를 차지하곤 했다.\n\n마음의 기쁨(플로렌스)을 향한 선장의 아버지 같은 애정과 찬탄에는 변함이 없었다. 그러나 카커 씨와의 마지막 면담 이후로, 커틀 선장은 그 젊은 아가씨와 사랑하는 소년 월터를 위해 전에 자기가 끼어들었던 일이 자기가 바랐던 만큼, 그리고 당시에 믿었던 만큼 과연 그렇게 호의적인 결과를 낳았던가 하는 의구심을 품게 되었다. 한마디로 선장은 자기가 득보다 실을 더 많이 끼친 것은 아닐까 하는 심각한 걱정으로 괴로워했다. 그리하여 그는 회한과 겸허함 속에서, 누구에게도 해를 끼치는 길에서 자기 자신을 비켜 두는 것으로, 말하자면 위험한 인물인 자기 자신을 배 밖으로 내던지는 것으로, 자기가 생각해 낼 수 있는 최선의 속죄를 행했다.\n\n그리하여 기구들 사이에 자기 자신을 파묻은 채, 선장은 돔비 씨의 집 근처에는 결코 가지 않았고, 플로렌스나 니퍼 양에게도 어떤 식으로든 소식을 전하지 않았다. 그는 퍼치 씨가 다음번에 찾아왔을 때조차, 함께해 주어 고맙기는 하나, 자기가 무심코 어떤 화약고를 터뜨리게 될지 모르는 처지라 그런 친분 일체에서 손을 떼었노라고 무뚝뚝하게 알리며, 그와도 인연을 끊었다. 이 스스로 부과한 은둔 속에서 선장은 그라인더 롭 말고는 누구와도 한마디 나누지 않은 채 며칠이고 몇 주고 보냈는데, 롭을 그는 사심 없는 애착과 충실함의 본보기로 여겼다. 이 은둔 속에서 선장은 저녁이면 꾸러미를 응시하면서 담배를 피우며 플로렌스와 가엾은 월터를 생각하며 앉아 있었고, 마침내 그 둘은 그의 소박한 상상 속에서 죽어 버린 것처럼, 그리고 그가 처음 기억하던 아름답고 순진한 아이들로서 영원한 젊음 속으로 떠나가 버린 것처럼 여겨지곤 했다.\n\n그러나 선장은 그렇게 사색에 잠기는 와중에도 자기 자신의 향상이나 그라인더 롭의 정신적 도야를 게을리하지는 않았다. 그 젊은이는 대체로 매일 저녁 한 시간씩 어떤 책을 골라 선장에게 소리 내어 읽어 주어야 했다. 그리고 선장은 모든 책이 진실이라고 무조건 믿었으므로, 이런 방법으로 수많은 놀라운 사실들을 쌓아 갔다. 일요일 밤이면 선장은 잠자리에 들기 전에 늘 산상에서 한 번 행해진 어떤 거룩한 설교를 직접 읽었다. 그리고 비록 그는 그 본문을 책 없이 제 나름의 방식으로 인용하는 데 익숙했지만, 마치 그 모든 것을 그리스어로 통째로 외워 두고 그 모든 구절에 대해 얼마든지 격렬한 신학적 논고를 써낼 수 있는 사람이기라도 한 것처럼, 그 천상의 정신을 경건하게 이해하며 읽는 듯이 보였다.\n\n그라인더 롭으로 말하자면, 그라인더 학교의 그 훌륭한 제도 아래서 영감받은 성서에 대한 그의 경외심이란, 유다의 모든 지파의 온갖 고유명사에 자기 지적 정강이를 끊임없이 부딪쳐 멍들이고, 특히 벌로써 어려운 구절들을 단조롭게 되풀이하고, 여섯 살 적에 가죽 반바지를 입은 채 일요일이면 세 번씩, 아주 무덥고 아주 높은 교회 좌석에 끌려가, 졸음이 쏟아지는 그의 머리에 대고 마치 더없이 바쁜 벌처럼 웅웅거리는 커다란 오르간 소리를 들으며 키워진 것이었으니—그라인더 롭은 선장이 읽기를 멈추면 크게 감화받은 듯한 시늉을 부렸으나, 읽기가 진행되는 동안에는 대체로 하품을 하고 꾸벅꾸벅 졸았다. 다만 후자의 사실은 그 선량한 선장이 의심조차 한 적이 없었다.\n\n커틀 선장은 또한 사업가로서 장부를 적기 시작했다. 거기에 그는 날씨에 관한 관찰과, 짐마차며 그 밖의 탈것들의 흐름에 관한 관찰을 기록해 두었는데, 그 동네에서 그것들이 아침과 낮의 대부분 동안에는 서쪽으로, 저녁 무렵에는 동쪽으로 향한다는 것을 관찰했다. 한 주에 두세 사람의 길손이 나타나 안경에 관해 \"그에게 말을 걸었고\"—선장은 그렇게 적었다—확실히 사기로 정한 것은 아니었으나 다시 들러 보겠노라고 했기에, 선장은 사업이 나아지고 있다고 판단하고 일지에 그런 취지의 기록을 남겼다. 그때 바람은 (그가 제일 먼저 기록한 바) 꽤 신선하게, 서북서로 불고 있었으며, 밤사이에 바뀐 것이었다.\n\n선장의 주된 골칫거리 가운데 하나는 투츠 씨였는데, 그는 자주 찾아왔고, 별말은 없으면서도 그 작은 안쪽 거실이 낄낄대기에 적합한 방이라는 생각을 품고 있는 듯, 선장과의 친분은 조금도 진전시키지 못한 채, 그 점에서 그 방의 편의를 누리며 한 번에 삼십 분씩 죽치고 앉아 있곤 했다. 최근의 경험으로 신중해진 선장은, 투츠 씨가 겉으로 보이는 대로 온순한 위인인지, 아니면 깊이 교활하게 본심을 감추는 위선자인지를 도무지 마음으로 가늠할 수가 없었다. 그가 돔비 양을 자주 입에 올리는 것은 수상쩍었으나, 선장은 투츠 씨가 겉으로 자기를 의지하는 데 대해 남모를 호의를 품고 있었기에, 당분간은 그에게 불리하게 단정하기를 삼갔다. 그저 그가 자기 마음에 가장 가까운 그 화제에 다가설 때마다, 이루 형언할 수 없는 명민함으로 그를 곁눈질할 뿐이었다.\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 어느 날 늘 그러듯 느닷없이 불쑥 내뱉었다, \"제 그 제안을 호의적으로 생각해 주셔서, 저에게 선장님과 친분을 맺는 기쁨을 베풀어 주실 수 있겠는지요?\"\n\n\"음, 이보게, 내 사정이 이러하네,\" 마침내 행동 방침을 정한 선장이 대답했다, \"내가 그 문제를 곰곰이 뒤집어 생각해 봤네.\"\n\n\"길스 선장님, 정말 친절하십니다,\" 투츠 씨가 응수했다. \"대단히 감사합니다. 맹세코 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데, 길스 선장님, 저에게 선장님과 친분을 맺는 기쁨을 베풀어 주시는 것은 자선 행위나 다름없을 것입니다. 정말 그렇고말고요.\"\n\n\"이보게, 형제,\" 선장이 천천히 따져 말했다, \"나는 자네를 모른다네.\"\n\n\"하지만 선장님, 선장님과 친분을 맺는 기쁨을 저에게 베풀어 주시지 않는다면,\" 투츠 씨가 제 요지를 굳건히 지키며 대답했다, \"저를 결코 아실 수가 없잖습니까.\"\n\n선장은 이 말의 독창성과 위력에 충격을 받은 듯, 자기가 기대했던 것보다 그 안에 훨씬 더 많은 것이 들어 있다고 생각하는 양 투츠 씨를 바라보았다.\n\n\"옳은 말일세, 이보게,\" 선장이 생각에 잠겨 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다, \"또 사실이고. 자, 이걸 보게. 자네가 나에게 어떤 말을 몇 마디 했는데, 그걸로 보아 자네가 어떤 사랑스러운 여인을 흠모한다는 걸 내가 알게 됐단 말일세. 그렇지?\"\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 모자를 든 손으로 격렬하게 손짓하며 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"흠모라는 말로는 부족합니다. 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데, 제 심정이 어떠한지는 선장님께서 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다. 만약 제가 검게 물들여져 돔비 양의 노예가 될 수 있다면, 저는 그것을 영광으로 여길 것입니다. 만약 제 전 재산을 희생해서 돔비 양의 개로 환생할 수 있다면, 저는—저는 정말이지 결코 꼬리 흔들기를 멈추지 않을 것 같습니다. 너무도 완벽하게 행복할 테니까요, 길스 선장님!\"\n\n투츠 씨는 눈물 어린 눈으로 그렇게 말하며, 깊은 감정에 사로잡혀 모자를 가슴에 꼭 눌렀다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 연민에 마음이 움직인 선장이 응수했다, \"자네가 진심이라면—\"\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 외쳤다, \"저는 마음이 이런 상태이고, 또 너무도 끔찍이 진심이라서, 만약 시뻘겋게 단 쇳조각이나 시뻘건 숯불이나 녹은 납이나 타는 봉랍이나 그런 종류의 어떤 것에 대고 그걸 맹세할 수 있다면, 제 심정을 달래기 위해서라도 기꺼이 제 몸을 다치게 하고 싶을 정도입니다.\" 그러고는 투츠 씨가 그 무서운 목적을 이룰 만큼 충분히 고통스러운 수단이라도 찾는 양, 방 안을 황급히 둘러보았다.\n\n선장은 번들거리는 모자를 머리 뒤로 밀어 올리고, 묵직한 손으로 얼굴을 아래로 쓸어내리며—그 과정에서 코를 더욱 얼룩덜룩하게 만들면서—투츠 씨 앞에 떡 버티고 서서, 그의 외투 옷깃을 거머쥐고는, 다음과 같은 말로 그에게 일렀다. 투츠 씨는 자못 주의를 기울이며, 또 다소 의아한 듯, 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다.\n\n\"이보게, 자네가 진심이라면 말일세,\" 선장이 말했다, \"자네는 자비를 받을 대상인데, 자비란 영국인의 머리 위 왕관에 박힌 가장 빛나는 보석이라네. 그건 자네가 '룰 브리타니아'에 정해진 헌장을 샅샅이 뒤져 보면 나오는 거고, 찾고 보면 그게 바로 그 천국의 천사들이 그토록 여러 번 거듭 노래하던 바로 그 헌장이지. 자, 잠깐! 자네의 그 제안이라는 게 나를 조금 당황하게 만든단 말일세. 어째서냐? 왜냐하면 알다시피 나는 이 수역에서 오직 내 한 몸만 건사할 뿐, 동행하는 배도 없고, 어쩌면 그런 걸 바라지도 않으니까. 침착하게! 자네가 먼저 나를 불러 세운 건 자네가 위임을 받은 어떤 젊은 아가씨 일 때문이었지. 그런데 만약 자네와 내가 조금이라도 서로 동행하는 사이가 되려면, 그 젊은 여인의 이름은 결코 입에 올려서도 안 되고 언급해서도 안 되네. 그 이름을 너무 함부로 입에 올린 탓에 지금까지 무슨 해가 끼쳐졌는지 내가 알 수 없는 노릇이라, 그래서 나는 거기서 딱 멈춘다네. 내 말 알아듣겠나, 형제?\"\n\n\"음, 양해해 주십시오, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"제가 가끔 선장님 말씀을 잘 따라가지 못하더라도요. 하지만 정말이지 저는—돔비 양을 입에 올릴 수 없다는 건 괴로운 일입니다, 길스 선장님. 제가 정말이지 여기에 이렇게 끔찍한 짐을 지고 있어서요!\"—투츠 씨가 두 손으로 애처롭게 셔츠 앞자락을 만졌다—\"밤이고 낮이고 꼭 누가 제 위에 올라앉아 있는 것처럼 느껴진단 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그게,\" 선장이 말했다, \"내가 내거는 조건일세. 그게 자네에게 가혹하다면, 형제, 아마 그럴지도 모르지만, 그건 멀찍이 피해서 방향을 돌려, 유쾌하게 갈라서면 되네!\"\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 응수했다, \"어찌 된 영문인지 저도 잘 모르겠습니다만, 제가 처음 여기 왔을 때 선장님이 저에게 해 주신 말씀 이후로, 저는—저는 다른 거의 누구와 함께 돔비 양에 대해 이야기하느니, 차라리 선장님과 함께 있으면서 그분을 생각하는 편이 낫겠다는 느낌이 듭니다. 그러니, 길스 선장님, 만약 저에게 선장님과 친분을 맺는 기쁨을 베풀어 주신다면, 저는 선장님이 내거신 조건대로 그것을 매우 기쁘게 받아들이겠습니다. 저는 정직하고 싶습니다, 길스 선장님,\" 내밀었던 손을 잠시 거두며 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"그래서 말씀드리지 않을 수 없는데, 저는 돔비 양을 생각하지 않을 수가 없습니다. 그분을 생각하지 않겠다고 약속하는 것은 저로서는 불가능한 일입니다.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 이 솔직한 고백으로 투츠 씨에 대한 평가가 한껏 좋아진 선장이 말했다, \"사람의 생각이란 바람과 같아서, 누구도 그걸 오랜 기간 한꺼번에 확실하게 책임질 수는 없는 법일세. 그럼 말에 관한 약조로 하지?\"\n\n\"말에 관해서라면, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 응수했다, \"제가 저 자신을 묶어 둘 수 있을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 즉석에서 그 자리에서 커틀 선장에게 그 약조의 표시로 손을 내밀었고, 선장은 흐뭇하고 너그럽게 겸양을 베푸는 태도로 정식으로 그에게 자기 친분을 허락했다. 투츠 씨는 그 친분을 얻은 것에 무척 마음을 놓고 기뻐하는 듯, 방문이 끝날 때까지 황홀하게 낄낄거렸다. 선장은 선장대로 그 후견인의 위치를 차지하게 된 것이 싫지 않았고, 자기 자신의 신중함과 선견지명에 더없이 흡족했다.\n\n그러나 커틀 선장이 후자의 자질에 아무리 풍부했다 한들, 바로 그날 저녁 그는 그라인더 롭이라는, 그에 못지않게 순진하고 단순한 젊은이로부터 놀라움을 받았다. 그 천진한 녀석은 같은 탁자에서 차를 마시며 찻잔과 받침 위로 얌전히 고개를 숙인 채, 안경 너머로 무척 힘겹게, 그러나 매우 위엄 있게 신문을 읽고 있는 자기 주인을 한동안 곁눈질로 살피더니, 이렇게 말하며 침묵을 깼다—\n\n\"아! 죄송합니다만, 선장님, 혹시 비둘기가 좀 필요하지는 않으시겠지요, 나리?\"\n\n\"아니다, 이놈아,\" 선장이 대답했다.\n\n\"제 것을 처분하고 싶어서요, 선장님,\" 롭이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그래?\" 선장이 텁수룩한 눈썹을 조금 치켜올리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"네. 저 갑니다, 선장님, 괜찮으시다면요,\" 롭이 말했다.\n\n\"가? 어디를 간단 말이냐?\" 선장이 안경 너머로 그를 돌아보며 물었다.\n\n\"네? 제가 선장님을 떠나려 한다는 걸 모르셨어요, 선장님?\" 롭이 음흉한 미소를 지으며 물었다.\n\n선장은 신문을 내려놓고, 안경을 벗고는, 그 탈주병에게로 시선을 모았다.\n\n\"아, 네, 선장님, 저는 미리 알려드리는 겁니다. 선장님께서 진작에 아셨을 줄 알았는데요, 아마도요,\" 롭이 두 손을 비비며 일어서서 말했다. \"선장님, 곧 사람을 좀 구해 주시면 저로서는 큰 편의가 되겠습니다. 내일 아침까지는 못 구하실 것 같긴 한데요, 선장님. 어떠세요, 가능하실 것 같으세요?\"\n\n\"그래, 너는 네 깃발을 버리고 떠날 작정이구나, 이놈아?\" 그의 얼굴을 한참 살핀 뒤 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 이건 너무 가혹하잖아요, 선장님,\" 마음 여린 롭이 순식간에 상처받고 분개하여 외쳤다, \"정당하게 미리 알려드리는 것뿐인데 그렇게 인상을 쓰며 탈주병이라고 불리다니요. 선장님은 가엾은 놈을 그런 이름으로 부를 권리가 없어요, 선장님. 제가 하인이고 선장님이 주인이라고 해서, 가서 저를 그렇게 모함하셔도 되는 건 아니잖아요. 제가 무슨 잘못을 했는데요? 자, 선장님, 제 죄가 뭔지 좀 알려 주시겠어요?\"\n\n상심한 그라인더는 울면서 외투 소맷동으로 눈을 가렸다.\n\n\"자, 선장님,\" 상처받은 젊은이가 외쳤다, \"제 죄에 이름을 붙여 보세요! 제가 뭘 어쨌다는 거예요? 제가 무슨 재산이라도 훔쳤나요? 집에 불이라도 질렀나요? 그랬다면 어째서 저를 고발해서 재판에 부치지 않으세요? 하지만 가엾은 놈이, 선장님 좋으라고 제 앞길을 막을 형편이 못 된다는 이유로, 선장님께 좋은 하인 노릇을 해 온 놈의 인격을 빼앗으시다니, 이게 얼마나 큰 상처이며, 충실한 봉사에 대한 얼마나 못된 보답인가요! 이런 식으로 젊은 놈들이 망가지고 그릇된 길로 내몰리는 거라고요. 선장님이 놀라워요, 정말로요.\"\n\n이 모든 것을 그라인더는 눈물 어린 칭얼대는 소리로 울부짖으며, 조심조심 문 쪽으로 물러섰다.\n\n\"그래서 너는 다른 일자리를 구한 거로구나, 이놈아?\" 선장이 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 선장님, 그렇게 말씀하시니 말인데, 저는 다른 일자리를 구했어요,\" 점점 더 뒤로 물러서며 롭이 외쳤다. \"여기 일자리보다 더 나은 일자리고, 거기서는 선장님의 칭찬 같은 건 아쉬울 것도 없는 데죠, 선장님. 그게 저로서는 다행이에요. 제가 가난하고, 선장님 좋으라고 제 앞길을 막을 형편이 못 된다고 선장님이 저에게 온갖 진흙을 끼얹으신 마당에 말이에요. 네, 저는 다른 일자리를 구했어요. 그리고 선장님을 사람도 없이 내버려 두는 게 마음에 걸리지만 않았다면, 선장님, 가난하다고, 또 선장님 좋으라고 제 앞길을 막을 형편이 못 된다고 그런 이름들을 들으며 견디느니, 차라리 지금 당장 거기로 가 버렸을 거예요. 어째서 제가 가난하다고, 또 선장님 좋으라고 제 앞길을 막지 않는다고 저를 나무라시는 거예요, 선장님? 어떻게 그렇게 자신을 깎아내리실 수가 있어요?\"\n\n\"이보게, 잘 들어,\" 평화로운 선장이 대답했다. \"그런 말들을 더는 풀어내지 말게.\"\n\n\"그럼 선장님도 선장님 말들을 더는 풀어 넣지 마세요,\" 격앙된 순진둥이가 칭얼대는 소리를 더 높이며, 가게 안으로 물러서면서 응수했다. \"제 인격을 빼앗기느니 차라리 제 피를 빼앗기는 게 낫겠어요.\"\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 선장이 침착하게 이어 말했다, \"자네도 어쩌면 밧줄 끄트머리라는 게 있다는 걸 들어 봤을 테니까.\"\n\n\"오, 제가 그런 걸 들어 봤다고요, 선장님?\" 비아냥대는 그라인더가 외쳤다. \"아뇨, 못 들어 봤는데요. 그런 물건은 들어 본 적도 없어요!\"\n\n\"음,\" 선장이 말했다, \"자네가 눈을 똑바로 뜨고 살피지 않으면, 머잖아 그것에 대해 더 잘 알게 되리라는 게 내 생각일세. 나는 자네 신호를 읽을 줄 안다네, 이놈아. 가도 좋아.\"\n\n\"오! 제가 당장 가도 된다고요, 선장님?\" 롭이 자기의 성공에 우쭐대며 외쳤다. \"하지만 명심하세요! 저는 당장 가게 해 달라고 한 적 없어요, 선장님. 선장님이 제 발로 내쫓으신 거니까, 또다시 제 인격을 빼앗으시면 안 돼요. 그리고 제 품삯을 한 푼도 떼시면 안 돼요, 선장님!\"\n\n그의 고용주는 양철통을 꺼내 탁자 위에 그라인더의 돈을 한 푼도 빠짐없이 세어 놓는 것으로 마지막 문제를 해결했다. 롭은 훌쩍거리고 흐느끼며, 그 심정에 몹시 상처를 입은 채, 동전을 하나하나 집어 들면서 그때마다 한 번씩 흐느끼고 훌쩍거리고는, 손수건 안에 매듭을 지어 따로따로 묶었다. 그런 다음 그는 지붕 위로 올라가 모자와 주머니에 비둘기를 가득 채웠다. 그러고는 계산대 밑 자기 잠자리로 내려와, 마치 옛 정에 가슴이 미어지기라도 하는 양 한층 더 큰 소리로 훌쩍이고 흐느끼며 짐 보따리를 꾸렸다. 그런 다음 \"안녕히 계세요, 선장님. 저는 악의 없이 떠납니다!\" 하고 칭얼대고는, 문간으로 나가더니 작별의 모욕으로 어린 항해사 인형의 코를 잡아당기고, 거리를 따라 의기양양하게 히죽거리며 떠나갔다.\n\n홀로 남은 선장은 무슨 별나거나 뜻밖의 일이 일어나기라도 했냐는 듯 다시 신문 읽기를 이어 가, 더없이 부지런히 읽어 내려갔다. 그러나 커틀 선장은 수많은 단어를 읽었으되, 단 한 마디도 이해하지 못했다. 그라인더 롭이 신문의 이 단을 타고 올라갔다 저 단을 타고 내려갔다 하며 온통 휘젓고 다녔기 때문이다.\n\n이 훌륭한 선장이 지금까지 자신이 완전히 버림받았다고 느낀 적이 있었는지는 의심스럽다. 그러나 이제 늙은 솔 길스도, 월터도, 마음의 기쁨도 그에게서 정말로 사라졌고, 이제 카커 씨는 그를 속이고 잔인하게 조롱했다. 그것들은 모두 그 거짓된 롭 안에 대표되어 있었으니, 그는 자기 안에 따뜻이 살아 있던 추억들을 그 롭에게 여러 번 늘어놓았었다. 그는 그 거짓된 롭을 믿었고, 또 기꺼이 믿었었다. 그는 그 롭을 옛 선원들 가운데 마지막으로 남은 동료로 삼았었다. 그는 그를 오른팔로 삼아 어린 항해사 인형의 지휘를 맡았었다. 그는 그에게 자기 의무를 다하려 했고, 마치 둘이 함께 난파를 당해 어느 무인지경에 던져진 사이라도 되는 양 그 소년에게 거의 다정한 마음을 품었었다. 그런데 이제 그 거짓된 롭이 일종의 성소나 다름없던 바로 그 거실에까지 불신과 배신과 비열함을 들여놓고 보니, 커틀 선장은 그다음에는 거실 자체가 가라앉아 버린다 해도, 그것이 가라앉는다고 해서 자기가 그리 크게 놀라거나 별다른 큰 근심을 느낄 것 같지도 않았다.\n\n그리하여 커틀 선장은 깊은 주의를 기울이되 아무런 이해 없이 신문을 읽었고, 그리하여 커틀 선장은 롭에 대해 혼잣말로도 한마디도 하지 않았으며, 자기가 그를 생각하고 있음을 스스로 인정하지도 않았고, 자기가 로빈슨 크루소만큼이나 외롭게 느끼는 것에 롭이 무슨 관련이 있다는 것을 아주 멀리서나마 인정하려 들지도 않았다.\n\n그와 똑같이 차분하고 사무적인 태도로, 선장은 땅거미가 질 무렵 리든홀 시장으로 건너가, 거기서 근무 중인 사설 야경꾼과 약정을 맺어, 매일 밤낮으로 와서 나무 항해사의 덧문을 올리고 내려 주도록 했다. 그런 다음 그는 음식점에 들러 그때까지 항해사에게 공급되던 일일 식량을 절반으로 줄였고, 술집에 들러 그 배신자의 맥주를 끊었다. \"내 젊은 친구가요,\" 선장이 술집 여종업원에게 설명하며 말했다, \"내 젊은 친구가 더 나은 자리로 갔답니다, 아가씨.\" 끝으로 선장은 계산대 밑의 잠자리를 차지하여, 재산의 유일한 수호자로서 위층 대신 밤마다 거기서 자기로 결심했다.\n\n그날 이후로 커틀 선장은 매일 그 잠자리에서 일어나, 마치 염소 가죽 모자로 몸단장을 마무리하는 크루소처럼 고독한 모습으로 아침 여섯 시에 번들거리는 모자를 척 눌러썼다. 그리고 비록 그 야만족, 즉 맥스팅거 부족의 습격에 대한 두려움은 다소 식어 있긴 했으나—마치 그 외로운 선원이 식인종들의 기척 하나 없이 긴 시간이 흐르면서 비슷한 우려를 누그러뜨리곤 했던 것처럼—그는 여전히 규칙적인 방어 작전의 일과를 지켰고, 자기 피난처 성에서 먼저 정찰하지 않고서는 결코 모자 쓴 여인과 마주치지 않았다. 그러는 동안(이 기간에 그는 시외에 나와 있다고 편지로 알려 온 투츠 씨로부터 아무런 방문도 받지 못했다) 자기 자신의 목소리가 귀에 이상하게 들리기 시작했다. 그리고 그는 재고품을 닦고 정리하는 데, 또 계산대 뒤에 앉아 책을 읽거나 창밖을 내다보는 데 어찌나 많은 시간을 들였던지 깊은 명상에 잠기는 버릇이 들어, 딱딱한 번들거리는 모자가 이마에 남긴 그 붉은 자국이 이따금 지나친 사색으로 다시 욱신거리곤 했다.\n\n이제 일 년이 다 차자, 커틀 선장은 그 꾸러미를 여는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼다. 그러나 그는 그것을 자기에게 가져다준 그라인더 롭이 보는 앞에서 늘 열 작정이었던 데다, 누군가가 보는 앞에서 여는 것이 격식에 맞고 선박답게 단정한 일이라는 생각을 품고 있었으므로, 증인이 없는 탓에 딱하게도 곤란해졌다. 이 곤경 속에서, 그는 어느 날 선박 소식란에 존 번스비 선장이 모는 코셔스 클라라호가 연안 항해를 마치고 도착했다는 공지가 실린 것을 유난히 반가워하며 맞이했다. 그러고는 그 철학자에게 즉시 우편으로 편지를 보내, 자기 거처에 관해서는 절대적인 비밀을 지켜 달라고 당부하며, 저녁 무렵에 일찌감치 방문해 주는 호의를 베풀어 달라고 청했다.\n\n확신에 따라 움직이는 부류의 현자 가운데 하나인 번스비는, 자기가 그런 취지의 편지를 받았다는 확신을 머릿속에 철저히 새기는 데 며칠이 걸렸다. 그러나 일단 그 사실과 씨름하여 그것을 장악하고 나자, 그는 곧바로 자기 소년을 시켜 \"오늘 밤 가신답니다\"라는 전갈을 보냈다. 그 말을 전하고 사라지라는 지시를 받은 소년은, 마치 신비로운 경고를 띤 콜타르 묻은 유령처럼 자기 임무를 완수했다.\n\n그것을 받고 무척 기뻐한 선장은 파이프와 럼주와 물을 마련해 놓고, 안쪽 거실에서 손님을 기다렸다. 여덟 시 무렵, 가게 문밖에서 바다의 황소가 우는 듯한 깊고 낮은 울음소리가 나더니, 이어 지팡이로 문짝을 두드리는 소리가 나, 커틀 선장의 귀 기울인 귀에 번스비가 곁에 와 있음을 알렸다. 그는 즉시 그를 들였는데, 번스비는 여느 때처럼 텁수룩하고 헐렁한 차림에, 그 무뚝뚝한 마호가니 빛 얼굴은 자기 앞에 있는 것은 아무것도 의식하지 못한 채, 세상의 전혀 다른 어느 구석에서 벌어지고 있는 무언가를 주의 깊게 관찰하고 있는 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"번스비,\" 선장이 그의 손을 움켜쥐며 말했다, \"어떤가, 이 사람아, 어떤가?\"\n\n\"동지,\" 번스비 내부의 목소리가, 사령관 본인 쪽에서는 아무런 기색도 없이 대답했다, \"정정하다네, 정정해.\"\n\n\"번스비!\" 선장이 그의 천재성에 대한 억누를 수 없는 경의를 바치며 말했다, \"자네가 왔구먼! 다이아몬드보다도 더 빛나는 의견을 내놓을 수 있는 사람이 말이야—그러니 콜타르 묻은 바지를 입고 빛나는 다이아몬드처럼 내게 빛나는 사내를 데려오라, 그건 자네가 스탠펠 예산서를 샅샅이 뒤져 보면 나오는 거고, 찾거든 적어 두게. 자네가 왔어, 바로 이 자리에서 의견을 하나 내놓았던 사람이, 그게 한 글자 한 글자 다 들어맞았던 그 사람이.\" 선장은 그것을 진심으로 믿었다.\n\n\"오, 그래?\" 번스비가 으르렁댔다.\n\n\"한 글자 한 글자 다,\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"어째서?\" 번스비가 처음으로 자기 친구를 바라보며 으르렁댔다. \"어느 쪽으로? 만약 그렇다면, 왜 아니겠나? 그러므로.\" 이 신탁 같은 말들과 함께—그 말들은 선장을 거의 어지럽게 만들 지경이었고, 그를 그토록 광활한 사색과 추측의 바다로 띄워 보냈다—그 현자는 자기 도선사 외투를 벗는 것을 순순히 도움받고는, 친구를 따라 안쪽 거실로 들어갔다. 거기서 그의 손은 이내 럼주 병에 내려앉아 독한 그로그 한 잔을 빚어 냈고, 곧이어 파이프에 내려앉아 그것을 채우고 불을 붙여 피우기 시작했다.\n\n커틀 선장은 이 세부적인 일에서 손님을 흉내 냈으나, 그 위대한 사령관의 황홀하고 흔들림 없는 태도는 그의 능력으로는 도무지 따라갈 수 없는 경지였으므로, 난롯가 맞은편 구석에 앉아 그를 공손히 지켜보며, 마치 번스비 쪽에서 무슨 격려나 호기심의 표명이 나와 자기를 자기 용건으로 이끌어 주기를 기다리는 듯했다. 그러나 그 마호가니 철학자는 한 번을 빼고는 따뜻함과 담배 말고는 아무것도 느끼는 기색을 보이지 않았으니—그 한 번이란, 잔을 들이려고 입에서 파이프를 떼면서 자기 이름이 잭 번스비라고 지나가는 말처럼 지독히 퉁명스럽게 한 것이었는데, 이는 대화의 실마리를 거의 열어 주지 못하는 선언이었다—선장은 짤막한 찬사의 머리말로 그의 주의를 청한 뒤, 솔 삼촌이 떠난 전말을, 그것이 자기 자신의 삶과 처지에 일으킨 변화와 더불어 죄다 이야기했다. 그리고 그 꾸러미를 탁자 위에 올려놓는 것으로 끝을 맺었다.\n\n오랜 침묵 끝에, 번스비 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"열까?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n번스비가 다시 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n그리하여 선장은 봉인을 뜯어, 접힌 종이 두 장을 드러냈다. 그는 그 각각의 겉면에 적힌 글을 이렇게 읽었다. \"솔로몬 길스의 마지막 유언장.\" \"네드 커틀에게 보내는 편지.\"\n\n번스비는 그린란드 해안에 눈길을 둔 채, 그 내용에 귀 기울이는 듯이 보였다. 그래서 선장은 목청을 가다듬으려 헛기침을 하고는, 편지를 소리 내어 읽었다.\n\n\"'사랑하는 네드 커틀에게. 내가 서인도제도로 떠나려고 집을 나섰을 때—'\"\n\n여기서 선장은 멈추어, 번스비를 뚫어지게 바라보았는데, 번스비는 그린란드 해안을 한결같이 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"—'사랑하는 내 아이의 소식을 절망적으로 찾아 나섰을 때, 나는 만약 자네가 내 계획을 알게 된다면 그것을 막거나 나와 동행하리라는 것을 알았네. 그래서 나는 그것을 비밀에 부쳤지. 만약 자네가 언젠가 이 편지를 읽는다면, 네드, 나는 십중팔구 죽었을 걸세. 그때면 자네는 옛 친구의 어리석음을 쉬이 용서해 줄 테고, 그가 그런 무모한 항해에 나서며 헤매던 그 불안과 막막함을 헤아려 주겠지. 그러니 그 이야기는 그만하세. 나는 가엾은 내 아이가 언젠가 이 글을 읽거나, 그 솔직한 얼굴을 다시 보여 주어 자네 눈을 기쁘게 해 주리라는 희망은 거의 없네.' 그래, 그래, 더는 없지,\" 커틀 선장이 슬프게 골똘히 생각하며 말했다. \"더는 없어. 거기 그가 누워 있구나, 그 모든 날들을—\"\n\n음악적인 귀를 가진 번스비 씨가 갑자기 \"비스케이 만 깊은 곳에, 오!\" 하고 우렁차게 외쳤는데, 이것이 떠나간 이의 가치에 바치는 적절한 헌사로서 그 선량한 선장을 어찌나 감동시켰던지, 선장은 답례로 그의 손을 흔들며 눈물을 훔치지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래!\" 번스비의 비가가 채광창에서 울리고 떨리기를 그치자, 선장이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"쓰라린 고난을, 오랜 세월 그는 견뎠으니, 자, 그 두루마리를 샅샅이 뒤져 거기서 찾아보세.\"\n\n\"의사들도,\" 번스비가 한마디 했다, \"헛수고였다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그렇고말고,\" 선장이 말했다, \"이삼백 길 물속에서 그들이 무슨 소용이 있겠나!\" 그러고는 다시 편지로 돌아가 읽어 내려갔다. \"'그러나 만약 이것을 열 때 그 애가 곁에 있다면,'\" 선장이 자기도 모르게 주위를 둘러보고는 고개를 저었다. \"'혹은 다른 어느 때에든 이를 알게 된다면,'\" 선장이 다시 고개를 저었다. \"'그 애에게 내 축복이 있기를! 동봉한 서류가 법적으로 제대로 작성되지 않았다 해도, 그것은 거의 문제가 되지 않네. 이해관계가 있는 사람은 자네와 그 애뿐이고, 내 소박한 바람은, 만약 그 애가 살아 있다면 얼마 안 되는 그것을 그 애가 갖는 것이고, 만약 (내가 두려워하듯) 그렇지 않다면 자네가 그것을 갖는 것이네, 네드. 자네가 내 바람을 존중해 주리라는 걸 나는 아네. 그 일로, 또 그 밖에 자네의 온갖 우정으로 하여, 솔로몬 길스에게 하느님의 축복이 있기를.' 번스비!\" 선장이 그에게 엄숙하게 호소하며 말했다, \"이걸 자네는 어떻게 생각하나? 자네는 거기 앉아 있는, 어린 시절부터 줄곧 머리가 깨져 온 사람이고, 그렇게 터진 솔기마다 새 의견이 들어찬 사람이지. 자, 이걸 자네는 어떻게 생각하나?\"\n\n\"만약에,\" 번스비가 평소답지 않게 즉각 대답했다, \"그가 죽었다면, 내 의견으로는 그는 더는 돌아오지 않을 걸세. 만약에 그가 살아 있다면, 내 의견으로는 그는 돌아올 걸세. 내가 그가 돌아온다고 말하는가? 아니지. 어째서 아닌가? 왜냐하면 이 관찰의 방위는 그 적용에 달려 있기 때문일세.\"\n\n\"번스비!\" 그 저명한 친구의 의견에서 무언가를 알아내는 데 겪는 어려움의 막대함에 비례하여 그 가치를 매긴 듯한 커틀 선장이 말했다. \"번스비,\" 선장이 감탄으로 완전히 어리둥절해져서 말했다, \"자네는 머릿속 무게를 가볍게도 지고 다니는구먼, 그게 나 같은 톤수의 사람은 금세 가라앉히고도 남을 텐데 말이야. 하지만 이 유언에 관해서라면, 나는 그 재산에 손을 댈 생각이 조금도 없네—천만의 말씀이지!—다만 더 정당한 임자를 위해 그것을 지켜 둘 뿐이고, 나는 여전히 그 정당한 임자인 솔 길스가 살아서 돌아오기를 바라네. 그가 아무런 소식도 보내오지 않은 게 이상하긴 하지만 말이야. 자, 자네 의견은 어떤가, 번스비, 이 서류들을 다시 챙겨 넣고, 겉면에 이를 아무 날 존 번스비와 에드워드 커틀이 보는 앞에서 열었노라고 표시해 두는 것에 대해서?\"\n\n번스비가 그린란드 해안에서든 다른 어디서든 이 제안에 아무런 이의를 발견하지 못했으므로, 그것은 실행에 옮겨졌다. 그리하여 그 위대한 인물은 잠시 눈을 현재로 가져와, 그 특유의 겸손함으로 대문자 사용은 일체 삼간 채, 겉장에 자기 자필 서명을 적어 넣었다. 커틀 선장도 자기의 왼손잡이 서명을 덧붙인 다음, 꾸러미를 철제 금고에 넣어 잠그고는, 손님에게 한 잔 더 타고 파이프를 한 대 더 피우라고 청했다. 그리고 자기도 똑같이 하면서, 가엾은 늙은 기구 제작자의 있음직한 운명을 두고 난롯가에서 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n그런데 이제 어찌나 압도적이고 끔찍한 놀라움이 닥쳐왔던지, 번스비가 곁에 있어 주지 않았더라면 커틀 선장은 그 아래 가라앉아, 그 운명의 시각부터 영영 끝장난 사람이 되고 말았을 것이다.\n\n선장이, 그런 손님을 들이는 만족 속에서나마, 어떻게 문을 닫기만 하고 잠그지는 않았는지—그가 그런 태만을 범한 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다—그것은 영영 한낱 추측의 문제로, 혹은 운명을 향한 막연한 비난으로 남을 수밖에 없는 수수께끼 가운데 하나다. 그러나 바로 그 잠그지 않은 문으로, 이 고요한 순간에, 그 무시무시한 맥스팅거가 거실 안으로 돌진해 들어왔으니, 알렉산더 맥스팅거를 어머니의 두 팔에 안고, 혼란과 복수를 (줄리아나 맥스팅거와, 그 귀여운 아이의 오라비이자 자기 어린 시절 놀이판에서 흔히 차울리로 통하던 찰스 맥스팅거는 말할 것도 없이) 뒤에 거느린 채였다. 그녀가 어찌나 빠르고도 소리 없이, 마치 동인도 부두 근방에서 몰아치는 한 줄기 바람처럼 들이닥쳤던지, 커틀 선장은 명상에 잠겨 짓고 있던 그 평온한 얼굴이 공포와 경악의 얼굴로 바뀌기도 전에, 어느새 자기가 그녀를 바라보며 앉아 있는 바로 그 와중에 처해 있는 자신을 발견했다.\n\n그러나 커틀 선장은 자기 불행의 전모를 깨닫는 순간, 자기 보존 본능이 도주의 시도를 명했다. 거실에서 가파른 지하실 계단으로 이어지는 그 작은 문으로 돌진하여, 선장은 마치 멍이며 타박상 따위는 아랑곳 않고 오직 땅속 깊은 곳으로 몸을 숨기려는 사람처럼, 머리부터 들이밀며 그 계단으로 돌진해 갔다. 이 용감한 분투로 그는 아마 성공했을 테지만, 줄리아나와 차울리의 애정 어린 성향이 문제였다. 그 둘이 그의 다리에 매달려—그 사랑스러운 아이들이 한 다리씩 붙잡고서—구슬프게 울부짖으며 그를 자기네 친구라고 우겨 댔던 것이다. 그러는 사이, 알렉산더 맥스팅거를 미리 거꾸로 뒤집어 잰 따귀 세례의 사정거리 안에 들여놓고, 그런 다음 독자가 그를 처음 보았던 그대로 식히려고 앉혀 두지 않고서는 어떤 중대한 행동에도 결코 착수하는 법이 없는 맥스팅거 부인은, 이번에는 마치 복수의 여신들에게 바치는 제물이라도 되는 양 그 엄숙한 의식을 거행했다. 그러고는 그 제물을 바닥에 내려놓고, 끼어든 번스비에게 할퀴기를 위협하는 듯한 결연한 목적의 기세로 선장에게 덤벼들었다.\n\n두 손위 맥스팅거 아이의 울음소리와, 어린 알렉산더의 통곡—그는 그 동화 같은 존재의 시기 중 절반 동안 얼굴이 시퍼렜으니, 가히 얼룩덜룩한 유년기를 보냈다 할 만했다—이 어우러져 이 습격을 한층 더 무시무시하게 만들었다. 그러나 다시 정적이 깃들고, 선장이 심한 식은땀을 흘리며 맥스팅거 부인을 얌전히 바라보며 서 있게 되었을 때, 그 공포는 절정에 달했다.\n\n\"오, 커틀 선장님, 커틀 선장님!\" 맥스팅거 부인이 턱을 빳빳이 굳히고, 그것을—만약 그 성별의 약함만 아니었다면 그녀의 주먹이라고 묘사했을 만한 것과 더불어—흔들어 대며 말했다. \"오, 커틀 선장님, 커틀 선장님, 감히 내 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보면서 그 자리에서 거꾸러지지 않을 수가 있어요!\"\n\n도무지 대담해 보이지는 않는 선장이 힘없이 \"침착하게!\" 하고 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"오, 내가 당신을 내 지붕 아래 들였을 때 나는 약하고 잘 믿는 바보였어요, 커틀 선장님, 그랬다고요!\" 맥스팅거 부인이 외쳤다. \"내가 그 사람에게 퍼부은 은혜며, 내 아이들을 마치 그 사람이 제 친아비라도 되는 양 그 사람을 사랑하고 존경하도록 길러 낸 것을 생각하면! 우리 동네에 가정부든 하숙인이든, 내가 그 사람 때문에, 또 그 사람의 퍼마시기며 처마시기 때문에 돈을 잃었다는 걸 모르는 사람이 하나도 없는데 말이에요\"—맥스팅거 부인은 어떤 뜻을 표현하기 위해서라기보다는 두운과 가중을 노려 마지막 말을 썼다—\"또 모두가 하나같이, 부지런한 여자한테, 제 어린 식솔들을 위해 일찍 일어나고 늦게까지 일하는 여자한테 폐를 끼친다고, 또 그 사람의 온갖 퍼마시기며 처마시기에도 불구하고, 내가 그 사람한테 쏟은 정성과 수고가 얼마나 대단했던지, 누구든 마음만 먹으면 그 마룻바닥이나 계단 어느 한 군데에서 저녁밥은 물론이고 그래요, 차까지도 먹을 수 있을 만큼 내 누추한 집을 깨끗이 건사해 놓은 여자한테 폐를 끼친다고, 부끄러운 줄 알라고 외쳐 댔을 때 말이에요!\"\n\n맥스팅거 부인은 숨을 고르려고 멈추었다. 그리고 커틀 선장의 처마시기를 이렇게 두 번째로 멋들어지게 끌어들인 의기양양함에 그녀의 얼굴이 상기되었다.\n\n\"그런데 그 사람은 달아나아아아 버렸어요!\" 맥스팅거 부인이 마지막 음절을 길게 늘여 외치는 바람에, 가엾은 선장은 자기 자신을 사내들 가운데 가장 비열한 자로 여기게 되었다. \"그러고는 일 년 내내 코빼기도 안 비쳐요! 여자 하나를 피해서요! 그 사람 양심이라는 게 고작 이래요! 그 여자를 떳떳이 마아아안날 용기는 없는 거죠\"—다시 길게 늘인 음절—\"그저 흉악범처럼 슬그머니 달아나는 거예요. 글쎄, 만약 우리 이 아기가,\" 맥스팅거 부인이 갑자기 빠르게 말했다, \"달아날 생각을 품기라도 한다면, 나는 그 애가 온통 채찍 자국으로 뒤덮일 때까지 어미로서 내 의무를 다할 거예요!\"\n\n어린 알렉산더는 이것을 머잖아 이행될 확실한 약속으로 해석하고는, 두려움과 슬픔에 겨워 나뒹굴며 바닥에 누워, 제 신발 밑창을 드러낸 채 어찌나 귀가 멍멍하도록 울부짖었던지, 맥스팅거 부인은 그를 두 팔에 안아 올릴 수밖에 없었고, 거기서 그가 거듭 울음을 터뜨릴 때마다 그의 이를 죄다 흔들어 빼놓을 듯한 흔들기로 이따금 그를 달랬다.\n\n\"커틀 선장이라는 이 작자가 참 잘난 사내라니까요,\" 맥스팅거 부인이 선장의 이름 첫 음절에 날카롭게 힘을 주며 말했다, \"그런 사람을 위해 애를 태우고—잠을 설치고—기절까지 하고—심지어 죽었다고 여기고—그 사람 소식을 캐물으며 미친 여자처럼 온 도시를 헤매고 다녔다니! 오, 참 잘난 사내야! 하 하 하 하! 그 사람이야말로 그 모든 수고와 마음고생을 받아 마땅하고, 그보다 더한 것도 받아 마땅하지요. 그까짓 건 아무것도 아니에요, 천만에! 하 하 하 하! 커틀 선장님,\" 맥스팅거 부인이 목소리와 태도가 심하게 표변하며 말했다, \"당신이 집으로 돌아올 건지 알고 싶군요.\"\n\n겁에 질린 선장은 마치 모자를 도로 써서 자수하는 것밖에는 달리 도리가 없다고 여기는 듯, 자기 모자 속을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"커틀 선장님,\" 맥스팅거 부인이 똑같이 단호한 태도로 되풀이했다, \"당신이 집으로 돌아올 건지 알고 싶다고요, 선생.\"\n\n선장은 갈 채비가 자못 되어 있는 듯했으나, \"그 일로 그렇게 큰 소동을 피우지는 말자\"는 취지의 말을 힘없이 내비쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그래,\" 번스비가 달래는 어조로 말했다. \"잠깐 멈추쇼, 아주머니, 멈추라고!\"\n\n\"실례지만, 당신은 대체 누구셔요!\" 맥스팅거 부인이 정숙한 도도함으로 응수했다. \"당신은 브리그 플레이스 9번지에 하숙한 적이 있나요, 선생? 내 기억력이 시원찮을지는 몰라도, 그 점에선 아니라고 봐요. 내 앞에 9번지에 졸슨 부인이라는 사람이 살았는데, 어쩌면 당신이 나를 그 여자로 착각하나 보군요. 당신의 그 허물없는 태도를 내가 설명할 길은 그것밖에 없네요, 선생.\"\n\n\"자, 자, 아주머니, 멈추쇼, 멈춰!\" 번스비가 말했다.\n\n커틀 선장은 깨어 있는 두 눈으로 그것이 행해지는 것을 보면서도, 그 위대한 인물이 하는 일이라 해도 도무지 믿기지 않았다. 그러나 번스비는 대담하게 다가가, 그 텁수룩한 푸른 팔을 맥스팅거 부인의 허리에 두르고는, 그 일을 해내는 마법 같은 솜씨로, 그리고 그 몇 마디 말로—그는 더는 말하지 않았다—그녀를 어찌나 부드럽게 누그러뜨렸던지, 그녀는 잠시 그를 바라보더니 눈물에 녹아들며, 이제는 어린아이조차 자기를 이겨먹을 수 있을 만큼 기가 푹 꺾였노라고 말했다.\n\n말문이 막히고 완전히 얼이 빠진 채, 선장은 번스비가 이 가차 없는 여인을 차츰차츰 구슬려 가게로 데려가고, 럼주와 물과 양초를 가지러 돌아왔다가 그것들을 그녀에게 가져다주며, 단 한마디도 입에 올리는 기색 없이 그녀를 달래는 것을 지켜보았다. 이윽고 번스비가 도선사 외투를 걸친 채 안을 들여다보며 말했다, \"커틀, 내가 집까지 호위선 노릇을 해 주려 가네.\" 그러자 커틀 선장은, 차라리 자기가 브리그 플레이스로 무사히 호송되도록 직접 쇠고랑을 차는 편이 덜 당황스러웠을 만큼 어리둥절해하며, 맥스팅거 부인을 선두로 그 가족이 평화롭게 줄지어 빠져나가는 것을 보았다. 그는 가까스로 양철통을 내려, 옛 총애를 받던 줄리아나 맥스팅거와, 자기가 천성적으로 항해사 체격이라는 명분으로 그에게 권리를 주장하던 차울리의 손에 몰래 돈을 좀 쥐여 줄 틈밖에 없었다. 그러고 나자 항해사 인형은 그들 모두에게 버림받았다. 번스비는 자기가 빈틈없이 일을 처리하고, 배에 오르기 전에 다시 네드 커틀을 불러 보겠노라고 속삭이고는, 일행 가운데 마지막 사람으로서 자기 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다.\n\n작은 거실로 돌아와 홀로 남은 자신을 발견했을 때, 선장은 처음에는 자기가 잠결에 걸어다니고 있는 것은 아닐까, 아니면 피와 살을 지닌 한 가족이 아니라 환영에 시달린 것은 아닐까 하는 어수선한 생각에 사로잡혔다. 뒤이어 코셔스 클라라호의 사령관에 대한 한없는 신뢰와 헤아릴 수 없는 찬탄이 밀려와, 선장을 경이로운 황홀경에 빠뜨렸다.\n\n그러나 시간이 흐르고 번스비가 다시 나타나지 않자, 선장은 또 다른 종류의 불편한 의구심을 품기 시작했다. 번스비가 교묘히 브리그 플레이스로 꾀여 가, 거기서 자기 친구를 대신할 인질로 단단히 붙잡혀 있는 것은 아닐까. 그렇다면 명예를 아는 사내로서 선장은 자기 자신의 자유를 희생하여 그를 풀어 주는 것이 마땅할 터였다. 혹은 그가 맥스팅거 부인에게 공격당해 패하고는, 그 낭패를 당한 뒤라 차마 모습을 드러내기가 부끄러운 것은 아닐까. 혹은 맥스팅거 부인이 그 변덕스러운 성미로 마음을 고쳐먹고 다시 항해사 인형으로 쳐들어오려고 발길을 돌렸는데, 번스비가 지름길로 그녀를 안내하는 척하며 시내의 황량하고 외진 곳들 사이에서 그 가족을 따돌리려 애쓰고 있는 것은 아닐까. 무엇보다도, 만약 맥스팅거 가족이든 번스비든 아무 소식도 더 듣지 못하게 된다면—이런 놀랍고 예측 불가능한 사건들의 얽힘 속에서라면 능히 그럴 법도 했다—자기, 커틀 선장이 어떻게 처신하는 것이 마땅할 것인가.\n\n그는 지칠 때까지 이 모든 것을 곱씹었으나, 여전히 번스비는 오지 않았다. 그는 언제든 잠자리에 들 수 있도록 계산대 밑에 잠자리를 다 마련해 두었으나, 여전히 번스비는 오지 않았다. 마침내 선장이, 적어도 그날 밤만은 그를 단념하고 옷을 벗기 시작했을 때, 다가오는 바퀴 소리가 들리더니, 문 앞에서 멈추고는, 뒤이어 번스비의 외침이 들려왔다.\n\n선장은 맥스팅거 부인을 떨쳐 낼 수 없어, 마차에 태워 도로 데려온 것은 아닐까 하는 생각에 몸서리쳤다.\n\n그러나 아니었다. 번스비는 커다란 상자 하나 말고는 아무것도 동반하지 않았는데, 그것을 제 손으로 가게 안으로 끌어들이고는, 다 끌어들이자마자 그 위에 걸터앉았다. 커틀 선장은 그것이 맥스팅거 부인의 집에 두고 온 자기 궤짝임을 알아보았고, 양초를 손에 든 채 번스비를 더 주의 깊게 살펴보고는, 그가 돛을 셋이나 펄럭이고 있다고, 쉬운 말로 하자면 취해 있다고 믿었다. 그러나 그것을 확신하기는 어려웠으니, 그 사령관은 멀쩡할 때조차 얼굴에 아무런 표정의 흔적이 없는 사람이었다.\n\n\"커틀,\" 사령관이 궤짝에서 내려와 뚜껑을 열며 말했다, \"이게 자네 살림살이인가?\"\n\n커틀 선장은 안을 들여다보고 자기 물건임을 확인했다.\n\n\"꽤 팽팽하고 말끔하게 챙겨 왔지, 응, 동지?\" 번스비가 말했다.\n\n고마워하면서도 어리둥절한 선장이 그의 손을 움켜쥐고, 자기의 놀란 심정을 담은 대답을 막 쏟아 내려는데, 번스비가 손목을 한번 홱 비틀어 빠져나가고는, 그 빙빙 도는 눈으로 윙크를 해 보이려고 애쓰는 듯했다. 그러나 그 상태에서 그 시도가 빚어낸 유일한 결과란 그가 하마터면 균형을 잃을 뻔한 것뿐이었다. 그런 다음 그는 불쑥 문을 열고, 코셔스 클라라호에 다시 합류하려고 전속력으로 쏜살같이 사라졌으니—이는 자기가 한 건 했다고 여길 때마다 그가 으레 보이는 습관으로 짐작되었다.\n\n번스비가 자주 찾아오는 것을 즐기는 성미가 아니었으므로, 커틀 선장은 이튿날, 혹은 그가 자기의 너그러운 뜻을 그런 식으로 알려 오기 전까지는, 그것이 아니라면 얼마간의 시간이 흐를 때까지는 그에게 가거나 사람을 보내지 않기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 선장은 이튿날 아침 자기의 고독한 생활을 다시 시작했고, 여러 날의 아침과 한낮과 밤에 걸쳐 늙은 솔 길스에 대해, 또 그를 두고 번스비가 내놓은 견해에 대해, 또 그가 돌아오리라는 희망에 대해 깊이 생각했다. 그런 생각을 많이 한 끝에 커틀 선장의 희망은 더욱 굳건해졌다. 그리고 그는 문간에서 기구 제작자를 기다리며—이제 그 묘한 자유 속에서 그렇게 해 볼 엄두를 냈으니—그 희망과 자기 자신을 다독였고, 그가 뜻밖에 집에 돌아올 경우에 대비해 그의 의자를 제자리에 놓고 작은 거실을 예전 그대로 정돈해 두었다. 또한 그는 사려 깊게도, 노인이 돌아왔을 때 충격받지 않도록, 학생 시절 월터의 어떤 작은 초상화를 늘 걸려 있던 못에서 내려 두었다. 선장은 또 이따금 그가 바로 어느 날 돌아오리라는 예감을 품기도 했다. 그리고 어느 특별한 일요일에는 너무도 자신만만한 나머지 저녁밥을 갑절로 차리라고 시키기까지 했다. 그러나 늙은 솔로몬은 오지 않았다. 그리고 이웃들은 여전히, 번들거리는 모자를 쓴 그 뱃사람이 저녁이면 가게 문간에 서서 거리를 위아래로 두리번거리는 것을 눈여겨보았다.\n\n# CHAPTER XL. Domestic Relations\n\n돔비 씨 같은 기질의 사내가, 자신이 스스로에게 불러일으킨 그런 정신과 맞부딪쳤을 때 그 거만하고 가혹한 성미가 누그러진다거나, 그가 갑옷처럼 두르고 살아온 차갑고 단단한 자존심이 도도한 멸시며 반항과 끊임없이 충돌함으로써 한층 유연해진다는 것은, 사물의 이치에 맞지 않는 일이었다. 그런 천성이 짊어진 저주란—그것이 제 안에 품고 있는 무거운 응보의 주된 부분이란—이런 것이다. 곧 굴종과 양보가 그 악한 성질을 부풀려 그것이 자라나는 양식이 되는 한편, 저항과, 그 가혹한 요구에 대한 의문 제기 또한 그것을 못지않게 키운다는 것. 그 안의 악은 정반대의 것들 속에서도 똑같이 성장과 번식의 수단을 찾아낸다. 그것은 단것에서도 쓴것에서도 지지와 생명을 끌어낸다. 떠받들리든 인정받지 못하든, 그것은 여전히 자기가 옥좌를 차지한 가슴을 노예로 삼는다. 그리고 숭배받든 거부당하든, 어두운 우화 속의 악마만큼이나 모진 주인이다.\n\n첫 번째 아내를 대할 때, 돔비 씨는 그 차갑고 드높은 오만 속에서, 거의 스스로를 그렇다고 여기던 저 초연한 존재처럼 처신했다. 그녀가 그를 처음 보았을 때 그는 \"돔비 씨\"였고, 그녀가 죽었을 때에도 그는 \"돔비 씨\"였다. 그는 결혼 생활 내내 자신의 위대함을 내세웠고, 그녀는 온순하게 그것을 인정했다. 그는 옥좌 꼭대기에 자신의 멀찍한 위엄의 자리를 지켰고, 그녀는 그 가장 낮은 계단에 자신의 비천한 자리를 지켰다. 그렇게 자신의 단 하나의 관념에 외로이 매여 사는 것이 그에게 얼마나 큰 도움이 되었던가. 그는 두 번째 아내의 그 도도한 성품이 자신의 것에 더해지리라고—자신의 것에 녹아들어 자신의 위대함을 한층 드높여 주리라고—상상했었다. 그는 에디스의 도도함이 자기 도도함에 복종하는 가운데, 자신이 어느 때보다도 더 거만해지는 모습을 그려 보았다. 그는 그것이 자기에게 맞서 떨쳐 일어날 가능성 따위는 단 한 번도 마음에 두지 않았다. 그런데 이제, 일상의 매 걸음과 매 굽이마다 그것이 자기 앞길에 솟아올라, 차갑고 반항적이며 경멸에 찬 얼굴을 자기에게 고정시키는 것을 발견하자, 그의 이 자존심은 그 충격 앞에 시들거나 고개를 떨어뜨리기는커녕 새로운 싹을 틔워, 어느 때보다도 더 응축되고 격렬해졌으며, 더 음울하고 뚱하고 성가시며 굽힘 없는 것이 되었다.\n\n그런 갑옷을 두른 자 또한, 늘 또 다른 무거운 응보를 지니고 다니는 법이다. 그것은 회유와 사랑과 신뢰를 막아 내고, 바깥에서 오는 온갖 부드러운 공감, 온갖 믿음, 온갖 다정함, 온갖 부드러운 감정을 막아 내는 데에는 끄떡없다. 그러나 자존심에 깊이 박히는 칼날에는, 강철 앞의 맨가슴만큼이나 무방비하다. 그리고 거기에는, 다른 어떤 상처에도—아니, 설령 자존심 그 자체의 갑옷 두른 손이 무장 해제되어 쓰러진 더 약한 자존심에 입힌 상처에도—뒤따르지 않는 그런 괴로운 곪음이 사무쳐 든다.\n\n그런 상처가 바로 그의 것이었다. 그는 옛 방의 고독 속에서 그 상처를 날카롭게 느꼈다. 그는 이제 다시 그곳으로 자주 물러나 길고 외로운 시간을 보내기 시작했다. 늘 거만하고 강력하면서도, 자기가 가장 강하고자 하는 곳에서는 늘 무력해지고 굴욕당하는 것이 그의 운명인 듯했다. 그 운명을 이루어 내도록 정해진 자는 누구인 듯했던가?\n\n누구? 자기가 아들의 마음을 사로잡았듯 자기 아내의 마음을 사로잡을 수 있었던 자는 누구였던가? 그가 어두운 구석에 앉아 있을 때, 그 새로운 승리를 그에게 보여 준 자는 누구였던가? 그의 온갖 수단으로도 하지 못한 일을 사소한 한마디로 해낸 자는 누구였던가? 그의 사랑도 존중도 주목도 받지 못하면서, 그런 것을 받았던 이들이 죽어 갈 때 무성히 자라나 아름다워진 자는 누구였던가? 어미 잃은 갓난아이였던 그 애를, 자기가 그 애를 미워하게 될까 두려워하며 일종의 불안과 함께 자주 곁눈질하던 바로 그 아이가 아니라면 누구일 수 있었겠는가. 그리고 그 불길한 예감은 이루어졌으니, 그는 정녕 마음속으로 그 애를 미워했던 것이다.\n\n그렇다, 그는 그것을 증오로 삼고자 했고, 또 증오로 만들었다. 비록 신부를 데리고 귀가하던 그 잊을 수 없는 밤에 그 애가 그 앞에 나타났을 때의 그 빛의 불꽃 몇 점이 이따금 여전히 그 애 주위에 어른거렸음에도. 이제 그는 그 애가 아름답다는 것을 알았다. 그 애가 우아하고 사람의 마음을 끈다는 것, 그리고 그 애가 여인됨의 환한 새벽녘에 자신에게 하나의 놀라움으로 다가왔다는 것을 그는 부정하지 않았다. 그러나 그는 이것조차 그 애에게 불리하게 돌렸다. 자신이 모든 마음으로부터 소외되었다는 둔한 자각과, 평생 밀쳐 내 온 것에 대한 막연한 갈망 속에서, 이 불행한 사내는 뚱하고 병적인 곱씹음에 잠겨, 자기 권리와 억울함을 일그러진 그림으로 그려 내고는, 그것으로 그 애에 맞서 스스로를 정당화했다. 그 애가 자기에게 더 어울리는 사람이 될 듯할수록, 그는 그 애의 의무와 복종에 대해 미리 권리를 앞당겨 주장하려는 마음이 더욱 커졌다. 대체 그 애가 언제 그에게 의무와 복종을 보인 적이 있던가? 그 애가 우아하게 꾸며 준 것이 그의 삶이던가—아니면 에디스의 삶이던가? 그 애가 그 매력을 처음 드러낸 상대가 그였던가—아니면 에디스였던가? 아니, 그와 그 애는 그 애가 태어난 이래로 아비와 자식 같았던 적이 한 번도 없었다! 그들은 늘 서로 소원했다. 그 애는 사사건건 어디서나 그의 뜻을 거슬렀다. 이제 그 애는 그에게 맞서 한패가 되어 있었다. 바로 그 애의 아름다움이 그에게 완강하던 성품들을 누그러뜨려, 부자연스러운 승리로 그를 모욕했다.\n\n이 모든 것 가운데에는, 비록 자신이 불리한 처지에 놓였다는 데서 이기적으로 일깨워진 것이라 할지라도, 그 애가 그의 삶을 어떤 것으로 만들어 줄 수 있었을지에 견주어 자기 가슴속에서 깨어난 어떤 감정의 웅얼거림이 있었는지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 자기 자존심의 바다가 출렁이는 소리로 그 멀리서 울리는 천둥을 잠재웠다. 그는 자기 자존심 외에는 아무것도 견뎌 내려 하지 않았다. 그리고 모순과 비참함과 스스로 자초한 고통의 더미인 그 자존심 속에서, 그는 그 애를 미워했다.\n\n자기를 사로잡은 그 침울하고 완고하며 뚱한 악령에 맞서, 그의 아내는 종류가 다른 자기 자존심을 온 힘을 다해 들이댔다. 그들은 결코 함께 행복한 삶을 이끌어 갈 수 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그런 요소들의 고집스럽고 단호한 싸움보다 그 삶을 더 불행하게 만들 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그의 자존심은 자신의 장엄한 우위를 지키고 그녀로 하여금 그것을 인정하게 만드는 데에 매여 있었다. 그녀는 끝까지, 차라리 죽도록 고문당할지언정, 차분하고 굽힘 없는 멸시의 도도한 시선만을 그에게 돌릴 뿐이었을 것이다. 에디스에게서 그런 인정을 받겠다니! 그는 그녀가 그의 손을 잡는 그 최고의 영예에 이르기까지 어떤 폭풍과 몸부림을 거쳐 떠밀려 왔는지 거의 알지 못했다. 그가 자기를 아내라 부르도록 허락했을 때 그녀가 얼마나 많은 것을 양보했다고 여겼는지 그는 거의 알지 못했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 그녀에게 자기가 최고임을 보여 주기로 작정했다. 그의 뜻 외에는 어떤 뜻도 있어서는 안 되었다. 그는 그녀가 도도하기를 바랐지만, 그것은 자기에 맞서서가 아니라 자기를 위한 도도함이어야 했다. 그가 홀로 앉아 점점 더 굳어 가는 동안, 그는 그녀가 외출했다가 돌아오는 소리를, 마치 자기가 그녀의 마부라도 되는 양 그의 호불호나 만족이나 불만 따위에는 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고 런던 사교 생활의 굴레를 밟는 소리를 자주 듣곤 했다. 그녀의 차갑고 도도한 무관심은—그 자신의, 누구도 넘볼 수 없던 속성을 빼앗긴 것이었기에—다른 어떤 종류의 대우보다도 더 그를 찔렀다. 그리하여 그는 그녀를 자신의 장엄하고 위엄 있는 뜻에 굽히게 만들겠다고 결심했다.\n\n그는 오랫동안 이런 생각들과 씨름하던 끝에, 어느 날 밤 그녀가 늦게 귀가하는 소리를 듣고서 그녀를 그녀 자신의 방으로 찾아갔다. 그녀는 화사한 드레스 차림으로 혼자 있었고, 방금 막 어머니의 방에서 나온 참이었다. 그가 그녀에게 다가갔을 때 그녀의 얼굴은 우울하고 수심에 잠겨 있었다. 그러나 그 얼굴은 문간의 그를 알아챘다. 그녀 앞에 놓인 거울을 흘끗 본 그는, 그가 너무도 잘 아는, 찌푸린 이마와 어두워진 아름다움을 마치 액자 속 그림처럼 즉시 보았기 때문이다.\n\n\"돔비 부인,\" 그가 들어서며 말했다, \"당신과 몇 마디 나눌 수 있도록 양해를 구해야겠소.\"\n\n\"내일 하시죠,\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"지금만 한 때가 없소, 부인,\" 그가 응수했다. \"당신은 자기 처지를 잘못 알고 있소. 나는 내 때를 내가 고르는 데 익숙하지, 남이 골라 주는 데 익숙하지 않소. 당신은 내가 누구이고 어떤 사람인지 거의 모르는 모양이오, 돔비 부인.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 그녀가 대답했다, \"당신을 아주 잘 알고 있는 것 같은데요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그녀는 그를 바라보았고, 금과 보석으로 반짝이는 하얀 팔을 부풀어 오른 가슴 위에 포갠 채 눈길을 돌렸다.\n\n그녀가 덜 아름답고, 그 차가운 침착함 속에서 덜 위엄 있었더라면, 그의 더없는 자존심을 꿰뚫고 들어오는 그 불리함의 감각을 그에게 심어 줄 힘은 없었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀에게는 그 힘이 있었고, 그는 그것을 날카롭게 느꼈다. 그는 방 안을 둘러보았다. 화려한 몸치장의 도구들이며 사치스러운 옷가지들이 여기저기 흩어진 채 함부로 방치되어 있는 것을 보았다. 그저 변덕이나 부주의에서가 아니라(적어도 그는 그렇게 생각했다), 값비싼 물건들에 대한 한결같고 도도한 무시에서 그러한 것을. 그리고 그는 그것을 점점 더 절감했다. 화관, 깃털 장식, 보석, 레이스, 비단과 공단—어디를 보든, 그는 멸시받고 내팽개쳐지고 하찮게 여겨진 부를 보았다. 그녀의 가슴 위에서 초조하게 오르내리는 그 다이아몬드들조차—결혼 선물이었다—목을 두른 사슬을 끊고 그녀가 밟고 다닐 수 있는 바닥으로 굴러떨어지려고 헐떡이는 것만 같았다.\n\n그는 자신의 불리함을 느꼈고, 또 그것을 드러냈다. 이 빛깔의 풍요로움과 관능적인 광채 속에서 엄숙하고 어색하게, 그 도도한 안주인을 향해 어색하고 거북하게—그 안주인의 사람을 밀쳐 내는 아름다움을, 그 모든 것이 마치 수많은 거울 조각 속처럼 그의 사방에 되비추어 내고 있었다—그는 당혹과 어색함을 의식했다. 그녀의 멸시에 찬 침착함에 보탬이 되는 것이라면 무엇이든 그를 짜증나게 하지 않을 수 없었다. 자기 자신에게 짜증이 나고 화가 난 채, 그는 자리에 앉아, 조금도 나아지지 않은 기분으로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"돔비 부인, 우리 둘 사이에 어떤 이해에 이르는 것이 매우 필요하오. 당신의 행실이 나는 마음에 들지 않소, 부인.\"\n\n그녀는 그저 다시 한번 그를 흘끗 보고는, 다시 눈길을 돌렸다. 그러나 한 시간을 떠들었어도 그보다 적게 말하지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n\"다시 말하지만, 돔비 부인, 마음에 들지 않소. 나는 이미 그것이 시정되기를 요청할 기회를 가진 바 있소. 이제 나는 그것을 강력히 요구하는 바요.\"\n\n\"당신은 첫 번째 항의에 알맞은 때를 골랐고, 두 번째 항의에는 알맞은 태도와 알맞은 말을 골라 쓰시는군요. 강력히 요구하신다! 저에게요!\"\n\n\"부인,\" 돔비 씨가 더없이 비위 상하게 하는 위엄을 부리며 말했다, \"나는 당신을 내 아내로 삼았소. 당신은 내 이름을 지니고 있소. 당신은 내 지위와 내 명성에 결부되어 있소. 세상 사람들이 대체로 당신이 그 결연으로 영예를 입었다고 여기는 쪽이라고까지는 말하지 않겠소. 그러나 나는 내 인척과 내게 딸린 자들에게 '강력히 요구하는' 데 익숙하다는 것은 말해 두겠소.\"\n\n\"저는 그 둘 중 어느 쪽으로 여기시겠어요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"아마도 나는 내 아내가 그 두 가지 성격을 모두 함께 지녀야 한다고—혹은 함께 지니고 있으며, 어쩔 도리가 없다고—생각하는지도 모르겠소, 돔비 부인.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 한결같은 눈길을 떨구고, 떨리는 입술을 다물었다. 그는 그녀의 가슴이 두근거리는 것을 보았고, 그녀의 얼굴이 붉어졌다가 하얗게 질리는 것을 보았다. 이 모든 것을 그는 알 수 있었고 또 알았다. 그러나 한마디 말이 그녀의 마음 깊은 구석에서 그녀를 잠잠하게 하려고 속삭이고 있다는 것은, 그리고 그 말이 플로렌스라는 것은, 그는 알 수 없었다.\n\n눈먼 바보가, 벼랑을 향해 돌진하고 있었구나! 그는 그녀가 자기를 두려워하며 서 있다고 생각했다.\n\n\"당신은 너무 돈이 많이 드오, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"당신은 낭비가 심하오. 당신은 나에게 쓸모없고, 사실 전체적으로 보아 나에게 불쾌한, 그런 종류의 교제를 가꾸는 데에 막대한 돈을—혹은 대부분의 신사들 주머니에서라면 막대한 액수가 될 돈을—허비하고 있소. 나는 이 모든 면에서 전면적인 변화가 있어야 한다고 강력히 요구하는 바요. 운명이 당신의 처분에 맡겨 둔 만큼의 그런 재력의 십분의 일이라도 가지게 된 그 신기함 속에서, 부인들이 흔히 갑작스러운 극단으로 치닫는다는 것은 나도 알고 있소. 그런 극단은 이미 차고 넘쳤소. 이제는 그레인저 부인의 사뭇 다른 경험들이 돔비 부인의 교훈이 되어 주기를 바라오.\"\n\n여전히 고정된 눈길, 떨리는 입술, 두근거리는 가슴, 이제 진홍빛이었다가 하얗게 질리는 얼굴. 그리고 여전히 그 깊은 속삭임, 플로렌스, 플로렌스가, 그녀의 심장 박동 속에서 그녀에게 말을 걸고 있었다.\n\n그녀에게서 이런 변화를 보면서 그의 그 거만한 자만심은 한껏 부풀어 올랐다. 그녀가 지난날 자기를 멸시했던 것과, 자기가 방금 느낀 그 불리함의 감각으로도 모자라, 이제 그녀가 (그가 그렇게 받아들이기로는) 굴복하는 것까지 더해져, 그것은 그의 가슴이 담아 두기에 너무도 거대해져서 온갖 한계를 터뜨려 버렸다. 아니, 그의 드높은 뜻과 의향에 누가 오래 맞설 수 있단 말인가! 그는 그녀를 정복하기로 결심했고, 자, 보라, 이 꼴이렷다!\n\n\"또한 부탁하건대, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 군주의 명령 같은 어조로 말했다, \"내가 떠받들어지고 복종받아야 할 사람이라는 것을 분명히 이해해 주시오. 세상 앞에서 명백한 경의의 표시와 고백이 내게 있어야 한다는 것을 말이오, 부인. 나는 이것에 익숙하오. 나는 그것을 내 권리로서 요구하오. 한마디로, 나는 그것을 가져야겠소. 나는 그것을, 당신에게 닥쳐온 세속적 출세에 대한 부당하지 않은 보답이라고 여기오. 그리고 그것이 당신에게 요구되는 것에도, 당신이 그것을 행하는 것에도, 아무도 놀라지 않으리라 믿소.—나에게—나에게 말이오!\" 그가 힘주어 덧붙였다.\n\n그녀에게서는 한마디도 없었다. 그녀에게서는 아무 변화도 없었다. 그녀의 눈은 그에게 고정되어 있었다.\n\n\"나는 당신 어머니에게서 들었소, 돔비 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 위압적인 무게를 실어 말했다, \"당신도 분명 알고 있는 것을 말이오. 다름 아니라, 어머니의 건강에는 브라이턴이 권장된다는 것 말이오. 카커 씨가 친절을 베풀어 주었소.\"\n\n그녀가 갑자기 변했다. 그녀의 얼굴과 가슴이, 마치 성난 석양의 붉은 빛이 거기 던져지기라도 한 듯 달아올랐다. 그 변화를 놓치지 않고 거기에 자기 나름의 해석을 붙이며, 돔비 씨가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"카커 씨가 친절을 베풀어, 거기 내려가 한동안 지낼 집을 한 채 마련해 주었소. 식솔들이 런던으로 돌아오면, 나는 그 집안을 더 잘 관리하기 위해 내가 필요하다고 여기는 조치들을 취할 것이오. 그중 하나는, 브라이턴에서 (그것이 성사된다면) 매우 점잖게 영락한 사람 하나를, 곧 전에 우리 집안에서 막중한 자리를 맡았던 핍친 부인이라는 이를 가정부로 고용하는 일이 될 것이오. 이 정도 규모의 집안이 그저 명목상으로만 관리되어서는, 돔비 부인, 유능한 우두머리가 필요한 법이오.\"\n\n그가 이 말에 이르기 전에 그녀는 이미 자세를 바꾸어, 이제는—여전히 그를 한결같이 바라보면서—제 팔에 두른 팔찌를 빙글빙글 돌리며 앉아 있었다. 여자다운 가벼운 손길로 돌리는 것이 아니라, 매끄러운 살갗 위로 누르고 끌어당겨, 마침내 그 하얀 팔에 붉은 줄이 드러날 때까지였다.\n\n\"내가 보아하니,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다—\"그리고 이로써 지금 당신에게 말해 둘 필요가 있다고 여기는 것을 끝맺겠소, 돔비 부인—내가 방금 보아하니, 부인, 카커 씨에 대한 내 언급이 묘한 태도로 받아들여졌소. 언젠가 내가 우연히, 그 믿을 만한 대리인 앞에서, 당신이 내 손님들을 맞이하는 방식에 대해 내가 품은 반대를 당신에게 지적했을 때, 당신은 그가 그 자리에 있는 것에 이의를 제기하는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼소. 당신은 그 이의를 극복해야 할 것이오, 부인, 그리고 십중팔구는 그와 비슷한 여러 자리에서 그것에 익숙해져야 할 것이오. 당신 손안에 있는 처방, 곧 나에게 불평의 빌미를 주지 않는 처방을 택하지 않는다면 말이오. 카커 씨는,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. 방금 본 그 감정의 동요 끝에, 그는 자기의 도도한 아내를 굴복시킬 이 수단을 매우 소중히 여기게 되었고, 또 어쩌면 그 신사에게 새롭고 의기양양한 모습으로 자신의 힘을 과시하고 싶은 마음이 얼마간 있었던 것이다. \"카커 씨가 내 신임을 받고 있으니, 돔비 부인, 그가 그 정도까지는 당신의 신임도 충분히 받을 수 있을 것이오. 바라건대, 돔비 부인,\" 그가 잠시 뒤 말을 이었다. 그 잠깐 사이에, 점점 더 거만해지면서 그는 자기 생각을 더욱 다듬어 두었다. \"나로서는 카커 씨에게 당신에 대한 그 어떤 반대나 항의의 전갈을 맡길 필요가 없게 되기를 바라오. 그러나 내가 베풀 수 있는 최고의 영예를 수여한 부인을 상대로 자주 사소한 언쟁을 벌이는 것은 내 지위와 명성을 깎아내리는 일일 터이므로, 필요하다고 여겨지면 나는 거리낌 없이 그의 도움을 빌릴 것이오.\"\n\n\"자, 이제,\" 그는 자신의 도덕적 장엄함 속에서 일어서며—어느 때보다도 더 뻣뻣하고 더 뚫고 들어갈 수 없는 사내가 되어 일어서며—생각했다. \"그녀는 나를, 그리고 나의 결심을 알았겠지.\"\n\n그토록 팔찌를 눌러 대던 손이 그녀의 가슴 위에 무겁게 얹혔다. 그러나 그녀는 변함없는 얼굴로 여전히 그를 바라보았고, 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"기다리세요! 제발! 당신에게 할 말이 있어요.\"\n\n어찌하여 그녀는 그러지 못했던가. 그리고 그녀로 하여금 몇 분 동안 그러지 못하게 만든 그 내면의 몸부림은 무엇이었던가. 그동안 그녀가 제 얼굴에 가한 그 강한 억제 속에서, 그 얼굴은 어떤 조각상처럼이나 굳어 있었다—굽힘도 굽히지 않음도, 호의도 증오도, 자존심도 비굴함도 없이, 오직 무언가를 찾는 응시만으로 그를 바라보면서.\n\n\"제가 언제 당신더러 제 손을 구하라고 꾄 적이 있나요? 제가 언제 당신을 얻으려고 무슨 술책이라도 부린 적이 있나요? 당신이 저를 쫓아다닐 때, 제가 결혼한 뒤보다 당신에게 더 살갑게 군 적이 있나요? 제가 지금의 저와 다른 모습으로 당신을 대한 적이 있나요?\"\n\n\"그런 논의에 들어가는 것은, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"전혀 불필요하오.\"\n\n\"제가 당신을 사랑한다고 생각하셨나요? 제가 사랑하지 않는다는 걸 아셨나요? 당신은, 이 사람아! 제 마음 따위에 마음 쓴 적이라도 있나요, 아니면 그 하찮은 것을 얻겠다고 스스로 마음먹은 적이라도 있나요? 우리 거래에 사랑인 척하는 가련한 시늉이라도 있었던가요? 당신 쪽에든, 제 쪽에든?\"\n\n\"이런 질문들은,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"모두 핵심에서 한참 벗어난 것이오, 부인.\"\n\n그녀는 그가 떠나지 못하도록 그와 문 사이로 옮겨 서서, 자기 위풍당당한 자태를 한껏 곧추세우고는, 여전히 그를 한결같이 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신은 그 질문들 하나하나에 답을 하고 있어요. 제가 말하기도 전에 당신은 제게 답하고 있어요, 다 보여요. 어찌 안 그럴 수 있겠어요. 저만큼이나 그 비참한 진실을 잘 아는 당신인데. 자, 말해 보세요. 만일 제가 당신을 헌신적으로 사랑한다 한들, 방금 당신이 요구한 대로 제 모든 의지와 존재를 당신에게 바치는 것보다 더 할 수 있는 게 있겠어요? 만일 제 마음이 순결하고 한 번도 시험받지 않은 것이며 당신이 그 마음의 우상이라 한들, 당신이 그 이상을 요구할 수 있겠어요. 그 이상을 가질 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"아마 그럴 수 없겠지,\" 그가 차갑게 응수했다.\n\n\"당신은 제가 얼마나 다른지 알고 있어요. 당신은 지금 제가 당신을 바라보는 것을 보고 있고, 제 얼굴에 어려 숨 쉬는 당신을 향한 정열의 뜨거움을 읽을 수 있을 거예요.\" 도도한 입술의 단 한 번의 비틀림도, 검은 눈동자의 단 한 번의 번득임도 없이, 오직 그 똑같이 골똘하고 무언가를 찾는 눈길만이 이 말들과 함께했다. \"당신은 제 지난 내력을 다 알아요. 당신은 제 어머니에 대해 말했죠. 당신은 저를 깎아내리거나, 굽히거나, 꺾어서 순종과 복종에 이르게 할 수 있다고 생각하나요?\"\n\n돔비 씨는, 자기가 만 파운드를 마련할 수 있다고 생각하느냐는 물음에 지을 법한 그런 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"만일 여기 무언가 예사롭지 않은 것이 있다면,\" 그녀가, 그 미동도 없고 그 밖에는 아무런 표정도 없는 응시에서 한순간도 움츠러들지 않은 채, 이마 앞에서 손을 살짝 움직이며 말했다, \"여기 예사롭지 않은 감정들이 있다는 것을 제가 아는 것처럼,\" 가슴 위에 누르고 있던 손을 들어 올렸다가 다시 무겁게 내려놓으며, \"제가 지금 당신에게 하려는 호소에 예사로운 의미가 담겨 있지 않다는 것을 헤아려 주세요. 그래요, 제가 하려고 하니까요.\" 그녀는 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 무언가에 즉각 대답이라도 하듯 이렇게 말했다. \"당신에게 호소하려고요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 빳빳한 크라바트가 사각거리고 바스락거리도록 턱을 조금 거들먹거리며 숙이고는, 그 호소를 들으려고 가까이 있던 소파에 앉았다.\n\n\"만일 당신이, 제가 지금 이런 천성을 지닌 사람이라고 믿어 줄 수 있다면,\"—그는 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 반짝이는 것을 본 것 같았고, 비록 그녀의 뺨에는 한 방울도 떨어지지 않았고 그녀가 여전히 한결같이 그를 바라보았음에도, 자기가 그녀에게서 그 눈물을 끌어냈다고 흐뭇하게 생각했다—\"곧 제가 지금 하는 이 말을 저 자신조차 거의 믿을 수 없게 만들 그런 천성을, 그러니까 제 남편이 된 어떤 남자에게라도, 그러나 그 누구보다도 당신에게 하는 이 말을 거의 믿을 수 없게 만들 그런 천성을 지닌 사람이라고 믿어 준다면, 당신은 어쩌면 이 말에 더 무게를 둘 수 있을 거예요. 우리가 향해 가고 있고, 또 닥칠지도 모를 그 어두운 끝에서, 우리는 우리 자신만 끌어들이는 게 아니라(그건 그리 대단치 않을지도 모르죠) 다른 이들까지 끌어들이게 될 거예요.\"\n\n다른 이들이라! 그는 그 말이 누구를 가리키는지 알았고, 무겁게 미간을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"저는 다른 이들을 위해 당신에게 말하는 거예요. 또한 당신 자신을 위해, 그리고 저를 위해서요. 결혼한 이래로 당신은 저에게 거만하게 굴었고, 저는 똑같이 갚아 주었어요. 당신은 저에게, 그리고 우리 주위의 모든 이에게, 날마다 시시각각, 당신이 저와 맺어진 것으로 제가 우아해지고 격이 높아졌다고 여긴다는 것을 보여 주었어요. 저는 그렇게 생각하지 않고, 그것 또한 보여 주었죠. 당신은 우리 둘이 각자의 길을 가는 것을 이해하지 못하거나, (당신 힘이 미치는 한) 그러도록 둘 작정이 없는 모양이에요. 그러면서 그 대신 저에게서, 당신이 결코 얻지 못할 그런 경의를 기대하고 있죠.\"\n\n비록 그녀의 얼굴은 여전히 그대로였지만, 그녀가 들이쉰 바로 그 숨결 속에 이 \"결코\"라는 말의 강조된 확언이 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"저는 당신에게 아무런 다정함도 느끼지 않아요. 그건 당신도 알죠. 제가 그것을 느끼거나 느낄 수 있다 한들, 당신은 거기에 조금도 마음 쓰지 않을 거예요. 당신이 저에게 아무런 다정함도 느끼지 않는다는 것 또한 저는 잘 알고요. 그러나 우리는 함께 묶여 있고, 우리를 묶고 있는 그 매듭 속에는, 제가 말했듯, 다른 이들도 함께 얽혀 있어요. 우리는 둘 다 죽어야 하고, 우리는 둘 다 이미 죽은 이와, 저마다 한 어린 자식으로 이어져 있어요. 우리 서로 삼가도록 해요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는, 마치 오! 고작 이게 전부였단 말인가, 하고 말하려는 듯, 길게 숨을 들이쉬었다.\n\n\"이 말들과, 거기에 담긴 그 의미를 저에게서 사 갈 수 있는 부는 세상에 없어요,\" 그녀가, 그를 지켜보면서 한층 창백해지는 한편 그 진지함 속에서 두 눈은 더욱 빛을 더하며 말을 이었다. \"한번 부질없는 숨결로 던져 버리고 나면, 어떤 부나 권력으로도 그것들을 되불러 올 수는 없어요. 저는 진심이에요. 저는 이 말들을 저울질해 보았고, 제가 떠맡는 것에 충실할 거예요. 만일 당신이 당신 쪽에서 삼가겠다고 약속한다면, 저도 제 쪽에서 삼가겠다고 약속하겠어요. 우리는 더없이 불행한 한 쌍이에요. 서로 다른 까닭에서, 결혼을 축복하거나 정당화하는 온갖 정서가 우리 안에서 뿌리째 뽑혀 버린 한 쌍이죠. 그러나 시간이 흐르면, 우리 사이에 얼마간의 우정이나, 얼마간 서로 어울림이 생겨날지도 몰라요. 만일 당신도 그 노력을 기울인다면, 저도 그렇게 되기를 바라 보려고 애쓰겠어요. 그리고 저는, 제가 젊음이나 한창때를 써 온 것보다 더 낫고 더 행복하게 노년을 쓰게 되기를 바라보겠어요.\"\n\n처음부터 끝까지 그녀는 오르지도 내리지도 않는, 낮고 담담한 목소리로 말했다. 말을 멈추면서, 그녀는 스스로를 그토록 정념 없고 또렷하게 다스리려고 동원했던 그 손은 떨어뜨렸으나, 그토록 한결같이 그를 살펴 온 그 눈만은 거두지 않았다.\n\n\"부인,\" 돔비 씨가 더없는 위엄을 부리며 말했다, \"나는 이런 별스러운 종류의 어떤 제안도 받아들일 수 없소.\"\n\n그녀는 여전히, 조금의 변화도 없이 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"나는,\" 돔비 씨가 말하면서 일어섰다, \"당신이 내 의견과 기대를 이미 알고 있는 사안을 두고 당신과 시간을 끌거나 흥정하는 데에 동의할 수 없소, 돔비 부인. 나는 내 최후통첩을 밝혔으니, 부인, 그것에 매우 진지하게 주의를 기울여 주기를 요청할 따름이오.\"\n\n그 얼굴이 예전의 표정으로, 한층 짙어진 강렬함으로 변하는 것을 보다니! 그 눈이 무언가 비천하고 역겨운 대상에게서 그러듯 떨구어지는 것을 보다니! 그 도도한 이마에 불이 켜지는 것을 보다니! 멸시와 분노와 격분과 혐오가 솟아나고, 창백하고 텅 빈 진지함이 안개처럼 사라지는 것을 보다니! 그는 보지 않을 도리가 없었다. 비록 보면서 경악했음에도.\n\n\"가세요!\" 그녀가 도도한 손으로 문 쪽을 가리키며 말했다. \"우리의 처음이자 마지막인 흉금을 트는 일은 끝났어요. 그 무엇으로도 우리는 이제부터의 우리보다 더 서로 남남이 될 수는 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"나는 내 정당한 길을 갈 것이오, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"어떤 일반적인 열변에도 흔들리지 않고 말이오, 부디 그리 아시오.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 등을 돌리고는, 아무 대꾸 없이 거울 앞에 앉았다.\n\n\"나는 당신의 한층 나아진 의무감과, 더 올바른 감정과, 더 나은 성찰을 믿겠소, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 단 한마디도 대답하지 않았다. 그는 거울 속에서, 마치 자기가 벽에 붙은 보이지 않는 거미나 바닥에 기는 딱정벌레라도 된 양—아니, 차라리 그녀가 마지막으로 그에게서 등을 돌렸을 때 보였다가 짓밟혀, 땅바닥의 치욕스럽고 죽은 벌레들 틈에 잊힌 그 거미나 딱정벌레라도 된 양—그를 조금이라도 거들떠보는 기색을 더는 보지 못했다.\n\n그는 문으로 나가면서, 환히 밝혀진 사치스러운 방을, 사방에 펼쳐진 아름답고 반짝이는 물건들을, 거울 앞에 앉은 화려한 드레스 차림의 에디스의 자태를, 그리고 거울이 그에게 비추어 보인 에디스의 얼굴을 돌아보았다. 그러고는 이 모든 것의 생생한 그림과, (이따금 사람의 머릿속에 떠오르곤 하는) 종잡을 수 없고 까닭 모를 어떤 상념을—다음번에 이것들을 볼 때 모두 어떤 모습일까 하는 상념을—마음에 담은 채, 사색에 잠기던 자기 옛 방으로 향했다.\n\n그 밖에는, 돔비 씨는 매우 과묵했고, 매우 위엄 있었으며, 자기 목적을 관철하리라고 매우 자신만만했다. 그리고 그는 줄곧 그러했다.\n\n그는 식솔들을 따라 브라이턴에 갈 작정은 아니었다. 그러나 하루 이틀 뒤 출발하던 날 아침, 그는 식사 자리에서 클레오파트라에게 자기가 곧 내려갈 것으로 기대해도 좋다고 너그럽게 알려 주었다. 클레오파트라를 어디든 몸에 좋다고 권장되는 곳으로 데려가는 데에는 지체할 겨를이 없었다. 사실 그녀는 기우는 듯했고, 흙으로, 흙답게 돌아가고 있었으니까.\n\n이렇다 할 두 번째 발작을 겪지는 않았지만, 그 늙은 여인은 첫 번째 발작에서 회복하던 것이 도리어 뒷걸음질 친 듯 보였다. 그녀는 더 야위고 쪼그라들었으며, 그 치매가 더 종잡을 수 없어지고, 마음과 기억 속에서 더 기묘한 혼란을 빚어냈다. 이 마지막 병증의 여러 징후 가운데 하나로, 그녀는 살아 있는 사위와 죽은 사위, 두 사위의 이름을 뒤섞는 버릇에 빠져들었다. 그래서 대개 돔비 씨를 \"그레인지비\"라거나 \"돔버\"라거나, 또는 그 둘을 가리지 않고 함께 불렀다.\n\n그러나 그녀는 젊었다, 여전히 매우 젊었다. 그리고 그 젊음을 뽐내며 그녀는 떠나기 전 아침 식사 자리에, 특별히 맞춘 새 보닛에, 늙은 갓난아이의 것처럼 수놓고 장식 끈을 두른 여행복 차림으로 나타났다. 이제는 그녀에게 날아갈 듯한 보닛을 씌우는 일도, 일단 씌워 놓은 보닛을 그 가엾게 끄덕이는 머리 뒤에 제자리에 붙들어 두는 일도 쉽지 않았다. 이번 경우 그것은 늘 한쪽으로 기울어져 있다는 별스러운 효과뿐 아니라, 하녀 플라워스가 끊임없이 그 꼭대기를 톡톡 두드린다는 효과까지 자아냈는데, 플라워스는 그 임무를 수행하느라 아침 식사 내내 뒤편에 시중들고 서 있었다.\n\n\"자, 내 사랑하는 그레인지비,\" 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"너 꼭 약속해 줘야 해,\" 그녀는 어떤 말은 토막 내고 어떤 말은 아예 잘라먹었다, \"아주 빨리 내려오겠다고.\"\n\n\"방금 말씀드렸습니다만, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 큰 소리로 힘들여 응수했다, \"하루 이틀 안에 가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙구나, 돔버!\"\n\n이때, 부인들에게 작별 인사를 하러 온 소령이, 불사의 존재가 지을 법한 초연한 침착함으로 졸중에 걸린 두 눈을 부릅뜨고 스큐턴 부인의 얼굴을 빤히 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 마님, 이 늙은 조에게는 오라고 안 하시는군요!\"\n\n\"수상쩍은 인간 같으니, 저게 누구지?\" 클레오파트라가 혀 짧은 소리로 말했다. 그러나 플라워스가 보닛을 톡 두드린 것이 그녀의 기억을 흔들어 깨운 듯, 그녀가 덧붙였다, \"오! 당신 자신을 말하는 거구려, 이 짓궂은 양반아!\"\n\n\"고약하리만치 이상하군요, 선생,\" 소령이 돔비 씨에게 속삭였다. \"안 좋은 경우예요. 결코 충분히 싸매질 않으셨거든요.\" 소령은 턱까지 단추를 채우고 있었다. \"아니, J. B.가 조라고 하면 늙은 조 백스톡 말고 누구를 가리키겠소—조지프—마님의 종—조 말이오, 마님? 여기! 바로 이 사람이오! 여기 백스톡의 풀무가 있소이다, 마님!\" 소령이 자기 가슴을 쩌렁쩌렁 울리도록 한 대 치며 외쳤다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 에디스—그레인지비—참 별스러운 일이지,\" 클레오파트라가 짜증스럽게 말했다, \"저 소령이—\"\n\n\"백스톡! J. B.!\" 소령이, 그녀가 자기 이름을 더듬는 것을 보고 외쳤다.\n\n\"뭐, 아무려면 어때,\" 클레오파트라가 말했다. \"에디스, 얘야, 너도 알다시피 나는 통 이름을 못 외운다니까—그게 뭐였더라? 오!—참 별스러운 일이야, 그렇게 많은 사람들이 나를 보러 내려오고 싶어 하다니. 나 오래 가는 거 아니야. 돌아올 거라고. 그 사람들도 내가 돌아올 때까지는 분명 기다릴 수 있겠지!\"\n\n클레오파트라는 이렇게 말하면서 식탁을 죽 둘러보았고, 매우 불안해 보였다.\n\n\"손님들은 안 받을 거야—정말이지 손님은 바라지 않아,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"약간의 휴식—그런 종류의 것 말이야—그게 내가 필요로 하는 거라고. 이 마비가 떨어져 나갈 때까지는 어떤 역겨운 짐승들도 나한테 접근해선 안 돼.\" 그러고는 그 교태 부리던 버릇을 으스스하게 되살려 부채로 소령을 톡 치는 시늉을 했지만, 엉뚱하게도 사뭇 다른 방향에 있던 돔비 씨의 찻잔을 엎질러 버렸다.\n\n그러더니 그녀는 위더스를 불러, 자기 방의 사소한 손질 몇 가지에 대해 말을 남겨 두도록 각별히 챙기라고 분부했다. 그것은 자기가 돌아오기 전에 모두 끝나 있어야 하고, 또 자기가 얼마나 빨리 돌아올지 알 수 없으니 당장 착수해야 한다는 것이었다. 약속이 매우 많고, 찾아가 보아야 할 온갖 사람들이 있다면서. 위더스는 합당한 공손함으로 이 지시들을 받아들이고, 그것을 이행하겠노라 보증했다. 그러나 그가 그녀에게서 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러나자, 그는 소령을 묘하게 바라보지 않을 수 없는 듯했고, 소령은 돔비 씨를 묘하게 바라보지 않을 수 없는 듯했으며, 돔비 씨는 클레오파트라를 묘하게 바라보지 않을 수 없는 듯했고, 클레오파트라는 보닛을 한쪽 눈 위로 끄덕이지 않을 수 없는 듯, 또 나이프와 포크를 쓰면서 접시에 달그락거리며 마치 캐스터네츠라도 치는 듯하지 않을 수 없는 듯했다.\n\n오직 에디스만이 식탁의 어느 얼굴에도 눈을 들지 않았고, 어머니가 무슨 말을 하거나 무슨 짓을 해도 결코 당혹스러워하는 기색이 없었다. 그녀는 어머니의 두서없는 이야기에 귀를 기울이거나, 적어도 말을 걸어오면 어머니 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다. 필요할 때면 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디 대답했고, 때로는 어머니가 횡설수설하면 멈추게 하거나, 한 음절의 말로 그 빗나간 생각을 본디 자리로 되돌려 놓았다. 그러나 어머니는, 다른 일에는 아무리 종잡을 수 없어도, 이 한 가지에는 한결같았으니—언제나 딸을 눈여겨본다는 것이었다. 그녀는 그 대리석 같은 고요함과 준엄함을 띤 아름다운 얼굴을, 어떤 때는 일종의 두려운 감탄으로, 어떤 때는 그 얼굴을 미소로 움직여 보려는 실없고 어리석은 노력으로, 어떤 때는 그 얼굴에게 자기가 무시당하고 있다고 여기며 변덕스러운 눈물과 질투 어린 머리 흔듦으로 바라보았다. 늘 그 얼굴을 향한 어떤 끌림과 더불어서였는데, 그 끌림은 그녀의 다른 생각들처럼 출렁이는 법이 없이 한결같이 그녀를 사로잡고 있었다. 에디스에게서 그녀는 때때로 플로렌스에게로 눈을 옮겼다가, 다시 에디스에게로 되돌아오곤 했는데, 그 모양이 자못 거칠었다. 또 때로는 딸의 얼굴에서 벗어나려는 듯 다른 데를 보려고 애쓰기도 했다. 그러나 그 얼굴은 청하지 않는 한 어머니의 얼굴을 찾지 않았고 단 한 번의 눈길로도 어머니를 성가시게 한 적이 없었음에도, 그녀는 다시 그 얼굴로 되돌아오지 않을 수 없는 듯했다.\n\n아침 식사가 끝나자, 스큐턴 부인은 소령의 팔에 소녀처럼 기대는 척했으나 반대쪽에서는 하녀 플라워스가 무겁게 부축하고 뒤에서는 시동 위더스가 떠받친 채로, 자신과 플로렌스와 에디스를 브라이턴으로 데려갈 마차로 안내되었다.\n\n\"그러면 조지프는 정녕 추방당한 겁니까?\" 소령이 보랏빛 얼굴을 마차 발판 너머로 들이밀며 말했다. \"이런, 마님, 클레오파트라께서는 그토록 매정하시어, 충실한 안토니우스 백스톡이 어전에 다가오는 것을 금하신단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"저리 가요!\" 클레오파트라가 말했다. \"당신은 못 견디겠어. 당신이 아주 얌전히 굴면, 내가 돌아왔을 때 만나 줄게.\"\n\n\"조지프에게 희망을 품고 살아도 된다고 전해 주십시오, 마님,\" 소령이 말했다. \"안 그러면 절망 속에 죽을 테니까요.\"\n\n클레오파트라가 몸을 떨며 등을 기댔다. \"에디스, 얘야,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저이에게 전해 줘—\"\n\n\"뭘요?\"\n\n\"저렇게 끔찍한 말들을 한다니까,\" 클레오파트라가 말했다. \"저이는 저렇게 끔찍한 말을 쓴단 말이야!\"\n\n에디스는 소령에게 물러가라는 신호를 보내고, 출발하라는 분부를 내린 뒤, 그 못마땅한 소령을 돔비 씨에게 떠넘겼다. 소령은 휘파람을 불며 돔비 씨에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"내 한마디 하리다, 선생,\" 소령이 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 두 다리를 한껏 벌리고 서서 말했다, \"우리의 어여쁜 벗 한 분이 곤경의 거리로 옮겨 갔습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이오, 소령?\" 돔비 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"내 말인즉슨, 돔비,\" 소령이 응수했다, \"당신이 곧 인척 잃은 고아가 되리란 거지요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 자신을 두고 이렇게 익살맞게 묘사한 것이 어찌나 마뜩잖았던지, 소령은 엄숙함의 표시로 그 말 기침 같은 소리로 끝을 맺었다.\n\n\"이런, 선생,\" 소령이 말했다, \"사실을 감추어 봐야 소용없소. 조는 무뚝뚝한 사람이오, 선생. 그게 그의 천성이지요. 늙은 조시를 받아들이려거든, 있는 그대로 받아들여야 합니다. 그러면 당신은 그가 지독히도 녹슨, 이가 촘촘히 박힌 늙은 줄칼, J. B.라는 줄칼임을 알게 될 거요. 돔비,\" 소령이 말했다, \"당신 부인의 어머니가 떠나가고 계십니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"내 우려하건대,\" 돔비 씨가 꽤나 철학적인 태도로 응수했다, \"스큐턴 부인이 흔들리고 계시지요.\"\n\n\"흔들린다고요, 돔비!\" 소령이 말했다. \"박살 났지요!\"\n\n\"그러나 환경의 변화와,\" 돔비 씨가 말을 이었다, \"보살핌이 있으면 아직 많은 것을 할 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"믿지 마시오, 선생,\" 소령이 응수했다. \"이런, 선생, 그분은 결코 충분히 싸매질 않으셨소. 사람이 싸매지 않으면,\" 소령이 자기 담황색 조끼의 단추 하나를 더 채우며 말했다, \"기댈 것이 아무것도 없는 법이오. 그러나 어떤 사람들은 기어이 죽으려 들지요. 기어이 그러고 마는 거요. 이런, 정말 그런다니까. 고집을 부리는 거지요. 내 한마디 하리다, 돔비, 그것이 화려하지는 않을지도 모르오. 세련되지 않을지도 모르오. 거칠고 투박할지도 모르오. 그러나 진짜배기 옛 잉글랜드 백스톡의 끈기가 조금만 있으면, 선생, 인간이라는 종족에게 세상에 더없는 좋은 일을 해 줄 거외다.\"\n\n이 귀중한 정보를 전한 뒤, 다른 어떤 자질을 갖추었든 못 갖추었든 간에 분명 골수 보수파였고, 정확히 규정된 적이 한 번도 없는 그 \"진짜배기 옛 잉글랜드\"라는 분류에 드는 소령은, 그 가재 같은 눈알과 졸중을 데리고 클럽으로 가서, 거기서 온종일 음식에 켁켁거렸다.\n\n클레오파트라는 어떤 때는 안달복달하다가, 어떤 때는 자기만족에 젖고, 때로는 깨어 있다가, 때로는 잠들고, 어느 때나 어린아이 같은 채로, 그날 밤 브라이턴에 닿아, 여느 때처럼 무너져 내려 침대에 뉘어졌다. 음울한 공상을 부린다면, 마땅히 그래야 할 하녀보다 더 강력한 해골 하나가, 그녀를 위해 그 위에 화사함을 드리우려고 내려진 장밋빛 휘장 곁에서 지켜보고 있는 모습을 그려 볼 법도 했다.\n\n그녀가 날마다 마차를 타고 바람을 쐬어야 하고, 또 날마다 밖에 나가, 가능하면 걷는 것이 중요하다는 것이 의료 권위자들의 고위 회의에서 결정되었다. 에디스는 그녀를 모실 채비가 되어 있었다—언제나, 그 똑같은 기계적인 보살핌과 미동 없는 아름다움으로 그녀를 모실 채비가 되어 있었다—그리고 그들은 둘이서만 마차를 타고 나갔다. 어머니의 병세가 더 나빠진 지금, 에디스는 플로렌스가 있으면 마음이 불편했고, 그래서 입맞춤과 함께 플로렌스에게, 자기들 둘이서만 가는 편이 낫겠다고 말했던 것이다.\n\n스큐턴 부인은 어느 특정한 날, 첫 번째 발작에서 회복하면서 드러났던 그 우유부단하고 까다롭고 질투 어린 기분에 잠겨 있었다. 마차 안에 한동안 말없이 앉아 에디스를 지켜보던 그녀가, 딸의 손을 잡고 정열적으로 입을 맞추었다. 그 손은 내밀어지지도 거두어지지도 않은 채, 그저 그녀가 들어 올리는 대로 맡겨졌다가, 놓아 주자 거의 감각이 없는 것처럼 다시 떨어져 내렸다. 이에 그녀는 훌쩍이고 신음하기 시작하여, 자기가 어떤 어미였는데, 또 어찌 이리 잊혔는가 하고 말했다! 그녀는 마차에서 내린 뒤에도 변덕스러운 간격으로 이 짓을 계속했다. 위더스와 지팡이의 양쪽 부축을 받아 그녀가 절뚝절뚝 걷고, 에디스가 그 곁을 걸으며, 마차가 조금 뒤에서 천천히 따라오는 그때에도.\n\n음산하고 잔뜩 찌푸린, 바람 부는 날이었고, 그들은 다운스의 언덕 위에 나와 있었는데, 그들과 하늘 사이에는 헐벗고 휑한 땅의 비탈뿐이었다. 어머니는 자기 푸념의 단조로움에서 짜증스러운 만족을 느끼며, 여전히 이따금 낮은 목소리로 그것을 되풀이하고 있었고, 딸의 도도한 자태가 그 곁에서 천천히 움직이고 있었다. 그때 그들 앞의 어두운 능선을 넘어 두 형상이 다가오는 것이 보였는데, 멀리서 보니 그것이 어찌나 자기들 둘을 과장되게 흉내 낸 듯하던지, 에디스는 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n그녀가 멈춤과 거의 동시에 그 두 형상도 멈추었다. 그리고 에디스가 보기에 마치 제 어머니의 일그러진 그림자 같던 그 하나가, 손으로 그들 쪽을 가리키며 다른 하나에게 간곡히 무어라 말했다. 그 하나는 되돌아가고 싶어 하는 듯했으나, 다른 하나는—그 안에서 에디스는 자기와 닮은 무언가를, 두려움이 아주 없지는 않은 어떤 예사롭지 않은 느낌으로 그녀를 찌를 만큼 충분히 알아보았다—다가왔다. 그러고는 두 형상이 함께 다가왔다.\n\n이 관찰의 대부분을 그녀는 그들 쪽으로 걸어가면서 해냈으니, 그녀가 멈췄던 것은 한순간이었기 때문이다. 더 가까이서 살펴보니 그들은 시골을 떠도는 이들처럼 초라하게 차려입었고, 젊은 여자 쪽은 뜨개질감인지 그와 비슷한 팔 물건들을 들고 있었으며, 늙은 여자는 빈손으로 힘겹게 걷고 있었다.\n\n그러나 차림새로 보나, 품위로 보나, 아름다움으로 보나 아무리 동떨어져 있을지언정, 에디스는 그 젊은 여자를 여전히 자기 자신과 견주어 보지 않을 수 없었다. 어쩌면 그것은, 아직 그 얼굴이라는 표지에는 쓰여 있지 않을지언정 자기 영혼 속에 도사리고 있음을 그녀가 아는 어떤 흔적들을, 그 여자의 얼굴에서 보았기 때문인지도 몰랐다. 그러나 그 여자가 다가오면서, 시선을 마주 돌려 빛나는 두 눈을 그녀에게 고정시키고, 틀림없이 그녀 자신의 분위기와 풍채를 얼마간 풍기며, 그녀 자신의 생각을 똑같이 되비추는 듯하자, 그녀는 마치 날이 어두워지고 바람이 차가워지기라도 한 듯, 한기가 몸을 타고 기어오르는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n이제 그들은 가까이 다가와 있었다. 늙은 여자는 손을 끈질기게 내밀며, 스큐턴 부인에게 구걸하려고 멈춰 섰다. 젊은 여자도 멈추었고, 그녀와 에디스는 서로의 눈을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"팔 게 무엇이오?\" 에디스가 말했다.\n\n\"이것뿐이에요,\" 그 여자가, 자기 물건들을 쳐다보지도 않고 그것들을 내밀며 응수했다. \"저 자신은 오래전에 팔아 버렸고요.\"\n\n\"마님, 저 애 말을 믿지 마세요,\" 늙은 여자가 스큐턴 부인에게 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"저 애가 하는 말을 믿지 마세요. 저 애는 저렇게 말하는 걸 좋아한답니다. 제 잘난, 못된 딸년이지요. 제가 저 애한테 해 준 게 얼만데, 마님, 저 애가 제게 주는 거라곤 비난뿐이에요. 저것 좀 보세요, 마님, 저 애가 그 눈초리로 제 불쌍한 늙은 어미한테 어떻게 대드는지를요.\"\n\n스큐턴 부인이 떨리는 손으로 지갑을 꺼내, 돈을 좀 찾으려고 다급히 더듬는 동안—다른 늙은 여자가 그것을 게걸스레 지켜보았고, 그 다급함과 노쇠함 속에서 둘의 머리가 거의 맞닿을 지경이었다—에디스가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"당신을 본 적이 있소,\" 늙은 여자를 향해 그녀가 말했다, \"전에.\"\n\n\"네, 마님,\" 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며. \"저 아래 워릭셔에서요. 나무들 사이에서 맞던 그 아침에요. 마님이 제게 아무것도 안 주려 하셨을 때요. 하지만 그 신사분은, 그분은 제게 뭔가를 주셨답니다! 오, 그분께 복을, 그분께 복을 내려 주소서!\" 늙은 여자가 그 뼈만 남은 손을 치켜들고, 제 딸을 향해 소름 끼치게 이를 드러내며 우물거렸다.\n\n\"나를 말려 봐야 소용없다, 에디스!\" 스큐턴 부인이, 딸에게서 나올 반대를 성나게 앞질러 막으며 말했다. \"넌 이 일에 대해 아무것도 몰라. 난 말려도 안 들을 거다. 이 사람은 분명 훌륭한 여자이고 좋은 어미야.\"\n\n\"네, 마님, 그럼요,\" 늙은 여자가 그 탐욕스러운 손을 내밀며 재잘거렸다. \"고맙습니다, 마님. 주님의 복이 함께하시길, 마님. 육 펜스만 더요, 어여쁜 마님, 마님도 좋은 어머니이시니까요.\"\n\n\"그리고 나 역시 가끔은 충분히 불효를 당하지요, 정말이오, 우리 착한 노친네,\" 스큐턴 부인이 훌쩍이며 말했다. \"자! 나랑 악수합시다. 당신은 참 착한 노친네야—그 뭐랄까가 가득한 사람—그런 것 말이야. 당신은 온통 애정이며 기타 등등이지, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 마님!\"\n\n\"그럼, 분명 그럴 테지. 그리고 그 신사 같은 사람 그레인지비도 그렇고. 정말이지 당신과 또 한 번 악수를 해야겠어. 그럼 이제 가도 돼요, 알겠지. 그리고 바라건대,\" 딸을 향해 말하며, \"넌 더 많은 고마움과, 타고난 그 뭐라더라와, 그 나머지 것들을 보여 드리거라—난 도통 이름을 못 외우니—이 착한 노친네가 네게 해 준 것보다 더 나은 어미는 세상에 없었으니 말이다. 가자, 에디스!\"\n\n클레오파트라의 폐허가 훌쩍이며 비틀비틀 걸어가, 눈가에 연지가 있을지 모른다는 것을 조심스레 떠올리며 눈물을 훔치는 동안, 늙은 여자는 우물거리며 제 돈을 세면서 다른 쪽으로 절뚝절뚝 걸어갔다. 에디스와 그 젊은 여자 사이에는 더는 한마디 말도, 다른 어떤 몸짓도 오가지 않았으나, 둘 다 한순간도 서로에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 그들은 지금까지 마주 선 채로 있었는데, 이윽고 에디스가, 마치 꿈에서 깨어나기라도 하듯 천천히 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n\"넌 예쁜 여자야,\" 그녀의 그림자가 그녀의 뒷모습을 바라보며 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 예쁜 얼굴이 우리를 구해 주진 못하지. 그리고 넌 도도한 여자야. 하지만 도도함도 우리를 구해 주진 못해. 우리가 다시 만날 때는, 서로를 알아 둘 필요가 있겠어!\"\n\n# CHAPTER XLI. New Voices in the Waves\n\n모든 것이 늘 그러했듯이 흘러간다. 파도는 제 신비를 거듭거듭 되뇌느라 목이 쉬어 있고, 모래밭에는 먼지가 쌓여 있으며, 바닷새들은 솟구쳐 올랐다 맴돌고, 바람과 구름은 자취 없는 비행에 나선다. 하얀 팔들은 달빛 속에서, 저 멀리 보이지 않는 나라를 향해 손짓한다.\n\n플로렌스는 그토록 슬프게, 그러나 그토록 행복하게 밟았던 옛 자리에 다시 서서, 애틋하고 우수에 잠긴 즐거움을 느끼며, 그 고요한 곳에 있던 동생을 떠올린다. 그곳에서 그와 그녀는 그의 침상 둘레로 물이 솟아오르는 가운데 몇 번이고 몇 번이고 함께 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 이제, 그녀가 그곳에 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있노라니, 바다의 거칠고 나직한 속삭임 속에서 동생의 짧은 이야기가 다시 들려오고, 동생이 한 바로 그 말들이 되풀이되는 것을 듣는다. 그리고 그 이후로—외로운 집에서, 또 그 집이 변모한 화려한 행렬 속에서—자기의 모든 삶과 희망과 슬픔이 그 신비로운 노래의 후렴 속에 한 자락을 차지하고 있음을 깨닫는다.\n\n그리고 멀찍이서 거니는 상냥한 투츠 씨는, 자기가 그토록 흠모하여 여기까지 뒤따라온 그 모습을 애틋하게 바라보면서도 섬세한 마음 탓에 이런 때에 차마 방해하지 못하는데, 그 역시 물결 위에 떠오르는 어린 돔비의 진혼곡을 듣는다. 플로렌스를 찬미하는 그 영원한 마드리갈의 잔잔한 틈새에서 솟았다 가라앉는 그 노래를. 그렇다! 그리고 가엾은 투츠 씨는 어렴풋이나마 깨닫는다. 그 노래가 자기가 더 총명했고 머리가 흐리멍덩하지 않다고 느끼던 시절에 관한 무언가를 말하고 있음을. 그리고 자기가 지금은 둔하고 어리석으며 남의 웃음거리가 되는 것 말고는 별 쓸모가 없지 않은가 두려워질 때 눈에 차오르는 눈물이, 그가 시골에서 (투츠의 비용으로) 라키 보이와의 큰 시합을 대비해 훈련 중인 저 닭대가리 같은 사내가 없는 덕분에 당장은 치킨에 대한 책임에서 풀려나 있다는, 위로가 되는 그 깨우침이 주는 만족을 줄어들게 한다.\n* 치킨: '게임 치킨(Game Chicken)'은 투츠가 고용한 권투 선생을 가리키는 별명이다.\n\n그러나 투츠 씨는 그 노래가 다정한 생각을 그에게 속삭여 줄 때 용기를 얻고, 더디게, 그리고 가는 길에 여러 번 망설이며 멈춰 서면서, 플로렌스에게 다가간다. 더듬거리고 얼굴을 붉히며, 투츠 씨는 그녀 가까이 다가오자 놀란 척한다. 그러고는 (그녀가 타고 온 마차를 런던에서부터 한 치도 빠짐없이 바짝 뒤따라왔으며, 그 바퀴가 일으킨 먼지에 숨이 막히는 것조차 사랑스러웠으면서) 평생 이렇게 놀란 적이 없었다고 말한다.\n\n\"게다가 다이오지니스도 데려오셨군요, 돔비 아가씨!\" 투츠 씨가 말한다. 그토록 다정하고 스스럼없이 내밀어진 그 작은 손의 감촉에 온몸이 짜릿하다.\n\n다이오지니스가 거기 있는 것은 의심할 바 없고, 투츠 씨가 그 녀석을 주목할 이유가 있는 것도 의심할 바 없다. 녀석이 곧장 투츠 씨의 다리로 달려들어, 마치 몽타르지의 그 개라도 되는 양 필사적으로 덤벼들다 제풀에 나뒹굴기 때문이다. 그러나 녀석은 사랑스러운 여주인에게 제지당한다.\n\n* 몽타르지의 개: 주인을 살해한 자를 끝까지 물어 정의를 밝혔다는 중세 프랑스 전설 속의 충견.\n\n\"엎드려, 다이, 엎드려. 누가 우리를 처음 친구로 만들어 줬는지 기억 안 나니, 다이? 부끄러운 줄 알아야지!\"\n오! 다이가 사랑스러운 뺨을 그녀의 손에 비비고, 달아났다 되돌아오고, 그녀 주위를 빙빙 돌며 짖고, 지나가는 누구에게든 곤두박질치듯 달려들어 제 헌신을 내보일 만도 하다. 투츠 씨도 누구에게든 곤두박질치듯 달려들고 싶은 심정이다. 군인 신사 하나가 지나가는데, 투츠 씨는 그를 향해 전속력으로 돌진하는 것보다 더 좋은 일이 없을 것만 같다.\n\n\"다이오지니스는 제 고향 공기 속에 있는 셈이지요, 그렇지 않습니까, 돔비 아가씨?\" 투츠 씨가 말한다.\n\n플로렌스가 고마운 미소로 동의한다.\n\n\"돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠 씨가 말한다, \"실례입니다만, 혹시 블림버 댁까지 걸어가고 싶으시다면, 제가—제가 그리로 가는 길이라서요.\"\n\n플로렌스는 말없이 투츠 씨의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼고, 둘은 다이오지니스를 앞세운 채 함께 걸어간다. 투츠 씨의 다리는 후들거린다. 그리고 그는 화려하게 차려입었으면서도, 버지스 상회의 그 걸작품들이 몸에 맞지 않는 것 같고 주름이 잡힌 것 같은 느낌이 들어, 차라리 그 가장 번쩍이는 부츠를 신고 올 걸 그랬다고 아쉬워한다.\n\n블림버 박사의 집은, 겉모습이 여전히 학구적이고 사색에 잠긴 듯한 분위기를 풍긴다. 그리고 저 위에는, 그녀가 창백한 얼굴을 찾아 올려다보곤 하던 창문이 있다. 그 창백한 얼굴은 그녀를 보면 환해졌고, 여위어 가던 작은 손은 그녀가 지나갈 때 입맞춤을 보내며 흔들리곤 했다. 문을 열어 주는 것은 여전히 그 눈이 약한 젊은이인데, 투츠 씨를 보자 짓는 그 얼빠진 웃음은 나약한 성품을 그대로 형상화한 듯하다. 그들은 박사의 서재로 안내되는데, 거기서는 눈먼 호메로스와 미네르바가 예전처럼 그들을 맞이하고, 현관의 큰 시계가 차분히 똑딱거리는 소리가 들린다. 그리고 지구본들은 늘 놓이던 자리에 그대로 서 있어서, 마치 세상도 가만히 멈춰 있고, 그 안의 어떤 것도, 만물을 굴러가게 하는 동시에 모든 것을 땅으로 불러들이는 보편의 법칙을 따라 스러진 적이 없는 것만 같다.\n\n그리고 여기, 학구적인 다리를 한 블림버 박사가 있다. 그리고 여기, 하늘색 모자를 쓴 블림버 부인이 있다. 그리고 여기, 모래빛의 작은 곱슬머리 줄과 반짝이는 안경을 쓴 코닐리어가, 여전히 죽은 언어들의 무덤 속에서 묘지기처럼 일하고 있다. 여기, 그가 학교의 \"새 학생\"으로서 쓸쓸하고 낯설게 앉아 있던 그 책상이 있다. 그리고 저쪽에서, 옛 방에서 옛 원칙대로 옛 생활을 영위하는 옛 학생들의 멀찍한 웅성거림이 들려온다!\n\n\"투츠,\" 블림버 박사가 말한다, \"자네를 보니 참 반갑네, 투츠.\"\n\n투츠 씨가 답례로 빙긋 웃는다.\n\n\"또한 이렇게 좋은 분과 함께 있는 자네를 보니 반갑군, 투츠,\" 블림버 박사가 말한다.\n\n투츠 씨는, 얼굴이 새빨개진 채로, 돔비 아가씨를 우연히 만났으며, 돔비 아가씨가 자기처럼 옛 장소를 보고 싶어 해서 함께 오게 되었노라고 설명한다.\n\n\"틀림없이,\" 블림버 박사가 말한다, \"우리 어린 벗들 사이를 거닐어 보고 싶으실 테지요, 돔비 아가씨. 한때는 모두 자네의 동문들이었지, 투츠. 우리의 작은 주랑(柱廊)에는 투츠 군이 떠난 이래 새 제자가 들어온 일이 없는 것 같구려, 여보,\" 블림버 박사가 코닐리어에게 말한다.\n\n\"비더스톤만 빼면요,\" 코닐리어가 응수한다.\n\n\"아, 그렇지,\" 박사가 말한다. \"비더스톤은 투츠 군에게는 새 얼굴이지.\"\n\n플로렌스에게도 거의 새 얼굴이다. 교실에서 비더스톤은—더 이상 핍친 부인 댁의 비더스톤 도련님이 아닌—깃과 넥타이를 두르고 시계를 차고 있는 모습이다. 그러나 어떤 불길한 벵골의 별 아래 태어난 비더스톤은 잉크투성이가 되어 있다. 그리고 그의 사전은 끊임없이 들춰진 탓에 잔뜩 부풀어 올라 닫히지가 않고, 정말이지 이렇게 시달리는 걸 도무지 못 견디겠다는 듯이 하품을 하듯 입을 벌리고 있다. 그 주인 비더스톤도 블림버 박사의 최고 압박을 받아 그렇게 하품을 하는데, 다만 비더스톤의 하품에는 악의와 으르렁거림이 서려 있어서, 그가 \"늙은 블림버\"를 인도에서 붙잡을 수 있었으면 좋겠다고 말하는 것을 사람들이 들은 적이 있다. 그러면 블림버는 머지않아 자기(비더스톤)네 막일꾼 몇 명에게 들려 내륙으로 끌려가서, 교살단에게 넘겨지게 될 거다. 그가 장담할 수 있다는 거다.\n\n브리그스는 여전히 지식의 맷돌을 돌리고 있다. 토저도 그렇고, 존슨도 그렇고, 나머지 전부가 그렇다. 나이가 더 든 학생들은 주로, 어렸을 때 알던 것을 죄다 엄청난 수고를 들여 잊어버리는 일에 매달려 있다. 모두가 여전히 예전처럼 공손하고 창백하다. 그리고 그들 사이에서, 학사 학위를 지닌 피더 씨가, 그 뼈마디 굵은 손과 빳빳한 머리칼로, 여전히 열심히 일하고 있다. 마침 지금은 헤로도토스 음전(音栓)을 당겨 놓은 채이고, 그의 다른 음전들은 등 뒤 선반 위에 놓여 있다.\n\n* 음전: 피더가 기계처럼 정해진 교과만 되풀이하는 것을 풍금 음전에 빗댄 표현.\n이 진중한 젊은 신사들 사이에서조차, 해방된 투츠의 방문은 엄청난 파문을 일으킨다. 그는 루비콘강을 건너 다시는 돌아오지 않겠노라 맹세한 사람으로서 일종의 경외심으로 떠받들어지고, 그의 옷의 재단과 장신구의 유행을 두고 손으로 입을 가린 채 수군거림이 오간다. 투츠 씨와 같은 시절을 보내지 않은 그 신경질적인 비더스톤은, 어린 학생들 앞에서 투츠를 경멸하는 척하며, 자기는 더 잘 안다고, 그가 벵골에 와서 그딴 짓을 하는 걸 한번 보고 싶다고, 거기서는 자기 어머니가 어느 라자의 발받침에서 꺼낸, 자기 소유의 에메랄드를 갖고 있다고 말한다. 어디 해 봐!\n\n플로렌스의 모습에도 어리둥절한 감정이 일깨워지는데, 모든 젊은 신사가 그녀를 보자마자 다시금 사랑에 빠진다. 앞서 말한 그 신경질적인 비더스톤만은 예외인데, 그는 청개구리 심사로 사랑에 빠지기를 거부한다. 투츠 씨에 대한 시커먼 질투심이 일고, 브리그스는 그자가 결국 그렇게까지 나이가 많은 건 아니라는 의견을 내놓는다. 그러나 이 깎아내리는 빈정거림은, 투츠 씨가 학사 학위를 지닌 피더 씨에게 큰 소리로 \"어떻게 지내나, 피더?\" 하고 인사하며 오늘 베드퍼드에서 함께 저녁을 들자고 청하는 통에 금세 무너지고 만다. 그런 위업을 행한 권리로 보아, 그는 마음만 먹으면 아무 이의 없이 올드 파 노릇이라도 할 수 있을 것이다.\n\n* 올드 파: 152세까지 살았다고 전해지는 전설적 장수 노인 토머스 파.\n악수가 무수히 오가고, 절도 무수히 오가며, 젊은 신사들은 저마다 돔비 아가씨의 호감 속에서 투츠를 끌어내리고 싶어 안달이다. 그러다가, 투츠 씨가 자기 옛 책상에 빙긋 웃음을 한 번 선사하고 나서, 플로렌스와 그는 블림버 부인 및 코닐리어와 함께 물러난다. 그리고 맨 마지막에 나와 문을 닫는 블림버 박사가 그들 뒤에서 이렇게 말하는 것이 들린다. \"여러분, 이제 우리는 학업을 재개합시다.\" 그것이, 그리고 그 밖에 거의 아무것도 아닌 것이, 박사가 바다더러 하는 말이라고 듣는 것이요, 평생토록 들어 온 말이다.\n\n이윽고 플로렌스는 슬그머니 빠져나와 블림버 부인 및 코닐리어와 함께 위층의 옛 침실로 올라간다. 거기서는 자기도 다른 누구도 필요치 않다고 느끼는 투츠 씨는, 서재 문간에 서서 박사와 이야기를 나눈다. 아니, 오히려 박사가 자기에게 하는 이야기를 듣고 서서, 자기가 어쩌다 이 서재를 대단한 성소로, 또 둥글게 휜 다리를 한 박사를, 마치 성직자풍의 피아노포르테처럼 생긴 그 박사를 어찌하여 무시무시한 사람이라고 여겼던가 의아해한다. 플로렌스가 곧 내려와 작별을 고한다. 투츠 씨도 작별을 고한다. 그리고 그동안 내내 눈이 약한 젊은이를 사정없이 괴롭혀 온 다이오지니스는 문밖으로 튀어 나가, 절벽 아래를 향해 기쁜 도전의 짖음을 짖어 댄다. 한편 밀리어와 박사의 또 다른 여자 하인이 위층 창밖으로 내다보며, \"저 투츠 양반 좀 봐\" 하고 웃고는, 돔비 아가씨에 대해서 이렇게 말한다. \"근데 정말이지, 안 그래—저 아가씨 자기 동생 닮지 않았어, 좀 더 예쁘기만 하고?\"\n\n플로렌스가 내려왔을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 눈물 자국이 있는 것을 본 투츠 씨는, 미칠 듯이 걱정스럽고 불안해져서, 처음에는 자기가 방문을 제안한 것이 잘못이 아니었나 두려워한다. 그러나 그녀가 다시 그곳에 와 보게 되어 무척 기쁘다고 말하고, 바닷가를 따라 걸으면서 그 모든 일에 대해 아주 명랑하게 이야기하는 통에, 그는 곧 마음을 놓는다. 거기 그 목소리들과, 그녀의 그 감미로운 목소리가 어우러진 가운데, 돔비 씨의 집 가까이에 이르러 투츠 씨가 그녀와 헤어져야 할 때가 되자, 그는 자유의지라고는 한 조각도 남지 않을 만큼 사로잡혀 버린다. 그녀가 작별로 손을 내밀자, 그는 그 손을 차마 놓지 못한다.\n\n\"돔비 아가씨, 실례입니다만,\" 투츠 씨가 슬픈 안절부절못하며 말한다, \"혹시 제가—제가—해도 괜찮으시다면—\"\n\n플로렌스의 미소 띤, 아무것도 모르는 표정에 그는 그만 딱 멈추어 버린다.\n\n\"제가—해도 괜찮으시다면—그러니까, 만일 제가, 그게 주제넘은 짓이라고 여기지 않으신다면요, 돔비 아가씨, 만일 제가—아무런 격려도 받지 않은 채, 만일 제가 희망을 품어도 된다면 말입니다, 그러니까,\" 투츠 씨가 말한다.\n\n플로렌스가 미심쩍은 듯 그를 바라본다.\n\n\"돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠 씨가 말한다. 이제 발을 뺄 수 없게 되었다고 느끼면서. \"저는 정말이지 아가씨를 흠모하는 그런 상태라서, 제 자신을 어찌해야 할지 모르겠습니다. 저는 가장 한심한 가련뱅이입니다. 지금 여기가 광장 모퉁이만 아니라면, 저는 무릎을 꿇고, 아무런 격려도 받지 못한 채라도, 그저 제가—제가, 아가씨가 어쩌면—하고 가능하다고 생각해 봐도 좋을 거라고 희망하게만 해 달라고 빌고 또 애원할 것입니다—\"\n\n\"오, 제발, 그러지 마세요!\" 플로렌스가 외친다. 순간 무척 놀라고 괴로워하며. \"오, 제발 그러지 마세요, 투츠 씨. 멈춰 주세요, 부디. 더는 말씀하지 마세요. 제게 베푸는 친절이자 호의로 여기고, 그러지 말아 주세요.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 끔찍하게 당황하여, 입이 떡 벌어진다.\n\n\"당신은 제게 그토록 잘해 주셨어요,\" 플로렌스가 말한다, \"저는 당신께 정말 감사하고, 제게 다정한 벗이 되어 주신 당신을 좋아할 이유가 충분하고, 또 정말 당신을 무척 좋아해요.\" 그러면서 그 천진한 얼굴이 세상에서 가장 정직하고 다정스러운 표정으로 그를 향해 미소 짓는다. \"그러니 분명 당신은 그저 작별 인사를 하려던 거지요!\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠 씨가 말한다, \"제가—제가—바로 그 뜻이었습니다. 별일 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"안녕히 가세요!\" 플로렌스가 외친다.\n\n\"안녕히 계세요, 돔비 아가씨!\" 투츠 씨가 더듬거린다. \"부디 그 일에 대해서는 아무것도 마음에 두지 마시기 바랍니다. 그건—그건 별일 아니에요, 고맙습니다. 세상에 조금도 대수로울 게 없는 일입니다.\"\n\n가엾은 투츠 씨는 절망에 빠진 채 호텔로 돌아가, 침실 문을 안에서 잠그고, 침대에 몸을 내던지고는, 오랫동안 거기 누워 있다. 그래도 그것이 마치 세상에서 가장 대수로운 일이라도 되는 듯이. 그러나 학사 학위를 지닌 피더 씨가 저녁을 먹으러 오기로 되어 있는데, 이것이 투츠 씨에게는 잘된 일이다. 안 그랬다면 그가 언제나 다시 일어날지 알 수 없었을 테니까. 투츠 씨는 그를 맞이하고 후하게 대접하기 위해 일어나지 않을 수 없다.\n\n그리고 그 사교적 미덕인 환대의 너그러운 영향력은 (포도주와 진수성찬은 말할 것도 없고) 투츠 씨의 마음을 열고, 그를 데워 대화로 이끈다. 그는 학사 학위를 지닌 피더 씨에게 광장 모퉁이에서 무슨 일이 있었는지는 말하지 않는다. 그러나 피더 씨가 \"그게 언제 성사되는 건가?\" 하고 묻자, 투츠 씨는 \"어떤 주제들이라는 게 있는 법이지\" 하고 대꾸하는데, 이 말은 피더 씨를 즉각 한두 단계 풀이 죽게 만든다. 투츠 씨는 덧붙여서, 블림버가 무슨 권리로 자기가 돔비 아가씨와 함께 있었던 것을 들먹였는지 모르겠다고, 만일 그가 그걸로 무례하게 굴 작정이었다고 생각된다면, 박사든 뭐든 가만두지 않았을 거라고 말한다. 다만 그저 그자가 무지해서 그런 거려니 짐작한다고 한다. 피더 씨는 의심할 여지가 없다고 말한다.\n\n그러나 피더 씨는 절친한 벗인지라, 그 주제에서 배제되지는 않는다. 투츠 씨가 요구하는 것은 그저, 그 이야기를 은밀하게, 또 감정을 담아서 거론해야 한다는 것뿐이다. 포도주를 몇 잔 들고 나서, 그는 돔비 아가씨의 건강을 위해 건배하며 한마디 한다. \"피더, 자네는 내가 이 건배를 제안하는 심정이 어떤 건지 짐작도 못 할 거야.\" 피더 씨가 대답한다. \"아니, 짐작하고말고, 친애하는 투츠. 그게 자네 명예를 크게 빛내 주는 거라네, 이 사람아.\" 그러고 나서 피더 씨는 우정에 북받쳐 악수를 청하고는, 혹시라도 투츠에게 형제가 필요해지면 어디서 자기를 찾을 수 있는지 안다고, 우편으로든 소포로든 말이라고 한다. 피더 씨는 또, 조언을 해도 된다면, 투츠 씨에게 기타를, 적어도 플루트라도 배우라고 권하고 싶다고 한다. 여자들은 구애를 받을 때 음악을 좋아하는 법이고, 자기도 그 이로움을 몸소 겪어 봤다는 것이다.\n\n이 말이 학사 학위를 지닌 피더 씨로 하여금, 자기가 코닐리어 블림버에게 눈독을 들이고 있다는 고백에 이르게 한다. 그는 투츠 씨에게, 자기는 안경에 거부감이 없으며, 만일 박사가 점잖게 처신하여 일을 그만둔다면, 자, 그러면 그들은—생계가 보장되는 셈이라고 알려 준다. 그가 보기에는, 사람이 일을 해서 상당한 돈을 모았으면 마땅히 그 일을 그만두어야 한다는 것이고, 그러면 코닐리어가 그 일에 어떤 사내라도 자랑스러워할 만한 조력자가 되어 줄 거라고 한다. 투츠 씨는 돔비 아가씨에 대한 찬사를 미친 듯이 쏟아 내고, 가끔은 자기 머리통을 날려 버리고 싶은 심정이라는 암시로 대답한다. 피더 씨는 그것이 경솔한 짓이 될 거라고 강력히 타이르며, 삶과 화해하는 방편으로 코닐리어의 초상화를, 안경까지 그려진 그것을 그에게 내보인다.\n\n이렇게 이 조용한 영혼들은 저녁을 보낸다. 그리고 저녁이 밤에게 자리를 내주자, 투츠 씨는 피더 씨와 함께 걸어서 집으로 가, 블림버 박사의 문 앞에서 그와 헤어진다. 그러나 피더 씨는 계단을 오르기만 했다가, 투츠 씨가 가 버리자 다시 내려와, 홀로 바닷가를 거닐며 자기 앞날을 생각한다. 피더 씨는, 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어가는 동안, 블림버 박사가 일을 그만둘 거라고 일러 주는 파도 소리를 또렷이 듣는다. 그리고 그 집의 겉모습을 바라보며, 박사가 먼저 거기에 칠을 새로 하고 말끔히 수리해 두리라고 생각하면서, 부드럽고 낭만적인 즐거움을 느낀다.\n\n투츠 씨도 마찬가지로, 자기 보석을 담고 있는 그 함 바깥을 이리저리 헤매고 있다. 그리고 한심하기 그지없는 마음 상태로, 경찰의 의심도 사 가면서, 불빛이 보이는 어느 창문을 응시하는데, 그곳이 플로렌스의 방임에 틀림없다고 여긴다. 그러나 그게 아니다. 그곳은 스큐턴 부인의 방이니까. 그리고 다른 방에서 잠든 플로렌스가 옛 정경들 한가운데서 사랑스러운 꿈을 꾸고, 그 옛 추억들이 다시 살아나는 동안, 그 인내심 강했던 소년의 자리를 같은 무대 위에서 냉혹한 현실로 대신하여, 그 무대를 다시금—그러나 얼마나 다르게!—쇠락과 죽음에 결부시키는 그 인물이, 그곳에 잠 못 든 채 불평하며 누워 있다. 그것은 흉하고 초췌하게, 안식 없는 침상 위에 누워 있다. 그리고 그 곁에는, 자기의 무정한 아리따움이 자아내는 그 두려움 속에서—병자의 흐려져 가는 눈에는 그것이 두려움이기에—에디스가 앉아 있다. 밤의 적막 속에서 파도는 그들에게 무슨 말을 하는가?\n\n\"에디스, 나를 치려고 쳐든 저 돌 팔뚝은 뭐냐? 너는 안 보이느냐?\"\n\n\"아무것도 없어요, 어머니, 어머니의 헛것일 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내 헛것이라니! 모든 게 내 헛것이라는 거지. 봐라! 네가 저걸 못 본다는 게 가당키나 하냐?\"\n\n\"정말로, 어머니, 아무것도 없어요. 만약 그런 게 있다면 제가 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"꼼짝 않고?\" 사납게 딸을 노려보며—\"이제 사라졌구나—그런데 너는 어쩌면 그렇게 꼼짝도 않느냐? 저건 내 헛것이 아니다, 에디스. 네가 내 곁에 앉아 있는 걸 보고 있자니 몸이 다 서늘해진다.\"\n\n\"죄송해요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"죄송하다고! 너는 늘 죄송해하는 것 같구나. 하지만 그게 나를 위한 건 아니지!\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그녀가 운다. 그리고 베개 위에서 가만있지 못하는 머리를 이쪽저쪽 뒤척이면서, 홀대받았다느니, 자기가 어떤 어미였느니, 그들이 만났던 그 착한 노파가 어떤 어미였느니, 그런 어미를 둔 딸자식들이 얼마나 차갑게 보답하는지를 늘어놓는다. 그 두서없는 넋두리 한가운데서 그녀는 멈추고, 딸을 바라보고는, 자기 정신이 나가고 있다고 외치며, 침대에 얼굴을 파묻는다.\n\n에디스는 가엾은 마음에 그녀에게 몸을 굽혀 말을 건넨다. 병든 노파는 그녀의 목을 끌어안고는, 공포에 질린 표정으로 말한다.\n\n\"에디스! 우리 곧 집에 가는 거지. 돌아가는 거지. 내가 다시 집으로 가게 된다는 뜻이지?\"\n\n\"네, 어머니, 네.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 사람이 한 말—그 사람 이름이 뭐더라, 나는 도무지 이름을 못 외운다니까—소령—우리가 떠나올 때 했던 그 끔찍한 말—그건 사실이 아니지? 에디스!\" 비명을 지르며 멍하니 응시하며, \"나한테 그게 문제인 게 아니지.\"\n\n밤마다 창가에는 불빛이 타오르고, 그 인물은 침대에 누워 있고, 에디스는 그 곁에 앉아 있고, 잠들지 못하는 파도는 밤새도록 그 둘을 부른다. 밤마다 파도는 제 신비를 거듭거듭 되뇌느라 목이 쉬어 있고, 모래밭에는 먼지가 쌓여 있으며, 바닷새들은 솟구쳐 올랐다 맴돌고, 바람과 구름은 자취 없는 비행에 나서 있다. 하얀 팔들은 달빛 속에서, 저 멀리 보이지 않는 나라를 향해 손짓한다.\n\n그리고 여전히 병든 노파는 구석을 들여다보는데, 그곳에는 돌 팔뚝이—어느 무덤의 조상(彫像) 일부라고 그녀는 말한다—자기를 치려고 쳐들려 있다. 마침내 그것이 떨어진다. 그러고 나자 말을 잃은 한 노파가 침대에 누워 있고, 그녀는 몸이 굽고 오그라들었으며, 그 절반이 죽어 있다.\n\n그것이 바로, 해가 비웃도록 분칠하고 덧대 꾸민 그 인물의 모습이니, 날마다 사람들 틈을 헤치며 천천히 끌려 다니고, 그렇게 가면서 그토록 좋은 어미였던 그 착한 노파를 찾으며, 사람들 사이를 헛되이 들여다보면서 얼굴을 일그러뜨린다. 그것이 바로, 자주 바다의 가장자리로 수레에 실려 내려가 그곳에 세워지는 그 인물이니, 그러나 그 위로 어떤 바람도 신선함을 불어넣지 못하고, 그것을 위해 대양의 속삭임도 위로의 말 한마디 건네지 못한다. 그녀는 누운 채 몇 시간이고 그 소리에 귀를 기울인다. 그러나 그 말은 그녀에게 어둡고 음울하기만 하고, 그녀의 얼굴에는 두려움이 어려 있으며, 그 눈이 드넓은 바다 위를 헤맬 때면, 하늘과 땅 사이에 펼쳐진 황량함의 너른 자락밖에 보이지 않는다.\n\n그녀는 플로렌스를 좀처럼 보지 못하고, 보게 되더라도, 화를 내며 얼굴을 일그러뜨려 보인다. 에디스는 늘 그녀 곁에 있으면서 플로렌스를 가까이 오지 못하게 한다. 그리고 플로렌스는 밤에 자기 침대에서, 그런 모습의 죽음을 생각하며 몸서리치고, 죽음이 찾아온 것은 아닌가 하여 자주 잠에서 깨어 귀를 기울인다. 에디스 말고는 아무도 그녀를 돌보지 않는다. 그녀를 보는 눈이 적을수록 좋다. 그래서 딸이 홀로 침상 곁을 지킨다.\n\n그 그늘진 얼굴에 그늘이 더해지고, 그 날카로운 이목구비가 한층 더 날카로워지며, 두 눈 앞의 베일이 두꺼워져, 흐릿한 세상을 가리는 관보(棺褓)가 되는 때가 왔다. 이불 위를 헤매던 그녀의 두 손이 힘없이 손바닥을 맞대 모이고는 딸을 향해 움직인다. 그리고 그녀의 것 같지 않은, 우리 인간의 언어를 말하는 그 어떤 목소리 같지도 않은 목소리가 말한다. \"내가 너를 길렀으니까!\"\n\n에디스는 눈물 한 방울 없이, 가라앉아 가는 그 머리에 자기 목소리를 더 가까이 가져가려 무릎을 꿇고, 대답한다.\n\n\"어머니, 제 말 들리세요?\"\n\n눈을 한껏 크게 뜬 채, 그녀는 대답으로 고개를 끄덕이려 애쓴다.\n\n\"제가 결혼하기 전날 밤을 기억하세요?\"\n\n머리는 미동도 없지만, 어쩐지 그렇다는 뜻을 드러낸다.\n\n\"그때 저는 어머니께, 거기서 어머니가 한 몫을 용서하며, 제 몫은 하느님께서 용서해 주시기를 빈다고 말했어요. 우리 사이에 지나간 시간은 끝났다고 말했지요. 지금 다시 그렇게 말할게요. 입을 맞춰 주세요, 어머니.\"\n\n에디스가 그 하얀 입술에 입을 대자, 잠시 모든 것이 고요하다. 한순간 뒤, 그녀의 어머니가, 소녀 같은 웃음과 클레오파트라 같은 자태의 해골을 하고서, 침대에서 몸을 일으킨다.\n\n장밋빛 휘장을 쳐라. 바람과 구름 말고도 무언가가 날아오르고 있다. 장밋빛 휘장을 바짝 쳐라!\n\n그 사건의 소식이 시내에 있는 돔비 씨에게 전해지고, 돔비 씨는 (아직 바덴바덴으로 갈 마음을 정하지 못한) 사촌 피닉스를 찾아가는데, 그 역시 방금 그 소식을 받은 참이다. 사촌 피닉스처럼 사람 좋은 인물은 결혼식이나 장례식에 안성맞춤인 사람이고, 집안에서 그가 차지하는 위치로 보아 그와 의논하는 것이 마땅하다.\n\n\"돔비,\" 사촌 피닉스가 말했다, \"맹세코, 이런 우울한 일로 자네를 보게 되어 정말 가슴이 아프네. 가엾은 우리 이모님! 정말이지 지독히도 활기찬 분이셨는데.\"\n\n돔비 씨가 대답한다, \"정말 그러셨지요.\"\n\n\"그리고 화장을 하시면,\" 사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"정말이지 젊어 보이셨거든, 나이를 생각하면 말이야. 분명히 자네 결혼식 날, 나는 그분이 앞으로 이십 년은 거뜬하실 거라고 생각했었지. 실은, 브룩스 클럽에서 어떤 사람한테 그렇게 말했었네—꼬마 빌리 조퍼라고—자네도 분명 알 거야—눈에 외알 안경을 낀 사람 말이야.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 모른다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙인다. \"장례 절차에 관해서,\" 그가 넌지시 말한다, \"혹시 무슨 의견이 있으신지—\"\n\n\"음, 맹세코,\" 사촌 피닉스가 턱을 쓰다듬으며 말한다. 소맷부리 아래로 손이 딱 그만큼 나와 있어서 겨우 쓰다듬을 수 있다. \"나도 정말 모르겠네. 우리 시골 영지의 정원에 영묘(靈廟)가 하나 있긴 한데, 아무래도 손볼 데가 많을 거야. 실은 형편없는 꼴이지. 내 주머니 사정이 좀 쪼들리지만 않았어도 손을 좀 봐 뒀을 텐데. 게다가 사람들이 그 철책 안으로 들어와 소풍 놀이를 벌인다지 뭔가.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 그건 안 되겠다고 분명히 생각한다.\n\n\"마을에 유난히 좋은 교회가 하나 있긴 해,\" 사촌 피닉스가 생각에 잠겨 말한다. \"앵글로노르만 양식의 순수한 표본이고, 레이디 제인 핀치베리가—코르셋을 꽉 졸라매는 여자 말이야—아주 훌륭하게 스케치해 두기도 했지. 그런데 듣자니 회칠을 해서 다 망쳐 놨다더군. 게다가 길이 멀어.\"\n\n\"차라리 브라이턴 자체가 어떨까요,\" 돔비 씨가 제안한다.\n\n\"내 명예를 걸고, 돔비, 그보다 나은 데가 없을 것 같네,\" 사촌 피닉스가 말한다. \"보다시피 바로 이 자리이고, 아주 명랑한 곳이지.\"\n\n\"그러면 언제가,\" 돔비 씨가 넌지시 말한다, \"편하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"나는 꼭,\" 사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"자네가 가장 좋다고 생각하는 날이면 어느 날이든 맞추겠노라 약속하겠네. 가엾은 우리 이모님을—그 경계까지—실은, 무덤까지 모시는 일에 큰 기쁨을(물론 우울한 기쁨이지만) 느낄 걸세,\" 사촌 피닉스가 다른 식의 표현을 떠올리지 못한 채 말한다.\n\n\"월요일이면 시내를 떠나기에 괜찮겠습니까?\" 돔비 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"월요일이면 나한테 더할 나위 없이 좋네,\" 사촌 피닉스가 대답한다. 그리하여 돔비 씨는 그날 사촌 피닉스를 모시고 내려가기로 정하고, 곧 작별을 고하는데, 사촌 피닉스가 계단까지 배웅하며 헤어질 즈음에 이렇게 말한다. \"정말이지 자네가 이 일로 그토록 수고를 하게 되어 무척 미안하네, 돔비.\" 이에 돔비 씨는 대답한다. \"천만에요.\"\n\n정해진 시각에 사촌 피닉스와 돔비 씨가 만나, 브라이턴으로 내려가, 그 둘만으로 고인을 잃고 애도하는 다른 모든 조문객을 대신하여, 고인의 유해를 안식처로 따라간다. 사촌 피닉스는 장례 마차에 앉아 길에서 수많은 지인을 알아보지만, 예의를 차려 그들에게 다른 내색은 하지 않고, 그저 그들이 지나갈 때마다 돔비 씨에게 알려 주려고 소리 내어 하나씩 짚어 나간다. \"톰 존슨이로군. 화이트 클럽 사람으로, 의족을 한 사내지. 아니, 여기 웬일인가, 토미? 혈통 좋은 암말을 탄 폴리로군. 스몰더 자매들이야\"—그런 식이다. 의식이 진행되는 동안 사촌 피닉스는 의기소침해져서, 이런 일들을 겪으면 사람이 정말이지 자기도 흔들리기 시작했구나 생각하게 된다고 말하고, 의식이 끝났을 즈음에는 그의 눈도 정말로 촉촉이 젖는다. 그러나 그는 곧 기운을 회복한다. 스큐턴 부인의 다른 친척과 친구들도 마찬가지인데, 그들 가운데 소령은 클럽에서 그녀가 도무지 몸을 충분히 싸매는 법이 없었다고 줄곧 떠벌리고, 한편 그 등을 드러낸, 눈꺼풀 때문에 그토록 애를 먹는 젊은 아가씨는, 작은 비명을 지르며, 그분이 엄청나게 나이가 많았던 게 틀림없으며, 온갖 끔찍한 병으로 돌아가셨고, 그 얘기는 꺼내선 안 된다고 말한다.\n\n이리하여 에디스의 어머니는 자기 소중한 친구들에게 입에 오르내리지도 못한 채 누워 있다. 그 친구들은 제 신비를 거듭거듭 되뇌느라 목이 쉰 파도 소리에 귀가 먹었고, 모래밭에 쌓인 먼지에도, 달빛 속에서 저 멀리 보이지 않는 나라를 향해 손짓하는 하얀 팔들에도 눈이 멀어 있다. 그러나 모든 것이 늘 그러했듯이, 그 미지의 바다 가장자리에서 흘러간다. 그리고 그곳에 홀로 서서 파도 소리에 귀 기울이는 에디스의 발치에는, 그녀가 인생의 길을 위에 흩뿌리며 걸어갈, 축축한 해초가 밀려와 던져진다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLII. Confidential and Accidental\n\n더 이상 커틀 선장의 거무튀튀한 작업복과 차양 모자 차림이 아니라, 갈색 제복 한 벌을 든든히 차려입은—그 제복은 무척 점잖고 얌전한 척하면서도 실은 재단사가 만들고 싶어 할 만큼 흡족하고 자신만만한 물건이었다—그라인더 롭은, 겉모습이 이렇게 탈바꿈한 데다, 자기 양심이라는 그 뻔뻔스러운 악기에서 갈채의 음악을 한껏 울려 가며, 그 둘도 없는 호인들의 곁을 어떻게 의기양양하게 떨쳐 냈는지를 떠올리며 여가의 몇 분을 그들을 향해 거들먹대는 일에 쏟을 때를 제외하고는, 속으로는 선장이며 미드십맨이며 전혀 안중에도 없이, 이제는 자기 후원자인 카커 씨를 섬기고 있었다. 카커 씨의 집에 들어앉아 그의 시중을 들면서, 롭은 그 흰 이를 두려움과 떨림으로 둥근 눈으로 지켜보았으며, 그 눈을 어느 때보다 더 크게 떠야 할 필요가 있다고 느꼈다.\n\n그가 어떤 강력한 마법사의 부림을 받게 되어 그 이들이 그의 가장 강한 주문이었다 한들, 온몸을 통째로 그 이들 앞에서 이보다 더 떨 수는 없었을 것이다. 소년은 이 후원자에게서 어떤 힘과 권위를 느꼈는데, 그것이 그의 온 신경을 빨아들이고 가장 무조건적인 복종과 순종을 강요했다. 그는 카커 씨가 없을 때조차 그에 대해 생각하는 것만으로도 자신이 안전하다고 여기지 못했으니, 그에게 처음 매이게 된 그날 아침처럼 당장 멱살이 잡히기라도 할까, 그리고 그 이가 하나하나 자기를 찾아내어 마음속 온갖 공상을 추궁하기라도 할까 두려웠던 것이다. 면전에서는, 카커 씨가 자기의 은밀한 생각을 읽어 낸다는 것, 혹은 마음만 먹으면 의지를 조금만 발휘해도 읽어 낼 수 있다는 것을, 카커 씨가 자기를 쳐다볼 때 자기를 보고 있다는 것 못지않게 의심하지 않았다. 그 지배력은 어찌나 완벽했고 그를 어찌나 사로잡았던지, 그는 도무지 생각이라곤 거의 하지 못한 채, 후원자가 자기에게 행사하는 그 거역할 수 없는 명령과 자기를 마음대로 부릴 수 있는 권능에 대한 인상이 끊임없이 부풀어 오르는 것으로 마음이 가득 찬 채, 다른 모든 것에 대해서는 정신이 정지된 상태로 그의 비위를 살피며 서서 그의 명령을 미리 헤아리려 애쓰곤 했다.\n\n롭은 어쩌면—당시의 마음 상태로는 그것을 캐묻는 것 자체가 여간한 무모함이 아니었을 것이다—자기가 이 영향력에 이토록 완전히 굴복하는 것이 어느 부분이라도, 그 후원자가 자기도 그라인더 학교에서 변변찮은 학생으로 배운 어떤 음흉한 술수의 대가일지 모른다는 막연한 의심이 떠다녔기 때문은 아닌지 스스로 알아보지 않았던 모양이다. 그러나 롭이 그를 두려워하는 만큼이나 그를 우러러보았던 것만은 분명하다. 어쩌면 카커 씨가 자기 권능의 원천을 더 잘 알고 있었을 터인데, 그 권능은 그가 그것을 다루는 방식에 의해 조금도 손상되지 않았다.\n\n선장의 부림에서 벗어난 바로 그날 밤, 롭은 비둘기를 처분하고—급한 마음에 손해 보는 흥정까지 해 가며—곧장 카커 씨의 집으로 내려가, 칭찬을 기대하는 듯 상기된 얼굴로 새 주인 앞에 기세 좋게 나타났다.\n\n\"이런, 망나니 녀석!\" 카커 씨가 그의 보따리를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"네 일자리를 버리고 나한테로 왔단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"오, 죄송합니다만, 나리,\" 롭이 더듬거렸다, \"지난번에 제가 여기 왔을 때, 나리께서 말씀하셨잖습니까—\"\n\n\"내가 말했다고,\" 카커 씨가 응수했다, \"내가 뭐라고 했더냐?\"\n\n\"죄송합니다만, 나리, 아무 말씀도 안 하셨습니다, 나리,\" 롭이 이 물음의 어조에 경계심이 들어, 잔뜩 당황한 채 대답했다.\n\n그의 후원자는 잇몸을 활짝 드러내며 그를 바라보았고, 집게손가락을 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"넌 험한 끝을 보게 될 거다, 내 떠돌이 친구야. 내 눈에 다 보인다. 네 앞에는 파멸이 기다리고 있어.\"\n\n\"오, 제발 그러지 마십시오, 나리!\" 롭이 두 다리를 후들거리며 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 나리, 저는 그저 나리를 위해 일하고, 나리를 시중들고, 시키시는 건 무엇이든 충실히 하고 싶을 뿐입니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"네가 나하고 무슨 관계라도 맺을 거라면,\" 그의 후원자가 응수했다, \"시키는 건 무엇이든 충실히 하는 게 좋을 거다.\"\n\n\"네, 그건 압니다, 나리,\" 고분고분한 롭이 애원했다. \"확실히 알고 있습니다, 나리. 한 번만 저를 시험 삼아 써 주신다면요, 나리! 그리고 만에 하나라도 제가 나리 뜻을 거스르는 짓을 하는 걸 발견하시거든, 저를 죽이셔도 좋습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"이 개 같은 놈아!\" 카커 씨가 의자에 등을 기대며, 그를 향해 태연히 미소 지으며 말했다. \"네가 나를 속이려 들기라도 하면, 내가 너한테 할 짓에 비하면 그건 아무것도 아니다.\"\n\n\"네, 나리,\" 비굴한 그라인더가 대답했다, \"확실히 무섭게 저를 짓밟으시겠지요, 나리. 황금 기니로 매수당한다 해도, 저는 그런 짓을 하려 들지 않겠습니다, 나리.\"\n\n칭찬에 대한 기대가 완전히 꺾인 채, 풀이 죽은 그라인더는 후원자를 바라보며, 그러면서도 보지 않으려 헛되이 애쓰며 서 있었으니, 그것은 비슷한 처지의 똥개가 흔히 드러내는 그런 불안이었다.\n\n\"그래, 옛 일자리를 버리고 여기 와서 나더러 너를 거두어 달라고 청하는 거란 말이지, 응?\" 카커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 죄송합니다만, 나리,\" 롭이 대답했다. 사실 그렇게 한 것은 후원자 자신의 지시에 따른 것이었으나, 그는 그런 취지를 조금이라도 내비쳐 자기를 변호할 엄두를 내지 못했다.\n\n\"좋다!\" 카커 씨가 말했다. \"넌 나를 알지, 얘야?\"\n\n\"죄송합니다만, 네, 압니다, 나리,\" 롭이 모자를 만지작거리며, 여전히 카커 씨의 눈에 붙들린 채, 거기서 벗어나려 헛되이 애쓰며 대답했다.\n\n카커 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다. \"그럼 조심해라!\"\n\n롭은 여러 차례 짤막한 절로 이 경고를 똑똑히 알아들었음을 표시하고는, 문밖으로 나갈 수 있다는 전망에 크게 안도하며 절을 하면서 문 쪽으로 물러나고 있었는데, 그때 후원자가 그를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"이봐!\" 그가 거칠게 그를 도로 불러 외쳤다. \"넌 말이지—그 문 닫아라.\"\n\n롭은 자기 목숨이 그 민첩함에 달리기라도 한 듯 복종했다.\n\n\"넌 엿듣는 데 익숙하지. 그게 무슨 뜻인지 아느냐?\"\n\n\"엿듣는 거, 말씀입니까, 나리?\" 롭이 당황해서 잠시 생각한 끝에 어림짐작으로 말했다.\n\n그의 후원자가 고개를 끄덕였다. \"그리고 엿보고, 그런 것들 말이다.\"\n\n\"전 여기서는 그런 짓 안 하겠습니다, 나리,\" 롭이 대답했다. \"맹세코, 정말로 안 하겠습니다, 나리, 만약 한다면 죽어도 좋습니다, 나리, 무슨 대가를 약속받든 말입니다. 시키지 않으신 한, 그런 짓을 하겠다고 나서는 건 온 세상을 다 준대도 못 할 일이라 여기겠습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"안 하는 게 좋을 거다. 넌 지껄이고 고자질하는 데도 익숙하지,\" 그의 후원자가 더없이 냉정하게 말했다. \"여기선 그걸 조심해라, 안 그러면 넌 영락없는 망종이다.\" 그러고는 다시 미소 지으며, 다시 집게손가락으로 그에게 경고했다.\n\n그라인더의 숨은 경악으로 짧고 가빠졌다. 그는 자기 의도의 결백함을 항변하려 애썼으나, 멍한 복종 상태에서 미소 짓는 신사를 멀거니 쳐다볼 수밖에 없었다. 미소 짓는 신사는 그것으로 꽤 만족한 듯, 잠시 말없이 그를 살펴본 뒤 아래층으로 내려가라 명하고는, 그가 자기에게 고용되었음을 알려 주었다.\n\n이것이 카커 씨가 그라인더 롭을 들인 경위였고, 그 신사에 대한 외경에 찬 그의 헌신은, 가능하다면, 섬기는 시간이 한 분 한 분 흐를수록 더 굳어지고 자라났다.\n\n몇 달간 이어진 섬김의 끝 무렵, 어느 이른 아침, 롭은 약속에 따라 자기 주인과 아침을 함께하러 온 돔비 씨에게 정원 문을 열어 주었다. 바로 그 순간 주인 자신도, 그 귀하신 손님을 맞이하러, 또 온 이를 다 드러내며 환대하러 서둘러 나왔다.\n\n\"여기서 뵙게 될 줄은,\" 카커가 그가 말에서 내리는 것을 거든 뒤 말했다, \"정말이지 꿈에도 생각 못 했습니다. 제 달력에서는 참으로 특별한 날이군요. 무엇이든 마음대로 하실 수 있는 분에게야 어떤 일도 그리 특별할 게 없겠지만, 저 같은 사람에게는 사정이 사뭇 다르지요.\"\n\n\"여긴 취향이 좋은 곳이군, 카커,\" 돔비 씨가, 잔디밭에 황송하게 멈춰 서서 주위를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 말씀해 주실 형편이 되시는 분이지요,\" 카커가 응수했다. \"감사합니다.\"\n\n\"실은,\" 돔비 씨가 도도한 생색을 내며 말했다, \"누구라도 그렇게 말할 걸세. 적어도 그만한 정도로는, 아주 널찍하고 짜임새 있게 잘 꾸민 곳이야—꽤 우아하네.\"\n\n\"적어도 그만한 정도로는, 참으로 그렇지요,\" 카커가 깎아내리는 투로 응수했다. \"그 단서가 필요하지요. 뭐! 이 얘긴 이만하면 됐고, 칭찬해 주실 형편이 되시긴 하나, 그래도 감사드립니다. 안으로 드시겠습니까?\"\n\n집 안으로 들어선 돔비 씨는, 그럴 만한 이유가 있었기에, 방들의 완벽한 배치와, 거기 넘쳐 나는 안락과 효과를 위한 수많은 장치들을 눈여겨보았다. 카커 씨는 겸손을 과시하느라 이 주목을 공손한 미소로 받으며, 그 미묘한 의미를 이해하고 고맙게 여긴다고, 그러나 사실 이 오두막은 자기 처지의 사람에게는 과분할 만큼 훌륭한—어쩌면 이렇게 누추하긴 해도 자기 같은 사람이 차지하기에는 너무 좋은—곳이라고 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 어쩌면 이렇게 멀리 동떨어져 계신 나리께는, 실제보다 더 좋아 보이는 모양입니다,\" 그가 거짓된 입을 한껏 늘여 가며 말했다. \"마치 군주들이 거지의 삶에서 매력을 상상하듯 말이지요.\"\n\n그는 그렇게 말하며 돔비 씨에게 날카로운 시선과 날카로운 미소를 던졌고, 돔비 씨가 난롯불 앞에서 몸을 한껏 곧추세우는—그의 부관이 그토록 자주 흉내 내던 그 자세로—벽에 걸린 그림들을 돌아보았을 때는, 더욱 날카로운 시선과 더욱 날카로운 미소를 보냈다. 돔비 씨의 차가운 눈이 그것들 위를 무심히 훑었지만, 카커의 예리한 눈길은 그의 눈길과 동행하며, 그 보조를 맞추어, 그 눈이 어디로 가서 무엇을 보는지 정확히 짚어 냈다. 그 눈이 특히 한 그림 위에 머물렀을 때, 카커는 거의 숨도 쉬지 않는 듯했으니, 그 곁눈질의 살핌이 그토록 고양이처럼 빈틈없고 경계로 차 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그 위대한 우두머리의 눈은 다른 그림들에서와 마찬가지로 그 그림에서 지나갔고, 나머지 것들보다 더 감명받은 기색도 없어 보였다.\n\n카커는 그것을—그것은 이디스를 닮은 그림이었다—마치 살아 있는 것인 양 바라보았다. 그러면서 그의 얼굴에는 사악하고 소리 없는 웃음이 떠올랐는데, 그것은 한편으로는 그 그림을 향한 것 같았으나, 사실은 그토록 아무것도 모른 채 그 옆에 서 있는 위대한 인물을 모조리 비웃는 것이었다. 곧 아침 식사가 식탁에 차려졌고, 돔비 씨를 이 그림에 등을 진 의자로 청한 뒤, 카커는 평소처럼 그 맞은편 자기 자리에 앉았다.\n\n돔비 씨는 평소 버릇보다도 더 근엄했으며, 아주 말이 없었다. 화려한 새장 안 도금된 고리에 매달려 흔들거리는 앵무새가 헛되이 주의를 끌려 애썼으나, 카커는 손님을 살피느라 새에게 신경 쓸 겨를이 없었다. 그리고 손님은, 골똘한 명상에 빠진 채, 식탁보에서 눈을 떼지 않고, 그 빳빳한 목도리 너머로, 시무룩하다 해도 좋을 만큼 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 시중을 들던 롭으로 말하자면, 그의 온 기능과 정력이 주인을 살피는 데 너무도 사로잡혀 있어서, 이 손님이 바로 자기가 어린 시절 집안의 건강 증명서로서 안겨 가 보여졌던 그 위대한 신사이며, 자기가 가죽 반바지를 신세 진 분이라는 생각을 마음속에 들일 엄두조차 거의 내지 못했다.\n\n\"실례지만,\" 카커가 불쑥 말했다, \"돔비 부인께서는 어떠신지 여쭤도 될까요?\"\n\n그는 이 물음을 던지며, 턱을 손에 괸 채 비굴하게 몸을 앞으로 기울였다. 그와 동시에 그의 눈은 그림 쪽으로 올라갔으니, 마치 그 그림에게 \"자, 봐라, 내가 어떻게 이 사람을 끌고 가는지!\" 하고 말하는 듯했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 대답하며 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"돔비 부인은 아주 잘 지내네. 카커, 자네 말을 들으니 자네와 나누고 싶던 어떤 이야기가 생각나는군.\"\n\n\"로빈, 우린 좀 나가 있어도 좋다,\" 주인이 말하자, 그 부드러운 어조에도 로빈은 흠칫 놀라더니, 마지막 순간까지 후원자에게 눈을 고정한 채 사라졌다. \"물론 자네는 저 아이를 기억하지 못하겠지?\" 그 옭아매인 그라인더가 나가자 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"못하네,\" 돔비 씨가 더없이 도도한 무관심으로 말했다.\n\n\"나리 같으신 분이 기억하실 리가요. 거의 불가능한 일이지요,\" 카커가 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 저 아이는 나리께서 유모를 데려오셨던 그 집안 출신입니다. 어쩌면 나리께서 너그럽게도 저 아이의 교육을 맡으셨던 일을 기억하실지도 모르겠군요?\"\n\n\"그 아이인가?\" 돔비 씨가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"그 교육에 별로 보답이 못 되는 아이인 모양이군.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 변변찮은 망나니 녀석이지요,\" 카커가 어깨를 으쓱하며 응수했다. \"그런 평판을 듣습니다. 하지만 사실인즉, 제가 저 아이를 거둔 건, 다른 일자리를 도무지 못 구하던 차에, 저 아이가 나리께 무슨 청구권 같은 게 있다고 여기고는(틀림없이 집에서 그렇게 배웠겠지요), 줄곧 그 청원으로 나리 뒤꽁무니를 쫓아다니려 했기 때문입니다. 그리고 나리의 일과 관련한 저의 명확하고 공인된 관계가 단지 사무적인 성격일 뿐이라 해도, 저는 나리께 속한 모든 것에 자연스레 우러나오는 관심을 가지고 있어서—\"\n\n그는 다시 멈추었으니, 마치 자기가 돔비 씨를 충분히 멀리까지 끌고 왔는지 알아보려는 듯했다. 그리고 다시, 턱을 손에 괸 채, 그 그림을 곁눈질로 흘겼다.\n\n\"카커,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"나는 자네가 자네의—한정 짓지 않는다는 걸 잘 알고 있네—\"\n\n\"섬김을,\" 미소 짓는 주인이 거들었다.\n\n\"아니, 나는 차라리 자네의 마음 씀이라고 하겠네,\" 돔비 씨가 말하며, 그렇게 말하는 것이 그에게 후하고 우쭐한 찬사를 베푸는 것임을 잘 의식한 채, \"단지 우리의 사무적 관계에만 한정 짓지 않는다는 걸 말이지. 방금 자네가 언급한 그 사소한 일에서, 나의 감정과 희망과 실망에 대한 자네의 배려가 바로 그 한 예일세. 고맙네, 카커.\"\n\n카커 씨는 천천히 고개를 숙이고, 마치 어떤 행동으로든 돔비 씨의 신뢰의 물줄기를 흩뜨릴까 두려운 듯, 두 손을 아주 부드럽게 비볐다.\n\n\"자네가 그 일을 언급한 건 때맞춤일세,\" 돔비 씨가 잠시 망설인 뒤 말했다, \"그것이 내가 자네에게 말하려던 것으로 가는 길을 열어 주고, 또 그 일이 우리 사이에 전혀 새로운 관계를 끌어들이지는 않는다는 걸 일깨워 주니까. 비록 그것이 지금까지 내가—\"\n\n\"저를 영예롭게 해 주신 것보다,\" 카커가 다시 고개를 숙이며 거들었다. \"제가 얼마나 영광스러운지는 말씀드리지 않겠습니다. 나리 같으신 분이라면 마음 내키는 대로 베풀 수 있는 영예가 얼마나 큰지를 잘 아실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"돔비 부인과 나는,\" 돔비 씨가, 그 찬사를 위엄 어린 극기로 흘려넘기며 말했다, \"몇몇 점에서 의견이 그리 일치하지 않네. 우리는 아직 서로를 이해하지 못하는 듯해. 돔비 부인은 배워야 할 것이 있어.\"\n\n\"돔비 부인께서는 보기 드문 매력을 여럿 지니셨고, 의심할 바 없이 많은 찬미를 받는 데 익숙하셨겠지요,\" 그의 사소한 눈짓과 어조 하나하나를 살피는 그 매끄럽고 반드러운 자가 말했다. \"하지만 애정과 의무와 존경이 있는 곳에서는, 그런 까닭에서 빚어진 자잘한 잘못들은 곧 바로잡히기 마련입니다.\"\n\n돔비 씨의 생각은 본능적으로, 거만한 손이 문 쪽으로 뻗어지던 그때 아내의 화장실에서 자기를 바라보던 그 얼굴로 거슬러 날아갔다. 그리고 거기에 드러나 있던 애정과 의무와 존경을 떠올리며, 그는 자기를 살피는 그 눈이 분명히 본 것만큼이나 또렷이, 자기 얼굴로 피가 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"돔비 부인과 나는,\" 그가 말을 이었다, \"스큐턴 부인이 작고하기 전에, 내 불만의 원인을 두고 다소 의논을 한 적이 있네. 그 일에 대해서는, 자네가 우리—내 집에 와 있던 그날 저녁, 돔비 부인과 나 사이에 오간 일을 목격했으니, 대강은 짐작했을 테지.\"\n\n\"제가 그토록 그 자리에 있던 것을 후회했던 그날 말씀이군요,\" 미소 짓는 카커가 말했다. \"저 같은 처지의 사람이 나리의 허물없는 눈길을 받는 것을 반드시 자랑스러워해야 하겠지만—그렇다고 그것을 제 공으로 돌리지는 않습니다. 나리야 무엇을 하셔도 체통이 깎이지 않으시지요—또 돔비 부인이 나리의 성함을 받아 높여지시기 전에 일찍이 그분께 소개되는 영예를 누리긴 했습니다만, 정말이지, 그날 밤만은 제가 그런 각별한 행운의 대상이 되었던 것을 거의 후회했습니다.\"\n\n어떤 사람이 어떤 상황에서든, 자기의 황송한 자비와 후원으로 인해 두드러진 것을 후회할 수 있다는 것은, 돔비 씨로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 도덕적 진기함이었다. 그리하여 그는 위엄을 한층 더 끌어올려 응수했다. \"그래! 그건 또 왜인가, 카커?\"\n\n\"제가 염려하는 것은,\" 그 심복이 응수했다, \"저를 호의 어린 관심으로 보아 주실 마음이 도통 없으시던 돔비 부인께서—저 같은 처지로서야, 본래 도도하시고 그 도도함이 그토록 잘 어울리시는 부인께 그런 걸 기대할 수야 없습니다만—그 대화에서의 제 죄 없는 역할을 쉬이 용서하지 않으실지도 모른다는 점입니다. 나리의 노여움은 결코 가벼운 일이 아니니까요, 잊지 마십시오. 더구나 제삼자 앞에서 그것을 받게 된다는 건—\"\n\n\"카커,\" 돔비 씨가 오만하게 말했다, \"내가 제일가는 고려 대상이라고 봐도 되겠지?\"\n\n\"오! 거기에 무슨 의심이 있겠습니까?\" 상대방이, 악명 높고 반박할 수 없는 사실을 인정하는 사람의 조바심으로 대답했다.\n\n\"우리 둘이 함께 거론될 때면, 돔비 부인은 부차적인 고려 대상이 된다고 생각하네만,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지 않은가?\"\n\n\"그렇지 않냐고요?\" 카커가 응수했다. \"물어보실 필요조차 없다는 걸 누구보다 잘 아시지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 카커,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"돔비 부인의 노여움을 사게 된 데 대한 자네의 후회가, 내 신뢰와 호의를 간직한다는 자네의 만족으로 거의 상쇄되기를 바라네.\"\n\n\"제가 보니, 불행히도,\" 카커가 응수했다, \"그 노여움을 사 버린 모양입니다. 돔비 부인께서 그것을 나리께 표하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"돔비 부인은 여러 의견을 표했네,\" 돔비 씨가 위엄 있는 냉담함과 무관심으로 말했다, \"내가 동조하지 않는, 또 논하거나 떠올리고 싶지도 않은 의견들을 말이지. 나는 얼마 전, 이미 자네에게 말한 대로, 내가 고집할 필요가 있다고 느낀 가정 내 공경과 복종의 몇 가지 점을 돔비 부인에게 알려 주었네. 나는 부인의 평안과 안녕, 그리고 내 위엄을 위해, 그 점들에서 즉시 행실을 고치는 것이 마땅하다고 부인을 납득시키지 못했네. 그래서 나는 돔비 부인에게, 만약 내가 다시 이의를 제기하거나 항의할 필요가 생긴다면, 내 심복인 자네를 통해 내 의견을 부인에게 전하겠다고 알려 주었지.\"\n\n카커가 그에게 던지는 시선에는, 그 머리 위 그림을 향한 악마 같은 시선이 섞여 들어, 한 줄기 번개처럼 그 그림에 내리꽂혔다.\n\n\"자, 카커,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"나는 망설임 없이 자네에게 말하네, 나는 내 뜻을 관철하겠어. 나는 가지고 놀 만한 사람이 아닐세. 돔비 부인은, 내 뜻이 곧 법이며, 내 평생의 규율 전체에 단 하나의 예외도 허락할 수 없다는 걸 알아야 해. 자네는 이 임무를 떠맡아 주는 호의를 베풀게나. 나한테서 나온 일이니, 자네가 점잖게 어떤 후회를 내비치든—그건 돔비 부인을 위해 내가 고맙게 여기는 바지만—그리 달갑지 않은 일은 아니리라 믿네. 그리고 자네는, 분명히 믿건대, 다른 어떤 용무 못지않게 그것을 정확히 수행하는 호의를 베풀어 주겠지.\"\n\n\"아시지 않습니까,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"그저 명령만 하시면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"알지,\" 돔비 씨가 위엄 있게 수긍의 뜻을 표하며 말했다, \"그저 명령만 하면 된다는 걸. 이 일을 밀고 나가는 것이 필요해. 돔비 부인은 의심할 바 없이 여러 면에서 매우 자격 있는 부인이니—\"\n\n\"나리의 선택에 영예까지 안겨 드릴 만한 분이시지요,\" 카커가 하품하듯 이를 드러내며 거들었다.\n\n\"그래, 자네가 굳이 그런 식으로 표현하겠다면,\" 돔비 씨가 자기의 점잔 빼는 어조로 말했다. \"그런데 지금으로서는, 돔비 부인이 그 선택에 마땅히 돌아가야 할 영예를 돌리고 있다고는 보지 않네. 돔비 부인에게는 뿌리 뽑아야 할 반항의 기질이 있어. 반드시 극복해야 할 기질이지. 돔비 부인은 도무지 이해하지 못하는 모양이야,\" 돔비 씨가 힘주어 말했다, \"이 나에게 반항한다는 생각 자체가 가당치 않고 터무니없다는 것을 말일세.\"\n\n\"우리 시티 사람들은 나리를 더 잘 알지요,\" 카커가 입이 귀에 걸리도록 미소 지으며 응수했다.\n\n\"자네는 나를 더 잘 알지,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. \"그러기를 바라네. 하지만 사실, 나는 돔비 부인에게 정당한 평가를 해 줄 의무가 있네. 비록 부인의 그 뒤 행실(그건 변함이 없네만)과는 모순돼 보일지 몰라도, 내가 앞서 말한 그 일에서 다소 엄하게 나의 못마땅함과 결의를 표했을 때, 내 훈계가 매우 강력한 효과를 낸 듯 보였단 말이지.\" 돔비 씨는 더없이 거창한 위엄으로 그 말을 토해 냈다. \"그러니, 카커, 나를 대신해 돔비 부인에게 이렇게 알리는 호의를 베풀어 주게. 우리의 지난 대화가 아직 그 효과를 보이지 않은 데 다소 놀라며, 나는 그것을 부인에게 다시 상기시키지 않을 수 없다고 말일세. 그 대화에서 부인에게 내린 명령에 따라 행실을 단속할 것을 내가 고집한다고. 부인의 행실에 나는 만족하지 못한다고. 크게 불만이라고. 그리고 만약 부인이, 첫 번째 돔비 부인이 그러했고, 또 감히 덧붙이건대 부인의 자리에 있는 어떤 부인이라도 그러했을 것처럼, 내 뜻에 맞추어 처신할 분별과 합당한 감정을 갖지 못한다면, 나는 자네를 통해 한층 더 달갑지 않고 노골적인 통보를 전하게 하는, 매우 불쾌한 부득이함에 처하게 되리라고 말일세.\"\n\n\"첫 번째 돔비 부인은 매우 행복하게 사셨지요,\" 카커가 말했다.\n\n\"첫 번째 돔비 부인은 분별이 대단했지,\" 돔비 씨가 죽은 이에 대한 신사다운 관용으로 말했다, \"그리고 감정도 매우 올발랐고.\"\n\n\"돔비 아가씨는 어머니를 닮았다고 보십니까?\" 카커가 말했다.\n\n순식간에, 어둡게, 돔비 씨의 얼굴이 변했다. 그의 심복이 그것을 날카롭게 살폈다.\n\n\"제가 괴로운 화제에 다가갔군요,\" 그가, 자신의 열띤 눈과는 도무지 어울리지 않는, 부드럽고 후회 어린 어조로 말했다. \"부디 용서해 주십시오. 제가 가진 관심 탓에 이런 연상의 사슬을 그만 잊었습니다. 부디 용서해 주십시오.\"\n\n그러나 그렇게 말하면서도, 그의 열띤 눈은 그에 못지않게 바싹 돔비 씨의 떨군 얼굴을 훑었다. 그러고는 그 그림을 향해 이상하리만치 의기양양한 시선을 쏘았으니, 마치 자기가 어떻게 다시 그를 끌고 가는지, 또 무슨 일이 닥쳐오는지 증언해 달라고 호소하는 듯했다.\n\n\"카커,\" 돔비 씨가 식탁 여기저기를 둘러보며, 다소 달라지고 한결 다급해진 목소리로, 그리고 한층 핼쑥해진 입술로 말했다, \"사과할 일은 없네. 자네가 잘못 짚었어. 그 연상은 자네가 짐작하는 것 같은 어떤 회상이 아니라, 지금 다루는 일과 결부된 거야. 나는 내 딸을 대하는 돔비 부인의 태도가 못마땅하네.\"\n\n\"실례지만,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"잘 이해가 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이렇게 알아 두게,\" 돔비 씨가 응수했다, \"자네가 그것을—그것을, 원한다면—나로부터 돔비 부인에게 직접 제기하는 이의의 거리로 삼아도 좋다는 걸 말이지. 부디 부인에게 일러 주게, 부인이 내 딸에게 헌신을 과시하는 것이 나에게 거슬린다고. 그건 사람들 눈에 띄기 십상이야. 그건 사람들로 하여금, 내 딸을 대하는 돔비 부인과, 나 자신을 대하는 돔비 부인을 견주어 보게 하기 십상이지. 부디 돔비 부인에게 분명히 알려 주게, 내가 그것에 반대한다고. 그리고 부인이 즉시 내 반대에 따르기를 내가 기대한다고. 돔비 부인이 진심일 수도 있고, 변덕을 부리는 것일 수도 있고, 나에게 맞서는 것일 수도 있겠지. 하지만 어느 경우든, 그리고 모든 경우든, 나는 그것에 반대하네. 만약 돔비 부인이 진심이라면, 그만두기를 더더욱 꺼려선 안 되지. 그런 과시로는 내 딸에게 보탬이 되지 않을 테니까. 만약 내 아내에게 나에 대한 마땅한 복종을 넘어 넘쳐나는 어떤 다정함과 의무가 있다면, 어쩌면 그건 제 마음 가는 데 베풀어도 좋겠지. 하지만 나는 먼저 복종을 받겠어!—카커,\" 돔비 씨가, 말하면서 솟구쳤던 그 예사롭지 않은 격정을 억누르고, 자기의 위대함을 단언할 때 으레 쓰던 어조에 가까운 투로 돌아가며 말했다, \"부디 이 점을 빠뜨리거나 얼버무리지 말고, 자네 지시의 매우 중대한 일부로 여겨 주게.\"\n\n카커 씨는 고개를 숙이고, 식탁에서 일어나, 매끈한 턱에 손을 댄 채 난롯불 앞에 생각에 잠긴 듯 서서, 절반은 인간이고 절반은 짐승인 어떤 수도원의 조각상 같은, 혹은 낡은 빗물받이 위에서 흘겨보는 얼굴 같은 사악한 교활함으로 돔비 씨를 내려다보았다. 돔비 씨는 차츰 평정을 되찾으며, 혹은 자기가 고고한 자리를 차지했다는 의식 속에 격정을 식히며, 점점 다시 뻣뻣해져 앉아서는, 거대한 결혼반지 같은 고리 안에서 이리저리 흔들리는 앵무새를 바라보았다.\n\n\"실례합니다만,\" 침묵 끝에 카커가 갑자기 다시 자기 의자에 앉아, 그것을 돔비 씨의 맞은편으로 끌어당기며 말했다, \"제가 분명히 이해하게 해 주십시오. 돔비 부인께서는 나리가 저를 노여움의 전령으로 삼으실 가능성을 알고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇네,\" 돔비 씨가 대답했다. \"내가 그렇게 말했네.\"\n\n\"그렇군요,\" 카커가 재빨리 응수했다. \"그런데 왜죠?\"\n\n\"왜라니!\" 돔비 씨가 다소 망설이며 되풀이했다. \"내가 부인에게 말했으니까.\"\n\n\"아,\" 카커가 응수했다. \"하지만 왜 부인에게 말씀하셨습니까? 보십시오,\" 그는 미소 지으며, 고양이가 발톱을 감춘 발을 얹듯, 부드러운 벨벳 같은 손을 돔비 씨의 팔에 살며시 얹으며 말을 이었다, \"제가 나리의 마음속을 완벽히 이해할수록, 그만큼 더 쓸모가 있고, 효과적으로 부려지는 행복을 누릴 가능성이 높아지니까요. 제가 이해하기로는—제가 돔비 부인의 호의는 받지 못하고 있지요. 제 처지에서야 그걸 바랄 까닭이 없습니다만, 제가 그걸 받지 못한 것이 사실이지요?\"\n\n\"아마 그럴 테지,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"따라서,\" 카커가 이어 갔다, \"나리가 저를 통해 돔비 부인께 통보를 하시는 것이, 그 부인께는 특히나 입에 쓴 일이 되겠군요?\"\n\n\"내가 보기엔,\" 돔비 씨가 도도한 신중함으로, 그러면서도 다소 거북하게 말했다, \"그 일에 대한 돔비 부인의 생각은, 그것이 자네와 나에게 보이는 모습으로는, 그 일의 일부가 아닐세, 카커. 하지만 그럴 수도 있겠지.\"\n\n\"그리고—실례입니다만—제가 나리를 오해하는 걸까요,\" 카커가 말했다, \"나리가 이 일에서, 돔비 부인의 도도함을 꺾을 유력한 수단을—저는 이 말을, 적정한 선 안에 두면 그분처럼 미모와 교양이 출중한 부인을 꾸미고 빛내 주는 어떤 자질을 뜻하는 것으로 씁니다—그러니까, 부인을 벌하려는 게 아니라, 나리가 그토록 자연스럽고 정당하게 요구하시는 복종으로 끌어내릴 유력한 수단을 보고 계신다고 제가 생각한다면 말입니다?\"\n\n\"카커, 알다시피 나는,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"내가 적절하다고 여기는 어떤 행동 방침에 대해 이렇게 속속들이 이유를 대는 데 익숙지 않네만, 이 점에 대해서는 아무것도 부정하지 않겠네. 자네가 거기에 무슨 이의를 두고 있다면, 그건 정말 별개의 문제고, 자네에게 이의가 있다는 말만 해도 충분하네. 하지만 솔직히 나는, 내가 자네에게 맡길 수 있는 어떤 신뢰가 자네를 깎아내리리라고는 생각하지 않았네—\"\n\n\"오! 제가 깎인다고요!\" 카커가 외쳤다. \"나리를 섬기는 일에!\"\n\n\"—혹은 자네를,\" 돔비 씨가 이어 갔다, \"난처한 처지에 놓으리라고도 말일세.\"\n\n\"제가 난처한 처지에요!\" 카커가 외쳤다. \"저는 자랑스럽게—기쁘게—나리의 신임을 받들겠습니다. 솔직히 고백건대, 제가 보잘것없는 의무와 헌신을 그 발치에 바치고 싶은 부인께—그분이 나리의 아내가 아니십니까!—새로운 미움의 빌미를 드리지 않았으면 했습니다만, 나리의 바람이야 물론 세상 그 어떤 고려보다 으뜸이지요. 게다가, 돔비 부인께서 이런 자잘한 판단의 잘못—감히 말씀드리자면, 그 처지의 새로움에서 비롯된 잘못—에서 돌아서시면, 제가 떠맡는 그 사소한 역할에서, 한낱 한 톨의—제 멀고 다른 처지로는 그 이상은 어림없습니다—나리에 대한 존경과, 나리께 모든 것을 바치는 희생, 즉 부인께서 날마다 큰 곳간에 그득 쌓아 두는 것을 기쁨이요 특권으로 여기실 그 존경과 희생의 한 톨만을 알아봐 주시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n돔비 씨에게는, 그 순간, 다시금 손을 문 쪽으로 뻗은 그녀가 보이는 듯했고, 또 자기 심복의 부드러운 말소리를 뚫고 \"이후로 우리가 지금보다 더 남남이 될 수는 없어요!\"라는 말의 메아리가 들리는 듯했다. 그러나 그는 그 환상을 떨쳐 냈고, 결의는 흔들리지 않았으며, 이렇게 말했다, \"물론이지, 의심할 바 없네.\"\n\n\"더는 없습니다,\" 카커가, 의자를 원래 자리로 도로 끌어당기며—아직 둘 다 아침을 거의 들지 못했던 것이다—앉기 전에 대답을 기다리며 잠시 멈춰 말했다.\n\n\"없네,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"이것 말고는. 부디 유념해 주게, 카커, 돔비 부인에게 보내는 어떤 전갈이든, 자네가 맡았거나 맡게 될 그 어떤 전갈이든, 답변을 허용하지 않는다는 걸. 부디 나에게 어떤 답변도 가져오지 말게. 돔비 부인에게는, 우리 사이에 걸린 어떤 문제를 두고 미적거리거나 타협하는 것은 내 체통에 맞지 않으며, 내가 한 말이 곧 결정이라고 통고해 두었네.\"\n\n카커 씨는 이 신임장을 이해했다는 뜻을 표했고, 둘은 입맛 닿는 대로 아침 식사에 들어갔다. 그라인더 역시 때맞춰 다시 나타나, 한순간도 쉬지 않고 주인에게 눈을 고정한 채, 경배에 찬 몽상으로 시간을 보냈다. 식사가 끝나자, 돔비 씨의 말이 다시 끌려 나왔고, 카커 씨도 자기 말에 올라, 둘은 함께 시티를 향해 말을 몰았다.\n\n카커 씨는 기분이 더없이 좋았고, 말도 많았다. 돔비 씨는 자기가 응당 이야기를 들을 권리가 있는 사람이라는 군주 같은 태도로 그의 말을 받았고, 이따금 대화를 이어 가도록 몇 마디 던지는 황송함을 베풀었다. 그렇게 둘은 자못 제 격에 맞게 말을 달렸다. 그러나 돔비 씨는, 그 위엄에 걸맞게, 등자를 아주 길게 늘이고, 고삐를 아주 느슨히 쥔 채, 자기 말이 어디로 가는지 굽어볼 황송함을 좀처럼 베풀지 않았다. 그 결과로, 돔비 씨의 말이 빠른 속보로 달리다가 흩어진 돌멩이에 발이 걸려 비틀거리며 그를 내동댕이쳤고, 그 위로 뒹굴었으며, 일어나려고 발버둥 치면서 쇠를 박은 발굽으로 발길질을 해 그를 걷어찼다.\n\n눈이 빠르고, 손이 야무지며, 솜씨 좋은 기수였던 카커 씨는 순식간에 땅에 내려, 발버둥 치는 짐승을 제 발로 세우고 굴레를 잡았다. 그러지 않았더라면 그날 아침의 그 신임은 돔비 씨의 마지막 신임이 되었을 것이다. 그러나 그 행동의 상기됨과 분주함이 얼굴에 붉게 남아 있는 와중에도, 그는 이 하나하나를 다 드러낸 채 엎어진 우두머리 위로 몸을 굽혔고, 허리를 숙이며 중얼거렸다, \"이제 돔비 부인에게 단단히 노여움 살 빌미를 주었군, 그녀가 안다면 말이지!\"\n\n의식을 잃고 머리와 얼굴에서 피를 흘리는 돔비 씨는, 카커의 지시에 따라, 도로를 손질하던 몇몇 인부들에 의해 멀지 않은 가장 가까운 선술집으로 옮겨졌고, 거기서 곧 사방에서 잇따라 도착한 여러 외과의사들의 진료를 받았는데, 그들은 사막에서 죽어 가는 낙타 주위로 독수리가 모여든다는 말처럼, 어떤 신비로운 본능에 이끌려 온 듯했다. 그를 의식으로 되돌려 놓느라 적잖이 애쓴 뒤, 이 신사들은 그 부상의 성질을 살폈다. 바로 가까이 사는 외과의사 하나는 다리의 복합 골절이라고 강력히 주장했고, 그것은 선술집 주인의 의견이기도 했다. 그러나 멀리 살면서 우연히 그 동네에 와 있던 외과의사 둘이 어찌나 사심 없이 그 의견에 맞섰던지, 결국 환자는 심하게 베이고 멍들기는 했어도, 갈비뼈 몇 대 말고는 어떤 뼈도 부러지지 않았으며, 밤이 되기 전에 조심해서 집으로 옮겨도 좋다는 결론이 내려졌다. 그의 부상을 처치하고 붕대로 감는 데, 그것은 긴 작업이었는데, 그러고 나서 마침내 그가 안정을 취하게 남겨지자, 카커 씨는 다시 말에 올라, 그 소식을 집에 전하러 말을 달렸다.\n\n가장 좋을 때조차도—생김새와 이목구비의 반듯함으로 말하자면 제법 준수한 얼굴이긴 했으나—교활하고 잔인했던 그의 얼굴은, 이 심부름을 나설 때 가장 험악했다. 마음속 생각들의 교활함과 잔인함이—계략이나 음모라기보다는 아득한 가능성의 암시들이—그를 부추겨, 마치 남자와 여자를 사냥하는 듯 말을 몰게 했다. 마침내 고삐를 당기고, 사람들 다니는 큰길로 접어들면서 속도를 늦추어, 그는 흰 발의 말을 평소처럼 조심조심 길을 골라 걷게 다잡았고, 그 매끄럽고 잠잠하며 웅크린 태도와 상아 같은 미소 아래로 최대한 자기를 감추었다.\n\n그는 곧장 돔비 씨의 집으로 말을 몰아, 문 앞에서 내려, 긴한 용무로 돔비 부인을 뵙기를 청했다. 그를 돔비 씨 자신의 방으로 안내한 하인은, 곧 돌아와, 지금은 돔비 부인이 방문객을 맞이하시는 시간이 아니라고, 그리고 진작 그 말씀을 드리지 못해 죄송하다고 전했다.\n\n차가운 응대를 충분히 예상했던 카커 씨는, 명함에다, 무례를 무릅쓰고 면담을 졸라야겠으며, 만약 이번 일이 자기 처사를 충분히 정당화할 만한 것이라고 똑같이 확신하지 않았더라면 두 번씩이나(이 대목에 그는 밑줄을 그었다) 이렇게 대담하게 나서지는 않았을 것이라고 적었다. 잠깐의 지체 끝에, 돔비 부인의 하녀가 나타나, 그를 위층의 한 거실로 안내했는데, 거기에는 이디스와 플로렌스가 함께 있었다.\n\n그는 일찍이 이디스가 이토록 절반만큼이라도 아름답다고 생각한 적이 없었다. 그녀의 얼굴과 자태의 우아함을 그토록 흠모해 왔고, 그것이 그의 육감적인 기억 속에 그토록 생생히 깃들어 있었음에도, 그는 그녀가 이토록 절반만큼이라도 아름답다고 여긴 적이 없었다.\n\n문간에 선 그에게 그녀의 시선이 도도하게 떨어졌다. 그러나 그는—들어서며 고개를 숙이는 동작 중이었을 뿐인데도—자기가 쥔 새로운 권능을 어쩔 수 없이 드러내는 어떤 표정으로 플로렌스를 바라보았다. 그리고 그 시선이 처지며 흔들리는 것을, 또 이디스가 그를 맞으려 반쯤 몸을 일으키는 것을 보는 것이 그의 승리였다.\n\n그는 무척 안타깝다고, 깊이 비통하다고 했다. 매우 사소한 사고의 소식에 마음의 준비를 시켜 드리려 어떤 내키지 않는 심정으로 왔는지 모르겠다고 했다. 그는 돔비 부인에게 진정하시기를 간청했다. 자기의 신성한 명예를 걸고, 놀랄 까닭은 없다고 했다. 그러나 돔비 씨가—\n\n플로렌스가 갑자기 비명을 질렀다. 그는 그녀가 아니라 이디스를 바라보았다. 이디스가 그녀를 진정시키고 안심시켰다. 그녀는 비탄의 비명을 지르지 않았다. 아니, 아니었다.\n\n돔비 씨가 말을 타다가 사고를 당했다고. 말이 미끄러져 그가 내동댕이쳐졌다고.\n\n플로렌스가 미친 듯이 외쳤다, 아버지가 크게 다쳤다고, 돌아가셨다고!\n\n아닙니다. 명예를 걸고, 돔비 씨는 처음에는 정신을 잃으셨으나 곧 회복하셨으며, 다치긴 했어도 결코 어떤 위험에 처하시지는 않았다고. 만약 이것이 사실이 아니었다면, 이 괴로운 불청객인 자기로서는 결코 돔비 부인 앞에 나설 용기를 내지 못했을 것이라고. 정말로 사실이라고, 그가 엄숙히 그녀에게 장담했다.\n\n이 모든 것을 그는, 마치 플로렌스가 아니라 이디스에게 대답하듯, 그리고 자기 눈과 미소를 이디스에게 고정한 채 말했다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 돔비 씨가 어디에 누워 있는지를 그녀에게 일러 주고, 그를 집으로 모셔 오도록 마차 한 대를 자기에게 내어 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"어머니,\" 플로렌스가 눈물 속에 더듬거렸다, \"제가 감히 가도 된다면!\"\n\n이 말을 들었을 때 이디스에게 눈을 두고 있던 카커 씨는, 그녀에게 은밀한 눈짓을 보내며 가볍게 고개를 저었다. 그는 그녀가 자기에게 그 아름다운 눈으로 대답하기 전에 스스로와 얼마나 싸우는지를 보았다. 그러나 그는 그녀에게서 그 대답을 비틀어 짜냈다—자기가 그것을 받아 내리라는 것을, 아니면 입을 열어 플로렌스의 가슴을 도려내리라는 것을 그녀에게 보였다—그리고 그녀는 그것을 그에게 내주었다. 아침에 그 그림을 바라보았던 것처럼, 그녀가 눈길을 돌렸을 때 그는 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이렇게 청하라는 지시를 받았습니다,\" 그가 말했다, \"새 가정부—핍친 부인이라는 이름인 것 같습니다만—에게—\"\n\n그에게서 빠져나가는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그는 그녀 또한 돔비 씨가 아내에게 가하는 또 하나의 무시거리임을 한순간에 알아챘다.\n\n\"—돔비 씨께서 아래층 당신의 처소에 잠자리를 마련해 두기를 바라신다고 알리라는 것이었지요. 다른 어떤 방보다 그 방들을 선호하신다고요. 저는 거의 곧바로 돔비 씨께 돌아가겠습니다. 그분의 안락을 위해 온갖 주의가 기울여졌으며, 그분이 온갖 정성의 대상이심을, 부인, 굳이 장담드릴 필요는 없겠지요. 다시 한번 말씀드립니다만, 조금도 놀라실 까닭이 없습니다. 부인조차도 아주 마음을 놓으셔도 됩니다, 믿어 주십시오.\"\n\n그는 더없이 극진한 공경과 회유의 태도로 절하며 물러갔다. 그러고는 돔비 씨의 방으로 돌아가, 거기서 마차 한 대를 시티의 자기에게로 뒤따라 보내도록 조처한 뒤, 다시 말에 올라 천천히 그리로 달렸다. 그는 가는 내내 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 거기서도 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었으며, 돔비 씨를 남겨 둔 곳으로 돌아가는 마차 안에서도 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그가 다시 온전히 제정신으로 돌아와 자기 이를 의식하게 된 것은, 오직 그 신사의 긴 의자 곁에 앉아 있을 때뿐이었다.\n\n땅거미 질 무렵, 욱신거림과 통증으로 심하게 시달리던 돔비 씨는, 부축을 받아 마차에 태워졌고, 그 한쪽에 외투와 베개로 받쳐졌으며, 다른 한쪽에는 그의 심복이 동행했다. 그는 흔들려서는 안 되었기에, 그들은 걸음걸이보다 조금 빠른 정도로 움직였다. 그래서 그가 집에 당도했을 때는 어둠이 완연했다. 핍친 부인이, 신랄하고 험상궂게, 또—온 식솔이 익히 알고 있듯—페루의 광산을 잊지 않은 채, 문에서 그를 맞이했고, 그를 방으로 옮기는 것을 거드는 하인들에게 입심 사나운 식초를 몇 차례 잘게 흩뿌려 정신이 번쩍 들게 했다. 카커 씨는 그가 무사히 잠자리에 들 때까지 곁에 머물렀고, 그러고 나서, 그가 자기 집안을 다스리는 그 빼어난 식인귀 말고는 어떤 여자 방문객도 받기를 마다했으므로, 그 주인의 상태에 대한 보고를 가지고 다시 한번 돔비 부인을 찾아갔다.\n\n그는 다시 이디스가 플로렌스와 단둘이 있는 것을 보았고, 다시 자기의 위로의 말을 통째로 이디스에게, 마치 그녀가 더없이 절절하고 애정 어린 근심에 사로잡힌 사람이라도 되는 양 건넸다. 그는 그 공손한 동정에 어찌나 진지했던지, 작별 인사를 할 때, 그 순간 플로렌스 쪽으로 한 번 더 눈길을 보내며—감히 그녀의 손을 잡고, 그 위로 몸을 굽혀 입술을 대었다.\n\n이디스는 손을 빼지도, 뺨에 솟은 홍조와 두 눈의 형형한 빛, 그리고 온몸의 팽팽한 긴장에도 불구하고, 그 손으로 그의 준수한 얼굴을 치지도 않았다. 그러나 자기 방에 홀로 있게 되었을 때, 그녀는 그 손을 대리석 벽난로 선반에 내리쳤고, 그리하여 단번에 그것은 멍이 들고 피가 났다. 그러고는 그것을 자기에게서 멀리 떼어, 환히 타오르는 불 가까이에 댄 채, 마치 그것을 불 속에 처넣어 태워 버리고도 싶다는 듯이 했다.\n\n밤이 이슥하도록 그녀는, 사그라드는 불꽃 곁에, 어둡고 위협적인 아름다움으로, 홀로 앉아, 마치 자기 생각이 만져지기라도 하는 것인 양, 그것을 벽에 드리우기라도 한 것인 양, 벽 위에 어른거리는 거뭇한 그림자들을 지켜보았다. 능욕과 모욕의 온갖 형상들이며, 닥쳐올지 모를 일들의 시커먼 예고들이, 그녀 앞에서 흐릿하고 거인처럼 일렁였는데, 그 가운데 분노에 찬 한 형상이 그것들을 그녀에게 맞서도록 도열시켰다. 그리고 그 형상은 그녀의 남편이었다.\n\n# 제38장\n\n오래전에 꿈에서 깨어난 플로렌스는 아버지와 에디스 사이의 소원함을 비통한 심정으로 지켜보았다. 그 거리가 점점 더 벌어지는 것을 보았고, 두 사람 사이에 날마다 더 큰 쓰라림이 자라나는 것을 알았다. 날이 갈수록 늘어 가는 깨달음은 그녀의 사랑과 희망 위에 드리운 그늘을 짙게 했고, 잠시 잠들어 있던 옛 슬픔을 다시 일깨워, 그것을 이전보다 한층 견디기 힘든 것으로 만들었다.\n\n진실하고 진지한 천성에서 우러난 자연스러운 애정이 고통으로 변해 버리고, 가장 부드러운 보호와 가장 소중한 보살핌 대신 무심하거나 매정한 거절이 들어서는 일은—얼마나 괴로운 일이었는지는 플로렌스 말고는 누구도 알지 못하리라!—참으로 괴로웠다. 깊은 가슴속으로 그토록 느껴 왔던 것을 느끼면서, 단 한 번의 응답의 손길이 주는 행복조차 알지 못하는 것은 괴로운 일이었다. 그러나 그토록 다정하고 소중한 아버지든 에디스든 그 어느 쪽을 의심하지 않을 수 없게 되고, 두 사람 각각에 대한 자신의 사랑을 번갈아 두려움과 불신과 의아함으로 곱씹어야 하는 것은 훨씬 더 괴로운 일이었다.\n\n그러나 플로렌스는 이제 그렇게 하기 시작했다. 그리고 그렇게 하는 것은 도무지 달아날 수 없는 과업으로서, 바로 그녀 영혼의 순수함 자체가 그녀에게 지운 것이었다. 그녀는 아버지가 자기에게 그러하듯 에디스에게도 차갑고 완고하며, 단단하고 굽힐 줄 모르고 양보가 없는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 솟구치는 눈물과 함께 스스로에게 물었다. 혹시 사랑하던 자기 어머니도 그런 대접 때문에 불행해져 시들어 죽어 간 것은 아니었을까? 그러다가 그녀는 에디스가 자기를 제외한 모든 사람에게 얼마나 도도하고 위엄을 부렸는지, 아버지를 얼마나 경멸하며 대했는지, 얼마나 멀찌감치 거리를 두었는지, 그리고 집에 돌아온 그날 밤에 무슨 말을 했는지를 떠올리곤 했다. 그러면 곧 플로렌스에게 거의 죄악처럼 다가오는 생각이 있었으니, 자기가 아버지와 대립하는 사람을 사랑하고 있다는 것, 그리고 아버지가 그것을 알면 자기를 고독한 방 안에서 떠올리며 천성을 거스른 자식—태어날 때부터 아버지의 애정을 끝내 얻지 못했다는, 그토록 눈물지어 온 그 옛 잘못에 이 새로운 잘못까지 더한 자식—으로 여길 것이 틀림없다는 생각이었다. 에디스의 다음번 다정한 말 한마디, 다음번 다정한 눈길이 이 생각들을 다시 흔들어 놓아, 그것들이 마치 시커먼 배은망덕처럼 여겨지게 했다. 그토록 외롭고 상처 입어 풀이 죽은 플로렌스의 마음을 북돋워 주고, 가장 훌륭한 위로자가 되어 준 사람이 그녀 말고 또 누가 있었던가! 이렇듯 그 온화한 천성으로 두 사람 모두를 그리워하고, 두 사람 모두의 비참함을 함께 아파하며, 두 사람 모두에 대한 자기 도리를 의심하는 속삭임을 품은 채로, 플로렌스는 더 넓어지고 확장된 사랑 속에서, 에디스 곁에서, 그 슬픔에 잠긴 집 안에서 나뉘지 않은 비밀을 홀로 쌓아 두고 아름다운 어머니가 그 위에 한 줄기 빛으로 떠오르지 않았던 시절보다 더 많은 것을 견뎌 냈다.\n\n이 모든 것을 훨씬 능가했을 단 하나의 지독한 불행만은 플로렌스에게서 비껴갔다. 에디스가 자기를 향한 다정함으로 인해 아버지와의 사이를 더 벌려 놓는다거나, 아버지에게 새로운 미움의 빌미를 준다는 것을 그녀는 추호도 의심한 적이 없었다. 만약 플로렌스가 그런 원인에서 그런 결과가 빚어질 수 있음을 짐작이라도 했더라면, 가엾고 사랑이 많은 그 소녀가 얼마나 큰 슬픔을 느꼈을지, 얼마나 큰 희생을 치르려 했을지, 그리고 그 무게에 짓눌려 자식들의 사랑을 물리치지 않으시고 그들의 시달리고 부서진 마음을 내치지 않으시는 더 높으신 아버지의 곁으로 그녀가 얼마나 빠르고 확실하게 조용히 건너갔을지는 오직 하늘만이 아실 것이다! 그러나 사정은 그렇지 않았고, 그것은 다행한 일이었다.\n\n이제 플로렌스와 에디스 사이에서는 이런 문제들에 관해 단 한마디도 오가지 않았다. 에디스는 그런 점에 있어서는 두 사람 사이에 무덤 그 자체와도 같은 단절과 침묵이 있어야 한다고 말했고, 플로렌스도 그 말이 옳다고 느꼈다.\n\n이러한 형편 속에서 그녀의 아버지가 다치고 거동을 못 하는 몸으로 집에 실려 왔다. 그는 침울하게 자기 방으로 틀어박혔고, 하인들의 시중을 받았을 뿐 에디스는 다가가지 않았으며, 자정 무렵에 물러간 카커 씨 말고는 벗도 동무도 없었다.\n\n\"게다가 그자가 어찌나 좋은 동무인지요, 플로이 아가씨,\" 수전 닙퍼가 말했다. \"어휴, 그자는 정말 귀하신 물건이라니까요! 혹시라도 그자가 추천서 한 장이 필요해진대도 저한테는 절대 오지 말라지, 무슨 짓을 하든 간에요. 제가 그자한테 할 말은 그게 전부예요.\"\n\n\"수전, 제발,\" 플로렌스가 타일렀다, \"그러지 마.\"\n\n\"오, '그러지 마'라고 말씀하시는 거야 쉽지요, 플로이 아가씨,\" 닙퍼가 잔뜩 격분해서 대꾸했다. \"하지만 정말이지 죄송한 말씀이지만 우리 신세가 어찌나 한심한 지경에 이르렀는지 사람 온몸의 피가 죄다 바늘방석으로 변해 버릴 지경이라고요, 사방에서 그렇게 콕콕 찔러 대니. 오해는 마세요, 플로이 아가씨, 새어머님을 두고 하는 말은 전혀 아니에요, 그분이야 좀 콧대가 높으시긴 해도—그 점에 대해 제가 뭐라 할 자격은 없지만요—늘 저를 숙녀에게 하듯이 대해 주셨으니까요. 하지만 핍친 부인 같은 사람들이며, 그런 사람들을 우리 머리 위에 앉혀 놓고 아가씨 아버님 방문 앞에서 악어처럼 보초나 서게 하는 데에 이르면(그래도 그것들이 알을 안 까는 것만은 고맙게 여겨야지요!) 우리 신세가 너무나도 기가 막혀지는 거예요!\"\n\n\"아빠는 핍친 부인을 좋게 여기시잖니, 수전,\" 플로렌스가 대꾸했다, \"그리고 가정부를 고르실 권리야 아빠한테 있는 거고. 그러니 제발 그러지 마.\"\n\n\"그럼요 플로이 아가씨,\" 닙퍼가 대꾸했다, \"아가씨가 그러지 말라고 하시면 저는 절대로 안 합니다, 그러길 바라고요. 하지만 핍친 부인은 저한테 풋내 나는 산딸기처럼 시큰거리게 만든다니까요, 아가씨, 딱 그 짝이에요.\"\n\n수전은 이날 밤, 즉 돔비 씨가 집으로 실려 온 그 밤에 평소답지 않게 격앙되고 마침표라곤 없는 말투로 떠들어 댔는데, 플로렌스가 아버지의 안부를 알아보라고 그녀를 아래층으로 내려보냈을 때, 천적인 핍친 부인에게 전갈을 전달할 수밖에 없었던 탓이었다. 그 부인은 그것을 돔비 씨에게 들고 들어가지도 않고, 제멋대로 닙퍼 아가씨가 \"거만한 대답\"이라고 부르는 것을 자기 책임하에 돌려보낸 것이다. 수전 닙퍼는 이것을, 페루의 광산으로 고통받는 그 모범적인 수난자 측의 주제넘은 짓이자, 자기 아가씨를 깔보는 도저히 용서할 수 없는 처사로 해석했다. 그래서 그만큼 그녀의 격앙된 상태는 각별했다. 그러나 그녀는 그 결혼 이후로 줄곧 의심과 불신이 크게 부풀어 오른 상태였다. 플로렌스가 차지한 것과 같은 다른 신분의 누군가에게 강하고 진실한 애착을 품는, 그녀와 같은 부류의 사람들이 대개 그렇듯이, 수전은 질투가 무척 심했고, 그 질투는 자연히 에디스를 향했으니, 에디스가 그녀의 옛 영토를 나누어 가지며 두 사람 사이에 끼어들었기 때문이다. 자기 어린 여주인이 그동안 천대받던 무대에서 마땅한 제자리를 향해 올라서고, 아버지의 아름다운 부인을 동무이자 보호자로 두게 된 것을 수전 닙퍼가 진심으로 자랑스럽고 기뻐하면서도, 그녀는 자기 영역의 어떤 부분도 그 아름다운 부인에게 넘겨주는 데에는 못마땅함과 막연한 적의가 없을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 적의에 대해, 그 부인의 성품에 깃든 오만과 격정을 예리하게 간파해 낸 덕분에 사심 없는 정당한 구실을 어김없이 찾아내곤 했다. 그러므로 결혼 이후로 다소 물러설 수밖에 없었던 뒷전에서 닙퍼 아가씨는 집안일 전반을, 돔비 부인에게서는 결코 좋은 일이 나오지 않으리라는 단호한 확신을 품은 채 지켜보았다. 다만 가능한 모든 기회마다, 자기는 그 부인을 험담할 생각이 조금도 없다는 것을 공언하는 데에 항상 무척 신경을 썼다.\n\n\"수전,\" 탁자에 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있던 플로렌스가 말했다, \"아주 늦었구나. 오늘 밤엔 더 필요한 게 없어.\"\n\n\"아아, 플로이 아가씨!\" 닙퍼가 대꾸했다. \"정말이지 저는 그 옛 시절이 자주 그리워져요, 제가 이보다 훨씬 더 늦은 시각까지 아가씨 곁을 지키다가 지쳐서 잠들어 버리고 아가씨는 안경알처럼 말똥말똥 깨어 있던 그 시절이요. 하지만 이젠 새어머님이 오셔서 아가씨랑 함께 앉아 계시니, 정말 그게 고마운 일이지요. 그분들에 대해선 한마디도 흠잡을 게 없고요.\"\n\n\"동무가 하나도 없을 때 내 옛 동무가 누구였는지 나는 잊지 않을 거야, 수전,\" 플로렌스가 부드럽게 대꾸했다, \"절대로!\" 그러고는 고개를 들어, 그 미천한 벗의 목에 팔을 두르고, 그 얼굴을 자기 얼굴 쪽으로 끌어당겨, 잘 자라는 인사와 함께 거기에 입을 맞추었다. 그러자 닙퍼 아가씨는 어찌나 마음이 누그러졌는지 흐느껴 울기 시작했다.\n\n\"자 플로이 아가씨,\" 수전이 말했다, \"제가 다시 한번 아래층에 내려가서 아버님이 어떠신지 보고 올게요, 아가씨가 그분 때문에 마음 졸이시는 거 다 알아요, 제발 제가 다시 아래층에 내려가서 제 손으로 직접 그 방문을 두드려 보게 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"아니,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"가서 자. 아침이면 더 알게 될 거야. 아침에 내가 직접 알아볼게. 어머니가 다녀가셨을 거야, 아마도.\" 플로렌스는 얼굴을 붉혔으니, 사실 그런 기대는 품지 않았기 때문이다. \"아니면 지금 거기 계실지도 모르고. 잘 자!\"\n\n수전은 너무나 마음이 누그러진 터라 돔비 부인이 남편 곁에 붙어 있을 가능성에 대한 자기 속내를 입에 올리지 못한 채 말없이 물러갔다. 홀로 남은 플로렌스는 이내 지난날 자주 그랬던 것처럼 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었고, 눈물이 뺨을 타고 흘러내리는 것을 막지 않았다. 이 집안의 불화와 불행이 주는 비참함, 언젠가는 아버지의 가슴에 받아들여지리라는, 그것을 희망이라 부를 수 있다면, 이제 시들어 버린 그 희망, 두 사람 사이에서 오가는 의심과 두려움, 두 사람 모두를 향한 그 순결한 가슴의 그리움, 한때 밝은 희망과 약속의 환영이었던 것이 이런 식으로 끝나 버린 데서 오는 묵직한 실망과 회한—이 모든 것이 그녀의 마음속으로 한꺼번에 몰려와 눈물을 빠르게 쏟게 했다. 어머니와 남동생은 죽었고, 아버지는 그녀에게 무심하며, 에디스는 아버지와 맞서 그를 내치면서도 그녀를 사랑하고 그녀의 사랑을 받으니, 마치 그녀의 애정은 어디에 깃들든 결코 잘 자라날 수 없을 것만 같았다. 그 미약한 생각은 곧 잠잠해졌지만, 그것이 솟아난 바탕이 된 생각들은 너무나 진실하고 강해서 그 생각과 함께 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없었다. 그리고 그것들이 그 밤을 황량하게 만들었다.\n\n그러한 상념들 가운데, 하루 종일 떠올랐던 것처럼, 아버지의 모습이 솟아올랐다. 상처를 입고 고통 속에서, 가장 가까이 있어야 할 이들의 보살핌도 받지 못한 채 자기 방에 홀로 있으며, 더디 가는 시간을 외로운 고통 속에서 보내고 있는 아버지의 모습이. 그녀를 깜짝 놀라게 하여 두 손을 마주 잡게 한 무서운 생각—비록 그녀의 마음속에 새로운 것은 아니었지만—즉 아버지가 죽어 버려, 그녀를 보지도 못하고 그녀의 이름을 부르지도 못할지 모른다는 생각이 온몸에 전율을 일으켰다. 그 동요 속에서 그녀는 다시 한번 살그머니 아래층으로 내려가 그 방문 앞까지 가 볼까 하는 생각을 했고, 그 생각을 하며 몸을 떨었다.\n\n그녀는 자기 방문 앞에서 귀를 기울였다. 집 안은 고요했고 모든 불이 꺼져 있었다. 밤마다 그 방문 앞으로 순례를 다니곤 하던 때로부터 참으로 오랜, 오랜 시간이 흘렀구나, 하고 그녀는 생각했다. 한밤중에 그 방에 들어갔다가 아버지가 그녀를 계단 아래까지 데려다주던 때로부터 참으로 오랜, 오랜 시간이 흘렀구나, 하고 그녀는 애써 떠올렸다!\n\n옛날과 똑같은 어린아이의 마음을 가슴속에 간직한 채, 그 어린아이의 다정하고 수줍은 눈빛과 곱슬곱슬 풍성한 머리카락마저 그대로 지닌 채, 이른 처녀의 꽃봉오리로 피어난 지금에도 어린 시절 못지않게 아버지에게는 낯선 존재인 플로렌스는, 걸음마다 귀를 기울이며 계단을 살금살금 내려가 그의 방으로 다가갔다. 집 안에서는 아무도 움직이지 않았다. 방문은 바람이 통하도록 반쯤 열려 있었고, 안은 어찌나 고요한지 불 타는 소리가 들리고 벽난로 선반 위에 놓인 시계의 똑딱이는 소리를 헤아릴 수 있을 정도였다.\n\n그녀는 안을 들여다보았다. 그 방에서는 담요를 두른 가정부가 난롯불 앞 안락의자에 깊이 잠들어 있었다. 그 방과 옆방 사이의 문은 반쯤 닫혀 있었고 그 앞에는 가리개가 쳐져 있었다. 그러나 거기에는 불빛이 있었고, 그 빛이 그의 침대 처마 장식을 비추고 있었다. 모든 것이 어찌나 고요했는지 그녀는 그의 숨소리만으로도 그가 잠들어 있음을 알 수 있었다. 이것이 그녀에게 가리개를 돌아 그의 침실 안을 들여다볼 용기를 주었다.\n\n잠든 그의 얼굴과 마주치는 것은, 마치 그것을 보게 되리라 예상치 못했던 것처럼 큰 놀라움이었다. 플로렌스는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 멈춰 섰고, 만약 그가 그때 깨어났더라면 그녀는 그대로 거기 있을 수밖에 없었을 것이다.\n\n그의 이마에는 베인 상처가 있었고, 사람들이 그의 머리를 적셔 두어서, 머리카락이 베개 위에 흠뻑 젖은 채 헝클어져 있었다. 침대 밖으로 내놓은 한쪽 팔은 붕대가 감겨 있었고, 그는 몹시 창백했다. 그러나 첫 번째 재빠른 일별 뒤에, 그리고 그가 조용히 잠들어 있다는 첫 확인 뒤에, 플로렌스를 그 자리에 뿌리내리게 한 것은 이것이 아니었다. 그녀의 눈에 그를 그토록 엄숙해 보이게 한 것은, 이것과는 아주 다른, 이것을 넘어서는 무언가였다.\n\n그녀는 평생 그의 얼굴을 본 적이 없었으나, 그 얼굴에는—혹은 그녀가 그렇게 여긴 것인지도 모르지만—언제나 그녀를 의식하는 어떤 거북한 기색이 어려 있었다. 그녀는 평생 그의 얼굴을 본 적이 없었으나, 그럴 때마다 그녀의 마음속 희망은 가라앉았고, 그 수줍은 눈길은 그 매정하고 사랑 없고 밀쳐 내는 듯한 냉혹함 앞에서 떨어지곤 했다. 그러나 지금 그 얼굴을 바라보면서, 그녀는 자기 어린 시절을 어둡게 했던 그 먹구름에서 벗어난 그 얼굴을 난생처음 보았다. 그 자리에는 잔잔하고 평온한 밤이 깃들어 있었다. 그녀가 거기서 본 것만으로 판단한다면, 그는 그녀를 축복하며 잠든 것이라 해도 좋을 정도였다.\n\n깨어나세요, 매정한 아버지여! 깨어나세요, 지금, 시무룩한 사람이여! 시간이 흘러가고 있어요. 시각이 성난 발걸음으로 다가오고 있어요. 깨어나세요!\n\n그의 얼굴에는 아무런 변화가 없었다. 그리고 그녀가 그것을 두려운 마음으로 지켜보고 있을 때, 그 미동도 없는 모습은 이미 떠나간 이들의 얼굴을 떠올리게 했다. 그들도 저렇게 보였고, 그도 저렇게 될 것이었다. 그리고 울고 있는 그의 자식인 그녀도, 그때가 언제일지는 누가 말할 수 있으랴! 그리고 그들을 둘러싼 사랑과 미움과 무관심으로 가득한 온 세상의 사람들도 모두 그렇게 될 것이었다. 그때가 닥쳐오면, 지금 그녀가 하려는 이 일 때문에 그것이 그에게 더 무겁지는 않을 것이며, 그녀에게는 그것이 어쩌면 조금은 더 가볍게 내려앉을지도 몰랐다.\n\n그녀는 살그머니 침대로 다가가, 숨을 들이마시고, 몸을 굽혀, 그의 얼굴에 가만히 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 자기 얼굴을 잠시 그 곁에 가져다 댔으며, 차마 그에게 대지 못하던 그 팔을 베개 위로 둘러 그를 감쌌다.\n\n깨어나요, 운명 지어진 사람이여, 그녀가 곁에 있을 때! 시간이 흘러가고 있어요. 시각이 성난 발걸음으로 다가오고 있어요. 그 발이 이미 이 집 안에 들어와 있어요. 깨어나요!\n\n그녀는 마음속으로 하느님께 기도했다. 아버지를 축복해 주시고, 만약 가능하다면 그를 자기에게로 누그러지게 해 주시고, 그렇지 못하더라도 그가 잘못이 있다면 그를 용서해 주시고, 거의 불경처럼 여겨지는 이 기도를 드리는 자신을 용서해 주십사고. 그렇게 기도하며, 눈물로 흐려진 눈으로 그를 돌아보고, 수줍게 살그머니 빠져나와, 그녀는 그의 방을 나서서 다른 방을 가로질러 사라졌다.\n\n이제 그는 계속 잠들어도 좋다. 잠들 수 있는 동안 계속 잠들어도 좋다. 그러나 깨어났을 때 그 가냘픈 모습을 찾을 것이며, 그때가 닥쳐왔을 때 그것이 자기 곁에 있음을 발견하기를!\n\n플로렌스가 살금살금 위층으로 올라갈 때, 그 마음은 슬프고 비통했다. 그 고요한 집은 그녀가 내려갔을 때보다 한층 더 음산해져 있었다. 그녀가 한밤중에 지켜보고 온 그 잠은, 그녀에게는 죽음과 삶이 하나로 어우러진 듯한 엄숙함을 띠고 있었다. 그녀 자신이 한 일의 은밀함과 침묵은 그 밤을 은밀하고 고요하며 짓누르는 것으로 만들었다. 그녀는 자기 방으로 계속 가기가 내키지 않았고, 거의 갈 수가 없을 듯한 심정이었다. 그래서 응접실로 발길을 돌렸는데, 그곳에는 구름에 가린 달빛이 블라인드 사이로 비쳐 들고 있었다. 그녀는 텅 빈 거리를 내다보았다.\n\n바람이 음울하게 불고 있었다. 가로등들은 핼쑥해 보였고, 추워서 떠는 것처럼 흔들렸다. 하늘에는 빛이라기보다는 완전한 어둠이라고는 할 수 없는 무언가가 멀리서 어슴푸레 깜박이고 있었다. 그리고 불길한 밤은, 괴로운 임종을 맞는 죽어 가는 이들이 그러하듯, 떨며 안절부절못하고 있었다. 플로렌스는 병상 곁의 불침번을 서던 때에 이 황량한 시각을 눈여겨보고, 마치 그것에 대한 어떤 숨은 본능적 반감이라도 있는 듯 그 영향을 느끼던 것을 떠올렸다. 그리고 지금은 그것이 너무나도, 너무나도 음울했다.\n\n그날 밤 어머니는 그녀의 방에 오지 않았는데, 그것이 그녀가 잠자리에 들지 않고 늦도록 앉아 있었던 한 가지 까닭이었다. 전반적인 불안 못지않게, 누군가와 이야기를 나누고 싶고 그 음울함과 침묵의 마법을 깨고 싶은 간절한 그리움에서, 플로렌스는 어머니가 잠자는 방을 향해 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n방문은 안에서 잠겨 있지 않아서, 망설이는 그녀의 손길에 부드럽게 열렸다. 그녀는 밝은 불빛이 타오르고 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 안을 들여다보고는 더욱 놀랐으니, 어머니가 옷을 채 다 벗지도 않은 차림으로, 무너져 부스러져 내린 난롯불의 잿더미 곁에 앉아 있었던 것이다. 그녀의 두 눈은 허공을 뚫어지게 응시하고 있었고, 그 눈빛에, 그 얼굴에, 그 자태에, 그리고 금방이라도 벌떡 일어설 듯이 의자 팔걸이를 움켜쥔 그 손아귀에, 플로렌스는 자기를 섬뜩하게 할 만큼 격렬한 감정을 보았다.\n\n\"어머니!\" 그녀가 외쳤다, \"무슨 일이세요?\"\n\n에디스가 흠칫 놀랐다. 그녀가 너무나 기이한 두려움을 얼굴에 담은 채 플로렌스를 바라보는 바람에, 플로렌스는 아까보다 더 겁이 났다.\n\n\"어머니!\" 플로렌스가 황급히 다가서며 말했다. \"사랑하는 어머니! 무슨 일이세요?\"\n\n\"몸이 좀 안 좋았단다,\" 에디스가 몸을 떨면서도 여전히 똑같은 기이한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. \"흉한 꿈을 꾸었어, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그럼 아직 주무시지도 않으신 거예요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"그래,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"반쯤 깨어 있는 꿈이었지.\"\n\n그녀의 표정이 차츰 부드러워졌다. 그리고 플로렌스가 더 가까이, 자기 품 안으로 다가오게 두면서, 그녀는 다정한 태도로 말했다. \"그런데 내 작은 새가 여기서 무얼 하고 있을까? 내 작은 새가 여기서 무얼 하고 있는 거니?\"\n\n\"어머니, 오늘 밤 어머니를 뵙지 못해서, 그리고 아빠가 어떠신지 알 수가 없어서 마음이 편치 않았어요. 그래서 저는—\"\n\n플로렌스는 거기서 말을 멈추고 더는 말하지 않았다.\n\n\"늦었니?\" 에디스가, 자기 검은 머리카락에 뒤섞여 얼굴 위로 흘러내린 곱슬머리를 다정하게 쓸어 넘겨 주며 물었다.\n\n\"아주 늦었어요. 거의 동틀 무렵이에요.\"\n\n\"동틀 무렵이라고!\" 그녀가 놀라서 되뇌었다.\n\n\"사랑하는 어머니, 손을 어쩌다 그러셨어요?\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n에디스는 손을 갑자기 거두어들였고, 한순간 아까와 똑같은 기이한 두려움(그 안에는 일종의 거친 회피가 깃들어 있었다)으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그러나 곧 이렇게 말했다, \"아무것도 아니야, 아무것도. 부딪힌 거란다.\" 그러고는 \"내 플로렌스!\"라고 말했고, 이어 가슴이 들썩이더니 격정적으로 흐느껴 울었다.\n\n\"어머니!\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"오 어머니, 제가 무얼 할 수 있을까요, 제가 무얼 해야 우리가 더 행복해질까요? 뭔가 있나요?\"\n\n\"아무것도 없어,\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"정말 그래요? 정녕 그럴 수는 없는 건가요? 우리가 약속한 것에도 불구하고 제가 지금 제 마음속에 있는 것을 말한다 해도,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"저를 탓하지 않으실 거죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"소용없는 일이야,\" 그녀가 대답했다, \"소용없어. 내가 말했잖니, 얘야, 흉한 꿈을 꾸었다고. 그 무엇도 그 꿈들을 바꾸거나, 다시 찾아오는 걸 막을 수는 없어.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠어요,\" 플로렌스가, 바라볼수록 어두워지는 듯한 그 동요하는 얼굴을 응시하며 말했다.\n\n\"꿈을 꾸었단다,\" 에디스가 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"선을 행하는 데는 전혀 무력하고 악을 행하는 데는 온통 강력한 어떤 오만의 꿈을. 여러 부끄러운 세월 동안 채찍질당하고 들볶이면서도 결코 제 자신을 향한 것 말고는 반격해 본 적이 없는 오만, 깊은 굴욕을 의식하게 하여 그 주인을 비천하게 만들었으면서도, 정작 그 주인이 그것에 당당히 분노하거나 그것을 피하거나 '이래서는 안 돼!'라고 말하도록 결코 도와준 적이 없는 오만. 올바로 인도되었더라면 어쩌면 더 나은 것으로 이어졌을지도 모르나, 그 같은 소유자에게 속한 다른 모든 것과 마찬가지로 그릇 인도되고 비뚤어진 나머지, 자기 경멸과 한낱 무모함과 파멸이 되어 버린 오만의 꿈을.\"\n\n이제 그녀는 플로렌스를 바라보지도, 그녀에게 말하지도 않고, 마치 혼자 있는 것처럼 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"꿈을 꾸었단다,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"이 자기 경멸에서, 이 비참하고 무력하고 가련한 오만에서 비롯된 그런 무관심과 무감각의 꿈을. 그것이 옛날의, 익숙한, 손짓하는 그 손가락에 굴복한 채로—오 어머니, 오 어머니!—그러면서도 그것을 경멸하면서, 무기력한 걸음으로 제단 앞까지 나아간 꿈을. 그래서 날마다 어떤 새로운 형태로 찔리느니 차라리 단번에 영영 제 자신에게 가증스러운 존재가 되기를 마다하지 않은 꿈을. 비루하고 가련한 것!\"\n\n그러고는 이제 점점 차오르고 어두워지는 감정과 함께, 그녀는 플로렌스가 들어왔을 때와 똑같은 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"그리고 꿈을 꾸었단다,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그 오만이 어떤 목적을 이루려는 뒤늦은 첫 시도에서, 어느 비천한 발에 짓밟히고 짓이겨졌지만, 돌아서서 그를 노려보는 꿈을. 그것이 상처 입고, 쫓기고, 개들에게 물어뜯기면서도, 막다른 데서 맞서며 굴복하지 않는 꿈을. 아니, 굴복하려 한들 그럴 수가 없는 꿈을. 그러나 그것이 증오로 내몰리고 있는 꿈을.\"\n\n그녀의 움켜쥔 손이 자기가 붙들고 있던 떨리는 팔을 더욱 세게 죄었다. 그리고 그 놀라고 의아해하는 얼굴을 내려다보면서, 찌푸린 미간이 가라앉았다. \"오 플로렌스!\" 그녀가 말했다, \"내가 오늘 밤 거의 미쳐 버린 것 같구나!\" 그러고는 그 도도한 머리를 그녀의 목에 기대고 다시 울었다.\n\n\"나를 떠나지 마! 내 곁에 있어 줘! 나에겐 너 말고는 희망이 없어!\" 이 말을 그녀는 수십 번이나 되풀이했다.\n\n이윽고 그녀는 차츰 차분해졌고, 플로렌스의 눈물과 이런 때아닌 시각에 그녀가 깨어 있는 것에 대한 연민으로 가득 찼다. 그리고 이제 날이 밝아 오자, 에디스는 그녀를 품에 안아 침대에 눕히고, 자기는 눕지 않은 채 그 곁에 앉아 잠을 청해 보라고 일렀다.\n\n\"너는 지쳤으니까, 얘야, 그리고 슬프니까, 쉬어야 해.\"\n\n\"저는 정말이지 오늘 밤 슬퍼요, 사랑하는 어머니,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"하지만 어머니도 지치고 슬프시잖아요.\"\n\n\"네가 이렇게 내 가까이서 잠들어 있으면 그렇지 않단다, 얘야.\"\n\n둘은 서로 입을 맞추었고, 지칠 대로 지친 플로렌스는 차츰 부드러운 잠 속으로 빠져들었다. 그러나 그 곁에 있는 얼굴 위로 눈이 감기는 순간, 아래층에 있는 그 얼굴을 떠올리는 것이 너무나 슬퍼서, 그녀의 손은 위안을 구하듯 에디스에게로 더 가까이 다가갔다. 하지만 그 와중에도, 혹시 아버지를 저버리는 것은 아닐까 하여 그 손은 멈칫거렸다. 그래서 그녀는 잠 속에서 그 둘을 서로 화해시키려, 그리고 자기가 둘 다 사랑한다는 것을 그들에게 보여 주려 애썼지만 그러지 못했고, 깨어 있을 때의 슬픔이 그녀의 꿈의 일부가 되었다.\n\n곁에 앉은 에디스는, 발그레한 뺨 위에 젖은 채 드리운 그 검은 속눈썹을 내려다보았다. 진실을 알고 있었기에, 그녀는 다정함과 연민으로 내려다보았다. 그러나 그녀 자신의 눈에는 잠이 깃들지 않았다. 날이 밝아 오는 동안에도 그녀는 그 평온한 손을 자기 손에 쥔 채 여전히 깨어 지켜보고 앉아 있었다. 그리고 이따금, 그 잠든 얼굴을 바라보며 속삭였다, \"내 곁에 있어 줘, 플로렌스. 나에겐 너 말고는 희망이 없어!\"\n\n# 제39장\n\n날이 밝아 오자, 비록 해만큼 이른 것은 아니었지만, 수전 닙퍼 양이 자리에서 일어났다. 이 젊은 처녀의 유달리 날카로운 검은 두 눈에는 어딘가 무거운 기색이 어려 있어, 그 반짝임이 다소 누그러졌고, 또—평소의 그 눈답지 않게—이따금 감겨 있을 수도 있겠다는 가능성을 내비쳤다. 게다가 두 눈가가 부어 있어, 마치 간밤에 울기라도 한 것 같았다. 그러나 닙퍼는 풀이 죽기는커녕 유난히 활기차고 대담했으며, 그녀의 온 기운이 어떤 큰일을 치르려고 단단히 벼려진 듯했다. 이는 그녀의 옷차림에서도 드러났는데, 평소보다 훨씬 더 꼭 끼고 단정했다. 또 그녀가 집 안을 돌아다닐 때 이따금 머리를 홱홱 젖히는 모습에서도 드러났는데, 그것은 결연한 의지를 어마어마하게 잘 나타내고 있었다.\n\n한마디로, 그녀는 어떤 결심을, 그것도 야심 찬 결심을 굳혀 둔 터였다. 그 결심이란 다름이 아니라—돔비 씨 앞까지 뚫고 들어가, 그 신사와 단둘이 이야기를 나누겠다는 것이었다. \"그러겠다고 노상 말해 왔지,\" 그녀는 그날 아침 머리를 여러 번 홱홱 젖히며 위협하는 투로 혼잣말을 했다, \"이제는 정말 해 보겠어!\"\n\n이 필사적인 계획을 이루려고 스스로를 다그치며, 자기 특유의 날카로움으로 수전 닙퍼는 오전 내내 현관과 계단을 맴돌았으나, 돌격하기에 알맞은 기회를 찾지 못했다. 그러나 이 좌절에도 그녀는 조금도 꺾이지 않았으니, 그것은 오히려 자극이 되어 그녀의 투지를 돋우었다. 그녀는 경계를 조금도 늦추지 않았고, 마침내 저녁 무렵에 알아냈다. 그녀의 철천지원수 핍친 부인이 밤새 깨어 있었노라는 핑계를 대고 자기 방에서 졸고 있다는 것을, 그리고 돔비 씨가 시중드는 이도 없이 소파에 누워 있다는 것을.\n\n이번에는 머리만이 아니라 온몸을 한 번 홱 떨고 나서, 닙퍼는 돔비 씨의 방문까지 발끝으로 다가가 노크했다. \"들어오시오!\" 돔비 씨가 말했다. 수전은 마지막으로 한 번 더 몸을 떨며 스스로를 다독이고는 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n난롯불을 바라보고 있던 돔비 씨는 방문객을 보고 깜짝 놀란 시선을 던지며, 팔을 짚고 몸을 조금 일으켰다. 닙퍼는 무릎을 굽혀 절을 했다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"송구하오나, 나리, 나리께 드릴 말씀이 있사옵니다,\" 수전이 말했다.\n\n돔비 씨는 그 말을 되뇌듯 입술을 움직였으나, 이 젊은 여자의 주제넘음에 너무도 놀란 나머지 그 말을 소리 내어 입 밖에 낼 수가 없는 모양이었다.\n\n\"제가 나리를 모신 지,\" 수전 닙퍼가 평소의 빠른 말투로 말했다, \"이제 십이 년이 되었습니다. 플로이 아가씨, 제 아가씨를 모셔 왔지요. 제가 이 댁에 처음 왔을 때 아가씨는 아직 말도 또렷이 못 하셨고, 리처즈 부인이 이 집에 갓 들어왔을 때 저는 이미 고참이었습니다. 제가 므두셀라는 아닐지 몰라도, 품에 안긴 갓난아이는 아니옵니다.\"\n\n* 므두셀라: 구약 성경에 나오는 가장 오래 산 인물.\n돔비 씨는 팔을 짚고 몸을 일으킨 채 그녀를 바라보았으나, 이 예비 진술에 아무런 토를 달지 않았다.\n\n\"제 아가씨보다 더 사랑스럽고 더 복된 아가씨는 세상에 없었습니다, 나리,\" 수전이 말했다, \"그리고 저는 어떤 이들보다 훨씬 더 잘 알고 있어야 마땅하지요, 왜냐하면 저는 아가씨가 슬퍼하시는 것도 보았고 기뻐하시는 것도 보았으니까요(기뻐하실 일이 많지는 않았습니다만). 동생분과 함께 계신 것도 보았고 홀로 계신 것도 보았습니다. 어떤 이들은 아가씨를 도통 본 적도 없지요. 그래서 저는 누구에게든 말합니다—정말로요!\" 여기서 그 검은 눈의 처녀는 머리를 흔들고 발을 가볍게 굴렀다. \"플로이 아가씨야말로 이 세상에 숨을 쉰 이들 가운데 가장 복되고 가장 사랑스러운 천사라고요. 제가 갈가리 찢기는 한이 있어도 그렇게 말하겠습니다, 나리, 제가 폭스의 순교자는 못 될지언정 말입니다.\"\n* 폭스의 순교자: 존 폭스의 「순교자 열전」에 등장하는 신앙을 위해 목숨을 바친 인물들.\n\n돔비 씨는 분노와 놀라움으로, 쓰러졌을 때보다 한층 더 창백해졌다. 그리고 자기 두 눈을, 또한 두 귀를, 자기를 속이는 것이라 탓하기라도 하듯 그 말하는 여자에게서 시선을 떼지 않았다.\n\n\"플로이 아가씨께 진실하고 충실하지 않을 사람은 아무도 없을 겁니다, 나리,\" 수전이 말을 이었다, \"그리고 저는 십이 년을 모셨다 해서 무슨 공을 내세우려는 것이 아닙니다. 제가 아가씨를 사랑하니까요—그래요, 저는 누구에게든 그렇게 말합니다!\"—여기서 그 검은 눈의 처녀는 다시 머리를 흔들고 다시 발을 가볍게 구르며 흐느낌을 삼켰다—\"하지만 진실하고 충실한 봉사가 제게 말할 권리를 준다고 생각합니다. 그러니 지금 옳든 그르든, 저는 말해야 하고 또 말하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리냐, 이 여자야?\" 돔비 씨가 그녀를 노려보며 말했다. \"어찌 감히?\"\n\n\"제가 드리려는 말씀은, 나리, 공손하게, 모욕 없이, 그러나 거리낌 없이 드리는 것이옵니다. 어찌 감히 그러는지는 저도 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그렇게 합니다!\" 수전이 말했다. \"오! 나리께서는 제 아가씨를 모르십니다, 나리, 정말 모르세요. 아셨다면 결코 그렇게까지 모르실 리가 없지요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 노발대발하여 종 끈을 잡으려고 손을 뻗었다. 그러나 난롯불 그쪽 편에는 종 끈이 없었고, 그는 부축 없이는 일어나 반대편으로 건너갈 수가 없었다. 닙퍼의 재빠른 눈은 그의 무력함을 즉시 알아챘고, 나중에 그녀가 술회한 대로, 이제 그를 손아귀에 넣었다는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n\"플로이 아가씨는,\" 수전 닙퍼가 말했다, \"가장 헌신적이고 가장 인내심 많고 가장 효성스럽고 아름다운 따님이십니다. 영국에서 가장 위대하고 가장 부유한 이들을 다 합친 것만큼 위대하고 부유한 신사라 할지라도, 그 어떤 신사도, 그 누구도, 아가씨를 자랑스러워하지 않을 수가 없고, 또 자랑스러워하는 것이 마땅합니다. 그가 아가씨의 가치를 제대로 안다면, 차라리 자기 위엄과 재산을 한 조각씩 잃고 누더기를 걸친 채 이 집 저 집 빌어먹는 한이 있더라도—누구에게든 말하지만, 정말 그럴 겁니다!\" 수전 닙퍼가 울음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"이 집에서 제가 그토록 고통받는 것을 보아 온 그 여린 가슴에 슬픔을 안겨 주느니 그렇게 하겠지요!\"\n\n\"여자야,\" 돔비 씨가 외쳤다, \"방에서 나가라.\"\n\n\"송구하오나, 나리, 설령 일자리를 떠나야 한다 해도 나가지 않겠습니다,\" 흔들림 없는 닙퍼가 대답했다, \"제가 그토록 오랜 세월을 보내고 그토록 많은 것을 보아 온 이 자리를요—나리께서 그런 이유로 저를 플로이 아가씨 곁에서 내쫓으실 만큼 매정하지는 않으시리라 바라옵니다만—나머지 할 말을 다 할 때까지는 지금 나가지 않겠습니다. 저는 인도의 과부가 아니고, 그렇지도 않고, 그렇게 되고 싶지도 않습니다만, 일단 제 몸을 산 채로 태우겠다고 마음을 먹는다면, 정말 그렇게 할 겁니다! 그리고 저는 끝까지 말하기로 마음을 먹었습니다.\"\n\n* 인도의 과부: 남편의 화장 장작더미에 함께 몸을 던져 순사하는 인도의 풍습(수티)을 따른 여인.\n\n그 말이 사실임은, 수전 닙퍼의 말 못지않게 그녀의 얼굴 표정으로도 또렷이 드러났다.\n\"나리를 모시는 이들 가운데,\" 그 검은 눈의 처녀가 말을 이었다, \"저보다 더 나리를 두려워해 온 사람은 없습니다. 그것이 얼마나 진실인지는, 제가 감히 이런 말씀을 드리는 것을 보시면 아실 겁니다. 저는 나리께 말씀드리려고 수백 번도 더 생각했지만, 어젯밤까지는 도무지 마음을 굳히지 못했습니다. 하지만 어젯밤이 저를 결심하게 만들었지요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 격분의 발작 속에서, 그 자리에 있지도 않은 종 끈을 다시 한번 움켜잡으려 했고, 그것이 없으니 차라리 자기 머리카락을 쥐어뜯었다.\n\n\"저는 보았습니다,\" 수전 닙퍼가 말했다, \"플로이 아가씨가 어렸을 적, 그토록 상냥하고 인내심 깊어 가장 훌륭한 여인들도 본받을 만한 그 어린것이 애쓰고 또 애쓰는 것을요. 여린 동생분의 공부를 돕느라 밤마다 거의 한밤중까지 앉아 계신 것을 보았고, 다른 때에도 동생분을 돕고 지켜보시는 것을 보았습니다—누구는 잘 알지요 언제인지를—저는 보았습니다, 격려도 도움도 없이, 아가씨가 자라나 숙녀가 되시는 것을요, 고맙게도! 어느 모임에 가든 그 모임의 품격이요 자랑이 되는 분으로 말입니다. 그리고 저는 늘 보았습니다, 아가씨가 모질게 외면당하면서도 그것을 뼈저리게 느끼시는 것을요—누구에게든 말하지만, 정말 그랬습니다!—그러면서도 한마디도 하지 않았지요. 하지만 윗사람에게 겸손하고 공손히 처신한다는 것이 새긴 우상을 숭배하는 것은 아니지 않습니까. 그러니 저는 말하겠고, 또 말해야만 합니다!\"\n\n\"거기 누구 없느냐?\" 돔비 씨가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"사내들은 어디 있느냐? 여자들은 어디 있느냐? 거기 아무도 없느냐?\"\n\n\"저는 간밤 늦도록 제 사랑하는 아가씨를 잠자리에 들지 못하게 두었습니다,\" 수전이 조금도 멈추지 않고 말했다, \"그리고 저는 그 까닭을 알지요, 나리께서 편찮으셨고 아가씨는 얼마나 편찮으신지 모르셨으니까요. 그것만으로도 아가씨를 비참하게 만들기에 충분했고, 저는 정말 그러신 것을 보았습니다. 저는 공작새는 아닐지언정, 두 눈은 있습니다—그래서 저는 제 방에서 잠시 앉아 있었지요. 아가씨가 외로워하시며 저를 찾으실지도 모른다는 생각에서요. 그리고 저는 아가씨가 살그머니 아래층으로 내려와, 마치 친아버지를 바라보는 것이 죄라도 되는 양 이 방문 앞에 왔다가, 다시 살그머니 돌아가 저 적막한 응접실로 들어가서는, 차마 들을 수가 없을 만큼 울고 또 우시는 것을 보았습니다. 저는 그것을 차마 들을 수가 없습니다,\" 수전 닙퍼가 검은 눈을 훔치며, 돔비 씨의 격노한 얼굴에 그 눈을 굽힘 없이 고정한 채 말했다. \"그 소리를 들은 것이 이번이 처음도 아닙니다, 헤아릴 수 없이 여러 번 들었지요. 나리께서는 친따님을 모르십니다, 나리. 나리께서는 지금 무슨 짓을 하고 계신지 모르십니다, 나리. 누구에게든 말하지만,\" 수전 닙퍼가 마지막으로 폭발하며 외쳤다, \"이것은 죄스러운 수치입니다!\"\n\n\"아니, 이런 발칙한 것 봤나!\" 핍친 부인의 목소리가 외쳤다. 동시에 그 어여쁜 페루 광산 마님의 검은 봄바진 옷자락이 방 안으로 휩쓸려 들어왔다. \"이게 대체 무슨 일이람?\"\n\n* 봄바진: 견사와 모직을 섞어 짠 검은빛 천으로, 흔히 상복에 쓰였다.\n수전은 핍친 부인을 위해, 둘이 처음 알게 되었을 때 그녀에게만 쓰려고 따로 고안해 둔 눈빛을 베풀어 주고는, 대답할 일은 돔비 씨에게 넘겼다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 일이냐고?\" 돔비 씨가 거의 거품을 물고 되뇌었다. \"이게 무슨 일이냐, 부인? 이 집안을 통솔하며 질서를 유지할 의무를 진 당신이야말로 물어볼 자격이 있겠지. 이 여자를 아시오?\"\n\n\"저 애에 대해서는 좋은 점이라곤 거의 모릅니다, 나리,\" 핍친 부인이 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"어찌 감히 여기 왔느냐, 이 계집애야? 썩 물러가지 못해!\"\n\n그러나 굽힐 줄 모르는 닙퍼는, 핍친 부인에게 또 한 번 눈길을 베풀어 줄 뿐, 그대로 머물러 있었다.\n\n\"이 집안을 관리한다는 게 고작 이런 거요, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"이런 자가 멋대로 와서 내게 말을 늘어놓게 내버려 두는 것이? 신사가—제 집에서—제 방에서—하녀들의 무례에 시달리다니!\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 나리,\" 핍친 부인이 그 단단한 잿빛 눈에 앙심을 담고 응수했다, \"저로서도 지극히 통탄스럽습니다. 이보다 더 도리에 어긋난 일도 없고, 이보다 더 분수와 이치를 벗어난 일도 없지요. 하지만 송구하게도 말씀드리자면, 나리, 이 젊은 계집은 도무지 통제가 안 됩니다. 돔비 아가씨가 버릇을 잘못 들여서, 그 누구의 말도 듣지 않습니다. 너도 알지, 안 그러냐,\" 핍친 부인이 날카롭게, 수전 닙퍼를 향해 머리를 흔들며 말했다. \"부끄러운 줄 알아라, 이 계집애야! 썩 물러가!\"\n\n\"내 밑에 통제할 수 없는 자들이 있다면, 핍친 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 다시 난롯불 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다, \"그런 자들을 어떻게 처리해야 할지는 당신이 알 거라 짐작하오. 당신이 여기 무엇 때문에 있는지는 아시겠지? 데리고 나가시오!\"\n\n\"나리, 저도 어찌해야 할지 압니다,\" 핍친 부인이 받아쳤다, \"물론 그리하겠습니다. 수전 닙퍼,\" 그녀는 유난히 매몰차게 그녀의 말을 끊었다, \"지금 이 시각부터 한 달 뒤에 그만두는 거다.\"\n\n\"오, 그러시겠다고요!\" 수전이 거만하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래,\" 핍친 부인이 응수했다, \"그리고 내게 웃지 마라, 이 깍쟁이야, 안 그러면 무슨 영문인지 내가 알아낼 테니! 당장 물러가지 못해!\"\n\n\"당장 나가려던 참이에요, 그건 믿으셔도 됩니다,\" 수다스러운 닙퍼가 말했다. \"저는 이 집에서 제 아가씨를 모신 지 십이 년인데, 핍친이라는 이름을 가진 사람이 통보했다고 해서 단 한 시간도 더 머물지 않을 겁니다, 그건 믿으세요, 핍친 여사님.\"\n\n\"못된 쓰레기 잘 치웠다!\" 그 노발대발한 노부인이 말했다. \"냉큼 나가, 안 그러면 끌어내게 할 테니!\"\n\n\"제게 위안이 되는 건,\" 수전이 돔비 씨를 돌아보며 말했다, \"오늘 제가 진작 했어야 마땅하고 아무리 자주, 아무리 분명하게 해도 지나치지 않을 한 가지 진실을 말했다는 거예요. 그리고 어떤 핍친들도—그 수가 부디 많지 않기를 바랍니다만\" (여기서 핍친 부인이 매우 날카롭게 \"썩 물러가!\"라고 내뱉었고, 닙퍼 양은 그 눈빛을 되풀이했다) \"제가 한 말을 거두게 할 수는 없을 거예요, 설령 그들이 오전 열 시에 시작해서 밤 열두 시까지 한순간도 쉬지 않고 통보를 해 대다가 기진맥진해 죽는 일이 일 년 내내 이어진다 해도 말이죠—그렇게만 된다면 정말 경사일 텐데요!\"\n\n이 말과 함께, 닙퍼 양은 자기 원수를 앞세워 방에서 나갔다. 그리고 분기탱천한 핍친의 숨 막히는 분노를 뒤로하고, 더없이 위풍당당하게 자기 방으로 올라가, 자기 짐 상자들 사이에 주저앉아 울기 시작했다.\n\n이 가냘픈 심사에서 그녀는, 매우 건전하고 상쾌한 효과와 함께, 문밖에서 들려온 핍친 부인의 목소리에 곧 깨어났다.\n\n\"저 뻔뻔스러운 계집이,\" 흉포한 핍친이 말했다, \"통보를 받아들이겠다는 거냐, 아니냐?\"\n\n닙퍼 양은 안에서 대답하기를, 방금 말한 그런 사람은 이쪽 부분에 살지 않으며, 그 이름은 핍친이고, 가정부 방에 가면 찾을 수 있을 거라고 했다.\n\n\"이 건방진 화냥년!\" 핍친 부인이 문 손잡이를 덜컹덜컹 흔들며 받아쳤다. \"당장 물러가. 짐을 즉시 싸! 어떻게 감히 옛날에는 더 좋은 시절을 보냈던 양가집 여인에게 이런 식으로 말을 해?\"\n\n이에 닙퍼 양은 자기 성채 안에서 대꾸하기를, 핍친 부인을 겪어야 했던 그 좋은 시절이 가엾기 짝이 없다고, 그리고 자기로 말할 것 같으면 일 년 중 가장 나쁜 날이 저 마님 수준에 딱 맞다고 생각한다고, 다만 그런 날조차 저 마님에게는 너무 과분하다고 했다.\n\n\"하지만 굳이 수고스럽게 제 방문 앞에서 소란을 피우실 것도,\" 수전 닙퍼가 말했다, \"그 눈으로 열쇠 구멍을 더럽히실 것도 없어요. 저는 짐을 싸서 나갈 테니까요, 맹세하셔도 좋아요.\"\n\n이 소식에 노마님은 활기찬 만족을 표하고는, 젊은 계집들이라는 족속 일반에 대한, 특히 돔비 아가씨에게 버릇이 잘못 들여진 뒤의 그들의 결함에 대한 몇 가지 일반론을 늘어놓은 다음, 닙퍼의 품삯을 마련하러 물러갔다. 그러자 수전은 즉각 위엄 있게 떠날 수 있도록 짐 상자들을 정돈하느라 분주히 움직였다. 그러면서 내내, 플로렌스를 생각하며 진심으로 흐느꼈다.\n\n그녀가 애석해하는 바로 그 대상은 오래지 않아 그녀에게 찾아왔다. 수전 닙퍼가 핍친 부인과 한바탕 소동을 벌였다는 것, 둘 다 돔비 씨에게 호소했다는 것, 돔비 씨 방에서 전례 없는 일이 벌어졌다는 것, 그리고 수전이 떠난다는 것이 곧 온 집안에 퍼졌기 때문이다. 이 어수선한 소문의 뒷부분이 정확하다는 것을 플로렌스는 알게 되었으니, 그녀가 방에 들어섰을 때 수전은 마지막 짐 상자를 잠그고 보닛을 쓴 채 그 위에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"수전!\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다. \"나를 떠난다고! 네가!\"\n\n\"오, 제발 부탁이에요, 플로이 아가씨,\" 수전이 흐느끼며 말했다, \"제게 한마디도 하지 마세요, 안 그러면 저 핍-친들 앞에서 추태를 부리고 말 거예요. 무슨 일이 있어도 그들에게 제가 우는 꼴은 보이고 싶지 않다고요, 플로이 아가씨!\"\n\n\"수전!\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"내 사랑하는 아이, 내 오랜 벗아! 너 없이 나는 어떻게 하니! 어떻게 그렇게 떠날 수가 있어?\"\n\n\"못, 못, 못 해요, 제 사랑하는 플로이 아가씨, 정말 못 합니다,\" 수전이 흐느꼈다. \"하지만 어쩔 도리가 없어요. 저는 제 도리를 다했어요, 아가씨, 정말로요. 제 잘못이 아니랍니다. 저는 다 체념했어요. 한 달을 더 머물 수가 없어요, 안 그러면 그때 가서는 도저히 사랑하는 아가씨를 떠날 수 없을 테니까요. 처음에나 마지막에나 어차피 떠나야 한다면, 제게 말을 걸지 마세요, 플로이 아가씨, 제가 제법 굳세긴 해도 대리석 문기둥은 아니거든요, 사랑하는 아가씨.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이니? 왜 그래?\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"내게 말해 주지 않을 거야?\" 수전이 머리를 흔들고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"안, 안 돼요, 사랑하는 아가씨,\" 수전이 대답했다. \"묻지 마세요, 저는 말씀드릴 수 없으니까요. 그리고 무슨 일이 있어도 저를 붙들겠다는 말씀은 하지 마세요, 그럴 수가 없고 아가씨 스스로를 그르치실 뿐이니까요. 그러니 부디 신의 가호가 있기를, 제 소중한 아가씨, 그리고 이 오랜 세월 동안 제가 끼친 어떤 해악이든, 제가 부린 어떤 성깔이든 용서해 주세요!\"\n\n이 간청을 진심으로 토해 내며, 수전은 두 팔로 자기 아가씨를 끌어안았다.\n\n\"사랑하는 아가씨, 아가씨를 모시러 올 사람도, 기꺼이 모실 사람도, 또 잘되고 진실하게 모실 사람도 많을 거예요,\" 수전이 말했다, \"하지만 저처럼 다정히 모시거나 저만큼 절반이라도 애틋이 사랑할 사람은 없을 거예요, 그게 제 위안이랍니다. 안, 안녕히, 어여쁜 플로이 아가씨!\"\n\n\"어디로 갈 거니, 수전?\" 눈물짓는 아가씨가 물었다.\n\n\"시골에 오빠가 하나 있어요, 아가씨—에식스에서 농사를 짓지요,\" 가슴이 미어지는 닙퍼가 말했다, \"소도, 돼지도 아주 많이 기른답니다. 저는 거기 마차를 타고 내려가 오빠랑 지낼 거예요. 저는 신경 쓰지 마세요, 저축은행에 돈도 좀 있으니까 당분간은 다른 일자리를 구할 필요도 없어요, 그럴 수도, 그럴 수도, 그럴 수도 없고요, 제 마음의 주인이신 아가씨!\" 수전은 슬픔의 폭발로 말을 맺었는데, 마침 아래층에서 핍친 부인이 말하는 목소리가 들려 그 슬픔이 알맞게 끊겼다. 그 소리를 듣자 그녀는 붉게 부어오른 눈을 닦고, 토울린슨 씨에게 마차를 불러 짐 상자들을 내려 달라고 짐짓 쾌활한 척 부르는 시늉을 우울하게 해 보였다.\n\n플로렌스는 창백하고 황급하고 괴로워하면서도, 아버지와 그 부인 사이에 새로운 불화를 일으킬까 하는 두려움에(몇 분 전 그 부인의 엄하고 분노한 얼굴이 그녀에게 경고가 되었다), 또 자기가 어떤 식으로든 자기도 모르는 새 이미 오랜 하녀이자 벗의 해고에 얽혀 있는 것은 아닐까 하는 우려에, 여기서마저 쓸데없이 끼어드는 것을 삼간 채, 울면서 아래층 이디스의 화장실로 따라 내려갔다. 수전이 작별 인사로 무릎을 굽혀 절을 하려고 그리로 간 것이었다.\n\n\"자, 마차도 왔고, 짐 상자도 여기 있으니, 어서 물러가지 못해!\" 마침 그 순간 모습을 드러내며 핍친 부인이 말했다. \"송구합니다, 마님, 그러나 돔비 씨의 명령이 지엄하셔서요.\"\n\n이디스는 하녀의 손길에 몸을 맡긴 채—그녀는 만찬에 나가려던 참이었다—오만한 얼굴을 그대로 지키며 조금도 아랑곳하지 않았다.\n\n\"여기 네 품삯이다,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 자기 방식에 따라, 또 광산 시절을 떠올리며, 하인들을 브라이턴의 어린 하숙생들을 닦달하던 식으로 닦달하는 데 익숙해져 있었으니, 그 탓에 비더스톤 도련님은 영영 시큰둥해지고 말았던 것이다. \"그리고 이 집이 네 뒷모습을 보는 게 빠르면 빠를수록 좋겠구나.\"\n\n수전은 마 핍친에게 마땅히 베풀어 주어야 할 그 눈빛조차 보낼 기운이 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 돔비 부인에게 무릎을 굽혀 절을 하고(부인은 한마디 말도 없이 고개를 끄덕였고, 그 눈은 플로렌스 외에는 누구도 피했다), 자기 어린 아가씨에게 마지막 작별의 포옹을 한 번 안기고, 그 답례로 마지막 포옹을 받았다. 이 위기의 순간, 가엾은 수전의 얼굴은, 그 감정의 격렬함과, 흐느낌 소리 하나라도 새어 나가 핍친 부인에게 승리감을 안겨 줄세라 기를 쓰고 그것을 틀어막는 통에, 일찍이 목격된 적 없는 가장 기상천외한 일련의 관상학적 현상을 펼쳐 보였다.\n\n\"송구합니다, 아가씨, 정말로요,\" 짐 상자들과 함께 문밖에 있던 토울린슨이 플로렌스에게 말을 건넸다, \"하지만 투츠 도련님이 응접실에 와 계시는데, 안부를 전하시며 디오게네스와 도련님이 어찌 지내시는지 여쭙고 싶다 하십니다.\"\n\n생각만큼이나 잽싸게, 플로렌스는 슬며시 빠져나와 아래층으로 서둘러 내려갔다. 거기에는 더없이 화려한 옷을 차려입은 투츠 씨가, 그녀가 올지 어떨지 마음을 졸이며 몹시 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬고 있었다.\n\n\"오, 안녕하세요, 돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"맙소사!\"\n\n이 마지막 외침은, 플로렌스의 얼굴에 어린 괴로움을 보고 깊이 염려한 투츠 씨에게서 비롯된 것이었다. 그 바람에 그는 한바탕 킬킬거리려던 것을 뚝 멈추고, 절망의 화신이 되고 말았다.\n\n\"투츠 씨,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"당신은 제게 참으로 다정하고 참으로 정직하셔서, 제가 부탁 하나쯤 드려도 되겠다 싶어요.\"\n\n\"돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"부디 하나만 말씀해 주시면, 그것이—그것이 제게 식욕을 돋워 줄 겁니다. 그 식욕이라는 것을,\" 투츠 씨가 약간의 감회를 담아 말했다, \"저는 오래도록 낯설게 지내 왔답니다.\"\n\n\"제 오랜 벗인 수전이, 제가 둔 가장 오랜 벗인 수전이,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"갑자기 이 집을 떠나게 되었어요, 그것도 외톨이로, 가엾은 아이가요. 집으로 돌아가는데, 시골로 좀 들어가는 거예요. 그 애가 마차에 탈 때까지 보살펴 주시기를 부탁드려도 될까요?\"\n\n\"돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"참으로 제게 영광이고 은혜로운 일입니다. 제가 브라이턴에서 그토록 짐승처럼 굴었는데도 이렇게 저를 믿어 주시다니—\"\n\n\"네,\" 플로렌스가 황급히 말했다—\"아니에요—그 일은 생각지 마세요. 그럼 친절을 베푸셔서—가 주시겠어요? 그리고 그 애가 나오면 맞이할 준비를 하고 계셔 주시겠어요? 천번만번 고맙습니다! 덕분에 제 마음이 한결 놓여요. 그 애가 그렇게까지 외롭지는 않겠지요. 제가 당신께 얼마나 고마운지, 당신이 얼마나 좋은 벗인지, 짐작도 못 하실 거예요!\" 플로렌스는 간절한 마음으로 그에게 거듭거듭 고마워했다. 그러자 투츠 씨도 간절한 마음으로 서둘러 자리를 떴다—다만 뒷걸음으로, 그녀의 모습을 한순간도 놓치지 않으려고 그렇게 했다.\n\n핍친 부인이 가엾은 수전을 몰아내고, 디오게네스가 그녀 주위를 깡충거리며 그 봄바진 치맛자락에 덥석 물어 대는 통에 핍친 부인을 극도로 질겁하게 만들고, 그녀의 목소리만 들려도 괴로워 울부짖는—그 선량한 시중꾼 노파야말로 디오게네스의 가슴속에서 가장 애지중지하는 혐오의 대상이었으니—그 광경을 현관에서 본 플로렌스는, 차마 나갈 용기가 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 수전이 하인들과 일일이 악수하고, 한 번 돌아서서 자기 옛집을 바라보는 것을 보았다. 또 디오게네스가 마차를 뒤쫓아 뛰쳐나가 따라가고 싶어 하며, 그 마차 삯에 더는 자기 몫이 없다는 사실을 도무지 믿지 못하겠다는 듯 항변하는 것을 보았다. 그러고는 문이 닫혔고, 소동이 끝났으며, 그 누구도 대신할 수 없는 오랜 벗을 잃은 슬픔에 그녀의 눈물이 빠르게 흘렀다. 그 누구도. 그 누구도.\n\n충실하고 미더운 사람인 투츠 씨는, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 그 마차를 세우고 수전 닙퍼에게 자기가 맡은 임무를 일러 주었는데, 그 말에 그녀는 전보다 더 울었다.\n\n\"맹세코, 정말이지!\" 투츠 씨가 그녀 곁에 자리를 잡으며 말했다. \"당신 심정이 절절히 와닿습니다. 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 제가 짐작하는 것만큼이나 당신 자신도 자기 심정을 더 잘 알 수는 없을 거라 봅니다. 돔비 아가씨를 떠나야만 하는 것보다 더 끔찍한 일은 상상할 수가 없어요.\"\n\n이제 수전은 슬픔에 자기를 온전히 내맡겼고, 그 모습은 정말로 가슴 뭉클했다.\n\n\"저기요,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"그러지 말아요! 아니, 그러니까, 좀 그렇게 해요, 아시겠죠!\"\n\n\"무얼 그렇게 하라는 거예요, 투츠 씨!\" 수전이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그러니까, 제 집으로 가서, 출발하기 전에 식사를 좀 하자는 거예요,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"제 요리사는 더없이 점잖은 여인이거든요—제가 본 사람 중에 가장 자애로운 사람이지요—당신을 편안하게 해 주는 걸 기뻐할 겁니다. 그 사람 아들은,\" 투츠 씨가 덧붙이는 추천의 말로 말했다, \"블루코트 학교에서 교육을 받았고, 화약 공장에서 폭발에 휩쓸린 적이 있답니다.\"\n\n* 블루코트 학교: 가난한 아이들을 위해 세워진 자선 교육기관 크라이스트 호스피털의 별칭.\n수전이 이 친절한 제안을 받아들이자, 투츠 씨는 그녀를 자기 거처로 데려갔고, 거기서 두 사람은 앞서 말한 그 가정부의 영접을 받았는데, 그 가정부는 투츠 씨가 평한 그대로의 사람임을 톡톡히 입증했다. 또 치킨의 영접도 받았는데, 치킨은 마차에 부인이 한 사람 타고 있는 것을 보고는, 처음에는 자기가 늘 권하던 대로 돔비 씨가 두 동강 나고 돔비 아가씨가 납치된 줄로 알았다. 이 신사는 닙퍼 양에게 상당한 놀라움을 불러일으켰으니, 라키 보이에게 패한 탓에 그의 얼굴이 어찌나 처참하게 망가져 있었던지, 보는 이들이 마음 편히 사교 자리에 내놓기 어려울 지경이었기 때문이다. 치킨 본인은 이 응징을, 시합 초반에 운수 사납게도 코너에 몰려 라키 녀석에게 호되게 두들겨 맞고 보기 좋게 매트에 나뒹군 탓이라고 돌렸다. 그러나 그 대접전의 공개된 기록에 따르면, 라키 보이가 처음부터 끝까지 일방적으로 경기를 주도했고, 치킨은 얻어맞고, 코피가 터지고, 매운맛을 보고, 비틀거리게 되고, 헐떡이며 일어나고, 그와 비슷한 갖가지 기묘한 곤욕을 줄줄이 견디다가, 끝내 결딴이 나고 마무리되었다는 것이었다.\n\n푸짐한 식사와 융숭한 대접을 마친 뒤, 수전은 또 다른 이륜마차를 타고 역마차 정류소로 출발했는데, 이번에도 투츠 씨가 안에 타고, 치킨이 마부석에 앉았다. 치킨은 그 인품의 도덕적 무게와 영웅적 기개로 이 작은 일행에 어떤 위엄을 더해 주기는 했으되, 물리적으로 말하자면 그 무수히 붙인 반창고들 때문에 일행을 꾸며 주는 구실은 거의 하지 못했다. 그러나 치킨은 은밀히 한 가지 맹세를 새겨 두었으니, 어느 술집의 영업권과 비품 일체보다 못한 대가로는 결코 투츠 씨를 떠나지 않겠다는 것이었다(투츠 씨는 은밀히 그를 떨쳐 내고 싶어 안달이 나 있었다). 그리고 그 방면으로 진출해 되도록 빨리 술로 자멸하고픈 야심을 품고 있었기에, 그는 자기 곁을 달갑잖게 만드는 것이 자기가 취할 수단이라 여겼다.\n\n수전이 타고 떠날 야간 역마차가 막 출발하려던 참이었다. 투츠 씨는 그녀를 안에 태우고 나서, 마부가 마차에 오르려 할 때까지 우물쭈물 창가에서 머뭇거렸다. 그러다가 발판에 올라서서, 등불 빛에 비친 불안하고 어수선한 얼굴을 들이밀며 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"저기요, 수전! 돔비 아가씨 말인데요—\"\n\n\"네, 도련님.\"\n\n\"혹시 그분이—그러니까—응?\"\n\n\"송구하지만, 투츠 씨,\" 수전이 말했다, \"무슨 말씀인지 안 들려요.\"\n\n\"혹시 그분이, 그러니까—당장은 아니더라도, 세월이 지나면—오랜 세월이 지나면—저를—저를 사랑하게 될 수 있을까요? 그게 말이죠!\" 가엾은 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 어림없어요!\" 수전이 머리를 흔들며 대답했다. \"결코 안 될 거라고, 저는 말씀드리겠어요. 결코요!\"\n\n\"고마워요!\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"괜찮습니다. 안녕히 가세요. 괜찮아요, 고맙습니다!\"\n\n# CHAPTER XLV. The Trusty Agent\n\n그날 이디스는 혼자 외출했다가 일찍 집으로 돌아왔다. 그녀의 마차가 그녀가 사는 거리를 굴러올 때는 열 시를 겨우 몇 분 넘긴 시각이었다.\n\n그녀의 얼굴에는 옷을 차려입을 때 보였던 것과 똑같은 억지스러운 침착함이 어려 있었고, 머리에 두른 화관은 똑같이 차갑고 흔들림 없는 이마를 둘러싸고 있었다. 그러나 그 화관의 잎과 꽃이 격정에 찬 손에 갈가리 찢겨 조각나거나, 두근거리고 갈피를 잃은 머리가 어디든 쉴 곳을 찾아 변덕스레 더듬는 통에 형체를 잃은 모습을 보는 편이, 그런 평정을 장식하고 있는 것보다는 차라리 나았을 것이다. 그토록 완고하고, 그토록 다가갈 수 없으며, 그토록 가차 없으니, 그런 여인의 본성은 무엇으로도 부드럽게 할 수 없을 것이며, 삶의 모든 것이 그것을 단단하게 굳혀 놓았으리라고 사람들은 생각했을 것이다.\n\n자기 집 문 앞에 이르러 그녀가 마차에서 내리려 할 때, 누군가가 현관에서 조용히 나와 모자를 벗은 채 그녀에게 팔을 내밀었다. 하인이 옆으로 밀려난 터라 그녀는 그 팔을 잡는 것 외에 다른 도리가 없었고, 그제야 그것이 누구의 팔인지를 알았다.\n\n\"환자분은 좀 어떠신가요, 선생?\" 그녀가 입술을 비틀며 물었다.\n\n\"좋아지셨습니다,\" 카커가 대답했다. \"아주 잘 회복되고 계십니다. 밤이라 저는 그분 곁을 떠나왔지요.\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 숙이고 계단을 올라가려 했는데, 그가 뒤를 따라오며 계단 아래에서 말했다.\n\n\"부인! 잠시 뵙는 영광을 청해도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 눈길을 뒤로 돌렸다. \"지금은 때가 아닙니다, 선생. 게다가 저는 피곤합니다. 급한 용무인가요?\"\n\n\"매우 급합니다,\" 카커가 대답했다. \"이렇게 운 좋게 부인을 뵙게 되었으니, 제 청을 밀어붙이도록 허락해 주십시오.\"\n\n그녀는 잠시 그의 번들거리는 입을 내려다보았고, 그는 위쪽 위풍당당한 차림으로 서 있는 그녀를 올려다보며, 다시 한번 그녀가 얼마나 아름다운지를 생각했다.\n\n\"돔비 아가씨는 어디 있느냐?\" 그녀가 하인에게 큰 소리로 물었다.\n\n\"오전 거실에 계십니다, 마님.\"\n\n\"그리로 안내해라!\" 계단 아래에서 주의 깊게 기다리는 신사에게 다시 눈길을 돌리고, 따라와도 좋다는 것을 가벼운 고갯짓으로 알린 뒤, 그녀는 앞서 나아갔다.\n\n\"실례합니다! 부인! 돔비 부인!\" 부드럽고 날렵한 카커가 어느새 그녀의 옆에 와서 외쳤다. \"돔비 아가씨가 자리에 없도록 해 주시기를 간청해도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n그녀는 재빠른 눈길로 그를 마주 보았으나, 똑같이 자제하며 흔들림이 없었다.\n\n\"제가 드릴 말씀을 돔비 아가씨가 알지 못하도록 해 드리고 싶습니다,\" 카커가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"적어도, 부인, 아가씨가 그것을 알게 할지 말지는 부인의 결정에 맡기고자 합니다. 그것이 제가 부인께 마땅히 해야 할 도리입니다. 부인께 진 빚이지요. 지난번 우리가 만난 일을 생각하면, 제가 달리 행동한다면 그것은 끔찍한 일이 될 것입니다.\"\n\n그녀는 천천히 그의 얼굴에서 눈을 거두고, 하인에게로 돌아서서 말했다. \"다른 방으로.\" 하인은 어느 응접실로 안내하여 재빨리 불을 밝히고는 두 사람을 남겨 두고 나갔다. 그가 머무는 동안에는 한마디도 오가지 않았다. 이디스는 난롯가 긴 의자에 옥좌에 오르듯 앉았고, 카커 씨는 모자를 손에 든 채 양탄자에 눈을 떨군 채로, 그녀에게서 조금 떨어진 자리에 섰다.\n\n\"선생의 말씀을 듣기 전에,\" 문이 닫히자 이디스가 말했다, \"제 말을 먼저 들어주시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"돔비 부인의 말씀을 듣는 것은,\" 그가 대답했다, \"비록 마땅치 않은 책망의 어조라 하더라도, 제가 그토록 크게 여기는 영광이라, 설령 제가 만사에 부인의 종이 아니라 해도, 그런 바람에는 기꺼이 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"만일 방금 두고 온 그 사람에게서,\" 카커 씨는 마치 놀란 척을 꾸미려는 듯 눈을 들었으나, 그녀가 그 눈을 마주쳐 만약 그럴 의도였다면 그를 막아 버렸다. \"저에게 전할 어떤 전갈이라도 받으셨다면, 그것을 전하려 들지 마십시오. 저는 받지 않겠으니까요. 선생이 그런 심부름으로 오셨는지 굳이 물을 것도 없겠지요. 저는 한동안 선생을 기다려 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"이런 목적으로, 전적으로 제 뜻에 반하여 이곳에 있게 된 것은,\" 그가 대답했다, \"저의 불운입니다. 제가 두 가지 목적으로 여기 왔다는 것을 말씀드리도록 허락해 주십시오. 그것은 그중 하나입니다.\"\n\n\"그 하나는, 선생,\" 그녀가 대답했다, \"끝났습니다. 혹시 다시 그 이야기로 돌아오신다면—\"\n\n\"돔비 부인께서,\" 카커가 더 가까이 다가오며 말했다, \"금하신 것을 무릅쓰고 제가 그 이야기로 돌아오리라 여기실 수 있겠습니까? 돔비 부인께서, 저의 불운한 처지는 헤아리지 않으시고, 저를 제 지시자와 떼어 놓을 수 없는 사람으로만 여기시어 저에게 크고도 고의적인 부당함을 안기시려 그토록 마음을 굳히셨단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"선생,\" 이디스가 어두운 눈길을 그에게 가득 쏟으며, 점점 차오르는 격정으로 그녀의 도도한 콧방울과 부풀어 오른 목을 한껏 일으키고, 눈처럼 흰 깃의 이웃을 감당할 수 있는 어깨 위로 헐겁게 걸친 옷의 섬세한 흰 솜털을 흔들리게 하면서 말했다, \"어찌하여 선생은 지금까지 해 온 것처럼 제 앞에 나타나, 남편에 대한 사랑이며 의무를 운운하고, 제가 행복한 결혼 생활을 하고 있으며 그를 존경한다고 믿는 척하십니까? 선생이 알면서—저도 그보다 더 잘은 모릅니다, 선생. 저는 선생의 모든 눈길에서 그것을 보았고 모든 말에서 그것을 들었으니까요—우리 사이에 애정이 있기는커녕 혐오와 경멸만이 있으며, 그의 아내가 된 저 자신을 경멸하는 것 못지않게 제가 그를 경멸한다는 것을 알면서, 어찌 감히 저를 그렇게 모욕하시는 겁니까? 부당함이라! 선생이 제게 겪게 한 그 고통과, 선생이 제게 안긴 모욕에 대한 저의 감정에 정당한 응답을 했더라면, 저는 선생을 죽여 버렸을 것입니다!\"\n\n그녀는 그가 왜 이러는지 물었다. 그녀가 자신의 자존심과 분노, 그리고 자기비하에 눈이 멀지만 않았더라면—그토록 사납게 그에게 눈길을 쏟고 있었으니 실제로 눈이 멀어 있었지만—그의 얼굴에서 그 답을 보았을 것이다. 그녀를 이런 고백에 이르게 하는 것, 그것이 답이었다.\n\n그녀는 그것을 보지 못했고, 그것이 거기 있든 없든 개의치 않았다. 그녀는 오직 자신이 겪었고 또 겪어야 할 모욕과 몸부림만을 보았으며, 그 아래에서 괴로워 몸을 뒤틀었다. 그를 본다기보다 그것들을 응시하며 앉아 있는 동안, 그녀는 황금 실로 손목에 매달려 부채 노릇을 하던, 어느 진귀하고 아름다운 새의 날개에서 깃털을 뜯어내어 바닥에 비처럼 흩뿌렸다.\n\n그는 그녀의 눈길 아래에서 움츠러들지 않고, 그녀의 통제를 벗어난 분노의 겉 표시들이 가라앉을 때까지, 충분한 대답을 마련해 두고 곧 그것을 내놓으려는 사람의 태도로 서 있었다. 그러고는 타오르는 그녀의 두 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"부인,\" 그가 말했다, \"저는 오늘 이전부터, 제가 부인의 호의를 얻지 못했다는 것을 알고 있었고, 그 까닭도 알고 있었습니다. 그렇습니다. 저는 그 까닭을 알았지요. 부인께서 그토록 솔직하게 말씀해 주셨으니, 부인의 신임을 얻게 되어 이렇게 마음이 놓이는지라—\"\n\n\"신임이라니!\" 그녀가 경멸하며 되받았다.\n\n그는 그 말을 그냥 흘려 넘겼다.\n\n\"—저는 아무것도 숨기는 척하지 않겠습니다. 저는 처음부터, 부인 쪽에 돔비 씨에 대한 애정이 없다는 것을 알았습니다—그토록 다른 두 사람 사이에 어찌 애정이 있을 수 있겠습니까? 그리고 그 이후로, 무관심보다 더 강한 감정이 부인의 가슴속에 생겨났다는 것도 알았습니다—부인께서 처하신 상황에서 어찌 그렇지 않을 수 있겠습니까? 그러나 제가 이 앎을 부인께 그토록 노골적인 말로 감히 털어놓을 처지였겠습니까?\"\n\n\"선생이,\" 그녀가 대꾸했다, \"그와 다른 믿음을 가진 척 꾸미고, 그것을 뻔뻔스럽게 날마다 저에게 들이밀 처지였단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"부인, 그러했습니다,\" 그가 간절히 받아쳤다. \"제가 그보다 덜 했더라면, 그것 말고 다른 무엇이라도 했더라면, 저는 지금 이렇게 부인께 말씀드리고 있지 못했을 것입니다. 그리고 저는 내다보았습니다—누군들 저보다 더 잘 내다볼 수 있겠습니까, 저보다 돔비 씨를 더 오래 겪은 사람이 누가 있겠습니까?—부인의 성품이, 제가 믿지 않았던 바이지만, 그의 첫 부인의 그 순종적이고 고분고분한 성품처럼 무르고 순순한 것으로 드러나지 않는 한—\"\n\n도도한 미소가 떠오르자, 그는 이 말을 다시 되풀이해도 좋다는 빌미를 얻었다.\n\n\"제가 믿지 않았다고 말씀드린 바이지만—우리가 지금 이르른 것과 같은 이해가 쓸모 있게 될 때가 올 법하다고요.\"\n\n\"누구에게 쓸모 있다는 겁니까, 선생?\" 그녀가 경멸하며 따져 물었다.\n\n\"부인께요. 제 자신에게도라고 덧붙이지는 않겠습니다. 그러면 제가 정직하게 베풀 수 있는 그 한정된 돔비 씨 칭찬마저 삼가라고 스스로에게 경고하는 셈이 될 테니까요. 혐오와 경멸이,\" 대단한 표정으로, \"그토록 날카로운 분에게 거슬리는 말을 하는 불운을 겪지 않으려고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"선생이 '한정된 칭찬'을 고백하고, 그를—그분의 으뜸가는 조언자이자 아첨꾼이면서—그런 폄하의 어조로 말하는 것이,\" 이디스가 말했다, \"정직한 일입니까?\"\n\n\"조언자라—그렇습니다,\" 카커가 말했다. \"아첨꾼이라—아닙니다. 약간의 사양은 인정해야 할 듯하여 두렵습니다만. 그러나 우리의 이해관계와 편의는 우리 가운데 많은 이로 하여금 느끼지도 않는 것을 공언하게 만들곤 하지요. 우리는 날마다 이해와 편의에 따른 동업을, 이해와 편의에 따른 우정을, 이해와 편의에 따른 거래를, 이해와 편의에 따른 결혼을 하고 있습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 핏빛처럼 붉은 입술을 깨물었으나, 그를 향해 지켜보는 어둡고 준엄한 감시는 흔들리지 않았다.\n\n\"부인,\" 카커 씨가 그녀 가까이에 있는 의자에 앉으며, 더없이 깊고 더없이 사려 깊은 존경의 태도로 말했다, \"이제 전적으로 부인을 섬기는 데 바쳐진 몸인데, 솔직하게 말씀드리기를 어찌 머뭇거리겠습니까? 부인처럼 타고나신 분이라면, 남편의 성품을 어떤 점에서 바꾸어 더 나은 형태로 빚어낼 수 있으리라 여기시는 것도 당연한 일이었습니다.\"\n\n\"저에게는 당연하지 않았습니다, 선생,\" 그녀가 되받았다. \"저는 그런 기대도 의도도 결코 품은 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n도도하고 굴하지 않는 얼굴은, 그가 내미는 어떤 가면도 쓰지 않기로 굳게 마음먹었으며, 그런 자에게 자신이 어떤 모습으로 비치든 개의치 않고 거리낌 없이 자신을 드러내기로 작정했음을 그에게 보여 주었다.\n\n\"적어도,\" 그가 다시 말을 이었다, \"돔비 씨에게 굴복하지도 않으면서, 또 그와 그렇게 격렬하게 충돌하지도 않으면서, 그의 아내로서 함께 살아가는 것이 능히 가능하다고 여기신 것은 당연한 일이었습니다. 그러나 부인, 그렇게 생각하셨을 때 부인은 (그 후로 확인하셨듯이) 돔비 씨를 알지 못하셨습니다. 그분이 얼마나 까다롭고 거만한지, 또, 이렇게 말씀드려도 된다면, 그분이 얼마나 자신의 위대함의 노예가 되어, 짐 부리는 짐승처럼 자신의 개선 수레에 매여, 그 수레가 뒤에 있어 무엇이든 짓밟고 무엇이든 헤치며 끌려가야 한다는 생각밖에는 이 세상에 아무 생각이 없는지를 알지 못하셨습니다.\"\n\n이 자만의 비유를 음흉하게 음미하는 동안, 그의 이가 번뜩였고, 그는 계속 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"돔비 씨는, 부인, 정말이지 저를 위해서만큼이나 부인을 위해서도 진정한 배려를 할 줄 모르는 분입니다. 지나친 비교이지요. 일부러 그렇게 한 것입니다만, 더없이 정당한 비교이지요. 돔비 씨는, 자기 권세가 한껏 충만한 가운데, 저에게—어제 아침 그분 입으로 직접 들은 바입니다—부인과 자기 사이의 중개인이 되어 달라고 청했습니다. 제가 부인께 달갑지 않은 사람임을 알기 때문이고, 또 제가 부인의 고집에 대한 벌이 되기를 의도하기 때문이지요. 그리고 그 외에도, 그분은 정말로, 자기의 봉급 받는 종인 제가, 부인—제가 말씀 나누는 영광을 누리는 이 부인이 아니라, 그분 마음속에는 그런 분이 존재하지 않으니까요—그분의 일부인 자기 아내가 맞이하기에는 그 위엄을 떨어뜨리는 사절이라고 여기는 것입니다. 그분이 저더러 그렇게 부려진다고 대놓고 말할 때, 그분이 저를 얼마나 안중에도 두지 않는지, 제게도 어떤 개인적 감정이나 의견이 있을 수 있다는 가능성에 얼마나 무딘지를 부인께서는 짐작하실 수 있을 것입니다. 그분이 그런 심부름꾼으로 부인을 위협할 때, 그분이 부인의 감정에 얼마나 완전히 무관심한지도 아실 것입니다. 물론 그분이 그러셨다는 것을 부인께서는 잊지 않으셨을 테니까요.\"\n\n그녀는 여전히 그를 주의 깊게 지켜보았다. 그러나 그 역시 그녀를 지켜보았고, 그녀와 남편 사이에 오간 어떤 일을 자기가 알고 있다는 이 암시가, 마치 독화살처럼 그녀의 도도한 가슴속에 박혀 쓰라리게 욱신거린다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"제가 이 모든 것을 다시 끄집어내는 것은, 부인, 부인과 돔비 씨 사이의 골을 더 넓히려는 것이 아닙니다—천만에요! 그것이 저에게 무슨 득이 되겠습니까?—다만, 자기가 관련된 일에서는 누구든 헤아려져야 한다는 인식을 돔비 씨에게 심어 주는 것이 얼마나 가망 없는 일인지를 보여 드리는 본보기로 그러는 것입니다. 그분 곁에 있는 우리는, 저마다 다른 처지에서, 그분을 그런 사고방식 속에 굳어지게 하는 데 우리 몫을 다했다고 감히 말할 수 있습니다. 그러나 우리가 그러지 않았더라도 다른 이들이 그랬을 것입니다—아니면 그들은 그분 곁에 있지 못했겠지요. 그리고 그것은 처음부터 늘 그분 삶의 바로 그 근간이었습니다. 요컨대 돔비 씨는, 그분 앞에서 무릎을 꿇고 목을 굽힌, 순종적이고 의존적인 사람들 외에는 상대해 본 적이 없습니다. 그분은 성난 자존심과 강한 분노가 자기에게 맞선다는 것이 어떤 것인지 결코 알아 본 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 이제 그것을 알게 되겠지!\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하는 듯했다. 입술은 벌어지지 않았고 눈도 흔들리지 않았지만. 그는 부드러운 솜털이 다시 한번 떨리는 것을 보았고, 그녀가 그 아름다운 새의 깃털을 한순간 자기 가슴에 갖다 대는 것을 보았다. 그러고는 자기가 몸을 사리고 똬리 틀었던 그 고리를 한 겹 더 풀어냈다.\n\n\"돔비 씨는, 더없이 명예로운 신사이긴 하지만,\" 그가 말했다, \"조금이라도 반대에 부딪히면, 그 마음의 뒤틀림 탓에, 사실마저도 자기 견해에 맞추어 곡해하는 경향이 어찌나 강한지요. 그가—이보다 더 좋은 예를 들 수 있겠습니까!—그는 진심으로 믿고 있습니다(이제 제가 드리려는 말의 어리석음을 너그러이 보아 주십시오. 제 생각이 아니니까요). 스큐턴 부인의 애통한 별세 전, 그분이 기억하실지 모를 어떤 특별한 자리에서, 그가 지금의 아내에게 자기 의견을 엄하게 표명한 것이 시들어 버리게 하는 효과를 내어, 그 순간 그녀를 완전히 굴복시켰다고 말입니다!\"\n\n이디스가 웃었다. 그 웃음이 얼마나 거칠고 가락이 없었는지는 굳이 묘사할 필요가 없다. 그가 그것을 듣고 기뻐했다는 것으로 충분하다.\n\n\"부인,\" 그가 다시 말을 이었다, \"이 이야기는 이만하겠습니다. 부인 자신의 의견은 어찌나 강하고, 또, 제가 확신컨대, 어찌나 변할 수 없는지,\" 그는 그 말들을 천천히 그리고 대단히 힘주어 되풀이했다, \"이런 결점들에도 불구하고, 그리고 그것들을 제가 속속들이 알고 있으면서도, 제가 돔비 씨에게 익숙해졌고 그분을 존경하게 되었다고 말씀드리면, 또다시 부인의 노여움을 살까 거의 두렵습니다. 그러나 제가 그렇게 말하는 것은, 부디 믿어 주십시오, 부인 자신의 감정과는 그토록 완전히 어긋나고 부인께서 공감하실 수 없는 어떤 감정을 그저 뽐내기 위해서가 아니라\"—오, 이 말이 얼마나 또렷하고 분명하며 힘주어진 것이었던가!—\"이 불행한 일에서 제가 얼마나 열성으로 부인의 편인지, 그리고 제가 맡아야 할 역할을 얼마나 분개하며 여기는지를 부인께 확신시켜 드리기 위해서입니다!\"\n\n그녀는 그의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼기가 두려운 듯이 앉아 있었다.\n\n그리고 이제 그 똬리의 마지막 고리를 풀 차례였다!\n\n\"시간이 늦어 가는군요,\" 카커가 잠시 사이를 두고 말했다, \"그리고 부인께서는, 말씀하셨듯이, 피곤하시고요. 그러나 이 만남의 두 번째 목적을 잊어서는 안 되겠습니다. 제가 가진 충분한 까닭이 있어, 돔비 아가씨에 대한 부인의 애정 표현에 신중하시기를 권해 드려야겠습니다, 아니, 더없이 간곡하게 간청해야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"신중하라니! 무슨 뜻이지요?\"\n\n\"그 젊은 아가씨에게 너무 큰 애정을 드러내지 않도록 조심하시라는 말씀입니다.\"\n\n\"너무 큰 애정이라니요, 선생!\" 이디스가 넓은 이마를 찌푸리며 자리에서 일어나 말했다. \"누가 제 애정을 판단하고, 누가 그것을 재단한단 말입니까? 선생이요?\"\n\n\"제가 그러는 것이 아닙니다.\" 그는 당혹스러워하거나, 당혹스러운 척했다.\n\n\"그럼 누가요?\"\n\n\"누구일지 짐작이 안 가십니까?\"\n\n\"저는 짐작하고 싶지 않습니다,\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"부인,\" 그가 잠시 머뭇거린 뒤에 말했다. 그러는 동안에도 두 사람은 여전히 아까처럼 서로를 바라보고 있었다. \"저는 여기서 곤경에 처해 있습니다. 부인께서는 어떤 전갈도 받지 않으시겠다 하셨고, 그 화제로 되돌아가는 것을 저에게 금하셨습니다. 그러나 그 두 화제는 제가 보기에 어찌나 긴밀히 얽혀 있는지, 부인의 노여움을 거쳐 오긴 했어도 이제 부인의 신임을 얻는 영광을 누리게 된 한 사람에게서 이 막연한 경고를 받아들여 주지 않으신다면, 저는 부인께서 제게 내리신 명을 어길 수밖에 없습니다.\"\n\n\"선생이 그렇게 해도 좋다는 것을 아시지요, 선생,\" 이디스가 말했다. \"그렇게 하세요.\"\n\n그토록 창백하고, 그토록 떨며, 그토록 격정에 차 있다니! 그렇다면 그는 그 효과를 잘못 계산하지 않은 것이었다!\n\n\"그분의 지시는,\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"돔비 아가씨에 대한 부인의 태도가 그분에게 달갑지 않다는 것을 제가 부인께 알리라는 것이었습니다. 그것이 그분 자신에게 불리한 비교를 떠올리게 한다는 것이지요. 그분은 그것이 완전히 바뀌기를 바라며, 만일 부인께서 진심이라면, 반드시 그렇게 되리라 확신한다는 것입니다. 부인의 끊임없는 애정 표현이 그 대상에게 이로울 것이 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그것은 협박이군요,\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그것은 협박입니다,\" 그가 동의하는 그 소리 없는 방식으로 대답하고는, 큰 소리로 덧붙였다, \"그러나 부인을 겨눈 것은 아닙니다.\"\n\n도도하고 꼿꼿하며 위엄 있게, 그녀는 그를 마주하고 서 있었고, 가득 빛나는 형형한 눈으로 그를 꿰뚫어 보았으며, 경멸과 쓰라림으로 미소 짓고 있었다. 그러던 그녀가, 마치 발밑의 땅이 꺼지기라도 한 듯 주저앉았고, 그가 두 팔로 붙들지 않았더라면 한순간에 바닥에 쓰러졌을 것이다. 그러나 그가 그녀에게 손을 대는 그 순간, 그녀는 그만큼이나 순식간에 그를 뿌리치고는, 뒤로 물러나 손을 내뻗은 채 꼼짝 않고 다시 그를 마주했다.\n\n\"저를 두고 가 주세요. 오늘 밤은 더 말하지 마세요.\"\n\n\"저는 이 일의 시급함을 느낍니다,\" 카커 씨가 말했다, \"부인께서 그분의 마음 상태를 모르고 계신 데서, 어떤 예기치 못한 결과가, 또 얼마나 빨리 일어날지 알 수 없기 때문입니다. 돔비 아가씨가 지금, 자기의 오랜 하녀가 쫓겨난 일로 마음 아파하고 있다고 알고 있습니다. 그 자체로는 사소한 결과였을지 모르나 말이지요. 돔비 아가씨가 자리에 없게 해 달라고 청한 것을 부인께서는 나무라지 않으시겠지요. 그러기를 바라도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"나무라지 않습니다. 저를 두고 가 주세요, 선생.\"\n\n\"그 젊은 아가씨에 대한 부인의 애정이, 제가 확신컨대 매우 진실하고 강하기에, 부인께서 그 아가씨의 처지를 해치고 그 미래의 희망을 망쳐 버렸다는 생각에 시달리게 되는 것은 부인께 크나큰 불행이 되리라는 것을 저는 알았습니다,\" 카커가 서둘렀으나 간절하게 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 더는 안 됩니다. 부디 저를 두고 가세요.\"\n\n\"저는 그분을 시중드는 일로, 또 업무를 처리하는 일로 늘 이곳에 와 있을 것입니다. 부인께서 다시 뵙도록, 그리고 어찌해야 할지를 의논하고 부인의 뜻을 알도록 허락해 주시겠지요?\"\n\n그녀는 문 쪽을 가리켰다.\n\n\"제가 부인께 말씀을 나누었다는 것을 그분께 아직 알릴지, 아니면 기회가 없어서, 또는 다른 어떤 이유로 그러기를 미루었다고 그분이 여기도록 할지조차 저는 결정하지 못하겠습니다. 부인께서 저로 하여금 아주 빠른 시일 안에 부인과 의논할 수 있게 해 주셔야 하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"지금만 아니라면 언제든지요,\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 부인을 뵙고자 할 때는, 돔비 아가씨가 자리에 없어야 한다는 것을, 그리고 제가, 부인의 신임을 누리는 영광을 가진 사람으로서, 또 제 힘이 닿는 모든 도움을 부인께 드리러, 그리고 어쩌면 여러 경우에 그 아가씨에게서 화를 막아 드리러 오는 사람으로서 만남을 청한다는 것을 부인께서는 헤아려 주시겠지요?\"\n\n그녀는 그 흔들림 없는 눈길의 영향이 무엇이든 간에, 한순간이라도 그 영향에서 그를 풀어 주기를 두려워하는 듯한 똑같은 모습으로 여전히 그를 바라보면서 \"그러지요!\"라고 대답하고는, 다시 한번 그에게 가라고 일렀다.\n\n그는 따르는 듯 고개를 숙였으나, 문에 거의 다다랐을 때 돌아서서 말했다.\n\n\"저는 용서받았고, 제 잘못을 해명했습니다. 돔비 아가씨를 위해서, 그리고 저 자신을 위해서—가기 전에 부인의 손을 잡아 보아도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n그녀는 어젯밤 자기가 상하게 한 그 장갑 낀 손을 그에게 내밀었다. 그는 그것을 한 손에 쥐고 입을 맞춘 뒤 물러났다. 그리고 문을 닫고 나서, 그녀의 손을 잡았던 그 손을 흔들어 보이고는, 그것을 자기 가슴에 갖다 넣었다.\n\n이디스는 그날 밤 아무도 만나지 않고, 문을 잠근 채 홀로 있었다.\n\n그녀는 울지 않았다. 겉으로는 집으로 마차를 타고 올 때보다 더한 동요를 보이지 않았다. 그녀는 마차 안에서 떠받치던 만큼이나 도도한 머리를 베개에 누였고, 그녀의 기도는 이러했다.\n\n\"이 사람이 거짓말쟁이이게 하소서! 만일 그가 진실을 말했다면, 그 애는 저에게서 사라진 것이며, 저에게는 더 이상 희망이 남아 있지 않으니까요!\"\n\n한편 이 사람은 잠자리로 향하며 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼는데, 그녀의 격정이 얼마나 도도했는지, 그녀가 단 한 번을 빼고는 결코 돌리지 않던 그 검은 눈으로 자기 앞에 아름답게 앉아 있던 모습을, 그 흰 솜털이 어떻게 떨렸는지, 그 새의 깃털이 어떻게 바닥에 흩뿌려졌는지를 곱씹으며 야릇한 쾌감에 젖어 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVI. Recognizant and Reflective\n\n이 무렵 카커 씨의 생활과 습관에 일어나기 시작한 자잘한 변화들 가운데, 그가 사업에 매달리는 그 비상한 근면함과, 회사의 업무가 그에게 드러내 보이는 모든 세부 사항을 파고드는 그 철저함만큼 두드러진 것은 없었다. 그런 일에서 늘 활동적이고 예리했던 그였지만, 이제 그 스라소니 같은 경계심은 스무 배로 늘어났다. 그의 지칠 줄 모르는 감시는 날마다 새로운 형태로 그에게 닥쳐오는 현재의 온갖 사안과 보조를 맞추었을 뿐 아니라, 이렇게 사람을 온통 사로잡는 일들 한가운데서도 그는 짬을 냈다—아니, 짬을 만들어 냈다—회사가 지난 오랜 세월 동안 벌여 온 거래들과, 그 속에서 자기가 맡아 온 몫을 되짚어 보기 위해서였다. 점원들이 모두 가 버리고, 사무실은 어둡고 텅 비었으며, 비슷한 영업소들도 죄다 문을 닫은 뒤에, 카커 씨는 철제 방의 해부 구조를 송두리째 자기 앞에 펼쳐 놓은 채, 자기 대상의 가장 미세한 신경과 섬유까지 해부해 가는 사람의 끈기 있는 진척으로 장부와 서류의 비밀들을 탐색하곤 했다. 이런 때면 대개 남아 있던 전령 퍼치는 촛불 하나에 의지해 시세표를 읽으며 시간을 보내거나, 바깥 사무실에서 난롯불 앞에 졸다가 시시각각 석탄 통 속으로 머리부터 처박힐 위험을 무릅쓰곤 했는데, 비록 그 때문에 가정의 즐거움이 적잖이 줄어들기는 했어도, 이 열성적인 처신에 감탄의 헌사를 바치지 않을 수 없었다. 그리하여 그는 (이제 쌍둥이에게 젖을 먹이고 있는) 퍼치 부인에게, 자기네 시티의 지배인 양반이 얼마나 부지런하고 명민한지를 거듭거듭 늘어놓았다.\n\n카커 씨가 회사 업무에 쏟는 그 늘어나고 날카로워진 주의력을, 그는 자기 개인의 일에도 똑같이 적용했다. 비록 그가 그 사업의 동업자는 아니었지만—그 명예는 지금껏 오로지 위대한 돔비라는 이름의 상속자들에게만 주어진 것이었다—그는 회사 거래에서 얼마간의 보합을 받고 있었고, 돈을 유리하게 굴릴 수 있는 회사의 온갖 편의를 함께 누렸기에, 동방의 트리톤들 사이에 끼인 송사리들 눈에는 부자로 여겨졌다. 이 약삭빠른 관찰자들 사이에서는, 돔비 회사의 젬 카커가 자기 재산이 얼마나 되는지 둘러보고 있다는 둥, 빈틈없는 사내답게 좋은 때를 골라 돈을 거둬들이고 있다는 둥 하는 말이 나돌기 시작했다. 심지어 증권 거래소에서는 젬이 부유한 과부와 결혼하려 한다는 데 내기를 거는 일까지 있었다.\n\n그러나 이런 신경 쓸 일들도 카커 씨가 자기 우두머리를 살피는 일을, 또 그의 청결함이며 단정함이며 매끈함이며 그가 지닌 그 고양이 같은 온갖 특성을 조금도 방해하지 못했다. 그것은 그의 어떤 습관에 변화가 생겼다기보다, 그 사람 전체가 한층 강렬해졌다고 하는 편이 옳았다. 전에 그에게서 눈에 띄었던 모든 것이 지금도 눈에 띄었으되, 다만 더 큰 집중의 양을 띠고 있었다. 그는 무슨 일이든, 마치 그것 말고는 아무 일도 하지 않는 것처럼 하나하나 해치웠다—그 정도의 능력과 목적을 지닌 사람에게서 이는, 자기의 가장 예리한 능력을 벼리고 살려 두는 어떤 일을 하고 있다는 꽤 확실한 표시였다.\n\n그에게 일어난 단 하나의 뚜렷한 변화는, 거리를 따라 이리저리 말을 타고 갈 때면, 그가 깊은 명상에 빠져들곤 한다는 것이었다. 그것은 돔비 씨가 변을 당한 그날 아침, 그 신사의 집에서 나오며 빠져들었던 바로 그런 명상이었다. 이런 때면 그는 기계적으로 앞에 놓인 장애물을 피해 갔고, 목적지에 다다르거나, 무슨 갑작스러운 우연이나 충격이 그를 일깨우기 전까지는 아무것도 보지도 듣지도 못하는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n어느 날 흰 다리의 말을 이렇게 타고 돔비와 아들 회사의 회계 사무소로 가던 그는, 어느 두 쌍의 여자 눈이 자기를 지켜보고 있다는 것을, 또 그라인더 롭의 홀린 듯한 눈동자도 의식하지 못했다. 롭은 시간 엄수를 과시하느라 약속된 장소에서 한 거리쯤 떨어진 곳에서 기다리다가, 주인의 주의를 끌려고 헛되이 모자를 만지작거리고 또 만지작거렸으며, 주인이 말에서 내릴 때 등자를 잡아 줄 채비를 한 채 주인 곁에서 종종걸음으로 따라 걸었다.\n\n\"저기 가는 것 좀 봐!\" 이 두 여자 가운데 하나가 외쳤다. 늙은 여자였는데, 쪼글쪼글한 팔을 뻗어 곁에 바싹 붙어 선 동행에게 그를 가리켜 보였다. 동행은 젊은 여자였고, 자기처럼 어느 출입문 안으로 물러나 있었다.\n\n브라운 부인의 딸은, 브라운 부인의 이런 지시에 따라 밖을 내다보았다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 분노와 복수심이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"저 사람을 다시 볼 일은 없을 줄 알았는데,\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"하지만 보는 편이 나을지도 모르지, 어쩌면. 보이네. 보여!\"\n\n\"안 변했지!\" 늙은 여자가 게걸스러운 악의의 빛을 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"저 사람이 변해!\" 다른 여자가 받아쳤다. \"뭐 때문에? 저 사람이 무슨 고생을 했다고? 스무 사람 몫의 변화가 내 안에 있어. 그걸로 충분하지 않아?\"\n\n\"저기 가는 것 좀 봐!\" 늙은 여자가 붉은 눈으로 딸을 지켜보며 중얼거렸다. \"저렇게 편안하고 말끔하게 말 위에 앉아 있는데, 우리는 진창 속에 있구나.\"\n\n\"진창 그 자체지,\" 딸이 안달하며 말했다. \"우린 저 사람 말발굽 밑의 진흙이야. 우리가 뭐가 더 되겠어?\"\n\n다시 그를 바라보는 그 골똘함 속에서, 늙은 여자가 대꾸하려 들자 그녀는 마치 소리 따위에 시야가 가려지기라도 하는 양 손으로 황급히 제지하는 시늉을 했다. 어머니는 그가 아니라 딸을 지켜보며 잠자코 있었다. 마침내 딸의 타오르던 눈빛이 가라앉고, 그가 가 버린 것에 안도한 듯 길게 숨을 내쉴 때까지.\n\n\"얘야!\" 그제야 늙은 여자가 말했다. \"앨리스! 잘난 우리 앨리!\" 그녀는 딸의 주의를 끌려고 소맷자락을 살그머니 흔들었다. \"저 사람한테서 돈을 짜낼 수 있는데, 저렇게 그냥 보내 버릴 셈이냐? 아니, 그건 죄악이야, 내 딸아.\"\n\n\"내가 저 사람한테서 돈을 받지 않겠다고 말하지 않았어요?\" 그녀가 받아쳤다. \"아직도 내 말을 못 믿어요? 내가 저 사람 누이의 돈을 받았어요? 저 사람의 그 흰 손을 거쳐 온 줄 알면서 단돈 한 푼이라도 손댈 것 같아요?—정말로 거기에 독을 넣어서 도로 저 사람한테 돌려보낼 수 있는 거라면 또 모를까. 조용히 해요, 어머니, 그리고 갑시다.\"\n\n\"그런데 저 사람은 저렇게 부자고?\" 늙은 여자가 중얼거렸다. \"우리는 이렇게 가난한데!\"\n\n\"우리가 저 사람한테 갚아야 할 해코지를 조금도 갚아 줄 수 없으니 가난하지,\" 딸이 받아쳤다. \"저 사람이 나한테 그런 종류의 재물을 준다면, 받아서 쓰겠어요. 갑시다. 저 사람 말이나 본다고 무슨 소용 있어요. 가자고요, 어머니!\"\n\n그러나 그라인더 롭이 주인 없는 말을 끌고 거리를 도로 내려오는 광경에, 그 자체에는 없는 어떤 외적인 흥미라도 느낀 듯한 늙은 여자는, 그 젊은이를 더없이 진지하게 살펴보았다. 그러더니 마음에 품고 있던 의심이 무엇이든 그가 가까이 올수록 풀리는 듯, 두 눈을 빛내며 손가락을 입술에 댄 채 딸을 흘끗 보고는, 그가 지나가는 바로 그 순간에 출입문에서 나와 그의 어깨를 두드렸다.\n\n\"아니, 우리 발랄한 롭은 그동안 내내 어디 가 있었누!\" 그가 돌아서자 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그 발랄한 롭은, 이 인사에 발랄함이 어지간히 줄어든 채, 몹시 낭패한 얼굴로 눈에 물기가 차오르며 말했다.\n\n\"아이고! 가난한 녀석이 정직하게 밥벌이하면서 점잖게 처신하고 있는데, 좀 가만 내버려 둘 순 없소, 브라운 부인? 녀석이 주인 말을 정직한 마구간으로 데려가는 중인데, 길거리에서 말을 걸어 녀석한테서 평판을 앗아가는 건 또 뭐요—당신 마음대로 했으면 고양이며 개 먹이로 팔아넘겼을 그런 말을 말이오! 아니, 난,\" 그라인더는 마치 자기가 입은 온갖 피해의 절정이라도 되는 양 마지막 말을 꺼내며 말했다, \"당신이 진작에 죽은 줄로만 알았소!\"\n\n\"이것 봐라,\" 늙은 여자가 딸에게 호소하며 외쳤다, \"이게 나한테 말하는 꼴이라니, 몇 주 몇 달을 같이 알고 지냈고, 얘야, 비둘기 좋아하는 떠돌이며 새잡이들 틈에서 여러 번 이 녀석 편을 들어 줬던 나한테 말이다.\"\n\n\"새 얘긴 그만 좀 하시오, 브라운 부인?\" 롭이 더없이 고통스러운 어조로 받아쳤다. \"녀석 생각엔 그런 쪼그만 것들보다는 차라리 사자를 상대하는 편이 낫겠소, 그것들은 당신이 가장 방심한 순간에 늘 얼굴로 도로 날아드니까. 자, 안녕하시오, 그래 뭘 바라는 거요?\" 그라인더는 이 정중한 안부를, 마치 마지못해서인 듯, 그리고 잔뜩 분개하고 앙심을 품은 채 내뱉었다.\n\n\"옛 친구한테 말하는 꼴 좀 들어 봐라, 얘야!\" 브라운 부인이 다시 딸에게 호소하며 말했다. \"하지만 이 녀석 옛 친구들 중엔 나만큼 참을성 있는 사람들이 아닌 이들도 있지. 내가 만약 이 녀석이 아는 사람들, 함께 점찍어 두고 사기 쳤던 그런 자들한테 이 녀석이 어디 있는지 일러바치기라도 한다면—\"\n\n\"입 좀 다물 수 없소, 브라운 부인?\" 비참한 그라인더가, 마치 주인의 이가 자기 팔꿈치 옆에서 번뜩이는 걸 볼 거라 예상하기라도 한 듯 황급히 주위를 둘러보며 끼어들었다. \"녀석을 파멸시키는 게 뭐가 그리 즐겁소? 그 연세에! 이런저런 일들을 생각하셔야 할 나이에 말이오!\"\n\n\"참 멋진 말이로구나!\" 늙은 여자가 짐승의 목을 토닥이며 말했다.\n\n\"그냥 좀 놔두시오, 브라운 부인?\" 롭이 그녀의 손을 밀어내며 외쳤다. \"당신은 회개한 녀석을 미치게 만들 작정이오!\"\n\n\"아니, 내가 이 녀석한테 무슨 해를 끼친다고 그러냐, 얘야?\" 늙은 여자가 받아쳤다.\n\n\"해라뇨?\" 롭이 말했다. \"지푸라기 하나라도 닿으면 알아챌 주인을 둔 녀석이오.\" 그러고는 늙은 여자의 손이 잠시 닿았던 자리에 입김을 불고, 손가락으로 부드럽게 쓸어 주었는데, 마치 자기가 한 말을 진심으로 믿는 듯했다.\n\n늙은 여자는 뒤따라오는 딸을 향해 우물거리고 입을 놀려 가며 뒤를 돌아보면서도, 롭이 고삐를 손에 쥐고 걸어가는 동안 그의 발꿈치에 바짝 붙어 따라가며 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"좋은 자리지, 롭, 응?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"운이 좋구나, 내 새끼.\"\n\n\"아이고, 운 얘긴 마시오, 브라운 부인,\" 비참한 그라인더가 휙 돌아서서 멈춰 서며 받아쳤다. \"당신이 아예 안 왔거나, 가 버리기만 한다면, 그땐 정말 녀석이 그럭저럭 운 좋다고 할 수 있겠지. 좀 갈 수 없소, 브라운 부인, 날 따라오지 말고!\" 롭이 별안간 반항하며 흐느꼈다. \"저 젊은 여자가 당신 친구라면, 당신이 이렇게 망신스럽게 굴게 놔두지 말고 데려가는 게 어떻소!\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 늙은 여자가 얼굴을 그의 얼굴에 바싹 들이대며 악에 받친 히죽거림을 지었는데, 그것이 목 아래까지 늘어진 살갗을 온통 주름지게 했다. \"네가 옛 동무를 모른 척해? 다른 잠자리라곤 포장석밖에 없을 때 내 집에 쉰 번이나 숨어들어 구석에서 곤히 잤으면서, 나한테 이렇게 말한단 말이냐? 너랑 같이 사고팔고, 내 장사 방식대로 너를 도와줬건만, 이 학생 녀석아, 좀도둑 같은 놈아, 별의별 짓을 다 했는데, 나더러 가라고 해? 내일 아침이면 너를 둘러싸고 네 그림자 복사판처럼 너를 파멸로 따라다닐 옛 패거리를 불러 모을 수도 있는 나한테, 그 뻔뻔한 낯짝으로 대들어? 가마. 가자, 앨리스.\"\n\n\"멈춰요, 브라운 부인!\" 정신이 나간 그라인더가 외쳤다. \"뭣들 하는 거요? 화내지 마시오! 제발 저 사람 보내지 말아요. 난 아무 무례도 끼칠 생각 없었소. 처음에 '안녕하시오' 하고 인사했잖소? 그런데 당신이 대답을 안 했지. 안녕하시오? 게다가,\" 롭이 가련하게 말했다, \"이것 좀 보쇼! 녀석이 주인 말을 끌고 가서 솔질을 시켜야 하는데, 게다가 주인은 일어나는 일 하나하나를 다 꿰고 있는데, 길거리에 서서 이야기나 하고 있을 수 있겠소!\"\n\n늙은 여자는 어느 정도 화가 누그러진 시늉을 했지만, 여전히 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 입을 놀리고 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"마구간으로 같이 가서, 당신 몸에 좋은 걸로 한 잔 들지 그러시오, 브라운 부인?\" 롭이 말했다. \"그렇게 떼쓰지 말고 말이오, 그래 봤자 당신한테도 누구한테도 좋을 거 없소. 같이 갑시다, 좀 그래 주겠소?\" 롭이 말했다. \"말만 아니었으면, 정말이지 만나서 반갑소!\"\n\n이 변명과 함께, 롭은 절망의 처량한 그림처럼 돌아서서, 자기가 맡은 말을 골목길로 끌고 내려갔다. 늙은 여자는 딸을 향해 입을 놀려 가며 그의 뒤를 바짝 따랐다. 딸도 뒤따랐다.\n\n위로 커다란 교회 탑이 솟아 있고, 영업소라고는 포장업자 창고와 병 제조업자 창고뿐인 어느 조용한 작은 광장, 아니 안마당으로 접어들어, 그라인더 롭은 모퉁이에 있는 예스러운 마구간의 마부에게 흰 다리의 말을 넘겼다. 그러고는 브라운 부인과 그 딸에게 그 마구간 문간의 돌 의자에 앉으라고 청하고는, 곧 이웃 술집에서 백랍 됫박과 잔을 들고 다시 나타났다.\n\n\"여기 주인 양반—카커 씨를 위하여다, 얘야!\" 늙은 여자가, 술 마시기 전의 건배사인 양 천천히 말했다. \"그분께 신의 축복을!\"\n\n\"아니, 난 그분이 누군지 말한 적 없는데,\" 롭이 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 말했다.\n\n\"우리는 얼굴은 알지,\" 브라운 부인이 말했다. 그 우물거리는 입과 끄덕이는 머리가 골똘한 주의의 고정 속에 잠시 멈추었다. \"오늘 아침에 그분이 말에서 내리기 전에 지나가는 걸 봤다. 네가 말을 받으려고 채비하고 있을 때 말이야.\"\n\n\"아이, 아이,\" 롭이 받아쳤다, 자기의 그 채비성이 차라리 다른 데로 데려다주었더라면 싶은 표정이었다.—\"이 여자는 왜 이러오? 안 마시려는 거요?\"\n\n이 물음은 앨리스를 두고 한 것이었다. 그녀는 망토를 두른 채 조금 떨어져 앉아, 그가 다시 채워 내미는 잔에는 도통 관심을 두지 않았다.\n\n늙은 여자는 고개를 저었다. \"신경 쓰지 마라,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네가 알고 나면, 별난 애란다, 롭. 그건 그렇고 카커 씨는—\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 롭이 말했다. 마치 그 층층이 쌓인 창고들 어디에선가 카커 씨가 내려다보고 있기라도 한 양, 포장업자 창고와 병 제조업자 창고를 조심스레 올려다보며. \"조용히.\"\n\n\"아니, 그분은 여기 없잖니!\" 브라운 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그건 모르는 일이오,\" 롭이 중얼거렸다. 그의 시선은 교회 탑까지 떠돌았는데, 마치 그가 초자연적인 청력을 지니고 거기 있을지도 모른다는 듯이.\n\n\"좋은 주인이냐?\" 브라운 부인이 물었다.\n\n롭은 고개를 끄덕이고는, 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"지독하게 예리하지.\"\n\n\"도시 밖에서 사시지, 그렇지, 아가?\" 늙은 여자가 말했다.\n\n\"집에 계실 땐 그렇소,\" 롭이 받아쳤다. \"하지만 지금은 우리가 집에서 안 살아요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어디서?\" 늙은 여자가 물었다.\n\n\"하숙집이오. 돔비 씨 댁 근처에,\" 롭이 받아쳤다.\n\n젊은 여자가 어찌나 캐묻듯이, 또 어찌나 느닷없이 그에게 눈길을 박았던지, 롭은 그만 당황해서 다시 잔을 내밀었으나, 전과 마찬가지로 그녀에게는 아무런 효과가 없었다.\n\n\"돔비 씨—당신이랑 나랑 가끔 그분 얘기를 하곤 했잖소, 알지,\" 롭이 브라운 부인에게 말했다. \"당신이 나더러 그분 얘기를 하게 만들곤 했지.\"\n\n늙은 여자가 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그게, 돔비 씨가 말에서 떨어지셨소,\" 롭이 마지못해 말했다. \"그래서 우리 주인이 평소보다 더 자주 거기 가 계셔야 하오, 그분하고든, 돔비 부인하고든, 그 집 누구하고든 말이오. 그래서 우리가 도시로 나온 거요.\"\n\n\"그 두 사람은 사이가 좋으냐, 아가?\" 늙은 여자가 물었다.\n\"누구요?\" 롭이 받아쳤다.\n\n\"그 사람이랑 그 여자 말이다.\"\n\n\"뭐, 돔비 씨랑 돔비 부인 말이오?\" 롭이 말했다. \"내가 그걸 어찌 알겠소!\"\n\n\"그들 말고—주인 양반이랑 돔비 부인 말이다, 아가,\" 늙은 여자가 어르듯 받았다.\n\n\"모르오,\" 롭이 다시 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"그렇겠지요. 별걸 다 궁금해하시네, 브라운 부인! 말은 적게 할수록 탈이 적은 법이오.\"\n\n\"아니, 그게 무슨 해가 된다고!\" 늙은 여자가 웃음을 터뜨리고 손뼉을 치며 외쳤다. \"발랄한 롭이 형편이 펴니까 순해졌구나! 그게 무슨 해가 된다고.\"\n\n\"그래, 해 될 거 없는 줄 나도 알아요,\" 롭이 포장업자 창고와 병 제조업자 창고, 그리고 교회를 향한 그 미심쩍은 눈길을 다시 보내며 받아쳤다. \"하지만 입을 놀리는 건, 그게 우리 주인 외투 단추 수에 관한 거라 해도 안 될 일이오. 단언컨대 그분한테는 안 통해요. 차라리 녀석이 물에 빠져 죽는 게 낫지. 그분이 그렇게 말하오. 당신이 그분 이름을 이미 알고 있지 않았다면, 그게 뭔지조차 말 안 했을 거요. 다른 사람 얘기나 합시다.\"\n\n롭이 다시 한번 마당을 조심스레 둘러보는 사이, 늙은 여자는 딸에게 은밀히 신호를 보냈다. 순간적이었지만, 딸은 알아챘다는 가벼운 기색으로 소년의 얼굴에서 눈을 거두고는, 전처럼 망토에 싸인 채 앉았다.\n\n\"롭, 아가!\" 늙은 여자가 그를 의자 반대쪽 끝으로 손짓해 부르며 말했다. \"넌 늘 내가 아끼고 귀여워하던 애였지. 안 그러냐? 그런 거 알지?\"\n\n\"네, 브라운 부인,\" 그라인더가 아주 마지못한 태도로 대답했다.\n\n\"그런데도 날 떠날 수 있었구나!\" 늙은 여자가 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안으며 말했다. \"넌 떠나 버려서, 거의 알아보지도 못할 만큼 자라 놓고는, 가엾은 옛 친구한테 네가 얼마나 잘됐는지 말하러 한 번을 안 왔어, 이 잘난 녀석아! 어허, 어허!\"\n\n\"아이고, 동네에 빠삭하게 깨어 있는 주인을 둔 녀석한테 이건 정말 끔찍한 일이로구나!\" 비참한 그라인더가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 통곡 세례를 받다니!\"\n\n\"날 보러 안 올 테냐, 로비?\" 브라운 부인이 외쳤다. \"어허, 날 보러 영영 안 올 테냐?\"\n\n\"온다잖소! 그래, 가겠소!\" 그라인더가 받아쳤다.\n\n\"그래야 내 롭이지! 그래야 내 아가지!\" 브라운 부인이 쪼글쪼글한 얼굴의 눈물을 닦으며, 그를 다정하게 꼭 끌어안고 말했다. \"옛날 그 자리로, 롭?\"\n\n\"네,\" 그라인더가 대답했다.\n\n\"곧이지, 로비야?\" 브라운 부인이 외쳤다. \"그리고 자주?\"\n\n\"네. 네. 네,\" 롭이 대답했다. \"정말 그러겠소, 내 영혼과 몸을 걸고.\"\n\n\"그럼,\" 브라운 부인이 두 팔을 하늘로 쳐들고, 머리를 뒤로 젖혀 흔들며 말했다, \"이 녀석이 약속만 지킨다면, 내가 이 녀석이 어디 있는지 알아도 절대로 근처에 안 가고, 이 녀석에 대해선 한 마디도 입 밖에 안 내겠다! 절대로!\"\n\n이 외침이 비참한 그라인더에게는 위안의 한 방울처럼 여겨졌던지, 그는 그 말에 브라운 부인의 손을 잡아 흔들고는, 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 녀석을 좀 놔두고 자기 앞날을 망치지 말아 달라고 그녀에게 애원했다. 브라운 부인은 또 한 번 정답게 끌어안으며 그러마고 했다. 그러나 딸의 뒤를 따라가려다가, 손가락을 슬그머니 치켜든 채 돌아서서, 쉰 목소리로 속삭이며 돈을 좀 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"한 실링만, 아가!\" 그녀가 게걸스럽고 탐욕스러운 얼굴로 말했다. \"아니면 육 펜스라도! 옛정을 봐서. 내가 너무 가난해서. 게다가 내 잘난 딸년이\"—어깨너머로 돌아보며—\"저게 내 딸이다, 롭—날 거의 굶겨 죽인단다.\"\n\n그러나 마지못한 그라인더가 그것을 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주는 순간, 딸이 조용히 돌아와 그 손을 자기 손으로 붙잡고는 동전을 비틀어 빼냈다.\n\n\"뭐예요,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"어머니! 늘 돈이야! 처음부터 끝까지 돈이라니. 내가 방금 한 말을 그렇게도 안 새겨들어요? 자. 받아요!\"\n\n늙은 여자는 돈이 되돌려지자 신음 소리를 냈지만, 그 밖에 어떤 식으로도 그 반환에 맞서지는 않고, 딸의 곁에서 절뚝거리며 마당을 빠져나가, 그 마당이 이어진 골목길을 따라갔다. 놀라고 낭패한 롭은 그들을 멍하니 바라보다가, 그들이 멈춰 서서 이내 진지한 대화에 빠져드는 것을 보았다. 그리고 젊은 여자의 손이 (분명 그들이 이야기하는 누군가를 가리키는) 어둡고 위협적인 동작을 하는 것과, 브라운 부인 쪽에서 그것을 힘없이 흥얼대며 흉내 내는 것을 한두 번이 아니게 목격했는데, 그 때문에 그는 부디 자기가 그들의 화제가 아니기를 간절히 바랐다.\n\n그들이 가 버렸다는 당장의 위안과, 브라운 부인이 영원히 살 수는 없으며 자기를 괴롭힐 만큼 오래 살 것 같지도 않다는 앞날의 위안과 함께, 그라인더는—자기 비행을 후회하되 그저 그런 불쾌한 부수적 결과가 따른다는 점에서만 후회하면서—커틀 선장을 처리해 버린 그 감탄할 만한 솜씨를 떠올리며(이 회상은 어김없이 그를 신바람 나게 했다) 헝클어진 표정을 한결 평온한 빛으로 가다듬고는, 주인의 분부를 받으러 돔비 회계 사무소로 갔다.\n\n거기서 주인은, 눈매가 어찌나 예민하고 빈틈없었던지, 롭은 브라운 부인 일로 추궁당하는 게 아닌가 절반 넘게 예상하며 그 앞에서 벌벌 떨었다. 주인은 그에게 돔비 씨에게 보낼 여느 아침의 서류 상자와, 돔비 부인에게 보낼 쪽지를 건네면서, 그저 조심하고 신속하게 하라는 당부로 고개를 한 번 끄덕였을 뿐이었다—그라인더의 상상 속에서는 음울한 경고와 위협으로 가득 찬 그 알 수 없는 훈계는, 그에게는 어떤 말보다도 더 위력이 있었다.\n\n다시 혼자가 되어 제 방에 든 카커 씨는 일에 몰두하여 온종일 일했다. 그는 많은 방문객을 만났고, 숱한 서류를 검토했으며, 상인들이 모이는 여러 장소를 들고 났다. 그러고는 그날의 업무가 끝날 때까지 더는 어떤 상념에도 빠지지 않았다. 그러나 마침내 책상에서 여느 때처럼 서류를 다 치우고 나자, 그는 다시금 생각에 잠긴 기분에 빠져들었다.\n\n그가 평소의 자리에 평소의 자세로 서서, 두 눈을 바닥에 골똘히 고정한 채 있을 때, 그의 동생이 그날 동안 가지고 나갔던 편지 몇 통을 도로 가져오려고 들어왔다. 동생은 그것들을 조용히 책상에 놓고 곧장 나가려 했는데, 그때 지배인 카커 씨—그의 두 눈은 동생이 들어올 때부터 그에게 머물러, 마치 사무실 바닥이 아니라 이 시간 내내 그를 사색의 대상으로 삼아 온 것 같았다—가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 존 카커, 무슨 일로 여기 왔나?\"\n\n동생은 편지들을 가리키고는, 다시 물러나려 했다.\n\n\"이상하군,\" 지배인이 말했다, \"우리 주인께서 어떠신지 묻지도 않고 오갈 수 있다니.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침 회계 사무소에서, 돔비 씨가 잘 회복하고 계시다는 소식을 들었어,\" 동생이 대답했다.\n\n\"넌 참 온순한 친구지,\" 지배인이 미소 지으며 말했다.—\"하지만 세월이 흐르면서 그렇게 된 거지만—그분께 무슨 해라도 닥치면, 넌 비참해할 거다, 지금 단언컨대.\"\n\n\"진심으로 슬퍼할 거야, 제임스,\" 다른 사람이 받았다.\n\n\"슬퍼하겠지!\" 지배인이, 마치 호소할 다른 누군가가 그 자리에 있기라도 한 양 그를 가리키며 말했다. \"진심으로 슬퍼하겠지! 이 내 동생이 말이야! 이 회사의 말단이, 이 천대받는 폐물 조각이, 썩은 그림짝처럼 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌린 채 옆으로 밀쳐져, 하늘만이 아실 몇 년 동안 그렇게 방치된 이 친구가—그래도 온통 감사와 존경, 게다가 헌신까지 바치고 있다고, 날더러 믿으라는 거지!\"\n\n\"난 자네더러 아무것도 믿으라고 안 해, 제임스,\" 다른 사람이 받았다. \"자네보다 아래에 있는 다른 누구에게 하듯, 나한테도 공정하게 대해 줘. 자네가 물었으니, 내가 대답한 거야.\"\n\n\"그런데 넌, 이 비굴한 개야,\" 지배인이 유난스러운 짜증을 내며 말했다, \"그분한테 불평할 거리가 하나도 없나? 분개할 만한 오만한 처사도, 무례도, 어리석은 위세도, 어떤 종류의 강요도 없었어? 도대체 뭐야! 넌 사람이냐 쥐냐?\"\n\n\"두 사람이 그토록 오랜 세월을 함께 지내면서, 더구나 윗사람과 아랫사람으로 지내면서, 서로에게 불평할 거리가—적어도 본인 생각으로는—하나도 없다면, 그게 오히려 이상한 일이지,\" 존 카커가 대답했다. \"하지만 여기서의 내 이력을 제쳐 둔다면—\"\n\n\"여기서의 자기 이력이라!\" 지배인이 외쳤다. \"그래, 바로 그거야. 너를 극단적인 경우로 만드는 바로 그 사실이, 너를 이야기 전체에서 빼 버리는 거지! 그래서?\"\n\n\"그건 제쳐 두고—자네가 넌지시 말한 대로, 나만이 (다른 모든 이에게는 다행스럽게도) 그것을 지녔다는 점에서 내가 감사할 이유가 되는 그 이력은 제쳐 두고—이 회사에서 적어도 그만큼은 말하고 느끼지 않을 사람은 분명 아무도 없어. 자넨 여기 누구라도 회사 우두머리에게 닥치는 불행이나 변고에 무심하리라고, 또 그것을 진심으로 슬퍼하지 않으리라고 생각하나?\"\n\n\"너야말로 그분께 매여 있을 만한 충분한 이유가 있지!\" 지배인이 경멸하듯 말했다. \"아니, 넌 네가 여기 붙어 있는 게, 값싼 본보기로, 그 빛나는 회사의 명예를 드높여 주는 돔비와 아들의 너그러움을 보여 주는 유명한 사례로 쓰이기 위해서라는 걸 모르겠나?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 동생이 온화하게 대답했다, \"난 오래전부터, 더 친절하고 사심 없는 이유로 내가 여기 붙어 있는 거라고 믿어 왔어.\"\n\n\"하지만 넌 무슨 기독교 교훈이라도,\" 지배인이 살쾡이의 으르렁거림처럼 말했다, \"읊으려던 참이었지, 내가 봤어.\"\n\n\"아니야, 제임스,\" 다른 사람이 받았다, \"우리 사이의 형제라는 끈이 오래전에 끊겨 내버려진 것이긴 하지만—\"\n\n\"누가 그걸 끊었지, 선생?\" 지배인이 말했다.\n\n\"내가, 내 비행으로. 자네 탓으로 돌리는 게 아니야.\"\n\n지배인은 그 곤두선 입의 그 말없는 동작으로 \"오, 내 탓으로 안 돌리신다!\" 하고 답하고는, 계속하라고 했다.\n\n\"내 말은, 우리 사이에 그런 끈이 없다 해도, 부디 불필요한 조롱으로 날 공격하거나, 내가 한 말, 또는 하려는 말을 곡해하지 말아 달라는 거야. 난 그저 자네한테 한 가지를 일러 주려던 거야. 여기서 다른 누구보다 위로 발탁되어 승진과 신임과 영예를 받은(처음에 자네의 뛰어난 능력과 신뢰성 때문에 발탁된 거지, 나도 알아) 사람, 그 누구보다 돔비 씨와 더 허물없이 소통하고, 말하자면 그분과 대등한 처지에 서 있으며, 그분의 총애와 부유함을 입은 사람이 오직 자네뿐이라고—그분의 안녕과 명성을 아끼는 게 오직 자네뿐이라고 생각하는 건 잘못이라는 거야. 이 회사엔, 자네부터 가장 낮은 자리까지, 그 감정에 함께하지 않을 사람은 아무도 없다고, 난 진심으로 믿어.\"\n\n\"거짓말!\" 지배인이 별안간 화가 치밀어 벌게진 얼굴로 말했다. \"넌 위선자야, 존 카커, 거짓말을 하고 있어.\"\n\n\"제임스!\" 다른 사람이 이번엔 자기가 벌게지며 외쳤다. \"이런 모욕적인 말로 무슨 소리를 하는 거야? 왜 도발하지도 않은 나한테 그렇게 비열하게 그런 말을 쓰는 거야?\"\n\n\"내 말은,\" 지배인이 말했다, \"네 위선과 온순함이—이곳의 온갖 위선과 온순함이—나한테는 이만큼의 가치도 없다는 거야,\" 엄지와 손가락을 딱 튕기며, \"그리고 내가 그걸 마치 공기를 들여다보듯 꿰뚫어 본다는 거지! 여기 고용된 자들 가운데, 나와 가장 낮은 자리에 있는 자(너는 그자를 무척 배려하지, 그럴 만도 해, 멀지 않은 곳에 있으니까) 사이에 있는 자들 중에, 제 주인이 깎아내려지는 걸 보고 속으로 기뻐하지 않을 자는 하나도 없어. 몰래 그를 미워하지 않는 자, 그가 잘되기보다 못되기를 바라지 않는 자, 힘과 대담함만 있다면 그에게 등을 돌리지 않을 자는 하나도 없다고. 그의 총애에 가까울수록, 그의 오만에도 가까운 거고, 그에게 바싹 다가갈수록, 그에게서 멀어지는 거야. 그게 여기서의 신조다!\"\n\n\"누가 그런 말로 자네 귀를 더럽혔는지,\" 동생이 말했다. 그의 격앙된 감정은 이내 놀라움에 자리를 내준 터였다. \"혹은 왜 다른 사람이 아니라 날 시험하기로 했는지, 난 모르겠어. 하지만 자네가 날 시험하고, 날 떠보고 있었다는 건, 이제 확실해. 자넨 내가 자네한테서 본 적 없는 다른 태도와 다른 안색을 하고 있어. 단 한 번만 더 자네한테 말하지, 자넨 속고 있어.\"\n\n\"내가 속고 있다는 거 알아,\" 지배인이 말했다. \"내가 그렇게 말했잖나.\"\n\n\"내게가 아니라,\" 동생이 받았다. \"자네한테 그 일러 준 자가 있다면, 그자한테 속는 거지. 없다면, 자네 자신의 생각과 의심에 속는 거고.\"\n\n\"난 의심은 없어,\" 지배인이 말했다. \"내 건 확신이야. 너희 겁쟁이에 비굴하고 굽실거리는 개들! 다들 똑같은 시늉을 하고, 다들 똑같은 이야기를 위선으로 떠벌리고, 다들 똑같은 다짐을 징징대고, 다들 똑같은 빤한 비밀을 품고 있지.\"\n\n동생은 더 말하지 않고 물러나, 말을 맺으며 문을 닫았다. 지배인 카커 씨는 의자를 난롯불 바로 앞으로 끌어다 놓고, 부지깽이로 석탄을 살살 두드리기 시작했다.\n\n\"이 비겁하고 알랑거리는 악당들,\" 그는 두 줄의 반짝이는 이를 드러낸 채 중얼거렸다. \"이렇게나 충격받고 분개한 척하지 않을 자가 그중에 하나도 없지—! 흥! 하지만 그중에, 만약 힘과, 그것을 쓸 재치와 대담함을 동시에 가졌다면, 돔비의 오만을 흩어 놓고 내가 이 재를 긁어내듯 무자비하게 그것을 짓밟아 놓지 않을 자도 하나도 없어.\"\n\n석탄을 부수어 화격자에 흩뿌리면서, 그는 자기가 하는 짓을 생각에 잠긴 미소로 바라보았다. \"게다가 똑같이 손짓하는 여왕도 없이 말이지!\" 그는 곧 덧붙였다. \"거기엔 오만이 있어, 잊을 수 없는 오만이—우리 자신의 그 안면이 증인이지!\" 그러고는 더 깊은 몽상에 빠져들어, 거뭇해져 가는 화격자를 골똘히 들여다보며 앉아 있다가, 책에 몰두해 있던 사람처럼 일어나 주위를 둘러보더니, 모자와 장갑을 집어 들고 말이 기다리는 곳으로 가서, 올라타고는, 불 밝힌 거리들을 지나 말을 몰았다. 저녁이었기 때문이다.\n\n그는 돔비 씨의 집 가까이 말을 몰았다. 그러고는 거기에 다가가며 속보로 늦추고는, 창문들을 올려다보았다. 한때 플로렌스가 개와 함께 앉아 있는 것을 보았던 그 창문이, 불빛은 없었지만 가장 먼저 그의 주의를 끌었다. 그러나 그는 그 집의 높다란 정면 위로 시선을 옮겨 가며 미소 지었고, 그 대상을 거만하게 뒤로 흘려보내는 듯했다.\n\n\"한때는,\" 그가 말했다, \"떠오르는 너의 작은 별까지도 지켜보고, 필요하다면 너를 가릴 구름이 어느 방위에 있는지 알아 두는 게 잘하는 일이었지. 하지만 하나의 행성이 떠올랐고, 너는 그 빛 속에 사라져 버렸어.\"\n\n그는 흰 다리의 말을 거리 모퉁이로 돌려, 집 뒤편의 창문들 가운데서 빛나는 창 하나를 찾았다. 그것에는 어떤 위엄 있는 자태와, 장갑 낀 손과, 아름다운 새의 날개 깃털이 바닥에 흩뿌려지던 기억, 그리고 예복 위의 가볍고 흰 솜털이 마치 먼 폭풍이 일어날 때처럼 흔들리고 바스락거리던 기억이 결부되어 있었다. 이것들이, 그가 다시 돌아서서 어두워지고 인적 없는 공원들을 빠른 속도로 가로질러 달릴 때 그가 지니고 간 것들이었다.\n\n치명적인 진실로, 이것들은 한 여인과, 그를 미워하는 오만한 여인과 결부되어 있었다. 그러나 그 여인은 그의 교활함과, 자신의 오만함과 분노에 이끌려, 더디지만 확실한 정도로, 그의 동행을 견디게 되고, 조금씩 그를 받아들여, 자기 자신의 남편에 대한 도전적인 무시며, 자기 자신에 대한 높은 존중을 내던진 일을 자기에게 이야기할 특권을 가진 자로 여기게 된 터였다. 이것들은 그를 깊이 미워하는 한 여인과 결부되어 있었으니, 그녀는 그를 알았고, 그를 알기에 그를 불신했으며, 또 그가 자기를 알기에 그를 불신했다. 그러나 그녀는, 그에게 품은 미움에도 불구하고, 그가 날마다 자기에게 더, 더 가까이 다가오도록 허락함으로써 그 사나운 분노를 키워 갔다. 미움에도 불구하고! 바로 그 이유 때문에. 왜냐하면 그 미움의 밑바닥에, 그녀의 위협하는 눈으로도 꿰뚫기엔 너무 깊은 곳에—희미하게나마 그녀가 그 속을 들여다볼 수는 있었으나—어두운 보복이 놓여 있었으니, 그 가장 희미한 그림자조차 한 번 보고 몸서리치고 다시는 보지 못했더라도, 그것은 그녀의 영혼에 충분한 오점이 되었을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n그런 여인의 환영이 그가 말을 모는 동안 그의 주위를 떠돌았던가, 그 실체에 충실하게, 그에게 또렷이?\n\n그렇다. 그는 마음속으로 그녀를, 그녀의 모습 그대로 정확히 보았다. 그녀는 자기의 오만과 분노와 미움을 지닌 채 그와 동행했으니, 그 모든 것이 그녀의 아름다움만큼이나 그에게 또렷했다. 그리고 그에게 그녀의 미움보다 더 또렷한 것은 없었다. 그는 때로 그녀가 자기 곁에서 도도하고 쌀쌀맞게 있는 것을 보았고, 때로는 자기 말발굽 사이에 쓰러져 먼지를 뒤집어쓴 것을 보았다. 그러나 그는 언제나 그녀를 있는 그대로, 가식 없이 보았고, 그녀가 가고 있는 그 위험한 길 위에서 그녀를 지켜보았다.\n\n그리고 말 타기가 끝나고, 그가 새로 옷을 차려입고, 고개를 숙인 채 부드러운 목소리와 어르는 미소를 띠고 그녀의 환한 방의 불빛 속으로 들어섰을 때에도, 그는 여전히 그녀를 또렷이 보았다. 그는 심지어 그 장갑 낀 손의 비밀까지 짐작했고, 그 짐작 때문에 그 손을 자기 손 안에 그만큼 더 오래 쥐고 있었다. 그녀가 가고 있는 그 위험한 길 위에, 그는 여전히 있었다. 그리고 그녀가 그 위에 발자국 하나를 찍을 때마다, 그는 자기 발자국을 그 바로 옆에 똑바로 찍었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII. The Thunderbolt\n\n돔비 씨와 그의 아내 사이의 장벽은 세월이 흘러도 약해지지 않았다.\n어울리지 않는 부부, 제 자신에게도 서로에게도 불행한 두 사람, 묶인 손을 잇는 수갑 외에는 그 어떤 끈으로도 이어져 있지 않았고, 서로 떨어지려 발버둥 칠 때마다 그 수갑을 어찌나 모질게 잡아당겼던지 살갗을 뚫고 뼈에까지 쓸려 들어갔으니, 고통을 달래고 분노를 누그러뜨리는 위로자인 세월도 그들에게는 아무 도움이 되지 못했다. 그들의 자존심은, 종류와 대상은 서로 달랐어도 그 정도에서는 대등했다. 그리고 부싯돌 같은 그들의 대립 속에서 불꽃이 튀어 올랐으니, 그 불은 사정에 따라 연기만 피우기도 하고 활활 타오르기도 했지만, 둘이 함께 닿을 수 있는 범위 안의 모든 것을 태워 버렸고, 그들의 결혼 길을 잿더미의 길로 만들어 버렸다.\n\n그에게도 공정하기로 하자. 자기 생애라는 그 괴물 같은 망상은, 모래시계 속에서 흘러내리는 모래알 한 톨 한 톨마다 부풀어 오르며, 그로 하여금 그녀를 몰아붙이게 했지만, 그는 그것이 무엇을 향한 것인지도 거의 생각하지 않았고, 어떻게 하는지도 헤아리지 않았다. 그러나 그녀를 향한 그의 감정은, 그것이 어떤 것이었든 간에, 처음 그대로 남아 있었다. 그녀에게는 영문도 모를 큰 결함이 있었으니, 자기의 막대한 중요성을 인정하는 것에, 그리고 거기에 완전히 복종한다는 것을 시인하는 것에 스스로를 대립시킨다는 점이었다. 그 점에서는 그녀를 바로잡고 굴복시키는 것이 필요했다. 그러나 그 밖에는, 그는 여전히 그 특유의 차가운 방식으로 그녀를 이렇게 여겼다. 그녀가 마음만 먹는다면 자기의 선택과 이름에 영예를 더해 줄 수 있고, 자기 소유권에 위신을 비춰 줄 수 있는 귀부인이라고.\n\n이제 그녀는, 격정적이고 도도한 원한의 온 힘을 다해, 날마다 시시각각으로—벽에 비친 그림자들을 바라보며 앉아 있던 자기 방의 그 밤부터, 빠르게 다가오는 더 깊은 밤에 이르도록—자기를 겨냥해 갖가지 굴욕과 격분을 지휘하는 한 사람의 형상에 어두운 시선을 던졌다. 그리고 그 형상은, 여전히 그녀의 남편이었다.\n\n돔비 씨를 그토록 가차 없이 지배하는 그 주된 악덕은 부자연스러운 특성이었을까? 더러는 따져 볼 만한 일이리라. 자연이란 무엇이며, 인간이 그것을 어떻게 바꾸려 드는지, 그리고 그렇게 강제로 빚어진 일그러짐 속에서는 부자연스러운 것이야말로 자연스러운 것이 아닐는지를. 우리의 위대한 어머니의 그 어떤 아들이나 딸이든 좁은 울타리 안에 가두어 놓고, 그 갇힌 자를 단 하나의 관념에 묶어 두고, 주위에 둘러선 소수의 소심하거나 음흉한 사람들이 그것을 노예처럼 떠받들어 길러 준다면, 자유로운 정신의 날개를 펴고—그 날개는 곧 시들어 쓸모없어지지만—솟아올라 자연을 그 포괄적인 진실 속에서 본 적이 한 번도 없는, 기꺼이 갇혀 있는 그 포로에게 자연이란 도대체 무엇이겠는가!\n\n아아! 우리 주위 세상에는, 더없이 부자연스러우면서도, 그렇게 부자연스럽다는 점에서 더없이 자연스러운 것들이 그리도 적단 말인가? 치안판사나 재판관이 사회의 부자연스러운 버림받은 자들을 훈계하는 것을 들어 보라. 야만스러운 습성에서 부자연스럽고, 염치의 결여에서 부자연스럽고, 선과 악의 모든 구별을 잃고 뒤섞어 버린 점에서 부자연스러우며, 무지에서, 악덕에서, 무모함에서, 완고함에서, 마음에서, 외모에서, 모든 것에서 부자연스러운 자들을. 그러나 저 선량한 목사나 의사를 따라가 보라. 숨을 들이쉴 때마다 목숨을 걸고서, 우리의 마차 바퀴 소리와 보도의 돌을 밟는 날마다의 발길이 메아리치는 바로 그 안쪽에 자리한 그들의 소굴로 내려가는 그 사람을. 그 혐오스러운 광경의 세상을 둘러보라—수백만의 불멸하는 피조물들에게는 이 땅 위에 다른 세상이라곤 없으니—그것을 아주 가볍게 입에 올리기만 해도 인간성이 반발하고, 바로 옆 거리에 사는 까다로운 우아함은 귀를 막고 혀 짧은 소리로 \"난 안 믿어!\"라고 말하는 그런 세상을. 건강과 생명에 독이 되는 온갖 불순물로 더럽혀진 그 오염된 공기를 들이마셔 보라. 그리고 우리 인류에게 기쁨과 행복을 누리라고 주어진 모든 감각이 모욕당하고 병들고 역겨워져, 오직 비참과 죽음만이 들어올 수 있는 통로가 되는 것을 겪어 보라. 이 악취 나는 자리에 심긴 어떤 소박한 풀이나 꽃이나 멀쩡한 잡초가, 신께서 마련하신 대로 제 자연스러운 성장을 이루거나 작은 잎사귀들을 햇빛을 향해 펼칠 수 있으리라고 생각해 보려 해도 부질없다. 그러고 나서, 발육이 멈춘 몸과 사악한 얼굴을 한 어떤 끔찍한 아이를 불러 세우고, 그 아이의 부자연스러운 죄악을 늘어놓으며, 그토록 일찍부터 천국에서 멀리 떨어져 있음을 한탄해 보라—그러나 그 아이가 지옥에서 잉태되고, 태어나고, 길러졌다는 사실을 조금이라도 생각해 보라!\n\n물리 과학을 연구하여 그것을 인간의 건강에 적용하는 사람들은 우리에게 이렇게 말한다. 만일 오염된 공기에서 피어오르는 유독한 미립자들이 눈에 보이는 것이라면, 우리는 그것들이 그런 소굴 위에 짙은 검은 구름이 되어 드리워 있고, 도시의 더 나은 부분들을 더럽히려 천천히 굴러가는 것을 보게 되리라고. 그러나 만일 그것들과 함께 피어오르며, 우리 자연의 영원한 법칙에 따라 그것들과 떼어 놓을 수 없는 그 도덕적 역병까지도 눈에 보이게 할 수 있다면, 그 폭로는 얼마나 끔찍할 것인가! 그렇게 된다면 우리는 타락과 불경과 만취와 절도와 살인, 그리고 인류의 자연스러운 애정과 반감을 거스르는 이름 없는 죄들의 긴 행렬이, 그 저주받은 장소들 위에 드리운 채 기어 나와, 무고한 자들을 시들게 하고 순결한 자들 사이에 전염을 퍼뜨리는 것을 보게 되리라. 그렇게 된다면 우리는, 우리의 병원과 나병원으로 흘러드는 바로 그 독 묻은 샘물이 감옥을 범람시키고, 죄수 호송선을 깊이 가라앉히며, 바다를 건너 굴러가, 광대한 대륙들을 범죄로 뒤덮는 것을 보게 되리라. 그렇게 된다면 우리는 이 사실을 알고 소스라치게 놀라 서 있게 되리라. 우리 아이들을 쓰러뜨리고 아직 태어나지 않은 세대에까지 대물림될 질병을 우리가 만들어 내는 바로 그곳에서, 똑같은 확실한 과정을 통해, 순결이라곤 모르는 어린것과, 정숙함도 부끄러움도 없는 젊은이와, 고통과 죄밖에는 아무것도 무르익지 않은 장년과, 우리가 지닌 형상에 대한 치욕인 시들어 버린 노년, 곧 부자연스러운 인간성을 우리가 똑같이 길러 낸다는 것을! 우리가 가시나무에서 포도를, 엉겅퀴에서 무화과를 거두게 될 때, 우리의 사악한 도시 뒷골목의 오물에서 곡식의 들판이 솟아나고, 그것이 키워 내는 기름진 묘지에서 장미가 피어날 때, 그제야 우리는 자연스러운 인간성을 찾아볼 수 있을 것이며, 그것이 그런 씨앗에서 자라나는 것을 발견하게 되리라.\n\n오, 그 이야기 속의 절름발이 악마보다 더 강력하고 자비로운 손으로 집들의 지붕을 걷어 내고, 그리스도교를 믿는 백성들에게, 파괴의 천사가 그들 사이를 누비며 나아갈 때 그 행렬을 불려 주려고 그들의 가정 한복판에서 어떤 어두운 형상들이 빠져나오는지를 보여 줄 선한 영혼이 하나 있다면! 단 하룻밤만이라도, 우리가 너무 오래 방치해 온 그 현장들에서 솟아오르는 창백한 유령들을, 악덕과 열병이 함께 번식하는 그 짙고 음울한 공기에서—언제나 쏟아져 내리고 언제나 더 자욱하게 닥쳐오는 그 무서운 사회적 응보를 비처럼 퍼부으며—솟아오르는 그것들을 보여 줄 영혼이 있다면! 그런 밤 위에 밝아 오는 아침은 환하고 복될 것이다. 사람들이 자기 자신이 만들어 놓은 걸림돌들—그것들은 자기와 영원 사이의 길 위에 놓인 한낱 먼지 티끌에 불과하건만—에 더 이상 가로막히지 않고서, 한 공통된 기원을 지닌 피조물답게, 한 가족의 아버지께 한 가지 의무를 지고, 하나의 공통된 목적을 향해 나아가, 세상을 더 나은 곳으로 만드는 데 스스로를 바칠 것이기 때문이다!\n\n자기 주위의 인간 삶의 세계를 한 번도 내다본 적 없는 이들 가운데 더러를 흔들어 깨워, 그 세계와 자기 자신의 관계를 깨닫게 하고, 자기 자신의 옹졸한 공감과 평가 속에 깃든 자연의 왜곡—한번 시작되고 나면 그 전개에 있어, 알려진 가장 비천한 타락만큼이나 크면서도 또한 그만큼 자연스러운 그 왜곡—을 알게 해 주는 것만으로도, 그날은 못지않게 환하고 복될 것이다.\n\n그러나 그런 날은 돔비 씨에게도, 그의 아내에게도 한 번도 밝아 온 적이 없었다. 그리고 두 사람 각자의 길은 이미 정해져 있었다.\n\n그가 사고를 당한 뒤 이어진 여섯 달 동안, 그들은 서로에 대해 똑같은 관계를 유지했다. 대리석 바위라도 그녀만큼 완강하게 그의 앞을 가로막지는 못했을 것이며, 깊은 동굴 속 한 줄기 빛도 들지 않는 곳에 고인 어떤 차디찬 샘물도 그만큼 음울하거나 차갑지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n새 보금자리의 약속이 밝아 올 때 그녀의 가슴속에서 팔랑이던 희망은, 이제 플로렌스의 마음에서 완전히 사라지고 없었다. 그 보금자리는 거의 두 해가 되었다. 그리고 그녀 안에 깃든 그 끈기 있는 믿음조차도 그런 경험의 날마다의 시듦을 견뎌 내지는 못했다. 만일 그녀에게 희망 비슷한 어떤 어렴풋한 공상이라도 남아 있었다면, 그것은 이디스와 자기 아버지가 먼 훗날 어느 때엔가 함께 더 행복해질지도 모른다는 것이었지, 자기 아버지가 자기를 언젠가 사랑해 주리라는 것은 이제 조금도 없었다. 그에게서 무언가 작은 누그러짐을 본 것 같다고 상상했던 그 짧은 동안은, 그 전후의 오랜 냉담함에 대한 긴 기억 속에 묻혀 잊히고 말았고, 혹은 그저 서글픈 망상으로만 기억될 뿐이었다.\n\n플로렌스는 여전히 그를 사랑했다. 그러나 차츰차츰, 눈앞의 그 모진 현실로서가 아니라, 오히려 한때 그러했던, 혹은 그러했을 수도 있었을 어떤 소중한 사람으로서 그를 사랑하게 되었다. 어린 폴이나 자기 어머니의 추억을 사랑할 때 깃들던 그 누그러진 슬픔 같은 것이, 이제는 그를 떠올리는 그녀의 생각 속에도 스며들어, 그 생각들을 말하자면 하나의 소중한 추억으로 만들어 가는 듯했다. 그가 그녀에게는 죽은 것이나 다름없기 때문인지, 또 그 까닭의 일부는 그가 그녀의 옛 애정의 대상들과 한몫 얽혀 있기 때문인지, 또 일부는 그를 시들어 버린 희망들 및 그가 얼어붙게 한 다정함들과 오래 결부 지어 왔기 때문인지, 그녀로서는 말할 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀가 사랑했던 아버지는 그녀에게 점점 막연하고 꿈결 같은 관념이 되어 가기 시작했다. 자기가 사랑하던 오라비가 아직 살아 있어 어른으로 자라나 자기를 지켜 주고 아껴 주는 모습을, 때때로 마음속에 떠올려 보던 그 영상보다도 그녀의 실제 삶과 거의 더 실질적으로 이어져 있지 못했다.\n\n그 변화는, 그것을 변화라 부를 수 있다면, 어린 시절에서 여인 시절로 넘어가는 변화처럼 그녀에게 슬며시 다가왔고, 또 그 변화와 함께 찾아왔다. 플로렌스가 외로운 사색에 잠겨 이런 생각들을 의식하게 되었을 때, 그녀는 거의 열일곱 살이었다.\n\n이제 그녀는 자주 혼자였다. 그녀와 어머니 사이의 옛 정겨움이 크게 달라졌기 때문이다. 아버지가 사고를 당했을 때, 그리고 그가 아래층 자기 방에 누워 있던 무렵, 플로렌스는 이디스가 자기를 피한다는 것을 처음으로 알아챘다. 상처받고 충격받았으면서도, 둘이 정작 만났을 때 보이는 그녀의 애정과는 이것을 도무지 조화시킬 수 없었던 플로렌스는, 한 번 더, 밤에 그녀의 방으로 그녀를 찾아갔다.\n\n\"엄마,\" 플로렌스가 가만히 그녀 곁으로 다가가며 말했다, \"제가 엄마를 화나게 했나요?\"\n\n이디스는 \"아니\"라고 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 무슨 짓을 했나 봐요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"그게 뭔지 말씀해 주세요. 엄마는 저를 대하는 태도가 달라지셨어요, 사랑하는 엄마. 저는 아주 작은 변화라도 얼마나 곧바로 느끼는지 몰라요. 저는 온 마음을 다해 엄마를 사랑하니까요.\"\n\n\"나도 너를 그렇게 사랑한단다,\" 이디스가 말했다. \"아, 플로렌스, 지금 이 순간보다 더한 적이 없었음을 믿어 다오!\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 그렇게 자주 제게서 멀어지시고, 멀찍이 떨어져 계세요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다. \"그리고 왜 가끔 저를 그렇게 이상하게 바라보세요, 사랑하는 엄마? 그러시잖아요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n이디스는 어두운 눈으로 그렇다는 뜻을 나타냈다.\n\n\"왜요?\" 플로렌스가 애원하듯 되물었다. \"왜 그러시는지 말씀해 주세요, 제가 엄마를 어떻게 하면 더 기쁘게 해 드릴 수 있는지 알 수 있게요. 그리고 다시는 이러지 않겠다고 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"내 플로렌스야,\" 이디스가, 자기 목을 감싼 손을 잡고, 자기 앞 바닥에 무릎 꿇은 플로렌스가 그토록 사랑스럽게 들여다보는 그 눈을 들여다보며 대답했다. \"왜 그런지는 네게 말해 줄 수가 없단다. 내가 말할 일도, 네가 들을 일도 아니야. 하지만 그것이 그렇다는 것, 그리고 그래야만 한다는 것은 내가 안단다. 그렇지 않다면 내가 이러겠니?\"\n\n\"우리는 멀어지게 되는 건가요, 엄마?\" 플로렌스가 겁먹은 사람처럼 그녀를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n이디스의 말없는 입술이 \"그래\"라는 모양을 지었다.\n\n플로렌스는 점점 커지는 두려움과 의아함으로 그녀를 바라보았고, 마침내 얼굴을 타고 흘러내리는 눈물에 눈이 가려져 더는 그녀를 볼 수 없었다.\n\n\"플로렌스! 내 생명아!\" 이디스가 황급히 말했다, \"내 말 좀 들어 보렴. 나는 이 슬픔을 보고 있을 수가 없구나. 좀 진정하렴. 너도 보다시피 나는 차분하지 않니, 그렇다면 이게 나한테는 아무것도 아니란 말이냐?\"\n\n뒤의 말을 할 때 그녀는 다시 한결같은 목소리와 태도로 돌아갔고, 이내 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"완전히 멀어지는 건 아니야. 어느 정도만. 그것도 겉으로만 그런 거란다, 플로렌스. 내 가슴속에서는 나는 여전히 네게 똑같고, 앞으로도 영원히 그럴 테니까. 하지만 내가 하는 일은 나 자신을 위해 하는 게 아니란다.\"\n\n\"저를 위해서인가요, 엄마?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"그것이 무엇인지를 아는 것으로 충분하단다,\" 이디스가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. \"왜 그런지는 별로 중요하지 않아. 사랑하는 플로렌스, 우리가 덜 자주 만나는 편이 더 낫단다—그래야만 하고—꼭 그래야만 해. 우리 사이에 있었던 그 다정한 신뢰는 끊어져야만 한단다.\"\n\n\"언제요?\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다. \"오, 엄마, 언제요?\"\n\n\"지금부터,\" 이디스가 말했다.\n\n\"앞으로 영영요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다고 말하는 건 아니야,\" 이디스가 대답했다. \"나도 그건 모르겠구나. 또 우리 사이의 가까움이라는 것이, 아무리 좋게 봐도, 어울리지 않고 거룩하지 못한 결합이어서, 거기서 좋은 것이 나올 리 없다는 걸 내가 미리 알았어야 했다고는 말하지 않으마. 내가 여기까지 온 길은 네가 결코 밟지 않을 길들을 지나온 것이고, 앞으로 내 길이 어디로 뻗어 있을지는—하느님은 아시겠지—나는 보이지 않는구나—\"\n\n그녀의 목소리는 사그라들어 침묵으로 잦아들었다. 그리고 그녀는, 플로렌스가 전에 한 번 알아챈 적 있던 그 똑같은 기이한 두려움과 격렬한 회피의 빛을 띤 채, 플로렌스를 바라보며, 거의 그녀에게서 움츠러들 듯이 앉아 있었다. 곧이어 그 똑같은 어두운 자존심과 분노가 뒤를 이어, 거친 하프의 현을 가로지르는 성난 화음처럼, 그녀의 몸과 얼굴 위를 휩쓸고 지나갔다. 그러나 거기에는 그 어떤 부드러움도 겸손함도 뒤따르지 않았다. 이번에는 그녀가 고개를 떨구지도, 울지도, 플로렌스 말고는 아무 희망도 없다고 말하지도 않았다. 그녀는 마치 아름다운 메두사가 그를 정면으로 마주 보며 죽음으로 쓰러뜨리려는 듯, 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들었다. 그렇다, 만일 그녀에게 그런 마력이 있었더라면 그녀는 정말로 그렇게 했으리라.\n\n\"엄마,\" 플로렌스가 불안하게 말했다, \"엄마한테는 무언가 변화가 있어요, 제게 하시는 말씀 그 이상의, 저를 겁나게 하는 변화가요. 잠시만 엄마 곁에 있게 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"안 돼,\" 이디스가 말했다, \"안 된다, 사랑하는 아이야. 나는 지금 혼자 있는 게 가장 낫고, 무엇보다도 너에게서 떨어져 있는 게 가장 옳단다. 내게 아무것도 묻지 말고, 다만 이것만은 믿어 다오. 내가 너에게 변덕스럽거나 종잡을 수 없어 보일 때, 그것은 내 뜻으로도 아니고 나 자신을 위해서도 아니라는 것을. 우리가 전보다 서로 더 남남이 되었어도, 내 마음속에서는 너에게 변함이 없다는 것을 믿어 다오. 일찍이 너의 어두운 집을 더 어둡게 한 것을 용서해 다오—내가 그 위에 드리운 그림자라는 걸 나도 잘 안다—그리고 이 이야기는 다시는 하지 말자꾸나.\"\n\n\"엄마,\" 플로렌스가 흐느꼈다, \"우리 헤어지는 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"우리는 헤어지지 않으려고 이렇게 하는 거란다,\" 이디스가 말했다. \"더는 묻지 마. 가렴, 플로렌스! 내 사랑과 내 회한이 너와 함께 갈 거야!\"\n\n그녀는 플로렌스를 껴안고, 그녀를 내보냈다. 그리고 플로렌스가 방을 나설 때, 이디스는 마치 자기의 선한 천사가 저 모습으로 떠나가, 이제 자기를 제 것으로 차지하고 이마에 제 봉인을 찍는 그 오만하고 분에 찬 격정들에게 자기를 내맡기는 것을 보듯이, 멀어져 가는 그 모습을 바라보았다.\n\n그때부터, 플로렌스와 그녀는 예전과 같은 사이가 더는 아니었다. 며칠씩 잇따라, 그들은 식탁에서, 그것도 돔비 씨가 있을 때 말고는 좀처럼 마주치지 않았다. 그럴 때면 이디스는 오만하고, 굽힐 줄 모르며, 말이 없었고, 결코 그녀를 쳐다보지 않았다. 돔비 씨가 회복해 가는 동안에도, 또 그 후에도 자주 그랬듯이, 카커 씨가 그 자리에 있을 때면 이디스는 평소보다도 한층 더 그녀에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 지냈고, 한층 더 그녀에게 쌀쌀맞게 대했다. 그러나 그녀와 플로렌스는, 곁에 아무도 없을 때 마주치면, 비록 오만한 표정이 예전처럼 누그러지지는 않았어도, 옛날처럼 다정하게 서로를 껴안곤 했다. 그리고 그녀가 밤늦게 외출했다 돌아온 날이면, 으레 그랬듯이 어둠 속에서 플로렌스의 방으로 살그머니 올라가, 베개 위에 대고 \"잘 자거라\" 하고 속삭이곤 했다. 잠결에 그런 방문을 의식하지 못하면서도, 플로렌스는 때때로 그 부드럽게 속삭인 말들의 꿈에서 깨어나듯 깨어나, 얼굴에 닿는 입술의 감촉을 느끼는 것만 같았다. 그러나 달이 갈수록 그런 일은 점점 드물어졌다.\n\n이제 플로렌스 자신의 가슴속 공허함이 정말로 다시금 그녀의 둘레에 외로움을 빚어내기 시작했다. 그녀가 사랑하던 아버지의 영상이 어느새 한낱 추상으로 변해 버렸듯이, 이디스 역시, 그녀의 애정이 휘감겨 있던 다른 모든 이들의 운명을 따라, 날마다 멀어지고, 흐려지고, 점점 더 창백해져 갔다. 조금씩 조금씩, 그녀는 자기가 한때 그러했던 모습의 물러가는 유령처럼 플로렌스에게서 멀어져 갔다. 조금씩 조금씩, 둘 사이의 틈은 벌어지고 더 깊어 보였다. 조금씩 조금씩, 그녀가 보여 주었던 진지함과 다정함의 온갖 힘은, 플로렌스에게는 보이지 않는 깊은 벼랑 끝에 서서 감히 아래를 내려다보는, 대담하고 성난 강인함 속에 얼어붙어 갔다.\n\n이디스를 잃는다는 그 무거운 상실에 맞세울 만한 위안은 단 하나뿐이었고, 비록 그것이 그녀의 짓눌린 가슴에 변변찮은 위로일지라도, 그녀는 그것을 얼마간의 위안으로 여겨 보려 애썼다. 더는 두 사람을 향한 애정과 의무 사이에서 갈라지지 않게 되었으니, 플로렌스는 둘 다를 사랑하면서도 어느 쪽에게도 부당하지 않을 수 있었다. 자기의 애틋한 상상 속 그림자로서, 그녀는 두 사람을 자기 가슴속에 똑같은 자리에 놓고, 아무런 의혹으로도 그들을 그르치지 않을 수 있었다.\n\n그래서 그녀는 그렇게 하려 애썼다. 때때로, 그것도 자주, 이디스의 이 변화의 원인에 대한 의아한 추측들이 그녀의 마음속에 비집고 들어와 그녀를 겁나게 했다. 그러나 다시 말없는 슬픔과 외로움에 자기를 내맡긴 그 고요함 속에서, 그녀의 마음은 캐묻기를 좋아하는 마음이 아니었다. 플로렌스로서는 다만, 자기의 약속의 별이 이 집을 뒤덮은 전체적인 침울함 속에 흐려졌음을 떠올리며, 눈물 흘리고 체념하는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n이렇게, 어린 가슴의 넘쳐흐르는 사랑을 덧없는 형상들에 쏟아붓는 꿈속에서, 그리고 그 거센 물결이 도로 자기에게로 밀려오는 것 말고는 거의 겪어 본 것이 없는 현실 세계에서 살아가며, 플로렌스는 열일곱 살이 되었다. 그 외로운 삶이 그녀를 소심하고 내성적으로 만들기는 했어도, 그녀의 다정한 성품이나 진실한 천성을 모질게 만들지는 못했다. 천진한 순박함에 있어서는 아이였고, 정숙한 자립심과 깊고 강렬한 감정에 있어서는 여인이었으니, 아이와 여인이 동시에 그녀의 얼굴과 가냘프고 여린 자태에 함께 드러나, 거기서 우아하게 어우러지는 듯했다—마치 봄이 여름이 와도 차마 떠나려 하지 않고, 꽃들의 이른 아름다움을 그들의 만개와 뒤섞으려 하는 것처럼. 그러나 그녀의 떨리는 목소리에, 그녀의 고요한 눈에, 때로는 그녀의 머리 위에 깃든 듯한 어떤 슬기롭고 영묘한 빛에, 그리고 언제나 그녀의 아름다움 위에 어린 어떤 수심 어린 분위기에는, 죽은 소년에게서 보였던 것과 같은 어떤 표정이 있었다. 하인 방의 회의는 자기들끼리 그렇게 수군거리고, 고개를 가로저으며, 더욱 끈끈한 우의의 결속 속에서 더 많이 먹고 마셨다.\n\n이 눈썰미 좋은 무리에게는 돔비 씨와 돔비 부인에 대해, 그리고 두 사람 사이의 중재자로 보이는, 화해를 붙여 보려는 듯 오가면서도 끝내 그러지 못하는 카커 씨에 대해 할 말이 많았다. 그들은 모두 이 불편한 사태를 개탄했고, 핍친 부인(그 인기 없음으로 말하자면 따를 자가 없었다)이 여기에 어느 정도 손을 댔다는 데 모두 의견을 같이했다. 그러나 대체로, 그토록 좋은 화제를 집결점으로 삼게 된 것은 즐거운 일이었고, 그들은 그것을 한껏 부풀려 가며 무척 재미있어했다.\n\n이 집을 찾아오는 일반 방문객들, 그리고 돔비 씨 부부가 찾아가는 사람들은, 적어도 오만함이라는 점에서는 어지간히 어울리는 짝이라고 여겼고, 그 이상은 더 생각하지 않았다. 등을 드러낸 그 젊은 아가씨는 스큐턴 부인이 죽은 뒤로 한동안 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그녀는 특별히 친한 몇몇 벗들에게 평소처럼 매혹적인 작은 비명을 지르며, 자기는 그 집안을 묘비라느니 그런 끔찍한 것들에 대한 관념과 떼어 놓을 수가 없다고 말했다. 그러나 막상 다시 찾아왔을 때, 그녀는 돔비 씨가 시계에 금 인장 한 묶음을 차고 다니는 것 말고는 잘못된 데를 하나도 보지 못했는데, 그것만은 이미 타파된 미신이라며 그녀를 몹시 충격에 빠뜨렸다. 이 젊은 매혹꾼은 며느리란 원칙상 못마땅한 존재라고 여겼다. 그것 말고는 플로렌스에 대해 흠잡을 것이 없었으나, 다만 \"맵시\"가 가엾을 만큼 모자란다는 것뿐이었으니—이는 어쩌면 등을 두고 하는 말이었을 것이다. 격식 차린 행사 때만 이 집에 오는 많은 이들은 플로렌스가 누구인지조차 거의 알지 못했고, 집으로 돌아가며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"아니, 저 구석에 있던 사람이 돔비 양이었어? 아주 예쁘긴 한데, 좀 가냘프고 수심에 잠긴 모습이더군!\"\n\n지난 여섯 달의 그런 삶을 생각하면, 확실히 그럴 만도 했다. 플로렌스는 아버지가 이디스와 결혼한 지 두 돌이 되기 전날(첫 돌이 돌아왔을 때 스큐턴 부인은 마비에 걸려 누워 있었다), 두려움에 가까운 불안을 안고 만찬 식탁에 앉았다. 그렇게 느낄 근거라곤, 그 날짜라는 것과, 흘끗 스쳐 본 아버지 얼굴의 표정과, 늘 그녀에게 불쾌했지만 이날따라 그 어느 때보다도 더 불쾌하게 느껴지는 카커 씨의 존재밖에는 없었다.\n\n이디스는 화려하게 차려입고 있었다. 그녀와 돔비 씨는 저녁에 어느 큰 모임에 가기로 되어 있었고, 그날 만찬 시간은 늦었기 때문이다. 그녀는 모두가 식탁에 앉을 때까지 모습을 보이지 않았는데, 그때 카커 씨가 일어나 그녀를 의자로 안내했다. 아름답고 눈부신 그녀였지만, 그녀의 얼굴과 분위기에는 그녀를 플로렌스로부터, 또 모든 이로부터 영영 가망 없이 떼어 놓는 듯한 무언가가 있었다. 그런데도, 한순간, 플로렌스는 그녀의 눈이 자기에게로 향했을 때 거기 어린 한 줄기 다정함의 빛을 보았으니, 그것은 그녀가 물러나 가 있는 그 거리를 그 어느 때보다도 더 큰 슬픔과 회한의 원인으로 만들었다.\n\n만찬 중에는 이야기가 거의 오가지 않았다. 플로렌스는 아버지가 이따금 카커 씨에게 사업 문제를 이야기하는 것을, 그리고 그가 나직이 대답하는 것을 들었으나, 그들이 무슨 말을 하는지에는 거의 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 그저 만찬이 끝나기만을 바랐다. 후식이 식탁에 차려지고, 시중드는 하인 하나 없이 그들만 남게 되자, 아까부터 몇 번이나 불길한 징조 같은 헛기침을 해 대던 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"돔비 부인, 아마 알고 있을 텐데, 내가 가정부에게 내일 이곳에 손님 몇이 만찬에 올 거라고 일러 두었소.\"\n\n\"저는 집에서 만찬을 들지 않겠어요,\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"큰 모임은 아니오,\" 돔비 씨가, 그녀의 말을 듣지 못한 척 무심한 태도를 가장하며 말을 이었다. \"그저 열둘이나 열넷 정도요. 내 누이와 배그스톡 소령, 그리고 당신이 잘은 모르는 몇몇 사람들이오.\"\n\n\"저는 집에서 만찬을 들지 않겠어요,\" 그녀가 되풀이했다.\n\n\"내가 지금 이 날을 그리 즐거운 기억으로 간직할 이유가 아무리 미심쩍다 한들, 돔비 부인,\" 돔비 씨가, 그녀가 말하지 않기라도 한 듯 여전히 위엄 있게 말을 이어 갔다, \"세상 앞에서 지켜야 할 체면이라는 것이 이런 일에는 있는 법이오. 당신이 자기 자신에 대한 존중심이 없다 하더라도, 돔비 부인—\"\n\n\"저는 그런 거 없어요,\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"부인,\" 돔비 씨가 손으로 식탁을 치며 외쳤다, \"제발 내 말을 들으시오. 내 말은, 당신이 자기 자신에 대한 존중심이 없다 하더라도—\"\n\n\"그리고 제 말은, 그런 게 없다는 거예요,\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n그는 그녀를 바라보았다. 그러나 그 대답으로 그녀가 그에게 내보인 얼굴은, 죽음 그 자체가 들여다본다 해도 달라지지 않았으리라.\n\n\"카커,\" 돔비 씨가 한결 차분하게 그 신사 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다, \"전에도 자네가 나와 돔비 부인 사이의 전달 매개가 되어 준 적이 있고, 또 나로서는 적어도 나 개인에 관한 한 삶의 예의를 지키려 하니, 수고스럽겠지만 돔비 부인에게 이렇게 알려 주게. 부인이 자기 자신에 대한 존중심이 없다 하더라도, 나는 나 자신에 대한 존중심은 좀 있으니, 따라서 내일 일에 대한 내 결정을 고수하겠노라고.\"\n\n\"당신의 군주이신 주인 나리께 전하세요,\" 이디스가 말했다, \"제가 이 문제에 대해서는 이따가 말씀드릴 것이며, 그것도 그분과 단둘이서 말씀드리겠노라고요.\"\n\n\"카커 씨는, 부인,\" 그녀의 남편이 말했다, \"내가 당신에게 그 특권을 거절하지 않을 수 없게 하는 이유를 알고 있으니, 그런 전갈은 전하지 않아도 되네.\" 그는 말하면서 그녀의 눈이 움직이는 것을 보았고, 자기 눈으로 그 시선을 좇았다.\n\n\"당신 딸이 여기 있어요,\" 이디스가 말했다.\n\n\"내 딸은 여기 그대로 있을 거요,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n일어서 있던 플로렌스가 다시 자리에 앉아, 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 떨었다.\n\n\"내 딸은, 부인—\" 돔비 씨가 입을 열었다.\n\n그러나 이디스가, 조금도 높이지 않았는데도 어찌나 또렷하고 힘 있고 분명한지 회오리바람 속에서도 들렸을 법한 목소리로 그를 가로막았다.\n\n\"단둘이서 말씀드리겠다고 했잖아요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"정신이 나간 게 아니라면, 제 말을 새겨들으세요.\"\n\n\"나는 당신에게 말할 권한이 있소, 부인,\" 그녀의 남편이 응수했다, \"언제든 어디서든 내가 원하는 대로. 그리고 여기서 지금 말하는 것이 내 뜻이오.\"\n\n그녀는 방을 나가려는 듯 몸을 일으켰다. 그러나 다시 자리에 앉아, 겉으로는 더없이 침착하게 그를 바라보며, 똑같은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 하세요!\"\n\n\"먼저 말해 두어야겠는데, 당신의 태도에는 위협적인 기색이 있소, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다, \"그건 당신에게 어울리지 않소.\"\n\n그녀가 웃었다. 머리에 꽂힌 다이아몬드들이 흔들리며 떨려 반짝였다. 보석을 두른 이가 위험에 처하면 빛이 바랜다는 보석에 관한 우화들이 있다. 만일 이것들이 그런 보석이었더라면, 그 갇혀 있던 빛줄기들이 그 순간 달아나 버려, 그것들은 납덩이처럼 칙칙해졌으리라.\n\n카커는 눈을 내리깐 채 듣고 있었다.\n\n\"내 딸로 말하자면, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 자기 이야기의 실마리를 다시 이어 가며 말했다, \"그 애가 피해야 할 행실이 무엇인지를 아는 것은 나에 대한 그 애의 의무와 결코 어긋나지 않소. 지금 당신은 그 애에게 그런 종류의 아주 강력한 본보기를 보여 주고 있으니, 그 애가 거기서 교훈을 얻기를 바라오.\"\n\n\"저는 지금 당신을 막지 않겠어요,\" 그의 아내가, 눈도 목소리도 자세도 미동 없이 응수했다, \"설령 이 방이 불타고 있다 한들, 일어나 나가서 당신이 한마디라도 입에 올리는 수고를 덜어 드리지는 않겠어요.\"\n\n돔비 씨는, 그 경청에 대한 비꼬는 답례라도 하듯 고개를 까딱하고는, 말을 이어 갔다. 그러나 아까만큼 자제력을 갖추지는 못했으니, 플로렌스를 두고 보이는 이디스의 그 재빠른 불안과, 그와 그의 질책을 향한 이디스의 무관심이, 곪아 가는 상처처럼 그를 쓰라리게 하고 화나게 했기 때문이다.\n\n\"돔비 부인,\" 그가 말했다, \"완고한 기질이라는 것이 얼마나 개탄스러운 것이며 얼마나 마땅히 고쳐져야 하는 것인지를—특히 그것이, 야심과 잇속이 충족된 뒤에 부려질 때, 그것도 은혜를 모른 채 부려질 때 더더욱 그러하다는 것을—내 딸이 아는 것이 그 애의 향상에 어긋나지는 않을 것이오. 내 생각에는, 그 야심과 잇속 둘 다가, 당신이 지금 이 식탁에서 차지하고 있는 자리에 앉도록 당신을 부추기는 데 얼마간 한몫을 했을 거요.\"\n\n\"아니에요! 저는 일어나 나가서, 당신이 한마디라도 입에 올리는 수고를 덜어 드리지는 않겠어요,\" 그녀가, 앞서와 똑같이 되풀이했다, \"설령 이 방이 불타고 있다 한들요.\"\n\n\"당신이 이런 불쾌한 진실들을 듣는 사람들 앞에서 거북해하는 것도 어쩌면 충분히 자연스러운 일일 것이오, 돔비 부인,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 왜\"—그는 여기서 자기의 진짜 감정을 숨기지 못했고, 음울하게 플로렌스를 흘끔거리는 눈길을 어쩌지 못했다—\"왜 다른 누군가가, 이 일과 그토록 깊이 관련된 나 자신보다도 그것들에 더한 무게와 날카로움을 더할 수 있다는 건지, 나로서는 도무지 이해하는 척조차 못 하겠소. 당신 안에 당신이 한시바삐 억눌러야 할 반항적인 기질이 있다는 것을—당신이 반드시 억눌러야만 하는 거요, 돔비 부인—누군가의 면전에서 듣기 싫어하는 것도 어쩌면 충분히 자연스러운 일일 거요. 그리고 유감스럽게도, 나는 그 기질이—우리가 결혼하기 전에도 한 번 이상, 다소의 의구심과 불쾌감을 품고 본 적이 있는데—당신의 돌아가신 어머니를 향해 드러나는 것을 기억하고 있소. 하지만 그 해법은 당신 자신의 손안에 있소. 시작할 때, 나는 내 딸이 여기 있다는 것을 결코 잊지 않았소, 돔비 부인. 내일은, 부디 잊지 마시오, 여러 사람이 자리에 있다는 것을. 그리고 체면을 얼마쯤은 차려서, 당신의 손님들을 합당한 태도로 맞아 주기를 바라오.\"\n\n\"그러니까 당신은 그것으로도 모자란다는 거군요,\" 이디스가 말했다, \"당신과 나 사이에 있었던 일을 당신이 아는 것으로도 모자라고, 여기를 바라보며\"—여전히 눈을 내리깐 채 듣고 있는 카커를 가리키며—\"당신이 내게 가한 모욕들을 떠올릴 수 있는 것으로도 모자라고, 여기를 바라보며\"—처음이자 마지막으로 살짝 떨리는 손으로 플로렌스를 가리키며—\"당신이 한 짓을, 그리고 그 짓을 하면서 날마다, 시시각각, 끊임없이 내게 안겨 준 그 교묘한 고통을 떠올릴 수 있는 것으로도 모자라고, 한 해의 그 어느 날보다도 내게는, 차라리 그때 죽었더라면 싶은 어떤 몸부림(마땅히 치러야 할 것이었으나, 당신 같은 사람으로서는 짐작도 못 할 그런 몸부림) 때문에 잊지 못할 바로 이날이라는 것으로도 모자란다는 거군요! 이 모든 것에 더해서, 당신은 내가 얼마나 깊이 떨어졌는지를 저 아이에게 똑똑히 보게 하는 그 마지막 극치의 비열함까지 보태는 거예요. 당신은 알면서도—내가 저 아이의 평안을 위해, 내 생애의 단 하나뿐인 다정한 감정과 애착을 희생하게 만든 것을 알면서도—내가 저 아이를 위해서라면, 할 수만 있다면 지금이라도—하지만 나는 할 수 없어요, 내 영혼이 당신에게서 너무도 움츠러드니까—내 자신을 온전히 당신의 뜻에 맡기고, 당신이 거느린 가장 온순한 종이 되었으리라는 것을 알면서도요!\"\n\n이것은 돔비 씨의 위대함에 봉사하는 길이 아니었다. 그녀의 말에 그 해묵은 감정이, 그것이 일찍이 지녀 본 적 없는 더 강하고 더 사나운 존재로 일깨워졌다. 다시 한번, 그의 생애의 이 거친 길목에서, 방치된 자기 자식이, 이 반항적인 여자의 입을 통해서까지도, 그가 무력한 곳에서 강력한 존재로, 그가 아무것도 아닌 곳에서 전부인 존재로 내세워진 것이다!\n\n그는 마치 말을 한 것이 플로렌스이기라도 한 듯 그녀에게로 홱 돌아서서, 방에서 나가라고 명했다. 플로렌스는 얼굴을 가린 채, 나가면서 떨며 울며 그 말에 따랐다.\n\n\"이제 알겠소, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 성난 승리의 홍조를 띠며 말했다, \"당신의 애정을 그쪽으로 돌려놓은 그 반항의 정신을 말이오. 하지만 그것은 막혔소, 돔비 부인. 그것은 막혔고, 도로 돌려보내졌소!\"\n\n\"당신에게는 더 안된 일이죠!\" 그녀가, 여전히 변함없는 목소리와 태도로 대답했다. \"암요!\" 그녀가 그렇게 말하자 그가 홱 돌아섰으므로 그녀가 덧붙였다, \"내게 안된 일은, 당신에게는 그 이천만 배로 안된 일이에요. 다른 건 다 흘려듣더라도 그것만은 새겨들으세요.\"\n\n그녀의 검은 머리 위에 걸린 다이아몬드의 아치가 별들의 다리처럼 번쩍이며 반짝였다. 거기에는 아무런 경고도 없었으니, 그렇지 않았더라면 그것들은 빛바랜 명예처럼 칙칙하고 흐릿하게 변했으리라. 카커는 여전히 눈을 내리깐 채 앉아 듣고 있었다.\n\n\"돔비 부인,\" 돔비 씨가, 자기의 오만한 침착함을 되도록 추스르며 말했다, \"당신은 이런 식의 행동으로는 나를 회유하지도, 어떤 목적에서든 나를 돌려세우지도 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"이건 내 안에 있는 것에 비하면 희미한 표현일망정, 그래도 유일하게 참된 표현이에요,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 만일 이것이 당신을 회유하리라고 생각한다면, 인간의 어떤 노력으로든 억누를 수 있는 것이라면, 나는 이것을 억눌렀을 거예요. 나는 당신이 청하는 건 아무것도 하지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"나는 청하는 데는 익숙지 않소, 돔비 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 명하는 거요.\"\n\n\"나는 내일도, 또 앞으로 그 어떤 내일이 돌아와도, 당신의 집에서 아무 자리도 지키지 않겠어요. 나는 그 누구에게도, 당신이 어느 때에 사들인 말 안 듣는 노예라고 전시되지 않겠어요. 내가 내 결혼 기념일을 지킨다면, 그것을 치욕의 날로 지킬 거예요. 자기 자신에 대한 존중심이라니! 세상 앞에서의 체면이라니! 그런 것들이 내게 무슨 의미가 있나요? 당신은 그것들이 내게 아무 의미도 없게 만들려고 할 수 있는 짓을 다 했고, 그래서 그것들은 아무 의미도 없어요.\"\n\n\"카커,\" 돔비 씨가, 미간을 찌푸린 채, 한순간 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다, \"돔비 부인은 이 모든 일에서 자기 자신과 나를 어찌나 잊어버리고, 또 나를 내 성품에 도무지 어울리지 않는 처지에 놓아두는지, 나는 이 사태를 끝맺지 않을 수 없네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 나를 풀어 주세요,\" 이디스가, 줄곧 그래 왔듯이 목소리도 표정도 거동도 미동 없이 말했다, \"나를 묶고 있는 그 사슬에서. 나를 가게 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"부인?\" 돔비 씨가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"나를 풀어 줘요. 나를 자유롭게 해 줘요!\"\n\n\"부인?\" 그가 되풀이했다, \"돔비 부인?\"\n\n\"그에게 전하세요,\" 이디스가, 자기의 도도한 얼굴을 카커에게 향하며 말했다, \"내가 우리 사이의 별거를 바란다고요. 그러는 편이 낫겠다고요. 내가 그에게 그것을 권한다고요. 그것이 그가 내거는 조건대로 이루어져도 좋다고—그의 재산 따위 내게는 아무것도 아니니까—다만 빠를수록 좋다고 전하세요.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 돔비 부인!\" 그녀의 남편이 더없는 경악을 띠며 말했다, \"내가 그런 제안에 귀를 기울이는 일이 가능하리라고 생각하는 거요? 내가 누구인지 알기나 하오, 부인? 내가 무엇을 대표하는지 알기나 하오? 돔비와 아들이라는 이름을 들어 본 적이나 있소? 사람들이 돔비 씨가—돔비 씨가!—제 아내와 별거했다고 입에 올린다는 거요! 미천한 것들이 돔비 씨와 그의 집안일을 떠들어 댄다는 거요! 정말로 생각하는 거요, 돔비 부인, 내가 내 이름이 그런 일에 얽혀 떠돌아다니도록 내버려 두리라고? 흥, 흥, 부인! 부끄러운 줄 아시오! 당신은 어처구니없는 사람이오.\" 돔비 씨는 그야말로 소리 내어 웃었다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 웃은 식으로는 아니었다. 그를 똑바로 응시하며 그렇게 웃어 보일 바에는, 그녀는 차라리 죽어 버리는 편이 나았으리라. 그리고 그는, 자기의 영화 속에 거기 앉아 그 웃음을 듣고 있을 바에는, 차라리 죽어 버리는 편이 나았으리라.\n\n\"아니오, 돔비 부인,\" 그가 다시 말을 이었다. \"아니오, 부인. 당신과 나 사이에 별거란 있을 수 없는 일이오. 그러니 나는 당신이 더더욱 의무감을 깨닫게 되기를 권하는 바요. 그리고, 카커, 자네에게 막 말하려던 것인데—\"\n\n이제껏 줄곧 앉아 듣고 있던 카커 씨가, 이제 눈을 들었는데, 그 안에는 어떤 밝고 예사롭지 않은 빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"—자네에게 막 말하려던 것인데,\" 돔비 씨가 다시 이었다, \"이제 사태가 이 지경에 이르렀으니, 부탁하건대 돔비 부인에게 알려 주게. 그 누구에게도—그 누구에게도 말일세, 카커—내가 가로막히도록 내버려 두는 것은, 또 나에게 복종할 의무가 있는 자들에게, 나 자신보다 더 강력한 복종의 동기로 그 누군가가 떠받들어지도록 내버려 두는 것은, 내 삶의 방식이 아니라고. 내 딸이 입에 오르내린 것, 그리고 내 딸이 나에게 맞서는 데 이용되고 있는 것은, 부자연스러운 일이오. 내 딸이 실제로 돔비 부인과 한패인지 아닌지, 나는 알지 못하고, 또 알 바도 아니오. 하지만 오늘 돔비 부인이 한 말과, 오늘 내 딸이 들은 것으로 미루어, 부탁하건대 돔비 부인에게 알려 주게. 만일 그녀가 이 집을, 지금처럼 다툼의 무대로 계속 만든다면, 나는 저 부인 자신의 자백으로 보아 내 딸에게도 어느 정도 책임이 있다고 여길 것이며, 내 딸에게 나의 준엄한 노여움을 내릴 것이라고. 돔비 부인은 자기가 이러저러한 일을 한 것으로 '모자라느냐'고 물었소. 부디 그녀에게 답해 주게, 아니다, 모자란다고.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요!\" 카커가 끼어들며 외쳤다, \"허락해 주십시오! 제 처지가 아무리 좋게 봐도 괴로운 것이고, 더욱이 어른과 다른 의견을 품은 것처럼 보이는 일이 유난히 괴로운 것입니다만,\" 돔비 씨에게 말하면서, \"여쭙지 않을 수 없습니다. 별거 문제를 다시 한번 생각해 보시는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까. 그것이 어른의 드높은 공적 지위와 얼마나 양립하기 어려운지를 저도 압니다. 또 어른이 돔비 부인에게 이렇게 일러 주실 때—\"그의 눈 속의 그 빛이, 그가 한마디 한마디를 마치 여러 개의 종소리처럼 또렷이 떼어 가며 말할 때, 그녀에게 떨어졌다—\"오직 죽음만이 두 분을 갈라놓을 수 있다고, 그 외에는 아무것도 그럴 수 없다고 일러 주실 때, 그 결심이 얼마나 단호한지도 압니다. 하지만 돔비 부인이 이 집에 살면서, 어른의 말씀대로 이 집을 다툼의 무대로 만드는 한, 그녀가 그 다툼에 자기 몫을 담당할 뿐 아니라, 날마다 돔비 양을 곤란하게 한다는 것을(어른의 결심이 얼마나 단호한지를 제가 아니까요) 헤아려 보시면, 그녀를 그 끊임없는 심령의 자극에서, 그리고 또 다른 사람에게 부당하게 굴고 있다는, 거의 견딜 수 없는 그 끊임없는 자각에서 풀어 주지 않으시렵니까? 이것은—그렇다고 말하는 건 아닙니다만—어른의 탁월하고 범할 수 없는 지위의 보존을 위해 돔비 부인을 희생시키는 것처럼 보이지 않습니까?\"\n\n다시 그의 눈 속의 빛이, 이제 자기 얼굴에 어떤 기이하고 무시무시한 미소를 띤 채 남편을 바라보며 서 있는 그녀에게 떨어졌다.\n\n\"카커,\" 돔비 씨가, 오만하게 미간을 찌푸리며, 단호함을 의도한 어조로 응수했다, \"자네는 그런 문제에 대해 내게 조언을 함으로써 자네의 처지를 잘못 알고 있고, 또 (놀랍게도 알게 된 바이지만) 자네 조언의 성격에서 나를 잘못 알고 있네. 나는 더 할 말이 없네.\"\n\n\"어쩌면,\" 카커가, 어딘가 예사롭지 않고 형언키 어려운 조롱을 분위기에 담아 말했다, \"어른께서 제게 이곳에서 종사해 온 그 교섭을 맡기는 영예를 베푸셨을 때, 제 처지를 잘못 아셨는지도 모르지요\"—돔비 부인 쪽으로 손짓하며.\n\n\"전혀, 그렇지 않네, 전혀,\" 상대가 오만하게 응수했다. \"자네가 부려진 것은—\"\n\n\"미천한 사람으로서, 돔비 부인을 욕보이기 위해서였지요. 제가 잊었군요. 오, 그렇지요, 그것은 분명히 합의된 것이었습니다!\" 카커가 말했다. \"용서하십시오!\"\n\n그는, 비록 겸손하게 말하기는 했어도 그 말과는 어울리지 않는 공손한 태도로 돔비 씨에게 고개를 숙이면서, 그것을 그녀 쪽으로 돌리고, 살피는 눈길을 그쪽으로 둔 채로 있었다.\n\n그런 미소를 얼굴에 띤 채, 추락한 영혼의 그런 경멸과 아름다움의 위엄으로 일어서 있을 바에는, 그녀는 차라리 흉측해져 쓰러져 죽는 편이 나았으리라. 그녀는 머리 위에서 찬란히 빛나는 그 보석들의 보관(寶冠)으로 손을 들어 올려, 그것을 한 번에 잡아채는 그 힘에 풍성한 검은 머리가 인정사정없이 끌려 당겨져 어깨 위로 마구 흘러내리도록 하면서, 그 보석들을 바닥에 내던졌다. 양팔에서 그녀는 다이아몬드 팔찌를 풀어 내던지고, 반짝이는 그 무더기를 짓밟았다. 한마디 말도 없이, 그 빛나는 눈의 불길에 한 점 그늘도 없이, 그 무시무시한 미소를 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않은 채, 그녀는 문 쪽으로 움직여 가면서 마지막 순간까지 돔비 씨를 바라보았다. 그리고 그를 남겨 둔 채 떠났다.\n\n플로렌스는 방을 나가기 전에, 이디스가 자기를 여전히 사랑한다는 것, 자기를 위해 괴로워해 왔다는 것, 그리고 자기의 평안을 어지럽힐까 두려워 그 희생들을 조용히 감춰 왔다는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 들었다. 그녀는 그것에 대해 이디스에게 말하고 싶지는 않았다—그녀가 누구와 맞서고 있는지를 떠올리면 그럴 수도 없었다—그러나, 말없는 다정한 포옹 한 번으로, 자기가 그 모든 것을 다 느끼고 있으며 고마워한다는 것을 그녀에게 전하고 싶었다.\n\n그날 저녁 그녀의 아버지는 혼자 외출했고, 곧이어 자기 방에서 나온 플로렌스는 이디스를 찾아 온 집안을 돌아다녔으나 헛수고였다. 이디스는 자기 방에 있었는데, 플로렌스는 오래전부터 그곳에 발을 끊은 터였고, 자기도 모르게 새로운 분란을 일으킬까 두려워 이제는 감히 들어가 볼 엄두를 내지 못했다. 그래도 플로렌스는 잠자리에 들기 전에 그녀를 만나기를 바라며, 이 방 저 방을 옮겨 다니고, 그토록 으리으리하면서도 그토록 음산한 그 집을 헤매었으나, 어디에도 머무르지 못했다.\n\n그녀가, 계단에서 조금 떨어진 곳으로 열려 있고 큰 행사 때만 불을 밝히는 어느 연결 회랑을 가로질러 가고 있을 때, 아치로 된 그 트인 곳 너머로, 맞은편 몇 계단을 내려오는 한 남자의 모습이 보였다. 그것이 자기 아버지라고 짐작하여 본능적으로 겁이 난 그녀는, 어둠 속에 멈춰 서서, 아치 너머의 불빛 속을 응시했다. 그러나 그것은 혼자 내려오며 난간 너머로 현관 홀을 내려다보는 카커 씨였다. 그의 떠남을 알리는 종도 울리지 않았고, 시중드는 하인도 없었다. 그는 조용히 내려가, 손수 문을 열고, 미끄러지듯 빠져나가, 뒤로 살며시 문을 닫았다.\n\n이 남자에 대한 떨칠 수 없는 혐오감과, 또 어쩌면 그런 무고한 상황에서조차 어딘가 죄스럽고 짓누르는 듯한, 누군가를 몰래 엿보는 그 행위 자체가, 플로렌스를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨게 만들었다. 그녀의 피가 차갑게 식는 것만 같았다. 그녀는 될 수 있는 한 빨리—처음에는 움직인다는 것에 넘어설 수 없는 공포를 느꼈으므로—얼른 자기 방으로 가서 문을 잠갔다. 그러나 그러고 나서도, 개를 곁에 두고 방 안에 틀어박혀서도, 어딘가 가까이에서 위험이 도사리고 있기라도 한 듯, 오싹한 공포의 느낌을 떨치지 못했다.\n\n그것은 그녀의 꿈속까지 파고들어 밤새 그녀를 어지럽혔다. 아침에 일어났을 때, 그녀는 개운하지 못했고, 전날의 가정의 불행에 대한 무거운 기억을 안고 있었으며, 다시 모든 방에서 이디스를 찾았고, 아침 내내 이따금 그렇게 했다. 그러나 이디스는 자기 방에 머물러 있었고, 플로렌스는 그녀를 전혀 보지 못했다. 하지만 집에서 하려던 만찬이 미뤄졌다는 것을 알게 된 플로렌스는, 그녀가 이야기했던 약속을 지키러 저녁에 외출할 것 같다고 생각했고, 그때 계단에서 그녀를 만나 보려고 마음먹었다.\n\n저녁이 깃들었을 때, 그녀는 일부러 와 앉아 있던 방에서, 계단을 밟는 발소리를 들었는데, 그것이 이디스의 발소리라고 생각했다. 황급히 나와 그녀의 방 쪽으로 올라가던 플로렌스는, 혼자 내려오는 그녀와 곧바로 마주쳤다.\n\n눈물 어린 얼굴에 두 팔을 내민 자기를 보자, 이디스가 흠칫 물러서며 비명을 지르는 것을 보았을 때, 플로렌스의 그 놀라움과 의아함이란 어떠했겠는가!\n\n\"내게 다가오지 마!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"저리 비켜! 나를 지나가게 해 줘!\"\n\n\"엄마!\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"그 이름으로 나를 부르지 마! 내게 말 걸지 마! 나를 보지 마!—플로렌스!\" 플로렌스가 그녀 쪽으로 한 걸음 다가서자 뒷걸음치며, \"나를 만지지 마!\"\n\n플로렌스가 그 초췌한 얼굴과 빤히 응시하는 눈 앞에 못 박힌 듯 서 있는 동안, 그녀는, 꿈결에서처럼, 이디스가 두 손을 자기 눈 위로 펼치고는, 온몸을 부르르 떨며, 벽에 바짝 붙어 웅크린 채, 무슨 하등한 짐승처럼 자기 곁을 기어 지나가, 벌떡 일어나, 달아나 버리는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n플로렌스는 정신을 잃고 계단 위에 쓰러졌다. 그리고 거기서, 핍친 부인에게 발견된 것이라고 그녀는 짐작했다. 그녀는 그 이상 아무것도 알지 못했고, 정신을 차려 보니 자기 침대에 누워 있었으며, 핍친 부인과 하인 몇이 그녀를 둘러싸고 서 있었다.\n\n\"엄마는 어디 계세요?\"가 그녀의 첫 질문이었다.\n\n\"만찬에 나가셨소,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 아빠는요?\"\n\n\"돔비 씨는 자기 방에 계시오, 돔비 양,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다, \"그리고 아가씨가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일은, 옷을 벗고 지금 당장 잠자리에 드는 거요.\" 이것이 모든 병에 대한 그 약삭빠른 여인의 처방이었으니, 특히 기분의 침울함과 잠 못 이룸에 대해서 그러했다. 브라이턴 캐슬 시절에는 이런 죄목으로 수많은 어린 희생자들이 아침 열 시에 잠자리로 보내지곤 했다.\n\n복종을 약속하지는 않았으나, 아주 조용히 있고 싶다는 핑계를 대고, 플로렌스는 될 수 있는 한 빨리 핍친 부인과 그 시중꾼들의 보살핌에서 벗어났다. 혼자 남게 되자, 그녀는 계단에서 일어난 일을 떠올렸다. 처음에는 그것이 정말이었는지를 의심하면서, 그다음에는 눈물을 흘리면서, 그다음에는 전날 밤 느꼈던 것 같은, 형언할 수 없고 끔찍한 경악을 느끼면서.\n\n그녀는 이디스가 돌아올 때까지는 잠자리에 들지 않기로, 그리고 설령 그녀와 말을 나눌 수 없더라도, 적어도 그녀가 무사히 집에 돌아왔음을 확인하기로 결심했다. 어떤 막연하고 어렴풋한 두려움이 플로렌스를 이런 결심으로 이끌었는지, 그녀는 알지 못했고, 또 감히 생각해 보려 하지도 않았다. 그녀는 다만, 이디스가 돌아올 때까지는 자기의 욱신거리는 머리에도 두근거리는 가슴에도 안식이 없으리라는 것만 알 뿐이었다.\n\n저녁은 깊어 밤이 되었고, 자정이 왔으나, 이디스는 오지 않았다.\n\n플로렌스는 책을 읽을 수도, 한순간도 쉴 수도 없었다. 그녀는 자기 방 안을 거닐다가, 문을 열고 바깥 계단 회랑을 거닐다가, 창밖으로 밤을 내다보다가, 바람이 부는 소리와 비가 내리는 소리에 귀 기울이다가, 앉아서 난롯불 속의 형상들을 바라보다가, 일어나서 구름의 바다를 가로질러 폭풍에 떠밀린 배처럼 내달리는 달을 바라보았다.\n\n온 집안이 잠자리에 들었고, 다만 아래층에서 안주인의 귀가를 기다리는 하인 둘만 깨어 있었다.\n\n한 시. 멀리서 덜컹거리던 마차들이 방향을 돌리거나, 갑자기 멈추거나, 지나쳐 갔다. 정적은 차츰 깊어졌고, 한바탕 휘몰아치는 바람이나 한차례 쏟아지는 비를 빼고는 점점 더 드물게만 깨어졌다. 두 시. 이디스는 오지 않았다!\n\n한층 더 마음을 졸이며, 플로렌스는 자기 방을 거닐었고, 바깥 회랑을 거닐었으며, 유리에 맺힌 빗방울과 자기 눈에 어린 눈물로 흐릿하고 일렁이는 밤을 내다보았고, 아래의 그 안식과는 그토록 다르면서도 또 그토록 고요하고 외로운, 하늘의 그 분주함을 올려다보았다. 세 시! 난롯불에서 떨어지는 재 한 톨 한 톨에마다 어떤 공포가 서려 있었다. 이디스는 아직도 오지 않았다.\n\n점점 더 마음을 졸이며, 플로렌스는 자기 방을 거닐고, 회랑을 거닐었으며, 죄지은 얼굴을 감춘 채 황급히 달아나는 어떤 창백한 도망자를 닮았다는 새로운 공상을 품고서 달을 내다보았다. 네 시가 쳤다! 다섯 시! 이디스는 아직도 오지 않았다.\n\n그러나 이제 집안에 어떤 조심스러운 움직임이 일었다. 그리고 플로렌스는, 깨어 지키고 있던 사람 가운데 하나에게 잠을 깬 핍친 부인이, 일어나 아래층 자기 아버지의 방문으로 내려갔다는 것을 알게 되었다. 계단을 더 아래로 살금살금 내려가, 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 살피던 그녀는, 아버지가 잠옷 차림으로 나오는 것을, 그리고 아내가 집에 돌아오지 않았다는 말을 듣고 흠칫 놀라는 것을 보았다. 그는 마부가 거기 있는지 물어보라고 마구간으로 사람을 보냈고, 그 사람이 간 사이에 몹시 황급히 옷을 차려입었다.\n\n그 사람이, 마부를 데리고, 무척 서둘러 돌아왔다. 마부는 자기가 열 시 이후로 집에서 잠자리에 들어 있었다고 말했다. 그는 안주인을 그녀의 옛 집이 있는 브룩 가로 모셔다 드렸는데, 거기서 그녀를 카커 씨가 맞이했다고—\n\n플로렌스는 그가 내려오는 것을 보았던 바로 그 자리에 서 있었다. 다시 그녀는 그 광경의 이름 없는 공포로 부르르 떨었고, 그다음에 이어지는 말을 듣고 알아들을 만한 침착함도 거의 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n—그 카커 씨가, 마부가 말을 이어 가기를, 자기에게 안주인은 집으로 돌아가는 데 마차가 필요 없을 거라고 일러 주고, 자기를 돌려보냈다고 했다.\n\n그녀는 아버지의 얼굴이 새하얗게 질리는 것을 보았고, 그가 빠르고 떨리는 목소리로 돔비 부인의 하녀를 찾는 것을 들었다. 온 집안이 깨어났다. 하녀가 곧 거기 와 있었으니, 그녀 역시 무척 창백했고, 횡설수설 말하고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 자기가 안주인의 옷을 일찍—안주인이 외출하기 꼬박 두 시간 전에—입혀 드렸으며, 늘 그랬듯이, 밤에는 부르지 않을 거라는 말을 들었다고 했다. 그녀는 방금 안주인의 방에서 나오는 길인데, 다만—\n\n\"다만 뭔가! 그게 뭐였느냐?\" 플로렌스는 아버지가 미친 사람처럼 다그치는 것을 들었다.\n\n\"다만 안쪽 화장실이 잠겨 있고 열쇠가 없어졌어요.\"\n\n그녀의 아버지는 바닥에서 타고 있던 촛불 하나를—누군가가 거기 내려놓고 잊어버린 것이었다—움켜쥐고, 어찌나 사나운 기세로 계단을 뛰어 올라왔던지, 플로렌스는 겁에 질려 그의 앞에서 달아날 겨를도 거의 없었다. 그녀가, 두 손을 활짝 벌리고, 머리를 휘날리며, 얼굴은 넋 나간 사람 같은 모습으로 자기 방으로 도로 달려가는 동안, 그가 그 문을 두들겨 부수는 소리를 들었다.\n\n문이 부서지고, 그가 안으로 뛰어들었을 때, 그는 거기서 무엇을 보았던가? 아무도 알지 못했다. 그러나 값비싼 무더기를 이룬 채 바닥에 내던져져 있는 것은, 그녀가 그의 아내가 된 이후로 가졌던 온갖 장신구였고, 그녀가 입었던 온갖 드레스였으며, 그녀가 소유했던 온갖 것이었다. 이 방은 그가, 저 거울 속에서, 그 도도한 얼굴이 자기를 내치는 것을 보았던 그 방이었다. 이 방은 그가, 다음에 자기가 이것들을 보게 될 때면 어떻게 보일까를 하릴없이 궁금해했던 그 방이었다!\n\n그것들을 서랍 속으로 도로 쓸어 담아, 황급한 분노로 잠가 버리던 그는, 탁자 위에서 서류 몇 장을 보았다. 결혼할 때 그가 작성했던 재산 양도 증서와, 편지 한 통이었다. 그는 그녀가 떠났다는 것을 읽었다. 그는 자기가 명예를 더럽혔다는 것을 읽었다. 그는 그녀가, 자기의 그 치욕스러운 결혼기념일에, 자기가 그녀를 욕보이려고 골라 둔 바로 그 남자와 함께 달아났다는 것을 읽었다. 그리고 그는, 아직도 그녀가 끌려간 그곳에서 그녀를 찾아내, 그 의기양양한 얼굴에서 아름다움의 흔적을 죄다 맨손으로 두들겨 없애 버리겠다는 광기 어린 생각에 사로잡혀, 방에서, 또 집에서 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n플로렌스는, 자기가 무엇을 하는지도 모른 채, 거리를 헤매며 이디스를 찾아내, 그러고는 그녀를 품에 끌어안아, 구해 내어 도로 데려오겠다는 꿈결 같은 생각으로, 숄을 두르고 보닛을 썼다. 그러나 그녀가 황급히 계단으로 나와, 겁에 질린 하인들이 불을 들고 오르내리며 자기들끼리 속삭이고, 자기 아버지가 내려갈 때 슬금슬금 물러서는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀는 자기 자신의 무력함을 깨달았다. 그리고 이 일을 위해 화려하게 꾸며진 그 큰 방들 가운데 하나에 숨어, 슬픔으로 가슴이 터질 것만 같았다.\n\n아버지에 대한 연민이, 그녀를 휩쓴 슬픔의 홍수에 맞서 처음으로 또렷하게 떠오른 감정이었다. 그녀의 한결같은 천성은, 그가 번창하던 시절에, 점점 그토록 희미하고 어렴풋해진 그 관념의 화신이기라도 했던 것처럼, 곤경에 처한 그를 향해 그토록 뜨겁고 그토록 변함없이 돌아섰다. 비록 그녀는, 어떤 형체 없는 두려움의 암시를 통하지 않고서는, 그가 겪은 재난의 전모를 알지 못했으나, 그는 부당한 일을 당하고 버림받은 채 그녀 앞에 서 있었다. 그리고 다시 한번 그녀의 애타는 사랑이 그녀를 그의 곁으로 떠밀었다.\n\n그는 오래 나가 있지 않았다. 플로렌스가 아직 그 큰 방에서 울며 이런 생각들을 곱씹고 있을 때, 그녀는 그가 돌아오는 소리를 들었다. 그는 하인들에게 평소의 일을 보라고 명하고는, 자기 방으로 들어갔는데, 어찌나 무겁게 발을 디뎠던지 그가 이 끝에서 저 끝까지 오락가락 걷는 소리가 그녀에게 들렸다.\n\n다른 모든 때에는 겁이 많았으나, 역경에 처한 그를 향한 진심에 있어서는 대담했고, 지난날의 거절에도 굴하지 않은 채, 자기 애정의 충동에 곧바로 굴복하여, 플로렌스는 입은 차림 그대로 황급히 아래층으로 내려갔다. 그녀가 가벼운 발을 현관 홀에 들여놓았을 때, 그가 자기 방에서 나왔다. 그녀는, 마치 그의 목을 끌어안기라도 할 것처럼, 두 팔을 뻗고 \"오, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아빠!\" 하고 외치며, 거침없이 그에게로 서둘러 다가갔다.\n\n그리고 그녀는 정말 그렇게 했으리라. 그러나 광기에 휩싸인 그는 자기의 그 잔인한 팔을 쳐들어, 어찌나 모질게 그녀를 가로질러 후려쳤던지, 그녀가 대리석 바닥 위에서 비틀거렸다. 그리고 그 일격을 가하면서, 그는 그녀에게 이디스가 어떤 여자인지를 말하고, 너희가 늘 한패였으니 그녀를 따라가라고 명했다.\n\n그녀는 그의 발치에 주저앉지 않았다. 그녀는 떨리는 두 손으로 그를 보는 시선을 가리지 않았다. 그녀는 울지 않았다. 그녀는 단 한마디 책망의 말도 입에 올리지 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 바라보았고, 그녀의 가슴에서 황량한 절규가 터져 나왔다. 바라보는 동안, 그녀는 그가, 자기가 그토록 그에 맞서 지켜 온 그 애틋한 관념을 죽이는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 그녀는 그의 잔인함과 무관심과 증오가 그것 위에 군림하며 그것을 짓밟아 버리는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 자기에게 이 땅 위에 아버지가 없다는 것을 보았고, 고아가 되어, 그의 집에서 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n그의 집에서 뛰쳐나갔다. 한순간, 그녀의 손이 자물쇠에 닿았고, 절규가 그녀의 입술에 맺혔으며, 황급히 내려놓아져 녹아 사라져 가는 누런 촛불들과, 문 위로 들어오는 햇빛에 한층 더 창백해진 그의 얼굴이 거기 있었다. 또 한순간, 닫혀 있던 그 집의 짙은 어둠이(날이 밝은 지 오래였건만 누구도 열어젖힐 생각을 하지 못한 그 어둠이) 뜻밖의 환한 빛과 아침의 트인 자유에 자리를 내주었다. 그리고 플로렌스는, 눈물의 고통을 감추려 고개를 푹 숙인 채, 거리에 나와 있었다.\n\n# 제43장\n\n비탄과 수치와 공포의 광란 속에서, 의지할 데 없는 그 소녀는 밝은 아침의 햇살 속을 마치 그것이 겨울밤의 어둠이기라도 한 듯 허둥지둥 헤쳐 나갔다. 두 손을 쥐어짜고 비통하게 울면서, 가슴속 깊은 상처 말고는 아무것에도 감각이 없이, 사랑하던 모든 것을 잃은 충격으로 멍해진 채, 거대한 배가 난파한 외딴 해안에 홀로 살아남은 단 한 사람처럼, 그녀는 아무 생각도, 아무 희망도, 아무 목적도 없이 그저 어디든 도망쳐야 한다는 일념으로 달아났다.\n\n아침 빛에 반짝이는 긴 거리의 명랑한 풍경도, 푸른 하늘과 가벼운 구름의 모습도, 밤을 정복하여 그토록 발그레하고 장밋빛으로 물든 하루의 힘찬 신선함도, 그토록 상처 입은 그녀의 가슴에는 아무런 호응의 감정도 일깨우지 못했다. 어디든, 어디라도, 머리를 숨길 곳! 어디든, 어디라도, 피신할 곳, 다시는 자기가 도망쳐 나온 그곳을 바라보지 않을 곳!\n\n그러나 사람들은 이리저리 오갔고, 가게들은 문을 열고 있었으며, 하인들은 집집의 문간에 나와 있었고, 하루의 분투가 점점 부딪치고 으르렁대며 일어나고 있었다. 플로렌스는 자기 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 얼굴들에서 놀라움과 호기심을 보았고, 포장도로 위로 되돌아오는 긴 그림자들을 보았으며, 낯선 목소리들이 어디로 가느냐고, 무슨 일이냐고 묻는 것을 들었다. 처음에는 그 일들이 그녀를 더욱 겁먹게 하여 발걸음을 더 빨리하게 만들었지만, 그것들은 그녀를 어느 정도 제정신으로 돌아오게 하고 좀 더 침착해야 할 필요를 일깨워 주는 좋은 역할을 해 주었다.\n\n어디로 갈 것인가? 여전히 어디든, 어디라도! 여전히 앞으로 나아가면서, 그러나 어디로! 그녀는 자기가 런던이라는 거친 황야 속에서 길을 잃었던 단 한 번의 다른 때를—지금처럼 길을 잃은 것은 아니었지만—떠올렸고, 그 길로 갔다. 월터의 외삼촌 댁으로.\n\n흐느낌을 억누르고, 퉁퉁 부은 눈을 닦으며, 남의 눈에 띄지 않으려고 들뜬 거동을 가라앉히려 애쓰면서, 플로렌스는 할 수 있는 한 오래 좀 더 조용한 거리로만 다니기로 마음먹고 자기도 좀 더 차분하게 걷고 있었는데, 그때 햇살 비치는 포장도로 위로 낯익은 작은 그림자가 휙 지나가더니, 우뚝 멈춰 서고, 빙 돌아서서, 그녀에게 바짝 다가왔다가, 다시 달아나고, 그녀의 둘레를 빙글빙글 돌았다. 그러더니 디오게네스가, 숨을 헐떡이면서도 기쁜 짖음으로 거리를 쩌렁쩌렁 울리며, 그녀의 발치에 와 있었다.\n\n\"오, 디! 오, 사랑스럽고, 참되고, 충직한 디, 어떻게 여기까지 왔니? 내가 어떻게 너를 두고 떠날 수 있었을까, 디, 너는 결코 나를 떠나지 않을 텐데?\"\n\n플로렌스는 포장도로 위로 몸을 굽혀, 그 거칠고 늙고 다정하고 어수룩한 머리를 자기 가슴에 끌어안았고, 둘은 함께 일어나 함께 걸어갔다. 디는 땅에 붙어 있다기보다 땅에서 떠 있는 시간이 더 많았는데, 달려가는 주인에게 입을 맞추려 애쓰다가, 데굴데굴 굴러 넘어졌다가도 조금도 개의치 않고 다시 일어났고, 자기 종족에 대한 짓궂은 도전으로 큰 개들에게 달려들었으며, 문간 계단을 닦고 있는 어린 하녀들을 코끝으로 슬쩍 건드려 질겁하게 했고, 수천 가지 야단법석을 떠는 와중에도 끊임없이 멈춰 서서 플로렌스를 돌아보며, 들리는 거리 안의 모든 개들이 응답할 때까지, 그리고 밖으로 나올 수 있는 모든 개들이 그를 빤히 쳐다보러 나올 때까지 짖어 댔다.\n\n이 마지막 추종자와 함께, 플로렌스는 밝아 오는 아침과 점점 강해지는 햇살 속을 서둘러 도심을 향해 갔다. 으르렁대는 소리는 곧 더 커졌고, 행인들은 더 많아졌으며, 가게들은 더 분주해졌고, 마침내 그녀는 그쪽으로 흘러가는 생명의 물결에 떠밀려 가게 되었다. 그 물결은 시장과 저택, 감옥과 교회, 장터, 부와 빈곤, 선과 악을 무심히 흘러 지나갔으니, 마치 그 옆에 나란히 흐르는 넓은 강이 갈대와 버드나무와 푸른 이끼의 꿈에서 깨어나, 흐리고 어지러운 모습으로, 사람들의 일과 근심 사이를 굽이쳐 깊은 바다로 흘러가는 것과도 같았다.\n\n마침내 작은 항해사가 있는 곳이 시야에 들어왔다. 좀 더 가까이 가자, 작은 항해사 자신이 자기 자리에 서서, 여느 때처럼 관측에 골몰해 있는 것이 보였다. 좀 더 가까이 가자, 문이 열려 있어 그녀에게 들어오라고 손짓하고 있었다. 여정의 끝에 다가가면서 다시 걸음을 빨리한 플로렌스는, 길을 가로질러 달려가(법석에 다소 어리둥절해진 디오게네스가 바짝 뒤따랐다), 안으로 뛰어들어, 잘 기억하고 있는 그 작은 응접실의 문지방에 쓰러져 버렸다.\n\n니스 칠한 모자를 쓴 선장은 난롯불 위에 몸을 굽혀 아침 코코아를 끓이고 있었는데, 요리가 진행되는 동안 손쉽게 참고하려고, 그 우아한 장신구인 시계를 벽난로 선반 위에 올려놓은 참이었다. 발소리와 옷자락 스치는 소리를 듣자, 선장은 그 무시무시한 맥스틴저 부인을 떠올리며 가슴이 두근거려 몸을 돌렸는데, 바로 그 순간 플로렌스가 그를 향해 손짓을 하다가, 비틀거리며 바닥에 쓰러졌다.\n\n플로렌스만큼이나 창백한, 얼굴의 혹마디까지 새하얗게 질린 선장은, 그녀를 갓난아기처럼 들어 올려, 오래전 그녀가 잠들었던 바로 그 낡은 소파에 눕혔다.\n\n\"우리 '마음의 기쁨'이로구나!\" 선장이 그녀의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 말했다. \"그 귀여운 아가씨가 어엿한 여인이 다 되었구먼!\"\n\n커틀 선장은 이 새로운 모습의 그녀를 어찌나 깍듯이 존중하고, 어찌나 우러러보았던지, 그녀가 의식을 잃은 동안 천 파운드를 준대도 그녀를 자기 팔에 안고 있으려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"우리 '마음의 기쁨'!\" 선장이 얼굴에 더없는 놀라움과 동정을 가득 담은 채, 조금 뒤로 물러서며 말했다. \"네드 커틀을 손가락 하나로라도 부를 수 있거든, 그렇게 해 주오!\"\n\n그러나 플로렌스는 꼼짝도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"우리 '마음의 기쁨'!\" 선장이 떨면서 말했다. \"짠 바다 깊은 곳에 빠져 죽은 월터를 봐서라도, 정신을 차리고, 할 수 있거든 뭐든 좀 끌어올려 주오!\"\n\n이 감동적인 호소에도 그녀가 아무 반응이 없는 것을 보고, 커틀 선장은 아침 식탁에서 찬물이 든 대야를 집어 들어, 그녀의 얼굴에 조금 뿌렸다. 사태의 급박함에 못 이겨, 선장은 그 거대한 손을 보기 드물게 부드럽게 놀려, 그녀의 보닛을 벗겨 주고, 입술과 이마를 적셔 주고, 머리카락을 뒤로 넘겨 주고, 그러려고 벗어 든 자기 외투로 그녀의 발을 덮어 주고, 그녀의 손을—자기 손안에 들어가니 어찌나 작은지 만져 보고 깜짝 놀랄 정도였다—토닥여 주었으며, 그녀의 눈꺼풀이 떨리고 입술이 움직이기 시작하는 것을 보고, 한결 마음을 놓고 이 회복 조치들을 계속했다.\n\n\"기운 차려요,\" 선장이 말했다. \"기운을 차려! 버텨요, 우리 예쁜이, 버텨! 자! 이제 좀 나아졌소. 침착, 그게 핵심이고, 잘 침착하고 있소. 그대로 유지해요! 이거 한 모금만 마셔 봐요,\" 선장이 말했다. \"옳지! 이제 좀 어떻소, 우리 예쁜이, 이제 좀 어떻소?\"\n\n그녀의 회복이 이 단계에 이르자, 커틀 선장은 시계를 의사가 환자를 진료하는 일과 어렴풋이 연결지어 떠올리며, 자기 시계를 벽난로 선반에서 내려, 갈고리에 매달아 들고, 플로렌스의 손을 자기 손에 잡은 채, 마치 그 시계판이 무언가 해 주기를 기대하듯 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"좀 어떻소, 우리 예쁜이?\" 선장이 말했다. \"이제 좀 어떻소? 자네가 이 아가씨한테 효험을 좀 보인 것 같군그래,\" 선장이 숨죽인 소리로, 자기 시계에 흐뭇한 눈길을 던지며 말했다. \"아침마다 자넬 반 시간씩 뒤로 돌려놓고, 오후 무렵에 또 한 십오 분쯤 돌려놓으면, 자네는 견줄 자 거의 없고 능가할 자 하나도 없는 시계가 되지. 좀 어떻소, 우리 아가씨!\"\n\n\"커틀 선장님! 선장님이세요?\" 플로렌스가 몸을 조금 일으키며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그렇소, 그렇소, 우리 아가씨,\" 선장이 말했다. 자기가 생각해 낼 수 있는 가장 정중한 호칭으로서 그 표현이 한결 우아하다고 속으로 얼른 단정 지으며.\n\n\"월터의 외삼촌이 여기 계세요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"여기, 예쁜이?\" 선장이 응수했다. \"그분은 여기 안 계신 지 한참 됐소. 가엾은 월터를 찾아 떠난 뒤로는 소식도 끊겼지. 하지만,\" 선장은 인용구를 읊듯 말했다, \"비록 눈앞에서는 사라졌어도, 기억 속에 소중하니, 잉글랜드여, 고향이여, 그리고 아름다움이여!\"\n\n\"여기서 사세요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 우리 아가씨,\" 선장이 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 커틀 선장님!\" 플로렌스가 두 손을 맞잡고, 정신없이 외쳤다. \"저를 구해 주세요! 저를 여기 있게 해 주세요! 아무도 제가 어디 있는지 모르게 해 주세요! 무슨 일이 있었는지는 차차, 제가 할 수 있게 되면 말씀드릴게요. 저는 이 세상에 찾아갈 사람이 아무도 없어요. 저를 내쫓지 마세요!\"\n\n\"널 내쫓다니, 우리 아가씨!\" 선장이 소리쳤다. \"너를, 우리 '마음의 기쁨'을! 잠깐만 기다려요! 이 덧문을 닫고, 열쇠를 두 번 돌려 잠가 버립시다!\"\n\n이 말과 함께, 선장은 한 손과 갈고리를 더없이 능숙하게 놀려, 문의 덧문을 꺼내 와 끼우고, 단단히 고정하고, 문 자체도 잠갔다.\n\n그가 플로렌스 곁으로 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 그의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추었다. 그 행동의 가련함, 그것이 그에게 보내는 호소, 그것이 드러내는 신뢰, 그녀 얼굴에 어린 말할 수 없는 슬픔, 그녀가 너무도 분명히 겪어 왔고 그때도 겪고 있는 마음의 고통, 그녀의 지난 내력에 대한 그의 앎, 지금의 외롭고 지치고 보호받지 못하는 그녀의 모습, 이 모든 것이 한꺼번에 마음씨 고운 선장에게 밀려들어, 그는 그야말로 연민과 다정함으로 넘쳐흘렀다.\n\n\"우리 아가씨,\" 선장이 콧날을 팔로 문질러 닦아 광낸 구리처럼 반짝이게 하며 말했다, \"에드워드 커틀한테는 한마디도 하지 말아요, 네 처지가 잔잔하고 편안하게 흘러가게 됐다고 느낄 그런 때가 올 때까지는. 오늘도 아니고, 내일도 아닐 거요. 그리고 널 넘겨준다거나, 네가 어디 있는지 일러바친다거나 하는 일은, 그래, 진실로, 하느님의 도우심을 빌어, 결단코 하지 않겠소, 교회 교리문답을 걸고, 명심해 두시오!\"\n\n이 말을 선장은, 인용까지 죄다, 한 숨에, 매우 엄숙하게 했다. \"그래, 진실로\" 하는 대목에서 모자를 벗었다가, 말을 다 끝맺고 나서야 다시 썼다.\n\n플로렌스가 그에게 고마움을 표하고, 자기가 얼마나 그를 믿는지 보여 주기 위해 할 수 있는 일은 단 한 가지뿐이었고, 그녀는 그것을 했다. 피 흘리는 자기 마음의 마지막 피난처인 이 거친 사내에게 매달려, 그녀는 그의 정직한 어깨에 머리를 기대고, 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안았으며, 무릎을 꿇고 그를 축복하려 했으나, 그가 그 뜻을 알아채고 참된 사내답게 그녀를 부축해 일으켰다.\n\n\"침착!\" 선장이 말했다. \"침착! 보다시피 넌 서 있기엔 너무 기운이 없으니, 우리 예쁜이, 여기 다시 누워야 해요. 자, 자!\" 선장이 그녀를 소파에 들어 올려 눕히고 자기 외투로 덮어 주는 모습은, 화려한 구경거리 백 개를 합친 것만큼 볼 만한 광경이었다. \"그리고 이제,\" 선장이 말했다, \"아침을 좀 들어야 해요, 아가씨, 그리고 개한테도 좀 줘야지. 그러고 나서는 솔 길스 영감의 방으로 올라가, 천사처럼 거기서 잠이 들어요.\"\n\n커틀 선장은 디오게네스를 언급하면서 그를 토닥여 주었고, 디오게네스도 그 호의를 정중히 절반쯤 받아들였다. 회복 조치가 진행되는 동안 그는 선장에게 달려들지, 우정을 내밀지를 두고 분명 마음이 두 갈래였던 터라, 꼬리를 흔들었다가 이빨을 드러냈다가, 이따금 으르렁거리기까지 하며 그 갈등을 표현했다. 그러나 이제는 그 의심이 모두 사라졌다. 그가 선장을 가장 상냥한 사람 중 하나로, 개로서 알고 지내는 것이 영광인 사람으로 여기게 된 것이 분명했다.\n\n이러한 확신의 증거로, 디오게네스는 선장이 차와 토스트를 만드는 동안 시중을 들며, 그의 살림살이에 활기찬 관심을 보였다. 그러나 마음씨 고운 선장이 플로렌스를 위해 그렇게 차린 음식은 헛수고였으니, 그녀는 그것들에 대접을 표하려 무던히 애썼지만 아무것도 입에 댈 수 없었고, 그저 울고 또 울 뿐이었다.\n\n\"자, 자!\" 동정심 많은 선장이 말했다, \"한숨 자고 나면, 우리 '마음의 기쁨'아, 좀 더 기운이 날 거요. 자, 이제 자네 몫을 나눠 주마, 이 녀석.\" 디오게네스에게 한 말이었다. \"그리고 자네는 위층에서 네 주인을 지켜야 해.\"\n\n그런데 디오게네스는, 군침을 흘리고 눈을 번득이며 차려진 자기 아침밥을 노려보고 있었으면서도, 그것이 앞에 놓이자 게걸스럽게 달려들기는커녕, 귀를 쫑긋 세우더니, 가게 문으로 쏜살같이 달려가 거기서 사납게 짖어 댔다. 마치 굴을 파서 밖으로 빠져나가기로 작정한 듯, 문 밑바닥을 머리로 파헤치면서.\n\n\"거기 누가 있는 건 아닐까요!\" 플로렌스가 놀라 물었다.\n\n\"아니오, 우리 아가씨,\" 선장이 대답했다. \"아무 소리도 안 내고 거기 있을 사람이 누가 있겠소! 마음 단단히 먹어요, 예쁜이. 그냥 지나가는 사람들일 뿐이오.\"\n\n그래도 디오게네스는 짖고 또 짖었고, 파고 또 팠으니, 끈질긴 광기로 그러했다. 그리고 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울일 때마다, 무언가 새로운 확신을 마음속에 받아들이는 듯, 다시 짖고 파헤치기를 열두 번도 더 했다. 가까스로 달래져 아침밥으로 돌아올 때조차, 그는 매우 미심쩍은 기색으로 어슬렁어슬렁 돌아왔고, 한 입도 대기 전에 또다시 발작을 일으키며 달려가 버렸다.\n\n\"누군가 엿듣고 지켜보는 사람이 있을지도 몰라요,\" 플로렌스가 속삭였다. \"제가 들어오는 걸 본 사람이—어쩌면 저를 따라온 사람이.\"\n\n\"그 아가씨는 아니겠지요, 우리 아가씨, 그렇소?\" 선장이 번뜩이는 생각이 떠올라 말했다.\n\n\"수전이요?\" 플로렌스가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"아, 아니에요! 수전은 제 곁을 떠난 지 오래됐어요.\"\n\n\"버림받은 건 아니길 바라오?\" 선장이 말했다. \"그 아가씨가 달아났다는 말은 말아 주오, 우리 예쁜이!\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요!\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다. \"그 애는 세상에서 가장 참된 마음을 가진 사람 중 하나예요!\"\n\n선장은 이 대답에 크게 안도하여, 만족스러운 마음을 표하느라 그 딱딱한 니스 칠 모자를 벗고, 공처럼 둘둘 만 손수건으로 머리 전체를 톡톡 두드렸으며, 무한히 흐뭇한 표정으로, 환한 얼굴로, 자기도 그럴 줄 알았노라고 여러 번 되뇌었다.\n\n\"자, 이제 좀 잠잠해졌느냐, 형제?\" 선장이 디오게네스에게 말했다. \"아무도 거기 없었소, 우리 아가씨, 안심해요!\"\n\n디오게네스는 그것을 그리 확신하지 못했다. 그 문은 여전히 이따금씩 그의 관심을 끌었고, 그는 그 문제를 잊지 못한 채, 문 둘레를 킁킁대며 혼자 으르렁거렸다. 이 사건과 더불어, 플로렌스의 피로와 쇠약함을 살핀 선장은, 솔 길스의 방을 그녀의 휴식처로 당장 마련해 두기로 결심했다. 그리하여 그는 부랴부랴 집 꼭대기로 올라가, 자기 상상력과 형편이 허락하는 한 최선을 다해 그 방을 꾸몄다.\n\n그 방은 이미 매우 깨끗했다. 게다가 선장은 단정한 사람이고 매사를 배 안처럼 말끔히 갖추는 데 익숙한 터라, 침대를 깨끗한 흰 천으로 죄다 덮어 긴 의자로 탈바꿈시켰다. 비슷한 궁리로, 선장은 작은 화장대를 일종의 제단으로 만들어, 그 위에 은 찻숟가락 두 개, 화분 하나, 망원경 하나, 그 유명한 자기 시계, 휴대용 빗 하나, 노래책 한 권을 진귀한 물건들의 작은 수집품인 양 늘어놓았는데, 그 모양새가 그럴듯해 보였다. 창문을 어둡게 가리고, 바닥에 깔린 양탄자 조각들을 반듯하게 편 뒤, 선장은 이 준비물들을 크게 흡족해하며 둘러보고는, 플로렌스를 그녀의 안식처로 데려오려고 다시 작은 응접실로 내려갔다.\n\n선장은 무슨 일이 있어도 플로렌스가 계단을 걸어 올라갈 수 있으리라고는 믿으려 하지 않았다. 설령 그런 생각이 머릿속에 들어왔다 해도, 그녀에게 그렇게 하도록 내버려 두는 것은 손님맞이 도리에 어긋나는 괘씸한 일이라 여겼을 것이다. 플로렌스는 너무 기운이 없어 그 점을 따질 수도 없었고, 선장은 그녀를 단번에 들쳐 안고 올라가, 눕히고, 커다란 방수 외투로 덮어 주었다.\n\n\"우리 아가씨!\" 선장이 말했다, \"여긴 사다리를 치워 버린 세인트폴 대성당 꼭대기에 있는 것만큼이나 안전하오. 무엇보다도 잠이 너에게 필요한 것이고, 부디 상처 입은 마음의 가냘프고 작은 목소리를 달래 줄 그 향유 덕에 네가 거뜬해진 모습을 보여 줄 수 있기를! 무엇이든 필요한 게 있거든, 우리 '마음의 기쁨'아, 이 누추한 집이나 거리가 내놓을 수 있는 것이라면, 저 문밖에서 가까이 떠돌며 망보고 있을 에드워드 커틀한테 한마디만 전해 주오, 그러면 그 사내는 기쁨에 겨워 부르르 떨 거요.\" 선장은 플로렌스가 내민 손에, 어느 옛 편력 기사 못지않은 의협심으로 입을 맞추고는, 발끝으로 살금살금 방을 나가는 것으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n작은 응접실로 내려와, 커틀 선장은 혼자서 황급히 의논을 한 끝에, 가게 문을 잠시 열어 두고, 적어도 지금은 그 주위를 어슬렁거리는 사람이 아무도 없다는 것을 스스로 확인해 두기로 결심했다. 그래서 그는 문을 열어 놓고 문지방에 서서, 날카롭게 망을 보며, 안경 너머로 온 거리를 훑어보았다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 길스 선장?\" 곁에서 한 목소리가 말했다. 선장이 내려다보니, 자기가 수평선을 훑고 있는 사이에 투츠 씨가 자기 곁에 올라타 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"잘 지냈나, 이 사람아?\" 선장이 대답했다.\n\n\"네, 저는 꽤 잘 지냅니다, 고맙습니다, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"아시다시피 저는 이제 결코 제가 바라는 만큼의 상태가 못 됩니다. 앞으로도 더 나아지리라고는 기대하지 않고요.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 커틀 선장과 대화할 때면, 둘 사이의 약조 때문에 자기 인생의 그 위대한 주제에 이 이상으로는 결코 가까이 다가가지 않았다.\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"선장님과 한마디 나누는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있다면 좋겠는데요, 그게—그게 좀 특별한 일이라서요.\"\n\n\"그게 말일세, 이 사람아,\" 선장이 응접실로 앞장서 가며 대답했다, \"내가 오늘 아침엔 딱히 한가하다 할 처지가 못 되거든. 그러니 자네가 좀 서둘러 줄 수 있다면, 고맙게 여기겠네.\"\n\n\"물론입죠, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다. 선장의 말뜻을 좀처럼 알아듣지 못하는 사람이었다. \"서두르는 거야말로 제가 딱 바라는 바입니다. 당연하고말고요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 이 사람아,\" 선장이 응수했다. \"그렇게 하게!\"\n\n선장은 자기가 품은 그 어마어마한 비밀에 어찌나 짓눌려 있었던지—바로 그 순간 돔비 양이 자기 지붕 아래 있는데, 그것도 모르는 순진한 투츠가 자기 맞은편에 앉아 있다는 사실에—이마에 진땀이 솟았고, 니스 칠한 모자를 손에 든 채 그것을 천천히 닦으면서도 투츠 씨의 얼굴에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다. 자기 나름으로 무언가 마음 졸일 비밀스러운 사정이 있는 듯한 투츠 씨는, 선장의 응시에 어찌나 말할 수 없이 당황했던지, 한동안 말없이 멍하니 그를 바라보다가, 의자에서 불안하게 몸을 들썩이더니 말했다.\n\n\"실례합니다만, 길스 선장님, 혹시 저한테서 뭔가 이상한 점이라도 보이시는 건 아니지요?\"\n\n\"아닐세, 이 사람아,\" 선장이 대답했다. \"아닐세.\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 말이죠,\" 투츠 씨가 낄낄 웃으며 말했다, \"제가 점점 야위어 가고 있다는 거 저도 압니다. 그 얘기를 꺼내셔도 전혀 개의치 않으셔도 돼요. 저는—저는 오히려 그게 좋습니다. 버지스 상회에서 제 치수를 다시 쟀어요, 제가 그 정도로 말랐거든요. 그게 저한테는 만족스럽습니다. 저는—저는 그게 기쁩니다. 저는—할 수만 있다면 차라리 쇠약해져 갔으면 좋겠어요. 아시다시피 저는 그저 한 마리 짐승일 뿐이니까요, 땅거죽이나 뜯어 먹고 사는, 길스 선장님.\"\n\n투츠 씨가 이런 식으로 말을 이어 갈수록, 선장은 자기 비밀에 더욱 짓눌리며 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 이 불안의 원인에다, 투츠 씨를 떼어 내고 싶은 마음까지 겹쳐, 선장은 실로 어찌나 겁먹고 묘한 상태였던지, 설령 유령과 대화를 나누고 있었다 한들 이보다 더 큰 동요를 드러내기도 어려웠을 것이다.\n\n\"그런데 제가 말씀드리려던 건요, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"오늘 아침 일찍 마침 이쪽으로 오던 길이었는데요—사실대로 말씀드리면, 선장님과 아침을 함께하러 오는 길이었습니다. 잠으로 말할 것 같으면, 아시다시피 저는 이제 도통 잠을 못 잡니다. 야경꾼이나 다름없는데, 다만 저는 삯을 못 받고, 그 사람은 마음에 걸리는 게 아무것도 없다는 점이 다르지요.\"\n\n\"계속하게, 이 사람아!\" 선장이 타이르는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"물론입죠, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"지당하신 말씀! 오늘 아침 일찍(한 시간쯤 전에) 마침 이쪽으로 오다가, 문이 닫힌 걸 보고는—\"\n\n\"뭐! 자네가 거기서 기다리고 있었나, 형제?\" 선장이 캐물었다.\n\n\"전혀 아닙니다, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다. \"잠시도 멈추지 않았어요. 선장님이 외출하신 줄 알았거든요. 그런데 그 사람이 말하길—그런데 말입니다, 선장님은 개를 안 기르시지요, 길스 선장님?\"\n\n선장은 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그렇고말고요,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"제가 한 말이 바로 그겁니다. 안 기르시는 줄 제가 알고 있었죠. 어떤 개 한 마리가, 길스 선장님, 어떤 분과 관련이 있는데—하지만 죄송합니다. 그건 금지된 영역이죠.\"\n\n선장은 투츠 씨가 제 몸집의 두 배로 부풀어 오르는 것처럼 보일 때까지 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그리고 디오게네스가 문득 아래로 내려와 응접실에 제삼자로 끼어들 생각을 할까 봐 떠올리자, 선장의 이마에는 또다시 진땀이 솟았다.\n\n\"그 사람이 말하길,\" 투츠 씨가 계속했다, \"가게 안에서 개 짖는 소리를 들었다는 거예요. 그럴 리 없다는 걸 제가 아니까, 그렇게 말해 줬죠. 그런데도 그 사람은 마치 그 개를 직접 보기라도 한 것처럼 단언하더군요.\"\n\n\"어떤 사람 말인가, 이 사람아?\" 선장이 물었다.\n\n\"그게 말이죠, 바로 그게 문제입니다, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 거동의 불안함이 눈에 띄게 더해지며 말했다. \"무슨 일이 있었는지, 또는 무슨 일이 없었는지 말하는 건 제 몫이 아니에요. 사실 저도 모르겠고요. 저는 도무지 잘 이해되지 않는 온갖 일에 휘말리곤 하는데, 제 생각엔 제—제 머리에 좀 모자란 구석이 있는 것 같아요, 요컨대.\"\n\n선장은 동의의 표시로 자기 머리를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"하지만 그 사람이, 우리가 걸어 나오는데 말하길,\" 투츠 씨가 계속했다, \"현재의 상황에서 무슨 일이 일어날지도 모른다는 걸 선장님이 아신다고—'모른다'를 아주 힘주어 말하더군요—그리고 만일 선장님더러 채비를 갖춰 두라고 부탁하면, 분명히 채비를 하고 오실 거라고요.\"\n\n\"사람이라니, 이 사람아.\" 선장이 되뇌었다.\n\n\"어떤 사람인지는 정말 저도 모르겠어요, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"전혀 짐작이 안 갑니다. 그런데 문간에 와 보니, 그 사람이 거기서 기다리고 있더군요. 그러더니 제가 다시 돌아갈 거냐고 묻길래, 그렇다고 했죠. 그리고 선장님을 아느냐고 묻길래, 그렇다고, 선장님과 친분을 나누는 기쁨을 누리고 있다고—선장님이 얼마간 권유를 하신 끝에 저한테 친분을 나누는 기쁨을 주셨다고 했어요. 그랬더니 그 사람이, 사정이 그렇다면, 제가 방금 말씀드린 현재의 상황이며 채비를 하고 오시라는 얘기를 선장님께 전해 주고, 선장님을 뵙는 즉시, 단 일 분이라도 좋으니 아주 중요한 용무로 모퉁이를 돌아 브로글리 중개인 가게로 잠깐 들러 주십사 부탁드려 달라더군요. 자, 그래서 말씀인데요, 길스 선장님—그게 무엇이든 간에, 저는 그게 아주 중요한 일이라고 확신합니다. 그러니 지금 잠깐 다녀오시겠다면, 선장님이 돌아오실 때까지 제가 여기서 기다리고 있겠습니다.\"\n\n선장은, 가지 않음으로써 어떤 식으로든 플로렌스를 위태롭게 할까 봐 하는 두려움과, 투츠 씨에게 집을 맡겨 두었다가 비밀을 들킬지도 모른다는 끔찍함 사이에서 갈팡질팡했으니, 그 마음의 동요는 투츠 씨조차 못 알아챌 수 없을 만한 광경이었다. 그러나 그 젊은 신사는, 자기의 항해사 친구가 그저 자기가 가질 면담에 대비해 채비를 하는 상태일 뿐이라고 여겨, 아주 흡족해했고, 자기 자신의 신중한 처신을 낄낄 웃지 않고는 되돌아볼 수 없었다.\n\n마침내 선장은, 두 악 가운데 덜한 쪽으로, 브로글리 중개인 가게로 잠깐 다녀오기로 결심했다. 그에 앞서 집 위층으로 통하는 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 주머니에 넣었다. \"그게 말일세,\" 선장이 적잖은 부끄러움과 망설임을 담아 투츠 씨에게 말했다, \"내가 이렇게 하는 걸 양해해 준다면 말이지, 형제.\"\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"선장님이 무엇을 하시든, 저로서는 흡족합니다.\"\n\n선장은 진심으로 그에게 고마워하고, 오 분 안에 돌아오겠다고 약속한 뒤, 투츠 씨에게 그 알 수 없는 전갈을 맡긴 사람을 찾아 밖으로 나갔다. 가엾은 투츠 씨는 홀로 남겨지자 소파에 드러누웠으니, 바로 거기에 마지막으로 누웠던 이가 누구였는지는 꿈에도 모른 채, 천창을 올려다보며 돔비 양에 대한 환상에 자신을 내맡기고는, 시간이며 장소며 다 잊어버렸다.\n\n그가 그렇게 한 것은 잘된 일이었다. 선장은 오래 가 있지는 않았지만, 자기가 말했던 것보다는 훨씬 오래 가 있었기 때문이다. 돌아왔을 때 그는 실로 몹시 창백했고, 크게 동요해 있었으며, 심지어 눈물을 흘린 것처럼 보이기까지 했다. 그는 찬장으로 가서 됫병에서 럼주를 한 모금 들이켤 때까지 말하는 능력을 잃어버린 듯하더니, 깊이 숨을 한 번 내쉬고는, 한 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 의자에 앉았다.\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠가 다정하게 말했다, \"아무 일도 없기를 바라고 또 믿습니다만?\"\n\n\"고맙네, 이 사람아, 아무 일도 없네,\" 선장이 말했다. \"오히려 정반대일세.\"\n\n\"넋이 나가신 것 같은 모습이신데요, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아닌 게 아니라, 이 사람아, 내가 좀 얼이 빠졌네,\" 선장이 인정했다. \"그래.\"\n\n\"제가 할 수 있는 일이 있을까요, 길스 선장님?\" 투츠 씨가 물었다. \"있다면, 저를 부려 주세요.\"\n\n선장은 얼굴에서 손을 떼고, 보기 드문 연민과 다정함의 표정으로 그를 바라보더니, 그의 손을 잡아 힘껏 흔들었다.\n\n\"아니, 고맙네,\" 선장이 말했다. \"아무것도 없네. 다만 지금은 자네가 자리를 비켜 준다면 고맙겠네. 내 생각에, 형제,\" 그의 손을 다시 한번 꼭 쥐며, \"월터 다음으로는, 그리고 다른 본보기로 치자면, 자네는 여태껏 세상에 나온 그 누구 못지않은 좋은 사람일세.\"\n\n\"맹세코 그리고 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데요, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 선장의 손을 다시 흔들기에 앞서 한 번 툭 치며 대답했다, \"선장님의 좋은 평을 얻게 되어 저로서는 기쁘기 그지없습니다. 고맙습니다.\"\n\n\"그러니 기운을 내고 힘을 좀 내게,\" 선장이 그의 등을 토닥이며 말했다. \"아니! 세상에 귀여운 아가씨가 한 사람만 있는 건 아니잖나!\"\n\n\"저한테는 그렇지 않습니다, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"저한테는 그렇지 않아요, 정말입니다. 돔비 양을 향한 제 감정의 상태는 말로 다 할 수 없는 그런 것이어서, 제 마음은 하나의 무인도이고, 그분이 그곳에 홀로 사십니다. 저는 날마다 더 쇠약해져 가는데, 저는 그게 자랑스럽습니다. 제가 부츠를 벗었을 때 제 다리를 보신다면, 보답받지 못한 사랑이라는 게 어떤 건지 얼마쯤 짐작이 가실 겁니다. 의사가 키나나무 껍질을 처방했는데, 저는 안 먹습니다, 제 체질에 어떤 식으로든 기력이 붙는 건 바라지 않으니까요. 차라리 안 그러는 게 낫지요. 하지만 이건 금지된 영역이죠. 길스 선장님, 안녕히 계십시오!\"\n\n커틀 선장은 투츠 씨의 따뜻한 작별 인사에 진심으로 화답하고는, 그의 등 뒤로 문을 잠그고, 앞서 그를 바라보던 것과 똑같은 보기 드문 연민과 다정함의 표정으로 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 플로렌스가 자기를 찾는지 보려고 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n위층으로 올라가는 선장의 얼굴에는 완전한 변화가 일어나 있었다. 그는 손수건으로 눈을 닦고, 그날 아침 이미 한 번 그랬던 것처럼 소매로 콧날을 문질러 닦았지만, 그의 얼굴은 그야말로 달라져 있었다. 지금 그는 더없이 행복해 보인다고 여길 수도 있었고, 지금 그는 슬퍼 보인다고 여길 수도 있었다. 그러나 그의 이목구비에 깃든 그런 종류의 진지함은 거기에는 아주 새로운 것이었으며, 마치 그 얼굴이 어떤 승화의 과정을 거치기라도 한 듯 크나큰 개선을 이루어 놓은 것이었다.\n\n그는 갈고리로 플로렌스의 방문을 부드럽게 두세 번 두드렸다. 그러나 아무 대답이 없자, 우선 살그머니 안을 들여다보았고, 그다음에는 들어가 보았다. 그 두 번째 걸음을 감행하게 된 것은, 아마도 디오게네스가 낯익게 알아봐 준 덕분이었을 것이다. 디오게네스는 그녀의 긴 의자 옆 바닥에 길게 누운 채, 굳이 일어나는 수고는 하지 않고, 선장을 향해 꼬리를 흔들며 눈을 끔벅여 주었다.\n\n그녀는 깊이 잠들어 있었고, 잠결에 신음하고 있었다. 커틀 선장은 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움과 슬픔에 완전히 외경심을 품고서, 그녀의 머리를 받쳐 주고, 흘러내린 그녀를 덮은 외투를 고쳐 덮어 주고, 그녀가 계속 잘 수 있도록 창문을 조금 더 어둡게 가리고는, 다시 살그머니 빠져나와 계단에서 자기 망보는 자리를 잡았다. 이 모든 것을, 플로렌스 자신의 그것만큼이나 가벼운 손길과 발걸음으로 했다.\n\n이 뒤섞인 세상에서, 전능하신 분의 선하심을 보여 주는 더 아름다운 증거가 어느 쪽인지—섬세한 감수성과 어루만지는 공감을 위해 빚어져, 아픔과 슬픔을 돌보도록 만들어진 그 가냘픈 손가락들인지, 아니면 마음이 한순간에 가르치고 이끌고 부드럽게 만드는, 커틀 선장의 그 거칠고 단단한 손인지—오래도록 가리기 쉽지 않은 문제로 남아 있어도 좋으리라!\n\n플로렌스는 자기 긴 의자에서, 갈 곳 없는 처지도 부모 없는 신세도 잊은 채 잠들었고, 커틀 선장은 계단에서 망을 보았다. 여느 때보다 더 큰 흐느낌이나 신음이 이따금 그를 그녀의 방문 앞으로 데려갔지만, 차츰 그녀는 더 평온히 잠들었고, 선장의 망보기는 방해받지 않았다.\n\n# 제44장\n\n플로렌스가 잠에서 깨어나기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸렸다. 한낮이 되었다가, 다시 저물어 가는 시각이 되도록, 마음도 몸도 편치 못한 채 그녀는 줄곧 잠들어 있었다. 낯선 침대도, 거리의 소음과 소란도, 가려진 창밖에서 비쳐 드는 빛도 의식하지 못했다. 이제는 존재하지 않는 그 집에서 일어난 일에 대한 완전한 무의식만은, 기진맥진한 깊은 잠으로도 만들어 낼 수 없었다. 그 일에 대한 어떤 흐릿하고 슬픈 기억이, 불안하게 졸뿐 결코 잠들지는 않은 채, 그녀의 모든 휴식에 스며 있었다. 반쯤 잠재워진 고통의 감각 같은 둔중한 슬픔이 늘 그녀 곁에 있었고, 그녀의 창백한 뺨은 반쯤 닫힌 문 사이로 이따금 머리를 들이밀어 살펴보던 정직한 선장이 보고 싶어 했을 것보다 더 자주 눈물에 젖어 있었다.\n\n해가 서쪽으로 낮게 기울며, 붉은 안개 사이로 비스듬히 비쳐 나와, 도시 교회 첨탑의 맞은편 총안과 격자 세공들을 그 빛살로 꿰뚫었으니, 마치 그것들을 관통하여 박히는 황금 화살 같았고—멀리 강과 그 평평한 둑을 가로질러 불의 길처럼 번쩍였으며—바다 멀리 나가서는 배들의 돛을 환히 비추었고—시골 언덕마루의 고요한 묘지에서 바라보면, 먼 풍경을 붉은 기운과 노을빛에 잠기게 하여 마치 땅과 하늘을 하나의 찬란한 충만 속에 뒤섞어 놓은 듯했다—바로 그때 플로렌스가 무거운 눈을 떠, 처음에는 누운 채 주위의 낯선 벽들을 흥미도 알아봄도 없이 바라보았고, 거리의 소음에도 똑같이 무심한 태도로 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 곧 그녀는 침상에서 벌떡 일어나, 놀라고 멍한 눈빛으로 주위를 둘러보고는, 모든 것을 기억해 냈다.\n\n\"우리 예쁜 아가씨,\" 선장이 문을 두드리며 말했다, \"기분이 좀 어떻수?\"\n\n\"그리운 친구분,\" 플로렌스가 그에게로 황급히 다가서며 외쳤다, \"선장님이세요?\"\n\n선장은 그 호칭에 너무도 큰 자부심을 느꼈고, 그녀가 자기를 보았을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 스치는 기쁨의 빛에 너무도 흐뭇하여, 대답 대신 말없는 흡족함으로 자기 갈고리에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"기분이 좀 어떻수, 빛나는 다이아몬드 같은 아가씨?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"제가 정말 오래 잤나 봐요,\" 플로렌스가 대답했다. \"제가 언제 여기 왔죠? 어제였나요?\"\n\n\"바로 오늘이라우, 복받은 오늘 말이우, 아가씨,\" 선장이 대답했다.\n\n\"밤이 없었나요? 아직도 낮인가요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"이제 저녁으로 접어드는 참이라우, 예쁜 아가씨,\" 선장이 창문 커튼을 젖히며 말했다. \"보시구려!\"\n\n플로렌스는 선장의 팔에 손을 얹은 채, 그토록 서글프고 수줍어하며, 선장은 거친 얼굴과 우람한 몸집으로 그토록 조용히 그녀를 감싸 보호하며, 밝은 저녁 하늘의 장밋빛 속에 한마디 말도 없이 서 있었다. 만일 그 감정을 입 밖에 내어야 했더라면 그것을 어떤 기묘한 말투로 빚어냈을지 몰라도, 선장은 그 고요한 시간 속에, 그리고 그 부드러워진 아름다움 속에 플로렌스의 상처 입은 마음을 넘쳐흐르게 할 무언가가 있음을, 가장 언변 좋은 사람만큼이나 또렷하게 느꼈다. 그리고 그런 눈물은 흐르도록 내버려 두는 편이 낫다는 것도. 그래서 커틀 선장은 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그녀의 팔이 더 가까이 자기 팔을 부여잡는 것을 느끼고, 그 외로운 머리가 더 다가와 자기의 투박한 거친 푸른 소맷자락에 기대는 것을 느끼자, 그는 우툴두툴한 손으로 그것을 부드럽게 눌러 주었고, 그것을 이해했고, 또 이해받았다.\n\n\"이제 좀 나아졌수, 예쁜 아가씨!\" 선장이 말했다. \"기운 내우, 기운 내우, 내 아래층으로 내려가서 저녁을 좀 차려 보겠수. 나중에 혼자서 내려올 수 있겠수, 예쁜 아가씨, 아니면 에드워드 커틀이 데리러 올라올까?\"\n\n플로렌스가 혼자서 충분히 계단을 내려갈 수 있다고 그를 안심시키자, 선장은 그것을 허락하는 것이 자기 접대의 도리에 어긋나는 게 아닐까 미심쩍어하면서도 그녀가 그렇게 하도록 내버려 두고, 곧장 작은 거실 난롯불에 닭 한 마리를 굽기 시작했다. 더 솜씨 좋게 요리를 해내려고 그는 외투를 벗고, 소맷부리를 걷어 올리고, 번들거리는 모자를 썼는데, 그는 무언가 정교하거나 까다로운 일에 착수할 때면 이 조력자 없이는 결코 손을 대는 법이 없었다.\n\n선장이 그녀가 잠든 동안 마련해 둔 맑은 물에 욱신거리는 머리와 화끈거리는 얼굴을 식힌 후, 플로렌스는 헝클어진 머리를 매만지러 작은 거울 앞으로 갔다. 그때 그녀는 알아챘다—한순간에, 곧바로 외면해 버렸으니까, 자기 가슴에 성난 손이 남긴 거무스름해져 가는 자국이 있다는 것을.\n\n그것을 본 순간 그녀의 눈물이 다시 터져 나왔다. 그녀는 그것이 부끄럽고 두려웠다. 그러나 그것이 그에 대한 어떤 분노로 그녀를 이끌지는 않았다. 집도 없고 아버지도 없게 된 그녀는 그에게 모든 것을 용서했다. 자기가 그를 용서할 필요가 있다거나 용서하고 있다는 생각조차 거의 하지 않았다. 다만 그녀는 그 실체로부터 달아났듯이 그에 대한 생각으로부터도 달아났고, 그는 완전히 사라져 없어졌다. 세상에 그런 사람은 없었다.\n\n무엇을 해야 할지, 어디서 살아야 할지, 플로렌스는—가엾고 세상 물정 모르는 소녀!—아직 헤아릴 수가 없었다. 그녀에게는 흐릿한 꿈이 있었다. 아주 먼 어딘가에서 가르칠 어린 자매들을 찾아, 그 아이들이 자기에게 다정히 굴고, 어떤 가명으로 그 아이들에게 정을 붙이고, 아이들이 행복한 가정에서 자라나 시집을 가서는 자기들의 늙은 가정교사에게 잘해 주고, 어쩌면 때가 되면 자기들의 딸들 교육을 그녀에게 맡길지도 모른다는 꿈. 그리고 그녀는 생각했다. 플로렌스 돔비가 잊힌 뒤에, 자기 비밀을 무덤까지 안고 가는 백발의 여인이 되어 간다는 것이 얼마나 기이하고 서글픈 일일까 하고. 그러나 지금은 그 모든 것이 그녀에게 흐릿하고 어슴푸레했다. 그녀는 다만 이 땅 위에 자기에게는 아버지가 없다는 것만을 알았고, 모두에게 가려 숨긴 채 오직 하늘에 계신 아버지께만 드러낸 그 간구하는 머리로, 그 말을 여러 번 되뇌었다.\n\n그녀가 지닌 얼마 안 되는 돈은 몇 기니에 지나지 않았다. 그 일부로 옷가지를 좀 사야 했으니, 지금 입고 있는 것 말고는 가진 옷이 없었기 때문이다. 그녀는 너무도 막막하여 자기 돈이 얼마나 빨리 바닥날지 헤아릴 마음의 여유가 없었다—세상일에는 너무도 어린아이 같아서, 설령 다른 근심이 덜했더라도 그 문제로 크게 마음 졸이지는 못했을 것이다. 그녀는 생각을 가라앉히고 눈물을 그치려 애썼고, 지끈거리는 머릿속의 어수선함을 진정시키며, 일어난 일이 몇 주나 몇 달 전이 아니라—그렇게만 여겨졌지만—그저 몇 시간 전의 사건이라고 스스로 믿어 보려 했다. 그러고는 그 친절한 보호자에게로 내려갔다.\n\n선장은 식탁보를 무척 정성스럽게 펴 놓고, 작은 냄비에 달걀 소스를 만들고 있었다. 그러는 동안 끈에 매달려 난롯불 앞에서 돌아가며 노릇노릇 구워지는 닭에 대단한 관심을 기울이며 이따금 기름을 끼얹어 주었다. 더 편안하라고 이미 따뜻한 구석으로 밀어다 놓은 소파에 쿠션을 받쳐 플로렌스를 기대 앉히고 나서, 선장은 비상한 솜씨로 요리를 이어 갔다. 두 번째 작은 냄비에 뜨거운 그레이비를 만들고, 세 번째 냄비에 감자 한 줌을 삶고, 첫 번째 냄비의 달걀 소스도 결코 잊지 않으며, 더없이 요긴한 숟가락으로 일 분마다 골고루 기름을 끼얹고 휘저었다. 이런 수고들에 더해 선장은 자그마한 프라이팬도 살펴야 했는데, 그 안에서는 소시지 몇 개가 더없이 음악적인 소리로 지글지글 보글보글 끓고 있었다. 그리고 이 일들이 한창 무르익어 열기를 더할 때의 선장만큼 환히 빛나는 요리사도 없었으니, 그의 얼굴과 번들거리는 모자 중 어느 것이 더 환하게 빛나는지 가려낼 수 없을 정도였다.\n\n마침내 저녁 식사가 완전히 준비되자, 커틀 선장은 요리할 때 못지않은 능숙함으로 음식을 접시에 담아 차려 냈다. 그러고는 번들거리는 모자를 벗고 외투를 입음으로써 저녁 식사를 위한 정장을 갖추었다. 그 일을 마치자 그는 소파에 앉은 플로렌스에게 바싹 식탁을 밀어다 붙이고, 식전 기도를 올리고, 갈고리를 풀어 내고 그 자리에 포크를 끼워 넣고는, 식탁의 주인 노릇을 했다.\n\n\"아가씨,\" 선장이 말했다, \"기운 내우, 그리고 많이 좀 들어 보구려. 자, 옆에서 대기하겠수, 우리 아가씨! 닭 다릿살이라우. 소스라우. 소시지라우. 그리고 감자!\" 이 모든 것을 선장은 접시 위에 가지런히 늘어놓고, 그 요긴한 숟가락으로 뜨거운 그레이비를 전부에 부어, 자기가 애지중지하는 손님 앞에 차려 놓았다.\n\n\"앞쪽 죽은 등(燈) 덮개도 한 줄로 죄다 올려놨고, 아가씨,\" 선장이 격려하듯 말했다, \"모든 게 다 아늑하게 준비됐수. 좀 들어 보구려, 예쁜 아가씨. 월터가 여기 있었다면—\"\n\n\"아! 지금 그분이 제 오라버니이기만 했다면!\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그러지! 그렇게 상심하지 마우, 예쁜 아가씨!\" 선장이 말했다, \"이 늙은이 봐서라도, 멈추구려! 그 애는 아가씨한테 타고난 천생의 벗 같은 사람이었지, 안 그렇수, 아가씨?\"\n\n플로렌스는 대답할 말이 없었다. 그녀는 다만 \"오, 그리운, 그리운 폴! 오, 월터!\"라고만 말했다.\n\n\"그 애가 밟고 다니던 바로 그 널빤지마저,\" 선장이 그녀의 수그린 얼굴을 바라보며 중얼거렸다, \"월터한테는 결코 만족할 줄 모르는 수사슴이 시냇물을 귀히 여기는 만큼이나 높이 떠받들어졌다우! 지금도 그 애가 눈에 선하우, 바로 그 돔비 회사 장부에 이름이 올라가던 날, 저녁 식사 자리에서 얼굴이 이슬로 반짝이며—아니 그 애의 겸손한 마음씨로, 갓 피어난 장미처럼 반짝이며—아가씨 얘기를 하던 모습이. 그래, 그래! 우리 가엾은 월터가 여기 있다면, 아가씨—아니, 있을 수만 있다면—그 애는 물에 빠져 죽었으니까, 안 그렇수?\"\n\n플로렌스는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 물에 빠져 죽었지,\" 선장이 달래듯 말했다, \"내 말했듯이, 그 애가 여기 있을 수만 있다면 아가씨한테 빌고 또 빌었을 거라우, 우리 귀한 아가씨, 자기 고운 건강을 챙겨서 조금이라도 좀 들라고 말이우. 그러니 버텨 내우, 아가씨, 월터를 위해서라고 여기고, 그 고운 머리를 바람 쪽으로 들어 올리구려.\"\n\n플로렌스는 선장을 기쁘게 해 주려고 한 입 먹어 보려 애썼다. 그동안 선장은 자기 저녁밥은 까맣게 잊은 듯, 칼과 포크를 내려놓고 의자를 소파 쪽으로 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"월터는 단정한 녀석이었지, 안 그렇수, 귀한 아가씨?\" 선장이 한동안 말없이 앉아 그녀에게 눈길을 고정한 채 턱을 문지르고 나서 말했다, \"그리고 용감한 녀석이었고, 착한 녀석이었지?\"\n\n플로렌스는 눈물을 글썽이며 그렇다고 했다.\n\n\"그리고 그 애는 물에 빠져 죽었지, 어여쁜 아가씨, 안 그렇수?\" 선장이 달래는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n플로렌스는 또다시 그렇다고 할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"그 애는 아가씨보다 나이가 많았지, 아가씨,\" 선장이 이어 갔다, \"하지만 처음엔 아가씨네 둘이 꼭 어린아이 둘 같았지, 안 그렇수?\"\n\n플로렌스는 \"네\"라고 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 월터는 물에 빠져 죽었지,\" 선장이 말했다. \"안 그렇수?\"\n\n이 물음을 거듭하는 것이 위안의 기묘한 원천이었으나, 커틀 선장에게는 그것이 하나의 위안인 듯했으니, 그는 몇 번이고 다시 그 물음으로 되돌아왔다. 플로렌스는 손도 대지 않은 저녁밥을 밀어 내고 소파에 다시 기대고 싶은 마음에, 자기가 그를 실망시켰다고 느끼면서도—그 온갖 수고 끝에 진심으로 그를 기쁘게 해 주고 싶었으나—그에게 손을 내밀었고, 그는 그것을 자기 손에(그 손은 잡으면서 떨렸다) 쥐고는, 저녁 식사며 그녀가 입맛이 없다는 것이며 죄다 까맣게 잊어버린 듯, 곱씹는 듯한 연민의 어조로 이따금 중얼중얼 이어 갔다, \"가엾은 월터. 아아! 물에 빠져 죽었지. 안 그렇수?\" 그리고 늘 그녀의 대답을 기다렸으니, 이 별난 사색의 큰 요점이 거기에 있는 듯했다.\n\n선장이 닭고기와 소시지가 식탁에 차려져 있다는 것을 떠올려 디오게네스의 도움을 받아 먹어 치우기 시작했을 무렵에는 그것들은 차게 식었고 그레이비와 달걀 소스는 굳어 있었다. 둘이 힘을 합치자 잔치는 금세 끝났다. 플로렌스가 식탁을 치우고 거실을 정돈하고 난로를 쓸어 내는 일을 거드는 그 조용한 살림 솜씨에 선장이 느낀 기쁨과 경탄은—그녀가 자기를 거들기 시작하자 그가 내뱉은 항의의 열렬함에나 견줄 만한 것이었는데—차츰 그 정도가 높아져, 마침내 그는 자기는 아무것도 하지 못하고, 마치 그녀가 자기를 위해 그 일들을 우아하게 해 주는 어떤 요정이기라도 한 듯 그녀를 바라보며 서 있을 수밖에 없었다. 그의 이마에 난 붉은 자국이 형언할 수 없는 감탄 속에서 다시 벌겋게 달아올랐다.\n\n그러나 플로렌스가 벽난로 선반에서 그의 파이프를 내려 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 피우라고 권하자, 선량한 선장은 그녀의 그 배려에 너무도 어리둥절하여, 마치 평생 파이프라고는 한 번도 쥐어 본 적이 없는 사람처럼 그것을 쥐고 있었다. 마찬가지로, 플로렌스가 작은 찬장을 들여다보고 술병을 꺼내 청하지도 않았는데 그를 위해 완벽한 그로그 한 잔을 타서 그의 팔꿈치 곁에 놓아 주자, 그의 불그레한 코가 창백해졌으니, 그토록 큰 영광과 대접을 받았다고 느꼈던 것이다. 그가 완전한 만족의 황홀경에 빠져 파이프에 담배를 채워 넣자 플로렌스가 그를 위해 불을 붙여 주었고—선장은 그것을 거절하거나 막을 힘이 없었다—그러고는 낡은 소파의 제자리로 돌아가 어찌나 다정하고 고마운 미소를 띠며 그를 바라보았던지, 그녀의 쓸쓸한 마음이 그녀의 얼굴이 그렇듯 슬픔을 헤치고 그에게로 향하고 있음을 그토록 또렷이 보여 주는 그 미소에, 파이프 연기가 선장의 목구멍으로 들어가 기침을 하게 만들고, 선장의 눈으로 들어가 눈을 깜박이고 눈물 짓게 만들었다.\n\n선장이 이 효과의 원인이 파이프 자체에 숨어 있는 척하려고 애쓰던 모습, 그 원인을 찾으려고 파이프 통을 들여다보다가 거기서 찾지 못하자 대를 통해 후 불어 내는 척하던 그 꼴이란 놀라우리만치 흐뭇한 광경이었다. 파이프가 곧 좀 나은 상태가 되자, 그는 능숙한 흡연가에게 어울리는 그 안식의 경지에 빠져들었다. 그러나 눈은 플로렌스에게 고정한 채로, 이루 형언할 수 없이 환하게 빛나는 평온함으로, 이따금 멈춰 입술에서 작은 구름을 내뿜으며, 천천히 연기를 뿜어냈으니, 마치 그것이 그의 입에서 흘러나오는 두루마리이고 거기에 \"가엾은 월터, 아아. 물에 빠져 죽었지, 안 그렇수?\"라는 글귀가 적혀 있기라도 한 듯했다. 그러고 나서는 더없이 부드럽게 다시 담배를 피우곤 했다.\n\n겉모습으로는 그토록 달랐지만—여리고 젊은 아름다움을 지닌 플로렌스와 울퉁불퉁한 얼굴에 풍상에 시달린 크고 떡 벌어진 몸집과 거친 목소리를 지닌 커틀 선장보다 더 뚜렷한 대조도 좀처럼 없었으리라—세상의 방식이며 세상의 곤혹과 위험에 대한 천진난만한 무지에 있어서는 그 둘이 거의 같은 수준이었다. 바람과 날씨를 빼고는 모든 일에서의 무경험에 있어, 그 순박함과 잘 믿는 마음과 너그럽게 신뢰하는 마음에 있어, 어떤 아이도 커틀 선장을 능가할 수 없었으리라. 믿음과 소망과 사랑이 그의 천성 전체를 나누어 차지하고 있었다. 한 가지 기묘한 종류의 낭만, 도무지 상상력이라곤 없으면서도 완벽하게 비현실적이고, 세속적인 분별이나 실행 가능성 따위는 전혀 고려하지 않는 그 낭만만이 그의 성품 속에서 그것들과 동무하는 유일한 것이었다. 선장이 앉아 담배를 피우며 플로렌스를 바라보는 동안, 그녀가 주인공으로 등장하는 어떤 있을 수 없는 그림들이 그의 마음속에 떠올랐는지는 하느님만 아실 일이었다. 그녀 자신의, 앞에 놓인 삶에 대한 생각들도 그만큼 막연하고 불확실했으나, 그처럼 낙관적이지는 않았다. 그리고 그녀의 눈물이 그녀가 바라보는 빛 속에서 프리즘의 빛깔을 자아내듯이, 그녀는 그 새롭고 무거운 슬픔을 헤치고서, 이미 저 먼 하늘에 희미하게 빛나는 무지개를 보고 있었다. 어느 이야기책 속 떠도는 공주와 착한 괴물이 난롯가에 앉아, 커틀 선장과 가엾은 플로렌스가 나눈 것처럼 이야기를 나눈다 해도—그 둘과 그리 달라 보이지는 않았을 것이다.\n\n선장은 플로렌스를 곁에 둔다는 것에 무슨 곤란이 있다거나, 그로 인해 어떤 책임이 따른다는 생각 따위로는 조금도 마음 졸이지 않았다. 덧문을 올려 닫고 문을 잠그고 나니, 그 점에 대해서는 아주 흡족했다. 설령 그녀가 형평법 법원의 피후견인이었다 한들 커틀 선장에게는 아무런 차이가 없었을 것이다. 그는 세상에서 그런 따위 고려로 마음 졸일 사람과는 가장 거리가 먼 사내였다.\n\n그리하여 선장은 파이프를 아주 편안하게 피웠고, 플로렌스와 그는 저마다의 방식으로 사색에 잠겼다. 파이프가 다 타자 둘은 차를 좀 마셨다. 그러고 나서 플로렌스는 그에게 자기가 당장 필요한 몇 가지 물건을 살 수 있는 이웃 가게로 데려다 달라고 청했다. 이미 날이 꽤 어두웠으므로 선장은 승낙했다. 다만 먼저 조심스레 밖을 내다보았으니, 그가 맥스팅거 부인에게서 숨어 지내던 시절에 늘 하던 버릇이었다. 그리고 어떤 예기치 못한 상황으로 무력에 호소할 필요가 생길 경우에 대비해 자기의 큼직한 지팡이로 무장했다.\n\n커틀 선장이 플로렌스에게 자기 팔을 내어 주고, 이삼백 야드쯤 그녀를 호위하며, 내내 환히 경계의 눈초리를 늦추지 않고, 그 대단한 조심성과 수많은 예방책으로 지나가는 모든 이의 이목을 끌면서 느낀 그 자부심은 이루 말할 수 없었다. 가게에 다다르자, 선장은 사들일 물건이 입을 옷가지인 만큼, 그 구매가 이루어지는 동안 자리를 비켜 주는 것이 섬세한 도리라고 여겼다. 그러나 그 전에 그는 양철통을 계산대 위에 올려놓고, 가게의 젊은 아가씨에게 그 안에 십사 파운드 이 실링이 들어 있노라고 알려 주며, 만일 그 액수로 자기 조카딸의 자그마한 차림새 비용을 치르기에 모자라거든—\"조카딸\"이라는 말에서 그는 더없이 의미심장한 눈길을 플로렌스에게 던지고, 거기에 영리함과 비밀스러움을 담은 손짓을 곁들였다—부디 친절히 \"크게 외쳐\" 알려 달라고, 그러면 자기 주머니에서 부족한 만큼을 채우겠노라고 청했다. 그러고는 가게에 깊은 인상을 심고 재력을 과시하는 한 수로, 짐짓 자기의 큼직한 시계를 들여다보고서, 선장은 갈고리에 입을 맞춰 조카딸에게 인사하고 창밖으로 물러났는데, 거기서 그의 큰 얼굴이 이따금 비단과 리본 사이로 안을 들여다보는 모습이 볼 만한 구경거리였으니, 플로렌스가 뒷문으로 감쪽같이 채여 가 버린 게 아닐까 하는 역력한 근심이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"고마우신 커틀 선장님,\" 플로렌스가 꾸러미 하나를 들고 나오며 말했다. 그 크기가 어찌나 작던지 일꾼 하나가 짐짝 한 더미를 따라 들고 나오리라 기대했던 선장은 크게 실망했다. \"저는 이 돈 정말 필요 없어요. 한 푼도 안 썼답니다. 저한테 제 돈이 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"아가씨,\" 당황한 선장이 둘 앞으로 곧게 뻗은 거리를 똑바로 바라보며 대꾸했다, \"이 늙은이 대신 그걸 좀 맡아 주겠수, 그렇게 좀 해 주시구려, 내가 그걸 달라고 할 때까지 말이우?\"\n\n\"제가 늘 두던 자리에 도로 넣어 두고,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"거기 보관하면 될까요?\"\n\n선장은 이 제안에 조금도 흡족해하지 않았으나, 이렇게 대답했다, \"그래, 그래, 아무 데나 두구려, 아가씨, 다시 어디서 찾을지만 알고 있다면 말이우. 그건 나한테는 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없수,\" 선장이 말했다. \"내가 진작 그걸 안 내던진 게 이상할 정도라우.\"\n\n선장은 그 순간 적잖이 풀이 죽었으나, 플로렌스의 팔이 닿자마자 다시 기운을 되찾았고, 둘은 올 때와 똑같은 예방책을 갖추고 돌아왔다. 선장은 작은 미드십맨 가게의 문을 열고, 오랜 숙련만이 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 그 민첩함으로 안으로 쑥 들어갔다. 아침에 플로렌스가 잠들어 있는 동안, 그는 리든홀 시장에서 으레 파란 우산 아래 앉아 닭을 파는 어느 나이 든 부인의 딸을 고용해, 와서 그녀의 방을 정돈하고 그녀가 필요로 하는 자잘한 시중을 들어 주게 해 두었다. 이제 이 처녀가 모습을 나타내자, 플로렌스는 자기 주변의 모든 것이, 비록 한때 자기가 집이라 불렀던 그 끔찍한 꿈에서만큼 멋지지는 않을지언정, 그만큼 편리하고 정돈되어 있음을 알았다.\n\n다시 둘만 남게 되자, 선장은 그녀에게 마른 토스트 한 조각을 먹고 향료 넣은 니거스 한 잔(그가 완벽하게 만들어 낸 것이었다)을 마셔야 한다고 우겼다. 그리고 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 다정한 말과 앞뒤도 안 맞는 인용구로 그녀를 북돋우며, 위층 침실로 데려다주었다. 그러나 그 역시 마음에 무언가 걸리는 것이 있어, 태도가 편치 못했다.\n\n\"잘 자우, 소중한 아가씨,\" 커틀 선장이 그녀의 방문 앞에서 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n플로렌스는 그의 얼굴로 입술을 들어 올려, 그에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n다른 때였다면 선장은 그녀의 그런 애정과 감사의 표시에 그만 마음의 평정을 잃었을 것이다. 그러나 지금은, 그것을 매우 절절히 느끼면서도, 앞서 드러냈던 것보다 한층 더 불안한 빛으로 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 그녀를 떠나기를 꺼리는 듯했다.\n\n\"가엾은 월터!\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은, 가엾은 월터!\" 플로렌스가 한숨지었다.\n\n\"물에 빠져 죽었지, 안 그렇수?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n플로렌스는 고개를 가로젓고 한숨지었다.\n\n\"잘 자우, 아가씨!\" 커틀 선장이 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"하느님의 가호가 있으시길, 그립고 친절하신 친구분!\"\n\n그러나 선장은 여전히 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"무슨 일 있으세요, 고마우신 커틀 선장님?\" 플로렌스가, 그때의 심정으로는 쉬이 놀라며 말했다. \"제게 하실 말씀이라도 있으세요?\"\n\n\"아가씨한테 할 말이라니!\" 선장이 당황하여 그녀의 눈길을 마주하며 대답했다. \"아니, 아니우. 내가 무슨 아가씨한테 할 말이 있겠수, 예쁜 아가씨! 설마 내가 무슨 좋은 소식을 들려줄 게 있으리라 기대하는 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"아니에요!\" 플로렌스가 고개를 가로저으며 말했다.\n\n선장은 안타까운 듯 그녀를 바라보고는 \"아니우\"라고 되풀이했다—여전히 머뭇거리며, 여전히 어쩔 줄 몰라 하면서.\n\n\"가엾은 월터!\" 선장이 말했다. \"내 월터, 내가 너를 그렇게 부르곤 했지! 늙은 솔 길스의 조카! 너를 알던 모두에게 오월의 꽃처럼 반가운 녀석! 어디로 가 버렸느냐, 용감한 녀석아? 물에 빠져 죽었지, 안 그렇수?\"\n\n이 돈호의 끝을 플로렌스에게 던지는 이 느닷없는 물음으로 맺고, 선장은 그녀에게 잘 자라고 인사하고는 계단을 내려갔다. 그동안 플로렌스는 계단 꼭대기에 남아, 그가 내려가는 길을 비추려고 촛불을 내밀고 있었다. 그는 어둠 속으로 사라졌고, 멀어지는 발소리로 미루어 보아 작은 거실로 막 접어드는 참이었는데, 그때 그의 머리와 어깨가 마치 깊은 물속에서처럼 뜻밖에 다시 불쑥 솟아올랐으니, 보아하니 \"물에 빠져 죽었지, 안 그렇수, 예쁜 아가씨?\"라는 말을 되풀이하는 것 말고는 다른 목적이라곤 없는 듯했다. 다정한 위로의 어조로 그 말을 하고 나서 그는 사라졌다.\n\n플로렌스는 자기가 그곳으로 피신함으로써, 비록 자연스러운 일이긴 했어도 무심코 자기 보호자의 마음속에 이런 연상들을 일깨워 버린 것이 무척 미안했다. 그래서 선장이 망원경과 노래책과 그 밖의 진귀한 물건들을 늘어놓은 작은 탁자 앞에 앉아, 월터와 지난날 그와 얽힌 모든 일을 떠올리다가, 거의 침대에 누워 그대로 스러져 사라지고 싶다는 마음마저 들 지경이었다. 그러나 자기가 사랑했던 죽은 이를 향한 그 외로운 그리움 속에서도, 집에 대한 생각은—돌아갈 가능성도—아직 그런 곳이 존재한다거나 자기 아버지를 품어 줄 곳이라는 떠올림도—단 한 번도 그녀의 마음속에 끼어들지 않았다. 그녀는 그 살해가 저질러지는 것을 보았다. 그토록 많은 것을 견디며 그를 소중히 품어 온 그 마지막 남은 자연스러운 모습 속에서, 그는 그녀의 마음에서 뜯겨 나가고, 짓이겨지고, 살해되었다. 그 생각이 너무도 끔찍하여, 그녀는 두 눈을 가리고, 그 행위에 대한, 혹은 그 짓을 저지른 잔인한 손에 대한 가장 작은 기억으로부터도 부르르 떨며 움츠러들었다. 만일 그녀의 사랑하는 마음이 그 일 이후에도 그의 모습을 간직할 수 있었다면, 그 마음은 산산이 부서졌을 것이다. 그러나 그럴 수가 없었다. 그리고 그 빈자리는, 자기의 부서진 조각들과 마주하는 모든 것으로부터 달아나는 사나운 두려움으로 채워졌으니—그토록 깊고, 그토록 짓밟힌 사랑의 심연이 아니고서는 결코 솟아날 수 없었을 그런 두려움이었다.\n\n그녀는 거울을 들여다볼 엄두가 나지 않았다. 가슴 위의 거무스름해져 가는 자국을 보면, 마치 자기가 무언가 사악한 것을 몸에 지니고 다니기라도 하는 듯 자기 자신이 두려워졌기 때문이다. 그녀는 황급하고 떨리는 손으로, 그것도 어둠 속에서 그것을 가렸다. 그러고는 지친 머리를 누이고, 울었다.\n\n선장은 오랫동안 잠자리에 들지 않았다. 그는 꼬박 한 시간을 가게와 작은 거실을 오락가락 거닐었고, 그 운동으로 마음을 가다듬은 듯, 진지하고 사려 깊은 얼굴로 앉아, 기도서에서 바다에서 쓰도록 정해진 기도문들을 소리 내어 읽었다. 이것들은 쉽게 처리되지 않았으니, 선량한 선장이 대단히 더디고 거친 낭독자인 데다, 어려운 단어에서 자주 멈추어, \"자, 이놈아! 기운차게!\"라거나 \"침착해, 에드워드 커틀, 침착해!\" 따위로 스스로를 북돋우곤 했는데, 그것이 어떤 난관에서 벗어나는 데 큰 효력이 있었다. 게다가 그의 안경이 시력을 크게 방해했다. 그러나 이런 장애에도 불구하고, 선장은 진심으로 골똘했던지라 그 예배문을 맨 마지막 줄까지, 그것도 진정한 감정을 담아 읽었다. 그리고 다 읽고 나서 그것을 무척 흡족해하며, 평온한 가슴과 더없이 자애로운 얼굴로 계산대 밑으로 들어가 잠자리에 들었다(다만 그 전에 위층으로 올라가 플로렌스의 방문 앞에서 귀를 기울였다).\n\n선장은 밤사이 여러 번 일어나, 자기가 맡은 아가씨가 조용히 쉬고 있는지 확인했다. 그리고 한 번은, 동틀 무렵에, 그녀가 깨어 있는 것을 알게 되었으니, 그녀가 자기 방문 가까이에서 발소리를 듣고는 그게 그인지 알려고 불렀던 것이다.\n\n\"그렇수, 아가씨,\" 선장이 으르렁대는 듯한 속삭임으로 대답했다. \"다 괜찮수, 다이아몬드 같은 아가씨?\"\n\n플로렌스는 그에게 고맙다고 하고 \"네\"라고 말했다.\n\n선장은 이토록 좋은 기회를 놓칠 수 없어, 입을 열쇠 구멍에 대고는 쉰 듯한 미풍처럼 그 사이로 외쳤다, \"가엾은 월터! 물에 빠져 죽었지, 안 그렇수?\" 그러고 나서 그는 물러나, 다시 잠자리에 들어 일곱 시까지 잤다.\n\n그날 하루 종일 그는 그 불안하고 어쩔 줄 모르는 태도에서 벗어나지 못했다. 다만 플로렌스는 작은 거실에서 바느질에 여념이 없어, 전날보다는 한결 차분하고 평온했다. 그녀가 일감에서 눈을 들 때면 거의 언제나, 선장이 자기를 바라보며 사려 깊게 턱을 쓰다듬고 있는 것이 눈에 띄었다. 그리고 그는 무언가 매우 은밀한 이야기라도 하려는 듯 안락의자를 그녀에게 바싹 끌어당겼다가, 어떻게 말문을 떼야 할지 마음을 정하지 못하겠다는 듯 다시 끌어다 멀어지기를 어찌나 자주 했던지, 하루가 지나는 동안 그는 그 위태로운 작은 배를 타고 거실을 완전히 한 바퀴 돌았으며, 매우 곤란한 처지에 빠진 채 한 번 이상이나 징두리 판자나 벽장 문에 부딪쳐 좌초하기도 했다.\n\n마침내 커틀 선장이 플로렌스 곁에 닻을 제대로 내리고 조금이라도 조리 있게 이야기하기 시작한 것은 땅거미가 질 무렵이 되어서였다. 그러나 난롯불 빛이 그 작은 방의 벽과 천장에, 그리고 탁자 위에 늘어놓은 찻쟁반과 찻잔과 받침에, 그리고 불꽃을 향해 돌린 그녀의 고요한 얼굴에 어리어, 그 빛이 그녀의 눈에 그렁그렁한 눈물에 비치고 있을 때, 선장은 긴 침묵을 이렇게 깨뜨렸다.\n\n\"아가씨는 바다에 나가 본 적이 한 번도 없지요, 우리 아가씨?\"\n\n\"네,\" 플로렌스가 대답했다.\n\"아아,\" 선장이 경건하게 말했다, \"그건 전능하신 원소라우. 깊은 바다에는 경이가 가득하다우, 예쁜 아가씨. 바람이 울부짖고 파도가 굽이칠 때 그걸 떠올려 보구려. 폭풍우 치는 밤이 어찌나 캄캄한지,\" 선장이 엄숙하게 갈고리를 치켜들며 말했다, \"번쩍이는 번개가 그걸 드러내 줄 때 말고는 눈앞에 든 제 손도 안 보일 지경일 때 그걸 떠올려 보구려. 그리고 폭풍과 어둠을 뚫고 달리고, 달리고, 또 달릴 때, 마치 끝없는 세상을 향해 곧장 머리부터 들이박으며 달려가는 것 같을 때, 영원무궁토록, 아멘, 그리고 발견될 때는 그걸 기록으로 남겨 둘지어다. 바로 그런 때라우, 우리 아름다운 아가씨, 사내가 (먼저 책을 한번 죽 훑어보고서) 자기 동료한테 이렇게 말할 만한 때가 말이우. '북서풍이 거세게 부는구나, 빌. 들어 봐, 지금 저 으르렁대는 소리 안 들리나! 주여 저들을 도우소서, 지금 뭍에 있는 가엾은 이들이 다 어찌나 측은한지!'\" 바다의 공포에 특히 들어맞는 이 인용구를 선장은 더없이 인상 깊은 태도로 읊었고, 우렁찬 \"대기하라!\"로 끝맺었다.\n\n\"무서운 폭풍을 겪어 보신 적 있으세요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"아 그럼이지, 아가씨, 나도 궂은 날씨라면 겪을 만큼 겪었수,\" 선장이 떨리는 손으로 머리를 훔치며 말했다, \"그리고 험한 일도 당할 만큼 당했고. 하지만—하지만 내가 말하려던 건 내 얘기가 아니라우. 우리 사랑하는 녀석,\" 그녀에게 더 가까이 다가서며, \"월터, 그 귀한 녀석, 물에 빠져 죽은.\"\n\n선장이 어찌나 떨리는 목소리로 말하고, 어찌나 창백하고 동요된 얼굴로 플로렌스를 바라보았던지, 그녀는 겁에 질려 그의 손에 매달렸다.\n\n\"얼굴이 달라지셨어요,\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다. \"한순간에 변하셨어요. 무슨 일이세요? 고마우신 커틀 선장님, 선장님을 보니 등골이 서늘해져요!\"\n\n\"아니! 아가씨,\" 선장이 손으로 그녀를 부축하며 대답했다, \"당황하지 마우. 아니, 아니우! 다 괜찮수, 다 괜찮수, 우리 아가씨. 내가 말하던 대로—월터—그 애가—그 애가 물에 빠져 죽었지. 안 그렇수?\"\n\n플로렌스는 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 핏기가 들었다 가셨다 했고, 그녀는 한 손을 가슴에 얹었다.\n\n\"깊은 바다에는 위험과 위난이 도사리고 있다우, 우리 아름다운 아가씨,\" 선장이 말했다. \"그리고 수많은 용감한 배와, 수많고 수많은 대담한 가슴 위로, 그 은밀한 바닷물이 덮어 버리고는, 아무 이야기도 들려준 적이 없다우. 하지만 깊은 바다에는 살아남는 일도 있는 법이라우, 그래서 때로는 스무 명 가운데 한 사람이—아! 어쩌면 백 명 가운데 한 사람이, 예쁜 아가씨—하느님의 자비로 구원을 받아, 죽은 것으로 단념된 끝에 집으로 돌아와, 모두가 죽었더라는 이야기를 전하기도 한다우. 내가—내가 이런 종류의 이야기를 하나 안다우, 내 마음의 기쁨인 아가씨,\" 선장이 더듬거리며 말했다, \"언젠가 내가 들은 이야기지. 그리고 이런 화제로 접어든 김에, 아가씨하고 나하고 난롯가에 단둘이 앉아 있으니, 어쩌면 내가 그 이야기를 들려주는 걸 듣고 싶을지도 모르겠수. 그러겠수, 아가씨?\"\n\n플로렌스는 자기로서도 억누르거나 이해할 수 없는 동요에 떨면서, 자기도 모르게 그의 눈길을 따라갔다. 그 눈길은 그녀의 등 뒤로, 등불이 켜져 있는 가게 쪽을 향했다. 그녀가 머리를 돌리는 순간, 선장이 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 손을 내밀어 가로막았다.\n\n\"저기엔 아무것도 없수, 우리 아름다운 아가씨,\" 선장이 말했다. \"저쪽은 보지 마우.\"\n\n\"왜 안 되나요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n선장은 저쪽은 침침하다느니, 난롯불이 더 정겹다느니 하고 무어라 중얼거렸다. 그는 지금까지 열려 있던 문을 빠끔히 닫고, 다시 자리에 앉았다. 플로렌스는 눈으로 그를 좇으며, 그의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"그 이야기는 어떤 배에 관한 것이었수, 아가씨,\" 선장이 입을 뗐다, \"런던 항을 떠나, 순풍을 받고 좋은 날씨에, 어디로 향하던 배냐면—당황하지 마우, 아가씨, 그 배는 그저 바깥으로 나가는 길이었수, 예쁜 아가씨, 그저 바깥으로 나가는 길이었다우!\"\n\n플로렌스의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정이 선장을 불안하게 했다. 선장 자신도 무척 달아오르고 허둥대어, 그녀 못지않은 동요를 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"계속할까요, 아름다운 아가씨?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 네, 어서요!\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다.\n\n선장은 목구멍에 걸린 무언가를 삼키기라도 하려는 듯 꿀꺽 침을 삼키고, 초조하게 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그 불운한 배는 바다 한복판에서 그런 험악한 날씨를 만났는데, 우리 사랑하는 아가씨, 그런 날씨는 이십 년에 한 번도 불지 않는 거라우. 뭍에서는 숲을 뿌리째 뽑고 마을을 쓰러뜨리는 폭풍이 일었고, 그 위도의 바다에서는 여태껏 진수된 그 어떤 튼튼한 배도 살아남지 못할 강풍이 일었다우. 날이면 날마다 그 불운한 배는 장하게도 버티며 용감히 제 도리를 다했다고 들었수, 예쁜 아가씨. 하지만 거의 한 방에 뱃전이 부서지고, 돛대와 키가 깎여 나가고, 으뜸가는 일꾼이 바다로 휩쓸려 가고, 자비라곤 없이 점점 더 거세게 불어 대는 폭풍의 처분에 내맡겨졌다우. 그동안 파도는 배 위로 들이치고, 배를 두들겨 부수고, 천둥 치듯 들이닥칠 때마다 조개껍데기처럼 배를 박살 냈지. 굽이쳐 밀려가는 산더미 같은 물마다 그 위의 검은 점 하나하나가 그 배의 목숨 한 조각이거나 살아 있는 사람 하나였다우. 그렇게 배는 산산조각이 났수, 아름다운 아가씨, 그 배에 탔던 이들의 무덤 위에는 결코 풀 한 포기 돋지 않을 거라우.\"\n\n\"모두 죽은 건 아니었죠!\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다. \"몇 사람은 구조됐죠!—한 사람이라도요?\"\n\n\"그 불운한 배에는,\" 선장이 의자에서 일어나, 엄청난 기운과 환희로 주먹을 불끈 쥐며 말했다, \"한 젊은이가 있었다우, 늠름한 젊은이가—내가 전해 듣기로는—소년 시절에 난파선에서의 용감한 행적에 관해 읽고 이야기하기를 좋아하던—내가 직접 들었수! 내가 직접 들었다니까!—그래서 위기의 순간에 그걸 기억해 냈지. 가장 억세고 노련한 뱃사람들이 쓰러져 나뒹굴 때, 그는 의연하고 명랑했다우. 그에게 용기를 준 건 뭍에 좋아하고 사랑할 사람이 없어서가 아니라, 바로 그의 타고난 마음이었수. 나는 그게 그 애 얼굴에 서린 걸 봤다우, 그 애가 어린아이에 지나지 않았을 때부터—아, 여러 번이나!—그땐 나는 그저 그 애가 잘생겨서 그런 줄로만 알았지, 하느님 그 애를 축복하소서!\"\n\n\"그래서 그는 구조됐나요!\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다. \"구조됐나요!\"\n\n\"그 용감한 젊은이는,\" 선장이 말했다,—\"나를 보구려, 예쁜 아가씨! 뒤돌아보지 말고—\"\n\n플로렌스는 \"왜 안 되나요?\"라고 되풀이할 힘조차 거의 없었다.\n\n\"저기엔 아무것도 없으니까 그렇수, 우리 아가씨,\" 선장이 말했다. \"당황하지 마우, 예쁜 사람! 그러지 마우, 우리 모두에게 소중했던 월터를 봐서라도! 그 젊은이는,\" 선장이 말했다, \"가장 뛰어난 이들과 함께 일하고, 겁먹은 이들 곁을 지키고, 불평 한마디 두려운 기색 하나 내비치지 않고, 모두에게 기운을 북돋아 주어, 마치 자기가 제독이라도 되는 양 그들이 그를 떠받들게 했다우—그 젊은이는, 이등 항해사와 선원 한 사람과 함께, 그 배에 올랐던 그 모든 뛰는 가슴 가운데 유일하게 살아남은 자가 되어—난파선 조각에 몸을 묶은 채, 폭풍우 치는 바다 위를 떠돌았다우.\"\n\n\"그들은 구조됐나요?\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"여러 날 여러 밤을 그들은 그 끝없는 물 위를 떠돌았수,\" 선장이 말했다, \"그러다 마침내—아니! 그쪽은 보지 마우, 예쁜 아가씨!—돛단배 한 척이 그들을 향해 다가왔고, 주님의 자비로 그들은 배에 실렸다우. 둘은 산 채로, 하나는 죽은 채로.\"\n\n\"그중 누가 죽었나요?\" 플로렌스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 말하는 그 젊은이는 아니라우,\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"하느님 감사합니다! 오, 하느님 감사합니다!\"\n\n\"아멘!\" 선장이 황급히 응수했다. \"당황하지 마우! 잠깐만 더, 아가씨! 굳센 마음으로!—그 배를 타고 그들은 긴 항해를 했수, 해도를 가로질러 곧장 멀리 나아갔지(어디에도 들를 데가 없었으니까). 그리고 그 항해 중에 그와 함께 구조됐던 그 선원은 죽었수. 하지만 그는 목숨을 건졌고, 그리고—\"\n\n선장은 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 모른 채, 빵 덩어리에서 한 조각을 잘라 자기 갈고리(그것이 그의 평소 토스트 굽는 포크였다)에 꿰어, 이제 그것을 난롯불에 갖다 대고 있었다. 얼굴에 큰 감정을 담고 플로렌스의 등 뒤를 바라보면서, 빵이 땔감처럼 활활 타들어 가도록 내버려 둔 채로.\n\n\"목숨을 건졌고,\" 플로렌스가 되풀이했다, \"그리고요—?\"\n\n\"그리고 그 배를 타고 집으로 돌아왔수,\" 선장이 여전히 같은 방향을 바라보며 말했다, \"그리고—겁먹지 마우, 예쁜 아가씨—그리고 뭍에 내렸수. 그리고 어느 날 아침, 자기 친구들이 자기를 물에 빠져 죽은 줄로 알 거란 걸 알기에, 슬쩍 살펴보려고 조심스레 제 집 문 앞으로 다가왔다가, 뜻밖의—그것에 그만 비켜 달아났는데—\"\n\n\"개가 뜻밖에 짖는 소리에요?\" 플로렌스가 재빨리 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇수,\" 선장이 우렁차게 외쳤다. \"침착하우, 우리 아가씨! 용기를 내우! 아직 뒤돌아보지 말고. 저길 보우! 벽 위를!\"\n\n그녀 바로 가까이 벽 위에 한 사내의 그림자가 있었다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 둘러보았고, 날카로운 외마디 비명과 함께, 자기 등 뒤에 선 월터 게이를 보았다!\n\n그녀는 그를 오라버니로밖에, 무덤에서 구해 낸 오라버니로밖에, 난파에서 살아남아 자기 곁에 있는 오라버니로밖에 생각하지 못했다. 그래서 그의 품으로 달려들었다. 온 세상에서 그가 자기의 희망, 위안, 피난처, 천생의 보호자인 것만 같았다. \"월터를 잘 보살펴 다오, 나는 월터를 좋아했단다!\" 그렇게 말하던 그 애처로운 목소리에 대한 그리운 기억이, 밤중의 음악처럼 그녀의 영혼으로 밀려들었다. \"오, 어서 와요, 그리운 월터! 이 상처 입은 가슴으로 어서 와요!\" 그녀는 그 말을 입 밖에 낼 수는 없었으나 그 말을 느꼈고, 순결한 포옹으로 그를 끌어안았다.\n\n커틀 선장은 일종의 정신착란에 빠져, 갈고리에 꿰인 새까매진 토스트로 자기 머리를 닦으려 들었다. 그러나 그것이 그 목적에는 영 어울리지 않는 물건임을 알고는, 그것을 번들거리는 모자 안에 넣고, 그 번들거리는 모자를 간신히 머리에 쓰고, 「사랑스러운 페그」 한 소절을 불러 보려다 첫 마디에서 막혀 버리고는, 가게로 물러갔다가, 이내 얼굴이 온통 벌게지고 시커멓게 얼룩진 채, 셔츠 깃의 풀기는 완전히 빠져 버린 채로 부랴부랴 돌아와, 이 말을 했다.\n\n\"월터, 이놈아, 여기 내가 너한테 공동으로 넘겨주고 싶은 자그마한 재산이 좀 있다!\"\n\n선장은 황급히 큼직한 시계와 찻숟가락들과 설탕 집게와 양철통을 꺼내 탁자 위에 늘어놓고는, 자기 큰 손으로 그것들을 쓸어 월터의 모자 안에 담았다. 그러나 그 별난 튼튼한 금고를 월터에게 건네면서, 그는 다시금 어찌나 북받쳤던지, 또 한 번 가게로 물러나야만 했고, 처음 물러났을 때보다 더 오랫동안 모습을 감추었다.\n\n그러나 월터가 그를 찾아내어 데려왔다. 그러자 선장의 큰 걱정거리는, 플로렌스가 이 새로운 충격으로 탈이 나지 않을까 하는 것이었다. 그것이 어찌나 진지하게 마음에 걸렸던지, 그는 아주 멀쩡하게 제정신을 차려, 앞으로 며칠간은 월터의 모험에 대해 더 이상 언급하는 것을 단호히 금했다. 그러고 나서 커틀 선장은 모자 속 토스트를 치워 낼 만큼은 충분히 마음을 가다듬고 찻쟁반 앞 자기 자리에 앉았다. 그러나 한쪽에서는 월터가 자기 어깨를 잡고, 다른 한쪽에서는 플로렌스가 눈물 어린 축하의 말을 속삭이는 것을 알아채자, 선장은 또 별안간 줄행랑을 쳐, 족히 십 분간 행방이 묘연했다.\n\n그러나 평생토록 선장의 얼굴이, 마침내 찻쟁반 앞에 가만히 앉아 플로렌스에게서 월터로, 월터에게서 플로렌스로 눈길을 옮길 때만큼 환히 빛나고 반들거린 적은 없었다. 그리고 이 효과는, 그가 지난 반 시간 동안 외투 소맷자락으로 자기 얼굴에 베푼 엄청난 양의 광내기로 생겨난 것도, 조금이라도 더해진 것도 아니었다. 그것은 오로지 그의 내면의 감정이 빚어낸 효과였다. 선장의 내면에는 영광과 기쁨이 있어, 그것이 그의 얼굴 전체로 번져 거기에 완벽한 광휘를 이루어 놓았다.\n\n선장이, 되찾은 자기 녀석의 햇볕에 그을린 뺨과 용감한 눈을 바라보며 느낀 자부심, 그 젊음의 너그러운 열정과 그 모든 솔직하고 희망찬 자질이 그 싱그럽고 건강한 태도와 그 열의에 찬 얼굴에 다시 한번 빛나는 것을 바라보며 느낀 자부심만으로도, 그의 얼굴에 이 빛 비슷한 것이 지펴졌을 것이다. 그가 플로렌스에게로 눈길을 돌릴 때의 그 감탄과 공감만으로도—그녀의 아름다움과 우아함과 순결함은 그 자신보다 더 진실하고 더 열성적인 옹호자를 얻을 수 없었을 것인데—그에게 똑같은 영향을 미쳤을 것이다. 그러나 그가 사방에 두른 그 빛의 충만함은, 오직 그 둘을 함께 바라보는 가운데, 그리고 그 결합에서 솟아나 반짝이며 빛을 내며 그의 머릿속으로 들어와 그 둘레를 춤추던 그 모든 공상 속에서만 자라날 수 있었던 것이다.\n\n가엾은 늙은 솔 아저씨에 대해 둘이 어떻게 이야기를 나누고, 그의 실종에 관한 자잘한 사정 하나하나에 어떻게 머물렀는지. 그 노인의 부재와 플로렌스의 불행으로 그들의 기쁨이 어떻게 누그러졌는지. 다시 짖어 댈까 봐 선장이 얼마 전 위층으로 꾀어 올려 두었던 디오게네스를 어떻게 풀어 주었는지—그 모든 것을 선장은, 비록 끊임없이 안절부절못하며 가게로 몇 번이고 더 짧게 뛰어들기는 했어도, 충분히 알아들었다. 그러나 그는, 월터가 플로렌스를 말하자면 새롭고 아득히 먼 자리에서 바라보고 있다는 것을, 그의 눈이 그 사랑스러운 얼굴을 자주 찾으면서도 그 누이다운 애정의 솔직한 눈길과는 좀처럼 마주치지 못하고, 그녀의 눈이 그를 향해 들릴 때면 슬그머니 거두어진다는 것을—자기 곁에 앉은 것이 월터의 유령이라고 믿지 않은 것만큼이나—꿈에도 알아채지 못했다. 그는 그 둘이 그 젊음과 아름다움 속에 함께 있는 것을 보았고, 그들의 어린 시절 이야기를 알고 있었으며, 그의 그 큰 푸른 조끼 아래에는 그런 한 쌍에 대한 감탄과 그들이 다시 만나게 된 것에 대한 감사 말고는 다른 무엇이 들어앉을 자리가 한 치도 없었다.\n\n둘은 이렇게 밤이 깊도록 앉아 있었다. 선장은 일주일이라도 이렇게 앉아 있어 흡족했을 것이다. 그러나 월터가 그날 밤 작별을 고하려고 일어섰다.\n\n\"가시려고요, 월터!\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"어디로요?\"\n\n\"그 앤 당분간 브로글리네 가게에 가서 해먹을 매단다우, 아가씨,\" 커틀 선장이 말했다. \"부르면 들리는 데에 있는 거지요, 내 마음의 기쁨인 아가씨.\"\n\n\"제가 당신을 떠나게 만든 거예요, 월터,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"당신 자리에 집 없는 누이가 들어앉은 거니까요.\"\n\n\"그리운 돔비 아가씨,\" 월터가 머뭇거리며 대답했다—\"이렇게 부르는 게 너무 외람된 게 아니라면요!—\"\n\n\"월터!\" 그녀가 놀라서 소리쳤다.\n\n\"—제가 당신을 보고 당신과 이야기할 수 있도록 허락받은 것보다 저를 더 행복하게 만들 수 있는 것이 있다면, 그건 제가 이 땅 위에서 당신께 단 한순간이라도 도움이 될 수 있는 무슨 방도가 있다는 것을 발견하는 일이 아닐까요! 당신을 위해서라면 제가 어디인들 못 가겠으며, 무엇인들 못 하겠습니까?\"\n\n그녀는 미소 지으며 그를 오라버니라 불렀다.\n\n\"당신은 너무 달라졌어요,\" 월터가 말했다—\n\n\"제가 달라졌다고요!\" 그녀가 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"—제게요,\" 월터가 마치 소리 내어 생각하는 듯 부드럽게 말했다, \"제게 달라졌어요. 제가 떠날 땐 당신은 그런 어린아이였는데, 이제 보니 당신은—오! 너무도 다른 무언가가—\"\n\n\"하지만 당신의 누이잖아요, 월터. 우리가 헤어질 때 서로에게 무엇을 약속했는지 잊지 않으셨죠?\"\n\n\"잊다니요!\" 그러나 그는 더 이상 말하지 않았다.\n\n\"그리고 설령 잊으셨다 해도—설령 고난과 위험이 그것을 당신 마음에서 몰아냈다 해도—그렇지 않았지만요—당신은 이제 그것을 기억해 내실 거예요, 월터, 제가 이렇게 가난하고 버림받아, 이곳 말고는 집도 없고, 제 말을 들어 주는 이 두 분 말고는 벗도 없는 처지가 된 것을 보셨으니!\"\n\n\"기억해 내고말고요! 하느님이 아십니다, 그러고말고요!\" 월터가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 월터,\" 플로렌스가 흐느낌과 눈물 사이로 외쳤다. \"그리운 오라버니! 저에게 이 세상을 헤쳐 갈 무슨 길을 좀 알려 주세요—제가 혼자 걸어가, 그 안에서 애써 일하며, 때때로 당신을 누이를 지켜 주고 돌봐 줄 사람으로 떠올릴 수 있는, 어떤 누추한 오솔길이라도요! 오, 저를 도와주세요, 월터, 저는 도움이 너무도 절실하답니다!\"\n\n\"돔비 아가씨! 플로렌스! 당신을 돕기 위해서라면 저는 죽을 수도 있어요. 하지만 당신의 가족은 자존심 높고 부유한 분들이에요. 당신의 아버님께서—\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 돼요! 월터!\" 그녀가 비명을 지르며, 그를 그 자리에 못 박아 버린 공포의 자세로 두 손을 들어 머리를 감쌌다. \"그 말은 하지 마세요!\"\n\n그는 그 시각 이후로, 그녀가 그 이름에서 자기를 막아서던 그 목소리와 그 표정을 결코 잊지 못했다. 그는 자기가 백 년을 산다 해도 결코 그것을 잊지 못하리란 것을 느꼈다.\n\n어디든지—어디든 좋으나—다만 집만은 결코 안 된다! 모든 것이 지나갔고, 모든 것이 사라졌고, 모든 것이 잃어졌고, 산산이 부서졌다! 그녀가 입 밖에 내지 못한 그 멸시와 고통의 온 내력이 그 외침과 표정 속에 담겨 있었다. 그리고 그는 자기가 결코 그것을 잊지 못하리란 것을 느꼈고, 정말로 결코 잊지 못했다.\n\n그녀는 그 부드러운 얼굴을 선장의 어깨에 기대고, 자기가 어떻게, 왜 도망쳐 나왔는지를 들려주었다. 월터는 경외감에 잠겨 생각했다. 만일 그녀가 그렇게 이야기하며 흘린 슬픔의 눈물 한 방울 한 방울이, 그녀가 결코 이름 부르지도 탓하지도 않는 그 사람의 머리 위에 떨어지는 저주였더라면, 차라리 그 사람에게는 그편이, 그토록 강하고 위력 있는 사랑으로부터 버림받는 것보다는 나았으리라고.\n\n\"자, 됐수, 우리 귀한 아가씨!\" 그녀가 말을 그치자 선장이 말했다. 그녀가 이야기하는 동안 선장은 깊은 주의를 기울였으니, 번들거리는 모자를 온통 비뚜름하게 쓰고 입을 떡 벌린 채 귀를 기울이고 있었던 것이다. \"그쳐요, 그쳐요, 내 눈물아! 월터, 이 사랑하는 녀석아, 오늘 밤은 물러가서, 이 예쁜 아가씨는 이 늙은이한테 맡겨 다오!\"\n\n월터는 그녀의 손을 자기 두 손으로 잡아, 입술로 가져가 입을 맞추었다. 이제 그는 그녀가 정말로 집 없이 떠도는 도망자임을 알았다. 그러나 그렇기에 그에게는 더욱 귀하면서도, 그녀가 마땅히 누릴 신분의 온갖 부와 자긍심 속에 있을 때보다도 더 멀게 느껴졌으니, 소년 시절의 꿈속에서 그를 어질어질하게 만들었던 그 높은 곳에 있을 때보다도 더 아득해 보였다.\n\n그런 사색에 조금도 시달리지 않는 커틀 선장은, 플로렌스를 그녀의 방까지 호위해 주고, 그녀의 방문 밖 그 마법에 걸린 땅에서—그에게는 정말로 그러했으니—이따금씩 망을 보다가, 마음이 충분히 놓일 만큼 그녀에 대해 안심이 된 후에야 계산대 밑으로 들어가 잠자리에 들었다. 그러려고 망보기를 단념하면서도, 그는 황홀한 마음에 또 한 번 열쇠 구멍에 대고 \"물에 빠져 죽었지. 안 그렇수, 예쁜 아가씨?\"라고 외치지 않을 수 없었고—혹은 아래층으로 내려가서는 「사랑스러운 페그」의 그 소절을 또 한 번 시도해 보지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그것이 어쩐지 목구멍에 걸려 도무지 제대로 부를 수가 없었다. 그래서 그는 잠자리에 들었고, 늙은 솔 길스가 맥스팅거 부인과 결혼하여, 그 부인에게 어느 비밀의 방에 갇힌 채 변변찮은 식량으로 연명하는 포로 신세가 되어 있는 꿈을 꾸었다.\n\n# CHAPTER L. Mr Toots’s Complaint\n\n나무로 만든 항해사 가게의 위층에는 빈방이 하나 있었는데, 옛날에는 월터의 침실이었던 곳이다. 월터는 아침 일찍 선장을 깨워, 작은 응접실에서 그 방을 가장 잘 꾸며 줄 만한 가구를 그리로 옮기자고 제안했다. 그러면 플로렌스가 일어났을 때 그 방을 차지할 수 있을 터였다. 그런 일로 얼굴이 새빨개지고 숨이 차도록 애쓰는 것보다 커틀 선장에게 더 기쁜 일은 없었으므로, 그는 (자기 말마따나) 의욕을 가지고 달려들었고, 두어 시간 만에 이 다락방은 일종의 육상 선실로 탈바꿈했다. 응접실에서 가장 멋진 세간들로 꾸며졌는데, 거기에는 그 타타르 프리깃함마저 포함되어 있었으니, 선장은 그것을 굴뚝 선반 위에 걸어 놓고는 어찌나 기뻐했던지, 그 뒤 반 시간 동안은 감탄에 넋을 잃은 채 그것을 보며 뒷걸음질 치는 일밖에 아무것도 하지 못했다.\n\n선장은 월터가 아무리 설득해도 큰 시계의 태엽을 감거나, 차통을 도로 받거나, 설탕집게와 찻숟가락에 손을 대려 하지 않았다. \"아니, 아니다, 얘야.\" 그런 권유가 있을 때마다 선장은 한결같이 대답했다. \"내가 그 자그마한 재산을 공동 명의로 양도해 버렸느니라.\" 이 말을 그는 더없는 진지함과 엄숙함을 담아 되풀이했는데, 분명 그 말에 의회 법령과 같은 효력이 있다고 믿는 듯했고, 또 자기가 무슨 새로운 소유권 인정으로 스스로를 옭아매지 않는 한 그런 양도 형식에는 흠을 찾을 수 없으리라고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n새 배치의 이점은, 플로렌스에게 더 큰 은둔처를 제공해 주는 것 외에도, 항해사 인형을 평소의 관측 위치로 되돌려 놓을 수 있고, 또 가게 덧문도 내릴 수 있게 된 것이었다. 후자의 의식에는, 아무것도 모르는 선장이 아무리 그것을 대수롭지 않게 여겼다 하더라도, 전혀 쓸데없는 일은 아니었다. 전날, 덧문이 닫힌 채로 있는 바람에 동네에 어찌나 큰 소동이 일었던지, 이 계기 제작자의 집은 유난히 많은 대중의 주목을 받는 영광을 누렸고, 해 뜰 때부터 해 질 때까지 아무 때나 길 건너편에서 굶주린 구경꾼들 무리가 뚫어지게 쳐다보았던 것이다. 한가한 자들과 부랑자들은 선장의 운명에 특히 흥미를 느꼈다. 그들은 끊임없이 진창에 엎드려 가게 창문 아래 지하실 쇠창살에 눈을 갖다 대고는, 그가 한쪽 구석에 매달려 있는데 그 외투 자락이 보인다는 공상으로 상상을 즐겼다. 그러나 이렇게 그를 자리매김하는 것은 반대편 일파에게 거세게 반박당했으니, 이들은 그가 계단에서 망치에 맞아 살해되어 누워 있다는 의견이었다. 그러니 이런 소문들의 주인공이 아침 일찍 자기 가게 문 앞에 마치 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 멀쩡하고 정정하게 서 있는 것이 발견되었을 때, 다소간의 불만을 일으키지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 구역의 교구 관리는 야심 찬 성격의 사내였는데, 문을 부수고 들어가는 자리에 참석하여 정복 차림으로 검시관 앞에서 증언하는 영예를 기대하고 있던 터라, 길 건너 이웃에게 저 번들거리는 모자를 쓴 작자가 여기서 그런 짓을 안 하는 게 좋을 거라고—무엇을 안 하는 게 좋다는 건지는 더 구체적으로 언급하지 않은 채—말하기까지 했고, 더 나아가 자기, 곧 교구 관리가 그자를 눈여겨보고 있겠노라고 했다.\n\n\"커틀 선장님,\" 월터가 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 말했다. 두 사람이 일을 끝낸 뒤 가게 문 앞에 서서 쉬며, 그 낯익은 옛 거리를 내려다보고 있을 때였다. 아직 이른 아침이었다. \"그 오랜 시간 동안 솔 외삼촌 소식이 전혀 없었군요!\"\n\n\"전혀 없었지, 얘야.\" 선장이 고개를 저으며 대답했다.\n\n\"저를 찾으러 가신 거예요, 친절하고 다정한 노인이.\" 월터가 말했다. \"그런데도 선장님께 편지 한 장 안 보내시다니! 하지만 왜 안 보내실까요? 선장님이 제게 주신 이 봉투에서, 외삼촌은 사실상 이렇게 말씀하셨잖아요.\" 그러며 주머니에서 종이를 꺼냈는데, 그것은 식견 높은 번스비가 있는 자리에서 개봉되었던 것이다. \"이 봉투를 열기 전까지 자기 소식을 전혀 듣지 못한다면, 자기가 죽은 줄로 믿으셔도 좋다고요. 천만에! 하지만 외삼촌이 돌아가셨다 해도 선장님은 그 소식을 들으셨을 거예요! 분명 누군가가, 외삼촌이 직접 못 쓰셨다면 그분 부탁으로 대신 편지를 써서, '아무 날, 제 집에서' 혹은 '제 보살핌 아래' 혹은 그 비슷하게, '런던의 솔로몬 길스 씨가 세상을 떠났으며, 이것이 그분의 마지막 기념물이요 마지막 당부입니다'라고 전했을 거예요.\"\n\n선장은 전에 이런 명료한 개연성의 높이에 올라본 적이 없었으므로, 그것이 열어 보이는 드넓은 전망에 크게 감명받아, 사려 깊게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"잘 말했다, 얘야. 아주 잘 말했어.\"\n\n\"저는 이 일을 곰곰이 생각해 봤어요, 아니, 적어도,\" 월터가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"잠 못 이룬 밤 내내 이런저런 것을 생각해 봤는데, 커틀 선장님, 저는 우리 솔 외삼촌(부디 축복받으시길!)이 살아 계시고, 또 돌아오시리라고 믿지 않을 수가 없어요. 외삼촌이 떠나신 것 자체는 그리 이상하게 여기지 않아요. 그분 성격에 늘 있던 그 신비로운 기질이라든가, 저에 대한 그 깊은 애정—그분의 다른 모든 생애의 고려가 그 앞에서는 아무것도 아니게 되어 버리던 그 애정, 그분에게서 더없이 훌륭한 아버지를 얻었던 저보다 그것을 더 잘 알 사람은 없겠지요—\" 여기서 월터의 목소리는 흐릿하고 잠겨 들었고, 그는 거리를 따라 시선을 돌렸다. \"그런 것을 빼고 보더라도, 제 말은, 저는 종종 읽고 들었어요. 가까이 사랑하는 친척이 바다에서 난파당한 것으로 여겨질 때, 사람들이 그 실종된 배의 소식이 닿을 만한 해변 그곳으로 내려가 살러 간다는 것을요. 비록 다른 곳보다 겨우 한두 시간 먼저 소식을 들을 뿐인데도 말이에요. 심지어 그 배가 향하던 목적지까지 그 항로를 따라가는 사람들도 있다지요, 마치 자기가 가면 소식이 생겨나기라도 할 것처럼요. 저도 그런 일이라면 남들 못지않게, 아니 어쩌면 많은 이들보다 더 빨리 할 것 같아요. 하지만 외삼촌이 그토록 분명히 편지하시려 했으면서 왜 선장님께 안 쓰셨는지, 또 어떻게 타지에서 돌아가셨는데 선장님이 다른 누군가의 손을 통해서도 그걸 모르실 수 있는지, 그건 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없어요.\"\n\n커틀 선장은 고개를 저으며, 잭 번스비 자신도 그것을 헤아리지 못했다고, 게다가 그 사람은 꽤 팽팽한 의견을 내놓을 줄 아는 인물이라고 말했다.\n\n\"만약 외삼촌이 분별없는 젊은이여서, 흥겨운 패거리한테 꾀여 어느 술집으로 끌려가, 몸에 지닌 돈푼 때문에 처치당할 만한 사람이었다면,\" 월터가 말했다. \"아니면 무모한 뱃사람이어서, 두세 달 치 봉급을 주머니에 넣고 상륙했더라면, 저는 그분이 사라져 흔적도 없이 떠나신 걸 이해할 수 있을 거예요. 하지만 그분이 그런 분이었으니까—또 지금도 그러시길 바라니까—저는 그걸 믿을 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"월터, 얘야,\" 선장이 줄곧 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 월터를 안타까운 눈으로 바라보며 물었다. \"그럼 너는 이 일을 어떻게 보느냐?\"\n\n\"커틀 선장님,\" 월터가 대답했다. \"저도 어떻게 봐야 할지 모르겠어요. 외삼촌이 편지를 전혀 안 쓰셨다고 봐야겠지요! 그건 의심할 여지가 없겠죠?\"\n\n\"솔 길스가 편지를 썼다 치면, 얘야,\" 선장이 따지듯 대답했다. \"그 편지는 어디 있겠느냐?\"\n\n\"외삼촌이 그 편지를 누군가 개인의 손에 맡기셨다고 쳐 봐요,\" 월터가 의견을 냈다. \"그런데 그것이 잊혔거나, 무심히 내던져졌거나, 분실되었다고요. 저로서는 그게 다른 경우보다 더 그럴듯하기까지 해요. 한마디로, 커틀 선장님, 저는 그 다른 경우는 떠올리는 것조차 견딜 수 없을 뿐만 아니라, 떠올릴 수도 없고, 떠올리지도 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"희망이란다, 알겠느냐, 월터,\" 선장이 슬기롭게 말했다. \"희망 말이다. 너를 살아 움직이게 하는 게 바로 그거야. 희망은 부표(浮標)란다, 네 『꼬마 가수』 감상 부문을 샅샅이 뒤지면 나오는 그것 말이다. 하지만 얘야, 다른 부표들과 마찬가지로 그건 떠 있기만 할 뿐, 어디로 조종할 수는 없는 거란다. 희망의 선수상(船首像) 옆에는,\" 선장이 말했다. \"닻도 하나 있지. 그렇지만 닻을 내려 박을 바닥을 찾지 못한다면 닻을 가진들 무슨 소용이겠느냐?\"\n\n커틀 선장이 이 말을 한 것은 자기 본연의 모습에서라기보다는, 슬기로운 시민이자 가장(家長)으로서 경험 없는 젊은이에게 지혜의 창고에서 한 조각을 나누어 주어야 한다는 자격에서였다. 사실 그가 이 말을 하는 동안 그의 얼굴은 월터에게서 옮아온 새로운 희망으로 환히 빛나고 있었다. 그리고 그는 적절하게도 월터의 등을 한 대 치며, 열의를 담아 이렇게 말함으로써 결론을 맺었다. \"만세, 얘야! 개인적으로 말하자면, 나도 네 의견과 같다.\"\n\n월터는 명랑하게 웃으며 그 인사를 되돌려 주고는 말했다.\n\n\"지금으로서는 외삼촌에 대해 딱 한마디만 더 하겠어요, 커틀 선장님. 외삼촌이 보통의 경로로—우편선이나 선편 편지로요, 무슨 말씀인지 아시죠—편지를 쓰셨을 가능성은 없겠죠—\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 얘야.\" 선장이 동의하며 말했다.\n\n\"—그런데 선장님이 어쩌다 그 편지를 못 받으셨을 가능성은요?\"\n\n\"이런, 월터,\" 선장이 살짝 엄한 표정에 가까운 기색으로 그에게 눈을 돌리며 말했다. \"내가 그 과학자 양반, 늙은 솔 길스, 네 외삼촌을 잃은 그날 이후로, 그분 소식이라면 무엇이든 밤낮으로 살피지 않았겠느냐? 늘 그분과 너를 생각하느라 내 마음이 무겁고 경계를 늦추지 않았겠느냐? 자나 깨나 내가 내 자리를 지키지 않았겠느냐, 또 이 항해사 인형이 멀쩡히 붙어 있는 한 내가 그 자리를 떠나는 걸 떳떳잖게 여기지 않았겠느냐!\"\n\n\"네, 커틀 선장님,\" 월터가 그의 손을 꼭 잡으며 대답했다. \"그러셨다는 거 알아요, 또 선장님이 하시는 말씀과 느끼시는 모든 것이 얼마나 충직하고 진실한지도 알아요. 확신해요. 제가 그것을 확신하는 게, 제 발이 다시 이 문지방을 밟고 있다는 것이나 제가 다시 이 참된 손을 잡고 있다는 것만큼이나 확실하다는 걸, 선장님은 의심하지 않으시죠. 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니다, 월터.\" 선장이 환한 얼굴로 대답했다.\n\n\"더는 어떤 억측도 하지 않겠어요,\" 월터가 선장의 거친 손을 뜨겁게 흔들며 말했고, 선장도 그에 못지않은 선의로 그의 손을 마주 흔들었다. \"다만 한마디 덧붙일 건, 제가 외삼촌의 재산에 손을 댄다면 하늘이 용서치 않으실 거예요, 커틀 선장님! 그분이 여기 남겨 두신 모든 것은, 가장 참된 관리인이자 가장 친절한 분의 보살핌 아래 그대로 있을 거예요—그리고 그분 이름이 커틀이 아니라면, 그분에겐 이름이 없는 거지요! 자, 둘도 없는 친구분, 이제—돔비 양에 대해서요.\"\n\n월터가 이 마지막 두 마디에 이르렀을 때 그의 태도에 변화가 있었다. 그리고 그가 그 말을 입에 올렸을 때, 그의 자신감과 명랑함이 모두 그를 떠나 버린 듯했다.\n\n\"어젯밤 제가 돔비 양의 아버지 이야기를 꺼냈을 때 돔비 양이 저를 막기 전까지,\" 월터가 말했다. \"—어떻게 막았는지 기억하시죠?\"\n\n선장은 잘 기억했고, 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"저는 그 전까지는,\" 월터가 말했다. \"우리가 해야 할 어려운 의무가 하나뿐이라고 생각했어요. 그건 그분을 설득해서 친지들과 연락하게 하고, 집으로 돌아가게 하는 거였지요.\"\n\n선장은 힘없이 \"잠깐!\" 혹은 \"대기!\" 혹은 그 상황에 마찬가지로 들어맞는 무언가를 중얼거렸다. 그러나 그 말은 이 통고를 받은 충격으로 인한 완전한 낭패감 탓에 어찌나 힘없게 나왔던지, 그게 무슨 말이었는지는 그저 추측에 맡길 따름이다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 월터가 말했다. \"그건 끝났어요. 저는 더 이상 그렇게 생각하지 않아요. 차라리 저는, 살아남은 뒤로 꿈속에서 그토록 자주 떠다니던 그 난파선 조각 위로 다시 던져져, 거기 표류하고 떠밀리다 죽는 편을 택하겠어요!\"\n\n\"만세, 얘야!\" 선장이 걷잡을 수 없는 만족감에 터져 외쳤다. \"만세! 만세! 만세!\"\n\n\"저토록 젊고, 저토록 착하고, 아름다운 분이,\" 월터가 말했다. \"저토록 곱게 자라고, 그토록 다른 운명을 타고난 분이, 거친 세상과 맞서 싸워야 한다니! 하지만 우리는 그분의 뒤에 있는 모든 것을 끊어 놓은 그 심연을 봤어요, 비록 그것이 얼마나 깊은지는 오직 그분 자신밖에 모르겠지만요. 그리고 돌아갈 길은 없어요.\"\n\n커틀 선장은 이 말을 온전히 이해하지는 못했으나 크게 찬동했고, 바람이 완전히 고물 쪽에서 분다고 강하게 맞장구치는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"그분이 여기 혼자 계셔서는 안 되겠죠, 그렇죠, 커틀 선장님?\" 월터가 근심스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄다, 얘야,\" 선장이 잠시 슬기롭게 궁리한 뒤 대답했다. \"나도 모르겠다. 네가 여기서 그분 말동무가 되어 드리고, 또 너희 둘이 공동으로—\"\n\n\"커틀 선장님!\" 월터가 항의했다. \"제가 여기 있다니요! 돔비 양은 그 순결하고 천진한 마음으로 저를 양오라비로 여기고 있어요. 하지만 만약 제가 그런 자격으로 그분에게 허물없이 다가갈 권리가 있다고 믿는 척한다면—제가 명예를 걸고 그래선 안 된다는 사실을 잊은 척한다면, 제 마음의 간계와 죄가 어떻겠어요?\"\n\n\"월터, 얘야,\" 선장이 다소간 낭패감을 되살리며 넌지시 말했다. \"다른 자격은 없는 게냐, 가령—\"\n\n\"오!\" 월터가 대답했다. \"선장님은 제가 그분의 존경—그분이 품은 것과 같은 그런 존경—을 잃기를 바라세요? 그리고 그분이 이토록 믿고 이토록 무방비한 채로 여기 피신해 와 계신 것을 이용해, 저 자신을 그분의 연인으로 높이려 애써서, 저와 그분의 천사 같은 얼굴 사이에 영원히 장막을 치기를 바라세요? 제가 무슨 말을 하고 있는 거죠? 제가 그럴 수 있다면, 세상에 선장님보다 더 저를 반대하실 분은 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"월터, 얘야,\" 선장이 점점 더 풀이 죽으며 말했다. \"두 사람이 속박의 집에서 맺어지면 안 될 정당한 사유나 장애가 있다면—그건 네가 그 대목을 샅샅이 뒤져 적어 둘 일이지만—나는 그것을 결혼 예고에서 약속되고 맹세된 대로 선언해야 한다고 믿는다. 그래, 다른 자격은 없는 게로구나, 그렇지, 얘야?\"\n\n월터는 활기차게 손을 저어 아니라는 뜻을 표했다.\n\n\"음, 얘야,\" 선장이 천천히 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"이 일로 내가 뱃머리가 잔뜩 가라앉은 기분이라는 걸, 또 완전히 방향을 잃었다는 걸 부인하진 않으마. 하지만 그 아씨로 말할 것 같으면, 월터, 잘 들어라, 그분에 대한 존중과 의무라면, 그건 제아무리 실망스럽다 해도 내 규약에서도 존중이요 의무다. 그러니 나는 네 항적을 따르마, 얘야, 또 너는 분명 네 본분에 맞게 행동하고 있다고 느낀다. 그래, 다른 자격은 없는 게지, 그렇지?\" 선장은 무너져 내린 자기 성(城)의 폐허를 곱씹으며, 몹시 낙담한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"자, 커틀 선장님,\" 월터가 선장의 기운을 북돋우려고 한결 명랑한 기색으로 새 화제를 꺼냈다—그러나 그 무엇도 그것을 해낼 수 없었으니, 선장은 너무도 마음이 무거웠던 것이다—\"제 생각엔 우리가 애를 써서, 돔비 양이 여기 머무시는 동안 그분의 적절한 시중을 들어 드릴 만하고 믿을 수 있는 누군가를 찾아야 할 것 같아요. 그분의 친척들은 안 될 거예요. 돔비 양은 그들 모두가 자기 아버지에게 굽실거린다고 느끼는 게 분명하니까요. 수전은 어떻게 됐을까요?\"\n\n\"그 젊은 여자 말이냐?\" 선장이 대답했다. \"내가 보기엔 그 아가씨가 '마음의 기쁨'의 뜻을 거슬러 다시 떠나보내진 것 같다. 아씨가 처음 왔을 때 내가 그 여자 얘기를 슬쩍 꺼냈더니, 그 여자를 아주 높이 평가하면서, 떠난 지 오래됐다고 하더구나.\"\n\n\"그럼,\" 월터가 말했다. \"돔비 양에게 그 여자가 어디로 갔는지 여쭤 봐 주세요, 우리가 찾아볼게요. 아침이 깊어 가니, 돔비 양도 곧 일어나실 거예요. 선장님이 그분의 가장 좋은 친구잖아요. 위층에서 그분을 기다리세요, 여기 아래 일은 다 제게 맡기시고요.\"\n\n선장은 정말이지 몹시 풀이 죽어, 월터가 이 말을 하며 내쉰 한숨을 따라 쉬고는 그대로 따랐다. 플로렌스는 자기 새 방이 무척 마음에 들었고, 월터를 보고 싶어 했으며, 옛 친구 수전을 다시 맞이할 수 있다는 전망에 기뻐 어쩔 줄 몰랐다. 그러나 플로렌스는 수전이 어디로 갔는지 말할 수 없었으니, 다만 에식스 어디라는 것뿐, 또 자기 기억으로는 투츠 씨 말고는 아무도 말할 수 없으리라고 했다.\n\n이 정보를 가지고 침울한 선장은 월터에게 돌아가, 투츠 씨가 바로 문지방에서 마주쳤던 그 젊은 신사이며, 자기 친구이고, 재산이 있는 젊은 신사이며, 돔비 양을 가망 없이 흠모하고 있다는 사실을 알려 주었다. 선장은 또한 월터가 죽었다고 여겨졌다는 소식이 어떻게 처음으로 자기를 투츠 씨와 알게 만들었는지, 또 투츠 씨가 자기 연정에 대해 입을 다물기로 그들 사이에 엄숙한 조약과 협약이 있다는 것도 이야기해 주었다.\n\n그렇다면 문제는 플로렌스가 투츠 씨를 믿을 수 있느냐였는데, 플로렌스가 미소 지으며 \"오, 그럼요, 진심으로요!\" 하고 말하자, 이제 투츠 씨가 어디 사는지 알아내는 것이 중요해졌다. 이것은 플로렌스도 몰랐고, 선장도 잊어버렸다. 선장이 작은 응접실에서 월터에게 투츠 씨가 분명 곧 이리 올 거라고 말하고 있는데, 바로 그때 투츠 씨 본인이 들어왔다.\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 아무런 격식도 없이 응접실로 뛰어들며 말했다. \"저는 지금 거의 정신착란에 가까운 심정입니다!\"\n\n투츠 씨는 마치 박격포에서 쏘아 내듯 그 말을 발사한 뒤에야 월터를 알아보았고, 비참함의 낄낄거림이라고 할 만한 것으로 그를 알아보았다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 선생,\" 투츠 씨가 이마를 짚으며 말했다. \"하지만 제가 지금 제정신이 나가려는, 아니 이미 나간 상태라서, 이런 처지에 놓인 사람이 예의 비슷한 걸 차린다면 그건 공허한 조롱일 따름입니다. 길스 선장님, 단둘이 만나는 호의를 청하고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"이런, 형제여,\" 선장이 그의 손을 잡으며 대답했다. \"자네야말로 우리가 찾고 있던 사람일세.\"\n\n\"오, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"제가 찾는 대상이 되다니, 그게 대체 무슨 찾기란 말입니까! 저는 면도할 엄두도 못 냈습니다, 이렇게 무모한 상태라서요. 옷도 솔질 못 했고요. 머리는 떡이 졌습니다. 치킨한테 만약 내 부츠를 닦겠다고 들면 네놈을 내 앞에 시체로 뻗게 만들겠다고 했지요!\"\n\n이렇게 마음이 흐트러졌다는 온갖 표시가 투츠 씨의 모습에서 확인되었으니, 그것은 거칠고 사납기 그지없었다.\n\n\"여길 보게, 형제여,\" 선장이 말했다. \"여기 이 사람이 늙은 솔 길스의 조카 월터일세. 바다에서 죽었다고 여겨졌던 바로 그 사람 말이야.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 이마에서 손을 떼고 월터를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 투츠 씨가 더듬거렸다. \"이 무슨 비참함의 뒤엉킴이람! 안녕하십니까? 저—저—저는 당신이 무척 흠뻑 젖으셨겠다 싶어 걱정입니다. 길스 선장님, 가게에서 한마디 나누는 걸 허락해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n그는 선장의 외투를 붙잡고 그와 함께 나가며 속삭였다.\n\n\"그러니까, 길스 선장님, 저 사람이 바로, 그 사람과 돔비 양이 천생연분이라고 선장님이 말씀하셨던 그 당사자입니까?\"\n\n\"이런, 그래, 얘야,\" 비탄에 잠긴 선장이 대답했다. \"한때 나도 그런 마음이었지.\"\n\n\"그런데 이런 때에!\" 투츠 씨가 다시 이마에 손을 대며 외쳤다. \"하필이면!—증오스러운 연적이라니! 적어도, 저 사람은 증오스러운 연적은 아니지,\" 투츠 씨가 다시 생각하며 딱 멈추고 손을 떼며 말했다. \"내가 저 사람을 미워할 게 뭐람? 아니지. 만약 내 애정이 정말로 사심 없는 거였다면, 길스 선장님, 지금 그것을 증명하게 해 주십시오!\"\n\n투츠 씨는 별안간 응접실로 도로 쏜살같이 들어가, 월터의 손을 꽉 쥐고 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까? 감기 안 걸리셨길 바랍니다. 저는—저는 당신과 알고 지내는 기쁨을 주신다면 무척 기쁘겠습니다. 이날을 여러 번 행복하게 맞이하시길 빕니다. 맹세코, 진심으로,\" 투츠 씨가 월터의 얼굴과 풍채와 더 잘 알게 되면서 점점 다정해지며 말했다. \"당신을 만나서 정말 기쁩니다!\"\n\n\"진심으로 고맙습니다,\" 월터가 말했다. \"이보다 더 참되고 따뜻한 환영은 바랄 수도 없겠어요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요, 정말로?\" 투츠 씨가 여전히 그의 손을 흔들며 말했다. \"참 친절하십니다. 정말 감사합니다. 안녕하십니까? 저—거기 다들 안녕히 잘 계시던가요, 그—그러니까, 저—제 말은, 당신이 마지막으로 오신 곳이 어디든 거기 사람들 말입니다.\"\n\n이 모든 좋은 인사와 더 좋은 의도에 월터는 의젓하게 응답했다.\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"저는 엄격히 명예를 지키고 싶습니다만, 이제는 어떤 일정한 주제에 대해 언급하는 것이 허락되리라 믿습니다, 그러니까—\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 얘야,\" 선장이 대답했다. \"거리낌 없이, 거리낌 없이.\"\n\n\"그러면, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 월터스 중위님—혹시 돔비 씨 댁에서 더없이 끔찍한 사건들이 벌어졌다는 걸, 또 돔비 양 본인이 자기 아버지를 떠났다는 걸 아십니까? 제 생각으로 그 아버지란,\" 투츠 씨가 잔뜩 흥분하여 말했다. \"—대리석 기념비라거나 맹금이라거나 하고 부르는 게 차라리 칭찬일 만한, 그런 짐승입니다—그리고 그분이 어디 계신지 찾을 수가 없고, 아무도 모르는 곳으로 가 버리셨다는 걸요?\"\n\n\"이 소식을 어떻게 들으셨는지 여쭤 봐도 될까요?\" 월터가 물었다.\n\n\"월터스 중위님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. 그는 자기 나름의 독특한 과정을 거쳐 이 호칭에 이른 것인데, 아마도 월터의 세례명을 뱃사람 직업과 뒤섞고는, 그와 선장 사이에 어떤 관계가 있어서 그게 당연히 둘의 직함에까지 미친다고 짐작한 모양이었다. \"월터스 중위님, 저는 솔직하게 대답하는 데 아무 거리낌이 없습니다. 사실인즉, 돔비 양과 관련된 모든 일에 제가 지극히 관심을 두는 까닭에—무슨 사심이 있어서가 아닙니다, 월터스 중위님, 저로서는 모두를 위해 제가 할 수 있는 가장 유능한 일이 제 존재를 끝내는 것임을 잘 알고 있고, 제 존재란 그저 거추장스러운 것으로만 여겨질 수 있으니까요—저는 이따금 하인 하나에게 약간의 돈을 쥐여 주는 버릇이 있었습니다. 타울린슨이라는 이름의 더없이 점잖은 젊은이로, 그 집안에 한동안 살아온 사람이지요. 그 타울린슨이 어제저녁 저에게 이런 사정을 알려 주었습니다. 그 뒤로, 길스 선장님—그리고 월터스 중위님—저는 완전히 미칠 지경이었고, 밤새 소파에 누워 있었지요, 지금 보시는 이 폐허가 된 몰골로요.\"\n\n\"투츠 씨,\" 월터가 말했다. \"제가 당신 마음을 덜어 드릴 수 있어서 기쁩니다. 부디 진정하세요. 돔비 양은 무사하고 안녕하십니다.\"\n\n\"선생!\" 투츠 씨가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 그와 새로이 악수하며 외쳤다. \"그 안도감이 어찌나 넘치고 이루 말할 수 없는지, 만약 지금 당신이 돔비 양이 결혼했다고 말씀하신대도 저는 미소 지을 수 있을 겁니다. 그래요, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 선장에게 호소하며 말했다. \"제 영혼과 육신을 걸고, 정말로 그렇게 생각합니다, 그 직후에 제가 저 자신에게 무슨 짓을 하든 간에, 저는 미소 지을 수 있을 것 같습니다, 이렇게나 안도가 되니까요.\"\n\n\"당신처럼 너그러운 마음을 가진 분께는, 돔비 양에게 도움을 드릴 수 있다는 걸 알게 되시면 더욱 큰 안도이자 기쁨일 겁니다,\" 월터가 조금도 더디지 않게 인사를 되돌려 주며 말했다. \"커틀 선장님, 투츠 씨를 위층으로 좀 모셔다 주시겠어요?\"\n\n선장이 투츠 씨에게 손짓하자, 그는 어리둥절한 얼굴로 그 뒤를 따랐고, 집 꼭대기로 올라가, 안내인의 사전 설명 한마디 없이 플로렌스의 새 은신처로 안내되었다.\n\n가엾은 투츠 씨가 그녀를 보고 느낀 놀라움과 기쁨은 어찌나 컸던지, 오로지 격에 넘치는 행동으로밖에는 분출구를 찾을 수 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 달려가, 손을 붙잡고, 입 맞추고, 떨어뜨렸다가, 다시 붙잡고, 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 눈물을 흘리고, 낄낄거렸다. 그러느라 디오게네스에게 물어뜯길 위험은 도통 아랑곳하지 않았는데, 디오게네스는 이런 거동에 자기 여주인을 해치려는 무언가가 있다는 믿음에 사로잡혀 그의 주위를 빙빙 돌았으니, 마치 어느 지점을 노려 공격에 들어갈지만 정하지 못했을 뿐, 그에게 끔찍한 해를 끼치기로는 단단히 작정한 듯했다.\n\n\"오 디, 이 못된, 잊어버린 강아지야! 친애하는 투츠 씨, 당신을 뵈니 정말 기뻐요!\"\n\n\"고맙습니다,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"저는 꽤 잘 지냅니다, 정말 감사합니다, 돔비 양. 댁의 온 가족분들도 다 그러시길 바랍니다.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 자기가 무슨 말을 하고 있는지 전혀 모르는 채 이렇게 말하고는, 의자에 앉아 플로렌스를 빤히 바라보았는데, 그 얼굴에는 어떤 얼굴이 보일 수 있는 것 중에서도 가장 생생한 기쁨과 절망의 다툼이 벌어지고 있었다.\n\n\"길스 선장님과 월터스 중위님이 말씀하시기를, 돔비 양,\" 투츠 씨가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"제가 당신께 무슨 도움을 드릴 수 있다더군요. 만약 제가 무슨 수로든, 제가 어버이를 죽인 자처럼—독립된 재산을 가진 사람이라기보다 훨씬 더 그렇게 처신했던 그 브라이턴에서의 그날의 기억을 씻어 낼 수 있다면,\" 투츠 씨가 매서운 자책으로 말했다. \"저는 한 줄기 기쁨을 띤 채 고요한 무덤 속으로 가라앉을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"부디, 투츠 씨,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"우리가 알고 지낸 어떤 일도 제가 잊기를 바라지 마세요. 저는 결코 잊을 수 없어요, 믿어 주세요. 당신은 늘 제게 너무도 친절하고 좋은 분이셨어요.\"\n\n\"돔비 양,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다. \"제 감정을 헤아려 주시는 것이야말로 당신의 천사 같은 성품의 일부지요. 천 번이라도 감사드립니다. 그건 전혀 대수롭지 않은 일입니다.\"\n\n\"우리가 당신께 여쭤 보려던 건,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"수전이, 제 곁을 떠날 때 당신이 친절하게도 마차 사무소까지 바래다주셨던 그 수전이 어디서 찾아질지 기억하시느냐 하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 분명히는 기억이 안 나는군요, 돔비 양,\" 투츠 씨가 잠시 궁리한 뒤 말했다. \"마차에 적혀 있던 곳의 정확한 이름이 말입니다. 다만 그 여자가 거기서 멈추지 않고 더 멀리 갈 거라고 말했던 건 기억이 납니다. 하지만, 돔비 양, 만약 당신의 목적이 그 여자를 찾아 이리로 데려오는 것이라면, 저와 치킨이 그 여자를 데려오겠습니다, 제 헌신과 치킨의 대단한 지력이 보장할 수 있는 온갖 신속함을 다해서요.\"\n\n투츠 씨가 쓸모 있는 사람이 될 수 있다는 전망에 어찌나 분명히 기뻐하고 생기를 되찾던지, 또 그 헌신의 사심 없는 진심이 어찌나 의심할 여지 없던지, 그를 거절한다면 잔인한 일이 되었으리라. 플로렌스는 본능적인 섬세함으로 조금의 방해도 두지 않으려 삼갔으나, 그를 감사로 압도하는 것만은 삼가지 않았다. 그리고 투츠 씨는 자랑스럽게 그 임무를 즉각 수행할 것으로 떠맡았다.\n\n\"돔비 양,\" 투츠 씨가 그녀가 내민 손에 손을 대며 말했는데, 가망 없는 사랑의 아픔이 그를 꿰뚫고 지나가 얼굴에 번뜩여 드러났다. \"안녕히 계세요! 외람되지만 한 말씀 드리자면, 당신의 불행이 저를 더없이 비참하게 만들며, 또 당신은 길스 선장님 바로 다음으로 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다. 저는 제 자신의 부족함을 잘 압니다, 돔비 양—그건 조금도 대수롭지 않은 일이지요, 고맙습니다—하지만 저는 전적으로 의지하셔도 좋은 사람입니다, 정말이지 그렇습니다, 돔비 양.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 투츠 씨는 방에서 나왔는데, 다시 선장이 동행했다. 선장은 조금 떨어진 곳에 서서, 모자를 겨드랑이에 끼고 갈고리손으로 흐트러진 머리칼을 매만지며, 방금 오간 일을 자못 흥미롭게 지켜보고 있던 참이었다. 그리고 그들 뒤로 문이 닫히자, 투츠 씨 인생의 빛은 다시 어둡게 흐려졌다.\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 그 신사가 계단 거의 맨 아래쯤에서 멈춰 서 돌아보며 말했다. \"솔직히 말씀드리면, 저는 지금 이 순간 월터스 중위님을 향해, 제 가슴속에 품고 싶은 그런 전적으로 우호적인 감정으로 그분을 대할 만한 심정이 아닙니다. 우리가 늘 자기 감정을 다스릴 수는 없는 법이지요, 길스 선장님, 그러니 저를 옆문으로 내보내 주신다면 각별한 호의로 알겠습니다.\"\n\n\"형제여,\" 선장이 대답했다. \"자네 항로는 자네가 정하게. 어떤 항로를 택하든 그건 떳떳하고 뱃사람다운 일일 걸세, 나는 아주 확신하네.\"\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"정말 친절하십니다. 선장님이 좋게 봐주시는 게 제겐 위안입니다. 한 가지,\" 투츠 씨가 반쯤 열린 문 뒤 통로에 서서 말했다. \"선장님이 명심해 주시길 바라는 게 있는데, 또 월터스 중위님께도 알려 주셨으면 하는 것입니다. 저는 이제 제 재산을 온전히 물려받았습니다, 아시다시피요, 그런데—그런데 저는 그걸 어찌해야 할지 모르겠습니다. 만약 제가 금전적인 면에서 조금이라도 쓸모가 될 수 있다면, 저는 편안하고 매끄럽게 고요한 무덤 속으로 미끄러져 들 텐데요.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 더 말하지 않고 조용히 빠져나가, 선장이 어떤 대꾸도 못 하게 자기 뒤로 문을 닫아 버렸다.\n\n플로렌스는 이 착한 사람이 떠난 뒤로도 오래도록 그를 생각했으니, 아픔과 기쁨이 뒤섞인 감정으로 그러했다. 그는 너무도 정직하고 따뜻한 마음을 지녀서, 그를 다시 보고 자기가 곤경에 처했을 때 그가 진실하다는 것을 확인받는 것은 무엇과도 바꿀 수 없는 기쁨이요 위안이었다. 그러나 바로 그 까닭에, 자기가 그에게 한순간의 불행을 안기거나, 한 가닥 입김으로 그 무해한 삶의 흐름을 흩뜨린다고 생각하니 너무도 가슴이 아파, 그녀의 두 눈에 눈물이 가득 차고 가슴이 연민으로 넘쳤다. 커틀 선장도 자기 나름의 방식으로 투츠 씨를 많이 생각했고, 월터도 그러했다. 저녁이 되어 모두가 플로렌스의 새 방에 함께 앉아 있을 때, 월터는 그를 더없이 열렬하게 칭찬했고, 그가 집을 나서며 했던 말을 플로렌스에게 전했는데, 자기 자신의 정직함과 공감이 그 말을 둘러쌀 수 있는 온갖 우아한 논평과 찬사의 꾸밈을 곁들였다.\n\n투츠 씨는 이튿날에도, 그다음 날에도, 또 며칠 동안 다시 오지 않았다. 그동안 플로렌스는 새로운 불안 없이, 늙은 계기 제작자 집 꼭대기에서 새장 속의 조용한 새처럼 살았다. 그러나 날이 갈수록 플로렌스는 점점 더 또렷이 기운을 잃고 고개를 떨구었고, 죽은 아이의 얼굴에서 보였던 그 표정을 그녀는 자주 높은 창문에서 하늘로 향했으니, 마치 그 아이가 밝은 기슭에 대해 이야기했던 그 천사를 찾는 듯했다. 아이는 자기 작은 침대에 누워 그렇게 말했었다.\n\n플로렌스는 최근 들어 약하고 여위었으며, 자기가 겪은 동요도 건강에 영향을 미치지 않은 것은 아니었다. 그러나 지금 그녀를 괴롭히는 것은 육신의 병이 아니었다. 그녀는 마음이 괴로웠고, 그 괴로움의 원인은 월터였다.\n\n자기에게 관심을 두고, 자기를 염려하고, 자기를 위해 봉사하는 것을 자랑스럽고 기뻐하며, 그 모든 것을 자기 성격다운 열의와 열정으로 드러내면서도, 플로렌스는 그가 자기를 피한다는 것을 알아챘다. 긴긴 하루 내내 그는 좀처럼 그녀의 방에 다가오지 않았다. 그녀가 그를 찾으면 그는 왔고, 그 순간만큼은 다시, 그녀가 사람들이 빤히 쳐다보던 거리에서 길 잃은 아이였을 때 기억하던 그대로 진지하고 환했다. 그러나 그는 곧 어색해졌고—그녀의 예민한 애정은 그것을 모르기에는 너무도 살피고 있었다—불안해졌으며, 곧 그녀를 떠났다. 부르지 않으면 그는 아침부터 밤까지 온종일 결코 오지 않았다. 저녁 어스름이 깃들면 그는 늘 거기 있었고, 그때가 그녀에게는 가장 행복한 시간이었으니, 그때만큼은 어린 시절의 그 옛 월터가 변하지 않았다고 반쯤은 믿을 수 있었던 까닭이다. 그러나 그때조차도, 어떤 사소한 말이나 눈빛이나 정황이, 둘 사이에는 넘을 수 없는 형언키 어려운 간극이 있다는 것을 그녀에게 보여 주곤 했다.\n\n그리고 그녀는, 월터에게 일어난 이 큰 변화의 이런 드러남이, 그것을 감추려는 그의 온갖 안간힘에도 불구하고 나타난다는 것을 알아채지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀는 생각했다. 자기를 헤아리는 마음에서, 또 자기 친절한 손으로 그녀에게 어떤 상처도 입히지 않으려는 간절함에서, 그가 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 자잘한 꾀와 위장에 기대고 있다고. 그럴수록 플로렌스는 그에게 일어난 변화의 크기를 더욱더 절감했고, 그럴수록 오라비가 이렇게 멀어지는 것에 더욱더 자주 눈물지었다.\n\n마음씨 착한 선장—지칠 줄 모르고, 다정하고, 한결같이 열성적인 그녀의 친구도—그것을 알아챘다고 플로렌스는 생각했고, 그것이 그를 아프게 했다. 그는 처음만큼 명랑하고 희망에 차 있지 못했고, 저녁에 셋이 함께 있을 때면 자못 서글픈 얼굴로 그녀와 월터를 번갈아 가며 슬쩍슬쩍 바라보곤 했다.\n\n마침내 플로렌스는 월터에게 말하기로 마음먹었다. 이제 그가 멀어지는 까닭을 알 것 같았고, 자기가 그것을 알아챘으며 거기에 온전히 따르고 그를 책망하지 않는다고 말한다면, 그것이 자기의 벅찬 마음에 위안이 되고 또 그의 마음도 더 편하게 해 주리라고 생각했다.\n\n플로렌스가 이 결심을 한 것은 어느 일요일 오후였다. 충직한 선장은 놀라우리만치 큰 셔츠 깃을 단 채로 그녀 곁에 앉아, 안경을 쓰고 책을 읽고 있었는데, 그녀가 그에게 월터가 어디 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아래층에 있는 것 같다, 아씨.\" 선장이 대답했다.\n\n\"그분과 이야기를 좀 하고 싶어요,\" 플로렌스가 마치 아래층으로 내려가려는 듯 황급히 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"이리로 불러올리마, 아가씨,\" 선장이 말했다. \"눈 깜짝할 새에.\"\n\n그러고는 선장이 무척 민첩하게 자기 책을 어깨에 둘러멨다—그는 일요일에는 더 점잖아 보인다는 이유로 아주 큼직한 책만 읽는 것을 자기 의무로 삼았던 것인데, 여러 해 전에 헌책 노점에서 어마어마한 책 한 권을 흥정해 사 두었으나, 언제든 다섯 줄만 읽어도 도무지 갈피를 못 잡을 지경이어서, 그 책이 무슨 내용을 다루는지조차 아직 알아내지 못한 터였다—그러고는 물러갔다. 곧 월터가 나타났다.\n\n\"커틀 선장님이 말씀하시기를, 돔비 양,\" 그가 들어오며 열심히 운을 떼다가—그녀의 얼굴을 보고는 멈추었다.\n\n\"오늘은 몸이 좋지 않으시군요. 괴로워 보이세요. 우셨군요.\"\n\n그가 어찌나 다정하게, 또 목소리에 어찌나 뜨거운 떨림을 담아 말했던지, 그 말소리에 그녀의 두 눈에 눈물이 쏟아졌다.\n\n\"월터,\" 플로렌스가 부드럽게 말했다. \"저는 몸이 좀 안 좋고, 또 울고 있었어요. 당신에게 할 말이 있어요.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 맞은편에 앉아, 아름답고 순결한 그녀의 얼굴을 바라보았다. 그러자 그의 얼굴이 창백해지고 입술이 떨렸다.\n\n\"당신은, 당신이 살아 돌아왔다는 걸 제가 알게 된 그날 밤에—아, 사랑하는 월터, 그날 밤 제가 느꼈던 것, 또 제가 바랐던 것이라니!—말씀하셨어요.\"\n\n그가 둘 사이의 탁자 위에 떨리는 손을 얹고, 앉아서 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"—제가 변했다고요. 당신이 그렇게 말하는 걸 듣고 저는 놀랐지만, 이제는 제가 변했다는 걸 알아요. 저한테 화내지 마세요, 월터. 그때는 제가 너무 기뻐서 그것을 생각할 겨를이 없었어요.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 다시 어린아이처럼 보였다. 그가 보고 들은 것은 그 천진하고, 믿음 가득하고, 사랑스러운 아이였다. 그가 그 발치에 이 세상의 부귀를 다 바쳤을 그 사랑하는 여인이 아니었다.\n\n\"제가 당신을 마지막으로 봤던 때를 기억하세요, 월터, 당신이 떠나기 전에요?\"\n\n그가 가슴속에 손을 넣어 작은 돈주머니 하나를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"저는 늘 이걸 목에 걸고 다녔어요! 만약 제가 깊은 바닷속으로 가라앉았더라도, 이건 바다 밑바닥까지 저와 함께였을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼 앞으로도 이걸 지니실 거죠, 월터, 옛정을 봐서라도요?\"\n\n\"제가 죽을 때까지요!\"\n\n그녀는, 마치 그 작은 기념의 정표를 그에게 건넨 그날로부터 단 하루도 지나지 않은 듯, 두려움 없이 또 꾸밈없이 자기 손을 그의 손 위에 얹었다.\n\n\"그게 기뻐요. 저는 늘 그렇게 생각하면 기쁠 거예요, 월터. 그날 저녁 우리가 함께 이야기할 때, 이 변화의 생각이 우리 마음에 동시에 떠오르는 듯했던 걸 기억하세요?\"\n\n\"아뇨!\" 그가 의아한 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"그래요, 월터. 그때 이미 저는 당신의 희망과 앞날을 해치는 원인이 되어 있었어요. 그때는 그렇게 생각하기가 두려웠지만, 이제는 알아요. 그때 당신이 너그러이도 자기 역시 그걸 알고 있다는 걸 제게 감출 수 있었다 해도, 지금은 그러지 못해요, 아무리 전처럼 너그럽게 애쓰셔도요. 당신은 애쓰고 계세요. 그것에 대해 깊이, 진심으로 감사드려요, 월터. 하지만 당신은 성공하지 못해요. 당신은 자기 자신의 고난 속에서, 또 가장 사랑하는 친척의 고난 속에서 너무도 많이 고통받았기에, 당신에게 닥친 그 모든 위험과 고통의 무고한 원인을 도저히 못 본 척할 수가 없어요. 당신은 그런 처지의 저를 도무지 잊을 수 없고, 그래서 우리는 더 이상 오누이가 될 수 없어요. 하지만, 사랑하는 월터, 제가 이 일로 당신을 탓한다고 생각하지는 마세요. 저는 그것을 알 수 있었어야—알았어야 했지만—기쁨에 겨워 잊었던 거예요. 제가 바라는 건 단 하나, 이 감정이 더 이상 비밀이 아니게 되었을 때 당신이 저를 좀 덜 괴롭게 떠올렸으면 하는 거예요. 그리고 제가 청하는 건 단 하나, 월터, 한때 당신의 누이였던 그 가엾은 아이의 이름으로, 이제 제가 다 알게 되었으니, 저 때문에 자기 자신과 씨름하며 스스로를 괴롭히지 말아 달라는 거예요!\"\n\n월터는 그녀가 이 말을 하는 동안 그녀를 바라보았는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 경이와 놀라움으로 가득 찼던지 다른 것이 들어설 자리가 없었다. 이제 그는 그토록 애원하듯 자기 손에 닿아 있던 그 손을 붙잡아, 자기 두 손 사이에 쥐었다.\n\n\"오, 돔비 양,\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 당신께 마땅히 돌려 드려야 할 것이 무엇인가 하는 의식과 씨름하느라 그토록 많이 괴로워하는 동안, 정작 당신께는 방금 그 말씀으로 제게 드러내신 그런 고통을 안겨 드렸다는 게 정말일까요? 결단코, 하늘에 맹세코, 저는 당신을 제 소년 시절과 청년 시절의 유일하고, 빛나고, 순결하고, 축복받은 추억으로밖에는 생각한 적이 없습니다. 처음부터 결코, 또 마지막까지 결코, 저는 제 삶 속에서 당신이 차지한 그 자리를, 신성한 것, 결코 가벼이 여겨선 안 될 것, 아무리 귀히 여겨도 모자랄 것, 죽을 때까지 결코 잊어선 안 될 것으로밖에는 여기지 않을 것입니다. 우리가 헤어지던 그날 밤처럼 당신이 바라보고 또 말하는 것을 다시 보고 듣는 것은, 제게 무어라 말로 다 할 수 없는 행복입니다. 그리고 당신의 오라비로서 사랑받고 신뢰받는 것은, 제가 받아 소중히 간직할 수 있는 그다음으로 큰 선물입니다!\"\n\n\"월터,\" 플로렌스가 그를 진지하게, 그러나 빛이 변해 가는 얼굴로 바라보며 말했다. \"이 모든 것을 희생하면서까지 제게 마땅히 돌려야 한다는 그것이 뭐죠?\"\n\n\"존경입니다,\" 월터가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"경의지요.\"\n\n그녀의 얼굴에 홍조가 떠올랐고, 그녀는 수줍게 또 생각에 잠겨 자기 손을 거두었다. 그러면서도 여전히 조금도 수그러들지 않은 진지함으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저에게는 오라비의 권리가 없습니다,\" 월터가 말했다. \"저에게는 오라비의 자격이 없습니다. 저는 한 아이를 두고 떠났습니다. 그런데 한 여인을 만났습니다.\"\n\n홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 온통 번졌다. 그녀는 그가 더 말하지 말기를 애원하는 듯한 몸짓을 했고, 얼굴을 두 손에 묻었다.\n\n둘은 한동안 말이 없었다. 그녀는 울고 있었다.\n\n\"저는 그토록 믿음 가득하고, 순결하고, 착한 마음에 마땅히 그래야 합니다,\" 월터가 말했다. \"비록 제 마음이 찢어진다 해도, 그 마음에서 저 자신을 떼어 내기까지 해야 합니다. 어찌 감히 그것이 제 누이의 마음이라고 말하겠습니까!\"\n\n그녀는 여전히 울고 있었다.\n\n\"만약 당신이 행복했더라면, 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 사랑하고 흠모하는 친구들에 둘러싸이고, 당신이 태어나 누렸어야 할 그 신분을 부러움 사게 만드는 온갖 것에 둘러싸여 있었더라면,\" 월터가 말했다. \"그리고 그때 당신이 지난날을 다정하게 추억하며 저를 오라비라 불러 주었더라면, 저는 제 멀리 떨어진 자리에서도 그 이름에 응답할 수 있었을 겁니다, 그렇게 함으로써 당신의 티 없는 진실을 욕보인다는 마음의 거리낌 없이요. 하지만 여기서—지금은!\"\n\n\"오, 고마워요, 고마워요, 월터! 제가 당신을 그토록 잘못 본 것을 용서해 주세요. 제겐 조언해 줄 사람이 아무도 없었어요. 저는 완전히 혼자예요.\"\n\n\"플로렌스!\" 월터가 격정에 차 말했다. \"저는 불과 몇 분 전만 해도 그 무엇도 제 입에서 끄집어내지 못하리라 여겼던 말을 내뱉도록 떠밀리고 있습니다. 만약 제가 형편이 넉넉했더라면, 만약 언젠가 당신을 당신의 본래 신분에 가까운 자리로 되돌려 드릴 만한 재력이나 가망이 조금이라도 제게 있었더라면, 저는 당신께 말씀드렸을 겁니다. 당신이 제게—단 하나의 이름을, 다른 무엇보다 앞서는 권리를, 곧 당신을 지키고 보살필 권리를 내려 주실 수 있다고요—제가 당신께 품은 사랑과 경의, 그리고 제 온 마음이 당신의 것이라는 사실 말고는 어느 것 하나 그럴 자격이 없는 저이지만요. 저는 당신께 말씀드렸을 겁니다. 그것이야말로 당신이 제게 주실 수 있는 유일한 권리, 곧 제가 감히 받아들이고 감히 주장할 수 있는, 당신을 지키고 수호할 그 권리라고요. 그러나 만약 제게 그 권리가 있다면, 저는 그것을 너무도 귀하고 값을 매길 수 없는 신뢰로 여길 것이기에, 제 삶의 온전한 진심과 열정을 다 바쳐도 그 값어치를 보잘것없이밖에 못 갚을 거라고요.\"\n\n머리는 여전히 수그러져 있었고, 눈물은 여전히 떨어졌으며, 가슴은 흐느낌으로 부풀어 올랐다.\n\n\"사랑하는 플로렌스! 더없이 사랑하는 플로렌스! 그렇게 부르는 것이 얼마나 주제넘고 무모한 일인지 헤아릴 겨를도 없이, 저는 마음속으로 당신을 그렇게 불렀습니다. 마지막으로 한 번만, 당신의 그 사랑스러운 이름으로 당신을 부르고, 또 제가 한 말을 누이로서 잊어 주신다는 표시로 이 부드러운 손에 손을 대게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n그녀가 고개를 들어, 두 눈에 그토록 엄숙한 다정함을 담고, 눈물 너머로 그에게 그토록 고요하고 밝고 잔잔한 미소를 비추며, 그 몸과 목소리에 그토록 낮고 부드러운 떨림을 띠고 그에게 말했으니, 그의 마음 가장 깊은 현(絃)이 울렸고, 그것을 듣는 그의 눈앞이 흐려졌다.\n\n\"아니에요, 월터, 저는 그것을 잊을 수 없어요. 세상을 다 준다 해도 그것을 잊지 않겠어요. 당신은—당신은 무척 가난한가요?\"\n\n\"저는 그저 떠도는 사람일 뿐입니다,\" 월터가 말했다. \"먹고살려고 바다를 건너 항해를 다니는 거지요. 그게 지금 제 천직입니다.\"\n\n\"또 곧 다시 떠나시나요, 월터?\"\n\n\"아주 곧요.\"\n\n그녀는 잠시 그를 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 그러더니 수줍게 자기 떨리는 손을 그의 손에 얹었다.\n\n\"저를 아내로 맞아 주신다면, 월터, 저는 당신을 진심으로 사랑할 거예요. 저를 당신과 함께 가게 해 주신다면, 월터, 저는 두려움 없이 세상 끝까지라도 가겠어요. 저는 당신을 위해 포기할 게 아무것도 없어요—내놓을 것도 없고, 버릴 사람도 없으니까요. 하지만 제 모든 사랑과 삶을 당신께 바치겠어요, 그리고 제게 분별과 기억이 남아 있는 한, 마지막 숨으로 당신의 이름을 하느님께 불러 올리겠어요.\"\n\n그가 그녀를 가슴으로 끌어당겨, 자기 뺨을 그녀의 뺨에 갖다 댔다. 그리고 이제 더는 밀쳐지지도, 더는 외롭지도 않은 채, 그녀는 정말로 울었다, 자기가 사랑하는 연인의 가슴에 기대어.\n\n축복받은 일요일의 종소리여, 넋을 잃고 행복에 겨운 그 두 사람의 귀에 그토록 평온하게 울려 퍼지누나! 축복받은 일요일의 평화와 고요여, 그들 영혼 속의 잔잔함과 어우러져 그들 둘레에 거룩한 공기를 자아내누나! 축복받은 황혼이 살며시 깃들어, 그녀가 잠들 때 그토록 다독이듯 또 진중하게 그녀를 그늘 지우누나, 마치 잠재운 아이처럼, 자기가 매달린 그 가슴 위에서 잠드는 그녀를!\n\n오, 그토록 가벼이 거기 놓인 사랑과 믿음의 무게여! 그래, 감긴 두 눈을 내려다보아라, 월터, 자랑스럽고 다정한 눈길로. 이 넓디넓은 온 세상에서 그 눈이 지금 찾는 것은 오직 너뿐이니—오직 너뿐이니!\n\n선장은 사위가 아주 캄캄해질 때까지 작은 응접실에 남아 있었다. 그는 월터가 앉아 있던 의자에 앉아, 채광창을 올려다보았는데, 그렇게 하는 사이 낮이 조금씩 조금씩 스러지고 별들이 빼꼼 내려다보았다. 그는 초에 불을 붙이고, 파이프에 불을 붙여 다 태우고는, 도대체 위층에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는 건지, 또 왜 자기를 차 마시러 부르지 않는 건지 의아해했다.\n\n그가 한창 의아해하고 있을 때 플로렌스가 그의 곁으로 왔다.\n\n\"오! 아씨!\" 선장이 외쳤다. \"이런, 아씨와 월터가 한참 동안 이야기를 길게 나누셨군요, 우리 아가씨.\"\n\n플로렌스가 작은 손을 그의 외투의 커다란 단추 하나에 둘러 감고는, 그의 얼굴을 내려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"친애하는 선장님, 괜찮으시다면 한 가지 말씀드릴 게 있어요.\"\n\n선장은 그게 무슨 말인지 들으려고 꽤 잽싸게 고개를 들었다. 그 덕에 플로렌스를 좀 더 또렷이 보게 되자, 그는 의자를 뒤로 밀고, 자기 몸도 의자째 갈 수 있는 데까지 한껏 물렸다.\n\n\"뭐라고요! 마음의 기쁨이여!\" 선장이 별안간 의기양양해져 외쳤다. \"그거예요?\"\n\n\"네!\" 플로렌스가 간절히 말했다.\n\n\"월터! 신랑! 그거란 말이오?\" 선장이 자기 번들거리는 모자를 채광창 쪽으로 휙 던져 올리며 우렁차게 외쳤다.\n\n\"네!\" 플로렌스가 웃으면서 동시에 울면서 외쳤다.\n\n선장은 즉시 그녀를 와락 끌어안았다. 그러고는 번들거리는 모자를 주워 머리에 쓰고, 그녀의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼우고는 그녀를 다시 위층으로 데려갔다. 거기서 그는 이제 자기 인생 최대의 농담을 펼칠 때가 되었다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"이런, 월터 얘야!\" 선장이 더없이 흐뭇한 보온 냄비 같은 얼굴로 문 안을 들여다보며 말했다. \"그래, 다른 자격은 없는 게로구나, 그렇지?\"\n\n그는 이 익살에 하마터면 숨이 막힐 뻔했는데, 차를 마시는 동안 적어도 마흔 번은 그 말을 되풀이했고, 그 사이사이 외투 소매로 환하게 빛나는 얼굴을 닦고 손수건으로 머리를 온통 두드려 댔다. 그러나 그에게는 마음이 내킬 때 기댈 만한 더 진중한 기쁨의 원천도 없지 않았으니, 그가 월터와 플로렌스를 이루 말할 수 없는 기쁨으로 바라보며 낮은 소리로 이렇게 말하는 것이 거듭 들렸기 때문이다.\n\n\"에드워드 커틀, 이 사람아, 자네가 그 자그마한 재산을 공동 명의로 양도해 버렸을 때만큼 자네 평생 더 나은 항로를 잡은 적은 없었네!\"\n\n# CHAPTER LI. Mr Dombey and the World\n\n돔비 씨는 날들이 흘러가는 동안 무엇을 하고 있는가? 그는 한 번이라도 자기 딸을 생각하는가, 아니면 그 애가 어디로 갔는지 궁금해하기라도 하는가? 그 애가 집으로 돌아와, 그 적막한 집에서 예전의 생활을 이어 가고 있다고 짐작하는가? 누구도 그를 대신해 답할 수 없다. 그는 그 이후로 한 번도 그 애의 이름을 입에 올린 적이 없다. 그의 집안 식구들은 그가 단호히 입을 다물고 있는 화제에 다가서기에는 그를 너무도 두려워하고 있으며, 그에게 감히 물어볼 수 있는 단 한 사람마저도 그는 즉시 입을 막아 버린다.\n\n\"오, 폴!\" 그의 누이가 플로렌스가 떠난 날 방 안으로 슬그머니 들어오며 중얼거린다. \"네 아내! 그 벼락출세한 여자 말이야! 내가 어렴풋이 들은 게 사실이라는 게, 그게 가능한 일이니, 그 여자가 너의 그 유례없는 헌신에—내가 장담컨대, 그 여자의 변덕과 오만에 맞춰 네 친혈육마저 희생시키기에 이른 그 헌신에—이런 식으로 보답한다는 게? 가엾은 내 동생아!\"\n\n첫 번째 만찬회 날 자기가 저녁 식사에 초대받지 못했던 것을 절절히 떠올리게 하는 이 말과 함께, 칙 부인은 손수건을 한껏 동원하며 돔비 씨의 목에 매달린다. 그러나 돔비 씨는 냉랭하게 그녀를 떼어내어 의자로 안내한다.\n\n\"고맙소, 루이자,\" 그가 말한다, \"네 애정의 이런 표시는 말이오. 하지만 우리 대화가 다른 어떤 주제로 옮겨 가기를 바라오. 내가 내 운명을 한탄하거나, 위로가 필요하다고 토로하거든, 그때 친절을 베풀어 위로해 주면 되오, 루이자.\"\n\n\"오, 폴,\" 그의 누이가 손수건을 얼굴에 댄 채 고개를 가로저으며 대꾸한다, \"나는 네 그 대단한 기개를 잘 알아. 그러니 이토록 고통스럽고 역겨운 주제에 대해서는 더 이상 말하지 않으마.\" 이 두 형용사의 머리 위로 칙 부인은 통렬한 분노를 쏟아붓는다. \"하지만 제발 한 가지만 묻게 해 줘—나를 충격에 빠뜨리고 비탄에 잠기게 할 무언가를 듣게 될까 두렵긴 하다만—그 불행한 아이 플로렌스가—\"\n\n\"루이자!\" 그녀의 동생이 매섭게 말한다, \"조용히 해! 이 얘기는 한마디도 더 하지 마!\"\n\n칙 부인은 그저 고개를 가로젓고 손수건을 쓰며, 더 이상 돔비답지 않은 타락한 돔비들을 두고 신음할 수 있을 뿐이다. 그러나 플로렌스가 이디스의 도주에 연루된 것인지, 아니면 그녀를 따라간 것인지, 너무 많은 일을 한 것인지, 너무 적게 한 것인지, 무언가를 한 것인지 아무것도 하지 않은 것인지, 그녀로서는 도무지 알 길이 없다.\n\n그는 한 치의 일탈도 없이 나아가며, 자기 생각과 감정을 제 가슴속에 단단히 가두어 누구에게도 내보이지 않는다. 그는 딸을 찾는 일을 하지 않는다. 그 애가 누이와 함께 있다고 여길 수도, 제 지붕 아래 있다고 여길 수도 있다. 그 애를 끊임없이 생각하고 있을 수도, 한 번도 생각하지 않을 수도 있다. 그가 보이는 어떤 기색으로도 그건 매한가지다.\n\n그러나 이것만은 분명하다. 그는 자기가 그 애를 잃었다고는 생각하지 않는다. 그는 진실을 조금도 의심하지 않는다. 그는 자기의 우뚝 솟은 우월함 속에 너무 오래 갇혀 지내며, 그 아래 길에서 인내심 강하고 온순한 존재로 있는 그 애를 보아 왔기에, 그런 두려움 따위는 품지 않는다. 자기의 치욕으로 흔들리기는 했어도, 그는 아직 땅바닥까지 낮아지지는 않았다. 뿌리는 넓고 깊으며, 세월이 흐르는 동안 그 잔뿌리들은 사방으로 뻗어 주위 온갖 것에서 양분을 빨아들였다. 나무는 일격을 맞았으나, 쓰러지지는 않았다.\n\n비록 그가 자기 안의 세계를 바깥세상으로부터 숨긴다 해도—그 바깥세상은 지금으로서는 단 하나의 목적만을 지녔으며, 그것은 그가 가는 곳마다 그를 열심히 지켜보는 일이라고 그는 믿거니와—움푹 꺼진 두 눈과 뺨, 초췌한 이마, 침울하고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 분위기로 새어 나오는 그 세계의 반항적인 흔적들만큼은 숨길 수가 없다. 예전과 다름없이 속을 꿰뚫어 볼 수 없는 사람이지만, 그는 여전히 달라진 사람이며, 예전과 다름없이 오만하지만, 그는 꺾인 것이다. 그렇지 않다면 그런 흔적이 거기 있을 리 없다.\n\n세상. 세상이 그를 어떻게 여기는지, 그를 어떻게 바라보는지, 그에게서 무엇을 보는지, 그리고 무엇이라고 말하는지—이것이 그의 마음을 떠나지 않고 괴롭히는 악령이다. 그것은 그가 있는 곳이라면 어디에나 있고, 그보다 더 나쁜 것은, 그가 없는 곳에도 어디에나 있다는 점이다. 그것은 그와 함께 하인들 사이로 나오면서도, 동시에 등 뒤에 남아 수군대게 내버려 둔다. 그는 그것이 거리에서 자기 뒤를 손가락질하는 것을 본다. 그것은 그의 회계 사무소에서 그를 기다린다. 그것은 상인들 가운데서 부유한 자들의 어깨너머로 곁눈질한다. 그것은 군중 사이를 손짓하며 재잘대고 돌아다닌다. 그것은 어느 곳에서든 늘 그를 앞질러 가 있다. 그리고 그가 떠나고 나면 늘 가장 분주해진다는 것을 그는 안다. 밤에 그가 제 방에 틀어박혀 있을 때면, 그것은 그의 집 안에도, 그 바깥에도 있어, 보도를 밟는 발소리로 들리고, 탁자 위 인쇄물 속에 보이며, 철길 위로 또 배 안에서 이리저리 김을 뿜으며 다닌다. 어디서나 잠시도 쉬지 않고 분주하며, 오직 그 한 사람만을 화제로 삼는다.\n\n그것은 그의 상상이 빚어낸 허깨비가 아니다. 그것은 그 자신의 마음속에서만큼이나 다른 사람들의 마음속에서도 활발하다. 일부러 그와 이야기를 나누려고 바덴바덴에서 찾아온 피닉스 사촌이 그 증인이다. 그 우정 어린 임무에 피닉스 사촌과 동행한 백스톡 소령이 그 증인이다.\n\n돔비 씨는 평소의 위엄으로 그들을 맞이하며, 예전의 자세 그대로 난롯불 앞에 꼿꼿이 선다. 그는 세상이 그들의 두 눈을 통해 자기를 바라보고 있음을 느낀다. 그것이 벽에 걸린 그림들의 응시 속에 있음을. 책장 위의 피트 씨가 그것을 대변하고 있음을. 벽에 걸린 그 자신의 지도 속에도 눈들이 있음을.\n\n\"유난히 추운 봄이오,\" 돔비 씨가 말한다—세상을 속이려고.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 선생,\" 소령이 우정의 열기에 차서 말한다, \"조지프 백스톡은 위선 따위엔 영 서툰 사람입니다. 친구들을 멀리하고 냉대하고 싶으시거든, 돔비, J. B.는 그런 용도에 맞는 사람이 못 됩니다. 조는 거칠고 억센 사람이외다, 선생. 무뚝뚝하지요, 선생, 무뚝뚝한 게 바로 조올시다. 고(故) 요크 공작 전하께서 황공하게도 저에게 이런 말씀을 해 주셨소이다, 그럴 만한 자격이 있든 없든—그건 아무래도 좋소—'요점으로 곧장 파고드는 데 있어 내가 믿을 만한 사람이 군대에 하나 있다면, 그게 바로 조다—조 백스톡이지.'\"\n\n돔비 씨는 동의의 뜻을 내비친다.\n\n\"자, 돔비,\" 소령이 말한다, \"나는 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이오. 우리 친구 피닉스도—내가 감히 말해도 된다면—\"\n\n\"영광이지요, 그럼요,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말한다.\n\n\"—역시,\" 소령이 고개를 끄덕이며 말을 잇는다, \"세상 물정에 밝은 사람입니다. 돔비, 당신도 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이오. 자, 세상 물정에 밝은 세 사람이 한데 모이고, 게다가 친구 사이라면—나는 그렇게 믿거니와—\" 그가 다시 한번 피닉스 사촌에게 호소한다.\n\n\"그럼요,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말한다, \"더없이 다정한 사이지요.\"\n\n\"—친구 사이라면,\" 소령이 말을 잇는다, \"늙은 조의 견해로는(내가 틀릴 수도 있소만), 어떤 특정한 주제에 대한 세상의 의견이란 아주 쉽게 알아낼 수 있는 거올시다.\"\n\n\"의심할 여지가 없지요,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말한다. \"사실상, 그건 아주 자명한 종류의 일입니다. 소령, 나는 내 친구 돔비가 내 말을 꼭 들어주었으면 하는 간절한 바람이 있소이다. 사람을 행복하게 해 줄 온갖 자질을 두루 갖추었던 나의 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 친척이, 마땅히 지켜야 할 바를—사실상, 세상에 대해 마땅히 지켜야 할 바를—그토록 까맣게 잊고서, 그토록 유별난 방식으로 처신하고 말았다는 데 대해 내가 품은 지극한 경악과 유감을 표하고 싶다는 거올시다. 나는 그 이후로 줄곧 지독한 우울 상태에 빠져 있었소이다. 실로 어젯밤에는 롱 색스비에게—키가 6피트 10인치나 되는 사내인데, 내 친구 돔비도 아마 아는 사이일 거요—그 일이 나를 형편없이 뒤흔들어 놓고 속을 뒤집어 놓았다고 말했소이다. 이런 종류의 치명적인 파국은 사람으로 하여금 사건이란 게 참으로 섭리적인 방식으로 일어난다는 걸 곱씹게 만든다오,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말한다, \"왜냐하면 만일 내 숙모가 그때 살아 계셨더라면, 그분처럼 지독히도 활달한 여인에게 미쳤을 영향은 그야말로 쇠약일 테고, 그분은 사실상 희생자가 되어 쓰러지고 마셨을 테니 말이외다.\"\n\n\"자, 돔비!—\" 소령이 대단한 기세로 말을 다시 잇는다.\n\n\"실례하오,\" 피닉스 사촌이 끼어든다. \"한마디만 더 하게 해 주시오. 내 친구 돔비가 이런 말을 하도록 허락해 주리라 믿소만, 이번 일로 내가 처해 있는 그 지독히도 고통스러운 상태에 무언가가 더해질 수 있다면, 그건 바로 나의 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 친척이(여전히 그렇게 부르도록 허락을 구해야겠소만), 자기 남편보다 훨씬 신분이 낮은 사람—사실상, 이가 새하얀 사내 말이오—과 그토록 처신해 버렸다고 여겨지는 데 대한 세상의 당연한 경악일 거외다. 하지만 나는 내 친구 돔비에게, 다소 단호하게나마, 나의 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 친척의 죄가 완전히 입증되기 전까지는 그녀를 죄인으로 몰지 말기를 청하는 한편, 내 친구 돔비에게 이것만은 확언해 두고자 하오. 내가 대표하는 가문, 이제 거의 절멸 직전에 이른 그 가문은(사내에게는 지독히도 서글픈 생각이오만) 그의 앞길에 어떤 장애도 놓지 않을 것이며, 앞날을 내다보아 그가 제시할 수 있는 어떤 명예로운 처리 방식에도 기꺼이 동의하리라는 것이오. 나는 내 친구 돔비가 이 지극히 우울한 일에서 나를 움직이고 있는 그 의도를 믿어 주리라 믿소. 그리고—어—사실상, 내 친구 돔비를 더 이상의 소견으로 번거롭게 할 필요는 없을 듯하오.\"\n\n돔비 씨는 눈을 들지 않은 채 고개를 숙이고는 침묵한다.\n\n\"자, 돔비,\" 소령이 말한다, \"우리 친구 피닉스가, 늙은 조 B.가 여태껏 그보다 더 뛰어난 것을 들어본 적이 없는—아니, 맹세코, 선생! 한 번도 없소!\"—소령이 정말이지 시퍼래져서는 지팡이의 한가운데를 움켜쥐며 말한다—\"그 정도의 웅변으로 부인에 관한 사정을 진술했으니, 나는 우리의 우정을 빌려, 돔비, 이 일의 또 다른 측면에 대해 한마디 하겠소. 선생,\" 소령이 그 말울음 같은 기침을 하며 말한다, \"이런 일에 있어 세상은 의견을 가지고 있고, 그건 반드시 충족되어야 하는 거올시다.\"\n\n\"나도 알고 있소,\" 돔비 씨가 대꾸한다.\n\n\"당연히 알고 계시지요, 돔비,\" 소령이 말한다. \"빌어먹을, 선생, 당신이 알고 있다는 걸 나는 알고 있소. 당신만 한 그릇의 사람이 그걸 모를 리가 없지요.\"\n\n\"모르지 않기를 바라오,\" 돔비 씨가 대답한다.\n\n\"돔비!\" 소령이 말한다, \"나머지는 짐작하실 거외다. 나는 솔직히 말하겠소—어쩌면 너무 이른 것일지 모르나—백스톡 집안은 늘 솔직히 말해 왔으니까. 그래서 얻은 게 별로 없긴 합니다, 선생. 하지만 그건 백스톡의 피에 흐르는 거올시다. 이 사내에게 한 발 쏘아야 하오. 당신 곁엔 J. B.가 있소이다. 그는 친구라는 이름을 자처하는 사람이오. 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\"\n\n\"소령,\" 돔비 씨가 대꾸한다, \"고맙소. 때가 오면 당신 손에 나를 맡기겠소. 아직 그때가 오지 않았기에, 나는 당신에게 말을 삼가고 있었던 거요.\"\n\n\"그 작자는 어디 있소, 돔비?\" 소령이 한동안 숨을 헐떡이며 그를 바라본 끝에 묻는다.\n\n\"모르오.\"\n\n\"그자에 관한 무슨 소식이라도 있소?\" 소령이 묻는다.\n\n\"있소.\"\n\n\"돔비, 그 말을 들으니 기쁘오,\" 소령이 말한다. \"축하하오.\"\n\n\"양해해 주시오—소령, 당신마저도,\" 돔비 씨가 대답한다, \"지금으로서는 더 이상의 자세한 이야기로 들어가는 것을 말이오. 그 소식은 기이한 종류의 것이고, 기이하게 얻은 것이오. 별 가치 없는 것으로 드러날 수도 있고, 사실로 드러날 수도 있소. 지금은 뭐라 말할 수 없소. 내 설명은 여기서 멈추어야겠소.\"\n\n이것이 소령의 시뻘건 열의에 비하면 무미건조한 답변이기는 하나, 소령은 그것을 너그럽게 받아들이며, 세상이 곧 마땅한 보상을 받게 될 그토록 좋은 전망이 있다는 생각에 흡족해한다. 그러고는 피닉스 사촌이, 자신의 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 친척의 남편으로부터 그에 합당한 감사의 표시를 받고, 피닉스 사촌과 백스톡 소령은 물러가며, 그 남편을 다시 세상과 마주하도록, 그리고 자기 일에 관한 세상의 심중을 그들이 대변한 바와, 세상의 정당하고 합당한 기대를 한가로이 곱씹도록 남겨 둔다.\n\n그런데 가정부 방에 앉아 눈물을 흘리며, 두 손을 치켜든 채 핍친 부인에게 낮은 목소리로 이야기하고 있는 이는 누구인가? 자기 것이 아닌 듯한, 아주 꼭 끼는 검은 보닛 속에 얼굴을 감춘 한 부인이다. 톡스 양인데, 이 변장을 자기 하녀에게서 빌려, 프린세스 플레이스에서 이렇게 은밀히 찾아와, 돔비 씨의 상태에 관한 어떤 소식을 얻으려고 핍친 부인과의 옛 친분을 되살리려는 참이다.\n\n\"그분은 이 일을 어떻게 견디고 계세요, 여보세요?\" 톡스 양이 묻는다.\n\n\"뭐,\" 핍친 부인이 그 퉁명스러운 투로 말한다, \"거의 평소랑 똑같아요.\"\n\n\"겉으로는 그렇겠지요,\" 톡스 양이 말을 보탠다. \"하지만 속으로 느끼시는 건!\"\n\n핍친 부인의 단단한 잿빛 눈이 미심쩍은 빛을 띠며, 세 번 또렷이 끊어서 대답한다. \"아! 글쎄요. 그렇겠지요.\"\n\n\"솔직히 내 생각을 말하자면, 루크리셔,\" 핍친 부인이 말한다—그녀는 여전히 톡스 양을 루크리셔라고 부르는데, 톡스 양이 가엾고 비쩍 마른 어린 계집아이였던 시절에 아이를 휘어잡는 그 사업의 첫 실험을 바로 이 부인에게 했던 까닭이다—\"솔직히 내 생각을 말하자면, 루크리셔, 잘 떨어져 나갔다 싶어요. 나 같으면 그런 뻔뻔한 낯짝들은 여기 두고 싶지 않으니까!\"\n\n\"뻔뻔하다마다요! 정말 잘 말씀하셨어요, 뻔뻔하다고, 핍친 부인!\" 톡스 양이 대꾸했다. \"그분을 떠나다니! 그렇게 고결한 풍채의 사내를!\" 그러고는 여기서 톡스 양은 감정에 북받친다.\n\n\"고결한지 어떤지는 난 잘 모르겠고, 정말이지,\" 핍친 부인이 짜증스레 코를 문지르며 말한다. \"이건 알지요—사람이 시련을 만나면 그걸 견뎌야 한다는 거요. 어이구, 참! 나도 내 평생에 견딜 만큼 견뎌 봤어요! 무슨 야단법석이람! 그 여자는 가 버렸고, 잘 치워졌고요. 아무도 돌아오기를 바라지 않을 거예요, 내 생각엔!\"\n\n페루 광산을 암시하는 이 말에 톡스 양이 떠나려고 일어서자, 핍친 부인은 종을 울려 타울린슨을 불러 그녀를 바래다주게 한다. 타울린슨 씨는 톡스 양을 오랜만에 본지라 씨익 웃으며 안녕하시냐고 인사하고는, 그 보닛 차림 때문에 처음엔 못 알아봤다고 말한다.\n\n\"그럭저럭 잘 지내요, 타울린슨, 고마워요,\" 톡스 양이 말한다. \"부탁이 있는데, 혹시 여기서 나를 보게 되더라도 그 얘긴 하지 말아 줘요. 내가 찾아오는 건 그저 핍친 부인을 뵈러 오는 것뿐이니까요.\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다, 아가씨,\" 타울린슨이 말한다.\n\n\"끔찍한 일들이 다 일어나는군요, 타울린슨,\" 톡스 양이 말한다.\n\n\"정말이지 그렇습죠, 아가씨,\" 타울린슨이 대꾸한다.\n\n\"나는 바라요, 타울린슨,\" 투들 가족을 가르치는 동안 훈계조의 말투와 지나가는 기회를 놓치지 않고 교화하려는 버릇을 익힌 톡스 양이 말한다, \"여기서 일어난 일이 당신에게 하나의 경고가 되기를 말이에요, 타울린슨.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 아가씨, 그럼요,\" 타울린슨이 말한다.\n\n그는 이 경고가 자기 같은 처지에서는 어떤 식으로 작용해야 마땅할지 곰곰이 생각하기 시작하는 듯한데, 그때 식초처럼 시큼한 핍친 부인이 불쑥 그를 들쑤시며 \"뭘 하고 있는 거예요? 어째서 아가씨를 문까지 모셔다 드리지 않고?\" 하고 다그치자, 그는 톡스 양을 밖으로 안내한다. 돔비 씨의 방을 지날 때 그녀는 검은 보닛 속 가장 깊숙한 데로 움츠러들어 발끝으로 걷는다. 그리고 그를 그토록 떠나지 않고 따라다니면서, 그에 대해 그토록 큰 슬픔과 염려를 품은 존재는 세상에 또 없으니, 톡스 양은 그 마음을 검은 보닛 속에 담아 거리로 가지고 나가, 새로 켜진 가로등 불빛으로부터 그것을 그늘 속에 감춰 집으로 가져가려 애쓴다.\n\n그러나 톡스 양은 돔비 씨의 세상의 일부가 아니다. 그녀는 저녁 어스름이면 매일 다시 찾아온다. 비 오는 밤에는 보닛에 덧신과 우산을 보태고서. 그리고 타울린슨의 씨익 웃는 낯과 핍친 부인의 핀잔과 매정한 거절을 견뎌 낸다. 모두가 그저 그분이 어떻게 지내시는지, 그분이 불행을 어떻게 견디시는지 물어보기 위함이다. 하지만 그녀는 돔비 씨의 세상과는 아무 상관이 없다. 그 세상은 예전처럼 까다롭고 들볶아 대며 그녀 없이도 굴러간다. 그리고 그녀는, 결코 밝지도 특출나지도 않은 하나의 별로서, 또 다른 어떤 체계의 한구석에서 제 자그마한 궤도를 돌며, 그것을 아주 잘 알고서, 찾아오고, 울고, 떠나가며, 그것으로 만족한다. 참으로 톡스 양은 돔비 씨를 그토록 괴롭히는 세상보다 훨씬 만족시키기 쉬운 사람이다!\n\n회계 사무소에서는 사무원들이 그 엄청난 사달을 온갖 명암으로 따져 보지만, 무엇보다도 카커 씨의 자리를 누가 차지할지를 궁금해한다. 그들은 대체로 그 자리가 보수의 일부를 깎이고, 새로 고안된 견제와 제약으로 불편해지리라는 의견이다. 그리고 그 자리에 대한 일말의 희망조차 없는 자들은 차라리 그 자리를 맡지 않는 편이 낫겠다고, 그것이 누구 차지가 되든 조금도 부럽지 않다고 단언한다. 돔비 씨의 어린 아들이 죽은 이래로 회계 사무소에서 지금처럼 들끓는 동요는 없었다. 그러나 그곳의 그런 흥분은 모두 사교적인, 아니 흥겹다고까지 할 만한 방향으로 흘러, 좋은 친목을 다지는 데로 이어진다. 이 상서로운 계기에 회계 사무소에서 인정받는 익살꾼과, 몇 달째 그와 사생결단의 반목을 벌여 온 야심 찬 경쟁자 사이에 화해가 이루어진다. 그리고 다행히 되살아난 그들의 우정을 기념하여 조촐한 만찬이 제안되어, 이웃 선술집에서 열리는데, 익살꾼이 좌장석에 앉고 경쟁자가 부좌장 노릇을 한다. 식탁보가 치워진 뒤 이어지는 연설은 좌장이 열며, 그는 말하길, 여러분, 지금이 사사로운 불화를 다툴 때가 아님을 자기 스스로도 숨길 수 없노라고 한다. 굳이 더 자세히 언급할 필요는 없으나 몇몇 일요 신문과, 굳이 이름을 댈 필요 없는 어떤 일간지에서도 더러 다루어진 바 있는 최근의 사건들이(여기서 좌중의 다른 모두가 알아들을 만한 웅얼거림으로 그 신문 이름을 댄다) 자기로 하여금 곰곰이 생각하게 만들었노라고 한다. 그리고 자기와 로빈슨이 이런 순간에 사사로운 갈등을 품는다면, 그것은 돔비 회사의 신사 여러분이 늘 두드러져 왔다고 자기가 생각하고 또 바랄 만한 그 대의에 대한 선량한 마음씨를 영원히 부정하는 일이 되리라고 느낀다는 것이다. 로빈슨은 이에 한 사람의 사내답게, 또 한 형제답게 화답한다. 그리고 산수 실수 탓에 끊임없이 해고 통보를 받아 가며 삼 년째 사무소에 있던 한 신사가 완전히 새로운 면모를 드러내어, 짜릿한 연설을 불쑥 터뜨리는데, 그 가운데 그는, 우리의 존경하는 우두머리께서 그분의 가정에 닥친 그 황폐함을 다시는 겪지 않으시기를! 하고 말하고, \"그분께서 다시는\" 으로 시작하는 갖가지 말을 잇따라 늘어놓으니, 우레와 같은 박수갈채로 환영받는다. 요컨대 더없이 유쾌한 저녁이 흘러가는데, 다만 카커 씨의 지난 연간 수입이 대략 얼마나 되었을지를 두고 다투던 두 풋내기 사이의 언쟁에 잠시 가로막힐 뿐이니, 이들은 디캔터를 들고 서로를 위협하다가 잔뜩 흥분한 채로 끌려 나간다. 이튿날 사무소에서는 소다수가 두루 찾는 물건이 되고, 그 일행 대다수는 청구서를 바가지라고 여긴다.\n\n심부름꾼 퍼치로 말하자면, 그는 평생 신세를 망칠 판이다. 그는 다시 또 노상 술집 카운터에 처박혀, 한턱을 얻어먹고는 끔찍하게 거짓말을 늘어놓는다. 들리는 바로는, 그가 어디서든 지난 사달에 관련된 사람이라면 누구든 만났고, 그들에게 경우에 따라 \"선생님\" 혹은 \"마님\" 하고는 \"어째서 그리 안색이 창백하십니까?\" 하고 물었다는 것인데, 그러면 저마다 머리끝부터 발끝까지 부르르 떨며 \"오, 퍼치!\" 하고는 달아났다는 것이다. 이런 엄청난 짓들에 대한 자각 탓인지, 아니면 술 마신 뒤의 반동 탓인지, 퍼치 씨는 평소 발스 폰드에서 퍼치 부인과 함께하며 위안을 찾던 저녁 그 시각이면 극도의 의기소침에 빠진다. 그리고 퍼치 부인은 적잖이 애를 태우니, 이제 그가 여자에 대한 믿음이 흔들려, 밤에 집에 돌아오면 자기가 어느 자작과 눈이 맞아 달아나 버린 것을 발견하리라 반쯤 예상하고 있는 게 아닌가 두려운 까닭이다—\"이게 말이에요,\" 그녀가 어느 막역한 여자 친구에게 말하듯, \"여자 탈을 쓴 그런 못된 것들이 책임져야 할 일이라니까요, 피 부인. 그것들이 제 몸에 끼치는 해악보다도, 그것들이 우리한테 어떻게 비치게 만드느냐가 문제예요, 마님. 그리고 그게 퍼치의 눈에 보이거든요.\"\n\n같은 시기에 돔비 씨의 하인들은 아주 방탕해져서, 다른 일자리에는 쓸모없게 되어 가고 있다. 그들은 밤마다 거한 야식을 들고, 식탁에 김 나는 술을 올려놓고 \"그 얘기를 곱씹는다.\" 타울린슨 씨는 열 시 반만 지나면 늘 눈물을 글썽이며, 모퉁이 집에 사는 것은 결코 좋은 일이 못 된다고 자기가 말하지 않았느냐고 거듭 묻곤 한다. 그들은 플로렌스 아가씨에 대해 수군대며, 그녀가 어디 있을지 궁금해한다. 하지만 돔비 씨가 모른다면 돔비 부인은 알 거라는 데 의견을 모은다. 이 말이 그들을 그 부인 이야기로 이끄니, 요리사가 이르길, 그분은 그래도 위풍당당한 데가 있었잖아요, 안 그래요? 하지만 너무 도도했죠! 모두가 그녀는 너무 도도했다는 데 동의하고, 타울린슨 씨의 옛 연인이었던 (아주 정숙한) 하녀는, 마치 땅바닥이 제 발을 디디기에도 모자란다는 듯 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들고 다니는 사람들 얘기는 자기한테 두 번 다시 꺼내지 말아 달라고 간청한다.\n\n이 일에 관해 입에 오르고 행해지는 모든 것은, 돔비 씨를 제외하고는, 합창으로 이루어진다. 돔비 씨와 세상만이 단둘이 마주하고 있다.\n\n# CHAPTER LII. Secret Intelligence\n\n착한 브라운 부인과 그녀의 딸 앨리스는 자기네 거처에서 말없이 함께 앉아 있었다. 이른 저녁이었고, 봄도 깊어 가는 무렵이었다. 돔비 씨가 배그스톡 소령에게 그 기묘한 정보에 관해—기묘하게 손에 넣은, 아무 가치도 없는 것으로 드러날 수도 있고 사실로 드러날 수도 있는 그 정보에 관해—이야기한 지 며칠 지나지 않았고, 세상은 아직 만족하지 못한 채였다.\n\n어머니와 딸은 오랫동안 한마디도 주고받지 않은 채 앉아 있었다. 거의 미동조차 없었다. 노파의 얼굴은 약삭빠르게 초조하고 기대에 차 있었다. 딸의 얼굴 역시 기대에 차 있었으나 그 정도는 덜 날카로웠고, 때때로 그것은 차츰 짙어지는 실망과 불신이라도 어린 듯 어두워지곤 했다. 노파는 그 표정의 변화에 아랑곳하지 않았는데, 비록 눈은 자주 그쪽을 향했으나, 그저 입을 우물거리고 무언가를 씹으며 확신에 차서 귀를 기울이고 앉아 있었다.\n\n그들의 거처는 비록 가난하고 비참했으나, 착한 브라운 부인 혼자 살던 시절만큼 완전히 처참하지는 않았다. 청결과 정돈을 위한 몇 가지 시도가 엿보였는데, 다만 그것이 무심하고 집시 같은 방식으로 이루어진 터라 한눈에 보아도 그 젊은 여인과 결부 지을 만한 것이었다. 두 사람이 침묵을 지키는 사이 저녁 그림자가 짙어지고 깊어져, 마침내 거뭇해진 벽이 사방에 깔린 어둠 속에 거의 묻혀 버렸다.\n\n그때 앨리스가 그토록 오래 이어진 침묵을 깨고 말했다.\n\n\"포기하셔도 돼요, 어머니. 그 사람 여기 안 와요.\"\n\n\"죽어도 안 포기해!\" 노파가 조바심을 내며 응수했다. \"그자는 여기 올 거야.\"\n\n\"두고 보면 알겠죠,\" 앨리스가 말했다.\n\n\"그자를 보게 될 거다,\" 어머니가 응수했다.\n\n\"심판의 날에나요,\" 딸이 말했다.\n\n\"내가 노망이 들었다고 생각하는 게지, 다 안다!\" 노파가 쉰 소리로 외쳤다. \"그게 내 친딸한테서 받는 존경이고 효도란 말이지. 하지만 난 네가 생각하는 것보다 더 영리해. 그자는 올 거야. 며칠 전에 길에서 내가 그자의 외투를 건드렸더니, 마치 내가 두꺼비라도 되는 양 돌아보더구나. 하지만 세상에, 내가 그 이름들을 입에 올리며 그 둘이 어디 있는지 알아내고 싶지 않으냐고 물었을 때 그자의 얼굴을 봤어야 하는데!\"\n\n\"그렇게 화를 냈어요?\" 딸이 순식간에 흥미를 느껴 정신이 번쩍 들어 물었다.\n\n\"화를 내? 피라도 봤느냐고 물어라. 그게 더 어울리는 말이지. 화를 내? 하, 하! 그걸 고작 화났다고 하다니!\" 노파는 절뚝거리며 찬장으로 가서 초에 불을 붙였는데, 그 초를 탁자로 가져오자 그녀의 입놀림이 보기 흉할 정도로 또렷이 드러났다. \"그 둘에 대해 네가 생각하거나 말할 때 그 얼굴을 두고도 그저 화났다고밖에 못 할 게다.\"\n\n사실 그것은 그저 화난 것과는 달랐으니, 그녀는 웅크린 암호랑이처럼 꼼짝 않고 앉아 두 눈을 이글거리고 있었다.\n\n\"쉿!\" 노파가 의기양양하게 말했다. \"발소리가 들려. 여기 사는 사람이나 이쪽으로 자주 오는 사람의 걸음걸이가 아니야. 우리는 저렇게 안 걷지. 저런 이웃이 있으면 우리도 우쭐해지련만! 저 소리 들리니?\"\n\n\"어머니 말씀이 맞는 것 같아요,\" 앨리스가 낮은 목소리로 대답했다. \"조용히! 문을 여세요.\"\n\n앨리스가 숄을 끌어당겨 몸을 감싸자, 노파는 그 말에 따랐다. 그러고는 밖을 내다보며 손짓해 돔비 씨를 들였다. 돔비 씨는 문 안에 발을 들여놓고는 걸음을 멈추고 미심쩍은 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"나리 같은 훌륭한 신사분께는 누추한 곳입지요,\" 노파가 절을 하며 재잘거렸다. \"그렇게 말씀드렸습니다만, 해될 건 없습죠.\"\n\n\"저건 누구요?\" 돔비 씨가 그녀의 동행을 보며 물었다.\n\n\"제 어여쁜 딸이옵니다,\" 노파가 말했다. \"나리께서 신경 쓰실 것 없습니다. 저 애는 다 알고 있으니까요.\"\n\n그의 얼굴에 그림자가 드리웠으니, \"다 알지 못하는 자가 어디 있겠는가!\" 하고 소리 내어 신음한 것이나 다름없이 분명한 표정이었다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 똑바로 바라보았고, 그녀 역시 그의 존재를 아랑곳하지 않고 그를 바라보았다. 그가 그녀에게서 시선을 거두자 얼굴의 그림자는 한층 짙어졌고, 그러고 나서도 그 시선은 슬그머니 다시 그녀에게로 되돌아갔으니, 마치 그녀의 당돌한 눈빛과 그것이 불러일으키는 어떤 기억에 사로잡히기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 돔비 씨가 자기 팔꿈치 곁에서 낄낄거리며 곁눈질하는 늙은 마녀에게 말했다. 그녀는 그가 몸을 돌려 말을 건네자 슬그머니 제 딸을 가리키고는 두 손을 비비더니 다시 가리켰다. \"이보게! 내가 여기 온 것은 나 자신이 약해서, 내 신분을 잊은 짓임을 알고 있네. 그러나 자네는 내가 왜 왔는지, 그리고 며칠 전 길에서 나를 멈춰 세웠을 때 무엇을 제안했는지 알 게야. 내가 알고자 하는 것에 관하여 자네가 내게 들려줄 게 무엇인가. 또 어찌하여 내가 권력과 재력을 다 쏟고도 헛되이 얻지 못한 정보를, 이런 누추한 오두막에서 제 발로 얻을 수 있단 말인가,\" 그는 경멸하는 눈길로 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"내 생각엔,\" 그는 그녀를 엄하게 살핀 뒤 잠시 사이를 두었다가 말했다, \"자네가 나를 가지고 장난을 치거나 속이려 들 만큼 뻔뻔하지는 않을 게야. 그러나 만일 그런 속셈이라면, 그 계략의 문턱에서 멈추는 편이 나을 게야. 내 기분은 장난칠 만한 것이 못 되고, 내 보답은 가혹할 테니까.\"\n\n\"오, 거만하고 모진 나리시여!\" 노파가 고개를 가로젓고 쪼글쪼글한 손을 비비며 낄낄거렸다. \"오, 모질고 모질고 모질도다! 하지만 나리께서는 우리 눈이 아니라 나리 두 눈으로 직접 보시고 나리 두 귀로 직접 들으실 겁니다. 그리고 나리께서 그들의 자취를 좇게 되시면, 그 대가로 뭐든 좀 치르시는 것쯤은 마다하지 않으시겠지요, 안 그렇습니까, 귀하신 나리?\"\n\n\"돈이라면,\" 돔비 씨가 이 물음에 안도하고 확신을 얻은 듯 응수했다, \"있을 법하지 않은 일도 이루어 낸다는 것을 나도 아네. 이처럼 뜻밖이고 가망 없어 보이는 수단조차 쓸모 있게 만들 수도 있겠지. 그래. 믿을 만한 정보를 받는다면 무엇이든 값을 치르겠네. 그러나 먼저 그 정보를 손에 넣고, 그 가치를 내 스스로 판단해야겠네.\"\n\n\"돈보다 더 강한 것을 모르시나요?\" 젊은 여인이 일어서지도 자세를 바꾸지도 않은 채 물었다.\n\n\"여기서는 없을 듯싶군,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"다른 곳에서는 더 강한 무언가가 있다는 걸 아셔야 할 텐데요, 제가 보기엔,\" 그녀가 응수했다. \"여자의 분노라는 걸 모르시나요?\"\n\n\"입버릇이 건방진 계집이로구나,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"평소엔 안 그래요,\" 그녀가 아무 감정도 드러내지 않고 대답했다. \"지금 당신에게 말하는 것은, 우리를 더 잘 이해하고 우리를 더 믿게 하려는 거예요. 여자의 분노란 여기서나 당신의 훌륭한 저택에서나 거의 똑같답니다. 나는 화가 나 있어요. 여러 해 동안 그래 왔지요. 내가 화낼 까닭은 당신 못지않게 충분하고, 그 대상은 똑같은 남자예요.\"\n\n그는 자기도 모르게 흠칫하며 놀란 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그래요,\" 그녀가 일종의 웃음과 함께 말했다. \"우리 사이가 아무리 멀어 보여도, 사실이 그래요. 어찌하여 그런지는 중요치 않아요. 그건 내 사연이고, 내 사연은 나 혼자 간직할 테니까. 나는 당신과 그자를 한자리에 끌어다 놓으려 해요. 그자에게 격렬한 분노를 품고 있으니까요. 저기 계신 우리 어머니는 욕심 많고 가난한 분이에요. 어머니라면 주워 모을 수 있는 어떤 소식이든, 어떤 것이든, 어떤 사람이든, 돈을 받고 팔아 치울 거예요. 어머니가 당신이 알고 싶어 하는 것을 손에 넣게 도와준다면, 어머니께 얼마쯤 치르는 게 온당하기는 하겠죠. 하지만 그건 내 동기가 아니에요. 내 동기가 무엇인지는 이미 말했고, 설령 당신이 어머니와 육 펜스를 두고 흥정하고 깎아 댄다 해도 그 동기는 내게 똑같이 강하고 충분하답니다. 나는 할 말을 다 했어요. 내 건방진 혀는 더는 말하지 않을 거예요, 당신이 내일 해 뜰 때까지 여기서 기다린다 해도요.\"\n\n이 말이 자기가 기대하던 벌이를 깎아내리는 쪽으로 흐르자 적잖이 불안해하던 노파는, 돔비 씨의 소매를 살며시 잡아당기며 딸의 말은 신경 쓰지 말라고 속삭였다. 그는 초췌한 얼굴로 두 사람을 번갈아 노려보더니, 평소보다 한층 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"계속해 보게—무엇을 알고 있는가?\"\n\n\"오, 그리 서두르지 마십쇼, 나리! 누군가를 기다려야 합니다요,\" 노파가 대답했다. \"그건 다른 사람한테서 얻어 내야 하는 겁니다—파내야지요—쥐어짜고 비틀어서 그자한테서 끄집어내야 한단 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소린가?\" 돔비 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"인내심을 가지셔야지요,\" 그녀는 갈고리 같은 손을 그의 팔 위에 얹으며 쉰 소리로 말했다. \"인내심을요. 제가 알아내겠습니다. 할 수 있다는 걸 압니다요! 그자가 그걸 내게서 감추려 든다면,\" 착한 브라운 부인이 열 손가락을 갈고리처럼 구부리며 말했다, \"그자한테서 뜯어내고야 말 겁니다!\"\n\n돔비 씨는 그녀가 절뚝거리며 문으로 가서 다시 밖을 내다보는 동안 눈으로 그녀를 좇았다. 그러고는 그 시선이 그녀의 딸을 찾았다. 그러나 그녀는 무표정하고 말없이, 그에게 아랑곳하지 않은 채 그대로 있었다.\n\n\"이보게, 자네 말은,\" 그는 브라운 부인의 굽은 형체가 고개를 가로저으며 혼자 재잘거리며 돌아오자 말했다, \"여기 또 다른 사람이 오기로 되어 있다는 건가?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다!\" 노파가 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그자에게서 내게 쓸모 있을 정보를 캐낼 작정이고?\"\n\n\"그렇습죠,\" 노파가 다시 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"낯선 자인가?\"\n\n\"체!\" 노파가 날카로운 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"그게 무슨 상관이람! 그래, 그래, 아닙니다. 나리께 낯선 자는 아닙죠. 하지만 그자는 나리를 못 봅니다. 나리를 무서워해서 입을 안 열 테니까요. 나리는 저 문 뒤에 서 계시면서, 그자를 직접 판단하시면 됩니다. 우리는 그냥 믿어 달라고 청하는 게 아닙니다. 뭐라굽쇼! 나리께선 저 문 뒤의 방이 미심쩍으신 게로군요? 오, 부자 양반들의 의심이라니! 그럼, 들여다보십쇼.\"\n\n그녀의 날카로운 눈은 그가 본의 아니게 드러낸 이 감정의 기색을 알아챘는데, 그것은 이런 상황에서 무리도 아니었다. 그녀는 그 감정을 풀어 주려고 자기가 말한 문으로 초를 가져갔다. 돔비 씨는 안을 들여다보고 그것이 텅 비고 다 쓰러져 가는 방임을 확인하더니, 초를 제자리에 도로 갖다 놓으라고 손짓했다.\n\n\"얼마나 있어야,\" 그가 물었다, \"이 사람이 오는가?\"\n\n\"오래 안 걸립니다,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"나리께서 잠깐 짬을 내어 앉아 계시겠습니까?\"\n\n그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 머무를지 떠날지 결단을 내리지 못한 듯, 또 애초에 여기 와 있다는 사실로 자기 자신과 다투기라도 하는 듯, 망설이는 기색으로 방 안을 거닐기 시작했다. 그러나 곧 그의 발걸음은 더 느리고 무거워졌고, 그의 얼굴은 한층 엄숙하게 생각에 잠겼다. 그가 여기 온 목적이 마음속에 또렷이 자리 잡고 다시금 부풀어 올랐기 때문이었다.\n\n그가 이렇게 눈을 바닥에 두고 이리저리 거니는 동안, 그를 맞으려고 일어났던 의자에 다시 앉은 브라운 부인은 새로이 귀를 기울였다. 그의 단조로운 발소리 탓인지, 늙은 탓에 흐릿해진 귀 탓인지, 그녀는 듣는 게 어찌나 더딘지, 바깥의 발소리가 딸의 귀에는 이미 몇 순간 동안 울렸고 딸이 황급히 고개를 들어 그 다가옴을 어머니에게 알리려 한 뒤에야 비로소 노파가 그 소리에 정신을 차렸다. 그러나 그제야 그녀는 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 \"그자가 왔다!\" 하고 속삭이고는 손님을 그 망보는 자리로 서둘러 데려갔고, 술병과 잔을 탁자 위에 놓되 어찌나 재빠른지 문간에 모습을 드러낸 연마공 롭의 목에 두 팔을 두를 채비까지 갖추었다.\n\n\"드디어 내 어여쁜 아들이 왔구나,\" 브라운 부인이 외쳤다, \"마침내!—오호, 오호! 넌 꼭 내 친아들 같다니까, 로비!\"\n\n\"오! 브라운 아주머니!\" 연마공이 항의했다. \"이러지 마세요! 사람을 짓누르고 목을 조르지 않고선 좋아할 수가 없는 거예요? 내 손에 든 새장 좀 조심하라니까요?\"\n\n\"나보다 새장을 먼저 챙기다니!\" 노파가 천장을 향해 부르짖었다. \"어미보다 더한 정을 이 애한테 쏟는 나를 두고!\"\n\n\"아니, 정말이지 저도 아주머니께 무척 감사해요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 불운한 청년이 잔뜩 짜증을 내며 말했다, \"하지만 아주머닌 사람한테 너무 집착하신다니까요. 저도 물론 아주머니를 무척 좋아하고, 다 그렇긴 하지만요. 그래도 전 아주머니를 숨 막히게 짓누르진 않잖아요, 안 그래요, 브라운 아주머니?\"\n\n하긴 그 표정과 말투를 보면, 적당한 기회만 있다면 그렇게 짓누르는 것도 결코 마다하지 않을 듯했다.\n\n\"새장 얘기까지 꺼내다니!\" 연마공이 훌쩍거렸다. \"그게 무슨 죄라도 되는 양! 자, 여기 좀 보세요! 이게 누구 건지 아세요?\"\n\n\"주인 양반 거겠지, 얘야?\" 노파가 씩 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"맞아요!\" 연마공이 천으로 싼 커다란 새장을 탁자 위로 들어 올리며, 이로 물고 손으로 풀어 헤치며 대답했다. \"이건 우리 앵무새예요, 이게.\"\n\n\"카커 씨의 앵무새냐, 롭?\"\n\n\"입 좀 다물어 주시겠어요, 브라운 아주머니?\" 다그침에 시달린 연마공이 응수했다. \"왜 자꾸 이름을 들먹이는 거예요? 정말이지,\" 롭이 격해진 감정에 두 손으로 머리를 쥐어뜯으며 말했다, \"이 아주머닌 사람을 미치게 만들기 딱이라니까!\"\n\n\"뭐! 이 배은망덕한 놈, 나한테 핀잔을 줘!\" 노파가 곧장 사납게 외쳤다.\n\n\"맙소사, 브라운 아주머니, 아니에요!\" 연마공이 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 응수했다. \"이렇게 사람이—! 제가 아주머니를 끔찍이 아끼지 않나요, 브라운 아주머니?\"\n\n\"정말이냐, 귀여운 롭아? 진짜로 그러냐, 우리 병아리야?\" 그 말과 함께 브라운 부인은 그를 다시 한번 다정한 포옹으로 끌어안았고, 그가 다리로 몇 차례 격렬하고도 헛된 발버둥을 치고 머리카락이 온통 곤두설 때까지 놓아주지 않았다.\n\n\"오!\" 연마공이 응수했다, \"이렇게 사랑 공세에 흠씬 두들겨 맞다니 이게 무슨 꼴이람. 차라리 저 아주머니가—요즘 어떻게 지내셨어요, 브라운 아주머니?\"\n\n\"아! 지난주 오늘 밤 이후로 통 안 왔잖니!\" 노파가 책망하는 눈길로 그를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 브라운 아주머니,\" 연마공이 응수했다, \"오늘이 일주일째니까 오늘 밤에 오겠다고 했잖아요, 안 그랬어요? 그래서 이렇게 왔잖아요. 어쩜 그러세요! 좀 사리에 맞게 구셨으면 좋겠어요, 브라운 아주머니. 변명하느라 목이 다 쉬었고, 끌어안기는 통에 얼굴이 다 번들거린다니까요!\" 그는 마치 문제의 그 부드러운 윤기라도 닦아내려는 듯 소매로 얼굴을 박박 문질렀다.\n\n\"한 모금 마시고 기운 차려라, 우리 로빈,\" 노파가 병에서 잔에 술을 따라 그에게 건네며 말했다.\n\n\"고마워요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 연마공이 응수했다. \"아주머니 건강을 위해. 그리고 오래오래—기타 등등.\" 그 표정으로 미루어 보아, 그 말에 그다지 정성스러운 축복이 담긴 것 같지는 않았다. \"그리고 이 아가씨 건강을 위해,\" 연마공이 앨리스를 흘끗 보며 말했는데, 그녀는 그의 눈에는 그의 뒤쪽 벽에 시선을 못 박고 앉아 있는 듯 보였으나 실은 문가의 돔비 씨 얼굴을 보고 있었다, \"이 아가씨께도 같은 걸, 그것도 듬뿍 빕니다!\"\n\n그는 이 두 가지 축원에 잔을 비우고는 그것을 내려놓았다.\n\n\"자, 이봐요, 브라운 아주머니!\" 그가 이어 말했다. \"이제 좀 사리에 맞게 얘기해 봐요. 아주머닌 새에 대해 잘 아시고, 새 다루는 법에도 밝으시잖아요. 제가 톡톡히 대가를 치르고 알게 된 일이지만요.\"\n\n\"대가라고!\" 브라운 부인이 되받았다.\n\n\"만족 말이에요,\" 연마공이 응수했다. \"어쩜 그렇게 사람 말꼬리를 잡으세요, 브라운 아주머니! 또 머릿속이 새하얘졌잖아요.\"\n\n\"새에 밝다고 했지, 로비,\" 노파가 거들었다.\n\n\"맞아요!\" 연마공이 말했다. \"그게요, 제가 이 앵무새를 돌봐야 하게 됐어요—이런저런 물건이 팔리고, 어떤 살림이 다 흩어지는 바람에요—그리고 지금은 남의 눈에 띄고 싶지가 않아서, 아주머니가 한 일주일쯤 이 녀석을 봐주시고 먹이고 재워 주셨으면 하는데, 그래 주시겠어요? 제가 어차피 들락날락해야 한다면,\" 연마공이 풀 죽은 얼굴로 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다, \"올 만한 구실이라도 있는 편이 낫겠죠.\"\n\n\"올 만한 구실이라고?\" 노파가 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"아주머니 말고요, 제 말은요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 비굴한 롭이 응수했다. \"물론 아주머니 말고 다른 구실이 필요하다는 건 아니에요, 정말로요, 브라운 아주머니. 제발 또 시작하진 마세요.\"\n\n\"이 애는 날 좋아하지 않아! 내가 이 애를 위하는 만큼 날 위해 주질 않아!\" 브라운 부인이 앙상한 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"하지만 그 새는 잘 돌봐 주마.\"\n\n\"그것도 정말 잘 돌봐 주셔야 해요, 아시겠죠, 브라운 아주머니,\" 롭이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"혹시라도 깃털을 한 번이라도 결 반대로 쓰다듬으셨다간, 들통날 거라고요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그렇게나 빈틈없다더냐, 롭?\" 브라운 부인이 잽싸게 물었다.\n\n\"빈틈없냐고요, 브라운 아주머니!\" 롭이 되뇌었다. \"하지만 이건 입에 올릴 게 아니에요.\"\n\n불쑥 말을 멈추고는, 방 건너편으로 겁먹은 눈길을 던지는 것도 잊지 않은 채, 롭은 다시 잔을 채워 천천히 비우고 고개를 가로저었다. 그러고는 방금 입에 올린 그 위험한 화제에서 벗어나 보려는 셈으로, 손가락을 앵무새 새장 철망에 대고 이리저리 긁기 시작했다.\n\n노파는 그를 곁눈으로 슬쩍 살피더니, 의자를 그에게 더 가까이 끌어당기고는, 자기가 부르자 금빛 둥근 지붕에서 내려온 앵무새를 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"이제 일자리가 없는 게냐, 로비?\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마세요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 연마공이 퉁명스레 응수했다.\n\n\"그럼 그냥 식대만 받고 있는 거냐, 롭?\" 브라운 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"예쁜 폴리야!\" 연마공이 말했다.\n\n노파가 그에게 흘긴 눈길은, 귀때기가 위험하다는 걸 알아차리라는 경고일 법했으나, 이번엔 그가 앵무새를 들여다볼 차례라, 그의 상상 속에서 그녀의 성난 찌푸림이 아무리 생생했을지언정 그의 육안에는 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"주인 양반이 너를 안 데려간 게 이상하구나, 롭,\" 노파가 살살 구슬리는 목소리로, 그러나 한층 악의 어린 표정으로 말했다.\n\n롭은 앵무새를 들여다보고 철망에 집게손가락을 굴리는 데 어찌나 몰두했던지 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n노파는 탁자 위로 숙인 그의 더부룩한 머리채를 머리카락 한 올 차이로 움켜쥐려다가, 손가락을 거두고는, 어르려 애쓰느라 목이 메는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"로비야, 우리 아가.\"\n\n\"왜요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 연마공이 응수했다.\n\n\"주인 양반이 너를 안 데려간 게 이상하다고 했다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마세요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 연마공이 응수했다.\n\n브라운 부인은 즉시 오른손으로는 그의 머리채를, 왼손으로는 그의 목을 움켜쥐고는, 그 애지중지하는 대상을 어찌나 무시무시한 기세로 붙들었던지, 그의 얼굴이 삽시간에 거뭇해지기 시작했다.\n\n\"브라운 아주머니!\" 연마공이 소리쳤다, \"놓으세요, 좀! 뭐 하는 거예요? 사람 살려, 아가씨! 브라운 아주—주—!\"\n\n그러나 젊은 여인은 그의 직접적인 호소에도, 그의 알아들을 수 없는 발버둥에도 똑같이 꿈쩍하지 않은 채 그저 가만히 있었다. 마침내 롭이 자기를 덮친 노파와 실랑이하며 한쪽 구석으로 몰린 끝에 빠져나와, 두 팔꿈치로 제 몸을 막아 가며 숨을 헐떡이며 거기 섰고, 노파 역시 숨을 헐떡이며 분노와 조바심에 발을 구르며, 그에게 또 한 번 덮칠 기운을 그러모으는 듯 보였다. 이 위기의 순간에 앨리스가 끼어들어 한마디 했으나, 연마공 편을 드는 말은 아니었다.\n\n\"잘하셨어요, 어머니. 그자를 갈가리 찢어 버리세요!\"\n\n\"뭐예요, 아가씨!\" 롭이 엉엉 울었다. \"아가씨까지 내 편이 아니란 거예요? 내가 뭘 어쨌다고요? 내가 왜 갈가리 찢겨야 하는지 알고나 싶네요? 두 분 다 아무 해도 끼친 적 없는 사람을 왜 붙잡고 목을 조르는 거예요? 그러고도 여자라고요!\" 겁에 질려 시달린 연마공이 외투 소맷부리를 눈에 갖다 대며 말했다. \"정말 어이가 없네요! 여자의 다정함은 어디 갔어요?\"\n\n\"이 배은망덕한 개 같은 놈!\" 브라운 부인이 헐떡이며 말했다. \"건방지고 모욕적인 개 같은 놈!\"\n\n\"제가 뭘 어쨌다고 아주머니를 화나게 했다는 거예요, 브라운 아주머니?\" 겁먹은 롭이 받아쳤다. \"조금 전만 해도 저한테 무척 정이 깊으셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"퉁명스러운 대답에 골난 말로 내 말을 싹둑 잘라 버리다니,\" 노파가 말했다. \"이 나를! 내가 어쩌다 주인 양반하고 그 마님 얘기로 잡담이나 좀 나누고 싶어졌기로서니, 감히 이 나를 데리고 이랬다저랬다 장난을 쳐! 하지만 이제 너랑은 더 말 안 한다, 이놈아. 자, 가거라!\"\n\n\"정말로요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 비굴한 연마공이 응수했다, \"전 가고 싶다는 내색은 한 번도 안 했어요. 제발 그렇게 말씀하지 마세요, 브라운 아주머니.\"\n\n\"난 아예 말을 안 하겠다,\" 브라운 부인이 갈고리 같은 손가락을 놀리며 말했는데, 그 바람에 그는 구석에서 제 몸뚱이를 절반으로 움츠렸다. \"저 녀석하고는 더 이상 한마디도 안 한다. 배은망덕한 개 같은 놈. 내가 내쳤다. 자, 가게 둬라! 그리고 내가 그 뒤꽁무니에 말 많은 자들을 붙여 줄 테다. 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 자들, 거머리처럼 들러붙고 여우처럼 슬금슬금 뒤를 밟을 자들을. 뭐! 저 녀석도 그 작자들을 알지. 제 옛날 짓거리며 옛날 수법도 알고. 잊어버렸다 해도 그 작자들이 곧 일깨워 줄 거다. 자, 이제 가게 둬라, 그리고 저 녀석이 그런 패거리한테 늘 이리저리 따라붙이며 주인 양반 일을 어떻게 해내고 주인 양반 비밀을 어떻게 지키는지 두고 보자고. 하, 하, 하! 그 작자들은 너나 나랑은 영 다른 부류임을 알게 될 게다, 앨리. 저 녀석이 너나 나한테는 그렇게 입을 꼭 다물지만. 자, 이제 가게 둬라, 어서 가게 둬!\"\n\n노파는 연마공이 이루 말할 수 없이 당황하는 가운데, 그 비틀린 형체로 지름 사 피트쯤 되는 원을 빙글빙글 돌며, 끊임없이 이 말들을 되풀이하고, 머리 위로 주먹을 흔들고, 입을 우물거렸다.\n\n\"브라운 아주머니,\" 롭이 구석에서 조금 나오며 애원했다, \"다시 생각해 보시면, 냉정하게 보시면 사람을 해치진 않으실 거잖아요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"내게 말 걸지 마라,\" 브라운 부인이 여전히 노여워하며 원을 돌며 말했다. \"자, 가게 둬라, 어서 가게 둬!\"\n\n\"브라운 아주머니,\" 시달린 연마공이 호소했다, \"전 그럴 뜻이 없었어요—오, 사람이 이런 처지에 빠지다니 이게 무슨 꼴이람!—전 그저 말조심을 했을 뿐이에요, 브라운 아주머니. 그분이 모든 걸 빠삭하게 아시니까 늘 그러는 거라고요. 하지만 더 멀리 새 나갈 일이 아니란 걸 알았어야 했는데. 정말이지 저는 잡담쯤은 얼마든지 좋아요,\" 비참한 얼굴로 그가 말했다, \"어떤 자질구레한 잡담이든요, 브라운 아주머니. 제발 이러지 마세요. 오, 여기 가엾은 놈을 위해 한마디만 거들어 주실 순 없어요?\" 연마공이 절망에 빠져 딸에게 호소하며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 어머니, 저 애가 하는 말 들으셨죠,\" 그녀가 엄한 목소리로, 초조하게 고개를 까딱이며 끼어들었다. \"한 번만 더 시켜 보세요. 그래도 또 틀어지거든, 마음대로 망쳐 버리고 끝장내 버리세요.\"\n\n브라운 부인은 이 더없이 다정한 권고에 마음이 움직인 듯, 이내 울부짖기 시작하더니, 차츰 누그러져 사죄하는 연마공을 품에 끌어안았다. 그는 이루 말할 수 없는 비탄의 얼굴로 그녀를 끌어안고는, 희생물이 다 그렇듯, 자기의 그 존경스러운 벗 곁에 바싹 붙은 본래 자리로 돌아갔으며, 정반대 성격을 적나라하게 드러내려는 표정과 싸워 가며 억지로 짜낸 살가운 낯빛을 적잖이 띤 채, 그녀가 자기 팔짱을 끼고 그대로 두는 것을 가만히 받아들였다.\n\n\"그래, 주인 양반은 어떠시냐, 귀여운 우리 아가?\" 브라운 부인이 이렇게 화기애애한 자세로 앉아 서로 잔을 부딪친 뒤에 말했다.\n\n\"쉿! 죄송하지만 좀 더 작게 말씀해 주실 수 없어요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 롭이 간청했다. \"그게, 잘 지내시는 것 같아요, 고마워요.\"\n\n\"일자리에서 안 잘렸지, 로비?\" 브라운 부인이 구슬리는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"그게, 잘린 것도 아니고, 그렇다고 있는 것도 아니고,\" 롭이 우물거렸다. \"전—전 아직 봉급은 받고 있어요, 브라운 아주머니.\"\n\n\"그런데 할 일은 없고, 롭?\"\n\n\"지금은 딱히 할 일은 없어요, 브라운 아주머니, 그저—눈을 부릅뜨고 있는 것 말고는요,\" 연마공이 처량하게 눈을 굴리며 말했다.\n\n\"주인 양반은 외국에 가셨고, 롭?\"\n\n\"오, 제발요, 브라운 아주머니, 다른 거로 잡담 좀 하시면 안 돼요?\" 연마공이 절망에 못 이겨 외쳤다.\n\n성마른 브라운 부인이 곧장 일어서자, 시달린 연마공이 그녀를 붙들고 더듬거렸다. \"네—네, 브라운 아주머니, 외국에 계신 것 같아요. 저 아가씬 뭘 저렇게 빤히 보는 거예요?\" 그가 딸을 가리키며 덧붙였는데, 그녀의 눈은 이제 다시 뒤쪽에서 내다보는 그 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다.\n\n\"저 애는 신경 쓰지 마라, 얘야,\" 노파가 그가 돌아보지 못하도록 더 바짝 끌어안으며 말했다. \"원래 저런다—저게 저 애 버릇이야. 말해 봐라, 롭. 그 마님을 본 적 있느냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"오, 브라운 아주머니, 무슨 마님요?\" 연마공이 가련히 애원하는 어조로 외쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 마님이라니?\" 그녀가 받아쳤다. \"그 마님 말이다. 돔비 부인 말이야.\"\n\n\"네, 한 번 본 것 같아요,\" 롭이 대답했다.\n\n\"그 마님이 떠나던 날 밤이지, 로비, 응?\" 노파가 그의 귀에 대고 말하며, 그의 얼굴에 일어나는 변화 하나하나를 놓치지 않았다. \"아하! 그날 밤이었다는 걸 알지.\"\n\n\"그게 그날 밤이었다는 걸 아신다면, 아시잖아요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 롭이 대답했다, \"사람한테 집게를 들이대서 억지로 말하게 할 것까진 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"그날 밤 그들이 어디로 갔지, 롭? 곧장 갔어? 어떻게 갔지? 넌 그 마님을 어디서 봤니? 웃던? 울던? 죄다 말해 봐라,\" 늙은 마귀할멈이 그를 더욱 바짝 끌어안고는, 자기 팔짱에 낀 그의 손을 다른 손바닥에 대고 토닥이며, 침침한 눈으로 그의 얼굴 주름 하나하나를 샅샅이 살피며 외쳤다. \"자! 시작해라! 죄다 듣고 싶다. 뭐냐, 롭, 얘야! 너랑 나랑은 함께 비밀을 지킬 수 있잖니, 응? 전에도 그래 왔고. 그들이 먼저 어디로 갔지, 롭?\"\n\n비참한 연마공은 숨을 헉 들이쉬고는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"벙어리가 됐냐?\" 노파가 성을 내며 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 브라운 아주머니, 아니에요! 사람더러 번갯불이 되라는 거예요. 차라리 제가 전기 유체였으면 좋겠네,\" 어리둥절한 연마공이 중얼거렸다. \"누군가한테 한 방 먹여서 끝장을 내 버리게요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고 하는 게냐?\" 노파가 씩 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"아주머니께 제 사랑을 빈다고요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 거짓말쟁이 롭이 잔에서 위안을 찾으며 응수했다. \"그들이 먼저 어디로 갔냐고요? 그분하고 그 마님 말씀이세요?\"\n\n\"그래!\" 노파가 간절히 말했다. \"그 둘 말이다.\"\n\n\"그게요, 그들은 아무 데도 안 갔어요—같이는, 그러니까요,\" 롭이 대답했다.\n\n노파는 그의 머리와 목을 다시 한번 움켜쥐고 싶은 강한 충동이라도 인 듯 그를 바라보았으나, 그의 얼굴에 어린 어떤 고집스러운 비밀스러움에 제지당했다.\n\n\"그게 바로 그 수법이었어요,\" 마지못한 연마공이 말했다. \"그래서 아무도 그들이 가는 걸 못 봤고, 어떻게 갔는지 말할 수 있는 사람도 없는 거예요. 따로따로 갔다니까요, 브라운 아주머니.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그래! 정해 둔 곳에서 만나려고,\" 노파가 잠시 말없이 그의 얼굴을 날카롭게 뜯어본 뒤 낄낄거렸다.\n\n\"아니, 어디서 만나려는 게 아니었다면, 그냥 집에 있는 거나 마찬가지였겠죠, 안 그래요, 브라운 아주머니?\" 마지못한 연마공이 응수했다.\n\n\"그래, 롭? 그래서?\" 노파가 그의 팔을 자기 팔에 더욱 단단히 끼며 말했는데, 마치 간절한 나머지 그가 빠져나갈까 봐 두려워하는 듯했다.\n\n\"아니, 우리 아직도 충분히 얘기 안 한 거예요, 브라운 아주머니?\" 연마공이 응수했는데, 그는 억울함과 취기와 고문당하는 듯한 기분 사이에서 어찌나 눈물이 그렁그렁해졌는지, 거의 모든 대답마다 외투 자락을 이쪽저쪽 눈에 갖다 대고는 부질없는 항의의 칭얼거림을 내뱉었다. \"그 마님이 그날 밤 웃었냐고요? 웃었는지 물으신 거잖아요, 브라운 아주머니?\"\n\n\"아니면 울었는지?\" 노파가 동의의 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕이며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"둘 다 아니에요,\" 연마공이 말했다. \"그 마님하고 제가—오, 기어이 저한테서 캐내고야 마실 작정이군요, 브라운 아주머니! 그럼 지금 엄숙히 맹세하세요, 누구한테도 절대 말 안 하겠다고요.\"\n\n브라운 부인은 선뜻 그러마 했으니, 천성이 음흉한 데다, 이 일에서 다른 의도는 없고 그저 숨어 있는 손님이 직접 듣게 하려는 것뿐이었다.\n\n\"그 마님은 그러니까, 저랑 같이 사우샘프턴까지 내려가는 동안,\" 연마공이 말했다, \"석상처럼 의연했어요. 아침이 돼서도 한결같으셨고요, 브라운 아주머니. 그리고 날이 밝기도 전에 혼자 정기선을 타고 떠나실 때—제가 그 마님 하인인 척하면서 무사히 배에 오르시는 걸 지켜봤는데—그때도 한결같으셨어요. 자, 이제 만족하세요, 브라운 아주머니?\"\n\n\"아니, 롭. 아직 아니다,\" 브라운 부인이 단호하게 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 이런 여자가 어디 있담!\" 불운한 롭이 제 무력함을 두고 힘없이 한탄하며 터뜨렸다. \"그다음엔 또 뭘 알고 싶으세요, 브라운 아주머니?\"\n\n\"주인 양반은 어떻게 됐지? 어디로 갔어?\" 그녀는 여전히 그를 단단히 붙든 채, 날카로운 눈으로 그의 얼굴을 바짝 들여다보며 물었다.\n\n\"맹세코, 모르겠어요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 롭이 대답했다. \"맹세코 그분이 뭘 했는지도, 어디로 갔는지도, 그분에 관한 어떤 것도 몰라요. 다만 우리가 헤어질 때 입조심하라고 제게 하신 말씀만 알 뿐이에요. 그리고 이건 친구로서 하는 말인데요, 브라운 아주머니, 지금 우리가 하는 말을 한마디라도 옮기느니, 차라리 권총으로 자결하시거나 이 집에 틀어박혀 불을 질러 버리시는 게 나아요. 그분은 아주머니께 복수하기 위해서라면 못 할 일이 없으니까요. 아주머닌 그분을 저만큼도 잘 모르세요, 브라운 아주머니. 그분한테서는 결코 안전하지 못하다니까요, 정말이에요.\"\n\n\"내가 맹세하지 않았느냐,\" 노파가 받아쳤다, \"그리고 그 맹세를 지키지 않겠느냐?\"\n\n\"뭐, 정말 지켜 주시길 바라요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 롭이 다소 미심쩍어하며, 또 은근한 위협이 없지 않은 태도로 응수했다. \"아주머니 자신을 위해서라도요, 저 못지않게 말이에요.\"\n\n그는 이 우정 어린 경고를 하면서 그녀를 바라보고는 고개를 끄덕여 강조했다. 그러나 기괴하게 일그러지는 그 누런 얼굴과, 늙고 겨울처럼 싸늘한 매서운 눈빛을 가진 그 족제비 같은 눈을 제 얼굴에 그토록 바짝 마주하고 있기가 거북해, 그는 불편한 듯 시선을 떨구고 의자에 움츠려 앉았다. 마치 더는 어떤 질문에도 대답하지 않겠노라 시무룩하게 선언하려고 마음을 다잡는 듯했다. 노파는 여전히 전처럼 그를 붙든 채, 이 틈을 타 오른손 집게손가락을 허공에 치켜들어, 이제 곧 이어질 것에 특히 주의를 기울이라고 숨어 있는 관찰자에게 은밀한 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"롭,\" 그녀가 더없이 어르는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 브라운 아주머니, 또 뭐예요?\" 격분한 연마공이 응수했다.\n\n\"롭! 그 마님하고 주인 양반이 어디서 만나기로 했지?\"\n\n롭은 점점 더 안절부절못하며, 위를 봤다 아래를 봤다 하고, 엄지손가락을 깨물었다가 조끼에 닦더니, 마침내 자기를 괴롭히는 자를 곁눈으로 흘기며 말했다. \"제가 어떻게 알아요, 브라운 아주머니?\"\n\n노파는 다시 아까처럼 손가락을 치켜들고 대꾸했다. \"이봐, 얘야! 거기까지 끌고 와서 거기서 날 내버려 둬 봤자 소용없다. 난 알아야겠어.\" 그러고는 그의 대답을 기다렸다. 롭은 당황한 듯 잠시 멈췄다가, 불쑥 터뜨렸다. \"외국 지명을 제가 어떻게 발음해요, 브라운 아주머니? 어쩜 이렇게 사리에 안 맞는 분이세요!\"\n\n\"하지만 누가 말하는 걸 들었잖니, 로비,\" 그녀가 단호하게 받아쳤다, \"그러니 그게 어떤 소리였는지는 알 게다. 자!\"\n\n\"누가 말하는 건 한 번도 못 들었어요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 연마공이 응수했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 노파가 잽싸게 받아쳤다, \"글로 쓴 걸 봤을 게고, 철자도 댈 수 있겠구나.\"\n\n롭은 웃음과 울음 사이의 짜증스러운 외마디 소리를 내며—이런 시달림 속에서도 그는 브라운 부인의 교활함에 다소 감탄하지 않을 수 없었던 것이다—마지못해 조끼 주머니를 뒤적이더니, 거기서 작은 분필 조각을 하나 꺼냈다. 노파는 그것이 그의 엄지와 손가락 사이에 끼여 있는 것을 보자 눈을 반짝였고, 그가 거기에 단어를 쓸 수 있도록 전나무 탁자 위에 황급히 자리를 비우고는, 떨리는 손으로 다시 한번 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"자, 미리 말해 두는데요, 브라운 아주머니,\" 롭이 말했다, \"다른 건 물어봐야 소용없어요. 다른 건 아무것도 대답 안 할 거예요. 못 해요. 그들이 만나기로 한 게 얼마나 뒤였는지, 둘이서만 떠나기로 한 게 누구 계획이었는지, 저도 아주머니만큼이나 몰라요. 더 이상은 아무것도 몰라요. 제가 이 단어를 어떻게 알아냈는지 말씀드리면, 믿으실 거예요. 말씀드릴까요, 브라운 아주머니?\"\n\n\"그래, 롭.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 브라운 아주머니. 그 사정은—이제 더는 안 물으실 거죠, 아시죠?\" 롭이 이제 빠르게 졸리고 멍해지는 눈을 그녀에게 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"한마디도 더 안 묻는다,\" 브라운 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요, 사정은 이래요. 어떤 분이 그 마님을 제게 맡기고 떠날 때, 그분이 행선지를 적은 종잇조각을 마님 손에 쥐여 주면서, 혹시 잊으실까 봐 그런다고 하셨어요. 마님은 잊을까 봐 걱정하는 기색이 아니었어요. 그분이 등을 돌리자마자 그걸 찢어 버리셨으니까요. 그리고 제가 마차 발판을 접어 올릴 때, 그 조각 하나를 털어 냈어요—나머지는 창밖으로 뿌리신 모양이에요. 나중에 찾아봤지만 거기엔 한 조각도 없었거든요. 그 위엔 단어가 딱 하나만 적혀 있었는데, 정 알고 싶으시고 알아야겠다면, 그게 바로 이거예요. 하지만 명심하세요! 아주머닌 맹세하신 거예요, 브라운 아주머니!\"\n\n브라운 부인은 안다고 했다. 롭은 더 할 말이 없자, 탁자 위에 천천히 힘들게 분필로 쓰기 시작했다.\n\n\"'D' 자네,\" 그가 그 글자를 다 쓰자 노파가 소리 내어 읽었다.\n\n\"입 좀 다물어 주시겠어요, 브라운 아주머니?\" 그가 그것을 손으로 가리고는 짜증스레 그녀를 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"소리 내어 읽는 건 싫어요. 좀 가만히 계세요!\"\n\n\"그럼 크게 써라, 롭,\" 그녀가 은밀한 신호를 되풀이하며 응수했다. \"내 눈이 어두워서 활자라도 잘 안 보인다.\"\n\n혼자 투덜대며 마지못해 다시 작업으로 돌아간 롭은 단어를 계속 써 나갔다. 그가 고개를 숙이는 사이, 그가 자기도 모르게 그토록 애써 일러 주고 있는 그 사람이, 그의 등 뒤 문가에서 그의 어깨로부터 한 걸음 못 미치는 곳까지 다가와, 탁자 위로 기어가는 그 손의 자취를 향해 간절히 시선을 던졌다. 동시에 앨리스는 맞은편 의자에서 그것이 글자를 이루어 가는 모양을 유심히 지켜보며, 그가 한 글자씩 만들 때마다 소리 내지 않고 입술로 따라 했다. 글자 하나가 끝날 때마다 그녀의 눈과 돔비 씨의 눈이 마주쳤으니, 마치 둘이 서로에게서 확인을 구하기라도 하는 듯했다. 그렇게 두 사람은 함께 D.I.J.O.N.이라고 철자를 읽어 냈다.\n\n\"자!\" 연마공이 그 단어를 지우려고 황급히 손바닥에 침을 묻히며 말했다. 그러고는 문질러 지우는 것으로도 모자라, 외투 소매로 흔적을 죄다 박박 문대고 평평하게 밀어, 탁자에서 분필 색깔조차 사라질 때까지 닦아 냈다. \"자, 이제 만족하시죠, 브라운 아주머니!\"\n\n노파는 만족한다는 표시로 그의 팔을 놓아주고 등을 토닥였다. 그러자 연마공은 굴욕감과 닦달과 취기에 짓눌려, 탁자 위에 두 팔을 포개고 그 위에 머리를 얹더니 잠이 들었다.\n\n그가 한참 깊이 잠들어 코를 우렁차게 골기 시작한 뒤에야, 노파는 돔비 씨가 숨어 있는 문 쪽을 돌아보며, 방을 가로질러 나가라고 손짓했다. 그러면서도 그녀는 롭 위로 맴돌며, 그 은밀한 발걸음이 문으로 건너가는 동안 그가 혹시 머리를 들면 두 손으로 눈을 가리거나 머리를 도로 눌러 버릴 채비를 했다. 그러나 그녀의 눈길이 잠든 자를 날카롭게 살피는 한편, 깨어 있는 자에게도 날카로웠으니, 그가 자기 손으로 그녀의 손을 건드리며 온갖 조심에도 불구하고 금화 부딪치는 짤랑 소리를 내자, 그 눈길은 까마귀의 그것처럼 번득이고 탐욕스러웠다.\n\n딸의 어두운 시선은 그가 문까지 가는 것을 좇으며, 그가 얼마나 창백한지, 그의 황급한 발걸음이 조금의 지체도 그에게는 견딜 수 없는 속박임을 어떻게 드러내는지, 그가 얼마나 행동에 옮겨 떠나고 싶어 안달인지를 똑똑히 새겼다. 그가 등 뒤로 문을 닫자, 그녀는 어머니를 돌아보았다. 노파는 종종걸음으로 그녀에게 다가와, 손을 펴 그 안에 든 것을 보여 주고는, 시기심과 탐욕에 다시 손을 꼭 움켜쥐고 속삭였다.\n\n\"저자가 무슨 짓을 할까, 앨리?\"\n\n\"못된 짓이겠죠,\" 딸이 말했다.\n\n\"살인을?\" 노파가 물었다.\n\n\"상처 입은 자존심에 미쳐 버린 사람이니, 그럴지도 모르죠. 우리가 무슨 말을 하든, 그자 자신이 무슨 말을 하든 상관없이요.\"\n\n그녀의 눈길은 어머니의 것보다 더 밝았고, 거기서 타오르는 불길은 더 사나웠다. 그러나 그녀의 얼굴은 입술까지도 핏기가 없었다.\n\n두 사람은 더는 말이 없었고, 따로 떨어져 앉았다. 어머니는 제 돈과 마주하고, 딸은 제 생각과 마주한 채, 각자의 눈길이 희미하게 밝혀진 방의 어둠 속에서 빛났다. 롭은 잠을 자며 코를 골았다. 아랑곳받지 못한 앵무새만이 부산했다. 그것은 구부러진 부리로 새장 철망을 비틀고 잡아당기며, 둥근 지붕까지 기어올라 파리처럼 그 지붕을 따라 가다가 머리부터 거꾸로 다시 내려오고, 가느다란 창살 하나하나를 흔들고 물어뜯고 덜그럭거렸으니, 마치 제 주인의 위험을 알아채고 길을 뚫고 나가 날아가서 그에게 그것을 알리고 싶어 미칠 듯한 것만 같았다.\n\n# CHAPTER LIII. More Intelligence\n\n배신자와 같은 피를 나눈 두 사람—그가 의절한 형과 누이—이 있었으니, 이즈음 그들에게는 그의 죄의 무게가, 그토록 깊이 해를 입은 그 사람에게보다도 오히려 더 무겁게 얹혀 있었다. 세상은 캐묻기 좋아하고 사람을 들볶기 일쑤였지만, 돔비 씨에게는 그것이 추적과 복수를 향해 마음을 단단히 다잡게 해 주는 봉사가 되었다. 그것은 그의 격정을 자극했고, 그의 자존심을 쏘아 댔으며, 그의 생애의 단 하나뿐인 관념을 새로운 모양으로 비틀었고, 자신의 분노를 어느 정도 채우는 일을 그의 지적 존재 전체가 귀결되는 목표로 만들었다. 그의 본성에 깃든 모든 완고함과 무자비함, 그 단단하고 뚫리지 않는 성질, 그 음울함과 까다로움, 자기 자신의 중요성에 대한 과장된 자각, 남들이 그 중요성을 충분히 인정해 주는 가운데 조금이라도 흠이 보이면 분개하고 마는 그 시기심 어린 성벽—이 모든 것이 마치 여러 갈래의 물줄기가 하나로 합쳐지듯 이쪽으로 쏠렸고, 그 조류 위에 그를 실어 날랐다. 인류 가운데 가장 충동적으로 격정에 휘둘리고 가장 폭력적으로 본능에 내몰리는 자라 해도, 이 지경에 이른 음침한 돔비 씨보다는 마주치기에 한결 부드러운 적이었을 것이다. 빳빳한 크라바트에 주름 하나 없는 그 근엄한 신사보다는 차라리 야수가 돌려세우거나 달래기에 더 수월했을 것이다.\n\n그러나 바로 그 목적의 강렬함이 거의 그 일에서의 행동을 대신하는 셈이 되었다. 배신자가 어디로 달아났는지 아직 알지 못하는 동안, 그것은 자신의 재앙으로부터 마음을 돌려놓고 다른 전망으로 마음을 사로잡아 주는 구실을 했다. 그가 잘못 총애했던 자의 형과 누이에게는 그런 위안이 없었다. 그들의 내력에서 지난날의 것이든 지금의 것이든 모든 것이, 그의 죄를 그들에게 한층 더 비통한 의미로 만들었다.\n\n누이는 이따금 슬프게 생각했을지도 모른다. 자신이 그의 곁에 남아, 한때 그러했던 동무이자 벗으로 머물러 있었더라면, 그가 빠져든 그 죄를 면할 수 있었을지도 모른다고. 설령 그녀가 그렇게 생각했다 하더라도, 그것은 자신이 한 일에 대한 후회도, 자기 의무에 대한 추호의 의심도, 자기 헌신을 값매기거나 부풀리는 마음도 없는 것이었다. 그러나 잘못을 저지르고 뉘우치는 형에게 이런 가능성이 떠오를 때면, 이따금 그러했듯이, 그것은 그가 거의 견딜 수 없을 만큼 날카롭고 책망하는 손길로 그의 가슴을 쳤다. 매정한 동생에게 되갚으려는 생각 따위는 그의 마음에 들지 않았다. 자기 자신에 대한 새로운 단죄, 자신의 못남에 대한 거듭되는 내면의 탄식, 그리고 자기 혼자만 그 폐허 속에 서 있는 것이 아니라는 사실—그것이 그에게는 위안이자 동시에 자책이었다—이런 부류의 성찰만이, 그 깨달음이 그의 안에서 불러일으키는 전부였다.\n\n돔비 씨의 세계가 그의 아내의 도주로 가장 분주하던 바로 그날, 그 저녁이 앞 장을 덮어 내린 바로 그날, 형과 누이가 이른 아침을 들며 앉아 있던 방의 창문이, 작은 현관으로 다가오는 한 사내의 뜻밖의 그림자로 어두워졌다. 그 사내는 사환 퍼치였다.\n\n\"어젯밤 받은 지시대로, 이른 시간에 발스 폰드에서 건너왔습니다요,\" 퍼치 씨가 은밀하게 방문 안을 들여다보며 말했고, 진흙 한 점 묻지 않은 신발을 매트 위에서 빙 둘러 가며 닦으려고 멈춰 섰다. \"지시인즉슨, 카커 나리, 아침에 외출하시기 전에 꼭 편지 한 장을 가져다드리라는 것이었습죠. 한 시간 반은 일찍 도착했어야 마땅했지만,\" 퍼치 씨가 풀죽은 투로 말했다, \"퍼치 부인의 건강 상태가 그래서요. 정말이지 간밤에 다섯 번이나 또렷이, 아내를 잃는 줄 알았습니다요.\"\n\n\"부인이 그렇게 편찮으신가요?\" 해리엇이 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그게 말입니다,\" 퍼치 씨가 먼저 돌아서서 문을 조심스레 닫고는 말했다, \"우리 회사에서 일어난 일을 아내가 어찌나 가슴에 사무치게 받아들이는지요, 아가씨. 보시다시피 신경이 아주 섬세해서, 금세 풀어져 버린단 말씀입니다요. 하기야 제아무리 강한 신경이라도 흔들릴 만한 일이긴 하지요, 암요. 아가씨께서도 무척 마음 아프실 테지요, 두말할 것 없이.\"\n\n해리엇은 한숨을 억누르고 오라비를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"저 자신도 정말이지 제 보잘것없는 방식으로나마 마음이 아픕니다요,\" 퍼치 씨가 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말을 이었다, \"이런 일을 겪어 보라는 부름을 받지 않았더라면 도무지 믿지 못했을 정도로 말입죠. 거의 술기운 같은 효과가 저한테 나타난다니까요. 정말이지 매일 아침마다, 마치 간밤에 몸에 좋을 만큼보다 더 많이 들이켜기라도 한 것 같은 기분이 드는 거예요.\"\n\n퍼치 씨의 행색이 자기 증상에 대한 이 설명을 입증해 주었다. 거기에는 술잔과 결부될 법한 열병 같은 나른함의 기색이 있었으니, 사실 그것은 그가 매일같이 술집 카운터에서 자신을 발견하게 되고, 거기서 술 대접을 받고 이런저런 질문에 답하던 그 숱한 일들로 거슬러 올라갈 수 있는 것이었으리라.\n\n\"그래서 제가 헤아릴 수 있습죠,\" 퍼치 씨가 고개를 저으며 은빛 속삭임 같은 목소리로 말했다, \"이 더없이 고통스러운 폭로의 일에서 특히나 묘하게 처지가 된 분들의 심정을 말입니다요.\"\n\n여기서 퍼치 씨는 자기에게 속내를 털어놓아 주기를 기다렸다. 그러나 아무런 속내도 받지 못하자, 손으로 입을 가리고 헛기침을 했다. 그래도 아무 반응이 없자, 모자 뒤로 헛기침을 했고, 그것도 아무 소용이 없자, 모자를 바닥에 내려놓고 가슴팍 안주머니에서 편지를 찾았다.\n\n\"제 기억이 옳다면 답장은 필요 없었습니다요,\" 퍼치 씨가 상냥한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그래도 한번 훑어봐 주신다면 고맙겠습니다요, 나리.\"\n\n존 카커는 돔비 씨의 봉인을 뜯고, 매우 짤막한 내용을 다 읽고는 대답했다, \"아니요. 답장은 필요 없소.\"\n\n\"그럼 안녕히 계십시오, 아가씨,\" 퍼치가 문 쪽으로 한 걸음 옮기며 말했다, \"그리고 정말이지, 이번의 고통스러운 폭로로 인해 마음을 어쩔 수 없이 상하는 것 이상으로는 부디 상하지 않으시길 빕니다요. 신문들은 말입니다,\" 퍼치 씨가 다시 두 걸음 물러서서, 한층 비밀스러운 속삭임으로 형과 누이를 한꺼번에 향해 말했다, \"여러분이 짐작도 못 하실 만큼 그 소식에 안달이 났습죠. 일요일판 신문 가운데 하나는, 파란 외투에 흰 모자를 쓴 작자였는데, 전에 저한테 뇌물을 주려고까지 했었지요—굳이 그게 어찌 됐는지 말씀드릴 필요는 없겠지요?—어젯밤 여덟 시 이십 분이나 되도록 우리 안마당을 어슬렁거리고 있더란 말입니다요. 제 눈으로 똑똑히 봤습죠, 경리실 열쇠 구멍에 눈을 갖다 대고 있는 걸요. 그 구멍이 특허품이라 안이 안 보이는데도 말입니다요. 또 다른 작자는,\" 퍼치 씨가 말했다, \"군복 같은 장식 단추를 단 자였는데, 종일토록 킹스 암스의 응접실에 죽치고 있더군요. 제가 지난주에 거기서 우연히 사소한 한마디를 흘렸더니, 이튿날 아침, 그게 일요일이었습죠, 그게 인쇄물에 떡하니 실린 걸 봤습니다요. 정말 놀랄 만큼 잘도 부풀려져 있더라고요.\"\n\n퍼치 씨는 그 단락을 꺼내 보이기라도 하려는 듯 다시 가슴팍 안주머니로 손을 가져갔으나, 아무런 격려도 받지 못하자 비버 가죽 장갑을 꺼내고 모자를 집어 든 뒤 작별을 고했다. 그리고 정오가 채 되기도 전에, 퍼치 씨는 킹스 암스를 비롯한 여러 곳에서 몇몇 엄선된 청중에게 이렇게 들려주었다. 카커 아가씨가 울음을 터뜨리며 자기 두 손을 덥석 붙잡고는 \"오! 다정하고 다정한 퍼치, 당신을 보는 것만이 제게 남은 유일한 위안이에요!\" 하고 말했다고, 그리고 존 카커 씨는 무시무시한 목소리로 \"퍼치, 나는 그를 의절했소. 다시는 그를 내 동생이라 입에 올리지 마시오!\" 하고 말했다고.\n\n\"존 오라버니,\" 두 사람만 남아 몇 분간 말없이 있다가 해리엇이 말했다. \"저 편지에 나쁜 소식이 들었군요.\"\n\n\"그래. 하지만 뜻밖의 일은 아니다,\" 그가 대답했다. \"어제 그분을 봤거든.\"\n\n\"그분이라뇨?\"\n\n\"돔비 씨 말이다. 내가 거기 있는 동안 두 번이나 경리실을 지나가셨지. 그 전까지는 그분을 피할 수 있었지만, 물론 그걸 오래 바랄 수는 없는 노릇이었어. 그분이 내 존재를 무언가 거슬리는 것으로 여기는 게 얼마나 당연한 일인지 나는 안다. 나 스스로도 그럴 수밖에 없다고 느꼈으니까.\"\n\n\"그분이 그렇게 말씀하시던가요?\"\n\n\"아니, 아무 말도 하지 않으셨어. 하지만 그분의 시선이 잠시 내게 머무는 걸 봤고, 그래서 앞으로 닥칠 일에—이미 닥친 일에 마음의 준비를 했지. 나는 해고됐다!\"\n\n그녀는 될 수 있는 대로 충격받지 않은, 희망을 잃지 않은 표정을 지으려 애썼으나, 여러 가지 이유로 그것은 괴로운 소식이었다.\n\n\"'굳이 자네에게 말할 필요도 없겠지,'\" 존 카커가 편지를 읽었다, \"'앞으로 자네 이름이, 내 이름과 아무리 동떨어진 관계로 엮인다 한들, 어찌하여 부자연스럽게 들리게 될지, 또 어찌하여 그 이름을 지닌 자를 날마다 보는 일이 내게 견딜 수 없는 것이 될지를. 나는 오늘부로 우리 사이의 모든 고용 관계가 끝났음을 통고하며, 앞으로 나 또는 내 회사와의 어떠한 교신도 자네 쪽에서 결코 새로이 시도하지 말 것을 요구하는 바이다.'—넉넉히 긴 통고 기간에 상당하는 액수가 돈으로 동봉되어 있고, 이것이 내 해고 통지다. 하느님은 아시겠지, 해리엇, 우리가 모든 걸 떠올려 보면 이건 너그럽고 사려 깊은 처사야!\"\n\n\"남이 저지른 잘못을 두고 오라버니를 벌하는 것이 조금이라도 너그럽고 사려 깊은 일이라고 한다면요, 존 오라버니,\" 그녀가 부드럽게 대답했다, \"그렇겠지요.\"\n\n\"우리는 그분에게 불길한 핏줄이었어,\" 존 카커가 말했다. \"그분이 우리 이름 소리만 들어도 움찔하고, 우리 피에 무언가 저주받고 사악한 것이 흐른다고 여기는 것도 무리가 아니야. 나도 거의 그렇게 생각할 뻔했다, 해리엇, 네가 없었더라면.\"\n\n\"오라버니, 그렇게 말하지 마세요. 오라버니가 말하는 대로, 또 그렇게 믿는 대로, 나를 사랑할 어떤 특별한 이유가 있다 하더라도—나는 아니라고 하지만요!—제발 그런 거칠고 미친 말은 듣지 않게 해 주세요!\"\n\n그는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 그러나 곧 그녀가 곁으로 다가와 그 한 손을 자기 손안에 쥐도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n\"이토록 오랜 세월 끝에 이렇게 헤어지는 건 서글픈 일이지, 나도 안다,\" 누이가 말했다, \"그 까닭이 우리 둘 모두에게 끔찍하다는 것도. 게다가 우리는 살아가야 하고, 그러자면 살길을 둘러봐야지요. 자, 자! 우리는 그렇게 할 수 있어요, 기죽지 말고. 애써 나아가는 것은, 그것도 함께 애써 나아가는 것은 우리의 괴로움이 아니라 우리의 자랑이에요, 존 오라버니!\"\n\n그녀의 입가에 미소가 어렸다. 그녀는 그의 뺨에 입을 맞추며 부디 기운을 내라고 간청했다.\n\n\"오, 사랑하는 누이여! 네 자신의 고귀한 뜻으로, 파멸한 사내에게 묶여 버리다니! 평판이 무너지고, 제 친구 하나 없으며, 네 친구마저 모조리 쫓아 버린 사내에게 말이다!\"\n\n\"존 오라버니!\" 그녀가 황급히 그의 입술에 손을 갖다 댔다, \"날 위해서요! 우리가 함께 지내 온 그 오랜 세월을 떠올려서라도요!\" 그는 입을 다물었다. \"자, 이제 말씀드릴게요, 오라버니,\" 조용히 그의 곁에 앉으며 그녀가 말했다, \"나도 오라버니처럼 이 일을 예상하고 있었어요. 그리고 이 일을 생각하며, 그것이 일어날까 두려워하며, 될 수 있는 대로 마음의 준비를 하면서, 만일 그렇게 된다면 오라버니께 이 말을 하기로 마음먹었지요. 내가 오라버니께 비밀 하나를 감추고 있었다고, 그리고 우리에게는 친구가 한 분 있다고요.\"\n\n\"그 친구분 이름이 뭔데, 해리엇?\" 그가 서글픈 미소를 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"사실 모르겠어요. 하지만 그분은 언젠가 제게 자기 우정과, 우리를 돕고 싶다는 뜻을 아주 진지하게 밝히셨어요. 그리고 저는 오늘까지도 그분을 믿어요.\"\n\n\"해리엇!\" 의아해하는 오라비가 외쳤다, \"그 친구분이 어디 사시는데?\"\n\n\"그것도 모르겠어요,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 그분은 우리 둘을 다 아시고, 우리 내력을—우리의 그 자잘한 내력을 전부 아세요, 존 오라버니. 그분이 직접 그렇게 하자고 하셔서, 제가 오라버니께 그분이 다녀가신 비밀을 감춰 온 거예요. 오라버니가 그 사실을 아시면 마음 아파하실까 봐요.\"\n\n\"여기를! 그분이 여기에 다녀가셨다고, 해리엇?\"\n\n\"여기, 이 방에요. 한 번.\"\n\n\"어떤 분이시던?\"\n\n\"젊은 분은 아니었어요. 그분 말씀으로는 '머리가 희끗하고, 점점 더 희어져 가는' 분이라고요. 하지만 너그럽고, 솔직하고, 선량한 분인 게 분명해요.\"\n\n\"한 번밖에 못 봤다고, 해리엇?\"\n\n\"이 방에서는 단 한 번이에요,\" 누이가 뺨에 아주 희미하고 덧없는 홍조를 띠며 말했다, \"하지만 여기 계실 때, 그분은 지나는 길에 일주일에 한 번씩 저를 볼 수 있게 해 달라고 간청하셨어요. 우리가 잘 지내고 있다는, 그분 손에서 아무것도 필요로 하지 않는다는 표시로요. 그분이 베풀 수 있는 봉사가 있으면 무엇이든 주겠다고 하셨을 때—그게 방문의 목적이었지요—제가 우리에게는 필요한 게 없다고 말씀드렸거든요.\"\n\n\"그래서 일주일에 한 번씩—\"\n\n\"그 뒤로 매주 한 번씩, 늘 같은 날, 같은 시각에 그분이 지나가셨어요. 언제나 걸어서, 언제나 같은 방향으로요—런던 쪽으로요. 그리고 잠깐 멈춰 서서 저에게 고개를 숙이고, 다정한 보호자처럼 명랑하게 손을 흔드시는 것 외에는 결코 더 머무는 법이 없으셨지요. 그 묘한 만남을 제안하셨을 때 그렇게 약속하셨고, 어찌나 충실하고 기분 좋게 지키셨던지, 처음에 제가 그 일을 두고 조금이라도 거북함을 느낀 적이 있었다 해도—그런 적이 있었던 것 같지도 않아요, 존 오라버니, 그분의 태도가 어찌나 꾸밈없고 진실했던지요—그건 곧 사라져 버리고, 그 날이 다가오면 오히려 기쁘기만 했답니다. 그런데 지난 월요일에는—이 끔찍한 일이 있고 처음 맞는 월요일이었죠—그분이 지나가지 않으셨어요. 그래서 그분이 안 오신 게 혹시 이번에 일어난 일과 무슨 관련이 있는 건 아닐까 하고 궁금해하고 있었어요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 말이냐?\" 오라비가 물었다.\n\n\"어떻게인지는 모르겠어요. 그저 그 우연의 일치를 두고 이리저리 짐작만 해 봤을 뿐, 그걸 설명해 보려고 한 건 아니에요. 그분이 분명 다시 오시리라는 건 확실해요. 그분이 오시면, 사랑하는 존 오라버니, 제가 마침내 오라버니께 이 이야기를 했다고 말씀드리고, 두 분을 만나게 해 드릴게요. 그분은 분명 우리가 새 살길을 찾도록 도와주실 거예요. 그분이 간청하신 건 제 삶과 오라버니의 삶을 조금이나마 편하게 해 줄 무언가를 하게 해 달라는 것이었어요. 그래서 저는 우리가 언젠가 친구를 필요로 하게 되면 그분을 떠올리겠다고 약속드렸지요. 그때가 되면 그분 이름도 더는 비밀이 아니게 되는 거고요.\"\n\n\"해리엇,\" 오라비가 골똘히 귀 기울여 듣고 있다가 말했다, \"그 신사분을 내게 그려 보아라. 나를 그토록 잘 아는 분이라면 나도 분명 알아봐야 할 텐데.\"\n\n누이는 자기 손님의 이목구비며 체격이며 옷차림을 될 수 있는 한 생생하게 그려 보였다. 그러나 존 카커는, 본디 그 인물을 전혀 모르는 탓인지, 아니면 그녀의 묘사에 어떤 흠이 있어서인지, 아니면 그가 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 이리저리 거닐며 마음이 다른 데 가 있어서인지, 그녀가 내놓은 초상을 알아보지 못했다.\n\n그렇지만 두 사람은 그분이 다음번에 나타나면 오라비가 직접 그 실물을 보기로 합의했다. 이렇게 결말이 나자, 누이는 한결 가벼워진 마음으로 집안일에 몰두했다. 그리고 돔비 회사의 옛 차석이었던 그 머리 희끗한 사내는 뜻밖에 얻은 자유의 첫날을 정원 일을 하며 보냈다.\n\n밤이 꽤 깊어, 누이는 바늘을 놀리고 오라비는 소리 내어 책을 읽고 있을 때, 문을 두드리는 소리에 둘은 방해를 받았다. 달아난 동생과 관련하여 그들 주위로 짙게 드리워진 막연한 불안과 두려움의 분위기 속에서, 그곳에서는 흔치 않은 이 소리가 거의 섬뜩하게 느껴졌다. 오라비가 문으로 가는 동안, 누이는 앉은 채 겁먹은 듯 귀를 기울였다. 누군가 그에게 말을 걸었고, 그는 대답하며 놀란 듯했다. 그리고 몇 마디 말이 오간 뒤, 두 사람이 함께 다가왔다.\n\n\"해리엇,\" 오라비가 늦은 손님을 데리고 들어오며 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"모핀 씨란다—제임스와 함께 돔비 회사에 그토록 오래 계시던 그분이야.\"\n\n누이는 마치 유령이라도 들어온 듯 흠칫 물러섰다. 문간에는 그 낯 모를 친구가 서 있었으니, 희끗희끗 센 검은 머리에, 불그레한 얼굴, 넓고 훤한 이마, 그리고 그녀가 그토록 오래 비밀을 지켜 온 그 갈색 눈이었다!\n\n\"존 오라버니!\" 그녀가 거의 숨이 막힌 채 말했다. \"오늘 제가 말씀드린 그 신사분이에요!\"\n\n\"이 신사가 말입니다, 해리엇 아가씨,\" 손님이 들어서며 말했다—그는 문간에서 잠시 멈춰 섰던 터였다—\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 무척 마음이 놓이는군요. 여기 오는 내내 제 처지를 어찌 설명할까 이런저런 방도를 궁리해 봤습니다만, 어느 것도 마음에 차질 않더군요. 존 군, 나는 여기서 아주 낯선 사람은 아니라네. 방금 자네가 문간에서 나를 봤을 때 깜짝 놀랐지. 지금은 더 놀란 듯하군. 뭐! 지금 사정으로 보면 그것도 무리는 아니지. 우리가 이토록 습관의 피조물만 아니었다면, 이렇게 자주 놀랄 일도 없었을 텐데 말이야.\"\n\n이때쯤 그는, 그녀가 그토록 또렷이 기억하는 그 진심 어린 다정함과 정중함을 능숙하게 뒤섞은 태도로 해리엇에게 인사를 건넸고, 그녀 곁에 앉아 장갑을 벗어 탁자 위 모자 안에 던져 넣었다.\n\n\"내가 자네 누이를 보고 싶어졌다는 것, 존 군, 또 내 나름의 방식으로 그 소망을 채웠다는 것에는 조금도 놀랄 일이 없다네,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 뒤로 내 방문이 규칙적이었다는 것도—누이가 자네에게 말했을지 모르지만—거기에는 별스러운 게 없어. 그건 곧 습관이 되었거든. 우리는 습관의 피조물이니까—습관의 피조물이지!\"\n\n두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 의자에 기대앉은 채, 그는 마치 형제와 누이가 함께 있는 모습을 보는 것이 흥미롭다는 듯 둘을 바라보았다. 그러고는 어딘가 짜증 섞인 사색의 어조로 말을 이었다. \"바로 이 습관이라는 것이, 더 나은 일을 해낼 능력이 있는 우리 중 어떤 이들은 루시퍼 같은 오만과 완고함 속에 굳혀 버리고—또 어떤 이들은 악행 속에 굳히고 깊이 빠뜨리며—더 많은 이들을 무관심 속에 가둔다네—그것은 우리 흙의 기질에 따라 우리를 날마다 굳혀서 마치 조각상처럼 만들고, 그러면서도 새로운 인상과 신념에 대해서는 조각상처럼 무감각한 채로 우리를 내버려 두지. 그것이 내게 끼친 영향을 자네가 판단해 보게, 존 군. 굳이 햇수를 댈 것도 없이 오랜 세월 동안, 나는 돔비 회사의 경영에서 작고도 정확히 정해진 내 몫만을 맡았고, 자네 동생이—그 자는 스스로 악당임을 입증했지! 누이께서 내가 부득이 이 말을 꺼내는 걸 용서하시겠지—제 영향력을 자꾸자꾸 넓혀, 마침내 사업도 그 주인도 그의 공이 되어 버리는 걸 지켜보았네. 또 자네가 그 후미진 책상에서 날마다 고되게 일하는 것도 보았지. 그러면서도 나는 내 의무의 그 한 가닥 밖으로는 될 수 있는 한 신경 쓰지 않는 데 만족했고, 내 주위의 모든 일이 마치 거대한 기계처럼 날마다 의문 없이 굴러가게 내버려 두었네—그게 그것의 습관이자 내 습관이었지—그리고 그 모든 걸 당연하게 여기고, 다 옳다고 치부했어. 내 수요일 밤은 어김없이 돌아왔고, 우리 사중주 모임은 어김없이 열렸으며, 내 첼로는 음이 잘 맞았고, 내 세계에는 아무런 잘못도 없었지—설령 무언가 있었다 해도 대수롭지 않았고—적든 많든 그건 내 알 바가 아니었던 거야.\"\n\n\"그 모든 세월 동안 회사의 그 누구보다도 선생님이 더 존경받고 사랑받으셨다는 건 제가 장담할 수 있습니다,\" 존 카커가 말했다.\n\n\"흥! 그저 마음씨 좋고 무던했을 뿐이겠지,\" 상대가 대답했다, \"그것도 내 습관이었어. 그게 지배인의 마음에도 들었고, 그가 부린 사람들 마음에도 들었으며, 무엇보다 내게 가장 잘 맞았지. 나는 내게 할당된 일을 했고, 어느 쪽에도 아첨하지 않았으며, 그런 게 전혀 필요 없는 자리를 차지하고 있어서 기뻤다네. 그렇게 지금껏 지내 왔을 거야, 내 방의 벽이 얇지만 않았더라면 말이지. 그게 지배인 방과는 판자 칸막이 하나로 나뉘어 있었다고 자네 누이에게 말해 주게나.\"\n\n\"두 방은 붙어 있었어요. 어쩌면 본래 한 방이었던 것을, 모핀 씨 말씀대로 나누어 놓은 것이겠지요,\" 오라비가 설명을 마저 이어 가 주기를 바라며 그를 돌아보고 말했다.\n\n\"나는 휘파람도 불고, 콧노래도 흥얼거리고, 베토벤의 B조 소나타를 처음부터 끝까지 정확히 연주해 보이기도 했네. 내가 다 듣고 있다는 걸 그에게 알리려고 말이야,\" 모핀 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 그는 결코 나를 개의치 않더군. 물론 내가 사적인 성격의 무언가를 엿들을 만한 거리에 있는 일은 드물었지. 하지만 그런 거리에 있게 되어, 무언가를 알아챌 수밖에 없을 때면, 나는 밖으로 나가 버렸네. 한번은 두 형제가 나누는 대화 중에 나가 버린 적이 있어, 존 군. 그 대화의 처음에는 젊은 월터 게이도 끼어 있었지. 하지만 방을 나서기 전에 그 일부를 엿들었다네. 자네는 그게 어떤 내용이었는지 누이에게 말해 줄 만큼은 기억하고 있겠지?\"\n\n\"그건 말이다, 해리엇,\" 오라비가 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"지난날의 일과, 회사 안에서 우리 형제가 처한 상대적인 위치에 관한 것이었어.\"\n\n\"그 내용 자체는 내게 새로운 게 아니었지만, 새로운 면모로 다가왔다네. 그것이 내 습관을 흔들었지—세상 사람 열에 아홉이 가진 습관, 즉 내가 익숙해져 있다는 이유만으로 내 주위의 모든 게 옳다고 믿어 버리는 그 습관 말일세,\" 손님이 말했다. \"그래서 나는 두 형제의 내력을 되짚어 보고, 그것을 곰곰이 생각해 보게 되었어. 내 생각엔 그게 거의 내 평생 처음으로 이런 사색의 갈래에 빠져든 순간이었던 것 같네—지금 우리에게 익숙하고 너무도 당연한 그 많은 것들이, 언젠가 우리 모두가 반드시 서게 될 그 새롭고 아득한 시점에서 바라보면 과연 어떻게 보일까? 그 아침 이후로 나는, 흔히 말하듯이, 좀 덜 마음씨 좋은 사람이 되었고, 전체적으로 덜 무던하고 덜 안일한 사람이 되었지.\"\n\n그는 한 손으로 탁자를 톡톡 두드리며 한두 분쯤 앉아 있다가, 마치 제 고백을 어서 털어 버리고 싶어 안달이라도 난 듯 서둘러 말을 이었다.\n\n\"내가 무엇을 해야 할지, 아니 무엇이든 할 수 있을지 미처 알기도 전에, 같은 두 형제 사이에 또 한 차례 대화가 있었고, 거기서 그들의 누이가 언급되었네. 나는 그 대화의 온갖 부스러기와 토막을 가리지 않고 마음껏 흘러들게 내버려 두면서도 양심에 거리낌이 없었어. 그것들을 마땅히 내 것으로 여겼던 거지. 그 뒤로 나는 직접 그 누이를 보려고 이곳에 왔네. 처음에 정원 문 앞에 멈춰 섰을 때는, 어느 가난한 이웃의 사람됨을 알아보러 왔다는 핑계를 댔지. 하지만 그 핑계에서 벗어나 딴소리를 하고 말았고, 해리엇 아가씨가 나를 의심하셨던 것 같네. 두 번째에는 들어가게 해 달라고 청해서, 안으로 들어가, 하고 싶은 말을 했지. 자네 누이는 그때 내게서 도움을 받지 않을 이유들을 보여 주셨고, 나는 감히 그것을 반박할 수 없었어. 다만 우리 사이에 연락할 방도를 마련해 두었는데, 그것이 며칠 전까지 끊어지지 않고 이어졌다네. 근래 내게 떨어진 중대한 일들 때문에 그걸 이어 가지 못하게 되기 전까지는 말이야.\"\n\n\"제가 이걸 얼마나 까맣게 몰랐는지요,\" 존 카커가 말했다, \"선생님을 날마다 뵈면서도 말입니다! 해리엇이 선생님 성함을 짐작이라도 할 수 있었다면—\"\n\n\"실은 말일세, 존 군,\" 손님이 끼어들었다, \"내가 그걸 혼자 간직한 데는 두 가지 까닭이 있었네. 첫째 까닭만으로는 꼭 그래야 했을지 모르겠지만—사람이 좋은 의도를 가졌다고 공치사를 받을 일은 아니니까—어쨌든 나는 자네에게 무언가 진짜 도움이 될 만한 일을 할 수 있게 되기 전까지는 내 정체를 밝히지 않기로 마음먹었네. 둘째 까닭은, 자네 동생이 두 사람에 대해 마음을 누그러뜨릴 가능성이 조금이라도 남아 있기를 늘 바랐기 때문이야. 그런 경우에, 그처럼 의심 많고 경계심 강한 성품을 가진 자가 자네들이 나에게 몰래 도움을 받았다는 걸 알아챌 위험이 있다면, 거기엔 새롭고도 치명적인 불화의 씨앗이 생길 위험이 있다고 느꼈지. 그래서 나는, 그의 노여움을 차라리 내 쪽으로 돌리는 한이 있더라도—그건 아무래도 좋았네만—회사의 우두머리를 통해 자네들을 도울 기회를 엿보기로 단단히 작정했었네. 그러나 죽음, 구애, 결혼, 가정의 불행이라는 어지러운 일들 탓에, 이 오랜, 참으로 오랜 세월 동안 우리에게는 자네 동생 말고는 우두머리라 할 사람이 없었어. 그리고 우리에게는 차라리,\" 손님이 목소리를 낮추며 말했다, \"생명 없는 몸뚱이가 우두머리였던 편이 더 나았을 걸세.\"\n\n그는 이 마지막 말이 본의 아니게 입 밖으로 새어 나왔음을 의식한 듯, 한 손은 오라비에게, 한 손은 누이에게 내밀며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"내가 하고 싶었던 말은, 그리고 그 이상도, 이제 다 했네. 내가 뜻하는 바는 말로 다할 수 없는 것이지만, 자네들이 알아듣고 믿어 주리라 믿네. 때가 왔네, 존 군—참으로 불운하고 불행하게도 왔지만—내가 그 오랜 세월 이어진 속죄의 분투를 방해하지 않으면서 자네를 도울 수 있는 때가 말이야. 자네는 자네 자신의 어떤 행동 때문이 아니라, 오늘 그 분투에서 해고당한 셈이니까. 이미 늦었군. 오늘 밤엔 더 말할 게 없네. 자네는 내 조언이나 일깨움 없이도 여기 있는 이 보물을 지켜 낼 테지.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 떠나려고 일어섰다.\n\n\"하지만 자네가 먼저 가게, 존 군,\" 그가 너그러운 투로 말했다, \"불을 들고서, 하고 싶은 말이 무엇이든 그건 말하지 말고 말일세.\" 존 카커는 가슴이 벅차올라, 할 수만 있다면 말로 그것을 풀어내고 싶었다. \"그리고 내가 자네 누이와 한마디 나누게 해 주게. 우리는 전에도, 그것도 이 방에서, 단둘이 이야기를 나눈 적이 있다네. 자네가 여기 있는 편이 더 자연스러워 보이긴 하지만 말이야.\"\n\n그를 눈으로 좇으며 배웅하고 나서, 그는 다정하게 해리엇에게로 돌아서서, 한층 낮은 목소리로, 사뭇 달라진 더 진지한 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"누이로 지내야 하는 것이 불행인 그 사내에 대해, 아가씨는 내게 무언가 묻고 싶으시겠지요.\"\n\n\"여쭙기가 두려워요,\" 해리엇이 말했다.\n\n\"아가씨가 한 번도 아니고 여러 번 그토록 간절하게 나를 바라보셨으니,\" 손님이 대답했다, \"그 물음을 알아맞힐 수 있을 것 같군요. 그가 돈에 손을 댔느냐? 그것이지요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"손대지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"하느님 감사합니다!\" 해리엇이 말했다. \"존 오라버니를 위해서요.\"\n\n\"그가 여러모로 직무상의 신뢰를 저버렸다는 것,\" 모핀 씨가 말했다, \"자기가 대표하던 회사를 위해서보다 제 자신을 위해서 거래하고 투기한 적이 더 잦았다는 것, 회사를 엄청난 모험으로 끌고 들어가 종종 막대한 손실로 이어지게 했다는 것, 고용주의 허영과 야심을 억눌러야 마땅했고 또 그렇게 할 힘이 있었음에도, 그것이 이리저리 어디로 향하는지를 보여 주기는커녕 늘 그 허영과 야심을 부추겼다는 것—이런 일들은 이제 아가씨께 그리 놀랍지 않으실 겁니다. 회사가 막대한 자원을 지녔다는 평판을 부풀리고, 다른 상인들의 회사와 견주어 더없이 화려하게 돋보이게 하려고 여러 사업이 벌어졌는데, 그 사업들이 가져올 수도 있는—몇 차례 운수 나쁜 변동만 닥쳐도 가져오기 십상인—파멸적인 결과를 헤아리려면 침착한 머리가 필요할 정도지요. 세계 곳곳에 걸친 회사의 그 숱한 거래들 한복판에서—오직 그만이 실마리를 쥐고 있는 거대한 미로 같은 것이지요—그는 기회를 잡았고, 또 그걸 이용한 듯합니다. 결과가 드러나도 그 갖가지 결과를 떠다니는 채로 내버려 두고, 사실 대신에 추정과 두루뭉술한 말로 채워 넣는 식으로 말이지요. 그런데 근래에 들어서는—제 말을 따라오고 계십니까, 해리엇 아가씨?\"\n\n\"완벽하게요, 완벽하게요,\" 그녀가 겁에 질린 얼굴을 그에게 고정한 채 대답했다. \"제발 가장 나쁜 것까지 한꺼번에 다 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"근래에 들어 그는, 이 결과들을 아주 또렷하고 명료하게 만드는 데 가장 큰 공을 들인 듯합니다. 그 결과들이 아무리 많고 다양하다 해도, 비밀 장부만 들춰 보면 누구든 놀랄 만큼 손쉽게 그것들을 파악할 수 있도록 말이지요. 마치 제 고용주에게, 그 지배적인 격정에 영합한 결과 무엇이 닥쳐왔는지를 단번에 넓게 펼쳐 보여 주기로 작정이라도 한 것처럼요! 그가 그 격정에 비열하게 영합하고, 부패하게 아첨하는 것을 늘 일삼아 왔다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 회사의 일과 관련된 그의 범죄성은 주로 거기에 있지요.\"\n\n\"가시기 전에 한 마디만 더요, 선생님,\" 해리엇이 말했다. \"이 모든 일에 위험은 없는 건가요?\"\n\n\"위험이라니요?\" 그가 조금 머뭇거리며 되물었다.\n\n\"회사의 신용에 말이에요.\"\n\n\"아가씨께 솔직히 말씀드리고, 또 아가씨를 온전히 믿지 않을 수가 없군요,\" 모핀 씨가 잠시 그녀의 얼굴을 살핀 뒤 말했다.\n\n\"믿으셔도 돼요. 정말로 믿으셔도 됩니다!\"\n\n\"믿어도 되겠지요. 회사의 신용에 위험이 있느냐고요? 아니요, 전혀 없습니다. 어려움은 있을지 모르지요, 더 크거나 더 작은 어려움이요. 하지만 위험은 없습니다—다만—참으로 다만—회사의 우두머리가 그 사업들을 줄이는 쪽으로 마음을 돌리지 못하고, 회사가 지금까지 자기가 늘 스스로에게 그려 보여 온 그 위치 말고 다른 어떤 위치에 있다는 것을, 아니 있을 수 있다는 것을 한사코 믿으려 들지 않은 채, 그것을 제 힘 이상으로 밀어붙인다면 모르지요. 그러면 휘청거리게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그럴 염려는 없는 거지요?\" 해리엇이 말했다.\n\n\"우리 사이에 어설픈 신뢰란 없어야지요,\" 그가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 대답했다. \"돔비 씨는 지금 그 누구도 가까이 다가갈 수 없는 분이고, 그의 마음 상태는 오만하고, 무모하고, 비이성적이며, 걷잡을 수 없습니다. 하지만 지금 그분은 온갖 정상의 범위를 넘어설 만큼 어지럽고 동요되어 있으니, 그것도 지나갈지 모르지요. 이제 아가씨는 모든 걸 다 아셨습니다, 가장 나쁜 것도 가장 좋은 것도요. 오늘 밤은 더 말하지 맙시다, 안녕히 주무세요!\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추고, 오라비가 들어오기를 기다리며 서 있던 문 쪽으로 나가, 오라비가 무슨 말을 하려 하자 명랑하게 옆으로 비켜서게 하고는, 어차피 곧 자주 만나게 될 테니 말하고 싶으면 다음에 하라고, 지금은 그럴 겨를이 없다고 일렀다. 그리고 어떤 감사의 말도 자기를 뒤따르지 못하도록 빠른 걸음으로 떠나갔다.\n\n형과 누이는 거의 동이 틀 무렵까지 난롯가에 앉아 이야기를 나누었다. 그들 앞에 열린 새로운 세계를 언뜻 본 탓에 잠을 이루지 못했으니, 마치 오래전 외딴 해안에서 난파당한 두 사람이, 이미 체념에 늙어 버리고 다른 집은 생각조차 잃어버렸을 무렵에야 마침내 배 한 척이 다가온 것 같은 심정이었다. 그러나 또 다른, 사뭇 다른 종류의 불안도 그들을 깨어 있게 했다. 이 빛이 그들에게 비쳐 든 그 어둠이 다시 주위로 모여들었다. 그리고 죄지은 동생의 그림자가, 그의 발이 한 번도 디딘 적 없는 그 집 안에 드리워 있었다.\n\n그 그림자는 쫓아낼 수도, 해가 떠도 사라지지도 않았다. 이튿날 아침에도 그것은 거기 있었다. 정오에도, 밤에도. 밤이 가장 어둡고 가장 또렷했으니, 이제 이야기할 그대로다.\n\n존 카커는 친구가 보낸 약속 편지에 따라 외출했고, 해리엇은 홀로 집에 남았다. 그녀가 혼자 있은 지 몇 시간이 지났다. 음울하고 무거운 저녁과 점점 깊어 가는 어스름은 그녀의 마음을 짓누르는 무게를 걷어 내기에 좋은 것이 못 되었다. 오래도록 보지 못해 알지도 못하는 그 동생의 모습이 무시무시한 형상으로 그녀 주위를 떠돌았다. 그는 죽어 있었고, 죽어 가고 있었고, 그녀를 부르고 있었고, 그녀를 노려보고 있었고, 그녀에게 얼굴을 찌푸리고 있었다. 마음속의 그 영상들이 어찌나 집요하고 또렷하던지, 어스름이 짙어질수록 그녀는 고개를 들어 방의 어두운 구석을 바라보기가 두려웠다. 그 흥분한 상상이 낳은 그의 망령이 거기서 자기를 놀래키려 기다리고 있을까 봐. 한번은 그가 옆방에 숨어 있다는 환상에 사로잡혀—그것이 얼마나 병적인 환상인지 잘 알았고 조금도 믿지 않았으면서도—제 마음을 다잡으려고 억지로 그리로 가 보았다. 그러나 헛일이었다. 방을 나서는 순간 그곳은 다시 어슴푸레한 공포에 휩싸였고, 그녀는 마치 그 막연한 두려움의 인상들이 단단한 땅에 뿌리박은 돌 거인이기라도 한 듯, 도무지 거기서 벗어날 힘이 없었다.\n\n거의 어두워졌을 무렵, 그녀는 창가에 앉아 손으로 머리를 괴고 아래를 내려다보고 있었다. 그때 방 안의 어둠이 갑자기 짙어지는 것을 느끼고 눈을 들었다가, 자기도 모르게 비명을 질렀다. 유리에 바싹 붙어, 창백하고 겁에 질린 얼굴 하나가 안을 들여다보고 있었다. 한순간은 무언가를 찾는 듯 멍하니, 그러더니 그 눈이 그녀에게 머물면서 환히 빛났다.\n\n\"들여보내 줘요! 들여보내 줘요! 당신에게 할 말이 있어요!\" 그러면서 손이 유리를 두드려 댔다.\n\n그녀는 그 검고 긴 머리의 여자를 곧바로 알아보았다. 비 오던 어느 밤 자기가 따뜻함과 음식과 잠자리를 베풀어 주었던 여자였다. 그 여자의 사나운 행동을 떠올리며 자연히 두려운 마음이 든 해리엇은, 창가에서 조금 물러서서 어찌할 바를 모르고 겁에 질린 채 서 있었다.\n\n\"들여보내 줘요! 당신에게 말하게 해 줘요! 나는 고마워하고 있어요—조용히—겸손하게—당신이 바라는 대로 뭐든지 할게요. 그저 당신에게 말하게만 해 줘요.\"\n\n그 간청의 격렬한 태도, 얼굴에 어린 간절한 표정, 애원하듯 치켜든 두 손의 떨림, 그리고 그 순간 그녀 자신의 처지와도 닮은 어떤 두려움과 공포가 깃든 목소리—이런 것들이 해리엇의 마음을 움직였다. 그녀는 서둘러 문으로 가 문을 열었다.\n\n\"들어가도 될까요, 아니면 여기서 말할까요?\" 여자가 그녀의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"무엇을 원하는 거예요? 할 말이 뭐예요?\"\n\n\"많지 않아요, 하지만 다 말하게 해 줘요. 안 그러면 영영 말하지 못할 거예요. 지금 나는 그냥 가 버리고 싶은 유혹이 들어요. 무슨 손들이 나를 문에서 끌어내는 것만 같아요. 이번 한 번만 나를 믿어 줄 수 있다면, 들어가게 해 줘요!\"\n\n그녀의 그 절박함이 또다시 통했고, 두 사람은 작은 부엌의 난롯불 빛 속으로 들어갔다. 전에 그녀가 앉아서 먹고 옷을 말리던 곳이었다.\n\n\"거기 앉아요,\" 앨리스가 그녀 곁에 무릎 꿇으며 말했다, \"그리고 나를 봐요. 나를 기억하지요?\"\n\n\"기억해요.\"\n\n\"내가 무엇이었는지, 또 어디서 왔는지, 누더기를 걸치고 다리를 절며, 사나운 바람과 비바람을 머리에 맞으며 왔다고 말한 것을 기억하지요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그날 밤 내가 돌아와서, 당신이 준 돈을 흙바닥에 내던지고, 당신과 당신 핏줄을 내던졌던 걸 알지요. 자, 지금 여기 무릎 꿇은 나를 봐요. 지금의 나는 그때의 나보다 덜 간절한가요?\"\n\n\"당신이 청하는 것이,\" 해리엇이 부드럽게 말했다, \"용서라면—\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 아니에요!\" 상대가 자존심 어린, 사나운 눈빛으로 응수했다. \"내가 청하는 건 믿어 달라는 거예요. 자, 그때의 나로서도, 지금의 나로서도, 내가 믿을 만한 사람인지 당신이 판단해 봐요.\"\n\n여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채, 두 눈은 난롯불에 두고, 그 불빛이 그녀의 망가진 미모와 거칠게 헝클어진 검은 머리를 비추는 가운데, 그녀는 그 긴 머리 타래 한 가닥을 어깨 너머로 끌어다 손에 감고는, 말하는 동안 생각에 잠긴 듯 그것을 깨물고 잡아 뜯으며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"내가 젊고 예뻤을 때, 그리고 이게,\" 손에 쥔 머리채를 경멸하듯 잡아당기며, \"그저 곱게 다루어지기만 하고 아무리 칭찬해도 모자랄 정도였을 때, 어릴 적 나를 그다지 마음에 두지 않던 우리 어머니가 내 장점을 알아채고는 나를 아끼고, 나를 자랑스러워했어요. 어머니는 탐욕스럽고 가난해서, 나를 일종의 재산으로 삼으려 했지요. 어떤 귀부인도 딸을 두고 그런 생각을 한 적이 없을 거예요, 분명히. 또 그런 듯이 행동한 적도 없겠지요—그런 일은 결코 없으니까요, 우리 다 알다시피—그러니 그건, 딸을 그릇되게 키워서 거기서 화가 비롯되는 어미의 사례란 오직 우리 같은 비참한 인간들 사이에서만 있다는 걸 보여 주는 거지요.\"\n\n마치 그 순간 듣는 이가 있다는 것조차 잊은 듯 난롯불을 바라보며, 그녀는 긴 머리 타래를 손에 단단히 칭칭 감으며 꿈결 같은 투로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그 끝에 무슨 일이 있었는지는 말할 것도 없지요. 우리 같은 처지에서 그런 일들의 끝에 비참한 결혼 따위가 오지는 않아요. 그저 비참함과 파멸뿐이지요. 비참함과 파멸이 내게 닥쳤어요—내게 닥쳤어요.\"\n\n난롯불을 음울하게 응시하던 눈을 재빨리 들어 해리엇의 얼굴을 향하며, 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 시간을 허비하고 있군요, 한시도 헛되이 할 틈이 없는데. 하지만 내가 모든 걸 다 생각해 보지 않았더라면, 지금 여기 와 있지도 않을 거예요. 비참함과 파멸이 내게 닥쳤어요, 말했듯이. 나는 수명 짧은 노리개가 되었다가, 그런 물건들보다도 더 잔인하고 무심하게 내던져졌지요. 누구의 손에 그랬다고 생각해요?\"\n\n\"왜 내게 묻는 거예요?\" 해리엇이 말했다.\n\n\"왜 떨고 있어요?\" 앨리스가 간절한 눈빛으로 응수했다. \"그자의 그 처사가 나를 악마로 만들었어요. 나는 비참함과 파멸 속으로 자꾸자꾸 더 깊이 가라앉았지요. 어떤 강도질에 가담했는데—그 일의 모든 부분에 가담했지만 그 몫만은 받지 못했어요—발각되어, 친구 하나 없이, 한 푼도 없이 재판을 받게 됐어요. 비록 어린 계집애였지만, 그자의 말 한마디가 나를 구할 수 있었다 해도, 그자에게 한마디 청하느니 차라리 죽음을 택했을 거예요. 정말이에요! 어떤 식으로든 고안해 낼 수 있는 어떤 죽음이라도요. 하지만 늘 탐욕스러운 우리 어머니가 내 이름으로 그자에게 사람을 보내, 내 처지의 진상을 죄다 일러바치고, 마지막으로 작은 선물 하나를 겸손히 빌고 간청했어요—이 손가락 수만큼도 안 되는 몇 파운드를요. 그자가 믿기로는 제 발치에 엎드린 셈인 나를, 그 비참함 속에서 손가락을 튕겨 내쫓고, 이 보잘것없는 기억의 표시 하나조차 남기지 않고 가 버린 게 누구라고 생각해요? 내가 외국으로 보내져, 자기를 더 성가시게 할 일이 없는 곳으로 가서, 죽어 거기서 썩어 버리리라는 데 흡족해하면서요. 이게 누구라고 생각해요?\"\n\n\"왜 내게 묻는 거예요?\" 해리엇이 되풀이했다.\n\n\"왜 떨고 있어요?\" 앨리스가 그녀의 팔에 손을 얹고 그 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다, \"그 답이 당신 입술에 떠올라 있는데요! 당신 오빠 제임스였어요.\"\n\n해리엇은 점점 더 떨었지만, 자기에게 머무는 그 간절한 눈길에서 시선을 돌리지 않았다.\n\n\"당신이 그자의 누이라는 걸 알았을 때—그건 그날 밤이었지요—나는 지치고 다리를 절며 돌아와, 당신의 선물을 내팽개쳤어요. 그날 밤 나는, 그자를 인적 없는 곳에서 곁에 아무도 없이 찾아낼 수만 있다면, 그를 찔러 죽이려고 지치고 절뚝거리는 몸으로 온 세상을 다 헤매고 다닐 수도 있을 것만 같았어요. 내가 그 모든 것에 진심이었다는 걸 믿어요?\"\n\n\"믿어요! 맙소사, 그런데 왜 또 온 거예요?\"\n\n\"그 뒤로,\" 앨리스가 여전히 그녀의 팔을 붙잡고, 여전히 같은 눈빛으로 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다, \"나는 그자를 봤어요! 내 눈으로 그자를 좇았어요. 환한 대낮에요. 내 가슴속에 잠들어 있던 원한의 불씨가 조금이라도 남아 있었다면, 그자에게 눈길이 닿는 순간 그게 활활 타올랐어요. 그자가 어떤 오만한 사내에게 못된 짓을 해서, 그를 자기 철천지원수로 만들었다는 걸 당신도 알지요. 만약 내가 그 사내에게 그자에 대한 정보를 일러바쳤다면 어떨까요?\"\n\n\"정보라뇨!\" 해리엇이 되풀이했다.\n\n\"만약 내가 당신 오빠의 비밀을 아는 자를 찾아냈다면 어떨까요. 그가 어떻게 달아났는지, 그와 그 도주의 동행이 어디로 갔는지를 아는 자를요. 만약 내가 그자로 하여금 자기가 아는 것을 한마디 한마디 죄다, 그걸 들으려고 숨어 있는 원수 앞에서 털어놓게 만들었다면 어떨까요? 만약 내가 그때 곁에 앉아, 이 원수의 얼굴을 들여다보며, 그것이 거의 사람의 것이라 할 수 없을 만큼 변해 가는 걸 지켜보았다면요? 만약 내가 그가 미쳐서 추격에 나서는 걸 봤다면요? 만약 내가 지금, 그가 사람이라기보다 악귀에 가까운 모습으로 길에 올라, 몇 시간 안에 그자를 따라잡고야 말리라는 걸 안다면요?\"\n\n\"손을 치워요!\" 해리엇이 움찔 물러서며 말했다. \"가 버려요! 당신 손길이 내겐 끔찍해요!\"\n\n\"내가 이 일을 했어요,\" 상대가 그 방해에도 아랑곳없이 간절한 눈빛으로 말을 이었다. \"내가 정말로 그렇게 한 듯이 말하고, 또 그렇게 보이지 않나요? 내가 하는 말을 믿어요?\"\n\n\"믿을 수밖에 없을 것 같아요. 내 팔을 놓아 줘요!\"\n\n\"아직요. 한순간만 더요. 이토록 오래 이어져, 나를 이 일을 하도록 내몬 내 복수심이 얼마나 컸을지 짐작이 갈 거예요.\"\n\n\"끔찍해요!\" 해리엇이 말했다.\n\n\"그러니 지금 당신이 나를 본다면,\" 앨리스가 쉰 목소리로 말했다, \"이렇게 다시, 땅에 조용히 무릎 꿇고, 내 손은 당신 팔에 얹고, 내 눈은 당신 얼굴에 두고 있는 나를 본다면, 내가 하는 이 말에 예사롭지 않은 간절함이 담겨 있다는 걸, 또 내 가슴속에서 예사롭지 않은 싸움이 벌어졌다는 걸 믿어 줄 거예요. 이런 말을 입에 올리기가 부끄럽지만, 나는 마음이 누그러졌어요. 나 자신이 경멸스러워요. 온종일, 그리고 어젯밤 내내 나 자신과 싸웠어요. 하지만 까닭도 없이 그자에게 마음이 누그러져서, 할 수만 있다면 내가 한 일을 돌이키고 싶어요. 그자를 쫓는 사람이 저토록 눈멀고 앞뒤 없는 동안에는 그 둘이 만나지 않게 하고 싶어요. 어젯밤 그가 나서는 걸 당신이 봤다면, 그 위험을 더 잘 알 거예요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 막을 수 있어요? 내가 뭘 할 수 있죠?\" 해리엇이 외쳤다.\n\n\"밤새도록,\" 상대가 다급히 말을 이었다, \"나는 그자의 꿈을 꿨어요—그러면서도 잠은 자지 않았지만요—피투성이가 된 그자의 꿈을요. 온종일 그자가 내 곁에 어른거렸어요.\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 할 수 있죠?\" 해리엇이 그 말에 몸서리치며 외쳤다.\n\n\"누구든 그자에게 편지를 쓰든, 사람을 보내든, 직접 가든 할 사람이 있다면, 한시도 지체하지 말게 하세요. 그는 디종에 있어요. 그 지명을 알아요, 그게 어디 있는지요?\"\n\n\"알아요.\"\n\n\"그가 원수로 만든 그 사내가 광란에 빠져 있다고, 만약 그자가 그 사내의 접근을 우습게 안다면 그 사내를 모르는 거라고 경고하세요. 그가 길에 올랐다고—분명히 그래요!—서둘러 다가오고 있다고 말하세요. 시간이 있는 동안—시간이 있다면요—어서 달아나라고, 아직은 그를 만나지 말라고 다그치세요. 한 달쯤이면 몇 년만큼의 차이를 만들어 낼 거예요. 나 때문에 그 둘이 마주치지 않게 하세요. 그곳만 아니면 어디든요! 지금만 아니면 언제든요! 그자의 원수가 그자를 쫓아, 제 힘으로 그자를 찾아내게 하세요, 나를 통해서가 아니라요! 그러지 않아도 이미 내 머리에는 충분히 짊어진 게 있으니까요.\"\n\n난롯불은 더 이상 그녀의 새까만 머리에도, 치켜든 얼굴에도, 간절한 눈에도 비치지 않았다. 그녀의 손은 해리엇의 팔에서 사라졌고, 그녀가 있던 자리는 텅 비어 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER LIV. The Fugitives\n\n차 마실 시간, 자정을 한 시간 앞둔 무렵. 장소는 프랑스식 아파트로, 대여섯 개쯤의 방으로 이루어져 있었다—음침하고 차가운 현관 혹은 복도, 식당, 응접실, 침실, 그리고 안쪽 응접실, 즉 부두아르가 있었는데, 이 부두아르는 다른 방들보다 작고 더 외진 곳이었다. 이 모든 방은 본계단 쪽으로 난 큰 문 한 쌍으로 막혀 있었으나, 각 방마다 두세 쌍씩 제 나름의 문을 갖추고 있어서, 아파트의 나머지 부분과, 혹은 벽 안쪽의 어떤 좁은 통로와 통하는 여러 갈래의 길을 만들고 있었다. 그 통로는 이런 집들에서 드물지 않은 일이지만, 아래쪽에 잘 드러나지 않는 출구가 있는 어떤 뒷계단으로 이어져 있었다. 이 모든 것이 어찌나 큰 호텔의 이층에 자리 잡고 있었던지, 한가운데의 네모진 안뜰 한 면에 늘어선 창문 한 줄을 다 차지하지도 못할 정도였는데, 그 안뜰은 저택의 네 면 전부가 굽어보고 있었다.\n\n이 방들에는, 우울하게 느껴질 만큼 충분히 빛이 바랜, 그러면서도 위세를 부리는 겉치레로 살림살이의 세세한 부분들을 막아 세우고 거추장스럽게 할 만큼 충분히 눈부신, 어떤 화려한 기운이 감돌고 있었다. 벽과 천장에는 금박과 채색이 입혀져 있었고, 마룻바닥은 밀랍을 먹여 윤이 났으며, 진홍색 휘장이 창과 문과 거울에서 꽃줄 모양으로 늘어져 있었다. 또 나뭇가지나 짐승의 뿔처럼 옹이지고 서로 뒤얽힌 촛대가 벽 판자에서 튀어나와 있었다. 그러나 낮 동안, (지금은 꼭 닫혀 있는) 격자 덧창을 열어 빛을 들이면, 이 호화로운 장식들 사이로 닳고 해진 자국, 먼지 자국, 햇볕과 습기와 연기의 흔적, 그리고 오랫동안 쓰이지도 거주되지도 않고 방치된 흔적이 눈에 띄었다. 그럴 때면 삶의 그런 구경거리와 장난감들도 마치 생명을 지닌 것처럼 예민해 보였고, 감옥에 갇힌 사람들이 그러하듯 시들어 가는 듯했다. 밤이 와서 활활 타오르는 촛불 무리가 켜져도, 비록 전반적인 광채가 그것들을 그늘 속에 밀어 넣기는 했으나, 그 흔적을 온전히 지워 버리지는 못했다.\n\n이날 밤, 밝은 촛불의 광채와 거울에 비친 그 반사, 금박 조각들과 화려한 색채는 한 방에만—방금 헤아린 방들 가운데 그 안쪽의 더 작은 방에만 한정되어 있었다. 등불 하나가 희미하게 타고 있는 현관에서, 열린 문들의 어두운 원근을 통해 바라보면, 그 방은 보석처럼 빛나고 값져 보였다. 그 광휘의 한가운데에 한 아름다운 여인이 앉아 있었다—이디스였다.\n\n그녀는 혼자였다. 여전히 똑같이 반항적이고 경멸에 찬 여인이었다. 뺨은 조금 야위었고, 눈은 보기에 조금 더 커지고 더 윤기가 돌았으나, 그 오만한 자태는 조금도 다름이 없었다. 그녀의 이마에는 수치의 그늘이 없었고, 뒤늦은 뉘우침이 그 도도한 목을 굽히지도 않았다. 여전히 위풍당당하고 위엄에 차 있으면서도, 자기 자신과 그 밖의 모든 것에는 아랑곳하지 않은 채, 그녀는 검은 눈을 내리깔고 앉아 누군가를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n책도, 바느질거리도, 그 어떤 소일거리도 없이, 오로지 자기 생각만이 더디게 흐르는 시간을 달래 주었다. 어떤 목적이, 어떤 빈틈이라도 채울 만큼 강력한 목적이 그녀를 사로잡고 있었다. 입술을 꼭 다문 채로—잠시라도 그것을 통제에서 풀어 놓으면 그 입술은 파르르 떨렸다—콧구멍을 벌름거리며, 두 손을 맞잡은 채, 가슴속에서 부풀어 오르는 그 목적과 함께, 그녀는 앉아서 기다렸다.\n\n바깥문에서 열쇠 소리가 나고 현관에서 발소리가 들리자, 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 \"거기 누구냐?\"라고 외쳤다. 대답은 프랑스어였고, 두 남자가 짤랑거리는 쟁반을 들고 들어와 저녁 식사 준비를 했다.\n\n\"누가 그렇게 하라고 시켰지?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"무슈께서 그렇게 분부하셨습니다, 이 아파트를 빌리시기로 한 그때에 말입니다. 무슈께서는, 도중에 한 시간 머무르시며 마담께 편지를 남기실 때 그렇게 말씀하셨습니다—마담께서는 그 편지를 분명 받으셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"받았네.\"\n\n\"천 번 용서를 빕니다! 혹시 그것이 잊혔을지도 모른다는 갑작스러운 걱정이 그를 덮쳤다는 것이었다\"—인근 식당에서 온, 큼직한 수염을 기른 대머리 사내였다—\"절망과 더불어 말입니다! 무슈께서는 저녁 식사가 그 시각에 준비되어 있어야 한다고 말씀하셨습니다. 또한 편지에 적어 내리신 분부에 대해 마담께 미리 알려 두셨다고도 하셨습니다. 무슈께서는 황금머리 식당에 영광을 베푸시어 저녁 식사를 정선되고 섬세하게 차려 달라 청하셨습니다. 무슈께서는 황금머리 식당에 두신 그 신뢰가 잘못되지 않았음을 아시게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n이디스는 더는 말이 없었으나, 그들이 두 사람 몫으로 식탁을 차리고 그 위에 포도주를 놓는 동안 생각에 잠겨 바라보았다. 그녀는 그들이 일을 끝내기도 전에 일어나, 등불을 하나 들고 침실로, 또 응접실로 들어가, 서두르면서도 면밀하게 모든 문을 살펴보았다. 특히 앞쪽 방에서 벽 안의 통로로 통하는 문 하나를 유심히 보았다. 그녀는 거기서 열쇠를 빼내어 바깥쪽에 꽂아 두었다. 그러고는 다시 돌아왔다.\n\n그 사내들은—둘 가운데 두 번째는 거무튀튀하고 신경질적인 얼굴에, 재킷을 걸치고, 말끔히 면도를 했으며, 새카만 머리를 바짝 깎은 자였다—식탁 준비를 끝내고 서서 그것을 바라보고 있었다. 앞서 말을 했던 자가, 마담께서는 무슈가 도착하시기까지 오래 걸리리라 보시느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"모르겠네. 다 마찬가지야.\"\n\n\"용서를 빕니다! 저녁 식사가 있지 않습니까! 당장 드셔야 합니다. 무슈께서는(천사처럼—아니 프랑스 사람처럼—그게 그거지만, 아무튼 프랑스어를 하시는 분이) 당신의 시간 엄수에 대해 대단히 힘주어 말씀하셨습니다. 그런데 영국 민족은 시간 엄수에 그토록 대단한 재능이 있으니 말입니다. 아! 무슨 소리지! 맙소사, 여기 무슈가 오셨습니다. 보십시오!\"\n\n과연, 두 사내 가운데 다른 자가 들여보낸 무슈가, 번득이는 이를 드러내며 어두운 방들을 지나 마치 입처럼 다가왔다. 그러고는 그 빛과 색채의 성소에 다다라, 온몸을 곧추세운 채, 마담을 끌어안고 프랑스어로 그녀를 자기의 사랑스러운 아내라 불렀다.\n\n\"맙소사! 마담께서 기절하시려나 봅니다. 마담께서 기쁨에 겨워 어쩔 줄 모르시는군요!\" 수염을 기른 대머리 사내가 그것을 알아채고 소리쳤다.\n\n마담은 다만 움츠리며 부르르 떨었을 뿐이다. 그 말이 채 끝나기도 전에, 그녀는 커다란 의자의 벨벳 등받이에 한 손을 얹고 서 있었다. 몸은 한껏 곧추세우고, 얼굴은 미동도 없었다.\n\n\"프랑수아가 저녁 식사를 가지러 황금머리 식당으로 날아갔습니다. 이런 때 그는 천사나 새처럼 날아다니지요. 무슈의 짐은 그분 방에 있습니다. 모든 게 마련되었습니다. 저녁 식사는 곧 도착할 것입니다.\" 대머리 사내가 절을 하고 미소를 지으며 이런 사실들을 알렸고, 이윽고 저녁 식사가 도착했다.\n\n따뜻한 요리들은 보온 그릇에 담겨 있었고, 차가운 요리들은 이미 차려져 있었으며, 그릇을 바꿔 놓을 여분은 찬장 위에 마련되어 있었다. 무슈는 이 배치에 만족했다. 식탁이 작은 것이 그의 마음에 썩 들었다. 보온 그릇을 바닥에 내려놓고 물러가라고 했다. 접시는 자기 손으로 치우겠다는 것이었다.\n\n\"용서를 빕니다!\" 대머리 사내가 정중하게 말했다. \"그건 안 될 말씀입니다!\"\n\n무슈의 생각은 달랐다. 그날 밤 더는 시중이 필요 없다고 했다.\n\n\"하오나 마담께서는—\" 대머리 사내가 넌지시 운을 뗐다.\n\n\"마담께는,\" 무슈가 응수했다, \"당신의 하녀가 있네. 그것으로 충분해.\"\n\n\"백만 번 용서를 빕니다! 아닙니다! 마담께는 하녀가 없으십니다!\"\n\n\"나는 혼자 여기 왔다네,\" 이디스가 말했다. \"내가 그렇게 하기로 한 거지. 나는 여행에 아주 익숙해. 시중은 필요 없네. 내게 누구도 보낼 필요 없어.\"\n\n그리하여 무슈는, 처음에 안 된다고 했던 그 일을 끝내 고집하여, 두 시중꾼을 바깥문까지 뒤따라가서는, 그들이 나간 뒤 밤을 대비해 문을 단단히 잠갔다. 대머리 사내는 나가면서 절을 하려고 돌아서다가, 마담이 여전히 커다란 의자의 벨벳 등받이에 한 손을 얹고 서 있는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 똑바로 앞만 바라보고 있을 뿐 자기에게는 전혀 아랑곳하지 않는다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n카커가 문을 잠그는 소리가 중간의 방들을 통해 울려 퍼지며, 그 가장 멀고 끝에 있는 방까지 죽은 듯 잦아들어 다다랐을 때, 그 소리에 대성당 시계가 열두 시를 치는 소리가 이디스의 귓가에 섞여 들었다. 그녀는 그가, 마치 자기도 그 소리를 듣고 귀 기울이는 듯, 잠시 멈춰 서는 것을 들었다. 그러더니 그가 정적을 가로질러 긴 발소리의 행렬을 늘어놓으며, 지나오는 동안 등 뒤로 모든 문을 닫으면서 그녀에게로 돌아왔다. 그녀의 손은 잠시 벨벳 의자를 떠나, 식탁 위에 놓인 칼을 손 닿는 데로 가져왔다. 그러고는 전과 똑같이 서 있었다.\n\n\"혼자서 여기 오다니 참 이상도 하지, 내 사랑!\" 그가 들어서며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 그녀가 응수했다.\n\n그녀의 어조가 어찌나 거칠었던지, 고개를 홱 돌리는 것이 어찌나 사납고, 자태가 어찌나 거부에 차 있고, 찌푸린 이맛살이 어찌나 새카맸던지, 그는 등불을 손에 든 채 멈춰 서서, 마치 그녀가 자기를 꼼짝 못 하게 후려치기라도 한 듯 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그러니까 내 말은,\" 그가 마침내 등불을 내려놓으며, 더없이 정중한 미소를 지으며 되풀이했다, \"혼자서 여기 오다니 참 이상하다는 거요! 분명 쓸데없는 조심이었고, 자칫 일을 그르칠 뻔도 했소. 르아브르나 루앙에서 시중들 사람을 구하기로 되어 있었고, 또 (내 사랑, 당신이 여자들 가운데 가장 아름답듯이) 가장 변덕스럽고 까다로운 여자였다 한들, 그럴 시간은 넉넉했을 텐데.\"\n\n그녀의 눈이 그를 향해 이상하게 번득였으나, 그녀는 손을 의자에 얹은 채 서서 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"나는 한 번도,\" 카커가 다시 입을 열었다, \"오늘 밤만큼 당신이 아름다워 보이는 걸 본 적이 없소. 이 가혹한 시련의 시간 동안 내가 마음속에 품고 다녔던, 밤낮으로 응시했던 그 그림조차도 실물에는 미치지 못하는구려.\"\n\n한마디도 없었다. 눈길조차 없었다. 그녀의 눈은 내리깐 속눈썹에 완전히 가려져 있었으나, 고개는 곧추세우고 있었다.\n\n\"가혹하고 매정한 조건이었소!\" 카커가 미소 지으며 말했다, \"하지만 그것도 다 채워지고 지나갔으니, 지금 이 순간을 더욱 달콤하고 더욱 안전하게 만들어 주는구려. 시칠리아가 우리의 은신처가 될 거요. 세상에서 가장 한가롭고 편한 그곳에서, 내 영혼이여, 우리 둘 다 옛 노예살이를 보상받읍시다.\"\n\n그가 즐거이 그녀에게로 다가가던 순간, 그녀가 별안간 식탁에서 칼을 낚아채고 한 발 뒤로 물러섰다.\n\n\"가만히 있어!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"안 그러면 당신을 죽여 버리겠어!\"\n\n그녀의 그 갑작스러운 변화, 그 눈에서 번득이고 그 이마를 환히 밝히는 치솟는 분노와 격렬한 혐오가, 마치 불길이 그를 막아 세우기라도 한 듯 그를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"가만히 있어!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"목숨이 아깝거든 한 발짝도 다가오지 마!\"\n\n둘은 서로를 바라보며 서 있었다. 그의 얼굴에는 분노와 경악이 어려 있었으나, 그는 그것을 억누르고 가볍게 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 이런! 쳇, 우리뿐인데, 아무도 보지도 듣지도 못하는데. 이런 정숙한 척하는 수작으로 나를 겁줄 수 있을 거라 생각하오?\"\n\n\"이 외진 곳을, 가까이에 도움 청할 데가 없다는 걸 내게 상기시켜서,\" 그녀가 사납게 대꾸했다, \"내가 마음먹은 어떤 목적, 결심한 어떤 길에서 나를 겁주어 물러나게 할 수 있을 거라 생각해? 일부러 여기 혼자 와 있는 이 나를? 내가 당신을 두려워했다면 당신을 피하지 않았겠어? 당신을 두려워했다면, 이 깊은 밤에 여기 와서, 이제 말하려는 것을 당신 면전에 대고 말하려 하겠어?\"\n\n\"그래서 그게 뭔데,\" 그가 말했다, \"이 아름다운 표독스러운 여인아? 다른 어떤 여자가 제일 기분 좋을 때보다도 이러는 게 더 아름답군그래?\"\n\n\"아무것도 말하지 않겠어,\" 그녀가 응수했다, \"당신이 저 의자로 돌아갈 때까지는—다만 이것 한 번만 다시 말하지—내게 다가오지 마! 한 발짝도 더 가까이 오지 마. 분명히 말하는데, 만약 그러면, 하늘이 우리를 굽어보는 가운데, 당신을 죽여 버리겠어!\"\n\n\"나를 당신 남편으로 착각한 거요?\" 그가 이를 드러내며 응수했다.\n\n대꾸할 가치도 없다는 듯, 그녀는 팔을 뻗어 의자를 가리켰다. 그는 입술을 깨물고, 이맛살을 찌푸리고, 웃더니, 좌절하고 우유부단하며 안달하는 기색—감추지 못하는 그 기색을 띤 채 거기 앉았다. 그러고는 신경질적으로 손톱을 깨물며, 그녀의 변덕을 재미있어하는 척하면서도, 쓰라린 낭패감을 안고 그녀를 곁눈질로 바라보았다.\n\n그녀는 칼을 식탁 위에 내려놓고, 한 손으로 가슴께를 짚으며 말했다.\n\n\"여기 사랑의 노리개 따위가 아닌 무언가가 들어 있어. 다시 한번 당신 손이 닿느니, 차라리 이걸 당신한테 쓰겠어—그리고 당신도 알지, 내가 말하는 이 순간에—이 세상에 사는 그 어떤 기어다니는 것에게보다도 덜 망설이며 말이야.\"\n\n그는 농담조로 웃는 시늉을 하며, 저녁 식사가 식어 가니 어서 연극을 끝내라고 그녀에게 청했다. 그러나 그녀를 바라보는 그 은밀한 눈길은 한층 더 음침하고 음울해졌고, 그는 한 번 발을 바닥에 구르며 욕설을 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"몇 번이나,\" 이디스가 그에게 가장 어두운 눈길을 던지며 말했다, \"당신의 뻔뻔한 악행이 모욕과 능멸로 나를 덮쳤지? 그 매끄러운 태도로, 그 비웃는 말과 눈길로, 몇 번이나 나는 내 구애와 내 결혼을 두고 빈정거림을 당했지? 몇 번이나 당신은 그 사랑스럽고 상처 입은 아이를 향한 내 사랑의 상처를 헤집어 갈가리 찢어 놓았지? 이 년 동안 내가 그 위에서 몸부림쳐 온 그 불길을, 그것이 나를 가장 고통스럽게 할 때, 몇 번이나 당신은 부채질하며, 절박한 복수를 하라고 나를 부추겼지?\"\n\n\"틀림없이, 부인,\" 그가 응수했다, \"당신은 셈을 잘 적어 두었을 테고, 그것도 꽤 정확하겠지. 자, 이디스. 당신의 가엾은 남편에게라면, 이 정도면 충분히 괜찮았겠지만—\"\n\n\"흥, 만약,\" 그녀가, 그가 아무리 태연한 척해도 그 아래에서 움츠러들 만한, 오만한 경멸과 혐오의 눈길로 그를 훑어보며 말했다, \"그를 경멸할 다른 모든 이유가 깃털처럼 다 날아가 버린대도, 당신을 자기 고문이자 총신으로 삼았다는 사실 하나만으로도, 그 이유들의 자리를 거의 채우고도 남았을 거야.\"\n\n\"그래서 그게 당신이 나와 함께 달아난 이유요?\" 그가 비아냥거리며 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그리고 우리가 마지막으로 얼굴을 맞대고 있는 이유지. 비열한 인간! 우리는 오늘 밤 만났고, 오늘 밤 헤어져. 내가 말을 마친 그 순간부터 단 한 순간도 나는 여기 머무르지 않을 테니까!\"\n\n그는 가장 흉악한 표정으로 그녀를 돌아보며, 손으로 식탁을 움켜쥐었다. 그러나 일어서지도, 달리 대꾸하거나 위협하지도 않았다.\n\n\"나는,\" 그녀가 그를 굳건히 마주하며 말했다, \"어릴 적부터 수치를 당하고 무감각해지도록 단련된 여자야. 내 영혼이 진저리를 칠 때까지 나는 내놓아지고 퇴짜 맞고, 진열되고 값 매겨졌어. 내게 밑천이 될 만한 재주나 기품이 하나라도 있으면, 그것은 마치 동네 외치는 사람이 거리거리에서 떠벌리듯, 내 몸값을 올리려고 죄다 자랑되고 팔려 나갔지. 나의 가엾고 오만한 가족들은 그것을 지켜보며 흡족해했고, 우리 사이의 모든 정은 내 가슴속에서 죽어 갔어. 그들 가운데 애완견에게 쏟을 만큼이라도 내가 마음 쓰는 이는 하나도 없어. 나는 이 세상에 홀로 서 있어, 이 세상이 내게 얼마나 공허한 것이었는지, 그리고 나 자신이 그 안에서 얼마나 공허한 한 부분이었는지 똑똑히 기억하면서. 당신은 이걸 알지, 그리고 그래서 내 평판 따위가 내게는 아무 가치도 없다는 것도 알지.\"\n\n\"그래, 나도 그럴 거라 짐작했소,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그걸 계산에 넣었지,\" 그녀가 되받았다, \"그래서 나를 쫓아다닌 거고. 나를 이 꼴로 빚어낸 그 손들이 날마다 부리는 짓에 대해, 무관심 말고는 어떤 저항도 하지 못할 만큼 무덤덤해져서, 또 내 결혼이 적어도 그들이 나를 이리저리 내다 파는 짓만은 막아 주리라는 것을 알았기에, 나는 목에 올가미를 두른 어떤 여자가 어느 장터에서 팔려 나가듯, 그처럼 치욕스럽게 내가 팔려 가도록 내버려 두었지. 당신도 알잖아.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 그가 이를 죄다 드러내며 말했다, \"나도 알지.\"\n\n\"그리고 그걸 계산에 넣었지,\" 그녀가 다시 한번 되받았다, \"그래서 나를 쫓아다닌 거고. 결혼한 그날부터, 나는 한 비열한 악당으로부터 그처럼 새로운 수치에—그처럼 끈질긴 유혹과 추근거림에(마치 그것이 가장 천박한 말로 쓰여 길목마다 내 손에 떠밀어지기라도 한 듯 또렷이 드러난 그 추근거림에) 내몰린 나 자신을 발견했어. 그래서 나는 그때까지 진정한 굴욕을 알지 못했던 것처럼 느껴졌지. 이 수치를 내 남편이 내게 못 박았어. 자기가 직접 나를 그 안에 에워쌌고, 자기 손으로, 자기 행위로, 수백 번을 거듭하여 나를 그 안에 적셔 넣었지. 그리하여—그 두 사람에게 내가 쉴 수 있는 모든 자리에서 떠밀리고—그 두 사람에게, 내 안에 남은 사랑과 부드러움의 마지막 도피처를 내어 주거나, 아니면 그 죄 없는 대상에게 새로운 불행이 되거나 둘 중 하나로 내몰리고—이쪽에서 저쪽으로 쫓기며, 한쪽을 피하면 다른 쪽이 들러붙는 처지가 되어, 두 사람 다를 향한 내 분노는 거의 광기에 이르도록 치솟았어. 어느 쪽을 향해 더 높이 치솟았는지는 나도 모르겠어—주인인지, 아니면 그 종인지!\"\n\n그는 그녀가 분개한 아름다움의 그 절정에 차서 자기 앞에 서 있는 것을 유심히 지켜보았다. 그녀는 단호했다. 그는 보았다. 굴할 줄 모르는 여인이었다. 벌레를 두려워하지 않듯 그를 조금도 두려워하지 않았다.\n\n\"명예니 정조니 하는 것을 당신에게 무어라 말하겠어!\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그게 당신에게 무슨 의미가 있겠으며, 내 입에서 나온들 무슨 의미가 있겠어! 하지만 만약 내가, 당신 손이 살짝만 닿아도 혐오감으로 내 피가 싸늘해진다고, 당신을 처음 보고 미워한 그 순간부터 지금까지—그 뒤로 매분 당신을 알아 갈수록 내 본능적인 역겨움이 한층 더해진 지금까지, 당신은 내게 이 땅에 둘도 없는 혐오스러운 존재였다고 말한다면, 그러면 어쩌겠어?\"\n\n그는 희미하게 웃으며 대꾸했다, \"허! 그러면 어쩌겠소, 나의 여왕이여?\"\n\n\"그날 밤, 당신이 거들었던 그 장면에 대담해져서, 감히 내 방에 와서 내게 말을 걸었던 그날 밤,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"무슨 일이 있었지?\"\n\n그가 어깨를 으쓱하더니 웃었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었지?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"당신 기억이 어찌나 또렷한지,\" 그가 말했다, \"당신이 다 떠올릴 수 있을 거라 믿어 의심치 않소.\"\n\n\"떠올릴 수 있지,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"들어 봐! 그때 당신은 이 도망을—이 도망이 아니라, 당신이 도망이라 여겼던 그 도망을 제안하면서, 내게 말했지. 당신에게 그 밀회를 허락하고, 당신이 그러는 게 좋다고 여기면 거기서 발각되도록 당신을 내버려 둠으로써, 또 그 전에도 여러 번 당신과 단둘이 있도록 내버려 둠으로써—그 기회를 만든 건 당신이었다고 했지만—또 내가 남편에게 혐오감 말고는 아무 감정도 없고 나 자신에 대해서도 아무 미련이 없다고 당신에게 공공연히 털어놓음으로써—나는 끝장났다고. 내 이름을 더럽힐 힘을 당신에게 내주었으며, 정숙하다는 평판 속에 사는 내 삶이 당신 입김 하나에 달려 있게 되었다고.\"\n\n\"사랑에서는 어떤 책략도—\" 그가 미소 지으며 끼어들었다. \"옛 격언에도—\"\n\n\"그날 밤,\" 이디스가 말했다, \"그리고 바로 그때, 내가 오랫동안 무언가와—내 좋은 평판에 대한 존중이 아닌 무언가와—그게 무엇인지 나는 알 수 없지만, 어쩌면 그 마지막 도피처에 매달리려는 마음이었을 그 무언가와 벌여 온 싸움이 끝나 버렸어. 그날 밤, 그리고 바로 그때, 나는 격정과 원한 말고는 모든 것에서 등을 돌렸어. 나는 일격을 가해 당신의 오만한 주인을 먼지 속에 처박았고, 당신을 거기, 내 앞에, 지금 나를 바라보며 내가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 아는 그 자리에 앉혀 놓았지.\"\n\n그가 거센 욕설을 내뱉으며 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰다. 그녀는 손을 가슴께에 넣었고, 손가락 하나 떨지 않았으며, 머리카락 한 올도 흔들리지 않았다. 그가 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 그녀도 그러했다. 둘 사이에는 식탁과 의자가 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"이 남자가 그날 밤 내 입술에 제 입술을 댔고, 오늘 밤 또다시 그랬듯이 나를 제 팔에 안았던 것을 내가 잊는다면,\" 이디스가 그를 가리키며 말했다, \"내 뺨에 묻은 그 입맞춤의 오점을—플로렌스가 자기의 죄 없는 얼굴을 가져다 댔을 그 뺨에 묻은 오점을 내가 잊는다면—그 오점이 내게 뜨겁게 묻어 있는 동안 그 애를 만났던 일을, 그리고 그 애를 보았을 때 어떤 홍수처럼 내게 밀려들던 깨달음을—내 사랑이 불러온 박해에서 그 애를 풀어 주면서, 내 이름을 통해 그 애 이름에 수치와 오욕을 안겨 주었고, 또 앞으로 영원토록 나는 그 애 마음속에서 죄 지은 한 인간을 처음으로 피했던 일을 떠올리게 하는 외로운 형상이 되리라는 그 깨달음을 내가 잊는다면—그렇다면, 이제부터 내가 갈라선 남편이여, 나는 지난 이 년을 잊고, 내가 한 일을 되돌리며, 당신의 착각을 풀어 주겠어!\"\n\n번득이는 그녀의 눈이 잠시 치켜 올라갔다가 다시 카커에게로 떨어졌고, 그녀는 왼손에 편지 몇 통을 내밀었다.\n\n\"이것들 봐!\" 그녀가 경멸에 차서 말했다. \"당신은 당신이 쓰고 다니는 그 가짜 이름으로 이것들을 내게 부쳤지. 하나는 여기로, 몇 통은 내가 오는 길의 다른 곳으로. 봉인은 뜯지 않았어. 도로 가져가!\"\n\n그녀는 그것들을 손안에서 구겨 그의 발치에 내던졌다. 그리고 이제 그를 바라보는 그녀의 얼굴에는 미소가 어려 있었다.\n\n\"우리는 오늘 밤 만나고 오늘 밤 헤어져,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 시칠리아의 나날과 육욕의 안식에 너무 일찍 빠졌어. 당신은 좀 더 오래 알랑거리고, 아첨하고, 배신자의 노릇을 하면서, 더 부자가 될 수도 있었을 텐데. 당신은 그 향락의 은둔을 비싼 값을 치르고 사는 거야!\"\n\n\"이디스!\" 그가 손으로 그녀를 위협하며 되받았다. \"앉아! 이 짓을 그만둬! 대체 무슨 악마가 당신한테 들린 거야?\"\n\n\"그것들의 이름은 군대처럼 많지,\" 그녀가 마치 그를 짓뭉개 버리기라도 할 듯 그 오만한 몸을 곧추세우며 대꾸했다. \"당신과 당신 주인이 풍요로운 한 집안에서 그것들을 불러일으켰으니, 그것들이 당신들 둘 다를 갈가리 찢어 놓을 거야. 그에게 거짓되고, 그 죄 없는 아이에게 거짓되고, 어느 모로 보나 어디서나 거짓된 자여, 가서 나를 두고 떠벌리고, 그러면서 단 한 번이라도 당신이 거짓말을 하고 있음을 알아채고 이를 갈아 봐!\"\n* 군대처럼 많지: 신약성서(마가복음 5장)에서 예수가 귀신 들린 자에게 이름을 묻자 \"내 이름은 군대(Legion)니 우리가 많음이니이다\"라고 답한 데서 온 표현.\n\n그는 그녀 앞에 서서, 무어라 중얼거리고 위협하며, 그녀를 제압할 수 있을 무언가라도 찾는 듯 사방을 노려보았다. 그러나 그녀는 똑같이 굴할 줄 모르는 기개로, 조금도 흔들림 없이 그에게 맞섰다.\n\n\"당신이 떠벌리는 자랑 하나하나마다,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"나는 나의 승리를 거두지. 나는 당신 안에서 내가 아는 가장 비열한 인간을, 그 오만한 폭군의 기생충이자 도구를 골라내. 그래야 그자의 상처가 더 깊이 파이고, 더 쓰라리게 곪을 테니까. 떠벌려서 나를 위해 그자에게 복수해 줘! 당신은 오늘 밤 어떻게 여기 왔는지 알지. 거기 어떻게 움츠리고 서 있는지 알지. 내가 당신을 보는 빛깔만큼 가증스럽지는 않다 해도, 그만큼 경멸스러운 빛깔로 칠해진 당신 자신의 모습이 당신 눈에도 보이지. 그러니 떠벌려서, 당신 자신에게 나를 위한 복수를 해 줘.\"\n\n그의 입가에는 거품이 일었고, 이마에는 땀이 맺혔다. 만약 그녀가 단 한 번이라도, 단 반 순간만이라도 흔들렸더라면, 그는 그녀를 옴짝달싹 못 하게 붙들었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 바위처럼 단단했고, 그녀의 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈은 한 번도 그에게서 떠나지 않았다.\n\n\"우린 이렇게는 헤어지지 않아,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 당신을 그 미친 성깔대로 가게 놔둘 만큼 얼간이인 줄 알아?\"\n\n\"당신은,\" 그녀가 대답했다, \"내가 붙들려 머물 사람인 줄 알아?\"\n\n\"한번 해 보지, 내 사랑,\" 그가 사납게 고갯짓을 하며 말했다.\n\n\"가까이 와서 한번 해 보겠다면, 하느님의 자비가 당신에게 있기를!\" 그녀가 응수했다.\n\n\"그럼,\" 그가 말했다, \"내가 그런 떠벌림이니 자랑이니 하는 짓을 안 한다면 어쩔 거요? 나도 돌아선다면? 자!\"—그러더니 그의 이가 다시 번쩍 빛났다. \"우린 이걸 두고 협정을 맺어야 해, 안 그러면 나도 무슨 뜻밖의 수를 쓸지 모르니까. 앉아, 앉으라고!\"\n\n\"너무 늦었어!\" 그녀가 불을 뿜는 듯한 눈으로 외쳤다. \"나는 내 명성과 좋은 이름을 바람결에 던져 버렸어! 내게 들러붙을 그 수치를 견디기로 마음먹었지—그것이 거짓으로 들러붙은 것임을 안다는 것도—당신 역시 그걸 안다는 것도—그리고 그는 그것을 모르며, 결코 알 수 없고, 결코 알게 되지도 않으리라는 것도 알면서 말이야. 나는 죽어 가면서도 아무 내색도 하지 않을 거야. 이걸 위해서, 나는 이 깊은 밤에 여기 당신과 단둘이 와 있는 거야. 이걸 위해서, 나는 가짜 이름으로, 당신의 아내인 척하며 여기서 당신을 만난 거야. 이걸 위해서, 나는 저 사내들에게 여기 모습을 보였고, 여기 남겨졌던 거야. 이제 그 무엇도 당신을 구할 수 없어.\"\n\n그는 그녀를, 그 아름다움 그대로 마룻바닥에 못 박아, 두 팔을 옆구리에 떨구게 하여, 자기 손아귀에 넣을 수만 있다면 제 영혼이라도 팔았을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 바라보면서 그녀가 두렵지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 그녀 안에서 도저히 거스를 수 없는 어떤 힘을 보았다. 그는 그녀가 필사적이며, 그녀의 그 꺼지지 않는 증오가 그 무엇 앞에서도 멈추지 않으리라는 것을 보았다. 그의 눈은 그처럼 거칠고 어울리지 않는 결의로 그녀의 흰 가슴께에 찔러 넣은 그 손을 좇았고, 만약 그 손이 자기를 겨누었다가 빗나간다면, 그 손은 그만큼 빠르게 거기, 그녀 자신을 겨눌 것이라는 생각이 들었다.\n\n그래서 그는 감히 그녀에게로 다가서지 못했다. 그러나 그가 들어왔던 문이 등 뒤에 있었기에, 그는 그것을 잠그려고 뒷걸음질했다.\n\n\"마지막으로, 내 경고를 들어! 당신 자신을 조심해!\" 그녀가 말하며 다시 미소 지었다. \"당신은 배신당했어, 모든 배신자가 그러하듯. 당신이 이곳에 있다는 것, 혹은 있을 예정이었다는 것, 혹은 있었다는 것이 알려졌어. 내가 살아 있는 것이 분명하듯, 오늘 밤 거리에서 마차 안에 있는 내 남편을 보았어!\"\n\n\"이 갈보 같은 년, 거짓말이야!\" 카커가 소리쳤다.\n\n바로 그 순간, 현관에서 종이 요란하게 울렸다. 그가 새파랗게 질렸고, 그녀는 마치 그 부름에 그 소리가 응답해 온 마법사처럼 손을 치켜들었다.\n\n\"들어 봐! 저 소리 들리지?\"\n\n그는 등을 문에 기댔다. 그녀에게 어떤 변화가 이는 것을 보고, 그녀가 자기를 지나쳐 가려고 다가오는 줄 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 어느새 그녀는 침실로 통하는 맞은편 문을 통해 사라졌고, 그 문들은 그녀의 등 뒤로 닫혔다.\n\n그녀가 일단 돌아서자, 그 굽힐 줄 모르고 양보할 줄 모르던 표정이 일단 바뀌자, 그는 자기가 그녀를 능히 다룰 수 있겠다고 느꼈다. 그는 이 한밤의 경보로 일어난 갑작스러운 공포가 그녀를 굴복시켰다고 생각했고, 그녀의 그 극도로 지친 상태를 생각하면 더욱 그러기 쉬우리라 여겼다. 그는 문을 활짝 열어젖히고, 거의 곧바로 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n그러나 방은 어두웠고, 그녀가 그의 부름에 아무런 대답도 하지 않자, 그는 하는 수 없이 등불을 가지러 돌아갔다. 그는 등불을 치켜들고, 그녀가 어느 구석엔가 웅크리고 있는 것을 보게 되리라 기대하며 사방을 두루 둘러보았으나, 방은 비어 있었다. 그래서 그는 낯선 곳에 있는 사람의 미덥지 못한 걸음걸이로 응접실로, 또 식당으로 차례차례 들어가, 겁에 질려 두리번거리며 칸막이와 긴 의자 뒤를 들여다보았다. 그러나 그녀는 거기에 없었다. 아니, 현관에도 없었으니, 그곳은 어찌나 휑한지 한눈에 다 볼 수 있었다.\n\n이 내내, 종 울리는 소리는 끊임없이 거듭되었고, 바깥에 있는 자들은 문을 두들기고 있었다. 그는 등불을 멀찍이 내려놓고, 그쪽으로 다가가 귀를 기울였다. 여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 떠들고 있었다. 적어도 그 가운데 둘은 영어였다. 문이 두꺼웠고 몹시 어수선했지만, 그는 그 가운데 한 목소리를 너무도 잘 알고 있었기에 그것이 누구의 목소리인지 의심할 여지가 없었다.\n\n그는 다시 등불을 집어 들고, 모든 방을 빠르게 되짚어 오면서, 방을 하나씩 나설 때마다 멈춰 서서, 불을 머리 위로 치켜든 채 그녀를 찾아 둘러보았다. 그가 그렇게 침실에 서 있을 때, 벽 안의 작은 통로로 통하는 문이 그의 눈에 들어왔다. 그가 그리로 가 보니 문은 반대편에서 잠겨 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 빠져나가면서 베일을 떨어뜨렸고, 그것이 문에 끼인 채였다.\n\n이 내내 계단에 있는 사람들은 종을 울리고, 손과 발로 두드려 댔다.\n\n그는 겁쟁이는 아니었다. 그러나 이 소리들, 그 전에 벌어진 일, 현관에서 돌아오는 길에도 그를 어리둥절하게 만든 이 장소의 낯섦, 그의 계략이 좌절된 것(이상한 말이지만, 만약 그 계략이 성공했더라면 그는 훨씬 더 대담했을 것이다), 때아닌 시각, 어떤 우호적인 도움이라도 청할 만한 사람이 가까이 아무도 없다는 기억, 무엇보다도, 자기가 그 신뢰를 짓밟고 그토록 배신하여 속여 넘긴 그 사내가, 자기 얼굴에서 가면이 벗겨진 채 자기를 알아보고 도전하려고 거기 와 있다는 갑작스러운 깨달음—그의 심장마저 납덩이처럼 쿵쾅거리게 만든 그 깨달음이, 그의 온몸을 공포로 꿰뚫었다. 그는 베일이 끼인 그 문을 열어 보려 했으나, 억지로 열 수가 없었다. 그는 창문 하나를 열고, 덧창의 격자 사이로 안뜰을 내려다보았다. 그러나 너무 높은 곳이었고, 바닥의 돌은 무자비했다.\n\n종소리와 두드리는 소리가 여전히 계속되고—그의 공포도 그러하여—그는 침실에 있는 그 문으로 돌아가, 거듭하여 새로 힘을 쓰며, 한 번 한 번 앞선 것보다 더 끈질기게 애쓴 끝에, 그것을 비틀어 열었다. 작은 계단이 멀지 않은 곳에 있는 것을 보고, 밤공기가 올라오는 것을 느끼며, 그는 모자와 외투를 가지러 살그머니 돌아갔다가, 등 뒤로 그 문을 할 수 있는 한 단단히 닫고는, 등불을 손에 든 채 살금살금 내려가, 거리가 보이자 그것을 끄고, 한구석에 놓아둔 다음, 별들이 빛나고 있는 바깥으로 나섰다.\n\n# CHAPTER LV. Rob the Grinder loses his Place\n\n「분쇄기」 롭이 일자리를 잃다\n거리와 안뜰을 가르는 철문을 지키는 문지기는 자기 오두막의 작은 쪽문을 열어 둔 채 어디론가 가 버렸다. 의심할 바 없이 큰 계단 입구에서 들려오는 멀리 떨어진 소란에 한몫 끼려고 간 것이리라. 카커는 빗장을 살그머니 들어 올리고 살금살금 빠져나가, 삐걱거리는 철문을 되도록 소리 나지 않게 등 뒤로 닫고는 서둘러 자리를 떴다.\n\n굴욕과 부질없는 분노의 열병 속에서, 그를 사로잡은 공포가 그를 완전히 지배해 버렸다. 그 공포가 어찌나 높이 치솟았던지, 그는 두 시간 전만 해도 전혀 안중에도 없던 그 사내와 마주치느니 차라리 거의 어떤 위험이든 눈먼 채 무릅쓰려 했을 것이다. 결코 예상하지 못한 그의 거센 도착, 그 목소리의 울림, 두 사람이 얼굴을 맞대고 마주칠 뻔했던 그 아슬아슬함—처음의 순간적인 경악의 충격이 지나간 뒤였다면, 그는 이런 것쯤은 끝까지 견뎌 내고, 그 어떤 악당 못지않게 자기 죄에 대담한 낯을 들이밀었을 것이다. 그러나 자기가 묻어 둔 지뢰가 도리어 자기 발밑에서 터져 버린 듯한 일은, 그의 모든 강단과 자만을 갈가리 찢고 산산조각 낸 것 같았다. 어떤 파충류처럼 걷어차이고, 덫에 걸려 조롱당하고, 자기가 천천히 그 마음에 독을 부어 넣어 마침내 자기 쾌락의 한낱 도구로 가라앉혀 놓았다고 여겼던 그 도도한 여인에게 도리어 역공당하고 짓밟혀, 자기 속임수에 자기가 속았음을 깨닫고 여우 가죽이 벗겨진 채, 그는 부끄럽고 천하고 두려운 몸으로 슬그머니 달아났다.\n\n거리를 살금살금 빠져나가는 동안, 추격당한다는 이 두려움과는 전혀 동떨어진 또 다른 공포가, 마치 전기 충격처럼 갑자기 그를 덮쳐 왔다. 알 수도 없고 설명할 수도 없는 어떤 환영 같은 공포였는데, 땅의 떨림과—날개 단 죽음처럼 무언가가 공중을 가르며 휙 쓸고 지나가는 것과 결부되어 있었다. 그는 그것을 지나가게 두려는 듯 움츠러들었다. 그것은 가 버린 것도 아니었고, 애초에 거기 있었던 적도 없었으나, 뒤에 남긴 그 소름 끼치는 공포란 얼마나 깜짝 놀랄 만한 것이었던가.\n\n그는 근심으로 가득 찬 그 사악한 얼굴을 밤하늘로 들어 올렸다. 그곳에서는, 그가 처음 바깥 공기 속으로 살그머니 나섰을 때와 똑같이, 평화로 가득 찬 별들이 그를 비추고 있었다. 그는 멈춰 서서 무엇을 해야 할지 생각했다. 법이 그를 보호해 주지 않을지도 모르는 낯설고 외딴곳에서 사냥당할 것이라는 두려움—자기 계획의 폐허 한가운데 이토록 갑작스레 홀로 남겨진 데서 비롯된, 그곳이 낯설고 외딴곳이라는 느낌의 생경함—이탈리아나 시칠리아 같은, 어느 어두운 길모퉁이에서든 자기를 암살하라고 사람을 살 수도 있다고 생각되는 곳에 지금 피신처를 구하는 것에 대한 더 큰 두려움—죄와 공포의 변덕스러움—어쩌면 자기 모든 계략이 거꾸로 돌아선 것과 행동에서 어떤 공명을 이룬 까닭이었는지, 그것이 그로 하여금 자기도 발길을 돌려 영국으로 가도록 떠밀었다.\n\n\"어쨌든 거기가 더 안전해. 만일 이 어리석은 놈에게 한판 맞붙기로 결심하지 않는다 해도,\" 그는 생각했다, \"지금 여기 외국에 있는 것보다는 거기서 추적당할 가능성이 더 적어. 그리고 만일 맞붙기로 한다면(이 빌어먹을 발작이 끝나고 나면), 적어도 나는 말 한마디 나눌 사람도, 의논할 사람도, 곁에 서 줄 사람도 없이 혼자는 아닐 거야. 쥐처럼 몰려서 들볶이지는 않을 거야.\"\n\n그는 이디스의 이름을 중얼거리며 주먹을 꽉 쥐었다. 육중한 건물들의 그늘 속을 살금살금 따라가면서, 그는 이를 악물고 그녀의 머리 위에 무시무시한 저주를 퍼붓듯 중얼거렸으며, 마치 그녀를 찾기라도 하듯 좌우를 두리번거렸다. 그렇게 해서 그는 어느 여관 안마당의 문 앞까지 슬그머니 다가갔다. 사람들은 잠자리에 들어 있었으나, 그가 종을 울리자 곧 등불을 든 사내 하나가 나타났고, 그와 함께 그는 이내 어둑한 마차 보관소 안에서 파리까지 가는 낡은 사륜마차를 빌리는 흥정을 하고 있었다.\n\n흥정은 짧게 끝났고, 곧 말을 데리러 사람을 보냈다. 말이 도착하면 마차가 자기 뒤를 따라오게 하라는 말을 남기고, 그는 다시 슬그머니 빠져나가, 읍내를 지나, 오래된 성벽을 지나, 탁 트인 길로 나섰다. 그 길은 어두운 평원을 따라 한 줄기 시냇물처럼 미끄러져 사라지는 것 같았다.\n\n그 길은 어디로 흘러가는 것일까? 그 끝은 무엇일까? 그가 마음속에 그런 어떤 물음을 품은 채 멈춰 서서, 가느다란 나무들이 길을 표시해 주는 음울한 평지를 바라보고 있을 때, 다시 그 죽음의 비행이 휙 다가왔다가, 다시 격렬하고 거역할 수 없는 기세로 지나가더니, 다시 그의 마음속의 한낱 공포에 지나지 않게 되었다—그 풍경처럼 어둡고, 그 가장 먼 끝자락처럼 형체가 분명치 않은 공포였다.\n\n바람도 없었고, 밤의 깊은 그늘 위로 지나가는 그림자 하나 없었으며, 소리도 없었다. 도시는 여기저기 불빛이 켜진 채 그의 뒤편에 놓여 있었고, 별이 총총한 세계들은 첨탑과 지붕의 석조 건축물에 가려져 있었는데, 그 건축물들은 하늘을 배경으로 거의 어떤 형체도 드러내지 못했다. 어둡고 적막한 거리가 그의 사방 곳곳에 펼쳐져 있었고, 시계가 희미하게 두 시를 치고 있었다.\n\n그는 오랜 시간 같고 먼 길 같은 거리를 앞으로 나아갔다. 자주 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울이면서. 마침내 말방울 소리가 그의 초조한 귀를 반겼다. 어떤 때는 더 부드럽게, 어떤 때는 더 크게, 어떤 때는 들리지 않다가, 어떤 때는 험한 길 위에서 아주 느리게 울리고, 어떤 때는 경쾌하고 흥겹게 다가오더니, 마침내 큰 고함과 채찍질 소리와 함께, 눈까지 천으로 감싼 그림자 같은 마부가 발버둥 치는 네 마리 말을 그의 곁에 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"거기 누구요! 무슈?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"무슈께서 이 깜깜한 한밤중에 먼 길을 걸어오셨군요.\"\n\n\"상관없소. 사람마다 제 할 일이 있는 법이지. 역참에 다른 말을 주문해 둔 사람이 있었소?\"\n\n\"천 마리 악마가!—아, 실례했습니다! 다른 말이요? 이 시각에? 아니요.\"\n\n\"이보게, 친구. 나는 무척 급하다네. 우리 얼마나 빨리 달릴 수 있는지 한번 봅시다! 빠르면 빠를수록 술값이 더 많아질 거요. 자, 그럼 출발하세! 어서!\"\n\n\"이랴! 워! 이랴! 히!\" 검은 풍경 위로, 흙먼지를 물보라처럼 흩날리며, 전속력으로 내달렸다!\n\n덜컹거리는 소리와 소란이 그 도망자의 다급하고 어긋난 생각들과 메아리쳐 울렸다. 바깥에도 분명한 것이 없었고, 안에도 분명한 것이 없었다. 사물들이 휙휙 스쳐 지나가며, 서로 뒤섞이고, 어렴풋이 보이는가 싶더니, 어수선하게 시야에서 사라져, 가 버렸다! 길 바로 옆으로 변해 가는 울타리와 오두막 조각들 너머에는, 음울하게 내려앉은 황무지가 있었다. 그의 마음속에 떠올랐다가 모습을 드러내자마자 사라져 버리는 변화무쌍한 영상들 너머에는, 공포와 분노와 좌절된 악행의 검은 광막함이 펼쳐져 있었다. 이따금, 산의 바람 한 줄기가 멀리 쥐라산맥에서 불어와, 평원을 따라 잦아들었다. 때로는, 그토록 사납고 끔찍하던 그 돌진이, 다시 그의 환상 속을 휩쓸고 지나갔다가, 사라지면서, 그의 피에 한기를 남겼다.\n\n말들의 머리가 뒤섞인 혼란 위로 어른거리는 등불은, 그림자 같은 마부와, 펄럭이는 그의 망토와 한데 어우러져, 그의 생각에 응답하듯 분명치 않은 형체를 수천 가지나 만들어 냈다. 책상과 책에 몸을 숙인 채, 기억 속 자세 그대로 있는 낯익은 사람들의 그림자들, 그가 달아나고 있는 그 사내나 이디스의 기이한 환영들, 울리는 방울 소리와 구르는 바퀴 소리 속에서 되풀이되는, 입에 올려졌던 말들, 어젯밤을 한 달 전으로, 한 달 전을 어젯밤으로 만들어 버리는 시간과 장소의 혼란—이제 집은 희망 너머로 멀어졌다가, 이제 당장 닿을 수 있는 곳에 있고—그의 마음속과 그의 사방의 온갖 소란, 불협화, 다급함, 어둠, 혼란.—이랴! 히! 검은 풍경 위로 전속력으로 내달렸다. 흙먼지가 물보라처럼 날리고, 김을 내뿜는 말들이 마치 저마다 악마라도 태운 듯 콧김을 뿜으며 날뛰고, 어두운 길 위에서 미친 듯한 승리감에 휩싸여 달려갔다—어디로?\n\n다시 그 이름 없는 충격이 빠르게 다가왔고, 그것이 지나갈 때, 방울들이 그의 귓속에서 \"어디로?\" 하고 울렸다. 바퀴들이 그의 귓속에서 \"어디로?\" 하고 으르렁거렸다. 그 모든 소음과 덜컹거림이 그 외침으로 형체를 갖추었다. 빛과 그림자가 마치 작은 도깨비들처럼 말들의 머리 위에서 춤추었다. 이제 멈춤은 없었다. 늦춤도 없었다! 가자, 가자! 어두운 길 위로 그를 싣고 미친 듯이 달려라!\n\n그는 무엇 하나 제대로 생각할 수가 없었다. 한 가지 사색의 주제를 다른 주제와 떼어 내어, 한 번에 일 분이라도 그것 하나에만 머무를 만큼 따로 떼어 둘 수가 없었다. 지난날의 억제에 대한 음탕한 보상을 얻으려던 그의 계획이 무너진 것, 자기에게 진실하고 너그러웠으나 그가 그 가장 사소한 도도한 말과 표정조차 수년간 이자까지 쳐서 마음속에 쌓아 둔 그 사람에게 저지른 배신이 뒤엎인 것—거짓되고 음흉한 사내들은 늘 자기가 아첨하는 대상을 속으로 멸시하고 싫어하며, 자기가 무가치하다고 아는 그 경의를 바치고 받는 일에 늘 분개하는 법이므로—이런 것들이 그의 마음속에 가장 두드러진 주제들이었다. 자기를 그토록 덫에 빠뜨리고 복수를 한 그 여인을 향한 도사린 분노가 늘 거기 있었고, 그녀에게 보복할 거칠고 일그러진 계략들이 그의 머릿속을 떠다녔으나, 그 무엇도 또렷하지 않았다. 다급함과 모순이 그의 모든 생각에 배어 있었다. 이렇게 열에 들떠 부질없는 생각에 그토록 골몰하는 와중에도, 그의 한결같은 단 하나의 생각은, 어떤 막연한 때까지 사색을 미루겠다는 것이었다.\n\n그러다, 두 번째 결혼 이전의 옛 시절이 그의 기억 속에 떠올랐다. 그는 자기가 그 사내아이를 얼마나 질투했는지, 그 계집아이를 얼마나 질투했는지, 얼마나 교묘하게 침입자들을 멀찍이 떨어뜨려 놓고, 자기 말고는 아무도 넘지 못할 원을 자기 봉 둘레에 그어 놓았는지를 떠올렸다. 그러고는 생각했다. 이 모든 짓을 한 것이, 고작 그 가엾은 봉 하나로부터, 겁먹은 도둑처럼 지금 달아나기 위함이었던가?\n\n그는 자기 비겁함을 두고 자기 자신에게 손찌검이라도 하고 싶었으나, 그것이야말로 그의 패배의 바로 그 그림자여서, 그것과 떼어 낼 수가 없었다. 자기 자신의 간계에 대한 확신이 단 한 방에 그토록 산산이 부서진 것—자기 자신이 알기에도 그토록 비참한 도구였다는 것—은 마치 마비된 것과도 같았다. 그는 무력한 사나움으로 이디스에게 격노했고, 돔비 씨를 미워했고, 자기 자신을 미워했으나, 그래도 여전히 달아나고 있었고, 그 밖에 달리 아무것도 할 수가 없었다.\n\n거듭거듭 그는 뒤에서 바퀴 소리가 나는지 귀를 기울였다. 거듭거듭 그의 환상은 그 소리가, 점점 더 크게 다가오는 것을 들었다. 마침내 그는 이를 어찌나 확신했던지, 차라리 거리를 잃는 한이 있더라도 그런 불확실함보다는 낫다고 여겨, \"멈춰!\" 하고 외쳤다.\n\n그 말에 곧 마차와 말들과 마부가, 한꺼번에 뒤엉켜 길을 가로질러 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"제기랄!\" 마부가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 외쳤다, \"무슨 일이오?\"\n\n\"쉿! 저게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"뭐가요?\"\n\n\"저 소리?\"\n\n\"아, 맙소사, 조용히 해, 이 빌어먹을 도둑놈아!\" 방울을 흔드는 말 한 마리를 향해 그가 말했다. \"무슨 소리 말이오?\"\n\n\"뒤에서. 또 다른 마차가 전속력으로 달려오는 게 아니오? 저기! 저게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"이 돼지대가리 악당아, 가만있어!\" 다른 말 한 마리를 향해 외쳤는데, 그놈이 또 다른 말을 물어, 그놈이 나머지 두 마리를 놀라게 해, 그것들이 날뛰며 뒷걸음질 쳤다. \"오는 건 아무것도 없소.\"\n\n\"아무것도.\"\n\n\"네, 아무것도 없습니다, 저기 동트는 것 말고는.\"\n\n\"자네 말이 맞는 것 같군. 정말이지 이제 아무 소리도 안 들려. 가세!\"\n\n뒤엉킨 마차는, 말들에게서 피어오르는 김 구름에 반쯤 가려진 채, 처음에는 천천히 나아갔다. 가는 길에 공연히 제지당한 마부가 뚱한 얼굴로 주머니칼을 꺼내, 채찍에 새 가죽끈을 매단 까닭이었다. 그러더니 \"이랴, 워! 이랴, 히!\" 다시 한번, 사납게 내달렸다.\n\n그리고 이제 별들이 흐려지고, 날이 어슴푸레 밝아 오자, 그는 마차 안에 서서 뒤를 돌아보며, 자기가 지나온 길을 알아볼 수 있었고, 그 묵직하게 펼쳐진 평원 전체에 시야에 들어오는 나그네가 하나도 없음을 볼 수 있었다. 곧 환한 대낮이 되었고, 해가 밀밭과 포도밭을 비추기 시작했다. 길가의 돌무더기 옆 작은 임시 오두막에서 일어난 외로운 일꾼들이, 여기저기서, 큰길을 보수하거나 빵을 먹으며 일하고 있었다. 이윽고, 하루의 노동이나 장터로 가는 농부들이 있었고, 가난한 오두막 문가에서 빈둥거리며 그가 지나가는 것을 멀거니 바라보는 이들도 있었다. 그러고 나서는 발목까지 진창에 잠긴 역참 마당이 나왔는데, 김을 내뿜는 두엄 더미와 반쯤 허물어진 거대한 헛간들이 있었다. 그리고 이 우아한 광경을 굽어보는, 창문이 반쯤 가려지고, 푸른 곰팡이가 난간 두른 테라스에서부터 작은 탑들 위 촛불 끄개 모양의 가느다란 꼭대기에 이르기까지 게으르게 기어오른, 거대하고 오래된, 그늘 한 점 없이 휑하니 번쩍이는 석조 성관이 있었다.\n\n마차 한구석에 침울하게 웅크린 채, 오로지 빨리 가는 데만 정신이 팔려—탁 트인 들판이 나올 때면 언제든, 일 마일을 내처 일어서서 뒤를 돌아보았는데, 그럴 때만은 예외였다—그는 여전히 사색을 막연히 미루며, 여전히 부질없는 생각에 늘 시달리며, 계속 나아갔다.\n\n수치와 실망과 낭패가 그의 가슴을 갉아먹었다. 따라잡히거나 마주칠지 모른다는 끊임없는 불안이—그가 가고 있는 길을 따라 마주 오는 나그네들에게조차 까닭 없이 겁을 먹었으므로—그를 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 밤중에 그를 덮쳐 왔던 그 견디기 힘든 두려움과 공포가, 낮에도 조금도 약해지지 않은 채 되돌아왔다. 단조로운 방울 소리와 말발굽 소리, 그의 불안과 부질없는 분노의 단조로움, 그가 빙글빙글 돌리고 또 돌린 공포와 후회와 격정의 단조로운 수레바퀴—이것들이 그 여정을, 자기 자신의 고통 말고는 무엇 하나 도무지 현실 같지 않은 하나의 환영처럼 만들어 버렸다.\n\n그것은 이런 환영이었다. 지평선을 향해 멀리 뻗어 나가, 늘 물러나기만 할 뿐 결코 닿지 못하는 긴 길들의 환영, 오르막과 내리막으로 이어진 포장이 형편없는 읍내들—그곳에서는 얼굴들이 어두운 문간과 흐릿한 창문으로 다가왔고, 진흙투성이 암소와 황소들이 줄지어 좁고 긴 거리에 팔리려고 매여 있었는데, 들이받고 음매 울며, 그 무딘 머리에 들이박을 만한 곤봉으로 후려치는 매를 맞고 있었다—다리들, 십자가들, 교회들, 역참 마당들, 제 뜻을 거슬러 새로 매여지는 말들, 김을 내뿜고 헐떡이며, 마구간 문가에서 축 늘어진 머리를 처량하게 한데 모으는 지난 구간의 말들, 검은 십자가들이 무덤 속에 비스듬히 박히고, 그 위의 시든 화환들이 떨어져 내리는 작은 묘지들의 환영, 그리고 다시, 오르막과 내리막으로, 저 미덥지 못한 지평선을 향해 제 몸을 끌어가는 길고 긴 길들의 환영.\n\n아침과 정오와 해 질 녘의, 밤과 일찍 떠오르는 달의 환영. 긴 길들을 잠시 뒤로하고 거친 포장도로에 다다른 것의, 그 위를 덜컹덜컹 부딪치며 달리다가, 집 지붕들 사이로 우뚝 솟은 큰 교회 탑을 올려다본 것의 환영. 마차에서 내려 허겁지겁 음식을 먹고, 아무런 기운도 북돋워 주지 못하는 포도주를 들이켠 것의 환영. 걸어 나오니 거지 떼 한가운데였던 것의 환영—눈꺼풀을 떨어 대는 맹인들을, 그 얼굴에 촛불을 들이대는 노파들이 이끌고 있었고, 백치 소녀들, 절름발이들, 간질 환자들, 중풍 환자들이 있었다—그 아우성 속을 지나가며, 어떤 추격자가 앞으로 밀고 들어오는 것을 알아볼까 다급한 두려움을 안고, 자기 자리에서 치켜든 얼굴들과 내뻗은 손들을 내려다본 것의 환영. 길고 긴 길 위로 다시 전속력으로 달려가며, 멍하고 얼이 빠진 채 한구석에 웅크리거나, 몸을 일으켜 몇 마일 떨어진 곳에서 그 끝없는 길의 한 자락 위에 달이 희미하게 비치는 곳을 바라보거나, 누가 뒤따라오는지 보려고 뒤를 돌아본 것의 환영.\n\n결코 잠들지 못한 것의, 그러나 때로는 눈을 감지 못한 채 꾸벅꾸벅 졸다가, 깜짝 놀라 벌떡 일어나, 상상 속 목소리에 소리 내어 대꾸한 것의 환영. 자기가 거기 있는 것을, 달아난 것을, 그녀를 놓아 보낸 것을, 그와 맞서 대들지 않은 것을 두고 자기 자신을 저주한 것의 환영. 온 세상과, 그러나 무엇보다 자기 자신과 죽기 살기로 다툰 것의 환영. 실려 가는 동안 자기 검은 심사로 모든 것을 시들게 한 것의 환영.\n\n그것은 과거와 현재의 일들이 모두 한데 뒤섞인, 열에 들뜬 환영이었다. 자기 인생과 여정이 하나로 뒤섞인 것의 환영. 가야만 하는 어딘가로 미친 듯이 떠밀려 가는 것의 환영. 그가 지나가는 새로운 것들 사이로 옛 장면들이 불쑥 솟아오르는 것의 환영. 지나간 일과 멀리 있는 일을 곱씹고 곰곰이 생각하며, 자기가 마주치는 실제 사물들에는 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않는 듯하면서도, 그것들에 어리둥절해하고 있다는, 그리고 그것들이 사라진 뒤에도 그 영상들이 모조리 자기 달아오른 머릿속에 빽빽이 들어차 있다는, 지치고 진을 빼는 의식에 사로잡혀 있는 것의 환영.\n\n변화에 변화를 거듭하되, 여전히 그 똑같은 단조로운 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리, 그리고 휴식 없음의 환영. 읍내와 시골, 역참 마당들, 말들, 마부들, 언덕과 골짜기, 빛과 어둠, 흙길과 포장길, 높은 곳과 움푹한 곳, 궂은 날씨와 갠 날씨, 그리고 여전히 그 똑같은 단조로운 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리, 그리고 휴식 없음의 환영. 마침내 더 분주한 길들을 따라, 저 멀리 수도를 향해 나아가며, 오래된 대성당들을 돌아 휙 지나고, 예전보다 길 위에 덜 드문드문 흩어진 작은 읍내와 마을들을 쏜살같이 통과하며, 지나가는 사람들이 자기를 쳐다보는 가운데 망토를 얼굴까지 끌어올린 채 한구석에 가려 앉아 있는 것의 환영.\n\n자꾸자꾸 굴러가며, 늘 사색을 미루면서도 늘 생각에 시달린 것의 환영. 자기가 길 위에 있은 시간을 셈할 수도, 자기 여정의 시각과 장소의 지점들을 헤아릴 수도 없었던 것의 환영. 목이 타고 어지러우며, 반쯤 미쳐 있는 것의 환영. 이 모든 것에도 불구하고, 마치 멈출 수 없기라도 한 듯 앞으로 밀고 나아가, 파리로 들어선 것의 환영—그곳에서는 탁한 강이, 떠들썩한 두 줄기 삶과 움직임의 흐름 사이로, 거침없이 제 빠른 물길을 이어 가고 있었다.\n\n그러고는, 다리들과 부두들과 끝없이 이어지는 거리들의 어수선한 환영. 술집들, 물장수들, 거대한 인파, 군인들, 마차들, 군대의 북소리, 아케이드들의 환영. 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리의 단조로움이 마침내 천지를 뒤덮은 소음과 소란 속에 묻혀 버린 것의 환영. 그가 들어왔던 관문과는 다른 관문을 통해 또 다른 마차를 타고 빠져나가면서 그 소음이 차츰 잦아든 것의 환영. 바닷가를 향해 나아갈수록, 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리의 단조로움과, 휴식 없음이 되살아난 것의 환영.\n\n다시 한번 해 질 녘과, 밤이 내린 것의 환영. 다시 긴 길들과, 깊은 밤과, 길가 창문들의 희미한 불빛들의 환영. 그리고 여전히 그 옛 단조로운 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리, 그리고 휴식 없음의 환영. 새벽과, 동틀 녘과, 해돋이의 환영. 언덕을 천천히 올라, 그 꼭대기에서 상쾌한 바닷바람을 느끼고, 멀리 파도의 가장자리에 아침 햇살이 비치는 것을 본 것의 환영. 밀물이 가득 찼을 때 항구로 내려가, 고기잡이배들이 떠가는 것과, 그것들을 기다리는 기쁨에 찬 아낙들과 아이들을 본 것의 환영. 물가에 널려 말려지는 그물들과 뱃사람들의 옷가지의 환영. 분주한 선원들과, 돛대와 삭구 사이로 높이 울리는 그들의 목소리의 환영. 물의 출렁임과 환함과, 천지에 가득한 반짝임의 환영.\n\n해안에서 멀어지며, 그곳이 물 위의 안개가 되었을 때, 갑판에서 그곳을 돌아본 것의 환영—해가 내리쬐는 곳마다 환한 땅뙈기가 여기저기 조금씩 트여 있었다. 잔잔한 바다의 너울과 번뜩임과 속삭임의 환영. 배의 항로 위, 대양에 또 다른 잿빛 선이, 빠르게 더 또렷하고 더 높이 자라나는 것의 환영. 그 위로 절벽들과 건물들과, 풍차 하나와, 교회 하나가, 점점 더 또렷이 보이게 되는 것의 환영. 마침내 잔잔한 물속으로 김을 내뿜으며 들어가, 사람들이 무리 지어 내려다보며 배에 탄 벗들에게 인사를 건네는 부두에 정박한 것의 환영. 배에서 내려, 그들 사이를 빠르게 지나, 누구든 피하면서, 마침내 다시 영국에 와 있는 것의 환영.\n\n그는 꿈결에, 자기가 아는 외딴 시골 마을로 내려가, 그곳에서 조용히 누워 지내면서, 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 남몰래 알아보고, 어떻게 행동할지 결정하리라 생각했었다. 여전히 그 똑같이 얼이 빠진 상태에서, 그는 철도의 어느 역을 떠올렸다. 거기서 자기 목적지로 갈라져 들어가야 했고, 거기에 조용한 여관이 하나 있었다. 그는 여기서 머물며 쉬리라고 어렴풋이 마음먹었다.\n\n이 작정을 품고, 그는 되도록 빨리 철도 객차 안으로 슬그머니 들어가, 거기 망토에 싸인 채 마치 잠든 듯 누워, 이내 바다에서 멀리 떨어져 내륙의 푸르름 깊숙이 실려 갔다. 목적지에 다다르자 그는 밖을 내다보며, 그곳을 주의 깊게 살폈다. 그곳에 대한 그의 인상은 틀리지 않았다. 작은 숲 가장자리에 자리한 외딴곳이었다. 이 목적에 맞게 새로 지었거나 개조한 집 단 한 채가, 산뜻한 정원에 둘러싸여 거기 서 있었다. 가장 가까운 작은 읍내는 몇 마일 떨어져 있었다. 그렇게 해서 그는 여기서 내렸다. 그러고는 아무에게도 들키지 않고 곧장 여관 안으로 들어가, 서로 통하는, 충분히 외진 위층 방 두 개를 잡았다.\n\n그의 목적은 쉬는 것, 그리고 자기 자신에 대한 통제와 마음의 평정을 되찾는 것이었다. 얼빠진 낭패감과 분노가—그래서 방 안을 거닐 때면 그는 이를 갈았다—그를 완전히 사로잡고 있었다. 멈추게 할 수도 방향을 잡아 줄 수도 없는 그의 생각은, 여전히 제멋대로 떠돌며, 그를 그 뒤로 질질 끌고 다녔다. 그는 망연자실했고, 죽도록 지쳐 있었다.\n\n그러나, 그가 다시는 결코 쉬어서는 안 된다는 저주라도 받은 듯, 졸음에 겨운 그의 감각은 의식을 놓아주지 않았다. 이 점에 관한 한, 그는 그것들에 대해, 마치 그것들이 다른 사람의 것이기라도 한 양, 아무런 영향력도 행사하지 못했다. 그것들이 그로 하여금 현재의 소리와 사물에 주목하게끔 강요한 것은 아니었으나, 그의 여정의 그 온통 다급한 환영에서 떠나려 하지 않았다. 그것은 끊임없이 그의 눈앞에 한꺼번에 펼쳐져 있었다. 그녀가 거기 서서, 그 어둡고 경멸 어린 눈으로 다시 그를 노려보았다. 그런데도 그는 여전히, 읍내와 시골을, 빛과 어둠을, 궂은 날씨와 갠 날씨를, 흙길과 포장길을, 언덕과 골짜기를, 높은 곳과 움푹한 곳을 가로질러 달려가고 있었다. 단조로운 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리, 그리고 휴식 없음에 지치고 겁먹은 채로.\n\n\"오늘이 무슨 요일인가?\" 그가 저녁 식사 준비를 하고 있는 종업원에게 물었다.\n\"요일 말씀이십니까, 손님?\"\n\n\"수요일인가?\"\n\n\"수요일이요, 손님? 아닙니다, 손님. 목요일입니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"내가 잊었군. 시각은 어떻게 되나? 내 시계가 안 감겨 있어서.\"\n\n\"다섯 시 몇 분 전입니다, 손님. 오랫동안 여행하셨나 봅니다, 손님?\"\n\"그렇다네.\"\n\n\"기차로요, 손님?\"\n\n\"그렇다네.\"\n\"무척 정신이 어지러우시지요, 손님. 저야 기차로 여행하는 일이 그리 익숙지 않습니다만, 손님, 신사분들이 흔히들 그렇게 말씀하시더군요.\"\n\n\"이곳에 신사들이 많이 오나?\"\n\n\"대체로 그런대로 옵니다, 손님. 지금은 아무도 안 계십니다. 요즘은 좀 한산하지요, 손님. 모든 게 한산합니다, 손님.\"\n\n그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그가 누워 있던 소파 위에 일어나 앉아, 두 팔을 각각 무릎에 얹고 앞으로 몸을 숙인 채, 바닥을 응시하고 있었다. 그는 단 일 분도 제 주의력을 다스릴 수가 없었다. 그것은 제멋대로 내달았으나, 단 한순간도 잠 속에 빠져들지는 않았다.\n\n그는 저녁 식사 후 포도주를 잔뜩 마셨으나, 헛일이었다. 그런 인위적인 수단으로는 그의 눈에 잠이 찾아들지 않았다. 한층 더 갈피를 잡지 못하게 된 그의 생각은, 그를 한층 더 무자비하게 그 뒤로 끌고 다녔다—마치 그런 속죄형을 선고받은 가엾은 자가, 야생마들의 발꿈치에 매달려 끌려가기라도 하는 것처럼. 망각도 없었고, 휴식도 없었다.\n\n마시고 곰곰이 생각하며, 상상 속에서 이리저리 끌려다니면서 얼마나 오래 앉아 있었는지는, 그 자신보다 더 정확히 말할 수 있는 이가 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그가 갑작스러운 공포에 벌떡 일어나 귀를 기울였을 때, 자기가 촛불 곁에 오랫동안 앉아 있었다는 것만은 알고 있었다.\n\n이제는, 정말로, 환상이 아니었다. 땅이 흔들렸고, 집이 덜컹거렸으며, 그 사납고 거센 돌진이 공중에 있었다! 그는 그것이 다가왔다가, 휙 쏜살같이 지나가는 것을 느꼈다. 창가로 황급히 달려가 그것이 무엇인지 보았을 때조차, 그는, 마치 바라보는 것이 안전치 않기라도 한 듯, 그것에서 움츠러든 채 서 있었다.\n\n그토록 매끄럽게 우레처럼 내달려, 멀리 골짜기를 가로지르며 한 줄기 섬광과 음산한 연기로 자취를 남기고는, 가 버린 그 불의 악마에게 저주가 있기를! 그는 마치 자기가 그 진로에서 뽑혀 나와, 갈가리 찢기는 것을 면하기라도 한 듯한 기분이 들었다. 그것의 가장 희미한 웅웅거림조차 잦아들고, 달빛 속에 한 점을 향해 뻗어 가는 것을 알아볼 수 있는 그 철길이 사막처럼 텅 비고 고요해진 지금까지도, 그것은 그를 움츠러들고 몸서리치게 만들었다.\n\n쉴 수가 없는 데다, 저항할 수 없이—혹은 그가 그렇게 여기기에—이 철길에 이끌려, 그는 밖으로 나가, 그 가장자리를 어슬렁거리며, 기차가 지나간 자취 위에 아직 김을 내뿜으며 놓여 있는 불씨들로 그것이 간 방향을 짚어 보았다. 그것이 사라져 간 방향으로 반시간쯤 어슬렁거린 뒤, 그는 돌아서서 반대편으로 걸었다—여전히 철길 가장자리를 따라서—여관 정원을 지나, 한참을 내려가며, 다리들과 신호기들과 등불들을 신기한 듯 바라보고, 또 다른 악마가 언제 지나갈까 궁금해했다.\n\n땅의 떨림과, 그의 귓속의 빠른 진동, 멀리서 들리는 날카로운 비명 소리, 빠르게 두 개의 붉은 눈으로 바뀌는 다가오는 희미한 빛, 그리고 이글거리는 석탄을 떨어뜨리는 사나운 불길, 으르렁대며 부풀어 오르는 거대한 덩어리가 저항할 수 없이 밀어닥치는 것, 거센 바람과 덜컹거림—또 한 대가 왔다가 가 버렸고, 그는 마치 제 몸을 지탱하려는 듯 문 하나를 붙들고 있었다!\n\n그는 또 한 대를, 그리고 또 한 대를 기다렸다. 그는 아까 있던 지점으로 도로 걸어갔다가, 다시 그곳으로 돌아오기를 거듭하며, 여전히, 자기 여정의 그 지긋지긋한 환영을 통해, 다가오는 이 괴물들을 기다렸다. 그는 어떤 기차가 그곳에 멈춰 서기를 기다리며 역 주변을 서성였다. 그러다 한 대가 정말로 멈춰 서서, 물을 받으려고 떼어 놓이자, 그는 그것과 나란히 서서, 그 묵직한 바퀴들과 놋쇠 전면을 지켜보며, 그것이 얼마나 잔인한 힘과 위력을 지녔는지 생각했다. 으윽! 그 거대한 바퀴들이 천천히 도는 것을 보며, 그 아래 깔려 짓이겨지는 것을 생각하다니!\n\n포도주와, 그리고 휴식의 결핍으로—그토록 지쳤건만 무엇으로도 가실 줄 모르는 그 결핍으로—어수선해진 채, 이 생각들과 사물들은 그의 머릿속에서 병적인 중요성을 띠어 갔다. 자정이 다 되어서야 비로소 자기 방으로 돌아갔을 때에도, 그것들은 여전히 그를 따라다녔고, 그는 또 한 대가 다가오기를 귀 기울이며 앉아 있었다.\n\n잠은 바라지도 않고 찾아든 침대에서도 마찬가지였다. 그는 여전히 누워 귀를 기울였다. 떨림과 진동이 느껴지면, 일어나 창가로 가서, (그 위치에서 볼 수 있는 한) 희미한 빛이 두 개의 붉은 눈으로 바뀌는 것과, 이글거리는 석탄을 떨어뜨리는 사나운 불길과, 휙 지나가며 달아나는 그 거인의 돌진과, 골짜기를 따라 남는 섬광과 연기의 자취를 지켜보았다. 그러고는, 거기에는 그에게 휴식이 없으므로, 해돋이에 떠날 작정인 방향을 흘끗 바라보았다. 그러고는 다시 누워, 또 한 대가 올 때까지, 자기 여정의 환영과, 그 옛 단조로운 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리에 시달렸다. 이것이 밤새도록 이어졌다. 자기 자신에 대한 통제를 되찾기는커녕, 밤이 깊어 갈수록, 가능하다면 그것을 점점 더 잃어 가는 것 같았다. 새벽이 밝아 올 때에도, 그는 여전히 생각에 시달리며, 여전히 더 나은 상태가 될 때까지 사색을 미루고 있었다. 과거와 현재와 미래가 모두 그의 앞에 어수선하게 떠다녔고, 그는 그중 어느 하나라도 차분히 바라볼 힘을 모조리 잃어버린 터였다.\n\n\"몇 시에,\" 그가, 이제 촛불을 들고 들어오는, 간밤에 자기 시중을 들었던 사내에게 물었다, \"내가 여기서 떠난다고 했지?\"\n\n\"네 시 십오 분쯤입니다, 손님. 급행이 네 시에 통과합니다, 손님.—그건 안 섭니다.\"\n\n그는 욱신거리는 머리를 손으로 쓸어내리며, 자기 시계를 보았다. 거의 세 시 반이었다.\n\n\"손님과 함께 가는 분은 아마 아무도 없을 겁니다, 손님,\" 사내가 말했다. \"여기 신사 두 분이 계십니다만, 손님, 그분들은 런던행 기차를 기다리고 계십니다.\"\n\n\"아무도 없다고 했던 것 같은데,\" 카커가, 화가 나거나 의심스러울 때 짓던 그 옛 미소의 유령을 띠고 그를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"그때는 없었습니다, 손님. 두 신사분이 밤중에, 여기 서는 단거리 기차로 오셨습니다, 손님. 따뜻한 물 드릴까요, 손님?\"\n\n\"아니, 그리고 촛불은 가져가게. 나한테는 날이 충분히 밝았으니까.\"\n\n옷을 반쯤 입은 채 침대에 몸을 던졌던 그는, 사내가 방을 나설 때 창가에 서 있었다. 밤에 뒤이어 차가운 아침빛이 찾아들었고, 하늘에는 이미, 다가오는 해의 붉은 기운이 번져 있었다. 그는 물로 머리와 얼굴을 씻었으나—그에게는 그 안에 어떤 식혀 주는 기운도 없었다—허둥지둥 옷을 입고, 셈을 치르고는, 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n공기가 그에게 숨결을 불어 올 때 한기 어리고 스산하게 느껴졌다. 이슬이 잔뜩 내려 있었고, 그토록 달아오른 몸인데도, 그것이 그를 떨게 했다. 간밤에 자기가 거닐던 곳과, 아침이 되어 그 의미를 잃은 채 타오르는 신호등들을 흘끗 본 뒤, 그는 해가 떠오르는 쪽으로 몸을 돌려, 그것이 풍경 위로 솟구쳐 오를 때, 그 영광 가운데서 그것을 바라보았다.\n\n그토록 경외롭고, 그 아름다움이 그토록 초월적이며, 그토록 신성하리만치 장엄했다. 그것이, 세상이 시작된 이래 제 광선이 비추어 온 그 모든 그릇됨과 사악함에도 아랑곳없이, 고요하고 평온하게 솟아오르는 곳을 향해, 그가 그 빛바랜 두 눈을 던졌을 때, 지상의 미덕과 천상의 미덕에 대한 어떤 미약한 감각이, 그에게마저, 모습을 드러내지 않았다고 누가 말할 수 있으랴? 그가 일찍이 누이나 형제를 한 가닥 다정함과 회한으로 떠올린 적이 있다면, 그것이 바로 그때가 아니었다고 누가 말할 수 있으랴?\n\n그는 그때 그런 한 가닥 감정이 필요했다. 죽음이 그에게 닥쳐 있었다. 그는 살아 있는 세상에서 떼어져—무덤 속으로 내려가고 있었다.\n\n그는 자기가 생각해 둔 그 시골 마을까지 가는 여비를 치렀다. 그러고는 홀로 이리저리 거닐며, 한쪽으로는 골짜기를 가로질러 뻗은 철길을 따라, 다른 쪽으로는 가까이 있는 어두운 다리 쪽을 바라보고 있었다. 그러다, 자기가 오락가락 거니는 그 나무 단의 한쪽 끝에 막혀 있는 곳에서 걸음을 돌릴 때, 그는 자기가 달아나 온 그 사내가, 자기가 들어왔던 바로 그 문에서 나오는 것을 보았다. 그리고 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다.\n\n놀라움의 그 빠르고 불안정한 동요 속에서, 그는 비틀거리다, 아래쪽 선로로 미끄러져 떨어졌다. 그러나 곧바로 발을 가누고는, 그 선로 위에서 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러나, 둘 사이에 좀 더 넓은 간격을 두려고, 짧고 빠르게 숨을 몰아쉬며 자기 추격자를 바라보았다.\n\n그는 한 차례 외침을 들었고—또 한 차례—그 얼굴이 복수에 찬 격정에서 희미한 메스꺼움과 공포로 바뀌는 것을 보았다—땅이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다—그 돌진이 닥쳤음을 한순간에 알아챘다—날카로운 비명을 질렀다—주위를 둘러보았다—대낮 속에 흐릿하게 침침해진 그 붉은 눈이 바로 코앞에 다가온 것을 보았다—두들겨 쓰러뜨려져, 채여 올려져, 들쭉날쭉한 맷돌 위로 휘말려, 그것이 그를 빙글빙글 돌리며, 사지를 갈가리 찢어 내고, 제 불같은 열기로 그의 생명의 물줄기를 핥아 들이고는, 그의 토막 난 살점들을 공중으로 내던졌다.\n\n얼굴을 알아본 적 있던 그 나그네가 혼절에서 깨어났을 때, 그는 멀리서 그들이 무언가 덮인 것을, 묵직하고 미동도 없이 널빤지 위에 놓인 것을, 네 사내 사이에 끼워 들고 오는 것을 보았다. 그리고 다른 이들이, 길 위에서 킁킁대며 그의 피를 한 줄기 재와 함께 빨아들이던 개 몇 마리를 쫓아 버리는 것을 보았다.\n\n# CHAPTER LVI. Several People delighted, and the Game Chicken disgusted\n\n해군 후보생은 온통 생기로 넘쳤다. 투츠 씨와 수전이 마침내 도착한 것이다. 수전은 정신이 나간 처녀처럼 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔고, 투츠 씨와 치킨은 응접실로 들어갔다.\n\n\"오, 내 예쁘고 사랑스럽고 다정한 플로이 아가씨!\" 니퍼가 플로렌스의 방으로 뛰어 들어오며 외쳤다, \"일이 이 지경에 이르다니, 시중들 사람 하나 없이, 내 집이라 부를 곳 하나 없이 여기 계시다니 내 소중한 비둘기를 이렇게 만나다니 하지만 다시는 절대로 절대로 떠나지 않을 거예요 플로이 아가씨, 제가 이끼를 모으지는 못할지언정 굴러다니는 돌멩이는 아니고 제 마음도 돌멩이는 아니니까요 그렇지 않으면 지금처럼 이렇게 터지지도 않았을 거예요 오 이런 오 이런!\"\n\n* 이끼를 모으지 못해도 굴러다니는 돌멩이는 아니다: \"구르는 돌에는 이끼가 끼지 않는다\"는 속담을 비튼 말.\n이 말들을 단 한 군데도 멈춤 없이 쏟아 내면서, 니퍼 양은 여주인 곁에 무릎을 꿇은 채 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았다.\n\n\"오 내 사랑!\" 수전이 외쳤다, \"지난 일은 다 알아요 다 알아요 내 가여운 아가 숨이 막혀요 숨 좀 쉬게 해 줘요!\"\n\n\"수전, 착하고 다정한 수전!\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"오 아가씨께 축복을! 아가씨가 어린아이였을 때 제가 그 어린 하녀였는데! 그런데 정말로, 정말로 진짜로 결혼을 하시는 거예요?\" 수전이 고통과 기쁨, 자랑스러움과 슬픔, 그 밖에 하느님만 아실 갖가지 뒤엉킨 감정에 북받쳐 외쳤다.\n\n\"누가 그러던?\" 플로렌스가 말했다.\n\n\"오 이런 맙소사! 그 더없이 순진한 토츠 씨가요,\" 수전이 히스테릭하게 대답했다. \"제가 그분 말이 틀림없다는 걸 알았어요 아가씨, 그분이 그토록 상심하셨으니까요. 그분은 세상에서 제일 헌신적이고 제일 순진한 아기 같은 분이세요! 그래서 우리 아가씨가,\" 수전은 다시 한번 꼭 끌어안고 눈물을 터뜨리며 말을 이었다, \"정말로 정말로 결혼을 하시는 거예요!\"\n\n니퍼가 이 주제로 끊임없이 되돌아올 때마다 거기에 어린 연민과 기쁨, 다정함과 보호하려는 마음과 안타까움이 뒤섞여 있었고, 그럴 때마다 고개를 들어 그 젊은 얼굴을 들여다보고 입을 맞추고는 다시 여주인의 어깨에 머리를 기대며 그녀를 어루만지고 흐느꼈으니, 그 나름으로는 이 세상에서 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 여성스럽고 갸륵한 모습이었다.\n\n\"자, 자!\" 곧 플로렌스의 달래는 목소리가 들렸다. \"이제 완전히 정신을 차렸구나, 다정한 수전!\"\n\n니퍼 양은 여주인의 발치 바닥에 주저앉아 웃고 흐느끼며, 한 손으로는 손수건을 눈에 갖다 대고 다른 손으로는 자기 얼굴을 핥는 다이오지니즈를 쓰다듬으면서, 한결 진정되었다고 고백했다. 그러고는 그 증거랍시고 조금 더 웃고 울었다.\n\n\"제, 제, 제 평생 그 토츠 씨 같은 분은 본 적이 없어요,\" 수전이 말했다, \"태어나서 단 한 번도요!\"\n\n\"무척 친절하시지,\" 플로렌스가 거들었다.\n\n\"게다가 어찌나 우스운지요!\" 수전이 흐느꼈다. \"그 무례한 치킨을 마부석에 앉혀 두고 마차 안에서 저한테 그렇게 떠들어 대시던 모습이라니!\"\n\n\"무슨 얘기를, 수전?\" 플로렌스가 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n\"오 월터스 중위님이며, 길스 선장님이며, 다정한 플로이 아가씨며, 그리고 적막한 무덤 얘기였어요,\" 수전이 말했다.\n\n\"적막한 무덤이라니!\" 플로렌스가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"그분 말씀이,\" 여기서 수전은 격렬한 히스테릭한 웃음을 터뜨렸다, \"이제 당장 거기로 내려가서 아주 편안히 누워 버리시겠다지만, 아이고 다정한 플로이 아가씨 그럴 리가 있겠어요, 그분은 남들 행복한 걸 보는 게 너무 좋아서 그럴 수가 없으세요, 솔로몬은 아닐지 몰라도,\" 니퍼가 여느 때처럼 거침없이 말을 이었다, \"그렇다고 제가 솔로몬이라고 하는 건 아니지만 이건 말할 수 있어요 그분보다 덜 이기적인 사람은 인간 본성이 일찍이 알지 못했다고요!\"\n\n니퍼 양은 여전히 히스테릭한 상태라, 이 힘찬 선언을 마치고는 한참을 끝없이 웃어 댔다. 그러고는 플로렌스에게, 그분이 아래층에서 아가씨를 뵙기를 기다리고 계시다고 알렸다. 그것이 그분이 이번 원정길에 겪은 수고에 대한 풍성한 보답이 되리라고 했다.\n\n플로렌스는 수전에게, 그분의 친절에 감사를 드릴 기쁨을 자기에게 허락해 주십사 투츠 씨에게 청해 달라고 간곡히 부탁했다. 수전은 잠시 후 그 젊은 신사를 데려왔는데, 여전히 행색이 몹시 흐트러져 있었고 말도 심하게 더듬었다.\n\n\"돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"다시금 허락을 받아—그—응시할—아니, 응시한다기보다는—제가 무슨 말을 하려던 건지 정확히 모르겠습니다만, 그건 아무래도 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 너무도 자주 감사를 드려야 하니,\" 플로렌스가 천진한 감사의 빛을 얼굴 가득 빛내며 두 손을 그에게 내밀고 대답했다, \"남은 말이 없어서, 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠 씨가 엄숙한 목소리로 말했다, \"만일 아가씨께서, 그 천사 같은 본성에 어긋나지 않게, 저를 저주하실 수 있다면, 그렇게 하시는 편이—이렇게 말씀드려도 된다면—이 분에 넘치는 친절한 말씀으로 저를 쓰러뜨리시는 것보다 한없이 덜 저를 무너뜨릴 것입니다. 그 말씀이 제게 미치는 영향은—말하자면—그런데,\" 투츠 씨가 불쑥 말했다, \"이건 곁가지로 새는 얘기이고, 아무래도 좋은 일입니다.\"\n\n이 말에 대답할 길이 다시 감사를 드리는 것밖에는 없는 듯하여, 플로렌스는 다시 한번 그에게 감사했다.\n\n\"이 기회에,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"괜찮으시다면, 돔비 아가씨, 한마디 해명을 드리고 싶습니다. 저는 좀 더 일찍 수전과 함께 돌아오는 기쁨을 누렸어야 했지요. 그런데 첫째로, 수전이 찾아간 친척 집 이름을 우리가 몰랐고, 둘째로, 수전이 그 친척 집을 떠나 멀리 떨어진 또 다른 친척에게 가 버려서, 치킨의 명민함이 아니었다면 그 짧은 시간 안에 그녀를 찾아낼 길은 거의 없었으리라 생각합니다.\"\n\n플로렌스도 분명 그러했으리라고 믿었다.\n\n\"하지만 그게 요점은 아닙니다,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"수전과 함께한 길은, 정말이지, 돔비 아가씨, 제 마음 상태에서 말로 표현하기보다 짐작하시는 편이 더 쉬울 위로이자 만족이었습니다. 그 여정 자체가 보상이었지요. 하지만 그것도 요점은 아닙니다. 돔비 아가씨, 제가 전에도 말씀드렸듯이 저는 머리 회전이 빠른 사람으로 여겨지지 않는다는 걸 압니다. 그 점은 저도 잘 알고 있습니다. 제 자신의—너무 심한 표현이 아니라면, 제 머리가 얼마나 둔한지를—저보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없으리라 생각합니다. 하지만 돔비 아가씨, 그런데도 저는 월터스 중위님과 관련된—그—사정을 알아차리고 있습니다. 그 사정이 제게 어떤 고통을 안겨 주었든(그건 아무래도 좋은 일입니다만), 저는 말씀드리지 않을 수 없습니다, 월터스 중위님은 그의—그의 이마에 내려앉은 그 축복을 받기에 합당해 보이는 분이라고요. 부디 그분이 그 축복을 오래도록 누리고, 또 그 가치를 알아보시기를, 굳이 이름을 댈 것도 없는 아주 다르고 아주 보잘것없는 어떤 사람이 그러했을 만큼이나! 하지만 그것도 요점은 아닙니다. 돔비 아가씨, 길스 선장님은 제 친구이십니다. 그래서 이제 흘러갈 그 기간 동안, 제가 이따금 이곳에 드나드는 것을 보시면 길스 선장님께서 기뻐하시리라 믿습니다. 저로서도 그렇게 드나드는 것이 기쁠 것입니다. 하지만 저는 한때 브라이턴의 광장 모퉁이에서 제 처지를 치명적으로 그르쳤던 일을 잊을 수가 없습니다. 만일 제 존재가 아가씨께 조금이라도 불쾌하다면, 부디 지금 제게 그 점을 말씀해 주십사 청할 뿐이고, 제가 아가씨의 뜻을 완벽히 이해하리라는 점을 약속드립니다. 저는 그것을 조금도 매정하다 여기지 않을 것이며, 다만 아가씨의 솔직한 마음을 영광스럽게 들려주신 것에 더없이 기뻐하고 행복해할 따름입니다.\"\n\n\"투츠 씨,\" 플로렌스가 대답했다, \"이렇게 오래되고 진실한 제 친구이신 당신이 이제 이 집에 발길을 끊으신다면, 저를 무척 불행하게 만드실 거예요. 당신을 뵙는 것은 결코, 결코 기쁨 말고는 다른 어떤 감정도 제게 주지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠 씨가 손수건을 꺼내며 말했다, \"제가 눈물을 흘린다면, 그건 기쁨의 눈물입니다. 아무래도 좋은 일이고, 정말 감사드립니다. 아가씨께서 그토록 친절히 말씀해 주셨으니, 한 말씀 드려도 된다면, 저는 더는 제 몸가짐을 소홀히 할 생각이 없습니다.\"\n\n플로렌스는 더없이 어여쁜 당혹스러운 표정으로 이 말을 받아들였다.\n\n\"제 말씀은,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"적막한 무덤이 저를 부를 때까지, 대체로 한 인간으로서의 의무로 여겨, 제 자신을 최대한 가다듬고, 또—또 제 부츠를—형편이 허락하는 한—최대한 반짝반짝 닦아 두겠다는 뜻입니다. 사적이고 개인적인 성격의 말씀을 들이미는 것은 이번이 마지막입니다, 돔비 아가씨. 정말 진심으로 감사드립니다. 제가 대체로 제 친구들이 바라는 만큼, 혹은 제 자신이 바라는 만큼 분별이 있지는 못할지라도, 정말이지, 제 명예를 걸고, 사려 깊고 친절한 것이 무엇인지는 각별히 분별할 줄 압니다. 저는,\" 투츠 씨가 격정에 찬 어조로 말했다, \"지금 이 순간 제 감정을 더없이 놀라운 방식으로 표현할 수 있을 것만 같습니다, 만일—만일—그 첫머리를 떼기만 할 수 있다면요.\"\n\n그러나 일이 분쯤 기다려도 그 첫머리가 떠오르지 않는 듯하자, 투츠 씨는 황급히 작별을 고하고 아래층으로 내려가 선장을 찾았는데, 가게에 있었다.\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"지금부터 우리 사이에 오갈 이야기는 신성한 비밀의 봉인 아래 이루어지는 것입니다. 이것은, 길스 선장님, 위층에서 저와 돔비 아가씨 사이에 오간 이야기의 후속편입니다.\"\n\n\"아래위로 다 말이지, 응, 젊은이?\" 선장이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했는데, 선장의 말뜻을 전혀 알지 못한 덕분에 동의하는 그의 열의는 한층 더 고조되었다. \"제가 알기로, 길스 선장님, 돔비 아가씨께서는 머지않아 월터스 중위님과 맺어지시는 거지요?\"\n\n\"암, 그렇지, 젊은이. 우리는 여기서 다 한배를 탄 동지일세,—월터하고 그 어여쁜 색시는 청혼 공고가 끝나는 대로 속박의 집에서 하나로 맺어질 걸세,\" 커틀 선장이 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였다.\n\n\"청혼 공고라니요, 길스 선장님!\" 투츠 씨가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"저기, 저 아래 교회에서 말일세,\" 선장이 어깨 너머로 엄지손가락을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"오! 네!\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그러고 나면,\" 선장이 쉰 목소리로 속삭이며, 손등으로 투츠 씨의 가슴을 톡톡 치고는 한없는 감탄의 눈빛으로 그에게서 물러서며 말했다, \"그 뒤엔 뭐가 이어지겠나? 그 어여쁜 색시가, 외국에서 온 새처럼 곱게 자란 그 색시가, 월터하고 함께 포효하는 망망대해로 중국 가는 항해를 떠나는 거라네!\"\n\n\"세상에, 길스 선장님!\" 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다네!\" 선장이 고개를 끄덕였다. \"월터가 항로를 완전히 벗어나게 만든 그 허리케인 속에서 난파했을 때 그를 건져 올린 배가, 중국 무역선이었거든. 그래서 월터가 그 항해에 올랐고, 배 위에서나 뭍에서나 신임을 얻었네—여태 발 디딘 그 어떤 젊은이 못지않게 영리하고 착한 녀석이니까—그러다 광둥에서 화물 관리인이 죽는 바람에 (전에 서기 노릇을 했던 터라) 그 자리에 올랐고, 이제는 같은 선주의 다른 배에서 화물 관리인이 되었다네. 그래서, 알겠나,\" 선장이 생각에 잠겨 되뇌었다, \"그 어여쁜 색시가 월터하고 함께 포효하는 망망대해로 중국 가는 항해를 떠나는 거지.\"\n\n투츠 씨와 커틀 선장은 함께 한숨을 내쉬었다. \"그럼 어떤가?\" 선장이 말했다. \"색시는 그를 진실로 사랑하지. 그도 색시를 진실로 사랑하고. 그 색시를 사랑하고 보살펴야 했을 사람들은, 그 색시를 멸망하는 짐승처럼 대했네. 집에서 쫓겨난 색시가 나한테 찾아와 저 판자 위에 쓰러졌을 때, 그 상처 입은 마음은 부서져 있었어. 나는 알아. 나, 에드워드 커틀이, 그걸 봤네. 진실하고 다정하고 한결같은 사랑만이 그 마음을 다시 이어 붙일 수 있어. 만일 내가 그걸 모르고, 또 월터가 색시의 진실한 사랑이고, 형제여, 색시가 월터의 진실한 사랑인 걸 몰랐다면, 색시를 보내느니 차라리 이 파란 두 팔다리를 잘라 버렸을 걸세. 하지만 나는 알아, 그러니 어떤가! 그러니, 내 말은, 하느님께서 두 사람과 함께하시기를, 분명 그러실 거야! 아멘!\"\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"악수하는 기쁨을 좀 누리게 해 주십시오. 선장님은 말씀하시는 방식이, 등줄기를 타고 기분 좋은 온기가 쭉 올라오게 하는 데가 있습니다. 저도 아멘 하겠습니다. 길스 선장님, 저 역시 돔비 아가씨를 흠모해 왔다는 걸 아실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"기운 내게!\" 선장이 투츠 씨의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"버텨 내게, 젊은이!\"\n\n\"제 뜻이 그렇습니다, 길스 선장님,\" 기운을 차린 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"기운을 내려고 합니다. 또 가능한 한 버텨 내려고도 합니다. 적막한 무덤이 입을 벌릴 때, 길스 선장님, 저는 묻힐 준비가 되어 있을 겁니다. 그 전에는 아니고요. 하지만 지금 당장은 제 자신을 다스릴 힘이 있다고 확신할 수 없으니, 제가 선장님께 드리고 싶은 말씀, 그리고 선장님께서 월터스 중위님께 전해 주신다면 특별한 은혜로 여길 말씀은 다음과 같습니다.\"\n\n\"다음과 같다 이거지,\" 선장이 따라 했다. \"침착하게!\"\n\n\"돔비 아가씨께서 너무도 말할 수 없이 친절하셔서,\" 투츠 씨가 눈물 어린 눈으로 말을 이었다, \"제 존재가 자기에게 불쾌하기는커녕 그 반대라고 말씀해 주셨고, 또 선장님이며 여기 계신 모든 분이, 분명—,\" 투츠 씨가 잠시 풀이 죽어 말했다, \"착오로 태어난 듯한 한 사람에게 못지않게 너그럽고 관대하시니, 저는 우리 모두가 함께 있을 수 있는 그 짧은 동안, 저녁마다 드나들겠습니다. 하지만 제가 청하는 건 이것입니다. 만일 어느 순간엔가 제가 월터스 중위님의 행복을 차마 바라볼 수 없어 밖으로 뛰쳐나가게 되더라도, 부디, 길스 선장님, 선장님도 중위님도 그것을 제 잘못이 아니라 제 불운으로, 혹은 내적인 갈등이 부족해서가 아니라고 여겨 주시기를 바랍니다. 제가 살아 있는 어떤 이에게도 악의를 품지 않는다는 걸—누구보다 월터스 중위님 자신에게는 더더욱—확신해 주시고, 또 제가 산책하러 나갔다거나, 아마 왕립 거래소에 가서 몇 시인지 보러 갔다거나 하고 무심히 말씀해 주시기를 바랍니다. 길스 선장님, 만일 선장님께서 이 약속에 응해 주시고, 또 월터스 중위님을 대신해 보증해 주실 수 있다면, 제 재산의 상당 부분을 치르고서라도 헐값으로 여길 만큼 제 마음에 위안이 될 것입니다.\"\n\n\"젊은이,\" 선장이 대답했다, \"더 말 말게. 자네가 어떤 깃발을 올려도, 월터하고 내가 알아보고 응답하지 못할 일은 없을 걸세.\"\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"제 마음이 크게 놓입니다. 저는 여기 계신 모든 분의 좋은 평판을 지키고 싶습니다. 저는—저는—제 명예를 걸고, 비록 서툴게 드러낼지라도, 진심에서 우러나옵니다. 아시다시피,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"그건 마치 버지스 상회가 어느 손님에게 더없이 비범한 바지 한 벌로 호의를 베풀고 싶었지만, 머릿속에 그린 대로 마름질해 내지 못한 것과 꼭 같지요.\"\n\n이 적절한 비유를 든 것을 조금 자랑스러워하는 듯한 표정으로, 투츠 씨는 커틀 선장에게 축복을 빌고 떠나갔다.\n\n정직한 선장은, 자기 마음의 기쁨인 그 아가씨를 집에 두고 수전이 그녀를 보살피는 가운데, 환하게 빛나는 행복한 사람이었다. 날이 흘러갈수록 그는 날마다 더 환해지고 더 행복해졌다. 수전과 몇 차례 의논을 한 끝에(선장은 수전의 지혜를 깊이 존경했고, 또 그녀가 맥스팅거 부인에게 용맹스럽게 자기 몸을 내던졌던 일을 결코 잊을 수 없었다), 그는 플로렌스에게, 리든홀 시장에서 평소 파란 우산 아래 앉아 있던 그 노부인의 딸을, 신중함을 기하고 사생활을 고려하는 차원에서, 그들이 모르지 않고 또 안심하고 믿을 수 있는 누군가로 갈음하여 집안일을 임시로 맡기자고 제안했다. 그 자리에 있던 수전은, 전에 선장에게 미리 내놓았던 제안을 밀어붙여, 리처즈 부인의 이름을 댔다. 플로렌스는 그 이름에 얼굴이 환해졌다. 그리고 수전은 바로 그날 오후 리처즈 부인의 의향을 떠보러 투들 댁으로 떠났다가, 바로 그날 저녁 의기양양하게 돌아왔는데, 그 곁에는 바로 그 발그레한 뺨에 사과 같은 얼굴을 한 폴리가 함께 있었다. 폴리가 플로렌스 앞에 나타났을 때 보인 애정 어린 모습은, 수전 니퍼 자신의 것에 못지않을 정도였다.\n\n이 작전이 성취되자—선장은 여기서, 사실 무엇이 됐든 무슨 일이 이루어질 때마다 그러했듯이, 남다른 만족을 느꼈다—플로렌스는 다음으로 다가오는 이별을 위해 수전의 마음을 준비시켜야 했다. 이것은 훨씬 더 어려운 일이었으니, 니퍼 양은 결연한 성격이라, 옛 여주인과 두 번 다시 떨어지지 않으려고 돌아왔노라 단단히 마음먹고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"품삯 얘기라면요 다정한 플로이 아가씨,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그런 걸 입에 올릴 생각을 하셔서 저를 넌지시 떠보거나 서운하게 하지는 않으실 거예요, 전 돈을 모아 두었고 이런 때에 제 사랑과 의무를 팔지는 않을 테니까요 설령 저축은행하고 제가 생판 남남이라 해도, 아니 그 은행들이 산산조각이 났다 해도요, 그래도 아가씨께서는 가여운 어머님이 떠나가신 그때부터 한 번도 제 곁을 떠나신 적이 없으셨고, 또 제가 자랑할 만한 사람은 못 되어도 아가씨는 저한테 익숙하시잖아요 오 그 오랜 세월 동안 함께한 제 소중한 여주인님 저를 두고 어디로 가실 생각일랑 마세요, 그건 안 되고 또 그럴 수도 없으니까요!\"\n\n\"다정한 수전, 나는 멀고 먼 항해를 떠나는 거야.\"\n\n\"그래서요 플로이 아가씨, 그게 어쨌다는 거예요? 그러면 더더욱 제가 필요하시겠죠. 항해가 멀고 가깝고는 제 눈엔 문제도 아니에요, 천만다행이지요!\" 성마른 수전 니퍼가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만, 수전, 나는 월터와 함께 가는 거야, 그리고 나는 월터와 함께라면 어디든—어디든 갈 거야! 월터는 가난하고, 나는 무척 가난해, 그래서 나는 이제 스스로를 건사하는 법도, 그이를 돕는 법도 배워야 해.\"\n\n\"다정한 플로이 아가씨!\" 수전이 다시금 울음을 터뜨리고 격렬하게 고개를 흔들며 외쳤다, \"아가씨가 스스로를 건사하고 또 남들까지 돕는 거며, 더없이 인내심 많고 더없이 진실한 고귀한 마음을 지니신 거야 새삼스러운 일이 아니지만, 하지만 월터 게이 씨한테 제가 얘기해서 그분하고 매듭을 짓게 해 주세요, 아가씨를 혼자 세상 저 너머로 떠나보내는 건 저는 못해요, 절대 안 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"혼자라니, 수전?\" 플로렌스가 대답했다. \"혼자? 월터가 나를 데려가는데!\" 아, 그 얼굴에 어린 그 환하고 놀랍고 황홀한 미소라니!—그가 그것을 봤어야 했는데. \"내가 부탁하지 않으면 분명 너는 월터한테 말하지 않을 거지,\" 그녀가 다정하게 덧붙였다. \"그러니 제발 그러지 마, 응.\"\n\n수전이 흐느꼈다. \"왜 안 되나요, 플로이 아가씨?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"나는 그이의 아내가 되어, 내 온 마음을 그이에게 바치고, 그이와 함께 살고 그이와 함께 죽으려는 거니까. 만일 네가 나한테 한 말을 그이한테 한다면, 그이는 내가 앞날을 두려워한다거나, 너에게 나를 두고 두려워할 무슨 까닭이 있다고 생각할지도 몰라. 아니, 수전, 다정한 수전, 나는 그이를 사랑하는걸!\"\n\n니퍼 양은 이 말들의 잔잔한 열정과, 거기에 담긴 소박하고 진심 어린, 온몸에 스며든 진지함—말하는 이의 얼굴을 그 어느 때보다 아름답고 순결하게 만든 그 진지함—에 너무도 감동한 나머지, 그저 다시 그녀에게 매달려 울 수밖에 없었다. 어린 여주인이 정말로, 정말로 결혼을 하시는구나, 하면서, 예전에 그랬듯이 그녀를 가엾어하고 어루만지고 지켜 주려 했다.\n\n그러나 니퍼는, 비록 여성스러운 약한 면에 휘둘리기 쉬웠으되, 그 무시무시한 맥스팅거에게 맞서는 일만큼이나 자기 자신을 다잡는 일에도 거의 능했다. 그때부터 그녀는 다시는 그 주제로 돌아가지 않았고, 늘 명랑하고 활기차고 부산하고 희망에 차 있었다. 다만 투츠 씨에게는 은밀히, 자기는 그저 그동안 버티고 있는 것뿐이며, 모든 일이 끝나고 돔비 아가씨가 떠나고 나면 보기에 안쓰러운 꼴이 되리라 예상해도 좋다고 일러 주기는 했다. 그러자 투츠 씨도 자기 처지 역시 그렇다고, 그러니 둘이서 함께 눈물을 섞으면 되겠다고 했다. 하지만 그녀는 그 밖에는 플로렌스 앞에서나 후보생관의 울타리 안에서 결코 자기 속내를 드러내지 않았다.\n\n플로렌스의 혼수가 비록 단출하고 수수했을지언정—그녀가 참여했던 지난번 결혼식을 위해 마련된 것과는 얼마나 대조적이던가!—그것을 갖추는 데에는 할 일이 꽤 많았고, 수전 니퍼는 하루 종일 그녀 곁에서 바느질꾼 쉰 명 몫의 열의를 모아 일했다. 만일 커틀 선장이 이 혼수 마련에 허락만 받았다면 보탰을 그 놀라운 기여 품목들이란—분홍색 양산이며, 색색의 비단 양말이며, 파란 신발이며, 그 밖에 배 위에서도 못지않게 필요할 갖가지 물건들이며—다 늘어놓자면 적잖은 지면을 차지할 것이다. 그러나 그는 여러 가지 그럴싸한 핑계에 넘어가, 자기 기여를 반짇고리와 화장 상자로 한정하게 되었는데, 그 둘 모두 돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 큰 것으로 구했다. 그 후 열흘이나 보름 동안, 그는 대체로 하루의 대부분을 이 상자들을 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 그 상자들에 대한 극도의 감탄과, 그것들이 충분히 화려하지 못하다는 풀죽은 의구심 사이에서 갈팡질팡했고, 그것들을 완벽하게 하는 데 꼭 필요하다고 여긴 어떤 엉뚱한 물건을 사러 거리로 뛰쳐나가기 일쑤였다. 그러나 그의 회심의 일격은, 어느 날 아침 그 두 상자를 갑작스레 들고 나가, 각 뚜껑 위에 박아 넣은 놋쇠 하트에 플로렌스 게이라는 두 단어를 새겨 넣은 것이었다. 그 후 그는 작은 응접실에서 혼자 연달아 파이프 네 대를 피웠고, 그만한 시간이 흐른 뒤에 혼자 낄낄거리고 있는 것이 발견되었다.\n\n월터는 하루 종일 바빠 밖에 나가 있었으나, 아침마다 일찍 와서 플로렌스를 보았고 늘 저녁을 그녀와 함께 보냈다. 플로렌스는 자기 높은 방을 떠나는 일이 없었는데, 다만 그가 올 시각이 되면 살그머니 아래층으로 내려가 그를 기다리거나, 그의 자랑스럽게 감싸 안은 팔에 보호받으며 다시 문간까지 그를 배웅하고, 이따금 거리를 살짝 내다보러 갈 때만은 예외였다. 황혼 녘이면 그들은 늘 함께였다. 오 복된 시간이여! 오 마침내 안식을 찾은 떠돌던 마음이여! 오 그토록 많은 것이 잠겨 든, 깊고 마르지 않는 거대한 사랑의 샘이여!\n\n그 잔인한 자국은 아직도 그녀의 가슴에 남아 있었다. 그것은 그녀가 들이쉬는 숨결과 더불어 그녀의 아버지를 거슬러 일어섰고, 그가 그녀를 가슴에 끌어안을 때면 그녀와 연인 사이에 가로놓였다. 그러나 그녀는 그것을 잊었다. 그녀를 향해 뛰는 그 가슴 속에서, 또 그를 향해 뛰는 자기 가슴 속에서, 더 거친 모든 가락은 들리지 않았고, 차갑고 사랑 없는 모든 마음은 잊혔다. 그녀는 가냘프고 연약했으되, 그 안에는 그의 한결같은 모습 하나로부터 날아가 깃들 세계를 빚어낼 수 있고 또 실제로 빚어낸, 그런 사랑의 힘이 있었다.\n\n그 황혼 녘이면, 그토록 자랑스럽고 그토록 다정한 그 팔에 감싸여, 그에게 더 바싹 다가가 그 안에서 움츠러들 때면, 그 큰 저택과 옛 시절이 추억 속에서 그녀 앞에 떠오르는 일이 얼마나 잦았던가! 그녀가 그 방으로 내려갔다가 결코 잊을 수 없는 그 눈빛을 마주쳤던 그 밤을 떠올릴 때면, 그토록 사랑스러운 진지함으로 자기를 지켜보는 그 눈을 향해 눈을 들어 올리고, 그런 안식처에서 행복에 겨워 우는 일이 얼마나 잦았던가! 그녀가 그곳에 매달리면 매달릴수록, 세상을 떠난 그 소중한 아이가 그녀의 마음속에 더 많이 떠올랐다. 다만, 마치 아버지를 마지막으로 본 것이 그가 잠들어 있고 자기가 그 얼굴에 입 맞췄을 때이기라도 한 듯, 그녀는 늘 그를 그 모습 그대로 떠나보냈고, 공상 속에서 결코 그 시각을 넘어가지 않았다.\n\n\"월터, 여보,\" 어느 날 저녁, 거의 어두워졌을 무렵 플로렌스가 말했다. \"오늘 내가 무슨 생각을 했는지 알아요?\"\n\n\"시간이 얼마나 빨리 흘러가는지, 또 우리가 얼마나 곧 바다 위에 떠 있을지 생각했겠지요, 사랑스러운 플로렌스?\"\n\n\"그건 아니에요, 월터, 그 생각도 하긴 했지만요. 내가 당신한테 얼마나 짐이 되는지 생각했어요.\"\n\n\"소중하고 거룩한 짐이지요, 사랑하는 사람! 그야, 나도 가끔 그런 생각을 해요.\"\n\n\"당신은 웃고 있군요, 월터. 그게 나보다 당신 마음에 훨씬 더 자주 떠오르는 거 알아요. 하지만 내 말은 비용을 두고 하는 말이에요.\"\n\n\"비용이라니, 내 사람?\"\n\n\"돈 말이에요, 여보. 수전하고 내가 이렇게 바쁘게 매달려 준비하는 이 모든 것들 말이에요—나는 나를 위해서는 거의 아무것도 살 수가 없었어요. 당신은 전에도 가난했지요. 그런데 내가 당신을 얼마나 더 가난하게 만들게 될까요, 월터!\"\n\n\"그리고 얼마나 더 부유하게도 만들지요, 플로렌스!\"\n\n플로렌스는 웃으며 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 월터가 말했다, \"오래전에—내가 바다로 나가기 전에—작은 지갑을 하나 선물받은 적이 있어요, 여보, 그 안에 돈이 들어 있었지요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 플로렌스가 서글프게 웃으며 대답했다, \"아주 조금이었죠! 아주 조금요, 월터! 하지만, 당신은 생각해선 안 돼요,\" 그러면서 그녀는 가벼운 손을 그의 어깨에 얹고 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다, \"내가 당신에게 이렇게 짐이 되는 걸 후회한다고는요. 아니에요, 사랑하는 이여, 나는 그게 기뻐요. 그게 행복해요. 온 세상을 다 준대도 다른 식으로는 싫어요!\"\n\n\"나도 정말 그래요, 다정한 플로렌스.\"\n\n\"네! 하지만, 월터, 당신은 결코 나만큼 그것을 느낄 수는 없어요. 나는 당신이 너무 자랑스러워요! 당신을 이야기하는 사람들이, 당신은 이곳에 몸을 의탁했던 가난하고 버림받은 처녀와, 다른 집도, 다른 친구도 없는, 아무것도—아무것도 없던 그런 처녀와 결혼했다고 말할 수밖에 없으리라는 걸 아는 것만으로도 내 마음은 그토록 큰 기쁨으로 부풀어 올라요! 오, 월터, 내가 당신에게 수백만금을 가져다줄 수 있었다 한들, 당신을 위해 지금만큼 행복할 수는 없었을 거예요!\"\n\n\"그리고 당신은요, 다정한 플로렌스? 당신은 아무것도 아닌가요?\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"네, 아무것도 아니에요, 월터. 당신의 아내라는 것 말고는 아무것도요.\" 가벼운 손이 그의 목을 살며시 감았고, 목소리는 더 가까이—더 가까이 다가왔다. \"나는 이제, 당신이 아닌 것은 더 이상 아무것도 아니에요. 나는 이제, 당신이 아닌 어떤 세속의 희망도 없어요. 나는 이제, 당신이 아닌 어떤 소중한 것도 없어요.\"\n\n오! 그러니 투츠 씨가 그날 저녁 그 작은 모임을 떠나, 왕립 거래소에 가서 자기 시계를 맞춘다고 두 번이나 나가고, 또 문득 떠오른 은행가와의 약속을 지키러 한 번 나가고, 또 올드게이트 펌프까지 잠깐 한 바퀴 돌고 오느라 한 번 나간 것도 당연한 일이었다!\n\n그러나 그가 이런 원정길에 나서기 전에, 아니 사실 그가 오기 전, 또 불이 들어오기 전에, 월터가 말했다.\n\n\"플로렌스, 여보, 우리 배의 짐 싣는 일이 거의 끝나가고, 아마 바로 우리 결혼식 날에 배가 강을 따라 내려갈 거예요. 그날 아침 떠나서, 일주일 안에 그레이브젠드에서 배에 오를 때까지 켄트에 머물면 어떨까요?\"\n\n\"당신 좋으실 대로요, 월터. 나는 어디서든 행복할 거예요. 하지만—\"\n\n\"네, 내 생명?\"\n\n\"알다시피,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"우리는 결혼식 잔치도 없을 테고, 아무도 우리 옷차림으로 우리를 다른 사람들과 구별하지 못할 거예요. 우리가 같은 날 떠나니까, 그날 아침—일찍—교회에 가기 전에, 나를—나를 어딘가로 데려다줄래요, 월터?\"\n\n월터는, 그토록 진실한 연인이 그토록 진실로 사랑받는 처지라면 마땅히 그러하듯, 그녀의 뜻을 알아차린 듯했고, 입맞춤으로 선뜻 그러마 하고 약속을 다졌다—아마 한 번이 아니라 두 번, 세 번, 혹은 다섯 번, 여섯 번이었으리라. 그리고 그 그윽하고 평온한 저녁에, 플로렌스는 무척 행복했다.\n\n이윽고 그 고요한 방으로 수전 니퍼가 촛불을 들고 들어왔다. 곧이어 차와, 선장과, 들락날락하던 투츠 씨가 들어왔는데, 그는 앞서 말한 대로 그 후에도 자주 자리를 떴고 안절부절못한 저녁을 보냈다. 그러나 이것이 그의 평소 모습은 아니었다. 그는 대개 니퍼 양의 조언과 지도를 받으며 선장과 크리비지 놀이를 하는 데에 힘입어 아주 잘 지냈고, 그 놀이에 딸린 셈으로 마음을 다른 데로 돌렸으니, 그것이 자기 자신을 완전히 헷갈리게 만드는 데 아주 효과적인 방편임을 알게 되었던 것이다.\n\n이런 자리에서 선장의 얼굴은, 일찍이 관찰된 표정의 조합과 연속의 가장 빼어난 본보기 가운데 하나를 보여 주었다. 그가 본능적으로 지닌 섬세함과 플로렌스를 향한 기사도적인 마음씨는, 지금이 떠들썩한 흥겨움이나 격렬한 만족의 표출을 드러낼 때가 아니라고 그에게 일러 주었다. 한편 '어여쁜 페그'의 떠도는 추억은 끊임없이 새어 나올 출구를 찾아 다투며, 어떤 돌이킬 수 없는 표출로 선장이 그만 자기 처지를 그르치도록 부추겼다. 그러다가, 따로 떨어져 앉은 플로렌스와 월터를 향한—그 젊음과 사랑과 준수함이 참으로 잘 어울리고 우아함과 흥미로 가득한 두 사람을 향한—감탄이 어찌나 그를 온통 사로잡던지, 그는 카드를 내려놓고 손수건으로 머리를 온통 톡톡 두드리며 두 사람을 향해 환히 빛나곤 했다. 그러다 어쩌면 투츠 씨가 갑자기 밖으로 뛰쳐나가는 것을 보고, 자기가 무심결에 정말이지 그 신사를 비참하게 만드는 데 단단히 한몫했음을 깨닫게 되곤 했다. 이 깨달음은 투츠 씨가 돌아올 때까지 선장을 깊은 우울에 빠뜨렸다. 그가 돌아오면 선장은 다시 카드를 잡으며, 니퍼 양에게 곁눈질과 끄덕임을 여러 번 보내고 갈고리를 정중히 흔들어 보였는데, 그건 더는 그러지 않겠다는 뜻이었다. 그 뒤에 이어지는 상태야말로 아마 그의 최고의 모습이었으리라. 그때 그는 얼굴에서 온갖 표정을 다 지워 버리려 애쓰면서, 방 안을 둘러보며 앉아 있었는데, 그 모든 표정이 한꺼번에 얼굴에 실려 서로 뒤엉켜 다투었다. 플로렌스와 월터를 향한 기쁨에 찬 감탄은 늘 나머지를 다 눌러 이기고, 투츠 씨가 다시 한번 바깥으로 뛰쳐나가지 않는 한, 승자로 남아 거리낌 없이 드러났다. 투츠 씨가 뛰쳐나가면 선장은 죄를 뉘우치는 죄인처럼 그가 돌아올 때까지 앉아 있었고, 이따금 낮고 책망하는 목소리로 자기 자신에게 \"버텨 내라!\"고 명하거나, \"에드워드 커틀, 이 사람아\"에게 그 처신에서 보이는 조심성 부족을 두고 무어라 으르렁대며 타이르곤 했다.\n\n그러나 투츠 씨에게 가장 혹독한 시련 가운데 하나는 그가 스스로 자초한 것이었다. 선장이 말했던 그 교회에서의 청혼 공고 가운데 마지막 것을 듣게 될 일요일이 다가오자, 투츠 씨는 수전 니퍼에게 자기 심정을 이렇게 털어놓았다.\n\n\"수전,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"나는 그 건물 쪽으로 끌려가는 것 같습니다. 나를 돔비 아가씨에게서 영원히 갈라놓을 그 말들이, 알다시피, 조종(弔鐘)처럼 내 귀를 때리겠지만, 정말이지 내 명예를 걸고, 나는 그 말들을 꼭 들어야만 할 것 같습니다. 그러니,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"내일 나와 함께 그 성스러운 건물에 가 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n니퍼 양은, 그것이 투츠 씨에게 조금이라도 위안이 된다면 기꺼이 그러겠노라고 밝혔으나, 가겠다는 생각일랑 버리시라고 간청했다.\n\n\"수전,\" 투츠 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 대답했다, \"내 구레나룻이 나 말고 다른 누구의 눈에 띄기 시작하기도 전부터, 나는 돔비 아가씨를 흠모했습니다. 아직 블림버의 속박에 매인 희생양이던 시절에도, 나는 돔비 아가씨를 흠모했습니다. 법적인 관점에서 더는 내 재산에서 떨어져 있을 수 없게 되어—그래서—그래서 그것을 손에 넣게 되었을 때에도, 나는 돔비 아가씨를 흠모했습니다. 그녀를 월터스 중위님에게 맡기고, 나를—나를 암흑에 넘기는, 알다시피, 그 결혼 공고는,\" 투츠 씨가 강한 표현을 찾느라 머뭇거린 뒤 말했다, \"끔찍할 수도 있고, 끔찍할 것입니다만, 나는 그 말이 선포되는 것을 꼭 듣고 싶은 심정입니다. 내 발밑의 땅이 확실히 잘려 나갔다는 것, 그리고 내게 품을 희망도, 그—혹은 그—한마디로, 딛고—딛고 설 다리 하나조차 남지 않았다는 것을 꼭 알고 싶은 심정입니다.\"\n\n수전 니퍼는 그저 투츠 씨의 가련한 처지를 가엾이 여기고, 이런 형편이니 그와 함께 가겠노라 응할 수밖에 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 이튿날 아침 그렇게 했다.\n\n월터가 그 목적으로 골라 둔 교회는, 어느 안뜰에 자리한 곰팡내 나는 낡은 교회로, 뒷골목과 안마당이 미로처럼 둘러싸고 있었고, 둘레에는 작은 묘지가 딸려 있었으며, 그 교회 자체는 이웃집들이 이룬 일종의 지하 납골당 같은 곳에 파묻혀, 메아리치는 돌바닥이 깔려 있었다. 그것은 크고 어둑하고 초라한 더미 같은 건물로, 높다란 낡은 떡갈나무 신도석들이 있었는데, 일요일마다 스무 명 남짓한 사람들이 그 사이에서 길을 잃었다. 한편 목사의 목소리는 그 텅 빈 공간을 졸린 듯 울려 퍼졌고, 오르간은 마치 바람과 습기를 막아 줄 회중이 없어 교회가 배앓이라도 하는 양 우르릉거리고 굴러 댔다. 그러나 이 도시 교회는 다른 교회들과 함께하지 못해 시름에 잠긴 처지와는 거리가 멀어서, 강 위에 배의 돛대들이 빽빽이 모여 있듯 첨탑들이 그 둘레에 옹기종기 모여 있었다. 그 첨탑 꼭대기에서 세어 보려 해도, 너무 많아 헤아리기 어려웠을 것이다. 근처의 거의 모든 안뜰과 막다른 곳마다 교회가 하나씩 있었다. 일요일 아침 수전과 투츠 씨가 그 교회로 향했을 때, 어지러이 뒤섞인 종소리는 귀가 먹먹할 지경이었다. 가까이 붙은 스무 개의 교회가 사람들에게 들어오라고 아우성치고 있었다.\n\n문제의 그 길 잃은 두 마리 양은 교회지기에 의해 널찍한 신도석에 갇혔고, 일찍 온 터라 한동안 앉아서 회중을 세어 보거나, 탑 높은 곳에서 실망에 찬 종소리를 듣거나, 칸막이 뒤편 현관에서 어느 초라한 작은 노인이 한 발을 등자에 걸친 채 그 종을 울리는—마치 '울새 누가 죽였나' 노래 속 황소처럼—모습을 바라보았다. 투츠 씨는 독경대 위의 큰 책들을 한참 살펴보고는, 결혼 공고를 어디에 보관하는지 궁금하다고 니퍼 양에게 속삭였으나, 그 젊은 숙녀는 그저 고개를 가로젓고 눈살을 찌푸리며, 당분간 세속적인 종류의 모든 접근을 물리쳤다.\n* 울새 누가 죽였나: 영국의 전래 동요. 노래에 종을 울리는 황소가 나온다.\n\n그러나 투츠 씨는 결혼 공고에서 도무지 마음을 떼지 못하는 듯, 예배의 도입부 내내 줄곧 그것을 살피고 있는 게 분명했다. 공고를 읽을 시간이 다가오자, 그 가련한 젊은 신사는 큰 불안과 떨림을 드러냈는데, 회랑 맨 앞줄에 선장이 뜻밖에 나타난 일도 그 불안을 덜어 주지 못했다. 서기가 목사에게 명단을 올렸을 때, 그때 앉아 있던 투츠 씨는 신도석 좌석을 꽉 붙들었다. 그러나 월터 게이와 플로렌스 돔비의 이름이, 그 결합의 세 번째이자 마지막 단계에 이르렀다고 큰 소리로 낭독되자, 그는 감정에 완전히 압도된 나머지 모자도 없이 교회 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔고, 그 뒤를 교회지기와 신도석 안내인, 그리고 마침 그 자리에 있던 두 명의 의사 신사가 뒤따랐다. 그중 먼저 말한 교회지기는 곧 그 물건(모자)을 가지러 돌아와, 니퍼 양에게 그 신사 일로 불안해하지 마시라고, 그 신사가 자기 불편은 아무래도 좋다고 말하더라고 속삭여 일러 주었다.\n\n니퍼 양은, 매주 그 높은 등받이 신도석들 사이에서 길을 잃는 유럽의 그 한 부분을 이루는 사람들의 눈이 자기에게 쏠려 있음을 느꼈으니, 일이 여기서 끝났다 해도 이 사건만으로 충분히 난처했을 것이다. 더구나 회랑 맨 앞줄의 선장은 자기가 이 일과 무슨 알 수 없는 연관이 있음을 회중에게 드러내지 않을 도리가 거의 없을 만큼 노골적인 의식 상태에 있었으니 더욱 그러했다. 그러나 투츠 씨의 극심한 안절부절못함이 그녀의 처지의 미묘함을 고통스럽게 키우고 또 질질 끌었다. 그 젊은 신사는, 그런 심정으로는 묘지에 홀로 남아 적막한 명상에 잠겨 있을 수도 없었거니와, 또 자기가 어느 정도 방해한 그 예식에 대한 존경을 분명 표하고 싶기도 했던지, 갑자기 돌아왔다—다만 신도석으로 되돌아온 것이 아니라, 현관 선반에 놓아둔 매주 베푸는 빵 한 몫을 받아 가는 데 익숙한 두 노부인 사이의, 통로에 있는 빈 좌석에 자리를 잡았다. 이렇게 결합한 채로 투츠 씨는 머물러 있었고, 회중을 크게 어지럽혔으니, 그들로서는 그를 쳐다보지 않을 도리가 없었던 것이다. 그러다 감정이 다시 그를 짓누르자, 그는 소리 없이 갑작스레 떠나갔다. 더는 교회 안에서 자기 자신을 믿고 맡길 엄두를 못 내면서도, 그래도 그 안에서 벌어지는 일에 어떤 식으로든 함께하고 싶었던 투츠 씨는, 그 후 이따금 처량한 얼굴로 이 창문 저 창문을 들여다보는 모습으로 눈에 띄었다. 그가 밖에서 접근할 수 있는 창문이 여럿이었고 또 그의 안절부절못함이 워낙 컸던 탓에, 다음번에 그가 어느 창문에 나타날지 짐작하기 어려웠을 뿐 아니라, 설교가 베풀어 준 비교적 한가한 시간 동안 회중 전체가 이를테면 서로 다른 창문들의 확률을 점쳐 봐야 할 지경이 되었다. 투츠 씨가 묘지에서 움직이는 모양이 어찌나 별난지, 그는 대개 온갖 예측을 다 어그러뜨리는 듯했고, 마술사의 인형처럼 가장 예상치 못한 곳에 나타났다. 그리고 이 알 수 없는 출몰의 효과는, 그가 안을 들여다보기는 어렵고 다른 모든 사람이 밖을 내다보기는 쉽다는 사정 때문에 한층 더 커졌다. 그런 까닭에 그는 번번이, 모든 눈이 자기에게 쏠려 있음을 문득 알아차리고 사라질 때까지, 얼굴을 유리에 바싹 대고 예상보다 더 오래 머물러 있곤 했다.\n\n투츠 씨 쪽의 이런 행동거지와, 또 선장이 드러낸 그것에 대한 강한 개인적 의식 탓에, 니퍼 양의 처지는 그토록 책임이 막중해져서, 그녀는 예배가 끝난 것에 크게 안도했다. 돌아오는 길에 투츠 씨가 그녀와 선장에게, 이제 자기에게 희망이 없다는 걸 확실히 알고 나니, 알다시피, 한결 편안하다고—적어도 정확히 더 편안하다기보다는, 더 편안하고도 온전하게 비참하다고—일러 주었을 때, 그녀는 평소만큼 그에게 살갑지 못했다.\n\n이제 정말로 시간은 쏜살같이 흘러, 결혼식으로 정해진 날의 전날 저녁이 되었다. 그들은 모두 후보생관의 위층 방에 모여 있었고, 방해받을 걱정은 없었으니, 이제 그 집에는 세 든 사람이 하나도 없어 후보생이 그 집을 통째로 차지하고 있었던 것이다. 그들은 내일을 앞두고 진지하고 차분했으나, 그래도 어지간히 명랑하기도 했다. 플로렌스는, 월터를 바로 곁에 둔 채, 선장에게 줄 작별 선물로 마련한 작은 바느질감을 마무리하고 있었다. 선장은 투츠 씨와 크리비지를 하고 있었다. 투츠 씨는 수전 니퍼에게 자기 패를 어떻게 낼지 조언을 구하고 있었다. 니퍼 양은 마땅한 비밀과 신중함을 다해 그것을 일러 주고 있었다. 다이오지니즈는 귀를 기울이다가, 이따금 거친 데다 반쯤 짓눌린 짧은 짖음을 터뜨렸는데, 그러고 나서는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었나 스스로 의심하는 듯, 반쯤 부끄러워하는 기색이었다.\n\n\"가만, 가만!\" 선장이 다이오지니즈에게 말했다, \"너 왜 그러느냐? 오늘 밤은 마음이 영 편치 않은 모양이구나, 이 녀석!\"\n\n다이오지니즈는 꼬리를 흔들었으나, 곧바로 귀를 쫑긋 세우더니 또 한 차례 짧게 짖었다. 그러고는 다시 꼬리를 흔들어 선장에게 사과했다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 말이다, 다이,\" 선장이 카드를 생각에 잠겨 들여다보고 갈고리로 턱을 쓰다듬으며 말했다, \"네가 리처즈 부인을 의심하는 게로구나. 하지만 네가 내가 생각하는 그런 짐승이라면, 그 생각을 고쳐먹는 게 좋을 게다. 그 부인의 인상이야말로 그 부인의 보증서니까. 자, 형제여,\" 투츠 씨에게 하는 말이었다, \"준비됐거든, 어서 패를 던지게.\"\n\n선장은 더없이 침착하게, 또 놀이에 주의를 기울이며 말했다. 그런데 별안간 그의 손에서 카드가 떨어지고, 입과 눈이 휘둥그레지고, 두 다리가 끌어올려졌다가 의자 앞으로 쭉 뻗어 나오더니, 그는 멍하니 어리둥절한 채 문을 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 둘러앉은 사람들을 돌아보니 아무도 자기를, 또 자기가 놀란 까닭을 알아채지 못한 것을 보고, 선장은 크게 헐떡이며 정신을 차리고는 탁자를 엄청나게 내리치면서 우렁찬 고함으로 \"솔 길스 어이!\" 하고 외쳤고, 폴리와 함께 방으로 들어온 비바람에 닳은 더플코트의 품으로 와락 무너져 들었다.\n\n다음 순간, 월터가 그 비바람에 닳은 더플코트의 품에 안겨 있었다. 또 다음 순간, 플로렌스가 그 비바람에 닳은 더플코트의 품에 안겨 있었다. 또 다음 순간, 커틀 선장은 리처즈 부인과 니퍼 양을 끌어안고는, 투츠 씨와 격렬하게 악수를 하며, 갈고리를 머리 위로 흔들면서 외쳤다, \"만세, 젊은이, 만세!\" 이에 투츠 씨는 이 일들을 도무지 어찌 된 영문인지 알 길이 없었던지라, 더없이 공손하게 대답했다, \"여부가 있겠습니까, 길스 선장님, 선장님 보시기에 옳은 대로 무엇이든지요!\"\n\n그 비바람에 닳은 더플코트와, 거기 딸린 못지않게 비바람에 닳은 모자와 목도리는, 선장과 플로렌스에게서 돌아서 다시 월터에게로 향했고, 그 비바람에 닳은 더플코트와 모자와 목도리 아래에서 마치 한 노인이 흐느끼는 듯한 소리가 새어 나왔다. 그러는 동안 그 텁수룩한 소맷자락이 월터를 꼭 끌어안았다. 이 잠깐 동안 사방이 온통 고요했고, 선장은 부지런히 코를 문질러 닦았다. 그러나 그 더플코트와 모자와 목도리가 다시 몸을 일으키자, 플로렌스가 살며시 그쪽으로 다가갔다. 그녀와 월터가 그것들을 벗겨 내자, 그 늙은 기계 제작자가 모습을 드러냈는데, 예전보다 조금 야위고 더 근심에 찌든 채, 그 낡은 웨일스 가발에 낡은 커피색 외투에 바구니 모양 단추를 단 차림이었고, 주머니에서는 그 낡았으나 틀림없는 정밀시계가 째깍거리고 있었다.\n\n\"여전히 학식이 가득하시군요,\" 환히 빛나는 선장이 말했다, \"예나 다름없이! 솔 길스, 솔 길스, 이 오랜 세월 동안 대체 뭘 하고 다니셨소, 내 늙은 친구?\"\n\n\"눈이 반쯤 멀었네, 네드,\" 노인이 말했다, \"그리고 기뻐서 거의 귀먹고 말도 못 할 지경일세.\"\n\n\"바로 그 목소리야,\" 선장이, 자기 얼굴조차 미처 다 담아내지 못할 환희에 차서 둘러보며 말했다—\"학식이 예나 다름없이 가득한 바로 그 목소리야! 솔 길스, 닻을 내리게나, 이 사람아, 자네야말로 꼿꼿한 늙은 족장이니 자네 포도나무와 무화과나무 아래에 자리를 잡고, 그 자네 모험담을 자네 그 친근한 목소리로 한번 죽 풀어 보게나. '이는 게으름뱅이의 목소리로다,'\" 선장이 자못 인상 깊게, 갈고리로 인용문임을 알리며 말했다, \"'그가 푸념하는 소리를 내 들었노라, 너희가 나를 너무 일찍 깨웠으니, 내 다시 졸아야겠도다. 그의 원수들을 흩으시고, 그들을 쓰러지게 하소서!'\"\n* 게으름뱅이의 목소리: 아이작 와츠의 시 「게으름뱅이」와 시편 구절을 뒤섞은 인용. 선장은 늘 성구를 엉뚱하게 짜깁기한다.\n\n선장은 그 자리에 있는 모두의 심정을 흐뭇하게 대변하기라도 한 사람 같은 태도로 자리에 앉았다가, 곧바로 다시 일어나 투츠 씨를 소개하려 했다. 투츠 씨는 누군가 길스라는 이름의 권리를 주장하는 듯한 사람이 도착한 일로 적잖이 당혹스러워했다.\n\n\"비록,\" 투츠 씨가 더듬거렸다, \"선생님과 알고 지내는 기쁨은 누리지 못했습니다만, 선생님께서—선생님께서—\"\n\n\"보이지 않는 곳으로 사라졌으나 기억 속에 소중히 남으셨던 동안에 말이지요,\" 선장이 낮은 목소리로 거들었다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 길스 선장님!\" 투츠 씨가 동의했다. \"비록 그 일이 있기 전에 선생님과—솔스 씨와,\" 투츠 씨가 번뜩이는 착상에 힘입어 그 이름을 짚어 내며 말했다, \"알고 지내는 기쁨은 누리지 못했습니다만, 정말이지 저는—그러니까—선생님을 알게 되어 더없이 기쁩니다. 부디,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"기대할 수 있는 만큼은 건강하시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n이 정중한 말과 함께, 투츠 씨는 얼굴을 붉히고 낄낄거리며 자리에 앉았다. 늙은 기계 제작자는, 월터와 플로렌스 사이의 한쪽 구석에 앉아, 온통 미소와 기쁨에 차 지켜보고 있는 폴리를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며, 선장에게 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"네드 커틀, 내 소중한 친구, 비록 여기서 일어난 사건들의 변화에 관해서는 저기 저 유쾌한 내 친구한테서 조금 듣긴 했네만—정말이지 떠돌이가 집에 돌아온 걸 반겨 주는 얼굴치고 어쩜 저리 유쾌한 얼굴이람!\" 노인이 말을 끊고는, 예의 그 꿈꾸는 듯한 버릇대로 두 손을 비비며 말했다.\n\n\"저 말 좀 듣게나!\" 선장이 자못 엄숙하게 외쳤다. \"온 인류를 호리는 것은 여자라네. 그러니,\" 투츠 씨에게 옆으로 살짝 덧붙이며, \"형제여, 자네 아담과 이브를 한번 살펴보게나.\"\n\n\"꼭 그렇게 하겠습니다, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"비록 사건들의 변화에 관해 저 친구한테서 조금 듣긴 했네만,\" 기계 제작자가 주머니에서 낡은 안경을 꺼내 예의 그 버릇대로 이마에 얹으며 말을 이었다, \"워낙 크고 뜻밖의 일들인 데다, 내 소중한 아이를 본 것과, 또 저—,\" 플로렌스의 내리깐 눈을 흘끗 보고는, 문장을 마저 맺으려 하지 않은 채 말했다—\"그것 때문에 너무도 벅차서, 나는—나는 오늘 밤엔 별 말을 못 하겠네. 그런데 내 소중한 네드 커틀, 자네는 왜 편지를 안 했나?\"\n\n선장의 이목구비에 그려진 놀라움은 투츠 씨를 단단히 겁먹게 했고, 그의 눈은 그것에 완전히 못 박혀, 선장의 얼굴에서 도무지 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다.\n\n\"편지라니!\" 선장이 따라 했다. \"편지를 하라니, 솔 길스?\"\n\n\"그렇다네,\" 노인이 말했다, \"바베이도스로든, 자메이카로든, 데메라라로든, 그게 내가 부탁한 거였잖나.\"\n\n\"자네가 부탁했다니, 솔 길스?\" 선장이 되뇌었다.\n\n\"그렇다네,\" 노인이 말했다. \"모르겠나, 네드? 설마 잊지는 않았겠지? 내가 자네한테 편지할 때마다 말일세.\"\n\n선장은 번들거리는 모자를 벗어 갈고리에 걸고, 손으로 뒤에서부터 머리를 매만지며, 둘러앉은 사람들을 멍하니 바라보고 앉아 있었다. 영락없이 어리둥절한 체념의 화신이었다.\n\n\"자네 내 말을 못 알아듣는 모양이군, 네드!\" 늙은 솔이 일렀다.\n\n\"솔 길스,\" 선장이 한참을 말없이 그와 다른 이들을 바라본 뒤에 대답했다, \"나는 갈피를 못 잡고 표류하는 중일세. 그 모험담에 관해 한두 마디 풀어 주게나, 응! 내가 어떻게든 닻을 내릴 수가 없으니, 어찌하나? 어찌하나?\" 선장이 곰곰 생각에 잠겨, 사방을 두리번거리며 말했다.\n\n\"자네도 알지 않나, 네드,\" 솔 길스가 말했다, \"내가 왜 여기를 떠났는지. 자네 내 꾸러미를 열어 봤나, 네드?\"\n\n\"암, 그럼, 그럼,\" 선장이 말했다. \"물론, 그 꾸러미를 열어 봤네.\"\n\n\"그래서 읽어 봤나?\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 읽었네,\" 선장이, 그를 유심히 바라보며 대답하고는, 기억을 더듬어 그것을 외워 보였다. \"'내 소중한 네드 커틀, 내가 사랑하는—을 찾을 소식을 절망적으로 구하러 서인도제도로 집을 떠날 적에—' 저기 그 아이가 앉아 있군! 저기 월터가 있어!\" 선장이 마치 무언가 실재하고 의심할 수 없는 것을 붙들게 되어 안도하는 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"자, 네드. 이제 잠깐만 들어 보게!\" 노인이 말했다. \"내가 처음 편지를 썼을 때—그건 바베이도스에서였는데—자네가 그 편지를 받는 건 그해가 다 가기 한참 전이겠지만, 그래도 그 꾸러미를 열어 봐 주면 좋겠다고 했네, 내가 떠난 까닭을 거기 적어 두었으니까. 좋아, 네드. 두 번째, 세 번째, 아마 네 번째로 편지를 썼을 때는—그건 자메이카에서였는데—나는 여전히 같은 처지라, 도무지 안정을 찾을 수가 없고, 내 아이가 죽었는지 살았는지 알지 못하고는 그 지역을 떠날 수가 없다고 했네. 그다음에 편지를 썼을 때는—그건, 내 생각에, 데메라라에서였지, 아니었나?\"\n\n\"그건 자기 생각에 데메라라에서였다는군, 아니었느냐고!\" 선장이 막막하게 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"—나는 이렇게 썼네,\" 늙은 솔이 말을 이었다, \"여전히 확실한 소식은 아직 얻지 못했다고. 그 지역에서 나를 여러 해 알고 지낸 선장들과 그 밖의 사람들을 많이 만났는데, 그들이 여기저기 배편을 마련해 나를 도와주었고, 나도 이따금 내 솜씨로 그들에게 조금이나마 보답할 수 있었다고. 다들 나를 딱하게 여기고, 내 떠돌이 신세에 일종의 관심을 가져 주는 듯하다고. 그리고 나는, 죽을 때까지 내 아이의 소식을 찾아 떠돌아다니는 게 내 운명이 되려나 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다고.\"\n\n\"학식 있는 방황하는 네덜란드인이 다 된 것 같다는 생각이 들기 시작했다 이거지!\" 선장이 앞서처럼, 그리고 자못 진지하게 말했다.\n\n* 방황하는 네덜란드인: 영원히 바다를 떠도는 저주받은 유령선 전설.\n\"하지만 어느 날 소식이 왔네, 네드,—그건 내가 다시 돌아간 뒤의 바베이도스로였는데,—본국으로 향하던 중국 무역선과 교신이 있었고, 그 배에 내 아이가 타고 있다는 소식이었지. 그래서, 네드, 나는 다음 배에 배편을 얻어 집으로 돌아왔고, 오늘 밤 집에 닿아 보니 그게 사실이었네, 천만다행하게도!\" 노인이 경건하게 말했다.\n\n선장은 자못 경건하게 고개를 숙인 뒤, 투츠 씨를 시작으로 기계 제작자에 이르기까지 둘러앉은 무리를 죽 둘러보고는, 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"솔 길스! 내가 지금 하려는 말은 말일세, 자네가 펼칠 수 있는 돛이란 돛은 죄다 바닥줄에서 깨끗이 날려 버리고, 자네를 휘청 옆구리째 자빠뜨리게 만들 만한 말일세. 그 편지들 가운데 단 한 통도 에드워드 커틀한테 전해진 적이 없네. 그 편지들 가운데 단 한 통도,\" 선장이 자기 선언을 한층 더 엄숙하고 인상 깊게 만들려고 되풀이했다, \"잉글랜드의 뱃사람, 집에서 편히 지내며 빛나는 시간을 한 시간 한 시간 알차게 보내는, 에드워드 커틀한테 전해진 적이 없단 말일세!\"\n* 빛나는 시간을 알차게 보내는: 아이작 와츠의 동시 「부지런한 꿀벌」 구절을 따온 것.\n\n\"게다가 내 손으로 직접 부쳤다네! 그리고 내 손으로 직접 주소를 적었어, 브리그 플레이스 9번지라고!\" 늙은 솔이 외쳤다.\n\n선장의 얼굴에서 핏기가 싹 가셨다가 다시 발갛게 확 돌아왔다.\n\n\"브리그 플레이스 9번지라니, 솔 길스, 그게 무슨 뜻인가, 이 사람아?\" 선장이 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻이냐고? 자네 하숙집 말일세, 네드,\" 노인이 대답했다. \"그 무어라는 부인 말이야! 다음엔 내 이름도 잊어버리겠군, 하지만 나는 시류에 뒤처져 있으니—늘 그랬다는 거 자네도 알지 않나—그리고 정신이 무척 어수선해. 그—부인이—\"\n\n\"솔 길스!\" 선장이 마치 세상에서 가장 있을 법하지 않은 경우를 들이대듯 말했다, \"자네가 기억해 내려는 게 설마 맥스팅거라는 이름은 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"바로 그거야!\" 기계 제작자가 외쳤다. \"그렇고말고, 네드. 맥스팅거 부인!\"\n\n이제 더 커질 수 없을 만큼 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고, 얼굴의 혹들이 완전히 환하게 빛나는 커틀 선장은, 더없이 구슬픈 가락의 길고 날카로운 휘파람을 한 번 불고는, 말문이 막힌 채 모두를 멍하니 바라보며 서 있었다.\n\n\"미안하지만 그걸 한 번만 더 죽 풀어 주겠나, 솔 길스?\" 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그 편지들을 전부,\" 솔 아저씨가, 오른손 집게손가락으로 왼손 손바닥을 두드리며 박자를 맞추었는데, 그 한결같음과 또렷함은 그의 주머니 속 틀림없는 정밀시계에마저 부끄럽지 않을 정도였다, \"내가 내 손으로 직접 부치고, 내 손으로 직접 주소를 적어, 브리그 플레이스 9번지 맥스팅거 부인 댁의 커틀 선장 앞으로 보냈다네.\"\n\n선장은 갈고리에 걸어 둔 번들거리는 모자를 벗어 들고 안을 들여다보더니, 도로 쓰고 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"이거 참, 다들 들어 보게,\" 선장이 더없이 낭패한 꼴로 둘러보며 말했다, \"내가 거기서 줄행랑쳐 나왔단 말일세!\"\n\n\"그래서 선장님이 어디로 가셨는지 아무도 몰랐던 거군요, 커틀 선장님?\" 월터가 황급히 외쳤다.\n\n\"이것 보게나, 월터,\" 선장이 고개를 저으며 말했다, \"그 여자는 내가 이 가게를 맡으러 오는 걸 절대로 허락하지 않았을 걸세. 줄행랑치는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었지. 아이고 이 사람아, 월터!\" 선장이 말했다, \"자네는 그 여자가 잠잠할 때만 봤지! 하지만 그 여자가 성난 노여움이 솟구칠 때를 한번 봐 보게—그러곤 잘 새겨 두게나!\"\n\n\"제가 본때를 보여 줬을 텐데요!\" 니퍼가 나직이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그럴 것 같나, 정말로, 아가씨?\" 선장이 힘없는 감탄을 담아 대답했다. \"허, 아가씨, 참으로 갸륵한 말이로군. 하지만 나라면 차라리 먼저 맞서고 싶은 사나운 짐승이 한 마리도 없을 지경이라네. 나는 그 누구도 당해 낼 수 없는 친구 하나의 도움을 받아서야 겨우 내 궤짝을 빼낼 수 있었지. 거기로 편지를 보내 봐야 아무 소용이 없었을 걸세. 그 여자는 편지 한 통도 받아 두지 않았을 거야, 정말로,\" 선장이 말했다, \"그런 형편에서는! 허, 편지 배달부 노릇을 할 만한 값어치가 도무지 없었을 게야!\"\n\n\"그럼 분명한 건,\" 월터가 말했다, \"우리 모두가, 그중에서도 특히 선장님과 솔 아저씨가, 적잖은 근심을 안은 데 대해 맥스팅거 부인께 고마워해도 좋겠다는 거네요, 커틀 선장님.\"\n\n이렇게 작고한 맥스팅거 씨의 그 단호한 미망인에게 다들 신세를 진 셈이라는 것이 너무도 분명했으므로, 선장은 그 점을 따지지 않았다. 다만 아무도 그 주제를 들먹이지 않았고 특히 월터는 그것을—전에 그와 선장이 그 일을 두고 나눴던 마지막 대화를 떠올리며—애써 피했음에도, 선장은 자기 처지가 다소 부끄러웠던지, 거의 오 분 동안이나 먹구름 속에 잠겨 있었다—그로서는 유례없이 긴 시간이었다. 이윽고 그의 얼굴이라는 태양이 다시 한번 솟아올라, 유난한 광채로 바라보는 모두를 비추었고, 그는 모두와 거듭거듭 악수하는 발작에 빠져들었다.\n\n이른 시각이었으나, 솔 아저씨와 월터가 서로의 항해와 위험에 관해 한참 동안 캐묻고 난 뒤에, 그들은 월터만 빼고 모두 플로렌스의 방을 비우고 응접실로 내려갔다. 곧이어 월터가 그들과 합류하여, 플로렌스가 조금 서글프고 마음이 무거워져 잠자리에 들었다고 일러 주었다. 아래층에서 목소리를 낸들 그녀를 방해할 리는 없었으련만, 그 후로 그들은 모두 속삭이는 소리로 이야기했다. 그리고 저마다 제 나름의 방식으로 월터의 아리따운 어린 신부를 향해 무척 다정하고 부드러운 마음을 느꼈다. 솔 아저씨가 흡족하도록 그녀에 관한 모든 일에 대한 긴 설명이 있었다. 또 투츠 씨는, 월터가 자기 이름과 봉사를 중요하게, 또 자기 존재를 그 작은 회의에 꼭 필요하게 만들어 준 그 섬세함을 무척이나 절감했다.\n\n\"투츠 씨,\" 월터가 그와 집 문간에서 작별하며 말했다, \"우리 내일 아침에 보는 거지요?\"\n\n\"월터스 중위님,\" 투츠 씨가 그의 손을 뜨겁게 부여잡으며 대답했다, \"분명 참석하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"오랫동안 우리가 만나는 건 오늘 밤이 마지막입니다—어쩌면 영영 만나는 마지막 밤일 수도 있고요,\" 월터가 말했다. \"당신처럼 고결한 마음이라면, 내 생각에, 또 다른 마음이 거기에 묶일 때 무언가를 느끼지 않을 수 없을 겁니다. 내가 당신에게 무척 감사하고 있다는 걸 부디 알아주시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"월터스 씨,\" 투츠 씨가 자못 감동하여 대답했다, \"당신이 그럴 만한 까닭이 있다는 걸 느낄 수 있다면 나도 기쁘겠습니다.\"\n\n\"플로렌스가,\" 월터가 말했다, \"자기 본래의 이름을 지니는 이 마지막 밤에, 내게 약속을 시켰습니다—바로 방금, 당신이 우리 둘만 남겨 두고 갔을 때였지요—당신에게 전해 달라고 말입니다—그녀의 다정한 사랑과 더불어—\"\n\n투츠 씨는 문기둥에 손을 얹고, 그 손에 눈길을 떨구었다.\n\n\"—그녀의 다정한 사랑과 더불어,\" 월터가 말했다, \"그녀가 당신보다 더 소중히 여길 친구는 결코 가질 수 없으리라고요. 늘 자기를 진심으로 헤아려 준 당신을 향한 기억은 결코 잊을 수 없으리라고요. 오늘 밤 기도 속에서 당신을 기억하며, 자기가 멀리 떠난 뒤에도 당신이 자기를 생각해 주기를 바란다고요. 당신을 위해 제가 무어라 전할까요?\"\n\n\"이렇게 전해 주십시오, 월터,\" 투츠 씨가 또렷하지 못한 소리로 대답했다, \"나는 날마다 그녀를 생각할 테지만, 그녀가 사랑하는, 또 그녀를 사랑하는 사람과 결혼했다는 걸 아는 데서 오는 행복을 느끼지 않고서는 결코 그러지 않으리라고요. 부디 이렇게도 전해 주십시오, 그녀의 남편이 그녀를—그녀만큼이나!—받을 자격이 있다고 나는 확신하며, 그녀의 선택이 기쁘다고요.\"\n\n투츠 씨는 이 마지막 말에 이르자 한결 또렷해져서, 문기둥에서 눈을 들어, 그 말을 꿋꿋이 내뱉었다. 그러고 나서 그는 다시 한번 월터의 손을 뜨겁게 흔들었고, 월터도 지체 없이 그것에 화답했으며, 그는 집을 향해 길을 나섰다.\n\n투츠 씨에게는 치킨이 따라붙었는데, 그는 근래 저녁마다 그를 데리고 와 가게에 남겨 두었으니, 바깥에서 무슨 예기치 못한 사정이 생길 경우 그 걸출한 인물의 무용이 후보생관에 도움이 되리라는 생각에서였다. 그런데 치킨은 이번에는 영 기분이 좋아 보이지 않았다. 가스등이 사람을 속인 것이거나, 아니면 정말로, 투츠 씨가 길을 건너다 플로렌스가 잠든 방을 어깨 너머로 돌아보았을 때, 그는 흉측하게 눈을 치켜뜨고 코까지 일그러뜨렸다. 집으로 가는 길에 그는, 평화로운 호신술의 사범답지 않게, 다른 길손들을 향한 공격적인 의도를 자못 노골적으로 드러냈다. 집에 다다라서는, 투츠 씨를 거처까지 모셔다 드린 뒤 그 방에 남겨 두고 가는 대신, 그 앞에 버티고 서서 흰 모자챙을 두 손으로 잡고 무게를 가늠하듯 흔들며, 머리와 코를(둘 다 여러 번 부러져 시원찮게 고쳐진 것이었다) 씰룩거렸는데, 그 태도에는 단연 무례한 기색이 어려 있었다.\n\n그의 후원자는 제 생각에 푹 잠겨 있던 터라 한동안 이것을 알아채지 못했고, 사실 치킨이 결코 무시당하지 않겠다고 작정하고 혀와 이로 여러 번 딱딱 소리를 내어 주의를 끌고 나서야 비로소 알아챘다.\n\n\"자, 나리,\" 치킨이 마침내 투츠 씨의 눈길을 붙들자 무뚝뚝하게 말했다, \"이 사기 노름을 여기서 끝장낼 작정이신지, 아니면 이겨 보러 들어가실 작정이신지 알고 싶습니다요.\"\n\n\"치킨,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"분명히 말해 보게.\"\n\n\"그럼, 사정인즉 이렇습니다요, 나리,\" 치킨이 말했다. \"저는 말 한마디도 헛되이 버리는 위인이 아니거든요. 사정은 이렇습죠. 그놈들 중에 누구라도 떡이 되게 패 줄 작정이신지요?\"\n\n이 질문을 던지면서 치킨은 모자를 떨어뜨리고, 왼손으로 슬쩍 피하고 찌르는 시늉을 하더니, 오른손으로 가상의 적에게 맹렬한 일격을 날리고, 머리를 날렵하게 흔든 뒤, 자세를 추슬렀다.\n\n\"자, 나리,\" 치킨이 말했다. \"사기로 가실 겁니까, 배짱으로 가실 겁니까? 어느 쪽입니까?\"\n\n\"치킨,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"자네 표현은 거칠고, 뜻은 알쏭달쏭하군.\"\n\n\"그럼, 이렇게 말씀드리죠, 나리,\" 치킨이 말했다. \"사정은 이렇다는 겁니다. 그건 치사한 짓이라고요.\"\n\n\"치사한 게 뭐란 말인가, 치킨?\" 투츠 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"이게 그렇다 이겁니다,\" 치킨이 부러진 코를 무시무시하게 찡그리며 말했다. \"거 보십쇼! 자, 나리! 아니! 나리께서 이 혼사를 그 영감탱이한테,\" 이 깔보는 호칭으로 그 시합꾼이 돔비 씨를 가리키려 한 것이라고 그 후 짐작되었다, \"고자질해서 깨 버릴 수도 있는데다, 그 신랑 놈이며 그 패거리 죄다 숨도 못 쉬고 정신도 못 차리게 뻗어 버리게 만들 수도 있는데, 그냥 손 들고 마실 작정입니까? 손을 들어요?\" 치킨이 경멸하듯 힘주어 말했다. \"허, 그건 치사한 짓이라고요!\"\n\n\"치킨,\" 투츠 씨가 엄하게 말했다, \"자네는 영락없는 독수리로군! 자네 속내는 흉악하기 짝이 없네.\"\n\n\"제 속내는 배짱과 멋입니다요, 나리,\" 치킨이 대답했다. \"그게 바로 제 속내라고요. 저는 치사한 짓은 못 봐 줍니다. 저는 대중 앞에 나서는 몸이고, 리틀 헬레펀트 술집 카운터에서 제 소문이 자자한 몸인데, 제 주인 양반이 가서 치사한 짓을 하면 안 되지요. 허, 그건 치사한 짓이라니까요,\" 치킨이 한층 힘주어 말했다. \"사정이 그렇다는 겁니다. 그건 치사한 짓이에요.\"\n\n\"치킨,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"자네는 정나미가 떨어지는군.\"\n\n\"나리,\" 치킨이 모자를 쓰며 대답했다, \"그럼 피차 마찬가지입니다요. 자! 제안 하나 하죠! 나리께서 술집 차리는 일을 두고 한두 번 저한테 말씀하신 적이 있잖습니까. 됐고요! 내일 저한테 오십 파운드짜리 한 장 주시고, 절 보내 주십쇼.\"\n\n\"치킨,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"자네가 토해 낸 그 역겨운 속내를 듣고 나니, 그런 조건으로 헤어지게 되어 차라리 기쁘군.\"\n\n\"그럼 결정 났습니다,\" 치킨이 말했다. \"거래 성사입죠. 나리의 이런 처신은 제 장부에 맞지가 않아요. 허, 그건 치사한 짓이라고요,\" 치킨이 말했는데, 그는 그 대목을 넘어서지도, 또 그 못 미쳐 멈추지도 못하는 듯했다. \"사정이 그렇다는 겁니다. 그건 치사한 짓이에요!\"\n\n이리하여 투츠 씨와 치킨은 이 도덕적 인식의 양립 불가를 이유로 헤어지기로 합의했다. 그리고 투츠 씨는 잠자리에 누워, 자기 처녀 시절의 마지막 밤에 자기를 친구로 생각해 주고, 또 자기에게 다정한 사랑을 보내 준 플로렌스의 행복한 꿈을 꾸었다.\n\n# 제52장\n\n교구지기 사운즈 씨와 좌석 안내인 미프 부인은 돔비 씨가 결혼식을 올렸던 그 훌륭한 교회의 제자리에 일찌감치 와 있다. 인도에서 온 누런 얼굴의 노신사가 오늘 아침 젊은 아내를 맞아들이려 하고 있으며, 하객을 가득 태운 마차 여섯 대가 올 것으로 예상되는 데다, 미프 부인은 그 누런 얼굴의 노신사가 교회까지 가는 길을 다이아몬드로 포장하고도 그것을 거의 아쉬워하지 않을 정도라는 이야기를 들은 터다.\n\n혼인의 축도는 한층 격이 높은 것으로, 매우 존귀하신 한 분, 즉 주임 사제께서 집전하실 예정이며, 신부는 특별한 선물처럼 호스 가즈에서 일부러 온 어떤 사람의 손에 이끌려 넘겨질 것이다.\n* 호스 가즈: 런던에 있던 영국 육군 본부.\n\n미프 부인은 오늘 아침 평소보다 더 평범한 사람들을 못마땅해한다. 그리고 그녀는 그 문제에 늘 강경한 견해를 갖고 있는데, 그것이 무료 좌석과 결부되어 있기 때문이다. 미프 부인은 정치경제학을 공부한 사람은 아니지만(그 학문이 비국교도들과 관련 있다고 여긴다. \"침례교도나 웨슬리교도, 뭐 그런 부류들 말이지\"라고 그녀는 말한다), 도대체 평범한 사람들이 무슨 자격으로 결혼을 하는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없다. \"쳇, 망할 것들,\" 미프 부인이 말한다, \"그 사람들 머리 위에서나 똑같은 말을 읽어 주는데, 받는 건 금화 대신 6펜스라니까!\"\n\n교구지기 사운즈 씨는 미프 부인보다 너그러운 편이다—하기야 그는 좌석 안내인은 아니니까. \"해야 할 일이지요, 부인,\" 그가 말한다. \"우리가 그 사람들을 결혼시켜 줘야지요. 우리한테는 앞장서서 행진할 국민학교도 있어야 하고, 상비군도 있어야 하니까요. 그 사람들을 결혼시켜 줘야지요, 부인,\" 사운즈 씨가 말한다, \"그래야 나라가 굴러가지요.\"\n\n사운즈 씨가 계단에 앉아 있고 미프 부인이 교회 안에서 먼지를 털고 있을 때, 수수한 차림의 젊은 한 쌍이 들어온다. 미프 부인의 풀 죽은 보닛이 그들을 향해 매섭게 돌아간다. 이렇게 이른 방문에서 그녀는 야반도주 결혼의 낌새를 알아챈 것이다. 그러나 그들은 결혼하려는 게 아니다—\"그저,\" 신사가 말한다, \"교회를 한 바퀴 둘러보고 싶을 뿐입니다.\" 그가 미프 부인의 손바닥에 점잖은 사례를 슬쩍 쥐여 주자, 그녀의 식초처럼 시큼한 얼굴이 누그러지고, 풀 죽은 보닛과 비쩍 마른 몸이 까딱거리며 바스락거린다.\n\n미프 부인은 다시 먼지를 털고 방석을 두툼하게 부풀린다—그 누런 얼굴의 노신사가 무릎이 약하다는 소문이 있기 때문이다—그러면서도 교회를 둘러보고 있는 젊은 한 쌍에게서 좌석 안내인 특유의 흐리멍덩한 눈길을 떼지 않는다. \"에헴,\" 미프 부인이 헛기침을 한다. 그녀의 기침은 자기 관할의 어떤 무릎방석에 든 건초보다도 메마르다. \"조만간 어느 아침에 우리한테 오시겠구먼, 얘들아, 내 짐작이 크게 틀리지 않다면 말이지!\"\n\n그들은 죽은 누군가를 기리기 위해 세워진 벽의 명판을 바라보고 있다. 미프 부인에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 있지만, 미프 부인은 한쪽 눈으로도 그녀가 어떻게 그의 팔에 기대고 있는지, 그의 머리가 어떻게 그녀 위로 숙여져 있는지 알아볼 수 있다. \"그래, 그래,\" 미프 부인이 말한다, \"이보다 못한 짝을 만날 수도 있었지. 둘이 참 단정한 한 쌍이니까!\"\n\n미프 부인의 말에 사사로운 뜻은 없다. 그녀는 그저 자기 장사 밑천에 대해 말할 뿐이다. 그녀는 한 쌍에 대해 관을 두고 갖는 것 이상의 호기심을 거의 품지 않는다. 그녀는 어찌나 비쩍 마르고 꼿꼿하고 메마른 노부인인지—어찌나 좌석 같은 여인인지—나무토막에서 그만큼의 개인적 정감이라도 찾아내는 편이 나을 정도다. 그런데 살집이 있고 외투에 진홍색을 두른 사운즈 씨는 기질이 다르다. 그는 둘이 계단에 서서 젊은 한 쌍이 멀어져 가는 모습을 지켜보며, 저 여자는 몸매가 예쁘지 않냐고, 그리고 자기가 본 한에서는(나오면서 고개를 숙이고 있었으니까) 보기 드물게 예쁜 얼굴이라고 말한다. \"통틀어 말이지요, 미프 부인,\" 사운즈 씨가 음미하듯 말한다, \"저 여자는 그야말로 막 피어나는 장미꽃봉오리라고 할 만하지요.\"\n\n미프 부인은 풀 죽은 보닛을 비쩍 마르게 한 번 끄덕여 동의를 표하지만, 이 말이 어찌나 마음에 들지 않는지, 속으로는 사운즈 씨가 아무리 큰돈을 준다 해도, 교구지기이긴 하지만, 절대 그의 아내가 되지는 않겠다고 다짐한다.\n\n그러면 교회를 떠나 문밖으로 나서는 젊은 한 쌍은 무슨 이야기를 나누고 있는가?\n\n\"고마워요, 월터! 이제 나는 행복하게 떠날 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 돌아오면, 플로렌스, 우리 다시 와서 그의 무덤을 보러 가요.\"\n\n플로렌스는 눈물로 반짝이는 눈을 들어 그의 다정한 얼굴을 바라본다. 그러고는 그의 팔을 붙잡고 있는 그 다른 작고 얌전한 손 위에, 비어 있는 손을 포개어 꼭 쥔다.\n\n\"아직 이른 시간이에요, 월터, 거리도 거의 텅 비어 있고요. 걸어가요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신이 너무 지칠 거예요, 내 사랑.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요! 우리가 맨 처음 함께 걸었을 때는 정말 많이 지쳤지만, 오늘은 그렇지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n이렇게—그다지 변하지 않은 채—그녀는, 그때처럼 순수하고 진실한 마음으로—그는, 그때처럼 솔직하고 희망에 차서, 그리고 그녀를 더욱 자랑스러워하며—플로렌스와 월터는, 결혼식 아침에, 함께 거리를 걸어간다.\n\n오래전 그 어린 시절의 산책에서조차, 그들이 주위의 온 세상으로부터 오늘만큼 멀리 떨어져 있던 적은 없었다. 오래전 그 어린 발들은 지금 그들의 발이 밟는 것 같은 마법에 걸린 땅을 밟아 본 적이 없었다. 아이들의 신뢰와 사랑은 여러 번 주어질 수 있고, 여러 곳에서 돋아날 수 있다. 그러나 그 나뉘지 않은 보물을 간직한 플로렌스의 여인다운 마음은 단 한 번만 내어줄 수 있으며, 멸시나 변심을 만나면 시들어 죽어 갈 뿐이다.\n\n그들은 가장 한적한 거리를 골라, 그녀의 옛집이 서 있는 거리 근처로는 가지 않는다. 화창하고 따뜻한 여름 아침이라, 그들이 도심을 뒤덮은 어두워지는 안개를 향해 걸어가는 동안 해가 그들을 비춘다. 상점에서는 재물이 모습을 드러내고, 보석과 금과 은이 금세공인의 햇빛 드는 진열창에서 번득이며, 그들이 지나갈 때 큰 저택들이 위풍당당한 그늘을 그들 위로 드리운다. 그러나 그 빛 속을 지나, 그 그늘 속을 지나, 그들은 주위의 모든 것을 잊은 채 다정히 함께 걸어간다. 지금 서로의 안에 지닌 것보다 더한 재물도, 더 자랑스러운 보금자리도 생각하지 않으면서.\n\n차츰 그들은 더 어둡고 더 좁은 거리로 접어드는데, 거기서는 이제 누렇기도 하고 붉기도 한 해가 안개 사이로, 오직 길모퉁이에서나, 나무 한 그루가 있거나 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 교회 가운데 하나가 있거나 포장된 길과 층계가 있거나 묘한 작은 텃밭 한 뙈기가 있거나 묘지가 있는 작고 트인 공간에서만 보인다. 그 묘지에는 몇 안 되는 무덤과 묘비가 거의 새카맣게 변해 있다. 다정하게 그리고 믿음으로, 그 모든 좁은 마당과 골목과 그늘진 거리를 지나, 플로렌스는 그의 아내가 되기 위해 그의 팔에 매달려 걸어간다.\n\n이제 그녀의 가슴이 더 빨리 뛴다. 월터가 그들의 교회가 아주 가깝다고 말해 주기 때문이다. 그들은 문 앞에 짐마차가 늘어서고 분주한 짐꾼들이 길을 막고 있는 커다란 창고 무더기 몇 채를 지나는데—플로렌스는 그것을 보지도 듣지도 못한다—이윽고 공기가 고요해지고, 날이 어두워지며, 그녀는 지하실 같은 묘한 냄새가 나는 교회 안에서 떨고 있다.\n\n실망스러운 종을 울리는 종지기인 초라하고 작은 노인이 현관에 서서, 모자를 세례반 안에 넣어 두었다—그는 교회지기여서 그곳이 아주 제집처럼 편하기 때문이다. 그는 그들을 낡고 갈색이며 판자를 덧댄 먼지투성이 제의실로 안내하는데, 그곳은 선반을 들어낸 모서리 찬장 같다. 거기 좀먹은 등록부들이 빛바랜 코담배 같은 냄새를 풍기는 바람에 눈물 글썽이던 니퍼가 재채기를 했다.\n\n이 낡고 먼지투성이인 곳에서, 곁에 자기 남편 말고는 마음 붙일 대상 하나 없이 선 젊은 신부는 어쩌면 그토록 앳되고 또 아름다운지. 맞은편 아치 길 아래, 완벽한 기둥 요새 뒤편에서 김빠진 신문 가게 비슷한 것을 꾸려 가는 먼지투성이 늙은 사무원이 있다. 오직 자기 한 몸만 건사하면서도 그것만으로도 할 일이 충분하다고 여기는 먼지투성이 늙은 좌석 안내인이 있다. 옆 마당에 회관을 가진 어느 명예로운 동업조합과 무슨 관련이 있는 먼지투성이 늙은 교구지기가 있는데(이들은 지난 일요일 토츠 씨가 만난 그 교구지기와 좌석 안내인이다), 그 회관에는 살아 있는 사람이라면 아무도 본 적 없는 스테인드글라스 창이 하나 있다. 제단 위로, 칸막이 위로, 회랑 둘레로, 그리고 천육백구십사 년에 그 명예로운 동업조합의 회장과 간사들이 한 일에 관한 명문 위로, 먼지투성이 나무 선반과 처마 장식들이 들쭉날쭉 삐죽삐죽 끼어 있다. 설교단과 독경대 위로는 먼지투성이 낡은 음향판들이 있는데, 마치 집전하는 목사들이 무례한 짓을 할 경우 그들 위로 떨어뜨릴 뚜껑처럼 보인다. 먼지를 수용할 온갖 가능한 시설이 다 갖추어져 있는데, 다만 묘지만은 예외여서, 그 점에서는 시설이 매우 한정되어 있다.\n\n선장과 솔 삼촌, 그리고 토츠 씨가 왔다. 목사는 제의실에서 법의를 걸치고 있고, 사무원은 그 둘레를 돌며 거기 묻은 먼지를 불어 낸다. 그리고 신랑과 신부는 제단 앞에 선다. 수전 니퍼를 들러리로 친다면 모를까, 들러리는 없다. 그리고 커틀 선장보다 더 나은 신부 측 아버지 역도 없다. 나무다리를 단 한 사내가, 시들한 사과를 우물거리며 한 손에 파란 자루를 들고, 무슨 일이 벌어지나 보려고 안을 들여다본다. 그러나 별로 흥미로운 게 없다는 걸 알고는 다시 쿵쿵 걸어 나가, 문밖 메아리들 사이로 또각또각 길을 뚫고 간다.\n\n제단 앞에 겁먹은 머리를 숙인 채 무릎 꿇은 플로렌스 위로는 자비로운 한 줄기 빛도 비치는 것이 보이지 않는다. 아침의 발광체는 가로막혀 있어 그곳을 비추지 않는다. 밖에는 참새들이 조금씩 짹짹거리는 앙상한 나무 한 그루가 있고, 창 맞은편 염색업자의 다락방, 햇빛이 작은 구멍처럼 든 곳에는 검은 새 한 마리가 있어 예식이 진행되는 내내 큰 소리로 휘파람을 분다. 그리고 나무다리를 단 사내가 쿵쿵 걸어 멀어져 간다. 먼지투성이 사무원의 아멘 소리는, 맥베스의 그것처럼, 목구멍에 조금 걸리는 듯하다. 그러나 커틀 선장이 그를 거들어 주는데, 어찌나 선의가 넘치게 하는지 그는 그 단어로 된 전에 없던 응답을 셋이나 새로이 끼워 넣는다. 그런 응답은 이전까지 예식에 한 번도 도입된 적이 없는 것이다.\n\n그들은 혼인을 맺고, 재채기를 부르는 낡은 등록부 가운데 하나에 이름을 서명했으며, 목사의 법의는 다시 먼지 속으로 돌려졌고, 목사는 집으로 돌아갔다. 어두운 교회의 어두운 구석에서, 플로렌스는 수전 니퍼에게로 돌아서서 그녀의 품에 안겨 흐느끼고 있다. 토츠 씨의 눈은 벌겋다. 선장은 자기 코를 문질러 댄다. 솔 삼촌은 이마에 올려 두었던 안경을 끌어내리고는 문가로 걸어 나갔다.\n\n\"네게 신의 가호가 있기를, 수전, 사랑하는 수전! 내가 월터를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 그리고 그를 사랑할 만한 까닭이 내게 얼마나 많은지 언젠가 네가 증언할 수 있게 된다면, 그를 위해 그렇게 해 주렴. 잘 있어! 잘 있어!\"\n\n그들은 미드십맨으로 돌아가기보다 이대로 헤어지는 편이 낫겠다고 생각해 둔 터다. 마차 한 대가 바로 근처에서 그들을 기다리고 있다.\n\n니퍼 양은 말을 잇지 못한다. 그저 흐느끼고 목이 메어, 아씨를 끌어안을 뿐이다. 토츠 씨가 다가와, 그녀에게 기운을 내라고 권하며 그녀를 맡아 돌본다. 플로렌스는 그에게 손을 내밀고—벅찬 마음에 그에게 입술까지 내어 주고—솔 삼촌과 커틀 선장에게 입을 맞춘 뒤, 젊은 남편에게 이끌려 떠나간다.\n\n그러나 수전은 플로렌스가 자기에 대한 슬픈 기억을 안고 떠나는 것을 견딜 수가 없다. 자기는 이렇게가 아니라 전혀 다르게 굴려고 했었던 터라, 스스로를 모질게 자책한다. 자기 체면을 만회하려고 마지막 한 번의 노력을 기울이기로 작정하고, 그녀는 토츠 씨에게서 벗어나 마차를 찾아내어 작별의 미소를 보여 주려고 달려간다. 그녀의 의도를 알아챈 선장이 그녀를 뒤쫓아 나선다. 그 역시 가능하다면 그들을 환호로 배웅하는 것이 자기 의무라고 느끼기 때문이다. 솔 삼촌과 토츠 씨는 둘이 함께 교회 밖에 남아 그들을 기다린다.\n\n마차는 떠났지만, 거리가 가파르고 좁고 막혀 있어서, 수전은 저 멀리 마차가 멈춰 서 있는 것을 볼 수 있다고, 그렇게 확신한다. 커틀 선장이 언덕을 내달리는 그녀를 뒤따르며, 광택 나는 모자를 흔들어 전체에게 보내는 신호로 삼는데, 그 신호가 맞는 마차를 끌어들일 수도 있고 그러지 못할 수도 있다.\n\n수전이 선장을 앞질러, 마차를 따라잡는다. 그녀가 창 안을 들여다보니, 월터가 보이고, 그 곁에 다정한 얼굴이 보인다. 그녀는 두 손을 마주치며 외친다.\n\n\"플로이 아가씨, 내 사랑! 저를 봐요! 우리 모두 이제 이렇게 행복해요, 아가씨! 한 번만 더 작별 인사를요, 내 보배 아가씨, 한 번만 더요!\"\n\n수전이 어떻게 그렇게 하는지는 자기도 모르지만, 어느새 창문에 닿아, 아가씨에게 입을 맞추고, 두 팔로 그 목을 감싸 안는다.\n\n\"우리 모두 이제 이렇게—이렇게 행복해요, 사랑하는 플로이 아가씨!\" 수전이 수상쩍게 숨을 들이켜며 말한다. \"아가씨는, 아가씨는 이제 저한테 화나지 않으셨죠. 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"화라니, 수전!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요. 화 안 나신 거 알아요. 화 안 나신 거 안다고요, 내 귀염둥이, 내 사랑하는 아가씨!\" 수전이 소리친다. \"그리고 여기 선장님도 오셨어요—아가씨 친구인 선장님 말이에요—한 번 더 작별 인사를 하러요!\"\n\n\"만세, 내 마음의 기쁨이여!\" 선장이 격한 감정이 어린 얼굴로 목청껏 외친다. \"만세, 월터 내 아들아. 만세! 만세!\"\n\n한쪽 창에는 젊은 남편이, 다른 쪽 창에는 젊은 아내가 있고, 이쪽 문에는 선장이 매달려 있고, 저쪽은 수전 니퍼가 꼭 붙들고 있고, 마차는 가든 안 가든 가야만 하는 처지인 데다, 마차가 머뭇거리는 통에 다른 모든 짐마차와 마차들이 소란스러우니, 네 바퀴 위에 이만한 혼란이 일었던 적은 일찍이 없었다. 그러나 수전 니퍼는 용감하게 자기 자리를 지킨다. 그녀는 마지막 순간까지 눈물을 흘리면서도 미소를 지으며, 아씨를 향해 웃는 얼굴을 잃지 않는다. 마침내 그녀가 뒤에 처졌을 때에도, 선장은 셔츠 깃을 격렬하게 펄럭이며, \"만세, 내 아들아! 만세, 내 마음의 기쁨이여!\" 외치면서 계속 문가에 나타났다 사라졌다 하는데, 마침내 더는 마차를 따라가려는 것이 가망 없어질 때까지 그러기를 멈추지 않는다. 마침내 마차가 떠나 버리자, 수전 니퍼는 선장과 다시 합류한 뒤 인사불성 상태에 빠져, 정신을 차리도록 어느 빵집으로 들려 들어간다.\n\n솔 삼촌과 토츠 씨는 교회 묘지에서 난간의 갓돌 위에 앉아, 커틀 선장과 수전이 돌아올 때까지 끈기 있게 기다린다. 둘 다 말을 하거나 말을 거는 것을 조금도 바라지 않으니, 그들은 더없이 좋은 동무이며 아주 만족스럽다. 그들 모두 다시 작은 미드십맨에 도착해 아침 식탁에 둘러앉지만, 아무도 한 입도 먹지 못한다. 커틀 선장은 토스트를 게걸스레 먹는 시늉을 하지만, 그것이 속임수임을 자인하고 그만둔다. 토츠 씨는 아침을 먹고 나서 저녁에 다시 오겠다고 말하고는, 마치 두 주 동안 잠자리에 들지 못한 듯한 막연한 느낌을 품은 채 온종일 시내를 떠돈다.\n\n그들이 함께 어울려 지내곤 했던, 그리고 이제 그 안에서 너무도 많은 것이 사라져 버린 그 집과 그 방에는 묘한 매력이 깃들어 있다. 그것은 이별의 슬픔을 더하면서도, 동시에 그 슬픔을 달래 준다. 토츠 씨는 밤에 와서 수전 니퍼에게, 온종일 이토록 비참한 적이 없었지만 그래도 이게 좋다고 말한다. 그는 수전 니퍼와 단둘이 있게 되자 속내를 털어놓으며, 그녀가 돔비 아가씨가 그를 사랑하게 될 가망에 대해 그 솔직한 견해를 들려주었을 때 자기 심정이 어땠는지를 이야기한다. 이런 공통된 추억과 그들의 눈물이 자아낸 흉금을 트는 분위기 속에서, 토츠 씨는 둘이 함께 나가 저녁거리를 좀 사 오자고 제안한다. 니퍼 양이 동의하자, 그들은 자잘한 것들을 잔뜩 사고, 리처즈 부인의 도움을 받아, 선장과 늙은 솔이 돌아오기 전에 저녁상을 제법 그럴듯하게 차려 놓는다.\n\n선장과 늙은 솔은 배에 올라가, 거기에 다이를 자리 잡게 해 두고, 궤짝들이 실리는 것을 보고 왔다. 그들은 월터의 인기에 대해, 그가 곁에 누리게 될 안락함에 대해, 그리고 그가 자기 작은 아내를 놀래 주려고 선장이 \"한 폭의 그림\"이라 부르는 것으로 선실을 꾸미느라 아침저녁으로 묵묵히 일해 온 듯하다는 것에 대해, 할 이야기가 많다. \"제독의 선실이라도 말이지,\" 선장이 말한다, \"이보다 더 말끔하진 않을 거요.\"\n\n그러나 선장의 가장 큰 기쁨 가운데 하나는, 그 큰 시계와 각설탕 집게와 찻숟가락들이 배에 실려 있음을 안다는 것이다. 그래서 그는 몇 번이고 거듭 혼잣말로 중얼거린다. \"에드워드 커틀, 이 친구야, 자네 그 자그마한 재산을 공동 명의로 넘겨준 그때만큼 평생 더 나은 항로를 잡은 적이 없다네. 자네는 땅이 어떻게 자리 잡혔는지 잘 봤어, 에드워드,\" 선장이 말한다, \"그리고 그건 자네 명예가 될 일이야, 이 친구야.\"\n\n늙은 계기 제작자는 예전보다 더 정신이 산만하고 흐리멍덩해져서, 이 결혼과 이별을 무척이나 마음 아파한다. 그러나 그는 오랜 동지인 네드 커틀이 곁에 있어 크게 위로받고 있으며, 고맙고 흡족한 얼굴로 저녁 식탁에 앉는다.\n\n\"내 아이는 무사히 지켜져서 잘 자라고 있어,\" 늙은 솔 길스가 두 손을 비비며 말한다. \"내가 감사하고 행복해하지 않을 무슨 까닭이 있겠나!\"\n\n아직 식탁에 자리를 잡지 않고, 한동안 안절부절못하며 서성이다가 이제 제자리에 망설이며 서 있는 선장이, 미심쩍은 듯 길스 씨를 바라보며 말한다.\n\n\"솔! 저 아래에 그 묵은 마데이라가 마지막 한 병 있잖나. 오늘 밤 그걸 올려다가, 월터와 그 아내를 위해 마시고 싶나, 이 사람아?\"\n\n계기 제작자는 선장을 애틋하게 바라보더니, 커피색 외투의 가슴 안주머니에 손을 넣어 수첩을 꺼내고는, 거기서 편지 한 장을 빼낸다.\n\n\"돔비 씨께,\" 노인이 말한다. \"월터한테서 온 거야. 삼 주 뒤에 부치라고. 읽어 주지.\"\n\n\"'선생님. 저는 선생님의 따님과 결혼했습니다. 그녀는 저와 함께 먼 항해에 올랐습니다. 그녀에게 헌신한다는 것은 그녀나 선생님께 아무런 권리를 주장하지 않는다는 뜻이지만, 제가 그러하다는 것은 하느님께서 아십니다.\n\n\"'어찌하여, 그녀를 이 세상 그 무엇보다도 사랑하면서도, 저는 후회 없이 그녀를 제 삶의 불확실함과 위험에 묶어 두었는가, 그것을 선생님께 말씀드리지는 않겠습니다. 선생님께서는 그 까닭을 아시며, 또 그녀의 아버지이시니까요.\n\n\"'그녀를 나무라지 마십시오. 그녀는 한 번도 선생님을 나무란 적이 없습니다.\n\n\"'선생님께서 저를 언젠가 용서해 주시리라고는 생각하지도, 바라지도 않습니다. 그것만큼 제가 기대하지 않는 일도 없습니다. 그러나 만일 플로렌스 곁에 늘 누군가가, 지난 슬픔의 기억을 지워 주는 것을 제 삶의 큰 사명으로 삼는 누군가가 있다고 믿는 것이 선생님께 위안이 될 그런 순간이 온다면, 저는 엄숙히 단언합니다, 선생님께서는 그 순간에 그 믿음 안에서 마음 놓으셔도 된다고요.'\"\n\n솔로몬은 편지를 조심스레 수첩에 도로 넣고, 수첩을 다시 외투에 집어넣는다.\n\n\"그 묵은 마데이라 마지막 한 병은 아직 마시지 마세, 네드,\" 노인이 생각에 잠겨 말한다. \"아직은 아니야.\n\n\"아직은 아니지,\" 선장이 동의한다. \"그래. 아직은 아니야.\"\n\n수전과 토츠 씨도 같은 생각이다. 한동안의 침묵 끝에 그들은 모두 저녁 식탁에 둘러앉아, 다른 것으로 젊은 부부를 위해 잔을 든다. 그리고 그 묵은 마데이라 마지막 한 병은 여전히 그 먼지와 거미줄 속에, 건드려지지 않은 채 남아 있다.\n\n며칠이 흘렀고, 한 척의 위풍당당한 배가 순풍에 흰 날개를 펼친 채 먼바다에 나가 있다.\n\n갑판 위에는, 배에 탄 가장 거친 사내에게조차 우아하고 아름답고 해롭지 않은 무언가의 형상으로—거기 있어 좋고 기쁜 무언가, 항해를 순조롭게 해 줄 듯한 무언가의 형상으로—플로렌스가 있다. 때는 밤이고, 그녀와 월터는 단둘이 앉아, 자기들과 달 사이로 바다 위에 드리운 엄숙한 빛의 길을 바라보고 있다.\n\n이윽고 그녀는 눈에 차오르는 눈물 때문에 그것을 또렷이 볼 수 없게 된다. 그러자 그녀는 그의 가슴에 머리를 묻고, 두 팔로 그의 목을 감싸며 말한다. \"오 월터, 사랑하는 이여, 나는 정말 행복해요!\"\n\n남편은 그녀를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안고, 둘은 아주 고요하며, 위풍당당한 배는 잔잔히 나아간다.\n\n\"바닷소리를 들으며,\" 플로렌스가 말한다, \"그것을 바라보고 앉아 있으면, 참 많은 날들이 마음에 떠올라요. 정말 많은 생각이 들어요—\"\n\n\"폴 생각이지, 내 사랑. 나도 알아요.\"\n\n폴과 월터 생각이다. 그리고 파도 속의 목소리들은 그 끊임없는 속삭임으로, 늘 플로렌스에게 사랑을 속삭인다—이 세상의 경계로도, 시간의 끝으로도 한정되지 않고, 바다 너머로, 하늘 너머로, 저 멀리 보이지 않는 나라에까지 여전히 뻗어 가는, 영원하고 가없는 사랑을.\n\n# CHAPTER LVIII. After a Lapse\n\n바다는 한 해 내내 썰물과 밀물을 거듭했다. 한 해 내내 바람과 구름이 오고 갔으며, 시간의 쉼 없는 작업이 폭풍 속에서도 햇빛 속에서도 이루어졌다. 한 해 내내 인간사의 운수와 변천의 조류가 저마다 정해진 길로 흘러갔다. 한 해 내내 그 유명한 돔비와 아들 회사는 살아남기 위한 싸움을 벌였으니, 어긋난 사고들과, 불확실한 소문들과, 실패한 모험들과, 불리한 시운(時運)과, 그리고 무엇보다도 그 우두머리의 맹목에 맞선 싸움이었다. 그는 사업을 머리카락 한 올만큼도 줄이려 하지 않았고, 자기가 그토록 무리하게 폭풍에 맞세운 배가 약하여 그것을 견딜 수 없다는 한마디 경고도 들으려 하지 않았다.\n\n그 한 해가 다하자, 그 거대한 회사가 무너졌다.\n\n어느 여름날 오후, 시내 교회에서의 결혼식이 있은 지 며칠 모자라는 한 해가 지난 무렵, 거래소에는 큰 파산에 대한 웅성거림과 속삭임이 일었다. 그곳에서 잘 알려진, 어떤 차갑고 거만한 사람이 그 자리에 없었고, 그를 대신할 사람도 없었다. 이튿날 돔비와 아들이 거래를 중단했다는 소문이 널리 퍼졌고, 그다음 날 밤에는 파산자 명단이 발표되었는데, 그 첫머리에 바로 그 이름이 올라 있었다.\n\n세상은 이제 정말이지 매우 분주했고, 할 말이 많았다. 그것은 순진하게 잘 믿고, 또 몹시 부당한 대우를 받는 세상이었다. 그것은 다른 어떤 종류의 파산도 결코 존재하지 않는 세상이었다. 그 속에는 종교며 애국심이며 미덕이며 명예라는 썩은 둑 위에서 멀리까지 두루 장사를 하는 눈에 띄는 인물이라곤 하나도 없었다. 누군가가 제법 그럴듯하게 살아가면서, 아무런 자산도 없이 막대한 선(善)의 액수를 갚아 주겠노라 약속하며 유통시키는, 거론할 만한 분량의 한낱 종잇조각이라곤 하나도 없었다. 돈을 빼고는 그 어떤 것에서도, 그 어디에서도 부족함이라곤 없었다. 세상은 참으로 몹시 노여워했다. 그리고 더 나쁜 세상이었다면 자기들이야말로 허울과 가식의 능란한 장사꾼이었으리라 짐작될 법한 사람들이, 특히나 대단히 분개하는 것이 눈에 띄었다.\n\n이 일은 환경의 노리갯감인 심부름꾼 퍼치 씨에게 방탕으로의 새로운 유혹을 안겨 주었다! 퍼치 씨의 운명이란 늘 잠에서 깨어 보니 자기가 유명해져 있는 것이었던 모양이다. 그는, 말하자면 바로 어제, 사랑의 도피며 그에 뒤따른 사건들로 누리던 명성에서 사사로운 생활로 가라앉았는데, 이제 이 파산으로 그 어느 때보다 더 중요한 인물이 되었다. 이제 그가 앉아서, 옛 사무원들을 거의 모조리 빠르게 갈아치운 회계사들과 그 밖의 사람들의 낯선 얼굴을 지켜보던 바깥 사무실의 자기 받침대 자리에서 슬며시 빠져나와, 바깥 안마당에 모습을 드러내거나, 멀어 봤자 킹스 암스 술집의 바에 모습을 드러내기만 하면, 그는 무수한 질문을 받았는데, 그 가운데에는 그가 무엇을 마시겠느냐는 흥미로운 질문이 거의 틀림없이 끼어 있었다. 그러면 퍼치 씨는, 그와 퍼치 부인이 볼스 폰드 바깥의 집에서 \"일이 잘못되어 가고 있다\"고 처음 의심하기 시작했을 때 겪은 극심한 불안의 시간들을 장황하게 늘어놓곤 했다. 그러면 퍼치 씨는, 마치 고인이 된 회사의 시신이 옆방에 묻히지 못한 채 누워 있기라도 한 듯 낮은 목소리로, 입을 떡 벌리고 듣는 청중에게, 퍼치 부인이 일이 잘못되어 가고 있음을 처음 짐작하게 된 것은 그(퍼치)가 잠결에 \"파운드당 십이 실링 구 펜스, 파운드당 십이 실링 구 펜스!\"라고 신음하는 소리를 들었기 때문이라고 이야기하곤 했다. 그는 그 몽유병적 행동이, 돔비 씨의 얼굴에 일어난 변화가 자기에게 남긴 인상에서 비롯되었으리라고 짐작했다. 그러면 그는, 자기가 언젠가 \"감히 여쭙겠습니다만, 나리, 마음이 편치 않으신지요?\"라고 물었더니, 돔비 씨가 \"내 충직한 퍼치—아니, 그럴 수는 없네!\"라고 대답하고는, 이어서 손을 이마에 갖다 대며 \"나를 혼자 두게, 퍼치!\"라고 했다는 이야기를 들려주곤 했다. 그러면, 요컨대 퍼치 씨는, 자기 처지의 희생자로서, 온갖 거짓말을 늘어놓았다. 가슴 뭉클한 성질의 거짓말로는 스스로 눈물까지 흘렸고, 어제 지어낸 이야기가 되풀이되다 보니 오늘은 어딘가 진실 비슷한 것을 띠게 된다고 정말로 믿었다.\n\n퍼치 씨는 이런 회담을 늘 온순하게 한마디 덧붙이며 끝맺었으니, 물론 자기 의혹이 어떠했든 간에(마치 그가 무슨 의혹이라도 품은 적이 있었던 것처럼!) 자기가 맡은 신임을 저버리는 것은 자기 할 일이 아니지 않으냐는 것이었다. 그 정서는(채권자라곤 결코 한 사람도 그 자리에 없었으니) 그의 마음씨에 큰 영예가 되는 것으로 받아들여졌다. 그리하여 그는 대개 누그러진 양심을 안고 떠났으며, 자기 받침대 자리로 돌아갈 때면 흐뭇한 인상을 뒤에 남겼다. 그러고는 다시 앉아서, 그 큰 비밀인 장부를 그토록 함부로 다루는 회계사들과 그 밖의 사람들의 낯선 얼굴을 지켜보거나, 이따금 발끝으로 살금살금 돔비 씨의 텅 빈 방에 들어가 불을 쑤셔 돋우거나, 문간에 나가 바람을 쐬며 아는 떠돌이 누구하고든 조금 더 구슬픈 잡담을 나누거나, 혹은 갖가지 자잘한 친절로 수석 회계사의 비위를 맞추곤 했다. 퍼치 씨는 회사의 일이 청산되고 나면 그에게서 어느 화재보험회사 심부름꾼 자리를 얻을 수 있으리라 기대하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n배그스톡 소령에게 이 파산은 그야말로 하나의 재앙이었다. 소령은 동정심 많은 인물도 아니었고—그의 관심은 온통 J. B.에게만 쏠려 있었으니—숨을 헐떡이고 목이 막히는 신체적 측면이 아니고서는 격한 감정에 잘 휩쓸리는 사람도 아니었다. 그러나 그는 클럽에서 자기 친구 돔비를 어찌나 떠벌리고 다녔는지, 회원들 전체의 머리 위로 그를 어찌나 휘둘러 댔는지, 그의 부유함을 끊임없이 주장하며 그들을 어찌나 눌러 댔는지, 그러니 클럽도 인간인지라, 소령에게 되받아치는 것을 기뻐했다. 곧 대단히 걱정스러운 양하며, 이 엄청난 파국이 조금이라도 예상되었던 일이냐고, 그의 친구 돔비는 그것을 어떻게 견디고 있느냐고 물어 댄 것이다. 그런 질문에 소령은 얼굴이 시뻘게져서, 대체로 고약한 세상이라고, 조이가 한두 가지는 알지만 당했다고, 어린애처럼 당했다고 대답하곤 했다. 만일 이 일을 J. 배그스톡에게, 그가 돔비와 함께 외국으로 가서 그 부랑자를 프랑스 곳곳으로 쫓아다닐 때 예언했더라면, J. 배그스톡은 당신을 코웃음으로 일축했을 거라고, 맹세코, 당신을 코웃음으로 일축했을 거라고 했다! 조는 속았고, 넘어갔고, 눈가림을 당했고, 눈을 가린 채 끌려다녔지만, 이제는 다시 활짝 깨어 두 눈 부릅뜨고 있다고. 그러니 만일 조의 아버지가 내일 무덤에서 일어난다 해도, 그 늙은 협잡꾼에게 단돈 한 푼도 맡기지 않을 것이며, 도리어 그에게 당신 아들 조시는 다시 당하기엔 너무 노련한 군인이라고 말해 줄 거라고. 자기는 의심 많고, 까칠하고, 괴팍하고, 닳고 닳은, J. B. 불신자라고. 그리고 만일 켄트 공작과 요크 공작 두 분 전하께 친히 알현하여 칭찬을 들은 영예를 지닌, 구식의 거칠고 강인한 늙은 소령의 위엄에 어울리는 일이라면, 통 속에 들어앉아 그 안에서 살아도 좋다면, 천만에! 그는 인류를 향한 경멸을 보여 주려고 내일이라도 폴 몰 한복판에 통 하나를 갖다 놓겠노라고 했다!\n\n이 모든 것을, 그리고 같은 곡조의 무수한 변주를, 소령은 어찌나 많은 졸중(卒中) 증세를 보이며, 어찌나 머리를 휘저으며, 부당한 대우와 분노에 대해 어찌나 사납게 으르렁대며 쏟아 냈는지, 클럽의 젊은 회원들은 그가 친구 돔비의 회사에 돈을 투자했다가 잃은 것이라 짐작했다. 다만 조를 더 잘 아는 나이 든 군인들과 더 능구렁이 같은 자들은 그런 말을 들으려고도 하지 않았다. 그 불운한 토인은 아무런 의견도 내놓지 않은 채 끔찍하게 시달렸다. 하루도 빠짐없이 매시간 소령에게 규칙적으로 일제 사격을 당하여 구멍이 숭숭 뚫린 그의 도덕적 감정에서만이 아니라, 끊임없이 팽팽하게 시달려 온 신체적 타격과 부딪힘에 대한 그의 예민함에서도 그러했다. 파산 후 꼬박 여섯 주 동안, 이 가엾은 이방인은 구둣주걱과 솔이 빗발치는 우기(雨期) 속에서 살았다.\n\n칙 부인은 그 끔찍한 역경의 문제에 대해 세 가지 생각을 품고 있었다. 첫째는 자기로서는 그것을 도무지 이해할 수 없다는 것이었다. 둘째는 자기 오라비가 노력을 하지 않았다는 것이었다. 셋째는 만일 그 첫 번째 파티가 있던 날 자기가 만찬에 초대받았더라면 이런 일은 결코 일어나지 않았을 것이며, 자기가 그때 그렇게 말했었다는 것이었다.\n\n그 누구의 의견도 그 불행을 막지 못했고, 덜어 주지도, 더 무겁게 하지도 못했다. 회사의 일은 형편이 닿는 한 최선으로 청산될 것이며, 돔비 씨는 자기가 가진 모든 것을 선뜻 내놓고 누구에게도 아무런 호의를 청하지 않는다는 것이 알려졌다. 사업을 재개하는 일은 논외인데, 그가 그러한 타협을 염두에 둔 어떠한 우호적 협상에도 귀를 기울이려 하지 않기 때문이라는 것. 상인들 사이에서 존경받는 사람으로서 그가 지녔던 신임과 명예의 자리를 모두 내놓았다는 것. 어떤 이들의 말로는 그가 죽어 가고 있다는 것. 또 다른 이들의 말로는 그가 우울증으로 미쳐 가고 있다는 것. 모든 이의 말로는 그가 폐인이 된 사람이라는 것이었다.\n\n사무원들은 자기들끼리 조촐한 위로의 만찬을 가진 뒤에 흩어졌는데, 그 만찬은 익살스러운 노래로 흥겨워졌고 더없이 훌륭하게 끝났다. 더러는 외국에 자리를 얻었고, 더러는 본국의 다른 회사에 들어갔으며, 더러는 시골에 사는, 갑자기 각별한 애정이 떠오른 친척들을 찾아갔고, 더러는 신문에 구직 광고를 냈다. 그 옛 직원들 가운데 퍼치 씨만 홀로 남아, 받침대 자리에 앉아 회계사들을 바라보거나, 거기서 벌떡 일어나, 자기를 화재보험회사에 넣어 주기로 한 수석 회계사의 비위를 맞추러 가곤 했다. 회계 사무실은 이내 더럽고 방치된 모습이 되었다. 안마당 모퉁이의 슬리퍼와 개 목걸이 파는 주인장은, 이제 돔비 씨가 그곳에 나타난다 해도 모자챙에 집게손가락을 갖다 대는 것이 과연 온당한 일일지 의심했을 것이다. 그리고 흰 앞치마 밑에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 표 운반인은, 야망에 관하여 그럴듯하고 건전한 교훈을 늘어놓았으니, 야망(ambition)이 파멸(perdition)과 운(韻)이 맞게 되어 있는 것은, 그가 보기에, 다 까닭이 있어서라고 했다.\n\n개암빛 눈을 한 독신 남자로, 머리칼과 구레나룻에 희끗희끗 백발이 섞인 모핀 씨는, 어쩌면 그 회사의 분위기 안에서—물론 그 우두머리는 예외로 하고—그 회사에 닥친 재난으로 진심으로 깊이 마음 아파한 유일한 사람이었을 것이다. 그는 여러 해 동안 돔비 씨를 마땅한 존경과 경의로 대해 왔으나, 자기 본연의 성품을 감춘 적도, 비굴하게 그에게 아첨한 적도, 자기 목적의 출세를 위해 주인의 으뜸가는 정념에 영합한 적도 없었다. 그러므로 그에게는 복수해야 할 자기 모멸도 없었고, 빠르게 튕겨 나가며 풀어 줄, 오래 조여 둔 용수철도 없었다. 그는 회사의 거래 기록 가운데 복잡하거나 어려운 것은 무엇이든 풀어내려고 이른 아침부터 늦은 밤까지 일했다. 설명이 필요한 것은 무엇이든 설명하려고 늘 대기하고 있었고, 때로는 밤늦도록 자기 옛 방에 앉아, 자기가 통달함으로써 돔비 씨가 직접 거론되는 고통을 덜어 줄 수 있는 사항들을 연구했다. 그러고는 이즐링턴의 집으로 돌아가, 잠자리에 들기 전에 첼로에서 더없이 음울하고 처량한 소리를 뽑아내며 마음을 가라앉히곤 했다.\n\n어느 날 저녁 그는 이 가락 좋게 투덜대는 악기로 스스로를 달래고 있었는데, 그날의 일들로 무척 기운이 빠진 터라, 그 가장 낮은 음들에서 위안을 긁어내고 있었다. 그때 그의 하숙집 여주인이(다행히 귀가 먹어, 이런 연주에 대해 자기 뼛속에서 무언가 우르릉거리는 느낌 말고는 달리 의식하는 것이 없었다) 한 부인의 방문을 알렸다.\n\n\"상복을 입으셨어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n첼로가 즉시 멈췄다. 그리고 연주자는 그것을 더없이 부드럽고 조심스럽게 소파에 내려놓고는, 그 부인을 들여보내라는 신호를 했다. 그는 곧장 뒤따라 나갔고, 계단에서 해리엇 카커를 만났다.\n\n\"혼자시군요!\" 그가 말했다, \"오늘 아침에는 존이 여기 왔었는데! 무슨 일이라도 있는 거요, 아가씨? 아니, 그게 아니로군요,\" 그가 덧붙였다, \"당신 얼굴이 전혀 다른 이야기를 들려주는걸요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 거기서 보시는 건 이기적인 속내가 드러난 것일까 봐 걱정스럽네요.\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"그건 아주 유쾌한 속내인걸요,\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 설령 이기적이라 해도, 당신에게서는 보기 드문 새로운 것이니 볼 만한 가치가 있지요. 하지만 난 그렇게 믿지 않아요.\"\n\n이 무렵 그는 그녀에게 의자를 권해 두었고, 자기는 맞은편에 앉았다. 두 사람 사이의 소파에는 첼로가 아늑하게 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"제가 혼자 온 것이나, 존이 제가 온다고 선생님께 말씀드리지 않은 것이나, 놀라실 일이 아니에요,\" 해리엇이 말했다. \"제가 왜 왔는지 말씀드리면 그것도 이해하실 거예요. 지금 말씀드려도 될까요?\"\n\n\"그보다 더 나은 일을 하실 수는 없지요.\"\n\n\"바쁘진 않으셨어요?\"\n\n그는 소파에 놓인 첼로를 가리키며 말했다, \"하루 종일 바빴지요. 여기 내 증인이 있어요. 내 온갖 근심을 다 저것에게 털어놓았답니다. 나 자신의 근심 말고는 털어놓을 게 없으면 좋으련만.\"\n\n\"회사는 끝난 건가요?\" 해리엇이 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"완전히 끝났어요.\"\n\n\"결코 재개되지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"결코.\"\n\n그녀의 입술이 소리 없이 그 말을 되뇔 때에도, 그녀 얼굴의 밝은 표정에는 그늘이 드리우지 않았다. 그는 이를 다소 무심결에 살짝 놀라며 지켜보는 듯하다가, 다시 말했다.\n\n\"결코. 내가 당신에게 한 말을 기억하겠지요. 그를 납득시키는 일은 처음부터 줄곧 불가능했어요. 그와 도리를 따져 보는 일도 불가능했고, 때로는 그에게 다가가는 것조차 불가능했지요. 최악의 일이 일어났고, 회사는 무너졌으니, 다시는 일으켜 세워지지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그러면 돔비 씨는, 본인은 파멸한 건가요?\"\n\n\"파멸했어요.\"\n\n\"개인 재산은 하나도 남지 않을까요? 아무것도?\"\n\n그녀의 목소리에 담긴 어떤 간절함과, 거의 기쁨에 가까운 그녀의 눈빛이, 그를 점점 더 놀라게 하는 듯했다. 그를 실망시키기도 하고, 자기 자신의 감정과는 어긋나게 거슬리는 듯도 했다. 그는 한 손의 손가락으로 탁자를 두드리며, 안타깝게 그녀를 바라보고 고개를 저으며, 잠시 뒤에 말했다.\n\n\"돔비 씨가 가진 자산의 규모는 제가 정확히 알지 못해요. 하지만 그것이 분명 매우 크다 해도, 그의 채무는 어마어마하지요. 그는 높은 명예와 청렴을 지닌 신사예요. 그의 처지에 놓인 사람이라면 누구라도, 자기와 거래해 온 사람들의 손실을 아주 약간, 거의 느낄 수도 없을 만큼만 늘리는 조건을 내세움으로써 자기 자신을 구하고, 살아갈 만한 잔여 재산을 남겨 둘 수 있었을 거예요. 또 그의 처지에 놓인 많은 사람이 그렇게 했을 테고요. 하지만 그는 자기 재산이 닿는 한 마지막 한 푼까지 갚겠다고 결심했어요. 그의 말로는, 그것으로 회사를 청산하거나 거의 청산할 것이며, 누구도 큰 손해를 보지 않으리라는 거예요. 아, 해리엇 양, 악덕이라는 것이 때로는 지나치게 밀어붙인 미덕에 지나지 않는다는 것을, 우리가 지금보다 더 자주 떠올린다 해도 우리에게 해될 것은 없을 거예요! 그의 자존심이 이 일에서는 훌륭하게 드러나는군요.\"\n\n그녀는 표정에 거의, 아니 전혀 변화 없이, 그리고 자기 마음속의 무언가에 골몰해 있음을 보여 주는 분산된 주의로 그의 말을 들었다. 그가 입을 다물자, 그녀가 황급히 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"그분을 요즘 뵌 적 있으세요?\"\n\n\"아무도 그를 보지 못해요. 그의 사정에 닥친 이번 위기로 인해 그가 집 밖으로 나와야 할 필요가 있으면, 그는 그 일을 위해 나왔다가 다시 집으로 돌아가 틀어박혀, 아무도 만나려 하지 않아요. 그가 내게 편지를 한 통 보냈는데, 우리의 지난 인연을 그것이 받을 만한 것보다 더 높이 인정하면서, 내게 작별을 고하더군요. 형편이 더 나았던 시절에도 그와 그리 많이 왕래하지 못했던 처지라, 이제 와서 그에게 불쑥 끼어드는 것이 조심스럽지만, 그래도 그렇게 해 보려고 애썼어요. 편지도 쓰고, 거기 찾아가기도 하고, 간청도 했지요. 다 헛수고였어요.\"\n\n그는 그녀가 지금껏 보인 것보다 더 큰 관심을 무엇으로든 보여 주기를 바라는 듯 그녀를 지켜보았다. 그리고 그녀에게 더 깊은 인상을 주려는 듯 엄숙하고 절절하게 말했다. 그러나 그녀에게는 아무런 변화가 없었다.\n\n\"자, 자, 해리엇 양,\" 그가 실망한 기색으로 말했다, \"이건 본론이 아니로군요. 당신은 이런 이야기를 들으러 온 게 아니지요. 무언가 다른, 더 즐거운 주제가 당신 마음속에 있군요. 그걸 내 마음속에도 있게 해 줘요, 그러면 우리가 좀 더 대등하게 이야기를 나눌 수 있을 테니. 자!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 같은 주제예요,\" 해리엇이 솔직하고 빠르게 놀라며 대답했다. \"그게 같은 주제인 것이 당연하지 않겠어요? 존과 제가 요즘 이 큰 변화에 대해 무척 많이 생각하고 이야기한 것이 자연스러운 일 아닌가요? 그분이 그토록 여러 해 섬긴 돔비 씨가—어떤 처지에서였는지는 아시잖아요—선생님이 말씀하신 대로 영락하셨는데, 우리는 이렇게 부유하니!\"\n\n그녀의 얼굴은, 비록 그토록 선량하고 진실한 얼굴이었고, 또 개암빛 눈을 한 독신 남자 모핀 씨가 그것을 처음 본 그 순간부터 그에게 그토록 흐뭇한 얼굴이었지만, 환희의 빛으로 밝혀진 그 순간에는, 그 어느 때보다도 그를 덜 흐뭇하게 했다.\n\n\"우리의 형편이 어떤 연유로 바뀌었는지 새삼 일깨워 드릴 필요는 없겠지요,\" 해리엇이 자기 검은 옷으로 눈길을 떨구며 말했다. \"우리 오빠 제임스가, 그 끔찍한 날, 유언장도 남기지 않고, 우리 말고는 아무 친척도 남기지 않았다는 것을 선생님은 잊지 않으셨을 거예요.\"\n\n이제 그 얼굴은, 비록 창백하고 우울했지만, 조금 전보다 그에게 더 흐뭇했다. 그는 한결 명랑하게 숨을 쉬는 듯했다.\n\n\"선생님은 아시잖아요,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"우리 집안의 내력을, 선생님이 그토록 진실하게 말씀하신 그 불운하고 불행한 신사분과 얽힌 제 두 오빠의 내력을요. 선생님은 우리—존과 제가—얼마나 바라는 것이 적은지, 그리고 우리가 그토록 여러 해를 함께 살아온 끝에 돈이 얼마나 쓸데가 없는지, 그리고 이제 선생님의 친절 덕분에 오빠가 우리에게 넉넉한 수입을 벌고 있다는 것을 아시지요. 제가 선생님께 청하러 온 부탁이 무엇인지 들으실 마음의 준비가 안 되신 건 아니지요?\"\n\n\"잘 모르겠어요. 일 분 전만 해도 그랬는데, 지금은, 준비가 안 된 것 같군요.\"\n\n\"세상을 떠난 오빠에 대해서는 아무 말 않겠어요. 죽은 이들이 우리가 하는 일을 안다면—하지만 제 말을 아시겠지요. 살아 있는 오빠에 대해서라면 많은 것을 말할 수 있겠지만, 제가 선생님의 없어서는 안 될 도움을 청하러 온 이 의무의 행위가 바로 오빠 자신의 것이며, 그것이 이루어지기까지 오빠가 마음 놓을 수 없으리라는 것 말고 무슨 말을 더 해야겠어요!\"\n\n그녀는 다시 눈을 들었다. 그러자 그녀를 지켜보던 그 관찰력 있는 눈에는, 그녀 얼굴의 환희의 빛이 아름다워 보이기 시작했다.\n\n\"선생님,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다, \"이 일은 아주 조용히, 비밀스럽게 이루어져야 해요. 선생님의 경험과 지식이 그것을 해낼 방도를 일러 주겠지요. 어쩌면 돔비 씨가, 그것이 자기 재산의 난파 속에서 뜻밖에 건져 낸 무엇이라고 믿게 될 수도 있고, 혹은 그가 크게 거래해 온 사람들 가운데 누군가가 그의 명예롭고 올곧은 인품에 바치는 자발적인 헌사라고, 혹은 잊혀 있던 오래된 빚을 갚는 것이라고 믿게 될 수도 있겠지요. 그것을 해낼 방도는 많을 거예요. 선생님이 가장 좋은 방도를 고르시리라는 걸 알아요. 제가 청하러 온 부탁은, 선생님이 그 친절하고 너그럽고 사려 깊은 방식으로 우리를 위해 그것을 해 주십사 하는 거예요. 존에게는 결코 그 일을 말씀하지 마시고요. 이 보상의 행위에서 오빠가 누리는 가장 큰 행복은, 그것을 비밀스럽게, 알려지지 않고, 인정받지 않은 채 하는 것이니까요. 또 상속 재산 가운데 아주 작은 부분만 우리에게 떼어 두시고, 돔비 씨가 살아가는 동안 나머지의 이자를 평생토록 받도록 해 주세요. 우리의 비밀을 충실히 지켜 주시고요—하지만 그러시리라는 걸 확신해요. 그리고 이제부터는, 선생님과 저 사이에서조차 이 일이 좀처럼 입에 오르지 않고, 다만 제 생각 속에서, 하늘에 감사할 새로운 까닭으로, 그리고 오빠에 대한 기쁨과 자랑으로만 살아 있게 해 주세요.\"\n\n의로운 사람 아흔아홉 명 가운데 회개한 죄인 하나가 천국에 들어설 때, 천사들의 얼굴에 떠오르는 환희의 빛이 바로 그러할 것이다. 그것은 그녀의 눈에 가득 차오른 기쁨의 눈물로도 흐려지거나 빛바래지 않았고, 오히려 그 눈물로 인해 더욱 밝게 빛났다.\n\n\"해리엇 양,\" 모핀 씨가 한동안 잠잠히 있다가 말했다, \"이건 미처 예상치 못한 일이군요. 내가 제대로 이해한 거라면, 상속 재산에서 당신 자신의 몫도, 존의 몫과 마찬가지로, 당신의 그 선한 목적에 쓸 수 있도록 하고 싶다는 거지요?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"우리가 그토록 오랜 세월 모든 것을 함께 나누어 왔고, 따로 떨어진 근심도, 희망도, 목적도 없었는데, 제가 이 일에서 제 몫에서 제외되는 것을 어찌 견딜 수 있겠어요? 끝까지 오빠의 동반자요 동무가 되겠다는 주장을 제가 내세우면 안 되는 건가요?\"\n\n\"내가 그것을 두고 왈가왈부하다니, 천만의 말씀이에요!\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"선생님의 다정한 도움에 기대도 될까요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래도 된다는 걸 알았어요!\"\n\n\"내가 만일 마음과 영혼을 다해 당신에게 그 다짐을 드릴 수 없다면, 나는—내가 그러기를 바라는, 혹은 기꺼이 그렇다고 믿고 싶은 사람보다—더 못난 사람일 거예요. 당신은 전적으로 기대도 좋아요. 내 명예를 걸고, 당신의 비밀을 지키겠어요. 그리고 만일 돔비 씨가, 영향을 줄 방도라곤 없어 보이는 어떤 결심에 따라 행동하여, 내가 우려하는 만큼 영락한 것으로 드러난다면, 당신과 존이 함께 결심한 그 계획을 이루도록 내가 돕겠어요.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀고, 진심 어린 행복한 얼굴로 그에게 감사했다.\n\n\"해리엇,\" 그가 그 손을 자기 손에 잡아 둔 채 말했다. \"당신이 이제 할 수 있는 어떤 희생이든—무엇보다도 한낱 돈의 어떤 희생이든—그 가치에 대해 당신에게 말한다는 것은 부질없고 주제넘은 일일 거예요. 당신에게 그 목적을 다시 생각해 보라거나 거기에 좁은 한계를 두라고 호소하는 것은, 내 생각에, 그에 못지않게 그러하겠지요. 나의 보잘것없는 자아를 들이밀어, 위대한 내력의 위대한 결말을 망칠 권리는 내게 없어요. 당신이 내게 맡기는 것 앞에 고개를 숙일 권리는 온전히 내게 있지요. 그것이 내 빈약한 세속의 지식보다 더 높고 더 선한 영감의 원천에서 비롯된 것임을 확신하니까요. 단지 이 말만 하겠어요. 나는 당신의 충실한 집사예요. 그리고 나는 당신 자신을 빼고는, 세상의 그 누가 되기보다, 차라리 당신의 그 집사이자 당신이 택한 벗이 되고 싶어요.\"\n\n그녀는 다시 진심으로 그에게 감사하고, 그에게 잘 자라는 인사를 했다.\n\n\"집에 가시는 거요?\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 함께 가지요.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤은 안 돼요. 지금은 집에 가는 게 아니에요. 혼자 다녀와야 할 방문이 있거든요. 내일 와 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래,\" 그가 말했다, \"내일 가지요. 그동안 이 일을, 그리고 우리가 어떻게 하는 것이 가장 좋을지를 생각해 보겠어요. 그리고 어쩌면 그것을, 사랑하는 해리엇, 그것을 생각하면서—그리고—그리고 그것과 더불어 나도 조금은 생각해 주시겠지요.\"\n\n그는 그녀를 문간에 대기시켜 둔 마차까지 부축해 내려보냈다. 그리고 만일 그의 하숙집 여주인이 귀가 먹지 않았더라면, 마차가 떠난 뒤 그가 다시 위층으로 올라가며, 우리란 습관의 피조물이며, 늙은 독신으로 산다는 것은 서글픈 습관이라고 중얼거리는 소리를 들었을 것이다.\n\n두 의자 사이 소파에 첼로가 놓여 있었는데, 그는 빈 의자를 치워 두지도 않은 채 그것을 집어 들고, 앉아서 거기에 대고 웅웅거리며, 빈 의자를 향해 천천히 고개를 저으면서, 오래오래 그렇게 있었다. 그가 처음에 그 악기에 실어 보낸 표정은, 비록 끔찍하게 애처롭고 부드러웠지만, 그가 자기 자신의 얼굴에 실어 그 빈 의자에 베푼 표정에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그 표정이 어찌나 진실했던지, 그는 한 번 이상 커틀 선장의 처방에 의지하여, 소맷자락으로 얼굴을 문질러야 했다. 그러나 차츰, 첼로는 그 자신의 심경과 어우러져, 가락 좋게 미끄러져 「조화로운 대장장이」로 들어갔고, 그는 그것을 거듭거듭 연주하다가, 마침내 혈색 좋고 평온한 그의 얼굴이 진짜 대장장이의 모루 위에 놓인 진짜 쇠붙이처럼 번득였다. 요컨대, 첼로와 그 빈 의자는 자정 무렵까지 그의 독신 생활의 동무가 되어 주었다. 그리고 그가 저녁을 들 때, 소파 구석에 곧추세워진 첼로는, 조화로운 대장장이들로 가득 찬 한 주물 공장의 잠재된 화음을 잔뜩 머금은 채, 그 비뚜름한 눈으로 빈 의자를 향해, 이루 말할 수 없는 총기로 추파를 던지는 듯했다.\n\n해리엇이 그 집을 떠나자, 그녀가 빌린 마차의 마부는, 그에게는 분명 새로울 것 없는 길을 잡아, 그 교외 지역을 이리저리 샛길로 누비다가, 정원들 사이에 자그마하고 조용한 옛집 몇 채가 서 있는 어느 빈터에 이르렀다. 그 가운데 한 집의 정원 문 앞에서 마부가 멈추자, 해리엇이 내렸다.\n\n그녀가 종을 부드럽게 울리자, 살빛이 희고 눈썹을 치켜올린 채 머리를 한쪽으로 떨군, 비통해 보이는 여인이 응답했다. 여인은 그녀를 보자 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는, 정원을 가로질러 집으로 그녀를 안내했다.\n\n\"간호인, 오늘 밤 환자분은 좀 어떤가요?\" 해리엇이 말했다.\n\n\"형편이 안 좋답니다, 아가씨, 그게 걱정이에요. 아, 저 사람을 보면 가끔 어쩜 그리도 우리 외삼촌네 베치 제인이 떠오르는지!\" 살빛 흰 여인이 일종의 구슬픈 황홀경에 잠겨 대답했다.\n\n\"어떤 점에서요?\" 해리엇이 물었다.\n\n\"아가씨, 모든 점에서요,\" 상대가 대답했다, \"다만 저 사람은 다 큰 어른이고, 베치 제인은 죽음의 문턱에 이르렀을 때 아직 어린애였다는 것만 빼고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 애가 회복되었다고 하지 않았나요,\" 해리엇이 부드럽게 말했다, \"그러니 더더욱 희망을 가질 까닭이 있는 거지요, 위컴 부인.\"\n\n\"아, 아가씨, 희망이란 그것을 견딜 만한 기운을 가진 이들에게나 좋은 것이지요!\" 위컴 부인이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"제 기운은 그만 못하답니다만, 그렇다고 제가 그걸 원망하는 건 아니에요. 그렇게 복받은 이들을 부러워할 뿐이지요!\"\n\n\"좀 더 명랑해지려고 애써 보셔야지요.\" 해리엇이 일렀다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 아가씨, 정말요,\" 위컴 부인이 침울하게 말했다. \"제가 설령 그러고 싶은 마음이 든다 해도, 이런 처지의 외로움이—이렇게 거리낌 없이 말씀드리는 걸 용서하세요—스물네 시간 안에 그럴 힘을 빼앗아 갈 거예요. 하지만 저는 그럴 마음이 전혀 없답니다. 차라리 안 그러는 게 낫지요. 제가 그나마 가지고 있던 쥐꼬리만 한 기운마저, 몇 해 전에 브라이튼에서 잃어버렸는데, 저는 그게 오히려 제게 나은 것 같아요.\"\n\n사실 이 사람은 바로, 어린 폴의 유모로 리처즈 부인을 대신했던 그 위컴 부인으로, 사람 좋은 핍친 부인의 지붕 아래서 바로 그 잃어버림을 얻었다고 스스로 여기던 이였다. 오래도록 관습으로 떠받들어진, 그 훌륭하고 사려 깊은 옛 제도—나머지 인류 가운데에서 손에 넣을 수 있는 가장 음울하고 마뜩잖은 사람들을 으레 골라내어, 젊은이를 가르치는 교사로, 미덕을 가리키는 이정표로, 사감으로, 감독으로, 병상의 간병인으로, 그리고 그 비슷한 일들을 맡게 하는 제도—는, 위컴 부인을 간호인으로서 아주 잘되는 일에 자리 잡게 해 주었고, 그녀의 진중한 자질이 그녀를 우러르는 수많은 연줄로부터 각별히 칭송받도록 이끌었다.\n\n위컴 부인은, 눈썹을 치켜올리고 머리를 한쪽으로 기울인 채, 깨끗하고 정갈한 방으로 이어지는 위층 길을 밝히며 올라갔다. 그 방은 침대가 놓인, 희미하게 불 밝힌 또 다른 방으로 통해 있었다. 첫 번째 방에서는 한 노파가 열린 창밖의 어둠을 기계적으로 응시하며 앉아 있었다. 두 번째 방에서는, 침대 위에 길게 누운 채, 어느 겨울밤 비바람을 마다하고 떨쳐 냈던 한 형체의 그림자가 누워 있었다. 이제는 알아보기 어려웠으니, 다만 핏기 없는 얼굴과 그 주위의 온통 흰 것들에 대비되어 그토록 새카맣게 두드러지는 긴 검은 머리칼로나 알아볼 수 있을 따름이었다.\n\n오, 그 강한 눈과, 그 쇠약한 몸뚱이! 해리엇이 들어왔을 때 그토록 간절하고 환하게 문 쪽으로 돌아간 그 눈, 스스로 들어 올리지도 못한 채 베개 위에서 그토록 느릿하게 돌아간 그 가냘픈 머리!\n\n\"앨리스!\" 방문객의 부드러운 목소리가 말했다, \"내가 오늘 밤은 늦었나요?\"\n\n\"늘 늦은 것 같지만, 늘 일찍 오시지요.\"\n\n해리엇은 이제 침대 곁에 앉아, 거기 놓인 야윈 손 위에 자기 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"좀 나아졌어요?\"\n\n침대 발치에 서 있던 위컴 부인은, 비통한 망령처럼, 이 견해를 부정하느라 단호하고도 힘차게 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그런 건 아무래도 상관없어요!\" 앨리스가 희미하게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"오늘 나아지든 나빠지든, 그래 봤자 하루 차이일 뿐인걸요—어쩌면 그만큼도 안 되고요.\"\n\n위컴 부인은, 진중한 인물답게, 신음 소리로 동의를 표했다. 그러고는 환자의 발을 더듬어 차디찬 돌처럼 굳어 있기를 기대하기라도 하듯, 이불 밑자락을 몇 번 싸늘하게 두드려 보고는, 마치 \"이왕 여기 와 있는 김에, 전에 했던 대로 약을 다시 한 번 먹입시다\"라고 말하기라도 하는 양, 탁자 위의 약병들 사이에서 쩔그렁거리며 다녔다.\n\n\"아니에요,\" 앨리스가 방문객에게 속삭였다, \"사악한 행실과, 회한과, 떠돌이 생활과, 궁핍과, 모진 날씨와, 안의 폭풍과 밖의 폭풍이, 제 목숨을 닳아 없애 버렸어요. 그리 오래가지는 못할 거예요.\"\n\n그녀는 말하면서 그 손을 끌어 올려, 거기에 자기 얼굴을 갖다 대었다.\n\n\"제가 여기 누워서, 이따금 이런 생각을 해요. 제가 얼마나 고마워할 수 있는지 아가씨에게 보여 드릴 만큼만이라도 시간을 가질 때까지 살았으면 좋겠다고요! 그건 약한 마음이고, 이내 지나가 버려요. 이대로가 아가씨에게는 더 나아요. 제게도 더 낫고요!\"\n\n황량한 겨울 저녁, 난롯가에서 그 손을 잡았을 때와는 그녀가 그 손을 붙잡은 모습이 어찌나 다른지! 경멸, 분노, 반항, 무모함이여, 여기를 보라! 이것이 그 끝이다.\n\n약병들 사이에서 충분히 쩔그렁거린 위컴 부인이, 이제 약을 내왔다. 위컴 부인은 환자가 약을 마시는 동안 그녀를 빤히 쏘아보고는, 입을 앙다물고, 눈썹도 함께 찡그리며, 고개를 저었으니, 고문을 당해도 자기 입으로 가망 없는 경우라고는 말하지 않겠다는 뜻이었다. 그런 다음 위컴 부인은, 재 위에 재를, 흙 위에 흙을 흩뿌리는 여자 무덤지기 같은 태도로—그녀는 진중한 인물이었으니까—방 안에 식혀 주는 약을 조금 뿌리고는, 아래층에서 무슨 장례식 구운 고기라도 들러 물러갔다.\n\n\"제가 아가씨를 찾아가, 제가 한 일을 말씀드린 게,\" 앨리스가 물었다, \"그리고 이제는 누구도 뒤쫓기엔 너무 늦었다는 말씀을 아가씨가 들으신 게, 얼마나 되었지요?\"\n\n\"일 년하고도 더 되었어요.\" 해리엇이 말했다.\n\n\"일 년하고도 더,\" 앨리스가 그녀의 얼굴을 깊이 응시하며 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"아가씨가 저를 이리로 데려온 지도 여러 달이 지났군요!\"\n\n해리엇이 \"그래요\"라고 대답했다.\n\n\"저를 이리로 데려오셨지요, 부드러움과 친절의 힘으로요. 이런 저를요!\" 앨리스가 손 뒤로 얼굴을 움츠리며 말했다, \"그리고 여인의 눈길과 말로, 천사의 행실로, 저를 사람답게 만들어 주셨어요!\"\n\n해리엇은 그녀에게 몸을 굽혀, 그녀를 가라앉히고 달랬다. 이윽고 앨리스는 아까처럼 손을 얼굴에 댄 채 누워, 자기 어머니를 불러 달라고 청했다.\n\n해리엇이 한 번 이상 그녀를 불렀으나, 노파는 열린 창밖의 어둠을 내다보는 데 어찌나 골몰해 있던지 듣지 못했다. 해리엇이 그녀에게 가서 그녀를 건드리고 나서야, 그녀는 일어나 다가왔다.\n\n\"어머니,\" 앨리스가 그 손을 다시 잡고, 자기의 빛나는 눈을 다정스레 방문객에게 고정한 채, 노파에게는 그저 손가락으로 까딱 동작만 보내며 말했다, \"어머니가 아시는 걸 이분께 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤에, 우리 아가?\"\n\n\"네, 어머니,\" 앨리스가 희미하고 엄숙하게 대답했다, \"오늘 밤에요!\"\n\n경악과 회한과 비탄으로 정신이 어지러워진 듯한 노파는, 해리엇이 앉은 쪽의 맞은편으로, 침대 곁을 따라 기어 왔다. 그러고는 무릎을 꿇어, 시든 얼굴을 이불보와 같은 높이로 가져오고, 손을 뻗어 딸의 팔에 닿게 하고는, 입을 열었다.\n\n\"내 어여쁜 딸아—\"\n\n오 하느님, 그녀가 거기서 말을 멈추고, 침대에 누운 그 가엾은 형체를 응시하며 내지른 그 비명이라니!\n\n\"오래전에 변해 버렸어요, 어머니! 오래전에 시들어 버렸고요,\" 앨리스가 그녀를 쳐다보지도 않은 채 말했다. \"이제 와서 그 일로 슬퍼하지 마세요.\"\n\n\"—내 딸,\" 노파가 더듬더듬 말했다, \"곧 나아져서, 그 고운 얼굴로 그치들을 다 부끄럽게 만들 내 딸.\"\n\n앨리스는 해리엇을 향해 구슬프게 미소 짓고, 그녀의 손을 조금 더 가까이 어루만졌으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"곧 나아질 거라니까, 내 말은,\" 노파가 쪼그라든 주먹으로 텅 빈 허공을 위협하며 되풀이했다, \"그리고 그 고운 얼굴로 그치들을 다 부끄럽게 만들 거야—그럴 거야. 내 말은 그럴 거라니까! 그래야 해!\"—마치 그녀에게 반박하는, 침대 곁의 보이지 않는 어떤 적수와 격렬하게 다투기라도 하는 것처럼—\"내 딸은 외면당하고 내쫓겼지만, 마음만 먹으면 콧대 높은 양반들과의 친척 관계도 내세울 수 있다고. 아! 콧대 높은 양반들과 말이야! 당신네 목사도 결혼반지도 없는 친척 관계라는 게 있는 법이야—그치들이 그걸 맺을 수는 있어도, 끊을 수는 없지—그리고 내 딸은 좋은 집안과 닿아 있다고. 돔비 부인을 내게 보여 봐, 그러면 내 앨리스의 사촌을 보여 줄 테니.\"\n\n해리엇은 노파에게서 자기 얼굴을 골똘히 응시하는 그 빛나는 눈으로 시선을 옮겼고, 거기서 확증을 얻었다.\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 노파가, 끄덕이는 머리를 섬뜩한 허영으로 빳빳이 세우며 외쳤다. \"내가 지금은 늙고 추하지만,—세월보다는 험한 삶과 버릇 탓에 훨씬 더 늙었지만,—나도 한때는 누구 못지않게 젊었다고. 아! 누구 못지않게 예쁘기도 했지! 나도 한창때는 싱싱한 시골 처녀였단다, 아가,\" 침대 너머로 해리엇에게 팔을 뻗으며, \"그렇게 보이기도 했고. 우리 시골 동네로 말이야, 돔비 부인의 아버지하고 그 형제분이, 런던에서 놀러 오던 신사들 가운데 제일 명랑하고 제일 인기 있는 분들이었지—하기야 두 분 다 돌아가신 지 오래됐지만! 어이구, 어이구, 정말 오래됐어! 우리 앨리의 아비였던 그 형제분이, 둘 중에 제일 오래 사셨지.\"\n\n그녀는 머리를 조금 쳐들고, 딸의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 마치 제 젊은 날의 기억에서, 제 자식의 기억으로 날아가 버린 듯이. 그러더니 갑자기, 그녀는 얼굴을 침대에 파묻고, 두 손과 두 팔로 머리를 감싸 가렸다.\n\n\"두 분이 어찌나 닮았던지,\" 노파가 고개도 들지 않은 채 말했다, \"나이 차이가 얼마 안 나는 형제 둘을 보고 그만큼 닮았다 할 만큼이었지—내 기억으로는 둘 사이가 한 살 남짓밖에 차이가 안 났어—그리고 만일 내 딸을, 내가 언젠가 한 번 본 것처럼, 그 다른 분의 딸과 나란히 세워 놓고 보았더라면, 옷차림과 살아온 처지가 아무리 달라도, 둘이 서로 닮았다는 걸 보았을 거야. 오! 그 닮음은 사라져 버리고, 이제 내 딸이—오직 내 딸만이—이렇게 변해 가야 한단 말인가!\"\n\n\"우리 모두 변할 거예요, 어머니, 차례차례,\" 앨리스가 말했다.\n\n\"차례라고!\" 노파가 외쳤다, \"한데 어째서 내 딸만큼 빨리 그 여자의 차례는 안 오느냔 말이야! 그 어미도 변했을 테지—그 여자도 나만큼 늙어 보였고, 분칠을 했어도 주름이 자글자글했으니까—하지만 그래도 그 여자는 곱더라. 내가, 이 내가, 그 여자보다 뭘 더 못한 짓을 했길래, 내 딸만 저기 누워 시들어 가야 한단 말이냐!\"\n\n그녀는 또 한 번 그 거친 비명을 지르며, 자기가 나왔던 방으로 달려 들어갔다. 그러나 이내, 그 변덕스러운 기분에, 되돌아와서는 해리엇에게로 슬금슬금 다가와 말했다.\n\n\"저게 바로 앨리스가 아가씨께 전하라던 거예요, 아가. 그게 다예요. 내가 어느 여름철에, 저 멀리 워릭셔에서, 그 여자가 누군지, 그 여자에 관한 온갖 걸 캐묻기 시작했을 때 알아낸 거지요. 그런 친척이라야 그때 내겐 아무 쓸모가 없었어요. 그치들은 나를 친척으로 인정하지도 않았을 테고, 내게 줄 것도 없었으니까. 우리 앨리스만 아니었으면, 나중에 어쩌면 돈이나 좀 달라고 그치들에게 청했을지도 몰라요. 만일 내가 그랬더라면, 우리 애가 나를 거의 죽이려 들었을 거예요, 내 생각엔. 그 애도 제 나름으로는 그 여자만큼이나 콧대가 높았지요,\" 노파가 두려운 듯 딸의 얼굴을 만지고는 손을 거두며 말했다, \"지금은 저렇게 얌전하지만 말이에요. 하지만 그래도 그 고운 얼굴로 그치들을 부끄럽게 만들 거예요. 하하! 부끄럽게 만들고말고, 내 어여쁜 딸이!\"\n\n그녀가 물러나며 짓는 웃음은, 그녀의 비명보다 더 끔찍했다. 그 웃음이 끝내 잦아들며 터져 나온 백치 같은 비탄의 절규보다도 더 끔찍했고, 그녀가 제 옛 자리에 주저앉아 어둠을 응시하던 그 노망 든 기색보다도 더 끔찍했다.\n\n앨리스의 눈은 이 내내 해리엇에게 고정되어 있었고, 그녀는 그 손을 한 번도 놓지 않았다. 이제 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"여기 누워서, 아가씨가 이걸 아셨으면 좋겠다고 느꼈어요. 어쩌면 이게, 저를 모질게 만드는 데 일조하곤 했던 무언가를 설명해 줄지도 모른다고 생각했거든요. 제가 나쁜 짓을 하는 동안, 제 저버린 의무에 대한 말을 어찌나 많이 들었던지, 저는 오히려 제게 마땅한 의무가 다해지지 않았다는, 그리고 씨를 뿌린 대로 거둔 거라는 믿음을 갖게 되었어요. 어찌어찌 저는 이렇게 생각하게 됐지요. 귀부인들도 나쁜 가정과 나쁜 어머니를 두면 자기들 식으로 그릇된 길에 빠지지만, 다만 그들의 길은 제 길만큼 더럽지 않으며, 그러니 그들은 그것을 하느님께 감사해야 한다고요. 그건 다 지나간 일이에요. 이제는 그게, 제가 잘 기억하거나 이해할 수 없는 꿈처럼 느껴져요. 아가씨가 여기 앉아 제게 글을 읽어 주기 시작한 뒤로, 날마다 점점 더 꿈처럼 되어 갔어요. 저는 그저 제가 기억할 수 있는 대로 그걸 말씀드리는 거예요. 제게 조금만 더 읽어 주시겠어요?\"\n\n해리엇이 책을 펴려고 손을 빼려는데, 앨리스가 잠시 그 손을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"제 어머니를 잊지 않으실 거지요? 제게 무슨 원망할 까닭이 있다 해도, 저는 어머니를 용서해요. 어머니가 저를 용서하시고, 마음으로 미안해하신다는 걸 저는 알아요. 어머니를 잊지 않으실 거지요?\"\n\"결코 잊지 않을게요, 앨리스!\"\n\n\"잠깐만요. 제가 읽어 주시는 동안 아가씨의 그 다정한 얼굴 속에서 그 말씀을 볼 수 있게, 머리를 이렇게 두어 주세요, 아가씨.\"\n\n해리엇이 그대로 따라, 읽었다—지치고 무거운 짐을 진 모든 이를 위한, 이 땅의 모든 비참한 이, 타락한 이, 버림받은 이를 위한, 그 영원한 책을 읽었다—앞 못 보고, 다리 절고, 중풍 든 거지도, 죄인도, 수치로 더럽혀진 여인도, 우리네 고운 흙덩이들 모두에게 따돌림당하는 이도, 저마다 한 몫을 지니고 있어, 이 세상이 지속되는 모든 세월 동안 인간의 그 어떤 오만도, 무관심도, 궤변도 그것을 앗아 가거나 한 톨의 천분의 일만큼도 줄일 수 없는, 그 복된 이야기를 읽었다—인간 삶의 전 과정과, 그 모든 희망과 슬픔을 두루, 태어남에서 죽음까지, 갓난아이 적부터 노년까지, 그 삶의 모든 장면과 단계에, 그 모든 고통과 비애에 다정한 연민과 관심을 품으셨던 그분의 사역을 읽었다.\n\n\"내일 아침,\" 해리엇이 책을 덮으며 말했다, \"아주 일찍 올게요.\"\n\n여전히 그녀의 얼굴에 고정되어 있던 그 빛나는 눈이, 잠시 감겼다가 다시 떠졌다. 그리고 앨리스는 그녀에게 입 맞추고 그녀를 축복했다.\n\n그 같은 눈이 문간까지 그녀를 따라갔다. 그리고 그 눈빛 속에, 그리고 그 평온한 얼굴 위에, 문이 닫혔을 때 미소가 어려 있었다.\n\n그 눈은 결코 돌려지지 않았다. 그녀는 한 손을 가슴에 얹고, 자기에게 읽어 준 그 거룩한 이름을 중얼거렸다. 그리고 생명이 그녀의 얼굴에서, 거두어진 빛처럼 떠나갔다.\n\n이제 거기에 남은 것이라곤, 비가 두드려 댄 그 죽을 운명의 집의 잔해와, 겨울바람에 나부끼던 그 검은 머리칼뿐이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER LIX. Retribution\n\n길고 음울한 거리의 그 큰 저택에 다시금 변화가 찾아왔다. 한때 플로렌스의 어린 시절과 외로움이 깃든 무대였던 그곳. 그것은 여전히 큰 저택이었고, 바람과 비를 막아 내었으며, 지붕에 구멍이 뚫리지도, 창문이 깨지지도, 벽이 허물어지지도 않았다. 그러나 그것은 그럼에도 폐허였고, 쥐들이 그곳에서 달아났다.\n\n타울린슨 씨와 그 일행은 처음에는 자기들이 듣는 그 형체 없는 소문을 도무지 믿으려 하지 않았다. 요리사는 우리 댁의 신용이 그깟 일로 그리 쉽게 흔들릴 만한 게 아니지, 감사하게도, 라고 말했고, 타울린슨 씨는 다음번에는 잉글랜드 은행이 무너진다든가 런던탑의 보석이 경매에 넘어간다든가 하는 소리를 듣게 될 것 같다고 했다. 그러나 다음에 관보가 나왔고, 퍼치 씨가 왔다. 퍼치 씨는 부엌에서 그 일을 함께 이야기하고 즐거운 저녁을 보내려고 퍼치 부인을 데려왔다.\n\n그 일에 더 이상 의심의 여지가 없게 되자, 타울린슨 씨의 가장 큰 관심사는 그 파산이 그럴듯하게 큰 규모이기를—적어도 십만 파운드는 되기를—바라는 것이었다. 퍼치 씨는 십만 파운드로도 그것을 거의 다 메우지 못하리라 생각했다. 퍼치 부인과 요리사를 필두로 한 여인들은 \"시-십만 파-운드!\"를 끔찍한 만족감과 함께 자주 되뇌었다—마치 그 말을 다루는 것이 그 돈을 다루는 것과 같기라도 한 듯이. 그리고 타울린슨 씨에게 눈독을 들이고 있는 하녀는 그 금액의 백분의 일이라도 자기가 골라잡은 사내에게 줄 수 있었으면 하고 바랐다. 타울린슨 씨는 여전히 자기가 당한 옛 원한을 잊지 못하고, 외국인 같으면 그렇게 많은 돈을 가지고 도대체 무얼 할 줄 알겠느냐, 구레나룻에다 쏟아붓는 거나 빼면 말이지, 라고 한마디 했는데, 그 신랄한 비꼼에 하녀는 눈물을 흘리며 자리를 떴다.\n\n그러나 오래 자리를 비우지는 않았다. 마음씨가 무척 좋다는 평판을 듣는 요리사가, 무슨 일을 하든 이제는 서로 곁을 지켜야지, 타울린슨 씨, 우리가 얼마나 빨리 뿔뿔이 흩어질지 모르는 일이니까, 라고 말했기 때문이다. 우리는 이 집에서 (요리사가 말했다) 장례식 하나, 결혼식 하나, 그리고 야반도주 하나를 함께 겪었지, 그러니 지금 같은 이런 때에 자기들끼리 화목하지 못했다는 소리는 듣지 말자고. 퍼치 부인은 이 가슴 뭉클한 연설에 크게 감동하여, 요리사야말로 천사라고 대놓고 말했다. 타울린슨 씨는 요리사에게, 자기가 보고 싶어 하는 그 좋은 정을 가로막는 일은 결코 없을 거라고 대답하고는, 하녀를 찾으러 잠시 자리를 떠났다가 이내 그 젊은 아가씨를 팔짱 끼고 돌아와서는, 부엌을 향해 외국인 운운한 것은 그저 농담이었으며, 자기와 앤은 이제 좋을 때나 나쁠 때나 서로를 맞이하기로 결심했고, 옥스퍼드 마켓에 자리를 잡아 청과물과 약초와 거머리를 두루 다루는 장사를 할 작정이니, 여러분의 후한 애고를 각별히 부탁드린다고 알렸다. 이 발표는 환호로 맞아졌고, 퍼치 부인은 영혼을 미래로 던져 넣은 채, \"딸들 말이야,\" 하고 요리사의 귀에 엄숙한 속삭임으로 말했다.\n\n이 아래쪽 영역에서는, 잔치 없이 집안의 불행이 지나갈 수 없었다. 그래서 요리사는 저녁거리로 따뜻한 요리 한두 가지를 후딱 만들어 냈고, 타울린슨 씨는 같은 환대의 목적에 바칠 바닷가재 샐러드를 버무렸다. 핍친 부인까지도 이 사태에 마음이 동하여 종을 울리고는, 남은 그 췌장 고기 조금을 데워서 데운 셰리주 한 잔의 사분의 일가량과 함께 쟁반에 담아 저녁으로 가져다 달라고, 자기가 몸이 좀 좋지 않으니 그리해 달라고 아래로 전갈을 보냈다.\n\n돔비 씨에 대한 이야기도 조금 오갔지만, 아주 조금이었다. 주로 그가 이 일이 벌어지리란 것을 얼마나 오래전부터 알고 있었느냐 하는 추측이었다. 요리사가 빈틈없이 말했다, \"오, 오래됐고말고, 정말이지! 그건 내가 장담해.\" 그리고 퍼치 씨에게 물어보니, 그도 요리사의 견해에 동의했다. 누군가는 그가 앞으로 어찌할지, 어디 자리라도 얻어 나갈지 궁금해했다. 타울린슨 씨는 그러지 않을 것 같다며, 좀 나은 축에 드는 점잖은 양로원 어딘가에 들어가 몸을 의탁하지 않겠느냐고 넌지시 말했다. \"아, 거기서 자그마한 정원도 갖고 말이지, 아시잖아요,\" 요리사가 애처롭게 말했다, \"봄이면 스위트피도 가꾸고.\" \"바로 그렇지,\" 타울린슨 씨가 말했다, \"그리고 무슨무슨 형제회의 일원이 되는 거고.\" \"우린 다 형제지요,\" 퍼치 부인이 술잔을 들이켜다 잠시 멈추고 말했다. \"자매들만 빼고,\" 퍼치 씨가 말했다. \"강하던 자들이 어쩜 이리 무너졌누!\" 요리사가 한마디 했다. \"교만하면 넘어지는 법이지, 늘 그래 왔고 앞으로도 그럴 거야!\" 하녀가 한마디 보탰다.\n\n이런 소회를 풀어놓으면서 그들이 얼마나 흐뭇해하는지, 그리고 그 공통의 충격을 체념으로 견뎌 내며 얼마나 기독교인다운 한마음을 느끼는지, 참으로 놀라운 일이었다. 이 더없이 훌륭한 심경 상태에 단 한 번의 훼방이 끼어들었는데, 그것은 신분이 낮은—검은 양말을 신은—어린 부엌 하녀가 일으킨 것이었다. 그 아이는 오랫동안 입을 벌린 채 앉아 있다가 별안간 그 입에서 이런 뜻의 말을 내뱉었다, \"혹시 품삯을 못 받게 되면 어쩌죠!\" 일동은 잠시 말문이 막힌 채 앉아 있었다. 그러나 요리사가 가장 먼저 정신을 차리고 그 젊은 여자에게 돌아서서는, 네가 그 밥을 얻어먹는 집안을 어찌 감히 그런 부정직한 억측으로 모욕하느냐고, 그리고 명예의 한 조각이라도 남은 사람이라면 누가 가엾은 하인들의 쥐꼬리만 한 품삯을 떼어먹겠느냐고 따져 물었다. \"그게 네 신앙심에서 나온 생각이라면, 메리 도스,\" 요리사가 발끈하여 말했다, \"넌 도대체 어디로 가려는 건지 모르겠구나.\"\n\n타울린슨 씨도 알지 못했다. 아무도 몰랐다. 그리고 그 어린 부엌 하녀는 정작 자기 자신도 정확히 모르는 듯한 데다 일동의 목소리에 매도당하여, 옷을 두르듯 부끄러움에 온통 휩싸였다.\n\n며칠이 지나자, 낯선 사람들이 그 집에 드나들기 시작하더니, 마치 자기들이 거기 사는 양 식당에서 서로 약속을 잡았다. 특히, 모자이크풍 아라비아 사람 같은 인상에 아주 묵직한 시곗줄을 두른 한 신사가 있었는데, 그는 응접실에서 휘파람을 불었고, 늘 펜과 잉크를 주머니에 넣고 다니는 다른 신사를 기다리는 동안 타울린슨 씨에게 (\"영감\"이라는 허물없는 호칭으로) 저 진홍색에 금빛 도는 휘장들이 새로 샀을 때 값이 얼마나 됐을 것 같으냐고 물었다. 식당에 드나드는 사람과 약속은 날마다 더 많아졌고, 신사마다 주머니에 펜과 잉크를 지니고 있는 듯했으며, 또 저마다 그것을 쓸 일이 있는 듯했다. 마침내 경매가 있을 거라는 말이 나왔고, 그러자 펜과 잉크를 주머니에 넣은 사람들이 더 많이 도착했는데, 이들은 카펫 모자를 쓴 한 무리의 인부들을 지휘했다. 그 인부들은 곧장 카펫을 뜯어내고 가구를 마구 두들겨 옮기며, 현관과 계단에 자기네 신발 자국을 수천 개나 찍어 댔다.\n\n아래층의 회의단은 이 내내 한자리에 모여 있었고, 할 일이 없는지라 먹는 일로 가히 묘기를 부렸다. 마침내 어느 날 그들은 한꺼번에 핍친 부인의 방으로 호출되었고, 그 어여쁜 페루 여인에게서 이런 말을 들었다.\n\n\"너희 주인 어른이 곤경에 빠지셨다,\" 핍친 부인이 톡 쏘듯 말했다. \"그건 다들 알고 있겠지?\"\n\n타울린슨 씨가 대변인으로서, 그 사실을 대체로 알고 있노라고 인정했다.\n\n\"그래서 너희들 죄다 제 앞가림이나 하려고 눈치만 보고 있는 게지, 내 장담하마,\" 핍친 부인이 그들을 향해 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했다.\n\n뒤쪽에서 날카로운 목소리가 외쳤다, \"당신이나 마찬가지지!\"\n\n\"그게 네 생각이냐, 이 뻔뻔한 것아, 응?\" 노기등등한 핍친이 사람들 머리 너머로 불같은 눈초리를 던지며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 핍친 부인, 그렇답니다,\" 요리사가 앞으로 나서며 응수했다. \"그래서 어쩌시겠다는 거죠, 네?\"\n\n\"흥, 그럼 네 멋대로 빨리 나가려무나,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다. \"빠르면 빠를수록 좋지. 다시는 네 낯짝을 안 봤으면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그 억센 핍친은 캔버스 천 자루를 꺼내더니, 그날까지의 품삯에 한 달 치를 더하여 헤아려 주었다. 그러고는 그 돈을 받았다는 영수증이 마지막 한 획까지 정식으로 서명될 때까지 돈을 꽉 움켜쥐고 있다가, 그제야 마지못해 놓아주었다. 이런 식의 절차를 핍친 부인은 집안 식구 한 사람 한 사람에게 되풀이하여, 마침내 모두에게 품삯을 치렀다.\n\n\"이제 가고 싶은 자는 제 갈 길을 가도 좋고,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다, \"남고 싶은 자는 일주일쯤 식대 받고 여기 남아 쓸모 있게 굴어도 좋다. 다만,\" 발끈하기 잘하는 핍친이 말했다, \"저 칠칠치 못한 요리사만은 당장 나가야 해.\"\n\n\"그러고말고요,\" 요리사가 말했다, \"꼭 그러겠어요! 안녕히 계세요, 핍친 부인, 그리고 진심으로 부인의 그 어여쁜 인상에 대해 칭찬을 드릴 수 있었으면 좋겠네요!\"\n\n\"썩 꺼지지 못해,\" 핍친 부인이 발을 구르며 말했다.\n\n요리사는 인자한 위엄을 풍기며 미끄러지듯 나갔는데, 그것이 핍친 부인을 몹시 약 올렸다. 그리고 이내 아래층에서 나머지 동맹자들과 합류했다.\n\n그러자 타울린슨 씨는, 우선 먹을 것을 조금 간단히 들 것을 제안하고 싶으며, 그 간식을 들면서 지금 자기들이 처한 형편에 들어맞을 만한 제안을 하나 내놓고 싶다고 말했다. 다과가 마련되고 다들 아주 흔쾌히 나눠 먹은 뒤, 타울린슨 씨의 제안인즉슨, 요컨대 요리사가 떠나는 마당에, 우리가 우리 자신에게 진실하지 않으면 누구도 우리에게 진실하지 않을 거라는 것이었다. 우리는 이 집에서 오래 살았고, 서로 사이좋게 지내려고 무던히도 애를 썼다는 것이었다. (이 대목에서 요리사가 감격하여 \"옳소, 옳소!\" 하고 외쳤고, 또다시 와 있던 데다 목까지 차오른 퍼치 부인은 눈물을 흘렸다.) 그리고 지금 같은 때에는 마땅히 \"하나가 가면 모두 간다!\"는 마음가짐이어야 한다고 그는 생각한다는 것이었다. 하녀는 이 너그러운 정서에 크게 감동하여 따뜻하게 찬동했다. 요리사는 그것이 옳다고 느낀다며, 다만 그것이 자기에게 바치는 인사치레가 아니라 의무감에서 나온 것이기를 바랄 뿐이라고 말했다. 타울린슨 씨는 의무감에서 나온 것이라고 대답하고, 또 이제 자기 의견을 밝히지 않을 수 없게 된 마당이니 터놓고 말하건대, 경매니 뭐니 하는 일이 진행되고 있는 집에 남아 있는 것이 그리 점잖은 일이라고는 생각지 않는다고 했다. 하녀도 그건 확실하다며, 그 증거로, 바로 오늘 아침 카펫 모자를 쓴 낯선 사내가 계단에서 자기에게 입을 맞추려 들었다고 이야기했다. 이 말에 타울린슨 씨는 그 무례한 자를 찾아내어 \"박살내려고\" 의자에서 벌떡 일어서려 했으나, 여인들에게 붙들리고 말았다. 여인들은 그에게 진정하라고, 그런 추잡한 일이 벌어지는 곳은 당장 떠나는 것이 더 쉽고 현명하다는 점을 헤아려 보라고 간곡히 청했다. 퍼치 부인은 그 일을 새로운 시각에서 내놓으며, 자기 방에 틀어박혀 있는 돔비 씨에 대한 배려를 보아서라도 황급히 물러나는 것이 마땅하다고까지 했다. \"그도 그럴 것이,\" 그 착한 여인이 말했다, \"한때 자기가 어마어마한 부자인 줄 알게끔 속였던 그 가엾은 하인들 가운데 누구라도 마주친다면, 그 어른 심정이 어떠하겠어요!\" 요리사는 이 도덕적 고려에 어찌나 크게 감명을 받았던지, 퍼치 부인은 거기에 더해 직접 지어낸 것이며 어디서 가려 뽑은 것이며 경건한 격언을 여럿 보탰다. 이리하여 모두가 떠나야 한다는 것이 분명한 일이 되었다. 짐 상자가 꾸려지고, 삯마차가 불려 왔으며, 그날 저녁 땅거미가 질 무렵에는 그 무리 가운데 단 한 사람도 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n그 집은 길고 음울한 거리에, 크고 풍우에 끄떡없이 서 있었다. 그러나 그것은 폐허였고, 쥐들이 그곳에서 달아났다.\n\n카펫 모자를 쓴 인부들은 가구를 계속 함부로 굴려 댔고, 펜과 잉크를 지닌 신사들은 그 목록을 작성하며, 결코 앉으라고 만들어지지 않은 가구 위에 걸터앉고, 결코 그 위에서 먹으라고 만들어지지 않은 또 다른 가구 위에서 술집에서 가져온 빵과 치즈를 먹었으니, 귀한 물건들을 엉뚱한 용도에 갖다 쓰는 데서 즐거움을 느끼는 듯했다. 가구의 무질서한 뒤섞임 또한 벌어졌다. 매트리스와 침구가 식당에 나타났고, 유리그릇과 도자기가 온실로 들어갔으며, 거창한 만찬 식기 일습이 큰 응접실의 긴 소파 위에 수북이 늘어놓였고, 다발로 묶인 계단 난간 줄이 대리석 벽난로 위를 장식했다. 마침내 인쇄된 광고지를 붙인 양탄자 한 장이 발코니에 내걸렸고, 그와 비슷한 부착물이 현관문 양옆을 꾸몄다.\n\n그러더니 온종일, 거리에는 곰팡내 나는 이륜마차와 사륜 짐마차의 행렬이 늘어섰다. 그리고 유대인이며 기독교인이며 할 것 없이 남루한 흡혈귀 떼가 집 안을 휘젓고 다니며, 손가락 마디로 판유리 거울을 두드려 소리를 내보고, 그랜드 피아노로 불협화의 옥타브를 쾅쾅 쳐 보고, 젖은 집게손가락으로 그림 위를 쓱쓱 문지르고, 제일 좋은 식칼 날에 입김을 호호 불어 보고, 더러운 주먹으로 의자와 소파의 쿠션을 쿡쿡 찔러 보고, 깃털 침대를 마구 헝클어 놓고, 모든 서랍을 열었다 닫았다 하고, 은수저와 포크를 손으로 가늠해 보고, 휘장과 아마포의 올 하나하나까지 들여다보며 무엇이든 깎아내렸다. 온 집 안에 은밀한 구석이라곤 없었다. 보풀투성이에 코담배 냄새 풍기는 낯선 자들이 다락방의 옷장을 들여다보듯 호기심 어린 눈으로 부엌의 화덕 속을 빤히 들여다보았다. 보풀 빠진 모자를 쓴 뚱뚱한 사내들이 침실 창밖을 내다보며 거리의 친구들과 농지거리를 주고받았다. 조용히 계산속이 밝은 자들은 목록을 들고 화장실로 물러나, 몽당연필로 그 여백에 메모를 적었다. 중개인 둘은 비상용 사다리까지 침범하여 집 꼭대기에서 동네를 한 바퀴 빙 둘러보았다. 그 우글거림과 웅성거림, 그리고 오르락내리락은 며칠이고 계속되었다. 일급 현대식 가정용 가구 외 일습이 전시 중이었다.\n\n그러더니 가장 좋은 응접실에는 탁자로 만든 울타리가 세워졌다. 그리고 돌려 깎은 다리가 달린, 일급의, 프랑스식으로 광을 낸, 펼침식의, 망원경처럼 늘어나는 스페인산 마호가니 만찬 탁자 위에 경매인의 강단이 세워졌다. 그리고 유대인이며 기독교인이며 할 것 없는 남루한 흡혈귀 떼와, 보풀투성이에 코담배 냄새 풍기는 낯선 자들과, 보풀 빠진 모자를 쓴 뚱뚱한 사내들이 그 둘레에 모여들어, 벽난로 선반까지 포함하여 손 닿는 것마다 죄다 걸터앉고는 값을 부르기 시작했다. 방들은 온종일 후끈하고, 웅웅거리고, 먼지투성이였다. 그리고—그 열기와 웅성거림과 먼지를 훌쩍 넘어, 그 위로—경매인의 머리와 어깨, 목소리와 망치가 줄곧 분주히 움직였다. 카펫 모자를 쓴 인부들은 경매 물품을 이리저리 굴리느라 허둥대고 사나워졌으나, 물품은 여전히 나갑니다, 나갑니다, 낙찰! 여전히 새것이 올라왔다. 이따금 농담이 오가고 일제히 웃음보가 터졌다. 이것이 그날 온종일, 그리고 뒤이은 사흘 동안 계속되었다. 일급 현대식 가정용 가구 외 일습이 판매 중이었다.\n\n그러더니 곰팡내 나는 이륜마차와 사륜 짐마차가 다시 나타났고, 그와 더불어 용수철 짐마차와 짐수레, 그리고 짐을 묶는 밧줄을 든 짐꾼 부대가 왔다. 온종일, 카펫 모자를 쓴 인부들은 나사돌리개와 침대 죔쇠를 비틀어 대거나, 무거운 짐을 진 채 열댓 명씩 한 덩어리로 계단에서 비틀거리거나, 스페인산 마호가니며 최고급 자단이며 판유리로 된 바위 덩어리 같은 것들을 들어 올려 이륜마차와 사륜 짐마차, 용수철 짐마차와 짐수레에 실었다. 포장을 씌운 짐마차에서 외바퀴 손수레에 이르기까지, 온갖 종류의 짐 나르는 수레가 대기하고 있었다. 가엾은 폴의 자그마한 침대는 당나귀 두 마리가 끄는 수레에 실려 나갔다. 거의 일주일 내내, 일급 현대식 가정용 가구 외 일습이 운반되어 나가는 중이었다.\n\n마침내 그것이 죄다 사라졌다. 집 안에 남은 것이라곤 목록에서 흩어져 나온 낱장들과, 어수선하게 널린 짚과 건초 부스러기와, 현관문 뒤에 줄지어 선 백랍 단지들뿐이었다. 카펫 모자를 쓴 인부들은 나사돌리개와 침대 죔쇠를 자루에 거두어 담고, 그것을 어깨에 메고 떠나갔다. 펜과 잉크를 지닌 신사들 가운데 하나가 마지막 마무리로 집을 한 바퀴 돌며, 이 탐나는 가족용 저택의 임대에 관한 광고지를 창문마다 붙이고 덧창을 닫았다. 마침내 그도 카펫 모자를 쓴 인부들의 뒤를 따라갔다. 침입자는 한 사람도 남지 않았다. 그 집은 폐허였고, 쥐들이 그곳에서 달아났다.\n\n핍친 부인의 거처는, 창문 블라인드가 빈틈없이 내려진 일층의 그 잠긴 방들과 함께, 이 전반적인 황폐화를 면했다. 핍친 부인은 그 절차가 진행되는 내내 자기 방에서 근엄하고 돌처럼 굳은 채로 있었다. 그러지 않으면 이따금 경매장을 들여다보며 물건들이 얼마에 팔리는지 살피고, 특정한 안락의자 하나에 값을 불렀다. 핍친 부인은 그 안락의자의 최고가 응찰자가 되었고, 칙 부인이 찾아왔을 때 자기 소유물 위에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"제 오라비는 좀 어떤가요, 핍친 부인?\" 칙 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"나야 도무지 알 길이 없지요,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다. \"그분은 내게 말 한마디 건네는 영광을 베푸시는 법이 없으니. 자기 방 바로 옆방에 음식과 술을 들여놓게 하고는, 아무도 없을 때 나와서 자기가 들 것을 들지요. 내게 물어봐야 소용없어요. 식은 자두죽을 먹다 입을 덴 남쪽 나라 그 사내만큼도 그분에 대해 아는 게 없으니까요.\"\n\n이것을 신랄한 핍친은 몸을 홱 비틀며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 어쩜 좋아!\" 칙 부인이 부드럽게 외쳤다. \"이게 얼마나 더 가야 한단 말이에요! 제 오라비가 노력을 하지 않으면, 핍친 부인, 그분은 대체 어찌 되는 거죠? 이쯤 됐으면 노력하지 않은 결과가 어떤지 충분히 보았을 테니, 그 치명적인 잘못만은 경계할 줄 알았을 텐데요, 정말이지.\"\n\n\"이런, 호들갑 좀 봐!\" 핍친 부인이 코를 문지르며 말했다. \"이걸 두고 야단법석이 어지간하군요, 내가 보기엔. 그렇게 대단한 일도 아니구먼. 사람이 불행을 겪고 가구를 처분해야 하는 일이야 예전에도 있었지요. 나야말로 분명 그랬고요!\"\n\n\"제 오라비는,\" 칙 부인이 심오하게 말을 이었다, \"어찌나 별난지—어찌나 묘한 사람인지 몰라요. 내가 본 중에 가장 별난 사람이에요. 누가 믿겠어요, 그 자식답잖은 아이가 결혼해서 외국으로 떠났다는 소식을 받았을 때—이제 와선 내가 그 아이에겐 어딘가 심상찮은 데가 있다고 늘 말했던 걸 떠올리면 위안이 되긴 하지만요. 하긴 아무도 내 말엔 신경도 안 쓰지만—누가 믿겠냐고요, 글쎄, 그분이 그때 내게 홱 돌아서서는, 내 태도로 미루어 그 애가 우리 집에 와 있는 줄로 짐작했었다고 말하다니요? 어머나 세상에! 또 누가 믿겠어요, 내가 그분에게 그저, '폴, 내가 무척 어리석은지도 모르지, 분명 그렇겠지만, 도무지 네 사정이 어쩌다 이 지경이 됐는지 모르겠구나,' 하고 말했더니, 그분이 정말로 내게 버럭 화를 내며, 자기가 부르기 전에는 다시는 보러 오지 말라고 하다니요! 어머, 세상에나!\"\n\n\"아!\" 핍친 부인이 말했다. \"그분이 광산 일하고 좀 더 인연이 있었더라면 좋았을 것을. 그랬으면 그 성미를 한번 단단히 시험받았을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그래서,\" 칙 부인이 핍친 부인의 말은 전혀 아랑곳하지 않고 다시 말을 이었다, \"이게 어떻게 끝나려는 걸까요? 그게 내가 알고 싶은 거예요. 제 오라비는 대체 어쩔 작정인 거죠? 뭐라도 해야 할 텐데. 제 방에 틀어박혀 있어 봐야 아무 소용없잖아요. 일거리가 제 발로 그분을 찾아오진 않을 테니까요. 안 되죠. 그분이 일거리를 찾아가야 해요. 그런데 왜 안 가는 거죠? 평생 사업가로 살았으니 어디로 가야 할지는 알 거 아니에요. 좋아요. 그럼 왜 거기로 안 가는 거냐고요?\"\n\n칙 부인은 이 강력한 추론의 사슬을 벼려 낸 뒤, 그것을 감탄하느라 잠시 입을 다물고 있었다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 그 신중한 부인이 따지는 듯한 태도로 말했다, \"그분처럼 이 끔찍하게 거북한 일들이 다 지나가는 내내 여기 틀어박혀 있는 고집이 세상에 어디 있겠어요? 갈 데가 없어서 그러는 것도 아닌데 말이죠. 당연히 우리 집으로 올 수 있었잖아요. 거기선 제집처럼 편히 지낼 수 있다는 걸 그분도 알 텐데요? 칙 씨는 그 얘기를 아주 지겹도록 했고, 나도 내 입으로 직접 말했다고요, '아니 폴, 설마 네 사정이 이 지경이 됐다고 해서 우리처럼 가까운 친척에게마저 더는 마음 편히 못 온다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지? 우리가 세상 다른 사람들과 똑같다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지?' 하지만 천만에요. 여기서 내내 버티고, 지금도 여기 있잖아요. 어머, 세상에, 만약 이 집이 세놓이기라도 하면! 그땐 그분이 어쩌려고요? 그땐 여기 못 있죠. 그래도 버티려 들면, 퇴거 명령에, 부동산 점유 회복 소송에, 별의별 일이 다 벌어질 거고, 그러면 그분은 떠나야 해요. 그럴 거면 마지막에 떠날 게 아니라 애초에 떠나는 게 낫지 않겠어요? 그러니 다시 아까 내가 한 말로 돌아가서, 나는 당연히 묻게 되는 거죠, 이게 대체 어떻게 끝나려는 거냐고요?\"\n\n\"적어도 나에 관한 한 이게 어떻게 끝날지는 내가 알지요,\" 핍친 부인이 대답했다, \"그거면 나한텐 충분하고요. 나는 단숨에 떠날 참이에요.\"\n\n\"단, 무엇으로요, 핍친 부인?\" 칙 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"단숨에요,\" 핍친 부인이 날카롭게 되받았다.\n\n\"아, 그래요! 정말이지 부인을 탓할 순 없네요, 핍친 부인,\" 칙 부인이 솔직하게 말했다.\n\n\"부인이 탓할 수 있다 한들 내겐 매한가지지만요,\" 빈정대는 핍친이 대답했다. \"어쨌든 나는 떠나요. 여기 더는 못 있어요. 일주일이면 죽고 말 거예요. 어제는 내 손으로 직접 돼지갈비를 구워야 했는데, 그런 일엔 익숙지가 않거든요. 다음엔 내 몸이 탈이 나고 말 거예요. 게다가 내가 여기 올 때 브라이턴에 꽤 괜찮은 거래처를 두고 왔는데—꼬마 팽키네 식구들만 해도 나한텐 일 년에 족히 팔십 파운드는 되는 자리였다고요—그걸 내버릴 형편이 못 돼요. 조카에게 편지를 써 뒀고, 지금쯤 나를 기다리고 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"제 오라비에게는 말씀하셨나요?\" 칙 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"오, 그래요, 그분에게 말하라니 참 쉽게도 말하시네요,\" 핍친 부인이 되받았다. \"그게 어떻게 되는데요? 어제 그분에게 큰 소리로 외쳤다고요, 내가 여기 있어 봐야 쓸모가 없으니, 차라리 리처즈 부인을 불러오게 하시는 게 낫겠다고요. 그분은 그렇다는 뜻으로 뭔가 끙 소리를 냈고, 그래서 내가 사람을 보냈지요. 끙 소리라니, 참! 그분이 핍친 씨였더라면, 끙거릴 만한 까닭이라도 좀 있었을 텐데. 흥! 더는 못 참겠어요!\"\n\n이 대목에서, 페루 광산의 밑바닥에서 그토록 많은 꿋꿋함과 미덕을 길어 올린 이 모범적인 여인은, 칙 부인을 문까지 배웅하려고 쿠션 깔린 자기 소유물에서 일어났다. 칙 부인은 마지막까지 자기 오라비의 별난 성격을 한탄하며, 자기의 명민함과 명석한 두뇌에 잔뜩 마음을 빼앗긴 채 소리 없이 물러갔다.\n\n저녁 땅거미가 질 무렵, 비번이 된 투들 씨가 폴리와 짐 상자 하나를 데리고 도착하여, 쪽 소리 나는 입맞춤과 함께 그 둘을 텅 빈 집의 현관에 남겨 두었는데, 그 한적한 분위기가 투들 씨의 마음을 몹시 무겁게 했다.\n\n\"내 말 좀 들어 봐, 폴리, 여보,\" 투들 씨가 말했다, \"내가 이제 기관사가 되었고 세상살이도 그럭저럭 넉넉하니, 지난날의 은혜만 아니었다면 당신이 여기 와서 이렇게 우중충해지게 두진 않았을 거야. 하지만 지난날의 은혜란 건, 폴리, 결코 잊어서는 안 되는 거지. 게다가 역경에 처한 이들에겐 당신 얼굴이 강심제 같은 법이야. 그러니 한 번 더 입맞춤이나 하자고, 여보. 당신이 옳은 일 하는 것밖에 더 바라는 게 없다는 거 내가 알아. 그리고 내 생각엔, 이건 옳고 마땅한 일을 하는 거야. 잘 자, 폴리!\"\n\n이 무렵 핍친 부인은 검은 봄버진 치마와 검은 보닛과 숄을 두르고 거뭇하게 모습을 드러냈다. 그리고 자기 소지품을 꾸려 놓았고, 자기 의자를(전에 돔비 씨가 아끼던 의자였고 경매에서 거저나 다름없이 산 것이었다) 현관문 가까이에 준비해 두었으며, 오늘 밤 개인 용무로 브라이턴에 가는 길에 개인적인 약조에 따라 자기를 들러 태우고 집까지 데려다줄 사륜 합승마차만을 기다리고 있었다.\n\n이윽고 그것이 왔다. 핍친 부인의 옷가지가 안으로 건네져 챙겨 넣어진 다음, 핍친 부인의 의자가 그다음으로 건네져, 건초 묶음 몇 단 사이의 알맞은 구석에 놓였다. 그 상냥한 여인이 여행하는 동안 그 의자에 앉아 갈 작정이었기 때문이다. 핍친 부인 본인이 그다음으로 안으로 건네져, 험상궂게 자리를 잡았다. 그 딱딱한 잿빛 눈에는 뱀처럼 번득이는 빛이 어렸으니, 버터 바른 토스트의 거듭되는 차례며, 잇따라 나올 뜨거운 갈비며, 어린아이들을 들볶고 윽박지르는 일이며, 가엾은 베리에게 매섭게 쏘아붙이는 일이며, 그 밖에 그 식인귀의 성에서 누리던 온갖 즐거움을 미리 그려 보는 듯했다. 핍친 부인은 합승마차가 출발하자 거의 웃음을 터뜨릴 뻔하면서, 검은 봄버진 치마를 가지런히 매만지고 안락의자의 쿠션 사이에 몸을 편안히 묻었다.\n\n그 집은 어찌나 완전한 폐허인지 쥐들마저 달아나, 단 한 마리도 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n그러나 폴리는, 그 버려진 저택에 홀로 있었으나—그곳의 옛 주인이 머리를 숨기고 있는 그 잠긴 방들 안에 동무가 있을 리 없으니—오래 홀로 있지는 않았다. 밤이었다. 그녀는 가정부 방에 앉아 일을 하며, 이곳이 얼마나 외로운 집인지, 또 여기에 어떤 사연이 깃들어 있는지를 잊으려 애쓰고 있었다. 그때 현관문에서 노크 소리가 났는데, 이렇게 텅 빈 곳에 울려 퍼지는 노크 소리가 그렇듯 더없이 크게 울렸다. 문을 열고 그녀는, 꼭 맞는 검은 보닛을 쓴 여인의 모습을 동반한 채 메아리치는 현관을 가로질러 되돌아왔다. 톡스 양이었고, 톡스 양의 두 눈은 붉었다.\n\n\"오, 폴리,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"방금 아이들과 짧은 수업을 좀 하려고 들렀더니, 당신이 나한테 남긴 전갈을 받았지 뭐예요. 그래서 마음을 조금이나마 추스르자마자 당신을 뒤따라왔어요. 여기 당신 말고는 아무도 없나요?\"\n\n\"네! 한 사람도 없어요,\" 폴리가 말했다.\n\n\"그분은 보셨어요?\" 톡스 양이 속삭였다.\n\n\"어머나,\" 폴리가 대답했다, \"아뇨. 그분은 여러 날째 모습을 안 보이셨어요. 방에서 도통 나오시질 않는다고들 하더라고요.\"\n\n\"어디 편찮으시다던가요?\" 톡스 양이 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 마님, 제가 알기론 그렇진 않아요,\" 폴리가 대답했다, \"마음만 빼면요. 거기가 단단히 안 좋으실 거예요, 가엾은 어른!\"\n\n톡스 양의 가엾어하는 마음이 어찌나 컸던지 그녀는 거의 말을 잇지 못했다. 그녀는 이제 애송이는 아니었으나, 나이와 독신 생활로 매정해지지는 않았다. 그녀의 마음은 무척 다정했고, 그 연민은 무척 진실했으며, 그 경의는 무척 진심이었다. 물고기 눈알이 박힌 로켓 목걸이 아래에, 톡스 양은 한결 덜 별난 겉모습을 한 많은 이들보다 나은 자질을 지니고 있었으니, 그 자질이란 저 위대한 추수꾼의 수확 때에 떨어지는 가장 훌륭한 겉모습이며 가장 빛나는 껍데기보다도, 여러 해의 해가 거듭 도는 동안 더 오래 살아남을 그런 것이었다.\n\n톡스 양이 자리를 뜨기까지, 그리고 폴리가 휑한 계단에 일렁이는 촛불을 들고 동무 삼아 거리 저편으로 그녀의 뒷모습을 바라보고서, 그 음산한 집으로 되돌아가 무거운 문 빗장으로 그 텅 빔을 덜컹 울리고 미끄러지듯 잠자리에 들기를 내키지 않아 하기까지는, 오랜 시간이 걸렸다. 그러나 이 모든 일을 폴리는 해냈다. 그리고 아침이면 그 어두컴컴한 방 가운데 한 곳에, 미리 준비하라고 일러 들은 물건들을 차려 놓고, 그런 다음 물러나 이튿날 아침 같은 시각이 되기까지는 다시 들어가지 않았다. 거기엔 종이 있었으나 결코 울리지 않았고, 이따금 오락가락하는 발소리가 들리긴 했어도 그것이 밖으로 나오는 법은 없었다.\n\n톡스 양은 그날 일찌감치 다시 왔다. 그때부터 톡스 양의 일거리는 이 방들로 이튿날 아침 들여보낼 자그마한 별미를—혹은 그녀에게 별미인 것을—마련하는 것이 되었다. 그녀는 그 일에서 어찌나 큰 만족을 얻었던지, 그때부터 꼬박꼬박 그 일에 매달렸다. 그리고 분 바른 머리에 변발을 했던 고인이 남긴 빈약한 살림에서 가려 뽑은 갖가지 별미를 자기 작은 바구니에 담아 날마다 가져왔다. 그녀는 또한, 곱슬머리를 마는 종이에 싸서, 자기 끼니로 먹을 차가운 고기 조각이며 양의 혀며 닭 반 마리 따위를 가져왔고, 이 음식들을 폴리와 나누며, 쥐들마저 달아난 그 폐허가 된 집에서 거의 온종일을 보냈다. 무슨 소리만 나도 화들짝 놀라 몸을 숨기고, 죄인처럼 살그머니 드나들면서, 오직 자기가 흠모하는 그 몰락한 대상에게 진실하기만을 바랐으니—그에게도 알려지지 않고, 단 한 사람의 가난하고 소박한 여인을 빼고는 온 세상에 알려지지도 않은 채로.\n\n소령은 그것을 알고 있었다. 그러나 그 일로 누가 더 현명해지는 일은 없었어도, 소령만은 훨씬 더 즐거워졌다. 소령은 호기심이 발동하여, 원주민에게 이따금 그 집을 지켜보며 돔비가 어찌 되어 가는지 알아내라고 일렀다. 원주민이 톡스 양의 한결같음을 보고하자, 소령은 웃다가 거의 숨이 막혀 죽을 뻔했다. 그는 그때부터 줄곧 더 시퍼레져서는, 게 눈 같은 두 눈을 머리에서 툭 튀어나오게 한 채, 줄곧 혼자 씩씩거리며 말했다, \"제기랄, 그 여자는 타고난 멍청이로군!\"\n\n그러면 그 몰락한 사내는. 그는 어떻게 시간을 보냈을까, 홀로?\n\n\"훗날 이 방에서 그 일을 기억하게 하라!\" 그는 그 일을 기억했다. 그것이 이제 그의 마음을 짓눌렀으니, 다른 그 무엇보다도 더 무겁게.\n\n\"훗날 이 방에서 그 일을 기억하게 하라! 지붕 위로 떨어지는 비, 문밖에서 슬피 우는 바람은, 그 구슬픈 소리 속에 어쩌면 앞날을 미리 아는 능력을 지니고 있을지도 모른다. 훗날 이 방에서 그 일을 기억하게 하라!\"\n\n그는 그 일을 기억했다. 비참한 밤에 그는 그것을 생각했고, 음산한 낮에, 처참한 새벽에, 유령 같고 기억에 시달리는 황혼녘에 그것을 생각했다. 그는 그 일을 기억했다. 고뇌 속에서, 슬픔 속에서, 회한 속에서, 절망 속에서! \"아빠! 아빠! 저한테 말 좀 해 주세요, 사랑하는 아빠!\" 그는 그 말을 다시 들었고, 그 얼굴을 보았다. 그는 그것이 떨리는 두 손 위로 무너지는 것을 보았고, 한 줄기 길게 늘어진 나직한 울부짖음이 위로 올라가는 것을 들었다.\n\n그는 쓰러졌고, 다시는 일으켜 세워질 수 없었다. 그의 세속적 파멸의 그 밤에는 내일의 태양이 없었고, 그의 가정적 치욕의 그 오점에는 정화가 없었다. 다행히도, 그 무엇도 죽은 그의 아이를 되살려 낼 수는 없었다. 그러나 지난 모든 시절에서 그토록 다르게 만들 수 있었던 것—지난날 그 자체를 그토록 다르게 만들 수 있었던 것, 비록 지금은 이것을 거의 생각지 못했지만—자기 자신이 빚어낸 것, 그토록 손쉽게 축복으로 빚어 낼 수 있었으나 여러 해에 걸쳐 그토록 한결같이 저주로 만들려고 작정했던 것, 그것이 그의 영혼의 날카로운 비탄이었다.\n\n오! 그는 그 일을 기억했다! 그날 밤 지붕 위로 떨어지던 비, 문밖에서 슬피 울던 바람은, 그 구슬픈 소리 속에 앞날을 미리 아는 능력을 지니고 있었던 것이다. 그는 이제 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알았다. 그는 이제, 운명의 가장 가혹한 일격보다도 더 깊이 자기 고개를 숙이게 만든 그것을, 자기가 제 머리 위로 불러들였음을 알았다. 그는 이제, 거부당하고 버림받는다는 것이 무엇인지 알았다. 자기가 그 죄 없는 딸의 마음속에서 시들게 했던 사랑의 꽃송이 하나하나가 이제 잿가루가 되어 자기 위로 눈처럼 내려앉고 있는 지금에 와서.\n\n그는 자기와 신부가 집으로 돌아오던 그날 밤의 모습 그대로의 딸을 떠올렸다. 그는 이제 버려진 그 집의 온갖 가정사 속에서의 그녀의 모습을 떠올렸다. 그는 이제, 자기 주위의 모든 것 가운데 오직 그녀만이 결코 변하지 않았다고 생각했다. 그의 아들은 스러져 흙이 되었고, 그의 도도한 아내는 더럽혀진 인간으로 전락했으며, 그의 아첨꾼이자 친구는 가장 못된 악당으로 둔갑했고, 그의 재산은 녹아 사라졌으며, 그를 감싸 주던 바로 그 벽들조차 그를 낯선 이로 바라보았다. 오직 그녀만이 언제나 한결같이 그 온화하고 다정한 눈길을 그에게 돌렸다. 그렇다, 가장 나중까지, 그리고 마지막 순간까지. 그녀는 결코 그에게 변한 적이 없었고—그 또한 그녀에게 결코 변한 적이 없었으며—그리고 그녀는 떠나 버렸다.\n\n그것들이 하나하나 그의 마음 앞에서 떨어져 나갈 때—그의 아기, 그의 희망, 그의 아내, 그의 친구, 그의 재산이—오, 그가 그녀를 보아 온 그 안개가 얼마나 걷혀, 그녀의 참모습을 그에게 드러내 보였던가! 오, 이렇게 되느니 차라리 그가 그녀를 자기 아들처럼 사랑하고, 자기 아들을 잃듯 그녀를 잃어, 그 둘을 함께 그 이른 무덤에 뉘었더라면 얼마나 나았을까!\n\n그의 자존심 속에서—그는 아직도 자존심이 셌으니까—그는 세상이 자기에게서 거리낌 없이 떨어져 나가게 두었다. 세상이 떨어져 나가자 그는 그것을 떨쳐 냈다. 세상의 얼굴이 자기에 대한 동정을 드러내는 것이라 여기든, 자기에 대한 무관심을 드러내는 것이라 여기든, 그는 그것을 똑같이 피했다. 그것은 어느 쪽 모습이든 똑같은 정도로 피해야 할 것이었다. 그는 자기 비참함 속에서 어떤 동무도 떠올리지 못했으니, 오직 자기가 내쫓아 버린 그 한 사람을 빼고는. 자기가 그녀에게 무슨 말을 했을지, 혹은 그녀에게서 어떤 위로를 기꺼이 받았을지, 그는 스스로 그려 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 그는, 자기가 그녀를 견뎌 내기만 했더라면 그녀가 자기에게 진실했으리란 것을 늘 알고 있었다. 그는, 그녀가 다른 어느 때보다도 지금 자기를 더 사랑했으리란 것을 늘 알고 있었다. 그는 그것이 그녀의 천성에 깃들어 있음을, 자기 머리 위에 하늘이 있음만큼이나 확신했다. 그리고 그는 외로움 속에서, 시간이 가고 또 가도록 그렇게 생각하며 앉아 있었다. 날이면 날마다 이 말을 되뇌었고, 밤이면 밤마다 그에게 이 깨달음을 드러내 보였다.\n\n그것은, 의심할 바 없이 (한동안은 더디게 나아갔을지언정), 그녀의 젊은 남편이 보낸 편지를 받고, 그녀가 떠나 버렸음을 확실히 알게 된 데서 시작되었다. 그럼에도—그는 자기 파멸 속에서도 그토록 자존심이 셌고, 혹은 그녀를 오직 자기 것이 될 수도 있었으나 돌이킬 길 없이 잃어버린 무언가로만 떠올렸기에—설령 옆방에서 그녀의 목소리를 들을 수 있었다 한들, 그는 그녀에게 가지 않았을 것이다. 설령 거리에서 그녀를 볼 수 있었고, 그녀가 늘 그러던 눈길로 자기를 바라보는 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않았다 한들, 그는 옛날의 그 차갑고 용서할 줄 모르는 얼굴로 지나쳐 갔을 것이며, 그녀에게 말을 건네지도, 그 얼굴을 누그러뜨리지도 않았을 것이다, 비록 그 직후에 자기 가슴이 찢어질지라도. 처음에 그녀의 결혼이나 남편에 관하여 그의 생각이 아무리 격렬하고 그의 분노가 아무리 모질었던들, 그것은 이제 죄다 지나간 일이었다. 그는 주로 무엇이 될 수도 있었으나 되지 못한 것을 생각했다. 무엇이 있었는가 하면, 그것은 죄다 이 한 가지로 요약되었으니, 곧 그녀는 떠나 버렸고, 그는 슬픔과 회한에 짓눌려 고개를 숙이고 있다는 것이었다.\n\n그리고 이제 그는, 자기에게 그 집에서 두 아이가 태어났음을, 그리고 자기와 그 휑하니 넓고 텅 빈 벽들 사이에는, 슬프지만 끊어 내기 힘든, 두 번의 어린 시절과 두 번의 상실에 얽힌 어떤 끈이 있음을 느꼈다. 그는 이 감정이 처음 그의 가슴속에 뿌리를 내린 날 저녁에 그 집을 떠나려고—떠나야 한다는 것은 알았으나 어디로 갈지는 알지 못한 채—마음먹었었다. 그러나 그는 하룻밤을 더 머물기로, 그리고 그 밤에 다시 한번 방들을 돌아다녀 보기로 결심했다.\n\n그는 깊은 밤이 되었을 때 자기 칩거에서 나와, 손에 촛불을 들고 살그머니 계단을 올라갔다. 거기 찍힌 모든 발자국들은—그것들을 흔한 거리바닥만큼이나 흔한 것으로 만들어 놓았건만—그가 틀어박혀 귀를 기울이는 동안 그 하나하나가 당시 자기 뇌리에 박히는 듯했었다고 그는 생각했다. 그는 그것들의 수효를, 그리고 그 분주함과 다툼을—발이 발을 짓밟고, 위로 난 자취와 아래로 난 자취가 서로 밀치는 것을—바라보며, 그 시련 동안 자기가 얼마나 많은 고통을 겪었음에 틀림없는지, 또 자기가 얼마나 변해 버린 사람이 될 수밖에 없었는지를 절대적인 두려움과 경이로움으로 생각했다. 그는 또한 생각했다, 오, 이 세상 어딘가에, 한순간에 저 자국들의 절반쯤은 닳아 없애 버릴 수도 있었을 가벼운 발걸음이 있지는 않았을까!—그러고는 고개를 숙이고, 올라가면서 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n그는 그 발걸음이 자기 앞에서 나아가는 것을 거의 본 듯했다. 그는 멈춰 서서, 천창을 향해 위를 올려다보았다. 그러자 한 형상이, 그 자신이 어린아이였으나 한 아이를 안고서, 가면서 노래를 부르는 한 형상이, 다시 거기 있는 듯했다. 이윽고, 그것은 똑같은 그 형상이, 홀로, 숨을 멈춘 채 한순간 멈춰 서서, 눈물 젖은 얼굴 둘레로 빛나는 머리칼을 헐겁게 드리운 채, 그를 돌아보고 있었다.\n\n그는 방들을 돌아다녔다. 얼마 전까지만 해도 그토록 호사스러웠으나, 이제는 그토록 휑하고 음산하며, 보아하니 그 형태와 크기마저 그토록 달라진 방들을. 발자국들이 여기에도 빼곡했고, 자기가 겪은 그 고통에 대한 똑같은 생각이 그를 어리둥절하게 하고 겁먹게 했다. 그는 자기 머릿속의 이 온갖 뒤엉킴이 자기를 미치게 하지나 않을까, 그리고 자기 생각들이 이미 저 발자국들처럼 갈피를 잃고서, 똑같이 종잡을 수 없는 뒤얽힘과 어렴풋한 형상들의 갖가지 변형으로 서로 이어 붙고 있지나 않은가 두려워지기 시작했다.\n\n그는 그녀가 홀로 있을 때 이 방들 가운데 어디서 지냈는지조차 알지 못했다. 그는 그 방들을 떠나, 더 높은 곳으로 돌아다니러 가게 된 것이 다행스러웠다. 여기에는 그의 거짓된 아내, 그의 거짓된 친구이자 종복, 그의 거짓된 자존심의 근거에 얽힌 온갖 연상이 가득했다. 그러나 그는 이제 그것들을 죄다 밀쳐 두고, 오직 자기 두 아이만을 비참하게, 나약하게, 애틋하게 떠올렸다.\n\n어디에나, 그 발자국들! 그것들은 작은 침대가 있던 저 높은 곳 옛 방에도 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그는 거기서, 이 가엾고 망가진 사내가, 벽에 기대어 바닥에 몸을 던지고 눈물이 흐르는 대로 내버려 둘 만한 빈자리조차 좀처럼 찾기 어려웠다. 그는 오래전 여기서 그토록 많은 눈물을 흘렸기에, 다른 어느 곳에서보다 이곳에서 자기 나약함을 덜 부끄러워했다—어쩌면 그런 의식 때문에, 여기 오는 것에 대해 스스로에게 핑계를 댔는지도 몰랐다. 여기로, 어깨를 구부정하게 늘어뜨리고 턱을 가슴에 떨군 채, 그는 왔었다. 여기서, 깊은 밤에 맨바닥에 몸을 내던지고, 그는 홀로 울었다—그때조차 자존심이 센 사내였으니, 만일 어떤 다정한 손길이 내밀어졌거나 어떤 다정한 얼굴이 들여다보았더라면, 그는 벌떡 일어나 돌아서서, 자기 골방으로 내려가 버렸을 그런 사내였다.\n\n날이 밝자 그는 다시 자기 방에 틀어박혔다. 그는 오늘 떠나려고 마음먹었었으나, 자기에게 남은 마지막이자 유일한 것으로서 그 집의 이 끈에 매달렸다. 내일 떠나야지. 내일이 왔다. 내일 떠나야지. 어떤 인간도 알지 못하는 가운데, 밤마다 그는 밖으로 나와, 약탈당한 그 집을 유령처럼 떠돌았다. 날이 밝아 오는 많은 아침이면, 변해 버린 그의 얼굴이, 아직 빛에 어슴푸레하게밖에 비치지 않는 창문의 닫힌 블라인드 뒤로 늘어진 채, 자기 두 아이를 잃은 일을 곱씹었다. 그것은 이제 아이 하나가 아니었다. 그는 생각 속에서 그 둘을 다시 합쳐 놓았고, 그 둘은 결코 갈라지지 않았다. 오, 그가 그 둘을 지난날의 자기 사랑 속에서, 그리고 죽음 속에서 합쳐 놓을 수 있었더라면, 그리고 하나가 죽은 것보다도 그토록 더 못한 처지가 되지 않았더라면!\n\n격렬한 정신적 동요와 혼란은, 최근의 고통을 겪기 전에도, 그에게 새삼스러운 것이 아니었다. 그것은 고집스럽고 음울한 천성에게는 결코 새삼스러운 것이 아니다. 그런 천성은 그렇게 되려고 모질게 애쓰는 법이니까. 오래 밑이 파인 땅은 종종 한순간에 무너져 내리는 법이다. 여기서 그토록 여러 방식으로 밑이 파이고, 약해지고, 조금씩 부서져 내린 것은, 시곗바늘이 문자판 위를 움직여 감에 따라 점점 더, 점점 더 그러하였다.\n\n마침내 그는 자기가 아예 떠날 필요가 없을지도 모른다는 생각을 하기 시작했다. 그는 채권자들이 자기에게 남겨 준 것을(그들이 더 많이 남겨 주지 않은 것은 그 자신의 처사 탓이었다) 그래도 내어 줄 수 있었고, 자기와 그 폐허가 된 집 사이의 끈을, 오직 저 다른 한 가닥의 연결을 끊어 냄으로써만 끊어 낼 수 있었다—\n\n바로 그때 그의 발소리가, 그가 오락가락 거니는 동안, 옛 가정부 방에서 들려왔다. 그러나 그것이 지닌 참뜻으로 들린 것은 아니었으니, 그렇지 않았더라면 그것은 소름 끼치는 소리였으리라.\n\n세상은 그의 주위에서 무척 분주하고 부산스러웠다. 그는 그것을 다시금 의식하게 되었다. 그것은 소곤거리고 재잘거렸다. 그것은 결코 잠잠하지 않았다. 이것이, 그리고 발자국들의 뒤엉킴과 복잡함이, 그를 죽을 만큼 괴롭혔다. 사물들이 그의 눈에 흐릿하고 적갈색을 띠기 시작했다. 돔비와 아들은 이제 없었다—그의 아이들도 이제 없었다. 이것을 잘 생각해 봐야지, 내일.\n\n그는 내일 그것을 생각했다. 그리고 자기 의자에 앉아 생각하다가, 이따금 거울 속에서 이런 그림을 보았다.\n\n유령 같고, 초췌하고, 수척한 자기 자신의 형상이, 텅 빈 벽난로 위로 골똘히, 골똘히 잠겨 있었다. 이제 그것은 고개를 들어, 자기 얼굴의 주름과 패인 곳들을 살펴보았다. 이제 다시 고개를 떨구고는, 새로이 골똘히 잠겼다. 이제 그것은 일어나 이리저리 거닐었다. 이제 옆방으로 들어가더니, 화장대에서 무언가를 가슴에 품고 돌아왔다. 이제 그것은 문 아래쪽을 바라보며, 생각에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n—쉿! 무엇을?\n\n그것은, 만일 피가 그쪽으로 흘러나와 현관으로 새어 나간다면, 그토록 멀리까지 가는 데 오랜 시간이 걸리겠다고 생각하고 있었다. 그것은 너무도 은밀하고 더디게, 기어가듯 흘러서, 여기에 게으른 작은 웅덩이를 하나 짓고, 저기서 한 번 멈칫하고, 그러고는 또 다른 작은 웅덩이를 하나 짓다 보면, 치명상을 입은 사내는 오직 그것을 통해서만, 죽었거나 죽어 가는 채로 발견될 수 있겠다고. 그것은 이것을 오랫동안 생각한 끝에 다시 일어나, 한 손을 가슴에 품은 채 이리저리 거닐었다. 그는 이따금 그것을 흘끔거리며, 그 동작을 지켜보는 것에 무척 호기심을 느꼈고, 그 손이 얼마나 사악하고 살기등등해 보이는지를 눈여겨보았다.\n\n이제 그것은 다시 생각하고 있었다! 그것은 무엇을 생각하고 있었던가?\n\n사람들이, 그 피가 그토록 멀리까지 기어가면 그 피를 밟고서, 저 수많은 발자국들 사이로 그것을 온 집 안에, 아니 거리에까지 묻혀 다니지나 않을까 하는 것을.\n\n그것은 텅 빈 벽난로에 두 눈을 둔 채 앉았다. 그리고 그것이 생각 속에 빠져들었을 때, 방 안으로 한 줄기 빛이, 한 가닥 햇살이 비쳐 들었다. 그것은 전혀 개의치 않고, 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있었다. 별안간 그것은, 무시무시한 얼굴로, 그 죄지은 손에 가슴속에 품은 것을 움켜쥐고 일어났다. 그때 그것은 한 외침에 멈춰 섰다—거칠고, 크고, 가슴을 찌르는 듯한, 사랑이 넘치고, 환희에 찬 외침에—그리고 그는 거울 속에서 자기 자신의 모습만을, 그리고 자기 무릎 곁에서, 자기 딸을 보았다!\n\n그렇다. 그의 딸을! 그녀를 보라! 여기를 보라! 바닥에 엎드려, 그에게 매달려, 그를 부르며, 두 손을 모으고, 그에게 빌고 있는 그녀를.\n\n\"아빠! 사랑하는 아빠! 절 용서해 주세요, 용서해 주세요! 저는 무릎 꿇고 용서를 빌러 돌아왔어요. 그것 없이는 저는 다시는 행복할 수 없어요!\"\n\n여전히 변함이 없었다. 온 세상에서, 변함이 없었다. 그 비참하던 밤과 똑같은 그 얼굴을 그에게 들어 올리며. 그의 용서를 빌면서!\n\n\"사랑하는 아빠, 오, 저를 낯설게 바라보지 마세요! 저는 아빠를 떠날 생각이 결코 없었어요. 그 전에도 그 후에도 그런 건 한 번도 생각해 본 적 없어요. 저는 떠나갈 때 겁에 질려서, 도무지 생각이란 걸 할 수가 없었어요. 아빠, 사랑하는 아빠, 저는 변했어요. 저는 뉘우치고 있어요. 제 잘못을 알아요. 이제는 제 도리를 더 잘 알아요. 아빠, 저를 내치지 마세요, 안 그러면 저는 죽고 말 거예요!\"\n\n그는 비틀거리며 자기 의자로 갔다. 그는 그녀가 자기 두 팔을 자기 목에 둘러 감는 것을 느꼈고, 그녀가 자기 두 팔을 그의 목에 두르는 것을 느꼈으며, 그녀의 입맞춤이 자기 얼굴에 닿는 것을 느꼈고, 그녀의 젖은 뺨이 자기 뺨에 맞닿는 것을 느꼈으며, 느꼈다—오, 얼마나 사무치게!—자기가 저질러 온 그 모든 것을.\n\n자기가 멍들게 했던 그 가슴에, 자기가 거의 부숴 놓았던 그 심장에, 그녀는 이제 두 손으로 가린 그의 얼굴을 뉘었고, 흐느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"아빠, 사랑하는 아빠, 저는 어미가 되었어요. 제게는 머지않아 월터를, 제가 아빠를 부르는 그 이름으로 부르게 될 아이가 있어요. 그 애가 태어났을 때, 그리고 제가 그 애를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알았을 때, 저는 아빠를 떠나면서 제가 무슨 짓을 한 것인지 알게 되었어요. 저를 용서해 주세요, 사랑하는 아빠! 오 부디 저와 제 어린아이에게 하느님의 축복을 빌어 주세요!\"\n\n그는 할 수만 있었다면 그렇게 말했을 것이다. 그는 두 손을 들어 그녀에게 용서를 빌었을 것이나, 그녀가 자기 두 손으로 그 손을 붙잡아, 황급히 끌어 내렸다.\n\n\"제 어린아이는 바다 위에서 태어났어요, 아빠. 저는 하느님께 (그리고 월터도 저를 위해 그랬어요) 제 목숨을 살려 주셔서 집에 돌아올 수 있게 해 달라고 빌었어요. 뭍에 닿을 수 있게 되자마자, 저는 아빠에게로 돌아왔어요. 우리 다시는 갈라지지 말아요, 아빠. 다시는 결코 갈라지지 말아요!\"\n\n이제 백발이 된 그의 머리가 그녀의 팔에 감싸였다. 그리고 그는 그것이 전에는 한 번도, 결코 한 번도 그렇게 안긴 적이 없었음을 생각하며 신음했다.\n\n\"저랑 같이 집에 가요, 아빠, 그래서 제 아기를 보세요. 사내아이예요, 아빠. 그 애 이름은 폴이에요. 제 생각엔—제 바람으로는—그 애가 닮은 것 같아요—\"\n\n그녀의 눈물이 그녀의 말을 막았다.\n\n\"사랑하는 아빠, 제 아이를 봐서, 우리가 그 애에게 지어 준 이름을 봐서, 저를 봐서, 월터를 용서해 주세요. 그이는 제게 무척 다정하고 자상해요. 저는 그이와 너무나 행복해요. 우리가 결혼한 건 그이 잘못이 아니었어요. 제 잘못이었어요. 제가 그이를 너무나 사랑했거든요.\"\n\n그녀는 더한층 다정하게, 더한층 간절하게, 그에게 더 바싹 매달렸다.\n\n\"그이는 제 마음의 보배예요, 아빠. 저는 그이를 위해서라면 죽을 수도 있어요. 그이는 제가 그러듯이 아빠를 사랑하고 공경할 거예요. 우리는 우리 어린아이에게 아빠를 사랑하고 공경하라고 가르칠 거예요. 그리고 그 애가 알아들을 만하면, 아빠에게 한때 그 이름을 가진 아들이 있었는데 그 애가 죽어서 아빠가 무척 슬퍼하셨다고, 하지만 그 애는 하늘나라로 갔고 우리가 쉴 때가 오면 거기서 다들 그 애를 만나길 바란다고 그 애에게 말해 줄 거예요. 입맞춤해 주세요, 아빠, 월터와—제 더없이 소중한 남편과—저더러 돌아오라고 가르쳐 준 그 어린아이의 아버지와 화해하시겠다는 약속으로요. 아빠. 저더러 돌아오라고 가르쳐 준 그 애 말이에요!\"\n\n그녀가 또 한차례 눈물을 쏟으며 그에게 더 바싹 매달릴 때, 그는 그녀의 입술에 입맞추고는, 두 눈을 들어 올리며 말했다, \"오 하느님, 저를 용서해 주소서, 제겐 그것이 너무도 절실하나이다!\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 다시 고개를 떨구고, 그녀를 애통해하며 어루만졌다. 그리고 온 집 안에 오래도록, 오래도록 아무 소리도 없었다. 플로렌스와 함께 스며든 그 찬란한 햇살 속에서, 두 사람은 서로의 품에 꼭 안긴 채 있었다.\n\n그는 그녀의 간청에 고분고분 따르며 외출 채비를 했다. 그리고 비실비실한 걸음걸이로 걸으며, 자기가 그토록 오래 틀어박혀 있던, 거울 속에서 그 그림을 보았던 그 방을 떨리는 몸으로 돌아보면서, 그녀와 함께 현관으로 나섰다. 플로렌스는, 그가 마지막 이별을 새삼 떠올릴까 봐 주위를 거의 둘러보지도 않은 채—그도 그럴 것이 두 사람의 발은 그가 광기에 사로잡혀 그녀를 내리쳤던 바로 그 돌바닥 위에 있었으니—그에게 바싹 붙어, 그의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않고, 그의 팔을 자기에게 두른 채, 문 앞에 기다리고 있던 마차로 그를 이끌어, 그를 데리고 떠났다.\n\n그러자 톡스 양과 폴리가 숨어 있던 데서 나와, 눈물을 흘리며 기뻐 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다. 그러고는 그의 옷가지며 책이며 그 밖의 것들을 무척 정성스레 꾸려, 저녁에 플로렌스가 그것들을 가지러 보낸 어떤 사람들에게 차례에 따라 넘겨주었다. 그러고서 그 두 사람은 외로운 그 집에서 마지막 차 한 잔을 들었다.\n\n\"그러니 돔비와 아들은, 내가 어떤 슬픈 자리에서 말했듯이,\" 톡스 양이 숱한 회상을 마무리 지으며 말했다, \"결국, 폴리, 정말이지 딸이었던 거예요.\"\n\n\"그것도 착한 딸이고요!\" 폴리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"맞아요,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"그리고 폴리, 당신이 그 애가 어린아이였을 때부터 늘 그 애의 동무가 되어 준 건 당신에게 명예로운 일이에요. 당신은 나보다 훨씬 전부터 그 애의 동무였잖아요, 폴리,\" 톡스 양이 말했다. \"그리고 당신은 착한 사람이에요. 로빈!\"\n\n톡스 양은, 형편이 그저 그런 데다 풀 죽은 기색이 역력한, 외진 구석에 앉아 있던 머리통이 탄알처럼 둥근 젊은이에게 말을 건넸다. 일어서자 그는 그라인더의 몸매와 이목구비를 드러내 보였다.\n\n\"로빈,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"방금 너희 어머니에게, 너도 들었겠지만, 그분이 착한 사람이라고 말했단다.\"\n\n\"정말 그러세요, 아가씨,\" 그라인더가 다소 감정을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"좋아, 로빈,\" 톡스 양이 말했다, \"네가 그렇게 말해 주니 기쁘구나. 자, 로빈, 네가 간곡히 청한 대로 너를 우리 집 하인으로 한번 시험 삼아 써 보면서 네가 다시 점잖은 신분을 되찾도록 해 줄 참이니, 나는 이 뜻깊은 자리를 빌려 한마디 하마. 네게는 착한 어머니가 있고 또 늘 있어 왔다는 것을 결코 잊지 말고, 그분께 위안이 되도록 처신하기를 부디 애쓰기 바란다.\"\n\n\"맹세코 그러겠습니다, 아가씨,\" 그라인더가 대답했다. \"저는 별의별 일을 다 겪었고, 이제 제 마음가짐은 한 사내 녀석이—\"\n\n\"제발 그 말버릇은 고쳤으면 좋겠구나, 로빈,\" 톡스 양이 정중하게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그럼, 아가씨, 한 사내놈이—\"\n\n\"고맙다만, 로빈, 아니다,\" 톡스 양이 대답했다, \"나는 한 개인이라고 하는 게 더 좋겠구나.\"\n\n\"한 개인의—,\" 그라인더가 말했다.\n\n\"훨씬 낫구나,\" 톡스 양이 흐뭇하게 한마디 했다. \"한없이 더 적절해!\"\n\n\"—그것만큼 곧을 수 있습니다,\" 로브가 말을 이었다. \"제가 그라인더가 돼 버린 게 아니었다면요, 아가씨, 어머니, 그게 어린 사—개인에게는 더없이 불운한 일이었지만요—\"\n\n\"정말 아주 좋구나,\" 톡스 양이 흐뭇해하며 한마디 했다.\n\n\"—그리고 제가 새들한테 홀려 빗나가지 않고, 그담에 못된 일자리에 빠져들지만 않았어도,\" 그라인더가 말했다, \"저는 더 잘됐을 거예요. 하지만 한 개—\"\n\n\"개인이—\" 톡스 양이 거들었다.\n\n\"—인이 고쳐 나가기엔 결코 늦지 않은 법이죠. 그리고 저는 아가씨의 자비로운 시험 덕분에 고쳐 나가기를 바라고요, 아가씨. 그리고 어머니, 아버지랑 형제자매들에게 제 사랑을 전해 주시길 바라며, 그리 말씀드립니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 들으니 정말 무척 기쁘구나,\" 톡스 양이 한마디 했다. \"떠나기 전에 빵에 버터 바른 것 좀하고 차 한 잔 들겠니, 로빈?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 아가씨,\" 그라인더가 대답했다. 그는 마치 상당 기간 아주 부족한 양만 배급받기라도 했던 것처럼, 곧장 자기 어금니를 더없이 놀라운 솜씨로 놀려 대기 시작했다.\n\n톡스 양이 때맞춰 보닛을 쓰고 숄을 두르고, 폴리도 그러자, 로브는 어머니를 꼭 끌어안고 새 여주인을 따라 떠나갔는데, 그것이 폴리에게 어찌나 희망에 찬 흐뭇함을 안겨 주었던지, 그 뒷모습을 바라보는 그녀의 두 눈에 어린 무언가가 가스등 둘레로 환한 둥근 빛 고리를 만들어 냈다. 그러고서 폴리는 불을 끄고, 집 문을 잠그고, 가까운 곳의 대리인에게 열쇠를 건네주고는, 갈 수 있는 한 빠르게 집으로 갔다. 자기가 뜻밖에 돌아가면 거기서 자아낼 새된 환희를 기뻐하면서. 그 큰 저택은, 그 안에서 겪어 온 그 모든 일과 그것이 지켜본 그 모든 변화에 대해 벙어리가 된 채, 거리를 향해 어둡고 말없는 거인처럼 찌푸린 얼굴로 서 있었다. 이 탐나는 가족용 저택의 임대권을 처분한다는 빤히 노려보는 듯한 공고로, 그 어떤 가까운 캐물음도 가로막으면서.\n\n# CHAPTER LX. Chiefly Matrimonial\n\n블림버 박사 부부가 반년마다 한 번씩 성대하게 여는 잔치—이 점잖은 학교에서 공부하는 모든 어린 신사들에게 일찌감치 참석해 주는 기쁨을 청하고, 시각은 일곱 시 반이며 목적은 카드리유 춤인 그 모임—은 이 무렵에 어김없이 열렸다. 그리고 어린 신사들은 경박하다 할 만한 꼴사나운 기색 없이, 학문으로 배가 잔뜩 부른 상태에서 저마다의 집으로 떠나갔다. 스케틀즈 군은 외국으로 가서, 자기 아버지 바넷 스케틀즈 경의 가문을 영구히 빛내게 되었는데, 그 아버지의 인기 있는 처신은 그에게 외교관 직책을 안겨 주었고, 그 직무의 영예는 그 자신과 스케틀즈 부인에 의해, 심지어 자기 나라 사람들조차 만족할 만큼 잘 수행되었다—이는 거의 기적으로 여겨졌다. 토저 군은 이제 키 큰 청년이 되어 웰링턴 부츠를 신고 다녔는데, 고대에 어찌나 통달했던지 영어 지식에 있어서만큼은 진짜 고대 로마인에 거의 맞먹을 정도였다. 이는 그의 착한 부모의 마음을 더없이 애틋한 감정으로 적셔 놓았으며, 브릭스 군의 아버지와 어머니로 하여금(그의 학식은 어수선하게 꾸린 짐짝처럼 너무 빽빽이 채워져 있어서 정작 필요한 것은 무엇 하나 꺼내 쓸 수가 없었다) 의기소침하여 고개를 숙이게 만들었다. 사실 이 후자의 어린 신사가 지식의 나무에서 애써 따 모은 열매는 어찌나 많은 압력을 받았던지, 일종의 지적인 노퍽 비핀이 되어 본래의 형태나 맛이라고는 하나도 남아 있지 않았다. 비더스톤 군으로 말하자면, 주입식 교육이 그에게는 더 다행스럽고도 드물지 않은 결과를 가져와, 주입 장치가 작동을 멈추자 아무런 흔적도 남기지 않았으므로, 훨씬 편안한 처지였다. 그리고 그는 그때 벵골로 가는 배에 올라 있었는데, 어찌나 감탄스러울 만큼 빠르게 잊어 가고 있던지, 그의 명사 변화법이 항해가 끝날 때까지 버텨 줄지조차 의심스러울 지경이었다.\n* 노퍽 비핀: 오래 눌러 말린 사과의 일종으로 본래 형태를 잃은 모양.\n\n블림버 박사는 으레 하던 대로, 잔치가 있는 날 아침이면 어린 신사들에게 \"여러분, 다음 달 이십오일에 학업을 재개하겠습니다\"라고 말했을 터인데, 이번에는 그 관례에서 벗어나 이렇게 말했다. \"여러분, 우리의 벗 킨킨나투스가 자기 농장으로 물러갔을 때, 그는 자기 후임으로 지명하고자 하는 로마인을 원로원에 내세우지 않았습니다. 그러나 여기 한 로마인이 있습니다.\" 블림버 박사는 피더 군 학사의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"여러분, '아돌레스켄스 임프리미스 그라비스 엣 독투스'(특히 진중하고 학식 있는 젊은이)이니, 물러가는 킨킨나투스인 내가 나의 작은 원로원에 미래의 독재관으로 내세우고자 하는 사람입니다. 여러분, 우리는 다음 달 이십오일에 피더 군 학사의 지도 아래 학업을 재개하겠습니다.\" 이 말에(블림버 박사는 이에 앞서 모든 학부모를 일일이 찾아가 정중하게 설명해 둔 터였다) 어린 신사들은 환호했다. 그리고 토저 군이 나머지 모두를 대표하여 즉시 박사에게 은제 잉크병을 바쳤는데, 모국어는 거의 들어 있지 않고 라틴어 인용이 열다섯 군데, 그리스어 인용이 일곱 군데나 든 연설을 곁들였다. 이것은 더 어린 신사들의 불만과 시샘을 자아냈으니, 그들은 이렇게 투덜거렸다. \"허, 참. 토저 영감이야 잘됐겠지만, 우리가 토저 영감 잘난 척하라고 돈을 모은 건 아니잖아, 안 그래? 토저 영감이 무슨 권리로 남보다 더 나서는 거야? 그의 잉크병도 아닌데. 애들 물건을 왜 가만 놔두질 못해?\" 그러고는 다른 불만의 표현들을 중얼거렸는데, 그것들은 그를 토저 영감이라고 부르는 것 말고는 달리 풀 길을 찾지 못하는 듯했다.\n* 킨킨나투스: 로마의 정치가로, 위기 때 독재관이 되었다가 임무를 마치고 농장으로 돌아간 인물.\n\n피더 군 학사와 아리따운 코르넬리아 블림버 사이에 혼인을 염두에 두고 있다는 따위의 말은 어린 신사들에게 한마디도 새어 나간 적이 없었고, 암시조차 흘려진 적이 없었다. 특히 블림버 박사는, 그 무엇도 자기를 그보다 더 놀라게 할 수는 없다는 듯한 표정을 지으려고 일부러 애쓰는 것 같았다. 그러나 그럼에도 불구하고 어린 신사들은 모두 그것을 아주 잘 알고 있었고, 친척과 친구들의 모임으로 떠날 때 그들은 경외감을 품고 피더 군에게 작별을 고했다.\n\n피더 군의 가장 낭만적인 꿈들이 이루어졌다. 박사는 집 바깥을 새로 칠하고 구석구석 손보기로, 그리고 사업을 그만두고 코르넬리아도 내주기로 결심한 터였다. 칠하고 수리하는 일은 어린 신사들이 떠난 바로 그날 시작되었는데, 이제 보라! 혼례를 올리는 아침이 밝았고, 코르넬리아는 새 안경을 끼고서 혼인의 제단으로 인도되기를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n박식한 다리를 지닌 박사와, 라일락빛 보닛을 쓴 블림버 부인과, 긴 손가락 마디와 뻣뻣한 머리털을 한 피더 군 학사와, 예식을 집전할 피더 군의 형제 알프레드 피더 신부 석사가 모두 응접실에 모여 있었다. 오렌지꽃을 들고 들러리들을 거느린 코르넬리아가 막 내려왔는데, 옛날처럼 외양이 다소 죄어든 듯 보였으나 무척 매력적이었다. 그때 문이 열리고, 눈이 약한 청년이 큰 소리로 다음과 같이 알렸다.\n\n\"투츠 부부 도착이옵니다!\"\n이에 투츠 씨가 들어왔는데, 무척 뚱뚱해져 있었고, 그의 팔짱을 낀 부인은 아주 우아하고 어울리게 차려입었으며 새까만 눈이 무척 반짝였다.\n\n\"블림버 부인,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"제 아내를 소개하게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n블림버 부인은 그녀를 맞이하게 되어 기뻐했다. 블림버 부인은 다소 점잔을 빼긴 했지만 더없이 친절했다.\n\n\"그리고 저를 오래 알고 계시니까,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"단언하건대 이 사람은 여태껏 살았던 여인들 가운데 가장 비범한 여인 중 하나입니다.\"\n\n\"여보!\" 투츠 부인이 나무라듯 말했다.\n\n\"맹세코 정말 그렇습니다,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"정말이지—정말이지, 블림버 부인, 이 사람은 더할 나위 없이 비범한 여인입니다.\"\n\n투츠 부인이 명랑하게 웃었고, 블림버 부인은 그녀를 코르넬리아에게로 데려갔다. 투츠 씨는 그쪽을 향해 인사를 올리고, 자기의 옛 스승에게 경례를 했는데, 스승은 그의 부부 생활을 두고 이렇게 말했다. \"허, 투츠, 허, 투츠! 그래, 자네도 우리 같은 사람이 되었군그래, 응, 투츠?\" 그러고서 투츠 씨는 피더 군 학사와 함께 창가로 물러났다.\n\n피더 군 학사는 기분이 한껏 들떠서, 투츠 씨에게 권투하는 시늉을 하더니 손등으로 그의 가슴뼈를 솜씨 좋게 톡 쳤다.\n\n\"이봐, 친구!\" 피더 군이 웃으며 말했다. \"이야! 우리도 드디어! 걸려들어서 끝장났구먼. 그렇지?\"\n\n\"피더,\" 투츠 씨가 응수했다. \"축하하네. 자네가 나만큼이나—나만큼이나—나만큼이나 부부 생활에서 더없이 행복하다면, 더 바랄 게 없을 걸세.\"\n\n\"보다시피 나는 옛 친구들을 잊지 않아,\" 피더 군이 말했다. \"내 혼례에 부른 거잖나, 투츠.\"\n\n\"피더,\" 투츠 씨가 진지하게 응수했다, \"사실은 말이지, 내 혼례가 치러질 때까지는 자네에게 연락할 수 없게 만든 몇 가지 사정이 있었네. 첫째로, 나는 돔비 양에 관한 일로 자네에게 완전히 짐승처럼 굴었지. 그래서 자네가 내 혼례에 초대받으면 당연히 돔비 양과 하는 혼례인 줄로 기대할 거라고 느꼈고, 그러면 설명이 필요해질 텐데, 맹세코 그 시점에서는 그 설명이 나를 완전히 무너뜨렸을 걸세. 둘째로, 우리 혼례는 엄격하게 비공개였네. 참석한 사람은 나와 투츠 부인의 친구 한 사람뿐이었는데, 그는—어디 소속인지는 정확히 모르겠지만—함장이라네.\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"하지만 그건 중요한 게 아니지. 피더, 투츠 부인과 내가 해외 여행을 떠나기 전에 무슨 일이 있었는지 적어 보냈으니, 우정의 도리는 충분히 다했기를 바라네.\"\n\n\"투츠, 이 친구야,\" 피더 군이 그의 두 손을 흔들며 말했다, \"농담이었어.\"\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 피더,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"내 혼인을 자네가 어떻게 생각하는지 들려준다면 기쁘겠네.\"\n\n\"훌륭해!\" 피더 군이 응수했다.\n\n\"훌륭하다고 생각한단 말이지, 피더?\" 투츠 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"그렇다면 그게 내게는 얼마나 훌륭하겠나! 자네는 저 사람이 얼마나 비범한 여인인지 결코 알 수 없을 테니까.\"\n\n피더 군은 기꺼이 그렇다고 인정하려 했다. 그러나 투츠 씨는 고개를 내저으며, 그것이 가능하다는 말은 들으려고 하지 않았다.\n\n\"있잖나,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"내가 아내에게 바란 것은—한마디로, 지각이었네. 돈은, 피더, 내게 있었지. 지각은—나는—특별히 없었어.\"\n\n피더 군이 \"아, 아니, 자네도 있었지, 투츠!\" 하고 중얼거렸다. 그러나 투츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 피더, 나는 없었네. 왜 감추겠나? 없었어. 나는 지각이라는 게 바로 저기 있다는 걸 알았지.\" 투츠 씨가 자기 아내 쪽으로 손을 뻗으며 말했다, \"그것도 잔뜩. 신분 문제로 반대하거나 기분 상해 할 친척도 내겐 없었네. 친척이라곤 없었으니까. 내겐 후견인 말고는 내게 딸린 사람이 평생 아무도 없었는데, 그 사람을, 피더, 나는 늘 해적이자 바다도둑이라고 여겨 왔지. 그러니, 알겠지만,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"내가 그 사람의 의견을 받아들일 리는 없었지.\"\n\n\"그렇지,\" 피더 군이 말했다.\n\n\"그래서,\" 투츠 씨가 이어 말했다, \"나는 내 뜻대로 행동했네. 그렇게 한 날은 빛나는 날이었지! 피더! 저 여인의 지력이 어느 정도인지는 나밖에 모를 걸세. 만일 언젠가 여성의 권리, 그런 부류의 모든 것이 제대로 다뤄진다면, 그건 저 사람의 강력한 지성을 통해서일 걸세—수전, 여보!\" 투츠 씨가 갑자기 창밖을 내다보며 말했다, \"제발 무리하지 마!\"\n\n\"여보,\" 투츠 부인이 말했다, \"그냥 얘기하고 있었을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 여보,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"제발 무리하지 마. 정말 조심해야 돼. 그러지 마, 수전, 무리하지 마. 이 사람은 너무 쉽게 흥분해서,\" 투츠 씨가 블림버 부인에게만 들리도록 말했다, \"그러고 나면 의사 선생 말은 까맣게 잊어버린답니다.\"\n\n블림버 부인이 투츠 부인에게 조심해야 한다고 일러 주고 있을 때, 피더 군 학사가 그녀에게 팔을 내밀어, 교회로 가려고 기다리고 있는 마차들로 데려갔다. 블림버 박사는 투츠 부인을 모셨다. 투츠 씨는 아리따운 신부를 모셨는데, 그녀의 반짝이는 안경 둘레로 얇은 드레스를 입은 두 작은 들러리들이 나방처럼 팔랑거렸다. 피더 군의 형제 알프레드 피더 석사는 이미 앞서가서 자기의 공식 직무를 맡고 있었다.\n\n예식은 훌륭하게 거행되었다. 곱슬곱슬 잔머리를 한 코르넬리아는, 치킨이라면 말했을 법한 표현대로, 아주 침착하게 \"입장했고\", 블림버 박사는 단단히 마음을 굳힌 사람처럼 그녀를 신랑에게 넘겨주었다. 얇은 드레스를 입은 작은 들러리들이 가장 괴로워하는 것 같았다. 블림버 부인은 감동했으나 그저 가만히 그러했다. 그녀는 돌아오는 길에 알프레드 피더 신부 석사에게, 키케로가 투스쿨룸에 은거해 있는 모습을 볼 수만 있었다면 이제 더는 이루지 못한 소원이 하나도 없었을 거라고 말했다.\n\n* 키케로: 로마의 정치가·문인으로, 투스쿨룸의 별장에 은거했다.\n그 뒤에는 아침 식사가 있었는데, 똑같은 소규모 일행으로 한정되었다. 거기서 피더 군 학사는 어마어마하게 흥이 올랐고, 그것이 투츠 부인에게도 옮아붙어서, 투츠 씨가 식탁 너머로 \"여보, 수전, 무리하지 마!\" 하고 말하는 소리가 여러 번 들렸다. 그중 압권은, 투츠 씨가 연설을 하는 것이 자기 책무라고 느낀 점이었다. 그래서 투츠 부인의 온갖 전신 신호식 만류에도 불구하고, 그는 난생처음 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"저는 정말이지,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"이 집에서—이따금 어떤—어떤 정신의 혼란이라 할 만한 일이 제게 가해지긴 했지만, 그건 중요하지 않고 누구를 탓하지도 않습니다—언제나 블림버 박사 댁 식구처럼 대접받았고, 상당 기간 제 책상도 따로 가졌던 터라—제 친구 피더가—이렇게 되는 것을—그냥—두고—볼—수가—없습니다—\"\n\n투츠 부인이 \"결혼하는 것을\" 하고 거들었다.\n\n\"이 자리에 어울리지 않거나 아주 흥미가 없지는 않을 것 같습니다만,\" 투츠 씨가 기뻐하는 얼굴로 말했다, \"제 아내가 더없이 비범한 여인이고, 저보다 이걸 훨씬 잘했으리라는 점을 말씀드리겠습니다—제 친구 피더가 결혼하는 것을—특히—\"\n\n투츠 부인이 \"블림버 양과\" 하고 거들었다.\n\n\"피더 부인과 말이오, 여보!\" 투츠 씨가 사적인 의논을 하듯 낮은 어조로 말했다. \"'하느님이 짝지어 주신 것을', 알다시피, '사람이'—알지 않소? 저는 제 친구 피더가 결혼하는 것을—특히 피더 부인과 결혼하는 것을—그분들을 위한—그분들을 위한—건배를 제의하지 않고서는 그냥 둘 수가 없습니다. 그러니,\" 투츠 씨가 마치 드높은 비상에 필요한 영감을 구하듯 아내에게 눈을 고정한 채 말했다, \"혼인의 횃불이 기쁨의 봉화가 되기를, 그리고 오늘 우리가 그분들의 길에 뿌린 꽃들이—그—우울을—몰아내는 것이—되기를 빕니다!\"\n\n은유를 좋아하는 블림버 박사가 이 말에 흡족하여 \"아주 좋아, 투츠! 정말 잘 말했네, 투츠!\" 하고는 고개를 끄덕이며 손뼉을 쳤다. 피더 군이 답례로 감상이 섞인 익살스러운 연설을 했다. 그 뒤 알프레드 피더 석사는 블림버 박사 부부를 두고 무척 기분 좋게 말했고, 피더 군 학사는 그에 못지않게 얇은 드레스를 입은 작은 들러리들을 두고 그러했다. 그러자 블림버 박사가 낭랑한 목소리로, 자기와 블림버 부인이 거하려는 골풀밭과 그들의 오두막 둘레를 윙윙 맴돌 벌에 관하여 목가적인 양식으로 몇 가지 생각을 펼쳤다. 그 직후, 박사의 두 눈이 유난히 반짝이고, 그의 사위가 이미 시간이란 노예를 위해 만들어진 거라고 평하면서 투츠 부인이 노래를 부르느냐고 물어 왔으므로, 신중한 블림버 부인이 모임을 파하고 코르넬리아를 떠나보냈다. 코르넬리아는 더없이 차분하고 편안하게, 마음의 임과 함께 역마차에 올랐다.\n\n투츠 부부는 베드퍼드 호텔로 물러났다(투츠 부인은 옛날 처녀 시절 니퍼라는 이름으로 그곳에 와 본 적이 있었다). 그리고 거기서 편지 한 통을 발견했는데, 투츠 씨가 그것을 읽는 데 어찌나 엄청난 시간이 걸렸던지 투츠 부인이 겁을 먹었다.\n\n\"여보, 수전,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"겁먹는 건 무리하는 것보다 더 나빠. 제발 진정해!\"\n\n\"누구한테서 온 거예요?\" 투츠 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 여보,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"길스 함장한테서야. 흥분하지 마. 월터스와 돔비 양이 귀국한다는군!\"\n\n\"여보,\" 투츠 부인이 소파에서 재빨리 몸을 일으키며, 새하얗게 질린 채 말했다, \"날 속이려 하지 마요, 소용없으니까. 돌아오신 거죠—당신 얼굴에 환히 다 보여요!\"\n\n\"이 사람은 더없이 비범한 여인이야!\" 투츠 씨가 황홀한 감탄에 잠겨 외쳤다. \"당신 말이 완벽하게 맞아, 여보, 그분들이 돌아왔어. 돔비 양이 아버지를 만났고, 두 분이 화해했어!\"\n\n\"화해했다고요!\" 투츠 부인이 손뼉을 치며 외쳤다.\n\n\"여보,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"제발 무리하지 마. 의사 선생을 꼭 기억해! 길스 함장 말로는—적어도 그렇게 말한 건 아니지만, 내가 헤아려 낼 수 있는 바로는, 그가 뜻하는 건—돔비 양이 불운한 아버지를 옛집에서 모셔 나와, 자기와 월터스가 사는 집으로 옮겼다는 거야. 그 댁에서 그분이 몹시 앓아누워 있는데—임종이 가깝다고 여겨지고—밤낮으로 그녀가 곁에서 간호한다는 거지.\"\n\n투츠 부인이 몹시 서럽게 울기 시작했다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 수전,\" 투츠 씨가 응수했다, \"제발, 제발, 할 수 있는 한, 의사 선생을 기억해! 못 하겠으면, 그건 중요하지 않으니까—그래도 꼭 애써 봐!\"\n\n그의 아내는 옛 태도가 문득 되살아나서, 자기의 소중한 아가, 자기의 어린 아씨, 자기의 사랑하는 분에게 데려가 달라고 어찌나 애처롭게 애원하던지, 그런 식의 온갖 말로 빌어 대니, 동정심과 감탄이 누구보다 강렬한 투츠 씨는 진심으로 마음 깊은 곳에서 응낙했다. 그래서 그들은 즉시 떠나, 함장의 편지에 답하여 모습을 드러내기로 합의했다.\n\n그런데 사물들 사이의 어떤 숨은 인연, 혹은 어떤 우연의 일치가, 바로 그날 함장 본인을(투츠 부부가 곧 그를 향해 떠날 참이었다) 혼인이라는 꽃길의 행렬에 끌어들였다. 주역으로서가 아니라 조역으로서였다. 그것은 우연히 일어났는데, 사연은 이러했다.\n\n함장은 플로렌스와 그녀의 아기를 잠시나마 보고 한없이 흐뭇해한 뒤, 월터와 긴 이야기를 나누고 나서 산책을 나섰다. 인간사의 변천을 두고 홀로 명상에 잠길 필요를 느꼈고, 돔비 씨의 몰락을 두고 자기의 번들거리는 모자를 깊이 흔들 필요를 느꼈기 때문이었다. 돔비 씨에 대해서는, 그의 천성의 너그러움과 순박함이 생생하게 일깨워진 터였다. 함장은 그 불행한 신사 때문에 정말이지 몹시 풀이 죽었을 터였으나, 아기 생각이 떠오르면 그렇지 않았다. 그 생각은 떠오를 때마다 그에게 어찌나 강렬한 만족을 주었던지, 그는 길을 걸으며 소리 내어 웃었고, 실로 한 번 이상, 갑작스러운 기쁨의 충동에 번들거리는 모자를 공중에 던졌다가 다시 받아 들곤 하여, 구경꾼들을 적잖이 놀라게 했다. 이 두 가지 상반된 명상거리가 함장에게 안긴 명암의 빠른 교차는 그의 마음에 어찌나 힘겨운 시련이던지, 그는 마음을 가라앉히기 위해 긴 산책이 필요하다고 느꼈다. 그리고 조화로운 연상의 영향에는 큰 힘이 있는 법이므로, 그는 이 산책의 무대로 자기 옛 동네를 골랐다. 돛대며 노며 도르래 만드는 이들, 배 비스킷 굽는 이들, 석탄 부리는 인부들, 역청 끓이는 가마들, 뱃사람들, 운하들, 부두들, 도개교들, 그 밖에 마음을 달래 주는 온갖 풍경 속으로.\n\n이 평화로운 풍경들, 특히 라임하우스 홀과 그 일대는 함장의 마음을 가라앉히는 데 어찌나 효험이 있던지, 그는 평정을 되찾아 걸어갔고, 사실 숨죽인 소리로 '어여쁜 페그'의 발라드를 흥얼거리며 즐기고 있었다. 그런데 길모퉁이를 도는 순간, 그는 자기를 향해 다가오는 의기양양한 행렬을 보고는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 굳어져 말을 잃었다.\n\n이 무시무시한 광경의 선두에는 그 단호한 여인 맥스팅거 부인이 서 있었다. 그녀는 추호의 흔들림 없는 결의의 표정을 지킨 채, 자기의 완강한 가슴팍에 어마어마한 시계와 그 부속물들을 보란 듯이 매달고 있었는데, 함장은 그것이 번스비의 물건임을 한눈에 알아보았다. 그녀는 다름 아닌 그 현명한 뱃사람을 팔짱에 끼고 인도하고 있었고, 그는 낯선 땅으로 끌려가는 포로의 넋 나가고 우울한 얼굴로, 그녀의 뜻에 고분고분 자신을 내맡기고 있었다. 그들 뒤로는 어린 맥스팅거들이 떼를 지어 의기양양하게 나타났다. 그 뒤로는 무섭고 굳센 인상의 두 부인이, 키 작은 신사 하나를 가운데 끼고 인도해 오고 있었는데, 그 신사는 높은 모자를 쓰고 역시 의기양양해했다. 행렬 맨 뒤에는 우산을 든 번스비의 소년이 나타났다. 일행 전체가 잘 갖춰진 행군 대형을 이루었고, 그 무리에 감도는 무시무시한 멋스러움은, 설령 부인들의 담대한 표정이 없었더라도, 이것이 제물을 바치는 행렬이며 그 제물이 번스비임을 충분히 알려 주었을 것이다.\n\n함장의 첫 충동은 달아나는 것이었다. 이것은 번스비의 첫 충동이기도 한 듯했는데, 그 실행은 부질없을 것이 뻔했다. 그러나 일행 쪽에서 알아보는 외침이 터져 나오고, 알렉산더 맥스팅거가 두 팔을 벌리고 함장에게 달려오니, 함장은 깃발을 내렸다.\n\n\"이런, 커틀 함장님!\" 맥스팅거 부인이 말했다. \"이거야말로 정말 뜻밖의 만남이군요! 이제 저는 아무 원한도 없어요, 커틀 함장님—제가 무슨 트집을 잡을까 봐 걱정하실 것 없어요. 저는 다른 마음으로 제단에 나아가기를 바라니까요.\" 여기서 맥스팅거 부인은 잠시 멈추더니, 몸을 곧추세우고 긴 숨으로 가슴을 부풀리며, 제물을 가리켜 말했다. \"제 '남편'이옵니다, 커틀 함장님!\"\n\n비굴해진 번스비는 오른쪽도 왼쪽도, 자기 신부도 자기 친구도 보지 않고, 그저 똑바로 앞을, 아무것도 아닌 곳을 응시했다. 함장이 손을 내미니 번스비도 손을 내밀었으나, 함장의 인사에 답하여 한마디 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"커틀 함장님,\" 맥스팅거 부인이 말했다, \"지난 적의를 풀고 싶으시다면, 그리고 제 '남편'인 함장님 친구가 독신으로서 마지막인 모습을 보고 싶으시다면, 저희와 함께 예배당에 와 주시면 '기쁘'겠어요. 여기 한 부인이 계신데,\" 맥스팅거 부인이 둘 중 더 담대한 쪽을 향해 돌아서며 말했다, \"제 들러리예요, 함장님의 보호를 받으면 기뻐할 거예요, 커틀 함장님.\"\n\n높은 모자를 쓴 키 작은 신사는, 알고 보니 다른 부인의 남편이었는데, 한 동료 인간이 자기와 같은 처지로 전락하는 것을 분명 고소해하는 듯, 이 말에 자리를 비켜 주며 그 부인을 커틀 함장에게 넘겼다. 부인은 즉시 그를 붙들고는, 지체할 시간이 없다고 말하며, 우렁찬 목소리로 전진하라는 호령을 내렸다.\n\n함장이 자기 친구를 걱정하는 마음은, 처음에는 자기 자신에 대한 약간의 걱정과 뒤섞이지 않을 수 없었다—혹시 강제로 결혼당할지 모른다는 어렴풋한 공포가 그를 사로잡았던 것이다. 그러다 예식 절차에 대한 자기 지식이 그를 구해 주었고, \"예, 하겠습니다\"라고 말해야 하는 법적 의무를 떠올린 그는, 무슨 질문을 받든 또렷이 \"안 하겠습니다\"라고 대답하기로 작정하는 한 자기는 안전하다고 느꼈다—이 모든 것이 그를 땀투성이로 만들었고, 한동안은 그가 이제 한 부분을 이루게 된 그 행렬의 움직임도, 자기의 아리따운 동행이 하는 이야기도 알아차리지 못하게 만들었다. 그러나 마음이 덜 동요하게 되자, 그는 이 부인에게서 다음을 알게 되었다. 그녀는 세관에 근무했던 보컴 씨의 미망인이며, 맥스팅거 부인의 가장 절친한 벗으로 그녀를 자기 성별의 귀감으로 여기고 있고, 함장에 대해서는 자주 들어 왔으며 이제 그가 지난 삶을 뉘우쳤기를 바라고, 번스비 씨가 자기가 얼마나 큰 복을 얻었는지 알고 있기를 믿지만, 남자들이란 그런 복을 잃기 전에는 그것이 무엇인지 좀처럼 알지 못하지 않을까 두렵다는 등, 그 비슷한 이야기를 더 늘어놓았다.\n\n이러는 내내 함장은, 보컴 부인이 신랑에게서 줄곧 눈을 떼지 않는다는 것과, 골목길이나 달아나기에 알맞아 보이는 좁은 모퉁이에 가까워질 때마다 그녀가 그가 탈출을 시도하면 길을 끊으려고 바짝 경계한다는 것을 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다. 다른 부인 역시 자기 남편인 높은 모자 쓴 키 작은 신사와 마찬가지로, 미리 짜 둔 계획에 따라 분명히 경계 태세였다. 그리고 그 가련한 남자는 맥스팅거 부인에게 어찌나 단단히 붙들려 있던지, 달아나서 살아남으려는 어떤 노력도 부질없게 되어 버렸다. 이는 실로 한낱 구경꾼들에게도 빤히 보였으니, 그들은 야유와 고함으로 그 사실에 대한 자기들의 통찰을 드러냈다. 그 모든 것에 무서운 맥스팅거는 요지부동 무관심했고, 번스비 자신은 아무것도 의식하지 못하는 상태인 듯했다.\n\n함장은 단음절로든 신호로든 그 철학자에게 말을 걸어 보려고 여러 번 시도했으나, 경계병의 빈틈없는 감시 탓에, 그리고 번스비의 기질에 늘 따라붙는 특유의 난점—어떤 외적이고 가시적인 표시로도 그의 주의를 일깨우기 어렵다는 점—탓에 번번이 실패했다. 그렇게 그들은 예배당에 다다랐다. 말끔하게 회칠한 건물로, 멜키세데크 하울러 신부가 최근에 빌린 곳이었다. 그는 간곡한 청을 받고서, 세상이 이 년 더 존속하도록 허락하기로 했으나, 그다음에는 세상이 반드시 끝나야 한다고 자기 신도들에게 일러둔 터였다.\n\n멜키세데크 신부가 즉흥 기도를 올리고 있는 동안, 함장은 신랑의 귀에 대고 으르렁거릴 기회를 잡았다.\n\n\"기운 좀 어떤가, 이 사람아, 기운 좀 어때?\"\n\n이에 번스비는 멜키세데크 신부의 존재 따위는 잊은 채—그의 절박한 처지가 아니고서는 무엇으로도 용서받을 수 없을 만큼—대답했다.\n\n\"빌어먹게 나빠.\"\n\n\"잭 번스비,\" 함장이 속삭였다, \"자네 이걸 자네 자유 의지로 하는 건가?\"\n\n번스비 씨가 \"아니\" 하고 답했다.\n\n\"그럼 왜 하는 건가, 이 사람아?\" 함장이 자연스럽게 물었다.\n\n번스비는 여전히, 변함없는 표정으로 세상의 반대편을 응시한 채, 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"왜 줄행랑을 안 치나?\" 함장이 말했다. \"응?\" 번스비가 한순간 희망의 빛을 번득이며 속삭였다.\n\n\"줄행랑을 치게,\" 함장이 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 소용이 있겠나?\" 의기소침한 현자가 응수했다. \"또 날 붙잡고 말 텐데.\"\n\n\"해 보게!\" 함장이 응수했다. \"힘내! 자! 지금이 기회야. 줄행랑을 치게, 잭 번스비!\"\n\n그러나 잭 번스비는 그 충고를 따르기는커녕, 구슬픈 속삭임으로 말했다.\n\n\"이게 다 자네 그 궤짝에서 비롯됐어. 그날 밤 내가 왜 그 여자를 항구로 데려다줬을까?\"\n\n\"이 사람아,\" 함장이 더듬거렸다, \"나는 자네가 그 여자를 휘어잡은 줄 알았지, 그 여자가 자네를 휘어잡은 게 아니라. 자네처럼 그런 식견을 가진 사람이!\"\n\n번스비 씨는 그저 억눌린 신음만 토해 냈다.\n\n\"자!\" 함장이 팔꿈치로 그를 쿡 찌르며 말했다, \"지금이 기회야! 줄행랑을 치게! 내가 자네 퇴각을 엄호하지. 시간이 가고 있어. 번스비! 자유를 위해서야. 한 번만 해 보겠나?\"\n\n번스비는 미동도 없었다.\n\n\"번스비!\" 함장이 속삭였다, \"두 번째로 해 보겠나?\"\n\n번스비는 두 번째도 하지 않으려 했다.\n\n\"번스비!\" 함장이 다그쳤다, \"자유를 위해서야. 세 번째로 해 보겠나? 지금 아니면 영영 못 해!\"\n\n번스비는 그때도 하지 않았고, 끝내 영영 하지 않았다. 맥스팅거 부인이 그 직후 곧바로 그와 결혼해 버렸기 때문이다.\n\n함장에게 이 예식에서 가장 끔찍했던 광경 중 하나는, 줄리아나 맥스팅거가 거기서 보인 섬뜩한 흥미와, 그 앞날이 촉망되는 아이—이미 자기 어미를 빼닮은—가 온 절차를 지켜보던 그 무서운 정신 집중이었다. 함장은 거기서, 끝없이 뻗어 나가는 일련의 덫을 보았다. 뱃사람의 혈통이 운명적으로 짊어진, 억압과 강제의 시대가 줄줄이 이어지는 것을 보았다. 그것은 보컴 부인과 다른 부인의 흔들림 없는 굳건함이나, 높은 모자 쓴 키 작은 신사의 의기양양함, 심지어 맥스팅거 부인의 무시무시한 완강함보다도 더 잊지 못할 광경이었다. 어린 맥스팅거 사내아이들은 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 거의 이해하지 못했고 관심도 더더욱 없었다. 그들은 예식 내내 주로 서로의 반장화를 밟는 데 몰두해 있었다. 그러나 그 한심한 아이들이 빚어낸 대조는, 줄리아나에게 깃든 조숙한 여인을 도리어 돋보이게 하고 장식해 줄 뿐이었다. 일이 년만 더 지나면, 저 아이가 사는 곳에 머문다는 것은 파멸이리라고 함장은 생각했다.\n\n예식은 어린 식구들이 일제히 번스비 씨에게 달려드는 것으로 마무리되었는데, 그들은 그를 아버지라는 다정한 이름으로 부르며 그에게 동전을 졸라 댔다. 이렇게 애정이 쏟아지는 일이 끝나고 행렬이 다시 출발하려는 참에, 알렉산더 맥스팅거의 뜻밖의 격정 탓에 잠시 지체되었다. 그 귀여운 아이는, 보아하니 예배당이 평소의 종교 의식이 아닌 다른 목적으로 들어서는 곳일 때면 예배당과 묘비를 연결지어 생각했던 듯, 자기 어머니가 이제 점잖게 매장되어 자기에게서 영영 사라져 버린다고 굳게 믿었고 도무지 다른 말로는 설득되지 않았다. 이 확신의 고통 속에서 그는 놀라운 기세로 비명을 질러 댔고 얼굴이 시퍼레졌다. 다정한 성품을 보여 주는 이런 표시들이 어미에게 아무리 감동적이었다 한들, 그것을 알아주는 마음이 나약함으로 흐르도록 내버려 두는 것은 그 비범한 여인의 성격에 없는 일이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 흔들고, 쿡쿡 찌르고, 고함치고, 그 비슷하게 그의 머리를 다루는 방법으로 그의 이성을 일깨우려 했으나 헛되자, 그를 바깥으로 데리고 나가 다른 방법을 써 보았다. 그것은 혼례 일행에게, 박수 소리를 닮은 날카로운 소리가 빠르게 연달아 들리고, 뒤이어 알렉산더가 안마당에서 가장 차가운 포석에 닿은 채 잔뜩 벌게진 얼굴로 큰 소리로 통탄하는 모습으로 드러났다.\n\n그러고 나서 행렬이 다시 대형을 갖추어, 혼례 잔치가 차려진 브리그 플레이스로 갈 채비가 되자, 왔던 대로 돌아갔다. 가는 길에 번스비는 최근 얻은 행복을 두고 구경꾼들로부터 익살스러운 축하를 숱하게 받았다. 함장은 그 집 문 앞까지 동행했으나, 보컴 부인의 한결 부드러워진 태도에 불안해진 나머지—이제 그녀는 자기에게 맡겨졌던 막중한 임무에서 풀려나(부인들의 경계심과 민첩함은 신랑이 무사히 결혼하자 눈에 띄게 줄어들었다) 그를 위해 관심을 보일 여유가 더 많아진 터였다—그 집과 포로를 거기 두고 자리를 떴다. 약속이 있다고 가냘프게 둘러대고는, 곧 돌아오겠다고 약속했다. 함장에게는 불안할 또 다른 이유가 있었으니, 비록 분명 그럴 뜻은 없었고 그 철학자의 능력에 대한 끝없는 믿음 때문이긴 했으나, 자기가 번스비를 덫에 빠뜨린 첫 번째 원인이었다는 것을 후회스럽게 곱씹었기 때문이다.\n\n목조 항해사 가게에 있는 노인 솔 길스에게로 돌아가면서, 돔비 씨가 어떤지 먼저 들러 묻지 않는 것은—비록 그가 누워 있는 집이 런던 바깥에, 멀리 어느 싱그러운 황야의 경계에 있긴 했으나—함장의 행로에서 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 그래서 그는 지치면 수레를 얻어 타며, 즐겁게 그 길을 헤쳐 나갔다.\n\n블라인드가 내려져 있고 집이 어찌나 고요하던지, 함장은 노크하기조차 거의 겁이 났다. 그러나 문에 귀를 대 보니, 안에서 바로 가까이 낮은 목소리들이 들렸고, 가만히 두드리자 투츠 씨가 그를 들여보내 주었다. 사실 투츠 씨와 그의 아내는 막 도착한 참이었다. 항해사 가게로 함장을 찾으러 갔다가, 거기서 주소를 알아낸 것이었다.\n\n그렇게 막 도착한 것치고는, 투츠 부인은 어느새 누군가에게서 아기를 받아 안고 계단에 앉아, 아기를 품에 안고 어르고 있었다. 플로렌스가 그녀 곁에 몸을 굽히고 있었다. 투츠 부인이 어미와 아이 중 어느 쪽을 더 안고 어르는지, 또 플로렌스가 투츠 부인을 더 애틋해하는지 투츠 부인이 플로렌스를 더 애틋해하는지, 아니면 둘 다 아기를 더 애틋해하는지, 아무도 가려낼 수 없었을 것이다. 그것은 그토록 사랑과 격정이 어린 작은 무리였다.\n\n\"그래, 아버님께서 많이 편찮으세요, 사랑하는 플로이 아씨?\" 수전이 물었다.\n\n\"정말, 정말 편찮으셔,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"하지만, 수전, 얘, 너 옛날처럼 나한테 그렇게 말하면 안 돼. 그런데 이게 뭐야?\" 플로렌스가 그녀의 옷을 만지며 놀라서 말했다. \"옛날 네 옷이잖아, 얘? 옛날 네 모자랑 잔머리랑 다?\"\n\n수전이 울음을 터뜨리며, 자기를 그토록 신기해하며 만져 준 그 작은 손에 입맞춤을 쏟아부었다.\n\n\"돔비 양,\" 투츠 씨가 앞으로 나서며 말했다, \"제가 설명하지요. 이 사람은 더없이 비범한 여인입니다. 이 사람에 견줄 만한 이는 많지 않아요! 이 사람은 늘 말해 왔지요—우리가 결혼하기 전부터, 그리고 오늘까지도 말해 왔습니다—아씨가 집에 돌아오시면, 아씨를 모실 때 입던 옷 말고 다른 옷차림으로는 아씨께 가지 않겠다고요. 자기가 낯설어 보여서 아씨가 자기를 덜 좋아하실까 봐서요. 저로 말하면 그 옷이,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"무엇보다도 좋습니다. 그 옷을 입은 이 사람을 흠모하지요! 돔비 양, 이 사람은 다시 아씨의 하녀가, 아씨의 유모가, 예전에 그랬던 모든 것이, 아니 그 이상이 되어 드릴 겁니다. 이 사람은 조금도 변하지 않았어요. 그런데, 수전, 여보,\" 투츠 씨가 깊은 감정과 드높은 감탄을 담아 말하더니 덧붙였다, \"내가 바라는 건 단 하나, 당신이 의사 선생을 기억하고, 너무 무리하지 않는 것뿐이야!\"\n\n# CHAPTER LXI. Relenting\n\n플로렌스에게는 도움이 필요했다. 아버지에게도 그 필요가 절실했고, 그래서 옛 친구의 도움이 더없이 귀중해졌다. 죽음이 그의 머리맡에 서 있었다. 한때의 그가 남긴 그림자에 지나지 않게, 정신은 무너지고 몸은 위태롭게 병든 채로, 그는 딸의 손이 마련해 준 침대에 지친 머리를 뉘었고, 그 뒤로 다시는 머리를 들지 못했다.\n\n딸은 늘 그의 곁에 있었다. 그는 대체로 딸을 알아보았다. 다만 머릿속이 혼미해지면, 자기가 딸에게 말을 건네는 상황을 흔히 뒤죽박죽으로 만들곤 했다. 그래서 때로는 마치 자기 아들이 갓 죽은 것처럼 딸에게 말을 건넸다. 그러고는 비록 그 작은 침대 곁에서 딸이 시중든 일을 자기가 입에 올리지는 않았지만, 그래도 자기는 그것을 보았노라고—보았노라고 딸에게 말하고는, 얼굴을 가리고 흐느끼며 야윈 손을 내밀곤 했다. 때로는 딸 본인에 대해 그녀를 찾기도 했다. \"플로렌스는 어디 있느냐?\" \"여기 있어요, 아빠, 여기 있어요.\" \"나는 그 애를 모른다!\" 그는 부르짖곤 했다. \"우리가 너무 오래 떨어져 있어서, 나는 그 애를 모른다!\" 그러고는 딸이 그의 동요를 달래 주고, 다른 때라면 그토록 애써 닦아 내려던 눈물을 다시 불러낼 때까지, 멍한 두려움이 그를 사로잡곤 했다.\n\n그는 옛날 자기가 좇던 일들의 장면들을 두서없이 헤매었다—그 가운데 많은 곳에서 플로렌스는 귀 기울여 듣다가 그를 놓쳐 버렸다—때로는 몇 시간씩. 그는 그 어린아이 같은 물음을, \"돈이 뭐야?\"를 되풀이하고는, 그것을 곰곰 따지고, 그것에 대해 생각하고, 좋은 답을 찾으려 스스로와 다소 조리 있게 따져 보았다. 마치 바로 그 순간까지 한 번도 그런 물음을 받아 본 적이 없는 것처럼. 그는 자기 옛 회사의 이름을 이만 번이나 골똘히 되풀이하면서, 그 한 번 한 번마다 베개 위에서 머리를 돌리곤 했다. 그는 자기 자식들을 세어 보곤 했다—하나—둘—멈추고, 다시 돌아가, 똑같은 식으로 다시 시작했다.\n\n그러나 이것은 그의 정신이 가장 혼란스러운 상태에 빠졌을 때의 일이었다. 그 병의 다른 모든 국면에서, 그리고 가장 한결같이 머무는 국면에서, 그의 정신은 언제나 플로렌스에게로 향했다. 그가 가장 자주 하는 것은 이러했다. 그는 그토록 최근에 떠올린 그 밤을, 딸이 자기 방으로 내려왔던 그 밤을 되새기면서, 자기 마음이 자기를 찔러 후회하게 만들었고 그래서 자기가 딸을 뒤따라 나가 계단을 올라 딸을 찾았다고 상상하곤 했다. 그러다가 그 시간을, 그 숱한 발소리가 나던 훗날과 뒤섞으면서, 그 발소리의 수에 놀라며 딸을 뒤따라가면서 그것을 세기 시작했다. 그러면 문득, 다른 발소리들 사이로 피 묻은 발자국 하나가 지나갔다. 그리고 그 뒤로는 이따금씩, 문들이 열린 채 서 있기 시작했는데, 그 너머로 거울 속에 어떤 끔찍한 그림들이 보였다—초췌한 사내들이 가슴속에 무언가를 감추고 있는 그림이었다. 그래도 그 숱한 발소리와 여기저기의 피 묻은 발자국 사이로, 플로렌스의 발걸음이 있었다. 여전히 딸은 앞서 걸어가고 있었다. 여전히 그 잠 못 드는 정신은, 따라가고 세면서, 오르는 데 몇 해가 걸리는 거대한 탑의 꼭대기를 향하듯, 더 멀리, 더 높이 나아갔다.\n\n어느 날 그는 한참 전에 말한 사람이 수전이 아니었느냐고 물었다.\n\n플로렌스는 \"네, 그래요, 아빠\"라고 말하고는, 그 애를 보고 싶으시냐고 물었다.\n\n그는 \"무척\"이라고 말했다. 그러자 수전이, 적잖이 떨면서, 그의 침대 곁에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n그것은 그에게 큰 위안이 되는 듯했다. 그는 그녀에게 가지 말아 달라고, 그녀가 했던 말을 자기가 용서한다는 것을 알아 달라고, 그러니 머물러 달라고 청했다. 이제 플로렌스와 자기는 아주 달라졌으며, 아주 행복하다고 그는 말했다. 이것을 좀 보라고! 그가 말한 것은, 그 부드러운 머리를 자기 베개로 끌어당겨 자기 곁에 뉘는 일이었다.\n\n그는 며칠, 몇 주씩 이렇게 지냈다. 마침내, 한 사내의 희미하고 가냘픈 형상이 되어 침대에 누운 채, 입술에 아주 가까이 귀를 대고 들어야만 알아들을 수 있을 만큼 낮은 목소리로 말하면서, 그는 평온해졌다. 이제 그에게는, 창문을 열어 두고 거기 누워 여름 하늘과 나무들을 내다보는 것이, 그리고 저녁에는 저무는 노을을 내다보는 것이 어렴풋이 기쁜 일이 되었다. 구름과 잎사귀의 그림자를 지켜보며, 그림자에 어떤 공감을 느끼는 듯한 것이. 그가 그러는 것은 자연스러웠다. 그에게 삶과 세상은 그 그림자 이외에 아무것도 아니었으니.\n\n그는 이제 플로렌스의 피로를 헤아린다는 것을 보이기 시작했다. 그래서 자주 자기 쇠약함을 무릅쓰고 딸에게 속삭이곤 했다, \"가서 거닐어라, 내 가장 사랑하는 아이야, 향긋한 공기 속을. 네 착한 남편에게 가렴!\" 한번은 월터가 그의 방에 있을 때, 그는 가까이 와서 몸을 굽혀 달라고 손짓했다. 그러고는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며, 자기가 죽으면 자기 아이를 그에게 맡길 수 있음을 안다고 다짐의 말을 속삭였다.\n\n어느 날 저녁, 해 질 무렵, 그가 보기 좋아하던 대로 플로렌스와 월터가 함께 그의 방에 앉아 있을 때였다. 플로렌스가 품에 아기를 안고서, 낮은 목소리로 그 작은 녀석에게 노래를 불러 주기 시작했는데, 죽은 아이에게 그토록 자주 불러 주던 옛 가락을 불렀다. 그는 그때는 그것을 견디지 못했다. 그는 떨리는 손을 들어 올려 그만두라고 애원했다. 그러나 이튿날 그는 딸에게 그것을 다시 불러 달라고, 그리고 저녁마다 자주 그래 달라고 청했다. 딸은 그렇게 했다. 그는 얼굴을 돌린 채 귀를 기울였다.\n\n어느 때 플로렌스는 그의 창가에 앉아 있었는데, 자기와 옛 시중꾼—여전히 그녀의 충실한 동무였다—사이에 반짇고리를 두고 있었다. 그는 졸음에 빠져들어 있었다. 아름다운 저녁이었고, 아직 두 시간의 햇빛이 남아 있었다. 그 고요함과 정적이 플로렌스를 무척 생각에 잠기게 했다. 그녀는 한순간 모든 것을 잊었다—그 침대 위의 그토록 달라진 형상이 처음 자기를 아름다운 어머니께 인사시켜 주던 그때만을 빼고. 그때 의자 등받이에 기대어 있던 월터의 손길이 닿자, 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n\"여보,\" 월터가 말했다, \"아래층에 당신과 이야기하고 싶어 하는 분이 와 계시오.\"\n\n그녀는 월터가 심각해 보인다고 생각하고는, 무슨 일이라도 생겼느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 아니오, 여보!\" 월터가 말했다. \"내가 그분을 직접 뵙고 이야기도 나누었소. 아무 일도 없소. 가 보겠소?\"\n\n플로렌스는 그의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼우고는, 검은 눈의 토츠 부인에게—그녀는 검은 눈의 여인이 할 수 있는 한 가장 활기차고 야무지게 일감에 앉아 있었다—아버지를 맡기고 남편과 함께 아래층으로 내려갔다. 정원으로 통하는 아담한 작은 응접실에, 한 신사가 앉아 있다가 그녀가 들어서자 일어나 다가오려 했으나, 다리에 어떤 특이한 점이 있어 옆으로 빗나가다가 탁자에 가로막혀서야 겨우 멈춰 섰다.\n\n그제야 플로렌스는 피닉스 사촌을 알아보았는데, 처음에는 잎사귀들의 그늘 속이라 그를 알아보지 못했던 것이다. 피닉스 사촌은 그녀의 손을 잡고 결혼을 축하했다.\n\n\"진작에,\" 피닉스 사촌이 플로렌스가 앉자 따라 앉으며 말했다, \"축하 인사를 드릴 기회가 더 일찍 있었더라면 하고 정말이지 바랐습니다만, 사실인즉슨, 워낙 많은 고통스러운 사건들이, 말하자면 줄줄이 꼬리를 물고 일어나는 바람에, 저 자신도 지독히도 끔찍한 처지에 빠져서, 온갖 종류의 사교에는 도무지 어울릴 수가 없었습니다. 제가 유지한 유일한 사교라고는 저 자신과의 사교뿐이었지요. 그런데 사실인즉슨, 한 사람이 자기 자신을 무한정 지루하게 만들 능력이 있다는 걸 알게 되는 것은, 자기 자신의 밑천에 대한 좋은 평가에는 결코 우쭐할 만한 일이 못 됩니다.\"\n\n플로렌스는 이 신사의 거동에—해롭지 않은 자잘한 기벽이 따라붙긴 했어도 그 거동은 언제나 신사다웠다—어떤 형언할 수 없는 거북함과 불안함이 깃든 것에서, 그리고 못지않게 월터의 거동에서, 어떤 목적을 향해 더 직접적으로 다가가는 무언가가 이 뒤에 이어지리라는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"제 친구 게이 군에게—그를 그렇게 부르는 영광을 누려도 된다면 말입니다만—제가 말씀드린 바 있습니다,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"제 친구 돔비가 매우 확실하게 차도가 있다고 들으니 기쁘기 그지없다고요. 제 친구 돔비가 한낱 재산의 손실 따위에 마음을 너무 많이 잠식당하는 일이 없기를 바랍니다. 저로서는 제가 큰 재산의 손실을 겪어 봤다고는 말할 수가 없습니다. 사실인즉슨, 애당초 잃을 만한 큰 재산을 가져 본 적이 없으니까요. 그러나 잃을 수 있는 만큼은 제가 다 잃었지요. 그런데도 제가 그것을 딱히 마음에 두지는 않더라고요. 저는 제 친구 돔비가 지독히도 명예로운 사람임을 압니다. 그리고 이것이 만인의 한결같은 생각임을 아는 것은, 제 친구 돔비에게 큰 위로가 될 만한 일입니다. 심지어 토미 스크루저—극도로 신경질적인 기질의 사람으로, 아마 제 친구 게이도 아는 사람일 텐데—조차도 그 사실을 반박하는 말은 한마디도 못 할 겁니다.\"\n\n플로렌스는 그 어느 때보다도 더, 무언가 다가올 것이 있음을 느꼈다. 그래서 간절히 그것을 기다렸다. 어찌나 간절했던지, 마치 그녀가 입을 열기라도 한 것처럼 피닉스 사촌이 대답했다.\n\n\"사실인즉슨,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"제 친구 게이와 제가, 당신께 한 가지 청을 드리는 것이 온당한지를 의논해 온 터입니다. 그리고 저는 제 친구 게이의—그는 저를 지극히 친절하고 솔직한 태도로 대해 주었고, 그 점에 대해 저는 그에게 큰 신세를 졌습니다—동의를 얻어 그 청을 드리려 합니다. 제 친구 돔비의 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 따님처럼 그토록 상냥한 부인이라면 굳이 많이 졸라 댈 필요가 없으리라는 것을 저도 잘 압니다만, 제 친구 게이의 영향력과 찬동에 힘입고 있음을 알게 되어 기쁩니다. 제가 의회에 몸담던 시절, 한 사람이 무슨 종류든 동의안을 내놓을 때—그 시절엔 그런 일이 드물었습니다. 우리는 아주 단단히 고삐가 잡혀 있었거든요. 양편의 지도자들이 다들 빈틈없는 규율가들이어서, 저 같은 평의원들에게는 그게 지독히도 좋은 일이었지요. 우리 중 상당수가 열에 들떠 끊임없이 제 꼴을 드러내고 싶어 했는데, 그걸 막아 주었으니까요—제가 의회에 몸담던 시절에, 말하자면, 한 사람이 무슨 자잘한 사적 콩알총이라도 한 방 쏘아 올릴 허락을 얻었을 때, 그가 자기 의견이 피트 씨의 가슴속에서 메아리 없이 사라지지는 않으리라 믿어 다행이라고 말하는 것이, 그에게는 늘 큰 점수를 따는 일로 여겨졌지요. 사실인즉슨, 폭풍을 뚫고 배를 몰아온 그 키잡이 말입니다. 그러면 지독히도 많은 친구들이 즉각 환호하며 그의 기운을 북돋아 주었지요. 하지만 사실인즉슨, 이 친구들은 피트 씨의 이름이 거론될 때마다 더없이 요란하게 환호하라는 명을 받고 있어서, 어찌나 능숙해졌던지 그 이름이 늘 그들을 잠에서 깨우곤 했답니다. 그리고 그들은 그것 말고 무슨 일이 벌어지는지에 대해서는 워낙 깜깜무식이어서, '담소가' 브라운이—재무위원회의 네 병들이 술꾼으로, 아마 제 친구 게이의 아버님은 알았을 사람입니다. 제 친구 게이의 시절보다는 전이니까요—이런 말을 흔히 하곤 했지요. 만일 누군가가 제자리에서 일어나, 로비에서 한 의원께서 발작의 마지막 단계에 빠져 있음을 의회에 알리게 되어 유감이며, 그 의원의 이름이 피트라고 말한다면, 갈채가 떠나갈 듯 쏟아졌을 거라고요.\"\n\n이렇게 본론이 미루어지자 플로렌스는 안절부절못하게 되었다. 그래서 점점 더해 가는 동요 속에 피닉스 사촌에게서 월터에게로 눈길을 옮겼다.\n\n\"여보,\" 월터가 말했다, \"아무 일도 없소.\"\n\n\"아무 일도 없습니다, 제 명예를 걸고,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다. \"그리고 제가 당신께 한순간이나마 불안을 끼친 원인이 된 것이 몹시 송구스럽습니다. 정말이지 아무 일도 없음을 단언해 드립니다. 제가 드릴 청은 그저—그러나 정말이지 어찌나 지극히 별난 일로 보이는지, 제 친구 게이가 그—사실인즉슨, 그 얼음을 깨 주는 호의를 베풀어 준다면 저로서는 더없이 고맙겠습니다,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다.\n\n이렇게 부탁을 받고, 또 플로렌스가 그를 향해 돌린 눈길에서도 못지않게 부탁을 받은 월터가 말했다.\n\n\"내 가장 사랑하는 사람, 이것뿐이오. 당신이 아는 이 신사분과 함께 런던으로 마차를 타고 가는 것이오.\"\n\n\"제 친구 게이도 함께—실례했습니다!\" 피닉스 사촌이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"—그리고 나와 함께—어딘가를 방문하는 거요.\"\n\n\"누구를요?\" 플로렌스가 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 보며 물었다.\n\n\"감히 청하건대,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"그 물음에 대한 답을 다그치지 말아 주신다면, 제가 외람되이 이 부탁을 드리는 모험을 해 보겠습니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 알아요, 월터?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그리고 옳다고 생각해요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 다만 당신도 그렇게 생각하리라 확신하기 때문이오. 비록 내가 아주 잘 이해하는 어떤 이유들이 있어서, 미리 더 말하지 않는 편이 낫긴 하지만 말이오.\"\n\n\"아빠가 아직 주무시거나, 깨어 계시더라도 저를 잠시 보내 주실 수 있다면, 곧바로 가겠어요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다. 그러고는 조용히 일어나, 조금은 겁먹었지만 더없이 신뢰가 담긴 눈빛으로 그들을 흘끗 보고는 방을 나갔다.\n\n그녀가 그들과 동행할 채비를 하고 돌아왔을 때, 그들은 창가에서 함께 진지하게 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 플로렌스는 그토록 짧은 시간에 두 사람을 그토록 가깝게 만든 화제가 무엇일까 궁금하지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀가 들어서자 남편이 자랑스러움과 사랑이 담긴 눈빛으로 말을 멈추는 것에는 궁금해하지 않았다. 그녀가 그를 볼 때면 늘 그 눈빛이 그녀에게 머물러 있었으니까.\n\n\"제 친구 돔비에게,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"명함을 한 장 남기고 가겠습니다. 그가 시간이 갈수록 건강과 기력을 되찾기를 진심으로 빕니다. 그리고 제 친구 돔비가, 저를 그의 인품을—사실인즉슨, 영국 상인으로서, 또 지독히도 곧은 신사로서의 그의 인품을—지독히도 따뜻하게 흠모하는 사람으로 여겨 주는 호의를 베풀어 주기를 바랍니다. 제 시골 영지는 더없이 황폐하게 무너져 가는 형편입니다만, 만일 제 친구 돔비가 요양이 필요해서 그곳에 거처를 정한다면, 그는 그곳이 놀랍도록 건강에 좋은 곳임을 알게 될 겁니다—그럴 수밖에요, 놀랍도록 따분한 곳이니까요. 만일 제 친구 돔비가 신체의 쇠약으로 고생하고 있다면, 그리고 제가 자주 제 자신에게 효험을 보았던 것을 추천하도록 허락해 준다면—저는 때때로 지독히도 몸이 안 좋은 사람이었고, 사람들이 아주 흥청망청 살던 시절에 꽤 자유분방하게 살았던 사람입니다만—사실인즉슨, 달걀노른자를 설탕과 육두구와 함께 셰리주 한 잔에 휘저어, 아침에 마른 토스트 한 조각과 함께 들라고 말씀드리겠습니다. 본드 스트리트에서 권투장을 운영하던 잭슨이—대단히 뛰어난 자질의 사람으로, 그 명성은 분명 제 친구 게이도 알 겁니다—말하기를, 링에 오를 훈련을 할 때는 셰리 대신 럼을 쓴다고 하더군요. 이 경우에는 제 친구 돔비가 병약한 상태이니 셰리를 추천하겠습니다. 럼은 그를—사실인즉슨, 그의 머리로—치고 올라가서 그를 지독히도 끔찍한 상태에 빠뜨릴 수도 있으니까요.\"\n\n이 모든 것을 피닉스 사촌은 누가 봐도 초조하고 안절부절못하는 기색으로 늘어놓았다. 그러고는 플로렌스에게 팔을 내주고, 한사코 정원으로 나가려 작정한 듯한 자기 고집스러운 다리에 있는 힘껏 제동을 걸면서, 그녀를 문간으로 이끌어, 그녀를 맞으려고 대기 중이던 마차에 태웠다.\n\n월터가 그 뒤를 따라 올라탔고, 마차는 출발했다.\n\n마차로 가는 길은 육칠 마일쯤 되었다. 런던 서쪽에 자리한 어떤 칙칙하고 위엄 있는 거리들을 지날 무렵, 땅거미가 지고 있었다. 플로렌스는 이때쯤 자기 손을 월터의 손에 넣고 있었다. 그리고 마차가 접어드는 새 거리마다, 점점 더해 가는 동요 속에 무척 간절히 들여다보고 있었다.\n\n마침내 마차가, 아버지의 불행한 결혼식이 거행되었던 브룩 스트리트의 그 집 앞에 멈춰 섰을 때, 플로렌스가 말했다, \"월터, 여기가 어디예요? 누가 여기 있는 거예요?\" 월터가 그녀를 다독이며 대답하지 않자, 그녀는 집 정면을 올려다보았고, 마치 사람이 살지 않는 듯 창문이 모두 닫혀 있는 것을 보았다. 피닉스 사촌은 이때쯤 마차에서 내려, 손을 내밀고 있었다.\n\n\"당신은 안 와요, 월터?\"\n\n\"안 가오, 나는 여기 남아 있겠소. 떨지 마오, 두려워할 것 없소, 내 가장 사랑하는 플로렌스.\"\n\n\"알아요, 월터, 당신이 이렇게 가까이 있으니. 그건 확실해요, 하지만—\"\n\n문이 노크도 없이 살며시 열렸고, 피닉스 사촌은 그녀를 여름 저녁 공기 속에서 그 답답하고 음울한 집 안으로 이끌었다. 어느 때보다 더 침침하고 거무튀튀한 그 집은, 결혼식 날 이후로 줄곧 닫혀 있었던 듯, 그 뒤로 줄곧 어둠과 슬픔을 쌓아 둔 듯했다.\n\n플로렌스는 떨면서 어둑한 계단을 올라, 안내자와 함께 응접실 문 앞에서 멈춰 섰다. 그는 말없이 문을 열고, 자기는 그 자리에 남은 채, 그녀에게 안쪽 방으로 들어가 달라고 간청하는 몸짓을 해 보였다. 플로렌스는 한순간 망설이다가 그대로 따랐다.\n\n창가의 탁자 곁에, 무언가를 쓰거나 그리고 있었던 듯한 한 부인이 앉아 있었는데, 저무는 빛을 향해 돌린 그 머리는 한 손에 받쳐져 있었다. 머뭇거리며 다가가던 플로렌스는, 마치 움직이는 힘을 잃기라도 한 듯 문득 멈춰 섰다. 그 부인이 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 그녀가 말했다, \"이게 어찌 된 일이냐?\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 돼요!\" 플로렌스가 부르짖으며, 그녀가 일어서자 뒷걸음질 치며 두 손을 내밀어 그녀를 막으려 했다. \"어머니!\"\n\n두 사람은 서로를 바라보며 서 있었다. 격정과 자존심이 그것을 닳게 했으나, 그것은 에디스의 얼굴이었고, 여전히 아름답고 위엄 있었다. 그것은 플로렌스의 얼굴이었고, 그것이 드러내는 그 온갖 겁먹은 회피에도 불구하고, 거기에는 연민이, 슬픔이, 고마운 정겨운 추억이 깃들어 있었다. 두 얼굴에는 저마다 경이와 두려움이 선명하게 어려 있었다. 저마다 그토록 고요하고 말없이, 돌이킬 수 없는 과거의 검은 심연 너머로 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n먼저 변한 것은 플로렌스였다. 그녀는 울음을 터뜨리며, 벅찬 가슴으로 말했다, \"오, 어머니, 어머니! 왜 우리가 이렇게 만나야 하나요? 아무도 없을 때 어머니는 왜 제게 그토록 다정하셨나요, 우리가 이렇게 만나야 하는데?\"\n\n에디스는 그녀 앞에 말없이, 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다. 그녀의 눈은 그 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다.\n\n\"저는 감히 그 일은 생각할 수가 없어요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"저는 아빠의 병상에서 오는 길이에요. 우리는 이제 결코 떨어지지 않아요. 다시는 떨어지지 않을 거예요. 만일 제가 아빠에게 용서를 빌기를 원하신다면, 그렇게 할게요, 어머니. 제가 청한다면 아빠가 이제는 거의 틀림없이 들어주실 거예요. 하늘도 어머니께 그것을 허락하시고, 어머니를 위로해 주시기를!\"\n\n그녀는 한마디도 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"월터가—저는 그와 결혼했고, 우리에게는 아들이 있어요,\" 플로렌스가 머뭇거리며 말했다—\"문간에 와 있어요, 저를 여기 데려다주었어요. 어머니가 뉘우치고 계신다고, 어머니가 변하셨다고 그에게 말할게요,\" 플로렌스가 애처롭게 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. \"그러면 그가 저와 함께 아빠에게 말해 줄 거예요, 분명히요. 이것 말고 제가 할 수 있는 일이 또 있을까요?\"\n\n에디스가 침묵을 깨고, 눈도 손발도 움직이지 않은 채 천천히 대답했다.\n\n\"네 이름에, 네 남편의 이름에, 네 아이의 이름에 묻은 오점은. 그것이 언젠가 용서받을 수 있겠느냐, 플로렌스?\"\n\n\"그것이 언젠가 용서받겠느냐고요, 어머니? 이미 용서되었어요! 아낌없이, 아낌없이, 월터에 의해서도 저에 의해서도요. 그것이 어머니께 조금이라도 위안이 된다면, 그보다 더 확실히 믿으셔도 좋은 것은 없어요. 어머니는—어머니는,\" 플로렌스가 더듬거리며 말했다, \"아빠 이야기는 안 하시지만, 어머니가 제게 아빠께 용서를 빌어 달라고 바라시는 걸 저는 분명히 알아요. 분명히 그러실 거예요.\"\n\n그녀는 한마디도 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"그렇게 할게요!\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"어머니가 허락하신다면 그것을 받아다 드릴게요. 그러면 어쩌면, 우리가 예전에 서로에게 그랬던 것에 좀 더 가깝게 작별할 수 있을 거예요. 저는,\" 플로렌스가 아주 부드럽게, 그녀에게 더 가까이 다가서며 말했다, \"어머니가 두려워서, 또는 어머니로 인해 욕먹을까 겁이 나서 어머니에게서 뒷걸음질 친 게 아니에요. 저는 그저 아빠에 대한 제 도리를 다하고 싶을 뿐이에요. 저는 아빠에게 무척 소중하고, 아빠는 제게 무척 소중해요. 하지만 어머니가 제게 무척 잘해 주셨다는 걸 저는 결코 잊을 수가 없어요. 오, 하늘에 비세요,\" 플로렌스가 그녀의 품에 안기며 부르짖었다, \"하늘에 비세요, 어머니, 이 모든 죄와 수치를 어머니께 용서하시기를, 그리고 제가 옛날의 어머니를 떠올릴 때 이렇게 하지 않을 수 없는 것을 (만일 그것이 잘못이라면) 용서하시기를!\"\n\n에디스는, 마치 그 손길 아래 무너지듯, 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉아 그녀의 목을 끌어안았다.\n\n\"플로렌스!\" 그녀가 부르짖었다. \"내 더 나은 천사야! 내가 다시 미쳐 버리기 전에, 내 고집이 되돌아와 나를 벙어리로 만들기 전에, 나를 믿어 다오, 내 영혼을 걸고 나는 결백하다!\"\n\n\"어머니!\"\n\n\"많은 것에 죄가 있지! 우리 사이에 영영 황무지를 갈라놓는 그 일에 죄가 있어. 내 남은 평생 내내, 순결함과 무구함에서—이 세상 누구보다도 너에게서—나를 갈라놓을 수밖에 없는 그것에 죄가 있어. 눈멀고 격정에 찬 원한에 죄가 있어, 그것은 지금조차도 내가 뉘우치지 않고, 뉘우칠 수 없고, 뉘우치지 않을 거야. 하지만 그 죽은 자와의 죄는 없어. 하느님 앞에 맹세코!\"\n\n그녀는 땅바닥에 무릎을 꿇은 채, 두 손을 들어 올려 그것을 맹세했다.\n\n\"플로렌스!\" 그녀가 말했다, \"더없이 순결하고 더없이 선한 사람아—내가 사랑하는—오래전에 나를 변화시킬 수도 있었고, 한때는 나 같은 여자에게서조차 얼마간의 변화를 정말로 이루어 낸 사람아—나를 믿어 다오, 나는 그 일에는 결백하다. 그리고 한 번 더, 내 황량한 가슴에, 이 사랑스러운 머리를 마지막으로 뉘게 해 다오!\"\n\n그녀는 마음이 움직여 울고 있었다. 옛날에 더 자주 이러했더라면, 그녀는 지금 더 행복했을 것이다.\n\n\"이 세상 그 무엇도,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"나에게서 부인의 말을 쥐어짜 낼 수는 없었을 거야. 어떤 사랑도, 어떤 증오도, 어떤 희망도, 어떤 위협도. 나는 입을 다문 채 죽겠다고 말했어. 나는 그렇게 할 수 있었고, 그렇게 했을 거야, 만일 우리가 결코 만나지 않았더라면, 플로렌스.\"\n\n\"바라건대,\" 피닉스 사촌이 문간으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어서며, 몸은 절반은 방 안에, 절반은 방 밖에 둔 채로 말했다, \"제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척이, 제가 자그마한 계략을 써서 이 만남을 성사시킨 것을 너그러이 봐 주기를 바랍니다. 처음부터 제가, 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척이 매우 불행하게도 그 하얀 이를 가진 고인과 부정한 관계를 맺었을 가능성에 대해 전적으로 의심하지 않았다고는 말할 수 없습니다. 사실인즉슨, 이 세상에서는—지독히도 기묘한 일들로 유명하고, 한 사람의 경험 안에서 단연 가장 이해 불가능한 것인 이 세상에서는—그런 종류의 매우 묘한 결합을 보게 되니까요. 하지만 제가 제 친구 돔비에게 말씀드린 대로, 저는 그것이 완전히 입증되기 전까지는 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척의 죄를 인정할 수가 없었습니다. 그리고 그 고인이, 사실인즉슨, 지독히도 끔찍한 방식으로 목숨을 잃었을 때, 그녀의 처지가 매우 고통스러운 것임을 느끼고—게다가 우리 집안이 그녀에게 더 마음을 쓰지 않은 데 다소 잘못이 있고, 우리가 무심한 집안이라는 것을 느끼고—또한 제 이모님이, 비록 지독히도 활달한 분이긴 했어도, 아마도 가장 훌륭한 어머니는 아니었으리라는 것을 느끼고—저는 외람되이 프랑스로 그녀를 찾아가, 형편이 몹시 쪼들리는 사내가 베풀 수 있는 만큼의 보호를 베풀어 드렸습니다. 그때 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척은, 제가 제 나름으로는 지독히도 괜찮은 사람이라고 믿는다는 영광스러운 말씀을 제게 해 주었습니다. 그래서 제 보호 아래 자기를 맡긴 것이지요. 사실인즉슨, 저는 이것이 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척 편에서의 친절한 처사라고 이해했습니다. 저는 극도로 비실비실해져 가는 처지인지라, 그녀의 보살핌에서 큰 위안을 얻었으니까요.\"\n\n에디스는, 플로렌스를 소파로 데려가 앉혔는데, 마치 그에게 더는 말하지 말아 달라고 청하는 듯한 손짓을 했다.\n\n\"제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척은,\" 피닉스 사촌이 여전히 문간을 어슬렁거리며 말을 이었다, \"그녀의 마음의 평안을 위해, 또 저 자신의 평안과, 그 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 따님을 우리가 그토록 흠모하는 제 친구 돔비의 평안을 위해, 제가 제 소견의 실타래를 마저 풀어내더라도 너그러이 봐 줄 겁니다. 그녀는, 처음부터 그녀와 제가 그녀의 가출 문제를 한 번도 입에 올린 적이 없음을 기억할 겁니다. 저의 인상은, 분명히, 그 일에는 그녀가 마음만 먹으면 설명할 수 있는 어떤 비밀이 있다는 것이었지요. 하지만 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척이 지독히도 단호한 여인인지라, 저는 그녀가 사실인즉슨 함부로 다룰 사람이 아님을 알았기에, 어떤 논의에도 끼어들지 않았습니다. 하지만 근래에, 그녀가 마음을 열 수 있는 지점이 제 친구 돔비의 따님에 대한 매우 강한 종류의 애정인 듯함을 알아차리고는, 만일 제가 양편 모두에게 뜻밖일 만남을 성사시킬 수 있다면, 그것이 이로운 결과로 이어질지도 모른다는 생각이 들었습니다. 그래서, 우리가 이렇게 은밀한 방식으로 런던에 와 있는 지금—이탈리아 남부로 가서, 사실인즉슨, 우리의 영원한 안식처로 갈 때까지 그곳에 자리를 잡기 전에 말입니다. 그건 한 사내에게는 지독히도 달갑잖은 생각이지요—저는 제 친구 게이의 거처를 찾아내는 일에 몰두했고—보기 드물게 솔직한 성품의 잘생긴 사내로, 아마 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척도 아는 사람일 텐데—그의 상냥한 아내를 이곳으로 모셔오는 기쁨을 누렸습니다. 그리고 이제,\" 피닉스 사촌이, 그 경박한 거동과 어수룩한 말투를 뚫고 빛나는 진실하고 진정한 간절함을 담아 말했다, \"저는 제 친척에게 간곡히 청합니다. 중도에 멈추지 말고, 자기가 잘못한 것이 무엇이든 할 수 있는 데까지 바로잡으라고요—집안의 명예를 위해서가 아니라, 자기 자신의 명성을 위해서가 아니라, 불행한 사정들 때문에 그녀가 공허하고, 사실인즉슨, 허튼소리에 가까운 것으로 여기게 된 그런 고려들 가운데 어느 것을 위해서도 아니라—그것이 옳은 게 아니라 그른 일이기 때문에 말입니다.\"\n\n이 말 뒤에 피닉스 사촌의 다리가 그를 데리고 나가는 데 동의했다. 그래서 두 사람을 함께 남겨 둔 채, 그는 문을 닫았다.\n\n에디스는 몇 분 동안 말없이 있었고, 플로렌스는 그녀 곁에 바짝 붙어 앉아 있었다. 이윽고 그녀는 가슴에서 봉인된 종이 한 장을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"오랫동안 나 자신과 따져 보았어,\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"이것을 아예 써야 할지를, 갑작스럽게 혹은 사고로 죽을 경우를 대비해, 그게 없어 곤란해질 것을 느끼면서. 그 뒤로 줄곧, 언제 어떻게 이것을 없앨까 곰곰이 생각해 왔지. 받아라, 플로렌스. 거기에 진실이 적혀 있어.\"\n\n\"아빠께 드리는 건가요?\" 플로렌스가 물었다.\n\n\"네가 원하는 누구에게든 주는 거야,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그건 너에게 주는 것이고, 너에 의해 얻어진 거야. 그분은 달리 어떤 방법으로도 결코 그것을 가질 수 없었을 거야.\"\n\n두 사람은 짙어 가는 어둠 속에서 다시 말없이 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"어머니,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"아빠는 재산을 잃었어요. 죽음의 문턱까지 갔었고요. 지금도 회복하지 못하실지 몰라요. 어머니가 아빠께 전하라고 할 말이 있을까요?\"\n\n\"네가 그분에게 무척 소중하다고,\" 에디스가 물었다, \"내게 말했지?\"\n\n\"네!\" 플로렌스가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 만났던 것이 유감이라고 그분에게 전해라.\"\n\n\"그게 다인가요?\" 플로렌스가 잠시 뒤에 말했다.\n\n\"만일 그분이 묻거든, 내가 한 일을 뉘우치지 않는다고—아직은—전해라. 내일 다시 그 일을 해야 한다면, 나는 그렇게 할 테니까. 하지만 만일 그분이 변한 사람이라면—\"\n\n그녀는 멈췄다. 플로렌스의 손길이 말없이 닿는 데에 그녀를 멈추게 하는 무언가가 있었다.\n\n\"—하지만 변한 사람이 되었으니, 그분은 이제 알아, 결코 그렇지는 않으리라는 걸. 차라리 그런 일이 아예 없었으면 한다고 내가 바란다고 전해라.\"\n\n\"제가 이렇게 말해도 될까요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다, \"어머니가 아빠가 겪으신 고난을 듣고 슬퍼하셨다고요?\"\n\n\"안 된다,\" 그녀가 대답했다, \"만일 그 고난들이 그분에게 자기 딸이 무척 소중하다는 것을 가르쳐 주었다면. 만일 그 고난들이 그 교훈을 가져다주었다면, 그분은 언젠가 그것들로 인해 스스로 슬퍼하지 않을 거야, 플로렌스.\"\n\n\"어머니는 아빠가 잘되기를 바라고, 아빠가 행복하기를 바라시잖아요. 분명히 그러시잖아요!\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"오! 제가 언젠가 훗날 기회가 닿으면, 그렇게 말할 수 있게 해 주세요?\"\n\n에디스는 검은 눈으로 앞을 한결같이 응시한 채 앉아, 플로렌스가 그 간청을 되풀이할 때까지 대답하지 않았다. 그러다가 그녀는 자기 팔 안에 플로렌스의 손을 끌어당기며, 바깥의 밤을 향한 그 사색에 잠긴 눈길 그대로, 말했다.\n\n\"그분에게 전해라, 만일 지금 자기 처지에서, 내 과거를 측은히 여길 어떤 까닭을 찾을 수 있다면, 내가 그렇게 해 달라고 청한다는 전갈을 보냈다고. 그분에게 전해라, 만일 지금 자기 처지에서, 나를 덜 모질게 생각할 어떤 까닭을 찾을 수 있다면, 내가 그렇게 해 달라고 청했다고. 그분에게 전해라, 우리가 서로에게 죽은 것이나 다름없고, 영원의 이편에서는 다시 만나지 못할지언정, 이제 우리 사이에는 예전에는 결코 없었던 공통된 감정 하나가 있음을 그분이 안다고.\"\n\n그녀의 매서움이 누그러지는 듯했고, 그 검은 눈에는 눈물이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"나는 거기에 나를 맡길 거야,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그분이 나를 더 좋게 생각하도록, 또 내가 그분을 더 좋게 생각하도록. 그분이 자기 플로렌스를 가장 사랑할 때, 나를 가장 덜 미워할 거야. 그분이 그 애와 그 애의 아이들로 가장 자랑스럽고 행복할 때, 우리 결혼 생활의 어두운 환영 속 자기 몫에 대해 가장 뉘우치게 될 거야. 그때는 나도 뉘우치고 있을 거야—그때 그분이 그것을 알게 해 다오—그리고 내가 나를 지금의 나로 만든 그 모든 까닭을 그토록 많이 생각했을 때, 그분을 지금의 그분으로 만든 까닭들에 대해서도 더 많이 헤아렸어야 했음을 생각할 거야. 그때는 그분의 잘못의 몫을 용서하려 애쓰겠어. 그분도 내 몫을 용서하려 애쓰게 해 다오!\"\n\n\"오 어머니!\" 플로렌스가 말했다. \"이런 기이한 만남과 헤어짐 속에서도, 이 말을 들으니 제 마음이 얼마나 가벼워지는지 몰라요!\"\n\n\"내 귀에는 기이한 말이로구나,\" 에디스가 말했다, \"내 목소리의 울림과는 동떨어진 말이야! 하지만 내가 그분으로 하여금 나를 그렇게 믿게 만든 그 비참한 인간이었다 하더라도, 너와 그분이 서로에게 무척 소중하다는 걸 들었으니, 나는 여전히 이 말을 할 수 있었으리라 생각한다. 네가 가장 소중할 때, 그분이 늘 느끼게 해 다오, 자기가 나를 생각하는 데 가장 너그럽다는 것을—내가 그분을 생각하는 데 가장 너그럽다는 것을! 그것이 내가 그분에게 보내는 마지막 말이다! 자, 잘 가거라, 내 생명아!\"\n\n그녀는 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안고, 자기 여인으로서의 사랑과 다정함의 영혼을 한꺼번에 모두 쏟아붓는 듯했다.\n\n\"이 입맞춤은 네 아이에게! 이 입맞춤들은 네 머리 위에 축복이 되기를! 내 사랑하는 플로렌스, 내 어여쁜 아이야, 잘 가거라!\"\n\n\"다시 만나요!\" 플로렌스가 부르짖었다.\n\n\"다시는! 다시는 안 돼! 이 어두운 방에 나를 두고 떠날 때, 나를 무덤에 두고 떠난다고 생각해라. 다만 내가 한때 존재했다는 것을, 그리고 내가 너를 사랑했다는 것을 기억해 다오!\"\n\n그리고 플로렌스는 그녀의 얼굴을 더는 보지 못한 채, 그러나 마지막까지 그녀의 포옹과 어루만짐을 받으며, 그녀를 떠났다.\n\n피닉스 사촌이 문간에서 그녀를 맞아, 그 칙칙한 식당으로 그녀를 데리고 내려가 월터에게 데려다주었고, 그녀는 그의 어깨에 머리를 기대고 울었다.\n\n\"지독히도 안타깝습니다,\" 피닉스 사촌이, 더없이 꾸밈없는 태도로, 조금도 감추는 기색 없이 소맷부리를 들어 눈으로 가져가며 말했다, \"제 친구 돔비의 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 따님이자 제 친구 게이의 상냥한 아내가, 방금 끝난 그 면담으로 그 예민한 심성을 그토록 심히 상하고 마음 아파하게 되다니요. 하지만 제가 최선을 다해 행동했기를, 그리고 제 명예로운 친구 돔비가 이번에 밝혀진 사실들로 마음의 짐을 덜기를 바라고 또 믿습니다. 제 친구 돔비가 우리 집안과의 인연으로, 사실인즉슨, 지독히도 엉망진창인 상태에 빠지게 된 것을 저는 몹시 한탄합니다. 하지만 만일 그 흉악한 악당 바커—하얀 이를 가진 사내—만 아니었더라면, 모든 일이 꽤 순조롭게 흘러갔으리라고 굳게 믿습니다. 저에 대해 보기 드물게 좋은 견해를 품어 주는 영광을 베푼 제 친척에 관해서는, 제 친구 게이의 상냥한 아내에게 단언할 수 있습니다. 그녀가, 사실인즉슨, 제가 그녀의 아버지가 되어 줄 것을 믿어도 좋다고요. 그리고 인생의 변화들, 또 우리가 끊임없이 자기 자신을 처신하는 그 기이한 방식에 관해서는, 제가 할 수 있는 말이라고는, 제 친구 셰익스피어와 더불어—한 시대를 위해서가 아니라 모든 시대를 위해 살았던 사람으로, 분명 제 친구 게이도 아는 사람입니다만—그것이 한낱 꿈의 그림자 같다는 것뿐입니다.\"\n\n# 제57장\n\n오랫동안 햇빛을 보지 못한 채 먼지와 거미줄로 허옇게 뒤덮여 있던 한 병이 양지로 옮겨져 나왔다. 그리고 그 안의 황금빛 포도주는 식탁 위로 광채를 흩뿌린다.\n\n그것은 오래된 마데이라의 마지막 한 병이다.\n\n\"길스 씨, 당신 말이 옳소,\" 돔비 씨가 말한다. \"이건 매우 진귀하고 더없이 감미로운 포도주요.\"\n\n자리를 함께한 선장은 기쁨으로 환하게 빛난다. 그 달아오른 이마 둘레에는 그야말로 희열의 후광이 어려 있다.\n\n\"우리는 늘 다짐했었습죠, 나리,\" 길스 씨가 말한다. \"네드와 저, 그러니까 저희 둘이 말입니다요—\"\n\n돔비 씨가 선장을 향해 고개를 끄덕이고, 선장은 말없는 만족감으로 점점 더 환히 빛난다.\n\n\"—언젠가 어느 날엔가 이 술을, 무사히 집에 돌아온 월터를 위해 마시자고요. 비록 이런 집은 생각도 못 했습니다만. 나리, 저희의 오랜 변덕을 못마땅히 여기지 않으신다면, 이 첫 잔을 월터와 그의 아내에게 바치도록 하시지요.\"\n\n\"월터와 그의 아내를 위하여!\" 돔비 씨가 말한다. \"플로렌스, 내 아가야\"—그러고는 몸을 돌려 딸에게 입을 맞춘다.\n\n\"월터와 그의 아내를 위하여!\" 투츠 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"월터와 그의 아내를 위하여!\" 선장이 외친다. \"만세!\" 그러고는 선장이 자기 잔을 다른 잔에 부딪치고 싶어 안달하는 기색을 보이자, 돔비 씨가 선뜻 손을 내밀어 잔을 갖다 댄다. 나머지 사람들도 뒤따른다. 그러자 마치 자그마한 결혼 종소리가 한바탕 울려 퍼지듯, 흥겹고 명랑한 쟁쟁거림이 인다.\n\n땅에 묻힌 다른 포도주들도, 그 옛날 마데이라가 그러했듯, 점점 더 오래되어 간다. 그리고 병마다 먼지와 거미줄이 두텁게 쌓여 간다.\n\n돔비 씨는 백발의 신사다. 그 얼굴에는 근심과 고통의 묵직한 자국이 새겨져 있다. 그러나 그것은 영영 지나가 버려 그 뒤에 맑은 저녁을 남긴 폭풍의 흔적이다.\n\n야심에 찬 계획들은 더 이상 그를 괴롭히지 않는다. 그의 유일한 자랑거리는 딸과 사위다. 그는 말이 적고 사려 깊고 조용한 태도를 지녔으며, 늘 딸과 함께 있다. 톡스 양도 심심찮게 이 가족 모임에 끼는데, 거기에 아주 헌신적이며 큰 사랑을 받는 사람이다. 한때 위풍당당했던 그 후원자에 대한 그녀의 흠모는, 프린세스 플레이스에서 충격을 받았던 그 아침 이래로 줄곧 플라토닉한 것이지만, 조금도 약해지지 않았다.\n\n그의 재산이 난파된 잔해에서 그에게 흘러들어 온 것은 아무것도 없다. 다만 어떻게 들어오는지 모를 일정한 연간 금액이 있는데, 그것은 출처를 캐려 들지 말아 달라는 간곡한 청과 함께, 그것이 한 빚이자 보상의 행위라는 다짐과 함께 들어온다. 그는 이 일을 두고 자기의 옛 점원과 의논해 보았는데, 그 점원은 떳떳이 받아도 좋다고 단언하며, 옛 회사 시절의 잊힌 어떤 거래에서 비롯된 것임이 틀림없다고 했다.\n\n그 개암빛 눈동자의 독신남은, 이제 더 이상 독신이 아니어서, 지금은 백발의 부(副)점원의 누이와 결혼한 몸이다. 그는 가끔, 그러나 드물게, 옛 상관을 찾아간다. 그가 옛 고용주에게서 물러나 지내야 하는 데에는 백발 부점원의 내력에 한 가지 이유가 있고, 그의 이름에는 더 강한 이유가 있다. 그리고 그가 누이와 매부와 함께 살기에, 그들도 그 은둔 생활을 함께한다. 월터는 가끔 그들을 보러 가고—플로렌스도 마찬가지인데—그 정겨운 집에는 피아노포르테와 첼로를 위해 편곡된 묵직한 이중주와, '화목한 대장장이들'의 노고가 울려 퍼진다.\n\n그런데 이 달라진 시절에 나무로 만든 해군 사관후보생은 어떻게 지낼까? 그야, 여전히 이 자리에 있으니, 오른발을 앞으로 내민 채 삯마차들을 향해 부지런히 일하면서, 어느 때보다 더 빈틈없는 모습이다. 삼각모에서 버클 달린 구두까지 새로 칠을 한 까닭이다. 그리고 그 위쪽에는 황금빛 글자로, 길스 앤드 커틀, 이라는 이 이름들이 눈부시게 빛난다.\n\n사관후보생이 여느 때의 수월한 장사 너머로 무슨 큰 거래를 한 건 아니다. 그러나 리든홀 시장의 푸른 우산 둘레로 반 마일쯤 되는 일대에서는 이런 말들이 돈다. 길스 씨가 옛날에 해 둔 투자 가운데 몇 가지가 놀라우리만치 잘 풀리고 있다고. 그리고 그가 그런 면에서 시류에 뒤처졌다고 스스로 여겼던 것과는 달리, 실은 시류보다 조금 앞섰던 것이어서, 때와 계획이 무르익기를 기다려야 했을 뿐이라고. 길스 씨의 돈이 스스로 굴러가기 시작했고, 그것도 꽤 활기차게 자꾸자꾸 불어나고 있다는 소문이다. 분명한 것은, 커피빛 양복 차림으로, 크로노미터를 주머니에 넣고, 안경을 이마에 걸친 채 가게 문간에 서 있는 그가, 손님이 오지 않는다고 애를 태우는 기색은 전혀 없이, 예나 지금이나 여전히 안개에 싸인 듯 흐리멍덩하면서도 무척 유쾌하고 만족스러워 보인다는 점이다.\n\n그의 동업자 커틀 선장으로 말하자면, 선장의 마음속에는 어떤 현실보다도 나은, 장사라는 한 편의 허구가 자리 잡고 있다. 선장은 마치 사관후보생의 도움 없이는 어떤 배도 런던 항을 떠나지 못하기라도 하는 양, 이 나라의 통상과 항해에 사관후보생이 얼마나 중요한지를 더없이 확신하고 있다. 문 위에 걸린 자기 이름을 향한 그의 기쁨은 다함이 없다. 그는 하루에도 스무 번씩 길을 건너가 맞은편에서 그것을 바라본다. 그리고 그럴 때마다 어김없이 이렇게 말한다. \"에드워드 커틀, 이 사람아, 자네 어머니가 자네가 언젠가 학문하는 사람이 될 줄 아셨더라면, 그 착한 노인네가 정말이지 깜짝 놀라 자빠지셨을 게야!\"\n\n그런데 여기 투츠 씨가 무서운 속도로 사관후보생을 향해 들이닥치고 있고, 자그마한 응접실로 뛰어들어 오는 투츠 씨의 얼굴은 몹시 벌겋다.\n\n\"길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말한다. \"그리고 솔스 씨, 투츠 부인이 식구를 하나 더 늘렸다는 소식을 기쁘게 알려 드립니다.\"\n\n\"그거 그 사람 면목이 서는 일이로군!\" 선장이 외친다.\n\n\"축하드립니다, 투츠 씨!\" 늙은 솔이 말한다.\n\n\"고맙습니다,\" 투츠 씨가 흐뭇하게 웃는다. \"정말 감사합니다. 두 분이 들으시면 기뻐하실 줄 알고, 그래서 제가 직접 내려온 겁니다. 우리가 정말이지 자꾸 늘어나고 있어요, 아시겠지만. 플로렌스가 있고, 수전이 있고, 이제 여기 또 자그마한 새 식구가 하나 생겼지요.\"\n\n\"여자 식구입니까?\" 선장이 묻는다.\n\n\"네, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말한다. \"그래서 저는 기쁩니다. 그 더없이 비범한 여인을 더 자주 되풀이해 낼 수 있을수록, 제 생각엔, 더 좋은 일이지요!\"\n\n\"준비!\" 선장이 목이 없는 그 오래된 손잡이 술병 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말한다—저녁이라, 사관후보생 집에서 늘 적당히 차려 두는 파이프와 술잔들이 식탁 위에 놓여 있는 까닭이다. \"그 사람을 위하여, 부디 그 사람이 앞으로도 식구를 잔뜩 더 늘리기를!\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 길스 선장님,\" 기뻐하는 투츠 씨가 말한다. \"저도 그 뜻에 한목소리를 내겠습니다. 허락해 주신다면, 이런 상황에서 제가 그렇게 하는 것이 누구에게도 거슬리지 않을 테니, 파이프를 하나 피워 볼까 합니다.\"\n\n그리하여 투츠 씨가 담배를 피우기 시작하고, 마음을 활짝 연 채 무척 수다스러워진다.\n\n\"그 사랑스러운 여인이 자신의 빼어난 분별력을 보여 준 온갖 놀라운 사례들 가운데서도, 길스 선장님과 솔스 씨,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다. \"돔비 양에 대한 제 헌신을 그토록 완벽하게 이해해 준 것보다 더 놀라운 일은 없다고 생각합니다.\"\n\n그의 두 청중이 모두 동의한다.\n\n\"왜냐하면 아시겠지만,\" 투츠 씨가 말한다. \"저는 돔비 양에 대한 제 마음을 한 번도 바꾼 적이 없거든요. 예나 지금이나 똑같습니다. 그분은 지금도 제게, 제가 월터스와 알고 지내기 전에 그랬던 것과 똑같이 변함없는 눈부신 환영입니다. 투츠 부인과 제가 처음으로 그—한마디로, 그 애틋한 연정에 대해 이야기하기 시작했을 때 말입니다, 길스 선장님.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그럼, 이 사람아,\" 선장이 말한다. \"우리 모두를 빙 돌게 만드는 그것 말이지—그건 자네가 그 책을 펼쳐 봐야 할 텐데—\"\n\n\"꼭 그러겠습니다, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 자못 진지하게 말한다. \"우리가 처음 그런 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했을 때, 저는 제가, 말하자면 시들어 버린 꽃이라는 걸 설명했지요, 아시겠지만.\"\n\n선장은 이 비유를 크게 마음에 들어 하며, 활짝 피는 어떤 꽃도 장미만은 못하다고 중얼거린다.\n\n\"그런데 세상에나,\" 투츠 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"그 사람은 제 감정 상태를 저 자신만큼이나 훤히 알고 있었어요. 제가 그 사람에게 따로 일러 줄 게 하나도 없었지요. 그 사람은 저와 말 없는 무덤 사이를 가로막아 줄 수 있던 단 한 사람이었고, 실제로 그렇게 해 주었는데, 그 방식이 제 영원한 흠모를 자아낼 만한 것이었습니다. 그 사람은 이 세상에서 제가 돔비 양만큼 우러러보는 사람이 아무도 없다는 걸 압니다. 이 세상에서 제가 돔비 양을 위해서라면 못 할 일이 하나도 없다는 걸 압니다. 제가 돔비 양을 그 성(性) 가운데 가장 아름답고, 가장 사랑스럽고, 가장 천사 같은 분으로 여긴다는 걸 압니다. 그에 대한 그 사람의 소견이 뭔지 아십니까? 분별력의 극치지요. '여보, 당신 말이 옳아요. 나도 그렇게 생각해요.'\"\n\n\"나도 그렇소!\" 선장이 말한다.\n\n\"나도 그렇소,\" 솔 길스가 말한다.\n\n\"그러고는,\" 투츠 씨가 파이프를 사색에 잠긴 듯 몇 번 빨고 나서 다시 말을 잇는데, 그동안 그의 얼굴에는 더없이 흡족한 상념이 어려 있었다. \"제 아내가 얼마나 관찰력이 뛰어난 여인인지요! 얼마나 명민한지요! 어떤 소견들을 내놓는지요! 바로 어젯밤이었어요, 우리가 부부의 행복을 누리며 앉아 있을 때—솔직히 맹세코, '행복'이라는 말로는 제 아내와 함께 있을 때의 제 감정을 표현하기엔 한참 모자랍니다만—그 사람이 우리 벗 월터스의 지금 처지를 생각하면 얼마나 놀라운 일이냐고 하더군요. '여기,' 제 아내가 그러는데, '이이가 그 어린 신부와 함께한 첫 긴 항해를 마치고 바닷일에서 풀려나 있잖아요'—아시다시피 그랬지요, 솔스 씨.\"\n\n\"맞고말고요,\" 늙은 기계 제작자가 두 손을 비비며 말한다.\n\n\"'여기 이이가,' 제 아내가 그러기를, '그 일에서 곧바로 풀려나서, 같은 회사로부터 집에서 막중한 신임을 받는 자리에 임명되었고, 다시 한번 그럴 만한 사람임을 보여 주면서, 더없이 빠르게 사다리를 올라가고, 모두에게 사랑받고, 자기 운수가 가장 좋은 바로 그때에 삼촌의 도움을 받고 있잖아요'—제 생각에도 그렇지요, 솔스 씨? 제 아내는 늘 정확하거든요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지요, 그래요—황금을 실은 우리의 잃어버린 배 가운데 몇 척이 정말로 돌아왔답니다,\" 늙은 솔이 웃으며 대답한다. \"작은 배들이지요, 투츠 씨, 그래도 우리 아이한테는 쓸모가 있었어요!\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다,\" 투츠 씨가 말한다. \"제 아내가 틀린 걸 결코 못 보실 겁니다. '여기 이이가,' 그 더없이 비범한 여인이 그러기를, '이런 처지에 놓여 있는데—그래서 뭐가 따라올까요? 뭐가 따라올까요?' 하고 투츠 부인이 짚었지요. 자, 길스 선장님, 그리고 솔스 씨, 제 아내의 통찰력이 얼마나 깊은지 잘 봐 주십시오. '바로 이거예요. 돔비 씨의 바로 그 눈앞에서, 한 토대가 닦이고 있는데, 그 위에 한 채의—한 채의 건축물이'—이게 투츠 부인이 쓴 말입니다,\" 투츠 씨가 의기양양하게 말한다. \"'서서히 솟아오르고 있어서, 어쩌면 그이가 한때 우두머리였던 그 회사에 맞먹거나, 어쩌면 능가할지도 모르는데, 그 회사의 자그마한 시작은 (흔한 잘못이지만 나쁜 잘못이라고 투츠 부인이 그러더군요) 그이의 기억에서 빠져나가 버렸지요. 그리하여,' 제 아내가 그러기를, '결국 그이의 딸로부터, 또 하나의 돔비 앤드 선이 떠오를'—아니 '솟아오를'이었네요. 그게 투츠 부인이 쓴 말입니다—'의기양양하게!'\"\n\n투츠 씨는 파이프의 도움을 받아—그것을 웅변의 목적에 쓰게 되어 무척 다행스러워하는데, 본래 용도대로 쓰면 매우 불편한 느낌이 드는 까닭이다—아내의 이 예언적인 문장을 어찌나 거창하게 살려 내는지, 선장은 잔뜩 흥분한 나머지 번들거리는 모자를 내던지며 외친다.\n\n\"솔 길스, 학문하는 사람이자 내 오랜 동무여, 월터가 처음 장사에 발을 들이던 그날 밤, 내가 그 친구더러 펼쳐 보라고 한 게 뭐였더라? 바로 이 인용구 아니었나, '다시 돌아오라 휘팅턴, 런던 시장이여, 그리고 늙거든 그것에서 결코 떠나지 말지어다.' 그 말 아니었나, 솔 길스?\"\n\n\"분명 그랬지, 네드,\" 늙은 기계 제작자가 대답한다. \"잘 기억하고 있다네.\"\n\n\"그럼 내 한마디 함세,\" 선장이 의자에 등을 기대고는 어마어마한 고함을 내지를 채비로 가슴을 가다듬으며 말한다. \"내 자네들한테 '어여쁜 페그'를 처음부터 끝까지 불러 줄 테니, 두 사람 다 후렴 부를 준비를 단단히 하게나!\"\n\n땅에 묻힌 포도주는, 그 옛날 마데이라가 그러했듯, 점점 더 오래되어 간다. 그리고 병마다 먼지와 거미줄이 두텁게 쌓여 간다.\n\n가을날이 환히 빛나고, 바닷가 모래밭에는 종종 한 젊은 여인과 백발의 신사가 있다. 그들과 함께, 혹은 그 가까이에 두 아이가 있으니, 사내아이와 계집아이다. 그리고 대개는 늙은 개 한 마리가 그들과 동무한다.\n\n백발의 신사는 그 어린 사내아이와 함께 걷고, 아이와 이야기를 나누고, 아이의 놀이를 거들고, 아이의 시중을 들고, 마치 아이가 자기 삶의 목적이기라도 한 양 아이를 지켜본다. 아이가 생각에 잠기면 백발의 신사도 생각에 잠긴다. 그리고 이따금 아이가 그의 곁에 앉아 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 이것저것 물을 때면, 그는 그 자그마한 손을 자기 손에 쥐고는, 그것을 잡은 채 대답하기를 잊는다. 그러면 아이가 말한다.\n\n\"왜 그러세요, 할아버지! 제가 또 그 불쌍한 어린 외삼촌을 그렇게나 닮았나요?\"\n\n\"그래, 폴. 하지만 그 아인 약했고, 너는 아주 튼튼하단다.\"\n\n\"네, 맞아요, 저는 아주 튼튼해요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 아인 바닷가 자그마한 침대에 누워 있었지만, 너는 뛰어다닐 수 있지.\"\n\n그러고 나면 그들은 다시 부지런히 이리저리 거닐어 가는데, 백발의 신사는 아이가 자유롭게 뛰노는 모습을 보는 걸 가장 좋아하는 까닭이다. 그리고 그들이 함께 돌아다니는 동안, 그 둘 사이의 인연에 얽힌 이야기가 떠돌며 그들을 따라다닌다.\n\n그러나 플로렌스 말고는 아무도, 그 계집아이를 향한 백발 신사의 애정이 얼마나 깊은지 알지 못한다. 그 이야기만은 결코 떠돌지 않는다. 아이 본인조차도 그가 그 일에 대해 지키는 어떤 은밀함을 거의 의아해할 정도다. 그는 아이를 제 가슴속에 고이 간직한다. 그는 아이의 얼굴에 그늘이 지는 것을 차마 보지 못한다. 그는 아이가 외따로 떨어져 앉아 있는 것을 차마 보지 못한다. 그는 아이가 푸대접을 받는다고, 그런 일이 없는데도 공연히 짐작한다. 그는 잠든 아이를 보려고 살그머니 다가간다. 아침에 아이가 와서 자기를 깨워 주는 것을 그는 기뻐한다. 그는 곁에 아무도 없을 때, 아이를 가장 애틋이 여기고 가장 다정히 대한다. 그럴 때면 아이가 이따금 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"할아버지, 왜 저한테 입맞추실 때 우세요?\"\n\n그는 그저 \"어린 플로렌스! 어린 플로렌스!\" 하고 대답할 뿐, 아이의 진지한 두 눈에 드리운 곱슬머리를 쓸어 넘겨 줄 뿐이다.\n\n물결 속의 목소리들은 밤낮으로 그에게 플로렌스에 대해 나직이 속삭인다—저녁이면 그와, 활짝 핀 그의 딸과, 그녀의 남편이 그 물결 곁에 있을 때, 혹은 열린 창가에 앉아 그 노호를 귀 기울여 들을 때 가장 또렷하다. 그것들은 그에게 플로렌스에 대해, 그리고 달라진 그의 마음에 대해 말한다. 플로렌스에 대해, 그리고 영원하고 가없는 사랑을, 바다 너머로, 하늘 너머로, 저 멀리 보이지 않는 나라에까지 여전히 뻗어 가는 그 사랑을 그녀에게 끊임없이 속삭이는 일에 대해 말한다.\n\n저 거대한 바다로부터 솟아오르는 목소리들이, 너무 늦지 않게 우리와 저편 기슭의 보이지 않는 나라 사이에 깃들기를! 차라리, 훨씬 더 차라리, 그 목소리들이 우리 어린 시절의 귓가에 그 나라에 대해 속삭여 주고, 그 빠른 강물이 우리를 서둘러 데려가 주기를!\n\n1848년 서문\n이 인사의 자리에서 독자들에게 작별을 고할 늘 누리던 기회를 나는 포기할 수가 없다. 비록 우리가 방금 마친 이 여정의 매 단계마다 그들이 보여 준 그지없이 따뜻하고 진지한 공감을 그저 감사히 받들 수 있을 따름이지만 말이다.\n\n만일 그들 가운데 누군가가 이 소설의 굴대를 이루는 주요 사건 가운데 하나에서 슬픔을 느꼈다면, 그것이 그 슬픔을 함께 나눈 이들을 서로에게 더욱 정답게 묶어 주는 그런 부류의 슬픔이기를 나는 바란다. 이것은 나로서 사심 없는 마음만은 아니다. 적어도 그 누구 못지않게 나도 그 슬픔을 느꼈노라 주장할 수 있겠으며, 그 체험에서 내가 맡은 몫으로 하여 정겹게 기억되기를 진정 바라기 때문이다.\n\n데번셔 테라스, 1848년 3월 24일.\n1867년 서문\n\n나는 사람들의 성격을 올바르게 관찰하는 능력(혹은 습관)이 드문 것이라고 감히 믿는 바이다. 나는 내 경험 안에서, 사람들의 얼굴을 올바르게 관찰하는 능력(혹은 습관)조차도 결코 흔한 것이 아님을 알게 되었다. 앞엣것의 결함에서 비롯한다고 내가 짐작하는, 판단상의 가장 흔한 두 가지 착오는, 수줍음을 오만으로 혼동하는 것—이건 실로 매우 흔한 착오다—그리고 완고한 천성이란 끊임없이 자기 자신과 싸움을 벌이며 존재한다는 것을 이해하지 못하는 것이다.\n\n돔비 씨는 이 책에서도, 또 실제 삶에서도, 격렬한 변화를 겪지 않는다. 자신의 부당함에 대한 자각이 그의 내면에 줄곧 자리하고 있다. 그가 그것을 억누르면 억누를수록, 그는 그만큼 더 부당해질 수밖에 없다. 내면의 수치심과 외부의 상황이 그 싸움을 한 주, 혹은 하루 만에 끝장낼 수도 있겠으나, 그것은 여러 해에 걸친 싸움이었으며, 승부가 오래도록 팽팽히 저울질된 끝에야 비로소 끝까지 가려진다.\n\n나는 이 책을 제네바 호숫가에서 쓰기 시작했고, 영국에서 이어 쓰기에 앞서 몇 달 동안 프랑스에서 써 나갔다. 글쓰기와 그 글을 쓴 장소 사이의 연상이 내 마음속에 어찌나 묘하게 강한지, 오늘날까지도 나는, 비록 내 머릿속으로 그 자그마한 사관후보생 집의 계단을 하나하나 다 알고, 플로렌스가 혼인한 그 교회의 신도석 하나하나, 혹은 블림버 박사의 학교에 있던 젊은 신사들의 침대 하나하나를 두고 단언할 수 있으면서도, 여전히 어렴풋이 커틀 선장이 맥스팅거 부인을 피해 스위스의 산속에 틀어박혀 있는 모습을 떠올린다. 마찬가지로, 우연한 계기로 물결이 늘 무어라 말하던 것이 떠오를 때면, 내 기억은 겨우내 파리의 거리를 헤매고 다닌다—내 어린 벗과 내가 서로 헤어지는 그 장(章)을 다 쓰던 날 밤, 내가 무거운 마음으로 안절부절못하며 헤매고 다녔던 것처럼 말이다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-821"]=[[["Dombey sat in the corner of the darkened room in the great arm-chair by the bedside, and Son lay tucked up warm in a little basket bedstead, carefully disposed on a low settee immediately in front of the fire and close to it, as if his constitution were analogous to that of a muffin, and it was essential to toast him brown while he was very new.", "돔비는 어두컴컴한 방 한쪽 구석, 침대 곁의 커다란 안락의자에 앉아 있었고, 아들은 따뜻하게 감싸인 채 자그마한 바구니 침대에 누워 있었다. 그 침대는 난롯불 바로 앞에, 그것도 아주 가까이 낮은 긴 의자 위에 조심스럽게 놓여 있었는데, 마치 그의 체질이 머핀과 비슷해서 갓 만들어졌을 때 노릇노릇하게 구워 두는 것이 꼭 필요하기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["Dombey was about eight-and-forty years of age.", "돔비는 나이가 마흔여덟쯤이었다."], ["Son about eight-and-forty minutes.", "아들은 태어난 지 마흔여덟 분쯤 되었다."], ["Dombey was rather bald, rather red, and though a handsome well-made man, too stern and pompous in appearance, to be prepossessing.", "돔비는 다소 머리가 벗어졌고, 얼굴이 다소 붉었으며, 잘생기고 체격 좋은 사내였지만 인상이 지나치게 엄격하고 위엄을 부리는 탓에 호감을 주지는 못했다."], ["Son was very bald, and very red, and though (of course) an undeniably fine infant, somewhat crushed and spotty in his general effect, as yet.", "아들은 머리가 아주 벗어졌고, 얼굴이 아주 붉었으며, 비록 (당연히) 흠잡을 데 없이 잘생긴 갓난아이였으나 전체적인 인상은 아직 어딘가 우그러지고 얼룩덜룩했다."], ["On the brow of Dombey, Time and his brother Care had set some marks, as on a tree that was to come down in good time—remorseless twins they are for striding through their human forests, notching as they go—while the countenance of Son was crossed with a thousand little creases, which the same deceitful Time would take delight in smoothing out and wearing away with the flat part of his scythe, as a preparation of the surface for his deeper operations.", "돔비의 이마에는 세월과 그의 형제인 근심이 몇 개의 자국을 새겨 놓았는데, 마치 때가 되면 베어 넘길 나무에 표시를 해 둔 것 같았다—그 무자비한 쌍둥이는 인간이라는 숲을 성큼성큼 누비며 지나가는 길마다 표를 새기는 법이다—한편 아들의 얼굴에는 천 개의 자잘한 주름이 가로질러 있었으니, 그 똑같이 교활한 세월이 자기 낫의 평평한 면으로 그 주름들을 펴고 닳게 하는 일을, 더 깊은 작업을 위한 표면 손질로 즐겨 하게 될 것이었다."]], [["Dombey, exulting in the long-looked-for event, jingled and jingled the heavy gold watch-chain that depended from below his trim blue coat, whereof the buttons sparkled phosphorescently in the feeble rays of the distant fire.", "돔비는 오래 기다려 온 일이 이루어진 것에 우쭐하여, 단정한 푸른 외투 아래 늘어진 묵직한 금시곗줄을 짤랑짤랑 흔들고 또 흔들었으며, 그 외투의 단추들은 멀리 있는 난롯불의 희미한 빛 속에서 인광처럼 반짝였다."], ["Son, with his little fists curled up and clenched, seemed, in his feeble way, to be squaring at existence for having come upon him so unexpectedly.", "아들은 작은 주먹을 꼭 말아 쥔 채, 자기 식의 가냘픈 방식으로, 자기에게 이토록 뜻밖에 닥쳐온 존재라는 것을 향해 권투 자세라도 취하는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["“The House will once again, Mrs Dombey,”", "\"우리 회사는 다시 한번, 돔비 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “be not only in name but in fact Dombey and Son;”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"이름뿐 아니라 실제로도 돔비와 아들이 될 거요.\""], ["and he added, in a tone of luxurious satisfaction, with his eyes half-closed as if he were reading the name in a device of flowers, and inhaling their fragrance at the same time; “Dom-bey and Son!”", "그러고는 흐뭇하기 그지없는 어조로, 마치 꽃으로 꾸민 도안 속에서 그 이름을 읽으며 동시에 그 향기를 들이마시기라도 하듯 눈을 반쯤 감고 덧붙였다. \"돔—비와 아들!\""]], [["The words had such a softening influence, that he appended a term of endearment to Mrs Dombey’s name (though not without some hesitation, as being a man but little used to that form of address): and said, “Mrs Dombey, my—my dear.”", "그 말이 어찌나 마음을 누그러뜨렸던지, 그는 돔비 부인의 이름 뒤에 애정을 담은 호칭을 덧붙였다(다만 그런 호칭에 거의 익숙지 않은 사람인지라 약간의 망설임이 없지는 않았다). 그러고는 말했다, \"돔비 부인, 내—내 사랑.\""]], [["A transient flush of faint surprise overspread the sick lady’s face as she raised her eyes towards him.", "병든 부인은 그를 향해 눈을 들어 올리며, 옅은 놀라움의 빛이 얼굴에 잠시 스쳐 지나갔다."]], [["“He will be christened Paul, my—Mrs Dombey—of course.”", "\"이 아이는 폴이라고 세례를 받게 될 거요, 내—돔비 부인—물론이지.\""]], [["She feebly echoed, “Of course,”", "부인은 가냘프게 \"물론이죠\"라고 따라 했다."], ["or rather expressed it by the motion of her lips, and closed her eyes again.", "아니, 오히려 입술의 움직임으로 그렇게 나타내고는 다시 눈을 감았다."]], [["“His father’s name, Mrs Dombey, and his grandfather’s!", "\"제 아비의 이름이고, 제 할아비의 이름이오, 돔비 부인!"], ["I wish his grandfather were alive this day!", "그 애 할아비가 오늘 살아 있었더라면!"], ["There is some inconvenience in the necessity of writing Junior,”", "'주니어'라고 써야 하는 번거로움이 좀 있긴 하지만,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, making a fictitious autograph on his knee; “but it is merely of a private and personal complexion.", "돔비 씨는 무릎 위에 가상의 서명을 그어 보이며 말했다, \"그건 그저 사적이고 개인적인 성격일 뿐이오."], ["It doesn’t enter into the correspondence of the House.", "회사의 거래 서신에는 들어가지 않소."], ["Its signature remains the same.”", "그 서명은 똑같이 유지되지.\""], ["And again he said “Dombey and Son,” in exactly the same tone as before.", "그러고는 다시 한번, 앞서와 똑같은 어조로 \"돔비와 아들\"이라고 말했다."]], [["Those three words conveyed the one idea of Mr Dombey’s life.", "그 세 단어는 돔비 씨의 생애에서 단 하나의 관념을 담고 있었다."], ["The earth was made for Dombey and Son to trade in, and the sun and moon were made to give them light.", "대지는 돔비와 아들이 장사를 하라고 만들어졌고, 해와 달은 그들에게 빛을 주라고 만들어졌다."], ["Rivers and seas were formed to float their ships; rainbows gave them promise of fair weather; winds blew for or against their enterprises; stars and planets circled in their orbits, to preserve inviolate a system of which they were the centre.", "강과 바다는 그들의 배를 띄우라고 형성되었으며, 무지개는 그들에게 맑은 날씨를 약속해 주었고, 바람은 그들의 사업에 유리하게 혹은 불리하게 불었으며, 별과 행성들은 그들이 중심이 되는 체계를 침범당하지 않게 보존하려고 제 궤도를 돌았다."], ["Common abbreviations took new meanings in his eyes, and had sole reference to them.", "흔한 약자들도 그의 눈에는 새로운 의미를 띠었고 오로지 그들과만 관련되었다."], ["A. D.", "A."], ["had no concern with Anno Domini, but stood for anno Dombei—and Son.", "D.는 안노 도미니(주님의 해)와는 아무 상관이 없었고, 안노 돔베이—와 아들을 뜻했다."]], [["He had risen, as his father had before him, in the course of life and death, from Son to Dombey, and for nearly twenty years had been the sole representative of the Firm.", "그는 자기 아버지가 전에 그랬던 것처럼, 삶과 죽음의 과정을 거치며 '아들'에서 '돔비'로 올라섰고, 거의 이십 년 동안 회사의 유일한 대표자였다."], ["Of those years he had been married, ten—married, as some said, to a lady with no heart to give him; whose happiness was in the past, and who was content to bind her broken spirit to the dutiful and meek endurance of the present.", "그 세월 가운데 십 년을 결혼한 몸으로 지냈다—어떤 이들의 말로는, 그에게 줄 마음을 갖지 못한 부인과 결혼했다고 했다. 그녀의 행복은 과거에 있었고, 그녀는 상처 입은 영혼을 현재의 순종적이고 온순한 인내에 매어 두는 데 만족했다고 했다."], ["Such idle talk was little likely to reach the ears of Mr Dombey, whom it nearly concerned; and probably no one in the world would have received it with such utter incredulity as he, if it had reached him.", "그런 부질없는 소문이 정작 그것과 가장 깊이 관련된 돔비 씨의 귀에 들어갈 리는 거의 없었다. 또 설령 그것이 그에게 닿았다 하더라도, 세상에 그만큼 완전히 믿지 않을 사람도 아마 없었을 것이다."], ["Dombey and Son had often dealt in hides, but never in hearts.", "돔비와 아들은 가죽은 자주 거래했어도, 마음은 결코 거래한 적이 없었다."], ["They left that fancy ware to boys and girls, and boarding-schools and books.", "그런 공상 같은 물건은 소년 소녀들에게, 그리고 기숙학교와 책들에게 맡겨 두었다."], ["Mr Dombey would have reasoned: That a matrimonial alliance with himself must, in the nature of things, be gratifying and honourable to any woman of common sense.", "돔비 씨라면 이렇게 따졌을 것이다. 자기와의 혼인 결합은, 사물의 본성상, 상식을 갖춘 여자라면 누구에게나 흡족하고 영예로운 일임에 틀림없다고."], ["That the hope of giving birth to a new partner in such a House, could not fail to awaken a glorious and stirring ambition in the breast of the least ambitious of her sex.", "그런 회사의 새 동업자를 낳을 수 있다는 희망은, 자기 성별 가운데 가장 야심 없는 여자의 가슴속에서조차 영광스럽고 가슴 벅찬 야심을 일깨우지 않을 수 없다고."], ["That Mrs Dombey had entered on that social contract of matrimony: almost necessarily part of a genteel and wealthy station, even without reference to the perpetuation of family Firms: with her eyes fully open to these advantages.", "돔비 부인은 혼인이라는 그 사회적 계약에—집안 회사를 영속시키는 문제를 차치하고서라도 품위 있고 부유한 신분에는 거의 필연적으로 따르는 그 계약에—이런 이점들을 두 눈 똑똑히 뜨고 들어왔다고."], ["That Mrs Dombey had had daily practical knowledge of his position in society.", "돔비 부인은 사교계에서 자기의 지위를 날마다 실질적으로 알고 지냈다고."], ["That Mrs Dombey had always sat at the head of his table, and done the honours of his house in a remarkably lady-like and becoming manner.", "돔비 부인은 늘 그의 식탁 상석에 앉아, 그의 집안의 접대를 매우 귀부인답고 보기 좋은 태도로 해 왔다고."], ["That Mrs Dombey must have been happy.", "돔비 부인은 행복했음에 틀림없다고."], ["That she couldn’t help it.", "행복하지 않을 수 없었으리라고."]], [["Or, at all events, with one drawback.", "또는, 어쨌든 한 가지 결함만 빼고는. 그렇다."], ["Yes. That he would have allowed.", "그 점은 그도 인정했을 것이다."], ["With only one; but that one certainly involving much.", "단 하나뿐이지만, 그 하나에는 확실히 많은 것이 걸려 있었다."], ["With the drawback of hope deferred.", "바로 이루어지기를 미루어 온 희망이라는 결함."], ["That hope deferred, which, (as the Scripture very correctly tells us, Mr Dombey would have added in a patronising way; for his highest distinct idea even of Scripture, if examined, would have been found to be; that as forming part of a general whole, of which Dombey and Son formed another part, it was therefore to be commended and upheld) maketh the heart sick.", "그 미루어진 희망은 (성경이 매우 정확하게 일러 주듯이, 라고 돔비 씨는 생색내는 투로 덧붙였을 것이다. 그가 성경에 대해 가진 가장 분명한 관념을 따져 보면, 돔비와 아들이 또 다른 한 부분을 이루는 어떤 전체의 한 부분을 형성하는 것이므로, 그러니 칭송하고 떠받들 만하다는 것이었으니까) 마음을 병들게 하는 법이다."], ["They had been married ten years, and until this present day on which Mr Dombey sat jingling and jingling his heavy gold watch-chain in the great arm-chair by the side of the bed, had had no issue.", "그들은 결혼한 지 십 년이 되었지만, 돔비 씨가 침대 곁의 커다란 안락의자에 앉아 묵직한 금시곗줄을 짤랑짤랑 흔들고 있는 바로 오늘에 이르도록, 자식이 없었다."]], [["—To speak of; none worth mentioning.", "—말할 만한 자식 말이다."], ["There had been a girl some six years before, and the child, who had stolen into the chamber unobserved, was now crouching timidly, in a corner whence she could see her mother’s face.", "거론할 가치가 있는 자식이 없었다. 한 육 년쯤 전에 딸아이가 하나 있었는데, 아무도 모르게 방 안으로 살그머니 들어온 그 아이는 지금 어미의 얼굴이 보이는 구석에 겁먹은 듯 웅크리고 있었다."], ["But what was a girl to Dombey and Son!", "그러나 돔비와 아들에게 딸이란 무엇이란 말인가!"], ["In the capital of the House’s name and dignity, such a child was merely a piece of base coin that couldn’t be invested—a bad Boy—nothing more.", "회사의 이름과 위엄이라는 자본 안에서 그런 아이란 그저 투자할 수 없는 한 닢의 위조 동전—나쁜 사내아이—그 이상도 이하도 아니었다."]], [["Mr Dombey’s cup of satisfaction was so full at this moment, however, that he felt he could afford a drop or two of its contents, even to sprinkle on the dust in the by-path of his little daughter.", "그러나 이 순간 돔비 씨의 만족이라는 잔은 어찌나 가득 차 있었던지, 그 내용물을 한두 방울쯤은, 어린 딸의 샛길에 깔린 먼지 위에 뿌려 줄 정도로는 여유가 있다고 느꼈다."]], [["So he said, “Florence, you may go and look at your pretty brother, if you like, I daresay.", "그래서 그는 말했다, \"플로렌스, 보고 싶으면 가서 네 예쁜 동생을 봐도 좋다, 그래."], ["Don’t touch him!”", "손은 대지 말고!\""]], [["The child glanced keenly at the blue coat and stiff white cravat, which, with a pair of creaking boots and a very loud ticking watch, embodied her idea of a father; but her eyes returned to her mother’s face immediately, and she neither moved nor answered.", "아이는 그 푸른 외투와 빳빳한 흰 크라바트를 날카롭게 흘끗 보았다. 그것은 삐걱거리는 부츠 한 켤레와 아주 큰 소리로 똑딱이는 시계와 더불어, 아이가 아버지에 대해 품은 관념을 이루는 것이었다. 그러나 아이의 눈은 곧바로 어미의 얼굴로 되돌아갔고, 아이는 움직이지도 대답하지도 않았다."]], [["“Her insensibility is as proof against a brother as against every thing else,”", "\"저 애의 무감각함은 다른 모든 것에 그러하듯 동생에게도 끄떡없군,\""], ["said Mr Dombey to himself He seemed so confirmed in a previous opinion by the discovery, as to be quite glad of it.”", "돔비 씨는 속으로 중얼거렸다. 이 발견으로 그는 전부터 품어 온 생각이 확증된 듯하여, 오히려 그 사실이 꽤 흡족한 모양이었다."]], [["Next moment, the lady had opened her eyes and seen the child; and the child had run towards her; and, standing on tiptoe, the better to hide her face in her embrace, had clung about her with a desperate affection very much at variance with her years.", "다음 순간, 부인이 눈을 떴고 아이를 보았다. 아이는 어미에게로 달려갔다. 그리고 발끝으로 서서, 어미의 품에 얼굴을 더 잘 묻으려는 듯, 제 나이와는 도무지 어울리지 않는 필사적인 애정으로 어미에게 매달렸다."]], [["“Oh Lord bless me!”", "\"오, 맙소사!\""], ["said Mr Dombey, rising testily.", "돔비 씨가 짜증스럽게 일어서며 말했다."], ["“A very ill-advised and feverish proceeding this, I am sure.", "\"이건 정말이지 매우 경솔하고 신경을 자극하는 짓이오."], ["Please to ring there for Miss Florence’s nurse.", "저기 종을 울려서 플로렌스 아가씨의 유모를 좀 부르시오."], ["Really the person should be more care-”", "정말이지 사람이 좀 더 조심을—\""]], [["“Wait!", "\"잠깐!"], ["I—had better ask Doctor Peps if he’ll have the goodness to step upstairs again perhaps.", "차라리—펩스 박사에게 다시 한번 위층으로 올라와 주시지 않겠느냐고 부탁드리는 게 낫겠소."], ["I’ll go down.", "내가 내려가겠소."], ["I’ll go down.", "내려가겠소."], ["I needn’t beg you,”", "굳이 부탁할 것도 없겠지만,\""], ["he added, pausing for a moment at the settee before the fire, “to take particular care of this young gentleman, Mrs ——”", "그는 난롯불 앞 긴 의자 곁에서 잠시 멈춰 서서 덧붙였다, \"이 어린 신사분을 각별히 잘 보살펴 주시오, 그—부인.\""]], [["“Blockitt, Sir?”", "\"블로킷이옵니다, 나리?\""], ["suggested the nurse, a simpering piece of faded gentility, who did not presume to state her name as a fact, but merely offered it as a mild suggestion.", "유모가 조심스레 일러 주었다. 빛바랜 점잖음을 지닌, 실없이 웃는 여인이었는데, 자기 이름을 사실로서 단언하려 들지는 않고 그저 부드러운 제안처럼 내놓았다."]], [["“Of this young gentleman, Mrs Blockitt.”", "\"이 어린 신사분을 말이오, 블로킷 부인.\""]], [["“No, Sir, indeed.", "\"네, 나리, 여부가 있겠습니까."], ["I remember when Miss Florence was born—”", "플로렌스 아가씨가 태어나셨을 때를 기억하옵니다—\""]], [["“Ay, ay, ay,”", "\"그래, 그래, 그래,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, bending over the basket bedstead, and slightly bending his brows at the same time.", "돔비 씨가 바구니 침대 위로 몸을 굽히며, 동시에 살짝 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["“Miss Florence was all very well, but this is another matter.", "\"플로렌스 아가씨는 다 좋았소, 그러나 이건 다른 문제요."], ["This young gentleman has to accomplish a destiny.", "이 어린 신사분에게는 이루어야 할 운명이 있소."], ["A destiny, little fellow!”", "운명이란다, 꼬마야!\""], ["As he thus apostrophised the infant he raised one of his hands to his lips, and kissed it; then, seeming to fear that the action involved some compromise of his dignity, went, awkwardly enough, away.", "이렇게 갓난아이에게 일장 연설을 하면서 그는 한 손을 입술로 가져가 입을 맞추었다. 그러더니 그 행동이 자기 위엄을 다소 손상시키는 것은 아닐까 두려워하는 듯, 어색하기 짝이 없는 걸음으로 자리를 떴다."]], [["Doctor Parker Peps, one of the Court Physicians, and a man of immense reputation for assisting at the increase of great families, was walking up and down the drawing-room with his hands behind him, to the unspeakable admiration of the family Surgeon, who had regularly puffed the case for the last six weeks, among all his patients, friends, and acquaintances, as one to which he was in hourly expectation day and night of being summoned, in conjunction with Doctor Parker Pep.", "궁정 시의(侍醫) 가운데 한 사람이자 명문가의 출산을 돕는 일로 막대한 명성을 떨치는 파커 펩스 박사가, 두 손을 뒤로 한 채 응접실을 오락가락 거닐고 있었다. 그 모습은 집안 주치의의 말할 수 없는 감탄을 자아냈는데, 이 주치의는 지난 육 주 동안 자기 환자, 친구, 지인들 사이에서 이 일을 두고, 자기가 파커 펩스 박사와 함께 밤낮으로 시시각각 호출되기를 고대하고 있는 건이라고 줄곧 떠벌려 온 터였다."]], [["“Well, Sir,”", "\"자, 그래,\""], ["said Doctor Parker Peps in a round, deep, sonorous voice, muffled for the occasion, like the knocker; “do you find that your dear lady is at all roused by your visit?”", "파커 펩스 박사가 둥글고 깊고 우렁찬 목소리로, 이번 경우에 맞게 문고리처럼 소리를 죽여 말했다, \"선생의 방문으로 댁의 사랑스러운 부인께서 조금이라도 기운을 차리신 듯합니까?\""]], [["“Stimulated as it were?”", "\"말하자면 자극을 받으셨는지요?\""], ["said the family practitioner faintly: bowing at the same time to the Doctor, as much as to say, “Excuse my putting in a word, but this is a valuable connexion.”", "집안 주치의가 가냘프게 말했다. 동시에 박사에게 절을 했는데, 그 절은 마치 \"한마디 거드는 무례를 용서하십시오, 그러나 이건 귀중한 인연이라서요\"라고 말하는 듯했다."]], [["Mr Dombey was quite discomfited by the question.", "돔비 씨는 그 질문에 완전히 당황했다."], ["He had thought so little of the patient, that he was not in a condition to answer it.", "그는 환자에 대해 너무도 생각해 둔 것이 없어서 대답할 처지가 아니었다."], ["He said that it would be a satisfaction to him, if Doctor Parker Peps would walk upstairs again.", "그는 파커 펩스 박사가 다시 위층으로 올라가 주신다면 자기로서는 흡족하겠다고 말했다."]], [["“Good!", "\"좋습니다!"], ["We must not disguise from you, Sir,”", "선생께 숨길 수는 없는 일입니다만,\""], ["said Doctor Parker Peps, “that there is a want of power in Her Grace the Duchess—I beg your pardon; I confound names; I should say, in your amiable lady.", "파커 펩스 박사가 말했다, \"공작부인 마님께 기력이 부족하시어—실례했습니다. 제가 이름을 혼동했군요."], ["That there is a certain degree of languor, and a general absence of elasticity, which we would rather—not—”", "댁의 상냥하신 부인께 그렇다는 말씀입니다. 어느 정도의 무기력함과, 전반적인 탄력의 부재가 있어서, 저희로서는 차라리—없었으면—\""]], [["“See,”", "\"하는 바입니다,\""], ["interposed the family practitioner with another inclination of the head.", "집안 주치의가 다시 한번 고개를 숙이며 끼어들었다."]], [["“Quite so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다,\""], ["said Doctor Parker Peps, “which we would rather not see.", "파커 펩스 박사가 말했다, \"저희로서는 차라리 보고 싶지 않은 것이지요. 캥커비 부인의 체계가—실례했습니다."], ["It would appear that the system of Lady Cankaby—excuse me: I should say of Mrs Dombey: I confuse the names of cases—”", "돔비 부인이라 해야지요. 제가 증례들의 이름을 혼동해서—\""]], [["“So very numerous,”", "\"하도 많으시니,\""], ["murmured the family practitioner—“can’t be expected I’m sure—quite wonderful if otherwise—Doctor Parker Peps’s West-End practice—”", "집안 주치의가 중얼거렸다—\"그러시는 것도 무리가 아니지요—오히려 안 그러시면 놀라운 일입니다—파커 펩스 박사님 같은 웨스트엔드 진료라면—\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙습니다,\""], ["said the Doctor, “quite so.", "박사가 말했다, \"바로 그렇습니다."], ["It would appear, I was observing, that the system of our patient has sustained a shock, from which it can only hope to rally by a great and strong—”", "제가 말씀드리려던 것은, 우리 환자분의 체계가 충격을 받은 듯하다는 것입니다. 그 충격에서 회복하려면 오직 크고 강력한—\""]], [["“And vigorous,”", "\"그리고 활기찬,\""], ["murmured the family practitioner.", "집안 주치의가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Quite so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다,\""], ["assented the Doctor—“and vigorous effort.", "박사가 동의했다—\"그리고 활기찬 노력을 바랄 수밖에 없습니다."], ["Mr Pilkins here, who from his position of medical adviser in this family—no one better qualified to fill that position, I am sure.”", "여기 계신 필킨스 씨는, 이 댁의 의학 자문이라는 위치로 보아—그 위치를 채우기에 이분보다 더 적임자는 없으리라 확신합니다.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["murmured the family practitioner.", "집안 주치의가 중얼거렸다."], ["“‘Praise from Sir Hubert Stanley!’”", "\"'휴버트 스탠리 경의 칭찬이로군!'\""]], [["“You are good enough,”", "\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니,\""], ["returned Doctor Parker Peps, “to say so.", "파커 펩스 박사가 응수했다, \"황송합니다."], ["Mr Pilkins who, from his position, is best acquainted with the patient’s constitution in its normal state (an acquaintance very valuable to us in forming our opinions in these occasions), is of opinion, with me, that Nature must be called upon to make a vigorous effort in this instance; and that if our interesting friend the Countess of Dombey—I beg your pardon; Mrs Dombey—should not be—”", "필킨스 씨는, 그 위치로 보아, 정상 상태에서의 환자분의 체질을 가장 잘 알고 계시니(이런 경우 저희가 견해를 세우는 데 매우 귀중한 지식이지요), 저와 마찬가지로, 이번 경우에는 자연이 활기찬 노력을 발휘해 주어야 한다는 의견이십니다. 그리고 만일 우리의 흥미로운 벗, 돔비 백작부인께서—정말 실례했습니다, 돔비 부인께서—그러지—\""]], [["“Able,”", "\"못하신다면,\""], ["said the family practitioner.", "집안 주치의가 말했다."]], [["“To make,”", "\"발휘하지,\""], ["said Doctor Parker Peps.", "파커 펩스 박사가 말했다."]], [["“That effort,”", "\"그 노력을,\""], ["said the family practitioner.", "집안 주치의가 말했다."]], [["“Successfully,”", "\"성공적으로,\""], ["said they both together.", "둘이 함께 말했다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["added Doctor Parker Peps, alone and very gravely, “a crisis might arise, which we should both sincerely deplore.”", "파커 펩스 박사가 혼자서, 매우 엄숙하게 덧붙였다, \"우리 두 사람 모두가 진심으로 애통해할 위기가 닥칠 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["With that, they stood for a few seconds looking at the ground.", "그 말과 함께, 둘은 몇 초 동안 바닥을 내려다보며 서 있었다."], ["Then, on the motion—made in dumb show—of Doctor Parker Peps, they went upstairs; the family practitioner opening the room door for that distinguished professional, and following him out, with most obsequious politeness.", "그러다가 파커 펩스 박사가—말없이 몸짓으로—신호하자, 둘은 위층으로 올라갔다. 집안 주치의가 그 저명한 동업자를 위해 방문을 열어 주고, 더없이 알랑거리는 정중함으로 그 뒤를 따라 나갔다."]], [["To record of Mr Dombey that he was not in his way affected by this intelligence, would be to do him an injustice.", "돔비 씨가 이 소식에 자기 나름으로는 동요하지 않았다고 기록한다면, 그것은 그에게 부당한 일이 될 것이다."], ["He was not a man of whom it could properly be said that he was ever startled, or shocked; but he certainly had a sense within him, that if his wife should sicken and decay, he would be very sorry, and that he would find a something gone from among his plate and furniture, and other household possessions, which was well worth the having, and could not be lost without sincere regret.", "그는 언제 놀라거나 충격받은 적이 있다고 함부로 말할 수 있는 사람은 아니었다. 그러나 그는 분명, 만일 아내가 병들어 시들어 간다면 자기는 무척 안타까울 것이며, 자기의 은식기며 가구며 그 밖의 살림살이 가운데서 가질 만한 가치가 충분했던 어떤 것이 사라져 버린 것을, 진심 어린 애석함 없이는 잃을 수 없는 어떤 것이 사라져 버린 것을 느끼게 되리라는 정도의 감각은 마음속에 지니고 있었다."], ["Though it would be a cool, business-like, gentlemanly, self-possessed regret, no doubt.", "다만 그것은 의심할 바 없이 냉정하고 사무적이며 신사답고 침착한 애석함일 터였다."]], [["His meditations on the subject were soon interrupted, first by the rustling of garments on the staircase, and then by the sudden whisking into the room of a lady rather past the middle age than otherwise but dressed in a very juvenile manner, particularly as to the tightness of her bodice, who, running up to him with a kind of screw in her face and carriage, expressive of suppressed emotion, flung her arms around his neck, and said, in a choking voice,", "이 문제에 대한 그의 사색은 곧 중단되었다. 처음에는 계단에서 옷자락 스치는 소리가 났고, 다음에는 한 부인이 방 안으로 휙 들이닥쳤다. 중년을 다소 넘긴 편이었으나 옷차림은 매우 젊은이답게, 특히 몸통을 꽉 조인 것이 그러했는데, 억눌린 감정이 드러나는 듯 얼굴과 거동이 비틀어진 채 그에게 달려와서는, 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안고, 목멘 소리로 말했다,"]], [["“My dear Paul!", "\"오, 폴!"], ["He’s quite a Dombey!”", "영락없는 돔비 집안이구나!\""]], [["“Well, well!”", "\"자, 자!\""], ["returned her brother—for Mr Dombey was her brother—“I think he is like the family.", "그녀의 오라비가—돔비 씨는 그녀의 오라비였다—응수했다. \"내 생각에도 집안을 닮은 것 같소."], ["Don’t agitate yourself, Louisa.”", "그렇게 흥분하지 마시오, 루이자.\""]], [["“It’s very foolish of me,”", "\"내가 너무 어리석지,\""], ["said Louisa, sitting down, and taking out her pocket-handkerchief, “but he’s—he’s such a perfect Dombey!”", "루이자가 자리에 앉아 손수건을 꺼내며 말했다, \"하지만 그 애는—그 애는 정말이지 완벽한 돔비란 말이야!\""]], [["Mr Dombey coughed.", "돔비 씨가 헛기침을 했다."]], [["“It’s so extraordinary,”", "\"정말 놀라워,\""], ["said Louisa; smiling through her tears, which indeed were not overpowering, “as to be perfectly ridiculous.", "루이자가 눈물 사이로 미소 지으며 말했다. 사실 그 눈물이 그리 주체할 수 없는 정도는 아니었다."], ["So completely our family.", "\"어처구니없을 만큼 놀랍다니까."], ["I never saw anything like it in my life!”", "완전히 우리 집안이야. 나는 평생 이런 건 본 적이 없어!\""]], [["“But what is this about Fanny, herself?”", "\"그런데 패니는, 패니 본인은 어떻소?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“How is Fanny?”", "\"패니는 어떻소?\""]], [["“My dear Paul,”", "\"오, 폴,\""], ["returned Louisa, “it’s nothing whatever.", "루이자가 응수했다, \"아무것도 아니야."], ["Take my word, it’s nothing whatever.", "내 말 믿어, 정말 아무것도 아니야."], ["There is exhaustion, certainly, but nothing like what I underwent myself, either with George or Frederick.", "기진맥진하긴 했지, 물론. 하지만 내가 조지나 프레더릭 때 겪은 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니야."], ["An effort is necessary.", "노력이 필요할 뿐이야."], ["That’s all.", "그게 다라고."], ["If dear Fanny were a Dombey!—But I daresay she’ll make it; I have no doubt she’ll make it.", "사랑스러운 패니가 돔비 사람이기만 했어도!—하지만 분명 해낼 거야. 틀림없이 해낼 거야."], ["Knowing it to be required of her, as a duty, of course she’ll make it.", "그게 자기 의무로 요구되는 일임을 아니까, 당연히 해낼 거야."], ["My dear Paul, it’s very weak and silly of me, I know, to be so trembly and shaky from head to foot; but I am so very queer that I must ask you for a glass of wine and a morsel of that cake.”", "오, 폴, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 이렇게 떨고 흔들리는 게 정말 약하고 어리석은 짓이라는 거 나도 알아. 하지만 너무 이상하게 떨려서, 포도주 한 잔하고 저 케이크 한 조각만 부탁해야겠어.\""]], [["Mr Dombey promptly supplied her with these refreshments from a tray on the table.", "돔비 씨는 즉시 탁자 위 쟁반에서 그 다과를 가져다주었다."]], [["“I shall not drink my love to you, Paul,”", "\"네 건강을 위해 마시지는 않겠어, 폴,\""], ["said Louisa: “I shall drink to the little Dombey.", "루이자가 말했다. \"꼬마 돔비를 위해 마실 거야."], ["Good gracious me!—it’s the most astonishing thing I ever knew in all my days, he’s such a perfect Dombey.”", "어머나 세상에!—내 평생 알아 온 것 중에 가장 놀라운 일이야, 그 애가 어쩜 그렇게 완벽한 돔비인지.\""]], [["Quenching this expression of opinion in a short hysterical laugh which terminated in tears, Louisa cast up her eyes, and emptied her glass.", "이 의견의 표명을 짤막한 히스테릭한 웃음으로 삼켰는데, 그 웃음은 끝내 눈물로 변했고, 루이자는 눈을 치켜뜨며 잔을 비웠다."]], [["“I know it’s very weak and silly of me,” she repeated, “to be so trembly and shaky from head to foot, and to allow my feelings so completely to get the better of me, but I cannot help it.", "\"이렇게 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 흔들리고, 내 감정에 완전히 사로잡히는 게 정말 약하고 어리석은 짓이라는 거 나도 알아,\""], ["I thought I should have fallen out of the staircase window as I came down from seeing dear Fanny, and that tiddy ickle sing.”", "그녀가 되풀이했다, \"하지만 어쩔 수가 없어. 사랑스러운 패니랑 그 쪼끄만 아가를 보고 내려오다가 계단 창문 밖으로 떨어질 뻔했다니까.\""], ["These last words originated in a sudden vivid reminiscence of the baby.", "이 마지막 말은 갓난아이에 대한 갑작스럽고 생생한 회상에서 비롯된 것이었다."]], [["They were succeeded by a gentle tap at the door.", "그 말 뒤를 이어 문에서 부드러운 노크 소리가 났다."]], [["“Mrs Chick,”", "\"칙 부인,\""], ["said a very bland female voice outside, “how are you now, my dear friend?”", "바깥에서 매우 상냥한 여자 목소리가 말했다, \"이제 좀 어떠세요, 내 소중한 친구?\""]], [["“My dear Paul,”", "\"오, 폴,\""], ["said Louisa in a low voice, as she rose from her seat, “it’s Miss Tox.", "루이자가 자리에서 일어서며 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"톡스 양이야."], ["The kindest creature!", "더없이 친절한 사람이지!"], ["I never could have got here without her!", "그녀가 없었으면 여기까지 오지도 못했을 거야!"], ["Miss Tox, my brother Mr Dombey.", "톡스 양, 이쪽은 제 오라비 돔비 씨예요."], ["Paul, my dear, my very particular friend Miss Tox.”", "폴, 이쪽은 내 아주 각별한 친구 톡스 양이야.\""]], [["The lady thus specially presented, was a long lean figure, wearing such a faded air that she seemed not to have been made in what linen-drapers call “fast colours” originally, and to have, by little and little, washed out.", "이렇게 특별히 소개된 부인은 길쭉하고 마른 체격으로, 어찌나 빛바랜 분위기를 풍기는지 본디 포목상들이 말하는 \"물 안 빠지는 색\"으로 지어진 것이 아니라, 조금씩 조금씩 색이 빠져 버린 것처럼 보였다."], ["But for this she might have been described as the very pink of general propitiation and politeness.", "이 점만 아니라면 그녀는 일반적인 비위 맞추기와 정중함의 더할 나위 없는 본보기라고 묘사될 만했다."], ["From a long habit of listening admiringly to everything that was said in her presence, and looking at the speakers as if she were mentally engaged in taking off impressions of their images upon her soul, never to part with the same but with life, her head had quite settled on one side.", "자기 앞에서 하는 모든 말을 감탄하며 경청하고, 말하는 이들을 마치 그 모습을 마음속으로 영혼에 새겨 두어 죽기 전에는 결코 떼어 놓지 않으려는 듯 바라보는 오랜 습관 탓에, 그녀의 머리는 완전히 한쪽으로 기울어 있었다."], ["Her hands had contracted a spasmodic habit of raising themselves of their own accord as in involuntary admiration.", "그녀의 두 손은 저도 모르는 감탄을 표하듯 제멋대로 들어 올려지는 경련성 습관이 들어 있었다."], ["Her eyes were liable to a similar affection.", "그녀의 두 눈도 비슷한 증세에 걸리기 쉬웠다."], ["She had the softest voice that ever was heard; and her nose, stupendously aquiline, had a little knob in the very centre or key-stone of the bridge, whence it tended downwards towards her face, as in an invincible determination never to turn up at anything.", "그녀는 일찍이 들어 본 적 없을 만큼 부드러운 목소리를 지녔으며, 엄청나게 매부리진 그녀의 코는 콧대의 한가운데, 곧 그 마룻돌 격인 자리에 작은 혹이 하나 있어, 거기서부터 얼굴 쪽으로 처져 내려가 있었으니, 마치 무슨 일에도 결코 콧대를 치켜올리지 않겠다는 굽힐 수 없는 결의를 보이는 듯했다."]], [["Miss Tox’s dress, though perfectly genteel and good, had a certain character of angularity and scantiness.", "톡스 양의 옷차림은 더없이 점잖고 좋았으나, 어딘가 모나고 옹색한 데가 있었다. 그녀는 보닛과 모자에 시들시들한 자그마한 꽃들을 즐겨 꽂았다."], ["She was accustomed to wear odd weedy little flowers in her bonnets and caps.", "때로는 그녀의 머리카락 사이에서 기이한 풀잎들이 눈에 띄기도 했다."], ["Strange grasses were sometimes perceived in her hair; and it was observed by the curious, of all her collars, frills, tuckers, wristbands, and other gossamer articles—indeed of everything she wore which had two ends to it intended to unite—that the two ends were never on good terms, and wouldn’t quite meet without a struggle.", "그리고 호기심 많은 이들은 그녀의 깃, 주름 장식, 가슴 장식, 소맷부리, 그 밖의 온갖 얇디얇은 물건들에 대해—사실 그녀가 걸친 것 가운데 양쪽 끝을 맞붙이게 되어 있는 것은 무엇이든—그 양쪽 끝이 결코 사이가 좋은 법이 없어, 한바탕 씨름을 하지 않고서는 도무지 만나려 들지 않는다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["She had furry articles for winter wear, as tippets, boas, and muffs, which stood up on end in rampant manner, and were not at all sleek.", "그녀는 겨울에 걸칠 모피 물건들을 가지고 있었으니, 어깨걸이며 보아며 토시 같은 것들이었는데, 그것들은 사납게 곤두서 있어 도무지 매끈하지가 않았다."], ["She was much given to the carrying about of small bags with snaps to them, that went off like little pistols when they were shut up; and when full-dressed, she wore round her neck the barrenest of lockets, representing a fishy old eye, with no approach to speculation in it.", "그녀는 똑딱이가 달린 작은 가방들을 들고 다니기를 무척 좋아했는데, 그 가방들은 닫힐 때면 자그마한 권총처럼 탁 소리를 냈다. 그리고 성장(盛裝)을 할 때면, 목에 더없이 빈약한 로켓을 걸었는데, 거기에는 생기라곤 조금도 비치지 않는 물고기 눈 같은 늙은 눈알 하나가 새겨져 있었다."], ["These and other appearances of a similar nature, had served to propagate the opinion, that Miss Tox was a lady of what is called a limited independence, which she turned to the best account.", "이런 것들과 그와 비슷한 부류의 다른 모습들로 인해, 톡스 양은 이른바 제한된 자립 수단을 지닌 부인이며, 그것을 최대한 잘 활용한다는 견해가 퍼지게 되었다."], ["Possibly her mincing gait encouraged the belief, and suggested that her clipping a step of ordinary compass into two or three, originated in her habit of making the most of everything.", "어쩌면 그녀의 종종걸음 또한 그 믿음을 부추겨, 보통 보폭의 한 걸음을 두세 걸음으로 잘게 나누는 것이 무엇이든 최대한 알뜰히 쓰는 그녀의 습관에서 비롯된 것임을 짐작하게 했을지 모른다."]], [["“I am sure,”", "\"제가 확실히 말씀드리건대,\""], ["said Miss Tox, with a prodigious curtsey, “that to have the honour of being presented to Mr Dombey is a distinction which I have long sought, but very little expected at the present moment.", "톡스 양이 어마어마하게 큰 절을 하며 말했다, \"돔비 씨께 소개되는 영예를 누리는 것은 오래도록 바라 왔으면서도 지금 이 순간 도무지 기대하지 못한 영광이옵니다."], ["My dear Mrs Chick—may I say Louisa!”", "사랑하는 칙 부인—루이자라고 불러도 될까요!\""]], [["Mrs Chick took Miss Tox’s hand in hers, rested the foot of her wine-glass upon it, repressed a tear, and said in a low voice, “God bless you!”", "칙 부인은 톡스 양의 손을 자기 손으로 잡아, 그 위에 포도주 잔의 발을 얹고는, 눈물을 억누르며 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"하느님의 축복이 함께하길!\""]], [["“My dear Louisa then,”", "\"그럼 사랑하는 루이자,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “my sweet friend, how are you now?”", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"내 다정한 친구, 이제 좀 어떠세요?\""]], [["“Better,”", "\"좀 나아요,\""], ["Mrs Chick returned.", "칙 부인이 응수했다."], ["“Take some wine.", "\"포도주 좀 들어요."], ["You have been almost as anxious as I have been, and must want it, I am sure.”", "당신도 나만큼이나 마음 졸였을 테니, 분명 필요할 거예요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey of course officiated, and also refilled his sister’s glass, which she (looking another way, and unconscious of his intention) held straight and steady the while, and then regarded with great astonishment, saying, “My dear Paul, what have you been doing!”", "물론 돔비 씨가 거들었고, 누이의 잔도 다시 채워 주었는데, 누이는 (다른 데를 보느라 그의 의도를 알아채지 못한 채) 그동안 잔을 곧고 흔들림 없이 들고 있다가, 이윽고 크게 놀란 표정으로 그것을 바라보며 말했다, \"오, 폴, 도대체 무슨 짓을 한 거야!\""]], [["“Miss Tox, Paul,”", "\"톡스 양은, 폴,\""], ["pursued Mrs Chick, still retaining her hand, “knowing how much I have been interested in the anticipation of the event of today, and how trembly and shaky I have been from head to foot in expectation of it, has been working at a little gift for Fanny, which I promised to present.", "칙 부인이 여전히 그녀의 손을 잡은 채 말을 이었다, \"내가 오늘 있을 일을 얼마나 마음 졸이며 기대해 왔는지, 또 그것을 기다리며 머리끝부터 발끝까지 얼마나 떨고 흔들렸는지를 알고서, 패니에게 줄 작은 선물을 만들어 왔어. 내가 전해 주겠다고 약속했지."], ["Miss Tox is ingenuity itself.”", "톡스 양은 그야말로 손재주 그 자체라니까.\""]], [["“My dear Louisa,”", "\"오, 루이자,\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“Don’t say so.”", "\"그런 말씀 마세요.\""]], [["“It is only a pincushion for the toilette table, Paul,”", "\"화장대에 둘 바늘꽂이일 뿐이야, 폴,\""], ["resumed his sister; “one of those trifles which are insignificant to your sex in general, as it’s very natural they should be—we have no business to expect they should be otherwise—but to which we attach some interest.”", "그의 누이가 말을 이었다. \"당신네 남자들에게는 대체로 하찮은 그런 자질구레한 물건이지—그러는 게 지극히 당연하고, 우리도 달리 기대할 처지는 아니지만—그래도 우리 여자들은 거기에 어느 정도 의미를 두지.\""]], [["“Miss Tox is very good,”", "\"톡스 양은 참 친절하시군요,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“And I do say, and will say, and must say,”", "\"그리고 내가 말하건대, 또 말할 것이며, 또 말하지 않을 수 없는데,\""], ["pursued his sister, pressing the foot of the wine-glass on Miss Tox’s hand, at each of the three clauses, “that Miss Tox has very prettily adapted the sentiment to the occasion.", "그의 누이가 세 구절마다 한 번씩 톡스 양의 손 위에 포도주 잔의 발을 누르며 말을 이었다, \"톡스 양이 이 자리에 어울리게 글귀를 참으로 곱게 붙여 놓았어."], ["I call ‘Welcome little Dombey’ Poetry, myself!”", "나는 '환영해요, 꼬마 돔비'를 시(詩)라고 부르겠어!\""]], [["“Is that the device?”", "\"그게 그 도안이오?\""], ["inquired her brother.", "그녀의 오라비가 물었다."]], [["“That is the device,”", "\"그게 그 도안이야,\""], ["returned Louisa.", "루이자가 응수했다."]], [["“But do me the justice to remember, my dear Louisa,”", "\"하지만 부디 공정하게 헤아려 주세요, 사랑하는 루이자,\""], ["said Miss Tox in a tone of low and earnest entreaty, “that nothing but the—I have some difficulty in expressing myself—the dubiousness of the result would have induced me to take so great a liberty: ‘Welcome, Master Dombey,’ would have been much more congenial to my feelings, as I am sure you know.", "톡스 양이 낮고 간절한 애원의 어조로 말했다, \"오직—제 자신을 표현하기가 좀 어렵습니다만—결과가 불확실하다는 점 때문에 제가 이토록 큰 무례를 무릅쓰게 된 것이라고요. '환영합니다, 돔비 도련님'이 제 마음에는 훨씬 더 어울렸을 거라는 걸 분명 아실 거예요."], ["But the uncertainty attendant on angelic strangers, will, I hope, excuse what must otherwise appear an unwarrantable familiarity.”", "하지만 천사 같은 손님에게 따르는 불확실함이, 그러지 않았더라면 분에 넘치는 친근함으로 보였을 것을 부디 용서해 주리라 믿습니다.\""], ["Miss Tox made a graceful bend as she spoke, in favour of Mr Dombey, which that gentleman graciously acknowledged.", "톡스 양은 이렇게 말하며 돔비 씨를 향해 우아하게 몸을 굽혔고, 그 신사는 정중하게 그것을 받아 주었다."], ["Even the sort of recognition of Dombey and Son, conveyed in the foregoing conversation, was so palatable to him, that his sister, Mrs Chick—though he affected to consider her a weak good-natured person—had perhaps more influence over him than anybody else.", "앞서의 대화 속에 담긴 돔비와 아들에 대한 그런 종류의 인정조차 그에게는 무척 흡족했던 터라, 비록 그가 누이 칙 부인을 마음 약하고 사람 좋은 여자쯤으로 여기는 척했지만, 어쩌면 그녀는 세상 누구보다도 그에게 더 큰 영향력을 지니고 있었는지 모른다."]], [["“My dear Paul,”", "\"오, 폴,\""], ["that lady broke out afresh, after silently contemplating his features for a few moments, “I don’t know whether to laugh or cry when I look at you, I declare, you do so remind me of that dear baby upstairs.”", "그 부인이 몇 분 동안 말없이 그의 이목구비를 바라보다가 다시 입을 열었다, \"너를 보고 있으면 웃어야 할지 울어야 할지 모르겠어, 정말이지, 위층의 그 사랑스러운 아가가 어찌나 떠오르는지.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"자!\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with a sweet smile, “after this, I forgive Fanny everything!”", "칙 부인이 달콤한 미소를 지으며 말했다, \"이렇게 된 이상, 나는 패니의 모든 걸 용서하겠어!\""]], [["It was a declaration in a Christian spirit, and Mrs Chick felt that it did her good. Not that she had anything particular to forgive in her sister-in-law, nor indeed anything at all, except her having married her brother—in itself a species of audacity—and her having, in the course of events, given birth to a girl instead of a boy: which, as Mrs Chick had frequently observed, was not quite what she had expected of her, and was not a pleasant return for all the attention and distinction she had met with.", "그것은 기독교 정신에서 나온 선언이었고, 칙 부인은 그 말이 자기에게 흐뭇함을 안긴다고 느꼈다. 그렇다고 올케에게 딱히 용서할 무엇이 있었던 것은 아니었다. 아니, 사실 아무것도 없었다. 다만 자기 오라비와 결혼한 것—그 자체가 일종의 무엄함이었다—과, 일이 흘러가는 가운데 사내아이가 아닌 계집아이를 낳은 것을 빼면 말이다. 그 일에 대해서 칙 부인은 자주, 자기가 올케에게 기대했던 바와는 사뭇 다른 일이며, 올케가 받아 온 온갖 배려와 융숭한 대접에 대한 흐뭇한 보답은 못 된다고 한마디씩 해 온 터였다."]], [["Mr Dombey being hastily summoned out of the room at this moment, the two ladies were left alone together.", "이때 돔비 씨가 황급히 방 밖으로 불려 나가서, 두 부인만 함께 남게 되었다."], ["Miss Tox immediately became spasmodic.", "톡스 양은 즉시 경련하듯 들떴다."]], [["“I knew you would admire my brother.", "\"당신이 내 오라비를 흠모하게 될 줄 알았어요."], ["I told you so beforehand, my dear,”", "미리 말했잖아요, 그렇죠,\""], ["said Louisa.", "루이자가 말했다."], ["Miss Tox’s hands and eyes expressed how much.", "톡스 양의 두 손과 두 눈이 얼마나 그러한지를 표현했다."], ["“And as to his property, my dear!”", "\"게다가 그이의 재산으로 말하자면!\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Miss Tox, with deep feeling.", "톡스 양이 깊은 감정을 담아 말했다."]], [["“Im-mense!”", "\"어—마어마하지!\""]], [["“But his deportment, my dear Louisa!”", "\"하지만 그분의 그 거동이라니, 사랑하는 루이자!\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“His presence!", "\"그 위풍이라니!"], ["His dignity!", "그 위엄이라니!"], ["No portrait that I have ever seen of anyone has been half so replete with those qualities.", "제가 여태껏 본 어느 누구의 초상화도 그런 자질이 절반도 담겨 있지 않았어요."], ["Something so stately, you know: so uncompromising: so very wide across the chest: so upright!", "어찌나 당당한지: 어찌나 타협이라곤 모르는지: 가슴이 어찌나 떡 벌어졌는지: 어찌나 꼿꼿한지!"], ["A pecuniary Duke of York, my love, and nothing short of it!”", "돈으로 치면 요크 공작이세요, 그래요, 그에 못지않으세요!\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“That’s what I should designate him.”", "\"저 같으면 그렇게 일컫겠어요.\""]], [["“Why, my dear Paul!”", "\"아니, 폴!\""], ["exclaimed his sister, as he returned, “you look quite pale!", "그가 돌아오자 누이가 외쳤다, \"안색이 아주 창백하잖아!"], ["There’s nothing the matter?”", "무슨 일 있는 건 아니지?\""]], [["“I am sorry to say, Louisa, that they tell me that Fanny—”", "\"안타깝게도 말이오, 루이자, 사람들 말이 패니가—\""]], [["“Now, my dear Paul,”", "\"자, 폴,\""], ["returned his sister rising, “don’t believe it.", "누이가 일어서며 응수했다, \"그런 말 믿지 마."], ["Do not allow yourself to receive a turn unnecessarily.", "공연히 마음 흔들리지 말고."], ["Remember of what importance you are to society, and do not allow yourself to be worried by what is so very inconsiderately told you by people who ought to know better.", "네가 사회에 얼마나 중요한 존재인지를 생각하고, 더 잘 알아야 마땅한 사람들이 분별없이 떠벌리는 말에 휘둘리지 마."], ["Really I’m surprised at them.”", "정말이지 그 사람들이 어이가 없네.\""]], [["“I hope I know, Louisa,”", "\"내 처신은 나도 안다고 생각하오, 루이자,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, stiffly, “how to bear myself before the world.”", "돔비 씨가 뻣뻣하게 말했다, \"세상 앞에서 어떻게 처신해야 하는지를.\""]], [["“Nobody better, my dear Paul.", "\"누구보다도 잘 알지, 폴."], ["Nobody half so well.", "누구도 너만큼 못해."], ["They would be ignorant and base indeed who doubted it.”", "그걸 의심하는 자가 있다면 정말 무지하고 천한 자일 거야.\""]], [["“Ignorant and base indeed!”", "\"정말 무지하고 천한 자고말고요!\""], ["echoed Miss Tox softly.", "톡스 양이 부드럽게 따라 말했다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["pursued Louisa, “if you have any reliance on my experience, Paul, you may rest assured that there is nothing wanting but an effort on Fanny’s part.", "루이자가 말을 이었다, \"내 경험을 조금이라도 믿는다면, 폴, 부족한 건 패니 쪽의 노력 하나뿐이라고 확신해도 좋아."], ["And that effort,”", "그리고 그 노력을,\""], ["she continued, taking off her bonnet, and adjusting her cap and gloves, in a business-like manner, “she must be encouraged, and really, if necessary, urged to make.", "그녀는 사무적인 태도로 보닛을 벗고 모자와 장갑을 매만지며 말을 이었다, \"패니가 발휘하도록 북돋아 주어야 하고, 또 정말 필요하다면, 다그쳐서라도 발휘하게 해야 해."], ["Now, my dear Paul, come upstairs with me.”", "자, 폴, 나랑 같이 위층으로 가자.\""]], [["Mr Dombey, who, besides being generally influenced by his sister for the reason already mentioned, had really faith in her as an experienced and bustling matron, acquiesced; and followed her, at once, to the sick chamber.", "돔비 씨는, 앞서 말한 이유로 평소에도 누이에게 영향을 받기도 했거니와, 경험 많고 부산한 부인이라며 그녀를 정말로 신뢰하기도 했던 터라, 그 말에 따랐다. 그러고는 즉시 그녀를 따라 병실로 갔다."]], [["The lady lay upon her bed as he had left her, clasping her little daughter to her breast.", "부인은 그가 떠날 때 그대로 침대에 누워, 어린 딸을 가슴에 꼭 끌어안고 있었다."], ["The child clung close about her, with the same intensity as before, and never raised her head, or moved her soft cheek from her mother’s face, or looked on those who stood around, or spoke, or moved, or shed a tear.", "아이는 아까와 똑같은 강렬함으로 어미에게 바싹 매달려 있었고, 고개를 들지도, 부드러운 뺨을 어미의 얼굴에서 떼지도, 둘러선 이들을 쳐다보지도, 말하지도, 움직이지도, 눈물 한 방울 흘리지도 않았다."]], [["“Restless without the little girl,”", "\"그 어린 딸이 없으면 부인께서 불안해하셔서,\""], ["the Doctor whispered Mr Dombey.", "박사가 돔비 씨에게 속삭였다."], ["“We found it best to have her in again.”", "\"다시 들여보내는 게 좋겠다고 판단했습니다.\""]], [["“Can nothing be done?”", "\"무슨 방도가 없겠소?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 물었다."]], [["The Doctor shook his head.", "박사가 고개를 저었다."], ["“We can do no more.”", "\"더는 손쓸 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["The windows stood open, and the twilight was gathering without.", "창문들은 열려 있었고, 바깥에는 땅거미가 내려앉고 있었다."]], [["The scent of the restoratives that had been tried was pungent in the room, but had no fragrance in the dull and languid air the lady breathed.", "시도해 본 강심제들의 냄새가 방 안에 코를 찌르도록 배어 있었으나, 부인이 들이쉬는 흐릿하고 나른한 공기 속에서는 아무런 향기도 풍기지 않았다."]], [["There was such a solemn stillness round the bed; and the two medical attendants seemed to look on the impassive form with so much compassion and so little hope, that Mrs Chick was for the moment diverted from her purpose.", "침대 둘레에는 어찌나 엄숙한 정적이 감돌았는지, 또 두 의사가 그 미동도 없는 형체를 어찌나 큰 연민과 어찌나 적은 희망으로 바라보았는지, 칙 부인은 한순간 자기 목적을 잊고 말았다."], ["But presently summoning courage, and what she called presence of mind, she sat down by the bedside, and said in the low precise tone of one who endeavours to awaken a sleeper:", "그러나 이내 용기를 그러모으고, 스스로 침착함이라 부르는 것을 그러모아, 침대 곁에 앉아, 잠든 이를 깨우려는 사람의 낮고 또박또박한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Fanny!", "\"패니!"], ["Fanny!”", "패니!\""]], [["There was no sound in answer but the loud ticking of Mr Dombey’s watch and Doctor Parker Peps’s watch, which seemed in the silence to be running a race.", "대답으로는 아무 소리도 없었다. 다만 돔비 씨의 시계와 파커 펩스 박사의 시계가 크게 똑딱이는 소리뿐이었는데, 그 정적 속에서 두 시계는 경주라도 벌이는 듯했다."]], [["“Fanny, my dear,”", "\"패니, 얘,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with assumed lightness, “here’s Mr Dombey come to see you.", "칙 부인이 짐짓 경쾌한 척하며 말했다, \"여기 돔비 씨가 너를 보러 오셨어."], ["Won’t you speak to him?", "말 좀 안 할래?"], ["They want to lay your little boy—the baby, Fanny, you know; you have hardly seen him yet, I think—in bed; but they can’t till you rouse yourself a little.", "사람들이 네 어린 아들을—아가 말이야, 패니, 알지? 아직 거의 보지도 못했잖아—침대에 뉘려고 하는데, 네가 좀 정신을 차려야 그럴 수가 있어."], ["Don’t you think it’s time you roused yourself a little?", "이제 좀 정신을 차릴 때가 됐다고 생각하지 않니?"], ["Eh?”", "응?\""]], [["She bent her ear to the bed, and listened: at the same time looking round at the bystanders, and holding up her finger.", "그녀는 침대 쪽으로 귀를 기울이고 들었다. 동시에 둘러선 이들을 돌아보며 손가락을 들어 보였다."]], [["“Eh?”", "\"응?\""], ["she repeated, “what was it you said, Fanny?", "그녀가 되풀이했다, \"뭐라고 했어, 패니?"], ["I didn’t hear you.”", "못 들었어.\""]], [["No word or sound in answer.", "대답으로는 한마디 말도, 아무 소리도 없었다."], ["Mr Dombey’s watch and Dr Parker Peps’s watch seemed to be racing faster.", "돔비 씨의 시계와 파커 펩스 박사의 시계가 더 빨리 내달리는 듯했다."]], [["“Now, really, Fanny my dear,”", "\"자, 정말이지, 패니야,\""], ["said the sister-in-law, altering her position, and speaking less confidently, and more earnestly, in spite of herself, “I shall have to be quite cross with you, if you don’t rouse yourself.", "올케가 자세를 바꾸며, 자기도 모르게 덜 자신 있는, 그러나 더 간절한 어조로 말했다, \"네가 정신을 차리지 않으면 너한테 정말 화를 내야 할지도 몰라."], ["It’s necessary for you to make an effort, and perhaps a very great and painful effort which you are not disposed to make; but this is a world of effort you know, Fanny, and we must never yield, when so much depends upon us.", "너는 노력을 해야 해, 어쩌면 네가 내키지 않는 아주 크고 고통스러운 노력을 말이야. 하지만 이건 노력의 세상이잖니, 패니, 우리에게 이토록 많은 게 걸려 있을 때는 결코 굴해서는 안 돼."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Try!", "해 봐!"], ["I must really scold you if you don’t!”", "안 그러면 정말로 야단칠 거야!\""]], [["The race in the ensuing pause was fierce and furious.", "뒤이은 침묵 속의 경주는 사납고 맹렬했다."], ["The watches seemed to jostle, and to trip each other up.", "두 시계는 서로 밀치고 서로 발을 거는 듯했다."]], [["“Fanny!”", "\"패니!\""], ["said Louisa, glancing round, with a gathering alarm.", "루이자가 점점 커지는 불안으로 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“Only look at me.", "\"나만 봐."], ["Only open your eyes to show me that you hear and understand me; will you?", "내 말을 듣고 알아듣는다는 걸 보여 주게 눈만 떠 줘, 응?"], ["Good Heaven, gentlemen, what is to be done!”", "맙소사, 선생님들, 어떻게 해야 하나요!\""]], [["The two medical attendants exchanged a look across the bed; and the Physician, stooping down, whispered in the child’s ear.", "두 의사가 침대 너머로 눈빛을 주고받았다. 그리고 시의가 몸을 굽혀 아이의 귀에 속삭였다."], ["Not having understood the purport of his whisper, the little creature turned her perfectly colourless face and deep dark eyes towards him; but without loosening her hold in the least.", "그 속삭임의 뜻을 알아듣지 못한 채, 그 자그마한 것은 핏기라고는 전혀 없는 얼굴과 깊고 검은 눈을 그에게로 돌렸으나, 끌어안은 손은 조금도 늦추지 않았다."]], [["The whisper was repeated.", "속삭임이 되풀이되었다."]], [["“Mama!”", "\"엄마!\""], ["said the child.", "아이가 말했다."]], [["The little voice, familiar and dearly loved, awakened some show of consciousness, even at that ebb.", "그 자그마하고, 익숙하고, 더없이 사랑스러운 목소리가, 그토록 기력이 다한 와중에도 얼마간의 의식의 기미를 일깨웠다."], ["For a moment, the closed eye lids trembled, and the nostril quivered, and the faintest shadow of a smile was seen.", "잠시, 감긴 눈꺼풀이 떨렸고, 콧방울이 실룩였으며, 더없이 희미한 미소의 그림자가 어렸다."]], [["“Mama!”", "\"엄마!\""], ["cried the child sobbing aloud.", "아이가 소리 내어 흐느끼며 외쳤다."], ["“Oh dear Mama!", "\"오, 사랑하는 엄마!"], ["oh dear Mama!”", "오, 사랑하는 엄마!\""]], [["The Doctor gently brushed the scattered ringlets of the child, aside from the face and mouth of the mother.", "박사가 아이의 흩어진 곱슬머리를 어미의 얼굴과 입에서 부드럽게 쓸어 넘겼다. 아, 그것들은 거기 얼마나 고요히 누워 있었는지."], ["Alas how calm they lay there; how little breath there was to stir them!", "그것들을 흔들 숨결이 얼마나 가냘프게밖에 남아 있지 않았는지!"]], [["Thus, clinging fast to that slight spar within her arms, the mother drifted out upon the dark and unknown sea that rolls round all the world.", "이렇게, 제 두 팔 안에 놓인 그 가냘픈 부유물을 꼭 붙든 채, 어미는 온 세상을 굽이도는 그 어둡고 알 수 없는 바다 위로 떠밀려 나갔다."]], [["CHAPTER II. In which Timely Provision is made for an Emergency that will sometimes arise in the best-regulated Families.", "가장 잘 다스려진 가정에서도 때때로 일어나기 마련인 비상사태에 대비하여 시의적절한 대비가 이루어지는 장."]], [["I shall never cease to congratulate myself,”", "\"나는 평생 두고두고 스스로를 대견하게 여길 거예요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick,” on having said, when I little thought what was in store for us,—really as if I was inspired by something,—that I forgave poor dear Fanny everything.", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"앞으로 우리에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지 까맣게 모르던 그때—정말 무언가에 영감이라도 받은 사람처럼—가엾고 사랑스러운 패니에게 모든 걸 다 용서한다고 말해 둔 일을요."], ["Whatever happens, that must always be a comfort to me!”", "무슨 일이 벌어지든, 그건 언제까지나 내게 위안이 될 거예요!\""]], [["Mrs Chick made this impressive observation in the drawing-room, after having descended thither from the inspection of the mantua-makers upstairs, who were busy on the family mourning.", "칙 부인은 이 인상적인 소견을 응접실에서 토로했다. 위층에서 가족 상복을 짓느라 바쁜 양재사들을 둘러보고 내려온 뒤였다."], ["She delivered it for the behoof of Mr Chick, who was a stout bald gentleman, with a very large face, and his hands continually in his pockets, and who had a tendency in his nature to whistle and hum tunes, which, sensible of the indecorum of such sounds in a house of grief, he was at some pains to repress at present.", "그녀는 이 말을 칙 씨를 위해 들려준 것이었는데, 칙 씨는 살집 좋고 머리가 벗어진 신사로 얼굴이 매우 컸고 두 손은 늘 주머니에 찔러 넣고 있었으며, 천성적으로 휘파람을 불고 콧노래를 흥얼거리는 버릇이 있었으나, 슬픔의 집에서 그런 소리가 무례하다는 것을 의식하여 지금은 그것을 억누르느라 애를 좀 쓰고 있었다."]], [["“Don’t you over-exert yourself, Loo,”", "\"너무 무리하지 마시오, 루.\""], ["said Mr Chick, “or you’ll be laid up with spasms, I see.", "칙 씨가 말했다, \"안 그러면 경련으로 드러눕게 될 거요, 내 보아하니."], ["Right tol loor rul!", "라잇 톨 루르 룰!"], ["Bless my soul, I forgot!", "아이고 맙소사, 깜빡했군!"], ["We’re here one day and gone the next!”", "우리는 오늘 여기 있다가 내일이면 가 버리는 신세인데!\""]], [["Mrs Chick contented herself with a glance of reproof, and then proceeded with the thread of her discourse.", "칙 부인은 나무라는 눈길 한 번으로 만족하고는, 다시 이야기의 갈피를 이어 갔다."]], [["“I am sure,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["she said, “I hope this heart-rending occurrence will be a warning to all of us, to accustom ourselves to rouse ourselves, and to make efforts in time where they’re required of us.", "그녀가 말했다, \"이 가슴 미어지는 일이 우리 모두에게 경종이 되었으면 해요. 평소에 스스로를 분발시키는 데 익숙해지고, 우리에게 요구되는 노력을 제때 기울이도록 말이에요."], ["There’s a moral in everything, if we would only avail ourselves of it.", "모든 일에는 교훈이 담겨 있지요, 우리가 그것을 받아들이기만 한다면요."], ["It will be our own faults if we lose sight of this one.”", "이 교훈을 놓쳐 버린다면 그건 순전히 우리 잘못일 거예요.\""]], [["Mr Chick invaded the grave silence which ensued on this remark with the singularly inappropriate air of “A cobbler there was;”", "칙 씨는 이 말에 뒤따른 무거운 침묵을 \"구두장이가 하나 있었네\"라는, 도무지 어울리지 않는 가락으로 침범했다."], ["and checking himself, in some confusion, observed, that it was undoubtedly our own faults if we didn’t improve such melancholy occasions as the present.", "그러더니 좀 당황한 듯 스스로를 멈추고는, 지금 같은 이런 우울한 때를 우리가 유익하게 활용하지 못한다면 그건 분명 우리 잘못이라고 한마디 덧붙였다."]], [["“Which might be better improved, I should think, Mr C.,”", "\"제 생각엔, 칙 씨,\""], ["retorted his helpmate, after a short pause, “than by the introduction, either of the college hornpipe, or the equally unmeaning and unfeeling remark of rump-te-iddity, bow-wow-wow!”—which Mr Chick had indeed indulged in, under his breath, and which Mrs Chick repeated in a tone of withering scorn.", "그의 반려가 잠깐 멈췄다가 응수했다, \"대학 호른파이프 가락을 끌어들이거나, 그에 못지않게 뜻도 없고 정도 없는 럼-트-이디티, 바우-와우-와우 같은 소리를 늘어놓는 것보다는, 더 유익하게 활용할 방도가 있을 텐데요!\"—과연 칙 씨가 입속으로 그런 가락을 흥얼거린 터였고, 칙 부인은 그것을 사람을 시들게 할 만한 경멸의 어조로 되뇌었다."]], [["“Merely habit, my dear,”", "\"그저 버릇일 뿐이오, 여보.\""], ["pleaded Mr Chick.", "칙 씨가 변명했다."]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"무슨 소리예요!"], ["Habit!”", "버릇이라뇨!\""], ["returned his wife.", "그의 아내가 응수했다."], ["“If you’re a rational being, don’t make such ridiculous excuses. Habit!", "\"당신이 이성을 가진 존재라면, 그런 우스꽝스러운 변명은 하지 마세요."], ["If I was to get a habit (as you call it) of walking on the ceiling, like the flies, I should hear enough of it, I daresay.”", "버릇이라니! 만약 내가 파리처럼 천장을 걸어 다니는 (당신 말마따나) 버릇을 들이면, 아마 그 소리를 실컷 듣게 될 테죠.\""]], [["It appeared so probable that such a habit might be attended with some degree of notoriety, that Mr Chick didn’t venture to dispute the position.", "그런 버릇이라면 어느 정도 악명을 떨치게 될 법도 했으므로, 칙 씨는 감히 그 견해에 토를 달지 못했다."]], [["“Bow-wow-wow!”", "\"바우-와우-와우!\""], ["repeated Mrs Chick with an emphasis of blighting contempt on the last syllable.", "칙 부인이 마지막 음절에 사람을 옴짝달싹 못 하게 하는 경멸을 실어 되뇌었다."], ["“More like a professional singer with the hydrophobia, than a man in your station of life!”", "\"당신 신분의 사람이라기보다는, 공수병에 걸린 직업 가수에 더 가깝지 뭐예요!\""]], [["“How’s the Baby, Loo?”", "\"아기는 어떻소, 루?\""], ["asked Mr Chick: to change the subject.", "칙 씨가 화제를 돌리려고 물었다."]], [["“What Baby do you mean?”", "\"무슨 아기를 말하는 거예요?\""], ["answered Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 대답했다."]], [["“The poor bereaved little baby,”", "\"가엾게도 어미를 여읜 그 어린 아기 말이오,\""], ["said Mr Chick.", "칙 씨가 말했다."], ["“I don’t know of any other, my dear.”", "\"내가 아는 아기는 그 애밖에 없는데, 여보.\""]], [["“You don’t know of any other,”", "\"당신이 아는 아기가 그 애밖에 없다고요,\""], ["retorted Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 응수했다."], ["“More shame for you, I was going to say.”", "\"더 부끄러운 일이라고 말하려던 참이에요.\""]], [["Mr Chick looked astonished.", "칙 씨는 어리둥절한 표정을 지었다."]], [["“I am sure the morning I have had, with that dining-room downstairs, one mass of babies, no one in their senses would believe.”", "\"정말이지 오늘 아침에 내가 겪은 일은, 저 아래층 식당이 온통 아기들 천지가 되어서—제정신인 사람이라면 아무도 믿지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["“One mass of babies!”", "\"온통 아기들 천지라니!\""], ["repeated Mr Chick, staring with an alarmed expression about him.", "칙 씨가 놀란 표정으로 주위를 둘러보며 되뇌었다."]], [["“It would have occurred to most men,”", "\"웬만한 사람이라면 떠올렸을 거예요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “that poor dear Fanny being no more,—those words of mine will always be a balm and comfort to me,” here she dried her eyes; “it becomes necessary to provide a Nurse.”", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"가엾고 사랑스러운 패니가 이제 세상에 없으니—이 말은 언제까지나 내게 위안이고 위로가 될 거예요\"—여기서 그녀는 눈물을 닦았다—\"유모를 구하는 일이 꼭 필요해졌다는 걸요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Ah!”", "아!\""], ["said Mr Chick.", "칙 씨가 말했다."], ["“Toor-ru!—such is life, I mean.", "\"투르-루!—그게 인생이라는 거지, 내 말은."], ["I hope you are suited, my dear.”", "마땅한 사람을 구했길 바라오, 여보.\""]], [["“Indeed I am not,”", "\"천만에요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick; “nor likely to be, so far as I can see, and in the meantime the poor child seems likely to be starved to death.", "칙 부인이 말했다. \"구할 가망도 없어요, 내가 보기엔."], ["Paul is so very particular—naturally so, of course, having set his whole heart on this one boy—and there are so many objections to everybody that offers, that I don’t see, myself, the least chance of an arrangement.", "그러는 사이에 가엾은 아이는 굶어 죽을 판이고요. 폴은 워낙 까다로워서—하기야 당연하지요, 이 아들 하나에 온 마음을 쏟았으니—나서는 사람마다 트집거리가 어찌나 많은지, 내가 보기엔 일이 성사될 가망이 도무지 없어요."], ["Meanwhile, of course, the child is—”", "그러는 동안 아이는, 물론—\""]], [["“Going to the Devil,”", "\"악마한테로 가고 있겠지,\""], ["said Mr Chick, thoughtfully, “to be sure.”", "칙 씨가 생각에 잠겨 말했다, \"그렇고말고.\""]], [["Admonished, however, that he had committed himself, by the indignation expressed in Mrs Chick’s countenance at the idea of a Dombey going there; and thinking to atone for his misconduct by a bright suggestion, he added:", "그러나 돔비 집안 사람이 그런 데로 간다는 생각에 칙 부인의 얼굴에 떠오른 분노를 보고 자기가 실언했음을 깨달은 칙 씨는, 멋진 제안 하나로 자기 실수를 만회하려는 생각에 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Couldn’t something temporary be done with a teapot?”", "\"찻주전자로 임시변통 뭐라도 할 수 없을까?\""]], [["If he had meant to bring the subject prematurely to a close, he could not have done it more effectually.", "화제를 너무 일찍 끝내 버리려고 작정했다 한들, 이보다 더 효과적으로 그럴 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["After looking at him for some moments in silent resignation, Mrs Chick said she trusted he hadn’t said it in aggravation, because that would do very little honour to his heart.", "칙 부인은 말없이 체념한 채 그를 몇 초 동안 바라보고 나서, 부디 당신이 화를 돋우려고 그 말을 한 건 아니길 바란다고, 만약 그렇다면 당신 마음씨에 그리 명예로운 일이 못 될 테니까 하고 말했다."], ["She trusted he hadn’t said it seriously, because that would do very little honour to his head.", "또 진심으로 한 말이 아니길 바란다고, 만약 그렇다면 당신 머리에 그리 명예로운 일이 못 될 테니까 하고 덧붙였다."], ["As in any case, he couldn’t, however sanguine his disposition, hope to offer a remark that would be a greater outrage on human nature in general, we would beg to leave the discussion at that point.", "어느 쪽이든 간에, 당신이 아무리 낙천적인 기질이라 한들 인간 본성 전반에 대해 이보다 더한 모욕이 될 만한 말을 내놓기를 바랄 수는 없을 테니, 이쯤에서 논의를 그만두기로 했으면 좋겠다고 했다."]], [["Mrs Chick then walked majestically to the window and peeped through the blind, attracted by the sound of wheels.", "그러고 나서 칙 부인은 바퀴 소리에 이끌려 위풍당당하게 창가로 걸어가 블라인드 사이로 내다보았다."], ["Mr Chick, finding that his destiny was, for the time, against him, said no more, and walked off.", "칙 씨는 당분간 운명이 자기 편이 아님을 알고 더 말하지 않고 자리를 떴다."], ["But it was not always thus with Mr Chick.", "그러나 칙 씨가 늘 이런 식인 것은 아니었다."], ["He was often in the ascendant himself, and at those times punished Louisa roundly.", "그도 종종 기세를 잡는 때가 있었고, 그럴 때면 루이자를 호되게 혼내 주었다."], ["In their matrimonial bickerings they were, upon the whole, a well-matched, fairly-balanced, give-and-take couple.", "부부 싸움에서 두 사람은 대체로 잘 어울리는, 꽤 균형 잡힌, 주고받는 한 쌍이었다."], ["It would have been, generally speaking, very difficult to have betted on the winner.", "대개는 누가 이길지 점치기가 매우 어려웠다."], ["Often when Mr Chick seemed beaten, he would suddenly make a start, turn the tables, clatter them about the ears of Mrs Chick, and carry all before him.", "칙 씨가 패한 듯 보일 때면 종종 그는 별안간 분발하여 형세를 뒤집고, 그 형세를 칙 부인의 귀에 대고 요란하게 휘둘러서 단번에 판을 휩쓸곤 했다."], ["Being liable himself to similar unlooked for checks from Mrs Chick, their little contests usually possessed a character of uncertainty that was very animating.", "칙 씨 자신도 칙 부인에게서 그와 비슷한 뜻밖의 제동을 당하기 일쑤였으므로, 두 사람의 자그마한 다툼은 대개 매우 생기 넘치는 불확실성의 성격을 띠었다."]], [["Miss Tox had arrived on the wheels just now alluded to, and came running into the room in a breathless condition.", "방금 언급한 그 바퀴를 타고 막 도착한 톡스 양이 숨을 헐떡이며 방 안으로 뛰어 들어왔다."]], [["“My dear Louisa,”", "\"사랑하는 루이자,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “is the vacancy still unsupplied?”", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"그 빈자리는 아직 못 채웠나요?\""]], [["“You good soul, yes,”", "\"착한 분, 그래요, 아직이에요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Then, my dear Louisa,”", "\"그렇다면, 사랑하는 루이자,\""], ["returned Miss Tox, “I hope and believe—but in one moment, my dear, I’ll introduce the party.”", "톡스 양이 응수했다, \"내 바라고 믿건대—아니, 잠깐만요, 당장 그 사람을 소개할게요.\""]], [["Running downstairs again as fast as she had run up, Miss Tox got the party out of the hackney-coach, and soon returned with it under convoy.", "올라올 때만큼이나 빠르게 다시 아래층으로 뛰어 내려간 톡스 양은, 그 사람을 삯마차에서 내리게 하고는, 곧 그 일행을 거느리고 돌아왔다."]], [["It then appeared that she had used the word, not in its legal or business acceptation, when it merely expresses an individual, but as a noun of multitude, or signifying many: for Miss Tox escorted a plump rosy-cheeked wholesome apple-faced young woman, with an infant in her arms; a younger woman not so plump, but apple-faced also, who led a plump and apple-faced child in each hand; another plump and also apple-faced boy who walked by himself; and finally, a plump and apple-faced man, who carried in his arms another plump and apple-faced boy, whom he stood down on the floor, and admonished, in a husky whisper, to “kitch hold of his brother Johnny.”", "그제야 드러난 사실인즉, 톡스 양은 그 단어를 법률이나 사무에서 쓰는 의미, 곧 한 개인을 뜻하는 뜻으로 쓴 게 아니라, 집합 명사, 곧 여럿을 가리키는 뜻으로 쓴 것이었다. 톡스 양이 거느리고 온 것은, 통통하고 볼이 발그레하며 건강하고 사과 같은 얼굴의 젊은 여자로 품에 갓난아이를 안고 있었고, 그만큼 통통하지는 않으나 역시 사과 같은 얼굴의 더 젊은 여자가 양손에 통통하고 사과 같은 얼굴의 아이를 하나씩 잡고 있었으며, 또 다른 통통하고 사과 같은 얼굴의 사내아이가 혼자 걸어왔고, 마지막으로 통통하고 사과 같은 얼굴의 사내가 또 다른 통통하고 사과 같은 얼굴의 사내아이를 품에 안고 와서 바닥에 내려놓고는, 쉰 목소리로 속삭이며 \"형 조니를 꼭 붙잡고 있으라\"고 타일렀다."]], [["“My dear Louisa,”", "\"사랑하는 루이자,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “knowing your great anxiety, and wishing to relieve it, I posted off myself to the Queen Charlotte’s Royal Married Females,” which you had forgot, and put the question, Was there anybody there that they thought would suit?", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"당신이 크게 걱정하고 있는 걸 알고, 그 걱정을 덜어 주고 싶어서, 내가 직접 퀸 샬럿 왕립 기혼 부인회로 달려갔어요—당신이 깜빡한 그곳 말이에요—그리고 물었지요, 거기에 적합하다고 생각되는 사람이 있느냐고요."], ["No, they said there was not.", "없다고들 하더군요."], ["When they gave me that answer, I do assure you, my dear, I was almost driven to despair on your account.", "그 대답을 들었을 때, 정말이지 여보, 당신을 생각하면 거의 절망에 빠질 지경이었어요."], ["But it did so happen, that one of the Royal Married Females, hearing the inquiry, reminded the matron of another who had gone to her own home, and who, she said, would in all likelihood be most satisfactory.", "그런데 마침 그 왕립 기혼 부인 하나가 그 문의를 듣고는, 자기 집으로 돌아간 또 다른 사람을 사감에게 일러 주면서, 그 사람이라면 십중팔구 가장 만족스러울 거라고 하지 뭐예요."], ["The moment I heard this, and had it corroborated by the matron—excellent references and unimpeachable character—I got the address, my dear, and posted off again.”", "그 말을 듣자마자, 그리고 사감에게서—훌륭한 추천에다 흠잡을 데 없는 인품이라는—확인을 받자마자, 나는 주소를 얻어서, 여보, 다시 달려간 거예요.\""]], [["“Like the dear good Tox, you are!”", "\"역시 착하고 다정한 톡스 양이라니까요!\""], ["said Louisa.", "루이자가 말했다."]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"천만에요,\""], ["returned Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 응수했다."], ["“Don’t say so.", "\"그런 말 마세요."], ["Arriving at the house (the cleanest place, my dear!", "그 집에 도착해 보니(어찌나 깨끗한지, 여보!"], ["You might eat your dinner off the floor), I found the whole family sitting at table; and feeling that no account of them could be half so comfortable to you and Mr Dombey as the sight of them all together, I brought them all away.", "바닥에 음식을 놓고 먹어도 될 정도였어요), 온 가족이 식탁에 둘러앉아 있더군요. 그래서 이 사람들에 대한 어떤 설명도 다 함께 모인 모습을 직접 보는 것의 절반만큼도 당신과 돔비 씨께 든든하지 못하리라 싶어서, 다 같이 데려온 거예요."], ["This gentleman,”", "이 사람이,\""], ["said Miss Tox, pointing out the apple-faced man, “is the father.", "톡스 양이 사과 같은 얼굴의 사내를 가리키며 말했다, \"아버지예요."], ["Will you have the goodness to come a little forward, Sir?”", "좀 앞으로 나와 주시겠어요, 선생?\""]], [["The apple-faced man having sheepishly complied with this request, stood chuckling and grinning in a front row.", "사과 같은 얼굴의 사내는 멋쩍게 그 청에 응하여, 킬킬 웃으며 앞줄에 서서 이를 드러내고 씩 웃었다."]], [["“This is his wife, of course,”", "\"이쪽은 물론 그의 아내고요,\""], ["said Miss Tox, singling out the young woman with the baby.", "톡스 양이 갓난아이를 안은 젊은 여자를 가리키며 말했다."], ["“How do you do, Polly?”", "\"잘 지냈어요, 폴리?\""]], [["“I’m pretty well, I thank you, Ma’am,”", "\"덕분에 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다, 마님,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["By way of bringing her out dexterously, Miss Tox had made the inquiry as in condescension to an old acquaintance whom she hadn’t seen for a fortnight or so.", "폴리를 자연스럽게 끌어내려고, 톡스 양은 마치 한 보름쯤 못 본 오랜 지인을 대하듯 너그럽게 물은 것이었다."]], [["“I’m glad to hear it,”", "\"그렇다니 다행이에요,\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“The other young woman is her unmarried sister who lives with them, and would take care of her children.", "\"저쪽 젊은 여자는 그 사람의 미혼인 여동생인데, 함께 살면서 아이들을 돌봐 줄 거예요."], ["Her name’s Jemima.", "이름은 제미마예요."], ["How do you do, Jemima?”", "잘 지냈어요, 제미마?\""]], [["“I’m pretty well, I thank you, Ma’am,”", "\"덕분에 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다, 마님,\""], ["returned Jemima.", "제미마가 응수했다."]], [["“I’m very glad indeed to hear it,”", "\"정말이지 그렇다니 무척 다행이에요,\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“I hope you’ll keep so. Five children.", "\"계속 그렇게 지내길 바라요."], ["Youngest six weeks.", "아이가 다섯이에요."], ["The fine little boy with the blister on his nose is the eldest.", "막내가 생후 육 주 됐고요. 코에 물집이 잡힌 저 예쁜 사내아이가 맏이예요."], ["The blister, I believe,”", "저 물집은 내 생각에,\""], ["said Miss Tox, looking round upon the family, “is not constitutional, but accidental?”", "톡스 양이 가족들을 둘러보며 말했다, \"체질적인 게 아니라 사고로 생긴 거지요?\""]], [["The apple-faced man was understood to growl, “Flat iron.”", "사과 같은 얼굴의 사내가 \"다리미요\" 하고 으르렁거린 것으로 짐작되었다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Sir,”", "\"미안하지만, 선생,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “did you—”", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"방금 뭐라고—\""]], [["“Flat iron,”", "\"다리미요,\""], ["he repeated.", "그가 되풀이했다."]], [["“Oh yes,”", "\"오, 그렇군요,\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“Yes!", "\"그래요!"], ["quite true.", "정말 그렇겠네요."], ["I forgot.", "깜빡했어요."], ["The little creature, in his mother’s absence, smelt a warm flat iron.", "그 어린것이 엄마가 없는 사이에 뜨거운 다리미 냄새를 맡은 거군요."], ["You’re quite right, Sir.", "맞는 말씀이에요, 선생."], ["You were going to have the goodness to inform me, when we arrived at the door that you were by trade a—”", "우리가 문 앞에 도착했을 때, 친절하게도 당신이 직업이 뭐라고 일러 주려던 참이었지요—\""]], [["“Stoker,”", "\"화부입니다,\""], ["said the man.", "사내가 말했다."]], [["“A choker!”", "\"교살자라니!\""], ["said Miss Tox, quite aghast.", "톡스 양이 화들짝 놀라 말했다."]], [["“Stoker,”", "\"화부요,\""], ["said the man.", "사내가 말했다."], ["“Steam ingine.”", "\"증기 기관 말입니다.\""]], [["“Oh-h!", "\"오-호!"], ["Yes!”", "그래요!\""], ["returned Miss Tox, looking thoughtfully at him, and seeming still to have but a very imperfect understanding of his meaning.", "톡스 양이 그를 생각에 잠겨 바라보며, 여전히 그 뜻을 매우 어렴풋이밖에 이해하지 못한 듯 응수했다. \"그래, 그 일이 마음에 드세요, 선생?\""]], [["“And how do you like it, Sir?”", "\"뭐가 말입니까, 마님?\" 사내가 말했다."]], [["“Which, Mum?”", "\"그거 말이에요,\""], ["said the man. “That,”", "톡스 양이 대답했다."], ["replied Miss Tox. “Your trade.”", "\"당신 직업 말이에요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Pretty well, Mum.", "그럭저럭 괜찮습니다, 마님."], ["The ashes sometimes gets in here;”", "재가 가끔 이 안으로 들어와서요.\""], ["touching his chest: “and makes a man speak gruff, as at the present time.", "그가 가슴을 가리켰다. \"그래서 지금처럼 사람 목소리가 거칠어집니다."], ["But it is ashes, Mum, not crustiness.”", "하지만 그건 재 때문이지, 성미가 거칠어서가 아닙니다, 마님.\""]], [["Miss Tox seemed to be so little enlightened by this reply, as to find a difficulty in pursuing the subject.", "톡스 양은 이 대답으로도 깨달은 바가 너무 적어서 그 화제를 이어 가기가 어려운 듯했다."], ["But Mrs Chick relieved her, by entering into a close private examination of Polly, her children, her marriage certificate, testimonials, and so forth.", "그러나 칙 부인이 폴리와 그 아이들, 혼인 증명서, 추천서 등을 꼼꼼히 사적으로 검토하기 시작하면서 그녀를 구해 주었다."], ["Polly coming out unscathed from this ordeal, Mrs Chick withdrew with her report to her brother’s room, and as an emphatic comment on it, and corroboration of it, carried the two rosiest little Toodles with her.", "폴리가 이 시련에서 흠 없이 통과하자, 칙 부인은 그 결과 보고를 들고 자기 오라비의 방으로 물러갔는데, 그것에 대한 강조 어린 논평이자 뒷받침으로, 가장 발그레한 투들 가의 어린것 둘을 데리고 갔다."], ["Toodle being the family name of the apple-faced family.", "투들은 그 사과 같은 얼굴의 가족의 성이었다."]], [["Mr Dombey had remained in his own apartment since the death of his wife, absorbed in visions of the youth, education, and destination of his baby son.", "돔비 씨는 아내가 죽은 뒤로 자기 방에 틀어박혀, 어린 아들의 소년기와 교육과 진로에 대한 환상에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Something lay at the bottom of his cool heart, colder and heavier than its ordinary load; but it was more a sense of the child’s loss than his own, awakening within him an almost angry sorrow.", "그의 차가운 마음 밑바닥에는, 평소의 짐보다 더 차갑고 무거운 무언가가 가라앉아 있었다. 그러나 그것은 자기 자신의 상실감이라기보다 아이가 잃은 것에 대한 감각이었고, 그것이 그의 안에서 거의 분노에 가까운 슬픔을 불러일으켰다."], ["That the life and progress on which he built such hopes, should be endangered in the outset by so mean a want; that Dombey and Son should be tottering for a nurse, was a sore humiliation.", "자기가 그토록 큰 희망을 걸어 둔 생명과 그 앞날이, 출발부터 이토록 하찮은 결핍 때문에 위태로워진다는 것, 돔비와 아들이 유모 하나를 구하지 못해 비틀거린다는 것은 쓰라린 굴욕이었다."], ["And yet in his pride and jealousy, he viewed with so much bitterness the thought of being dependent for the very first step towards the accomplishment of his soul’s desire, on a hired serving-woman who would be to the child, for the time, all that even his alliance could have made his own wife, that in every new rejection of a candidate he felt a secret pleasure.", "그러면서도 그는 자존심과 질투심에 사로잡혀, 자기 영혼의 소망을 이루는 그 첫걸음을 떼는 데에서부터 고용된 하녀에게 의존해야 한다는 생각을—그 여자가 당분간 아이에게, 자기와의 결혼으로 자기 아내가 될 수 있었던 그 모든 것이 되어 줄 거라는 생각을—어찌나 씁쓸하게 바라보았던지, 후보자가 하나씩 거절될 때마다 은근한 쾌감을 느낄 정도였다."], ["The time had now come, however, when he could no longer be divided between these two sets of feelings.", "그러나 이제는 더 이상 이 두 갈래 감정 사이에서 갈팡질팡할 수 없는 때가 왔다."], ["The less so, as there seemed to be no flaw in the title of Polly Toodle after his sister had set it forth, with many commendations on the indefatigable friendship of Miss Tox.", "더욱이 그의 누이가 톡스 양의 지칠 줄 모르는 우정을 거듭 칭찬하며 폴리 투들의 자격을 늘어놓은 뒤로, 그 자격에는 아무런 흠도 없어 보였으니 더욱 그러했다."]], [["“These children look healthy,”", "\"이 아이들은 건강해 보이는군,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“But my God, to think of their some day claiming a sort of relationship to Paul!”", "\"그러나 맙소사, 이 아이들이 언젠가 폴과 일종의 친척 관계를 주장하게 될 거라고 생각하면!\""]], [["“But what relationship is there!”", "\"하지만 무슨 친척 관계가 있겠어요!\""], ["Louisa began—", "루이자가 입을 떼었다—"]], [["“Is there!”", "\"있겠느냐고!\""], ["echoed Mr Dombey, who had not intended his sister to participate in the thought he had unconsciously expressed.", "돔비 씨가 따라 말했다. 그는 자기도 모르게 입 밖에 낸 그 생각에 누이가 끼어들 줄은 생각지 못한 터였다."], ["“Is there, did you say, Louisa!”", "\"있겠느냐고 했나, 루이자?\""]], [["“Can there be, I mean—”", "\"내 말은, 있을 수 있겠—\""]], [["“Why none,”", "\"아무 관계도 없지,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, sternly.", "돔비 씨가 매섭게 말했다."], ["“The whole world knows that, I presume.", "\"온 세상이 그걸 알 거다, 짐작건대."], ["Grief has not made me idiotic, Louisa.", "슬픔이 나를 백치로 만든 건 아니야, 루이자."], ["Take them away, Louisa!", "이 아이들을 데리고 나가, 루이자!"], ["Let me see this woman and her husband.”", "이 여자와 그 남편을 만나 봐야겠다.\""]], [["Mrs Chick bore off the tender pair of Toodles, and presently returned with that tougher couple whose presence her brother had commanded.", "칙 부인은 그 어린 투들 한 쌍을 데리고 나갔다가, 이내 오라비가 들라고 한 그 더 억센 부부를 데리고 돌아왔다."]], [["“My good woman,”", "\"이보게, 착한 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, turning round in his easy chair, as one piece, and not as a man with limbs and joints, “I understand you are poor, and wish to earn money by nursing the little boy, my son, who has been so prematurely deprived of what can never be replaced.", "돔비 씨가 안락의자에서 팔다리와 관절을 가진 사람이 아니라 한 덩어리처럼 통째로 몸을 돌리며 말했다, \"듣자 하니 자네는 가난하여, 내 아들인 이 어린 사내아이를 젖을 먹여 키워서 돈을 벌고 싶다고 하더군. 이 아이는 무엇으로도 결코 대신할 수 없는 것을 너무 일찍 잃었네."], ["I have no objection to your adding to the comforts of your family by that means.", "자네가 그렇게 해서 자네 가족의 생계에 보탬을 하겠다는 데에 나는 이의가 없네."], ["So far as I can tell, you seem to be a deserving object.", "내가 보기엔 자네는 그럴 자격이 있는 사람 같군."], ["But I must impose one or two conditions on you, before you enter my house in that capacity.", "그러나 자네가 그 자격으로 내 집에 들어오기 전에, 한두 가지 조건을 두어야겠네."], ["While you are here, I must stipulate that you are always known as—say as Richards—an ordinary name, and convenient.", "자네가 여기 있는 동안에는, 자네가 늘—그러니까 리처즈라는 이름으로 불려야 한다는 점을 못 박아 두겠네. 흔하고 편리한 이름이지."], ["Have you any objection to be known as Richards?", "리처즈라고 불리는 데에 이의가 있나?"], ["You had better consult your husband.”", "남편과 상의해 보는 게 낫겠군.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"어떤가?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, after a pretty long pause.", "돔비 씨가 꽤 오랜 침묵 끝에 말했다."], ["“What does your husband say to your being called Richards?”", "\"자네가 리처즈라고 불리는 것에 대해 남편은 뭐라고 하나?\""]], [["As the husband did nothing but chuckle and grin, and continually draw his right hand across his mouth, moistening the palm, Mrs Toodle, after nudging him twice or thrice in vain, dropped a curtsey and replied “that perhaps if she was to be called out of her name, it would be considered in the wages.”", "남편이라는 자는 그저 킬킬 웃으며 이를 드러내고 씩 웃을 뿐, 줄곧 오른손으로 입을 쓱쓱 문지르며 손바닥을 적시기만 했으므로, 투들 부인은 그를 두세 번 헛되이 쿡쿡 찔러 본 뒤에, 무릎을 살짝 굽혀 인사하고는 \"혹시 제 이름이 아닌 다른 이름으로 불린다면, 그것이 품삯에 반영될지\" 하고 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, of course,”", "\"오, 물론이지,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“I desire to make it a question of wages, altogether.", "\"이건 전적으로 품삯의 문제로 삼고 싶네."], ["Now, Richards, if you nurse my bereaved child, I wish you to remember this always.", "자, 리처즈, 자네가 어미 잃은 내 아이에게 젖을 먹인다면, 늘 이 점을 명심해 주게."], ["You will receive a liberal stipend in return for the discharge of certain duties, in the performance of which, I wish you to see as little of your family as possible.", "자네는 일정한 의무를 다하는 대가로 넉넉한 급료를 받게 될 텐데, 그 의무를 수행하는 동안 자네 가족과는 되도록 적게 만나기를 바라네."], ["When those duties cease to be required and rendered, and the stipend ceases to be paid, there is an end of all relations between us.", "그 의무가 더 이상 요구되지도 행해지지도 않고, 급료가 더 이상 지급되지 않게 되면, 우리 사이의 모든 관계는 끝나는 거네."], ["Do you understand me?”", "내 말 알아듣겠나?\""]], [["Mrs Toodle seemed doubtful about it; and as to Toodle himself, he had evidently no doubt whatever, that he was all abroad.", "투들 부인은 그것을 미심쩍어하는 듯했다. 투들 본인으로 말하자면, 자기가 완전히 갈피를 못 잡고 있다는 데에는 분명 한 점의 의심도 없어 보였다."]], [["“You have children of your own,”", "\"자네는 자네 자식이 있지,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“It is not at all in this bargain that you need become attached to my child, or that my child need become attached to you.", "\"이 계약에는, 자네가 내 아이에게 정을 붙인다거나 내 아이가 자네에게 정을 붙인다거나 하는 일은 전혀 들어 있지 않네."], ["I don’t expect or desire anything of the kind.", "나는 그런 종류의 것은 기대하지도 바라지도 않네."], ["Quite the reverse.", "오히려 그 반대야."], ["When you go away from here, you will have concluded what is a mere matter of bargain and sale, hiring and letting: and will stay away.", "자네가 여기서 떠날 때면, 자네는 그저 사고팔고, 빌리고 빌려주는 거래를 마친 것일 뿐이니, 그대로 떠나 있게."], ["The child will cease to remember you; and you will cease, if you please, to remember the child.”", "아이는 자네를 더 이상 기억하지 않게 될 거고, 자네도, 괜찮다면, 아이를 더 이상 기억하지 않게 될 걸세.\""]], [["Mrs Toodle, with a little more colour in her cheeks than she had had before, said “she hoped she knew her place.”", "투들 부인은 전보다 볼에 약간 더 핏기가 돈 채로, \"제 분수는 안다고 생각합니다\" 하고 말했다."]], [["“I hope you do, Richards,”", "\"안다니 다행이군, 리처즈,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“I have no doubt you know it very well.", "\"자네가 그걸 아주 잘 알고 있으리라 의심치 않네."], ["Indeed it is so plain and obvious that it could hardly be otherwise.", "사실 그건 너무도 명백하고 뻔한 일이라, 달리 될 수가 없겠지."], ["Louisa, my dear, arrange with Richards about money, and let her have it when and how she pleases.", "루이자, 얘야, 리처즈와 돈에 관해 매듭짓고, 본인이 원하는 때에 원하는 방식으로 받게 해 주거라."], ["Mr what’s-your name, a word with you, if you please!”", "이름이 뭐라고 했나—자네, 잠깐 할 말이 있네!\""]], [["Thus arrested on the threshold as he was following his wife out of the room, Toodle returned and confronted Mr Dombey alone.", "아내를 따라 방을 나서려던 차에 문턱에서 이렇게 멈춰 세워진 투들은, 되돌아와 홀로 돔비 씨와 마주 섰다."], ["He was a strong, loose, round-shouldered, shuffling, shaggy fellow, on whom his clothes sat negligently: with a good deal of hair and whisker, deepened in its natural tint, perhaps by smoke and coal-dust: hard knotty hands: and a square forehead, as coarse in grain as the bark of an oak.", "그는 건장하고 헐렁하며 어깨가 둥글고 발을 끌며 걷는 텁수룩한 사내로, 옷이 몸에 아무렇게나 걸쳐져 있었다. 머리와 구레나룻이 무성했는데, 그 본래 색조는 아마 매연과 석탄 먼지로 더 짙어진 듯했다."], ["A thorough contrast in all respects, to Mr Dombey, who was one of those close-shaved close-cut moneyed gentlemen who are glossy and crisp like new bank-notes, and who seem to be artificially braced and tightened as by the stimulating action of golden showerbaths.", "손은 단단하고 옹이졌으며, 이마는 떡갈나무 껍질만큼이나 결이 거칠고 네모졌다. 모든 면에서 돔비 씨와는 완전한 대조였으니, 돔비 씨는 새 지폐처럼 반들반들하고 빳빳하며, 황금 샤워의 자극적인 작용으로 인위적으로 조여지고 팽팽해진 듯 보이는, 말끔히 면도하고 단정하게 차려입은 돈 많은 신사 가운데 하나였다."]], [["“You have a son, I believe?”", "\"자네에겐 아들이 있다지?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Four on ’em, Sir.", "\"넷입니다, 나리."], ["Four hims and a her.", "사내가 넷에 계집이 하나죠."], ["All alive!”", "모두 살아 있고요!\""]], [["“Why, it’s as much as you can afford to keep them!”", "\"이런, 그 아이들을 먹여 살리는 게 자네 힘에 부치겠군!\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I couldn’t hardly afford but one thing in the world less, Sir.”", "\"세상에서 딱 한 가지 일만 빼면, 그보다 더 힘에 부칠 일이 없겠습죠, 나리.\""]], [["“What is that?”", "\"그게 뭔가?\""]], [["“To lose ’em, Sir.”", "\"그 애들을 잃는 일입죠, 나리.\""]], [["“Can you read?”", "\"글은 읽을 줄 아나?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Why, not partickk’ller, Sir.”", "\"뭐, 그리 잘은 못 합니다, 나리.\""]], [["“Write?”", "\"쓸 줄은?\""]], [["“With chalk, Sir?”", "\"분필로 말입니까요, 나리?\""]], [["“With anything?”", "\"무엇으로든?\""]], [["“I could make shift to chalk a little bit, I think, if I was put to it,”", "\"어쩔 수 없이 시켜야 한다면, 분필로 조금쯤은 끄적일 수 있을 것 같습니다요,\""], ["said Toodle after some reflection.", "투들이 잠시 생각하고서 말했다."]], [["“And yet,”", "\"그런데도,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “you are two or three and thirty, I suppose?”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"자네 나이가 서른두셋쯤 됐을 텐데?\""]], [["“Thereabouts, I suppose, Sir,”", "\"그쯤 됐을 겁니다요, 나리,\""], ["answered Toodle, after more reflection", "투들이 좀 더 생각하고서 대답했다."]], [["“Then why don’t you learn?”", "\"그렇다면 왜 배우지 않나?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 물었다."]], [["“So I’m a going to, Sir.", "\"이제 배우려고요, 나리."], ["One of my little boys is a going to learn me, when he’s old enough, and been to school himself.”", "제 어린 아들놈 하나가 충분히 자라서 제 스스로 학교를 다니게 되면, 저를 가르쳐 줄 겁니다요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, after looking at him attentively, and with no great favour, as he stood gazing round the room (principally round the ceiling) and still drawing his hand across and across his mouth.", "돔비 씨가 그를 유심히, 그다지 호의 없이 바라보며 말했다. 그동안 투들은 방 안을(주로 천장을) 둘러보며 여전히 손으로 입을 쓱쓱 문지르고 있었다."], ["“You heard what I said to your wife just now?”", "\"방금 내가 자네 아내에게 한 말을 들었나?\""]], [["“Polly heerd it,”", "\"폴리가 들었습죠,\""], ["said Toodle, jerking his hat over his shoulder in the direction of the door, with an air of perfect confidence in his better half.", "투들이 자기 더 나은 반쪽에 대한 완전한 신뢰를 담아, 모자를 어깨 너머 문 쪽으로 휙 젖히며 말했다."], ["“It’s all right.”", "\"다 괜찮습니다요.\""]], [["“But I ask you if you heard it.", "\"하지만 내가 묻는 건 자네가 들었느냐는 거네."], ["You did, I suppose, and understood it?”", "들었겠지, 짐작건대, 그리고 알아들었겠지?\""], ["pursued Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 캐물었다."]], [["“I heerd it,”", "\"듣긴 들었습죠,\""], ["said Toodle, “but I don’t know as I understood it rightly Sir, “account of being no scholar, and the words being—ask your pardon—rayther high.", "투들이 말했다, \"하지만 제대로 알아들었는지는 모르겠습니다요, 나리, 제가 배운 게 없는 데다 말씀이—실례지만—좀 어려워서요."], ["But Polly heerd it.", "하지만 폴리가 들었습죠."], ["It’s all right.”", "다 괜찮습니다요.\""]], [["“As you appear to leave everything to her,”", "\"자네가 만사를 아내에게 맡기는 듯하니,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, frustrated in his intention of impressing his views still more distinctly on the husband, as the stronger character, “I suppose it is of no use my saying anything to you.”", "돔비 씨가, 더 강한 성격인 남편에게 자기 견해를 한층 더 또렷이 새겨 두려던 의도가 좌절되어 말했다, \"자네에게 무슨 말을 해 봤자 소용없겠군.\""]], [["“Not a bit,”", "\"전혀 소용없습죠,\""], ["said Toodle.", "투들이 말했다."], ["“Polly heerd it.", "\"폴리가 들었습죠."], ["She’s awake, Sir.”", "그 사람은 빈틈없으니까요, 나리.\""]], [["“I won’t detain you any longer then,”", "\"그렇다면 더 붙잡지 않겠네,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, disappointed.", "돔비 씨가 실망하여 응수했다."], ["“Where have you worked all your life?”", "\"자네는 평생 어디서 일했나?\""]], [["“Mostly underground, Sir, “till I got married.", "\"결혼하기 전까진 대체로 땅속에서요, 나리."], ["I come to the level then.", "그러고 나서 지상으로 올라왔습죠."], ["I’m a going on one of these here railroads when they comes into full play.”", "이 철도라는 게 본격적으로 돌아가게 되면, 그중 하나에 들어갈 작정입니다요.\""]], [["As he added in one of his hoarse whispers, “We means to bring up little Biler to that line,”", "그러고는 쉰 목소리의 속삭임으로 \"우리는 어린 바일러를 그 길로 키울 작정입니다요\""], ["Mr Dombey inquired haughtily who little Biler was.", "하고 덧붙이자, 돔비 씨는 거만하게 어린 바일러가 누구냐고 물었다."]], [["“The eldest on ’em, Sir,”", "\"맏이입니다요, 나리,\""], ["said Toodle, with a smile.", "투들이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“It ain’t a common name.", "\"흔한 이름은 아니지요."], ["Sermuchser that when he was took to church the gen’lm’n said, it wamm’t a chris’en one, and he couldn’t give it.", "어찌나 그런지, 그 애를 교회에 데려갔을 때 목사님이 그건 세례명이 못 되니 줄 수 없다고 하시더군요."], ["But we always calls him Biler just the same.", "하지만 우리는 그래도 변함없이 바일러라고 부릅니다요."], ["For we don’t mean no harm.", "나쁜 뜻은 없으니까요."], ["Not we.”", "우리는요.\""]], [["“Do you mean to say, Man,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["inquired Mr Dombey; looking at him with marked displeasure, “that you have called a child after a boiler?”", "돔비 씨가 그를 뚜렷한 불쾌감을 담아 바라보며 물었다, \"자네 말은, 아이 이름을 보일러(보일러)를 따서 지었다는 건가?\""]], [["“No, no, Sir,”", "\"아뇨, 아뇨, 나리,\""], ["returned Toodle, with a tender consideration for his mistake.", "투들이 자기 오해를 너그럽게 헤아리며 응수했다. \"그럴 리가요!"], ["“I should hope not!", "아닙니다, 나리."], ["No, Sir. Arter a BILER Sir.", "바-일러를 따서요, 나리."], ["The Steamingine was a’most as good as a godfather to him, and so we called him Biler, don’t you see!”", "그 증기 기관이 그 애한테는 거의 대부나 다름없어서, 그래서 바일러라고 부른 겁니다요, 아시겠어요!\""]], [["As the last straw breaks the laden camel’s back, this piece of information crushed the sinking spirits of Mr Dombey.", "마지막 지푸라기 한 올이 짐 실은 낙타의 등을 부러뜨리듯, 이 한마디 정보는 가라앉아 가던 돔비 씨의 기력을 짓이겨 버렸다."], ["He motioned his child’s foster-father to the door, who departed by no means unwillingly: and then turning the key, paced up and down the room in solitary wretchedness.", "그는 아이의 양부에게 문 쪽으로 가라는 손짓을 했고, 그자는 조금도 마지못한 기색 없이 떠났다. 그러고 나서 돔비 씨는 열쇠를 돌려 문을 잠그고, 홀로 비참한 심정으로 방 안을 오락가락 거닐었다."]], [["It would be harsh, and perhaps not altogether true, to say of him that he felt these rubs and gratings against his pride more keenly than he had felt his wife’s death: but certainly they impressed that event upon him with new force, and communicated to it added weight and bitterness. It was a rude shock to his sense of property in his child, that these people—the mere dust of the earth, as he thought them—should be necessary to him; and it was natural that in proportion as he felt disturbed by it, he should deplore the occurrence which had made them so. For all his starched, impenetrable dignity and composure, he wiped blinding tears from his eyes as he paced up and down his room; and often said, with an emotion of which he would not, for the world, have had a witness, “Poor little fellow!”", "그가 이런 마찰과 자존심의 긁힘을, 아내의 죽음을 느낀 것보다 더 절절히 느꼈다고 말한다면, 그것은 가혹할 뿐 아니라 어쩌면 전적으로 옳지도 않을 것이다. 그러나 그것들이 그 죽음이라는 사건을 그에게 새로운 힘으로 각인시키고, 거기에 한층 더한 무게와 쓰라림을 더해 준 것만은 분명했다. 자기 아이에 대한 소유 감각에 그것은 거친 충격이었다—그가 보기에 한낱 땅의 티끌 같은 이 사람들이 자기에게 꼭 필요한 존재라는 것이. 그러니 그것에 마음이 흔들리는 만큼, 그런 필요를 만들어 낸 그 사건을 한탄하는 것도 자연스러운 일이었다. 그 빳빳하고 꿰뚫을 수 없는 위엄과 침착함에도 불구하고, 그는 방 안을 오락가락 거니는 동안 앞을 가리는 눈물을 닦았다. 그리고 세상 무엇과도 바꾸지 않을 만큼 결코 누구에게도 보이고 싶지 않은 격정에 사로잡혀, 자주 \"가엾은 어린것!\" 하고 중얼거렸다."]], [["It may have been characteristic of Mr Dombey’s pride, that he pitied himself through the child. Not poor me.", "그가 아이를 통해 자기 자신을 가엾이 여겼다는 것은, 어쩌면 돔비 씨의 자존심을 잘 보여 주는 일이었을 것이다."], ["Not poor widower, confiding by constraint in the wife of an ignorant Hind who has been working “mostly underground”", "가엾은 나, 가 아니었다. 평생 \"대체로 땅속에서\""], ["all his life, and yet at whose door Death had never knocked, and at whose poor table four sons daily sit—but poor little fellow!", "일해 왔지만 죽음이 그 집 문을 한 번도 두드린 적 없고, 그 초라한 식탁에 매일 네 아들이 둘러앉는, 무지한 막일꾼의 아내에게 어쩔 수 없이 의지하게 된 가엾은 홀아비, 가 아니었다—바로 가엾은 어린것이었다."]], [["Those words being on his lips, it occurred to him—and it is an instance of the strong attraction with which his hopes and fears and all his thoughts were tending to one centre—that a great temptation was being placed in this woman’s way.", "그 말이 입가에 맴돌던 차에, 문득 그에게 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다—그것은 그의 희망과 두려움과 온갖 생각이 하나의 중심으로 쏠려 가던 강한 이끌림을 보여 주는 한 예였는데—이 여자 앞에 커다란 유혹이 놓여 있다는 생각이었다."], ["Her infant was a boy too.", "그녀의 갓난아이도 사내아이였다."], ["Now, would it be possible for her to change them?", "자, 그녀가 두 아이를 바꿔치기하는 것이 가능하지 않을까?"]], [["Though he was soon satisfied that he had dismissed the idea as romantic and unlikely—though possible, there was no denying—he could not help pursuing it so far as to entertain within himself a picture of what his condition would be, if he should discover such an imposture when he was grown old.", "그는 곧, 그 생각이 공상적이며 일어날 법하지 않다고 여겨 떨쳐 버리긴 했지만—하기야 가능성을 부인할 수는 없었다—그래도 그것을 끝까지 따라가 보지 않을 수 없어서, 만약 자기가 늙은 뒤에 그런 속임수를 알아차린다면 자기 처지가 어떨지 마음속으로 그려 보았다."], ["Whether a man so situated would be able to pluck away the result of so many years of usage, confidence, and belief, from the impostor, and endow a stranger with it?", "그런 처지에 놓인 사람이, 그토록 오랜 세월에 걸쳐 길들고 신뢰하고 믿어 온 그 결실을, 사기꾼에게서 빼앗아 낯선 자에게 물려줄 수 있을까?"]], [["But it was idle speculating thus. It couldn’t happen.", "그러나 이렇게 헤아려 보는 것은 부질없는 일이었다."], ["In a moment afterwards he determined that it could, but that such women were constantly observed, and had no opportunity given them for the accomplishment of such a design, even when they were so wicked as to entertain it.", "그런 일은 일어날 수 없었다. 그러나 잠시 뒤에 그는, 일어날 수도 있지만, 그런 여자들은 늘 감시당하므로 설령 그런 음흉한 계략을 품을 만큼 사악하다 한들 그것을 실행할 기회가 주어지지 않는다고 단정했다."], ["In another moment, he was remembering how few such cases seemed to have ever happened.", "또 잠시 뒤에는, 그런 사례가 여태껏 일어난 적이 거의 없는 듯하다는 사실을 떠올렸다."], ["In another moment he was wondering whether they ever happened and were not found out.", "또 잠시 뒤에는, 그런 일이 실제로 일어났는데도 발각되지 않은 것은 아닐까 하고 자문했다."]], [["As his unusual emotion subsided, these misgivings gradually melted away, though so much of their shadow remained behind, that he was constant in his resolution to look closely after Richards himself, without appearing to do so.", "그 평소답지 않은 격정이 가라앉으면서 이런 불안도 차츰 녹아 사라졌지만, 그 그림자가 워낙 많이 뒤에 남은 탓에, 그는 티 내지 않으면서 자기가 직접 리처즈를 면밀히 살펴보겠다는 결심만은 굳게 지켰다."], ["Being now in an easier frame of mind, he regarded the woman’s station as rather an advantageous circumstance than otherwise, by placing, in itself, a broad distance between her and the child, and rendering their separation easy and natural.", "이제 한결 편안한 마음이 된 그는, 그 여자의 신분이 오히려 유리한 사정이라고 보게 되었다. 그 신분 자체가 그녀와 아이 사이에 넓은 거리를 두어, 둘의 이별을 쉽고 자연스럽게 만들어 주기 때문이었다."], ["Thence he passed to the contemplation of the future glories of Dombey and Son, and dismissed the memory of his wife, for the time being, with a tributary sigh or two.", "거기서부터 그는 돔비와 아들의 미래의 영광을 그려 보는 데로 넘어갔고, 당분간은 아내의 기억을, 추모의 한숨 한두 번과 함께 떨쳐 버렸다."]], [["Meanwhile terms were ratified and agreed upon between Mrs Chick and Richards, with the assistance of Miss Tox; and Richards being with much ceremony invested with the Dombey baby, as if it were an Order, resigned her own, with many tears and kisses, to Jemima.", "그러는 사이에 톡스 양의 도움을 받아 칙 부인과 리처즈 사이에 조건이 비준되고 합의되었다. 그리고 리처즈는 마치 무슨 훈장이라도 받듯이 대단히 격식을 갖추어 돔비 가의 갓난아이를 떠맡았고, 자기 아이는 숱한 눈물과 입맞춤과 함께 제미마에게 넘겼다."], ["Glasses of wine were then produced, to sustain the drooping spirits of the family; and Miss Tox, busying herself in dispensing “tastes”", "그러고 나서 가족의 처진 기운을 북돋우려고 포도주 잔들이 나왔다."], ["to the younger branches, bred them up to their father’s business with such surprising expedition, that she made chokers of four of them in a quarter of a minute.", "톡스 양은 어린 가지들에게 \"맛보기\"를 나누어 주느라 분주했는데, 어찌나 놀랍도록 신속하게 그들을 그 아버지의 직업으로 길러 냈던지, 십오 초 만에 그중 넷을 교살자로 만들어 버렸다."]], [["“You’ll take a glass yourself, Sir, won’t you?”", "\"선생도 한 잔 드시겠죠, 그렇죠?\""], ["said Miss Tox, as Toodle appeared.", "투들이 나타나자 톡스 양이 말했다."]], [["“Thankee, Mum,”", "\"고맙습니다요, 마님,\""], ["said Toodle, “since you are suppressing.”", "투들이 말했다, \"권하시니 말입니다요.\""]], [["“And you’re very glad to leave your dear good wife in such a comfortable home, ain’t you, Sir?”", "\"그리고 사랑하는 착한 아내를 이렇게 편안한 집에 두고 가게 되어 무척 기쁘시죠, 그렇죠, 선생?\""], ["said Miss Tox, nodding and winking at him stealthily.", "톡스 양이 그에게 고개를 끄덕이고 슬그머니 눈짓하며 말했다."]], [["“No, Mum,”", "\"아닙니다, 마님,\""], ["said Toodle.", "투들이 말했다."], ["“Here’s wishing of her back agin.”", "\"도로 데려가고 싶을 뿐입니다요.\""]], [["Polly cried more than ever at this.", "폴리는 이 말에 그 어느 때보다 더 울었다."], ["So Mrs Chick, who had her matronly apprehensions that this indulgence in grief might be prejudicial to the little Dombey (“acid, indeed,” she whispered Miss Tox), hastened to the rescue.", "그래서 칙 부인은, 이런 슬픔에 빠지는 것이 어린 돔비에게 해로울지 모른다는 주부다운 걱정에서(\"그야말로 산성이 되니까요\"라고 그녀는 톡스 양에게 속삭였다), 서둘러 구원에 나섰다."]], [["“Your little child will thrive charmingly with your sister Jemima, Richards,”", "\"자네 어린 아이는 자네 동생 제미마 손에서 더없이 잘 자랄 거예요, 리처즈,\""], ["said Mrs Chick; “and you have only to make an effort—this is a world of effort, you know, Richards—to be very happy indeed.", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"자네는 그저 노력만 하면—세상은 노력이잖아요, 알다시피, 리처즈—정말로 아주 행복해질 거예요."], ["You have been already measured for your mourning, haven’t you, Richards?”", "상복 치수는 이미 쟀지요, 그렇죠, 리처즈?\""]], [["“Ye—es, Ma’am,”", "\"네—네, 마님,\""], ["sobbed Polly.", "폴리가 흐느꼈다."]], [["“And it’ll fit beautifully.", "\"그리고 그게 아주 멋지게 맞을 거예요."], ["I know,”", "내가 알지요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “for the same young person has made me many dresses.", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"그 똑같은 젊은 사람이 내 옷도 여러 벌 지어 줬으니까요."], ["The very best materials, too!”", "더구나 최고의 옷감으로요!\""]], [["“Lor, you’ll be so smart,”", "\"어머, 어찌나 맵시가 날는지,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “that your husband won’t know you; will you, Sir?”", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"남편이 자넬 못 알아보겠어요. 그렇죠, 선생?\""]], [["“I should know her,”", "\"저는 알아봅니다요,\""], ["said Toodle, gruffly, “anyhows and anywheres.”", "투들이 퉁명스레 말했다, \"어떤 모습이든 어디서든요.\""]], [["Toodle was evidently not to be bought over.", "투들은 분명 매수당할 사람이 아니었다."]], [["“As to living, Richards, you know,”", "\"먹고 지내는 거로 말하면, 리처즈, 알다시피,\""], ["pursued Mrs Chick, “why, the very best of everything will be at your disposal.", "칙 부인이 이어 말했다, \"무엇이든 가장 좋은 것이 자네 마음대로 차려질 거예요."], ["You will order your little dinner every day; and anything you take a fancy to, I’m sure will be as readily provided as if you were a Lady.”", "매일 자기 끼니를 주문하면 되고, 무엇이든 자네가 먹고 싶은 게 있으면, 마치 자네가 귀부인이라도 되는 양 척척 마련될 거예요, 틀림없이.\""]], [["“Yes to be sure!”", "\"그래요, 물론이지요!\""], ["said Miss Tox, keeping up the ball with great sympathy.", "톡스 양이 깊은 동정심으로 맞장구를 치며 말했다."], ["“And as to porter!—quite unlimited, will it not, Louisa?”", "\"그리고 흑맥주로 말하면!—전혀 제한이 없잖아요, 그렇죠, 루이자?\""]], [["“Oh, certainly!”", "\"오, 그럼요!\""], ["returned Mrs Chick in the same tone.", "칙 부인이 같은 어조로 응수했다."], ["“With a little abstinence, you know, my dear, in point of vegetables.”", "\"다만 채소는, 알다시피, 여보, 조금 절제하고요.\""]], [["“And pickles, perhaps,”", "\"그리고 어쩌면 절임류도요,\""], ["suggested Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 거들었다."]], [["“With such exceptions,”", "\"그런 예외만 빼면,\""], ["said Louisa, “she’ll consult her choice entirely, and be under no restraint at all, my love.”", "루이자가 말했다, \"본인이 전적으로 자기 취향대로 고르면 되고, 아무 제약도 없을 거예요, 얘야.\""]], [["“And then, of course, you know,”", "\"그리고 또, 물론, 알다시피,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “however fond she is of her own dear little child—and I’m sure, Louisa, you don’t blame her for being fond of it?”", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"이 사람이 제 사랑스러운 어린 아이를 아무리 끔찍이 여긴다 해도—그야 분명, 루이자, 자기 아이를 끔찍이 여긴다고 자네가 나무라진 않겠지요?\""]], [["“Oh no!”", "\"오, 천만에요!\""], ["cried Mrs Chick, benignantly.", "칙 부인이 인자하게 외쳤다."]], [["“Still,”", "\"그래도,\""], ["resumed Miss Tox, “she naturally must be interested in her young charge, and must consider it a privilege to see a little cherub connected with the superior classes, gradually unfolding itself from day to day at one common fountain—is it not so, Louisa?”", "톡스 양이 다시 말했다, \"이 사람은 자연히 자기가 맡은 어린것에게 관심을 갖게 될 테고, 상류 계급과 연을 맺은 어린 천사가 하나의 공동 샘에서 날마다 조금씩 피어나는 걸 보는 게 특권이라고 여기게 될 거예요—그렇지 않겠어요, 루이자?\""]], [["“Most undoubtedly!”", "\"의심할 여지 없이 그럼요!\""], ["said Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."], ["“You see, my love, she’s already quite contented and comfortable, and means to say goodbye to her sister Jemima and her little pets, and her good honest husband, with a light heart and a smile; don’t she, my dear?”", "\"보다시피, 얘야, 이 사람은 벌써 아주 만족하고 편안해져서, 가벼운 마음으로 미소 띤 채 동생 제미마와 어린 귀염둥이들과 착하고 정직한 남편에게 작별 인사를 하려는 참이잖아요. 그렇죠, 자네?\""]], [["“Oh yes!”", "\"오, 그럼요!\""], ["cried Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 외쳤다."], ["“To be sure she does!”", "\"물론 그렇고말고요!\""]], [["Notwithstanding which, however, poor Polly embraced them all round in great distress, and coming to her spouse at last, could not make up her mind to part from him, until he gently disengaged himself, at the close of the following allegorical piece of consolation:", "그렇지만 그럼에도 불구하고, 가엾은 폴리는 큰 고통 속에서 모두를 차례로 끌어안았고, 마지막으로 남편에게 다가가서는 그와 헤어질 결심이 도무지 서지 않았다. 결국 남편이 다음과 같은 비유적인 위로의 말을 마치며 부드럽게 그녀를 떼어 놓고 나서야 헤어졌다."]], [["“Polly, old “ooman, whatever you do, my darling, hold up your head and fight low.", "\"폴리, 여보, 무슨 일이 있든, 내 사랑, 고개는 들고 낮게 싸우라고."], ["That’s the only rule as I know on, that’ll carry anyone through life.", "그게 사람을 살면서 헤쳐 나가게 해 주는, 내가 아는 유일한 규칙이야."], ["You always have held up your head and fought low, Polly.", "당신은 늘 고개를 들고 낮게 싸워 왔지, 폴리."], ["Do it now, or Bricks is no longer so.", "지금도 그렇게 해, 안 그러면 브릭스도 더는 안 그럴 테니까."], ["God bless you, Polly!", "신의 가호가 있기를, 폴리!"], ["Me and J’mima will do your duty by you; and with relating to your’n, hold up your head and fight low, Polly, and you can’t go wrong!”", "나랑 제미마가 당신 몫의 의무를 대신해 줄게. 그리고 당신 일에 관해선, 고개를 들고 낮게 싸우라고, 폴리, 그러면 절대 그르칠 일이 없어!\""]], [["Fortified by this golden secret, Polly finally ran away to avoid any more particular leave-taking between herself and the children.", "이 황금 같은 비결에 힘을 얻은 폴리는, 마침내 자기와 아이들 사이에 더 구구한 작별이 벌어지는 것을 피하려고 달아나듯 빠져나갔다."], ["But the stratagem hardly succeeded as well as it deserved; for the smallest boy but one divining her intent, immediately began swarming upstairs after her—if that word of doubtful etymology be admissible—on his arms and legs; while the eldest (known in the family by the name of Biler, in remembrance of the steam engine) beat a demoniacal tattoo with his boots, expressive of grief; in which he was joined by the rest of the family.", "그러나 그 책략은 받아 마땅한 만큼 잘 들어맞지는 못했다. 막내 바로 위의 사내아이가 그녀의 의도를 알아채고는, 곧장 팔다리로 기어 그녀를 쫓아 계단을 위로 떼 지어 올라가기 시작했으니—그 어원이 미심쩍은 표현이 허용된다면 말이다—한편 맏이(증기 기관을 기념하여 집안에서 바일러라 불리는)는 슬픔을 드러내며 부츠로 악마 같은 북장단을 두드려 댔고, 거기에 나머지 식구들도 가세했다."]], [["A quantity of oranges and halfpence thrust indiscriminately on each young Toodle, checked the first violence of their regret, and the family were speedily transported to their own home, by means of the hackney-coach kept in waiting for that purpose.", "오렌지와 반 페니짜리 동전을 어린 투들들 하나하나에게 닥치는 대로 안겨 주자, 그들의 격렬한 서러움도 일단 누그러졌다. 그리고 그 목적으로 대기시켜 둔 삯마차에 실려, 식구들은 곧 자기네 집으로 옮겨졌다."], ["The children, under the guardianship of Jemima, blocked up the window, and dropped out oranges and halfpence all the way along.", "아이들은 제미마의 보호 아래 창문을 틀어막고서, 길을 가는 내내 오렌지와 반 페니짜리 동전을 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Mr Toodle himself preferred to ride behind among the spikes, as being the mode of conveyance to which he was best accustomed.", "투들 씨 본인은, 그것이 자기가 가장 익숙한 이동 방식인지라, 뒤쪽의 쇠창살 사이에 앉아 가는 편을 택했다."]], [["CHAPTER III. In which Mr Dombey, as a Man and a Father, is seen at the Head of the Home-Department", "돔비 씨가 한 사람의 인간이자 아버지로서, 가정 부서의 수장으로 모습을 드러내는 장"]], [["The funeral of the deceased lady having been “performed” to the entire satisfaction of the undertaker, as well as of the neighbourhood at large, which is generally disposed to be captious on such a point, and is prone to take offence at any omissions or short-comings in the ceremonies, the various members of Mr Dombey’s household subsided into their several places in the domestic system.", "고인이 된 부인의 장례식이 장의사를 더없이 만족시키며 \"치러졌고\", 또한 이런 일에는 흔히 트집을 잡기 일쑤이며 의식의 빠진 점이나 부족한 점에 곧잘 기분 상하기 쉬운 인근 사람들 모두를 두루 만족시키며 치러진 뒤, 돔비 씨 집안의 여러 식구들은 가정이라는 체계 속 저마다의 자리로 가라앉아 들어갔다."], ["That small world, like the great one out of doors, had the capacity of easily forgetting its dead; and when the cook had said she was a quiet-tempered lady, and the house-keeper had said it was the common lot, and the butler had said who’d have thought it, and the housemaid had said she couldn’t hardly believe it, and the footman had said it seemed exactly like a dream, they had quite worn the subject out, and began to think their mourning was wearing rusty too.", "바깥세상이라는 큰 세계가 그러하듯, 그 작은 세계 역시 죽은 이를 쉽게 잊어버리는 능력을 지니고 있었다. 요리사가 조용한 성품의 부인이셨다고 말하고, 가정부가 그게 사람의 공통된 운명이라고 말하고, 집사가 누가 그럴 줄 알았겠느냐고 말하고, 하녀가 도무지 믿기지가 않는다고 말하고, 하인이 영락없이 꿈만 같다고 말하고 나자, 이들은 그 화제를 완전히 닳아 없애 버렸고, 자기들의 상복 또한 이제 낡아빠져 가고 있다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["On Richards, who was established upstairs in a state of honourable captivity, the dawn of her new life seemed to break cold and grey.", "위층에 명예로운 포로 신세로 자리 잡은 리처즈에게는, 새로운 삶의 여명이 차갑고 잿빛으로 밝아 오는 듯했다. 돔비 씨의 집은 큰 집으로, 포틀랜드 플레이스와 브라이언스톤 스퀘어 사이 지역에 있는, 키 높고 어둡고 지독하게 점잖은 거리의 응달 쪽에 있었다."], ["Mr Dombey’s house was a large one, on the shady side of a tall, dark, dreadfully genteel street in the region between Portland Place and Bryanstone Square.", "모퉁이 집이었는데, 넓디넓은 앞마당이 딸려 있어 그 안에는 창살 박힌 창문들이 노려보고, 쓰레기통으로 이어지는 비뚤어진 눈매의 문들이 흘끔거리는 지하 저장고들이 있었다. 음울한 위세를 풍기는 집으로, 뒤쪽이 둥글게 돌아간 형태였고, 거기에는 자갈 깔린 안마당을 굽어보는 일련의 응접실 전체가 들어 있었다."], ["It was a corner house, with great wide areas containing cellars frowned upon by barred windows, and leered at by crooked-eyed doors leading to dustbins.", "그 안마당에는 줄기와 가지가 시커멓게 그을린 앙상한 나무 두 그루가 서 있었는데, 잎이 어찌나 연기에 그을려 말라붙었던지 살랑거린다기보다 달그락거렸다."], ["It was a house of dismal state, with a circular back to it, containing a whole suite of drawing-rooms looking upon a gravelled yard, where two gaunt trees, with blackened trunks and branches, rattled rather than rustled, their leaves were so smoked-dried.", "여름 햇살이 그 거리에 드는 일은 결코 없었다. 다만 아침 식사 무렵, 물수레와 헌옷 장수와 제라늄 파는 사람들과 우산 수선공과, 그리고 길을 가며 네덜란드제 시계의 작은 종을 딸랑딸랑 울리는 사내와 함께 햇살이 찾아올 때를 빼고는."], ["The summer sun was never on the street, but in the morning about breakfast-time, when it came with the water-carts and the old clothes men, and the people with geraniums, and the umbrella-mender, and the man who trilled the little bell of the Dutch clock as he went along.", "그러나 그 햇살도 이내 사라져 그날은 다시 돌아오지 않았다. 그리고 그 뒤를 따라가던 악대며 띄엄띄엄 이어지는 펀치 인형극이 떠나고 나면, 거리는 더없이 음산한 손풍금과 흰 쥐들의 먹잇감으로 남겨졌다."], ["It was soon gone again to return no more that day; and the bands of music and the straggling Punch’s shows going after it, left it a prey to the most dismal of organs, and white mice; with now and then a porcupine, to vary the entertainments; until the butlers whose families were dining out, began to stand at the house-doors in the twilight, and the lamp-lighter made his nightly failure in attempting to brighten up the street with gas.", "이따금 여흥에 변화를 주려는 듯 호저가 한 마리 나타나기도 했다. 그러다가 가족이 외식하러 나간 집사들이 땅거미 속에서 현관문 앞에 서 있기 시작하고, 가스등 켜는 사람이 거리를 가스 불빛으로 환히 밝히려다 밤마다 실패하는 시각이 올 때까지 그러했다."]], [["It was as blank a house inside as outside.", "집은 바깥만큼이나 안도 휑뎅그렁했다."], ["When the funeral was over, Mr Dombey ordered the furniture to be covered up—perhaps to preserve it for the son with whom his plans were all associated—and the rooms to be ungarnished, saving such as he retained for himself on the ground floor.", "장례가 끝나자 돔비 씨는 가구에 덮개를 씌우라고—아마도 자기의 모든 계획이 결부된 아들을 위해 보존해 두려는 것이었으리라—그리고 자기가 일층에서 쓰려고 남겨 둔 방들을 제외한 모든 방의 장식을 거두어 내라고 명했다."], ["Accordingly, mysterious shapes were made of tables and chairs, heaped together in the middle of rooms, and covered over with great winding-sheets.", "그리하여 탁자와 의자들은 방 한가운데 무더기로 쌓인 채 커다란 수의 같은 천으로 덮여 신비로운 형상을 이루었다."], ["Bell-handles, window-blinds, and looking-glasses, being papered up in journals, daily and weekly, obtruded fragmentary accounts of deaths and dreadful murders.", "종 손잡이며 창문 블라인드며 거울들은 일간지와 주간지로 발라 싸여, 죽음과 끔찍한 살인 사건에 관한 단편적인 기사들을 단편적으로 들이밀었다."], ["Every chandelier or lustre, muffled in holland, looked like a monstrous tear depending from the ceiling’s eye.", "모든 샹들리에며 장식 조명은 무명천에 싸여, 천장의 눈에서 늘어진 한 방울의 괴물 같은 눈물처럼 보였다."], ["Odours, as from vaults and damp places, came out of the chimneys.", "굴뚝에서는 지하 납골당이나 축축한 곳에서 나는 듯한 냄새가 새어 나왔다."], ["The dead and buried lady was awful in a picture-frame of ghastly bandages.", "죽어 묻힌 부인은 섬뜩한 붕대로 된 액자 틀 속에서 무시무시했다."], ["Every gust of wind that rose, brought eddying round the corner from the neighbouring mews, some fragments of the straw that had been strewn before the house when she was ill, mildewed remains of which were still cleaving to the neighbourhood: and these, being always drawn by some invisible attraction to the threshold of the dirty house to let immediately opposite, addressed a dismal eloquence to Mr Dombey’s windows.", "바람이 한 차례 일 때마다 이웃 마구간 골목 모퉁이를 돌아 소용돌이치며, 부인이 앓을 때 집 앞에 깔아 두었던 짚 부스러기를 실어 왔는데, 곰팡이 슨 그 잔해는 여전히 인근에 들러붙어 있었다. 그리고 그것들은 언제나 어떤 보이지 않는 인력에 이끌려, 바로 맞은편 즉시 세놓는다는 더러운 집의 문지방으로 끌려가서는, 돔비 씨의 창문들을 향해 음울한 웅변을 늘어놓았다."]], [["The apartments which Mr Dombey reserved for his own inhabiting, were attainable from the hall, and consisted of a sitting-room; a library, which was in fact a dressing-room, so that the smell of hot-pressed paper, vellum, morocco, and Russia leather, contended in it with the smell of divers pairs of boots; and a kind of conservatory or little glass breakfast-room beyond, commanding a prospect of the trees before mentioned, and, generally speaking, of a few prowling cats.", "돔비 씨가 자기 거처로 남겨 둔 방들은 현관에서 들어갈 수 있었는데, 거실 하나와 서재 하나가 있었다. 서재라고는 하나 실은 옷 갈아입는 방이어서, 다리미질한 종이와 모조 양피지와 모로코가죽과 러시아가죽의 냄새가 그 안에서 여러 켤레의 부츠 냄새와 다투었다."], ["These three rooms opened upon one another. In the morning, when Mr Dombey was at his breakfast in one or other of the two first-mentioned of them, as well as in the afternoon when he came home to dinner, a bell was rung for Richards to repair to this glass chamber, and there walk to and fro with her young charge.", "그리고 그 너머에는 일종의 온실, 또는 작은 유리 아침식사용 방이 있었는데, 앞서 말한 나무들과, 대체로 어슬렁거리는 고양이 몇 마리가 내다보이는 전망을 갖추고 있었다."], ["From the glimpses she caught of Mr Dombey at these times, sitting in the dark distance, looking out towards the infant from among the dark heavy furniture—the house had been inhabited for years by his father, and in many of its appointments was old-fashioned and grim—she began to entertain ideas of him in his solitary state, as if he were a lone prisoner in a cell, or a strange apparition that was not to be accosted or understood.", "이 세 방은 서로 통해 있었다. 아침에 돔비 씨가 앞서 말한 두 방 가운데 어느 한 곳에서 아침을 들 때, 또 오후에 그가 만찬을 들러 집에 돌아왔을 때, 종이 울려 리처즈를 이 유리방으로 불러들였고, 거기서 그녀는 어린 도련님을 안고 이리저리 거닐었다."], ["Mr Dombey came to be, in the course of a few days, invested in his own person, to her simple thinking, with all the mystery and gloom of his house.", "이런 때에 어두컴컴한 저편에 앉아, 어둡고 묵직한 가구들 사이에서 갓난아이 쪽을 바라보는 돔비 씨를 언뜻언뜻 보게 되면서—그 집은 그의 부친이 여러 해 살았던 집이라 많은 비품들이 구식이고 음산했다—리처즈는 그 외로운 처지의 그에 대해, 마치 그가 감방에 홀로 갇힌 죄수이거나, 말을 걸 수도 이해할 수도 없는 기이한 유령이기라도 한 듯한 생각을 품기 시작했다."], ["As she walked up and down the glass room, or sat hushing the baby there—which she very often did for hours together, when the dusk was closing in, too—she would sometimes try to pierce the gloom beyond, and make out how he was looking and what he was doing.", "며칠이 지나자 돔비 씨는, 그녀의 소박한 생각 속에서, 그 자신의 일신에 그 집의 온갖 신비와 음울함을 두른 존재가 되었다. 유리방을 이리저리 거닐거나, 거기서 갓난아이를 어르며 앉아 있을 때—땅거미가 질 무렵에도 종종 몇 시간씩 그렇게 하곤 했는데—그녀는 이따금 저편의 어둠을 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰며, 그가 어떤 표정을 짓고 있는지, 무엇을 하고 있는지 알아내려 했다."], ["Sensible that she was plainly to be seen by him, however, she never dared to pry in that direction but very furtively and for a moment at a time.", "그러나 자기가 그에게 훤히 보인다는 것을 알고 있었기에, 그쪽을 엿보려는 시도는 결코 감히 하지 못했고, 다만 아주 몰래, 한 번에 한순간씩만 곁눈질했을 뿐이다."], ["Consequently she made out nothing, and Mr Dombey in his den remained a very shade.", "따라서 그녀는 아무것도 알아내지 못했고, 자기 소굴 속의 돔비 씨는 여전히 하나의 그림자에 머물러 있었다."]], [["Little Paul Dombey’s foster-mother had led this life herself, and had carried little Paul through it for some weeks; and had returned upstairs one day from a melancholy saunter through the dreary rooms of state (she never went out without Mrs Chick, who called on fine mornings, usually accompanied by Miss Tox, to take her and Baby for an airing—or in other words, to march them gravely up and down the pavement, like a walking funeral); when, as she was sitting in her own room, the door was slowly and quietly opened, and a dark-eyed little girl looked in.", "어린 폴 돔비의 유모는 몇 주 동안 이런 생활을 몸소 해 왔고, 어린 폴을 안고 그 생활을 헤쳐 왔다. 그러던 어느 날 그녀는 음산한 위세의 방들을 거니는 우울한 산책을 마치고 위층으로 돌아온 참이었다(그녀는 칙 부인 없이는 결코 외출하지 않았는데, 칙 부인은 날 좋은 아침이면, 대개 톡스 양을 대동하고 찾아와 그녀와 아기를 바람 쐬러 데리고 나갔다—다시 말해, 걸어가는 장례 행렬처럼 보도를 따라 엄숙하게 이리저리 행진시켰다). 그녀가 자기 방에 앉아 있는데, 문이 천천히 그리고 조용히 열리더니, 검은 눈의 어린 소녀가 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["“It’s Miss Florence come home from her aunt’s, no doubt,”", "\"이모님 댁에서 돌아오신 플로렌스 아가씨인 게 분명하군,\""], ["thought Richards, who had never seen the child before.", "그 아이를 한 번도 본 적 없는 리처즈는 생각했다."], ["“Hope I see you well, Miss.”", "\"안녕하셨어요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Is that my brother?”", "\"저게 제 동생인가요?\""], ["asked the child, pointing to the Baby.", "아이가 아기를 가리키며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, my pretty,”", "\"네, 예쁜 아가씨,\""], ["answered Richards.", "리처즈가 대답했다."], ["“Come and kiss him.”", "\"이리 와서 입맞춰 주세요.\""]], [["But the child, instead of advancing, looked her earnestly in the face, and said:", "그러나 아이는 다가오기는커녕 그녀의 얼굴을 진지하게 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“What have you done with my Mama?”", "\"우리 엄마를 어떻게 한 거예요?\""]], [["“Lord bless the little creeter!”", "\"세상에 가엾은 것 같으니!\" 리처즈가 외쳤다."], ["cried Richards, “what a sad question!", "\"어쩜 그렇게 슬픈 질문을! 제가요?"], ["I done? Nothing, Miss.”", "아무것도 안 했답니다, 아가씨.\""]], [["“What have they done with my Mama?”", "\"우리 엄마를 어떻게 한 거예요?\""], ["inquired the child, with exactly the same look and manner.", "아이가 똑같은 표정과 똑같은 태도로 다시 물었다."]], [["“I never saw such a melting thing in all my life!”", "\"내 평생 이렇게 가슴 아픈 일은 본 적이 없네!\""], ["said Richards, who naturally substituted for this child one of her own, inquiring for herself in like circumstances.", "리처즈가 말했다. 그녀는 자연스레 이 아이 자리에 자기 아이 하나를 놓고, 같은 처지에서 자기를 찾아 묻는 모습을 떠올렸던 것이다."], ["“Come nearer here, my dear Miss!", "\"이리 가까이 와요, 가엾은 아가씨!"], ["Don’t be afraid of me.”", "저를 무서워하지 마세요.\""]], [["“I am not afraid of you,”", "\"전 당신이 무섭지 않아요,\""], ["said the child, drawing nearer.", "아이가 가까이 다가오며 말했다."], ["“But I want to know what they have done with my Mama.”", "\"하지만 우리 엄마를 어떻게 했는지 알고 싶어요.\""]], [["Her heart swelled so as she stood before the woman, looking into her eyes, that she was fain to press her little hand upon her breast and hold it there.", "그 여인 앞에 서서 그녀의 눈을 들여다보는 동안, 아이의 가슴이 어찌나 벅차올랐던지, 아이는 작은 손을 제 가슴에 갖다 대어 거기 누르고 있어야만 했다."], ["Yet there was a purpose in the child that prevented both her slender figure and her searching gaze from faltering.", "그러나 아이에게는 어떤 결연한 뜻이 있어서, 그 가냘픈 몸도, 캐묻는 듯한 눈길도 흔들리지 않게 해 주었다."]], [["“My darling,”", "\"우리 아가야,\""], ["said Richards, “you wear that pretty black frock in remembrance of your Mama.”", "리처즈가 말했다, \"그렇게 예쁜 검은 옷을 입은 건 엄마를 기억하기 위해서랍니다.\""]], [["“I can remember my Mama,”", "\"전 우리 엄마를 기억할 수 있어요,\""], ["returned the child, with tears springing to her eyes, “in any frock.”", "아이가 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 대답했다, \"어떤 옷을 입어도요.\""]], [["“But people put on black, to remember people when they’re gone.”", "\"하지만 사람들은 떠나간 이들을 기억하려고 검은 옷을 입는답니다.\""]], [["“Where gone?”", "\"어디로 떠났는데요?\""], ["asked the child.", "아이가 물었다."]], [["“Come and sit down by me,”", "\"이리 와서 제 곁에 앉아요,\""], ["said Richards, “and I’ll tell you a story.”", "리처즈가 말했다, \"그럼 이야기를 하나 들려드릴게요.\""]], [["With a quick perception that it was intended to relate to what she had asked, little Florence laid aside the bonnet she had held in her hand until now, and sat down on a stool at the Nurse’s feet, looking up into her face.", "자기가 물어본 것에 관한 이야기일 거라고 재빨리 알아챈 어린 플로렌스는, 그때까지 손에 들고 있던 보닛을 옆에 내려놓고, 유모의 발치 걸상에 앉아 그녀의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."]], [["“Once upon a time,”", "\"옛날 옛적에,\""], ["said Richards, “there was a lady—a very good lady, and her little daughter dearly loved her.”", "리처즈가 말했다, \"한 부인이 있었어요—아주 착한 부인이었는데, 그 어린 딸이 부인을 무척이나 사랑했답니다.\""]], [["“A very good lady and her little daughter dearly loved her,”", "\"아주 착한 부인이었는데 그 어린 딸이 부인을 무척이나 사랑했어요,\""], ["repeated the child.", "아이가 따라 했다."]], [["“Who, when God thought it right that it should be so, was taken ill and died.”", "\"그런데 하느님께서 그렇게 되는 게 옳다고 생각하셨을 때, 부인은 병에 걸려 돌아가셨답니다.\""]], [["The child shuddered.", "아이가 몸서리쳤다."]], [["“Died, never to be seen again by anyone on earth, and was buried in the ground where the trees grow.”", "\"돌아가셔서, 이 세상 누구에게도 다시는 보이지 않게 되었고, 나무들이 자라는 땅에 묻혔어요.\""]], [["“The cold ground?”", "\"차가운 땅에요?\""], ["said the child, shuddering again.", "아이가 또 한 번 몸서리치며 말했다."]], [["“No! The warm ground,”", "\"아니에요!"], ["returned Polly, seizing her advantage, “where the ugly little seeds turn into beautiful flowers, and into grass, and corn, and I don’t know what all besides.", "따뜻한 땅이랍니다,\" 폴리가 기회를 붙잡으며 대답했다, \"못생긴 작은 씨앗들이 아름다운 꽃으로, 그리고 풀로, 곡식으로, 그밖에 뭐든 다 되는 그런 땅이지요."], ["Where good people turn into bright angels, and fly away to Heaven!”", "착한 사람들이 빛나는 천사로 변해서 하늘나라로 날아가는 곳이랍니다!\""]], [["The child, who had dropped her head, raised it again, and sat looking at her intently.", "고개를 떨구고 있던 아이가 다시 고개를 들고는, 앉아서 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“So; let me see,”", "\"자, 그러니까,\""], ["said Polly, not a little flurried between this earnest scrutiny, her desire to comfort the child, her sudden success, and her very slight confidence in her own powers.", "폴리가 말했다. 이 진지한 응시와, 아이를 위로하려는 마음과, 뜻밖의 성공과, 자기 능력에 대한 미미하기 짝이 없는 자신감 사이에서 적잖이 당황한 채였다."], ["“So, when this lady died, wherever they took her, or wherever they put her, she went to GOD!", "\"그래서 이 부인이 돌아가셨을 때, 사람들이 부인을 어디로 모셔 갔든, 어디에 모셨든, 부인은 하느님께로 갔답니다! 그리고 이 부인은 하느님께 기도드렸어요,\""], ["and she prayed to Him, this lady did,”", "폴리가 진심으로 간절했던 터라 한없이 감격에 겨워 말했다, \"어린 딸이 그것을 마음속에 확실히 믿도록 가르쳐 달라고요."], ["said Polly, affecting herself beyond measure; being heartily in earnest, “to teach her little daughter to be sure of that in her heart: and to know that she was happy there and loved her still: and to hope and try—Oh, all her life—to meet her there one day, never, never, never to part any more.”", "그리고 엄마가 거기서 행복하며 여전히 딸을 사랑하고 있다는 걸 딸이 알게 해 달라고요. 그리고 언젠가 그곳에서 엄마를 만나, 다시는, 다시는, 다시는 헤어지지 않게 되기를—오, 평생토록—바라고 또 애쓰게 해 달라고요.\""]], [["“It was my Mama!”", "\"우리 엄마였어요!\""], ["exclaimed the child, springing up, and clasping her round the neck.", "아이가 벌떡 일어나 그녀의 목을 끌어안으며 외쳤다."]], [["“And the child’s heart,”", "\"그리고 그 아이의 마음은,\""], ["said Polly, drawing her to her breast: “the little daughter’s heart was so full of the truth of this, that even when she heard it from a strange nurse that couldn’t tell it right, but was a poor mother herself and that was all, she found a comfort in it—didn’t feel so lonely—sobbed and cried upon her bosom—took kindly to the baby lying in her lap—and—there, there, there!”", "폴리가 아이를 자기 가슴으로 끌어당기며 말했다, \"그 어린 딸의 마음은 이 진실로 어찌나 가득 찼던지, 그 이야기를 제대로 들려줄 줄도 모르고 그저 자신도 가엾은 한 어머니일 뿐인 낯선 유모에게서 들었을 때조차, 거기서 위안을 찾았답니다—그렇게 외롭지 않게 느껴졌고요—그 유모의 가슴에 기대어 흐느껴 울었지요—제 무릎에 누운 갓난아기에게 다정하게 마음을 주었어요—그리고—그래, 그래, 그래!\""], ["said Polly, smoothing the child’s curls and dropping tears upon them.", "폴리가 아이의 곱슬머리를 쓰다듬으며 그 위에 눈물을 떨구며 말했다."], ["“There, poor dear!”", "\"그래, 가엾은 것!\""]], [["“Oh well, Miss Floy!", "\"어머나, 플로이 아가씨!"], ["And won’t your Pa be angry neither!”", "아버님께서 또 노여워하시지 않겠어요!\""], ["cried a quick voice at the door, proceeding from a short, brown, womanly girl of fourteen, with a little snub nose, and black eyes like jet beads.", "문가에서 빠른 목소리가 외쳤다. 작달막하고 까무잡잡하며 어른티 나는 열네 살 소녀의 목소리였는데, 들창코에다 흑옥 구슬 같은 검은 눈을 하고 있었다."], ["“When it was “tickerlerly given out that you wasn’t to go and worrit the wet nurse.”", "\"유모를 귀찮게 하러 가면 안 된다고 그렇게 특별히 일러두었는데.\""]], [["“She don’t worry me,”", "\"전혀 귀찮지 않은데요,\""], ["was the surprised rejoinder of Polly.", "폴리가 놀라 대꾸했다."], ["“I am very fond of children.”", "\"전 아이들을 무척 좋아한답니다.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["but begging your pardon, Mrs Richards, that don’t matter, you know,”", "그래도 죄송한 말씀이지만, 리처즈 부인, 그건 상관없는 일이잖아요,\""], ["returned the black-eyed girl, who was so desperately sharp and biting that she seemed to make one’s eyes water.", "검은 눈의 소녀가 대답했다. 어찌나 지독하게 날카롭고 톡 쏘는지 듣는 이의 눈에서 눈물이 핑 돌 정도였다."], ["“I may be very fond of pennywinkles, Mrs Richards, but it don’t follow that I’m to have ’em for tea.”", "\"제가 경단고둥을 무척 좋아할 수도 있지요, 리처즈 부인, 그렇다고 차 마실 때마다 그걸 먹어야 한다는 법은 없잖아요.\""]], [["“Well, it don’t matter,”", "\"뭐, 상관없어요,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, thank’ee, Mrs Richards, don’t it!”", "\"어머, 고맙기도 해라, 리처즈 부인, 상관없으시다니!\""], ["returned the sharp girl.", "날카로운 소녀가 대꾸했다."], ["“Remembering, however, if you’ll be so good, that Miss Floy’s under my charge, and Master Paul’s under your’n.”", "\"그래도 죄송하지만 기억해 주세요. 플로이 아가씨는 제 담당이고, 폴 도련님은 부인 담당이라는 걸요.\""]], [["“But still we needn’t quarrel,”", "\"그래도 우리가 다툴 필요는 없잖아요,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“Oh no, Mrs Richards,”", "\"어머 그럼요, 리처즈 부인,\""], ["rejoined Spitfire.", "스핏파이어가 응수했다."], ["“Not at all, I don’t wish it, we needn’t stand upon that footing, Miss Floy being a permanency, Master Paul a temporary.”", "\"전혀요, 저도 그러길 바라지 않아요, 그렇게 따지고 들 것까진 없죠, 플로이 아가씨는 붙박이고 폴 도련님은 임시니까요.\""], ["Spitfire made use of none but comma pauses; shooting out whatever she had to say in one sentence, and in one breath, if possible.", "스핏파이어는 쉼표만큼의 멈춤밖에 쓰지 않았다. 할 말이 무엇이든 가능하면 한 문장에, 한 호흡에 쏘아 내뱉었다."]], [["“Miss Florence has just come home, hasn’t she?”", "\"플로렌스 아가씨가 막 집에 돌아오신 거죠, 그렇죠?\""], ["asked Polly.", "폴리가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, Mrs Richards, just come, and here, Miss Floy, before you’ve been in the house a quarter of an hour, you go a smearing your wet face against the expensive mourning that Mrs Richards is a wearing for your Ma!”", "\"네, 리처즈 부인, 막 오셨어요, 그런데 플로이 아가씨, 집에 들어온 지 십오 분도 안 됐는데, 부인이 아가씨 어머님 때문에 입고 계신 그 비싼 상복에다 젖은 얼굴을 비벼 대고 계시네요!\""], ["With this remonstrance, young Spitfire, whose real name was Susan Nipper, detached the child from her new friend by a wrench—as if she were a tooth.", "이렇게 나무라면서, 본명이 수전 닙퍼인 어린 스핏파이어는 아이를 새 친구에게서 홱 떼어 냈다—마치 이를 뽑아내듯이."], ["But she seemed to do it, more in the excessively sharp exercise of her official functions, than with any deliberate unkindness.", "그러나 그것은 어떤 의도적인 매정함에서라기보다는, 자기 직무를 지나치게 날카롭게 수행하느라 그런 것 같았다."]], [["“She’ll be quite happy, now she has come home again,”", "\"이제 다시 집에 돌아오셨으니 아주 행복해지실 거예요,\""], ["said Polly, nodding to her with an encouraging smile upon her wholesome face, “and will be so pleased to see her dear Papa tonight.”", "폴리가 자기 건강한 얼굴에 격려의 미소를 띠며 그녀에게 고개를 끄덕여 보였다, \"그리고 오늘 밤 사랑하는 아버님을 뵙게 되면 무척 기뻐하시겠지요.\""]], [["“Lork, Mrs Richards!”", "\"세상에, 리처즈 부인!\""], ["cried Miss Nipper, taking up her words with a jerk.", "닙퍼 양이 그 말을 홱 받아채며 외쳤다. \"그러지 마세요."], ["“Don’t. See her dear Papa indeed!", "사랑하는 아버님이라니요!"], ["I should like to see her do it!”", "아가씨가 그러시는 꼴을 한번 보고 싶네요!\""]], [["“Won’t she then?”", "\"그럼 안 그러신다고요?\""], ["asked Polly.", "폴리가 물었다."]], [["“Lork, Mrs Richards, no, her Pa’s a deal too wrapped up in somebody else, and before there was a somebody else to be wrapped up in she never was a favourite, girls are thrown away in this house, Mrs Richards, I assure you.”", "\"세상에, 리처즈 부인, 그럼요, 아가씨 아버님은 딴 사람한테 너무 푹 빠져 계신 데다, 푹 빠질 딴 사람이 생기기 전에도 아가씨는 결코 귀염받은 적이 없어요. 이 집에선 여자아이들은 내버려져요, 리처즈 부인, 정말이라니까요.\""]], [["The child looked quickly from one nurse to the other, as if she understood and felt what was said.", "아이는 한 유모에게서 다른 유모에게로 재빨리 눈길을 옮겼다. 마치 오가는 말을 알아듣고 느끼기라도 하는 듯이."]], [["“You surprise me!”", "\"놀랍네요!\""], ["cried Polly.", "폴리가 외쳤다."], ["“Hasn’t Mr Dombey seen her since—”", "\"돔비 씨께서 그 후로 아가씨를 못 보셨어요—\""]], [["“No,” interrupted Susan Nipper.", "\"못 보셨죠,\""], ["“Not once since, and he hadn’t hardly set his eyes upon her before that for months and months, and I don’t think he’d have known her for his own child if he had met her in the streets, or would know her for his own child if he was to meet her in the streets to-morrow, Mrs Richards, as to me,”", "수전 닙퍼가 가로막았다. \"그 후로 한 번도요, 그리고 그 전에도 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 아가씨한테 거의 눈길도 안 주셨어요, 제 생각엔 길거리에서 마주쳤어도 자기 자식인 줄 못 알아보셨을 거예요, 내일 길거리에서 마주친대도 자기 자식인 줄 못 알아보실걸요, 리처즈 부인, 저로 말하자면,\""], ["said Spitfire, with a giggle, “I doubt if he’s aweer of my existence.”", "스핏파이어가 키득 웃으며 말했다, \"제가 이 세상에 있는 줄이나 아실지 의심스럽다니까요.\""]], [["“Pretty dear!”", "\"가엾은 것!\""], ["said Richards; meaning, not Miss Nipper, but the little Florence.", "리처즈가 말했다. 닙퍼 양이 아니라 어린 플로렌스를 두고 한 말이었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["there’s a Tartar within a hundred miles of where we’re now in conversation, I can tell you, Mrs Richards, present company always excepted too,”", "지금 우리가 이야기 나누는 데서 백 마일 안에 폭군 하나가 있다는 거, 제가 말씀드릴 수 있어요, 리처즈 부인, 물론 여기 계신 분들은 언제나 예외고요,\""], ["said Susan Nipper; “wish you good morning, Mrs Richards, now Miss Floy, you come along with me, and don’t go hanging back like a naughty wicked child that judgments is no example to, don’t!”", "수전 닙퍼가 말했다. \"그럼 안녕히 계세요, 리처즈 부인, 자 플로이 아가씨, 저랑 같이 가요, 그리고 본보기로 삼아도 소용없는 못되고 고약한 아이처럼 그렇게 뒤로 빼지 마세요, 어서요!\""]], [["In spite of being thus adjured, and in spite also of some hauling on the part of Susan Nipper, tending towards the dislocation of her right shoulder, little Florence broke away, and kissed her new friend, affectionately.", "이렇게 다그침을 받고, 또 수전 닙퍼가 오른쪽 어깨가 빠지도록 잡아당기는데도 불구하고, 어린 플로렌스는 뿌리치고 나와 새 친구에게 애정 어린 입맞춤을 했다."]], [["“Oh dear!", "\"어머나!"], ["after it was given out so “tickerlerly, that Mrs Richards wasn’t to be made free with!”", "리처즈 부인을 함부로 대하면 안 된다고 그렇게 특별히 일러두었는데!\""], ["exclaimed Susan.", "수전이 외쳤다."], ["“Very well, Miss Floy!”", "\"아주 좋아요, 플로이 아가씨!\""]], [["“God bless the sweet thing!”", "\"하느님께서 이 사랑스러운 아이를 축복하시길!\""], ["said Richards, “Good-bye, dear!”", "리처즈가 말했다. \"잘 가요, 아가야!\""]], [["“Good-bye!” returned the child.", "\"안녕히 계세요!\""], ["“God bless you!", "아이가 대답했다."], ["I shall come to see you again soon, and you’ll come to see me?", "\"하느님이 당신을 축복하시길! 곧 또 보러 올게요, 당신도 저를 보러 올 거죠?"], ["Susan will let us.", "수전이 그렇게 해 줄 거예요."], ["Won’t you, Susan?”", "그렇지, 수전?\""]], [["Spitfire seemed to be in the main a good-natured little body, although a disciple of that school of trainers of the young idea which holds that childhood, like money, must be shaken and rattled and jostled about a good deal to keep it bright.", "스핏파이어는, 어린 사고력을 기르는 사람들 가운데 어린 시절이란 돈처럼 반짝반짝하게 유지하려면 많이 흔들고 달그락거리고 떠밀어 대야 한다고 믿는 그런 학파의 제자이긴 했지만, 대체로 마음씨 고운 작은 사람인 듯했다."], ["For, being thus appealed to with some endearing gestures and caresses, she folded her small arms and shook her head, and conveyed a relenting expression into her very-wide-open black eyes.", "이렇게 몇 가지 다정한 몸짓과 어루만짐으로 호소를 받자, 그녀는 작은 두 팔을 팔짱 끼고 고개를 흔들며, 한껏 크게 뜬 검은 눈에 누그러지는 빛을 띠었던 것이다."]], [["“It ain’t right of you to ask it, Miss Floy, for you know I can’t refuse you, but Mrs Richards and me will see what can be done, if Mrs Richards likes, I may wish, you see, to take a voyage to Chaney, Mrs Richards, but I mayn’t know how to leave the London Docks.”", "\"아가씨가 그걸 청하시는 건 옳지 않아요, 플로이 아가씨, 제가 아가씨한테 거절을 못 한다는 걸 아시면서, 하지만 리처즈 부인이 좋으시다면 리처즈 부인이랑 저랑 어떻게 할 수 있을지 알아볼게요, 보세요, 저야 차이나로 항해를 떠나고 싶을 수도 있지만, 런던 부두를 어떻게 벗어나야 할지 모를 수도 있는 거잖아요, 리처즈 부인.\""]], [["Richards assented to the proposition.", "리처즈는 그 말에 동의했다."]], [["“This house ain’t so exactly ringing with merry-making,”", "\"이 집이 그렇게까지 흥겨운 놀이로 떠들썩한 것도 아니니,\""], ["said Miss Nipper, “that one need be lonelier than one must be.", "닙퍼 양이 말했다, \"굳이 필요 이상으로 외로울 까닭은 없죠."], ["Your Toxes and your Chickses may draw out my two front double teeth, Mrs Richards, but that’s no reason why I need offer ’em the whole set.”", "댁의 톡스 양이며 칙 부인이며 하는 분들이 제 앞니 두 개를 뽑아 갈 수는 있어도, 그렇다고 제가 한 벌 다 내드려야 할 이유는 없으니까요, 리처즈 부인.\""]], [["This proposition was also assented to by Richards, as an obvious one.", "이 말 역시 리처즈는 당연한 것으로 받아들여 동의했다."]], [["“So I’m agreeable, I’m sure,”", "\"그러니 저야 기꺼이,\""], ["said Susan Nipper, “to live friendly, Mrs Richards, while Master Paul continues a permanency, if the means can be planned out without going openly against orders, but goodness gracious Miss Floy, you haven’t got your things off yet, you naughty child, you haven’t, come along!”", "수전 닙퍼가 말했다, \"폴 도련님이 붙박이로 계시는 동안에는 다정하게 지낼 마음이에요, 리처즈 부인, 대놓고 분부를 어기지 않으면서 방도를 짜낼 수만 있다면요, 그런데 아이고 세상에 플로이 아가씨, 아직 옷도 안 벗으셨네요, 이 말 안 듣는 아이 같으니, 아직도, 어서 와요!\""]], [["With these words, Susan Nipper, in a transport of coercion, made a charge at her young ward, and swept her out of the room.", "이 말과 함께 수전 닙퍼는 강제의 발작에 휩싸여 어린 담당 아가씨에게 돌격하더니 아이를 휩쓸 듯 방 밖으로 데리고 나갔다."]], [["The child, in her grief and neglect, was so gentle, so quiet, and uncomplaining; was possessed of so much affection that no one seemed to care to have, and so much sorrowful intelligence that no one seemed to mind or think about the wounding of, that Polly’s heart was sore when she was left alone again.", "슬픔과 방치 속에서도 아이는 어찌나 온순하고, 어찌나 조용하며, 불평이 없던지. 또 아무도 가지려 하지 않는 듯한 애정을 그토록 많이 지녔고, 아무도 그것이 상처받든 말든 마음 쓰거나 생각조차 하지 않는 듯한 슬픈 분별력을 그토록 많이 지녔던지, 다시 혼자 남게 되자 폴리의 마음이 쓰라렸다."], ["In the simple passage that had taken place between herself and the motherless little girl, her own motherly heart had been touched no less than the child’s; and she felt, as the child did, that there was something of confidence and interest between them from that moment.", "자기와 그 어미 없는 어린 소녀 사이에 오간 그 소박한 일을 통해, 폴리 자신의 어머니다운 마음도 아이의 마음 못지않게 감동받았던 것이다. 그리고 그녀는 아이가 그렇게 느끼듯이, 그 순간부터 둘 사이에 어떤 신뢰와 관심이 자리 잡았음을 느꼈다."]], [["Notwithstanding Mr Toodle’s great reliance on Polly, she was perhaps in point of artificial accomplishments very little his superior.", "투들 씨가 폴리를 크게 신뢰하긴 했지만, 인위적인 교양이라는 점에서 보자면 그녀는 어쩌면 그보다 별로 나을 것이 없었다."], ["She had been good-humouredly working and drudging for her life all her life, and was a sober steady-going person, with matter-of-fact ideas about the butcher and baker, and the division of pence into farthings.", "그녀는 평생 살아가기 위해 싹싹하게 일하고 고되게 애써 왔으며, 푸줏간과 빵집에 관한, 그리고 페니를 파딩으로 나누는 일에 관한 실제적인 생각을 지닌, 진지하고 착실한 사람이었다."], ["But she was a good plain sample of a nature that is ever, in the mass, better, truer, higher, nobler, quicker to feel, and much more constant to retain, all tenderness and pity, self-denial and devotion, than the nature of men.", "그러나 그녀는, 모든 다정함과 연민, 자기희생과 헌신을 한결 더 잘 느끼고, 그것을 훨씬 더 한결같이 간직하는 점에서, 통틀어 보면 언제나 남자의 천성보다 더 낫고, 더 진실하며, 더 높고, 더 고귀한 어떤 천성의 훌륭하고 소박한 본보기였다."], ["And, perhaps, unlearned as she was, she could have brought a dawning knowledge home to Mr Dombey at that early day, which would not then have struck him in the end like lightning.", "그리고 어쩌면, 못 배운 처지였음에도, 그녀는 바로 그 이른 날에 어떤 동트는 깨달음을 돔비 씨에게 가져다줄 수도 있었으리라—그랬더라면 그것이 끝내 벼락처럼 그를 내리치지는 않았을 그런 깨달음을."]], [["But this is from the purpose.", "그러나 이것은 본래 이야기에서 벗어난 말이다."], ["Polly only thought, at that time, of improving on her successful propitiation of Miss Nipper, and devising some means of having little Florence aide her, lawfully, and without rebellion.", "그때 폴리가 생각한 것은 다만, 닙퍼 양을 성공적으로 달랜 일을 더 발전시키는 것과, 어린 플로렌스가 적법하게, 반항을 사지 않고 자기를 돕게 할 어떤 방도를 궁리하는 것뿐이었다."], ["An opening happened to present itself that very night.", "그런 기회가 바로 그날 밤 마침 찾아왔다."]], [["She had been rung down into the glass room as usual, and had walked about and about it a long time, with the baby in her arms, when, to her great surprise and dismay, Mr Dombey—whom she had seen at first leaning on his elbow at the table, and afterwards walking up and down the middle room, drawing, each time, a little nearer, she thought, to the open folding doors—came out, suddenly, and stopped before her.", "여느 때처럼 종이 울려 유리방으로 불려 내려간 그녀는, 아기를 품에 안고 그곳을 오래도록 이리저리 거닐고 있었다. 그러던 차에, 그녀가 처음에는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 있다가, 나중에는 가운데 방을 오락가락 거닐며 그때마다 열린 접이문 쪽으로 조금씩 더 가까이 다가오는 듯하던 돔비 씨가—그녀를 몹시 놀라고 당황하게 하며—갑자기 나오더니 그녀 앞에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Good evening, Richards.”", "\"안녕하시오, 리처즈.\""]], [["Just the same austere, stiff gentleman, as he had appeared to her on that first day.", "첫날 그녀에게 비쳤던 것과 똑같은, 근엄하고 뻣뻣한 신사였다."], ["Such a hard-looking gentleman, that she involuntarily dropped her eyes and her curtsey at the same time.", "어찌나 험상궂은 인상의 신사였던지, 그녀는 저도 모르게 눈길을 떨구는 동시에 절을 했다."]], [["“How is Master Paul, Richards?”", "\"폴 도련님은 어떻소, 리처즈?\""]], [["“Quite thriving, Sir, and well.”", "\"아주 잘 자라고 계세요, 나리, 건강하시고요.\""]], [["“He looks so,”", "\"그렇게 보이는군,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, glancing with great interest at the tiny face she uncovered for his observation, and yet affecting to be half careless of it.", "돔비 씨가 그녀가 살펴보라고 들춰 보인 자그마한 얼굴을 큰 관심으로 흘끗 보면서, 그러면서도 그것에 반쯤 무심한 척하며 말했다."], ["“They give you everything you want, I hope?”", "\"필요한 건 다 챙겨 주는 모양이지?\""]], [["“Oh yes, thank you, Sir.”", "\"네, 그럼요, 감사합니다, 나리.\""]], [["She suddenly appended such an obvious hesitation to this reply, however, that Mr Dombey, who had turned away; stopped, and turned round again, inquiringly.", "그런데 그녀가 갑자기 이 대답에 너무도 뻔한 망설임을 덧붙이는 바람에, 이미 돌아섰던 돔비 씨가 걸음을 멈추고 다시 돌아서며 캐묻는 듯한 표정을 지었다."]], [["“If you please, Sir, the child is very much disposed to take notice of things,”", "\"외람되오나, 나리, 도련님이 사물에 무척 주의를 기울이는 성미라서요,\""], ["said Richards, with another curtsey, “and—upstairs is a little dull for him, perhaps, Sir.”", "리처즈가 다시 한번 절하며 말했다, \"그런데—위층이 도련님에게는 좀 따분할지도 모르겠어요, 나리.\""]], [["“I begged them to take you out for airings, constantly,”", "\"항상 자네를 데리고 바람 쐬러 나가라고 일러두었는데,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“Very well!", "\"좋아!"], ["You shall go out oftener.", "더 자주 나가도록 하게."], ["You’re quite right to mention it.”", "그 점을 말해 준 건 아주 잘한 일이야.\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, Sir,”", "\"송구하옵니다, 나리,\""], ["faltered Polly, “but we go out quite plenty Sir, thank you.”", "폴리가 우물거렸다, \"그래도 저희는 충분히 자주 나가고 있어요, 나리, 감사합니다.\""]], [["“What would you have then?”", "\"그럼 무엇을 바라는 건가?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Indeed Sir, I don’t exactly know,”", "\"실은 나리, 저도 정확히는 모르겠어요,\""], ["said Polly, “unless—”", "폴리가 말했다, \"다만—\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"다만?\""]], [["“I believe nothing is so good for making children lively and cheerful, Sir, as seeing other children playing about ’em,”", "\"아이들을 생기 있고 명랑하게 만드는 데는, 다른 아이들이 곁에서 노는 걸 보는 것만 한 게 없는 듯해요, 나리,\""], ["observed Polly, taking courage.", "폴리가 용기를 내어 말했다."]], [["“I think I mentioned to you, Richards, when you came here,”", "\"자네가 여기 왔을 때 내가 일러두었던 것 같은데, 리처즈,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with a frown, “that I wished you to see as little of your family as possible.”", "돔비 씨가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다, \"자네 가족과는 되도록 적게 만나기를 바란다고 말이야.\""]], [["“Oh dear yes, Sir, I wasn’t so much as thinking of that.”", "\"어머나 그럼요, 나리, 그걸 두고 한 말은 결코 아니었어요.\""]], [["“I am glad of it,”", "\"그렇다니 다행이군,\""], ["said Mr Dombey hastily.", "돔비 씨가 서둘러 말했다."], ["“You can continue your walk if you please.”", "\"원한다면 산책을 계속하게.\""]], [["With that, he disappeared into his inner room; and Polly had the satisfaction of feeling that he had thoroughly misunderstood her object, and that she had fallen into disgrace without the least advancement of her purpose.", "그 말과 함께 그는 안쪽 방으로 사라졌다. 그리고 폴리는, 그가 자기 의도를 완전히 잘못 알아들었다는 것과, 자기 목적은 조금도 진전시키지 못한 채 미움만 샀다는 것을 느끼는 만족을 누렸다."]], [["Next night, she found him walking about the conservatory when she came down.", "이튿날 밤, 그녀가 내려가 보니 그가 온실을 거닐고 있었다."], ["As she stopped at the door, checked by this unusual sight, and uncertain whether to advance or retreat, he called her in.", "이 흔치 않은 광경에 멈칫하여 문가에 멈춰 선 채, 나아가야 할지 물러서야 할지 망설이고 있는데, 그가 그녀를 안으로 불러들였다."], ["His mind was too much set on Dombey and Son, it soon appeared, to admit of his having forgotten her suggestion.", "그의 마음은, 곧 드러났듯이, 너무도 돔비와 아들에 쏠려 있어서 그녀의 제안을 잊을 여지가 없었던 것이다."]], [["“If you really think that sort of society is good for the child,”", "\"그런 또래들과 어울리는 게 정말 아이에게 좋다고 생각한다면,\""], ["he said sharply, as if there had been no interval since she proposed it, “where’s Miss Florence?”", "그가, 마치 그녀가 그것을 제안한 이후로 시간이 전혀 흐르지 않았다는 듯, 날카롭게 말했다, \"플로렌스 아가씨는 어디 있나?\""]], [["“Nothing could be better than Miss Florence, Sir,”", "\"플로렌스 아가씨보다 더 좋은 분은 없을 거예요, 나리,\""], ["said Polly eagerly, “but I understood from her maid that they were not to—”", "폴리가 간절히 말했다, \"그런데 아가씨의 시중드는 아이한테서 듣기로는 그래선 안 된다고—\""]], [["Mr Dombey rang the bell, and walked till it was answered.", "돔비 씨는 종을 울리고, 그것에 응답이 올 때까지 거닐었다."]], [["“Tell them always to let Miss Florence be with Richards when she chooses, and go out with her, and so forth.", "\"플로렌스 아가씨가 원할 때면 언제든 리처즈와 함께 있고, 함께 외출하고, 그렇게 하도록 늘 일러두게."], ["Tell them to let the children be together, when Richards wishes it.”", "리처즈가 바랄 때면 아이들이 함께 있도록 하라고 일러두게.\""]], [["The iron was now hot, and Richards striking on it boldly—it was a good cause and she bold in it, though instinctively afraid of Mr Dombey—requested that Miss Florence might be sent down then and there, to make friends with her little brother.", "이제 쇠가 달구어졌고, 리처즈는 그것을 과감히 내리쳤다—옳은 일이었기에 그녀는 그 일에 담대했다, 비록 본능적으로 돔비 씨가 두렵기는 했지만—해서 플로렌스 아가씨를 지금 당장 이리로 내려보내, 어린 동생과 친해지게 해 달라고 청했다."]], [["She feigned to be dandling the child as the servant retired on this errand, but she thought that she saw Mr Dombey’s colour changed; that the expression of his face quite altered; that he turned, hurriedly, as if to gainsay what he had said, or she had said, or both, and was only deterred by very shame.", "하인이 이 심부름을 하러 물러가는 동안, 그녀는 아기를 어르는 척했다. 그러나 그녀는 돔비 씨의 안색이 변하는 것을 보았다고 생각했다. 그의 얼굴 표정이 완전히 달라지는 것을, 그가 자기가 한 말을, 또는 그녀가 한 말을, 또는 둘 다를 번복하려는 듯 황급히 돌아서다가, 오직 부끄러움 때문에 그만두는 것을 보았다고 생각했다."]], [["And she was right.", "그녀의 생각은 옳았다."], ["The last time he had seen his slighted child, there had been that in the sad embrace between her and her dying mother, which was at once a revelation and a reproach to him.", "자기가 박대한 아이를 마지막으로 보았을 때, 아이와 죽어 가는 어미 사이의 그 슬픈 포옹 속에는, 그에게 하나의 계시인 동시에 하나의 책망이 되는 무언가가 있었다."], ["Let him be absorbed as he would in the Son on whom he built such high hopes, he could not forget that closing scene.", "그가 그토록 큰 희망을 거는 아들에게 아무리 몰두한다 한들, 그는 그 마지막 장면을 잊을 수 없었다."], ["He could not forget that he had had no part in it.", "자기가 거기서 아무 몫도 차지하지 못했다는 것을 잊을 수 없었다."], ["That, at the bottom of its clear depths of tenderness and truth lay those two figures clasped in each other’s arms, while he stood on the bank above them, looking down a mere spectator—not a sharer with them—quite shut out.", "그 다정함과 진실의 맑은 심연 밑바닥에는 서로의 품에 꼭 안긴 두 사람의 모습이 놓여 있었고, 한편 그는 그 위쪽 둑에 서서, 한낱 구경꾼으로—그들과 함께하는 자가 아니라—아래를 내려다보며, 완전히 차단되어 있었다는 것을."]], [["Unable to exclude these things from his remembrance, or to keep his mind free from such imperfect shapes of the meaning with which they were fraught, as were able to make themselves visible to him through the mist of his pride, his previous feeling of indifference towards little Florence changed into an uneasiness of an extraordinary kind.", "이런 것들을 기억에서 몰아내지도, 또 그것들이 잔뜩 품고 있던 의미의 불완전한 형상들—그의 자만이라는 안개를 뚫고 그에게 보이게 될 만큼의 형상들—로부터 마음을 자유롭게 지키지도 못한 채, 어린 플로렌스를 향한 그의 이전의 무관심한 감정은 비상한 종류의 불안으로 바뀌었다."], ["Young as she was, and possessing in any eyes but his (and perhaps in his too) even more than the usual amount of childish simplicity and confidence, he almost felt as if she watched and distrusted him.", "그녀는 그토록 어렸고, 그의 눈을 제외한 누구의 눈에나(어쩌면 그의 눈에도) 보통 아이보다도 더 많은 어린아이다운 순진함과 신뢰를 지니고 있었음에도, 그는 거의 그녀가 자기를 지켜보며 의심하는 것처럼 느꼈다."], ["As if she held the clue to something secret in his breast, of the nature of which he was hardly informed himself.", "마치 그녀가, 그 자신조차 거의 알지 못하는 어떤 성질의, 그의 가슴속 비밀스러운 무언가의 실마리를 쥐고 있는 것처럼."], ["As if she had an innate knowledge of one jarring and discordant string within him, and her very breath could sound it.", "마치 그녀가 그의 내면에 있는 어떤 거슬리고 불협화한 현을 타고난 듯이 알고 있어서, 그녀의 숨결만으로도 그것을 울릴 수 있는 것처럼."]], [["His feeling about the child had been negative from her birth.", "아이에 대한 그의 감정은 그녀가 태어난 때부터 부정적인 것이었다."], ["He had never conceived an aversion to her: it had not been worth his while or in his humour.", "그가 그녀에게 혐오를 품은 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그럴 만한 가치도, 그럴 기분도 그에게는 없었으니까."], ["She had never been a positively disagreeable object to him.", "그녀가 그에게 적극적으로 불쾌한 대상이었던 적도 없었다."], ["But now he was ill at ease about her.", "그러나 이제 그는 그녀를 두고 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["She troubled his peace.", "그녀가 그의 평온을 어지럽혔다."], ["He would have preferred to put her idea aside altogether, if he had known how.", "방법만 알았더라면 그는 차라리 그녀에 대한 생각 자체를 아예 치워 버리고 싶었으리라."], ["Perhaps—who shall decide on such mysteries!—he was afraid that he might come to hate her.", "어쩌면—이런 신비를 누가 판가름할 수 있으랴!—그는 자기가 그녀를 미워하게 될까 봐 두려웠는지도 모른다."]], [["When little Florence timidly presented herself, Mr Dombey stopped in his pacing up and down and looked towards her. Had he looked with greater interest and with a father’s eye, he might have read in her keen glance the impulses and fears that made her waver; the passionate desire to run clinging to him, crying, as she hid her face in his embrace, “Oh father, try to love me! there’s no one else!” the dread of a repulse; the fear of being too bold, and of offending him; the pitiable need in which she stood of some assurance and encouragement; and how her overcharged young heart was wandering to find some natural resting-place, for its sorrow and affection.", "어린 플로렌스가 머뭇머뭇 모습을 드러내자, 돔비 씨는 왔다 갔다 하던 걸음을 멈추고 그녀 쪽을 바라보았다. 만일 그가 더 큰 관심을 가지고, 아버지의 눈으로 바라보았더라면, 그녀의 그 간절한 눈길 속에서, 그녀를 망설이게 하는 충동과 두려움을 읽어 냈을지도 모른다. 그의 품에 얼굴을 묻으며 매달려, \"오 아버지, 절 사랑하려고 애써 주세요! 제겐 아무도 없어요!\" 하고 울부짖으며 그에게 달려가 매달리고 싶은 열렬한 갈망을. 퇴짜 맞을지 모른다는 두려움을. 너무 대담했다가 그의 기분을 상하게 할까 하는 겁을. 어떤 다짐과 격려가 절실히 필요한 그 가련한 처지를. 그리고 그녀의 벅찬 어린 가슴이 제 슬픔과 애정을 위해 어떤 자연스러운 안식처를 찾아 헤매고 있다는 것을."]], [["But he saw nothing of this.", "그러나 그는 이 가운데 아무것도 보지 못했다."], ["He saw her pause irresolutely at the door and look towards him; and he saw no more.", "그는 그녀가 문가에서 우유부단하게 멈춰 서서 자기 쪽을 바라보는 것을 보았다. 그리고 그 이상은 아무것도 보지 못했다."]], [["“Come in,”", "\"들어오너라,\""], ["he said, “come in: what is the child afraid of?”", "그가 말했다, \"들어와, 이 아이는 뭘 그리 무서워하는 거냐?\""]], [["She came in; and after glancing round her for a moment with an uncertain air, stood pressing her small hands hard together, close within the door.", "그녀가 들어왔다. 그러고는 잠시 막연한 표정으로 주위를 둘러본 뒤, 작은 두 손을 꼭 맞잡은 채, 문 바로 안쪽에 서 있었다."]], [["“Come here, Florence,”", "\"이리 오너라, 플로렌스,\""], ["said her father, coldly.", "아버지가 차갑게 말했다."], ["“Do you know who I am?”", "\"내가 누군지 아느냐?\""]], [["“Yes, Papa.”", "\"네, 아빠.\""]], [["“Have you nothing to say to me?”", "\"내게 할 말이 없느냐?\""]], [["The tears that stood in her eyes as she raised them quickly to his face, were frozen by the expression it wore.", "빠르게 그의 얼굴로 들어 올린 그녀의 눈에 고였던 눈물은, 그 얼굴이 띤 표정에 얼어붙고 말았다."], ["She looked down again, and put out her trembling hand.", "그녀는 다시 아래를 내려다보고, 떨리는 손을 내밀었다."]], [["Mr Dombey took it loosely in his own, and stood looking down upon her for a moment, as if he knew as little as the child, what to say or do.", "돔비 씨는 그것을 제 손에 헐겁게 잡고, 잠시 그녀를 내려다보고 서 있었다. 마치 무슨 말을 하거나 어떻게 해야 할지를 그 아이만큼이나 모르는 듯이."]], [["“There!", "\"자!"], ["Be a good girl,”", "착하게 굴어라,\""], ["he said, patting her on the head, and regarding her as it were by stealth with a disturbed and doubtful look.", "그가 그녀의 머리를 토닥이며, 어딘가 어수선하고 미심쩍은 눈길로 슬그머니 그녀를 살피듯 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Go to Richards!", "\"리처즈에게 가거라!"], ["Go!”", "가!\""]], [["His little daughter hesitated for another instant as though she would have clung about him still, or had some lingering hope that he might raise her in his arms and kiss her.", "그의 어린 딸은 한순간 더 머뭇거렸다. 마치 여전히 그에게 매달리고 싶기라도 한 듯, 또는 그가 자기를 두 팔로 안아 올려 입맞춰 주리라는 한 가닥 미련이 남아 있기라도 한 듯이."], ["She looked up in his face once more.", "그녀는 다시 한번 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."], ["He thought how like her expression was then, to what it had been when she looked round at the Doctor—that night—and instinctively dropped her hand and turned away.", "그는 그때 그녀의 표정이, 그날 밤 박사 쪽을 돌아보았을 때의 표정과 어찌나 닮았던가를 떠올렸고—저도 모르게 그녀의 손을 놓으며 돌아섰다."]], [["It was not difficult to perceive that Florence was at a great disadvantage in her father’s presence.", "플로렌스가 아버지 앞에서 몹시 불리한 처지에 있다는 것은 알아채기 어렵지 않았다."], ["It was not only a constraint upon the child’s mind, but even upon the natural grace and freedom of her actions.", "그것은 아이의 마음을 옥죌 뿐 아니라, 그녀의 동작이 지닌 타고난 우아함과 자유로움마저 옥죄었다."], ["As she sported and played about her baby brother that night, her manner was seldom so winning and so pretty as it naturally was, and sometimes when in his pacing to and fro, he came near her (she had, perhaps, for the moment, forgotten him) it changed upon the instant and became forced and embarrassed.", "그날 밤 어린 동생 곁에서 까불며 노는 동안에도, 그녀의 태도는 본래의 그토록 사랑스럽고 어여쁜 모습인 적이 드물었고, 이따금 그가 왔다 갔다 거닐다가 그녀 가까이 다가오면(아마 그 순간 그녀는 그를 잊고 있었으리라) 그녀의 태도는 순식간에 변하여 부자연스럽고 어색해졌다."]], [["Still, Polly persevered with all the better heart for seeing this; and, judging of Mr Dombey by herself, had great confidence in the mute appeal of poor little Florence’s mourning dress.", "그래도 폴리는 이것을 보며 한층 더 의욕을 내어 꾸준히 애썼다. 그리고 돔비 씨를 제 마음에 비추어 헤아리며, 가엾은 어린 플로렌스의 상복이 보내는 무언의 호소에 큰 기대를 걸었다."], ["“It’s hard indeed,”", "\"정말이지 야박한 일이지,\""], ["thought Polly, “if he takes only to one little motherless child, when he has another, and that a girl, before his eyes.”", "폴리는 생각했다, \"눈앞에 또 다른 어미 없는 아이가, 그것도 딸이 하나 더 있는데, 한 아이한테만 마음을 준다면.\""]], [["So, Polly kept her before his eyes, as long as she could, and managed so well with little Paul, as to make it very plain that he was all the livelier for his sister’s company.", "그래서 폴리는 될 수 있는 한 오래도록 그녀를 그의 눈앞에 두었고, 어린 폴을 어찌나 잘 다루었던지, 도련님이 누이가 곁에 있어서 한결 더 생기 넘친다는 것을 아주 분명하게 드러내 보였다."], ["When it was time to withdraw upstairs again, she would have sent Florence into the inner room to say good-night to her father, but the child was timid and drew back; and when she urged her again, said, spreading her hands before her eyes, as if to shut out her own unworthiness, “Oh no, no!", "다시 위층으로 물러갈 때가 되자, 그녀는 플로렌스를 안쪽 방으로 들여보내 아버지에게 잘 자라는 인사를 드리게 하려 했으나, 아이는 겁을 내며 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 그리고 그녀가 다시 권하자, 아이는 자신의 못남을 가려 버리려는 듯 두 손을 펴서 눈앞을 가리며 말했다, \"오 안 돼요, 안 돼요!"], ["He don’t want me.", "아빠는 절 원하지 않아요."], ["He don’t want me!”", "절 원하지 않아요!\""]], [["The little altercation between them had attracted the notice of Mr Dombey, who inquired from the table where he was sitting at his wine, what the matter was.", "둘 사이의 그 자그마한 실랑이가 돔비 씨의 주의를 끌었고, 포도주를 들며 앉아 있던 탁자에서 그는 무슨 일이냐고 물었다."]], [["“Miss Florence was afraid of interrupting, Sir, if she came in to say good-night,”", "\"플로렌스 아가씨가, 잘 자라는 인사를 드리러 들어오면 방해가 될까 봐 겁내셨어요, 나리,\""], ["said Richards.", "리처즈가 말했다."]], [["“It doesn’t matter,”", "\"상관없네,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대답했다."], ["“You can let her come and go without regarding me.”", "\"나는 신경 쓰지 말고 아가씨가 오가게 두게.\""]], [["The child shrunk as she listened—and was gone, before her humble friend looked round again.", "그 말을 들으며 아이는 움츠러들었다—그리고 그녀의 변변찮은 친구가 다시 돌아보았을 때는 이미 가 버린 뒤였다."]], [["However, Polly triumphed not a little in the success of her well-intentioned scheme, and in the address with which she had brought it to bear: whereof she made a full disclosure to Spitfire when she was once more safely entrenched upstairs.", "그러나 폴리는 자기의 선의에서 비롯된 계획이 성공한 것과, 그것을 이루어 낸 수완에 적잖이 우쭐했다. 그래서 다시 위층 안전한 진지에 자리 잡자, 그것을 스핏파이어에게 죄다 털어놓았다."], ["Miss Nipper received that proof of her confidence, as well as the prospect of their free association for the future, rather coldly, and was anything but enthusiastic in her demonstrations of joy.", "닙퍼 양은 그런 신뢰의 증표도, 또 앞으로 둘이 자유로이 어울리게 되리라는 전망도 다소 냉랭하게 받아들였고, 기쁨을 드러내는 데는 전혀 열의가 없었다."]], [["“I thought you would have been pleased,”", "\"좋아할 줄 알았는데,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“Oh yes, Mrs Richards, I’m very well pleased, thank you,”", "\"어머 그럼요, 리처즈 부인, 아주 좋아요, 고맙습니다,\""], ["returned Susan, who had suddenly become so very upright that she seemed to have put an additional bone in her stays.", "수전이 대답했다. 그녀는 갑자기 어찌나 꼿꼿해졌던지 코르셋에 뼈대를 하나 더 끼워 넣은 것 같았다."]], [["“You don’t show it,”", "\"그런 티가 안 나는데요,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["Being only a permanency I couldn’t be expected to show it like a temporary,”", "저야 한낱 붙박이일 뿐이니, 임시처럼 티를 내길 바랄 순 없죠,\""], ["said Susan Nipper.", "수전 닙퍼가 말했다."], ["“Temporaries carries it all before ’em here, I find, but though there’s a excellent party-wall between this house and the next, I mayn’t exactly like to go to it, Mrs Richards, notwithstanding!”", "\"여기선 임시들이 다 차지하더군요, 제가 보니까요, 하지만 이 집이랑 옆집 사이에 훌륭한 칸막이벽이 있다 해도, 그렇다고 제가 굳이 거길 넘어가고 싶은 건 아니에요, 리처즈 부인, 그래도 말이죠!\""]], [["CHAPTER IV. In which some more First Appearances are made on the Stage of these Adventures\nThough the offices of Dombey and Son were within the liberties of the City of London, and within hearing of Bow Bells, when their clashing voices were not drowned by the uproar in the streets, yet were there hints of adventurous and romantic story to be observed in some of the adjacent objects.", "돔비와 아들의 사무소는 런던 시(市)의 자치 구역 안에, 거리의 소란에 그 부딪치는 소리가 묻히지 않을 때면 보 교회의 종소리가 들리는 범위 안에 자리하고 있었지만, 그래도 그 근방의 어떤 사물들 속에는 모험과 낭만이 깃든 이야기의 암시가 엿보였다."], ["Gog and Magog held their state within ten minutes’ walk; the Royal Exchange was close at hand; the Bank of England, with its vaults of gold and silver “down among the dead men”", "고그와 마고그가 걸어서 십 분 거리 안에 위풍당당하게 서 있었고, 왕립 거래소가 바로 곁에 있었으며, 지하의 금고 속에 금과 은을 \"죽은 자들 틈에\""], ["underground, was their magnificent neighbour.", "묻어 둔 영국 은행이 그들의 으리으리한 이웃이었다."], ["Just round the corner stood the rich East India House, teeming with suggestions of precious stuffs and stones, tigers, elephants, howdahs, hookahs, umbrellas, palm trees, palanquins, and gorgeous princes of a brown complexion sitting on carpets, with their slippers very much turned up at the toes.", "모퉁이를 막 돌면 부유한 동인도 회사 건물이 서 있었는데, 귀한 천과 보석, 호랑이, 코끼리, 코끼리 등에 얹는 가마, 물담뱃대, 양산, 야자수, 가마, 그리고 양탄자 위에 발끝이 한껏 위로 말려 올라간 슬리퍼를 신고 앉아 있는 갈색 피부의 호화로운 왕자들에 관한 온갖 상상을 불러일으켰다."], ["Anywhere in the immediate vicinity there might be seen pictures of ships speeding away full sail to all parts of the world; outfitting warehouses ready to pack off anybody anywhere, fully equipped in half an hour; and little timber midshipmen in obsolete naval uniforms, eternally employed outside the shop doors of nautical Instrument-makers in taking observations of the hackney carriages.", "바로 그 근방 어디서든 돛을 활짝 펴고 세계 곳곳으로 질주해 가는 배 그림이며, 누구든 어디로든 반 시간 안에 완전한 채비를 갖춰 떠나보낼 준비가 된 출항 용품 창고며, 항해 기구 제작자의 가게 문 밖에서 한물간 해군 제복 차림으로 영원히 삯마차들을 관측하는 일에 매달려 있는 자그마한 나무 견습 사관 인형을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["Sole master and proprietor of one of these effigies—of that which might be called, familiarly, the woodenest—of that which thrust itself out above the pavement, right leg foremost, with a suavity the least endurable, and had the shoe buckles and flapped waistcoat the least reconcileable to human reason, and bore at its right eye the most offensively disproportionate piece of machinery—sole master and proprietor of that Midshipman, and proud of him too, an elderly gentleman in a Welsh wig had paid house-rent, taxes, rates, and dues, for more years than many a full-grown midshipman of flesh and blood has numbered in his life; and midshipmen who have attained a pretty green old age, have not been wanting in the English Navy.", "이 인형들 가운데 하나의—허물없이 말하자면 가장 나무토막 같다고 할 만한, 보도 위로 오른쪽 다리를 앞으로 내밀며 도무지 참아 줄 수 없는 상냥함으로 제 몸을 쑥 내밀고 있는, 그리고 인간의 이성과는 도무지 화해할 수 없는 구두 죔쇠와 날개처럼 펄럭이는 조끼를 달고 있으며, 오른쪽 눈에는 비위 상할 만큼 어울리지 않게 큰 기계 장치를 달고 있는—그 견습 사관 인형의 유일한 주인이자 소유주로서, 그를 자랑스러워하기까지 하는 한 노신사가 있었다. 그는 웨일스 가발을 쓴 채로, 살아 있는 다 자란 견습 사관이 평생 헤아린 햇수보다도 더 오랜 세월 동안 집세며 세금이며 부과금을 꼬박꼬박 치러 왔는데, 영국 해군에는 제법 노련한 고령에 이른 견습 사관도 없지 않았다."]], [["The stock-in-trade of this old gentleman comprised chronometers, barometers, telescopes, compasses, charts, maps, sextants, quadrants, and specimens of every kind of instrument used in the working of a ship’s course, or the keeping of a ship’s reckoning, or the prosecuting of a ship’s discoveries.", "이 노신사가 거래하는 물품에는 크로노미터, 기압계, 망원경, 나침반, 해도, 지도, 육분의, 사분의, 그리고 배의 항로를 잡거나 배의 위치를 계산하거나 배의 탐사를 수행하는 데 쓰이는 온갖 종류의 기구 견본이 들어 있었다."], ["Objects in brass and glass were in his drawers and on his shelves, which none but the initiated could have found the top of, or guessed the use of, or having once examined, could have ever got back again into their mahogany nests without assistance.", "그의 서랍과 선반에는 놋쇠와 유리로 된 물건들이 놓여 있었는데, 그것들은 통달한 사람이 아니고서는 어디가 위쪽인지 찾을 수도, 무슨 쓰임새인지 짐작할 수도 없었으며, 한번 꺼내어 살펴본 다음에는 도움 없이는 결코 그 마호가니 둥지로 도로 집어넣을 수도 없는 것들이었다."], ["Everything was jammed into the tightest cases, fitted into the narrowest corners, fenced up behind the most impertinent cushions, and screwed into the acutest angles, to prevent its philosophical composure from being disturbed by the rolling of the sea.", "모든 물건이 더없이 빠듯한 상자 속에 끼워지고, 가장 좁은 구석에 맞춰지고, 가장 주제넘은 쿠션 뒤에 둘러쳐지고, 가장 날카로운 각도로 비틀려 박혀 있었으니, 바다의 흔들림으로 그 철학적인 평정이 흐트러지지 않게 하려는 것이었다."], ["Such extraordinary precautions were taken in every instance to save room, and keep the thing compact; and so much practical navigation was fitted, and cushioned, and screwed into every box (whether the box was a mere slab, as some were, or something between a cocked hat and a star-fish, as others were, and those quite mild and modest boxes as compared with others); that the shop itself, partaking of the general infection, seemed almost to become a snug, sea-going, ship-shape concern, wanting only good sea-room, in the event of an unexpected launch, to work its way securely to any desert island in the world.", "자리를 아끼고 물건을 빈틈없이 챙기려고 어느 것에나 그토록 비상한 주의가 기울여졌으며, 어찌나 많은 실용 항해술이 (어떤 것은 그저 평평한 판 모양이었고, 어떤 것은 삼각모와 불가사리의 중간쯤 되는 모양이었는데, 그것들조차 다른 것들에 비하면 아주 점잖고 얌전한 상자였다) 상자마다 빠짐없이 맞춰지고 쿠션에 싸이고 비틀려 박혀 있었던지, 그 가게 자체가 그 보편적인 전염에 물든 듯 거의 아늑하고 항해에 알맞은 배 모양의 살림처럼 보였으니, 뜻밖에 진수하게 되는 경우 넉넉한 바다 공간만 있다면 세계 어느 무인도까지든 무사히 헤쳐 나갈 수 있을 것만 같았다."]], [["Many minor incidents in the household life of the Ships’ Instrument-maker who was proud of his little Midshipman, assisted and bore out this fancy.", "자기의 자그마한 견습 사관 인형을 자랑스러워하던 그 선박 기구 제작자의 가정생활에서 일어난 자잘한 사건들이 이 공상을 거들고 뒷받침해 주었다."], ["His acquaintance lying chiefly among ship-chandlers and so forth, he had always plenty of the veritable ships’ biscuit on his table.", "그의 교제는 주로 선구상(船具商) 따위들 사이에 이루어진 터라, 그의 식탁에는 늘 진짜 선박용 비스킷이 넉넉히 놓여 있었다."], ["It was familiar with dried meats and tongues, possessing an extraordinary flavour of rope yarn.", "그것은 밧줄 가닥 같은 묘한 풍미를 지닌 말린 고기와 우설(牛舌)에 익숙해 있었다."], ["Pickles were produced upon it, in great wholesale jars, with “dealer in all kinds of Ships’ Provisions” on the label; spirits were set forth in case bottles with no throats.", "식탁 위에는 \"온갖 종류의 선박용 식료품 거래상\"이라는 상표가 붙은 커다란 도매용 항아리에 든 절임이 차려졌고, 술은 목이 없는 사각 병에 담겨 나왔다."], ["Old prints of ships with alphabetical references to their various mysteries, hung in frames upon the walls; the Tartar Frigate under weigh, was on the plates; outlandish shells, seaweeds, and mosses, decorated the chimney-piece; the little wainscotted back parlour was lighted by a sky-light, like a cabin.", "벽에는 갖가지 신비한 부분에 알파벳 표시를 붙인 옛 배 그림들이 액자에 걸려 있었으며, 접시에는 출항 중인 타타르 프리깃함이 그려져 있었고, 벽난로 선반은 진기한 조개껍데기와 해초와 이끼로 장식되어 있었으며, 판자를 두른 자그마한 뒷거실은 선실처럼 천장 채광창으로 빛이 들었다."]], [["Here he lived too, in skipper-like state, all alone with his nephew Walter: a boy of fourteen who looked quite enough like a midshipman, to carry out the prevailing idea.", "여기서 그는 또한 선장 같은 위세로, 조카 월터와 단둘이서만 살았다. 월터는 열네 살 소년으로, 그 흔한 인상을 뒷받침할 만큼 견습 사관과 꽤나 닮아 보였다."], ["But there it ended, for Solomon Gills himself (more generally called old Sol) was far from having a maritime appearance.", "그러나 닮은 것은 거기까지였으니, 솔로몬 길스 자신은 (대개 늙은 솔이라 불렸다) 바닷사람다운 풍모와는 거리가 멀었다."], ["To say nothing of his Welsh wig, which was as plain and stubborn a Welsh wig as ever was worn, and in which he looked like anything but a Rover, he was a slow, quiet-spoken, thoughtful old fellow, with eyes as red as if they had been small suns looking at you through a fog; and a newly-awakened manner, such as he might have acquired by having stared for three or four days successively through every optical instrument in his shop, and suddenly came back to the world again, to find it green.", "일찍이 누가 썼던 것 중에서도 가장 수수하고 고집스러운 웨일스 가발이라 할 만한 그 가발—그것을 쓰면 그는 도무지 바다의 떠돌이로는 보이지 않았다—은 말할 것도 없거니와, 그는 안개를 뚫고 그대를 들여다보는 작은 두 개의 태양처럼 새빨간 눈을 한, 굼뜨고 말수 적고 생각 많은 노인이었다. 마치 사나흘을 연거푸 자기 가게의 모든 광학 기구를 들여다보다가 갑자기 세상으로 되돌아와 그것이 푸르른 것을 발견하기라도 한 듯한, 막 잠에서 깬 듯한 태도가 있었다."], ["The only change ever known in his outward man, was from a complete suit of coffee-colour cut very square, and ornamented with glaring buttons, to the same suit of coffee-colour minus the inexpressibles, which were then of a pale nankeen.", "그의 겉모습에서 일어난 유일한 변화라고 알려진 것은, 매우 각지게 재단되고 번쩍이는 단추로 장식된 커피색 정장 한 벌에서, 바지만 옅은 황색 무명천으로 바뀐 똑같은 커피색 정장으로 바뀐 것뿐이었다."], ["He wore a very precise shirt-frill, and carried a pair of first-rate spectacles on his forehead, and a tremendous chronometer in his fob, rather than doubt which precious possession, he would have believed in a conspiracy against it on part of all the clocks and watches in the City, and even of the very Sun itself.", "그는 매우 단정한 셔츠 주름 장식을 달았고, 이마에는 일급 안경 한 벌을 얹었으며, 회중시계 주머니에는 엄청난 크로노미터를 지니고 다녔는데, 그 귀중한 소유물을 의심하느니 차라리 시내의 온갖 괘종시계며 회중시계가, 그리고 심지어 태양 그 자체까지도 그것에 맞서 음모를 꾸미고 있다고 믿어 버릴 그런 사람이었다."], ["Such as he was, such he had been in the shop and parlour behind the little Midshipman, for years upon years; going regularly aloft to bed every night in a howling garret remote from the lodgers, where, when gentlemen of England who lived below at ease had little or no idea of the state of the weather, it often blew great guns.", "그는 이런 사람이었고, 또 이러한 모습으로 그 자그마한 견습 사관 인형 뒤편의 가게와 거실에서 여러 해 동안 지내 왔으며, 밤마다 어김없이 세입자들과는 멀리 떨어진 바람 윙윙대는 다락방으로 올라가 잠을 잤다. 그 다락방에서는, 아래층에서 편안히 사는 영국 신사들이 날씨가 어떤지 거의 짐작도 못 할 때에도, 흔히 바람이 대포처럼 거세게 몰아쳤다."]], [["It is half-past five o’clock, and an autumn afternoon, when the reader and Solomon Gills become acquainted.", "독자와 솔로몬 길스가 알게 되는 때는 어느 가을날 오후 다섯 시 반이다."], ["Solomon Gills is in the act of seeing what time it is by the unimpeachable chronometer.", "솔로몬 길스는 그 흠잡을 데 없는 크로노미터로 몇 시인지 보고 있는 참이다."], ["The usual daily clearance has been making in the City for an hour or more; and the human tide is still rolling westward.", "시내에서는 으레 있는 하루의 퇴근 행렬이 한 시간 남짓 이어져 왔고, 인파는 여전히 서쪽으로 밀려가고 있다."], ["“The streets have thinned,”", "\"거리가 많이 한산해졌군.\""], ["as Mr Gills says, “very much.”", "길스 씨가 말하는 대로다."], ["It threatens to be wet tonight.", "오늘 밤은 비가 올 듯하다."], ["All the weatherglasses in the shop are in low spirits, and the rain already shines upon the cocked hat of the wooden Midshipman.", "가게의 모든 기압계가 풀이 죽어 있고, 빗방울이 벌써 나무 견습 사관의 삼각모 위에서 반짝인다."]], [["“Where’s Walter, I wonder!”", "\"월터가 어디 있는지 모르겠군!\""], ["said Solomon Gills, after he had carefully put up the chronometer again.", "솔로몬 길스가 크로노미터를 다시 조심스레 넣어 두고는 말했다."], ["“Here’s dinner been ready, half an hour, and no Walter!”", "\"저녁이 차려진 지 반 시간이나 됐는데, 월터는 코빼기도 안 보이니!\""]], [["Turning round upon his stool behind the counter, Mr Gills looked out among the instruments in the window, to see if his nephew might be crossing the road.", "계산대 뒤 걸상 위에서 몸을 돌려, 길스 씨는 진열창의 기구들 너머로 내다보며 조카가 길을 건너오는 중은 아닌지 살폈다. 아니었다."], ["No. He was not among the bobbing umbrellas, and he certainly was not the newspaper boy in the oilskin cap who was slowly working his way along the piece of brass outside, writing his name over Mr Gills’s name with his forefinger.", "그는 흔들거리는 우산들 사이에도 없었고, 가게 밖 놋쇠판을 따라 천천히 나아가며 집게손가락으로 길스 씨의 이름 위에 제 이름을 쓰고 있는, 기름먹인 모자를 쓴 신문팔이 소년도 분명 아니었다."]], [["“If I didn’t know he was too fond of me to make a run of it, and go and enter himself aboard ship against my wishes, I should begin to be fidgetty,”", "\"저 녀석이 날 너무 좋아해서 줄행랑을 치거나 내 뜻을 거슬러 배에 올라 입대해 버리지는 않으리라는 걸 몰랐다면, 안절부절못하기 시작했을 텐데.\""], ["said Mr Gills, tapping two or three weather-glasses with his knuckles.", "길스 씨가 손가락 마디로 기압계 두세 개를 톡톡 두드리며 말했다."], ["“I really should.", "\"정말 그랬을 거야."], ["All in the Downs, eh!", "다운스 해역 일대가 온통!"], ["Lots of moisture!", "습기가 잔뜩이야!"], ["Well!", "뭐!"], ["it’s wanted.”", "필요한 거지.\""]], [["“I believe,”", "\"내 생각에는,\""], ["said Mr Gills, blowing the dust off the glass top of a compass-case, “that you don’t point more direct and due to the back parlour than the boy’s inclination does after all.", "길스 씨가 나침반 함의 유리 뚜껑에서 먼지를 후 불며 말했다, \"결국 저 녀석의 마음이 뒷거실 쪽을 가리키는 것만큼 너도 그렇게 똑바로 정확하게 가리키지는 못할 게다."], ["And the parlour couldn’t bear straighter either.", "그리고 거실 또한 그보다 더 곧게 향할 수는 없지."], ["Due north.", "정북이야."], ["Not the twentieth part of a point either way.”", "어느 쪽으로도 한 점의 이십분의 일도 빗나가지 않는다고.\""]], [["“Halloa, Uncle Sol!”", "\"안녕하세요, 솔 삼촌!\""]], [["“Halloa, my boy!”", "\"오, 어서 오너라, 얘야!\""], ["cried the Instrument-maker, turning briskly round.", "기구 제작자가 활기차게 몸을 돌리며 외쳤다."], ["“What!", "\"아니!"], ["you are here, are you?”", "너 와 있었구나, 그렇지?\""]], [["A cheerful looking, merry boy, fresh with running home in the rain; fair-faced, bright-eyed, and curly-haired.", "쾌활해 보이는 명랑한 소년이었다. 빗속을 달려온 터라 생기가 돌았고, 얼굴이 희고, 눈이 반짝이며, 머리가 곱슬곱슬했다."]], [["“Well, Uncle, how have you got on without me all day?", "\"자, 삼촌, 하루 종일 저 없이 어떻게 지내셨어요?"], ["Is dinner ready?", "저녁 다 됐어요?"], ["I’m so hungry.”", "배가 무척 고파요.\""]], [["“As to getting on,”", "\"지내는 거로 말하자면,\""], ["said Solomon good-naturedly, “it would be odd if I couldn’t get on without a young dog like you a great deal better than with you.", "솔로몬이 사람 좋게 말했다, \"너 같은 강아지 녀석이 없어도, 있을 때보다 훨씬 잘 지내지 못한다면 그게 더 이상한 일이지."], ["As to dinner being ready, it’s been ready this half hour and waiting for you.", "저녁이 다 됐냐 하면, 반 시간 전부터 다 돼서 너를 기다리고 있었다."], ["As to being hungry, I am!”", "배가 고프냐 하면, 나야말로 고프단다!\""]], [["“Come along then, Uncle!”", "\"그럼 어서 가요, 삼촌!\""], ["cried the boy.", "소년이 외쳤다."], ["“Hurrah for the admiral!”", "\"제독 만세!\""]], [["“Confound the admiral!”", "\"제독은 무슨!\""], ["returned Solomon Gills.", "솔로몬 길스가 응수했다."], ["“You mean the Lord Mayor.”", "\"시장님을 말하는 거겠지.\""]], [["“No I don’t!”", "\"아니에요!\""], ["cried the boy.", "소년이 외쳤다."], ["“Hurrah for the admiral!", "\"제독 만세!"], ["Hurrah for the admiral!", "제독 만세!"], ["For-ward!”", "앞으로—옷!\""]], [["At this word of command, the Welsh wig and its wearer were borne without resistance into the back parlour, as at the head of a boarding party of five hundred men; and Uncle Sol and his nephew were speedily engaged on a fried sole with a prospect of steak to follow.", "이 호령 한마디에, 웨일스 가발과 그 주인은, 마치 오백 명의 적함 돌격대 선두에 서기라도 한 듯, 저항할 새도 없이 뒷거실로 떠밀려 갔다. 그리하여 솔 삼촌과 조카는 곧 튀긴 가자미 요리에 몰두하게 되었고, 뒤이어 스테이크가 나올 전망이었다."]], [["“The Lord Mayor, Wally,”", "\"시장님 말이다, 월리,\""], ["said Solomon, “for ever!", "솔로몬이 말했다, \"영원하시기를!"], ["No more admirals.", "제독은 이제 그만."], ["The Lord Mayor’s your admiral.”", "시장님이 네 제독이시다.\""]], [["“Oh, is he though!”", "\"오, 정말 그래요?\""], ["said the boy, shaking his head.", "소년이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."], ["“Why, the Sword Bearer’s better than him.", "\"그래도 검(劍) 의장(儀仗)관이 그분보다 나아요."], ["He draws his sword sometimes.”", "그분은 가끔 검을 뽑잖아요.\""]], [["“And a pretty figure he cuts with it for his pains,”", "\"그러느라 애써 봤자 꼴이 우습기만 하지.\""], ["returned the Uncle.", "삼촌이 응수했다."], ["“Listen to me, Wally, listen to me.", "\"내 말 좀 들어 봐라, 월리, 들어 봐."], ["Look on the mantelshelf.”", "저 벽난로 선반을 봐.\""]], [["“Why who has cocked my silver mug up there, on a nail?”", "\"아니 누가 제 은잔을 저 위에다, 못에다 걸어 놨어요?\""], ["exclaimed the boy.", "소년이 소리쳤다."]], [["“I have,”", "\"내가 그랬다.\""], ["said his Uncle.", "삼촌이 말했다."], ["“No more mugs now.", "\"이제 잔(머그)은 그만이야."], ["We must begin to drink out of glasses today, Walter.", "오늘부터 우리는 유리잔으로 마셔야 한다, 월터."], ["We are men of business.", "우리는 사업하는 사람이야."], ["We belong to the City.", "시(市)에 속한 사람들이고."], ["We started in life this morning.”", "우리는 오늘 아침 인생의 첫발을 내디뎠지.\""]], [["“Well, Uncle,”", "\"좋아요, 삼촌.\""], ["said the boy, “I’ll drink out of anything you like, so long as I can drink to you.", "소년이 말했다. \"삼촌을 위해 마실 수만 있다면, 무엇으로든 마시겠어요."], ["Here’s to you, Uncle Sol, and Hurrah for the—”", "삼촌을 위하여, 솔 삼촌, 그리고—만세—\""]], [["“Lord Mayor,”", "\"시장님.\""], ["interrupted the old man.", "노인이 끼어들었다."]], [["“For the Lord Mayor, Sheriffs, Common Council, and Livery,”", "\"시장님과, 주(州) 장관님들과, 시 참사회와, 동업조합을 위하여.\""], ["said the boy.", "소년이 말했다."], ["“Long life to ’em!”", "\"그분들 만수무강을!\""]], [["The uncle nodded his head with great satisfaction.", "삼촌은 매우 흡족해하며 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“And now,”", "\"자, 이제,\""], ["he said, “let’s hear something about the Firm.”", "그가 말했다, \"그 회사 이야기를 좀 들어 보자.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["there’s not much to be told about the Firm, Uncle,”", "회사에 대해서는 별로 할 얘기가 없어요, 삼촌.\""], ["said the boy, plying his knife and fork.", "소년이 나이프와 포크를 놀리며 말했다. \"엄청나게 어두침침한 사무소예요."], ["“It’s a precious dark set of offices, and in the room where I sit, there’s a high fender, and an iron safe, and some cards about ships that are going to sail, and an almanack, and some desks and stools, and an inkbottle, and some books, and some boxes, and a lot of cobwebs, and in one of ’em, just over my head, a shrivelled-up blue-bottle that looks as if it had hung there ever so long.”", "그리고 제가 앉아 있는 방에는 높은 난로 가리개가 있고, 철제 금고가 있고, 출항할 배들에 관한 카드가 좀 붙어 있고, 달력이 있고, 책상과 걸상이 몇 개 있고, 잉크병이 하나 있고, 책이 좀 있고, 상자가 몇 개 있고, 거미줄이 잔뜩 쳐져 있어요. 그리고 그중 하나에는, 바로 제 머리 위에, 쪼그라든 쉬파리가 한 마리 걸려 있는데, 아주 오래전부터 거기 매달려 있던 것처럼 보여요.\""]], [["“Nothing else?”", "\"그것뿐이냐?\""], ["said the Uncle.", "삼촌이 말했다."]], [["“No, nothing else, except an old birdcage (I wonder how that ever came there!) and a coal-scuttle.”", "\"네, 그것뿐이에요. 낡은 새장 하나만 빼고요 (그게 어떻게 거기 들어왔는지 모르겠어요!) 그리고 석탄통 하나도요.\""]], [["“No bankers’ books, or cheque books, or bills, or such tokens of wealth rolling in from day to day?”", "\"날마다 굴러 들어오는 부의 징표 같은, 은행 장부나 수표책이나 어음 같은 건 없더냐?\""], ["said old Sol, looking wistfully at his nephew out of the fog that always seemed to hang about him, and laying an unctuous emphasis upon the words.", "늙은 솔이, 늘 자기 주위에 드리워진 듯한 그 안개 너머로 조카를 그윽이 바라보며, 그 말들에 진하게 힘을 주어 물었다."]], [["“Oh yes, plenty of that I suppose,”", "\"오, 네, 그런 건 잔뜩 있겠죠, 아마.\""], ["returned his nephew carelessly; “but all that sort of thing’s in Mr Carker’s room, or Mr Morfin’s, or Mr Dombey’s.”", "조카가 무심하게 응수했다. \"하지만 그런 것들은 죄다 카커 씨 방이나, 모핀 씨 방이나, 돔비 씨 방에 있어요.\""]], [["“Has Mr Dombey been there today?”", "\"오늘 돔비 씨가 거기 왔더냐?\""], ["inquired the Uncle.", "삼촌이 물었다."]], [["“Oh yes!", "\"오, 네!"], ["In and out all day.”", "하루 종일 들락날락하셨어요.\""]], [["“He didn’t take any notice of you, I suppose?”.", "\"너한테는 별로 눈길을 주지 않았겠지, 아마?\""]], [["“Yes he did.", "\"아뇨, 주셨어요."], ["He walked up to my seat,—I wish he wasn’t so solemn and stiff, Uncle,—and said, ‘Oh!", "제 자리로 걸어오셔서—그분이 그렇게 엄숙하고 뻣뻣하지만 않으셨으면 좋겠어요, 삼촌—이러시더라고요. '오!"], ["you are the son of Mr Gills the Ships’ Instrument-maker.’ ‘Nephew, Sir,’ I said.", "네가 선박 기구 제작자 길스 씨의 아들이로구나.' '조카입니다, 나리.'"], ["‘I said nephew, boy,’ said he.", "제가 말했죠. '내가 조카라고 했다, 얘야.'"], ["But I could take my oath he said son, Uncle.”", "그분이 그러셨어요. 하지만 그분이 아들이라고 하셨다는 데 제 목을 걸 수 있어요, 삼촌.\""]], [["“You’re mistaken I daresay.", "\"네가 잘못 들은 모양이다."], ["It’s no matter.”", "그런 건 아무래도 좋아.\""]], [["“No, it’s no matter, but he needn’t have been so sharp, I thought.", "\"네, 아무래도 좋지만, 그렇게 쌀쌀맞게 안 하셔도 됐을 텐데 싶었어요."], ["There was no harm in it though he did say son.", "아들이라고 하셨다 해도 해될 게 없잖아요."], ["Then he told me that you had spoken to him about me, and that he had found me employment in the House accordingly, and that I was expected to be attentive and punctual, and then he went away.", "그러고 나서 삼촌이 저에 관해 그분께 말씀하셨고, 그래서 그분이 회사에 제 일자리를 마련해 주셨다고, 그리고 제가 성실하고 시간을 잘 지킬 것을 기대한다고 하시고는, 가 버리셨어요."], ["I thought he didn’t seem to like me much.”", "그분은 절 별로 좋아하시지 않는 것 같았어요.\""]], [["“You mean, I suppose,”", "\"내 짐작에 네 말은,\""], ["observed the Instrument-maker, “that you didn’t seem to like him much?”", "기구 제작자가 말했다, \"네가 그분을 별로 좋아하지 않는 것처럼 보였다는 뜻이겠지?\""]], [["“Well, Uncle,”", "\"글쎄요, 삼촌.\""], ["returned the boy, laughing.", "소년이 웃으며 응수했다."], ["“Perhaps so; I never thought of that.”", "\"아마 그럴지도요. 그건 미처 생각 못 했네요.\""]], [["Solomon looked a little graver as he finished his dinner, and glanced from time to time at the boy’s bright face.", "솔로몬은 저녁을 마치면서 조금 더 진지한 표정이 되어, 이따금 소년의 환한 얼굴을 흘끗거렸다."], ["When dinner was done, and the cloth was cleared away (the entertainment had been brought from a neighbouring eating-house), he lighted a candle, and went down below into a little cellar, while his nephew, standing on the mouldy staircase, dutifully held the light.", "저녁이 끝나고 식탁보가 치워지자 (음식은 이웃 식당에서 가져온 것이었다), 그는 촛불을 켜 들고 자그마한 지하 저장고로 내려갔고, 그동안 조카는 곰팡내 나는 계단에 서서 충실히 불을 비춰 주었다."], ["After a moment’s groping here and there, he presently returned with a very ancient-looking bottle, covered with dust and dirt.", "여기저기 잠시 더듬은 끝에, 그는 곧 먼지와 때를 뒤집어쓴 아주 오래되어 보이는 병 하나를 들고 돌아왔다."]], [["“Why, Uncle Sol!”", "\"아니, 솔 삼촌!\""], ["said the boy, “what are you about?", "소년이 말했다, \"뭐 하시는 거예요?"], ["that’s the wonderful Madeira!—there’s only one more bottle!”", "그건 그 굉장한 마데이라잖아요!—이제 한 병밖에 안 남았는데!\""]], [["Uncle Sol nodded his head, implying that he knew very well what he was about; and having drawn the cork in solemn silence, filled two glasses and set the bottle and a third clean glass on the table.", "솔 삼촌은 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 아주 잘 안다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러고는 엄숙한 침묵 속에 코르크 마개를 뽑아, 잔 두 개를 채우고 병과 깨끗한 세 번째 잔을 식탁에 놓았다."]], [["“You shall drink the other bottle, Wally,”", "\"나머지 한 병은 네가 마시거라, 월리.\""], ["he said, “when you come to good fortune; when you are a thriving, respected, happy man; when the start in life you have made today shall have brought you, as I pray Heaven it may!—to a smooth part of the course you have to run, my child.", "그가 말했다, \"네게 좋은 운이 찾아왔을 때, 네가 번창하고 존경받는 행복한 사람이 되었을 때, 네가 오늘 내디딘 인생의 첫발이 너를—하늘이 그러기를 비노라!—네가 달려야 할 경주에서 평탄한 구간으로 데려다주었을 때 말이다, 얘야."], ["My love to you!”", "너를 위하여 마시마!\""]], [["Some of the fog that hung about old Sol seemed to have got into his throat; for he spoke huskily.", "늙은 솔을 둘러싸고 있던 안개의 일부가 그의 목구멍으로 들어가 버린 듯, 그는 쉰 목소리로 말했다."], ["His hand shook too, as he clinked his glass against his nephew’s.", "조카의 잔에 제 잔을 부딪칠 때 그의 손도 떨렸다."], ["But having once got the wine to his lips, he tossed it off like a man, and smacked them afterwards.", "그러나 일단 술을 입에 대자, 그는 사내답게 단숨에 들이켜고는 입맛을 쩝쩝 다셨다."]], [["“Dear Uncle,”", "\"사랑하는 삼촌,\""], ["said the boy, affecting to make light of it, while the tears stood in his eyes, “for the honour you have done me, et cetera, et cetera.", "소년이 눈에 눈물이 고인 채, 짐짓 대수롭지 않은 척하며 말했다, \"저에게 베풀어 주신 영광에 대하여, 기타 등등, 기타 등등."], ["I shall now beg to propose Mr Solomon Gills with three times three and one cheer more.", "이제 저는 외람되이 솔로몬 길스 씨를 위하여 세 번씩 세 번 그리고 한 번 더 환호할 것을 제안합니다."], ["Hurrah!", "만세!"], ["and you’ll return thanks, Uncle, when we drink the last bottle together; won’t you?”", "그리고 삼촌도 답례 인사를 해 주셔야 해요, 우리가 마지막 병을 함께 마실 때 말이에요. 그러실 거죠?\""]], [["They clinked their glasses again; and Walter, who was hoarding his wine, took a sip of it, and held the glass up to his eye with as critical an air as he could possibly assume.", "두 사람은 다시 잔을 부딪쳤고, 술을 아껴 두려던 월터는 한 모금 홀짝이고는, 자기가 지을 수 있는 최대한 까다로운 태도로 잔을 눈높이까지 들어 올렸다."]], [["His Uncle sat looking at him for some time in silence.", "삼촌은 한동안 말없이 그를 바라보고 앉아 있었다."], ["When their eyes at last met, he began at once to pursue the theme that had occupied his thoughts, aloud, as if he had been speaking all the time.", "마침내 두 사람의 눈이 마주치자, 그는 곧바로, 마치 내내 말해 오던 것처럼, 소리 내어 자기 머릿속을 차지하고 있던 화제를 이어 갔다."]], [["“You see, Walter,”", "\"보아라, 월터,\""], ["he said, “in truth this business is merely a habit with me.", "그가 말했다, \"사실 이 장사는 나한테는 그저 습관일 뿐이란다."], ["I am so accustomed to the habit that I could hardly live if I relinquished it: but there’s nothing doing, nothing doing.", "이 습관에 어찌나 익숙해졌는지, 그걸 놓아 버리면 살아가기조차 힘들 정도지. 하지만 되는 일이 없어, 되는 일이 없다고."], ["When that uniform was worn,”", "저 제복이 입혀지던 시절에는,\""], ["pointing out towards the little Midshipman, “then indeed, fortunes were to be made, and were made.", "자그마한 견습 사관 인형 쪽을 가리키며, \"그땐 정말이지 한밑천 잡을 수 있었고, 또 잡았지."], ["But competition, competition—new invention, new invention—alteration, alteration—the world’s gone past me.", "하지만 경쟁, 경쟁—새로운 발명, 새로운 발명—변화, 변화—세상이 날 앞질러 가 버렸어."], ["I hardly know where I am myself, much less where my customers are.”", "나는 내가 어디 있는지조차 거의 모르겠으니, 내 손님들이 어디 있는지는 더더욱 모르지.\""]], [["“Never mind ’em, Uncle!”", "\"손님들 따위 신경 쓰지 마세요, 삼촌!\""]], [["“Since you came home from weekly boarding-school at Peckham, for instance—and that’s ten days,”", "\"이를테면 네가 페컴의 주(週) 단위 기숙학교에서 집에 돌아온 뒤로—그게 열흘 됐는데,\""], ["said Solomon, “I don’t remember more than one person that has come into the shop.”", "솔로몬이 말했다, \"가게에 들어온 사람이라곤 한 사람밖에 기억이 안 나는구나.\""]], [["“Two, Uncle, don’t you recollect?", "\"두 사람이에요, 삼촌, 기억 안 나세요?"], ["There was the man who came to ask for change for a sovereign—”", "1소버린을 잔돈으로 바꿔 달라고 물으러 온 남자가 있었잖아요—\""]], [["“That’s the one,”", "\"그게 그 한 사람이지.\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."]], [["“Why Uncle!", "\"아니 삼촌!"], ["don’t you call the woman anybody, who came to ask the way to Mile-End Turnpike?”", "마일엔드 통행료 징수소로 가는 길을 물으러 온 여자는 사람으로 안 치세요?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["it’s true,”", "맞다.\""], ["said Solomon, “I forgot her.", "솔로몬이 말했다, \"그 여자를 깜박했구나."], ["Two persons.”", "두 사람이다.\""]], [["“To be sure, they didn’t buy anything,”", "\"하긴 그 사람들은 아무것도 안 샀죠.\""], ["cried the boy.", "소년이 외쳤다."]], [["“No.", "\"그래."], ["They didn’t buy anything,”", "아무것도 안 샀지.\""], ["said Solomon, quietly.", "솔로몬이 조용히 말했다."]], [["“Nor want anything,”", "\"뭘 원하지도 않았고요.\""], ["cried the boy.", "소년이 외쳤다."]], [["“No.", "\"그래."], ["If they had, they’d gone to another shop,”", "원했다면 다른 가게로 갔을 테지.\""], ["said Solomon, in the same tone.", "솔로몬이 같은 어조로 말했다."]], [["“But there were two of ’em, Uncle,”", "\"하지만 두 사람이었어요, 삼촌.\""], ["cried the boy, as if that were a great triumph.", "소년이, 마치 그것이 대단한 승리라도 되는 양 외쳤다."], ["“You said only one.”", "\"삼촌은 한 사람뿐이라고 하셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Well, Wally,”", "\"그래, 월리,\""], ["resumed the old man, after a short pause: “not being like the Savages who came on Robinson Crusoe’s Island, we can’t live on a man who asks for change for a sovereign, and a woman who inquires the way to Mile-End Turnpike.", "노인이 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 다시 말했다. \"우리는 로빈슨 크루소의 섬에 찾아온 야만인들과는 다르니, 1소버린을 잔돈으로 바꿔 달라는 남자와 마일엔드 통행료 징수소 가는 길을 묻는 여자에 기대어 살 수는 없지."], ["As I said just now, the world has gone past me.", "방금 말했듯이, 세상이 나를 앞질러 가 버렸어."], ["I don’t blame it; but I no longer understand it.", "세상을 탓하지는 않아. 하지만 나는 더 이상 세상을 이해하지 못한다."], ["Tradesmen are not the same as they used to be, apprentices are not the same, business is not the same, business commodities are not the same.", "상인들도 예전 같지 않고, 견습공들도 예전 같지 않고, 장사도 예전 같지 않고, 장사하는 물건들도 예전 같지 않아."], ["Seven-eighths of my stock is old-fashioned.", "내 재고의 팔분의 칠은 구식이지."], ["I am an old-fashioned man in an old-fashioned shop, in a street that is not the same as I remember it.", "나는 구식 거리에서—내가 기억하는 그 거리와는 같지 않은 거리에서—구식 가게를 하는 구식 사람이야."], ["I have fallen behind the time, and am too old to catch it again.", "나는 시대에 뒤처졌고, 다시 따라잡기엔 너무 늙었어."], ["Even the noise it makes a long way ahead, confuses me.”", "저 멀리 앞에서 시대가 내는 소리조차 나를 어리둥절하게 만든단다.\""]], [["Walter was going to speak, but his Uncle held up his hand.", "월터가 무어라 말하려 했지만, 삼촌이 손을 들어 막았다."]], [["“Therefore, Wally—therefore it is that I am anxious you should be early in the busy world, and on the world’s track.", "\"그래서, 월리—그래서 나는 네가 일찌감치 분주한 세상에 들어가, 세상의 궤도에 오르기를 바라는 거란다."], ["I am only the ghost of this business—its substance vanished long ago; and when I die, its ghost will be laid.", "나는 이 장사의 유령일 뿐이야—그 알맹이는 오래전에 사라져 버렸지. 그리고 내가 죽으면 그 유령도 잠들 게다."], ["As it is clearly no inheritance for you then, I have thought it best to use for your advantage, almost the only fragment of the old connexion that stands by me, through long habit.", "그러니 이게 네게 물려줄 유산이 못 됨이 명백하니, 나는 오랜 습관 덕에 그나마 내게 남아 있는, 옛 인연의 거의 유일한 조각을 너를 위해 쓰는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 생각했단다."], ["Some people suppose me to be wealthy.", "어떤 이들은 나를 부유하다고 짐작하지."], ["I wish for your sake they were right.", "너를 위해서라면 그들 말이 맞기를 바란다."], ["But whatever I leave behind me, or whatever I can give you, you in such a House as Dombey’s are in the road to use well and make the most of.", "하지만 내가 무엇을 남기든, 또 무엇을 네게 줄 수 있든, 너는 돔비 씨네 같은 회사에서 그것을 잘 활용하고 최대한으로 살릴 길에 들어선 거야."], ["Be diligent, try to like it, my dear boy, work for a steady independence, and be happy!”", "부지런히 일하고, 그 일을 좋아하려고 애쓰고, 안정된 자립을 위해 일하고, 그리고 행복하거라, 얘야!\""]], [["“I’ll do everything I can, Uncle, to deserve your affection.", "\"삼촌의 애정에 보답할 수 있도록 제가 할 수 있는 건 다 하겠어요."], ["Indeed I will,”", "정말로요.\""], ["said the boy, earnestly.", "소년이 간절하게 말했다."]], [["“I know it,”", "\"안다.\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."], ["“I am sure of it,”", "\"확신한다.\""], ["and he applied himself to a second glass of the old Madeira, with increased relish.", "그러고는 더욱 흡족한 마음으로 그 오래된 마데이라 두 잔째에 매달렸다."], ["“As to the Sea,”", "\"바다로 말하자면,\""], ["he pursued, “that’s well enough in fiction, Wally, but it won’t do in fact: it won’t do at all.", "그가 말을 이었다, \"그건 소설 속에서야 그럴듯하지, 월리, 하지만 현실에서는 안 될 일이야."], ["It’s natural enough that you should think about it, associating it with all these familiar things; but it won’t do, it won’t do.”", "도무지 안 될 일이지. 네가 그걸 이 모든 친숙한 물건들과 결부시켜 생각하는 거야 당연한 일이다만, 안 될 일이야, 안 될 일이지.\""]], [["Solomon Gills rubbed his hands with an air of stealthy enjoyment, as he talked of the sea, though; and looked on the seafaring objects about him with inexpressible complacency.", "그러면서도 솔로몬 길스는 바다 이야기를 할 때면 은근히 즐기는 기색으로 두 손을 비볐고, 주위의 항해 관련 물건들을 형언할 수 없는 흐뭇함으로 바라보았다."]], [["“Think of this wine for instance,”", "\"예를 들어 이 술을 생각해 보렴.\""], ["said old Sol, “which has been to the East Indies and back, I’m not able to say how often, and has been once round the world.", "늙은 솔이 말했다, \"이건 내가 몇 번이나 그랬는지 말할 수도 없을 만큼 동인도까지 갔다가 돌아왔고, 한 번은 세계를 일주하기까지 했지."], ["Think of the pitch-dark nights, the roaring winds, and rolling seas:”", "칠흑같이 어두운 밤들, 포효하는 바람, 출렁이는 바다를 생각해 보아라.\""]], [["“The thunder, lightning, rain, hail, storm of all kinds,”", "\"천둥, 번개, 비, 우박, 온갖 종류의 폭풍이요.\""], ["said the boy.", "소년이 말했다."]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"그렇고말고,\""], ["said Solomon,—“that this wine has passed through.", "솔로몬이 말했다, \"이 술이 그 모든 걸 헤치고 지나왔다는 거야."], ["Think what a straining and creaking of timbers and masts: what a whistling and howling of the gale through ropes and rigging:”", "목재며 돛대가 얼마나 삐걱대고 끼익거렸을지, 밧줄과 삭구 사이로 강풍이 얼마나 휘파람 불며 울부짖었을지 생각해 보렴.\""]], [["“What a clambering aloft of men, vying with each other who shall lie out first upon the yards to furl the icy sails, while the ship rolls and pitches, like mad!”", "\"미친 듯이 배가 흔들리고 곤두박질하는 와중에, 누가 먼저 활대 위로 기어 나가 얼어붙은 돛을 걷느냐 서로 다투며 사람들이 돛대 위로 기어오르는 걸요!\""], ["cried his nephew.", "조카가 외쳤다."]], [["“Exactly so,”", "\"바로 그렇지,\""], ["said Solomon: “has gone on, over the old cask that held this wine.", "솔로몬이 말했다, \"그 모든 일이, 이 술을 담았던 그 오래된 통 위에서 벌어졌다는 거야."], ["Why, when the Charming Sally went down in the—”", "그래, 매혹의 샐리호가 가라앉았을 때—\""]], [["“In the Baltic Sea, in the dead of night; five-and-twenty minutes past twelve when the captain’s watch stopped in his pocket; he lying dead against the main-mast—on the fourteenth of February, seventeen forty-nine!”", "\"발트해에서, 한밤중에 말이죠. 선장의 회중시계가 그의 주머니 속에서 멈춘 게 열두 시 이십오 분, 선장은 큰 돛대에 기댄 채 죽어 있었고요—1749년 2월 14일이었어요!\""], ["cried Walter, with great animation.", "월터가 잔뜩 신이 나서 외쳤다."]], [["“Ay, to be sure!”", "\"그래, 그렇고말고!\""], ["cried old Sol, “quite right!", "늙은 솔이 외쳤다, \"아주 정확해!"], ["Then, there were five hundred casks of such wine aboard; and all hands (except the first mate, first lieutenant, two seamen, and a lady, in a leaky boat) going to work to stave the casks, got drunk and died drunk, singing ‘Rule Britannia’, when she settled and went down, and ending with one awful scream in chorus.”", "그때 배에는 그런 술이 오백 통이나 실려 있었지. 그리고 (일등 항해사, 일등 사관, 선원 둘, 그리고 물 새는 보트에 탄 여인 하나만 빼고) 모든 선원이 술통을 부수려 달려들었다가, 곤드레만드레 취해서는 '룰 브리타니아'를 부르며 취한 채로 죽어 갔고, 배가 가라앉아 물속으로 잠길 때, 일제히 끔찍한 비명 하나로 끝을 맺었지.\""]], [["“But when the George the Second drove ashore, Uncle, on the coast of Cornwall, in a dismal gale, two hours before daybreak, on the fourth of March, “seventy-one, she had near two hundred horses aboard; and the horses breaking loose down below, early in the gale, and tearing to and fro, and trampling each other to death, made such noises, and set up such human cries, that the crew believing the ship to be full of devils, some of the best men, losing heart and head, went overboard in despair, and only two were left alive, at last, to tell the tale.”", "* 룰 브리타니아: 영국을 찬양하는 애국 가요. \"하지만 조지 2세호가, 삼촌, 음울한 강풍 속에서 콘월 해안에 좌초했을 때, 그게 날 밝기 두 시간 전, 1771년 3월 4일이었는데, 그 배엔 말이 이백 마리 가까이 실려 있었어요. 그런데 강풍이 일자마자 아래쪽에서 말들이 풀려나 이리저리 날뛰며 서로 짓밟아 죽이는 바람에, 어찌나 요란한 소리를 내고 사람처럼 울부짖었던지, 선원들은 배가 악마로 가득 찼다고 믿었고, 가장 뛰어난 사람들 몇몇이 정신이 나가 절망 속에 바다로 뛰어들었으며, 결국 살아남아 이 이야기를 전한 사람은 단 둘뿐이었어요.\""]], [["“And when,”", "\"그리고 언제냐 하면,\""], ["said old Sol, “when the Polyphemus—”", "늙은 솔이 말했다, \"폴리페모스호가 말이다—\""]], [["“Private West India Trader, burden three hundred and fifty tons, Captain, John Brown of Deptford.", "\"버든 삼백오십 톤짜리 개인 서인도 무역선이죠. 선장은 뎁트퍼드의 존 브라운."], ["Owners, Wiggs and Co.,”", "선주는 위그스 상회였고요.\""], ["cried Walter.", "월터가 외쳤다."]], [["“The same,”", "\"바로 그거다.\""], ["said Sol; “when she took fire, four days’ sail with a fair wind out of Jamaica Harbour, in the night—”", "솔이 말했다, \"그 배가 자메이카 항을 떠나 순풍을 타고 나흘 항해한 끝에, 한밤중에 불이 났을 때—\""]], [["“There were two brothers on board,”", "\"배에는 형제 둘이 타고 있었어요.\""], ["interposed his nephew, speaking very fast and loud, “and there not being room for both of them in the only boat that wasn’t swamped, neither of them would consent to go, until the elder took the younger by the waist, and flung him in.", "조카가 매우 빠르고 큰 소리로 끼어들었다, \"그리고 물에 잠기지 않은 단 한 척의 보트에 두 사람이 다 탈 자리가 없자, 둘 중 누구도 가려 하지 않다가, 형이 동생의 허리를 붙잡아 보트 안으로 던져 넣었어요. 그러자 동생이 보트에서 일어나 외쳤죠."], ["And then the younger, rising in the boat, cried out, ‘Dear Edward, think of your promised wife at home.", "'사랑하는 에드워드 형, 집에서 형을 기다리는 약혼녀를 생각해. 난 그냥 소년일 뿐이야."], ["I’m only a boy. No one waits at home for me.", "집에서 날 기다리는 사람은 아무도 없어."], ["Leap down into my place!’ and flung himself in the sea!”", "내 자리로 뛰어 내려와!' 그러고는 바다로 몸을 던졌어요!\""]], [["The kindling eye and heightened colour of the boy, who had risen from his seat in the earnestness of what he said and felt, seemed to remind old Sol of something he had forgotten, or that his encircling mist had hitherto shut out.", "자기가 말하고 느끼는 바에 대한 간절함으로 자리에서 일어선 소년의, 불타오르는 눈빛과 짙어진 혈색이, 늙은 솔에게 자기가 잊고 있던 무언가를, 혹은 자기를 둘러싼 안개가 지금껏 가로막아 온 무언가를 일깨우는 듯했다."], ["Instead of proceeding with any more anecdotes, as he had evidently intended but a moment before, he gave a short dry cough, and said, “Well!", "바로 한순간 전까지만 해도 분명히 그러려고 했던 것처럼 일화를 더 이어 가는 대신, 그는 짧고 메마른 기침을 한 번 하고는 말했다. \"자!"], ["suppose we change the subject.”", "화제를 좀 바꿔 보는 게 어떻겠니.\""]], [["The truth was, that the simple-minded Uncle in his secret attraction towards the marvellous and adventurous—of which he was, in some sort, a distant relation, by his trade—had greatly encouraged the same attraction in the nephew; and that everything that had ever been put before the boy to deter him from a life of adventure, had had the usual unaccountable effect of sharpening his taste for it.", "사실인즉, 단순한 마음을 지닌 그 삼촌은 자기 장사로 인해 어떤 의미에서는 먼 친척뻘이라 할 수 있는 그 경이롭고 모험적인 것에 은근히 끌리는 마음 때문에, 조카에게도 그와 똑같은 끌림을 크게 부추겨 온 터였다. 그리하여 소년이 모험적인 삶에 빠지지 않게 하려고 그 앞에 놓아 둔 모든 것이, 도리어 그것에 대한 소년의 취향을 날카롭게 벼리는, 늘 그렇듯 설명할 수 없는 효과를 내고 만 것이었다."], ["This is invariable.", "이건 변함없는 법칙이다."], ["It would seem as if there never was a book written, or a story told, expressly with the object of keeping boys on shore, which did not lure and charm them to the ocean, as a matter of course.", "소년들을 뭍에 붙들어 두려는 명백한 목적으로 쓰인 책이나 들려준 이야기치고, 도리어 그들을 당연한 일인 양 바다로 꾀어내고 매혹하지 않은 것은 단 한 번도 없었던 것만 같다."]], [["But an addition to the little party now made its appearance, in the shape of a gentleman in a wide suit of blue, with a hook instead of a hand attached to his right wrist; very bushy black eyebrows; and a thick stick in his left hand, covered all over (like his nose) with knobs.", "그런데 이제 그 작은 모임에 한 사람이 더 모습을 드러냈으니, 헐렁한 푸른 정장 차림에, 오른쪽 손목에는 손 대신 갈고리가 달린 신사였다. 매우 텁수룩한 검은 눈썹에, 왼손에는 (그의 코처럼) 온통 혹투성이인 굵은 지팡이를 들고 있었다."], ["He wore a loose black silk handkerchief round his neck, and such a very large coarse shirt collar, that it looked like a small sail.", "목에는 헐렁한 검은 비단 손수건을 둘렀고, 어찌나 큼지막하고 거친 셔츠 깃을 달았는지 마치 작은 돛처럼 보였다."], ["He was evidently the person for whom the spare wine-glass was intended, and evidently knew it; for having taken off his rough outer coat, and hung up, on a particular peg behind the door, such a hard glazed hat as a sympathetic person’s head might ache at the sight of, and which left a red rim round his own forehead as if he had been wearing a tight basin, he brought a chair to where the clean glass was, and sat himself down behind it.", "그는 분명 여분의 술잔이 마련된 바로 그 사람이었고, 또 자기도 그걸 분명히 알고 있었다. 그는 거친 겉외투를 벗어, 문 뒤의 특정한 못에다 걸었는데—그것은 보기만 해도 마음 약한 사람은 머리가 지끈거릴 만큼 딱딱하게 광택 나는 모자였으며, 꽉 끼는 대야라도 쓰고 있었던 것처럼 그의 이마에 붉은 자국을 남겨 놓았다—그러고는 의자를 깨끗한 잔이 있는 곳으로 가져다 그 뒤에 자리 잡고 앉았다."], ["He was usually addressed as Captain, this visitor; and had been a pilot, or a skipper, or a privateersman, or all three perhaps; and was a very salt-looking man indeed.", "이 방문객은 으레 선장이라 불렸으며, 도선사였거나, 선장이었거나, 사략선원이었거나, 어쩌면 그 셋 다였을 것이고, 정말이지 짠 바닷물에 절은 듯한 사내였다."]], [["His face, remarkable for a brown solidity, brightened as he shook hands with Uncle and nephew; but he seemed to be of a laconic disposition, and merely said:", "갈색의 단단함이 두드러지는 그의 얼굴은, 삼촌과 조카와 악수를 나누며 환하게 밝아졌다. 그러나 그는 과묵한 기질인 듯, 그저 이렇게만 말했다."]], [["“How goes it?”", "\"어찌 지내시오?\""]], [["“All well,”", "\"다들 잘 있소.\""], ["said Mr Gills, pushing the bottle towards him.", "길스 씨가 그에게 병을 밀어 주며 말했다."]], [["He took it up, and having surveyed and smelt it, said with extraordinary expression:", "그는 그것을 집어 들고, 찬찬히 살펴보고 냄새를 맡고는, 유난스러운 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“The?”", "\"그건가?\""]], [["“The,”", "\"그거요.\""], ["returned the Instrument-maker.", "기구 제작자가 대답했다."]], [["Upon that he whistled as he filled his glass, and seemed to think they were making holiday indeed.", "그 말에 그는 잔을 채우며 휘파람을 불었고, 정말로 잔칫날이라도 맞은 듯 여기는 듯했다."]], [["“Wal”r!”", "\"월—터!\""], ["he said, arranging his hair (which was thin) with his hook, and then pointing it at the Instrument-maker, “Look at him! Love!", "그가 갈고리로 (숱이 적은) 머리카락을 매만지고는, 그것으로 기구 제작자를 가리키며 말했다, \"저분을 보거라!"], ["Honour!", "사랑하라!"], ["And Obey!", "공경하라!"], ["Overhaul your catechism till you find that passage, and when found turn the leaf down.", "그리고 순종하라! 그 구절을 찾을 때까지 네 교리문답서를 샅샅이 뒤져서, 찾거든 그 쪽 귀퉁이를 접어 두렴."], ["Success, my boy!”", "성공을 비네, 얘야!\""]], [["He was so perfectly satisfied both with his quotation and his reference to it, that he could not help repeating the words again in a low voice, and saying he had forgotten ’em these forty year.", "그는 자기가 인용한 말과 그것을 들먹인 방식 둘 다에 어찌나 완벽하게 만족했던지, 그 말을 낮은 목소리로 다시 한번 되뇌지 않을 수 없었고, 자기가 그 말들을 사십 년 동안이나 잊고 있었다고 했다."]], [["“But I never wanted two or three words in my life that I didn’t know where to lay my hand upon ’em, Gills,”", "\"하지만 살면서 내가 두세 마디 말이 필요할 때 그걸 어디서 끌어다 댈지 몰랐던 적은 한 번도 없었네, 길스.\""], ["he observed.", "그가 말했다."], ["“It comes of not wasting language as some do.”", "\"그건 다 어떤 자들처럼 말을 허투루 낭비하지 않은 덕이지.\""]], [["The reflection perhaps reminded him that he had better, like young Norval’s father, ‘increase his store.’ At any rate he became silent, and remained so, until old Sol went out into the shop to light it up, when he turned to Walter, and said, without any introductory remark:—", "이런 생각을 하다 보니 어쩌면, 젊은 노발의 아버지처럼, 자기도 '재산을 늘리는' 편이 낫겠다는 생각이 떠올랐는지도 모른다. 아무튼 그는 입을 다물었고, 늙은 솔이 가게에 불을 밝히려고 밖으로 나갈 때까지 줄곧 잠자코 있었다. 솔이 나가자 그는 월터에게로 돌아서서, 아무런 서두도 없이 말했다. * 젊은 노발: 18세기 비극 「더글러스」에 나오는 인물로, 그의 아버지가 검소함으로 재산을 늘렸다는 대사를 빗댄 것."]], [["“I suppose he could make a clock if he tried?”", "\"내 짐작에 저분이 마음만 먹으면 시계도 만들 수 있겠지?\""]], [["“I shouldn’t wonder, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"그러셔도 놀랍지 않을 거예요, 커틀 선장님.\""], ["returned the boy.", "소년이 응수했다."]], [["“And it would go!”", "\"그리고 그게 잘 갈 거야!\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, making a species of serpent in the air with his hook.", "커틀 선장이 갈고리로 허공에 일종의 뱀 모양을 그리며 말했다."], ["“Lord, how that clock would go!”", "\"이런, 그 시계가 얼마나 잘 가겠나!\""]], [["For a moment or two he seemed quite lost in contemplating the pace of this ideal timepiece, and sat looking at the boy as if his face were the dial.", "잠시 동안 그는 그 가상의 시계가 가는 속도를 곰곰이 떠올리는 데 완전히 빠져든 듯, 마치 소년의 얼굴이 시계 문자판이라도 되는 양 그를 바라보고 앉아 있었다."]], [["“But he’s chock-full of science,”", "\"하지만 저분은 학식이 그득하시지.\""], ["he observed, waving his hook towards the stock-in-trade.", "그가 재고품 쪽으로 갈고리를 휘저으며 말했다."], ["“Look’ye here!", "\"여길 봐!"], ["Here’s a collection of ’em.", "여기 그것들이 한가득 모여 있잖나."], ["Earth, air, or water.", "땅이든, 하늘이든, 물이든."], ["It’s all one.", "다 매한가지야."], ["Only say where you’ll have it.", "어디서 가질지 말하기만 하면 돼."], ["Up in a balloon?", "기구를 타고 하늘 위로?"], ["There you are.", "자, 됐네."], ["Down in a bell? There you are.", "종 모양 잠수기를 타고 물속 아래로?"], ["D’ye want to put the North Star in a pair of scales and weigh it?", "자, 됐어. 북극성을 저울 한 쌍에 올려 무게를 달고 싶나?"], ["He’ll do it for you.”", "저분이 자네 대신 해 주실 걸세.\""]], [["It may be gathered from these remarks that Captain Cuttle’s reverence for the stock of instruments was profound, and that his philosophy knew little or no distinction between trading in it and inventing it.", "이런 말들로 미루어, 그 기구 재고품에 대한 커틀 선장의 경외심이 깊었으며, 그의 철학으로는 그것을 거래하는 일과 발명하는 일 사이에 거의, 혹은 전혀 구별이 없었음을 짐작할 수 있다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["he said, with a sigh, “it’s a fine thing to understand ’em.", "그가 한숨지으며 말했다, \"그것들을 이해한다는 건 멋진 일이지."], ["And yet it’s a fine thing not to understand ’em.", "하지만 또 그것들을 이해하지 못한다는 것도 멋진 일이야."], ["I hardly know which is best.", "어느 쪽이 더 나은지 도무지 모르겠네."], ["It’s so comfortable to sit here and feel that you might be weighed, measured, magnified, electrified, polarized, played the very devil with: and never know how.”", "이렇게 여기 앉아서, 내가 무게가 재어지고, 치수가 측정되고, 확대되고, 전기를 띠고, 편광되고, 흠씬 농락당할 수도 있는데, 어떻게 그렇게 되는지는 전혀 모른다고 느끼는 게 어찌나 마음 편한지.\""]], [["Nothing short of the wonderful Madeira, combined with the occasion (which rendered it desirable to improve and expand Walter’s mind), could have ever loosened his tongue to the extent of giving utterance to this prodigious oration.", "그 굉장한 마데이라가, 이 상황과 (월터의 마음을 길러 주고 넓혀 줄 만하다는 점에서) 어우러지지 않았다면, 결코 그의 혀가 이런 엄청난 일장 연설을 토해 낼 만큼 풀어지는 일은 없었을 것이다."], ["He seemed quite amazed himself at the manner in which it opened up to view the sources of the taciturn delight he had had in eating Sunday dinners in that parlour for ten years.", "그가 십 년 동안 그 거실에서 일요일 저녁을 먹으며 누려 온 그 과묵한 즐거움의 원천이, 그 연설로 환히 드러나는 모양새에 그 자신조차 자못 놀라는 듯했다."], ["Becoming a sadder and a wiser man, he mused and held his peace.", "한층 슬프고도 현명해진 사람이 되어, 그는 사색에 잠긴 채 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"자!\""], ["cried the subject of this admiration, returning.", "이 감탄의 대상이 돌아오며 외쳤다."], ["“Before you have your glass of grog, Ned, we must finish the bottle.”", "\"자네가 그로그주 한 잔을 들기 전에, 네드, 우리는 이 병을 비워야 하네.\""]], [["“Stand by!”", "\"준비됐네!\""], ["said Ned, filling his glass.", "네드가 자기 잔을 채우며 말했다."], ["“Give the boy some more.”", "\"얘한테도 좀 더 따라 주게.\""]], [["“No more, thank’e, Uncle!”", "\"전 그만이에요, 고맙습니다, 삼촌!\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"아니다, 아니야.\""], ["said Sol, “a little more.", "솔이 말했다, \"조금만 더."], ["We’ll finish the bottle, to the House, Ned—Walter’s House.", "우리는 이 병을 비워 회사를 위해 건배하자꾸나, 네드—월터의 회사 말이다."], ["Why it may be his House one of these days, in part.", "아니, 언젠가는 이게 부분적으로나마 저 애의 회사가 될지도 모르지."], ["Who knows?", "누가 알겠나?"], ["Sir Richard Whittington married his master’s daughter.”", "리처드 휘팅턴 경도 자기 주인의 딸과 결혼했잖나.\""]], [["“‘Turn again Whittington, Lord Mayor of London, and when you are old you will never depart from it,’”", "\"'돌아오너라 휘팅턴, 런던 시장이여, 그리고 늙거든 결코 거기서 떠나지 말지어다.'\""], ["interposed the Captain.", "선장이 끼어들었다."], ["“Wal”r!", "\"월—터!"], ["Overhaul the book, my lad.”", "그 책을 샅샅이 뒤져 봐라, 얘야.\""]], [["“And although Mr Dombey hasn’t a daughter,” Sol began.", "* 휘팅턴: 가난한 소년에서 런던 시장이 되었다는 전설의 인물 딕 휘팅턴."]], [["“Yes, yes, he has, Uncle,”", "\"그리고 돔비 씨한테는 딸이 없지만—\""], ["said the boy, reddening and laughing.", "솔이 운을 뗐다."]], [["“Has he?” cried the old man.", "\"아뇨, 있어요, 삼촌.\""], ["“Indeed I think he has too.”", "소년이 얼굴을 붉히며 웃었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"있다고?\""], ["I know he has,”", "노인이 외쳤다. \"정말이지 내 생각에도 있는 것 같구나.\""], ["said the boy.", "\"오! 분명히 있어요.\""], ["“Some of ’em were talking about it in the office today.", "소년이 말했다. \"오늘 사무소에서 몇 사람이 그 얘기를 하더라고요."], ["And they do say, Uncle and Captain Cuttle,”", "그리고 사람들이 그러는데요, 삼촌, 그리고 커틀 선장님,\" 목소리를 낮추며, \"그분이 그 딸을 싫어하게 되셨대요."], ["lowering his voice, “that he’s taken a dislike to her, and that she’s left, unnoticed, among the servants, and that his mind’s so set all the while upon having his son in the House, that although he’s only a baby now, he is going to have balances struck oftener than formerly, and the books kept closer than they used to be, and has even been seen (when he thought he wasn’t) walking in the Docks, looking at his ships and property and all that, as if he was exulting like, over what he and his son will possess together.", "그래서 딸은 하인들 틈에서 거들떠보지도 않은 채 내버려져 있고, 그분 마음은 온통 회사에 아들을 들이는 데만 쏠려 있어서, 비록 아들이 아직 갓난아기일 뿐인데도, 예전보다 더 자주 결산을 하게 하고, 장부도 예전보다 더 꼼꼼히 관리하게 하시고, 게다가 (아무도 안 본다고 여기실 때) 부두를 거닐며 자기 배들과 재산과 그런 걸 둘러보시는 게 목격되기까지 했대요. 마치 자기와 아들이 함께 소유하게 될 것을 두고 의기양양해하시는 것처럼요."], ["That’s what they say.", "사람들이 그렇게 말해요."], ["Of course, I don’t know.”", "물론 저야 모르지만요.\""]], [["“He knows all about her already, you see,”", "\"그분은 벌써 그 애에 관해 죄다 알고 계신 게로구나, 봐라.\""], ["said the instrument-maker.", "기구 제작자가 말했다."]], [["“Nonsense, Uncle,”", "\"말도 안 돼요, 삼촌.\""], ["cried the boy, still reddening and laughing, boy-like.", "소년이, 여전히 소년답게 얼굴을 붉히며 웃으며 외쳤다."], ["“How can I help hearing what they tell me?”", "\"사람들이 들려주는 걸 제가 안 들을 수가 있나요?\""]], [["“The son’s a little in our way at present, I’m afraid, Ned,”", "\"그 아들이 지금 우리에게는 좀 거치적거리는군, 안 그런가, 네드.\""], ["said the old man, humouring the joke.", "노인이 농을 맞장구치며 말했다."]], [["“Very much,”", "\"아주 그렇지.\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Nevertheless, we’ll drink him,”", "\"그렇긴 해도, 우리 그 아이를 위해 마시세.\""], ["pursued Sol.", "솔이 말을 이었다."], ["“So, here’s to Dombey and Son.”", "\"자, 돔비와 아들을 위하여.\""]], [["“Oh, very well, Uncle,”", "\"오, 좋아요, 삼촌.\""], ["said the boy, merrily.", "소년이 흥겹게 말했다."], ["“Since you have introduced the mention of her, and have connected me with her and have said that I know all about her, I shall make bold to amend the toast.", "\"삼촌이 그 딸 이야기를 꺼내셨고, 저를 그 딸과 엮으셨고, 또 제가 그 딸에 관해 죄다 안다고 하셨으니, 외람되이 그 건배사를 고쳐 보겠어요."], ["So here’s to Dombey—and Son—and Daughter!”", "그러니, 돔비—와 아들—그리고 딸을 위하여!\""]], [["# CHAPTER V. Paul’s Progress and Christening", "# CHAPTER V. Paul’s Progress and Christening"]], [["Little Paul, suffering no contamination from the blood of the Toodles, grew stouter and stronger every day.", "어린 폴은 투들 집안의 피로 인해 조금도 더럽혀지지 않은 채, 날마다 더 튼튼하고 건강하게 자랐다."], ["Every day, too, he was more and more ardently cherished by Miss Tox, whose devotion was so far appreciated by Mr Dombey that he began to regard her as a woman of great natural good sense, whose feelings did her credit and deserved encouragement.", "또한 날마다 톡스 양에게 점점 더 뜨겁게 사랑받았는데, 그녀의 헌신을 돔비 씨가 어느 정도 알아주게 되어 그녀를 타고난 양식이 뛰어난 여인, 그 감정이 본인에게 영예가 되고 격려받을 만한 여인으로 여기기 시작했다."], ["He was so lavish of this condescension, that he not only bowed to her, in a particular manner, on several occasions, but even entrusted such stately recognitions of her to his sister as “pray tell your friend, Louisa, that she is very good,” or “mention to Miss Tox, Louisa, that I am obliged to her;”", "그는 이런 겸양을 어찌나 아낌없이 베풀었던지, 여러 차례에 걸쳐 특별한 태도로 그녀에게 절을 했을 뿐 아니라, 누이를 통해 이런 위엄 있는 인사까지 전했으니, 이를테면 \"루이자, 네 친구에게 그녀가 참 착하다고 전해 주려무나\"라거나 \"루이자, 톡스 양에게 내가 그녀에게 신세를 졌다고 전해 다오\""], ["specialities which made a deep impression on the lady thus distinguished.", "하는 식이었다. 이런 특별한 대우들은 그렇게 두드러진 영예를 입은 그 부인에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다."]], [["Whether Miss Tox conceived that having been selected by the Fates to welcome the little Dombey before he was born, in Kirby, Beard and Kirby’s Best Mixed Pins, it therefore naturally devolved upon her to greet him with all other forms of welcome in all other early stages of his existence—or whether her overflowing goodness induced her to volunteer into the domestic militia as a substitute in some sort for his deceased Mama—or whether she was conscious of any other motives—are questions which in this stage of the Firm’s history herself only could have solved.", "톡스 양은 어린 돔비가 태어나기도 전에 커비, 비어드, 앤드 커비표 최상급 혼합 핀으로써 그를 맞이하도록 운명의 여신들에게 선택받았으니, 그러므로 그의 존재의 다른 모든 초기 단계마다 다른 모든 형태의 환대로 그를 맞이하는 일이 자연히 자신에게 떨어진 것이라 여겼는지—아니면 넘쳐흐르는 선량함에 이끌려 그의 돌아가신 어머니를 어느 정도 대신하는 자로서 집안의 의용군에 자원한 것인지—아니면 다른 어떤 동기를 스스로 의식하고 있었는지—이런 물음들은 회사 역사의 이 단계에서는 오직 그녀 자신만이 풀 수 있는 것이었다."], ["Nor have they much bearing on the fact (of which there is no doubt), that Miss Tox’s constancy and zeal were a heavy discouragement to Richards, who lost flesh hourly under her patronage, and was in some danger of being superintended to death.", "또한 이 물음들은, 톡스 양의 변함없음과 열성이 리처즈에게 무거운 낙담을 안겨 주었다는 사실(이는 의심의 여지가 없다)과도 별 상관이 없었으니, 리처즈는 그 후원 아래 시시각각 살이 빠져 갔고, 감독을 받다가 죽음에 이를 위험마저 어느 정도 있었다."]], [["Miss Tox was often in the habit of assuring Mrs Chick, that nothing could exceed her interest in all connected with the development of that sweet child; and an observer of Miss Tox’s proceedings might have inferred so much without declaratory confirmation.", "톡스 양은 종종 칙 부인에게, 저 사랑스러운 아이의 성장과 관련된 모든 일에 자신이 품은 관심은 그 무엇으로도 능가할 수 없을 것이라고 단언하곤 했다. 그리고 톡스 양의 행동을 지켜본 사람이라면 그런 단언적 확인이 없더라도 그만큼은 미루어 짐작할 수 있었을 것이다."], ["She would preside over the innocent repasts of the young heir, with ineffable satisfaction, almost with an air of joint proprietorship with Richards in the entertainment.", "그녀는 형언할 수 없는 만족감으로, 거의 리처즈와 그 잔치를 공동으로 소유한 듯한 태도로, 어린 상속자의 순진무구한 식사를 주재하곤 했다."], ["At the little ceremonies of the bath and toilette, she assisted with enthusiasm.", "목욕과 몸단장이라는 자그마한 의식에서도 그녀는 열성적으로 거들었다."], ["The administration of infantine doses of physic awakened all the active sympathy of her character; and being on one occasion secreted in a cupboard (whither she had fled in modesty), when Mr Dombey was introduced into the nursery by his sister, to behold his son, in the course of preparation for bed, taking a short walk uphill over Richards’s gown, in a short and airy linen jacket, Miss Tox was so transported beyond the ignorant present as to be unable to refrain from crying out, “Is he not beautiful Mr Dombey!", "갓난아이에게 약을 먹이는 일은 그녀의 성품에 깃든 온갖 적극적인 동정심을 일깨웠다. 그리고 한번은 돔비 씨가 누이의 안내로 아들을 보려고 육아실에 들어왔을 때—마침 폴이 잠자리에 들 채비를 하는 중에, 짧고 시원한 리넨 윗도리만 걸친 채 리처즈의 가운 위로 비탈을 오르듯 짧은 산책을 하고 있을 무렵—톡스 양은 (얌전을 떨며 달아나 들어간) 벽장 안에 몸을 숨기고 있었는데, 무지한 현재를 넘어서 어찌나 황홀경에 빠졌던지 \"어쩜 이렇게 예쁠까요, 돔비 씨!"], ["Is he not a Cupid, Sir!”", "큐피드가 따로 없네요, 나리!\""], ["and then almost sinking behind the closet door with confusion and blushes.", "하고 소리치지 않을 수 없었고, 그러고는 당황과 홍조에 휩싸여 벽장 문 뒤로 거의 주저앉을 뻔했다."]], [["“Louisa,”", "\"루이자,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, one day, to his sister, “I really think I must present your friend with some little token, on the occasion of Paul’s christening.", "어느 날 돔비 씨가 누이에게 말했다, \"내 생각엔 폴의 세례를 기념해서 네 친구에게 자그마한 기념품이라도 하나 줘야 할 것 같구나."], ["She has exerted herself so warmly in the child’s behalf from the first, and seems to understand her position so thoroughly (a very rare merit in this world, I am sorry to say), that it would really be agreeable to me to notice her.”", "그 여자가 처음부터 그 아이를 위해 그토록 따뜻하게 애써 왔고, 또 자기 처지를 그토록 철저히 이해하고 있는 듯하니(유감스럽게도 이 세상에서 매우 드문 미덕이지), 그녀를 좀 알아주는 일이 내게도 정말 흐뭇한 일이 될 거다.\""]], [["Let it be no detraction from the merits of Miss Tox, to hint that in Mr Dombey’s eyes, as in some others that occasionally see the light, they only achieved that mighty piece of knowledge, the understanding of their own position, who showed a fitting reverence for his.", "돔비 씨의 눈에는—이따금 세상 빛을 보는 다른 몇몇 눈에도 그렇듯—자기 처지를 이해한다는 그 대단한 지혜의 경지에 이른 자란, 오로지 그의 처지에 합당한 경의를 표하는 자들뿐이었다는 점을 슬쩍 일러둔다 해도, 그것이 톡스 양의 공로를 깎아내리는 일이 되지는 않을 것이다."], ["It was not so much their merit that they knew themselves, as that they knew him, and bowed low before him.", "그들의 공로는 자기 자신을 안다는 데 있다기보다, 그를 알고 그 앞에 깊이 머리를 숙인다는 데 있었던 것이다."]], [["“My dear Paul,”", "\"폴 오라버니,\""], ["returned his sister, “you do Miss Tox but justice, as a man of your penetration was sure, I knew, to do.", "누이가 응수했다, \"톡스 양에게 정당한 평가를 내리시는군요. 오라버니처럼 통찰력 있는 분이라면 분명 그러시리라 제가 알고 있었지요."], ["I believe if there are three words in the English language for which she has a respect amounting almost to veneration, those words are, Dombey and Son.”", "만일 영어에 그녀가 거의 숭배에 가까운 존경을 품은 단어가 세 개 있다면, 그 단어는 바로 돔비와 아들일 거예요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “I believe it.", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"나도 그렇게 믿는다."], ["It does Miss Tox credit.”", "톡스 양의 명예가 되는 일이지.\""]], [["“And as to anything in the shape of a token, my dear Paul,”", "\"그리고 기념품 같은 것에 관해서라면, 폴 오라버니,\""], ["pursued his sister, “all I can say is that anything you give Miss Tox will be hoarded and prized, I am sure, like a relic.", "누이가 말을 이었다, \"오라버니가 톡스 양에게 무엇을 주든 그것은 분명 성유물처럼 소중히 간직되고 귀히 여겨질 거예요."], ["But there is a way, my dear Paul, of showing your sense of Miss Tox’s friendliness in a still more flattering and acceptable manner, if you should be so inclined.”", "하지만 폴 오라버니, 만일 그럴 마음이 있으시다면, 톡스 양의 다정함에 대한 오라버니의 마음을 더욱 기쁘게 하고 받아들여질 만한 방식으로 보여 주는 길이 있긴 해요.\""]], [["“How is that?”", "\"그게 무엇이냐?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Godfathers, of course,”", "\"대부모는, 물론,\""], ["continued Mrs Chick, “are important in point of connexion and influence.”", "칙 부인이 말을 이었다, \"인연과 영향력이라는 면에서 중요하지요.\""]], [["“I don’t know why they should be, to my son,”", "\"내 아들에게 그게 왜 중요한지 모르겠구나,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, coldly.", "돔비 씨가 차갑게 말했다."]], [["“Very true, my dear Paul,”", "\"지당하신 말씀이에요, 폴 오라버니,\""], ["retorted Mrs Chick, with an extraordinary show of animation, to cover the suddenness of her conversion; “and spoken like yourself.", "칙 부인이 갑작스러운 변심을 감추려고 유난히 활기를 부리며 응수했다. \"오라버니다운 말씀이세요."], ["I might have expected nothing else from you.", "다른 말씀을 기대할 수가 없지요."], ["I might have known that such would have been your opinion.", "그런 의견을 가지셨으리라는 걸 진작 알았어야 했는데."], ["Perhaps;”", "어쩌면,\""], ["here Mrs Chick faltered again, as not quite comfortably feeling her way; “perhaps that is a reason why you might have the less objection to allowing Miss Tox to be godmother to the dear thing, if it were only as deputy and proxy for someone else.", "여기서 칙 부인은 그다지 편치 않은 마음으로 길을 더듬듯 다시 머뭇거렸다, \"어쩌면 그게 바로, 톡스 양을 그 귀여운 아이의 대모로 삼는 데 오라버니가 덜 반대하실 만한 이유일지도 모르겠어요. 그저 다른 누군가의 대리인이자 대행자로서 말이지요."], ["That it would be received as a great honour and distinction, Paul, I need not say.”", "그것이 크나큰 영예이자 영광으로 받아들여지리라는 건, 폴, 굳이 말할 필요도 없지요.\""]], [["“Louisa,”", "\"루이자,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, after a short pause, “it is not to be supposed—”", "돔비 씨가 잠시 침묵한 뒤 말했다, \"설마 그렇게 생각하는 건—\""]], [["“Certainly not,”", "\"물론 아니에요,\""], ["cried Mrs Chick, hastening to anticipate a refusal, “I never thought it was.”", "거절을 미리 막으려 서두르며 칙 부인이 외쳤다, \"저는 한 번도 그렇게 생각한 적 없어요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey looked at her impatiently.", "돔비 씨가 짜증스럽게 누이를 바라보았다."]], [["“Don’t flurry me, my dear Paul,”", "\"저를 당황하게 하지 마세요, 폴 오라버니,\""], ["said his sister; “for that destroys me.", "누이가 말했다, \"그러면 저는 무너지고 말아요."], ["I am far from strong.", "저는 도무지 튼튼하질 못해요."], ["I have not been quite myself, since poor dear Fanny departed.”", "가엾은 패니가 떠난 뒤로 제정신이 아니랍니다.\""]], [["Mr Dombey glanced at the pocket-handkerchief which his sister applied to her eyes, and resumed:", "돔비 씨는 누이가 두 눈에 갖다 대는 손수건을 흘끗 보고는 말을 이었다. \"설마 그렇게 생각하지는 않겠지, 라고 내가 말하는 거다—\""]], [["“It is not be supposed, I say—”", "\"그리고 저는,\""], ["“And I say,” murmured Mrs Chick, “that I never thought it was.”", "칙 부인이 중얼거렸다, \"한 번도 그렇게 생각한 적 없다고 말씀드리는 거예요.\""]], [["“Good Heaven, Louisa!”", "\"맙소사, 루이자!\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“No, my dear Paul,”", "\"아니에요, 폴 오라버니,\""], ["she remonstrated with tearful dignity, “I must really be allowed to speak.", "그녀가 눈물 어린 위엄을 지키며 항변했다, \"저도 정말 말 좀 하게 해 주세요."], ["I am not so clever, or so reasoning, or so eloquent, or so anything, as you are.", "저는 오라버니만큼 영리하지도, 사리에 밝지도, 언변이 좋지도, 그 무엇도 못해요."], ["I know that very well.", "저도 잘 알아요."], ["So much the worse for me.", "그래서 더 딱한 거지요."], ["But if they were the last words I had to utter—and last words should be very solemn to you and me, Paul, after poor dear Fanny—I would still say I never thought it was.", "하지만 이게 제가 내뱉을 마지막 말이라 해도—그리고 가엾은 패니가 떠난 마당에, 폴, 마지막 말이란 오라버니와 제게 무척 엄숙한 것이어야 하는데—저는 여전히 한 번도 그렇게 생각한 적 없다고 말하겠어요. 더구나,\""], ["And what is more,” added Mrs Chick with increased dignity, as if she had withheld her crushing argument until now, “I never did think it was.”", "칙 부인이, 마치 결정적인 논거를 지금까지 아껴 두기라도 했다는 듯 한층 위엄을 더하며 덧붙였다, \"저는 진짜로 단 한 번도 그렇게 생각한 적이 없어요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey walked to the window and back again.", "돔비 씨는 창가로 걸어갔다가 다시 돌아왔다."]], [["“It is not to be supposed, Louisa,”", "\"설마 그렇게 생각하지는 않겠지, 루이자,\""], ["he said (Mrs Chick had nailed her colours to the mast, and repeated “I know it isn’t,” but he took no notice of it), “but that there are many persons who, supposing that I recognised any claim at all in such a case, have a claim upon me superior to Miss Tox’s.", "그가 말했다(칙 부인은 자기 깃발을 돛대에 못 박아 걸고는 \"그렇지 않다는 거 알아요\"라고 되풀이했으나, 그는 거기에 아랑곳하지 않았다), \"이런 경우에 내가 누구의 권리든 조금이라도 인정한다고 친다면, 톡스 양보다 더 우선하는 권리를 내게 주장할 사람이 많이 있으리라는 점은 부정할 수 없겠지."], ["But I do not. I recognise no such thing.", "하지만 나는 인정하지 않아."], ["Paul and myself will be able, when the time comes, to hold our own—the House, in other words, will be able to hold its own, and maintain its own, and hand down its own of itself, and without any such common-place aids.", "그런 건 전혀 인정하지 않는다. 폴과 나는, 때가 오면, 스스로를 지켜낼 수 있을 거야—다시 말해, 우리 회사는 스스로를 지키고, 스스로를 유지하고, 그런 흔해 빠진 도움 없이도 스스로의 힘으로 대를 이어 물려줄 수 있을 거다."], ["The kind of foreign help which people usually seek for their children, I can afford to despise; being above it, I hope.", "사람들이 흔히 제 자식들을 위해 구하는 그런 외부의 도움 따위는 나는 능히 경멸할 수 있어. 나는 그런 것 위에 있는 사람이니까."], ["So that Paul’s infancy and childhood pass away well, and I see him becoming qualified without waste of time for the career on which he is destined to enter, I am satisfied.", "그러니 폴의 유아기와 어린 시절이 잘 흘러가서, 그 애가 들어서도록 정해진 그 길을 향해 시간 낭비 없이 자질을 갖추어 가는 모습을 내가 보게 된다면, 나는 만족이야."], ["He will make what powerful friends he pleases in after-life, when he is actively maintaining—and extending, if that is possible—the dignity and credit of the Firm.", "그 애가 훗날 인생에서, 회사의 위엄과 신용을 적극적으로 지켜 나가며—가능하다면 넓혀 나가며—마음 내키는 대로 권세 있는 친구들을 사귀게 되겠지."], ["Until then, I am enough for him, perhaps, and all in all.", "그때까지는 나 하나로 그 애에게 충분하고, 어쩌면 전부일 게다."], ["I have no wish that people should step in between us.", "나는 사람들이 우리 사이에 끼어드는 걸 원치 않아."], ["I would much rather show my sense of the obliging conduct of a deserving person like your friend.", "차라리 네 친구처럼 마땅한 사람의 호의로운 행실에 대한 내 마음을 표하는 편이 훨씬 낫지."], ["Therefore let it be so; and your husband and myself will do well enough for the other sponsors, I daresay.”", "그러니 그렇게 하자꾸나. 그리고 네 남편과 내가 나머지 대부 노릇은 충분히 잘 해낼 수 있을 게다.\""]], [["In the course of these remarks, delivered with great majesty and grandeur, Mr Dombey had truly revealed the secret feelings of his breast.", "대단한 위엄과 장중함으로 늘어놓은 이 말들 가운데, 돔비 씨는 진실로 자기 가슴속에 숨은 감정을 드러냈다."], ["An indescribable distrust of anybody stepping in between himself and his son; a haughty dread of having any rival or partner in the boy’s respect and deference; a sharp misgiving, recently acquired, that he was not infallible in his power of bending and binding human wills; as sharp a jealousy of any second check or cross; these were, at that time the master keys of his soul.", "자신과 아들 사이에 누가 끼어드는 것에 대한 형언할 수 없는 불신, 그 아이의 존경과 순종을 두고 어떤 경쟁자나 동업자라도 두게 될까 하는 오만한 두려움, 인간의 의지를 굽히고 묶는 자신의 힘이 결코 틀림없는 것은 아닐지도 모른다는, 근래에 새로 생긴 날카로운 의구심, 두 번째 견제나 방해라도 있을세라 그만큼 날카로운 질투—이런 것들이 그 무렵 그의 영혼을 여는 으뜸 열쇠였다."], ["In all his life, he had never made a friend.", "그는 평생 한 번도 벗을 사귄 적이 없었다."], ["His cold and distant nature had neither sought one, nor found one.", "차갑고 거리를 두는 그의 천성은 벗을 구한 적도, 찾은 적도 없었다."], ["And now, when that nature concentrated its whole force so strongly on a partial scheme of parental interest and ambition, it seemed as if its icy current, instead of being released by this influence, and running clear and free, had thawed for but an instant to admit its burden, and then frozen with it into one unyielding block.", "그리고 지금, 그 천성이 부성애의 관심과 야심이라는 한쪽으로 치우친 계획에 온 힘을 그토록 강하게 쏟아붓자, 마치 그 얼음 같은 물줄기가 이 영향으로 풀려나 맑고 자유로이 흐르기는커녕, 그 짐을 받아들이려고 한순간 녹았다가 다시 그 짐과 함께 얼어붙어 굽힐 줄 모르는 한 덩어리가 된 듯했다."]], [["Elevated thus to the godmothership of little Paul, in virtue of her insignificance, Miss Tox was from that hour chosen and appointed to office; and Mr Dombey further signified his pleasure that the ceremony, already long delayed, should take place without further postponement. His sister, who had been far from anticipating so signal a success, withdrew as soon as she could, to communicate it to her best of friends; and Mr Dombey was left alone in his library. He had already laid his hand upon the bellrope to convey his usual summons to Richards, when his eye fell upon a writing-desk, belonging to his deceased wife, which had been taken, among other things, from a cabinet in her chamber. It was not the first time that his eye had lighted on it He carried the key in his pocket; and he brought it to his table and opened it now—having previously locked the room door—with a well-accustomed hand.", "이리하여 톡스 양은 자신의 보잘것없음 덕분에 어린 폴의 대모 자리로 격상되어, 그 시각부터 그 직책에 선택되고 임명되었다. 그리고 돔비 씨는 더 나아가, 이미 오래 미뤄진 그 의식을 더는 연기하지 말고 거행해야겠다는 뜻을 밝혔다. 이토록 눈에 띄는 성공은 전혀 예상치 못했던 그의 누이는 가능한 한 빨리 물러나, 그 둘도 없는 벗에게 이 소식을 전했다. 그리고 돔비 씨는 서재에 혼자 남았다. 그는 리처즈를 부르려고 평소처럼 종 줄에 손을 가져갔는데, 그때 그의 눈이 죽은 아내의 것이던 글쓰기용 책상에 가닿았다. 그것은 다른 물건들과 함께 그녀의 방 장식장에서 꺼내온 것이었다. 그의 눈이 거기에 머문 것은 처음이 아니었다. 그는 주머니에 그 열쇠를 지니고 다녔다. 그는 그것을 탁자로 가져와, 미리 방문을 잠근 다음, 익숙한 손길로 열었다."]], [["From beneath a leaf of torn and cancelled scraps of paper, he took one letter that remained entire.", "찢기고 줄 그어진 종이 부스러기들이 쌓인 한 겹 아래에서, 그는 온전히 남은 편지 한 통을 꺼냈다."], ["Involuntarily holding his breath as he opened this document, and “bating in the stealthy action something of his arrogant demeanour, he sat down, resting his head upon one hand, and read it through.", "이 문서를 펼치며 저도 모르게 숨을 죽이고, 그 은밀한 동작 속에서 평소의 오만한 태도를 다소 누그러뜨린 채, 그는 한 손으로 머리를 받치고 앉아 그것을 끝까지 읽었다."]], [["He read it slowly and attentively, and with a nice particularity to every syllable.", "그는 천천히, 주의 깊게, 음절 하나하나에 세심한 정성을 기울여 읽었다."], ["Otherwise than as his great deliberation seemed unnatural, and perhaps the result of an effort equally great, he allowed no sign of emotion to escape him.", "그토록 신중한 태도가 부자연스러워 보이고, 어쩌면 그만큼이나 큰 노력의 결과로 보였다는 점 말고는, 그는 어떤 감정의 기색도 새어 나오게 두지 않았다."], ["When he had read it through, he folded and refolded it slowly several times, and tore it carefully into fragments.", "다 읽고 나자 그는 그것을 천천히 여러 번 접고 또 접더니, 조심스럽게 조각조각 찢었다."], ["Checking his hand in the act of throwing these away, he put them in his pocket, as if unwilling to trust them even to the chances of being re-united and deciphered; and instead of ringing, as usual, for little Paul, he sat solitary, all the evening, in his cheerless room.", "그것들을 내던지려던 손을 멈추고는, 다시 맞춰져 해독될지도 모를 운에마저 맡기기 싫다는 듯 주머니에 넣었다. 그리고 평소처럼 어린 폴을 불러오라고 종을 울리는 대신, 그는 그 음울한 방에서 저녁 내내 홀로 앉아 있었다."]], [["There was anything but solitude in the nursery; for there, Mrs Chick and Miss Tox were enjoying a social evening, so much to the disgust of Miss Susan Nipper, that that young lady embraced every opportunity of making wry faces behind the door.", "그러나 육아실은 외로움과는 거리가 멀었다. 그곳에서는 칙 부인과 톡스 양이 즐거운 저녁을 보내고 있었는데, 수전 니퍼 양은 그것이 어찌나 못마땅했던지 기회만 있으면 문 뒤에서 얼굴을 찡그렸다."], ["Her feelings were so much excited on the occasion, that she found it indispensable to afford them this relief, even without having the comfort of any audience or sympathy whatever.", "그녀는 이 일로 감정이 어찌나 격해졌던지, 청중이나 동조자의 위안 하나 없이도 그렇게라도 감정을 풀어내지 않고는 견딜 수 없었다."], ["As the knight-errants of old relieved their minds by carving their mistress’s names in deserts, and wildernesses, and other savage places where there was no probability of there ever being anybody to read them, so did Miss Susan Nipper curl her snub nose into drawers and wardrobes, put away winks of disparagement in cupboards, shed derisive squints into stone pitchers, and contradict and call names out in the passage.", "옛 편력 기사들이 사막이며 황야며, 그것을 읽어 줄 사람이 있을 가망이라곤 없는 그 밖의 야만스러운 곳에 사랑하는 여인의 이름을 새기며 마음을 달랬듯이, 수전 니퍼 양도 서랍과 옷장에 대고 들창코를 찡그리고, 벽장 안에 멸시의 눈짓을 감추어 두고, 돌 항아리에 비웃는 곁눈질을 흘려 넣고, 복도에 나가 대들고 욕을 퍼부었다."]], [["The two interlopers, however, blissfully unconscious of the young lady’s sentiments, saw little Paul safe through all the stages of undressing, airy exercise, supper and bed; and then sat down to tea before the fire.", "그러나 이 두 침입자는 그 처녀의 심정 따위는 더없이 행복하게도 까맣게 모른 채, 어린 폴이 옷 벗기, 시원한 운동, 저녁 식사, 잠자리에 이르는 온갖 단계를 무사히 거치는 것을 지켜보았고, 그런 다음 난롯불 앞에 앉아 차를 마셨다."], ["The two children now lay, through the good offices of Polly, in one room; and it was not until the ladies were established at their tea-table that, happening to look towards the little beds, they thought of Florence.", "두 아이는 이제 폴리의 주선으로 한 방에 누워 있었는데, 두 부인이 찻상에 자리를 잡고 나서야 비로소 우연히 자그마한 침대들 쪽을 바라보다가 플로렌스를 떠올렸다."]], [["“How sound she sleeps!”", "\"참 곤히도 자네!\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."]], [["“Why, you know, my dear, she takes a great deal of exercise in the course of the day,”", "\"아니, 글쎄, 얘야, 저 애는 하루 종일 운동을 어찌나 많이 하는지,\""], ["returned Mrs Chick, “playing about little Paul so much.”", "칙 부인이 응수했다, \"어린 폴 곁에서 그렇게 노니 말이야.\""]], [["“She is a curious child,”", "\"신기한 아이예요,\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"얘,\""], ["retorted Mrs Chick, in a low voice: “Her Mama, all over!”", "칙 부인이 낮은 목소리로 응수했다, \"제 어미를 쏙 빼닮았다니까!\""]], [["“In-deed!”", "\"저-런!\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“Ah dear me!”", "\"어머나 가엾어라!\""]], [["A tone of most extraordinary compassion Miss Tox said it in, though she had no distinct idea why, except that it was expected of her.", "톡스 양은 더없이 유별난 연민의 어조로 그렇게 말했는데, 사실은 그것이 자기에게 기대되는 반응이라는 것 말고는 왜 그래야 하는지 뚜렷한 생각도 없었다."]], [["“Florence will never, never, never be a Dombey,”", "\"플로렌스는 결코, 결코, 결코 돔비가 되지 못할 거야,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “not if she lives to be a thousand years old.”", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"천 살까지 산다 해도 안 될 거야.\""]], [["Miss Tox elevated her eyebrows, and was again full of commiseration.", "톡스 양은 눈썹을 치켜올리며 다시금 가엾은 마음에 가득 찼다."]], [["“I quite fret and worry myself about her,”", "\"나는 저 애 때문에 정말이지 속을 끓이고 마음을 졸인다니까,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with a sigh of modest merit.", "겸손한 미덕의 한숨을 쉬며 칙 부인이 말했다."], ["“I really don’t see what is to become of her when she grows older, or what position she is to take.", "\"저 애가 나이 들면 대체 어떻게 될지, 어떤 처지를 차지하게 될지 정말 모르겠어."], ["She don’t gain on her Papa in the least.", "제 아빠한테 조금도 환심을 못 사는걸."], ["How can one expect she should, when she is so very unlike a Dombey?”", "저렇게 돔비를 안 닮았으니, 어떻게 그러기를 바라겠어?\""]], [["Miss Tox looked as if she saw no way out of such a cogent argument as that, at all.", "톡스 양은 그토록 설득력 있는 논거에서 빠져나갈 길이라곤 도무지 보이지 않는다는 표정을 지었다."]], [["“And the child, you see,”", "\"그리고 저 애는, 알다시피,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, in deep confidence, “has poor dear Fanny’s nature.", "칙 부인이 깊은 속내를 털어놓듯 말했다, \"가엾은 패니의 천성을 그대로 물려받았어."], ["She’ll never make an effort in after-life, I’ll venture to say.", "장담컨대 저 애는 훗날 인생에서 결코 노력이란 걸 하지 않을 거야."], ["Never!", "결코!"], ["She’ll never wind and twine herself about her Papa’s heart like—”", "저 애는 결코 제 아빠의 마음을 칭칭 감고 휘감지 못할 거야, 마치—\""]], [["“Like the ivy?”", "\"담쟁이덩굴처럼요?\""], ["suggested Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 거들었다."]], [["“Like the ivy,”", "\"담쟁이덩굴처럼,\""], ["Mrs Chick assented.", "칙 부인이 수긍했다."], ["“Never!", "\"결코!"], ["She’ll never glide and nestle into the bosom of her Papa’s affections like—the—”", "저 애는 결코 제 아빠의 애정의 품 안으로 사뿐히 미끄러져 들어가 안기지 못할 거야, 마치—그—\""]], [["“Startled fawn?”", "\"놀란 새끼 사슴처럼요?\""], ["suggested Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 거들었다."]], [["“Like the startled fawn,”", "\"놀란 새끼 사슴처럼,\""], ["said Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."], ["“Never!", "\"결코!"], ["Poor Fanny!", "가엾은 패니!"], ["Yet, how I loved her!”", "그래도, 내가 그 애를 얼마나 사랑했던지!\""]], [["“You must not distress yourself, my dear,”", "\"너무 괴로워하지 마세요, 얘,\""], ["said Miss Tox, in a soothing voice.", "톡스 양이 달래는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Now really!", "\"정말이지!"], ["You have too much feeling.”", "당신은 정이 너무 깊어요.\""]], [["“We have all our faults,”", "\"우리 모두 결점이야 있지,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, weeping and shaking her head.", "칙 부인이 울며 고개를 저었다. \"분명 우리도 그렇겠지."], ["“I daresay we have. I never was blind to hers.", "나는 한 번도 그 애의 결점에 눈감은 적 없어."], ["I never said I was.", "그런 적 없다고 말한 적도 없고. 천만에."], ["Far from it. Yet how I loved her!”", "그래도 내가 그 애를 얼마나 사랑했던지!\""]], [["What a satisfaction it was to Mrs Chick—a common-place piece of folly enough, compared with whom her sister-in-law had been a very angel of womanly intelligence and gentleness—to patronise and be tender to the memory of that lady: in exact pursuance of her conduct to her in her lifetime: and to thoroughly believe herself, and take herself in, and make herself uncommonly comfortable on the strength of her toleration!", "칙 부인에게는—여성다운 지성과 온화함의 천사라 할 만했던 올케에 비하면 그저 흔해 빠진 어리석음 덩어리에 지나지 않았던 그녀에게는—그 부인의 기억을 너그럽게 굽어보며 다정을 베푸는 일이, 그것도 생전에 그 부인을 대하던 행실 그대로, 그리하여 자기 자신을 철저히 믿고 자기 자신을 감쪽같이 속이며, 그 관용을 빌미로 유난히 흐뭇해하는 일이 어찌나 흡족했던지!"], ["What a mighty pleasant virtue toleration should be when we are right, to be so very pleasant when we are wrong, and quite unable to demonstrate how we come to be invested with the privilege of exercising it!", "우리가 옳을 때조차 그토록 유쾌한 관용이라는 미덕이, 우리가 틀렸을 때도 그렇게나 유쾌하고, 더구나 우리가 어떻게 그것을 행사할 특권을 갖게 되었는지 도무지 증명할 수 없을 때조차 그렇다니, 관용이란 얼마나 굉장히 유쾌한 미덕인가!"]], [["Mrs Chick was yet drying her eyes and shaking her head, when Richards made bold to caution her that Miss Florence was awake and sitting in her bed.", "칙 부인이 아직 눈물을 훔치며 고개를 젓고 있을 때, 리처즈가 용기를 내어 플로렌스 아가씨가 잠에서 깨어 침대에 일어나 앉아 있다고 알렸다."], ["She had risen, as the nurse said, and the lashes of her eyes were wet with tears.", "유모의 말로는, 아이가 일어났는데 속눈썹이 눈물로 젖어 있더라는 것이었다."], ["But no one saw them glistening save Polly.", "그러나 그 눈물이 반짝이는 것을 본 사람은 폴리 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["No one else leant over her, and whispered soothing words to her, or was near enough to hear the flutter of her beating heart.", "그 위로 몸을 굽혀 달래는 말을 속삭여 준 사람도, 두근거리는 그 심장의 떨림을 들을 만큼 가까이 다가간 사람도 달리 없었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["dear nurse!”", "유모 아줌마!\""], ["said the child, looking earnestly up in her face, “let me lie by my brother!”", "아이가 그녀의 얼굴을 진지하게 올려다보며 말했다, \"동생 옆에서 자게 해 주세요!\""]], [["“Why, my pet?”", "\"왜 그러니, 우리 아가?\""], ["said Richards.", "리처즈가 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I think he loves me,”", "동생은 나를 사랑하는 것 같아요,\""], ["cried the child wildly.", "아이가 격하게 외쳤다."], ["“Let me lie by him.", "\"동생 옆에서 자게 해 주세요."], ["Pray do!”", "제발요!\""]], [["Mrs Chick interposed with some motherly words about going to sleep like a dear, but Florence repeated her supplication, with a frightened look, and in a voice broken by sobs and tears.", "칙 부인이 착한 아이답게 자야지 하는 어미다운 말로 끼어들었으나, 플로렌스는 겁먹은 표정으로, 흐느낌과 눈물에 끊기는 목소리로 애원을 거듭했다."]], [["“I’ll not wake him,”", "\"깨우지 않을게요,\""], ["she said, covering her face and hanging down her head.", "아이가 얼굴을 가리고 고개를 떨구며 말했다."], ["“I’ll only touch him with my hand, and go to sleep.", "\"그냥 손으로만 만지고 잘게요."], ["Oh, pray, pray, let me lie by my brother tonight, for I believe he’s fond of me!”", "오, 제발, 제발, 오늘 밤만 동생 옆에서 자게 해 주세요, 동생은 나를 좋아하는 것 같단 말이에요!\""]], [["Richards took her without a word, and carrying her to the little bed in which the infant was sleeping, laid her down by his side.", "리처즈는 아무 말 없이 아이를 안아, 갓난아이가 자고 있는 자그마한 침대로 데려가 그 곁에 뉘었다."], ["She crept as near him as she could without disturbing his rest; and stretching out one arm so that it timidly embraced his neck, and hiding her face on the other, over which her damp and scattered hair fell loose, lay motionless.", "아이는 동생의 잠을 방해하지 않을 만큼 최대한 가까이 다가가 기어들었다. 그러고는 한 팔을 뻗어 머뭇거리듯 동생의 목을 감싸 안고, 다른 팔에 얼굴을 묻은 채—그 위로 축축하고 흐트러진 머리카락이 풀어져 흘러내렸다—꼼짝 않고 누워 있었다."]], [["“Poor little thing,”", "\"가엾은 것,\""], ["said Miss Tox; “she has been dreaming, I daresay.”", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"꿈을 꾸었나 봐요.\""]], [["Dreaming, perhaps, of loving tones for ever silent, of loving eyes for ever closed, of loving arms again wound round her, and relaxing in that dream within the dam which no tongue can relate.", "꿈을 꾸었던 것이리라, 어쩌면, 영원히 잠잠해진 다정한 목소리를, 영원히 감겨 버린 다정한 눈을, 다시 한번 자기를 감싸 안았다가 그 꿈속에서, 어떤 혀로도 풀어낼 수 없는 그 둑 안에서 스르르 풀려 가는 다정한 팔을."], ["Seeking, perhaps—in dreams—some natural comfort for a heart, deeply and sorely wounded, though so young a child’s: and finding it, perhaps, in dreams, if not in waking, cold, substantial truth.", "어쩌면 찾고 있었으리라—꿈속에서—그토록 어린 아이의 것이건만 깊고도 아프게 상처 입은 마음을 위한 어떤 자연스러운 위안을. 그리고 어쩌면 찾아냈으리라, 꿈속에서, 깨어 있을 때가 아니라면, 차갑고 실체 있는 진실을."], ["This trivial incident had so interrupted the current of conversation, that it was difficult of resumption; and Mrs Chick moreover had been so affected by the contemplation of her own tolerant nature, that she was not in spirits.", "이 사소한 사건이 대화의 흐름을 어찌나 끊어 놓았던지 다시 잇기가 어려웠다. 게다가 칙 부인은 자기 자신의 관대한 천성을 곱씹다 보니 너무도 감동에 젖어 기운이 나질 않았다."], ["The two friends accordingly soon made an end of their tea, and a servant was despatched to fetch a hackney cabriolet for Miss Tox.", "그리하여 두 벗은 곧 차를 끝마쳤고, 하인 하나가 톡스 양을 위해 삯마차를 부르러 보내졌다."], ["Miss Tox had great experience in hackney cabs, and her starting in one was generally a work of time, as she was systematic in the preparatory arrangements.", "톡스 양은 삯마차에 관한 한 경험이 풍부해서, 그것을 타고 출발하는 일은 으레 시간이 걸리는 작업이었는데, 사전 준비 절차에서 늘 체계적이었기 때문이다."]], [["“Have the goodness, if you please, Towlinson,”", "\"미안하지만, 타울린슨,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “first of all, to carry out a pen and ink and take his number legibly.”", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"무엇보다 먼저, 펜과 잉크를 가지고 나가서 마차 번호를 또렷이 적어 주겠어요?\""]], [["“Yes, Miss,”", "\"네, 아가씨,\""], ["said Towlinson.", "타울린슨이 말했다."]], [["“Then, if you please, Towlinson,”", "\"그리고, 미안하지만, 타울린슨,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “have the goodness to turn the cushion.", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"방석을 좀 뒤집어 주겠어요."], ["Which,”", "그건,\""], ["said Miss Tox apart to Mrs Chick, “is generally damp, my dear.”", "톡스 양이 칙 부인에게 따로 말했다, \"대개 축축하답니다, 얘.\""]], [["“Yes, Miss,”", "\"네, 아가씨,\""], ["said Towlinson.", "타울린슨이 말했다."]], [["“I’ll trouble you also, if you please, Towlinson,”", "\"그리고 또 폐를 끼쳐서 미안하지만, 타울린슨,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “with this card and this shilling.", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"이 명함하고 이 실링을 좀 부탁해요."], ["He’s to drive to the card, and is to understand that he will not on any account have more than the shilling.”", "마부더러 명함에 적힌 곳으로 몰고 가라고, 그리고 무슨 일이 있어도 이 실링보다 더는 못 받는다는 걸 알아두라고 해 줘요.\""]], [["“No, Miss,”", "\"네, 아가씨,\""], ["said Towlinson.", "타울린슨이 말했다."]], [["“And—I’m sorry to give you so much trouble, Towlinson,”", "\"그리고—이렇게 폐를 많이 끼쳐 미안해요, 타울린슨,\""], ["said Miss Tox, looking at him pensively.", "톡스 양이 그를 수심에 잠긴 듯 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Not at all, Miss,”", "\"천만에요, 아가씨,\""], ["said Towlinson.", "타울린슨이 말했다."]], [["“Mention to the man, then, if you please, Towlinson,”", "\"그러면, 미안하지만, 타울린슨,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “that the lady’s uncle is a magistrate, and that if he gives her any of his impertinence he will be punished terribly.", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"마부에게 이 부인의 삼촌이 치안판사라는 걸, 그리고 만일 이 부인에게 조금이라도 무례하게 굴면 끔찍하게 벌을 받을 거라는 걸 일러둬요."], ["You can pretend to say that, if you please, Towlinson, in a friendly way, and because you know it was done to another man, who died.”", "미안하지만 그렇게 말해 주면 돼요, 타울린슨, 친근한 투로, 그리고 또 다른 어떤 남자한테 그렇게 했더니 그 사람이 죽었다는 걸 당신이 알고 있어서 그러는 것처럼 말이에요.\""]], [["“Certainly, Miss,”", "\"물론입니다, 아가씨,\""], ["said Towlinson.", "타울린슨이 말했다."]], [["“And now good-night to my sweet, sweet, sweet, godson,”", "\"자 그럼 이제 잘 자거라, 내 사랑스럽고, 사랑스럽고, 사랑스러운 대자야,\""], ["said Miss Tox, with a soft shower of kisses at each repetition of the adjective; “and Louisa, my dear friend, promise me to take a little something warm before you go to bed, and not to distress yourself!”", "톡스 양이 형용사를 되풀이할 때마다 부드러운 입맞춤을 한바탕 퍼부으며 말했다. \"그리고 루이자, 내 소중한 벗이여, 자기 전에 따뜻한 걸 좀 들고, 너무 괴로워하지 않겠다고 약속해 줘요!\""]], [["It was with extreme difficulty that Nipper, the black-eyed, who looked on steadfastly, contained herself at this crisis, and until the subsequent departure of Mrs Chick.", "검은 눈의 니퍼는 이 고비에, 그리고 그 뒤 칙 부인이 떠날 때까지, 줄곧 꼼짝 않고 지켜보면서 자신을 억누르느라 더없이 애를 먹었다."], ["But the nursery being at length free of visitors, she made herself some recompense for her late restraint.", "그러나 마침내 육아실에 손님이 없어지자, 그녀는 조금 전 억눌렀던 것에 대한 보상을 스스로에게 톡톡히 베풀었다."]], [["“You might keep me in a strait-waistcoat for six weeks,”", "\"나를 육 주 동안 구속복에 가둬 둔다 해도,\" 니퍼가 말했다, \"그걸 벗고 나면 더 화만 돋울걸요, 저 두 그리핀* 같은 인간들을 본 적이 있어요, 리처즈 부인?\""], ["said Nipper, “and when I got it off I’d only be more aggravated, who ever heard the like of them two Griffins, Mrs Richards?”", "* 그리핀: 사자 몸에 독수리 머리와 날개를 단 전설 속 괴수. 여기서는 못마땅한 두 부인을 빗댄 말."]], [["“And then to talk of having been dreaming, poor dear!”", "\"게다가 꿈을 꾼 거라느니, 가엾은 우리 아가!\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“Oh you beauties!”", "\"오, 잘들 노시는군!\""], ["cried Susan Nipper, affecting to salute the door by which the ladies had departed.", "수전 니퍼가 두 부인이 떠난 문을 향해 인사하는 시늉을 하며 외쳤다."], ["“Never be a Dombey won’t she?", "\"결코 돔비가 되지 못할 거라고요?"], ["It’s to be hoped she won’t, we don’t want any more such, one’s enough.”", "안 되기를 바라야지요, 우린 더는 그런 사람 필요 없으니까, 하나로 충분하다고요.\""]], [["“Don’t wake the children, Susan dear,”", "\"애들 깨우지 마요, 수전,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“I’m very much beholden to you, Mrs Richards,”", "\"리처즈 부인, 정말 황송하기 그지없네요,\""], ["said Susan, who was not by any means discriminating in her wrath, “and really feel it as a honour to receive your commands, being a black slave and a mulotter.", "수전이 말했는데, 그녀는 도무지 화풀이 대상을 가리는 법이 없었다, \"검둥이 노예에 흑백 혼혈인 주제에 부인의 분부를 받드는 것을 영광으로 여긴답니다."], ["Mrs Richards, if there’s any other orders, you can give me, pray mention ’em.”", "리처즈 부인, 다른 분부가 또 있으면 어서 말씀하시지요.\""]], [["“Nonsense; orders,”", "\"무슨 말도 안 되는 소리, 분부라니,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["bless your heart, Mrs Richards,”", "어쩜 이렇게 마음씨도 고우실까, 리처즈 부인,\""], ["cried Susan, “temporaries always orders permanencies here, didn’t you know that, why wherever was you born, Mrs Richards?", "수전이 외쳤다, \"이 집에선 임시직이 항상 정규직한테 분부를 내린답니다, 그것도 몰랐어요, 아니 대체 어디서 태어나신 거예요, 리처즈 부인?"], ["But wherever you was born, Mrs Richards,”", "하지만 어디서 태어났든, 리처즈 부인,\""], ["pursued Spitfire, shaking her head resolutely, “and whenever, and however (which is best known to yourself), you may bear in mind, please, that it’s one thing to give orders, and quite another thing to take ’em.", "스핏파이어*가 단호히 고개를 저으며 말을 이었다, \"그리고 언제, 어떻게 태어났든(그건 부인 자신이 제일 잘 알겠지요만), 부디 명심하세요, 분부를 내리는 것과 그걸 받드는 것은 전혀 별개의 일이라는 걸요. 어떤 사람이 다른 사람한테 다리에서 머리부터 거꾸로 사십오 피트 물속으로 뛰어들라고 시킬 순 있어요, 리처즈 부인, 하지만 그 사람이 뛰어드는 것과는 거리가 한참 멀 수도 있다고요.\""], ["A person may tell a person to dive off a bridge head foremost into five-and-forty feet of water, Mrs Richards, but a person may be very far from diving.”", "* 스핏파이어: '성깔 있는 사람'을 뜻하는 별명. 여기서는 수전 니퍼를 가리킨다."]], [["“There now,”", "\"이것 봐요,\""], ["said Polly, “you’re angry because you’re a good little thing, and fond of Miss Florence; and yet you turn round on me, because there’s nobody else.”", "폴리가 말했다, \"당신은 착한 사람인 데다 플로렌스 아가씨를 좋아하니까 화가 난 거예요. 그런데 달리 화풀이할 사람이 없으니 나한테 돌아서는 거고요.\""]], [["“It’s very easy for some to keep their tempers, and be soft-spoken, Mrs Richards,”", "\"어떤 사람들은 성질 참기도, 부드럽게 말하기도 쉽지요, 리처즈 부인,\""], ["returned Susan, slightly mollified, “when their child’s made as much of as a prince, and is petted and patted till it wishes its friends further, but when a sweet young pretty innocent, that never ought to have a cross word spoken to or of it, is rundown, the case is very different indeed.", "수전이 조금 누그러져 응수했다, \"제 아이가 왕자님처럼 떠받들어지고, 제 곁 사람들이 차라리 멀리 가 버렸으면 싶을 만큼 쓰다듬어지고 어루만져질 땐 말이에요. 하지만 모진 말 한마디 듣지도, 들어서도 안 될 사랑스럽고 어리고 예쁘고 죄 없는 아이가 깎아내려질 땐, 사정이 정말 영 딴판이지요."], ["My goodness gracious me, Miss Floy, you naughty, sinful child, if you don’t shut your eyes this minute, I’ll call in them hobgoblins that lives in the cock-loft to come and eat you up alive!”", "어머나 세상에, 플로이 아가씨, 이 못되고 나쁜 아이야, 당장 눈 안 감으면, 다락에 사는 도깨비들을 불러다가 산 채로 잡아먹게 할 테야!\""]], [["Here Miss Nipper made a horrible lowing, supposed to issue from a conscientious goblin of the bull species, impatient to discharge the severe duty of his position.", "여기서 니퍼 양은 자기 직책의 엄중한 의무를 어서 다하지 못해 안달이 난 어떤 양심적인 황소 도깨비에게서 나옴직한 무시무시한 울음소리를 흉내 냈다."], ["Having further composed her young charge by covering her head with the bedclothes, and making three or four angry dabs at the pillow, she folded her arms, and screwed up her mouth, and sat looking at the fire for the rest of the evening.", "그러고는 이불로 어린 아가씨의 머리를 덮고 베개를 서너 번 성난 손길로 두들겨 어린 보호 대상을 더 진정시킨 뒤, 팔짱을 끼고 입을 꼭 다문 채, 저녁 내내 난롯불을 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["Though little Paul was said, in nursery phrase, “to take a deal of notice for his age,” he took as little notice of all this as of the preparations for his christening on the next day but one; which nevertheless went on about him, as to his personal apparel, and that of his sister and the two nurses, with great activity.", "어린 폴은 육아실 식 표현으로 \"제 나이치고 꽤 눈치가 빠르다\"는 말을 들었지만, 정작 이 모든 일에 대해서는 그다음다음 날의 세례식 준비에 무관심하듯 별 관심을 두지 않았다."], ["Neither did he, on the arrival of the appointed morning, show any sense of its importance; being, on the contrary, unusually inclined to sleep, and unusually inclined to take it ill in his attendants that they dressed him to go out.", "그럼에도 그 준비는, 그의 옷차림이며 누이의 옷차림이며 두 유모의 옷차림에 이르기까지, 그를 둘러싸고 매우 활기차게 진행되었다. 또한 그는 정해진 날 아침이 밝아 와도 그날의 중요성을 조금도 알아차리는 기색이 없었으니, 오히려 유난히 잠이 쏟아지고, 외출 차림으로 옷을 입히는 시중꾼들에게 유난히 못마땅해할 뿐이었다."]], [["It happened to be an iron-grey autumnal day, with a shrewd east wind blowing—a day in keeping with the proceedings.", "마침 무쇠처럼 잿빛인 가을날이었고, 날카로운 동풍이 불고 있었다—이날의 행사와 잘 어울리는 날씨였다."], ["Mr Dombey represented in himself the wind, the shade, and the autumn of the christening.", "돔비 씨는 그 자신이 이 세례식의 바람이요, 그늘이요, 가을이었다."], ["He stood in his library to receive the company, as hard and cold as the weather; and when he looked out through the glass room, at the trees in the little garden, their brown and yellow leaves came fluttering down, as if he blighted them.", "그는 날씨만큼이나 굳고 차갑게 서재에 서서 손님들을 맞이했다. 유리로 된 방을 통해 작은 정원의 나무들을 내다보자, 갈색과 노란색 잎들이 마치 그가 시들게 하기라도 한 듯 팔랑팔랑 떨어져 내렸다."]], [["Ugh!", "윽!"], ["They were black, cold rooms; and seemed to be in mourning, like the inmates of the house.", "검고 차가운 방들이었다. 마치 이 집 식구들처럼 상복을 입고 있는 듯했다."], ["The books precisely matched as to size, and drawn up in line, like soldiers, looked in their cold, hard, slippery uniforms, as if they had but one idea among them, and that was a freezer.", "책들은 크기가 정확히 맞춰져, 병사들처럼 줄지어 늘어선 채, 그 차갑고 딱딱하고 매끈한 제복 차림으로, 마치 자기들끼리 단 하나의 관념만을 품고 있는 듯, 그리고 그것이 냉동기인 듯 보였다."], ["The bookcase, glazed and locked, repudiated all familiarities.", "유리를 끼우고 자물쇠를 채운 책장은 일체의 친밀함을 거부했다."], ["Mr Pitt, in bronze, on the top, with no trace of his celestial origin about him, guarded the unattainable treasure like an enchanted Moor.", "청동상이 된 피트 씨*는 그 천상의 기원이라곤 흔적도 남기지 않은 채 맨 꼭대기에서, 마법에 걸린 무어인처럼 그 가닿을 수 없는 보물을 지키고 있었다."], ["A dusty urn at each high corner, dug up from an ancient tomb, preached desolation and decay, as from two pulpits; and the chimney-glass, reflecting Mr Dombey and his portrait at one blow, seemed fraught with melancholy meditations.", "높다란 모퉁이마다 놓인, 옛 무덤에서 파낸 먼지투성이 항아리 하나하나가 두 개의 설교단에서처럼 황폐와 쇠락을 설교했다. 그리고 돔비 씨와 그의 초상화를 단번에 비추는 벽난로 거울은 우울한 명상으로 가득 찬 듯했다."]], [["The stiff and stark fire-irons appeared to claim a nearer relationship than anything else there to Mr Dombey, with his buttoned coat, his white cravat, his heavy gold watch-chain, and his creaking boots.", "* 피트 씨: 윌리엄 피트(영국 총리)의 흉상을 말한다. 뻣뻣하고 앙상한 난로 연장들은, 단추 채운 외투에 흰 크라바트, 묵직한 금시곗줄, 삐걱거리는 부츠를 갖춘 돔비 씨와, 그곳의 다른 무엇보다 더 가까운 친족 관계를 주장하는 듯 보였다."], ["But this was before the arrival of Mr and Mrs Chick, his lawful relatives, who soon presented themselves.", "그러나 이는 그의 합법적 친척인 칙 부부가 도착하기 전의 일이었고, 그들은 곧 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["“My dear Paul,”", "\"폴 오라버니,\""], ["Mrs Chick murmured, as she embraced him, “the beginning, I hope, of many joyful days!”", "칙 부인이 그를 끌어안으며 속삭였다, \"수많은 기쁜 날들의 시작이 되기를 바라요!\""]], [["“Thank you, Louisa,”", "\"고맙다, 루이자,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, grimly.", "돔비 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."], ["“How do you do, Mr John?”", "\"잘 지냈나, 존?\""]], [["“How do you do, Sir?”", "\"안녕하십니까, 처남?\""], ["said Chick.", "칙이 말했다."]], [["He gave Mr Dombey his hand, as if he feared it might electrify him.", "그는 마치 감전이라도 될까 두려운 듯 돔비 씨에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["Mr Dombey took it as if it were a fish, or seaweed, or some such clammy substance, and immediately returned it to him with exalted politeness.", "돔비 씨는 그것을 마치 물고기나 해초, 또는 그 비슷한 물컹한 물건이라도 되는 양 받아 들고는, 곧바로 한껏 정중하게 그에게 돌려주었다."]], [["“Perhaps, Louisa,”", "\"루이자,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, slightly turning his head in his cravat, as if it were a socket, “you would have preferred a fire?”", "돔비 씨가 마치 크라바트가 받침대라도 되는 양 그 안에서 고개를 살짝 돌리며 말했다, \"혹시 불을 피우는 편이 좋았겠느냐?\""]], [["“Oh, my dear Paul, no,”", "\"오, 폴 오라버니, 아니에요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, who had much ado to keep her teeth from chattering; “not for me.”", "이를 딱딱 부딪치지 않으려고 무진 애를 쓰며 칙 부인이 말했다, \"저는 괜찮아요.\""]], [["“Mr John,”", "\"존,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “you are not sensible of any chill?”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"자넨 한기가 느껴지지 않나?\""]], [["Mr John, who had already got both his hands in his pockets over the wrists, and was on the very threshold of that same canine chorus which had given Mrs Chick so much offence on a former occasion, protested that he was perfectly comfortable.", "존은 이미 두 손을 손목까지 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채, 전에 칙 부인을 그토록 거슬리게 했던 바로 그 개 짖는 듯한 합창의 문턱에 막 서 있던 참이었는데, 자기는 더없이 편안하다고 단언했다."]], [["He added in a low voice, “With my tiddle tol toor rul”—when he was providentially stopped by Towlinson, who announced:", "그는 낮은 목소리로 \"내 티들 톨 투르 룰\" 하고 덧붙였다—그때 마침 다행스럽게도 타울린슨이 가로막으며 알렸다. \"톡스 양 드십니다!\""]], [["“Miss Tox!”", "그러자 그 어여쁜 매혹자가 들어섰으니, 푸르스름한 코에 형언할 수 없이 얼어붙은 얼굴이었다."], ["And enter that fair enslaver, with a blue nose and indescribably frosty face, referable to her being very thinly clad in a maze of fluttering odds and ends, to do honour to the ceremony.", "이는 그녀가 의식에 예를 표하느라 펄럭이는 자투리들의 미로 속에 아주 얇게 차려입은 탓이었다."]], [["“How do you do, Miss Tox?”", "\"안녕하시오, 톡스 양?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["Miss Tox, in the midst of her spreading gauzes, went down altogether like an opera-glass shutting-up; she curtseyed so low, in acknowledgment of Mr Dombey’s advancing a step or two to meet her.", "톡스 양은 펼쳐진 거즈 천 한가운데서 마치 오페라글라스가 접히듯 그대로 폭 내려앉았다. 그녀를 맞으려 돔비 씨가 한두 걸음 다가서자, 그것에 답하느라 어찌나 낮게 무릎을 굽혀 인사했던지."]], [["“I can never forget this occasion, Sir,”", "\"저는 결코 이날을 잊지 못할 거예요, 나리,\""], ["said Miss Tox, softly.", "톡스 양이 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“’Tis impossible.", "\"도무지 불가능하지요."], ["My dear Louisa, I can hardly believe the evidence of my senses.”", "사랑하는 루이자, 내 오감의 증거를 믿기조차 어렵네요.\""]], [["If Miss Tox could believe the evidence of one of her senses, it was a very cold day. That was quite clear.", "만일 톡스 양이 자기 오감 중 하나의 증거를 믿을 수 있었다면, 그날은 무척 추운 날이었다."], ["She took an early opportunity of promoting the circulation in the tip of her nose by secretly chafing it with her pocket handkerchief, lest, by its very low temperature, it should disagreeably astonish the baby when she came to kiss it.", "그것만은 아주 분명했다. 그녀는 일찌감치 기회를 잡아, 코끝의 혈액 순환을 돕고자 손수건으로 남몰래 코를 문질렀으니, 아기에게 입을 맞출 때 그 너무도 낮은 온도로 아기를 불쾌하게 놀라게 할까 봐서였다."]], [["The baby soon appeared, carried in great glory by Richards; while Florence, in custody of that active young constable, Susan Nipper, brought up the rear.", "아기는 곧 모습을 드러냈는데, 리처즈가 한껏 위풍당당하게 안고 나왔다. 한편 플로렌스는 저 민첩한 어린 순경 수전 니퍼의 호위를 받으며 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Though the whole nursery party were dressed by this time in lighter mourning than at first, there was enough in the appearance of the bereaved children to make the day no brighter.", "육아실 일행은 이때쯤 처음보다는 옅은 상복 차림이었으나, 그래도 부모 잃은 아이들의 모습에는 그날을 조금도 더 밝게 해 줄 수 없게 만드는 무언가가 충분히 깃들어 있었다."], ["The baby too—it might have been Miss Tox’s nose—began to cry.", "아기도 또한—어쩌면 톡스 양의 코 때문이었는지—울기 시작했다."], ["Thereby, as it happened, preventing Mr Chick from the awkward fulfilment of a very honest purpose he had; which was, to make much of Florence.", "마침 그 바람에, 칙 씨가 자기 나름의 매우 정직한 뜻을 어색하게 실행에 옮기는 일을 막았으니, 그 뜻이란 플로렌스를 귀여워해 주려는 것이었다."], ["For this gentleman, insensible to the superior claims of a perfect Dombey (perhaps on account of having the honour to be united to a Dombey himself, and being familiar with excellence), really liked her, and showed that he liked her, and was about to show it in his own way now, when Paul cried, and his helpmate stopped him short—", "이 신사는, 완벽한 돔비의 우월한 권리에는 둔감했으나(어쩌면 자기 자신이 돔비 집안과 결합하는 영예를 누린 까닭에, 그 빼어남에 익숙해진 탓이었으리라), 진심으로 그 아이를 좋아했고, 또 좋아한다는 것을 드러냈으며, 지금 막 제 나름의 방식으로 그것을 드러내려던 참이었다. 그런데 폴이 울음을 터뜨렸고, 그의 조력자가 그를 다급히 가로막았다—"]], [["“Now Florence, child!”", "\"자 플로렌스, 얘야!\""], ["said her aunt, briskly, “what are you doing, love?", "그녀의 고모가 활기차게 말했다, \"뭐 하고 있니, 아가?"], ["Show yourself to him.", "동생한테 네 모습을 보여 주렴."], ["Engage his attention, my dear!”", "동생의 관심을 끌어 봐, 얘야!\""]], [["The atmosphere became or might have become colder and colder, when Mr Dombey stood frigidly watching his little daughter, who, clapping her hands, and standing on tip-toe before the throne of his son and heir, lured him to bend down from his high estate, and look at her.", "돔비 씨가 자기 어린 딸을 차갑게 지켜보며 서 있는 동안, 분위기는 점점 더 차가워졌거나, 그럴 뻔했다. 딸아이는 손뼉을 치며 발끝으로 서서, 아들이자 상속자인 동생의 옥좌 앞에서, 그 높은 지위에서 몸을 굽혀 자기를 봐 달라고 동생을 꾀었다."], ["Some honest act of Richards’s may have aided the effect, but he did look down, and held his peace.", "리처즈의 어떤 정직한 행동이 그 효과를 거들었는지도 모르나, 아무튼 동생은 정말로 아래를 내려다보았고, 잠자코 있었다."], ["As his sister hid behind her nurse, he followed her with his eyes; and when she peeped out with a merry cry to him, he sprang up and crowed lustily—laughing outright when she ran in upon him; and seeming to fondle her curls with his tiny hands, while she smothered him with kisses.", "누이가 유모 뒤로 숨자, 동생은 눈으로 그녀를 좇았다. 그리고 그녀가 동생에게 즐겁게 소리치며 빼꼼 내다보자, 동생은 벌떡 일어나 우렁차게 까르륵 소리를 냈고—그녀가 자기에게 달려들자 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸으며—그녀가 입맞춤으로 동생을 뒤덮는 동안, 자그마한 손으로 그녀의 곱슬머리를 어루만지는 듯했다."]], [["Was Mr Dombey pleased to see this?", "돔비 씨는 이 광경을 보고 기뻤을까?"], ["He testified no pleasure by the relaxation of a nerve; but outward tokens of any kind of feeling were unusual with him.", "그는 신경 하나 풀리는 것으로도 기쁨을 드러내지 않았다. 하지만 어떤 종류의 감정이든 그것을 겉으로 드러내는 일은 그에게 드문 일이었다."], ["If any sunbeam stole into the room to light the children at their play, it never reached his face.", "설령 어떤 햇살이 방 안으로 슬며시 들어와 노는 아이들을 비추었다 한들, 그것은 결코 그의 얼굴에 가닿지 못했다."], ["He looked on so fixedly and coldly, that the warm light vanished even from the laughing eyes of little Florence, when, at last, they happened to meet his.", "그가 어찌나 뚫어지게, 또 차갑게 바라보았던지, 마침내 어린 플로렌스의 웃는 눈이 우연히 그의 눈과 마주쳤을 때, 그 따뜻한 빛마저 사라져 버렸다."]], [["It was a dull, grey, autumn day indeed, and in a minute’s pause and silence that took place, the leaves fell sorrowfully.", "과연 흐리고 잿빛이 도는 가을날이었다. 그리고 잠시 말이 끊기고 침묵이 흐르는 동안, 잎들이 서글피 떨어져 내렸다."]], [["“Mr John,”", "\"존,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, referring to his watch, and assuming his hat and gloves.", "돔비 씨가 시계를 보며 모자와 장갑을 갖추고 말했다."], ["“Take my sister, if you please: my arm today is Miss Tox’s.", "\"미안하지만 내 누이를 부탁하네. 오늘 내 팔은 톡스 양의 것이라서."], ["You had better go first with Master Paul, Richards.", "리처즈, 자네가 폴 도련님을 모시고 먼저 가는 게 좋겠어."], ["Be very careful.”", "아주 조심하게.\""]], [["In Mr Dombey’s carriage, Dombey and Son, Miss Tox, Mrs Chick, Richards, and Florence.", "돔비 씨의 마차에는 돔비와 아들, 톡스 양, 칙 부인, 리처즈, 그리고 플로렌스가 탔다."], ["In a little carriage following it, Susan Nipper and the owner Mr Chick.", "그 뒤를 따르는 작은 마차에는 수전 니퍼와 그 마차의 주인 격인 칙 씨가 탔다."], ["Susan looking out of window, without intermission, as a relief from the embarrassment of confronting the large face of that gentleman, and thinking whenever anything rattled that he was putting up in paper an appropriate pecuniary compliment for herself.", "수전은 저 신사의 커다란 얼굴을 마주하는 거북함을 면하려고 쉴 새 없이 창밖을 내다보며, 무언가 달그락거리는 소리가 날 때마다 그가 자기에게 줄 적당한 금전적 사례를 종이에 싸고 있는 거라고 생각했다."]], [["Once upon the road to church, Mr Dombey clapped his hands for the amusement of his son.", "교회로 가는 길에 한 번, 돔비 씨는 아들을 즐겁게 해 주려고 손뼉을 쳤다."], ["At which instance of parental enthusiasm Miss Tox was enchanted.", "이런 부성애의 발로에 톡스 양은 황홀해했다."], ["But exclusive of this incident, the chief difference between the christening party and a party in a mourning coach consisted in the colours of the carriage and horses.", "그러나 이 일을 빼면, 세례식 일행과 상가 마차에 탄 일행 사이의 주된 차이라곤 마차와 말의 빛깔뿐이었다."]], [["Arrived at the church steps, they were received by a portentous beadle.", "교회 계단에 다다르자, 그들은 위풍당당한 교구지기의 영접을 받았다."], ["Mr Dombey dismounting first to help the ladies out, and standing near him at the church door, looked like another beadle.", "돔비 씨는 부인들을 내리는 것을 돕고자 먼저 마차에서 내려, 교회 문 가까이 그의 곁에 서니, 또 다른 교구지기처럼 보였다."], ["A beadle less gorgeous but more dreadful; the beadle of private life; the beadle of our business and our bosoms.", "덜 호화롭지만 더 무시무시한 교구지기, 사생활의 교구지기, 우리 일과 우리 가슴속의 교구지기 말이다."]], [["Miss Tox’s hand trembled as she slipped it through Mr Dombey’s arm, and felt herself escorted up the steps, preceded by a cocked hat and a Babylonian collar.", "톡스 양은 자기 손을 돔비 씨의 팔에 슬며시 끼고, 삼각모와 바빌론식 옷깃을 앞세워 계단을 인도받아 오르는 자신을 느끼며, 손을 떨었다."], ["It seemed for a moment like that other solemn institution, “Wilt thou have this man, Lucretia?”", "그 순간 그것은 마치 또 다른 엄숙한 제도와도 같았다, \"그대는 이 남자를 맞이하겠소, 루크레티아?\""], ["“Yes, I will.”", "\"예, 그러겠어요.\""]], [["“Please to bring the child in quick out of the air there,”", "\"바람 부는 그곳에서 어서 아이를 안으로 데려오십시오,\""], ["whispered the beadle, holding open the inner door of the church.", "교구지기가 교회 안쪽 문을 열어 잡으며 속삭였다."]], [["Little Paul might have asked with Hamlet “into my grave?” so chill and earthy was the place.", "어린 폴은 햄릿처럼 \"내 무덤 속으로?\"* 하고 물을 법도 했으니, 그곳은 그토록 한기 가득하고 흙냄새가 났다."], ["The tall, shrouded pulpit and reading desk; the dreary perspective of empty pews stretching away under the galleries, and empty benches mounting to the roof and lost in the shadow of the great grim organ; the dusty matting and cold stone slabs; the grisly free seats in the aisles; and the damp corner by the bell-rope, where the black trestles used for funerals were stowed away, along with some shovels and baskets, and a coil or two of deadly-looking rope; the strange, unusual, uncomfortable smell, and the cadaverous light; were all in unison.", "천으로 가린 높다란 설교단과 낭독대, 회랑 아래로 멀리 뻗은 빈 신도석들의 음울한 풍경, 지붕까지 솟아올라 거대하고 음산한 오르간의 그늘 속으로 사라지는 빈 의자들, 먼지투성이 깔개와 차가운 돌판들, 측랑의 으스스한 무료 좌석들, 그리고 종 줄 옆 축축한 모퉁이—거기엔 장례에 쓰는 검은 받침대들이 삽 몇 자루, 바구니 몇 개, 죽음을 연상시키는 밧줄 한두 사리와 함께 치워져 있었다—그 낯설고 유별나고 불쾌한 냄새, 그리고 시체 같은 빛까지, 이 모든 것이 한 가락으로 어우러져 있었다. 그것은 춥고 음울한 광경이었다."], ["It was a cold and dismal scene.", "* \"내 무덤 속으로?\": 셰익스피어 『햄릿』에서 따온 구절."]], [["“There’s a wedding just on, Sir,”", "\"방금 결혼식이 하나 진행 중입니다, 나리,\""], ["said the beadle, “but it’ll be over directly, if you’ll walk into the westry here.”", "교구지기가 말했다, \"하지만 곧 끝날 테니, 이쪽 제의실로 들어와 계시면 됩니다.\""]], [["Before he turned again to lead the way, he gave Mr Dombey a bow and a half smile of recognition, importing that he (the beadle) remembered to have had the pleasure of attending on him when he buried his wife, and hoped he had enjoyed himself since.", "다시 앞장서 길을 안내하려 돌아서기 전에, 그는 돔비 씨에게 절을 하고 알아본다는 듯 반쯤 미소를 지었으니, 그 뜻인즉 자기(교구지기)가 그분이 부인을 묻을 때 모시는 기쁨을 누렸던 것을 기억하고 있으며, 그 후로 즐겁게 지내셨기를 바란다는 것이었다."]], [["The very wedding looked dismal as they passed in front of the altar.", "제단 앞을 지날 때 그 결혼식조차 음울해 보였다."], ["The bride was too old and the bridegroom too young, and a superannuated beau with one eye and an eyeglass stuck in its blank companion, was giving away the lady, while the friends were shivering.", "신부는 너무 늙었고 신랑은 너무 젊었으며, 한쪽 눈은 멀어 그 텅 빈 눈에 안경알을 박은, 한물간 멋쟁이가 신부를 넘겨주고 있었고, 하객들은 떨고 있었다."], ["In the vestry the fire was smoking; and an over-aged and over-worked and under-paid attorney’s clerk, “making a search,” was running his forefinger down the parchment pages of an immense register (one of a long series of similar volumes) gorged with burials.", "제의실에서는 난롯불이 연기를 피우고 있었고, 너무 늙고 너무 혹사당하고 너무 박봉인 변호사 사무원 하나가 \"기록을 뒤지며\", 매장 기록으로 가득 찬 어마어마한 등록부(비슷한 책들이 길게 이어진 가운데 한 권)의 양피지 책장을 집게손가락으로 짚어 내려가고 있었다."], ["Over the fireplace was a ground-plan of the vaults underneath the church; and Mr Chick, skimming the literary portion of it aloud, by way of enlivening the company, read the reference to Mrs Dombey’s tomb in full, before he could stop himself.", "벽난로 위에는 교회 아래 지하 납골당의 평면도가 걸려 있었는데, 칙 씨가 일행의 흥을 돋우려고 그 글자 부분을 소리 내어 훑어보다가, 미처 멈추기도 전에 돔비 부인의 무덤에 관한 항목을 빠짐없이 읽어 버리고 말았다."]], [["After another cold interval, a wheezy little pew-opener afflicted with an asthma, appropriate to the churchyard, if not to the church, summoned them to the font—a rigid marble basin which seemed to have been playing a churchyard game at cup and ball with its matter of fact pedestal, and to have been just that moment caught on the top of it.", "또 한 차례 추운 막간이 지난 뒤, 교회보다는 차라리 묘지에나 어울릴 천식을 앓는, 쌕쌕거리는 자그마한 좌석 안내인이 그들을 세례반으로 불렀다. 그것은 뻣뻣한 대리석 대야로, 무미건조한 받침대를 짝 삼아 컵앤볼* 놀이를 하다가 마침 그 위에 막 얹힌 채로 붙잡히기라도 한 듯 보였다."], ["Here they waited some little time while the marriage party enrolled themselves; and meanwhile the wheezy little pew-opener—partly in consequence of her infirmity, and partly that the marriage party might not forget her—went about the building coughing like a grampus.", "여기서 그들은 결혼식 일행이 등록을 마치는 동안 잠시 기다렸다. 그리고 그동안 쌕쌕거리는 자그마한 좌석 안내인은—일부는 그 병약함 탓이고, 일부는 결혼식 일행이 자기를 잊지 않게 하려는 셈으로—고래처럼 기침을 해 대며 건물 안을 돌아다녔다."]], [["Presently the clerk (the only cheerful-looking object there, and he was an undertaker) came up with a jug of warm water, and said something, as he poured it into the font, about taking the chill off; which millions of gallons boiling hot could not have done for the occasion.", "* 컵앤볼: 막대 끝의 컵에 실로 매단 공을 받아 올리는 놀이. 이윽고 사무원(그곳에서 유일하게 쾌활해 보이는 존재였는데, 정작 장의사였다)이 따뜻한 물 한 주전자를 들고 와서, 그것을 세례반에 부으며 한기를 가시게 하는 거라는 둥 무어라 말했으나, 그것은 끓는 물 수백만 갤런으로도 이번 경우에는 해낼 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Then the clergyman, an amiable and mild-looking young curate, but obviously afraid of the baby, appeared like the principal character in a ghost-story, “a tall figure all in white;”", "그러고 나서 성직자가, 상냥하고 온화해 보이는 젊은 부목사였으나 보아하니 아기를 무서워하는 기색이 역력했는데, 마치 유령 이야기의 주인공처럼 \"온통 흰옷을 입은 키 큰 형상\"으로 나타났다."], ["at sight of whom Paul rent the air with his cries, and never left off again till he was taken out black in the face.", "그 모습을 보자 폴은 울음으로 허공을 찢었고, 시퍼렇게 질린 얼굴로 들려 나갈 때까지 한 번도 그치지 않았다."]], [["Even when that event had happened, to the great relief of everybody, he was heard under the portico, during the rest of the ceremony, now fainter, now louder, now hushed, now bursting forth again with an irrepressible sense of his wrongs.", "모두에게 크나큰 안도를 안기며 그 일이 벌어진 뒤에도, 의식이 진행되는 내내 그가 현관 아래에서 우는 소리가 들려왔으니, 이제는 잦아들었다가, 이제는 커졌다가, 이제는 잠잠해졌다가, 다시금 억누를 수 없는 억울함을 토하며 터져 나오곤 했다."], ["This so distracted the attention of the two ladies, that Mrs Chick was constantly deploying into the centre aisle, to send out messages by the pew-opener, while Miss Tox kept her Prayer-book open at the Gunpowder Plot, and occasionally read responses from that service.", "이것이 두 부인의 주의를 어찌나 산만하게 했던지, 칙 부인은 좌석 안내인을 통해 전갈을 내보내려고 끊임없이 가운뎃 통로로 진출했고, 톡스 양은 자기 기도서를 화약 음모 사건* 부분에 펼쳐 놓은 채 이따금 그 예배문에서 응답 구절을 읽곤 했다."]], [["During the whole of these proceedings, Mr Dombey remained as impassive and gentlemanly as ever, and perhaps assisted in making it so cold, that the young curate smoked at the mouth as he read.", "* 화약 음모 사건: 1605년의 사건을 기념하는 예배가 영국 기도서에 실려 있었다. 이 모든 절차가 진행되는 동안, 돔비 씨는 여느 때처럼 냉담하고 신사다운 태도를 유지했으며, 어쩌면 그 덕에 어찌나 추워졌던지 젊은 부목사가 글을 읽는 동안 입에서 김이 새어 나올 정도였다."], ["The only time that he unbent his visage in the least, was when the clergyman, in delivering (very unaffectedly and simply) the closing exhortation, relative to the future examination of the child by the sponsors, happened to rest his eye on Mr Chick; and then Mr Dombey might have been seen to express by a majestic look, that he would like to catch him at it.", "그가 조금이라도 얼굴을 풀었던 단 한 번은, 성직자가 (매우 꾸밈없고 소박하게) 대부모가 장차 그 아이를 가르쳐 시험할 일에 관한 마무리 권면을 전하던 중, 마침 칙 씨에게 눈길을 멈추었을 때였다. 그때 돔비 씨는, 그가 그 일을 게을리하다가는 단단히 혼쭐을 내 주겠다는 뜻을 위엄 어린 눈빛으로 드러내는 듯 보였다."]], [["It might have been well for Mr Dombey, if he had thought of his own dignity a little less; and had thought of the great origin and purpose of the ceremony in which he took so formal and so stiff a part, a little more.", "돔비 씨가 자기 위엄에 대해서는 조금 덜 생각하고, 자기가 그토록 격식 차리고 뻣뻣하게 한몫 끼어든 그 의식의 위대한 기원과 목적에 대해서는 조금 더 생각했더라면, 그에게 더 좋았을지도 모른다."], ["His arrogance contrasted strangely with its history.", "그의 오만함은 그 의식의 내력과 묘하게 대조되었다."]], [["When it was all over, he again gave his arm to Miss Tox, and conducted her to the vestry, where he informed the clergyman how much pleasure it would have given him to have solicited the honour of his company at dinner, but for the unfortunate state of his household affairs.", "모든 것이 끝나자, 그는 다시 톡스 양에게 팔을 내주어 제의실로 인도했고, 거기서 성직자에게, 만찬에 모시는 영예를 청할 수 있었다면 자기로서는 얼마나 기뻤겠느냐만, 안타깝게도 집안 형편이 그러하지 못하다고 알렸다."], ["The register signed, and the fees paid, and the pew-opener (whose cough was very bad again) remembered, and the beadle gratified, and the sexton (who was accidentally on the doorsteps, looking with great interest at the weather) not forgotten, they got into the carriage again, and drove home in the same bleak fellowship.", "등록부에 서명을 마치고, 사례비를 치르고, 좌석 안내인(기침이 또 무척 심해진)을 챙기고, 교구지기를 흡족하게 해 주고, 묘지기(마침 우연히 문간 계단에 서서 날씨를 자못 흥미롭게 살피고 있던)도 잊지 않은 다음, 그들은 다시 마차에 올라 똑같이 황량한 동행 속에 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["There they found Mr Pitt turning up his nose at a cold collation, set forth in a cold pomp of glass and silver, and looking more like a dead dinner lying in state than a social refreshment.", "집에 도착하니 피트 씨*가 차가운 냉찬 앞에서 코를 치켜올리고 있었는데, 그 냉찬은 유리그릇과 은그릇의 싸늘한 위세 속에 차려져, 정겨운 다과라기보다는 죽은 만찬이 빈소에 안치된 꼴에 가까웠다."], ["On their arrival Miss Tox produced a mug for her godson, and Mr Chick a knife and fork and spoon in a case.", "그들이 도착하자 톡스 양은 대자에게 줄 머그잔을 내놓았고, 칙 씨는 갑에 든 나이프와 포크와 숟가락을 내놓았다."], ["Mr Dombey also produced a bracelet for Miss Tox; and, on the receipt of this token, Miss Tox was tenderly affected.", "돔비 씨도 톡스 양에게 줄 팔찌를 내놓았는데, 이 선물을 받자 톡스 양은 가슴 뭉클하게 감격했다. * 피트 씨: 앞서 언급한 청동 흉상을 가리킨다."]], [["“Mr John,”", "\"존,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “will you take the bottom of the table, if you please?", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"미안하지만 식탁 끝자리에 좀 앉아 주겠나?"], ["What have you got there, Mr John?”", "자네 앞에 놓인 건 뭔가, 존?\""]], [["“I have got a cold fillet of veal here, Sir,”", "\"여긴 송아지 등심 냉채가 있습니다, 처남,\""], ["replied Mr Chick, rubbing his numbed hands hard together.", "칙 씨가 곱은 두 손을 세게 비비며 대답했다."], ["“What have you got there, Sir?”", "\"거긴 뭐가 있습니까, 처남?\""]], [["“This,”", "\"이건,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, “is some cold preparation of calf’s head, I think.", "돔비 씨가 응수했다, \"송아지 머리 냉채인 것 같군."], ["I see cold fowls—ham—patties—salad—lobster.", "냉계며—햄이며—파이며—샐러드며—바닷가재가 보이는군."], ["Miss Tox will do me the honour of taking some wine?", "톡스 양, 포도주를 좀 드시는 영광을 베풀어 주시겠소?"], ["Champagne to Miss Tox.”", "톡스 양께 샴페인을.\""]], [["There was a toothache in everything.", "모든 것에 이가 시렸다."], ["The wine was so bitter cold that it forced a little scream from Miss Tox, which she had great difficulty in turning into a “Hem!”", "포도주는 어찌나 쓰리도록 차가웠던지 톡스 양에게서 작은 비명을 끌어냈고, 그녀는 그것을 \"에헴!\" 소리로 바꾸느라 무진 애를 먹었다."], ["The veal had come from such an airy pantry, that the first taste of it had struck a sensation as of cold lead to Mr Chick’s extremities.", "송아지 고기는 어찌나 바람 잘 통하는 식료품 저장실에서 나왔던지, 칙 씨는 그 첫맛에 사지에 차가운 납이 닿는 듯한 감각을 느꼈다."], ["Mr Dombey alone remained unmoved.", "오직 돔비 씨만이 꿈쩍하지 않았다."], ["He might have been hung up for sale at a Russian fair as a specimen of a frozen gentleman.", "그는 러시아 시장에 얼어붙은 신사의 표본으로 내걸려 팔려도 좋을 법했다."]], [["The prevailing influence was too much even for his sister.", "이 압도적인 기운은 그의 누이에게마저 너무 버거웠다."], ["She made no effort at flattery or small talk, and directed all her efforts to looking as warm as she could.", "그녀는 아첨이나 잡담을 시도하지도 않은 채, 가능한 한 따뜻해 보이려는 데에만 온 힘을 쏟았다."]], [["“Well, Sir,”", "\"자, 처남,\""], ["said Mr Chick, making a desperate plunge, after a long silence, and filling a glass of sherry; “I shall drink this, if you’ll allow me, Sir, to little Paul.”", "칙 씨가 긴 침묵 끝에 필사적으로 뛰어들 듯, 셰리주 한 잔을 채우며 말했다, \"괜찮으시다면, 처남, 이 술을 어린 폴을 위해 마시겠습니다.\""]], [["“Bless him!”", "\"아가에게 축복을!\""], ["murmured Miss Tox, taking a sip of wine.", "톡스 양이 포도주를 한 모금 마시며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Dear little Dombey!”", "\"사랑스러운 어린 돔비!\""], ["murmured Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Mr John,”", "\"존,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with severe gravity, “my son would feel and express himself obliged to you, I have no doubt, if he could appreciate the favour you have done him.", "돔비 씨가 엄숙하기 그지없는 진중함으로 말했다, \"내 아들이 자네가 베푼 호의를 헤아릴 수만 있다면, 의심할 바 없이 자네에게 고마움을 느끼고 또 그렇게 표현하겠지."], ["He will prove, in time to come, I trust, equal to any responsibility that the obliging disposition of his relations and friends, in private, or the onerous nature of our position, in public, may impose upon him.”", "그 애는 장차, 사적으로는 친척과 벗들의 호의로운 성품이, 또 공적으로는 우리 처지의 막중한 본성이 그에게 지울지도 모를 그 어떤 책임에도 능히 부응하리라고, 나는 믿네.\""]], [["The tone in which this was said admitting of nothing more, Mr Chick relapsed into low spirits and silence.", "이렇게 말하는 어조가 더 이상 무엇도 덧붙일 여지를 주지 않았으므로, 칙 씨는 다시금 풀죽은 침묵에 빠졌다."], ["Not so Miss Tox, who, having listened to Mr Dombey with even a more emphatic attention than usual, and with a more expressive tendency of her head to one side, now leant across the table, and said to Mrs Chick softly:", "그러나 톡스 양은 그렇지 않았으니, 평소보다 한층 더 힘주어 주의를 기울여, 또 한층 더 표나게 고개를 한쪽으로 기울인 채 돔비 씨의 말을 듣고 있던 그녀는, 이제 탁자 너머로 몸을 기울여 칙 부인에게 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“Louisa!”", "\"루이자!\""]], [["“My dear,”", "\"왜 그래, 얘?\""], ["said Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Onerous nature of our position in public may—I have forgotten the exact term.”", "\"공적으로 우리 처지의 막중한 본성이—그 정확한 말이 기억이 안 나네.\""]], [["“Expose him to,”", "\"그에게 노출시킬지도,\""], ["said Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Pardon me, my dear,”", "\"미안하지만, 얘,\""], ["returned Miss Tox, “I think not.", "톡스 양이 응수했다, \"아닌 것 같아."], ["It was more rounded and flowing.", "더 둥글고 유려했어."], ["Obliging disposition of relations and friends in private, or onerous nature of position in public—may—impose upon him!”", "사적으로는 친척과 벗들의 호의로운 성품이, 또 공적으로는 처지의 막중한 본성이—그에게—지울지도!\""]], [["“Impose upon him, to be sure,”", "\"그에게 지울지도, 그렇지,\""], ["said Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."]], [["Miss Tox struck her delicate hands together lightly, in triumph; and added, casting up her eyes, “eloquence indeed!”", "톡스 양은 의기양양하여 가냘픈 두 손을 가볍게 마주쳤다. 그러고는 눈을 치켜뜨며 덧붙였다, \"참으로 명연설이야!\""]], [["Mr Dombey, in the meanwhile, had issued orders for the attendance of Richards, who now entered curtseying, but without the baby; Paul being asleep after the fatigues of the morning.", "그러는 동안 돔비 씨는 리처즈를 들이라는 명을 내려 두었고, 그녀가 이제 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 들어왔으나 아기는 데려오지 않았다. 폴은 아침의 피로 끝에 잠들어 있었던 것이다."], ["Mr Dombey, having delivered a glass of wine to this vassal, addressed her in the following words: Miss Tox previously settling her head on one side, and making other little arrangements for engraving them on her heart.", "돔비 씨는 이 가신에게 포도주 한 잔을 건넨 다음, 다음과 같은 말로 그녀에게 일렀다. 톡스 양은 그에 앞서 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고, 그 말을 가슴에 새기려는 자잘한 채비들을 갖추었다."]], [["“During the six months or so, Richards, which have seen you an inmate of this house, you have done your duty.", "\"리처즈, 네가 이 집의 식구로 지낸 지난 여섯 달가량, 너는 네 의무를 다했다."], ["Desiring to connect some little service to you with this occasion, I considered how I could best effect that object, and I also advised with my sister, Mrs—”", "이 기회에 너에게 자그마한 보답을 결부시키고자, 나는 그 목적을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 이룰 수 있을까 궁리했고, 또 내 누이 부인과도—\""]], [["“Chick,”", "\"칙입니다,\""], ["interposed the gentleman of that name.", "그 이름을 가진 신사가 끼어들었다."]], [["“Oh, hush if you please!”", "\"아, 부디 좀 조용히 해요!\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."]], [["“I was about to say to you, Richards,”", "\"리처즈, 내가 너에게 말하려던 것은,\""], ["resumed Mr Dombey, with an appalling glance at Mr John, “that I was further assisted in my decision, by the recollection of a conversation I held with your husband in this room, on the occasion of your being hired, when he disclosed to me the melancholy fact that your family, himself at the head, were sunk and steeped in ignorance.”", "돔비 씨가 존을 향해 섬뜩한 눈길을 던지며 말을 이었다, \"내 결정에 한 가지 더 보탬이 된 것이 있었으니, 네가 고용될 때 이 방에서 네 남편과 나누었던 대화의 기억이다. 그때 그는 네 가족이, 그 자신을 우두머리로 하여, 무지에 빠져 푹 절어 있다는 그 서글픈 사실을 내게 털어놓았지.\""]], [["Richards quailed under the magnificence of the reproof.", "리처즈는 그 장엄한 질책 앞에 움츠러들었다."]], [["“I am far from being friendly,” pursued Mr Dombey, “to what is called by persons of levelling sentiments, general education.", "\"나는, 평등주의적 정서를 지닌 자들이 보편 교육이라 부르는 것과는 거리가 먼 사람이다,\""], ["But it is necessary that the inferior classes should continue to be taught to know their position, and to conduct themselves properly.", "돔비 씨가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 하층 계급은 자기 처지를 알고 처신을 바르게 하도록 계속 가르침을 받을 필요가 있지."], ["So far I approve of schools.", "그런 한에서 나는 학교를 찬성한다."], ["Having the power of nominating a child on the foundation of an ancient establishment, called (from a worshipful company) the Charitable Grinders; where not only is a wholesome education bestowed upon the scholars, but where a dress and badge is likewise provided for them; I have (first communicating, through Mrs Chick, with your family) nominated your eldest son to an existing vacancy; and he has this day, I am informed, assumed the habit.", "어느 유서 깊은 자선기관—(어느 명예로운 동업조합의 이름을 따서) 자선 연마공이라 불리는 곳—에 아이 하나를 추천할 권한이 내게 있는데, 그곳에서는 학생들에게 건전한 교육을 베풀 뿐 아니라 옷과 표장까지 마련해 준다. 나는 (먼저 칙 부인을 통해 네 가족과 연락을 취한 뒤) 네 맏아들을 빈자리에 추천했고, 들으니 그 애가 오늘 그 복장을 갖추어 입었다는구나."], ["The number of her son, I believe,”", "그 애의 번호가, 아마,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, turning to his sister and speaking of the child as if he were a hackney-coach, is one hundred and forty-seven.", "돔비 씨가 누이 쪽으로 돌아서며, 마치 그 아이가 삯마차이기라도 한 듯 말했다, \"백사십칠번이지."], ["Louisa, you can tell her.”", "루이자, 네가 알려 주려무나.\""]], [["“One hundred and forty-seven,”", "\"백사십칠번이에요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick “The dress, Richards, is a nice, warm, blue baize tailed coat and cap, turned up with orange coloured binding; red worsted stockings; and very strong leather small-clothes.", "칙 부인이 말했다. \"그 복장은, 리처즈, 멋지고 따뜻한 파란색 나사 연미복에 모자인데, 주황색 가두리로 접어 올렸고, 빨간 털 양말에, 아주 튼튼한 가죽 반바지란다."], ["One might wear the articles one’s self,”", "그런 물건이라면 자기가 직접 입어도,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with enthusiasm, “and be grateful.”", "칙 부인이 열을 올리며 말했다, \"고마워할 만하지.\""]], [["“There, Richards!”", "\"자, 리처즈!\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“Now, indeed, you may be proud.", "\"이제 정말 자랑스러워해도 되겠어요."], ["The Charitable Grinders!”", "자선 연마공이라니!\""]], [["“I am sure I am very much obliged, Sir,”", "\"정말 너무나 감사드립니다, 나리,\""], ["returned Richards faintly, “and take it very kind that you should remember my little ones.”", "리처즈가 가냘프게 응수했다, \"제 어린것들을 기억해 주셔서 더없이 고마우신 일이고요.\""], ["At the same time a vision of Biler as a Charitable Grinder, with his very small legs encased in the serviceable clothing described by Mrs Chick, swam before Richards’s eyes, and made them water.", "그와 동시에, 빌러*가 자선 연마공이 되어, 칙 부인이 묘사한 그 실용적인 옷에 그 자그마한 다리를 끼워 넣은 모습이 리처즈의 눈앞에 어른거려, 두 눈에 눈물이 고였다. * 빌러: 리처즈의 맏아들 로빈의 별명."]], [["“I am very glad to see you have so much feeling, Richards,”", "\"네가 그렇게 정이 깊은 걸 보니 정말 기쁘구나, 리처즈,\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."]], [["“It makes one almost hope, it really does,”", "\"이러면 거의 희망이 생겨요, 정말로요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, who prided herself on taking trustful views of human nature, “that there may yet be some faint spark of gratitude and right feeling in the world.”", "인간 본성을 신뢰의 눈으로 바라보는 것을 자랑으로 여기는 칙 부인이 말했다, \"이 세상에 아직도 감사와 올바른 정서의 희미한 불씨나마 남아 있을지 모른다는 희망이요.\""]], [["Richards deferred to these compliments by curtseying and murmuring her thanks; but finding it quite impossible to recover her spirits from the disorder into which they had been thrown by the image of her son in his precocious nether garments, she gradually approached the door and was heartily relieved to escape by it.", "리처즈는 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고 감사의 말을 중얼거리며 이런 칭찬들에 예를 표했다. 그러나 조숙한 아랫도리 차림을 한 아들의 모습에 휘저어진 마음의 혼란에서 도무지 기운을 되찾을 수 없자, 차츰 문 쪽으로 다가가더니 그리로 빠져나가게 되어 진심으로 안도했다."]], [["Such temporary indications of a partial thaw that had appeared with her, vanished with her; and the frost set in again, as cold and hard as ever.", "그녀와 함께 나타났던, 부분적인 해빙의 그런 일시적 기미는 그녀와 함께 사라졌다. 그리고 다시금 서리가 내려앉았으니, 여전히 차갑고 단단했다."], ["Mr Chick was twice heard to hum a tune at the bottom of the table, but on both occasions it was a fragment of the Dead March in Saul.", "칙 씨가 식탁 끝자리에서 곡조를 흥얼거리는 것이 두 번 들렸으나, 두 번 다 「사울」에 나오는 「장송 행진곡」\\의 한 토막이었다."], ["The party seemed to get colder and colder, and to be gradually resolving itself into a congealed and solid state, like the collation round which it was assembled.", "일행은 점점 더 차가워져 가는 듯했고, 자기들이 둘러앉은 냉찬처럼 점점 응고되어 단단한 상태로 굳어 가는 듯했다. 마침내 칙 부인이 톡스 양을 바라보았고, 톡스 양도 그 눈길을 되받았으며, 둘 다 일어서서 정말 갈 시간이 되었다고 말했다."], ["At length Mrs Chick looked at Miss Tox, and Miss Tox returned the look, and they both rose and said it was really time to go.", "돔비 씨가 이 통고를 완벽히 평정한 태도로 받아들이자, 그들은 그 신사에게 작별을 고하고, 이윽고 칙 씨의 보호 아래 떠났다."], ["Mr Dombey receiving this announcement with perfect equanimity, they took leave of that gentleman, and presently departed under the protection of Mr Chick; who, when they had turned their backs upon the house and left its master in his usual solitary state, put his hands in his pockets, threw himself back in the carriage, and whistled “With a hey ho chevy!”", "칙 씨는, 그들이 그 집에 등을 돌리고 그 주인을 여느 때의 외로운 처지에 남겨 두고 나서자, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 마차에 몸을 푹 젖힌 채 「헤이 호 체비!」\\를 처음부터 끝까지 휘파람으로 불었다. 그러면서 그 얼굴에는 어찌나 음울하고 무시무시한 반항의 표정을 떠올렸던지, 칙 부인은 감히 항의하거나 어떤 식으로든 그를 건드리지 못했다."], ["all through; conveying into his face as he did so, an expression of such gloomy and terrible defiance, that Mrs Chick dared not protest, or in any way molest him.", "* 「사울」의 「장송 행진곡」: 헨델의 오라토리오 「사울」에 나오는 곡. * 「헤이 호 체비!」: 당시의 사냥 노래."]], [["Richards, though she had little Paul on her lap, could not forget her own first-born.", "리처즈는 어린 폴을 무릎에 안고 있으면서도 자기 맏이를 잊을 수 없었다."], ["She felt it was ungrateful; but the influence of the day fell even on the Charitable Grinders, and she could hardly help regarding his pewter badge, number one hundred and forty-seven, as, somehow, a part of its formality and sternness.", "그것이 배은망덕한 일이라는 것을 그녀도 느꼈다. 그러나 그날의 기운은 자선 연마공들에게마저 드리워졌고, 그녀는 백사십칠번이라 새겨진 그의 백랍 표장이 어쩐지 그 격식과 엄격함의 일부인 양 여겨지는 것을 도무지 떨칠 수 없었다."], ["She spoke, too, in the nursery, of his “blessed legs,” and was again troubled by his spectre in uniform.", "그녀는 또한 육아실에서 그의 \"복받은 다리\"에 관해 이야기하다가, 제복 차림을 한 그의 환영에 다시금 시달렸다."]], [["“I don’t know what I wouldn’t give,”", "\"무엇이든 못 내줄 게 없어요,\""], ["said Polly, “to see the poor little dear before he gets used to ’em.”", "폴리가 말했다, \"그 가엾은 어린것이 그 옷에 익숙해지기 전에 한 번만 볼 수 있다면요.\""]], [["“Why, then, I tell you what, Mrs Richards,”", "\"아니, 그럼, 내 말 들어 봐요, 리처즈 부인,\""], ["retorted Nipper, who had been admitted to her confidence, “see him and make your mind easy.”", "그녀의 속내를 듣게 된 니퍼가 응수했다, \"가서 보고 마음 편히 가지세요.\""]], [["“Mr Dombey wouldn’t like it,”", "\"돔비 씨가 좋아하지 않을 거예요,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, wouldn’t he, Mrs Richards!”", "\"오, 그분이 안 좋아하시겠어요, 리처즈 부인!\""], ["retorted Nipper, “he’d like it very much, I think when he was asked.”", "니퍼가 응수했다, \"제 생각엔 여쭤보면 무척 좋아하실 텐데요.\""]], [["“You wouldn’t ask him, I suppose, at all?”", "\"그렇다고 당신이 그분께 여쭤보진 않겠죠, 설마?\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“No, Mrs Richards, quite contrairy,”", "\"아니죠, 리처즈 부인, 정반대예요,\""], ["returned Susan, “and them two inspectors Tox and Chick, not intending to be on duty tomorrow, as I heard ’em say, me and Miss Floy will go along with you tomorrow morning, and welcome, Mrs Richards, if you like, for we may as well walk there as up and down a street, and better too.”", "수전이 응수했다, \"그리고 저 두 감독관 톡스랑 칙은 내일은 근무할 생각이 없다는데, 그렇게들 말하는 걸 제가 들었거든요, 그러니 저랑 플로이 아가씨가 내일 아침 부인이랑 함께 갈게요, 그럼 좋잖아요, 리처즈 부인, 부인만 괜찮으시면요, 거리를 이리저리 오르내리느니 거기까지 걸어가는 게 차라리 낫고, 더 나은 일이니까요.\""]], [["Polly rejected the idea pretty stoutly at first; but by little and little she began to entertain it, as she entertained more and more distinctly the forbidden pictures of her children, and her own home.", "폴리는 처음에는 그 생각을 제법 단호히 물리쳤다. 그러나 자기 아이들과 자기 집의 금단의 그림들을 점점 더 또렷이 머릿속에 그리게 되면서, 조금씩 그 생각을 받아들이기 시작했다."], ["At length, arguing that there could be no great harm in calling for a moment at the door, she yielded to the Nipper proposition.", "마침내 그녀는, 잠깐 문 앞에 들르는 것쯤이야 큰 해가 될 리 없다고 따져 보고는, 니퍼의 제안에 굴복했다."]], [["The matter being settled thus, little Paul began to cry most piteously, as if he had a foreboding that no good would come of it.", "이렇게 일이 정해지자, 어린 폴이 어찌나 가엾게 울기 시작했던지, 마치 거기서 좋은 일이라곤 생기지 않으리라는 예감이라도 든 듯했다."]], [["“What’s the matter with the child?”", "\"아기가 왜 그러는 거예요?\""], ["asked Susan.", "수전이 물었다."]], [["“He’s cold, I think,”", "\"추운 것 같아요,\""], ["said Polly, walking with him to and fro, and hushing him.", "폴리가 아기를 안고 이리저리 거닐며 어르며 말했다."]], [["It was a bleak autumnal afternoon indeed; and as she walked, and hushed, and, glancing through the dreary windows, pressed the little fellow closer to her breast, the withered leaves came showering down.", "과연 쌀쌀한 가을 오후였다. 그리고 그녀가 거닐고, 어르고, 음울한 창문 너머를 흘끗 내다보며 그 어린것을 자기 가슴에 더 바짝 끌어안는 동안, 시든 잎들이 우수수 쏟아져 내렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI. Paul’s Second Deprivation", "# 제2장"]], [["Polly was beset by so many misgivings in the morning, that but for the incessant promptings of her black-eyed companion, she would have abandoned all thoughts of the expedition, and formally petitioned for leave to see number one hundred and forty-seven, under the awful shadow of Mr Dombey’s roof.", "폴리는 아침이 되자 너무도 많은 불안에 시달려서, 검은 눈의 동행이 끊임없이 부추기지 않았더라면 그 나들이에 대한 생각을 모두 접고, 돔비 씨의 지붕이라는 무시무시한 그늘 아래에서 백사십칠 번지를 보러 가게 해 달라고 정식으로 청을 넣었을 것이다."], ["But Susan who was personally disposed in favour of the excursion, and who (like Tony Lumpkin), if she could bear the disappointments of other people with tolerable fortitude, could not abide to disappoint herself, threw so many ingenious doubts in the way of this second thought, and stimulated the original intention with so many ingenious arguments, that almost as soon as Mr Dombey’s stately back was turned, and that gentleman was pursuing his daily road towards the City, his unconscious son was on his way to Staggs’s Gardens.", "그러나 수전은 그 소풍에 개인적으로 마음이 쏠려 있었고, (토니 럼킨처럼) 남의 실망은 그럭저럭 의연하게 견딜 수 있을지언정 자기 자신을 실망시키는 일만큼은 도무지 참지 못하는 성미여서, 이 두 번째 생각에 갖가지 교묘한 의문을 던져 놓고 본래의 의도를 갖가지 교묘한 논변으로 부추긴 끝에, 돔비 씨의 위엄 있는 등이 돌아서고 그 신사가 시티를 향해 날마다의 길을 좇아가기가 무섭게, 그의 영문 모르는 아들은 스택스 가든으로 가는 길에 올라 있었다."]], [["This euphonious locality was situated in a suburb, known by the inhabitants of Staggs’s Gardens by the name of Camberling Town; a designation which the Strangers’ Map of London, as printed (with a view to pleasant and commodious reference) on pocket handkerchiefs, condenses, with some show of reason, into Camden Town.", "이 듣기 좋은 지명의 동네는 어느 교외에 있었는데, 스택스 가든 주민들은 그곳을 캠벌링 타운이라 불렀다. 이 명칭을 (기분 좋고 편리하게 참조하라는 취지로) 손수건에 인쇄한 「외지인용 런던 지도」는 나름의 근거를 내세워 캠든 타운으로 줄여 놓았다."], ["Hither the two nurses bent their steps, accompanied by their charges; Richards carrying Paul, of course, and Susan leading little Florence by the hand, and giving her such jerks and pokes from time to time, as she considered it wholesome to administer.", "두 유모는 맡은 아이들을 데리고 이리로 발걸음을 옮겼다. 물론 리처즈가 폴을 안고, 수전이 어린 플로렌스의 손을 이끌면서, 때때로 건강에 좋다고 여겨질 만큼 아이를 홱홱 잡아당기고 쿡쿡 찔러 주었다."]], [["The first shock of a great earthquake had, just at that period, rent the whole neighbourhood to its centre.", "바로 그 무렵 큰 지진의 첫 충격이 동네 전체를 한복판까지 갈라놓았다."], ["Traces of its course were visible on every side.", "그 진로의 자취가 사방에 뚜렷이 드러나 있었다."], ["Houses were knocked down; streets broken through and stopped; deep pits and trenches dug in the ground; enormous heaps of earth and clay thrown up; buildings that were undermined and shaking, propped by great beams of wood.", "집들은 무너졌고, 거리는 뚫리다 막혔으며, 땅에는 깊은 구덩이와 도랑이 파였고, 거대한 흙더미와 진흙더미가 솟아올랐으며, 밑이 파헤쳐져 흔들리는 건물들은 굵은 목재 들보로 떠받쳐져 있었다."], ["Here, a chaos of carts, overthrown and jumbled together, lay topsy-turvy at the bottom of a steep unnatural hill; there, confused treasures of iron soaked and rusted in something that had accidentally become a pond.", "여기에는 뒤집히고 뒤엉킨 수레들이 가파르고 부자연스러운 언덕 밑바닥에 거꾸로 처박혀 있었고, 저기에는 우연히 못이 되어 버린 어떤 곳에서 쇠붙이의 어수선한 보물들이 물에 젖어 녹슬어 가고 있었다."], ["Everywhere were bridges that led nowhere; thoroughfares that were wholly impassable; Babel towers of chimneys, wanting half their height; temporary wooden houses and enclosures, in the most unlikely situations; carcases of ragged tenements, and fragments of unfinished walls and arches, and piles of scaffolding, and wildernesses of bricks, and giant forms of cranes, and tripods straddling above nothing.", "어디에나 아무 데로도 이어지지 않는 다리가 있었고, 전혀 통행할 수 없는 큰길이 있었으며, 높이가 절반밖에 안 되는 바벨탑 같은 굴뚝들과, 도무지 어울리지 않는 자리에 들어선 임시 목조 가옥과 울타리, 다 해진 셋집의 잔해, 미완성 벽과 아치의 파편, 비계 더미, 벽돌의 황야, 거대한 형체의 기중기들, 그리고 아무것도 없는 허공 위에 다리를 벌리고 선 삼각대들이 있었다."], ["There were a hundred thousand shapes and substances of incompleteness, wildly mingled out of their places, upside down, burrowing in the earth, aspiring in the air, mouldering in the water, and unintelligible as any dream.", "미완성의 형상과 실체가 십만 가지나 되어, 제자리를 벗어나 마구 뒤섞이고, 거꾸로 뒤집히고, 땅속을 파고들고, 허공을 향해 솟구치고, 물속에서 썩어 가며, 그 어떤 꿈처럼이나 알아볼 수 없었다."], ["Hot springs and fiery eruptions, the usual attendants upon earthquakes, lent their contributions of confusion to the scene.", "지진에 으레 따라붙는 온천과 화염의 분출이 이 광경에 혼란의 몫을 보태었다."], ["Boiling water hissed and heaved within dilapidated walls; whence, also, the glare and roar of flames came issuing forth; and mounds of ashes blocked up rights of way, and wholly changed the law and custom of the neighbourhood.", "허물어진 벽 안에서 끓는 물이 쉭쉭거리며 솟구쳤고, 그곳에서 또한 불길의 섬광과 굉음이 뿜어 나왔으며, 잿더미가 통행로를 막아 동네의 법도와 관습을 송두리째 바꾸어 놓았다."]], [["In short, the yet unfinished and unopened Railroad was in progress; and, from the very core of all this dire disorder, trailed smoothly away, upon its mighty course of civilisation and improvement.", "요컨대, 아직 완공되지도 개통되지도 않은 철도가 공사 중이었던 것이다. 그리고 이 끔찍한 무질서의 한복판으로부터, 문명과 발전의 그 웅대한 행로를 따라 매끄럽게 뻗어 나가고 있었다."]], [["But as yet, the neighbourhood was shy to own the Railroad.", "그러나 아직 동네는 그 철도를 인정하기를 꺼렸다."], ["One or two bold speculators had projected streets; and one had built a little, but had stopped among the mud and ashes to consider farther of it.", "대담한 투기꾼 한둘이 거리를 기획했고, 한 사람은 조금 짓다가 진흙과 잿더미 속에 멈추어 서서 좀 더 궁리해 보는 중이었다."], ["A bran-new Tavern, redolent of fresh mortar and size, and fronting nothing at all, had taken for its sign The Railway Arms; but that might be rash enterprise—and then it hoped to sell drink to the workmen.", "갓 바른 회반죽과 아교풀 냄새가 물씬 풍기는, 정면이 아무것도 마주하지 않은 신축 선술집 한 채가 「철도 정거장」이라는 간판을 내걸었는데, 그것은 경솔한 모험일 수도 있었으나, 어쨌든 인부들에게 술을 팔 수 있기를 바랐던 것이다."], ["So, the Excavators’ House of Call had sprung up from a beer-shop; and the old-established Ham and Beef Shop had become the Railway Eating House, with a roast leg of pork daily, through interested motives of a similar immediate and popular description.", "그리하여 한 맥줏집에서 「굴착 인부 집결소」가 생겨났고, 오래전부터 자리 잡은 「햄·소고기 가게」는 「철도 식당」이 되어, 비슷하게 당장 눈앞의 인기에 영합하는 속셈으로 날마다 돼지 다리 구이를 내놓았다."], ["Lodging-house keepers were favourable in like manner; and for the like reasons were not to be trusted.", "하숙집 주인들도 마찬가지로 호의적이었으나, 같은 이유로 신뢰할 수 없는 자들이었다."], ["The general belief was very slow.", "사람들의 일반적인 믿음은 매우 더뎠다."], ["There were frowzy fields, and cow-houses, and dunghills, and dustheaps, and ditches, and gardens, and summer-houses, and carpet-beating grounds, at the very door of the Railway.", "철도 바로 문턱에 곰팡내 나는 들판과 외양간과 거름더미와 쓰레기더미와 도랑과 텃밭과 정자와 양탄자 터는 마당이 있었다."], ["Little tumuli of oyster shells in the oyster season, and of lobster shells in the lobster season, and of broken crockery and faded cabbage leaves in all seasons, encroached upon its high places.", "굴 철에는 굴 껍데기, 바닷가재 철에는 바닷가재 껍데기, 그리고 사철 내내 깨진 그릇과 시든 양배추 잎이 작은 무덤처럼 쌓여 철도의 높은 자리를 잠식했다."], ["Posts, and rails, and old cautions to trespassers, and backs of mean houses, and patches of wretched vegetation, stared it out of countenance.", "기둥과 난간, 침입자에게 경고하는 낡은 푯말, 초라한 집들의 뒷면, 비참하게 자란 식물의 무더기들이 철도를 무안하게 노려보았다."], ["Nothing was the better for it, or thought of being so.", "철도 덕에 나아진 것은 아무것도 없었고, 나아지리라 생각하는 이도 없었다."], ["If the miserable waste ground lying near it could have laughed, it would have laughed it to scorn, like many of the miserable neighbours.", "만일 그 곁에 놓인 비참한 황무지가 웃을 수 있었더라면, 비참한 이웃들 가운데 많은 이들처럼, 그것을 비웃었을 것이다."]], [["Staggs’s Gardens was uncommonly incredulous.", "스택스 가든은 유달리 믿지 않았다."], ["It was a little row of houses, with little squalid patches of ground before them, fenced off with old doors, barrel staves, scraps of tarpaulin, and dead bushes; with bottomless tin kettles and exhausted iron fenders, thrust into the gaps.", "그것은 작은 집들이 한 줄로 늘어선 곳으로, 집 앞에는 초라한 땅뙈기가 조금씩 있었고, 낡은 문짝과 통널과 방수포 조각과 죽은 덤불로 울타리를 둘렀으며, 그 틈새에는 밑 빠진 양철 주전자와 다 닳은 무쇠 난로받이를 쑤셔 넣어 두었다."], ["Here, the Staggs’s Gardeners trained scarlet beans, kept fowls and rabbits, erected rotten summer-houses (one was an old boat), dried clothes, and smoked pipes.", "여기서 스택스 가든의 정원사들은 빨간 강낭콩을 길렀고, 닭과 토끼를 쳤으며, 썩어 가는 정자(그중 하나는 낡은 배였다)를 세우고, 빨래를 말리고, 담배를 피웠다."], ["Some were of opinion that Staggs’s Gardens derived its name from a deceased capitalist, one Mr Staggs, who had built it for his delectation.", "어떤 이들은 스택스 가든이라는 이름이 죽은 자본가, 곧 자기 즐길 거리로 그곳을 지었다는 어느 스택스 씨에게서 비롯되었다고 보았다."], ["Others, who had a natural taste for the country, held that it dated from those rural times when the antlered herd, under the familiar denomination of Staggses, had resorted to its shady precincts.", "또 시골에 대한 타고난 취향을 지닌 다른 이들은, 뿔 달린 무리가 스택스라는 친숙한 이름으로 그 그늘진 경내를 즐겨 찾던 그 전원의 시절로 거슬러 올라간다고 여겼다."], ["Be this as it may, Staggs’s Gardens was regarded by its population as a sacred grove not to be withered by Railroads; and so confident were they generally of its long outliving any such ridiculous inventions, that the master chimney-sweeper at the corner, who was understood to take the lead in the local politics of the Gardens, had publicly declared that on the occasion of the Railroad opening, if ever it did open, two of his boys should ascend the flues of his dwelling, with instructions to hail the failure with derisive cheers from the chimney-pots.", "그것이야 어찌 되었든, 스택스 가든은 그 주민들에게 철도 따위로는 시들지 않을 신성한 숲으로 여겨졌다. 또 그곳이 그런 우스꽝스러운 발명품보다 오래 살아남으리라 다들 어찌나 확신했던지, 그 가든의 지역 정치를 주도하는 것으로 알려진 길모퉁이의 굴뚝 청소부 우두머리는, 만일 철도가 개통되는 일이 있다면, 그 개통일에 자기 두 아들을 자기 집 굴뚝으로 올려 보내, 굴뚝 갓머리에서 조롱의 환호로 그 실패를 맞이하라고 일러두겠노라 공언한 터였다."]], [["To this unhallowed spot, the very name of which had hitherto been carefully concealed from Mr Dombey by his sister, was little Paul now borne by Fate and Richards", "이제껏 그 이름조차 누이의 손에 의해 돔비 씨에게서 조심스레 감추어져 온 이 불경한 곳으로, 어린 폴은 이제 운명과 리처즈에게 안겨 실려 왔다."]], [["“That’s my house, Susan,”", "\"저기가 우리 집이에요, 수전,\""], ["said Polly, pointing it out.", "폴리가 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“Is it, indeed, Mrs Richards?”", "\"정말요, 리처즈 부인?\""], ["said Susan, condescendingly.", "수전이 짐짓 거들먹거리며 말했다."]], [["“And there’s my sister Jemima at the door, I do declare”", "\"어머나, 문간에 우리 제미마가 있네,\""], ["cried Polly, “with my own sweet precious baby in her arms!”", "폴리가 외쳤다, \"내 사랑스럽고 귀한 아기를 품에 안고서!\""]], [["The sight added such an extensive pair of wings to Polly’s impatience, that she set off down the Gardens at a run, and bouncing on Jemima, changed babies with her in a twinkling; to the unutterable astonishment of that young damsel, on whom the heir of the Dombeys seemed to have fallen from the clouds.", "그 광경에 폴리의 조바심에 어찌나 큰 날개가 한 쌍 돋았던지, 그녀는 가든을 따라 달음박질치며 내려가 제미마에게 와락 안기더니, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 아기를 맞바꾸어 버렸다. 그 젊은 처녀는 형언할 수 없이 놀랐는데, 돔비가의 상속자가 그녀에게 마치 구름에서 떨어진 것만 같았던 것이다."]], [["“Why, Polly!”", "\"어머, 폴리!\""], ["cried Jemima. “You!", "제미마가 외쳤다."], ["what a turn you have given me!", "\"언니가! 깜짝 놀랐잖아!"], ["who’d have thought it!", "누가 생각이나 했겠어!"], ["come along in Polly!", "어서 들어와, 폴리!"], ["How well you do look to be sure!", "정말이지 얼굴 좋아 보이네!"], ["The children will go half wild to see you Polly, that they will.”", "애들이 언니를 보면 반쯤 미쳐 날뛸 거야, 폴리, 정말로 그럴걸.\""]], [["That they did, if one might judge from the noise they made, and the way in which they dashed at Polly and dragged her to a low chair in the chimney corner, where her own honest apple face became immediately the centre of a bunch of smaller pippins, all laying their rosy cheeks close to it, and all evidently the growth of the same tree.", "아이들이 내는 소리와 폴리에게 달려들어 굴뚝 모퉁이의 나지막한 의자로 끌고 가던 모양새로 미루어 보건대, 정말로 그랬다. 그 의자에서 폴리의 정직한 사과 같은 얼굴은 이내 더 작은 사과 알들의 한가운데가 되었으니, 모두가 발그레한 볼을 거기에 바짝 갖다 대었고, 모두가 분명히 같은 나무에서 자란 열매들이었다."], ["As to Polly, she was full as noisy and vehement as the children; and it was not until she was quite out of breath, and her hair was hanging all about her flushed face, and her new christening attire was very much dishevelled, that any pause took place in the confusion.", "폴리로 말하자면, 아이들 못지않게 시끌벅적하고 격렬했다. 그러다 마침내 숨이 완전히 차오르고, 머리카락이 발갛게 달아오른 얼굴 주위로 온통 흘러내리고, 새로 마련한 세례식 옷차림이 몹시 헝클어지고 나서야, 그 소란에 잠시 짬이 생겼다."], ["Even then, the smallest Toodle but one remained in her lap, holding on tight with both arms round her neck; while the smallest Toodle but two mounted on the back of the chair, and made desperate efforts, with one leg in the air, to kiss her round the corner.", "그때조차도 막내에서 둘째인 투들이 그녀의 무릎에 남아 두 팔로 그 목을 꼭 끌어안고 있었고, 막내에서 셋째인 투들은 의자 등받이에 올라타 한쪽 다리를 허공에 든 채 모퉁이로 돌아 그녀에게 입 맞추려고 필사의 노력을 기울였다."]], [["“Look!", "\"봐!"], ["there’s a pretty little lady come to see you,”", "너희를 보러 예쁜 꼬마 아가씨가 오셨단다,\""], ["said Polly; “and see how quiet she is!", "폴리가 말했다. \"그리고 얼마나 얌전한지 좀 보렴!"], ["what a beautiful little lady, ain’t she?”", "정말 아름다운 꼬마 아가씨지, 그렇지 않니?\""]], [["This reference to Florence, who had been standing by the door not unobservant of what passed, directed the attention of the younger branches towards her; and had likewise the happy effect of leading to the formal recognition of Miss Nipper, who was not quite free from a misgiving that she had been already slighted.", "플로렌스에 대한 이 언급은—그 자리에서 벌어진 일을 눈여겨보지 않은 것은 아닌 채 문간에 서 있던 그 아이에게로—어린 가지들의 주의를 돌렸을 뿐 아니라, 이미 자기가 푸대접을 받았다는 불안에서 그리 자유롭지 못했던 니퍼 양을 정식으로 알아보게 하는 행복한 효과까지 가져왔다."]], [["“Oh do come in and sit down a minute, Susan, please,”", "\"아 들어와서 잠깐 앉아요, 수전, 제발,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."], ["“This is my sister Jemima, this is.", "\"이쪽이 우리 동생 제미마예요. 이 사람이요."], ["Jemima, I don’t know what I should ever do with myself, if it wasn’t for Susan Nipper; I shouldn’t be here now but for her.”", "제미마, 수전 니퍼 양이 없었으면 내가 나 자신을 어떻게 했을지 모르겠어. 이 사람 아니었으면 지금 여기 오지도 못했을 거야.\""]], [["“Oh do sit down, Miss Nipper, if you please,”", "\"아 앉으세요, 니퍼 양, 제발,\""], ["quoth Jemima.", "제미마가 말했다."]], [["Susan took the extreme corner of a chair, with a stately and ceremonious aspect.", "수전은 위엄 있고 격식 차린 표정으로 의자의 맨 끝 모퉁이에 걸터앉았다."]], [["“I never was so glad to see anybody in all my life; now really I never was, Miss Nipper,”", "\"내 평생 누굴 보고 이렇게 반가웠던 적이 없어요. 정말이지, 정말 없었어요, 니퍼 양,\""], ["said Jemima.", "제미마가 말했다."]], [["Susan relaxing, took a little more of the chair, and smiled graciously.", "수전은 조금 누그러져서 의자를 조금 더 차지하고는, 우아하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“Do untie your bonnet-strings, and make yourself at home, Miss Nipper, please,”", "\"보닛 끈을 풀고 편히 계세요, 니퍼 양, 제발,\""], ["entreated Jemima.", "제미마가 간청했다."], ["“I am afraid it’s a poorer place than you’re used to; but you’ll make allowances, I’m sure.”", "\"익숙하신 데보다 누추한 곳이라 걱정이네요. 하지만 분명 너그러이 봐주시겠지요.\""]], [["The black-eyed was so softened by this deferential behaviour, that she caught up little Miss Toodle who was running past, and took her to Banbury Cross immediately.", "이 깍듯한 태도에 검은 눈은 어찌나 누그러졌던지, 마침 뛰어 지나가던 어린 투들 양을 와락 붙잡아 곧장 「밴버리 십자가」 놀이를 시켜 주었다."]], [["“But where’s my pretty boy?”", "\"그런데 우리 예쁜 사내아이는 어디 있니?\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."], ["“My poor fellow?", "\"내 가엾은 녀석은?"], ["I came all this way to see him in his new clothes.”", "새 옷 입은 모습을 보려고 이 먼 길을 왔는데.\""]], [["“Ah what a pity!”", "\"아 어쩌면 좋아!\""], ["cried Jemima.", "제미마가 외쳤다."], ["“He’ll break his heart, when he hears his mother has been here.", "\"엄마가 여기 왔었다는 걸 들으면 그 애 가슴이 미어질 거야."], ["He’s at school, Polly.”", "학교에 갔어, 폴리.\""]], [["“Gone already!”", "\"벌써 갔다고!\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"응."], ["He went for the first time yesterday, for fear he should lose any learning.", "배움을 조금이라도 놓칠까 봐 어제 처음으로 갔거든. 하지만 반공일이야, 폴리."], ["But it’s half-holiday, Polly: if you could only stop till he comes home—you and Miss Nipper, leastways,”", "그 애가 집에 올 때까지만 머물 수 있다면—언니랑 니퍼 양은 적어도 말이야,\""], ["said Jemima, mindful in good time of the dignity of the black-eyed.", "제미마가 검은 눈의 위신을 때맞춰 헤아리며 말했다."]], [["“And how does he look, Jemima, bless him!”", "\"그런데 그 애 모습이 어때, 제미마, 그 애 좀 봐 줘!\""], ["faltered Polly.", "폴리가 더듬거리며 물었다."]], [["“Well, really he don’t look so bad as you’d suppose,”", "\"음, 정말이지 언니가 짐작하는 만큼 나빠 보이진 않아,\""], ["returned Jemima.", "제미마가 대답했다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Polly, with emotion, “I knew his legs must be too short.”", "폴리가 감격하여 말했다. \"다리가 너무 짧을 게 틀림없다고 생각했어.\""]], [["“His legs is short,”", "\"다리가 짧긴 해,\" 제미마가 대답했다."], ["returned Jemima; “especially behind; but they’ll get longer, Polly, every day.”", "\"특히 뒤쪽이 그래. 하지만 날마다 길어질 거야, 폴리.\""]], [["It was a slow, prospective kind of consolation; but the cheerfulness and good nature with which it was administered, gave it a value it did not intrinsically possess.", "그것은 더디고 막연한 위로였다. 그러나 그것을 베푸는 명랑함과 선량함이, 본디 지니지 못한 값어치를 거기에 더해 주었다."], ["After a moment’s silence, Polly asked, in a more sprightly manner:", "잠시 침묵한 뒤, 폴리가 좀 더 활기찬 투로 물었다."]], [["“And where’s Father, Jemima dear?”—for by that patriarchal appellation, Mr Toodle was generally known in the family.", "\"그런데 아버지는 어디 계셔, 제미마 얘?\"—투들 씨는 집안에서 대개 이 가장다운 호칭으로 통했으니까."]], [["“There again!”", "\"또 그러네!\""], ["said Jemima.", "제미마가 말했다."], ["“What a pity!", "\"어쩌면 좋아!"], ["Father took his dinner with him this morning, and isn’t coming home till night.", "아버지는 오늘 아침 도시락을 싸 가셨거든, 그래서 밤이 되어야 오셔. 하지만 늘 언니 이야기를 하고, 애들한테 언니 얘기를 들려주신다니까."], ["But he’s always talking of you, Polly, and telling the children about you; and is the peaceablest, patientest, best-temperedest soul in the world, as he always was and will be!”", "그리고 세상에서 제일 온순하고, 제일 참을성 있고, 제일 너그러운 분이야. 늘 그러셨고 앞으로도 그러실 거야!\""]], [["“Thankee, Jemima,”", "\"고마워, 제미마,\""], ["cried the simple Polly; delighted by the speech, and disappointed by the absence.", "순박한 폴리가 외쳤다. 그 말에 기뻐하면서도, 만나지 못한 데 실망하면서."]], [["“Oh you needn’t thank me, Polly,”", "\"아 고마워할 거 없어, 폴리,\""], ["said her sister, giving her a sounding kiss upon the cheek, and then dancing little Paul cheerfully.", "동생이 그녀의 볼에 쪽 소리 나게 입을 맞추고는, 어린 폴을 즐겁게 어르며 말했다. \"나도 가끔 언니 얘기를 똑같이 하는걸."], ["“I say the same of you sometimes, and think it too.”", "그리고 진심으로 그렇게 생각하고.\""]], [["In spite of the double disappointment, it was impossible to regard in the light of a failure a visit which was greeted with such a reception; so the sisters talked hopefully about family matters, and about Biler, and about all his brothers and sisters: while the black-eyed, having performed several journeys to Banbury Cross and back, took sharp note of the furniture, the Dutch clock, the cupboard, the castle on the mantel-piece with red and green windows in it, susceptible of illumination by a candle-end within; and the pair of small black velvet kittens, each with a lady’s reticule in its mouth; regarded by the Staggs’s Gardeners as prodigies of imitative art.", "이중의 실망에도 불구하고, 이런 환대로 맞이된 방문을 실패로 여기기란 불가능했다. 그리하여 자매는 집안일에 대해, 바일러에 대해, 그 애의 형제자매 모두에 대해 희망찬 이야기를 나누었다."], ["The conversation soon becoming general lest the black-eyed should go off at score and turn sarcastic, that young lady related to Jemima a summary of everything she knew concerning Mr Dombey, his prospects, family, pursuits, and character.", "한편 검은 눈은 「밴버리 십자가」까지 몇 차례 다녀오고는, 가구며 네덜란드제 시계며 찬장이며 벽난로 선반 위의 성(城)—그 안에 빨강과 초록 창문이 있어 안쪽에 양초 토막을 넣으면 불을 밝힐 수 있었다—이며, 저마다 입에 부인용 손가방을 문 한 쌍의 작은 검은 벨벳 새끼 고양이를—스택스 가든의 정원사들이 모방 예술의 경이로 여기던 그것을—날카롭게 살폈다. 검은 눈이 함부로 빈정거리는 쪽으로 내달릴까 하여 대화는 이내 다 함께 나누는 것이 되었고, 그 젊은 아가씨는 제미마에게 자기가 돔비 씨와 그의 장래, 가족, 일, 성격에 관해 아는 모든 것을 요약해 들려주었다."], ["Also an exact inventory of her personal wardrobe, and some account of her principal relations and friends.", "또한 자기의 개인 의상 목록을 정확히 일러 주고, 자기의 주요 친척과 벗들에 대해서도 얼마간 설명했다."], ["Having relieved her mind of these disclosures, she partook of shrimps and porter, and evinced a disposition to swear eternal friendship.", "이런 폭로로 마음의 짐을 던 뒤, 그녀는 새우와 흑맥주를 들고는 영원한 우정을 맹세할 기색을 내비쳤다."]], [["Little Florence herself was not behind-hand in improving the occasion; for, being conducted forth by the young Toodles to inspect some toad-stools and other curiosities of the Gardens, she entered with them, heart and soul, on the formation of a temporary breakwater across a small green pool that had collected in a corner.", "어린 플로렌스 자신도 이 기회를 활용하는 데 뒤처지지 않았다. 어린 투들들의 안내로 가든의 독버섯이며 그 밖의 진기한 것들을 구경하러 나갔다가, 그 애들과 함께 마음과 영혼을 다해, 한쪽 구석에 고인 작은 초록빛 웅덩이를 가로지르는 임시 방파제 쌓기에 착수했던 것이다."], ["She was still busily engaged in that labour, when sought and found by Susan; who, such was her sense of duty, even under the humanizing influence of shrimps, delivered a moral address to her (punctuated with thumps) on her degenerate nature, while washing her face and hands; and predicted that she would bring the grey hairs of her family in general, with sorrow to the grave.", "그녀가 여전히 그 작업에 분주히 몰두해 있을 때, 수전이 찾아내었다. 새우의 인간미 어린 영향 아래에서조차 의무감이 어찌나 투철했던지, 수전은 그녀의 얼굴과 손을 씻기면서 그 타락한 천성을 두고 (퍽퍽 때리는 것으로 구두점을 찍어 가며) 일장 훈계를 늘어놓았고, 그녀가 온 집안 식구의 흰머리를 슬픔과 함께 무덤으로 데려가고 말 것이라 예언했다."], ["After some delay, occasioned by a pretty long confidential interview above stairs on pecuniary subjects, between Polly and Jemima, an interchange of babies was again effected—for Polly had all this time retained her own child, and Jemima little Paul—and the visitors took leave.", "폴리와 제미마가 위층에서 금전 문제에 관해 꽤 긴 은밀한 면담을 나누느라 얼마간 지체된 뒤—그동안 폴리는 줄곧 자기 아이를, 제미마는 어린 폴을 안고 있었으니—다시 아기를 맞바꾸었고, 방문객들은 작별을 고했다."]], [["But first the young Toodles, victims of a pious fraud, were deluded into repairing in a body to a chandler’s shop in the neighbourhood, for the ostensible purpose of spending a penny; and when the coast was quite clear, Polly fled: Jemima calling after her that if they could only go round towards the City Road on their way back, they would be sure to meet little Biler coming from school.", "그러나 우선 어린 투들들은 경건한 속임수의 희생양이 되어, 표면상 동전 한 푼 쓰러 간다는 명목으로, 다 함께 떼 지어 동네의 잡화점으로 가도록 유인되었다. 그리하여 사방이 완전히 트이자 폴리는 달아났다. 제미마는 돌아가는 길에 시티 로드 쪽으로 빙 돌아갈 수만 있다면, 학교에서 돌아오는 어린 바일러를 분명 만날 거라고 뒤에서 외쳐 댔다."]], [["“Do you think that we might make time to go a little round in that direction, Susan?”", "\"우리가 시간을 내서 그쪽으로 좀 돌아갈 수 있을까요, 수전?\""], ["inquired Polly, when they halted to take breath.", "폴리가 숨을 돌리려 멈춰 섰을 때 물었다."]], [["“Why not, Mrs Richards?”", "\"안 될 게 뭐 있어요, 리처즈 부인?\""], ["returned Susan.", "수전이 대꾸했다."]], [["“It’s getting on towards our dinner time you know,”", "\"우리 점심때가 다가오고 있잖아요, 아시다시피,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["But lunch had rendered her companion more than indifferent to this grave consideration, so she allowed no weight to it, and they resolved to go “a little round.”", "그러나 점심 요기가 그 동행으로 하여금 이 중대한 고려에 도무지 무관심하게 만들어 놓았던 터라, 수전은 거기에 아무런 무게도 두지 않았고, 두 사람은 \"조금 돌아가기로\" 결정했다."]], [["Now, it happened that poor Biler’s life had been, since yesterday morning, rendered weary by the costume of the Charitable Grinders.", "그런데, 가엾은 바일러의 삶은 어제 아침 이래로 「자선 연마공」의 복장 때문에 고달파져 있었다."], ["The youth of the streets could not endure it.", "거리의 청소년들은 그것을 참아 내지 못했다."], ["No young vagabond could be brought to bear its contemplation for a moment, without throwing himself upon the unoffending wearer, and doing him a mischief.", "어떤 어린 부랑아도 그것을 한순간 바라보고 견딜 수 없었고, 그러면 그 죄 없는 착용자에게 달려들어 해코지를 해 댔다."], ["His social existence had been more like that of an early Christian, than an innocent child of the nineteenth century.", "그의 사회적 존재는 십구 세기의 죄 없는 아이라기보다는 초기 기독교인의 그것에 더 가까웠다."], ["He had been stoned in the streets.", "그는 거리에서 돌팔매질을 당했다."], ["He had been overthrown into gutters; bespattered with mud; violently flattened against posts.", "시궁창에 처박혔고, 진흙을 뒤집어썼으며, 기둥에 거칠게 짓눌렸다."], ["Entire strangers to his person had lifted his yellow cap off his head, and cast it to the winds.", "그를 전혀 모르는 낯선 자들이 그의 노란 모자를 머리에서 벗겨 바람에 날려 버렸다."], ["His legs had not only undergone verbal criticisms and revilings, but had been handled and pinched.", "그의 다리는 말로 비판받고 욕먹었을 뿐 아니라, 만져지고 꼬집혔다."], ["That very morning, he had received a perfectly unsolicited black eye on his way to the Grinders’ establishment, and had been punished for it by the master: a superannuated old Grinder of savage disposition, who had been appointed schoolmaster because he didn’t know anything, and wasn’t fit for anything, and for whose cruel cane all chubby little boys had a perfect fascination.", "바로 그날 아침에는 연마공 학교로 가는 길에 전혀 청하지도 않은 눈탱이를 하나 얻어맞았는데, 그 일로 교장에게 벌을 받기까지 했다. 그 교장은 사나운 기질의 늙은 퇴역 연마공으로, 아무것도 아는 게 없고 무엇에도 쓸모가 없다는 이유로 교장에 임명된 자였으며, 그의 잔인한 회초리에 토실토실한 사내아이들은 하나같이 완전히 매료되어 있었다."]], [["Thus it fell out that Biler, on his way home, sought unfrequented paths; and slunk along by narrow passages and back streets, to avoid his tormentors.", "이리하여 바일러는 집으로 돌아가는 길에 사람 다니지 않는 길을 찾았고, 자기를 괴롭히는 자들을 피하려고 좁은 통로와 뒷골목을 따라 슬그머니 빠져나갔다."], ["Being compelled to emerge into the main road, his ill fortune brought him at last where a small party of boys, headed by a ferocious young butcher, were lying in wait for any means of pleasurable excitement that might happen.", "큰길로 나설 수밖에 없게 되자, 불운이 그를 마침내, 사나운 젊은 백정을 우두머리로 한 한 무리의 사내아이들이 무슨 신나는 흥밋거리든 생기기를 노리며 매복해 있던 곳으로 이끌었다."], ["These, finding a Charitable Grinder in the midst of them—unaccountably delivered over, as it were, into their hands—set up a general yell and rushed upon him.", "이들은 자기네 한복판에서, 말하자면 영문 모르게 자기네 손아귀에 떨어진 「자선 연마공」을 발견하자, 일제히 함성을 지르며 그에게 달려들었다."]], [["But it so fell out likewise, that, at the same time, Polly, looking hopelessly along the road before her, after a good hour’s walk, had said it was no use going any further, when suddenly she saw this sight.", "그러나 또한 마침 그때, 폴리가 족히 한 시간을 걸은 끝에 앞쪽 길을 절망적으로 살펴보며 이제 더 가 봤자 소용없다고 막 말하던 참에, 별안간 이 광경을 보게 되었다."], ["She no sooner saw it than, uttering a hasty exclamation, and giving Master Dombey to the black-eyed, she started to the rescue of her unhappy little son.", "그것을 보자마자 그녀는 다급한 외침을 내지르며 돔비 도련님을 검은 눈에게 떠넘기고는, 자기의 불행한 어린 아들을 구하러 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["Surprises, like misfortunes, rarely come alone.", "뜻밖의 일이란, 불행처럼, 좀처럼 홀로 오지 않는 법이다."], ["The astonished Susan Nipper and her two young charges were rescued by the bystanders from under the very wheels of a passing carriage before they knew what had happened; and at that moment (it was market day) a thundering alarm of “Mad Bull!”", "깜짝 놀란 수전 니퍼와 그녀가 맡은 두 아이는, 영문도 모르는 사이에, 지나가던 마차 바퀴 바로 밑에서 구경꾼들에게 구조되었다. 그리고 바로 그 순간(장날이었다) \"미친 소다!\""], ["was raised.", "하는 우레 같은 경보가 일었다."]], [["With a wild confusion before her, of people running up and down, and shouting, and wheels running over them, and boys fighting, and mad bulls coming up, and the nurse in the midst of all these dangers being torn to pieces, Florence screamed and ran.", "사람들이 이리저리 내달리며 고함치고, 바퀴들이 그들을 깔아뭉개고, 사내아이들이 싸우고, 미친 소가 들이닥치고, 이 모든 위험의 한복판에서 유모가 갈가리 찢기는 어지러운 혼란을 눈앞에 두고서, 플로렌스는 비명을 지르며 달아났다."], ["She ran till she was exhausted, urging Susan to do the same; and then, stopping and wringing her hands as she remembered they had left the other nurse behind, found, with a sensation of terror not to be described, that she was quite alone.", "그녀는 지쳐 쓰러질 때까지 달렸고, 수전에게도 그러라고 다그쳤다. 그러다가 멈춰 서서, 다른 유모를 뒤에 두고 왔다는 것을 떠올리며 두 손을 비틀던 차에, 형언할 수 없는 공포의 감각과 더불어, 자기가 완전히 홀로라는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“Susan!", "\"수전!"], ["Susan!”", "수전!\""], ["cried Florence, clapping her hands in the very ecstasy of her alarm.", "플로렌스가 공포의 절정에서 두 손을 마주치며 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, where are they?", "\"오, 어디 있어요?"], ["where are they?”", "다들 어디 있어요?\""]], [["“Where are they?”", "\"다들 어디 있느냐고?\""], ["said an old woman, coming hobbling across as fast as she could from the opposite side of the way.", "한 늙은 여자가 길 건너편에서 할 수 있는 한 빨리 절뚝거리며 다가오면서 말했다."], ["“Why did you run away from ’em?”", "\"왜 그 사람들한테서 도망쳤어?\""]], [["“I was frightened,”", "\"무서웠어요,\""], ["answered Florence.", "플로렌스가 대답했다."], ["“I didn’t know what I did.", "\"제가 뭘 한 건지도 몰랐어요."], ["I thought they were with me.", "다들 저랑 같이 있는 줄 알았어요."], ["Where are they?”", "어디 있어요?\""]], [["The old woman took her by the wrist, and said, “I’ll show you.”", "늙은 여자는 그녀의 손목을 붙잡고 말했다, \"내가 보여 주마.\""]], [["She was a very ugly old woman, with red rims round her eyes, and a mouth that mumbled and chattered of itself when she was not speaking.", "그녀는 매우 못생긴 늙은 여자로, 눈가에 붉은 테가 둘려 있었고, 말을 하지 않을 때면 입이 저절로 우물거리며 떠들어 댔다. 그녀는 비참한 행색이었고, 팔에 가죽 몇 장을 걸치고 있었다."], ["She was miserably dressed, and carried some skins over her arm.", "어쨌든 플로렌스를 얼마간 뒤따라온 모양이었으니, 숨이 차 있었던 것이다."], ["She seemed to have followed Florence some little way at all events, for she had lost her breath; and this made her uglier still, as she stood trying to regain it: working her shrivelled yellow face and throat into all sorts of contortions.", "그래서 그녀는 숨을 돌리려 애쓰며 서 있는 동안 더더욱 못생겨 보였다. 쪼글쪼글한 누런 얼굴과 목을 온갖 모양으로 일그러뜨리면서."]], [["Florence was afraid of her, and looked, hesitating, up the street, of which she had almost reached the bottom.", "플로렌스는 그녀가 무서웠고, 망설이며, 거의 끝까지 다다른 그 거리 위쪽을 올려다보았다."], ["It was a solitary place—more a back road than a street—and there was no one in it but her-self and the old woman.", "그곳은 인적 없는 곳—거리라기보다는 뒷길에 가까운 곳—이었고, 그곳에는 그녀 자신과 늙은 여자 말고는 아무도 없었다."]], [["“You needn’t be frightened now,”", "\"이제 무서워할 거 없어,\""], ["said the old woman, still holding her tight.", "늙은 여자가 여전히 그녀를 꽉 붙든 채 말했다."], ["“Come along with me.”", "\"나랑 같이 가자.\""]], [["“I—I don’t know you.", "\"저—저는 할머니를 몰라요."], ["What’s your name?”", "이름이 뭐예요?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“Mrs Brown,”", "\"브라운 부인이란다,\""], ["said the old woman.", "늙은 여자가 말했다."], ["“Good Mrs Brown.”", "\"착한 브라운 부인이지.\""]], [["“Are they near here?”", "\"다들 이 근처에 있어요?\""], ["asked Florence, beginning to be led away.", "플로렌스가 끌려가기 시작하면서 물었다."]], [["“Susan ain’t far off,”", "\"수전은 멀지 않은 데 있어,\""], ["said Good Mrs Brown; “and the others are close to her.”", "착한 브라운 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 다른 사람들은 그 옆에 바짝 붙어 있고.\""]], [["“Is anybody hurt?”", "\"다친 사람은 없어요?\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."]], [["“Not a bit of it,”", "\"하나도 없지,\""], ["said Good Mrs Brown.", "착한 브라운 부인이 말했다."]], [["The child shed tears of delight on hearing this, and accompanied the old woman willingly; though she could not help glancing at her face as they went along—particularly at that industrious mouth—and wondering whether Bad Mrs Brown, if there were such a person, was at all like her.", "아이는 이 말을 듣고 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리며 늙은 여자를 기꺼이 따라갔다. 그러면서도 가는 동안 그 얼굴을—특히 그 부지런히 움직이는 입을—흘끔거리며, 만일 나쁜 브라운 부인이라는 사람이 있다면 이 사람과 조금이라도 닮았을까 궁금해하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["They had not gone far, but had gone by some very uncomfortable places, such as brick-fields and tile-yards, when the old woman turned down a dirty lane, where the mud lay in deep black ruts in the middle of the road.", "멀리 가지는 않았으나, 벽돌 굽는 들판이며 기와 마당 같은 매우 불쾌한 곳들을 지나, 늙은 여자는 어느 더러운 골목으로 꺾어 들었는데, 그곳은 길 한복판에 진흙이 깊고 검은 바큇자국을 이루며 깔려 있었다."], ["She stopped before a shabby little house, as closely shut up as a house that was full of cracks and crevices could be.", "그녀는 초라한 작은 집 앞에 멈춰 섰는데, 그 집은 갈라진 틈과 균열로 가득한 집이 닫힐 수 있는 한 빈틈없이 닫혀 있었다."], ["Opening the door with a key she took out of her bonnet, she pushed the child before her into a back room, where there was a great heap of rags of different colours lying on the floor; a heap of bones, and a heap of sifted dust or cinders; but there was no furniture at all, and the walls and ceiling were quite black.", "보닛에서 꺼낸 열쇠로 문을 열고, 그녀는 아이를 떠밀어 안쪽 방으로 들여보냈다. 그곳에는 갖가지 색깔의 누더기 더미가 바닥에 잔뜩 쌓여 있었고, 뼈 무더기와 체로 거른 먼지나 잿가루 더미가 있었으나, 가구라고는 전혀 없었으며, 벽과 천장이 온통 새까맸다."]], [["The child became so terrified the she was stricken speechless, and looked as though about to swoon.", "아이는 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지 말문이 막혀 버렸고, 금방이라도 까무러칠 것처럼 보였다."]], [["“Now don’t be a young mule,”", "\"자 어린 노새처럼 굴지 마,\""], ["said Good Mrs Brown, reviving her with a shake.", "착한 브라운 부인이 한 차례 흔들어 아이를 정신 차리게 하며 말했다."], ["“I’m not a going to hurt you.", "\"널 해치진 않을 거야."], ["Sit upon the rags.”", "누더기 위에 앉아.\""]], [["Florence obeyed her, holding out her folded hands, in mute supplication.", "플로렌스는 그 말에 따랐고, 두 손을 모아 내밀며 말없이 애원했다."]], [["“I’m not a going to keep you, even, above an hour,”", "\"한 시간 넘게 널 붙잡아 두지도 않을 거야,\""], ["said Mrs Brown.", "브라운 부인이 말했다."], ["“D’ye understand what I say?”", "\"내 말 알아듣겠어?\""]], [["The child answered with great difficulty, “Yes.”", "아이는 몹시 힘겹게 \"네\"라고 대답했다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["said Good Mrs Brown, taking her own seat on the bones, “don’t vex me.", "착한 브라운 부인이 뼈 무더기 위에 자기 자리를 잡고 앉으며 말했다, \"날 성가시게 하지 마."], ["If you don’t, I tell you I won’t hurt you.", "그러지만 않으면, 내 말하건대, 널 해치지 않을 거야."], ["But if you do, I’ll kill you.", "하지만 성가시게 굴면, 널 죽여 버릴 테니까."], ["I could have you killed at any time—even if you was in your own bed at home.", "난 언제든 너를 죽게 만들 수 있어—네가 집에서 네 침대에 누워 있다 해도 말이야."], ["Now let’s know who you are, and what you are, and all about it.”", "자 이제 네가 누군지, 뭐 하는 애인지, 그 모든 걸 좀 알아보자.\""]], [["The old woman’s threats and promises; the dread of giving her offence; and the habit, unusual to a child, but almost natural to Florence now, of being quiet, and repressing what she felt, and feared, and hoped; enabled her to do this bidding, and to tell her little history, or what she knew of it.", "늙은 여자의 위협과 약속, 그녀의 비위를 거스를까 하는 두려움, 그리고 아이에게는 드물지만 이제 플로렌스에게는 거의 천성이 되어 버린 습성—조용히 있으면서, 자기가 느끼고 두려워하고 바라는 것을 억누르는 습성—이 그 분부를 따르게 하고, 자기의 짧은 내력을, 아니 자기가 아는 만큼을 이야기하게 해 주었다."], ["Mrs Brown listened attentively, until she had finished.", "브라운 부인은 아이가 다 마칠 때까지 주의 깊게 들었다."]], [["“So your name’s Dombey, eh?”", "\"그래 네 이름이 돔비라고, 어?\""], ["said Mrs Brown.", "브라운 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I want that pretty frock, Miss Dombey,”", "\"저 예쁜 원피스가 갖고 싶구나, 돔비 아가씨,\""], ["said Good Mrs Brown, “and that little bonnet, and a petticoat or two, and anything else you can spare.", "착한 브라운 부인이 말했다, \"그리고 저 작은 보닛하고, 페티코트 한두 장하고, 또 네가 내줄 수 있는 건 뭐든."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Take ’em off.”", "벗어.\""]], [["Florence obeyed, as fast as her trembling hands would allow; keeping, all the while, a frightened eye on Mrs Brown.", "플로렌스는 떨리는 손이 허락하는 한 빨리 따랐다. 그러는 내내 겁먹은 눈을 브라운 부인에게서 떼지 못한 채로."], ["When she had divested herself of all the articles of apparel mentioned by that lady, Mrs B. examined them at leisure, and seemed tolerably well satisfied with their quality and value.", "그 부인이 말한 의류를 죄다 벗어 내자, 브라운 부인은 그것들을 느긋이 살펴보더니, 그 품질과 값어치에 그럭저럭 흡족해하는 듯했다."]], [["“Humph!”", "\"흠!\""], ["she said, running her eyes over the child’s slight figure, “I don’t see anything else—except the shoes.", "그녀가 아이의 가냘픈 몸을 눈으로 훑으며 말했다, \"신발 말고는 별다른 게 안 보이는구나."], ["I must have the shoes, Miss Dombey.”", "신발도 가져야겠다, 돔비 아가씨.\""]], [["Poor little Florence took them off with equal alacrity, only too glad to have any more means of conciliation about her.", "가엾은 어린 플로렌스는 똑같이 선뜻 그것을 벗었으니, 자기 곁에 비위를 맞출 수단이 더 생긴 것이 그저 기쁘기만 했다."], ["The old woman then produced some wretched substitutes from the bottom of the heap of rags, which she turned up for that purpose; together with a girl’s cloak, quite worn out and very old; and the crushed remains of a bonnet that had probably been picked up from some ditch or dunghill.", "그러자 늙은 여자는 그러려고 뒤집어엎은 누더기 더미 바닥에서 형편없는 대용품 몇 가지를 꺼내 왔다. 다 닳아 빠지고 몹시 낡은 소녀용 망토와, 아마도 어느 도랑이나 거름더미에서 주워 왔을 짓이겨진 보닛 잔해와 함께였다."], ["In this dainty raiment, she instructed Florence to dress herself; and as such preparation seemed a prelude to her release, the child complied with increased readiness, if possible.", "이 우아한 의복을 입으라고 그녀는 플로렌스에게 일렀고, 그런 준비가 풀려날 전조처럼 보였기에 아이는, 가능하다면, 한층 더 선뜻 따랐다."]], [["In hurriedly putting on the bonnet, if that may be called a bonnet which was more like a pad to carry loads on, she caught it in her hair which grew luxuriantly, and could not immediately disentangle it.", "서둘러 보닛을 쓰다가—짐을 머리에 일 때 받치는 깔개에 더 가까운 것을 보닛이라 부를 수 있다면—그것이 풍성하게 자란 머리카락에 걸렸는데, 좀처럼 즉시 풀 수가 없었다."], ["Good Mrs Brown whipped out a large pair of scissors, and fell into an unaccountable state of excitement.", "착한 브라운 부인은 큼직한 가위를 휙 꺼내 들더니, 영문 모를 흥분 상태에 빠졌다."]], [["“Why couldn’t you let me be!”", "\"왜 가만히 못 놔둬!\""], ["said Mrs Brown, “when I was contented?", "브라운 부인이 말했다, \"내가 만족하고 있었는데!"], ["You little fool!”", "이 어린 바보야!\""]], [["“I beg your pardon.", "\"죄송해요."], ["I don’t know what I have done,”", "제가 뭘 잘못했는지 모르겠어요,\""], ["panted Florence.", "플로렌스가 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["“I couldn’t help it.”", "\"어쩔 수가 없었어요.\""]], [["“Couldn’t help it!”", "\"어쩔 수 없었다고!\""], ["cried Mrs Brown.", "브라운 부인이 외쳤다."], ["“How do you expect I can help it?", "\"그럼 내가 어떻게 참을 수 있겠니?"], ["Why, Lord!”", "아니, 세상에!\""], ["said the old woman, ruffling her curls with a furious pleasure, “anybody but me would have had ’em off, first of all.”", "늙은 여자가 사나운 즐거움에 차 아이의 곱슬머리를 헝클어뜨리며 말했다, \"나 말고 다른 사람이었으면 진작에 죄다 잘라 버렸을 거야.\""]], [["Florence was so relieved to find that it was only her hair and not her head which Mrs Brown coveted, that she offered no resistance or entreaty, and merely raised her mild eyes towards the face of that good soul.", "플로렌스는 브라운 부인이 탐낸 것이 자기 머리일 뿐 자기 목숨이 아니라는 것을 알고 어찌나 안심했던지, 아무 저항도 애원도 하지 않고, 그저 온순한 눈을 그 착한 사람의 얼굴을 향해 들어 올릴 따름이었다."]], [["“If I hadn’t once had a gal of my own—beyond seas now—that was proud of her hair,”", "\"나도 한때 내 딸이 있었지—지금은 바다 건너에 있지만—제 머리카락을 자랑스러워하던 애였지,\""], ["said Mrs Brown, “I’d have had every lock of it.", "브라운 부인이 말했다, \"그 애만 아니었으면 한 올도 남김없이 잘라 버렸을 거야."], ["She’s far away, she’s far away!", "그 애는 멀리 가 있어, 멀리 가 있다고!"], ["Oho!", "오호!"], ["Oho!”", "오호!\""]], [["Mrs Brown’s was not a melodious cry, but, accompanied with a wild tossing up of her lean arms, it was full of passionate grief, and thrilled to the heart of Florence, whom it frightened more than ever.", "브라운 부인의 울음은 가락 좋은 것이 아니었으나, 비쩍 마른 두 팔을 거칠게 치켜드는 것과 어우러져, 격정적인 비탄으로 가득했고, 플로렌스의 가슴까지 떨리게 했으니, 아이는 어느 때보다 더 겁에 질렸다."], ["It had its part, perhaps, in saving her curls; for Mrs Brown, after hovering about her with the scissors for some moments, like a new kind of butterfly, bade her hide them under the bonnet and let no trace of them escape to tempt her.", "어쩌면 그것이 그녀의 곱슬머리를 구하는 데 한몫했는지도 모른다. 브라운 부인은 가위를 들고서 한동안 그녀의 주위를, 마치 새로운 종류의 나비처럼 맴돌더니, 머리카락을 보닛 밑에 감추고 한 올도 빠져나와 자기를 유혹하는 일이 없게 하라고 일렀던 것이다."], ["Having accomplished this victory over herself, Mrs Brown resumed her seat on the bones, and smoked a very short black pipe, mowing and mumbling all the time, as if she were eating the stem.", "자기 자신에 대한 이 승리를 거두고 나서, 브라운 부인은 다시 뼈 무더기 위 자기 자리에 앉아, 매우 짧은 검은 담뱃대를 피웠는데, 줄곧 무언가를 씹어 대고 우물거리는 것이 마치 담뱃대 자루를 먹고 있는 것만 같았다."]], [["When the pipe was smoked out, she gave the child a rabbit-skin to carry, that she might appear the more like her ordinary companion, and told her that she was now going to lead her to a public street whence she could inquire her way to her friends.", "담뱃대를 다 피우자, 그녀는 아이에게 토끼 가죽을 하나 들려 주어, 늘 데리고 다니는 자기 동행처럼 더 보이게 했다. 그러고는 이제 사람들이 다니는 거리로 데려가, 거기서 벗들에게 가는 길을 물어볼 수 있게 해 주겠다고 말했다."], ["But she cautioned her, with threats of summary and deadly vengeance in case of disobedience, not to talk to strangers, nor to repair to her own home (which may have been too near for Mrs Brown’s convenience), but to her father’s office in the City; also to wait at the street corner where she would be left, until the clock struck three.", "그러나 만일 말을 듣지 않으면 즉결로 치명적인 복수를 하겠다는 위협을 곁들여, 낯선 이들에게 말을 걸지 말 것이며, (브라운 부인의 편의에는 너무 가까웠을지도 모를) 제 집으로 가지 말고, 시티에 있는 아버지의 사무소로 가라고 단단히 일렀다. 또 자기가 두고 갈 길모퉁이에서 세 시를 칠 때까지 기다리라고도 했다."], ["These directions Mrs Brown enforced with assurances that there would be potent eyes and ears in her employment cognizant of all she did; and these directions Florence promised faithfully and earnestly to observe.", "이 지시들을, 브라운 부인은 자기가 부리는 막강한 눈과 귀가 아이가 하는 모든 일을 다 알고 있을 거라는 다짐으로써 다그쳤다. 그리고 이 지시들을 플로렌스는 충실하고 간절하게 지키겠노라 약속했다."]], [["At length, Mrs Brown, issuing forth, conducted her changed and ragged little friend through a labyrinth of narrow streets and lanes and alleys, which emerged, after a long time, upon a stable yard, with a gateway at the end, whence the roar of a great thoroughfare made itself audible.", "마침내 브라운 부인은 밖으로 나서, 변장하고 누더기를 걸친 자기 어린 벗을 좁은 거리와 골목과 뒷길의 미로 사이로 데리고 갔는데, 한참 만에 어느 마구간 마당으로 빠져나왔다. 그 끝에 대문이 하나 있었고, 거기서 큰 한길의 굉음이 들려왔다."], ["Pointing out this gateway, and informing Florence that when the clocks struck three she was to go to the left, Mrs Brown, after making a parting grasp at her hair which seemed involuntary and quite beyond her own control, told her she knew what to do, and bade her go and do it: remembering that she was watched.", "이 대문을 가리켜 보이며, 시계가 세 시를 치면 왼쪽으로 가라고 일러 준 뒤, 브라운 부인은 도무지 자기 뜻과는 상관없이 저절로 그러는 듯 마지막으로 아이의 머리카락을 한 번 움켜쥐고는, 무엇을 해야 할지 알 테니 가서 그대로 하라며, 감시당하고 있음을 잊지 말라고 일렀다."]], [["With a lighter heart, but still sore afraid, Florence felt herself released, and tripped off to the corner.", "한결 가벼워진 마음으로, 그러나 여전히 몹시 두려운 채, 플로렌스는 자기가 풀려난 것을 깨닫고 모퉁이로 종종걸음 쳐 갔다."], ["When she reached it, she looked back and saw the head of Good Mrs Brown peeping out of the low wooden passage, where she had issued her parting injunctions; likewise the fist of Good Mrs Brown shaking towards her.", "그곳에 다다라 뒤를 돌아보니, 착한 브라운 부인이 마지막 분부를 내리던 그 나지막한 나무 통로 밖으로 머리를 내밀고 있는 것이 보였다. 마찬가지로 착한 브라운 부인이 자기를 향해 주먹을 흔들고 있는 것도 보였다."], ["But though she often looked back afterwards—every minute, at least, in her nervous recollection of the old woman—she could not see her again.", "그러나 그 뒤로 자주 뒤를 돌아보았어도—늙은 여자에 대한 불안한 기억 속에서 적어도 일 분에 한 번씩은 돌아보았어도—다시는 그녀를 볼 수 없었다."]], [["Florence remained there, looking at the bustle in the street, and more and more bewildered by it; and in the meanwhile the clocks appeared to have made up their minds never to strike three any more. At last the steeples rang out three o’clock; there was one close by, so she couldn’t be mistaken; and—after often looking over her shoulder, and often going a little way, and as often coming back again, lest the all-powerful spies of Mrs Brown should take offence—she hurried off, as fast as she could in her slipshod shoes, holding the rabbit-skin tight in her hand.", "플로렌스는 그곳에 머물러, 거리의 북적임을 바라보며 점점 더 어리둥절해했다. 그러는 사이 시계들은 다시는 세 시를 치지 않기로 작정한 듯했다. 마침내 첨탑들이 세 시를 알렸다. 바로 가까이에 하나가 있었으므로 잘못 들었을 리 없었다. 그리하여—자꾸 어깨너머로 돌아보고, 자꾸 조금 가다가, 그만큼 자꾸 다시 돌아오기를 거듭한 끝에, 브라운 부인의 그 전능한 첩자들이 비위 상하지 않도록—그녀는 헐렁한 신발로 낼 수 있는 한 빨리, 토끼 가죽을 손에 꼭 쥔 채, 서둘러 떠났다."]], [["All she knew of her father’s offices was that they belonged to Dombey and Son, and that that was a great power belonging to the City.", "아버지의 사무소에 대해 그녀가 아는 것이라고는, 그것이 돔비와 아들의 것이라는 것, 그리고 그것이 시티에 속한 큰 권세라는 것뿐이었다."], ["So she could only ask the way to Dombey and Son’s in the City; and as she generally made inquiry of children—being afraid to ask grown people—she got very little satisfaction indeed.", "그래서 그녀는 시티에 있는 돔비와 아들로 가는 길만을 물을 수 있었다. 그리고 대개—어른들에게는 묻기가 무서워—아이들에게 길을 물었던 까닭에, 정말이지 신통한 답을 거의 얻지 못했다."], ["But by dint of asking her way to the City after a while, and dropping the rest of her inquiry for the present, she really did advance, by slow degrees, towards the heart of that great region which is governed by the terrible Lord Mayor.", "그러나 얼마 뒤로는 시티로 가는 길을 묻고, 나머지 물음은 당분간 접어 둔 덕분에, 그녀는 정말로, 더디게나마, 무시무시한 시장 각하가 다스리는 그 거대한 구역의 심장부를 향해 나아가고 있었다."]], [["Tired of walking, repulsed and pushed about, stunned by the noise and confusion, anxious for her brother and the nurses, terrified by what she had undergone, and the prospect of encountering her angry father in such an altered state; perplexed and frightened alike by what had passed, and what was passing, and what was yet before her; Florence went upon her weary way with tearful eyes, and once or twice could not help stopping to ease her bursting heart by crying bitterly.", "걷느라 지치고, 떠밀리고 채이고, 소음과 혼란에 얼떨떨하고, 남동생과 유모들 걱정에, 자기가 겪은 일에 겁먹고, 이토록 달라진 행색으로 화난 아버지와 마주칠 일에 두려워하며, 지나간 일과 지금 벌어지는 일과 앞으로 닥칠 일에 한결같이 어리둥절하고 겁에 질려서, 플로렌스는 눈물 어린 눈으로 고단한 길을 갔고, 한두 번은 터질 듯한 가슴을 달래려고 쓰라리게 울며 멈춰 서지 않을 수 없었다."], ["But few people noticed her at those times, in the garb she wore: or if they did, believed that she was tutored to excite compassion, and passed on.", "그러나 그런 때에 그녀가 입은 옷차림 때문에 그녀를 눈여겨보는 사람은 거의 없었다. 설령 눈여겨본다 해도, 동정을 사도록 길들여진 아이려니 여기고 그냥 지나쳤다."], ["Florence, too, called to her aid all the firmness and self-reliance of a character that her sad experience had prematurely formed and tried: and keeping the end she had in view steadily before her, steadily pursued it.", "플로렌스 또한, 자기의 슬픈 경험이 일찍이 빚어내고 시험해 둔 성품의 온갖 굳건함과 자립심을 불러내었다. 그리고 자기가 마음에 둔 목표를 한결같이 앞에 두고서, 한결같이 그것을 좇았다."]], [["It was full two hours later in the afternoon than when she had started on this strange adventure, when, escaping from the clash and clangour of a narrow street full of carts and waggons, she peeped into a kind of wharf or landing-place upon the river-side, where there were a great many packages, casks, and boxes, strewn about; a large pair of wooden scales; and a little wooden house on wheels, outside of which, looking at the neighbouring masts and boats, a stout man stood whistling, with his pen behind his ear, and his hands in his pockets, as if his day’s work were nearly done.", "그녀가 이 기이한 모험에 나선 때보다 오후로 족히 두 시간이 더 흐른 뒤, 수레와 짐마차로 가득한 좁은 거리의 충돌과 소음에서 빠져나와, 그녀는 강가의 어느 부두인지 하역장인지로 살며시 들여다보았다. 그곳에는 짐 꾸러미와 통과 상자가 잔뜩 흩어져 있었고, 큰 나무 저울 한 쌍이 있었으며, 바퀴 달린 작은 나무 오두막이 있었는데, 그 바깥에서 한 건장한 사내가 이웃한 돛대와 배들을 바라보며 휘파람을 불고 있었다. 펜을 귀 뒤에 꽂고 두 손을 주머니에 넣은 채, 마치 그날 일이 거의 끝났다는 듯이."]], [["“Now then!”", "\"이봐!\""], ["said this man, happening to turn round.", "이 사내가 마침 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“We haven’t got anything for you, little girl.", "\"여기엔 너한테 줄 게 아무것도 없다, 꼬마 아가씨."], ["Be off!”", "저리 가!\""]], [["“If you please, is this the City?”", "\"저, 여기가 시티인가요?\""], ["asked the trembling daughter of the Dombeys.", "떨고 있는 돔비가의 딸이 물었다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["It’s the City.", "시티지."], ["You know that well enough, I daresay.", "너도 잘 알 텐데, 분명. 저리 가!"], ["Be off! We haven’t got anything for you.”", "너한테 줄 건 아무것도 없어.\""]], [["“I don’t want anything, thank you,”", "\"아무것도 바라지 않아요, 고맙습니다,\""], ["was the timid answer.", "하는 것이 그 수줍은 대답이었다."], ["“Except to know the way to Dombey and Son’s.”", "\"다만 돔비와 아들로 가는 길을 알고 싶어서요.\""]], [["The man who had been strolling carelessly towards her, seemed surprised by this reply, and looking attentively in her face, rejoined:", "무심히 그녀 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어오던 사내는 이 대답에 놀란 듯했고, 그녀의 얼굴을 유심히 들여다보며 되받았다."]], [["“Why, what can you want with Dombey and Son’s?”", "\"아니, 돔비와 아들에 무슨 볼일이 있는데?\""]], [["“To know the way there, if you please.”", "\"그리로 가는 길을 알고 싶어요, 부탁이에요.\""]], [["The man looked at her yet more curiously, and rubbed the back of his head so hard in his wonderment that he knocked his own hat off.", "사내는 더욱 호기심 어린 눈으로 그녀를 보았고, 어찌나 세게 뒤통수를 긁었던지 제 모자가 떨어져 버렸다."]], [["“Joe!”", "\"조!\""], ["he called to another man—a labourer—as he picked it up and put it on again.", "그는 그것을 주워 다시 쓰면서 다른 사내—한 인부—를 불렀다."]], [["“Joe it is!”", "\"조 여기 있소!\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["“Where’s that young spark of Dombey’s who’s been watching the shipment of them goods?”", "\"저 짐 싣는 거 지켜보던 돔비네 그 젊은 풋내기 어디 갔나?\""]], [["“Just gone, by tt’other gate,”", "\"방금 저쪽 문으로 갔는데요,\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["“Call him back a minute.”", "\"잠깐 다시 불러 와.\""]], [["Joe ran up an archway, bawling as he went, and very soon returned with a blithe-looking boy.", "조는 아치 통로를 뛰어 올라가며 큰 소리로 외쳤고, 이내 쾌활해 보이는 소년 하나를 데리고 돌아왔다."]], [["“You’re Dombey’s jockey, ain’t you?”", "\"네가 돔비네 심부름꾼이지, 안 그래?\""], ["said the first man.", "처음의 사내가 말했다."]], [["“I’m in Dombey’s House, Mr Clark,”", "\"돔비 상회에서 일합니다, 클라크 씨,\""], ["returned the boy.", "소년이 대답했다."]], [["“Look’ye here, then,”", "\"그럼 이쪽을 좀 봐,\""], ["said Mr Clark.", "클라크 씨가 말했다."]], [["Obedient to the indication of Mr Clark’s hand, the boy approached towards Florence, wondering, as well he might, what he had to do with her.", "클라크 씨의 손짓이 가리키는 대로, 소년은 플로렌스 쪽으로 다가왔다. 자기가 이 아이와 무슨 상관이 있는지 어리둥절해하면서."], ["But she, who had heard what passed, and who, besides the relief of so suddenly considering herself safe at her journey’s end, felt reassured beyond all measure by his lively youthful face and manner, ran eagerly up to him, leaving one of the slipshod shoes upon the ground and caught his hand in both of hers.", "그러나 오간 이야기를 들었던 데다, 자기 여정의 끝에 별안간 안전하다는 안도감에 더해, 그의 생기 넘치는 앳된 얼굴과 거동에 더할 나위 없이 안심한 그녀는, 헐렁한 신발 한 짝을 땅바닥에 떨어뜨린 채 그에게 한달음에 달려가, 두 손으로 그의 손을 붙잡았다."]], [["“I am lost, if you please!”", "\"저 길을 잃었어요, 제발!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Lost!”", "\"길을 잃었다고!\""], ["cried the boy.", "소년이 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, I was lost this morning, a long way from here—and I have had my clothes taken away, since—and I am not dressed in my own now—and my name is Florence Dombey, my little brother’s only sister—and, oh dear, dear, take care of me, if you please!”", "\"네, 오늘 아침에 여기서 한참 먼 데서 길을 잃었어요—그러고 나서 제 옷을 빼앗겼고요—지금 이건 제 옷이 아니에요—제 이름은 플로렌스 돔비예요, 제 어린 남동생의 단 하나뿐인 누나예요—그리고, 오 제발, 제발, 저를 좀 보살펴 주세요, 제발!\""], ["sobbed Florence, giving full vent to the childish feelings she had so long suppressed, and bursting into tears.", "플로렌스가 그토록 오래 억눌러 온 어린아이다운 감정을 한껏 터뜨리며, 울음을 터뜨리며 흐느꼈다."], ["At the same time her miserable bonnet falling off, her hair came tumbling down about her face: moving to speechless admiration and commiseration, young Walter, nephew of Solomon Gills, Ships’ Instrument-maker in general.", "동시에 그 비참한 보닛이 벗겨지면서 머리카락이 얼굴 주위로 와르르 쏟아져 내렸으니, 솔로몬 길스—일반 선박 계기 제작자—의 조카인 젊은 월터를 말없는 경탄과 연민으로 감동시켰다."]], [["Mr Clark stood rapt in amazement: observing under his breath, I never saw such a start on this wharf before.", "클라크 씨는 놀라움에 사로잡혀 서 있었다. 이 부두에서 이런 깜짝 놀랄 일은 처음 본다고 그는 나직이 중얼거렸다."], ["Walter picked up the shoe, and put it on the little foot as the Prince in the story might have fitted Cinderella’s slipper on.", "월터는 신발을 집어 들어, 이야기 속의 왕자가 신데렐라의 구두를 신겼을 법한 모양으로 그 작은 발에 신겨 주었다."], ["He hung the rabbit-skin over his left arm; gave the right to Florence; and felt, not to say like Richard Whittington—that is a tame comparison—but like Saint George of England, with the dragon lying dead before him.", "그는 토끼 가죽을 왼팔에 걸치고, 오른팔을 플로렌스에게 내주었다. 그리고 리처드 휘팅턴 같다기보다는—그건 너무 밋밋한 비유다—오히려 발치에 용을 쓰러뜨려 둔 잉글랜드의 성 게오르기우스가 된 기분이었다."]], [["“Don’t cry, Miss Dombey,”", "\"울지 마세요, 돔비 아가씨,\""], ["said Walter, in a transport of enthusiasm.", "월터가 열정에 들떠 말했다."], ["“What a wonderful thing for me that I am here!", "\"제가 여기 있다는 게 저한테는 얼마나 놀라운 일인지!"], ["You are as safe now as if you were guarded by a whole boat’s crew of picked men from a man-of-war.", "이제 아가씨는 군함에서 뽑힌 일등 수병으로 이루어진 보트 한 척의 호위를 받는 것만큼이나 안전해요."], ["Oh, don’t cry.”", "오, 울지 마세요.\""]], [["“I won’t cry any more,”", "\"이젠 안 울게요,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“I am only crying for joy.”", "\"그냥 기뻐서 우는 거예요.\""]], [["“Crying for joy!”", "\"기뻐서 운다니!\""], ["thought Walter, “and I’m the cause of it!", "월터는 생각했다. \"그리고 그 까닭이 나라니!\""], ["Come along, Miss Dombey.", "\"어서 가요, 돔비 아가씨."], ["There’s the other shoe off now!", "이제 다른 신발도 벗겨졌네요!"], ["Take mine, Miss Dombey.”", "제 걸 신으세요, 돔비 아가씨.\""]], [["“No, no, no,”", "\"아니, 아니, 안 돼요,\""], ["said Florence, checking him in the act of impetuously pulling off his own.", "플로렌스가 그가 다급히 제 신발을 벗으려 하는 것을 말리며 말했다."], ["“These do better.", "\"이게 더 나아요."], ["These do very well.”", "이걸로 충분해요.\""]], [["“Why, to be sure,”", "\"하긴 그렇네요,\""], ["said Walter, glancing at her foot, “mine are a mile too large.", "월터가 그녀의 발을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"제 건 일 마일은 더 크니까요."], ["What am I thinking about!", "내가 무슨 생각을 한 거지!"], ["You never could walk in mine!", "제 걸 신고는 도저히 못 걸으실 텐데!"], ["Come along, Miss Dombey.", "어서 가요, 돔비 아가씨."], ["Let me see the villain who will dare molest you now.”", "이제 누가 감히 아가씨를 괴롭히는지 어디 한번 봅시다.\""]], [["So Walter, looking immensely fierce, led off Florence, looking very happy; and they went arm-in-arm along the streets, perfectly indifferent to any astonishment that their appearance might or did excite by the way.", "그리하여 월터는 한껏 사나운 표정을 지으며, 무척 행복해 보이는 플로렌스를 데리고 길을 나섰다. 그들은 팔짱을 끼고 거리를 따라 걸었으니, 그들의 모습이 길에서 일으킬 법한, 또는 실제로 일으킨 그 어떤 놀라움에도 도무지 아랑곳하지 않았다."]], [["It was growing dark and foggy, and beginning to rain too; but they cared nothing for this: being both wholly absorbed in the late adventures of Florence, which she related with the innocent good faith and confidence of her years, while Walter listened as if, far from the mud and grease of Thames Street, they were rambling alone among the broad leaves and tall trees of some desert island in the tropics—as he very likely fancied, for the time, they were.", "날은 어둡고 안개가 끼었으며, 비까지 내리기 시작했다. 그러나 그들은 이런 데에는 조금도 개의치 않았다. 둘 다 플로렌스가 방금 겪은 모험에 온전히 빠져 있었으니, 그녀는 제 나이답게 순진한 진심과 신뢰로 그 일을 이야기했고, 월터는, 템스가의 진흙과 기름때에서 멀리 떨어져, 마치 둘이서 어느 열대의 무인도, 그 너른 잎사귀와 키 큰 나무들 사이를 단둘이 거니는 양 귀 기울였다—실로 그 순간 그는 둘이서 그러고 있다고 곧잘 상상했을 것이다."]], [["“Have we far to go?”", "\"멀리 가야 하나요?\""], ["asked Florence at last, lilting up her eyes to her companion’s face.", "마침내 플로렌스가 눈을 들어 동행의 얼굴을 보며 물었다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["By-the-bye,”", "그러고 보니,\""], ["said Walter, stopping, “let me see; where are we?", "월터가 멈춰 서며 말했다, \"가만, 우리가 어디쯤이지?"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["I know.", "알았다."], ["But the offices are shut up now, Miss Dombey.", "그런데 사무소는 지금 문을 닫았어요, 돔비 아가씨."], ["There’s nobody there.", "거기엔 아무도 없어요."], ["Mr Dombey has gone home long ago.", "돔비 씨는 한참 전에 댁으로 가셨을 거고요."], ["I suppose we must go home too?", "우리도 댁으로 가야겠지요?"], ["or, stay.", "아니, 잠깐만요."], ["Suppose I take you to my Uncle’s, where I live—it’s very near here—and go to your house in a coach to tell them you are safe, and bring you back some clothes.", "제가 사는 우리 삼촌 댁으로 아가씨를 모시면 어떨까요—여기서 아주 가깝거든요—그리고 마차로 아가씨 댁에 가서 안전하다고 알리고, 옷을 좀 가져다드릴게요."], ["Won’t that be best?”", "그게 제일 낫지 않을까요?\""]], [["“I think so,”", "\"그런 것 같아요,\""], ["answered Florence.", "플로렌스가 대답했다."], ["“Don’t you?", "\"안 그래요?"], ["What do you think?”", "어떻게 생각하세요?\""]], [["As they stood deliberating in the street, a man passed them, who glanced quickly at Walter as he went by, as if he recognised him; but seeming to correct that first impression, he passed on without stopping.", "그들이 거리에 서서 궁리하고 있을 때, 한 사내가 그들 곁을 지나가면서 월터를 알아본 듯 휙 흘끗 보았으나, 그 첫인상을 바로잡으려는 듯, 멈추지 않고 지나가 버렸다."]], [["“Why, I think it’s Mr Carker,”", "\"어, 카커 씨인 것 같은데,\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."], ["“Carker in our House.", "\"우리 상회의 카커요."], ["Not Carker our Manager, Miss Dombey—the other Carker; the Junior—Halloa!", "우리 지배인 카커가 아니에요, 돔비 아가씨—다른 카커, 동생 쪽이에요—이봐요!"], ["Mr Carker!”", "카커 씨!\""]], [["“Is that Walter Gay?”", "\"월터 게이인가?\""], ["said the other, stopping and returning.", "그 다른 사람이 멈춰 서서 되돌아오며 말했다."], ["“I couldn’t believe it, with such a strange companion.”", "\"이런 묘한 동행이 있어서, 도무지 믿을 수가 없었네.\""]], [["As he stood near a lamp, listening with surprise to Walter’s hurried explanation, he presented a remarkable contrast to the two youthful figures arm-in-arm before him.", "가로등 곁에 서서 월터의 다급한 설명을 놀라움에 차 듣고 있는 그는, 자기 앞에서 팔짱을 낀 두 앳된 모습과는 놀랍도록 대조를 이루었다. 그는 늙지는 않았으나 머리가 하얬다."], ["He was not old, but his hair was white; his body was bent, or bowed as if by the weight of some great trouble: and there were deep lines in his worn and melancholy face.", "몸은 굽었거나, 마치 무슨 큰 근심의 무게에 짓눌린 듯 휘어 있었다. 그리고 지치고 우울한 얼굴에는 깊은 주름이 패여 있었다."], ["The fire of his eyes, the expression of his features, the very voice in which he spoke, were all subdued and quenched, as if the spirit within him lay in ashes.", "그의 눈빛도, 이목구비의 표정도, 말하는 목소리마저도, 모두 가라앉고 꺼져 있었으니, 마치 그 안의 영혼이 잿더미가 되어 누워 있는 것만 같았다."], ["He was respectably, though very plainly dressed, in black; but his clothes, moulded to the general character of his figure, seemed to shrink and abase themselves upon him, and to join in the sorrowful solicitation which the whole man from head to foot expressed, to be left unnoticed, and alone in his humility.", "그는 점잖게, 그러나 매우 수수하게, 검은 옷을 입고 있었다. 그러나 그의 옷은 그 몸의 전반적인 됨됨이에 맞춰 빚어진 듯, 그의 몸 위에서 움츠러들고 비굴해지며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그 사람 전체가 드러내는 그 슬픈 간청—눈에 띄지 않게, 자기의 비천함 속에 홀로 내버려 두어 달라는 간청—에 동참하는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["And yet his interest in youth and hopefulness was not extinguished with the other embers of his soul, for he watched the boy’s earnest countenance as he spoke with unusual sympathy, though with an inexplicable show of trouble and compassion, which escaped into his looks, however hard he strove to hold it prisoner.", "그럼에도 젊음과 희망에 대한 그의 관심은 그 영혼의 다른 잿불과 함께 꺼지지는 않았으니, 소년이 말하는 동안 그는 남다른 공감으로 그 진지한 얼굴을 지켜보았다. 다만, 아무리 가두어 두려 애써도 표정으로 새어 나오는, 영문 모를 근심과 연민의 기색을 띠고서였다."], ["When Walter, in conclusion, put to him the question he had put to Florence, he still stood glancing at him with the same expression, as if he had read some fate upon his face, mournfully at variance with its present brightness.", "월터가 끝으로, 플로렌스에게 던졌던 그 질문을 그에게 던졌을 때에도, 그는 여전히 그 똑같은 표정으로 소년을 흘끔거리며 서 있었으니, 마치 그 얼굴에서, 지금의 그 환함과는 슬프게도 어긋나는 어떤 운명을 읽어 낸 것만 같았다."]], [["“What do you advise, Mr Carker?”", "\"어떻게 하면 좋을까요, 카커 씨?\""], ["said Walter, smiling.", "월터가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“You always give me good advice, you know, when you do speak to me.", "\"당신은 저한테 늘 좋은 조언을 해 주시잖아요, 말을 걸어 주실 때면요."], ["That’s not often, though.”", "자주는 아니지만요.\""]], [["“I think your own idea is the best,”", "\"네 생각이 제일 나은 것 같구나,\""], ["he answered: looking from Florence to Walter, and back again.", "그가 대답했다. 플로렌스에게서 월터에게로, 다시 그 반대로 시선을 옮기면서."]], [["“Mr Carker,”", "\"카커 씨,\""], ["said Walter, brightening with a generous thought, “Come!", "월터가 너그러운 생각에 환해지며 말했다, \"자!"], ["Here’s a chance for you.", "당신한테 기회가 왔어요."], ["Go you to Mr Dombey’s, and be the messenger of good news.", "당신이 돔비 씨 댁으로 가서, 좋은 소식을 전하는 심부름꾼이 되세요."], ["It may do you some good, Sir.", "그러면 당신한테 좀 도움이 될지도 몰라요, 선생님."], ["I’ll remain at home.", "저는 집에 남을게요."], ["You shall go.”", "당신이 가세요.\""]], [["“I!”", "\"내가!\""], ["returned the other.", "그 다른 사람이 되받았다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Why not, Mr Carker?”", "안 될 게 뭐 있어요, 카커 씨?\""], ["said the boy.", "소년이 말했다."]], [["He merely shook him by the hand in answer; he seemed in a manner ashamed and afraid even to do that; and bidding him good-night, and advising him to make haste, turned away.", "그는 대답 대신 그저 소년의 손을 흔들어 잡을 뿐이었다. 그조차도 어쩐지 부끄럽고 두려워하는 듯했다. 그리고 잘 자라고, 서두르라고 이른 뒤, 돌아서 가 버렸다."]], [["“Come, Miss Dombey,”", "\"가요, 돔비 아가씨,\""], ["said Walter, looking after him as they turned away also, “we’ll go to my Uncle’s as quick as we can.", "그들도 돌아서면서 월터가 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며 말했다, \"되도록 빨리 우리 삼촌 댁으로 가요."], ["Did you ever hear Mr Dombey speak of Mr Carker the Junior, Miss Florence?”", "돔비 씨가 동생 카커 씨에 대해 말씀하시는 걸 들어 보신 적 있어요, 플로렌스 아가씨?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요,\""], ["returned the child, mildly, “I don’t often hear Papa speak.”", "아이가 온순하게 대답했다, \"저는 아빠가 말씀하시는 걸 자주 듣지 못해요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["true!", "그렇겠네!"], ["more shame for him,” thought Walter.", "그건 그분이 부끄러워하셔야 할 일인데,\""], ["After a minute’s pause, during which he had been looking down upon the gentle patient little face moving on at his side, he said, “The strangest man, Mr Carker the Junior is, Miss Florence, that ever you heard of.", "월터는 생각했다. 잠시 말이 없던 동안 그는 제 곁에서 걸어가는 그 온순하고 참을성 있는 작은 얼굴을 내려다보고 있다가, 이윽고 말했다, \"동생 카커 씨는 플로렌스 아가씨가 들어 본 사람 중에 가장 이상한 분이에요."], ["If you could understand what an extraordinary interest he takes in me, and yet how he shuns me and avoids me; and what a low place he holds in our office, and how he is never advanced, and never complains, though year after year he sees young men passed over his head, and though his brother (younger than he is), is our head Manager, you would be as much puzzled about him as I am.”", "그분이 저한테 얼마나 유별난 관심을 두시는지, 그러면서도 어떻게 저를 멀리하고 피하시는지, 또 우리 사무소에서 얼마나 낮은 자리에 계신지, 어떻게 한 번도 승진하지 못하시는지, 해가 가도 젊은 사람들이 자기 머리 위로 올라가는 걸 보면서도, 또 그분의 동생이(그분보다 어린데) 우리 수석 지배인인데도, 한 번도 불평하지 않으시는지를 아신다면, 저만큼이나 그분에 대해 어리둥절하실 거예요.\""]], [["As Florence could hardly be expected to understand much about it, Walter bestirred himself with his accustomed boyish animation and restlessness to change the subject; and one of the unfortunate shoes coming off again opportunely, proposed to carry Florence to his uncle’s in his arms.", "플로렌스가 그것에 대해 많이 이해하리라고는 도무지 기대할 수 없었으므로, 월터는 평소의 소년다운 활기와 부산함으로 화제를 바꾸려 애썼다. 그러던 차에 마침 그 불운한 신발 한 짝이 또 벗겨지자, 플로렌스를 안고 삼촌 댁까지 데려가겠다고 했다."], ["Florence, though very tired, laughingly declined the proposal, lest he should let her fall; and as they were already near the wooden Midshipman, and as Walter went on to cite various precedents, from shipwrecks and other moving accidents, where younger boys than he had triumphantly rescued and carried off older girls than Florence, they were still in full conversation about it when they arrived at the Instrument-maker’s door.", "플로렌스는 몹시 지쳤음에도, 그가 자기를 떨어뜨릴까 봐 웃으며 그 제안을 사양했다. 그리고 그들은 어느새 그 나무 해군 사관후보생 가까이에 와 있었고, 월터가 난파라든가 그 밖의 가슴 뭉클한 사고들에서, 자기보다 더 어린 소년들이 플로렌스보다 더 나이 든 소녀들을 보란 듯이 구해 내어 데리고 갔던 갖가지 선례를 계속 들먹이고 있던 까닭에, 계기 제작자의 문 앞에 다다랐을 때에도 둘은 여전히 그 일을 두고 한창 이야기를 나누고 있었다."]], [["“Holloa, Uncle Sol!”", "\"이봐요, 솔 삼촌!\""], ["cried Walter, bursting into the shop, and speaking incoherently and out of breath, from that time forth, for the rest of the evening.", "월터가 가게로 뛰어들며 외쳤는데, 그때부터 그날 저녁 내내, 숨을 헐떡이며 두서없이 말했다."], ["“Here’s a wonderful adventure!", "\"굉장한 모험이 있었어요!"], ["Here’s Mr Dombey’s daughter lost in the streets, and robbed of her clothes by an old witch of a woman—found by me—brought home to our parlour to rest—look here!”", "여기 돔비 씨의 따님이 거리에서 길을 잃고, 마녀 같은 늙은 여자한테 옷을 빼앗겼는데—제가 찾아내서—쉬시라고 우리 응접실로 모셔 왔어요—여기 좀 보세요!\""]], [["“Good Heaven!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["said Uncle Sol, starting back against his favourite compass-case.", "솔 삼촌이 자기가 아끼는 나침반 상자에 등을 부딪치며 뒷걸음질 쳤다."], ["“It can’t be!", "\"그럴 리가!"], ["Well, I—”", "아니, 나는—\""]], [["“No, nor anybody else,”", "\"네, 다른 누구라도 그럴 거예요,\""], ["said Walter, anticipating the rest.", "월터가 나머지 말을 앞질러 말했다."], ["“Nobody would, nobody could, you know.", "\"아무도 못 믿을 거고, 못 믿겠죠, 아시잖아요."], ["Here!", "자!"], ["just help me lift the little sofa near the fire, will you, Uncle Sol—take care of the plates—cut some dinner for her, will you, Uncle—throw those shoes under the grate.", "작은 소파를 난롯가로 옮기는 것 좀 도와주세요, 솔 삼촌—접시들 조심하시고요—이 아가씨한테 저녁거리 좀 썰어 주실래요, 삼촌—저 신발은 쇠살대 밑으로 던져 두세요."], ["Miss Florence—put your feet on the fender to dry—how damp they are—here’s an adventure, Uncle, eh?—God bless my soul, how hot I am!”", "플로렌스 아가씨—발을 난로받이에 올려놓고 말리세요—얼마나 축축한지—굉장한 모험이죠, 삼촌, 그렇죠?—세상에, 나 정말 덥다!\""]], [["Solomon Gills was quite as hot, by sympathy, and in excessive bewilderment.", "솔로몬 길스도 덩달아 똑같이 더웠고, 또 어쩔 줄 모르게 어리둥절했다."], ["He patted Florence’s head, pressed her to eat, pressed her to drink, rubbed the soles of her feet with his pocket-handkerchief heated at the fire, followed his locomotive nephew with his eyes, and ears, and had no clear perception of anything except that he was being constantly knocked against and tumbled over by that excited young gentleman, as he darted about the room attempting to accomplish twenty things at once, and doing nothing at all.", "그는 플로렌스의 머리를 토닥이고, 먹으라고 권하고, 마시라고 권하고, 난롯불에 데운 손수건으로 그녀의 발바닥을 문질러 주었으며, 끊임없이 움직이는 조카를 눈과 귀로 좇았으나, 한 번에 스무 가지 일을 해내려고 방 안을 휙휙 날아다니면서도 정작 아무것도 못 하는 그 들뜬 젊은 신사에게 줄곧 부딪치고 채여 넘어진다는 것 말고는 아무것도 또렷이 알아채지 못했다."]], [["“Here, wait a minute, Uncle,”", "\"자, 잠깐만요, 삼촌,\""], ["he continued, catching up a candle, “till I run upstairs, and get another jacket on, and then I’ll be off.", "그가 양초를 집어 들며 말을 이었다, \"제가 위층에 뛰어 올라가 다른 웃옷으로 갈아입고 올 동안만요. 그러고 나서 떠날게요."], ["I say, Uncle, isn’t this an adventure?”", "참, 삼촌, 이거 모험 아니에요?\""]], [["“My dear boy,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["said Solomon, who, with his spectacles on his forehead and the great chronometer in his pocket, was incessantly oscillating between Florence on the sofa, and his nephew in all parts of the parlour, “it’s the most extraordinary—”", "솔로몬이 말했다. 안경을 이마에 얹고 그 큰 정밀시계를 주머니에 넣은 채, 소파 위의 플로렌스와 응접실 곳곳에 있는 조카 사이를 쉴 새 없이 오가면서, \"이건 정말이지 가장 기이한—\""]], [["“No, but do, Uncle, please—do, Miss Florence—dinner, you know, Uncle.”", "\"아니, 그러지 마시고, 삼촌, 제발—그러세요, 플로렌스 아가씨—저녁이요, 아시잖아요, 삼촌.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래, 그래,\""], ["cried Solomon, cutting instantly into a leg of mutton, as if he were catering for a giant.", "솔로몬이 외치며, 마치 거인에게 차려 주기라도 하는 양 곧장 양고기 다리를 썰어 들어갔다."], ["“I’ll take care of her, Wally!", "\"내가 이 애를 돌보마, 월리!"], ["I understand.", "알겠다."], ["Pretty dear!", "예쁜 것!"], ["Famished, of course.", "당연히 굶주렸겠지."], ["You go and get ready.", "너는 가서 채비하렴."], ["Lord bless me!", "맙소사!"], ["Sir Richard Whittington thrice Lord Mayor of London.”", "런던 시장을 세 번이나 지낸 리처드 휘팅턴 경이라니.\""]], [["Walter was not very long in mounting to his lofty garret and descending from it, but in the meantime Florence, overcome by fatigue, had sunk into a doze before the fire.", "월터가 자기의 높다란 다락방으로 올라갔다 내려오는 데에는 그리 오래 걸리지 않았으나, 그사이에 플로렌스는 피로에 못 이겨 난롯가에서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작했다."], ["The short interval of quiet, though only a few minutes in duration, enabled Solomon Gills so far to collect his wits as to make some little arrangements for her comfort, and to darken the room, and to screen her from the blaze.", "그 짧은 고요의 짬은, 비록 단 몇 분에 지나지 않았어도, 솔로몬 길스가 정신을 가다듬어 그녀가 편하도록 자그마한 채비를 좀 하고, 방을 어둡게 하고, 불꽃에서 그녀를 가려 주기에는 충분했다."], ["Thus, when the boy returned, she was sleeping peacefully.", "그리하여 소년이 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 평온하게 잠들어 있었다."]], [["“That’s capital!”", "\"잘됐다!\""], ["he whispered, giving Solomon such a hug that it squeezed a new expression into his face.", "그가 속삭이며, 솔로몬을 어찌나 꽉 끌어안았던지 그 얼굴에 새로운 표정이 짜내어졌다."], ["“Now I’m off.", "\"이제 떠날게요."], ["I’ll just take a crust of bread with me, for I’m very hungry—and don’t wake her, Uncle Sol.”", "빵 한 조각만 들고 갈게요, 무척 배가 고프거든요—그리고 이 애를 깨우지 마세요, 솔 삼촌.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"그래, 그래,\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."], ["“Pretty child.”", "\"예쁜 아이로구나.\""]], [["“Pretty, indeed!”", "\"예쁘다마다요!\""], ["cried Walter.", "월터가 외쳤다."], ["“I never saw such a face, Uncle Sol.", "\"저런 얼굴은 처음 봐요, 솔 삼촌."], ["Now I’m off.”", "이제 떠날게요.\""]], [["“That’s right,”", "\"그게 좋겠다,\""], ["said Solomon, greatly relieved.", "솔로몬이 크게 안도하며 말했다."]], [["“I say, Uncle Sol,”", "\"참, 솔 삼촌,\""], ["cried Walter, putting his face in at the door.", "월터가 문틈으로 얼굴을 들이밀며 외쳤다."]], [["“Here he is again,”", "\"또 왔네,\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."]], [["“How does she look now?”", "\"이 애 지금 모습이 어때요?\""]], [["“Quite happy,”", "\"아주 행복해 보이는구나,\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."]], [["“That’s famous!", "\"그거 좋네요!"], ["now I’m off.”", "이제 떠날게요.\""]], [["“I hope you are,”", "\"그러길 바란다,\""], ["said Solomon to himself.", "솔로몬이 혼잣말했다."]], [["“I say, Uncle Sol,”", "\"참, 솔 삼촌,\""], ["cried Walter, reappearing at the door.", "월터가 다시 문간에 나타나며 외쳤다."]], [["“Here he is again!”", "\"또 왔네!\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."]], [["“We met Mr Carker the Junior in the street, queerer than ever.", "\"거리에서 동생 카커 씨를 만났는데, 어느 때보다 더 이상했어요."], ["He bade me good-bye, but came behind us here—there’s an odd thing!—for when we reached the shop door, I looked round, and saw him going quietly away, like a servant who had seen me home, or a faithful dog.", "저한테 작별 인사를 하고는, 우리 뒤를 따라 여기까지 왔지 뭐예요—참 묘한 일이죠!—우리가 가게 문에 다다랐을 때 제가 돌아보니, 그분이 조용히 가 버리는 게 보였거든요, 마치 저를 집까지 바래다준 하인처럼, 아니면 충직한 개처럼요."], ["How does she look now, Uncle?”", "이 애 지금 모습이 어때요, 삼촌?\""]], [["“Pretty much the same as before, Wally,”", "\"아까랑 거의 똑같다, 월리,\""], ["replied Uncle Sol.", "솔 삼촌이 대답했다."]], [["“That’s right.", "\"그거 좋네요."], ["Now I am off!”", "이제 정말 떠날게요!\""]], [["And this time he really was: and Solomon Gills, with no appetite for dinner, sat on the opposite side of the fire, watching Florence in her slumber, building a great many airy castles of the most fantastic architecture; and looking, in the dim shade, and in the close vicinity of all the instruments, like a magician disguised in a Welsh wig and a suit of coffee colour, who held the child in an enchanted sleep.", "그리고 이번에는 정말로 떠났다. 솔로몬 길스는 저녁 먹을 생각도 없이 난로 맞은편에 앉아, 잠든 플로렌스를 지켜보며, 더없이 환상적인 건축양식의 허황된 누각을 잔뜩 지어 올렸다. 그리고 그 어슴푸레한 그늘 속에서, 그 모든 계기들에 둘러싸여, 마치 웨일스풍 가발과 커피색 양복으로 변장한 마법사가 아이를 마법의 잠에 빠뜨려 둔 것처럼 보였다."]], [["In the meantime, Walter proceeded towards Mr Dombey’s house at a pace seldom achieved by a hack horse from the stand; and yet with his head out of window every two or three minutes, in impatient remonstrance with the driver.", "그러는 사이, 월터는 삯마차 정거장의 삯말로는 좀처럼 내기 힘든 속도로 돔비 씨의 저택을 향해 나아갔다. 그러면서도 이삼 분마다 창밖으로 머리를 내밀어, 조바심을 내며 마부를 나무라곤 했다."], ["Arriving at his journey’s end, he leaped out, and breathlessly announcing his errand to the servant, followed him straight into the library, we there was a great confusion of tongues, and where Mr Dombey, his sister, and Miss Tox, Richards, and Nipper, were all congregated together.", "여정의 끝에 다다르자 그는 펄쩍 뛰어내려, 숨을 헐떡이며 하인에게 용건을 알리고는, 그 뒤를 곧장 따라 서재로 들어갔다. 거기서는 떠드는 소리로 한바탕 어지러웠는데, 돔비 씨와 그의 누이, 그리고 톡스 양과 리처즈와 니퍼가 모두 한데 모여 있었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I beg your pardon, Sir,”", "실례합니다, 선생님,\""], ["said Walter, rushing up to him, “but I’m happy to say it’s all right, Sir.", "월터가 그에게 달려가며 말했다, \"그렇지만 다 잘 풀렸다는 걸 기쁘게 말씀드립니다, 선생님."], ["Miss Dombey’s found!”", "돔비 아가씨를 찾았습니다!\""]], [["The boy with his open face, and flowing hair, and sparkling eyes, panting with pleasure and excitement, was wonderfully opposed to Mr Dombey, as he sat confronting him in his library chair.", "트인 얼굴에 머리카락을 휘날리고 눈을 반짝이며, 기쁨과 흥분으로 헐떡이는 그 소년은, 서재의 의자에 앉아 그를 마주한 돔비 씨와 놀랍도록 대조를 이루었다."]], [["“I told you, Louisa, that she would certainly be found,”", "\"내가 말했지, 루이자, 그 애는 분명 찾을 거라고,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, looking slightly over his shoulder at that lady, who wept in company with Miss Tox.", "돔비 씨가 톡스 양과 더불어 울고 있는 그 부인을 어깨너머로 슬쩍 보며 말했다."], ["“Let the servants know that no further steps are necessary.", "\"더 이상 손쓸 필요가 없다고 하인들에게 알려라."], ["This boy who brings the information, is young Gay, from the office.", "이 소식을 가져온 이 소년은 사무소의 젊은 게이로군."], ["How was my daughter found, Sir?", "내 딸을 어떻게 찾았소, 선생?"], ["I know how she was lost.”", "어쩌다 잃어버렸는지는 알고 있소만.\""], ["Here he looked majestically at Richards.", "여기서 그는 위엄 있게 리처즈를 노려보았다."], ["“But how was she found?", "\"그런데 어떻게 찾았소?"], ["Who found her?”", "누가 찾았소?\""]], [["“Why, I believe I found Miss Dombey, Sir,”", "\"음, 제가 돔비 아가씨를 찾은 것 같습니다, 선생님,\""], ["said Walter modestly, “at least I don’t know that I can claim the merit of having exactly found her, Sir, but I was the fortunate instrument of—”", "월터가 겸손하게 말했다, \"적어도 제가 꼭 찾았다고 그 공을 내세울 수 있을지는 모르겠습니다만, 선생님, 제가 운 좋은 도구가 되어—\""]], [["“What do you mean, Sir,”", "\"무슨 말이오, 선생,\""], ["interrupted Mr Dombey, regarding the boy’s evident pride and pleasure in his share of the transaction with an instinctive dislike, “by not having exactly found my daughter, and by being a fortunate instrument?", "돔비 씨가 끼어들었다. 이 일에서 자기 몫에 대해 소년이 뚜렷이 내비치는 자부심과 기쁨을 본능적인 반감으로 바라보며, \"내 딸을 꼭 찾은 것은 아니라느니, 운 좋은 도구가 되었다느니 하는 게?"], ["Be plain and coherent, if you please.”", "분명하고 조리 있게 말하시오, 부탁이오.\""]], [["It was quite out of Walter’s power to be coherent; but he rendered himself as explanatory as he could, in his breathless state, and stated why he had come alone.", "조리 있게 말하기란 월터의 능력을 한참 벗어나는 일이었으나, 그는 숨 가쁜 처지에서나마 할 수 있는 한 또렷이 설명했고, 자기가 왜 혼자 왔는지를 밝혔다."]], [["“You hear this, girl?”", "\"이거 들었느냐, 얘?\""], ["said Mr Dombey sternly to the black-eyed.", "돔비 씨가 검은 눈에게 엄하게 말했다."], ["“Take what is necessary, and return immediately with this young man to fetch Miss Florence home.", "\"필요한 것을 챙겨서, 이 젊은이와 함께 당장 돌아가 플로렌스 아가씨를 집으로 데려와라."], ["Gay, you will be rewarded to-morrow.”", "게이, 자네에게는 내일 상을 내리겠네.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["thank you, Sir,”", "고맙습니다, 선생님,\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."], ["“You are very kind.", "\"정말 친절하십니다."], ["I’m sure I was not thinking of any reward, Sir.”", "저는 정말이지 어떤 상도 생각하지 않았습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“You are a boy,”", "\"자네는 소년이야,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, suddenly and almost fiercely; “and what you think of, or affect to think of, is of little consequence.", "돔비 씨가 별안간, 거의 사납게 말했다. \"그리고 자네가 무얼 생각하든, 혹은 생각하는 척하든, 그건 별 대수롭지 않은 일이지."], ["You have done well, Sir.", "자네는 잘했네, 선생."], ["Don’t undo it.", "그걸 망치지 마시게."], ["Louisa, please to give the lad some wine.”", "루이자, 이 아이에게 포도주를 좀 주시오.\""]], [["Mr Dombey’s glance followed Walter Gay with sharp disfavour, as he left the room under the pilotage of Mrs Chick; and it may be that his mind’s eye followed him with no greater relish, as he rode back to his Uncle’s with Miss Susan Nipper.", "돔비 씨의 시선은 칙 부인의 안내를 받아 방을 나서는 월터 게이를 날카로운 못마땅함으로 좇았다. 그리고 월터가 수전 니퍼 양과 함께 삼촌 댁으로 마차를 타고 돌아갈 때에도, 그의 마음의 눈이 그를 더 흐뭇한 마음으로 좇았던 것은 아닐 터였다."]], [["There they found that Florence, much refreshed by sleep, had dined, and greatly improved the acquaintance of Solomon Gills, with whom she was on terms of perfect confidence and ease.", "거기서 그들은, 잠으로 한껏 기운을 차린 플로렌스가 저녁을 먹고서, 솔로몬 길스와의 사이가 부쩍 가까워져, 그와 더없이 미덥고 편안한 사이가 되어 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The black-eyed (who had cried so much that she might now be called the red-eyed, and who was very silent and depressed) caught her in her arms without a word of contradiction or reproach, and made a very hysterical meeting of it.", "검은 눈은(어찌나 많이 울었던지 이제 붉은 눈이라 불릴 만했고, 매우 말이 없고 풀이 죽어 있었다) 한마디 반박이나 책망도 없이 그녀를 와락 품에 안았고, 무척 히스테릭한 상봉을 벌였다."], ["Then converting the parlour, for the nonce, into a private tiring room, she dressed her, with great care, in proper clothes; and presently led her forth, as like a Dombey as her natural disqualifications admitted of her being made.", "그러고는 잠깐 동안 그 응접실을 사사로운 분장실로 삼아, 매우 정성스레 그녀에게 제대로 된 옷을 입혔다. 이윽고, 타고난 결격 사유가 허락하는 한 최대한 돔비답게 꾸민 모습으로 그녀를 데리고 나섰다."]], [["“Good-night!”", "\"안녕히 계세요!\""], ["said Florence, running up to Solomon.", "플로렌스가 솔로몬에게 달려가며 말했다."], ["“You have been very good to me.”", "\"정말 잘해 주셨어요.\""]], [["Old Sol was quite delighted, and kissed her like her grand-father.", "늙은 솔은 무척 기뻐하며, 마치 손녀에게 하듯 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Good-night, Walter!", "\"안녕, 월터!"], ["Good-bye!”", "잘 있어요!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Good-bye!”", "\"잘 가요!\""], ["said Walter, giving both his hands.", "월터가 두 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“I’ll never forget you,”", "\"당신을 절대 잊지 않을게요,\""], ["pursued Florence.", "플로렌스가 말을 이었다."], ["“No!", "\"아니요!"], ["indeed I never will.", "정말로 절대 안 잊을 거예요."], ["Good-bye, Walter!”", "잘 있어요, 월터!\""]], [["In the innocence of her grateful heart, the child lifted up her face to his.", "고마운 마음의 천진함 속에서, 아이는 얼굴을 들어 그를 향했다."], ["Walter, bending down his own, raised it again, all red and burning; and looked at Uncle Sol, quite sheepishly.", "월터는 제 얼굴을 숙였다가, 온통 빨갛게 달아오른 채 다시 들고는, 무척 쑥스러워하며 솔 삼촌을 보았다."]], [["“Where’s Walter?”", "\"월터는 어디 있죠?\""], ["“Good-night, Walter!”", "\"잘 있어요, 월터!\""], ["“Good-bye, Walter!”", "\"잘 가요, 월터!\""], ["“Shake hands once more, Walter!”", "\"한 번만 더 악수해요, 월터!\""], ["This was still Florence’s cry, after she was shut up with her little maid, in the coach.", "작은 하녀와 함께 마차에 갇히고 나서도, 이것이 여전히 플로렌스의 외침이었다."], ["And when the coach at length moved off, Walter on the door-step gaily returned the waving of her handkerchief, while the wooden Midshipman behind him seemed, like himself, intent upon that coach alone, excluding all the other passing coaches from his observation.", "그리고 마차가 마침내 떠나갈 때, 문간 계단에 선 월터는 그녀가 흔드는 손수건에 쾌활하게 답했다. 한편 그 뒤의 나무 해군 사관후보생은, 그 자신처럼, 그 마차 하나에만 온 신경을 쏟으며 지나가는 다른 모든 마차는 시야에서 제쳐 놓는 듯했다."]], [["In good time Mr Dombey’s mansion was gained again, and again there was a noise of tongues in the library.", "이윽고 돔비 씨의 저택에 다시 다다랐고, 서재에서는 또다시 떠드는 소리가 일었다."], ["Again, too, the coach was ordered to wait—“for Mrs Richards,” one of Susan’s fellow-servants ominously whispered, as she passed with Florence.", "마차에는 또다시 기다리라는 분부가 내려졌다—\"리처즈 부인을 위해서\"라고, 수전의 동료 하인 하나가 플로렌스와 함께 지나가는 그녀를 보고 불길하게 속삭였다."]], [["The entrance of the lost child made a slight sensation, but not much.", "잃어버렸던 아이의 등장은 약간의 동요를 일으켰으나, 그리 대단하지는 않았다."], ["Mr Dombey, who had never found her, kissed her once upon the forehead, and cautioned her not to run away again, or wander anywhere with treacherous attendants.", "그 애를 한 번도 찾아 나선 적 없는 돔비 씨는 그 이마에 한 번 입을 맞추고는, 다시는 달아나지 말 것이며 믿지 못할 시중꾼들과 어디로든 떠돌지 말라고 일렀다."], ["Mrs Chick stopped in her lamentations on the corruption of human nature, even when beckoned to the paths of virtue by a Charitable Grinder; and received her with a welcome something short of the reception due to none but perfect Dombeys.", "칙 부인은, 「자선 연마공」이 미덕의 길로 손짓해 부른다 해도, 인간 본성의 타락을 두고 늘어놓던 한탄을 멈추었다. 그리고 완전한 돔비 사람에게만 마땅한 환대에는 어딘가 못 미치는 환영으로 그 애를 맞이했다."], ["Miss Tox regulated her feelings by the models before her.", "톡스 양은 자기 앞에 놓인 본보기에 맞춰 자기 감정을 조절했다."], ["Richards, the culprit Richards, alone poured out her heart in broken words of welcome, and bowed herself over the little wandering head as if she really loved it.", "리처즈, 그 죄인 리처즈만이 홀로 끊긴 환영의 말들로 마음을 쏟아 내었고, 마치 정말로 그 애를 사랑하기라도 하는 양 그 떠돌다 온 작은 머리 위로 몸을 숙였다."]], [["“Ah, Richards!”", "\"아, 리처즈!\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with a sigh.", "칙 부인이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“It would have been much more satisfactory to those who wish to think well of their fellow creatures, and much more becoming in you, if you had shown some proper feeling, in time, for the little child that is now going to be prematurely deprived of its natural nourishment.", "\"이제 막 천성의 양분을 너무 일찍 빼앗기게 될 그 어린아이를 위해, 자네가 진작에 어떤 합당한 정을 보였더라면, 자기 동포를 좋게 생각하고 싶어 하는 이들에게 훨씬 더 흡족하고, 또 자네에게도 훨씬 더 어울리는 일이었을 텐데.\""]], [["“Cut off,”", "\"끊겨 버렸어요,\""], ["said Miss Tox, in a plaintive whisper, “from one common fountain!”", "톡스 양이 애처로운 속삭임으로 말했다, \"하나의 공통된 샘에서!\""]], [["“If it was my ungrateful case,”", "\"만일 그게 내 배은망덕한 처지였다면,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, solemnly, “and I had your reflections, Richards, I should feel as if the Charitable Grinders’ dress would blight my child, and the education choke him.”", "칙 부인이 엄숙하게 말했다, \"그리고 내가 자네 같은 생각을 한다면, 리처즈, 나는 「자선 연마공」의 옷이 내 아이를 시들게 하고, 그 교육이 내 아이의 목을 조를 것만 같은 기분일 거야.\""]], [["For the matter of that—but Mrs Chick didn’t know it—he had been pretty well blighted by the dress already; and as to the education, even its retributive effect might be produced in time, for it was a storm of sobs and blows.", "사실인즉—그러나 칙 부인은 몰랐지만—그 애는 그 옷 때문에 이미 꽤나 시들어 있었다. 그리고 교육으로 말하자면, 그 응보의 효과조차 머지않아 나타날 만했으니, 그것은 흐느낌과 매질의 폭풍이었으니까."]], [["“Louisa!”", "\"루이자!\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“It is not necessary to prolong these observations.", "\"이런 이야기를 길게 늘어놓을 필요는 없소."], ["The woman is discharged and paid.", "저 여자는 해고되고 삯도 치러졌소."], ["You leave this house, Richards, for taking my son—my son,”", "너는 이 집을 떠나라, 리처즈, 내 아들을—내 아들을,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, emphatically repeating these two words, “into haunts and into society which are not to be thought of without a shudder.", "돔비 씨가 이 두 단어를 힘주어 되풀이하며 말했다, \"몸서리 없이는 생각조차 할 수 없는 소굴과 무리 속으로 데려간 죄로 말이다."], ["As to the accident which befel Miss Florence this morning, I regard that as, in one great sense, a happy and fortunate circumstance; inasmuch as, but for that occurrence, I never could have known—and from your own lips too—of what you had been guilty.", "오늘 아침 플로렌스 아가씨에게 닥친 사고로 말하자면, 나는 그것을, 어떤 큰 의미에서, 다행스럽고 운 좋은 일로 여기네. 왜냐하면 그 일이 없었더라면, 나는 결코—그것도 네 입으로—네가 무슨 잘못을 저질렀는지 알 수 없었을 테니까."], ["I think, Louisa, the other nurse, the young person,”", "내 생각에는, 루이자, 다른 유모, 저 젊은 사람은,\""], ["here Miss Nipper sobbed aloud, “being so much younger, and necessarily influenced by Paul’s nurse, may remain.", "여기서 니퍼 양이 큰 소리로 흐느꼈다, \"워낙 훨씬 어리고, 폴의 유모에게 부득이 영향을 받았을 테니, 남아도 좋겠소."], ["Have the goodness to direct that this woman’s coach is paid to”—Mr Dombey stopped and winced—“to Staggs’s Gardens.”", "부디 이 여자의 마차 삯을—\" 돔비 씨가 말을 멈추고 움찔했다—\"스택스 가든까지로 지불하도록 일러 주시오.\""]], [["Polly moved towards the door, with Florence holding to her dress, and crying to her in the most pathetic manner not to go away.", "폴리는 문 쪽으로 움직였고, 플로렌스가 그 옷자락에 매달려 가지 말라고 더없이 애처롭게 울며 매달렸다."], ["It was a dagger in the haughty father’s heart, an arrow in his brain, to see how the flesh and blood he could not disown clung to this obscure stranger, and he sitting by.", "자기가 부정할 수 없는 피붙이가, 자기는 곁에 앉아 있는데도, 이 보잘것없는 낯선 이에게 그토록 매달리는 것을 보는 일은, 그 오만한 아버지의 가슴에 꽂히는 비수요, 그의 머릿속에 박히는 화살이었다."], ["Not that he cared to whom his daughter turned, or from whom turned away.", "자기 딸이 누구에게로 향하든, 또 누구에게서 등을 돌리든, 그가 마음 쓴 것은 아니었다."], ["The swift sharp agony struck through him, as he thought of what his son might do.", "그 빠르고 날카로운 고통이 그를 꿰뚫고 지나간 것은, 자기 아들이라면 어떻게 할까 하는 생각이 들었기 때문이었다."]], [["His son cried lustily that night, at all events.", "어쨌든 그의 아들은 그날 밤 목청껏 울었다."], ["Sooth to say, poor Paul had better reason for his tears than sons of that age often have, for he had lost his second mother—his first, so far as he knew—by a stroke as sudden as that natural affliction which had darkened the beginning of his life.", "솔직히 말하자면, 가엾은 폴은 그 나이의 아이들이 흔히 그러는 것보다 더 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어 눈물을 흘렸다. 자기의 두 번째 어머니를—그가 아는 한 첫 번째 어머니를—제 삶의 시작을 어둡게 했던 그 천부의 비통만큼이나 갑작스러운 일격으로 잃어버렸으니까."], ["At the same blow, his sister too, who cried herself to sleep so mournfully, had lost as good and true a friend.", "그 똑같은 타격으로, 그토록 구슬프게 울다 잠든 그 누나 또한 그만큼이나 착하고 진실한 벗을 잃었다."], ["But that is quite beside the question.", "그러나 그것은 지금 이야기와는 사뭇 동떨어진 문제다."], ["Let us waste no words about it.", "그것에 대해서는 말을 낭비하지 말기로 하자."]], [["CHAPTER VII. A Bird’s-eye Glimpse of Miss Tox’s Dwelling-place: also of the State of Miss Tox’s Affections", "톡스 양의 거처를 새의 눈으로 내려다본 풍경, 그리고 톡스 양의 애정 상태에 대하여"]], [["Miss Tox inhabited a dark little house that had been squeezed, at some remote period of English History, into a fashionable neighbourhood at the west end of the town, where it stood in the shade like a poor relation of the great street round the corner, coldly looked down upon by mighty mansions.", "톡스 양은 영국 역사의 어느 머나먼 시기에 도시 서쪽 끝의 멋들어진 동네 안으로 비집고 들어와 끼인, 어둑한 작은 집에 살았다. 그 집은 모퉁이를 돌면 나오는 큰 거리의 가난한 친척처럼 그늘 속에 서서, 위풍당당한 저택들에게 냉랭하게 내려다보였다."], ["It was not exactly in a court, and it was not exactly in a yard; but it was in the dullest of No-Thoroughfares, rendered anxious and haggard by distant double knocks.", "그곳은 딱히 골목 안이라고도 할 수 없고 딱히 마당 안이라고도 할 수 없었으나, 막다른 길 가운데서도 가장 따분한 막다른 길에 있어, 멀리서 들려오는 이중 노크 소리에 불안하고 초췌해져 있었다."], ["The name of this retirement, where grass grew between the chinks in the stone pavement, was Princess’s Place; and in Princess’s Place was Princess’s Chapel, with a tinkling bell, where sometimes as many as five-and-twenty people attended service on a Sunday.", "돌포장의 틈새마다 풀이 돋아나는 이 은거지의 이름은 프린세스 플레이스였다. 그리고 프린세스 플레이스에는 댕그랑거리는 종이 달린 프린세스 채플이 있었는데, 일요일이면 많을 때는 스물다섯 명이나 되는 사람들이 예배에 참석하기도 했다."], ["The Princess’s Arms was also there, and much resorted to by splendid footmen.", "프린세스 암스도 그곳에 있었는데, 화려한 하인들이 자주 드나드는 곳이었다."], ["A sedan chair was kept inside the railing before the Princess’s Arms, but it had never come out within the memory of man; and on fine mornings, the top of every rail (there were eight-and-forty, as Miss Tox had often counted) was decorated with a pewter-pot.", "프린세스 암스 앞 난간 안에는 가마 하나가 놓여 있었으나, 사람들의 기억으로는 그것이 한 번도 밖으로 나온 적이 없었다. 그리고 화창한 아침이면 난간의 꼭대기마다(톡스 양이 여러 번 세어 본 바로는 마흔여덟 개였다) 백랍 단지가 하나씩 장식처럼 얹혀 있었다."]], [["There was another private house besides Miss Tox’s in Princess’s Place: not to mention an immense Pair of gates, with an immense pair of lion-headed knockers on them, which were never opened by any chance, and were supposed to constitute a disused entrance to somebody’s stables.", "프린세스 플레이스에는 톡스 양의 집 외에도 다른 개인 주택이 한 채 더 있었다. 어쩌다가도 결코 열리는 법이 없는, 사자 머리 모양의 거대한 노커 한 쌍이 달린 거대한 대문 한 쌍은 말할 것도 없거니와—그 대문은 누군가의 마구간으로 통하던, 지금은 쓰이지 않는 입구일 것으로 짐작되었다."], ["Indeed, there was a smack of stabling in the air of Princess’s Place; and Miss Tox’s bedroom (which was at the back) commanded a vista of Mews, where hostlers, at whatever sort of work engaged, were continually accompanying themselves with effervescent noises; and where the most domestic and confidential garments of coachmen and their wives and families, usually hung, like Macbeth’s banners, on the outward walls.", "사실 프린세스 플레이스의 공기에는 마구간 냄새가 살짝 배어 있었다. 그리고 (뒤쪽에 있던) 톡스 양의 침실에서는 마구간 골목이 한눈에 내다보였는데, 그곳에서는 마부들이 무슨 일을 하고 있든 끊임없이 부글거리는 소리를 입에서 내며 일했고, 마부와 그 아내와 가족들의 더없이 사사롭고 은밀한 옷가지들이 으레 맥베스의 군기처럼 바깥벽에 널려 있었다."]], [["At this other private house in Princess’s Place, tenanted by a retired butler who had married a housekeeper, apartments were let Furnished, to a single gentleman: to wit, a wooden-featured, blue-faced Major, with his eyes starting out of his head, in whom Miss Tox recognised, as she herself expressed it, “something so truly military;”", "프린세스 플레이스의 이 다른 개인 주택은, 가정부와 결혼한 은퇴한 집사가 세 들어 사는 곳이었는데, 가구가 딸린 방들이 독신 신사 한 사람에게 세놓여 있었다. 즉, 나무토막 같은 이목구비에 푸르죽죽한 얼굴을 하고 눈알이 머리에서 튀어나올 듯한 소령이었는데, 톡스 양은 그에게서, 그녀 자신의 표현을 빌리자면 \"정말이지 군인다운 무언가\"를 알아보았다."], ["and between whom and herself, an occasional interchange of newspapers and pamphlets, and such Platonic dalliance, was effected through the medium of a dark servant of the Major’s who Miss Tox was quite content to classify as a “native,”", "그리고 그녀와 그 사이에는, 이따금 신문과 소책자를 주고받는 일과 그런 플라톤식의 정담이, 소령의 검은 하인을 매개로 하여 이루어졌다."], ["without connecting him with any geographical idea whatever.", "톡스 양은 그 하인을 어떤 지리적 관념과도 연결 짓지 않은 채 그저 \"원주민\"으로 분류해 두는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["Perhaps there never was a smaller entry and staircase, than the entry and staircase of Miss Tox’s house.", "톡스 양의 집 현관과 계단보다 더 작은 현관과 계단은 아마 없었을 것이다. 아마 위에서 아래까지 통틀어, 그것은 영국에서 가장 불편하고 가장 비뚤어진 작은 집이었을 것이다."], ["Perhaps, taken altogether, from top to bottom, it was the most inconvenient little house in England, and the crookedest; but then, Miss Tox said, what a situation!", "그러나 그래도 톡스 양은 말했다, 이 위치가 어떤가! 겨울에는 그곳에서 햇빛을 거의 받을 수 없었다."], ["There was very little daylight to be got there in the winter: no sun at the best of times: air was out of the question, and traffic was walled out.", "형편이 가장 좋을 때조차 해가 들지 않았다. 바람은 말할 것도 없었고, 오가는 행인은 담벼락에 막혀 있었다."], ["Still Miss Tox said, think of the situation!", "그래도 톡스 양은 말했다, 이 위치를 생각해 보라니까!"], ["So said the blue-faced Major, whose eyes were starting out of his head: who gloried in Princess’s Place: and who delighted to turn the conversation at his club, whenever he could, to something connected with some of the great people in the great street round the corner, that he might have the satisfaction of saying they were his neighbours.", "눈알이 머리에서 튀어나올 듯한 푸르죽죽한 얼굴의 소령도 똑같이 말했다. 그는 프린세스 플레이스를 자랑스러워했고, 클럽에서 기회만 닿으면 화제를 모퉁이를 돌면 나오는 큰 거리의 위대한 인물들과 관련된 무언가로 돌리기를 좋아했으니, 그들이 자기 이웃이라고 말할 수 있는 만족을 누리기 위해서였다."]], [["In short, with Miss Tox and the blue-faced Major, it was enough for Princess’s Place—as with a very small fragment of society, it is enough for many a little hanger-on of another sort—to be well connected, and to have genteel blood in its veins.", "요컨대 톡스 양과 푸르죽죽한 얼굴의 소령에게는, 프린세스 플레이스가—사회의 아주 작은 한 조각에게, 또 다른 종류의 보잘것없는 부류 중 많은 식객들에게 그러하듯이—좋은 연줄을 지니고 그 혈관에 점잖은 피가 흐른다는 것으로 족했다."], ["It might be poor, mean, shabby, stupid, dull. No matter.", "가난하든, 초라하든, 누추하든, 어리석든, 따분하든 상관없었다."], ["The great street round the corner trailed off into Princess’s Place; and that which of High Holborn would have become a choleric word, spoken of Princess’s Place became flat blasphemy.", "모퉁이를 돌면 나오는 큰 거리가 프린세스 플레이스로 슬며시 이어졌고, 하이 홀번에서라면 발끈하는 욕설이 되었을 말도 프린세스 플레이스를 두고 한다면 노골적인 신성모독이 되었다."]], [["The dingy tenement inhabited by Miss Tox was her own; having been devised and bequeathed to her by the deceased owner of the fishy eye in the locket, of whom a miniature portrait, with a powdered head and a pigtail, balanced the kettle-holder on opposite sides of the parlour fireplace.", "톡스 양이 사는 그 칙칙한 셋집은 그녀 자신의 것이었으니, 로켓 속 생선 같은 눈의 고인이 된 주인이 그녀에게 유언으로 물려준 것이었다. 그 주인의 분 바른 머리에 땋은 머리를 늘어뜨린 작은 초상화가, 응접실 벽난로의 양쪽 끝에서 주전자 받침과 균형을 이루고 있었다."], ["The greater part of the furniture was of the powdered-head and pig-tail period: comprising a plate-warmer, always languishing and sprawling its four attenuated bow legs in somebody’s way; and an obsolete harpsichord, illuminated round the maker’s name with a painted garland of sweet peas.", "가구의 대부분은 분 바른 머리와 땋은 머리 시대의 것이었으니, 늘 비실비실하게 가느다란 네 활 모양 다리를 누군가의 길목에 떡 벌리고 있는 접시 보온기와, 제작자의 이름 둘레에 스위트피 화환이 그려진 한물간 하프시코드가 그중에 있었다."], ["In any part of the house, visitors were usually cognizant of a prevailing mustiness; and in warm weather Miss Tox had been seen apparently writing in sundry chinks and crevices of the wainscoat with the wrong end of a pen dipped in spirits of turpentine.", "집안 어느 구석에서든 방문객들은 으레 떠도는 곰팡내를 알아챘다. 그리고 따뜻한 날씨면, 톡스 양이 테레빈유에 적신 펜의 잘못된 끝으로 벽판의 온갖 틈과 갈라진 곳에 무언가를 쓰는 듯한 모습이 목격되곤 했다."]], [["Although Major Bagstock had arrived at what is called in polite literature, the grand meridian of life, and was proceeding on his journey downhill with hardly any throat, and a very rigid pair of jaw-bones, and long-flapped elephantine ears, and his eyes and complexion in the state of artificial excitement already mentioned, he was mightily proud of awakening an interest in Miss Tox, and tickled his vanity with the fiction that she was a splendid woman who had her eye on him.", "배그스톡 소령은 고상한 문학에서 이른바 인생의 한복판이라 일컫는 지점에 다다라, 목이라곤 거의 없고 턱뼈는 매우 뻣뻣하며 코끼리처럼 길게 늘어진 귀를 단 채, 그리고 두 눈과 안색은 앞서 언급한 인위적으로 흥분한 상태로, 내리막 여정을 걸어 내려가는 중이었지만, 톡스 양의 관심을 일깨운 것을 어마어마하게 자랑스러워했고, 그녀가 자기에게 눈독을 들이는 멋진 여인이라는 허구로 자기 허영심을 간질였다."], ["This he had several times hinted at the club: in connexion with little jocularities, of which old Joe Bagstock, old Joey Bagstock, old J.", "이를 그는 클럽에서 여러 번 넌지시 비쳤으니, 늙은 조 배그스톡, 늙은 조이 배그스톡, 늙은 J."], ["Bagstock, old Josh Bagstock, or so forth, was the perpetual theme: it being, as it were, the Major’s stronghold and donjon-keep of light humour, to be on the most familiar terms with his own name.", "배그스톡, 늙은 조시 배그스톡 따위가 영원한 주제가 되는 자질구레한 농담과 곁들여서였다. 말하자면 자기 자신의 이름과 더없이 허물없는 사이가 되는 것이야말로, 소령의 경쾌한 익살의 아성이요 본성루였던 것이다."]], [["“Joey B., Sir,”", "\"조이 B.는 말이오, 선생,\""], ["the Major would say, with a flourish of his walking-stick, “is worth a dozen of you.", "소령은 지팡이를 한 번 휘두르며 말하곤 했다, \"당신 같은 사람 열둘 몫은 하지요."], ["If you had a few more of the Bagstock breed among you, Sir, you’d be none the worse for it.", "당신네 가운데 배그스톡 혈통이 몇 명만 더 있었더라면, 선생, 당신네한테 조금도 손해 될 게 없었을 거요."], ["Old Joe, Sir, needn’t look far for a wife even now, if he was on the look-out; but he’s hard-hearted, Sir, is Joe—he’s tough, Sir, tough, and de-vilish sly!”", "늙은 조는 말이오, 선생, 마음만 먹는다면 지금이라도 아내 구하려고 멀리 찾아다닐 필요가 없소. 하지만 그는 무정한 사람이오, 선생, 조라는 사람은 말이오—그는 질기지요, 선생, 질기고, 지독히도 교활하다오!\""], ["After such a declaration, wheezing sounds would be heard; and the Major’s blue would deepen into purple, while his eyes strained and started convulsively.", "이런 선언 뒤에는 쌕쌕거리는 소리가 들리곤 했다. 그리고 소령의 푸른빛은 자줏빛으로 짙어졌고, 그동안 두 눈은 발작하듯 부릅떠지며 튀어나왔다."]], [["Notwithstanding his very liberal laudation of himself, however, the Major was selfish.", "그러나 자기 자신을 그토록 후하게 칭송했음에도, 소령은 이기적이었다."], ["It may be doubted whether there ever was a more entirely selfish person at heart; or at stomach is perhaps a better expression, seeing that he was more decidedly endowed with that latter organ than with the former.", "마음으로 보아 그보다 더 완전히 이기적인 사람이 일찍이 있었을지 의심스러울 정도였다. 아니, 마음이라기보다는 위장이라고 하는 편이 더 적절할 터인데, 그가 분명 전자의 기관보다는 후자의 기관을 훨씬 더 두둑이 타고났기 때문이다."], ["He had no idea of being overlooked or slighted by anybody; least of all, had he the remotest comprehension of being overlooked and slighted by Miss Tox.", "그는 누군가에게 무시당하거나 홀대받는다는 생각 따위는 하지 못했다. 더구나 톡스 양에게 무시당하고 홀대받는다는 것은 추호도 이해할 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["And yet, Miss Tox, as it appeared, forgot him—gradually forgot him.", "그런데 보아하니, 톡스 양은 그를 잊고 있었다—차츰차츰 그를 잊어 갔다."], ["She began to forget him soon after her discovery of the Toodle family.", "그녀는 투들 가족을 발견한 직후부터 그를 잊기 시작했다."], ["She continued to forget him up to the time of the christening.", "세례식 무렵까지 줄곧 그를 잊어 갔다."], ["She went on forgetting him with compound interest after that.", "그 후로는 복리로 불어나듯 더더욱 그를 잊어 갔다."], ["Something or somebody had superseded him as a source of interest.", "무언가 혹은 누군가가 관심거리로서 그의 자리를 대신했던 것이다."]], [["“Good morning, Ma’am,”", "\"안녕하시오, 부인,\""], ["said the Major, meeting Miss Tox in Princess’s Place, some weeks after the changes chronicled in the last chapter.", "지난 장에 기록된 변화들이 있은 지 몇 주 뒤에, 프린세스 플레이스에서 톡스 양과 마주친 소령이 말했다."]], [["“Good morning, Sir,”", "\"안녕하세요, 선생님,\""], ["said Miss Tox; very coldly.", "톡스 양은 매우 쌀쌀맞게 말했다."]], [["“Joe Bagstock, Ma’am,”", "\"조 배그스톡은 말입니다, 부인,\""], ["observed the Major, with his usual gallantry, “has not had the happiness of bowing to you at your window, for a considerable period.", "소령은 여느 때의 정중함으로 말했다, \"꽤 오랫동안 부인의 창가에서 부인께 인사 올리는 행복을 누리지 못했습니다."], ["Joe has been hardly used, Ma’am.", "조는 박대를 당했지요, 부인."], ["His sun has been behind a cloud.”", "그의 태양이 구름 뒤에 가려 있었습니다.\""]], [["Miss Tox inclined her head; but very coldly indeed.", "톡스 양은 고개를 숙였다. 그러나 정말이지 매우 쌀쌀맞게."]], [["“Joe’s luminary has been out of town, Ma’am, perhaps,”", "\"조의 빛나는 별께서 어쩌면 도시를 떠나 계셨던 게 아닌지요, 부인,\""], ["inquired the Major.", "소령이 물었다."]], [["“I? out of town?", "\"제가요?"], ["oh no, I have not been out of town,”", "도시를 떠나요? 오 아니에요, 저는 도시를 떠난 적이 없는걸요,\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“I have been much engaged lately.", "\"요즘 매우 바빴답니다."], ["My time is nearly all devoted to some very intimate friends.", "제 시간은 거의 다 어떤 아주 가까운 벗들에게 바쳐지고 있어요."], ["I am afraid I have none to spare, even now.", "지금도 여유가 통 없을까 걱정이네요."], ["Good morning, Sir!”", "안녕히 가세요, 선생님!\""]], [["As Miss Tox, with her most fascinating step and carriage, disappeared from Princess’s Place, the Major stood looking after her with a bluer face than ever: muttering and growling some not at all complimentary remarks.", "톡스 양이 더없이 매혹적인 걸음걸이와 자태로 프린세스 플레이스에서 사라지자, 소령은 그 어느 때보다 더 푸르죽죽해진 얼굴로 그녀의 뒷모습을 바라보고 서서, 도무지 칭찬이라고는 할 수 없는 말들을 중얼중얼 으르렁거렸다."]], [["“Why, damme, Sir,”", "\"아니, 빌어먹을, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, rolling his lobster eyes round and round Princess’s Place, and apostrophizing its fragrant air, “six months ago, the woman loved the ground Josh Bagstock walked on.", "소령은 가재 같은 두 눈알을 프린세스 플레이스 이리저리로 굴리며, 그 향긋한 공기를 향해 외쳤다, \"여섯 달 전만 해도, 저 여자는 조시 배그스톡이 밟고 다니는 땅까지 사랑했단 말이야."], ["What’s the meaning of it?”", "이게 대체 무슨 영문이지?\""]], [["The Major decided, after some consideration, that it meant mantraps; that it meant plotting and snaring; that Miss Tox was digging pitfalls.", "소령은 한참 생각한 끝에 그것이 덫을 뜻한다고, 음모와 올가미를 뜻한다고, 톡스 양이 함정을 파고 있는 것이라고 결론지었다."], ["“But you won’t catch Joe, Ma’am,”", "\"하지만 부인, 당신은 조를 잡지 못할 거요,\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“He’s tough, Ma’am, tough, is J.B.", "\"그는 질기지요, 부인, J.B.는 질겨요."], ["Tough, and de-vilish sly!”", "질기고, 지독히도 교활하다오!\""], ["over which reflection he chuckled for the rest of the day.", "그는 이 생각을 곱씹으며 그날 하루 내내 낄낄거렸다."]], [["But still, when that day and many other days were gone and past, it seemed that Miss Tox took no heed whatever of the Major, and thought nothing at all about him.", "그러나 그래도, 그날과 다른 여러 날이 지나고 흘러가도, 톡스 양은 소령에게 전혀 아랑곳하지 않는 듯했고, 그에 대해서는 도무지 아무 생각도 하지 않는 듯했다."], ["She had been wont, once upon a time, to look out at one of her little dark windows by accident, and blushingly return the Major’s greeting; but now, she never gave the Major a chance, and cared nothing at all whether he looked over the way or not.", "한때 그녀는 어쩌다 자기의 작고 어두운 창문 중 하나로 밖을 내다보다가, 얼굴을 붉히며 소령의 인사에 답하곤 했다. 그러나 이제는 소령에게 그럴 기회를 도무지 주지 않았고, 그가 길 건너편을 쳐다보든 말든 전혀 개의치 않았다."], ["Other changes had come to pass too.", "다른 변화들도 일어났다."], ["The Major, standing in the shade of his own apartment, could make out that an air of greater smartness had recently come over Miss Tox’s house; that a new cage with gilded wires had been provided for the ancient little canary bird; that divers ornaments, cut out of coloured card-boards and paper, seemed to decorate the chimney-piece and tables; that a plant or two had suddenly sprung up in the windows; that Miss Tox occasionally practised on the harpsichord, whose garland of sweet peas was always displayed ostentatiously, crowned with the Copenhagen and Bird Waltzes in a Music", "소령은 자기 방 그늘 속에 서서, 최근 톡스 양의 집에 한결 산뜻한 기운이 감돌게 되었음을 알아챌 수 있었다. 늙은 작은 카나리아를 위해 금박 입힌 철사로 만든 새 새장이 마련되었고, 색색의 마분지와 종이를 오려 만든 갖가지 장식물이 벽난로 선반과 탁자들을 꾸미는 듯했으며, 화초 한두 포기가 창가에 불쑥 돋아났고, 톡스 양이 이따금 하프시코드를 연주했는데, 그 스위트피 화환은 코펜하겐 왈츠와 버드 왈츠가 실린 악보로 머리에 관을 쓴 듯 늘 보란 듯이 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["# Book of Miss Tox’s own copying.", "# 제3장"]], [["Over and above all this, Miss Tox had long been dressed with uncommon care and elegance in slight mourning.", "토록스 양이 직접 베껴 적은 책이었다. 이 모든 것에 더하여, 토록스 양은 오래전부터 보기 드물게 정성스럽고 우아하게, 가벼운 상복 차림을 하고 있었다."], ["But this helped the Major out of his difficulty; and he determined within himself that she had come into a small legacy, and grown proud.", "그러나 이것이 소령을 곤혹스러움에서 건져 주었다. 그는 속으로, 그녀가 작은 유산을 물려받아 거만해진 것이라고 단정해 버렸다."]], [["It was on the very next day after he had eased his mind by arriving at this decision, that the Major, sitting at his breakfast, saw an apparition so tremendous and wonderful in Miss Tox’s little drawing-room, that he remained for some time rooted to his chair; then, rushing into the next room, returned with a double-barrelled opera-glass, through which he surveyed it intently for some minutes.", "이런 결론에 이르러 마음의 짐을 던 바로 그 이튿날, 소령은 아침 식사 자리에 앉아 있다가, 토록스 양의 자그마한 응접실에서 어찌나 어마어마하고 놀라운 환영이 보였던지, 한동안 의자에 못 박힌 듯 앉아 있었다. 그러더니 옆방으로 달려들어가 쌍안식 오페라 망원경을 들고 돌아와서는, 그것을 통해 몇 분 동안 그 환영을 뚫어지게 살폈다."]], [["“It’s a Baby, Sir,”", "\"아기야, 이런, 오만 파운드를 걸고,\""], ["said the Major, shutting up the glass again, “for fifty thousand pounds!”", "소령은 다시 망원경을 접으며 말했다, \"갓난아기란 말이야!\""]], [["The Major couldn’t forget it.", "소령은 그 일을 잊을 수가 없었다."], ["He could do nothing but whistle, and stare to that extent, that his eyes, compared with what they now became, had been in former times quite cavernous and sunken.", "그는 휘파람을 불고 멍하니 바라보는 것밖에는 아무것도 할 수 없었는데, 그 정도가 어찌나 심했던지 지금 그렇게 변한 두 눈에 비하면 예전의 그의 눈은 차라리 움푹 꺼지고 들어가 있었다고 할 만했다."], ["Day after day, two, three, four times a week, this Baby reappeared.", "날이면 날마다, 한 주에 두 번, 세 번, 네 번씩 이 아기가 다시 나타났다."], ["The Major continued to stare and whistle.", "소령은 여전히 멍하니 바라보며 휘파람을 불었다."], ["To all other intents and purposes he was alone in Princess’s Place.", "그 밖의 모든 면에서 그는 프린세스 플레이스에 홀로 있는 셈이었다."], ["Miss Tox had ceased to mind what he did.", "토록스 양은 그가 무엇을 하든 더는 신경 쓰지 않았다."], ["He might have been black as well as blue, and it would have been of no consequence to her.", "그가 푸른빛이 아니라 검은빛이었다 한들, 그녀에게는 아무런 상관이 없었을 것이다."]], [["The perseverance with which she walked out of Princess’s Place to fetch this baby and its nurse, and walked back with them, and walked home with them again, and continually mounted guard over them; and the perseverance with which she nursed it herself, and fed it, and played with it, and froze its young blood with airs upon the harpsichord, was extraordinary.", "* 푸른빛: 원문 'blue'는 우울함을 뜻하는 동시에 소령 이름 'Bagstock'을 떠올리게 하는 말장난으로, 검은빛(black)과 대비된다. 그녀가 이 아기와 그 유모를 데리러 프린세스 플레이스를 나서고, 그들과 함께 걷고, 다시 그들을 집까지 바래다주고, 끊임없이 그들을 지키며 보초를 서던 그 끈기란, 그리고 직접 아기를 돌보고, 먹이고, 놀아 주고, 하프시코드로 곡조를 들려주어 그 어린 핏줄을 얼어붙게 만들던 그 끈기란 실로 대단한 것이었다."], ["At about this same period too, she was seized with a passion for looking at a certain bracelet; also with a passion for looking at the moon, of which she would take long observations from her chamber window.", "또한 거의 같은 무렵, 그녀는 어떤 팔찌 하나를 바라보는 일에 열을 올렸고, 달을 바라보는 일에도 열을 올려서, 제 침실 창문에서 오래도록 달을 관찰하곤 했다."], ["But whatever she looked at; sun, moon, stars, or bracelet; she looked no more at the Major.", "그러나 그녀가 무엇을 바라보든—해든, 달이든, 별이든, 팔찌든—소령만큼은 더 이상 바라보지 않았다."], ["And the Major whistled, and stared, and wondered, and dodged about his room, and could make nothing of it.", "그리고 소령은 휘파람을 불고, 멍하니 바라보고, 의아해하고, 제 방 안을 이리저리 서성거렸으나, 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었다."]], [["“You’ll quite win my brother Paul’s heart, and that’s the truth, my dear,”", "\"자네는 정말이지 우리 오라버니 폴의 마음을 완전히 사로잡을 거야, 그건 틀림없어, 자네,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, one day.", "어느 날 칙 부인이 말했다."]], [["Miss Tox turned pale.", "토록스 양의 얼굴이 창백해졌다."]], [["“He grows more like Paul every day,”", "\"날이 갈수록 폴을 더 닮아 가는구나,\""], ["said Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."]], [["Miss Tox returned no other reply than by taking the little Paul in her arms, and making his cockade perfectly flat and limp with her caresses.", "토록스 양은 어린 폴을 두 팔에 안고서, 쓰다듬느라 그 아이의 모자 장식을 완전히 납작하고 흐물흐물하게 만들어 놓는 것 말고는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["“His mother, my dear,”", "\"이 아이의 어머니 말인데요, 자네,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “whose acquaintance I was to have made through you, does he at all resemble her?”", "토록스 양이 말했다, \"내가 자네를 통해 알게 되었어야 할 분 말이에요, 이 아이가 그분을 조금이라도 닮았나요?\""]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"전혀요,\""], ["returned Louisa", "루이자가 응수했다."]], [["“She was—she was pretty, I believe?”", "\"그분은—그분은 예뻤다고들 하던데요?\""], ["faltered Miss Tox.", "토록스 양이 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“Why, poor dear Fanny was interesting,”", "\"글쎄, 가엾고 사랑스러운 패니는 매력적인 사람이었어요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, after some judicial consideration.", "칙 부인이 한참 신중히 따져 본 뒤에 말했다."], ["“Certainly interesting.", "\"분명 매력적이었지요."], ["She had not that air of commanding superiority which one would somehow expect, almost as a matter of course, to find in my brother’s wife; nor had she that strength and vigour of mind which such a man requires.”", "어쩐지 거의 당연하게, 우리 오라버니의 아내라면 으레 갖추었으리라 기대하게 되는 그런 군림하는 듯한 위엄은 없었어요. 또 그런 남자에게 필요한 그런 정신의 힘과 기백도 없었고요.\""]], [["Miss Tox heaved a deep sigh.", "토록스 양이 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“But she was pleasing:”", "\"하지만 사람을 기쁘게 하는 데가 있었어요.\""], ["said Mrs Chick: “extremely so.", "칙 부인이 말했다. \"더할 나위 없이요."], ["And she meant!—oh, dear, how well poor Fanny meant!”", "게다가 마음씨는—오, 세상에, 가엾은 패니는 얼마나 마음씨가 고왔는지!\""]], [["“You Angel!”", "\"이 천사 같으니!\""], ["cried Miss Tox to little Paul.", "토록스 양이 어린 폴에게 외쳤다."], ["“You Picture of your own Papa!”", "\"네 아빠를 꼭 빼닮은 그림 같은 아이!\""]], [["If the Major could have known how many hopes and ventures, what a multitude of plans and speculations, rested on that baby head; and could have seen them hovering, in all their heterogeneous confusion and disorder, round the puckered cap of the unconscious little Paul; he might have stared indeed. Then would he have recognised, among the crowd, some few ambitious motes and beams belonging to Miss Tox; then would he perhaps have understood the nature of that lady’s faltering investment in the Dombey Firm.", "만일 소령이, 얼마나 많은 희망과 모험이, 얼마나 숱한 계획과 기대가 그 아기의 머리 위에 걸려 있는지를 알 수 있었더라면, 그리고 그것들이 온갖 잡다한 혼란과 무질서 속에서, 아무것도 모르는 어린 폴의 주름진 모자 둘레를 맴도는 모습을 볼 수 있었더라면, 그는 정말이지 눈이 휘둥그레졌을 것이다. 그랬다면 그는 그 무리 가운데서 토록스 양에게 속한 야심에 찬 티끌과 들보 몇 개를 알아보았을 것이고, 그랬다면 아마도 돔비 회사에 대한 그 부인의 머뭇거리는 투자의 본질을 이해했을 것이다. * 티끌과 들보: 「마태복음」 7장의 \"남의 눈 속 티끌과 제 눈 속 들보\" 비유를 빗댄 표현."]], [["If the child himself could have awakened in the night, and seen, gathered about his cradle-curtains, faint reflections of the dreams that other people had of him, they might have scared him, with good reason.", "만일 그 아이 자신이 한밤중에 깨어나, 제 요람 휘장 둘레에 모여든, 다른 사람들이 저를 두고 꾸는 꿈의 희미한 잔영들을 보았더라면, 그것들은 마땅히 그를 겁에 질리게 했을지도 모른다."], ["But he slumbered on, alike unconscious of the kind intentions of Miss Tox, the wonder of the Major, the early sorrows of his sister, and the stern visions of his father; and innocent that any spot of earth contained a Dombey or a Son.", "그러나 그는 잠을 자고 있었으니, 토록스 양의 친절한 의도도, 소령의 의아함도, 누이의 이른 슬픔도, 아버지의 준엄한 환상도 똑같이 알지 못한 채, 그리고 이 땅 어느 한 귀퉁이에 돔비나 아들이라는 것이 존재한다는 사실조차 모르는 채, 천진하게 잠들어 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII. Paul’s Further Progress, Growth and Character", "# 제4장"]], [["Beneath the watching and attentive eyes of Time—so far another Major—Paul’s slumbers gradually changed.", "시간이라는—말하자면 또 하나의 소령이라 할—그 지켜보고 주의 깊은 눈길 아래, 폴의 잠은 차츰 변해 갔다. 점점 더 많은 빛이 그 잠 속으로 새어 들었고, 점점 더 또렷한 꿈들이 그것을 흔들어 놓았다."], ["More and more light broke in upon them; distincter and distincter dreams disturbed them; an accumulating crowd of objects and impressions swarmed about his rest; and so he passed from babyhood to childhood, and became a talking, walking, wondering Dombey.", "갈수록 쌓여 가는 사물과 인상들의 무리가 그의 휴식 주위로 몰려들었다. 그렇게 그는 갓난아기 시절에서 어린아이 시절로 넘어갔고, 말하고 걷고 신기해하는 돔비가 되었다."]], [["On the downfall and banishment of Richards, the nursery may be said to have been put into commission: as a Public Department is sometimes, when no individual Atlas can be found to support it The Commissioners were, of course, Mrs Chick and Miss Tox: who devoted themselves to their duties with such astonishing ardour that Major Bagstock had every day some new reminder of his being forsaken, while Mr Chick, bereft of domestic supervision, cast himself upon the gay world, dined at clubs and coffee-houses, smelt of smoke on three different occasions, went to the play by himself, and in short, loosened (as Mrs Chick once told him) every social bond, and moral obligation.", "리처즈가 쫓겨나 추방된 뒤로, 육아실은 위원회 관리 체제에 들어갔다고 할 만했다. 어떤 공공 부서가 그것을 떠받칠 만한 개개인의 아틀라스를 찾지 못할 때 더러 그러하듯이 말이다. 그 위원들은 물론 칙 부인과 톡스 양이었고, 둘은 어찌나 놀라운 열성으로 자기들 임무에 몰두했던지, 백스톡 소령은 날마다 자기가 버림받았다는 새로운 사실을 새삼 떠올리게 되었으며, 한편 가정의 감독을 잃은 칙 씨는 화려한 사교계에 몸을 던져 클럽과 커피하우스에서 식사하고, 세 번에 걸쳐 담배 냄새를 풍기고, 혼자 연극을 보러 가고, 요컨대 (칙 부인이 한번 그에게 말했듯이) 온갖 사회적 결속과 도덕적 의무를 느슨하게 풀어 놓았다."]], [["Yet, in spite of his early promise, all this vigilance and care could not make little Paul a thriving boy.", "그러나 어릴 적의 그 모든 장래성에도 불구하고, 이 모든 경계와 보살핌으로도 어린 폴을 무럭무럭 자라는 아이로 만들지는 못했다."], ["Naturally delicate, perhaps, he pined and wasted after the dismissal of his nurse, and, for a long time, seemed but to wait his opportunity of gliding through their hands, and seeking his lost mother.", "아마도 본디 허약한 체질이었던지, 그는 유모가 쫓겨난 뒤로 시들고 야위어 갔고, 오랫동안 그저 사람들의 손 사이로 미끄러져 빠져나가 잃어버린 어미를 찾아 떠날 기회만 기다리는 듯이 보였다."], ["This dangerous ground in his steeple-chase towards manhood passed, he still found it very rough riding, and was grievously beset by all the obstacles in his course.", "어른으로 향하는 그 장애물 경주에서 이 위험한 구간을 지나고서도, 그는 여전히 무척 험한 길을 달려야 했고, 가는 길의 온갖 장애물에 시달리며 괴로움을 겪었다."], ["Every tooth was a break-neck fence, and every pimple in the measles a stone wall to him.", "이가 하나 날 때마다 그것은 목이 부러질 만큼 위험한 울타리였고, 홍역으로 돋는 발진 하나하나가 그에게는 돌담이었다."], ["He was down in every fit of the hooping-cough, and rolled upon and crushed by a whole field of small diseases, that came trooping on each other’s heels to prevent his getting up again.", "백일해 발작이 일 때마다 그는 쓰러졌고, 그가 다시 일어나지 못하도록 줄줄이 꼬리를 물고 몰려오는 자잘한 질병들의 한 무리에게 짓밟히고 깔려 으스러졌다."], ["Some bird of prey got into his throat instead of the thrush; and the very chickens turning ferocious—if they have anything to do with that infant malady to which they lend their name—worried him like tiger-cats.", "개똥지빠귀 대신 어떤 맹금류가 그의 목구멍 속으로 들어왔고, 병아리들조차—그 새끼 새들이 이름을 빌려준 그 유아병과 무슨 관련이 있다면—사납게 변해서 호랑이 살쾡이처럼 그를 괴롭혔다. * 개똥지빠귀(thrush)는 영어로 구내염(아구창)을 뜻하기도 하고, 병아리(chicken)는 수두(chicken-pox)를 가리키는 말장난이다."]], [["The chill of Paul’s christening had struck home, perhaps to some sensitive part of his nature, which could not recover itself in the cold shade of his father; but he was an unfortunate child from that day.", "폴의 세례식 때의 그 냉기가, 어쩌면 그의 본성 가운데 어떤 예민한 부분을 깊숙이 찔렀던 것인지도 모른다. 그 부분은 아버지의 차가운 그늘 속에서 도무지 회복될 수 없었다."], ["Mrs Wickam often said she never see a dear so put upon.", "어쨌든 그날 이후로 그는 불운한 아이였다. 위컴 부인은 그토록 시달림을 받는 귀한 아이는 본 적이 없다고 곧잘 말했다."]], [["Mrs Wickam was a waiter’s wife—which would seem equivalent to being any other man’s widow—whose application for an engagement in Mr Dombey’s service had been favourably considered, on account of the apparent impossibility of her having any followers, or anyone to follow; and who, from within a day or two of Paul’s sharp weaning, had been engaged as his nurse. Mrs Wickam was a meek woman, of a fair complexion, with her eyebrows always elevated, and her head always drooping; who was always ready to pity herself, or to be pitied, or to pity anybody else; and who had a surprising natural gift of viewing all subjects in an utterly forlorn and pitiable light, and bringing dreadful precedents to bear upon them, and deriving the greatest consolation from the exercise of that talent.", "위컴 부인은 어느 식당 종업원의 아내였는데—이는 다른 어느 사내의 미망인이나 다를 바 없다는 뜻인 듯했다—돔비 씨 댁에서 일하게 해 달라는 그녀의 청원은, 그녀에게 따라다닐 정인(情人)이나 따를 사람이 있을 가능성이 도무지 없어 보인다는 이유로 호의적으로 받아들여졌다. 그녀는 폴이 모질게 젖을 뗀 지 하루 이틀 만에 그의 유모로 고용되었다. 위컴 부인은 온순한 여인으로, 살결이 희고, 눈썹은 늘 치켜올라가 있었으며, 고개는 늘 수그리고 있었다. 그녀는 자기 자신을 가엾이 여기거나, 남에게 가엾이 여겨지거나, 다른 누군가를 가엾이 여길 채비가 늘 되어 있었다. 또 모든 일을 더없이 처량하고 가련한 빛으로 바라보고, 거기에 무시무시한 선례들을 끌어다 붙이며, 그 재주를 발휘하는 데서 더없는 위안을 얻는 놀라운 천성의 재능을 지니고 있었다."]], [["It is hardly necessary to observe, that no touch of this quality ever reached the magnificent knowledge of Mr Dombey.", "이런 기질의 기미가 돔비 씨의 그 장엄한 인식에는 단 한 차례도 닿지 않았다는 것은 굳이 짚을 필요도 없겠다."], ["It would have been remarkable, indeed, if any had; when no one in the house—not even Mrs Chick or Miss Tox—dared ever whisper to him that there had, on any one occasion, been the least reason for uneasiness in reference to little Paul.", "만일 조금이라도 닿았더라면 그것이야말로 정말 놀라운 일이었으리라. 집안에서—심지어 칙 부인이나 톡스 양조차도—어린 폴과 관련하여 단 한 번이라도 조금이나마 불안해할 이유가 있었다는 것을 그에게 감히 속삭인 사람이 아무도 없었으니 말이다."], ["He had settled, within himself, that the child must necessarily pass through a certain routine of minor maladies, and that the sooner he did so the better.", "그는 마음속으로, 아이가 반드시 어떤 사소한 질병들의 일정한 통과 의례를 거쳐야 하며, 그 일을 빨리 치를수록 좋다고 단정해 두었다."], ["If he could have bought him off, or provided a substitute, as in the case of an unlucky drawing for the militia, he would have been glad to do so, on liberal terms.", "만일 민병대에 뽑히는 운 나쁜 제비를 뽑았을 때처럼 그 일을 돈을 주고 면하거나 대리인을 세울 수 있었다면, 그는 후한 조건으로 기꺼이 그렇게 했을 것이다."], ["But as this was not feasible, he merely wondered, in his haughty manner, now and then, what Nature meant by it; and comforted himself with the reflection that there was another milestone passed upon the road, and that the great end of the journey lay so much the nearer.", "그러나 그것이 가능하지 않았으므로, 그는 그저 이따금 거만한 태도로 자연이 도대체 무슨 뜻으로 이러는가 의아해할 따름이었다. 그리고 길 위에서 이정표 하나를 또 지났으니 여정의 그 위대한 종착점이 그만큼 더 가까워졌다는 생각으로 스스로를 위로했다."], ["For the feeling uppermost in his mind, now and constantly intensifying, and increasing in it as Paul grew older, was impatience.", "지금 그의 마음에서 가장 위에 자리한 감정, 그리고 폴이 자랄수록 끊임없이 강해지고 커져 가는 감정은 조바심이었기 때문이다."], ["Impatience for the time to come, when his visions of their united consequence and grandeur would be triumphantly realized.", "그들 부자가 한데 어우러진 위세와 위대함에 대한 자신의 환상이 의기양양하게 실현될 그날이 오기를 바라는 조바심."]], [["Some philosophers tell us that selfishness is at the root of our best loves and affections.", "어떤 철학자들은 이기심이야말로 우리의 가장 좋은 사랑과 애정의 뿌리라고 우리에게 일러 준다."], ["Mr Dombey’s young child was, from the beginning, so distinctly important to him as a part of his own greatness, or (which is the same thing) of the greatness of Dombey and Son, that there is no doubt his parental affection might have been easily traced, like many a goodly superstructure of fair fame, to a very low foundation.", "돔비 씨의 어린 아들은 처음부터 그에게, 자기 자신의 위대함의—혹은 (같은 말이지만) 돔비와 아들의 위대함의—한 부분으로서 너무도 분명하게 중요했으므로, 그의 부정(父情) 또한 빛나는 명성을 자랑하는 숱한 훌륭한 상부구조들과 마찬가지로 매우 천한 토대까지 손쉽게 거슬러 올라가 더듬어 볼 수 있었으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 그러나 그는 자기가 가진 사랑을 다해 아들을 사랑했다."], ["But he loved his son with all the love he had.", "그의 서리 낀 마음에 따뜻한 자리가 한 군데라도 있었다면, 그 자리는 아들이 차지했다."], ["If there were a warm place in his frosty heart, his son occupied it; if its very hard surface could receive the impression of any image, the image of that son was there; though not so much as an infant, or as a boy, but as a grown man—the “Son”", "그 더없이 단단한 표면이 어떤 형상이라도 새겨 받을 수 있었다면, 그곳에는 그 아들의 형상이 있었다. 다만 그것은 갓난아기로서나 소년으로서가 아니라, 다 자란 어른—회사의 '아들'—로서의 형상이었다."], ["of the Firm. Therefore he was impatient to advance into the future, and to hurry over the intervening passages of his history.", "그러므로 그는 미래로 나아가, 그 사이에 끼인 자기 인생의 대목들을 서둘러 지나가고 싶어 안달이었다."], ["Therefore he had little or no anxiety about them, in spite of his love; feeling as if the boy had a charmed life, and must become the man with whom he held such constant communication in his thoughts, and for whom he planned and projected, as for an existing reality, every day.", "그러므로 그는 사랑에도 불구하고 그 대목들에 대해 걱정이 거의, 아니 전혀 없었다. 마치 그 아이가 마법에 걸린 목숨을 지녀, 자기가 생각 속에서 그토록 끊임없이 교감하고, 또 이미 존재하는 현실인 양 날마다 계획하고 구상하는 그 어른이 되고야 말 것처럼 느꼈으니까."]], [["Thus Paul grew to be nearly five years old.", "이렇게 폴은 자라서 거의 다섯 살이 되었다."], ["He was a pretty little fellow; though there was something wan and wistful in his small face, that gave occasion to many significant shakes of Mrs Wickam’s head, and many long-drawn inspirations of Mrs Wickam’s breath.", "그는 예쁘장한 꼬마였다. 다만 그 자그마한 얼굴에는 어딘가 핏기 없고 수심에 잠긴 데가 있어서, 위컴 부인이 의미심장하게 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 길게 한숨을 들이쉬게 만드는 빌미가 되곤 했다."], ["His temper gave abundant promise of being imperious in after-life; and he had as hopeful an apprehension of his own importance, and the rightful subservience of all other things and persons to it, as heart could desire.", "그의 성미는 훗날 자라서 거만해지리라는 것을 충분히 약속했고, 그는 자기 자신의 중요함과, 그 앞에 다른 모든 사물과 사람이 마땅히 복종해야 한다는 것에 대해, 더 바랄 나위 없을 만큼 야무진 자각을 지니고 있었다. 그는 이따금 충분히 어린아이답고 장난기가 넘쳤으며 시무룩한 기질도 아니었다."], ["He was childish and sportive enough at times, and not of a sullen disposition; but he had a strange, old-fashioned, thoughtful way, at other times, of sitting brooding in his miniature arm-chair, when he looked (and talked) like one of those terrible little Beings in the Fairy tales, who, at a hundred and fifty or two hundred years of age, fantastically represent the children for whom they have been substituted.", "그러나 어떤 때는 묘하고 예스럽고 생각에 잠긴 태도로, 자기의 자그마한 안락의자에 앉아 골똘히 생각에 빠지곤 했는데, 그럴 때면 그는 동화 속에 나오는 그 무서운 작은 존재들 가운데 하나처럼 보이고 또 그렇게 말했다. 백오십 살이나 이백 살쯤 된 그 존재들은, 자기들이 대신 들어앉은 그 아이들을 기괴하게 흉내 내며 나타나는 법이다."], ["He would frequently be stricken with this precocious mood upstairs in the nursery; and would sometimes lapse into it suddenly, exclaiming that he was tired: even while playing with Florence, or driving Miss Tox in single harness.", "그는 위층 육아실에서 곧잘 이런 조숙한 기분에 사로잡히곤 했다. 그리고 때로는, 플로렌스와 놀고 있을 때나 톡스 양을 외바퀴 마차 끌듯 끌고 다닐 때조차도, 별안간 그 기분에 빠져들어 자기가 피곤하다고 외치곤 했다."], ["But at no time did he fall into it so surely, as when, his little chair being carried down into his father’s room, he sat there with him after dinner, by the fire.", "그러나 그가 그 기분에 그토록 확실하게 빠져드는 때는, 자기의 작은 의자가 아버지의 방으로 옮겨져 내려가, 식사 후 난롯가에서 아버지와 함께 거기 앉아 있을 때만 한 적이 없었다."], ["They were the strangest pair at such a time that ever firelight shone upon.", "그런 때 두 사람은 난롯불이 일찍이 비춘 적이 없을 만큼 기이한 한 쌍이었다."], ["Mr Dombey so erect and solemn, gazing at the glare; his little image, with an old, old face, peering into the red perspective with the fixed and rapt attention of a sage.", "돔비 씨는 너무나 꼿꼿하고 근엄하게 그 불빛을 응시하고, 그의 작은 분신은 늙디늙은 얼굴로 그 붉게 펼쳐진 광경을 현인의 고정되고 넋 나간 주의력으로 들여다보았다."], ["Mr Dombey entertaining complicated worldly schemes and plans; the little image entertaining Heaven knows what wild fancies, half-formed thoughts, and wandering speculations.", "돔비 씨는 복잡한 세속의 계획과 구상을 품고 있었고, 그 작은 분신은 하느님만이 아실 어떤 거친 공상과 채 다 영글지 못한 생각과 떠도는 사색을 품고 있었다."], ["Mr Dombey stiff with starch and arrogance; the little image by inheritance, and in unconscious imitation.", "돔비 씨는 풀 먹인 옷과 오만함으로 뻣뻣했고, 그 작은 분신은 물려받은 천성으로, 또 자기도 모르는 흉내로 뻣뻣했다."], ["The two so very much alike, and yet so monstrously contrasted.", "둘은 그토록 똑 닮았으면서도, 또 그토록 어처구니없이 대조를 이루었다."]], [["On one of these occasions, when they had both been perfectly quiet for a long time, and Mr Dombey only knew that the child was awake by occasionally glancing at his eye, where the bright fire was sparkling like a jewel, little Paul broke silence thus:", "그런 때 가운데 한번은, 둘 다 오랫동안 완전히 조용히 있어서, 돔비 씨는 이따금 아이의 눈을—거기에는 밝은 난롯불이 보석처럼 반짝이고 있었다—흘끗 보고서야 아이가 깨어 있다는 것을 알 수 있었는데, 그때 어린 폴이 이렇게 침묵을 깨뜨렸다."]], [["“Papa!", "\"아빠!"], ["what’s money?”", "돈이 뭐예요?\""]], [["The abrupt question had such immediate reference to the subject of Mr Dombey’s thoughts, that Mr Dombey was quite disconcerted.", "그 느닷없는 질문은 돔비 씨가 생각하던 바로 그 주제와 너무나 직접 맞닿아 있어서, 돔비 씨는 완전히 당황하고 말았다."]], [["“What is money, Paul?”", "\"돈이 무엇이냐고, 폴?\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Money?”", "\"돈?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said the child, laying his hands upon the elbows of his little chair, and turning the old face up towards Mr Dombey’s; “what is money?”", "아이가 자기 작은 의자의 팔걸이에 두 손을 얹고, 늙은 얼굴을 돔비 씨 쪽으로 치켜들며 말했다. \"돈이 뭐예요?\""]], [["Mr Dombey was in a difficulty.", "돔비 씨는 난처해졌다."], ["He would have liked to give him some explanation involving the terms circulating-medium, currency, depreciation of currency, paper, bullion, rates of exchange, value of precious metals in the market, and so forth; but looking down at the little chair, and seeing what a long way down it was, he answered: “Gold, and silver, and copper.", "그는 유통 매개, 통화, 통화 가치의 하락, 지폐, 지금(地金), 환율, 시장에서의 귀금속 가치, 따위의 용어가 들어간 어떤 설명을 해 주고 싶었다. 그러나 그 작은 의자를 내려다보고, 그것이 얼마나 까마득히 아래에 있는가를 보고는, 이렇게 대답했다."], ["Guineas, shillings, half-pence.", "\"금하고, 은하고, 구리란다. 기니, 실링, 반 페니."], ["You know what they are?”", "그게 뭔지 알지?\""]], [["“Oh yes, I know what they are,”", "\"네, 그럼요, 그게 뭔지 알아요,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."], ["“I don’t mean that, Papa.", "\"그걸 말하는 게 아니에요, 아빠."], ["I mean what’s money after all?”", "그래도 돈이 결국 뭐냐는 거예요.\""]], [["Heaven and Earth, how old his face was as he turned it up again towards his father’s!", "천지신명이여, 아이가 그 얼굴을 다시 아버지 쪽으로 치켜들었을 때 그 얼굴은 어찌 그리 늙어 보이던지!"]], [["“What is money after all!”", "\"돈이 결국 무엇이냐니!\""], ["said Mr Dombey, backing his chair a little, that he might the better gaze in sheer amazement at the presumptuous atom that propounded such an inquiry.", "돔비 씨는 의자를 약간 뒤로 빼며 말했다. 그런 질문을 던지는 그 건방진 티끌 같은 존재를 더 잘, 그저 어이가 없어 바라보기 위해서였다."]], [["“I mean, Papa, what can it do?”", "\"제 말은요, 아빠, 돈이 뭘 할 수 있냐는 거예요,\""], ["returned Paul, folding his arms (they were hardly long enough to fold), and looking at the fire, and up at him, and at the fire, and up at him again.", "폴이 두 팔을 (팔짱을 끼기엔 거의 길이가 모자랐지만) 끼고서, 난롯불을 보았다가, 아버지를 올려다보았다가, 다시 난롯불을 보았다가, 또 아버지를 올려다보며 대꾸했다."]], [["Mr Dombey drew his chair back to its former place, and patted him on the head.", "돔비 씨는 의자를 본디 자리로 다시 끌어당기고는, 아이의 머리를 토닥였다."], ["“You’ll know better by-and-by, my man,”", "\"차차 더 잘 알게 될 거다, 우리 아들,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Money, Paul, can do anything.”", "\"돈은 말이다, 폴, 무엇이든 다 할 수 있단다.\""], ["He took hold of the little hand, and beat it softly against one of his own, as he said so.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 그 작은 손을 잡아, 자기 한 손에 대고 부드럽게 톡톡 쳤다."]], [["But Paul got his hand free as soon as he could; and rubbing it gently to and fro on the elbow of his chair, as if his wit were in the palm, and he were sharpening it—and looking at the fire again, as though the fire had been his adviser and prompter—repeated, after a short pause:", "그러나 폴은 되도록 빨리 손을 빼내고는, 마치 자기 재치가 손바닥에 들어 있어 그것을 갈기라도 하는 듯, 자기 의자의 팔걸이에 손을 가만히 이리저리 문질렀다—그리고 다시 난롯불을 바라보았는데, 마치 그 불이 자기의 조언자이자 길잡이라도 되는 듯했다—그러더니 잠깐 뜸을 들였다가 되물었다."]], [["“Anything, Papa?”", "\"무엇이든요, 아빠?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Anything—almost,”", "무엇이든—거의,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Anything means everything, don’t it, Papa?”", "\"무엇이든이라는 건 모든 거라는 뜻이죠, 그렇죠, 아빠?\""], ["asked his son: not observing, or possibly not understanding, the qualification.", "아들이 물었다. 그 단서를 알아차리지 못했거나, 어쩌면 이해하지 못한 채로."]], [["“It includes it: yes,”", "\"모든 걸 포함하지, 그래,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Why didn’t money save me my Mama?”", "\"그럼 왜 돈은 우리 엄마를 살려 주지 않았어요?\""], ["returned the child.", "아이가 되물었다."], ["“It isn’t cruel, is it?”", "\"돈은 잔인하지 않잖아요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Cruel!”", "\"잔인하다고!\""], ["said Mr Dombey, settling his neckcloth, and seeming to resent the idea.", "돔비 씨는 목도리를 매만지며, 그런 생각에 발끈하는 듯이 말했다. \"아니다."], ["“No. A good thing can’t be cruel.”", "좋은 것은 잔인할 수가 없지.\""]], [["“If it’s a good thing, and can do anything,”", "\"그게 좋은 거고, 또 무엇이든 할 수 있다면,\""], ["said the little fellow, thoughtfully, as he looked back at the fire, “I wonder why it didn’t save me my Mama.”", "그 꼬마가 다시 난롯불을 돌아보며 생각에 잠겨 말했다, \"왜 우리 엄마를 살려 주지 않았는지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["He didn’t ask the question of his father this time.", "이번에는 아버지에게 그 질문을 던진 것이 아니었다."], ["Perhaps he had seen, with a child’s quickness, that it had already made his father uncomfortable.", "어쩌면 그 질문이 이미 아버지를 불편하게 만들었다는 것을, 아이 특유의 눈치 빠름으로 알아차렸는지도 모른다."], ["But he repeated the thought aloud, as if it were quite an old one to him, and had troubled him very much; and sat with his chin resting on his hand, still cogitating and looking for an explanation in the fire.", "그러나 그는 그 생각을, 마치 그것이 자기에게는 아주 오래된 것이고 또 자기를 무척 괴롭혀 온 것인 양 소리 내어 되풀이했다. 그리고 턱을 손에 괸 채, 여전히 골똘히 생각하며 난롯불 속에서 어떤 해답을 찾는 듯이 앉아 있었다."]], [["Mr Dombey having recovered from his surprise, not to say his alarm (for it was the very first occasion on which the child had ever broached the subject of his mother to him, though he had had him sitting by his side, in this same manner, evening after evening), expounded to him how that money, though a very potent spirit, never to be disparaged on any account whatever, could not keep people alive whose time was come to die; and how that we must all die, unfortunately, even in the City, though we were never so rich.", "돔비 씨는 놀람에서—아니, 거의 경악이라 해도 좋을 것에서(아이가 이런 식으로 자기 곁에 저녁마다 앉아 있었음에도, 어머니라는 주제를 그에게 꺼낸 것은 이번이 그야말로 처음이었으니까)—정신을 차리고는, 아이에게 이렇게 설명해 주었다. 돈이라는 것은 비록 매우 강력한 정령이어서 무슨 이유로도 결코 깎아내릴 것이 못 되지만, 죽을 때가 된 사람들을 살아 있게 할 수는 없다고."], ["But how that money caused us to be honoured, feared, respected, courted, and admired, and made us powerful and glorious in the eyes of all men; and how that it could, very often, even keep off death, for a long time together.", "그리고 우리는 안타깝게도 누구나 다 죽어야 하는데, 아무리 부유하더라도, 심지어 시티 안에서도 그렇다고. 그러나 돈은 우리가 존경받고, 두려움의 대상이 되고, 우러름을 받고, 떠받들리고, 칭송받게 해 주며, 모든 사람의 눈에 우리를 강력하고 영예롭게 만들어 준다고."], ["How, for example, it had secured to his Mama the services of Mr Pilkins, by which he, Paul, had often profited himself; likewise of the great Doctor Parker Peps, whom he had never known.", "그리고 돈은 종종 죽음마저도, 그것도 꽤 오랫동안, 물리칠 수 있다고. 예를 들어, 돈이 그의 엄마에게 필킨스 씨의 진료를 보장해 주었으며, 그 덕을 폴 자신도 곧잘 보았고, 또한 폴이 한 번도 본 적 없는 저 위대한 파커 펩스 박사의 진료도 마찬가지였다고."], ["And how it could do all, that could be done.", "그리고 돈은 무릇 할 수 있는 일이라면 다 해낼 수 있다고."], ["This, with more to the same purpose, Mr Dombey instilled into the mind of his son, who listened attentively, and seemed to understand the greater part of what was said to him.", "이런 이야기를, 같은 취지의 더 많은 말과 함께, 돔비 씨는 아들의 마음에 불어넣어 주었고, 아이는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울이며 자기에게 하는 말의 대부분을 이해하는 듯이 보였다."]], [["“It can’t make me strong and quite well, either, Papa; can it?”", "\"돈이 저를 튼튼하고 아주 건강하게 만들어 줄 수도 없죠, 아빠? 그렇죠?\""], ["asked Paul, after a short silence; rubbing his tiny hands.", "폴이 잠시 잠잠히 있다가, 자그마한 두 손을 비비며 물었다."]], [["“Why, you are strong and quite well,”", "\"아니, 너는 튼튼하고 아주 건강한걸,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["“Are you not?”", "\"그렇지 않으냐?\""]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["the age of the face that was turned up again, with an expression, half of melancholy, half of slyness, on it!", "다시 치켜든 그 얼굴은, 절반은 우울하고 절반은 능청스러운 표정을 띤 채로, 어찌 그리 늙어 보이던지!"]], [["“You are as strong and well as such little people usually are?", "\"너는 너만 한 꼬마들이 으레 그렇듯 튼튼하고 건강하지? 응?\""], ["Eh?” said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Florence is older than I am, but I’m not as strong and well as Florence, “I know,”", "\"플로렌스는 저보다 나이가 많은데, 저는 플로렌스만큼 튼튼하고 건강하지 못해요, 저도 알아요,\""], ["returned the child; “and I believe that when Florence was as little as me, she could play a great deal longer at a time without tiring herself.", "아이가 대꾸했다. \"그리고 플로렌스가 저만큼 어렸을 때는, 지치지 않고 훨씬 더 오래 놀 수 있었을 거예요."], ["I am so tired sometimes,”", "저는 가끔 너무 피곤해요,\""], ["said little Paul, warming his hands, and looking in between the bars of the grate, as if some ghostly puppet-show were performing there, “and my bones ache so (Wickam says it’s my bones), that I don’t know what to do.”", "어린 폴이 두 손을 녹이며, 마치 그 안에서 무슨 유령 인형극이라도 벌어지고 있는 듯이 난로 살창 사이를 들여다보면서 말했다, \"그리고 뼈가 너무 아파서요(위컴이 그러는데 제 뼈래요), 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“Ay!", "\"그래!"], ["But that’s at night,”", "하지만 그건 밤이지,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, drawing his own chair closer to his son’s, and laying his hand gently on his back; “little people should be tired at night, for then they sleep well.”", "돔비 씨가 자기 의자를 아들의 의자에 더 가까이 끌어당기고, 한 손을 아이의 등에 가만히 얹으며 말했다. \"꼬마들은 밤이면 피곤한 게 당연하지, 그래야 잠을 잘 자니까.\""]], [["“Oh, it’s not at night, Papa,”", "\"아, 밤이 아니에요, 아빠,\" 아이가 대꾸했다."], ["returned the child, “it’s in the day; and I lie down in Florence’s lap, and she sings to me.", "\"낮이에요. 그러면 저는 플로렌스 무릎에 누워요, 그럼 플로렌스가 노래를 불러 줘요."], ["At night I dream about such cu-ri-ous things!”", "밤에는 정말 이-상-한 것들을 꿈꿔요!\""]], [["And he went on, warming his hands again, and thinking about them, like an old man or a young goblin.", "그러고 그는 다시 두 손을 녹이며, 그 손을 두고 골똘히 생각에 잠겼는데, 마치 노인이나 어린 도깨비 같았다."]], [["Mr Dombey was so astonished, and so uncomfortable, and so perfectly at a loss how to pursue the conversation, that he could only sit looking at his son by the light of the fire, with his hand resting on his back, as if it were detained there by some magnetic attraction.", "돔비 씨는 너무도 놀랍고, 너무도 불편하고, 어떻게 대화를 이어 가야 할지 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수가 없어서, 그저 한 손을 아이의 등에 얹은 채—마치 어떤 자력에 이끌려 거기 붙들려 있는 것처럼—난롯불 빛에 아들을 바라보고 앉아 있을 수밖에 없었다."], ["Once he advanced his other hand, and turned the contemplative face towards his own for a moment.", "한번은 그가 다른 한 손을 내밀어, 그 사색에 잠긴 얼굴을 잠시 자기 쪽으로 돌렸다."], ["But it sought the fire again as soon as he released it; and remained, addressed towards the flickering blaze, until the nurse appeared, to summon him to bed.", "그러나 그가 놓아 주자마자 그 얼굴은 다시 난롯불을 찾았고, 유모가 그를 잠자리로 부르러 나타날 때까지, 깜박이는 불꽃 쪽을 향한 채로 있었다."]], [["“I want Florence to come for me,”", "\"플로렌스가 저를 데리러 왔으면 좋겠어요,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“Won’t you come with your poor Nurse Wickam, Master Paul?”", "\"가엾은 유모 위컴이랑 가지 않으시려고요, 폴 도련님?\""], ["inquired that attendant, with great pathos.", "그 시중꾼이 매우 애처로운 투로 물었다."]], [["“No, I won’t,”", "\"안 가요,\""], ["replied Paul, composing himself in his arm-chair again, like the master of the house.", "폴이 마치 이 집의 주인이라도 되는 양 다시 안락의자에 자세를 고쳐 앉으며 대꾸했다."]], [["Invoking a blessing upon his innocence, Mrs Wickam withdrew, and presently Florence appeared in her stead.", "위컴 부인은 그의 순진함에 축복을 빌어 주고 물러갔고, 이내 그 대신 플로렌스가 나타났다."], ["The child immediately started up with sudden readiness and animation, and raised towards his father in bidding him good-night, a countenance so much brighter, so much younger, and so much more child-like altogether, that Mr Dombey, while he felt greatly reassured by the change, was quite amazed at it.", "아이는 곧바로 갑작스러운 채비와 생기로 벌떡 일어나, 아버지에게 잘 자라고 인사하며 그를 향해 얼굴을 들어 올렸는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 훨씬 더 환하고, 훨씬 더 어려 보이고, 전체적으로 훨씬 더 어린아이다웠던지, 돔비 씨는 그 변화에 크게 안도하면서도 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["After they had left the room together, he thought he heard a soft voice singing; and remembering that Paul had said his sister sung to him, he had the curiosity to open the door and listen, and look after them.", "둘이 함께 방을 나간 뒤, 그는 부드러운 목소리로 노래하는 소리가 들리는 듯했다. 그리고 폴이 누이가 자기에게 노래를 불러 준다고 했던 말이 떠올라, 호기심에 문을 열고 귀를 기울이며 둘의 뒷모습을 바라보았다."], ["She was toiling up the great, wide, vacant staircase, with him in her arms; his head was lying on her shoulder, one of his arms thrown negligently round her neck.", "누이는 그 크고 널찍하고 텅 빈 계단을, 아이를 품에 안고 힘겹게 올라가고 있었다. 아이의 머리는 누이의 어깨에 얹혀 있었고, 한쪽 팔은 무심한 듯 그녀의 목에 둘려 있었다."], ["So they went, toiling up; she singing all the way, and Paul sometimes crooning out a feeble accompaniment.", "그렇게 둘은 힘겹게 올라갔다. 누이는 내내 노래를 불렀고, 폴은 이따금 가냘프게 흥얼거리며 화음을 보탰다."], ["Mr Dombey looked after them until they reached the top of the staircase—not without halting to rest by the way—and passed out of his sight; and then he still stood gazing upwards, until the dull rays of the moon, glimmering in a melancholy manner through the dim skylight, sent him back to his room.", "돔비 씨는 둘이 계단 꼭대기에 이를 때까지—도중에 쉬려고 멈춰 서기도 하면서—그 뒷모습을 바라보았고, 둘이 그의 시야에서 사라진 뒤에도, 흐릿한 채광창을 통해 우울하게 가물거리는 달의 희미한 빛이 그를 자기 방으로 돌려보낼 때까지, 여전히 위쪽을 올려다보며 서 있었다."]], [["Mrs Chick and Miss Tox were convoked in council at dinner next day; and when the cloth was removed, Mr Dombey opened the proceedings by requiring to be informed, without any gloss or reservation, whether there was anything the matter with Paul, and what Mr Pilkins said about him.", "이튿날 저녁 식사 때 칙 부인과 톡스 양이 회의에 소집되었다. 그리고 식탁보가 걷히자, 돔비 씨가 절차를 시작하며, 폴에게 무슨 문제가 있는지, 그리고 필킨스 씨가 아이에 대해 무어라 했는지를, 아무런 꾸밈이나 숨김 없이 알려 달라고 요구했다."]], [["“For the child is hardly,”", "\"왜냐하면 그 아이가,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “as stout as I could wish.”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"내가 바라는 만큼 튼튼하지가 않아서 말이오.\""]], [["“My dear Paul,”", "\"폴, 너는,\""], ["returned Mrs Chick, “with your usual happy discrimination, which I am weak enough to envy you, every time I am in your company; and so I think is Miss Tox.”", "칙 부인이 대꾸했다, \"여느 때처럼 그 행복한 분별력으로—그건 너와 함께 있을 때마다 내가 부럽다 못해 약해질 지경인데—그리고 톡스 양도 그렇게 생각하리라 믿어.\""]], [["“Oh my dear!”", "\"오, 부인!\""], ["said Miss Tox, softly, “how could it be otherwise?", "톡스 양이 나직이 말했다, \"어찌 안 그러겠어요?"], ["Presumptuous as it is to aspire to such a level; still, if the bird of night may—but I’ll not trouble Mr Dombey with the sentiment.", "그런 경지를 우러러보려는 것은 외람된 일이지만, 그래도 밤의 새가 감히—아니, 돔비 씨께 그런 감상으로 폐를 끼치진 않겠어요."], ["It merely relates to the Bulbul.”", "그저 불불에 관한 거랍니다.\""]], [["Mr Dombey bent his head in stately recognition of the Bulbuls as an old-established body.", "* 불불(Bulbul): 페르시아 시에서 장미를 사모하는 새, 곧 나이팅게일을 뜻한다."]], [["“With your usual happy discrimination, my dear Paul,” resumed Mrs Chick, “you have hit the point at once.", "돔비 씨는 불불이라는 것을 오래도록 자리 잡은 유서 깊은 집단인 양 여기며, 위엄 있게 고개를 끄덕여 그 존재를 인정했다."], ["Our darling is altogether as stout as we could wish.", "\"여느 때처럼 그 행복한 분별력으로, 폴,\" 칙 부인이 말을 이었다, \"너는 단번에 핵심을 짚었어."], ["The fact is, that his mind is too much for him.", "우리 귀염둥이는 전체적으로 우리가 바랄 만큼 튼튼해."], ["His soul is a great deal too large for his frame.", "사실은, 그 애 정신이 그 애에게 너무 벅찬 거야."], ["I am sure the way in which that dear child talks!”", "영혼이 그 애 몸에 비해 너무나 큰 거지. 그 귀여운 아이가 말하는 품이라니!\""], ["said Mrs Chick, shaking her head; “no one would believe.", "칙 부인이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"아무도 안 믿을 거야."], ["His expressions, Lucretia, only yesterday upon the subject of Funerals!”", "글쎄, 루크리셔, 바로 어제 그 애가 장례식이라는 주제에 대해 한 말이라니!\""]], [["“I am afraid,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, interrupting her testily, “that some of those persons upstairs suggest improper subjects to the child.", "돔비 씨가 짜증스럽게 누이의 말을 가로막으며 말했다, \"위층에 있는 사람들 가운데 누군가가 아이에게 부적절한 주제를 들이미는 것 같소."], ["He was speaking to me last night about his—about his Bones,”", "그 애가 어젯밤 나한테 자기—자기 뼈에 대해 이야기를 하더란 말이오,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, laying an irritated stress upon the word.", "돔비 씨가 그 말에 짜증스러운 힘을 주며 말했다."], ["“What on earth has anybody to do with the—with the—Bones of my son?", "\"도대체 누가 내 아들의—그—뼈와 무슨 상관이 있단 말이오?"], ["He is not a living skeleton, I suppose.”", "그 애가 살아 있는 해골은 아니지 않소, 설마.\""]], [["“Very far from it,”", "\"그렇고말고요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with unspeakable expression.", "칙 부인이 형언할 수 없는 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“I hope so,”", "\"그러길 바라오,\""], ["returned her brother.", "그녀의 오라비가 대꾸했다."], ["“Funerals again!", "\"또 장례식이라니!"], ["who talks to the child of funerals?", "누가 아이한테 장례식 이야기를 한단 말이오?"], ["We are not undertakers, or mutes, or grave-diggers, I believe.”", "우리가 장의사도, 장례 곡꾼도, 무덤 파는 사람도 아니지 않소.\""]], [["“Very far from it,”", "\"그렇고말고요,\""], ["interposed Mrs Chick, with the same profound expression as before.", "칙 부인이 아까와 똑같은 깊은 표정으로 끼어들었다."]], [["“Then who puts such things into his head?”", "\"그럼 누가 그런 것들을 그 애 머릿속에 넣어 준단 말이오?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“Really I was quite dismayed and shocked last night.", "\"정말이지 어젯밤엔 나도 꽤나 당황하고 놀랐소."], ["Who puts such things into his head, Louisa?”", "누가 그런 것들을 그 애 머릿속에 넣어 주느냐고, 루이자?\""]], [["“My dear Paul,”", "\"폴,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, after a moment’s silence, “it is of no use inquiring.", "칙 부인이 잠시 잠잠히 있다가 말했다, \"물어봐야 소용없어. 내 솔직히 말하지만, 나는 위컴이 아주 명랑한 기질을 지닌 사람이라거나, 이른바—\""], ["I do not think, I will tell you candidly that Wickam is a person of very cheerful spirit, or what one would call a—”", "\"모무스의 딸이라고는,\" 톡스 양이 나직이 거들었다."]], [["“A daughter of Momus,” Miss Tox softly suggested.", "* 모무스(Momus): 그리스 신화의 조롱과 비난, 풍자의 신."]], [["“Exactly so,”", "\"바로 그래,\""], ["said Mrs Chick; “but she is exceedingly attentive and useful, and not at all presumptuous; indeed I never saw a more biddable woman.", "칙 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 그 여자는 더없이 세심하고 쓸모가 있고, 또 전혀 주제넘지가 않아."], ["I would say that for her, if I was put upon my trial before a Court of Justice.”", "정말이지 그렇게 고분고분한 여자는 본 적이 없어. 내가 법정에 서서 재판을 받게 되더라도 그 점만은 그 여자를 위해 말해 주겠어.\""]], [["“Well!", "\"흥!"], ["you are not put upon your trial before a Court of Justice, at present, Louisa,”", "너는 지금 법정에 서서 재판을 받는 게 아니다, 루이자,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, chafing, “and therefore it don’t matter.”", "돔비 씨가 발끈하며 대꾸했다, \"그러니 그건 상관없는 일이야.\""]], [["“My dear Paul,”", "\"폴,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, in a warning voice, “I must be spoken to kindly, or there is an end of me,”", "칙 부인이 경고하는 목소리로 말했다, \"나한테는 다정하게 말해 줘야 해, 안 그러면 나는 끝장이야,\""], ["at the same time a premonitory redness developed itself in Mrs Chick’s eyelids which was an invariable sign of rain, unless the weather changed directly.", "그와 동시에 칙 부인의 눈꺼풀에는 비를 예고하는 붉은 기운이 번지기 시작했는데, 그것은 날씨가 곧장 바뀌지 않는 한 어김없이 비가 온다는 징조였다."]], [["“I was inquiring, Louisa,”", "\"내가 물은 건, 루이자,\""], ["observed Mr Dombey, in an altered voice, and after a decent interval, “about Paul’s health and actual state.”", "돔비 씨가 어조를 바꾸어, 그리고 점잖이 한 박자 쉬고서 말했다, \"폴의 건강과 지금의 실제 상태에 대해서였소.\""]], [["“If the dear child,”", "\"우리 귀여운 아이가,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, in the tone of one who was summing up what had been previously quite agreed upon, instead of saying it all for the first time, “is a little weakened by that last attack, and is not in quite such vigorous health as we could wish; and if he has some temporary weakness in his system, and does occasionally seem about to lose, for the moment, the use of his—”", "칙 부인이, 마치 모든 것을 처음 이야기하는 게 아니라 이미 다 합의된 바를 요약하는 사람의 어조로 말했다, \"그 지난번 발작으로 조금 약해졌고, 우리가 바랄 만큼 그렇게 정정하지는 못하다면, 그리고 그 애 체질에 일시적인 허약함이 좀 있어서, 이따금 잠깐씩 자기—자기—그—을 못 쓰게 될 것처럼 보인다면—\""]], [["Mrs Chick was afraid to say limbs, after Mr Dombey’s recent objection to bones, and therefore waited for a suggestion from Miss Tox, who, true to her office, hazarded “members.”", "칙 부인은 돔비 씨가 방금 뼈에 대해 반발한 터라 사지(四肢)라는 말을 하기가 겁이 나서, 톡스 양의 도움말을 기다렸다. 톡스 양은 제 소임에 충실하게 \"지체(肢體)\"라는 말을 슬쩍 던졌다."]], [["“Members!”", "\"지체라니!\""], ["repeated Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 되받았다."]], [["“I think the medical gentleman mentioned legs this morning, my dear Louisa, did he not?”", "\"오늘 아침에 그 의학 선생이 다리라고 한 것 같은데, 루이자, 안 그래요?\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."]], [["“Why, of course he did, my love,”", "\"아니, 당연히 그랬지, 얘,\""], ["retorted Mrs Chick, mildly reproachful.", "칙 부인이 부드럽게 나무라듯 받아쳤다."], ["“How can you ask me?", "\"어떻게 나한테 그걸 물어?"], ["You heard him.", "너도 들었잖니."], ["I say, if our dear Paul should lose, for the moment, the use of his legs, these are casualties common to many children at his time of life, and not to be prevented by any care or caution.", "내 말은, 우리 귀여운 폴이 잠깐씩 다리를 못 쓰게 된다 하더라도, 이런 건 그 또래의 많은 아이들에게 흔한 사고이고, 그 어떤 보살핌이나 조심으로도 막을 수 없는 일이라는 거야."], ["The sooner you understand that, Paul, and admit that, the better.", "그걸 빨리 알아들을수록, 폴, 그리고 그걸 인정할수록 좋아."], ["If you have any doubt as to the amount of care, and caution, and affection, and self-sacrifice, that has been bestowed upon little Paul, I should wish to refer the question to your medical attendant, or to any of your dependants in this house.", "만일 어린 폴에게 쏟아부은 보살핌과 조심과 애정과 자기희생의 양에 대해 조금이라도 의심이 든다면, 그 문제는 네 주치의에게나, 아니면 이 집안의 어느 아랫사람에게라도 한번 물어보고 싶구나. 타울린슨을 불러 봐,\""], ["Call Towlinson,” said Mrs Chick, “I believe he has no prejudice in our favour; quite the contrary.", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"그 사람은 우리한테 좋은 편견 같은 건 없는 줄로 아는데, 오히려 정반대지."], ["I should wish to hear what accusation Towlinson can make!”", "타울린슨이 무슨 비난을 할 수 있는지 한번 들어 보고 싶어!\""]], [["“Surely you must know, Louisa,”", "\"분명 너도 알 텐데, 루이자,\""], ["observed Mr Dombey, “that I don’t question your natural devotion to, and regard for, the future head of my house.”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"나는 내 집안의 미래 가장에 대한 너의 타고난 헌신과 애정을 의심하는 게 아니야.\""]], [["“I am glad to hear it, Paul,”", "\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘구나, 폴,\""], ["said Mrs Chick; “but really you are very odd, and sometimes talk very strangely, though without meaning it, I know.", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"하지만 정말이지 너는 참 별나고, 가끔 아주 이상하게 말을 한다니까, 본심은 아니라는 거 나도 알지만 말이야."], ["If your dear boy’s soul is too much for his body, Paul, you should remember whose fault that is—who he takes after, I mean—and make the best of it.", "만일 네 귀여운 아들의 영혼이 그 애 몸에 비해 너무 크다면, 폴, 그게 누구 탓인지를—그러니까 그 애가 누구를 닮았는지를—기억하고, 그걸 좋게 받아들여야 해."], ["He’s as like his Papa as he can be.", "그 애는 아빠를 더할 나위 없이 빼닮았어."], ["People have noticed it in the streets.", "길거리에서도 사람들이 알아본다니까."], ["The very beadle, I am informed, observed it, so long ago as at his christening.", "내가 듣기로는, 세례식 때 벌써 그 교구 직원조차도 그걸 알아봤대."], ["He’s a very respectable man, with children of his own.", "그 사람은 아주 점잖은 사람이고, 자기 자식들도 있어."], ["He ought to know.”", "알 만한 사람이지.\""]], [["“Mr Pilkins saw Paul this morning, I believe?”", "\"필킨스 씨가 오늘 아침에 폴을 보았다지요?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, he did,”", "\"그래, 봤어,\""], ["returned his sister.", "누이가 대꾸했다."], ["“Miss Tox and myself were present.", "\"톡스 양과 나도 함께 있었어."], ["Miss Tox and myself are always present.", "톡스 양과 나는 늘 함께 있지."], ["We make a point of it.", "우리는 그걸 원칙으로 삼고 있으니까."], ["Mr Pilkins has seen him for some days past, and a very clever man I believe him to be.", "필킨스 씨는 며칠째 그 애를 봐 왔는데, 내 보기엔 아주 영리한 사람이야. 그 사람 말로는 이렇다 할 게 없대."], ["He says it is nothing to speak of; which I can confirm, if that is any consolation; but he recommended, today, sea-air.", "그게 조금이라도 위안이 된다면, 나도 그 말을 확인해 줄 수 있어. 그런데 오늘은 바닷바람을 권했어."], ["Very wisely, Paul, I feel convinced.”", "아주 현명한 처사야, 폴, 나는 확신해.\""]], [["“Sea-air,”", "\"바닷바람,\""], ["repeated Mr Dombey, looking at his sister.", "돔비 씨가 누이를 보며 되뇌었다."]], [["“There is nothing to be made uneasy by, in that,”", "\"거기엔 불안해할 게 하나도 없어,\""], ["said Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."], ["“My George and Frederick were both ordered sea-air, when they were about his age; and I have been ordered it myself a great many times.", "\"우리 집 조지랑 프레더릭도 둘 다 그 또래 무렵에 바닷바람을 처방받았고, 나도 여러 번 처방받았는걸."], ["I quite agree with you, Paul, that perhaps topics may be incautiously mentioned upstairs before him, which it would be as well for his little mind not to expatiate upon; but I really don’t see how that is to be helped, in the case of a child of his quickness.", "폴, 나도 네 말에 전적으로 동의해, 어쩌면 위층에서 그 애 앞에서 부주의하게 입에 올린 화제들이 있어서, 그 애의 어린 마음이 그것을 곱씹지 않는 편이 나았을 거라는 점에 말이야. 하지만 그 애처럼 영민한 아이의 경우엔 그걸 어떻게 막을 수 있는지 정말 모르겠어."], ["If he were a common child, there would be nothing in it.", "그 애가 평범한 아이라면야 아무 문제도 없겠지."], ["I must say I think, with Miss Tox, that a short absence from this house, the air of Brighton, and the bodily and mental training of so judicious a person as Mrs Pipchin for instance—”", "나도 톡스 양과 같은 생각인데, 이 집을 잠시 떠나 있는 것과, 브라이턴의 공기와, 이를테면 핍친 부인처럼 그토록 분별 있는 사람의 신체적·정신적 훈련이라면—\""]], [["“Who is Mrs Pipchin, Louisa?”", "\"핍친 부인이 누구야, 루이자?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey; aghast at this familiar introduction of a name he had never heard before.", "돔비 씨가, 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 이름을 이렇게 허물없이 들이미는 데에 어이없어하며 물었다."]], [["“Mrs Pipchin, my dear Paul,”", "\"핍친 부인은, 폴,\""], ["returned his sister, “is an elderly lady—Miss Tox knows her whole history—who has for some time devoted all the energies of her mind, with the greatest success, to the study and treatment of infancy, and who has been extremely well connected.", "누이가 대꾸했다, \"나이 지긋한 부인인데—톡스 양이 그 사람 내력을 다 알아—얼마 전부터 자기 정신의 온 힘을 다 쏟아, 더없이 큰 성공을 거두며 유아 연구와 보육에 힘써 온 사람이고, 인맥도 아주 좋았어."], ["Her husband broke his heart in—how did you say her husband broke his heart, my dear?", "그 사람 남편은 가슴이 찢어져 죽었는데—얘, 그 남편이 어쩌다 가슴이 찢어져 죽었다고 했더라?"], ["I forget the precise circumstances.", "정확한 사정을 잊어버렸네.\""]], [["“In pumping water out of the Peruvian Mines,”", "\"페루 광산에서 물을 퍼내다가요,\""], ["replied Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 대답했다."]], [["“Not being a Pumper himself, of course,”", "\"물론 본인이 직접 물을 펐다는 건 아니고,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, glancing at her brother; and it really did seem necessary to offer the explanation, for Miss Tox had spoken of him as if he had died at the handle; “but having invested money in the speculation, which failed.", "칙 부인이 오라비를 흘끗 보며 말했다. 정말이지 그 설명을 덧붙일 필요가 있어 보였는데, 톡스 양이 마치 그가 펌프 손잡이를 잡은 채 죽기라도 한 것처럼 말했기 때문이다."], ["I believe that Mrs Pipchin’s management of children is quite astonishing.", "\"그 사업에 돈을 투자했는데, 그게 실패한 거야. 핍친 부인의 아이 다루는 솜씨는 정말 놀라운가 봐."], ["I have heard it commended in private circles ever since I was—dear me—how high!”", "내가—저런—이만했을 때부터 사람들이 사적인 자리에서 그걸 칭찬하는 걸 들어 왔으니까!\""], ["Mrs Chick’s eye wandered about the bookcase near the bust of Mr Pitt, which was about ten feet from the ground.", "칙 부인의 눈은 피트 씨 흉상 옆 책장 언저리를 헤맸는데, 그것은 바닥에서 십 피트쯤 되는 높이였다."]], [["“Perhaps I should say of Mrs Pipchin, my dear Sir,”", "\"핍친 부인에 대해 제가 한 말씀 드리는 게 좋을 듯합니다, 선생님,\""], ["observed Miss Tox, with an ingenuous blush, “having been so pointedly referred to, that the encomium which has been passed upon her by your sweet sister is well merited.", "톡스 양이 순진한 듯 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다, \"이렇게 콕 집어 거론되었으니 말입니다. 선생님의 다정하신 누이께서 그분에게 보내신 찬사는 마땅한 것입니다."], ["Many ladies and gentleman, now grown up to be interesting members of society, have been indebted to her care.", "지금은 다 자라 사회의 흥미로운 일원이 된 숱한 신사 숙녀들이 그분의 보살핌에 신세를 졌지요."], ["The humble individual who addresses you was once under her charge.", "지금 선생님께 말씀드리는 이 미천한 사람도 한때 그분의 보호 아래 있었답니다."], ["I believe juvenile nobility itself is no stranger to her establishment.”", "또 제가 알기로는 어린 귀족들조차 그분의 시설에 낯설지 않다고 해요.\""]], [["“Do I understand that this respectable matron keeps an establishment, Miss Tox?”", "\"그 점잖은 부인이 시설을 운영한다는 말이오, 톡스 양?\""], ["the Mr Dombey, condescendingly.", "돔비 씨가 짐짓 너그러이 물었다."]], [["“Why, I really don’t know,”", "\"글쎄요, 정말이지 모르겠어요,\""], ["rejoined that lady, “whether I am justified in calling it so.", "그 부인이 대꾸했다, \"제가 그것을 그렇게 불러도 될지 말이에요."], ["It is not a Preparatory School by any means.", "결코 예비학교 같은 건 아니에요."], ["Should I express my meaning,”", "제 뜻을 표현하자면,\""], ["said Miss Tox, with peculiar sweetness, “if I designated it an infantine Boarding-House of a very select description?”", "톡스 양이 유달리 다정하게 말했다, \"그것을 아주 엄선된 부류의 유아 기숙관이라고 일컬으면 될까요?\""]], [["“On an exceedingly limited and particular scale,”", "\"지극히 한정되고 특별한 규모로 말이지요,\""], ["suggested Mrs Chick, with a glance at her brother.", "칙 부인이 오라비를 흘끗 보며 거들었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Exclusion itself!”", "배타성 그 자체죠!\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."]], [["There was something in this.", "여기엔 무언가가 있었다."], ["Mrs Pipchin’s husband having broken his heart of the Peruvian mines was good.", "핍친 부인의 남편이 페루 광산 때문에 가슴이 찢어져 죽었다는 것은 좋았다."], ["It had a rich sound.", "그것은 풍성한 울림이 있었다."], ["Besides, Mr Dombey was in a state almost amounting to consternation at the idea of Paul remaining where he was one hour after his removal had been recommended by the medical practitioner.", "게다가 돔비 씨는, 의사가 옮기라고 권한 마당에 폴이 한 시간이라도 더 지금 있는 곳에 머문다는 생각에 거의 경악에 가까운 상태였다."], ["It was a stoppage and delay upon the road the child must traverse, slowly at the best, before the goal was reached.", "그것은, 잘해 봐야 더디게 갈 수밖에 없는 그 종착점에 닿기까지 아이가 가로질러야 할 그 길 위에서의 멈춤이요 지연이었다. 그들이 핍친 부인을 추천한 것은 그에게 큰 무게가 있었다."], ["Their recommendation of Mrs Pipchin had great weight with him; for he knew that they were jealous of any interference with their charge, and he never for a moment took it into account that they might be solicitous to divide a responsibility, of which he had, as shown just now, his own established views.", "두 사람이 자기들이 맡은 아이에 대한 그 어떤 간섭도 시샘한다는 것을 그는 알았기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 방금 보여 주었듯이 자기 나름의 확고한 견해를 가진 그 책임을, 두 사람이 어쩌면 나누어 지고 싶어 할지도 모른다는 것은 그는 한순간도 염두에 두지 않았다."], ["Broke his heart of the Peruvian mines, mused Mr Dombey.", "페루 광산 때문에 가슴이 찢어져 죽었다, 돔비 씨는 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["Well!", "흠!"], ["a very respectable way of doing It.", "참으로 점잖게 죽는 방식이로군."]], [["“Supposing we should decide, on to-morrow’s inquiries, to send Paul down to Brighton to this lady, who would go with him?”", "\"내일 알아본 끝에, 폴을 브라이턴의 그 부인에게 내려보내기로 결정한다면, 누가 그 애와 함께 가야 하지?\""], ["inquired Mr Dombey, after some reflection.", "돔비 씨가 한참 생각한 끝에 물었다."]], [["“I don’t think you could send the child anywhere at present without Florence, my dear Paul,”", "\"지금으로선 플로렌스 없이는 그 애를 어디로도 보낼 수 없을 거야, 폴,\""], ["returned his sister, hesitating.", "누이가 머뭇거리며 대꾸했다."], ["“It’s quite an infatuation with him.", "\"그 애가 누이한테 아주 푹 빠져 있거든."], ["He’s very young, you know, and has his fancies.”", "알다시피 그 애는 무척 어리고, 또 제 나름의 변덕이 있으니까.\""]], [["Mr Dombey turned his head away, and going slowly to the bookcase, and unlocking it, brought back a book to read.", "돔비 씨는 고개를 돌리고, 천천히 책장으로 가서 자물쇠를 열고는, 읽을 책 한 권을 가지고 돌아왔다."]], [["“Anybody else, Louisa?”", "\"또 누가 있겠나, 루이자?\""], ["he said, without looking up, and turning over the leaves.", "그가 고개도 들지 않고, 책장을 넘기며 말했다."]], [["“Wickam, of course.", "\"위컴은 당연하고."], ["Wickam would be quite sufficient, I should say,”", "위컴이면 충분할 거야, 내 보기엔,\""], ["returned his sister.", "누이가 대꾸했다."], ["“Paul being in such hands as Mrs Pipchin’s, you could hardly send anybody who would be a further check upon her.", "\"폴이 핍친 부인 같은 분의 손에 맡겨지는 마당에, 그 부인을 한층 더 견제할 만한 사람은 보내려야 보내기 어려울 거야."], ["You would go down yourself once a week at least, of course.”", "너야 물론 적어도 일주일에 한 번은 직접 내려가 볼 테고.\""]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론이지,\""], ["said Mr Dombey; and sat looking at one page for an hour afterwards, without reading one word.", "돔비 씨가 말했다. 그러고는 그 뒤로 한 시간 동안, 한 글자도 읽지 않으면서 한 쪽만 들여다보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["This celebrated Mrs Pipchin was a marvellous ill-favoured, ill-conditioned old lady, of a stooping figure, with a mottled face, like bad marble, a hook nose, and a hard grey eye, that looked as if it might have been hammered at on an anvil without sustaining any injury.", "이 명성 자자한 핍친 부인은 놀랍도록 못생기고 심술궂은 노부인으로, 허리가 구부정하고, 질 나쁜 대리석처럼 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴에, 갈고리 같은 코와, 모루 위에서 망치질을 당해도 끄떡없을 듯한 단단한 잿빛 눈을 지니고 있었다."], ["Forty years at least had elapsed since the Peruvian mines had been the death of Mr Pipchin; but his relict still wore black bombazeen, of such a lustreless, deep, dead, sombre shade, that gas itself couldn’t light her up after dark, and her presence was a quencher to any number of candles.", "페루 광산이 핍친 씨의 죽음이 된 지 적어도 사십 년은 흘렀건만, 그 미망인은 여태도 검은 봄버진을 입고 다녔는데, 그 빛깔이 어찌나 윤기 없고 짙고 죽은 듯 침침했던지, 어두워진 뒤엔 가스등으로도 그녀를 환히 밝힐 수가 없었고, 그녀가 있으면 아무리 많은 촛불도 꺼지는 듯했다."], ["She was generally spoken of as “a great manager” of children; and the secret of her management was, to give them everything that they didn’t like, and nothing that they did—which was found to sweeten their dispositions very much.", "사람들은 흔히 그녀를 아이들의 \"대단한 관리자\"라고 일컬었는데, 그 관리법의 비결은, 아이들이 싫어하는 것은 무엇이든 다 주고, 좋아하는 것은 아무것도 주지 않는 것이었다—그러면 아이들의 성정이 매우 누그러지는 것으로 드러났다."], ["She was such a bitter old lady, that one was tempted to believe there had been some mistake in the application of the Peruvian machinery, and that all her waters of gladness and milk of human kindness, had been pumped out dry, instead of the mines.", "그녀가 어찌나 신랄한 노부인이었던지, 페루의 그 펌프 기계가 잘못 쓰인 게 아닐까, 광산 대신 그녀의 모든 기쁨의 샘물과 인정의 젖이 죄다 바싹 퍼내져 버린 게 아닐까 믿고 싶어질 지경이었다. * 봄버진(bombazeen): 비단과 모직을 섞어 짠 직물로, 흔히 상복에 쓰였다."]], [["The Castle of this ogress and child-queller was in a steep by-street at Brighton; where the soil was more than usually chalky, flinty, and sterile, and the houses were more than usually brittle and thin; where the small front-gardens had the unaccountable property of producing nothing but marigolds, whatever was sown in them; and where snails were constantly discovered holding on to the street doors, and other public places they were not expected to ornament, with the tenacity of cupping-glasses.", "이 식인귀 같은, 아이 잡는 노파의 성채는 브라이턴의 어느 가파른 골목길에 있었다. 그곳은 흙이 유달리 백악질에 부싯돌투성이고 메말랐으며, 집들은 유달리 잘 부서지고 얇았다."], ["In the winter time the air couldn’t be got out of the Castle, and in the summer time it couldn’t be got in.", "그곳의 자그마한 앞뜰들은 무엇을 심든 메리골드 말고는 아무것도 피우지 못하는 영문 모를 성질을 지녔고, 그곳에서는 달팽이들이 거리로 난 문이며, 도무지 장식할 거라고 기대되지 않는 다른 공공장소들에 부항단지처럼 끈질기게 들러붙어 있는 것이 늘 발견되곤 했다."], ["There was such a continual reverberation of wind in it, that it sounded like a great shell, which the inhabitants were obliged to hold to their ears night and day, whether they liked it or no.", "겨울철엔 성채에서 공기를 빼낼 수가 없었고, 여름철엔 공기를 들일 수가 없었다. 그 안에서 바람이 어찌나 끊임없이 메아리쳤던지, 마치 커다란 소라 껍데기 같아서, 거주자들은 좋든 싫든 밤낮으로 그것을 귀에 대고 있을 수밖에 없었다."], ["It was not, naturally, a fresh-smelling house; and in the window of the front parlour, which was never opened, Mrs Pipchin kept a collection of plants in pots, which imparted an earthy flavour of their own to the establishment.", "본디 향긋한 냄새가 나는 집은 아니었다. 그리고 한 번도 열리지 않는 앞쪽 거실 창가에 핍친 부인은 화분에 심은 식물들을 모아 두었는데, 그것들이 자기들 나름의 흙냄새를 그 시설에 보태 주었다."], ["However choice examples of their kind, too, these plants were of a kind peculiarly adapted to the embowerment of Mrs Pipchin.", "게다가 그 종류로는 더없이 빼어난 표본들이었음에도, 이 식물들은 핍친 부인을 두르기에 유달리 어울리는 종류였다. 거기엔 선인장 표본이 대여섯 점 있었는데, 털북숭이 뱀들처럼 가느다란 나무오리를 휘감고 비틀려 있었다."], ["There were half-a-dozen specimens of the cactus, writhing round bits of lath, like hairy serpents; another specimen shooting out broad claws, like a green lobster; several creeping vegetables, possessed of sticky and adhesive leaves; and one uncomfortable flower-pot hanging to the ceiling, which appeared to have boiled over, and tickling people underneath with its long green ends, reminded them of spiders—in which Mrs Pipchin’s dwelling was uncommonly prolific, though perhaps it challenged competition still more proudly, in the season, in point of earwigs.", "또 다른 표본 하나는 초록빛 바닷가재처럼 넓적한 집게발을 뻗치고 있었다. 끈적끈적 들러붙는 잎을 지닌 덩굴 식물도 여럿 있었고, 천장에 매달린 보기 흉한 화분이 하나 있었는데, 그것은 마치 끓어 넘친 것처럼 보였으며, 길게 늘어진 초록빛 끝자락으로 그 아래를 지나는 사람들을 간질여 거미를 떠올리게 했다—거미로 말하자면 핍친 부인의 거처가 유난히 풍성하게 길러 냈으나, 어쩌면 철이 되면 집게벌레라는 점에서 한층 더 자랑스레 경쟁을 걸었을지도 모른다."]], [["Mrs Pipchin’s scale of charges being high, however, to all who could afford to pay, and Mrs Pipchin very seldom sweetening the equable acidity of her nature in favour of anybody, she was held to be an old “lady of remarkable firmness, who was quite scientific in her knowledge of the childish character.”", "그렇지만 핍친 부인의 청구 요금 기준은, 낼 형편이 되는 모든 이에게 높았고, 또 핍친 부인은 누구를 위해서든 자기 본성의 한결같은 신맛을 좀체 누그러뜨리는 법이 없었으므로, 그녀는 \"어린아이의 성격에 관한 지식이 가히 과학적인, 의지가 놀랍도록 굳은 노부인\"으로 통했다."], ["On this reputation, and on the broken heart of Mr Pipchin, she had contrived, taking one year with another, to eke out a tolerable sufficient living since her husband’s demise.", "이런 평판과, 핍친 씨의 찢어진 가슴 덕분에, 그녀는 남편이 죽은 이래로 이 해 저 해 따져 가며 그럭저럭 넉넉한 생계를 꾸려 올 수 있었다."], ["Within three days after Mrs Chick’s first allusion to her, this excellent old lady had the satisfaction of anticipating a handsome addition to her current receipts, from the pocket of Mr Dombey; and of receiving Florence and her little brother Paul, as inmates of the Castle.", "칙 부인이 처음 그녀를 입에 올린 지 사흘 만에, 이 훌륭한 노부인은 돔비 씨의 주머니에서 자기의 현 수입에 짭짤한 보탬이 더해지리라 미리 내다보는 흐뭇함을 누렸고, 플로렌스와 그녀의 어린 동생 폴을 성채의 식구로 맞아들이게 되었다."]], [["Mrs Chick and Miss Tox, who had brought them down on the previous night (which they all passed at an Hotel), had just driven away from the door, on their journey home again; and Mrs Pipchin, with her back to the fire, stood, reviewing the new-comers, like an old soldier.", "전날 밤 둘을 데리고 내려온(다들 한 호텔에서 그 밤을 보냈다) 칙 부인과 톡스 양은 막 문 앞에서 마차를 몰아 다시 집으로 가는 길에 올랐다. 그리고 핍친 부인은 난로를 등진 채 서서, 늙은 병사처럼 새로 온 아이들을 살피고 있었다."], ["Mrs Pipchin’s middle-aged niece, her good-natured and devoted slave, but possessing a gaunt and iron-bound aspect, and much afflicted with boils on her nose, was divesting Master Bitherstone of the clean collar he had worn on parade.", "핍친 부인의 중년 조카딸은, 마음씨 곱고 헌신적인 그녀의 노예였으나 비쩍 마르고 쇠틀에 죈 듯한 인상에 코에 뾰루지가 잔뜩 나서 무척 시달리는 사람이었는데, 비더스톤 도련님이 행진할 때 차고 있던 깨끗한 깃을 벗기고 있었다."], ["Miss Pankey, the only other little boarder at present, had that moment been walked off to the Castle Dungeon (an empty apartment at the back, devoted to correctional purposes), for having sniffed thrice, in the presence of visitors.", "지금으로선 단 하나뿐인 또 다른 어린 기숙생 팽키 양은, 손님들 앞에서 세 번 코를 훌쩍였다는 죄로, 방금 성채 지하 감옥(뒤편에 있는, 교정 목적에 바쳐진 빈방)으로 끌려간 참이었다."]], [["“Well, Sir,”", "\"자, 도련님,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin to Paul, “how do you think you shall like me?”", "핍친 부인이 폴에게 말했다, \"내가 어떨 것 같은가? 마음에 들 것 같은가?\""]], [["“I don’t think I shall like you at all,”", "\"아주머니가 전혀 마음에 안 들 것 같아요,\""], ["replied Paul.", "폴이 대꾸했다."], ["“I want to go away.", "\"저는 가고 싶어요."], ["This isn’t my house.”", "여긴 우리 집이 아니에요.\""]], [["“No.", "\"아무렴."], ["It’s mine,”", "여긴 내 집이지,\""], ["retorted Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 받아쳤다."]], [["“It’s a very nasty one,”", "\"아주 고약한 집이에요,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“There’s a worse place in it than this though,”", "\"하지만 이 집엔 여기보다 더 나쁜 데도 있단다,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, “where we shut up our bad boys.”", "핍친 부인이 말했다, \"우리가 나쁜 아이들을 가둬 두는 데 말이야.\""]], [["“Has he ever been in it?”", "\"쟤도 거기 들어가 봤어요?\""], ["asked Paul: pointing out Master Bitherstone.", "폴이 비더스톤 도련님을 가리키며 물었다."]], [["Mrs Pipchin nodded assent; and Paul had enough to do, for the rest of that day, in surveying Master Bitherstone from head to foot, and watching all the workings of his countenance, with the interest attaching to a boy of mysterious and terrible experiences.", "핍친 부인이 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였고, 그날 남은 시간 동안 폴은, 비더스톤 도련님을 머리끝부터 발끝까지 뜯어보고, 그 얼굴에 떠오르는 온갖 움직임을 지켜보는 일로 분주했다. 신비롭고 끔찍한 경험을 한 소년에게 따라붙기 마련인 그 흥미를 느끼면서 말이다."]], [["At one o’clock there was a dinner, chiefly of the farinaceous and vegetable kind, when Miss Pankey (a mild little blue-eyed morsel of a child, who was shampoo’d every morning, and seemed in danger of being rubbed away, altogether) was led in from captivity by the ogress herself, and instructed that nobody who sniffed before visitors ever went to Heaven.", "한 시가 되자 저녁 식사가 나왔는데, 주로 곡물과 채소 종류였다. 그때 팽키 양(아침마다 마사지를 받아서, 통째로 문질러 닳아 없어질 위험에 처한 듯한, 온순하고 자그마한 파란 눈의 한 조각 아이였다)이 그 식인귀 노파에게 직접 이끌려 갇혀 있던 데서 나왔고, 손님들 앞에서 코를 훌쩍이는 자는 누구도 천국에 가지 못한다는 가르침을 받았다."], ["When this great truth had been thoroughly impressed upon her, she was regaled with rice; and subsequently repeated the form of grace established in the Castle, in which there was a special clause, thanking Mrs Pipchin for a good dinner.", "이 위대한 진리가 그녀에게 톡톡히 새겨지자, 그녀에게는 쌀밥이 상으로 주어졌다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 성채에서 정해진 형식의 감사 기도를 읊었는데, 거기엔 맛있는 저녁을 주신 핍친 부인에게 감사하는 특별 조항이 들어 있었다."], ["Mrs Pipchin’s niece, Berinthia, took cold pork.", "핍친 부인의 조카딸 베린시아는 식은 돼지고기를 먹었다."], ["Mrs Pipchin, whose constitution required warm nourishment, made a special repast of mutton-chops, which were brought in hot and hot, between two plates, and smelt very nice.", "핍친 부인은, 체질상 따뜻한 영양이 필요하다 하여, 양고기 갈비로 특식을 차렸는데, 그것은 접시 두 장 사이에 끼워 뜨겁고 뜨겁게 들어왔고 아주 맛있는 냄새가 났다."]], [["As it rained after dinner, and they couldn’t go out walking on the beach, and Mrs Pipchin’s constitution required rest after chops, they went away with Berry (otherwise Berinthia) to the Dungeon; an empty room looking out upon a chalk wall and a water-butt, and made ghastly by a ragged fireplace without any stove in it. Enlivened by company, however, this was the best place after all; for Berry played with them there, and seemed to enjoy a game at romps as much as they did; until Mrs Pipchin knocking angrily at the wall, like the Cock Lane Ghost revived, they left off, and Berry told them stories in a whisper until twilight.", "식사 후에 비가 와서 바닷가로 산책을 나갈 수도 없었고, 또 갈비를 먹은 핍친 부인의 체질이 휴식을 요했으므로, 아이들은 베리(다른 말로 베린시아)와 함께 지하 감옥으로 갔다. 그곳은 백악 벽과 빗물통이 내다보이는 빈방이었고, 안에 난로 한 짝 없는 너덜너덜한 벽난로 자리 때문에 으스스했다. 그러나 사람들이 있어 흥겨워지니 그곳은 결국 가장 좋은 곳이었다. 베리가 거기서 아이들과 놀아 주었고, 또 아이들 못지않게 뒹굴며 노는 놀이를 즐기는 듯 보였으니까. 그러다 핍친 부인이 되살아난 콕 레인 유령처럼 화가 나서 벽을 두드리자, 아이들은 놀이를 멈추었고, 베리는 땅거미가 질 때까지 속삭이는 목소리로 이야기를 들려주었다. * 콕 레인 유령(Cock Lane Ghost): 18세기 런던에서 화제가 된 유명한 유령 소동."]], [["For tea there was plenty of milk and water, and bread and butter, with a little black tea-pot for Mrs Pipchin and Berry, and buttered toast unlimited for Mrs Pipchin, which was brought in, hot and hot, like the chops.", "차로는 물 탄 우유와, 버터 바른 빵이 넉넉히 나왔고, 핍친 부인과 베리를 위한 자그마한 검은 찻주전자와, 핍친 부인을 위한 무제한의 버터 바른 토스트가 있었는데, 그 토스트는 갈비처럼 뜨겁고 뜨겁게 들어왔다."], ["Though Mrs Pipchin got very greasy, outside, over this dish, it didn’t seem to lubricate her internally, at all; for she was as fierce as ever, and the hard grey eye knew no softening.", "핍친 부인은 이 음식으로 겉은 기름투성이가 되었지만, 그것이 그녀의 속을 조금도 매끄럽게 해 주지는 못하는 듯했다. 그녀는 여전히 사나웠고, 그 단단한 잿빛 눈은 누그러질 줄을 몰랐다."]], [["After tea, Berry brought out a little work-box, with the Royal Pavilion on the lid, and fell to working busily; while Mrs Pipchin, having put on her spectacles and opened a great volume bound in green baize, began to nod.", "차를 마신 뒤, 베리는 뚜껑에 왕실 별궁이 그려진 자그마한 반짇고리를 꺼내 부지런히 바느질에 매달렸다. 한편 핍친 부인은 안경을 쓰고 초록색 베이즈 천으로 장정된 큰 책을 펼쳐 들고는 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작했다."], ["And whenever Mrs Pipchin caught herself falling forward into the fire, and woke up, she filliped Master Bitherstone on the nose for nodding too.", "그리고 핍친 부인은 자기가 난롯불 쪽으로 앞으로 고꾸라지려는 것을 알아채고 깨어날 때마다, 비더스톤 도련님도 꾸벅거린다는 이유로 그의 코를 손가락으로 튕겼다."]], [["At last it was the children’s bedtime, and after prayers they went to bed.", "마침내 아이들이 잘 시간이 되었고, 기도를 마친 뒤 다들 잠자리에 들었다."], ["As little Miss Pankey was afraid of sleeping alone in the dark, Mrs Pipchin always made a point of driving her upstairs herself, like a sheep; and it was cheerful to hear Miss Pankey moaning long afterwards, in the least eligible chamber, and Mrs Pipchin now and then going in to shake her.", "어린 팽키 양은 어둠 속에서 혼자 자는 것을 무서워했으므로, 핍친 부인은 늘 양 몰듯이 그녀를 직접 위층으로 몰아넣는 것을 원칙으로 삼았다. 그리고 그 뒤로 한참 동안 가장 살기 나쁜 방에서 팽키 양이 끙끙대는 소리와, 핍친 부인이 이따금 들어가 그녀를 흔들어 대는 소리를 듣자니 흥겨웠다."], ["At about half-past nine o’clock the odour of a warm sweet-bread (Mrs Pipchin’s constitution wouldn’t go to sleep without sweet-bread) diversified the prevailing fragrance of the house, which Mrs Wickam said was “a smell of building;”", "아홉 시 반쯤이 되자, 따뜻한 스위트브레드(핍친 부인의 체질은 스위트브레드 없이는 잠들지 않았다)의 냄새가, 집안에 감도는 그 향기—위컴 부인이 \"집 짓는 냄새\"라고 한—에 변주를 더했다. 그러고 얼마 지나지 않아 성채에는 잠이 내려앉았다."], ["and slumber fell upon the Castle shortly after.", "* 스위트브레드(sweet-bread): 송아지나 양의 흉선이나 췌장으로 만든 요리."]], [["The breakfast next morning was like the tea over night, except that Mrs Pipchin took her roll instead of toast, and seemed a little more irate when it was over.", "이튿날 아침 식사는 지난밤의 차와 비슷했다. 다만 핍친 부인이 토스트 대신 둥근 빵을 먹었고, 식사가 끝나자 좀 더 성을 내는 듯했다는 점만 달랐다."], ["Master Bitherstone read aloud to the rest a pedigree from Genesis (judiciously selected by Mrs Pipchin), getting over the names with the ease and clearness of a person tumbling up the treadmill.", "비더스톤 도련님이 나머지 아이들에게 「창세기」에서 뽑은 족보를(핍친 부인이 분별 있게 골라 준 것이었다) 소리 내어 읽었는데, 마치 발판 디딤틀을 굴러 올라가는 사람처럼 쉽고도 또렷하게 이름들을 척척 넘겼다."], ["That done, Miss Pankey was borne away to be shampoo’d; and Master Bitherstone to have something else done to him with salt water, from which he always returned very blue and dejected.", "그것이 끝나자 팽키 양은 마사지를 받으러 끌려갔고, 비더스톤 도련님은 소금물로 무언가 다른 처치를 받으러 끌려갔는데, 거기서 돌아올 때면 그는 늘 시퍼렇고 풀이 죽어 있었다."], ["Paul and Florence went out in the meantime on the beach with Wickam—who was constantly in tears—and at about noon Mrs Pipchin presided over some Early Readings.", "그동안 폴과 플로렌스는 위컴과 함께 바닷가로 나갔는데—위컴은 줄곧 눈물바람이었다—정오쯤 되면 핍친 부인이 몇몇 조기 독서 시간을 주재했다."], ["It being a part of Mrs Pipchin’s system not to encourage a child’s mind to develop and expand itself like a young flower, but to open it by force like an oyster, the moral of these lessons was usually of a violent and stunning character: the hero—a naughty boy—seldom, in the mildest catastrophe, being finished off anything less than a lion, or a bear.", "어린아이의 마음을 어린 꽃처럼 펼치고 자라나게 북돋우는 것이 아니라, 굴 까듯이 억지로 비틀어 여는 것이 핍친 부인의 방침이었던 까닭에, 이 수업들의 교훈은 으레 난폭하고 충격적인 성격을 띠었다. 주인공—못된 사내아이—은, 아무리 온건한 결말이라도, 적어도 사자나 곰에게 끝장나는 것이 보통이었다."]], [["Such was life at Mrs Pipchin’s.", "핍친 부인의 집에서의 생활이란 이런 것이었다."], ["On Saturday Mr Dombey came down; and Florence and Paul would go to his Hotel, and have tea.", "토요일이면 돔비 씨가 내려왔다. 그러면 플로렌스와 폴은 그의 호텔로 가서 차를 마셨다."], ["They passed the whole of Sunday with him, and generally rode out before dinner; and on these occasions Mr Dombey seemed to grow, like Falstaff’s assailants, and instead of being one man in buckram, to become a dozen.", "둘은 일요일 하루를 온전히 그와 함께 보냈고, 대개는 저녁 식사 전에 마차를 타고 나가곤 했다. 그런 때 돔비 씨는 폴스타프를 습격한 자들처럼 점점 불어나서, 거친 삼베옷을 입은 한 사람이 아니라 열두 사람쯤 되는 듯했다."], ["Sunday evening was the most melancholy evening in the week; for Mrs Pipchin always made a point of being particularly cross on Sunday nights.", "일요일 저녁은 한 주 가운데 가장 우울한 저녁이었다. 핍친 부인이 일요일 밤이면 늘 유달리 심술궂게 구는 것을 원칙으로 삼았기 때문이다."], ["Miss Pankey was generally brought back from an aunt’s at Rottingdean, in deep distress; and Master Bitherstone, whose relatives were all in India, and who was required to sit, between the services, in an erect position with his head against the parlour wall, neither moving hand nor foot, suffered so acutely in his young spirits that he once asked Florence, on a Sunday night, if she could give him any idea of the way back to Bengal.", "팽키 양은 보통 로팅딘의 고모 집에서 깊은 시름에 잠긴 채 돌아왔고, 친척이 모두 인도에 있는 비더스톤 도련님은, 예배와 예배 사이에 손발 하나 까딱 않고 머리를 거실 벽에 댄 채 꼿꼿한 자세로 앉아 있어야 했는데, 그의 어린 마음이 어찌나 사무치게 괴로웠던지, 한번은 일요일 밤에 플로렌스에게 벵골로 돌아가는 길을 조금이라도 일러 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물은 적도 있었다. * 폴스타프를 습격한 자들: 셰익스피어 「헨리 4세」에서 폴스타프가 강도 둘에게 습격당했다고 허풍을 떨면서, 이야기할 때마다 적의 수가 점점 불어났던 대목을 가리킨다."]], [["But it was generally said that Mrs Pipchin was a woman of system with children; and no doubt she was.", "그러나 사람들은 으레, 핍친 부인이 아이들을 다루는 데 체계가 잡힌 여자라고 말했다. 의심할 바 없이 그랬다."], ["Certainly the wild ones went home tame enough, after sojourning for a few months beneath her hospitable roof.", "분명 그 거친 아이들도 몇 달 동안 그녀의 따뜻한 지붕 아래 머물고 나면 충분히 길든 채로 집에 돌아갔다."], ["It was generally said, too, that it was highly creditable of Mrs Pipchin to have devoted herself to this way of life, and to have made such a sacrifice of her feelings, and such a resolute stand against her troubles, when Mr Pipchin broke his heart in the Peruvian mines.", "또 사람들은 으레, 핍친 씨가 페루 광산에서 가슴이 찢어져 죽었을 때, 핍친 부인이 이렇게 사는 방식에 자신을 바치고, 그토록 자기 감정을 희생하며, 그토록 자기 고난에 맞서 의연히 버텨 온 것은 참으로 칭찬할 만한 일이라고 말했다."]], [["At this exemplary old lady, Paul would sit staring in his little arm-chair by the fire, for any length of time.", "이 본보기 같은 노부인을, 폴은 난롯가의 자기 자그마한 안락의자에 앉아 한없이 빤히 바라보곤 했다."], ["He never seemed to know what weariness was, when he was looking fixedly at Mrs Pipchin.", "그는 핍친 부인을 뚫어지게 바라볼 때면 피로라는 것을 도무지 모르는 듯했다."], ["He was not fond of her; he was not afraid of her; but in those old, old moods of his, she seemed to have a grotesque attraction for him.", "그는 그녀를 좋아하지도 않았고, 그녀를 무서워하지도 않았다. 그러나 그 늙디늙은 기분에 잠겨 있을 때면, 그녀가 그에게 어딘가 기괴한 끌림을 지닌 듯했다."], ["There he would sit, looking at her, and warming his hands, and looking at her, until he sometimes quite confounded Mrs Pipchin, Ogress as she was.", "그는 거기 앉아, 그녀를 바라보고, 두 손을 녹이고, 또 그녀를 바라보았는데, 그러다 보면 식인귀 같은 핍친 부인조차 이따금 적잖이 당황할 지경이었다."], ["Once she asked him, when they were alone, what he was thinking about.", "한번은 둘만 있을 때 그녀가 그에게 무슨 생각을 하고 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“You,”", "\"아주머니요,\""], ["said Paul, without the least reserve.", "폴이 조금도 거리낌 없이 말했다."]], [["“And what are you thinking about me?”", "\"그래, 나에 대해 무슨 생각을 하는데?\""], ["asked Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 물었다."]], [["“I’m thinking how old you must be,”", "\"아주머니가 도대체 몇 살일까 생각하고 있어요,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“You mustn’t say such things as that, young gentleman,”", "\"그런 말은 하면 못쓴단다, 도련님,\""], ["returned the dame.", "그 노부인이 대꾸했다."], ["“That’ll never do.”", "\"그러면 절대 안 돼.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜요?\""], ["asked Paul.", "폴이 물었다."]], [["“Because it’s not polite,”", "\"무례한 거니까,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, snappishly.", "핍친 부인이 톡 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“Not polite?”", "\"무례한 거예요?\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“No.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“It’s not polite,”", "\"양고기 갈비랑 토스트를 다 먹어 치우는 게 무례한 거래요,\""], ["said Paul, innocently, “to eat all the mutton chops and toast”, Wickam says.", "폴이 천진하게 말했다, \"위컴이 그러던데요.\""]], [["“Wickam,”", "\"위컴은,\""], ["retorted Mrs Pipchin, colouring, “is a wicked, impudent, bold-faced hussy.”", "핍친 부인이 얼굴을 붉히며 받아쳤다, \"사악하고, 뻔뻔하고, 낯짝 두꺼운 계집이야.\""]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"그게 뭐예요?\""], ["inquired Paul.", "폴이 물었다."]], [["“Never you mind, Sir,”", "\"도련님은 알 거 없어요,\""], ["retorted Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 받아쳤다."], ["“Remember the story of the little boy that was gored to death by a mad bull for asking questions.”", "\"질문을 했다고 미친 황소한테 뿔에 받혀 죽은 어린 사내아이 이야기를 기억하렴.\""]], [["“If the bull was mad,”", "\"황소가 미쳤다면,\""], ["said Paul, “how did he know that the boy had asked questions?", "폴이 말했다, \"그 사내아이가 질문을 했다는 걸 어떻게 알았어요?"], ["Nobody can go and whisper secrets to a mad bull.", "미친 황소한테 가서 비밀을 속삭여 줄 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없잖아요."], ["I don’t believe that story.”", "저는 그 이야기 안 믿어요.\""]], [["“You don’t believe it, Sir?”", "\"그걸 안 믿는다고, 도련님?\""], ["repeated Mrs Pipchin, amazed.", "핍친 부인이 어이없어하며 되받았다."]], [["“No,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“Not if it should happen to have been a tame bull, you little Infidel?”", "\"그게 혹시 길든 황소였더라도 안 믿겠다는 거냐, 이 꼬마 이교도야?\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 말했다."]], [["As Paul had not considered the subject in that light, and had founded his conclusions on the alleged lunacy of the bull, he allowed himself to be put down for the present.", "폴은 그 문제를 그런 각도에서 생각해 본 적이 없었고, 자기 결론을 황소가 미쳤다는 그 주장에 근거해 세웠던 터라, 지금으로선 잠자코 물러나 주기로 했다."], ["But he sat turning it over in his mind, with such an obvious intention of fixing Mrs Pipchin presently, that even that hardy old lady deemed it prudent to retreat until he should have forgotten the subject.", "그러나 그는 머잖아 핍친 부인을 꼼짝 못 하게 만들 작정이 너무나 뻔히 드러나는 태도로, 그 일을 마음속에서 곱씹으며 앉아 있었기에, 그 강단 있는 노부인조차 그가 그 주제를 잊을 때까지 일단 물러나 있는 편이 현명하겠다고 여겼다."]], [["From that time, Mrs Pipchin appeared to have something of the same odd kind of attraction towards Paul, as Paul had towards her.", "그때부터 핍친 부인은, 폴이 그녀에게 그러하듯, 폴에게 무언가 그 비슷한 묘한 종류의 끌림을 느끼는 듯했다."], ["She would make him move his chair to her side of the fire, instead of sitting opposite; and there he would remain in a nook between Mrs Pipchin and the fender, with all the light of his little face absorbed into the black bombazeen drapery, studying every line and wrinkle of her countenance, and peering at the hard grey eye, until Mrs Pipchin was sometimes fain to shut it, on pretence of dozing.", "그녀는 그에게, 난로 맞은편에 앉는 대신 의자를 자기 쪽으로 옮기게 했다. 그러면 그는 핍친 부인과 난로 울 사이의 한구석에 자리 잡고서, 자기 작은 얼굴의 빛을 죄다 그 검은 봄버진 옷자락에 빨아들인 채, 그녀의 얼굴 구석구석의 선과 주름을 살피고, 그 단단한 잿빛 눈을 들여다보곤 했으니, 핍친 부인은 이따금 조는 척하며 눈을 감을 수밖에 없을 지경이었다."], ["Mrs Pipchin had an old black cat, who generally lay coiled upon the centre foot of the fender, purring egotistically, and winking at the fire until the contracted pupils of his eyes were like two notes of admiration.", "핍친 부인에게는 늙은 검은 고양이가 한 마리 있었는데, 보통은 난로 울의 한가운데 발에 똬리를 틀고 누워, 자기밖에 모르는 듯 가르랑거리며 불을 향해 눈을 깜박이곤 했다. 그러다 보면 그 오므라든 눈동자가 마치 느낌표 두 개 같았다."], ["The good old lady might have been—not to record it disrespectfully—a witch, and Paul and the cat her two familiars, as they all sat by the fire together.", "그렇게 셋이 다 함께 난롯가에 앉아 있노라면, 그 착한 노부인은—무례하게 적어 두려는 것은 아니지만—마녀였을지도 모르고, 폴과 고양이는 그녀의 두 부리는 마귀였을지도 몰랐다."], ["It would have been quite in keeping with the appearance of the party if they had all sprung up the chimney in a high wind one night, and never been heard of any more.", "만일 어느 날 밤 거센 바람이 불 때 셋이 다 함께 굴뚝으로 솟구쳐 올라가 다시는 소식이 끊겼다 한들, 그 일행의 모습과 더없이 잘 어울렸으리라."]], [["This, however, never came to pass.", "그러나 그런 일은 끝내 일어나지 않았다."], ["The cat, and Paul, and Mrs Pipchin, were constantly to be found in their usual places after dark; and Paul, eschewing the companionship of Master Bitherstone, went on studying Mrs Pipchin, and the cat, and the fire, night after night, as if they were a book of necromancy, in three volumes.", "고양이와, 폴과, 핍친 부인은, 날이 어두워지면 늘 그들의 평소 자리에서 변함없이 찾아볼 수 있었다. 그리고 폴은 비더스톤 도련님과 어울리기를 마다하고, 밤마다 핍친 부인과, 고양이와, 난롯불을, 마치 그것들이 세 권으로 된 마법서라도 되는 양 줄곧 연구해 나갔다."]], [["Mrs Wickam put her own construction on Paul’s eccentricities; and being confirmed in her low spirits by a perplexed view of chimneys from the room where she was accustomed to sit, and by the noise of the wind, and by the general dulness (gashliness was Mrs Wickam’s strong expression) of her present life, deduced the most dismal reflections from the foregoing premises.", "위컴 부인은 폴의 별난 행동을 자기 나름으로 해석했다. 그리고 자기가 늘 앉아 있곤 하던 방에서 보이는 어지러운 굴뚝들의 광경과, 바람 소리와, 지금 자기 생활의 전반적인 따분함(위컴 부인의 힘준 표현으로는 '소름 끼침'이었다)으로 우울한 기분이 더욱 굳어진 그녀는, 앞서 말한 전제들로부터 더없이 음울한 사색을 끌어냈다."], ["It was a part of Mrs Pipchin’s policy to prevent her own “young hussy”—that was Mrs Pipchin’s generic name for female servant—from communicating with Mrs Wickam: to which end she devoted much of her time to concealing herself behind doors, and springing out on that devoted maiden, whenever she made an approach towards Mrs Wickam’s apartment.", "자기네 \"어린 계집\"이—이것이 여자 하인을 가리키는 핍친 부인의 통칭이었다—위컴 부인과 통하는 것을 막는 것이 핍친 부인의 방침 가운데 하나였다. 그 목적을 위해 그녀는 자기 시간의 많은 부분을, 문 뒤에 숨어 있다가 그 가엾은 처녀가 위컴 부인의 방 쪽으로 다가갈 때마다 튀어나오는 데에 바쳤다."], ["But Berry was free to hold what converse she could in that quarter, consistently with the discharge of the multifarious duties at which she toiled incessantly from morning to night; and to Berry Mrs Wickam unburdened her mind.", "그러나 베리는, 아침부터 밤까지 쉴 새 없이 매달려 일하는 그 잡다한 임무를 다하는 것과 어긋나지 않는 한, 그 방면에서 나눌 수 있는 만큼의 이야기는 자유로이 나눌 수 있었다. 그리하여 위컴 부인은 베리에게 자기 속내를 털어놓곤 했다."]], [["“What a pretty fellow he is when he’s asleep!”", "\"잠들었을 땐 정말 예쁜 아이네요!\""], ["said Berry, stopping to look at Paul in bed, one night when she took up Mrs Wickam’s supper.", "어느 날 밤 위컴 부인의 야식을 들고 올라간 베리가, 침대에 누운 폴을 보려고 걸음을 멈추며 말했다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["sighed Mrs Wickam.", "위컴 부인이 한숨지었다."], ["“He need be.”", "\"그래야지요.\""]], [["“Why, he’s not ugly when he’s awake,”", "\"아니, 깨어 있어도 못생기지 않았는걸요,\""], ["observed Berry.", "베리가 말했다."]], [["“No, Ma’am.", "\"그럼요, 아가씨."], ["Oh, no.", "오, 그럼요."], ["No more was my Uncle’s Betsey Jane,”", "우리 외삼촌네 베치 제인도 안 그랬어요,\""], ["said Mrs Wickam.", "위컴 부인이 말했다."]], [["Berry looked as if she would like to trace the connexion of ideas between Paul Dombey and Mrs Wickam’s Uncle’s Betsey Jane.", "베리는 폴 돔비와 위컴 부인의 외삼촌네 베치 제인 사이에 무슨 생각의 연결 고리가 있는지 짚어 보고 싶다는 표정이었다."]], [["“My Uncle’s wife,”", "\"우리 외삼촌네 안사람이,\""], ["Mrs Wickam went on to say, “died just like his Mama.", "위컴 부인이 말을 이었다, \"그 애 엄마처럼 꼭 그렇게 죽었어요."], ["My Uncle’s child took on just as Master Paul do.”", "외삼촌네 아이도 폴 도련님처럼 꼭 그렇게 시름시름했지요.\""]], [["“Took on!", "\"시름시름했다니!"], ["You don’t think he grieves for his Mama, sure?”", "설마 저 애가 엄마를 그리워한다는 건 아니죠?\""], ["argued Berry, sitting down on the side of the bed.", "베리가 침대 가에 앉으며 따지듯 말했다."], ["“He can’t remember anything about her, you know, Mrs Wickam.", "\"엄마에 대해선 아무것도 기억 못 할 거 아니에요, 위컴 부인."], ["It’s not possible.”", "그럴 리가 없어요.\""]], [["“No, Ma’am,”", "\"그렇지요, 아가씨,\""], ["said Mrs Wickam “No more did my Uncle’s child.", "위컴 부인이 말했다. \"우리 외삼촌네 아이도 그랬어요."], ["But my Uncle’s child said very strange things sometimes, and looked very strange, and went on very strange, and was very strange altogether.", "그래도 외삼촌네 아이는 가끔 아주 이상한 말을 했고, 아주 이상하게 굴었고, 아주 이상하게 행동했고, 전체적으로 아주 이상했지요."], ["My Uncle’s child made people’s blood run cold, some times, she did!”", "외삼촌네 아이는 가끔 사람들 피를 얼어붙게 했답니다, 정말이지요!\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어떻게요?\""], ["asked Berry.", "베리가 물었다."]], [["“I wouldn’t have sat up all night alone with Betsey Jane!”", "\"저라면 베치 제인이랑 밤새 단둘이 깨어 앉아 있지는 않았을 거예요!\""], ["said Mrs Wickam, “not if you’d have put Wickam into business next morning for himself.", "위컴 부인이 말했다, \"다음 날 아침에 위컴을 제 사업이라도 차려 준다 해도요."], ["I couldn’t have done it, Miss Berry.", "저는 그렇게는 못 했을 거예요, 베리 아가씨.\""]], [["Miss Berry naturally asked why not?", "베리 양은 당연히 왜 못 하느냐고 물었다."], ["But Mrs Wickam, agreeably to the usage of some ladies in her condition, pursued her own branch of the subject, without any compunction.", "그러나 위컴 부인은, 그녀 같은 처지의 일부 여인네들의 버릇대로, 아무런 거리낌도 없이 자기 나름의 이야기 가지를 좇아 나갔다."]], [["“Betsey Jane,”", "\"베치 제인은,\""], ["said Mrs Wickam, “was as sweet a child as I could wish to see.", "위컴 부인이 말했다, \"제가 보고 싶다 할 만큼 사랑스러운 아이였어요."], ["I couldn’t wish to see a sweeter.", "그보다 더 사랑스러운 아이는 보고 싶어도 못 봤을 거예요."], ["Everything that a child could have in the way of illnesses, Betsey Jane had come through.", "아이가 앓을 수 있는 병이란 병은 베치 제인이 다 거쳐 갔지요. 그 애한테는 경련이,\""], ["The cramps was as common to her,” said Mrs Wickam, “as biles is to yourself, Miss Berry.”", "위컴 부인이 말했다, \"아가씨한테 뾰루지가 흔한 것만큼이나 흔했답니다, 베리 아가씨.\""], ["Miss Berry involuntarily wrinkled her nose.", "베리 양은 자기도 모르게 코를 찡그렸다."]], [["“But Betsey Jane,”", "\"그런데 베치 제인은,\""], ["said Mrs Wickam, lowering her voice, and looking round the room, and towards Paul in bed, “had been minded, in her cradle, by her departed mother.", "위컴 부인이 목소리를 낮추고, 방 안을 둘러보고, 침대에 누운 폴 쪽을 바라보며 말했다, \"요람에 있을 때, 돌아가신 제 어머니가 그 애를 돌봤어요."], ["I couldn’t say how, nor I couldn’t say when, nor I couldn’t say whether the dear child knew it or not, but Betsey Jane had been watched by her mother, Miss Berry!”", "어떻게 그랬는지도 모르겠고, 언제 그랬는지도 모르겠고, 그 귀여운 아이가 그걸 알았는지 어쨌는지도 모르겠지만, 베치 제인은 제 어머니의 보살핌을 받았답니다, 베리 아가씨!\""], ["and Mrs Wickam, with a very white face, and with watery eyes, and with a tremulous voice, again looked fearfully round the room, and towards Paul in bed.", "그러고 위컴 부인은, 새하얗게 질린 얼굴과 눈물 어린 눈과 떨리는 목소리로, 다시 한번 겁먹은 듯 방 안을 둘러보고, 침대에 누운 폴 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["“Nonsense!”", "\"말도 안 돼요!\""], ["cried Miss Berry—somewhat resentful of the idea.", "베리 양이—그 생각에 다소 발끈하여—외쳤다."]], [["“You may say nonsense!", "\"말도 안 된다고 하셔도 좋아요!"], ["I ain’t offended, Miss.", "저는 기분 안 상해요, 아가씨."], ["I hope you may be able to think in your own conscience that it is nonsense; you’ll find your spirits all the better for it in this—you’ll excuse my being so free—in this burying-ground of a place; which is wearing of me down.", "양심으로 그게 말도 안 된다고 생각하실 수 있으면 좋겠네요. 그러면 이런 데서—이렇게 자유롭게 말하는 걸 용서하세요—이런 무덤 같은 데서 마음이 한결 나으실 거예요."], ["Master Paul’s a little restless in his sleep.", "저는 여기서 진이 다 빠지고 있답니다. 폴 도련님이 자면서 좀 뒤척이네요."], ["Pat his back, if you please.”", "등 좀 토닥여 주세요, 부탁이에요.\""]], [["“Of course you think,”", "\"그럼 부인 생각엔,\""], ["said Berry, gently doing what she was asked, “that he has been nursed by his mother, too?”", "베리가 시키는 대로 살며시 토닥이며 말했다, \"이 애도 제 어머니의 보살핌을 받았다는 거죠?\""]], [["“Betsey Jane,”", "\"베치 제인은,\""], ["returned Mrs Wickam in her most solemn tones, “was put upon as that child has been put upon, and changed as that child has changed.", "위컴 부인이 더없이 엄숙한 어조로 대꾸했다, \"이 아이가 시달려 온 것처럼 시달렸고, 이 아이가 변해 온 것처럼 변했어요."], ["I have seen her sit, often and often, think, think, thinking, like him.", "저는 그 애가, 이 애처럼, 생각하고, 생각하고, 또 생각하며 앉아 있는 걸 몇 번이고 몇 번이고 봤어요."], ["I have seen her look, often and often, old, old, old, like him.", "그 애가, 이 애처럼, 늙고, 늙고, 또 늙어 보이는 걸 몇 번이고 몇 번이고 봤어요."], ["I have heard her, many a time, talk just like him.", "그 애가, 이 애랑 꼭 같이 말하는 걸 여러 번 들었어요."], ["I consider that child and Betsey Jane on the same footing entirely, Miss Berry.”", "저는 이 아이랑 베치 제인이 완전히 똑같은 처지라고 봐요, 베리 아가씨.\""]], [["“Is your Uncle’s child alive?”", "\"부인 외삼촌네 아이는 살아 있어요?\""], ["asked Berry.", "베리가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, Miss, she is alive,”", "\"네, 아가씨, 살아 있어요,\""], ["returned Mrs Wickam with an air of triumph, for it was evident.", "위컴 부인이 의기양양한 기색으로 대꾸했다."], ["Miss Berry expected the reverse; “and is married to a silver-chaser.", "베리 양이 그 반대를 예상했던 것이 분명했기 때문이다."], ["Oh yes, Miss, SHE is alive,”", "\"그리고 은세공장이한테 시집갔지요."], ["said Mrs Wickam, laying strong stress on her nominative case.", "오, 그럼요, 아가씨, 그 애는 살아 있어요,\" 위컴 부인이 주어에 힘을 잔뜩 실어 말했다."]], [["It being clear that somebody was dead, Mrs Pipchin’s niece inquired who it was.", "누군가가 죽었다는 것이 분명해지자, 핍친 부인의 조카딸은 그게 누구냐고 물었다."]], [["“I wouldn’t wish to make you uneasy,”", "\"아가씨를 불안하게 만들고 싶진 않아요,\""], ["returned Mrs Wickam, pursuing her supper.", "위컴 부인이 야식을 들며 대꾸했다."], ["“Don’t ask me.”", "\"묻지 마세요.\""]], [["This was the surest way of being asked again.", "이것이야말로 다시 묻게 만드는 가장 확실한 방법이었다."], ["Miss Berry repeated her question, therefore; and after some resistance, and reluctance, Mrs Wickam laid down her knife, and again glancing round the room and at Paul in bed, replied:", "그래서 베리 양은 자기 질문을 되풀이했고, 위컴 부인은 얼마간 버티고 망설인 끝에 칼을 내려놓고는, 다시 방 안과 침대에 누운 폴을 흘끗 보며 대답했다."]], [["“She took fancies to people; whimsical fancies, some of them; others, affections that one might expect to see—only stronger than common.", "\"그 애는 사람들한테 정을 붙였어요. 그중엔 변덕스러운 정도 있었고, 또 어떤 건 으레 볼 법한 그런 애정이었지요—다만 보통보다 더 강했을 뿐."], ["They all died.”", "그런데 그 사람들이 다 죽었어요.\""]], [["This was so very unexpected and awful to Mrs Pipchin’s niece, that she sat upright on the hard edge of the bedstead, breathing short, and surveying her informant with looks of undisguised alarm.", "이것이 핍친 부인의 조카딸에게는 너무도 뜻밖이고 너무도 섬뜩했던지, 그녀는 침대틀의 딱딱한 모서리에 꼿꼿이 앉아, 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며, 노골적인 경악의 눈빛으로 자기에게 이야기를 들려주는 그 여자를 바라보았다."]], [["Mrs Wickam shook her left fore-finger stealthily towards the bed where Florence lay; then turned it upside down, and made several emphatic points at the floor; immediately below which was the parlour in which Mrs Pipchin habitually consumed the toast.", "위컴 부인은 왼쪽 집게손가락을 플로렌스가 누워 있는 침대 쪽으로 슬그머니 흔들어 보였다. 그러더니 그것을 거꾸로 뒤집어, 바닥을 향해 힘주어 여러 번 가리켰다. 그 바로 아래가, 핍친 부인이 으레 토스트를 먹어 치우는 그 거실이었다."]], [["“Remember my words, Miss Berry,”", "\"제 말 명심하세요, 베리 아가씨,\""], ["said Mrs Wickam, “and be thankful that Master Paul is not too fond of you.", "위컴 부인이 말했다, \"그리고 폴 도련님이 아가씨를 너무 좋아하지 않는 걸 고맙게 여기세요."], ["I am, that he’s not too fond of me, I assure you; though there isn’t much to live for—you’ll excuse my being so free—in this jail of a house!”", "저는, 그 애가 저를 너무 좋아하지 않는 게 고마워요, 정말이에요. 하긴 이런 데서—이렇게 자유롭게 말하는 걸 용서하세요—이런 감옥 같은 집에서 살아갈 낙이라곤 별로 없지만요!\""]], [["Miss Berry’s emotion might have led to her patting Paul too hard on the back, or might have produced a cessation of that soothing monotony, but he turned in his bed just now, and, presently awaking, sat up in it with his hair hot and wet from the effects of some childish dream, and asked for Florence.", "베리 양의 동요가 그녀로 하여금 폴의 등을 너무 세게 토닥이게 했을 수도 있고, 아니면 그 마음을 달래 주던 단조로운 손길을 멈추게 했을 수도 있었다. 그러나 바로 그때 폴이 잠자리에서 몸을 뒤척이더니, 곧 잠에서 깨어 침대에 일어나 앉았다. 그의 머리카락은 무슨 어린아이다운 꿈의 여파로 뜨겁고 축축했고, 그는 플로렌스를 찾았다."]], [["She was out of her own bed at the first sound of his voice; and bending over his pillow immediately, sang him to sleep again.", "플로렌스는 그의 목소리가 들리자마자 자기 침대에서 일어나, 곧장 그의 베개 위로 몸을 굽히고는, 노래를 불러 그를 다시 잠들게 했다."], ["Mrs Wickam shaking her head, and letting fall several tears, pointed out the little group to Berry, and turned her eyes up to the ceiling.", "위컴 부인은 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 눈물을 몇 방울 떨구며, 그 자그마한 무리를 베리에게 가리켜 보이고는, 눈을 천장으로 치떴다."]], [["“He’s asleep now, my dear,”", "\"이제 잠들었어요, 아가씨,\""], ["said Mrs Wickam after a pause, “you’d better go to bed again.", "위컴 부인이 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했다, \"아가씨도 다시 잠자리에 드시는 게 좋겠어요."], ["Don’t you feel cold?”", "춥지 않으세요?\""]], [["“No, nurse,”", "\"아뇨, 유모,\""], ["said Florence, laughing.", "플로렌스가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Not at all.”", "\"전혀요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["sighed Mrs Wickam, and she shook her head again, expressing to the watchful Berry, “we shall be cold enough, some of us, by and by!”", "위컴 부인이 한숨지었다. 그리고 다시 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 지켜보는 베리에게 이렇게 말하는 듯했다, \"머잖아 우리 가운데 누군가는 충분히 차가워질 거예요!\""]], [["Berry took the frugal supper-tray, with which Mrs Wickam had by this time done, and bade her good-night.", "베리는 위컴 부인이 이제 다 비운 그 검소한 야식 쟁반을 집어 들고, 그녀에게 잘 자라고 인사했다."]], [["“Good-night, Miss!”", "\"안녕히 주무세요, 아가씨!\""], ["returned Wickam softly.", "위컴이 나직이 대꾸했다."], ["“Good-night!", "\"안녕히요!"], ["Your aunt is an old lady, Miss Berry, and it’s what you must have looked for, often.”", "아가씨 고모님은 연로한 분이시니까, 베리 아가씨, 그건 아가씨도 진작부터 각오하고 계셨을 일이잖아요.\""]], [["This consolatory farewell, Mrs Wickam accompanied with a look of heartfelt anguish; and being left alone with the two children again, and becoming conscious that the wind was blowing mournfully, she indulged in melancholy—that cheapest and most accessible of luxuries—until she was overpowered by slumber.", "이 위로의 작별 인사에 위컴 부인은 진심에서 우러난 비통한 표정을 곁들였다. 그리고 다시 두 아이와 단둘이 남아, 바람이 구슬프게 불고 있다는 것을 깨닫자, 그녀는 우울—그 가장 값싸고 가장 손쉬운 사치—에 잠겨 들었고, 마침내 잠에 압도되고 말았다."]], [["Although the niece of Mrs Pipchin did not expect to find that exemplary dragon prostrate on the hearth-rug when she went downstairs, she was relieved to find her unusually fractious and severe, and with every present appearance of intending to live a long time to be a comfort to all who knew her.", "핍친 부인의 조카딸은 아래층으로 내려가면서 그 본보기 같은 용이 난롯가 깔개 위에 엎어져 있으리라고 기대하지는 않았으나, 그녀가 유난히 까다롭고 모질며, 또 아는 모든 이에게 위안이 되고자 오래오래 살아갈 작정이라는 기색이 지금 온통 드러나 있는 것을 보고는 안도했다."], ["Nor had she any symptoms of declining, in the course of the ensuing week, when the constitutional viands still continued to disappear in regular succession, notwithstanding that Paul studied her as attentively as ever, and occupied his usual seat between the black skirts and the fender, with unwavering constancy.", "또 그녀에게는 이어지는 한 주 동안에도 쇠약해질 기미라곤 전혀 없었으니, 그 체질을 위한 음식들은 여전히 차례차례 규칙적으로 사라져 갔고, 한편 폴은 여느 때처럼 열심히 그녀를 연구하며, 그 검은 치맛자락과 난로 울 사이의 평소 자리를 변함없는 한결같음으로 차지하고 있었다."]], [["But as Paul himself was no stronger at the expiration of that time than he had been on his first arrival, though he looked much healthier in the face, a little carriage was got for him, in which he could lie at his ease, with an alphabet and other elementary works of reference, and be wheeled down to the sea-side.", "그러나 그 무렵이 다 지나도록 폴 자신은, 얼굴빛은 훨씬 건강해 보였으나, 처음 도착했을 때보다 조금도 더 튼튼해지지 않았으므로, 그를 위해 자그마한 손수레가 마련되었다. 그 안에서 그는 알파벳판이며 그 밖의 기초 참고서들을 곁에 두고 편안히 누워, 바닷가로 끌려 내려갈 수 있었다."], ["Consistent in his odd tastes, the child set aside a ruddy-faced lad who was proposed as the drawer of this carriage, and selected, instead, his grandfather—a weazen, old, crab-faced man, in a suit of battered oilskin, who had got tough and stringy from long pickling in salt water, and who smelt like a weedy sea-beach when the tide is out.", "자기의 별난 취향에 한결같던 아이는, 이 수레를 끌 사람으로 제안된 혈색 좋은 사내아이는 마다하고, 그 대신 그 아이의 할아버지를 골랐다. 쪼글쪼글하고 늙은, 게처럼 생긴 사내였는데, 너덜너덜한 기름먹인 옷을 입고 있었으며, 짠물에 오래 절여진 탓에 질기고 힘줄이 도드라졌고, 썰물 때 해초가 깔린 바닷가 같은 냄새가 났다."]], [["With this notable attendant to pull him along, and Florence always walking by his side, and the despondent Wickam bringing up the rear, he went down to the margin of the ocean every day; and there he would sit or lie in his carriage for hours together: never so distressed as by the company of children—Florence alone excepted, always.", "이 별난 시중꾼이 그를 끌고, 플로렌스는 늘 그의 곁에서 걷고, 풀이 죽은 위컴은 맨 뒤를 따르는 가운데, 그는 날마다 바다의 가장자리로 내려갔다. 그리고 그곳에서 그는 몇 시간씩 한꺼번에 자기 수레에 앉거나 누워 있곤 했다. 다른 아이들이 함께 있을 때만큼 그를 괴롭게 하는 것은 없었다—언제나 플로렌스만은 예외였지만."]], [["“Go away, if you please,”", "\"제발 저리 가 줘,\""], ["he would say to any child who came to bear him company.", "그는 자기와 어울리려고 다가오는 아이에게라면 누구에게든 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["“Thank you, but I don’t want you.”", "\"고맙지만, 너는 필요 없어.\""]], [["Some small voice, near his ear, would ask him how he was, perhaps.", "이따금 그의 귓전에서 어떤 작은 목소리가, 그에게 안녕하냐고 물었을지도 모른다."]], [["“I am very well, I thank you,”", "\"난 아주 잘 있어, 고마워,\""], ["he would answer.", "그는 이렇게 대답하곤 했다."], ["“But you had better go and play, if you please.”", "\"그래도 제발 가서 노는 게 좋을 거야.\""]], [["Then he would turn his head, and watch the child away, and say to Florence, “We don’t want any others, do we?", "그러고는 고개를 돌려, 그 아이가 멀어지는 것을 지켜보다가, 플로렌스에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"우리는 다른 애들은 필요 없지, 그렇지?"], ["Kiss me, Floy.”", "뽀뽀해 줘, 플로이.\""]], [["He had even a dislike, at such times, to the company of Wickam, and was well pleased when she strolled away, as she generally did, to pick up shells and acquaintances.", "그런 때면 그는 위컴이 함께 있는 것조차 싫어해서, 그녀가 으레 그러듯 조개껍데기며 아는 사람을 주우러 어슬렁어슬렁 가 버리면 무척 좋아했다."], ["His favourite spot was quite a lonely one, far away from most loungers; and with Florence sitting by his side at work, or reading to him, or talking to him, and the wind blowing on his face, and the water coming up among the wheels of his bed, he wanted nothing more.", "그가 가장 좋아하는 자리는 한적하기 그지없는 곳으로, 거니는 사람들 대부분에게서 멀리 떨어져 있었다. 그리고 플로렌스가 곁에 앉아 바느질을 하거나, 그에게 책을 읽어 주거나, 그에게 말을 걸어 주고, 바람이 그의 얼굴에 불어오고, 물이 그의 침대 바퀴 사이로 밀려들어 오면, 그는 더 바랄 것이 없었다."]], [["“Floy,”", "\"플로이,\""], ["he said one day, “where’s India, where that boy’s friends live?”", "어느 날 그가 말했다, \"그 애 친척들이 사는 인도는 어디 있어?\""]], [["“Oh, it’s a long, long distance off,”", "\"오, 아주아주 먼 데 있어,\""], ["said Florence, raising her eyes from her work.", "플로렌스가 바느질하던 데서 눈을 들며 말했다."]], [["“Weeks off?”", "\"몇 주나 걸리는 데?\""], ["asked Paul.", "폴이 물었다."]], [["“Yes dear.", "\"응, 얘."], ["Many weeks’ journey, night and day.”", "밤낮으로 몇 주나 걸리는 여정이야.\""]], [["“If you were in India, Floy,”", "\"플로이, 네가 인도에 있으면,\""], ["said Paul, after being silent for a minute, “I should—what is it that Mama did?", "폴이 잠깐 잠잠히 있다가 말했다, \"나는—엄마가 한 게 뭐였지?"], ["I forget.”", "잊어버렸어.\""]], [["“Loved me!”", "\"날 사랑하셨지!\""], ["answered Florence.", "플로렌스가 대답했다."]], [["“No, no.", "\"아니, 아니."], ["Don’t I love you now, Floy?", "나도 지금 너를 사랑하잖아, 플로이?"], ["What is it?—Died.", "그게 뭐였지?—돌아가신 거."], ["If you were in India, I should die, Floy.”", "네가 인도에 있으면, 나는 죽을 거야, 플로이.\""]], [["She hurriedly put her work aside, and laid her head down on his pillow, caressing him.", "플로렌스는 황급히 바느질감을 옆으로 치우고, 그의 베개 위에 머리를 누이며 그를 어루만졌다."], ["And so would she, she said, if he were there.", "그리고 자기도, 만일 그가 거기 있다면 그럴 거라고 말했다."], ["He would be better soon.", "그는 곧 나아질 거라고."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I am a great deal better now!”", "난 지금도 훨씬 나은걸!\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“I don’t mean that.", "\"그런 뜻이 아니야."], ["I mean that I should die of being so sorry and so lonely, Floy!”", "나는 너무 슬프고 너무 외로워서 죽을 거라는 거야, 플로이!\""]], [["Another time, in the same place, he fell asleep, and slept quietly for a long time.", "또 한번은, 같은 자리에서, 그가 잠이 들어 오랫동안 고요히 잤다."], ["Awaking suddenly, he listened, started up, and sat listening.", "그러다 별안간 잠에서 깨어, 귀를 기울이고, 벌떡 일어나, 앉아서 귀를 기울였다."]], [["Florence asked him what he thought he heard.", "플로렌스는 그에게 무슨 소리가 들리는 것 같으냐고 물었다."]], [["“I want to know what it says,”", "\"그게 뭐라고 하는지 알고 싶어,\""], ["he answered, looking steadily in her face.", "그가 그녀의 얼굴을 가만히 들여다보며 대답했다."], ["“The sea” Floy, what is it that it keeps on saying?”", "\"바다 말이야, 플로이, 저게 자꾸 뭐라고 하는 거지?\""]], [["She told him that it was only the noise of the rolling waves.", "플로렌스는 그것이 그저 굽이치는 파도 소리일 뿐이라고 일러 주었다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But I know that they are always saying something.", "\"하지만 나는 저 파도가 늘 무언가를 말하고 있다는 걸 알아."], ["Always the same thing.", "늘 똑같은 걸 말이야."], ["What place is over there?”", "저기 저 너머엔 어떤 데가 있어?\""], ["He rose up, looking eagerly at the horizon.", "그는 일어나서, 수평선을 간절히 바라보았다."]], [["She told him that there was another country opposite, but he said he didn’t mean that: he meant further away—farther away!", "플로렌스는 맞은편에 또 다른 나라가 있다고 일러 주었으나, 그는 그걸 말하는 게 아니라고 했다. 더 멀리—더 멀리 말이야!"]], [["Very often afterwards, in the midst of their talk, he would break off, to try to understand what it was that the waves were always saying; and would rise up in his couch to look towards that invisible region, far away.", "그 뒤로도 아주 자주, 둘이 이야기를 나누다가도, 그는 문득 말을 멈추고, 그 파도가 늘 무어라고 하는 것인지를 헤아려 보려 했다. 그리고 자기 침상에서 일어나, 저 멀리 보이지 않는 그 나라 쪽을 바라보곤 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX. In which the Wooden Midshipman gets into Trouble", "# CHAPTER IX. In which the Wooden Midshipman gets into Trouble"]], [["That spice of romance and love of the marvellous, of which there was a pretty strong infusion in the nature of young Walter Gay, and which the guardianship of his Uncle, old Solomon Gills, had not very much weakened by the waters of stern practical experience, was the occasion of his attaching an uncommon and delightful interest to the adventure of Florence with Good Mrs Brown.", "젊은 월터 게이의 천성에는 낭만에 대한 기질과 경이로운 것을 사랑하는 마음이 꽤 강하게 배어 있었는데, 그의 외삼촌인 노(老) 솔로몬 길스의 보살핌도 냉정하고 실질적인 경험이라는 물로 그것을 그리 크게 약화시키지는 못했다. 바로 그 기질 때문에 그는 플로렌스가 착한 브라운 부인과 겪은 그 모험에 남다르고 즐거운 흥미를 붙이게 되었다."], ["He pampered and cherished it in his memory, especially that part of it with which he had been associated: until it became the spoiled child of his fancy, and took its own way, and did what it liked with it.", "그는 그 일을 기억 속에서 애지중지 키우고 보듬었는데, 특히 자기가 관여했던 부분이 그러했다. 마침내 그것은 그의 공상이 떠받드는 응석받이 아이가 되어, 제멋대로 굴며 마음껏 그 공상을 가지고 놀았다."]], [["The recollection of those incidents, and his own share in them, may have been made the more captivating, perhaps, by the weekly dreamings of old Sol and Captain Cuttle on Sundays.", "그 사건들과 거기서 자신이 맡았던 역할에 대한 회상은, 어쩌면 일요일마다 노(老) 솔과 커틀 선장이 함께 펼치는 주간(週間) 공상 덕분에 더욱 매혹적인 것이 되었는지도 모른다."], ["Hardly a Sunday passed, without mysterious references being made by one or other of those worthy chums to Richard Whittington; and the latter gentleman had even gone so far as to purchase a ballad of considerable antiquity, that had long fluttered among many others, chiefly expressive of maritime sentiments, on a dead wall in the Commercial Road: which poetical performance set forth the courtship and nuptials of a promising young coal-whipper with a certain “lovely Peg,”", "그 훌륭한 단짝들 가운데 어느 한 사람이 리처드 휘팅턴을 신비롭게 언급하지 않고 지나가는 일요일은 거의 없었다. 그중 후자인 신사는 한술 더 떠, 상업로(路)의 어느 허물어진 담벼락에 주로 바다와 관련된 정서를 담은 수많은 다른 노래들 사이에서 오랫동안 펄럭이던, 상당히 오래된 노래 한 편을 사들이기까지 했다."], ["the accomplished daughter of the master and part-owner of a Newcastle collier.", "그 시적 작품은 유망한 젊은 석탄 하역부가 어느 \"사랑스러운 페그\", 곧 뉴캐슬 석탄선의 선장 겸 공동 선주의 재주 많은 딸과 벌인 구애와 혼인을 노래한 것이었다."], ["In this stirring legend, Captain Cuttle descried a profound metaphysical bearing on the case of Walter and Florence; and it excited him so much, that on very festive occasions, as birthdays and a few other non-Dominical holidays, he would roar through the whole song in the little back parlour; making an amazing shake on the word Pe-e-eg, with which every verse concluded, in compliment to the heroine of the piece.", "이 가슴 설레는 전설 속에서 커틀 선장은 월터와 플로렌스의 경우에 들어맞는 심오한 형이상학적 의미를 발견했다. 그것이 어찌나 그를 들뜨게 했던지, 생일이나 그 밖의 몇몇 비(非)주일 휴일 같은 아주 떠들썩한 날이면, 그는 작은 뒷방에서 그 노래 전체를 우렁차게 불러 젖혔고, 작품의 여주인공에 대한 경의로, 매 절(節)이 끝맺는 페에에에그라는 말에서 놀라운 떨림을 넣곤 했다."]], [["But a frank, free-spirited, open-hearted boy, is not much given to analysing the nature of his own feelings, however strong their hold upon him: and Walter would have found it difficult to decide this point.", "그러나 솔직하고 자유로운 정신을 지닌, 마음이 활짝 열린 소년이란, 제 감정이 아무리 자기를 강하게 붙잡고 있다 한들, 그 감정의 본질을 분석하는 데는 그리 능하지 않은 법이다. 월터로서도 이 점을 판단하기는 어려웠으리라."], ["He had a great affection for the wharf where he had encountered Florence, and for the streets (albeit not enchanting in themselves) by which they had come home.", "그는 플로렌스를 만났던 그 부두에 큰 애정을 품었고, (그 거리들 자체가 매혹적이지는 않았으나) 그들이 집으로 돌아올 때 지나온 거리들에도 애정을 품었다."], ["The shoes that had so often tumbled off by the way, he preserved in his own room; and, sitting in the little back parlour of an evening, he had drawn a whole gallery of fancy portraits of Good Mrs Brown.", "그 길에서 그토록 자주 벗겨졌던 신발을 그는 자기 방에 간직해 두었다. 그리고 저녁이면 작은 뒷방에 앉아, 착한 브라운 부인의 상상 초상화를 화랑이라도 차릴 만큼 잔뜩 그려 댔다."], ["It may be that he became a little smarter in his dress after that memorable occasion; and he certainly liked in his leisure time to walk towards that quarter of the town where Mr Dombey’s house was situated, on the vague chance of passing little Florence in the street.", "어쩌면 그 잊지 못할 사건 이후로 그는 옷차림에 조금 더 멋을 부리게 되었는지도 모른다. 또 그가 한가한 시간이면 돔비 씨의 저택이 자리한 동네 쪽으로 걷기를, 거리에서 어린 플로렌스를 스칠지도 모른다는 막연한 기대로 즐겨 했던 것도 분명하다."], ["But the sentiment of all this was as boyish and innocent as could be.", "그러나 이 모든 것의 정서는 더할 나위 없이 소년답고 순수했다."], ["Florence was very pretty, and it is pleasant to admire a pretty face.", "플로렌스는 무척 예뻤고, 예쁜 얼굴을 흠모하는 것은 즐거운 일이었다."], ["Florence was defenceless and weak, and it was a proud thought that he had been able to render her any protection and assistance.", "플로렌스는 무방비하고 연약했으며, 자기가 그녀에게 얼마간 보호와 도움을 줄 수 있었다는 것은 자랑스러운 생각이었다."], ["Florence was the most grateful little creature in the world, and it was delightful to see her bright gratitude beaming in her face.", "플로렌스는 세상에서 가장 고마워할 줄 아는 작은 존재였고, 그 환한 감사가 얼굴에 빛나는 모습을 보는 것은 기쁜 일이었다."], ["Florence was neglected and coldly looked upon, and his breast was full of youthful interest for the slighted child in her dull, stately home.", "플로렌스는 방치되고 냉대받는 처지였으며, 그 음울하고 위엄 서린 집안에서 푸대접받는 아이를 향해 그의 가슴은 젊은이다운 관심으로 가득했다."]], [["Thus it came about that, perhaps some half-a-dozen times in the course of the year, Walter pulled off his hat to Florence in the street, and Florence would stop to shake hands.", "그리하여 한 해에 어쩌면 대여섯 번쯤, 월터는 거리에서 플로렌스에게 모자를 벗어 인사했고, 플로렌스는 멈춰 서서 악수를 나누곤 했다."], ["Mrs Wickam (who, with a characteristic alteration of his name, invariably spoke of him as “Young Graves”) was so well used to this, knowing the story of their acquaintance, that she took no heed of it at all.", "위컴 부인은(그의 이름을 제 식대로 바꾸어 한결같이 \"청년 그레이브스\"라 불렀는데) 두 사람이 알게 된 사연을 알고 있던 터라 이런 일에 어찌나 익숙했던지, 전혀 개의치 않았다."], ["Miss Nipper, on the other hand, rather looked out for these occasions: her sensitive young heart being secretly propitiated by Walter’s good looks, and inclining to the belief that its sentiments were responded to.", "반면에 니퍼 양은 오히려 그런 기회를 노렸으니, 그녀의 예민한 젊은 가슴이 월터의 잘생긴 외모에 은근히 호감을 품고서, 제 감정이 화답받고 있다는 믿음 쪽으로 기울었기 때문이다."]], [["In this way, Walter, so far from forgetting or losing sight of his acquaintance with Florence, only remembered it better and better.", "이런 식으로, 월터는 플로렌스와의 친분을 잊거나 시야에서 놓치기는커녕, 오히려 점점 더 또렷이 기억할 따름이었다."], ["As to its adventurous beginning, and all those little circumstances which gave it a distinctive character and relish, he took them into account, more as a pleasant story very agreeable to his imagination, and not to be dismissed from it, than as a part of any matter of fact with which he was concerned.", "그 모험적인 시작과, 거기에 독특한 성격과 운치를 더해 준 자잘한 정황들에 대해서는, 자기가 얽혀 있는 어떤 사실의 일부로서가 아니라, 자기 상상에 무척 잘 맞아 떨쳐 버릴 수 없는 즐거운 이야기로서 더 많이 헤아렸다."], ["They set off Florence very much, to his fancy; but not himself.", "그 정황들은 그의 공상 속에서 플로렌스를 한껏 돋보이게 했으나, 정작 자신은 그렇지 못했다."], ["Sometimes he thought (and then he walked very fast) what a grand thing it would have been for him to have been going to sea on the day after that first meeting, and to have gone, and to have done wonders there, and to have stopped away a long time, and to have come back an Admiral of all the colours of the dolphin, or at least a Post-Captain with epaulettes of insupportable brightness, and have married Florence (then a beautiful young woman) in spite of Mr Dombey’s teeth, cravat, and watch-chain, and borne her away to the blue shores of somewhere or other, triumphantly.", "때로 그는 (그러고 나면 무척 빠르게 걸었는데) 첫 만남 다음 날에 자기가 바다로 나가게 되었더라면, 그래서 정말로 떠나, 거기서 놀라운 일들을 해내고, 오랫동안 멀리 머물다가, 돌고래의 온갖 빛깔을 두른 제독으로, 적어도 견딜 수 없을 만큼 번쩍이는 견장을 단 전대장(全大將)으로 돌아와서, 돔비 씨의 이빨이며 크라바트며 시곗줄 따위에 아랑곳없이 (그때쯤 아리따운 처녀가 되어 있을) 플로렌스와 혼인하여, 어딘가의 푸른 해안으로 그녀를 의기양양하게 데려갔더라면 얼마나 멋진 일이었을까 하고 생각하곤 했다."], ["But these flights of fancy seldom burnished the brass plate of Dombey and Son’s Offices into a tablet of golden hope, or shed a brilliant lustre on their dirty skylights; and when the Captain and Uncle Sol talked about Richard Whittington and masters’ daughters, Walter felt that he understood his true position at Dombey and Son’s, much better than they did.", "그러나 이런 공상의 비상이 돔비와 아들 회사 사무소의 놋쇠 명패를 황금빛 희망의 명판으로 광나게 닦아 주거나, 그 더러운 채광창에 찬란한 광채를 드리워 주는 일은 좀처럼 없었다. 그리고 선장과 솔 외삼촌이 리처드 휘팅턴이며 주인의 딸들 이야기를 늘어놓을 때면, 월터는 돔비와 아들 회사에서의 자기 진짜 처지를 그들보다 훨씬 잘 알고 있다고 느꼈다."]], [["So it was that he went on doing what he had to do from day to day, in a cheerful, pains-taking, merry spirit; and saw through the sanguine complexion of Uncle Sol and Captain Cuttle; and yet entertained a thousand indistinct and visionary fancies of his own, to which theirs were work-a-day probabilities.", "그렇게 그는 날마다 해야 할 일을, 명랑하고 부지런하며 즐거운 마음으로 해 나갔다. 그리고 솔 외삼촌과 커틀 선장의 낙천적인 기질을 꿰뚫어 보면서도, 한편으로는 자기 나름의 막연하고 몽환적인 공상을 수도 없이 품었으니, 그것에 비하면 그들의 공상은 그저 일상적인 개연성에 지나지 않았다."], ["Such was his condition at the Pipchin period, when he looked a little older than of yore, but not much; and was the same light-footed, light-hearted, light-headed lad, as when he charged into the parlour at the head of Uncle Sol and the imaginary boarders, and lighted him to bring up the Madeira.", "피프친 시절의 그의 형편이 이러했는데, 그 무렵 그는 예전보다 조금 나이 들어 보이긴 했으나 그리 크게는 아니었고, 솔 외삼촌과 가상의 하숙인들을 거느린 채 거실로 돌격해 들어가 마데이라 술을 가져오라고 외삼촌의 발치를 비춰 주던 그때와 똑같이, 발걸음 가볍고 마음 가볍고 머릿속 가벼운 청년이었다."]], [["“Uncle Sol,”", "\"솔 외삼촌,\""], ["said Walter, “I don’t think you’re well.", "월터가 말했다, \"어디 편찮으신 것 같아요."], ["You haven’t eaten any breakfast.", "아침도 통 안 드셨잖아요."], ["I shall bring a doctor to you, if you go on like this.”", "이러시면 제가 의사를 모셔 올 거예요.\""]], [["“He can’t give me what I want, my boy,”", "\"의사라도 내가 원하는 걸 줄 수는 없단다, 얘야,\""], ["said Uncle Sol.", "솔 외삼촌이 말했다."], ["“At least he is in good practice if he can—and then he wouldn’t.”", "\"줄 수 있다면야 용한 의사겠지—그렇대도 안 주려 들겠지만.\""]], [["“What is it, Uncle?", "\"그게 뭔데요, 외삼촌?"], ["Customers?”", "손님이요?\""]], [["“Ay,”", "\"그래,\""], ["returned Solomon, with a sigh.", "솔로몬이 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다."], ["“Customers would do.”", "\"손님이라면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["“Confound it, Uncle!”", "\"제기랄, 외삼촌!\""], ["said Walter, putting down his breakfast cup with a clatter, and striking his hand on the table: “when I see the people going up and down the street in shoals all day, and passing and re-passing the shop every minute, by scores, I feel half tempted to rush out, collar somebody, bring him in, and make him buy fifty pounds’ worth of instruments for ready money.", "월터가 아침 찻잔을 달그락 내려놓고 손으로 탁자를 치며 말했다. \"하루 종일 사람들이 떼 지어 거리를 오르락내리락하고, 시시각각 수십 명씩 가게 앞을 지나가고 또 지나가는 걸 보고 있노라면, 저는 당장 밖으로 뛰쳐나가 아무나 멱살을 잡아 끌고 들어와서, 현금으로 오십 파운드어치 기구를 사게 만들고 싶은 충동이 반쯤은 든다니까요."], ["What are you looking in at the door for?—”", "거기 문간에서 뭘 들여다보시는 거요?—\""], ["continued Walter, apostrophizing an old gentleman with a powdered head (inaudibly to him of course), who was staring at a ship’s telescope with all his might and main.", "월터는 가루를 뿌린 머리를 한 노신사를 향해 (물론 그에게는 들리지 않게) 한마디 던지며 말을 이었는데, 그 노인은 온 힘을 다해 선박용 망원경을 뚫어져라 보고 있었다. \"그래 봤자 소용없소."], ["“That’s no use. I could do that.", "그런 거야 나도 할 수 있지."], ["Come in and buy it!”", "들어와서 사시지 그래요!\""]], [["The old gentleman, however, having satiated his curiosity, walked calmly away.", "그러나 노신사는 호기심을 다 채우고는 태연히 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["“There he goes!”", "\"저것 봐요!\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."], ["“That’s the way with ’em all.", "\"다들 저 모양이라니까요."], ["But, Uncle—I say, Uncle Sol”—for the old man was meditating and had not responded to his first appeal.", "하지만 외삼촌—이봐요, 솔 외삼촌\"—노인이 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 채 그의 첫 호소에 응답하지 않았던 것이다."], ["“Don’t be cast down.", "\"낙심하지 마세요."], ["Don’t be out of spirits, Uncle.", "기운 빠지지 마세요, 외삼촌."], ["When orders do come, they’ll come in such a crowd, you won’t be able to execute ’em.”", "주문이 들어오기 시작하면, 어찌나 한꺼번에 몰려들지 다 처리하지도 못하실걸요.\""]], [["“I shall be past executing ’em, whenever they come, my boy,”", "\"주문이 들어올 때쯤이면 나는 그걸 처리할 처지가 못 될 게다, 얘야,\""], ["returned Solomon Gills.", "솔로몬 길스가 대답했다."], ["“They’ll never come to this shop again, till I am out of t.”", "\"내가 이 가게에서 나가기 전엔 주문이 다시는 안 들어올 테니.\""]], [["“I say, Uncle!", "\"이봐요, 외삼촌!"], ["You musn’t really, you know!”", "정말 그러시면 안 돼요, 아시잖아요!\""], ["urged Walter.", "월터가 다그쳤다."], ["“Don’t!”", "\"그러지 마세요!\""]], [["Old Sol endeavoured to assume a cheery look, and smiled across the little table at him as pleasantly as he could.", "노 솔은 애써 명랑한 표정을 지어 보이려 하면서, 작은 탁자 너머로 그에게 최대한 다정하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“There’s nothing more than usual the matter; is there, Uncle?”", "\"평소보다 더한 무슨 일이 있는 건 아니죠, 외삼촌?\""], ["said Walter, leaning his elbows on the tea tray, and bending over, to speak the more confidentially and kindly.", "월터가 찻쟁반에 팔꿈치를 괴고 몸을 기울여, 한층 은밀하고 다정하게 말했다."], ["“Be open with me, Uncle, if there is, and tell me all about it.”", "\"혹시 그렇다면 저한테 솔직히 털어놓으세요, 외삼촌, 무슨 일인지 다 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“No, no, no,”", "\"아니, 아니, 아니다,\""], ["returned Old Sol.", "노 솔이 대답했다."], ["“More than usual? No, no.", "\"평소보다 더한 일?"], ["What should there be the matter more than usual?”", "아니, 아니야. 평소보다 더한 무슨 일이 있겠니?\""]], [["Walter answered with an incredulous shake of his head.", "월터는 못 믿겠다는 듯 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["“That’s what I want to know,”", "\"그게 바로 제가 알고 싶은 거예요,\""], ["he said, “and you ask me!", "그가 말했다, \"그런데 도리어 저한테 물으시다니요!"], ["I’ll tell you what, Uncle, when I see you like this, I am quite sorry that I live with you.”", "제가 말씀드릴게요, 외삼촌, 외삼촌이 이러시는 걸 보면, 외삼촌이랑 같이 사는 게 정말 미안해져요.\""]], [["Old Sol opened his eyes involuntarily.", "노 솔은 자기도 모르게 눈을 휘둥그레 떴다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Though nobody ever was happier than I am and always have been with you, I am quite sorry that I live with you, when I see you with anything in your mind.”", "외삼촌이랑 함께 지내는 것보다 더 행복했던 사람은 없을 만큼, 저는 늘 행복했지만요, 외삼촌이 마음에 뭔가 담아 두고 있는 걸 보면, 외삼촌이랑 같이 사는 게 정말 미안해져요.\""]], [["“I am a little dull at such times, I know,”", "\"그럴 때면 내가 좀 시무룩해지는 거 나도 안다,\""], ["observed Solomon, meekly rubbing his hands.", "솔로몬이 온순하게 두 손을 비비며 말했다."]], [["“What I mean, Uncle Sol,”", "\"제 말씀은요, 솔 외삼촌,\""], ["pursued Walter, bending over a little more to pat him on the shoulder, “is, that then I feel you ought to have, sitting here and pouring out the tea instead of me, a nice little dumpling of a wife, you know,—a comfortable, capital, cosy old lady, who was just a match for you, and knew how to manage you, and keep you in good heart.", "월터가 조금 더 몸을 기울여 그의 어깨를 토닥이며 이어 갔다, \"그럴 때면 저 대신 여기 앉아 차를 따라 줄, 동글동글하고 자그마한 아내가 외삼촌한테 있어야 마땅하다는 거예요—편안하고 더없이 좋고 아늑한 노부인 말이에요, 외삼촌한테 딱 어울리고, 외삼촌을 어떻게 다뤄야 할지 알고, 외삼촌 기운을 북돋아 줄 줄 아는 그런 분이요."], ["Here am I, as loving a nephew as ever was (I am sure I ought to be!) but I am only a nephew, and I can’t be such a companion to you when you’re low and out of sorts as she would have made herself, years ago, though I’m sure I’d give any money if I could cheer you up.", "여기 저로 말하면, 어느 조카 못지않게 외삼촌을 사랑하긴 하지만요 (당연히 그래야죠!) 그래도 저는 그저 조카일 뿐이라, 외삼촌이 가라앉고 기분이 안 좋을 때, 그분이 오래전에 그래 주었을 만큼 좋은 동무가 되어 드리지를 못해요, 외삼촌 기운을 북돋아 드릴 수만 있다면 무슨 돈이라도 다 내놓을 텐데 말이에요."], ["And so I say, when I see you with anything on your mind, that I feel quite sorry you haven’t got somebody better about you than a blundering young rough-and-tough boy like me, who has got the will to console you, Uncle, but hasn’t got the way—hasn’t got the way,”", "그래서 저는, 외삼촌이 마음에 뭔가 담아 두고 있는 걸 볼 때면, 저처럼 어설프고 거칠고 미숙한 풋내기 소년보다 더 나은 누군가가 외삼촌 곁에 없는 게 정말 안타깝다는 거예요. 저는 외삼촌을 위로하려는 마음은 있어도, 그 방법을 모르거든요—방법을 몰라요,\""], ["repeated Walter, reaching over further yet, to shake his Uncle by the hand.", "월터는 외삼촌의 손을 잡으려고 더욱더 몸을 뻗으며 되풀이했다."]], [["“Wally, my dear boy,”", "\"월리, 사랑하는 얘야,\""], ["said Solomon, “if the cosy little old lady had taken her place in this parlour five and forty years ago, I never could have been fonder of her than I am of you.”", "솔로몬이 말했다, \"그 아늑한 자그마한 노부인이 마흔다섯 해 전에 이 거실에 자리 잡았다 한들, 내가 지금 너를 아끼는 것만큼 그녀를 더 아낄 수는 없었을 게다.\""]], [["“I know that, Uncle Sol,”", "\"저도 알아요, 솔 외삼촌,\""], ["returned Walter.", "월터가 대답했다."], ["“Lord bless you, I know that.", "\"정말이지 저도 알아요."], ["But you wouldn’t have had the whole weight of any uncomfortable secrets if she had been with you, because she would have known how to relieve you of ’em, and I don’t.”", "하지만 그분이 곁에 있었다면, 외삼촌이 불편한 비밀이라는 짐 전부를 혼자 짊어지지는 않으셨을 거예요, 그분은 그 짐을 덜어 드릴 줄 알았을 테니까요, 저는 모르지만요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, you do,”", "\"아니, 아니야, 너도 안다,\""], ["returned the Instrument-maker.", "기구 제조공이 대답했다."]], [["“Well then, what’s the matter, Uncle Sol?”", "\"그럼 도대체 무슨 일이세요, 솔 외삼촌?\""], ["said Walter, coaxingly.", "월터가 어르듯이 말했다."], ["“Come!", "\"자요!"], ["What’s the matter?”", "무슨 일이세요?\""]], [["Solomon Gills persisted that there was nothing the matter; and maintained it so resolutely, that his nephew had no resource but to make a very indifferent imitation of believing him.", "솔로몬 길스는 아무 일도 없다고 우겼고, 어찌나 단호히 버티던지, 그의 조카로서는 믿는 시늉을 영 어설프게나마 해 보는 수밖에 도리가 없었다."]], [["“All I can say is, Uncle Sol, that if there is—”", "\"제가 드릴 말씀은요, 솔 외삼촌, 만약 무슨 일이 있다면—\""]], [["“But there isn’t,”", "\"하지만 아무 일도 없대도,\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋아요,\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."], ["“Then I’ve no more to say; and that’s lucky, for my time’s up for going to business.", "\"그럼 더는 드릴 말씀이 없네요. 그게 다행이에요, 일하러 갈 시간이 됐으니까요."], ["I shall look in by-and-by when I’m out, to see how you get on, Uncle.", "나갔다가 이따 들러서, 어떻게 지내시는지 볼게요, 외삼촌. 그리고 명심하세요, 외삼촌!"], ["And mind, Uncle! I’ll never believe you again, and never tell you anything more about Mr Carker the Junior, if I find out that you have been deceiving me!”", "만약 외삼촌이 저를 속이고 있었다는 걸 알아내면, 다시는 외삼촌 말을 안 믿을 거고, 카커 주니어 씨에 관한 얘기도 다시는 안 해 드릴 거예요!\""]], [["Solomon Gills laughingly defied him to find out anything of the kind; and Walter, revolving in his thoughts all sorts of impracticable ways of making fortunes and placing the wooden Midshipman in a position of independence, betook himself to the offices of Dombey and Son with a heavier countenance than he usually carried there.", "솔로몬 길스는 웃으며 그런 건 어디 알아낼 테면 알아내 보라고 받아쳤다. 그리고 월터는, 한밑천 잡아 그 나무 사관후보생을 떳떳한 독립의 처지에 올려놓을 온갖 비현실적인 방법들을 머릿속에서 굴리며, 평소보다 한층 무거운 얼굴로 돔비와 아들 회사 사무소로 향했다."]], [["There lived in those days, round the corner—in Bishopsgate Street Without—one Brogley, sworn broker and appraiser, who kept a shop where every description of second-hand furniture was exhibited in the most uncomfortable aspect, and under circumstances and in combinations the most completely foreign to its purpose.", "그 시절 모퉁이를 돌면—비숍스게이트 스트리트 위다우트에—브로글리라는 자가 살았는데, 공인 중개인 겸 감정인이었다. 그는 가게를 하나 꾸렸는데, 거기에는 온갖 종류의 중고 가구가 더없이 거북한 모양새로, 그 본래 용도와는 가장 완전히 동떨어진 상황과 조합으로 진열되어 있었다."], ["Dozens of chairs hooked on to washing-stands, which with difficulty poised themselves on the shoulders of sideboards, which in their turn stood upon the wrong side of dining-tables, gymnastic with their legs upward on the tops of other dining-tables, were among its most reasonable arrangements.", "의자 수십 개가 세면대에 걸려 있고, 그 세면대는 간신히 찬장 어깨 위에서 균형을 잡고 있으며, 그 찬장은 다시 식탁의 뒤집힌 쪽에 올라서 있고, 또 그 식탁은 다리를 위로 쳐든 채 다른 식탁 위에서 곡예를 부리고 있는 것이, 그 가게에서 가장 합리적인 배치 축에 들었다."], ["A banquet array of dish-covers, wine-glasses, and decanters was generally to be seen, spread forth upon the bosom of a four-post bedstead, for the entertainment of such genial company as half-a-dozen pokers, and a hall lamp.", "접시 덮개며 포도주잔이며 디캔터를 잔칫상처럼 늘어놓은 것은, 부지깽이 대여섯 개와 현관 등(燈) 같은 정겨운 손님들을 대접하느라, 흔히 네 기둥 침대의 한복판에 펼쳐져 있는 광경으로 볼 수 있었다."], ["A set of window curtains with no windows belonging to them, would be seen gracefully draping a barricade of chests of drawers, loaded with little jars from chemists’ shops; while a homeless hearthrug severed from its natural companion the fireside, braved the shrewd east wind in its adversity, and trembled in melancholy accord with the shrill complainings of a cabinet piano, wasting away, a string a day, and faintly resounding to the noises of the street in its jangling and distracted brain.", "딸린 창문도 없는 창문 커튼 한 벌이, 약방에서 가져온 작은 단지들을 잔뜩 인 서랍장 바리케이드를 우아하게 휘감고 있는가 하면, 한편으로는 천연의 짝인 난롯가에서 떨어져 나와 갈 곳 없어진 난로 깔개가, 그 역경 속에서 매서운 동풍에 맞서며, 하루에 현 하나씩 닳아 가다가 그 어지럽고 산만한 머릿속으로 거리의 소음에 희미하게 울려 대는 어느 캐비닛 피아노의 날카로운 푸념에 우울하게 장단을 맞추어 떨고 있었다."], ["Of motionless clocks that never stirred a finger, and seemed as incapable of being successfully wound up, as the pecuniary affairs of their former owners, there was always great choice in Mr Brogley’s shop; and various looking-glasses, accidentally placed at compound interest of reflection and refraction, presented to the eye an eternal perspective of bankruptcy and ruin.", "손가락 하나 까딱 않고, 옛 주인들의 금전 사정만큼이나 도무지 제대로 태엽을 감을 수 없을 듯 보이는, 멈춰 선 시계들은 브로글리 씨의 가게에 늘 풍성하게 구비되어 있었다. 또 반사와 굴절의 복리(複利)로 우연히 마주 놓인 여러 거울들은, 보는 이의 눈에 파산과 몰락의 영원한 원근법을 펼쳐 보였다."]], [["Mr Brogley himself was a moist-eyed, pink-complexioned, crisp-haired man, of a bulky figure and an easy temper—for that class of Caius Marius who sits upon the ruins of other people’s Carthages, can keep up his spirits well enough.", "브로글리 씨 본인은 눈이 촉촉하고 안색이 발그레하며 머리가 곱슬곱슬한 사람으로, 덩치가 큼직하고 성미가 느긋했다—남의 카르타고 폐허 위에 걸터앉는 부류의 카이우스 마리우스라면, 능히 기운을 잃지 않고 지낼 수 있는 법이니까."], ["He had looked in at Solomon’s shop sometimes, to ask a question about articles in Solomon’s way of business; and Walter knew him sufficiently to give him good day when they met in the street.", "그는 이따금 솔로몬의 가게에 들러 솔로몬의 업종에 속하는 물품에 관해 한두 가지 물어보곤 했다. 그래서 월터도 거리에서 그와 마주치면 인사를 건넬 만큼은 그를 알고 있었다."], ["But as that was the extent of the broker’s acquaintance with Solomon Gills also, Walter was not a little surprised when he came back in the course of the forenoon, agreeably to his promise, to find Mr Brogley sitting in the back parlour with his hands in his pockets, and his hat hanging up behind the door.", "그러나 그 중개인이 솔로몬 길스와 알고 지내는 정도 역시 그것이 전부였던지라, 월터가 약속대로 오전 중에 돌아왔을 때 브로글리 씨가 뒷방에 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 앉아 있고, 그의 모자가 문 뒤에 걸려 있는 것을 보고는 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["“Well, Uncle Sol!” said Walter.", "\"어, 솔 외삼촌!\""], ["The old man was sitting ruefully on the opposite side of the table, with his spectacles over his eyes, for a wonder, instead of on his forehead.", "월터가 말했다. 노인은 탁자 맞은편에 침울하게 앉아 있었는데, 웬일로 안경을 이마가 아니라 눈에 걸치고 있었다."], ["“How are you now?”", "\"이제 좀 어떠세요?\""]], [["Solomon shook his head, and waved one hand towards the broker, as introducing him.", "솔로몬은 고개를 저으며, 한 손으로 중개인 쪽을 가리켜 그를 소개하듯 했다."]], [["“Is there anything the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이 있는 거예요?\""], ["asked Walter, with a catching in his breath.", "월터가 숨이 멎는 듯한 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“No, no.", "\"아니, 아니오."], ["There’s nothing the matter, said Mr Brogley.", "아무 일도 없어요,\" 브로글리 씨가 말했다."], ["“Don’t let it put you out of the way.”", "\"이 일로 너무 마음 쓰지 마시오.\""]], [["Walter looked from the broker to his Uncle in mute amazement.", "월터는 중개인에게서 외삼촌에게로, 말없이 어리둥절한 눈길을 옮겼다."]], [["“The fact is,”", "\"실은 말이오,\""], ["said Mr Brogley, “there’s a little payment on a bond debt —three hundred and seventy odd, overdue: and I’m in possession.”", "브로글리 씨가 말했다, \"증서 채무에 대한 소액 변제 건이 좀 있는데—삼백칠십몇 파운드가 연체되어서, 내가 점유하고 있는 거요.\""]], [["“In possession!”", "\"점유라니요!\""], ["cried Walter, looking round at the shop.", "월터가 가게를 휘둘러보며 외쳤다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"그렇소!\""], ["said Mr Brogley, in confidential assent, and nodding his head as if he would urge the advisability of their all being comfortable together.", "브로글리 씨가 은밀히 동의하듯 말하며, 우리 모두 다 같이 사이좋게 지내는 편이 좋겠다고 권하기라도 하는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“It’s an execution.", "\"차압이오."], ["That’s what it is.", "그게 그거요."], ["Don’t let it put you out of the way.", "너무 마음 쓰지 마시오."], ["I come myself, because of keeping it quiet and sociable.", "조용하고 사근사근하게 처리하려고 내가 직접 온 거요."], ["You know me.", "나를 알잖소."], ["It’s quite private.”", "아주 사적인 일이오.\""]], [["“Uncle Sol!”", "\"솔 외삼촌!\""], ["faltered Walter.", "월터가 더듬거렸다."]], [["“Wally, my boy,”", "\"월리, 얘야,\""], ["returned his uncle.", "외삼촌이 대답했다."], ["“It’s the first time.", "\"이런 건 처음이란다."], ["Such a calamity never happened to me before.", "이런 재앙은 내게 한 번도 닥친 적이 없었어."], ["I’m an old man to begin.”", "시작하기엔 나는 늙은이로구나.\""], ["Pushing up his spectacles again (for they were useless any longer to conceal his emotion), he covered his face with his hand, and sobbed aloud, and his tears fell down upon his coffee-coloured waistcoat.", "그는 (이제 감정을 숨기는 데 더는 쓸모가 없어진) 안경을 다시 이마로 밀어 올리고는, 한 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 소리 내어 흐느꼈고, 그의 눈물이 커피색 조끼 위로 떨어졌다."]], [["“Uncle Sol!", "\"솔 외삼촌!"], ["Pray!", "제발!"], ["oh don’t!”", "오, 그러지 마세요!\""], ["exclaimed Walter, who really felt a thrill of terror in seeing the old man weep.", "월터가 외쳤는데, 노인이 우는 것을 보고는 정말이지 섬뜩한 공포를 느꼈던 것이다."], ["“For God’s sake don’t do that.", "\"제발 그러지 마세요."], ["Mr Brogley, what shall I do?”", "브로글리 씨, 저는 어떡하면 좋죠?\""]], [["“I should recommend you looking up a friend or so,”", "\"친구분이라도 한두 분 찾아가서,\""], ["said Mr Brogley, “and talking it over.”", "브로글리 씨가 말했다, \"의논해 보시길 권하겠소.\""]], [["“To be sure!”", "\"맞아요!\""], ["cried Walter, catching at anything.", "월터가 지푸라기라도 잡듯 외쳤다."], ["“Certainly!", "\"그래요!"], ["Thankee.", "고마워요."], ["Captain Cuttle’s the man, Uncle.", "커틀 선장님이 적임자예요, 외삼촌."], ["Wait till I run to Captain Cuttle.", "제가 커틀 선장님께 달려갔다 올 때까지 기다리세요."], ["Keep your eye upon my Uncle, will you, Mr Brogley, and make him as comfortable as you can while I am gone?", "제가 없는 동안 우리 외삼촌 좀 지켜봐 주시고, 최대한 편안하게 해 드릴 수 있죠, 브로글리 씨?"], ["Don’t despair, Uncle Sol.", "절망하지 마세요, 솔 외삼촌."], ["Try and keep a good heart, there’s a dear fellow!”", "부디 마음 굳게 먹어 보세요, 우리 좋은 외삼촌!\""]], [["Saying this with great fervour, and disregarding the old man’s broken remonstrances, Walter dashed out of the shop again as hard as he could go; and, having hurried round to the office to excuse himself on the plea of his Uncle’s sudden illness, set off, full speed, for Captain Cuttle’s residence.", "월터는 이 말을 무척 열렬하게 내뱉으며, 노인의 띄엄띄엄한 만류에도 아랑곳없이, 다시 있는 힘껏 가게에서 뛰쳐나갔다. 그리고 사무소로 부랴부랴 돌아가 외삼촌이 갑자기 편찮으시다는 핑계로 양해를 구한 다음, 전속력으로 커틀 선장의 거처를 향해 떠났다."]], [["Everything seemed altered as he ran along the streets.", "거리를 달려가는 동안 모든 것이 달라 보였다."], ["There were the usual entanglement and noise of carts, drays, omnibuses, waggons, and foot passengers, but the misfortune that had fallen on the wooden Midshipman made it strange and new.", "손수레며 짐마차며 합승마차며 사륜마차며 보행자들이 평소처럼 뒤엉켜 시끄러웠지만, 그 나무 사관후보생에게 닥친 불운이 그 모든 것을 낯설고 새롭게 만들었다."], ["Houses and shops were different from what they used to be, and bore Mr Brogley’s warrant on their fronts in large characters.", "집과 가게들도 예전과 달라져, 정면에 브로글리 씨의 영장이 큼직한 글자로 붙어 있는 듯했다."], ["The broker seemed to have got hold of the very churches; for their spires rose into the sky with an unwonted air.", "그 중개인은 교회들마저 손아귀에 넣은 것 같았으니, 교회 첨탑들이 어딘가 낯선 기운을 띤 채 하늘로 솟아 있었던 것이다."], ["Even the sky itself was changed, and had an execution in it plainly.", "하늘 자체도 달라져, 그 안에 차압이 들어 있는 것이 역력했다."]], [["Captain Cuttle lived on the brink of a little canal near the India Docks, where there was a swivel bridge which opened now and then to let some wandering monster of a ship come roaming up the street like a stranded leviathan.", "커틀 선장은 인디아 부두 근처의 작은 운하 가장자리에 살았는데, 거기에는 회전교(回轉橋)가 하나 있어, 이따금 다리를 열어 헤매던 괴물 같은 배 한 척이 좌초된 거대한 바다 짐승처럼 거리 위로 어슬렁어슬렁 거슬러 올라오게 해 주었다."], ["The gradual change from land to water, on the approach to Captain Cuttle’s lodgings, was curious.", "커틀 선장의 숙소로 다가갈수록 육지에서 물로 점차 변해 가는 모습은 진기했다."], ["It began with the erection of flagstaffs, as appurtenances to public-houses; then came slop-sellers’ shops, with Guernsey shirts, sou’wester hats, and canvas pantaloons, at once the tightest and the loosest of their order, hanging up outside.", "그것은 술집의 부속물인 양 깃대들이 세워지는 것으로 시작되었다. 다음으로는 헌옷 장수들의 가게가 나왔는데, 거지 셔츠며 방수 모자며, 같은 부류 중에서도 가장 꽉 끼면서 동시에 가장 헐렁한 범포 바지가 바깥에 걸려 있었다."], ["These were succeeded by anchor and chain-cable forges, where sledgehammers were dinging upon iron all day long.", "그다음으로는 닻과 사슬 케이블을 만드는 대장간들이 이어져, 거기서는 큰 쇠망치가 하루 종일 쇠를 땅땅 두드려 댔다."], ["Then came rows of houses, with little vane-surmounted masts uprearing themselves from among the scarlet beans.", "그다음으로는 집들이 늘어서 있었는데, 진홍빛 강낭콩 사이로 작은 풍향계 단 돛대들이 솟아 있었다."], ["Then, ditches.", "그다음은 도랑들."], ["Then, pollard willows.", "그다음은 가지 친 버드나무들."], ["Then, more ditches.", "그다음은 또 도랑들."], ["Then, unaccountable patches of dirty water, hardly to be descried, for the ships that covered them.", "그다음은 까닭 모를, 더러운 물웅덩이들이 여기저기 있었는데, 그 위를 뒤덮은 배들 때문에 거의 분간하기 어려울 지경이었다."], ["Then, the air was perfumed with chips; and all other trades were swallowed up in mast, oar, and block-making, and boatbuilding.", "그다음으로는 대팻밥 냄새가 공기에 배어 있었고, 다른 모든 업종이 돛대, 노, 도르래 제작과 배 만드는 일에 삼켜져 버렸다."], ["Then, the ground grew marshy and unsettled.", "그다음으로는 땅이 질척하고 불안정해졌다."], ["Then, there was nothing to be smelt but rum and sugar.", "그다음으로는 럼주와 설탕 말고는 아무 냄새도 나지 않았다."], ["Then, Captain Cuttle’s lodgings—at once a first floor and a top storey, in Brig Place—were close before you.", "그다음으로는 커틀 선장의 숙소가—브릭 플레이스에 있는, 이층이자 동시에 꼭대기 층인 그 방이—바로 코앞에 있었다."]], [["The Captain was one of those timber-looking men, suits of oak as well as hearts, whom it is almost impossible for the liveliest imagination to separate from any part of their dress, however insignificant.", "선장은 목재처럼 생긴 사람들 부류에 속했는데, 마음씨뿐 아니라 옷차림까지 참나무로 한 벌 지어 입은 듯한 그런 사람들이라, 아무리 생기 넘치는 상상력으로도 그를 옷의 어느 한 부분과—그것이 아무리 하찮은 것일지라도—떼어 놓기란 거의 불가능했다."], ["Accordingly, when Walter knocked at the door, and the Captain instantly poked his head out of one of his little front windows, and hailed him, with the hard glared hat already on it, and the shirt-collar like a sail, and the wide suit of blue, all standing as usual, Walter was as fully persuaded that he was always in that state, as if the Captain had been a bird and those had been his feathers.", "그래서 월터가 문을 두드리자 선장이 즉시 자기의 작은 앞창 하나로 머리를 쑥 내밀고 그를 불렀을 때, 그 머리에 어느새 단단한 광택 모자가 얹혀 있고, 셔츠 깃이 돛처럼 서 있으며, 헐렁한 푸른 한 벌이 평소처럼 죄다 갖추어져 있는 것을 보고는, 월터는 선장이 늘 그런 상태로 있다고, 마치 선장이 새이고 그것들이 그의 깃털이기라도 한 양 완전히 믿어 버렸다."]], [["“Wal”r, my lad!”", "\"월터, 얘야!\""], ["said Captain Cuttle.", "커틀 선장이 말했다."], ["“Stand by and knock again.", "\"대기하고서 다시 두드려 봐라."], ["Hard!", "세게!"], ["It’s washing day.”", "오늘이 빨래하는 날이거든.\""]], [["Walter, in his impatience, gave a prodigious thump with the knocker.", "월터는 조급한 마음에 노커를 어마어마하게 쾅 내리쳤다."]], [["“Hard it is!”", "\"세게 두드렸군!\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, and immediately drew in his head, as if he expected a squall.", "커틀 선장이 말하더니, 마치 돌풍이라도 예상하는 듯 즉시 머리를 안으로 끌어들였다."]], [["Nor was he mistaken: for a widow lady, with her sleeves rolled up to her shoulders, and her arms frothy with soap-suds and smoking with hot water, replied to the summons with startling rapidity.", "그의 예상은 빗나가지 않았다. 소매를 어깨까지 걷어 올리고, 두 팔에 비누 거품이 일고 뜨거운 물 김이 모락모락 피어오르는 어느 과부가, 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 잽싸게 그 부름에 응답해 나왔던 것이다."], ["Before she looked at Walter she looked at the knocker, and then, measuring him with her eyes from head to foot, said she wondered he had left any of it.", "그 여인은 월터를 보기에 앞서 노커를 보고는, 이어 그를 머리부터 발끝까지 눈으로 가늠하더니, 노커가 남아나기는 했는지 모르겠다고 말했다."]], [["“Captain Cuttle’s at home, I know,”", "\"커틀 선장님 댁에 계신 거 알아요,\""], ["said Walter with a conciliatory smile.", "월터가 비위를 맞추려는 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Is he?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["replied the widow lady.", "과부가 응수했다."], ["“In-deed!”", "\"그-래요!\""]], [["“He has just been speaking to me,”", "\"방금 저한테 말씀하셨는걸요,\""], ["said Walter, in breathless explanation.", "월터가 숨을 헐떡이며 설명했다."]], [["“Has he?”", "\"그러셨어요?\""], ["replied the widow lady.", "과부가 응수했다."], ["“Then p’raps you’ll give him Mrs MacStinger’s respects, and say that the next time he lowers himself and his lodgings by talking out of the winder she’ll thank him to come down and open the door too.”", "\"그럼 아마 그분께 맥스팅어 부인이 안부 여쭙더라고 전해 주시고, 다음번에 또 창밖으로 떠들어 대서 당신 체면이랑 당신 하숙집 격을 떨어뜨릴 양이면, 친절히 좀 내려와서 문도 직접 열어 주시라고 말씀드려 줘요.\""], ["Mrs MacStinger spoke loud, and listened for any observations that might be offered from the first floor.", "맥스팅어 부인은 큰 소리로 말하면서, 이층에서 무슨 한마디가 나오나 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“I’ll mention it,”", "\"전해 드릴게요,\""], ["said Walter, “if you’ll have the goodness to let me in, Ma’am.”", "월터가 말했다, \"들어가게 해 주시면요, 부인.\""]], [["For he was repelled by a wooden fortification extending across the doorway, and put there to prevent the little MacStingers in their moments of recreation from tumbling down the steps.", "문간을 가로질러 친 나무 방벽 때문에 그가 막혀 있었던 것인데, 그것은 어린 맥스팅어들이 노는 동안 계단 아래로 굴러떨어지지 않게 거기 세워 둔 것이었다."]], [["“A boy that can knock my door down,”", "\"우리 집 문짝을 다 부숴 놓을 수 있는 애라면,\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger, contemptuously, “can get over that, I should hope!”", "맥스팅어 부인이 경멸하듯 말했다, \"저쯤은 넘어올 수 있겠지요, 안 그래요!\""], ["But Walter, taking this as a permission to enter, and getting over it, Mrs MacStinger immediately demanded whether an Englishwoman’s house was her castle or not; and whether she was to be broke in upon by “raff.”", "그러나 월터가 이것을 들어와도 좋다는 허락으로 받아들여 그것을 넘어서자, 맥스팅어 부인은 곧장 영국 여인의 집은 그녀의 성(城)인지 아닌지, 그리고 자기가 \"잡것들\"한테 불쑥 침입당해도 되는 건지 따져 물었다."], ["On these subjects her thirst for information was still very importunate, when Walter, having made his way up the little staircase through an artificial fog occasioned by the washing, which covered the banisters with a clammy perspiration, entered Captain Cuttle’s room, and found that gentleman in ambush behind the door.", "월터가 빨래 때문에 생겨난 인공 안개를 뚫고—그 안개는 난간을 끈적끈적한 땀으로 뒤덮고 있었다—작은 계단을 올라가 커틀 선장의 방에 들어서서, 그 신사가 문 뒤에 매복해 있는 것을 발견했을 때에도, 이 문제들에 관한 그녀의 갈증은 여전히 끈질기게 정보를 갈구하고 있었다."]], [["“Never owed her a penny, Wal”r,”", "\"저 여자한테 단돈 한 푼 빚진 적 없다, 월터,\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, in a low voice, and with visible marks of trepidation on his countenance.", "커틀 선장이 낮은 목소리로, 얼굴에 두려운 기색을 역력히 드러내며 말했다."], ["“Done her a world of good turns, and the children too.", "\"오히려 저 여자한테, 그리고 애들한테도 숱하게 좋은 일을 해 줬지."], ["Vixen at times, though.", "그래도 가끔 표독스러운 여편네라니까."], ["Whew!”", "휘유!\""]], [["“I should go away, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"저 같으면 떠나 버리겠어요, 커틀 선장님,\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."]], [["“Dursn’t do it, Wal”r,”", "\"감히 그러지를 못한다, 월터,\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."], ["“She’d find me out, wherever I went.", "\"내가 어디로 가든 저 여자가 기어이 찾아낼 거야."], ["Sit down.", "앉아라."], ["How’s Gills?”", "길스는 좀 어떠냐?\""]], [["The Captain was dining (in his hat) off cold loin of mutton, porter, and some smoking hot potatoes, which he had cooked himself, and took out of a little saucepan before the fire as he wanted them.", "선장은 (모자를 쓴 채로) 식은 양고기 허릿살, 흑맥주, 그리고 김이 모락모락 나는 감자 몇 개로 식사를 하고 있었는데, 그 감자는 자기가 직접 삶아 필요할 때마다 난롯불 앞 작은 냄비에서 꺼내 먹는 것이었다."], ["He unscrewed his hook at dinner-time, and screwed a knife into its wooden socket instead, with which he had already begun to peel one of these potatoes for Walter.", "그는 식사 때면 자기 갈고리를 비틀어 빼고, 그 나무 소켓에 대신 칼을 비틀어 끼웠는데, 이미 그 칼로 월터에게 줄 감자 하나를 까기 시작한 참이었다."], ["His rooms were very small, and strongly impregnated with tobacco-smoke, but snug enough: everything being stowed away, as if there were an earthquake regularly every half-hour.", "그의 방은 무척 비좁았고 담배 연기가 짙게 배어 있었지만, 그런대로 아늑했다. 마치 삼십 분마다 정기적으로 지진이라도 나는 양, 모든 것이 차곡차곡 치워져 있었다."]], [["“How’s Gills?”", "\"길스는 좀 어떠냐?\""], ["inquired the Captain.", "선장이 물었다."]], [["Walter, who had by this time recovered his breath, and lost his spirits—or such temporary spirits as his rapid journey had given him—looked at his questioner for a moment, said “Oh, Captain Cuttle!”", "이때쯤이면 숨을 돌렸으나 기운은—다급한 여정이 잠시 불어넣어 준 그 일시적인 기운마저—잃어버린 월터는, 묻는 이를 잠시 바라보다가, \"오, 커틀 선장님!\""], ["and burst into tears.", "하더니 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["No words can describe the Captain’s consternation at this sight.", "이 광경에 선장이 얼마나 경악했는지는 말로 다 표현할 수 없다."], ["Mrs MacStinger faded into nothing before it.", "맥스팅어 부인 따위는 그 앞에서 흔적도 없이 사라져 버렸다."], ["He dropped the potato and the fork—and would have dropped the knife too if he could—and sat gazing at the boy, as if he expected to hear next moment that a gulf had opened in the City, which had swallowed up his old friend, coffee-coloured suit, buttons, chronometer, spectacles, and all.", "그는 감자와 포크를 떨어뜨렸고—할 수만 있었다면 칼도 떨어뜨렸으리라—소년을 빤히 바라보며 앉아 있었으니, 마치 다음 순간 시(市)에 심연이 열려 자기 옛 친구를, 커피색 양복이며 단추며 정밀시계며 안경이며 죄다 한꺼번에 삼켜 버렸다는 소식이라도 들으려는 듯했다."]], [["But when Walter told him what was really the matter, Captain Cuttle, after a moment’s reflection, started up into full activity. He emptied out of a little tin canister on the top shelf of the cupboard, his whole stock of ready money (amounting to thirteen pounds and half-a-crown), which he transferred to one of the pockets of his square blue coat; further enriched that repository with the contents of his plate chest, consisting of two withered atomies of tea-spoons, and an obsolete pair of knock-knee’d sugar-tongs; pulled up his immense double-cased silver watch from the depths in which it reposed, to assure himself that that valuable was sound and whole; re-attached the hook to his right wrist; and seizing the stick covered over with knobs, bade Walter come along.", "그러나 월터가 정말로 무슨 일인지 그에게 이야기하자, 커틀 선장은 잠시 생각하고 나서 벌떡 일어나 본격적으로 움직이기 시작했다. 그는 찬장 맨 위 선반에 있던 작은 양철통을 비워, 자기의 현금 재산 전부를(십삼 파운드 반 크라운에 달했다) 꺼내어 네모난 푸른 외투의 호주머니 하나에 옮겨 넣었다. 또 시들어 빠진 찻숟가락 두 개의 잔해와 한물간 안짱다리 설탕 집게 한 벌로 이루어진 은제품 상자의 내용물로 그 호주머니를 한층 더 불렸다. 그러고는 깊숙이 묻혀 잠들어 있던 자기의 거대한 이중 케이스 은시계를 끄집어내어, 그 귀중품이 멀쩡하고 온전한지 확인했다. 갈고리를 다시 오른쪽 손목에 부착하고, 옹이가 잔뜩 덮인 지팡이를 움켜쥔 다음, 월터에게 가자고 일렀다."]], [["Remembering, however, in the midst of his virtuous excitement, that Mrs MacStinger might be lying in wait below, Captain Cuttle hesitated at last, not without glancing at the window, as if he had some thoughts of escaping by that unusual means of egress, rather than encounter his terrible enemy.", "그러나 그 의로운 흥분의 와중에도, 맥스팅어 부인이 아래층에서 매복해 기다리고 있을지 모른다는 데에 생각이 미치자, 선장은 마침내 머뭇거렸다. 그러면서 창문을 흘끔거렸는데, 그 무서운 적과 맞닥뜨리느니 차라리 그 유별난 출구로 빠져나갈까 하는 생각을 얼마간 품은 듯했다."], ["He decided, however, in favour of stratagem.", "그러나 그는 결국 책략 쪽으로 마음을 정했다."]], [["“Wal”r,”", "\"월터,\""], ["said the Captain, with a timid wink, “go afore, my lad.", "선장이 겁먹은 듯 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다. \"네가 앞장서라, 얘야."], ["Sing out, ‘good-bye, Captain Cuttle,’ when you’re in the passage, and shut the door.", "복도에 나가거든 '안녕히 계세요, 커틀 선장님' 하고 소리쳐 외친 다음 문을 닫아."], ["Then wait at the corner of the street “till you see me.", "그러고는 거리 모퉁이에서 내가 보일 때까지 기다려라.\""]], [["These directions were not issued without a previous knowledge of the enemy’s tactics, for when Walter got downstairs, Mrs MacStinger glided out of the little back kitchen, like an avenging spirit.", "이런 지시는 적의 전술을 미리 알고서 내린 것이었다. 월터가 아래층으로 내려가자, 맥스팅어 부인이 복수의 정령처럼 작은 뒷부엌에서 스르르 미끄러져 나왔던 것이다."], ["But not gliding out upon the Captain, as she had expected, she merely made a further allusion to the knocker, and glided in again.", "그러나 자기가 예상했던 대로 선장에게 미끄러져 나온 게 아닌지라, 그녀는 노커에 관한 언급을 한 번 더 덧붙이고는 다시 스르르 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["Some five minutes elapsed before Captain Cuttle could summon courage to attempt his escape; for Walter waited so long at the street corner, looking back at the house, before there were any symptoms of the hard glazed hat.", "커틀 선장이 탈출을 감행할 용기를 그러모으기까지는 오 분쯤 걸렸다. 그동안 월터는 거리 모퉁이에서, 그 단단한 광택 모자가 나타날 기미가 보이기를 기다리며 집 쪽을 돌아보며 그토록 오래 서 있었다."], ["At length the Captain burst out of the door with the suddenness of an explosion, and coming towards him at a great pace, and never once looking over his shoulder, pretended, as soon as they were well out of the street, to whistle a tune.", "마침내 선장은 폭발하듯 별안간 문을 박차고 나와, 어깨너머로 단 한 번도 돌아보지 않은 채 빠른 걸음으로 그에게 다가왔고, 거리를 완전히 벗어나자마자 휘파람으로 곡조를 부는 척했다."]], [["“Uncle much hove down, Wal”r?”", "\"외삼촌이 많이 가라앉으셨더냐, 월터?\""], ["inquired the Captain, as they were walking along.", "함께 걸으며 선장이 물었다."]], [["“I am afraid so.", "\"그런 것 같아요."], ["If you had seen him this morning, you would never have forgotten it.”", "오늘 아침 그 모습을 보셨다면, 결코 잊지 못하셨을 거예요.\""]], [["“Walk fast, Wal”r, my lad,”", "\"빨리 걸어라, 월터, 얘야,\""], ["returned the Captain, mending his pace; “and walk the same all the days of your life.", "선장이 발걸음을 재촉하며 대답했다, \"그리고 평생 그렇게 걸어라."], ["Overhaul the catechism for that advice, and keep it!”", "그 충고는 교리문답서를 샅샅이 뒤져서라도 찾아 명심해 두려무나!\""]], [["The Captain was too busy with his own thoughts of Solomon Gills, mingled perhaps with some reflections on his late escape from Mrs MacStinger, to offer any further quotations on the way for Walter’s moral improvement They interchanged no other word until they arrived at old Sol’s door, where the unfortunate wooden Midshipman, with his instrument at his eye, seemed to be surveying the whole horizon in search of some friend to help him out of his difficulty.", "선장은 솔로몬 길스에 대한 제 생각으로—거기에 어쩌면 방금 맥스팅어 부인에게서 벗어난 데 대한 얼마간의 회상이 뒤섞여 있었으리라—너무 바빠서, 가는 길에 월터의 도덕적 향상을 위한 인용구를 더 내놓지는 못했다. 두 사람은 노 솔의 집 문 앞에 이를 때까지 더는 한마디도 주고받지 않았다. 거기서는 그 불운한 나무 사관후보생이 기구를 눈에 댄 채, 곤경에서 자기를 구해 줄 어떤 친구라도 찾으려는 듯 온 지평선을 살피고 있는 것 같았다."]], [["“Gills!”", "\"길스!\""], ["said the Captain, hurrying into the back parlour, and taking him by the hand quite tenderly.", "선장이 뒷방으로 황급히 들어가 자못 다정하게 그의 손을 잡으며 말했다."], ["“Lay your head well to the wind, and we’ll fight through it.", "\"고개를 단단히 바람 쪽으로 향하게나, 그러면 우리가 이 난관을 뚫고 나갈 거야."], ["All you’ve got to do,”", "자네가 할 일은,\""], ["said the Captain, with the solemnity of a man who was delivering himself of one of the most precious practical tenets ever discovered by human wisdom, “is to lay your head well to the wind, and we’ll fight through it!”", "선장은 인간의 지혜가 여태껏 발견한 가장 귀중한 실천 신조 가운데 하나를 토해 내는 사람의 엄숙함으로 말했다, \"그저 고개를 단단히 바람 쪽으로 향하는 것뿐이야, 그러면 우리가 이 난관을 뚫고 나갈 테니까!\""]], [["Old Sol returned the pressure of his hand, and thanked him.", "노 솔은 그의 손길에 마주 힘을 주며 고마워했다."]], [["Captain Cuttle, then, with a gravity suitable to the nature of the occasion, put down upon the table the two tea-spoons and the sugar-tongs, the silver watch, and the ready money; and asked Mr Brogley, the broker, what the damage was.", "그러고 나서 커틀 선장은, 사안의 성격에 걸맞은 엄숙함으로, 찻숟가락 두 개와 설탕 집게, 은시계, 그리고 현금을 탁자 위에 내려놓고는, 중개인 브로글리 씨에게 손해액이 얼마냐고 물었다."]], [["“Come!", "\"자!"], ["What do you make of it?”", "얼마로 보시오?\""], ["said Captain Cuttle.", "커틀 선장이 말했다."]], [["“Why, Lord help you!”", "\"원, 이거 참!\""], ["returned the broker; “you don’t suppose that property’s of any use, do you?”", "중개인이 대답했다. \"설마 그 재산이 무슨 쓸모가 있다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 아니라는 거요?\""], ["inquired the Captain.", "선장이 물었다."]], [["“Why?", "\"왜냐고요?"], ["The amount’s three hundred and seventy, odd,”", "금액이 삼백칠십몇 파운드란 말이오,\""], ["replied the broker.", "중개인이 대답했다."]], [["“Never mind,”", "\"상관없소,\" 선장이 응수했다."], ["returned the Captain, though he was evidently dismayed by the figures: “all’s fish that comes to your net, I suppose?”", "다만 그 숫자에 적잖이 당황한 기색이 역력했다. \"당신 그물에 걸려드는 거라면 뭐든 다 고기 아니겠소?\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론이지요,\""], ["said Mr Brogley.", "브로글리 씨가 말했다."], ["“But sprats ain’t whales, you know.”", "\"하지만 정어리가 고래는 아니잖소, 아시다시피.\""]], [["The philosophy of this observation seemed to strike the Captain.", "이 말의 철학이 선장의 마음을 친 모양이었다."], ["He ruminated for a minute; eyeing the broker, meanwhile, as a deep genius; and then called the Instrument-maker aside.", "그는 잠시 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨, 그동안 중개인을 심오한 천재라도 되는 양 바라보았다. 그러더니 기구 제조공을 한쪽으로 불러냈다."]], [["“Gills,”", "\"길스,\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, “what’s the bearings of this business?", "커틀 선장이 말했다, \"이 일의 정황이 어떤 건가?"], ["Who’s the creditor?”", "채권자가 누구야?\""]], [["“Hush!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["returned the old man.", "노인이 대답했다."], ["“Come away.", "\"이리 와."], ["Don’t speak before Wally.", "월리 앞에서는 말하지 말게."], ["It’s a matter of security for Wally’s father—an old bond.", "월리 아버지 보증 건이야—오래된 증서지."], ["I’ve paid a good deal of it, Ned, but the times are so bad with me that I can’t do more just now.", "내가 그동안 많이 갚았네, 네드, 하지만 형편이 너무 어려워서 지금 당장은 더 어쩔 수가 없어."], ["I’ve foreseen it, but I couldn’t help it.", "이렇게 될 줄 짐작은 했지만, 막을 도리가 없었지."], ["Not a word before Wally, for all the world.”", "무슨 일이 있어도 월리 앞에서는 한마디도 말게.\""]], [["“You’ve got some money, haven’t you?”", "\"돈이 좀 있지 않은가?\""], ["whispered the Captain.", "선장이 속삭였다."]], [["“Yes, yes—oh yes—I’ve got some,”", "\"있어, 있고말고—오 그럼—좀 있지,\""], ["returned old Sol, first putting his hands into his empty pockets, and then squeezing his Welsh wig between them, as if he thought he might wring some gold out of it; “but I—the little I have got, isn’t convertible, Ned; it can’t be got at.", "노 솔이 대답하며, 먼저 텅 빈 호주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣었다가, 이어 마치 거기서 금이라도 짜낼 수 있다고 여기는 듯 두 손 사이에 자기 웨일스 가발을 쥐어짰다. \"하지만 내가—가진 그 얼마 안 되는 돈은 현금으로 바꿀 수가 없네, 네드."], ["I have been trying to do something with it for Wally, and I’m old fashioned, and behind the time.", "손댈 수가 없는 거야. 그걸 월리를 위해 뭔가 해 보려고 애써 왔는데, 나는 구식이고 시대에 뒤떨어졌어."], ["It’s here and there, and—and, in short, it’s as good as nowhere,”", "여기저기 흩어져 있고, 그러니까—그러니까, 한마디로 없는 거나 마찬가지야,\""], ["said the old man, looking in bewilderment about him.", "노인이 어쩔 줄 모르고 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["He had so much the air of a half-witted person who had been hiding his money in a variety of places, and had forgotten where, that the Captain followed his eyes, not without a faint hope that he might remember some few hundred pounds concealed up the chimney, or down in the cellar.", "그는 제 돈을 여기저기 갖가지 곳에 숨겨 두었다가 어디에 두었는지 잊어버린, 반쯤 얼이 빠진 사람 같은 분위기를 어찌나 짙게 풍기던지, 선장은 그의 눈길을 따라가며, 혹시 굴뚝 위나 지하실 아래에 감춰 둔 몇백 파운드라도 노인이 기억해 내지 않을까 하는 희미한 기대를 품지 않을 수 없었다."], ["But Solomon Gills knew better than that.", "그러나 솔로몬 길스에게는 그럴 만한 게 없다는 걸 본인이 잘 알았다."]], [["“I’m behind the time altogether, my dear Ned,”", "\"나는 시대에 완전히 뒤떨어졌어, 사랑하는 네드,\""], ["said Sol, in resigned despair, “a long way.", "솔이 체념한 절망 속에서 말했다, \"한참이나 말이야."], ["It’s no use my lagging on so far behind it.", "이렇게까지 멀찍이 뒤처져 비척대는 건 아무 소용이 없어."], ["The stock had better be sold—it’s worth more than this debt—and I had better go and die somewhere, on the balance.", "차라리 재고를 파는 게 낫겠어—이 빚보다는 값이 더 나가니까—그리고 나는 그 차액을 들고 어디 가서 죽는 게 낫겠지."], ["I haven’t any energy left.", "내겐 남은 기력이 없어."], ["I don’t understand things.", "세상일을 모르겠고."], ["This had better be the end of it.", "차라리 이걸로 끝을 보는 게 나아."], ["Let ’em sell the stock and take him down,”", "재고를 팔게 하고, 저 친구도 끌어내리라고 하게,\""], ["said the old man, pointing feebly to the wooden Midshipman, “and let us both be broken up together.”", "노인이 나무 사관후보생을 가리키며 힘없이 말했다, \"우리 둘 다 함께 해체되게 두라고.\""]], [["“And what d’ye mean to do with Wal”r?”", "\"그럼 월터는 어떻게 할 작정인가?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“There, there!", "\"자, 자!"], ["Sit ye down, Gills, sit ye down, and let me think o’ this.", "앉게, 길스, 앉아서, 내가 이 일을 좀 생각해 보게 해 줘."], ["If I warn’t a man on a small annuity, that was large enough till today, I hadn’t need to think of it.", "내가 오늘까지는 넉넉했던 쥐꼬리만 한 연금이나 받는 사람이 아니었다면, 이런 걸 생각할 필요도 없었을 텐데."], ["But you only lay your head well to the wind,”", "하지만 자네는 그저 고개를 단단히 바람 쪽으로 향하기만 하게,\""], ["said the Captain, again administering that unanswerable piece of consolation, “and you’re all right!”", "선장이 그 반박할 수 없는 위로의 말을 다시 한번 일러 주며 말했다, \"그러면 다 잘될 거야!\""]], [["Old Sol thanked him from his heart, and went and laid it against the back parlour fire-place instead.", "노 솔은 진심으로 그에게 고마워하고는, 가서 그 고개를 뒷방 난로 벽에 기대었다."]], [["Captain Cuttle walked up and down the shop for some time, cogitating profoundly, and bringing his bushy black eyebrows to bear so heavily on his nose, like clouds setting on a mountain, that Walter was afraid to offer any interruption to the current of his reflections.", "커틀 선장은 한동안 가게 안을 이리저리 거닐며 깊이 골똘히 궁리했는데, 마치 산 위에 구름이 내려앉듯 그 텁수룩한 검은 눈썹을 코 위로 어찌나 무겁게 모았던지, 월터는 그 사색의 흐름에 감히 끼어들어 방해할 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["Mr Brogley, who was averse to being any constraint upon the party, and who had an ingenious cast of mind, went, softly whistling, among the stock; rattling weather-glasses, shaking compasses as if they were physic, catching up keys with loadstones, looking through telescopes, endeavouring to make himself acquainted with the use of the globes, setting parallel rulers astride on to his nose, and amusing himself with other philosophical transactions.", "일행에게 조금이라도 거북함을 주기 싫어하고, 또 기발한 데가 있는 성미의 브로글리 씨는, 나직이 휘파람을 불며 재고 사이를 돌아다녔다. 기압계를 덜그럭거리고, 나침반을 마치 물약 흔들 듯 흔들어 대고, 자석 달린 열쇠들을 집어 들고, 망원경을 들여다보고, 지구의(地球儀)의 용도에 정통해 보려고 애쓰고, 평행자를 코에 걸터 얹어 보고, 그 밖의 다른 철학적 행위들로 제 흥을 돋우면서 말이다."]], [["“Wal”r!”", "\"월터!\""], ["said the Captain at last.", "마침내 선장이 말했다."], ["“I’ve got it.”", "\"방법을 찾았다.\""]], [["“Have you, Captain Cuttle?”", "\"찾으셨어요, 커틀 선장님?\""], ["cried Walter, with great animation.", "월터가 무척 활기를 띠며 외쳤다."]], [["“Come this way, my lad,”", "\"이리 와 봐라, 얘야,\" 선장이 말했다."], ["said the Captain.", "\"재고가 담보야."], ["“The stock’s the security.", "나도 또 하나의 담보고."], ["I’m another. Your governor’s the man to advance money.”", "돈을 내줄 사람은 네 사장님이지.\""]], [["“Mr Dombey!”", "\"돔비 씨라고요!\""], ["faltered Walter.", "월터가 더듬거렸다."]], [["The Captain nodded gravely.", "선장이 엄숙하게 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“Look at him,”", "\"저 양반을 봐라,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Look at Gills.", "\"길스를 봐."], ["If they was to sell off these things now, he’d die of it.", "지금 저 사람들이 이 물건들을 다 팔아 치운다면, 저 양반은 그 때문에 죽고 말 거야."], ["You know he would.", "너도 그럴 거라는 걸 알지."], ["We mustn’t leave a stone unturned—and there’s a stone for you.”", "우리는 어떤 수단도 빠뜨려선 안 돼—그리고 저기 너한테 한 가지 수단이 있는 거다.\""]], [["“A stone!—Mr Dombey!”", "\"수단이라뇨!—돔비 씨가요!\""], ["faltered Walter.", "월터가 더듬거렸다."]], [["“You run round to the office, first of all, and see if he’s there,”", "\"우선 사무소로 달려가서, 그분이 거기 계신지 보고 와라,\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, clapping him on the back.", "커틀 선장이 그의 등을 탁 치며 말했다."], ["“Quick!”", "\"어서!\""]], [["Walter felt he must not dispute the command—a glance at his Uncle would have determined him if he had felt otherwise—and disappeared to execute it.", "월터는 그 명령에 토를 달아서는 안 된다고 느꼈고—설령 달리 느꼈다 하더라도 외삼촌을 한번 흘끗 보았으면 마음을 굳혔으리라—그것을 이행하러 사라졌다."], ["He soon returned, out of breath, to say that Mr Dombey was not there.", "그는 곧 숨을 헐떡이며 돌아와, 돔비 씨가 거기 안 계시다고 말했다."], ["It was Saturday, and he had gone to Brighton.", "토요일이라, 그분은 브라이턴에 가셨다는 것이었다."]], [["“I tell you what, Wal”r!”", "\"내가 너한테 말해 주마, 월터!\""], ["said the Captain, who seemed to have prepared himself for this contingency in his absence.", "선장이 말했는데, 그는 월터가 없는 동안 이런 만일의 사태에 대비해 둔 듯했다."], ["“We’ll go to Brighton.", "\"우리가 브라이턴으로 가는 거야."], ["I’ll back you, my boy.", "내가 너를 후원해 주마, 얘야."], ["I’ll back you, Wal”r.", "내가 너를 후원해 줄게, 월터."], ["We’ll go to Brighton by the afternoon’s coach.”", "오후 마차로 브라이턴에 가는 거다.\""]], [["If the application must be made to Mr Dombey at all, which was awful to think of, Walter felt that he would rather prefer it alone and unassisted, than backed by the personal influence of Captain Cuttle, to which he hardly thought Mr Dombey would attach much weight.", "도무지 생각만 해도 끔찍한 일이지만, 굳이 돔비 씨에게 청을 넣어야 한다면, 월터로서는 커틀 선장의 개인적 영향력을 등에 업기보다—그런 것에 돔비 씨가 별 무게를 두리라고는 도무지 생각되지 않았으니—차라리 혼자 아무 도움 없이 청하는 편이 더 낫다고 느꼈다."], ["But as the Captain appeared to be of quite another opinion, and was bent upon it, and as his friendship was too zealous and serious to be trifled with by one so much younger than himself, he forbore to hint the least objection.", "그러나 선장은 전혀 다른 의견인 데다 거기에 단단히 마음을 굳히고 있었고, 또 그의 우정이 자기보다 한참 어린 사람이 함부로 가볍게 여길 수 없을 만큼 열렬하고 진지했던 까닭에, 월터는 조금의 이의도 비치지 않았다."], ["Cuttle, therefore, taking a hurried leave of Solomon Gills, and returning the ready money, the teaspoons, the sugar-tongs, and the silver watch, to his pocket—with a view, as Walter thought, with horror, to making a gorgeous impression on Mr Dombey—bore him off to the coach-office, without a minute’s delay, and repeatedly assured him, on the road, that he would stick by him to the last.", "그리하여 커틀은 솔로몬 길스에게 황급히 작별을 고하고, 현금이며 찻숟가락이며 설탕 집게며 은시계를 다시 호주머니에 집어넣고는—월터가 섬뜩하게 짐작하기로는, 돔비 씨에게 으리으리한 인상을 주려는 속셈이었다—단 일 분도 지체 없이 그를 마차 사무소로 데려갔고, 길에서 거듭, 끝까지 그의 곁을 지키겠노라고 다짐했다."]], [["# CHAPTER X. Containing the Sequel of the Midshipman’s Disaster", "# CHAPTER X. Containing the Sequel of the Midshipman’s Disaster"]], [["Major Bagstock, after long and frequent observation of Paul, across Princess’s Place, through his double-barrelled opera-glass; and after receiving many minute reports, daily, weekly, and monthly, on that subject, from the native who kept himself in constant communication with Miss Tox’s maid for that purpose; came to the conclusion that Dombey, Sir, was a man to be known, and that J.", "배그스톡 소령은 프린세스 플레이스 건너편에서 쌍안 오페라글라스로 폴을 오래도록 자주 관찰한 끝에, 그리고 그 목적으로 톡스 양의 하녀와 끊임없이 연락을 주고받아 온 원주민 하인에게서 그 일에 관해 날마다, 주마다, 달마다 시시콜콜한 보고를 받은 끝에, 돔비라는 사람은 알아 둘 만한 인물이며, 그와 친분을 트기에 J."], ["B. was the boy to make his acquaintance.", "B.야말로 적임자라는 결론에 이르렀다."]], [["Miss Tox, however, maintaining her reserved behaviour, and frigidly declining to understand the Major whenever he called (which he often did) on any little fishing excursion connected with this project, the Major, in spite of his constitutional toughness and slyness, was fain to leave the accomplishment of his desire in some measure to chance, “which,”", "그러나 톡스 양은 줄곧 조심스러운 태도를 지키며, 소령이 이 계획과 관련하여 슬쩍 떠보려고 찾아올 때마다(그는 자주 찾아왔다) 무슨 말인지 모르겠다는 듯 쌀쌀맞게 시치미를 떼었으므로, 소령은 타고난 끈질김과 교활함에도 불구하고 자기 소원의 성취를 어느 정도 운에 맡길 수밖에 없었다. \"그 운이라는 것이,\""], ["as he was used to observe with chuckles at his club, “has been fifty to one in favour of Joey B., Sir, ever since his elder brother died of Yellow Jack in the West Indies.”", "그가 클럽에서 낄낄거리며 곧잘 하던 말마따나, \"이 조이 B.에게는, 형님이 서인도제도에서 황열병으로 죽은 이래로, 줄곧 오십 대 일로 유리하게 작용해 왔다 이 말씀이오.\""]], [["It was some time coming to his aid in the present instance, but it befriended him at last.", "이번 경우에는 그 운이 그를 도우러 오기까지 다소 시간이 걸렸지만, 결국에는 그의 편이 되어 주었다. 검은 하인이 자세한 사정과 함께 톡스 양이 브라이턴 일로 자리를 비웠다고 보고하자, 소령은 문득 벵골에 있는 자기 친구 빌 비더스톤에 대한 애틋한 회상에 사로잡혔다."], ["When the dark servant, with full particulars, reported Miss Tox absent on Brighton service, the Major was suddenly touched with affectionate reminiscences of his friend Bill Bitherstone of Bengal, who had written to ask him, if he ever went that way, to bestow a call upon his only son.", "그 친구는 혹시 그쪽으로 가게 되거든 자기 외아들을 한번 들여다봐 달라고 편지로 부탁해 온 적이 있었던 것이다. 그런데 그 똑같은 검은 하인이 폴이 핍친 부인 댁에 있다고 보고하자, 소령은—비더스톤 도련님이 영국에 도착했을 때 가져온, 그러나 그가 조금이라도 신경 쓸 생각은 추호도 없었던 그 편지를 떠올리며—눈앞에 펼쳐진 기회를 알아보았다."], ["But when the same dark servant reported Paul at Mrs Pipchin’s, and the Major, referring to the letter favoured by Master Bitherstone on his arrival in England—to which he had never had the least idea of paying any attention—saw the opening that presented itself, he was made so rabid by the gout, with which he happened to be then laid up, that he threw a footstool at the dark servant in return for his intelligence, and swore he would be the death of the rascal before he had done with him: which the dark servant was more than half disposed to believe.", "그러자 마침 통풍으로 몸져누워 있던 그는 어찌나 광분했던지, 그 소식을 전한 대가로 검은 하인에게 발판을 집어 던지고는, 이 일이 끝나기 전에 그 악당 놈을 죽여 버리고 말겠다고 욕설을 퍼부었다. 검은 하인은 그 말을 반쯤은 진심으로 믿어 두는 편이었다."]], [["At length the Major being released from his fit, went one Saturday growling down to Brighton, with the native behind him; apostrophizing Miss Tox all the way, and gloating over the prospect of carrying by storm the distinguished friend to whom she attached so much mystery, and for whom she had deserted him.", "마침내 발작에서 풀려난 소령은 어느 토요일, 뒤에 원주민 하인을 거느린 채 으르렁거리며 브라이턴으로 내려갔다. 가는 내내 톡스 양에게 욕을 퍼부으면서, 그녀가 그토록 신비에 싸 두고 또 그를 위해 자기를 버리기까지 한 그 고귀한 친구라는 자를 기습적으로 함락시킬 전망에 흐뭇해했다."]], [["“Would you, Ma’am, would you!”", "\"어디 한번, 부인, 어디 한번!\""], ["said the Major, straining with vindictiveness, and swelling every already swollen vein in his head.", "소령이 복수심으로 안간힘을 쓰며, 이미 부풀어 오른 머리의 핏줄을 모조리 더 부풀리며 말했다."], ["“Would you give Joey B. the go-by, Ma’am?", "\"이 조이 B.를 따돌려 보시겠다 이 말씀이오, 부인?"], ["Not yet, Ma’am, not yet!", "아직은 안 되지, 부인, 아직은 안 돼!"], ["Damme, not yet, Sir.", "빌어먹을, 아직은 안 되고말고."], ["Joe is awake, Ma’am.", "조는 멀쩡히 깨어 있소, 부인."], ["Bagstock is alive, Sir.", "배그스톡은 시퍼렇게 살아 있고."], ["J.", "J."], ["B. knows a move or two, Ma’am.", "B.는 수 한두 가지는 알고 있단 말이오, 부인."], ["Josh has his weather-eye open, Sir.", "조시는 빈틈없이 망을 보고 있소."], ["You’ll find him tough, Ma’am.", "부인은 그가 만만치 않다는 걸 알게 될 거요."], ["Tough, Sir, tough is Joseph.", "만만치 않지, 조지프는 만만치 않은 사람이오."], ["Tough, and de-vilish sly!”", "만만치 않고, 게다가 지독히도 교활하지!\""]], [["And very tough indeed Master Bitherstone found him, when he took that young gentleman out for a walk.", "그리고 비더스톤 도련님은 소령이 그 어린 신사를 산책에 데리고 나갔을 때, 그가 과연 지독히도 만만치 않은 사람임을 알게 되었다."], ["But the Major, with his complexion like a Stilton cheese, and his eyes like a prawn’s, went roving about, perfectly indifferent to Master Bitherstone’s amusement, and dragging Master Bitherstone along, while he looked about him high and low, for Mr Dombey and his children.", "그러나 소령은 스틸턴 치즈 같은 안색에 새우 같은 눈을 하고서, 비더스톤 도련님이 재미있어하든 말든 전혀 아랑곳하지 않은 채 이리저리 쏘다니며, 위아래로 사방을 두리번거리며 돔비 씨와 그 아이들을 찾느라 비더스톤 도련님을 질질 끌고 다녔다."]], [["In good time the Major, previously instructed by Mrs Pipchin, spied out Paul and Florence, and bore down upon them; there being a stately gentleman (Mr Dombey, doubtless) in their company.", "때맞춰 소령은, 미리 핍친 부인에게서 귀띔을 받아 둔 터라, 폴과 플로렌스를 발견하고 그들에게로 들이닥쳤다. 그들과 함께 위엄 있는 신사 한 분(분명 돔비 씨였다)이 있었다."], ["Charging with Master Bitherstone into the very heart of the little squadron, it fell out, of course, that Master Bitherstone spoke to his fellow-sufferers.", "비더스톤 도련님을 앞세우고 그 작은 무리의 한복판으로 돌격해 들어가니, 당연한 일로 비더스톤 도련님이 같은 처지의 동무들에게 말을 걸게 되었다."], ["Upon that the Major stopped to notice and admire them; remembered with amazement that he had seen and spoken to them at his friend Miss Tox’s in Princess’s Place; opined that Paul was a devilish fine fellow, and his own little friend; inquired if he remembered Joey B.", "그러자 소령은 걸음을 멈추고 그 아이들을 알은체하며 감탄하더니, 프린세스 플레이스의 친구 톡스 양 댁에서 그들을 보고 말을 나눈 적이 있다는 것을 깜짝 놀란 듯 떠올렸다. 그러고는 폴이 지독히도 멋진 녀석이며 자기의 어린 친구라고 단언하고, 소령인 이 조이 B.를 기억하느냐고 물었다."], ["the Major; and finally, with a sudden recollection of the conventionalities of life, turned and apologised to Mr Dombey.", "그러더니 마침내, 세상의 격식이라는 것이 문득 떠오른 듯, 돔비 씨를 향해 돌아서서 사과했다."]], [["“But my little friend here, Sir,”", "\"하지만 여기 이 어린 친구가 말입니다, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “makes a boy of me again: An old soldier, Sir—Major Bagstock, at your service—is not ashamed to confess it.”", "소령이 말했다, \"저를 다시 소년으로 만들어 놓는군요. 늙은 군인이—저는 배그스톡 소령이라 합니다, 잘 부탁드립니다—그걸 고백하기를 부끄러워하지 않습니다.\""], ["Here the Major lifted his hat.", "여기서 소령은 모자를 벗어 들었다."], ["“Damme, Sir,”", "\"빌어먹을, 선생,\""], ["cried the Major with sudden warmth, “I envy you.”", "소령이 갑작스러운 열기를 띠며 외쳤다, \"저는 선생이 부럽습니다.\""], ["Then he recollected himself, and added, “Excuse my freedom.”", "그러더니 정신을 차리고 덧붙였다. \"제 무례를 용서하십시오.\""]], [["Mr Dombey begged he wouldn’t mention it.", "돔비 씨는 그런 말씀은 마시라고 청했다."]], [["“An old campaigner, Sir,”", "\"고참병이,\""], ["said the Major, “a smoke-dried, sun-burnt, used-up, invalided old dog of a Major, Sir, was not afraid of being condemned for his whim by a man like Mr Dombey.", "소령이 말했다, \"연기에 그을리고 햇볕에 그을린, 다 닳아빠진, 폐물이 다 된 늙은 개 같은 소령이 말입니다, 선생, 돔비 씨 같은 분에게 제 변덕을 두고 책망을 받을까 두려워하지는 않습니다."], ["I have the honour of addressing Mr Dombey, I believe?”", "제가 지금 돔비 씨를 마주하고 있는 영광을 누리는 것이라 믿습니다만?\""]], [["“I am the present unworthy representative of that name, Major,”", "\"제가 그 이름의, 지금으로서는, 변변치 못한 대표자올시다, 소령,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 응수했다."]], [["“By G—, Sir!”", "\"맙소사, 선생!\""], ["said the Major, “it’s a great name.", "소령이 말했다, \"그건 위대한 이름입니다."], ["It’s a name, Sir,”", "그건,\""], ["said the Major firmly, as if he defied Mr Dombey to contradict him, and would feel it his painful duty to bully him if he did, “that is known and honoured in the British possessions abroad.", "소령이 마치 돔비 씨더러 반박해 보라고 도전하듯, 그리고 만약 반박한다면 그를 윽박지르는 것이 자기의 괴로운 의무라고 여기기라도 하듯 단호하게 말했다, \"선생, 해외의 영국 영토에서 익히 알려지고 존경받는 이름입니다."], ["It is a name, Sir, that a man is proud to recognise.", "사람이 알아본다는 것만으로도 자랑스러워할 만한 이름이지요."], ["There is nothing adulatory in Joseph Bagstock, Sir.", "이 조지프 배그스톡에게는 아첨이라는 것이 없습니다, 선생."], ["His Royal Highness the Duke of York observed on more than one occasion, ‘there is no adulation in Joey.", "요크 공작 전하께서도 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번 말씀하셨지요, '조이에게는 아첨이라는 게 없다."], ["He is a plain old soldier is Joe.", "조는 꾸밈없는 늙은 군인이다."], ["He is tough to a fault is Joseph:’ but it’s a great name, Sir.", "조지프는 흠이라 할 만큼 만만치 않은 사람이다'라고요. 하지만 그건 위대한 이름입니다, 선생."], ["By the Lord, it’s a great name!”", "맹세코, 위대한 이름이지요!\""], ["said the Major, solemnly.", "소령이 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“You are good enough to rate it higher than it deserves, perhaps, Major,”", "\"아마도 소령께서는 그 이름을 분에 넘치게 높이 쳐 주시는군요,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 응수했다."]], [["“No, Sir,”", "\"아닙니다, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, in a severe tone.", "소령이 준엄한 어조로 말했다."], ["No, Mr Dombey, let us understand each other.", "\"아닙니다, 돔비 씨, 우리 서로를 분명히 알고 지냅시다."], ["That is not the Bagstock vein, Sir.", "그건 배그스톡의 본색이 아닙니다, 선생."], ["You don’t know Joseph B.", "선생께서는 이 조지프 B.를 모르십니다."], ["He is a blunt old blade is Josh.", "조시는 무뚝뚝한 늙은 칼날 같은 사람입니다."], ["No flattery in him, Sir.", "그에게는 아첨이라는 게 없습니다, 선생."], ["Nothing like it.”", "그런 건 조금도 없지요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey inclined his head, and said he believed him to be in earnest, and that his high opinion was gratifying.", "돔비 씨는 고개를 숙이고는, 그가 진심인 것으로 믿으며 그처럼 높이 평가해 주니 흐뭇하다고 말했다."]], [["“My little friend here, Sir,”", "\"여기 이 어린 친구가 말입니다, 선생,\""], ["croaked the Major, looking as amiably as he could, on Paul, “will certify for Joseph Bagstock that he is a thorough-going, down-right, plain-spoken, old Trump, Sir, and nothing more.", "소령이 폴을 향해 최대한 상냥한 표정을 지어 보이며 쉰 목소리로 말했다, \"이 조지프 배그스톡이 철두철미하고, 솔직담백하고, 거침없이 말하는 늙은 호인이라는 것을, 그 이상도 이하도 아니라는 것을 증언해 줄 겁니다, 선생."], ["That boy, Sir,”", "저 아이는요, 선생,\""], ["said the Major in a lower tone, “will live in history.", "소령이 목소리를 낮추어 말했다, \"역사에 길이 남을 겁니다."], ["That boy, Sir, is not a common production.", "저 아이는 흔해 빠진 인물이 아닙니다."], ["Take care of him, Mr Dombey.”", "잘 보살피십시오, 돔비 씨.\""]], [["Mr Dombey seemed to intimate that he would endeavour to do so.", "돔비 씨는 그러도록 애써 보겠다는 뜻을 넌지시 비치는 듯했다."]], [["“Here is a boy here, Sir,”", "\"여기 또 한 녀석이 있습니다, 선생,\""], ["pursued the Major, confidentially, and giving him a thrust with his cane.", "소령이 은밀하게 말을 이으며, 지팡이로 그 아이를 쿡 찔렀다."], ["“Son of Bitherstone of Bengal.", "\"벵골의 비더스톤의 아들입지요."], ["Bill Bitherstone formerly of ours.", "빌 비더스톤은 한때 우리 부대에 있었습니다."], ["That boy’s father and myself, Sir, were sworn friends.", "저 아이의 아비와 저는, 선생, 맹세한 친구 사이였습니다."], ["Wherever you went, Sir, you heard of nothing but Bill Bitherstone and Joe Bagstock.", "어딜 가든, 선생, 빌 비더스톤과 조 배그스톡 이야기밖에 들리지 않았지요."], ["Am I blind to that boy’s defects?", "제가 저 아이의 결점을 못 본 척하느냐고요?"], ["By no means.", "천만에요."], ["He’s a fool, Sir.”", "저 녀석은 바보입니다, 선생.\""]], [["Mr Dombey glanced at the libelled Master Bitherstone, of whom he knew at least as much as the Major did, and said, in quite a complacent manner, “Really?”", "돔비 씨는 적어도 소령만큼은 그에 대해 알고 있는, 그 비방당한 비더스톤 도련님을 흘끗 보고는, 자못 흡족한 태도로 말했다. \"정말입니까?\""]], [["“That is what he is, sir,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 선생,\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“He’s a fool.", "\"저 녀석은 바보예요."], ["Joe Bagstock never minces matters.", "이 조 배그스톡은 절대 말을 빙빙 돌리지 않습니다."], ["The son of my old friend Bill Bitherstone, of Bengal, is a born fool, Sir.”", "제 옛 친구, 벵골의 빌 비더스톤의 아들놈은 타고난 바보랍니다, 선생.\""], ["Here the Major laughed till he was almost black.", "여기서 소령은 얼굴이 거의 검어지도록 웃어 댔다."], ["“My little friend is destined for a public school, I presume, Mr Dombey?”", "\"제 어린 친구는 퍼블릭스쿨로 보낼 작정이시겠지요, 돔비 씨?\""], ["said the Major when he had recovered.", "소령이 정신을 차리고 나서 말했다."]], [["“I am not quite decided,”", "\"아직 확실히 정하지는 못했습니다,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 응수했다."], ["“I think not.", "\"안 보낼 것 같습니다."], ["He is delicate.”", "이 아이는 몸이 약하거든요.\""]], [["“If he’s delicate, Sir,”", "\"몸이 약하다면, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “you are right.", "소령이 말했다, \"옳은 판단이십니다."], ["None but the tough fellows could live through it, Sir, at Sandhurst.", "샌드허스트에서는 만만치 않은 녀석들만 버텨 낼 수 있습니다, 선생."], ["We put each other to the torture there, Sir.", "거기서 우리는 서로를 고문했지요, 선생."], ["We roasted the new fellows at a slow fire, and hung ’em out of a three pair of stairs window, with their heads downwards.", "새로 들어온 녀석들을 약한 불에 천천히 구웠고, 머리를 거꾸로 한 채 삼층 창문 밖으로 매달아 놓았습니다."], ["Joseph Bagstock, Sir, was held out of the window by the heels of his boots, for thirteen minutes by the college clock.”", "이 조지프 배그스톡도, 선생, 부츠 뒤축에 매달려 학교 시계로 십삼 분 동안 창밖에 걸려 있었답니다.\""]], [["The Major might have appealed to his countenance in corroboration of this story.", "소령은 이 이야기의 입증을 위해 자기 얼굴을 증거로 내세울 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["It certainly looked as if he had hung out a little too long.", "확실히 그 얼굴은 그가 좀 지나치게 오래 매달려 있었던 것처럼 보였으니까."]], [["“But it made us what we were, Sir,”", "\"하지만 그것이 우리를 지금의 우리로 만들었지요, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, settling his shirt frill.", "소령이 셔츠 주름 장식을 매만지며 말했다."], ["“We were iron, Sir, and it forged us.", "\"우리는 무쇠였고, 그것이 우리를 단련시켰습니다."], ["Are you remaining here, Mr Dombey?”", "여기 계속 머무르십니까, 돔비 씨?\""]], [["“I generally come down once a week, Major,”", "\"보통 일주일에 한 번씩 내려옵니다, 소령,\""], ["returned that gentleman.", "그 신사가 응수했다."], ["“I stay at the Bedford.”", "\"베드퍼드에 묵습니다.\""]], [["“I shall have the honour of calling at the Bedford, Sir, if you’ll permit me,”", "\"허락하신다면, 베드퍼드로 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리고 싶습니다, 선생,\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“Joey B., Sir, is not in general a calling man, but Mr Dombey’s is not a common name.", "\"이 조이 B.는, 선생, 대체로 남을 찾아다니는 사람이 아닙니다만, 돔비 씨의 이름은 흔한 이름이 아니니까요."], ["I am much indebted to my little friend, Sir, for the honour of this introduction.”", "이렇게 소개받는 영광을 누리게 해 준 제 어린 친구에게 큰 신세를 졌습니다, 선생.\""]], [["Mr Dombey made a very gracious reply; and Major Bagstock, having patted Paul on the head, and said of Florence that her eyes would play the Devil with the youngsters before long—“and the oldsters too, Sir, if you come to that,”", "돔비 씨는 매우 정중하게 답례했다. 그리고 배그스톡 소령은 폴의 머리를 토닥이고, 플로렌스를 두고는 그 눈이 머지않아 젊은이들을 애먹일 거라고—\"나이 든 사람들도 마찬가지지요, 그건 그렇고요, 선생,\""], ["added the Major, chuckling very much—stirred up Master Bitherstone with his walking-stick, and departed with that young gentleman, at a kind of half-trot; rolling his head and coughing with great dignity, as he staggered away, with his legs very wide asunder.", "하고 소령이 한껏 낄낄거리며 덧붙였다—말한 뒤, 지팡이로 비더스톤 도련님을 쿡 찔러 일으켜 그 어린 신사를 데리고, 일종의 반쯤 종종걸음으로 떠났다. 다리를 한껏 벌린 채 비틀거리며 멀어지는 동안, 머리를 흔들고 자못 위엄 있게 기침을 해 댔다."]], [["In fulfilment of his promise, the Major afterwards called on Mr Dombey; and Mr Dombey, having referred to the army list, afterwards called on the Major.", "소령은 자기 약속을 지켜, 나중에 돔비 씨를 찾아갔다. 그리고 돔비 씨는 육군 명부를 확인해 본 뒤, 나중에 소령을 찾아갔다."], ["Then the Major called at Mr Dombey’s house in town; and came down again, in the same coach as Mr Dombey.", "그러고는 소령이 시내의 돔비 씨 집으로 찾아갔다가, 돔비 씨와 같은 마차를 타고 다시 내려왔다."], ["In short, Mr Dombey and the Major got on uncommonly well together, and uncommonly fast: and Mr Dombey observed of the Major, to his sister, that besides being quite a military man he was really something more, as he had a very admirable idea of the importance of things unconnected with his own profession.", "요컨대 돔비 씨와 소령은 유난히 잘, 그리고 유난히 빠르게 가까워졌다. 그리고 돔비 씨는 누이에게 소령을 두고, 그는 꽤나 군인다운 사람일 뿐만 아니라 실로 그 이상의 무언가가 있는데, 자기 직업과 무관한 일들의 중요성에 대해서도 매우 훌륭한 식견을 지녔기 때문이라고 평했다."]], [["At length Mr Dombey, bringing down Miss Tox and Mrs Chick to see the children, and finding the Major again at Brighton, invited him to dinner at the Bedford, and complimented Miss Tox highly, beforehand, on her neighbour and acquaintance.", "마침내 돔비 씨는 톡스 양과 칙 부인을 데리고 아이들을 보러 내려왔다가, 소령이 또 브라이턴에 있는 것을 알고는, 그를 베드퍼드의 만찬에 초대하고, 그에 앞서 톡스 양에게 그녀의 이웃이자 지인을 두고 높이 칭찬했다."]], [["“My dearest Louisa,”", "\"내 사랑하는 루이자,\""], ["said Miss Tox to Mrs Chick, when they were alone together, on the morning of the appointed day, “if I should seem at all reserved to Major Bagstock, or under any constraint with him, promise me not to notice it.”", "약속한 날 아침, 두 사람만 함께 있을 때 톡스 양이 칙 부인에게 말했다. \"혹시 내가 배그스톡 소령에게 조금이라도 서먹하게 굴거나 어딘가 거북해하는 것처럼 보이거든, 부디 모르는 척해 주겠다고 약속해 줘요.\""]], [["“My dear Lucretia,”", "\"내 사랑하는 루크리셔,\""], ["returned Mrs Chick, “what mystery is involved in this remarkable request?", "칙 부인이 응수했다. \"이 별난 부탁에 무슨 비밀이 숨어 있는 거예요?"], ["I must insist upon knowing.”", "꼭 알아야겠어요.\""]], [["“Since you are resolved to extort a confession from me, Louisa,”", "\"기어이 나한테서 고백을 짜내려 작정했다면, 루이자,\""], ["said Miss Tox instantly, “I have no alternative but to confide to you that the Major has been particular.”", "톡스 양이 곧바로 말했다, \"소령이 각별했다는 걸 털어놓는 수밖에 없겠네요.\""]], [["“Particular!”", "\"각별했다고요!\""], ["repeated Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 따라 했다."]], [["“The Major has long been very particular indeed, my love, in his attentions,”", "\"소령은 그 관심에 있어서 오래도록 정말 각별했어요, 자기,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “occasionally they have been so very marked, that my position has been one of no common difficulty.”", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"이따금 그게 어찌나 노골적이던지, 내 처지가 여간 곤란하지 않았다니까요.\""]], [["“Is he in good circumstances?”", "\"그 사람 형편은 괜찮은가요?\""], ["inquired Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 물었다."]], [["“I have every reason to believe, my dear—indeed I may say I know,”", "\"내가 믿을 만한 근거는 충분해요, 자기—사실 안다고 해도 될 만큼요,\""], ["returned Miss Tox, “that he is wealthy.", "톡스 양이 응수했다, \"그가 부유하다는 걸요."], ["He is truly military, and full of anecdote.", "그는 진정한 군인이고, 일화도 무궁무진해요."], ["I have been informed that his valour, when he was in active service, knew no bounds.", "현역으로 복무할 때 그 용맹함이 끝이 없었다고 들었어요."], ["I am told that he did all sorts of things in the Peninsula, with every description of fire-arm; and in the East and West Indies, my love, I really couldn’t undertake to say what he did not do.”", "이베리아 반도에서는 온갖 종류의 화기를 가지고 별의별 일을 다 했다고 하고요, 자기, 동인도와 서인도에서는 정말이지, 그가 안 한 일이 뭔지 내가 다 말할 수 없을 정도예요.\""]], [["“Very creditable to him indeed,”", "\"정말 그 사람에게는 명예로운 일이네요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “extremely so; and you have given him no encouragement, my dear?”", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"지극히 그렇고말고요. 그래서 자기는 그에게 아무 기대도 갖게 하지 않았어요?\""]], [["“If I were to say, Louisa,”", "\"내가 만약, 루이자,\""], ["replied Miss Tox, with every demonstration of making an effort that rent her soul, “that I never encouraged Major Bagstock slightly, I should not do justice to the friendship which exists between you and me.", "톡스 양이 영혼을 쥐어짜는 듯한 안간힘을 온통 내보이며 대답했다, \"배그스톡 소령에게 조금도 기대를 갖게 하지 않았다고 말한다면, 그건 자기와 나 사이에 존재하는 우정에 떳떳치 못한 일이 될 거예요."], ["It is, perhaps, hardly in the nature of woman to receive such attentions as the Major once lavished upon myself without betraying some sense of obligation.", "한때 소령이 나한테 쏟아부은 그런 관심을, 어떤 의무감도 내비치지 않고 받아넘기는 건 아마 여자의 본성상 거의 불가능한 일이겠지요. 하지만 그건 다 지난 일이에요—먼 옛날 일이지요."], ["But that is past—long past. Between the Major and me there is now a yawning chasm, and I will not feign to give encouragement, Louisa, where I cannot give my heart.", "소령과 나 사이에는 이제 입을 떡 벌린 깊은 골이 가로놓여 있고, 나는 내 마음을 줄 수 없는 곳에서 기대를 갖게 하는 척하지는 않을 거예요, 루이자."], ["My affections,”", "내 애정은요,\""], ["said Miss Tox—“but, Louisa, this is madness!”", "톡스 양이 말했다—\"하지만, 루이자, 이건 미친 짓이에요!\""], ["and departed from the room.", "그러고는 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["All this Mrs Chick communicated to her brother before dinner: and it by no means indisposed Mr Dombey to receive the Major with unwonted cordiality.", "칙 부인은 이 모든 것을 만찬 전에 자기 오라비에게 전했다. 그것은 돔비 씨가 평소답지 않은 따뜻함으로 소령을 맞이하는 데 결코 방해가 되지 않았다."], ["The Major, for his part, was in a state of plethoric satisfaction that knew no bounds: and he coughed, and choked, and chuckled, and gasped, and swelled, until the waiters seemed positively afraid of him.", "소령은 소령대로, 끝을 모르는 충만한 만족의 상태에 빠져 있었다. 그는 기침을 하고, 컥컥거리고, 낄낄거리고, 헐떡이고, 부풀어 올라서, 급기야 급사들이 그를 정말로 무서워하는 듯이 보일 지경이었다."]], [["“Your family monopolises Joe’s light, Sir,”", "\"댁의 가족이 조의 햇빛을 독차지하고 있습니다, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, when he had saluted Miss Tox.", "소령이 톡스 양에게 인사를 마치고 나서 말했다."], ["“Joe lives in darkness.", "\"조는 어둠 속에 살고 있지요."], ["Princess’s Place is changed into Kamschatka in the winter time.", "프린세스 플레이스는 겨울철에 캄차카로 변해 버렸습니다."], ["There is no ray of sun, Sir, for Joey B., now.”", "이제 이 조이 B.에게는 햇살 한 줄기 들지 않아요, 선생.\""]], [["“Miss Tox is good enough to take a great deal of interest in Paul, Major,”", "\"톡스 양은 친절하게도 폴에게 무척 관심을 가져 주십니다, 소령,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey on behalf of that blushing virgin.", "돔비 씨가 얼굴을 붉히는 그 처녀를 대신해 응수했다."]], [["“Damme Sir,”", "\"빌어먹을, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “I’m jealous of my little friend.", "소령이 말했다, \"저는 제 어린 친구가 질투 납니다."], ["I’m pining away Sir.", "저는 시들어 가고 있어요, 선생."], ["The Bagstock breed is degenerating in the forsaken person of old Joe.”", "배그스톡 가문은 이 버림받은 늙은 조라는 인물 속에서 퇴락하고 있습니다.\""], ["And the Major, becoming bluer and bluer and puffing his cheeks further and further over the stiff ridge of his tight cravat, stared at Miss Tox, until his eyes seemed as if he were at that moment being overdone before the slow fire at the military college.", "그러고는 소령은 점점 더 시퍼레지고, 꽉 조인 크라바트의 빳빳한 등성이 위로 두 볼을 점점 더 부풀리며, 마치 그 순간 군사학교의 약한 불에 지나치게 구워지고 있기라도 한 것처럼 눈이 튀어나올 지경으로 톡스 양을 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["Notwithstanding the palpitation of the heart which these allusions occasioned her, they were anything but disagreeable to Miss Tox, as they enabled her to be extremely interesting, and to manifest an occasional incoherence and distraction which she was not at all unwilling to display. The Major gave her abundant opportunities of exhibiting this emotion: being profuse in his complaints, at dinner, of her desertion of him and Princess’s Place: and as he appeared to derive great enjoyment from making them, they all got on very well.", "이런 암시들이 그녀에게 가슴 두근거림을 일으키기는 했지만, 그것들은 톡스 양에게 결코 불쾌하지 않았다. 덕분에 그녀는 매우 흥미로운 사람이 될 수 있었고, 이따금 종잡을 수 없고 정신없어하는 모습을 내보일 수 있었는데, 그녀로서는 그런 모습을 드러내는 것이 전혀 싫지 않았던 것이다. 소령은 그녀에게 이런 감정을 드러낼 기회를 듬뿍 마련해 주었다. 만찬 내내 그가 자기와 프린세스 플레이스를 저버렸다며 늘어놓는 불평이 끊이지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 그런 불평을 늘어놓는 데서 큰 즐거움을 얻는 듯이 보였으므로, 자리는 다들 매우 화기애애하게 흘러갔다."]], [["None the worse on account of the Major taking charge of the whole conversation, and showing as great an appetite in that respect as in regard of the various dainties on the table, among which he may be almost said to have wallowed: greatly to the aggravation of his inflammatory tendencies.", "소령이 대화 전체를 도맡아, 식탁 위의 갖가지 진미에 대해서 그러했던 것 못지않게 그 방면에서도 왕성한 식욕을 보였다고 해서 분위기가 나빠지지는 않았다. 그 진미들로 말하자면 그가 거의 뒹굴다시피 했다고 해도 좋을 정도였으니, 그의 염증성 체질을 크게 악화시켰다."], ["Mr Dombey’s habitual silence and reserve yielding readily to this usurpation, the Major felt that he was coming out and shining: and in the flow of spirits thus engendered, rang such an infinite number of new changes on his own name that he quite astonished himself.", "돔비 씨의 평소 과묵함과 조심성이 이런 침범에 선뜻 자리를 내어 주자, 소령은 자기가 두각을 드러내며 빛나고 있다고 느꼈다. 그리하여 솟구치는 기운에 자기 이름을 가지고 어찌나 끝없이 새로운 변주를 울려 대던지, 스스로도 깜짝 놀랄 지경이었다."], ["In a word, they were all very well pleased.", "한마디로, 다들 매우 흡족해했다."], ["The Major was considered to possess an inexhaustible fund of conversation; and when he took a late farewell, after a long rubber, Mr Dombey again complimented the blushing Miss Tox on her neighbour and acquaintance.", "소령은 무궁무진한 화제의 보고를 지닌 사람으로 여겨졌다. 그리고 그가 긴 카드놀이 한 판을 마친 뒤 늦게야 작별을 고하자, 돔비 씨는 다시 한번 얼굴을 붉히는 톡스 양에게 그녀의 이웃이자 지인을 두고 칭찬을 건넸다."]], [["But all the way home to his own hotel, the Major incessantly said to himself, and of himself, “Sly, Sir—sly, Sir—de-vil-ish sly!”", "그러나 자기 호텔로 돌아오는 내내, 소령은 자기 자신을 향해, 또 자기 자신에 대해 끊임없이 중얼거렸다, \"교활하지, 선생—교활해, 선생—지독히도 교활해!\""], ["And when he got there, sat down in a chair, and fell into a silent fit of laughter, with which he was sometimes seized, and which was always particularly awful.", "그리고 그곳에 도착해서는 의자에 앉아, 이따금 그를 사로잡곤 하는 소리 없는 웃음 발작에 빠져들었는데, 그것은 언제나 유난히 무시무시했다."], ["It held him so long on this occasion that the dark servant, who stood watching him at a distance, but dared not for his life approach, twice or thrice gave him over for lost.", "이번에는 그 발작이 어찌나 오래 그를 붙들고 놓아주지 않던지, 멀찍이 서서 그를 지켜보면서도 죽었다 깨어나도 감히 다가가지는 못하던 검은 하인이 두세 번이나 그가 영영 끝장났다고 단념할 정도였다."], ["His whole form, but especially his face and head, dilated beyond all former experience; and presented to the dark man’s view, nothing but a heaving mass of indigo.", "그의 온몸이, 특히 얼굴과 머리가, 일찍이 없던 정도로 부풀어 올라, 검은 하인의 눈에는 그저 출렁이는 쪽빛 덩어리 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["At length he burst into a violent paroxysm of coughing, and when that was a little better burst into such ejaculations as the following:", "마침내 그는 격렬한 기침 발작을 터뜨렸고, 그것이 좀 가라앉자 다음과 같은 외침들을 쏟아냈다."]], [["“Would you, Ma’am, would you? Mrs Dombey, eh, Ma’am?", "\"어디 한번, 부인, 어디 한번 해 보시지?"], ["I think not, Ma’am.", "돔비 부인이라, 응, 부인?"], ["Not while Joe B.", "어림없지, 부인."], ["can put a spoke in your wheel, Ma’am.", "이 조 B.가 부인의 수레바퀴에 막대기 하나 꽂아 넣을 수 있는 한은 안 되고말고, 부인. 이 J."], ["J. B.“s even with you now, Ma’am.", "B.가 지금 부인과 비긴 셈이오, 부인."], ["He isn’t altogether bowled out, yet, Sir, isn’t Bagstock.", "이 배그스톡이 아직 완전히 나가떨어진 건 아니란 말씀이오, 선생."], ["She’s deep, Sir, deep, but Josh is deeper.", "부인은 속이 깊지, 선생, 깊고말고, 하지만 이 조시는 더 깊단 말이오."], ["Wide awake is old Joe—broad awake, and staring, Sir!”", "이 늙은 조는 멀쩡히 깨어 있소—활짝 깨어, 눈을 부릅뜨고 있다 이 말씀이오, 선생!\""], ["There was no doubt of this last assertion being true, and to a very fearful extent; as it continued to be during the greater part of that night, which the Major chiefly passed in similar exclamations, diversified with fits of coughing and choking that startled the whole house.", "이 마지막 단언이 진실이라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었고, 그것도 매우 무시무시한 정도로 그러했다. 그 밤의 대부분 동안 그것이 계속되었으니, 소령은 그 밤을 주로 비슷한 외침들로 보냈는데, 그 사이사이에 온 집안을 놀라게 하는 기침과 컥컥거리는 발작이 끼어들었다."]], [["It was on the day after this occasion (being Sunday) when, as Mr Dombey, Mrs Chick, and Miss Tox were sitting at breakfast, still eulogising the Major, Florence came running in: her face suffused with a bright colour, and her eyes sparkling joyfully: and cried,", "이 일이 있은 다음 날(일요일이었다), 돔비 씨와 칙 부인과 톡스 양이 아침 식탁에 앉아 여전히 소령을 추켜세우고 있을 때, 플로렌스가 뛰어들어 왔다. 얼굴은 발그레하게 상기되고 눈은 기쁨으로 반짝이며, 그 애가 외쳤다."]], [["“Papa!", "\"아빠!"], ["Papa!", "아빠!"], ["Here’s Walter!", "월터가 왔어요!"], ["and he won’t come in.”", "그런데 안 들어오려고 해요.\""]], [["“Who?”", "\"누구라고?\""], ["cried Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 외쳤다."], ["“What does she mean?", "\"저 애가 무슨 말을 하는 거지?"], ["What is this?”", "이게 무슨 일이야?\""]], [["“Walter, Papa!”", "\"월터요, 아빠!\""], ["said Florence timidly; sensible of having approached the presence with too much familiarity.", "플로렌스가 너무 허물없이 그 앞에 다가왔음을 깨닫고는 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["“Who found me when I was lost.”", "\"제가 길을 잃었을 때 저를 찾아 준 사람이에요.\""]], [["“Does she mean young Gay, Louisa?”", "\"저 애가 게이라는 젊은이를 말하는 거요, 루이자?\""], ["inquired Mr Dombey, knitting his brows.", "돔비 씨가 미간을 찌푸리며 물었다."], ["“Really, this child’s manners have become very boisterous.", "\"정말이지, 이 아이 행실이 아주 거칠어졌군."], ["She cannot mean young Gay, I think.", "게이라는 젊은이를 말하는 건 아닐 텐데."], ["See what it is, will you?”", "무슨 일인지 좀 알아봐 주겠소?\""]], [["Mrs Chick hurried into the passage, and returned with the information that it was young Gay, accompanied by a very strange-looking person; and that young Gay said he would not take the liberty of coming in, hearing Mr Dombey was at breakfast, but would wait until Mr Dombey should signify that he might approach.", "칙 부인이 서둘러 복도로 나갔다가, 그것이 게이라는 젊은이이며 매우 괴상하게 생긴 사람과 함께 왔다는 소식을 가지고 돌아왔다. 그리고 게이라는 젊은이가 말하기를, 돔비 씨가 아침 식사 중이라는 말을 듣고는 함부로 들어가는 무례를 범하지 않겠으며, 돔비 씨가 들어와도 좋다는 뜻을 비칠 때까지 기다리겠다고 했다는 것이었다."]], [["“Tell the boy to come in now,”", "\"이제 그 애더러 들어오라고 하시오,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“Now, Gay, what is the matter?", "\"자, 게이, 무슨 일인가?"], ["Who sent you down here?", "누가 자네를 여기로 보냈나?"], ["Was there nobody else to come?”", "달리 올 사람이 없었나?\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, Sir,”", "\"송구합니다, 선생님,\""], ["returned Walter.", "월터가 응수했다."], ["“I have not been sent.", "\"저는 보내져서 온 것이 아닙니다."], ["I have been so bold as to come on my own account, which I hope you’ll pardon when I mention the cause.", "외람되게도 제 발로 온 것인데, 그 까닭을 말씀드리면 용서해 주시리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["But Mr Dombey, without attending to what he said, was looking impatiently on either side of him (as if he were a pillar in his way) at some object behind.", "그러나 돔비 씨는 그의 말에 귀를 기울이지 않고, 마치 그가 길을 가로막은 기둥이라도 되는 양 그의 양옆으로, 뒤에 있는 어떤 물체를 못마땅하다는 듯 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"저게 뭔가?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“Who is that?", "\"저 사람은 누구지?"], ["I think you have made some mistake in the door, Sir.”", "자네가 문을 잘못 찾아온 모양이군, 선생.\""]], [["“Oh, I’m very sorry to intrude with anyone, Sir,”", "\"오, 누군가와 함께 끼어들어 송구합니다, 선생님,\""], ["cried Walter, hastily: “but this is—this is Captain Cuttle, Sir.”", "월터가 황급히 외쳤다. \"하지만 이분은—이분은 커틀 선장이십니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“Wal”r, my lad,”", "\"월터, 이 사람아,\""], ["observed the Captain in a deep voice: “stand by!”", "선장이 굵은 목소리로 일렀다. \"버티고 서 있게!\""]], [["At the same time the Captain, coming a little further in, brought out his wide suit of blue, his conspicuous shirt-collar, and his knobby nose in full relief, and stood bowing to Mr Dombey, and waving his hook politely to the ladies, with the hard glazed hat in his one hand, and a red equator round his head which it had newly imprinted there.", "이와 동시에 선장은 조금 더 안으로 들어서며, 그 헐렁한 푸른 옷과 눈에 띄는 셔츠 깃과 울퉁불퉁한 코를 환히 드러내고는, 돔비 씨에게 절을 하고 갈고리를 정중히 흔들어 부인들에게 인사했다. 한 손에는 딱딱하고 번들거리는 모자를 들었고, 머리에는 그 모자가 방금 새겨 놓은 붉은 적도가 한 바퀴 둘러져 있었다."]], [["Mr Dombey regarded this phenomenon with amazement and indignation, and seemed by his looks to appeal to Mrs Chick and Miss Tox against it.", "돔비 씨는 이 진기한 광경을 놀라움과 분개로 바라보며, 그 표정으로 칙 부인과 톡스 양에게 항의를 호소하는 듯했다."], ["Little Paul, who had come in after Florence, backed towards Miss Tox as the Captain waved his hook, and stood on the defensive.", "플로렌스를 뒤따라 들어온 어린 폴은, 선장이 갈고리를 흔들자 톡스 양 쪽으로 물러서며 방어 자세를 취했다."]], [["“Now, Gay,”", "\"자, 게이,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“What have you got to say to me?”", "\"나한테 할 말이 무엇인가?\""]], [["Again the Captain observed, as a general opening of the conversation that could not fail to propitiate all parties, “Wal”r, standby!”", "선장은 다시 한번, 모두를 무마하지 않을 수 없는 대화의 전반적인 서두로서 일렀다. \"월터, 버티고 서 있게!\""]], [["“I am afraid, Sir,”", "\"송구합니다만, 선생님,\""], ["began Walter, trembling, and looking down at the ground, “that I take a very great liberty in coming—indeed, I am sure I do.", "월터가 떨면서, 바닥을 내려다보며 입을 열었다, \"제가 이렇게 찾아온 것이 매우 큰 무례인 줄 압니다—정말이지 그런 줄 압니다."], ["I should hardly have had the courage to ask to see you, Sir, even after coming down, I am afraid, if I had not overtaken Miss Dombey, and—”", "여기까지 내려온 뒤에도, 만약 제가 돔비 아가씨를 마주치지 않았다면, 감히 선생님을 뵙겠다고 청할 용기조차 내지 못했을 것입니다, 그리고—\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"음!\""], ["said Mr Dombey, following his eyes as he glanced at the attentive Florence, and frowning unconsciously as she encouraged him with a smile.", "돔비 씨가, 월터가 주의 깊게 듣고 있는 플로렌스를 흘끗 보는 데 따라 시선을 옮기며, 그 애가 미소로 그를 격려하자 자기도 모르게 인상을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["“Go on, if you please.”", "\"계속하게, 어서.\""]], [["“Ay, ay,”", "\"옳지, 옳소,\""], ["observed the Captain, considering it incumbent on him, as a point of good breeding, to support Mr Dombey.", "선장이, 돔비 씨를 거드는 것이 예법상 자기 도리라고 여기고는 일렀다. \"잘 말했어!"], ["“Well said! Go on, Wal”r.”", "계속하게, 월터.\""]], [["Captain Cuttle ought to have been withered by the look which Mr Dombey bestowed upon him in acknowledgment of his patronage.", "커틀 선장은 자기를 후원하는 데 대한 사례로 돔비 씨가 그에게 던진 그 눈길에 위축되어 시들어 마땅했을 것이다."], ["But quite innocent of this, he closed one eye in reply, and gave Mr Dombey to understand, by certain significant motions of his hook, that Walter was a little bashful at first, and might be expected to come out shortly.", "그러나 그런 줄은 전혀 모른 채, 그는 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보이고, 갈고리로 모종의 의미심장한 동작을 해 가며, 월터가 처음에는 좀 수줍어하지만 곧 본색을 드러낼 거라는 뜻을 돔비 씨에게 알렸다."]], [["“It is entirely a private and personal matter, that has brought me here, Sir,”", "\"제가 여기 온 것은 전적으로 사사롭고 개인적인 일 때문입니다, 선생님,\""], ["continued Walter, faltering, “and Captain Cuttle—”", "월터가 더듬거리며 말을 이었다, \"그리고 커틀 선장님께서—\""]], [["“Here!”", "\"여기 있네!\""], ["interposed the Captain, as an assurance that he was at hand, and might be relied upon.", "선장이, 자기가 곁에 있으며 믿어도 좋다는 보증으로서 끼어들었다."]], [["“Who is a very old friend of my poor Uncle’s, and a most excellent man, Sir,”", "\"이분은 제 가엾은 삼촌의 아주 오랜 친구이시고, 더없이 훌륭하신 분인데요, 선생님,\""], ["pursued Walter, raising his eyes with a look of entreaty in the Captain’s behalf, “was so good as to offer to come with me, which I could hardly refuse.”", "월터가 선장을 위하여 간청하는 눈빛으로 눈을 들며 말을 이었다, \"친절하게도 저와 함께 와 주시겠다고 하셨고, 저로서는 차마 거절할 수가 없었습니다.\""]], [["“No, no, no;”", "\"아니, 아니, 아니지,\""], ["observed the Captain complacently.", "선장이 흐뭇하게 일렀다."], ["“Of course not.", "\"거절할 거야 없지."], ["No call for refusing.", "거절할 까닭이 없고말고."], ["Go on, Wal”r.”", "계속하게, 월터.\""]], [["“And therefore, Sir,”", "\"그래서요, 선생님,\""], ["said Walter, venturing to meet Mr Dombey’s eye, and proceeding with better courage in the very desperation of the case, now that there was no avoiding it, “therefore I have come, with him, Sir, to say that my poor old Uncle is in very great affliction and distress.", "월터가 용기를 내어 돔비 씨의 눈을 마주하고는, 이제 피할 수 없게 된 처지의 그 절박함 속에서 한결 나은 용기로 말을 이어 갔다, \"그래서 제가 이분과 함께 찾아온 것은, 제 가엾은 늙은 삼촌이 매우 큰 고난과 곤경에 처해 있다는 말씀을 드리기 위해서입니다."], ["That, through the gradual loss of his business, and not being able to make a payment, the apprehension of which has weighed very heavily upon his mind, months and months, as indeed I know, Sir, he has an execution in his house, and is in danger of losing all he has, and breaking his heart.", "장사가 차츰 기울고, 갚아야 할 돈을 마련하지 못하게 되어—그 걱정이 여러 달 내내 삼촌의 마음을 무겁게 짓눌러 왔는데요, 정말이지 제가 아는 사실입니다, 선생님—집에 차압이 들어와, 가진 것을 모조리 잃고 가슴이 무너질 위기에 놓여 있습니다."], ["And that if you would, in your kindness, and in your old knowledge of him as a respectable man, do anything to help him out of his difficulty, Sir, we never could thank you enough for it.”", "그러니 만약 선생님께서 친절을 베푸시어, 또 삼촌을 존경할 만한 사람으로 오래 알아 오신 정으로, 그 어려움에서 벗어나도록 무엇이든 도와주신다면, 선생님, 저희로서는 아무리 감사를 드려도 모자랄 것입니다.\""]], [["Walter’s eyes filled with tears as he spoke; and so did those of Florence.", "월터는 말을 하면서 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다. 플로렌스의 눈에도 그러했다."], ["Her father saw them glistening, though he appeared to look at Walter only.", "그 애 아버지는 월터만 바라보는 척했지만, 그 눈물이 반짝이는 것을 보았다."]], [["“It is a very large sum, Sir,”", "\"매우 큰 액수입니다, 선생님,\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."], ["“More than three hundred pounds.", "\"삼백 파운드가 넘습니다."], ["My Uncle is quite beaten down by his misfortune, it lies so heavy on him; and is quite unable to do anything for his own relief.", "제 삼촌은 그 불운에 완전히 짓눌려 버렸습니다, 그게 어찌나 무겁게 짓누르는지요. 그래서 자기 자신을 구하기 위해 아무것도 할 수 없는 형편입니다."], ["He doesn’t even know yet, that I have come to speak to you.", "제가 선생님께 말씀드리러 온 것조차 아직 모르고 계십니다."], ["You would wish me to say, Sir,”", "제가 정확히 무엇을 바라는지 말해 주기를 원하시겠지요, 선생님,\""], ["added Walter, after a moment’s hesitation, “exactly what it is I want.", "월터가 잠시 망설인 뒤에 덧붙였다, \"저도 정말 잘 모르겠습니다, 선생님."], ["I really don’t know, Sir. There is my Uncle’s stock, on which I believe I may say, confidently, there are no other demands, and there is Captain Cuttle, who would wish to be security too.", "제 삼촌의 재고품이 있는데, 거기에는 다른 청구가 없다고 자신 있게 말씀드릴 수 있을 것 같습니다, 그리고 여기 커틀 선장님께서도 보증을 서 주시기를 바라고 계십니다."], ["I—I hardly like to mention,”", "저는—제 입으로 말씀드리기는 좀 무엇하지만,\""], ["said Walter, “such earnings as mine; but if you would allow them—accumulate—payment—advance—Uncle—frugal, honourable, old man.”", "월터가 말했다, \"저 같은 사람의 벌이라도, 만약 선생님께서 허락하신다면—쌓아서—상환—선불—삼촌은—검소하고, 정직한, 늙은 분이십니다.\""], ["Walter trailed off, through these broken sentences, into silence: and stood with downcast head, before his employer.", "월터는 이렇게 끊어진 문장들을 헤매다가 침묵 속으로 잦아들었고, 고용주 앞에서 고개를 떨군 채 서 있었다."]], [["Considering this a favourable moment for the display of the valuables, Captain Cuttle advanced to the table; and clearing a space among the breakfast-cups at Mr Dombey’s elbow, produced the silver watch, the ready money, the teaspoons, and the sugar-tongs; and piling them up into a heap that they might look as precious as possible, delivered himself of these words:", "이때가 그 귀중품들을 내보이기에 좋은 순간이라 여기고, 커틀 선장은 식탁으로 다가갔다. 그리고 돔비 씨의 팔꿈치 곁의 아침 식사용 찻잔들 사이로 빈자리를 마련하고는, 은시계와 현금과 찻숟가락과 각설탕 집게를 꺼내 놓았다. 그러고는 그것들을 최대한 값져 보이게 한 무더기로 쌓아 올리고서, 다음과 같은 말을 늘어놓았다."]], [["“Half a loaf’s better than no bread, and the same remark holds good with crumbs.", "\"빵 반 덩이가 빵 없는 것보다 낫지요, 그 말은 빵부스러기에도 똑같이 들어맞습니다."], ["There’s a few.", "여기 몇 점 있습니다."], ["Annuity of one hundred pound premium also ready to be made over.", "연금 백 파운드짜리 증서도 양도할 채비가 되어 있고요."], ["If there is a man chock full of science in the world, it’s old Sol Gills.", "만약 세상에 학식이 가득 찬 사람이 있다면, 그건 이 늙은 솔 길스올시다."], ["If there is a lad of promise—one flowing,”", "만약 장래가 촉망되는 젊은이가 있다면—젖과 꿀이,\""], ["added the Captain, in one of his happy quotations, “with milk and honey—it’s his nevy!”", "선장이 그 절묘한 인용 가운데 하나를 끼워 넣으며 덧붙였다, \"흐르는 젊은이가 있다면, 그건 바로 저 아이의 조카랍니다!\""]], [["The Captain then withdrew to his former place, where he stood arranging his scattered locks with the air of a man who had given the finishing touch to a difficult performance.", "그러고 나서 선장은 마치 어려운 공연에 마무리 손질을 끝낸 사람 같은 태도로 흐트러진 머리칼을 매만지며, 원래 서 있던 자리로 물러났다."]], [["When Walter ceased to speak, Mr Dombey’s eyes were attracted to little Paul, who, seeing his sister hanging down her head and silently weeping in her commiseration for the distress she had heard described, went over to her, and tried to comfort her: looking at Walter and his father as he did so, with a very expressive face.", "월터가 말을 그치자, 돔비 씨의 시선이 어린 폴에게로 쏠렸다. 폴은 누이가 고개를 떨군 채 방금 들은 곤경에 대한 가엾은 마음으로 소리 없이 울고 있는 것을 보고는, 누이에게 다가가 위로하려 애쓰며, 그러면서 매우 의미심장한 얼굴로 월터와 자기 아버지를 바라보고 있었다."], ["After the momentary distraction of Captain Cuttle’s address, which he regarded with lofty indifference, Mr Dombey again turned his eyes upon his son, and sat steadily regarding the child, for some moments, in silence.", "커틀 선장의 연설로 잠시 산만해졌다가—그는 그것을 거만하게 무관심한 태도로 대했다—돔비 씨는 다시 아들에게로 눈길을 돌려, 한동안 말없이 그 아이를 물끄러미 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["“What was this debt contracted for?”", "\"이 빚은 무엇 때문에 진 것인가?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey, at length.", "돔비 씨가 마침내 물었다."], ["“Who is the creditor?”", "\"채권자는 누구인가?\""]], [["“He don’t know,”", "\"이 친구는 모릅니다,\""], ["replied the Captain, putting his hand on Walter’s shoulder.", "선장이 월터의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 대답했다. \"제가 압니다."], ["“I do. It came of helping a man that’s dead now, and that’s cost my friend Gills many a hundred pound already.", "지금은 죽고 없는 사람을 도와주다가 생긴 빚인데, 그 일로 제 친구 길스가 이미 수백 파운드를 날렸지요."], ["More particulars in private, if agreeable.”", "괜찮으시다면 자세한 사정은 따로 말씀드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“People who have enough to do to hold their own way,”", "\"제 앞가림을 해 나가기에도 벅찬 사람들은,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, unobservant of the Captain’s mysterious signs behind Walter, and still looking at his son, “had better be content with their own obligations and difficulties, and not increase them by engaging for other men.", "돔비 씨가, 월터 뒤에서 선장이 보내는 알 수 없는 신호들은 알아채지 못한 채 여전히 아들을 바라보며 말했다, \"남의 빚을 떠맡아 그것을 늘릴 게 아니라, 자기 자신의 채무와 어려움에 만족하는 편이 낫지. 그건 또한 부정직하고 주제넘은 짓이야,\""], ["It is an act of dishonesty and presumption, too,”", "돔비 씨가 준엄하게 말했다. \"큰 주제넘음이지."], ["said Mr Dombey, sternly; “great presumption; for the wealthy could do no more.", "그런 일은 부유한 자나 할 수 있는 거니까."], ["Paul, come here!”", "폴, 이리 오너라!\""]], [["The child obeyed: and Mr Dombey took him on his knee.", "아이는 시키는 대로 했다. 돔비 씨는 그 아이를 무릎에 앉혔다."]], [["“If you had money now—”", "\"네가 지금 돈이 있다면—\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“Look at me!”", "\"날 보아라!\""]], [["Paul, whose eyes had wandered to his sister, and to Walter, looked his father in the face.", "폴은, 누이에게로, 또 월터에게로 헤매던 눈길로, 아버지의 얼굴을 마주 보았다."]], [["“If you had money now,”", "\"네가 지금 돈이 있다면,\""], ["said Mr Dombey; “as much money as young Gay has talked about; what would you do?”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"저 게이라는 젊은이가 말한 만큼의 돈이 있다면, 너는 어떻게 하겠느냐?\""]], [["“Give it to his old Uncle,”", "\"그 늙은 삼촌에게 주겠어요,\""], ["returned Paul.", "폴이 응수했다."]], [["“Lend it to his old Uncle, eh?”", "\"그 늙은 삼촌에게 빌려주겠다는 거지, 응?\""], ["retorted Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 받아넘겼다."], ["“Well!", "\"그래!"], ["When you are old enough, you know, you will share my money, and we shall use it together.”", "네가 다 크면, 알다시피, 너는 내 돈을 함께 나누어 갖게 될 것이고, 우리는 그걸 함께 쓰게 될 게다.\""]], [["“Dombey and Son,”", "\"돔비와 아들이요,\""], ["interrupted Paul, who had been tutored early in the phrase.", "일찍부터 그 문구를 익혀 온 폴이 끼어들었다."]], [["“Dombey and Son,”", "\"돔비와 아들이지,\""], ["repeated his father.", "그 애 아버지가 따라 했다."], ["“Would you like to begin to be Dombey and Son, now, and lend this money to young Gay’s Uncle?”", "\"이제부터 돔비와 아들이 되어, 저 게이라는 젊은이의 삼촌에게 이 돈을 빌려주는 일을 시작해 보고 싶으냐?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["if you please, Papa!”", "그래 주신다면요, 아빠!\""], ["said Paul: “and so would Florence.”", "폴이 말했다. \"그리고 플로렌스도 그러고 싶어 할 거예요.\""]], [["“Girls,”", "\"여자아이들은,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “have nothing to do with Dombey and Son.", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"돔비와 아들과는 아무 상관이 없단다."], ["Would you like it?”", "너는 그러고 싶으냐?\""]], [["“Yes, Papa, yes!”", "\"네, 아빠, 네!\""]], [["“Then you shall do it,”", "\"그러면 네가 그렇게 하려무나,\""], ["returned his father.", "그 애 아버지가 응수했다."], ["“And you see, Paul,”", "\"그리고 너도 알겠지, 폴,\""], ["he added, dropping his voice, “how powerful money is, and how anxious people are to get it.", "그가 목소리를 낮추며 덧붙였다, \"돈이 얼마나 힘이 센지, 그리고 사람들이 그걸 얻으려고 얼마나 안달하는지를 말이다."], ["Young Gay comes all this way to beg for money, and you, who are so grand and great, having got it, are going to let him have it, as a great favour and obligation.”", "저 게이라는 젊은이는 돈을 구걸하러 이 먼 길을 왔고, 그토록 대단하고 위대한 너는, 그 돈을 가졌으니, 큰 호의이자 은혜로서 그가 그것을 갖도록 해 주려는 거란다.\""]], [["Paul turned up the old face for a moment, in which there was a sharp understanding of the reference conveyed in these words: but it was a young and childish face immediately afterwards, when he slipped down from his father’s knee, and ran to tell Florence not to cry any more, for he was going to let young Gay have the money.", "폴은 잠시 그 늙은이 같은 얼굴을 들었는데, 거기에는 이 말 속에 담긴 암시에 대한 예리한 이해가 어려 있었다. 그러나 곧이어, 그 애가 아버지의 무릎에서 미끄러져 내려와 플로렌스에게 더는 울지 말라고, 게이라는 젊은이가 돈을 갖도록 해 줄 거라고 일러 주러 달려갈 때는, 그것은 어리고 천진한 아이의 얼굴이었다."]], [["Mr Dombey then turned to a side-table, and wrote a note and sealed it.", "그러고 나서 돔비 씨는 옆 탁자로 돌아서서 쪽지 한 장을 써서 봉했다."], ["During the interval, Paul and Florence whispered to Walter, and Captain Cuttle beamed on the three, with such aspiring and ineffably presumptuous thoughts as Mr Dombey never could have believed in.", "그동안 폴과 플로렌스는 월터에게 소곤거렸고, 커틀 선장은 돔비 씨로서는 도저히 믿을 수 없을 만큼 야심차고 이루 말할 수 없이 주제넘은 생각에 잠겨 그 세 사람을 향해 흐뭇하게 미소 지었다."], ["The note being finished, Mr Dombey turned round to his former place, and held it out to Walter.", "쪽지를 다 쓰고 나서, 돔비 씨는 원래 자리로 돌아서며 그것을 월터에게 내밀었다."]], [["“Give that,”", "\"이것을,\""], ["he said, “the first thing to-morrow morning, to Mr Carker.", "그가 말했다, \"내일 아침 제일 먼저 카커 씨에게 전하게."], ["He will immediately take care that one of my people releases your Uncle from his present position, by paying the amount at issue; and that such arrangements are made for its repayment as may be consistent with your Uncle’s circumstances.", "그가 즉시 손을 써서, 내 사람 가운데 하나가 문제의 금액을 지불하여 자네 삼촌을 지금의 처지에서 풀어 줄 것이고, 또 자네 삼촌의 형편에 맞도록 그 상환에 관한 합의가 이루어지도록 할 걸세."], ["You will consider that this is done for you by Master Paul.”", "이 일은 폴 도련님이 자네를 위해 해 준 것임을 명심하게.\""]], [["Walter, in the emotion of holding in his hand the means of releasing his good Uncle from his trouble, would have endeavoured to express something of his gratitude and joy.", "월터는 자기의 선량한 삼촌을 곤경에서 구해 낼 수단을 손에 쥔 감격에 겨워, 자기의 감사와 기쁨을 어떻게든 표현하려 했다."], ["But Mr Dombey stopped him short.", "그러나 돔비 씨가 그를 막았다."]], [["“You will consider that it is done,”", "\"이 일은 폴 도련님이 해 준 것임을 명심하게,\""], ["he repeated, “by Master Paul.", "그가 되풀이했다."], ["I have explained that to him, and he understands it.", "\"내가 그 애에게 그렇게 설명했고, 그 애도 그렇게 이해하고 있네."], ["I wish no more to be said.”", "더는 아무 말도 듣고 싶지 않네.\""]], [["As he motioned towards the door, Walter could only bow his head and retire.", "그가 문 쪽으로 손짓하자, 월터는 그저 고개를 숙이고 물러날 수밖에 없었다."], ["Miss Tox, seeing that the Captain appeared about to do the same, interposed.", "톡스 양은 선장도 똑같이 하려는 듯이 보이자, 끼어들었다."]], [["“My dear Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["she said, addressing Mr Dombey, at whose munificence both she and Mrs Chick were shedding tears copiously; “I think you have overlooked something.", "그녀가, 그 후한 인심에 자기와 칙 부인이 함께 눈물을 펑펑 흘리고 있던 돔비 씨를 향해 말했다, \"무언가 빠뜨리신 것 같습니다."], ["Pardon me, Mr Dombey, I think, in the nobility of your character, and its exalted scope, you have omitted a matter of detail.”", "용서하세요, 돔비 씨, 제 생각에는, 선생님의 고귀한 성품과 그 드높은 도량 가운데서, 사소한 한 가지를 빠뜨리신 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Indeed, Miss Tox!”", "\"그래요, 톡스 양!\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“The gentleman with the—Instrument,”", "\"저—기구를 가지신 신사분이,\""], ["pursued Miss Tox, glancing at Captain Cuttle, “has left upon the table, at your elbow—”", "톡스 양이 커틀 선장을 흘끗 보며 말을 이었다, \"선생님 팔꿈치 곁 탁자 위에 놓아두고 가신 것이—\""]], [["“Good Heaven!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["said Mr Dombey, sweeping the Captain’s property from him, as if it were so much crumb indeed.", "돔비 씨가, 마치 그것들이 정말로 빵부스러기라도 되는 양 선장의 소지품을 자기에게서 휙 쓸어내며 말했다."], ["“Take these things away.", "\"이것들을 치우시오."], ["I am obliged to you, Miss Tox; it is like your usual discretion.", "고맙소, 톡스 양. 평소의 그 사려 깊음다우시오."], ["Have the goodness to take these things away, Sir!”", "부디 이것들을 좀 치워 주시오, 선생!\""]], [["Captain Cuttle felt he had no alternative but to comply.", "커틀 선장은 따르는 것 외에 달리 도리가 없다고 느꼈다."], ["But he was so much struck by the magnanimity of Mr Dombey, in refusing treasures lying heaped up to his hand, that when he had deposited the teaspoons and sugar-tongs in one pocket, and the ready money in another, and had lowered the great watch down slowly into its proper vault, he could not refrain from seizing that gentleman’s right hand in his own solitary left, and while he held it open with his powerful fingers, bringing the hook down upon its palm in a transport of admiration.", "그러나 손이 닿는 데 수북이 쌓여 있는 보물을 마다하는 돔비 씨의 그 도량에 어찌나 깊이 감동했던지, 찻숟가락과 각설탕 집게를 한쪽 주머니에, 현금을 다른 쪽 주머니에 넣고, 그 커다란 시계를 제 격납고로 천천히 내려 넣고 나서는, 자기의 외짝 왼손으로 그 신사의 오른손을 덥석 붙잡지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 억센 손가락으로 손바닥을 펴서 잡은 채, 감격에 겨워 그 위로 갈고리를 내리찍었다."], ["At this touch of warm feeling and cold iron, Mr Dombey shivered all over.", "이 따뜻한 감정과 차디찬 쇠붙이의 접촉에, 돔비 씨는 온몸을 부르르 떨었다."]], [["Captain Cuttle then kissed his hook to the ladies several times, with great elegance and gallantry; and having taken a particular leave of Paul and Florence, accompanied Walter out of the room.", "그러고 나서 커틀 선장은 매우 우아하고 정중한 태도로 부인들을 향해 여러 차례 갈고리에 입을 맞추어 인사했다. 그러고는 폴과 플로렌스에게 각별히 작별을 고한 뒤, 월터를 데리고 방을 나갔다."], ["Florence was running after them in the earnestness of her heart, to send some message to old Sol, when Mr Dombey called her back, and bade her stay where she was.", "플로렌스가 진심에서 우러난 마음으로 늙은 솔에게 무슨 말을 전하려고 그들을 뒤쫓아 달려가려는데, 돔비 씨가 그 애를 다시 불러 그 자리에 있으라고 일렀다."]], [["“Will you never be a Dombey, my dear child!”", "\"넌 도무지 돔비답게 되지 않으려는 거니, 얘야!\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with pathetic reproachfulness.", "칙 부인이 애처롭게 나무라듯 말했다."]], [["“Dear aunt,”", "\"고모,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“Don’t be angry with me.", "\"저한테 화내지 마세요."], ["I am so thankful to Papa!”", "저는 아빠가 너무 고마워서 그래요!\""]], [["She would have run and thrown her arms about his neck if she had dared; but as she did not dare, she glanced with thankful eyes towards him, as he sat musing; sometimes bestowing an uneasy glance on her, but, for the most part, watching Paul, who walked about the room with the new-blown dignity of having let young Gay have the money.", "그 애는, 감히 그럴 수만 있었다면, 달려가 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안았을 것이다. 그러나 감히 그러지 못했으므로, 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있는 그를 향해 감사 어린 눈길을 보냈다. 그는 이따금 그 애에게 불편한 눈길을 던졌지만, 대개는 폴을 지켜보고 있었다. 폴은 게이라는 젊은이가 돈을 갖도록 해 주었다는 새로 갓 피어난 위엄을 띠고 방 안을 거닐고 있었다."]], [["And young Gay—Walter—what of him?", "그러면 게이라는 젊은이는—월터는—그는 어찌 되었을까?"]], [["He was overjoyed to purge the old man’s hearth from bailiffs and brokers, and to hurry back to his Uncle with the good tidings.", "그는 늙은이의 화롯가에서 집행관과 중개인들을 몰아내게 된 것에, 그리고 그 기쁜 소식을 가지고 삼촌에게 서둘러 돌아갈 수 있게 된 것에 뛸 듯이 기뻤다."], ["He was overjoyed to have it all arranged and settled next day before noon; and to sit down at evening in the little back parlour with old Sol and Captain Cuttle; and to see the Instrument-maker already reviving, and hopeful for the future, and feeling that the wooden Midshipman was his own again.", "다음 날 정오 전에 모든 일이 매듭지어지고 정리된 것에, 그리고 저녁이면 작은 뒷거실에 늙은 솔과 커틀 선장과 함께 앉아, 그 기구 제작자가 벌써 생기를 되찾아 앞날에 희망을 품고, 나무로 된 항해사 견습생이 다시 자기 것이 되었음을 느끼는 모습을 보게 된 것에 뛸 듯이 기뻤다."], ["But without the least impeachment of his gratitude to Mr Dombey, it must be confessed that Walter was humbled and cast down.", "그러나 돔비 씨에 대한 그의 감사를 조금도 의심할 바 없으면서도, 월터가 풀이 죽고 의기소침해졌다는 것은 인정해야 한다."], ["It is when our budding hopes are nipped beyond recovery by some rough wind, that we are the most disposed to picture to ourselves what flowers they might have borne, if they had flourished; and now, when Walter found himself cut off from that great Dombey height, by the depth of a new and terrible tumble, and felt that all his old wild fancies had been scattered to the winds in the fall, he began to suspect that they might have led him on to harmless visions of aspiring to Florence in the remote distance of time.", "우리가 갓 피어나려던 희망이 어떤 거친 바람에 회복할 수 없을 만큼 꺾여 버렸을 때, 만약 그것이 무성히 자랐더라면 어떤 꽃을 피웠을지 그려 보는 데 가장 마음이 기우는 법이다. 그리고 이제, 월터는 새롭고 끔찍한 추락의 깊이만큼 그 위대한 돔비의 높이로부터 동떨어져 있음을 깨닫고, 자기의 그 모든 옛 부질없는 공상이 그 추락 속에서 바람에 흩어져 버렸음을 느끼면서, 그 공상들이 자기를 이끌어, 까마득히 먼 훗날 플로렌스를 사모해 보겠다는 무해한 환상으로 데려갔을지도 모른다는 것을 어렴풋이 의심하기 시작했다."]], [["The Captain viewed the subject in quite a different light.", "선장은 이 일을 전혀 다른 시각으로 보았다."], ["He appeared to entertain a belief that the interview at which he had assisted was so very satisfactory and encouraging, as to be only a step or two removed from a regular betrothal of Florence to Walter; and that the late transaction had immensely forwarded, if not thoroughly established, the Whittingtonian hopes.", "그는 자기가 거든 그 면담이 매우 흡족하고 고무적이어서, 플로렌스를 월터에게 정식으로 약혼시키는 데서 한두 걸음밖에 떨어져 있지 않다고 믿는 듯했다. 또 이번 일이 그 휘팅턴식 희망을 완전히 굳히지는 못했더라도 엄청나게 진척시켰다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["Stimulated by this conviction, and by the improvement in the spirits of his old friend, and by his own consequent gaiety, he even attempted, in favouring them with the ballad of “Lovely Peg” for the third time in one evening, to make an extemporaneous substitution of the name “Florence;”", "이런 확신에, 그리고 자기 옛 친구의 기운이 살아난 것에, 또 그로 인한 자기 자신의 흥겨움에 들떠, 그는 심지어, 그날 저녁 세 번째로 그들에게 \"어여쁜 페그\"라는 발라드를 들려주는 자리에서, 즉흥적으로 \"플로렌스\"라는 이름으로 바꿔 부르려고 시도하기까지 했다."], ["but finding this difficult, on account of the word Peg invariably rhyming to leg (in which personal beauty the original was described as having excelled all competitors), he hit upon the happy thought of changing it to Fle-e-eg; which he accordingly did, with an archness almost supernatural, and a voice quite vociferous, notwithstanding that the time was close at hand when he must seek the abode of the dreadful Mrs MacStinger.", "그러나 페그라는 단어가 으레 레그(다리)와 운이 맞는지라(원래 노래의 주인공은 그 신체적 아름다움에 있어 모든 경쟁자를 능가했다고 묘사되어 있었다) 이것이 어렵다는 것을 알고, 그것을 플레-에-에그로 바꾸는 절묘한 묘안을 떠올렸다. 그리하여 그는, 머지않아 그 무시무시한 맥스팅거 부인의 거처를 찾아가야 할 시각이 코앞에 다가와 있었음에도 불구하고, 거의 초자연적이라 할 만한 익살과 자못 우렁찬 목소리로 그렇게 불러 댔다."]], [["That same evening the Major was diffuse at his club, on the subject of his friend Dombey in the City.", "바로 그날 저녁, 소령은 자기 클럽에서 시내의 친구 돔비를 화제로 장광설을 늘어놓았다."], ["“Damme, Sir,”", "\"빌어먹을, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “he’s a prince, is my friend Dombey in the City.", "소령이 말했다, \"그는 왕족이라니까요, 시내의 내 친구 돔비는요."], ["I tell you what, Sir.", "내 말 잘 들어 보시오, 선생."], ["If you had a few more men among you like old Joe Bagstock and my friend Dombey in the City, Sir, you’d do!”", "만약 당신들 가운데 이 늙은 조 배그스톡이나 시내의 내 친구 돔비 같은 사람이 몇 명만 더 있었더라면, 선생, 제법 쓸 만했을 거란 말이오!\""]], [["# CHAPTER XI. Paul’s Introduction to a New Scene", "# CHAPTER XI. Paul’s Introduction to a New Scene"]], [["Mrs Pipchin’s constitution was made of such hard metal, in spite of its liability to the fleshly weaknesses of standing in need of repose after chops, and of requiring to be coaxed to sleep by the soporific agency of sweet-breads, that it utterly set at naught the predictions of Mrs Wickam, and showed no symptoms of decline.", "핍친 부인의 체질은, 갈비 요리를 먹은 뒤 휴식을 필요로 하고 췌장 요리라는 수면제의 도움 없이는 잠을 청하기 어려운 육체적 약점이 있기는 했으나, 그토록 단단한 금속으로 빚어진 터라 위컴 부인의 예언을 깡그리 무시한 채 쇠약의 기미라고는 도무지 보이지 않았다."], ["Yet, as Paul’s rapt interest in the old lady continued unbated, Mrs Wickam would not budge an inch from the position she had taken up.", "그럼에도 폴이 그 노부인에게 품은 넋 나간 듯한 관심이 조금도 수그러들지 않았으므로, 위컴 부인은 자기가 잡은 입장에서 한 치도 물러서지 않았다."], ["Fortifying and entrenching herself on the strong ground of her Uncle’s Betsey Jane, she advised Miss Berry, as a friend, to prepare herself for the worst; and forewarned her that her aunt might, at any time, be expected to go off suddenly, like a powder-mill.", "자기 삼촌의 벳시 제인이라는 든든한 발판 위에 자기를 무장하고 진지를 구축한 채, 그녀는 친구로서 베리 아가씨에게 최악의 경우에 대비하라고 충고했으며, 이모가 화약 공장처럼 언제든 별안간 터져 버릴지 모른다고 미리 일러두었다."]], [["“I hope, Miss Berry,”", "\"바라건대, 베리 아가씨,\""], ["Mrs Wickam would observe, “that you’ll come into whatever little property there may be to leave.", "위컴 부인은 이렇게 말하곤 했다, \"남길 만한 소소한 재산이 무엇이든 있다면 그게 아가씨 차지가 되었으면 해요."], ["You deserve it, I am sure, for yours is a trying life.", "아가씨야말로 그럴 자격이 충분하지요, 정말이지 고된 삶을 사니까요."], ["Though there don’t seem much worth coming into—you’ll excuse my being so open—in this dismal den.”", "하긴 차지할 만한 게 그리 많아 보이지는 않지만—이렇게 터놓고 말하는 걸 용서해요—이 음침한 굴속에서는요.\""]], [["Poor Berry took it all in good part, and drudged and slaved away as usual; perfectly convinced that Mrs Pipchin was one of the most meritorious persons in the world, and making every day innumerable sacrifices of herself upon the altar of that noble old woman.", "가엾은 베리는 그 모든 말을 좋게 받아들이며 여느 때처럼 죽도록 일하고 시중을 들었다. 핍친 부인이 세상에서 가장 갸륵한 사람 가운데 하나라고 철석같이 믿으며, 날마다 그 고귀한 노파의 제단에 자기 자신을 무수히 제물로 바쳤다."], ["But all these immolations of Berry were somehow carried to the credit of Mrs Pipchin by Mrs Pipchin’s friends and admirers; and were made to harmonise with, and carry out, that melancholy fact of the deceased Mr Pipchin having broken his heart in the Peruvian mines.", "그러나 베리의 이 모든 희생은 어쩐 영문인지 핍친 부인의 친구들과 찬미자들에 의해 죄다 핍친 부인의 공적으로 돌아갔으며, 고인이 된 핍친 씨가 페루의 광산에서 상심하여 죽었다는 그 우울한 사실과 어우러져 그것을 떠받치는 데 쓰였다."]], [["For example, there was an honest grocer and general dealer in the retail line of business, between whom and Mrs Pipchin there was a small memorandum book, with a greasy red cover, perpetually in question, and concerning which divers secret councils and conferences were continually being held between the parties to that register, on the mat in the passage, and with closed doors in the parlour.", "이를테면 소매업을 하는 정직한 식료품상 겸 잡화상이 하나 있었는데, 그와 핍친 부인 사이에는 기름때 묻은 붉은 표지의 자그마한 외상 장부 한 권이 끊임없이 문제가 되었고, 그 장부에 얽힌 당사자들 사이에서는 복도의 깔개 위에서, 또 응접실에서 문을 닫아건 채 갖가지 은밀한 협의와 회담이 줄곧 벌어지곤 했다."], ["Nor were there wanting dark hints from Master Bitherstone (whose temper had been made revengeful by the solar heats of India acting on his blood), of balances unsettled, and of a failure, on one occasion within his memory, in the supply of moist sugar at tea-time.", "또 비더스톤 도련님(인도의 뙤약볕이 피에 작용한 탓에 성미가 앙심을 품게 된 아이였다)이 정산되지 않은 잔액에 관해서, 그리고 자기 기억으로 한 번은 차 마시는 시간에 흑설탕 공급에 차질이 빚어졌던 일에 관해서 음울한 암시를 흘리는 일도 없지 않았다."], ["This grocer being a bachelor and not a man who looked upon the surface for beauty, had once made honourable offers for the hand of Berry, which Mrs Pipchin had, with contumely and scorn, rejected.", "이 식료품상은 독신이었고 겉모습에서 아름다움을 찾는 사람이 아닌지라 한때 베리에게 정식으로 청혼을 한 적이 있었는데, 핍친 부인은 그것을 모욕과 경멸로써 물리쳤다."], ["Everybody said how laudable this was in Mrs Pipchin, relict of a man who had died of the Peruvian mines; and what a staunch, high, independent spirit the old lady had.", "모두가 페루 광산에서 죽은 사람의 미망인인 핍친 부인의 그 처사가 얼마나 칭찬할 만한 일이며, 그 노부인이 얼마나 굳세고 고결하며 독립적인 기개를 지녔는지 입을 모았다."], ["But nobody said anything about poor Berry, who cried for six weeks (being soundly rated by her good aunt all the time), and lapsed into a state of hopeless spinsterhood.", "그러나 여섯 주 동안 울었던(그동안 내내 갸륵한 이모에게 호되게 야단을 맞았다) 가엾은 베리에 대해서는, 그리고 절망적인 노처녀 신세로 떨어진 그녀에 대해서는 아무도 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Berry’s very fond of you, ain’t she?”", "\"베리가 너를 무척 좋아하지, 그렇지 않니?\""], ["Paul once asked Mrs Pipchin when they were sitting by the fire with the cat.", "폴이 한번은 고양이와 함께 난롯가에 앉아 있을 때 핍친 부인에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""], ["asked Paul.", "폴이 물었다."]], [["“Why!”", "\"왜라니!\""], ["returned the disconcerted old lady.", "당황한 노부인이 되받았다."], ["“How can you ask such things, Sir!", "\"어떻게 그런 걸 물어보니, 도련님!"], ["why are you fond of your sister Florence?”", "너는 왜 네 누나 플로렌스를 좋아하지?\""]], [["“Because she’s very good,”", "\"누나는 아주 착하니까요,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."], ["“There’s nobody like Florence.”", "\"플로렌스 같은 사람은 없어요.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"흥!\""], ["retorted Mrs Pipchin, shortly, “and there’s nobody like me, I suppose.”", "핍친 부인이 퉁명스레 쏘아붙였다, \"그럼 나 같은 사람도 없을 테지, 아마.\""]], [["“Ain’t there really though?”", "\"근데 정말 없어요?\""], ["asked Paul, leaning forward in his chair, and looking at her very hard.", "폴이 의자에서 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 그녀를 아주 빤히 바라보면서 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"없지,\""], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."]], [["“I am glad of that,”", "\"그거 다행이에요,\""], ["observed Paul, rubbing his hands thoughtfully.", "폴이 생각에 잠긴 듯 두 손을 비비며 말했다."], ["“That’s a very good thing.”", "\"그거 아주 잘된 일이에요.\""]], [["Mrs Pipchin didn’t dare to ask him why, lest she should receive some perfectly annihilating answer.", "핍친 부인은 어째서냐고 차마 묻지 못했다. 무언가 완전히 절멸시켜 버릴 듯한 대답이 돌아올까 두려웠던 것이다."], ["But as a compensation to her wounded feelings, she harassed Master Bitherstone to that extent until bed-time, that he began that very night to make arrangements for an overland return to India, by secreting from his supper a quarter of a round of bread and a fragment of moist Dutch cheese, as the beginning of a stock of provision to support him on the voyage.", "그러나 상처받은 감정에 대한 보상으로, 그녀는 잠자리에 들 시간까지 비더스톤 도련님을 어찌나 들볶았던지, 그 아이는 바로 그날 밤부터 인도로 육로를 통해 돌아갈 채비를 하기 시작했다. 항해 동안 자기를 지탱해 줄 식량을 비축하는 첫걸음으로, 저녁 식사에서 빵 한 덩이의 사분의 일과 눅눅한 네덜란드 치즈 한 조각을 빼돌려 감춘 것이다."]], [["Mrs Pipchin had kept watch and ward over little Paul and his sister for nearly twelve months.", "핍친 부인은 어린 폴과 그 누이를 거의 열두 달 가까이 지켜보며 돌봐 왔다."], ["They had been home twice, but only for a few days; and had been constant in their weekly visits to Mr Dombey at the hotel.", "그들은 두 번 집에 다녀왔으나 며칠씩에 불과했고, 호텔에 머무는 돔비 씨를 매주 빠짐없이 찾아뵙곤 했다."], ["By little and little Paul had grown stronger, and had become able to dispense with his carriage; though he still looked thin and delicate; and still remained the same old, quiet, dreamy child that he had been when first consigned to Mrs Pipchin’s care.", "조금씩 조금씩 폴은 튼튼해져 갔고 더는 유모차가 없어도 될 만큼 되었다. 그래도 여전히 야위고 가냘파 보였으며, 처음 핍친 부인의 손에 맡겨졌을 때와 똑같이, 예나 다름없는 그 조용하고 꿈꾸는 듯한 아이로 남아 있었다."], ["One Saturday afternoon, at dusk, great consternation was occasioned in the Castle by the unlooked-for announcement of Mr Dombey as a visitor to Mrs Pipchin.", "어느 토요일 오후 해 질 무렵, 돔비 씨가 핍친 부인을 찾아왔다는 뜻밖의 통보로 '성'에 큰 소동이 일었다."], ["The population of the parlour was immediately swept upstairs as on the wings of a whirlwind, and after much slamming of bedroom doors, and trampling overhead, and some knocking about of Master Bitherstone by Mrs Pipchin, as a relief to the perturbation of her spirits, the black bombazeen garments of the worthy old lady darkened the audience-chamber where Mr Dombey was contemplating the vacant arm-chair of his son and heir.", "응접실에 있던 사람들은 즉각 회오리바람의 날개에라도 실린 듯 위층으로 휩쓸려 올라갔고, 침실 문들을 요란하게 쾅쾅 닫고 머리 위에서 우당탕거린 다음, 핍친 부인이 동요한 마음을 달래느라 비더스톤 도련님을 한 차례 쥐어박고 나서, 그 갸륵한 노부인의 검은 봄버진 옷자락이 돔비 씨가 자기 아들이자 후계자의 빈 안락의자를 바라보고 있던 접견실을 어둡게 드리웠다. * 봄버진: 견·모 혼방의 검은 상복용 직물."]], [["“Mrs Pipchin,”", "\"핍친 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “How do you do?”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"안녕하시오?\""]], [["“Thank you, Sir,”", "\"고맙습니다, 나리,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, “I am pretty well, considering.”", "핍친 부인이 말했다, \"그럭저럭 잘 지내고 있습니다, 형편을 생각하면요.\""]], [["Mrs Pipchin always used that form of words.", "핍친 부인은 늘 그런 식으로 말했다."], ["It meant, considering her virtues, sacrifices, and so forth.", "그 말은 자기의 미덕이며 희생이며 그런 것들을 생각하면, 이라는 뜻이었다."]], [["“I can’t expect, Sir, to be very well,”", "\"제가 아주 잘 지내기를 바랄 수야 없지요, 나리,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, taking a chair and fetching her breath; “but such health as I have, I am grateful for.”", "핍친 부인이 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 숨을 고르고 말했다. \"그래도 이만한 건강이나마 감사히 여깁니다.\""]], [["Mr Dombey inclined his head with the satisfied air of a patron, who felt that this was the sort of thing for which he paid so much a quarter.", "돔비 씨는 분기마다 그만한 돈을 치르는 것이 바로 이런 종류의 것을 위함이라고 느끼는 후원자의 흡족한 태도로 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["After a moment’s silence he went on to say:", "잠시 침묵한 뒤 그는 말을 이었다."]], [["“Mrs Pipchin, I have taken the liberty of calling, to consult you in reference to my son.", "\"핍친 부인, 제 아들에 관해 의논드리려고 실례를 무릅쓰고 찾아왔습니다."], ["I have had it in my mind to do so for some time past; but have deferred it from time to time, in order that his health might be thoroughly re-established.", "한동안 그럴 생각을 품고 있었으나, 그 애의 건강이 완전히 회복되도록 하기 위해 차일피일 미루어 왔지요."], ["You have no misgivings on that subject, Mrs Pipchin?”", "그 점에 대해서는 부인께서도 우려가 없으시지요, 핍친 부인?\""]], [["“Brighton has proved very beneficial, Sir,”", "\"브라이턴은 무척 이로운 것으로 드러났습니다, 나리,\""], ["returned Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 대답했다."], ["“Very beneficial, indeed.”", "\"정말이지 무척 이로웠지요.\""]], [["“I purpose,”", "\"저는,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “his remaining at Brighton.”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"그 애를 브라이턴에 계속 두려고 합니다.\""]], [["Mrs Pipchin rubbed her hands, and bent her grey eyes on the fire.", "핍친 부인은 두 손을 비비며 잿빛 눈을 난롯불에 떨구었다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["pursued Mr Dombey, stretching out his forefinger, “but possibly that he should now make a change, and lead a different kind of life here.", "돔비 씨가 집게손가락을 쭉 뻗으며 말을 이었다, \"하지만 이제는 그 애가 변화를 꾀하여, 이곳에서 지금과는 다른 종류의 생활을 해 나가는 편이 좋을 듯합니다."], ["In short, Mrs Pipchin, that is the object of my visit.", "요컨대, 핍친 부인, 그것이 제가 찾아온 목적입니다."], ["My son is getting on, Mrs Pipchin.", "제 아들이 자라고 있습니다, 핍친 부인."], ["Really, he is getting on.”", "정말로, 자라고 있어요.\""]], [["There was something melancholy in the triumphant air with which Mr Dombey said this.", "돔비 씨가 이 말을 하는 그 의기양양한 태도에는 어딘가 우울한 데가 있었다."], ["It showed how long Paul’s childish life had been to him, and how his hopes were set upon a later stage of his existence.", "그것은 폴의 어린 시절이 그에게 얼마나 길게 느껴졌는지를, 그리고 그의 희망이 그 아이의 생애의 더 나중 단계에 얼마나 쏠려 있는지를 드러냈다."], ["Pity may appear a strange word to connect with anyone so haughty and so cold, and yet he seemed a worthy subject for it at that moment.", "연민이란 이토록 오만하고 차가운 사람과 결부시키기에 묘한 말로 보일지 모르나, 그 순간만큼은 그가 연민을 받을 만한 대상으로 보였다."]], [["“Six years old!”", "\"여섯 살이라!\""], ["said Mr Dombey, settling his neckcloth—perhaps to hide an irrepressible smile that rather seemed to strike upon the surface of his face and glance away, as finding no resting-place, than to play there for an instant.", "돔비 씨가 자기 목깃을 매만지며 말했다—아마도 억누를 수 없는 미소를 감추기 위함이었으리라. 그 미소는 그의 얼굴 표면에 잠시 머무는 것이라기보다는, 머물 곳을 찾지 못해 표면을 스치고 비껴가는 듯했다."], ["“Dear me, six will be changed to sixteen, before we have time to look about us.”", "\"이런, 우리가 미처 둘러볼 새도 없이 여섯이 열여섯으로 바뀌어 버리겠군요.\""]], [["“Ten years,”", "\"십 년은,\""], ["croaked the unsympathetic Pipchin, with a frosty glistening of her hard grey eye, and a dreary shaking of her bent head, “is a long time.”", "동정심이라고는 없는 핍친 부인이 그 단단한 잿빛 눈을 서리처럼 번득이며, 굽은 머리를 음울하게 가로저으며 갈라진 소리로 말했다, \"긴 세월이지요.\""]], [["“It depends on circumstances, returned Mr Dombey; “at all events, Mrs Pipchin, my son is six years old, and there is no doubt, I fear, that in his studies he is behind many children of his age—or his youth,”", "\"그건 사정에 따라 다르지요,\" 돔비 씨가 되받았다."], ["said Mr Dombey, quickly answering what he mistrusted was a shrewd twinkle of the frosty eye, “his youth is a more appropriate expression.", "\"어쨌든, 핍친 부인, 제 아들은 여섯 살이고, 유감스럽게도 학업에서 또래의 많은 아이들에게—아니, 동년배의 많은 아이들에게,\" 돔비 씨는 서리 같은 눈에서 날카로운 반짝임을 보았다고 의심한 것에 얼른 대응하여 말을 고쳤다, \"동년배라는 표현이 더 적절하겠군요."], ["Now, Mrs Pipchin, instead of being behind his peers, my son ought to be before them; far before them.", "그러니까, 핍친 부인, 제 아들은 또래보다 뒤처질 것이 아니라 앞서야 합니다. 한참 앞서야지요."], ["There is an eminence ready for him to mount upon.", "그 애가 올라설 높은 자리가 마련되어 있습니다."], ["There is nothing of chance or doubt in the course before my son.", "제 아들 앞에 놓인 길에는 우연이나 의심의 여지가 없습니다."], ["His way in life was clear and prepared, and marked out before he existed.", "그 애의 인생행로는 그 애가 태어나기도 전에 이미 명확하게 정해지고 닦여 있었습니다."], ["The education of such a young gentleman must not be delayed.", "그런 어린 신사의 교육은 미뤄져서는 안 됩니다."], ["It must not be left imperfect.", "불완전하게 남겨져서도 안 되고요."], ["It must be very steadily and seriously undertaken, Mrs Pipchin.”", "매우 꾸준하고 진지하게 착수해야 합니다, 핍친 부인.\""]], [["“Well, Sir,”", "\"그렇지요, 나리,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, “I can say nothing to the contrary.”", "핍친 부인이 말했다, \"그에 반대되는 말씀은 제가 드릴 게 없습니다.\""]], [["“I was quite sure, Mrs Pipchin,”", "\"부인 같은 양식 있는 분이라면 그러실 수도, 그러실 마음도 없으리라 확신했습니다, 핍친 부인,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, approvingly, “that a person of your good sense could not, and would not.”", "돔비 씨가 흡족해하며 응수했다."]], [["“There is a great deal of nonsense—and worse—talked about young people not being pressed too hard at first, and being tempted on, and all the rest of it, Sir,”", "\"어린아이들을 처음부터 너무 몰아붙여서는 안 된다느니, 살살 꾀어 가며 가르쳐야 한다느니, 어쩌니저쩌니 하는 헛소리가—더 고약한 소리들이—얼마나 많이들 떠도는지요, 나리,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, impatiently rubbing her hooked nose.", "핍친 부인이 매부리코를 짜증스레 문지르며 말했다."], ["“It never was thought of in my time, and it has no business to be thought of now.", "\"제 시절에는 그런 생각은 입에 올린 적도 없었고, 지금도 그래서는 안 됩니다."], ["My opinion is ‘keep ’em at it’.”", "제 의견은 '쉴 새 없이 시켜라'입니다.\""]], [["“My good madam,”", "\"훌륭하신 부인,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, “you have not acquired your reputation undeservedly; and I beg you to believe, Mrs Pipchin, that I am more than satisfied with your excellent system of management, and shall have the greatest pleasure in commending it whenever my poor commendation—”", "돔비 씨가 되받았다, \"부인께서는 그 명성을 거저 얻으신 것이 아니로군요. 그리고 핍친 부인, 부디 믿어 주십시오, 저는 부인의 그 탁월한 관리 방식에 더할 나위 없이 만족하며, 저의 변변찮은 칭찬이라도—\""], ["Mr Dombey’s loftiness when he affected to disparage his own importance, passed all bounds—“can be of any service.", "자기 중요성을 짐짓 깎아내릴 때의 돔비 씨의 거만함은 도를 넘었다—\"무슨 보탬이 될 수 있는 자리라면 언제든 기꺼이 그것을 추천할 것입니다."], ["I have been thinking of Doctor Blimber’s, Mrs Pipchin.”", "저는 블림버 박사의 학교를 생각해 보고 있었습니다, 핍친 부인.\""]], [["“My neighbour, Sir?” said Mrs Pipchin.", "\"제 이웃 말씀이군요, 나리?\""], ["“I believe the Doctor’s is an excellent establishment.", "핍친 부인이 말했다. \"그 박사의 학교는 훌륭한 곳으로 알고 있습니다."], ["I’ve heard that it’s very strictly conducted, and there is nothing but learning going on from morning to night.”", "무척 엄격하게 운영되고, 아침부터 밤까지 오로지 공부만 한다고 들었습니다.\""]], [["“And it’s very expensive,”", "\"게다가 학비가 무척 비싸지요,\""], ["added Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 덧붙였다."]], [["“And it’s very expensive, Sir,”", "\"게다가 학비가 무척 비싸고요, 나리,\""], ["returned Mrs Pipchin, catching at the fact, as if in omitting that, she had omitted one of its leading merits.", "핍친 부인이 그 사실을 냉큼 받아 되받았다. 마치 그것을 빠뜨린다면 그 학교의 으뜸가는 장점 가운데 하나를 빠뜨리는 셈이라는 듯이."]], [["“I have had some communication with the Doctor, Mrs Pipchin,”", "\"제가 박사와 몇 차례 연락을 주고받았습니다, 핍친 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, hitching his chair anxiously a little nearer to the fire, “and he does not consider Paul at all too young for his purpose.", "돔비 씨가 의자를 난롯불 쪽으로 조바심치듯 조금 더 끌어당기며 말했다, \"그런데 그분은 폴이 자기 목적에 비추어 너무 어리다고는 전혀 여기지 않으십니다."], ["He mentioned several instances of boys in Greek at about the same age.", "거의 같은 나이에 그리스어를 배운 소년들의 사례를 여럿 들어 주셨지요."], ["If I have any little uneasiness in my own mind, Mrs Pipchin, on the subject of this change, it is not on that head.", "이번 변화에 대해 제 마음속에 자그마한 불안이 하나 있다면, 핍친 부인, 그것은 그 점에 관한 것이 아닙니다."], ["My son not having known a mother has gradually concentrated much—too much—of his childish affection on his sister.", "제 아들은 어머니를 알지 못한 탓에 점차 자기의 어린 애정을 너무 많이—지나치게 많이—누이에게 쏟아 왔습니다."], ["Whether their separation—”", "그 둘을 떼어 놓는 것이—\""], ["Mr Dombey said no more, but sat silent.", "돔비 씨는 더 말하지 않고 잠자코 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Hoity-toity!”", "\"원, 별소리를!\""], ["exclaimed Mrs Pipchin, shaking out her black bombazeen skirts, and plucking up all the ogress within her.", "핍친 부인이 검은 봄버진 치맛자락을 펄럭이며, 자기 안의 식인귀 같은 기질을 한껏 끌어올리며 외쳤다."], ["“If she don’t like it, Mr Dombey, she must be taught to lump it.”", "\"그 애가 그걸 싫어한다면, 돔비 씨, 참고 견디도록 가르쳐야지요.\""], ["The good lady apologised immediately afterwards for using so common a figure of speech, but said (and truly) that that was the way she reasoned with ’em.", "갸륵한 부인은 그렇게 상스러운 표현을 쓴 것을 곧바로 사과했으나, 자기는 아이들을 그런 식으로 타이른다고 (사실대로) 말했다."]], [["Mr Dombey waited until Mrs Pipchin had done bridling and shaking her head, and frowning down a legion of Bitherstones and Pankeys; and then said quietly, but correctively, “He, my good madam, he.”", "돔비 씨는 핍친 부인이 고개를 빳빳이 세웠다 가로저었다 하며 비더스톤이며 팽키 무리를 잔뜩 노려보아 굴복시키는 동작을 마칠 때까지 기다렸다가, 조용히, 그러나 바로잡듯 말했다. \"그 애 말입니다, 훌륭하신 부인, 그 애요.\" [* 핍친 부인이 누이 플로렌스를 가리켜 'she'라 했으나, 돔비 씨는 아들 폴을 두고 한 말임을 정정한 것이다.]"]], [["Mrs Pipchin’s system would have applied very much the same mode of cure to any uneasiness on the part of Paul, too; but as the hard grey eye was sharp enough to see that the recipe, however Mr Dombey might admit its efficacy in the case of the daughter, was not a sovereign remedy for the son, she argued the point; and contended that change, and new society, and the different form of life he would lead at Doctor Blimber’s, and the studies he would have to master, would very soon prove sufficient alienations.", "핍친 부인의 방식이라면 폴 쪽의 어떤 불안에 대해서도 거의 똑같은 치료법을 적용했을 터였다. 그러나 그 단단한 잿빛 눈은, 돔비 씨가 딸의 경우에는 그 처방의 효험을 인정할지언정 아들에게는 그것이 만병통치약이 못 된다는 것을 알아챌 만큼 날카로웠으므로, 그녀는 그 점을 따져 들었다."], ["As this chimed in with Mr Dombey’s own hope and belief, it gave that gentleman a still higher opinion of Mrs Pipchin’s understanding; and as Mrs Pipchin, at the same time, bewailed the loss of her dear little friend (which was not an overwhelming shock to her, as she had long expected it, and had not looked, in the beginning, for his remaining with her longer than three months), he formed an equally good opinion of Mrs Pipchin’s disinterestedness.", "그러면서 변화와, 새로운 교제와, 블림버 박사의 학교에서 그가 영위할 다른 형태의 생활과, 그가 익혀야 할 학업이 머지않아 충분한 이별의 계기가 되어 줄 것이라고 주장했다. 이것이 돔비 씨 자신의 희망이자 믿음과 맞아떨어졌으므로, 그 신사는 핍친 부인의 식견을 한층 더 높이 평가하게 되었다."], ["It was plain that he had given the subject anxious consideration, for he had formed a plan, which he announced to the ogress, of sending Paul to the Doctor’s as a weekly boarder for the first half year, during which time Florence would remain at the Castle, that she might receive her brother there, on Saturdays.", "그리고 핍친 부인이 동시에 사랑스러운 어린 친구를 잃게 됨을 애통해했으므로(그것이 그녀에게 그리 큰 충격은 아니었으니, 오래전부터 예상해 온 일인 데다 애초에 그가 석 달 넘게 자기 곁에 머무르리라고는 기대하지도 않았던 까닭이다), 그는 핍친 부인의 사심 없음에 대해서도 똑같이 좋은 평가를 내렸다. 그가 이 문제를 노심초사하며 숙고했음은 분명했으니, 한 가지 계획을 세워 그 식인귀에게 알린 것이다."], ["This would wean him by degrees, Mr Dombey said; possibly with a recollection of his not having been weaned by degrees on a former occasion.", "곧, 처음 반년 동안은 폴을 박사의 학교에 주간 기숙생으로 보내고, 그동안 플로렌스는 '성'에 남아 토요일마다 그곳에서 동생을 맞이하게 한다는 것이었다. 이렇게 하면 그 애를 차츰차츰 떼어 놓을 수 있으리라고 돔비 씨는 말했다—아마도 예전에 그 애가 차츰차츰 젖을 떼지 못했던 일을 떠올렸는지도 모른다."]], [["Mr Dombey finished the interview by expressing his hope that Mrs Pipchin would still remain in office as general superintendent and overseer of his son, pending his studies at Brighton; and having kissed Paul, and shaken hands with Florence, and beheld Master Bitherstone in his collar of state, and made Miss Pankey cry by patting her on the head (in which region she was uncommonly tender, on account of a habit Mrs Pipchin had of sounding it with her knuckles, like a cask), he withdrew to his hotel and dinner: resolved that Paul, now that he was getting so old and well, should begin a vigorous course of education forthwith, to qualify him for the position in which he was to shine; and that Doctor Blimber should take him in hand immediately.", "돔비 씨는 폴이 브라이턴에서 학업을 이어 가는 동안에도 핍친 부인이 아들의 총괄 감독자이자 관리자로서 계속 그 직책에 머물러 주기를 바란다는 뜻을 표하는 것으로 회견을 마무리했다. 그리고 폴에게 입을 맞추고, 플로렌스와 악수하고, 위풍당당한 옷깃을 두른 비더스톤 도련님을 둘러본 다음, 팽키 아가씨의 머리를 토닥여 그 아이를 울리고(이 부위는 핍친 부인이 손가락 마디로 술통 두드리듯 톡톡 쳐 보는 버릇이 있는 탓에 유난히 아픈 곳이었다) 호텔과 만찬을 향해 물러갔다. 이제 그토록 나이도 들고 건강도 좋아진 폴이 곧장 활기찬 교육 과정을 시작하여, 그가 빛을 발하게 될 그 지위에 걸맞은 자격을 갖추도록 해야 하며, 블림버 박사가 당장 그를 맡아야 한다고 굳게 결심하면서."]], [["Whenever a young gentleman was taken in hand by Doctor Blimber, he might consider himself sure of a pretty tight squeeze.", "블림버 박사에게 맡겨진 어린 신사라면 누구든 꽤나 단단히 쥐어짜이리라 각오하는 편이 좋았다."], ["The Doctor only undertook the charge of ten young gentlemen, but he had, always ready, a supply of learning for a hundred, on the lowest estimate; and it was at once the business and delight of his life to gorge the unhappy ten with it.", "박사는 어린 신사를 열 명만 맡았으나, 아무리 적게 잡아도 백 명분의 학식을 늘 비축해 두고 있었다. 그리고 그 불행한 열 명에게 그것을 잔뜩 처넣어 주는 것이야말로 그의 평생의 사업이자 기쁨이었다."]], [["In fact, Doctor Blimber’s establishment was a great hot-house, in which there was a forcing apparatus incessantly at work.", "사실 블림버 박사의 학교는 거대한 온실로, 그 안에서는 촉성 장치가 쉴 새 없이 돌아가고 있었다."], ["All the boys blew before their time.", "모든 소년들이 제철보다 일찍 꽃을 피웠다."], ["Mental green-peas were produced at Christmas, and intellectual asparagus all the year round.", "정신의 풋완두가 크리스마스에 산출되었고, 지성의 아스파라거스가 일 년 내내 산출되었다."], ["Mathematical gooseberries (very sour ones too) were common at untimely seasons, and from mere sprouts of bushes, under Doctor Blimber’s cultivation.", "수학의 구스베리(그것도 아주 신 것)가 블림버 박사의 재배 아래 철 아닌 때에, 그저 덤불의 새순에 불과한 것에서 흔히 열렸다."], ["Every description of Greek and Latin vegetable was got off the driest twigs of boys, under the frostiest circumstances.", "온갖 종류의 그리스어와 라틴어 채소가, 더없이 서리 내린 환경 속에서, 소년이라는 바싹 마른 잔가지에서 거둬졌다."], ["Nature was of no consequence at all.", "자연 따위는 전혀 중요하지 않았다."], ["No matter what a young gentleman was intended to bear, Doctor Blimber made him bear to pattern, somehow or other.", "어떤 어린 신사가 무엇을 맺도록 타고났든, 블림버 박사는 어떻게든 그를 정해진 본보기대로 맺게 만들었다."]], [["This was all very pleasant and ingenious, but the system of forcing was attended with its usual disadvantages.", "이 모든 것이 무척 흐뭇하고 기발했으나, 촉성 재배의 방식에는 으레 따르기 마련인 폐단이 뒤따랐다."], ["There was not the right taste about the premature productions, and they didn’t keep well.", "그 설익은 산물들에는 제대로 된 맛이 없었고, 오래 보존되지도 않았다."], ["Moreover, one young gentleman, with a swollen nose and an excessively large head (the oldest of the ten who had “gone through”", "게다가 코가 부어오르고 머리가 지나치게 큰 어린 신사 하나(모든 것을 '다 거쳐 낸'"], ["everything), suddenly left off blowing one day, and remained in the establishment a mere stalk.", "열 명 가운데 가장 나이 든 아이였다)는 어느 날 갑자기 꽃 피우기를 그만두더니 학교 안에 한낱 줄기로만 남고 말았다."], ["And people did say that the Doctor had rather overdone it with young Toots, and that when he began to have whiskers he left off having brains.", "그리고 사람들은 박사가 어린 투츠에게 다소 도를 넘게 했다고, 그래서 그가 구레나룻이 나기 시작할 무렵에는 머리 쓰기를 그만두었다고들 수군거렸다."]], [["There young Toots was, at any rate; possessed of the gruffest of voices and the shrillest of minds; sticking ornamental pins into his shirt, and keeping a ring in his waistcoat pocket to put on his little finger by stealth, when the pupils went out walking; constantly falling in love by sight with nurserymaids, who had no idea of his existence; and looking at the gas-lighted world over the little iron bars in the left-hand corner window of the front three pairs of stairs, after bed-time, like a greatly overgrown cherub who had sat up aloft much too long.", "어쨌든 어린 투츠는 거기 있었다. 더없이 굵직한 목소리와 더없이 새된 머리를 지닌 채, 셔츠에 장식 핀을 꽂고, 학생들이 산책하러 나갈 때 몰래 새끼손가락에 끼려고 조끼 주머니에 반지를 하나 넣어 두고, 자기 존재를 알 리도 없는 보모들에게 보기만 하면 끊임없이 반하며, 취침 시간 뒤에는 정면 삼층 계단 왼쪽 구석 창문의 작은 쇠창살 너머로 가스등 켜진 세상을 내다보았는데, 그 모습이 마치 높은 데 너무 오래 앉아 있어 지나치게 웃자란 거대한 천사 같았다."]], [["The Doctor was a portly gentleman in a suit of black, with strings at his knees, and stockings below them.", "박사는 검은 정장을 차려입은 풍채 좋은 신사로, 무릎께에 끈이 달려 있고 그 아래로 양말을 신고 있었다."], ["He had a bald head, highly polished; a deep voice; and a chin so very double, that it was a wonder how he ever managed to shave into the creases.", "그는 반들반들 윤이 나는 대머리에, 깊은 목소리에, 턱이 어찌나 겹졌는지 그 주름 사이를 대체 어떻게 면도하는지 신기할 지경이었다."], ["He had likewise a pair of little eyes that were always half shut up, and a mouth that was always half expanded into a grin, as if he had, that moment, posed a boy, and were waiting to convict him from his own lips.", "또한 늘 반쯤 감겨 있는 작은 눈 한 쌍과, 늘 반쯤 히죽거림으로 벌어진 입을 지니고 있었는데, 마치 방금 어떤 소년에게 질문을 던져 놓고 그 입에서 나온 말로 그를 잡아내려고 기다리는 듯한 모양새였다."], ["Insomuch, that when the Doctor put his right hand into the breast of his coat, and with his other hand behind him, and a scarcely perceptible wag of his head, made the commonest observation to a nervous stranger, it was like a sentiment from the sphynx, and settled his business.", "그리하여 박사가 오른손을 외투 가슴팍에 찔러 넣고, 다른 손은 등 뒤로 한 채, 머리를 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 까딱이며, 긴장한 낯선 이에게 더없이 평범한 말을 건넬 때면, 그것이 마치 스핑크스의 잠언처럼 들려 상대를 옴짝달싹 못 하게 만들었다."]], [["The Doctor’s was a mighty fine house, fronting the sea.", "박사의 집은 바다를 마주한 으리으리한 저택이었다. 안은 즐거운 양식이 아니라 오히려 정반대였다."], ["Not a joyful style of house within, but quite the contrary.", "빛바랜 색의 커튼들은, 그 폭이 인색하고 빈약하여, 풀이 죽은 채 창문 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 있었다. 탁자와 의자들은 마치 셈에 늘어선 숫자처럼 줄지어 치워져 있었다."], ["Sad-coloured curtains, whose proportions were spare and lean, hid themselves despondently behind the windows.", "격식 차린 방들에는 난롯불이 어찌나 드물게 지펴졌던지 우물처럼 느껴졌고, 손님은 두레박 노릇을 하는 격이었다. 식당은 세상에서 먹고 마시는 일이 일어날 법한 가장 마지막 장소처럼 보였다."], ["The tables and chairs were put away in rows, like figures in a sum; fires were so rarely lighted in the rooms of ceremony, that they felt like wells, and a visitor represented the bucket; the dining-room seemed the last place in the world where any eating or drinking was likely to occur; there was no sound through all the house but the ticking of a great clock in the hall, which made itself audible in the very garrets; and sometimes a dull cooing of young gentlemen at their lessons, like the murmurings of an assemblage of melancholy pigeons.", "온 집 안을 통틀어 현관의 커다란 시계가 째깍대는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 없었는데, 그 소리는 다락방에까지 들렸다. 그리고 이따금 어린 신사들이 공부하며 내는 둔탁한 구구 소리가, 마치 우울한 비둘기 떼가 모여 웅얼대는 소리처럼 들려왔다."]], [["Miss Blimber, too, although a slim and graceful maid, did no soft violence to the gravity of the house.", "블림버 양 또한, 비록 날씬하고 우아한 처녀였으나, 그 집의 엄숙함을 부드럽게 해치는 일이라고는 없었다."], ["There was no light nonsense about Miss Blimber.", "블림버 양에게는 가벼운 객쩍음이라고는 없었다."], ["She kept her hair short and crisp, and wore spectacles.", "그녀는 머리를 짧고 부스스하게 유지했으며 안경을 썼다."], ["She was dry and sandy with working in the graves of deceased languages.", "죽은 언어들의 무덤을 파헤치는 일로 메마르고 까칠해진 사람이었다."], ["None of your live languages for Miss Blimber.", "블림버 양에게 살아 있는 언어 따위는 어림없었다."], ["They must be dead—stone dead—and then Miss Blimber dug them up like a Ghoul.", "언어는 죽어야 했다—완전히 죽어야 했다—그래야 블림버 양이 시체 도굴꾼처럼 그것들을 파냈다."]], [["Mrs Blimber, her Mama, was not learned herself, but she pretended to be, and that did quite as well.", "그녀의 어머니인 블림버 부인은 자신이 학식 있는 사람은 아니었으나 그런 척했고, 그것으로 충분히 통했다."], ["She said at evening parties, that if she could have known Cicero, she thought she could have died contented.", "그녀는 저녁 모임에서, 만일 키케로를 알 수 있었더라면 만족하며 죽을 수 있었으리라 생각한다고 말하곤 했다."], ["It was the steady joy of her life to see the Doctor’s young gentlemen go out walking, unlike all other young gentlemen, in the largest possible shirt-collars, and the stiffest possible cravats.", "박사의 어린 신사들이 다른 모든 소년들과는 달리 되도록 큼직한 셔츠 깃과 되도록 빳빳한 크라바트를 두르고 산책하러 나가는 모습을 보는 것이 그녀의 한결같은 기쁨이었다."], ["It was so classical, she said.", "그게 어찌나 고전적인지 모른다고 그녀는 말했다."]], [["As to Mr Feeder, B.A., Doctor Blimber’s assistant, he was a kind of human barrel-organ, with a little list of tunes at which he was continually working, over and over again, without any variation.", "블림버 박사의 조교인 피더 학사로 말하자면, 그는 일종의 인간 손풍금 같은 존재로, 변주라고는 전혀 없이 곡목 몇 가지가 적힌 짧은 목록을 끊임없이 거듭거듭 연주하고 있었다."], ["He might have been fitted up with a change of barrels, perhaps, in early life, if his destiny had been favourable; but it had not been; and he had only one, with which, in a monotonous round, it was his occupation to bewilder the young ideas of Doctor Blimber’s young gentlemen.", "운명이 그의 편이었더라면 어쩌면 어린 시절에 다른 곡통으로 교체할 수 있게 장치되었을지도 모르나, 그렇지 못했다. 그에게는 단 하나의 곡통밖에 없었고, 그것으로 단조로운 회전을 거듭하며 블림버 박사네 어린 신사들의 어린 머리를 어리둥절하게 만드는 것이 그의 일이었다."], ["The young gentlemen were prematurely full of carking anxieties.", "어린 신사들은 일찌감치 갉아먹는 듯한 근심으로 가득 차 있었다."], ["They knew no rest from the pursuit of stony-hearted verbs, savage noun-substantives, inflexible syntactic passages, and ghosts of exercises that appeared to them in their dreams.", "그들은 돌처럼 매정한 동사며, 사나운 명사며, 굽힐 줄 모르는 구문 단락이며, 꿈속에까지 나타나는 연습 문제의 유령들을 쫓느라 한시도 쉬지 못했다."], ["Under the forcing system, a young gentleman usually took leave of his spirits in three weeks.", "그 촉성 재배의 방식 아래에서, 어린 신사는 보통 삼 주 만에 기운을 작별했다."], ["He had all the cares of the world on his head in three months.", "석 달이면 세상의 온갖 근심을 머리에 짊어졌다."], ["He conceived bitter sentiments against his parents or guardians in four; he was an old misanthrope, in five; envied Curtius that blessed refuge in the earth, in six; and at the end of the first twelvemonth had arrived at the conclusion, from which he never afterwards departed, that all the fancies of the poets, and lessons of the sages, were a mere collection of words and grammar, and had no other meaning in the world.", "넉 달이면 부모나 후견인에 대해 쓰디쓴 감정을 품게 되었고, 다섯 달이면 늙은 염세가가 되었으며, 여섯 달이면 땅속 그 복된 피난처를 차지한 쿠르티우스를 부러워했다. 그리고 첫 일 년이 끝날 무렵에는 그 뒤로 결코 벗어나지 못할 결론에 다다랐으니, 곧 시인들의 온갖 공상이며 현인들의 가르침이란 한낱 낱말과 문법의 모음에 불과하며 세상에 다른 뜻이라고는 없다는 것이었다."]], [["But he went on blow, blow, blowing, in the Doctor’s hothouse, all the time; and the Doctor’s glory and reputation were great, when he took his wintry growth home to his relations and friends.", "그러나 그러는 내내 그는 박사의 온실 안에서 줄곧 꽃을 피우고 또 피웠다. 그리고 그가 그 겨울 동안 자란 결실을 친척과 친구들에게로 가져갈 때면 박사의 영광과 명성은 드높았다. * 쿠르티우스: 로마 전설 속 마르쿠스 쿠르티우스. 갈라진 땅속 심연에 스스로 뛰어들어 나라를 구한 청년."]], [["Upon the Doctor’s door-steps one day, Paul stood with a fluttering heart, and with his small right hand in his father’s.", "어느 날 박사네 집 현관 계단 위에, 폴은 두근거리는 가슴으로, 자그마한 오른손을 아버지의 손에 맡긴 채 서 있었다."], ["His other hand was locked in that of Florence.", "그의 다른 손은 플로렌스의 손에 꼭 쥐여 있었다."], ["How tight the tiny pressure of that one; and how loose and cold the other!", "한쪽의 그 자그마한 쥐임은 얼마나 꼭 죄어 있었으며, 다른 한쪽은 얼마나 느슨하고 차가웠던지!"]], [["Mrs Pipchin hovered behind the victim, with her sable plumage and her hooked beak, like a bird of ill-omen.", "핍친 부인이 그 검은 깃털과 매부리 같은 부리를 하고서, 흉조처럼 그 제물 뒤를 맴돌았다."], ["She was out of breath—for Mr Dombey, full of great thoughts, had walked fast—and she croaked hoarsely as she waited for the opening of the door.", "그녀는 숨이 차 있었다—돔비 씨가 큰 생각들로 가득 차 빠르게 걸어온 탓이다—그리고 문이 열리기를 기다리며 쉰 소리로 갈갈댔다."]], [["“Now, Paul,”", "\"자, 폴,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, exultingly.", "돔비 씨가 의기양양하게 말했다."], ["“This is the way indeed to be Dombey and Son, and have money.", "\"이것이야말로 돔비와 아들이 되어 돈을 벌어들이는 길이란다."], ["You are almost a man already.”", "너는 벌써 거의 어엿한 사내가 다 되었어.\""]], [["“Almost,”", "\"거의요,\""], ["returned the child.", "아이가 되받았다."]], [["Even his childish agitation could not master the sly and quaint yet touching look, with which he accompanied the reply.", "그 어린아이다운 동요조차도, 그 대답에 곁들인 그 능청맞고 별스러우면서도 가슴 뭉클한 표정을 누르지는 못했다."]], [["It brought a vague expression of dissatisfaction into Mr Dombey’s face; but the door being opened, it was quickly gone.", "그것은 돔비 씨의 얼굴에 막연한 불만의 빛을 떠오르게 했으나, 문이 열리는 바람에 그 빛은 이내 사라졌다."]], [["“Doctor Blimber is at home, I believe?”", "\"블림버 박사 댁에 계시지요, 아마?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["The man said yes; and as they passed in, looked at Paul as if he were a little mouse, and the house were a trap.", "하인은 그렇다고 답했다. 그러고는 그들이 들어설 때, 마치 폴이 자그마한 생쥐이고 이 집은 덫이기라도 한 듯 그를 바라보았다."], ["He was a weak-eyed young man, with the first faint streaks or early dawn of a grin on his countenance.", "그는 눈이 약한 젊은이로, 그 얼굴에는 막 동트는 새벽의 첫 희미한 줄기처럼 히죽거림의 기미가 어려 있었다."], ["It was mere imbecility; but Mrs Pipchin took it into her head that it was impudence, and made a snap at him directly.", "그것은 한낱 멍청함이었으나, 핍친 부인은 그것을 건방진 짓이라고 단정하고 즉각 그를 향해 딱 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“How dare you laugh behind the gentleman’s back?”", "\"어떻게 감히 신사분 등 뒤에서 웃는 게냐?\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 말했다."], ["“And what do you take me for?”", "\"그래, 나를 뭘로 보는 거냐?\""]], [["“I ain’t a laughing at nobody, and I’m sure I don’t take you for nothing, Ma’am,”", "\"저는 아무도 비웃지 않았고, 정말이지 마님을 무엇으로도 본 적이 없습니다요,\""], ["returned the young man, in consternation.", "젊은이가 당황하여 되받았다."]], [["“A pack of idle dogs!”", "\"빈둥대는 개 같은 것들 같으니!\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, “only fit to be turnspits.", "핍친 부인이 말했다, \"고작 개 쳇바퀴나 돌릴 주제에."], ["Go and tell your master that Mr Dombey’s here, or it’ll be worse for you!”", "가서 네 주인에게 돔비 씨가 오셨다고 일러라, 안 그러면 네 신세가 더 고약해질 테니!\""]], [["The weak-eyed young man went, very meekly, to discharge himself of this commission; and soon came back to invite them to the Doctor’s study.", "* 개 쳇바퀴(turnspit): 옛날 부엌에서 개를 쳇바퀴에 넣어 돌리며 고기 굽는 꼬챙이를 돌리게 한 데서 온 천한 일거리."]], [["“You’re laughing again, Sir,” said Mrs Pipchin, when it came to her turn, bringing up the rear, to pass him in the hall.", "눈이 약한 젊은이는 무척 고분고분하게 그 심부름을 하러 갔고, 이내 돌아와 그들을 박사의 서재로 안내했다."]], [["“I ain’t,”", "\"또 웃는구나, 이놈,\""], ["returned the young man, grievously oppressed. “I never see such a thing as this!”", "핍친 부인이 맨 뒤를 따라가며 현관에서 그의 곁을 지나칠 차례가 되자 말했다."]], [["“What is the matter, Mrs Pipchin?”", "\"안 웃었습니다요,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, looking round.", "젊은이가 몹시 짓눌린 채 되받았다."], ["“Softly! Pray!”", "\"이런 일은 난생처음 봅니다요!\""]], [["Mrs Pipchin, in her deference, merely muttered at the young man as she passed on, and said, “Oh! he was a precious fellow”—leaving the young man, who was all meekness and incapacity, affected even to tears by the incident. But Mrs Pipchin had a way of falling foul of all meek people; and her friends said who could wonder at it, after the Peruvian mines!", "\"무슨 일이오, 핍친 부인?\" 돔비 씨가 돌아보며 말했다. \"조용히! 제발요!\" 핍친 부인은 그 공손함을 지키느라, 지나가면서 그저 젊은이를 향해 중얼거리며 \"오! 참으로 귀하신 몸이지\"라고만 했다—그리고 온통 고분고분함과 무능함 덩어리인 그 젊은이를, 그 일로 눈물이 다 날 지경으로 만들어 놓고 가 버렸다. 그러나 핍친 부인에게는 온순한 사람들에게는 죄다 시비를 거는 버릇이 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 친구들은, 페루 광산 일을 겪었으니 그게 뭐 놀랄 일이냐고들 했다!"]], [["The Doctor was sitting in his portentous study, with a globe at each knee, books all round him, Homer over the door, and Minerva on the mantel-shelf.", "박사는 그 위압적인 서재에 앉아 있었는데, 양 무릎께에 지구의가 하나씩 놓이고, 사방에 책이 둘러 있고, 문 위에는 호메로스가, 벽난로 선반 위에는 미네르바가 있었다."], ["“And how do you do, Sir?”", "\"그래, 안녕하시오, 나리?\""], ["he said to Mr Dombey, “and how is my little friend?”", "그가 돔비 씨에게 말했다, \"그래, 우리 어린 친구는 어떤가?\""], ["Grave as an organ was the Doctor’s speech; and when he ceased, the great clock in the hall seemed (to Paul at least) to take him up, and to go on saying, “how, is, my, lit, tle, friend?", "박사의 말투는 오르간처럼 장중했다. 그가 말을 멈추자, 현관의 큰 시계가 (적어도 폴에게는) 그 말을 이어받아 \"어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는?"], ["how, is, my, lit, tle, friend?”", "어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는?\""], ["over and over and over again.", "하고 거듭거듭, 한없이 되뇌는 듯했다."]], [["The little friend being something too small to be seen at all from where the Doctor sat, over the books on his table, the Doctor made several futile attempts to get a view of him round the legs; which Mr Dombey perceiving, relieved the Doctor from his embarrassment by taking Paul up in his arms, and sitting him on another little table, over against the Doctor, in the middle of the room.", "어린 친구는 너무 자그마하여 박사가 앉은 자리에서는 탁자 위 책 너머로 도무지 보이지가 않았으므로, 박사는 책상다리 옆으로 돌아 그를 보려고 몇 차례 헛된 시도를 했다. 이를 알아챈 돔비 씨가, 폴을 팔로 안아 올려 방 한가운데 박사 맞은편의 또 다른 자그마한 탁자 위에 앉혀 줌으로써 박사를 그 난처함에서 구해 주었다."]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["said the Doctor, leaning back in his chair with his hand in his breast.", "박사가 손을 가슴팍에 찌른 채 의자에 등을 기대며 말했다."], ["“Now I see my little friend.", "\"이제 우리 어린 친구가 보이는군."], ["How do you do, my little friend?”", "안녕한가, 우리 어린 친구?\""]], [["The clock in the hall wouldn’t subscribe to this alteration in the form of words, but continued to repeat how, is, my, lit, tle, friend?", "현관의 시계는 이렇게 말의 형태가 바뀐 것에는 동의하지 않은 채, 여전히 \"어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는? 어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는?\""], ["how, is, my, lit, tle, friend?”", "하고 되뇌기를 그치지 않았다."]], [["“Very well, I thank you, Sir,”", "\"아주 잘 지냅니다, 고맙습니다, 선생님,\""], ["returned Paul, answering the clock quite as much as the Doctor.", "폴이 되받았다. 박사 못지않게 그 시계에게도 대답을 하면서."]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["said Doctor Blimber.", "블림버 박사가 말했다."], ["“Shall we make a man of him?”", "\"이 아이를 어엿한 사내로 만들어 볼까요?\""]], [["“Do you hear, Paul?”", "\"듣고 있니, 폴?\""], ["added Mr Dombey; Paul being silent.", "돔비 씨가 덧붙였다. 폴이 잠자코 있었던 까닭이다."]], [["“Shall we make a man of him?”", "\"이 아이를 어엿한 사내로 만들어 볼까요?\""], ["repeated the Doctor.", "박사가 거듭 말했다."]], [["“I had rather be a child,”", "\"저는 어린아이로 있는 게 더 좋아요,\""], ["replied Paul.", "폴이 대답했다."]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"허!\""], ["said the Doctor.", "박사가 말했다."], ["“Why?”", "\"어째서지?\""]], [["The child sat on the table looking at him, with a curious expression of suppressed emotion in his face, and beating one hand proudly on his knee as if he had the rising tears beneath it, and crushed them.", "아이는 탁자 위에 앉아 그를 바라보았는데, 그 얼굴에는 억눌린 감정이 묘하게 어려 있었고, 한 손으로는 마치 그 아래에 솟구치는 눈물이 있어 그것을 으스러뜨리기라도 하는 듯 자기 무릎을 자랑스레 두드렸다."], ["But his other hand strayed a little way the while, a little farther—farther from him yet—until it lighted on the neck of Florence.", "그러나 그러는 동안 다른 손은 조금씩, 조금 더 멀리—그에게서 더더욱 멀리—더듬어 가더니, 마침내 플로렌스의 목에 가 닿았다."], ["“This is why,”", "\"이게 그 까닭이에요,\""], ["it seemed to say, and then the steady look was broken up and gone; the working lip was loosened; and the tears came streaming forth.", "하고 그 손이 말하는 듯했다. 그러더니 그 차분한 표정이 무너져 사라지고, 떨리는 입술이 풀리며, 눈물이 줄줄 쏟아져 나왔다."]], [["“Mrs Pipchin,”", "\"핍친 부인,\""], ["said his father, in a querulous manner, “I am really very sorry to see this.”", "그의 아버지가 짜증스러운 투로 말했다, \"이런 모습을 보게 되어 정말이지 무척 유감입니다.\""]], [["“Come away from him, do, Miss Dombey,”", "\"그 애한테서 떨어져요, 어서요, 돔비 아가씨,\""], ["quoth the matron.", "그 노부인이 말했다."]], [["“Never mind,”", "\"괜찮습니다,\""], ["said the Doctor, blandly nodding his head, to keep Mrs Pipchin back.", "박사가 핍친 부인을 막느라 부드럽게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["“Never mind; we shall substitute new cares and new impressions, Mr Dombey, very shortly.", "\"괜찮아요. 머지않아 새로운 근심거리와 새로운 인상으로 채워 줄 테니까요, 돔비 씨."], ["You would still wish my little friend to acquire—”", "그래도 우리 어린 친구가 익히기를 여전히 바라시는 것은—\""]], [["“Everything, if you please, Doctor,”", "\"전부입니다, 부디, 박사님,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, firmly.", "돔비 씨가 단호하게 되받았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said the Doctor, who, with his half-shut eyes, and his usual smile, seemed to survey Paul with the sort of interest that might attach to some choice little animal he was going to stuff.", "박사가 말했다. 반쯤 감긴 눈과 여느 때의 미소로, 마치 자기가 박제로 만들려는 어떤 진귀한 작은 짐승을 살펴보는 듯한 흥미를 띠고 폴을 훑어보면서."], ["“Yes, exactly. Ha!", "\"네, 바로 그렇지요."], ["We shall impart a great variety of information to our little friend, and bring him quickly forward, I daresay.", "하! 우리 어린 친구에게 아주 다양한 지식을 전수하여, 분명 빠르게 진보시킬 것입니다."], ["I daresay.", "분명히요."], ["Quite a virgin soil, I believe you said, Mr Dombey?”", "완전히 처녀지라고 하셨지요, 돔비 씨, 아마?\""]], [["“Except some ordinary preparation at home, and from this lady,”", "\"집에서, 그리고 이 부인에게서 받은 평범한 준비를 빼면 그렇습니다,\" 돔비 씨가 핍친 부인을 소개하며 대답했다."], ["replied Mr Dombey, introducing Mrs Pipchin, who instantly communicated a rigidity to her whole muscular system, and snorted defiance beforehand, in case the Doctor should disparage her; “except so far, Paul has, as yet, applied himself to no studies at all.”", "그러자 그녀는 즉각 온몸의 근육을 뻣뻣하게 굳히고는, 박사가 자기를 깎아내릴 경우에 대비하여 미리부터 콧방귀로 도전장을 내밀었다. \"그 정도를 빼면, 폴은 아직껏 어떤 학업에도 손을 댄 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["Doctor Blimber inclined his head, in gentle tolerance of such insignificant poaching as Mrs Pipchin’s, and said he was glad to hear it.", "블림버 박사는 핍친 부인이 한 정도의 그 하찮은 밀렵을 너그러이 봐주는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕이고는, 그 말을 들으니 기쁘다고 했다."], ["It was much more satisfactory, he observed, rubbing his hands, to begin at the foundation.", "두 손을 비비며 그가 말하기를, 기초부터 시작하는 것이 훨씬 만족스럽다는 것이었다."], ["And again he leered at Paul, as if he would have liked to tackle him with the Greek alphabet, on the spot.", "그러고는 다시 폴을 곁눈질했는데, 마치 그 자리에서 당장 그리스어 알파벳으로 그를 붙들고 늘어지고 싶기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["“That circumstance, indeed, Doctor Blimber,”", "\"실은 그 사정만으로도, 블림버 박사님,\""], ["pursued Mr Dombey, glancing at his little son, “and the interview I have already had the pleasure of holding with you, renders any further explanation, and consequently, any further intrusion on your valuable time, so unnecessary, that—”", "돔비 씨가 자기 어린 아들을 흘낏 보며 말을 이었다, \"그리고 제가 이미 박사님과 나누는 기쁨을 누렸던 그 회견만으로도, 더 이상의 설명이, 따라서 박사님의 귀중한 시간에 대한 더 이상의 침범이 너무도 불필요해진지라—\""]], [["“Now, Miss Dombey!”", "\"자, 돔비 아가씨!\""], ["said the acid Pipchin.", "시큼한 핍친 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Permit me,”", "\"잠깐만요,\""], ["said the Doctor, “one moment.", "박사가 말했다, \"잠시만요."], ["Allow me to present Mrs Blimber and my daughter; who will be associated with the domestic life of our young Pilgrim to Parnassus Mrs Blimber,”", "제 아내와 딸을 소개하게 해 주십시오. 파르나소스를 향한 우리 어린 순례자의 가정생활에 함께하게 될 사람들입니다."], ["for the lady, who had perhaps been in waiting, opportunely entered, followed by her daughter, that fair Sexton in spectacles, “Mr Dombey.", "블림버 부인이오,\" 마침 대기하고 있었던 듯한 그 부인이 때맞춰 들어섰고, 그 뒤로 안경 쓴 그 어여쁜 묘지기인 딸이 따라 들어왔다, \"돔비 씨일세."], ["My daughter Cornelia, Mr Dombey.", "제 딸 코닐리아입니다, 돔비 씨."], ["Mr Dombey, my love,”", "돔비 씨는, 여보,\""], ["pursued the Doctor, turning to his wife, “is so confiding as to—do you see our little friend?”", "박사가 아내 쪽으로 돌아서며 말을 이었다, \"이렇게도 우리를 믿어 주셔서—우리 어린 친구가 보이시오?\""]], [["Mrs Blimber, in an excess of politeness, of which Mr Dombey was the object, apparently did not, for she was backing against the little friend, and very much endangering his position on the table.", "블림버 부인은 돔비 씨를 향한 지나친 공손함에 빠져 있던 나머지, 어린 친구가 보이지 않았던 모양이다. 그녀가 어린 친구 쪽으로 뒷걸음질을 치는 바람에 탁자 위 그의 자리가 몹시 위태로워졌으니 말이다."], ["But, on this hint, she turned to admire his classical and intellectual lineaments, and turning again to Mr Dombey, said, with a sigh, that she envied his dear son.", "그러나 그 귀띔에 그녀는 돌아서서 그의 고전적이고 지적인 이목구비를 감탄하며 바라보았고, 다시 돔비 씨 쪽으로 돌아서더니, 한숨을 쉬며 그 사랑스러운 아드님이 부럽다고 말했다."]], [["“Like a bee, Sir,”", "\"꼭 한 마리 벌 같답니다, 나리,\""], ["said Mrs Blimber, with uplifted eyes, “about to plunge into a garden of the choicest flowers, and sip the sweets for the first time Virgil, Horace, Ovid, Terence, Plautus, Cicero.", "블림버 부인이 눈을 치켜뜨며 말했다, \"더없이 빼어난 꽃들이 핀 정원으로 막 뛰어들어, 그 단꿀을 처음으로 맛보려는 벌이지요—베르길리우스, 호라티우스, 오비디우스, 테렌티우스, 플라우투스, 키케로."], ["What a world of honey have we here.", "여기 얼마나 꿀이 넘쳐 나는 세계가 펼쳐져 있는지요."], ["It may appear remarkable, Mr Dombey, in one who is a wife—the wife of such a husband—”", "아내 된 자가—그것도 이런 남편을 둔 아내가—이런 말을 하는 게 기이하게 보일는지 모르나—\""]], [["“Hush, hush,”", "\"쉿, 쉿,\""], ["said Doctor Blimber.", "블림버 박사가 말했다."], ["“Fie for shame.”", "\"원, 부끄럽지도 않소.\""]], [["“Mr Dombey will forgive the partiality of a wife,”", "\"돔비 씨께서야 아내의 편애를 용서해 주시겠지요,\""], ["said Mrs Blimber, with an engaging smile.", "블림버 부인이 사람을 끄는 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["Mr Dombey answered “Not at all:”", "돔비 씨는 \"천만에요\"라고 답했다."], ["applying those words, it is to be presumed, to the partiality, and not to the forgiveness.", "짐작건대 그 말은 용서가 아니라 편애 쪽에 갖다 붙인 것이리라."]], [["“And it may seem remarkable in one who is a mother also,”", "\"그리고 어미 된 자가 하는 말로도 기이하게 보일는지 모르나,\""], ["resumed Mrs Blimber.", "블림버 부인이 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["“And such a mother,”", "\"그것도 이런 어머니께서요,\""], ["observed Mr Dombey, bowing with some confused idea of being complimentary to Cornelia.", "돔비 씨가 코닐리아에게 무언가 어렴풋이 찬사를 보낸다는 생각으로 고개를 숙이며 말했다."]], [["“But really,”", "\"하지만 정말이지,\""], ["pursued Mrs Blimber, “I think if I could have known Cicero, and been his friend, and talked with him in his retirement at Tusculum (beau-ti-ful Tusculum!), I could have died contented.”", "블림버 부인이 말을 이었다, \"만일 제가 키케로를 알 수 있었더라면, 그의 벗이 되어 투스쿨룸의 그 은거지에서 그와 이야기를 나눌 수 있었더라면(아—름다운 투스쿨룸!), 저는 만족하며 죽을 수 있었으리라 생각합니다.\""]], [["A learned enthusiasm is so very contagious, that Mr Dombey half believed this was exactly his case; and even Mrs Pipchin, who was not, as we have seen, of an accommodating disposition generally, gave utterance to a little sound between a groan and a sigh, as if she would have said that nobody but Cicero could have proved a lasting consolation under that failure of the Peruvian Mines, but that he indeed would have been a very Davy-lamp of refuge.", "학문에 대한 열광이란 어찌나 전염성이 강한지, 돔비 씨는 자기 경우도 바로 그러하다고 반쯤 믿게 되었다. 그리고 앞서 보았듯 대체로 너그러운 성미가 못 되는 핍친 부인조차도, 신음과 한숨 사이의 자그마한 소리를 토해 냈는데, 마치 페루 광산의 그 실패 앞에서 키케로 말고는 그 누구도 변치 않는 위안이 되어 줄 수 없었으리라고, 정녕 그라면 피난처의 데이비 안전등 같은 존재가 되었으리라고 말하려는 듯했다. * 데이비 안전등: 험프리 데이비가 발명한 광산용 안전등. 갱내의 폭발을 막아 주는 빛이라는 뜻."]], [["Cornelia looked at Mr Dombey through her spectacles, as if she would have liked to crack a few quotations with him from the authority in question.", "코닐리아는 안경 너머로 돔비 씨를 바라보았는데, 마치 그 화제의 권위자인 키케로에서 인용구 몇 마디를 그와 주고받고 싶기라도 한 듯했다."], ["But this design, if she entertained it, was frustrated by a knock at the room-door.", "그러나 그녀가 그런 의도를 품었다 하더라도, 그것은 방문을 두드리는 노크 소리에 가로막혀 좌절되고 말았다."]], [["“Who is that?”", "\"누구지?\""], ["said the Doctor.", "박사가 말했다."], ["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Come in, Toots; come in.", "들어오게, 투츠. 들어와."], ["Mr Dombey, Sir.”", "돔비 씨일세.\""], ["Toots bowed.", "투츠가 절을 했다."], ["“Quite a coincidence!”", "\"참으로 묘한 우연이로군요!\""], ["said Doctor Blimber.", "블림버 박사가 말했다."], ["“Here we have the beginning and the end.", "\"여기 시작과 끝이 다 모였습니다."], ["Alpha and Omega.", "알파와 오메가지요."], ["Our head boy, Mr Dombey.”", "우리 학교의 수석 학생입니다, 돔비 씨.\""]], [["The Doctor might have called him their head and shoulders boy, for he was at least that much taller than any of the rest.", "박사는 그를 머리와 어깨까지 큰 학생이라 불러도 좋았을 것이다. 그가 적어도 그만큼은 다른 누구보다도 키가 컸기 때문이다."], ["He blushed very much at finding himself among strangers, and chuckled aloud.", "그는 낯선 이들 틈에 끼인 것을 알고 몹시 얼굴을 붉히며 소리 내어 킥킥거렸다."]], [["“An addition to our little Portico, Toots,”", "\"우리 작은 학당에 한 명이 더 늘었네, 투츠,\""], ["said the Doctor; “Mr Dombey’s son.”", "박사가 말했다. \"돔비 씨의 아드님일세.\""]], [["Young Toots blushed again; and finding, from a solemn silence which prevailed, that he was expected to say something, said to Paul, “How are you?”", "어린 투츠는 다시 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그러고는 좌중에 감도는 엄숙한 침묵으로 미루어 자기가 무언가 말을 해야 할 처지임을 알아채고, 폴에게 \"안녕?\""], ["in a voice so deep, and a manner so sheepish, that if a lamb had roared it couldn’t have been more surprising.", "하고 말했는데, 그 목소리가 어찌나 굵고 그 태도가 어찌나 머쓱하던지, 양 한 마리가 으르렁댔다 해도 그보다 더 놀랍지는 않았을 것이다."]], [["“Ask Mr Feeder, if you please, Toots,”", "\"피더 선생에게 좀 일러 주게, 투츠,\""], ["said the Doctor, “to prepare a few introductory volumes for Mr Dombey’s son, and to allot him a convenient seat for study.", "박사가 말했다, \"돔비 씨 아드님을 위해 입문용 책을 몇 권 마련하고, 공부하기 편한 자리를 하나 배정해 달라고 말일세."], ["My dear, I believe Mr Dombey has not seen the dormitories.”", "여보, 돔비 씨께서 아직 기숙사를 못 보신 것 같소.\""]], [["“If Mr Dombey will walk upstairs,”", "\"돔비 씨께서 위층으로 올라와 주신다면,\""], ["said Mrs Blimber, “I shall be more than proud to show him the dominions of the drowsy god.”", "블림버 부인이 말했다, \"잠의 신이 다스리는 영토를 보여 드리게 되어 더없는 영광이겠습니다.\""]], [["With that, Mrs Blimber, who was a lady of great suavity, and a wiry figure, and who wore a cap composed of sky-blue materials, proceeded upstairs with Mr Dombey and Cornelia; Mrs Pipchin following, and looking out sharp for her enemy the footman.", "그 말과 함께, 매우 상냥하고 깡마른 몸매에, 하늘색 천으로 지은 모자를 쓴 블림버 부인이 돔비 씨와 코닐리아를 데리고 위층으로 올라갔다. 핍친 부인이 그 뒤를 따르며, 자기 원수인 그 하인을 매섭게 살피고 다녔다."]], [["While they were gone, Paul sat upon the table, holding Florence by the hand, and glancing timidly from the Doctor round and round the room, while the Doctor, leaning back in his chair, with his hand in his breast as usual, held a book from him at arm’s length, and read.", "그들이 나가 있는 동안, 폴은 탁자 위에 앉아 플로렌스의 손을 잡은 채, 박사에게서 방 안 구석구석으로 겁먹은 듯 눈길을 옮겨 다녔다. 한편 박사는 여느 때처럼 손을 가슴팍에 찌른 채 의자에 등을 기대고, 책을 팔 길이만큼 멀찍이 들고서 읽었다."], ["There was something very awful in this manner of reading.", "그 책 읽는 방식에는 어딘가 무척 무시무시한 데가 있었다. 그토록 단호하고, 무덤덤하고, 굽힐 줄 모르고, 냉혈한 같은 식의 일 처리였다."], ["It was such a determined, unimpassioned, inflexible, cold-blooded way of going to work.", "그 방식은 박사의 얼굴을 고스란히 드러내 보였다. 그리하여 박사가 자기 저자를 향해 미심쩍은 듯 미소 짓거나, 미간을 찌푸리거나, 고개를 가로저으며 \"그런 소리 마시오, 선생; 나는 더 잘 안다오\""], ["It left the Doctor’s countenance exposed to view; and when the Doctor smiled suspiciously at his author, or knit his brows, or shook his head and made wry faces at him, as much as to say, “Don’t tell me, Sir; I know better,” it was terrific.", "하고 말하기라도 하듯 그를 향해 얼굴을 찡그릴 때면, 그 광경은 가공할 만했다."]], [["Toots, too, had no business to be outside the door, ostentatiously examining the wheels in his watch, and counting his half-crowns.", "투츠 역시 문밖에 머물며 보란 듯이 자기 회중시계의 톱니바퀴를 들여다보고 자기 반 크라운짜리 동전들을 세고 있을 까닭이 없었다."], ["But that didn’t last long; for Doctor Blimber, happening to change the position of his tight plump legs, as if he were going to get up, Toots swiftly vanished, and appeared no more.", "그러나 그것도 오래가지 못했다. 블림버 박사가 마침 일어서려는 듯 그 통통하게 죈 다리의 자세를 바꾸자, 투츠는 잽싸게 사라져 다시는 나타나지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["Mr Dombey and his conductress were soon heard coming downstairs again, talking all the way; and presently they re-entered the Doctor’s study.", "이내 돔비 씨와 그 안내자가 줄곧 이야기를 나누며 다시 아래층으로 내려오는 소리가 들렸고, 곧이어 그들은 박사의 서재로 다시 들어섰다."]], [["“I hope, Mr Dombey,”", "\"바라건대, 돔비 씨,\""], ["said the Doctor, laying down his book, “that the arrangements meet your approval.”", "박사가 책을 내려놓으며 말했다, \"이 조처들이 마음에 드셨으면 합니다.\""]], [["“They are excellent, Sir,”", "\"더할 나위 없습니다, 박사님,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Very fair, indeed,”", "\"정말이지 꽤 괜찮군요,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, in a low voice; never disposed to give too much encouragement.", "핍친 부인이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 결코 지나친 격려를 보낼 마음은 없는 그녀였다."]], [["“Mrs Pipchin,”", "\"핍친 부인이,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, wheeling round, “will, with your permission, Doctor and Mrs Blimber, visit Paul now and then.”", "돔비 씨가 휙 돌아서며 말했다, \"박사님과 블림버 부인의 허락을 얻어, 이따금 폴을 찾아오겠습니다.\""]], [["“Whenever Mrs Pipchin pleases,”", "\"핍친 부인께서 원하실 때면 언제든지요,\""], ["observed the Doctor.", "박사가 말했다."]], [["“Always happy to see her,”", "\"언제든 반갑게 맞이하겠습니다,\""], ["said Mrs Blimber.", "블림버 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I think,”", "\"제 생각에는,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “I have given all the trouble I need, and may take my leave.", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"끼쳐야 할 폐는 다 끼친 듯하니, 이만 작별하는 게 좋겠군요."], ["Paul, my child,”", "폴, 내 아들,\""], ["he went close to him, as he sat upon the table.", "그는 탁자 위에 앉아 있는 아이에게 가까이 다가갔다."], ["“Good-bye.”", "\"잘 있거라.\""]], [["“Good-bye, Papa.”", "\"안녕히 가세요, 아빠.\""]], [["The limp and careless little hand that Mr Dombey took in his, was singularly out of keeping with the wistful face.", "돔비 씨가 자기 손에 쥔 그 맥없고 무심한 자그마한 손은, 그 애틋한 얼굴과는 유난히도 어울리지 않았다."], ["But he had no part in its sorrowful expression.", "그러나 그 슬픈 표정에 그는 아무런 몫이 없었다."], ["It was not addressed to him.", "그것은 그를 향한 것이 아니었다. 아니, 아니었다."], ["No, no. To Florence—all to Florence.", "플로렌스를 향한 것이었다—온통 플로렌스를 향한 것이었다."]], [["If Mr Dombey in his insolence of wealth, had ever made an enemy, hard to appease and cruelly vindictive in his hate, even such an enemy might have received the pang that wrung his proud heart then, as compensation for his injury.", "만일 돔비 씨가 그 부의 오만함으로 인해, 좀처럼 누그러지지 않고 그 증오에 있어 잔인하게 앙심을 품는 어떤 원수를 만든 적이 있었다면, 그런 원수조차도 그 순간 그의 오만한 가슴을 쥐어짠 그 고통을, 자기가 입은 상처에 대한 보상으로 흡족히 받아들였을 것이다."]], [["He bent down, over his boy, and kissed him.", "그는 자기 아이 위로 몸을 굽혀 입을 맞추었다."], ["If his sight were dimmed as he did so, by something that for a moment blurred the little face, and made it indistinct to him, his mental vision may have been, for that short time, the clearer perhaps.", "그렇게 하는 동안 그의 시야가, 잠시 그 자그마한 얼굴을 흐릿하게 가려 또렷이 보이지 않게 만든 무언가로 인해 침침해졌다면, 그 짧은 동안만은 어쩌면 그의 마음의 시야가 도리어 더 맑았는지도 모른다."]], [["“I shall see you soon, Paul.", "\"곧 보자꾸나, 폴."], ["You are free on Saturdays and Sundays, you know.”", "알다시피 토요일과 일요일에는 자유로우니까.\""]], [["“Yes, Papa,”", "\"네, 아빠,\""], ["returned Paul: looking at his sister.", "폴이 누이를 바라보며 되받았다."], ["“On Saturdays and Sundays.”", "\"토요일과 일요일에요.\""]], [["“And you’ll try and learn a great deal here, and be a clever man,”", "\"그리고 여기서 많은 걸 배우려고 애써서, 영리한 사내가 되어야지,\""], ["said Mr Dombey; “won’t you?”", "돔비 씨가 말했다. \"그러겠지?\""]], [["“I’ll try,”", "\"애써 볼게요,\""], ["returned the child, wearily.", "아이가 지친 듯 되받았다."]], [["“And you’ll soon be grown up now!”", "\"그러면 너도 이제 곧 어른이 되겠구나!\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["very soon!”", "아주 곧이요!\""], ["replied the child.", "아이가 대답했다."], ["Once more the old, old look passed rapidly across his features like a strange light.", "다시 한번 그 오래되고 오래된 표정이 야릇한 빛처럼 그의 이목구비를 빠르게 스쳐 갔다."], ["It fell on Mrs Pipchin, and extinguished itself in her black dress.", "그것은 핍친 부인에게 내려앉았다가, 그녀의 검은 옷 속에서 꺼지고 말았다."], ["That excellent ogress stepped forward to take leave and to bear off Florence, which she had long been thirsting to do.", "그 갸륵한 식인귀는 작별을 고하고 플로렌스를 데려가려고 앞으로 나섰는데, 그것은 그녀가 오래도록 갈망해 온 일이었다."], ["The move on her part roused Mr Dombey, whose eyes were fixed on Paul.", "그녀의 그 움직임이, 폴에게 눈길을 고정하고 있던 돔비 씨를 일깨웠다."], ["After patting him on the head, and pressing his small hand again, he took leave of Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, and Miss Blimber, with his usual polite frigidity, and walked out of the study.", "그는 폴의 머리를 토닥이고 그 자그마한 손을 다시 한번 꼭 쥔 다음, 여느 때의 그 정중한 냉랭함으로 블림버 박사와 블림버 부인과 블림버 양에게 작별을 고하고 서재를 걸어 나갔다."]], [["Despite his entreaty that they would not think of stirring, Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, and Miss Blimber all pressed forward to attend him to the hall; and thus Mrs Pipchin got into a state of entanglement with Miss Blimber and the Doctor, and was crowded out of the study before she could clutch Florence.", "자기를 배웅하느라 자리에서 일어설 생각일랑 하지 말아 달라는 그의 간청에도 불구하고, 블림버 박사와 블림버 부인과 블림버 양은 모두 앞다투어 그를 현관까지 배웅하러 나섰다. 그리하여 핍친 부인은 블림버 양과 박사 틈에 얽혀 들고 말았고, 미처 플로렌스를 붙잡기도 전에 서재 밖으로 떠밀려 나갔다."], ["To which happy accident Paul stood afterwards indebted for the dear remembrance, that Florence ran back to throw her arms round his neck, and that hers was the last face in the doorway: turned towards him with a smile of encouragement, the brighter for the tears through which it beamed.", "폴은 그 다행스러운 우연 덕분에, 플로렌스가 그의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안으려고 도로 달려왔던 것, 그리고 문간에서 마지막으로 보인 얼굴이 그녀의 얼굴이었던 것을, 그 소중한 추억으로 두고두고 간직하게 되었다. 그 얼굴은 그를 향해, 눈물 사이로 빛나기에 더더욱 환한 격려의 미소를 띤 채 돌려져 있었다."]], [["It made his childish bosom heave and swell when it was gone; and sent the globes, the books, blind Homer and Minerva, swimming round the room.", "그 얼굴이 사라지자 그의 어린 가슴은 들먹이며 부풀어 올랐다. 그리고 지구의들과 책들과 눈먼 호메로스와 미네르바가 방 안을 빙글빙글 헤엄치게 만들었다."], ["But they stopped, all of a sudden; and then he heard the loud clock in the hall still gravely inquiring “how, is, my, lit, tle, friend?", "그러나 그것들은 별안간 멈추었다. 그러고 나서 그는 현관의 그 큰 시계가 여전히 장중하게 \"어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는?"], ["how, is, my, lit, tle, friend?”", "어, 떤, 가, 우, 리, 어, 린, 친, 구, 는?\""], ["as it had done before.", "하고 전처럼 묻고 있는 소리를 들었다."]], [["He sat, with folded hands, upon his pedestal, silently listening.", "그는 두 손을 모은 채 자기 받침대 위에 앉아 잠자코 귀를 기울였다."], ["But he might have answered “weary, weary!", "그러나 그는 이렇게 대답할 수도 있었으리라. \"지쳤어요, 지쳤어!"], ["very lonely, very sad!”", "너무 외로워요, 너무 슬퍼요!\""], ["And there, with an aching void in his young heart, and all outside so cold, and bare, and strange, Paul sat as if he had taken life unfurnished, and the upholsterer were never coming.", "그리고 거기, 어린 가슴속에 쑤시는 듯한 공허함을 안고서, 바깥의 모든 것이 그토록 차갑고, 헐벗고, 낯선 가운데, 폴은 마치 가구 하나 없이 텅 빈 삶을 떠맡았는데 가구장이는 영영 오지 않을 사람처럼 앉아 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII. Paul’s Education", "# CHAPTER XII. Paul’s Education"]], [["After the lapse of some minutes, which appeared an immense time to little Paul Dombey on the table, Doctor Blimber came back.", "탁자 위에 앉은 어린 폴 돔비에게는 한없이 긴 시간처럼 느껴진 몇 분이 흐른 뒤, 블림버 박사가 돌아왔다."], ["The Doctor’s walk was stately, and calculated to impress the juvenile mind with solemn feelings.", "박사의 걸음걸이는 위엄이 있었고, 어린 마음에 엄숙한 감정을 새겨 넣도록 계산된 것이었다."], ["It was a sort of march; but when the Doctor put out his right foot, he gravely turned upon his axis, with a semi-circular sweep towards the left; and when he put out his left foot, he turned in the same manner towards the right.", "그것은 일종의 행진이었다. 다만 박사가 오른발을 내디딜 때면 엄숙하게 자기 축을 중심으로 왼쪽을 향해 반원을 그리며 돌았고, 왼발을 내디딜 때면 같은 식으로 오른쪽을 향해 돌았다."], ["So that he seemed, at every stride he took, to look about him as though he were saying, “Can anybody have the goodness to indicate any subject, in any direction, on which I am uninformed?", "그래서 그는 한 걸음 한 걸음 옮길 때마다 주위를 두루 둘러보는 것 같았으니, 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"어느 방향으로든, 내가 알지 못하는 주제를 누구든 친절히 일러 줄 수 있겠는가?"], ["I rather think not.”", "아무래도 없을 것 같군.\""]], [["Mrs Blimber and Miss Blimber came back in the Doctor’s company; and the Doctor, lifting his new pupil off the table, delivered him over to Miss Blimber.", "블림버 부인과 블림버 양이 박사를 따라 함께 들어왔다. 그리고 박사는 자기의 새 제자를 탁자에서 들어 내려 블림버 양에게 넘겨주었다."]], [["“Cornelia,”", "\"코닐리아,\""], ["said the Doctor, “Dombey will be your charge at first.", "박사가 말했다, \"돔비는 처음에 네가 맡도록 해라."], ["Bring him on, Cornelia, bring him on.”", "가르쳐 놓거라, 코닐리아, 가르쳐 놓아.\""]], [["Miss Blimber received her young ward from the Doctor’s hands; and Paul, feeling that the spectacles were surveying him, cast down his eyes.", "블림버 양은 자기의 어린 피후견인을 박사의 손에서 받았다. 폴은 그 안경이 자기를 살펴보고 있음을 느끼고 눈을 내리깔았다."]], [["“How old are you, Dombey?”", "\"몇 살이니, 돔비?\""], ["said Miss Blimber.", "블림버 양이 말했다."]], [["“Six,”", "\"여섯 살이에요,\""], ["answered Paul, wondering, as he stole a glance at the young lady, why her hair didn’t grow long like Florence’s, and why she was like a boy.", "폴이 대답하며, 그 아가씨를 슬쩍 훔쳐보고는, 어째서 그녀의 머리는 플로렌스처럼 길게 자라지 않았는지, 어째서 그녀가 사내아이 같은지 의아해했다."]], [["“How much do you know of your Latin Grammar, Dombey?”", "\"라틴어 문법은 얼마나 아니, 돔비?\""], ["said Miss Blimber.", "블림버 양이 말했다."]], [["“None of it,”", "\"하나도 몰라요,\""], ["answered Paul.", "폴이 대답했다."], ["Feeling that the answer was a shock to Miss Blimber’s sensibility, he looked up at the three faces that were looking down at him, and said:", "그 대답이 블림버 양의 감수성에 충격을 주었음을 느끼고, 그는 자기를 내려다보고 있는 세 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["“I haven’t been well.", "\"제가 몸이 좋지 않았거든요."], ["I have been a weak child.", "저는 허약한 아이였어요."], ["I couldn’t learn a Latin Grammar when I was out, every day, with old Glubb.", "매일 밖에서 늙은 글럽 할아버지랑 같이 있을 때는 라틴어 문법을 배울 수가 없었어요."], ["I wish you’d tell old Glubb to come and see me, if you please.”", "괜찮으시면, 늙은 글럽 할아버지더러 저를 보러 와 달라고 해 주셨으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“What a dreadfully low name”", "\"어쩜 그렇게 끔찍하게 천한 이름이람,\""], ["said Mrs Blimber.", "블림버 부인이 말했다."], ["“Unclassical to a degree!", "\"고전과는 거리가 멀어도 한참 멀구나!"], ["Who is the monster, child?”", "그 괴물 같은 자가 누구냐, 얘야?\""]], [["“What monster?”", "\"무슨 괴물이요?\""], ["inquired Paul.", "폴이 물었다."]], [["“Glubb,”", "\"글럽 말이다,\""], ["said Mrs Blimber, with a great disrelish.", "블림버 부인이 몹시 못마땅한 투로 말했다."]], [["“He’s no more a monster than you are,”", "\"그 할아버지는 부인보다 더 괴물도 아니에요,\""], ["returned Paul.", "폴이 응수했다."]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["cried the Doctor, in a terrible voice.", "박사가 무시무시한 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Ay, ay, ay?", "\"어, 어, 어?"], ["Aha!", "아하!"], ["What’s that?”", "그게 무슨 소리냐?\""]], [["Paul was dreadfully frightened; but still he made a stand for the absent Glubb, though he did it trembling.", "폴은 끔찍이 겁이 났다. 그래도 그는 자리에 없는 글럽을 위해 버티고 섰다. 떨면서이긴 했지만."]], [["“He’s a very nice old man, Ma’am,”", "\"그분은 아주 좋은 할아버지예요, 부인,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“He used to draw my couch.", "\"제 침상을 끌어 주곤 하셨어요."], ["He knows all about the deep sea, and the fish that are in it, and the great monsters that come and lie on rocks in the sun, and dive into the water again when they’re startled, blowing and splashing so, that they can be heard for miles.", "깊은 바다에 대해서도, 그 안에 사는 물고기에 대해서도, 햇볕을 쬐러 바위에 나와 누워 있다가 놀라면 다시 물속으로 뛰어드는, 그러면서 어찌나 콧김을 내뿜고 물보라를 일으키는지 몇 마일 밖에서도 들리는 커다란 괴물들에 대해서도 다 아세요."], ["There are some creatures, said Paul, warming with his subject, “I don’t know how many yards long, and I forget their names, but Florence knows, that pretend to be in distress; and when a man goes near them, out of compassion, they open their great jaws, and attack him.", "어떤 생물들은요,\" 폴이 자기 화제에 신이 나서 말했다, \"몇 야드나 되는지 모를 만큼 길고, 이름은 잊어버렸지만 플로렌스는 알아요, 그것들이 괴로운 척하다가, 어떤 사람이 가엾어서 가까이 다가가면 커다란 아가리를 벌리고 덤벼들어요."], ["But all he has got to do,”", "하지만 그럴 때 해야 할 일은요,\""], ["said Paul, boldly tendering this information to the very Doctor himself, “is to keep on turning as he runs away, and then, as they turn slowly, because they are so long, and can’t bend, he’s sure to beat them.", "폴이 다른 누구도 아닌 박사 본인에게 이 정보를 대담하게 내놓으며 말했다, \"도망가면서 계속 방향을 트는 거예요. 그러면 그것들은 몸이 너무 길어서 굽힐 수가 없으니까 천천히 돌게 되고, 그래서 분명히 그것들을 따돌릴 수 있어요."], ["And though old Glubb don’t know why the sea should make me think of my Mama that’s dead, or what it is that it is always saying—always saying! he knows a great deal about it.", "그리고 늙은 글럽 할아버지는 어째서 바다가 저를 돌아가신 엄마 생각이 나게 하는지, 또 바다가 늘 무어라고 하는 그 말이—늘 무어라고 하는데!—무엇인지는 모르시지만, 바다에 대해 아주 많이 아세요."], ["And I wish,”", "그래서 저는요,\""], ["the child concluded, with a sudden falling of his countenance, and failing in his animation, as he looked like one forlorn, upon the three strange faces, “that you’d let old Glubb come here to see me, for I know him very well, and he knows me.”", "아이는 갑자기 얼굴빛이 흐려지고 생기가 꺾이며, 그 낯선 세 얼굴을 마치 버림받은 사람처럼 올려다보면서 말을 맺었다, \"늙은 글럽 할아버지가 여기 와서 저를 봐 주게 해 주셨으면 좋겠어요. 저는 그분을 아주 잘 알고, 그분도 저를 아시니까요.\""]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["said the Doctor, shaking his head; “this is bad, but study will do much.”", "박사가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"이건 좋지 않군, 그러나 공부가 많은 것을 해 줄 게다.\""]], [["Mrs Blimber opined, with something like a shiver, that he was an unaccountable child; and, allowing for the difference of visage, looked at him pretty much as Mrs Pipchin had been used to do.", "블림버 부인은 몸을 살짝 떠는 듯한 기색으로, 그 아이가 영문을 알 수 없는 아이라고 단언했다. 그러고는 얼굴 생김새의 차이를 감안하면, 펩친 부인이 늘 그를 바라보던 것과 거의 똑같은 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Take him round the house, Cornelia,”", "\"집 안을 한 바퀴 데리고 다니거라, 코닐리아,\""], ["said the Doctor, “and familiarise him with his new sphere.", "박사가 말했다, \"그래서 이 아이를 자기의 새 영역에 익숙해지게 하여라."], ["Go with that young lady, Dombey.”", "저 아가씨와 함께 가거라, 돔비.\""]], [["Dombey obeyed; giving his hand to the abstruse Cornelia, and looking at her sideways, with timid curiosity, as they went away together.", "돔비는 따랐다. 난해한 코닐리아에게 손을 내맡기고, 함께 걸어가는 동안 머뭇거리는 호기심으로 그녀를 곁눈질하면서."], ["For her spectacles, by reason of the glistening of the glasses, made her so mysterious, that he didn’t know where she was looking, and was not indeed quite sure that she had any eyes at all behind them.", "그녀의 안경은 알의 번들거림 때문에 그녀를 어찌나 신비롭게 만드는지, 그는 그녀가 어디를 보고 있는지 알 수가 없었고, 정말이지 그 뒤에 눈이 있기는 한 것인지조차 영 확신이 서지 않았다."]], [["Cornelia took him first to the schoolroom, which was situated at the back of the hall, and was approached through two baize doors, which deadened and muffled the young gentlemen’s voices.", "코닐리아는 우선 그를 교실로 데려갔다. 교실은 현관홀 뒤쪽에 자리 잡고 있었고, 어린 신사들의 목소리를 죽이고 누그러뜨리는 베이즈 천을 댄 문 두 짝을 거쳐 들어가게 되어 있었다."], ["Here, there were eight young gentlemen in various stages of mental prostration, all very hard at work, and very grave indeed.", "그곳에는 정신적으로 갖가지 단계의 쇠진에 빠진 여덟 명의 어린 신사들이 있었는데, 다들 무척 열심히 매달려 있었고 정말이지 무척 엄숙했다."], ["Toots, as an old hand, had a desk to himself in one corner: and a magnificent man, of immense age, he looked, in Paul’s young eyes, behind it.", "고참인 투츠는 한쪽 구석에 자기만의 책상을 차지하고 있었다. 그 뒤에 앉은 그는, 폴의 어린 눈에는 어마어마하게 나이 많은 당당한 사내로 보였다."]], [["Mr Feeder, B.A., who sat at another little desk, had his Virgil stop on, and was slowly grinding that tune to four young gentlemen.", "다른 작은 책상에 앉은 피더 학사는 자기의 베르길리우스 음전을 잡아당겨 놓고, 그 가락을 네 명의 어린 신사들에게 천천히 갈아 내고 있었다."], ["Of the remaining four, two, who grasped their foreheads convulsively, were engaged in solving mathematical problems; one with his face like a dirty window, from much crying, was endeavouring to flounder through a hopeless number of lines before dinner; and one sat looking at his task in stony stupefaction and despair—which it seemed had been his condition ever since breakfast time.", "나머지 넷 가운데 둘은 발작하듯 이마를 움켜쥔 채 수학 문제를 푸는 데 몰두해 있었고, 하나는 하도 많이 울어 얼굴이 더러운 창문처럼 된 채 저녁 식사 전까지 가망 없이 많은 행을 어떻게든 헤쳐 나가려 애쓰고 있었으며, 하나는 돌처럼 굳은 망연자실과 절망에 빠져 자기 과제를 바라보며 앉아 있었는데—그 모습은 아침 식사 시간 이후로 줄곧 그의 상태였던 것 같았다. Bitherstone."]], [["The appearance of a new boy did not create the sensation that might have been expected.", "새로 온 아이의 등장은 기대했을 법한 소동을 전혀 일으키지 않았다."], ["Mr Feeder, B.A. (who was in the habit of shaving his head for coolness, and had nothing but little bristles on it), gave him a bony hand, and told him he was glad to see him—which Paul would have been very glad to have told him, if he could have done so with the least sincerity.", "피더 선생은(시원하라고 머리를 박박 미는 버릇이 있어서 잔털만 까칠하게 남아 있었다, 문학사 학위 소지자였다) 그에게 뼈마디 굵은 손을 내밀며 만나서 반갑다고 말했다—폴로서는 조금이라도 진심을 담아 그렇게 말할 수만 있었다면 자기도 그 말을 무척 기쁘게 했을 것이었다."], ["Then Paul, instructed by Cornelia, shook hands with the four young gentlemen at Mr Feeder’s desk; then with the two young gentlemen at work on the problems, who were very feverish; then with the young gentleman at work against time, who was very inky; and lastly with the young gentleman in a state of stupefaction, who was flabby and quite cold.", "그런 다음 폴은 코닐리아의 지시에 따라 피더 선생의 책상에 있는 네 명의 어린 신사들과 악수했고, 그다음에는 문제 풀이에 열중하느라 무척 열이 오른 두 어린 신사와, 그다음에는 시간에 쫓겨 작업하느라 잉크투성이가 된 어린 신사와, 마지막으로 멍한 상태에 빠진 어린 신사와 악수했는데, 그 아이는 흐물흐물하고 아주 차가웠다."]], [["Paul having been already introduced to Toots, that pupil merely chuckled and breathed hard, as his custom was, and pursued the occupation in which he was engaged.", "폴은 이미 투츠에게 소개된 터라, 그 학생은 그저 평소 버릇대로 낄낄거리며 거친 숨을 내쉬고는 자기가 하던 일을 계속했다. 그것은 고된 일은 아니었다."], ["It was not a severe one; for on account of his having “gone through”", "그가 (한 가지 이상의 의미에서) 너무 많은 것을 \"거쳐 온\""], ["so much (in more senses than one), and also of his having, as before hinted, left off blowing in his prime, Toots now had licence to pursue his own course of study: which was chiefly to write long letters to himself from persons of distinction, adds “P.", "데다, 앞서 넌지시 비쳤듯이 한창때에 성장을 멈춘 까닭에, 투츠는 이제 제 나름의 공부를 추구해도 좋다는 허락을 받았던 것이다. 그 공부란 주로 명망 있는 인사들로부터 자기 자신에게 긴 편지를 쓰고 \"브라이턴, 서식스, P."], ["Toots, Esquire, Brighton, Sussex,” and to preserve them in his desk with great care.", "투츠 귀하\"라고 덧붙인 다음, 그것을 책상 속에 아주 소중히 간직하는 일이었다."]], [["These ceremonies passed, Cornelia led Paul upstairs to the top of the house; which was rather a slow journey, on account of Paul being obliged to land both feet on every stair, before he mounted another. But they reached their journey’s end at last; and there, in a front room, looking over the wild sea, Cornelia showed him a nice little bed with white hangings, close to the window, on which there was already beautifully written on a card in round text—down strokes very thick, and up strokes very fine—DOMBEY; while two other little bedsteads in the same room were announced, through like means, as respectively appertaining unto BRIGGS and TOZER.", "이 의례가 다 끝나자, 코닐리아는 폴을 데리고 집 꼭대기까지 위층으로 올라갔다. 폴이 다음 계단을 오르기 전에 매번 두 발을 한 계단에 디뎌야 했던 탓에 다소 더딘 여정이었다. 그래도 마침내 그들은 여정의 끝에 다다랐다. 그리고 거기, 거친 바다를 내려다보는 앞쪽 방에서, 코닐리아는 그에게 흰 휘장이 드리워진 자그마하고 아담한 침대를 보여 주었는데, 창문 바로 곁에 놓인 그 침대에는 이미 카드에 둥근 정자체로 아름답게 쓰인 글씨가—내려긋는 획은 아주 굵게, 올려긋는 획은 아주 가늘게—돔비라고 적혀 있었다. 한편 같은 방에 있는 다른 두 개의 작은 침대는 같은 방식으로 각각 브릭스와 토저에게 속한 것임을 알리고 있었다."]], [["Just as they got downstairs again into the hall, Paul saw the weak-eyed young man who had given that mortal offence to Mrs Pipchin, suddenly seize a very large drumstick, and fly at a gong that was hanging up, as if he had gone mad, or wanted vengeance.", "그들이 다시 아래층 현관에 내려서자마자, 폴은 핍친 부인에게 그토록 치명적인 모욕을 안겨 주었던 눈이 약한 젊은이가 갑자기 아주 큰 북채를 움켜쥐고는, 미치기라도 했거나 복수라도 하려는 듯 매달려 있는 징을 향해 달려드는 것을 보았다."], ["Instead of receiving warning, however, or being instantly taken into custody, the young man left off unchecked, after having made a dreadful noise.", "그러나 그 젊은이는 경고를 받지도, 당장 붙들려 가지도 않았고, 무시무시한 소리를 한바탕 내고 나서 제지당하는 일 없이 그만두었다."], ["Then Cornelia Blimber said to Dombey that dinner would be ready in a quarter of an hour, and perhaps he had better go into the schoolroom among his “friends.”", "그러자 코닐리아 블림버가 돔비에게 십오 분 후면 저녁 식사가 준비될 테니 어쩌면 교실로 들어가 자기 \"친구들\" 사이에 끼어 있는 편이 좋겠다고 말했다."]], [["So Dombey, deferentially passing the great clock which was still as anxious as ever to know how he found himself, opened the schoolroom door a very little way, and strayed in like a lost boy: shutting it after him with some difficulty.", "그리하여 돔비는, 여전히 그의 안부를 궁금해하던 그 큰 시계 곁을 정중하게 지나, 교실 문을 아주 조금만 열고는 길 잃은 아이처럼 안으로 들어섰다. 그러고는 다소 애를 써서 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["His friends were all dispersed about the room except the stony friend, who remained immoveable.", "그의 친구들은 돌 같은 친구만 빼고 모두 방 안 여기저기 흩어져 있었고, 그 돌 같은 친구는 꿈쩍도 않고 그대로 있었다."], ["Mr Feeder was stretching himself in his grey gown, as if, regardless of expense, he were resolved to pull the sleeves off.", "피더 선생은 회색 가운을 입은 채 기지개를 켜고 있었는데, 마치 비용 따위는 아랑곳 않고 소매를 통째로 잡아 뜯기로 작정한 것 같았다."]], [["“Heigh ho hum!”", "\"하이 호 흠!\""], ["cried Mr Feeder, shaking himself like a cart-horse.", "피더 선생이 짐마차 끄는 말처럼 몸을 부르르 떨며 외쳤다."], ["“Oh dear me, dear me!", "\"아이고 맙소사, 맙소사!"], ["Ya-a-a-ah!”", "야아아아!\""]], [["Paul was quite alarmed by Mr Feeder’s yawning; it was done on such a great scale, and he was so terribly in earnest.", "폴은 피더 선생의 하품에 적잖이 놀랐다. 어찌나 큰 규모로 했는지, 또 어찌나 끔찍하게 진지하던지."], ["All the boys too (Toots excepted) seemed knocked up, and were getting ready for dinner—some newly tying their neckcloths, which were very stiff indeed; and others washing their hands or brushing their hair, in an adjoining ante-chamber—as if they didn’t think they should enjoy it at all.", "다른 아이들도(투츠만 빼고) 죄다 녹초가 된 듯했고, 저녁 식사를 준비하고 있었다—어떤 아이들은 새로 목도리를 매고 있었는데 그게 정말이지 아주 빳빳했고, 또 어떤 아이들은 옆에 딸린 대기실에서 손을 씻거나 머리를 빗고 있었다—마치 식사를 조금도 즐기지 못할 거라고 여기는 것처럼."]], [["Young Toots who was ready beforehand, and had therefore nothing to do, and had leisure to bestow upon Paul, said, with heavy good nature:", "미리 준비를 끝내서 할 일이 없던, 그래서 폴에게 시간을 내어 줄 여유가 있던 어린 투츠가 묵직한 호의를 담아 말했다."]], [["“Sit down, Dombey.”", "\"앉아, 돔비.\""]], [["“Thank you, Sir,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선배님,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["His endeavouring to hoist himself on to a very high window-seat, and his slipping down again, appeared to prepare Toots’s mind for the reception of a discovery.", "폴이 아주 높은 창가 자리에 기어 올라가려고 애쓰다가 다시 미끄러져 내려오는 모습은, 투츠의 머릿속에 어떤 발견을 받아들일 준비를 시킨 듯했다."]], [["“You’re a very small chap;”", "\"너 참 조그만 녀석이구나,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Sir, I’m small,”", "\"네, 선배님, 저는 작아요,\""], ["returned Paul.", "폴이 대꾸했다."], ["“Thank you, Sir.”", "\"고맙습니다, 선배님.\""]], [["For Toots had lifted him into the seat, and done it kindly too.", "투츠가 그를 들어 자리에 올려놓아 주었는데, 그것도 친절하게 해 주었기 때문이었다."]], [["“Who’s your tailor?”", "\"네 재단사는 누구지?\""], ["inquired Toots, after looking at him for some moments.", "투츠가 잠시 그를 바라본 후 물었다."]], [["“It’s a woman that has made my clothes as yet,”", "\"지금까지 제 옷을 만든 건 어떤 여자분이에요,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."], ["“My sister’s dressmaker.”", "\"제 누나의 양재사예요.\""]], [["“My tailor’s Burgess and Co.,”", "\"내 재단사는 버지스 상회야,\""], ["said Toots.", "투츠가 말했다."], ["“Fash’nable.", "\"고급이지."], ["But very dear.”", "하지만 아주 비싸.\""]], [["Paul had wit enough to shake his head, as if he would have said it was easy to see that; and indeed he thought so.", "폴은 그쯤은 보면 안다는 듯 고개를 끄덕일 정도의 재치는 있었다. 그리고 실제로 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["“Your father’s regularly rich, ain’t he?”", "\"네 아버지는 어김없이 부자지, 그렇지 않아?\""], ["inquired Mr Toots.", "투츠가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, Sir,”", "\"네, 선배님,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."], ["“He’s Dombey and Son.”", "\"저희 아버지는 돔비와 아들이에요.\""]], [["“And which?”", "\"그리고 뭐라고?\""], ["demanded Toots.", "투츠가 따져 물었다."]], [["“And Son, Sir,”", "\"그리고 아들이요, 선배님,\""], ["replied Paul.", "폴이 대답했다."]], [["Mr Toots made one or two attempts, in a low voice, to fix the Firm in his mind; but not quite succeeding, said he would get Paul to mention the name again to-morrow morning, as it was rather important.", "투츠는 낮은 목소리로 그 회사 이름을 머릿속에 새겨 두려고 한두 번 애썼지만, 영 잘되지 않자 내일 아침에 폴에게 그 이름을 다시 한번 말해 달라고 해야겠다고 말했다. 다소 중요한 일이기 때문이라는 것이었다."], ["And indeed he purposed nothing less than writing himself a private and confidential letter from Dombey and Son immediately.", "사실 그가 마음먹은 것은 다름 아니라 곧장 돔비와 아들로부터 자기 자신에게 사적이고 비밀스러운 편지 한 통을 쓰는 일이었다."]], [["By this time the other pupils (always excepting the stony boy) gathered round.", "이 무렵 다른 학생들이(언제나 돌 같은 아이만 빼고) 둘러 모였다. 그들은 예의 바르되 창백했고, 낮은 소리로 말했으며, 어찌나 풀이 죽어 있던지, 그 무리의 전반적인 분위기에 견주면 비더스톤 도련님은 완벽한 밀러* 거나 완전한 농담 책이라 할 만했다."], ["They were polite, but pale; and spoke low; and they were so depressed in their spirits, that in comparison with the general tone of that company, Master Bitherstone was a perfect Miller, or complete Jest", "그렇지만 비더스톤도 나름대로 억울하다는 감정을 품고 있기는 했다. * 밀러: 당대에 널리 읽힌 농담집 「조 밀러의 농담집」을 빗댄 표현으로, 유쾌하고 익살스러운 사람을 뜻한다."]], [["# Book.” And yet he had a sense of injury upon him, too, had Bitherstone.", "# Book.” And yet he had a sense of injury upon him, too, had Bitherstone."]], [["“You sleep in my room, don’t you?”", "책.” 그런데도 비더스톤은 자기 안에 어떤 억울함 같은 것을 품고 있기도 했다."], ["asked a solemn young gentleman, whose shirt-collar curled up the lobes of his ears.", "“너, 내 방에서 같이 자지, 그렇지?” 셔츠 깃이 귓불 끝까지 말려 올라간, 근엄한 어린 신사가 물었다."]], [["“Master Briggs?”", "“브릭스 도련님인가요?”"], ["inquired Paul.", "폴이 물었다."]], [["“Tozer,”", "“토저야,”"], ["said the young gentleman.", "그 어린 신사가 말했다."]], [["Paul answered yes; and Tozer pointing out the stony pupil, said that was Briggs.", "폴이 그렇다고 대답하자, 토저는 그 돌처럼 굳은 학생을 가리키며 저 애가 브릭스라고 했다."], ["Paul had already felt certain that it must be either Briggs or Tozer, though he didn’t know why.", "폴은 이미, 왜인지는 몰라도 브릭스 아니면 토저일 것이 틀림없다고 확신하던 참이었다."]], [["“Is yours a strong constitution?”", "“넌 체질이 튼튼하니?”"], ["inquired Tozer.", "토저가 물었다."]], [["Paul said he thought not.", "폴은 아닌 것 같다고 말했다."], ["Tozer replied that he thought not also, judging from Paul’s looks, and that it was a pity, for it need be.", "토저는 폴의 생김새로 보아 자기 생각에도 아닌 것 같다고, 그건 안된 일이라고, 튼튼할 필요가 있는 것이니까 하고 대답했다."], ["He then asked Paul if he were going to begin with Cornelia; and on Paul saying “yes,”", "그러고는 폴에게 코닐리아부터 시작할 거냐고 물었고, 폴이 “응”"], ["all the young gentlemen (Briggs excepted) gave a low groan.", "하고 대답하자, 어린 신사들은 (브릭스만 빼고) 모두 낮게 신음 소리를 냈다."]], [["It was drowned in the tintinnabulation of the gong, which sounding again with great fury, there was a general move towards the dining-room; still excepting Briggs the stony boy, who remained where he was, and as he was; and on its way to whom Paul presently encountered a round of bread, genteelly served on a plate and napkin, and with a silver fork lying crosswise on the top of it. Doctor Blimber was already in his place in the dining-room, at the top of the table, with Miss Blimber and Mrs Blimber on either side of him. Mr Feeder in a black coat was at the bottom. Paul’s chair was next to Miss Blimber; but it being found, when he sat in it, that his eyebrows were not much above the level of the table-cloth, some books were brought in from the Doctor’s study, on which he was elevated, and on which he always sat from that time— carrying them in and out himself on after occasions, like a little elephant and castle.", "그 소리는 징의 땡땡거림에 묻혀 버렸다. 징이 다시 한번 무시무시하게 울리자, 다들 식당 쪽으로 우르르 움직였다. 다만 돌처럼 굳은 소년 브릭스만은 여전히 있던 자리에, 있던 그대로 남아 있었다. 그에게로 가는 길에 폴은 곧 둥근 빵 하나와 마주쳤는데, 그것은 접시와 냅킨에 점잖게 담겨 있었고 그 위에는 은포크 하나가 가로로 놓여 있었다. 블림버 박사는 이미 식당의 식탁 상석 제자리에 앉아 있었고, 양옆으로 블림버 양과 블림버 부인이 자리했다. 검은 외투를 입은 피더 씨는 말석에 있었다. 폴의 의자는 블림버 양의 옆자리였다. 그러나 그가 거기 앉고 보니 눈썹이 식탁보 높이보다 그리 높지 않다는 것이 드러나, 박사의 서재에서 책 몇 권을 가져와 그 위에 그를 올려 앉혔다. 그때부터 그는 늘 그 위에 앉았으며—나중에는 직접 그 책들을 들고 드나들었으니, 마치 자그마한 코끼리와 망루 같았다."]], [["Grace having been said by the Doctor, dinner began.", "박사가 식전 기도를 올린 뒤, 식사가 시작되었다."], ["There was some nice soup; also roast meat, boiled meat, vegetables, pie, and cheese.", "맛있는 수프가 있었고, 구운 고기, 삶은 고기, 채소, 파이, 치즈도 있었다."], ["Every young gentleman had a massive silver fork, and a napkin; and all the arrangements were stately and handsome.", "어린 신사들은 저마다 묵직한 은포크와 냅킨을 가졌고, 차림새는 모두 위엄 있고 훌륭했다."], ["In particular, there was a butler in a blue coat and bright buttons, who gave quite a winey flavour to the table beer; he poured it out so superbly.", "특히 푸른 외투에 번쩍이는 단추를 단 집사가 있었는데, 그가 어찌나 근사하게 따라 주는지 식탁용 맥주에 제법 포도주 같은 풍미가 감돌았다."]], [["Nobody spoke, unless spoken to, except Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, and Miss Blimber, who conversed occasionally.", "블림버 박사와 블림버 부인, 블림버 양만이 이따금 이야기를 나눌 뿐, 아무도 말을 걸어오지 않으면 입을 열지 않았다."], ["Whenever a young gentleman was not actually engaged with his knife and fork or spoon, his eye, with an irresistible attraction, sought the eye of Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, or Miss Blimber, and modestly rested there.", "어린 신사는 누구든 칼이나 포크나 숟가락에 실제로 매달려 있지 않을 때면, 그 눈이 거역할 수 없는 인력에 끌려 블림버 박사나 블림버 부인이나 블림버 양의 눈을 찾았고, 거기에 얌전히 머물렀다."], ["Toots appeared to be the only exception to this rule.", "투츠만이 이 규칙의 유일한 예외인 듯했다."], ["He sat next Mr Feeder on Paul’s side of the table, and frequently looked behind and before the intervening boys to catch a glimpse of Paul.", "그는 폴이 앉은 쪽 식탁에서 피더 씨 옆자리에 앉아, 사이에 끼인 소년들의 앞뒤로 자주 고개를 빼며 폴을 한 번이라도 보려 했다."]], [["Only once during dinner was there any conversation that included the young gentlemen.", "식사하는 동안 어린 신사들이 끼어든 대화는 단 한 번뿐이었다."], ["It happened at the epoch of the cheese, when the Doctor, having taken a glass of port wine, and hemmed twice or thrice, said:", "그것은 치즈가 나올 무렵에 일어났다. 박사가 포트와인을 한 잔 들고 두세 번 헛기침을 하더니 말했다."]], [["“It is remarkable, Mr Feeder, that the Romans—”", "“피더 군, 참으로 주목할 만한 일이오. 로마인들이—”"]], [["At the mention of this terrible people, their implacable enemies, every young gentleman fastened his gaze upon the Doctor, with an assumption of the deepest interest.", "이 무시무시한 종족, 자기들의 가차 없는 원수의 이름이 나오자, 어린 신사들은 저마다 더없이 깊은 흥미를 가장하며 박사에게 시선을 고정했다."], ["One of the number who happened to be drinking, and who caught the Doctor’s eye glaring at him through the side of his tumbler, left off so hastily that he was convulsed for some moments, and in the sequel ruined Doctor Blimber’s point.", "그중 한 명이 마침 음료를 마시고 있다가 텀블러 옆면 너머로 자기를 노려보는 박사의 눈을 마주치고는 너무 급히 마시기를 멈춘 탓에 한동안 발작을 일으켰고, 결국 블림버 박사의 말머리를 망쳐 놓고 말았다."]], [["“It is remarkable, Mr Feeder,”", "“참으로 주목할 만한 일이오, 피더 군,”"], ["said the Doctor, beginning again slowly, “that the Romans, in those gorgeous and profuse entertainments of which we read in the days of the Emperors, when luxury had attained a height unknown before or since, and when whole provinces were ravaged to supply the splendid means of one Imperial Banquet—”", "박사가 천천히 다시 시작했다. “로마인들이 말이오, 황제들의 시대에 관해 우리가 읽는 그 화려하고 사치스러운 향연들에서—사치가 그 전에도 그 후에도 알려진 적 없는 경지에 이르렀던 때, 단 한 번의 황실 연회에 쓸 그 휘황한 자재를 마련하느라 온 속주들이 약탈당하던 때—”"]], [["Here the offender, who had been swelling and straining, and waiting in vain for a full stop, broke out violently.", "여기서 그 말썽꾸러기, 그동안 부풀어 오르며 안간힘을 쓰고 마침표를 헛되이 기다리던 그자가 격렬하게 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Johnson,”", "“존슨,”"], ["said Mr Feeder, in a low reproachful voice, “take some water.”", "피더 씨가 낮고 나무라는 목소리로 말했다, “물 좀 마시게.”"]], [["The Doctor, looking very stern, made a pause until the water was brought, and then resumed:", "박사는 매우 근엄한 표정으로 물이 올 때까지 잠시 멈추었다가 다시 이었다."]], [["“And when, Mr Feeder—”", "“그리고 말이오, 피더 군—”"]], [["But Mr Feeder, who saw that Johnson must break out again, and who knew that the Doctor would never come to a period before the young gentlemen until he had finished all he meant to say, couldn’t keep his eye off Johnson; and thus was caught in the fact of not looking at the Doctor, who consequently stopped.", "그러나 피더 씨는 존슨이 또 터져 나올 것이 분명하다는 걸 알았고, 박사가 하려는 말을 모두 마치기 전에는 결코 어린 신사들 앞에서 한 마디 끝맺지 않으리라는 것도 알았기에, 존슨에게서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다. 그렇게 그는 박사를 보고 있지 않은 현장을 들켰고, 그 바람에 박사는 또 멈추었다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Sir,”", "“죄송합니다, 선생님,”"], ["said Mr Feeder, reddening.", "피더 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“I beg your pardon, Doctor Blimber.”", "“죄송합니다, 블림버 박사님.”"]], [["“And when,”", "“그리고 말이오,”"], ["said the Doctor, raising his voice, “when, Sir, as we read, and have no reason to doubt—incredible as it may appear to the vulgar—of our time—the brother of Vitellius prepared for him a feast, in which were served, of fish, two thousand dishes—”", "박사가 목소리를 높이며 말했다, “선생, 우리가 읽기로—또 의심할 이유도 없거니와, 우리 시대의 속된 자들에게는 믿기 어려운 일로 보일지언정—비텔리우스의 형이 그를 위해 한 잔치를 마련했는데, 거기에 생선 요리만 이천 접시가 차려졌고—”"]], [["“Take some water, Johnson—dishes, Sir,”", "“물 좀 마시게, 존슨—접시들 말이오, 선생,”"], ["said Mr Feeder.", "피더 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Of various sorts of fowl, five thousand dishes.”", "“온갖 종류의 새 요리가 오천 접시였소.”"]], [["“Or try a crust of bread,”", "“아니면 빵 껍질이라도 좀 베어 물든가,”"], ["said Mr Feeder.", "피더 씨가 말했다."]], [["“And one dish,”", "“그리고 한 접시는,”"], ["pursued Doctor Blimber, raising his voice still higher as he looked all round the table, “called, from its enormous dimensions, the Shield of Minerva, and made, among other costly ingredients, of the brains of pheasants—”", "블림버 박사가 식탁을 빙 둘러보며 목소리를 한층 더 높여 계속했다, “그 어마어마한 크기 때문에 미네르바의 방패라 불렸으며, 그 밖의 값비싼 재료들 가운데 꿩의 골—”"]], [["“Ow, ow, ow!”", "“욱, 욱, 욱!”"], ["(from Johnson.)", "(존슨에게서.)"]], [["“Woodcocks—”", "“멧도요—”"]], [["“Ow, ow, ow!”", "“욱, 욱, 욱!”"]], [["“The sounds of the fish called scari—”", "“스카리라 불리는 물고기의—”"]], [["“You’ll burst some vessel in your head,”", "“자네 머릿속 혈관이 터지겠네,”"], ["said Mr Feeder.", "피더 씨가 말했다."], ["“You had better let it come.”", "“차라리 그냥 터뜨리는 게 낫겠어.”"]], [["“And the spawn of the lamprey, brought from the Carpathian Sea,”", "“그리고 카르파티아 해에서 가져온 칠성장어의 알로 만들어졌소,”"], ["pursued the Doctor, in his severest voice; “when we read of costly entertainments such as these, and still remember, that we have a Titus—”", "박사가 더없이 준엄한 목소리로 계속했다. “이런 값비싼 향연들을 읽으면서도, 우리에게 티투스 같은 자가 있었음을 여전히 떠올린다면—”"]], [["“What would be your mother’s feelings if you died of apoplexy!”", "“자네가 졸중으로 죽으면 자네 어머니 심정이 어떻겠나!” 피더 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr Feeder.", "“도미티아누스 같은 자—”"]], [["“A Domitian—” “And you’re blue, you know,”", "“게다가 자네 얼굴이 새파래졌어, 알겠나,”"], ["said Mr Feeder.", "피더 씨가 말했다."]], [["“A Nero, a Tiberius, a Caligula, a Heliogabalus, and many more, pursued the Doctor; “it is, Mr Feeder—if you are doing me the honour to attend—remarkable; VERY remarkable, Sir—”", "“네로, 티베리우스, 칼리굴라, 헬리오가발루스, 그 밖에도 숱한 자들이 있었으니,” 박사가 계속했다. “그것은, 피더 군—만일 자네가 송구스럽게도 내 말을 듣고 있다면—주목할 만하오. 참으로 주목할 만한 일이오, 선생—”"]], [["But Johnson, unable to suppress it any longer, burst at that moment into such an overwhelming fit of coughing, that although both his immediate neighbours thumped him on the back, and Mr Feeder himself held a glass of water to his lips, and the butler walked him up and down several times between his own chair and the sideboard, like a sentry, it was a full five minutes before he was moderately composed.", "그러나 존슨은 더는 억누를 수가 없어, 바로 그 순간 감당 못 할 기침 발작으로 터져 나오고 말았으니, 양옆의 두 친구가 등을 두드려 주고, 피더 씨가 친히 물잔을 입에 대 주고, 집사가 보초처럼 그를 제 의자와 찬장 사이로 여러 번 걸려 주었음에도, 어지간히 진정되기까지 꼬박 오 분이 걸렸다."], ["Then there was a profound silence.", "그러고 나서는 깊은 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "“여러분,”"], ["said Doctor Blimber, “rise for Grace!", "블림버 박사가 말했다, “기도를 위해 일어서시오!"], ["Cornelia, lift Dombey down”—nothing of whom but his scalp was accordingly seen above the tablecloth.", "코닐리아, 돔비를 내려놓아라.”—그래서 식탁보 위로는 그의 정수리밖에 보이지 않게 되었다."], ["“Johnson will repeat to me tomorrow morning before breakfast, without book, and from the Greek Testament, the first chapter of the Epistle of Saint Paul to the Ephesians.", "“존슨은 내일 아침 식사 전에 책을 보지 않고, 그리스어 신약성서로 사도 바울이 에베소인에게 보낸 편지 제1장을 내게 외워 들려주게."], ["We will resume our studies, Mr Feeder, in half-an-hour.”", "피더 군, 우리는 삼십 분 후에 공부를 다시 시작하겠소.”"]], [["The young gentlemen bowed and withdrew.", "어린 신사들이 절을 하고 물러갔다."], ["Mr Feeder did likewise.", "피더 씨도 그렇게 했다."], ["During the half-hour, the young gentlemen, broken into pairs, loitered arm-in-arm up and down a small piece of ground behind the house, or endeavoured to kindle a spark of animation in the breast of Briggs.", "그 삼십 분 동안 어린 신사들은 둘씩 짝을 지어 팔짱을 끼고 집 뒤의 자그마한 마당을 이리저리 거닐거나, 브릭스의 가슴속에 한 점 생기의 불꽃이라도 지펴 보려 애썼다."], ["But nothing happened so vulgar as play.", "그러나 놀이처럼 속된 일은 결코 일어나지 않았다."], ["Punctually at the appointed time, the gong was sounded, and the studies, under the joint auspices of Doctor Blimber and Mr Feeder, were resumed.", "정해진 시간에 어김없이 징이 울렸고, 블림버 박사와 피더 씨의 합동 주재 아래 공부가 다시 시작되었다."]], [["As the Olympic game of lounging up and down had been cut shorter than usual that day, on Johnson’s account, they all went out for a walk before tea.", "그날은 존슨의 일 때문에 이리저리 거니는 그 올림픽 경기가 평소보다 짧게 잘려 버린 탓에, 그들은 모두 차 마시기 전에 산책을 나갔다."], ["Even Briggs (though he hadn’t begun yet) partook of this dissipation; in the enjoyment of which he looked over the cliff two or three times darkly.", "브릭스마저 (아직 시작도 안 했으면서) 이 방탕에 끼었는데, 그 즐거움 가운데서 그는 두세 번 벼랑 너머를 음울하게 내려다보았다."], ["Doctor Blimber accompanied them; and Paul had the honour of being taken in tow by the Doctor himself: a distinguished state of things, in which he looked very little and feeble.", "블림버 박사가 그들을 따라나섰고, 폴은 박사 본인에게 직접 손을 잡혀 끌려가는 영예를 누렸다. 대단한 상황이었지만, 그 속에서 그는 무척 작고 가냘파 보였다."]], [["Tea was served in a style no less polite than the dinner; and after tea, the young gentlemen rising and bowing as before, withdrew to fetch up the unfinished tasks of that day, or to get up the already looming tasks of to-morrow.", "차는 식사 못지않게 점잖은 방식으로 차려졌다. 차를 마신 뒤 어린 신사들은 앞서처럼 일어나 절을 하고 물러가서는, 그날 끝내지 못한 과제를 마저 하거나, 이미 어렴풋이 다가오는 내일의 과제를 준비했다."], ["In the meantime Mr Feeder withdrew to his own room; and Paul sat in a corner wondering whether Florence was thinking of him, and what they were all about at Mrs Pipchin’s.", "그동안 피더 씨는 자기 방으로 물러갔고, 폴은 한쪽 구석에 앉아 플로렌스가 자기를 생각하고 있을까, 핍친 부인 댁에서는 다들 무얼 하고 있을까 궁금해했다."]], [["Mr Toots, who had been detained by an important letter from the Duke of Wellington, found Paul out after a time; and having looked at him for a long while, as before, inquired if he was fond of waistcoats.", "웰링턴 공작에게서 온 중요한 편지에 붙들려 있던 투츠 씨가 얼마 후 폴을 찾아냈다. 그는 전처럼 한참 동안 폴을 바라보더니, 조끼를 좋아하느냐고 물었다."]], [["Paul said “Yes, Sir.”", "폴은 “네, 좋아해요” 하고 말했다."]], [["“So am I,”", "“나도 그래,”"], ["said Toots.", "투츠가 말했다."]], [["No word more spoke Toots that night; but he stood looking at Paul as if he liked him; and as there was company in that, and Paul was not inclined to talk, it answered his purpose better than conversation.", "투츠는 그날 밤 그 이상은 한 마디도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그는 폴이 마음에 든다는 듯 폴을 바라보며 서 있었고, 거기에 동무가 되어 주는 무엇이 있었기에, 그리고 폴도 말할 기분이 아니었기에, 그것은 대화보다 폴의 뜻에 더 잘 맞았다."]], [["At eight o’clock or so, the gong sounded again for prayers in the dining-room, where the butler afterwards presided over a side-table, on which bread and cheese and beer were spread for such young gentlemen as desired to partake of those refreshments.", "여덟 시쯤 식당에서 기도를 위해 또 한번 징이 울렸다. 그 뒤 집사가 곁상을 주재했는데, 거기에는 그런 다과를 들고 싶어 하는 어린 신사들을 위해 빵과 치즈와 맥주가 차려져 있었다."], ["The ceremonies concluded by the Doctor’s saying, “Gentlemen, we will resume our studies at seven to-morrow;”", "의식은 박사의 “여러분, 우리는 내일 일곱 시에 공부를 다시 시작하겠소”라는 말로 끝났다."], ["and then, for the first time, Paul saw Cornelia Blimber’s eye, and saw that it was upon him.", "그리고 그때 처음으로 폴은 코닐리아 블림버의 눈을 보았고, 그 눈이 자기에게 향해 있음을 보았다."], ["When the Doctor had said these words, “Gentlemen, we will resume our studies at seven tomorrow,” the pupils bowed again, and went to bed.", "박사가 “여러분, 우리는 내일 일곱 시에 공부를 다시 시작하겠소”라고 말하자, 학생들은 다시 절을 하고 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["In the confidence of their own room upstairs, Briggs said his head ached ready to split, and that he should wish himself dead if it wasn’t for his mother, and a blackbird he had at home.", "위층 자기들 방의 은밀함 속에서, 브릭스는 머리가 쪼개질 듯이 아프다고, 어머니와 집에 두고 온 검은지빠귀 한 마리만 아니었다면 차라리 죽고 싶다고 말했다."], ["Tozer didn’t say much, but he sighed a good deal, and told Paul to look out, for his turn would come to-morrow.", "토저는 별말은 하지 않았으나 한숨을 잔뜩 내쉬며, 내일은 네 차례가 올 테니 조심하라고 폴에게 일렀다."], ["After uttering those prophetic words, he undressed himself moodily, and got into bed.", "그 예언 같은 말을 내뱉은 뒤, 그는 침울하게 옷을 벗고 침대로 들어갔다."], ["Briggs was in his bed too, and Paul in his bed too, before the weak-eyed young man appeared to take away the candle, when he wished them good-night and pleasant dreams.", "눈이 약한 젊은이가 촛불을 가지러 나타나 잘 자고 좋은 꿈 꾸라고 인사할 무렵에는, 브릭스도 침대에, 폴도 침대에 들어가 있었다."], ["But his benevolent wishes were in vain, as far as Briggs and Tozer were concerned; for Paul, who lay awake for a long while, and often woke afterwards, found that Briggs was ridden by his lesson as a nightmare: and that Tozer, whose mind was affected in his sleep by similar causes, in a minor degree talked unknown tongues, or scraps of Greek and Latin—it was all one to Paul—which, in the silence of night, had an inexpressibly wicked and guilty effect.", "그러나 그의 자비로운 소원은 브릭스와 토저에 관한 한 헛된 것이었으니, 오래도록 잠 못 이루다가 그 뒤에도 자주 깬 폴이 보니, 브릭스는 자기 수업에 악몽처럼 짓눌려 있었고, 잠결에 비슷한 이유로 정신이 시달린 토저는 그보다 덜한 정도로 알 수 없는 말—그리스어와 라틴어 토막들—을 중얼거렸는데, 폴에게는 그게 다 그게 그거였지만, 밤의 정적 속에서 그것은 말할 수 없이 사악하고 죄스러운 느낌을 자아냈다."]], [["Paul had sunk into a sweet sleep, and dreamed that he was walking hand in hand with Florence through beautiful gardens, when they came to a large sunflower which suddenly expanded itself into a gong, and began to sound.", "폴은 달콤한 잠에 빠져들어, 플로렌스와 손을 잡고 아름다운 정원들을 거니는 꿈을 꾸었는데, 그들이 커다란 해바라기 한 송이에 이르자 그것이 별안간 징으로 부풀어 오르더니 울리기 시작했다."], ["Opening his eyes, he found that it was a dark, windy morning, with a drizzling rain: and that the real gong was giving dreadful note of preparation, down in the hall.", "눈을 떠 보니, 어둡고 바람 부는 아침이었고 가랑비가 보슬보슬 내리고 있었다. 그리고 진짜 징이 아래층 현관에서 무시무시한 준비의 신호를 울리고 있었다."]], [["So he got up directly, and found Briggs with hardly any eyes, for nightmare and grief had made his face puffy, putting his boots on: while Tozer stood shivering and rubbing his shoulders in a very bad humour.", "그래서 그는 곧장 일어났는데, 보니 악몽과 슬픔으로 얼굴이 퉁퉁 부어 눈이 거의 보이지 않는 브릭스가 부츠를 신고 있었고, 토저는 부들부들 떨며 어깨를 문지르면서 몹시 기분이 좋지 않은 채 서 있었다. 가엾은 폴은 익숙지 않아서 옷을 쉽게 입지 못했다."], ["Poor Paul couldn’t dress himself easily, not being used to it, and asked them if they would have the goodness to tie some strings for him; but as Briggs merely said “Bother!” and Tozer, “Oh yes!”", "그래서 끈 몇 개를 좀 매어 주는 친절을 베풀어 주지 않겠느냐고 물었지만, 브릭스는 그저 “귀찮게!”라고만 하고 토저는 “오 그래!”라고만 하기에, 그는 그 밖의 채비를 마친 뒤 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["he went down when he was otherwise ready, to the next storey, where he saw a pretty young woman in leather gloves, cleaning a stove.", "한 층 아래에서 그는 가죽 장갑을 낀 예쁜 젊은 여자가 난로를 닦고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The young woman seemed surprised at his appearance, and asked him where his mother was.", "그 젊은 여자는 그가 나타난 것에 놀란 듯, 어머니가 어디 계시냐고 물었다."], ["When Paul told her she was dead, she took her gloves off, and did what he wanted; and furthermore rubbed his hands to warm them; and gave him a kiss; and told him whenever he wanted anything of that sort—meaning in the dressing way—to ask for “Melia; which Paul, thanking her very much, said he certainly would.", "폴이 어머니는 돌아가셨다고 하자, 그녀는 장갑을 벗고 그가 바라던 일을 해 주었다. 게다가 그의 손을 비벼 따뜻하게 데워 주고, 입을 맞추어 주고는, 그런 종류의 일이—옷 입는 쪽으로—필요할 때면 언제든 ‘밀리아’를 찾으라고 일러 주었다."], ["He then proceeded softly on his journey downstairs, towards the room in which the young gentlemen resumed their studies, when, passing by a door that stood ajar, a voice from within cried, “Is that Dombey?”", "폴은 그녀에게 무척 고마워하며 꼭 그러겠다고 말했다. 그러고는 어린 신사들이 공부를 다시 시작하는 방을 향해 살그머니 아래층 길을 계속 내려가던 중, 빠끔히 열린 문 하나를 지나는데 안에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다."], ["On Paul replying, “Yes, Ma’am:”", "“돔비니?” 폴이 “네, 부인”"], ["for he knew the voice to be Miss Blimber’s: Miss Blimber said, “Come in, Dombey.”", "하고 대답하자—그 목소리가 블림버 양의 것임을 알았기에—블림버 양이 말했다. “들어오너라, 돔비.”"], ["And in he went.", "그래서 그는 들어갔다."]], [["Miss Blimber presented exactly the appearance she had presented yesterday, except that she wore a shawl.", "블림버 양은 숄을 두른 것만 빼면 어제와 똑같은 모습을 하고 있었다."], ["Her little light curls were as crisp as ever, and she had already her spectacles on, which made Paul wonder whether she went to bed in them.", "그녀의 자그마한 밝은색 곱슬머리는 여전히 빳빳했고, 벌써 안경을 쓰고 있어서 폴은 그녀가 안경을 쓴 채 잠자리에 드는 것은 아닐까 궁금해졌다."], ["She had a cool little sitting-room of her own up there, with some books in it, and no fire But Miss Blimber was never cold, and never sleepy.", "그녀는 그 위층에 자기만의 서늘한 작은 거실을 가지고 있었는데, 거기에는 책 몇 권이 있었고 난롯불은 없었다. 그러나 블림버 양은 결코 춥지도, 결코 졸리지도 않았다."]], [["Now, Dombey,”", "“자, 돔비,”"], ["said Miss Blimber, “I am going out for a constitutional.”", "블림버 양이 말했다, “나는 건강 산책을 나갈 거란다.”"]], [["Paul wondered what that was, and why she didn’t send the footman out to get it in such unfavourable weather.", "폴은 그게 뭔지, 또 이렇게 궂은 날씨에 왜 하인을 시켜 받아 오게 하지 않는지 궁금했다."], ["But he made no observation on the subject: his attention being devoted to a little pile of new books, on which Miss Blimber appeared to have been recently engaged.", "그러나 그는 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그의 주의는 블림버 양이 최근에 매달려 있었던 듯한 새 책 한 무더기에 쏠려 있었다."]], [["“These are yours, Dombey,”", "“이건 네 거야, 돔비,”"], ["said Miss Blimber.", "블림버 양이 말했다."]], [["“All of ’em, Ma’am?”", "“전부 다요, 부인?”"], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래,”"], ["returned Miss Blimber; “and Mr Feeder will look you out some more very soon, if you are as studious as I expect you will be, Dombey.”", "블림버 양이 대답했다, “그리고 네가 내 기대대로 공부에 열심이라면, 돔비, 피더 씨가 곧 더 골라 줄 거란다.”"]], [["“Thank you, Ma’am,”", "“고맙습니다, 부인,”"], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“I am going out for a constitutional,”", "“나는 건강 산책을 나갈 거야,” 블림버 양이 다시 말했다."], ["resumed Miss Blimber; “and while I am gone, that is to say in the interval between this and breakfast, Dombey, I wish you to read over what I have marked in these books, and to tell me if you quite understand what you have got to learn.", "“그리고 내가 나가 있는 동안, 그러니까 지금부터 아침 식사까지의 사이에, 돔비, 네가 이 책들에서 내가 표시해 둔 것을 읽어 보고, 네가 배워야 할 것을 충분히 이해했는지 내게 말해 주었으면 한다. 시간을 허비하지 마라, 돔비."], ["Don’t lose time, Dombey, for you have none to spare, but take them downstairs, and begin directly.”", "너에게는 남는 시간이 없으니까. 책을 아래층으로 가지고 내려가서 곧장 시작하렴.”"]], [["“Yes, Ma’am,”", "“네, 부인,”"], ["answered Paul.", "폴이 대답했다."]], [["There were so many of them, that although Paul put one hand under the bottom book and his other hand and his chin on the top book, and hugged them all closely, the middle book slipped out before he reached the door, and then they all tumbled down on the floor.", "책이 어찌나 많았던지, 폴이 한 손은 맨 밑의 책 아래에 받치고 다른 한 손과 턱은 맨 위의 책에 얹고 그 전부를 바짝 끌어안았는데도, 문에 닿기 전에 가운데 책이 미끄러져 빠지더니 죄다 바닥으로 와르르 쏟아졌다."], ["Miss Blimber said, “Oh, Dombey, Dombey, this is really very careless!”", "블림버 양은 “오, 돔비, 돔비, 정말 너무 조심성이 없구나!”"], ["and piled them up afresh for him; and this time, by dint of balancing them with great nicety, Paul got out of the room, and down a few stairs before two of them escaped again.", "하고는 그를 위해 책을 다시 쌓아 주었다. 이번에는 매우 정교하게 균형을 잡은 덕에 폴은 방에서 나가 계단을 몇 칸 내려갔으나, 그러다 두 권이 또 빠져나갔다."], ["But he held the rest so tight, that he only left one more on the first floor, and one in the passage; and when he had got the main body down into the schoolroom, he set off upstairs again to collect the stragglers.", "그래도 나머지는 어찌나 꽉 붙들었던지, 이 층에 한 권을, 복도에 한 권을 더 떨어뜨렸을 뿐이었다. 그리고 본대(本隊)를 교실까지 내려놓고 나서, 그는 낙오병들을 거두러 다시 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["Having at last amassed the whole library, and climbed into his place, he fell to work, encouraged by a remark from Tozer to the effect that he “was in for it now;”", "마침내 장서 전부를 모아 자기 자리로 기어 올라간 그는, 토저가 “이제 너도 꼼짝없이 걸려들었군” 하고 한 말에 격려를 받으며 일에 착수했다."], ["which was the only interruption he received till breakfast time.", "그 말이 아침 식사 시간까지 그가 받은 유일한 방해였다."], ["At that meal, for which he had no appetite, everything was quite as solemn and genteel as at the others; and when it was finished, he followed Miss Blimber upstairs.", "그 식사 때—그에게는 입맛이 전혀 없었지만—모든 것이 다른 끼니들과 똑같이 엄숙하고 점잖았다. 식사가 끝나자, 그는 블림버 양을 따라 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["“Now, Dombey,”", "“자, 돔비,”"], ["said Miss Blimber.", "블림버 양이 말했다."], ["“How have you got on with those books?”", "“그 책들은 어떻게 되어 가고 있니?”"]], [["They comprised a little English, and a deal of Latin—names of things, declensions of articles and substantives, exercises thereon, and preliminary rules—a trifle of orthography, a glance at ancient history, a wink or two at modern ditto, a few tables, two or three weights and measures, and a little general information.", "그 책들에는 영어가 조금, 라틴어가 잔뜩 들어 있었다—사물의 이름들, 관사와 명사의 격변화, 그에 관한 연습 문제와 예비 규칙들—철자법 약간, 고대사 한 번 훑기, 근대사 한두 번 곁눈질하기, 표 몇 개, 도량형 두세 가지, 그리고 일반 상식 약간."], ["When poor Paul had spelt out number two, he found he had no idea of number one; fragments whereof afterwards obtruded themselves into number three, which slided into number four, which grafted itself on to number two.", "가엾은 폴이 둘째 항목을 더듬더듬 익히고 나니, 첫째 항목은 도무지 모르겠는 것이었다. 그리고 그 첫째 항목의 조각들이 나중에 셋째 항목으로 비집고 들어왔고, 셋째 항목은 넷째 항목으로 미끄러졌으며, 넷째 항목은 다시 둘째 항목에 접붙었다."], ["So that whether twenty Romuluses made a Remus, or hic haec hoc was troy weight, or a verb always agreed with an ancient Briton, or three times four was Taurus a bull, were open questions with him.", "그래서 로물루스 스무 명이 모이면 레무스 하나가 되는지, ‘히크 하이크 호크’가 트로이 중량인지, 동사가 늘 고대 브리튼인과 일치하는지, 사 곱하기 삼이 황소 타우루스인지 하는 것들이 그에게는 모두 미해결의 문제로 남았다."]], [["“Oh, Dombey, Dombey!”", "“오, 돔비, 돔비!”"], ["said Miss Blimber, “this is very shocking.”", "블림버 양이 말했다, “이건 정말 형편없구나.”"]], [["“If you please,”", "“괜찮으시다면,”"], ["said Paul, “I think if I might sometimes talk a little to old Glubb, I should be able to do better.”", "폴이 말했다, “제 생각엔 이따금 늙은 글럽 아저씨하고 조금씩 이야기를 나눌 수 있다면, 더 잘할 수 있을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Nonsense, Dombey,”", "“말도 안 되는 소리, 돔비,”"], ["said Miss Blimber.", "블림버 양이 말했다."], ["“I couldn’t hear of it.", "“그런 건 들어 줄 수 없어."], ["This is not the place for Glubbs of any kind.", "여긴 어떤 종류든 글럽 같은 자가 있을 자리가 아니야."], ["You must take the books down, I suppose, Dombey, one by one, and perfect yourself in the day’s instalment of subject A, before you turn at all to subject B.", "책을 한 권씩 가지고 내려가야 할 게다, 돔비. 그리고 B 과목으로 넘어가기 전에 우선 그날 분량의 A 과목을 완벽히 익혀야 해."], ["I am sorry to say, Dombey, that your education appears to have been very much neglected.”", "유감스럽지만, 돔비, 네 교육이 너무 많이 소홀히 되어 온 것 같구나.”"]], [["“So Papa says,”", "“아빠도 그렇게 말씀하세요,”"], ["returned Paul; “but I told you—I have been a weak child.", "폴이 대답했다. “하지만 제가 말씀드렸잖아요—저는 허약한 아이였어요."], ["Florence knows I have.", "플로렌스도 그걸 알아요."], ["So does Wickam.”", "위컴도 알고요.”"]], [["“Who is Wickam?”", "“위컴이 누구지?”"], ["asked Miss Blimber.", "블림버 양이 물었다."]], [["“She has been my nurse,”", "“제 유모였어요,”"], ["Paul answered.", "폴이 대답했다."]], [["“I must beg you not to mention Wickam to me, then,”", "“그렇다면 내게 위컴 이야기는 꺼내지 말아 주었으면 한다,”"], ["said Miss Blimber.", "블림버 양이 말했다."], ["“I couldn’t allow it”.", "“그건 허용할 수 없어.”"]], [["“You asked me who she was,”", "“부인이 누구냐고 물으셨잖아요,”"], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“Very well,”", "“좋아,”"], ["returned Miss Blimber; “but this is all very different indeed from anything of that sort, Dombey, and I couldn’t think of permitting it.", "블림버 양이 대답했다. “하지만 이건 그런 따위와는 정말이지 전혀 다른 거야, 돔비."], ["As to having been weak, you must begin to be strong.", "그리고 나는 그런 걸 허락할 생각이 없다. 허약했던 것으로 말하면, 이제는 튼튼해지기 시작해야지."], ["And now take away the top book, if you please, Dombey, and return when you are master of the theme.”", "자, 이제 맨 위의 책을 가져가렴, 돔비. 그리고 그 주제를 완전히 터득하거든 돌아오너라.”"]], [["Miss Blimber expressed her opinions on the subject of Paul’s uninstructed state with a gloomy delight, as if she had expected this result, and were glad to find that they must be in constant communication.", "블림버 양은 폴의 무지한 상태에 대해 자기 의견을 음울한 기쁨으로 표명했는데, 마치 이런 결과를 예상하고 있었고, 그래서 둘이 끊임없이 소통해야만 하리라는 것을 알게 되어 기뻐하는 듯했다. 폴은 시킨 대로 맨 위의 과제를 가지고 물러나, 아래층에서 그것에 매달려 끙끙댔다."], ["Paul withdrew with the top task, as he was told, and laboured away at it, down below: sometimes remembering every word of it, and sometimes forgetting it all, and everything else besides: until at last he ventured upstairs again to repeat the lesson, when it was nearly all driven out of his head before he began, by Miss Blimber’s shutting up the book, and saying, “Go on, Dombey!”", "어떤 때는 한 마디 한 마디 다 기억하다가도, 어떤 때는 그것을 전부, 또 그 밖의 모든 것까지 다 잊어버렸다. 그러다 마침내 그 수업을 외워 보려고 다시 위층으로 올라갔는데, 시작하기도 전에 블림버 양이 책을 탁 덮고 “계속해 봐, 돔비!”"], ["a proceeding so suggestive of the knowledge inside of her, that Paul looked upon the young lady with consternation, as a kind of learned Guy Fawkes, or artificial Bogle, stuffed full of scholastic straw.", "하고 말하는 바람에 머릿속의 것이 거의 다 쫓겨 나가 버렸다. 그것은 그녀 안에 든 지식을 너무도 잘 암시하는 행동이어서, 폴은 그 젊은 숙녀를 일종의 박학한 가이 포크스, 혹은 학문적인 짚으로 가득 채워진 인조 허수아비 같은 존재로 여기며 경악하여 바라보았다."]], [["He acquitted himself very well, nevertheless; and Miss Blimber, commending him as giving promise of getting on fast, immediately provided him with subject B; from which he passed to C, and even D before dinner.", "* 가이 포크스: 1605년 영국 의회 폭파 음모(화약 음모 사건)의 가담자로, 해마다 그의 모형 인형을 만들어 태우는 풍습이 있었다. 그래도 그는 매우 훌륭히 해냈고, 블림버 양은 빠르게 발전할 가능성을 보여 준다고 그를 칭찬하며 즉시 B 과목을 내주었다."], ["It was hard work, resuming his studies, soon after dinner; and he felt giddy and confused and drowsy and dull.", "거기서 그는 저녁 식사 전에 C로, 심지어 D로까지 넘어갔다. 식사 후 얼마 지나지 않아 공부를 다시 시작하는 것은 고된 일이었다."], ["But all the other young gentlemen had similar sensations, and were obliged to resume their studies too, if there were any comfort in that.", "그는 어지럽고 혼란스럽고 졸리고 멍했다. 그러나 다른 어린 신사들도 모두 비슷한 느낌이었고, 그들 역시 공부를 다시 시작해야만 했으니, 거기에 무슨 위안이라도 있다면 말이다."], ["It was a wonder that the great clock in the hall, instead of being constant to its first inquiry, never said, “Gentlemen, we will now resume our studies,”", "현관의 그 커다란 시계가 처음의 그 물음에만 한결같지 않고 “여러분, 이제 공부를 다시 시작합시다”라고 말하지 않은 것이 신기할 정도였다."], ["for that phrase was often enough repeated in its neighbourhood.", "그 어구가 그 근방에서 충분히 자주 되풀이되었으니까."], ["The studies went round like a mighty wheel, and the young gentlemen were always stretched upon it.", "공부는 거대한 바퀴처럼 빙빙 돌았고, 어린 신사들은 늘 그 위에 늘여 매달려 있었다."]], [["After tea there were exercises again, and preparations for next day by candlelight.", "차를 마신 뒤에는 또 연습 문제와, 촛불 아래의 내일 준비가 있었다."], ["And in due course there was bed; where, but for that resumption of the studies which took place in dreams, were rest and sweet forgetfulness.", "그리고 차례가 되어 잠자리가 있었으니, 그곳에서는 꿈속에서 일어나는 그 공부의 재개만 아니라면, 안식과 달콤한 망각이 있었다."]], [["Oh Saturdays!", "오 토요일이여!"], ["Oh happy Saturdays, when Florence always came at noon, and never would, in any weather, stay away, though Mrs Pipchin snarled and growled, and worried her bitterly.", "오 행복한 토요일이여, 그날이면 플로렌스는 늘 정오에 왔고, 핍친 부인이 으르렁대고 투덜대며 모질게 괴롭혀도, 어떤 날씨에도 결코 오지 않는 법이 없었다."], ["Those Saturdays were Sabbaths for at least two little Christians among all the Jews, and did the holy Sabbath work of strengthening and knitting up a brother’s and a sister’s love.", "그 토요일들은 그 모든 유대인 가운데 적어도 두 어린 그리스도인에게는 안식일이었으며, 오누이의 사랑을 굳건히 하고 단단히 엮어 주는 거룩한 안식일의 일을 해 주었다."]], [["Not even Sunday nights—the heavy Sunday nights, whose shadow darkened the first waking burst of light on Sunday mornings—could mar those precious Saturdays.", "일요일 밤들조차—그 무거운 일요일 밤들, 그 그림자가 일요일 아침에 처음 깨어나며 터져 나오는 빛을 어둡게 했던 그 밤들조차—그 소중한 토요일들을 망칠 수는 없었다."], ["Whether it was the great sea-shore, where they sat, and strolled together; or whether it was only Mrs Pipchin’s dull back room, in which she sang to him so softly, with his drowsy head upon her arm; Paul never cared.", "그들이 함께 앉고 거닐던 그 드넓은 바닷가였든, 아니면 그녀가 졸린 그의 머리를 자기 팔에 기대게 하고 그토록 부드럽게 노래를 불러 주던 핍친 부인의 칙칙한 뒷방일 뿐이었든, 폴은 결코 개의치 않았다."], ["It was Florence.", "그것은 플로렌스였다."], ["That was all he thought of.", "그가 생각하는 것은 그뿐이었다."], ["So, on Sunday nights, when the Doctor’s dark door stood agape to swallow him up for another week, the time was come for taking leave of Florence; no one else.", "그래서 일요일 밤, 박사의 어두운 문이 그를 또 한 주일 동안 삼키려고 입을 벌리고 서 있을 때, 작별할 시간이 온 것은 플로렌스였다. 다른 누구도 아니었다."]], [["Mrs Wickam had been drafted home to the house in town, and Miss Nipper, now a smart young woman, had come down.", "위컴 부인은 시내의 집으로 다시 차출되어 갔고, 이제 맵시 있는 젊은 여자가 된 니퍼 양이 내려와 있었다."], ["To many a single combat with Mrs Pipchin, did Miss Nipper gallantly devote herself, and if ever Mrs Pipchin in all her life had found her match, she had found it now.", "니퍼 양은 핍친 부인과의 숱한 일대일 결투에 용감히 자신을 바쳤고, 만일 핍친 부인이 평생 단 한 번이라도 자기와 맞먹는 적수를 만난 적이 있다면, 바로 이제 그를 만난 것이었다."], ["Miss Nipper threw away the scabbard the first morning she arose in Mrs Pipchin’s house.", "니퍼 양은 핍친 부인 댁에서 처음 일어난 그 아침에 칼집을 내던져 버렸다."], ["She asked and gave no quarter.", "그녀는 자비를 구하지도 베풀지도 않았다."], ["She said it must be war, and war it was; and Mrs Pipchin lived from that time in the midst of surprises, harassings, and defiances, and skirmishing attacks that came bouncing in upon her from the passage, even in unguarded moments of chops, and carried desolation to her very toast.", "그녀는 이것은 전쟁이어야 한다고 했고, 정말 전쟁이었다. 그때부터 핍친 부인은 기습과 시달림과 도전, 그리고 복도에서 튀어 들어오는 소규모 공격들 한가운데서 살았으니, 그 공격은 양고기 토막을 먹는 무방비한 순간에마저 들이닥쳐 그녀의 토스트에까지 황폐함을 안겨 주었다."]], [["Miss Nipper had returned one Sunday night with Florence, from walking back with Paul to the Doctor’s, when Florence took from her bosom a little piece of paper, on which she had pencilled down some words.", "어느 일요일 밤 니퍼 양은 폴을 박사 댁까지 다시 데려다주고 플로렌스와 함께 돌아온 참이었는데, 그때 플로렌스가 가슴에서 작은 종잇조각을 꺼냈다. 거기에는 그녀가 연필로 적어 둔 몇 마디 말이 있었다."]], [["“See here, Susan,” she said.", "“이거 봐, 수전,”"], ["“These are the names of the little books that Paul brings home to do those long exercises with, when he is so tired.", "그녀가 말했다. “이건 폴이 그렇게 지칠 때 그 긴 연습 문제를 하려고 집에 가져오는 작은 책들의 이름이야."], ["I copied them last night while he was writing.”", "어젯밤에 걔가 쓰고 있는 동안 베껴 둔 거란다.”"]], [["“Don’t show ’em to me, Miss Floy, if you please,”", "“제발 그건 제게 보여 주지 마세요, 플로이 아가씨,”"], ["returned Nipper, “I’d as soon see Mrs Pipchin.”", "니퍼가 대답했다. “그럴 바엔 핍친 부인을 보는 게 낫겠어요.”"]], [["“I want you to buy them for me, Susan, if you will, tomorrow morning.", "“네가 이걸 나 대신 사다 줬으면 해, 수전, 괜찮다면 내일 아침에."], ["I have money enough,”", "나 돈은 충분히 있어,”"], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Why, goodness gracious me, Miss Floy,” returned Miss Nipper, “how can you talk like that, when you have books upon books already, and masterses and mississes a teaching of you everything continual, though my belief is that your Pa, Miss Dombey, never would have learnt you nothing, never would have thought of it, unless you’d asked him—when he couldn’t well refuse; but giving consent when asked, and offering when unasked, Miss, is quite two things; I may not have my objections to a young man’s keeping company with me, and when he puts the question, may say ‘yes,’ but that’s not saying ‘would you be so kind as like me.’”", "“아니, 세상에 맙소사, 플로이 아가씨,” 니퍼 양이 대답했다, “책에 책이 또 있고, 선생님이며 사모님이며 온갖 걸 끊임없이 가르치는데 어떻게 그런 말씀을 하세요. 하기야 제 생각엔, 돔비 아가씨, 아가씨 아버님은 아가씨가 청하기 전엔 아무것도 가르치려 하지 않았을 거예요, 생각조차 안 하셨을 거고요—청하면 차마 거절은 못 하셨겠지만요. 하지만 청할 때 승낙하는 것과 청하지 않아도 먼저 내미는 것은, 아가씨, 전혀 다른 두 가지예요. 어떤 청년이 저랑 사귀는 걸 제가 마다하지 않을 수도 있고, 그 사람이 청혼하면 ‘예’라고 할 수도 있지만, 그건 ‘부디 저를 좋아해 주시겠어요’라고 말하는 거랑은 다르잖아요.”"]], [["“But you can buy me the books, Susan; and you will, when you know why I want them.”", "“하지만 넌 그 책들을 사다 줄 수 있잖아, 수전. 그리고 내가 왜 그게 필요한지 알면, 너도 사다 줄 거야.”"]], [["“Well, Miss, and why do you want ’em?”", "“그럼요, 아가씨, 그런데 그건 왜 필요하신데요?”"], ["replied Nipper; adding, in a lower voice, “If it was to fling at Mrs Pipchin’s head, I’d buy a cart-load.”", "니퍼가 대답하고는 더 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. “그게 핍친 부인 머리통에 던지려는 거라면, 한 수레라도 사 오겠어요.”"]], [["“Paul has a great deal too much to do, Susan,”", "“폴은 할 일이 너무 많아, 수전,”"], ["said Florence, “I am sure of it.”", "플로렌스가 말했다. “나 확신해.”"]], [["“And well you may be, Miss,”", "“정말 그러실 만해요, 아가씨,”"], ["returned her maid, “and make your mind quite easy that the willing dear is worked and worked away.", "그녀의 하녀가 대답했다. “그 착한 도련님이 일에 시달리고 또 시달린다고 마음 푹 놓고 믿으셔도 돼요."], ["If those is Latin legs,”", "만약 저게 라틴어식 다리라면,”"], ["exclaimed Miss Nipper, with strong feeling—in allusion to Paul’s; “give me English ones.”", "폴의 다리를 빗대어 니퍼 양이 격한 감정으로 외쳤다, “전 영국식 다리를 갖겠어요.”"]], [["“I am afraid he feels lonely and lost at Doctor Blimber’s, Susan,”", "“폴이 블림버 박사 댁에서 외롭고 막막함을 느끼는 것 같아 걱정이야, 수전,”"], ["pursued Florence, turning away her face.", "플로렌스가 얼굴을 돌리며 계속했다."]], [["“Ah,”", "“아,”"], ["said Miss Nipper, with great sharpness, “Oh, them ‘Blimbers’”", "니퍼 양이 무척 날카롭게 말했다, “오, 그 ‘블림버’들이라니!”"]], [["“Don’t blame anyone,”", "“아무도 탓하지 마,”"], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“It’s a mistake.”", "“이건 잘못된 일이야.”"]], [["“I say nothing about blame, Miss,” cried Miss Nipper, “for I know that you object, but I may wish, Miss, that the family was set to work to make new roads, and that Miss Blimber went in front and had the pickaxe.”", "“탓에 대해선 아무 말도 안 할게요, 아가씨,” 니퍼 양이 외쳤다. “아가씨가 싫어하시는 걸 아니까요. 하지만 아가씨, 저는 바랄 수는 있잖아요. 그 집안 식구들이 새 길을 닦는 일에 부려졌으면, 그리고 블림버 양이 앞장서서 곡괭이를 들었으면 하고요.”"]], [["After this speech, Miss Nipper, who was perfectly serious, wiped her eyes.", "이 말을 마친 뒤, 더없이 진지했던 니퍼 양은 눈가를 닦았다."]], [["“I think I could perhaps give Paul some help, Susan, if I had these books,”", "“이 책들이 있으면 어쩌면 내가 폴을 좀 도와줄 수 있을 것 같아, 수전,”"], ["said Florence, “and make the coming week a little easier to him.", "플로렌스가 말했다, “그리고 다가오는 한 주를 걔한테 조금이라도 수월하게 해 줄 수 있을 것 같아."], ["At least I want to try.", "적어도 해 보고 싶어."], ["So buy them for me, dear, and I will never forget how kind it was of you to do it!”", "그러니 사다 줘, 응. 네가 해 준 게 얼마나 고마운 일인지 결코 잊지 않을게!”"]], [["It must have been a harder heart than Susan Nipper’s that could have rejected the little purse Florence held out with these words, or the gentle look of entreaty with which she seconded her petition.", "플로렌스가 이 말과 함께 내미는 작은 지갑을, 혹은 자기 청을 거드는 그 부드러운 애원의 눈빛을 거절할 수 있는 마음이라면, 수전 니퍼의 마음보다 더 모진 것이어야 했으리라."], ["Susan put the purse in her pocket without reply, and trotted out at once upon her errand.", "수전은 아무 대답 없이 지갑을 주머니에 넣고, 곧장 심부름을 떠나 종종걸음으로 나갔다."]], [["The books were not easy to procure; and the answer at several shops was, either that they were just out of them, or that they never kept them, or that they had had a great many last month, or that they expected a great many next week But Susan was not easily baffled in such an enterprise; and having entrapped a white-haired youth, in a black calico apron, from a library where she was known, to accompany her in her quest, she led him such a life in going up and down, that he exerted himself to the utmost, if it were only to get rid of her; and finally enabled her to return home in triumph.", "그 책들은 구하기가 쉽지 않았다. 여러 가게에서 돌아온 대답은, 마침 다 떨어졌다거나, 원래 그런 건 두지 않는다거나, 지난달에 잔뜩 들여놓았었다거나, 다음 주에 잔뜩 들어올 예정이라는 것이었다. 그러나 수전은 그런 일에서 쉽게 좌절할 사람이 아니었다. 그래서 자기가 잘 아는 어느 도서 대여점에서 검은 옥양목 앞치마를 두른, 머리가 흰 젊은이 하나를 꾀어내어 함께 탐색에 나서게 했는데, 그를 이리저리 끌고 다니며 어찌나 들볶았던지, 그는 오로지 그녀에게서 벗어나기 위해서라도 온 힘을 다했고, 마침내 그녀가 의기양양하게 집으로 돌아갈 수 있게 해 주었다."]], [["With these treasures then, after her own daily lessons were over, Florence sat down at night to track Paul’s footsteps through the thorny ways of learning; and being possessed of a naturally quick and sound capacity, and taught by that most wonderful of masters, love, it was not long before she gained upon Paul’s heels, and caught and passed him.", "그리하여 이 보물들을 가지고, 플로렌스는 자기 자신의 그날 공부가 끝난 뒤 밤이면 자리에 앉아, 가시밭 같은 배움의 길을 따라 폴의 발자취를 더듬어 나갔다. 그리고 타고난 빠르고 건실한 능력을 갖춘 데다, 그 모든 스승 가운데 가장 놀라운 스승인 사랑에게 가르침을 받았으니, 오래지 않아 그녀는 폴의 발꿈치를 따라잡았고, 그를 따라잡아 앞질렀다."]], [["Not a word of this was breathed to Mrs Pipchin: but many a night when they were all in bed, and when Miss Nipper, with her hair in papers and herself asleep in some uncomfortable attitude, reposed unconscious by her side; and when the chinking ashes in the grate were cold and grey; and when the candles were burnt down and guttering out;—Florence tried so hard to be a substitute for one small Dombey, that her fortitude and perseverance might have almost won her a free right to bear the name herself.", "이 일은 핍친 부인에게는 한마디도 새어 나가지 않았다. 그러나 다들 잠자리에 들고, 머리에 종이를 만 니퍼 양이 어느 불편한 자세로 잠든 채 그녀 곁에서 무심히 쉬고 있을 때, 그리고 난로 안의 짤랑이는 잿불이 차갑게 식어 잿빛이 되고, 촛불이 다 타들어 가 녹아 흘러내릴 때—플로렌스는 한 명의 작은 돔비를 대신하려고 너무도 애써서, 그 의연함과 끈기만으로도 그 이름을 스스로 떳떳이 지닐 자격을 거의 얻어 낼 만했던 숱한 밤이 있었다."]], [["And high was her reward, when one Saturday evening, as little Paul was sitting down as usual to “resume his studies,”", "그리고 그녀의 보답은 컸다. 어느 토요일 저녁, 어린 폴이 여느 때처럼 “공부를 다시 시작하려고”"], ["she sat down by his side, and showed him all that was so rough, made smooth, and all that was so dark, made clear and plain, before him.", "자리에 앉았을 때, 그녀가 그 곁에 앉아, 그토록 거칠던 모든 것이 매끄러워지고, 그토록 어둡던 모든 것이 또렷하고 분명해진 것을 그의 앞에 펼쳐 보여 준 것이다."], ["It was nothing but a startled look in Paul’s wan face—a flush—a smile—and then a close embrace—but God knows how her heart leapt up at this rich payment for her trouble.", "그것은 그저 폴의 핼쑥한 얼굴에 떠오른 놀란 표정—발그레함—미소—그리고 이어지는 꼭 껴안음뿐이었으나, 자기 수고에 대한 이 풍성한 보답에 그녀의 가슴이 얼마나 뛰어올랐는지는 하느님만이 아실 일이었다."]], [["“Oh, Floy!”", "“오, 플로이!”"], ["cried her brother, “how I love you!", "그녀의 남동생이 외쳤다, “내가 누나를 얼마나 사랑하는지!"], ["How I love you, Floy!”", "내가 누나를 얼마나 사랑하는지 몰라, 플로이!”"]], [["“And I you, dear!”", "“나도 너를 사랑해, 얘야!”"]], [["“Oh!", "“오!"], ["I am sure of that, Floy.”", "그건 나도 알아, 플로이.”"]], [["He said no more about it, but all that evening sat close by her, very quiet; and in the night he called out from his little room within hers, three or four times, that he loved her.", "그는 그것에 대해 더는 말하지 않았으나, 그날 저녁 내내 그녀 곁에 바싹 붙어 아주 조용히 앉아 있었다. 그리고 밤이 되자 그녀의 방 안에 딸린 자기 작은 방에서 세 번이나 네 번, 누나를 사랑한다고 외쳤다."]], [["Regularly, after that, Florence was prepared to sit down with Paul on Saturday night, and patiently assist him through so much as they could anticipate together of his next week’s work.", "그 뒤로 줄곧, 플로렌스는 토요일 밤이면 폴과 함께 앉아, 둘이서 함께 미리 헤아릴 수 있는 만큼 그의 다음 주 공부를 참을성 있게 도울 채비를 했다."], ["The cheering thought that he was labouring on where Florence had just toiled before him, would, of itself, have been a stimulant to Paul in the perpetual resumption of his studies; but coupled with the actual lightening of his load, consequent on this assistance, it saved him, possibly, from sinking underneath the burden which the fair Cornelia Blimber piled upon his back.", "자기가 지금 끙끙대는 곳을 플로렌스가 바로 앞서 애써 지나갔다는 흐뭇한 생각만으로도, 끝없이 공부를 다시 시작하는 폴에게는 그 자체로 자극이 되었을 터였다. 그러나 그 도움에 따라 짐이 실제로 가벼워진 것과 더불어, 그 생각은, 아리따운 코닐리아 블림버가 그의 등에 쌓아 올린 짐 밑에 그가 짓눌려 가라앉는 것을 어쩌면 막아 주었다."]], [["It was not that Miss Blimber meant to be too hard upon him, or that Doctor Blimber meant to bear too heavily on the young gentlemen in general.", "그것은 블림버 양이 그를 너무 모질게 대하려 했기 때문도, 블림버 박사가 어린 신사들 일반을 너무 호되게 짓누르려 했기 때문도 아니었다."], ["Cornelia merely held the faith in which she had been bred; and the Doctor, in some partial confusion of his ideas, regarded the young gentlemen as if they were all Doctors, and were born grown up.", "코닐리아는 그저 자기가 자라며 배운 신조를 굳게 지킬 뿐이었다. 그리고 박사는 생각이 다소 어수선하게 뒤섞여 있어서, 어린 신사들을 마치 그들이 모두 박사이고, 또 다 자란 어른으로 태어나기라도 한 양 여겼다."], ["Comforted by the applause of the young gentlemen’s nearest relations, and urged on by their blind vanity and ill-considered haste, it would have been strange if Doctor Blimber had discovered his mistake, or trimmed his swelling sails to any other tack.", "어린 신사들의 가장 가까운 친척들의 갈채에 흐뭇해하고, 그들의 맹목적인 허영과 분별없는 조급함에 부추김을 받았으니, 블림버 박사가 자기 잘못을 깨닫거나, 부풀어 오른 돛을 다른 방향으로 손질했다면 그것이 도리어 이상한 일이었을 것이다."]], [["Thus in the case of Paul.", "폴의 경우가 바로 그러했다."], ["When Doctor Blimber said he made great progress and was naturally clever, Mr Dombey was more bent than ever on his being forced and crammed.", "블림버 박사가 그가 큰 진보를 이루었으며 천성이 영리하다고 말하자, 돔비 씨는 그 어느 때보다 그를 더 밀어붙이고 더 채워 넣어야 한다는 데 골몰했다."], ["In the case of Briggs, when Doctor Blimber reported that he did not make great progress yet, and was not naturally clever, Briggs senior was inexorable in the same purpose.", "브릭스의 경우, 블림버 박사가 아직 큰 진보를 이루지 못했으며 천성이 영리하지 않다고 보고하자, 브릭스 씨 어른은 똑같은 목적에 대해 추호도 흔들림이 없었다."], ["In short, however high and false the temperature at which the Doctor kept his hothouse, the owners of the plants were always ready to lend a helping hand at the bellows, and to stir the fire.", "요컨대, 박사가 자기 온실을 아무리 높고 거짓된 온도로 유지하든, 그 화초들의 주인들은 언제나 풀무질을 거들고 불을 더 돋우려 기꺼이 나섰다."]], [["Such spirits as he had in the outset, Paul soon lost of course.", "처음에 폴이 가졌던 만큼의 기운은 물론 그도 곧 잃어버렸다."], ["But he retained all that was strange, and old, and thoughtful in his character: and under circumstances so favourable to the development of those tendencies, became even more strange, and old, and thoughtful, than before.", "그러나 그 성품 가운데 기묘하고, 노숙하고, 사색적인 면은 모두 그대로 간직했다. 그리고 그런 경향이 자라나기에 그토록 알맞은 상황 아래서, 그는 전보다 한층 더 기묘하고, 노숙하고, 사색적인 아이가 되었다."]], [["The only difference was, that he kept his character to himself.", "다만 한 가지 다른 점이라면, 그가 자기 성품을 혼자만 간직했다는 것이었다. 그는 날마다 더 사색에 잠기고 더 말수가 줄었다."], ["He grew more thoughtful and reserved, every day; and had no such curiosity in any living member of the Doctor’s household, as he had had in Mrs Pipchin.", "그리고 박사네 집안의 그 어떤 살아 있는 식구에 대해서도, 핍친 부인에게 가졌던 만큼의 호기심을 갖지 않았다. 그는 혼자 있기를 좋아했다."], ["He loved to be alone; and in those short intervals when he was not occupied with his books, liked nothing so well as wandering about the house by himself, or sitting on the stairs, listening to the great clock in the hall.", "그리고 책에 매달리지 않은 그 짧은 틈이면, 혼자 집 안을 이리저리 거닐거나, 계단에 앉아 현관의 그 커다란 시계에 귀 기울이는 것만큼 좋아하는 일이 없었다. 그는 집 안의 모든 벽지와 친했다."], ["He was intimate with all the paperhanging in the house; saw things that no one else saw in the patterns; found out miniature tigers and lions running up the bedroom walls, and squinting faces leering in the squares and diamonds of the floor-cloth.", "그 무늬 속에서 다른 누구도 못 보는 것들을 보았다. 침실 벽을 타고 달리는 자그마한 호랑이며 사자를 찾아냈고, 바닥 깔개의 네모와 마름모 무늬 속에서 곁눈질하며 히죽이는 얼굴들을 발견했다."]], [["The solitary child lived on, surrounded by this arabesque work of his musing fancy, and no one understood him.", "그 외로운 아이는 자기 사색의 공상이 빚어낸 이 아라베스크 같은 무늬에 둘러싸여 살아갔고, 아무도 그를 이해하지 못했다."], ["Mrs Blimber thought him “odd,” and sometimes the servants said among themselves that little Dombey “moped;”", "블림버 부인은 그를 “별나다”고 여겼고, 하인들은 이따금 자기들끼리 어린 돔비가 “우울해한다”고 말했다."], ["but that was all.", "그러나 그것이 전부였다."]], [["Unless young Toots had some idea on the subject, to the expression of which he was wholly unequal.", "어린 투츠가 이 일에 대해 어떤 생각을 품고 있었던 것이 아니라면 말이다. 다만 그는 그것을 표현할 능력이 전혀 없었다."], ["Ideas, like ghosts (according to the common notion of ghosts), must be spoken to a little before they will explain themselves; and Toots had long left off asking any questions of his own mind.", "생각이라는 것은 유령처럼 (유령에 대한 흔한 관념을 따르자면) 먼저 조금 말을 걸어 주어야 비로소 자기를 설명하는 법인데, 투츠는 오래전에 자기 마음에 어떤 물음을 던지는 일을 그만두었다. 그의 두개골이라는 그 납빛 궤짝에서 어떤 안개가 피어 나왔을지도 모르고, 그것이 만일 모양과 형태를 갖출 수 있었다면 정령이 되었을지도 모른다."], ["Some mist there may have been, issuing from that leaden casket, his cranium, which, if it could have taken shape and form, would have become a genie; but it could not; and it only so far followed the example of the smoke in the Arabian story, as to roll out in a thick cloud, and there hang and hover.", "그러나 그러지 못했다. 그것은 그저, 두꺼운 구름이 되어 굽이쳐 나와서는 거기 걸려 맴도는 정도로만 아라비아 이야기 속 연기의 본을 따랐을 뿐이다."], ["But it left a little figure visible upon a lonely shore, and Toots was always staring at it.", "그러나 그것은 한 외로운 바닷가에 자그마한 형상 하나를 어렴풋이 남겨 놓았고, 투츠는 늘 그것을 빤히 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“How are you?”", "“잘 있었니?”"], ["he would say to Paul, fifty times a day.", "그는 하루에도 쉰 번씩 폴에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["“Quite well, Sir, thank you,”", "“네, 아주 잘 지냈어요, 고맙습니다.”"], ["Paul would answer.", "폴이 대답하곤 했다."], ["“Shake hands,”", "“악수하자,”"], ["would be Toots’s next advance.", "그것이 투츠의 다음 진전이었다."]], [["Which Paul, of course, would immediately do.", "물론 폴은 즉시 그렇게 했다."], ["Mr Toots generally said again, after a long interval of staring and hard breathing, “How are you?”", "투츠 씨는 한참 빤히 바라보며 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉰 끝에, 으레 다시 “잘 있었니?” 하고 말했다."], ["To which Paul again replied, “Quite well, Sir, thank you.”", "그러면 폴은 다시 “네, 아주 잘 지냈어요, 고맙습니다” 하고 대답했다."]], [["One evening Mr Toots was sitting at his desk, oppressed by correspondence, when a great purpose seemed to flash upon him.", "어느 날 저녁 투츠 씨가 편지에 짓눌려 책상 앞에 앉아 있을 때, 어떤 큰 결심이 그에게 번뜩 떠오른 듯했다."], ["He laid down his pen, and went off to seek Paul, whom he found at last, after a long search, looking through the window of his little bedroom.", "그는 펜을 내려놓고 폴을 찾으러 갔는데, 한참을 뒤진 끝에 마침내 그가 자기 작은 침실 창문 밖을 내다보고 있는 것을 발견했다."]], [["“I say!”", "“있잖아!”"], ["cried Toots, speaking the moment he entered the room, lest he should forget it; “what do you think about?”", "투츠가 방에 들어서는 순간, 잊어버릴까 봐 곧바로 말했다. “넌 무슨 생각을 하니?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“오!"], ["I think about a great many things,”", "저는 아주 많은 것들을 생각해요,”"], ["replied Paul.", "폴이 대답했다."]], [["“Do you, though?”", "“그래, 정말?”"], ["said Toots, appearing to consider that fact in itself surprising.", "투츠가 그 사실 자체를 놀랍게 여기는 듯이 말했다."], ["“If you had to die,”", "“만약에 죽어야 한다면 말이에요,”"], ["said Paul, looking up into his face—Mr Toots started, and seemed much disturbed.", "폴이 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다—투츠 씨는 흠칫 놀라며 몹시 동요한 듯 보였다."]], [["“Don’t you think you would rather die on a moonlight night, when the sky was quite clear, and the wind blowing, as it did last night?”", "“달빛 비치는 밤에, 하늘이 아주 맑고 바람이, 어젯밤처럼 불 때 죽는 게 더 낫다고 생각하지 않으세요?”"]], [["Mr Toots said, looking doubtfully at Paul, and shaking his head, that he didn’t know about that.", "투츠 씨는 미심쩍은 듯 폴을 바라보며 고개를 저었고, 그건 잘 모르겠다고 말했다."]], [["“Not blowing, at least,”", "“바람이 불기보다는,”"], ["said Paul, “but sounding in the air like the sea sounds in the shells. It was a beautiful night.", "폴이 말했다, “공기 속에서 소리가 나는 거예요, 조개껍데기 속에서 바다 소리가 나듯이요."], ["When I had listened to the water for a long time, I got up and looked out.", "아름다운 밤이었어요. 한참 동안 물소리를 듣고 나서 일어나 밖을 내다봤어요."], ["There was a boat over there, in the full light of the moon; a boat with a sail.”", "저기 저편에, 달빛이 가득한 가운데 배 한 척이 있었어요. 돛을 단 배요.”"]], [["The child looked at him so steadfastly, and spoke so earnestly, that Mr Toots, feeling himself called upon to say something about this boat, said, “Smugglers.”", "그 아이가 어찌나 한결같이 그를 바라보며 어찌나 진지하게 말하는지, 투츠 씨는 이 배에 대해 무언가 말해야 할 것 같은 의무감을 느껴 “밀수꾼들이로군” 하고 말했다."], ["But with an impartial remembrance of there being two sides to every question, he added, “or Preventive.”", "그러나 모든 문제에는 양면이 있다는 것을 공정하게 떠올리고는 “아니면 단속선이거나”라고 덧붙였다."]], [["“A boat with a sail,”", "“돛을 단 배요,”"], ["repeated Paul, “in the full light of the moon.", "폴이 되풀이했다, “달빛이 가득한 가운데."], ["The sail like an arm, all silver.", "돛이 마치 팔 같았어요, 온통 은빛으로."], ["It went away into the distance, and what do you think it seemed to do as it moved with the waves?”", "그게 저 멀리로 멀어져 갔는데, 그게 물결을 따라 움직이면서 무얼 하는 것 같았는지 아세요?”"]], [["“Pitch,”", "“출렁였겠지,”"], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["“It seemed to beckon,”", "“손짓하는 것 같았어요,”"], ["said the child, “to beckon me to come!—There she is!", "그 아이가 말했다, “나더러 오라고 손짓하는 것 같았어요!—저기 누나가 있어요!"], ["There she is!”", "저기 누나가 있어요!”"]], [["Toots was almost beside himself with dismay at this sudden exclamation, after what had gone before, and cried “Who?”", "이 갑작스러운 외침에, 앞서 있었던 일들 끝에 나온 것이라, 투츠는 경악하여 거의 제정신이 아니게 되어 “누가?” 하고 외쳤다."]], [["“My sister Florence!”", "“우리 누나 플로렌스요!”"], ["cried Paul, “looking up here, and waving her hand.", "폴이 외쳤다, “여기 위를 올려다보면서 손을 흔들고 있어요."], ["She sees me—she sees me!", "누나가 날 봐요—날 본다고요!"], ["Good-night, dear, good-night, good-night.”", "잘 자, 누나, 잘 자, 잘 자.”"]], [["His quick transition to a state of unbounded pleasure, as he stood at his window, kissing and clapping his hands: and the way in which the light retreated from his features as she passed out of his view, and left a patient melancholy on the little face: were too remarkable wholly to escape even Toots’s notice.", "순식간에 끝없는 기쁨의 상태로 옮겨 가서, 창가에 서서 입을 맞추고 손뼉을 치는 그 모습, 그리고 그녀가 시야에서 사라지자 빛이 그의 얼굴에서 물러나며 그 작은 얼굴에 참을성 있는 우수를 남겨 놓는 그 모양은, 투츠의 눈마저 온전히 비껴가기에는 너무도 두드러진 것이었다."], ["Their interview being interrupted at this moment by a visit from Mrs Pipchin, who usually brought her black skirts to bear upon Paul just before dusk, once or twice a week, Toots had no opportunity of improving the occasion: but it left so marked an impression on his mind that he twice returned, after having exchanged the usual salutations, to ask Mrs Pipchin how she did.", "마침 이 순간 핍친 부인의 방문으로 그들의 만남이 중단되었는데—그녀는 보통 일주일에 한두 번, 해질 무렵 직전에 자기 검은 치마를 끌고 폴에게 들이닥쳤다—그래서 투츠는 이 기회를 이용할 여지가 없었다. 그러나 그것은 그의 마음에 너무도 뚜렷한 인상을 남겨, 그는 으레 하는 인사를 나눈 뒤에 두 번이나 되돌아와 핍친 부인에게 안녕하시냐고 물었다."], ["This the irascible old lady conceived to be a deeply devised and long-meditated insult, originating in the diabolical invention of the weak-eyed young man downstairs, against whom she accordingly lodged a formal complaint with Doctor Blimber that very night; who mentioned to the young man that if he ever did it again, he should be obliged to part with him.", "이 성마른 노부인은 그것을, 아래층의 그 눈 약한 젊은이의 악마적인 발상에서 비롯된, 깊이 꾸며지고 오래 궁리된 모욕이라 여겼다. 그래서 그녀는 바로 그날 밤 블림버 박사에게 정식으로 항의를 제기했고, 박사는 그 젊은이에게 만약 다시 한번 그런 짓을 하면 그를 내보낼 수밖에 없을 거라고 일러두었다."]], [["The evenings being longer now, Paul stole up to his window every evening to look out for Florence.", "이제 저녁이 길어졌으므로, 폴은 저녁마다 자기 창가로 살그머니 올라가 플로렌스를 내다보았다."], ["She always passed and repassed at a certain time, until she saw him; and their mutual recognition was a gleam of sunshine in Paul’s daily life.", "그녀는 그가 자기를 볼 때까지 늘 정해진 시간에 오갔다. 그리고 그들이 서로를 알아보는 것은 폴의 하루하루에 비치는 한 줄기 햇살이었다."], ["Often after dark, one other figure walked alone before the Doctor’s house.", "어둠이 깔린 뒤에도 또 다른 한 형상이 박사의 집 앞을 홀로 거니는 일이 잦았다."], ["He rarely joined them on the Saturdays now.", "그는 이제 토요일에는 좀처럼 그들과 함께하지 않았다."], ["He could not bear it.", "그는 그것을 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["He would rather come unrecognised, and look up at the windows where his son was qualifying for a man; and wait, and watch, and plan, and hope.", "그는 차라리 아무도 모르게 와서, 자기 아들이 어엿한 사내가 되어 가고 있는 그 창문들을 올려다보며, 기다리고, 지켜보고, 계획하고, 바라곤 했다."]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["could he but have seen, or seen as others did, the slight spare boy above, watching the waves and clouds at twilight, with his earnest eyes, and breasting the window of his solitary cage when birds flew by, as if he would have emulated them, and soared away!", "그가 만일 볼 수만 있었더라면, 혹은 다른 이들이 보듯 볼 수만 있었더라면—저 위의 그 가냘프고 여윈 소년이, 황혼녘에 진지한 두 눈으로 물결과 구름을 바라보고, 새들이 날아 지날 때면 마치 그들을 흉내 내어 훨훨 날아가 버리고 싶다는 듯 자기 외로운 새장의 창문에 가슴을 들이대고 있는 그 모습을!"]], [["# CHAPTER XIII. Shipping Intelligence and Office Business", "# CHAPTER XIII. Shipping Intelligence and Office Business"]], [["Mr Dombey’s offices were in a court where there was an old-established stall of choice fruit at the corner: where perambulating merchants, of both sexes, offered for sale at any time between the hours of ten and five, slippers, pocket-books, sponges, dogs’ collars, and Windsor soap; and sometimes a pointer or an oil-painting.", "돔비 씨의 사무소는 어느 안마당에 자리하고 있었는데, 그 모퉁이에는 좋은 과일을 파는 오래된 노점이 하나 있었다. 그곳에서는 남녀를 막론한 떠돌이 장사꾼들이 열 시부터 다섯 시 사이 아무 때나, 슬리퍼며 수첩이며 해면이며 개 목줄이며 윈저 비누를 팔겠다고 내놓았고, 더러는 포인터 사냥개나 유화 한 점을 내놓기도 했다."]], [["The pointer always came that way, with a view to the Stock Exchange, where a sporting taste (originating generally in bets of new hats) is much in vogue.", "포인터는 늘 그쪽으로 끌려왔는데, 증권거래소를 노린 것이었다. 그곳에서는 (대개 새 모자를 건 내기에서 비롯된) 사냥 취미가 한창 유행이었다."], ["The other commodities were addressed to the general public; but they were never offered by the vendors to Mr Dombey.", "다른 물건들은 일반 대중을 상대로 한 것이었으나, 장사꾼들이 그것들을 돔비 씨에게 들이미는 일은 결코 없었다."], ["When he appeared, the dealers in those wares fell off respectfully.", "그가 나타나면 그런 잡화를 다루는 자들은 공손히 물러섰다."], ["The principal slipper and dogs’ collar man—who considered himself a public character, and whose portrait was screwed on to an artist’s door in Cheapside—threw up his forefinger to the brim of his hat as Mr Dombey went by.", "슬리퍼와 개 목줄을 파는 우두머리 격 사내는—그는 스스로를 공인이라 여겼고, 그의 초상은 칩사이드의 어느 화가 문에 나사로 박혀 있었다—돔비 씨가 지나갈 때면 집게손가락을 들어 모자챙에 갖다 댔다."], ["The ticket-porter, if he were not absent on a job, always ran officiously before, to open Mr Dombey’s office door as wide as possible, and hold it open, with his hat off, while he entered.", "표찰을 단 짐꾼은, 일하러 나가 자리를 비운 게 아니라면, 언제나 부산스레 앞서 달려가 돔비 씨의 사무소 문을 한껏 활짝 열어젖히고는, 모자를 벗은 채 그가 들어가는 동안 그 문을 잡고 서 있었다."]], [["The clerks within were not a whit behind-hand in their demonstrations of respect.", "안에 있는 서기들도 공경의 표시에서는 조금도 뒤지지 않았다."], ["A solemn hush prevailed, as Mr Dombey passed through the outer office.", "돔비 씨가 바깥 사무실을 가로질러 지나갈 때면 엄숙한 정적이 감돌았다."], ["The wit of the Counting-House became in a moment as mute as the row of leathern fire-buckets hanging up behind him.", "회계실의 익살은 그 즉시, 그의 등 뒤에 걸려 있는 가죽 소화통 한 줄처럼 말없이 잠잠해졌다."], ["Such vapid and flat daylight as filtered through the ground-glass windows and skylights, leaving a black sediment upon the panes, showed the books and papers, and the figures bending over them, enveloped in a studious gloom, and as much abstracted in appearance, from the world without, as if they were assembled at the bottom of the sea; while a mouldy little strong room in the obscure perspective, where a shaded lamp was always burning, might have represented the cavern of some ocean monster, looking on with a red eye at these mysteries of the deep.", "간유리 창문과 채광창을 통해 걸러져 들어와 유리판에 검은 침전물을 남기는, 그 김빠지고 밋밋한 햇빛은, 장부와 서류들, 그리고 그 위로 몸을 굽힌 형체들을 비추었는데, 그들은 골똘한 어스름에 감싸인 채 마치 바다 밑바닥에 모여 있기라도 한 듯 바깥세상에서 동떨어진 모습이었다. 한편 어둑한 저편 구석에 곰팡내 나는 작은 금고실에서는 갓을 씌운 등불이 늘 타고 있었는데, 그것은 마치 어느 대양 괴물의 동굴 같아서, 붉은 눈으로 이 심해의 신비를 지켜보는 듯했다."]], [["When Perch the messenger, whose place was on a little bracket, like a timepiece, saw Mr Dombey come in—or rather when he felt that he was coming, for he had usually an instinctive sense of his approach—he hurried into Mr Dombey’s room, stirred the fire, carried fresh coals from the bowels of the coal-box, hung the newspaper to air upon the fender, put the chair ready, and the screen in its place, and was round upon his heel on the instant of Mr Dombey’s entrance, to take his great-coat and hat, and hang them up.", "전령 퍼치는—그의 자리는 마치 괘종시계처럼 작은 받침 선반 위에 있었다—돔비 씨가 들어오는 것을 보면, 아니 오히려 그가 오고 있음을 느끼면(그는 대개 본능적인 감각으로 그가 다가오는 것을 알아챘다), 돔비 씨의 방으로 부리나케 들어가 불을 헤집고, 석탄통 깊은 데서 새 석탄을 가져오고, 신문을 난로 울에 걸어 데우고, 의자를 갖춰 놓고, 가리개를 제자리에 두었다가, 돔비 씨가 들어서는 바로 그 순간에 발꿈치를 빙 돌려 그의 외투와 모자를 받아 걸었다."], ["Then Perch took the newspaper, and gave it a turn or two in his hands before the fire, and laid it, deferentially, at Mr Dombey’s elbow.", "그러고 나서 퍼치는 신문을 집어 들고 불 앞에서 손으로 한두 번 뒤집은 뒤, 공손하게 돔비 씨의 팔꿈치 곁에 놓았다."], ["And so little objection had Perch to being deferential in the last degree, that if he might have laid himself at Mr Dombey’s feet, or might have called him by some such title as used to be bestowed upon the Caliph Haroun Alraschid, he would have been all the better pleased.", "그리고 퍼치는 더없이 극진하게 공손한 태도를 취하는 데 조금도 거리낌이 없어서, 만일 돔비 씨의 발치에 자기 몸을 엎드릴 수 있었다면, 혹은 칼리프 하룬 알라시드에게 바쳐지곤 했던 그런 칭호로 그를 부를 수 있었다면, 그는 더더욱 기뻐했을 것이다."]], [["As this honour would have been an innovation and an experiment, Perch was fain to content himself by expressing as well as he could, in his manner, You are the light of my Eyes.", "이런 영예는 새로운 시도이자 실험이 될 터였으므로, 퍼치는 자기 태도로써 할 수 있는 한 잘 표현하는 것으로 만족할 수밖에 없었다. 당신은 제 눈의 빛이옵니다."], ["You are the Breath of my Soul.", "당신은 제 영혼의 숨결이옵니다."], ["You are the commander of the Faithful Perch!", "당신은 충실한 퍼치의 사령관이시옵니다!"], ["With this imperfect happiness to cheer him, he would shut the door softly, walk away on tiptoe, and leave his great chief to be stared at, through a dome-shaped window in the leads, by ugly chimney-pots and backs of houses, and especially by the bold window of a hair-cutting saloon on a first floor, where a waxen effigy, bald as a Mussulman in the morning, and covered, after eleven o’clock in the day, with luxuriant hair and whiskers in the latest Christian fashion, showed him the wrong side of its head for ever.", "이런 불완전한 행복으로 마음을 달래며, 그는 문을 살며시 닫고 발끝으로 걸어 나가, 위대한 우두머리를 남겨 두었다—납지붕에 난 둥근 돔 모양 창문을 통해 흉한 굴뚝갓들과 집들의 뒤편이, 특히 이 층에 있는 이발관의 당돌한 창문이 그를 빤히 바라보도록. 그 창문 안에는 밀랍 인형이 하나 있었는데, 아침이면 무슬림처럼 대머리였다가, 한낮 열한 시가 지나면 최신 기독교식 유행에 따라 풍성한 머리카락과 구레나룻을 뒤집어쓴 채, 영원히 자기 머리의 잘못된 쪽을 그에게 보여 주었다."]], [["Between Mr Dombey and the common world, as it was accessible through the medium of the outer office—to which Mr Dombey’s presence in his own room may be said to have struck like damp, or cold air—there were two degrees of descent.", "돔비 씨와, 바깥 사무실을 매개로 닿을 수 있는 보통 세상 사이에는—자기 방 안에 있는 돔비 씨의 존재는 바깥 사무실에 마치 습기나 찬 공기처럼 닿았다고 할 만했다—두 단계의 하강이 있었다."], ["Mr Carker in his own office was the first step; Mr Morfin, in his own office, was the second.", "자기 사무실에 있는 카커 씨가 첫 단계였고, 자기 사무실에 있는 모핀 씨가 둘째 단계였다."], ["Each of these gentlemen occupied a little chamber like a bath-room, opening from the passage outside Mr Dombey’s door.", "이 두 신사는 저마다 욕실 같은 작은 방을 하나씩 차지했는데, 그 방들은 돔비 씨의 문 바깥 복도에서 통하게 되어 있었다."], ["Mr Carker, as Grand Vizier, inhabited the room that was nearest to the Sultan.", "카커 씨는 대신(大臣)으로서 술탄에게 가장 가까운 방에 살았다."], ["Mr Morfin, as an officer of inferior state, inhabited the room that was nearest to the clerks.", "모핀 씨는 그보다 낮은 직책의 관리로서 서기들에게 가장 가까운 방에 살았다."]], [["The gentleman last mentioned was a cheerful-looking, hazel-eyed elderly bachelor: gravely attired, as to his upper man, in black; and as to his legs, in pepper-and-salt colour.", "마지막에 언급된 신사는 쾌활해 보이는, 개암빛 눈을 한 중년의 독신남이었다. 윗도리는 검은색으로 점잖게 차려입었고, 다리께는 후추소금빛 색으로 차려입었다."], ["His dark hair was just touched here and there with specks of gray, as though the tread of Time had splashed it; and his whiskers were already white.", "그의 검은 머리는 여기저기 희끗희끗한 점이 막 박히기 시작했는데, 마치 세월의 발걸음이 그것을 튀겨 놓은 듯했고, 구레나룻은 이미 하얗게 셌다."], ["He had a mighty respect for Mr Dombey, and rendered him due homage; but as he was of a genial temper himself, and never wholly at his ease in that stately presence, he was disquieted by no jealousy of the many conferences enjoyed by Mr Carker, and felt a secret satisfaction in having duties to discharge, which rarely exposed him to be singled out for such distinction.", "그는 돔비 씨를 대단히 존경하여 마땅한 경의를 표했다. 그러나 그 자신이 워낙 싹싹한 기질이었고 그 위엄 있는 존재 앞에서 결코 완전히 마음 편한 법이 없었으므로, 카커 씨가 누리는 수많은 회담을 두고 시샘 같은 것에 시달리지 않았으며, 오히려 그렇게 특별히 지목당해 그런 영예를 받는 일에 좀처럼 노출되지 않는 직무를 맡고 있다는 데서 은근한 만족을 느꼈다."], ["He was a great musical amateur in his way—after business; and had a paternal affection for his violoncello, which was once in every week transported from Islington, his place of abode, to a certain club-room hard by the Bank, where quartettes of the most tormenting and excruciating nature were executed every Wednesday evening by a private party.", "그는 나름대로 대단한 음악 애호가였다—일과를 마친 뒤에 말이다. 그리고 자기 첼로에 아비 같은 애정을 품고 있었는데, 그 첼로는 매주 한 번씩 그의 거처인 이즐링턴에서 은행 가까이의 어느 클럽 방으로 옮겨졌고, 그곳에서는 사적인 모임이 매주 수요일 저녁마다 더없이 괴롭고 고통스러운 성질의 사중주를 연주하곤 했다."]], [["Mr Carker was a gentleman thirty-eight or forty years old, of a florid complexion, and with two unbroken rows of glistening teeth, whose regularity and whiteness were quite distressing.", "카커 씨는 서른여덟이나 마흔쯤 된 신사로, 혈색이 불그스레했고, 번쩍이는 이가 두 줄로 빠짐없이 가지런했는데, 그 규칙성과 흰빛이 자못 거북할 정도였다."], ["It was impossible to escape the observation of them, for he showed them whenever he spoke; and bore so wide a smile upon his countenance (a smile, however, very rarely, indeed, extending beyond his mouth), that there was something in it like the snarl of a cat.", "그 이를 눈여겨보지 않을 도리가 없었으니, 그가 말할 때마다 그것을 드러냈기 때문이다. 그리고 그는 얼굴에 어찌나 활짝 웃음을 띠고 있던지(다만 그 웃음은 정말이지 좀처럼 입가를 넘어서는 일이 없었다), 거기에는 고양이가 으르렁대는 것 같은 무언가가 있었다."], ["He affected a stiff white cravat, after the example of his principal, and was always closely buttoned up and tightly dressed.", "그는 자기 우두머리를 본떠 빳빳한 흰 크라바트를 즐겨 매었고, 늘 단추를 꼭 채우고 옷을 단단히 차려입었다."], ["His manner towards Mr Dombey was deeply conceived and perfectly expressed.", "돔비 씨를 대하는 그의 태도는 깊이 고안된 것이었고 완벽하게 표현되어 있었다."], ["He was familiar with him, in the very extremity of his sense of the distance between them.", "그는 두 사람 사이의 거리감을 극도로 의식하는 가운데 그를 허물없이 대했다."], ["“Mr Dombey, to a man in your position from a man in mine, there is no show of subservience compatible with the transaction of business between us, that I should think sufficient.", "\"돔비 씨, 당신 같은 지위에 계신 분에게 저 같은 위치에 있는 자가 보일 수 있는 굴종의 모습이라면, 우리 사이의 업무 처리에 어울릴 만큼 충분하다고 제가 여길 만한 것은 없습니다. 솔직히 말씀드립니다, 나리, 저는 아예 그것을 포기합니다."], ["I frankly tell you, Sir, I give it up altogether.", "제 스스로의 마음을 만족시킬 수 없으리라 느끼니까요."], ["I feel that I could not satisfy my own mind; and Heaven knows, Mr Dombey, you can afford to dispense with the endeavour.”", "그리고 하늘은 아시겠지요, 돔비 씨, 당신께서는 그런 노력 따위 없이도 능히 지내실 만한 분이십니다.\""], ["If he had carried these words about with him printed on a placard, and had constantly offered it to Mr Dombey’s perusal on the breast of his coat, he could not have been more explicit than he was.", "만일 그가 이 말들을 벽보에 인쇄해 지니고 다니면서 외투 가슴팍에 붙여 끊임없이 돔비 씨가 읽도록 내밀었다 한들, 지금보다 더 명확할 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["This was Carker the Manager.", "이것이 지배인 카커였다."], ["Mr Carker the Junior, Walter’s friend, was his brother; two or three years older than he, but widely removed in station.", "동생 카커, 곧 월터의 친구는 그의 형제였다. 그보다 두세 살 위였으나 지위로는 한참 떨어져 있었다."], ["The younger brother’s post was on the top of the official ladder; the elder brother’s at the bottom.", "동생 쪽은 회사 사다리의 꼭대기에 자리했고, 형 쪽은 맨 밑바닥에 자리했다."], ["The elder brother never gained a stave, or raised his foot to mount one.", "형은 결코 한 칸도 올라가지 못했고, 한 칸을 오르려고 발을 들지도 않았다."], ["Young men passed above his head, and rose and rose; but he was always at the bottom.", "젊은 사람들이 그의 머리 위를 지나 오르고 또 올랐다. 그러나 그는 늘 맨 밑바닥에 있었다."], ["He was quite resigned to occupy that low condition: never complained of it: and certainly never hoped to escape from it.", "그는 그 낮은 처지를 차지하는 데 완전히 체념해 있었다. 결코 그것을 불평하지 않았고, 거기서 벗어나기를 바라는 일도 분명 결코 없었다."]], [["“How do you do this morning?”", "\"오늘 아침 안녕하십니까?\""], ["said Mr Carker the Manager, entering Mr Dombey’s room soon after his arrival one day: with a bundle of papers in his hand.", "어느 날 돔비 씨가 도착한 직후, 지배인 카커가 손에 서류 뭉치를 든 채 돔비 씨의 방으로 들어서며 말했다."]], [["“How do you do, Carker?”", "\"안녕하시오, 카커?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Coolish!”", "\"날이 좀 쌀쌀하군요!\""], ["observed Carker, stirring the fire.", "카커가 불을 헤집으며 말했다."]], [["“Rather,”", "\"그렇구려,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Any news of the young gentleman who is so important to us all?”", "\"우리 모두에게 그토록 중요한 그 어린 신사분에 관해 무슨 소식이라도 있습니까?\""], ["asked Carker, with his whole regiment of teeth on parade.", "카커가 자기 이의 전 연대를 사열대에 세우듯 드러내며 물었다."]], [["“Yes—not direct news—I hear he’s very well,”", "\"있소—직접적인 소식은 아니지만—아주 잘 지낸다고 들었소,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["Who had come from Brighton over-night.", "그는 간밤에 브라이턴에서 올라온 터였다."], ["But no one knew It.", "그러나 그것을 아는 이는 아무도 없었다."]], [["“Very well, and becoming a great scholar, no doubt?”", "\"아주 잘 지내고, 의심할 바 없이 훌륭한 학자가 되어 가고 있겠지요?\""], ["observed the Manager.", "지배인이 말했다."]], [["“I hope so,”", "\"그러기를 바라오,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대답했다."]], [["“Egad!”", "\"저런!\""], ["said Mr Carker, shaking his head, “Time flies!”", "카커 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"세월이 빠릅니다!\""]], [["“I think so, sometimes,”", "\"이따금 그런 생각이 들기도 하오,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, glancing at his newspaper.", "돔비 씨가 신문을 흘끗 보며 대답했다."]], [["“Oh! You!", "\"오!"], ["You have no reason to think so,”", "당신께서요! 당신께는 그렇게 생각하실 까닭이 없지요,\""], ["observed Carker.", "카커가 말했다."], ["“One who sits on such an elevation as yours, and can sit there, unmoved, in all seasons—hasn’t much reason to know anything about the flight of time.", "\"당신처럼 그런 높은 자리에 앉아, 어느 철에나 동요 없이 거기 앉아 계실 수 있는 분이라면—세월의 흐름에 대해 무엇을 알 까닭이 별로 없습니다."], ["It’s men like myself, who are low down and are not superior in circumstances, and who inherit new masters in the course of Time, that have cause to look about us.", "저 같은 사람들이지요, 낮은 데 있고 처지가 나을 것도 없으며, 세월이 흐르는 동안 새 주인을 물려받게 되는 그런 자들이야말로, 주위를 살필 이유가 있는 법입니다."], ["I shall have a rising sun to worship, soon.”", "저도 머잖아 떠오르는 해를 떠받들게 되겠지요.\""]], [["“Time enough, time enough, Carker!”", "\"아직 멀었소, 아직 멀었소, 카커!\""], ["said Mr Dombey, rising from his chair, and standing with his back to the fire.", "돔비 씨가 의자에서 일어나 불에 등을 대고 서며 말했다."], ["“Have you anything there for me?”", "\"거기 나한테 줄 것이라도 있소?\""]], [["“I don’t know that I need trouble you,”", "\"굳이 번거롭게 해 드릴 것까진 없을 듯합니다만,\""], ["returned Carker, turning over the papers in his hand.", "카커가 손에 든 서류를 뒤적이며 대답했다."], ["“You have a committee today at three, you know.”", "\"오늘 세 시에 위원회가 있으시다는 건 아시지요.\""]], [["“And one at three, three-quarters,”", "\"세 시 사십오 분에도 하나 있고,\""], ["added Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Catch you forgetting anything!”", "\"당신이 무얼 잊는 일이 어디 있겠습니까!\""], ["exclaimed Carker, still turning over his papers.", "카커가 여전히 서류를 뒤적이며 외쳤다."], ["“If Mr Paul inherits your memory, he’ll be a troublesome customer in the House.", "\"폴 도련님이 당신의 기억력을 물려받으신다면, 회사에서 꽤나 골칫거리 손님이 되시겠습니다."], ["One of you is enough.”", "당신 한 분으로도 충분하니까요.\""]], [["“You have an accurate memory of your own,”", "\"당신 자신도 정확한 기억력을 지녔잖소,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Oh! I!”", "\"오!"], ["returned the manager.", "제가요!\" 지배인이 대답했다."], ["“It’s the only capital of a man like me.”", "\"그건 저 같은 사람에게 유일한 자본이니까요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey did not look less pompous or at all displeased, as he stood leaning against the chimney-piece, surveying his (of course unconscious) clerk, from head to foot.", "돔비 씨는 벽난로 선반에 기댄 채, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 (물론 그 자신은 의식하지 못하는) 서기를 훑어보면서, 거만함이 조금도 덜하지도, 조금도 언짢아하지도 않은 모습이었다."], ["The stiffness and nicety of Mr Carker’s dress, and a certain arrogance of manner, either natural to him or imitated from a pattern not far off, gave great additional effect to his humility.", "카커 씨 옷차림의 빳빳함과 단정함, 그리고 그에게 타고난 것이든 멀지 않은 본보기에서 흉내 낸 것이든 어떤 거만한 태도는, 그의 겸손함에 한층 큰 효과를 더했다."], ["He seemed a man who would contend against the power that vanquished him, if he could, but who was utterly borne down by the greatness and superiority of Mr Dombey.", "그는 자기를 짓누르는 권력에 맞서 싸울 수 있다면 싸우려 들 사람이로되, 돔비 씨의 위대함과 우월함에 철저히 짓눌려 있는 사람처럼 보였다."]], [["“Is Morfin here?”", "\"모핀은 여기 있소?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey after a short pause, during which Mr Carker had been fluttering his papers, and muttering little abstracts of their contents to himself.", "돔비 씨가 잠시 사이를 둔 뒤 물었다. 그동안 카커 씨는 서류를 팔랑팔랑 넘기며 그 내용의 요점을 혼자 자그맣게 중얼거리고 있었다."]], [["“Morfin’s here,”", "\"모핀은 여기 있습니다,\""], ["he answered, looking up with his widest and almost sudden smile; “humming musical recollections—of his last night’s quartette party, I suppose—through the walls between us, and driving me half mad.", "그가 더없이 활짝, 거의 느닷없는 웃음을 띠며 고개를 들어 대답했다. \"음악적 회상을 흥얼거리고 있지요—제 짐작에 간밤 사중주 모임의 회상일 겁니다—우리 사이의 벽 너머로 말입니다, 저를 반쯤 미치게 하면서요."], ["I wish he’d make a bonfire of his violoncello, and burn his music-books in it.”", "그 첼로로 모닥불이나 피워서 거기다 악보책을 죄다 태워 버렸으면 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["“You respect nobody, Carker, I think,”", "\"카커, 당신은 그 누구도 존경하지 않는구려,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“No?”", "\"안 그렇습니까?\""], ["inquired Carker, with another wide and most feline show of his teeth.", "카커가 다시 한번 활짝, 더없이 고양이 같은 모습으로 이를 드러내며 물었다."], ["“Well!", "\"글쎄요!"], ["Not many people, I believe.", "많지는 않다고 봅니다."], ["I wouldn’t answer perhaps,”", "아마 장담할 수는 없겠군요,\""], ["he murmured, as if he were only thinking it, “for more than one.”", "그가 마치 혼자 생각하는 것처럼 중얼거렸다, \"한 사람 말고는요.\""]], [["A dangerous quality, if real; and a not less dangerous one, if feigned.", "위험한 자질이다, 그것이 진짜라면. 그리고 그에 못지않게 위험한 자질이다, 꾸민 것이라 해도."], ["But Mr Dombey hardly seemed to think so, as he still stood with his back to the fire, drawn up to his full height, and looking at his head-clerk with a dignified composure, in which there seemed to lurk a stronger latent sense of power than usual.", "그러나 돔비 씨는 그렇게 여기는 것 같지 않았으니, 그는 여전히 불에 등을 댄 채 한껏 키를 곧추세우고 서서, 평소보다 더 강한 잠재적 권력의 감각이 숨어 있는 듯한 위엄 있는 침착함으로 수석 서기를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Talking of Morfin,”", "\"모핀 이야기가 나왔으니 말입니다만,\""], ["resumed Mr Carker, taking out one paper from the rest, “he reports a junior dead in the agency at Barbados, and proposes to reserve a passage in the Son and Heir—she’ll sail in a month or so—for the successor.", "카커 씨가 나머지에서 서류 한 장을 꺼내며 말을 이었다. \"바베이도스 대리점에서 하급 직원 하나가 죽었다고 보고하면서, 그 후임을 위해 '아들과 후계자호'에 자리 하나를 예약해 두자고 제안합니다—그 배는 한 달쯤 뒤에 출항합니다."], ["You don’t care who goes, I suppose?", "누가 가든 상관없으시지요?"], ["We have nobody of that sort here.”", "여기엔 그런 부류에 맞는 사람이 없으니까요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey shook his head with supreme indifference.", "돔비 씨는 더없이 무관심하게 고개를 저었다."]], [["“It’s no very precious appointment,”", "\"그리 대단한 자리는 아닙니다,\""], ["observed Mr Carker, taking up a pen, with which to endorse a memorandum on the back of the paper.", "카커 씨가 펜을 집어 들어 서류 뒷면에 기록을 적으며 말했다."], ["“I hope he may bestow it on some orphan nephew of a musical friend.", "\"그가 그 자리를 어느 음악 친구의 고아가 된 조카한테나 베풀어 주었으면 합니다."], ["It may perhaps stop his fiddle-playing, if he has a gift that way.", "혹 그 아이에게 그런 재주가 있다면, 그러면 바이올린 켜는 일은 그만두게 될지도 모르지요."], ["Who’s that?", "거기 누구요?"], ["Come in!”", "들어오시오!\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, Mr Carker.", "\"실례합니다, 카커 씨."], ["I didn’t know you were here, Sir,”", "여기 계신 줄 몰랐습니다, 나리,\""], ["answered Walter; appearing with some letters in his hand, unopened, and newly arrived.", "월터가 손에 개봉하지 않은, 새로 도착한 편지 몇 통을 들고 나타나며 대답했다."], ["“Mr Carker the junior, Sir—”", "\"동생 카커 씨가요, 나리—\""]], [["At the mention of this name, Mr Carker the Manager was or affected to be, touched to the quick with shame and humiliation.", "이 이름이 거론되자, 지배인 카커 씨는 수치와 굴욕에 정통으로 찔린 듯했다, 아니면 그런 척했다."], ["He cast his eyes full on Mr Dombey with an altered and apologetic look, abased them on the ground, and remained for a moment without speaking.", "그는 달라진, 사죄하는 듯한 눈빛으로 돔비 씨를 똑바로 바라보았다가, 그 눈을 바닥으로 떨구고는 잠시 말없이 서 있었다."]], [["“I thought, Sir,”", "\"내 생각엔 말이지,\""], ["he said suddenly and angrily, turning on Walter, “that you had been before requested not to drag Mr Carker the Junior into your conversation.”", "그가 월터에게 홱 돌아서며 느닷없이 성난 목소리로 말했다, \"자네는 전에 동생 카커 씨를 자네 대화에 끌어들이지 말라고 부탁받았던 것 같은데.\""]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "\"죄송합니다,\""], ["returned Walter.", "월터가 대답했다."], ["“I was only going to say that Mr Carker the Junior had told me he believed you were gone out, or I should not have knocked at the door when you were engaged with Mr Dombey.", "\"다만 동생 카커 씨가 제게 당신께서 외출하신 것 같다고 하셔서요, 그래서 한 말씀 드리려던 것뿐입니다. 그렇지 않았다면, 당신께서 돔비 씨와 일을 보고 계실 때 문을 두드리지는 않았을 겁니다."], ["These are letters for Mr Dombey, Sir.”", "이건 돔비 씨께 온 편지들입니다, 나리.\""]], [["“Very well, Sir,”", "\"좋아,\""], ["returned Mr Carker the Manager, plucking them sharply from his hand.", "지배인 카커 씨가 그의 손에서 편지들을 홱 낚아채며 대답했다."], ["“Go about your business.”", "\"자네 일이나 보러 가게.\""]], [["But in taking them with so little ceremony, Mr Carker dropped one on the floor, and did not see what he had done; neither did Mr Dombey observe the letter lying near his feet.", "그런데 그렇게 격식 없이 편지를 받아 드는 통에, 카커 씨는 한 통을 바닥에 떨어뜨렸고, 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지 보지 못했다. 돔비 씨 또한 자기 발치께에 떨어진 편지를 알아채지 못했다."], ["Walter hesitated for a moment, thinking that one or other of them would notice it; but finding that neither did, he stopped, came back, picked it up, and laid it himself on Mr Dombey’s desk.", "월터는 두 사람 중 누군가가 그것을 알아차리려니 생각하며 잠시 머뭇거렸다. 그러나 둘 다 그러지 않는 것을 보고는, 멈춰 서서 되돌아와 그것을 주워, 손수 돔비 씨의 책상 위에 놓았다."], ["The letters were post-letters; and it happened that the one in question was Mrs Pipchin’s regular report, directed as usual—for Mrs Pipchin was but an indifferent penwoman—by Florence.", "그 편지들은 우편으로 온 것이었는데, 공교롭게도 문제의 그 편지는 핍친 부인의 정기 보고서였고, 늘 그렇듯—핍친 부인이 글솜씨가 시원찮은 까닭에—플로렌스가 받는 이를 적은 것이었다."], ["Mr Dombey, having his attention silently called to this letter by Walter, started, and looked fiercely at him, as if he believed that he had purposely selected it from all the rest.", "돔비 씨는 월터가 이 편지에 말없이 자기 주의를 끈 것에 흠칫 놀라, 마치 그가 일부러 나머지 가운데서 그것을 골라낸 것이라 믿기라도 하는 듯, 그를 사납게 노려보았다."]], [["“You can leave the room, Sir!”", "\"방에서 나가도 좋네, 자네!\""], ["said Mr Dombey, haughtily.", "돔비 씨가 거만하게 말했다."]], [["He crushed the letter in his hand; and having watched Walter out at the door, put it in his pocket without breaking the seal.", "그는 편지를 손안에서 구겼다. 그리고 월터가 문밖으로 나가는 것을 지켜본 뒤, 봉인을 뜯지도 않은 채 그것을 주머니에 집어넣었다."]], [["“These continual references to Mr Carker the Junior,”", "\"동생 카커 씨를 이렇게 끊임없이 언급하는 것은,\""], ["Mr Carker the Manager began, as soon as they were alone, “are, to a man in my position, uttered before one in yours, so unspeakably distressing—”", "둘만 남게 되자마자 지배인 카커 씨가 입을 열었다, \"저 같은 위치에 있는 자에게는, 더구나 당신 같은 분 앞에서 거론될 때는, 말할 수 없이 괴로운 일입니다—\""]], [["“Nonsense, Carker,”", "\"허튼소리요, 카커,\""], ["Mr Dombey interrupted.", "돔비 씨가 가로막았다."], ["“You are too sensitive.”", "\"당신은 너무 예민하오.\""]], [["“I am sensitive,”", "\"저는 예민합니다,\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“If one in your position could by any possibility imagine yourself in my place: which you cannot: you would be so too.”", "\"만일 당신 같은 위치에 계신 분이 어찌어찌하여 제 처지에 자기를 놓고 상상해 보실 수만 있다면—그건 불가능한 일이지만—당신께서도 그러실 겁니다.\""]], [["As Mr Dombey’s thoughts were evidently pursuing some other subject, his discreet ally broke off here, and stood with his teeth ready to present to him, when he should look up.", "돔비 씨의 생각이 분명 다른 어떤 주제를 좇고 있었으므로, 그의 분별 있는 동맹자는 여기서 말을 끊고, 그가 고개를 들 때 그에게 내보일 이를 준비한 채 서 있었다."]], [["“You want somebody to send to the West Indies, you were saying,”", "\"서인도제도로 보낼 사람이 필요하다고 하지 않았소,\""], ["observed Mr Dombey, hurriedly.", "돔비 씨가 서둘러 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"예,\""], ["replied Carker.", "카커가 대답했다."]], [["“Send young Gay.”", "\"게이 그 젊은이를 보내시오.\""]], [["“Good, very good indeed. Nothing easier,”", "\"좋습니다, 정말 아주 좋습니다."], ["said Mr Carker, without any show of surprise, and taking up the pen to re-endorse the letter, as coolly as he had done before.", "그보다 쉬운 일도 없지요,\" 카커 씨가 조금도 놀라는 기색 없이, 앞서와 똑같이 태연하게 펜을 집어 들어 서류에 다시 기록하며 말했다."], ["“‘Send young Gay.’”", "\"'게이 젊은이를 보낼 것.'\""]], [["“Call him back,”", "\"그를 다시 부르시오,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr Carker was quick to do so, and Walter was quick to return.", "카커 씨가 잽싸게 그렇게 했고, 월터가 잽싸게 돌아왔다."]], [["“Gay,”", "\"게이,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, turning a little to look at him over his shoulder.", "돔비 씨가 어깨 너머로 그를 보려고 몸을 조금 돌리며 말했다."], ["“Here is a—”", "\"여기 한 가지—\""]], [["“An opening,”", "\"자리가 났네,\""], ["said Mr Carker, with his mouth stretched to the utmost.", "카커 씨가 입을 한껏 벌리며 말했다."]], [["“In the West Indies.", "\"서인도제도에 말일세."], ["At Barbados.", "바베이도스에."], ["I am going to send you,”", "자네를 보낼 작정이네,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, scorning to embellish the bare truth, “to fill a junior situation in the counting-house at Barbados.", "돔비 씨가 있는 그대로의 사실을 꾸미는 것은 천하다는 듯 말했다, \"바베이도스 회계실의 하급 자리를 채우라고 말일세."], ["Let your Uncle know from me, that I have chosen you to go to the West Indies.”", "내가 자네를 서인도제도로 보내기로 정했다고 자네 삼촌에게 내 말로 전하게.\""]], [["Walter’s breath was so completely taken away by his astonishment, that he could hardly find enough for the repetition of the words “West Indies.”", "월터는 놀라움에 숨이 완전히 막혀, \"서인도제도\"라는 말을 따라 할 만큼의 숨도 겨우 찾을 수 있었다."]], [["“Somebody must go,”", "\"누군가는 가야 하네,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “and you are young and healthy, and your Uncle’s circumstances are not good.", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"그리고 자네는 젊고 건강하며, 자네 삼촌의 형편은 좋지 않으니까."], ["Tell your Uncle that you are appointed.", "자네 삼촌에게 자네가 임명되었다고 전하게."], ["You will not go yet.", "당장 가는 건 아닐세."], ["There will be an interval of a month—or two perhaps.”", "한 달—어쩌면 두 달쯤—간격이 있을 거네.\""]], [["“Shall I remain there, Sir?”", "\"제가 거기 머무는 것입니까, 나리?\""], ["inquired Walter.", "월터가 물었다."]], [["“Will you remain there, Sir!”", "\"자네가 거기 머무느냐고, 자네!\""], ["repeated Mr Dombey, turning a little more round towards him.", "돔비 씨가 그를 향해 조금 더 돌아서며 되풀이했다. \"무슨 소린가?"], ["“What do you mean? What does he mean, Carker?”", "저 친구가 무슨 소리를 하는 건가, 카커?\""]], [["“Live there, Sir,”", "\"거기서 사는 것입니까, 나리,\""], ["faltered Walter.", "월터가 더듬거렸다."]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론이지,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대답했다."]], [["Walter bowed.", "월터는 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“That’s all,”", "\"그게 전부일세,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, resuming his letters.", "돔비 씨가 다시 편지로 눈을 돌리며 말했다."], ["“You will explain to him in good time about the usual outfit and so forth, Carker, of course.", "\"물론 자네가 적당한 때에 통상적인 채비며 그런 것들에 대해 그에게 설명해 주게, 카커."], ["He needn’t wait, Carker.”", "그는 더 기다릴 것 없네, 카커.\""]], [["“You needn’t wait, Gay,”", "\"자넨 더 기다릴 것 없네, 게이,\""], ["observed Mr Carker: bare to the gums.", "카커 씨가 잇몸까지 드러내며 말했다."]], [["“Unless,”", "\"다만,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, stopping in his reading without looking off the letter, and seeming to listen.", "돔비 씨가 편지에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 읽기를 멈추고, 귀를 기울이는 듯하며 말했다."], ["“Unless he has anything to say.”", "\"다만 그가 무슨 할 말이 있다면 모르지.\""]], [["“No, Sir,”", "\"없습니다, 나리,\" 월터가 대답했다."], ["returned Walter, agitated and confused, and almost stunned, as an infinite variety of pictures presented themselves to his mind; among which Captain Cuttle, in his glazed hat, transfixed with astonishment at Mrs MacStinger’s, and his uncle bemoaning his loss in the little back parlour, held prominent places.", "그는 동요하고 혼란스러웠으며, 거의 멍해져 있었으니, 무수히 다양한 그림들이 그의 마음속에 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그 가운데서도 번들거리는 모자를 쓴 커틀 선장이 맥스팅어 부인 댁에서 놀라움에 못 박혀 있는 모습, 그리고 삼촌이 작은 뒷방 응접실에서 그를 잃은 슬픔에 잠겨 있는 모습이 두드러진 자리를 차지했다."], ["“I hardly know—I—I am much obliged, Sir.”", "\"저는 잘 모르겠습니다—저는—대단히 감사합니다, 나리.\""]], [["“He needn’t wait, Carker,”", "\"그는 더 기다릴 것 없네, 카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["And as Mr Carker again echoed the words, and also collected his papers as if he were going away too, Walter felt that his lingering any longer would be an unpardonable intrusion—especially as he had nothing to say—and therefore walked out quite confounded.", "그리고 카커 씨가 다시 그 말을 따라 하고, 자기도 나갈 양으로 서류를 챙기자, 월터는 자기가 더 오래 머무는 것이—더구나 할 말도 없는 마당에—용서받지 못할 무례가 되리라 느꼈다. 그래서 그는 완전히 어리둥절한 채로 걸어 나갔다."]], [["Going along the passage, with the mingled consciousness and helplessness of a dream, he heard Mr Dombey’s door shut again, as Mr Carker came out: and immediately afterwards that gentleman called to him.", "복도를 따라가면서, 꿈을 꿀 때처럼 또렷한 의식과 무력함이 뒤섞인 채로, 그는 카커 씨가 나오면서 돔비 씨의 문이 다시 닫히는 소리를 들었다. 그리고 곧이어 그 신사가 그를 불렀다."]], [["“Bring your friend Mr Carker the Junior to my room, Sir, if you please.”", "\"괜찮다면 자네 친구 동생 카커 씨를 내 방으로 데려오게, 자네.\""]], [["Walter went to the outer office and apprised Mr Carker the Junior of his errand, who accordingly came out from behind a partition where he sat alone in one corner, and returned with him to the room of Mr Carker the Manager.", "월터는 바깥 사무실로 가서 동생 카커 씨에게 자기 심부름을 알렸다. 그러자 그는 한쪽 구석에 홀로 앉아 있던 칸막이 뒤에서 나와, 그와 함께 지배인 카커 씨의 방으로 돌아갔다."]], [["That gentleman was standing with his back to the fire, and his hands under his coat-tails, looking over his white cravat, as unpromisingly as Mr Dombey himself could have looked.", "그 신사는 불에 등을 대고, 두 손을 외투 자락 밑에 넣은 채, 흰 크라바트 너머로, 돔비 씨 자신이 지을 법한 만큼이나 가망 없는 표정으로 내려다보며 서 있었다."], ["He received them without any change in his attitude or softening of his harsh and black expression: merely signing to Walter to close the door.", "그는 자세를 조금도 바꾸지도, 그 거칠고 어두운 표정을 누그러뜨리지도 않은 채 그들을 맞이했다. 다만 월터에게 문을 닫으라고 손짓했을 뿐이다."]], [["“John Carker,”", "\"존 카커,\""], ["said the Manager, when this was done, turning suddenly upon his brother, with his two rows of teeth bristling as if he would have bitten him, “what is the league between you and this young man, in virtue of which I am haunted and hunted by the mention of your name?", "그렇게 되자, 지배인이 자기 형제에게 홱 돌아서며 말했다. 마치 그를 물어뜯기라도 할 것처럼 두 줄의 이가 곤두서 있었다."], ["Is it not enough for you, John Carker, that I am your near relation, and can’t detach myself from that—”", "\"당신과 이 젊은이 사이에 무슨 결탁이 있길래, 내가 당신 이름이 거론되는 것에 시달리고 쫓겨다녀야 하는 거요? 존 카커, 내가 당신의 가까운 혈육이고 그것에서 나를 떼어 낼 수 없다는 것만으로는 당신에게 부족하단 말이오, 그—\""]], [["“Say disgrace, James,”", "\"치욕이라고 하게, 제임스,\""], ["interposed the other in a low voice, finding that he stammered for a word.", "상대가 그가 단어를 찾느라 더듬거리는 것을 알아채고 낮은 목소리로 끼어들었다."], ["“You mean it, and have reason, say disgrace.”", "\"자네는 그걸 말하려는 거고, 그럴 만한 이유도 있지, 치욕이라고 하게.\""]], [["“From that disgrace,”", "\"그 치욕에서 말이오,\""], ["assented his brother with keen emphasis, “but is the fact to be blurted out and trumpeted, and proclaimed continually in the presence of the very House!", "그의 형제가 날카롭게 힘주어 동의했다, \"그러나 그 사실이 회사 바로 그 한복판에서 함부로 불쑥 내뱉어지고, 나발 불듯 떠벌려지고, 끊임없이 공표되어야 하겠소?"], ["In moments of confidence too?", "그것도 비밀스러운 순간에 말이오?"], ["Do you think your name is calculated to harmonise in this place with trust and confidence, John Carker?”", "존 카커, 당신은 당신 이름이 이곳에서 신뢰며 믿음과 조화를 이루기에 알맞다고 생각하시오?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니,\""], ["returned the other.", "상대가 대답했다."], ["“No, James.", "\"아닐세, 제임스."], ["God knows I have no such thought.”", "하느님은 아시겠지만 나는 그런 생각은 하지 않네.\""]], [["“What is your thought, then?”", "\"그럼 당신 생각은 뭐요?\""], ["said his brother, “and why do you thrust yourself in my way?", "그의 형제가 말했다, \"그리고 당신은 왜 내 앞길에 끼어드는 거요?"], ["Haven’t you injured me enough already?”", "이미 나를 충분히 해치지 않았소?\""]], [["“I have never injured you, James, wilfully.”", "\"나는 결코 일부러 자네를 해친 적이 없네, 제임스.\""]], [["“You are my brother,”", "\"당신은 내 형제요,\""], ["said the Manager.", "지배인이 말했다."], ["“That’s injury enough.”", "\"그것만으로도 충분한 해악이지.\""]], [["“I wish I could undo it, James.”", "\"내가 그것을 되돌릴 수 있다면 좋으련만, 제임스.\""]], [["“I wish you could and would.”", "\"당신이 그럴 수 있고 또 그러기를 바라오.\""]], [["During this conversation, Walter had looked from one brother to the other, with pain and amazement.", "이 대화가 오가는 동안, 월터는 고통과 놀라움에 차서 두 형제를 번갈아 바라보았다."], ["He who was the Senior in years, and Junior in the House, stood, with his eyes cast upon the ground, and his head bowed, humbly listening to the reproaches of the other.", "나이로는 형이요 회사에서는 아우인 이는, 눈을 바닥에 떨구고 고개를 숙인 채, 다른 이의 비난을 겸허히 듣고 서 있었다."], ["Though these were rendered very bitter by the tone and look with which they were accompanied, and by the presence of Walter whom they so much surprised and shocked, he entered no other protest against them than by slightly raising his right hand in a deprecatory manner, as if he would have said, “Spare me!”", "그 비난들은 그것에 동반된 어조와 눈빛 때문에, 또 그것에 그토록 놀라고 충격받은 월터의 존재 때문에 몹시 쓰라리게 들렸으나, 그는 다른 항의는 일절 내놓지 않고, 그저 오른손을 살짝 들어 만류하는 듯한 몸짓으로 마치 \"나를 좀 봐주게!\"라고 말하려는 듯할 뿐이었다."], ["So, had they been blows, and he a brave man, under strong constraint, and weakened by bodily suffering, he might have stood before the executioner.", "그리하여, 만일 그것이 매질이었고 그가 강한 억압 아래 놓인, 육체적 고통으로 쇠약해진 용감한 사내였더라면, 그는 사형 집행인 앞에 그렇게 서 있었을 것이다."]], [["Generous and quick in all his emotions, and regarding himself as the innocent occasion of these taunts, Walter now struck in, with all the earnestness he felt.", "모든 감정에 있어 너그럽고 빠른 월터는, 자기가 이 조롱의 무고한 빌미라 여기고는, 이제 자기가 느끼는 진심을 다해 끼어들었다."]], [["“Mr Carker,”", "\"카커 씨,\""], ["he said, addressing himself to the Manager.", "그가 지배인을 향해 말했다."], ["“Indeed, indeed, this is my fault solely.", "\"정말이지, 정말이지, 이건 오로지 제 잘못입니다."], ["In a kind of heedlessness, for which I cannot blame myself enough, I have, I have no doubt, mentioned Mr Carker the Junior much oftener than was necessary; and have allowed his name sometimes to slip through my lips, when it was against your expressed wish.", "일종의 경솔함 때문에—제 스스로를 아무리 탓해도 모자랄 그 경솔함 때문에—저는, 틀림없이, 필요 이상으로 훨씬 자주 동생 카커 씨를 언급했습니다. 그리고 때로는 당신께서 분명히 바라지 않으신 데도 그분의 이름이 제 입에서 새어 나오도록 했습니다."], ["But it has been my own mistake, Sir.", "그러나 그건 제 잘못이었습니다, 나리."], ["We have never exchanged one word upon the subject—very few, indeed, on any subject.", "우리는 그 일에 관해 단 한마디도 주고받은 적이 없습니다—사실 어떤 주제든 거의 주고받지 않았습니다."], ["And it has not been,”", "그리고 그것은,\""], ["added Walter, after a moment’s pause, “all heedlessness on my part, Sir; for I have felt an interest in Mr Carker ever since I have been here, and have hardly been able to help speaking of him sometimes, when I have thought of him so much!”", "월터가 잠시 사이를 두었다가 덧붙였다, \"전부 제 경솔함만은 아니었습니다, 나리. 왜냐하면 저는 여기 온 이래로 줄곧 카커 씨에게 관심을 느껴 왔고, 그분을 그토록 많이 생각하다 보니 이따금 그분에 대해 말하지 않을 수 없었기 때문입니다!\""]], [["Walter said this from his soul, and with the very breath of honour.", "월터는 이 말을 영혼으로부터, 명예의 바로 그 숨결로 했다. 그는 숙인 머리와, 내리뜬 눈과, 들어 올린 손을 바라보며 생각했던 것이다."], ["For he looked upon the bowed head, and the downcast eyes, and upraised hand, and thought, “I have felt it; and why should I not avow it in behalf of this unfriended, broken man!”", "\"나는 그것을 느꼈다. 그러니 이 의지할 데 없고 무너진 사람을 위해, 내가 그것을 밝히지 못할 까닭이 어디 있는가!\""]], [["Mr Carker the Manager looked at him, as he spoke, and when he had finished speaking, with a smile that seemed to divide his face into two parts.", "지배인 카커 씨는 그가 말하는 동안, 그리고 그가 말을 마쳤을 때, 얼굴을 두 쪽으로 갈라 놓는 듯한 웃음을 띠고 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“You are an excitable youth, Gay,”", "\"자네는 흥분 잘하는 젊은이로군, 게이,\""], ["he said; “and should endeavour to cool down a little now, for it would be unwise to encourage feverish predispositions.", "그가 말했다. \"그러니 이제 좀 가라앉히도록 애써야 하네, 열에 들뜬 기질을 부추기는 건 현명치 못한 일이니까."], ["Be as cool as you can, Gay.", "되도록 냉정하게, 게이."], ["Be as cool as you can.", "되도록 냉정하게."], ["You might have asked Mr John Carker himself (if you have not done so) whether he claims to be, or is, an object of such strong interest.”", "자네는 존 카커 씨 본인에게 물어볼 수도 있었을 텐데(아직 안 물어봤다면 말이지), 그가 그토록 강한 관심의 대상이라고 주장하는지, 또는 정말 그런 대상인지를 말일세.\""]], [["“James, do me justice,”", "\"제임스, 나를 공정하게 대해 주게,\""], ["said his brother.", "그의 형제가 말했다."], ["“I have claimed nothing; and I claim nothing.", "\"나는 아무것도 주장하지 않았고, 아무것도 주장하지 않네."], ["Believe me, on my—”", "믿어 주게, 내—\""]], [["“Honour?”", "\"명예를 걸고?\""], ["said his brother, with another smile, as he warmed himself before the fire.", "그의 형제가 불을 쬐며 또 한 번 웃음을 띠고 말했다."]], [["“On my Me—on my fallen life!”", "\"내 자신을 걸고—내 무너진 인생을 걸고!\""], ["returned the other, in the same low voice, but with a deeper stress on his words than he had yet seemed capable of giving them.", "상대가 똑같이 낮은 목소리로, 그러나 지금껏 그가 낼 수 있어 보이던 것보다 더 깊이 말에 힘을 실어 대답했다."], ["“Believe me, I have held myself aloof, and kept alone.", "\"믿어 주게, 나는 거리를 두어 왔고, 홀로 지내 왔네."], ["This has been unsought by me.", "이건 내가 바란 일이 아니야."], ["I have avoided him and everyone.", "나는 그를, 그리고 모두를 피해 왔네."]], [["“Indeed, you have avoided me, Mr Carker,”", "\"정말이지, 당신은 저를 피해 오셨습니다, 카커 씨,\""], ["said Walter, with the tears rising to his eyes; so true was his compassion.", "월터가 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 말했다. 그만큼 그의 연민은 진실했다."], ["“I know it, to my disappointment and regret.", "\"저는 그것을 압니다, 실망과 안타까움 속에서요."], ["When I first came here, and ever since, I am sure I have tried to be as much your friend, as one of my age could presume to be; but it has been of no use.", "제가 처음 여기 왔을 때부터, 그리고 그 이후로 줄곧, 저는 분명 제 나이로 감히 그럴 수 있는 만큼 당신의 벗이 되려고 애썼습니다. 그러나 그건 아무 소용이 없었습니다."]], [["“And observe,”", "\"그리고 명심하게,\""], ["said the Manager, taking him up quickly, “it will be of still less use, Gay, if you persist in forcing Mr John Carker’s name on people’s attention.", "지배인이 그의 말을 잽싸게 받아 말했다, \"그건 더더욱 소용없는 일이 될 걸세, 게이, 만일 자네가 존 카커 씨의 이름을 사람들의 주의에 끈질기게 들이민다면 말이지."], ["That is not the way to befriend Mr John Carker.", "그건 존 카커 씨를 위하는 길이 아닐세."], ["Ask him if he thinks it is.”", "그것이 위하는 길인지 그에게 물어보게.\""]], [["“It is no service to me,”", "\"그건 나를 위하는 일이 아닐세,\""], ["said the brother.", "그의 형제가 말했다."], ["“It only leads to such a conversation as the present, which I need not say I could have well spared.", "\"그건 그저 지금 같은 대화로 이어질 뿐이고, 그런 건 충분히 없어도 좋았다는 말을 굳이 할 필요도 없네."], ["No one can be a better friend to me:”", "그 누구도 내게 더 나은 벗이 될 수는 없어.\""], ["he spoke here very distinctly, as if he would impress it upon Walter: “than in forgetting me, and leaving me to go my way, unquestioned and unnoticed.”", "그는 마치 월터에게 그것을 새겨 주려는 듯, 여기서 아주 또박또박 말했다. \"나를 잊어 주고, 묻지도 주목하지도 않은 채 내 길을 가도록 내버려 두는 것보다 말이지.\""]], [["“Your memory not being retentive, Gay, of what you are told by others,”", "\"자네는 남이 일러 준 것을 잘 간직하는 기억력은 아니니까, 게이,\""], ["said Mr Carker the Manager, warming himself with great and increased satisfaction, “I thought it well that you should be told this from the best authority,”", "지배인 카커 씨가 크고 한층 더한 만족 속에 불을 쬐며 말했다, \"자네가 이것을 가장 확실한 권위로부터 듣는 게 좋겠다 싶었네.\""], ["nodding towards his brother.", "그러면서 그는 자기 형제를 향해 고갯짓을 했다."], ["“You are not likely to forget it now, I hope.", "\"이제는 잊지 않겠지, 그러기를 바라네."], ["That’s all, Gay.", "그게 전부일세, 게이."], ["You can go.”", "자넨 가도 좋네.\""]], [["Walter passed out at the door, and was about to close it after him, when, hearing the voices of the brothers again, and also the mention of his own name, he stood irresolutely, with his hand upon the lock, and the door ajar, uncertain whether to return or go away.", "월터는 문밖으로 나가, 뒤로 문을 닫으려던 참이었다. 그런데 다시 두 형제의 목소리가 들려오고, 또 자기 자신의 이름이 거론되는 것이 들려오자, 그는 손을 자물쇠에 댄 채 문을 빠끔히 열어 둔 채로, 돌아가야 할지 떠나야 할지 마음을 정하지 못하고 머뭇거리며 섰다."], ["In this position he could not help overhearing what followed.", "이런 상태에서 그는 뒤이어진 말을 엿듣지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Think of me more leniently, if you can, James,”", "\"나를 좀 더 너그럽게 생각해 주게, 그럴 수 있다면, 제임스,\""], ["said John Carker, “when I tell you I have had—how could I help having, with my history, written here”—striking himself upon the breast—“my whole heart awakened by my observation of that boy, Walter Gay.", "존 카커가 말했다, \"내 말하건대, 나는—내 가슴에 새겨진 이런 내력을 지니고서 어찌 안 그럴 수 있었겠나\"—그는 자기 가슴을 치며 말했다—\"저 소년, 월터 게이를 지켜보면서 내 온 마음이 깨어났다네."], ["I saw in him when he first came here, almost my other self.”", "그가 처음 여기 왔을 때 나는 그에게서 거의 또 다른 나 자신을 보았어.\""]], [["“Your other self!”", "\"또 다른 자네 자신이라!\""], ["repeated the Manager, disdainfully.", "지배인이 경멸하듯 되풀이했다."]], [["“Not as I am, but as I was when I first came here too; as sanguine, giddy, youthful, inexperienced; flushed with the same restless and adventurous fancies; and full of the same qualities, fraught with the same capacity of leading on to good or evil.”", "\"지금의 나가 아니라, 나 역시 처음 여기 왔을 때의 나 말일세. 그처럼 혈기왕성하고, 들떠 있고, 젊고, 미숙하며, 그와 똑같은 안절부절못하고 모험심에 찬 공상에 달아올라 있었고, 똑같은 자질로 가득 찬, 선으로도 악으로도 이끌어 갈 똑같은 가능성을 품은 나 말이야.\""]], [["“I hope not,”", "\"그렇지 않기를 바라네,\""], ["said his brother, with some hidden and sarcastic meaning in his tone.", "그의 형제가 어조 속에 어떤 감추어진 비꼬는 뜻을 담아 말했다."]], [["“You strike me sharply; and your hand is steady, and your thrust is very deep,”", "\"자네는 나를 날카롭게 찌르는군. 그리고 자네 손은 흔들림이 없고, 그 찌름은 매우 깊네,\""], ["returned the other, speaking (or so Walter thought) as if some cruel weapon actually stabbed him as he spoke.", "상대가 대답했다. 그는 (적어도 월터가 보기엔) 마치 말하는 동안 어떤 잔인한 무기가 실제로 자기를 찔러 대기라도 하는 듯 말했다."], ["“I imagined all this when he was a boy.", "\"나는 그가 소년이었을 때 이 모든 것을 상상했네."], ["I believed it.", "나는 그것을 믿었어."], ["It was a truth to me.", "내게 그것은 진실이었네."], ["I saw him lightly walking on the edge of an unseen gulf where so many others walk with equal gaiety, and from which—”", "나는 그가, 그토록 많은 다른 이들이 똑같이 들떠서 걷는, 그리고 거기서—보이지 않는 심연의 가장자리를 가뿐히 걷고 있는 것을 보았네.\""]], [["“The old excuse,”", "\"늘 하던 그 핑계로군,\""], ["interrupted his brother, as he stirred the fire.", "그의 형제가 불을 헤집으며 가로막았다. \"그토록 많은 이들이라."], ["“So many.", "계속하게."], ["Go on. Say, so many fall.”", "그토록 많은 이들이 떨어진다고 말하게.\""]], [["“From which ONE traveller fell,”", "\"거기서 한 나그네가 떨어졌네,\""], ["returned the other, “who set forward, on his way, a boy like him, and missed his footing more and more, and slipped a little and a little lower; and went on stumbling still, until he fell headlong and found himself below a shattered man.", "상대가 대답했다, \"그도 그와 같은 소년으로 제 길을 떠났다가, 점점 더 발을 헛디뎌, 조금씩 조금씩 더 아래로 미끄러졌고, 머리부터 곤두박질쳐 떨어져 무너진 인간으로 자기 자신을 발견하기까지 계속 비틀거리며 나아갔지."], ["Think what I suffered, when I watched that boy.”", "내가 그 소년을 지켜볼 때 무엇을 견뎠을지 생각해 보게.\""]], [["“You have only yourself to thank for it,”", "\"그건 자네가 자초한 일이니 자네 자신을 탓할 수밖에 없지,\""], ["returned the brother.", "그의 형제가 대꾸했다."]], [["“Only myself,”", "\"오직 나 자신을,\""], ["he assented with a sigh.", "그가 한숨을 쉬며 동의했다."], ["“I don’t seek to divide the blame or shame.”", "\"나는 그 책망이나 수치를 나누려는 게 아닐세.\""]], [["“You have divided the shame,”", "\"자네는 그 수치를 나눠 가졌지,\""], ["James Carker muttered through his teeth.", "제임스 카커가 이 사이로 중얼거렸다."], ["And, through so many and such close teeth, he could mutter well.", "그리고 그토록 많고 그토록 빽빽한 이 사이로, 그는 능히 잘 중얼거릴 수 있었다."]], [["“Ah, James,”", "\"아, 제임스,\""], ["returned his brother, speaking for the first time in an accent of reproach, and seeming, by the sound of his voice, to have covered his face with his hands, “I have been, since then, a useful foil to you.", "그의 형제가 처음으로 책망의 어조로 말하며, 그 목소리의 울림으로 보아 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 듯 대답했다, \"나는 그 이후로 자네에게 쓸모 있는 받침대가 되어 주었네."], ["You have trodden on me freely in your climbing up.", "자네는 위로 기어오르면서 나를 마음껏 밟고 디뎠지."], ["Don’t spurn me with your heel!”", "자네 발뒤꿈치로 나를 짓밟지는 말게!\""]], [["A silence ensued.", "침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["After a time, Mr Carker the Manager was heard rustling among his papers, as if he had resolved to bring the interview to a conclusion.", "얼마 뒤, 지배인 카커 씨가 마치 이 만남을 끝맺기로 작정한 듯 서류를 부스럭거리는 소리가 들렸다."], ["At the same time his brother withdrew nearer to the door.", "동시에 그의 형제는 문 쪽으로 더 가까이 물러섰다."]], [["“That’s all,”", "\"그게 전부일세,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I watched him with such trembling and such fear, as was some little punishment to me, until he passed the place where I first fell; and then, though I had been his father, I believe I never could have thanked God more devoutly.", "\"나는 그토록 떨면서, 그토록 두려워하면서 그를 지켜보았네—그건 내게 얼마간의 작은 벌이었지—그가 내가 처음 떨어졌던 그 자리를 지나갈 때까지 말이야. 그러고 나서는, 비록 내가 그의 아비였다 한들, 나는 그보다 더 경건하게 하느님께 감사할 수는 없었으리라 믿네."], ["I didn’t dare to warn him, and advise him; but if I had seen direct cause, I would have shown him my example.", "나는 감히 그에게 경고하고 충고하지 못했네. 그러나 만일 직접적인 까닭을 보았더라면, 나는 그에게 내 본보기를 보여 주었을 걸세."], ["I was afraid to be seen speaking with him, lest it should be thought I did him harm, and tempted him to evil, and corrupted him: or lest I really should.", "나는 그와 이야기하는 것이 남의 눈에 띌까 두려웠네—내가 그를 해치고, 악으로 유혹하고, 타락시킨다고 여겨질까 봐, 아니면 정말 내가 그렇게 할까 봐."], ["There may be such contagion in me; I don’t know.", "내게는 그런 전염성이 있을지도 모르지. 나는 모르네."], ["Piece out my history, in connexion with young Walter Gay, and what he has made me feel; and think of me more leniently, James, if you can.”", "내 내력을 젊은 월터 게이와 연관 지어, 그리고 그가 내게 느끼게 한 것과 연결 지어 짜 맞추어 보게. 그리고 나를 좀 더 너그럽게 생각해 주게, 제임스, 그럴 수 있다면.\""]], [["With these words he came out to where Walter was standing.", "이 말과 함께 그는 월터가 서 있는 곳으로 나왔다."], ["He turned a little paler when he saw him there, and paler yet when Walter caught him by the hand, and said in a whisper:", "그는 거기 있는 그를 보고 얼굴이 조금 창백해졌고, 월터가 그의 손을 붙들고 속삭이듯 말하자 더욱 창백해졌다."]], [["“Mr Carker, pray let me thank you!", "\"카커 씨, 부디 제가 당신께 감사드리게 해 주십시오!"], ["Let me say how much I feel for you!", "제가 당신을 얼마나 안타깝게 여기는지 말하게 해 주십시오!"], ["How sorry I am, to have been the unhappy cause of all this!", "이 모든 일의 불행한 빌미가 된 것이 제게 얼마나 미안한지를요!"], ["How I almost look upon you now as my protector and guardian!", "제가 이제 당신을 얼마나 제 보호자요 후견인처럼 여기게 되었는지를요!"], ["How very, very much, I feel obliged to you and pity you!”", "제가 당신께 얼마나, 정말이지 얼마나 깊이 은혜를 느끼고 당신을 가엾이 여기는지를요!\""], ["said Walter, squeezing both his hands, and hardly knowing, in his agitation, what he did or said.", "월터가 그의 두 손을 꼭 움켜쥐며, 동요한 나머지 자기가 무엇을 하고 무슨 말을 하는지도 거의 알지 못한 채 말했다."]], [["Mr Morfin’s room being close at hand and empty, and the door wide open, they moved thither by one accord: the passage being seldom free from someone passing to or fro.", "모핀 씨의 방이 바로 가까이 있고 비어 있는 데다 문이 활짝 열려 있었으므로, 두 사람은 약속이라도 한 듯 그리로 옮겨 갔다. 복도는 누군가 오가는 일이 좀처럼 끊이지 않았기 때문이다."], ["When they were there, and Walter saw in Mr Carker’s face some traces of the emotion within, he almost felt as if he had never seen the face before; it was so greatly changed.", "그곳에 이르러, 월터가 카커 씨의 얼굴에서 속에 든 감정의 흔적을 보았을 때, 그는 마치 그 얼굴을 전에 본 적이 없는 것 같은 느낌마저 들었다. 그만큼 그것은 크게 변해 있었다."]], [["“Walter,”", "\"월터,\""], ["he said, laying his hand on his shoulder.", "그가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다."], ["“I am far removed from you, and may I ever be.", "\"나는 자네에게서 멀리 떨어져 있고, 앞으로도 늘 그러기를 바라네."], ["Do you know what I am?”", "자네는 내가 어떤 사람인지 아는가?\""]], [["“What you are!” appeared to hang on Walter’s lips, as he regarded him attentively.", "\"당신이 어떤 사람인지요!\"라는 말이 월터의 입술에 매달려 있는 듯했다, 그가 그를 주의 깊게 바라보는 동안."]], [["“It was begun,”", "\"그것은 시작되었네,\""], ["said Carker, “before my twenty-first birthday—led up to, long before, but not begun till near that time.", "카커가 말했다, \"내 스물한 번째 생일이 되기 전에—훨씬 전부터 그리로 이끌려 갔지만, 그때 가까이 가서야 시작되었지."], ["I had robbed them when I came of age.", "나는 성년이 되었을 때 그들의 것을 훔쳤네."], ["I robbed them afterwards.", "그 후로도 훔쳤어."], ["Before my twenty-second birthday, it was all found out; and then, Walter, from all men’s society, I died.”", "내 스물두 번째 생일이 되기 전에 그것이 죄다 발각되었지. 그러고 나서, 월터, 나는 모든 사람들의 사회에서 죽었네.\""]], [["Again his last few words hung trembling upon Walter’s lips, but he could neither utter them, nor any of his own.", "다시 그의 마지막 몇 마디가 월터의 입술에 떨리며 매달렸으나, 그는 그 말도, 자기 자신의 말도 한마디 입 밖에 낼 수 없었다."]], [["“The House was very good to me.", "\"회사는 내게 무척 너그러웠네."], ["May Heaven reward the old man for his forbearance!", "그 노인의 관용에 하늘이 보답하시기를!"], ["This one, too, his son, who was then newly in the Firm, where I had held great trust!", "이 사람 또한, 그의 아들 또한, 당시 막 회사에 들어온 사람이었는데—거기서 나는 큰 신임을 받고 있었지!"], ["I was called into that room which is now his—I have never entered it since—and came out, what you know me.", "나는 지금 그의 방이 된 그 방으로 불려 갔네—나는 그 이후로 한 번도 거기 들어간 적이 없네—그리고 자네가 아는 이 모습으로 나왔지."], ["For many years I sat in my present seat, alone as now, but then a known and recognised example to the rest.", "여러 해 동안 나는 지금의 내 자리에, 지금처럼 홀로 앉아 있었네, 다만 그때는 다른 이들에게 잘 알려지고 인정된 본보기였지."], ["They were all merciful to me, and I lived.", "그들은 모두 내게 자비를 베풀었고, 그래서 나는 살아남았네."], ["Time has altered that part of my poor expiation; and I think, except the three heads of the House, there is no one here who knows my story rightly.", "세월이 내 가엾은 속죄의 그 부분을 바꾸어 놓았지. 그래서 내 생각엔, 회사의 세 우두머리를 빼고는, 이곳에 내 사연을 제대로 아는 이는 아무도 없네."], ["Before the little boy grows up, and has it told to him, my corner may be vacant.", "그 어린 사내아이가 자라나 그 이야기를 듣게 되기 전에, 내 구석 자리는 비게 될지도 모르지."], ["I would rather that it might be so!", "차라리 그렇게 되었으면 싶네!"], ["This is the only change to me since that day, when I left all youth, and hope, and good men’s company, behind me in that room.", "이것이 그날 이후로 내게 일어난 유일한 변화일세—내가 모든 젊음과 희망과 선한 이들의 벗을 저 방 안에 두고 떠나온 그날 이후로 말이야. 하느님께서 자네에게 복 주시기를, 월터!"], ["God bless you, Walter! Keep you, and all dear to you, in honesty, or strike them dead!”", "자네와, 자네에게 소중한 모든 이를 정직 속에 지켜 주시기를, 그러지 못할 바엔 그들을 쳐서 죽게 하시기를!\""]], [["Some recollection of his trembling from head to foot, as if with excessive cold, and of his bursting into tears, was all that Walter could add to this, when he tried to recall exactly what had passed between them.", "그가 마치 지독한 추위에 떨듯 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨던 것과, 울음을 터뜨리던 것에 대한 어떤 기억이, 두 사람 사이에 정확히 무슨 일이 오갔는지 떠올리려 애썼을 때 월터가 거기에 보탤 수 있는 전부였다."]], [["When Walter saw him next, he was bending over his desk in his old silent, drooping, humbled way.", "월터가 그를 다음에 보았을 때, 그는 예전처럼 말없이, 축 늘어진, 풀죽은 모습으로 자기 책상 위에 몸을 굽히고 있었다."], ["Then, observing him at his work, and feeling how resolved he evidently was that no further intercourse should arise between them, and thinking again and again on all he had seen and heard that morning in so short a time, in connexion with the history of both the Carkers, Walter could hardly believe that he was under orders for the West Indies, and would soon be lost to Uncle Sol, and Captain Cuttle, and to glimpses few and far between of Florence Dombey—no, he meant Paul—and to all he loved, and liked, and looked for, in his daily life.", "그러자 그가 일하는 모습을 지켜보며, 그가 분명 두 사람 사이에 더 이상의 교류가 생기지 않도록 단단히 결심했음을 느끼면서, 그리고 그 짧은 시간 동안 그날 아침 두 카커 형제의 내력과 관련해 보고 들은 모든 것을 거듭 되새기면서, 월터는 자기가 서인도제도행 명령을 받았다는 것, 그리고 머잖아 솔 삼촌과 커틀 선장에게서, 또 플로렌스 돔비를—아니, 폴을 말하는 것이었다—드물게 어쩌다 한 번씩 보던 그 모습에서, 그리고 자기가 일상 속에서 사랑하고 좋아하며 기다리던 모든 것에서 떠나가게 되리라는 것을 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다."]], [["But it was true, and the news had already penetrated to the outer office; for while he sat with a heavy heart, pondering on these things, and resting his head upon his arm, Perch the messenger, descending from his mahogany bracket, and jogging his elbow, begged his pardon, but wished to say in his ear, Did he think he could arrange to send home to England a jar of preserved Ginger, cheap, for Mrs Perch’s own eating, in the course of her recovery from her next confinement?", "그러나 그것은 사실이었고, 그 소식은 이미 바깥 사무실까지 스며들어 있었다. 그가 무거운 마음으로 앉아 이런 일들을 곱씹으며 팔에 머리를 괴고 있을 때, 전령 퍼치가 자기 마호가니 받침 선반에서 내려와 그의 팔꿈치를 슬쩍 건드리며 양해를 구하더니, 귀에 대고 한마디 하고 싶다고 했다. 그가 보기엔, 다음번 산후조리에서 회복하는 동안 퍼치 부인이 직접 먹게끔, 절인 생강 한 단지를 싸게 영국으로 부쳐 줄 수 있도록 손을 좀 써 줄 수 있겠느냐는 것이었다."]], [["CHAPTER XIV. Paul grows more and more Old-fashioned, and goes Home for the Holidays\nWhen the Midsummer vacation approached, no indecent manifestations of joy were exhibited by the leaden-eyed young gentlemen assembled at Doctor Blimber’s.", "한여름 방학이 다가왔을 때, 블림버 박사의 학교에 모인, 눈빛이 흐리멍덩한 어린 신사들 사이에서는 기쁨의 점잖지 못한 표출 같은 것은 조금도 보이지 않았다."], ["Any such violent expression as “breaking up,”", "\"학교 파(罷)\""], ["would have been quite inapplicable to that polite establishment.", "같은 그런 격한 표현은 이 품위 있는 학원에는 전혀 어울리지 않았으리라."], ["The young gentlemen oozed away, semi-annually, to their own homes; but they never broke up.", "어린 신사들은 반년마다 자기네 집으로 스며 나갔다. 그러나 그들은 결코 학교를 \"파\"하지 않았다."], ["They would have scorned the action.", "그런 행동은 경멸했을 것이다."]], [["Tozer, who was constantly galled and tormented by a starched white cambric neckerchief, which he wore at the express desire of Mrs Tozer, his parent, who, designing him for the Church, was of opinion that he couldn’t be in that forward state of preparation too soon—Tozer said, indeed, that choosing between two evils, he thought he would rather stay where he was, than go home.", "토저는 빳빳하게 풀먹인 흰 케임브릭 목도리에 늘 쓸리고 시달렸는데, 그것은 그의 부모인 토저 부인의 명확한 바람에 따라 두른 것이었다."], ["However inconsistent this declaration might appear with that passage in Tozer’s Essay on the subject, wherein he had observed “that the thoughts of home and all its recollections, awakened in his mind the most pleasing emotions of anticipation and delight,”", "토저 부인은 아들을 성직자로 만들 작정이었던지라, 그렇게 미리 준비된 상태에 일찌감치 들어서는 것이 결코 이르지 않다는 의견이었다. 토저는 사실 두 가지 악 가운데 골라야 한다면 집에 가느니 차라리 여기 남는 편이 낫겠다고 말했다."], ["and had also likened himself to a Roman General, flushed with a recent victory over the Iceni, or laden with Carthaginian spoil, advancing within a few hours’ march of the Capitol, presupposed, for the purposes of the simile, to be the dwelling-place of Mrs Tozer, still it was very sincerely made.", "이 선언이 그 주제에 관한 토저의 작문 가운데, \"집 생각과 그에 얽힌 온갖 추억은 그의 마음속에 기대와 기쁨이라는 가장 흐뭇한 정서를 일깨운다\"고 적은 대목과, 또 자신을 이세니족을 막 무찌른 승리에 들떴거나 카르타고의 전리품을 잔뜩 짊어진 채 카피톨리누스—이 비유의 목적상 토저 부인의 거처라고 미리 상정해 둔—를 몇 시간의 행군 거리 안까지 진격해 가는 로마 장군에 견준 대목과는 아무리 모순되어 보일지라도, 그래도 그것은 매우 진심에서 나온 말이었다."], ["For it seemed that Tozer had a dreadful Uncle, who not only volunteered examinations of him, in the holidays, on abstruse points, but twisted innocent events and things, and wrenched them to the same fell purpose.", "왜냐하면 토저에게는 무시무시한 삼촌이 하나 있는 듯했는데, 그 삼촌은 방학 중에 난해한 논점들을 두고 그에게 자청해서 시험을 보일 뿐 아니라, 죄 없는 사건이며 사물들을 비틀고 쥐어짜 같은 흉악한 목적에 갖다 붙였기 때문이다."], ["So that if this Uncle took him to the Play, or, on a similar pretence of kindness, carried him to see a Giant, or a Dwarf, or a Conjuror, or anything, Tozer knew he had read up some classical allusion to the subject beforehand, and was thrown into a state of mortal apprehension: not foreseeing where he might break out, or what authority he might not quote against him.", "그래서 만일 이 삼촌이 그를 연극 구경에 데려가거나, 혹은 비슷하게 친절을 가장한 핑계로 거인이나 난쟁이나 마술사나 그 무엇이든 보러 데려가면, 토저는 삼촌이 그 주제에 관한 고전 인용을 미리 읽고 왔음을 알았기에 죽을 듯한 두려움에 빠지곤 했다. 삼촌이 어디서 그것을 터뜨릴지, 또 자기를 상대로 어떤 전거를 들이댈지 도무지 예측할 수 없었던 까닭이다."]], [["As to Briggs, his father made no show of artifice about it. He never would leave him alone.", "브리그스로 말하자면, 그 아버지는 책략 같은 것을 꾸미는 시늉조차 하지 않았다."], ["So numerous and severe were the mental trials of that unfortunate youth in vacation time, that the friends of the family (then resident near Bayswater, London) seldom approached the ornamental piece of water in Kensington Gardens, without a vague expectation of seeing Master Briggs’s hat floating on the surface, and an unfinished exercise lying on the bank.", "그는 결코 아들을 가만히 내버려두지 않았다. 그 불운한 소년이 방학 동안 겪는 정신적 시련이 어찌나 숱하고 가혹했던지, 그 집안의 친구들은(당시 런던 베이스워터 근처에 살았다) 켄싱턴 가든의 장식용 연못에 다가갈 때면 거의 언제나 막연히, 브리그스 도련님의 모자가 수면에 떠다니고 미처 끝내지 못한 연습 문제가 둑에 놓여 있는 광경을 보게 되지 않을까 하는 기대를 품곤 했다."], ["Briggs, therefore, was not at all sanguine on the subject of holidays; and these two sharers of little Paul’s bedroom were so fair a sample of the young gentlemen in general, that the most elastic among them contemplated the arrival of those festive periods with genteel resignation.", "그러니 브리그스는 방학이라는 주제에 대해 조금도 낙관적이지 않았다. 그리고 어린 폴의 침실을 함께 쓰는 이 두 동무는 어린 신사들 전반의 매우 적절한 표본이어서, 그들 가운데 가장 탄력 있는 축조차 그 축제 같은 시기의 도래를 점잖은 체념으로 바라보았다."]], [["It was far otherwise with little Paul.", "어린 폴은 사정이 전혀 달랐다."], ["The end of these first holidays was to witness his separation from Florence, but who ever looked forward to the end of holidays whose beginning was not yet come!", "이 첫 방학이 끝날 무렵이면 그는 플로렌스와 헤어지게 될 터였지만, 아직 시작되지도 않은 방학의 끝을 미리 내다보는 사람이 어디 있겠는가!"], ["Not Paul, assuredly.", "폴은 분명 아니었다."], ["As the happy time drew near, the lions and tigers climbing up the bedroom walls became quite tame and frolicsome.", "그 행복한 때가 가까워지자 침실 벽을 기어오르던 사자와 호랑이들은 아주 온순하고 장난스러워졌다."], ["The grim sly faces in the squares and diamonds of the floor-cloth, relaxed and peeped out at him with less wicked eyes.", "바닥 깔개의 네모와 마름모 무늬 속에 음흉하게 도사린 험상궂은 얼굴들도 누그러져, 한결 덜 사악한 눈으로 그를 빠끔 내다보았다."], ["The grave old clock had more of personal interest in the tone of its formal inquiry; and the restless sea went rolling on all night, to the sounding of a melancholy strain—yet it was pleasant too—that rose and fell with the waves, and rocked him, as it were, to sleep.", "근엄한 낡은 시계는 그 격식 차린 물음의 어조에 더 많은 개인적 관심을 담았고, 쉴 새 없는 바다는 밤새도록 굽이쳐 흐르며 구슬픈—그러나 그래도 기분 좋은—가락을 울렸으니, 그 가락은 물결과 더불어 솟았다 가라앉으며 그를 말하자면 흔들어 잠재워 주었다."]], [["Mr Feeder, B.A., seemed to think that he, too, would enjoy the holidays very much.", "문학사 피더 선생도 자기 역시 방학을 무척 즐기게 되리라 여기는 듯했다. 투츠 씨는 그때부터 죽 방학 같은 삶을 살아갈 작정이었다."], ["Mr Toots projected a life of holidays from that time forth; for, as he regularly informed Paul every day, it was his “last half” at Doctor Blimber’s, and he was going to begin to come into his property directly.", "그가 날마다 어김없이 폴에게 알려 주었듯이, 이번이 블림버 박사 학교에서의 \"마지막 학기\"였고, 곧장 자기 재산을 물려받기 시작할 참이었기 때문이다."]], [["It was perfectly understood between Paul and Mr Toots, that they were intimate friends, notwithstanding their distance in point of years and station.", "폴과 투츠 씨 사이에서는, 나이와 신분의 거리에도 불구하고 둘이 친밀한 벗이라는 사실이 완벽하게 양해되어 있었다."], ["As the vacation approached, and Mr Toots breathed harder and stared oftener in Paul’s society, than he had done before, Paul knew that he meant he was sorry they were going to lose sight of each other, and felt very much obliged to him for his patronage and good opinion.", "방학이 다가오면서 투츠 씨가 폴과 함께 있을 때 전보다 더 가쁘게 숨을 쉬고 더 자주 멍하니 바라보았는데, 폴은 그것이 둘이 서로 못 보게 되는 것을 그가 아쉬워한다는 뜻임을 알았고, 그의 후원과 호의에 무척 고마움을 느꼈다."], ["It was even understood by Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, and Miss Blimber, as well as by the young gentlemen in general, that Toots had somehow constituted himself protector and guardian of Dombey, and the circumstance became so notorious, even to Mrs Pipchin, that the good old creature cherished feelings of bitterness and jealousy against Toots; and, in the sanctuary of her own home, repeatedly denounced him as a “chuckle-headed noodle.”", "게다가 블림버 박사와 블림버 부인과 블림버 양은 물론 어린 신사들 전반에게도, 투츠가 어쩌다 보니 돔비의 보호자이자 후견인을 자처하고 나섰다는 것이 양해되어 있었고, 그 사정은 피프친 부인에게까지 알려질 만큼 널리 소문이 나서, 그 마음씨 좋은 노파는 투츠에 대해 쓰라림과 질투의 감정을 품게 되었다. 그래서 자기 집이라는 성역 안에서는 그를 \"멍청한 얼간이\"라며 거듭 비난했다."], ["Whereas the innocent Toots had no more idea of awakening Mrs Pipchin’s wrath, than he had of any other definite possibility or proposition.", "그러나 순진한 투츠는 피프친 부인의 분노를 살 생각이라곤, 다른 어떤 분명한 가능성이나 명제만큼이나 조금도 없었다."], ["On the contrary, he was disposed to consider her rather a remarkable character, with many points of interest about her.", "오히려 그는 그녀를 흥미로운 점이 많은, 꽤 놀라운 인물로 여기는 쪽이었다."], ["For this reason he smiled on her with so much urbanity, and asked her how she did, so often, in the course of her visits to little Paul, that at last she one night told him plainly, she wasn’t used to it, whatever he might think; and she could not, and she would not bear it, either from himself or any other puppy then existing: at which unexpected acknowledgment of his civilities, Mr Toots was so alarmed that he secreted himself in a retired spot until she had gone.", "이런 까닭에 그는 그녀가 어린 폴을 방문하는 동안 어찌나 정중하게 미소 지으며 안부를 자주 물었던지, 마침내 어느 날 밤 그녀가 그에게 대놓고 말하기를, 그가 어떻게 생각하든 자기는 그런 데 익숙지 않으며, 그에게서든 당시 살아 있는 그 어떤 풋내기에게서든 그런 것을 참을 수 없고 또 참지 않겠다고 했다. 자신의 정중함에 대한 이 뜻밖의 응답에 투츠 씨는 어찌나 놀랐던지, 그녀가 떠날 때까지 외진 구석에 몸을 숨겼다."], ["Nor did he ever again face the doughty Mrs Pipchin, under Doctor Blimber’s roof.", "그리고 다시는 블림버 박사의 지붕 아래에서 그 억센 피프친 부인과 맞닥뜨리지 않았다."]], [["They were within two or three weeks of the holidays, when, one day, Cornelia Blimber called Paul into her room, and said, “Dombey, I am going to send home your analysis.”", "방학이 두세 주쯤 남았을 무렵, 어느 날 코닐리어 블림버가 폴을 자기 방으로 불러 말했다. \"돔비, 네 분석표를 집으로 보낼 거란다.\""]], [["“Thank you, Ma’am,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선생님,\""], ["returned Paul.", "폴이 대답했다."]], [["“You know what I mean, do you, Dombey?”", "\"내가 무슨 말을 하는지 알겠니, 돔비?\""], ["inquired Miss Blimber, looking hard at him, through the spectacles.", "블림버 양이 안경 너머로 그를 뚫어지게 보며 물었다."]], [["“No, Ma’am,”", "\"아니요, 선생님,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“Dombey, Dombey,”", "\"돔비, 돔비,\""], ["said Miss Blimber, “I begin to be afraid you are a sad boy.", "블림버 양이 말했다, \"네가 딱한 아이가 아닐까 슬슬 걱정되는구나."], ["When you don’t know the meaning of an expression, why don’t you seek for information?”", "어떤 표현의 뜻을 모르면, 왜 정보를 구하지 않니?\""]], [["“Mrs Pipchin told me I wasn’t to ask questions,”", "\"피프친 부인이 저더러 질문을 하면 안 된다고 했어요,\""], ["returned Paul.", "폴이 대답했다."]], [["“I must beg you not to mention Mrs Pipchin to me, on any account, Dombey,”", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 내게 피프친 부인 얘기를 꺼내지 말아 주기 바란다, 돔비,\""], ["returned Miss Blimber.", "블림버 양이 응수했다."], ["“I couldn’t think of allowing it.", "\"나는 그것을 허용할 생각이 조금도 없어."], ["The course of study here, is very far removed from anything of that sort.", "여기서 하는 학습 과정은 그런 따위와는 한참 동떨어져 있단다."], ["A repetition of such allusions would make it necessary for me to request to hear, without a mistake, before breakfast-time to-morrow morning, from Verbum personale down to simillimia cygno.”", "그런 언급을 한 번만 더 하면, 내일 아침 식사 전까지 베르붐 페르소날레부터 시밀리마 키그노까지 한 군데도 틀리지 않고 외워 들려 달라고 청할 수밖에 없게 될 거야.\""]], [["“I didn’t mean, Ma’am—”", "\"저는 그런 뜻이—\""], ["began little Paul.", "어린 폴이 입을 떼었다."]], [["“I must trouble you not to tell me that you didn’t mean, if you please, Dombey,”", "\"미안하지만 그런 뜻이 아니었다는 말은 하지 말아 줬으면 한다, 돔비,\""], ["said Miss Blimber, who preserved an awful politeness in her admonitions.", "블림버 양이 말했다. 그녀는 훈계를 할 때에도 무시무시한 정중함을 잃지 않았다."], ["“That is a line of argument I couldn’t dream of permitting.”", "\"그건 내가 도저히 용납할 꿈도 못 꿀 논법이야.\""]], [["Paul felt it safest to say nothing at all, so he only looked at Miss Blimber’s spectacles.", "폴은 아무 말도 하지 않는 편이 가장 안전하겠다 싶어, 그저 블림버 양의 안경만 바라보았다."], ["Miss Blimber having shaken her head at him gravely, referred to a paper lying before her.", "블림버 양은 그를 향해 근엄하게 고개를 절레절레 흔든 뒤, 앞에 놓인 종이를 들여다보았다."]], [["“‘Analysis of the character of P.", "\"'P. 돔비의 성격 분석.'"], ["Dombey.’ If my recollection serves me,”", "내 기억이 맞다면,\""], ["said Miss Blimber breaking off, “the word analysis as opposed to synthesis, is thus defined by Walker.", "블림버 양이 말을 끊으며 말했다, \"분석이라는 낱말은 종합과 대비되어 워커가 이렇게 정의했단다."], ["‘The resolution of an object, whether of the senses or of the intellect, into its first elements.’ As opposed to synthesis, you observe.", "'감각의 대상이든 지성의 대상이든, 한 대상을 그 최초의 요소들로 분해하는 것.' 종합과 대비되어, 알겠지."], ["Now you know what analysis is, Dombey.”", "자, 이제 분석이 무엇인지 알겠지, 돔비.\""]], [["Dombey didn’t seem to be absolutely blinded by the light let in upon his intellect, but he made Miss Blimber a little bow.", "돔비는 자기 지성 속으로 쏟아져 들어온 그 빛에 완전히 눈이 멀어 버린 것 같지는 않았으나, 블림버 양에게 살짝 고개를 숙여 보였다."]], [["“‘Analysis,’”", "\"'P."], ["resumed Miss Blimber, casting her eye over the paper, “‘of the character of P.", "돔비의 성격 분석,'\" 블림버 양이 종이에 눈길을 던지며 다시 시작했다."], ["Dombey.’ I find that the natural capacity of Dombey is extremely good; and that his general disposition to study may be stated in an equal ratio.", "\"돔비의 타고난 자질은 매우 우수하며, 학습에 대한 전반적 성향도 그와 동등한 비율로 진술될 수 있음을 본다."], ["Thus, taking eight as our standard and highest number, I find these qualities in Dombey stated each at six three-fourths!”", "따라서 여덟을 우리의 기준이자 최고 수치로 삼을 때, 돔비에게서 이 자질들은 각각 6과 4분의 3으로 진술됨을 본다!\""]], [["Miss Blimber paused to see how Paul received this news.", "블림버 양은 폴이 이 소식을 어떻게 받아들이는지 보려고 잠시 멈추었다."], ["Being undecided whether six three-fourths meant six pounds fifteen, or sixpence three farthings, or six foot three, or three quarters past six, or six somethings that he hadn’t learnt yet, with three unknown something elses over, Paul rubbed his hands and looked straight at Miss Blimber.", "6과 4분의 3이 6파운드 15실링을 뜻하는지, 6펜스 3파딩인지, 6피트 3인치인지, 6시 45분인지, 아니면 아직 배우지 못한 무언가 여섯에 알 수 없는 또 다른 무언가 셋이 더해진 것인지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채, 폴은 두 손을 비비며 블림버 양을 똑바로 바라보았다."], ["It happened to answer as well as anything else he could have done; and Cornelia proceeded.", "그것은 마침 그가 할 수 있는 다른 어떤 행동 못지않게 적절한 반응이 되었고, 코닐리어는 계속했다."]], [["“‘Violence two.", "\"'난폭함 둘."], ["Selfishness two.", "이기심 둘."], ["Inclination to low company, as evinced in the case of a person named Glubb, originally seven, but since reduced.", "글럽이라는 자의 경우에서 드러난 바와 같이 천한 무리와 어울리려는 경향, 처음에는 일곱이었으나 이후 줄어듦."], ["Gentlemanly demeanour four, and improving with advancing years.’ Now what I particularly wish to call your attention to, Dombey, is the general observation at the close of this analysis.”", "신사다운 태도 넷, 나이가 들수록 향상되고 있음.' 자, 돔비, 내가 특히 네 주의를 환기하고 싶은 것은 이 분석의 끝에 붙은 전반적 소견이란다.\""]], [["Paul set himself to follow it with great care.", "폴은 무척 주의 깊게 그것을 따라가려 했다."]], [["“‘It may be generally observed of Dombey,’”", "\"'돔비에 관해 전반적으로 다음과 같이 관찰될 수 있다,'\""], ["said Miss Blimber, reading in a loud voice, and at every second word directing her spectacles towards the little figure before her: “‘that his abilities and inclinations are good, and that he has made as much progress as under the circumstances could have been expected.", "블림버 양이 큰 소리로 읽으며, 두 낱말마다 한 번씩 안경을 앞에 선 그 자그마한 형체 쪽으로 향했다. \"'그의 능력과 성향은 양호하며, 처지를 고려할 때 기대할 수 있는 만큼의 진보를 이루었다."], ["But it is to be lamented of this young gentleman that he is singular (what is usually termed old-fashioned) in his character and conduct, and that, without presenting anything in either which distinctly calls for reprobation, he is often very unlike other young gentlemen of his age and social position.’ Now, Dombey,”", "그러나 이 어린 신사에 관해 한탄스러운 바는, 그가 성격과 행실에서 특이하다는(흔히 노숙(老熟)하다고 일컬어지는) 점이며, 그 어느 쪽에서도 분명히 비난을 요할 만한 것을 드러내지 않으면서도, 같은 나이와 사회적 지위의 다른 어린 신사들과 자주 매우 다르다는 점이다.' 자, 돔비,\""], ["said Miss Blimber, laying down the paper, “do you understand that?”", "블림버 양이 종이를 내려놓으며 말했다, \"이게 무슨 말인지 알겠니?\""]], [["“I think I do, Ma’am,”", "\"알 것 같아요, 선생님,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“This analysis, you see, Dombey,”", "\"이 분석은, 알겠지, 돔비,\""], ["Miss Blimber continued, “is going to be sent home to your respected parent.", "블림버 양이 이어 말했다, \"존경하는 네 아버님께 보내질 거란다."], ["It will naturally be very painful to him to find that you are singular in your character and conduct.", "네가 성격과 행실에서 특이하다는 것을 아시면 당연히 무척 마음 아프실 거야. 우리에게도 당연히 마음 아픈 일이고."], ["It is naturally painful to us; for we can’t like you, you know, Dombey, as well as we could wish.”", "왜냐하면 우리는, 알다시피, 돔비, 우리가 바라는 만큼 너를 좋아할 수가 없으니까.\""]], [["She touched the child upon a tender point.", "그녀는 아이의 여린 곳을 건드렸다."], ["He had secretly become more and more solicitous from day to day, as the time of his departure drew more near, that all the house should like him.", "떠날 때가 점점 가까워질수록, 그는 날이 갈수록 남몰래 더더욱, 온 집안이 자기를 좋아해 주기를 간절히 바라게 되었던 것이다."], ["From some hidden reason, very imperfectly understood by himself—if understood at all—he felt a gradually increasing impulse of affection, towards almost everything and everybody in the place.", "자기 자신도 잘 이해하지 못하는—이해한다 해도 매우 불완전하게밖에 이해하지 못하는—어떤 숨은 이유에서, 그는 이곳의 거의 모든 것과 모든 사람을 향해 차츰 커져 가는 애정의 충동을 느꼈다."], ["He could not bear to think that they would be quite indifferent to him when he was gone.", "자기가 떠나고 나면 그들이 자기에게 아주 무심해지리라는 생각을 견딜 수 없었다."], ["He wanted them to remember him kindly; and he had made it his business even to conciliate a great hoarse shaggy dog, chained up at the back of the house, who had previously been the terror of his life: that even he might miss him when he was no longer there.", "그는 그들이 자기를 다정하게 기억해 주기를 바랐다. 그래서 그는, 전에는 자기 삶의 공포였던, 집 뒤편에 사슬로 묶인 크고 목쉰 텁수룩한 개까지도 어떻게든 구슬리는 일을 자기 임무로 삼았으니, 자기가 더는 그곳에 없을 때 그 개마저도 자기를 그리워해 주기를 바라서였다."]], [["Little thinking that in this, he only showed again the difference between himself and his compeers, poor tiny Paul set it forth to Miss Blimber as well as he could, and begged her, in despite of the official analysis, to have the goodness to try and like him.", "이 점에서도 자기와 또래들 사이의 차이를 다시 한번 드러낼 뿐이라는 것은 까맣게 모른 채, 가엾고 자그마한 폴은 그것을 블림버 양에게 할 수 있는 한 잘 펼쳐 보였고, 공식 분석표에도 불구하고 부디 자기를 좋아해 보려고 애써 주십사 청했다."], ["To Mrs Blimber, who had joined them, he preferred the same petition: and when that lady could not forbear, even in his presence, from giving utterance to her often-repeated opinion, that he was an odd child, Paul told her that he was sure she was quite right; that he thought it must be his bones, but he didn’t know; and that he hoped she would overlook it, for he was fond of them all.", "그 자리에 합류한 블림버 부인에게도 그는 같은 부탁을 올렸다. 그리고 그 부인이 그 앞에서까지도, 그가 별난 아이라는 자기가 늘 되풀이하던 의견을 입 밖에 내지 않을 수 없었을 때, 폴은 그녀에게, 부인 말씀이 아주 옳은 게 분명하다고, 자기 생각엔 자기 뼈 때문인 것 같지만 잘은 모르겠다고, 그러나 자기는 모두를 좋아하니 부디 그 점을 너그러이 봐주시기 바란다고 말했다."]], [["“Not so fond,”", "\"그렇게까지는 좋아하지 않지만요,\""], ["said Paul, with a mixture of timidity and perfect frankness, which was one of the most peculiar and most engaging qualities of the child, “not so fond as I am of Florence, of course; that could never be.", "폴이, 그 아이의 가장 독특하고 가장 사람을 끄는 자질 가운데 하나인, 수줍음과 완벽한 솔직함이 뒤섞인 투로 말했다, \"당연히 플로렌스만큼 좋아하지는 않지요. 그건 절대 그럴 수 없어요."], ["You couldn’t expect that, could you, Ma’am?”", "그걸 기대하실 수는 없잖아요, 그렇죠, 부인?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["the old-fashioned little soul!”", "노숙한 어린 영혼 같으니!\""], ["cried Mrs Blimber, in a whisper.", "블림버 부인이 속삭이듯 외쳤다."]], [["“But I like everybody here very much,”", "\"하지만 저는 여기 있는 모두를 무척 좋아해요,\""], ["pursued Paul, “and I should grieve to go away, and think that anyone was glad that I was gone, or didn’t care.”", "폴이 이어 말했다, \"그래서 떠나면서, 제가 가는 걸 누군가가 기뻐한다거나 신경도 안 쓴다고 생각하면 슬플 거예요.\""]], [["Mrs Blimber was now quite sure that Paul was the oddest child in the world; and when she told the Doctor what had passed, the Doctor did not controvert his wife’s opinion.", "블림버 부인은 이제 폴이 세상에서 가장 별난 아이라고 완전히 확신했다. 그리고 박사에게 무슨 일이 있었는지 이야기하자, 박사도 아내의 의견에 이의를 달지 않았다."], ["But he said, as he had said before, when Paul first came, that study would do much; and he also said, as he had said on that occasion, “Bring him on, Cornelia!", "다만 그는, 폴이 처음 왔을 때 했던 말처럼, 공부가 많은 것을 해 줄 거라고 했다. 또 그때처럼, \"끌어올려, 코닐리어!"], ["Bring him on!”", "끌어올려!\"라고도 했다."]], [["Cornelia had always brought him on as vigorously as she could; and Paul had had a hard life of it.", "코닐리어는 늘 할 수 있는 한 힘차게 그를 끌어올려 왔고, 폴은 고된 나날을 보냈다."], ["But over and above the getting through his tasks, he had long had another purpose always present to him, and to which he still held fast.", "그러나 과제를 헤쳐 나가는 일을 넘어, 그에게는 오래도록 늘 마음에 품고 또 여전히 굳게 붙들고 있는 또 다른 목적이 있었다."], ["It was, to be a gentle, useful, quiet little fellow, always striving to secure the love and attachment of the rest; and though he was yet often to be seen at his old post on the stairs, or watching the waves and clouds from his solitary window, he was oftener found, too, among the other boys, modestly rendering them some little voluntary service.", "그것은 온순하고 쓸모 있고 조용한 꼬마가 되어, 나머지 모두의 사랑과 정을 얻으려 늘 애쓰는 것이었다. 그래서 그는 여전히 계단의 그 옛 자리에 앉아 있거나 외딴 창에서 물결과 구름을 바라보는 모습으로 자주 보이긴 했어도, 다른 아이들 틈에서 겸손하게 자그마한 자발적 봉사를 베푸는 모습으로 더 자주 발견되기도 했다."], ["Thus it came to pass, that even among those rigid and absorbed young anchorites, who mortified themselves beneath the roof of Doctor Blimber, Paul was an object of general interest; a fragile little plaything that they all liked, and that no one would have thought of treating roughly.", "그리하여 블림버 박사의 지붕 아래에서 고행하는, 그 엄격하고 골몰에 빠진 어린 은수자들 사이에서조차 폴은 두루 관심의 대상이 되었으니, 모두가 좋아하고 아무도 거칠게 다룰 생각조차 하지 않을, 가냘프고 자그마한 노리개였다."], ["But he could not change his nature, or rewrite the analysis; and so they all agreed that Dombey was old-fashioned.", "그러나 그는 자기 본성을 바꿀 수도, 그 분석표를 고쳐 쓸 수도 없었다. 그래서 그들 모두 돔비가 노숙하다는 데 의견을 모았다."]], [["There were some immunities, however, attaching to the character enjoyed by no one else.", "그렇지만 다른 누구도 누리지 못하는, 그 성품에 딸린 몇 가지 특권은 있었다."], ["They could have better spared a newer-fashioned child, and that alone was much.", "그들로서는 더 신식인 아이를 잃는 편이 차라리 나았을 테고, 그것만으로도 대단한 일이었다."], ["When the others only bowed to Doctor Blimber and family on retiring for the night, Paul would stretch out his morsel of a hand, and boldly shake the Doctor’s; also Mrs Blimber’s; also Cornelia’s.", "다른 아이들이 밤에 물러가며 블림버 박사와 그 가족에게 그저 고개만 숙일 때, 폴은 자그마한 손을 내밀어 대담하게 박사의 손을, 또 블림버 부인의 손을, 또 코닐리어의 손을 잡고 흔들곤 했다."], ["If anybody was to be begged off from impending punishment, Paul was always the delegate.", "닥쳐오는 벌을 면해 달라고 누군가를 위해 빌어야 할 일이 있으면, 언제나 폴이 그 사절이었다."], ["The weak-eyed young man himself had once consulted him, in reference to a little breakage of glass and china.", "눈이 약한 그 젊은이조차 한번은 유리잔과 도자기를 조금 깨뜨린 일을 두고 그에게 상의한 적이 있었다."], ["And it was darkly rumoured that the butler, regarding him with favour such as that stern man had never shown before to mortal boy, had sometimes mingled porter with his table-beer to make him strong.", "그리고 어둑한 소문에 따르면, 그 엄한 사내가 어떤 소년에게도 전에는 보인 적 없는 호의로 그를 대하던 집사가, 그를 튼튼하게 해 주려고 이따금 그의 식탁용 약한 맥주에 흑맥주를 섞어 주었다고 했다."]], [["Over and above these extensive privileges, Paul had free right of entry to Mr Feeder’s room, from which apartment he had twice led Mr Toots into the open air in a state of faintness, consequent on an unsuccessful attempt to smoke a very blunt cigar: one of a bundle which that young gentleman had covertly purchased on the shingle from a most desperate smuggler, who had acknowledged, in confidence, that two hundred pounds was the price set upon his head, dead or alive, by the Custom House.", "이 광범위한 특권들에 더하여, 폴은 피더 선생의 방에 자유로이 드나들 권리도 있었다. 그 방에서 그는 두 번이나 투츠 씨를, 아주 끝이 뭉툭한 시가를 피우려다 실패한 끝에 정신이 아뜩해진 상태로, 바깥 공기 속으로 부축해 내보낸 적이 있었다."], ["It was a snug room, Mr Feeder’s, with his bed in another little room inside of it; and a flute, which Mr Feeder couldn’t play yet, but was going to make a point of learning, he said, hanging up over the fireplace.", "그 시가는 투츠 씨가 자갈밭에서 어느 더없이 막무가내인 밀수꾼에게 몰래 사들인 한 묶음 가운데 하나였는데, 그 밀수꾼은 은밀히 털어놓기를, 세관이 자기 머리에 산 채로든 죽은 채로든 이백 파운드의 현상금을 걸었다고 했다."], ["There were some books in it, too, and a fishing-rod; for Mr Feeder said he should certainly make a point of learning to fish, when he could find time.", "피더 선생의 방은 아늑했고, 그 안쪽의 또 다른 작은 방에 침대가 있었다. 그리고 피더 선생이 아직 불 줄은 모르지만 꼭 배우기로 작정했다고 말하는 플루트가 벽난로 위에 걸려 있었다."], ["Mr Feeder had amassed, with similar intentions, a beautiful little curly secondhand key-bugle, a chess-board and men, a Spanish Grammar, a set of sketching materials, and a pair of boxing-gloves.", "거기엔 책 몇 권과 낚싯대도 있었으니, 피더 선생은 시간이 나면 꼭 낚시를 배우기로 작정하겠다고 말했다. 피더 선생은 비슷한 의도로, 아름답고 작은 곱슬곱슬한 중고 키-뷰글 하나, 체스판과 말들, 스페인어 문법책 한 권, 스케치 도구 한 벌, 그리고 권투 장갑 한 켤레를 모아 두었다."], ["The art of self-defence Mr Feeder said he should undoubtedly make a point of learning, as he considered it the duty of every man to do; for it might lead to the protection of a female in distress.", "호신술은, 피더 선생 말로는, 모든 남자가 마땅히 해야 할 의무라 여기므로 반드시 배우기로 작정하겠다고 했으니, 곤경에 빠진 여인을 보호하는 일로 이어질 수도 있기 때문이었다."]], [["But Mr Feeder’s great possession was a large green jar of snuff, which Mr Toots had brought down as a present, at the close of the last vacation; and for which he had paid a high price, having been the genuine property of the Prince Regent.", "그러나 피더 선생의 가장 큰 보물은 코담배가 가득 든 큰 초록색 단지였는데, 그것은 지난 방학이 끝날 무렵 투츠 씨가 선물로 가져온 것이었다. 그는 그것에 비싼 값을 치렀는데, 섭정 왕세자의 진품 소장품이었기 때문이다."], ["Neither Mr Toots nor Mr Feeder could partake of this or any other snuff, even in the most stinted and moderate degree, without being seized with convulsions of sneezing.", "투츠 씨도 피더 선생도 이것이든 다른 어떤 코담배든, 아무리 인색하고 절제된 정도로라도 즐기려 들면 재채기의 발작에 사로잡히지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Nevertheless it was their great delight to moisten a box-full with cold tea, stir it up on a piece of parchment with a paper-knife, and devote themselves to its consumption then and there.", "그럼에도 그들이 가장 큰 낙으로 삼은 것은, 한 통 가득 찬 코담배를 찬 차로 적셔, 양피지 조각 위에 종이칼로 휘저은 다음, 그 자리에서 곧장 그것을 소비하는 데 몰두하는 일이었다."], ["In the course of which cramming of their noses, they endured surprising torments with the constancy of martyrs: and, drinking table-beer at intervals, felt all the glories of dissipation.", "그렇게 콧속을 틀어막는 동안 그들은 순교자 같은 의연함으로 놀라운 고통을 견뎠고, 틈틈이 식탁용 약한 맥주를 마셔 가며 방탕의 온갖 영광을 만끽했다."]], [["To little Paul sitting silent in their company, and by the side of his chief patron, Mr Toots, there was a dread charm in these reckless occasions: and when Mr Feeder spoke of the dark mysteries of London, and told Mr Toots that he was going to observe it himself closely in all its ramifications in the approaching holidays, and for that purpose had made arrangements to board with two old maiden ladies at Peckham, Paul regarded him as if he were the hero of some book of travels or wild adventure, and was almost afraid of such a slashing person.", "그들과 함께, 그것도 자기 으뜸 후원자 투츠 씨 곁에 말없이 앉아 있던 어린 폴에게는, 이런 무모한 자리들에 무시무시한 매력이 있었다. 그리고 피더 선생이 런던의 어두운 비밀들을 이야기하며, 투츠 씨에게 다가오는 방학에 자기가 직접 그것을 온갖 갈래로 면밀히 관찰할 작정이고, 그 목적으로 페컴에 사는 두 노처녀 댁에 하숙하기로 약속을 잡아 두었다고 말하자, 폴은 그를 무슨 여행기나 거친 모험담 속 영웅이라도 되는 양 바라보았고, 그토록 대담한 인물 앞에서 거의 두려움마저 느꼈다."]], [["Going into this room one evening, when the holidays were very near, Paul found Mr Feeder filling up the blanks in some printed letters, while some others, already filled up and strewn before him, were being folded and sealed by Mr Toots.", "방학이 무척 가까워진 어느 날 저녁 이 방에 들어갔을 때, 폴은 피더 선생이 인쇄된 편지 몇 장의 빈칸을 채워 넣고 있고, 이미 채워져 그의 앞에 흩어진 다른 몇 장은 투츠 씨가 접어 봉인하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Mr Feeder said, “Aha, Dombey, there you are, are you?”—for they were always kind to him, and glad to see him—and then said, tossing one of the letters towards him, “And there you are, too, Dombey.", "피더 선생이 말했다, \"어, 돔비, 너 거기 있었구나, 응?\"—그들은 늘 그에게 친절했고 그를 보면 반가워했다—그러더니 편지 한 장을 그에게 툭 던지며 말했다, \"그리고 너도 거기 있고, 돔비."], ["That’s yours.”", "그건 네 거야.\""]], [["“Mine, Sir?”", "\"제 거요, 선생님?\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“Your invitation,”", "\"네 초대장이지,\""], ["returned Mr Feeder.", "피더 선생이 대답했다."]], [["Paul, looking at it, found, in copper-plate print, with the exception of his own name and the date, which were in Mr Feeder’s penmanship, that Doctor and Mrs Blimber requested the pleasure of Mr P.", "폴이 그것을 들여다보니, 동판 인쇄로—피더 선생의 필체로 된 자기 이름과 날짜만 빼고—블림버 박사 부부가 이달 십칠 일 수요일 저녁에 열리는 이른 모임에 P."], ["Dombey’s company at an early party on Wednesday Evening the Seventeenth Instant; and that the hour was half-past seven o’clock; and that the object was Quadrilles.", "돔비 군이 참석해 주는 기쁨을 청한다는 것, 시각은 일곱 시 반이라는 것, 그리고 그 목적은 카드리유 춤이라는 것이 적혀 있었다."], ["Mr Toots also showed him, by holding up a companion sheet of paper, that Doctor and Mrs Blimber requested the pleasure of Mr Toots’s company at an early party on Wednesday Evening the Seventeenth Instant, when the hour was half-past seven o’clock, and when the object was Quadrilles.", "투츠 씨도 짝이 되는 종이 한 장을 들어 보이며, 블림버 박사 부부가 이달 십칠 일 수요일 저녁에 열리는 이른 모임에 투츠 씨가 참석해 주는 기쁨을 청하며, 시각은 일곱 시 반이고 목적은 카드리유 춤임을 보여 주었다."], ["He also found, on glancing at the table where Mr Feeder sat, that the pleasure of Mr Briggs’s company, and of Mr Tozer’s company, and of every young gentleman’s company, was requested by Doctor and Mrs Blimber on the same genteel Occasion.", "피더 선생이 앉아 있는 탁자를 흘긋 보니, 브리그스 군의 참석과 토저 군의 참석과 모든 어린 신사의 참석이 같은 품위 있는 자리를 위해 블림버 박사 부부에 의해 청해지고 있음도 알 수 있었다."]], [["Mr Feeder then told him, to his great joy, that his sister was invited, and that it was a half-yearly event, and that, as the holidays began that day, he could go away with his sister after the party, if he liked, which Paul interrupted him to say he would like, very much.", "그러자 피더 선생은 그에게, 그를 크게 기쁘게 하는 소식을, 그의 누이도 초대되었다는 것, 그리고 그것이 반년마다 열리는 행사라는 것, 또 방학이 그날부터 시작되므로 원한다면 모임이 끝난 뒤 누이와 함께 떠나도 된다는 것을 일러 주었는데, 폴은 그 말을 가로채며 정말 무척 그러고 싶다고 했다."], ["Mr Feeder then gave him to understand that he would be expected to inform Doctor and Mrs Blimber, in superfine small-hand, that Mr P.", "그러고 나서 피더 선생은, 그가 더없이 고운 작은 글씨로 블림버 박사 부부께, 정중한 초대에 따라 P."], ["Dombey would be happy to have the honour of waiting on them, in accordance with their polite invitation.", "돔비 군이 그분들을 모시는 영광을 누리게 되어 기쁘겠다고 알려야 함을 일러 주었다."], ["Lastly, Mr Feeder said, he had better not refer to the festive occasion, in the hearing of Doctor and Mrs Blimber; as these preliminaries, and the whole of the arrangements, were conducted on principles of classicality and high breeding; and that Doctor and Mrs Blimber on the one hand, and the young gentlemen on the other, were supposed, in their scholastic capacities, not to have the least idea of what was in the wind.", "끝으로 피더 선생은, 블림버 박사 부부가 들리는 데서는 이 잔치 자리를 입에 올리지 않는 편이 낫다고 했다. 이 예비 절차며 모든 준비가 고전적 격식과 고상한 교양의 원칙에 따라 진행되며, 한편으로 블림버 박사 부부와 다른 한편으로 어린 신사들은, 그 학자적 신분상, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 전혀 모르는 것으로 되어 있기 때문이라고 했다."]], [["Paul thanked Mr Feeder for these hints, and pocketing his invitation, sat down on a stool by the side of Mr Toots, as usual.", "폴은 이 귀띔에 대해 피더 선생에게 고맙다고 한 뒤, 초대장을 주머니에 넣고 여느 때처럼 투츠 씨 곁의 걸상에 앉았다."], ["But Paul’s head, which had long been ailing more or less, and was sometimes very heavy and painful, felt so uneasy that night, that he was obliged to support it on his hand.", "그러나 오래도록 다소간 앓아 왔고 때로 무척 무겁고 아프던 폴의 머리가 그날 밤은 어찌나 거북했던지, 그는 머리를 손으로 받칠 수밖에 없었다."], ["And yet it dropped so, that by little and little it sunk on Mr Toots’s knee, and rested there, as if it had no care to be ever lifted up again.", "그런데도 머리가 자꾸 떨궈져, 조금씩 조금씩 투츠 씨의 무릎 위로 내려앉더니, 다시는 들어 올려질 마음이 없다는 듯 거기에 얹혔다."]], [["That was no reason why he should be deaf; but he must have been, he thought, for, by and by, he heard Mr Feeder calling in his ear, and gently shaking him to rouse his attention.", "그것이 그가 귀가 멀어야 할 까닭은 아니었지만, 그는 자기가 귀가 멀었던 게 분명하다고 생각했다. 왜냐하면 얼마 뒤, 피더 선생이 자기 귀에 대고 부르며 정신을 차리게 하려고 부드럽게 흔드는 소리가 들렸기 때문이다."], ["And when he raised his head, quite scared, and looked about him, he found that Doctor Blimber had come into the room; and that the window was open, and that his forehead was wet with sprinkled water; though how all this had been done without his knowledge, was very curious indeed.", "그리고 잔뜩 겁먹은 채 머리를 들어 둘러보니, 블림버 박사가 방에 들어와 있었고, 창문이 열려 있었으며, 자기 이마가 뿌려진 물로 젖어 있었다. 그런데 이 모든 일이 자기도 모르는 사이에 어떻게 이루어졌는지는 참으로 기이했다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Come, come!", "자, 자! 됐다!"], ["That’s well! How is my little friend now?”", "우리 꼬마 친구, 지금은 좀 어떤가?\""], ["said Doctor Blimber, encouragingly.", "블림버 박사가 격려하듯 말했다."]], [["“Oh, quite well, thank you, Sir,”", "\"오, 아주 좋아요, 고맙습니다, 선생님,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["But there seemed to be something the matter with the floor, for he couldn’t stand upon it steadily; and with the walls too, for they were inclined to turn round and round, and could only be stopped by being looked at very hard indeed.", "그러나 바닥에 무슨 문제가 있는 듯, 그 위에 똑바로 설 수가 없었다. 벽에도 마찬가지로, 자꾸 빙빙 돌려 하는 통에, 아주 호되게 노려보아야만 겨우 멈출 수 있었다."], ["Mr Toots’s head had the appearance of being at once bigger and farther off than was quite natural; and when he took Paul in his arms, to carry him upstairs, Paul observed with astonishment that the door was in quite a different place from that in which he had expected to find it, and almost thought, at first, that Mr Toots was going to walk straight up the chimney.", "투츠 씨의 머리는 마땅한 정도보다 동시에 더 크고 더 멀리 있는 것처럼 보였다. 그리고 투츠 씨가 그를 위층으로 데려가려고 팔에 안았을 때, 폴은 문이 자기가 있으리라 예상한 곳과는 전혀 다른 데에 있는 것을 보고 놀랐고, 처음엔 투츠 씨가 굴뚝으로 곧장 걸어 올라가려는 게 아닌가 싶을 정도였다."]], [["It was very kind of Mr Toots to carry him to the top of the house so tenderly; and Paul told him that it was. But Mr Toots said he would do a great deal more than that, if he could; and indeed he did more as it was: for he helped Paul to undress, and helped him to bed, in the kindest manner possible, and then sat down by the bedside and chuckled very much; while Mr Feeder, B.A., leaning over the bottom of the bedstead, set all the little bristles on his head bolt upright with his bony hands, and then made believe to spar at Paul with great science, on account of his being all right again, which was so uncommonly facetious, and kind too in Mr Feeder, that Paul, not being able to make up his mind whether it was best to laugh or cry at him, did both at once.", "투츠 씨가 그를 집 꼭대기까지 그토록 다정하게 안아다 준 것은 무척 친절한 일이었고, 폴은 그에게 그렇다고 말했다. 그러나 투츠 씨는 할 수만 있다면 그보다 훨씬 더한 일도 하겠다고 했다. 그리고 사실 그는 더한 일을 했으니, 더없이 친절한 태도로 폴이 옷을 벗는 것을 돕고 잠자리에 드는 것을 도운 뒤, 침대 곁에 앉아 무척 껄껄거렸다. 한편 문학사 피더 선생은 침대 발치에 몸을 굽혀, 앙상한 두 손으로 폴의 머리에 난 짧은 솜털을 죄다 빳빳이 곤두세우더니, 폴이 다시 멀쩡해진 것을 기리느라 대단한 기교로 그에게 권투 흉내를 내며 시늉을 했다. 그것이 어찌나 유달리 익살스럽고 또 친절했던지, 폴은 그에게 웃어야 할지 울어야 할지 마음을 정하지 못한 채, 둘 다를 한꺼번에 했다."]], [["How Mr Toots melted away, and Mr Feeder changed into Mrs Pipchin, Paul never thought of asking; neither was he at all curious to know; but when he saw Mrs Pipchin standing at the bottom of the bed, instead of Mr Feeder, he cried out, “Mrs Pipchin, don’t tell Florence!”", "투츠 씨가 어떻게 사라져 버렸는지, 또 피더 선생이 어떻게 피프친 부인으로 바뀌었는지, 폴은 물어볼 생각조차 하지 않았고, 알고 싶은 마음도 전혀 없었다. 그러나 피더 선생 대신 피프친 부인이 침대 발치에 서 있는 것을 보자 그는 외쳤다, \"피프친 부인, 플로렌스에게는 말하지 마세요!\""]], [["“Don’t tell Florence what, my little Paul?”", "\"플로렌스에게 무얼 말하지 말라는 거니, 우리 꼬마 폴?\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, coming round to the bedside, and sitting down in the chair.", "피프친 부인이 침대 곁으로 돌아와 의자에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["“About me,”", "\"저에 대해서요,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin.", "피프친 부인이 말했다."]], [["“What do you think I mean to do when I grow up, Mrs Pipchin?”", "\"제가 자라면 무얼 할 생각인지 아세요, 피프친 부인?\""], ["inquired Paul, turning his face towards her on his pillow, and resting his chin wistfully on his folded hands.", "폴이 베개 위에서 얼굴을 그녀 쪽으로 돌리고, 깍지 낀 두 손에 턱을 그리운 듯 괴며 물었다."]], [["Mrs Pipchin couldn’t guess.", "피프친 부인은 짐작할 수 없었다."]], [["“I mean,”", "\"저는요,\""], ["said Paul, “to put my money all together in one Bank, never try to get any more, go away into the country with my darling Florence, have a beautiful garden, fields, and woods, and live there with her all my life!”", "폴이 말했다, \"제 돈을 모두 한 은행에 모아 두고, 더 벌려고 애쓰지 않고, 사랑하는 플로렌스랑 시골로 가서, 아름다운 정원과 들판과 숲을 갖고, 거기서 누나랑 평생 함께 살 거예요!\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"저런!\""], ["cried Mrs Pipchin.", "피프친 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."], ["“That’s what I mean to do, when I—”", "\"제가 그렇게 할 거예요, 제가—\""], ["He stopped, and pondered for a moment.", "그는 말을 멈추고 잠시 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["Mrs Pipchin’s grey eye scanned his thoughtful face.", "피프친 부인의 잿빛 눈이 그의 생각에 잠긴 얼굴을 살폈다."]], [["“If I grow up,”", "\"제가 자라면요,\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."], ["Then he went on immediately to tell Mrs Pipchin all about the party, about Florence’s invitation, about the pride he would have in the admiration that would be felt for her by all the boys, about their being so kind to him and fond of him, about his being so fond of them, and about his being so glad of it.", "그러더니 그는 곧바로 이어서, 피프친 부인에게 그 모임에 대해, 플로렌스의 초대에 대해, 모든 아이들이 누나에게 느낄 감탄에 자기가 가질 자랑스러움에 대해, 아이들이 자기에게 그토록 친절하고 자기를 좋아한다는 것에 대해, 자기가 아이들을 그토록 좋아한다는 것에 대해, 그리고 그것이 그토록 기쁘다는 것에 대해 죄다 이야기했다."], ["Then he told Mrs Pipchin about the analysis, and about his being certainly old-fashioned, and took Mrs Pipchin’s opinion on that point, and whether she knew why it was, and what it meant.", "그러고는 피프친 부인에게 그 분석에 대해, 자기가 분명 노숙하다는 것에 대해 이야기하고, 그 점에 대한 피프친 부인의 의견을 물었으며, 그녀가 그게 왜 그런지, 무슨 뜻인지 아는지도 물었다."], ["Mrs Pipchin denied the fact altogether, as the shortest way of getting out of the difficulty; but Paul was far from satisfied with that reply, and looked so searchingly at Mrs Pipchin for a truer answer, that she was obliged to get up and look out of the window to avoid his eyes.", "피프친 부인은 곤란에서 벗어나는 가장 빠른 길로, 그 사실 자체를 통째로 부인했다. 그러나 폴은 그 대답에 도무지 만족하지 못해, 더 진실한 답을 구하느라 피프친 부인을 어찌나 캐묻듯 바라보았던지, 그녀는 그의 눈길을 피하려고 일어나 창밖을 내다볼 수밖에 없었다."]], [["There was a certain calm Apothecary, who attended at the establishment when any of the young gentlemen were ill, and somehow he got into the room and appeared at the bedside, with Mrs Blimber.", "어린 신사들 가운데 누가 아플 때면 그 학원에 와 주는 어느 차분한 약제사가 있었는데, 어쩌다 보니 그가 방으로 들어와 블림버 부인과 함께 침대 곁에 나타났다."], ["How they came there, or how long they had been there, Paul didn’t know; but when he saw them, he sat up in bed, and answered all the Apothecary’s questions at full length, and whispered to him that Florence was not to know anything about it, if he pleased, and that he had set his mind upon her coming to the party.", "그들이 어떻게 거기 왔는지, 얼마나 오래 거기 있었는지 폴은 몰랐다. 그러나 그들을 보자 그는 침대에서 일어나 앉아, 약제사의 모든 질문에 빠짐없이 대답했고, 괜찮으시다면 플로렌스가 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르게 해 달라고, 자기는 누나가 그 모임에 오는 것에 마음을 굳혔다고 속삭였다."], ["He was very chatty with the Apothecary, and they parted excellent friends.", "그는 약제사와 무척 수다스럽게 이야기했고, 둘은 더없이 좋은 벗으로 헤어졌다."], ["Lying down again with his eyes shut, he heard the Apothecary say, out of the room and quite a long way off—or he dreamed it—that there was a want of vital power (what was that, Paul wondered!) and great constitutional weakness.", "다시 눈을 감고 누워 있을 때, 그는 약제사가 방 밖에서, 아주 멀찍이서—아니면 그가 꿈에서 들었던가—생명력이 부족하고(그게 뭘까, 폴은 궁금했다!) 체질이 매우 허약하다고 말하는 것을 들었다."], ["That as the little fellow had set his heart on parting with his school-mates on the seventeenth, it would be better to indulge the fancy if he grew no worse.", "그 꼬마가 십칠 일에 학교 동무들과 작별하는 데 마음을 둔 만큼, 더 나빠지지만 않는다면 그 소망을 들어주는 편이 낫겠다고도 했다."], ["That he was glad to hear from Mrs Pipchin, that the little fellow would go to his friends in London on the eighteenth.", "피프친 부인에게서 그 꼬마가 십팔 일에 런던의 가족에게 갈 거라는 말을 들으니 다행이라고도 했다."], ["That he would write to Mr Dombey, when he should have gained a better knowledge of the case, and before that day.", "사례를 더 잘 파악하게 되면, 그날 이전에 돔비 씨에게 편지를 쓰겠다고도 했다."], ["That there was no immediate cause for—what?", "당장 무엇무엇 할 까닭은 없다고도 했다—무엇 말인가?"], ["Paul lost that word.", "폴은 그 낱말을 놓쳤다."], ["And that the little fellow had a fine mind, but was an old-fashioned boy.", "그리고 그 꼬마는 훌륭한 정신을 지녔으나 노숙한 아이라고도 했다."]], [["What old fashion could that be, Paul wondered with a palpitating heart, that was so visibly expressed in him; so plainly seen by so many people!", "대체 무슨 노숙함이기에, 폴은 두근거리는 마음으로 궁금해했다, 자기에게서 그토록 또렷이 드러나고, 그토록 많은 사람의 눈에 그토록 분명히 보이는 것일까!"]], [["He could neither make it out, nor trouble himself long with the effort.", "그는 그것을 도무지 알아낼 수도 없었고, 그 노력으로 오래 자신을 괴롭히지도 못했다."], ["Mrs Pipchin was again beside him, if she had ever been away (he thought she had gone out with the Doctor, but it was all a dream perhaps), and presently a bottle and glass got into her hands magically, and she poured out the contents for him.", "피프친 부인이—설령 떠난 적이 있었다 해도(그는 그녀가 박사와 함께 나갔다고 생각했지만, 어쩌면 그것도 다 꿈이었는지 모른다)—다시 그의 곁에 와 있었고, 이내 병 하나와 잔 하나가 마법처럼 그녀의 손에 들리더니, 그녀가 그 안의 것을 그에게 따라 주었다. 그러고 나서 그는 블림버 부인이 손수 가져다준 진짜 맛있는 젤리를 좀 먹었다."], ["After that, he had some real good jelly, which Mrs Blimber brought to him herself; and then he was so well, that Mrs Pipchin went home, at his urgent solicitation, and Briggs and Tozer came to bed.", "그러자 어찌나 좋아졌던지, 피프친 부인은 그의 간곡한 청에 따라 집으로 갔고, 브리그스와 토저가 자러 왔다. 가엾은 브리그스는 자기 분석에 대해 끔찍하게 투덜거렸는데, 설령 그것이 화학 공정이었다 한들 그를 더 심란하게 하지는 못했을 것이다."], ["Poor Briggs grumbled terribly about his own analysis, which could hardly have discomposed him more if it had been a chemical process; but he was very good to Paul, and so was Tozer, and so were all the rest, for they every one looked in before going to bed, and said, “How are you now, Dombey?”", "그러나 그는 폴에게 무척 잘해 주었고, 토저도 그랬으며, 나머지도 다들 그랬다. 모두 자러 가기 전에 들여다보며 말했기 때문이다, \"지금은 좀 어때, 돔비?\""], ["“Cheer up, little Dombey!”", "\"기운 내, 꼬마 돔비!\""], ["and so forth.", "그런 식이었다."], ["After Briggs had got into bed, he lay awake for a long time, still bemoaning his analysis, and saying he knew it was all wrong, and they couldn’t have analysed a murderer worse, and—how would Doctor Blimber like it if his pocket-money depended on it?", "브리그스는 침대에 든 뒤에도 오래도록 잠 못 이룬 채 여전히 자기 분석을 한탄하며, 그게 죄다 틀렸다는 걸 안다고, 살인자를 분석해도 그보다 더 나쁘게는 못 했을 거라고, 그리고—만약 자기 용돈이 거기 달려 있다면 블림버 박사는 그걸 어떻게 받아들이겠느냐고 했다."], ["It was very easy, Briggs said, to make a galley-slave of a boy all the half-year, and then score him up idle; and to crib two dinners a-week out of his board, and then score him up greedy; but that wasn’t going to be submitted to, he believed, was it?", "한 학기 내내 아이를 갤리선 노예로 부려먹고는 게으르다고 점수를 매기고, 식비에서 일주일에 두 끼씩 빼돌리고는 탐욕스럽다고 점수를 매기는 건 아주 쉬운 일이라고, 브리그스는 말했다. 그러나 그런 건 가만 받아들여질 일이 아니라고 자기는 믿는다는데, 안 그러냐고?"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["Ah!", "아!"]], [["Before the weak-eyed young man performed on the gong next morning, he came upstairs to Paul and told him he was to lie still, which Paul very gladly did.", "이튿날 아침 눈 약한 젊은이가 징을 울리기 전에, 그는 위층으로 올라와 폴에게 가만히 누워 있으라고 일렀고, 폴은 무척 기꺼이 그렇게 했다."], ["Mrs Pipchin reappeared a little before the Apothecary, and a little after the good young woman whom Paul had seen cleaning the stove on that first morning (how long ago it seemed now!) had brought him his breakfast.", "피프친 부인이 약제사보다 조금 먼저 다시 나타났고, 그 첫날 아침(이제 그것이 얼마나 오래전 일처럼 느껴지는지!) 폴이 난로를 닦는 것을 보았던 그 착한 젊은 여인이 그에게 아침을 가져다준 직후였다."], ["There was another consultation a long way off, or else Paul dreamed it again; and then the Apothecary, coming back with Doctor and Mrs Blimber, said:", "멀찍이서 또 한 차례 상담이 있었고, 아니면 폴이 그것을 또 꿈꾸었거나 했다. 그러고 나서 약제사가 블림버 박사 부부와 함께 돌아와 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I think, Doctor Blimber, we may release this young gentleman from his books just now; the vacation being so very near at hand.”", "\"네, 제 생각엔, 블림버 박사님, 지금은 이 어린 신사를 책에서 풀어 주어도 될 듯합니다. 방학이 워낙 코앞에 다가왔으니까요.\""]], [["“By all means,”", "\"그렇게 하지요,\""], ["said Doctor Blimber.", "블림버 박사가 말했다."], ["“My love, you will inform Cornelia, if you please.”", "\"여보, 괜찮으면 코닐리어에게 알려 주시오.\""]], [["“Assuredly,”", "\"여부가 있겠어요,\""], ["said Mrs Blimber.", "블림버 부인이 말했다."]], [["The Apothecary bending down, looked closely into Paul’s eyes, and felt his head, and his pulse, and his heart, with so much interest and care, that Paul said, “Thank you, Sir.”", "약제사는 몸을 굽혀, 폴의 두 눈을 가까이 들여다보고, 그의 머리와 맥과 심장을 어찌나 흥미와 정성을 다해 짚어 보았던지, 폴이 말했다, \"고맙습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“Our little friend,”", "\"우리 꼬마 친구는,\""], ["observed Doctor Blimber, “has never complained.”", "블림버 박사가 말했다, \"한 번도 불평한 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“Oh no!”", "\"오, 그렇지요!\""], ["replied the Apothecary.", "약제사가 대답했다."], ["“He was not likely to complain.”", "\"이 아이는 불평할 아이가 아니지요.\""]], [["“You find him greatly better?”", "\"많이 나아진 것 같습니까?\""], ["said Doctor Blimber.", "블림버 박사가 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["he is greatly better, Sir,”", "많이 나아졌습니다, 박사님,\""], ["returned the Apothecary.", "약제사가 대답했다."]], [["Paul had begun to speculate, in his own odd way, on the subject that might occupy the Apothecary’s mind just at that moment; so musingly had he answered the two questions of Doctor Blimber.", "폴은 블림버 박사의 두 질문에 약제사가 어찌나 깊이 생각에 잠겨 답했던지, 바로 그 순간 약제사의 마음을 차지하고 있을 만한 화제를 자기 나름의 별난 방식으로 추측하기 시작했다."], ["But the Apothecary happening to meet his little patient’s eyes, as the latter set off on that mental expedition, and coming instantly out of his abstraction with a cheerful smile, Paul smiled in return and abandoned it.", "그러나 그 어린 환자가 그렇게 마음속 탐험을 떠나려는 찰나, 약제사가 우연히 그의 눈과 마주쳐 명랑한 미소와 함께 곧장 상념에서 빠져나오자, 폴도 마주 미소 짓고는 그 탐험을 그만두었다."]], [["He lay in bed all that day, dozing and dreaming, and looking at Mr Toots; but got up on the next, and went downstairs.", "그는 그날 온종일 침대에 누워, 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 꿈꾸며, 투츠 씨를 바라보았다. 그러나 이튿날엔 일어나 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["Lo and behold, there was something the matter with the great clock; and a workman on a pair of steps had taken its face off, and was poking instruments into the works by the light of a candle!", "그런데 어럽쇼, 그 큰 시계에 무슨 문제가 생겨, 사다리 위에 올라선 일꾼이 시계 문자판을 떼어 내고는, 촛불 빛으로 기계 속에 도구들을 쑤셔 넣고 있는 것이 아닌가!"], ["This was a great event for Paul, who sat down on the bottom stair, and watched the operation attentively: now and then glancing at the clock face, leaning all askew, against the wall hard by, and feeling a little confused by a suspicion that it was ogling him.", "이것은 폴에게 큰 사건이어서, 그는 맨 아래 계단에 앉아 그 작업을 주의 깊게 지켜보았다. 이따금 바로 옆 벽에 비스듬히 기대어 놓인 시계 문자판을 흘긋거리며, 그것이 자기에게 추파를 던지고 있는 게 아닐까 하는 의심에 조금 어리둥절해하면서."]], [["The workman on the steps was very civil; and as he said, when he observed Paul, “How do you do, Sir?”", "사다리 위의 일꾼은 무척 싹싹했다. 그래서 그가 폴을 보고 \"안녕하세요, 도련님?\""], ["Paul got into conversation with him, and told him he hadn’t been quite well lately.", "하고 말하자, 폴은 그와 대화에 들어가, 요즘 통 몸이 좋지 않았다고 말했다."], ["The ice being thus broken, Paul asked him a multitude of questions about chimes and clocks: as, whether people watched up in the lonely church steeples by night to make them strike, and how the bells were rung when people died, and whether those were different bells from wedding bells, or only sounded dismal in the fancies of the living.", "그렇게 서먹함이 깨지자, 폴은 그에게 종소리와 시계에 관해 숱한 질문을 던졌다. 이를테면, 사람들이 종을 치게 하려고 밤에 외딴 교회 첨탑 위에서 망을 보는지, 사람이 죽으면 종을 어떻게 울리는지, 또 그 종은 결혼 종과는 다른 종인지 아니면 그저 살아 있는 사람들의 상상 속에서만 음산하게 들리는 것인지 따위였다."], ["Finding that his new acquaintance was not very well informed on the subject of the Curfew Bell of ancient days, Paul gave him an account of that institution; and also asked him, as a practical man, what he thought about King Alfred’s idea of measuring time by the burning of candles; to which the workman replied, that he thought it would be the ruin of the clock trade if it was to come up again.", "새 친구가 옛 시절의 통금종에 대해서는 그다지 잘 알지 못함을 알고, 폴은 그에게 그 제도에 대해 설명해 주었다. 또 그에게, 실무에 밝은 사람으로서, 양초가 타는 것으로 시간을 재자던 앨프레드 왕의 생각을 어떻게 보느냐고 물었다."], ["In fine, Paul looked on, until the clock had quite recovered its familiar aspect, and resumed its sedate inquiry; when the workman, putting away his tools in a long basket, bade him good day, and went away.", "일꾼은, 만약 그게 다시 유행한다면 시계 장사는 망할 거라고 대답했다. 요컨대 폴은, 시계가 익숙한 모습을 완전히 되찾고 그 점잖은 물음을 다시 시작할 때까지 지켜보았다."], ["Though not before he had whispered something, on the door-mat, to the footman, in which there was the phrase “old-fashioned”—for Paul heard it.", "그러자 일꾼은 긴 바구니에 연장을 챙겨 넣고는, 그에게 잘 있으라고 인사하고 떠났다. 다만 그가 떠나기 전, 현관 매트 위에서 하인에게 무언가를 속삭였는데, 그 속에 \"노숙하다\"는 말이 들어 있었다—폴이 들었기 때문이다."]], [["What could that old fashion be, that seemed to make the people sorry!", "대체 무슨 노숙함이기에, 사람들을 그렇게 안타깝게 만드는 것 같단 말인가!"], ["What could it be!", "대체 무엇일까!"]], [["Having nothing to learn now, he thought of this frequently; though not so often as he might have done, if he had had fewer things to think of.", "이제 배울 것이 없어진지라, 그는 이것을 자주 생각했다. 다만 생각할 거리가 더 적었더라면 했을 만큼 자주는 아니었다."], ["But he had a great many; and was always thinking, all day long.", "그러나 그에게는 생각할 거리가 무척 많았고, 그는 온종일 늘 생각에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["First, there was Florence coming to the party.", "먼저, 플로렌스가 모임에 온다는 것이 있었다."], ["Florence would see that the boys were fond of him; and that would make her happy.", "플로렌스는 아이들이 자기를 좋아한다는 것을 보게 될 테고, 그러면 누나는 행복할 것이다."], ["This was his great theme.", "이것이 그의 큰 주제였다."], ["Let Florence once be sure that they were gentle and good to him, and that he had become a little favourite among them, and then she would always think of the time he had passed there, without being very sorry.", "플로렌스가 한번, 아이들이 자기에게 다정하고 잘해 주었다는 것, 자기가 그들 사이에서 작은 인기쟁이가 되었다는 것을 확신하게만 된다면, 누나는 자기가 거기서 보낸 시간을 언제나, 그리 슬퍼하지 않고 떠올릴 수 있을 것이다."], ["Florence might be all the happier too for that, perhaps, when he came back.", "어쩌면 그 덕에 누나는, 자기가 돌아왔을 때 한결 더 행복할지도 모른다."]], [["When he came back!", "자기가 돌아왔을 때라니!"], ["Fifty times a day, his noiseless little feet went up the stairs to his own room, as he collected every book, and scrap, and trifle that belonged to him, and put them all together there, down to the minutest thing, for taking home!", "하루에도 쉰 번씩, 그의 소리 없는 작은 발이 계단을 올라 자기 방으로 갔다. 자기 것인 책이며 종잇조각이며 자질구레한 물건을 죄다 모아, 가장 사소한 것까지도, 집으로 가져가려고 거기에 한데 모아 두면서!"], ["There was no shade of coming back on little Paul; no preparation for it, or other reference to it, grew out of anything he thought or did, except this slight one in connexion with his sister.", "어린 폴에게는 돌아온다는 그림자가 조금도 없었다. 그것에 대한 어떤 준비도, 그것에 대한 다른 어떤 언급도, 그가 생각하거나 행하는 어떤 것에서도 자라나지 않았으니, 다만 누이와 관련된 이 희미한 한 가지만이 예외였다."], ["On the contrary, he had to think of everything familiar to him, in his contemplative moods and in his wanderings about the house, as being to be parted with; and hence the many things he had to think of, all day long.", "오히려 그는, 사색에 잠긴 기분일 때나 집 안을 이리저리 거닐 때면, 자기에게 익숙한 모든 것을 이제 작별할 것으로서 생각해야 했다. 그래서 온종일 생각할 거리가 그토록 많았던 것이다."]], [["He had to peep into those rooms upstairs, and think how solitary they would be when he was gone, and wonder through how many silent days, weeks, months, and years, they would continue just as grave and undisturbed.", "그는 위층의 그 방들을 들여다보며, 자기가 떠나면 그곳들이 얼마나 적막할지 생각하고, 또 얼마나 많은 고요한 날과 주와 달과 해 동안 그곳들이 꼭 그렇게 근엄하고 어지럽혀지지 않은 채로 이어질지 궁금해해야 했다."], ["He had to think—would any other child (old-fashioned, like himself) stray there at any time, to whom the same grotesque distortions of pattern and furniture would manifest themselves; and would anybody tell that boy of little Dombey, who had been there once?", "그는 생각해야 했다—혹시 (자기처럼 노숙한) 또 다른 아이가 언젠가 거기로 흘러들어, 그 똑같이 기괴하게 일그러진 무늬와 가구가 그 아이에게도 모습을 드러낼까. 그리고 한때 거기 있었던 어린 돔비에 대해 그 아이에게 누가 이야기해 줄까."], ["He had to think of a portrait on the stairs, which always looked earnestly after him as he went away, eyeing it over his shoulder; and which, when he passed it in the company of anyone, still seemed to gaze at him, and not at his companion.", "그는 계단의 어느 초상화에 대해서도 생각해야 했는데, 그것은 그가 떠나갈 때 어깨 너머로 돌아보면 늘 진지하게 그의 뒤를 좇아 바라보았고, 그가 누군가와 함께 그 곁을 지날 때면 여전히 그의 동행이 아니라 그를 응시하는 것 같았다."], ["He had much to think of, in association with a print that hung up in another place, where, in the centre of a wondering group, one figure that he knew, a figure with a light about its head—benignant, mild, and merciful—stood pointing upward.", "그는 또 다른 곳에 걸린 어느 판화와 관련해서도 생각할 거리가 많았으니, 거기에는 경이로워하는 무리의 한가운데에, 그가 아는 한 형상이, 머리에 빛을 두른—자애롭고 온화하고 자비로운—한 형상이 위를 가리키며 서 있었다."]], [["At his own bedroom window, there were crowds of thoughts that mixed with these, and came on, one upon another, like the rolling waves. Where those wild birds lived, that were always hovering out at sea in troubled weather; where the clouds rose and first began; whence the wind issued on its rushing flight, and where it stopped; whether the spot where he and Florence had so often sat, and watched, and talked about these things, could ever be exactly as it used to be without them; whether it could ever be the same to Florence, if he were in some distant place, and she were sitting there alone.", "자기 침실 창가에서는, 이런 생각들과 뒤섞여, 굽이치는 물결처럼 잇따라 밀려오는 숱한 상념이 있었다. 거친 날씨면 늘 먼 바다 위를 맴돌던 그 야생의 새들은 어디에 사는지. 구름은 어디서 일어나 처음 시작되는지. 바람은 어디서 나와 그 휘몰아치는 비행을 시작하고, 또 어디서 멈추는지. 자기와 플로렌스가 그토록 자주 함께 앉아, 이런 것들을 바라보고 또 이야기하던 그 자리가, 자기들 없이도 옛날 그대로일 수 있을지. 자기가 어느 먼 곳에 있고 누나가 거기 홀로 앉아 있다면, 플로렌스에게 그곳이 예전과 똑같을 수 있을지."]], [["He had to think, too, of Mr Toots, and Mr Feeder, B.A., of all the boys; and of Doctor Blimber, Mrs Blimber, and Miss Blimber; of home, and of his aunt and Miss Tox; of his father; Dombey and Son, Walter with the poor old Uncle who had got the money he wanted, and that gruff-voiced Captain with the iron hand. Besides all this, he had a number of little visits to pay, in the course of the day; to the schoolroom, to Doctor Blimber’s study, to Mrs Blimber’s private apartment, to Miss Blimber’s, and to the dog. For he was free of the whole house now, to range it as he chose; and, in his desire to part with everybody on affectionate terms, he attended, in his way, to them all. Sometimes he found places in books for Briggs, who was always losing them; sometimes he looked up words in dictionaries for other young gentlemen who were in extremity; sometimes he held skeins of silk for Mrs Blimber to wind; sometimes he put Cornelia’s desk to rights; sometimes he would even creep into the Doctor’s study, and, sitting on the carpet near his learned feet, turn the globes softly, and go round the world, or take a flight among the far-off stars.", "그는 또 투츠 씨와 문학사 피더 선생과 모든 아이들에 대해서도 생각해야 했다. 또 블림버 박사와 블림버 부인과 블림버 양에 대해서도. 또 집에 대해, 고모와 톡스 양에 대해, 아버지에 대해. 돔비와 아들, 자기가 필요로 하던 돈을 마련한 가엾은 늙은 삼촌과 함께 있는 월터, 그리고 무쇠 갈고리 손을 한 그 걸걸한 목소리의 선장에 대해서도. 이 모든 것에 더하여, 그는 하루를 보내며 자그마한 방문을 여러 차례 해야 했다. 교실로, 블림버 박사의 서재로, 블림버 부인의 개인 거처로, 블림버 양의 방으로, 그리고 그 개에게로. 이제 그는 온 집을 마음대로 누빌 수 있게 되었으므로, 모두와 다정한 사이로 작별하고 싶은 바람에서, 자기 식으로 그들 모두를 살뜰히 챙겼다. 어떤 때는 늘 책 읽던 곳을 잃어버리는 브리그스를 위해 그 자리를 찾아 주었고, 어떤 때는 막다른 처지에 몰린 다른 어린 신사들을 위해 사전에서 낱말을 찾아 주었으며, 어떤 때는 블림버 부인이 실을 감도록 명주 타래를 잡아 주었고, 어떤 때는 코닐리어의 책상을 정돈해 주었다. 어떤 때는 심지어 박사의 서재로 살그머니 들어가, 그 학식 높은 발치의 양탄자에 앉아, 지구의를 부드럽게 돌리며 세계를 한 바퀴 돌거나, 저 먼 별들 사이로 날아오르기도 했다."]], [["In those days immediately before the holidays, in short, when the other young gentlemen were labouring for dear life through a general resumption of the studies of the whole half-year, Paul was such a privileged pupil as had never been seen in that house before.", "요컨대 방학 직전의 그 며칠 동안, 다른 어린 신사들이 한 학기 전체의 학업을 통째로 복습하느라 죽을힘을 다해 애쓰고 있을 때, 폴은 그 집에서 일찍이 본 적 없는 그런 특권을 누리는 학생이었다."], ["He could hardly believe it himself; but his liberty lasted from hour to hour, and from day to day; and little Dombey was caressed by everyone.", "그 자신도 좀처럼 믿기지 않았으나, 그의 자유는 시간이 가도 날이 가도 이어졌고, 꼬마 돔비는 모두에게서 귀여움을 받았다."], ["Doctor Blimber was so particular about him, that he requested Johnson to retire from the dinner-table one day, for having thoughtlessly spoken to him as “poor little Dombey;”", "블림버 박사는 그를 어찌나 각별히 여겼던지, 어느 날 존슨이 무심코 그를 \"가엾은 꼬마 돔비\"라고 불렀다는 이유로 그에게 식탁에서 물러나라고 명할 정도였다."], ["which Paul thought rather hard and severe, though he had flushed at the moment, and wondered why Johnson should pity him.", "폴은 그것이 다소 가혹하고 엄하다고 생각했는데, 비록 그 순간엔 자기도 얼굴이 화끈했고 존슨이 왜 자기를 가엾게 여기는지 의아하긴 했지만 말이다."], ["It was the more questionable justice, Paul thought, in the Doctor, from his having certainly overheard that great authority give his assent on the previous evening, to the proposition (stated by Mrs Blimber) that poor dear little Dombey was more old-fashioned than ever.", "더구나 그 처사가 더더욱 의심스러운 정의라고 폴이 여긴 것은, 바로 그 위대한 권위자가 전날 저녁, (블림버 부인이 내놓은) 가엾고 사랑스러운 꼬마 돔비가 그 어느 때보다 노숙해졌다는 명제에 동의하는 것을 자기가 분명히 엿들었기 때문이다."], ["And now it was that Paul began to think it must surely be old-fashioned to be very thin, and light, and easily tired, and soon disposed to lie down anywhere and rest; for he couldn’t help feeling that these were more and more his habits every day.", "그리고 이제 폴은, 무척 야위고, 가볍고, 쉬 지치고, 어디든 곧 드러누워 쉬고 싶어지는 것이야말로 틀림없이 노숙한 것이리라 생각하기 시작했다. 날이 갈수록 점점 더 이것들이 자기 버릇이 되어 가고 있음을 느끼지 않을 수 없었던 까닭이다."]], [["At last the party-day arrived; and Doctor Blimber said at breakfast, “Gentlemen, we will resume our studies on the twenty-fifth of next month.”", "마침내 모임 날이 왔다. 블림버 박사가 아침 식사 자리에서 말했다, \"여러분, 우리는 다음 달 이십오 일에 학업을 재개하겠습니다.\""], ["Mr Toots immediately threw off his allegiance, and put on his ring: and mentioning the Doctor in casual conversation shortly afterwards, spoke of him as “Blimber”!", "투츠 씨는 즉시 충성을 벗어던지고 반지를 끼었다. 그리고 잠시 뒤 무심한 대화 중에 박사를 언급하며, 그를 \"블림버\"라고 불렀다!"], ["This act of freedom inspired the older pupils with admiration and envy; but the younger spirits were appalled, and seemed to marvel that no beam fell down and crushed him.", "이 자유의 행위는 나이 든 학생들에게 감탄과 부러움을 불어넣었으나, 어린 영혼들은 질겁하여, 들보 하나 떨어져 그를 짓뭉개지 않는 것이 신기하다는 듯한 표정이었다."], ["Not the least allusion was made to the ceremonies of the evening, either at breakfast or at dinner; but there was a bustle in the house all day, and in the course of his perambulations, Paul made acquaintance with various strange benches and candlesticks, and met a harp in a green greatcoat standing on the landing outside the drawing-room door.", "아침 식사 때도 저녁 식사 때도, 그날 밤의 행사에 대한 언급은 조금도 없었다. 그러나 온종일 집안은 부산했고, 폴은 이리저리 거니는 동안 여러 낯선 의자며 촛대와 마주쳤고, 응접실 문 밖 층계참에 초록색 외투를 입고 서 있는 하프 하나와 맞닥뜨렸다."], ["There was something queer, too, about Mrs Blimber’s head at dinner-time, as if she had screwed her hair up too tight; and though Miss Blimber showed a graceful bunch of plaited hair on each temple, she seemed to have her own little curls in paper underneath, and in a play-bill too; for Paul read “Theatre Royal”", "저녁 식사 때 블림버 부인의 머리도 어딘가 묘했는데, 마치 머리채를 너무 단단히 비틀어 올린 것 같았다. 그리고 블림버 양은 양쪽 관자놀이에 곱게 땋은 머리 다발을 우아하게 드러냈으나, 그 밑에는 종이로 만 자기 곱슬머리를, 그것도 연극 광고지로 만 것을 두른 듯했다."], ["over one of her sparkling spectacles, and “Brighton” over the other.", "폴이 그녀의 반짝이는 안경 한쪽 위로는 \"왕립 극장\"을, 다른 한쪽 위로는 \"브라이턴\"을 읽었기 때문이다."]], [["There was a grand array of white waistcoats and cravats in the young gentlemen’s bedrooms as evening approached; and such a smell of singed hair, that Doctor Blimber sent up the footman with his compliments, and wished to know if the house was on fire.", "저녁이 다가오자 어린 신사들의 침실에는 흰 조끼와 크라바트가 위풍당당하게 늘어섰고, 머리카락 그을리는 냄새가 어찌나 진동했던지, 블림버 박사가 하인을 올려 보내 인사를 전하며 집에 불이 났는지 알고 싶다고 했다."], ["But it was only the hairdresser curling the young gentlemen, and over-heating his tongs in the ardour of business.", "그러나 그것은 그저 미용사가 어린 신사들의 머리를 지지면서, 일에 열중한 나머지 인두를 너무 달군 탓이었다."]], [["When Paul was dressed—which was very soon done, for he felt unwell and drowsy, and was not able to stand about it very long—he went down into the drawing-room; where he found Doctor Blimber pacing up and down the room full dressed, but with a dignified and unconcerned demeanour, as if he thought it barely possible that one or two people might drop in by and by.", "폴이 옷을 다 입자—몸이 편치 않고 졸렸으며 오래 서 있질 못해 그 일은 아주 금방 끝났다—그는 응접실로 내려갔다. 거기서 그는 블림버 박사가, 정장을 갖춰 입었으되 위엄 있고 무심한 태도로, 마치 한두 사람쯤이야 이따 들를 수도 있겠거니 여기는 듯, 방 안을 오락가락 거니는 것을 보았다."], ["Shortly afterwards, Mrs Blimber appeared, looking lovely, Paul thought; and attired in such a number of skirts that it was quite an excursion to walk round her.", "잠시 뒤 블림버 부인이 나타났는데, 폴이 보기에 사랑스러웠다. 어찌나 많은 치마를 겹쳐 입었는지, 그녀의 둘레를 한 바퀴 도는 것이 가히 한바탕 소풍 같았다."], ["Miss Blimber came down soon after her Mama; a little squeezed in appearance, but very charming.", "블림버 양은 어머니에 뒤이어 곧 내려왔는데, 다소 꽉 죄어든 듯한 모습이었으나 무척 매력적이었다."]], [["Mr Toots and Mr Feeder were the next arrivals.", "다음으로 도착한 이는 투츠 씨와 피더 선생이었다."], ["Each of these gentlemen brought his hat in his hand, as if he lived somewhere else; and when they were announced by the butler, Doctor Blimber said, “Ay, ay, ay!", "두 신사는 저마다 모자를 손에 든 채였는데, 마치 어딘가 딴 데 사는 사람들 같았다. 그리고 집사가 그들의 도착을 알리자, 블림버 박사가 \"이런, 이런, 이런!"], ["God bless my soul!”", "맙소사!\""], ["and seemed extremely glad to see them.", "하고는, 그들을 보고 무척 반가워하는 듯했다."], ["Mr Toots was one blaze of jewellery and buttons; and he felt the circumstance so strongly, that when he had shaken hands with the Doctor, and had bowed to Mrs Blimber and Miss Blimber, he took Paul aside, and said, “What do you think of this, Dombey?”", "투츠 씨는 온통 보석과 단추로 번쩍였다. 그리고 그는 그 사실을 어찌나 강하게 의식했던지, 박사와 악수를 하고 블림버 부인과 블림버 양에게 절을 한 뒤, 폴을 한쪽으로 데려가 말했다, \"이거 어때, 돔비?\""]], [["But notwithstanding this modest confidence in himself, Mr Toots appeared to be involved in a good deal of uncertainty whether, on the whole, it was judicious to button the bottom button of his waistcoat, and whether, on a calm revision of all the circumstances, it was best to wear his waistbands turned up or turned down.", "그러나 이렇게 자신에 대한 겸손한 확신을 보이면서도, 투츠 씨는 대체로 조끼의 맨 아래 단추를 채우는 것이 현명한지, 또 모든 정황을 차분히 다시 살필 때 허리띠를 위로 접어 올리는 것이 나은지 아래로 내리는 것이 나은지를 두고 적잖은 갈피를 못 잡는 듯했다."], ["Observing that Mr Feeder’s were turned up, Mr Toots turned his up; but the waistbands of the next arrival being turned down, Mr Toots turned his down.", "피더 선생의 것이 접어 올려진 것을 보고 투츠 씨는 자기 것도 올렸다. 그러나 다음 도착한 사람의 허리띠가 내려진 것을 보고 투츠 씨는 자기 것도 내렸다."], ["The differences in point of waistcoat-buttoning, not only at the bottom, but at the top too, became so numerous and complicated as the arrivals thickened, that Mr Toots was continually fingering that article of dress, as if he were performing on some instrument; and appeared to find the incessant execution it demanded, quite bewildering.", "조끼 단추 채우기를 두고 생기는 차이가, 맨 아래뿐 아니라 맨 위에서도, 손님이 늘어 갈수록 어찌나 숱하고 복잡해졌던지, 투츠 씨는 무슨 악기라도 연주하듯 끊임없이 그 옷가지를 만지작거렸고, 그것이 요구하는 쉴 새 없는 연주에 도무지 정신을 못 차리는 듯했다."]], [["All the young gentlemen, tightly cravatted, curled, and pumped, and with their best hats in their hands, having been at different times announced and introduced, Mr Baps, the dancing-master, came, accompanied by Mrs Baps, to whom Mrs Blimber was extremely kind and condescending.", "크라바트를 단단히 매고, 머리를 지지고, 에나멜 구두를 신고, 손에 가장 좋은 모자를 든 어린 신사들이 저마다 다른 시각에 도착이 알려지고 소개되고 나자, 무용 선생 뱁스 씨가 뱁스 부인과 함께 왔는데, 블림버 부인은 그 부인에게 무척 친절하고 너그러웠다."], ["Mr Baps was a very grave gentleman, with a slow and measured manner of speaking; and before he had stood under the lamp five minutes, he began to talk to Toots (who had been silently comparing pumps with him) about what you were to do with your raw materials when they came into your ports in return for your drain of gold.", "뱁스 씨는 더없이 근엄한 신사로, 말투가 느리고 또박또박했다. 그리고 등불 아래 선 지 오 분도 채 되기 전에, 그는 투츠를 붙들고(투츠는 그때껏 말없이 그와 에나멜 구두를 비교하고 있었다), 금을 유출하여 그 대가로 원자재가 항구로 들어왔을 때 그것을 어떻게 처리해야 하느냐는 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["Mr Toots, to whom the question seemed perplexing, suggested “Cook ’em.”", "그 질문이 난감해 보였던 투츠 씨는 \"삶아 버리죠\"라고 제안했다."], ["But Mr Baps did not appear to think that would do.", "그러나 뱁스 씨는 그것이 될 거라고는 여기지 않는 듯했다."]], [["Paul now slipped away from the cushioned corner of a sofa, which had been his post of observation, and went downstairs into the tea-room to be ready for Florence, whom he had not seen for nearly a fortnight, as he had remained at Doctor Blimber’s on the previous Saturday and Sunday, lest he should take cold.", "폴은 이제 자기 관찰 자리였던 소파의 푹신한 구석에서 슬그머니 빠져나와, 플로렌스를 맞을 준비를 하러 아래층 다과실로 내려갔다. 그는 거의 보름 가까이 누나를 보지 못했는데, 감기에 걸릴까 봐 지난 토요일과 일요일을 블림버 박사 댁에 머물렀기 때문이다."], ["Presently she came: looking so beautiful in her simple ball dress, with her fresh flowers in her hand, that when she knelt down on the ground to take Paul round the neck and kiss him (for there was no one there, but his friend and another young woman waiting to serve out the tea), he could hardly make up his mind to let her go again, or to take away her bright and loving eyes from his face.", "이내 누나가 왔다. 소박한 무도회 드레스를 입고, 손에 싱싱한 꽃을 든 모습이 어찌나 아름다웠던지, 그녀가 폴의 목을 끌어안고 입 맞추려고 바닥에 무릎을 꿇었을 때(거기에는 그의 친구와, 차를 따라 내려고 기다리던 또 다른 젊은 여인 말고는 아무도 없었으므로), 그는 누나를 다시 놓아 줄 마음도, 자기 얼굴에서 그 밝고 다정한 눈을 떼어 보낼 마음도 좀처럼 먹을 수가 없었다."]], [["“But what is the matter, Floy?”", "\"그런데 무슨 일이야, 플로이?\" 폴이 물었다."], ["asked Paul, almost sure that he saw a tear there.", "거기에 눈물이 어린 것을 본 것이 거의 확실했던 것이다."]], [["“Nothing, darling; nothing,”", "\"아무것도 아니야, 얘. 아무것도 아니야,\""], ["returned Florence.", "플로렌스가 대답했다."]], [["Paul touched her cheek gently with his finger—and it was a tear!", "폴이 손가락으로 그녀의 뺨을 살며시 만졌다—그것은 눈물이었다!"], ["“Why, Floy!”", "\"어머, 플로이!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“We’ll go home together, and I’ll nurse you, love,”", "\"우리 같이 집에 가자, 그러면 내가 너를 간호해 줄게, 아가,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Nurse me!”", "\"나를 간호한다고!\""], ["echoed Paul.", "폴이 따라 말했다."]], [["Paul couldn’t understand what that had to do with it, nor why the two young women looked on so seriously, nor why Florence turned away her face for a moment, and then turned it back, lighted up again with smiles.", "폴은 그것이 이 일과 무슨 상관인지, 또 두 젊은 여인이 왜 그렇게 심각하게 바라보는지, 또 플로렌스가 왜 잠시 얼굴을 돌렸다가 다시 미소로 환하게 빛나며 돌아오는지 도무지 알 수 없었다."]], [["“Floy,”", "\"플로이,\""], ["said Paul, holding a ringlet of her dark hair in his hand.", "폴이 누나의 검은 머리 한 가닥을 손에 쥐며 말했다."], ["“Tell me, dear, Do you think I have grown old-fashioned?”", "\"말해 줘, 누나. 누나는 내가 노숙해졌다고 생각해?\""]], [["His sister laughed, and fondled him, and told him “No.”", "누이는 웃으며 그를 어루만지고는 \"아니\"라고 말해 주었다."]], [["“Because I know they say so,”", "\"왜냐하면 사람들이 그렇게 말하거든,\""], ["returned Paul, “and I want to know what they mean, Floy.”", "폴이 대답했다, \"그게 무슨 뜻인지 알고 싶어, 플로이.\""]], [["But a loud double knock coming at the door, and Florence hurrying to the table, there was no more said between them.", "그러나 문에서 요란하게 두 번 두드리는 소리가 나고 플로렌스가 급히 탁자로 가는 바람에, 둘 사이에 더는 말이 오가지 않았다."], ["Paul wondered again when he saw his friend whisper to Florence, as if she were comforting her; but a new arrival put that out of his head speedily.", "폴은 자기 친구가 플로렌스에게, 마치 그녀를 달래는 듯 속삭이는 것을 보고 다시 의아해했다. 그러나 새로 도착한 손님이 그것을 금세 그의 머릿속에서 몰아냈다."]], [["It was Sir Barnet Skettles, Lady Skettles, and Master Skettles.", "도착한 이는 바닛 스케틀스 경과 스케틀스 부인과 스케틀스 도련님이었다."], ["Master Skettles was to be a new boy after the vacation, and Fame had been busy, in Mr Feeder’s room, with his father, who was in the House of Commons, and of whom Mr Feeder had said that when he did catch the Speaker’s eye (which he had been expected to do for three or four years), it was anticipated that he would rather touch up the Radicals.", "스케틀스 도련님은 방학 뒤에 새로 입학할 학생이었고, 피더 선생의 방에서는 하원 의원인 그 아버지에 대해 소문이 자자했다. 피더 선생은 그에 대해, 그가 (서너 해 동안 그러리라 기대되어 온) 의장의 눈에 들어 발언권을 얻게 되면, 급진파를 한바탕 손봐 주리라 예상된다고 말한 바 있었다."]], [["“And what room is this now, for instance?”", "\"그런데 이를테면 이 방은 지금 뭐 하는 방이지?\""], ["said Lady Skettles to Paul’s friend, “Melia.", "스케틀스 부인이 폴의 친구 밀리아에게 말했다."]], [["“Doctor Blimber’s study, Ma’am,”", "\"블림버 박사님의 서재입니다, 부인,\""], ["was the reply.", "하는 대답이었다."]], [["Lady Skettles took a panoramic survey of it through her glass, and said to Sir Barnet Skettles, with a nod of approval, “Very good.”", "스케틀스 부인은 안경을 통해 그 방을 한 바퀴 죽 둘러보더니, 바닛 스케틀스 경에게 흡족하다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다, \"아주 좋군요.\""], ["Sir Barnet assented, but Master Skettles looked suspicious and doubtful.", "바닛 경도 동의했으나, 스케틀스 도련님은 미심쩍고 의심스러운 표정이었다."]], [["“And this little creature, now,”", "\"그런데 이 자그마한 아이는,\""], ["said Lady Skettles, turning to Paul.", "스케틀스 부인이 폴에게로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“Is he one of the—”", "\"이 아이도 그—\""]], [["“Young gentlemen, Ma’am; yes, Ma’am,”", "\"어린 신사 가운데 하나입니다, 부인. 네, 부인,\""], ["said Paul’s friend.", "폴의 친구가 말했다."]], [["“And what is your name, my pale child?”", "\"네 이름은 무엇이니, 창백한 꼬마야?\""], ["said Lady Skettles.", "스케틀스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비예요,\""], ["answered Paul.", "폴이 대답했다."]], [["Sir Barnet Skettles immediately interposed, and said that he had had the honour of meeting Paul’s father at a public dinner, and that he hoped he was very well.", "바닛 스케틀스 경이 즉시 끼어들어, 자기가 어느 공식 만찬에서 폴의 아버지를 뵙는 영광을 누렸다고, 그분이 무척 평안하시길 바란다고 했다."], ["Then Paul heard him say to Lady Skettles, “City—very rich—most respectable—Doctor mentioned it.”", "그러고는 폴이 듣자니, 그가 스케틀스 부인에게 말했다, \"시티 사람—굉장한 부자—더없이 점잖은 분—박사가 말해 줬소.\""], ["And then he said to Paul, “Will you tell your good Papa that Sir Barnet Skettles rejoiced to hear that he was very well, and sent him his best compliments?”", "그러더니 그는 폴에게 말했다, \"착한 너의 아버님께, 바닛 스케틀스 경이 그분이 무척 평안하시다는 소식을 듣고 기뻐했으며, 진심 어린 안부를 전한다고 말씀드려 주겠니?\""]], [["“Yes, Sir,”", "\"네, 그러죠,\""], ["answered Paul.", "폴이 대답했다."]], [["“That is my brave boy,”", "\"용감한 우리 아이로구나,\""], ["said Sir Barnet Skettles.", "바닛 스케틀스 경이 말했다."], ["“Barnet,”", "\"바닛,\""], ["to Master Skettles, who was revenging himself for the studies to come, on the plum-cake, “this is a young gentleman you ought to know.", "다가올 학업에 대한 앙갚음을 자두 케이크에 풀고 있던 스케틀스 도련님에게 그가 말했다, \"이 아이는 네가 알아 두어야 할 어린 신사다."], ["This is a young gentleman you may know, Barnet,”", "이 아이는 네가 알고 지내도 좋을 어린 신사야, 바닛,\""], ["said Sir Barnet Skettles, with an emphasis on the permission.", "바닛 스케틀스 경은 그 허락에 힘주어 말했다."]], [["“What eyes!", "\"저 눈 좀 봐!"], ["What hair!", "저 머리카락!"], ["What a lovely face!”", "저 사랑스러운 얼굴!\""], ["exclaimed Lady Skettles softly, as she looked at Florence through her glass.", "스케틀스 부인이 안경을 통해 플로렌스를 바라보며 나직이 감탄했다."]], [["“My sister,”", "\"제 누나예요,\""], ["said Paul, presenting her.", "폴이 그녀를 소개하며 말했다."]], [["The satisfaction of the Skettleses was now complete.", "이제 스케틀스 일가의 만족은 완전했다."], ["And as Lady Skettles had conceived, at first sight, a liking for Paul, they all went upstairs together: Sir Barnet Skettles taking care of Florence, and young Barnet following.", "그리고 스케틀스 부인이 첫눈에 폴에게 호감을 품었던 터라, 그들은 모두 함께 위층으로 올라갔다. 바닛 스케틀스 경이 플로렌스를 보살피고, 어린 바닛이 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["Young Barnet did not remain long in the background after they had reached the drawing-room, for Dr Blimber had him out in no time, dancing with Florence.", "어린 바닛은 그들이 응접실에 이른 뒤로 그리 오래 뒤편에 머물러 있지 않았으니, 블림버 박사가 그를 곧장 끌어내 플로렌스와 춤을 추게 했기 때문이다."], ["He did not appear to Paul to be particularly happy, or particularly anything but sulky, or to care much what he was about; but as Paul heard Lady Skettles say to Mrs Blimber, while she beat time with her fan, that her dear boy was evidently smitten to death by that angel of a child, Miss Dombey, it would seem that Skettles Junior was in a state of bliss, without showing it.", "그는 폴이 보기에 특별히 행복해 보이지도, 뾰로통한 것 말고 무엇이라도 특별해 보이지도, 자기가 무얼 하고 있는지 그다지 신경 쓰는 것 같지도 않았다. 그러나 폴이, 스케틀스 부인이 부채로 박자를 맞추며 블림버 부인에게, 자기 귀한 아들이 저 천사 같은 아이, 돔비 양에게 분명 홀딱 반해 버린 것 같다고 말하는 것을 들으니, 어린 스케틀스는 겉으로 드러내지는 않아도 더없는 행복에 빠져 있는 모양이었다."]], [["Little Paul thought it a singular coincidence that nobody had occupied his place among the pillows; and that when he came into the room again, they should all make way for him to go back to it, remembering it was his.", "어린 폴은 아무도 그 베개들 사이 자기 자리를 차지하지 않았다는 것이, 또 자기가 다시 방에 들어오자 그것이 자기 자리임을 기억하고 모두 그가 거기로 돌아가도록 길을 비켜 준다는 것이 참 묘한 우연이라고 생각했다."], ["Nobody stood before him either, when they observed that he liked to see Florence dancing, but they left the space in front quite clear, so that he might follow her with his eyes.", "또 그들은 그가 플로렌스가 춤추는 것을 보고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아채자, 그의 앞에 아무도 서지 않고 그 앞 공간을 말끔히 비워 두어, 그가 눈으로 누나를 좇을 수 있게 해 주었다."], ["They were so kind, too, even the strangers, of whom there were soon a great many, that they came and spoke to him every now and then, and asked him how he was, and if his head ached, and whether he was tired.", "그들은 또 어찌나 친절했던지, 이내 무척 많아진 낯선 손님들조차도, 이따금 와서 그에게 말을 건네고, 좀 어떠냐고, 머리가 아프지는 않냐고, 피곤하지는 않냐고 물었다."], ["He was very much obliged to them for all their kindness and attention, and reclining propped up in his corner, with Mrs Blimber and Lady Skettles on the same sofa, and Florence coming and sitting by his side as soon as every dance was ended, he looked on very happily indeed.", "그는 그들의 그 모든 친절과 배려가 무척 고마웠다. 그리고 자기 구석에 받쳐져 비스듬히 기댄 채, 같은 소파에 블림버 부인과 스케틀스 부인을 둔 채, 또 춤이 한 곡 끝날 때마다 플로렌스가 와서 곁에 앉아 주는 가운데, 그는 참으로 행복하게 바라보았다."]], [["Florence would have sat by him all night, and would not have danced at all of her own accord, but Paul made her, by telling her how much it pleased him.", "플로렌스는 밤새 그의 곁에 앉아, 제 뜻으로는 춤을 전혀 추지 않으려 했을 것이다. 그러나 폴이, 그것이 자기를 얼마나 기쁘게 하는지 말해 주며 누나에게 그러게 했다."], ["And he told her the truth, too; for his small heart swelled, and his face glowed, when he saw how much they all admired her, and how she was the beautiful little rosebud of the room.", "그리고 그는 진심을 말한 것이기도 했다. 모두가 누나를 얼마나 흠모하는지, 누나가 그 방의 아름다운 작은 장미 봉오리임을 볼 때면, 그의 작은 가슴이 부풀고 얼굴이 달아올랐기 때문이다."]], [["From his nest among the pillows, Paul could see and hear almost everything that passed as if the whole were being done for his amusement.", "베개들 사이 자기 둥지에서, 폴은 마치 모든 것이 자기를 즐겁게 해 주려고 펼쳐지기라도 하는 듯, 벌어지는 거의 모든 것을 보고 들을 수 있었다."], ["Among other little incidents that he observed, he observed Mr Baps the dancing-master get into conversation with Sir Barnet Skettles, and very soon ask him, as he had asked Mr Toots, what you were to do with your raw materials, when they came into your ports in return for your drain of gold—which was such a mystery to Paul that he was quite desirous to know what ought to be done with them.", "그가 눈여겨본 자잘한 일들 가운데, 그는 무용 선생 뱁스 씨가 바닛 스케틀스 경과 대화에 빠져드는 것을, 그리고 곧 그에게도, 투츠 씨에게 물었던 것처럼, 금을 유출하여 그 대가로 원자재가 항구로 들어왔을 때 그것을 어떻게 처리해야 하느냐고 묻는 것을 보았다—그것은 폴에게 너무도 수수께끼여서, 그는 그것들을 대체 어떻게 해야 하는지 무척 알고 싶었다. 바닛 스케틀스 경은 그 문제에 대해 할 말이 많았고, 또 그렇게 말했다."], ["Sir Barnet Skettles had much to say upon the question, and said it; but it did not appear to solve the question, for Mr Baps retorted, Yes, but supposing Russia stepped in with her tallows; which struck Sir Barnet almost dumb, for he could only shake his head after that, and say, Why then you must fall back upon your cottons, he supposed.", "그러나 그것이 문제를 푸는 것 같지는 않았으니, 뱁스 씨가, 그렇지만 러시아가 자기네 수지(獸脂)를 들고 끼어든다고 가정해 보라고 응수했기 때문이다. 그 말에 바닛 경은 거의 말문이 막혀, 그저 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는, 그렇다면 면화로 물러설 수밖에 없겠지요, 라고 말할 수 있을 따름이었다."]], [["Sir Barnet Skettles looked after Mr Baps when he went to cheer up Mrs Baps (who, being quite deserted, was pretending to look over the music-book of the gentleman who played the harp), as if he thought him a remarkable kind of man; and shortly afterwards he said so in those words to Doctor Blimber, and inquired if he might take the liberty of asking who he was, and whether he had ever been in the Board of Trade.", "바닛 스케틀스 경은, 뱁스 씨가 (아주 외톨이가 되어, 하프를 켜는 신사의 악보집을 들여다보는 척하던) 뱁스 부인을 위로하러 갔을 때, 그를 마치 비범한 부류의 사람으로 여기는 듯 뒤를 좇아 바라보았다. 그리고 잠시 뒤 그는 블림버 박사에게 바로 그 말을 했고, 외람되지만 그가 누구인지, 또 무역위원회에 몸담은 적이 있는지 물어봐도 되겠느냐고 했다."], ["Doctor Blimber answered no, he believed not; and that in fact he was a Professor of—”", "블림버 박사는, 아니, 아닐 거라고, 사실 그는 무엇무엇의 교수라고 대답했다—"]], [["“Of something connected with statistics, I’ll swear?”", "\"통계에 관련된 무언가겠지요, 장담합니다만?\""], ["observed Sir Barnet Skettles.", "바닛 스케틀스 경이 말했다."]], [["“Why no, Sir Barnet,”", "\"그게, 아닙니다, 바닛 경,\""], ["replied Doctor Blimber, rubbing his chin.", "블림버 박사가 턱을 문지르며 대답했다."], ["“No, not exactly.”", "\"아니요, 꼭 그렇지는 않습니다.\""]], [["“Figures of some sort, I would venture a bet,”", "\"무슨 종류든 수치에 관한 거겠지요, 내기를 걸어도 좋습니다만,\""], ["said Sir Barnet Skettles.", "바닛 스케틀스 경이 말했다."]], [["“Why yes,”", "\"글쎄요, 그렇습니다,\""], ["said Doctor Blimber, yes, but not of that sort.", "블림버 박사가 말했다, \"네, 그렇지만 그런 종류는 아닙니다."], ["Mr Baps is a very worthy sort of man, Sir Barnet, and—in fact he’s our Professor of dancing.”", "뱁스 씨는 무척 훌륭한 부류의 사람이지요, 바닛 경, 그리고—실은 우리 무용 교수입니다.\""]], [["Paul was amazed to see that this piece of information quite altered Sir Barnet Skettles’s opinion of Mr Baps, and that Sir Barnet flew into a perfect rage, and glowered at Mr Baps over on the other side of the room.", "폴은 이 한마디 정보가 뱁스 씨에 대한 바닛 스케틀스 경의 의견을 완전히 바꿔 놓는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 바닛 경은 그만 노발대발하여, 방 저편의 뱁스 씨를 사납게 노려보았다."], ["He even went so far as to D— Mr Baps to Lady Skettles, in telling her what had happened, and to say that it was like his most con-sum-mate and con-foun-ded impudence.", "그는 심지어, 무슨 일이 있었는지 스케틀스 부인에게 이야기하면서 뱁스 씨를 \"빌어먹을\" 작자라 부르고, 그건 그 더없이 뻔뻔—스럽고 괘씸—한 무례라고까지 했다."]], [["There was another thing that Paul observed.", "폴이 눈여겨본 일이 또 하나 있었다."], ["Mr Feeder, after imbibing several custard-cups of negus, began to enjoy himself.", "피더 선생이 니거스 술을 커스터드 잔으로 여러 잔 들이켜더니, 흥겨워지기 시작한 것이다."], ["The dancing in general was ceremonious, and the music rather solemn—a little like church music in fact—but after the custard-cups, Mr Feeder told Mr Toots that he was going to throw a little spirit into the thing.", "전반적으로 춤은 격식을 차렸고 음악은 다소 엄숙했다—사실 교회 음악과 좀 비슷했다. 그러나 그 커스터드 잔들을 마신 뒤, 피더 선생은 투츠 씨에게, 자기가 거기에 활기를 좀 불어넣겠다고 했다."], ["After that, Mr Feeder not only began to dance as if he meant dancing and nothing else, but secretly to stimulate the music to perform wild tunes.", "그러고 나서 피더 선생은 정말로 춤만이 목적이라는 듯 춤을 추기 시작했을 뿐 아니라, 음악이 거친 가락을 연주하도록 은밀히 부추기기까지 했다."], ["Further, he became particular in his attentions to the ladies; and dancing with Miss Blimber, whispered to her—whispered to her!—though not so softly but that Paul heard him say this remarkable poetry,", "더 나아가 그는 숙녀들에게 유난히 살가워졌다. 그리고 블림버 양과 춤추면서, 그녀에게 속삭였다—그녀에게 속삭인 것이다!—그것도 폴이 이 놀라운 시구를 그가 읊는 것을 들을 만큼은 또렷이."]], [["“Had I a heart for falsehood framed, I ne’er could injure You!”", "\"거짓을 위해 빚어진 마음을 내가 가졌던들,\n결코 그대를 해칠 수는 없으리!\""]], [["This, Paul heard him repeat to four young ladies, in succession.", "이것을, 폴은 그가 네 명의 젊은 숙녀에게 차례로 되풀이하는 것을 들었다."], ["Well might Mr Feeder say to Mr Toots, that he was afraid he should be the worse for it to-morrow!", "피더 선생이 투츠 씨에게, 내일 이 일로 탈이 날까 봐 걱정이라고 말한 것도 무리가 아니었다!"]], [["Mrs Blimber was a little alarmed by this—comparatively speaking—profligate behaviour; and especially by the alteration in the character of the music, which, beginning to comprehend low melodies that were popular in the streets, might not unnaturally be supposed to give offence to Lady Skettles.", "블림버 부인은 이—비교적 말하자면—방탕한 행동에, 특히 음악의 성격이 변한 것에 다소 놀랐다. 거리에서 유행하던 천박한 가락들까지 끌어안기 시작한 그 음악이, 스케틀스 부인의 비위를 거스를지도 모른다고 여기는 것은 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["But Lady Skettles was so very kind as to beg Mrs Blimber not to mention it; and to receive her explanation that Mr Feeder’s spirits sometimes betrayed him into excesses on these occasions, with the greatest courtesy and politeness; observing, that he seemed a very nice sort of person for his situation, and that she particularly liked the unassuming style of his hair—which (as already hinted) was about a quarter of an inch long.", "그러나 스케틀스 부인은 무척 친절하게도, 블림버 부인에게 그런 건 입에 올리지 말아 달라고 부탁했다. 그리고 피더 선생의 흥이 이런 자리에서 이따금 그를 도를 넘게 만든다는 부인의 해명을, 더없는 정중함과 예의로 받아들이며, 그가 그 처지로서는 무척 좋은 사람 같다고, 또 자기는 특히 그의 그 잘난 체하지 않는 머리 모양이 마음에 든다고 했다—그 머리란 (이미 넌지시 비쳤듯이) 4분의 1인치쯤 길었다."]], [["Once, when there was a pause in the dancing, Lady Skettles told Paul that he seemed very fond of music.", "한번은 춤이 잠시 멈춘 사이, 스케틀스 부인이 폴에게, 너는 음악을 무척 좋아하는 것 같구나, 하고 말했다."], ["Paul replied, that he was; and if she was too, she ought to hear his sister, Florence, sing.", "폴은 그렇다고 대답하고는, 부인도 그러시다면 자기 누나 플로렌스가 노래하는 것을 들으셔야 한다고 했다."], ["Lady Skettles presently discovered that she was dying with anxiety to have that gratification; and though Florence was at first very much frightened at being asked to sing before so many people, and begged earnestly to be excused, yet, on Paul calling her to him, and saying, “Do, Floy!", "스케틀스 부인은 이내, 그 즐거움을 누리고 싶어 애가 탄다는 것을 깨달았다. 그리고 플로렌스는 그토록 많은 사람 앞에서 노래해 달라는 청에 처음엔 무척 겁을 먹고, 부디 양해해 달라고 간곡히 빌었으나, 폴이 자기에게로 불러 \"그러자, 플로이!"], ["Please!", "제발!"], ["For me, my dear!” she went straight to the piano, and began.", "나를 위해서, 얘!\"라고 말하자, 곧장 피아노로 가서 노래를 시작했다."], ["When they all drew a little away, that Paul might see her; and when he saw her sitting there all alone, so young, and good, and beautiful, and kind to him; and heard her thrilling voice, so natural and sweet, and such a golden link between him and all his life’s love and happiness, rising out of the silence; he turned his face away, and hid his tears.", "폴이 누나를 볼 수 있도록 모두 조금씩 비켜서고, 또 그녀가 거기 홀로 앉아 있는 것을—그토록 젊고, 착하고, 아름답고, 자기에게 다정한 그 모습을—그가 보았을 때, 그리고 그토록 자연스럽고 감미로운, 자기와 자기 삶의 모든 사랑과 행복 사이를 잇는 그런 황금의 고리 같은 그녀의 떨리는 목소리가 고요를 뚫고 솟아오르는 것을 들었을 때, 그는 얼굴을 돌리고 눈물을 감추었다."], ["Not, as he told them when they spoke to him, not that the music was too plaintive or too sorrowful, but it was so dear to him.", "사람들이 그에게 말을 걸었을 때 그가 그들에게 일러 주었듯이, 음악이 너무 애처롭거나 너무 구슬퍼서가 아니라, 그것이 그에게 너무도 소중해서였다."]], [["They all loved Florence.", "그들은 모두 플로렌스를 사랑했다."], ["How could they help it!", "어찌 그러지 않을 수 있었겠는가!"], ["Paul had known beforehand that they must and would; and sitting in his cushioned corner, with calmly folded hands; and one leg loosely doubled under him, few would have thought what triumph and delight expanded his childish bosom while he watched her, or what a sweet tranquillity he felt.", "폴은 그들이 반드시 그러리라는 것을, 또 그럴 것을 미리 알고 있었다. 그리고 푹신한 구석에 앉아, 두 손을 차분히 모으고, 한쪽 다리는 헐겁게 몸 밑으로 접은 채로, 그가 누나를 지켜보는 동안 그 어린 가슴을 어떤 승리감과 기쁨이 부풀렸는지, 또 그가 얼마나 달콤한 평온을 느꼈는지, 짐작할 이는 거의 없었을 것이다."], ["Lavish encomiums on “Dombey’s sister”", "\"돔비의 누나\"에 대한 아낌없는 찬사가 모든 아이들에게서 그의 귀에 닿았다."], ["reached his ears from all the boys: admiration of the self-possessed and modest little beauty was on every lip: reports of her intelligence and accomplishments floated past him, constantly; and, as if borne in upon the air of the summer night, there was a half intelligible sentiment diffused around, referring to Florence and himself, and breathing sympathy for both, that soothed and touched him.", "그 침착하고 겸손한 작은 미인에 대한 감탄이 모든 입에 올랐고, 그녀의 지성과 재주에 대한 이야기가 끊임없이 그의 곁을 흘러 지나갔다. 그리고 마치 여름밤의 대기에 실려 오기라도 한 듯, 주위에는 플로렌스와 자기에 관한, 절반쯤 알아들을 만한 어떤 정감이 퍼져 있어, 둘 모두에게 공감을 보내며, 그를 어루만지고 그의 마음을 적셨다."]], [["He did not know why.", "그는 왜 그런지 알지 못했다."], ["For all that the child observed, and felt, and thought, that night—the present and the absent; what was then and what had been—were blended like the colours in the rainbow, or in the plumage of rich birds when the sun is shining on them, or in the softening sky when the same sun is setting.", "그날 밤 그 아이가 본 것과 느낀 것과 생각한 모든 것—있는 것과 없는 것, 지금인 것과 지나간 것—이 무지개의 색깔처럼, 혹은 해가 비칠 때 화려한 새들의 깃털처럼, 혹은 그 같은 해가 질 때 부드러워지는 하늘처럼 뒤섞였던 것이다."], ["The many things he had had to think of lately, passed before him in the music; not as claiming his attention over again, or as likely evermore to occupy it, but as peacefully disposed of and gone.", "요즘 그가 생각해야 했던 그 숱한 것들이 음악 속에서 그의 앞을 지나갔다. 다시 그의 주의를 요구하려는 것도, 앞으로도 영영 그것을 차지할 듯한 것도 아니라, 평온히 마무리되어 떠나간 것으로서."], ["A solitary window, gazed through years ago, looked out upon an ocean, miles and miles away; upon its waters, fancies, busy with him only yesterday, were hushed and lulled to rest like broken waves.", "여러 해 전에 내다보던 외딴 창 하나가, 몇 마일이고 떨어진 바다를 내다보았다. 그 물결 위로, 바로 어제까지도 그에게 분주하던 상념들이, 부서진 물결처럼 잠잠해지고 잦아들어 쉬었다."], ["The same mysterious murmur he had wondered at, when lying on his couch upon the beach, he thought he still heard sounding through his sister’s song, and through the hum of voices, and the tread of feet, and having some part in the faces flitting by, and even in the heavy gentleness of Mr Toots, who frequently came up to shake him by the hand.", "바닷가 침상에 누워 있을 때 의아해하던 그 신비로운 속삭임이, 누나의 노래를 뚫고, 사람들의 목소리의 웅성거림과 발걸음 소리를 뚫고 여전히 울려오는 것 같았고, 또 스쳐 지나가는 얼굴들 속에도, 심지어 자주 다가와 그의 손을 잡아 주던 투츠 씨의 그 묵직한 다정함 속에도 어느 정도 깃들어 있는 듯했다."], ["Through the universal kindness he still thought he heard it, speaking to him; and even his old-fashioned reputation seemed to be allied to it, he knew not how.", "그 두루 퍼진 친절을 뚫고도, 그것이 여전히 자기에게 말을 거는 것을 듣는 것만 같았다. 그리고 그의 노숙하다는 평판마저도, 어찌 된 영문인지는 몰라도, 그것과 한 가닥으로 이어져 있는 것 같았다."], ["Thus little Paul sat musing, listening, looking on, and dreaming; and was very happy.", "그렇게 어린 폴은 골똘히 생각하며, 귀 기울이며, 바라보며, 꿈꾸며 앉아 있었다. 그리고 무척 행복했다."]], [["Until the time arrived for taking leave: and then, indeed, there was a sensation in the party.", "작별할 때가 올 때까지는 그러했다. 그리고 그때가 되자, 과연 모임에 한바탕 술렁임이 일었다."], ["Sir Barnet Skettles brought up Skettles Junior to shake hands with him, and asked him if he would remember to tell his good Papa, with his best compliments, that he, Sir Barnet Skettles, had said he hoped the two young gentlemen would become intimately acquainted.", "바닛 스케틀스 경이 어린 스케틀스를 데려와 그와 악수하게 하고는, 착한 너의 아버님께 잊지 말고, 자기, 바닛 스케틀스 경이, 두 어린 신사가 친밀하게 알고 지내게 되길 바란다고 했다는 말을 진심 어린 안부와 함께 전해 주겠느냐고 물었다."], ["Lady Skettles kissed him, and patted his hair upon his brow, and held him in her arms; and even Mrs Baps—poor Mrs Baps!", "스케틀스 부인은 그에게 입 맞추고, 이마 위로 흘러내린 머리를 쓰다듬어 주고, 그를 품에 안았다."], ["Paul was glad of that—came over from beside the music-book of the gentleman who played the harp, and took leave of him quite as heartily as anybody in the room.", "심지어 뱁스 부인까지도—가엾은 뱁스 부인! 폴은 그것이 기뻤다—하프 켜는 신사의 악보집 곁에서 건너와, 그 방의 누구 못지않게 진심으로 그에게 작별을 고했다."]], [["“Good-bye, Doctor Blimber,”", "\"안녕히 계세요, 블림버 박사님,\""], ["said Paul, stretching out his hand.", "폴이 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Good-bye, my little friend,”", "\"잘 가거라, 우리 꼬마 친구,\""], ["returned the Doctor.", "박사가 대답했다."]], [["“I’m very much obliged to you, Sir,”", "\"정말 고맙습니다, 선생님,\""], ["said Paul, looking innocently up into his awful face.", "폴이 그 무시무시한 얼굴을 천진하게 올려다보며 말했다."], ["“Ask them to take care of Diogenes, if you please.”", "\"괜찮으시면, 디오게네스를 잘 보살펴 달라고 일러 주세요.\""]], [["Diogenes was the dog: who had never in his life received a friend into his confidence, before Paul.", "디오게네스란 그 개였다. 그 개는 폴이 있기 전까지는 평생 단 한 명의 벗에게도 마음을 터놓은 적이 없었다."], ["The Doctor promised that every attention should be paid to Diogenes in Paul’s absence, and Paul having again thanked him, and shaken hands with him, bade adieu to Mrs Blimber and Cornelia with such heartfelt earnestness that Mrs Blimber forgot from that moment to mention Cicero to Lady Skettles, though she had fully intended it all the evening.", "박사는 폴이 없는 동안 디오게네스에게 온갖 보살핌을 베풀겠노라 약속했고, 폴은 그에게 다시 한번 고맙다고 한 뒤 악수를 했다. 그러고는 블림버 부인과 코닐리어에게도 어찌나 진심 어린 간절함으로 작별을 고했던지, 블림버 부인은 그 순간부터 키케로를 스케틀스 부인에게 언급하는 것을 잊고 말았다."], ["Cornelia, taking both Paul’s hands in hers, said, “Dombey, Dombey, you have always been my favourite pupil.", "저녁 내내 그럴 작정이 분명했는데도 말이다. 코닐리어는 폴의 두 손을 자기 두 손에 쥐고 말했다, \"돔비, 돔비, 너는 늘 내가 가장 아끼는 학생이었단다."], ["God bless you!”", "신의 가호가 있기를!\""], ["And it showed, Paul thought, how easily one might do injustice to a person; for Miss Blimber meant it—though she was a Forcer—and felt it.", "그것은, 한 사람을 얼마나 쉽게 오해할 수 있는지를 보여 주는 것이라고 폴은 생각했다. 블림버 양은—비록 닦달꾼이긴 했어도—진심으로 그 말을 했고, 또 진심으로 그렇게 느꼈으니까."]], [["A buzz then went round among the young gentlemen, of “Dombey’s going!”", "그러자 어린 신사들 사이에 \"돔비가 간대!\""], ["“Little Dombey’s going!”", "\"꼬마 돔비가 간대!\""], ["and there was a general move after Paul and Florence down the staircase and into the hall, in which the whole Blimber family were included.", "하는 웅성거림이 돌았고, 폴과 플로렌스를 뒤따라 계단을 내려와 현관으로 향하는 전반적인 움직임이 일었는데, 거기엔 블림버 일가가 모두 끼어 있었다."], ["Such a circumstance, Mr Feeder said aloud, as had never happened in the case of any former young gentleman within his experience; but it would be difficult to say if this were", "그런 일은, 피더 선생이 큰 소리로 말하기를, 자기가 겪은 한 어떤 전임 어린 신사의 경우에도 일어난 적이 없는 일이라고 했다. 그러나 이것이"]], [["sober fact or custard-cups.", "냉정한 사실이나 커스터드 컵 같은 것은 아니었다."], ["The servants, with the butler at their head, had all an interest in seeing Little Dombey go; and even the weak-eyed young man, taking out his books and trunks to the coach that was to carry him and Florence to Mrs Pipchin’s for the night, melted visibly.", "하인들도, 집사를 우두머리로 하여 모두가 어린 돔비가 떠나는 것을 보는 데 관심을 가졌고, 심지어 눈이 약한 그 젊은이조차 그날 밤 그와 플로렌스를 핍친 부인 댁으로 데려갈 마차에 책과 트렁크를 실어 내면서 눈에 띄게 마음이 녹았다."]], [["Not even the influence of the softer passion on the young gentlemen—and they all, to a boy, doted on Florence—could restrain them from taking quite a noisy leave of Paul; waving hats after him, pressing downstairs to shake hands with him, crying individually “Dombey, don’t forget me!”", "그 어린 신사들에게 부드러운 연정이 미치는 영향력조차도—그들은 하나같이, 한 아이도 빠짐없이 플로렌스를 끔찍이 좋아했다—폴에게 자못 떠들썩한 작별을 고하는 것을 막지는 못했다. 그들은 폴의 뒤로 모자를 흔들어 댔고, 아래층으로 우르르 밀려 내려와 그와 악수를 했으며, 저마다 \"돔비, 날 잊지 마!\""], ["and indulging in many such ebullitions of feeling, uncommon among those young Chesterfields.", "하고 외쳐 댔고, 그 어린 체스터필드들 사이에서는 보기 드문 그런 감정의 분출을 마음껏 쏟아 냈다."], ["Paul whispered Florence, as she wrapped him up before the door was opened, Did she hear them?", "폴은 문이 열리기 전에 플로렌스가 자기를 감싸 줄 때 그녀에게 속삭였다, 저 소리 들었어?"], ["Would she ever forget it?", "저걸 영영 잊지 않을 거지?"], ["Was she glad to know it?", "저걸 알게 되어 기뻐?"], ["And a lively delight was in his eyes as he spoke to her.", "그녀에게 말하는 그의 눈 속에는 생기 넘치는 기쁨이 어려 있었다."]], [["Once, for a last look, he turned and gazed upon the faces thus addressed to him, surprised to see how shining and how bright, and numerous they were, and how they were all piled and heaped up, as faces are at crowded theatres.", "마지막으로 한 번 보려고, 그는 몸을 돌려 그렇게 자기를 향해 있는 얼굴들을 바라보았는데, 그것들이 어찌나 빛나고 환하며 또 그렇게 수가 많은지, 그리고 붐비는 극장에서 얼굴들이 그러하듯 어찌나 차곡차곡 쌓이고 포개져 있는지를 보고 놀랐다."], ["They swam before him as he looked, like faces in an agitated glass; and next moment he was in the dark coach outside, holding close to Florence.", "그가 바라보는 동안 그것들은 흔들리는 거울 속의 얼굴들처럼 그의 눈앞에서 일렁였다. 그리고 다음 순간 그는 바깥 어두운 마차 안에서, 플로렌스에게 바싹 매달려 있었다."], ["From that time, whenever he thought of Doctor Blimber’s, it came back as he had seen it in this last view; and it never seemed to be a real place again, but always a dream, full of eyes.", "그때부터 블림버 박사의 학교를 떠올릴 때면 언제나 이 마지막 광경에서 본 모습 그대로 되살아났다. 그리고 그곳은 두 번 다시 실재하는 장소로 여겨지지 않았고, 언제나 눈으로 가득 찬 꿈처럼만 느껴졌다."]], [["This was not quite the last of Doctor Blimber’s, however.", "그러나 이것이 블림버 박사의 학교에 대한 정말로 마지막은 아니었다."], ["There was something else.", "또 한 가지가 더 있었다."], ["There was Mr Toots.", "바로 투츠 씨였다."], ["Who, unexpectedly letting down one of the coach-windows, and looking in, said, with a most egregious chuckle, “Is Dombey there?”", "그는 뜻밖에도 마차 창문 하나를 내리고 안을 들여다보며, 더없이 엉뚱한 낄낄거림과 함께 \"돔비 거기 있어?\""], ["and immediately put it up again, without waiting for an answer.", "하고 말하고는, 대답도 기다리지 않고 곧바로 창문을 도로 올렸다."], ["Nor was this quite the last of Mr Toots, even; for before the coachman could drive off, he as suddenly let down the other window, and looking in with a precisely similar chuckle, said in a precisely similar tone of voice, “Is Dombey there?”", "그리고 이것조차도 투츠 씨의 정말로 마지막은 아니었다. 마부가 마차를 몰고 떠나기도 전에, 그는 또 그렇게 갑작스럽게 다른 쪽 창문을 내리더니, 꼭 같은 낄낄거림과 함께 안을 들여다보며 꼭 같은 어조로 \"돔비 거기 있어?\""], ["and disappeared precisely as before.", "하고 말하고는, 꼭 앞서처럼 사라져 버렸다."]], [["How Florence laughed!", "플로렌스는 어찌나 웃어 댔던지!"], ["Paul often remembered it, and laughed himself whenever he did so.", "폴은 그 일을 자주 떠올렸고, 떠올릴 때마다 스스로도 웃었다."]], [["But there was much, soon afterwards—next day, and after that—which Paul could only recollect confusedly.", "그러나 그 직후—다음 날, 그리고 그 뒤로—폴이 그저 어렴풋하게만 기억할 수 있는 일들이 많았다."], ["As, why they stayed at Mrs Pipchin’s days and nights, instead of going home; why he lay in bed, with Florence sitting by his side; whether that had been his father in the room, or only a tall shadow on the wall; whether he had heard his doctor say, of someone, that if they had removed him before the occasion on which he had built up fancies, strong in proportion to his own weakness, it was very possible he might have pined away.", "이를테면, 어째서 집으로 돌아가지 않고 핍친 부인 댁에서 며칠 밤낮을 머물렀는지, 어째서 플로렌스가 곁에 앉아 있는 채로 자기가 침대에 누워 있었는지, 방 안에 있던 것이 정말 자기 아버지였는지 아니면 그저 벽에 비친 키 큰 그림자였는지, 자기 의사가 누군가에 대해, 만약 그가 자신의 약함에 비례하여 강하게 키워 낸 공상들을 품게 된 그 계기가 있기 전에 그를 옮겨 놓았더라면 그가 시들어 죽어 갔을 가능성이 매우 컸으리라고 말하는 것을 자기가 들었는지 하는 것들이."]], [["He could not even remember whether he had often said to Florence, “Oh Floy, take me home, and never leave me!”", "그는 자기가 플로렌스에게 \"오 플로이, 나 집에 데려다줘, 그리고 날 절대 떠나지 마!\" 하고 자주 말했는지조차 기억할 수 없었다."], ["but he thought he had.", "그래도 그랬던 것 같다고 생각했다."], ["He fancied sometimes he had heard himself repeating, “Take me home, Floy!", "때때로 그는 자기가 \"나 집에 데려다줘, 플로이! 집에 데려다줘!\""], ["take me home!”", "하고 되풀이하는 소리를 들었던 것만 같았다."]], [["But he could remember, when he got home, and was carried up the well-remembered stairs, that there had been the rumbling of a coach for many hours together, while he lay upon the seat, with Florence still beside him, and old Mrs Pipchin sitting opposite.", "그러나 집에 돌아와 그 낯익은 계단을 따라 안겨 올라갈 때, 자기가 마차 좌석에 누워 있는 동안—여전히 플로렌스가 곁에 있었고, 늙은 핍친 부인이 맞은편에 앉아 있는 채로—여러 시간을 잇따라 마차가 덜컹거렸던 일은 기억할 수 있었다."], ["He remembered his old bed too, when they laid him down in it: his aunt, Miss Tox, and Susan: but there was something else, and recent too, that still perplexed him.", "사람들이 자기를 눕혀 준 그 낡은 침대도, 자기 고모와 톡스 양과 수전도 기억났다. 그러나 또 무언가가, 그것도 최근의 무언가가 여전히 그를 어리둥절하게 했다."]], [["“I want to speak to Florence, if you please,”", "\"플로렌스하고 이야기하고 싶어요, 괜찮다면요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“To Florence by herself, for a moment!”", "\"플로렌스 혼자만요, 잠깐 동안요!\""]], [["She bent down over him, and the others stood away.", "그녀가 그의 위로 몸을 굽혔고, 다른 사람들은 물러섰다."]], [["“Floy, my pet, wasn’t that Papa in the hall, when they brought me from the coach?”", "\"플로이, 우리 귀염둥이, 마차에서 날 데려올 때 현관에 있던 게 아빠였지?\""]], [["“Yes, dear.”", "\"응, 그래.\""]], [["“He didn’t cry, and go into his room, Floy, did he, when he saw me coming in?”", "\"내가 들어오는 걸 보고, 울면서 자기 방으로 들어가시진 않았지, 플로이, 그렇지?\""]], [["Florence shook her head, and pressed her lips against his cheek.", "플로렌스는 고개를 가로젓고는, 그의 뺨에 입술을 갖다 댔다."]], [["“I’m very glad he didn’t cry,”", "\"아빠가 울지 않으셔서 정말 다행이야.\""], ["said little Paul.", "어린 폴이 말했다."], ["“I thought he did.", "\"난 우신 줄 알았어."], ["Don’t tell them that I asked.”", "내가 물어봤다고 그들한테 말하지 마.\""]], [["CHAPTER XV. Amazing Artfulness of Captain Cuttle, and a new Pursuit for Walter Gay\nWalter could not, for several days, decide what to do in the Barbados business; and even cherished some faint hope that Mr Dombey might not have meant what he had said, or that he might change his mind, and tell him he was not to go.", "월터는 며칠 동안이나 바베이도스 건을 두고 어떻게 해야 할지 마음을 정하지 못했다."], ["But as nothing occurred to give this idea (which was sufficiently improbable in itself) any touch of confirmation, and as time was slipping by, and he had none to lose, he felt that he must act, without hesitating any longer.", "심지어 돔비 씨가 한 말이 진심이 아니었거나, 아니면 마음을 바꾸어 가지 않아도 된다고 말해 줄지 모른다는 가냘픈 희망까지 품었다. 그러나 이 생각에(그 자체로도 충분히 가망 없는 것이었지만) 조금이라도 확신을 더해 줄 어떤 일도 일어나지 않았고, 시간은 흘러가는데 그에게는 허비할 시간이 없었으므로, 그는 더는 망설이지 말고 행동에 옮겨야 한다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Walter’s chief difficulty was, how to break the change in his affairs to Uncle Sol, to whom he was sensible it would be a terrible blow.", "월터의 가장 큰 난관은, 처지의 변화를 솔 삼촌에게 어떻게 알리느냐 하는 것이었다. 그는 그것이 삼촌에게 끔찍한 타격이 되리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["He had the greater difficulty in dashing Uncle Sol’s spirits with such an astounding piece of intelligence, because they had lately recovered very much, and the old man had become so cheerful, that the little back parlour was itself again.", "이 놀라운 소식으로 솔 삼촌의 기운을 꺾는 일이 더더욱 어려웠던 까닭은, 최근 들어 삼촌의 기운이 무척 회복되어 그 노인이 어찌나 명랑해졌던지 그 작은 뒤쪽 응접실이 예전 모습을 되찾았기 때문이었다."], ["Uncle Sol had paid the first appointed portion of the debt to Mr Dombey, and was hopeful of working his way through the rest; and to cast him down afresh, when he had sprung up so manfully from his troubles, was a very distressing necessity.", "솔 삼촌은 돔비 씨에게 진 빚의 첫 약정분을 갚았고, 나머지도 차차 헤쳐 나가리라는 희망에 부풀어 있었다. 그렇게 의연하게 곤경에서 떨쳐 일어선 그를 다시 주저앉히는 일은, 참으로 가슴 아픈 필연이었다."]], [["Yet it would never do to run away from him.", "그렇다고 그를 두고 도망쳐 버릴 수는 없는 노릇이었다."], ["He must know of it beforehand; and how to tell him was the point.", "삼촌은 미리 알고 있어야 했다. 다만 그것을 어떻게 말하느냐가 문제였다."], ["As to the question of going or not going, Walter did not consider that he had any power of choice in the matter.", "가느냐 마느냐 하는 문제에 대해서는, 월터는 자기에게 선택권이 있다고 여기지 않았다."], ["Mr Dombey had truly told him that he was young, and that his Uncle’s circumstances were not good; and Mr Dombey had plainly expressed, in the glance with which he had accompanied that reminder, that if he declined to go he might stay at home if he chose, but not in his counting-house.", "돔비 씨는 그가 젊고 삼촌의 형편이 좋지 못하다고 사실대로 말했다. 그리고 그 일깨움에 곁들인 눈빛으로, 만일 가기를 거절한다면 원한다면 집에 머물러도 좋으나 자기 회계 사무소에는 있을 수 없다고 분명히 표현했다."], ["His Uncle and he lay under a great obligation to Mr Dombey, which was of Walter’s own soliciting.", "삼촌과 그는 돔비 씨에게 큰 은혜를 입고 있었고, 그것은 월터 자신이 청해서 진 빚이었다."], ["He might have begun in secret to despair of ever winning that gentleman’s favour, and might have thought that he was now and then disposed to put a slight upon him, which was hardly just.", "그는 그 신사의 호의를 얻는 일을 마음속으로 단념하기 시작했을지도 모르고, 또 그 신사가 이따금 자기를 가볍게 여기려 든다고 생각했을지도 모르나, 그것은 도무지 공정하다고 할 수 없는 생각이었다."], ["But what would have been duty without that, was still duty with it—or Walter thought so—and duty must be done.", "그러나 그것이 없었더라도 의무였을 일은, 그것이 있어도 여전히 의무였고—적어도 월터는 그렇게 생각했다—의무란 다해야 하는 것이었다."]], [["When Mr Dombey had looked at him, and told him he was young, and that his Uncle’s circumstances were not good, there had been an expression of disdain in his face; a contemptuous and disparaging assumption that he would be quite content to live idly on a reduced old man, which stung the boy’s generous soul.", "돔비 씨가 그를 바라보며, 그가 젊고 삼촌의 형편이 좋지 못하다고 말했을 때, 그 얼굴에는 경멸의 빛이 어려 있었다. 그가 형편 줄어든 노인에게 얹혀 빈둥대며 사는 것을 아주 흡족해하리라는, 멸시하고 깔보는 듯한 단정이었으니, 그것이 소년의 너그러운 영혼을 찔렀다."], ["Determined to assure Mr Dombey, in so far as it was possible to give him the assurance without expressing it in words, that indeed he mistook his nature, Walter had been anxious to show even more cheerfulness and activity after the West Indian interview than he had shown before: if that were possible, in one of his quick and zealous disposition.", "자기 본성을 오해하고 있는 것이라고 돔비 씨에게, 말로 표현하지 않고도 가능한 한 확신시키려 마음먹은 월터는, 서인도 면담이 있은 뒤로는 그전보다 한층 더 명랑하고 활기차게 보이려 애썼다. 그처럼 재빠르고 열성적인 기질을 지닌 사람에게 그게 가능한 일이라면 말이다."], ["He was too young and inexperienced to think, that possibly this very quality in him was not agreeable to Mr Dombey, and that it was no stepping-stone to his good opinion to be elastic and hopeful of pleasing under the shadow of his powerful displeasure, whether it were right or wrong.", "그는 너무 어리고 미숙해서, 어쩌면 바로 그런 자질이 돔비 씨에게는 마음에 들지 않을 수도 있다는 것, 그리고 그 강력한 불쾌함의 그늘 아래서—그것이 옳든 그르든—여전히 탄력 있고 비위를 맞추려는 희망에 부푼 모습을 보이는 것이 그의 호감을 사는 디딤돌이 되기는커녕 오히려 그렇지 못하다는 것을 미처 생각하지 못했다."], ["But it may have been—it may have been—that the great man thought himself defied in this new exposition of an honest spirit, and purposed to bring it down.", "그러나 어쩌면—어쩌면—그 위대한 인물은 정직한 기개를 새로이 드러내는 이런 태도에서 자기가 도전받고 있다고 여기고, 그것을 꺾어 놓으려 마음먹었는지도 모른다."]], [["“Well!", "\"좋아!"], ["at last and at least, Uncle Sol must be told,” thought Walter, with a sigh.", "어쨌거나 마침내, 적어도 솔 삼촌에게는 말씀드려야 해.\""], ["And as Walter was apprehensive that his voice might perhaps quaver a little, and that his countenance might not be quite as hopeful as he could wish it to be, if he told the old man himself, and saw the first effects of his communication on his wrinkled face, he resolved to avail himself of the services of that powerful mediator, Captain Cuttle.", "월터는 한숨을 쉬며 생각했다. 그리고 만일 자기가 직접 노인에게 말하고 그 주름진 얼굴에 처음으로 떠오르는 반응을 보게 된다면, 어쩌면 목소리가 조금 떨릴지도 모르고 표정도 바라는 만큼 희망차 보이지는 않으리라는 것을 염려하여, 그는 그 막강한 중재자 커틀 선장의 도움을 빌리기로 마음먹었다."], ["Sunday coming round, he set off therefore, after breakfast, once more to beat up Captain Cuttle’s quarters.", "그래서 일요일이 돌아오자, 아침을 먹고 다시 한번 커틀 선장의 거처를 찾아 나섰다."]], [["It was not unpleasant to remember, on the way thither, that Mrs MacStinger resorted to a great distance every Sunday morning, to attend the ministry of the Reverend Melchisedech Howler, who, having been one day discharged from the West India Docks on a false suspicion (got up expressly against him by the general enemy) of screwing gimlets into puncheons, and applying his lips to the orifice, had announced the destruction of the world for that day two years, at ten in the morning, and opened a front parlour for the reception of ladies and gentlemen of the Ranting persuasion, upon whom, on the first occasion of their assemblage, the admonitions of the Reverend Melchisedech had produced so powerful an effect, that, in their rapturous performance of a sacred jig, which closed the service, the whole flock broke through into a kitchen below, and disabled a mangle belonging to one of the fold.", "그곳으로 가는 길에, 맥스팅거 부인이 일요일 아침마다 멀리까지 가서 멜기세덱 하울러 목사의 예배에 참석한다는 것을 떠올리니 나쁘지 않은 기분이었다. 이 목사는 어느 날 서인도 부두에서, 송곳으로 큰 술통에 구멍을 뚫고 그 구멍에 입을 대고 마셨다는 거짓 혐의(전체의 적이 일부러 그를 겨냥해 꾸며 낸 것이었다)로 해고당한 뒤, 이 년 뒤 바로 그날 오전 열 시에 세상이 멸망하리라고 선포하고는, 광신파 신사 숙녀들을 맞이하기 위해 앞쪽 응접실 하나를 열었다. 그들이 처음 모인 자리에서 멜기세덱 목사의 훈계가 어찌나 강력한 효과를 냈던지, 예배를 마무리하는 신성한 춤을 황홀하게 추다가 신도들 전체가 아래층 부엌으로 와르르 무너져 내려 신도 가운데 한 사람의 빨래 짜는 기계를 망가뜨려 놓았다."]], [["This the Captain, in a moment of uncommon conviviality, had confided to Walter and his Uncle, between the repetitions of lovely Peg, on the night when Brogley the broker was paid out.", "이 이야기를 선장은 어느 날 보기 드물게 흥겨운 기분에 젖어, 중개인 브로글리에게 돈을 다 갚던 날 밤, 아름다운 페그를 거듭 부르는 틈틈이 월터와 그의 삼촌에게 털어놓은 것이었다."], ["The Captain himself was punctual in his attendance at a church in his own neighbourhood, which hoisted the Union Jack every Sunday morning; and where he was good enough—the lawful beadle being infirm—to keep an eye upon the boys, over whom he exercised great power, in virtue of his mysterious hook.", "선장 자신은 동네에 있는 어느 교회에 어김없이 출석했는데, 그 교회는 일요일 아침마다 유니언 잭을 내걸었고, 그곳에서 그는—적법한 교회지기가 병약했으므로—소년들을 친절하게도 감독해 주었으니, 그 신비로운 갈고리의 위력으로 아이들에게 막강한 권세를 휘둘렀다."], ["Knowing the regularity of the Captain’s habits, Walter made all the haste he could, that he might anticipate his going out; and he made such good speed, that he had the pleasure, on turning into Brig Place, to behold the broad blue coat and waistcoat hanging out of the Captain’s open window, to air in the sun.", "선장의 규칙적인 습관을 아는 월터는, 그가 외출하기 전에 닿으려고 서둘렀고, 어찌나 발걸음을 재게 놀렸던지, 브리그 플레이스로 접어들었을 때 선장의 활짝 열린 창문 밖에 넓적한 푸른 외투와 조끼가 햇볕에 바람 쐬려고 내걸려 있는 것을 보는 기쁨을 누렸다."]], [["It appeared incredible that the coat and waistcoat could be seen by mortal eyes without the Captain; but he certainly was not in them, otherwise his legs—the houses in Brig Place not being lofty—would have obstructed the street door, which was perfectly clear.", "선장 없이 그 외투와 조끼만 사람의 눈에 보인다는 것은 도무지 믿기지 않는 일이었다. 그러나 그는 분명 그 옷 속에 들어 있지 않았으니, 만일 들어 있었다면 그의 두 다리가—브리그 플레이스의 집들은 높지 않았으므로—현관문을 가로막았을 텐데, 문은 말끔히 트여 있었다."], ["Quite wondering at this discovery, Walter gave a single knock.", "이 발견에 못내 의아해하며 월터는 한 번 노크했다."]], [["“Stinger,”", "\"스팅거.\""], ["he distinctly heard the Captain say, up in his room, as if that were no business of his.", "월터는 위층 방에서 선장이 마치 자기와는 상관없다는 듯 말하는 소리를 또렷이 들었다."], ["Therefore Walter gave two knocks.", "그래서 월터는 두 번 노크했다."]], [["“Cuttle,”", "\"커틀.\""], ["he heard the Captain say upon that; and immediately afterwards the Captain, in his clean shirt and braces, with his neckerchief hanging loosely round his throat like a coil of rope, and his glazed hat on, appeared at the window, leaning out over the broad blue coat and waistcoat.", "이어 선장이 그렇게 말하는 소리가 들렸다. 그러더니 곧 선장이, 깨끗한 셔츠에 멜빵을 메고, 밧줄 뭉치처럼 목에 헐겁게 감긴 목도리를 두르고, 번들거리는 모자를 쓴 채 창가에 나타나, 넓적한 푸른 외투와 조끼 너머로 몸을 내밀었다."]], [["“Wal”r!”", "\"월터!\""], ["cried the Captain, looking down upon him in amazement.", "선장이 놀란 얼굴로 그를 내려다보며 외쳤다."]], [["“Ay, ay, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"네, 네, 커틀 선장님.\""], ["returned Walter, “only me”", "월터가 대답했다. \"저뿐이에요.\""]], [["“What’s the matter, my lad?”", "\"무슨 일이냐, 얘야?\""], ["inquired the Captain, with great concern.", "선장이 몹시 걱정스럽게 물었다."], ["“Gills an’t been and sprung nothing again?”", "\"길스 영감님이 또 뭔가 일을 당하신 건 아니지?\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니요, 아니요.\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."], ["“My Uncle’s all right, Captain Cuttle.”", "\"삼촌은 아무 일 없으세요, 커틀 선장님.\""]], [["The Captain expressed his gratification, and said he would come down below and open the door, which he did.", "선장은 흡족함을 표하고는, 아래로 내려가 문을 열어 주겠다고 했고, 정말 그렇게 했다."]], [["“Though you’re early, Wal”r,”", "\"그래도 이른 시간이구나, 월터.\""], ["said the Captain, eyeing him still doubtfully, when they got upstairs:", "위층으로 올라갔을 때, 선장이 여전히 미심쩍은 눈으로 그를 살피며 말했다."]], [["“Why, the fact is, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"실은요, 커틀 선장님,\""], ["said Walter, sitting down, “I was afraid you would have gone out, and I want to benefit by your friendly counsel.”", "월터가 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"선장님이 외출하셨을까 봐 걱정돼서요, 선장님의 다정한 조언으로 도움을 좀 받고 싶어서요.\""]], [["“So you shall,”", "\"그럼 그래야지.\""], ["said the Captain; “what’ll you take?”", "선장이 말했다. \"뭘 들겠느냐?\""]], [["“I want to take your opinion, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"선장님 의견을 듣고 싶다는 거예요, 커틀 선장님.\""], ["returned Walter, smiling.", "월터가 미소 지으며 대답했다."], ["“That’s the only thing for me.”", "\"저한테 필요한 건 그것뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Come on then,”", "\"그럼 어디 말해 보거라.\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“With a will, my lad!”", "\"마음껏, 얘야!\""]], [["Walter related to him what had happened; and the difficulty in which he felt respecting his Uncle, and the relief it would be to him if Captain Cuttle, in his kindness, would help him to smooth it away; Captain Cuttle’s infinite consternation and astonishment at the prospect unfolded to him, gradually swallowing that gentleman up, until it left his face quite vacant, and the suit of blue, the glazed hat, and the hook, apparently without an owner.", "월터는 그에게 무슨 일이 있었는지를 들려주었다. 삼촌 문제로 자기가 느끼는 어려움과, 커틀 선장이 친절하게도 그 어려움을 누그러뜨리도록 도와준다면 얼마나 마음이 놓일지를 이야기했다. 자기 앞에 펼쳐진 그 전망에 대한 커틀 선장의 한없는 경악과 놀라움이 차츰 그 신사를 통째로 삼켜 버려서, 마침내 그의 얼굴은 멍하니 텅 비고, 푸른 옷과 번들거리는 모자와 갈고리는 임자가 없는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["“You see, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"보세요, 커틀 선장님,\""], ["pursued Walter, “for myself, I am young, as Mr Dombey said, and not to be considered.", "월터가 말을 이었다. \"저 자신으로 말하자면, 돔비 씨가 말씀하신 대로, 저는 젊고 따질 만한 사람도 못 돼요."], ["I am to fight my way through the world, I know; but there are two points I was thinking, as I came along, that I should be very particular about, in respect to my Uncle.", "세상을 헤치고 나아가야 한다는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 오는 길에 생각해 보니, 삼촌에 관해서는 제가 꼭 각별히 신경 써야 할 점이 두 가지가 있었어요."], ["I don’t mean to say that I deserve to be the pride and delight of his life—you believe me, I know—but I am.", "제가 삼촌 인생의 자랑이고 기쁨이 될 만하다는 뜻은 아니에요—제 말 믿으시죠, 알아요—하지만 저는 그래요."], ["Now, don’t you think I am?”", "자, 선장님도 그렇게 생각하지 않으세요?\""]], [["The Captain seemed to make an endeavour to rise from the depths of his astonishment, and get back to his face; but the effort being ineffectual, the glazed hat merely nodded with a mute, unutterable meaning.", "선장은 경악의 심연에서 솟아올라 자기 얼굴로 돌아오려고 애쓰는 듯했다. 그러나 그 노력이 헛수고로 끝나자, 번들거리는 모자만이 말없이, 형언할 수 없는 의미를 담아 끄덕였다."]], [["“If I live and have my health,”", "\"제가 살아 있고 건강하다면,\""], ["said Walter, “and I am not afraid of that, still, when I leave England I can hardly hope to see my Uncle again.", "월터가 말했다. \"그건 걱정하지 않지만, 그래도 영국을 떠나면 삼촌을 다시 뵐 수 있으리라곤 거의 기대하기 어려워요."], ["He is old, Captain Cuttle; and besides, his life is a life of custom—”", "삼촌은 늙으셨어요, 커틀 선장님. 게다가 삼촌의 삶이라는 건 단골손님의 삶이라서—\""]], [["“Steady, Wal”r!", "\"잠깐, 월터!"], ["Of a want of custom?”", "단골손님이 없는 삶 말이냐?\""], ["said the Captain, suddenly reappearing.", "선장이 불쑥 다시 나타나며 말했다."]], [["“Too true,”", "\"너무 맞는 말씀이에요.\""], ["returned Walter, shaking his head: “but I meant a life of habit, Captain Cuttle—that sort of custom.", "월터가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"하지만 제 말은 습관의 삶이라는 거예요, 커틀 선장님—그런 종류의 익숙함 말이에요."], ["And if (as you very truly said, I am sure) he would have died the sooner for the loss of the stock, and all those objects to which he has been accustomed for so many years, don’t you think he might die a little sooner for the loss of—”", "그리고 (선장님이 참으로 옳게 말씀하셨듯이, 정말 그래요) 가게며 그 오랜 세월 익숙해진 그 모든 물건을 잃으면 삼촌이 더 일찍 돌아가실 거라면, 또 잃는다면 조금 더 일찍 돌아가시지 않을까요—\""]], [["“Of his Nevy,”", "\"조카를 말이지.\""], ["interposed the Captain.", "선장이 끼어들었다."], ["“Right!”", "\"맞아!\""]], [["“Well then,”", "\"자 그럼,\" 월터가 명랑하게 말하려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["said Walter, trying to speak gaily, “we must do our best to make him believe that the separation is but a temporary one, after all; but as I know better, or dread that I know better, Captain Cuttle, and as I have so many reasons for regarding him with affection, and duty, and honour, I am afraid I should make but a very poor hand at that, if I tried to persuade him of it.", "\"어쨌든 우리는 이 이별이 그저 한때의 것이라고 삼촌이 믿도록 최선을 다해야 해요. 하지만 저는 더 잘 알고 있고, 아니 더 잘 알고 있을까 봐 두려운 처지라서요, 커틀 선장님."], ["That’s my great reason for wishing you to break it out to him; and that’s the first point.”", "또 제가 삼촌을 애정과 의무와 존경의 마음으로 대할 이유가 너무도 많아서, 만일 제가 그 일로 삼촌을 설득하려 든다면 아마 아주 서툴게 굴고 말 거예요. 그게 선장님이 삼촌에게 그 소식을 전해 주셨으면 하는 가장 큰 이유고요, 그게 첫 번째 점이에요.\""]], [["“Keep her off a point or so!”", "\"키를 한두 포인트 비껴 두게나!\""], ["observed the Captain, in a contemplative voice.", "선장이 사색에 잠긴 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“What did you say, Captain Cuttle?”", "\"뭐라고 하셨어요, 커틀 선장님?\""], ["inquired Walter.", "월터가 물었다."]], [["“Stand by!”", "\"대기하라!\""], ["returned the Captain, thoughtfully.", "선장이 깊이 생각하며 대답했다."]], [["Walter paused to ascertain if the Captain had any particular information to add to this, but as he said no more, went on.", "월터는 여기에 선장이 덧붙일 특별한 정보가 있는지 알아보려고 잠시 멈추었으나, 더는 말이 없자 말을 이었다."]], [["“Now, the second point, Captain Cuttle.", "\"이제 두 번째 점이에요, 커틀 선장님."], ["I am sorry to say, I am not a favourite with Mr Dombey.", "유감스럽지만, 저는 돔비 씨의 마음에 드는 사람이 아니에요. 저는 늘 최선을 다하려고 애써 왔고, 늘 그렇게 했어요."], ["I have always tried to do my best, and I have always done it; but he does not like me.", "하지만 그분은 저를 좋아하지 않으세요. 어쩌면 그분은 자기가 좋아하고 싫어하는 걸 어쩌지 못하시는지도 모르죠."], ["He can’t help his likings and dislikings, perhaps.", "그건 아무 말 않겠어요. 다만 제가 확실히 아는 건, 그분이 저를 좋아하지 않는다는 거예요."], ["I say nothing of that.", "그분은 이 자리를 좋은 자리라고 해서 보내시는 게 아니에요."], ["I only say that I am certain he does not like me.", "실제보다 더 나은 것처럼 꾸며 말하기를 거절하시고요."], ["He does not send me to this post as a good one; he disclaims to represent it as being better than it is; and I doubt very much if it will ever lead me to advancement in the House—whether it does not, on the contrary, dispose of me for ever, and put me out of the way.", "그리고 이 자리가 과연 저를 회사에서 출세시켜 줄지—오히려 정반대로, 저를 영영 처치해 버리고 비켜나게 하려는 건 아닐지, 저는 무척 의심스러워요. 그런데 이걸 삼촌에게는 한마디도 해서는 안 돼요, 커틀 선장님."], ["Now, we must say nothing of this to my Uncle, Captain Cuttle, but must make it out to be as favourable and promising as we can; and when I tell you what it really is, I only do so, that in case any means should ever arise of lending me a hand, so far off, I may have one friend at home who knows my real situation.", "우리는 이걸 될 수 있는 한 좋고 전도유망한 일로 꾸며 내야 해요. 그리고 제가 선장님께 그게 실제로 어떤 자리인지를 말씀드리는 건, 다만 혹시라도 저 멀리 떨어진 제게 손을 보태 줄 무슨 방도가 생길 경우에 대비해서, 제 실제 처지를 아는 친구 한 사람이 고향에 있게 하려는 것뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Wal”r, my boy,”", "\"월터, 얘야,\" 선장이 대답했다."], ["replied the Captain, “in the Proverbs of Solomon you will find the following words, ‘May we never want a friend in need, nor a bottle to give him!’ When found, make a note of.”", "\"솔로몬의 잠언에는 이런 말이 있단다, '곤경에 빠진 벗이 없게 하시고, 그에게 줄 술병도 떨어지지 않게 하소서!' 찾거든, 적어 두거라.\""]], [["Here the Captain stretched out his hand to Walter, with an air of downright good faith that spoke volumes; at the same time repeating (for he felt proud of the accuracy and pointed application of his quotation), “When found, make a note of.”", "여기서 선장은 더없이 진실한 태도로—그 한마디 한마디가 천 마디의 말을 담고 있었다—월터에게 손을 내밀고, 동시에 (자기 인용의 정확함과 적절한 적용에 자부심을 느끼는지라) 거듭 말했다. \"찾거든, 적어 두거라.\""]], [["“Captain Cuttle,”", "\"커틀 선장님,\""], ["said Walter, taking the immense fist extended to him by the Captain in both his hands, which it completely filled, next to my Uncle Sol, I love you.", "월터가 선장이 내민 그 거대한 주먹을 두 손으로 받쳐 쥐며 말했다. 두 손으로 가득 찰 만큼 큰 주먹이었다."], ["There is no one on earth in whom I can more safely trust, I am sure.", "\"솔 삼촌 다음으로, 저는 선장님을 사랑해요. 이 세상에 선장님보다 더 안심하고 믿을 사람은 없다고 확신해요."], ["As to the mere going away, Captain Cuttle, I don’t care for that; why should I care for that!", "그저 떠나는 것 자체에 대해서는, 커틀 선장님, 저는 개의치 않아요. 왜 개의치 않겠어요!"], ["If I were free to seek my own fortune—if I were free to go as a common sailor—if I were free to venture on my own account to the farthest end of the world—I would gladly go!", "만일 제가 자유로이 제 운을 찾아 나설 수 있다면—자유로이 평범한 선원으로 떠날 수 있다면—자유로이 제 힘으로 세상 끝까지라도 모험을 떠날 수 있다면—저는 기꺼이 가겠어요! 몇 해 전이었더라도 기꺼이 떠나, 무슨 일이 닥치든 운에 맡겼을 거예요."], ["I would have gladly gone, years ago, and taken my chance of what might come of it.", "하지만 그건 삼촌의 뜻에 어긋났고, 삼촌이 저를 위해 세워 둔 계획에도 어긋났어요."], ["But it was against my Uncle’s wishes, and against the plans he had formed for me; and there was an end of that.", "그래서 그걸로 끝이었죠. 하지만 제가 느끼는 건요, 커틀 선장님, 우리가 줄곧 조금 잘못 생각해 왔다는 거예요."], ["But what I feel, Captain Cuttle, is that we have been a little mistaken all along, and that, so far as any improvement in my prospects is concerned, I am no better off now than I was when I first entered Dombey’s House—perhaps a little worse, for the House may have been kindly inclined towards me then, and it certainly is not now.”", "제 앞날이 나아지는 일에 관한 한, 저는 돔비 회사에 처음 들어왔을 때보다 지금 조금도 더 나아진 게 없어요—어쩌면 조금 더 나빠졌는지도 몰라요. 그때는 회사가 저를 호의적으로 여겼을지도 모르지만, 지금은 분명 그렇지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“Turn again, Whittington,”", "\"되돌아가거라, 휘팅턴.\""], ["muttered the disconsolate Captain, after looking at Walter for some time.", "한참 동안 월터를 바라보던 선장이 서글프게 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Ay,”", "\"네,\""], ["replied Walter, laughing, “and turn a great many times, too, Captain Cuttle, I’m afraid, before such fortune as his ever turns up again.", "월터가 웃으며 대답했다. \"그것도 아주 여러 번 되돌아가야겠죠, 커틀 선장님."], ["Not that I complain,”", "그 사람의 운 같은 게 또다시 돌아오기 전까지는요."], ["he added, in his lively, animated, energetic way.", "그렇다고 불평하는 건 아니에요.\" 그는 특유의 활발하고 생기 넘치고 힘찬 태도로 덧붙였다."], ["“I have nothing to complain of.", "\"불평할 거리는 하나도 없어요."], ["I am provided for.", "저는 먹고살 길이 있어요."], ["I can live.", "살아갈 수 있고요."], ["When I leave my Uncle, I leave him to you; and I can leave him to no one better, Captain Cuttle.", "제가 삼촌을 떠나면, 삼촌을 선장님께 맡기는 거예요. 선장님보다 더 나은 분에게는 맡길 수가 없어요, 커틀 선장님."], ["I haven’t told you all this because I despair, not I; it’s to convince you that I can’t pick and choose in Dombey’s House, and that where I am sent, there I must go, and what I am offered, that I must take.", "제가 이 모든 걸 말씀드린 건 절망해서가 아니에요, 저는 절망 같은 거 안 해요. 다만 제가 돔비 회사에서 이것저것 골라잡을 처지가 못 된다는 걸, 보내는 곳이면 가야 하고 주어지는 것이면 받아야 한다는 걸 선장님께 납득시키려는 거예요."], ["It’s better for my Uncle that I should be sent away; for Mr Dombey is a valuable friend to him, as he proved himself, you know when, Captain Cuttle; and I am persuaded he won’t be less valuable when he hasn’t me there, every day, to awaken his dislike.", "제가 멀리 떠나는 게 삼촌에게는 더 나아요. 돔비 씨는 삼촌에게 귀중한 친구거든요, 언제 그걸 몸소 보여 주셨는지 선장님도 아시잖아요, 커틀 선장님."], ["So hurrah for the West Indies, Captain Cuttle!", "그리고 날마다 그분의 미움을 일깨우는 제가 그곳에 없으면, 그분이 덜 귀중한 친구가 되지는 않으리라 저는 확신해요."], ["How does that tune go that the sailors sing?", "그러니 서인도제도 만세예요, 커틀 선장님! 선원들이 부르는 그 노래가 어떻게 되더라?"]], [["“For the Port of Barbados, Boys!", "\"바베이도스 항구를 향하여, 친구들아!"], ["Cheerily!", "유쾌하게!"], ["Leaving old England behind us, Boys!", "정든 영국을 뒤로하고, 친구들아!"], ["Cheerily!”", "유쾌하게!\""], ["Here the Captain roared in chorus— “Oh cheerily, cheerily!", "여기서 선장이 합창으로 우렁차게 받았다— \"오 유쾌하게, 유쾌하게!"], ["Oh cheer-i-ly!”", "오 유쾌하—게!\""]], [["The last line reaching the quick ears of an ardent skipper not quite sober, who lodged opposite, and who instantly sprung out of bed, threw up his window, and joined in, across the street, at the top of his voice, produced a fine effect.", "마지막 소절이, 길 건너편에 하숙하던, 영 말짱한 정신은 아닌 어느 혈기왕성한 선장의 예민한 귀에 가닿았다. 그는 즉시 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 창문을 들어 올리고는 길 건너로 목청껏 합세했으니, 멋들어진 효과를 자아냈다."], ["When it was impossible to sustain the concluding note any longer, the skipper bellowed forth a terrific “ahoy!”", "마지막 음을 더는 끌 수 없게 되자, 그 선장은 무시무시한 \"어이!\""], ["intended in part as a friendly greeting, and in part to show that he was not at all breathed.", "소리를 내질렀는데, 한편으로는 친근한 인사로, 또 한편으로는 자기가 전혀 숨차지 않다는 것을 보이려는 뜻이었다."], ["That done, he shut down his window, and went to bed again.", "그렇게 하고 나서 그는 창문을 내리고 다시 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["“And now, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"자 이제, 커틀 선장님,\""], ["said Walter, handing him the blue coat and waistcoat, and bustling very much, “if you’ll come and break the news to Uncle Sol (which he ought to have known, days upon days ago, by rights), I’ll leave you at the door, you know, and walk about until the afternoon.”", "월터가 푸른 외투와 조끼를 건네주며 부산을 떨었다. \"솔 삼촌에게 가서 소식을 전해 주신다면(원래대로라면 며칠이고 전에 알고 계셨어야 할 일이지만요), 문 앞까지만 모셔다 드리고 저는 오후까지 여기저기 거닐다 올게요.\""]], [["The Captain, however, scarcely appeared to relish the commission, or to be by any means confident of his powers of executing it.", "그러나 선장은 그 임무를 도무지 달가워하는 기색이 아니었고, 그것을 해낼 자기 능력에 조금도 자신이 있어 보이지 않았다."], ["He had arranged the future life and adventures of Walter so very differently, and so entirely to his own satisfaction; he had felicitated himself so often on the sagacity and foresight displayed in that arrangement, and had found it so complete and perfect in all its parts; that to suffer it to go to pieces all at once, and even to assist in breaking it up, required a great effort of his resolution.", "그는 월터의 앞날과 모험을 그것과는 아주 다르게, 그리고 자기 마음에 쏙 들도록 짜 두었던 것이다. 그 구상에 드러난 명민함과 선견지명을 두고 그토록 여러 번 흐뭇해했고, 그것이 구석구석 빈틈없이 완벽하다고 여겨 왔던 터라, 그것이 한꺼번에 산산조각 나도록 내버려 두는 것은, 더구나 그것을 부수는 일에 거드는 것은, 대단한 결단의 노력이 필요했다."], ["The Captain, too, found it difficult to unload his old ideas upon the subject, and to take a perfectly new cargo on board, with that rapidity which the circumstances required, or without jumbling and confounding the two.", "게다가 선장은, 그 일에 대한 묵은 생각을 부려 놓고 정황이 요구하는 그 속도로 전혀 새로운 짐을 실어 들이는 것이, 그것도 둘을 뒤죽박죽 섞고 헷갈리지 않으면서 그렇게 하는 것이 영 어려웠다. 그리하여 그는 월터의 기분에 보조를 맞출 수 있을 만한 그 어떤 성급함과도 거리가 멀게, 외투와 조끼를 걸치기는커녕, 당장은 그 옷들을 도무지 몸에 두르려 들지 않았다."], ["Consequently, instead of putting on his coat and waistcoat with anything like the impetuosity that could alone have kept pace with Walter’s mood, he declined to invest himself with those garments at all at present; and informed Walter that on such a serious matter, he must be allowed to “bite his nails a bit”.", "그러고는 이렇게 중대한 문제에는 \"손톱을 좀 물어뜯도록\" 허락받아야겠다고 월터에게 알렸다."]], [["“It’s an old habit of mine, Wal”r,”", "\"이건 내 오랜 버릇이란다, 월터.\""], ["said the Captain, “any time these fifty year.", "선장이 말했다. \"지난 오십 년 동안 줄곧 그랬지."], ["When you see Ned Cuttle bite his nails, Wal”r, then you may know that Ned Cuttle’s aground.”", "네드 커틀이 손톱 물어뜯는 걸 보거든, 월터, 그건 네드 커틀이 좌초했다는 걸 알면 된다.\""]], [["Thereupon the Captain put his iron hook between his teeth, as if it were a hand; and with an air of wisdom and profundity that was the very concentration and sublimation of all philosophical reflection and grave inquiry, applied himself to the consideration of the subject in its various branches.", "그러면서 선장은 마치 그것이 손이기라도 한 양 쇠갈고리를 이로 물고는, 온갖 철학적 성찰과 진지한 탐구의 정수이자 승화라 할 만한 지혜롭고 심오한 태도로, 그 문제를 갈래갈래 따져 보는 데 골몰했다."]], [["“There’s a friend of mine,”", "\"내 친구가 하나 있는데,\""], ["murmured the Captain, in an absent manner, “but he’s at present coasting round to Whitby, that would deliver such an opinion on this subject, or any other that could be named, as would give Parliament six and beat ’em.", "선장이 멍하니 중얼거렸다. \"지금은 휘트비 쪽으로 연안을 돌고 있다만, 이 문제든 그 밖에 무슨 문제든 의견을 내놓으라면, 의회를 한 수 접게 하고도 이길 만한 친구야."], ["Been knocked overboard, that man,”", "그 사람은 바다에 두 번이나 떨어졌었지,\""], ["said the Captain, “twice, and none the worse for it.", "선장이 말했다. \"그런데도 멀쩡했어."], ["Was beat in his apprenticeship, for three weeks (off and on), about the head with a ring-bolt.", "견습 시절엔 세 주 동안(이따금) 머리를 고리 볼트로 얻어맞기도 했지."], ["And yet a clearer-minded man don’t walk.”", "그런데도 그 친구만큼 머릿속이 맑은 사람은 또 없다니까.\""]], [["In spite of his respect for Captain Cuttle, Walter could not help inwardly rejoicing at the absence of this sage, and devoutly hoping that his limpid intellect might not be brought to bear on his difficulties until they were quite settled.", "커틀 선장을 존경하는 마음에도 불구하고, 월터는 이 현자가 그 자리에 없는 것을 속으로 기뻐하지 않을 수 없었고, 자기 난관이 완전히 정리될 때까지는 그 맑디맑은 지성이 거기에 동원되지 않기를 간절히 바랐다."]], [["“If you was to take and show that man the buoy at the Nore,”", "\"자네가 만일 그 사람을 데려다 노어의 부표를 보여 주고,\""], ["said Captain Cuttle in the same tone, “and ask him his opinion of it, Wal”r, he’d give you an opinion that was no more like that buoy than your Uncle’s buttons are.", "커틀 선장이 같은 어조로 말했다. \"그것에 대한 의견을 물으면 말이지, 월터, 그 사람은 자네 삼촌의 단추가 그 부표를 안 닮은 만큼이나 그 부표하고 영 딴판인 의견을 내놓을 거야."], ["There ain’t a man that walks—certainly not on two legs—that can come near him.", "두 다리로 걸어 다니는 사람치고—분명 두 다리로 걸어 다니는 사람치고—그 친구 발끝에라도 미칠 자가 없어."], ["Not near him!”", "발끝에도 못 미친다고!\""]], [["“What’s his name, Captain Cuttle?”", "\"그분 이름이 뭐예요, 커틀 선장님?\""], ["inquired Walter, determined to be interested in the Captain’s friend.", "선장의 친구에게 관심을 가져 보기로 마음먹은 월터가 물었다."]], [["“His name’s Bunsby,”", "\"그 사람 이름은 번스비야.\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“But Lord, it might be anything for the matter of that, with such a mind as his!”", "\"하지만 원, 그만한 머리를 가진 사람이라면 이름 따위야 뭔들 어떻겠느냐!\""]], [["The exact idea which the Captain attached to this concluding piece of praise, he did not further elucidate; neither did Walter seek to draw it forth.", "이 마무리 칭찬에 선장이 정확히 어떤 뜻을 담았는지, 그는 더는 설명하지 않았다. 월터도 그것을 끌어내려 들지 않았다."], ["For on his beginning to review, with the vivacity natural to himself and to his situation, the leading points in his own affairs, he soon discovered that the Captain had relapsed into his former profound state of mind; and that while he eyed him steadfastly from beneath his bushy eyebrows, he evidently neither saw nor heard him, but remained immersed in cogitation.", "자기 자신과 자기 처지에 어울리는 그 활기로 제 일의 핵심을 다시 짚어 보기 시작했을 때, 그는 이내 선장이 전처럼 깊은 사색의 상태로 되돌아가 버린 것을 알아챘기 때문이다. 그 덥수룩한 눈썹 아래로 자기를 빤히 바라보면서도, 선장은 그를 보지도 듣지도 못하는 게 분명했고, 그저 골똘한 생각에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["In fact, Captain Cuttle was labouring with such great designs, that far from being aground, he soon got off into the deepest of water, and could find no bottom to his penetration.", "사실 커틀 선장은 어찌나 큰 구상에 골몰했던지, 좌초하기는커녕 이내 가장 깊은 물속으로 떠나가 자기 통찰의 바닥을 도무지 짚을 수가 없었다."], ["By degrees it became perfectly plain to the Captain that there was some mistake here; that it was undoubtedly much more likely to be Walter’s mistake than his; that if there were really any West India scheme afoot, it was a very different one from what Walter, who was young and rash, supposed; and could only be some new device for making his fortune with unusual celerity.", "차츰 선장에게는, 여기에 무슨 착오가 있는 게 분명하다는 것, 그것은 자기보다는 십중팔구 월터의 착오일 거라는 것, 만일 정말로 무슨 서인도제도 계획이 진행 중이라면 그것은 젊고 성급한 월터가 짐작하는 것과는 전혀 다른 것이라는 것, 그리고 그것은 보기 드물게 빠른 속도로 그의 재산을 일구어 주려는 어떤 새로운 방안일 수밖에 없다는 것이 더없이 분명해졌다."], ["“Or if there should be any little hitch between ’em,”", "\"혹시 둘 사이에 무슨 자그마한 차질이라도 있다면,\""], ["thought the Captain, meaning between Walter and Mr Dombey, “it only wants a word in season from a friend of both parties, to set it right and smooth, and make all taut again.”", "월터와 돔비 씨 사이를 말하는 것이었는데, 선장은 생각했다. \"그저 양쪽 모두의 친구가 제때 한마디 거들어 주면, 바로잡고 매끄럽게 해서 죄다 다시 팽팽하게 만들 수 있지.\""], ["Captain Cuttle’s deduction from these considerations was, that as he already enjoyed the pleasure of knowing Mr Dombey, from having spent a very agreeable half-hour in his company at Brighton (on the morning when they borrowed the money); and that, as a couple of men of the world, who understood each other, and were mutually disposed to make things comfortable, could easily arrange any little difficulty of this sort, and come at the real facts; the friendly thing for him to do would be, without saying anything about it to Walter at present, just to step up to Mr Dombey’s house—say to the servant “Would ye be so good, my lad, as report Cap’en Cuttle here?”—meet Mr Dombey in a confidential spirit—hook him by the button-hole—talk it over—make it all right—and come away triumphant!", "이런 생각에서 선장이 끌어낸 결론은 이러했다. 자기는 이미 브라이턴에서 돔비 씨와 무척 유쾌한 반 시간을 함께 보내며(돈을 빌리던 날 아침이었다) 그를 아는 즐거움을 누렸으니, 또 서로를 이해하고 서로 일을 원만히 풀어 갈 마음이 있는 두 세상 물정 밝은 사내라면 이 따위 자그마한 어려움쯤은 쉽게 정리하고 진짜 사실에 이를 수 있으니, 자기가 할 우정 어린 일은—지금 당장 월터에게는 그것에 관해 한마디도 않고—그저 돔비 씨 댁으로 슬쩍 올라가—하인에게 \"여보게, 미안하지만 커틀 선장이 왔다고 전해 주겠나?\"라고 일러—허물없는 정으로 돔비 씨를 만나—단춧구멍을 갈고리로 붙들고—그 일을 이야기해—죄다 바로잡고는—의기양양하게 물러나오는 것이었다!"]], [["As these reflections presented themselves to the Captain’s mind, and by slow degrees assumed this shape and form, his visage cleared like a doubtful morning when it gives place to a bright noon. His eyebrows, which had been in the highest degree portentous, smoothed their rugged bristling aspect, and became serene; his eyes, which had been nearly closed in the severity of his mental exercise, opened freely; a smile which had been at first but three specks—one at the right-hand corner of his mouth, and one at the corner of each eye—gradually overspread his whole face, and, rippling up into his forehead, lifted the glazed hat: as if that too had been aground with Captain Cuttle, and were now, like him, happily afloat again.", "이런 생각들이 선장의 마음속에 떠오르고 천천히 이런 모양과 꼴을 갖추어 가면서, 그의 얼굴은 미심쩍던 아침이 밝은 한낮에 자리를 내주듯 환하게 개었다. 더없이 불길하던 눈썹은 거칠게 곤두선 기색을 누그러뜨리고 평온해졌다. 정신적 노고의 준엄함에 거의 감겨 있던 눈은 시원하게 열렸다. 처음에는 그저 세 점—입 오른쪽 끝에 하나, 양쪽 눈가에 각각 하나—에 지나지 않던 미소가 차츰 온 얼굴로 번져 나가더니, 이마까지 물결쳐 올라가 번들거리는 모자를 들어 올렸다. 마치 그 모자마저도 커틀 선장과 함께 좌초해 있다가 이제 그처럼 다행히도 다시 떠오른 것 같았다."]], [["Finally, the Captain left off biting his nails, and said, “Now, Wal”r, my boy, you may help me on with them slops.”", "마침내 선장은 손톱 물어뜯기를 그만두고 말했다. \"자, 월터, 얘야, 그 옷가지 입는 걸 좀 거들어 다오.\""], ["By which the Captain meant his coat and waistcoat.", "선장이 말한 옷가지란 외투와 조끼였다."]], [["Walter little imagined why the Captain was so particular in the arrangement of his cravat, as to twist the pendent ends into a sort of pigtail, and pass them through a massive gold ring with a picture of a tomb upon it, and a neat iron railing, and a tree, in memory of some deceased friend.", "월터는, 선장이 어째서 그토록 까다롭게 크라바트를 매만지는지—늘어진 양 끝을 일종의 변발처럼 비틀어, 어느 죽은 친구를 기리는 무덤 그림과 말끔한 철책과 나무가 새겨진 묵직한 금반지에 꿰는지—거의 짐작도 하지 못했다."], ["Nor why the Captain pulled up his shirt-collar to the utmost limits allowed by the Irish linen below, and by so doing decorated himself with a complete pair of blinkers; nor why he changed his shoes, and put on an unparalleled pair of ankle-jacks, which he only wore on extraordinary occasions.", "또 선장이 어째서 셔츠 깃을, 그 아래의 아일랜드제 리넨이 허락하는 한계까지 한껏 추켜올려, 그렇게 함으로써 완벽한 한 쌍의 눈가리개로 자기를 장식하는지도, 또 어째서 신발을 갈아 신고, 특별한 경우에만 신는 둘도 없는 발목 장화 한 켤레를 꿰차는지도 짐작하지 못했다."], ["The Captain being at length attired to his own complete satisfaction, and having glanced at himself from head to foot in a shaving-glass which he removed from a nail for that purpose, took up his knotted stick, and said he was ready.", "마침내 선장은 자기가 완전히 만족할 만큼 차려입고는, 그러려고 못에서 떼어 낸 면도용 거울에 머리끝부터 발끝까지 자기 모습을 한번 비춰 보고 나서, 옹이 진 지팡이를 집어 들며 준비가 됐다고 말했다."]], [["The Captain’s walk was more complacent than usual when they got out into the street; but this Walter supposed to be the effect of the ankle-jacks, and took little heed of.", "거리로 나섰을 때 선장의 걸음걸이는 여느 때보다 우쭐해 있었으나, 월터는 그것을 발목 장화 탓이려니 여기고 별로 개의치 않았다. 얼마 가지 않아 둘은 꽃 파는 여인과 마주쳤다."], ["Before they had gone very far, they encountered a woman selling flowers; when the Captain stopping short, as if struck by a happy idea, made a purchase of the largest bundle in her basket: a most glorious nosegay, fan-shaped, some two feet and a half round, and composed of all the jolliest-looking flowers that blow.", "그러자 선장이, 무슨 묘안이라도 떠오른 듯 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 그녀의 바구니에서 가장 큰 다발을 사들였다. 그것은 더없이 화려한 부채꼴 꽃다발로, 둘레가 두 자 반쯤 되었고, 피어나는 꽃들 가운데 가장 흥겨워 보이는 것들로 죄다 엮은 것이었다."]], [["Armed with this little token which he designed for Mr Dombey, Captain Cuttle walked on with Walter until they reached the Instrument-maker’s door, before which they both paused.", "돔비 씨에게 줄 작정으로 이 자그마한 정표를 갖춰 든 채, 커틀 선장은 월터와 함께 걸어 악기 제작자의 문 앞에 이르렀고, 둘은 그 앞에서 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“You’re going in?”", "\"들어가실 거죠?\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["returned the Captain, who felt that Walter must be got rid of before he proceeded any further, and that he had better time his projected visit somewhat later in the day.", "선장이 대답했다. 그는 더 나아가기 전에 월터를 떼어 놓아야 한다는 것, 그리고 자기가 계획한 방문은 오후 늦게로 시각을 잡는 편이 낫겠다는 것을 느끼고 있었다."]], [["“And you won’t forget anything?”", "\"그리고 아무것도 잊지 않으실 거죠?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"안 잊지.\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."]], [["“I’ll go upon my walk at once,”", "\"그럼 저는 곧장 산책하러 가 볼게요,\""], ["said Walter, “and then I shall be out of the way, Captain Cuttle.”", "월터가 말했다. \"그러면 제가 방해되지 않을 테니까요, 커틀 선장님.\""]], [["“Take a good long “un, my lad!”", "\"한참 멀리, 오래 다녀오너라, 얘야!\""], ["replied the Captain, calling after him.", "선장이 그의 뒤에 대고 외쳤다."], ["Walter waved his hand in assent, and went his way.", "월터는 알겠다는 듯 손을 흔들고 제 길을 갔다."]], [["His way was nowhere in particular; but he thought he would go out into the fields, where he could reflect upon the unknown life before him, and resting under some tree, ponder quietly.", "그의 길은 딱히 정해진 데가 없었다. 다만 그는 들판으로 나가 자기 앞에 놓인 알 수 없는 삶을 곰곰이 생각하고, 어느 나무 아래 쉬며 조용히 사색에 잠겨 볼까 했다."], ["He knew no better fields than those near Hampstead, and no better means of getting at them than by passing Mr Dombey’s house.", "그는 햄스테드 근처보다 더 좋은 들판을 알지 못했고, 거기에 닿는 데 돔비 씨 댁을 지나는 것보다 더 나은 길도 알지 못했다."]], [["It was as stately and as dark as ever, when he went by and glanced up at its frowning front.", "그가 지나가며 그 찌푸린 듯한 정면을 올려다보았을 때, 그 집은 여느 때처럼 으리으리하고 음침했다."], ["The blinds were all pulled down, but the upper windows stood wide open, and the pleasant air stirring those curtains and waving them to and fro was the only sign of animation in the whole exterior.", "차양은 죄다 내려져 있었으나, 위층 창문들은 활짝 열려 있었고, 그 휘장을 흔들며 이리저리 나부끼게 하는 상쾌한 바람만이 그 외관 전체에서 유일하게 살아 있다는 표시였다."], ["Walter walked softly as he passed, and was glad when he had left the house a door or two behind.", "월터는 지나가며 발소리를 죽였고, 그 집을 한두 집 뒤로하고 나서야 마음이 놓였다."]], [["He looked back then; with the interest he had always felt for the place since the adventure of the lost child, years ago; and looked especially at those upper windows.", "그러고 나서 그는 뒤를 돌아보았다. 여러 해 전 길 잃은 아이의 그 모험이 있은 뒤로 줄곧 그 자리에 품어 온 관심을 안고서, 특히 그 위층 창문들을 바라보았다."], ["While he was thus engaged, a chariot drove to the door, and a portly gentleman in black, with a heavy watch-chain, alighted, and went in.", "그렇게 바라보고 있는 동안, 마차 한 대가 문 앞으로 들어서더니, 묵직한 시곗줄을 단 검은 옷차림의 풍채 좋은 신사가 내려 안으로 들어갔다."], ["When he afterwards remembered this gentleman and his equipage together, Walter had no doubt he was a physician; and then he wondered who was ill; but the discovery did not occur to him until he had walked some distance, thinking listlessly of other things.", "나중에 그 신사와 그의 마차를 함께 떠올리고서, 월터는 그가 의사임을 의심치 않았다. 그러고는 누가 아픈 걸까 궁금해졌으나, 그 짐작은 다른 일들을 하릴없이 생각하며 한참을 걷고 나서야 떠올랐다."]], [["Though still, of what the house had suggested to him; for Walter pleased himself with thinking that perhaps the time might come, when the beautiful child who was his old friend and had always been so grateful to him and so glad to see him since, might interest her brother in his behalf and influence his fortunes for the better.", "그래도 여전히, 그 집이 그에게 떠올리게 한 것에 잠겨 있었다. 월터는, 어쩌면 언젠가 때가 오면, 옛 친구이자 그 뒤로 줄곧 그토록 고마워하고 그를 보면 그토록 기뻐하던 그 아름다운 아이가, 자기를 위해 제 오라비의 관심을 끌어 주고 자기 앞날을 더 낫게 좌우해 줄지도 모른다고 생각하며 흐뭇해했다."], ["He liked to imagine this—more, at that moment, for the pleasure of imagining her continued remembrance of him, than for any worldly profit he might gain: but another and more sober fancy whispered to him that if he were alive then, he would be beyond the sea and forgotten; she married, rich, proud, happy.", "그는 이런 상상이 좋았는데—그 순간으로서는, 그것으로 얻을 어떤 세속적인 이득보다도, 그녀가 자기를 줄곧 기억해 준다고 상상하는 즐거움 때문이었다. 그러나 또 하나의 더 차분한 공상이 그에게 속삭였다."], ["There was no more reason why she should remember him with any interest in such an altered state of things, than any plaything she ever had.", "그때쯤 자기가 살아 있다 한들 바다 건너 멀리 잊힌 몸일 테고, 그녀는 결혼해서 부유하고 도도하고 행복할 거라고. 그토록 처지가 달라졌다면, 그녀가 어린 시절 가지고 놀던 어떤 장난감보다 더 깊은 관심으로 자기를 기억할 까닭은 없었다."], ["No, not so much.", "아니, 그만큼의 관심조차도 없으리라."]], [["Yet Walter so idealised the pretty child whom he had found wandering in the rough streets, and so identified her with her innocent gratitude of that night and the simplicity and truth of its expression, that he blushed for himself as a libeller when he argued that she could ever grow proud.", "그래도 월터는, 거친 거리를 헤매던 그 어여쁜 아이를 한껏 이상화했고, 그 밤의 천진한 고마움이며 그것을 드러내던 소박함과 진실함과 그녀를 너무도 단단히 하나로 묶어 두었던지라, 그녀가 언젠가 도도해질 수 있다고 따져 보는 자기 자신을, 명예를 헐뜯는 자라며 부끄러워했다."], ["On the other hand, his meditations were of that fantastic order that it seemed hardly less libellous in him to imagine her grown a woman: to think of her as anything but the same artless, gentle, winning little creature, that she had been in the days of Good Mrs Brown.", "한편으로 그의 사색은 어찌나 변덕스러운 갈래의 것이었던지, 그녀가 여인으로 자란 모습을 상상하는 것—착한 브라운 부인 시절에 그러했던, 그 한결같이 꾸밈없고 다정하고 사랑스러운 어린 존재 이외의 무엇으로 그녀를 떠올리는 것—조차 그에게는 못지않게 헐뜯는 일처럼 여겨졌다."], ["In a word, Walter found out that to reason with himself about Florence at all, was to become very unreasonable indeed; and that he could do no better than preserve her image in his mind as something precious, unattainable, unchangeable, and indefinite—indefinite in all but its power of giving him pleasure, and restraining him like an angel’s hand from anything unworthy.", "한마디로, 월터는 플로렌스에 대해 자기 자신과 따져 보는 일이 무릇 더없이 도리에 어긋난 짓이 되고 만다는 것을 깨달았다. 그리고 그녀의 모습을, 무언가 귀하고 닿을 수 없고 변치 않고 막연한 것으로—그에게 기쁨을 주고 천사의 손길처럼 그를 무엇이든 떳떳지 못한 것에서 붙들어 주는 그 힘만은 막연하지 않은, 그런 것으로—마음속에 간직하는 것보다 더 나은 일은 할 수 없다는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["It was a long stroll in the fields that Walter took that day, listening to the birds, and the Sunday bells, and the softened murmur of the town—breathing sweet scents; glancing sometimes at the dim horizon beyond which his voyage and his place of destination lay; then looking round on the green English grass and the home landscape.", "그날 월터는 들판에서 오래도록 거닐며, 새소리와 일요일의 종소리와 누그러진 도시의 웅성거림에 귀를 기울였고—달콤한 향내를 들이쉬었고, 이따금 항해와 목적지가 놓인 저 희미한 지평선 너머를 바라보다가, 다시 둘레의 푸른 영국 풀밭과 정든 풍경을 둘러보았다."], ["But he hardly once thought, even of going away, distinctly; and seemed to put off reflection idly, from hour to hour, and from minute to minute, while he yet went on reflecting all the time.", "그러나 그는 떠난다는 것조차도 또렷이 생각해 본 적이 거의 없었고, 시간시간, 분분이 사색을 하릴없이 미루는 듯하면서도, 정작 그러는 내내 줄곧 사색하고 있었다."]], [["Walter had left the fields behind him, and was plodding homeward in the same abstracted mood, when he heard a shout from a man, and then a woman’s voice calling to him loudly by name.", "월터가 들판을 뒤로하고, 똑같이 멍한 기분에 잠겨 집으로 터덜터덜 돌아오고 있을 때, 한 남자가 고함치는 소리가 들리더니, 이어 한 여자의 목소리가 큰 소리로 그의 이름을 부르는 것이 들렸다."], ["Turning quickly in his surprise, he saw that a hackney-coach, going in the contrary direction, had stopped at no great distance; that the coachman was looking back from his box and making signals to him with his whip; and that a young woman inside was leaning out of the window, and beckoning with immense energy.", "놀라 재빨리 돌아보니, 반대 방향으로 가던 삯마차 한 대가 멀지 않은 데 멈춰 서 있었고, 마부가 마부석에서 뒤를 돌아보며 채찍으로 그에게 신호를 보내고 있었으며, 안에서는 젊은 여자가 창밖으로 몸을 내밀고 엄청난 기세로 손짓하고 있었다."], ["Running up to this coach, he found that the young woman was Miss Nipper, and that Miss Nipper was in such a flutter as to be almost beside herself.", "그 마차로 달려가 보니, 그 젊은 여자는 니퍼 양이었고, 니퍼 양은 거의 제정신이 아닐 만큼 안절부절못하고 있었다."]], [["“Staggs’s Gardens, Mr Walter!”", "\"스태그스 가든이요, 월터 씨!\""], ["said Miss Nipper; “if you please, oh do!”", "니퍼 양이 말했다. \"제발요, 아, 좀요!\""]], [["“Eh?”", "\"네?\""], ["cried Walter; “what is the matter?”", "월터가 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이에요?\""]], [["“Oh, Mr Walter, Staggs’s Gardens, if you please!”", "\"아, 월터 씨, 스태그스 가든이요, 제발요!\""], ["said Susan.", "수전이 말했다."]], [["“There!”", "\"이거 보세요!\""], ["cried the coachman, appealing to Walter, with a sort of exalting despair; “that’s the way the young lady’s been a goin’ on for up’ards of a mortal hour, and me continivally backing out of no thoroughfares, where she would drive up.", "마부가 일종의 의기양양한 절망을 담아 월터에게 하소연하며 외쳤다. \"저 아가씨가 꼬박 한 시간 넘게 저러는 통에, 저는 아가씨가 들어가겠다는 막다른 골목들에서 끊임없이 후진해 빠져나오고 있다니까요."], ["I’ve had a many fares in this coach, first and last, but never such a fare as her.”", "이 마차에 첫 손님부터 마지막 손님까지 수도 없이 태워 봤지만, 이런 손님은 처음입니다.\""]], [["“Do you want to go to Staggs’s Gardens, Susan?”", "\"스태그스 가든에 가고 싶은 거예요, 수전?\""], ["inquired Walter.", "월터가 물었다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["She wants to go there!", "거기 가고 싶다잖습니까!"], ["WHERE IS IT?”", "근데 그게 어디냔 말이오?\""], ["growled the coachman.", "마부가 으르렁댔다."]], [["“I don’t know where it is!”", "\"그게 어딘지 모르겠어요!\""], ["exclaimed Susan, wildly.", "수전이 정신없이 외쳤다."], ["“Mr Walter, I was there once myself, along with Miss Floy and our poor darling Master Paul, on the very day when you found Miss Floy in the City, for we lost her coming home, Mrs Richards and me, and a mad bull, and Mrs Richards’s eldest, and though I went there afterwards, I can’t remember where it is, I think it’s sunk into the ground.", "\"월터 씨, 저도 한 번 거기 가 본 적 있어요, 플로이 아가씨랑 우리 가엾고 사랑스러운 폴 도련님이랑요, 바로 그날, 월터 씨가 시내에서 플로이 아가씨를 찾아 주신 그날 말이에요, 우리가 집에 오다가 아가씨를 잃어버렸잖아요, 리처즈 부인이랑 저랑요, 미친 황소랑, 리처즈 부인 맏이까지 있었는데요, 그러고 나서 제가 나중에 거기 가 보긴 했는데, 그게 어딘지 도무지 기억이 안 나요, 땅속으로 꺼져 버린 것 같아요."], ["Oh, Mr Walter, don’t desert me, Staggs’s Gardens, if you please!", "아, 월터 씨, 저를 버리지 마세요, 스태그스 가든이요, 제발요!"], ["Miss Floy’s darling—all our darlings—little, meek, meek Master Paul!", "플로이 아가씨의 사랑둥이—우리 모두의 사랑둥이—여리디여린 폴 도련님요!"], ["Oh Mr Walter!”", "아, 월터 씨!\""]], [["“Good God!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["cried Walter.", "월터가 외쳤다."], ["“Is he very ill?”", "\"그 애가 많이 아파요?\""]], [["“The pretty flower!”", "\"그 어여쁜 꽃송이가요!\""], ["cried Susan, wringing her hands, “has took the fancy that he’d like to see his old nurse, and I’ve come to bring her to his bedside, Mrs Staggs, of Polly Toodle’s Gardens, someone pray!”", "수전이 두 손을 쥐어짜며 외쳤다. \"옛 유모를 보고 싶다는 생각이 들었대요, 그래서 제가 그분을 도련님 머리맡으로 모셔 가려고 온 거예요, 폴리 투들의 가든에 사는 스태그스 부인 말이에요, 누구든 좀 제발요!\""]], [["Greatly moved by what he heard, and catching Susan’s earnestness immediately, Walter, now that he understood the nature of her errand, dashed into it with such ardour that the coachman had enough to do to follow closely as he ran before, inquiring here and there and everywhere, the way to Staggs’s Gardens.", "들은 말에 크게 마음이 움직이고, 곧장 수전의 절박함에 휩싸인 월터는, 이제 그녀의 심부름이 어떤 성격의 것인지 알게 되자, 어찌나 열성으로 거기에 뛰어들었던지, 마부는 앞장서 달리며 여기저기 가는 데마다 스태그스 가든으로 가는 길을 물어 대는 그를 바싹 뒤따르느라 진땀을 뺐다."]], [["There was no such place as Staggs’s Gardens.", "스태그스 가든이라는 곳은 없었다."], ["It had vanished from the earth.", "그것은 땅 위에서 사라져 버렸다."], ["Where the old rotten summer-houses once had stood, palaces now reared their heads, and granite columns of gigantic girth opened a vista to the railway world beyond.", "한때 낡고 썩은 정자들이 서 있던 자리에는 이제 궁궐 같은 건물들이 머리를 치켜들었고, 어마어마한 둘레의 화강암 기둥들이 저 너머 철도 세계로 통하는 전망을 열어젖혔다."], ["The miserable waste ground, where the refuse-matter had been heaped of yore, was swallowed up and gone; and in its frowsy stead were tiers of warehouses, crammed with rich goods and costly merchandise.", "옛날 폐기물이 쌓여 있던 그 비참한 황무지는 삼켜져 자취를 감추었고, 그 추레하던 자리에는 값진 물건과 비싼 상품으로 가득 찬 창고들이 층층이 늘어섰다."], ["The old by-streets now swarmed with passengers and vehicles of every kind: the new streets that had stopped disheartened in the mud and waggon-ruts, formed towns within themselves, originating wholesome comforts and conveniences belonging to themselves, and never tried nor thought of until they sprung into existence.", "옛 골목길에는 이제 온갖 종류의 행인과 탈것이 우글거렸다. 진창과 짐수레 바퀴 자국 속에서 의기소침하게 멈춰 섰던 새 거리들은 그 자체로 하나의 도시를 이루었으니, 거기 생겨나기 전에는 시도된 적도 생각된 적도 없던, 저마다의 건강한 안락과 편의를 만들어 냈다."], ["Bridges that had led to nothing, led to villas, gardens, churches, healthy public walks.", "아무 데로도 통하지 않던 다리들이 별장으로, 정원으로, 교회로, 건강에 좋은 공중 산책로로 통했다."], ["The carcasses of houses, and beginnings of new thoroughfares, had started off upon the line at steam’s own speed, and shot away into the country in a monster train.", "집들의 잔해와 새 도로의 시작점들은 증기 그 자체의 속도로 선로 위로 출발해, 괴물 같은 열차가 되어 시골로 쏜살같이 내달았다."]], [["As to the neighbourhood which had hesitated to acknowledge the railroad in its straggling days, that had grown wise and penitent, as any Christian might in such a case, and now boasted of its powerful and prosperous relation.", "띄엄띄엄 헤매던 시절에 철도를 인정하기를 망설였던 그 동네로 말하자면, 어떤 기독교도라도 그런 경우에 그러하듯 현명하고 뉘우치는 처지가 되어, 이제는 그 강력하고 번창하는 친척을 자랑하고 있었다."], ["There were railway patterns in its drapers’ shops, and railway journals in the windows of its newsmen.", "포목점 진열창에는 철도 무늬가, 신문 가게 창문에는 철도 신문이 놓여 있었다."], ["There were railway hotels, office-houses, lodging-houses, boarding-houses; railway plans, maps, views, wrappers, bottles, sandwich-boxes, and time-tables; railway hackney-coach and stands; railway omnibuses, railway streets and buildings, railway hangers-on and parasites, and flatterers out of all calculation.", "철도 호텔, 철도 사무소, 철도 셋집, 철도 하숙집이 있었고, 철도 도면, 지도, 풍경화, 포장지, 병, 샌드위치 통, 시간표가 있었고, 철도 삯마차와 마차 정류장이 있었으며, 철도 합승마차, 철도 거리와 건물, 철도에 빌붙어 사는 자들과 기생충들, 셀 수 없이 많은 아첨꾼들이 있었다."], ["There was even railway time observed in clocks, as if the sun itself had given in.", "심지어 시계도 철도 시각을 따랐으니, 마치 해마저도 항복한 듯했다."], ["Among the vanquished was the master chimney-sweeper, whilom incredulous at Staggs’s Gardens, who now lived in a stuccoed house three stories high, and gave himself out, with golden flourishes upon a varnished board, as contractor for the cleansing of railway chimneys by machinery.", "정복당한 자들 가운데는 굴뚝 청소부 우두머리가 있었는데, 한때 스태그스 가든에서 철도를 도무지 믿지 않던 그가, 이제는 삼 층짜리 회벽 집에 살면서, 니스 칠한 간판에 금빛 장식 글씨로, 기계를 이용한 철도 굴뚝 청소 도급업자라고 자기를 내세우고 있었다."]], [["To and from the heart of this great change, all day and night, throbbing currents rushed and returned incessantly like its life’s blood.", "이 거대한 변화의 심장으로, 또 그곳에서, 밤낮으로 하루 종일, 고동치는 흐름이 마치 그곳의 핏줄처럼 끊임없이 밀려들고 되돌아갔다."], ["Crowds of people and mountains of goods, departing and arriving scores upon scores of times in every four-and-twenty hours, produced a fermentation in the place that was always in action.", "떠나고 도착하기를 스물네 시간마다 수십 번씩 거듭하는 사람들의 무리와 산더미 같은 상품들은, 그곳에 언제나 작동하고 있는 발효를 일으켰다."], ["The very houses seemed disposed to pack up and take trips.", "집들조차 짐을 꾸려 여행을 떠나고 싶어 하는 것 같았다."], ["Wonderful Members of Parliament, who, little more than twenty years before, had made themselves merry with the wild railroad theories of engineers, and given them the liveliest rubs in cross-examination, went down into the north with their watches in their hands, and sent on messages before by the electric telegraph, to say that they were coming.", "불과 이십여 년 전만 해도 기술자들의 황당한 철도 이론을 비웃고, 반대 신문에서 그들에게 더없이 신랄한 핀잔을 안기던 그 놀라운 국회의원들이, 시계를 손에 든 채 북부로 내려갔고, 자기들이 간다고 알리려 전기 전신으로 미리 전갈을 보냈다."], ["Night and day the conquering engines rumbled at their distant work, or, advancing smoothly to their journey’s end, and gliding like tame dragons into the allotted corners grooved out to the inch for their reception, stood bubbling and trembling there, making the walls quake, as if they were dilating with the secret knowledge of great powers yet unsuspected in them, and strong purposes not yet achieved.", "밤낮으로 정복자인 기관차들은 저 멀리서 제 일을 하며 우르릉거렸고, 또는 매끄럽게 여정의 끝으로 다가와, 자기들을 맞이하려 한 치까지 파 놓은 지정된 모퉁이로 길든 용처럼 미끄러져 들어와서는, 부글거리고 떨며 거기 멈춰 서서 벽을 진동시켰으니, 마치 자기 안에 아직 짐작 못 한 거대한 힘과 아직 이루지 못한 강력한 목적을 은밀히 알고서 그것으로 부풀어 오르는 듯했다."]], [["But Staggs’s Gardens had been cut up root and branch.", "그러나 스태그스 가든은 뿌리째 가지째 잘려 나갔다."], ["Oh woe the day when “not a rood of English ground”—laid out in Staggs’s Gardens—is secure!", "오, 슬프도다, \"영국 땅 한 평\"도—스태그스 가든에 펼쳐진 그 한 평조차—안전하지 못한 그날이여!"]], [["At last, after much fruitless inquiry, Walter, followed by the coach and Susan, found a man who had once resided in that vanished land, and who was no other than the master sweep before referred to, grown stout, and knocking a double knock at his own door.", "마침내, 헛된 수소문을 많이 한 끝에, 월터는 마차와 수전을 거느린 채, 그 사라진 땅에 한때 살았던 한 남자를 찾아냈다. 그는 다름 아닌 앞서 말한 굴뚝 청소부 우두머리로, 뚱뚱해진 몸으로 제 집 문을 똑똑 두드리고 있었다."], ["He knowed Toodle, he said, well.", "그는 투들을 잘 안다고 말했다."], ["Belonged to the Railroad, didn’t he?", "철도에 다니지 않느냐고 했다."]], [["“Yes sir, yes!”", "\"네, 선생님, 맞아요!\""], ["cried Susan Nipper from the coach window.", "수전 니퍼가 마차 창문에서 외쳤다."]], [["Where did he live now? hastily inquired Walter.", "이제 그가 어디 사느냐고, 월터가 다급히 물었다."]], [["He lived in the Company’s own Buildings, second turning to the right, down the yard, cross over, and take the second on the right again.", "그는 회사 소유 건물에 살았는데, 오른쪽으로 두 번째 모퉁이를 돌아, 마당을 내려가, 가로질러 가서, 다시 오른쪽으로 두 번째 집이었다."], ["It was number eleven; they couldn’t mistake it; but if they did, they had only to ask for Toodle, Engine Fireman, and any one would show them which was his house.", "십일 호였으니, 못 알아볼 리가 없지만, 혹시 못 찾으면 그저 기관 화부 투들을 찾기만 하면 누구든 그의 집이 어딘지 가르쳐 줄 거라고 했다."], ["At this unexpected stroke of success Susan Nipper dismounted from the coach with all speed, took Walter’s arm, and set off at a breathless pace on foot; leaving the coach there to await their return.", "이 뜻밖의 성공에 수전 니퍼는 부랴부랴 마차에서 내려, 월터의 팔을 붙들고, 숨 가쁜 걸음으로 걸어서 출발했다. 마차는 거기 남겨 두어 그들이 돌아오기를 기다리게 했다."]], [["“Has the little boy been long ill, Susan?”", "\"그 어린 도련님이 아픈 지 오래됐어요, 수전?\""], ["inquired Walter, as they hurried on.", "서둘러 가면서 월터가 물었다."]], [["“Ailing for a deal of time, but no one knew how much,”", "\"오랫동안 시름시름 앓았는데, 얼마나 심한지는 아무도 몰랐어요.\" 수전이 말했다."], ["said Susan; adding, with excessive sharpness, “Oh, them Blimbers!”", "그러고는 몹시 날카롭게 덧붙였다. \"아, 그 블림버 사람들!\""]], [["“Blimbers?”", "\"블림버요?\""], ["echoed Walter.", "월터가 따라 물었다."]], [["“I couldn’t forgive myself at such a time as this, Mr Walter,”", "\"이런 때에 제 자신을 용서할 수가 없을 거예요, 월터 씨,\" 수전이 말했다."], ["said Susan, “and when there’s so much serious distress to think about, if I rested hard on anyone, especially on them that little darling Paul speaks well of, but I may wish that the family was set to work in a stony soil to make new roads, and that Miss Blimber went in front, and had the pickaxe!”", "\"이렇게 심각한 슬픔을 생각해야 할 때에, 제가 누구한테든 모질게 군다면, 더구나 그 어린 사랑둥이 폴 도련님이 좋게 말하는 사람들한테 그런다면요. 하지만 그 가족이 돌투성이 땅에서 새 길을 닦는 일에 부려지고, 블림버 양이 앞장서서 곡괭이를 들기를 바랄 수는 있겠죠!\""]], [["Miss Nipper then took breath, and went on faster than before, as if this extraordinary aspiration had relieved her.", "그러고 나서 니퍼 양은 숨을 돌리고는, 이 별난 소망이 마음을 풀어 주기라도 한 듯, 전보다 더 빠르게 걸어 나갔다."], ["Walter, who had by this time no breath of his own to spare, hurried along without asking any more questions; and they soon, in their impatience, burst in at a little door and came into a clean parlour full of children.", "이때쯤에는 자기 숨조차 가다듬을 여력이 없던 월터는 더는 아무것도 묻지 않고 부지런히 따라갔다. 그리고 둘은 조급한 마음에 곧 자그마한 문을 박차고 들어가, 아이들로 가득 찬 깨끗한 응접실로 들어섰다."]], [["“Where’s Mrs Richards?”", "\"리처즈 부인은 어디 계세요?\""], ["exclaimed Susan Nipper, looking round.", "수전 니퍼가 둘레를 둘러보며 외쳤다."], ["“Oh Mrs Richards, Mrs Richards, come along with me, my dear creetur!”", "\"아, 리처즈 부인, 리처즈 부인, 저랑 같이 가요, 우리 다정한 분!\""]], [["“Why, if it ain’t Susan!”", "\"아니, 수전 아니야!\""], ["cried Polly, rising with her honest face and motherly figure from among the group, in great surprise.", "폴리가 그 무리 가운데서, 그 정직한 얼굴과 자애로운 몸매로 몹시 놀라며 일어섰다."]], [["“Yes, Mrs Richards, it’s me,”", "\"네, 리처즈 부인, 저예요,\" 수전이 말했다."], ["said Susan, “and I wish it wasn’t, though I may not seem to flatter when I say so, but little Master Paul is very ill, and told his Pa today that he would like to see the face of his old nurse, and him and Miss Floy hope you’ll come along with me—and Mr Walter, Mrs Richards—forgetting what is past, and do a kindness to the sweet dear that is withering away.", "\"차라리 제가 아니었으면 싶긴 한데요, 이런 말 한다고 비위 맞추려는 것처럼 보이진 않겠죠, 하지만 어린 폴 도련님이 많이 아파요, 그래서 오늘 아버님께 옛 유모의 얼굴을 보고 싶다고 했대요. 그래서 도련님이랑 플로이 아가씨가 부디 저랑 같이 가 주셨으면 해요—그리고 월터 씨도요, 리처즈 부인—지난 일은 잊으시고, 시들어 가는 그 어여쁜 분께 친절을 베풀어 주세요."], ["Oh, Mrs Richards, withering away!”", "아, 리처즈 부인, 시들어 가고 있어요!\""], ["Susan Nipper crying, Polly shed tears to see her, and to hear what she had said; and all the children gathered round (including numbers of new babies); and Mr Toodle, who had just come home from Birmingham, and was eating his dinner out of a basin, laid down his knife and fork, and put on his wife’s bonnet and shawl for her, which were hanging up behind the door; then tapped her on the back; and said, with more fatherly feeling than eloquence, “Polly!", "수전 니퍼가 우는 통에, 폴리도 그녀를 보고, 또 그녀가 한 말을 듣고 눈물을 흘렸다. 그리고 아이들이 죄다 둘레로 모여들었고(새로 난 아기들도 여럿 끼어 있었다), 마침 버밍엄에서 막 돌아와 사발에 담긴 저녁을 먹고 있던 투들 씨는 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고, 문 뒤에 걸려 있던 아내의 보닛과 숄을 가져다 그녀에게 입혀 주고는, 등을 톡톡 두드리며, 능변이라기보다는 아버지다운 정으로 말했다."], ["cut away!”", "\"폴리! 어서 가!\""]], [["So they got back to the coach, long before the coachman expected them; and Walter, putting Susan and Mrs Richards inside, took his seat on the box himself that there might be no more mistakes, and deposited them safely in the hall of Mr Dombey’s house—where, by the bye, he saw a mighty nosegay lying, which reminded him of the one Captain Cuttle had purchased in his company that morning.", "그리하여 그들은 마부가 예상한 것보다 훨씬 일찍 마차로 돌아왔다. 월터는 수전과 리처즈 부인을 안에 태우고, 더는 착오가 없도록 자기는 마부석에 자리를 잡고는, 둘을 돔비 씨 댁 현관까지 무사히 데려다 놓았다—그런데 마침 거기서, 그는 커다란 꽃다발 하나가 놓여 있는 것을 보았으니, 그것은 그날 아침 커틀 선장이 자기와 함께 있을 때 사들인 그 꽃다발을 떠올리게 했다."], ["He would have lingered to know more of the young invalid, or waited any length of time to see if he could render the least service; but, painfully sensible that such conduct would be looked upon by Mr Dombey as presumptuous and forward, he turned slowly, sadly, anxiously, away.", "그는 어린 환자에 대해 더 알고 싶어 머무르고 싶었고, 아주 조금이라도 도움이 될 수 있을지 보려고 얼마든 기다리고 싶었다. 그러나 그런 행동이 돔비 씨에게는 주제넘고 나서는 짓으로 비치리라는 것을 뼈저리게 의식하고 있었던지라, 그는 천천히, 서글프게, 불안한 마음으로 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["He had not gone five minutes’ walk from the door, when a man came running after him, and begged him to return.", "문에서 채 오 분도 걸어가지 않았을 때, 한 남자가 그를 뒤쫓아 달려와 돌아와 달라고 청했다."], ["Walter retraced his steps as quickly as he could, and entered the gloomy house with a sorrowful foreboding.", "월터는 최대한 빨리 발길을 되돌려, 서글픈 예감을 안고 그 음침한 집으로 들어섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVI. What the Waves were always saying", "# CHAPTER XVI. What the Waves were always saying"]], [["Paul had never risen from his little bed.", "폴은 그 작은 침대에서 한 번도 일어나지 못했다."], ["He lay there, listening to the noises in the street, quite tranquilly; not caring much how the time went, but watching it and watching everything about him with observing eyes.", "그는 거기 누운 채 거리의 소음에 귀를 기울였는데, 더없이 평온했다. 시간이 어떻게 흘러가든 그다지 마음 쓰지 않으면서도, 시간을 지켜보고 자기 주위의 모든 것을 관찰하는 눈으로 바라보았다."]], [["When the sunbeams struck into his room through the rustling blinds, and quivered on the opposite wall like golden water, he knew that evening was coming on, and that the sky was red and beautiful.", "햇살이 바스락거리는 블라인드를 뚫고 그의 방으로 들이쳐 맞은편 벽 위에서 금빛 물결처럼 일렁일 때면, 그는 저녁이 다가오고 있으며 하늘이 붉고 아름답다는 것을 알았다."], ["As the reflection died away, and a gloom went creeping up the wall, he watched it deepen, deepen, deepen, into night.", "그 반사가 사그라들고 어스름이 벽을 타고 슬그머니 기어오르면, 그는 그것이 깊어지고, 깊어지고, 깊어져 밤이 되는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["Then he thought how the long streets were dotted with lamps, and how the peaceful stars were shining overhead.", "그러면 그는 긴 거리마다 등불이 점점이 박혀 있는 모습과, 머리 위에서 평화로운 별들이 빛나는 모습을 떠올렸다."], ["His fancy had a strange tendency to wander to the river, which he knew was flowing through the great city; and now he thought how black it was, and how deep it would look, reflecting the hosts of stars—and more than all, how steadily it rolled away to meet the sea.", "그의 공상은 이상하게도 강으로 흘러가는 버릇이 있었는데, 그 강이 이 거대한 도시를 가로질러 흐른다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 그리고 이제 그는 그 강이 얼마나 검은지, 무수한 별을 비추면 얼마나 깊어 보일지를 생각했다—그리고 무엇보다도, 그것이 바다를 만나러 얼마나 한결같이 굽이쳐 흘러가는지를 생각했다."]], [["As it grew later in the night, and footsteps in the street became so rare that he could hear them coming, count them as they passed, and lose them in the hollow distance, he would lie and watch the many-coloured ring about the candle, and wait patiently for day.", "밤이 더 깊어 거리의 발걸음 소리가 어찌나 드물어지던지 다가오는 소리를 듣고, 지나가는 동안 그 수를 세고, 텅 빈 먼 곳으로 사라지는 것을 들을 수 있게 되면, 그는 누운 채 촛불 둘레의 알록달록한 고리를 지켜보며 참을성 있게 날이 밝기를 기다렸다."], ["His only trouble was, the swift and rapid river.", "그의 유일한 괴로움은 그 빠르고 거센 강이었다."], ["He felt forced, sometimes, to try to stop it—to stem it with his childish hands—or choke its way with sand—and when he saw it coming on, resistless, he cried out!", "그는 때때로 그것을 멈추려고—제 어린 손으로 막아 보려고—혹은 모래로 그 길을 메우려고 애써야 할 것 같은 충동을 느꼈고, 그것이 막을 수 없이 다가오는 것을 보면 비명을 질렀다!"], ["But a word from Florence, who was always at his side, restored him to himself; and leaning his poor head upon her breast, he told Floy of his dream, and smiled.", "그러나 늘 곁에 있던 플로렌스의 한마디가 그를 제정신으로 되돌려 놓았고, 그러면 그는 가엾은 머리를 그녀의 가슴에 기댄 채 플로이에게 자기 꿈 이야기를 들려주며 미소 지었다."]], [["When day began to dawn again, he watched for the sun; and when its cheerful light began to sparkle in the room, he pictured to himself—pictured!", "다시 날이 밝아 오기 시작하면 그는 해를 기다렸다. 그리고 그 명랑한 빛이 방 안에서 반짝이기 시작하면, 그는 마음속으로 그려 보았다—그려 보았다니!"], ["he saw—the high church towers rising up into the morning sky, the town reviving, waking, starting into life once more, the river glistening as it rolled (but rolling fast as ever), and the country bright with dew.", "그는 보았다—높은 교회 첨탑들이 아침 하늘로 솟아오르는 모습을, 도시가 되살아나 깨어나며 다시 한번 생기로 화들짝 일어서는 모습을, 강이 굽이치며 반짝이는 모습을(그러나 여전히 빠르게 굽이쳐 흐르는 모습을), 그리고 이슬로 환히 빛나는 들판을. 낯익은 소리와 외침이 차츰 아래 거리로 들려왔다."], ["Familiar sounds and cries came by degrees into the street below; the servants in the house were roused and busy; faces looked in at the door, and voices asked his attendants softly how he was.", "집 안의 하인들은 깨어나 분주했고, 얼굴들이 문 안을 들여다보았으며, 목소리들이 그의 시중드는 이들에게 그가 어떠한지 가만히 물었다. 폴은 늘 자기가 직접 대답했다."], ["Paul always answered for himself, “I am better.", "\"저는 좀 나아졌어요. 훨씬 나아졌어요, 고마워요!"], ["I am a great deal better, thank you! Tell Papa so!”", "아빠한테도 그렇게 말해 줘요!\""]], [["By little and little, he got tired of the bustle of the day, the noise of carriages and carts, and people passing and repassing; and would fall asleep, or be troubled with a restless and uneasy sense again—the child could hardly tell whether this were in his sleeping or his waking moments—of that rushing river.", "조금씩, 그는 낮의 부산함에, 마차와 짐수레의 소음에, 오가는 사람들에 지쳐 갔다. 그러면 잠이 들거나, 다시금 그 거센 강에 대한 불안하고 편치 않은 느낌에 시달렸다—그것이 잠든 사이의 일인지 깨어 있는 동안의 일인지 아이로서는 도무지 분간할 수 없었다."], ["“Why, will it never stop, Floy?”", "\"플로이, 저건 왜 멈추질 않지?\""], ["he would sometimes ask her.", "그는 가끔 그녀에게 물었다."], ["“It is bearing me away, I think!”", "\"날 떠내려보내는 것 같아!\""]], [["But Floy could always soothe and reassure him; and it was his daily delight to make her lay her head down on his pillow, and take some rest.", "그러나 플로이는 언제나 그를 달래고 안심시킬 수 있었다. 그리고 그녀에게 그의 베개에 머리를 누이고 잠시 쉬게 하는 것이 그의 날마다의 기쁨이었다."]], [["“You are always watching me, Floy, let me watch you, now!”", "\"넌 늘 날 지켜보잖아, 플로이, 이번엔 내가 널 지켜보게 해 줘!\""], ["They would prop him up with cushions in a corner of his bed, and there he would recline the while she lay beside him: bending forward oftentimes to kiss her, and whispering to those who were near that she was tired, and how she had sat up so many nights beside him.", "그러면 사람들은 그를 침대 한구석에 쿠션으로 받쳐 일으켜 앉혔고, 그는 그녀가 곁에 누워 있는 동안 거기 기대어 있었다. 이따금 몸을 앞으로 굽혀 그녀에게 입을 맞추고, 가까이 있는 이들에게 그녀가 지쳤다고, 그녀가 자기 곁에서 얼마나 여러 밤을 새웠는지를 속삭이면서."]], [["Thus, the flush of the day, in its heat and light, would gradually decline; and again the golden water would be dancing on the wall.", "이렇게 낮의 열기와 빛 속에서 붉게 달아오른 하루가 차츰 기울어 갔다. 그리고 다시 금빛 물결이 벽 위에서 춤을 추곤 했다."]], [["He was visited by as many as three grave doctors—they used to assemble downstairs, and come up together—and the room was so quiet, and Paul was so observant of them (though he never asked of anybody what they said), that he even knew the difference in the sound of their watches.", "그를 찾아오는 근엄한 의사가 셋이나 되었는데—그들은 아래층에 모였다가 함께 올라오곤 했다—방이 어찌나 조용하고 폴이 그들을 어찌나 유심히 살피던지(다만 그들이 무슨 말을 하는지는 누구에게도 묻지 않았다), 그는 그들의 회중시계 소리의 차이까지 알아챌 정도였다."], ["But his interest centred in Sir Parker Peps, who always took his seat on the side of the bed.", "그러나 그의 관심은 파커 펩스 경에게 쏠려 있었는데, 그분은 늘 침대 한쪽에 자리를 잡았다."], ["For Paul had heard them say long ago, that that gentleman had been with his Mama when she clasped Florence in her arms, and died.", "폴은 오래전에 그들이 하는 말을 들은 적이 있었다. 그분이 자기 엄마가 플로렌스를 품에 안고 숨을 거두던 그 자리에 함께 있었다는 것을."], ["And he could not forget it, now.", "그리고 그는 이제 그것을 잊을 수가 없었다."], ["He liked him for it.", "그는 그 일로 그분을 좋아했다."], ["He was not afraid.", "그는 두렵지 않았다."]], [["The people round him changed as unaccountably as on that first night at Doctor Blimber’s—except Florence; Florence never changed—and what had been Sir Parker Peps, was now his father, sitting with his head upon his hand.", "그를 둘러싼 사람들은 블림버 박사네에서의 그 첫날 밤처럼 까닭 모르게 바뀌어 갔다—플로렌스만은 예외였다. 플로렌스는 결코 바뀌지 않았다—그리고 파커 펩스 경이었던 것이 이제는 한 손에 머리를 괸 채 앉아 있는 그의 아버지가 되어 있었다."], ["Old Mrs Pipchin dozing in an easy chair, often changed to Miss Tox, or his aunt; and Paul was quite content to shut his eyes again, and see what happened next, without emotion.", "안락의자에서 졸고 있던 늙은 피프친 부인은 종종 톡스 양으로, 혹은 그의 고모로 바뀌었다. 그래도 폴은 다시 눈을 감고 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어지는지를 아무 감정 없이 바라보는 것으로 흡족했다."], ["But this figure with its head upon its hand returned so often, and remained so long, and sat so still and solemn, never speaking, never being spoken to, and rarely lifting up its face, that Paul began to wonder languidly, if it were real; and in the night-time saw it sitting there, with fear.", "그러나 손에 머리를 괸 이 형상은 어찌나 자주 돌아오고, 어찌나 오래 머물며, 어찌나 가만히 그리고 엄숙하게 앉아, 말하는 일도 없고 누가 말을 거는 일도 없으며, 좀처럼 얼굴을 들지 않던지, 폴은 나른하게 그것이 진짜일까 궁금해하기 시작했다. 그리고 밤이 되면 그것이 거기 앉아 있는 것을 두려움에 차서 바라보았다."]], [["“Floy!”", "\"플로이!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“What is that?”", "\"저게 뭐야?\""]], [["“Where, dearest?”", "\"어디, 우리 아가?\""]], [["“There!", "\"저기!"], ["at the bottom of the bed.”", "침대 발치에.\""]], [["“There’s nothing there, except Papa!”", "\"저기엔 아무것도 없어, 아빠밖에는!\""]], [["The figure lifted up its head, and rose, and coming to the bedside, said: “My own boy!", "그 형상은 머리를 들고 일어나, 침대맡으로 다가와 말했다. \"내 아들아!"], ["Don’t you know me?”", "날 모르겠느냐?\""]], [["Paul looked it in the face, and thought, was this his father?", "폴은 그 얼굴을 들여다보며, 이것이 자기 아버지일까 생각했다."], ["But the face so altered to his thinking, thrilled while he gazed, as if it were in pain; and before he could reach out both his hands to take it between them, and draw it towards him, the figure turned away quickly from the little bed, and went out at the door.", "그러나 그가 보기에 너무도 변해 버린 그 얼굴은, 바라보는 동안 마치 고통에 잠긴 듯 떨렸다. 그리고 그가 두 손을 뻗어 그 얼굴을 감싸 쥐고 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기기도 전에, 그 형상은 작은 침대에서 급히 돌아서서 문밖으로 나가 버렸다."]], [["Paul looked at Florence with a fluttering heart, but he knew what she was going to say, and stopped her with his face against her lips.", "폴은 두근거리는 마음으로 플로렌스를 바라보았으나, 그녀가 무슨 말을 하려는지 알았기에, 자기 얼굴을 그녀의 입술에 갖다 대어 그 말을 막았다."], ["The next time he observed the figure sitting at the bottom of the bed, he called to it.", "다음번에 그 형상이 침대 발치에 앉아 있는 것을 보았을 때, 그는 그것을 불렀다."]], [["“Don’t be sorry for me, dear Papa!", "\"저 때문에 슬퍼하지 마세요, 아빠!"], ["Indeed I am quite happy!”", "정말 저는 아주 행복해요!\""]], [["His father coming and bending down to him—which he did quickly, and without first pausing by the bedside—Paul held him round the neck, and repeated those words to him several times, and very earnestly; and Paul never saw him in his room again at any time, whether it were day or night, but he called out, “Don’t be sorry for me!", "그의 아버지가 다가와 그에게 몸을 굽히자—이번에는 침대맡에서 먼저 멈추지 않고 재빨리 그렇게 했다—폴은 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안고, 그 말을 여러 번, 아주 간절하게 되풀이했다. 그리고 그 뒤로 폴은 낮이든 밤이든 방 안에서 그를 볼 때마다 어김없이 외쳤다."], ["Indeed I am quite happy!”", "\"저 때문에 슬퍼하지 마세요!"], ["This was the beginning of his always saying in the morning that he was a great deal better, and that they were to tell his father so.", "정말 저는 아주 행복해요!\" 이것이 그가 아침마다 자기는 훨씬 나아졌으니 아버지께 그렇게 전해 달라고 늘 말하게 된 시초였다."]], [["How many times the golden water danced upon the wall; how many nights the dark, dark river rolled towards the sea in spite of him; Paul never counted, never sought to know.", "금빛 물결이 벽 위에서 몇 번이나 춤을 추었는지, 그 어둡고 어두운 강이 그의 뜻과 상관없이 바다를 향해 몇 밤이나 굽이쳐 흘렀는지, 폴은 결코 세지 않았고, 알려고 하지도 않았다."], ["If their kindness, or his sense of it, could have increased, they were more kind, and he more grateful every day; but whether they were many days or few, appeared of little moment now, to the gentle boy.", "만일 그들의 다정함이, 혹은 그것을 느끼는 그의 마음이 더 커질 수 있었다면, 그들은 날마다 더 다정했고 그는 날마다 더 고마워했다. 그러나 그것이 여러 날이었는지 며칠 안 되는 날이었는지는, 이제 그 온순한 소년에게는 별로 중요치 않은 듯 보였다."]], [["One night he had been thinking of his mother, and her picture in the drawing-room downstairs, and thought she must have loved sweet Florence better than his father did, to have held her in her arms when she felt that she was dying—for even he, her brother, who had such dear love for her, could have no greater wish than that.", "어느 날 밤 그는 어머니를, 그리고 아래층 응접실에 걸린 그녀의 초상화를 생각하다가, 어머니는 사랑스러운 플로렌스를 아버지보다 더 사랑하셨음에 틀림없다고 생각했다. 자신이 죽어 가고 있음을 느꼈을 때 그 아이를 품에 안으셨으니까—왜냐하면 그토록 그녀를 깊이 사랑하는 오라비인 그조차도, 그보다 더 큰 소망은 가질 수 없었을 테니까."], ["The train of thought suggested to him to inquire if he had ever seen his mother?", "이 생각의 흐름은 자기가 어머니를 본 적이 있었던가 물어보고 싶은 마음을 불러일으켰다."], ["for he could not remember whether they had told him, yes or no, the river running very fast, and confusing his mind.", "그가 어머니를 본 적이 있다고 그들이 말해 주었는지 아닌지를 그는 기억할 수 없었는데, 강이 너무 빠르게 흘러 그의 머리를 어지럽혔기 때문이었다."]], [["“Floy, did I ever see Mama?”", "\"플로이, 내가 엄마를 본 적이 있어?\""]], [["“No, darling, why?”", "\"아니, 아가, 왜?\""]], [["“Did I ever see any kind face, like Mama’s, looking at me when I was a baby, Floy?”", "\"내가 아기였을 때 엄마 얼굴 같은, 다정한 얼굴이 날 들여다본 적이 있었어, 플로이?\""]], [["He asked, incredulously, as if he had some vision of a face before him.", "그는 마치 어떤 얼굴의 환영이 눈앞에 있기라도 한 듯, 믿기지 않는다는 투로 물었다."]], [["“Oh yes, dear!”", "\"오, 그럼, 있었지!\""]], [["“Whose, Floy?”", "\"누구 얼굴인데, 플로이?\""]], [["“Your old nurse’s.", "\"네 옛날 유모."], ["Often.”", "자주.\""]], [["“And where is my old nurse?”", "\"그럼 내 옛날 유모는 어디 있어?\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."], ["“Is she dead too?", "\"그 사람도 죽었어?"], ["Floy, are we all dead, except you?”", "플로이, 우리 모두 죽은 거야, 너만 빼고?\""]], [["There was a hurry in the room, for an instant—longer, perhaps; but it seemed no more—then all was still again; and Florence, with her face quite colourless, but smiling, held his head upon her arm.", "방 안이 잠시—어쩌면 좀 더 길었을지도 모르지만, 그래도 그 이상은 아닌 듯했다—술렁였다. 그러고는 다시 모든 것이 고요해졌다."], ["Her arm trembled very much.", "그리고 플로렌스가, 얼굴은 핏기 하나 없었으나 미소 지으며, 그의 머리를 자기 팔에 받쳐 안았다. 그녀의 팔은 몹시 떨렸다."]], [["“Show me that old nurse, Floy, if you please!”", "\"그 옛날 유모를 보여 줘, 플로이, 부탁이야!\""]], [["“She is not here, darling.", "\"지금 여기 안 계셔, 아가."], ["She shall come to-morrow.”", "내일 오실 거야.\""]], [["“Thank you, Floy!”", "\"고마워, 플로이!\""]], [["Paul closed his eyes with those words, and fell asleep.", "폴은 그 말과 함께 눈을 감고 잠이 들었다."], ["When he awoke, the sun was high, and the broad day was clear and warm.", "그가 깨어났을 때 해는 높이 떠 있었고, 환한 대낮은 맑고 따뜻했다. 그는 한동안 누워서, 활짝 열린 채 커튼이 바람에 바스락거리며 이리저리 나부끼는 창문을 바라보았다."], ["He lay a little, looking at the windows, which were open, and the curtains rustling in the air, and waving to and fro: then he said, “Floy, is it tomorrow?", "그러고는 말했다. \"플로이, 지금이 내일이야?"], ["Is she come?”", "그분 오셨어?\""]], [["Someone seemed to go in quest of her.", "누군가가 그녀를 찾으러 가는 것 같았다."], ["Perhaps it was Susan.", "아마 수전이었을 것이다."], ["Paul thought he heard her telling him when he had closed his eyes again, that she would soon be back; but he did not open them to see.", "폴이 다시 눈을 감았을 때, 그녀가 곧 돌아오겠다고 그에게 말하는 소리를 들은 것 같았으나, 그는 눈을 떠서 확인하지는 않았다."], ["She kept her word—perhaps she had never been away—but the next thing that happened was a noise of footsteps on the stairs, and then Paul woke—woke mind and body—and sat upright in his bed.", "그녀는 약속을 지켰다—어쩌면 한 번도 자리를 뜨지 않았는지도 모른다—그러나 다음으로 벌어진 일은 계단을 오르는 발걸음 소리였다. 그러자 폴이 깨어났다—마음도 몸도 깨어났다—그리고 침대에서 똑바로 일어나 앉았다."], ["He saw them now about him.", "그는 이제 자기 주위에 있는 그들이 보였다."], ["There was no grey mist before them, as there had been sometimes in the night.", "밤에 이따금 그러했던 것처럼 그들 앞을 가린 잿빛 안개는 없었다."], ["He knew them every one, and called them by their names.", "그는 그들을 하나하나 다 알아보았고, 이름을 불렀다."]], [["“And who is this?", "\"그런데 이분은 누구야?"], ["Is this my old nurse?”", "이분이 내 옛날 유모야?\""], ["said the child, regarding with a radiant smile, a figure coming in.", "아이가 안으로 들어오는 한 형상을 환한 미소로 바라보며 말했다."]], [["Yes, yes.", "그렇다, 그렇다."], ["No other stranger would have shed those tears at sight of him, and called him her dear boy, her pretty boy, her own poor blighted child.", "그를 보고 그런 눈물을 흘리며 그를 자기의 귀한 아들, 자기의 예쁜 아들, 자기의 가엾고 시들어 버린 아이라고 부를 다른 낯선 이는 없었을 것이다."], ["No other woman would have stooped down by his bed, and taken up his wasted hand, and put it to her lips and breast, as one who had some right to fondle it.", "그의 침대맡에 몸을 굽혀, 그의 야윈 손을 들어 올려 입술과 가슴에 갖다 대며, 마치 그 손을 어루만질 어떤 권리라도 가진 사람처럼 굴 다른 여인은 없었을 것이다."], ["No other woman would have so forgotten everybody there but him and Floy, and been so full of tenderness and pity.", "그곳에 있는 모든 이를 그렇게 잊어버리고 오직 그와 플로이만을 생각하며, 그토록 다정함과 연민으로 가득 찰 다른 여인은 없었을 것이다."]], [["“Floy!", "\"플로이!"], ["this is a kind good face!”", "이건 다정하고 좋은 얼굴이야!\""], ["said Paul.", "폴이 말했다."], ["“I am glad to see it again.", "\"다시 보게 되어 기뻐."], ["Don’t go away, old nurse!", "가지 마세요, 옛날 유모!"], ["Stay here.”", "여기 계세요.\""]], [["His senses were all quickened, and he heard a name he knew.", "그의 감각이 온통 또렷해졌고, 그는 자기가 아는 이름 하나를 들었다."]], [["“Who was that, who said ‘Walter’?”", "\"방금 '월터'라고 한 사람이 누구야?\""], ["he asked, looking round.", "그가 둘러보며 물었다."], ["“Someone said Walter.", "\"누가 월터라고 했어."], ["Is he here?", "그가 여기 있어?"], ["I should like to see him very much.”", "그를 정말 보고 싶어.\""]], [["Nobody replied directly; but his father soon said to Susan, “Call him back, then: let him come up!” Alter a short pause of expectation, during which he looked with smiling interest and wonder, on his nurse, and saw that she had not forgotten Floy, Walter was brought into the room. His open face and manner, and his cheerful eyes, had always made him a favourite with Paul; and when Paul saw him, he stretched Out his hand, and said “Good-bye!”", "아무도 곧바로 대답하지 않았다. 그러나 그의 아버지가 곧 수전에게 말했다. \"그럼 그 애를 다시 불러라. 올라오라고 해!\" 잠깐의 기대에 찬 침묵이 흐르는 동안, 그는 미소 띤 흥미와 경이로움으로 자기 유모를 바라보았고, 그녀가 플로이를 잊지 않았음을 알아보았다. 이윽고 월터가 방 안으로 데려와졌다. 그의 솔직한 얼굴과 태도, 그리고 그의 명랑한 눈은 늘 그를 폴이 좋아하는 사람으로 만들었다. 그리고 폴은 그를 보자 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"잘 가요!\""]], [["“Good-bye, my child!”", "\"잘 가렴, 얘야!\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, hurrying to his bed’s head.", "피프친 부인이 그의 침대맡으로 황급히 다가오며 말했다."], ["“Not good-bye?”", "\"잘 가라는 건 아니지?\""]], [["For an instant, Paul looked at her with the wistful face with which he had so often gazed upon her in his corner by the fire.", "한순간 폴은, 난롯가 자기 구석에서 그토록 자주 그녀를 바라보던 그 애틋한 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보았다. \"맞아요,\""], ["“Yes,” he said placidly, “good-bye!", "그가 차분하게 말했다, \"잘 가요!"], ["Walter dear, good-bye!”—turning his head to where he stood, and putting out his hand again.", "월터, 잘 가요!\"—그가 서 있는 쪽으로 머리를 돌리고 다시 손을 내밀면서."], ["“Where is Papa?”", "\"아빠는 어디 있어요?\""]], [["He felt his father’s breath upon his cheek, before the words had parted from his lips.", "그 말이 입술에서 떨어지기도 전에 그는 자기 뺨에 닿는 아버지의 숨결을 느꼈다."]], [["“Remember Walter, dear Papa,”", "\"월터를 기억해 주세요, 아빠,\""], ["he whispered, looking in his face.", "그가 아버지의 얼굴을 들여다보며 속삭였다."], ["“Remember Walter.", "\"월터를 기억해 주세요."], ["I was fond of Walter!”", "저는 월터가 좋았어요!\""], ["The feeble hand waved in the air, as if it cried “good-bye!”", "그 가냘픈 손이 허공에서 흔들렸다, 마치 월터에게 다시 한번 \"잘 가요!\""], ["to Walter once again.", "하고 외치기라도 하듯."]], [["“Now lay me down,”", "\"이제 날 눕혀 줘,\""], ["he said, “and, Floy, come close to me, and let me see you!”", "그가 말했다, \"그리고, 플로이, 내 곁에 바싹 와서, 너를 보게 해 줘!\""]], [["Sister and brother wound their arms around each other, and the golden light came streaming in, and fell upon them, locked together.", "누이와 동생은 서로의 몸을 두 팔로 휘감았고, 금빛 햇살이 흘러들어 와, 꼭 끌어안은 두 사람 위로 내려앉았다."]], [["“How fast the river runs, between its green banks and the rushes, Floy!", "\"초록 강둑과 갈대 사이로 강이 얼마나 빠르게 흐르는지, 플로이!"], ["But it’s very near the sea.", "하지만 바다가 아주 가까워."], ["I hear the waves!", "파도 소리가 들려!"], ["They always said so!”", "그들이 늘 그랬어!\""]], [["Presently he told her the motion of the boat upon the stream was lulling him to rest.", "이윽고 그는 그녀에게, 강물 위 배의 흔들림이 자기를 잠재워 쉬게 한다고 말했다."], ["How green the banks were now, how bright the flowers growing on them, and how tall the rushes!", "강둑은 이제 얼마나 푸른지, 그 위에 핀 꽃들은 얼마나 환한지, 갈대는 얼마나 키가 큰지!"], ["Now the boat was out at sea, but gliding smoothly on.", "이제 배는 바다로 나갔으나, 매끄럽게 미끄러져 나아갔다."], ["And now there was a shore before him.", "그리고 이제 그의 앞에 바닷가가 펼쳐졌다."], ["Who stood on the bank?—", "강둑 위에 선 이는 누구일까?—"]], [["He put his hands together, as he had been used to do at his prayers.", "그는 기도할 때 늘 하던 것처럼 두 손을 모았다. 그는 그렇게 하려고 팔을 떼지 않았다."], ["He did not remove his arms to do it; but they saw him fold them so, behind her neck.", "그러나 사람들은 그가 그녀의 목 뒤에서 두 손을 그렇게 모으는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Mama is like you, Floy.", "\"엄마는 너와 닮았어, 플로이."], ["I know her by the face!", "얼굴을 보니 알겠어!"], ["But tell them that the print upon the stairs at school is not divine enough.", "하지만 사람들한테 전해 줘, 학교 계단에 걸린 그 그림은 충분히 거룩하지 않다고."], ["The light about the head is shining on me as I go!”", "머리 둘레의 빛이 내가 가는 길을 비추고 있어!\""]], [["The golden ripple on the wall came back again, and nothing else stirred in the room.", "벽 위의 금빛 잔물결이 다시 돌아왔고, 방 안에서는 그 밖에 아무것도 움직이지 않았다."], ["The old, old fashion!", "그 오래되고 오래된 관습이여!"], ["The fashion that came in with our first garments, and will last unchanged until our race has run its course, and the wide firmament is rolled up like a scroll.", "우리가 처음 옷을 입던 때 함께 들어와, 우리 인류가 그 행로를 다하고 넓은 창공이 두루마리처럼 말려 올라갈 때까지 변함없이 이어질 그 관습이여."], ["The old, old fashion—Death!", "그 오래되고 오래된 관습—죽음이여!"]], [["Oh thank GOD, all who see it, for that older fashion yet, of Immortality!", "오, 그것을 보는 모든 이여, 더욱 오래된 또 하나의 관습, 영생의 관습을 두고 하느님께 감사드릴지어다!"], ["And look upon us, angels of young children, with regards not quite estranged, when the swift river bears us to the ocean!", "그리고 어린아이들의 천사들이여, 빠른 강물이 우리를 대양으로 실어 갈 때, 아주 멀어지지만은 않은 눈길로 우리를 굽어보소서!"]], [["“Dear me, dear me! To think,”", "\"어머나 세상에, 어머나 세상에!"], ["said Miss Tox, bursting out afresh that night, as if her heart were broken, “that Dombey and Son should be a Daughter after all!”", "생각 좀 해 봐,\" 톡스 양이 그날 밤 마치 가슴이 미어지는 듯 다시 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다, \"돔비와 아들이 결국은 돔비와 딸이었다니 말이야!\""]], [["# CHAPTER XVII. Captain Cuttle does a little Business for the Young People", "# 제12장"]], [["Captain Cuttle, in the exercise of that surprising talent for deep-laid and unfathomable scheming, with which (as is not unusual in men of transparent simplicity) he sincerely believed himself to be endowed by nature, had gone to Mr Dombey’s house on the eventful Sunday, winking all the way as a vent for his superfluous sagacity, and had presented himself in the full lustre of the ankle-jacks before the eyes of Towlinson.", "커틀 선장은 깊고 헤아릴 수 없는 음모를 꾸미는 그 놀라운 재능—투명할 만큼 단순한 사람들에게 흔히 그러하듯, 그도 천성적으로 그 재능을 타고났다고 진심으로 믿고 있던 그 재능—을 발휘하여, 그 운명적인 일요일에 돔비 씨의 집을 찾아갔다."], ["Hearing from that individual, to his great concern, of the impending calamity, Captain Cuttle, in his delicacy, sheered off again confounded; merely handing in the nosegay as a small mark of his solicitude, and leaving his respectful compliments for the family in general, which he accompanied with an expression of his hope that they would lay their heads well to the wind under existing circumstances, and a friendly intimation that he would “look up again”", "가는 내내 넘쳐흐르는 슬기로움을 발산하느라 연신 한쪽 눈을 찡긋거리며, 그는 발목까지 오는 반장화의 광채를 온전히 빛내며 타울린슨의 눈앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 그 사람에게서 닥쳐오는 재앙에 관한 소식을 듣고 크게 마음이 쓰인 커틀 선장은, 특유의 섬세함으로 당황한 채 다시 뱃머리를 돌렸다."], ["to-morrow.", "그저 자신의 관심의 자그마한 표시로 꽃다발을 건네고, 가족 전체에게 정중한 안부를 남겼는데, 그 안부에는 지금 같은 상황에서 머리를 바람 쪽으로 잘 두기를 바란다는 표현과, 내일 다시 \"올라와 보겠다\"는 다정한 언질이 곁들여졌다."]], [["The Captain’s compliments were never heard of any more.", "선장의 안부 인사는 그 뒤로 다시는 누구의 귀에도 들어가지 않았다."], ["The Captain’s nosegay, after lying in the hall all night, was swept into the dust-bin next morning; and the Captain’s sly arrangement, involved in one catastrophe with greater hopes and loftier designs, was crushed to pieces.", "선장의 꽃다발은 밤새 현관에 놓여 있다가 이튿날 아침 쓰레기통으로 쓸려 들어갔다. 그리고 더 큰 희망과 더 드높은 계획과 함께 한 재앙에 휘말린 선장의 은밀한 책략도 산산조각이 났다."], ["So, when an avalanche bears down a mountain-forest, twigs and bushes suffer with the trees, and all perish together.", "그렇게, 눈사태가 산속의 숲을 덮칠 때 잔가지와 덤불도 큰 나무들과 함께 화를 입고, 모두 함께 스러지는 법이다."]], [["When Walter returned home on the Sunday evening from his long walk, and its memorable close, he was too much occupied at first by the tidings he had to give them, and by the emotions naturally awakened in his breast by the scene through which he had passed, to observe either that his Uncle was evidently unacquainted with the intelligence the Captain had undertaken to impart, or that the Captain made signals with his hook, warning him to avoid the subject.", "월터가 일요일 저녁에 그 긴 산책과 그 잊지 못할 마무리를 끝내고 집으로 돌아왔을 때, 그는 처음에는 사람들에게 전해야 할 소식과, 자신이 겪고 지나온 그 장면 때문에 가슴속에 자연스레 일어난 감정에 너무 사로잡혀 있어서, 자신의 외삼촌이 선장이 전하기로 한 그 소식을 분명히 모르고 있다는 것도, 또 선장이 갈고리로 신호를 보내며 그 화제를 피하라고 경고하고 있다는 것도 알아차리지 못했다."], ["Not that the Captain’s signals were calculated to have proved very comprehensible, however attentively observed; for, like those Chinese sages who are said in their conferences to write certain learned words in the air that are wholly impossible of pronunciation, the Captain made such waves and flourishes as nobody without a previous knowledge of his mystery, would have been at all likely to understand.", "하기야 아무리 주의 깊게 살폈더라도 선장의 신호가 그리 알아듣기 쉬웠을 것 같지는 않다. 회담을 할 때 발음이 도무지 불가능한 어떤 학식 있는 글자들을 허공에 쓴다는 그 중국 현인들처럼, 선장은 그의 비법을 미리 알지 못하는 사람이라면 도무지 이해할 법하지 않은 그런 물결무늬와 장식적인 동작들을 만들어 보였던 것이다."]], [["Captain Cuttle, however, becoming cognisant of what had happened, relinquished these attempts, as he perceived the slender chance that now existed of his being able to obtain a little easy chat with Mr Dombey before the period of Walter’s departure.", "그러나 커틀 선장은 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알아차리게 되자, 월터가 떠나기 전에 돔비 씨와 약간의 편안한 한담을 나눌 수 있을 가능성이 이제 희박하다는 것을 깨닫고 그런 시도들을 그만두었다."], ["But in admitting to himself, with a disappointed and crestfallen countenance, that Sol Gills must be told, and that Walter must go—taking the case for the present as he found it, and not having it enlightened or improved beforehand by the knowing management of a friend—the Captain still felt an unabated confidence that he, Ned Cuttle, was the man for Mr Dombey; and that, to set Walter’s fortunes quite square, nothing was wanted but that they two should come together.", "그러나 솔 길스에게 사정을 알려야 하고 월터는 떠나야 한다는 것을—친구의 노련한 솜씨로 미리 사정을 밝히거나 개선해 두지 못한 채, 지금으로서는 닥친 그대로 받아들여야 한다는 것을—실망하고 풀이 죽은 얼굴로 스스로 인정하면서도, 선장은 자기, 네드 커틀이야말로 돔비 씨를 다룰 적임자라는 변함없는 확신을 여전히 품고 있었다. 그리고 월터의 앞날을 완전히 바로잡으려면 오직 그 두 사람이 함께 만나기만 하면 된다고 믿었다."], ["For the Captain never could forget how well he and Mr Dombey had got on at Brighton; with what nicety each of them had put in a word when it was wanted; how exactly they had taken one another’s measure; nor how Ned Cuttle had pointed out that resources in the first extremity, and had brought the interview to the desired termination.", "선장은 자기와 돔비 씨가 브라이턴에서 얼마나 잘 어울렸는지, 필요할 때 둘 다 얼마나 절묘하게 한마디씩 거들었는지, 서로의 됨됨이를 얼마나 정확히 가늠했는지, 그리고 네드 커틀이 절체절명의 순간에 그 해결책을 어떻게 제시하여 그 회담을 바라던 결말로 이끌었는지를 결코 잊을 수 없었던 것이다."], ["On all these grounds the Captain soothed himself with thinking that though Ned Cuttle was forced by the pressure of events to “stand by” almost useless for the present, Ned would fetch up with a wet sail in good time, and carry all before him.", "이 모든 근거에서 선장은, 비록 네드 커틀이 사태의 압박에 떠밀려 지금으로서는 거의 쓸모없이 \"대기\"해야 할 처지이긴 하나, 때가 되면 돛을 적셔 단숨에 달려와 모든 것을 휩쓸어 버리리라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로했다."]], [["Under the influence of this good-natured delusion, Captain Cuttle even went so far as to revolve in his own bosom, while he sat looking at Walter and listening with a tear on his shirt-collar to what he related, whether it might not be at once genteel and politic to give Mr Dombey a verbal invitation, whenever they should meet, to come and cut his mutton in Brig Place on some day of his own naming, and enter on the question of his young friend’s prospects over a social glass.", "이 선량한 망상의 영향 아래, 커틀 선장은 월터를 바라보며 그가 들려주는 이야기에 셔츠 깃에 눈물 한 방울을 떨구며 귀를 기울이고 앉아 있는 동안, 마음속으로 이런 궁리까지 했다. 두 사람이 만나게 되면 언제고 돔비 씨에게 자기가 정한 날에 브리그 플레이스로 와서 양고기 한 점을 들고, 다정한 술잔을 앞에 두고 젊은 친구의 앞날 문제를 논의하자고 직접 입으로 청하는 것이, 점잖으면서도 동시에 영리한 일이 아닐까 하는 것이었다."], ["But the uncertain temper of Mrs MacStinger, and the possibility of her setting up her rest in the passage during such an entertainment, and there delivering some homily of an uncomplimentary nature, operated as a check on the Captain’s hospitable thoughts, and rendered him timid of giving them encouragement.", "그러나 맥스팅거 부인의 변덕스러운 성미와, 그런 접대를 하는 동안 그녀가 복도에 진을 치고 앉아 거기서 무언가 칭찬과는 거리가 먼 설교를 늘어놓을 가능성이, 선장의 환대하려는 생각에 제동을 걸어, 그런 생각을 부추기기를 주저하게 만들었다."]], [["One fact was quite clear to the Captain, as Walter, sitting thoughtfully over his untasted dinner, dwelt on all that had happened; namely, that however Walter’s modesty might stand in the way of his perceiving it himself, he was, as one might say, a member of Mr Dombey’s family.", "월터가 손도 대지 않은 저녁밥을 앞에 두고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 그동안 일어난 모든 일을 곱씹는 동안, 선장에게는 한 가지 사실만은 아주 분명했다. 곧, 월터의 겸손함이 스스로 그것을 깨닫는 데 걸림돌이 될지언정, 그는 말하자면 돔비 씨 가족의 일원이라는 사실이었다."], ["He had been, in his own person, connected with the incident he so pathetically described; he had been by name remembered and commended in close association with it; and his fortunes must have a particular interest in his employer’s eyes.", "그가 그토록 애처롭게 묘사한 그 사건에 직접 연루되어 있었고, 그 사건과 긴밀히 연관되어 이름으로 기억되고 칭찬받았으니, 그의 앞날은 고용주의 눈에 특별한 관심거리일 수밖에 없었다."], ["If the Captain had any lurking doubt whatever of his own conclusions, he had not the least doubt that they were good conclusions for the peace of mind of the Instrument-maker.", "선장에게 자기 결론에 대해 조금이라도 숨은 의심이 있었다 하더라도, 그 결론이 악기 제조공의 마음의 평안을 위해서는 좋은 결론이라는 데에는 전혀 의심이 없었다."], ["Therefore he availed himself of so favourable a moment for breaking the West Indian intelligence to his friend, as a piece of extraordinary preferment; declaring that for his part he would freely give a hundred thousand pounds (if he had it) for Walter’s gain in the long-run, and that he had no doubt such an investment would yield a handsome premium.", "그래서 그는 이토록 적절한 순간을 틈타 그 서인도 제도 소식을 친구에게 대단한 영전이라도 되는 양 전했다. 자기로서는 (있기만 하다면) 월터의 장기적인 이익을 위해 십만 파운드라도 기꺼이 내놓겠으며, 그런 투자라면 틀림없이 두둑한 이문을 낳으리라고 단언하면서."]], [["Solomon Gills was at first stunned by the communication, which fell upon the little back-parlour like a thunderbolt, and tore up the hearth savagely.", "솔로몬 길스는 처음에는 그 소식에 망연자실했다. 그 소식은 작은 뒷거실에 벼락처럼 떨어져 난롯가를 사납게 헤집어 놓았던 것이다."], ["But the Captain flashed such golden prospects before his dim sight: hinted so mysteriously at Whittingtonian consequences; laid such emphasis on what Walter had just now told them: and appealed to it so confidently as a corroboration of his predictions, and a great advance towards the realisation of the romantic legend of Lovely Peg: that he bewildered the old man.", "그러나 선장이 그의 흐릿한 눈앞에 그토록 황금빛 전망을 번쩍여 보이고, 휘팅턴 식의 결말을 그토록 신비롭게 암시하고, 월터가 방금 들려준 이야기에 그토록 힘주어 강조하며, 그것을 자기 예언의 입증이자 '어여쁜 페그'라는 낭만적 전설의 실현을 향한 큰 진전이라며 그토록 확신에 차서 끌어다 대니, 노인은 어리둥절해지고 말았다."], ["Walter, for his part, feigned to be so full of hope and ardour, and so sure of coming home again soon, and backed up the Captain with such expressive shakings of his head and rubbings of his hands, that Solomon, looking first at him then at Captain Cuttle, began to think he ought to be transported with joy.", "월터로 말하자면, 희망과 열정에 가득 찬 척, 곧 다시 집으로 돌아오리라 확신하는 척하며, 머리를 의미심장하게 흔들고 두 손을 비비대며 선장을 거들었으니, 솔로몬은 처음에는 월터를, 다음에는 커틀 선장을 번갈아 바라보며, 자기가 마땅히 기쁨에 겨워해야 하는 게 아닌가 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["“But I’m behind the time, you understand,”", "\"그렇지만 나는, 알다시피, 시대에 뒤처진 사람이라서,\""], ["he observed in apology, passing his hand nervously down the whole row of bright buttons on his coat, and then up again, as if they were beads and he were telling them twice over: “and I would rather have my dear boy here.", "그가 변명하듯 말하며, 외투의 반짝이는 단추들을 한 줄로 죽 따라 초조하게 손을 쓸어내렸다가 다시 쓸어 올렸다. 마치 그것이 묵주 알이고 자기가 그것을 두 번 세고 있기라도 한 것처럼."], ["It’s an old-fashioned notion, I daresay.", "\"차라리 내 사랑하는 아이를 곁에 두고 싶구나. 구식 생각이겠지, 아마."], ["He was always fond of the sea He’s”—and he looked wistfully at Walter—“he’s glad to go.”", "저 애는 늘 바다를 좋아했어. 저 애는\"—그러고는 애틋하게 월터를 바라보았다—\"저 애는 가게 되어 기뻐하지.\""]], [["“Uncle Sol!”", "\"솔 삼촌!\""], ["cried Walter, quickly, “if you say that, I won’t go.", "월터가 다급히 외쳤다. \"그렇게 말씀하시면, 저 안 갈래요."], ["No, Captain Cuttle, I won’t.", "안 가요, 커틀 선장님, 안 갑니다."], ["If my Uncle thinks I could be glad to leave him, though I was going to be made Governor of all the Islands in the West Indies, that’s enough.", "제가 서인도 제도 섬들 전부의 총독이 된다 해도, 삼촌이 제가 삼촌을 두고 떠나는 걸 기뻐할 수 있다고 생각하신다면, 그걸로 충분합니다."], ["I’m a fixture.”", "저는 여기 못 박힌 듯 있을 거예요.\""]], [["“Wal”r, my lad,”", "\"월터, 얘야,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Steady!", "\"침착하게!"], ["Sol Gills, take an observation of your nevy.”", "솔 길스, 자네 조카를 한번 관찰해 보게.\""]], [["Following with his eyes the majestic action of the Captain’s hook, the old man looked at Walter.", "선장의 갈고리가 그리는 위풍당당한 동작을 눈으로 좇으며, 노인은 월터를 바라보았다."]], [["“Here is a certain craft,”", "\"여기 어떤 배 한 척이,\""], ["said the Captain, with a magnificent sense of the allegory into which he was soaring, “a-going to put out on a certain voyage.", "선장이 자기가 솟아오르고 있는 그 비유에 대한 웅대한 자부심을 품고 말했다, \"어떤 항해를 떠나려 하고 있네."], ["What name is wrote upon that craft indelibly?", "그 배에 지워지지 않게 쓰인 이름이 무엇인가?"], ["Is it The Gay?", "'게이' 호인가?"], ["or,”", "아니면,\""], ["said the Captain, raising his voice as much as to say, observe the point of this, “is it The Gills?”", "선장이 이 대목에 주목하라는 듯 목소리를 높여 말했다, \"'길스' 호인가?\""]], [["“Ned,”", "\"네드,\""], ["said the old man, drawing Walter to his side, and taking his arm tenderly through his, “I know.", "노인이 월터를 자기 곁으로 끌어당기며, 그의 팔을 다정하게 자기 팔에 끼고 말했다, \"나도 알아."], ["I know.", "알고말고."], ["Of course I know that Wally considers me more than himself always.", "물론 월리가 늘 자기보다 나를 더 위한다는 거 알지. 그게 내 마음에 걸리는 거야."], ["That’s in my mind. When I say he is glad to go, I mean I hope he is.", "저 애가 가게 되어 기뻐한다고 내가 말할 때, 그건 그러기를 바란다는 뜻이지."], ["Eh?", "응?"], ["look you, Ned and you too, Wally, my dear, this is new and unexpected to me; and I’m afraid my being behind the time, and poor, is at the bottom of it.", "자, 보게, 네드, 그리고 너도, 월리, 얘야, 이건 나한테 새롭고 뜻밖의 일이야. 그리고 내가 시대에 뒤처지고 가난한 게 이 일의 밑바탕에 있는 게 아닐까 두렵구나."], ["Is it really good fortune for him, do you tell me, now?”", "정말로 저 애한테 좋은 일인가, 자네들 말해 주게, 응?\""], ["said the old man, looking anxiously from one to the other.", "노인이 이쪽저쪽을 근심스레 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Really and truly?", "\"정말로, 진실로?"], ["Is it?", "그런가?"], ["I can reconcile myself to almost anything that advances Wally, but I won’t have Wally putting himself at any disadvantage for me, or keeping anything from me.", "월리한테 도움이 되는 일이라면 나는 거의 무엇에든 마음을 맞출 수 있지만, 월리가 나를 위해 자기를 불리하게 만들거나, 나한테 무얼 숨기는 건 용납 못 해."], ["You, Ned Cuttle!”", "자네, 네드 커틀!\""], ["said the old man, fastening on the Captain, to the manifest confusion of that diplomatist; “are you dealing plainly by your old friend?", "노인이 선장에게 다그쳐 들자, 그 외교가는 보란 듯이 당황했다. \"오랜 친구를 정직하게 대하고 있는 건가?"], ["Speak out, Ned Cuttle.", "솔직히 말해 보게, 네드 커틀."], ["Is there anything behind?", "뒤에 무슨 사정이 있나?"], ["Ought he to go?", "저 애가 마땅히 가야 하나?"], ["How do you know it first, and why?”", "자네는 그걸 어떻게 먼저 알았으며, 또 왜인가?\""]], [["As it was a contest of affection and self-denial, Walter struck in with infinite effect, to the Captain’s relief; and between them they tolerably reconciled old Sol Gills, by continued talking, to the project; or rather so confused him, that nothing, not even the pain of separation, was distinctly clear to his mind.", "그것이 애정과 자기희생의 겨룸이었던 만큼, 월터가 무한한 효과를 발휘하며 끼어들어 선장의 짐을 덜어 주었다. 그리고 둘이서 계속 이야기를 늘어놓아 늙은 솔 길스를 그 계획에 그런대로 수긍시켰다. 아니, 차라리 그를 어찌나 혼란스럽게 만들었던지, 그 무엇도, 심지어 이별의 아픔조차도 그의 마음에 또렷이 잡히지 않게 되었다."]], [["He had not much time to balance the matter; for on the very next day, Walter received from Mr Carker the Manager, the necessary credentials for his passage and outfit, together with the information that the Son and Heir would sail in a fortnight, or within a day or two afterwards at latest. In the hurry of preparation: which Walter purposely enhanced as much as possible: the old man lost what little self-possession he ever had; and so the time of departure drew on rapidly.", "그에게는 그 문제를 저울질할 시간이 그리 많지 않았다. 바로 이튿날 월터가 지배인 카커 씨로부터 항해와 채비에 필요한 증서들과 함께, '아들과 상속자' 호가 보름 후에, 늦어도 그로부터 하루 이틀 안에 출항하리라는 통지를 받았기 때문이다. 월터가 일부러 한껏 부풀린 준비의 분주함 속에서, 노인은 그나마 지니고 있던 얼마 안 되는 침착함마저 잃어버렸다. 그렇게 출항의 때가 빠르게 다가왔다."]], [["The Captain, who did not fail to make himself acquainted with all that passed, through inquiries of Walter from day to day, found the time still tending on towards his going away, without any occasion offering itself, or seeming likely to offer itself, for a better understanding of his position.", "날마다 월터에게 캐물어 일어나는 모든 일을 빠짐없이 알아내던 선장은, 자기 처지를 더 잘 이해할 기회가 나타나거나 나타날 듯한 기미도 없이 시간이 자꾸만 그가 떠나는 쪽으로 흘러가는 것을 보았다."], ["It was after much consideration of this fact, and much pondering over such an unfortunate combination of circumstances, that a bright idea occurred to the Captain.", "이 사실을 한참 고민하고, 이렇게 불운하게 얽힌 정황을 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 선장에게 한 가지 묘안이 떠올랐다."], ["Suppose he made a call on Mr Carker, and tried to find out from him how the land really lay!", "카커 씨를 방문해서, 그에게서 사정이 실제로 어떻게 돌아가는지를 알아내 보면 어떨까!"]], [["Captain Cuttle liked this idea very much.", "커틀 선장은 이 생각이 무척 마음에 들었다."], ["It came upon him in a moment of inspiration, as he was smoking an early pipe in Brig Place after breakfast; and it was worthy of the tobacco.", "그것은 그가 아침 식사 후 브리그 플레이스에서 이른 담배를 피우고 있을 때 영감의 순간에 찾아왔다. 그것은 그 담배에 값할 만한 생각이었다."], ["It would quiet his conscience, which was an honest one, and was made a little uneasy by what Walter had confided to him, and what Sol Gills had said; and it would be a deep, shrewd act of friendship.", "그것은 월터가 그에게 털어놓은 것과 솔 길스가 한 말 때문에 다소 불편해진, 그래도 정직한 그의 양심을 가라앉혀 줄 터였다. 그리고 그것은 깊고 빈틈없는 우정의 행위가 될 터였다."], ["He would sound Mr Carker carefully, and say much or little, just as he read that gentleman’s character, and discovered that they got on well together or the reverse.", "그는 카커 씨를 조심스레 떠보아, 그 신사의 성품을 읽고 둘이 서로 잘 통하는지 아니면 그 반대인지를 알아낸 뒤에, 그에 맞추어 많이 말하든 적게 말하든 할 작정이었다."]], [["Accordingly, without the fear of Walter before his eyes (who he knew was at home packing), Captain Cuttle again assumed his ankle-jacks and mourning brooch, and issued forth on this second expedition.", "그리하여 (집에서 짐을 꾸리고 있는 줄 아는) 월터를 눈앞에 두려워할 일 없이, 커틀 선장은 다시 발목까지 오는 반장화와 상장(喪章) 브로치를 갖추고, 이 두 번째 원정에 나섰다. 이번에는 업무를 보는 곳으로 가는 길이라 비위를 맞추는 꽃다발은 사지 않았다."], ["He purchased no propitiatory nosegay on the present occasion, as he was going to a place of business; but he put a small sunflower in his button-hole to give himself an agreeable relish of the country; and with this, and the knobby stick, and the glazed hat, bore down upon the offices of Dombey and Son.", "다만 시골의 정취를 기분 좋게 곁들이려고 단춧구멍에 작은 해바라기 한 송이를 꽂았다. 그리고 그것과 옹이진 지팡이와 번들거리는 모자를 갖추고서 돔비와 아들 회사의 사무소로 들이닥쳤다."]], [["After taking a glass of warm rum-and-water at a tavern close by, to collect his thoughts, the Captain made a rush down the court, lest its good effects should evaporate, and appeared suddenly to Mr Perch.", "가까운 선술집에서 따뜻한 럼주와 물을 한 잔 들이켜 생각을 가다듬은 뒤, 선장은 그 좋은 기운이 날아가 버릴까 봐 골목을 한달음에 달려 내려가, 퍼치 씨 앞에 불쑥 나타났다."]], [["“Matey,”", "\"여보게,\""], ["said the Captain, in persuasive accents.", "선장이 설득하는 어조로 말했다."], ["“One of your Governors is named Carker.”", "\"자네 사장님들 중에 카커라는 분이 계시지.\""]], [["Mr Perch admitted it; but gave him to understand, as in official duty bound, that all his Governors were engaged, and never expected to be disengaged any more.", "퍼치 씨는 그것을 인정했다. 그러나 직무상 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로, 자기 사장님들은 모두 일에 매여 계시고 앞으로도 결코 손이 빌 것 같지 않다는 뜻을 전했다."]], [["“Look’ee here, mate,”", "\"이보게, 여보게,\""], ["said the Captain in his ear; “my name’s Cap’en Cuttle.”", "선장이 그의 귀에 대고 말했다. \"내 이름은 커틀 선장일세.\""]], [["The Captain would have hooked Perch gently to him, but Mr Perch eluded the attempt; not so much in design, as in starting at the sudden thought that such a weapon unexpectedly exhibited to Mrs Perch might, in her then condition, be destructive to that lady’s hopes.", "선장이 퍼치를 갈고리로 살며시 끌어당기려 하자, 퍼치 씨는 그 시도를 피했다. 일부러라기보다는, 그런 무기가 퍼치 부인에게 느닷없이 보였다가는 지금 그녀의 처지로는 그녀의 희망을 결딴낼 수도 있겠다는 갑작스러운 생각에 흠칫 놀라서였다."]], [["“If you’ll be so good as just report Cap’en Cuttle here, when you get a chance,”", "\"기회가 되거든 여기 커틀 선장이 왔다고 좀 전해 주는 친절을 베풀어 주면,\""], ["said the Captain, “I’ll wait.”", "선장이 말했다, \"내 기다리겠네.\""]], [["Saying which, the Captain took his seat on Mr Perch’s bracket, and drawing out his handkerchief from the crown of the glazed hat which he jammed between his knees (without injury to its shape, for nothing human could bend it), rubbed his head well all over, and appeared refreshed.", "그러고는 선장은 퍼치 씨의 작은 선반 자리에 앉아, 두 무릎 사이에 끼워 둔 번들거리는 모자의 꼭대기에서 손수건을 끄집어내어(그 모양이 망가질 염려는 없었으니, 인간의 어떤 힘으로도 그것을 구부릴 수 없었던 까닭이다) 머리를 온통 박박 문지르고는, 한결 개운해진 듯했다."], ["He subsequently arranged his hair with his hook, and sat looking round the office, contemplating the clerks with a serene respect.", "그런 다음 갈고리로 머리를 매만지고 앉아서, 사무실을 둘러보며 사무원들을 평온한 존경의 눈길로 바라보았다."]], [["The Captain’s equanimity was so impenetrable, and he was altogether so mysterious a being, that Perch the messenger was daunted.", "선장의 평정심은 어찌나 파고들 틈이 없던지, 또 그는 도무지 신비롭기 그지없는 존재였던지라, 사환 퍼치는 기가 질렸다."]], [["“What name was it you said?”", "\"이름이 뭐라고 하셨지요?\""], ["asked Mr Perch, bending down over him as he sat on the bracket.", "퍼치 씨가, 선반에 앉은 선장 위로 몸을 굽히며 물었다."]], [["“Cap’en,”", "\"선장일세,\""], ["in a deep hoarse whisper.", "깊고 쉰 목소리로 속삭였다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Mr Perch, keeping time with his head.", "퍼치 씨가 고개로 박자를 맞추며 말했다."]], [["“Cuttle.”", "\"커틀.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["said Mr Perch, in the same tone, for he caught it, and couldn’t help it; the Captain, in his diplomacy, was so impressive.", "퍼치 씨가 같은 어조로 말했으니, 그가 그것을 옮아 따라 했고 또 어쩔 수가 없었던 것이다. 선장은 그 외교술에서 그토록 위압적이었다."], ["“I’ll see if he’s disengaged now.", "\"지금 손이 비셨는지 알아보겠습니다."], ["I don’t know.", "잘 모르겠네요."], ["Perhaps he may be for a minute.”", "잠깐 정도는 되실지도 모르죠.\""]], [["“Ay, ay, my lad, I won’t detain him longer than a minute,”", "\"그래, 그래, 여보게, 일 분 이상은 붙들지 않겠네,\""], ["said the Captain, nodding with all the weighty importance that he felt within him.", "선장이 자기 속에 품은 묵직한 중대함을 한껏 담아 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["Perch, soon returning, said, “Will Captain Cuttle walk this way?”", "퍼치가 곧 돌아와서 말했다. \"커틀 선장님, 이쪽으로 오시겠습니까?\""]], [["Mr Carker the Manager, standing on the hearth-rug before the empty fireplace, which was ornamented with a castellated sheet of brown paper, looked at the Captain as he came in, with no very special encouragement.", "지배인 카커 씨는 갈색 종이를 성벽 모양으로 오려 장식한 빈 벽난로 앞 난로 깔개 위에 서서, 들어오는 선장을 별로 특별할 것 없는 격려의 표정으로 바라보았다."]], [["“Mr Carker?”", "\"카커 씨이신가요?\""], ["said Captain Cuttle.", "커틀 선장이 말했다."]], [["“I believe so,”", "\"그런 것 같습니다만,\""], ["said Mr Carker, showing all his teeth.", "카커 씨가 이를 모조리 드러내 보이며 말했다."]], [["The Captain liked his answering with a smile; it looked pleasant.", "선장은 그가 미소로 대답하는 것이 마음에 들었다. 그것은 유쾌해 보였다."], ["“You see,”", "\"그러니까 말입니다,\" 선장이 작은 방 안을 천천히 눈으로 한 바퀴 굴리며, 셔츠 깃이 허락하는 만큼 방을 둘러보면서 입을 열었다."], ["began the Captain, rolling his eyes slowly round the little room, and taking in as much of it as his shirt-collar permitted; “I’m a seafaring man myself, Mr Carker, and Wal”r, as is on your books here, is almost a son of mine.”", "\"저 자신이 뱃사람이올시다, 카커 씨. 그리고 여기 댁의 장부에 올라 있는 월터로 말하자면, 거의 제 아들이나 다름없지요.\""]], [["“Walter Gay?”", "\"월터 게이 말입니까?\""], ["said Mr Carker, showing all his teeth again.", "카커 씨가 다시 이를 모조리 드러내 보이며 말했다."]], [["“Wal”r Gay it is,”", "\"월터 게이가 맞습니다,\""], ["replied the Captain, “right!”", "선장이 대답했다, \"옳습니다!\""], ["The Captain’s manner expressed a warm approval of Mr Carker’s quickness of perception.", "선장의 태도는 카커 씨의 눈치 빠름에 대한 따뜻한 찬사를 드러냈다."], ["“I’m a intimate friend of his and his Uncle’s.", "\"저는 그 애와 그 애 삼촌의 막역한 친구올시다."], ["Perhaps,”", "어쩌면,\""], ["said the Captain, “you may have heard your head Governor mention my name?—Captain Cuttle.”", "선장이 말했다, \"댁의 최고 사장님께서 제 이름을 언급하시는 걸 들으셨을지도 모르겠습니다만—커틀 선장 말입니다.\""]], [["“No!”", "\"아니요!\""], ["said Mr Carker, with a still wider demonstration than before.", "카커 씨가 앞서보다 한층 더 활짝 드러내 보이며 말했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그렇군요,\""], ["resumed the Captain, “I’ve the pleasure of his acquaintance.", "선장이 다시 말을 이었다, \"저는 그분과 알고 지내는 영광을 누리고 있지요."], ["I waited upon him down on the Sussex coast there, with my young friend Wal”r, when—in short, when there was a little accommodation wanted.”", "제가 젊은 친구 월터와 함께 저 서식스 해안으로 그분을 찾아뵈었습니다, 그때—한마디로, 약간의 편의가 필요했을 때 말입니다.\""], ["The Captain nodded his head in a manner that was at once comfortable, easy, and expressive.", "선장은 편안하면서도 느긋하고 또한 의미심장한 태도로 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“You remember, I daresay?”", "\"기억하시겠지요, 아마?\""]], [["“I think,”", "\"제 생각에는,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “I had the honour of arranging the business.”", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"제가 그 거래를 처리하는 영광을 누렸던 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“To be sure!”", "\"바로 그렇습니다!\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 응수했다."], ["“Right again!", "\"또 옳으십니다!"], ["you had.", "댁이 하셨지요."], ["Now I’ve took the liberty of coming here—", "자, 제가 외람되게도 여기 찾아온 것은—\""]], [["“Won’t you sit down?”", "\"앉으시지요?\""], ["said Mr Carker, smiling.", "카커 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Thank’ee,”", "\"고맙습니다,\""], ["returned the Captain, availing himself of the offer.", "선장이 그 권유를 받아들이며 응했다."], ["“A man does get more way upon himself, perhaps, in his conversation, when he sits down.", "\"사람이 앉아 있으면 이야기에 좀 더 탄력이 붙는 법이지요, 아마."], ["Won’t you take a cheer yourself?”", "댁도 의자에 앉지 그러십니까?\""]], [["“No thank you,”", "\"아니요, 괜찮습니다,\""], ["said the Manager, standing, perhaps from the force of winter habit, with his back against the chimney-piece, and looking down upon the Captain with an eye in every tooth and gum.", "지배인이 말했다. 어쩌면 겨울철 습관의 힘 때문인지, 굴뚝 장식 선반에 등을 기대고 서서, 이와 잇몸 하나하나에 눈이 박힌 듯한 눈길로 선장을 내려다보면서."], ["“You have taken the liberty, you were going to say—though it’s none—”", "\"외람되게도, 라고 말씀하시려던 참이군요—하기야 외람될 것 없습니다만—\""]], [["“Thank’ee kindly, my lad,”", "\"친절히 고맙습니다, 여보게,\""], ["returned the Captain: “of coming here, on account of my friend Wal”r.", "선장이 응수했다. \"제 친구 월터의 일로 여기 찾아왔다는 것이지요."], ["Sol Gills, his Uncle, is a man of science, and in science he may be considered a clipper; but he ain’t what I should altogether call a able seaman—not man of practice.", "그 애 삼촌 솔 길스는 학식 있는 사람이고, 학문으로 치자면 쾌속선이라 할 만하지요. 그렇지만 제가 통틀어 유능한 뱃사람이라 부를 만한 사람은 아닙니다—실무에 밝은 사람은 아니지요."], ["Wal”r is as trim a lad as ever stepped; but he’s a little down by the head in one respect, and that is, modesty.", "월터는 여태껏 갑판을 밟아 본 누구 못지않게 반듯한 녀석입니다만, 한 가지 점에서 뱃머리가 좀 처져 있는데, 그게 바로 겸손함이지요."], ["Now what I should wish to put to you,”", "그래서 제가 댁께 여쭙고 싶은 것은,\""], ["said the Captain, lowering his voice, and speaking in a kind of confidential growl, “in a friendly way, entirely between you and me, and for my own private reckoning, “till your head Governor has wore round a bit, and I can come alongside of him, is this.—Is everything right and comfortable here, and is Wal”r out’ard bound with a pretty fair wind?”", "선장이 목소리를 낮추어, 일종의 은밀한 으르렁거림으로 말했다, \"다정한 식으로, 전적으로 댁과 저 둘 사이에서, 그리고 제 개인적인 셈속으로, 댁의 최고 사장님께서 좀 방향을 바꾸셔서 제가 그분 곁에 다가갈 수 있을 때까지는 이렇습니다.—여기 모든 게 옳고 무탈한지요, 그리고 월터는 꽤 순조로운 바람을 받으며 바깥 항로로 떠나는 건지요?\""]], [["“What do you think now, Captain Cuttle?”", "\"자, 어떻게 생각하십니까, 커틀 선장님?\""], ["returned Carker, gathering up his skirts and settling himself in his position.", "카커가 옷자락을 추스르고 자세를 고쳐 잡으며 응수했다."], ["“You are a practical man; what do you think?”", "\"선장님은 실무에 밝은 분이시니, 어떻게 생각하십니까?\""]], [["The acuteness and the significance of the Captain’s eye as he cocked it in reply, no words short of those unutterable Chinese words before referred to could describe.", "선장이 그 대답으로 한쪽 눈을 치켜뜨며 보낸 눈빛의 예리함과 의미심장함은, 앞서 언급한 그 발음 불가능한 중국 글자들만큼 짧고 표현하기 어려운 말이 아니고서는 도무지 묘사할 수 없을 것이었다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"자!\""], ["said the Captain, unspeakably encouraged, “what do you say?", "선장이 이루 말할 수 없이 기운을 얻어 말했다, \"어떻게 보십니까?"], ["Am I right or wrong?”", "제가 옳습니까, 그릅니까?\""]], [["So much had the Captain expressed in his eye, emboldened and incited by Mr Carker’s smiling urbanity, that he felt himself in as fair a condition to put the question, as if he had expressed his sentiments with the utmost elaboration.", "선장은 카커 씨의 미소 띤 정중함에 용기와 자극을 받아 눈빛으로 어찌나 많은 것을 표현했던지, 자기 생각을 더없이 정성껏 말로 풀어놓았을 때나 다름없이 그 질문을 던지기에 충분한 상태라고 느꼈다."]], [["“Right,”", "\"옳습니다,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “I have no doubt.”", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"의심할 바 없지요.\""]], [["“Out’ard bound with fair weather, then, I say,”", "\"그렇다면 좋은 날씨에 바깥 항로로 떠나는 거로군요,\""], ["cried Captain Cuttle.", "커틀 선장이 외쳤다."]], [["Mr Carker smiled assent.", "카커 씨가 미소로 동의했다."]], [["“Wind right astarn, and plenty of it,”", "\"바람은 정통으로 고물 쪽에서, 그것도 넉넉히 불어 주고요,\""], ["pursued the Captain.", "선장이 이어 말했다."]], [["Mr Carker smiled assent again.", "카커 씨가 다시 미소로 동의했다."]], [["“Ay, ay!”", "\"옳거니, 옳거니!\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, greatly relieved and pleased.", "커틀 선장이 크게 안도하고 흐뭇해하며 말했다."], ["“I know’d how she headed, well enough; I told Wal”r so.", "\"그 배가 어느 쪽으로 뱃머리를 두는지 진작 잘 알고 있었지요. 월터에게도 그렇게 말했습니다."], ["Thank’ee, thank’ee.”", "고맙습니다, 고맙습니다.\""]], [["“Gay has brilliant prospects,”", "\"게이는 빛나는 앞날을 앞두고 있지요,\""], ["observed Mr Carker, stretching his mouth wider yet: “all the world before him.”", "카커 씨가 입을 한층 더 크게 벌리며 말했다, \"온 세상이 그의 것이지요.\""]], [["“All the world and his wife too, as the saying is,”", "\"속담대로, 온 세상에 그의 아내까지지요,\""], ["returned the delighted Captain.", "기뻐하는 선장이 응수했다."]], [["At the word “wife” (which he had uttered without design), the Captain stopped, cocked his eye again, and putting the glazed hat on the top of the knobby stick, gave it a twirl, and looked sideways at his always smiling friend.", "\"아내\"라는 말에(무심코 내뱉은 말이었다) 선장은 멈칫하더니, 다시 한쪽 눈을 치켜뜨고, 번들거리는 모자를 옹이진 지팡이 꼭대기에 얹어 빙그르르 돌리면서, 늘 미소 짓고 있는 친구를 곁눈질로 바라보았다."]], [["“I’d bet a gill of old Jamaica,”", "\"오래 묵은 자메이카 술 한 잔을 걸지요,\""], ["said the Captain, eyeing him attentively, “that I know what you’re a smiling at.”", "선장이 그를 유심히 바라보며 말했다, \"댁이 무얼 보고 미소 짓는지 제가 안다는 데 말입니다.\""]], [["Mr Carker took his cue, and smiled the more.", "카커 씨는 그 신호를 받아, 더욱 미소 지었다."]], [["“It goes no farther?”", "\"여기서 더 새 나가지 않겠지요?\""], ["said the Captain, making a poke at the door with the knobby stick to assure himself that it was shut.", "선장이 옹이진 지팡이로 문을 쿡 찔러 문이 닫혀 있는지 확인하며 말했다."]], [["“Not an inch,”", "\"한 치도 안 나갑니다,\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."]], [["“You’re thinking of a capital F perhaps?”", "\"혹시 대문자 F를 떠올리고 계신 건 아닙니까?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["Mr Carker didn’t deny it.", "카커 씨는 그것을 부인하지 않았다."]], [["“Anything about a L,”", "\"L에 관한 무언가는요,\""], ["said the Captain, “or a O?”", "선장이 말했다, \"아니면 O 같은 것은?\""]], [["Mr Carker still smiled.", "카커 씨는 여전히 미소 지었다."]], [["“Am I right, again?”", "\"제가 또 옳습니까?\""], ["inquired the Captain in a whisper, with the scarlet circle on his forehead swelling in his triumphant joy.", "선장이 속삭이며 물었다. 이마의 진홍빛 둥근 자국이 의기양양한 기쁨에 부풀어 오른 채로."]], [["Mr Carker, in reply, still smiling, and now nodding assent, Captain Cuttle rose and squeezed him by the hand, assuring him, warmly, that they were on the same tack, and that as for him (Cuttle) he had laid his course that way all along.", "카커 씨가 그에 답하여 여전히 미소 지으며 이제 고개를 끄덕여 동의하자, 커틀 선장은 자리에서 일어나 그의 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 둘이 같은 항로를 가고 있으며, 자기(커틀)로 말하자면 처음부터 줄곧 그쪽으로 침로를 잡아 왔노라고 따뜻하게 단언했다. \"그 애가 그 아가씨를 처음 알게 된 건,\""], ["“He know’d her first,” said the Captain, with all the secrecy and gravity that the subject demanded, “in an uncommon manner—you remember his finding her in the street when she was a’most a babby—he has liked her ever since, and she him, as much as two youngsters can.", "선장이 그 화제가 요구하는 온갖 은밀함과 진중함을 담아 말했다, \"예사롭지 않은 식이었지요—그 애가 아가씨를 거의 갓난아이였을 때 길거리에서 발견한 걸 기억하시지요—그 뒤로 줄곧 그 애는 그 아가씨를 좋아했고, 그 아가씨도 그 애를 좋아했지요, 젊은이 둘이서 그럴 수 있는 만큼 말입니다."], ["We’ve always said, Sol Gills and me, that they was cut out for each other.”", "솔 길스와 저는 늘 말해 왔습니다, 그 둘은 서로를 위해 점지된 사이라고요.\""]], [["A cat, or a monkey, or a hyena, or a death’s-head, could not have shown the Captain more teeth at one time, than Mr Carker showed him at this period of their interview.", "고양이든 원숭이든 하이에나든 해골이든, 카커 씨가 이 회견의 이 대목에서 선장에게 한꺼번에 드러내 보인 만큼 많은 이를 그에게 드러내 보일 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["“There’s a general indraught that way,”", "\"전반적으로 물길이 그쪽으로 빨려 들고 있지요,\""], ["observed the happy Captain.", "행복한 선장이 말했다."], ["“Wind and water sets in that direction, you see.", "\"바람도 물도 다 그쪽으로 들이친단 말입니다, 보세요."], ["Look at his being present t’other day!”", "그 애가 며칠 전 그 자리에 있었던 것만 봐도 그렇지요!\""]], [["“Most favourable to his hopes,”", "\"그의 희망에 더없이 유리하지요,\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Look at his being towed along in the wake of that day!”", "\"그날의 항적에 그 애가 끌려간 것만 봐도 그래요!\" 선장이 이어 말했다."], ["pursued the Captain. “Why what can cut him adrift now?”", "\"아, 이제 무엇이 그 애를 떨어져 나가게 할 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Nothing,”", "\"아무것도 없지요,\""], ["replied Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 대답했다."]], [["“You’re right again,”", "\"또 옳으십니다,\""], ["returned the Captain, giving his hand another squeeze.", "선장이 그의 손을 다시 한번 꼭 쥐며 응수했다. \"아무것도 없지요."], ["“Nothing it is.", "그렇고말고요!"], ["So! steady!", "침착하게!"], ["There’s a son gone: pretty little creetur.", "아들이 하나 떠났지요. 예쁘장한 꼬마였는데."], ["Ain’t there?”", "안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, there’s a son gone,”", "\"네, 아들이 하나 떠났지요,\""], ["said the acquiescent Carker.", "동의하는 카커가 말했다."]], [["“Pass the word, and there’s another ready for you,”", "\"한마디만 하면, 댁을 위해 또 하나 준비되어 있단 말입니다,\""], ["quoth the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Nevy of a scientific Uncle!", "\"학식 있는 삼촌의 조카!"], ["Nevy of Sol Gills!", "솔 길스의 조카!"], ["Wal”r!", "월터!"], ["Wal”r, as is already in your business!", "이미 댁의 사업에 들어와 있는 월터!"], ["And”—said the Captain, rising gradually to a quotation he was preparing for a final burst, “who—comes from Sol Gills’s daily, to your business, and your buzzums.”", "그리고\"—선장이 마지막 한 방을 위해 준비해 둔 인용구로 점차 고조되며 말했다, \"솔 길스의 집에서 날마다, 댁의 사업으로, 댁의 품으로—오는 그 애 말이지요.\""]], [["The Captain’s complacency as he gently jogged Mr Carker with his elbow, on concluding each of the foregoing short sentences, could be surpassed by nothing but the exultation with which he fell back and eyed him when he had finished this brilliant display of eloquence and sagacity; his great blue waistcoat heaving with the throes of such a masterpiece, and his nose in a state of violent inflammation from the same cause.", "앞의 짧은 문장들을 하나씩 마칠 때마다 팔꿈치로 카커 씨를 슬며시 쿡쿡 찌르던 선장의 흡족함은, 이 빛나는 웅변과 슬기로움의 과시를 끝냈을 때 몸을 뒤로 젖히며 그를 바라보던 그 의기양양함 말고는 무엇으로도 능가할 수 없는 것이었다. 그의 커다란 푸른 조끼는 그런 걸작을 낳는 진통에 들썩였고, 그의 코는 같은 이유로 격렬하게 벌게진 상태였다."]], [["“Am I right?”", "\"제가 옳습니까?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Captain Cuttle,”", "\"커틀 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Carker, bending down at the knees, for a moment, in an odd manner, as if he were falling together to hug the whole of himself at once, “your views in reference to Walter Gay are thoroughly and accurately right.", "카커 씨가 잠시 묘한 태도로 무릎을 굽히며 말했다. 마치 자기 자신을 한꺼번에 와락 끌어안으려고 몸을 한데 오그리는 듯이."], ["I understand that we speak together in confidence.", "\"월터 게이에 관한 선장님의 견해는 철두철미 정확하게 옳습니다. 우리가 비밀리에 이야기를 나누고 있다는 것으로 알고 있습니다만.\""]], [["“Honour!”", "\"명예를 걸고요!\""], ["interposed the Captain.", "선장이 끼어들었다."], ["“Not a word.”", "\"한마디도 안 합니다.\""]], [["“To him or anyone?”", "\"그에게도, 누구에게도요?\""], ["pursued the Manager.", "지배인이 이어 물었다."]], [["Captain Cuttle frowned and shook his head.", "커틀 선장은 미간을 찌푸리며 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“But merely for your own satisfaction and guidance—and guidance, of course,”", "\"다만 오로지 선장님 자신의 만족과 지침을 위해서일 뿐이지요—물론 지침이라 함은,\""], ["repeated Mr Carker, “with a view to your future proceedings.”", "카커 씨가 되풀이했다, \"앞으로의 선장님 행보를 염두에 둔 것이고요.\""]], [["“Thank’ee kindly, I am sure,”", "\"친절하시군요, 정말 고맙습니다,\""], ["said the Captain, listening with great attention.", "선장이 무척 주의 깊게 귀를 기울이며 말했다."]], [["“I have no hesitation in saying, that’s the fact.", "\"그게 사실이라고 말씀드리는 데 주저하지 않겠습니다."], ["You have hit the probabilities exactly.”", "선장님은 가능성을 정확히 짚어 내셨습니다.\""]], [["“And with regard to your head Governor,”", "\"그리고 댁의 최고 사장님에 관해서라면,\""], ["said the Captain, “why an interview had better come about nat’ral between us.", "선장이 말했다, \"그야 우리 사이에 자연스레 만남이 이루어지는 게 낫겠지요."], ["There’s time enough.”", "시간은 넉넉하니까요.\""]], [["Mr Carker, with his mouth from ear to ear, repeated, “Time enough.”", "카커 씨는 입을 양쪽 귀까지 늘어뜨린 채, \"시간은 넉넉하지요\"라고 되풀이했다."], ["Not articulating the words, but bowing his head affably, and forming them with his tongue and lips.", "다만 그 말을 소리 내어 또렷이 하지는 않고, 상냥하게 고개를 숙이며 혀와 입술로 그 말을 빚어 보일 뿐이었다."]], [["“And as I know—it’s what I always said—that Wal”r’s in a way to make his fortune,”", "\"그리고 제가 알기로는—제가 늘 말해 온 거지만—월터는 한밑천 잡을 길에 들어서 있으니까요,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“To make his fortune,”", "\"한밑천 잡을 길에 들어서 있지요,\""], ["Mr Carker repeated, in the same dumb manner.", "카커 씨가 똑같이 소리 없는 식으로 되풀이했다."]], [["“And as Wal”r’s going on this little voyage is, as I may say, in his day’s work, and a part of his general expectations here,”", "\"그리고 월터가 이 작은 항해를 떠나는 것도, 말하자면, 그 애의 일과 가운데 하나이고, 여기서 그 애가 품은 전반적인 기대의 한 부분이니까요,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Of his general expectations here,”", "\"여기서 그가 품은 전반적인 기대의 한 부분이지요,\""], ["assented Mr Carker, dumbly as before.", "카커 씨가 앞서처럼 소리 없이 수긍했다."]], [["“Why, so long as I know that,”", "\"그러니, 그것만 알면,\""], ["pursued the Captain, “there’s no hurry, and my mind’s at ease.", "선장이 이어 말했다, \"서두를 것 없고, 제 마음도 편안합니다.\""]], [["Mr Carker still blandly assenting in the same voiceless manner, Captain Cuttle was strongly confirmed in his opinion that he was one of the most agreeable men he had ever met, and that even Mr Dombey might improve himself on such a model.", "카커 씨가 여전히 같은 무언의 방식으로 부드럽게 수긍하자, 커틀 선장은 그가 자기가 여태껏 만나 본 사람들 가운데 가장 유쾌한 사람 축에 든다는, 그리고 돔비 씨조차도 그런 본보기를 따라 스스로를 갈고닦으면 좋겠다는 생각을 한층 굳혔다."], ["With great heartiness, therefore, the Captain once again extended his enormous hand (not unlike an old block in colour), and gave him a grip that left upon his smoother flesh a proof impression of the chinks and crevices with which the Captain’s palm was liberally tattooed.", "그리하여 선장은 매우 진심 어린 마음으로 다시 한번 자신의 거대한 손을(빛깔로 보아 낡은 도르래 토막과 별로 다르지 않은 손이었다) 내밀어, 그를 한 번 꽉 움켜쥐었다. 그 바람에 카커 씨의 매끈한 살갗에는 선장의 손바닥에 넉넉히 새겨진 틈과 갈라진 자국들이 또렷한 자국으로 찍혔다."]], [["“Farewell!”", "\"그럼 안녕히!\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“I ain’t a man of many words, but I take it very kind of you to be so friendly, and above-board.", "\"저는 말수가 많은 사람은 아닙니다만, 이렇게 다정하고 솔직하게 대해 주시니 무척 고맙게 여깁니다."], ["You’ll excuse me if I’ve been at all intruding, will you?”", "제가 조금이라도 폐를 끼쳤다면 용서해 주시겠지요?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"천만에요,\""], ["returned the other.", "상대가 응수했다."]], [["“Thank’ee.", "\"고맙습니다."], ["My berth ain’t very roomy,”", "제 거처는 그리 넓지는 않습니다만,\""], ["said the Captain, turning back again, “but it’s tolerably snug; and if you was to find yourself near Brig Place, number nine, at any time—will you make a note of it?—and would come upstairs, without minding what was said by the person at the door, I should be proud to see you.", "선장이 다시 몸을 돌리며 말했다, \"그런대로 아늑하지요. 그리고 혹시 언제고 브리그 플레이스 9번지 근처에 오시게 되면—적어 두시겠습니까?—문간에 있는 사람이 무슨 말을 하든 개의치 마시고 위층으로 올라와 주신다면, 뵙는 것이 자랑스럽겠습니다.\""]], [["With that hospitable invitation, the Captain said “Good day!”", "그렇게 환대의 초대를 건네고, 선장은 \"안녕히 계십시오!\"라고 말하고는 밖으로 걸어 나가 문을 닫았다."], ["and walked out and shut the door; leaving Mr Carker still reclining against the chimney-piece.", "카커 씨는 여전히 굴뚝 장식 선반에 기댄 채 남겨 두고서."], ["In whose sly look and watchful manner; in whose false mouth, stretched but not laughing; in whose spotless cravat and very whiskers; even in whose silent passing of his soft hand over his white linen and his smooth face; there was something desperately cat-like.", "그 교활한 눈빛과 경계하는 태도에는, 길게 늘어졌으되 웃지는 않는 그 거짓된 입에는, 흠 하나 없는 크라바트와 다름 아닌 그 구레나룻에는, 심지어 부드러운 손을 자신의 흰 아마포 옷과 매끄러운 얼굴 위로 말없이 쓸어내리는 그 동작에마저, 무언가 지독하게 고양이를 닮은 데가 있었다."]], [["The unconscious Captain walked out in a state of self-glorification that imparted quite a new cut to the broad blue suit.", "아무것도 모르는 선장은 자기 자랑에 도취된 채로 걸어 나왔는데, 그 도취가 그 폭이 넓은 푸른 양복에 아주 새로운 맵시를 더해 주었다."], ["“Stand by, Ned!”", "\"대기, 네드!\""], ["said the Captain to himself.", "선장이 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“You’ve done a little business for the youngsters today, my lad!”", "\"오늘 자네 젊은이들을 위해 작은 일 하나 해냈군, 이 친구야!\""]], [["In his exultation, and in his familiarity, present and prospective, with the House, the Captain, when he reached the outer office, could not refrain from rallying Mr Perch a little, and asking him whether he thought everybody was still engaged.", "그 의기양양함 속에서, 또 회사와 현재 및 앞으로 가까워질 친분을 생각하니, 선장은 바깥 사무실에 이르렀을 때 퍼치 씨를 조금 놀리지 않을 수 없어서, 아직도 다들 일에 매여 있다고 보느냐고 물었다."], ["But not to be bitter on a man who had done his duty, the Captain whispered in his ear, that if he felt disposed for a glass of rum-and-water, and would follow, he would be happy to bestow the same upon him.", "그러나 직분을 다한 사람에게 매정하게 굴고 싶지는 않아, 선장은 그의 귀에 대고, 만일 럼주와 물 한 잔이 당기거든 따라오라고, 기꺼이 한 잔 대접하겠노라고 속삭였다."]], [["Before leaving the premises, the Captain, somewhat to the astonishment of the clerks, looked round from a central point of view, and took a general survey of the officers part and parcel of a project in which his young friend was nearly interested.", "그곳을 떠나기 전에 선장은, 사무원들을 다소 놀라게 하면서, 한가운데 자리에서 둘러보며, 자기 젊은 친구가 깊이 관련된 그 계획의 한 부분이자 한 요소인 사무소를 전반적으로 한 차례 둘러보았다."], ["The strong-room excited his especial admiration; but, that he might not appear too particular, he limited himself to an approving glance, and, with a graceful recognition of the clerks as a body, that was full of politeness and patronage, passed out into the court.", "금고실은 그의 각별한 감탄을 자아냈다. 그러나 너무 유난스러워 보이지 않으려고, 그는 흡족한 눈길 한 번으로 그치고는, 정중함과 너그러움이 가득한 태도로 사무원들 전체에게 우아하게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 골목으로 걸어 나갔다."], ["Being promptly joined by Mr Perch, he conveyed that gentleman to the tavern, and fulfilled his pledge—hastily, for Perch’s time was precious.", "곧 퍼치 씨가 합류하자, 그는 그 신사를 선술집으로 데려가 약속을 지켰다—퍼치의 시간이 귀했으므로 서둘러서."]], [["“I’ll give you for a toast,”", "\"건배 구호로,\""], ["said the Captain, “Wal”r!”", "선장이 말했다, \"월터를 들겠네!\""]], [["“Who?”", "\"누구요?\""], ["submitted Mr Perch.", "퍼치 씨가 조심스레 여쭈었다."]], [["“Wal”r!”", "\"월터!\""], ["repeated the Captain, in a voice of thunder.", "선장이 우레 같은 목소리로 되풀이했다."]], [["Mr Perch, who seemed to remember having heard in infancy that there was once a poet of that name, made no objection; but he was much astonished at the Captain’s coming into the City to propose a poet; indeed, if he had proposed to put a poet’s statue up—say Shakespeare’s for example—in a civic thoroughfare, he could hardly have done a greater outrage to Mr Perch’s experience.", "퍼치 씨는 어린 시절에 옛날에 그런 이름의 시인이 한 사람 있었다는 이야기를 들은 적이 있는 것 같아, 이의를 달지 않았다. 그러나 선장이 시인을 위해 건배하자고 시내까지 온 것에는 무척 놀랐다."], ["On the whole, he was such a mysterious and incomprehensible character, that Mr Perch decided not to mention him to Mrs Perch at all, in case of giving rise to any disagreeable consequences.", "실로, 선장이 시인의 동상을—이를테면 셰익스피어의 동상을—시내 큰길에 세우자고 했더라도, 퍼치 씨의 경험에 이보다 더 큰 충격을 주기는 어려웠을 것이다. 통틀어, 선장은 어찌나 신비롭고 종잡을 수 없는 인물이었던지, 퍼치 씨는 행여 무슨 거북한 결과를 빚을세라, 그를 퍼치 부인에게는 아예 언급하지 않기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Mysterious and incomprehensible, the Captain, with that lively sense upon him of having done a little business for the youngsters, remained all day, even to his most intimate friends; and but that Walter attributed his winks and grins, and other such pantomimic reliefs of himself, to his satisfaction in the success of their innocent deception upon old Sol Gills, he would assuredly have betrayed himself before night.", "신비롭고 종잡을 수 없는 선장은, 젊은이들을 위해 작은 일 하나 해냈다는 생생한 자각을 품은 채, 가장 막역한 친구들에게조차 종일 그러한 모습으로 남아 있었다. 그리고 월터가 그의 윙크와 싱글거림과 그 밖의 그런 무언극 같은 기분 발산을, 늙은 솔 길스에게 친 순진한 속임수가 성공한 데 대한 만족 때문이라고 여기지 않았더라면, 그는 분명 밤이 되기 전에 제 속내를 드러내고 말았을 것이다."], ["As it was, however, he kept his own secret; and went home late from the Instrument-maker’s house, wearing the glazed hat so much on one side, and carrying such a beaming expression in his eyes, that Mrs MacStinger (who might have been brought up at Doctor Blimber’s, she was such a Roman matron) fortified herself, at the first glimpse of him, behind the open street door, and refused to come out to the contemplation of her blessed infants, until he was securely lodged in his own room.", "그러나 그렇게 여긴 덕분에, 그는 자기 비밀을 지킬 수 있었다. 그리고 악기 제조공의 집에서 밤늦게 집으로 돌아왔는데, 번들거리는 모자를 한쪽으로 어찌나 비뚜름하게 쓰고, 눈에 그토록 환한 표정을 담고 있었던지, 맥스팅거 부인은(그녀가 어찌나 로마의 귀부인 같은 사람이었던지, 블림버 박사 밑에서 자랐다 해도 믿을 정도였다) 그를 처음 흘끗 보자마자 열린 대문 뒤에 진을 치고, 그가 자기 방에 무사히 들어앉을 때까지는 자신의 소중한 아기들을 살피러 나오기를 거부했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVIII. Father and Daughter", "# CHAPTER XVIII. Father and Daughter"]], [["There is a hush through Mr Dombey’s house.", "돔비 씨의 집안에는 정적이 감돈다."], ["Servants gliding up and down stairs rustle, but make no sound of footsteps.", "하인들이 계단을 오르내리며 옷자락을 스치지만 발소리는 내지 않는다."], ["They talk together constantly, and sit long at meals, making much of their meat and drink, and enjoying themselves after a grim unholy fashion.", "그들은 끊임없이 서로 이야기를 나누고, 식사 자리에 오래 앉아, 음식과 음료를 한껏 즐기며, 음울하고 불경한 방식으로 흥겨워한다."], ["Mrs Wickam, with her eyes suffused with tears, relates melancholy anecdotes; and tells them how she always said at Mrs Pipchin’s that it would be so, and takes more table-ale than usual, and is very sorry but sociable.", "위컴 부인은 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 우울한 일화들을 늘어놓는다. 자기는 핍친 부인 댁에 있을 때부터 늘 이리될 줄 알았다고 말하고, 평소보다 식탁 맥주를 더 마시며, 무척 슬퍼하면서도 어울리기를 즐긴다."], ["Cook’s state of mind is similar.", "요리사의 심정도 비슷하다."], ["She promises a little fry for supper, and struggles about equally against her feelings and the onions.", "그녀는 저녁으로 작은 튀김 요리를 약속하고, 자기 감정과 양파에 똑같이 맞서 싸운다."], ["Towlinson begins to think there’s a fate in it, and wants to know if anybody can tell him of any good that ever came of living in a corner house.", "타울린슨은 여기에 어떤 운명이 깃든 게 아닌가 생각하기 시작하고, 모퉁이 집에 사는 게 누구에게라도 좋은 일이 된 적이 있는지 누가 좀 말해 줄 수 없겠느냐고 한다."], ["It seems to all of them as having happened a long time ago; though yet the child lies, calm and beautiful, upon his little bed.", "그들 모두에게 그것은 마치 오래전에 일어난 일처럼 느껴진다. 그렇지만 아이는 여전히, 평온하고 아름답게, 제 작은 침대에 누워 있다."]], [["After dark there come some visitors—noiseless visitors, with shoes of felt—who have been there before; and with them comes that bed of rest which is so strange a one for infant sleepers.", "날이 어두워진 뒤, 몇몇 방문객이 온다—발소리 없는 방문객들, 펠트 신을 신은 이들로, 전에도 와 본 적이 있다. 그리고 그들과 함께, 어린 잠든 이들에게는 참으로 낯선 그 안식의 침대가 들어온다."], ["All this time, the bereaved father has not been seen even by his attendant; for he sits in an inner corner of his own dark room when anyone is there, and never seems to move at other times, except to pace it to and fro.", "그 내내, 상심한 아버지의 모습은 시중드는 이에게조차 보이지 않았다. 누군가 방에 있을 때면 그는 어두운 제 방의 안쪽 구석에 앉아 있었고, 다른 때에는 방 안을 이리저리 거니는 것 말고는 결코 움직이지 않는 듯했다."], ["But in the morning it is whispered among the household that he was heard to go upstairs in the dead night, and that he stayed there—in the room—until the sun was shining.", "그러나 아침이 되자, 그가 깊은 밤중에 위층으로 올라가는 소리가 들렸으며, 거기—그 방에—해가 비칠 때까지 머물러 있었다는 말이 집안에 속삭이듯 돈다."]], [["At the offices in the City, the ground-glass windows are made more dim by shutters; and while the lighted lamps upon the desks are half extinguished by the day that wanders in, the day is half extinguished by the lamps, and an unusual gloom prevails.", "시내의 사무실에서는 간유리 창문이 덧문 때문에 한층 더 흐릿해져 있다. 책상 위에 켜 둔 등불은 안으로 흘러드는 햇빛에 반쯤 꺼져 보이고, 햇빛은 또 그 등불 때문에 반쯤 꺼져 보이며, 평소와 다른 음울함이 감돈다."], ["There is not much business done.", "업무는 별로 이루어지지 않는다."], ["The clerks are indisposed to work; and they make assignations to eat chops in the afternoon, and go up the river.", "서기들은 일할 마음이 내키지 않아, 오후에 갈비 요리를 먹고 강 상류로 놀러 갈 약속을 잡는다."], ["Perch, the messenger, stays long upon his errands; and finds himself in bars of public-houses, invited thither by friends, and holding forth on the uncertainty of human affairs.", "심부름꾼 퍼치는 심부름을 나가서 오래 머문다. 친구들의 초대를 받아 술집 바에 들어앉아, 인간사의 덧없음에 관해 일장 연설을 늘어놓는 자신을 발견한다."], ["He goes home to Ball’s Pond earlier in the evening than usual, and treats Mrs Perch to a veal cutlet and Scotch ale.", "그는 저녁에 평소보다 일찍 볼스 폰드의 집으로 돌아가, 퍼치 부인에게 송아지 커틀릿과 스카치 에일을 대접한다. 지배인 카커 씨는 누구에게도 한턱내지 않고, 누구에게도 대접받지 않는다."], ["Mr Carker the Manager treats no one; neither is he treated; but alone in his own room he shows his teeth all day; and it would seem that there is something gone from Mr Carker’s path—some obstacle removed—which clears his way before him.", "다만 제 방에 홀로 앉아 온종일 이를 드러내 보일 뿐이다. 그리고 카커 씨의 앞길에서 무언가가—어떤 장애물이 치워져—그의 앞이 훤히 트인 듯하다."]], [["Now the rosy children living opposite to Mr Dombey’s house, peep from their nursery windows down into the street; for there are four black horses at his door, with feathers on their heads; and feathers tremble on the carriage that they draw; and these, and an array of men with scarves and staves, attract a crowd. The juggler who was going to twirl the basin, puts his loose coat on again over his fine dress; and his trudging wife, one-sided with her heavy baby in her arms, loiters to see the company come out. But closer to her dingy breast she presses her baby, when the burden that is so easily carried is borne forth; and the youngest of the rosy children at the high window opposite, needs no restraining hand to check her in her glee, when, pointing with her dimpled finger, she looks into her nurse’s face, and asks “What’s that?”", "지금 돔비 씨의 집 맞은편에 사는 발그레한 아이들이 육아실 창문에서 거리를 내려다보며 엿본다. 그의 문 앞에 검은 말 네 마리가 머리에 깃털을 단 채 서 있고, 그것들이 끄는 마차 위에서도 깃털이 떨리고 있기 때문이다. 그리고 이것들이, 또 띠와 지팡이를 든 사내들의 행렬이 구경꾼들을 끌어모은다. 대야를 빙빙 돌리려던 곡예사는 고운 의상 위에 헐렁한 외투를 다시 걸친다. 그의 무거운 아기를 품에 안아 한쪽으로 기우뚱한 채 터벅터벅 걷던 아내는, 일행이 나오는 것을 보려고 발길을 멈춘다. 그러나 그토록 쉽게 옮겨지는 그 짐이 밖으로 운반되어 나올 때, 그녀는 칙칙한 가슴팍에 제 아기를 더욱 바싹 끌어안는다. 그리고 맞은편 높은 창가에 있는 발그레한 아이들 중 가장 어린 아이는, 보조개 진 손가락으로 가리키며 유모의 얼굴을 들여다보고 \"저게 뭐예요?\" 하고 물을 때, 그 즐거움을 제지할 어떤 손길도 필요로 하지 않는다."]], [["And now, among the knot of servants dressed in mourning, and the weeping women, Mr Dombey passes through the hall to the other carriage that is waiting to receive him.", "이제, 상복을 입은 하인 무리와 우는 여인들 사이로, 돔비 씨가 자기를 태우려고 기다리는 다른 마차를 향해 현관을 가로질러 간다."], ["He is not “brought down,” these observers think, by sorrow and distress of mind.", "이 지켜보는 이들이 보기에, 그는 슬픔과 마음의 고통에 \"무너지지\""], ["His walk is as erect, his bearing is as stiff as ever it has been.", "않았다. 그의 걸음걸이는 여느 때처럼 꼿꼿하고, 그의 태도는 여느 때처럼 뻣뻣하다."], ["He hides his face behind no handkerchief, and looks before him.", "그는 손수건 뒤로 얼굴을 가리지 않고, 앞을 똑바로 바라본다."], ["But that his face is something sunk and rigid, and is pale, it bears the same expression as of old.", "다만 그의 얼굴이 다소 꺼지고 굳었으며 창백하다는 점만 빼면, 예전과 똑같은 표정을 띠고 있다."], ["He takes his place within the carriage, and three other gentlemen follow.", "그는 마차 안의 자리에 앉고, 다른 신사 세 사람이 뒤따른다."], ["Then the grand funeral moves slowly down the street.", "그러고 나서 성대한 장례 행렬이 거리를 따라 천천히 나아간다."], ["The feathers are yet nodding in the distance, when the juggler has the basin spinning on a cane, and has the same crowd to admire it.", "깃털이 아직 멀리서 끄덕이고 있을 때, 곡예사는 이미 대야를 막대 위에서 돌리고 있고, 그것을 감탄하며 보는 똑같은 구경꾼들을 거느리고 있다."], ["But the juggler’s wife is less alert than usual with the money-box, for a child’s burial has set her thinking that perhaps the baby underneath her shabby shawl may not grow up to be a man, and wear a sky-blue fillet round his head, and salmon-coloured worsted drawers, and tumble in the mud.", "그러나 곡예사의 아내는 평소만큼 돈통을 들고 민첩하게 굴지 못하는데, 한 아이의 매장이, 어쩌면 자기의 초라한 숄 밑에 든 아기가 어른이 되어, 머리에 하늘색 띠를 두르고, 연어색 털실 바지를 입고, 진창에 뒹굴게 될 만큼 자라지 못할지도 모른다는 생각을 그녀의 마음속에 불러일으켰기 때문이다."]], [["The feathers wind their gloomy way along the streets, and come within the sound of a church bell.", "깃털들이 음울한 길을 따라 거리를 누비다가, 교회 종소리가 들리는 곳에 이른다."], ["In this same church, the pretty boy received all that will soon be left of him on earth—a name.", "바로 이 교회에서, 그 예쁜 사내아이는 머지않아 이 땅에 남게 될 자기의 전부—이름—를 받았다."], ["All of him that is dead, they lay there, near the perishable substance of his mother.", "죽은 그의 모든 것을, 그들은 그곳, 썩어 없어질 그 어미의 유해 가까이 눕힌다."], ["It is well.", "잘된 일이다."], ["Their ashes lie where Florence in her walks—oh lonely, lonely walks!—may pass them any day.", "그들의 재는, 플로렌스가 산책할 때—오, 외롭고도 외로운 산책!—언제든 그 곁을 지날 수 있는 곳에 놓여 있다."]], [["The service over, and the clergyman withdrawn, Mr Dombey looks round, demanding in a low voice, whether the person who has been requested to attend to receive instructions for the tablet, is there?", "예배가 끝나고 목사가 물러가자, 돔비 씨는 주위를 둘러보며, 명판에 새길 지시를 받으러 와 달라는 요청을 받은 사람이 와 있는지 낮은 목소리로 묻는다."]], [["Someone comes forward, and says “Yes.”", "누군가 앞으로 나서며 \"네\" 하고 말한다."]], [["Mr Dombey intimates where he would have it placed; and shows him, with his hand upon the wall, the shape and size; and how it is to follow the memorial to the mother.", "돔비 씨는 그것을 어디에 놓고 싶은지 알려 주고, 손을 벽에 댄 채 그 모양과 크기를, 그리고 그것이 어미의 추모비를 어떻게 뒤따르도록 할지를 일러 준다."], ["Then, with his pencil, he writes out the inscription, and gives it to him: adding, “I wish to have it done at once.", "그러고 나서 연필로 비문을 적어 그에게 건네며 덧붙인다. \"이걸 곧바로 해 주었으면 하오."]], [["“It shall be done immediately, Sir.”", "\"즉시 처리하겠습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“There is really nothing to inscribe but name and age, you see.”", "\"보다시피, 새길 것이라곤 이름과 나이밖에 정말 없소.\""]], [["The man bows, glancing at the paper, but appears to hesitate.", "그 사내는 종이를 흘끗 보며 고개를 숙이지만, 망설이는 듯하다."], ["Mr Dombey not observing his hesitation, turns away, and leads towards the porch.", "돔비 씨는 그의 망설임을 알아채지 못한 채 돌아서서 현관 쪽으로 걸어간다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Sir;”", "\"실례합니다, 나리.\" 그의 상복 망토에 가만히 손길이 닿는다."], ["a touch falls gently on his mourning cloak; “but as you wish it done immediately, and it may be put in hand when I get back—”", "\"곧바로 해 주기를 바라시고, 또 제가 돌아가는 대로 작업에 들어갈 수 있으니—\" \"무슨 일이오?\""]], [["“Well?” “Will you be so good as read it over again?", "\"이걸 한 번 더 읽어 봐 주시겠습니까?"], ["I think there’s a mistake.”", "잘못된 데가 있는 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디가?\""]], [["The statuary gives him back the paper, and points out, with his pocket rule, the words, “beloved and only child.”", "석공은 그에게 종이를 돌려주며, 자기 주머니 자로 \"사랑하는 외동딸\"이라는 글자를 가리킨다."]], [["“It should be, ‘son,’ I think, Sir?”", "\"이건 '아들'이어야 할 것 같습니다만, 나리?\""]], [["“You are right. Of course.", "\"자네 말이 맞네."], ["Make the correction.”", "물론이지. 고치게.\""]], [["The father, with a hastier step, pursues his way to the coach.", "아버지는 더 빠른 걸음으로 마차를 향해 길을 재촉한다."], ["When the other three, who follow closely, take their seats, his face is hidden for the first time—shaded by his cloak.", "바싹 뒤따르던 다른 세 사람이 자리에 앉을 때, 그의 얼굴은 처음으로 가려진다—망토에 그늘져서."], ["Nor do they see it any more that day.", "그리고 그날 그들은 그 얼굴을 더는 보지 못한다."], ["He alights first, and passes immediately into his own room.", "그는 먼저 마차에서 내려, 곧장 자기 방으로 들어간다."], ["The other mourners (who are only Mr Chick, and two of the medical attendants) proceed upstairs to the drawing-room, to be received by Mrs Chick and Miss Tox.", "다른 조문객들(칙 씨와 의사 시중꾼 두 사람뿐이다)은 응접실로 위층에 올라가, 칙 부인과 톡스 양의 맞이를 받는다."], ["And what the face is, in the shut-up chamber underneath: or what the thoughts are: what the heart is, what the contest or the suffering: no one knows.", "그리고 그 아래 닫힌 방 안에서 그 얼굴이 어떤지, 혹은 어떤 생각들이 오가는지, 그 마음이 어떤지, 그 갈등이나 고통이 어떠한지는 아무도 모른다."]], [["The chief thing that they know, below stairs, in the kitchen, is that “it seems like Sunday.”", "아래층 부엌에서 그들이 무엇보다 잘 아는 것은 \"꼭 일요일 같다\"는 것이다."], ["They can hardly persuade themselves but that there is something unbecoming, if not wicked, in the conduct of the people out of doors, who pursue their ordinary occupations, and wear their everyday attire.", "그들은 평소의 일과를 이어 가고 일상의 옷을 입은 바깥 사람들의 행동이, 사악하지는 않더라도 어딘가 온당치 못한 데가 있다고 거의 믿어 의심치 않는다."], ["It is quite a novelty to have the blinds up, and the shutters open; and they make themselves dismally comfortable over bottles of wine, which are freely broached as on a festival.", "차양을 걷어 올리고 덧문을 열어 두는 것이 자못 신기한 일이다. 그리고 그들은 마치 축제 때처럼 거리낌 없이 따 마시는 포도주 병을 앞에 두고, 음울하게나마 안락함을 누린다."], ["They are much inclined to moralise.", "그들은 자못 도덕적인 사색에 빠지고 싶어 한다."], ["Mr Towlinson proposes with a sigh, “Amendment to us all!” for which, as Cook says with another sigh, “There’s room enough, God knows.”", "타울린슨 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 \"우리 모두의 개과천선을 위하여!\"라고 제안하자, 요리사가 또 한숨을 쉬며 \"하느님도 아시다시피 그럴 여지야 충분하죠\"라고 말한다."], ["In the evening, Mrs Chick and Miss Tox take to needlework again.", "저녁에 칙 부인과 톡스 양은 다시 바느질감을 든다."], ["In the evening also, Mr Towlinson goes out to take the air, accompanied by the housemaid, who has not yet tried her mourning bonnet.", "또한 저녁에 타울린슨 씨는 바람을 쐬러 나가는데, 아직 자기 상복 보닛을 써 보지 못한 하녀가 함께한다."], ["They are very tender to each other at dusky street-corners, and Towlinson has visions of leading an altered and blameless existence as a serious greengrocer in Oxford Market.", "그들은 어스름한 길모퉁이에서 서로에게 무척 다정하며, 타울린슨은 옥스퍼드 마켓에서 진중한 청과상이 되어 새사람이 되고 흠 없는 삶을 꾸려 가는 환상에 잠긴다."]], [["There is sounder sleep and deeper rest in Mr Dombey’s house tonight, than there has been for many nights.", "오늘 밤 돔비 씨의 집에는, 여러 밤 동안 그랬던 것보다 더 깊은 잠과 더 깊은 안식이 깃든다."], ["The morning sun awakens the old household, settled down once more in their old ways.", "아침 해가 다시 한번 옛 일상으로 돌아간 그 오래된 식솔들을 깨운다."], ["The rosy children opposite run past with hoops.", "맞은편의 발그레한 아이들은 굴렁쇠를 굴리며 달려 지나간다."], ["There is a splendid wedding in the church.", "교회에서는 성대한 결혼식이 열린다."], ["The juggler’s wife is active with the money-box in another quarter of the town.", "곡예사의 아내는 동네 다른 구역에서 돈통을 들고 분주하다."], ["The mason sings and whistles as he chips out P-A-U-L in the marble slab before him.", "석공은 자기 앞의 대리석판에 P-A-U-L을 새기며 노래하고 휘파람을 분다."]], [["And can it be that in a world so full and busy, the loss of one weak creature makes a void in any heart, so wide and deep that nothing but the width and depth of vast eternity can fill it up!", "그리고 이토록 가득 차고 분주한 세상에서, 한 가냘픈 존재의 상실이 어느 가슴엔가, 광막한 영원의 넓이와 깊이가 아니고서는 그 무엇으로도 채울 수 없을 만큼 넓고 깊은 빈자리를 남긴다는 것이 과연 있을 수 있는 일일까!"], ["Florence, in her innocent affliction, might have answered, “Oh my brother, oh my dearly loved and loving brother!", "플로렌스라면, 그 순결한 비통 속에서, 이렇게 대답했으리라. \"오 내 동생, 오 내 그토록 사랑하고 사랑받던 동생!"], ["Only friend and companion of my slighted childhood!", "푸대접받던 내 어린 시절의 유일한 벗이자 동무!"], ["Could any less idea shed the light already dawning on your early grave, or give birth to the softened sorrow that is springing into life beneath this rain of tears!”", "그보다 못한 어떤 생각이, 네 이른 무덤 위에 이미 동터 오는 빛을 비출 수 있을까, 혹은 이 눈물의 빗속에서 피어나는 누그러진 슬픔을 낳을 수 있을까!\""]], [["“My dear child,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, who held it as a duty incumbent on her, to improve the occasion, “when you are as old as I am—”", "이 기회를 잘 활용하는 것이 자기에게 지워진 의무라 여기는 칙 부인이 말했다, \"네가 내 나이쯤 되면—\""]], [["“Which will be the prime of life,”", "\"그게 인생의 한창때죠,\""], ["observed Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 한마디 했다."]], [["“You will then,”", "\"그러면 너도,\""], ["pursued Mrs Chick, gently squeezing Miss Tox’s hand in acknowledgment of her friendly remark, “you will then know that all grief is unavailing, and that it is our duty to submit.”", "칙 부인이 그 다정한 말에 대한 답례로 톡스 양의 손을 부드럽게 꼭 쥐며 말을 이었다, \"그러면 너도 모든 슬픔이 부질없다는 것을, 그리고 순응하는 것이 우리의 의무라는 것을 알게 될 거다.\""]], [["“I will try, dear aunt I do try,”", "\"노력할게요, 고모. 정말 노력하고 있어요,\""], ["answered Florence, sobbing.", "플로렌스가 흐느끼며 대답했다."]], [["“I am glad to hear it,”", "\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘구나,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “because; my love, as our dear Miss Tox—of whose sound sense and excellent judgment, there cannot possibly be two opinions—”", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"왜냐하면, 얘야, 우리의 사랑하는 톡스 양께서도—그분의 건전한 분별력과 탁월한 판단력에 대해서는 두 가지 의견이 있을 수가 없지—\""]], [["“My dear Louisa, I shall really be proud, soon,”", "\"루이자, 나 정말이지 곧 우쭐해지겠어요,\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."]], [["“—will tell you, and confirm by her experience,”", "\"—너에게 말해 주고, 또 자기 경험으로 확증해 주시겠지만,\""], ["pursued Mrs Chick, “we are called upon on all occasions to make an effort It is required of us.", "칙 부인이 말을 이었다, \"우리는 어떤 경우에든 노력을 하라는 부름을 받는단다. 그게 우리에게 요구되는 일이지."], ["If any—my dear,”", "만약 어떤—얘야,\""], ["turning to Miss Tox, “I want a word.", "톡스 양을 돌아보며, \"단어가 하나 생각이 안 나요."], ["Mis—Mis-”", "인간—인간—\""]], [["“Demeanour?”", "\"품행 말인가요?\""], ["suggested Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 넌지시 일러 주었다."]], [["“No, no, no,”", "\"아니, 아니, 아니에요,\""], ["said Mrs Chic “How can you!", "칙 부인이 말했다."], ["Goodness me, it’s on, the end of my tongue.", "\"어쩜 그래요! 세상에, 혀끝에서 맴도는데."], ["Mis-”", "인간—\""]], [["“Placed affection?”", "\"빗나간 애정 말인가요?\""], ["suggested Miss Tox, timidly.", "톡스 양이 머뭇거리며 일러 주었다."]], [["“Good gracious, Lucretia!”", "\"맙소사, 루크리셔!\""], ["returned Mrs Chick “How very monstrous!", "칙 부인이 응수했다. \"어쩜 그리 끔찍한 소릴!"], ["Misanthrope, is the word I want.", "인간 혐오자, 그게 내가 찾던 단어예요."], ["The idea!", "별생각을 다!"], ["Misplaced affection!", "빗나간 애정이라니!"], ["I say, if any misanthrope were to put, in my presence, the question ‘Why were we born?’ I should reply, ‘To make an effort’.”", "내 말은, 만약 어떤 인간 혐오자가 내 앞에서 '우리는 왜 태어났는가?'라는 질문을 던진다면, 나는 '노력하기 위해서요'라고 대답하겠다는 거예요.\""]], [["“Very good indeed,”", "\"정말 훌륭해요,\""], ["said Miss Tox, much impressed by the originality of the sentiment “Very good.”", "톡스 양이 그 발상의 독창성에 깊이 감동하여 말했다. \"정말 훌륭해요.\""]], [["“Unhappily,”", "\"불행히도,\""], ["pursued Mrs Chick, “we have a warning under our own eyes.", "칙 부인이 말을 이었다, \"우리는 바로 우리 눈앞에 하나의 경고를 두고 있단다."], ["We have but too much reason to suppose, my dear child, that if an effort had been made in time, in this family, a train of the most trying and distressing circumstances might have been avoided.", "얘야, 만약 이 집안에서 제때에 노력을 기울였더라면, 가장 견디기 힘들고 비통한 일련의 사정들을 피할 수 있었으리라고 생각할 만한 이유가 우리에겐 너무도 많구나."], ["Nothing shall ever persuade me,”", "그 무엇도 나를 설득하지 못할 거다,\""], ["observed the good matron, with a resolute air, “but that if that effort had been made by poor dear Fanny, the poor dear darling child would at least have had a stronger constitution.”", "이 선량한 부인이 단호한 태도로 말했다, \"가엾고 사랑스러운 패니가 그 노력을 기울였더라면, 적어도 가엾고 사랑스러운 그 아이가 더 튼튼한 체질을 타고났으리라는 생각만은 말이다.\""]], [["Mrs Chick abandoned herself to her feelings for half a moment; but, as a practical illustration of her doctrine, brought herself up short, in the middle of a sob, and went on again.", "칙 부인은 잠시 자기 감정에 자신을 내맡겼다. 그러나 자기 학설의 실천적 본보기로서, 흐느낌의 한가운데서 딱 멈추고는, 다시 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“Therefore, Florence, pray let us see that you have some strength of mind, and do not selfishly aggravate the distress in which your poor Papa is plunged.”", "\"그러니 플로렌스, 부디 네가 어느 정도 마음의 굳셈을 지니고 있음을 보여 다오, 그리고 네 가엾은 아버지가 빠져 있는 그 비통함을 이기적으로 더 무겁게 하지는 말아 다오.\""]], [["“Dear aunt!”", "\"고모!\""], ["said Florence, kneeling quickly down before her, that she might the better and more earnestly look into her face.", "플로렌스가 그녀의 얼굴을 더 잘, 더 간절히 들여다보려고 그 앞에 얼른 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. \"아버지에 대해 더 말해 주세요."], ["“Tell me more about Papa.", "제발 아버지 이야기를 해 주세요!"], ["Pray tell me about him! Is he quite heartbroken?”", "아버지는 정말 마음이 무너지셨나요?\""]], [["Miss Tox was of a tender nature, and there was something in this appeal that moved her very much.", "톡스 양은 다정한 성품이었고, 이 호소에는 그녀를 몹시 흔들어 놓는 무언가가 있었다."], ["Whether she saw it in a succession, on the part of the neglected child, to the affectionate concern so often expressed by her dead brother—or a love that sought to twine itself about the heart that had loved him, and that could not bear to be shut out from sympathy with such a sorrow, in such sad community of love and grief—or whether she only recognised the earnest and devoted spirit which, although discarded and repulsed, was wrung with tenderness long unreturned, and in the waste and solitude of this bereavement cried to him to seek a comfort in it, and to give some, by some small response—whatever may have been her understanding of it, it moved Miss Tox.", "푸대접받던 이 아이가, 죽은 오라비가 그토록 자주 드러내던 그 애정 어린 관심을 이어받으려는 것으로 보았든—혹은 그를 사랑했던 그 마음에 자신을 휘감으려 하는, 그리고 그런 슬픔에 대한 공감에서, 그토록 슬픈 사랑과 비탄의 공동체에서 배제되는 것을 견딜 수 없어 하는 사랑으로 보았든—혹은 비록 내쳐지고 거부당했으나, 오래도록 보답받지 못한 다정함으로 쥐어짜인 채, 이 사별의 황량함과 외로움 속에서 그에게, 거기서 위안을 구하라고, 어떤 작은 응답으로 얼마간의 위안을 베풀어 달라고 부르짖던 그 간절하고 헌신적인 영혼을 알아본 것뿐이든—그녀가 그것을 어떻게 이해했든 간에, 그것은 톡스 양을 흔들어 놓았다."], ["For the moment she forgot the majesty of Mrs Chick, and, patting Florence hastily on the cheek, turned aside and suffered the tears to gush from her eyes, without waiting for a lead from that wise matron.", "그 순간 그녀는 칙 부인의 위엄도 잊고, 플로렌스의 뺨을 황급히 토닥이고는, 고개를 돌려, 그 현명한 부인의 선례를 기다리지도 않고 두 눈에서 눈물이 솟구치도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["Mrs Chick herself lost, for a moment, the presence of mind on which she so much prided herself; and remained mute, looking on the beautiful young face that had so long, so steadily, and patiently, been turned towards the little bed.", "칙 부인 자신도, 그토록 자랑스러워하던 그 침착함을 잠시 잃었다. 그리고 그토록 오래, 그토록 한결같이, 그토록 끈기 있게 그 작은 침대를 향하고 있던 그 아름다운 어린 얼굴을 바라보며, 말없이 있었다."], ["But recovering her voice—which was synonymous with her presence of mind, indeed they were one and the same thing—she replied with dignity:", "그러나 목소리를 되찾자—그것은 그녀의 침착함과 동의어였으니, 사실 그 둘은 하나이며 같은 것이었다—그녀는 위엄 있게 대답했다."]], [["“Florence, my dear child, your poor Papa is peculiar at times; and to question me about him, is to question me upon a subject which I really do not pretend to understand.", "\"플로렌스, 얘야, 네 가엾은 아버지는 때때로 별나시단다. 그리고 나에게 그분에 대해 묻는 것은, 내가 정말이지 이해하는 척조차 할 수 없는 주제에 대해 묻는 거란다."], ["I believe I have as much influence with your Papa as anybody has.", "내 생각에 나는 누구 못지않게 네 아버지에게 영향력을 지니고 있어."], ["Still, all I can say is, that he has said very little to me; and that I have only seen him once or twice for a minute at a time, and indeed have hardly seen him then, for his room has been dark.", "그래도 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤, 그분이 나에게 거의 아무 말씀도 하지 않으셨다는 것뿐이야. 그리고 나는 그분을 한두 번, 그것도 한 번에 잠깐씩밖에 못 뵈었고, 사실 그때조차 방이 어두워서 거의 못 뵌 거나 마찬가지란다."], ["I have said to your Papa, ‘Paul!’—that is the exact expression I used—‘Paul!", "나는 네 아버지께 '폴!'이라고—그게 내가 쓴 정확한 표현이야—'폴! 어째서 뭐든 기운 나는 걸 좀 드시지 않으세요?'"], ["why do you not take something stimulating?’ Your Papa’s reply has always been, ‘Louisa, have the goodness to leave me.", "하고 말했단다. 네 아버지의 대답은 늘 '루이자, 부디 나를 좀 내버려 두렴."], ["I want nothing.", "나는 아무것도 필요 없다."], ["I am better by myself.’ If I was to be put upon my oath to-morrow, Lucretia, before a magistrate,”", "혼자 있는 게 낫다'였어. 루크리셔, 내일 치안판사 앞에서 선서를 하라고 한대도,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “I have no doubt I could venture to swear to those identical words.”", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"나는 분명히 그 토씨 하나 틀림없는 말들을 두고 맹세할 수 있을 거예요.\""]], [["Miss Tox expressed her admiration by saying, “My Louisa is ever methodical!”", "톡스 양은 \"우리 루이자는 언제나 빈틈이 없다니까요!\"라고 말하며 감탄을 표했다."]], [["“In short, Florence,”", "\"요컨대, 플로렌스,\""], ["resumed her aunt, “literally nothing has passed between your poor Papa and myself, until today; when I mentioned to your Papa that Sir Barnet and Lady Skettles had written exceedingly kind notes—our sweet boy!", "고모가 말을 이었다, \"오늘까지 네 가엾은 아버지와 나 사이에는 말 그대로 아무 일도 없었단다. 오늘에야 내가 네 아버지께 바닛 경과 스케틀스 부인이 더없이 친절한 편지를 보내셨다고 말씀드렸지—우리 귀여운 아이!"], ["Lady Skettles loved him like a—where’s my pocket handkerchief?”", "스케틀스 부인은 그 애를 마치—내 손수건이 어디 갔지?\""]], [["Miss Tox produced one.", "톡스 양이 하나 꺼내 주었다."]], [["“Exceedingly kind notes, proposing that you should visit them for change of scene.", "\"더없이 친절한 편지로, 기분 전환 삼아 네가 그분들을 방문하면 어떻겠냐고 제안하셨단다."], ["Mentioning to your Papa that I thought Miss Tox and myself might now go home (in which he quite agreed), I inquired if he had any objection to your accepting this invitation.", "톡스 양과 내가 이제 집으로 돌아가도 되겠다고 생각한다고 네 아버지께 말씀드리면서(그분도 전적으로 동의하셨어), 네가 이 초대를 받아들이는 데 무슨 반대가 있으신지 여쭈었지."], ["He said, ‘No, Louisa, not the least!’”", "그분은 '아니, 루이자, 조금도 없네!' 하고 말씀하셨어.\""]], [["Florence raised her tearful eye.", "플로렌스가 눈물 어린 눈을 들어 올렸다."]], [["“At the same time, if you would prefer staying here, Florence, to paying this visit at present, or to going home with me—”", "\"그렇지만 만약, 플로렌스, 네가 지금 이 방문을 하거나 나와 함께 집으로 가는 것보다 여기 남는 편을 더 좋아한다면—\""]], [["“I should much prefer it, aunt,”", "\"훨씬 더 그러고 싶어요, 고모,\""], ["was the faint rejoinder.", "가냘픈 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Why then, child,”", "\"그러면, 얘야,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “you can.", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"그래도 된단다."], ["It’s a strange choice, I must say.", "참 이상한 선택이라고 말하지 않을 수 없구나."], ["But you always were strange.", "하지만 너는 늘 이상한 아이였지."], ["Anybody else at your time of life, and after what has passed—my dear Miss Tox, I have lost my pocket handkerchief again—would be glad to leave here, one would suppose.”", "네 나이의 다른 누구라도, 그리고 그런 일을 겪고 나면—얘 톡스 양, 또 손수건을 잃어버렸네요—여기를 떠나게 되어 기뻐할 거라고 다들 생각할 텐데.\""]], [["“I should not like to feel,” said Florence, “as if the house was avoided.", "\"이 집이 기피당하는 것처럼 느껴지는 건 싫어요,\""], ["I should not like to think that the—his—the rooms upstairs were quite empty and dreary, aunt.", "플로렌스가 말했다. \"위층의 그—그분의—방들이 텅 비고 쓸쓸하다고 생각하는 것도 싫고요, 고모."], ["I would rather stay here, for the present.", "당분간은 여기 있는 편이 더 좋아요."], ["Oh my brother!", "오 내 동생!"], ["oh my brother!”", "오 내 동생!\""]], [["It was a natural emotion, not to be suppressed; and it would make way even between the fingers of the hands with which she covered up her face.", "그것은 억누를 수 없는, 자연스러운 감정이었다. 그리고 그것은 그녀가 얼굴을 가린 두 손의 손가락 사이로조차 비집고 새어 나오려 했다."], ["The overcharged and heavy-laden breast must some times have that vent, or the poor wounded solitary heart within it would have fluttered like a bird with broken wings, and sunk down in the dust.", "그토록 벅차고 무거운 짐을 진 가슴은 이따금 그렇게 터져 나올 곳이 있어야만 했다. 그러지 않으면 그 안에 든 가엾고 상처 입은 외로운 마음이, 날개 부러진 새처럼 파닥이다가 먼지 속으로 가라앉아 버렸을 것이다."]], [["“Well, child!”", "\"자, 얘야!\""], ["said Mrs Chick, after a pause “I wouldn’t on any account say anything unkind to you, and that I’m sure you know.", "칙 부인이 잠시 사이를 두고 말했다. \"나는 결코 너에게 무정한 말을 할 생각은 추호도 없단다, 그건 너도 분명히 알 거야."], ["You will remain here, then, and do exactly as you like.", "그럼 너는 여기 남아서, 네가 하고 싶은 대로 꼭 그렇게 하렴."], ["No one will interfere with you, Florence, or wish to interfere with you, I’m sure.”", "누구도 너를 간섭하지 않을 거고, 간섭하고 싶어 하지도 않을 거야, 분명히.\""]], [["Florence shook her head in sad assent.", "플로렌스는 슬프게 동의하며 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“I had no sooner begun to advise your poor Papa that he really ought to seek some distraction and restoration in a temporary change,”", "\"내가 네 가엾은 아버지께 일시적인 기분 전환으로 얼마간 기분도 풀고 기운도 되찾으셔야 한다고 막 권하기 시작하자마자,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “than he told me he had already formed the intention of going into the country for a short time.", "칙 부인이 말했다, \"그분은 이미 잠시 시골에 내려갈 작정을 해 두셨다고 말씀하시더구나."], ["I’m sure I hope he’ll go very soon.", "정말이지 어서 빨리 가시면 좋겠어."], ["He can’t go too soon.", "빠르면 빠를수록 좋지."], ["But I suppose there are some arrangements connected with his private papers and so forth, consequent on the affliction that has tried us all so much—I can’t think what’s become of mine: Lucretia, lend me yours, my dear—that may occupy him for one or two evenings in his own room.", "하지만 우리 모두를 그토록 힘들게 한 이 비통한 일에 뒤따라, 개인 서류며 그런 것들과 관련된 정리할 일들이 좀 있어서—내 건 대체 어디로 갔는지 모르겠네. 루크리셔, 네 걸 좀 빌려 줘요, 얘—그분 방에서 한두 저녁쯤 그 일에 매달리실지도 모르겠다."], ["Your Papa’s a Dombey, child, if ever there was one,”", "네 아버지는, 얘야, 둘도 없는 진짜 돔비 사람이란다,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, drying both her eyes at once with great care on opposite corners of Miss Tox’s handkerchief “He’ll make an effort.", "칙 부인이 톡스 양의 손수건 양쪽 귀퉁이로 두 눈을 한꺼번에 아주 조심스레 닦으며 말했다. \"그분은 노력하실 거야."], ["There’s no fear of him.”", "그분에 대해서는 걱정할 게 없단다.\""]], [["“Is there nothing, aunt,”", "\"고모, 제가 무언가—\""], ["said Florence, trembling, “I might do to—”", "플로렌스가 떨면서 말했다, \"제가 할 수 있는 일이 없을까요—\""]], [["“Lord, my dear child,”", "\"맙소사, 얘야,\""], ["interposed Mrs Chick, hastily, “what are you talking about?", "칙 부인이 황급히 끼어들었다, \"무슨 소리니?"], ["If your Papa said to Me—I have given you his exact words, ‘Louisa, I want nothing; I am better by myself’—what do you think he’d say to you?", "네 아버지가 나에게—그분의 정확한 말을 네게 그대로 들려줬잖니, '루이자, 나는 아무것도 필요 없다, 혼자 있는 게 낫다'고—하셨다면, 너에게는 뭐라고 하실 것 같으냐?"], ["You mustn’t show yourself to him, child.", "너는 그분 앞에 나타나서는 안 된단다, 얘야."], ["Don’t dream of such a thing.”", "그런 건 꿈도 꾸지 마라.\""]], [["“Aunt,”", "\"고모,\""], ["said Florence, “I will go and lie down on my bed.”", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"저는 가서 침대에 좀 누워 있을게요.\""]], [["Mrs Chick approved of this resolution, and dismissed her with a kiss.", "칙 부인은 이 결심을 옳게 여기고, 입맞춤으로 그녀를 보냈다."], ["But Miss Tox, on a faint pretence of looking for the mislaid handkerchief, went upstairs after her; and tried in a few stolen minutes to comfort her, in spite of great discouragement from Susan Nipper.", "그러나 톡스 양은, 잃어버린 손수건을 찾는다는 어설픈 핑계로, 그녀를 따라 위층으로 올라갔다. 그리고 수전 니퍼의 큰 거부감에도 아랑곳없이, 몇 분 남짓 짬을 내어 그녀를 위로하려 애썼다."], ["For Miss Nipper, in her burning zeal, disparaged Miss Tox as a crocodile; yet her sympathy seemed genuine, and had at least the vantage-ground of disinterestedness—there was little favour to be won by it.", "니퍼 양은, 그 불타는 열성으로, 톡스 양을 악어라고 헐뜯었으니 말이다. 그렇지만 그녀의 공감은 진실해 보였고, 적어도 사심 없음이라는 유리한 입지가 있었다—그것으로 얻을 호의라곤 거의 없었으니."]], [["And was there no one nearer and dearer than Susan, to uphold the striving heart in its anguish?", "그런데 이 안간힘 쓰는 마음을 그 고뇌 속에서 떠받쳐 줄, 수전보다 더 가깝고 더 소중한 이는 없었던가?"], ["Was there no other neck to clasp; no other face to turn to?", "끌어안을 다른 목은 없었던가, 의지할 다른 얼굴은?"], ["no one else to say a soothing word to such deep sorrow?", "그런 깊은 슬픔에 위로의 말 한마디 건넬 다른 누군가는 없었던가?"], ["Was Florence so alone in the bleak world that nothing else remained to her? Nothing.", "플로렌스는 이 황량한 세상에서 그토록 외로워, 그녀에게 남은 것이 아무것도 없었던가?"], ["Stricken motherless and brotherless at once—for in the loss of little Paul, that first and greatest loss fell heavily upon her—this was the only help she had.", "아무것도 없었다. 한꺼번에 어미도 동생도 잃은 채—어린 폴을 잃은 것에서, 그 첫째가는 가장 큰 상실이 그녀에게 무겁게 내려앉았으니—이것이 그녀가 가진 유일한 의지처였다."], ["Oh, who can tell how much she needed help at first!", "오, 처음에 그녀가 얼마나 도움을 필요로 했는지 누가 말로 다 할 수 있으랴!"]], [["At first, when the house subsided into its accustomed course, and they had all gone away, except the servants, and her father shut up in his own rooms, Florence could do nothing but weep, and wander up and down, and sometimes, in a sudden pang of desolate remembrance, fly to her own chamber, wring her hands, lay her face down on her bed, and know no consolation: nothing but the bitterness and cruelty of grief. This commonly ensued upon the recognition of some spot or object very tenderly associated with him; and it made the miserable house, at first, a place of agony.", "처음에, 집이 다시 익숙한 일상으로 가라앉고, 하인들만 남고 다들 떠나 버렸으며, 아버지는 자기 방에 틀어박혀 있을 때, 플로렌스는 그저 울고, 이리저리 거니는 것밖에 할 수 없었다. 그리고 때로는 황량한 기억의 갑작스러운 고통에 사로잡혀, 제 방으로 달려가, 두 손을 비틀고, 침대에 얼굴을 묻고는, 아무 위안도 알지 못했다. 슬픔의 쓰라림과 잔혹함밖에는. 이런 일은 대개 그와 매우 다정하게 얽힌 어떤 장소나 물건을 알아본 끝에 뒤따라 일어났다. 그리고 그것은 그 비참한 집을, 처음에는, 고통의 장소로 만들었다."]], [["But it is not in the nature of pure love to burn so fiercely and unkindly long.", "그러나 순수한 사랑이 그토록 사납고 무정하게 오래 타오르는 것은 그 본성에 맞지 않는다."], ["The flame that in its grosser composition has the taint of earth may prey upon the breast that gives it shelter; but the fire from heaven is as gentle in the heart, as when it rested on the heads of the assembled twelve, and showed each man his brother, brightened and unhurt.", "그 거친 성분에 흙의 더러움이 섞인 불꽃은 그것을 품어 주는 가슴을 갉아먹을 수 있다. 그러나 하늘에서 내려온 불은, 모여 있던 열두 사람의 머리 위에 내려앉아 저마다에게 제 형제를 환히 밝혀진 모습으로, 상함 없이 보여 주었을 때처럼, 마음속에서도 온화하다."], ["The image conjured up, there soon returned the placid face, the softened voice, the loving looks, the quiet trustfulness and peace; and Florence, though she wept still, wept more tranquilly, and courted the remembrance.", "떠올린 그 모습과 더불어, 곧 평온한 얼굴이, 부드러운 목소리가, 사랑 어린 눈길이, 그 고요한 신뢰와 평화가 되돌아왔다. 그리고 플로렌스는, 여전히 울긴 했으나, 더 평온하게 울었고, 그 기억을 가까이 불러들였다."]], [["It was not very long before the golden water, dancing on the wall, in the old place, at the old serene time, had her calm eye fixed upon it as it ebbed away.", "오래지 않아, 옛 자리에서, 옛 고요한 시간에, 벽 위에서 일렁이던 금빛 물결을, 그것이 빠져나가며 잦아들 때, 그녀의 차분한 눈이 응시하게 되었다."], ["It was not very long before that room again knew her, often; sitting there alone, as patient and as mild as when she had watched beside the little bed.", "오래지 않아 그 방은 다시, 자주 그녀를 맞이하게 되었다. 그녀는 거기 홀로 앉아, 그 작은 침대 곁에서 지켜보던 때처럼 참을성 있고 온순했다."], ["When any sharp sense of its being empty smote upon her, she could kneel beside it, and pray GOD—it was the pouring out of her full heart—to let one angel love her and remember her.", "그것이 비어 있다는 어떤 날카로운 느낌이 그녀를 후려칠 때면, 그녀는 그 곁에 무릎을 꿇고, 하느님께 기도드릴 수 있었다—그것은 그녀의 벅찬 마음이 쏟아져 나오는 것이었다—천사 하나가 자기를 사랑하고 자기를 기억하게 해 달라고."]], [["It was not very long before, in the midst of the dismal house so wide and dreary, her low voice in the twilight, slowly and stopping sometimes, touched the old air to which he had so often listened, with his drooping head upon her arm.", "오래지 않아, 그토록 넓고 쓸쓸한 그 음울한 집 한가운데서, 황혼 녘 그녀의 낮은 목소리가, 더디게, 때로는 멈추면서, 그가 자기 팔에 고개를 떨군 채 그토록 자주 귀 기울이던 그 옛 가락을 어루만지게 되었다."], ["And after that, and when it was quite dark, a little strain of music trembled in the room: so softly played and sung, that it was more like the mournful recollection of what she had done at his request on that last night, than the reality repeated.", "그리고 그러고 나서, 날이 완전히 어두워졌을 때, 한 가닥 음악이 방 안에서 떨려 왔다. 어찌나 부드럽게 연주되고 노래되었던지, 그것은 그 마지막 밤에 그의 청에 따라 자기가 했던 일을, 슬프게 회상하는 것에 가까웠지, 그 실제를 되풀이하는 것 같지는 않았다."], ["But it was repeated, often—very often, in the shadowy solitude; and broken murmurs of the strain still trembled on the keys, when the sweet voice was hushed in tears.", "그러나 그것은 되풀이되었다, 자주—아주 자주, 그 어둑한 고독 속에서. 그리고 그 달콤한 목소리가 눈물 속으로 잦아들 때면, 그 가락의 끊어진 속삭임이 여전히 건반 위에서 떨리고 있었다."]], [["Thus she gained heart to look upon the work with which her fingers had been busy by his side on the sea-shore; and thus it was not very long before she took to it again—with something of a human love for it, as if it had been sentient and had known him; and, sitting in a window, near her mother’s picture, in the unused room so long deserted, wore away the thoughtful hours.", "이렇게 하여 그녀는, 바닷가에서 그의 곁에 앉아 자기 손가락이 바삐 매만지던 그 일거리를 바라볼 용기를 얻었다. 그리고 이렇게 하여 오래지 않아 그녀는 다시 그것을 손에 들게 되었다—마치 그것이 감각을 지녀 그를 알기라도 했던 듯, 그것에 대한 어떤 인간적인 사랑을 품고서. 그리고 그토록 오래 버려졌던 그 쓰지 않는 방, 어머니의 초상화 가까운 창가에 앉아, 생각에 잠긴 시간들을 흘려보냈다."]], [["Why did the dark eyes turn so often from this work to where the rosy children lived?", "어째서 그 검은 눈은 이 일거리에서 그토록 자주, 발그레한 아이들이 사는 쪽으로 돌아갔을까?"], ["They were not immediately suggestive of her loss; for they were all girls: four little sisters.", "그 아이들이 곧바로 그녀의 상실을 떠올리게 한 것은 아니었다. 그들은 모두 여자아이였으니까."], ["But they were motherless like her—and had a father.", "네 명의 어린 자매였다. 그러나 그들은 그녀처럼 어미가 없었다—그리고 아버지가 있었다."]], [["It was easy to know when he had gone out and was expected home, for the elder child was always dressed and waiting for him at the drawing-room window, or on the balcony; and when he appeared, her expectant face lighted up with joy, while the others at the high window, and always on the watch too, clapped their hands, and drummed them on the sill, and called to him. The elder child would come down to the hall, and put her hand in his, and lead him up the stairs; and Florence would see her afterwards sitting by his side, or on his knee, or hanging coaxingly about his neck and talking to him: and though they were always gay together, he would often watch her face as if he thought her like her mother that was dead. Florence would sometimes look no more at this, and bursting into tears would hide behind the curtain as if she were frightened, or would hurry from the window. Yet she could not help returning; and her work would soon fall unheeded from her hands again.", "그가 외출했다가 돌아올 때가 되었음을 알기란 쉬웠다. 큰 아이가 늘 차려입고 응접실 창가나 발코니에서 그를 기다리고 있었기 때문이다. 그가 나타나면, 그 아이의 기대에 찬 얼굴이 기쁨으로 환해졌고, 한편 높은 창가에 있던, 역시 늘 망을 보던 다른 아이들은 손뼉을 치고, 창턱을 두드리고, 그를 부르며 소리쳤다. 큰 아이는 현관으로 내려와, 그의 손에 제 손을 얹고, 그를 위층으로 이끌어 올라갔다. 그러면 플로렌스는 그 뒤에 그 아이가 그의 곁에, 혹은 그의 무릎에 앉아 있거나, 어리광 부리듯 그의 목에 매달려 그에게 이야기하는 모습을 보곤 했다. 그리고 그들은 늘 함께 즐거웠지만, 그는 마치 그 아이가 죽은 제 어미를 닮았다고 여기기라도 하는 듯, 자주 그 아이의 얼굴을 바라보곤 했다. 플로렌스는 때때로 이것을 더는 보지 못하고, 울음을 터뜨리며 마치 겁이라도 먹은 듯 커튼 뒤에 숨거나, 창가에서 황급히 자리를 뜨곤 했다. 그러나 그녀는 다시 돌아오지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그녀의 일거리는 곧 다시, 돌보아지지 못한 채 그녀의 두 손에서 떨어지곤 했다."]], [["It was the house that had been empty, years ago.", "그것은 여러 해 전에 비어 있던 그 집이었다."], ["It had remained so for a long time.", "그 집은 오랫동안 그대로 비어 있었다."], ["At last, and while she had been away from home, this family had taken it; and it was repaired and newly painted; and there were birds and flowers about it; and it looked very different from its old self.", "마침내, 그녀가 집을 떠나 있는 동안, 이 가족이 그 집을 들었다. 그리고 그 집은 수리되고 새로 칠해졌으며, 그 주위에는 새와 꽃들이 있었다."], ["But she never thought of the house.", "그래서 옛 모습과는 무척 달라 보였다."], ["The children and their father were all in all.", "그러나 그녀는 그 집은 결코 떠올리지 않았다. 그 아이들과 그들의 아버지가 전부였다."]], [["When he had dined, she could see them, through the open windows, go down with their governess or nurse, and cluster round the table; and in the still summer weather, the sound of their childish voices and clear laughter would come ringing across the street, into the drooping air of the room in which she sat.", "그가 저녁을 먹고 나면, 그녀는 열린 창문 너머로, 그 아이들이 가정교사나 유모와 함께 내려와 탁자 둘레에 모여드는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그리고 고요한 여름 날씨 속에서, 그 아이들의 어린 목소리와 맑은 웃음소리가 거리를 가로질러 울려와, 그녀가 앉아 있는 방의 가라앉은 공기 속으로 들어오곤 했다."], ["Then they would climb and clamber upstairs with him, and romp about him on the sofa, or group themselves at his knee, a very nosegay of little faces, while he seemed to tell them some story.", "그러고 나면 그들은 그와 함께 위층으로 기어오르듯 올라가, 소파에서 그를 둘러싸고 뛰놀거나, 그의 무릎가에 옹기종기 모였으니, 자그마한 얼굴들의 영락없는 꽃다발이었다. 그러는 동안 그는 그들에게 무슨 이야기인가를 들려주는 듯했다."], ["Or they would come running out into the balcony; and then Florence would hide herself quickly, lest it should check them in their joy, to see her in her black dress, sitting there alone.", "혹은 그들은 발코니로 달려 나오기도 했다. 그러면 플로렌스는, 검은 옷을 입고 거기 홀로 앉아 있는 자기를 보고 그들의 기쁨에 찬물을 끼얹게 될까 봐, 얼른 몸을 숨기곤 했다."]], [["The elder child remained with her father when the rest had gone away, and made his tea for him—happy little house-keeper she was then!—and sat conversing with him, sometimes at the window, sometimes in the room, until the candles came.", "나머지 아이들이 가 버리면 큰 아이는 아버지 곁에 남아 그를 위해 차를 끓였으니—그때 그 아이는 행복한 작은 살림꾼이었다!—그리고 양초가 들어올 때까지, 때로는 창가에서, 때로는 방 안에서, 그와 이야기를 나누며 앉아 있었다."], ["He made her his companion, though she was some years younger than Florence; and she could be as staid and pleasantly demure, with her little book or work-box, as a woman.", "그는 그 아이를, 비록 플로렌스보다 몇 살 어렸으나, 자기 동무로 삼았다. 그리고 그 아이는 제 작은 책이나 반짇고리를 들고서, 한 사람의 여인만큼이나 침착하고 사랑스럽도록 얌전할 수 있었다."], ["When they had candles, Florence from her own dark room was not afraid to look again.", "양초가 들어오면, 플로렌스는 제 어두운 방에서 다시 바라보기를 두려워하지 않았다."], ["But when the time came for the child to say “Good-night, Papa,”", "그러나 그 아이가 \"안녕히 주무세요, 아버지\""], ["and go to bed, Florence would sob and tremble as she raised her face to him, and could look no more.", "하고 말하고 잠자리에 들 시간이 되면, 플로렌스는 그를 향해 얼굴을 들어 올리며 흐느끼고 떨었고, 더는 바라볼 수 없었다."]], [["Though still she would turn, again and again, before going to bed herself from the simple air that had lulled him to rest so often, long ago, and from the other low soft broken strain of music, back to that house.", "그래도 그녀는, 자기가 잠자리에 들기 전이면, 오래전 그토록 자주 그를 잠재웠던 그 소박한 가락에서, 그리고 또 다른 낮고 부드러운 그 끊어진 음악의 가락에서, 다시 또다시 그 집으로 눈길을 돌리곤 했다."], ["But that she ever thought of it, or watched it, was a secret which she kept within her own young breast.", "그러나 그녀가 그 집을 떠올렸다는 것, 혹은 그 집을 지켜보았다는 것은, 그녀가 제 어린 가슴속에 간직한 비밀이었다."]], [["And did that breast of Florence—Florence, so ingenuous and true—so worthy of the love that he had borne her, and had whispered in his last faint words—whose guileless heart was mirrored in the beauty of her face, and breathed in every accent of her gentle voice—did that young breast hold any other secret?", "그런데 플로렌스의 그 가슴은—그토록 천진하고 진실한, 그가 그녀에게 품었던, 마지막 가냘픈 말로 속삭였던 그 사랑을 받을 만한, 그 꾸밈없는 마음이 얼굴의 아름다움에 비치고 부드러운 목소리의 한마디 한마디에 배어 나오는 그 플로렌스의 가슴은—그 어린 가슴은 또 다른 비밀을 품고 있었을까? 그렇다."], ["Yes. One more.", "하나 더."]], [["When no one in the house was stirring, and the lights were all extinguished, she would softly leave her own room, and with noiseless feet descend the staircase, and approach her father’s door.", "집 안의 누구도 움직이지 않고 등불이 모두 꺼졌을 때, 그녀는 가만히 제 방을 나와, 소리 없는 발걸음으로 계단을 내려가, 아버지의 방문에 다가가곤 했다."], ["Against it, scarcely breathing, she would rest her face and head, and press her lips, in the yearning of her love.", "그 문에 대고, 숨도 거의 쉬지 못한 채, 그녀는 얼굴과 머리를 기대고, 사랑의 그리움 속에서 입술을 갖다 댔다."], ["She crouched upon the cold stone floor outside it, every night, to listen even for his breath; and in her one absorbing wish to be allowed to show him some affection, to be a consolation to him, to win him over to the endurance of some tenderness from her, his solitary child, she would have knelt down at his feet, if she had dared, in humble supplication.", "그녀는 밤마다 그 문 밖 차가운 돌바닥에 웅크려, 그의 숨소리만이라도 들으려 했다. 그리고 그에게 어떤 애정이나마 보여 주고, 그에게 위안이 되어 주고, 그의 외로운 자식인 자기에게서 우러난 어떤 다정함을 견뎌 내도록 그를 마음 돌리게 하고 싶다는, 그 하나에 사로잡힌 소원으로, 그녀는 감히 그럴 수만 있었다면, 그의 발치에 무릎 꿇고 겸허히 애원했을 것이다."]], [["No one knew it.", "아무도 그것을 알지 못했다."], ["No one thought of it.", "아무도 그것을 생각하지 못했다."], ["The door was ever closed, and he shut up within.", "문은 늘 닫혀 있었고, 그는 그 안에 틀어박혀 있었다."], ["He went out once or twice, and it was said in the house that he was very soon going on his country journey; but he lived in those rooms, and lived alone, and never saw her, or inquired for her.", "그는 한두 번 외출했고, 집안에서는 그가 머지않아 시골 여행을 떠날 거라는 말이 돌았다. 그러나 그는 그 방들에서 살았고, 홀로 살았으며, 결코 그녀를 보지도, 그녀의 안부를 묻지도 않았다."], ["Perhaps he did not even know that she was in the house.", "어쩌면 그는 그녀가 집에 있다는 것조차 알지 못했을 것이다."]], [["One day, about a week after the funeral, Florence was sitting at her work, when Susan appeared, with a face half laughing and half crying, to announce a visitor.", "어느 날, 장례가 있은 지 일주일쯤 지났을 때, 플로렌스가 일거리를 하며 앉아 있는데, 수전이 반은 웃고 반은 우는 얼굴로 나타나, 방문객이 왔다고 알렸다."]], [["“A visitor!", "\"방문객!"], ["To me, Susan!”", "나한테, 수전?\""], ["said Florence, looking up in astonishment.", "플로렌스가 깜짝 놀라 고개를 들며 말했다."]], [["“Well, it is a wonder, ain’t it now, Miss Floy?” said Susan; “but I wish you had a many visitors, I do, indeed, for you’d be all the better for it, and it’s my opinion that the sooner you and me goes even to them old Skettleses, Miss, the better for both, I may not wish to live in crowds, Miss Floy, but still I’m not a oyster.”", "\"글쎄요, 신기한 일이지요, 안 그래요, 플로이 아가씨?\" 수전이 말했다. \"하지만 저는 아가씨한테 방문객이 많았으면 좋겠어요, 정말로요. 그래야 아가씨한테 훨씬 좋을 테니까요. 그리고 제 생각엔, 아가씨랑 제가 그 늙은 스케틀스 댁에라도 빨리 갈수록, 우리 둘 다한테 좋을 것 같아요. 저는 사람들 북적이는 데서 살고 싶진 않지만요, 플로이 아가씨, 그래도 저는 굴은 아니잖아요.\""]], [["To do Miss Nipper justice, she spoke more for her young mistress than herself; and her face showed it.", "니퍼 양을 공정하게 평하자면, 그녀는 자기 자신보다 어린 여주인을 위해 더 많이 말한 것이었다. 그리고 그녀의 얼굴이 그것을 보여 주었다."]], [["“But the visitor, Susan,”", "\"그런데 방문객이라니, 수전,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["Susan, with an hysterical explosion that was as much a laugh as a sob, and as much a sob as a laugh, answered,", "수전은, 웃음이기도 하고 흐느낌이기도 한, 흐느낌이기도 하고 웃음이기도 한 히스테릭한 폭발과 함께, 대답했다,"]], [["“Mr Toots!”", "\"투츠 씨예요!\""]], [["The smile that appeared on Florence’s face passed from it in a moment, and her eyes filled with tears.", "플로렌스의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 미소가 순식간에 사라졌고, 그녀의 두 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다."], ["But at any rate it was a smile, and that gave great satisfaction to Miss Nipper.", "그러나 어쨌든 그것은 미소였고, 그것이 니퍼 양에게 큰 만족을 주었다."]], [["“My own feelings exactly, Miss Floy,”", "\"제 심정도 꼭 그래요, 플로이 아가씨,\""], ["said Susan, putting her apron to her eyes, and shaking her head.", "수전이 앞치마를 눈에 갖다 대며 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["“Immediately I see that Innocent in the Hall, Miss Floy, I burst out laughing first, and then I choked.”", "\"현관에서 그 순진한 양반을 보자마자요, 플로이 아가씨, 저는 먼저 웃음이 터져 나왔다가, 그다음엔 목이 메었지 뭐예요.\""]], [["Susan Nipper involuntarily proceeded to do the like again on the spot.", "수전 니퍼는 저도 모르게 그 자리에서 또다시 그러기 시작했다."], ["In the meantime Mr Toots, who had come upstairs after her, all unconscious of the effect he produced, announced himself with his knuckles on the door, and walked in very briskly.", "그러는 동안 그녀를 따라 위층으로 올라온 투츠 씨는, 자기가 일으킨 효과는 전혀 알지 못한 채, 손가락 마디로 문을 두드려 자기 도착을 알리고, 무척 활기차게 안으로 들어왔다."]], [["“How d’ye do, Miss Dombey?”", "\"안녕하세요, 돔비 아가씨?\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“I’m very well, I thank you; how are you?”", "\"저는 아주 잘 지냅니다, 고맙습니다. 아가씨는 어떠세요?\""]], [["Mr Toots—than whom there were few better fellows in the world, though there may have been one or two brighter spirits—had laboriously invented this long burst of discourse with the view of relieving the feelings both of Florence and himself.", "투츠 씨는—세상에 그보다 나은 사내는 거의 없었으나, 그보다 머리가 잘 도는 사람은 한둘쯤 있었을지 모른다—플로렌스와 자기 둘 다의 심정을 풀어 주고자 이 긴 한바탕의 인사말을 애써 궁리해 낸 터였다."], ["But finding that he had run through his property, as it were, in an injudicious manner, by squandering the whole before taking a chair, or before Florence had uttered a word, or before he had well got in at the door, he deemed it advisable to begin again.", "그러나 의자에 앉기도 전에, 플로렌스가 한마디 하기도 전에, 또 자기가 문 안에 채 들어서기도 전에 그 전부를 탕진해 버려, 말하자면 자기 밑천을 분별없이 다 써 버렸음을 깨닫고는, 다시 시작하는 게 좋겠다고 여겼다."]], [["“How d’ye do, Miss Dombey?”", "\"안녕하세요, 돔비 아가씨?\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“I’m very well, I thank you; how are you?”", "\"저는 아주 잘 지냅니다, 고맙습니다. 아가씨는 어떠세요?\""]], [["Florence gave him her hand, and said she was very well.", "플로렌스가 그에게 손을 내밀며, 자기는 아주 잘 지낸다고 말했다."]], [["“I’m very well indeed,”", "\"저는 정말이지 아주 잘 지냅니다,\""], ["said Mr Toots, taking a chair.", "투츠 씨가 의자에 앉으며 말했다."], ["“Very well indeed, I am.", "\"정말 잘 지내요, 그렇습니다."], ["I don’t remember,”", "제 기억으로는,\""], ["said Mr Toots, after reflecting a little, “that I was ever better, thank you.”", "투츠 씨가 잠시 생각한 뒤에 말했다, \"이보다 더 좋았던 적이 없는 것 같습니다, 고맙습니다.\""]], [["“It’s very kind of you to come,”", "\"와 주셔서 정말 고마워요,\""], ["said Florence, taking up her work, “I am very glad to see you.”", "플로렌스가 일거리를 집어 들며 말했다. \"뵙게 되어 무척 기뻐요.\""]], [["Mr Toots responded with a chuckle.", "투츠 씨는 킬킬 웃음으로 답했다."], ["Thinking that might be too lively, he corrected it with a sigh.", "그것이 너무 들떠 보일까 싶어, 그는 한숨으로 바로잡았다."], ["Thinking that might be too melancholy, he corrected it with a chuckle.", "그것이 너무 우울해 보일까 싶어, 그는 다시 킬킬 웃음으로 바로잡았다."], ["Not thoroughly pleasing himself with either mode of reply, he breathed hard.", "어느 쪽 대답 방식으로도 자기 자신이 영 흡족하지 않아, 그는 거칠게 숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“You were very kind to my dear brother,”", "\"제 사랑하는 동생에게 무척 잘 대해 주셨지요,\""], ["said Florence, obeying her own natural impulse to relieve him by saying so.", "플로렌스가, 그렇게 말함으로써 그의 마음을 풀어 주려는 자기 자신의 자연스러운 충동에 따라 말했다."], ["“He often talked to me about you.”", "\"그 애가 자주 당신 얘기를 저에게 했어요.\""]], [["“Oh it’s of no consequence,”", "\"오, 그건 아무것도 아닙니다,\""], ["said Mr Toots hastily.", "투츠 씨가 황급히 말했다."], ["“Warm, ain’t it?”", "\"덥지요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“It is beautiful weather,”", "\"날씨가 참 좋아요,\""], ["replied Florence.", "플로렌스가 대답했다."]], [["“It agrees with me!” said Mr Toots.", "\"저한테는 잘 맞아요!\""], ["“I don’t think I ever was so well as I find myself at present, I’m obliged to you.", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"지금만큼 좋았던 적이 없는 것 같습니다, 고맙습니다."]], [["After stating this curious and unexpected fact, Mr Toots fell into a deep well of silence.", "이 기묘하고 뜻밖의 사실을 밝힌 뒤, 투츠 씨는 깊은 침묵의 우물 속으로 빠져들었다."]], [["“You have left Dr Blimber’s, I think?”", "\"블림버 박사님 댁을 떠나셨지요, 아마?\""], ["said Florence, trying to help him out.", "플로렌스가 그를 끌어내 보려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“I should hope so,”", "\"그랬기를 바라야지요,\""], ["returned Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["And tumbled in again.", "그러고는 다시 빠져들었다."]], [["He remained at the bottom, apparently drowned, for at least ten minutes.", "그는 적어도 십 분 동안, 보아하니 물에 빠진 채로, 바닥에 머물러 있었다."], ["At the expiration of that period, he suddenly floated, and said,", "그 시간이 다하자, 그는 불쑥 떠올라 말했다,"]], [["“Well!", "\"자!"], ["Good morning, Miss Dombey.”", "안녕히 계세요, 돔비 아가씨.\""]], [["“Are you going?”", "\"가시려고요?\""], ["asked Florence, rising.", "플로렌스가 일어서며 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know, though.", "\"그래도 모르겠네요."], ["No, not just at present,”", "아니, 지금 당장은 아니에요,\""], ["said Mr Toots, sitting down again, most unexpectedly.", "투츠 씨가, 더없이 뜻밖으로, 다시 자리에 앉으며 말했다."], ["“The fact is—I say, Miss Dombey!”", "\"사실은—저기, 돔비 아가씨!\""]], [["“Don’t be afraid to speak to me,”", "\"저한테 말씀하시는 걸 두려워하지 마세요,\""], ["said Florence, with a quiet smile, “I should be very glad if you would talk about my brother.”", "플로렌스가 잔잔한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"동생 얘기를 해 주신다면 저는 무척 기쁠 거예요.\""]], [["“Would you, though?”", "\"정말 그러시겠어요?\""], ["retorted Mr Toots, with sympathy in every fibre of his otherwise expressionless face.", "투츠 씨가, 그 밖에는 무표정한 얼굴의 모든 결마다 공감을 담아 응수했다."], ["“Poor Dombey!", "\"가엾은 돔비!"], ["I’m sure I never thought that Burgess and Co.—fashionable tailors (but very dear), that we used to talk about—would make this suit of clothes for such a purpose.”", "우리가 늘 얘기하던 버지스 상회—세련된 양복점이지만 무척 비싼—가 이런 용도로 이 양복을 지어 줄 거라곤 정말 한 번도 생각 못 했습니다.\""], ["Mr Toots was dressed in mourning.", "투츠 씨는 상복을 입고 있었다."], ["“Poor Dombey!", "\"가엾은 돔비!"], ["I say!", "저기!"], ["Miss Dombey!”", "돔비 아가씨!\""], ["blubbered Toots.", "투츠가 엉엉 울었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“There’s a friend he took to very much at last.", "\"그 애가 마지막에 무척 좋아하게 된 친구가 하나 있어요."], ["I thought you’d like to have him, perhaps, as a sort of keepsake.", "어쩌면 아가씨가 그 녀석을, 일종의 기념품처럼, 두고 싶어 하실 것 같아서요."], ["You remember his remembering Diogenes?”", "그 애가 디오게네스를 기억했던 거, 기억하세요?\""]], [["“Oh yes!", "\"오 네!"], ["oh yes”", "오 네!\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."]], [["“Poor Dombey!", "\"가엾은 돔비!"], ["So do I,”", "저도 기억합니다,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr Toots, seeing Florence in tears, had great difficulty in getting beyond this point, and had nearly tumbled into the well again.", "투츠 씨는 플로렌스가 눈물짓는 것을 보고, 이 대목을 넘어가기가 무척 어려워, 하마터면 다시 우물에 빠질 뻔했다."], ["But a chuckle saved him on the brink.", "그러나 킬킬 웃음이 그 가장자리에서 그를 구해 주었다."]], [["“I say,”", "\"저기요,\""], ["he proceeded, “Miss Dombey!", "그가 말을 이었다, \"돔비 아가씨!"], ["I could have had him stolen for ten shillings, if they hadn’t given him up: and I would: but they were glad to get rid of him, I think.", "그쪽에서 그 녀석을 내주지 않았더라면, 저는 십 실링에 그 녀석을 훔쳐 오게 할 수도 있었어요. 그리고 그렇게 했을 거예요."], ["If you’d like to have him, he’s at the door.", "하지만 제 생각엔 그쪽도 그 녀석을 떼어 내게 되어 좋아하는 눈치였어요."], ["I brought him on purpose for you.", "그 녀석을 두고 싶으시면, 문 앞에 와 있어요."], ["He ain’t a lady’s dog, you know,”", "아가씨 드리려고 일부러 데려왔거든요."], ["said Mr Toots, “but you won’t mind that, will you?”", "숙녀에게 어울리는 개는 아니에요, 아시다시피,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"하지만 그건 신경 안 쓰시겠지요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["In fact, Diogenes was at that moment, as they presently ascertained from looking down into the street, staring through the window of a hackney cabriolet, into which, for conveyance to that spot, he had been ensnared, on a false pretence of rats among the straw.", "사실, 디오게네스는 바로 그 순간, 그들이 곧 거리를 내려다보며 확인했듯이, 삯마차의 창문 너머로 노려보고 있었다. 그 마차에는, 그 자리로 실어 오기 위해, 짚 속에 쥐가 있다는 거짓 핑계로 그를 꾀어 넣은 것이었다."], ["Sooth to say, he was as unlike a lady’s dog as might be; and in his gruff anxiety to get out, presented an appearance sufficiently unpromising, as he gave short yelps out of one side of his mouth, and overbalancing himself by the intensity of every one of those efforts, tumbled down into the straw, and then sprung panting up again, putting out his tongue, as if he had come express to a Dispensary to be examined for his health.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그는 숙녀에게 어울리는 개와는 더없이 동떨어진 모습이었다. 그리고 밖으로 나가고 싶어 안달하는 그 무뚝뚝한 모습은 어지간히 시원찮아 보였으니, 그는 입 한쪽으로 짧게 캉캉 짖어 대고, 그 한 번 한 번의 안간힘에 너무 힘을 줘서 제 몸의 균형을 잃고 짚 속으로 고꾸라졌다가, 다시 헐떡이며 벌떡 솟구쳐 올라, 마치 건강 검진을 받으러 진료소에 급히 달려오기라도 한 양 혀를 쑥 내밀었다."]], [["But though Diogenes was as ridiculous a dog as one would meet with on a summer’s day; a blundering, ill-favoured, clumsy, bullet-headed dog, continually acting on a wrong idea that there was an enemy in the neighbourhood, whom it was meritorious to bark at; and though he was far from good-tempered, and certainly was not clever, and had hair all over his eyes, and a comic nose, and an inconsistent tail, and a gruff voice; he was dearer to Florence, in virtue of that parting remembrance of him, and that request that he might be taken care of, than the most valuable and beautiful of his kind.", "그러나 디오게네스가 여름날에 마주칠 수 있는 가장 우스꽝스러운 개였다 해도—덤벙대고, 못생기고, 굼뜨고, 머리통은 총알처럼 둥글고, 근처에 짖어 댈 만한 가치가 있는 적이 있다는 그릇된 생각에 노상 사로잡혀 있는 개였다 해도—그리고 비록 그가 결코 성미가 좋지 않았고, 분명 영리하지도 않았으며, 두 눈을 온통 털로 뒤덮고, 익살맞은 코에, 어딘가 어긋난 꼬리에, 무뚝뚝한 목소리를 지녔다 해도—그는, 그를 두고 작별의 순간에 남긴 그 기억과, 그를 잘 보살펴 달라던 그 부탁 덕분에, 플로렌스에게는 그 종족 가운데 가장 값지고 아름다운 개보다도 더 소중했다."], ["So dear, indeed, was this same ugly Diogenes, and so welcome to her, that she took the jewelled hand of Mr Toots and kissed it in her gratitude.", "정말이지 이 못생긴 디오게네스가 어찌나 소중하고, 그녀에게 어찌나 반가웠던지, 그녀는 투츠 씨의 보석 박힌 손을 잡고 고마운 마음에 거기에 입을 맞추었다."], ["And when Diogenes, released, came tearing up the stairs and bouncing into the room (such a business as there was, first, to get him out of the cabriolet!), dived under all the furniture, and wound a long iron chain, that dangled from his neck, round legs of chairs and tables, and then tugged at it until his eyes became unnaturally visible, in consequence of their nearly starting out of his head; and when he growled at Mr Toots, who affected familiarity; and went pell-mell at Towlinson, morally convinced that he was the enemy whom he had barked at round the corner all his life and had never seen yet; Florence was as pleased with him as if he had been a miracle of discretion.", "그리고 풀려난 디오게네스가 계단을 마구 뛰어올라 방 안으로 튀어 들어와서는(먼저 그를 삯마차에서 끄집어내느라 어찌나 한바탕 야단이었던지!), 온갖 가구 밑으로 파고들고, 제 목에 달랑거리며 매달린 긴 쇠사슬을 의자며 탁자 다리에 칭칭 감았다가, 두 눈이 머리에서 거의 튀어나올 지경이 되어 부자연스럽게 도드라져 보일 때까지 그것을 잡아당기고, 친한 척하는 투츠 씨에게 으르렁대고, 또 자기가 평생 모퉁이를 돌며 짖어 댔으되 여태 한 번도 본 적 없는 바로 그 적이라고 도덕적으로 확신하며 타울린슨에게 무작정 덤벼들 때, 플로렌스는 마치 그가 분별력의 기적이라도 되는 양 그를 기뻐했다."]], [["Mr Toots was so overjoyed by the success of his present, and was so delighted to see Florence bending down over Diogenes, smoothing his coarse back with her little delicate hand—Diogenes graciously allowing it from the first moment of their acquaintance—that he felt it difficult to take leave, and would, no doubt, have been a much longer time in making up his mind to do so, if he had not been assisted by Diogenes himself, who suddenly took it into his head to bay Mr Toots, and to make short runs at him with his mouth open.", "투츠 씨는 자기 선물의 성공에 어찌나 기뻤던지, 그리고 플로렌스가 디오게네스 위로 몸을 굽혀, 작고 섬세한 손으로 그의 거친 등을 쓰다듬는 것을—디오게네스는 그들이 안 첫 순간부터 그것을 너그러이 허락했다—보고 어찌나 즐거웠던지, 작별을 고하기가 어렵게 느껴졌다. 그래서, 만약 디오게네스 본인이 거들어 주지 않았더라면, 의심할 바 없이 작별을 결심하는 데 훨씬 더 오랜 시간이 걸렸을 것이다."], ["Not exactly seeing his way to the end of these demonstrations, and sensible that they placed the pantaloons constructed by the art of Burgess and Co.", "디오게네스는 불쑥 투츠 씨를 향해 짖어 대고, 입을 벌린 채 그에게 짧게 달려드는 짓을 머릿속에 떠올린 것이다."], ["in jeopardy, Mr Toots, with chuckles, lapsed out at the door: by which, after looking in again two or three times, without any object at all, and being on each occasion greeted with a fresh run from Diogenes, he finally took himself off and got away.", "이 시위가 어떻게 끝날지 영 갈피를 잡지 못하고, 또 그것이 버지스 상회의 솜씨로 지어진 바지를 위태롭게 한다는 것을 의식한 투츠 씨는, 킬킬 웃으며 문밖으로 슬그머니 빠져나갔다. 그러고는 아무 용건도 없이 두세 번 다시 안을 들여다보았다가, 그때마다 디오게네스가 새로이 달려드는 통에, 마침내 자리를 떠 가 버렸다."]], [["“Come, then, Di! Dear Di!", "\"이리 와, 그럼, 디!"], ["Make friends with your new mistress.", "사랑스러운 디! 새 여주인하고 친해지렴."], ["Let us love each other, Di!”", "우리 서로 사랑하자, 디!\""], ["said Florence, fondling his shaggy head.", "플로렌스가 그의 텁수룩한 머리를 어루만지며 말했다."], ["And Di, the rough and gruff, as if his hairy hide were pervious to the tear that dropped upon it, and his dog’s heart melted as it fell, put his nose up to her face, and swore fidelity.", "그러자 거칠고 무뚝뚝한 디는, 마치 그 텁수룩한 가죽이 거기 떨어진 눈물을 스며들게 하고, 그 눈물이 떨어지자 제 개의 마음이 녹아내리기라도 한 듯, 코를 들어 그녀의 얼굴에 갖다 대고는, 충성을 맹세했다."]], [["Diogenes the man did not speak plainer to Alexander the Great than Diogenes the dog spoke to Florence.", "사람 디오게네스가 알렉산드로스 대왕에게 한 말도, 개 디오게네스가 플로렌스에게 한 말보다 더 분명하지는 않았다."], ["He subscribed to the offer of his little mistress cheerfully, and devoted himself to her service.", "그는 작은 여주인의 제안에 기꺼이 동의하고, 그녀를 섬기는 데 헌신했다."], ["A banquet was immediately provided for him in a corner; and when he had eaten and drunk his fill, he went to the window where Florence was sitting, looking on, rose up on his hind legs, with his awkward fore paws on her shoulders, licked her face and hands, nestled his great head against her heart, and wagged his tail till he was tired.", "그를 위해 한쪽 구석에 곧바로 잔칫상이 차려졌다. 그리고 그가 배불리 먹고 마신 뒤, 그는 플로렌스가 앉아 바라보고 있는 창가로 가, 뒷다리로 일어서서, 어색한 앞발을 그녀의 어깨에 얹고, 그녀의 얼굴과 손을 핥고, 그 큰 머리를 그녀의 가슴에 파묻고, 지칠 때까지 꼬리를 흔들었다."], ["Finally, Diogenes coiled himself up at her feet and went to sleep.", "마침내, 디오게네스는 그녀의 발치에 몸을 둥글게 말고 잠이 들었다."]], [["Although Miss Nipper was nervous in regard of dogs, and felt it necessary to come into the room with her skirts carefully collected about her, as if she were crossing a brook on stepping-stones; also to utter little screams and stand up on chairs when Diogenes stretched himself, she was in her own manner affected by the kindness of Mr Toots, and could not see Florence so alive to the attachment and society of this rude friend of little Paul’s, without some mental comments thereupon that brought the water to her eyes.", "니퍼 양은 개에 대해서는 신경이 곤두서서, 마치 징검돌을 밟고 개울을 건너기라도 하듯 치맛자락을 조심스레 그러모은 채 방에 들어와야 한다고 여겼고, 또 디오게네스가 기지개를 켤 때면 작은 비명을 지르며 의자 위로 올라서야 한다고 여겼지만, 그녀는 그녀 나름으로 투츠 씨의 친절에 마음이 움직였다. 그리고 플로렌스가 어린 폴의 이 거친 친구의 애착과 벗함에 그토록 생기를 띠는 것을, 두 눈에 눈물이 차오를 만큼의 어떤 마음속 소회 없이는 차마 볼 수 없었다."], ["Mr Dombey, as a part of her reflections, may have been, in the association of ideas, connected with the dog; but, at any rate, after observing Diogenes and his mistress all the evening, and after exerting herself with much good-will to provide Diogenes a bed in an ante-chamber outside his mistress’s door, she said hurriedly to Florence, before leaving her for the night:", "그녀의 그 생각의 한 부분으로, 어쩌면 돔비 씨가, 관념의 연상 속에서, 그 개와 결부되었을지도 모른다. 그러나 어쨌든, 저녁 내내 디오게네스와 그 여주인을 지켜본 뒤, 그리고 여주인의 방문 밖 곁방에 디오게네스의 잠자리를 마련해 주려고 무척 호의를 다해 애쓴 뒤, 그녀는 밤 인사를 하고 떠나기 전에 플로렌스에게 황급히 말했다."]], [["“Your Pa’s a going off, Miss Floy, tomorrow morning.”", "\"아버님이 떠나신대요, 플로이 아가씨, 내일 아침에요.\""]], [["“To-morrow morning, Susan?”", "\"내일 아침에, 수전?\""]], [["“Yes, Miss; that’s the orders.", "\"네, 아가씨. 그렇게 분부가 내렸어요."], ["Early.”", "일찍요.\""]], [["“Do you know,”", "\"혹시,\""], ["asked Florence, without looking at her, “where Papa is going, Susan?”", "플로렌스가 그녀를 쳐다보지 않은 채 물었다, \"아버지가 어디로 가시는지 아니, 수전?\""]], [["“Not exactly, Miss.", "\"정확히는 몰라요, 아가씨."], ["He’s going to meet that precious Major first, and I must say if I was acquainted with any Major myself (which Heavens forbid), it shouldn’t be a blue one!”", "먼저 그 잘난 소령님을 만나러 가신대요. 그리고 제가 만약 어떤 소령이든 아는 사이라면(하느님 맙소사, 그럴 리야 없겠지만), 그게 푸르딩딩한 소령은 아닐 거라고 말씀드려야겠네요!\""]], [["“Hush, Susan!”", "\"쉿, 수전!\""], ["urged Florence gently.", "플로렌스가 부드럽게 타일렀다."]], [["“Well, Miss Floy,”", "\"글쎄요, 플로이 아가씨,\""], ["returned Miss Nipper, who was full of burning indignation, and minded her stops even less than usual.", "불타는 분노로 가득 차, 평소보다도 끊어 말하는 데 더 신경 쓰지 않는 니퍼 양이 대꾸했다."], ["“I can’t help it, blue he is, and while I was a Christian, although humble, I would have natural-coloured friends, or none.”", "\"어쩔 수가 없는 걸요, 그분은 푸르딩딩하다고요. 그리고 제가 비록 보잘것없을지언정 기독교인인 한, 저는 본래 살색 친구들을 두든지, 아니면 아예 없든지 할 거예요.\""]], [["It appeared from what she added and had gleaned downstairs, that Mrs Chick had proposed the Major for Mr Dombey’s companion, and that Mr Dombey, after some hesitation, had invited him.", "그녀가 덧붙인 말과, 또 아래층에서 주워들은 것으로 미루어 보아, 칙 부인이 소령을 돔비 씨의 동행으로 추천했고, 돔비 씨는 얼마간 망설인 끝에 그를 초대한 모양이었다."]], [["“Talk of him being a change, indeed!”", "\"그분이 무슨 기분 전환이 된다니, 참!\""], ["observed Miss Nipper to herself with boundless contempt.", "니퍼 양이 끝없는 경멸을 담아 혼잣말했다."], ["“If he’s a change, give me a constancy.”", "\"그분이 변화라면, 차라리 한결같음을 주세요.\""]], [["“Good-night, Susan,”", "\"잘 자, 수전,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Good-night, my darling dear Miss Floy.”", "\"안녕히 주무세요, 우리 사랑하는 플로이 아가씨.\""]], [["Her tone of commiseration smote the chord so often roughly touched, but never listened to while she or anyone looked on.", "그 애처로워하는 어조가, 그토록 자주 거칠게 건드려졌으되 그녀나 누군가가 지켜보는 동안에는 결코 귀 기울여지지 못한 그 심금을 울렸다."], ["Florence left alone, laid her head upon her hand, and pressing the other over her swelling heart, held free communication with her sorrows.", "홀로 남은 플로렌스는, 한 손에 머리를 괴고, 다른 손을 부풀어 오르는 가슴에 누른 채, 자기의 슬픔과 거리낌 없이 교감했다."]], [["It was a wet night; and the melancholy rain fell pattering and dropping with a weary sound.", "비 내리는 밤이었다. 그리고 그 우울한 비가 지친 듯한 소리로 후두두 떨어지고 또 떨어졌다."], ["A sluggish wind was blowing, and went moaning round the house, as if it were in pain or grief.", "굼뜬 바람이 불며, 마치 고통이나 비탄에 잠긴 듯, 집 둘레를 신음하며 돌았다."], ["A shrill noise quivered through the trees.", "날카로운 소리가 나무들 사이로 떨며 지나갔다."], ["While she sat weeping, it grew late, and dreary midnight tolled out from the steeples.", "그녀가 앉아 우는 동안, 밤이 깊어 갔고, 음울한 자정이 종탑들에서 울려 퍼졌다."]], [["Florence was little more than a child in years—not yet fourteen—and the loneliness and gloom of such an hour in the great house where Death had lately made its own tremendous devastation, might have set an older fancy brooding on vague terrors.", "플로렌스는 나이로 보면 아이나 다름없었으니—아직 열네 살도 채 안 되었다—죽음이 최근 제 끔찍한 황폐를 일으킨 그 큰 집에서, 그런 시각의 외로움과 음울함은, 더 나이 든 상상력이라면 막연한 공포에 잠겨 골똘하게 만들었을지도 모른다."], ["But her innocent imagination was too full of one theme to admit them.", "그러나 그녀의 순결한 상상은 단 하나의 주제로 너무도 가득 차, 그런 것들이 비집고 들 틈이 없었다."], ["Nothing wandered in her thoughts but love—a wandering love, indeed, and castaway—but turning always to her father.", "그녀의 생각 속에서 떠도는 것은 오직 사랑뿐이었으니—참으로 떠돌고, 내쫓긴 사랑이었으나—언제나 아버지에게로 향하는 사랑이었다."]], [["There was nothing in the dropping of the rain, the moaning of the wind, the shuddering of the trees, the striking of the solemn clocks, that shook this one thought, or diminished its interest.", "비가 떨어지는 소리에도, 바람이 신음하는 소리에도, 나무들이 떠는 소리에도, 엄숙한 시계들이 시각을 알리는 소리에도, 이 한 가지 생각을 흔들거나 그 절실함을 덜어 내는 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["Her recollections of the dear dead boy—and they were never absent—were itself, the same thing.", "그 사랑하는 죽은 사내아이에 대한 그녀의 기억은—그리고 그것은 결코 떠난 적이 없었다—그것 자체로, 같은 것이었다."], ["And oh, to be shut out: to be so lost: never to have looked into her father’s face or touched him, since that hour!", "그리고 오, 밖으로 내쳐져 있다는 것, 그토록 길을 잃고 있다는 것, 그 시각 이래로 아버지의 얼굴을 들여다보거나 그를 만져 본 적이 한 번도 없다는 것!"]], [["She could not go to bed, poor child, and never had gone yet, since then, without making her nightly pilgrimage to his door.", "가엾은 아이는, 그때 이래로, 아버지의 방문으로 가는 밤 순례를 하지 않고는 잠자리에 들 수 없었고, 한 번도 든 적이 없었다."], ["It would have been a strange sad sight, to see her now, stealing lightly down the stairs through the thick gloom, and stopping at it with a beating heart, and blinded eyes, and hair that fell down loosely and unthought of; and touching it outside with her wet cheek.", "그녀가 지금, 짙은 어둠 속을 가만히 계단을 내려가, 두근거리는 가슴과, 눈물로 흐려진 눈과, 생각 없이 풀어헤쳐져 흘러내린 머리칼로 그 문 앞에 멈춰 서서, 젖은 뺨으로 그 바깥을 어루만지는 모습을 보았다면, 참으로 기이하고 슬픈 광경이었으리라."], ["But the night covered it, and no one knew.", "그러나 밤이 그것을 가려 주었고, 아무도 알지 못했다."]], [["The moment that she touched the door on this night, Florence found that it was open.", "이날 밤, 그녀가 그 문에 손을 댄 순간, 플로렌스는 그것이 열려 있음을 발견했다."], ["For the first time it stood open, though by but a hair’s-breadth: and there was a light within.", "처음으로 그것이 열려 있었으니, 비록 머리카락 한 올 너비밖에 안 되었지만. 그리고 안에는 불빛이 있었다."], ["The first impulse of the timid child—and she yielded to it—was to retire swiftly.", "그 겁 많은 아이의 첫 충동은—그리고 그녀는 그것에 굴복했다—재빨리 물러서는 것이었다."], ["Her next, to go back, and to enter; and this second impulse held her in irresolution on the staircase.", "다음 충동은, 돌아가, 들어서는 것이었다. 그리고 이 두 번째 충동이 그녀를 계단 위에서 결단하지 못한 채 붙들어 두었다."]], [["In its standing open, even by so much as that chink, there seemed to be hope.", "그것이 그만큼의 틈만큼이라도 열려 있다는 데에, 희망이 깃들어 있는 듯했다."], ["There was encouragement in seeing a ray of light from within, stealing through the dark stern doorway, and falling in a thread upon the marble floor.", "안에서 새어 나온 한 줄기 불빛이, 그 어둡고 엄숙한 문간을 가로질러 스며 나와, 한 가닥 실처럼 대리석 바닥에 떨어지는 것을 보는 데에 격려가 있었다."], ["She turned back, hardly knowing what she did, but urged on by the love within her, and the trial they had undergone together, but not shared: and with her hands a little raised and trembling, glided in.", "그녀는, 자기가 무엇을 하는지도 잘 모른 채, 그러나 안에 있는 사랑과, 함께 겪었으되 나누지는 못한 그 시련에 떠밀려, 발길을 돌렸다. 그리고 두 손을 약간 들어 올린 채 떨면서, 안으로 미끄러져 들어갔다."]], [["Her father sat at his old table in the middle room.", "그녀의 아버지는 가운데 방의 그 오래된 탁자에 앉아 있었다."], ["He had been arranging some papers, and destroying others, and the latter lay in fragile ruins before him.", "그는 어떤 서류들은 정리하고 다른 것들은 없애고 있었으며, 후자는 그의 앞에 부서지기 쉬운 잔해로 놓여 있었다."], ["The rain dripped heavily upon the glass panes in the outer room, where he had so often watched poor Paul, a baby; and the low complainings of the wind were heard without.", "그가 아기였던 가엾은 폴을 그토록 자주 지켜보던 바깥방에서, 비가 유리창에 무겁게 똑똑 떨어졌다. 그리고 밖에서는 바람의 낮은 하소연이 들려왔다."]], [["But not by him.", "그러나 그에게는 들리지 않았다."], ["He sat with his eyes fixed on the table, so immersed in thought, that a far heavier tread than the light foot of his child could make, might have failed to rouse him.", "그는 두 눈을 탁자에 고정한 채, 생각에 너무도 깊이 잠겨 있어, 제 아이의 가벼운 발걸음보다 훨씬 더 묵직한 발소리라도 그를 깨우지 못했을 것이다."], ["His face was turned towards her.", "그의 얼굴은 그녀 쪽을 향하고 있었다."], ["By the waning lamp, and at that haggard hour, it looked worn and dejected; and in the utter loneliness surrounding him, there was an appeal to Florence that struck home.", "사그라드는 등불 아래, 그 초췌한 시각에, 그것은 지치고 풀 죽어 보였다. 그리고 그를 둘러싼 그 지독한 외로움 속에, 플로렌스의 가슴을 후벼 파는 어떤 호소가 있었다."]], [["“Papa!", "\"아버지!"], ["Papa!", "아버지!"], ["speak to me, dear Papa!”", "말씀 좀 해 주세요, 사랑하는 아버지!\""]], [["He started at her voice, and leaped up from his seat.", "그는 그녀의 목소리에 흠칫 놀라, 자리에서 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["She was close before him with extended arms, but he fell back.", "그녀는 두 팔을 벌린 채 그의 바로 앞에 있었으나, 그는 뒤로 물러섰다."]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이냐?\""], ["he said, sternly.", "그가 매섭게 말했다."], ["“Why do you come here?", "\"왜 여기 왔느냐?"], ["What has frightened you?”", "무엇이 너를 놀라게 했느냐?\""]], [["If anything had frightened her, it was the face he turned upon her.", "만약 무언가가 그녀를 놀라게 했다면, 그것은 그가 그녀에게로 돌린 그 얼굴이었다."], ["The glowing love within the breast of his young daughter froze before it, and she stood and looked at him as if stricken into stone.", "그의 어린 딸의 가슴속에서 타오르던 사랑이 그 앞에서 얼어붙었다. 그리고 그녀는 마치 돌로 굳어 버린 듯, 서서 그를 바라보았다."]], [["There was not one touch of tenderness or pity in it.", "거기에는 다정함이나 연민의 기색이 한 점도 없었다."], ["There was not one gleam of interest, parental recognition, or relenting in it.", "거기에는 관심이나, 어버이로서의 알아봄이나, 누그러짐의 빛이 한 줄기도 없었다."], ["There was a change in it, but not of that kind.", "거기에는 어떤 변화가 있었으나, 그런 종류의 것은 아니었다."], ["The old indifference and cold constraint had given place to something: what, she never thought and did not dare to think, and yet she felt it in its force, and knew it well without a name: that as it looked upon her, seemed to cast a shadow on her head.", "옛날의 그 무관심과 차가운 거북함이 어떤 것에 자리를 내주었으니—그것이 무엇인지 그녀는 결코 생각하지 못했고, 감히 생각하려 하지도 않았으나, 그래도 그 위력을 느꼈으며, 이름은 붙이지 못해도 그것을 잘 알았다. 그것은, 그녀를 바라볼 때, 그녀의 머리 위에 그늘을 드리우는 듯했다."]], [["Did he see before him the successful rival of his son, in health and life?", "그는 제 아들의, 건강과 생명에서의 성공한 경쟁자를 자기 앞에 두고 보았던 것일까?"], ["Did he look upon his own successful rival in that son’s affection?", "그 아들의 애정에서의 자기 자신의 성공한 경쟁자를 바라보았던 것일까?"], ["Did a mad jealousy and withered pride, poison sweet remembrances that should have endeared and made her precious to him?", "미친 질투와 시들어 버린 자존심이, 마땅히 그녀를 그에게 사랑스럽고 소중하게 만들었어야 할 그 달콤한 기억들을 독으로 물들였던 것일까?"], ["Could it be possible that it was gall to him to look upon her in her beauty and her promise: thinking of his infant boy!", "그 어린 아들을 생각하며, 그녀의 아름다움과 그 가능성을 바라보는 것이 그에게는 쓰디쓴 쓸개즙이었다는 것이 과연 있을 수 있는 일일까!"]], [["Florence had no such thoughts.", "플로렌스에게는 그런 생각이 없었다."], ["But love is quick to know when it is spurned and hopeless: and hope died out of hers, as she stood looking in her father’s face.", "그러나 사랑은 언제 자기가 내쳐지고 가망 없는지를 재빨리 안다. 그리고 그녀의 사랑에서, 그녀가 서서 아버지의 얼굴을 들여다볼 때, 희망이 사그라들었다."]], [["“I ask you, Florence, are you frightened?", "\"내가 묻잖느냐, 플로렌스, 놀랐느냐?"], ["Is there anything the matter, that you come here?”", "무슨 일이 있어서 여기 온 것이냐?\""]], [["“I came, Papa—”", "\"저는 왔어요, 아버지—\""]], [["“Against my wishes.", "\"내 뜻을 거슬러서."], ["Why?”", "왜?\""]], [["She saw he knew why: it was written broadly on his face: and dropped her head upon her hands with one prolonged low cry.", "그녀는 그가 왜인지 알고 있음을 보았다. 그것은 그의 얼굴에 큼지막하게 쓰여 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 한 차례 길고 나직한 외마디 소리와 함께 두 손에 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["Let him remember it in that room, years to come.", "그가 그것을 그 방에서 기억하기를, 다가올 세월에."], ["It has faded from the air, before he breaks the silence.", "그가 침묵을 깨기 전에, 그것은 이미 허공에서 사그라들었다."], ["It may pass as quickly from his brain, as he believes, but it is there.", "그것은, 그가 믿는 대로, 그의 머릿속에서도 그만큼 빠르게 지나가 버릴지 모른다."], ["Let him remember it in that room, years to come!", "그러나 그것은 거기 있다. 그가 그것을 그 방에서 기억하기를, 다가올 세월에!"]], [["He took her by the arm.", "그는 그녀의 팔을 붙들었다."], ["His hand was cold, and loose, and scarcely closed upon her.", "그의 손은 차갑고, 힘이 없었으며, 그녀를 거의 움켜쥐지 않았다."]], [["“You are tired, I daresay,”", "\"너는 피곤할 게다, 아마,\""], ["he said, taking up the light, and leading her towards the door, “and want rest.", "그가 등불을 집어 들고 그녀를 문 쪽으로 이끌며 말했다, \"그리고 쉬고 싶을 테지."], ["We all want rest.", "우리는 모두 쉬고 싶단다."], ["Go, Florence.", "가거라, 플로렌스."], ["You have been dreaming.”", "너는 꿈을 꾸고 있었던 거야.\""]], [["The dream she had had, was over then, God help her!", "그녀가 꾸었던 꿈은, 그때 끝나 버렸다, 하느님 그녀를 도우소서!"], ["and she felt that it could never more come back.", "그리고 그녀는 그것이 다시는 결코 돌아올 수 없으리라는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“I will remain here to light you up the stairs.", "\"네가 위층으로 올라가도록 불을 비춰 주려고 여기 있겠다."], ["The whole house is yours above there,”", "저 위 온 집이 다 네 것이다,\""], ["said her father, slowly.", "그녀의 아버지가 천천히 말했다."], ["“You are its mistress now.", "\"이제 네가 그 집의 안주인이야."], ["Good-night!”", "잘 자거라!\""]], [["Still covering her face, she sobbed, and answered “Good-night, dear Papa,”", "여전히 얼굴을 가린 채, 그녀는 흐느끼며 \"안녕히 주무세요, 사랑하는 아버지\""], ["and silently ascended.", "하고 대답하고는, 말없이 올라갔다."], ["Once she looked back as if she would have returned to him, but for fear.", "한 번 그녀는, 마치 그에게로 돌아가고 싶었으나 두려움 때문에 그러지 못하는 듯, 뒤를 돌아보았다."], ["It was a momentary thought, too hopeless to encourage; and her father stood there with the light—hard, unresponsive, motionless—until the fluttering dress of his fair child was lost in the darkness.", "그것은 한순간의 생각이었으니, 품기에는 너무도 가망 없었다. 그리고 그녀의 아버지는 등불을 들고 거기 서 있었다—굳고, 응답 없고, 미동도 없이—그 어여쁜 아이의 펄럭이는 옷자락이 어둠 속으로 사라질 때까지."]], [["Let him remember it in that room, years to come.", "그가 그것을 그 방에서 기억하기를, 다가올 세월에."], ["The rain that falls upon the roof: the wind that mourns outside the door: may have foreknowledge in their melancholy sound.", "지붕 위로 떨어지는 비도, 문밖에서 슬피 우는 바람도, 그 우울한 소리 속에 미리 아는 바를 품고 있을지 모른다."], ["Let him remember it in that room, years to come!", "그가 그것을 그 방에서 기억하기를, 다가올 세월에!"]], [["The last time he had watched her, from the same place, winding up those stairs, she had had her brother in her arms.", "그가 같은 자리에서 그녀가 그 계단을 올라가는 모습을 마지막으로 지켜보았을 때, 그녀는 동생을 품에 안고 있었다."], ["It did not move his heart towards her now, it steeled it: but he went into his room, and locked his door, and sat down in his chair, and cried for his lost boy.", "그것은 지금 그의 마음을 그녀에게로 움직이지 못했고, 오히려 그것을 무쇠처럼 굳혀 버렸다. 그러나 그는 제 방으로 들어가, 문을 잠그고, 의자에 앉아, 잃어버린 제 아들을 위해 울었다."]], [["Diogenes was broad awake upon his post, and waiting for his little mistress.", "디오게네스는 제 자리에서 완전히 깨어, 자기의 작은 여주인을 기다리고 있었다."]], [["“Oh, Di!", "\"오, 디!"], ["Oh, dear Di!", "오, 사랑하는 디!"], ["Love me for his sake!”", "그 애를 봐서 나를 사랑해 줘!\""]], [["Diogenes already loved her for her own, and didn’t care how much he showed it.", "디오게네스는 이미 그녀 자신을 봐서 그녀를 사랑하고 있었고, 그것을 얼마나 드러내든 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["So he made himself vastly ridiculous by performing a variety of uncouth bounces in the ante-chamber, and concluded, when poor Florence was at last asleep, and dreaming of the rosy children opposite, by scratching open her bedroom door: rolling up his bed into a pillow: lying down on the boards, at the full length of his tether, with his head towards her: and looking lazily at her, upside down, out of the tops of his eyes, until from winking and winking he fell asleep himself, and dreamed, with gruff barks, of his enemy.", "그래서 그는 곁방에서 갖가지 볼품없는 도약을 선보이며 한껏 우스꽝스러워졌고, 마침내 가엾은 플로렌스가 끝내 잠들어 맞은편의 발그레한 아이들 꿈을 꿀 때, 그녀의 침실 문을 긁어 열고, 제 잠자리를 베개처럼 둘둘 말아, 줄이 닿는 데까지 한껏 늘어진 채 마룻바닥에 머리를 그녀 쪽으로 두고 누워, 눈 위쪽으로 거꾸로 그녀를 게으르게 올려다보다가, 끔뻑끔뻑하던 끝에 저도 잠이 들어, 무뚝뚝하게 짖어 대며 제 적의 꿈을 꾸는 것으로 마무리했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIX. Walter goes away", "# CHAPTER XIX. Walter goes away"]], [["The wooden Midshipman at the Instrument-maker’s door, like the hard-hearted little Midshipman he was, remained supremely indifferent to Walter’s going away, even when the very last day of his sojourn in the back parlour was on the decline.", "나무로 깎은 견습 항해사는 기계 제작자의 가게 문 앞에서, 그 매정한 작은 항해사답게, 월터의 출발에 더없이 무심했다. 뒷방에서 머무는 마지막 날조차 저물어 가는데도 말이다."], ["With his quadrant at his round black knob of an eye, and his figure in its old attitude of indomitable alacrity, the Midshipman displayed his elfin small-clothes to the best advantage, and, absorbed in scientific pursuits, had no sympathy with worldly concerns.", "둥글고 까만 혹 같은 눈에 사분의를 댄 채, 굴복을 모르는 민첩함이라는 예의 그 자세를 취한 항해사는, 자기의 요정 같은 짧은 바지를 한껏 돋보이게 내보이며, 과학적 탐구에 몰두한 나머지 세속적인 일에는 아무런 공감도 없었다."], ["He was so far the creature of circumstances, that a dry day covered him with dust, and a misty day peppered him with little bits of soot, and a wet day brightened up his tarnished uniform for the moment, and a very hot day blistered him; but otherwise he was a callous, obdurate, conceited Midshipman, intent on his own discoveries, and caring as little for what went on about him, terrestrially, as Archimedes at the taking of Syracuse.", "그가 환경의 피조물인 점이 있다면, 건조한 날에는 먼지를 뒤집어쓰고, 안개 낀 날에는 검댕 조각들이 후추처럼 흩뿌려지며, 비 오는 날에는 빛바랜 제복이 잠시 산뜻해지고, 몹시 더운 날에는 살갗이 부풀어 오른다는 정도였다. 그 밖에는 무정하고 완고하고 자만에 찬 항해사로서, 오로지 자기의 발견에만 골몰할 뿐, 시러큐스가 함락될 때의 아르키메데스가 그러했듯이 자기 둘레에서 지상에 벌어지는 일에는 거의 관심이 없었다."]], [["Such a Midshipman he seemed to be, at least, in the then position of domestic affairs.", "적어도 당시의 집안 형편 속에서 그는 그런 항해사로 보였다."], ["Walter eyed him kindly many a time in passing in and out; and poor old Sol, when Walter was not there, would come and lean against the doorpost, resting his weary wig as near the shoe-buckles of the guardian genius of his trade and shop as he could.", "월터는 드나들며 여러 번 다정하게 그를 바라보았다. 그리고 월터가 없을 때면 가엾은 솔 노인이 와서 문기둥에 기대어, 자기 장사와 가게의 수호신인 그 인물의 구두 버클에 닿을 만큼 가까이 지친 가발을 기대 쉬곤 했다."], ["But no fierce idol with a mouth from ear to ear, and a murderous visage made of parrot’s feathers, was ever more indifferent to the appeals of its savage votaries, than was the Midshipman to these marks of attachment.", "그러나 귀에서 귀까지 입이 찢어지고 앵무새 깃털로 만든 살벌한 얼굴을 한 어떤 사나운 우상도, 자기를 섬기는 야만적인 신도들의 호소에 이 항해사가 이런 애착의 표시들에 보인 무심함보다 더 무심하지는 않았으리라."]], [["Walter’s heart felt heavy as he looked round his old bedroom, up among the parapets and chimney-pots, and thought that one more night already darkening would close his acquaintance with it, perhaps for ever.", "월터는 난간과 굴뚝들 사이 높은 곳에 있는 자기의 옛 침실을 둘러보며, 이미 어둑해져 가는 하룻밤이 더 지나면 이 방과의 인연도, 어쩌면 영영, 끝나리라는 생각에 마음이 무거웠다."], ["Dismantled of his little stock of books and pictures, it looked coldly and reproachfully on him for his desertion, and had already a foreshadowing upon it of its coming strangeness.", "자기의 자그마한 책과 그림들이 치워진 그 방은, 자기의 버림받음을 차갑게, 또 나무라듯 바라보았고, 이미 다가올 낯섦의 그림자가 드리워져 있었다. \"몇 시간만 더 지나면,\""], ["“A few hours more,”", "월터는 생각했다."], ["thought Walter, “and no dream I ever had here when I was a schoolboy will be so little mine as this old room.", "\"내가 학생이던 시절 여기서 꾼 어떤 꿈도 이 낡은 방만큼 내게서 멀어지지는 않으리라. 그 꿈은 잠 속에서 다시 찾아올지도 모르고, 나도 깨어서 이곳으로 돌아올지 모른다."], ["The dream may come back in my sleep, and I may return waking to this place, it may be: but the dream at least will serve no other master, and the room may have a score, and every one of them may change, neglect, misuse it.”", "그럴 수도 있겠지. 하지만 적어도 그 꿈만은 다른 어떤 주인도 섬기지 않을 테고, 이 방은 주인이 수두룩하게 들 테며, 그 하나하나가 방을 바꾸고, 방치하고, 함부로 쓸지도 모른다.\""]], [["But his Uncle was not to be left alone in the little back parlour, where he was then sitting by himself; for Captain Cuttle, considerate in his roughness, stayed away against his will, purposely that they should have some talk together unobserved: so Walter, newly returned home from his last day’s bustle, descended briskly, to bear him company.", "그러나 그의 삼촌을 작은 뒷방에 홀로 남겨 둘 수는 없었다. 삼촌은 그때 거기 혼자 앉아 있었던 것이다. 거친 데가 있으면서도 사려 깊은 커틀 선장은, 두 사람이 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 함께 이야기를 나눌 수 있도록, 일부러 자기 뜻을 거슬러 가며 자리를 비워 주었다. 그래서 마지막 날의 분주함을 막 마치고 집에 돌아온 월터는, 삼촌의 말동무가 되어 주려고 경쾌하게 아래층으로 내려왔다."]], [["“Uncle,”", "\"삼촌,\""], ["he said gaily, laying his hand upon the old man’s shoulder, “what shall I send you home from Barbados?”", "그는 노인의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 명랑하게 말했다. \"바베이도스에서 뭘 부쳐 드릴까요?\""]], [["“Hope, my dear Wally.", "\"희망이지, 우리 월리."], ["Hope that we shall meet again, on this side of the grave.", "우리가 이승에서 다시 만나리라는 희망 말이다."], ["Send me as much of that as you can.”", "그걸 부쳐 줄 수 있는 만큼 많이 부쳐 다오.\""]], [["“So I will, Uncle: I have enough and to spare, and I’ll not be chary of it!", "\"그럴게요, 삼촌. 제겐 차고 넘칠 만큼 있으니, 아낌없이 보내드릴게요!"], ["And as to lively turtles, and limes for Captain Cuttle’s punch, and preserves for you on Sundays, and all that sort of thing, why I’ll send you ship-loads, Uncle: when I’m rich enough.”", "그리고 펄떡이는 거북이며, 커틀 선장님 펀치에 넣을 라임이며, 삼촌 일요일에 드실 설탕절임이며, 그런 것들이라면, 삼촌, 제가 부자만 되면, 배에 가득가득 실어 보내드릴게요.\""]], [["Old Sol wiped his spectacles, and faintly smiled.", "솔 노인은 안경을 닦으며 희미하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“That’s right, Uncle!”", "\"바로 그거예요, 삼촌!\""], ["cried Walter, merrily, and clapping him half a dozen times more upon the shoulder.", "월터가 즐겁게 외치며, 또 대여섯 번 어깨를 토닥였다. \"삼촌이 절 기운 나게 해 주시잖아요!"], ["“You cheer up me! I’ll cheer up you!", "저도 삼촌을 기운 나게 해 드릴게요!"], ["We’ll be as gay as larks to-morrow morning, Uncle, and we’ll fly as high!", "우린 내일 아침 종달새처럼 명랑할 거예요, 삼촌. 종달새처럼 높이 날아오를 거고요!"], ["As to my anticipations, they are singing out of sight now.”", "제 기대로 말하면, 이젠 보이지 않을 만큼 높이 솟아 노래하고 있는걸요.\""]], [["“Wally, my dear boy,”", "\"월리, 우리 사랑하는 아이야,\" 노인이 대꾸했다."], ["returned the old man, “I’ll do my best, I’ll do my best.”", "\"나도 최선을 다하마. 최선을 다하마.\""]], [["“And your best, Uncle,”", "\"그리고 삼촌의 최선은,\""], ["said Walter, with his pleasant laugh, “is the best best that I know.", "월터가 그 유쾌한 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"제가 아는 최선 중에서도 으뜸가는 최선이지요."], ["You’ll not forget what you’re to send me, Uncle?”", "제게 뭘 부쳐 주실지 잊지 않으실 거죠, 삼촌?\""]], [["“No, Wally, no,”", "\"그럼, 월리, 잊지 않으마.\""], ["replied the old man; “everything I hear about Miss Dombey, now that she is left alone, poor lamb, I’ll write.", "노인이 대답했다. \"돔비 아가씨에 대해 듣는 건 뭐든지, 이제 그 가엾은 어린양이 홀로 남겨졌으니, 편지로 써 보내마."], ["I fear it won’t be much though, Wally.”", "하지만 그리 많지는 않을까 걱정이구나, 월리.\""]], [["“Why, I’ll tell you what, Uncle,”", "\"실은요, 제가 말씀드릴 게 있어요, 삼촌,\""], ["said Walter, after a moment’s hesitation, “I have just been up there.”", "월터가 잠시 머뭇거리다가 말했다. \"방금 거기 다녀왔거든요.\""]], [["“Ay, ay, ay?”", "\"이런, 이런, 이런?\""], ["murmured the old man, raising his eyebrows, and his spectacles with them.", "노인이 눈썹을, 그리고 그와 함께 안경을 치켜올리며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Not to see her,”", "\"아가씨를 보러 간 건 아니에요,\""], ["said Walter, “though I could have seen her, I daresay, if I had asked, Mr Dombey being out of town: but to say a parting word to Susan.", "월터가 말했다. \"물론, 청을 넣었다면 뵐 수도 있었겠지요, 돔비 씨가 시내에 안 계셨으니까요."], ["I thought I might venture to do that, you know, under the circumstances, and remembering when I saw Miss Dombey last.”", "그저 수전에게 작별 인사를 하려고요. 형편이 형편이니만큼, 또 제가 돔비 아가씨를 마지막으로 뵈었던 때를 떠올려 보면, 그 정도는 해도 괜찮겠다 싶었거든요.\""]], [["“Yes, my boy, yes,”", "\"그래, 우리 아이야, 그래.\""], ["replied his Uncle, rousing himself from a temporary abstraction.", "노인이 잠시의 멍한 상태에서 정신을 차리며 대답했다."]], [["“So I saw her,” pursued Walter, “Susan, I mean: and I told her I was off and away to-morrow. And I said, Uncle, that you had always had an interest in Miss Dombey since that night when she was here, and always wished her well and happy, and always would be proud and glad to serve her in the least: I thought I might say that, you know, under the circumstances. Don’t you think so?”", "\"그래서 그녀를 만났어요,\" 월터가 이어 갔다. \"수전 말이에요. 그리고 제가 내일 떠나 멀리 간다고 말했죠. 그리고 삼촌, 제가 이렇게 말했어요. 삼촌은 그날 밤 아가씨가 여기 오신 이래로 늘 돔비 아가씨에게 관심을 두셨고, 늘 잘되고 행복하시기를 바라셨고, 아무리 사소한 일이라도 아가씨를 위해 봉사할 수 있다면 늘 자랑스럽고 기쁘게 여기실 거라고요. 형편이 형편이니, 그 정도는 말해도 되겠다 싶었거든요. 안 그래요, 삼촌? 삼촌도 그렇게 생각하시죠?\""]], [["“Yes, my boy, yes,”", "\"그럼, 우리 아이야, 그래.\""], ["replied his Uncle, in the tone as before.", "노인이 아까와 같은 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“And I added,”", "\"그리고 제가 이렇게 덧붙였어요,\""], ["pursued Walter, “that if she—Susan, I mean—could ever let you know, either through herself, or Mrs Richards, or anybody else who might be coming this way, that Miss Dombey was well and happy, you would take it very kindly, and would write so much to me, and I should take it very kindly too.", "월터가 이어 갔다. \"만약 그녀가—수전 말이에요—혹시라도, 자기를 통해서든, 리처즈 부인을 통해서든, 아니면 이쪽으로 올 만한 누구를 통해서든, 돔비 아가씨가 잘 계시고 행복하시다는 걸 삼촌께 알려 줄 수 있다면, 삼촌이 무척 고맙게 여기시고 제게 그 소식을 편지로 적어 보내 주실 거고, 저 역시 무척 고맙게 여길 거라고요."], ["There!", "자! 정말이지, 삼촌,\""], ["Upon my word, Uncle,”", "월터가 말했다. \"어젯밤 내내 이 일을 할까 생각하느라 잠을 거의 못 잤어요."], ["said Walter, “I scarcely slept all last night through thinking of doing this; and could not make up my mind when I was out, whether to do it or not; and yet I am sure it is the true feeling of my heart, and I should have been quite miserable afterwards if I had not relieved it.”", "그러고도 밖에 나가서까지 할지 말지 마음을 정하지 못했죠. 하지만 이게 제 마음에서 우러난 진심임은 분명하고, 만일 그 마음을 풀어 놓지 않았더라면 두고두고 정말 비참했을 거예요.\""]], [["His honest voice and manner corroborated what he said, and quite established its ingenuousness.", "그의 정직한 목소리와 태도가 그가 한 말을 뒷받침해 주었고, 그 진솔함을 온전히 입증해 주었다."]], [["“So, if you ever see her, Uncle,”", "\"그러니, 삼촌, 혹시라도 그녀를 보시거든,\""], ["said Walter, “I mean Miss Dombey now—and perhaps you may, who knows!—tell her how much I felt for her; how much I used to think of her when I was here; how I spoke of her, with the tears in my eyes, Uncle, on this last night before I went away.", "월터가 말했다. \"이제는 돔비 아가씨 말이에요—그리고 어쩌면 보시게 될지도 모르죠, 누가 알아요!—제가 아가씨를 얼마나 위했는지, 여기 있을 때 아가씨 생각을 얼마나 자주 했는지, 떠나기 전 이 마지막 밤에 눈에 눈물이 고인 채 아가씨 이야기를 했다고 전해 주세요, 삼촌."], ["Tell her that I said I never could forget her gentle manner, or her beautiful face, or her sweet kind disposition that was better than all.", "제가 이렇게 말했다고 전해 주세요. 그 부드러운 태도며, 그 아름다운 얼굴이며, 무엇보다 가장 좋았던 그 다정하고 친절한 성품을 결코 잊을 수 없으리라고요."], ["And as I didn’t take them from a woman’s feet, or a young lady’s: only a little innocent child’s,”", "그리고 제가 그것들을 어떤 여인의 발이나 어떤 아가씨의 발에서 가져온 게 아니라, 그저 천진한 어린아이의 발에서 가져온 거니까요,\""], ["said Walter: “tell her, if you don’t mind, Uncle, that I kept those shoes—she’ll remember how often they fell off, that night—and took them away with me as a remembrance!”", "월터가 말했다. \"삼촌, 괜찮으시다면, 제가 그 신발을 간직했다고 전해 주세요—그날 밤 그게 얼마나 자주 벗겨졌는지 아가씨도 기억하실 거예요—그래서 기념으로 가져갔다고요!\""]], [["They were at that very moment going out at the door in one of Walter’s trunks.", "바로 그 순간 그 신발은 월터의 짐가방 하나에 담겨 문밖으로 나가고 있었다."], ["A porter carrying off his baggage on a truck for shipment at the docks on board the Son and Heir, had got possession of them; and wheeled them away under the very eye of the insensible Midshipman before their owner had well finished speaking.", "손수레에 그의 짐을 싣고 부두로 가서 '아들이자 후계자호'에 실으려던 짐꾼이 그 가방을 차지하여, 무심한 항해사의 바로 코앞에서 굴려 가 버렸으니, 그 임자가 미처 말을 다 끝내기도 전이었다."]], [["But that ancient mariner might have been excused his insensibility to the treasure as it rolled away.", "그러나 그 보물이 굴러가는 동안 그 옛 뱃사람이 보인 무심함은 너그러이 봐줄 만했다."], ["For, under his eye at the same moment, accurately within his range of observation, coming full into the sphere of his startled and intensely wide-awake look-out, were Florence and Susan Nipper: Florence looking up into his face half timidly, and receiving the whole shock of his wooden ogling!", "왜냐하면 바로 그 순간 그의 눈 아래, 정확히 그의 관측 범위 안에, 깜짝 놀라 한껏 눈을 부릅뜬 그의 망보는 시야 한복판으로, 플로렌스와 수전 니퍼가 들어왔기 때문이다. 플로렌스는 반쯤 수줍어하며 그의 얼굴을 올려다보고 있었고, 그 나무 인형의 곁눈질 세례를 고스란히 받고 있었다!"]], [["More than this, they passed into the shop, and passed in at the parlour door before they were observed by anybody but the Midshipman.", "그뿐만 아니라, 두 사람은 항해사 말고는 아무에게도 들키지 않은 채 가게로 들어와 응접실 문까지 들어섰다."], ["And Walter, having his back to the door, would have known nothing of their apparition even then, but for seeing his Uncle spring out of his own chair, and nearly tumble over another.", "그리고 문을 등지고 있던 월터는, 삼촌이 자기 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 다른 의자에 걸려 넘어질 뻔하는 것을 보지 못했더라면, 그때조차 두 사람이 나타난 것을 전혀 몰랐을 터였다."]], [["“Why, Uncle!”", "\"아니, 삼촌!\""], ["exclaimed Walter.", "월터가 외쳤다."], ["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이세요?\""]], [["Old Solomon replied, “Miss Dombey!”", "솔로몬 노인이 대답했다. \"돔비 아가씨!\""]], [["“Is it possible?”", "\"그럴 리가?\""], ["cried Walter, looking round and starting up in his turn.", "월터가 외치며 돌아보고 자기도 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["“Here!”", "\"여기에!\""]], [["Why, It was so possible and so actual, that, while the words were on his lips, Florence hurried past him; took Uncle Sol’s snuff-coloured lapels, one in each hand; kissed him on the cheek; and turning, gave her hand to Walter with a simple truth and earnestness that was her own, and no one else’s in the world!", "아니, 그것은 너무도 그럴 법하고 너무도 실제적인 일이어서, 그 말이 그의 입술에 채 머물러 있는 동안 플로렌스는 그의 곁을 지나쳐 가더니, 솔 삼촌의 코담배색 옷깃을 양손에 하나씩 잡고, 그의 뺨에 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 돌아서서, 이 세상에 오직 그녀만의 것이요 다른 누구의 것도 아닌 그 소박한 진실함과 진지함으로, 월터에게 손을 내밀었다!"]], [["“Going away, Walter?”", "\"떠나시나요, 월터?\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Miss Dombey,”", "\"네, 돔비 아가씨,\""], ["he replied, but not so hopefully as he endeavoured: “I have a voyage before me.”", "그가 대답했으나, 애써 보이려던 만큼 희망에 차 있지는 못했다. \"제 앞에 항해가 놓여 있어요.\""]], [["“And your Uncle,”", "\"그리고 삼촌께서는,\""], ["said Florence, looking back at Solomon.", "플로렌스가 솔로몬을 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“He is sorry you are going, I am sure.", "\"당신이 떠나시는 게 분명 섭섭하시겠지요."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["I see he is!", "정말 그러신 게 보여요!"], ["Dear Walter, I am very sorry too.”", "사랑하는 월터, 저도 무척 섭섭해요.\""]], [["“Goodness knows,”", "\"맙소사,\""], ["exclaimed Miss Nipper, “there’s a many we could spare instead, if numbers is a object, Mrs Pipchin as a overseer would come cheap at her weight in gold, and if a knowledge of black slavery should be required, them Blimbers is the very people for the sitiwation.”", "니퍼 양이 외쳤다. \"숫자가 문제라면 우리가 대신 보내 버려도 될 사람들이 수두룩한걸요, 핍친 부인은 감독으로 쳐서 제 몸무게만큼 금을 줘도 싼 편이고요, 흑인 노예 부리는 지식이 필요하다면 그 블림버네 사람들이야말로 그 자리에 딱 맞는 사람들이지요.\""]], [["With that Miss Nipper untied her bonnet strings, and after looking vacantly for some moments into a little black teapot that was set forth with the usual homely service on the table, shook her head and a tin canister, and began unasked to make the tea.", "그렇게 말하고 니퍼 양은 보닛 끈을 풀었다. 그러고는 탁자 위에 여느 때처럼 소박하게 차려진 차림 가운데 놓인 자그마한 검은 찻주전자를 잠시 멍하니 들여다보더니, 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 양철통도 흔들면서, 청하지도 않았는데 차를 우리기 시작했다."]], [["In the meantime Florence had turned again to the Instrument-maker, who was as full of admiration as surprise.", "그동안 플로렌스는 다시 기계 제작자에게로 돌아섰는데, 그는 놀라움만큼이나 감탄으로 가득 차 있었다. \"이렇게 자라셨다니!\""], ["“So grown!”", "솔 노인이 말했다."], ["said old Sol. “So improved!", "\"이렇게 좋아지셨다니!"], ["And yet not altered!", "그런데도 변치 않으셨어!"], ["Just the same!”", "꼭 그대로야!\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"정말요?\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Ye—yes,”", "\"그—그래요,\""], ["returned old Sol, rubbing his hands slowly, and considering the matter half aloud, as something pensive in the bright eyes looking at him arrested his attention.", "솔 노인이 천천히 두 손을 비비며, 자기를 바라보는 그 빛나는 눈 속의 어딘가 수심 어린 기색에 주의가 끌려, 반쯤 소리 내어 그 문제를 곱씹으며 대답했다."], ["“Yes, that expression was in the younger face, too!”", "\"그래, 그 표정은 그 어린 얼굴에도 있었지!\""]], [["“You remember me,”", "\"절 기억하시는군요,\""], ["said Florence with a smile, “and what a little creature I was then?”", "플로렌스가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그리고 그때 제가 얼마나 조그만 아이였는지도요?\""]], [["“My dear young lady,”", "\"아가씨,\""], ["returned the Instrument-maker, “how could I forget you, often as I have thought of you and heard of you since!", "기계 제작자가 대답했다. \"어떻게 제가 아가씨를 잊겠습니까, 그 뒤로 그토록 자주 아가씨를 생각하고 또 소식을 들어 왔는데요!"], ["At the very moment, indeed, when you came in, Wally was talking about you to me, and leaving messages for you, and—”", "정말이지 아가씨가 들어오시던 바로 그 순간에도, 월리는 제게 아가씨 이야기를 하면서, 아가씨께 전할 말을 남기고 있던 참이었어요, 그리고—\""]], [["“Was he?”", "\"그랬어요?\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“Thank you, Walter!", "\"고마워요, 월터!"], ["Oh thank you, Walter!", "오, 고마워요, 월터!"], ["I was afraid you might be going away and hardly thinking of me;”", "저는 당신이 떠나면서 절 거의 생각하지 않으실까 봐 걱정했거든요.\""], ["and again she gave him her little hand so freely and so faithfully that Walter held it for some moments in his own, and could not bear to let it go.", "그러고는 다시 그 작은 손을 어찌나 거리낌 없이, 어찌나 신실하게 내밀었던지, 월터는 그 손을 잠시 제 손 안에 쥔 채 차마 놓을 수가 없었다."]], [["Yet Walter did not hold it as he might have held it once, nor did its touch awaken those old day-dreams of his boyhood that had floated past him sometimes even lately, and confused him with their indistinct and broken shapes.", "그러나 월터는 한때 잡았을 법한 그런 식으로 그 손을 잡은 것이 아니었고, 그 손길도 소년 시절의 그 옛 백일몽들을 일깨우지는 않았다. 그 꿈들은 근래에도 이따금 그의 곁을 떠다니며, 흐릿하고 부서진 형상으로 그를 어지럽히곤 했던 것이다."], ["The purity and innocence of her endearing manner, and its perfect trustfulness, and the undisguised regard for him that lay so deeply seated in her constant eyes, and glowed upon her fair face through the smile that shaded—for alas!", "그녀의 사랑스러운 태도가 지닌 순수함과 천진함, 그 완벽한 신뢰, 그리고 그녀의 한결같은 눈동자 속에 그토록 깊이 자리 잡고서 미소를 통해 그 고운 얼굴 위로 빛나던—아아!"], ["it was a smile too sad to brighten—it, were not of their romantic race.", "그 미소는 얼굴을 환하게 밝히기에는 너무 슬픈 미소였으니—그를 향한 꾸밈없는 정, 이런 것들은 그 낭만적인 부류에 속하지 않았다."], ["They brought back to his thoughts the early death-bed he had seen her tending, and the love the child had borne her; and on the wings of such remembrances she seemed to rise up, far above his idle fancies, into clearer and serener air.", "그것들은 그녀가 보살피던 그 어린 임종의 자리, 그리고 그 아이가 그녀에게 품었던 사랑을 그의 생각 속으로 되불러 왔다. 그리고 그런 회상의 날개 위에서, 그녀는 그의 부질없는 공상들을 까마득히 넘어, 더 맑고 더 고요한 대기 속으로 떠오르는 듯했다."]], [["“I—I am afraid I must call you Walter’s Uncle, Sir,”", "\"제가—제가 어르신을 월터의 삼촌이라고 불러야 할까 봐요,\""], ["said Florence to the old man, “if you’ll let me.”", "플로렌스가 노인에게 말했다. \"허락해 주신다면요.\""]], [["“My dear young lady,”", "\"아가씨!\""], ["cried old Sol. “Let you!", "솔 노인이 외쳤다."], ["Good gracious!”", "\"허락이라뇨! 맙소사!\""]], [["“We always knew you by that name, and talked of you,”", "\"우린 늘 어르신을 그 이름으로 알았고, 그렇게 이야기했거든요,\""], ["said Florence, glancing round, and sighing gently.", "플로렌스가 둘레를 둘러보며, 가만히 한숨지으며 말했다."], ["“The nice old parlour!", "\"정겨운 옛 응접실이네요!"], ["Just the same!", "꼭 그대로예요!"], ["How well I recollect it!”", "제가 얼마나 잘 기억하고 있는지 몰라요!\""]], [["Old Sol looked first at her, then at his nephew, and then rubbed his hands, and rubbed his spectacles, and said below his breath, “Ah!", "솔 노인은 먼저 그녀를, 다음엔 조카를 바라보았다. 그러고는 두 손을 비비고, 안경을 닦으며, 숨죽인 소리로 말했다."], ["time, time, time!”", "\"아! 세월, 세월, 세월이로다!\""]], [["There was a short silence; during which Susan Nipper skilfully impounded two extra cups and saucers from the cupboard, and awaited the drawing of the tea with a thoughtful air.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 그동안 수전 니퍼는 찬장에서 여분의 찻잔과 받침 둘을 솜씨 좋게 꺼내 챙겨 두고는, 사색에 잠긴 표정으로 차가 우러나기를 기다렸다."]], [["“I want to tell Walter’s Uncle,”", "\"월터의 삼촌께 말씀드리고 싶은 게 있어요,\""], ["said Florence, laying her hand timidly upon the old man’s as it rested on the table, to bespeak his attention, “something that I am anxious about.", "플로렌스가 탁자 위에 놓인 노인의 손에 자기 손을 수줍게 얹어 그의 주의를 끌면서 말했다. \"제가 마음 쓰이는 일이에요."], ["He is going to be left alone, and if he will allow me—not to take Walter’s place, for that I couldn’t do, but to be his true friend and help him if I ever can while Walter is away, I shall be very much obliged to him indeed.", "어르신은 홀로 남으시게 될 텐데, 만약 허락해 주신다면—월터의 자리를 대신하려는 건 아니에요, 그건 제가 할 수 없는 일이니까요—그저 어르신의 참된 벗이 되어, 월터가 떠나 있는 동안 제가 할 수 있는 일이 있다면 어르신을 돕고 싶어요. 그러면 정말로 무척 감사하겠어요."], ["Will you?", "그래 주시겠어요?"], ["May I, Walter’s Uncle?”", "그래도 될까요, 월터의 삼촌?\""]], [["The Instrument-maker, without speaking, put her hand to his lips, and Susan Nipper, leaning back with her arms crossed, in the chair of presidency into which she had voted herself, bit one end of her bonnet strings, and heaved a gentle sigh as she looked up at the skylight.", "기계 제작자는 아무 말 없이 그녀의 손을 자기 입술로 가져갔고, 수전 니퍼는 스스로 의장 자리로 정해 앉은 의자에 팔짱을 끼고 등을 기댄 채, 보닛 끈 한쪽 끝을 깨물며, 천창을 올려다보면서 가만히 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“You will let me come to see you,”", "\"제가 찾아뵙는 걸 허락해 주세요,\" 플로렌스가 말했다."], ["said Florence, “when I can; and you will tell me everything about yourself and Walter; and you will have no secrets from Susan when she comes and I do not, but will confide in us, and trust us, and rely upon us.", "\"올 수 있을 때요. 그리고 어르신 자신과 월터에 관한 모든 걸 제게 말씀해 주세요."], ["And you’ll try to let us be a comfort to you?", "그리고 수전이 오고 제가 못 올 때에도 그 애에게 숨기는 일 없이, 우리에게 털어놓으시고, 우리를 믿으시고, 우리에게 기대 주세요. 그리고 우리가 어르신께 위안이 되게 해 주시겠어요?"], ["Will you, Walter’s Uncle?”", "그래 주시겠어요, 월터의 삼촌?\""]], [["The sweet face looking into his, the gentle pleading eyes, the soft voice, and the light touch on his arm made the more winning by a child’s respect and honour for his age, that gave to all an air of graceful doubt and modest hesitation—these, and her natural earnestness, so overcame the poor old Instrument-maker, that he only answered:", "자기 얼굴을 들여다보는 그 사랑스러운 얼굴, 그 부드럽게 간청하는 눈빛, 그 다정한 목소리, 그리고 그의 나이에 대한 한 아이의 존경과 경의가 더해져 더욱 마음을 사로잡는 그 가벼운 팔 위의 손길—그 존경과 경의가 모든 것에 우아한 망설임과 겸손한 머뭇거림의 분위기를 입혔다—이런 것들이, 그리고 그녀의 타고난 진지함이, 가엾은 늙은 기계 제작자를 어찌나 압도했던지, 그는 그저 이렇게 대답할 뿐이었다."]], [["“Wally!", "\"월리!"], ["say a word for me, my dear.", "나 대신 한마디 해 다오, 얘야."], ["I’m very grateful.”", "정말 고맙구나.\""]], [["“No, Walter,”", "\"아니에요, 월터,\""], ["returned Florence with her quiet smile.", "플로렌스가 그 잔잔한 미소를 띠며 대꾸했다."], ["“Say nothing for him, if you please.", "\"이분을 위해 아무 말도 하지 말아 주세요."], ["I understand him very well, and we must learn to talk together without you, dear Walter.”", "저는 이분을 아주 잘 이해해요. 그리고 우린 당신 없이 함께 이야기하는 법을 익혀야 해요, 사랑하는 월터.\""]], [["The regretful tone in which she said these latter words, touched Walter more than all the rest.", "이 마지막 말을 하던 그 아쉬워하는 어조가, 다른 무엇보다도 월터의 마음을 더 깊이 울렸다."]], [["“Miss Florence,”", "\"플로렌스 아가씨,\""], ["he replied, with an effort to recover the cheerful manner he had preserved while talking with his Uncle, “I know no more than my Uncle, what to say in acknowledgment of such kindness, I am sure.", "그는 삼촌과 이야기하는 동안 지켜 왔던 그 명랑한 태도를 되찾으려 애쓰며 대답했다. \"이런 친절에 대해 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지, 저도 삼촌만큼이나 알지 못하겠어요, 정말로요."], ["But what could I say, after all, if I had the power of talking for an hour, except that it is like you?”", "하지만 한 시간을 떠들 수 있다 한들, 결국 무슨 말을 더 할 수 있겠어요, 그게 꼭 아가씨답다는 말 말고는요?\""]], [["Susan Nipper began upon a new part of her bonnet string, and nodded at the skylight, in approval of the sentiment expressed.", "수전 니퍼는 보닛 끈의 새로운 부분을 깨물기 시작하더니, 거기 표현된 그 감정에 동의한다는 듯 천창을 향해 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["but, Walter,”", "하지만, 월터,\" 플로렌스가 말했다."], ["said Florence, “there is something that I wish to say to you before you go away, and you must call me Florence, if you please, and not speak like a stranger.”", "\"당신이 떠나시기 전에 제가 하고 싶은 말이 있어요. 그리고 부디 절 플로렌스라고 불러 주세요, 낯선 사람처럼 말씀하지 마시고요.\""]], [["“Like a stranger!”", "\"낯선 사람처럼이라뇨!\""], ["returned Walter, “No.", "월터가 대꾸했다. \"안 돼요."], ["I couldn’t speak so.", "그렇게는 말할 수 없어요."], ["I am sure, at least, I couldn’t feel like one.”", "적어도, 그렇게 느낄 수는 없을 거예요, 분명히요.\""]], [["“Ay, but that is not enough, and is not what I mean.", "\"그래요, 하지만 그걸로는 충분치 않고, 제가 말하려던 것도 그게 아니에요. 그러니까, 월터,\""], ["For, Walter,”", "플로렌스가 울음을 터뜨리며 덧붙였다. \"그 애는 당신을 무척 좋아했고, 죽기 전에 당신을 마음에 두었다고 했어요."], ["added Florence, bursting into tears, “he liked you very much, and said before he died that he was fond of you, and said ‘Remember Walter!’ and if you’ll be a brother to me, Walter, now that he is gone and I have none on earth, I’ll be your sister all my life, and think of you like one wherever we may be!", "그리고 '월터를 기억해!'라고 했어요. 그러니 만약 당신이 제게 오라버니가 되어 주신다면, 월터, 이제 그 애가 떠나고 이 세상에 제겐 형제가 하나도 없으니, 저는 평생 당신의 누이가 되어, 우리가 어디에 있든 당신을 형제처럼 생각할 거예요!"], ["This is what I wished to say, dear Walter, but I cannot say it as I would, because my heart is full.”", "이게 제가 하고 싶었던 말이에요, 사랑하는 월터. 하지만 제 바람처럼 말할 수가 없네요, 가슴이 벅차서요.\""]], [["And in its fulness and its sweet simplicity, she held out both her hands to him.", "그리고 그 벅차오름과 그 달콤한 소박함 속에서, 그녀는 두 손을 그에게 내밀었다."], ["Walter taking them, stooped down and touched the tearful face that neither shrunk nor turned away, nor reddened as he did so, but looked up at him with confidence and truth.", "월터는 그 손을 잡고, 몸을 굽혀, 눈물 어린 그 얼굴에 입을 맞추었다. 그가 그러는 동안에도 그 얼굴은 움츠러들지도, 돌아서지도, 붉어지지도 않고, 신뢰와 진실을 담아 그를 올려다보았다."], ["In that one moment, every shadow of doubt or agitation passed away from Walter’s soul.", "그 한순간에, 의심이나 동요의 그림자란 그림자는 모조리 월터의 영혼에서 사라졌다."], ["It seemed to him that he responded to her innocent appeal, beside the dead child’s bed: and, in the solemn presence he had seen there, pledged himself to cherish and protect her very image, in his banishment, with brotherly regard; to garner up her simple faith, inviolate; and hold himself degraded if he breathed upon it any thought that was not in her own breast when she gave it to him.", "그에게는 마치 자기가 그 죽은 아이의 침상 곁에서 그녀의 천진한 호소에 응답하는 것만 같았다. 그리고 거기서 보았던 그 엄숙한 임재 앞에서, 자기는 유배지에서도 형제의 정으로 그녀의 그 모습을 소중히 여기고 지켜 주리라, 그녀의 소박한 믿음을 더럽힘 없이 고이 간직하리라, 그리고 그녀가 그것을 자기에게 맡길 때 그녀 자신의 가슴속에 없던 어떤 생각이라도 거기 불어넣는다면 스스로를 천하게 여기리라, 이렇게 다짐했다."]], [["Susan Nipper, who had bitten both her bonnet strings at once, and imparted a great deal of private emotion to the skylight, during this transaction, now changed the subject by inquiring who took milk and who took sugar; and being enlightened on these points, poured out the tea.", "이 일이 오가는 동안 보닛 끈 두 가닥을 한꺼번에 깨물고 천창을 향해 남몰래 잔뜩 감정을 쏟아 내던 수전 니퍼는, 이제 누가 우유를 넣고 누가 설탕을 넣는지 물으며 화제를 돌렸다. 그리고 그 점들에 대해 답을 듣고는, 차를 따랐다."], ["They all four gathered socially about the little table, and took tea under that young lady’s active superintendence; and the presence of Florence in the back parlour, brightened the Tartar frigate on the wall.", "네 사람은 작은 탁자 둘레에 정답게 모여, 그 아가씨의 활발한 주관 아래 차를 마셨다. 그리고 뒷방에 깃든 플로렌스의 존재가, 벽에 걸린 타타르 전함 그림마저 환하게 밝혀 주었다."]], [["Half an hour ago Walter, for his life, would have hardly called her by her name.", "반 시간 전이라면 월터는 죽었다 깨어나도 그녀를 이름으로 부르지 못했으리라."], ["But he could do so now when she entreated him.", "그러나 이제는 그녀가 청하니 그럴 수 있었다."], ["He could think of her being there, without a lurking misgiving that it would have been better if she had not come.", "그녀가 차라리 오지 않는 편이 나았으리라는 은근한 불안 없이, 그녀가 거기 있다는 사실을 생각할 수 있었다."], ["He could calmly think how beautiful she was, how full of promise, what a home some happy man would find in such a heart one day.", "그녀가 얼마나 아름다운지, 얼마나 가능성으로 가득한지, 언젠가 어떤 행복한 사내가 그런 마음속에서 어떤 안식처를 찾게 될지를 차분히 생각할 수 있었다."], ["He could reflect upon his own place in that heart, with pride; and with a brave determination, if not to deserve it—he still thought that far above him—never to deserve it less.", "그 마음속 자기의 자리를 자랑스럽게 헤아릴 수 있었다. 그리고 비록 그럴 자격이 있다고는—그건 여전히 자기로서는 까마득히 높은 일이라 여겼으니—여기지 못할지언정, 적어도 그 자격을 더 잃지는 않으리라는 용감한 결심으로써."]], [["Some fairy influence must surely have hovered round the hands of Susan Nipper when she made the tea, engendering the tranquil air that reigned in the back parlour during its discussion.", "수전 니퍼가 차를 우릴 때 그녀의 손 둘레에 분명 어떤 요정의 기운이 맴돌았으리라, 그것을 마시며 이야기 나누는 동안 뒷방을 다스리던 그 평온한 공기를 자아낸 그 기운이."], ["Some counter-influence must surely have hovered round the hands of Uncle Sol’s chronometer, and moved them faster than the Tartar frigate ever went before the wind.", "솔 삼촌의 정밀 시계 바늘 둘레에도 분명 어떤 반대되는 기운이 맴돌며, 그 바늘들을 타타르 전함이 바람을 받아 나아간 그 어느 때보다도 빠르게 움직였으리라."], ["Be this as it may, the visitors had a coach in waiting at a quiet corner not far off; and the chronometer, on being incidentally referred to, gave such a positive opinion that it had been waiting a long time, that it was impossible to doubt the fact, especially when stated on such unimpeachable authority.", "어찌 되었든, 방문객들은 멀지 않은 한적한 모퉁이에 마차 한 대를 대기시켜 두고 있었다. 그리고 그 정밀 시계는, 우연히 한번 물어보자, 마차가 오래 기다리고 있노라고 어찌나 단호한 의견을 내놓던지, 그 사실을 의심하기란 불가능했으니, 그토록 흠잡을 데 없는 권위에서 나온 말이고 보면 더욱 그러했다."], ["If Uncle Sol had been going to be hanged by his own time, he never would have allowed that the chronometer was too fast, by the least fraction of a second.", "만약 솔 삼촌이 자기 시계 시각에 맞추어 교수형에 처해질 처지였다 한들, 그는 그 정밀 시계가 단 한순간이라도 빠르다고는 결코 인정하지 않았을 것이다."]], [["Florence at parting recapitulated to the old man all that she had said before, and bound him to their compact.", "플로렌스는 작별하면서 노인에게 자기가 앞서 한 말을 모두 되풀이하며, 그에게 그들의 약조를 다짐받았다."], ["Uncle Sol attended her lovingly to the legs of the wooden Midshipman, and there resigned her to Walter, who was ready to escort her and Susan Nipper to the coach.", "솔 삼촌은 다정하게 그녀를 나무 항해사의 다리께까지 배웅하고, 거기서 그녀를 월터에게 넘겨주었다. 월터는 그녀와 수전 니퍼를 마차까지 바래다줄 채비가 되어 있었다."]], [["“Walter,”", "\"월터,\""], ["said Florence by the way, “I have been afraid to ask before your Uncle.", "가는 길에 플로렌스가 말했다. \"삼촌 앞에서는 차마 묻기가 두려웠어요."], ["Do you think you will be absent very long?”", "아주 오래 떠나 계실 것 같으세요?\""]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"실은,\""], ["said Walter, “I don’t know.", "월터가 말했다."], ["I fear so.", "\"모르겠어요."], ["Mr Dombey signified as much, I thought, when he appointed me.”", "그럴까 봐 걱정이에요. 돔비 씨가 절 임명하실 때 그런 뜻을 내비치신 것 같았거든요.\""]], [["“Is it a favour, Walter?”", "\"그게 은혜로운 일인가요, 월터?\""], ["inquired Florence, after a moment’s hesitation, and looking anxiously in his face.", "플로렌스가 잠시 머뭇거리다가, 그의 얼굴을 근심스레 들여다보며 물었다."]], [["“The appointment?”", "\"그 임명이요?\""], ["returned Walter.", "월터가 되물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["Walter would have given anything to have answered in the affirmative, but his face answered before his lips could, and Florence was too attentive to it not to understand its reply.", "월터는 그렇다고 대답할 수만 있다면 무엇이든 내주었으련만, 그의 입술이 미처 답하기도 전에 그의 얼굴이 먼저 답해 버렸고, 플로렌스는 너무도 주의 깊게 그것을 보고 있었던지라 그 답을 이해하지 못할 리 없었다."]], [["“I am afraid you have scarcely been a favourite with Papa,”", "\"아빠한테 별로 사랑받지 못하셨나 봐요,\""], ["she said, timidly.", "그녀가 수줍게 말했다."]], [["“There is no reason,”", "\"제가 사랑받을,\""], ["replied Walter, smiling, “why I should be.”", "월터가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"까닭이 없는걸요.\""]], [["“No reason, Walter!”", "\"까닭이 없다뇨, 월터!\""]], [["“There was no reason,”", "\"까닭이 없었지요,\""], ["said Walter, understanding what she meant.", "월터가 그녀의 말뜻을 알아채고 말했다."], ["“There are many people employed in the House.", "\"회사에는 많은 사람이 고용되어 있어요."], ["Between Mr Dombey and a young man like me, there’s a wide space of separation.", "돔비 씨와 저 같은 젊은이 사이에는 까마득한 거리가 있고요."], ["If I do my duty, I do what I ought, and do no more than all the rest.”", "제가 제 의무를 다한다면, 마땅히 할 일을 하는 것일 뿐, 나머지 모든 사람보다 더 하는 건 아니에요.\""]], [["Had Florence any misgiving of which she was hardly conscious: any misgiving that had sprung into an indistinct and undefined existence since that recent night when she had gone down to her father’s room: that Walter’s accidental interest in her, and early knowledge of her, might have involved him in that powerful displeasure and dislike?", "플로렌스에게는 자기도 거의 의식하지 못하는 어떤 불안이 있었던 걸까. 근래 그녀가 아버지의 방으로 내려갔던 그날 밤 이후로, 흐릿하고 정의할 수 없는 형태로 싹터 버린 어떤 불안이—월터가 우연히 자기에게 보인 관심과, 자기에 대한 이른 시절의 인연이, 혹 그를 그 강력한 노여움과 미움 속으로 끌어들인 게 아닐까 하는 불안이 말이다."], ["Had Walter any such idea, or any sudden thought that it was in her mind at that moment?", "월터에게도 그런 생각이, 혹은 그것이 바로 그 순간 그녀의 마음속에 있다는 어떤 불현듯한 짐작이 있었던 걸까?"], ["Neither of them hinted at it.", "둘 중 누구도 그것을 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["Neither of them spoke at all, for some short time.", "둘 다 잠시 동안 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Susan, walking on the other side of Walter, eyed them both sharply; and certainly Miss Nipper’s thoughts travelled in that direction, and very confidently too.", "월터의 반대편에서 걷던 수전은 두 사람을 날카롭게 살폈다. 그리고 분명 니퍼 양의 생각은 그쪽으로, 그것도 자못 확신을 가지고 흘러가고 있었다."]], [["“You may come back very soon,”", "\"어쩌면 아주 곧 돌아오실지도 몰라요,\""], ["said Florence, “perhaps, Walter.”", "플로렌스가 말했다. \"그럴지도요, 월터.\""]], [["“I may come back,”", "\"제가 돌아올 때면,\""], ["said Walter, “an old man, and find you an old lady.", "월터가 말했다. \"어쩌면 노인이 되어 있고, 아가씨도 할머니가 되어 계실지 모르죠."], ["But I hope for better things.”", "하지만 저는 더 나은 일들을 바라요.\""]], [["“Papa,”", "\"아빠도,\" 플로렌스가 잠시 후에 말했다."], ["said Florence, after a moment, “will—will recover from his grief, and—speak more freely to me one day, perhaps; and if he should, I will tell him how much I wish to see you back again, and ask him to recall you for my sake.”", "\"언젠가는—슬픔에서 회복하시고—제게 좀 더 마음을 터놓고 말씀해 주실지도 몰라요. 그렇게 되면, 제가 당신이 다시 돌아오기를 얼마나 바라는지 말씀드리고, 저를 봐서라도 당신을 다시 불러 달라고 청할게요.\""]], [["There was a touching modulation in these words about her father, that Walter understood too well.", "아버지에 관한 이 말들 속에는 가슴 저미는 떨림이 깃들어 있었으니, 월터는 그것을 너무도 잘 알아들었다."]], [["The coach being close at hand, he would have left her without speaking, for now he felt what parting was; but Florence held his hand when she was seated, and then he found there was a little packet in her own.", "마차가 바로 곁에 있어서, 그는 아무 말 없이 그녀와 헤어지려 했다. 이제야 이별이 무엇인지 절감했기 때문이다. 그러나 플로렌스가 자리에 앉으며 그의 손을 잡았고, 그제야 그는 그녀의 손안에 작은 꾸러미가 하나 있음을 알았다."]], [["“Walter,”", "\"월터,\""], ["she said, looking full upon him with her affectionate eyes, “like you, I hope for better things.", "그녀가 그 애정 어린 눈으로 그를 그득히 바라보며 말했다. \"당신처럼, 저도 더 나은 일들을 바라요."], ["I will pray for them, and believe that they will arrive.", "그것들을 위해 기도할 거고, 그것들이 찾아오리라 믿을 거예요."], ["I made this little gift for Paul.", "이 작은 선물은 폴을 위해 만든 거예요."], ["Pray take it with my love, and do not look at it until you are gone away.", "부디 제 사랑과 함께 받아 주세요, 그리고 떠나기 전까지는 보지 말아 주세요."], ["And now, God bless you, Walter!", "그럼, 신의 가호가 함께하기를, 월터!"], ["never forget me.", "절 절대 잊지 마세요."], ["You are my brother, dear!”", "당신은 제 오라버니예요, 그래요!\""]], [["He was glad that Susan Nipper came between them, or he might have left her with a sorrowful remembrance of him.", "그는 수전 니퍼가 둘 사이에 끼어들어 주어 다행이라 여겼다. 안 그랬더라면 그녀에게 자기에 대한 슬픈 기억을 남겼을지도 모를 일이었다."], ["He was glad too that she did not look out of the coach again, but waved the little hand to him instead, as long as he could see it.", "그녀가 마차 밖으로 다시 내다보지 않고, 대신 보이지 않을 때까지 그 작은 손을 흔들어 준 것 또한 다행이라 여겼다."]], [["In spite of her request, he could not help opening the packet that night when he went to bed.", "그녀의 부탁에도 불구하고, 그는 그날 밤 잠자리에 들 때 그 꾸러미를 열어 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["It was a little purse: and there was money in it.", "그것은 작은 지갑이었고, 그 안에는 돈이 들어 있었다."]], [["Bright rose the sun next morning, from his absence in strange countries and up rose Walter with it to receive the Captain, who was already at the door: having turned out earlier than was necessary, in order to get under weigh while Mrs MacStinger was still slumbering.", "이튿날 아침 해는 낯선 나라들에 가 있던 자리에서 환히 떠올랐고, 월터도 그와 함께 일어나 선장을 맞았다. 선장은 이미 문 앞에 와 있었으니, 맥스팅거 부인이 아직 곤히 잠들어 있는 동안 출항하려고 필요 이상으로 일찍 나선 것이었다."], ["The Captain pretended to be in tip-top spirits, and brought a very smoky tongue in one of the pockets of the broad blue coat for breakfast.", "선장은 더없이 들뜬 기분인 척하며, 아침거리로 넓은 푸른 외투 주머니 하나에 잔뜩 훈제한 우설을 넣어 가져왔다."]], [["“And, Wal”r,”", "\"그런데, 월—러,\""], ["said the Captain, when they took their seats at table, if your Uncle’s the man I think him, he’ll bring out the last bottle of the Madeira on the present occasion.”", "선장이 식탁에 앉으며 말했다. \"자네 삼촌이 내가 생각하는 그런 사람이라면, 지금 이런 때 마지막 마데이라 한 병을 꺼내 오실 거야.\""]], [["“No, no, Ned,”", "\"아니, 안 되네, 네드,\""], ["returned the old man.", "노인이 대꾸했다. \"안 돼!"], ["“No! That shall be opened when Walter comes home again.”", "그건 월터가 다시 집에 돌아왔을 때 열 거야.\""]], [["“Well said!”", "\"옳거니!\""], ["cried the Captain.", "선장이 외쳤다."], ["“Hear him!”", "\"들어 보게나!\""]], [["“There it lies,”", "\"그건 저기 있다네,\""], ["said Sol Gills, “down in the little cellar, covered with dirt and cobwebs.", "솔 길스가 말했다. \"저 작은 지하실에, 먼지와 거미줄을 뒤집어쓴 채로."], ["There may be dirt and cobwebs over you and me perhaps, Ned, before it sees the light.”", "어쩌면 그게 빛을 보기 전에, 자네와 나 위에 먼지와 거미줄이 덮일지도 모르지, 네드.\""]], [["“Hear him!”", "\"들어 보게나!\""], ["cried the Captain.", "선장이 외쳤다."], ["“Good morality!", "\"훌륭한 교훈일세!"], ["Wal”r, my lad.", "월—러, 이 사람아."], ["Train up a fig-tree in the way it should go, and when you are old sit under the shade on it.", "무화과나무를 마땅히 갈 길로 가게 기르라, 그러면 늙어서 그 그늘 아래 앉으리라."], ["Overhaul the—Well,”", "펼쳐 보게나, 그—그런데,\""], ["said the Captain on second thoughts, “I ain’t quite certain where that’s to be found, but when found, make a note of.", "선장이 다시 생각하고는 말했다. \"그게 어디 나오는 말인지는 도통 모르겠지만, 찾거든 적어 두게나."], ["Sol Gills, heave ahead again!”", "솔 길스, 다시 앞으로 나아가게!\""]], [["“But there or somewhere, it shall lie, Ned, until Wally comes back to claim it,”", "\"하지만 거기든 어디든, 월리가 돌아와 그걸 찾을 때까지 놔둘 걸세, 네드,\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."], ["“That’s all I meant to say.”", "\"내가 하려던 말은 그게 다라네.\""]], [["“And well said too,”", "\"그것도 잘한 말일세,\""], ["returned the Captain; “and if we three don’t crack that bottle in company, I’ll give you two leave to.”", "선장이 대꾸했다. \"그리고 만약 우리 셋이 함께 그 병을 따지 못한다면, 자네 둘이서 따도 좋다고 내가 허락하지.\""]], [["Notwithstanding the Captain’s excessive joviality, he made but a poor hand at the smoky tongue, though he tried very hard, when anybody looked at him, to appear as if he were eating with a vast appetite.", "선장의 지나친 쾌활함에도 불구하고, 그는 그 훈제한 우설을 변변히 먹어 내지 못했다. 누가 그를 볼 때면 어마어마한 식욕으로 먹고 있는 양 보이려고 무던히 애를 썼지만 말이다."], ["He was terribly afraid, likewise, of being left alone with either Uncle or nephew; appearing to consider that his only chance of safety as to keeping up appearances, was in there being always three together.", "또한 그는 삼촌이든 조카든 어느 한쪽과 단둘이 남겨지는 것을 끔찍이도 두려워했다. 마치 겉모습을 지키는 데 있어 자기의 유일한 안전책이란 늘 셋이 함께 있는 것뿐이라고 여기는 듯했다."], ["This terror on the part of the Captain, reduced him to such ingenious evasions as running to the door, when Solomon went to put his coat on, under pretence of having seen an extraordinary hackney-coach pass: and darting out into the road when Walter went upstairs to take leave of the lodgers, on a feint of smelling fire in a neighbouring chimney.", "선장 쪽의 이 공포가 그를 그런 기발한 회피책들로 몰아넣었으니, 솔로몬이 외투를 입으러 갈 때면 굉장한 삯마차가 지나가는 걸 봤다는 핑계로 문께로 달려가고, 월터가 하숙인들에게 작별 인사를 하러 위층으로 올라갈 때면 이웃 굴뚝에서 불 냄새가 난다는 시늉으로 길로 뛰쳐나가는 식이었다."], ["These artifices Captain Cuttle deemed inscrutable by any uninspired observer.", "커틀 선장은 이런 술책들을 영감을 받지 못한 어떤 관찰자도 꿰뚫어 볼 수 없으리라 여겼다."]], [["Walter was coming down from his parting expedition upstairs, and was crossing the shop to go back to the little parlour, when he saw a faded face he knew, looking in at the door, and darted towards it.", "월터가 위층으로 작별 인사를 다녀와 내려오는 길에, 작은 응접실로 돌아가려고 가게를 가로지르고 있을 때, 그는 자기가 아는 한 빛바랜 얼굴이 문 안을 들여다보고 있는 것을 보고는 그쪽으로 달려갔다."]], [["“Mr Carker!”", "\"카커 씨!\""], ["cried Walter, pressing the hand of John Carker the Junior.", "월터가 동생 존 카커의 손을 꼭 잡으며 외쳤다."], ["“Pray come in!", "\"부디 들어오세요!"], ["This is kind of you, to be here so early to say good-bye to me.", "이렇게 일찍 절 배웅하러 와 주시다니 친절하시네요."], ["You knew how glad it would make me to shake hands with you, once, before going away.", "떠나기 전에 한 번 당신과 악수할 수 있다면 제가 얼마나 기쁠지 아셨던 거죠."], ["I cannot say how glad I am to have this opportunity.", "이런 기회를 얻게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지 말로 다 못 하겠어요."], ["Pray come in.”", "부디 들어오세요.\""]], [["“It is not likely that we may ever meet again, Walter,”", "\"우리가 다시 만날 일은 아마 없겠지요, 월터,\""], ["returned the other, gently resisting his invitation, “and I am glad of this opportunity too.", "상대가 그의 청을 부드럽게 뿌리치며 대답했다. \"그래서 나도 이 기회가 기쁘답니다."], ["I may venture to speak to you, and to take you by the hand, on the eve of separation.", "헤어지는 마당이니, 자네에게 말을 건네고, 자네 손을 잡아도 되겠지요."], ["I shall not have to resist your frank approaches, Walter, any more.”", "이젠 자네의 그 솔직한 다가옴을 더는 뿌리치지 않아도 되니까요, 월터.\""]], [["There was a melancholy in his smile as he said it, that showed he had found some company and friendship for his thoughts even in that.", "그가 그렇게 말할 때 그의 미소에는 어떤 우수가 어려 있었으니, 그것은 그가 바로 그 안에서조차 자기 생각의 길동무와 우정을 얼마간 찾아냈음을 보여 주었다."]], [["“Ah, Mr Carker!”", "\"아, 카커 씨!\""], ["returned Walter.", "월터가 대꾸했다."], ["“Why did you resist them?", "\"왜 그걸 뿌리치셨어요?"], ["You could have done me nothing but good, I am very sure.”", "제겐 좋은 일만 해 주실 수 있었을 텐데요, 정말 그렇게 믿어요.\""]], [["He shook his head.", "그는 고개를 저었다."], ["“If there were any good,”", "\"만약 이 세상에서 내가 할 수 있는,\""], ["he said, “I could do on this earth, I would do it, Walter, for you.", "그가 말했다. \"어떤 선한 일이 있다면, 월터, 자네를 위해 그렇게 했을 거요."], ["The sight of you from day to day, has been at once happiness and remorse to me.", "날마다 자네를 보는 것이 내겐 행복인 동시에 회한이었지요."], ["But the pleasure has outweighed the pain.", "하지만 그 기쁨이 그 고통보다 무거웠어요."], ["I know that, now, by knowing what I lose.”", "이제 내가 무엇을 잃는지 알게 되니, 그걸 알겠소.\""]], [["“Come in, Mr Carker, and make acquaintance with my good old Uncle,”", "\"들어오세요, 카커 씨, 그리고 제 착하고 늙으신 삼촌과 인사 나누세요,\""], ["urged Walter.", "월터가 권했다."], ["“I have often talked to him about you, and he will be glad to tell you all he hears from me.", "\"제가 삼촌께 당신 이야기를 자주 했거든요. 삼촌도 제게서 듣는 소식을 모두 당신께 기꺼이 전해 주실 거예요."], ["I have not,”", "저는,\" 월터가 그의 망설임을 알아채고는, 자기도 당혹스러워하며 말했다."], ["said Walter, noticing his hesitation, and speaking with embarrassment himself: “I have not told him anything about our last conversation, Mr Carker; not even him, believe me.", "\"삼촌께 우리의 그 마지막 대화에 대해선 아무것도 말하지 않았어요, 카커 씨. 삼촌께조차 말이지요, 믿어 주세요.\""]], [["The grey Junior pressed his hand, and tears rose in his eyes.", "머리 센 동생은 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었고, 두 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다."]], [["“If I ever make acquaintance with him, Walter,”", "\"내가 만약 언젠가 그분과 인사를 나눈다면, 월터,\""], ["he returned, “it will be that I may hear tidings of you.", "그가 대답했다. \"그건 자네 소식을 듣기 위해서일 거요."], ["Rely on my not wronging your forbearance and consideration.", "자네의 그 너그러움과 배려를 저버리지 않으리라 믿어 주오."], ["It would be to wrong it, not to tell him all the truth, before I sought a word of confidence from him.", "그분께 한마디 속내를 청하기 전에 진실을 모두 말씀드리지 않는다면, 그거야말로 그 너그러움을 저버리는 일이 될 테니까요."], ["But I have no friend or acquaintance except you: and even for your sake, am little likely to make any.”", "하지만 내겐 자네 말고는 벗도 아는 이도 없고, 자네를 봐서라도 누굴 새로 사귈 일은 거의 없을 거요.\""]], [["“I wish,”", "\"저는,\""], ["said Walter, “you had suffered me to be your friend indeed.", "월터가 말했다. \"당신이 절 진정한 벗으로 삼게 해 주셨더라면 했어요."], ["I always wished it, Mr Carker, as you know; but never half so much as now, when we are going to part.”", "늘 그러기를 바랐죠, 카커 씨, 아시다시피요. 하지만 우리가 헤어지려는 지금만큼 간절히 바란 적은 없었어요.\""]], [["“It is enough,”", "\"자네가 내 가슴속의 벗이었다는 것,\""], ["replied the other, “that you have been the friend of my own breast, and that when I have avoided you most, my heart inclined the most towards you, and was fullest of you.", "상대가 대답했다. \"그리고 내가 자네를 가장 피했을 때에도 내 마음은 가장 자네에게 기울었고, 자네로 가장 가득 차 있었다는 것, 그것으로 충분하다오."], ["Walter, good-bye!”", "월터, 잘 가게!\""]], [["“Good-bye, Mr Carker.", "\"안녕히 계세요, 카커 씨."], ["Heaven be with you, Sir!”", "하늘이 당신과 함께하기를, 어르신!\""], ["cried Walter with emotion.", "월터가 북받쳐 외쳤다."]], [["“If,”", "\"만약,\""], ["said the other, retaining his hand while he spoke; “if when you come back, you miss me from my old corner, and should hear from anyone where I am lying, come and look upon my grave.", "상대가 말하면서 그의 손을 놓지 않고 붙든 채 말했다. \"만약 자네가 돌아왔을 때, 내 그 옛 구석자리에 내가 보이지 않거든, 그리고 누군가에게서 내가 어디 누워 있는지 듣게 되거든, 와서 내 무덤을 한번 들여다봐 주오."], ["Think that I might have been as honest and as happy as you!", "나도 자네처럼 정직하고 행복할 수 있었으리라 생각해 주오!"], ["And let me think, when I know time is coming on, that some one like my former self may stand there, for a moment, and remember me with pity and forgiveness!", "그리고 내 때가 다가오고 있음을 알게 될 때, 예전의 나 같은 누군가가 거기 잠시 서서, 연민과 용서로 나를 기억해 주리라고, 그렇게 생각하게 해 주오!"], ["Walter, good-bye!”", "월터, 잘 가게!\""]], [["His figure crept like a shadow down the bright, sun-lighted street, so cheerful yet so solemn in the early summer morning; and slowly passed away.", "그의 모습은 이른 여름 아침에 그토록 명랑하면서도 그토록 엄숙한, 환한 햇살이 비치는 거리를 따라 그림자처럼 기어가더니, 천천히 사라져 갔다."]], [["The relentless chronometer at last announced that Walter must turn his back upon the wooden Midshipman: and away they went, himself, his Uncle, and the Captain, in a hackney-coach to a wharf, where they were to take steam-boat for some Reach down the river, the name of which, as the Captain gave it out, was a hopeless mystery to the ears of landsmen.", "마침내 그 매정한 정밀 시계가, 월터는 이제 나무 항해사에게 등을 돌려야 한다고 알려 주었다. 그리하여 그들은 떠났다."], ["Arrived at this Reach (whither the ship had repaired by last night’s tide), they were boarded by various excited watermen, and among others by a dirty Cyclops of the Captain’s acquaintance, who, with his one eye, had made the Captain out some mile and a half off, and had been exchanging unintelligible roars with him ever since.", "그 자신과 삼촌과 선장이, 삯마차를 타고 어느 부두로 가서, 거기서 강을 따라 내려가 어느 후미진 물길로 가는 증기선을 탈 참이었다. 선장이 일러 준 그 물길의 이름은 뭍사람의 귀에는 도무지 알 길 없는 수수께끼였다."], ["Becoming the lawful prize of this personage, who was frightfully hoarse and constitutionally in want of shaving, they were all three put aboard the Son and Heir.", "그 물길에 다다르자(배는 간밤의 밀물을 타고 그리로 옮겨 와 있었다), 흥분한 뱃사공들이 여럿 다가와 그들 곁에 배를 댔는데, 그중에는 선장과 안면이 있는, 더러운 외눈박이 거인 같은 자도 있었다. 그는 외눈 하나로 한 마장 반쯤 떨어진 데서부터 선장을 알아보고는, 그때부터 줄곧 선장과 알아들을 수 없는 고함을 주고받고 있던 터였다."], ["And the Son and Heir was in a pretty state of confusion, with sails lying all bedraggled on the wet decks, loose ropes tripping people up, men in red shirts running barefoot to and fro, casks blockading every foot of space, and, in the thickest of the fray, a black cook in a black caboose up to his eyes in vegetables and blinded with smoke.", "무섭게 목이 쉬고 천성적으로 면도가 필요해 보이는 이 인물의 정당한 노획물이 되어, 세 사람은 모두 '아들이자 후계자호'에 올랐다. 그리고 '아들이자 후계자호'는 자못 어수선한 상태였으니, 젖은 갑판 위에 돛이란 돛은 죄다 너저분하게 늘어져 있고, 풀린 밧줄들이 사람들의 발을 걸어 넘어뜨리고, 붉은 셔츠를 입은 사내들이 맨발로 이리저리 뛰어다니고, 술통들이 한 발짝의 공간도 남김없이 가로막고 있었으며, 그 난장판의 한복판에서는 검은 취사실의 흑인 요리사가 채소에 눈까지 파묻힌 채 연기에 눈이 멀어 있었다."]], [["The Captain immediately drew Walter into a corner, and with a great effort, that made his face very red, pulled up the silver watch, which was so big, and so tight in his pocket, that it came out like a bung.", "선장은 곧장 월터를 한쪽 구석으로 끌고 가더니, 얼굴이 새빨개지도록 안간힘을 써서, 어찌나 크고 주머니에 꼭 끼었던지 마개처럼 쑥 빠져나오는 그 은시계를 끄집어냈다."]], [["“Wal”r,”", "\"월—러,\""], ["said the Captain, handing it over, and shaking him heartily by the hand, “a parting gift, my lad.", "선장이 그것을 건네며 그의 손을 진심으로 힘껏 흔들고 말했다. \"작별 선물일세, 이 사람아."], ["Put it back half an hour every morning, and about another quarter towards the arternoon, and it’s a watch that’ll do you credit.”", "매일 아침마다 반 시간씩 늦춰 놓고, 오후께 가서 또 한 십오 분쯤 늦춰 놓으면, 자네에게 신용을 안겨 줄 시계라네.\""]], [["“Captain Cuttle!", "\"커틀 선장님!"], ["I couldn’t think of it!”", "그건 어림없어요!\""], ["cried Walter, detaining him, for he was running away.", "월터가, 달아나려는 그를 붙들며 외쳤다."], ["“Pray take it back.", "\"부디 도로 가져가세요."], ["I have one already.”", "저는 이미 하나 있는걸요.\""]], [["“Then, Wal”r,”", "\"그럼, 월—러,\""], ["said the Captain, suddenly diving into one of his pockets and bringing up the two teaspoons and the sugar-tongs, with which he had armed himself to meet such an objection, “take this here trifle of plate, instead.”", "선장이 느닷없이 주머니 하나로 손을 쑥 집어넣어, 바로 이런 반대에 맞서려고 무장해 둔 찻숟가락 둘과 각설탕 집게를 꺼내 들며 말했다. \"이 사소한 은붙이라도 대신 받게나.\""]], [["“No, no, I couldn’t indeed!” cried Walter, “a thousand thanks!", "\"아니, 아니에요, 정말 못 받아요!\""], ["Don’t throw them away, Captain Cuttle!”", "월터가 외쳤다. \"천 번 고마워요!"], ["for the Captain was about to jerk them overboard. “They’ll be of much more use to you than me.", "그걸 버리지는 마세요, 커틀 선장님!\"—선장이 그것들을 뱃전 너머로 휙 던지려던 참이었던 것이다.—\"그건 저보다 선장님께 훨씬 더 쓸모가 있을 거예요."], ["Give me your stick.", "선장님 지팡이를 주세요."], ["I have often thought I should like to have it.", "그게 갖고 싶다고 자주 생각했거든요."], ["There!", "자!"], ["Good-bye, Captain Cuttle!", "안녕히 계세요, 커틀 선장님!"], ["Take care of my Uncle!", "제 삼촌을 잘 보살펴 주세요!"], ["Uncle Sol, God bless you!”", "솔 삼촌, 신의 가호가 함께하기를요!\""]], [["They were over the side in the confusion, before Walter caught another glimpse of either; and when he ran up to the stern, and looked after them, he saw his Uncle hanging down his head in the boat, and Captain Cuttle rapping him on the back with the great silver watch (it must have been very painful), and gesticulating hopefully with the teaspoons and sugar-tongs. Catching sight of Walter, Captain Cuttle dropped the property into the bottom of the boat with perfect unconcern, being evidently oblivious of its existence, and pulling off the glazed hat hailed him lustily. The glazed hat made quite a show in the sun with its glistening, and the Captain continued to wave it until he could be seen no longer. Then the confusion on board, which had been rapidly increasing, reached its height; two or three other boats went away with a cheer; the sails shone bright and full above, as Walter watched them spread their surface to the favourable breeze; the water flew in sparkles from the prow; and off upon her voyage went the Son and Heir, as hopefully and trippingly as many another son and heir, gone down, had started on his way before her.", "월터가 두 사람을 다시 한번 흘끗 보기도 전에, 그들은 어수선한 틈에 뱃전 너머로 내려가 버렸다. 그가 고물로 뛰어가 그들을 뒤따라 바라보았을 때, 삼촌은 작은 배 안에서 고개를 떨구고 있었고, 커틀 선장은 그 커다란 은시계로 삼촌의 등을 톡톡 두드리며(무척 아팠을 것이 분명하다), 찻숟가락과 각설탕 집게를 들고 희망에 차서 손짓을 하고 있었다. 월터가 눈에 들어오자, 커틀 선장은 그 물건들을 그것의 존재 따위는 까맣게 잊은 듯 더없이 무심하게 배 바닥에 떨어뜨리고는, 칠을 한 모자를 벗어 들고 우렁차게 그를 불렀다. 칠을 한 모자가 햇빛 속에서 번쩍번쩍 자못 볼만했고, 선장은 그가 더는 보이지 않을 때까지 계속 그것을 흔들어 댔다. 그러자 배 위의 그 소동은, 빠르게 더해 가더니, 절정에 이르렀다. 다른 작은 배 두세 척이 환호 소리와 함께 떠나갔다. 월터가 지켜보는 가운데 돛들은 그 면을 순풍에 펼치며 머리 위에서 환하고 가득하게 빛났다. 뱃머리에서는 물이 반짝이며 흩날렸다. 그리하여 '아들이자 후계자호'는 항해 길에 올랐으니, 그보다 앞서 침몰해 간 숱한 다른 아들이자 후계자들이 제 길을 떠났던 것처럼, 그토록 희망에 차고 그토록 경쾌하게."]], [["Day after day, old Sol and Captain Cuttle kept her reckoning in the little back parlour and worked out her course, with the chart spread before them on the round table.", "날마다, 솔 노인과 커틀 선장은 작은 뒷방에서 그 배의 위치를 계산하고, 둥근 탁자 위에 해도를 펼쳐 놓고 그 항로를 짚어 나갔다."], ["At night, when old Sol climbed upstairs, so lonely, to the attic where it sometimes blew great guns, he looked up at the stars and listened to the wind, and kept a longer watch than would have fallen to his lot on board the ship.", "밤이면, 솔 노인이 그토록 쓸쓸하게 위층으로, 이따금 대포 소리처럼 바람이 휘몰아치는 다락방으로 올라가서는, 별들을 올려다보고 바람 소리에 귀를 기울이며, 배 위에서 자기 몫으로 떨어졌을 것보다 더 긴 망을 보곤 했다."], ["The last bottle of the old Madeira, which had had its cruising days, and known its dangers of the deep, lay silently beneath its dust and cobwebs, in the meanwhile, undisturbed.", "그동안, 순항하던 시절을 보내고 바다의 위험들을 겪어 본 그 마지막 오래된 마데이라 한 병은, 먼지와 거미줄 아래 말없이, 건드려지지 않은 채 누워 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XX. Mr Dombey goes upon a Journey", "# CHAPTER XX. Mr Dombey goes upon a Journey"]], [["Mr Dombey, Sir,”", "“돔비 씨, 선생,\""], ["said Major Bagstock, “Joey” B.", "백스톡 소령이 말했다, \"이 '조이'"], ["is not in general a man of sentiment, for Joseph is tough.", "B.는 본래 감상에 젖는 사내가 아니올시다. 조지프는 질긴 사람이거든요."], ["But Joe has his feelings, Sir, and when they are awakened—Damme, Mr Dombey,”", "하지만 조에게도 감정이란 게 있지요, 선생, 그리고 그게 한번 깨어나면—제기랄, 돔비 씨,\""], ["cried the Major with sudden ferocity, “this is weakness, and I won’t submit to it!”", "소령은 별안간 사납게 외쳤다, \"이건 나약함이오, 나는 거기에 굴복하지 않겠소!\""]], [["Major Bagstock delivered himself of these expressions on receiving Mr Dombey as his guest at the head of his own staircase in Princess’s Place.", "백스톡 소령이 이런 말을 쏟아 낸 것은, 프린세스 플레이스에 있는 자기 집 계단 꼭대기에서 돔비 씨를 손님으로 맞이하던 참이었다."], ["Mr Dombey had come to breakfast with the Major, previous to their setting forth on their trip; and the ill-starved Native had already undergone a world of misery arising out of the muffins, while, in connexion with the general question of boiled eggs, life was a burden to him.", "돔비 씨는 여행길에 나서기에 앞서 소령과 아침을 함께 먹으러 온 터였다. 그리고 그 박복한 원주민은 머핀을 둘러싼 갖은 비참을 이미 겪은 뒤였고, 삶은 달걀이라는 전반적인 문제와 관련해서는 인생이 그에게 무거운 짐이 되어 있었다."]], [["“It is not for an old soldier of the Bagstock breed,”", "\"백스톡 혈통의 늙은 군인이 할 짓은 아니지요,\""], ["observed the Major, relapsing into a mild state, “to deliver himself up, a prey to his own emotions; but—damme, Sir,”", "소령은 다시 온화한 상태로 돌아오며 말했다, \"제 감정에 사로잡혀 먹잇감이 되어 버리는 일 말이오. 하지만—제기랄, 선생,\""], ["cried the Major, in another spasm of ferocity, “I condole with you!”", "소령은 또 한 차례 사나운 발작을 일으키며 외쳤다, \"선생께 애도를 표하는 바요!\""]], [["The Major’s purple visage deepened in its hue, and the Major’s lobster eyes stood out in bolder relief, as he shook Mr Dombey by the hand, imparting to that peaceful action as defiant a character as if it had been the prelude to his immediately boxing Mr Dombey for a thousand pounds a side and the championship of England.", "소령의 자줏빛 얼굴은 한층 짙어졌고, 소령의 가재 같은 두 눈은 한결 또렷이 튀어나왔다. 그는 돔비 씨의 손을 잡고 흔들었는데, 그 평화로운 행위에 어찌나 도전적인 성격을 입혔던지, 마치 곧바로 돔비 씨와 한쪽에 천 파운드씩 걸고 영국 챔피언 자리를 두고 권투 시합이라도 벌이기 직전의 서막인 듯했다."], ["With a rotatory motion of his head, and a wheeze very like the cough of a horse, the Major then conducted his visitor to the sitting-room, and there welcomed him (having now composed his feelings) with the freedom and frankness of a travelling companion.", "그러고는 머리를 빙빙 돌리고 말의 기침과 아주 흡사한 쌕쌕거리는 소리를 내면서, 소령은 손님을 거실로 안내했고, 거기서 (이제 감정을 가라앉힌 채) 여행 길동무다운 거리낌 없고 솔직한 태도로 그를 맞이했다."]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\""], ["said the Major, “I’m glad to see you.", "소령이 말했다, \"당신을 만나 기쁘오."], ["I’m proud to see you.", "당신을 만나 자랑스럽소. 유럽에 J."], ["There are not many men in Europe to whom J. Bagstock would say that—for Josh is blunt.", "백스톡이 이런 말을 해 줄 사내는 많지 않소—조시는 무뚝뚝한 사람이거든요."], ["Sir: it’s his nature—but Joey B. is proud to see you, Dombey.”", "선생, 그게 그의 천성이오—하지만 조이 B.는 당신을 만나 자랑스럽소, 돔비.\""]], [["“Major,”", "\"소령,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, “you are very obliging.”", "돔비 씨가 응수했다, \"참으로 친절하시군요.\""]], [["“No, Sir,”", "\"천만에, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “Devil a bit!", "소령이 말했다, \"조금도 그렇지 않소!"], ["That’s not my character.", "그건 내 성품이 아니오."], ["If that had been Joe’s character, Joe might have been, by this time, Lieutenant-General Sir Joseph Bagstock, K.C.B., and might have received you in very different quarters.", "만약 그게 조의 성품이었다면, 조는 지금쯤 육군 중장 조지프 백스톡 경, 바스 훈장 수훈자가 되어 있었을 테고, 당신을 아주 다른 거처에서 맞이했을 게요."], ["You don’t know old Joe yet, I find.", "보아하니 당신은 아직 늙은 조를 잘 모르는 것 같소."], ["But this occasion, being special, is a source of pride to me.", "하지만 이번 일은 특별하니, 내게 자랑거리가 되는 것이오."], ["By the Lord, Sir,”", "맹세코, 선생,\""], ["said the Major resolutely, “it’s an honour to me!”", "소령이 단호하게 말했다, \"이건 내게 영광이오!\""]], [["Mr Dombey, in his estimation of himself and his money, felt that this was very true, and therefore did not dispute the point.", "돔비 씨는 자신과 자기 재산에 대한 평가에서, 이것이 매우 사실이라고 느꼈으므로 그 점을 따지고 들지 않았다."], ["But the instinctive recognition of such a truth by the Major, and his plain avowal of it, were very able.", "그러나 소령이 그런 진실을 본능적으로 알아보고 솔직히 인정하는 것은 매우 능란한 일이었다."], ["It was a confirmation to Mr Dombey, if he had required any, of his not being mistaken in the Major.", "그것은, 만약 돔비 씨에게 확증이 필요했다면, 자신이 소령을 잘못 보지 않았다는 확증이 되었다."], ["It was an assurance to him that his power extended beyond his own immediate sphere; and that the Major, as an officer and a gentleman, had a no less becoming sense of it, than the beadle of the Royal Exchange.", "그것은 자기 권세가 자기 자신의 직접적인 영역 너머까지 미치고 있다는 보증이었고, 또 소령이, 장교이자 신사로서, 왕립 거래소의 수위 못지않게 그 권세를 마땅히 알아보는 감각을 지녔다는 보증이었다."]], [["And if it were ever consolatory to know this, or the like of this, it was consolatory then, when the impotence of his will, the instability of his hopes, the feebleness of wealth, had been so direfully impressed upon him.", "이런 것, 혹은 이런 것과 비슷한 것을 안다는 게 언제고 위안이 된다면, 바로 그때야말로 위안이 되었다. 자기 의지의 무력함, 자기 희망의 불안정함, 부의 허약함이 그토록 처참하게 그의 가슴에 새겨진 그때였으니."], ["What could it do, his boy had asked him.", "그게 무슨 소용이 있단 말이오, 하고 자기 아들이 그에게 물었다. 이따금, 그 아이의 그 질문을 떠올릴 때면, 그는 자기 자신도 묻지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Sometimes, thinking of the baby question, he could hardly forbear inquiring, himself, what could it do indeed: what had it done?", "정말이지 그게 무슨 소용이 있었던가. 그게 무엇을 해 주었던가."]], [["But these were lonely thoughts, bred late at night in the sullen despondency and gloom of his retirement, and pride easily found its reassurance in many testimonies to the truth, as unimpeachable and precious as the Major’s.", "그러나 이것들은 고독한 생각이었다. 늦은 밤, 그의 칩거가 빚어내는 침울한 낙담과 어둠 속에서 자라난 생각이었고, 자존심은 소령의 그것만큼이나 흠잡을 데 없고 값진 여러 증언 속에서 손쉽게 다시금 안심을 찾아냈다."], ["Mr Dombey, in his friendlessness, inclined to the Major.", "돔비 씨는 벗이 없는 처지에서 소령에게 마음이 기울었다."], ["It cannot be said that he warmed towards him, but he thawed a little, The Major had had some part—and not too much—in the days by the seaside.", "그를 향해 따뜻해졌다고는 할 수 없으나, 약간 누그러지기는 했다. 소령은 바닷가에서 보낸 그 나날에 얼마간—그러나 지나치지 않게—한몫을 한 사람이었다."], ["He was a man of the world, and knew some great people.", "그는 세상 물정에 밝은 사내였고, 몇몇 대단한 사람들을 알고 있었다."], ["He talked much, and told stories; and Mr Dombey was disposed to regard him as a choice spirit who shone in society, and who had not that poisonous ingredient of poverty with which choice spirits in general are too much adulterated.", "말이 많았고 이야기를 들려주었다. 그래서 돔비 씨는 그를, 사교계에서 빛을 발하는 선택받은 인물로, 게다가 그런 선택받은 인물들이 흔히 너무 많이 섞여 들이는 가난이라는 독성 성분을 지니지 않은 인물로 여기는 쪽으로 기울었다."], ["His station was undeniable.", "그의 지위는 부인할 수 없는 것이었다."], ["Altogether the Major was a creditable companion, well accustomed to a life of leisure, and to such places as that they were about to visit, and having an air of gentlemanly ease about him that mixed well enough with his own City character, and did not compete with it at all.", "통틀어 보아 소령은 신뢰할 만한 동행이었다. 여가의 삶에, 그리고 그들이 막 찾아가려는 그런 장소들에 잘 익숙했고, 자신의 도시 상인다운 성품과 충분히 잘 어우러지면서도 그것과 전혀 경합하지 않는, 신사답고 편안한 분위기를 풍기고 있었다."], ["If Mr Dombey had any lingering idea that the Major, as a man accustomed, in the way of his calling, to make light of the ruthless hand that had lately crushed his hopes, might unconsciously impart some useful philosophy to him, and scare away his weak regrets, he hid it from himself, and left it lying at the bottom of his pride, unexamined.", "만약 돔비 씨에게, 소령이 자기 직업의 성질상 최근 자기 희망을 짓밟은 그 무자비한 손길을 가볍게 여기는 데 익숙한 사내인 만큼, 자기도 모르게 자기에게 어떤 쓸모 있는 철학을 전해 주고 자기의 나약한 회한을 쫓아 줄지도 모른다는 미련 같은 생각이 남아 있었다면, 그는 그것을 자기 자신에게마저 감추어 자존심의 밑바닥에 검토하지 않은 채 내버려 두었다."]], [["“Where is my scoundrel?”", "\"내 그 악당 놈은 어디 있느냐?\""], ["said the Major, looking wrathfully round the room.", "소령이 방 안을 노여운 눈으로 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["The Native, who had no particular name, but answered to any vituperative epithet, presented himself instantly at the door and ventured to come no nearer.", "특별한 이름이 없는, 그러나 어떤 욕설이든 갖다 붙이면 거기에 대답하는 그 원주민이 곧장 문간에 나타났으나, 더 가까이는 감히 다가오지 못했다."]], [["“You villain!”", "\"이 악당아!\""], ["said the choleric Major, “where’s the breakfast?”", "성마른 소령이 말했다, \"아침은 어디 있느냐?\""]], [["The dark servant disappeared in search of it, and was quickly heard reascending the stairs in such a tremulous state, that the plates and dishes on the tray he carried, trembling sympathetically as he came, rattled again, all the way up.", "그 검은 하인은 그것을 찾으러 사라졌다가, 곧 계단을 다시 올라오는 소리가 들렸는데, 어찌나 부들부들 떨던지, 그가 들고 오는 쟁반 위의 접시며 그릇들이 그와 한마음으로 떨면서 올라오는 내내 덜그럭덜그럭 다시 부딪쳤다."]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\" 소령이 말했다."], ["said the Major, glancing at the Native as he arranged the table, and encouraging him with an awful shake of his fist when he upset a spoon, “here is a devilled grill, a savoury pie, a dish of kidneys, and so forth.", "그는 원주민이 식탁을 차리는 모습을 흘끗 보면서, 그가 숟가락을 떨어뜨리자 무시무시하게 주먹을 흔들어 그를 다그쳤다. \"여기 매콤한 석쇠구이며, 맛깔난 파이며, 콩팥 요리 한 접시며, 그런 것들이 있소."], ["Pray sit down.", "자, 앉으시오."], ["Old Joe can give you nothing but camp fare, you see.”", "보다시피 늙은 조는 야영지 음식밖에 차려 드릴 게 없소이다.\""]], [["“Very excellent fare, Major,”", "\"아주 훌륭한 음식이오, 소령,\""], ["replied his guest; and not in mere politeness either; for the Major always took the best possible care of himself, and indeed ate rather more of rich meats than was good for him, insomuch that his Imperial complexion was mainly referred by the faculty to that circumstance.", "손님이 대답했다. 단지 예의로만 한 말은 아니었으니, 소령은 늘 자기 자신을 더없이 잘 돌보았고, 사실 기름진 고기를 몸에 좋을 정도 이상으로 먹어 댔던 까닭에, 그의 황제 같은 안색이 주로 그 사정 탓이라고 의학계에서들 보았던 것이다."]], [["“You have been looking over the way, Sir,”", "\"건너편을 보고 오셨소, 선생,\""], ["observed the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“Have you seen our friend?”", "\"우리 친구분을 보셨소?\""]], [["“You mean Miss Tox,”", "\"톡스 양 말씀이오,\""], ["retorted Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 받아쳤다."], ["“No.”", "\"아니오.\""]], [["“Charming woman, Sir,”", "\"매혹적인 여인이지요, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, with a fat laugh rising in his short throat, and nearly suffocating him.", "소령이 말했다. 살진 웃음이 그의 짧은 목에서 차올라 그를 거의 질식시킬 지경이었다."]], [["“Miss Tox is a very good sort of person, I believe,”", "\"톡스 양은 매우 좋은 부류의 사람인 것 같소,\""], ["replied Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대답했다."]], [["The haughty coldness of the reply seemed to afford Major Bagstock infinite delight.", "그 거만하고 냉랭한 대답이 백스톡 소령에게는 무한한 기쁨을 안겨 주는 듯했다."], ["He swelled and swelled, exceedingly: and even laid down his knife and fork for a moment, to rub his hands.", "그는 한껏, 자꾸자꾸 부풀어 올랐고, 심지어 잠시 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 두 손을 비비기까지 했다."]], [["“Old Joe, Sir,”", "\"늙은 조도, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “was a bit of a favourite in that quarter once.", "소령이 말했다, \"한때는 저쪽에서 제법 총애받는 사람이었소."], ["But Joe has had his day. J.", "하지만 조의 시절은 갔지요."], ["Bagstock is extinguished—outrivalled—floored, Sir.”", "J. 백스톡은 꺼져 버렸소—밀려났소—나가떨어졌소, 선생.\""]], [["“I should have supposed,”", "\"내 짐작에는,\""], ["Mr Dombey replied, “that the lady’s day for favourites was over: but perhaps you are jesting, Major.”", "돔비 씨가 대답했다, \"그 부인의 총애받을 시절이라는 것도 이미 끝났을 것 같소만. 어쩌면 농담을 하시는 거겠지요, 소령.\""]], [["“Perhaps you are jesting, Dombey?”", "\"어쩌면 당신이 농담을 하시는 거겠지요, 돔비?\""], ["was the Major’s rejoinder.", "소령이 응수했다."]], [["There never was a more unlikely possibility.", "이보다 더 있을 법하지 않은 가능성은 없었다."], ["It was so clearly expressed in Mr Dombey’s face, that the Major apologised.", "그것이 돔비 씨의 얼굴에 너무도 분명히 드러나 있었으므로, 소령이 사과를 했다."]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "\"용서하시오,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I see you are in earnest.", "\"당신이 진심이라는 걸 알겠소."], ["I tell you what, Dombey.”", "내 한마디 하리다, 돔비.\""], ["The Major paused in his eating, and looked mysteriously indignant.", "소령은 먹던 것을 멈추고, 알 수 없게 분개하는 표정을 지었다."], ["“That’s a de-vilish ambitious woman, Sir.”", "\"저건 지독히도 야심 많은 여자요, 선생.\""]], [["Mr Dombey said “Indeed?”", "돔비 씨는 차가운 무관심으로 \"그렇소?\"라고 말했다."], ["with frigid indifference: mingled perhaps with some contemptuous incredulity as to Miss Tox having the presumption to harbour such a superior quality.", "거기에는 아마, 톡스 양이 그런 고상한 자질을 품을 만큼 주제넘은 사람이라는 데 대한 얼마간 경멸 어린 불신도 섞여 있었을 것이다."]], [["“That woman, Sir,”", "\"저 여자는, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “is, in her way, a Lucifer.", "소령이 말했다, \"제 나름대로는 루시퍼요."], ["Joey B. has had his day, Sir, but he keeps his eyes.", "조이 B.의 시절은 갔소, 선생, 하지만 그는 눈만은 간직하고 있소."], ["He sees, does Joe.", "조는 본단 말이오."], ["His Royal Highness the late Duke of York observed of Joey, at a levee, that he saw.”", "작고하신 요크 공작 전하께서도 알현식에서 조이를 두고, 조는 볼 줄 안다고 평하셨소.\""]], [["The Major accompanied this with such a look, and, between eating, drinking, hot tea, devilled grill, muffins, and meaning, was altogether so swollen and inflamed about the head, that even Mr Dombey showed some anxiety for him.", "소령은 이 말과 함께 어찌나 대단한 눈빛을 보였던지, 그리고 먹고 마시고, 뜨거운 차에, 매콤한 석쇠구이에, 머핀에, 의미심장함까지 더해, 머리통이 온통 어찌나 부풀고 벌게졌던지, 돔비 씨조차 그를 좀 걱정하는 기색을 보였다."]], [["“That ridiculous old spectacle, Sir,”", "\"저 우스꽝스러운 늙은 구경거리는, 선생,\""], ["pursued the Major, “aspires.", "소령이 말을 이었다, \"야심을 품고 있소."], ["She aspires sky-high, Sir.", "하늘 높은 줄 모르고 야심을 품고 있단 말이오, 선생."], ["Matrimonially, Dombey.”", "혼인의 야심을 말이오, 돔비.\""]], [["“I am sorry for her,”", "\"그 여자가 안됐군요,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Don’t say that, Dombey,”", "\"그런 말 마시오, 돔비,\""], ["returned the Major in a warning voice.", "소령이 경고하는 어조로 응수했다."]], [["“Why should I not, Major?”", "\"어째서 그러면 안 되오, 소령?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["The Major gave no answer but the horse’s cough, and went on eating vigorously.", "소령은 말의 기침 말고는 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 다시 기운차게 먹기 시작했다."]], [["“She has taken an interest in your household,”", "\"그 여자는 당신네 가정에 관심을 가져 왔소,\""], ["said the Major, stopping short again, “and has been a frequent visitor at your house for some time now.”", "소령이 다시 뚝 멈추며 말했다, \"그리고 이제 한동안 당신 댁에 자주 드나드는 손님이 되어 왔지요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["replied Mr Dombey with great stateliness, “Miss Tox was originally received there, at the time of Mrs Dombey’s death, as a friend of my sister’s; and being a well-behaved person, and showing a liking for the poor infant, she was permitted—may I say encouraged—to repeat her visits with my sister, and gradually to occupy a kind of footing of familiarity in the family.", "돔비 씨가 대단히 위엄 있게 대답했다, \"톡스 양은 본래 돔비 부인이 세상을 떠났을 때, 내 누이의 벗으로서 그곳에 받아들여졌소. 그리고 행실이 바른 사람이고, 그 가엾은 갓난것을 좋아하는 기색을 보였기에, 누이와 더불어 방문을 거듭하는 것이—권장받았다고까지 말할 수 있겠소—허락되었고, 차츰 그 집안에서 일종의 친밀한 발판을 차지하게 되었소."], ["I have,”", "나는,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, in the tone of a man who was making a great and valuable concession, “I have a respect for Miss Tox.", "돔비 씨가, 크고 값진 양보를 베푸는 사람의 어조로 말했다, \"나는 톡스 양을 존중하오. 그 여자는 친절하게도 우리 집에서 자잘한 봉사를 많이 베풀어 주었소."], ["She has been so obliging as to render many little services in my house: trifling and insignificant services perhaps, Major, but not to be disparaged on that account: and I hope I have had the good fortune to be enabled to acknowledge them by such attention and notice as it has been in my power to bestow.", "어쩌면 사소하고 하찮은 봉사일지 모르겠소, 소령, 그러나 그렇다고 해서 깎아내릴 것은 아니지요. 그리고 나는 다행히도 내가 베풀 수 있는 만큼의 배려와 관심으로 그것을 인정해 줄 수 있었기를 바라오."], ["I hold myself indebted to Miss Tox, Major,”", "나는 톡스 양에게 빚을 졌다고 생각하오, 소령,\""], ["added Mr Dombey, with a slight wave of his hand, “for the pleasure of your acquaintance.”", "돔비 씨가 손을 가볍게 흔들며 덧붙였다, \"당신과 알게 된 즐거움에 대해서 말이오.\""]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\""], ["said the Major, warmly: “no! No, Sir!", "소령이 열을 띠며 말했다, \"안 되오!"], ["Joseph Bagstock can never permit that assertion to pass uncontradicted.", "안 되오, 선생! 조지프 백스톡은 그런 주장이 반박 없이 지나가도록 결코 두고 볼 수 없소."], ["Your knowledge of old Joe, Sir, such as he is, and old Joe’s knowledge of you, Sir, had its origin in a noble fellow, Sir—in a great creature, Sir.", "당신이 늙은 조를, 비록 보잘것없는 사람이나마, 알게 된 것과, 늙은 조가 당신을 알게 된 것은, 선생, 한 고귀한 친구에게서 비롯된 것이오—한 위대한 존재에게서 말이오, 선생. 돔비!\""], ["Dombey!”", "소령이 말했다."], ["said the Major, with a struggle which it was not very difficult to parade, his whole life being a struggle against all kinds of apoplectic symptoms, “we knew each other through your boy.”", "그가 그토록 분투하는 시늉을 내기란 그리 어렵지 않았으니, 그의 평생이 온갖 졸중 증세에 맞선 분투였던 까닭이다. \"우리는 당신 아드님을 통해 서로 알게 된 것이오.\""]], [["Mr Dombey seemed touched, as it is not improbable the Major designed he should be, by this allusion.", "돔비 씨는 이 언급에 마음이 움직인 듯했고, 소령이 그렇게 만들 작정이었던 것도 아마 사실이었을 것이다."], ["He looked down and sighed: and the Major, rousing himself fiercely, again said, in reference to the state of mind into which he felt himself in danger of falling, that this was weakness, and nothing should induce him to submit to it.", "그는 고개를 떨구고 한숨을 쉬었다. 그러자 소령은 사납게 정신을 가다듬고는, 자기가 빠져들 위험을 느끼는 그 심정 상태를 두고, 이것은 나약함이며 무엇으로도 자기를 거기에 굴복시킬 수 없다고 다시 한번 말했다."]], [["“Our friend had a remote connexion with that event,”", "\"우리 친구분은 그 일과 먼 인연이 있었던 거요,\""], ["said the Major, “and all the credit that belongs to her, J. B.", "소령이 말했다, \"그러니 그 여자에게 돌아갈 공로라면, J."], ["is willing to give her, Sir. Notwithstanding which, Ma’am,”", "B.는 기꺼이 그 여자에게 돌려줄 용의가 있소, 선생."], ["he added, raising his eyes from his plate, and casting them across Princess’s Place, to where Miss Tox was at that moment visible at her window watering her flowers, “you’re a scheming jade, Ma’am, and your ambition is a piece of monstrous impudence.", "그렇긴 하나, 마님,\" 그는 접시에서 눈을 들어, 프린세스 플레이스 건너편으로, 마침 그 순간 창가에서 꽃에 물을 주는 톡스 양이 보이는 곳으로 눈길을 던지며 덧붙였다, \"당신은 음흉한 여우요, 마님, 그리고 당신의 야심이란 가공할 만한 뻔뻔함의 표본이오."], ["If it only made yourself ridiculous, Ma’am,”", "그게 당신 자신만 우스꽝스럽게 만든다면, 마님,\""], ["said the Major, rolling his head at the unconscious Miss Tox, while his starting eyes appeared to make a leap towards her, “you might do that to your heart’s content, Ma’am, without any objection, I assure you, on the part of Bagstock.”", "소령은 자기를 알 리 없는 톡스 양을 향해 머리를 굴리며 말했고, 그동안 그의 튀어나온 두 눈은 그녀를 향해 뛰어오르는 듯했다, \"당신은 마음껏 그래도 좋소, 마님, 백스톡 쪽에서는 추호도 이의가 없으리라 장담하오.\""], ["Here the Major laughed frightfully up in the tips of his ears and in the veins of his head.", "여기서 소령은 귀 끝과 머릿속 혈관까지 동원해 무시무시하게 웃어 댔다."], ["“But when, Ma’am,”", "\"하지만 마님,\""], ["said the Major, “you compromise other people, and generous, unsuspicious people too, as a repayment for their condescension, you stir the blood of old Joe in his body.”", "소령이 말했다, \"당신이 다른 사람들을, 그것도 너그럽고 의심할 줄 모르는 사람들을, 그들의 호의에 대한 보답이랍시고 위태롭게 만든다면, 그건 늙은 조의 몸속 피를 끓게 하는 거요.\""]], [["“Major,”", "\"소령,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, reddening, “I hope you do not hint at anything so absurd on the part of Miss Tox as—”", "돔비 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다, \"톡스 양 쪽에서 무슨 그토록 터무니없는 것을 암시하시는 건 아니겠지요, 이를테면—\""]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\""], ["returned the Major, “I hint at nothing.", "소령이 응수했다, \"나는 아무것도 암시하지 않소."], ["But Joey B.", "하지만 조이 B.는 세상을 살아 본 사람이오, 선생."], ["has lived in the world, Sir: lived in the world with his eyes open, Sir, and his ears cocked: and Joe tells you, Dombey, that there’s a devilish artful and ambitious woman over the way.”", "눈을 똑바로 뜨고, 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 세상을 살아 본 사람이란 말이오. 그리고 조는 당신에게 말하오, 돔비, 저 건너편에 지독히도 교활하고 야심 많은 여자가 있다고 말이오.\""]], [["Mr Dombey involuntarily glanced over the way; and an angry glance he sent in that direction, too.", "돔비 씨는 자기도 모르게 건너편을 흘끗 보았다. 그리고 그 방향으로 노여운 눈길을 또한 던졌다."]], [["“That’s all on such a subject that shall pass the lips of Joseph Bagstock,”", "\"그런 주제에 관해서라면 조지프 백스톡의 입에서 나올 말은 그게 전부요,\""], ["said the Major firmly.", "소령이 단호하게 말했다."], ["“Joe is not a tale-bearer, but there are times when he must speak, when he will speak!—confound your arts, Ma’am,”", "\"조는 고자질쟁이가 아니오, 하지만 말을 해야만 할 때가, 말을 하고야 말 때가 있는 법이오!—당신의 그 잔꾀에 저주가 있기를, 마님,\""], ["cried the Major, again apostrophising his fair neighbour, with great ire,—“when the provocation is too strong to admit of his remaining silent.”", "소령이 또다시 자기 어여쁜 이웃을 향해 대단한 노여움으로 부르짖었다, \"도발이 너무 강해서 도저히 침묵을 지키고 있을 수 없을 때 말이오.\""]], [["The emotion of this outbreak threw the Major into a paroxysm of horse’s coughs, which held him for a long time.", "이 폭발의 격정으로 소령은 말의 기침 발작에 빠져들었고, 그것이 오래도록 그를 붙들었다."], ["On recovering he added:", "진정이 되자 그는 덧붙였다."]], [["“And now, Dombey, as you have invited Joe—old Joe, who has no other merit, Sir, but that he is tough and hearty—to be your guest and guide at Leamington, command him in any way you please, and he is wholly yours.", "\"자, 이제, 돔비, 당신이 조를—다른 아무 장점도 없고 그저 질기고 기운차다는 것밖에 없는 늙은 조를, 선생—리밍턴에서 당신의 손님이자 안내자로 청한 이상, 무슨 일이든 마음대로 그에게 명하시오, 그러면 그는 온전히 당신의 것이오."], ["I don’t know, Sir,”", "나는 모르겠소, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, wagging his double chin with a jocose air, “what it is you people see in Joe to make you hold him in such great request, all of you; but this I know, Sir, that if he wasn’t pretty tough, and obstinate in his refusals, you’d kill him among you with your invitations and so forth, in double-quick time.”", "소령이 익살스러운 기색으로 이중 턱을 흔들며 말했다, \"도대체 당신네 사람들이 조에게서 무엇을 보길래 다들 그를 그렇게 대단히 청하는지 말이오. 하지만 이것만은 아오, 선생, 만약 그가 어지간히 질기지 않고, 또 한사코 거절하지 않았더라면, 당신네들이 초대니 뭐니 하는 것으로 그를 단번에 죽여 버렸으리라는 것을 말이오.\""]], [["Mr Dombey, in a few words, expressed his sense of the preference he received over those other distinguished members of society who were clamouring for the possession of Major Bagstock.", "돔비 씨는 백스톡 소령을 차지하려고 아우성치는 다른 저명한 사교계 인사들보다 자기가 우선권을 누리게 된 데 대해 몇 마디로 사의를 표했다."], ["But the Major cut him short by giving him to understand that he followed his own inclinations, and that they had risen up in a body and said with one accord, “J.", "그러나 소령은 그의 말을 잘라, 자기는 자기 마음 가는 대로 따를 뿐이며, 그 마음들이 한 덩어리로 일어나 한목소리로 \"J."], ["B., Dombey is the man for you to choose as a friend.”", "B., 돔비야말로 자네가 벗으로 고를 사내일세\"라고 말했다는 것을 그에게 알아듣게 했다."]], [["The Major being by this time in a state of repletion, with essence of savoury pie oozing out at the corners of his eyes, and devilled grill and kidneys tightening his cravat: and the time moreover approaching for the departure of the railway train to Birmingham, by which they were to leave town: the Native got him into his great-coat with immense difficulty, and buttoned him up until his face looked staring and gasping, over the top of that garment, as if he were in a barrel.", "이쯤에서 소령은 포만 상태에 이르러, 맛깔난 파이의 진액이 눈가에서 배어 나오고, 매콤한 석쇠구이와 콩팥 요리가 그의 크라바트를 죄어 오고 있었다. 게다가 그들이 도시를 떠나 타고 갈 버밍엄행 철도 열차의 출발 시각도 다가오고 있었던지라, 원주민은 엄청난 애를 써서 그를 외투에 끼워 넣고, 단추를 채워 올린 끝에, 마치 그가 통 속에 들어 있기라도 한 양, 그 외투 위로 얼굴이 부릅뜨고 헐떡이는 꼴이 되도록 만들었다."], ["The Native then handed him separately, and with a decent interval between each supply, his washleather gloves, his thick stick, and his hat; which latter article the Major wore with a rakish air on one side of his head, by way of toning down his remarkable visage.", "그런 다음 원주민은 그에게 따로따로, 그것도 매번 적당한 간격을 두고서, 그의 섀미 가죽 장갑과, 그의 굵은 지팡이와, 그의 모자를 건넸다. 소령은 그 마지막 물건을, 자기의 두드러진 얼굴을 좀 누그러뜨리려는 듯, 멋스럽게 머리 한쪽에 비스듬히 얹어 썼다."], ["The Native had previously packed, in all possible and impossible parts of Mr Dombey’s chariot, which was in waiting, an unusual quantity of carpet-bags and small portmanteaus, no less apoplectic in appearance than the Major himself: and having filled his own pockets with Seltzer water, East India sherry, sandwiches, shawls, telescopes, maps, and newspapers, any or all of which light baggage the Major might require at any instant of the journey, he announced that everything was ready.", "원주민은 그에 앞서, 대기 중이던 돔비 씨의 사륜마차의 가능하고 불가능한 온갖 부분에다, 소령 자신만큼이나 졸중에 걸린 듯한 행색의 융단 가방과 작은 여행 가방을 유달리 많이 실어 두었다. 그리고 셀처 광천수며, 동인도산 셰리주며, 샌드위치며, 숄이며, 망원경이며, 지도며, 신문 따위로 자기 주머니를 가득 채웠으니—그 가벼운 짐들 가운데 무엇이든, 혹은 전부든 소령이 여행 중 언제 어느 순간에 필요로 할지 모를 일이었다—그러고 나서 그는 모든 준비가 끝났다고 알렸다."], ["To complete the equipment of this unfortunate foreigner (currently believed to be a prince in his own country), when he took his seat in the rumble by the side of Mr Towlinson, a pile of the Major’s cloaks and great-coats was hurled upon him by the landlord, who aimed at him from the pavement with those great missiles like a Titan, and so covered him up, that he proceeded, in a living tomb, to the railroad station.", "이 불운한 이방인(제 나라에서는 왕자라는 소문이 도는)이 톨린슨 씨 곁의 뒷좌석에 자리를 잡았을 때, 그의 채비를 완성하기 위해 여관 주인이 소령의 망토며 외투를 한 무더기 그에게 내던졌는데, 주인은 그 거대한 발사체들을 마치 타이탄처럼 보도에서 그를 겨냥해 던져, 그를 어찌나 뒤덮어 버렸던지, 그는 산 채로 무덤에 든 꼴로 철도역까지 갔다."]], [["But before the carriage moved away, and while the Native was in the act of sepulture, Miss Tox appearing at her window, waved a lilywhite handkerchief.", "그러나 마차가 움직이기 전, 원주민이 한창 매장당하고 있는 사이에, 톡스 양이 창가에 나타나 백합처럼 흰 손수건을 흔들었다."], ["Mr Dombey received this parting salutation very coldly—very coldly even for him—and honouring her with the slightest possible inclination of his head, leaned back in the carriage with a very discontented look.", "돔비 씨는 이 작별 인사를 매우 냉랭하게—그로서도 매우 냉랭하다 할 만큼—받았고, 머리를 더없이 미미하게 까딱여 그녀를 예우한 뒤, 몹시 못마땅한 표정으로 마차에 등을 기대었다."], ["His marked behaviour seemed to afford the Major (who was all politeness in his recognition of Miss Tox) unbounded satisfaction; and he sat for a long time afterwards, leering, and choking, like an over-fed Mephistopheles.", "그의 그 뚜렷한 태도는 (톡스 양을 알아보는 데 더없이 정중했던) 소령에게 한없는 만족을 안겨 주는 듯했다. 그래서 그는 그 후로도 오랫동안, 너무 많이 먹은 메피스토펠레스처럼 곁눈질을 하고 켁켁거리며 앉아 있었다."]], [["During the bustle of preparation at the railway, Mr Dombey and the Major walked up and down the platform side by side; the former taciturn and gloomy, and the latter entertaining him, or entertaining himself, with a variety of anecdotes and reminiscences, in most of which Joe Bagstock was the principal performer.", "철도역에서 출발 채비로 분주한 동안, 돔비 씨와 소령은 승강장을 나란히 오락가락 거닐었다. 전자는 말이 없고 침울했으며, 후자는 갖가지 일화와 회상으로 그를, 혹은 자기 자신을 즐겁게 했는데, 그 대부분에서 조 백스톡이 주연이었다."], ["Neither of the two observed that in the course of these walks, they attracted the attention of a working man who was standing near the engine, and who touched his hat every time they passed; for Mr Dombey habitually looked over the vulgar herd, not at them; and the Major was looking, at the time, into the core of one of his stories.", "두 사람 다, 이렇게 거니는 동안 그들이 기관차 가까이 서 있던 한 노동자의 주의를 끌고 있다는 것을, 그리고 그 사내가 그들이 지나갈 때마다 모자에 손을 갖다 댄다는 것을 알아채지 못했다. 돔비 씨는 으레 천한 무리를 향해 보는 게 아니라 그들 너머를 보는 사람이었고, 소령은 그때 자기 이야기 가운데 하나의 한복판을 들여다보고 있었던 까닭이다."], ["At length, however, this man stepped before them as they turned round, and pulling his hat off, and keeping it off, ducked his head to Mr Dombey.", "그러나 마침내 이 사내가, 그들이 몸을 돌리는 참에 그들 앞으로 나서더니, 모자를 벗어 든 채 그것을 벗어 든 채로 돔비 씨에게 머리를 조아렸다."]], [["“Beg your pardon, Sir,”", "\"실례합니다요, 나리,\""], ["said the man, “but I hope you’re a doin’ pretty well, Sir.”", "사내가 말했다, \"그저 별고 없으시기를 바랍니다요, 나리.\""]], [["He was dressed in a canvas suit abundantly besmeared with coal-dust and oil, and had cinders in his whiskers, and a smell of half-slaked ashes all over him.", "그는 석탄 가루와 기름이 잔뜩 묻은 즈크 작업복을 입고 있었고, 구레나룻에는 탄 부스러기가 박혀 있었으며, 온몸에서 반쯤 꺼진 재 냄새가 풍겼다."], ["He was not a bad-looking fellow, nor even what could be fairly called a dirty-looking fellow, in spite of this; and, in short, he was Mr Toodle, professionally clothed.", "그런데도 그는 못생긴 사내는 아니었고, 공정하게 말하자면 지저분해 보이는 사내라고조차 할 수 없었다. 요컨대 그는 직업복을 차려입은 투들 씨였다."]], [["“I shall have the honour of stokin’ of you down, Sir,”", "\"제가 영광스럽게도 나리를 모시고 화부 노릇을 하게 됐습니다요, 나리,\""], ["said Mr Toodle.", "투들 씨가 말했다."], ["“Beg your pardon, Sir.—I hope you find yourself a coming round?”", "\"실례합니다요, 나리.—이제 좀 차도가 있으신지요?\""]], [["Mr Dombey looked at him, in return for his tone of interest, as if a man like that would make his very eyesight dirty.", "돔비 씨는 그 관심 어린 어조에 대한 답례로, 마치 저런 사내가 자기의 시력마저 더럽히기라도 할 듯이 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“’Scuse the liberty, Sir,”", "\"외람된 말씀입니다만, 나리,\""], ["said Toodle, seeing he was not clearly remembered, “but my wife Polly, as was called Richards in your family—”", "자기가 또렷이 기억되고 있지 않음을 알아챈 투들이 말했다, \"나리 댁에서 리처즈로 불리던 제 처 폴리가—\""]], [["A change in Mr Dombey’s face, which seemed to express recollection of him, and so it did, but it expressed in a much stronger degree an angry sense of humiliation, stopped Mr Toodle short.", "돔비 씨의 얼굴에 변화가 일었다. 그를 기억해 낸 것을 나타내는 듯했고 실제로도 그러했으나, 그보다 훨씬 강한 정도로, 굴욕감에서 비롯된 노여움을 나타내는 변화였다. 그 변화가 투들 씨의 말을 뚝 멈추게 했다."]], [["“Your wife wants money, I suppose,”", "\"자네 처가 돈이 필요한 모양이군,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, putting his hand in his pocket, and speaking (but that he always did) haughtily.", "돔비 씨가 주머니에 손을 넣으며, 거만하게 (그러나 그건 늘 그러던 대로였지만) 말했다."]], [["“No thank’ee, Sir,”", "\"아닙니다요, 고맙습니다만, 나리,\""], ["returned Toodle, “I can’t say she does.", "투들이 대답했다, \"그렇다고는 말씀 못 드리겠는뎁쇼."], ["I don’t.”", "저도요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey was stopped short now in his turn: and awkwardly: with his hand in his pocket.", "이번에는 돔비 씨가 거꾸로 말이 막혔다. 그것도 어색하게. 주머니에 손을 넣은 채로."]], [["“No, Sir,”", "\"아닙니다요, 나리,\""], ["said Toodle, turning his oilskin cap round and round; “we’re a doin’ pretty well, Sir; we haven’t no cause to complain in the worldly way, Sir.", "투들이 자기 방수모를 빙글빙글 돌리며 말했다. \"저희는 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다요, 나리."], ["We’ve had four more since then, Sir, but we rubs on.”", "세상살이 형편으로는 불평할 거리가 없는뎁쇼, 나리. 그 뒤로 넷이나 더 봤습니다요, 나리, 그래도 어떻게든 헤쳐 나가고 있습죠.\""]], [["Mr Dombey would have rubbed on to his own carriage, though in so doing he had rubbed the stoker underneath the wheels; but his attention was arrested by something in connexion with the cap still going slowly round and round in the man’s hand.", "돔비 씨는 자기 마차 쪽으로 헤쳐 나갈 참이었고, 그러느라 그 화부를 바퀴 밑으로 밀어 깔아뭉갰더라도 개의치 않았을 것이다. 그러나 사내의 손에서 여전히 천천히 빙글빙글 돌아가는 그 모자와 관련된 무언가가 그의 주의를 붙들었다."]], [["“We lost one babby,”", "\"저희가 갓난것 하나를 잃었습죠,\""], ["observed Toodle, “there’s no denyin’.”", "투들이 말했다, \"그건 부정 못 합니다요.\""]], [["“Lately,”", "\"최근에 말인가,\""], ["added Mr Dombey, looking at the cap.", "돔비 씨가 모자를 바라보며 덧붙였다."]], [["“No, Sir, up’ard of three years ago, but all the rest is hearty.", "\"아닙니다요, 나리, 삼 년도 더 전입니다요, 하지만 나머지는 다들 튼튼합죠."], ["And in the matter o readin’, Sir,”", "그리고 글 읽기 말씀입니다만, 나리,\""], ["said Toodle, ducking again, as if to remind Mr Dombey of what had passed between them on that subject long ago, “them boys o’ mine, they learned me, among ’em, arter all.", "투들이, 오래전 그 문제를 두고 둘 사이에 오갔던 일을 돔비 씨에게 일깨우려는 듯 다시 머리를 조아리며 말했다, \"그 녀석들이, 제 자식들 말입죠, 자기네끼리 결국 저를 가르쳤습니다요."], ["They’ve made a wery tolerable scholar of me, Sir, them boys.”", "그 녀석들이 저를 제법 그럴듯한 학자로 만들어 놨습죠, 나리, 그 녀석들이요.\""]], [["“Come, Major!”", "\"갑시다, 소령!\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Beg your pardon, Sir,”", "\"실례합니다요, 나리,\""], ["resumed Toodle, taking a step before them and deferentially stopping them again, still cap in hand: “I wouldn’t have troubled you with such a pint except as a way of gettin’ in the name of my son Biler—christened Robin—him as you was so good as to make a Charitable Grinder on.”", "투들이 다시 입을 열어, 그들 앞으로 한 걸음 나서며 여전히 모자를 손에 든 채 공손하게 또 그들을 멈춰 세웠다. \"그저 제 아들 바일러—세례명은 로빈입니다만—나리께서 친절하게도 자선 연마공으로 만들어 주신 그 녀석의 이름을 끄집어내려는 마음이 아니었더라면, 이런 일로 나리를 성가시게 해 드리지는 않았을 겁니다요.\""]], [["“Well, man,”", "\"그래서, 이 사람아,\""], ["said Mr Dombey in his severest manner.", "돔비 씨가 더없이 준엄한 태도로 말했다."], ["“What about him?”", "\"그 애가 어쨌다는 건가?\""]], [["“Why, Sir,”", "\"그게 말입죠, 나리,\""], ["returned Toodle, shaking his head with a face of great anxiety and distress, “I’m forced to say, Sir, that he’s gone wrong.”", "투들이 몹시 근심하고 비통해하는 얼굴로 고개를 저으며 대답했다, \"송구하지만 말씀드려야겠습니다요, 나리, 그 녀석이 그릇된 길로 빠졌습니다요.\""]], [["“He has gone wrong, has he?”", "\"그릇된 길로 빠졌다, 그런가?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with a hard kind of satisfaction.", "돔비 씨가 어딘가 매정한 만족감을 띠며 말했다."]], [["“He has fell into bad company, you see, genelmen,”", "\"보시다시피, 나쁜 패거리에 끼어들었습죠, 나리들,\""], ["pursued the father, looking wistfully at both, and evidently taking the Major into the conversation with the hope of having his sympathy.", "아비가 두 사람을 애처롭게 바라보며 말을 이었는데, 분명 소령을 대화에 끌어들여 그의 동정을 얻고 싶어 하는 기색이었다."], ["“He has got into bad ways.", "\"나쁜 길에 들어섰습니다요."], ["God send he may come to again, genelmen, but he’s on the wrong track now!", "부디 다시 제정신을 차렸으면 좋겠습니다만, 나리들, 그 녀석은 지금 그릇된 길로 가고 있습니다요!"], ["You could hardly be off hearing of it somehow, Sir,”", "나리께서도 어떻게든 그 소식을 안 들으시기는 어려우셨을 겁니다요, 나리,\""], ["said Toodle, again addressing Mr Dombey individually; “and it’s better I should out and say my boy’s gone rather wrong.", "투들이 다시 돔비 씨 한 사람을 향해 말했다. \"그러니 제가 차라리 털어놓고, 제 아이가 좀 그릇되게 빠졌다고 말씀드리는 게 낫겠습죠."], ["Polly’s dreadful down about it, genelmen,”", "폴리가 그 일로 끔찍이 풀이 죽어 있습니다요, 나리들,\""], ["said Toodle with the same dejected look, and another appeal to the Major.", "투들이 똑같이 의기소침한 표정으로, 또다시 소령에게 호소하며 말했다."]], [["“A son of this man’s whom I caused to be educated, Major,”", "\"내가 교육을 시켜 준 이 사람의 아들이오, 소령,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, giving him his arm.", "돔비 씨가 그에게 팔을 내밀며 말했다."], ["“The usual return!”", "\"흔해 빠진 보답이지요!\""]], [["“Take advice from plain old Joe, and never educate that sort of people, Sir,”", "\"솔직한 늙은 조의 충고를 들으시오, 그런 부류의 인간들은 절대 교육시키지 마시오, 선생,\""], ["returned the Major.", "소령이 응수했다."], ["“Damme, Sir, it never does!", "\"제기랄, 선생, 그건 결코 잘되는 법이 없소!"], ["It always fails!”", "언제나 실패한단 말이오!\""]], [["The simple father was beginning to submit that he hoped his son, the quondam Grinder, huffed and cuffed, and flogged and badged, and taught, as parrots are, by a brute jobbed into his place of schoolmaster with as much fitness for it as a hound, might not have been educated on quite a right plan in some undiscovered respect, when Mr Dombey angrily repeating “The usual return!” led the Major away.", "이 순박한 아비는, 자기 아들, 그 한때의 연마공이, 핀잔과 주먹질을 당하고, 매질과 표딱지를 달고, 사냥개만큼이나 교사 자리에 어울리는 어떤 무지막지한 자—연줄로 그 자리를 꿰찬 자—한테서 앵무새처럼 배운 것이고 보면, 어쩌면 무언가 알려지지 않은 점에서 아주 옳은 방식으로 교육받지는 못한 것 아니겠느냐고 조심스레 아뢰려던 참이었는데, 그때 돔비 씨가 \"흔해 빠진 보답이지요!\"라고 노엽게 되풀이하며 소령을 데리고 가 버렸다."], ["And the Major being heavy to hoist into Mr Dombey’s carriage, elevated in mid-air, and having to stop and swear that he would flay the Native alive, and break every bone in his skin, and visit other physical torments upon him, every time he couldn’t get his foot on the step, and fell back on that dark exile, had barely time before they started to repeat hoarsely that it would never do: that it always failed: and that if he were to educate “his own vagabond,”", "그리고 소령은 돔비 씨의 마차 안으로 들어 올리기에 무거운 사람인지라, 공중에 떠올려진 채, 발판에 발을 디디지 못하고 그 검은 유배자 위로 미끄러져 내릴 때마다, 멈춰 서서 저 원주민의 산 가죽을 벗기고, 그 살가죽 속 뼈마디를 죄다 부러뜨리고, 그 밖의 온갖 육체적 고문을 가하겠노라 맹세하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["he would certainly be hanged.", "그래서 출발하기 전 겨우 짬을 내어, 그건 결코 잘될 리 없으며, 언제나 실패하는 법이고, 만약 자기가 \"제 집의 부랑아 놈\"을 교육시킨다면 틀림없이 그놈이 교수형을 당할 거라고 쉰 목소리로 되풀이했다."]], [["Mr Dombey assented bitterly; but there was something more in his bitterness, and in his moody way of falling back in the carriage, and looking with knitted brows at the changing objects without, than the failure of that noble educational system administered by the Grinders’ Company.", "돔비 씨는 씁쓸하게 동의했다. 그러나 그의 그 씁쓸함에는, 그리고 마차에 시무룩이 등을 기댄 채 미간을 찌푸리고 바깥의 스쳐 가는 사물들을 바라보는 그 침울한 태도에는, 연마공 회사가 운영하는 그 고귀한 교육 제도의 실패 이상의 무언가가 있었다."], ["He had seen upon the man’s rough cap a piece of new crape, and he had assured himself, from his manner and his answers, that he wore it for his son.", "그는 그 사내의 거친 모자에 새 검정 상장(喪章) 한 조각이 달린 것을 보았고, 사내의 태도와 대답으로 미루어, 그가 그것을 자기 아들을 위해 두르고 있다고 확신했던 것이다."]], [["So!", "그렇구나!"], ["from high to low, at home or abroad, from Florence in his great house to the coarse churl who was feeding the fire then smoking before them, everyone set up some claim or other to a share in his dead boy, and was a bidder against him!", "위에서나 아래에서나, 집 안에서나 밖에서나, 큰 저택에 있는 플로렌스로부터 지금 그들 앞에서 연기를 피우며 불을 때고 있는 저 거친 촌놈에 이르기까지, 누구나 다 죽은 자기 아들에 대한 몫을 두고 이런저런 권리를 주장하며 자기와 다투어 값을 부르는 입찰자였구나!"], ["Could he ever forget how that woman had wept over his pillow, and called him her own child! or how he, waking from his sleep, had asked for her, and had raised himself in his bed and brightened when she came in!", "그 여자가 자기 베갯머리에서 어떻게 울며 그 애를 제 자식이라 불렀는지, 또 그 애가 잠에서 깨어나 그 여자를 찾으며 침대에서 몸을 일으키고 그 여자가 들어오자 환하게 밝아졌는지, 그가 과연 그것을 잊을 수 있을까!"]], [["To think of this presumptuous raker among coals and ashes going on before there, with his sign of mourning!", "석탄과 재나 헤집고 다니는 저 주제넘은 자가, 상장 따위를 달고 저기서 앞장서 가다니!"], ["To think that he dared to enter, even by a common show like that, into the trial and disappointment of a proud gentleman’s secret heart!", "저런 자가, 저런 흔해 빠진 겉치레로나마, 거만한 신사의 은밀한 가슴속 시련과 좌절 속으로 감히 들어오려 하다니!"], ["To think that this lost child, who was to have divided with him his riches, and his projects, and his power, and allied with whom he was to have shut out all the world as with a double door of gold, should have let in such a herd to insult him with their knowledge of his defeated hopes, and their boasts of claiming community of feeling with himself, so far removed: if not of having crept into the place wherein he would have lorded it, alone!", "자기의 재산과 계획과 권세를 함께 나누었어야 할, 그리고 그 애와 손잡고 마치 금으로 된 이중문으로 막아 버리듯 온 세상을 차단했어야 할, 이 잃어버린 아이가, 저런 무리를 안으로 들여, 그의 좌절된 희망을 안다는 사실로 그를 모욕하게 하고, 그토록 동떨어진 자기와 감정의 공동체를 주장한다고 떠벌리게 하다니—설령 자기가 홀로 군림하려 했을 그 자리에 슬그머니 기어든 것까지는 아니라 해도 말이다!"]], [["He found no pleasure or relief in the journey.", "그는 여행에서 아무런 즐거움도 위안도 찾지 못했다."], ["Tortured by these thoughts he carried monotony with him, through the rushing landscape, and hurried headlong, not through a rich and varied country, but a wilderness of blighted plans and gnawing jealousies.", "이런 생각들에 시달리며, 그는 휙휙 스쳐 가는 풍경을 가로질러 단조로움을 함께 지고 갔고, 풍요롭고 다채로운 시골이 아니라 시들어 버린 계획과 갉아먹는 시기심의 황야 속으로 곤두박질치듯 달려갔다."], ["The very speed at which the train was whirled along, mocked the swift course of the young life that had been borne away so steadily and so inexorably to its foredoomed end.", "열차가 휩쓸려 가는 그 속도 자체가, 그토록 꾸준하고도 그토록 가차 없이 예정된 종말로 실려 가 버린 어린 생명의 빠른 행로를 비웃는 듯했다."], ["The power that forced itself upon its iron way—its own—defiant of all paths and roads, piercing through the heart of every obstacle, and dragging living creatures of all classes, ages, and degrees behind it, was a type of the triumphant monster, Death.", "제 길—제 것인 그 길—을 따라 억지로 밀고 나아가며, 온갖 길과 도로를 거역하고, 모든 장애물의 심장을 꿰뚫고, 온갖 계층과 연령과 신분의 살아 있는 존재들을 제 뒤에 매달아 끌고 가는 그 힘은, 의기양양한 괴물, 곧 죽음의 한 표상이었다."]], [["Away, with a shriek, and a roar, and a rattle, from the town, burrowing among the dwellings of men and making the streets hum, flashing out into the meadows for a moment, mining in through the damp earth, booming on in darkness and heavy air, bursting out again into the sunny day so bright and wide; away, with a shriek, and a roar, and a rattle, through the fields, through the woods, through the corn, through the hay, through the chalk, through the mould, through the clay, through the rock, among objects close at hand and almost in the grasp, ever flying from the traveller, and a deceitful distance ever moving slowly within him: like as in the track of the remorseless monster, Death!", "비명을 지르고, 으르렁대고, 덜컹거리며, 도시를 벗어나, 사람들의 거처 사이를 파고들어 거리를 윙윙 울리게 하고, 한순간 초원으로 번쩍 튀어나왔다가, 축축한 흙을 뚫고 들어가, 어둠과 무거운 공기 속을 쿵쿵대며 나아가다가, 다시 그토록 밝고 드넓은 햇빛 비치는 대낮으로 터져 나온다. 비명을 지르고, 으르렁대고, 덜컹거리며, 들판을 지나, 숲을 지나, 곡식밭을 지나, 건초밭을 지나, 백악을 지나, 부엽토를 지나, 진흙을 지나, 바위를 지나, 손 닿을 듯 거의 잡힐 듯 가까운 사물들 사이로—그것들은 여행자에게서 끊임없이 달아나고, 한편 기만적인 먼 거리는 그의 안에서 끊임없이 천천히 움직인다. 마치 저 무자비한 괴물, 죽음의 자취에서처럼!"]], [["Through the hollow, on the height, by the heath, by the orchard, by the park, by the garden, over the canal, across the river, where the sheep are feeding, where the mill is going, where the barge is floating, where the dead are lying, where the factory is smoking, where the stream is running, where the village clusters, where the great cathedral rises, where the bleak moor lies, and the wild breeze smooths or ruffles it at its inconstant will; away, with a shriek, and a roar, and a rattle, and no trace to leave behind but dust and vapour: like as in the track of the remorseless monster, Death!", "골짜기를 지나, 언덕 위로, 황야 곁으로, 과수원 곁으로, 공원 곁으로, 정원 곁으로, 운하 위를 건너, 강을 가로질러, 양 떼가 풀을 뜯는 곳으로, 물방앗간이 돌아가는 곳으로, 거룻배가 떠 있는 곳으로, 죽은 이들이 누워 있는 곳으로, 공장이 연기를 뿜는 곳으로, 시냇물이 흐르는 곳으로, 마을이 옹기종기 모인 곳으로, 거대한 대성당이 솟은 곳으로, 황량한 황무지가 펼쳐지고 거센 바람이 제멋대로 그것을 매끈하게 펴거나 헝클어뜨리는 곳으로. 비명을 지르고, 으르렁대고, 덜컹거리며, 뒤에 남길 자취라곤 먼지와 수증기밖에 없이. 마치 저 무자비한 괴물, 죽음의 자취에서처럼!"]], [["Breasting the wind and light, the shower and sunshine, away, and still away, it rolls and roars, fierce and rapid, smooth and certain, and great works and massive bridges crossing up above, fall like a beam of shadow an inch broad, upon the eye, and then are lost. Away, and still away, onward and onward ever: glimpses of cottage-homes, of houses, mansions, rich estates, of husbandry and handicraft, of people, of old roads and paths that look deserted, small, and insignificant as they are left behind: and so they do, and what else is there but such glimpses, in the track of the indomitable monster, Death!", "바람과 햇빛을, 소나기와 햇살을 헤치며, 멀리, 또 멀리, 그것은 사납고 빠르게, 매끄럽고 확실하게 구르고 으르렁댄다. 그리고 위로 가로질러 가는 거대한 건축물과 육중한 다리들이 한 치 너비의 그림자 줄기처럼 눈앞에 떨어졌다가 이내 사라진다. 멀리, 또 멀리, 줄곧 앞으로 또 앞으로. 오두막집들, 가옥들, 대저택들, 부유한 영지들, 농경과 수공업, 사람들, 버려진 듯 작고 하찮아 보이며 뒤로 멀어져 가는 옛 길과 오솔길들—그렇게 그것들은 뒤로 멀어진다. 그리고 저 불굴의 괴물, 죽음의 자취 속에 그런 일별들 말고 또 무엇이 있겠는가!"]], [["Away, with a shriek, and a roar, and a rattle, plunging down into the earth again, and working on in such a storm of energy and perseverance, that amidst the darkness and whirlwind the motion seems reversed, and to tend furiously backward, until a ray of light upon the wet wall shows its surface flying past like a fierce stream.", "비명을 지르고, 으르렁대고, 덜컹거리며, 다시 땅속으로 곤두박질쳐 들어가, 그토록 격렬한 활력과 끈기의 폭풍 속에서 일해 나가니, 어둠과 회오리바람 속에서 그 움직임이 거꾸로 뒤집혀, 미친 듯이 뒤로 향하는 것처럼 보이다가, 젖은 벽 위에 한 줄기 빛이 비치면서 그 표면이 사나운 물줄기처럼 스쳐 지나가는 것을 드러낸다."], ["Away once more into the day, and through the day, with a shrill yell of exultation, roaring, rattling, tearing on, spurning everything with its dark breath, sometimes pausing for a minute where a crowd of faces are, that in a minute more are not; sometimes lapping water greedily, and before the spout at which it drinks has ceased to drip upon the ground, shrieking, roaring, rattling through the purple distance!", "다시 한번 대낮 속으로, 그리고 대낮을 가로질러, 의기양양한 날카로운 비명을 지르며, 으르렁대고 덜컹대며 마구 내달려, 그 검은 숨결로 모든 것을 떨쳐 버리고, 때로는 한 무리의 얼굴들이 있는 곳에서 잠시 멈추었다가—다음 순간이면 그 얼굴들은 거기 없다—때로는 물을 게걸스레 들이켜, 그것이 마시는 주둥이에서 아직 물방울이 땅에 떨어지기를 그치기도 전에, 비명을 지르고, 으르렁대고, 덜컹거리며 자줏빛 먼 곳을 뚫고 나아간다!"]], [["Louder and louder yet, it shrieks and cries as it comes tearing on resistless to the goal: and now its way, still like the way of Death, is strewn with ashes thickly.", "점점 더 크게, 그것은 거역할 수 없이 목표를 향해 마구 내달리며 비명을 지르고 울부짖는다. 그리고 이제 그 길은, 여전히 죽음의 길과 같이, 잿더미로 두텁게 뒤덮여 있다."], ["Everything around is blackened.", "주위의 모든 것이 시커멓게 그을려 있다."], ["There are dark pools of water, muddy lanes, and miserable habitations far below.", "시커먼 물웅덩이들, 진창길들, 그리고 저 아래 비참한 거처들이 있다."], ["There are jagged walls and falling houses close at hand, and through the battered roofs and broken windows, wretched rooms are seen, where want and fever hide themselves in many wretched shapes, while smoke and crowded gables, and distorted chimneys, and deformity of brick and mortar penning up deformity of mind and body, choke the murky distance.", "바로 곁에는 들쭉날쭉한 벽들과 무너져 가는 집들이 있고, 두들겨 맞은 지붕과 깨진 창문 너머로 비참한 방들이 보이는데, 거기서는 궁핍과 열병이 갖가지 비참한 형상으로 몸을 숨기고 있는 한편, 연기와 빽빽이 들어찬 박공들과 일그러진 굴뚝들, 그리고 정신과 육체의 기형을 가두어 두는 벽돌과 회반죽의 기형이 그 칙칙한 먼 곳을 틀어막고 있다."], ["As Mr Dombey looks out of his carriage window, it is never in his thoughts that the monster who has brought him there has let the light of day in on these things: not made or caused them.", "돔비 씨가 마차 창밖을 내다볼 때, 자기를 이곳으로 데려온 그 괴물이 바로 이런 것들 위로 대낮의 빛을 들여보낸 것이지 그것들을 만들거나 빚어낸 게 아니라는 생각은 결코 그의 머릿속에 떠오르지 않는다."], ["It was the journey’s fitting end, and might have been the end of everything; it was so ruinous and dreary.", "그곳은 그 여행에 어울리는 종착지였고, 또한 만물의 종착지일 수도 있었다. 그만큼 그곳은 황폐하고 음산했다."]], [["So, pursuing the one course of thought, he had the one relentless monster still before him.", "그리하여 그 한 가닥 생각을 좇으면서, 그는 여전히 그 무자비한 괴물 하나를 눈앞에 두고 있었다."], ["All things looked black, and cold, and deadly upon him, and he on them.", "모든 것이 그에게 검고, 차갑고, 죽음 같아 보였고, 그 역시 그것들에게 그러했다."], ["He found a likeness to his misfortune everywhere.", "그는 어디서나 자기 불행과의 닮은꼴을 찾아냈다."], ["There was a remorseless triumph going on about him, and it galled and stung him in his pride and jealousy, whatever form it took: though most of all when it divided with him the love and memory of his lost boy.", "그의 주위에서는 무자비한 승리가 진행되고 있었고, 그것은 어떤 형태를 띠든 그의 자존심과 시기심을 쓰리게 하고 찔러 댔다. 그러나 그 무엇보다도, 그것이 자기와 더불어 죽은 아들의 사랑과 기억을 나누어 가질 때가 가장 그러했다."]], [["There was a face—he had looked upon it, on the previous night, and it on him with eyes that read his soul, though they were dim with tears, and hidden soon behind two quivering hands—that often had attended him in fancy, on this ride.", "한 얼굴이 있었다—그가 전날 밤 들여다보았던, 그리고 그것이, 비록 눈물로 흐려져 있었고 곧 떨리는 두 손 뒤로 가려졌으나, 그의 영혼을 읽어 내는 두 눈으로 그를 들여다보았던 그 얼굴이—이 여정 내내 그의 상상 속에 자주 따라붙었다."], ["He had seen it, with the expression of last night, timidly pleading to him.", "그는 그것을, 어젯밤의 그 표정 그대로, 자기에게 겁먹은 듯 호소하는 모습으로 보았다."], ["It was not reproachful, but there was something of doubt, almost of hopeful incredulity in it, which, as he once more saw that fade away into a desolate certainty of his dislike, was like reproach.", "그것은 책망하는 빛은 아니었으나, 거기에는 어떤 망설임이, 거의 희망 어린 불신 같은 것이 깃들어 있었고, 그것이 자기에 대한 그의 혐오라는 황량한 확신으로 사그라드는 것을 그가 다시 한번 보았을 때, 그것은 책망과도 같았다."], ["It was a trouble to him to think of this face of Florence.", "이 플로렌스의 얼굴을 생각하는 것은 그에게 괴로움이었다."]], [["Because he felt any new compunction towards it? No.", "그것을 향해 어떤 새로운 양심의 가책을 느꼈기 때문일까?"], ["Because the feeling it awakened in him—of which he had had some old foreshadowing in older times—was full-formed now, and spoke out plainly, moving him too much, and threatening to grow too strong for his composure.", "아니었다. 그 얼굴이 그의 안에 일깨운 감정이—더 오래전 시절에 어떤 옛 전조를 그가 이미 느낀 바 있던 그 감정이—이제 온전히 모습을 갖추고 분명하게 입을 열어, 그를 너무도 흔들어 놓으며, 그의 평정을 압도할 만큼 너무 강해질 듯 위협했기 때문이었다."], ["Because the face was abroad, in the expression of defeat and persecution that seemed to encircle him like the air.", "그 얼굴이 바깥에, 마치 공기처럼 그를 에워싸는 듯한 패배와 박해의 표정 속에 떠돌고 있었기 때문이었다."], ["Because it barbed the arrow of that cruel and remorseless enemy on which his thoughts so ran, and put into its grasp a double-handed sword.", "그 얼굴이, 그의 생각이 그토록 줄곧 좇고 있던 그 잔인하고 무자비한 적의 화살에 가시를 박고, 그 손에 양손잡이 칼을 쥐여 주었기 때문이었다."], ["Because he knew full well, in his own breast, as he stood there, tinging the scene of transition before him with the morbid colours of his own mind, and making it a ruin and a picture of decay, instead of hopeful change, and promise of better things, that life had quite as much to do with his complainings as death.", "그가 거기 서서, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 변천의 풍경을 제 마음의 병든 빛깔로 물들이며, 그것을 희망찬 변화나 더 나은 것들의 약속이 아니라 폐허요 쇠락의 그림으로 만들고 있던 그때, 자기 가슴속에서 너무도 잘 알고 있었기 때문이었다—이 한탄들과 관련해서는 죽음 못지않게 삶에도 책임이 있다는 것을."], ["One child was gone, and one child left.", "한 아이는 떠나갔고, 한 아이는 남았다."], ["Why was the object of his hope removed instead of her?", "어찌하여 그가 그토록 희망을 걸었던 대상이, 그 애 대신 데려가지고 만 것인가?"]], [["The sweet, calm, gentle presence in his fancy, moved him to no reflection but that.", "그의 상상 속의 그 다정하고, 차분하고, 온화한 존재는 그에게 오직 그 생각만을 불러일으켰다."], ["She had been unwelcome to him from the first; she was an aggravation of his bitterness now.", "그 애는 처음부터 그에게 달갑지 않았다. 이제 그 애는 그의 쓰라림을 더욱 악화시키는 존재였다."], ["If his son had been his only child, and the same blow had fallen on him, it would have been heavy to bear; but infinitely lighter than now, when it might have fallen on her (whom he could have lost, or he believed it, without a pang), and had not.", "만약 자기 아들이 외동이었고 똑같은 타격이 자기에게 떨어졌다면, 그것은 견디기 무거웠으리라. 그러나 그것이 그 애에게 떨어질 수도 있었으나(그 애라면 잃어도—그는 그렇게 믿었다—한 점 아픔 없이 잃을 수 있었으련만) 그러지 않은 지금에 비하면, 한없이 가벼웠으리라."], ["Her loving and innocent face rising before him, had no softening or winning influence.", "그의 눈앞에 떠오르는 그 애의 사랑스럽고 순결한 얼굴은 그를 누그러뜨리거나 사로잡는 아무런 힘도 없었다."], ["He rejected the angel, and took up with the tormenting spirit crouching in his bosom.", "그는 그 천사를 물리치고, 자기 가슴속에 웅크린 그 괴롭히는 영을 받아들였다."], ["Her patience, goodness, youth, devotion, love, were as so many atoms in the ashes upon which he set his heel.", "그 애의 인내와, 착함과, 젊음과, 헌신과, 사랑은, 그가 발뒤꿈치로 짓밟는 잿더미 속의 무수한 티끌에 지나지 않았다."], ["He saw her image in the blight and blackness all around him, not irradiating but deepening the gloom.", "그는 자기를 둘러싼 그 시들음과 어둠 속에서 그 애의 형상을 보았으니, 그것은 그 음울함을 환히 밝히는 게 아니라 한층 깊게 했다."], ["More than once upon this journey, and now again as he stood pondering at this journey’s end, tracing figures in the dust with his stick, the thought came into his mind, what was there he could interpose between himself and it?", "이 여정에서 한 번 이상, 그리고 이제 이 여정의 끝에서 지팡이로 먼지 위에 형상을 그리며 골똘히 서 있는 지금 다시, 한 가지 생각이 그의 마음에 떠올랐다. 자기 자신과 그것 사이에 끼워 넣을 만한 것이 무엇이 있을까?"]], [["The Major, who had been blowing and panting all the way down, like another engine, and whose eye had often wandered from his newspaper to leer at the prospect, as if there were a procession of discomfited Miss Toxes pouring out in the smoke of the train, and flying away over the fields to hide themselves in any place of refuge, aroused his friends by informing him that the post-horses were harnessed and the carriage ready.", "소령은 또 하나의 기관차처럼 내려오는 내내 씩씩대고 헐떡거렸고, 그의 눈은 신문에서 자주 벗어나 바깥 경치를 곁눈질했는데, 마치 패주한 톡스 양들의 행렬이 열차의 연기 속에서 쏟아져 나와 들판 너머로 날아가 어디든 피난처에 몸을 숨기기라도 하는 양 보였던 것이다—그런 소령이, 역마들이 매여 있고 마차가 준비되었다고 알리며 자기 친구를 일깨웠다."]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\""], ["said the Major, rapping him on the arm with his cane, “don’t be thoughtful.", "소령이 지팡이로 그의 팔을 톡톡 치며 말했다, \"그렇게 생각에 잠기지 마시오."], ["It’s a bad habit, Old Joe, Sir, wouldn’t be as tough as you see him, if he had ever encouraged it.", "그건 나쁜 버릇이오. 늙은 조도, 선생, 만약 그런 걸 부추긴 적이 있었더라면 보시다시피 이렇게 질긴 사람은 못 됐을 거요."], ["You are too great a man, Dombey, to be thoughtful.", "당신은 너무 대단한 인물이오, 돔비, 생각에 잠겨 있기에는 말이오."], ["In your position, Sir, you’re far above that kind of thing.”", "당신의 지위에서는, 선생, 그런 종류의 일 따위는 한참 초월해 있는 거요.\""]], [["The Major even in his friendly remonstrances, thus consulting the dignity and honour of Mr Dombey, and showing a lively sense of their importance, Mr Dombey felt more than ever disposed to defer to a gentleman possessing so much good sense and such a well-regulated mind; accordingly he made an effort to listen to the Major’s stories, as they trotted along the turnpike road; and the Major, finding both the pace and the road a great deal better adapted to his conversational powers than the mode of travelling they had just relinquished, came out of his entertainment.", "이렇게 소령이 그 다정한 충고 속에서조차 돔비 씨의 위엄과 명예를 헤아리고 그 중요성에 대한 생생한 감각을 보여 주니, 돔비 씨는 그토록 풍부한 양식과 그토록 잘 다스려진 정신을 지닌 신사에게 그 어느 때보다도 마음을 기울이고 싶어졌다. 그래서 그는 유료 도로를 따라 빠르게 달리는 동안 소령의 이야기에 귀 기울이려 애를 썼다. 그리고 소령은 그 속도도 그 길도 자기들이 방금 버리고 온 여행 방식보다 자기 화술에 훨씬 잘 맞는다는 것을 알고는, 한껏 흥이 올라 입담을 펼쳤다."]], [["But still the Major, blunt and tough as he was, and as he so very often said he was, administered some palatable catering to his companion’s appetite.", "그러나 무뚝뚝하고 질긴 사내인 소령은, 그리고 그 스스로도 그렇게 자주 말하던 대로의 사내인 그는, 그러면서도 자기 길동무의 식욕에 맞는 입맛 당기는 음식을 얼마간 차려 냈다."], ["He related, or rather suffered it to escape him, accidentally, and as one might say, grudgingly and against his will, how there was great curiosity and excitement at the club, in regard of his friend Dombey.", "그는 자기 친구 돔비를 두고 클럽에 대단한 호기심과 흥분이 일고 있다는 것을, 들려주었다기보다는 오히려 우연인 양, 그리고 말하자면 마지못해 본의 아니게 흘러나오도록 내버려 두었다."], ["How he was suffocated with questions, Sir.", "자기가 질문에 어찌나 시달려 숨이 막힐 지경인지, 선생."], ["How old Joe Bagstock was a greater man than ever, there, on the strength of Dombey.", "거기서는 늙은 조 백스톡이 돔비 덕분에 그 어느 때보다 대단한 인물이 되어 있는지."], ["How they said, “Bagstock, your friend Dombey now, what is the view he takes of such and such a question?", "사람들이 어떻게 \"백스톡, 자네 친구 돔비 말일세, 이러이러한 문제에 대해 그가 어떤 견해를 갖고 있나?\""], ["Though, by the Rood, Sir,”", "하고 묻는지."], ["said the Major, with a broad stare, “how they discovered that J.", "\"그렇긴 한데, 십자가에 걸고 맹세컨대, 선생,\" 소령이 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 말했다, \"도대체 J."], ["B. ever came to know you, is a mystery!”", "B.가 어쩌다 당신을 알게 됐는지를 그들이 어떻게 알아냈는지는 수수께끼란 말이오!\""]], [["In this flow of spirits and conversation, only interrupted by his usual plethoric symptoms, and by intervals of lunch, and from time to time by some violent assault upon the Native, who wore a pair of ear-rings in his dark-brown ears, and on whom his European clothes sat with an outlandish impossibility of adjustment—being, of their own accord, and without any reference to the tailor’s art, long where they ought to be short, short where they ought to be long, tight where they ought to be loose, and loose where they ought to be tight—and to which he imparted a new grace, whenever the Major attacked him, by shrinking into them like a shrivelled nut, or a cold monkey—in this flow of spirits and conversation, the Major continued all day: so that when evening came on, and found them trotting through the green and leafy road near Leamington, the Major’s voice, what with talking and eating and chuckling and choking, appeared to be in the box under the rumble, or in some neighbouring hay-stack.", "이렇게 기분이 도도하고 말이 도도하게 흘러가는 가운데, 그것을 가로막는 것이라곤 그의 여느 다혈증 증세와, 이따금의 점심 시간과, 그리고 때때로 원주민에게 가하는 어떤 격렬한 공격뿐이었다. 그 원주민은 짙은 갈색 귀에 귀고리 한 쌍을 달고 있었고, 그의 몸에 걸친 유럽식 옷은 어찌나 괴상하게 맞지 않던지—제멋대로, 그리고 재단술 따위는 아랑곳없이, 짧아야 할 데서 길고, 길어야 할 데서 짧고, 헐거워야 할 데서 죄고, 죄어야 할 데서 헐거웠다—소령이 그를 공격할 때마다 그는 쪼그라든 호두처럼, 혹은 추워하는 원숭이처럼 그 옷 속으로 움츠러들어 거기에 새로운 운치를 더했다—이렇게 기분과 입담이 흘러가는 가운데, 소령은 온종일을 보냈다."], ["Nor did the Major improve it at the Royal Hotel, where rooms and dinner had been ordered, and where he so oppressed his organs of speech by eating and drinking, that when he retired to bed he had no voice at all, except to cough with, and could only make himself intelligible to the dark servant by gasping at him.", "그리하여 저녁이 와서 그들이 리밍턴 가까운 푸르고 잎 무성한 길을 빠르게 달리고 있을 때, 소령의 목소리는, 떠들고 먹고 낄낄대고 켁켁댄 끝에, 뒷좌석 밑의 짐칸에 있거나 어디 근처 건초더미 속에 있는 것처럼 들렸다. 또한 소령은 방과 만찬을 미리 주문해 둔 로열 호텔에서도 자기 목소리를 회복하지 못했으니, 거기서 먹고 마시느라 자기 발성 기관을 어찌나 억눌렀던지, 잠자리에 들 무렵에는 기침할 때 말고는 목소리가 전혀 나오지 않았고, 검은 하인에게 자기 뜻을 알릴 수 있는 방법이라곤 그를 향해 헐떡거리는 것뿐이었다."]], [["He not only rose next morning, however, like a giant refreshed, but conducted himself, at breakfast like a giant refreshing.", "그러나 그는 이튿날 아침, 마치 원기를 되찾은 거인처럼 자리에서 일어났을 뿐 아니라, 아침 식사 자리에서는 거인이 원기를 되찾아 가듯 처신했다."], ["At this meal they arranged their daily habits.", "이 식사 자리에서 그들은 매일의 생활 방식을 짰다."], ["The Major was to take the responsibility of ordering everything to eat and drink; and they were to have a late breakfast together every morning, and a late dinner together every day.", "소령이 먹고 마실 모든 것을 주문하는 책임을 맡기로 했다. 그리고 그들은 매일 아침 함께 늦은 아침을, 매일 함께 늦은 만찬을 들기로 했다."], ["Mr Dombey would prefer remaining in his own room, or walking in the country by himself, on that first day of their sojourn at Leamington; but next morning he would be happy to accompany the Major to the Pump-room, and about the town.", "돔비 씨는 리밍턴 체류 첫날만큼은 자기 방에 머물거나 혼자 시골을 거니는 편이 좋겠다고 했다. 그러나 이튿날 아침에는 기꺼이 소령과 더불어 약수터에도, 시내 곳곳에도 함께 가겠노라고 했다."], ["So they parted until dinner-time.", "그래서 그들은 만찬 시간까지 헤어졌다."], ["Mr Dombey retired to nurse his wholesome thoughts in his own way.", "돔비 씨는 자기 식대로 자기의 건전한 생각을 가다듬으러 물러갔다."], ["The Major, attended by the Native carrying a camp-stool, a great-coat, and an umbrella, swaggered up and down through all the public places: looking into subscription books to find out who was there, looking up old ladies by whom he was much admired, reporting J.", "소령은 야영용 접의자와 외투와 우산을 든 원주민을 거느리고, 온갖 공공장소를 거들먹거리며 오르내렸다. 누가 와 있는지 알아보려고 회원 명부를 들여다보고, 자기를 무척 흠모하는 노부인들을 찾아다니며, J."], ["B. tougher than ever, and puffing his rich friend Dombey wherever he went.", "B.가 그 어느 때보다 질겨졌다고 떠벌리고, 가는 곳마다 자기의 부유한 친구 돔비를 추켜세웠다."], ["There never was a man who stood by a friend more staunchly than the Major, when in puffing him, he puffed himself.", "친구를 추켜세우면서 동시에 제 자신을 추켜세울 때만큼, 소령보다 더 충실하게 친구 곁을 지키는 사내는 없었다."]], [["It was surprising how much new conversation the Major had to let off at dinner-time, and what occasion he gave Mr Dombey to admire his social qualities.", "소령이 만찬 시간에 풀어놓을 새로운 이야깃거리를 얼마나 많이 갖고 있었는지, 그리고 돔비 씨에게 그의 사교적 자질을 감탄할 기회를 얼마나 많이 주었는지는 놀라운 일이었다."], ["At breakfast next morning, he knew the contents of the latest newspapers received; and mentioned several subjects in connexion with them, on which his opinion had recently been sought by persons of such power and might, that they were only to be obscurely hinted at.", "이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서 그는 가장 최근에 도착한 신문들의 내용을 꿰고 있었다. 그리고 그것들과 관련된 여러 화제를 언급했는데, 그 화제들에 대해 자기 의견을, 너무도 권세 있고 위세 있는 인물들이 최근에 구해 와서, 그저 어렴풋이 암시하는 수밖에 없다는 식이었다."], ["Mr Dombey, who had been so long shut up within himself, and who had rarely, at any time, overstepped the enchanted circle within which the operations of Dombey and Son were conducted, began to think this an improvement on his solitary life; and in place of excusing himself for another day, as he had thought of doing when alone, walked out with the Major arm-in-arm.", "그토록 오랫동안 제 안에 틀어박혀 지내 왔고, 어느 때든 돔비와 아들의 사업이 영위되는 그 마법의 동그라미 밖으로 좀처럼 발을 내디딘 적이 없던 돔비 씨는, 이것을 자기 고독한 삶에 견주어 한결 나은 것으로 여기기 시작했다. 그래서 혼자 있을 때 생각했던 것처럼 또 하루를 핑계 대고 빠지는 대신, 소령과 팔짱을 끼고 밖으로 나섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXI. New Faces", "# CHAPTER XXI. New Faces"]], [["The MAJOR, more blue-faced and staring—more over-ripe, as it were, than ever—and giving vent, every now and then, to one of the horse’s coughs, not so much of necessity as in a spontaneous explosion of importance, walked arm-in-arm with Mr Dombey up the sunny side of the way, with his cheeks swelling over his tight stock, his legs majestically wide apart, and his great head wagging from side to side, as if he were remonstrating within himself for being such a captivating object.", "소령은 그 어느 때보다 얼굴이 더 푸르뎅뎅하고 눈을 부라리며—말하자면 그 어느 때보다 더 농익은 모습으로—이따금 그 말[馬] 같은 기침을, 꼭 필요해서라기보다는 자기 중요성이 저절로 터져 나오는 양 한 번씩 뱉어 내면서, 돔비 씨와 팔짱을 끼고 길의 양지바른 쪽을 따라 걸었다. 빳빳한 목깃 위로 두 볼이 불룩 부풀고, 다리는 위엄 있게 한껏 벌리고, 커다란 머리는 이쪽저쪽으로 흔들거렸는데, 마치 자기가 이토록 매혹적인 존재인 것을 두고 속으로 항변이라도 하는 듯했다."], ["They had not walked many yards, before the Major encountered somebody he knew, nor many yards farther before the Major encountered somebody else he knew, but he merely shook his fingers at them as he passed, and led Mr Dombey on: pointing out the localities as they went, and enlivening the walk with any current scandal suggested by them.", "몇 야드 채 걷기도 전에 소령은 아는 사람과 마주쳤고, 다시 몇 야드 더 가기도 전에 또 다른 아는 사람과 마주쳤지만, 그는 지나가면서 그저 손가락을 까딱여 보일 뿐 돔비 씨를 계속 이끌고 갔다. 가는 길에 이런저런 장소를 가리켜 보이고, 그것들이 떠올려 주는 요즘의 추문들로 산책에 흥을 돋우면서."]], [["In this manner the Major and Mr Dombey were walking arm-in-arm, much to their own satisfaction, when they beheld advancing towards them, a wheeled chair, in which a lady was seated, indolently steering her carriage by a kind of rudder in front, while it was propelled by some unseen power in the rear.", "이런 식으로 소령과 돔비 씨가 서로 매우 흡족해하며 팔짱을 끼고 걷고 있을 때, 두 사람은 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 바퀴 달린 의자 하나를 보았다. 그 안에는 한 부인이 앉아, 앞쪽에 달린 일종의 키 같은 것으로 나른하게 제 수레를 조종하고 있었고, 의자는 뒤편의 보이지 않는 힘에 의해 밀려오고 있었다."], ["Although the lady was not young, she was very blooming in the face—quite rosy—and her dress and attitude were perfectly juvenile.", "부인은 젊지 않았지만 얼굴이 매우 화사하게 피어 있었고—아주 발그레했으며—옷차림과 자세는 완벽하게 어려 보였다."], ["Walking by the side of the chair, and carrying her gossamer parasol with a proud and weary air, as if so great an effort must be soon abandoned and the parasol dropped, sauntered a much younger lady, very handsome, very haughty, very wilful, who tossed her head and drooped her eyelids, as though, if there were anything in all the world worth looking into, save a mirror, it certainly was not the earth or sky.", "그 의자 옆에서, 마치 그토록 대단한 노력은 곧 그만두어 양산을 떨어뜨릴 수밖에 없다는 듯, 자기의 얇디얇은 양산을 자랑스럽고도 지친 태도로 들고 한가로이 거니는 이는 훨씬 젊은 부인이었다. 매우 아름답고, 매우 거만하며, 매우 제멋대로인 그 여인은 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 눈꺼풀을 내리깔았는데, 마치 거울을 빼고 이 세상에 들여다볼 가치가 있는 무언가가 있다 한들 그것이 결코 땅이나 하늘은 아니라는 듯했다."]], [["“Why, what the devil have we here, Sir!”", "\"아니, 이거 도대체 누가 오시는 겁니까, 선생!\""], ["cried the Major, stopping as this little cavalcade drew near.", "이 작은 행렬이 가까워지자 소령이 걸음을 멈추며 외쳤다."]], [["“My dearest Edith!”", "\"아이참, 내 사랑하는 에디스!\""], ["drawled the lady in the chair, “Major Bagstock!”", "의자 안의 부인이 느릿느릿 말했다, \"백스톡 소령님!\""]], [["The Major no sooner heard the voice, than he relinquished Mr Dombey’s arm, darted forward, took the hand of the lady in the chair and pressed it to his lips.", "소령은 그 목소리를 듣자마자 돔비 씨의 팔을 놓아 버리고 앞으로 달려 나가, 의자 안 부인의 손을 잡고 자기 입술에 가져다 댔다."], ["With no less gallantry, the Major folded both his gloves upon his heart, and bowed low to the other lady.", "못지않은 정중함으로 소령은 두 손에 낀 장갑을 가슴에 포개어 얹고, 다른 부인에게도 깊이 절했다."], ["And now, the chair having stopped, the motive power became visible in the shape of a flushed page pushing behind, who seemed to have in part outgrown and in part out-pushed his strength, for when he stood upright he was tall, and wan, and thin, and his plight appeared the more forlorn from his having injured the shape of his hat, by butting at the carriage with his head to urge it forward, as is sometimes done by elephants in Oriental countries.", "그러는 사이 의자가 멈추자, 그것을 밀던 동력원이 모습을 드러냈는데, 뒤에서 의자를 밀던 얼굴이 벌게진 시동이었다. 그는 한편으로는 키가 너무 웃자라고 한편으로는 힘이 다 빠진 듯이 보였으니, 똑바로 서니 키가 크고 핼쑥하며 비쩍 말랐고, 그 처지가 한층 처량해 보였던 것은 동방 나라들에서 코끼리들이 더러 그러듯 머리로 수레를 들이받아 앞으로 밀어 댄 탓에 모자의 모양을 망가뜨려 놓은 까닭이었다."]], [["“Joe Bagstock,”", "\"조 백스톡은,\""], ["said the Major to both ladies, “is a proud and happy man for the rest of his life.”", "소령이 두 부인 모두에게 말했다, \"남은 평생 자랑스럽고 행복한 사람입니다.\""]], [["“You false creature!”", "\"이 거짓말쟁이 양반!\""], ["said the old lady in the chair, insipidly.", "의자 안의 노부인이 맥없이 말했다."], ["“Where do you come from?", "\"어디서 오신 거예요?"], ["I can’t bear you.”", "정말 못 봐 주겠네.\""]], [["“Then suffer old Joe to present a friend, Ma’am,”", "\"그러시면 늙은 조가 친구를 한 사람 소개하게 해 주시지요, 마님,\""], ["said the Major, promptly, “as a reason for being tolerated. Mr Dombey, Mrs Skewton.”", "소령이 즉각 말했다, \"그래야 봐주실 명분이 생길 테니까요."], ["The lady in the chair was gracious.", "돔비 씨, 이쪽은 스큐턴 부인이십니다.\""], ["“Mr Dombey, Mrs Granger.”", "의자 안의 부인이 우아하게 응했다."], ["The lady with the parasol was faintly conscious of Mr Dombey’s taking off his hat, and bowing low.", "\"돔비 씨, 이쪽은 그레인저 부인이십니다.\" 양산을 든 부인은 돔비 씨가 모자를 벗고 깊이 절하는 것을 어렴풋이 의식했다."], ["“I am delighted, Sir,”", "\"이런 기회를 얻게 되어,\""], ["said the Major, “to have this opportunity.”", "소령이 말했다, \"참으로 기쁩니다, 선생.\""]], [["The Major seemed in earnest, for he looked at all the three, and leered in his ugliest manner.", "소령은 진심인 듯 보였으니, 세 사람을 모두 둘러보며 그 가장 못생긴 방식으로 곁눈질을 했기 때문이다."]], [["“Mrs Skewton, Dombey,”", "\"스큐턴 부인께서는, 돔비,\""], ["said the Major, “makes havoc in the heart of old Josh.”", "소령이 말했다, \"늙은 조시의 가슴에 큰 파란을 일으키신답니다.\""]], [["Mr Dombey signified that he didn’t wonder at it.", "돔비 씨는 그럴 만도 하다는 뜻을 비쳤다."]], [["“You perfidious goblin,”", "\"이 음흉한 도깨비 양반,\""], ["said the lady in the chair, “have done!", "의자 안의 부인이 말했다, \"그만하세요!"], ["How long have you been here, bad man?”", "여기 온 지는 얼마나 됐어요, 못된 양반?\""]], [["“One day,”", "\"하루 됐습니다,\""], ["replied the Major.", "소령이 대답했다."]], [["“And can you be a day, or even a minute,”", "\"한데 어떻게 하루를, 아니 단 일 분이라도,\""], ["returned the lady, slightly settling her false curls and false eyebrows with her fan, and showing her false teeth, set off by her false complexion, “in the garden of what’s-its-name.”", "부인이 부채로 자기 가짜 곱슬머리와 가짜 눈썹을 살짝 매만지고, 가짜 안색으로 돋보이게 한 가짜 이를 드러내 보이며 응수했다, \"그 뭐라더라 하는 곳의 정원에서 견딜 수가—\""]], [["“Eden, I suppose, Mama,”", "\"에덴 말씀이시겠죠, 엄마,\""], ["interrupted the younger lady, scornfully.", "젊은 부인이 경멸하듯 끼어들었다."]], [["“My dear Edith,”", "\"내 사랑하는 에디스,\""], ["said the other, “I cannot help it.", "다른 부인이 말했다, \"어쩔 수가 없구나."], ["I never can remember those frightful names—without having your whole Soul and Being inspired by the sight of Nature; by the perfume,”", "그 끔찍한 이름들을 도무지 기억하질 못하니—자연의 광경에 온 영혼과 존재가 온통 영감으로 차오르지 않고서야, 그 향기에,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, rustling a handkerchief that was faint and sickly with essences, “of her artless breath, you creature!”", "스큐턴 부인은 향수 때문에 은은하고도 들척지근한 손수건을 부스럭거리며 말했다, \"그 꾸밈없는 숨결의 향기에 말이에요, 이 양반아!\""]], [["The discrepancy between Mrs Skewton’s fresh enthusiasm of words, and forlornly faded manner, was hardly less observable than that between her age, which was about seventy, and her dress, which would have been youthful for twenty-seven.", "스큐턴 부인의 싱싱한 말의 열정과 처량하게 시든 태도 사이의 불일치는, 일흔쯤 되는 그녀의 나이와 스물일곱에게도 어려 보였을 옷차림 사이의 불일치 못지않게 눈에 띄었다."], ["Her attitude in the wheeled chair (which she never varied) was one in which she had been taken in a barouche, some fifty years before, by a then fashionable artist who had appended to his published sketch the name of Cleopatra: in consequence of a discovery made by the critics of the time, that it bore an exact resemblance to that Princess as she reclined on board her galley.", "바퀴 달린 의자 안에서의 그 자세는(그녀는 결코 그것을 바꾸지 않았다) 한 오십 년 전, 당시 유행하던 한 화가가 사륜마차에 탄 그녀의 모습을 그렸을 때 취한 자세였다. 그 화가는 발표한 그 스케치에 '클레오파트라'라는 이름을 붙였는데, 그것이 갤리선 갑판에 비스듬히 기댄 그 여왕의 모습과 똑같이 닮았다는 것을 당대의 비평가들이 발견한 까닭이었다."], ["Mrs Skewton was a beauty then, and bucks threw wine-glasses over their heads by dozens in her honour.", "그때 스큐턴 부인은 미인이었고, 멋쟁이들은 그녀를 기려 포도주잔을 머리 너머로 수십 개씩 던졌다."], ["The beauty and the barouche had both passed away, but she still preserved the attitude, and for this reason expressly, maintained the wheeled chair and the butting page: there being nothing whatever, except the attitude, to prevent her from walking.", "미모도 사륜마차도 둘 다 사라져 버렸지만, 그녀는 여전히 그 자세를 간직했으니, 바로 그 이유 하나만으로 바퀴 달린 의자와 들이받는 시동을 두고 있었던 것이다. 그 자세를 빼고는 그녀가 걷지 못할 이유가 전혀 없었으니 말이다."]], [["“Mr Dombey is devoted to Nature, I trust?”", "\"돔비 씨는 자연에 헌신하시는 분이겠죠?\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, settling her diamond brooch.", "스큐턴 부인이 다이아몬드 브로치를 매만지며 말했다."], ["And by the way, she chiefly lived upon the reputation of some diamonds, and her family connexions.", "그런데 말이 나온 김에 덧붙이자면, 그녀는 주로 어떤 다이아몬드들의 명성과 자기 집안의 인척 관계에 기대어 살아가고 있었다."]], [["“My friend Dombey, Ma’am,”", "\"제 친구 돔비는, 마님,\""], ["returned the Major, “may be devoted to her in secret, but a man who is paramount in the greatest city in the universe—”", "소령이 응수했다, \"남몰래 자연에 헌신할 수는 있겠지요, 그러나 우주에서 가장 위대한 도시에서 으뜸가는 사람으로서—\""]], [["“No one can be a stranger,”", "\"돔비 씨의 어마어마한 영향력을,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “to Mr Dombey’s immense influence.”", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"모르는 사람은 아무도 없지요.\""]], [["As Mr Dombey acknowledged the compliment with a bend of his head, the younger lady glancing at him, met his eyes.", "돔비 씨가 고개를 숙여 그 찬사에 답하자, 젊은 부인이 그를 흘끗 보다가 그의 눈과 마주쳤다."]], [["“You reside here, Madam?”", "\"부인께서는 이곳에 거주하십니까?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, addressing her.", "돔비 씨가 그녀에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“No, we have been to a great many places.", "\"아뇨, 저희는 여러 곳을 다녔어요."], ["To Harrogate and Scarborough, and into Devonshire.", "해러게이트와 스카버러, 그리고 데번셔에도요."], ["We have been visiting, and resting here and there.", "여기저기 방문하고, 머물러 쉬고 했답니다."], ["Mama likes change.”", "엄마가 변화를 좋아하셔서요.\""]], [["“Edith of course does not,”", "\"에디스는 물론 안 그렇지만요,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, with a ghastly archness.", "스큐턴 부인이 섬뜩하리만치 천연덕스럽게 말했다."]], [["“I have not found that there is any change in such places,”", "\"그런 곳들에 무슨 변화가 있다고는 느껴 보지 못했어요,\""], ["was the answer, delivered with supreme indifference.", "더없이 무심한 어조로 나온 대답이었다."]], [["“They libel me. There is only one change, Mr Dombey,”", "\"이 애가 저를 모함하네요."], ["observed Mrs Skewton, with a mincing sigh, “for which I really care, and that I fear I shall never be permitted to enjoy.", "제가 정말로 마음 쓰는 변화는, 돔비 씨, 단 하나뿐이에요,\" 스큐턴 부인이 점잔 빼는 한숨을 쉬며 말했다, \"그런데 그것만은 끝내 누리지 못하게 될까 봐 두렵답니다."], ["People cannot spare one.", "사람들이 저를 놓아주질 않거든요."], ["But seclusion and contemplation are my what-his-name—”", "하지만 은둔과 명상이야말로 제 뭐라더라 하는—\""]], [["“If you mean Paradise, Mama, you had better say so, to render yourself intelligible,”", "\"낙원을 말씀하시는 거면, 엄마, 알아듣기 쉽게 그냥 그렇게 말씀하시는 편이 낫겠어요,\""], ["said the younger lady.", "젊은 부인이 말했다."]], [["“My dearest Edith,”", "\"내 사랑하는 에디스,\""], ["returned Mrs Skewton, “you know that I am wholly dependent upon you for those odious names.", "스큐턴 부인이 응수했다, \"그 끔찍한 이름들은 죄다 너한테 의지하는 거 알잖니."], ["I assure you, Mr Dombey, Nature intended me for an Arcadian.", "정말이지, 돔비 씨, 자연은 저를 아르카디아 사람으로 점지했나 봐요."], ["I am thrown away in society.", "저는 사교계에서는 버려진 존재예요."], ["Cows are my passion.", "소[牛]가 제 열정이랍니다."], ["What I have ever sighed for, has been to retreat to a Swiss farm, and live entirely surrounded by cows—and china.”", "제가 늘 한숨지으며 바라 온 것은, 스위스의 농장으로 물러나 온통 소들에—그리고 도자기에 둘러싸여 사는 거였어요.\""]], [["This curious association of objects, suggesting a remembrance of the celebrated bull who got by mistake into a crockery shop, was received with perfect gravity by Mr Dombey, who intimated his opinion that Nature was, no doubt, a very respectable institution.", "이 기묘한 사물의 연결은, 실수로 도자기 가게에 들어간 그 유명한 황소의 기억을 떠올리게 했으나, 돔비 씨는 더없이 진지하게 그것을 받아들이며, 자연이란 의심할 바 없이 매우 존경할 만한 제도라는 견해를 내비쳤다."]], [["“What I want,”", "\"제가 원하는 건,\""], ["drawled Mrs Skewton, pinching her shrivelled throat, “is heart.”", "스큐턴 부인이 쪼글쪼글한 목을 꼬집으며 느릿느릿 말했다, \"마음이에요.\""], ["It was frightfully true in one sense, if not in that in which she used the phrase.", "그녀가 그 말을 쓴 뜻으로는 아닐지언정, 다른 한 가지 뜻으로는 그것은 무섭도록 사실이었다."], ["“What I want, is frankness, confidence, less conventionality, and freer play of soul.", "\"제가 원하는 건 솔직함, 신뢰, 격식의 줄어듦, 그리고 영혼의 더 자유로운 발휘예요."], ["We are so dreadfully artificial.”", "우리는 정말이지 끔찍하게 인위적이거든요.\""]], [["We were, indeed.", "실로 그러했다."]], [["“In short,”", "\"한마디로,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “I want Nature everywhere.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"저는 어디서나 자연을 원해요."], ["It would be so extremely charming.”", "그러면 더없이 매혹적일 텐데.\""]], [["“Nature is inviting us away now, Mama, if you are ready,”", "\"자연이 지금 우리를 불러내고 있어요, 엄마, 준비되셨으면요,\""], ["said the younger lady, curling her handsome lip.", "젊은 부인이 그 아름다운 입술을 비틀며 말했다."], ["At this hint, the wan page, who had been surveying the party over the top of the chair, vanished behind it, as if the ground had swallowed him up.", "이 암시에 의자 등받이 너머로 일행을 살피고 있던 핼쑥한 시동이, 마치 땅이 그를 집어삼키기라도 한 듯 의자 뒤로 사라졌다."]], [["“Stop a moment, Withers!”", "\"잠깐만, 위더스!\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, as the chair began to move; calling to the page with all the languid dignity with which she had called in days of yore to a coachman with a wig, cauliflower nosegay, and silk stockings.", "의자가 움직이기 시작하자 스큐턴 부인이 시동을 부르며 말했다. 가발과 꽃양배추 모양 꽃다발과 비단 양말을 갖춘 마부를 옛 시절에 부르던 그 나른한 위엄을 고스란히 담은 부름이었다."], ["“Where are you staying, abomination?”", "\"어디 머무시나요, 이 못된 양반?\""]], [["The Major was staying at the Royal Hotel, with his friend Dombey.", "소령은 자기 친구 돔비와 함께 로열 호텔에 머물고 있었다."]], [["“You may come and see us any evening when you are good,”", "\"착하게 구실 때는 언제든 저녁에 저희를 보러 오셔도 좋아요,\""], ["lisped Mrs Skewton.", "스큐턴 부인이 혀짤배기소리로 말했다."], ["“If Mr Dombey will honour us, we shall be happy.", "\"돔비 씨께서 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 저희야 기쁘지요."], ["Withers, go on!”", "위더스, 가자!\""]], [["The Major again pressed to his blue lips the tips of the fingers that were disposed on the ledge of the wheeled chair with careful carelessness, after the Cleopatra model: and Mr Dombey bowed.", "소령은 클레오파트라 모형을 본떠 조심스러운 무심함으로 바퀴 달린 의자의 가장자리에 얹힌 손가락 끝에 다시 한번 자기 푸른 입술을 갖다 댔고, 돔비 씨는 절을 했다."], ["The elder lady honoured them both with a very gracious smile and a girlish wave of her hand; the younger lady with the very slightest inclination of her head that common courtesy allowed.", "손위 부인은 매우 우아한 미소와 소녀 같은 손짓으로 두 사람에게 영광을 베풀었고, 손아래 부인은 통상의 예의가 허락하는 가장 미미한 고갯짓으로 답했다."]], [["The last glimpse of the wrinkled face of the mother, with that patched colour on it which the sun made infinitely more haggard and dismal than any want of colour could have been, and of the proud beauty of the daughter with her graceful figure and erect deportment, engendered such an involuntary disposition on the part of both the Major and Mr Dombey to look after them, that they both turned at the same moment.", "어머니의 주름진 얼굴, 그 위에 덧칠한 빛깔이 햇빛 아래서 차라리 빛깔이 없을 때보다 한없이 더 초췌하고 음울해 보이던 그 얼굴과, 우아한 자태와 꼿꼿한 자세를 지닌 딸의 도도한 미모, 그 마지막 모습은 소령과 돔비 씨 양쪽에게 그들을 뒤돌아보고 싶은 충동을 어찌나 무심결에 자아냈던지, 두 사람은 같은 순간에 동시에 돌아보았다."], ["The Page, nearly as much aslant as his own shadow, was toiling after the chair, uphill, like a slow battering-ram; the top of Cleopatra’s bonnet was fluttering in exactly the same corner to the inch as before; and the Beauty, loitering by herself a little in advance, expressed in all her elegant form, from head to foot, the same supreme disregard of everything and everybody.", "시동은 거의 제 그림자만큼이나 비스듬히 기운 채, 느릿한 공성추처럼 언덕 위로 의자를 힘겹게 밀어 올리고 있었고, 클레오파트라의 보닛 꼭대기는 조금 전과 정확히 한 치도 다르지 않은 같은 구석에서 나풀거리고 있었으며, 미인은 혼자 조금 앞서 한가로이 거닐면서, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그 우아한 자태 전체로 모든 것과 모든 이를 향한 똑같은 지극한 무관심을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“I tell you what, Sir,”", "\"내 말 좀 들어 보십시오, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, as they resumed their walk again.", "두 사람이 다시 걸음을 옮기면서 소령이 말했다."], ["“If Joe Bagstock were a younger man, there’s not a woman in the world whom he’d prefer for Mrs Bagstock to that woman.", "\"조 백스톡이 좀 더 젊은 사람이었다면, 백스톡 부인으로 저 여인보다 더 마음에 둘 여자는 이 세상에 없을 겁니다."], ["By George, Sir!”", "어이쿠, 선생!\""], ["said the Major, “she’s superb!”", "소령이 말했다, \"참으로 빼어난 여인이지요!\""]], [["“Do you mean the daughter?”", "\"딸 말이오?\""], ["inquired Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Is Joey B. a turnip, Dombey,”", "\"조이 비가 순무 대가립니까, 돔비,\""], ["said the Major, “that he should mean the mother?”", "소령이 말했다, \"어머니를 두고 그런 말을 하게요?\""]], [["“You were complimentary to the mother,”", "\"당신은 어머니에게 찬사를 보냈잖소,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 응수했다."]], [["“An ancient flame, Sir,”", "\"옛 연인이지요, 선생,\" 백스톡 소령이 낄낄 웃었다."], ["chuckled Major Bagstock.", "\"지독하게 오래된 연인이에요."], ["“Devilish ancient. I humour her.”", "제가 그저 비위를 맞춰 드리는 겁니다.\""]], [["“She impresses me as being perfectly genteel,”", "\"내게는 더없이 기품 있는 분으로 보이던데,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Genteel, Sir,”", "\"기품이라뇨, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, stopping short, and staring in his companion’s face.", "소령이 우뚝 멈춰 서서 동행의 얼굴을 빤히 쳐다보며 말했다."], ["“The Honourable Mrs Skewton, Sir, is sister to the late Lord Feenix, and aunt to the present Lord.", "\"스큐턴 부인 마님께서는, 선생, 작고하신 피닉스 경의 누이시고, 지금의 피닉스 경의 고모십니다."], ["The family are not wealthy—they’re poor, indeed—and she lives upon a small jointure; but if you come to blood, Sir!”", "그 집안은 부유하진 않아요—사실 가난하지요—그래서 부인은 얼마 안 되는 과부 급여로 살아갑니다. 하지만 혈통을 따지자면, 선생!\""], ["The Major gave a flourish with his stick and walked on again, in despair of being able to say what you came to, if you came to that.", "소령은 지팡이를 한 번 휘두르고는, 혈통을 따지자면 무엇이 나온다는 건지 도무지 말로 표현할 길이 없어 절망하며 다시 걸어 나갔다."]], [["“You addressed the daughter, I observed,”", "\"내가 보니 당신은 딸을 그레인저 부인이라고 부릅디다,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, after a short pause, “as Mrs Granger.”", "돔비 씨가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했다."]], [["“Edith Skewton, Sir,”", "\"에디스 스큐턴이,\""], ["returned the Major, stopping short again, and punching a mark in the ground with his cane, to represent her, “married (at eighteen) Granger of Ours;”", "소령이 다시 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 그녀를 표시하느라 지팡이로 땅에 자국을 콕 찍으며 응수했다, \"(열여덟에) 우리 연대의 그레인저와 결혼했지요.\""], ["whom the Major indicated by another punch.", "소령은 다시 한번 콕 찍어 그를 나타냈다."], ["“Granger, Sir,”", "\"그레인저는, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, tapping the last ideal portrait, and rolling his head emphatically, “was Colonel of Ours; a de-vilish handsome fellow, Sir, of forty-one.", "소령이 그 마지막 가상의 초상을 톡톡 두드리고 머리를 힘주어 끄덕이며 말했다, \"우리 연대의 대령이었습니다. 마흔하나의 지독하게 잘생긴 사내였지요, 선생."], ["He died, Sir, in the second year of his marriage.”", "그는, 선생, 결혼한 지 두 해째에 죽었습니다.\""], ["The Major ran the representative of the deceased Granger through and through the body with his walking-stick, and went on again, carrying his stick over his shoulder.", "소령은 고인이 된 그레인저를 나타내는 표시를 지팡이로 몸통을 푹 찔러 관통시키고는, 지팡이를 어깨에 둘러메고 다시 걸어 나갔다."]], [["“How long is this ago?”", "\"그게 얼마나 오래전 일이오?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey, making another halt.", "돔비 씨가 또 한 번 걸음을 멈추며 물었다."]], [["“Edith Granger, Sir,”", "\"에디스 그레인저는, 선생,\""], ["replied the Major, shutting one eye, putting his head on one side, passing his cane into his left hand, and smoothing his shirt-frill with his right, “is, at this present time, not quite thirty.", "소령이 한쪽 눈을 감고 머리를 한쪽으로 기울이며, 지팡이를 왼손으로 옮겨 쥐고 오른손으로는 셔츠의 주름 장식을 매만지며 대답했다, \"지금 이 순간 채 서른이 안 됐습니다."], ["And damme, Sir,”", "그리고 제기랄, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, shouldering his stick once more, and walking on again, “she’s a peerless woman!”", "소령이 다시 지팡이를 어깨에 둘러메고 다시 걸어 나가며 말했다, \"그녀는 둘도 없는 여인이지요!\""]], [["“Was there any family?”", "\"자식은 있었소?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey presently.", "이윽고 돔비 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, Sir,”", "\"있었지요, 선생,\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“There was a boy.”", "\"사내아이가 하나 있었습니다.\""]], [["Mr Dombey’s eyes sought the ground, and a shade came over his face.", "돔비 씨의 두 눈이 땅으로 향했고, 얼굴에 그늘이 드리웠다."]], [["“Who was drowned, Sir,” pursued the Major.", "\"그 애는 물에 빠져 죽었지요, 선생,\""], ["“When a child of four or five years old.”", "소령이 이어 말했다. \"네댓 살 어린아이였을 때요.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, raising his head.", "돔비 씨가 고개를 들며 말했다."]], [["“By the upsetting of a boat in which his nurse had no business to have put him,”", "\"유모가 도무지 태워서는 안 될 배에 그 애를 태웠다가 배가 뒤집어진 거지요,\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“That’s his history.", "\"그게 그 애의 사연입니다."], ["Edith Granger is Edith Granger still; but if tough old Joey B., Sir, were a little younger and a little richer, the name of that immortal paragon should be Bagstock.”", "에디스 그레인저는 여전히 에디스 그레인저예요. 하지만 이 질긴 늙은 조이 비가, 선생, 좀 더 젊고 좀 더 부유했더라면, 저 불멸의 본보기 같은 여인의 이름은 백스톡이 되었을 겁니다.\""]], [["The Major heaved his shoulders, and his cheeks, and laughed more like an over-fed Mephistopheles than ever, as he said the words.", "소령은 두 어깨와 두 볼을 들썩이며, 그 말을 하면서 그 어느 때보다도 배불리 먹은 메피스토펠레스처럼 웃었다."]], [["“Provided the lady made no objection, I suppose?”", "\"부인이 거절하지 않았다는 전제하에서겠지요?\""], ["said Mr Dombey coldly.", "돔비 씨가 차갑게 말했다."]], [["“By Gad, Sir,”", "\"맙소사, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “the Bagstock breed are not accustomed to that sort of obstacle.", "소령이 말했다, \"백스톡 가문은 그런 종류의 장애물에는 익숙지 않습니다."], ["Though it’s true enough that Edith might have married twenty times, but for being proud, Sir, proud.”", "하기야 에디스가 도도하지만 않았더라면, 선생, 도도하지만 않았더라면 스무 번은 결혼했을 거라는 게 사실이긴 합니다만.\""]], [["Mr Dombey seemed, by his face, to think no worse of her for that.", "돔비 씨는 그 얼굴로 보아, 그 점 때문에 그녀를 더 나쁘게 여기지는 않는 듯했다."]], [["“It’s a great quality after all,”", "\"따지고 보면 그것도 대단한 자질이지요,\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“By the Lord, it’s a high quality!", "\"맙소사, 그건 고귀한 자질입니다!"], ["Dombey!", "돔비!"], ["You are proud yourself, and your friend, Old Joe, respects you for it, Sir.”", "당신도 도도한 분이고, 당신의 친구 늙은 조는 그 점 때문에 당신을 존경하지요, 선생.\""]], [["With this tribute to the character of his ally, which seemed to be wrung from him by the force of circumstances and the irresistible tendency of their conversation, the Major closed the subject, and glided into a general exposition of the extent to which he had been beloved and doted on by splendid women and brilliant creatures.", "상황의 힘과 대화의 거스를 수 없는 흐름에 떠밀려 짜내듯 나온, 자기 동맹의 성품에 대한 이 헌사를 끝으로 소령은 그 화제를 마무리하고는, 자기가 그동안 화려한 여인들과 눈부신 미인들에게서 얼마나 사랑받고 떠받들어졌는지에 대한 일반적인 장광설로 슬그머니 빠져들었다."]], [["On the next day but one, Mr Dombey and the Major encountered the Honourable Mrs Skewton and her daughter in the Pump-room; on the day after, they met them again very near the place where they had met them first.", "그다음 다음 날, 돔비 씨와 소령은 펌프룸에서 스큐턴 부인 마님과 그녀의 딸을 마주쳤고, 그 이튿날에는 처음 만났던 바로 그 장소 근처에서 다시 그들과 마주쳤다."], ["After meeting them thus, three or four times in all, it became a point of mere civility to old acquaintances that the Major should go there one evening.", "이렇게 통틀어 서너 번 만나고 나니, 소령이 어느 저녁 그곳을 방문하는 것이 옛 지인에 대한 그저 마땅한 예의가 되어 버렸다."], ["Mr Dombey had not originally intended to pay visits, but on the Major announcing this intention, he said he would have the pleasure of accompanying him.", "돔비 씨는 본디 방문할 생각이 없었으나, 소령이 이 뜻을 밝히자 자기도 기꺼이 동행하겠다고 말했다."], ["So the Major told the Native to go round before dinner, and say, with his and Mr Dombey’s compliments, that they would have the honour of visiting the ladies that same evening, if the ladies were alone.", "그래서 소령은 원주민에게 저녁 식사 전에 한 바퀴 돌아 자기와 돔비 씨의 인사를 전하면서, 만일 부인들이 따로 계시다면 바로 그날 저녁 두 부인을 방문하는 영광을 누리고 싶다는 말을 전하라고 했다."], ["In answer to which message, the Native brought back a very small note with a very large quantity of scent about it, indited by the Honourable Mrs Skewton to Major Bagstock, and briefly saying, “You are a shocking bear and I have a great mind not to forgive you, but if you are very good indeed,”", "그 전갈에 대한 답으로 원주민은 향수를 잔뜩 뿌린 아주 작은 쪽지 하나를 가지고 돌아왔는데, 스큐턴 부인 마님이 백스톡 소령에게 쓴 것으로, 짤막하게 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"당신은 정말 못된 곰이고 용서하고 싶지 않은 마음이 굴뚝같지만, 정말로 아주 착하게 군다면,\""], ["which was underlined, “you may come.", "이 대목에는 밑줄이 그어져 있었다, \"오셔도 좋아요."], ["Compliments (in which Edith unites) to Mr Dombey.”", "돔비 씨께 인사 전합니다(에디스도 함께요).\""]], [["The Honourable Mrs Skewton and her daughter, Mrs Granger, resided, while at Leamington, in lodgings that were fashionable enough and dear enough, but rather limited in point of space and conveniences; so that the Honourable Mrs Skewton, being in bed, had her feet in the window and her head in the fireplace, while the Honourable Mrs Skewton’s maid was quartered in a closet within the drawing-room, so extremely small, that, to avoid developing the whole of its accommodations, she was obliged to writhe in and out of the door like a beautiful serpent. Withers, the wan page, slept out of the house immediately under the tiles at a neighbouring milk-shop; and the wheeled chair, which was the stone of that young Sisyphus, passed the night in a shed belonging to the same dairy, where new-laid eggs were produced by the poultry connected with the establishment, who roosted on a broken donkey-cart, persuaded, to all appearance, that it grew there, and was a species of tree.", "* 원주민: 소령이 거느린 인도 출신 하인을 가리키는, 당시 식민지 시대의 비하적 호칭. 스큐턴 부인 마님과 그녀의 딸 그레인저 부인은, 리밍턴에 있는 동안, 충분히 세련되고 충분히 값비싸지만 공간과 편의 면에서는 다소 옹색한 셋방에 머물렀다. 그래서 스큐턴 부인 마님은 침대에 누우면 발이 창문에, 머리가 벽난로에 가 있었고, 한편 스큐턴 부인 마님의 하녀는 응접실 안의 벽장에 거처를 두었는데, 그것이 어찌나 작던지, 그 안의 살림살이를 죄다 펼쳐 보이지 않으려면 마치 아름다운 뱀처럼 문 안팎으로 몸을 비틀며 드나들 수밖에 없었다. 핼쑥한 시동 위더스는 이웃 우유 가게의 기와 바로 밑에서 집 밖 잠을 잤고, 그 젊은 시시포스의 바위인 바퀴 달린 의자는 같은 낙농장에 딸린 헛간에서 밤을 보냈다. 그 헛간에서는 그 가게에 딸린 닭들이 갓 낳은 달걀을 생산했는데, 닭들은 부서진 당나귀 수레 위에 홰를 치고 앉아, 누가 봐도 그것이 거기서 자라난 일종의 나무라고 굳게 믿고 있는 듯했다."]], [["Mr Dombey and the Major found Mrs Skewton arranged, as Cleopatra, among the cushions of a sofa: very airily dressed; and certainly not resembling Shakespeare’s Cleopatra, whom age could not wither.", "돔비 씨와 소령이 가 보니 스큐턴 부인은 클레오파트라처럼 소파의 쿠션들 사이에 자리 잡고 있었다. 매우 산뜻하게 차려입었으나, 세월도 시들게 하지 못했던 셰익스피어의 클레오파트라와는 분명 닮지 않았다."], ["On their way upstairs they had heard the sound of a harp, but it had ceased on their being announced, and Edith now stood beside it handsomer and haughtier than ever.", "위층으로 올라오는 길에 두 사람은 하프 소리를 들었으나, 그들의 방문이 알려지자 그것이 멈췄고, 에디스는 이제 그 어느 때보다도 더 아름답고 더 도도한 모습으로 그 곁에 서 있었다."], ["It was a remarkable characteristic of this lady’s beauty that it appeared to vaunt and assert itself without her aid, and against her will.", "이 부인의 미모에는 한 가지 두드러진 특징이 있었으니, 그것은 그녀의 도움 없이, 아니 그녀의 의지를 거슬러 스스로를 뽐내고 내세우는 듯 보인다는 점이었다. 그녀는 자기가 아름답다는 것을 알았다."], ["She knew that she was beautiful: it was impossible that it could be otherwise: but she seemed with her own pride to defy her very self.", "달리 어쩔 도리가 없는 일이었다. 그러나 그녀는 자기 자신의 자존심으로 바로 자기 자신에게 도전하는 듯 보였다."]], [["Whether she held cheap attractions that could only call forth admiration that was worthless to her, or whether she designed to render them more precious to admirers by this usage of them, those to whom they were precious seldom paused to consider.", "오로지 자기에게는 무가치한 찬탄밖에 불러일으키지 못하는 그 매력들을 그녀가 하찮게 여기는 것인지, 아니면 그것들을 이렇게 다룸으로써 찬미자들에게 더욱 값지게 보이도록 꾀하는 것인지, 그 매력들을 값지게 여기는 이들은 좀처럼 멈춰 서서 헤아려 보는 법이 없었다."]], [["“I hope, Mrs Granger,”", "\"바라건대, 그레인저 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 그녀를 향해 한 걸음 다가서며 말했다, \"저희 때문에 연주를 멈추신 건 아니겠지요?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, advancing a step towards her, “we are not the cause of your ceasing to play?”", "\"댁들 때문이라뇨! 아, 아니에요!\""]], [["“You! oh no!”", "\"그럼 어째서 계속하지 않니, 내 사랑하는 에디스?\""], ["“Why do you not go on then, my dearest Edith?” said Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 말했다."]], [["“I left off as I began—of my own fancy.”", "\"시작했을 때처럼 그만둔 거예요—그저 제 기분에 따라서요.\""]], [["The exquisite indifference of her manner in saying this: an indifference quite removed from dulness or insensibility, for it was pointed with proud purpose: was well set off by the carelessness with which she drew her hand across the strings, and came from that part of the room.", "이렇게 말하는 그녀의 더없이 절묘한 무심함—둔함이나 무감각함과는 거리가 먼, 도도한 의도로 날이 선 그 무심함은, 손으로 줄들을 무심히 쓸어내리고는 그 방의 그쪽 자리에서 물러나는 그 무신경함과 잘 어울려 돋보였다."]], [["“Do you know, Mr Dombey,”", "\"그거 아세요, 돔비 씨,\""], ["said her languishing mother, playing with a hand-screen, “that occasionally my dearest Edith and myself actually almost differ—”", "그녀의 나른한 어머니가 손부채를 만지작거리며 말했다, \"이따금 내 사랑하는 에디스와 저는 정말로 거의 의견이 엇갈리곤 한답니다—\""]], [["“Not quite, sometimes, Mama?”", "\"가끔, 아주 엇갈리는 건 아니죠, 엄마?\""], ["said Edith.", "에디스가 말했다."]], [["“Oh never quite, my darling!", "\"아, 결코 아주 엇갈리는 법은 없지, 내 사랑!"], ["Fie, fie, it would break my heart,”", "저런, 저런, 그랬다간 내 가슴이 찢어질 거야,\""], ["returned her mother, making a faint attempt to pat her with the screen, which Edith made no movement to meet, “—about these old conventionalities of manner that are observed in little things?", "그녀의 어머니가 손부채로 딸을 가볍게 토닥이려고 힘없이 시도하며 응수했으나, 에디스는 그것을 맞이하려는 어떤 몸짓도 하지 않았다, \"—사소한 일들에서 지켜지는 그 케케묵은 격식들을 두고서 말이에요."], ["Why are we not more natural?", "우리는 어째서 더 자연스럽지 못한 걸까요?"], ["Dear me!", "어머나!"], ["With all those yearnings, and gushings, and impulsive throbbings that we have implanted in our souls, and which are so very charming, why are we not more natural?”", "우리 영혼 속에 심어진, 더없이 매혹적인 그 모든 동경과 북받침과 충동적인 떨림을 다 지니고 있으면서, 어째서 우리는 더 자연스럽지 못한 걸까요?\""]], [["Mr Dombey said it was very true, very true.", "돔비 씨는 참으로 옳은 말씀이라고, 참으로 옳다고 말했다."]], [["“We could be more natural I suppose if we tried?”", "\"우리도 노력하면 더 자연스러울 수 있을 텐데, 안 그래요?\""], ["said Mrs Skewton.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다."]], [["Mr Dombey thought it possible.", "돔비 씨는 그것이 가능하리라고 생각했다."]], [["“Devil a bit, Ma’am,”", "\"천만의 말씀입니다, 마님,\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“We couldn’t afford it.", "\"그럴 형편이 못 됩니다."], ["Unless the world was peopled with J.B.“s—tough and blunt old Joes, Ma’am, plain red herrings with hard roes, Sir—we couldn’t afford it.", "세상이 온통 제이 비들로—질기고 무뚝뚝한 늙은 조들로, 마님, 딱딱한 알을 밴 평범한 청어들로 채워지지 않는 한, 선생, 그럴 형편이 못 되지요."], ["It wouldn’t do.”", "어림없는 일입니다.\""]], [["“You naughty Infidel,”", "\"이 못된 이교도 양반,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “be mute.”", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"잠자코 계세요.\""]], [["“Cleopatra commands,”", "\"클레오파트라께서 명하시니,\""], ["returned the Major, kissing his hand, “and Antony Bagstock obeys.”", "소령이 손에 입을 맞추며 응수했다, \"안토니우스 백스톡은 따를 따름입니다.\""]], [["“The man has no sensitiveness,”", "\"이 사람은 감수성이 없어요,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, cruelly holding up the hand-screen so as to shut the Major out.", "스큐턴 부인이 손부채를 매정하게 들어 올려 소령을 가려 버리며 말했다."], ["“No sympathy.", "\"공감이라곤 없죠."], ["And what do we live for but sympathy!", "한데 우리가 공감 말고 무엇을 위해 산답니까!"], ["What else is so extremely charming!", "그 밖에 무엇이 그토록 매혹적이겠어요!"], ["Without that gleam of sunshine on our cold cold earth,”", "우리의 차디찬 이 땅 위에 비치는 그 한 줄기 햇살이 없다면,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, arranging her lace tucker, and complacently observing the effect of her bare lean arm, looking upward from the wrist, “how could we possibly bear it?", "스큐턴 부인이 자기 레이스 깃 장식을 매만지면서, 손목에서 위로 치켜든 자기의 드러난 야윈 팔의 효과를 흐뭇하게 살피며 말했다, \"우리가 도대체 그것을 어떻게 견딜 수 있겠어요?"], ["In short, obdurate man!”", "한마디로, 매정한 양반!\""], ["glancing at the Major, round the screen, “I would have my world all heart; and Faith is so excessively charming, that I won’t allow you to disturb it, do you hear?”", "손부채 너머로 소령을 흘끗 보며, \"저는 제 세상을 온통 마음으로 채우고 싶답니다. 그리고 신의란 어찌나 더없이 매혹적인지, 저는 당신이 그것을 어지럽히도록 두고 보지 않을 거예요, 알겠어요?\""]], [["The Major replied that it was hard in Cleopatra to require the world to be all heart, and yet to appropriate to herself the hearts of all the world; which obliged Cleopatra to remind him that flattery was insupportable to her, and that if he had the boldness to address her in that strain any more, she would positively send him home.", "소령은 세상이 온통 마음으로 채워지기를 요구하면서 동시에 온 세상의 마음을 죄다 자기 것으로 차지하려 드는 것은 클레오파트라로서 가혹한 처사라고 응답했고, 이에 클레오파트라는 아첨이란 자기로서는 참을 수 없는 것이며, 만일 그가 또 한 번 그런 투로 말을 거는 대담함을 보인다면 단연코 그를 집으로 돌려보내겠다는 점을 그에게 일깨워 줄 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Withers the Wan, at this period, handing round the tea, Mr Dombey again addressed himself to Edith.", "이 무렵 핼쑥한 위더스가 차를 돌리고 있을 때, 돔비 씨가 다시 에디스에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“There is not much company here, it would seem?”", "\"여기에는 어울리는 사람들이 그리 많지 않은 모양이지요?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, in his own portentous gentlemanly way.", "돔비 씨가 자기 특유의 거창하고 신사다운 투로 말했다."]], [["“I believe not.", "\"없는 것 같아요."], ["We see none.”", "저희는 아무도 만나지 않거든요.\""]], [["“Why really,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["observed Mrs Skewton from her couch, “there are no people here just now with whom we care to associate.”", "스큐턴 부인이 소파에서 한마디 했다, \"지금 여기에는 저희가 어울리고 싶은 사람들이 없답니다.\""]], [["“They have not enough heart,”", "\"마음이 부족한 이들이라서요,\""], ["said Edith, with a smile.", "에디스가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["The very twilight of a smile: so singularly were its light and darkness blended.", "미소라기보다는 그야말로 미소의 여명 같은 것이었으니, 그 빛과 어둠이 그토록 묘하게 뒤섞여 있었다."]], [["“My dearest Edith rallies me, you see!”", "\"내 사랑하는 에디스가 저를 놀려 대네요, 보세요!\""], ["said her mother, shaking her head: which shook a little of itself sometimes, as if the palsy twinkled now and then in opposition to the diamonds.", "그녀의 어머니가 고개를 흔들며 말했다. 그 고개는 더러 저절로 조금씩 떨렸는데, 마치 다이아몬드들에 맞서 이따금 중풍이 깜빡이기라도 하는 듯했다."], ["“Wicked one!”", "\"이 못된 것 같으니!\""]], [["“You have been here before, if I am not mistaken?”", "\"내가 틀리지 않았다면, 전에도 이곳에 와 보셨지요?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["Still to Edith.", "여전히 에디스에게였다."]], [["“Oh, several times.", "\"아, 여러 번요."], ["I think we have been everywhere.”", "저희는 안 가 본 데가 없는 것 같아요.\""]], [["“A beautiful country!”", "\"아름다운 고장이지요!\""]], [["“I suppose it is.", "\"그렇겠죠."], ["Everybody says so.”", "다들 그렇게 말하니까요.\""]], [["“Your cousin Feenix raves about it, Edith,”", "\"네 사촌 피닉스가 이곳에 푹 빠져 있잖니, 에디스,\""], ["interposed her mother from her couch.", "어머니가 소파에서 끼어들었다."]], [["The daughter slightly turned her graceful head, and raising her eyebrows by a hair’s-breadth, as if her cousin Feenix were of all the mortal world the least to be regarded, turned her eyes again towards Mr Dombey.", "딸은 그 우아한 고개를 살짝 돌리고, 마치 사촌 피닉스야말로 이 세상에서 가장 마음 쓸 가치가 없는 사람이라는 듯 눈썹을 머리카락 한 올 너비만큼 치켜올렸다가, 다시 눈을 돔비 씨 쪽으로 돌렸다."]], [["“I hope, for the credit of my good taste, that I am tired of the neighbourhood,”", "\"제 안목의 명예를 위해서라도, 저는 이 일대에 싫증이 났기를 바라요,\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“You have almost reason to be, Madam,”", "\"그럴 만도 하시지요, 부인,\""], ["he replied, glancing at a variety of landscape drawings, of which he had already recognised several as representing neighbouring points of view, and which were strewn abundantly about the room, “if these beautiful productions are from your hand.”", "그가 응수했다. 이미 여러 점이 인근의 경관을 그린 것임을 알아본 갖가지 풍경화들을 흘끗 보며 한 말이었으니, 그것들은 방 안 여기저기에 잔뜩 흩어져 있었다, \"이 아름다운 작품들이 부인의 손에서 나온 것이라면 말이지요.\""]], [["She gave him no reply, but sat in a disdainful beauty, quite amazing.", "그녀는 그에게 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 자못 놀라우리만치 도도한 미모 그대로 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Have they that interest?”", "\"그렇게 흥미를 끄는 작품들입니까?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“Are they yours?”", "\"이게 부인의 작품인가요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“And you play, I already know.”", "\"그리고 연주도 하시는 건 이미 알았고요.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“And sing?”", "\"노래도 하시고요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["She answered all these questions with a strange reluctance; and with that remarkable air of opposition to herself, already noticed as belonging to her beauty.", "그녀는 이 모든 물음에 묘한 마지못함으로 대답했다. 그리고 그 미모에 딸린 것으로 이미 언급한 바 있는, 자기 자신에 맞서는 그 두드러진 태도로."], ["Yet she was not embarrassed, but wholly self-possessed.", "그러나 그녀는 당황하지 않았고, 완전히 침착했다."], ["Neither did she seem to wish to avoid the conversation, for she addressed her face, and—so far as she could—her manner also, to him; and continued to do so, when he was silent.", "또 대화를 피하고 싶어 하는 것 같지도 않았으니, 얼굴을—그리고 할 수 있는 한 태도까지도—그에게로 향하고 있었고, 그가 말이 없을 때도 계속 그러했다."]], [["“You have many resources against weariness at least,”", "\"적어도 권태를 막을 방편은 많이 가지고 계시는군요,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Whatever their efficiency may be,”", "\"그것들이 얼마나 효과가 있든,\""], ["she returned, “you know them all now.", "그녀가 응수했다, \"이제 그것들을 모두 아시게 됐네요."], ["I have no more.”", "저한테는 더 이상 없어요.\""]], [["“May I hope to prove them all?”", "\"그것들을 죄다 시험해 봐도 좋겠습니까?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with solemn gallantry, laying down a drawing he had held, and motioning towards the harp.", "돔비 씨가 엄숙한 정중함으로, 들고 있던 그림 한 장을 내려놓고 하프 쪽을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“Oh certainly!", "\"아, 물론이죠!"], ["If you desire it!”", "원하신다면요!\""]], [["She rose as she spoke, and crossing by her mother’s couch, and directing a stately look towards her, which was instantaneous in its duration, but inclusive (if anyone had seen it) of a multitude of expressions, among which that of the twilight smile, without the smile itself, overshadowed all the rest, went out of the room.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하며 일어서서, 어머니의 소파 곁을 지나가며 그쪽으로 위엄 있는 눈길을 던졌는데, 그 눈길은 한순간 지나가 버렸으나 (누군가 보았더라면) 무수한 표정을 담고 있었고, 그중에서도 미소 자체는 없이 그 미소의 여명만이 다른 모든 표정을 압도하고 있었다. 그러고는 방을 나갔다."]], [["The Major, who was quite forgiven by this time, had wheeled a little table up to Cleopatra, and was sitting down to play picquet with her.", "이때쯤이면 완전히 용서받은 소령은 작은 탁자 하나를 클레오파트라에게로 끌어다 놓고, 그녀와 피켓 놀이를 하려고 앉아 있었다."], ["Mr Dombey, not knowing the game, sat down to watch them for his edification until Edith should return.", "그 놀이를 모르는 돔비 씨는, 에디스가 돌아올 때까지 견식을 넓힐 겸 두 사람을 지켜보려고 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“We are going to have some music, Mr Dombey, I hope?”", "\"우리, 음악을 좀 들을 수 있겠지요, 돔비 씨?\""], ["said Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 말했다."]], [["“Mrs Granger has been kind enough to promise so,”", "\"그레인저 부인께서 친절히 그러마고 약속해 주셨습니다,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["That’s very nice.", "그거 아주 좋네요."], ["Do you propose, Major?”", "소령은 어떻게 하시려고요?\""]], [["“No, Ma’am,”", "\"안 합니다, 마님,\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“Couldn’t do it.”", "\"못 하지요.\""]], [["“You’re a barbarous being,”", "\"당신은 야만스러운 존재예요,\""], ["replied the lady, “and my hand’s destroyed.", "부인이 응수했다, \"내 패가 다 망가졌잖아요."], ["You are fond of music, Mr Dombey?”", "음악을 좋아하시나요, 돔비 씨?\""]], [["“Eminently so,”", "\"대단히 좋아합니다,\""], ["was Mr Dombey’s answer.", "돔비 씨의 대답이었다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["It’s very nice,”", "음악은 참 좋지요,\""], ["said Cleopatra, looking at her cards.", "클레오파트라가 자기 패를 보며 말했다."], ["“So much heart in it—undeveloped recollections of a previous state of existence—and all that—which is so truly charming.", "\"그 안에 마음이 가득하잖아요—이전 생의 존재에 대한 채 펼쳐지지 않은 기억들 같은 것 말이에요—그런 거 다요—정말이지 매혹적이지요."], ["Do you know,”", "그거 아세요,\""], ["simpered Cleopatra, reversing the knave of clubs, who had come into her game with his heels uppermost, “that if anything could tempt me to put a period to my life, it would be curiosity to find out what it’s all about, and what it means; there are so many provoking mysteries, really, that are hidden from us.", "클레오파트라가 발뒤꿈치가 위로 향한 채 자기 패에 들어와 있던 클럽 잭을 뒤집으며 점잔 빼는 소리로 말했다, \"만약 무언가가 제 삶에 종지부를 찍도록 저를 유혹할 수 있다면, 그건 이 모든 게 다 무엇에 관한 것이고 무슨 뜻인지 알아내고 싶은 호기심일 거예요. 우리에게 감춰진, 정말 약 오르는 신비가 어찌나 많은지요."], ["Major, you to play!”", "소령, 당신 차례예요!\""]], [["The Major played; and Mr Dombey, looking on for his instruction, would soon have been in a state of dire confusion, but that he gave no attention to the game whatever, and sat wondering instead when Edith would come back.", "소령이 패를 냈다. 그리고 견문을 넓히자고 지켜보던 돔비 씨는 곧 끔찍한 혼란에 빠졌을 테지만, 그는 놀이에는 도무지 주의를 기울이지 않고 그 대신 에디스가 언제 돌아올지 궁금해하며 앉아 있었다."]], [["She came at last, and sat down to her harp, and Mr Dombey rose and stood beside her, listening.", "마침내 그녀가 와서 하프 앞에 앉았고, 돔비 씨는 일어나 그녀 곁에 서서 귀를 기울였다."], ["He had little taste for music, and no knowledge of the strain she played, but he saw her bending over it, and perhaps he heard among the sounding strings some distant music of his own, that tamed the monster of the iron road, and made it less inexorable.", "그는 음악에 별 취미가 없었고 그녀가 연주하는 곡조도 알지 못했으나, 그녀가 그 위로 몸을 숙이는 것을 보았고, 아마도 그 울리는 줄들 가운데서 어떤 멀리서 들려오는 그 자신만의 음악을 들었으리니, 그것은 그 무쇠 길의 괴물을 길들이고, 그것을 덜 가혹하게 만들어 주는 음악이었다."]], [["Cleopatra had a sharp eye, verily, at picquet.", "클레오파트라는 피켓을 할 때 실로 날카로운 눈을 지니고 있었다."], ["It glistened like a bird’s, and did not fix itself upon the game, but pierced the room from end to end, and gleamed on harp, performer, listener, everything.", "그 눈은 새의 눈처럼 반짝였고, 놀이에 붙박여 있지 않은 채 방을 이쪽 끝에서 저쪽 끝까지 꿰뚫었으며, 하프와 연주자와 듣는 이, 그 모든 것 위에서 번득였다."]], [["When the haughty beauty had concluded, she arose, and receiving Mr Dombey’s thanks and compliments in exactly the same manner as before, went with scarcely any pause to the piano, and began there.", "도도한 미인이 연주를 마치자, 그녀는 일어나서 조금 전과 똑같은 태도로 돔비 씨의 감사와 찬사를 받고는, 거의 한 치의 멈춤도 없이 피아노로 가 거기서 연주를 시작했다."]], [["Edith Granger, any song but that!", "에디스 그레인저, 그 노래만은 안 됩니다!"], ["Edith Granger, you are very handsome, and your touch upon the keys is brilliant, and your voice is deep and rich; but not the air that his neglected daughter sang to his dead son!", "에디스 그레인저, 당신은 매우 아름답고, 건반 위를 누비는 당신의 손길은 눈부시며, 당신의 목소리는 깊고 풍부합니다. 그러나 그것만은, 그가 거들떠보지 않는 딸이 그의 죽은 아들에게 불러 주던 그 곡조만은 안 됩니다!"]], [["Alas, he knows it not; and if he did, what air of hers would stir him, rigid man!", "아, 그는 그것을 알지 못한다. 또 설령 안다 한들, 그녀의 어떤 곡조가 이 완고한 사내의 마음을 움직이겠는가!"], ["Sleep, lonely Florence, sleep!", "잠들어라, 외로운 플로렌스, 잠들어라!"], ["Peace in thy dreams, although the night has turned dark, and the clouds are gathering, and threaten to discharge themselves in hail!", "밤이 어두워지고, 구름이 모여들어 우박을 쏟아부으려 위협한다 해도, 부디 네 꿈속만은 평안하기를!"]], [["# CHAPTER XXII. A Trifle of Management by Mr Carker the Manager", "# CHAPTER XXII. A Trifle of Management by Mr Carker the Manager"]], [["Mr Carker the Manager sat at his desk, smooth and soft as usual, reading those letters which were reserved for him to open, backing them occasionally with such memoranda and references as their business purport required, and parcelling them out into little heaps for distribution through the several departments of the House.", "지배인 카커 씨가 평소처럼 매끈하고 부드럽게 자기 책상 앞에 앉아, 그가 직접 뜯어보도록 따로 남겨 둔 편지들을 읽고 있었다. 이따금 그 편지의 업무상 취지가 요구하는 메모와 참조 사항을 그 뒷면에 덧붙이기도 하고, 또 회사의 여러 부서로 나눠 보내기 위해 작은 무더기로 분류해 두기도 했다."], ["The post had come in heavy that morning, and Mr Carker the Manager had a good deal to do.", "그날 아침 우편물이 잔뜩 들어와서, 지배인 카커 씨는 할 일이 꽤 많았다."]], [["The general action of a man so engaged—pausing to look over a bundle of papers in his hand, dealing them round in various portions, taking up another bundle and examining its contents with knitted brows and pursed-out lips—dealing, and sorting, and pondering by turns—would easily suggest some whimsical resemblance to a player at cards.", "이렇게 일하는 사람의 전반적인 동작은—손에 든 한 묶음의 서류를 잠시 멈춰 훑어보고, 그것을 여러 몫으로 나누어 돌리고, 또 다른 묶음을 집어 들어 미간을 찌푸리고 입술을 오므린 채 그 내용을 살피고—나누고, 분류하고, 번갈아 가며 궁리하는 모습은—카드놀이를 하는 사람과의 어떤 기이한 닮음을 손쉽게 떠올리게 했다."], ["The face of Mr Carker the Manager was in good keeping with such a fancy.", "지배인 카커 씨의 얼굴은 그런 상상과 잘 어울렸다."], ["It was the face of a man who studied his play, warily: who made himself master of all the strong and weak points of the game: who registered the cards in his mind as they fell about him, knew exactly what was on them, what they missed, and what they made: who was crafty to find out what the other players held, and who never betrayed his own hand.", "그것은 자기 패를 조심스럽게 궁리하는 사람의 얼굴이었다. 그 놀이의 강점과 약점을 모조리 제 것으로 만드는 사람, 카드가 자기 주변에 떨어지는 대로 마음속에 기록해 두고, 그 위에 무엇이 있는지, 무엇이 빠졌는지, 무엇을 이루었는지 정확히 아는 사람, 다른 사람들이 무슨 패를 쥐고 있는지 교활하게 알아내면서도 결코 제 패는 드러내지 않는 사람의 얼굴이었다."]], [["The letters were in various languages, but Mr Carker the Manager read them all.", "편지들은 여러 나라 말로 쓰여 있었지만, 지배인 카커 씨는 그것을 모두 읽어 냈다."], ["If there had been anything in the offices of Dombey and Son that he could read, there would have been a card wanting in the pack.", "돔비와 아들 회사의 사무실에 그가 읽을 수 있는 것이 무엇이라도 있었다면, 카드 한 벌에서 패 하나가 모자란 셈이 되었을 것이다."], ["He read almost at a glance, and made combinations of one letter with another and one business with another as he went on, adding new matter to the heaps—much as a man would know the cards at sight, and work out their combinations in his mind after they were turned.", "그는 거의 한눈에 읽어 내면서, 일을 진행하는 동안 한 편지를 다른 편지와, 한 거래를 다른 거래와 결합하고, 무더기에 새로운 사안을 더해 갔다—마치 한 사람이 카드를 보자마자 알아채고, 그것이 뒤집힌 뒤 그 조합을 마음속에서 풀어내는 것과 흡사했다."], ["Something too deep for a partner, and much too deep for an adversary, Mr Carker the Manager sat in the rays of the sun that came down slanting on him through the skylight, playing his game alone.", "동업자에게는 너무 깊고, 적수에게는 훨씬 더 깊은 무언가를 품은 채, 지배인 카커 씨는 채광창을 통해 비스듬히 내리쬐는 햇살 속에 앉아, 홀로 자기 놀이를 했다."]], [["And although it is not among the instincts wild or domestic of the cat tribe to play at cards, feline from sole to crown was Mr Carker the Manager, as he basked in the strip of summer-light and warmth that shone upon his table and the ground as if they were a crooked dial-plate, and himself the only figure on it. With hair and whiskers deficient in colour at all times, but feebler than common in the rich sunshine, and more like the coat of a sandy tortoise-shell cat; with long nails, nicely pared and sharpened; with a natural antipathy to any speck of dirt, which made him pause sometimes and watch the falling motes of dust, and rub them off his smooth white hand or glossy linen: Mr Carker the Manager, sly of manner, sharp of tooth, soft of foot, watchful of eye, oily of tongue, cruel of heart, nice of habit, sat with a dainty steadfastness and patience at his work, as if he were waiting at a mouse’s hole.", "비록 카드놀이를 하는 것이 고양이 족속의 야생적이거나 가정적인 본능에 속하지는 않지만, 지배인 카커 씨는 발끝부터 정수리까지 고양이 같았다. 그가 책상과 바닥 위에 비치는 한 줄기 여름 햇빛과 온기 속에서 일광욕을 즐기는 모습은, 마치 그 빛줄기가 비뚤어진 해시계 판이고 그 위의 유일한 형상이 그 자신인 듯했다. 머리카락과 구레나룻은 늘 빛깔이 부족했지만, 짙은 햇살 속에서는 평소보다 더 흐릿해서, 모래빛 얼룩무늬 고양이의 털과 한층 더 비슷해 보였다. 길고 곱게 다듬어 날카롭게 간 손톱에다, 한 점 티끌도 타고난 듯 싫어해서, 때로는 동작을 멈추고 떨어지는 먼지 알갱이를 지켜보다가 그것을 매끈하고 흰 손이나 윤기 나는 아마포에서 닦아 내곤 했다. 거동은 음흉하고, 이는 날카롭고, 발은 부드럽고, 눈은 빈틈없고, 혀는 기름지고, 마음은 잔인하고, 습관은 까다로운 지배인 카커 씨는, 마치 쥐구멍 앞에서 기다리기라도 하듯, 깔끔한 끈기와 인내로 자기 일에 매달려 앉아 있었다."]], [["At length the letters were disposed of, excepting one which he reserved for a particular audience.", "마침내 편지들이 모두 처리되었는데, 다만 특별한 청중을 위해 따로 남겨 둔 한 통은 예외였다."], ["Having locked the more confidential correspondence in a drawer, Mr Carker the Manager rang his bell.", "좀 더 은밀한 서신들을 서랍에 넣어 잠근 뒤, 지배인 카커 씨는 종을 울렸다."]], [["“Why do you answer it?”", "\"왜 자네가 응하는가?\""], ["was his reception of his brother.", "그가 자기 형을 맞이하는 인사였다."]], [["“The messenger is out, and I am the next,”", "\"심부름꾼이 나가 있어서, 그다음이 나야,\""], ["was the submissive reply.", "순종적인 대답이었다."]], [["“You are the next?”", "\"자네가 그다음이라고?\""], ["muttered the Manager.", "지배인이 중얼거렸다. \"그래!"], ["“Yes! Creditable to me!", "나로서는 영광스러운 일이군!"], ["There!”", "자!\""]], [["Pointing to the heaps of opened letters, he turned disdainfully away, in his elbow-chair, and broke the seal of that one which he held in his hand.", "뜯어 본 편지 무더기를 가리키고는, 그는 안락의자에 앉은 채 경멸하듯 몸을 돌려, 손에 쥐고 있던 그 한 통의 봉인을 뜯었다."]], [["“I am sorry to trouble you, James,”", "\"성가시게 해서 미안하네, 제임스,\""], ["said the brother, gathering them up, “but—”", "형이 그 편지들을 모으며 말했다, \"하지만—\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["you have something to say.", "뭔가 할 말이 있군."], ["I knew that.", "그럴 줄 알았어."], ["Well?”", "그래서?\""]], [["Mr Carker the Manager did not raise his eyes or turn them on his brother, but kept them on his letter, though without opening it.", "지배인 카커 씨는 눈을 들거나 형 쪽으로 돌리지 않고, 편지를 펴 보지도 않으면서 거기에 시선을 둔 채로 있었다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""], ["he repeated sharply.", "그가 날카롭게 되풀이했다."]], [["“I am uneasy about Harriet.”", "\"해리엇이 마음에 걸려서.\""]], [["“Harriet who?", "\"무슨 해리엇?"], ["what Harriet?", "어떤 해리엇?"], ["I know nobody of that name.”", "난 그런 이름의 사람을 모르네.\""]], [["“She is not well, and has changed very much of late.”", "\"그 애가 건강이 안 좋아, 요즘 들어 아주 많이 변했어.\""]], [["“She changed very much, a great many years ago,”", "\"걔야 아주 여러 해 전에 무척 많이 변했지,\""], ["replied the Manager; “and that is all I have to say.", "지배인이 응수했다. \"그게 내가 할 말의 전부야.\""]], [["“I think if you would hear me—", "\"한 번만 내 말을 들어 준다면—\""]], [["“Why should I hear you, Brother John?”", "\"내가 왜 자네 말을 들어야 하지, 존 형?\""], ["returned the Manager, laying a sarcastic emphasis on those two words, and throwing up his head, but not lifting his eyes.", "지배인이 그 두 단어에 비꼬는 강세를 실어, 고개를 치켜들면서도 눈은 들지 않은 채 응수했다."], ["“I tell you, Harriet Carker made her choice many years ago between her two brothers.", "\"분명히 말해 두지, 해리엇 카커는 여러 해 전에 자기 두 오라비 사이에서 선택을 했어."], ["She may repent it, but she must abide by it.”", "후회할 수는 있겠지만, 그 선택은 감수해야지.\""]], [["“Don’t mistake me.", "\"오해하지 마."], ["I do not say she does repent it.", "걔가 후회한다고 말하는 게 아니야."], ["It would be black ingratitude in me to hint at such a thing,”", "그런 걸 넌지시 비추는 건 내 쪽에서 새까만 배은망덕이지,\""], ["returned the other.", "상대가 응수했다."], ["“Though believe me, James, I am as sorry for her sacrifice as you.”", "\"하지만 믿어 줘, 제임스, 나도 너만큼이나 그 애의 희생이 안타까워.\""]], [["“As I?”", "\"나만큼?\""], ["exclaimed the Manager.", "지배인이 외쳤다."], ["“As I?”", "\"나만큼이라고?\""]], [["“As sorry for her choice—for what you call her choice—as you are angry at it,”", "\"그 애의 선택을—네가 그 애의 선택이라 부르는 것을—네가 화내는 만큼이나 나는 안타까워하고 있어,\""], ["said the Junior.", "동생이 말했다."]], [["“Angry?”", "\"화낸다?\""], ["repeated the other, with a wide show of his teeth.", "상대가 이를 한껏 드러내며 되풀이했다."]], [["“Displeased.", "\"불쾌해한다고."], ["Whatever word you like best.", "네가 좋아하는 단어 무엇이든."], ["You know my meaning.", "내 말뜻은 알잖아."], ["There is no offence in my intention.”", "너를 욕보일 생각은 없어.\""]], [["“There is offence in everything you do,”", "\"자네가 하는 모든 일에 욕보임이 있지,\""], ["replied his brother, glancing at him with a sudden scowl, which in a moment gave place to a wider smile than the last.", "그의 동생이 별안간 눈살을 찌푸리며 그를 흘끗 보면서 응수했다. 그 찌푸림은 곧 앞서보다 더 넓은 미소로 바뀌었다."], ["“Carry those papers away, if you please.", "\"그 서류들은 가져가 주게. 부디."], ["I am busy.", "난 바쁘니까.\""]], [["His politeness was so much more cutting than his wrath, that the Junior went to the door.", "그의 정중함은 노여움보다 훨씬 더 베이는 듯해서, 동생은 문 쪽으로 갔다."], ["But stopping at it, and looking round, he said:", "그러나 문 앞에서 멈춰 서서 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“When Harriet tried in vain to plead for me with you, on your first just indignation, and my first disgrace; and when she left you, James, to follow my broken fortunes, and devote herself, in her mistaken affection, to a ruined brother, because without her he had no one, and was lost; she was young and pretty.", "\"네가 처음 정당한 분노를 터뜨리고 내가 처음 망신을 당했을 때, 해리엇이 너에게 나를 변호해 주려고 애썼으나 헛수고였지. 그리고 그 애가 너를 떠나, 제임스, 무너진 내 운명을 따라와서, 잘못된 애정으로 망가진 오라비에게 자신을 바쳤을 때—나에게는 그 애 말고는 아무도 없었고, 길을 잃었으니까—그때 그 애는 젊고 예뻤어."], ["I think if you could see her now—if you would go and see her—she would move your admiration and compassion.”", "네가 지금 그 애를 볼 수 있다면—가서 본다면—그 애가 너의 감탄과 연민을 자아낼 거라고 생각해.\""]], [["The Manager inclined his head, and showed his teeth, as who should say, in answer to some careless small-talk, “Dear me!", "지배인은 고개를 기울이고 이를 드러냈는데, 마치 무심한 잡담에 대답이라도 하듯, \"저런! 그런가?\""], ["Is that the case?” but said never a word.", "하는 표정이었으나, 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["“We thought in those days: you and I both: that she would marry young, and lead a happy and light-hearted life,”", "\"그 시절 우리는 생각했지, 너도 나도 둘 다, 그 애가 일찍 결혼해서 행복하고 가뿐한 삶을 살리라고,\""], ["pursued the other.", "상대가 말을 이었다."], ["“Oh if you knew how cheerfully she cast those hopes away; how cheerfully she has gone forward on the path she took, and never once looked back; you never could say again that her name was strange in your ears.", "\"오, 그 애가 그 희망들을 얼마나 명랑하게 내던졌는지, 자기가 택한 길을 얼마나 명랑하게 걸어 나아가며 단 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않았는지 네가 안다면, 너는 다시는 그 애 이름이 네 귀에 낯설다고 말할 수 없을 거야."], ["Never!”", "결코!\""]], [["Again the Manager inclined his head and showed his teeth, and seemed to say, “Remarkable indeed!", "다시 지배인은 고개를 기울이고 이를 드러내며, \"참으로 놀랍군! 자네가 나를 아주 놀라게 하는걸!\""], ["You quite surprise me!”", "하고 말하는 듯했다."], ["And again he uttered never a word.", "그리고 다시 한마디도 입에 담지 않았다."]], [["“May I go on?”", "\"계속해도 되겠나?\""], ["said John Carker, mildly.", "존 카커가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“On your way?”", "\"가던 길을?\""], ["replied his smiling brother.", "미소 짓는 동생이 응수했다."], ["“If you will have the goodness.”", "\"그래 준다면 고맙겠네.\""]], [["John Carker, with a sigh, was passing slowly out at the door, when his brother’s voice detained him for a moment on the threshold.", "존 카커가 한숨을 쉬며 천천히 문밖으로 나서려는데, 동생의 목소리가 그를 문지방 위에 잠시 붙들어 두었다."]], [["“If she has gone, and goes, her own way cheerfully,”", "\"그 애가 명랑하게 제 길을 떠나갔고 또 그렇게 가고 있다면,\""], ["he said, throwing the still unfolded letter on his desk, and putting his hands firmly in his pockets, “you may tell her that I go as cheerfully on mine.", "그가 아직 펴지 않은 편지를 책상에 던지고 두 손을 주머니에 단단히 찔러 넣으며 말했다, \"내가 내 길을 똑같이 명랑하게 가고 있다고 그 애에게 전하게. 그 애가 단 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않았다면, 나는 가끔 뒤돌아본다고 전하게."], ["If she has never once looked back, you may tell her that I have, sometimes, to recall her taking part with you, and that my resolution is no easier to wear away;”", "그 애가 자네 편을 들었던 일을 떠올리려고 말이야. 그리고 내 결심은 닳아 없어지기가—\""], ["he smiled very sweetly here; “than marble.”", "여기서 그는 아주 달콤하게 미소 지었다, \"—대리석만큼이나 어렵다고.\""]], [["“I tell her nothing of you. We never speak about you.", "\"난 자네에 관해서는 그 애에게 아무것도 전하지 않아."], ["Once a year, on your birthday, Harriet says always, ‘Let us remember James by name, and wish him happy,’ but we say no more.”", "우리는 자네 이야기는 절대 하지 않으니까. 일 년에 한 번, 자네 생일에, 해리엇은 늘 '제임스의 이름을 떠올리고 그가 행복하기를 빌자'고 말하지만, 그 이상은 말하지 않아.\""]], [["“Tell it then, if you please,”", "\"그렇다면 그 말을,\""], ["returned the other, “to yourself.", "상대가 응수했다, \"자네 자신에게나 들려주게."], ["You can’t repeat it too often, as a lesson to you to avoid the subject in speaking to me.", "아무리 자주 되풀이해도 모자라지. 나에게 말할 때 그 화제를 피하라는 교훈으로 말이야."], ["I know no Harriet Carker.", "나는 해리엇 카커라는 사람을 몰라."], ["There is no such person.", "그런 사람은 없어."], ["You may have a sister; make much of her.", "자네에게는 누이가 있을지 모르지. 그 애를 끔찍이 아끼게나."], ["I have none.”", "나에게는 그런 사람 없으니까.\""]], [["Mr Carker the Manager took up the letter again, and waved it with a smile of mock courtesy towards the door.", "지배인 카커 씨는 다시 편지를 집어 들고는, 거짓 정중함의 미소를 띠며 문 쪽으로 그것을 흔들어 보였다."], ["Unfolding it as his brother withdrew, and looking darkly after him as he left the room, he once more turned round in his elbow-chair, and applied himself to a diligent perusal of its contents.", "형이 물러가는 동안 그것을 펴고, 형이 방을 나갈 때 어둡게 그 뒤를 노려본 뒤, 그는 다시 한번 안락의자에서 몸을 돌려, 그 내용을 부지런히 정독하기 시작했다."]], [["It was in the writing of his great chief, Mr Dombey, and dated from Leamington.", "그것은 그의 위대한 우두머리인 돔비 씨의 필체였고, 리밍턴에서 부친 것이었다."], ["Though he was a quick reader of all other letters, Mr Carker read this slowly; weighing the words as he went, and bringing every tooth in his head to bear upon them.", "다른 모든 편지는 빨리 읽는 그였지만, 카커는 이것은 천천히 읽었다. 진행하면서 단어 하나하나를 저울질하고, 머릿속의 이를 모조리 동원해 거기에 매달렸다."], ["When he had read it through once, he turned it over again, and picked out these passages.", "한 번 끝까지 읽고 나서, 그는 그것을 다시 뒤집어 다음 구절들을 골라냈다."], ["“I find myself benefited by the change, and am not yet inclined to name any time for my return.”", "\"나는 이 변화로 효과를 보고 있으며, 아직 돌아갈 시기를 정할 마음이 없소.\""], ["“I wish, Carker, you would arrange to come down once and see me here, and let me know how things are going on, in person.”", "\"카커, 한번 이리로 내려와서 나를 만나, 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 직접 알려 주도록 조처해 주면 좋겠소.\""], ["“I omitted to speak to you about young Gay.", "\"젊은 게이에 관해 자네에게 말하는 걸 빼먹었군."], ["If not gone per Son and Heir, or if Son and Heir still lying in the Docks, appoint some other young man and keep him in the City for the present.", "만일 '아들이자 후계자'호로 떠나지 않았거나, 혹은 그 배가 아직 부두에 정박해 있다면, 다른 젊은이를 하나 임명하고 그자는 당분간 시내에 두도록 하시오."], ["I am not decided.”", "나는 아직 결정하지 못했소.\""], ["“Now that’s unfortunate!”", "\"이런, 그건 불운한데!\""], ["said Mr Carker the Manager, expanding his mouth, as if it were made of India-rubber: “for he’s far away.”", "지배인 카커 씨가 마치 입이 고무로 만들어진 듯 쩍 벌리며 말했다. \"그자는 멀리 가 버렸으니 말이야.\""]], [["Still that passage, which was in a postscript, attracted his attention and his teeth, once more.", "추신에 적힌 그 구절이 다시 한번 그의 주의와 이를 끌어당겼다."]], [["“I think,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["he said, “my good friend Captain Cuttle mentioned something about being towed along in the wake of that day.", "그가 말했다, \"내 친애하는 벗 커틀 선장이 그날의 뒷물결에 끌려가는 것에 대해 뭔가 말했던 것 같은데."], ["What a pity he’s so far away!”", "그자가 그렇게 멀리 가 버렸다니 안타까운 노릇이군!\""]], [["He refolded the letter, and was sitting trifling with it, standing it long-wise and broad-wise on his table, and turning it over and over on all sides—doing pretty much the same thing, perhaps, by its contents—when Mr Perch the messenger knocked softly at the door, and coming in on tiptoe, bending his body at every step as if it were the delight of his life to bow, laid some papers on the table.", "그는 편지를 다시 접고는, 그것을 가지고 만지작거리며 앉아 있었다. 그것을 책상 위에 세로로도 가로로도 세워 보고, 사방으로 이리저리 뒤집어 보면서—아마 그 내용물에 대해서도 거의 똑같은 짓을 하고 있었을 것이다—그때 심부름꾼 퍼치 씨가 문을 부드럽게 두드리고는, 발끝으로 걸어 들어와, 한 걸음 한 걸음마다 마치 절하는 것이 인생의 낙이라도 되는 듯 몸을 굽히면서, 서류 몇 장을 책상에 내려놓았다."]], [["“Would you please to be engaged, Sir?”", "\"바쁘신 줄로 알아도 되겠습니까, 나리?\""], ["asked Mr Perch, rubbing his hands, and deferentially putting his head on one side, like a man who felt he had no business to hold it up in such a presence, and would keep it as much out of the way as possible.", "퍼치 씨가 두 손을 비비며, 마치 이런 분 앞에서 고개를 쳐들 자격이 없다고 느끼는 사람처럼, 그래서 되도록 고개를 멀찍이 비켜 두려는 사람처럼, 공손히 머리를 한쪽으로 기울이며 물었다."]], [["“Who wants me?”", "\"누가 나를 찾는가?\""]], [["“Why, Sir,”", "\"그게 말입니다, 나리,\""], ["said Mr Perch, in a soft voice, “really nobody, Sir, to speak of at present.", "퍼치 씨가 부드러운 목소리로 말했다, \"지금으로선 정말 말씀드릴 만한 사람은 아무도 없습니다, 나리."], ["Mr Gills the Ship’s Instrument-maker, Sir, has looked in, about a little matter of payment, he says: but I mentioned to him, Sir, that you was engaged several deep; several deep.”", "선박 계기 제작자 길스 씨가 들렀습니다, 나리, 약간의 대금 문제 때문이라고 합니다만, 제가 그분께 나리께서 몇 겹으로 바쁘시다고, 몇 겹으로 말입니다, 일러 드렸습니다.\""]], [["Mr Perch coughed once behind his hand, and waited for further orders.", "퍼치 씨는 손등에 대고 한 번 기침을 하고는, 추가 지시를 기다렸다."]], [["“Anybody else?”", "\"또 다른 사람은?\""]], [["“Well, Sir,”", "\"그게요, 나리,\""], ["said Mr Perch, “I wouldn’t of my own self take the liberty of mentioning, Sir, that there was anybody else; but that same young lad that was here yesterday, Sir, and last week, has been hanging about the place; and it looks, Sir,”", "퍼치 씨가 말했다, \"제 입으로 감히 다른 누가 있다고 말씀드릴 주제는 못 됩니다만, 어제도 여기 왔던, 그리고 지난주에도 왔던 바로 그 어린 녀석이, 나리, 근처를 어슬렁거리고 있습니다. 그리고, 나리,\""], ["added Mr Perch, stopping to shut the door, “dreadful unbusiness-like to see him whistling to the sparrows down the court, and making of ’em answer him.”", "퍼치 씨가 문을 닫으려고 멈추며 덧붙였다, \"그 녀석이 안마당에서 참새들한테 휘파람을 불고 또 그놈들이 자기한테 대답하게 만드는 꼴을 보면, 영 사업가답지 못해 보입니다.\""]], [["“You said he wanted something to do, didn’t you, Perch?”", "\"그자가 일거리를 원한다고 했다지, 그렇지 않은가, 퍼치?\""], ["asked Mr Carker, leaning back in his chair and looking at that officer.", "카커 씨가 의자에 기대앉아 그 직원을 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“Why, Sir,”", "\"그게요, 나리,\""], ["said Mr Perch, coughing behind his hand again, “his expression certainly were that he was in wants of a sitiwation, and that he considered something might be done for him about the Docks, being used to fishing with a rod and line: but—”", "퍼치 씨가 다시 손등에 대고 기침을 하며 말했다, \"그 녀석 말로는 분명히 일자리가 필요하다는 거였고요, 자기가 낚싯대와 낚싯줄로 고기를 잡는 데 익숙하니 부두 쪽에서 무슨 일이 생길 만하다고 여겼다는데요, 하지만—\""], ["Mr Perch shook his head very dubiously indeed.", "퍼치 씨는 정말이지 매우 미심쩍게 고개를 저었다."]], [["“What does he say when he comes?”", "\"올 때마다 뭐라고 하던가?\""], ["asked Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Indeed, Sir,”", "\"실은, 나리,\""], ["said Mr Perch, coughing another cough behind his hand, which was always his resource as an expression of humility when nothing else occurred to him, “his observation generally air that he would humbly wish to see one of the gentlemen, and that he wants to earn a living.", "퍼치 씨가 손등에 대고 또 한 번 기침을 했는데, 이는 다른 말이 떠오르지 않을 때 겸손의 표현으로 늘 쓰는 방편이었다, \"그 녀석 말은 대체로, 자기는 황송하오나 신사분들 가운데 한 분을 뵙고 싶다는 거고, 벌어먹고 살고 싶다는 겁니다."], ["But you see, Sir,”", "하지만 보십시오, 나리,\""], ["added Perch, dropping his voice to a whisper, and turning, in the inviolable nature of his confidence, to give the door a thrust with his hand and knee, as if that would shut it any more when it was shut already, “it’s hardly to be bore, Sir, that a common lad like that should come a prowling here, and saying that his mother nursed our House’s young gentleman, and that he hopes our House will give him a chance on that account.", "퍼치가 목소리를 속삭임으로 낮추고는, 그 비밀의 침범할 수 없는 성질대로, 이미 닫힌 문이 더 닫히기라도 할 듯 손과 무릎으로 문을 한 번 더 밀면서 말했다, \"저런 천한 녀석이 여기에 어슬렁거리며 와서, 제 어미가 우리 회사 어린 도련님의 유모였다느니, 그 덕에 우리 회사가 자기한테 기회를 줬으면 한다느니 하는 건, 나리, 정말 참아 주기 어렵습니다. 분명히 말씀드립니다만, 나리,\""], ["I am sure, Sir,” observed Mr Perch, “that although Mrs Perch was at that time nursing as thriving a little girl, Sir, as we’ve ever took the liberty of adding to our family, I wouldn’t have made so free as drop a hint of her being capable of imparting nourishment, not if it was never so!”", "퍼치 씨가 말했다, \"비록 퍼치 부인이 그 무렵에, 우리가 감히 식구로 들인 것 중에 더없이 잘 자라는 어린 계집아이에게 젖을 먹이고 있었습니다만, 그렇다고 제가 감히 그 사람이 젖을 먹일 능력이 있다는 둥 넌지시 비출 만큼 함부로 굴지는 않았을 겁니다, 결코 그러지 않았을 거예요!\""]], [["Mr Carker grinned at him like a shark, but in an absent, thoughtful manner.", "카커 씨는 상어처럼 그를 보고 씩 웃었으나, 멍하니 생각에 잠긴 듯한 태도였다."]], [["“Whether,”", "\"혹시,\""], ["submitted Mr Perch, after a short silence, and another cough, “it mightn’t be best for me to tell him, that if he was seen here any more he would be given into custody; and to keep to it!", "퍼치 씨가 잠시 침묵하고 또 한 번 기침한 뒤 조심스레 의견을 냈다, \"제가 그 녀석한테, 여기서 다시 한 번이라도 눈에 띄면 구금시키겠다고 일러 주고, 또 그대로 지키는 게 상책이 아닐는지요!"], ["With respect to bodily fear,”", "몸으로 겪는 두려움으로 말하자면,\""], ["said Mr Perch, “I’m so timid, myself, by nature, Sir, and my nerves is so unstrung by Mrs Perch’s state, that I could take my affidavit easy.”", "퍼치 씨가 말했다, \"저 자신이 천성적으로 어찌나 겁이 많은지요, 나리, 게다가 퍼치 부인 상태 때문에 신경이 어찌나 곤두서 있는지, 저는 진술서를 손쉽게 작성할 수 있을 정도입니다.\""]], [["“Let me see this fellow, Perch,”", "\"그자를 한번 보세, 퍼치,\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."], ["“Bring him in!”", "\"데려오게!\""]], [["“Yes, Sir.", "\"네, 나리."], ["Begging your pardon, Sir,”", "죄송합니다만, 나리,\""], ["said Mr Perch, hesitating at the door, “he’s rough, Sir, in appearance.”", "퍼치 씨가 문 앞에서 망설이며 말했다, \"그 녀석은, 나리, 행색이 거칩니다.\""]], [["“Never mind.", "\"상관없네."], ["If he’s there, bring him in.", "거기 있으면 데려오게."], ["I’ll see Mr Gills directly.", "길스 씨는 곧바로 만나겠네."], ["Ask him to wait.”", "기다려 달라고 청하게.\""]], [["Mr Perch bowed; and shutting the door, as precisely and carefully as if he were not coming back for a week, went on his quest among the sparrows in the court.", "퍼치 씨는 절을 했다. 그러고는 일주일 동안 돌아오지 않을 사람처럼 정확하고 조심스럽게 문을 닫고, 안마당의 참새들 사이로 자기 임무를 찾아 나섰다."], ["While he was gone, Mr Carker assumed his favourite attitude before the fire-place, and stood looking at the door; presenting, with his under lip tucked into the smile that showed his whole row of upper teeth, a singularly crouching apace.", "그가 나가 있는 동안, 카커 씨는 벽난로 앞에서 그가 좋아하는 자세를 취하고 서서 문을 바라보았다. 아랫입술을 미소 속으로 말아 넣어 윗니를 한 줄 통째로 드러내며, 묘하게 웅크려 노리는 듯한 모습이었다."]], [["The messenger was not long in returning, followed by a pair of heavy boots that came bumping along the passage like boxes.", "심부름꾼은 곧 돌아왔는데, 그 뒤로 무거운 부츠 한 켤레가 마치 상자처럼 복도를 따라 쿵쿵거리며 따라왔다."], ["With the unceremonious words “Come along with you!”—a very unusual form of introduction from his lips—Mr Perch then ushered into the presence a strong-built lad of fifteen, with a round red face, a round sleek head, round black eyes, round limbs, and round body, who, to carry out the general rotundity of his appearance, had a round hat in his hand, without a particle of brim to it.", "\"어서 따라와!\"라는 격식 없는 말—그의 입에서는 매우 이례적인 소개 형식이었다—과 함께, 퍼치 씨는 열다섯 살쯤 된 다부진 사내아이를 그 앞으로 안내했다. 둥글고 붉은 얼굴에, 둥글고 매끈한 머리, 둥근 검은 눈, 둥근 사지, 둥근 몸뚱이를 한 그 아이는, 전반적인 둥그스름함을 완성하기라도 하듯, 챙이라곤 한 조각도 없는 둥근 모자를 손에 들고 있었다."]], [["Obedient to a nod from Mr Carker, Perch had no sooner confronted the visitor with that gentleman than he withdrew.", "카커 씨의 끄덕임에 순종하여, 퍼치는 방문객을 그 신사 앞에 마주 세우자마자 물러갔다."], ["The moment they were face to face alone, Mr Carker, without a word of preparation, took him by the throat, and shook him until his head seemed loose upon his shoulders.", "두 사람이 단둘이 얼굴을 맞대는 순간, 카커 씨는 한마디 예비도 없이 그의 목덜미를 움켜쥐고, 머리가 어깨 위에서 헐거워질 것 같을 때까지 흔들어 댔다."]], [["The boy, who in the midst of his astonishment could not help staring wildly at the gentleman with so many white teeth who was choking him, and at the office walls, as though determined, if he were choked, that his last look should be at the mysteries for his intrusion into which he was paying such a severe penalty, at last contrived to utter—", "그 아이는 놀라움 한가운데서도, 자기 목을 조르고 있는, 그토록 흰 이가 많은 신사를 멍하니 노려보지 않을 수 없었고, 또 사무실 벽들도 노려보았다. 마치 자기가 목 졸려 죽는다면, 그 무단 침입의 대가로 이토록 혹독한 벌을 치르고 있는 그 신비로운 것들에 마지막 시선을 던지겠다고 작정한 듯했다. 그러다 마침내 그는 이렇게 내뱉었다—"]], [["“Come, Sir!", "\"이봐요, 나리!"], ["You let me alone, will you!”", "좀 놓아주세요, 네!\""]], [["“Let you alone!”", "\"놓아 달라고!\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."], ["“What!", "\"뭐!"], ["I have got you, have I?”", "내가 너를 잡았지, 그렇지?\""], ["There was no doubt of that, and tightly too. “You dog,”", "그 점은 의심의 여지가 없었고, 더구나 단단히 잡고 있었다."], ["said Mr Carker, through his set jaws, “I’ll strangle you!”", "\"이 개 같은 녀석,\" 카커 씨가 악문 턱 사이로 말했다, \"목을 졸라 죽여 버리겠다!\""]], [["Biler whimpered, would he though?", "바일러는 흐느끼며, 자기가 뭐 어쨌다고요?"], ["oh no he wouldn’t—and what was he doing of—and why didn’t he strangle some—body of his own size and not him: but Biler was quelled by the extraordinary nature of his reception, and, as his head became stationary, and he looked the gentleman in the face, or rather in the teeth, and saw him snarling at him, he so far forgot his manhood as to cry.", "오, 안 그랬어요—대체 자기가 뭘 했다는 거예요—왜 자기 같은 덩치를 골라 졸라 죽이지 않고 자기를 괴롭히느냐는 둥 했지만, 바일러는 자신을 맞이한 그 별난 방식에 기가 꺾여 버렸다. 머리가 가만해지고 그 신사의 얼굴을, 아니 차라리 그 이를 들여다보며 자기를 향해 이를 드러내고 으르렁대는 것을 보자, 그는 사내다움을 잊을 만큼 그만 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“I haven’t done nothing to you, Sir,”", "\"전 나리께 아무 짓도 안 했어요, 나리,\""], ["said Biler, otherwise Rob, otherwise Grinder, and always Toodle.", "바일러가 말했다. 달리 부르면 롭, 또 달리 부르면 그라인더, 그리고 언제나 투들이었다."]], [["“You young scoundrel!”", "\"이 젊은 악당 놈아!\""], ["replied Mr Carker, slowly releasing him, and moving back a step into his favourite position.", "카커 씨가 천천히 그를 놓아주고는, 좋아하는 자세로 한 걸음 물러서며 응수했다."], ["“What do you mean by daring to come here?”", "\"감히 여기 올 생각을 하다니, 무슨 속셈이냐?\""]], [["“I didn’t mean no harm, Sir,”", "\"나쁜 뜻은 없었어요, 나리,\""], ["whimpered Rob, putting one hand to his throat, and the knuckles of the other to his eyes.", "롭이 한 손을 목에 대고, 다른 손등을 눈에 갖다 대며 훌쩍였다. \"다시는 안 올게요, 나리."], ["“I’ll never come again, Sir. I only wanted work.”", "그냥 일거리가 필요했을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Work, young Cain that you are!”", "\"일거리라니, 이 어린 카인 같은 녀석아!\""], ["repeated Mr Carker, eyeing him narrowly.", "카커 씨가 그를 찬찬히 훑어보며 되풀이했다."], ["“Ain’t you the idlest vagabond in London?”", "\"넌 런던에서 제일가는 게으른 부랑자 아니냐?\""]], [["The impeachment, while it much affected Mr Toodle Junior, attached to his character so justly, that he could not say a word in denial.", "그 비난은 투들 2세를 무척이나 동요시켰지만, 그의 성격에 너무도 들어맞는 것이라, 그는 부인할 말을 한마디도 하지 못했다."], ["He stood looking at the gentleman, therefore, with a frightened, self-convicted, and remorseful air.", "그래서 그는 겁먹고, 스스로 죄를 인정하며, 뉘우치는 기색으로 그 신사를 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["As to his looking at him, it may be observed that he was fascinated by Mr Carker, and never took his round eyes off him for an instant.", "그를 바라본다는 것으로 말하자면, 그는 카커 씨에게 홀려 버려서 둥근 눈을 한순간도 그에게서 떼지 못했다는 점을 짚어 둘 만하다."]], [["“Ain’t you a thief?”", "\"넌 도둑 아니냐?\""], ["said Mr Carker, with his hands behind him in his pockets.", "카커 씨가 두 손을 등 뒤 주머니에 넣은 채 말했다."]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"아니에요, 나리,\""], ["pleaded Rob.", "롭이 항변했다."]], [["“You are!”", "\"도둑이지!\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I ain’t indeed, Sir,”", "\"정말 아니에요, 나리,\""], ["whimpered Rob.", "롭이 훌쩍였다."], ["“I never did such a thing as thieve, Sir, if you’ll believe me.", "\"절 믿어 주신다면, 전 도둑질 같은 건 한 번도 안 했어요, 나리."], ["I know I’ve been a going wrong, Sir, ever since I took to bird-catching and walking-matching. I’m sure a cove might think,”", "새 잡고 도보 시합 다니기 시작하면서부터 제가 빗나가기 시작한 건 압니다, 나리."], ["said Mr Toodle Junior, with a burst of penitence, “that singing birds was innocent company, but nobody knows what harm is in them little creeturs and what they brings you down to.”", "사람들은 노래하는 새가 순진한 동무라고 생각할지 모르겠지만,\" 투들 2세가 회개의 격정을 터뜨리며 말했다, \"그 자그마한 미물들 속에 무슨 해악이 들었는지, 또 그놈들이 사람을 어디까지 끌어내리는지는 아무도 모릅니다.\""]], [["They seemed to have brought him down to a velveteen jacket and trousers very much the worse for wear, a particularly small red waistcoat like a gorget, an interval of blue check, and the hat before mentioned.", "그놈들은 그를 몹시 낡아 빠진 골덴 윗옷과 바지, 목가리개처럼 생긴 유난히 작은 빨간 조끼, 사이로 드러나는 파란 체크무늬 천, 그리고 앞서 말한 모자로까지 끌어내린 듯했다."]], [["“I ain’t been home twenty times since them birds got their will of me,”", "\"그놈의 새들이 절 제멋대로 다룬 뒤로 집에 들어간 게 스무 번도 안 돼요,\""], ["said Rob, “and that’s ten months.", "롭이 말했다, \"그게 열 달째예요."], ["How can I go home when everybody’s miserable to see me!", "다들 절 보면 비참해하는데 어떻게 집에 들어가겠어요!"], ["I wonder,”", "제 생각엔,\""], ["said Biler, blubbering outright, and smearing his eyes with his coat-cuff, “that I haven’t been and drownded myself over and over again.”", "바일러가 아예 엉엉 울며 외투 소맷부리로 눈을 문질러 댔다, \"제가 몇 번이고 물에 빠져 죽지 않은 게 신기할 정도라니까요.\""]], [["All of which, including his expression of surprise at not having achieved this last scarce performance, the boy said, just as if the teeth of Mr Carker drew it out of him, and he had no power of concealing anything with that battery of attraction in full play.", "이 마지막의, 그 좀처럼 해내기 힘든 일을 결국 못 해냈다는 데 대한 놀라움의 표현까지 포함하여, 그 아이는, 마치 카커 씨의 이가 그것을 자기에게서 끌어내기라도 하는 듯, 그 매혹의 포대가 활짝 가동되는 한 무엇 하나 숨길 힘이 없다는 듯이 말해 버렸다."]], [["“You’re a nice young gentleman!”", "\"넌 참 훌륭한 도련님이로구나!\""], ["said Mr Carker, shaking his head at him.", "카커 씨가 그를 향해 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."], ["“There’s hemp-seed sown for you, my fine fellow!”", "\"네 놈을 위해 삼씨가 뿌려져 있다, 이 멋진 녀석아!\""]], [["“I’m sure, Sir,”", "* 삼씨: 교수형에 쓰는 밧줄(삼)을 만들 씨앗이라는 뜻으로, 교수형에 처해질 운명임을 빗댄 말."], ["returned the wretched Biler, blubbering again, and again having recourse to his coat-cuff: “I shouldn’t care, sometimes, if it was growed too.", "\"정말이지, 나리,\" 가엾은 바일러가 다시 엉엉 울며 또 외투 소맷부리에 의지하여 응수했다, \"어떤 때는 그게 자라 버려도 상관없을 것 같아요."], ["My misfortunes all began in wagging, Sir; but what could I do, exceptin’ wag?”", "제 불운은 전부 학교 빼먹는 데서 시작됐어요, 나리. 하지만 빼먹는 것 말고 제가 뭘 할 수 있었겠어요?\""]], [["“Excepting what?”", "\"뭘 말고라고?\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Wag, Sir.", "\"빼먹는 거요, 나리."], ["Wagging from school.”", "학교를 빼먹는 거요.\""]], [["“Do you mean pretending to go there, and not going?”", "\"학교에 가는 척하면서 안 가는 걸 말하는 거냐?\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Sir, that’s wagging, Sir,”", "\"네, 나리, 그게 빼먹는 거예요, 나리,\""], ["returned the quondam Grinder, much affected.", "한때 그라인더였던 아이가 몹시 감동한 듯 응수했다."], ["“I was chivied through the streets, Sir, when I went there, and pounded when I got there.", "\"거기 가려고 하면 길거리에서 쫓겨 다녔고요, 나리, 막상 가면 두들겨 맞았어요."], ["So I wagged, and hid myself, and that began it.”", "그래서 빼먹고, 숨어 다녔고, 그게 시작이었어요.\""]], [["“And you mean to tell me,”", "\"그러니까 너는 나에게,\""], ["said Mr Carker, taking him by the throat again, holding him out at arm’s-length, and surveying him in silence for some moments, “that you want a place, do you?”", "카커 씨가 다시 그의 목덜미를 잡아 팔 길이만큼 멀찍이 들고는 몇 초 동안 말없이 살피며 말했다, \"일자리를 원한다, 이 말이지?\""]], [["“I should be thankful to be tried, Sir,”", "\"한번 써 주시면 감사하겠습니다, 나리,\""], ["returned Toodle Junior, faintly.", "투들 2세가 가냘프게 응수했다."]], [["Mr Carker the Manager pushed him backward into a corner—the boy submitting quietly, hardly venturing to breathe, and never once removing his eyes from his face—and rang the bell.", "지배인 카커 씨는 그를 한쪽 구석으로 밀어붙였다—그 아이는 숨도 거의 쉬지 못하면서 조용히 순응했고, 단 한 번도 그의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않았다—그러고는 종을 울렸다."]], [["“Tell Mr Gills to come here.”", "\"길스 씨에게 이리 오시라고 전하게.\""]], [["Mr Perch was too deferential to express surprise or recognition of the figure in the corner: and Uncle Sol appeared immediately.", "퍼치 씨는 구석에 있는 그 인물을 보고 놀라움이나 알아본 기색을 내비치기에는 너무 공손했다. 그리고 솔 아저씨가 곧바로 나타났다."]], [["“Mr Gills!”", "\"길스 씨!\""], ["said Carker, with a smile, “sit down. How do you do?", "카커가 미소 지으며 말했다, \"앉으십시오."], ["You continue to enjoy your health, I hope?”", "안녕하셨습니까? 여전히 건강하시겠지요?\""]], [["“Thank you, Sir,”", "\"고맙습니다, 나리,\""], ["returned Uncle Sol, taking out his pocket-book, and handing over some notes as he spoke.", "솔 아저씨가 말하면서 수첩을 꺼내, 지폐 몇 장을 건넸다."], ["“Nothing ails me in body but old age.", "\"몸은 노쇠한 것 말고는 아무 탈 없습니다."], ["Twenty-five, Sir.”", "스물다섯입니다, 나리.\""]], [["“You are as punctual and exact, Mr Gills,”", "\"길스 씨, 당신은 시간에 정확하고 정밀하시군요,\""], ["replied the smiling Manager, taking a paper from one of his many drawers, and making an endorsement on it, while Uncle Sol looked over him, “as one of your own chronometers.", "미소 짓는 지배인이 자기의 수많은 서랍 가운데 하나에서 서류 한 장을 꺼내 거기에 배서를 하면서—솔 아저씨가 그 위로 들여다보는 동안—응수했다, \"꼭 당신네 정밀시계 중 하나처럼요."], ["Quite right.”", "아주 정확합니다.\""]], [["“The Son and Heir has not been spoken, I find by the list, Sir,”", "\"명단을 보니 '아들이자 후계자'호의 소식이 전해진 바 없는 것 같습니다만, 나리,\""], ["said Uncle Sol, with a slight addition to the usual tremor in his voice.", "솔 아저씨가 평소의 떨림에 약간을 더한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“The Son and Heir has not been spoken,” returned Carker.", "\"'아들이자 후계자'호의 소식은 전해진 바 없습니다,\""], ["“There seems to have been tempestuous weather, Mr Gills, and she has probably been driven out of her course.”", "카커가 응수했다. \"거센 폭풍우가 있었던 모양이고, 길스 씨, 그 배는 아마 항로에서 밀려 나갔을 겁니다.\""]], [["“She is safe, I trust in Heaven!”", "\"하늘에 비옵건대, 그 배가 무사하기를!\""], ["said old Sol.", "늙은 솔이 말했다."]], [["“She is safe, I trust in Heaven!”", "\"하늘에 비옵건대, 그 배가 무사하기를!\""], ["assented Mr Carker in that voiceless manner of his: which made the observant young Toodle tremble again.", "카커 씨가 그 특유의 소리 없는 방식으로 맞장구쳤는데, 그 모습은 눈썰미 있는 어린 투들을 다시 한번 떨게 만들었다."], ["“Mr Gills,”", "\"길스 씨,\""], ["he added aloud, throwing himself back in his chair, “you must miss your nephew very much?”", "그가 의자에 몸을 젖히며 소리 내어 덧붙였다, \"조카가 무척 그리우시겠군요?\""]], [["Uncle Sol, standing by him, shook his head and heaved a deep sigh.", "곁에 서 있던 솔 아저씨는 고개를 가로젓고 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“Mr Gills,”", "\"길스 씨,\""], ["said Carker, with his soft hand playing round his mouth, and looking up into the Instrument-maker’s face, “it would be company to you to have a young fellow in your shop just now, and it would be obliging me if you would give one house-room for the present.", "카커가 부드러운 손으로 자기 입가를 매만지며, 계기 제작자의 얼굴을 올려다보면서 말했다, \"지금 같은 때에 당신 가게에 젊은 친구가 하나 있으면 적적함을 달래 줄 테고, 또 당분간 한 사람에게 거처를 내준다면 나에게도 호의를 베푸시는 게 될 텐데요."], ["No, to be sure,”", "아, 물론,\""], ["he added quickly, in anticipation of what the old man was going to say, “there’s not much business doing there, I know; but you can make him clean the place out, polish up the instruments; drudge, Mr Gills.", "노인이 무슨 말을 하려는지 미리 짐작하고 그가 재빨리 덧붙였다, \"거기에 거래할 일이 많지 않다는 건 압니다. 하지만 그자에게 가게 청소를 시키고, 계기들을 닦게 하고, 막일을 시키면 되지요, 길스 씨."], ["That’s the lad!”", "바로 저 녀석입니다!\""]], [["Sol Gills pulled down his spectacles from his forehead to his eyes, and looked at Toodle Junior standing upright in the corner: his head presenting the appearance (which it always did) of having been newly drawn out of a bucket of cold water; his small waistcoat rising and falling quickly in the play of his emotions; and his eyes intently fixed on Mr Carker, without the least reference to his proposed master.", "솔 길스는 안경을 이마에서 눈으로 끌어내려, 구석에 똑바로 서 있는 투들 2세를 보았다. 그 아이의 머리는 (늘 그렇듯) 막 찬물 한 동이에서 끄집어낸 것 같은 모양새였고, 작은 조끼는 감정의 동요 속에서 빠르게 오르내렸으며, 두 눈은 자기의 주인이 될 사람은 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고 카커 씨에게 골똘히 고정되어 있었다."]], [["“Will you give him house-room, Mr Gills?”", "\"그에게 거처를 내주시겠습니까, 길스 씨?\""], ["said the Manager.", "지배인이 말했다."]], [["Old Sol, without being quite enthusiastic on the subject, replied that he was glad of any opportunity, however slight, to oblige Mr Carker, whose wish on such a point was a command: and that the wooden Midshipman would consider himself happy to receive in his berth any visitor of Mr Carker’s selecting.", "늙은 솔은 그 문제에 그다지 열성적이지는 않았으나, 아무리 사소한 것이라도 카커 씨에게 호의를 베풀 기회가 생겨 기쁘다고, 그런 문제에서 그분의 바람은 곧 명령이라고 대답했다. 그리고 나무 해군 소위 후보생은 카커 씨가 골라 보낸 손님이라면 누구든 제 선실에 맞아들이는 것을 행복으로 여길 것이라고 했다."]], [["Mr Carker bared himself to the tops and bottoms of his gums: making the watchful Toodle Junior tremble more and more: and acknowledged the Instrument-maker’s politeness in his most affable manner.", "카커 씨는 잇몸 위아래까지 드러냈는데, 그것은 눈썰미 있는 투들 2세를 점점 더 떨게 만들었다. 그러고는 더없이 상냥한 태도로 계기 제작자의 정중함에 화답했다."]], [["“I’ll dispose of him so, then, Mr Gills,”", "\"그럼 그자는 그렇게 처리하지요, 길스 씨,\""], ["he answered, rising, and shaking the old man by the hand, “until I make up my mind what to do with him, and what he deserves.", "그가 일어서서 노인의 손을 잡고 흔들며 대답했다, \"내가 그자를 어떻게 할지, 또 그자가 무얼 받아 마땅한지 마음을 정할 때까지요."], ["As I consider myself responsible for him, Mr Gills,”", "내가 그자에 대해 책임을 진다고 여기는 만큼, 길스 씨,\""], ["here he smiled a wide smile at Rob, who shook before it: “I shall be glad if you’ll look sharply after him, and report his behaviour to me.", "여기서 그는 롭을 향해 활짝 미소를 지었고, 롭은 그 앞에서 떨었다, \"그자를 빈틈없이 지켜봐 주시고, 그 행실을 나에게 보고해 주시면 고맙겠습니다."], ["I’ll ask a question or two of his parents as I ride home this afternoon—respectable people—to confirm some particulars in his own account of himself; and that done, Mr Gills, I’ll send him round to you to-morrow morning.", "오늘 오후 말을 타고 집으로 가는 길에 그자 부모에게—점잖은 사람들이지요—한두 가지 물어보아, 그자가 자기 자신에 대해 한 이야기 중 몇 가지 세세한 점을 확인하겠습니다. 그게 끝나면, 길스 씨, 내일 아침 그자를 당신께 보내지요."], ["Goodbye!”", "안녕히 계십시오!\""]], [["His smile at parting was so full of teeth, that it confused old Sol, and made him vaguely uncomfortable.", "작별할 때 그의 미소는 어찌나 이로 가득했던지, 늙은 솔을 어리둥절하게 만들고 어쩐지 막연히 불편하게 했다."], ["He went home, thinking of raging seas, foundering ships, drowning men, an ancient bottle of Madeira never brought to light, and other dismal matters.", "그는 사납게 날뛰는 바다와 침몰하는 배, 물에 빠지는 사람들, 결코 빛을 보지 못한 묵은 마데이라 술 한 병, 그 밖의 음울한 것들을 생각하며 집으로 갔다."]], [["“Now, boy!”", "\"자, 얘야!\""], ["said Mr Carker, putting his hand on young Toodle’s shoulder, and bringing him out into the middle of the room.", "카커 씨가 어린 투들의 어깨에 손을 얹고 방 한가운데로 끌어내며 말했다."], ["“You have heard me?”", "\"내 말 들었지?\""]], [["Rob said, “Yes, Sir.”", "롭이 \"네, 나리\" 하고 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps you understand,”", "\"아마 알아들었겠지,\""], ["pursued his patron, “that if you ever deceive or play tricks with me, you had better have drowned yourself, indeed, once for all, before you came here?”", "그의 후원자가 말을 이었다, \"만일 네가 나를 속이거나 나에게 농간을 부린다면, 차라리 이리로 오기 전에 단번에 정말로 물에 빠져 죽었던 편이 나았으리란 걸?\""]], [["There was nothing in any branch of mental acquisition that Rob seemed to understand better than that.", "지적 습득의 어느 분야에서도, 롭이 그 점보다 더 잘 알아듣는 듯한 것은 없었다."]], [["“If you have lied to me,”", "\"네가 나에게,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “in anything, never come in my way again.", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"무엇 하나라도 거짓말을 했다면, 다시는 내 앞에 얼씬도 하지 마라."], ["If not, you may let me find you waiting for me somewhere near your mother’s house this afternoon.", "그렇지 않거든, 오늘 오후 네 어미 집 근처 어딘가에서 나를 기다리고 있도록 해라."], ["I shall leave this at five o’clock, and ride there on horseback.", "나는 여길 다섯 시에 떠나, 말을 타고 그리로 갈 거다."], ["Now, give me the address.”", "자, 주소를 대라.\""]], [["Rob repeated it slowly, as Mr Carker wrote it down.", "롭은 카커 씨가 받아 적는 동안 그것을 천천히 되뇌었다."], ["Rob even spelt it over a second time, letter by letter, as if he thought that the omission of a dot or scratch would lead to his destruction.", "롭은 마치 점 하나, 획 하나라도 빠지면 자기 파멸을 부르기라도 할 듯이, 한 글자 한 글자 두 번이나 또박또박 불러 주기까지 했다."], ["Mr Carker then handed him out of the room; and Rob, keeping his round eyes fixed upon his patron to the last, vanished for the time being.", "카커 씨는 그러고 나서 그를 방 밖으로 내보냈다. 롭은 끝까지 둥근 눈을 후원자에게 고정한 채, 당분간 자취를 감추었다."]], [["Mr Carker the Manager did a great deal of business in the course of the day, and bestowed his teeth upon a great many people.", "지배인 카커 씨는 그날 하루 동안 사업을 엄청나게 처리했고, 수많은 사람에게 자기 이를 베풀었다."], ["In the office, in the court, in the street, and on “Change, they glistened and bristled to a terrible extent.", "사무실에서, 안마당에서, 거리에서, 그리고 거래소에서, 그 이는 무시무시할 정도로 번득이고 곤두섰다."], ["Five o’clock arriving, and with it Mr Carker’s bay horse, they got on horseback, and went gleaming up Cheapside.", "다섯 시가 되고, 그와 함께 카커 씨의 적갈색 말이 오자, 그들은 말에 올라 칩사이드를 따라 번쩍이며 올라갔다."]], [["As no one can easily ride fast, even if inclined to do so, through the press and throng of the City at that hour, and as Mr Carker was not inclined, he went leisurely along, picking his way among the carts and carriages, avoiding whenever he could the wetter and more dirty places in the over-watered road, and taking infinite pains to keep himself and his steed clean.", "그 시각 시내의 혼잡과 인파를 뚫고서는, 설령 그러고 싶다 한들 누구도 쉽게 빨리 달릴 수가 없었고, 또 카커 씨도 그럴 마음이 없었으므로, 그는 짐수레와 마차들 사이로 길을 골라 가며 한가로이 나아갔다. 물을 잔뜩 끼얹은 길에서 더 젖고 더 지저분한 곳은 될 수 있는 한 피하고, 자신과 말을 깨끗이 유지하려고 무진 애를 쓰면서."], ["Glancing at the passersby while he was thus ambling on his way, he suddenly encountered the round eyes of the sleek-headed Rob intently fixed upon his face as if they had never been taken off, while the boy himself, with a pocket-handkerchief twisted up like a speckled eel and girded round his waist, made a very conspicuous demonstration of being prepared to attend upon him, at whatever pace he might think proper to go.", "이렇게 느릿느릿 길을 가며 행인들을 흘끗거리던 그는, 문득 매끈한 머리의 롭이, 마치 한 번도 떼어 본 적 없다는 듯, 자기 얼굴에 골똘히 고정된 둥근 눈과 마주쳤다. 그 아이 자신은 얼룩무늬 뱀장어처럼 비틀어 꼰 손수건을 허리에 두르고서, 그가 어떤 속도로 가기로 작정하든 시중들 준비가 되어 있음을 아주 눈에 띄게 과시하고 있었다."]], [["This attention, however flattering, being one of an unusual kind, and attracting some notice from the other passengers, Mr Carker took advantage of a clearer thoroughfare and a cleaner road, and broke into a trot.", "이런 시중은, 아무리 우쭐한 것이라 해도, 흔치 않은 종류라 다른 행인들의 눈길을 좀 끄는지라, 카커 씨는 좀 더 트인 길과 좀 더 깨끗한 노면을 틈타 속보로 달리기 시작했다."], ["Rob immediately did the same.", "롭도 즉시 같이 그랬다."], ["Mr Carker presently tried a canter; Rob was still in attendance.", "카커 씨가 곧이어 가벼운 구보를 시도했지만, 롭은 여전히 시중을 들었다."], ["Then a short gallop; it was all one to the boy.", "그러더니 짤막한 질주를 했지만, 그 아이에게는 매한가지였다."], ["Whenever Mr Carker turned his eyes to that side of the road, he still saw Toodle Junior holding his course, apparently without distress, and working himself along by the elbows after the most approved manner of professional gentlemen who get over the ground for wagers.", "카커 씨가 그쪽 길가로 눈을 돌릴 때마다, 그는 여전히 투들 2세가 별로 힘들어하는 기색도 없이 제 길을 따라가는 것을 보았다. 그 아이는, 내기를 걸고 땅을 누비는 직업적인 신사들의 가장 인정받는 방식대로 두 팔꿈치를 놀려 가며 몸을 앞으로 밀어 나가고 있었다."]], [["Ridiculous as this attendance was, it was a sign of an influence established over the boy, and therefore Mr Carker, affecting not to notice it, rode away into the neighbourhood of Mr Toodle’s house.", "이런 시중이 우스꽝스럽긴 했어도, 그것은 그 아이에 대해 어떤 영향력이 확립되었다는 표시였으므로, 카커 씨는 그것을 못 본 척하면서 투들 씨 집 근처로 말을 몰아 갔다."], ["On his slackening his pace here, Rob appeared before him to point out the turnings; and when he called to a man at a neighbouring gateway to hold his horse, pending his visit to the buildings that had succeeded Staggs’s Gardens, Rob dutifully held the stirrup, while the Manager dismounted.", "그가 여기서 속도를 늦추자, 롭이 그 앞에 나타나 길모퉁이들을 일러 주었다. 그리고 그가 스택스의 정원 자리에 들어선 건물들을 방문하는 동안 말을 맡아 달라고 근처 대문에 있는 사내를 불렀을 때, 롭은 충직하게 등자를 잡아 주었고, 그동안 지배인은 말에서 내렸다."]], [["“Now, Sir,”", "\"자, 가자, 얘야,\""], ["said Mr Carker, taking him by the shoulder, “come along!”", "카커 씨가 그의 어깨를 잡으며 말했다, \"어서!\""]], [["The prodigal son was evidently nervous of visiting the parental abode; but Mr Carker pushing him on before, he had nothing for it but to open the right door, and suffer himself to be walked into the midst of his brothers and sisters, mustered in overwhelming force round the family tea-table.", "돌아온 탕자는 부모의 거처를 방문하는 것이 분명 긴장되는 모양이었지만, 카커 씨가 그를 앞으로 떠밀자, 그는 별수 없이 옳은 문을 열고, 가족용 차탁 둘레에 압도적인 머릿수로 모여 있는 형제자매들 한가운데로 끌려 들어가는 수밖에 없었다."], ["At sight of the prodigal in the grasp of a stranger, these tender relations united in a general howl, which smote upon the prodigal’s breast so sharply when he saw his mother stand up among them, pale and trembling, with the baby in her arms, that he lent his own voice to the chorus.", "낯선 이의 손아귀에 잡힌 탕자를 보자, 이 다정한 혈육들은 한꺼번에 일제히 울부짖었다. 그 울음은, 어머니가 아기를 품에 안은 채 그들 가운데서 창백하게 떨며 일어서는 것을 보았을 때, 탕자의 가슴을 어찌나 날카롭게 후벼 댔던지, 그는 자기 목소리도 그 합창에 보탰다."]], [["Nothing doubting now that the stranger, if not Mr Ketch in person, was one of that company, the whole of the young family wailed the louder, while its more infantine members, unable to control the transports of emotion appertaining to their time of life, threw themselves on their backs like young birds when terrified by a hawk, and kicked violently.", "이제 그 낯선 이가, 케치 씨 본인은 아닐지언정 그 패거리 중 하나라고 의심해 마지않은 채, 온 어린 식구가 더 크게 울부짖었다. 그중 더 어린 축은 제 나이에 걸맞은 감정의 격발을 가누지 못해, 매에게 놀란 어린 새처럼 벌렁 나자빠져 발을 거세게 버둥거렸다."], ["At length, poor Polly making herself audible, said, with quivering lips, “Oh Rob, my poor boy, what have you done at last!”", "마침내 가엾은 폴리가 겨우 목소리를 내어, 떨리는 입술로 말했다, \"오 롭, 내 가엾은 아들아, 이번엔 또 무슨 짓을 저질렀니!\" * 케치 씨: 17세기 영국의 악명 높은 사형 집행인 잭 케치(Jack Ketch)에서 유래한 말로, 교수형 집행인을 가리킴."]], [["“Nothing, mother,”", "\"아무 짓도 안 했어요, 엄마,\""], ["cried Rob, in a piteous voice, “ask the gentleman!”", "롭이 애처로운 목소리로 외쳤다, \"저 신사분께 여쭤보세요!\""]], [["“Don’t be alarmed,”", "\"놀라지 마십시오,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “I want to do him good.”", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"나는 이 아이를 위해 좋은 일을 해 주려는 겁니다.\""]], [["At this announcement, Polly, who had not cried yet, began to do so.", "이 말이 나오자, 그때까지 울지 않고 있던 폴리가 울기 시작했다."], ["The elder Toodles, who appeared to have been meditating a rescue, unclenched their fists.", "구출이라도 궁리하고 있던 듯하던 손위 투들들은 움켜쥐었던 주먹을 폈다."], ["The younger Toodles clustered round their mother’s gown, and peeped from under their own chubby arms at their desperado brother and his unknown friend.", "손아래 투들들은 어머니의 치맛자락에 둘러붙어, 제 통통한 팔 아래로 무모한 형과 그 정체 모를 친구를 훔쳐보았다."], ["Everybody blessed the gentleman with the beautiful teeth, who wanted to do good.", "모두가 좋은 일을 해 주려는, 아름다운 이를 가진 그 신사에게 축복을 빌었다."]], [["“This fellow,”", "\"이 녀석이,\""], ["said Mr Carker to Polly, giving him a gentle shake, “is your son, eh, Ma’am?”", "카커 씨가 폴리에게 말하며 롭을 가볍게 흔들었다, \"댁의 아들이군요, 그렇지요, 부인?\""]], [["“Yes, Sir,”", "\"네, 나리,\""], ["sobbed Polly, with a curtsey; “yes, Sir.”", "폴리가 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 흐느꼈다, \"네, 나리.\""]], [["“A bad son, I am afraid?”", "\"못된 아들인 모양인데요?\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Never a bad son to me, Sir,” returned Polly.", "\"저에게는 결코 못된 아들이었던 적이 없습니다, 나리,\""], ["“To whom then?” demanded Mr Carker.", "폴리가 응수했다."]], [["“He has been a little wild, Sir,” returned Polly, checking the baby, who was making convulsive efforts with his arms and legs to launch himself on Biler, through the ambient air, “and has gone with wrong companions: but I hope he has seen the misery of that, Sir, and will do well again.”", "\"그럼 누구에게 못됐단 말입니까?\" 카커 씨가 따져 물었다. \"이 애가 좀 거칠게 굴긴 했습니다, 나리,\" 폴리가 응수하며, 팔다리로 발작하듯 버둥거려 바일러를 향해 허공으로 몸을 날리려는 아기를 다독였다, \"그리고 나쁜 친구들과 어울렸지요. 하지만 이제 그 비참함을 깨달았으리라 믿습니다, 나리, 그래서 다시 잘 해낼 거예요.\""]], [["Mr Carker looked at Polly, and the clean room, and the clean children, and the simple Toodle face, combined of father and mother, that was reflected and repeated everywhere about him—and seemed to have achieved the real purpose of his visit.", "카커 씨는 폴리를, 그리고 깨끗한 방을, 깨끗한 아이들을, 또 아버지와 어머니가 한데 어우러진, 자기 주변 어디서나 비치고 되풀이되는 그 순박한 투들의 얼굴을 보았다—그리고 자기 방문의 진짜 목적을 이룬 듯했다."]], [["“Your husband, I take it, is not at home?”", "\"바깥주인은 집에 안 계신 모양이지요?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“No, Sir,”", "\"네, 나리,\""], ["replied Polly.", "폴리가 대답했다."], ["“He’s down the line at present.”", "\"지금은 노선에 나가 있습니다.\""]], [["The prodigal Rob seemed very much relieved to hear it: though still in the absorption of all his faculties in his patron, he hardly took his eyes from Mr Carker’s face, unless for a moment at a time to steal a sorrowful glance at his mother.", "탕자 롭은 그 말을 듣고 무척 안도하는 기색이었다. 그래도 여전히 온 정신을 자기 후원자에게 빼앗긴 채, 어머니에게 슬픈 눈길을 한 번씩 슬쩍 던지느라 잠깐씩 그러는 것 말고는, 카커 씨의 얼굴에서 거의 눈을 떼지 않았다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “I’ll tell you how I have stumbled on this boy of yours, and who I am, and what I am going to do for him.”", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"내가 어쩌다 댁의 이 아이와 마주치게 됐는지, 또 내가 누구인지, 그리고 이 아이를 위해 무엇을 해 주려는지 말씀드리지요.\""]], [["This Mr Carker did, in his own way; saying that he at first intended to have accumulated nameless terrors on his presumptuous head, for coming to the whereabout of Dombey and Son.", "카커 씨는 이를 자기 나름의 방식으로 풀어놓았다. 처음에는, 돔비와 아들 회사의 소재지까지 찾아온 그 주제넘은 머리통에 이름 모를 공포들을 잔뜩 쌓아 줄 작정이었다고 했다."], ["That he had relented, in consideration of his youth, his professed contrition, and his friends.", "그러나 그의 어린 나이와, 그가 입에 담은 회개와, 그의 가족을 생각해서 마음이 누그러졌다고 했다."], ["That he was afraid he took a rash step in doing anything for the boy, and one that might expose him to the censure of the prudent; but that he did it of himself and for himself, and risked the consequences single-handed; and that his mother’s past connexion with Mr Dombey’s family had nothing to do with it, and that Mr Dombey had nothing to do with it, but that he, Mr Carker, was the be-all and the end-all of this business.", "이 아이를 위해 무엇이든 해 주는 것이 경솔한 처사일까 봐, 신중한 이들의 비난을 살지도 모를 처사일까 봐 걱정되긴 하나, 자기는 이를 스스로, 자기 자신을 위해 하는 것이며, 그 결과는 혼자서 감수하겠다고 했다. 또 이 아이 어머니가 과거에 돔비 씨 가족과 맺었던 인연은 이 일과 아무 상관이 없고, 돔비 씨도 이 일과 아무 상관이 없으며, 오직 자기, 카커 씨가 이 일의 처음이요 끝이라고 했다."], ["Taking great credit to himself for his goodness, and receiving no less from all the family then present, Mr Carker signified, indirectly but still pretty plainly, that Rob’s implicit fidelity, attachment, and devotion, were for evermore his due, and the least homage he could receive.", "자신의 선행을 크게 자처하고, 그 자리에 있던 온 가족에게서 그에 못지않은 칭송을 받으며, 카커 씨는 에둘러서, 그러나 여전히 꽤 분명하게, 롭의 절대적인 충실과 애착과 헌신이 영원토록 자기에게 마땅히 돌아올 몫이며, 그것이야말로 자기가 받을 수 있는 최소한의 경의라는 뜻을 내비쳤다."], ["And with this great truth Rob himself was so impressed, that, standing gazing on his patron with tears rolling down his cheeks, he nodded his shiny head until it seemed almost as loose as it had done under the same patron’s hands that morning.", "이 위대한 진실에 롭 자신도 어찌나 깊이 감명을 받았던지, 후원자를 바라보며 두 뺨에 눈물을 줄줄 흘린 채, 그 번들거리는 머리를 끄덕거리는데, 그것이 그날 아침 바로 그 후원자의 손아귀에서 그랬던 것만큼이나 헐거워 보일 지경이었다."]], [["Polly, who had passed Heaven knows how many sleepless nights on account of this her dissipated firstborn, and had not seen him for weeks and weeks, could have almost kneeled to Mr Carker the Manager, as to a Good Spirit—in spite of his teeth.", "방탕한 맏아들 때문에 하늘만이 아실 만큼 수많은 밤을 뜬눈으로 지새우고, 또 몇 주 몇 주를 그 아이를 보지 못했던 폴리는, 그 이에도 불구하고, 지배인 카커 씨 앞에 마치 선한 정령 앞이라도 되는 듯 거의 무릎이라도 꿇을 뻔했다."], ["But Mr Carker rising to depart, she only thanked him with her mother’s prayers and blessings; thanks so rich when paid out of the Heart’s mint, especially for any service Mr Carker had rendered, that he might have given back a large amount of change, and yet been overpaid.", "그러나 카커 씨가 떠나려고 일어서자, 그녀는 그저 어미의 기도와 축복으로 그에게 감사할 따름이었다. 마음의 조폐국에서 찍어 낸 그런 감사는, 특히 카커 씨가 베푼 어떤 봉사에 대해서라면 너무도 값진 것이어서, 그는 거스름돈으로 큰 액수를 돌려주고도 여전히 받은 것이 넘쳤을 것이다."]], [["As that gentleman made his way among the crowding children to the door, Rob retreated on his mother, and took her and the baby in the same repentant hug.", "그 신사가 우글거리는 아이들 사이로 문을 향해 길을 헤쳐 가는 동안, 롭은 어머니에게로 물러서서, 어머니와 아기를 한 번에 회개의 포옹으로 끌어안았다."]], [["“I’ll try hard, dear mother, now.", "\"이제 정말 열심히 해 볼게요, 사랑하는 엄마."], ["Upon my soul I will!”", "맹세코 그럴게요!\""], ["said Rob.", "롭이 말했다."]], [["“Oh do, my dear boy!", "\"오, 그래 다오, 내 아들아!"], ["I am sure you will, for our sakes and your own!”", "우리를 위해서, 또 너 자신을 위해서, 꼭 그러리라 믿는다!\""], ["cried Polly, kissing him.", "폴리가 그에게 입맞추며 외쳤다."], ["“But you’re coming back to speak to me, when you have seen the gentleman away?”", "\"그런데 저 신사분을 배웅하고 나면, 나한테 이야기하러 돌아올 거지?\""]], [["“I don’t know, mother.”", "\"모르겠어요, 엄마.\""], ["Rob hesitated, and looked down.", "롭이 망설이며 고개를 떨구었다."], ["“Father—when’s he coming home?”", "\"아버지는—언제 집에 오세요?\""]], [["“Not till two o’clock to-morrow morning.”", "\"내일 새벽 두 시나 돼야 온단다.\""]], [["“I’ll come back, mother dear!”", "\"돌아올게요, 사랑하는 엄마!\""], ["cried Rob.", "롭이 외쳤다."], ["And passing through the shrill cry of his brothers and sisters in reception of this promise, he followed Mr Carker out.", "그리고 이 약속을 맞이하는 형제자매들의 날카로운 환호성을 헤치고, 그는 카커 씨를 따라 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["said Mr Carker, who had heard this.", "이 말을 들은 카커 씨가 말했다."], ["“You have a bad father, have you?”", "\"넌 못된 아버지를 두었느냐?\""]], [["“No, Sir!”", "\"아니에요, 나리!\""], ["returned Rob, amazed.", "롭이 깜짝 놀라 응수했다."], ["“There ain’t a better nor a kinder father going, than mine is.”", "\"우리 아버지보다 더 좋고 더 다정한 아버지는 세상에 없어요.\""]], [["“Why don’t you want to see him then?”", "\"그럼 왜 아버지를 보고 싶어 하지 않느냐?\""], ["inquired his patron.", "후원자가 물었다."]], [["“There’s such a difference between a father and a mother, Sir,”", "\"아버지하고 어머니는 너무 달라서요, 나리,\""], ["said Rob, after faltering for a moment.", "롭이 잠시 머뭇거리다 말했다."], ["“He couldn’t hardly believe yet that I was doing to do better—though I know he’d try to—but a mother—she always believes what’s good, Sir; at least, I know my mother does, God bless her!”", "\"아버지는 제가 더 잘 해 보려 한다는 걸 아직 거의 믿어 주지 못하실 거예요—믿어 주려고 애는 쓰시겠지만요—하지만 어머니는—어머니는 늘 좋은 것을 믿어 주세요, 나리. 적어도 제 어머니는 그러세요, 신의 가호가 있으시길!\""]], [["Mr Carker’s mouth expanded, but he said no more until he was mounted on his horse, and had dismissed the man who held it, when, looking down from the saddle steadily into the attentive and watchful face of the boy, he said:", "카커 씨의 입이 쩍 벌어졌지만, 그는 말 위에 올라타고, 말을 잡아 준 사내를 보낼 때까지 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러고는 안장에서 내려다보며, 주의 깊고 빈틈없이 살피는 그 아이의 얼굴을 똑바로 응시하면서 말했다."]], [["“You’ll come to me tomorrow morning, and you shall be shown where that old gentleman lives; that old gentleman who was with me this morning; where you are going, as you heard me say.”", "\"내일 아침 나에게 오너라. 그러면 너에게 그 노신사가 사는 곳을 보여 줄 테니. 오늘 아침 나와 함께 있던 그 노신사 말이다. 너도 내가 말하는 걸 들었듯, 네가 갈 곳이다.\""]], [["“Yes, Sir,”", "\"네, 나리,\""], ["returned Rob.", "롭이 응수했다."]], [["“I have a great interest in that old gentleman, and in serving him, you serve me, boy, do you understand?", "\"나는 그 노신사에게 큰 관심을 두고 있다. 그러니 그분을 섬기는 것이 곧 나를 섬기는 것이다, 얘야, 알아듣겠느냐?"], ["Well,”", "옳지,\""], ["he added, interrupting him, for he saw his round face brighten when he was told that: “I see you do.", "그가 말을 가로막으며 덧붙였다. 그 말을 듣자 아이의 둥근 얼굴이 환해지는 것을 보았기 때문이다, \"알아듣는구나."], ["I want to know all about that old gentleman, and how he goes on from day to day—for I am anxious to be of service to him—and especially who comes there to see him.", "나는 그 노신사에 관한 모든 것을, 그리고 그분이 날마다 어떻게 지내시는지를 알고 싶다—내가 그분께 도움이 되고 싶어 그러는 것이니까—특히 누가 그분을 보러 거기 오는지를."], ["Do you understand?”", "알아듣겠느냐?\""]], [["Rob nodded his steadfast face, and said “Yes, Sir,”", "롭은 그 한결같은 얼굴로 끄덕이며, 다시 \"네, 나리\""], ["again.", "하고 말했다."]], [["“I should like to know that he has friends who are attentive to him, and that they don’t desert him—for he lives very much alone now, poor fellow; but that they are fond of him, and of his nephew who has gone abroad.", "\"나는 그분에게 그분을 살뜰히 챙기는 벗들이 있다는 것, 또 그들이 그분을 저버리지 않는다는 것을 알고 싶다—그분은 이제 무척 외롭게 사시거든, 가엾은 양반. 하지만 그들이 그분을, 또 외국으로 떠난 그분의 조카를 좋아한다는 것을 말이다."], ["There is a very young lady who may perhaps come to see him.", "어쩌면 아주 젊은 아가씨가 한 분 그분을 보러 올지도 모른다."], ["I want particularly to know all about her.”", "나는 특히 그 아가씨에 관한 모든 것을 알고 싶다.\""]], [["“I’ll take care, Sir,”", "\"잘 살피겠습니다, 나리,\""], ["said the boy.", "아이가 말했다."]], [["“And take care,”", "\"그리고 명심해라,\""], ["returned his patron, bending forward to advance his grinning face closer to the boy’s, and pat him on the shoulder with the handle of his whip: “take care you talk about affairs of mine to nobody but me.”", "후원자가 씩 웃는 얼굴을 아이에게 더 가까이 들이밀며 몸을 앞으로 숙이고, 채찍 손잡이로 그의 어깨를 토닥이며 응수했다, \"내 일에 관해서는 나 말고는 그 누구에게도 발설하지 않도록 명심하란 말이다.\""]], [["“To nobody in the world, Sir,”", "\"세상 어느 누구에게도 안 합니다, 나리,\""], ["replied Rob, shaking his head.", "롭이 고개를 저으며 대답했다."]], [["“Neither there,”", "\"저기서도 안 되고,\""], ["said Mr Carker, pointing to the place they had just left, “nor anywhere else.", "카커 씨가 그들이 방금 떠나온 곳을 가리키며 말했다, \"다른 어디서도 안 된다."], ["I’ll try how true and grateful you can be. I’ll prove you!”", "네가 얼마나 진실하고 고마워할 줄 아는지 시험해 보겠다."], ["Making this, by his display of teeth and by the action of his head, as much a threat as a promise, he turned from Rob’s eyes, which were nailed upon him as if he had won the boy by a charm, body and soul, and rode away.", "너를 증명해 보이겠다!\" 이를 드러내고 고개를 놀려, 이를 약속인 동시에 위협으로 만들면서, 그는 마치 마법으로 그 아이의 몸과 영혼을 사로잡기라도 한 듯 자기에게 못 박혀 있는 롭의 눈에서 시선을 돌려, 말을 몰아 떠나갔다."], ["But again becoming conscious, after trotting a short distance, that his devoted henchman, girt as before, was yielding him the same attendance, to the great amusement of sundry spectators, he reined up, and ordered him off.", "그러나 잠시 속보로 달린 뒤, 다시금 자기의 충직한 부하가 아까처럼 띠를 두르고 똑같은 시중을 들고 있음을, 그것도 여러 구경꾼을 무척 즐겁게 하면서 그러고 있음을 알아채고는, 고삐를 당겨 그를 쫓아 보냈다."], ["To ensure his obedience, he turned in the saddle and watched him as he retired.", "그가 복종하도록, 그는 안장 위에서 몸을 돌려 그가 물러가는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["It was curious to see that even then Rob could not keep his eyes wholly averted from his patron’s face, but, constantly turning and turning again to look after him, involved himself in a tempest of buffetings and jostlings from the other passengers in the street: of which, in the pursuit of the one paramount idea, he was perfectly heedless.", "그때조차 롭이 후원자의 얼굴에서 눈을 온전히 돌리지 못하고, 끊임없이 돌아보고 또 돌아보며 그를 뒤좇아 바라보다가, 거리의 다른 행인들에게 떠밀리고 부딪치는 회오리에 휘말리는 광경은 진기했다. 그러나 그 단 하나의 압도적인 생각을 좇느라, 그는 그런 것에 전혀 아랑곳하지 않았다."]], [["Mr Carker the Manager rode on at a foot-pace, with the easy air of one who had performed all the business of the day in a satisfactory manner, and got it comfortably off his mind.", "지배인 카커 씨는, 그날 하루의 모든 일을 흡족하게 마치고 마음에서 편안히 떨쳐 낸 사람 같은 느긋한 분위기로, 걸음 속도에 맞춰 말을 몰아 나아갔다."], ["Complacent and affable as man could be, Mr Carker picked his way along the streets and hummed a soft tune as he went.", "사람으로서 더할 나위 없이 만족스럽고 상냥하게, 카커 씨는 거리를 따라 길을 골라 가며 부드러운 가락을 흥얼거렸다."], ["He seemed to purr, he was so glad.", "어찌나 기뻤던지 그는 고양이처럼 가르랑거리는 듯했다."]], [["And in some sort, Mr Carker, in his fancy, basked upon a hearth too.", "그리고 어떤 의미에서, 카커 씨는 상상 속에서 화롯가에서도 일광욕을 즐기고 있었다."], ["Coiled up snugly at certain feet, he was ready for a spring, Or for a tear, or for a scratch, or for a velvet touch, as the humour took him and occasion served.", "어떤 발치에 아늑하게 몸을 도사린 채, 그는 기분 내키는 대로, 또 기회가 닿는 대로, 덮칠 준비도, 할퀼 준비도, 또는 벨벳 같은 부드러운 손길을 내밀 준비도 되어 있었다."], ["Was there any bird in a cage, that came in for a share of his regards?", "그의 눈길 한몫을 받게 될 새장 속의 새라도 있었던가?"]], [["“A very young lady!”", "\"아주 젊은 아가씨라!\""], ["thought Mr Carker the Manager, through his song.", "지배인 카커 씨가 노래 속에서 생각했다. \"그래!"], ["“Ay! when I saw her last, she was a little child.", "내가 그 아가씨를 마지막으로 봤을 때, 그 애는 자그마한 어린아이였지."], ["With dark eyes and hair, I recollect, and a good face; a very good face!", "검은 눈에 검은 머리였던 게 기억나는군, 그리고 고운 얼굴이었지. 아주 고운 얼굴!"], ["I daresay she’s pretty.”", "분명 예쁠 거야.\""]], [["More affable and pleasant yet, and humming his song until his many teeth vibrated to it, Mr Carker picked his way along, and turned at last into the shady street where Mr Dombey’s house stood.", "더욱더 상냥하고 즐거워하며, 자기 노래를 흥얼거려 그 수많은 이가 거기에 떨릴 정도로 흥얼대면서, 카커 씨는 길을 골라 가다가, 마침내 돔비 씨의 집이 서 있는 그늘진 거리로 접어들었다."], ["He had been so busy, winding webs round good faces, and obscuring them with meshes, that he hardly thought of being at this point of his ride, until, glancing down the cold perspective of tall houses, he reined in his horse quickly within a few yards of the door.", "그는 고운 얼굴들 둘레에 거미줄을 치고 그것을 그물로 가리느라 어찌나 분주했던지, 키 큰 집들의 차가운 원근 풍경을 흘끗 내려다보다가 문에서 몇 야드 떨어진 곳에서 재빨리 말을 멈춰 세우기 전까지는, 자기가 이 길의 이 지점에 와 있다는 것조차 거의 생각지 못하고 있었다."], ["But to explain why Mr Carker reined in his horse quickly, and what he looked at in no small surprise, a few digressive words are necessary.", "그러나 카커 씨가 왜 재빨리 말을 멈춰 세웠는지, 또 적잖이 놀라서 무엇을 바라보았는지를 설명하려면, 옆길로 새는 몇 마디가 필요하다."]], [["Mr Toots, emancipated from the Blimber thraldom and coming into the possession of a certain portion of his worldly wealth, “which,” as he had been wont, during his last half-year’s probation, to communicate to Mr Feeder every evening as a new discovery, “the executors couldn’t keep him out of”", "블림버의 속박에서 풀려나 자기 세속적 재산의 일정 몫을 소유하게 된 투츠 씨는—그 재산은, 그가 마지막 반년의 수습 기간 동안 매일 저녁 새로운 발견이라도 되는 양 피더 씨에게 알리곤 했듯이, \"유언 집행인들이 도저히 그에게 안 줄 수 없는\""], ["had applied himself with great diligence, to the science of Life.", "것이었다—삶이라는 학문에 무척이나 부지런히 매달려 왔다."], ["Fired with a noble emulation to pursue a brilliant and distinguished career, Mr Toots had furnished a choice set of apartments; had established among them a sporting bower, embellished with the portraits of winning horses, in which he took no particle of interest; and a divan, which made him poorly.", "찬란하고 빼어난 이력을 좇으려는 고귀한 경쟁심에 불타올라, 투츠 씨는 멋들어진 거처 한 벌을 마련했다. 그 안에 사냥용 정자를 차려, 자기는 손톱만큼도 관심 없는 우승마들의 초상으로 장식해 두었고, 또 자기를 거북하게 만드는 긴 소파도 하나 들여놓았다."], ["In this delicious abode, Mr Toots devoted himself to the cultivation of those gentle arts which refine and humanise existence, his chief instructor in which was an interesting character called the Game Chicken, who was always to be heard of at the bar of the Black Badger, wore a shaggy white great-coat in the warmest weather, and knocked Mr Toots about the head three times a week, for the small consideration of ten and six per visit.", "이 호화로운 거처에서, 투츠 씨는 삶을 세련되고 인간답게 만드는 그 고상한 기예들을 갈고닦는 데 몰두했는데, 그 분야의 으뜸가는 스승은 게임 치킨이라 불리는 흥미로운 인물이었다. 그 사람은 늘 블랙 배저의 술집에서 소식을 들을 수 있었고, 가장 따뜻한 날씨에도 텁수룩한 흰 외투를 걸쳤으며, 한 번 방문에 십 실링 육 펜스라는 소소한 사례를 받고 일주일에 세 번씩 투츠 씨의 머리를 두들겨 주었다."]], [["The Game Chicken, who was quite the Apollo of Mr Toots’s Pantheon, had introduced to him a marker who taught billiards, a Life Guard who taught fencing, a jobmaster who taught riding, a Cornish gentleman who was up to anything in the athletic line, and two or three other friends connected no less intimately with the fine arts.", "투츠 씨의 만신전에서 그야말로 아폴로 격이었던 게임 치킨은 그에게 당구를 가르치는 점수원, 펜싱을 가르치는 근위 기병, 승마를 가르치는 말 임대업자, 운동 분야라면 무엇이든 능한 콘월 출신 신사, 그리고 못지않게 친밀하게 예술과 관련된 두세 명의 다른 친구들을 소개해 주었다."], ["Under whose auspices Mr Toots could hardly fail to improve apace, and under whose tuition he went to work.", "그들의 후원 아래라면 투츠 씨가 빠르게 실력을 키우지 못할 리 없었고, 그들의 지도 아래 그는 부지런히 정진해 나갔다."]], [["But however it came about, it came to pass, even while these gentlemen had the gloss of novelty upon them, that Mr Toots felt, he didn’t know how, unsettled and uneasy.", "그러나 어떻게 된 노릇인지, 이 신사들이 아직 새것의 광택을 띠고 있는 동안에도, 투츠 씨는 어쩐 영문인지 모르게 마음이 들뜨고 불안해졌다."], ["There were husks in his corn, that even Game Chickens couldn’t peck up; gloomy giants in his leisure, that even Game Chickens couldn’t knock down.", "그의 곡식 속에는 게임 치킨조차 쪼아 낼 수 없는 쭉정이가 들어 있었고, 그의 여가에는 게임 치킨조차 때려눕힐 수 없는 음울한 거인들이 도사리고 있었다."], ["Nothing seemed to do Mr Toots so much good as incessantly leaving cards at Mr Dombey’s door.", "돔비 씨의 집 문에 끊임없이 명함을 남기는 일만큼 투츠 씨에게 좋은 약은 없는 듯했다."], ["No taxgatherer in the British Dominions—that wide-spread territory on which the sun never sets, and where the tax-gatherer never goes to bed—was more regular and persevering in his calls than Mr Toots.", "해가 결코 지지 않고 세금 징수원이 결코 잠자리에 들지 않는, 그 드넓이 펼쳐진 영토, 영국령 전역의 어떤 세금 징수원도 투츠 씨보다 더 규칙적이고 끈질기게 방문하지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["Mr Toots never went upstairs; and always performed the same ceremonies, richly dressed for the purpose, at the hall door.", "투츠 씨는 결코 위층으로 올라가지 않았다. 그리고 그 일을 위해 한껏 화려하게 차려입고, 언제나 현관문에서 똑같은 의식을 치렀다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Good morning!”", "안녕하세요!\""], ["would be Mr Toots’s first remark to the servant.", "하는 것이 투츠 씨가 하인에게 건네는 첫마디였다."], ["“For Mr Dombey,”", "\"돔비 씨께 드리는 겁니다.\""], ["would be Mr Toots’s next remark, as he handed in a card.", "하는 것이 명함 한 장을 내밀며 하는 다음 말이었다."], ["“For Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 아가씨께 드리는 겁니다.\""], ["would be his next, as he handed in another.", "하는 것이 또 한 장을 내밀며 하는 그다음 말이었다."]], [["Mr Toots would then turn round as if to go away; but the man knew him by this time, and knew he wouldn’t.", "그러고 나면 투츠 씨는 마치 떠나려는 듯 돌아서곤 했다. 그러나 하인은 이제 그를 알아서, 그가 떠나지 않으리란 걸 알았다."]], [["“Oh, I beg your pardon,”", "\"오, 실례합니다만,\""], ["Mr Toots would say, as if a thought had suddenly descended on him.", "투츠 씨가 마치 문득 어떤 생각이 떠오른 듯 말하곤 했다."], ["“Is the young woman at home?”", "\"그 젊은 여인은 집에 있습니까?\""]], [["The man would rather think she was, but wouldn’t quite know.", "하인은 있는 것 같기는 한데 잘 모르겠다고 하곤 했다."], ["Then he would ring a bell that rang upstairs, and would look up the staircase, and would say, yes, she was at home, and was coming down.", "그러고는 위층으로 울리는 종을 울리고, 계단 위를 올려다보며, 네, 집에 있고 내려오고 있다고 말하곤 했다."], ["Then Miss Nipper would appear, and the man would retire.", "그러면 니퍼 양이 나타났고, 하인은 물러갔다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["How de do?”", "안녕하세요?\""], ["Mr Toots would say, with a chuckle and a blush.", "투츠 씨가 킥킥 웃으며 얼굴을 붉히며 말하곤 했다."]], [["Susan would thank him, and say she was very well.", "수전은 그에게 고맙다고 하며, 자기는 아주 잘 지낸다고 말하곤 했다."]], [["“How’s Diogenes going on?”", "\"디오게네스는 어떻게 지냅니까?\""], ["would be Mr Toots’s second interrogation.", "하는 것이 투츠 씨의 두 번째 질문이곤 했다."]], [["Very well indeed.", "아주 잘 지낸답니다."], ["Miss Florence was fonder and fonder of him every day.", "플로렌스 아가씨는 날이 갈수록 그 개를 더더욱 좋아하신답니다."], ["Mr Toots was sure to hail this with a burst of chuckles, like the opening of a bottle of some effervescent beverage.", "투츠 씨는 으레 이 말을, 무슨 거품 이는 음료수 병의 마개를 따는 것 같은 한바탕의 킥킥거림으로 반겼다."]], [["“Miss Florence is quite well, Sir,”", "\"플로렌스 아가씨는 아주 잘 지내십니다, 나리.\""], ["Susan would add.", "수전이 덧붙이곤 했다."]], [["“Oh, it’s of no consequence, thank’ee,”", "\"오, 별일 아닙니다, 고맙습니다.\""], ["was the invariable reply of Mr Toots; and when he had said so, he always went away very fast.", "하는 것이 투츠 씨의 한결같은 대답이었다. 그리고 그 말을 하고 나면, 그는 언제나 아주 빠르게 떠나가곤 했다."]], [["Now it is certain that Mr Toots had a filmy something in his mind, which led him to conclude that if he could aspire successfully in the fulness of time, to the hand of Florence, he would be fortunate and blest.", "자, 투츠 씨의 마음속에는 어떤 어렴풋한 것이 있어, 만일 때가 무르익었을 때 자기가 플로렌스의 손을 얻으려는 열망을 성공적으로 이룰 수 있다면, 자기는 운 좋고 복받은 사람이 되리라는 결론으로 그를 이끌었음이 분명하다."], ["It is certain that Mr Toots, by some remote and roundabout road, had got to that point, and that there he made a stand.", "투츠 씨가 어떤 멀고 에두른 길을 거쳐 그 지점에 이르렀고, 거기서 발걸음을 멈춰 섰음이 분명하다. 그의 마음은 상처 입었다."], ["His heart was wounded; he was touched; he was in love.", "그는 가슴이 동했다. 그는 사랑에 빠졌다."], ["He had made a desperate attempt, one night, and had sat up all night for the purpose, to write an acrostic on Florence, which affected him to tears in the conception.", "그는 어느 날 밤, 플로렌스에 관한 이합체시를 쓰겠다는 일념으로 필사적인 시도를 해, 그 일을 위해 밤을 꼬박 새우기도 했는데, 착상하면서 스스로 눈물지을 만큼 감동했다."], ["But he never proceeded in the execution further than the words “For when I gaze,”—the flow of imagination in which he had previously written down the initial letters of the other seven lines, deserting him at that point.", "그러나 그는 실행에서 \"내가 바라볼 때면\"이라는 말 이상으로는 나아가지 못했다—앞서 나머지 일곱 행의 첫 글자들을 적어 두었던 그 상상의 흐름이, 바로 그 지점에서 그를 저버린 것이다. * 이합체시(아크로스틱): 각 행의 첫 글자를 위에서 아래로 이으면 어떤 단어나 이름이 되도록 지은 시."]], [["Beyond devising that very artful and politic measure of leaving a card for Mr Dombey daily, the brain of Mr Toots had not worked much in reference to the subject that held his feelings prisoner.", "돔비 씨에게 매일 명함을 남긴다는 그 아주 교묘하고 약삭빠른 방책을 궁리해 낸 것 말고는, 투츠 씨의 두뇌는 자기 감정을 사로잡은 그 주제와 관련해 그다지 많이 작동하지 않았다."], ["But deep consideration at length assured Mr Toots that an important step to gain, was, the conciliation of Miss Susan Nipper, preparatory to giving her some inkling of his state of mind.", "그러나 깊이 숙고한 끝에 투츠 씨는 마침내, 손에 넣어야 할 중요한 한 걸음이란 곧 수전 니퍼 양의 환심을 사는 것이며, 그것이 그녀에게 자기 심중의 일단을 넌지시 알리기 위한 사전 준비라는 확신에 이르렀다."]], [["A little light and playful gallantry towards this lady seemed the means to employ in that early chapter of the history, for winning her to his interests.", "이 이야기의 그 초기 장에서, 이 숙녀를 자기 편으로 끌어들이기 위해 동원할 수단은, 그녀를 향한 약간의 가볍고 장난기 어린 구애인 듯싶었다."], ["Not being able quite to make up his mind about it, he consulted the Chicken—without taking that gentleman into his confidence; merely informing him that a friend in Yorkshire had written to him (Mr Toots) for his opinion on such a question. The Chicken replying that his opinion always was, “Go in and win,”", "그것에 대해 도무지 마음을 정하지 못한 그는 치킨에게 의논을—그 신사를 속내까지 끌어들이지는 않고, 그저 요크셔의 한 친구가 그(투츠 씨)에게 그런 문제에 대한 의견을 묻는 편지를 보냈다고만 알리면서—했다."], ["and further, “When your man’s before you and your work cut out, go in and do it,” Mr Toots considered this a figurative way of supporting his own view of the case, and heroically resolved to kiss Miss Nipper next day.", "치킨이, 자기 의견은 언제나 \"들어가서 이겨라\"이며, 더 나아가 \"네 상대가 네 앞에 있고 할 일이 정해졌으면, 들어가서 해치워라\"라고 대답하자, 투츠 씨는 이것을 자기 견해를 비유적으로 뒷받침해 주는 말로 여기고, 이튿날 니퍼 양에게 입맞추리라 영웅적으로 결심했다."]], [["Upon the next day, therefore, Mr Toots, putting into requisition some of the greatest marvels that Burgess and Co. had ever turned out, went off to Mr Dombey’s upon this design.", "그리하여 이튿날 투츠 씨는, 버지스 상회가 여태껏 만들어 낸 것 중 가장 위대한 경이로운 작품 몇 점을 동원하여, 이 계획을 품고 돔비 씨 댁으로 향했다."], ["But his heart failed him so much as he approached the scene of action, that, although he arrived on the ground at three o’clock in the afternoon, it was six before he knocked at the door.", "그러나 작전 무대로 다가갈수록 어찌나 가슴이 콩알만 해졌던지, 오후 세 시에 현장에 도착했음에도 막상 문을 두드린 것은 여섯 시였다."]], [["Everything happened as usual, down to the point where Susan said her young mistress was well, and Mr Toots said it was of no consequence.", "모든 일이 평소처럼 진행되어, 수전이 자기 어린 아가씨는 잘 지내신다고 말하고, 투츠 씨가 별일 아니라고 말하는 지점에 이르렀다."], ["To her amazement, Mr Toots, instead of going off, like a rocket, after that observation, lingered and chuckled.", "그런데 그녀가 깜짝 놀랍게도, 투츠 씨는 그 말 뒤에 로켓처럼 떠나 버리는 대신, 머뭇거리며 킥킥거렸다."]], [["“Perhaps you’d like to walk upstairs, Sir!”", "\"위층으로 올라오시겠어요, 나리!\""], ["said Susan.", "수전이 말했다."]], [["“Well, I think I will come in!”", "\"음, 들어가는 게 좋겠군요!\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["But instead of walking upstairs, the bold Toots made an awkward plunge at Susan when the door was shut, and embracing that fair creature, kissed her on the cheek.", "그러나 위층으로 올라가는 대신, 대담한 투츠는 문이 닫히자 수전에게 어색하게 와락 달려들어, 그 어여쁜 아가씨를 끌어안고 뺨에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Go along with you!”", "\"저리 가요!\""], ["cried Susan, “or Ill tear your eyes out.”", "수전이 외쳤다, \"안 그러면 눈을 후벼 파 버릴 테니.\""]], [["“Just another!”", "\"딱 한 번만 더!\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Go along with you!”", "\"저리 가라니까요!\""], ["exclaimed Susan, giving him a push “Innocents like you, too!", "수전이 그를 밀치며 외쳤다, \"당신처럼 순진한 사람도 참!"], ["Who’ll begin next?", "다음엔 누가 시작할 작정이람?"], ["Go along, Sir!”", "가요, 나리!\""]], [["Susan was not in any serious strait, for she could hardly speak for laughing; but Diogenes, on the staircase, hearing a rustling against the wall, and a shuffling of feet, and seeing through the banisters that there was some contention going on, and foreign invasion in the house, formed a different opinion, dashed down to the rescue, and in the twinkling of an eye had Mr Toots by the leg.", "수전은 정말로 곤경에 처한 것은 아니었으니, 웃음 때문에 말도 제대로 하기 어려울 지경이었기 때문이다. 그러나 계단에 있던 디오게네스는 벽에 무언가 스치는 소리와 발을 끄는 소리를 듣고, 난간 살 사이로 무슨 다툼이 벌어지고 있으며 집안에 외적이 침입했음을 보고는, 다른 견해를 품고, 구출하러 쏜살같이 내달아, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 투츠 씨의 다리를 물어 버렸다."]], [["Susan screamed, laughed, opened the street-door, and ran downstairs; the bold Toots tumbled staggering out into the street, with Diogenes holding on to one leg of his pantaloons, as if Burgess and Co.", "수전은 비명을 지르고, 웃고, 거리로 통하는 문을 열고는 아래층으로 달려 내려갔다. 대담한 투츠는 비틀거리며 거리로 나뒹굴었는데, 디오게네스가 그의 한쪽 바지통을 물고 늘어진 것이, 마치 버지스 상회가 자기 요리사라서 휴일의 흥겨운 식사거리로 그 맛있는 한 조각을 마련해 준 것 같았다."], ["were his cooks, and had provided that dainty morsel for his holiday entertainment; Diogenes shaken off, rolled over and over in the dust, got up again, whirled round the giddy Toots and snapped at him: and all this turmoil Mr Carker, reigning up his horse and sitting a little at a distance, saw to his amazement, issue from the stately house of Mr Dombey.", "떨쳐진 디오게네스는 흙먼지 속에 데굴데굴 굴렀다가 다시 일어나, 어질어질해진 투츠 둘레를 빙글빙글 돌며 그에게 덤벼들었다. 그리고 이 모든 소동이, 조금 떨어진 곳에 말을 세우고 앉아 있던 카커 씨의 눈에, 돔비 씨의 위풍당당한 저택에서 쏟아져 나오는 것을 그는 어리둥절하게 지켜보았다."]], [["Mr Carker remained watching the discomfited Toots, when Diogenes was called in, and the door shut: and while that gentleman, taking refuge in a doorway near at hand, bound up the torn leg of his pantaloons with a costly silk handkerchief that had formed part of his expensive outfit for the advent.", "카커 씨는 풀이 죽은 투츠를 지켜보며 머물러 있었다. 디오게네스가 불려 들어가고 문이 닫힐 때까지, 또 그 신사가 가까운 어느 문간으로 피신하여, 그날의 거사를 위해 마련한 값비싼 차림의 일부였던 호화로운 비단 손수건으로 찢어진 바지통을 동여매는 동안에도."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Sir,”", "\"실례합니다, 나리,\""], ["said Mr Carker, riding up, with his most propitiatory smile.", "카커 씨가 더없이 비위 맞추는 미소를 띠며 말을 몰아 다가와 말했다."], ["“I hope you are not hurt?”", "\"다치지 않으셨기를 바랍니다만?\""]], [["“Oh no, thank you,”", "\"오, 아닙니다, 고맙습니다,\""], ["replied Mr Toots, raising his flushed face, “it’s of no consequence”", "투츠 씨가 상기된 얼굴을 들며 응수했다, \"별일 아닙니다.\""], ["Mr Toots would have signified, if he could, that he liked it very much.", "투츠 씨는 할 수만 있었다면, 자기는 그게 무척 마음에 든다는 뜻을 내비치고 싶었을 것이다."]], [["“If the dog’s teeth have entered the leg, Sir—”", "\"개의 이가 다리에 박혔다면, 나리—\""], ["began Carker, with a display of his own.", "카커가 자기 이를 드러내 보이며 말문을 열었다."]], [["“No, thank you,”", "\"아니요, 고맙습니다,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “it’s all quite right.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"다 멀쩡합니다."], ["It’s very comfortable, thank you.”", "아주 편안한걸요, 고맙습니다.\""]], [["“I have the pleasure of knowing Mr Dombey,”", "\"저는 돔비 씨를 아는 영광을 누리고 있습니다,\""], ["observed Carker.", "카커가 말했다."]], [["“Have you though?”", "\"아, 그러십니까?\""], ["rejoined the blushing Took", "얼굴 붉히는 투츠가 응수했다."]], [["“And you will allow me, perhaps, to apologise, in his absence,”", "\"그리고 어쩌면, 그분이 안 계신 자리에서나마, 이런 변고에 대해 제가 사과드리고,\""], ["said Mr Carker, taking off his hat, “for such a misadventure, and to wonder how it can possibly have happened.”", "카커 씨가 모자를 벗으며 말했다, \"도대체 어쩌다 이런 일이 일어날 수 있었는지 의아해하는 것을 허락해 주시겠지요.\""]], [["Mr Toots is so much gratified by this politeness, and the lucky chance of making friends with a friend of Mr Dombey, that he pulls out his card-case which he never loses an opportunity of using, and hands his name and address to Mr Carker: who responds to that courtesy by giving him his own, and with that they part.", "투츠 씨는 이 정중함에, 그리고 돔비 씨의 친구와 벗이 되는 이 다행스러운 기회에 어찌나 흡족했던지, 한 번도 쓸 기회를 놓치는 법이 없는 명함첩을 꺼내, 카커 씨에게 자기 이름과 주소를 건넸다. 카커 씨는 그 호의에 자기 것을 건네는 것으로 화답했고, 그것으로 두 사람은 헤어졌다."]], [["As Mr Carker picks his way so softly past the house, looking up at the windows, and trying to make out the pensive face behind the curtain looking at the children opposite, the rough head of Diogenes came clambering up close by it, and the dog, regardless of all soothing, barks and growls, and makes at him from that height, as if he would spring down and tear him limb from limb.", "카커 씨가 그토록 부드럽게 그 집을 지나치며, 창문들을 올려다보고, 건너편 아이들을 바라보는 커튼 뒤의 수심 어린 얼굴을 알아보려 애쓰고 있을 때, 디오게네스의 거친 머리가 바로 그 곁으로 기어 올라왔다. 그러더니 그 개는 온갖 달램에도 아랑곳없이, 짖고 으르렁대며 그 높이에서 그에게 덤벼들었는데, 마치 뛰어내려 그를 사지를 찢어발기기라도 할 듯했다."]], [["Well spoken, Di, so near your Mistress!", "잘 짖었다, 디, 네 주인 아가씨와 그토록 가까이 있으니!"], ["Another, and another with your head up, your eyes flashing, and your vexed mouth worrying itself, for want of him!", "또 한 번, 그리고 또 한 번, 머리를 곧추세우고, 두 눈을 번득이며, 그를 물어뜯지 못해 안달이 난 입을 분에 못 이겨 우물거리면서!"], ["Another, as he picks his way along!", "또 한 번, 그가 길을 골라 지나가는 동안에!"], ["You have a good scent, Di,—cats, boy, cats!", "너는 후각이 좋구나, 디—고양이다, 얘야, 고양이!"]], [["# CHAPTER XXIII. Florence solitary, and the Midshipman mysterious", "# CHAPTER XXIII. Florence solitary, and the Midshipman mysterious"]], [["Florence lived alone in the great dreary house, and day succeeded day, and still she lived alone; and the blank walls looked down upon her with a vacant stare, as if they had a Gorgon-like mind to stare her youth and beauty into stone.", "플로렌스는 그 크고 음울한 집에서 홀로 살았다. 하루가 또 하루를 이어 갔으나, 그녀는 여전히 홀로 살았다. 텅 빈 벽들은 멍한 눈길로 그녀를 내려다보았는데, 마치 고르곤처럼 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움을 돌로 굳혀 버리려는 심산이라도 품은 듯했다."]], [["No magic dwelling-place in magic story, shut up in the heart of a thick wood, was ever more solitary and deserted to the fancy, than was her father’s mansion in its grim reality, as it stood lowering on the street: always by night, when lights were shining from neighbouring windows, a blot upon its scanty brightness; always by day, a frown upon its never-smiling face.", "마법의 이야기 속, 울창한 숲 한가운데 갇혀 있는 그 어떤 마법의 거처도, 거리를 향해 험상궂게 솟아 있는 그녀 아버지의 저택만큼 상상 속에서조차 외롭고 버려진 곳은 없었으리라. 밤이면 이웃집 창문에서 불빛이 새어 나올 때마다 그 빈약한 밝음 위에 찍힌 하나의 얼룩이었고, 낮이면 결코 미소 짓지 않는 그 얼굴 위에 새겨진 하나의 찌푸림이었다."]], [["There were not two dragon sentries keeping ward before the gate of this above, as in magic legend are usually found on duty over the wronged innocence imprisoned; but besides a glowering visage, with its thin lips parted wickedly, that surveyed all comers from above the archway of the door, there was a monstrous fantasy of rusty iron, curling and twisting like a petrifaction of an arbour over threshold, budding in spikes and corkscrew points, and bearing, one on either side, two ominous extinguishers, that seemed to say, “Who enter here, leave light behind!”", "마법의 전설 속에서 흔히 부당하게 갇힌 무고한 자를 지키느라 당직을 서는 두 마리 용 파수꾼이 이 집 문 앞을 지키고 있지는 않았다. 그러나 문 위 아치 너머로 모든 방문객을 살피며 가느다란 입술을 사악하게 벌린 험상궂은 형상 말고도, 문지방 위로 정자(亭子)가 돌처럼 굳어 버린 양 휘감기고 비틀린 녹슨 쇠붙이의 괴이한 환상물이 있었으니, 그것은 가시와 나선형 끝으로 싹을 틔우고, 양옆에 하나씩 불길한 촛불 끄개를 달고 있어, \"여기 들어오는 자여, 빛을 버리고 들라!\""], ["There were no talismanic characters engraven on the portal, but the house was now so neglected in appearance, that boys chalked the railings and the pavement—particularly round the corner where the side wall was—and drew ghosts on the stable door; and being sometimes driven off by Mr Towlinson, made portraits of him, in return, with his ears growing out horizontally from under his hat.", "하고 말하는 듯했다. 문에 새겨진 부적 같은 문자는 없었으나, 집의 외양이 이제 어찌나 방치되었던지 아이들이 난간과 보도에—특히 옆벽이 있는 모퉁이 둘레에—분필질을 해 댔고 마구간 문에 유령을 그렸으며, 가끔 타울린슨 씨에게 쫓겨나면 보복으로 그의 초상을 그렸는데, 모자 밑에서 귀가 수평으로 자라난 모습이었다."], ["Noise ceased to be, within the shadow of the roof.", "지붕의 그늘 안에서는 소리가 사라졌다."], ["The brass band that came into the street once a week, in the morning, never brayed a note in at those windows; but all such company, down to a poor little piping organ of weak intellect, with an imbecile party of automaton dancers, waltzing in and out at folding-doors, fell off from it with one accord, and shunned it as a hopeless place.", "일주일에 한 번 아침이면 거리로 들어오던 금관악대도 그 창문 안으로는 단 한 음도 울리지 않았다. 그뿐 아니라 머리가 둔한 가련하고 자그마한 손풍금 하나에, 접이문 사이로 왈츠를 추며 드나드는 멍청한 자동인형 무용단 패거리에 이르기까지, 그런 모든 떠들썩한 무리가 한결같이 그 집을 떠나, 가망 없는 곳으로 여겨 피해 갔다."]], [["The spell upon it was more wasting than the spell that used to set enchanted houses sleeping once upon a time, but left their waking freshness unimpaired.", "그 집에 걸린 마법은, 옛날 옛적 마법의 집들을 잠들게 했으되 깨어났을 때의 생기는 고스란히 남겨 두던 그 마법보다도 더 사람을 좀먹는 것이었다."]], [["The passive desolation of disuse was everywhere silently manifest about it.", "쓰지 않아 생긴 무력한 황폐함이 사방에서 소리 없이 드러나 있었다."], ["Within doors, curtains, drooping heavily, lost their old folds and shapes, and hung like cumbrous palls.", "집 안에서는 커튼들이 묵직하게 늘어진 채 옛 주름과 모양을 잃고, 거추장스러운 관(棺) 덮개처럼 걸려 있었다."], ["Hecatombs of furniture, still piled and covered up, shrunk like imprisoned and forgotten men, and changed insensibly.", "여전히 쌓아 올려 천을 덮어 둔 가구의 산더미들이, 갇혀 잊힌 사람들처럼 쪼그라들며 자기도 모르게 변해 갔다."], ["Mirrors were dim as with the breath of years.", "거울들은 세월의 입김이 서린 듯 뿌옜다."], ["Patterns of carpets faded and became perplexed and faint, like the memory of those years’ trifling incidents.", "양탄자의 무늬는 바래어 흐릿하고 희미해졌으니, 그 세월의 사소한 사건들에 대한 기억과도 같았다."], ["Boards, starting at unwonted footsteps, creaked and shook.", "익숙지 않은 발걸음에 놀란 마룻장들이 삐걱거리며 흔들렸다."], ["Keys rusted in the locks of doors.", "열쇠들은 문의 자물쇠 안에서 녹슬었다."], ["Damp started on the walls, and as the stains came out, the pictures seemed to go in and secrete themselves.", "습기가 벽에 번지기 시작했고, 얼룩이 배어 나오자 그림들은 안으로 들어가 제 모습을 감추는 듯했다."], ["Mildew and mould began to lurk in closets.", "곰팡이와 이끼가 벽장 안에 도사리기 시작했다."], ["Fungus trees grew in corners of the cellars.", "지하실 구석에서는 버섯나무가 자랐다."], ["Dust accumulated, nobody knew whence nor how; spiders, moths, and grubs were heard of every day.", "어디서 어떻게 생기는지 아무도 모르는 먼지가 쌓여 갔고, 거미와 나방과 굼벵이 이야기가 날마다 들렸다."], ["An exploratory blackbeetle now and then was found immovable upon the stairs, or in an upper room, as wondering how he got there.", "이따금 길을 잃고 탐색에 나선 검정딱정벌레가 계단이나 위층 방에서 꼼짝 않고 발견되곤 했는데, 마치 제가 어떻게 거기까지 왔는지 의아해하는 듯했다."], ["Rats began to squeak and scuffle in the night time, through dark galleries they mined behind the panelling.", "쥐들은 밤이면 끽끽거리며 다투기 시작했으니, 벽판 뒤에 파 놓은 어두운 회랑들을 누비고 다녔다."]], [["The dreary magnificence of the state rooms, seen imperfectly by the doubtful light admitted through closed shutters, would have answered well enough for an enchanted abode.", "닫힌 덧문 사이로 들어오는 흐릿한 빛에 어렴풋이 보이는 큰 응접실들의 음울한 화려함은, 마법에 걸린 거처로 손색이 없었으리라."], ["Such as the tarnished paws of gilded lions, stealthily put out from beneath their wrappers; the marble lineaments of busts on pedestals, fearfully revealing themselves through veils; the clocks that never told the time, or, if wound up by any chance, told it wrong, and struck unearthly numbers, which are not upon the dial; the accidental tinklings among the pendant lustres, more startling than alarm-bells; the softened sounds and laggard air that made their way among these objects, and a phantom crowd of others, shrouded and hooded, and made spectral of shape.", "가령 천 덮개 밑에서 슬며시 내민 도금 사자들의 빛바랜 발이라든가, 받침대 위 흉상들의 대리석 윤곽이 베일 너머로 무섭게 제 모습을 드러내는 것이라든가, 결코 시간을 알려 주지 않거나 어쩌다 태엽을 감으면 틀린 시간을 알리며 문자반에도 없는 기괴한 숫자를 치는 시계들이라든가, 매달린 샹들리에 사이에서 우연히 짤랑이는 소리가 경종보다 더 사람을 놀라게 하는 것이라든가, 이런 물건들 사이로, 그리고 천에 싸이고 두건이 씌워져 유령 같은 형상이 된 한 무리의 다른 것들 사이로 흘러드는 누그러진 소리와 더딘 공기가 그러했다."], ["But, besides, there was the great staircase, where the lord of the place so rarely set his foot, and by which his little child had gone up to Heaven.", "그러나 그 밖에도, 이 저택의 주인이 좀처럼 발을 들이지 않았던, 그리고 그의 어린 자식이 천국으로 올라가던 그 큰 계단이 있었다."], ["There were other staircases and passages where no one went for weeks together; there were two closed rooms associated with dead members of the family, and with whispered recollections of them; and to all the house but Florence, there was a gentle figure moving through the solitude and gloom, that gave to every lifeless thing a touch of present human interest and wonder.", "몇 주씩 아무도 다니지 않는 다른 계단과 복도들이 있었고, 죽은 가족들과 결부되어 그들에 대한 속삭이는 추억이 어린 닫힌 방 두 개가 있었다. 그리고 플로렌스를 제외한 온 집 안 사람들에게는, 그 고독과 음울 속을 움직이며 모든 생명 없는 것에 살아 있는 인간의 관심과 경이의 손길을 닿게 하는 한 부드러운 형상이 있었다."]], [["For Florence lived alone in the deserted house, and day succeeded day, and still she lived alone, and the cold walls looked down upon her with a vacant stare, as if they had a Gorgon-like mind to stare her youth and beauty into stone.", "플로렌스는 그 버려진 집에서 홀로 살았고, 하루가 또 하루를 이어 갔으나 여전히 홀로 살았으며, 차가운 벽들은 멍한 눈길로 그녀를 내려다보았는데, 마치 고르곤처럼 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움을 돌로 굳혀 버리려는 심산이라도 품은 듯했다."]], [["The grass began to grow upon the roof, and in the crevices of the basement paving.", "지붕 위에, 그리고 지하층 포석의 틈새에 풀이 돋기 시작했다."], ["A scaly crumbling vegetation sprouted round the window-sills.", "비늘처럼 부스러지는 식물이 창턱 둘레에 싹텄다."], ["Fragments of mortar lost their hold upon the insides of the unused chimneys, and came dropping down.", "쓰지 않는 굴뚝 안쪽에서는 회반죽 조각들이 붙들고 있던 자리를 놓치고 떨어져 내렸다."], ["The two trees with the smoky trunks were blighted high up, and the withered branches domineered above the leaves, Through the whole building white had turned yellow, yellow nearly black; and since the time when the poor lady died, it had slowly become a dark gap in the long monotonous street.", "그을린 둥치의 나무 두 그루는 높은 곳까지 시들어, 마른 가지들이 잎사귀 위로 거만하게 군림했다. 건물 전체에서 흰색이 누렇게, 누런색이 거의 검게 변했고, 그 가련한 부인이 세상을 떠난 그때 이후로, 그 집은 길고 단조로운 거리 한가운데 어두운 틈으로 서서히 변해 갔다."]], [["But Florence bloomed there, like the king’s fair daughter in the story.", "그러나 플로렌스는 그곳에서, 이야기 속 임금의 어여쁜 딸처럼 피어났다. 그녀의 책과 음악과 매일 오는 선생들이 그녀의 유일한 진짜 동무였다."], ["Her books, her music, and her daily teachers, were her only real companions, Susan Nipper and Diogenes excepted: of whom the former, in her attendance on the studies of her young mistress, began to grow quite learned herself, while the latter, softened possibly by the same influences, would lay his head upon the window-ledge, and placidly open and shut his eyes upon the street, all through a summer morning; sometimes pricking up his head to look with great significance after some noisy dog in a cart, who was barking his way along, and sometimes, with an exasperated and unaccountable recollection of his supposed enemy in the neighbourhood, rushing to the door, whence, after a deafening disturbance, he would come jogging back with a ridiculous complacency that belonged to him, and lay his jaw upon the window-ledge again, with the air of a dog who had done a public service.", "수전 니퍼와 다이오지니즈만은 예외였는데, 전자는 어린 여주인의 공부 시중을 들면서 자기도 제법 박식해지기 시작했고, 후자는 아마도 같은 영향으로 누그러졌는지, 여름날 아침 내내 창턱에 머리를 얹고서 거리를 향해 평온하게 눈을 떴다 감았다 하곤 했다. 때로는 수레에 실려 짖어 대며 지나가는 시끄러운 개를 의미심장하게 바라보려고 고개를 쳐들었고, 때로는 동네에 있다고 여기는 가상의 적에 대한 격앙되고 영문 모를 기억이 떠올라 문으로 달려들었다가, 귀가 먹먹할 만큼 한바탕 소동을 벌인 뒤, 자기 특유의 우스꽝스러운 만족감에 젖어 어슬렁어슬렁 돌아와서는, 공공의 봉사라도 마친 개의 태도로 다시 창턱에 턱을 얹곤 했다."]], [["So Florence lived in her wilderness of a home, within the circle of her innocent pursuits and thoughts, and nothing harmed her.", "이렇게 플로렌스는 자기 집이라는 황무지 안에서, 순수한 일과 생각의 울타리 안에서 살았고, 아무것도 그녀를 해치지 못했다."], ["She could go down to her father’s rooms now, and think of him, and suffer her loving heart humbly to approach him, without fear of repulse.", "이제 그녀는 아버지의 방으로 내려가, 그를 생각하고, 사랑하는 마음으로 겸손히 그에게 다가가도 거절당할 두려움이 없었다."], ["She could look upon the objects that had surrounded him in his sorrow, and could nestle near his chair, and not dread the glance that she so well remembered.", "슬픔에 잠긴 그를 둘러쌌던 물건들을 바라볼 수 있었고, 그의 의자 가까이 몸을 기댈 수 있었으며, 그토록 또렷이 기억하는 그 눈길을 두려워하지 않을 수 있었다."], ["She could render him such little tokens of her duty and service, as putting everything in order for him with her own hands, binding little nosegays for table, changing them as one by one they withered and he did not come back, preparing something for him every day, and leaving some timid mark of her presence near his usual seat.", "그녀는 그에게 자기 도리와 봉사의 작은 정표들을 바칠 수 있었으니, 제 손으로 그를 위해 모든 것을 정돈하고, 식탁에 놓을 작은 꽃다발을 묶고, 그것들이 하나하나 시들어도 그가 돌아오지 않으면 갈아 두고, 날마다 그를 위해 무언가를 마련하며, 그가 늘 앉던 자리 가까이에 자기가 다녀갔다는 수줍은 흔적을 남기는 일이었다."], ["Today, it was a little painted stand for his watch; tomorrow she would be afraid to leave it, and would substitute some other trifle of her making not so likely to attract his eye.", "오늘은 그의 시계를 올려놓을 작고 색칠한 받침대였고, 내일이면 그것을 그냥 두기가 겁이 나서, 그의 눈에 그리 띌 것 같지 않은 다른 자질구레한 손수 만든 물건으로 바꿔 놓곤 했다."], ["Waking in the night, perhaps, she would tremble at the thought of his coming home and angrily rejecting it, and would hurry down with slippered feet and quickly beating heart, and bring it away.", "밤중에 깨어나면, 그가 집에 돌아와 화를 내며 그것을 내쳐 버릴 생각에 떨면서, 슬리퍼 신은 발로 가슴을 두근거리며 서둘러 내려가 그것을 치워 오기도 했다."], ["At another time, she would only lay her face upon his desk, and leave a kiss there, and a tear.", "또 어떤 때는 그저 그의 책상에 얼굴을 대고, 거기에 입맞춤 하나와 눈물 하나를 남기고 올 뿐이었다."]], [["Still no one knew of this.", "그래도 아무도 이 일을 알지 못했다."], ["Unless the household found it out when she was not there—and they all held Mr Dombey’s rooms in awe—it was as deep a secret in her breast as what had gone before it.", "그녀가 없을 때 식구들이 알아챘다면 모를까—그리고 그들은 모두 돔비 씨의 방을 두려워했다—그것은 그 이전의 일들만큼이나 그녀 가슴속 깊은 비밀이었다."], ["Florence stole into those rooms at twilight, early in the morning, and at times when meals were served downstairs.", "플로렌스는 해 질 녘, 이른 아침, 그리고 식사가 아래층에서 차려질 때 그 방들로 몰래 들어갔다."], ["And although they were in every nook the better and the brighter for her care, she entered and passed out as quietly as any sunbeam, opting that she left her light behind.", "그리고 비록 그 방들이 구석구석 그녀의 보살핌으로 한결 좋아지고 밝아졌어도, 그녀는 한 줄기 햇살처럼 조용히 들어갔다 나왔으니, 다만 자기의 빛을 뒤에 남겨 둔다는 것만이 달랐다."]], [["Shadowy company attended Florence up and down the echoing house, and sat with her in the dismantled rooms.", "그림자 같은 동무들이 메아리치는 집 안을 오르내리며 플로렌스를 따라다녔고, 가구가 치워진 방들에서 그녀와 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["As if her life were an enchanted vision, there arose out of her solitude ministering thoughts, that made it fanciful and unreal.", "마치 그녀의 삶이 마법에 걸린 환영이기라도 한 듯, 그녀의 고독에서 시중드는 생각들이 일어나, 그 삶을 공상적이고 비현실적인 것으로 만들었다."], ["She imagined so often what her life would have been if her father could have loved her and she had been a favourite child, that sometimes, for the moment, she almost believed it was so, and, borne on by the current of that pensive fiction, seemed to remember how they had watched her brother in his grave together; how they had freely shared his heart between them; how they were united in the dear remembrance of him; how they often spoke about him yet; and her kind father, looking at her gently, told her of their common hope and trust in God.", "아버지가 자기를 사랑할 수 있었더라면, 그리고 자기가 귀여움받는 자식이었더라면 인생이 어떠했을까 너무도 자주 상상한 나머지, 때로는 한순간 거의 그것이 사실이라고 믿기까지 했고, 그 애상에 잠긴 허구의 물결에 실려, 두 사람이 함께 무덤 속 동생을 지켜보았던 일을, 동생의 마음을 서로 거리낌 없이 나누었던 일을, 그를 향한 애틋한 추억 속에서 하나로 맺어졌던 일을, 아직도 자주 그에 대해 이야기하는 일을 기억하는 듯했다. 그리고 다정한 아버지가 그녀를 부드럽게 바라보며, 하느님께 거는 둘의 공통된 소망과 믿음을 이야기해 주는 것을."], ["At other times she pictured to herself her mother yet alive.", "또 어떤 때는 어머니가 아직 살아 있는 모습을 그려 보았다."], ["And oh the happiness of falling on her neck, and clinging to her with the love and confidence of all her soul!", "오, 그 목에 매달려, 온 영혼의 사랑과 신뢰로 어머니에게 안기는 행복이여!"], ["And oh the desolation of the solitary house again, with evening coming on, and no one there!", "그리고 오, 저녁이 다가오는데 아무도 없는 그 외로운 집의 황량함이 다시금이여!"]], [["But there was one thought, scarcely shaped out to herself, yet fervent and strong within her, that upheld Florence when she strove and filled her true young heart, so sorely tried, with constancy of purpose.", "그러나 그녀 자신에게도 채 형체를 갖추지 못했으나, 그녀 안에서 뜨겁고 강하게 살아, 플로렌스가 애쓸 때 그녀를 떠받치고, 그토록 모질게 시련받은 그 진실한 어린 마음을 한결같은 목적으로 채워 주는 한 가지 생각이 있었다."], ["Into her mind, as into all others contending with the great affliction of our mortal nature, there had stolen solemn wonderings and hopes, arising in the dim world beyond the present life, and murmuring, like faint music, of recognition in the far-off land between her brother and her mother: of some present consciousness in both of her: some love and commiseration for her: and some knowledge of her as she went her way upon the earth.", "우리 죽어야 할 본성의 큰 슬픔과 싸우는 모든 이의 마음에 그러하듯, 그녀의 마음에도 엄숙한 의구와 소망이 스며들어 있었으니, 그것은 현세 너머의 어슴푸레한 세계에서 일어나, 동생과 어머니가 그 머나먼 땅에서 서로를 알아보리라는 것, 둘 다 그녀를 지금도 의식하고 있으리라는 것, 그녀를 향한 어떤 사랑과 연민이 있으리라는 것, 그리고 그녀가 이 땅 위에서 제 갈 길을 갈 때 둘이 그녀를 알아보고 있으리라는 것을, 희미한 음악처럼 속삭여 주었다."], ["It was a soothing consolation to Florence to give shelter to these thoughts, until one day—it was soon after she had last seen her father in his own room, late at night—the fancy came upon her, that, in weeping for his alienated heart, she might stir the spirits of the dead against him.", "이런 생각들에 깃들 자리를 내어 주는 것은 플로렌스에게 위로가 되는 마음의 안식이었다. 그러던 어느 날—그것은 그녀가 밤늦게 아버지의 방에서 마지막으로 그를 본 직후였다—그녀에게 한 가지 상념이 떠올랐으니, 멀어진 그의 마음 때문에 운다면 죽은 이들의 영혼을 그에게 맞서도록 부추기는 셈이 될지도 모른다는 것이었다."], ["Wild, weak, childish, as it may have been to think so, and to tremble at the half-formed thought, it was the impulse of her loving nature; and from that hour Florence strove against the cruel wound in her breast, and tried to think of him whose hand had made it, only with hope.", "그렇게 생각하는 것이, 그리고 그 채 형체를 갖추지 못한 상념에 떠는 것이 아무리 터무니없고 약하고 어린아이 같았다 해도, 그것은 그녀의 사랑하는 천성에서 우러난 충동이었다. 그리고 그 순간부터 플로렌스는 가슴속 그 잔인한 상처에 맞서 싸웠고, 그 상처를 낸 손의 주인을 오직 희망으로만 생각하려 애썼다."]], [["Her father did not know—she held to it from that time—how much she loved him.", "아버지는 자기가 그를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알지 못했다—그녀는 그때부터 그렇게 믿었다."], ["She was very young, and had no mother, and had never learned, by some fault or misfortune, how to express to him that she loved him.", "그녀는 매우 어렸고, 어머니가 없었으며, 어떤 잘못이나 불운으로 그를 사랑한다는 것을 그에게 표현하는 법을 끝내 배우지 못했다."], ["She would be patient, and would try to gain that art in time, and win him to a better knowledge of his only child.", "그녀는 참고 기다리며, 머지않아 그 기술을 익혀, 그가 자기의 외동딸을 더 잘 알도록 마음을 돌려놓으리라 했다."]], [["This became the purpose of her life.", "이것이 그녀 삶의 목적이 되었다."], ["The morning sun shone down upon the faded house, and found the resolution bright and fresh within the bosom of its solitary mistress, Through all the duties of the day, it animated her; for Florence hoped that the more she knew, and the more accomplished she became, the more glad he would be when he came to know and like her.", "아침 해가 빛바랜 집 위로 비쳐 내리며, 그 외로운 여주인의 가슴속에서 그 결심이 밝고 싱그럽게 살아 있는 것을 발견했다. 그것은 하루의 모든 일과 내내 그녀에게 활기를 불어넣었으니, 플로렌스는 자기가 더 많이 알고 더 많은 것을 갖추게 될수록, 아버지가 자기를 알고 좋아하게 될 때 더 기뻐하리라 바랐기 때문이다."], ["Sometimes she wondered, with a swelling heart and rising tear, whether she was proficient enough in anything to surprise him when they should become companions.", "때로는 가슴이 부풀고 눈물이 차오르며, 두 사람이 동무가 될 때 그를 놀라게 할 만큼 자기가 무엇 하나라도 충분히 능숙한지 자문해 보았다."], ["Sometimes she tried to think if there were any kind of knowledge that would bespeak his interest more readily than another.", "때로는 다른 어떤 것보다 그의 관심을 더 쉽게 끌 만한 지식이 있을까 생각해 보았다."], ["Always: at her books, her music, and her work: in her morning walks, and in her nightly prayers: she had her engrossing aim in view.", "언제나, 책과 음악과 일에서, 아침 산책에서, 밤기도에서, 그녀는 그 골똘한 목표를 눈앞에 두었다."], ["Strange study for a child, to learn the road to a hard parent’s heart!", "모진 아버지의 마음에 이르는 길을 배우다니, 어린아이에게는 참으로 기이한 공부였다!"]], [["There were many careless loungers through the street, as the summer evening deepened into night, who glanced across the road at the sombre house, and saw the youthful figure at the window, such a contrast to it, looking upward at the stars as they began to shine, who would have slept the worse if they had known on what design she mused so steadfastly.", "여름 저녁이 깊어 밤이 되어 갈 무렵, 거리를 한가로이 어슬렁거리던 많은 이들이 길 건너 그 음울한 집을 흘끗 바라보고, 창가에 선 그 젊은 모습을—그 집과 그토록 대조되는 모습을—반짝이기 시작하는 별들을 올려다보는 그 모습을 보았다. 그녀가 그토록 한결같이 골몰하는 생각이 무엇인지 알았더라면, 그들은 잠을 더 설쳤을 것이다."], ["The reputation of the mansion as a haunted house, would not have been the gayer with some humble dwellers elsewhere, who were struck by its external gloom in passing and repassing on their daily avocations, and so named it, if they could have read its story in the darkening face.", "날마다 자기 일로 그 집 앞을 오가다가 그 바깥의 음울함에 충격받아 그 저택을 유령의 집이라 부르던, 어딘가 다른 곳의 가난한 이들도, 어두워지는 그 얼굴에서 사연을 읽어 낼 수 있었더라면, 그 집의 그런 평판을 그리 유쾌하게 여기지는 못했으리라."], ["But Florence held her sacred purpose, unsuspected and unaided: and studied only how to bring her father to the understanding that she loved him, and made no appeal against him in any wandering thought.", "그러나 플로렌스는 의심받지도 도움받지도 못한 채 그 거룩한 목적을 간직했고, 오직 어떻게 하면 자기가 그를 사랑한다는 것을 아버지가 깨닫게 할까만을 궁리했으며, 어떤 떠도는 생각 속에서도 그를 원망하지 않았다."]], [["Thus Florence lived alone in the deserted house, and day succeeded day, and still she lived alone, and the monotonous walls looked down upon her with a stare, as if they had a Gorgon-like intent to stare her youth and beauty into stone.", "이렇게 플로렌스는 그 버려진 집에서 홀로 살았고, 하루가 또 하루를 이어 갔으나 여전히 홀로 살았으며, 단조로운 벽들은 멍한 눈길로 그녀를 내려다보았는데, 마치 고르곤처럼 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움을 돌로 굳혀 버리려는 심산이라도 품은 듯했다."]], [["Susan Nipper stood opposite to her young mistress one morning, as she folded and sealed a note she had been writing: and showed in her looks an approving knowledge of its contents.", "어느 날 아침, 플로렌스가 방금 쓴 쪽지를 접어 봉인하고 있을 때, 수전 니퍼가 그 앞에 마주 서서, 그 내용을 흡족하게 안다는 표정을 지어 보였다."]], [["“Better late than never, dear Miss Floy,”", "\"늦더라도 안 하느니보다 낫지요, 플로이 아가씨,\""], ["said Susan, “and I do say, that even a visit to them old Skettleses will be a Godsend.”", "수전이 말했다. \"그리고 제가 말씀드리는데, 그 늙은 스케틀스 댁에 다녀오는 것만 해도 천만다행이에요.\""]], [["# CHAPTER XXIV. The Study of a Loving Heart", "# CHAPTER XXIV. The Study of a Loving Heart"]], [["“It is very good of Sir Barnet and Lady Skettles, Susan,”", "\"바넷 경과 스케틀스 부인이 그렇게 친절히도 초대를 거듭해 주시니 참 고마운 일이지, 수전,\""], ["returned Florence, with a mild correction of that young lady’s familiar mention of the family in question, “to repeat their invitation so kindly.”", "플로렌스가 응수하며, 문제의 그 집안을 허물없이 입에 올린 이 처녀를 부드럽게 바로잡았다."]], [["Miss Nipper, who was perhaps the most thoroughgoing partisan on the face of the earth, and who carried her partisanship into all matters great or small, and perpetually waged war with it against society, screwed up her lips and shook her head, as a protest against any recognition of disinterestedness in the Skettleses, and a plea in bar that they would have valuable consideration for their kindness, in the company of Florence.", "니퍼 양은 아마 이 세상에서 가장 철저한 편파주의자였을 것이며, 크고 작은 모든 일에 그 편파성을 끌어들여, 그것을 무기 삼아 세상과 끊임없이 전쟁을 벌이는 사람이었던지라, 입술을 비죽 오므리고 고개를 저었으니, 스케틀스 댁의 사심 없음을 조금이라도 인정하는 데 항의하고, 그들이 플로렌스를 곁에 둠으로써 그 친절에 대해 톡톡한 보상을 받으리라는 점을 들어 반론을 제기하는 것이었다."]], [["“They know what they’re about, if ever people did,”", "\"세상에 속셈 있는 사람이 있다면 바로 그 사람들이라니까요,\""], ["murmured Miss Nipper, drawing in her breath “oh!", "니퍼 양이 숨을 들이켜며 중얼거렸다. \"오!"], ["trust them Skettleses for that!”", "그 점에선 그 스케틀스 댁을 믿으셔도 돼요!\""]], [["“I am not very anxious to go to Fulham, Susan, I confess,”", "\"솔직히 풀럼에 가는 게 그리 내키지는 않아, 수전,\""], ["said Florence thoughtfully: “but it will be right to go.", "플로렌스가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"하지만 가는 게 옳을 거야."], ["I think it will be better.”", "그게 나을 거라고 생각해.\""]], [["“Much better,”", "\"훨씬 낫고말고요,\""], ["interposed Susan, with another emphatic shake of her head.", "수전이 또 한 번 단호히 고개를 흔들며 끼어들었다."]], [["“And so,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["said Florence, “though I would prefer to have gone when there was no one there, instead of in this vacation time, when it seems there are some young people staying in the house, I have thankfully said yes.”", "플로렌스가 말했다. \"아무도 없을 때 가는 게 더 좋았겠지만, 이렇게 방학 때라서 집에 젊은 사람들 몇이 머물고 있는 것 같은데도, 고맙게 가겠다고 했어.\""]], [["“For which I say, Miss Floy, Oh be joyful!”", "\"그 점에 대해선 저는, 플로이 아가씨, 오 기뻐할 일이다! 하고 말씀드려요,\""], ["returned Susan, “Ah!", "수전이 응수했다. \"아!"], ["h—h!”", "흐—흠!\""]], [["This last ejaculation, with which Miss Nipper frequently wound up a sentence, at about that epoch of time, was supposed below the level of the hall to have a general reference to Mr Dombey, and to be expressive of a yearning in Miss Nipper to favour that gentleman with a piece of her mind.", "니퍼 양이 그 무렵 곧잘 말끝에 붙이던 이 마지막 감탄사는, 현관 아래쪽에서는 대체로 돔비 씨를 두고 하는 말로, 그 신사에게 한바탕 속마음을 들려주고 싶어 하는 니퍼 양의 갈망을 나타내는 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["But she never explained it; and it had, in consequence, the charm of mystery, in addition to the advantage of the sharpest expression.", "그러나 그녀는 결코 그것을 설명하지 않았으니, 그 결과 그 감탄사는 더없이 날카로운 표현이라는 이점에다 신비라는 매력까지 더하게 되었다."]], [["“How long it is before we have any news of Walter, Susan!”", "\"월터 소식을 들은 지가 참 오래됐네, 수전!\""], ["observed Florence, after a moment’s silence.", "플로렌스가 잠시 침묵한 뒤 말했다."]], [["“Long indeed, Miss Floy!”", "\"정말 오래됐어요, 플로이 아가씨!\""], ["replied her maid.", "하녀가 대답했다."], ["“And Perch said, when he came just now to see for letters—but what signifies what he says!”", "\"그리고 퍼치가 방금 편지 왔나 보러 왔을 때 그러는데—하지만 그 사람이 하는 말이 무슨 소용이람!\""], ["exclaimed Susan, reddening and breaking off.", "수전이 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 끊었다."], ["“Much he knows about it!”", "\"그 사람이 뭘 안다고!\""]], [["Florence raised her eyes quickly, and a flush overspread her face.", "플로렌스가 재빨리 눈을 들었고, 얼굴에 홍조가 번졌다."]], [["“If I hadn’t,”", "\"제가 만약,\""], ["said Susan Nipper, evidently struggling with some latent anxiety and alarm, and looking full at her young mistress, while endeavouring to work herself into a state of resentment with the unoffending Mr Perch’s image, “if I hadn’t more manliness than that insipidest of his sex, I’d never take pride in my hair again, but turn it up behind my ears, and wear coarse caps, without a bit of border, until death released me from my insignificance.", "수전 니퍼가 어떤 숨은 불안과 경계심과 싸우는 기색이 역력한 채, 죄 없는 퍼치 씨의 모습을 떠올리며 억지로 분개한 상태로 몰아가려 애쓰면서 어린 여주인을 정면으로 바라보며 말했다. \"제가 만약 그 남자들 중에서도 제일 맹탕인 그 작자보다 사내다움이 못하다면, 다시는 제 머리에 자부심 같은 건 안 가질 거예요, 머리를 귀 뒤로 넘겨 묶고, 가장자리 장식 하나 없는 거친 모자나 쓰고 다니다가, 죽음이 저를 이 하찮음에서 풀어 줄 때까지 그렇게 지내겠어요."], ["I may not be a Amazon, Miss Floy, and wouldn’t so demean myself by such disfigurement, but anyways I’m not a giver up, I hope.”", "제가 아마조네스는 아닐지 몰라요, 플로이 아가씨, 그런 흉한 꼴로 제 몸을 깎아내릴 마음도 없고요, 하지만 어쨌든 저는 쉽게 포기하는 사람은 아니에요, 그렇고말고요.\""]], [["“Give up!", "\"포기하다니!"], ["What?”", "뭘?\""], ["cried Florence, with a face of terror.", "플로렌스가 겁에 질린 얼굴로 외쳤다."]], [["“Why, nothing, Miss,”", "\"아니, 아무것도요, 아가씨,\""], ["said Susan.", "수전이 말했다."], ["“Good gracious, nothing!", "\"맙소사, 아무것도 아니에요!"], ["It’s only that wet curl-paper of a man, Perch, that anyone might almost make away with, with a touch, and really it would be a blessed event for all parties if someone would take pity on him, and would have the goodness!”", "그저 그 퍼치라는, 젖은 종이로 만 머리카락 같은 남자 이야기예요, 누구라도 손가락 하나로 거의 없애 버릴 수 있을 것 같은 사람이라서, 누가 그 사람을 가엾이 여겨 좋은 일 좀 해 준다면 정말 모두에게 다 복된 일일 거예요!\""]], [["“Does he give up the ship, Susan?”", "\"그 사람이 배를 포기하는 거야, 수전?\""], ["inquired Florence, very pale.", "플로렌스가 새파랗게 질려 물었다."]], [["“No, Miss,”", "\"아니에요, 아가씨,\""], ["returned Susan, “I should like to see him make so bold as do it to my face!", "수전이 응수했다. \"그 사람이 감히 제 면전에서 그러는 꼴을 좀 보고 싶네요!"], ["No, Miss, but he goes on about some bothering ginger that Mr Walter was to send to Mrs Perch, and shakes his dismal head, and says he hopes it may be coming; anyhow, he says, it can’t come now in time for the intended occasion, but may do for next, which really,”", "아니에요, 아가씨, 다만 월터 씨가 퍼치 부인에게 보내 주기로 했다는 그 골치 아픈 생강인지 뭔지를 가지고 계속 떠들면서, 우울하게 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는, 그게 오기를 바란다, 어쨌든 이번 일에는 제때 못 오겠지만 다음번엔 될지도 모른다, 그러는데, 정말이지,\""], ["said Miss Nipper, with aggravated scorn, “puts me out of patience with the man, for though I can bear a great deal, I am not a camel, neither am I,”", "니퍼 양이 더욱 경멸을 담아 말했다. \"그 사람 때문에 제 인내심이 다 바닥나요, 저도 어지간한 건 다 참지만, 제가 낙타는 아니거든요, 그리고,\""], ["added Susan, after a moment’s consideration, “if I know myself, a dromedary neither.”", "잠시 생각한 뒤 수전이 덧붙였다. \"제가 저를 알기로는, 단봉낙타도 아니고요.\""]], [["“What else does he say, Susan?”", "\"또 무슨 말을 하던, 수전?\""], ["inquired Florence, earnestly.", "플로렌스가 간곡히 물었다."], ["“Won’t you tell me?”", "\"말해 주지 않을래?\""]], [["“As if I wouldn’t tell you anything, Miss Floy, and everything!”", "\"아가씨한테 무엇이든, 죄다 말씀 안 드릴 리가 있겠어요, 플로이 아가씨!\""], ["said Susan.", "수전이 말했다."], ["“Why, nothing Miss, he says that there begins to be a general talk about the ship, and that they have never had a ship on that voyage half so long unheard of, and that the Captain’s wife was at the office yesterday, and seemed a little put out about it, but anyone could say that, we knew nearly that before.”", "\"아니, 아무것도 아니에요, 아가씨, 그 사람 말이, 배에 대해 사람들이 두루 수군대기 시작했다, 그 항로로 다니는 배 중에 이렇게 오래 소식 없는 배는 반만큼도 없었다, 그리고 어제 선장 부인이 사무소에 왔는데 그 일로 좀 마음이 상한 것 같더라, 그러는데, 그거야 누구라도 할 수 있는 말이고, 우리도 거의 그 정도는 진작 알고 있었잖아요.\""]], [["“I must visit Walter’s uncle,”", "\"집을 떠나기 전에 월터의 삼촌을 찾아뵈어야겠어,\""], ["said Florence, hurriedly, “before I leave home.", "플로렌스가 서둘러 말했다."], ["I will go and see him this morning.", "\"오늘 아침에 가서 뵐래."], ["Let us walk there, directly, Susan.”", "곧장 걸어서 가자, 수전.\""]], [["Miss Nipper having nothing to urge against the proposal, but being perfectly acquiescent, they were soon equipped, and in the streets, and on their way towards the little Midshipman.", "니퍼 양은 그 제안에 반대할 거리가 없었고 기꺼이 따랐으므로, 둘은 곧 채비를 마치고 거리로 나서, 자그마한 항해사 인형을 향해 길을 떠났다."]], [["The state of mind in which poor Walter had gone to Captain Cuttle’s, on the day when Brogley the broker came into possession, and when there seemed to him to be an execution in the very steeples, was pretty much the same as that in which Florence now took her way to Uncle Sol’s; with this difference, that Florence suffered the added pain of thinking that she had been, perhaps, the innocent occasion of involving Walter in peril, and all to whom he was dear, herself included, in an agony of suspense.", "브로글리라는 중개인이 가게를 차지하던 날, 그리고 첨탑 하나하나에서까지 압류가 집행되는 것처럼 느껴지던 그날, 가련한 월터가 커틀 선장에게로 갔을 때의 그 심정은, 지금 플로렌스가 솔 삼촌에게로 향하는 심정과 거의 같았다. 다만 한 가지 차이가 있었으니, 플로렌스는 자기가 어쩌면 월터를 위험에 빠뜨린 무고한 빌미였을지도 모른다는 생각, 그리고 그를 아끼는 모든 이를—자기 자신을 포함해—애타는 불안 속에 몰아넣은 빌미였을지도 모른다는 생각에 더한 고통을 겪었다."], ["For the rest, uncertainty and danger seemed written upon everything.", "그 밖에는, 불확실함과 위험이 모든 것 위에 쓰여 있는 듯했다."], ["The weathercocks on spires and housetops were mysterious with hints of stormy wind, and pointed, like so many ghostly fingers, out to dangerous seas, where fragments of great wrecks were drifting, perhaps, and helpless men were rocked upon them into a sleep as deep as the unfathomable waters.", "첨탑과 지붕 위의 풍향계들은 폭풍 같은 바람을 암시하며 신비롭게 움직였고, 수많은 유령의 손가락처럼, 어쩌면 거대한 난파선의 잔해가 떠다니고 무력한 사람들이 그 위에서 흔들리며 헤아릴 수 없는 바닷물만큼 깊은 잠에 빠져드는 위험한 바다를 가리켰다."], ["When Florence came into the City, and passed gentlemen who were talking together, she dreaded to hear them speaking of the ship, and saying it was lost.", "플로렌스는 시내로 들어와 함께 이야기하는 신사들 곁을 지날 때, 그들이 그 배 이야기를 하며 배가 침몰했다고 말하는 것을 들을까 두려웠다."], ["Pictures and prints of vessels fighting with the rolling waves filled her with alarm.", "굽이치는 파도와 싸우는 배들을 그린 그림과 판화들이 그녀를 공포로 가득 채웠다."], ["The smoke and clouds, though moving gently, moved too fast for her apprehensions, and made her fear there was a tempest blowing at that moment on the ocean.", "연기와 구름은 비록 잔잔히 움직였으나 그녀의 불안에 비하면 너무 빨리 움직여, 바로 지금 대양 위에 폭풍이 몰아치고 있는 것은 아닐까 두렵게 했다."], ["Susan Nipper may or may not have been affected similarly, but having her attention much engaged in struggles with boys, whenever there was any press of people—for, between that grade of human kind and herself, there was some natural animosity that invariably broke out, whenever they came together—it would seem that she had not much leisure on the road for intellectual operations.", "수전 니퍼도 비슷하게 영향을 받았는지는 알 수 없으나, 사람이 좀 몰리는 곳마다 사내아이들과 실랑이하느라 정신이 온통 팔려 있었으니—그 부류의 인간과 그녀 사이에는 마주칠 때마다 어김없이 터져 나오는 어떤 타고난 적의가 있었던 까닭에—길에서 지적인 활동을 할 여유는 별로 없었던 모양이다."]], [["Arriving in good time abreast of the wooden Midshipman on the opposite side of the way, and waiting for an opportunity to cross the street, they were a little surprised at first to see, at the Instrument-maker’s door, a round-headed lad, with his chubby face addressed towards the sky, who, as they looked at him, suddenly thrust into his capacious mouth two fingers of each hand, and with the assistance of that machinery whistled, with astonishing shrillness, to some pigeons at a considerable elevation in the air.", "마침 길 건너편 나무 항해사 인형 맞은편에 제때 다다라 길을 건널 기회를 기다리던 두 사람은, 처음에 기구 제작자의 문간에 둥근 머리의 한 소년이 통통한 얼굴을 하늘로 향한 채 서 있는 것을 보고 조금 놀랐다. 두 사람이 그를 바라보고 있는데, 그 소년이 별안간 양손 손가락 두 개씩을 제 널찍한 입속에 쑤셔 넣더니, 그 장치의 힘을 빌려 공중 상당히 높은 곳에 있는 비둘기 몇 마리를 향해 놀랄 만큼 날카롭게 휘파람을 불었다."]], [["“Mrs Richards’s eldest, Miss!”", "\"리처즈 부인 큰아들이에요, 아가씨!\""], ["said Susan, “and the worrit of Mrs Richards’s life!”", "수전이 말했다. \"리처즈 부인 인생의 골칫거리고요!\""]], [["As Polly had been to tell Florence of the resuscitated prospects of her son and heir, Florence was prepared for the meeting: so, a favourable moment presenting itself, they both hastened across, without any further contemplation of Mrs Richards’s bane.", "폴리가 플로렌스에게 자기 아들이자 후계자의 되살아난 앞날을 이야기해 준 적이 있었으므로, 플로렌스는 이 만남에 대비가 되어 있었다. 그리하여 좋은 기회가 생기자 두 사람은 리처즈 부인의 그 골칫덩이를 더는 눈여겨보지 않고 서둘러 길을 건넜다."], ["That sporting character, unconscious of their approach, again whistled with his utmost might, and then yelled in a rapture of excitement, “Strays!", "그 스포츠광은 둘이 다가오는 것을 알아채지 못한 채 다시 있는 힘껏 휘파람을 불었고, 이어 흥분에 도취해 \"도망친다! 후—웁!"], ["Whoo-oop!", "도망친다!\""], ["Strays!”", "하고 고함쳤다."], ["which identification had such an effect upon the conscience-stricken pigeons, that instead of going direct to some town in the North of England, as appeared to have been their original intention, they began to wheel and falter; whereupon Mrs Richards’s first born pierced them with another whistle, and again yelled, in a voice that rose above the turmoil of the street, “Strays!", "이 외침은 양심에 찔린 비둘기들에게 어찌나 효과가 있었던지, 원래 의도했던 듯 잉글랜드 북부의 어느 도시로 곧장 가는 대신 빙글빙글 돌며 머뭇거리기 시작했다. 그러자 리처즈 부인의 맏이는 또 한 번 휘파람으로 그것들을 꿰뚫고는, 거리의 소음을 뚫고 솟구치는 목소리로 다시 외쳤다."], ["Whoo-oop!", "\"도망친다! 후—웁!"], ["Strays!”", "도망친다!\""]], [["From this transport, he was abruptly recalled to terrestrial objects, by a poke from Miss Nipper, which sent him into the shop.", "이 황홀경에서 그는 니퍼 양의 쿡 찌름에 의해 갑작스레 지상의 사물로 불려 내려왔고, 그 찌름에 가게 안으로 밀려 들어갔다."]], [["“Is this the way you show your penitence, when Mrs Richards has been fretting for you months and months?”", "\"리처즈 부인이 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 네 걱정에 애를 태웠는데, 네 뉘우침이라는 게 고작 이거냐?\""], ["said Susan, following the poke.", "수전이 쿡 찌른 손길을 따라 들어가며 말했다."], ["“Where’s Mr Gills?”", "\"길스 씨는 어디 계셔?\""]], [["Rob, who smoothed his first rebellious glance at Miss Nipper when he saw Florence following, put his knuckles to his hair, in honour of the latter, and said to the former, that Mr Gills was out.”", "플로렌스가 뒤따라 들어오는 것을 본 롭은, 니퍼 양을 향한 처음의 반항적인 눈빛을 누그러뜨리고, 플로렌스에게 경의를 표하느라 주먹을 머리에 갖다 댄 다음, 수전에게 길스 씨는 외출 중이라고 말했다."]], [["“Fetch him home,”", "\"모셔 와,\""], ["said Miss Nipper, with authority, “and say that my young lady’s here.”", "니퍼 양이 위엄 있게 말했다. \"그리고 우리 아가씨가 와 계신다고 말씀드려.\""]], [["“I don’t know where he’s gone,”", "\"어디 가셨는지 모르는데요,\""], ["said Rob.", "롭이 말했다."]], [["“Is that your penitence?”", "\"그게 네 뉘우침이라는 거냐?\""], ["cried Susan, with stinging sharpness.", "수전이 쏘아붙이듯 날카롭게 외쳤다."]], [["“Why how can I go and fetch him when I don’t know where to go?”", "\"아니, 어디로 가야 할지도 모르는데 어떻게 모셔 와요?\""], ["whimpered the baited Rob.", "시달린 롭이 칭얼거렸다."], ["“How can you be so unreasonable?”", "\"어떻게 그렇게 막무가내세요?\""]], [["“Did Mr Gills say when he should be home?”", "\"길스 씨가 언제 돌아오신다고 하셨니?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, Miss,”", "\"네, 아가씨,\""], ["replied Rob, with another application of his knuckles to his hair.", "롭이 또 한 번 주먹을 머리에 갖다 대며 대답했다."], ["“He said he should be home early in the afternoon; in about a couple of hours from now, Miss.”", "\"오후 일찍 돌아오신다고 하셨어요, 지금부터 두 시간쯤 뒤요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Is he very anxious about his nephew?”", "\"조카 일로 무척 걱정하고 계시니?\""], ["inquired Susan.", "수전이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, Miss,”", "\"네, 아가씨,\""], ["returned Rob, preferring to address himself to Florence and slighting Nipper; “I should say he was, very much so.", "롭이 플로렌스에게 대답하기를 더 좋아하며 니퍼를 무시한 채 응수했다. \"제가 보기엔 아주 많이 그러세요."], ["He ain’t indoors, Miss, not a quarter of an hour together.", "한 번에 십오 분도 집 안에 못 계세요, 아가씨."], ["He can’t settle in one place five minutes.", "한자리에 오 분을 못 붙어 계시고요."], ["He goes about, like a—just like a stray,”", "그냥 막—꼭 도망친 놈처럼 돌아다니세요,\""], ["said Rob, stooping to get a glimpse of the pigeons through the window, and checking himself, with his fingers half-way to his mouth, on the verge of another whistle.", "롭이 창문 너머로 비둘기를 한번 보려고 허리를 굽혔다가, 또 한 번 휘파람을 불려고 손가락을 입까지 반쯤 가져가던 참에 스스로를 멈추며 말했다."]], [["“Do you know a friend of Mr Gills, called Captain Cuttle?”", "\"길스 씨의 친구분 중에 커틀 선장이라는 분을 아니?\""], ["inquired Florence, after a moment’s reflection.", "플로렌스가 잠시 생각한 뒤 물었다."]], [["“Him with a hook, Miss?”", "\"갈고리 달린 분이요, 아가씨?\""], ["rejoined Rob, with an illustrative twist of his left hand.", "롭이 왼손을 비틀어 보이며 응수했다. \"네, 아가씨."], ["Yes, Miss. He was here the day before yesterday.”", "그저께 여기 오셨어요.\""]], [["“Has he not been here since?”", "\"그 뒤로는 안 오셨고?\""], ["asked Susan.", "수전이 물었다."]], [["“No, Miss,”", "\"네, 아가씨,\""], ["returned Rob, still addressing his reply to Florence.", "롭이 여전히 플로렌스에게 대답하며 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps Walter’s Uncle has gone there, Susan,”", "\"어쩌면 월터의 삼촌이 거기 가셨는지도 몰라, 수전,\""], ["observed Florence, turning to her.", "플로렌스가 그녀에게 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“To Captain Cuttle’s, Miss?”", "\"커틀 선장 댁에요, 아가씨?\""], ["interposed Rob; “no, he’s not gone there, Miss.", "롭이 끼어들었다. \"아니에요, 거기 안 가셨어요, 아가씨."], ["Because he left particular word that if Captain Cuttle called, I should tell him how surprised he was, not to have seen him yesterday, and should make him stop till he came back.”", "커틀 선장이 들르거든, 어제 못 뵈어서 얼마나 놀랐는지 모른다고 전하고, 돌아오실 때까지 붙들어 두라고 특별히 일러두셨거든요.\""]], [["“Do you know where Captain Cuttle lives?”", "\"커틀 선장이 어디 사시는지 아니?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["Rob replied in the affirmative, and turning to a greasy parchment book on the shop desk, read the address aloud.", "롭은 안다고 대답하고는, 가게 책상 위의 기름때 묻은 양피지 장부를 펼쳐, 주소를 소리 내어 읽었다."]], [["Florence again turned to her maid and took counsel with her in a low voice, while Rob the round-eyed, mindful of his patron’s secret charge, looked on and listened.", "플로렌스는 다시 하녀에게 돌아서서 낮은 목소리로 의논했고, 그동안 둥근 눈의 롭은 후원자의 은밀한 당부를 명심한 채 지켜보고 귀를 기울였다."], ["Florence proposed that they could go to Captain Cuttle’s house; hear from his own lips, what he thought of the absence of any tidings of the Son and Heir; and bring him, if they could, to comfort Uncle Sol.", "플로렌스는 커틀 선장의 집으로 가서, '아들과 후계자' 호의 소식이 없는 것에 대해 그가 어떻게 생각하는지 그의 입으로 직접 듣고, 가능하다면 그를 데려와 솔 삼촌을 위로해 드리자고 제안했다."], ["Susan at first objected slightly, on the score of distance; but a hackney-coach being mentioned by her mistress, withdrew that opposition, and gave in her assent.", "수전은 처음에 거리가 멀다는 이유로 약간 반대했으나, 여주인이 삯마차를 언급하자 그 반대를 거두고 동의했다."], ["There were some minutes of discussion between them before they came to this conclusion, during which the staring Rob paid close attention to both speakers, and inclined his ear to each by turns, as if he were appointed arbitrator of the argument.", "이 결론에 이르기까지 둘 사이에 몇 분간 논의가 오갔는데, 그동안 멀뚱히 바라보던 롭은 두 말하는 이에게 바짝 주의를 기울이며 번갈아 한쪽씩 귀를 기울였으니, 마치 그 논쟁의 중재인으로 임명되기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["In time, Rob was despatched for a coach, the visitors keeping shop meanwhile; and when he brought it, they got into it, leaving word for Uncle Sol that they would be sure to call again, on their way back.", "이윽고 롭이 마차를 부르러 보내졌고, 그동안 손님들이 가게를 봐 주었다. 그가 마차를 가져오자 둘은 그것에 올라타며, 돌아오는 길에 꼭 다시 들르겠다는 말을 솔 삼촌에게 남겼다."], ["Rob having stared after the coach until it was as invisible as the pigeons had now become, sat down behind the desk with a most assiduous demeanour; and in order that he might forget nothing of what had transpired, made notes of it on various small scraps of paper, with a vast expenditure of ink.", "롭은 마차가 이제는 보이지 않게 된 비둘기들만큼이나 시야에서 사라질 때까지 그 뒤를 멀거니 바라보다가, 더없이 부지런한 태도로 책상 뒤에 앉았다. 그러고는 일어난 일을 하나도 잊지 않으려고, 잉크를 엄청나게 소비해 가며 여러 작은 종잇조각에 그것을 적어 두었다."], ["There was no danger of these documents betraying anything, if accidentally lost; for long before a word was dry, it became as profound a mystery to Rob, as if he had had no part whatever in its production.", "이 문서들은 행여 잃어버린대도 아무것도 누설할 위험이 없었으니, 글자가 미처 마르기도 전에 롭에게는, 마치 자기가 그것을 만드는 데 아무런 관여도 하지 않은 것처럼 심오한 수수께끼가 되어 버렸기 때문이다."]], [["While he was yet busy with these labours, the hackney-coach, after encountering unheard-of difficulties from swivel-bridges, soft roads, impassable canals, caravans of casks, settlements of scarlet-beans and little wash-houses, and many such obstacles abounding in that country, stopped at the corner of Brig Place.", "그가 아직 이 일에 분주한 동안, 삯마차는 회전 다리며 진창길이며 건널 수 없는 운하며 술통을 실은 짐마차 행렬이며 강낭콩 밭이며 자그마한 빨래터며, 그 지방에 흔한 그런 숱한 장애물들로부터 듣도 보도 못한 곤경을 겪은 끝에, 브리그 플레이스 모퉁이에 멈췄다."], ["Alighting here, Florence and Susan Nipper walked down the street, and sought out the abode of Captain Cuttle.", "여기서 내린 플로렌스와 수전 니퍼는 거리를 걸어 내려가 커틀 선장의 거처를 찾았다."]], [["It happened by evil chance to be one of Mrs MacStinger’s great cleaning days.", "공교롭게도 그날은 운 나쁘게 맥스팅거 부인의 대청소 날 가운데 하나였다."], ["On these occasions, Mrs MacStinger was knocked up by the policeman at a quarter before three in the morning, and rarely succumbed before twelve o’clock next night.", "이런 날이면 맥스팅거 부인은 새벽 두 시 사십오 분에 순경에게 깨워져, 좀처럼 이튿날 밤 열두 시 전에는 굴복하지 않았다."], ["The chief object of this institution appeared to be, that Mrs MacStinger should move all the furniture into the back garden at early dawn, walk about the house in pattens all day, and move the furniture back again after dark.", "이 행사의 주된 목적은, 맥스팅거 부인이 동틀 무렵 모든 가구를 뒷마당으로 옮기고, 온종일 나막신을 신은 채 집 안을 돌아다니다가, 날이 어두워진 뒤 그 가구를 다시 들여놓는 데 있는 듯했다."], ["These ceremonies greatly fluttered those doves the young MacStingers, who were not only unable at such times to find any resting-place for the soles of their feet, but generally came in for a good deal of pecking from the maternal bird during the progress of the solemnities.", "이 의식은 어린 맥스팅거 자식들이라는 그 비둘기들을 몹시 떨게 했으니, 그들은 그런 때면 발바닥을 둘 안식처조차 찾지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 이 엄숙한 행사가 진행되는 동안 어미 새에게 적잖이 쪼이기 일쑤였다."]], [["At the moment when Florence and Susan Nipper presented themselves at Mrs MacStinger’s door, that worthy but redoubtable female was in the act of conveying Alexander MacStinger, aged two years and three months, along the passage, for forcible deposition in a sitting posture on the street pavement: Alexander being black in the face with holding his breath after punishment, and a cool paving-stone being usually found to act as a powerful restorative in such cases.", "플로렌스와 수전 니퍼가 맥스팅거 부인의 문 앞에 나타난 바로 그 순간, 그 훌륭하나 가공할 만한 여인은 두 살 석 달 된 알렉산더 맥스팅거를 복도를 따라 끌고 가, 거리 보도 위에 앉은 자세로 강제로 내려놓는 중이었다. 알렉산더는 매를 맞은 뒤 숨을 참아 얼굴이 새카매져 있었는데, 이런 경우에는 대개 차가운 포석이 강력한 회복제로 작용한다고 여겨졌기 때문이다."]], [["The feelings of Mrs MacStinger, as a woman and a mother, were outraged by the look of pity for Alexander which she observed on Florence’s face.", "여자로서, 또 어미로서 맥스팅거 부인의 감정은, 플로렌스의 얼굴에 어린 알렉산더를 향한 연민의 빛을 보고 모욕당했다."], ["Therefore, Mrs MacStinger asserting those finest emotions of our nature, in preference to weakly gratifying her curiosity, shook and buffeted Alexander both before and during the application of the paving-stone, and took no further notice of the strangers.", "그리하여 맥스팅거 부인은 약하게 호기심이나 채우기보다 우리 본성의 가장 고귀한 그 감정을 내세워, 포석에 앉히기 전과 앉히는 동안에 걸쳐 알렉산더를 흔들고 두들겼으며, 낯선 이들에게는 더 이상 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않았다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Ma’am,”", "\"실례합니다, 부인,\""], ["said Florence, when the child had found his breath again, and was using it.", "아이가 다시 숨을 찾아 그것을 쓰고 있을 때 플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“Is this Captain Cuttle’s house?”", "\"여기가 커틀 선장 댁인가요?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니오,\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger.", "맥스팅거 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Not Number Nine?”", "\"9번지가 아닌가요?\""], ["asked Florence, hesitating.", "플로렌스가 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["“Who said it wasn’t Number Nine?”", "\"누가 9번지가 아니라고 했소?\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger.", "맥스팅거 부인이 말했다."]], [["Susan Nipper instantly struck in, and begged to inquire what Mrs MacStinger meant by that, and if she knew whom she was talking to.", "수전 니퍼가 즉시 끼어들어, 맥스팅거 부인이 그 말로 무슨 뜻을 말하는 건지, 또 지금 누구에게 말하고 있는지 알기나 하느냐고 물었다."]], [["Mrs MacStinger in retort, looked at her all over.", "맥스팅거 부인은 그 대꾸로 그녀를 위아래로 훑어보았다."], ["“What do you want with Captain Cuttle, I should wish to know?”", "\"커틀 선장한테 무슨 볼일이오, 그게 알고 싶구려?\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger.", "맥스팅거 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Should you?", "\"그러세요?"], ["Then I’m sorry that you won’t be satisfied,”", "그럼 만족 못 하시게 돼서 안됐네요,\""], ["returned Miss Nipper.", "니퍼 양이 응수했다."]], [["“Hush, Susan!", "\"쉿, 수전!"], ["If you please!”", "제발!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“Perhaps you can have the goodness to tell us where Captain Cuttle lives, Ma’am as he don’t live here.”", "\"커틀 선장이 여기 안 사신다면, 어디 사시는지 좀 일러 주실 수 있을까요, 부인.\""]], [["“Who says he don’t live here?”", "\"누가 여기 안 산다고 했소?\""], ["retorted the implacable MacStinger.", "그 무자비한 맥스팅거가 되받았다."], ["“I said it wasn’t Cap’en Cuttle’s house—and it ain’t his house—and forbid it, that it ever should be his house—for Cap’en Cuttle don’t know how to keep a house—and don’t deserve to have a house—it’s my house—and when I let the upper floor to Cap’en Cuttle, oh I do a thankless thing, and cast pearls before swine!”", "\"내가 여긴 커틀 선장 집이 아니라고 했지—실제로도 그 사람 집이 아니고—그게 그 사람 집이 되는 일은 천만에 없을 거고—커틀 선장은 집을 건사할 줄도 모르고—집을 가질 자격도 없으니—이건 내 집이오—위층을 커틀 선장한테 세준 거지, 오 나는 은혜도 모르는 짓을 하고, 돼지 앞에 진주를 던진 거라오!\""]], [["Mrs MacStinger pitched her voice for the upper windows in offering these remarks, and cracked off each clause sharply by itself as if from a rifle possessing an infinity of barrels.", "맥스팅거 부인은 이 말들을 늘어놓으며 목소리를 위층 창문을 향해 던졌고, 한 구절 한 구절을, 마치 무한한 총신을 가진 소총에서 쏘아 대듯 따로따로 날카롭게 터뜨렸다."], ["After the last shot, the Captain’s voice was heard to say, in feeble remonstrance from his own room, “Steady below!”", "마지막 한 방이 끝난 뒤, 선장의 목소리가 자기 방에서 힘없이 항의하듯 \"아래쪽, 진정하시오!\" 하고 말하는 것이 들렸다."]], [["“Since you want Cap’en Cuttle, there he is!”", "\"커틀 선장을 찾는다니, 저기 있소!\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger, with an angry motion of her hand.", "맥스팅거 부인이 화가 나서 손을 휘저으며 말했다."], ["On Florence making bold to enter, without any more parley, and on Susan following, Mrs MacStinger recommenced her pedestrian exercise in pattens, and Alexander MacStinger (still on the paving-stone), who had stopped in his crying to attend to the conversation, began to wail again, entertaining himself during that dismal performance, which was quite mechanical, with a general survey of the prospect, terminating in the hackney-coach.", "플로렌스가 더 이상 입씨름하지 않고 대담하게 들어서고 수전이 뒤따르자, 맥스팅거 부인은 나막신을 신고 거니는 운동을 다시 시작했다. 그리고 (여전히 포석 위에 있던) 알렉산더 맥스팅거는 그 대화에 귀 기울이느라 울음을 멈추었다가 다시 통곡하기 시작했으며, 그 음울한 공연 내내—그것은 아주 기계적인 것이었다—주위 경치를 두루 둘러보는 것으로 스스로를 달랬으니, 그 시선의 끝은 삯마차였다."]], [["The Captain in his own apartment was sitting with his hands in his pockets and his legs drawn up under his chair, on a very small desolate island, lying about midway in an ocean of soap and water.", "선장은 자기 방에서 두 손을 주머니에 찌르고 두 다리를 의자 밑으로 끌어당긴 채, 비누 거품과 물의 대양 한가운데쯤에 떠 있는 아주 자그마하고 황량한 섬 위에 앉아 있었다."], ["The Captain’s windows had been cleaned, the walls had been cleaned, the stove had been cleaned, and everything the stove excepted, was wet, and shining with soft soap and sand: the smell of which dry-saltery impregnated the air.", "선장의 창문은 닦여 있었고, 벽도 닦여 있었으며, 난로도 닦여 있었고, 난로를 제외한 모든 것이 젖은 채 무른 비누와 모래로 번들거렸는데, 그 건어물 가게 같은 냄새가 공기에 배어 있었다."], ["In the midst of the dreary scene, the Captain, cast away upon his island, looked round on the waste of waters with a rueful countenance, and seemed waiting for some friendly bark to come that way, and take him off.", "그 음울한 광경 한가운데서, 자기 섬에 표류해 버려진 선장은 침통한 얼굴로 물의 황무지를 둘러보았으니, 마치 다정한 배 한 척이 그쪽으로 와서 자기를 태워 가 주기를 기다리는 듯했다."]], [["But when the Captain, directing his forlorn visage towards the door, saw Florence appear with her maid, no words can describe his astonishment.", "그러나 선장이 처량한 얼굴을 문 쪽으로 향하다가, 플로렌스가 하녀와 함께 나타난 것을 보았을 때, 그 놀라움은 무어라 말로 표현할 수가 없었다. 맥스팅거 부인의 웅변이 다른 모든 소리를 어렴풋이밖에 분간할 수 없게 만들어 놓은 터라, 그는 술집 심부름꾼이나 우유 배달부보다 더 귀한 손님은 기대하지 않았다."], ["Mrs MacStinger’s eloquence having rendered all other sounds but imperfectly distinguishable, he had looked for no rarer visitor than the potboy or the milkman; wherefore, when Florence appeared, and coming to the confines of the island, put her hand in his, the Captain stood up, aghast, as if he supposed her, for the moment, to be some young member of the Flying Dutchman’s family.", "그래서 플로렌스가 나타나 섬의 가장자리로 다가와 자기 손에 손을 얹었을 때, 선장은 그녀를 순간 '방황하는 네덜란드인'* 가족의 젊은 일원쯤으로 여기기라도 한 듯 아연실색하여 일어섰다. * 방황하는 네덜란드인: 영원히 바다를 떠도는 저주받은 유령선과 그 선장에 관한 항해 전설."]], [["Instantly recovering his self-possession, however, the Captain’s first care was to place her on dry land, which he happily accomplished, with one motion of his arm.", "그러나 곧 침착함을 되찾은 선장은, 무엇보다 먼저 그녀를 마른 땅에 올려놓는 일에 마음을 썼고, 팔 한 번 놀려 다행히 그 일을 해냈다."], ["Issuing forth, then, upon the main, Captain Cuttle took Miss Nipper round the waist, and bore her to the island also.", "그러고서 큰 바다로 나아간 커틀 선장은 니퍼 양의 허리를 안아 그녀 또한 섬으로 옮겨다 놓았다."], ["Captain Cuttle, then, with great respect and admiration, raised the hand of Florence to his lips, and standing off a little (for the island was not large enough for three), beamed on her from the soap and water like a new description of Triton.", "그런 다음 커틀 선장은 더없는 경의와 감탄을 담아 플로렌스의 손을 들어 입술에 가져갔고, (섬이 세 사람을 두기에는 충분치 않았으므로) 조금 물러서서, 비누 거품과 물속에서 마치 새로운 종류의 트리톤*처럼 그녀를 향해 환하게 웃었다. * 트리톤: 그리스 신화의 바다 신으로, 상반신은 사람, 하반신은 물고기 형상."]], [["“You are amazed to see us, I am sure,”", "\"우리를 보고 놀라셨겠지요,\""], ["said Florence, with a smile.", "플로렌스가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["The inexpressibly gratified Captain kissed his hook in reply, and growled, as if a choice and delicate compliment were included in the words, “Stand by!", "이루 말할 수 없이 흐뭇해진 선장은 대답 대신 자기 갈고리에 입을 맞추고는, 그 말 속에 정교하고 섬세한 찬사라도 담긴 양 으르렁거렸다. \"대기하라!"], ["Stand by!”", "대기하라!\""]], [["“But I couldn’t rest,”", "\"하지만 도무지 가만있을 수가 없었어요,\""], ["said Florence, “without coming to ask you what you think about dear Walter—who is my brother now—and whether there is anything to fear, and whether you will not go and console his poor Uncle every day, until we have some intelligence of him?”", "플로렌스가 말했다. \"사랑하는 월터에 대해—이제는 제 오빠인 월터에 대해—선장님이 어떻게 생각하시는지, 무언가 두려워할 일이 있는지, 그리고 그 애 소식을 들을 때까지 매일 그 가엾은 삼촌을 위로하러 가 주시지 않겠는지 여쭤보지 않고는요.\""]], [["At these words Captain Cuttle, as by an involuntary gesture, clapped his hand to his head, on which the hard glazed hat was not, and looked discomfited.", "이 말에 커틀 선장은 무심결의 몸짓처럼 손을 머리로 가져갔으나, 거기에는 그 단단한 광택 모자가 없었고, 그는 당황한 기색을 보였다."]], [["“Have you any fears for Walter’s safety?”", "\"월터의 안전이 조금이라도 걱정되시나요?\""], ["inquired Florence, from whose face the Captain (so enraptured he was with it) could not take his eyes: while she, in her turn, looked earnestly at him, to be assured of the sincerity of his reply.", "플로렌스가 물었다. 선장은 (그녀의 얼굴에 그토록 황홀해진 나머지) 그 얼굴에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었고, 그녀는 그녀대로, 그의 대답이 진심인지 확인하려고 그를 간절히 바라보았다."]], [["“No, Heart’s-delight,”", "\"아닙니다, 내 마음의 기쁨이여,\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, “I am not afeard.", "커틀 선장이 말했다. \"두렵지 않습니다."], ["Wal”r is a lad as’ll go through a deal o’ hard weather.", "월'터는 모진 풍파를 숱하게 헤쳐 나갈 녀석이지요."], ["Wal”r is a lad as’ll bring as much success to that “ere brig as a lad is capable on.", "월'터는 한 녀석이 할 수 있는 만큼 그 배에 성공을 가져다줄 녀석입니다."], ["Wal”r,”", "월'터는,\""], ["said the Captain, his eyes glistening with the praise of his young friend, and his hook raised to announce a beautiful quotation, “is what you may call a out’ard and visible sign of an in’ard and spirited grasp, and when found make a note of.”", "선장이 어린 친구를 칭찬하느라 눈을 반짝이며, 멋진 인용구를 알리려 갈고리를 치켜들고 말했다. \"말하자면 내적이고 기개 있는 파악의, 외적이고 가시적인 표징이라 할 수 있지요, 발견하거든 적어 둘 것이라.\""]], [["Florence, who did not quite understand this, though the Captain evidently thought it full of meaning, and highly satisfactory, mildly looked to him for something more.", "플로렌스는 선장이 분명 그 말에 의미가 가득하고 더없이 만족스럽다고 여기는 듯했으나, 도무지 그 뜻을 이해하지 못한 채, 무언가 더 듣고 싶다는 듯 부드럽게 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“I am not afeard, my Heart’s-delight,”", "\"두렵지 않습니다, 내 마음의 기쁨이여,\""], ["resumed the Captain, “There’s been most uncommon bad weather in them latitudes, there’s no denyin’, and they have drove and drove and been beat off, may be t’other side the world.", "선장이 다시 말했다. \"그 위도에서 보기 드물게 사나운 날씨가 있었던 건 부인할 수 없고, 배가 떠밀리고 또 떠밀리고 휩쓸려 나가, 어쩌면 세상 저편까지 갔을지도 모르지요."], ["But the ship’s a good ship, and the lad’s a good lad; and it ain’t easy, thank the Lord,”", "하지만 그 배는 좋은 배고, 그 녀석은 좋은 녀석입니다. 그리고 다행히도,\""], ["the Captain made a little bow, “to break up hearts of oak, whether they’re in brigs or buzzums.", "선장이 가볍게 머리를 숙였다. \"참나무 같은 단단한 심장은, 그게 쌍돛배에 있든 가슴속에 있든 쉽게 부서지지 않는 법이지요."], ["Here we have ’em both ways, which is bringing it up with a round turn, and so I ain’t a bit afeard as yet.”", "여기서는 그게 두 가지 다 해당되니, 그야말로 닻줄을 한 바퀴 단단히 감은 셈이고, 그래서 나는 아직은 조금도 두렵지 않습니다.\""]], [["“As yet?”", "\"아직은요?\""], ["repeated Florence.", "플로렌스가 되물었다."]], [["“Not a bit,”", "\"조금도요,\""], ["returned the Captain, kissing his iron hand; “and afore I begin to be, my Hearts-delight, Wal”r will have wrote home from the island, or from some port or another, and made all taut and ship-shape.”", "선장이 자기 쇠손에 입을 맞추며 응수했다. \"그리고 두려워지기 시작하기 전에, 내 마음의 기쁨이여, 월'터가 그 섬에서든 어느 항구에서든 집으로 편지를 보내, 모든 걸 팽팽하게, 배 모양 반듯하게 갖춰 놓을 겁니다.\""], ["And with regard to old Sol Gills, here the Captain became solemn, “who I’ll stand by, and not desert until death do us part, and when the stormy winds do blow, do blow, do blow—overhaul the Catechism,”", "그러고서 늙은 솔 길스에 관해서는, 여기서 선장이 엄숙해졌다. \"그분이라면, 죽음이 우리를 갈라놓을 때까지 곁을 지키고 결코 버리지 않을 겁니다, 그리고 폭풍의 바람이 불고, 불고, 불 때면—교리문답서를 뒤져 보시오,\""], ["said the Captain parenthetically, “and there you’ll find them expressions—if it would console Sol Gills to have the opinion of a seafaring man as has got a mind equal to any undertaking that he puts it alongside of, and as was all but smashed in his “prenticeship, and of which the name is Bunsby, that “ere man shall give him such an opinion in his own parlour as’ll stun him.", "선장이 곁들여 말했다. \"거기에 그런 표현들이 나오니까요—만일 솔 길스에게, 자기가 맞붙는 어떤 일에도 능히 맞먹는 정신을 지녔고, 도제 시절에 거의 박살이 날 뻔했으며, 이름이 번스비라 하는, 한 뱃사람의 견해를 듣는 게 위로가 된다면, 그 사람이 그분의 거실에서 그분이 깜짝 놀랄 만한 견해를 들려줄 겁니다."], ["Ah!”", "아!\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, vauntingly, “as much as if he’d gone and knocked his head again a door!”", "커틀 선장이 으스대며 말했다. \"마치 가서 문에 머리를 박은 것만큼이나 말이지요!\""]], [["“Let us take this gentleman to see him, and let us hear what he says,”", "\"그분을 만나러 이 신사분을 모셔 가서, 뭐라고 하시는지 들어 봐요,\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."], ["“Will you go with us now?", "\"지금 우리와 함께 가시겠어요?"], ["We have a coach here.”", "여기 마차가 있어요.\""]], [["Again the Captain clapped his hand to his head, on which the hard glazed hat was not, and looked discomfited.", "선장은 다시 손을 머리로 가져갔으나 거기엔 그 단단한 광택 모자가 없었고, 당황한 기색을 보였다."], ["But at this instant a most remarkable phenomenon occurred.", "그러나 바로 그 순간, 더없이 기이한 현상이 일어났다."], ["The door opening, without any note of preparation, and apparently of itself, the hard glazed hat in question skimmed into the room like a bird, and alighted heavily at the Captain’s feet.", "아무런 예고도 없이, 그리고 보아하니 저절로 문이 열리더니, 문제의 그 단단한 광택 모자가 새처럼 방 안으로 휙 날아들어 선장의 발치에 묵직하게 내려앉았다."], ["The door then shut as violently as it had opened, and nothing ensued in explanation of the prodigy.", "그러고 나서 문은 열렸을 때만큼이나 거칠게 닫혔고, 그 기적을 설명해 줄 어떤 일도 뒤따르지 않았다."]], [["Captain Cuttle picked up his hat, and having turned it over with a look of interest and welcome, began to polish it on his sleeve.", "커틀 선장은 모자를 집어 들고, 흥미와 환영의 눈길로 그것을 이리저리 돌려 본 뒤, 소매에 대고 닦기 시작했다."], ["While doing so, the Captain eyed his visitors intently, and said in a low voice,\n“You see I should have bore down on Sol Gills yesterday, and this morning, but she—she took it away and kept it.", "그러면서 선장은 손님들을 유심히 바라보며 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"보다시피 어제도 오늘 아침에도 솔 길스에게 들이닥쳤어야 했는데, 그 여자가—그 여자가 이걸 가져가서 안 내놓았지요."], ["That’s the long and short of the subject.”", "그게 이 일의 전말입니다.\""]], [["“Who did, for goodness sake?”", "\"세상에, 누가요?\""], ["asked Susan Nipper.", "수전 니퍼가 물었다."]], [["“The lady of the house, my dear,”", "\"이 집 안주인이오, 아가씨,\""], ["returned the Captain, in a gruff whisper, and making signals of secrecy.", "선장이 거친 속삭임으로 응수하며 비밀 신호를 보냈다."], ["“We had some words about the swabbing of these here planks, and she—In short,”", "\"이 마룻장 닦는 일을 두고 몇 마디 주고받았는데, 그 여자가—요컨대,\""], ["said the Captain, eyeing the door, and relieving himself with a long breath, “she stopped my liberty.”", "선장이 문을 흘끔거리며 긴 한숨으로 마음을 달래고 말했다. \"내 자유를 막아 버린 거지요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I wish she had me to deal with!”", "그 여자가 저를 상대했으면 좋았을 텐데!\""], ["said Susan, reddening with the energy of the wish.", "수전이 그 소망의 기세로 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“I’d stop her!”", "\"제가 막아 버렸을 거예요!\""]], [["“Would you, do you, my dear?”", "\"그러시겠소, 정말로, 아가씨?\""], ["rejoined the Captain, shaking his head doubtfully, but regarding the desperate courage of the fair aspirant with obvious admiration.", "선장이 미심쩍게 고개를 저으면서도, 그 아리따운 지망자의 필사적인 용기를 분명한 감탄으로 바라보며 응수했다."], ["“I don’t know.", "\"글쎄요."], ["It’s difficult navigation.", "어려운 항해입니다."], ["She’s very hard to carry on with, my dear.", "그 여자는 상대하기가 아주 까다로워요, 아가씨."], ["You never can tell how she’ll head, you see.", "어느 쪽으로 뱃머리를 돌릴지 도무지 알 수가 없거든요."], ["She’s full one minute, and round upon you next. And when she in a tartar,”", "한순간엔 돛을 잔뜩 펴고, 다음 순간엔 휙 돌아서서 덤벼들지요."], ["said the Captain, with the perspiration breaking out upon his forehead.", "그러다 그 여자가 발끈하면,\" 선장이 이마에 땀이 배어 나오며 말했다."], ["There was nothing but a whistle emphatic enough for the conclusion of the sentence, so the Captain whistled tremulously.", "이 문장의 결말로는 휘파람만큼 강조될 만한 것이 없었으므로, 선장은 떨리게 휘파람을 불었다."], ["After which he again shook his head, and recurring to his admiration of Miss Nipper’s devoted bravery, timidly repeated, “Would you, do you think, my dear?”", "그러고 나서 다시 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는, 니퍼 양의 헌신적인 용기에 대한 감탄으로 되돌아가, 소심하게 되풀이했다. \"정말 그러시겠소, 아가씨?\""]], [["Susan only replied with a bridling smile, but that was so very full of defiance, that there is no knowing how long Captain Cuttle might have stood entranced in its contemplation, if Florence in her anxiety had not again proposed their immediately resorting to the oracular Bunsby.", "수전은 그저 새침하게 미소로만 대답했으나, 그 미소가 어찌나 도전으로 가득했던지, 플로렌스가 안달하며 신탁 같은 번스비에게 즉시 가 보자고 다시 제안하지 않았던들, 커틀 선장이 얼마나 오래 그것을 바라보며 넋을 잃고 서 있었을지 알 수 없는 노릇이었다."], ["Thus reminded of his duty, Captain Cuttle put on the glazed hat firmly, took up another knobby stick, with which he had supplied the place of that one given to Walter, and offering his arm to Florence, prepared to cut his way through the enemy.", "이렇게 자기 할 일을 일깨워진 선장은 광택 모자를 단단히 쓰고, 월터에게 준 그 지팡이를 대신해 마련해 둔 또 다른 옹이투성이 지팡이를 집어 들고는, 플로렌스에게 팔을 내밀어, 적진을 헤치고 나아갈 채비를 했다."]], [["It turned out, however, that Mrs MacStinger had already changed her course, and that she headed, as the Captain had remarked she often did, in quite a new direction.", "그러나 알고 보니 맥스팅거 부인은 이미 항로를 바꾸어, 선장이 그녀가 곧잘 그런다고 말했던 대로, 전혀 새로운 방향으로 뱃머리를 돌린 상태였다."], ["For when they got downstairs, they found that exemplary woman beating the mats on the doorsteps, with Alexander, still upon the paving-stone, dimly looming through a fog of dust; and so absorbed was Mrs MacStinger in her household occupation, that when Captain Cuttle and his visitors passed, she beat the harder, and neither by word nor gesture showed any consciousness of their vicinity.", "일행이 아래층으로 내려가 보니 그 본보기 같은 여인은 현관 계단에서 깔개를 두들기고 있었고, 알렉산더는 여전히 포석 위에서 먼지 안개 속에 어렴풋이 어른거렸다. 맥스팅거 부인은 자기 집안일에 어찌나 몰두해 있던지, 커틀 선장과 손님들이 지나갈 때 오히려 더 세게 두들겨 댈 뿐, 말로도 몸짓으로도 그들이 곁에 있다는 기색을 전혀 내비치지 않았다."], ["The Captain was so well pleased with this easy escape—although the effect of the door-mats on him was like a copious administration of snuff, and made him sneeze until the tears ran down his face—that he could hardly believe his good fortune; but more than once, between the door and the hackney-coach, looked over his shoulder, with an obvious apprehension of Mrs MacStinger’s giving chase yet.", "선장은 이 손쉬운 탈출에 어찌나 흡족했던지—깔개의 효과가 그에게는 코담배를 듬뿍 들이마신 것과 같아서, 얼굴에 눈물이 흐를 만큼 재채기를 해 댔는데도—자기 행운을 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다. 그래서 문과 삯마차 사이에서 한 번 이상 어깨 너머를 돌아보았으니, 맥스팅거 부인이 여태 뒤쫓아 오는 건 아닐까 하는 뚜렷한 우려에서였다."]], [["However, they got to the corner of Brig Place without any molestation from that terrible fire-ship; and the Captain mounting the coach-box—for his gallantry would not allow him to ride inside with the ladies, though besought to do so—piloted the driver on his course for Captain Bunsby’s vessel, which was called the Cautious Clara, and was lying hard by Ratcliffe.", "그러나 일행은 그 무시무시한 화공선(火攻船)에게서 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 브리그 플레이스 모퉁이에 이르렀다. 그리고 선장은—그 기사도 정신이, 안으로 들어와 타라고 간청받았는데도 숙녀들과 함께 마차 안에 타기를 허락하지 않았으므로—마부석에 올라, 번스비 선장의 배가 있는 쪽으로 마부를 인도했으니, 그 배는 '신중한 클라라' 호라 불렸고 래트클리프 바로 곁에 정박해 있었다."]], [["Arrived at the wharf off which this great commander’s ship was jammed in among some five hundred companions, whose tangled rigging looked like monstrous cobwebs half swept down, Captain Cuttle appeared at the coach-window, and invited Florence and Miss Nipper to accompany him on board; observing that Bunsby was to the last degree soft-hearted in respect of ladies, and that nothing would so much tend to bring his expansive intellect into a state of harmony as their presentation to the Cautious Clara.", "이 위대한 지휘관의 배가 오백 척쯤 되는 동료 배들 사이에 끼여 있는 부두에 다다랐는데, 그 뒤엉킨 삭구들은 반쯤 쓸려 내려간 거대한 거미줄처럼 보였다. 커틀 선장은 마차 창에 모습을 드러내, 플로렌스와 니퍼 양에게 자기와 함께 배에 올라가자고 청했다. 번스비가 숙녀에 관한 한 더없이 마음이 무른 사람이라, 그를 '신중한 클라라' 호로 소개하는 것보다 그 광대한 지성을 조화로운 상태로 이끄는 데 더 좋은 것은 없으리라고 말하면서였다."]], [["Florence readily consented; and the Captain, taking her little hand in his prodigious palm, led her, with a mixed expression of patronage, paternity, pride, and ceremony, that was pleasant to see, over several very dirty decks, until, coming to the Clara, they found that cautious craft (which lay outside the tier) with her gangway removed, and half-a-dozen feet of river interposed between herself and her nearest neighbour.", "플로렌스는 선뜻 응했다. 선장은 그 작은 손을 자기 거대한 손바닥에 쥐고서, 보기에 흐뭇한 후견과 부정(父情)과 자부심과 격식이 뒤섞인 표정으로, 몹시 더러운 갑판 여러 개를 가로질러 그녀를 이끌었다."], ["It appeared, from Captain Cuttle’s explanation, that the great Bunsby, like himself, was cruelly treated by his landlady, and that when her usage of him for the time being was so hard that he could bear it no longer, he set this gulf between them as a last resource.", "마침내 클라라 호에 이르러 보니, 그 신중한 배는 (배들이 늘어선 줄 바깥에 떠 있었는데) 건널 판자가 치워진 채, 가장 가까운 이웃 배와 자기 사이에 강물 여섯 자가량을 두고 있었다. 커틀 선장의 설명으로 미루어 보건대, 위대한 번스비도 그 자신처럼 셋집 안주인에게 모질게 시달리는지라, 안주인의 그때그때 처사가 더는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 가혹해지면 최후의 수단으로 둘 사이에 이런 심연을 두는 것이었다."]], [["“Clara a-hoy!”", "\"클라라 호 어어이!\""], ["cried the Captain, putting a hand to each side of his mouth.", "선장이 양손을 입 양옆에 대고 외쳤다."]], [["“A-hoy!”", "\"어어이!\""], ["cried a boy, like the Captain’s echo, tumbling up from below.", "마치 선장의 메아리처럼 한 소년이 아래에서 굴러 올라오며 외쳤다."]], [["“Bunsby aboard?”", "\"번스비 배에 있나?\""], ["cried the Captain, hailing the boy in a stentorian voice, as if he were half-a-mile off instead of two yards.", "선장이 두 자밖에 안 되는 거리인데도 마치 반 마일 떨어진 양 우렁찬 목소리로 그 소년을 향해 외쳤다."]], [["“Ay, ay!”", "\"예, 예!\""], ["cried the boy, in the same tone.", "소년이 같은 어조로 외쳤다."]], [["The boy then shoved out a plank to Captain Cuttle, who adjusted it carefully, and led Florence across: returning presently for Miss Nipper.", "그러고 나서 소년이 커틀 선장에게 판자를 밀어 내밀었고, 선장은 그것을 조심스레 맞춰 놓고 플로렌스를 건너게 한 다음, 곧 니퍼 양을 데리러 돌아왔다."], ["So they stood upon the deck of the Cautious Clara, in whose standing rigging, divers fluttering articles of dress were curing, in company with a few tongues and some mackerel.", "그렇게 두 사람은 '신중한 클라라' 호의 갑판 위에 섰는데, 그 고정 삭구에는 갖가지 옷가지가 펄럭이며, 우설 몇 점과 고등어 몇 마리와 더불어 말려지고 있었다."]], [["Immediately there appeared, coming slowly up above the bulk-head of the cabin, another bulk-head—human, and very large—with one stationary eye in the mahogany face, and one revolving one, on the principle of some lighthouses.", "곧 선실 격벽 위로 또 다른 격벽이—사람의, 그것도 아주 커다란 격벽이—천천히 솟아올랐다. 마호가니빛 얼굴에 한쪽 눈은 고정되어 있고 다른 한쪽 눈은 어떤 등대의 원리처럼 빙글빙글 도는 것이었다."], ["This head was decorated with shaggy hair, like oakum, which had no governing inclination towards the north, east, west, or south, but inclined to all four quarters of the compass, and to every point upon it.", "이 머리는 뱃밥처럼 텁수룩한 머리카락으로 장식되어 있었는데, 그 머리카락은 북이든 동이든 서든 남이든 어느 한 방향으로 쏠리는 법이 없이, 나침반의 네 방위 전부로, 그리고 그 위의 모든 지점으로 뻗쳐 있었다."], ["The head was followed by a perfect desert of chin, and by a shirt-collar and neckerchief, and by a dreadnought pilot-coat, and by a pair of dreadnought pilot-trousers, whereof the waistband was so very broad and high, that it became a succedaneum for a waistcoat: being ornamented near the wearer’s breastbone with some massive wooden buttons, like backgammon men.", "머리 뒤로는 완벽한 황무지 같은 턱이 따라 나왔고, 셔츠 깃과 목도리가, 그리고 두꺼운 방수 파일럿 외투가, 또 한 쌍의 두꺼운 방수 파일럿 바지가 따라 나왔으니, 그 바지춤은 어찌나 넓고 높던지 조끼 대용으로 쓰일 지경이라, 입은 이의 복장뼈 가까이에 주사위놀이 말 같은 묵직한 나무 단추 몇 개가 장식으로 달려 있었다."], ["As the lower portions of these pantaloons became revealed, Bunsby stood confessed; his hands in their pockets, which were of vast size; and his gaze directed, not to Captain Cuttle or the ladies, but the mast-head.", "이 바지의 아래쪽이 드러나자, 비로소 번스비의 전모가 밝혀졌다. 양손은 어마어마하게 큰 주머니에 찌른 채, 그 시선은 커틀 선장이나 숙녀들이 아니라 돛대 꼭대기를 향하고 있었다."]], [["The profound appearance of this philosopher, who was bulky and strong, and on whose extremely red face an expression of taciturnity sat enthroned, not inconsistent with his character, in which that quality was proudly conspicuous, almost daunted Captain Cuttle, though on familiar terms with him.", "이 철학자는 몸집이 크고 힘이 셌으며, 더없이 붉은 그 얼굴에는 과묵함의 표정이 왕좌에 앉은 듯 자리 잡고 있었으니, 그것은 과묵함이라는 자질이 자랑스럽게 두드러지는 그의 성격과 어긋나지 않았다. 그의 그 심오한 풍모는, 비록 그와 허물없는 사이인 커틀 선장조차 거의 주눅 들게 할 정도였다."], ["Whispering to Florence that Bunsby had never in his life expressed surprise, and was considered not to know what it meant, the Captain watched him as he eyed his mast-head, and afterwards swept the horizon; and when the revolving eye seemed to be coming round in his direction, said:", "번스비는 평생 단 한 번도 놀라움을 드러낸 적이 없으며 놀라움이 무엇인지조차 모르는 것으로 여겨진다고 플로렌스에게 속삭이며, 선장은 그가 돛대 꼭대기를 바라보다가 이윽고 수평선을 훑는 것을 지켜보았다. 그리고 그 빙글빙글 도는 눈이 자기 쪽으로 돌아오는 듯하자, 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Bunsby, my lad, how fares it?”", "\"번스비, 친구, 형편이 어떤가?\""]], [["A deep, gruff, husky utterance, which seemed to have no connexion with Bunsby, and certainly had not the least effect upon his face, replied, “Ay, ay, shipmet, how goes it?”", "깊고 거칠고 쉰 목소리가, 번스비와는 아무 연관도 없는 듯하고 그의 얼굴에는 분명 아무런 변화도 일으키지 않은 채 대답했다. \"예, 예, 동료, 어떻게 지내나?\""], ["At the same time Bunsby’s right hand and arm, emerging from a pocket, shook the Captain’s, and went back again.", "동시에 번스비의 오른손과 팔이 주머니에서 나와 선장의 손을 흔들고는 다시 들어갔다."]], [["“Bunsby,”", "\"번스비,\""], ["said the Captain, striking home at once, “here you are; a man of mind, and a man as can give an opinion.", "선장이 단번에 본론을 찔러 말했다. \"여기 자네가 있네, 정신을 갖춘 사람이자 견해를 내놓을 줄 아는 사람이지."], ["Here’s a young lady as wants to take that opinion, in regard of my friend Wal”r; likewise my t’other friend, Sol Gills, which is a character for you to come within hail of, being a man of science, which is the mother of invention, and knows no law.", "여기 한 젊은 숙녀가 내 친구 월'터에 관해 그 견해를 듣고 싶어 하네. 또 내 다른 친구 솔 길스에 관해서도 그렇고, 그분으로 말하자면 자네가 한번 가까이 사귀어 볼 만한 인물이라, 과학을 하는 사람이거든, 과학은 발명의 어머니요 어떤 법도 모르는 법이지."], ["Bunsby, will you wear, to oblige me, and come along with us?”", "번스비, 날 봐서, 침로를 돌려 우리와 함께 가 주겠나?\""]], [["The great commander, who seemed by expression of his visage to be always on the look-out for something in the extremest distance, and to have no ocular knowledge of anything within ten miles, made no reply whatever.", "그 위대한 지휘관은, 얼굴 표정으로 보아 늘 더없이 먼 거리의 무언가를 살피느라 십 마일 이내의 무엇에 대해서는 눈으로 아는 바가 전혀 없는 듯했는데, 아무런 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Here is a man,” said the Captain, addressing himself to his fair auditors, and indicating the commander with his outstretched hook, “that has fell down, more than any man alive; that has had more accidents happen to his own self than the Seamen’s Hospital to all hands; that took as many spars and bars and bolts about the outside of his head when he was young, as you’d want a order for on Chatham-yard to build a pleasure yacht with; and yet that his opinions in that way, it’s my belief, for there ain’t nothing like ’em afloat or ashore.”", "\"여기 이 사람은,\" 선장이 그 아리따운 청중을 향해 말하며 내뻗은 갈고리로 그 지휘관을 가리켰다. \"살아 있는 어떤 사람보다도 더 많이 떨어져 본 사람이고, 선원 병원이 모든 환자에게 겪은 것보다 더 많은 사고를 자기 한 몸에 당해 본 사람입니다. 젊었을 적에 머리 바깥쪽에 활대며 가로대며 볼트를 어찌나 많이 맞았던지, 그걸로 채텀 조선소에 유람용 요트 한 척을 짓는 주문을 넣어도 될 만큼이었지요. 그런데도 그 방면에 관한 그의 견해로 말하자면, 바다 위에서든 뭍에서든 그만한 것이 없다, 이게 내 믿음입니다.\""]], [["The stolid commander appeared by a very slight vibration in his elbows, to express some satisfaction in this encomium; but if his face had been as distant as his gaze was, it could hardly have enlightened the beholders less in reference to anything that was passing in his thoughts.", "그 둔중한 지휘관은 팔꿈치를 아주 살짝 떨어 이 찬사에 다소 만족을 표하는 듯했으나, 만일 그의 얼굴이 그의 시선만큼 멀리 떨어져 있었다 한들, 그의 생각 속에 무엇이 오가는지에 대해 보는 이들을 이보다 더 모르게 할 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["“Shipmet,”", "\"동료,\""], ["said Bunsby, all of a sudden, and stooping down to look out under some interposing spar, “what’ll the ladies drink?”", "번스비가 별안간, 가로지른 활대 밑을 내다보려 허리를 굽히며 말했다. \"숙녀분들은 뭘 마실까?\""]], [["Captain Cuttle, whose delicacy was shocked by such an inquiry in connection with Florence, drew the sage aside, and seeming to explain in his ear, accompanied him below; where, that he might not take offence, the Captain drank a dram himself, which Florence and Susan, glancing down the open skylight, saw the sage, with difficulty finding room for himself between his berth and a very little brass fireplace, serve out for self and friend.", "플로렌스와 결부된 그런 물음에 그 섬세함이 충격을 받은 커틀 선장은, 그 현인을 옆으로 데려가 귀에 대고 설명하는 듯하더니, 그를 데리고 아래로 내려갔다. 그곳에서 그가 기분 상하지 않도록 선장은 자기도 한잔 들이켰는데, 플로렌스와 수전이 열린 천창으로 내려다보니, 그 현인이 자기 침상과 아주 자그마한 놋쇠 벽난로 사이에 가까스로 몸 둘 자리를 찾아, 자기와 친구를 위한 술을 따르고 있었다."], ["They soon reappeared on deck, and Captain Cuttle, triumphing in the success of his enterprise, conducted Florence back to the coach, while Bunsby followed, escorting Miss Nipper, whom he hugged upon the way (much to that young lady’s indignation) with his pilot-coated arm, like a blue bear.", "둘은 곧 갑판에 다시 나타났고, 자기 계획의 성공에 의기양양해진 커틀 선장은 플로렌스를 마차로 도로 안내했다. 그동안 번스비는 니퍼 양을 호위하며 뒤따랐는데, 가는 길에 파일럿 외투를 입은 그 팔로 그녀를 (그 처녀가 몹시 분개하게도) 마치 푸른 곰처럼 껴안았다."]], [["The Captain put his oracle inside, and gloried so much in having secured him, and having got that mind into a hackney-coach, that he could not refrain from often peeping in at Florence through the little window behind the driver, and testifying his delight in smiles, and also in taps upon his forehead, to hint to her that the brain of Bunsby was hard at it.", "선장은 자기 신탁자를 안에 태우고는, 그를 확보했다는 것, 그 정신을 삯마차 안에 들여놓았다는 것에 어찌나 의기양양했던지, 마부 뒤의 작은 창으로 플로렌스를 자주 들여다보며 미소로, 또 자기 이마를 톡톡 두드려, 번스비의 두뇌가 한창 그 일에 골몰하고 있다는 것을 그녀에게 넌지시 알리지 않고는 배길 수가 없었다."], ["In the meantime, Bunsby, still hugging Miss Nipper (for his friend, the Captain, had not exaggerated the softness of his heart), uniformly preserved his gravity of deportment, and showed no other consciousness of her or anything.", "그러는 동안 번스비는 여전히 니퍼 양을 껴안은 채(그의 친구 선장이 그의 마음의 무름을 과장한 것은 아니었으므로) 한결같이 진중한 태도를 지켰으며, 그녀에 대해서도 다른 무엇에 대해서도 아무런 의식의 기색을 보이지 않았다."]], [["Uncle Sol, who had come home, received them at the door, and ushered them immediately into the little back parlour: strangely altered by the absence of Walter.", "집에 돌아와 있던 솔 삼촌이 문간에서 그들을 맞아, 즉시 그 자그마한 뒤쪽 거실로 안내했는데, 그곳은 월터의 부재로 묘하게 달라져 있었다."], ["On the table, and about the room, were the charts and maps on which the heavy-hearted Instrument-maker had again and again tracked the missing vessel across the sea, and on which, with a pair of compasses that he still had in his hand, he had been measuring, a minute before, how far she must have driven, to have driven here or there: and trying to demonstrate that a long time must elapse before hope was exhausted.", "탁자 위에, 그리고 방 곳곳에, 마음 무거운 기구 제작자가 그 사라진 배를 바다 위로 거듭거듭 좇아 본 해도와 지도들이 펼쳐져 있었다. 그는 아직 손에 쥐고 있는 컴퍼스 한 쌍으로, 바로 조금 전까지, 배가 이리저리 떠밀려 가려면 얼마나 멀리 떠밀렸어야 하는지를 재고 있었으며, 희망이 다하기까지 아직 오랜 시간이 걸릴 수밖에 없음을 입증해 보이려 애쓰던 참이었다."]], [["“Whether she can have run,”", "\"배가 달려갔을까,\""], ["said Uncle Sol, looking wistfully over the chart; “but no, that’s almost impossible or whether she can have been forced by stress of weather,—but that’s not reasonably likely.", "솔 삼촌이 해도를 애처롭게 들여다보며 말했다. \"아니, 그건 거의 불가능하지."], ["Or whether there is any hope she so far changed her course as—but even I can hardly hope that!”", "아니면 날씨가 사나워서 떠밀렸을까—하지만 그것도 그럴듯하지 않아."], ["With such broken suggestions, poor old Uncle Sol roamed over the great sheet before him, and could not find a speck of hopeful probability in it large enough to set one small point of the compasses upon.", "아니면 진로를 그만큼 바꿨을 가망이 조금이라도—하지만 나조차 그건 도무지 바라기 어렵구나!\" 이런 토막토막한 추측을 늘어놓으며, 가련한 늙은 솔 삼촌은 눈앞의 그 커다란 종이 위를 헤매었으나, 컴퍼스 한쪽 끝을 댈 만한, 희망적인 개연성이라곤 티끌만큼도 찾아낼 수 없었다."]], [["Florence saw immediately—it would have been difficult to help seeing—that there was a singular, indescribable change in the old man, and that while his manner was far more restless and unsettled than usual, there was yet a curious, contradictory decision in it, that perplexed her very much.", "플로렌스는 이내—못 알아채기가 어려웠을 것이다—그 노인에게 묘한, 말로 표현하기 어려운 변화가 있음을 알아차렸다. 그의 태도가 평소보다 훨씬 더 안절부절못하고 들떠 있으면서도, 그 안에는 묘하게 모순된 단호함이 깃들어 있어, 그녀를 몹시 어리둥절하게 했다."], ["She fancied once that he spoke wildly, and at random; for on her saying she regretted not to have seen him when she had been there before that morning, he at first replied that he had been to see her, and directly afterwards seemed to wish to recall that answer.", "그녀는 한 번 그가 종잡을 수 없이 마구잡이로 말한다고 여겼으니, 그날 아침 먼저 여기 왔을 때 그를 못 뵈어 아쉬웠다고 그녀가 말하자, 그는 처음에 자기가 그녀를 보러 갔었다고 대답했다가, 곧바로 그 대답을 거두고 싶어 하는 듯했기 때문이다."]], [["“You have been to see me?”", "\"저를 보러 오셨다고요?\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“Today?”", "\"오늘요?\""]], [["“Yes, my dear young lady,”", "\"그래, 아가씨,\""], ["returned Uncle Sol, looking at her and away from her in a confused manner.", "솔 삼촌이 그녀를 보았다 시선을 피했다 하며 혼란스러운 태도로 응수했다."], ["“I wished to see you with my own eyes, and to hear you with my own ears, once more before—”", "\"한 번 더 내 눈으로 너를 보고, 내 귀로 네 목소리를 듣고 싶었단다, 그—전에—\""], ["There he stopped.", "거기서 그는 말을 멈췄다."]], [["“Before when?", "\"전에 언제요?"], ["Before what?”", "무엇 전에요?\""], ["said Florence, putting her hand upon his arm.", "플로렌스가 그의 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다."]], [["“Did I say ‘before?’”", "\"내가 '전에'라고 했니?\""], ["replied old Sol.", "늙은 솔이 응수했다."], ["“If I did, I must have meant before we should have news of my dear boy.”", "\"그랬다면, 내 사랑하는 아이의 소식을 듣기 전에, 라는 뜻이었을 게다.\""]], [["“You are not well,”", "\"편찮으시군요,\""], ["said Florence, tenderly.", "플로렌스가 다정하게 말했다."], ["“You have been so very anxious I am sure you are not well.”", "\"너무 노심초사하셔서 분명 편찮으신 거예요.\""]], [["“I am as well,”", "\"나는 괜찮다,\""], ["returned the old man, shutting up his right hand, and holding it out to show her: “as well and firm as any man at my time of life can hope to be.", "노인이 오른손을 꼭 쥐었다가 펴 보이며 응수했다. \"내 나이의 사람이 바랄 수 있는 만큼 멀쩡하고 단단하단다."], ["See!", "보아라!"], ["It’s steady.", "떨림이 없지."], ["Is its master not as capable of resolution and fortitude as many a younger man?", "이 손의 주인이 어지간한 젊은이 못지않게 결단과 인내를 발휘할 수 있지 않겠느냐? 나는 그렇게 생각한다."], ["I think so. We shall see.”", "두고 보자꾸나.\""]], [["There was that in his manner more than in his words, though they remained with her too, which impressed Florence so much, that she would have confided her uneasiness to Captain Cuttle at that moment, if the Captain had not seized that moment for expounding the state of circumstance, on which the opinion of the sagacious Bunsby was requested, and entreating that profound authority to deliver the same.", "그의 말 속에서보다도 그 태도 속에 무언가가—비록 그 말들 또한 그녀의 마음에 남았지만—플로렌스에게 깊은 인상을 주어, 그녀는 그 순간 자기의 불안을 커틀 선장에게 털어놓았을 것이다. 만일 선장이 바로 그 순간을, 슬기로운 번스비의 견해를 청한 그 사정의 정황을 설명하고, 그 심오한 권위자에게 어서 그 견해를 내려 달라고 간청하는 데 쓰지 않았다면 말이다."]], [["Bunsby, whose eye continued to be addressed to somewhere about the half-way house between London and Gravesend, two or three times put out his rough right arm, as seeking to wind it for inspiration round the fair form of Miss Nipper; but that young female having withdrawn herself, in displeasure, to the opposite side of the table, the soft heart of the Commander of the Cautious Clara met with no response to its impulses.", "번스비는 그 눈이 여전히 런던과 그레이브젠드 사이 중간쯤 되는 어디께를 향한 채, 두세 번 거친 오른팔을 내뻗어, 영감을 얻으려는 듯 그것을 니퍼 양의 아리따운 자태에 휘감으려 했다. 그러나 그 처녀가 못마땅한 기색으로 탁자 맞은편으로 몸을 빼 버린 탓에, '신중한 클라라'"], ["After sundry failures in this wise, the Commander, addressing himself to nobody, thus spake; or rather the voice within him said of its own accord, and quite independent of himself, as if he were possessed by a gruff spirit:", "호 지휘관의 무른 마음은 그 충동에 아무런 응답도 얻지 못했다. 이런 식으로 몇 차례 실패한 뒤, 그 지휘관은 아무에게도 아닌 듯 말하기 시작했으니, 아니, 차라리 그 안의 목소리가 마치 그가 어떤 거친 정령에 씌기라도 한 양, 그와는 전혀 별개로 제멋대로 말했다."]], [["“My name’s Jack Bunsby!”", "\"내 이름은 잭 번스비요!\""]], [["“He was christened John,”", "\"세례명은 존이지요,\""], ["cried the delighted Captain Cuttle.", "기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르는 커틀 선장이 외쳤다."], ["“Hear him!”", "\"저 말 좀 들어 보시오!\""]], [["“And what I says,”", "\"그리고 내가 말한 건,\""], ["pursued the voice, after some deliberation, “I stands to.”", "그 목소리가 잠시 숙고한 뒤 이어 갔다. \"내가 지킨다.\""]], [["The Captain, with Florence on his arm, nodded at the auditory, and seemed to say, “Now he’s coming out.", "선장은 플로렌스를 팔에 낀 채 청중을 향해 고개를 끄덕였으니, 마치 \"이제 슬슬 나오는군."], ["This is what I meant when I brought him.”", "내가 그를 데려온 게 바로 이 때문이오\"라고 말하는 듯했다."]], [["“Whereby,”", "\"고로,\""], ["proceeded the voice, “why not?", "그 목소리가 계속했다. \"왜 아니겠소?"], ["If so, what odds?", "만일 그렇다면, 무슨 차이가 있겠소?"], ["Can any man say otherwise?", "누구든 달리 말할 수 있겠소? 없지."], ["No. Awast then!”", "그러니 잠깐 멈추시오!\""]], [["When it had pursued its train of argument to this point, the voice stopped, and rested.", "이 지점까지 논증의 갈래를 좇은 뒤, 목소리는 멈추고 쉬었다."], ["It then proceeded very slowly, thus:", "그러고는 아주 느릿느릿 이렇게 이어 갔다."]], [["“Do I believe that this here Son and Heir’s gone down, my lads?", "\"이 '아들과 후계자' 호가 가라앉았다고 내가 믿느냐, 친구들?"], ["Mayhap.", "어쩌면."], ["Do I say so? Which?", "내가 그렇다고 말하느냐?"], ["If a skipper stands out by Sen’ George’s Channel, making for the Downs, what’s right ahead of him?", "어느 쪽이냐? 한 선장이 세인트조지 해협을 끼고 나아가 다운스로 향한다면, 그의 바로 앞에 무엇이 있느냐?"], ["The Goodwins.", "굿윈 모래톱이지."], ["He isn’t forced to run upon the Goodwins, but he may.", "그가 굿윈에 올라앉도록 강요받는 건 아니지만, 그럴 수도 있지."], ["The bearings of this observation lays in the application on it.", "이 소견의 방위는 그걸 어떻게 적용하느냐에 달려 있소."], ["That ain’t no part of my duty.", "그건 내 직분의 몫이 아니지."], ["Awast then, keep a bright look-out for’ard, and good luck to you!”", "그러니 잠깐 멈추고, 뱃머리 쪽을 환히 살피며, 행운이 함께하기를!\""]], [["The voice here went out of the back parlour and into the street, taking the Commander of the Cautious Clara with it, and accompanying him on board again with all convenient expedition, where he immediately turned in, and refreshed his mind with a nap.", "여기서 목소리는 뒤쪽 거실을 빠져나가 거리로 나갔으니, '신중한 클라라' 호의 지휘관을 데리고 가서, 더없이 신속하게 그를 다시 배에 태워다 주었고, 거기서 그는 즉시 잠자리에 들어 낮잠으로 정신을 가다듬었다."]], [["The students of the sage’s precepts, left to their own application of his wisdom—upon a principle which was the main leg of the Bunsby tripod, as it is perchance of some other oracular stools—looked upon one another in a little uncertainty; while Rob the Grinder, who had taken the innocent freedom of peering in, and listening, through the skylight in the roof, came softly down from the leads, in a state of very dense confusion.", "그 현인의 가르침을 받든 학생들은—그의 지혜를 스스로 적용하라는, 번스비라는 삼각대의 주된 다리이자 어쩌면 다른 어떤 신탁의 의자에도 해당될 그 원칙에 따라 내버려진 채—약간의 막막함 속에 서로를 바라보았다. 한편 천진난만하게 지붕의 천창으로 들여다보며 엿듣는 자유를 누렸던 분쇄기 롭은, 몹시 짙은 혼란에 빠진 채 지붕에서 살그머니 내려왔다."], ["Captain Cuttle, however, whose admiration of Bunsby was, if possible, enhanced by the splendid manner in which he had justified his reputation and come through this solemn reference, proceeded to explain that Bunsby meant nothing but confidence; that Bunsby had no misgivings; and that such an opinion as that man had given, coming from such a mind as his, was Hope’s own anchor, with good roads to cast it in.", "그러나 커틀 선장은, 번스비가 자기 명성을 정당화하고 이 엄숙한 자문을 훌륭하게 치러 낸 그 멋진 방식으로 그에 대한 감탄이 가능하다면 더 커진 터라, 번스비의 말은 확신 외에 다른 뜻이 아니다, 번스비에게는 불안 따위 없다, 그런 정신에서 나온 그런 견해야말로 희망 자신의 닻이며, 그것을 내릴 좋은 정박지까지 갖춘 셈이라고 설명하기 시작했다."], ["Florence endeavoured to believe that the Captain was right; but the Nipper, with her arms tight folded, shook her head in resolute denial, and had no more trust in Bunsby than in Mr Perch himself.", "플로렌스는 선장이 옳다고 믿으려 애썼으나, 니퍼는 두 팔을 단단히 낀 채 단호히 부정하며 고개를 저었으니, 번스비를 퍼치 씨만큼도 믿지 않았다."]], [["The philosopher seemed to have left Uncle Sol pretty much where he had found him, for he still went roaming about the watery world, compasses in hand, and discovering no rest for them.", "그 철학자는 솔 삼촌을 발견했을 때와 거의 같은 상태로 남겨 둔 듯했으니, 노인은 여전히 컴퍼스를 손에 든 채 그 물의 세계를 헤매며 그것을 내려놓을 안식처를 찾지 못하고 있었다."], ["It was in pursuance of a whisper in his ear from Florence, while the old man was absorbed in this pursuit, that Captain Cuttle laid his heavy hand upon his shoulder.", "노인이 이 일에 몰두해 있는 동안 플로렌스가 귀에 속삭인 데 따라, 커틀 선장이 그의 어깨에 묵직한 손을 얹었다."]], [["“What cheer, Sol Gills?”", "\"기운 좀 어떻소, 솔 길스?\""], ["cried the Captain, heartily.", "선장이 진심으로 외쳤다."]], [["“But so-so, Ned,”", "\"그저 그렇소, 네드,\""], ["returned the Instrument-maker.", "기구 제작자가 응수했다."], ["“I have been remembering, all this afternoon, that on the very day when my boy entered Dombey’s House, and came home late to dinner, sitting just there where you stand, we talked of storm and shipwreck, and I could hardly turn him from the subject.”", "\"오후 내내 떠올리고 있었다오, 우리 아이가 돔비 회사에 들어가던 바로 그날, 저녁을 먹으러 늦게 집에 와서, 지금 자네가 서 있는 바로 거기 앉아, 우리가 폭풍과 난파 이야기를 나누었는데, 내가 도무지 그 애를 그 화제에서 돌려놓을 수가 없었던 일을 말이오.\""]], [["But meeting the eyes of Florence, which were fixed with earnest scrutiny upon his face, the old man stopped and smiled.", "그러나 자기 얼굴을 간절한 눈길로 살피는 플로렌스의 눈과 마주치자, 노인은 말을 멈추고 미소 지었다."]], [["“Stand by, old friend!”", "\"기운 내시오, 오랜 친구!\""], ["cried the Captain.", "선장이 외쳤다."], ["“Look alive!", "\"씩씩하게!"], ["I tell you what, Sol Gills; arter I’ve convoyed Heart’s-delight safe home,”", "내 말 들으시오, 솔 길스. 내 마음의 기쁨을 무사히 집까지 호송해 드린 다음,\""], ["here the Captain kissed his hook to Florence, “I’ll come back and take you in tow for the rest of this blessed day.", "여기서 선장이 플로렌스에게 갈고리로 입맞춤을 보냈다. \"다시 돌아와서 이 복된 날의 나머지 동안 당신을 예인해 가겠소."], ["You’ll come and eat your dinner along with me, Sol, somewheres or another.”", "어디든 함께 가서 저녁을 먹읍시다, 솔.\""]], [["“Not today, Ned!”", "\"오늘은 안 되네, 네드!\""], ["said the old man quickly, and appearing to be unaccountably startled by the proposition.", "노인이 그 제안에 영문 모르게 깜짝 놀란 듯, 재빨리 말했다."], ["“Not today.", "\"오늘은 안 돼."], ["I couldn’t do it!”", "그럴 수가 없어!\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 안 되오?\""], ["returned the Captain, gazing at him in astonishment.", "선장이 놀라 그를 바라보며 응수했다."]], [["“I—I have so much to do.", "\"내가—내가 할 일이 너무 많아서."], ["I—I mean to think of, and arrange.", "내가—생각하고 정리해야 할 게 많아."], ["I couldn’t do it, Ned, indeed.", "그럴 수가 없네, 네드, 정말로."], ["I must go out again, and be alone, and turn my mind to many things today.”", "다시 나가서 혼자 있으면서, 오늘은 여러 가지 일에 마음을 쏟아야 해.\""]], [["The Captain looked at the Instrument-maker, and looked at Florence, and again at the Instrument-maker.", "선장은 기구 제작자를 보고, 플로렌스를 보고, 다시 기구 제작자를 보았다."], ["“To-morrow, then,”", "\"그럼 내일,\""], ["he suggested, at last.", "마침내 그가 제안했다."]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"그래, 그래."], ["To-morrow,”", "내일,\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."], ["“Think of me to-morrow.", "\"내일 나를 생각해 주게."], ["Say to-morrow.”", "내일이라고 해 두세.\""]], [["“I shall come here early, mind, Sol Gills,”", "\"내가 일찍 여기로 오겠소, 명심하시오, 솔 길스,\""], ["stipulated the Captain.", "선장이 다짐을 두었다."]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"그래, 그래."], ["The first thing tomorrow morning,”", "내일 아침 제일 먼저,\" 늙은 솔이 말했다."], ["said old Sol; “and now good-bye, Ned Cuttle, and God bless you!”", "\"자, 그럼 잘 가게, 네드 커틀, 신의 가호가 있기를!\""]], [["Squeezing both the Captain’s hands, with uncommon fervour, as he said it, the old man turned to Florence, folded hers in his own, and put them to his lips; then hurried her out to the coach with very singular precipitation.", "이렇게 말하면서 노인은 보기 드문 열렬함으로 선장의 두 손을 꼭 쥔 다음, 플로렌스에게 돌아서서 그녀의 두 손을 제 손에 감싸 입술에 갖다 댔다. 그러고는 몹시 유별난 다급함으로 그녀를 서둘러 마차로 데리고 나갔다."], ["Altogether, he made such an effect on Captain Cuttle that the Captain lingered behind, and instructed Rob to be particularly gentle and attentive to his master until the morning: which injunction he strengthened with the payment of one shilling down, and the promise of another sixpence before noon next day.", "어쨌거나 그가 커틀 선장에게 어찌나 인상을 남겼던지, 선장은 뒤에 남아, 아침까지 주인에게 각별히 다정하고 세심하게 대하라고 롭에게 일렀다. 그리고 그 당부를, 즉석에서 1실링을 지불하고 이튿날 정오 전에 6펜스를 더 주겠다는 약속으로 단단히 못 박았다."], ["This kind office performed, Captain Cuttle, who considered himself the natural and lawful body-guard of Florence, mounted the box with a mighty sense of his trust, and escorted her home.", "이 친절한 임무를 마치고서, 자기를 플로렌스의 당연하고 정당한 호위병으로 여기는 커틀 선장은 막중한 사명감을 품고 마부석에 올라, 그녀를 집까지 바래다주었다. 헤어질 때 그는 솔 길스를 가까이서, 진실하게 곁을 지키겠노라고 그녀에게 다짐했다."], ["At parting, he assured her that he would stand by Sol Gills, close and true; and once again inquired of Susan Nipper, unable to forget her gallant words in reference to Mrs MacStinger, “Would you, do you think my dear, though?”", "그리고 맥스팅거 부인에 관한 그 용감한 말을 잊지 못해, 한 번 더 수전 니퍼에게 물었다. \"그래도, 정말 그러시겠소, 아가씨?\""]], [["When the desolate house had closed upon the two, the Captain’s thoughts reverted to the old Instrument-maker, and he felt uncomfortable.", "그 황량한 집이 두 사람을 안으로 들이고 문이 닫히자, 선장의 생각은 늙은 기구 제작자에게로 되돌아갔고, 그는 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["Therefore, instead of going home, he walked up and down the street several times, and, eking out his leisure until evening, dined late at a certain angular little tavern in the City, with a public parlour like a wedge, to which glazed hats much resorted.", "그래서 집으로 가는 대신 거리를 몇 차례 오르내렸고, 저녁이 될 때까지 짬을 메우느라, 시내의 어느 모난 자그마한 선술집에서 늦은 저녁을 먹었다. 그곳에는 쐐기처럼 생긴 공용 거실이 있었는데, 광택 모자들이 즐겨 드나드는 곳이었다."], ["The Captain’s principal intention was to pass Sol Gills’s, after dark, and look in through the window: which he did, The parlour door stood open, and he could see his old friend writing busily and steadily at the table within, while the little Midshipman, already sheltered from the night dews, watched him from the counter; under which Rob the Grinder made his own bed, preparatory to shutting the shop.", "선장의 주된 속셈은 날이 어두워진 뒤 솔 길스의 가게 앞을 지나며 창문 안을 들여다보는 것이었고, 실제로 그렇게 했다. 거실 문이 열려 있어, 그는 옛 친구가 안의 탁자에서 바삐, 꾸준히 무언가를 쓰고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Reassured by the tranquillity that reigned within the precincts of the wooden mariner, the Captain headed for Brig Place, resolving to weigh anchor betimes in the morning.", "한편 자그마한 항해사 인형은 이미 밤이슬을 피해 들여놓아진 채, 계산대에서 그를 지켜보고 있었으며, 그 계산대 밑에서는 분쇄기 롭이 가게 문을 닫을 채비로 자기 잠자리를 펴고 있었다. 그 나무 뱃사람의 경내를 다스리는 고요함에 안심한 선장은, 아침 일찍 닻을 올리기로 마음먹고 브리그 플레이스를 향해 뱃머리를 돌렸다."]], [["Sir Barnet and Lady Skettles, very good people, resided in a pretty villa at Fulham, on the banks of the Thames; which was one of the most desirable residences in the world when a rowing-match happened to be going past, but had its little inconveniences at other times, among which may be enumerated the occasional appearance of the river in the drawing-room, and the contemporaneous disappearance of the lawn and shrubbery.", "바넷 스케틀스 경과 스케틀스 부인은 매우 선량한 사람들로, 템스 강변 풀럼에 자리한 어여쁜 별장에 살고 있었다. 그곳은 조정 경기가 마침 지나갈 때면 세상에서 가장 탐낼 만한 거처였으나, 다른 때에는 자잘한 불편함이 없지 않았는데, 그 가운데 강물이 이따금 응접실에 모습을 드러내는 일과, 그와 동시에 잔디밭과 관목 숲이 자취를 감추는 일을 꼽을 수 있었다."]], [["Sir Barnet Skettles expressed his personal consequence chiefly through an antique gold snuffbox, and a ponderous silk pocket-kerchief, which he had an imposing manner of drawing out of his pocket like a banner and using with both hands at once.", "바넷 스케틀스 경은 자신의 인물됨을 주로 고풍스러운 금제 코담배갑과 묵직한 비단 손수건으로 드러냈는데, 그는 그 손수건을 마치 깃발처럼 위풍당당하게 주머니에서 꺼내어 양손으로 동시에 다루는 인상적인 태도를 지녔다."], ["Sir Barnet’s object in life was constantly to extend the range of his acquaintance.", "바넷 경의 인생 목표는 끊임없이 자기 교제의 범위를 넓혀 가는 것이었다."], ["Like a heavy body dropped into water—not to disparage so worthy a gentleman by the comparison—it was in the nature of things that Sir Barnet must spread an ever widening circle about him, until there was no room left.", "물에 떨어진 무거운 물체처럼—이런 비유로 이토록 훌륭한 신사를 깎아내릴 뜻은 없으나—사물의 본성상 바넷 경은 더 이상 자리가 남지 않을 때까지 자기 둘레로 점점 넓어지는 원을 퍼뜨릴 수밖에 없었다."], ["Or, like a sound in air, the vibration of which, according to the speculation of an ingenious modern philosopher, may go on travelling for ever through the interminable fields of space, nothing but coming to the end of his moral tether could stop Sir Barnet Skettles in his voyage of discovery through the social system.", "혹은 공기 중의 소리처럼, 그 진동이 어느 기발한 현대 철학자의 사색에 따르면 무한한 공간의 들판을 영원토록 여행해 갈 수도 있다는데, 바넷 스케틀스 경 또한 자기 도덕적 밧줄의 끝에 다다르는 것 외에는 그 무엇도 사교계라는 체계를 누비는 그의 발견 항해를 멈출 수 없었다."]], [["Sir Barnet was proud of making people acquainted with people.", "바넷 경은 사람과 사람을 알게 해 주는 일에 자부심을 느꼈다."], ["He liked the thing for its own sake, and it advanced his favourite object too.", "그는 그 일 자체를 좋아했고, 그것은 그가 가장 아끼는 목표를 진척시키기도 했다."], ["For example, if Sir Barnet had the good fortune to get hold of a law recruit, or a country gentleman, and ensnared him to his hospitable villa, Sir Barnet would say to him, on the morning after his arrival, “Now, my dear Sir, is there anybody you would like to know?", "이를테면, 바넷 경이 운 좋게 법조계 신참이나 시골 신사를 붙잡아 자기의 환대 넘치는 별장으로 꾀어들인다면, 바넷 경은 그가 도착한 다음 날 아침 이렇게 말할 것이었다. \"자, 친애하는 선생, 알고 싶은 분이 있으신가요?"], ["Who is there you would wish to meet?", "만나 보고 싶은 분이 누구신지요?"], ["Do you take any interest in writing people, or in painting or sculpturing people, or in acting people, or in anything of that sort?”", "혹시 글 쓰는 사람들이나, 그림이나 조각 하는 사람들, 연기하는 사람들, 혹은 그런 부류에 관심이 있으신가요?\""], ["Possibly the patient answered yes, and mentioned somebody, of whom Sir Barnet had no more personal knowledge than of Ptolemy the Great.", "아마도 그 환자는 그렇다고 답하며 누군가의 이름을 댈 것이고, 바넷 경은 그 사람에 대해 프톨레마이오스 대왕만큼이나 개인적으로 아는 바가 없었을 것이다."], ["Sir Barnet replied, that nothing on earth was easier, as he knew him very well: immediately called on the aforesaid somebody, left his card, wrote a short note,—“My dear Sir—penalty of your eminent position—friend at my house naturally desirous—Lady Skettles and myself participate—trust that genius being superior to ceremonies, you will do us the distinguished favour of giving us the pleasure,”", "바넷 경은 자기가 그를 아주 잘 안다며 세상에 그보다 더 쉬운 일은 없다고 답하고는, 즉시 앞서 말한 그 사람을 찾아가 명함을 두고 짧은 쪽지를 적었다.—\"친애하는 선생—선생의 저명하신 지위에 따르는 부담이겠지요만—제 집에 머무는 벗이 당연히 간절히 바라는 바이며—스케틀스 부인과 저 또한 함께 청하옵나니—천재란 격식을 초월하는 법이오니, 부디 저희에게 그 기쁨을 베푸시는 빛나는 호의를 주시기 바랍니다,\""], ["etc, etc.—and so killed a brace of birds with one stone, dead as door-nails.", "운운—그리하여 돌 하나로 새 두 마리를, 그것도 못대가리만큼 확실하게 잡아 버렸다."]], [["With the snuff-box and banner in full force, Sir Barnet Skettles propounded his usual inquiry to Florence on the first morning of her visit.", "코담배갑과 깃발을 한껏 동원한 채, 바넷 스케틀스 경은 플로렌스가 방문한 첫날 아침에 그녀에게 자기의 평소 그 질문을 던졌다."], ["When Florence thanked him, and said there was no one in particular whom she desired to see, it was natural she should think with a pang, of poor lost Walter.", "플로렌스가 그에게 감사하며 특별히 보고 싶은 사람은 없다고 말했을 때, 그녀가 가엾게 잃어버린 월터를 가슴 저미는 아픔과 함께 떠올린 것은 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["When Sir Barnet Skettles, urging his kind offer, said, “My dear Miss Dombey, are you sure you can remember no one whom your good Papa—to whom I beg you present the best compliments of myself and Lady Skettles when you write—might wish you to know?”", "바넷 스케틀스 경이 친절한 제안을 거듭하며 \"친애하는 돔비 아가씨, 당신의 훌륭하신 아버님께서—편지를 쓰실 때 부디 저와 스케틀스 부인의 안부를 정중히 전해 주십시오—당신이 알기를 바라실 만한 분이 정말로 한 명도 떠오르지 않으신가요?\""], ["it was natural, perhaps, that her poor head should droop a little, and that her voice should tremble as it softly answered in the negative.", "하고 말했을 때, 그녀의 가엾은 고개가 조금 수그러지고, 부드럽게 아니라고 답하는 목소리가 떨린 것도 아마 자연스러운 일이었으리라."]], [["Skettles Junior, much stiffened as to his cravat, and sobered down as to his spirits, was at home for the holidays, and appeared to feel himself aggrieved by the solicitude of his excellent mother that he should be attentive to Florence.", "스케틀스 주니어는 크라바트를 잔뜩 빳빳하게 매고 기운은 한결 가라앉은 채 방학을 맞아 집에 와 있었는데, 플로렌스에게 자상하게 굴라는 훌륭한 어머니의 간곡한 당부에 자기가 부당한 대접을 받는 양 느끼는 모양이었다."], ["Another and a deeper injury under which the soul of young Barnet chafed, was the company of Dr and Mrs Blimber, who had been invited on a visit to the paternal roof-tree, and of whom the young gentleman often said he would have preferred their passing the vacation at Jericho.", "어린 바넷의 영혼이 부대끼는 또 다른, 그리고 더 깊은 고통은 블림버 박사 부부의 동행이었으니, 이들은 친가의 지붕 아래 머물도록 초대받은 터였고, 그 젊은 신사는 차라리 그들이 예리코에서 방학을 보냈으면 좋았을 거라고 자주 말하곤 했다."]], [["“Is there anybody you can suggest now, Doctor Blimber?”", "\"이제 추천하실 만한 분이 있으신가요, 블림버 박사?\""], ["said Sir Barnet Skettles, turning to that gentleman.", "바넷 스케틀스 경이 그 신사를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“You are very kind, Sir Barnet,”", "\"매우 친절하시군요, 바넷 경,\""], ["returned Doctor Blimber.", "블림버 박사가 응수했다."], ["“Really I am not aware that there is, in particular.", "\"사실 딱히 떠오르는 사람은 없습니다."], ["I like to know my fellow-men in general, Sir Barnet.", "저는 동료 인간들을 두루 알고 지내는 것을 좋아하지요, 바넷 경."], ["What does Terence say?", "테렌티우스가 뭐라 했던가요?"], ["Anyone who is the parent of a son is interesting to me.”", "아들을 둔 부모라면 누구든 제게는 흥미로운 사람입니다.\""]], [["“Has Mrs Blimber any wish to see any remarkable person?”", "\"블림버 부인께서는 어떤 주목할 만한 인물을 만나 보고 싶으신지요?\""], ["asked Sir Barnet, courteously.", "바넷 경이 정중하게 물었다."]], [["Mrs Blimber replied, with a sweet smile and a shake of her sky-blue cap, that if Sir Barnet could have made her known to Cicero, she would have troubled him; but such an introduction not being feasible, and she already enjoying the friendship of himself and his amiable lady, and possessing with the Doctor her husband their joint confidence in regard to their dear son—here young Barnet was observed to curl his nose—she asked no more.", "블림버 부인은 달콤한 미소를 지으며 하늘색 모자를 흔들고는, 바넷 경이 자기를 키케로와 알게 해 주실 수 있었다면 폐를 끼쳤을 테지만, 그런 소개는 가능치 않은 일이고, 자기는 이미 경과 그의 상냥한 부인의 우정을 누리고 있으며, 남편인 박사와 함께 사랑하는 아들에 대한 두 사람의 공동의 신뢰를 지니고 있으니—이 대목에서 어린 바넷이 코를 찡그리는 것이 보였다—더 바랄 것이 없다고 답했다."]], [["Sir Barnet was fain, under these circumstances, to content himself for the time with the company assembled.", "이런 사정이고 보니 바넷 경은 당분간 모인 사람들로 만족할 수밖에 없었다. 플로렌스는 그것이 기뻤다."], ["Florence was glad of that; for she had a study to pursue among them, and it lay too near her heart, and was too precious and momentous, to yield to any other interest.", "그녀에게는 그들 가운데서 추구해야 할 연구가 하나 있었는데, 그것은 그녀의 마음에 너무도 가까이 놓여 있었고 너무도 소중하고 중대하여, 다른 어떤 흥미에도 자리를 내줄 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["There were some children staying in the house.", "집에는 머물고 있는 아이들이 몇 있었다."], ["Children who were as frank and happy with fathers and with mothers as those rosy faces opposite home.", "고향 맞은편의 그 발그레한 얼굴들처럼 아버지와 어머니에게 솔직하고 행복한 아이들이었다."], ["Children who had no restraint upon their love, and freely showed it.", "자기들의 사랑에 아무런 거리낌이 없어 그것을 자유로이 드러내는 아이들이었다."], ["Florence sought to learn their secret; sought to find out what it was she had missed; what simple art they knew, and she knew not; how she could be taught by them to show her father that she loved him, and to win his love again.", "플로렌스는 그 아이들의 비밀을 배우고자 했다. 자기가 놓쳐 버린 것이 무엇인지, 그 아이들은 알고 자기는 모르는 그 단순한 기술이 무엇인지, 어떻게 하면 그 아이들에게서 배워 아버지에게 그를 사랑한다는 것을 보여 주고 다시 그의 사랑을 얻을 수 있을지 알아내고자 했다."]], [["Many a day did Florence thoughtfully observe these children.", "플로렌스는 여러 날 동안 그 아이들을 골똘히 관찰했다."], ["On many a bright morning did she leave her bed when the glorious sun rose, and walking up and down upon the river’s bank, before anyone in the house was stirring, look up at the windows of their rooms, and think of them, asleep, so gently tended and affectionately thought of.", "여러 번의 환한 아침에, 그녀는 찬란한 해가 떠오를 때 잠자리에서 일어나, 집안의 누구도 깨어나기 전에 강둑을 오르내리며 거닐고는, 그 아이들 방의 창문을 올려다보며 그토록 다정하게 보살핌받고 애틋하게 생각되며 잠들어 있는 그들을 떠올렸다."], ["Florence would feel more lonely then, than in the great house all alone; and would think sometimes that she was better there than here, and that there was greater peace in hiding herself than in mingling with others of her age, and finding how unlike them all she was.", "그럴 때 플로렌스는 큰 집에 홀로 있을 때보다 더 외로움을 느꼈고, 때로는 여기보다 거기가 더 낫다고, 또래의 다른 아이들과 어울려 자기가 그들 모두와 얼마나 다른지 깨닫는 것보다 자신을 숨기는 편이 더 큰 평화가 있다고 생각하기도 했다."], ["But attentive to her study, though it touched her to the quick at every little leaf she turned in the hard book, Florence remained among them, and tried, with patient hope, to gain the knowledge that she wearied for.", "그러나 자기 연구에 마음을 쏟으며, 그 힘겨운 책의 잎새를 한 장 넘길 때마다 가슴 깊이 사무쳤음에도, 플로렌스는 그들 가운데 머물며 인내하는 희망으로 그토록 갈망하던 그 앎을 얻으려 애썼다."]], [["Ah!", "아!"], ["how to gain it! how to know the charm in its beginning!", "어떻게 그것을 얻을 것인가!"], ["There were daughters here, who rose up in the morning, and lay down to rest at night, possessed of fathers’ hearts already.", "그 비밀스러운 매력을 그 시작에서부터 어떻게 알 것인가! 여기에는 아침이면 일어나고 밤이면 쉬러 누우면서 이미 아버지의 마음을 차지하고 있는 딸들이 있었다."], ["They had no repulse to overcome, no coldness to dread, no frown to smooth away.", "그들에게는 극복해야 할 거절도, 두려워할 냉랭함도, 펴 줘야 할 찌푸린 미간도 없었다."], ["As the morning advanced, and the windows opened one by one, and the dew began to dry upon the flowers and youthful feet began to move upon the lawn, Florence, glancing round at the bright faces, thought what was there she could learn from these children?", "아침이 깊어 가고 창문들이 하나씩 열리며 꽃잎 위의 이슬이 마르기 시작하고 어린 발들이 잔디밭을 거닐기 시작할 때, 플로렌스는 그 환한 얼굴들을 둘러보며 이 아이들에게서 배울 수 있는 것이 무엇일까 생각했다."], ["It was too late to learn from them; each could approach her father fearlessly, and put up her lips to meet the ready kiss, and wind her arm about the neck that bent down to caress her.", "그들에게서 배우기에는 너무 늦었다. 저마다 두려움 없이 아버지에게 다가가, 언제든 준비된 입맞춤을 맞으러 입술을 내밀고, 자기를 어루만지려 굽혀 오는 목에 팔을 감을 수 있었다."], ["She could not begin by being so bold.", "그녀는 그토록 대담하게 시작할 수가 없었다."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["could it be that there was less and less hope as she studied more and more!", "연구를 거듭할수록 희망이 점점 더 줄어들기만 하는 것은 아닐까!"]], [["She remembered well, that even the old woman who had robbed her when a little child—whose image and whose house, and all she had said and done, were stamped upon her recollection, with the enduring sharpness of a fearful impression made at that early period of life—had spoken fondly of her daughter, and how terribly even she had cried out in the pain of hopeless separation from her child.", "그녀는 잘 기억하고 있었다. 어린아이였을 적 자기 것을 빼앗아 간 그 노파조차—그 노파의 모습과 그 집과 그녀가 한 말과 행동 모두가 인생의 그 이른 시기에 새겨진 무서운 인상의 지워지지 않는 날카로움으로 기억 속에 찍혀 있었는데—자기 딸을 다정하게 이야기했고, 그 노파마저도 제 아이와의 가망 없는 이별의 고통 속에서 얼마나 끔찍하게 부르짖었던가를."], ["But her own mother, she would think again, when she recalled this, had loved her well.", "그러나 자기 어머니는, 이 일을 떠올릴 때면 그녀는 다시 생각했다, 자기를 진심으로 사랑했었다고."], ["Then, sometimes, when her thoughts reverted swiftly to the void between herself and her father, Florence would tremble, and the tears would start upon her face, as she pictured to herself her mother living on, and coming also to dislike her, because of her wanting the unknown grace that should conciliate that father naturally, and had never done so from her cradle.", "그러다가 때때로, 그녀의 생각이 자기와 아버지 사이의 그 공허로 휙 되돌아갈 때면, 플로렌스는 몸을 떨었고, 어머니가 살아남아 자기를 역시 싫어하게 되는 모습을—그 아버지를 자연스럽게 누그러뜨려야 했으나 요람에서부터 한 번도 그러지 못한, 그 알 수 없는 우아함이 자기에게 결여되어 있기에—마음속에 그려 보며 얼굴에 눈물이 솟구쳤다."], ["She knew that this imagination did wrong to her mother’s memory, and had no truth in it, or base to rest upon; and yet she tried so hard to justify him, and to find the whole blame in herself, that she could not resist its passing, like a wild cloud, through the distance of her mind.", "그녀는 이런 상상이 어머니의 추억에 죄를 짓는 일이며, 거기에는 아무런 진실도 없고 기댈 근거도 없다는 것을 알았다. 그럼에도 그녀는 아버지를 정당화하고 모든 잘못을 자기 안에서 찾으려 너무도 안간힘을 쓴 나머지, 그 상상이 거친 구름처럼 자기 마음의 먼 자락을 가로질러 지나가는 것을 막을 수가 없었다."]], [["There came among the other visitors, soon after Florence, one beautiful girl, three or four years younger than she, who was an orphan child, and who was accompanied by her aunt, a grey-haired lady, who spoke much to Florence, and who greatly liked (but that they all did) to hear her sing of an evening, and would always sit near her at that time, with motherly interest.", "플로렌스가 온 지 얼마 안 되어 다른 방문객들 가운데 한 아름다운 소녀가 끼어 왔는데, 그녀보다 서너 살 어린 고아 아이로, 백발의 부인인 고모와 함께였다. 그 부인은 플로렌스에게 많은 이야기를 건넸고, 저녁마다 그녀가 노래하는 것을 듣기를 무척 좋아하여(하기야 다들 그랬다), 그럴 때면 늘 어머니 같은 관심으로 그녀 가까이에 앉곤 했다."], ["They had only been two days in the house, when Florence, being in an arbour in the garden one warm morning, musingly observant of a youthful group upon the turf, through some intervening boughs,—and wreathing flowers for the head of one little creature among them who was the pet and plaything of the rest, heard this same lady and her niece, in pacing up and down a sheltered nook close by, speak of herself.", "집에 머문 지 겨우 이틀 되던 어느 따뜻한 아침, 플로렌스가 정원의 정자에 앉아, 사이를 가로막은 나뭇가지들 너머로 잔디 위의 어린 무리를 골똘히 바라보며—그 무리 가운데서 모두의 귀염둥이이자 놀잇감인 자그마한 아이의 머리에 씌울 꽃 화환을 엮고 있을 때, 바로 그 부인과 조카가 가까운 한적한 구석을 거닐며 그녀에 대해 이야기하는 것을 들었다."]], [["“Is Florence an orphan like me, aunt?”", "\"플로렌스도 저처럼 고아인가요, 고모?\""], ["said the child.", "아이가 말했다."]], [["“No, my love.", "\"아니란다, 얘야."], ["She has no mother, but her father is living.”", "어머니는 안 계시지만 아버지는 살아 계셔.\""]], [["“Is she in mourning for her poor Mama, now?”", "\"그럼 지금 가엾은 어머니를 위해 상복을 입은 건가요?\""], ["inquired the child quickly.", "아이가 재빨리 물었다."]], [["“No; for her only brother.”", "\"아니, 하나밖에 없는 오빠를 위해서란다.\""]], [["“Has she no other brother?”", "\"다른 형제는 없나요?\""]], [["“None.”", "\"없어.\""]], [["“No sister?” “None,”", "\"언니나 여동생은요?\""]], [["“I am very, very sorry!”", "\"없단다.\" \"정말, 정말 안됐어요!\""], ["said the little girl", "어린 소녀가 말했다."]], [["As they stopped soon afterwards to watch some boats, and had been silent in the meantime, Florence, who had risen when she heard her name, and had gathered up her flowers to go and meet them, that they might know of her being within hearing, resumed her seat and work, expecting to hear no more; but the conversation recommenced next moment.", "이내 그들이 배 몇 척을 바라보려 멈춰 서서 그 사이 말이 없어졌을 때, 자기 이름을 듣고 자리에서 일어나 꽃을 챙겨 들고 그들을 마중하러 가서 자기가 그들의 말소리가 들리는 곳에 있음을 알리려던 플로렌스는, 더는 들을 것이 없으리라 여기고 다시 자리에 앉아 일감을 들었다. 그러나 다음 순간 대화가 다시 시작되었다."]], [["“Florence is a favourite with everyone here, and deserves to be, I am sure,”", "\"플로렌스는 여기 모두에게 사랑받아요, 그럴 만하기도 하고요, 정말이에요,\""], ["said the child, earnestly.", "아이가 진지하게 말했다."], ["“Where is her Papa?”", "\"그녀의 아버지는 어디 계세요?\""]], [["The aunt replied, after a moment’s pause, that she did not know.", "고모는 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 모르겠다고 답했다."], ["Her tone of voice arrested Florence, who had started from her seat again; and held her fastened to the spot, with her work hastily caught up to her bosom, and her two hands saving it from being scattered on the ground.", "그 목소리의 어조가, 다시 자리에서 벌떡 일어났던 플로렌스를 붙들어, 일감을 황급히 가슴께로 끌어안고 그것이 땅에 흩어지지 않도록 두 손으로 받친 채 그 자리에 못 박혀 있게 했다."]], [["“He is in England, I hope, aunt?”", "\"잉글랜드에 계시겠죠, 고모?\""], ["said the child.", "아이가 말했다."]], [["“I believe so.", "\"그럴 게다."], ["Yes; I know he is, indeed.”", "그래, 정말로 계신단다.\""]], [["“Has he ever been here?”", "\"여기 와 보신 적은 있어요?\""]], [["“I believe not.", "\"없는 것 같구나."], ["No.”", "없어.\""]], [["“Is he coming here to see her?”", "\"플로렌스를 보러 여기 오실 건가요?\""]], [["“I believe not.”", "\"안 오실 것 같구나.\""]], [["“Is he lame, or blind, or ill, aunt?”", "\"다리가 불편하시거나, 눈이 안 보이시거나, 편찮으신가요, 고모?\""], ["asked the child.", "아이가 물었다."]], [["The flowers that Florence held to her breast began to fall when she heard those words, so wonderingly spoke.", "그토록 의아한 듯 던져진 그 말을 들었을 때, 플로렌스가 가슴에 품고 있던 꽃들이 떨어지기 시작했다."], ["She held them closer; and her face hung down upon them.", "그녀는 꽃을 더 바짝 끌어안았고, 얼굴이 그 위로 수그러졌다."]], [["“Kate,”", "\"케이트,\""], ["said the lady, after another moment of silence, “I will tell you the whole truth about Florence as I have heard it, and believe it to be.", "다시 한순간의 침묵 뒤에 부인이 말했다, \"내가 들은 대로, 그리고 사실이라 믿는 대로, 플로렌스에 대한 모든 진실을 말해 주마."], ["Tell no one else, my dear, because it may be little known here, and your doing so would give her pain.”", "다른 누구에게도 말하면 안 돼, 얘야, 여기서는 이 일이 거의 알려지지 않았을지 모르고, 네가 말을 옮기면 그녀에게 아픔을 줄 테니까.\""]], [["“I never will!”", "\"절대로 안 할게요!\""], ["exclaimed the child.", "아이가 외쳤다."]], [["“I know you never will,”", "\"네가 절대 그러지 않으리란 걸 안다,\""], ["returned the lady.", "부인이 응수했다."], ["“I can trust you as myself.", "\"나는 너를 나 자신처럼 믿을 수 있어."], ["I fear then, Kate, that Florence’s father cares little for her, very seldom sees her, never was kind to her in her life, and now quite shuns her and avoids her.", "그렇다면 들어 봐, 케이트, 안타깝게도 플로렌스의 아버지는 그 애를 거의 마음에 두지 않고, 아주 드물게만 보며, 평생 한 번도 다정하게 대한 적이 없고, 지금은 그 애를 완전히 피하고 멀리한단다."], ["She would love him dearly if he would suffer her, but he will not—though for no fault of hers; and she is greatly to be loved and pitied by all gentle hearts.”", "그 애는 아버지가 허락만 한다면 그를 진심으로 사랑할 텐데, 아버지는 그러질 않아—그것도 그 애 잘못이라곤 없는데 말이지. 그러니 그 애는 모든 따뜻한 마음을 가진 사람들에게서 큰 사랑과 연민을 받아 마땅하단다.\""]], [["More of the flowers that Florence held fell scattering on the ground; those that remained were wet, but not with dew; and her face dropped upon her laden hands.", "플로렌스가 쥐고 있던 꽃이 더 많이 땅에 흩어져 떨어졌다. 남은 꽃들은 젖어 있었으나, 이슬 때문은 아니었다. 그리고 그녀의 얼굴이 그 가득 안긴 두 손 위로 떨어졌다."]], [["“Poor Florence!", "\"가엾은 플로렌스!"], ["Dear, good Florence!”", "사랑스럽고 착한 플로렌스!\""], ["cried the child.", "아이가 외쳤다."]], [["“Do you know why I have told you this, Kate?”", "\"내가 왜 이 이야기를 너에게 했는지 아니, 케이트?\""], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."]], [["“That I may be very kind to her, and take great care to try to please her.", "\"제가 그 애에게 아주 다정하게 대하고, 그 애를 기쁘게 해 주려고 무척 신경 쓰게 하려고요."], ["Is that the reason, aunt?”", "그게 이유인가요, 고모?\""]], [["“Partly,”", "\"일부는 그렇단다,\""], ["said the lady, “but not all.", "부인이 말했다, \"하지만 전부는 아니야."], ["Though we see her so cheerful; with a pleasant smile for everyone; ready to oblige us all, and bearing her part in every amusement here: she can hardly be quite happy, do you think she can, Kate?”", "우리가 보기엔 그 애가 그토록 명랑하고, 누구에게나 상냥한 미소를 보내고, 우리 모두에게 기꺼이 맞춰 주고, 여기서 모든 놀이에 한몫을 거들지만 말이다. 그래도 그 애가 온전히 행복할 수는 없겠지, 그럴 수 있을 것 같니, 케이트?\""]], [["“I am afraid not,”", "\"아닐 것 같아요,\""], ["said the little girl.", "어린 소녀가 말했다."]], [["“And you can understand,”", "\"그러니 이해할 수 있겠지,\""], ["pursued the lady, “why her observation of children who have parents who are fond of them, and proud of them—like many here, just now—should make her sorrowful in secret?”", "부인이 이어 말했다, \"자기를 아끼고 자랑스러워하는 부모를 둔 아이들을—지금 여기 많은 아이들처럼—그 애가 지켜보면, 왜 남몰래 슬퍼지는지 말이다.\""]], [["“Yes, dear aunt,”", "\"네, 고모,\""], ["said the child, “I understand that very well.", "아이가 말했다, \"그건 아주 잘 이해해요."], ["Poor Florence!”", "가엾은 플로렌스!\""]], [["More flowers strayed upon the ground, and those she yet held to her breast trembled as if a wintry wind were rustling them.", "더 많은 꽃이 땅에 흩어졌고, 그녀가 아직 가슴에 품고 있던 꽃들은 마치 겨울바람이 그것들을 흔드는 양 떨렸다."]], [["“My Kate,”", "\"내 케이트,\""], ["said the lady, whose voice was serious, but very calm and sweet, and had so impressed Florence from the first moment of her hearing it, “of all the youthful people here, you are her natural and harmless friend; you have not the innocent means, that happier children have—”", "진지하지만 매우 차분하고 다정하여, 플로렌스가 처음 들은 순간부터 깊은 인상을 받았던 그 목소리로 부인이 말했다, \"여기 있는 모든 젊은이 가운데서 너야말로 그 애에게 자연스럽고 해롭지 않은 친구란다. 너에게는 더 행복한 아이들이 가진 그 천진한 수단이 없어—\""]], [["“There are none happier, aunt!”", "\"고모보다 더 행복한 사람은 없어요!\""], ["exclaimed the child, who seemed to cling about her.", "아이가 부인에게 매달리는 듯 외쳤다."]], [["“—As other children have, dear Kate, of reminding her of her misfortune.", "\"—다른 아이들이 가진, 그 애에게 자기 불행을 떠올리게 하는 그런 수단 말이다, 사랑하는 케이트."], ["Therefore I would have you, when you try to be her little friend, try all the more for that, and feel that the bereavement you sustained—thank Heaven! before you knew its weight—gives you claim and hold upon poor Florence.”", "그래서 나는 네가 그 애의 작은 친구가 되려 할 때, 바로 그 점 때문에 더욱더 애써 주기를, 그리고 네가 겪은 사별이—그 무게를 알기 전에, 다행히도!—가엾은 플로렌스에 대한 너의 권리와 끈이 되어 준다는 것을 느껴 주기를 바란단다.\""]], [["“But I am not without a parent’s love, aunt, and I never have been,”", "\"하지만 저는 부모의 사랑이 없는 게 아니고, 한 번도 없었던 적이 없어요, 고모,\""], ["said the child, “with you.”", "아이가 말했다, \"고모가 계시니까요.\""]], [["“However that may be, my dear,”", "\"그렇다 하더라도, 얘야,\""], ["returned the lady, “your misfortune is a lighter one than Florence’s; for not an orphan in the wide world can be so deserted as the child who is an outcast from a living parent’s love.”", "부인이 응수했다, \"너의 불행은 플로렌스의 것보다 가볍단다. 살아 있는 부모의 사랑에서 내쳐진 아이만큼 넓은 세상에서 버림받을 수 있는 고아는 없으니까.\""]], [["The flowers were scattered on the ground like dust; the empty hands were spread upon the face; and orphaned Florence, shrinking down upon the ground, wept long and bitterly.", "꽃들은 먼지처럼 땅에 흩어졌다. 비어 버린 두 손은 얼굴을 가렸다. 그리고 고아가 된 플로렌스는 땅바닥에 웅크리며 오래도록 비통하게 울었다."]], [["But true of heart and resolute in her good purpose, Florence held to it as her dying mother held by her upon the day that gave Paul life.", "그러나 마음이 진실되고 자기의 선한 목적에 결연했던 플로렌스는, 폴에게 생명을 준 그날 죽어 가던 어머니가 자기를 붙들었듯이, 그 목적을 굳게 붙들었다."], ["He did not know how much she loved him.", "아버지는 그녀가 자기를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알지 못했다."], ["However long the time in coming, and however slow the interval, she must try to bring that knowledge to her father’s heart one day or other.", "그 깨달음이 찾아오기까지 아무리 오랜 시간이 걸리고 그 사이가 아무리 더디다 해도, 그녀는 언젠가는 그 앎을 아버지의 마음에 가닿게 하려 애써야 했다."], ["Meantime she must be careful in no thoughtless word, or look, or burst of feeling awakened by any chance circumstance, to complain against him, or to give occasion for these whispers to his prejudice.", "그동안 그녀는 어떤 우연한 상황에 깨어난 생각 없는 말 한마디나 표정이나 감정의 분출로도 아버지를 원망하거나, 그를 향한 이런 속삭임에 빌미를 주지 않도록 조심해야 했다."]], [["Even in the response she made the orphan child, to whom she was attracted strongly, and whom she had such occasion to remember, Florence was mindful of him.", "자기가 강하게 끌렸고 떠올릴 까닭이 그토록 많았던 그 고아 아이에게 응답할 때조차, 플로렌스는 아버지를 마음에 두었다."], ["If she singled her out too plainly (Florence thought) from among the rest, she would confirm—in one mind certainly: perhaps in more—the belief that he was cruel and unnatural.", "만일 자기가 그 아이를 다른 아이들 가운데서 너무 두드러지게 골라낸다면(플로렌스는 생각했다), 아버지가 잔인하고 인정머리 없는 사람이라는 믿음을—적어도 한 사람의 마음속에서, 어쩌면 더 여럿의 마음속에서—굳혀 버릴 것이었다."], ["Her own delight was no set-off to this.", "자기 자신의 기쁨은 이에 대한 보상이 되지 못했다."], ["What she had overheard was a reason, not for soothing herself, but for saving him; and Florence did it, in pursuance of the study of her heart.", "그녀가 엿들은 것은 자기를 위로할 이유가 아니라 아버지를 구할 이유였다. 그래서 플로렌스는, 자기 마음의 연구를 좇아 그렇게 했다."]], [["She did so always.", "그녀는 늘 그렇게 했다."], ["If a book were read aloud, and there were anything in the story that pointed at an unkind father, she was in pain for their application of it to him; not for herself.", "책을 소리 내어 읽을 때 이야기 속에 인정 없는 아버지를 가리키는 대목이 있으면, 그녀는 그들이 그것을 아버지에게 빗댈까 봐 마음 아파했다. 자기 자신을 위해서가 아니었다."], ["So with any trifle of an interlude that was acted, or picture that was shown, or game that was played, among them.", "그들 가운데서 공연되는 막간극이나, 보여 주는 그림이나, 벌이는 놀이의 사소한 것에도 마찬가지였다."], ["The occasions for such tenderness towards him were so many, that her mind misgave her often, it would indeed be better to go back to the old house, and live again within the shadow of its dull walls, undisturbed.", "아버지를 향해 그런 애틋함을 느낄 일이 너무도 많아서, 그녀의 마음은 차라리 옛집으로 돌아가 그 칙칙한 벽의 그늘 아래서 방해받지 않고 다시 사는 편이 정말 더 나으리라고 자주 흔들렸다."], ["How few who saw sweet Florence, in her spring of womanhood, the modest little queen of those small revels, imagined what a load of sacred care lay heavy in her breast!", "여인으로 피어나는 봄날을 맞은, 그 자그마한 잔치들의 다소곳한 어린 여왕인 사랑스러운 플로렌스를 본 사람들 가운데, 그 가슴속에 신성한 근심이 얼마나 무겁게 얹혀 있는지 짐작한 이가 얼마나 적었던가!"], ["How few of those who stiffened in her father’s freezing atmosphere, suspected what a heap of fiery coals was piled upon his head!", "아버지의 얼어붙은 분위기 속에서 뻣뻣해지던 사람들 가운데, 그의 머리 위에 이글거리는 숯불이 얼마나 수북이 쌓여 있는지 의심한 이가 얼마나 적었던가!"]], [["Florence pursued her study patiently, and, failing to acquire the secret of the nameless grace she sought, among the youthful company who were assembled in the house, often walked out alone, in the early morning, among the children of the poor.", "플로렌스는 인내하며 자기 연구를 계속했고, 집에 모인 젊은 무리 가운데서 자기가 찾던 그 이름 없는 우아함의 비밀을 끝내 터득하지 못하자, 이른 아침 종종 홀로 나가 가난한 이들의 아이들 사이를 거닐었다."], ["But still she found them all too far advanced to learn from.", "그러나 그 아이들 역시 모두 자기가 배우기에는 너무 앞서 나간 것을 알 뿐이었다."], ["They had won their household places long ago, and did not stand without, as she did, with a bar across the door.", "그들은 오래전에 자기 가정의 자리를 얻었고, 그녀처럼 문 앞에 빗장이 가로질린 채 바깥에 서 있지 않았다."]], [["There was one man whom she several times observed at work very early, and often with a girl of about her own age seated near him.", "그녀가 여러 번 매우 이른 시각에 일하는 것을 본 한 남자가 있었는데, 그의 곁에는 종종 자기 또래쯤 되는 소녀가 앉아 있었다."], ["He was a very poor man, who seemed to have no regular employment, but now went roaming about the banks of the river when the tide was low, looking out for bits and scraps in the mud; and now worked at the unpromising little patch of garden-ground before his cottage; and now tinkered up a miserable old boat that belonged to him; or did some job of that kind for a neighbour, as chance occurred.", "그는 매우 가난한 사람으로 일정한 일자리가 없어 보였는데, 어떤 때는 썰물 때 강둑을 돌아다니며 진흙 속에서 자투리며 부스러기를 찾고, 또 어떤 때는 자기 오두막 앞의 볼품없는 자그마한 텃밭에서 일하고, 또 어떤 때는 자기 소유의 형편없는 낡은 배를 손보거나, 기회가 닿으면 이웃을 위해 그런 종류의 일거리를 해 주었다."], ["Whatever the man’s labour, the girl was never employed; but sat, when she was with him, in a listless, moping state, and idle.", "그 남자의 일이 무엇이든 소녀는 한 번도 무언가를 하는 법이 없었고, 그와 함께 있을 때면 멍하니 풀죽은 상태로 빈둥대며 앉아 있었다."]], [["Florence had often wished to speak to this man; yet she had never taken courage to do so, as he made no movement towards her.", "플로렌스는 이 남자에게 말을 걸고 싶었던 적이 여러 번이었으나, 그가 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 기색이 없었기에 한 번도 그럴 용기를 내지 못했다."], ["But one morning when she happened to come upon him suddenly, from a by-path among some pollard willows which terminated in the little shelving piece of stony ground that lay between his dwelling and the water, where he was bending over a fire he had made to caulk the old boat which was lying bottom upwards, close by, he raised his head at the sound of her footstep, and gave her Good morning.", "그러나 어느 날 아침, 가지치기한 버드나무들 사이로 난 샛길에서—그 길은 그의 거처와 물 사이에 놓인, 자갈 깔린 비탈진 작은 땅뙈기에서 끝났다—갑자기 그와 마주쳤을 때, 마침 그는 바로 옆에 뒤집힌 채 놓인 낡은 배의 틈을 메우려고 피운 불 위로 몸을 굽히고 있었는데, 그녀의 발소리에 고개를 들고 안녕하시냐고 인사를 건넸다."]], [["“Good morning,”", "\"안녕하세요,\""], ["said Florence, approaching nearer, “you are at work early.”", "플로렌스가 더 가까이 다가서며 말했다, \"일찍 일하고 계시네요.\""]], [["“I’d be glad to be often at work earlier, Miss, if I had work to do.”", "\"할 일만 있다면야 더 자주 일찍 일하고 싶지요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Is it so hard to get?”", "\"일을 구하기가 그렇게 힘든가요?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“I find it so,”", "\"제게는 그렇습니다,\""], ["replied the man.", "남자가 답했다."]], [["Florence glanced to where the girl was sitting, drawn together, with her elbows on her knees, and her chin on her hands, and said:", "플로렌스는 소녀가 무릎에 팔꿈치를 얹고 두 손에 턱을 괸 채 몸을 잔뜩 웅크리고 앉아 있는 쪽을 흘끗 보고는 말했다."]], [["“Is that your daughter?”", "\"저 아이가 따님인가요?\""]], [["He raised his head quickly, and looking towards the girl with a brightened face, nodded to her, and said “Yes,”", "그는 재빨리 고개를 들어, 환해진 얼굴로 소녀 쪽을 바라보며 고개를 끄덕이고 \"그렇습니다\"라고 말했다."], ["Florence looked towards her too, and gave her a kind salutation; the girl muttered something in return, ungraciously and sullenly.", "플로렌스도 소녀 쪽을 바라보며 다정하게 인사를 건넸으나, 소녀는 무뚝뚝하고 시무룩하게 뭔가를 중얼거릴 뿐이었다."]], [["“Is she in want of employment also?”", "\"저 아이도 일자리가 필요한가요?\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["The man shook his head.", "남자가 고개를 저었다."], ["“No, Miss,”", "\"아닙니다, 아가씨,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I work for both,”", "\"둘 몫을 제가 벌지요.\""]], [["“Are there only you two, then?”", "\"그럼 두 분뿐인가요?\""], ["inquired Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“Only us two,”", "\"저희 둘뿐입니다,\""], ["said the man.", "남자가 말했다."], ["“Her mother has been dead these ten year.", "\"저 애 어미는 죽은 지 십 년이 됐지요."], ["Martha!”", "마사야!\""], ["(he lifted up his head again, and whistled to her) “won’t you say a word to the pretty young lady?”", "(그는 다시 고개를 들고 소녀에게 휘파람을 불었다) \"이 예쁜 아가씨께 한마디 안 하련?\""]], [["The girl made an impatient gesture with her cowering shoulders, and turned her head another way.", "소녀는 웅크린 어깨로 짜증스러운 몸짓을 하며 고개를 딴 데로 돌렸다."], ["Ugly, misshapen, peevish, ill-conditioned, ragged, dirty—but beloved! Oh yes!", "못생기고, 몸이 비뚤어졌고, 까탈스럽고, 성미가 고약하고, 누더기를 걸쳤고, 더러웠다—그러나 사랑받고 있었다!"], ["Florence had seen her father’s look towards her, and she knew whose look it had no likeness to.", "오, 그렇고말고! 플로렌스는 소녀를 향한 그 아버지의 눈길을 보았고, 그 눈길이 누구의 눈길과는 닮은 데가 없는지를 알았다."]], [["“I’m afraid she’s worse this morning, my poor girl!”", "\"안됐지만 우리 가엾은 애가 오늘 아침에 더 안 좋은가 봅니다!\""], ["said the man, suspending his work, and contemplating his ill-favoured child, with a compassion that was the more tender for being rougher.", "남자가 일손을 멈추고, 거친 만큼 더욱 애틋한 연민으로 못난 자식을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“She is ill, then!”", "\"그럼 아프단 말씀이군요!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["The man drew a deep sigh.", "남자는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["“I don’t believe my Martha’s had five short days’ good health,”", "\"우리 마사가 닷새 동안 성한 몸으로 지낸 적이 있는지 모르겠습니다,\""], ["he answered, looking at her still, “in as many long years.”", "그가 여전히 소녀를 바라보며 답했다, \"그 긴 세월 햇수만큼이나 되는 동안에요.\""]], [["“Ay! and more than that, John,”", "\"그렇소!"], ["said a neighbour, who had come down to help him with the boat.", "그것도 더 됐지, 존,\" 배를 거들어 주려고 내려온 이웃이 말했다."]], [["“More than that, you say, do you?”", "\"그것보다 더 됐다고, 그렇게 말하는가?\""], ["cried the other, pushing back his battered hat, and drawing his hand across his forehead.", "다른 이가 찌그러진 모자를 뒤로 밀고 손으로 이마를 쓸어내리며 외쳤다."], ["“Very like.", "\"그럴 만도 하지."], ["It seems a long, long time.”", "참 길고 긴 세월 같으니.\""]], [["“And the more the time,”", "\"그리고 세월이 갈수록,\""], ["pursued the neighbour, “the more you’ve favoured and humoured her, John, till she’s got to be a burden to herself, and everybody else.”", "이웃이 말을 이었다, \"자네는 저 애를 점점 더 떠받들고 받아 줘서, 끝내는 저 애가 제 자신에게도, 모두에게도 짐이 되도록 만들어 버렸어, 존.\""]], [["“Not to me,”", "\"내겐 아니야,\""], ["said her father, falling to his work.", "그녀의 아버지가 다시 일에 몰두하며 말했다."], ["“Not to me.”", "\"내겐 아니야.\""]], [["Florence could feel—who better?—how truly he spoke.", "플로렌스는 그가 얼마나 진실되게 말하는지 느낄 수 있었다—누가 그녀보다 더 잘 알겠는가?"], ["She drew a little closer to him, and would have been glad to touch his rugged hand, and thank him for his goodness to the miserable object that he looked upon with eyes so different from any other man’s.", "그녀는 그에게 조금 더 가까이 다가가, 그 거친 손을 만지고, 그가 다른 누구의 눈과도 그토록 다른 눈으로 바라보는 그 가엾은 존재에게 베푸는 친절에 감사하고 싶었다."]], [["“Who would favour my poor girl—to call it favouring—if I didn’t?”", "\"내가 아니면 누가 우리 가엾은 애를 받아 주겠소—그걸 받아 준다고 할 수 있다면 말이오만?\""], ["said the father.", "아버지가 말했다."]], [["“Ay, ay,”", "\"그래, 그래,\""], ["cried the neighbour.", "이웃이 외쳤다."], ["“In reason, John.", "\"그것도 정도껏 해야지, 존."], ["But you!", "그런데 자네는!"], ["You rob yourself to give to her.", "자네는 저 애한테 주려고 제 몸을 깎아 먹는다고."], ["You bind yourself hand and foot on her account.", "저 애 때문에 손발을 다 묶어 놓고 사는 거야."], ["You make your life miserable along of her.", "저 애 때문에 자네 인생을 비참하게 만든다고."], ["And what does she care!", "그런데 저 애가 그걸 알기나 하나!"], ["You don’t believe she knows it?”", "자네 정말로 저 애가 그걸 안다고 믿는 거야?\""]], [["The father lifted up his head again, and whistled to her.", "아버지는 다시 고개를 들고 소녀에게 휘파람을 불었다."], ["Martha made the same impatient gesture with her crouching shoulders, in reply; and he was glad and happy.", "마사는 그 답으로 웅크린 어깨로 똑같이 짜증스러운 몸짓을 했다. 그러자 그는 기쁘고 행복해했다."]], [["“Only for that, Miss,”", "\"바로 그것 때문이랍니다, 아가씨,\""], ["said the neighbour, with a smile, in which there was more of secret sympathy than he expressed; “only to get that, he never lets her out of his sight!”", "이웃이, 입 밖으로 드러낸 것보다 더 깊은 은밀한 연민이 담긴 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그것 하나 얻겠다고, 저 사람은 저 애를 한시도 눈에서 떼지 않는답니다!\""]], [["“Because the day’ll come, and has been coming a long while,”", "\"왜냐하면 그날이 올 테니까, 그것도 오래전부터 다가오고 있으니까,\""], ["observed the other, bending low over his work, “when to get half as much from that unfort’nate child of mine—to get the trembling of a finger, or the waving of a hair—would be to raise the dead.”", "다른 이가 일감 위로 몸을 낮게 숙이며 말했다, \"내 그 불행한 자식한테서 그 절반만이라도—손가락 하나 떨리는 것이나, 머리카락 한 올 흔들리는 것만이라도—얻으려면 죽은 사람을 되살려 내야 하는 그날이 말이오.\""]], [["Florence softly put some money near his hand on the old boat, and left him.", "플로렌스는 가만히 낡은 배 위, 그의 손 가까이에 돈을 얼마간 놓아두고 그를 떠났다."]], [["And now Florence began to think, if she were to fall ill, if she were to fade like her dear brother, would he then know that she had loved him; would she then grow dear to him; would he come to her bedside, when she was weak and dim of sight, and take her into his embrace, and cancel all the past? Would he so forgive her, in that changed condition, for not having been able to lay open her childish heart to him, as to make it easy to relate with what emotions she had gone out of his room that night; what she had meant to say if she had had the courage; and how she had endeavoured, afterwards, to learn the way she never knew in infancy?", "이제 플로렌스는 생각하기 시작했다. 만일 자기가 병이 들면, 사랑하는 오빠처럼 시들어 간다면, 아버지는 그제야 자기가 그를 사랑했다는 것을 알까. 그제야 자기를 사랑스럽게 여기게 될까. 자기가 쇠약해지고 눈이 침침해졌을 때 그가 머리맡으로 찾아와 자기를 품에 안고 지난 모든 것을 지워 줄까. 자기가 어린 마음을 그에게 열어 보일 수 없었던 것을, 그렇게 달라진 처지에서 그가 용서해 주어, 그날 밤 자기가 어떤 심정으로 그의 방을 나섰는지, 용기가 있었다면 무슨 말을 하려 했는지, 그리고 그 뒤에 어린 시절 끝내 알지 못했던 그 길을 배우려고 어떻게 애써 왔는지를 쉽게 이야기할 수 있게 해 줄까."]], [["Yes, she thought if she were dying, he would relent. She thought, that if she lay, serene and not unwilling to depart, upon the bed that was curtained round with recollections of their darling boy, he would be touched home, and would say, “Dear Florence, live for me, and we will love each other as we might have done, and be as happy as we might have been these many years!” She thought that if she heard such words from him, and had her arms clasped round him, she could answer with a smile, “It is too late for anything but this; I never could be happier, dear father!” and so leave him, with a blessing on her lips.", "그래, 그녀는 생각했다. 자기가 죽어 간다면 아버지도 마음을 누그러뜨리리라고. 자기가 그들의 사랑하던 소년에 대한 추억으로 둘러쳐진 그 침대 위에 평온히, 떠나기를 마다하지 않는 채 누워 있다면, 아버지는 가슴 깊이 감동받아 이렇게 말하리라고. \"사랑하는 플로렌스, 나를 위해 살아 다오. 그러면 우리는 그렇게 했어야 했던 것처럼 서로 사랑하고, 이 여러 해 동안 그럴 수 있었던 것처럼 행복하게 지내자꾸나!\" 그녀는 생각했다. 만일 그에게서 그런 말을 듣고 두 팔로 그를 끌어안을 수 있다면, 자기는 미소로 답할 수 있으리라고. \"이제 이것 말고는 모든 게 너무 늦었어요. 저는 이보다 더 행복할 수 없어요, 사랑하는 아버지!\" 그러고는 입술에 축복을 담은 채 그를 떠날 수 있으리라고."]], [["The golden water she remembered on the wall, appeared to Florence, in the light of such reflections, only as a current flowing on to rest, and to a region where the dear ones, gone before, were waiting, hand in hand; and often when she looked upon the darker river rippling at her feet, she thought with awful wonder, but not terror, of that river which her brother had so often said was bearing him away.", "그녀가 벽에서 떠올리던 그 황금빛 물은, 그런 상념의 빛 속에서 플로렌스에게, 안식을 향해, 그리고 먼저 떠나간 사랑하는 이들이 손에 손을 맞잡고 기다리는 곳을 향해 흘러가는 물줄기로만 보였다. 그리고 종종 그녀가 자기 발치에서 잔물결치는 더 어두운 강을 바라볼 때면, 오빠가 자기를 실어 가고 있다고 그토록 자주 말하던 그 강을, 경외에 찬 경이로움으로, 그러나 두려움 없이 떠올렸다."]], [["The father and his sick daughter were yet fresh in Florence’s mind, and, indeed, that incident was not a week old, when Sir Barnet and his lady going out walking in the lanes one afternoon, proposed to her to bear them company.", "그 아버지와 병든 딸이 아직 플로렌스의 마음에 생생하게 남아 있을 때, 사실 그 일이 있은 지 일주일도 채 안 되었을 때, 바넷 경과 그의 부인이 어느 오후 시골길로 산책을 나가며 그녀에게 동행을 청했다."], ["Florence readily consenting, Lady Skettles ordered out young Barnet as a matter of course.", "플로렌스가 선뜻 응하자, 스케틀스 부인은 으레 그렇듯 어린 바넷도 불러냈다."], ["For nothing delighted Lady Skettles so much, as beholding her eldest son with Florence on his arm.", "스케틀스 부인에게는 맏아들이 플로렌스를 팔에 끼고 가는 것을 보는 일만큼 기쁜 일이 없었기 때문이다."]], [["Barnet, to say the truth, appeared to entertain an opposite sentiment on the subject, and on such occasions frequently expressed himself audibly, though indefinitely, in reference to “a parcel of girls.”", "사실대로 말하자면, 바넷은 이 일에 대해 정반대의 감정을 품은 듯했고, 그런 자리에서 자주 들릴 만큼, 그러나 막연하게 \"계집애들 한 무리\" 운운하며 속내를 내비쳤다."], ["As it was not easy to ruffle her sweet temper, however, Florence generally reconciled the young gentleman to his fate after a few minutes, and they strolled on amicably: Lady Skettles and Sir Barnet following, in a state of perfect complacency and high gratification.", "그러나 그녀의 다정한 성미를 흐트러뜨리기란 쉽지 않은 일이라, 플로렌스는 대개 몇 분 만에 그 젊은 신사를 제 운명과 화해시켰고, 둘은 사이좋게 거닐었다. 스케틀스 부인과 바넷 경은 더없이 흡족하고 크게 만족스러운 상태로 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["This was the order of procedure on the afternoon in question; and Florence had almost succeeded in overruling the present objections of Skettles Junior to his destiny, when a gentleman on horseback came riding by, looked at them earnestly as he passed, drew in his rein, wheeled round, and came riding back again, hat in hand.", "바로 그 오후의 진행 순서가 그러했고, 플로렌스가 자기 운명에 대한 스케틀스 주니어의 현재의 반발을 거의 잠재우는 데 성공했을 무렵, 말을 탄 한 신사가 곁을 지나가다가 지나치면서 그들을 진지하게 바라보더니, 고삐를 당겨 말을 빙 돌려 모자를 손에 든 채 다시 말을 몰고 돌아왔다."]], [["The gentleman had looked particularly at Florence; and when the little party stopped, on his riding back, he bowed to her, before saluting Sir Barnet and his lady.", "그 신사는 특히 플로렌스를 바라보았다. 그가 말을 몰고 돌아와 작은 일행이 멈춰 섰을 때, 그는 바넷 경과 그의 부인에게 인사하기 전에 먼저 그녀에게 절을 했다."], ["Florence had no remembrance of having ever seen him, but she started involuntarily when he came near her, and drew back.", "플로렌스는 그를 본 기억이 전혀 없었으나, 그가 가까이 다가오자 자기도 모르게 흠칫 놀라며 뒤로 물러섰다."]], [["“My horse is perfectly quiet, I assure you,”", "\"제 말은 아주 얌전합니다, 분명히 말씀드리지요,\""], ["said the gentleman.", "신사가 말했다."]], [["It was not that, but something in the gentleman himself—Florence could not have said what—that made her recoil as if she had been stung.", "그것 때문이 아니라, 신사 그 자신의 무언가가—플로렌스로서는 무엇이라고 말할 수 없었다—마치 쏘이기라도 한 듯 그녀를 움찔하게 했다."]], [["“I have the honour to address Miss Dombey, I believe?”", "\"돔비 아가씨를 뵙는 영광을 누리는 것 같습니다만?\""], ["said the gentleman, with a most persuasive smile.", "신사가 더없이 설득력 있는 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["On Florence inclining her head, he added, “My name is Carker.", "플로렌스가 고개를 숙이자 그가 덧붙였다, \"제 이름은 카커입니다."], ["I can hardly hope to be remembered by Miss Dombey, except by name.", "돔비 아가씨께서 이름 말고 저를 기억해 주시기를 바라기는 어렵겠지요."], ["Carker.”", "카커입니다.\""]], [["Florence, sensible of a strange inclination to shiver, though the day was hot, presented him to her host and hostess; by whom he was very graciously received.", "날이 더운데도 이상하게 몸이 떨리는 것을 느낀 플로렌스는 그를 자기 주인 내외에게 소개했고, 그는 매우 정중하게 맞아들여졌다."]], [["“I beg pardon,”", "\"용서해 주십시오,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “a thousand times!", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"천 번이라도요!"], ["But I am going down tomorrow morning to Mr Dombey, at Leamington, and if Miss Dombey can entrust me with any commission, need I say how very happy I shall be?”", "다만 제가 내일 아침 리밍턴에 계신 돔비 씨께 내려가는 길인데, 만일 돔비 아가씨께서 제게 무슨 전갈이라도 맡겨 주신다면, 제가 얼마나 기쁠지 굳이 말씀드릴 필요가 있을까요?\""]], [["Sir Barnet immediately divining that Florence would desire to write a letter to her father, proposed to return, and besought Mr Carker to come home and dine in his riding gear.", "바넷 경은 플로렌스가 아버지에게 편지를 쓰고 싶어 하리라는 것을 즉시 알아채고는, 돌아가자고 제안하며 카커 씨에게 승마복 차림 그대로 집에 와서 만찬을 들기를 청했다."], ["Mr Carker had the misfortune to be engaged to dinner, but if Miss Dombey wished to write, nothing would delight him more than to accompany them back, and to be her faithful slave in waiting as long as she pleased.", "카커 씨는 안타깝게도 만찬 약속이 있었으나, 돔비 아가씨가 글을 쓰고자 하신다면 그들을 따라 돌아가 아가씨가 원하시는 만큼 오래도록 충직한 시종이 되어 시중드는 것보다 더 기쁜 일은 없겠다고 했다."], ["As he said this with his widest smile, and bent down close to her to pat his horse’s neck, Florence meeting his eyes, saw, rather than heard him say, “There is no news of the ship!”", "그가 더없이 활짝 웃으며 이 말을 하고는 자기 말의 목을 두드리려 그녀에게 바짝 몸을 굽혔을 때, 플로렌스는 그의 눈과 마주치며, 그가 \"배에 대한 소식은 없습니다!\"라고 말하는 것을 듣기보다는 차라리 보았다."]], [["Confused, frightened, shrinking from him, and not even sure that he had said those words, for he seemed to have shown them to her in some extraordinary manner through his smile, instead of uttering them, Florence faintly said that she was obliged to him, but she would not write; she had nothing to say.", "당황하고 겁에 질려 그에게서 움츠러든 채, 그가 그런 말을 했는지조차 확실치 않은 가운데—그가 그 말을 입 밖에 내는 대신 미소를 통해 무슨 기이한 방식으로 그녀에게 내보인 듯했기에—플로렌스는 그에게 고맙지만 편지는 쓰지 않겠다고, 할 말이 없다고 가냘프게 말했다."]], [["“Nothing to send, Miss Dombey?”", "\"보낼 것이 아무것도 없으십니까, 돔비 아가씨?\""], ["said the man of teeth.", "그 이빨의 사내가 말했다."]], [["“Nothing,”", "\"아무것도요,\""], ["said Florence, “but my—but my dear love—if you please.”", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"다만 제—다만 제 깊은 사랑을—괜찮으시다면요.\""]], [["Disturbed as Florence was, she raised her eyes to his face with an imploring and expressive look, that plainly besought him, if he knew—which he as plainly did—that any message between her and her father was an uncommon charge, but that one most of all, to spare her.", "당황한 와중에도 플로렌스는 애원하는 듯한, 그리고 의미심장한 눈빛으로 그의 얼굴을 향해 눈을 들어 올렸는데, 그것은 만일 그가 안다면—그는 분명히 알고 있었다—자기와 아버지 사이의 어떤 전갈이든 흔치 않은 부탁이지만, 그중에서도 특히 그 전갈만큼은 자기를 봐주기를 분명히 간청하는 것이었다."], ["Mr Carker smiled and bowed low, and being charged by Sir Barnet with the best compliments of himself and Lady Skettles, took his leave, and rode away: leaving a favourable impression on that worthy couple.", "카커 씨는 미소 지으며 깊이 절했고, 바넷 경으로부터 자기와 스케틀스 부인의 안부를 전해 달라는 부탁을 받고는 작별을 고하고 말을 몰아 떠났다. 그는 그 훌륭한 내외에게 호의적인 인상을 남겼다."], ["Florence was seized with such a shudder as he went, that Sir Barnet, adopting the popular superstition, supposed somebody was passing over her grave.", "그가 떠날 때 플로렌스는 어찌나 심하게 몸서리를 쳤던지, 바넷 경은 흔한 미신을 따라 누군가가 그녀의 무덤 위를 지나가는 모양이라고 여겼다."], ["Mr Carker turning a corner, on the instant, looked back, and bowed, and disappeared, as if he rode off to the churchyard straight, to do it.", "카커 씨는 모퉁이를 돌면서 그 즉시 뒤를 돌아보고 절을 하고는, 마치 그 일을 하러 곧장 교회 묘지로 말을 몰아가기라도 하듯 사라졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXV. Strange News of Uncle Sol", "# CHAPTER XXV. Strange News of Uncle Sol"]], [["Captain Cuttle, though no sluggard, did not turn out so early on the morning after he had seen Sol Gills, through the shop-window, writing in the parlour, with the Midshipman upon the counter, and Rob the Grinder making up his bed below it, but that the clocks struck six as he raised himself on his elbow, and took a survey of his little chamber.", "커틀 선장은 게으름뱅이는 아니었지만, 솔 길스가 가게 창문 너머로 응접실에서 글을 쓰고 있고, 카운터 위에는 미드십맨이 놓여 있으며, 로브 더 그라인더가 그 아래에서 잠자리를 펴고 있는 모습을 본 그 이튿날 아침에는, 그가 팔꿈치를 짚고 몸을 일으켜 자기 작은 방을 둘러보았을 때 시계가 여섯 시를 칠 만큼 일찍 일어났을 뿐이었다."], ["The Captain’s eyes must have done severe duty, if he usually opened them as wide on awaking as he did that morning; and were but roughly rewarded for their vigilance, if he generally rubbed them half as hard.", "만약 선장이 잠에서 깰 때마다 그날 아침처럼 눈을 크게 떴다면, 그의 눈은 혹독한 임무를 수행해 온 셈이었을 것이다. 또 평소에도 그날의 절반만큼이라도 세게 눈을 비볐다면, 그 경계심에 대한 보상은 거칠기 그지없었을 것이다."], ["But the occasion was no common one, for Rob the Grinder had certainly never stood in the doorway of Captain Cuttle’s room before, and in it he stood then, panting at the Captain, with a flushed and touzled air of Bed about him, that greatly heightened both his colour and expression.", "그러나 이번 경우는 예사로운 것이 아니었으니, 로브 더 그라인더가 커틀 선장의 방문 앞에 서 있은 적은 분명 한 번도 없었는데, 그가 그때 거기 서서 선장을 향해 헐떡이고 있었고, 그 몸에서는 막 잠자리에서 나온 듯 발갛게 상기되고 헝클어진 기색이 풍겨 그의 혈색과 표정을 한층 도드라지게 했던 것이다."]], [["“Holloa!”", "\"이런!\""], ["roared the Captain.", "선장이 고함쳤다."], ["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["Before Rob could stammer a word in answer, Captain Cuttle turned out, all in a heap, and covered the boy’s mouth with his hand.", "로브가 미처 한마디 대답을 더듬거리기도 전에, 커틀 선장은 와르르 몸을 굴려 침대에서 나와, 손으로 소년의 입을 막았다."]], [["“Steady, my lad,”", "\"진정해라, 얘야,\""], ["said the Captain, “don’t ye speak a word to me as yet!”", "선장이 말했다, \"아직 나한테 한마디도 하지 마라!\""]], [["The Captain, looking at his visitor in great consternation, gently shouldered him into the next room, after laying this injunction upon him; and disappearing for a few moments, forthwith returned in the blue suit.", "선장은 몹시 당황한 얼굴로 손님을 바라보며, 이렇게 명령을 내려 둔 뒤 그를 살며시 옆방으로 떠밀어 넣고는, 잠시 사라졌다가 곧바로 푸른 양복 차림으로 돌아왔다."], ["Holding up his hand in token of the injunction not yet being taken off, Captain Cuttle walked up to the cupboard, and poured himself out a dram; a counterpart of which he handed to the messenger.", "아직 명령이 해제되지 않았음을 알리는 표시로 한 손을 들어 올린 채, 커틀 선장은 찬장으로 걸어가 자기 몫의 술을 한 잔 따르고, 똑같은 한 잔을 전령에게 건넸다."], ["The Captain then stood himself up in a corner, against the wall, as if to forestall the possibility of being knocked backwards by the communication that was to be made to him; and having swallowed his liquor, with his eyes fixed on the messenger, and his face as pale as his face could be, requested him to “heave ahead.”", "그런 다음 선장은, 자기에게 전해질 소식에 뒤로 자빠질 가능성을 미리 막으려는 듯, 벽에 기대 한쪽 구석에 자리를 잡고 섰다. 그러고는 술을 들이켜고, 전령에게 시선을 못 박은 채, 더는 창백할 수 없을 만큼 창백한 얼굴로, 그에게 \"이제 출항하라\"고 청했다."]], [["“Do you mean, tell you, Captain?”", "\"제 말은, 말씀드리라는 건가요, 선장님?\""], ["asked Rob, who had been greatly impressed by these precautions.", "로브가 물었다. 이런 사전 절차에 그는 단단히 위압당해 있었다."]], [["“Ay!”", "\"그렇다!\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Well, Sir,”", "\"그게, 선장님,\""], ["said Rob, “I ain’t got much to tell.", "로브가 말했다, \"별로 말씀드릴 게 없어요."], ["But look here!”", "하지만 이걸 보세요!\""]], [["Rob produced a bundle of keys.", "로브가 열쇠 꾸러미를 꺼내 보였다."], ["The Captain surveyed them, remained in his corner, and surveyed the messenger.", "선장은 그것을 살펴보고는, 구석에 그대로 선 채, 전령을 살펴보았다."]], [["“And look here!”", "\"그리고 이것도 보세요!\""], ["pursued Rob.", "로브가 말을 이었다."]], [["The boy produced a sealed packet, which Captain Cuttle stared at as he had stared at the keys.", "소년은 봉인된 꾸러미를 꺼냈고, 커틀 선장은 열쇠를 노려보았던 것처럼 그것을 노려보았다."]], [["“When I woke this morning, Captain,”", "\"오늘 아침 제가 잠에서 깼을 때요, 선장님,\""], ["said Rob, “which was about a quarter after five, I found these on my pillow.", "로브가 말했다, \"다섯 시 십오 분쯤이었는데요, 베개 위에서 이것들을 발견했어요."], ["The shop-door was unbolted and unlocked, and Mr Gills gone.”", "가게 문은 빗장이 풀리고 자물쇠도 열려 있었고, 길스 씨는 가 버리셨어요.\""]], [["“Gone!”", "\"가 버려!\""], ["roared the Captain.", "선장이 고함쳤다."]], [["“Flowed, Sir,”", "\"떠나 버리셨어요, 선장님,\""], ["returned Rob.", "로브가 대답했다."]], [["The Captain’s voice was so tremendous, and he came out of his corner with such way on him, that Rob retreated before him into another corner: holding out the keys and packet, to prevent himself from being run down.", "선장의 목소리가 어찌나 무시무시하고, 또 그가 어찌나 기세 좋게 구석에서 튀어나왔던지, 로브는 그 앞에서 다른 구석으로 물러나며, 깔려 넘어지지 않으려고 열쇠와 꾸러미를 앞으로 내밀었다."]], [["“‘For Captain Cuttle,’ Sir,”", "\"'커틀 선장께'라고요, 선장님,\""], ["cried Rob, “is on the keys, and on the packet too.", "로브가 외쳤다, \"열쇠에도 적혀 있고, 꾸러미에도 적혀 있어요."], ["Upon my word and honour, Captain Cuttle, I don’t know anything more about it.", "제 명예를 걸고 맹세컨대, 커틀 선장님, 저는 이 일에 대해 더는 아무것도 몰라요."], ["I wish I may die if I do!", "거짓이면 죽어도 좋아요!"], ["Here’s a sitiwation for a lad that’s just got a sitiwation,”", "이제 막 일자리를 얻은 녀석한테 이게 무슨 꼴이람,\""], ["cried the unfortunate Grinder, screwing his cuff into his face: “his master bolted with his place, and him blamed for it!”", "불운한 그라인더는 소맷부리를 얼굴에 비벼 대며 외쳤다. \"주인은 자리를 박차고 달아나 버리고, 그 책임은 녀석한테 뒤집어씌우다니!\""]], [["These lamentations had reference to Captain Cuttle’s gaze, or rather glare, which was full of vague suspicions, threatenings, and denunciations.", "이 한탄은 커틀 선장의 시선, 아니 차라리 노려봄을 두고 한 것이었으니, 그 눈길에는 막연한 의심과 위협과 비난이 가득했던 것이다."], ["Taking the proffered packet from his hand, the Captain opened it and read as follows:—", "선장은 그가 내민 꾸러미를 손에서 받아들어 뜯고는 다음과 같이 읽었다."]], [["“‘My dear Ned Cuttle.", "\"'친애하는 네드 커틀에게."], ["Enclosed is my will!’”", "동봉한 것은 내 유언장일세!'\""], ["The Captain turned it over, with a doubtful look—“\"and Testament’—Where’s the Testament?”", "선장은 미심쩍은 표정으로 그것을 뒤집어 보았다. \"'그리고 유언 증서'라—유언 증서는 어디 있느냐?\""], ["said the Captain, instantly impeaching the ill-fated Grinder.", "선장은 말하며, 당장 그 불운한 그라인더를 탄핵했다."], ["“What have you done with that, my lad?”", "\"그걸 어쨌느냐, 얘야?\""]], [["“I never see it,”", "\"저는 본 적도 없어요,\""], ["whimpered Rob.", "로브가 흐느꼈다."], ["“Don’t keep on suspecting an innocent lad, Captain.", "\"죄 없는 녀석을 계속 의심하지 마세요, 선장님."], ["I never touched the Testament.”", "저는 유언 증서엔 손도 안 댔어요.\""]], [["Captain Cuttle shook his head, implying that somebody must be made answerable for it; and gravely proceeded:", "커틀 선장은 누군가는 그것에 대해 책임을 져야 한다는 뜻으로 고개를 가로젓고는, 엄숙하게 계속 읽었다."]], [["“‘Which don’t break open for a year, or until you have decisive intelligence of my dear Walter, who is dear to you, Ned, too, I am sure.’”", "\"'이것은 일 년 동안, 또는 자네에게도 분명 소중할 내 사랑하는 월터에 대한 결정적인 소식을 들을 때까지는 뜯지 말게, 네드.'\""], ["The Captain paused and shook his head in some emotion; then, as a re-establishment of his dignity in this trying position, looked with exceeding sternness at the Grinder.", "선장은 잠시 멈추고 다소 감정에 북받쳐 고개를 가로저었다. 그러더니 이 시련의 처지에서 자기 위엄을 다시 세우려는 듯, 그라인더를 더없이 준엄하게 노려보았다."], ["“‘If you should never hear of me, or see me more, Ned, remember an old friend as he will remember you to the last—kindly; and at least until the period I have mentioned has expired, keep a home in the old place for Walter.", "\"'만약 자네가 나에 대해 다시는 소식을 듣지도, 나를 다시 보지도 못하게 되거든, 네드, 옛 친구가 마지막까지 자네를 다정히 기억하듯, 그를 다정히 기억해 주게. 그리고 적어도 내가 말한 그 기한이 다할 때까지는, 옛 자리에 월터를 위한 보금자리를 지켜 주게."], ["There are no debts, the loan from Dombey’s House is paid off and all my keys I send with this.", "빚은 없네, 돔비 상사에서 빌린 돈은 다 갚았고, 내 열쇠는 모두 이것과 함께 보내네."], ["Keep this quiet, and make no inquiry for me; it is useless.", "이 일은 조용히 묻어 두고, 나를 찾으려 들지 말게. 부질없는 일이네."], ["So no more, dear Ned, from your true friend, Solomon Gills.’”", "그럼 이만 줄이네, 친애하는 네드, 자네의 진실한 벗, 솔로몬 길스로부터.'\""], ["The Captain took a long breath, and then read these words written below: “‘The boy Rob, well recommended, as I told you, from Dombey’s House.", "선장은 길게 숨을 들이쉬고는, 그 아래에 적힌 다음 글을 읽었다. \"'그 소년 로브는, 전에 말했듯이, 돔비 상사에서 추천받은 잘 보증된 아이일세."], ["If all else should come to the hammer, take care, Ned, of the little Midshipman.’”", "만약 다른 모든 것이 경매에 넘어가게 되거든, 네드, 그 작은 미드십맨을 잘 돌봐 주게.'\""]], [["To convey to posterity any idea of the manner in which the Captain, after turning this letter over and over, and reading it a score of times, sat down in his chair, and held a court-martial on the subject in his own mind, would require the united genius of all the great men, who, discarding their own untoward days, have determined to go down to posterity, and have never got there.", "선장이 이 편지를 몇 번이고 뒤집어 가며 스무 번쯤 읽고 난 뒤, 자기 의자에 주저앉아, 마음속으로 이 문제에 대한 군법 회의를 연 그 태도를 후세에 조금이라도 전하려면, 자기들의 불운한 시대를 내던지고 후세에 길이 남기로 결심했으나 끝내 거기 이르지 못한 모든 위인들의 천재성을 한데 모아야 할 것이다."], ["At first the Captain was too much confounded and distressed to think of anything but the letter itself; and even when his thoughts began to glance upon the various attendant facts, they might, perhaps, as well have occupied themselves with their former theme, for any light they reflected on them.", "처음에 선장은 너무도 어리둥절하고 비통하여 편지 그 자체 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수 없었다. 그리고 그의 생각이 거기 딸린 갖가지 정황을 흘끗거리기 시작했을 때조차, 그것들이 거기에 비추는 빛으로 보자면 차라리 앞서의 주제에나 골몰하는 편이 나았을 정도였다."], ["In this state of mind, Captain Cuttle having the Grinder before the court, and no one else, found it a great relief to decide, generally, that he was an object of suspicion: which the Captain so clearly expressed in his visage, that Rob remonstrated.", "이런 심정으로, 커틀 선장은 법정에 그라인더 말고는 아무도 세워 둔 사람이 없었던지라, 대체로 그가 의심의 대상이라고 단정하는 데서 큰 위안을 얻었다. 그 단정을 선장이 어찌나 분명하게 얼굴에 드러냈던지, 로브가 항변했다."]], [["“Oh, don’t, Captain!”", "\"오, 그러지 마세요, 선장님!\""], ["cried the Grinder.", "그라인더가 외쳤다."], ["“I wonder how you can!", "\"어떻게 그러실 수가 있어요!"], ["what have I done to be looked at, like that?”", "제가 그런 눈으로 보일 만한 무슨 짓을 했다고요?\""]], [["“My lad,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, “don’t you sing out afore you’re hurt.", "커틀 선장이 말했다, \"다치기도 전에 비명부터 지르지 마라."], ["And don’t you commit yourself, whatever you do.”", "그리고 무슨 일을 하든, 네 입으로 죄를 자백하지 마라.\""]], [["“I haven’t been and committed nothing, Captain!”", "\"저는 아무 짓도 안 했고 자백할 것도 없어요, 선장님!\""], ["answered Rob.", "로브가 대답했다."]], [["“Keep her free, then,”", "\"그럼 배를 막힘없이 두고,\""], ["said the Captain, impressively, “and ride easy.”", "선장이 의미심장하게 말했다, \"순탄히 항해해라.\""]], [["With a deep sense of the responsibility imposed upon him, and the necessity of thoroughly fathoming this mysterious affair as became a man in his relations with the parties, Captain Cuttle resolved to go down and examine the premises, and to keep the Grinder with him.", "자기에게 지워진 책임과, 당사자들과의 관계에 걸맞게 이 수수께끼 같은 사건을 철저히 헤아려야 할 필요를 깊이 느끼며, 커틀 선장은 가게로 내려가 현장을 조사하고, 그라인더를 곁에 붙들어 두기로 결심했다."], ["Considering that youth as under arrest at present, the Captain was in some doubt whether it might not be expedient to handcuff him, or tie his ankles together, or attach a weight to his legs; but not being clear as to the legality of such formalities, the Captain decided merely to hold him by the shoulder all the way, and knock him down if he made any objection.", "지금으로서는 그 젊은이를 체포된 상태로 여겼던 터라, 선장은 그에게 수갑을 채우거나, 발목을 묶어 두거나, 다리에 추를 매다는 편이 상책이 아닐까 다소 망설였다. 그러나 그런 격식들이 적법한지 분명치 않았으므로, 선장은 그저 가는 내내 그의 어깨를 붙들고 있다가, 그가 무슨 반항이라도 하면 때려눕히기로 마음먹었다."]], [["However, he made none, and consequently got to the Instrument-maker’s house without being placed under any more stringent restraint.", "그러나 그는 아무 반항도 하지 않았고, 따라서 더 엄중한 구속을 받지 않고 기구 제작자의 집에 이르렀다."], ["As the shutters were not yet taken down, the Captain’s first care was to have the shop opened; and when the daylight was freely admitted, he proceeded, with its aid, to further investigation.", "덧문이 아직 내려져 있지 않았으므로, 선장이 가장 먼저 챙긴 일은 가게를 여는 것이었다. 그리고 햇빛이 거리낌 없이 들어오게 되자, 그는 그 빛의 도움을 받아 한층 더 깊은 조사에 착수했다."]], [["The Captain’s first care was to establish himself in a chair in the shop, as President of the solemn tribunal that was sitting within him; and to require Rob to lie down in his bed under the counter, show exactly where he discovered the keys and packet when he awoke, how he found the door when he went to try it, how he started off to Brig Place—cautiously preventing the latter imitation from being carried farther than the threshold—and so on to the end of the chapter.", "선장이 가장 먼저 챙긴 일은, 자기 안에서 열리고 있는 그 엄숙한 법정의 재판장으로서 가게 안 의자에 자리를 잡는 것이었다. 그리고 로브에게 카운터 밑 잠자리에 누워, 잠에서 깼을 때 열쇠와 꾸러미를 발견한 정확한 위치, 문을 열어 보러 갔을 때 문이 어떻게 되어 있었는지, 어떻게 브리그 플레이스로 출발했는지를—다만 마지막 재연은 문지방 너머로 넘어가지 않도록 조심스레 막으면서—그렇게 처음부터 끝까지 그대로 보여 보라고 요구했다."], ["When all this had been done several times, the Captain shook his head and seemed to think the matter had a bad look.", "이 모든 것을 여러 차례 시킨 끝에, 선장은 고개를 가로저으며 사태가 좋지 않은 낌새라고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["Next, the Captain, with some indistinct idea of finding a body, instituted a strict search over the whole house; groping in the cellars with a lighted candle, thrusting his hook behind doors, bringing his head into violent contact with beams, and covering himself with cobwebs.", "다음으로, 선장은 시체라도 찾아내겠다는 어렴풋한 생각으로, 온 집 안을 샅샅이 뒤지기 시작했다. 불 켠 양초를 들고 지하실을 더듬고, 문 뒤로 갈고리를 찔러 넣고, 들보에 머리를 세게 부딪고, 온몸에 거미줄을 뒤집어썼다."], ["Mounting up to the old man’s bed-room, they found that he had not been in bed on the previous night, but had merely lain down on the coverlet, as was evident from the impression yet remaining there.", "노인의 침실로 올라가 보니, 그가 간밤에 잠자리에 든 것이 아니라 그저 침대보 위에 누워만 있었음을 알 수 있었는데, 거기 아직 남아 있는 자국을 보면 분명했다."]], [["“And I think, Captain,”", "\"제 생각엔요, 선장님,\""], ["said Rob, looking round the room, “that when Mr Gills was going in and out so often, these last few days, he was taking little things away, piecemeal, not to attract attention.”", "로브가 방 안을 둘러보며 말했다, \"길스 씨가 요 며칠 그렇게 자주 드나드신 건, 사람들 눈에 띄지 않으려고 자잘한 물건들을 조금씩 빼내신 거예요.\""]], [["“Ay!”", "\"그렇구나!\""], ["said the Captain, mysteriously.", "선장이 알 수 없다는 듯 말했다."], ["“Why so, my lad?”", "\"어째서 그러냐, 얘야?\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"왜냐면요,\""], ["returned Rob, looking about, “I don’t see his shaving tackle.", "로브가 둘러보며 대답했다, \"면도 도구가 안 보이거든요."], ["Nor his brushes, Captain.", "솔도요, 선장님."], ["Nor no shirts.", "셔츠도 한 장 없고요."], ["Nor yet his shoes.”", "신발도 그렇고요.\""]], [["As each of these articles was mentioned, Captain Cuttle took particular notice of the corresponding department of the Grinder, lest he should appear to have been in recent use, or should prove to be in present possession thereof.", "이 물건들이 하나하나 거론될 때마다, 커틀 선장은 그라인더의 해당 부위를 각별히 눈여겨보았는데, 그것이 최근에 사용된 듯이 보이거나, 지금 그가 그것을 지니고 있는 것으로 드러날까 봐서였다."], ["But Rob had no occasion to shave, was not brushed, and wore the clothes he had on for a long time past, beyond all possibility of a mistake.", "그러나 로브는 면도할 일도 없었고, 솔질도 안 한 상태였으며, 입고 있는 옷은 오래전부터 죽 걸치고 다닌 것이어서, 도무지 착각의 여지가 없었다."]], [["“And what should you say,”", "\"그래 네 생각엔,\""], ["said the Captain—“not committing yourself—about his time of sheering off?", "선장이 말했다, \"네 입으로 죄를 자백하는 건 아니고 말이다—그분이 줄행랑을 친 시각이 언제쯤일 것 같으냐?"], ["Hey?”", "응?\""]], [["“Why, I think, Captain,”", "\"글쎄요, 제 생각엔요, 선장님,\""], ["returned Rob, “that he must have gone pretty soon after I began to snore.”", "로브가 대답했다, \"제가 코를 골기 시작하고 얼마 안 돼서 가셨을 거예요.\""]], [["“What o’clock was that?”", "\"그게 몇 시였느냐?\""], ["said the Captain, prepared to be very particular about the exact time.", "선장이 정확한 시각을 무척 따져 물을 채비를 하며 말했다."]], [["“How can I tell, Captain!”", "\"제가 어떻게 알겠어요, 선장님!\""], ["answered Rob.", "로브가 대답했다."], ["“I only know that I’m a heavy sleeper at first, and a light one towards morning; and if Mr Gills had come through the shop near daybreak, though ever so much on tiptoe, I’m pretty sure I should have heard him shut the door at all events.”", "\"저는 처음엔 깊이 잠들었다가 새벽녘엔 얕게 잔다는 것만 알아요. 그러니까 만약 길스 씨가 동틀 무렵에 가게를 가로질러 가셨다면, 아무리 발끝으로 살금살금 걸으셨대도, 적어도 문 닫는 소리만은 분명히 들었을 거라고요.\""]], [["On mature consideration of this evidence, Captain Cuttle began to think that the Instrument-maker must have vanished of his own accord; to which logical conclusion he was assisted by the letter addressed to himself, which, as being undeniably in the old man’s handwriting, would seem, with no great forcing, to bear the construction, that he arranged of his own will to go, and so went.", "이 증언을 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 커틀 선장은 기구 제작자가 제 발로 사라진 게 틀림없다고 생각하기 시작했다. 이 논리적 결론에 이르는 데는 자기 앞으로 온 편지가 한몫했으니, 그것은 분명 노인의 필적이었던지라, 그가 제 뜻으로 떠나기로 정하고 그래서 떠났다는 해석을 별로 무리 없이 떠받칠 만했던 것이다."], ["The Captain had next to consider where and why?", "선장이 다음으로 따져 보아야 할 것은 어디로, 그리고 왜 갔느냐였다."], ["and as there was no way whatsoever that he saw to the solution of the first difficulty, he confined his meditations to the second.", "그러나 첫 번째 난제를 풀 길은 도무지 보이지 않았으므로, 그는 두 번째에만 생각을 가두었다."]], [["Remembering the old man’s curious manner, and the farewell he had taken of him; unaccountably fervent at the time, but quite intelligible now: a terrible apprehension strengthened on the Captain, that, overpowered by his anxieties and regrets for Walter, he had been driven to commit suicide.", "노인의 묘한 태도와, 그가 자기에게 작별을 고하던 모습을 떠올리니—그때는 까닭 모르게 절절했으나 이제는 충분히 납득되는 것이었다—선장의 마음속에는 끔찍한 우려가 점점 굳어졌다. 노인이 월터에 대한 근심과 회한에 짓눌린 나머지, 자살로 내몰린 것이 아닐까 하는 우려였다."], ["Unequal to the wear and tear of daily life, as he had often professed himself to be, and shaken as he no doubt was by the uncertainty and deferred hope he had undergone, it seemed no violently strained misgiving, but only too probable.", "노인이 입버릇처럼 스스로 일러 왔듯이 일상의 시달림을 견뎌 낼 만한 사람이 못 되었고, 그가 겪어 온 불확실함과 미루어진 희망에 분명 흔들려 있었던 만큼, 그것은 결코 억지로 짜낸 기우가 아니라 오히려 너무도 있을 법한 일로 여겨졌다."]], [["Free from debt, and with no fear for his personal liberty, or the seizure of his goods, what else but such a state of madness could have hurried him away alone and secretly?", "빚도 없고, 신변의 자유나 재산 압류를 두려워할 까닭도 없는데, 그런 광기 어린 상태가 아니고서야 무엇이 그를 홀로, 은밀히 떠나도록 다급하게 내몰았겠는가?"], ["As to his carrying some apparel with him, if he had really done so—and they were not even sure of that—he might have done so, the Captain argued, to prevent inquiry, to distract attention from his probable fate, or to ease the very mind that was now revolving all these possibilities.", "그가 옷가지를 좀 챙겨 갔다는 점으로 말하자면, 정말 그렇게 했다 하더라도—그들은 그것조차 확실치 않았다—사람들이 캐묻지 못하게 하려고, 자기에게 닥칠 운명에서 주의를 돌리려고, 아니면 바로 지금 이 모든 가능성을 곱씹고 있는 그 마음을 달래려고 그랬을 수도 있다고, 선장은 따졌다."], ["Such, reduced into plain language, and condensed within a small compass, was the final result and substance of Captain Cuttle’s deliberations: which took a long time to arrive at this pass, and were, like some more public deliberations, very discursive and disorderly.", "평이한 말로 추려, 좁은 테두리 안에 응축하자면, 그것이 곧 커틀 선장의 숙고가 다다른 최종 결론이자 골자였다. 그 숙고는 이 지경에 이르기까지 오랜 시간이 걸렸고, 어떤 좀 더 공적인 숙고들이 그러하듯, 몹시 산만하고 두서가 없었다."]], [["Dejected and despondent in the extreme, Captain Cuttle felt it just to release Rob from the arrest in which he had placed him, and to enlarge him, subject to a kind of honourable inspection which he still resolved to exercise; and having hired a man, from Brogley the Broker, to sit in the shop during their absence, the Captain, taking Rob with him, issued forth upon a dismal quest after the mortal remains of Solomon Gills.", "낙담하고 의기소침하기 이를 데 없는 가운데, 커틀 선장은 자기가 로브를 가두어 둔 체포 상태에서 풀어 주는 것이 옳다고 느꼈다. 그러고는 그를 풀어 주되, 자기가 여전히 행사하기로 마음먹은 일종의 명예로운 감독 아래 두기로 했다. 그리고 자기들이 자리를 비운 동안 가게를 지키도록 중개인 브로글리에게서 사람을 하나 고용한 뒤, 선장은 로브를 데리고 솔로몬 길스의 유해를 찾아 음울한 탐색에 나섰다."]], [["Not a station-house, or bone-house, or work-house in the metropolis escaped a visitation from the hard glazed hat.", "수도의 어느 파출소도, 시체 보관소도, 구빈원도 그 단단하고 번들거리는 모자의 방문을 면치 못했다."], ["Along the wharves, among the shipping on the bank-side, up the river, down the river, here, there, everywhere, it went gleaming where men were thickest, like the hero’s helmet in an epic battle.", "부두를 따라, 강가의 선박들 사이로, 강 상류로, 강 하류로, 여기로, 저기로, 사방으로, 그 모자는 사람이 가장 빽빽한 곳에서, 마치 서사시 전투에 나오는 영웅의 투구처럼 번뜩이며 누볐다."], ["For a whole week the Captain read of all the found and missing people in all the newspapers and handbills, and went forth on expeditions at all hours of the day to identify Solomon Gills, in poor little ship-boys who had fallen overboard, and in tall foreigners with dark beards who had taken poison—“to make sure,”", "꼬박 한 주 동안 선장은 온갖 신문과 전단에서 발견된 사람과 실종된 사람에 관한 기사를 모조리 읽고, 하루 중 어느 시각이건 가리지 않고 탐색에 나섰으니, 바다에 빠진 가엾고 어린 선원 소년들에게서, 독약을 삼킨 검은 수염의 키 큰 외국인들에게서 솔로몬 길스를 찾아내려—\"그게 그가 아닌지 확실히 하려고,\""], ["Captain Cuttle said, “that it wam’t him.”", "라고 커틀 선장은 말했다—애쓴 것이었다."], ["It is a sure thing that it never was, and that the good Captain had no other satisfaction.", "한 번도 그가 아니었다는 것, 그리고 그 선량한 선장이 그 밖에 아무런 위안도 얻지 못했다는 것은 확실하다."]], [["Captain Cuttle at last abandoned these attempts as hopeless, and set himself to consider what was to be done next.", "커틀 선장은 마침내 이 시도들을 가망 없는 일로 단념하고, 다음에는 무엇을 해야 할지 곰곰이 따져 보기 시작했다."], ["After several new perusals of his poor friend’s letter, he considered that the maintenance of “a home in the old place for Walter” was the primary duty imposed upon him.", "가엾은 친구의 편지를 여러 번 새로 정독한 끝에, 그는 \"옛 자리에 월터를 위한 보금자리\"를 지키는 것이 자기에게 지워진 으뜸가는 의무라고 여겼다."], ["Therefore, the Captain’s decision was, that he would keep house on the premises of Solomon Gills himself, and would go into the instrument-business, and see what came of it.", "따라서 선장의 결심은, 자기가 직접 솔로몬 길스의 가게에 살림을 차리고, 기구 사업에 뛰어들어, 어떻게 되어 가는지 보겠다는 것이었다."]], [["But as this step involved the relinquishment of his apartments at Mrs MacStinger’s, and he knew that resolute woman would never hear of his deserting them, the Captain took the desperate determination of running away.", "그러나 이 조치는 맥스팅어 부인 집의 자기 셋방을 내놓는 일을 수반하는데, 그 단호한 여인이 자기가 셋방을 버리고 떠난다는 말을 결코 들으려 하지 않으리라는 것을 알았으므로, 선장은 도망을 친다는 필사적인 결단을 내렸다."]], [["“Now, look ye here, my lad,”", "\"자, 잘 들어라, 얘야,\""], ["said the Captain to Rob, when he had matured this notable scheme, “to-morrow, I shan’t be found in this here roadstead till night—not till arter midnight p’rhaps.", "이 굉장한 계획을 다 짜낸 뒤 선장이 로브에게 말했다, \"내일 나는 밤이 되기 전엔 이 정박지에서 보이지 않을 거다—어쩌면 자정이 지나서야 올지도 몰라."], ["But you keep watch till you hear me knock, and the moment you do, turn-to, and open the door.”", "하지만 너는 내가 노크하는 소리를 들을 때까지 망을 봐라. 그리고 그 소리가 들리는 순간, 잽싸게 움직여서 문을 열어라.\""]], [["“Very good, Captain,”", "\"잘 알겠습니다, 선장님,\""], ["said Rob.", "로브가 말했다."]], [["“You’ll continue to be rated on these here books,”", "\"너는 이 명부에 계속 등록되어 있을 거다,\""], ["pursued the Captain condescendingly, “and I don’t say but what you may get promotion, if you and me should pull together with a will.", "선장이 거들먹거리며 말을 이었다, \"그리고 너랑 내가 마음을 모아 함께 노를 저어 나간다면, 네가 진급 못 할 거라곤 말 안 하마."], ["But the moment you hear me knock to-morrow night, whatever time it is, turn-to and show yourself smart with the door.”", "하지만 내일 밤 내가 노크하는 소리를 듣는 순간, 그게 몇 시건, 잽싸게 움직여서 날쌔게 문을 열어 보여라.\""]], [["“I’ll be sure to do it, Captain,”", "\"꼭 그렇게 하겠습니다, 선장님,\""], ["replied Rob.", "로브가 대답했다."]], [["“Because you understand,”", "\"왜냐면 말이다,\""], ["resumed the Captain, coming back again to enforce this charge upon his mind, “there may be, for anything I can say, a chase; and I might be took while I was waiting, if you didn’t show yourself smart with the door.”", "선장이 이 임무를 그의 마음에 단단히 새겨 두려고 다시 돌아와 말을 이었다, \"내 입으로 장담은 못 하겠다만, 추격이 붙을 수도 있어. 그러니 네가 날쌔게 문을 열어 보여 주지 않으면, 내가 기다리는 사이에 붙잡힐지도 모른단 말이다.\""]], [["Rob again assured the Captain that he would be prompt and wakeful; and the Captain having made this prudent arrangement, went home to Mrs MacStinger’s for the last time.", "로브는 자기가 잽싸고 빈틈없이 깨어 있겠노라고 선장에게 다시금 다짐했다. 그리고 선장은 이런 신중한 채비를 마친 뒤, 마지막으로 맥스팅어 부인의 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["The sense the Captain had of its being the last time, and of the awful purpose hidden beneath his blue waistcoat, inspired him with such a mortal dread of Mrs MacStinger, that the sound of that lady’s foot downstairs at any time of the day, was sufficient to throw him into a fit of trembling.", "그것이 마지막이라는 자각과, 푸른 조끼 아래 감춰 둔 그 무시무시한 속셈이 선장에게 맥스팅어 부인에 대한 죽을 듯한 공포를 불러일으켜서, 하루 중 어느 때건 아래층에서 그 여인의 발소리가 들리기만 해도 선장은 부들부들 떨기 시작할 지경이었다."], ["It fell out, too, that Mrs MacStinger was in a charming temper—mild and placid as a house—lamb; and Captain Cuttle’s conscience suffered terrible twinges, when she came up to inquire if she could cook him nothing for his dinner.", "게다가 공교롭게도 맥스팅어 부인은 더없이 좋은 기분이었으니—집에서 기르는 새끼 양처럼 온순하고 차분했다. 그래서 그녀가 올라와 저녁으로 무얼 좀 요리해 드릴까 묻자, 커틀 선장의 양심은 끔찍하게 욱신거리며 쑤셔 댔다."]], [["“A nice small kidney-pudding now, Cap’en Cuttle,”", "\"맛 좋은 콩팥 푸딩이라도 조금 어떠세요, 커틀 선장님,\""], ["said his landlady: “or a sheep’s heart.", "하숙집 안주인이 말했다. \"아니면 양 염통이라도요."], ["Don’t mind my trouble.”", "제 수고는 마음 쓰지 마시고요.\""]], [["“No thank’ee, Ma’am,”", "\"아닙니다, 고맙소만, 부인,\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."]], [["“Have a roast fowl,”", "\"통닭구이라도 드세요,\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger, “with a bit of weal stuffing and some egg sauce.", "맥스팅어 부인이 말했다, \"송아지 고기 소를 좀 넣고 달걀 소스를 곁들여서요."], ["Come, Cap’en Cuttle!", "자요, 커틀 선장님!"], ["Give yourself a little treat!”", "한번쯤 호사를 좀 누리시라고요!\""]], [["“No thank’ee, Ma’am,”", "\"아닙니다, 고맙소만, 부인,\""], ["returned the Captain very humbly.", "선장이 아주 풀 죽은 채로 대답했다."]], [["“I’m sure you’re out of sorts, and want to be stimulated,”", "\"분명히 기분이 안 좋으셔서 기운을 좀 돋워야 할 것 같은데요,\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger.", "맥스팅어 부인이 말했다."], ["“Why not have, for once in a way, a bottle of sherry wine?”", "\"어쩌다 한 번쯤, 셰리 와인 한 병 드시지 그러세요?\""]], [["“Well, Ma’am,”", "\"글쎄요, 부인,\""], ["rejoined the Captain, “if you’d be so good as take a glass or two, I think I would try that.", "선장이 응수했다, \"부인께서 한두 잔 드실 만큼 친절을 베풀어 주신다면야, 저도 한번 해 볼까 합니다."], ["Would you do me the favour, Ma’am,”", "부인, 제게 부탁 하나 들어주시겠습니까,\""], ["said the Captain, torn to pieces by his conscience, “to accept a quarter’s rent ahead?”", "선장이 양심에 갈가리 찢긴 채 말했다, \"한 분기 치 방세를 미리 받아 주시겠는지요?\""]], [["“And why so, Cap’en Cuttle?”", "\"아니, 어째서요, 커틀 선장님?\""], ["retorted Mrs MacStinger—sharply, as the Captain thought.", "맥스팅어 부인이—선장이 느끼기엔 날카롭게—되받았다."]], [["The Captain was frightened to dead “If you would Ma’am,”", "선장은 사색이 되도록 겁먹었다. \"그래 주신다면요, 부인,\""], ["he said with submission, “it would oblige me.", "그가 고분고분 말했다, \"제겐 큰 신세지요."], ["I can’t keep my money very well.", "제가 돈을 잘 간수를 못 해서요."], ["It pays itself out.", "자꾸 새어 나가 버린단 말입니다."], ["I should take it kind if you’d comply.”", "들어주신다면 고맙겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well, Cap’en Cuttle,”", "\"그럼요, 커틀 선장님,\""], ["said the unconscious MacStinger, rubbing her hands, “you can do as you please.", "영문도 모르는 맥스팅어 부인이 두 손을 비비며 말했다, \"선장님 좋으실 대로 하세요."], ["It’s not for me, with my family, to refuse, no more than it is to ask.”", "식구가 딸린 저로서야, 청하는 것도 그렇지만, 마다할 일도 아니지요.\""]], [["“And would you, Ma’am,”", "\"그리고 부인,\""], ["said the Captain, taking down the tin canister in which he kept his cash, from the top shelf of the cupboard, “be so good as offer eighteen-pence a-piece to the little family all round?", "선장이 찬장 맨 위 선반에서 돈을 보관해 두는 양철통을 내리며 말했다, \"저 딸린 식구들한테 골고루 한 사람당 일 실링 육 펜스씩 나눠 주는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠습니까?"], ["If you could make it convenient, Ma’am, to pass the word presently for them children to come for’ard, in a body, I should be glad to see ’em.”", "형편이 되신다면, 부인, 지금 그 애들한테 다 같이 앞으로 나오라고 말씀을 전해 주시면, 제가 그 애들을 보면 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["These innocent MacStingers were so many daggers to the Captain’s breast, when they appeared in a swarm, and tore at him with the confiding trustfulness he so little deserved.", "이 천진한 맥스팅어네 아이들이 떼 지어 나타나, 자기가 그토록 받을 자격 없는 그 믿음과 신뢰를 다해 그에게 매달리자, 그들은 선장의 가슴을 찌르는 숱한 비수가 되었다."], ["The eye of Alexander MacStinger, who had been his favourite, was insupportable to the Captain; the voice of Juliana MacStinger, who was the picture of her mother, made a coward of him.", "자기가 가장 아끼던 알렉산더 맥스팅어의 눈은 선장으로서는 차마 견딜 수 없었고, 제 어미를 빼다 박은 줄리아나 맥스팅어의 목소리는 그를 비겁자로 만들었다."]], [["Captain Cuttle kept up appearances, nevertheless, tolerably well, and for an hour or two was very hardly used and roughly handled by the young MacStingers: who in their childish frolics, did a little damage also to the glazed hat, by sitting in it, two at a time, as in a nest, and drumming on the inside of the crown with their shoes.", "그래도 커틀 선장은 그런대로 잘 겉모습을 꾸몄고, 한두 시간 동안 어린 맥스팅어네 아이들에게 몹시 시달리며 거칠게 다루어졌다. 그 아이들은 어린애다운 장난으로 번들거리는 모자에도 약간 손상을 입혔으니, 둥지 속에 들어앉듯 한 번에 둘씩 그 안에 들어앉아, 모자 꼭대기 안쪽을 신발로 둥둥 두드려 댄 것이다."], ["At length the Captain sorrowfully dismissed them: taking leave of these cherubs with the poignant remorse and grief of a man who was going to execution.", "마침내 선장은 슬픔에 잠겨 아이들을 물러가게 했으니, 처형장으로 끌려가는 사람 같은 사무치는 회한과 비탄으로 이 천사 같은 아이들에게 작별을 고했다."]], [["In the silence of night, the Captain packed up his heavier property in a chest, which he locked, intending to leave it there, in all probability for ever, but on the forlorn chance of one day finding a man sufficiently bold and desperate to come and ask for it.", "밤의 적막 속에서, 선장은 무거운 짐들을 궤짝에 챙겨 넣고 자물쇠를 채웠는데, 십중팔구 영영 거기 놔두고 갈 작정이었으나, 언젠가 그것을 찾으러 와서 달라고 할 만큼 대담하고 무모한 사람이 나타날지 모른다는 가망 없는 요행에 기대 둔 것이었다."], ["Of his lighter necessaries, the Captain made a bundle; and disposed his plate about his person, ready for flight.", "가벼운 필수품들로는 선장이 보따리를 하나 꾸렸고, 도주에 대비해 자기 식기를 몸에 챙겨 둘렀다."], ["At the hour of midnight, when Brig Place was buried in slumber, and Mrs MacStinger was lulled in sweet oblivion, with her infants around her, the guilty Captain, stealing down on tiptoe, in the dark, opened the door, closed it softly after him, and took to his heels.", "자정 무렵, 브리그 플레이스가 잠 속에 파묻히고 맥스팅어 부인이 아이들에게 둘러싸여 달콤한 망각 속에 잠들었을 때, 죄지은 선장은 어둠 속에서 발끝으로 살금살금 내려가, 문을 열고, 등 뒤로 살며시 닫고는, 줄행랑을 쳤다."]], [["Pursued by the image of Mrs MacStinger springing out of bed, and, regardless of costume, following and bringing him back; pursued also by a consciousness of his enormous crime; Captain Cuttle held on at a great pace, and allowed no grass to grow under his feet, between Brig Place and the Instrument-maker’s door.", "맥스팅어 부인이 침대에서 벌떡 일어나, 옷차림 따위 아랑곳없이 뒤쫓아 와 그를 도로 끌고 가는 형상에 쫓기고, 또 자기가 저지른 엄청난 죄의 의식에도 쫓기며, 커틀 선장은 빠른 걸음을 늦추지 않고, 브리그 플레이스와 기구 제작자의 문 사이에서 발밑에 풀 한 포기 자랄 틈도 주지 않았다."], ["It opened when he knocked—for Rob was on the watch—and when it was bolted and locked behind him, Captain Cuttle felt comparatively safe.", "그가 노크하자 문이 열렸으니—로브가 망을 보고 있었던 것이다—그리고 등 뒤로 문에 빗장이 걸리고 자물쇠가 채워지자, 커틀 선장은 비교적 안전하다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Whew!”", "\"휴!\""], ["cried the Captain, looking round him.", "선장이 주위를 둘러보며 외쳤다."], ["“It’s a breather!”", "\"한숨 돌리겠구나!\""]], [["“Nothing the matter, is there, Captain?”", "\"별일 없으신 거죠, 선장님?\""], ["cried the gaping Rob.", "입을 헤벌린 로브가 외쳤다."]], [["“No, no!”", "\"없다, 없어!\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, after changing colour, and listening to a passing footstep in the street.", "커틀 선장이 안색이 변했다가, 거리에서 지나가는 발소리에 귀를 기울인 끝에 말했다. \"하지만 잘 들어 둬라, 얘야."], ["“But mind ye, my lad; if any lady, except either of them two as you see t’other day, ever comes and asks for Cap’en Cuttle, be sure to report no person of that name known, nor never heard of here; observe them orders, will you?”", "만약 어떤 부인이든—네가 요전 날 본 그 두 사람을 빼고 말이다—와서 커틀 선장을 찾거든, 그런 이름을 가진 사람은 알지도 못하고, 여기서 들어 본 적도 없다고 꼭 그렇게 둘러대라. 그 명령을 잘 지키겠지, 알겠냐?\""]], [["“I’ll take care, Captain,”", "\"명심하겠습니다, 선장님,\""], ["returned Rob.", "로브가 대답했다."]], [["“You might say—if you liked,”", "\"네가 원한다면 이렇게 말해도 좋다,\""], ["hesitated the Captain, “that you’d read in the paper that a Cap’en of that name was gone to Australia, emigrating, along with a whole ship’s complement of people as had all swore never to come back no more.”", "선장이 머뭇거리며 말했다, \"그런 이름의 선장이 호주로, 다시는 돌아오지 않겠다고 다들 맹세한 한 배 가득한 사람들과 함께 이민을 떠났다고 신문에서 읽었다고 말이다.\""]], [["Rob nodded his understanding of these instructions; and Captain Cuttle promising to make a man of him, if he obeyed orders, dismissed him, yawning, to his bed under the counter, and went aloft to the chamber of Solomon Gills.", "로브는 이 지시를 알아들었다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였다. 그리고 선장은 명령을 잘 따르면 그를 어엿한 사내로 만들어 주겠노라 약속한 뒤, 하품을 하는 그를 카운터 밑 잠자리로 물러가게 하고, 자기는 솔로몬 길스의 방으로 올라갔다."]], [["What the Captain suffered next day, whenever a bonnet passed, or how often he darted out of the shop to elude imaginary MacStingers, and sought safety in the attic, cannot be told.", "이튿날 보닛이 지나갈 때마다 선장이 얼마나 시달렸는지, 혹은 상상 속의 맥스팅어들을 피하려고 얼마나 자주 가게 밖으로 뛰쳐나가 다락에서 안전을 구했는지는 이루 다 말할 수가 없다."], ["But to avoid the fatigues attendant on this means of self-preservation, the Captain curtained the glass door of communication between the shop and parlour, on the inside; fitted a key to it from the bunch that had been sent to him; and cut a small hole of espial in the wall.", "그러나 이런 자기 보호 수단에 따르는 고단함을 피하고자, 선장은 가게와 응접실 사이의 유리문에 안쪽으로 커튼을 쳤다. 그리고 자기에게 보내진 꾸러미 속 열쇠로 그 문에 맞는 열쇠 하나를 골라 끼우고, 벽에 작은 정찰용 구멍을 뚫었다."], ["The advantage of this fortification is obvious.", "이 요새화의 이점은 명백하다."], ["On a bonnet appearing, the Captain instantly slipped into his garrison, locked himself up, and took a secret observation of the enemy.", "보닛이 나타나면 선장은 즉각 자기 요새로 미끄러져 들어가, 안에서 문을 잠그고, 적을 몰래 살폈다."], ["Finding it a false alarm, the Captain instantly slipped out again.", "거짓 경보임을 알게 되면 선장은 즉각 다시 미끄러져 나왔다."], ["And the bonnets in the street were so very numerous, and alarms were so inseparable from their appearance, that the Captain was almost incessantly slipping in and out all day long.", "그런데 거리의 보닛이 어찌나 많았던지, 그리고 그것이 나타날 때마다 경보가 어찌나 따라붙던지, 선장은 하루 종일 거의 쉴 새 없이 들락날락하는 형편이었다."]], [["Captain Cuttle found time, however, in the midst of this fatiguing service to inspect the stock; in connexion with which he had the general idea (very laborious to Rob) that too much friction could not be bestowed upon it, and that it could not be made too bright.", "그래도 커틀 선장은 이 고단한 임무 와중에 짬을 내어 재고를 점검했는데, 그와 관련해서 그는 (로브에게는 몹시 고된 일이었으나) 아무리 문질러 닦아도 지나치지 않고, 아무리 반짝반짝하게 만들어도 모자란다는 식의 전반적인 생각을 품고 있었다."], ["He also ticketed a few attractive-looking articles at a venture, at prices ranging from ten shillings to fifty pounds, and exposed them in the window to the great astonishment of the public.", "또한 그는 그럴듯해 보이는 물건 몇 가지에 운수 삼아 십 실링에서 오십 파운드에 이르는 값을 매겨 가격표를 붙이고는, 진열창에 내놓아 사람들을 크게 놀라게 했다."]], [["After effecting these improvements, Captain Cuttle, surrounded by the instruments, began to feel scientific: and looked up at the stars at night, through the skylight, when he was smoking his pipe in the little back parlour before going to bed, as if he had established a kind of property in them.", "이런 개선을 이뤄 낸 뒤, 커틀 선장은 기구들에 둘러싸인 채 학구적인 기분이 들기 시작했다. 그래서 잠자리에 들기 전 작은 뒷방에서 파이프 담배를 피우며 밤이면 천창 너머로 별을 올려다보았는데, 마치 그것들에 대해 일종의 소유권이라도 세워 둔 듯한 태도였다."], ["As a tradesman in the City, too, he began to have an interest in the Lord Mayor, and the Sheriffs, and in Public Companies; and felt bound to read the quotations of the Funds every day, though he was unable to make out, on any principle of navigation, what the figures meant, and could have very well dispensed with the fractions.", "또 시내의 상인으로서, 그는 시장과 주 장관들과 공기업들에 관심을 갖기 시작했다. 그리고 항해술의 어떤 원리로도 그 숫자들이 무엇을 뜻하는지 도무지 알아낼 수 없었고, 분수 따위는 없는 편이 차라리 나았겠지만, 날마다 채권 시세를 읽어야 한다는 의무감을 느꼈다."], ["Florence, the Captain waited on, with his strange news of Uncle Sol, immediately after taking possession of the Midshipman; but she was away from home.", "플로렌스에게는, 미드십맨을 차지하자마자 곧바로, 선장이 솔 삼촌에 관한 이상한 소식을 들고 찾아갔다. 그러나 그녀는 집에 없었다."], ["So the Captain sat himself down in his altered station of life, with no company but Rob the Grinder; and losing count of time, as men do when great changes come upon them, thought musingly of Walter, and of Solomon Gills, and even of Mrs MacStinger herself, as among the things that had been.", "그래서 선장은 로브 더 그라인더 말고는 아무 벗도 없이, 바뀐 삶의 처지에 주저앉았다. 그리고 큰 변화가 닥칠 때 사람들이 으레 그러하듯 시간 가는 줄을 잊고서, 월터와 솔로몬 길스를, 심지어 맥스팅어 부인까지도, 모두 흘러간 옛일 가운데 하나인 양 골똘히 떠올렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVI. Shadows of the Past and Future", "# 제21장"]], [["Your most obedient, Sir,”", "“삼가 인사 올립니다, 선생.\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“Damme, Sir, a friend of my friend Dombey’s is a friend of mine, and I’m glad to see you!”", "\"이런, 선생, 내 친구 돔비의 친구라면 곧 내 친구이지요. 만나 뵙게 되어 반갑소이다!\""]], [["“I am infinitely obliged, Carker,”", "\"카커, 나는 한없이 고맙게 여기고 있네,\""], ["explained Mr Dombey, “to Major Bagstock, for his company and conversation.", "돔비 씨가 설명했다, \"배그스톡 소령께서 함께해 주시고 말벗이 되어 주신 데 대해서 말일세."], ["Major Bagstock has rendered me great service, Carker.”", "배그스톡 소령은 내게 큰 도움을 주셨어, 카커.\""]], [["Mr Carker the Manager, hat in hand, just arrived at Leamington, and just introduced to the Major, showed the Major his whole double range of teeth, and trusted he might take the liberty of thanking him with all his heart for having effected so great an Improvement in Mr Dombey’s looks and spirits.", "지배인 카커 씨는 막 리밍턴에 도착하여 막 소령에게 소개를 받은 참이었는데, 모자를 손에 든 채 소령에게 자신의 두 줄로 늘어선 치아를 남김없이 드러내 보이며, 돔비 씨의 안색과 기분을 이토록 크게 호전시켜 주신 데 대하여 진심으로 감사를 드리는 무례를 허락해 주시기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["“By Gad, Sir,”", "\"맙소사, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, in reply, “there are no thanks due to me, for it’s a give and take affair.", "소령이 대답했다, \"내게 감사할 것은 없소이다. 이건 주고받는 일이니까."], ["A great creature like our friend Dombey, Sir,”", "우리 친구 돔비처럼 위대한 인물이라면, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, lowering his voice, but not lowering it so much as to render it inaudible to that gentleman, “cannot help improving and exalting his friends.", "소령은 목소리를 낮추었으나, 그 신사에게 들리지 않을 만큼 낮추지는 않으면서 말했다, \"친구들을 향상시키고 드높여 주지 않을 수 없는 법이오."], ["He strengthens and invigorates a man, Sir, does Dombey, in his moral nature.”", "돔비는 사람을 강하게 하고 활기차게 해 줍니다, 선생, 그 사람의 도덕적 본성에 있어서 말이오.\""]], [["Mr Carker snapped at the expression.", "카커 씨는 그 표현에 덥석 달려들었다."], ["In his moral nature. Exactly.", "그 사람의 도덕적 본성에 있어서."], ["The very words he had been on the point of suggesting.", "바로 그것이었다. 그가 막 제안하려던 바로 그 말이었다."]], [["“But when my friend Dombey, Sir,”", "\"하지만 내 친구 돔비가, 선생,\""], ["added the Major, “talks to you of Major Bagstock, I must crave leave to set him and you right.", "소령이 덧붙였다, \"선생에게 배그스톡 소령에 대해 이야기하거든, 그를 그리고 선생을 바로잡아 드리는 것을 허락받아야겠소이다."], ["He means plain Joe, Sir—Joey B.—Josh. Bagstock—Joseph—rough and tough Old J., Sir.", "그는 그저 평범한 조를 뜻하는 거요, 선생—조이 비—조시 배그스톡—조지프—거칠고 억센 늙은 제이, 선생."], ["At your service.”", "마음껏 부려 주시오.\""]], [["Mr Carker’s excessively friendly inclinations towards the Major, and Mr Carker’s admiration of his roughness, toughness, and plainness, gleamed out of every tooth in Mr Carker’s head.", "소령을 향한 카커 씨의 지나치도록 다정한 호의와, 소령의 거칠고 억세고 소탈함에 대한 카커 씨의 감탄이, 카커 씨 머릿속에 박힌 이 하나하나에서 번뜩였다."]], [["“And now, Sir,”", "\"자 그럼, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “you and Dombey have the devil’s own amount of business to talk over.”", "소령이 말했다, \"선생과 돔비는 의논할 일이 산더미처럼 쌓여 있겠구려.\""]], [["“By no means, Major,”", "\"천만의 말씀입니다, 소령,\""], ["observed Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\""], ["said the Major, defiantly, “I know better; a man of your mark—the Colossus of commerce—is not to be interrupted.", "소령이 단호하게 말했다, \"내가 더 잘 알지. 자네 같은 거물—상업계의 거인—을 방해해서는 안 되지."], ["Your moments are precious.", "자네의 시간은 귀중하니까."], ["We shall meet at dinner-time.", "우리는 만찬 시간에 만나기로 하세."], ["In the interval, old Joseph will be scarce.", "그동안 늙은 조지프는 모습을 감추겠네."], ["The dinner-hour is a sharp seven, Mr Carker.”", "만찬 시간은 정확히 일곱 시요, 카커 씨.\""]], [["With that, the Major, greatly swollen as to his face, withdrew; but immediately putting in his head at the door again, said:", "그 말과 함께, 얼굴이 잔뜩 부풀어 오른 소령은 물러갔다. 그러나 곧바로 문에 머리를 다시 들이밀고는 말했다."]], [["“I beg your pardon.", "\"실례하네."], ["Dombey, have you any message to ’em?”", "돔비, 그분들께 전할 말이라도 있는가?\""]], [["Mr Dombey in some embarrassment, and not without a glance at the courteous keeper of his business confidence, entrusted the Major with his compliments.", "돔비 씨는 다소 당황한 채, 자기 사업상의 비밀을 정중히 지켜 주는 사람을 흘끗 보지 않을 수 없으면서, 소령에게 자신의 안부 인사를 부탁했다."]], [["“By the Lord, Sir,”", "\"맹세코, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “you must make it something warmer than that, or old Joe will be far from welcome.”", "소령이 말했다, \"그보다는 좀 더 따뜻한 말로 해 주어야 하네. 안 그러면 늙은 조는 도무지 환영받지 못할 테니.\""]], [["“Regards then, if you will, Major,”", "\"그렇다면 안부 인사를, 소령께서 좋으시다면,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 응수했다."]], [["“Damme, Sir,”", "\"이런, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, shaking his shoulders and his great cheeks jocularly: “make it something warmer than that.”", "소령이 익살스럽게 어깨와 커다란 양 볼을 흔들며 말했다, \"그보다는 좀 더 따뜻하게 해 주게.\""]], [["“What you please, then, Major,”", "\"그렇다면 소령께서 좋으실 대로 하시지요,\""], ["observed Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Our friend is sly, Sir, sly, Sir, de-vilish sly,”", "\"우리 친구는 능청맞단 말이야, 선생, 능청맞아, 지독하게 능청맞아,\""], ["said the Major, staring round the door at Carker.", "소령이 문 너머로 카커를 빤히 쳐다보며 말했다."], ["“So is Bagstock.”", "\"배그스톡도 그렇지만.\""], ["But stopping in the midst of a chuckle, and drawing himself up to his full height, the Major solemnly exclaimed, as he struck himself on the chest, “Dombey!", "그러나 낄낄거리던 도중에 멈추고는, 몸을 한껏 곧추세우면서, 소령은 자기 가슴을 치며 엄숙하게 외쳤다, \"돔비! 나는 자네의 심정이 부럽네."], ["I envy your feelings.", "신의 가호가 있기를!\""], ["God bless you!” and withdrew.", "그러고는 물러갔다."]], [["“You must have found the gentleman a great resource,”", "\"그 신사분이 자네에게 큰 의지가 되었던 모양이군요,\""], ["said Carker, following him with his teeth.", "카커가 그를 이로 좇으며 말했다."]], [["“Very great indeed,”", "\"정말 크게 그랬다네,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“He has friends here, no doubt,”", "\"여기 친구들이 있는 게 틀림없겠지요,\""], ["pursued Carker.", "카커가 이었다."], ["“I perceive, from what he has said, that you go into society here.", "\"그분이 한 말로 미루어 보니, 자네가 여기서 사교 모임에 나가고 있다는 걸 알겠습니다."], ["Do you know,”", "아십니까,\""], ["smiling horribly, “I am so very glad that you go into society!”", "끔찍하게 미소 지으며, \"자네가 사교 모임에 나간다니 저는 정말이지 기쁩니다!\""]], [["Mr Dombey acknowledged this display of interest on the part of his second in command, by twirling his watch-chain, and slightly moving his head.", "돔비 씨는 자기 부관이 보인 이 관심의 표시를 시곗줄을 빙빙 돌리고 고개를 살짝 끄덕이는 것으로 받아들였다."]], [["“You were formed for society,”", "\"자네는 사교계를 위해 태어난 사람입니다,\""], ["said Carker.", "카커가 말했다."], ["“Of all the men I know, you are the best adapted, by nature and by position, for society.", "\"제가 아는 모든 사람 가운데, 자네야말로 천성으로 보나 지위로 보나 사교계에 가장 잘 어울리는 분이지요."], ["Do you know I have been frequently amazed that you should have held it at arm’s length so long!”", "아십니까, 저는 자네가 그토록 오랫동안 사교계를 멀찌감치 떼어 놓고 지냈다는 데 자주 놀라곤 했습니다!\""]], [["“I have had my reasons, Carker.", "\"내게는 그럴 이유가 있었네, 카커."], ["I have been alone, and indifferent to it.", "나는 혼자였고, 그런 데 무관심했지."], ["But you have great social qualifications yourself, and are the more likely to have been surprised.”", "하지만 자네야말로 사교적 자질이 대단하니, 그만큼 더 놀랄 만하지 않은가.\""]], [["“Oh! I!”", "\"오!"], ["returned the other, with ready self-disparagement.", "제가요!\" 상대가 선뜻 자기를 낮추며 응수했다."], ["“It’s quite another matter in the case of a man like me.", "\"저 같은 사람의 경우는 전혀 다른 문제입니다."], ["I don’t come into comparison with you.”", "저는 자네와 비교가 되지 않지요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey put his hand to his neckcloth, settled his chin in it, coughed, and stood looking at his faithful friend and servant for a few moments in silence.", "돔비 씨는 손을 크라바트에 가져가 그 안에 턱을 묻고는 기침을 하더니, 잠시 말없이 자기의 충실한 벗이자 종복을 바라보며 서 있었다."]], [["“I shall have the pleasure, Carker,”", "\"내가 기쁘게도 자네를, 카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey at length: making as if he swallowed something a little too large for his throat: “to present you to my—to the Major’s friends.", "마침내 돔비 씨가 목구멍에 다소 큰 것을 삼키는 시늉을 하며 말했다, \"내—그 소령의 친구분들께 소개해 줄 수 있을 걸세."], ["Highly agreeable people.”", "무척 유쾌한 분들이지.\""]], [["“Ladies among them, I presume?”", "\"그분들 가운데 부인들도 계시겠지요?\""], ["insinuated the smooth Manager.", "매끄러운 지배인이 슬쩍 떠보았다."]], [["“They are all—that is to say, they are both—ladies,”", "\"그분들은 다—그러니까, 두 분 다—부인들이라네,\""], ["replied Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대답했다."]], [["“Only two?”", "\"두 분뿐입니까?\""], ["smiled Carker.", "카커가 미소 지었다."]], [["“They are only two.", "\"두 분뿐일세."], ["I have confined my visits to their residence, and have made no other acquaintance here.”", "나는 그분들의 거처로만 방문을 한정했고, 여기서 다른 사람과는 사귀지 않았네.\""]], [["“Sisters, perhaps?”", "\"자매분일까요?\""], ["quoth Carker.", "카커가 물었다."]], [["“Mother and daughter,”", "\"모녀라네,\""], ["replied Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대답했다."]], [["As Mr Dombey dropped his eyes, and adjusted his neckcloth again, the smiling face of Mr Carker the Manager became in a moment, and without any stage of transition, transformed into a most intent and frowning face, scanning his closely, and with an ugly sneer.", "돔비 씨가 눈을 내리깔고 다시 크라바트를 매만지는 동안, 지배인 카커 씨의 미소 띤 얼굴은 순식간에, 아무런 중간 단계도 없이, 더없이 골똘하고 찌푸린 얼굴로 바뀌어, 추한 비웃음을 머금은 채 그를 빈틈없이 살폈다."], ["As Mr Dombey raised his eyes, it changed back, no less quickly, to its old expression, and showed him every gum of which it stood possessed.", "돔비 씨가 눈을 들자, 그 얼굴은 그에 못지않게 빠르게 본래의 표정으로 되돌아가, 그에게 자기가 가진 잇몸을 하나도 빠짐없이 드러내 보였다."]], [["“You are very kind,”", "\"무척 친절하십니다,\""], ["said Carker, “I shall be delighted to know them.", "카커가 말했다, \"그분들을 알게 되어 기쁘겠습니다."], ["Speaking of daughters, I have seen Miss Dombey.”", "따님 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 저는 돔비 양을 뵈었습니다.\""]], [["There was a sudden rush of blood to Mr Dombey’s face.", "돔비 씨의 얼굴로 별안간 피가 솟구쳤다."]], [["“I took the liberty of waiting on her,”", "\"제가 무례를 무릅쓰고 그분을 찾아갔습니다,\""], ["said Carker, “to inquire if she could charge me with any little commission.", "카커가 말했다, \"혹시 제게 자그마한 심부름이라도 맡기실 게 있는지 여쭤보려고요."], ["I am not so fortunate as to be the bearer of any but her—but her dear love.”", "다만 저는 그분의—그분의 다정한 안부 말고는 아무것도 전할 만큼 운이 좋지는 못했습니다.\""]], [["Wolf’s face that it was then, with even the hot tongue revealing itself through the stretched mouth, as the eyes encountered Mr Dombey’s!", "그때 그것은 늑대의 얼굴이었으니, 그 눈이 돔비 씨의 눈과 마주치자 벌어진 입을 통해 뜨거운 혀까지 드러내 보이는 것이었다!"]], [["“What business intelligence is there?”", "\"무슨 사업상의 소식이라도 있는가?\""], ["inquired the latter gentleman, after a silence, during which Mr Carker had produced some memoranda and other papers.", "후자의 신사가 한동안 침묵한 뒤에 물었다. 그동안 카커 씨는 몇 가지 비망록과 다른 서류들을 꺼내 놓았다."]], [["“There is very little,”", "\"별로 없습니다,\""], ["returned Carker.", "카커가 대답했다."], ["“Upon the whole we have not had our usual good fortune of late, but that is of little moment to you.", "\"전반적으로 요즘 들어 평소만큼 운이 따르지는 않았습니다만, 그건 자네에게 그리 대수로운 일은 아니지요."], ["At Lloyd’s, they give up the Son and Heir for lost.", "로이드사에서는 '아들이자 후계자호'를 침몰한 것으로 처리했습니다."], ["Well, she was insured, from her keel to her masthead.”", "뭐, 그 배는 용골에서 돛대 꼭대기까지 보험에 들어 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“Carker,”", "\"카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, taking a chair near him, “I cannot say that young man, Gay, ever impressed me favourably—”", "돔비 씨가 그의 곁에 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 말했다, \"그 게이라는 젊은이가 내게 좋은 인상을 준 적이 있다고는 말할 수 없네만—\""]], [["“Nor me,”", "\"제게도 그렇습니다,\""], ["interposed the Manager.", "지배인이 끼어들었다."]], [["“—But I wish,”", "\"—하지만 나는,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, without heeding the interruption, “he had never gone on board that ship.", "돔비 씨가 그 끼어듦에 아랑곳하지 않고 말했다, \"그가 그 배에 결코 오르지 않았더라면 하고 바라네."], ["I wish he had never been sent out.", "그가 결코 파견되지 않았더라면 하고 말일세.\""]], [["“It is a pity you didn’t say so, in good time, is it not?”", "\"진작에 그렇게 말씀하시지 않은 게 안타깝지요, 안 그렇습니까?\""], ["retorted Carker, coolly.", "카커가 차분하게 응수했다."], ["“However, I think it’s all for the best.", "\"하지만 저는 그게 다 잘된 일이라 생각합니다."], ["I really, think it’s all for the best.", "정말이지, 다 잘된 일이라 생각해요."], ["Did I mention that there was something like a little confidence between Miss Dombey and myself?”", "제가 말씀드렸던가요, 돔비 양과 저 사이에 자그마한 신뢰 같은 게 생겼다는 걸요?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, sternly.", "돔비 씨가 엄하게 말했다."]], [["“I have no doubt,”", "\"저는 의심치 않습니다,\""], ["returned Mr Carker, after an impressive pause, “that wherever Gay is, he is much better where he is, than at home here.", "카커 씨가 인상적으로 뜸을 들인 뒤에 응수했다, \"게이가 어디에 있든, 그가 있는 그곳이 이곳 본가보다는 훨씬 낫다는 것을요."], ["If I were, or could be, in your place, I should be satisfied of that.", "만일 제가 자네 입장이라면, 또 그럴 수 있다면, 저는 그 점에 만족할 것입니다."], ["I am quite satisfied of it myself.", "저 스스로는 그 점에 아주 만족합니다."], ["Miss Dombey is confiding and young—perhaps hardly proud enough, for your daughter—if she have a fault.", "돔비 양은 사람을 잘 믿고 어립니다—어쩌면 자네의 따님치고는 자존심이 그리 충분치 못하다고 할까요—흠을 잡자면 말이지요."], ["Not that that is much though, I am sure.", "하긴 그것도 별것 아닙니다만, 분명히요."], ["Will you check these balances with me?”", "이 잔액들을 저와 함께 대조해 보시겠습니까?\""]], [["Mr Dombey leaned back in his chair, instead of bending over the papers that were laid before him, and looked the Manager steadily in the face.", "돔비 씨는 자기 앞에 펼쳐진 서류 위로 몸을 굽히는 대신, 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 지배인의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다."], ["The Manager, with his eyelids slightly raised, affected to be glancing at his figures, and to await the leisure of his principal.", "지배인은 눈꺼풀을 살짝 치켜뜬 채, 자기 숫자들을 들여다보며 상관이 여유가 날 때까지 기다리는 척했다."], ["He showed that he affected this, as if from great delicacy, and with a design to spare Mr Dombey’s feelings; and the latter, as he looked at him, was cognizant of his intended consideration, and felt that but for it, this confidential Carker would have said a great deal more, which he, Mr Dombey, was too proud to ask for.", "그는 마치 대단히 섬세한 배려에서, 또 돔비 씨의 감정을 다치지 않게 하려는 의도에서 그러는 것처럼 그런 척하는 모습을 내보였다. 그리고 후자는, 그를 바라보면서, 그의 의도적인 배려를 알아차렸고, 그것만 아니었다면 이 비밀스러운 카커가 훨씬 더 많은 말을 했으리라는 것을, 그러나 돔비 씨 자신은 너무 자존심이 강해 차마 그것을 청하지 못하리라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["It was his way in business, often.", "사업에서 그것은 흔히 그의 방식이었다."], ["Little by little, Mr Dombey’s gaze relaxed, and his attention became diverted to the papers before him; but while busy with the occupation they afforded him, he frequently stopped, and looked at Mr Carker again.", "차츰차츰 돔비 씨의 시선이 누그러졌고, 그의 주의는 앞에 놓인 서류 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다. 그러나 그 서류가 안겨 준 일에 몰두하면서도, 그는 자주 멈추고는 다시 카커 씨를 바라보았다."], ["Whenever he did so, Mr Carker was demonstrative, as before, in his delicacy, and impressed it on his great chief more and more.", "그가 그럴 때마다, 카커 씨는 앞서처럼 자기의 섬세함을 한껏 드러내 보였고, 그것을 자기의 위대한 상관에게 점점 더 깊이 각인시켰다."]], [["While they were thus engaged; and under the skilful culture of the Manager, angry thoughts in reference to poor Florence brooded and bred in Mr Dombey’s breast, usurping the place of the cold dislike that generally reigned there; Major Bagstock, much admired by the old ladies of Leamington, and followed by the Native, carrying the usual amount of light baggage, straddled along the shady side of the way, to make a morning call on Mrs Skewton.", "이렇게 둘이 일에 몰두해 있는 동안, 그리고 지배인의 능숙한 가꿈 아래, 가엾은 플로렌스에 관한 노여운 생각들이 돔비 씨의 가슴속에서 들끓고 자라나, 평소 그곳을 지배하던 차가운 혐오의 자리를 빼앗아 가는 동안, 리밍턴의 노부인들에게 크게 흠모받는 배그스톡 소령은, 평소만큼의 가벼운 짐을 든 원주민을 거느린 채, 길의 그늘진 쪽을 따라 다리를 벌려 성큼성큼 걸으며, 스큐턴 부인에게 아침 방문을 하러 갔다."], ["It being midday when the Major reached the bower of Cleopatra, he had the good fortune to find his Princess on her usual sofa, languishing over a cup of coffee, with the room so darkened and shaded for her more luxurious repose, that Withers, who was in attendance on her, loomed like a phantom page.", "소령이 클레오파트라의 정자에 다다른 것이 정오 무렵이었던지라, 그는 운 좋게도 자기의 공주가 평소의 소파에 앉아, 커피 한 잔을 앞에 두고 나른히 늘어져 있는 것을 보게 되었다. 방은 그녀의 한층 호사스러운 휴식을 위해 어찌나 어둡고 그늘지게 해 놓았던지, 그녀의 시중을 들고 있던 위더스는 유령 같은 시동처럼 어렴풋이 보였다."]], [["“What insupportable creature is this, coming in?”", "\"들어오는 이 못 참을 인간은 누구지?\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “I cannot hear it.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"도저히 견딜 수가 없어."], ["Go away, whoever you are!”", "누구든 간에 저리 가!\""]], [["“You have not the heart to banish J. B., Ma’am!”", "\"제이 비를 내쫓을 만큼 매정하지는 않으시겠지요, 마님!\""], ["said the Major halting midway, to remonstrate, with his cane over his shoulder.", "소령이 길 한가운데서 멈추어, 지팡이를 어깨에 걸친 채 항변했다."]], [["“Oh it’s you, is it?", "\"오 당신이군요?"], ["On second thoughts, you may enter,”", "다시 생각해 보니, 들어와도 좋겠어요,\""], ["observed Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 말했다."]], [["The Major entered accordingly, and advancing to the sofa pressed her charming hand to his lips.", "소령은 그에 따라 들어가서는, 소파로 다가가 그녀의 매혹적인 손을 입술에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“Sit down,”", "\"앉으세요,\""], ["said Cleopatra, listlessly waving her fan, “a long way off.", "클레오파트라가 나른히 부채를 흔들며 말했다, \"멀찌감치요."], ["Don’t come too near me, for I am frightfully faint and sensitive this morning, and you smell of the Sun.", "너무 가까이 오지 말아요. 오늘 아침 나는 끔찍하게 기운이 없고 예민한데, 당신한테서는 햇볕 냄새가 나니까요."], ["You are absolutely tropical.”", "완전히 열대 같다고요.\""]], [["“By George, Ma’am,”", "\"이런, 마님,\""], ["said the Major, “the time has been when Joseph Bagstock has been grilled and blistered by the Sun; then time was, when he was forced, Ma’am, into such full blow, by high hothouse heat in the West Indies, that he was known as the Flower.", "소령이 말했다, \"조지프 배그스톡이 햇볕에 구워지고 부르터졌던 시절이 있었지요. 마님, 그가 서인도제도의 무더운 온실 같은 열기에 떠밀려 활짝 만개하던 시절도 있었답니다."], ["A man never heard of Bagstock, Ma’am, in those days; he heard of the Flower—the Flower of Ours.", "그래서 그는 '꽃'이라 불렸지요. 그 시절엔 아무도 배그스톡 이야기를 들어 보지 못했어요, 마님."], ["The Flower may have faded, more or less, Ma’am,”", "사람들은 '꽃' 이야기를 들었지요—우리 연대의 꽃 말이오."], ["observed the Major, dropping into a much nearer chair than had been indicated by his cruel Divinity, “but it is a tough plant yet, and constant as the evergreen.”", "그 꽃은 다소간 시들었을지도 모릅니다, 마님,\" 소령이 자기의 무자비한 여신이 가리킨 자리보다 훨씬 더 가까운 의자에 털썩 앉으며 말했다, \"하지만 여전히 질긴 식물이고, 상록수처럼 한결같지요.\""]], [["Here the Major, under cover of the dark room, shut up one eye, rolled his head like a Harlequin, and, in his great self-satisfaction, perhaps went nearer to the confines of apoplexy than he had ever gone before.", "여기서 소령은 어두운 방을 은폐물 삼아 한쪽 눈을 감고, 할리퀸처럼 머리를 굴리면서, 자기만족이 어찌나 컸던지 어쩌면 이제껏 가 본 어느 때보다도 졸중의 경계에 더 가까이 다가갔는지도 모른다."]], [["“Where is Mrs Granger?”", "\"그레인저 부인은 어디 있느냐?\""], ["inquired Cleopatra of her page.", "클레오파트라가 시동에게 물었다."]], [["Withers believed she was in her own room.", "위더스는 그녀가 자기 방에 있는 것 같다고 했다."]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋아,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다."], ["“Go away, and shut the door.", "\"저리 가서, 문을 닫아라."], ["I am engaged.”", "손님 중이니까.\""]], [["As Withers disappeared, Mrs Skewton turned her head languidly towards the Major, without otherwise moving, and asked him how his friend was.", "위더스가 사라지자, 스큐턴 부인은 달리 움직이지는 않은 채 나른히 소령 쪽으로 고개를 돌리고는, 그의 친구가 어떻게 지내는지 물었다."]], [["“Dombey, Ma’am,”", "\"돔비 말입니다, 마님,\""], ["returned the Major, with a facetious gurgling in his throat, “is as well as a man in his condition can be.", "소령이 목구멍에서 익살스럽게 그르렁거리며 응수했다, \"그 처지에 놓인 사람치고는 그만하면 됐지요."], ["His condition is a desperate one, Ma’am.", "그의 처지가 절박하답니다, 마님."], ["He is touched, is Dombey!", "그는 가슴을 찔렸어요, 돔비가 말입니다!"], ["Touched!”", "찔렸지요!\""], ["cried the Major.", "소령이 외쳤다."], ["“He is bayonetted through the body.”", "\"몸뚱이를 총검에 꿰뚫린 거랍니다.\""]], [["Cleopatra cast a sharp look at the Major, that contrasted forcibly with the affected drawl in which she presently said:", "클레오파트라는 소령에게 날카로운 눈길을 던졌는데, 그것은 그녀가 이내 짐짓 늘어뜨려 말한 말투와는 극명히 대조되었다."]], [["“Major Bagstock, although I know but little of the world,—nor can I really regret my experience, for I fear it is a false place, full of withering conventionalities: where Nature is but little regarded, and where the music of the heart, and the gushing of the soul, and all that sort of thing, which is so truly poetical, is seldom heard,—I cannot misunderstand your meaning.", "\"배그스톡 소령, 비록 내가 세상에 대해 아는 것이 거의 없고—내 그 경험을 정말이지 후회하지도 않아요, 세상이란 시들어 가는 인습들로 가득한 거짓된 곳이 아닌가 싶으니까요. 거기서는 자연이 거의 존중받지 못하고, 마음의 음악이며 영혼의 솟구침이며, 그토록 참되게 시적인 그 모든 것들이 좀처럼 들리지 않잖아요—그래도 나는 당신의 뜻을 오해할 수는 없어요."], ["There is an allusion to Edith—to my extremely dear child,”", "당신의 말 속에는 에디스에 대한—나의 더없이 사랑스러운 아이에 대한 암시가 있군요,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, tracing the outline of her eyebrows with her forefinger, “in your words, to which the tenderest of chords vibrates excessively.”", "스큐턴 부인이 집게손가락으로 자기 눈썹의 윤곽을 따라 그리며 말했다, \"그 말에 가장 여린 마음의 현이 몹시 떨리는군요.\""]], [["“Bluntness, Ma’am,”", "\"무뚝뚝함은, 마님,\""], ["returned the Major, “has ever been the characteristic of the Bagstock breed.", "소령이 응수했다, \"언제나 배그스톡 가문의 특성이었지요."], ["You are right.", "옳은 말씀이오."], ["Joe admits it.”", "조도 인정합니다.\""]], [["“And that allusion,”", "\"그리고 그 암시는,\""], ["pursued Cleopatra, “would involve one of the most—if not positively the most—touching, and thrilling, and sacred emotions of which our sadly-fallen nature is susceptible, I conceive.”", "클레오파트라가 이었다, \"우리의 슬프게 타락한 본성이 느낄 수 있는 가장—단정코 가장이라고는 못할지라도—가슴 저미고, 짜릿하며, 신성한 감정들 가운데 하나를 포함하는 것이라고 나는 생각해요.\""]], [["The Major laid his hand upon his lips, and wafted a kiss to Cleopatra, as if to identify the emotion in question.", "소령은 손을 입술에 갖다 대고는, 마치 문제의 그 감정이 무엇인지 확인이라도 하듯, 클레오파트라에게 입맞춤을 날려 보냈다."]], [["“I feel that I am weak.", "\"나는 내가 약하다는 걸 느껴요."], ["I feel that I am wanting in that energy, which should sustain a Mama: not to say a parent: on such a subject,”", "이런 문제에 있어서 엄마를—부모라고는 못 하더라도—지탱해 주어야 할 그 기력이 내게 부족하다는 걸 느낀답니다,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, trimming her lips with the laced edge of her pocket-handkerchief; “but I can hardly approach a topic so excessively momentous to my dearest Edith without a feeling of faintness.", "스큐턴 부인이 손수건의 레이스 가장자리로 입술을 다듬으며 말했다, \"하지만 나의 가장 소중한 에디스에게 그토록 지극히 중대한 화제에는, 아찔한 느낌 없이는 도무지 다가갈 수가 없군요."], ["Nevertheless, bad man, as you have boldly remarked upon it, and as it has occasioned me great anguish:”", "그렇지만, 못된 양반, 당신이 그것을 대담하게 입에 올린 데다, 그것이 내게 큰 고통을 안겨 주었으니,\""], ["Mrs Skewton touched her left side with her fan: “I will not shrink from my duty.”", "스큐턴 부인이 부채로 자기 왼쪽 옆구리를 짚었다, \"나는 내 의무에서 물러서지 않겠어요.\""]], [["The Major, under cover of the dimness, swelled, and swelled, and rolled his purple face about, and winked his lobster eye, until he fell into a fit of wheezing, which obliged him to rise and take a turn or two about the room, before his fair friend could proceed.", "소령은 어둑함을 은폐물 삼아, 부풀고 또 부풀어, 자줏빛 얼굴을 이리저리 굴리며, 가재 같은 눈을 끔벅거리다가, 끝내 쌕쌕거리는 발작에 빠져들었으니, 그의 어여쁜 벗이 말을 이어 가기 전에 일어나 방 안을 한두 바퀴 돌지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Mr Dombey,”", "\"돔비 씨는,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, when she at length resumed, “was obliging enough, now many weeks ago, to do us the honour of visiting us here; in company, my dear Major, with yourself.", "스큐턴 부인이 마침내 다시 입을 열며 말했다, \"이제 여러 주 전에, 친절하게도 여기 우리를 찾아 주시는 영광을 베푸셨지요. 나의 친애하는 소령, 당신과 함께 말이에요."], ["I acknowledge—let me be open—that it is my failing to be the creature of impulse, and to wear my heart as it were, outside.", "내 인정하겠어요—솔직히 털어놓겠어요—내 결점이 충동의 산물이라는 것을, 그리고 말하자면 마음을 바깥에 내놓고 다닌다는 것을 말이에요."], ["I know my failing full well.", "나는 내 결점을 아주 잘 알아요."], ["My enemy cannot know it better.", "내 적이라 한들 그보다 더 잘 알지는 못할 거예요."], ["But I am not penitent; I would rather not be frozen by the heartless world, and am content to bear this imputation justly.”", "하지만 나는 뉘우치지 않아요. 차라리 매정한 세상에 얼어붙지 않는 편이 낫고, 이런 비난을 마땅히 감수하는 데 만족한답니다.\""]], [["Mrs Skewton arranged her tucker, pinched her wiry throat to give it a soft surface, and went on, with great complacency.", "스큐턴 부인은 가슴 장식을 매만지고, 거칠고 마른 목을 꼬집어 부드러운 표면을 만들고는, 크게 흡족해하며 말을 이었다."]], [["“It gave me (my dearest Edith too, I am sure) infinite pleasure to receive Mr Dombey.", "\"돔비 씨를 맞이하는 것은 내게(나의 가장 소중한 에디스에게도, 분명히) 한없는 기쁨이었어요."], ["As a friend of yours, my dear Major, we were naturally disposed to be prepossessed in his favour; and I fancied that I observed an amount of Heart in Mr Dombey, that was excessively refreshing.”", "나의 친애하는 소령, 당신의 친구로서, 우리는 자연히 그분에게 호의를 품게 되었지요. 그리고 나는 돔비 씨에게서 어떤 마음의 깊이를 보았다고 여겼는데, 그것이 더없이 신선하게 느껴졌답니다.\""]], [["“There is devilish little heart in Dombey now, Ma’am,”", "\"이제 돔비에게 마음이라곤 지독하게 적게 남아 있지요, 마님,\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."]], [["“Wretched man!”", "\"가엾은 양반!\""], ["cried Mrs Skewton, looking at him languidly, “pray be silent.”", "스큐턴 부인이 나른히 그를 바라보며 외쳤다, \"제발 좀 잠자코 계세요.\""]], [["“J. B.", "\"제이 비는 입을 다물지요, 마님,\""], ["is dumb, Ma’am,” said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."]], [["“Mr Dombey,”", "\"돔비 씨는,\""], ["pursued Cleopatra, smoothing the rosy hue upon her cheeks, “accordingly repeated his visit; and possibly finding some attraction in the simplicity and primitiveness of our tastes—for there is always a charm in nature—it is so very sweet—became one of our little circle every evening.", "클레오파트라가 자기 볼의 장밋빛을 매만지며 이었다, \"그에 따라 다시 방문을 하셨지요. 그리고 어쩌면 우리 취향의 소박함과 꾸밈없음에 무언가 끌림을 느끼셨는지—자연에는 언제나 매력이 있으니까요."], ["Little did I think of the awful responsibility into which I plunged when I encouraged Mr Dombey—to”—", "정말이지 더없이 감미롭지요—매일 저녁 우리의 작은 모임의 일원이 되셨답니다. 내가 돔비 씨를 부추겨서—그분이\"—"]], [["“To beat up these quarters, Ma’am,”", "\"이곳에 진을 치도록 말이지요, 마님,\""], ["suggested Major Bagstock.", "배그스톡 소령이 거들었다."]], [["“Coarse person!”", "\"천박한 양반!\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “you anticipate my meaning, though in odious language.”", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"비록 가증스러운 표현이긴 하지만, 당신이 내 뜻을 앞질러 말하는군요.\""]], [["Here Mrs Skewton rested her elbow on the little table at her side, and suffering her wrist to droop in what she considered a graceful and becoming manner, dangled her fan to and fro, and lazily admired her hand while speaking.", "여기서 스큐턴 부인은 곁에 놓인 작은 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 자기가 우아하고 어울린다고 여기는 자세로 손목을 늘어뜨린 채, 부채를 이리저리 흔들며, 말을 하는 동안 나른히 자기 손을 감탄하듯 바라보았다."]], [["“The agony I have endured,”", "\"내가 견뎌 온 고뇌란,\""], ["she said mincingly, “as the truth has by degrees dawned upon me, has been too exceedingly terrific to dilate upon.", "그녀가 새침하게 말했다, \"진실이 차츰 내게 밝아 옴에 따라, 도저히 자세히 늘어놓을 수 없을 만큼 너무도 끔찍했어요."], ["My whole existence is bound up in my sweetest Edith; and to see her change from day to day—my beautiful pet, who has positively garnered up her heart since the death of that most delightful creature, Granger—is the most affecting thing in the world.”", "내 온 존재는 나의 더없이 사랑스러운 에디스에게 매여 있는데, 그 애가 나날이 변해 가는 것을 보는 것이—그토록 유쾌하던 그레인저가 죽은 뒤로 단정코 제 마음을 꼭꼭 닫아 둔 나의 아름다운 보배가 말이에요—세상에서 가장 가슴 아픈 일이랍니다.\""]], [["Mrs Skewton’s world was not a very trying one, if one might judge of it by the influence of its most affecting circumstance upon her; but this by the way.", "스큐턴 부인의 세상이란, 그 가장 가슴 아픈 사정이 그녀에게 미친 영향으로 미루어 판단한다면, 그리 견디기 힘든 세상은 아니었다. 하지만 그건 그렇다 치고."]], [["“Edith,”", "\"에디스는,\""], ["simpered Mrs Skewton, “who is the perfect pearl of my life, is said to resemble me.", "스큐턴 부인이 점잔 빼며 말했다, \"내 인생의 완벽한 진주인데, 나를 닮았다고들 해요."], ["I believe we are alike.”", "우리가 비슷하긴 한 것 같아요.\""]], [["“There is one man in the world who never will admit that anyone resembles you, Ma’am,”", "\"세상에는 그 누구도 마님을 닮았다고는 결코 인정하지 않을 한 사내가 있지요, 마님,\""], ["said the Major; “and that man’s name is Old Joe Bagstock.”", "소령이 말했다. \"그 사내의 이름은 늙은 조 배그스톡이랍니다.\""]], [["Cleopatra made as if she would brain the flatterer with her fan, but relenting, smiled upon him and proceeded:", "클레오파트라는 부채로 그 아첨꾼의 골통을 후려칠 듯이 굴었으나, 누그러져 그를 향해 미소 짓고는 말을 이었다."]], [["“If my charming girl inherits any advantages from me, wicked one!”: the Major was the wicked one: “she inherits also my foolish nature.", "\"내 매력적인 딸이 나에게서 어떤 장점들을 물려받았다면, 짓궂은 양반!\"—그 짓궂은 양반이란 소령이었다—\"그 애는 내 어리석은 천성도 물려받았어요."], ["She has great force of character—mine has been said to be immense, though I don’t believe it—but once moved, she is susceptible and sensitive to the last extent.", "그 애는 대단한 성격의 힘을 지녔는데—내 것은 어마어마하다고들 했지만, 나는 믿지 않아요—한번 마음이 흔들리면, 끝 간 데까지 예민하고 민감해진답니다."], ["What are my feelings when I see her pining!", "그 애가 야위어 가는 것을 볼 때 내 심정이 어떻겠어요!"], ["They destroy me.", "그건 나를 무너뜨려요.\""]], [["The Major advancing his double chin, and pursing up his blue lips into a soothing expression, affected the profoundest sympathy.", "소령은 이중 턱을 앞으로 내밀고, 푸른 입술을 달래는 표정으로 오므리며, 더없이 깊은 동정을 짐짓 꾸며 보였다."]], [["“The confidence,”", "\"우리 사이에 줄곧 있어 온 그 신뢰란,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “that has subsisted between us—the free development of soul, and openness of sentiment—is touching to think of.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"영혼의 자유로운 펼침과 감정의 솔직함이란—생각만 해도 가슴 뭉클하답니다."], ["We have been more like sisters than Mama and child.”", "우리는 엄마와 자식이라기보다는 자매에 가까웠으니까요.\""]], [["“J. B.“s own sentiment,” observed the Major, “expressed by J. B. fifty thousand times!”", "\"제이 비 자신의 심정이오,\" 소령이 말했다, \"제이 비가 오만 번도 더 표현한 그 심정이지요!\""]], [["“Do not interrupt, rude man!”", "\"끼어들지 말아요, 무례한 양반!\""], ["said Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 말했다."], ["“What are my feelings, then, when I find that there is one subject avoided by us!", "\"그렇다면 우리가 피하는 화제가 하나 있다는 걸 알게 됐을 때 내 심정이 어떻겠어요!"], ["That there is a what’s-his-name—a gulf—opened between us.", "우리 사이에 무어라더라—틈이—갈라져 벌어졌다는 걸 알게 됐을 때 말이에요."], ["That my own artless Edith is changed to me!", "나의 그 꾸밈없는 에디스가 내게 변해 버렸다는 걸 알게 됐을 때!"], ["They are of the most poignant description, of course.”", "그건 물론 가장 가슴 저미는 종류의 심정이지요.\""]], [["The Major left his chair, and took one nearer to the little table.", "소령은 자기 의자를 떠나, 작은 탁자에 더 가까운 의자로 옮겨 앉았다."]], [["“From day to day I see this, my dear Major,”", "\"나는 나날이 이것을 봐요, 나의 친애하는 소령,\" 스큐턴 부인이 이었다."], ["proceeded Mrs Skewton.", "\"나날이 이것을 느낀답니다."], ["“From day to day I feel this.", "시시각각 나는 그토록 괴로운 결과를 불러온 그 지나친 믿음과 신뢰를 두고 스스로를 책망해요."], ["From hour to hour I reproach myself for that excess of faith and trustfulness which has led to such distressing consequences; and almost from minute to minute, I hope that Mr Dombey may explain himself, and relieve the torture I undergo, which is extremely wearing.", "그리고 거의 시시각각, 나는 돔비 씨가 자기 마음을 밝혀, 내가 겪고 있는 이 고문을—정말이지 진을 빼는 이 고문을—덜어 주기를 바란답니다. 하지만 아무 일도 일어나지 않아요, 나의 친애하는 소령."], ["But nothing happens, my dear Major; I am the slave of remorse—take care of the coffee-cup: you are so very awkward—my darling Edith is an altered being; and I really don’t see what is to be done, or what good creature I can advise with.”", "나는 회한의 노예예요—커피 잔 좀 조심해요. 당신은 어쩜 그렇게 서툴까—나의 사랑스러운 에디스는 딴사람이 되어 버렸고, 나는 정말이지 무엇을 어찌해야 할지, 어떤 마음씨 좋은 이와 의논할 수 있을지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["Major Bagstock, encouraged perhaps by the softened and confidential tone into which Mrs Skewton, after several times lapsing into it for a moment, seemed now to have subsided for good, stretched out his hand across the little table, and said with a leer,", "배그스톡 소령은, 스큐턴 부인이 여러 차례 잠깐씩 빠져들었다가 이제는 아예 가라앉아 버린 듯한 그 누그러지고 은밀한 어조에 어쩌면 고무되어, 작은 탁자 너머로 손을 뻗으며, 곁눈질하면서 말했다,"]], [["“Advise with Joe, Ma’am.”", "\"조와 의논하시지요, 마님.\""]], [["“Then, you aggravating monster,”", "\"그렇다면, 이 약 올리는 괴물 같으니,\""], ["said Cleopatra, giving one hand to the Major, and tapping his knuckles with her fan, which she held in the other: “why don’t you talk to me?", "클레오파트라가 한 손을 소령에게 내주고, 다른 손에 든 부채로 그의 손가락 마디를 톡톡 치며 말했다, \"왜 내게 말을 안 해 주는 거예요?"], ["you know what I mean.", "내 뜻을 알면서."], ["Why don’t you tell me something to the purpose?”", "왜 뭔가 도움 될 만한 걸 말해 주지 않는 거냐고요?\""]], [["The Major laughed, and kissed the hand she had bestowed upon him, and laughed again immensely.", "소령은 웃으며, 그녀가 자기에게 내준 손에 입을 맞추고는, 또 한 번 엄청나게 웃었다."]], [["“Is there as much Heart in Mr Dombey as I gave him credit for?”", "\"돔비 씨에게는 내가 믿었던 만큼 마음이 있을까요?\""], ["languished Cleopatra tenderly.", "클레오파트라가 다정하게 나른한 목소리로 물었다."], ["“Do you think he is in earnest, my dear Major?", "\"그분이 진심이라고 생각하세요, 나의 친애하는 소령?"], ["Would you recommend his being spoken to, or his being left alone?", "그분에게 말을 건네 보라고 권하시겠어요, 아니면 그냥 내버려 두라고 하시겠어요?"], ["Now tell me, like a dear man, what would you advise.”", "자 이제, 친애하는 양반답게, 어떻게 하면 좋을지 말해 줘요.\""]], [["“Shall we marry him to Edith Granger, Ma’am?”", "\"그를 에디스 그레인저와 혼인시켜 드릴까요, 마님?\""], ["chuckled the Major, hoarsely.", "소령이 쉰 목소리로 낄낄거렸다."]], [["“Mysterious creature!”", "\"알 수 없는 양반!\""], ["returned Cleopatra, bringing her fan to bear upon the Major’s nose.", "클레오파트라가 부채를 소령의 코에 들이대며 응수했다."], ["“How can we marry him?”", "\"어떻게 우리가 그를 혼인시킨다는 거죠?\""]], [["“Shall we marry him to Edith Granger, Ma’am, I say?”", "\"그를 에디스 그레인저와 혼인시켜 드릴까요, 마님, 라고 했지요?\""], ["chuckled the Major again.", "소령이 다시 낄낄거렸다."]], [["Mrs Skewton returned no answer in words, but smiled upon the Major with so much archness and vivacity, that that gallant officer considering himself challenged, would have imprinted a kiss on her exceedingly red lips, but for her interposing the fan with a very winning and juvenile dexterity.", "스큐턴 부인은 말로는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 소령을 향해 어찌나 짓궂고 발랄하게 미소 지었던지, 그 용감한 장교는 자기가 도전을 받았다고 여기고는 그녀의 몹시 붉은 입술에 입맞춤을 찍으려 했을 터인데, 그녀가 자못 사랑스럽고도 젊은이다운 솜씨로 부채를 끼워 넣는 바람에 그러지 못했다."], ["It might have been in modesty; it might have been in apprehension of some danger to their bloom.", "그것은 정숙함에서였을 수도, 그 입술의 발그레함에 어떤 위험이 닥칠까 염려해서였을 수도 있다."]], [["“Dombey, Ma’am,”", "\"돔비는, 마님,\""], ["said the Major, “is a great catch.”", "소령이 말했다, \"대단한 신랑감이지요.\""]], [["“Oh, mercenary wretch!”", "\"오, 잇속만 챙기는 양반 같으니!\""], ["cried Cleopatra, with a little shriek, “I am shocked.”", "클레오파트라가 가볍게 비명을 지르며 외쳤다, \"나는 충격받았어요.\""]], [["“And Dombey, Ma’am,” pursued the Major, thrusting forward his head, and distending his eyes, “is in earnest.", "\"그리고 돔비는, 마님,\""], ["Joseph says it; Bagstock knows it; J. B.", "소령이 머리를 앞으로 쑥 내밀고 눈을 부릅뜨며 이었다, \"진심입니다."], ["keeps him to the mark. Leave Dombey to himself, Ma’am.", "조지프가 그리 말하고, 배그스톡이 그걸 알며, 제이 비가 그를 그 마음에서 벗어나지 못하게 붙들어 둘 거예요."], ["Dombey is safe, Ma’am.", "돔비는 그냥 내버려 두세요, 마님."], ["Do as you have done; do no more; and trust to J.", "돔비는 안전합니다, 마님."], ["B. for the end.”", "지금까지 해 오신 대로 하시되, 그 이상은 하지 마시고, 끝맺음은 제이 비에게 맡겨 두시구려.\""]], [["“You really think so, my dear Major?”", "\"정말 그렇게 생각하세요, 나의 친애하는 소령?\""], ["returned Cleopatra, who had eyed him very cautiously, and very searchingly, in spite of her listless bearing.", "클레오파트라가 응수했다. 그녀는 나른한 태도에도 불구하고 무척 신중하게, 무척 면밀하게 그를 살펴보고 있었다."]], [["“Sure of it, Ma’am,”", "\"확신합니다, 마님,\""], ["rejoined the Major.", "소령이 응수했다."], ["“Cleopatra the peerless, and her Antony Bagstock, will often speak of this, triumphantly, when sharing the elegance and wealth of Edith Dombey’s establishment.", "\"비할 데 없는 클레오파트라와 그녀의 안토니우스 배그스톡은, 에디스 돔비의 저택의 우아함과 부를 함께 누리면서, 이 일을 두고 의기양양하게 자주 이야기하게 될 거예요."], ["Dombey’s right-hand man, Ma’am,”", "돔비의 오른팔이, 마님,\""], ["said the Major, stopping abruptly in a chuckle, and becoming serious, “has arrived.”", "소령이 낄낄거리던 것을 별안간 멈추고 진지해지며 말했다, \"도착했답니다.\""]], [["“This morning?”", "\"오늘 아침에요?\""], ["said Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 말했다."]], [["“This morning, Ma’am,”", "\"오늘 아침입니다, 마님,\""], ["returned the Major.", "소령이 응수했다."], ["“And Dombey’s anxiety for his arrival, Ma’am, is to be referred—take J.", "\"그리고 그의 도착에 대한 돔비의 조바심은, 마님, 이렇게 봐야 합니다—제이 비의 말을 믿으세요."], ["B.“s word for this; for Joe is devilish sly”—the Major tapped his nose, and screwed up one of his eyes tight: which did not enhance his native beauty—“to his desire that what is in the wind should become known to him” without Dombey’s telling and consulting him.", "조는 지독하게 능청맞으니까요\"—소령은 자기 코를 톡톡 두드리고 한쪽 눈을 꽉 찡그렸는데, 그것이 그의 타고난 미모를 돋보이게 하지는 못했다—\"그것은, 바람결에 무슨 일이 일어나고 있는지를, 돔비가 굳이 말하거나 의논하지 않고도 그 오른팔이 알아주었으면 하는 그의 바람 때문이지요."], ["For Dombey is as proud, Ma’am,”", "왜냐하면 돔비는, 마님,\""], ["said the Major, “as Lucifer.”", "소령이 말했다, \"루시퍼만큼이나 오만하거든요.\""]], [["“A charming quality,”", "\"매력적인 자질이로군요,\""], ["lisped Mrs Skewton; “reminding one of dearest Edith.”", "스큐턴 부인이 혀짤배기소리로 말했다, \"더없이 소중한 에디스를 떠올리게 하네요.\""]], [["“Well, Ma’am,”", "\"자 그럼, 마님,\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“I have thrown out hints already, and the right-hand man understands ’em; and I’ll throw out more, before the day is done.", "\"저는 이미 운을 떼어 두었고, 그 오른팔은 그것을 알아듣지요. 그리고 오늘 하루가 가기 전에 더 많은 운을 떼어 둘 거예요."], ["Dombey projected this morning a ride to Warwick Castle, and to Kenilworth, to-morrow, to be preceded by a breakfast with us.", "돔비는 오늘 아침, 내일 워릭 성과 케닐워스로 마차 나들이를 가는 일을 계획했는데, 그에 앞서 우리와 아침 식사를 함께하자는 거랍니다."], ["I undertook the delivery of this invitation.", "제가 이 초대를 전하는 일을 맡았지요."], ["Will you honour us so far, Ma’am?”", "그 정도로 저희에게 영광을 베풀어 주시겠습니까, 마님?\""], ["said the Major, swelling with shortness of breath and slyness, as he produced a note, addressed to the Honourable Mrs Skewton, by favour of Major Bagstock, wherein hers ever faithfully, Paul Dombey, besought her and her amiable and accomplished daughter to consent to the proposed excursion; and in a postscript unto which, the same ever faithfully Paul Dombey entreated to be recalled to the remembrance of Mrs Granger.", "소령이 가쁜 숨과 능청맞음으로 잔뜩 부풀어 오르며 말하고는, 명예로운 스큐턴 부인 앞으로 배그스톡 소령 편에 보낸다는 쪽지 하나를 꺼냈다. 거기에는 영원히 충실한 폴 돔비가, 그녀와 그녀의 상냥하고 교양 있는 따님이 제안된 그 나들이에 응해 주시기를 간청하고 있었으며, 추신에는 같은 영원히 충실한 폴 돔비가, 그레인저 부인의 기억에 자기를 되살려 주시기를 간곡히 청한다고 적혀 있었다."]], [["“Hush!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["said Cleopatra, suddenly, “Edith!”", "클레오파트라가 별안간 말했다, \"에디스!\""]], [["The loving mother can scarcely be described as resuming her insipid and affected air when she made this exclamation; for she had never cast it off; nor was it likely that she ever would or could, in any other place than in the grave.", "이 다정한 어머니가 이렇게 외칠 때 그 무미하고 꾸민 태도를 다시 갖추었다고는 거의 말할 수 없으니, 그녀는 그것을 단 한 번도 벗어 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 또 그녀가 무덤 말고 다른 어떤 곳에서 그것을 벗으려 하거나 벗을 수 있으리라고도 보기 어려웠다."], ["But hurriedly dismissing whatever shadow of earnestness, or faint confession of a purpose, laudable or wicked, that her face, or voice, or manner: had, for the moment, betrayed, she lounged upon the couch, her most insipid and most languid self again, as Edith entered the room.", "그러나 잠시 그녀의 얼굴이나 목소리나 태도가 드러냈던 진지함의 그림자나, 가상하든 사악하든 어떤 목적의 희미한 고백을, 그녀는 황급히 떨쳐 버리고는, 에디스가 방에 들어설 때 다시금 더없이 무미하고 더없이 나른한 자기 자신이 되어 긴 의자에 늘어졌다."]], [["Edith, so beautiful and stately, but so cold and so repelling.", "에디스, 그토록 아름답고 위엄 있으면서도, 그토록 차갑고 그토록 사람을 밀어내는."], ["Who, slightly acknowledging the presence of Major Bagstock, and directing a keen glance at her mother, drew back from a window, and sat down there, looking out.", "그녀는 배그스톡 소령의 존재를 가볍게 알은체하고, 어머니에게 날카로운 눈길을 던지더니, 창가에서 물러나 그곳에 앉아 바깥을 내다보았다."]], [["“My dearest Edith,”", "\"나의 더없이 소중한 에디스,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “where on earth have you been?", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"대체 어디 있었던 거니?"], ["I have wanted you, my love, most sadly.”", "얘야, 네가 정말 몹시도 보고 싶었단다.\""]], [["“You said you were engaged, and I stayed away,”", "\"손님 중이라고 하셔서, 비켜 있었어요,\""], ["she answered, without turning her head.", "그녀가 고개도 돌리지 않고 대답했다."]], [["“It was cruel to Old Joe, Ma’am,”", "\"그건 늙은 조에게 매정한 일이었지요, 마님,\""], ["said the Major in his gallantry.", "소령이 정중하게 말했다."]], [["“It was very cruel, I know,”", "\"매정했지요, 알아요,\""], ["she said, still looking out—and said with such calm disdain, that the Major was discomfited, and could think of nothing in reply.", "그녀가 여전히 바깥을 내다보며 말했다—그것도 어찌나 차분한 경멸을 담아 말했던지, 소령은 무안해져 무어라 응수할 말을 도무지 떠올리지 못했다."]], [["“Major Bagstock, my darling Edith,”", "\"배그스톡 소령은, 나의 사랑스러운 에디스,\""], ["drawled her mother, “who is generally the most useless and disagreeable creature in the world: as you know—”", "그녀의 어머니가 늘어지게 말했다, \"흔히는 세상에서 가장 쓸모없고 못마땅한 인간이지만, 너도 알다시피—\""]], [["“It is surely not worthwhile, Mama,”", "\"그런 말투를 차릴 필요는 분명 없잖아요, 엄마,\""], ["said Edith, looking round, “to observe these forms of speech.", "에디스가 돌아보며 말했다."], ["We are quite alone.", "\"우리뿐인걸요."], ["We know each other.”", "우린 서로를 알고요.\""]], [["The quiet scorn that sat upon her handsome face—a scorn that evidently lighted on herself, no less than them—was so intense and deep, that her mother’s simper, for the instant, though of a hardy constitution, drooped before it.", "그녀의 아름다운 얼굴에 깃든 그 조용한 경멸은—그들 못지않게 자기 자신에게도 분명히 내려앉는 경멸이었으니—어찌나 강렬하고 깊었던지, 그녀 어머니의 점잔 빼는 미소가, 비록 끈질긴 체질의 것이었음에도, 그 순간만은 그 앞에서 풀이 죽었다."]], [["“My darling girl,”", "\"나의 사랑스러운 딸아,\""], ["she began again.", "그녀가 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“Not woman yet?”", "\"아직 여자는 아니고요?\""], ["said Edith, with a smile.", "에디스가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“How very odd you are today, my dear!", "\"오늘따라 너 참 이상하구나, 얘야!"], ["Pray let me say, my love, that Major Bagstock has brought the kindest of notes from Mr Dombey, proposing that we should breakfast with him to-morrow, and ride to Warwick and Kenilworth.", "부디 한마디만 하게 해 다오, 얘야, 배그스톡 소령이 돔비 씨에게서 더없이 친절한 쪽지를 가져왔단다. 내일 그분과 아침 식사를 함께하고 워릭과 케닐워스로 마차 나들이를 가자는 제안이야."], ["Will you go, Edith?”", "가겠니, 에디스?\""]], [["“Will I go!”", "\"제가 가겠느냐고요!\""], ["she repeated, turning very red, and breathing quickly as she looked round at her mother.", "그녀가 얼굴이 새빨개지며, 어머니를 돌아보면서 가쁘게 숨을 쉬며 되뇌었다."]], [["“I knew you would, my own, observed the latter carelessly.", "\"갈 줄 알았다, 우리 아가,\" 후자가 무심하게 말했다."], ["“It is, as you say, quite a form to ask.", "\"네 말마따나, 묻는 건 순전히 형식이지."], ["Here is Mr Dombey’s letter, Edith.”", "여기 돔비 씨의 편지가 있다, 에디스.\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고마워요."], ["I have no desire to read it,”", "읽고 싶지 않은걸요,\""], ["was her answer.", "그녀의 대답이었다."]], [["“Then perhaps I had better answer it myself,”", "\"그럼 차라리 내가 직접 답장을 쓰는 게 낫겠구나,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “though I had thought of asking you to be my secretary, darling.”", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"너한테 내 비서가 되어 달라고 할까 했다만, 얘야.\""], ["As Edith made no movement, and no answer, Mrs Skewton begged the Major to wheel her little table nearer, and to set open the desk it contained, and to take out pen and paper for her; all which congenial offices of gallantry the Major discharged, with much submission and devotion.", "에디스가 아무런 움직임도, 아무런 대답도 하지 않자, 스큐턴 부인은 소령에게 자기의 작은 탁자를 더 가까이 밀어 달라고, 그 안에 든 책상을 펼쳐 달라고, 그리고 펜과 종이를 꺼내 달라고 청했다. 이 모든 마음 맞는 정중한 봉사를 소령은 자못 순순히, 헌신적으로 해냈다."]], [["“Your regards, Edith, my dear?”", "\"네 안부는 어찌할까, 에디스, 얘야?\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, pausing, pen in hand, at the postscript.", "스큐턴 부인이 추신 대목에서 펜을 든 채 멈추며 말했다."]], [["“What you will, Mama,”", "\"엄마 좋으실 대로요,\""], ["she answered, without turning her head, and with supreme indifference.", "그녀가 고개도 돌리지 않고, 더없이 무심하게 대답했다."]], [["Mrs Skewton wrote what she would, without seeking for any more explicit directions, and handed her letter to the Major, who receiving it as a precious charge, made a show of laying it near his heart, but was fain to put it in the pocket of his pantaloons on account of the insecurity of his waistcoat.", "스큐턴 부인은 더 분명한 지시를 구하지 않고 자기 좋을 대로 적은 다음, 편지를 소령에게 건넸다. 소령은 그것을 귀중한 물건처럼 받아 들고는, 가슴 가까이 갖다 대는 시늉을 했으나, 조끼가 미덥지 못한 탓에 어쩔 수 없이 바지 주머니에 넣었다."], ["The Major then took a very polished and chivalrous farewell of both ladies, which the elder one acknowledged in her usual manner, while the younger, sitting with her face addressed to the window, bent her head so slightly that it would have been a greater compliment to the Major to have made no sign at all, and to have left him to infer that he had not been heard or thought of.", "그런 다음 소령은 두 부인 모두에게 자못 세련되고 기사다운 작별 인사를 했는데, 손위 부인은 그것을 평소의 태도로 받아들였고, 손아래 부인은, 얼굴을 창 쪽으로 향한 채 앉아, 고개를 어찌나 살짝 숙였던지, 차라리 아무 표시도 하지 않고 소령으로 하여금 그가 들리지도 떠오르지도 않았다고 짐작하게 두는 편이 그에게는 더 큰 예의가 되었을 정도였다."]], [["“As to alteration in her, Sir,”", "\"그 여자의 변화로 말할 것 같으면, 선생,\" 소령이 돌아오는 길에 곰곰 생각했다."], ["mused the Major on his way back; on which expedition—the afternoon being sunny and hot—he ordered the Native and the light baggage to the front, and walked in the shadow of that expatriated prince: “as to alteration, Sir, and pining, and so forth, that won’t go down with Joseph Bagstock, None of that, Sir.", "이 여정에서—오후가 화창하고 무더운지라—소령은 원주민과 가벼운 짐을 앞세우고, 그 추방된 왕자의 그늘 속에서 걸었다. \"변화니, 야위어 간다느니 하는 따위로 말하자면, 그건 조지프 배그스톡에게는 안 통하지."], ["It won’t do here.", "그런 건 안 돼, 선생."], ["But as to there being something of a division between ’em—or a gulf as the mother calls it—damme, Sir, that seems true enough.", "여기서는 안 먹혀. 하지만 그 둘 사이에 무언가 갈라짐 같은 게—아니면 그 어미가 부르는 대로 틈 같은 게—있다는 건, 이런, 선생, 그건 충분히 사실로 보이는구먼."], ["And it’s odd enough!", "그리고 그것 참 묘하단 말이야!"], ["Well, Sir!”", "자 그럼, 선생!\""], ["panted the Major, “Edith Granger and Dombey are well matched; let ’em fight it out!", "소령이 헐떡이며 말했다, \"에디스 그레인저와 돔비는 잘 어울리는 짝이야. 둘이 한판 붙어 보라지!"], ["Bagstock backs the winner!”", "배그스톡은 이기는 쪽에 걸 테다!\""]], [["The Major, by saying these latter words aloud, in the vigour of his thoughts, caused the unhappy Native to stop, and turn round, in the belief that he was personally addressed.", "소령이 생각에 골몰한 나머지 이 마지막 말들을 큰 소리로 내뱉는 바람에, 가엾은 원주민은 자기더러 하는 말인 줄 알고 걸음을 멈추고 돌아섰다."], ["Exasperated to the last degree by this act of insubordination, the Major (though he was swelling with enjoyment of his own humour), at the moment of its occurrence instantly thrust his cane among the Native’s ribs, and continued to stir him up, at short intervals, all the way to the hotel.", "이 항명 행위에 극도로 격분한 소령은(비록 자기 익살을 즐기느라 잔뜩 부풀어 올라 있었음에도), 그 일이 벌어진 순간 즉시 지팡이를 원주민의 갈비뼈 사이에 들이밀고는, 호텔에 이르기까지 내내 짧은 간격을 두고 계속 그를 쑤셔 댔다."]], [["Nor was the Major less exasperated as he dressed for dinner, during which operation the dark servant underwent the pelting of a shower of miscellaneous objects, varying in size from a boot to a hairbrush, and including everything that came within his master’s reach.", "소령은 만찬을 위해 옷을 차려입는 동안에도 못지않게 격분해 있었으니, 그 작업이 진행되는 내내 그 검은 하인은 부츠에서 머리빗에 이르기까지 크기가 제각각인, 게다가 주인의 손이 닿는 곳에 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 닥치는 대로 던지는 잡다한 물건들의 우박을 고스란히 맞아야 했다."], ["For the Major plumed himself on having the Native in a perfect state of drill, and visited the least departure from strict discipline with this kind of fatigue duty.", "소령은 원주민을 완벽한 훈련 상태로 부린다는 데 자부심을 느꼈고, 엄격한 규율에서 조금이라도 벗어나는 일에는 이런 식의 고역 임무를 부과했던 것이다."], ["Add to this, that he maintained the Native about his person as a counter-irritant against the gout, and all other vexations, mental as well as bodily; and the Native would appear to have earned his pay—which was not large.", "게다가 그는 통풍을 비롯해, 육체적인 것은 물론 정신적인 것까지 온갖 골칫거리에 대한 발적제로 삼고자 원주민을 늘 곁에 두고 있었으니, 원주민은 자기 봉급을—그리 많지도 않은 봉급을—제값으로 번 셈이라 할 만했다."]], [["At length, the Major having disposed of all the missiles that were convenient to his hand, and having called the Native so many new names as must have given him great occasion to marvel at the resources of the English language, submitted to have his cravat put on; and being dressed, and finding himself in a brisk flow of spirits after this exercise, went downstairs to enliven “Dombey”", "마침내 소령은 손닿는 곳에 있던 던질 만한 것들을 죄다 동내고, 원주민에게 새로운 욕설을 어찌나 많이 퍼부었던지 그 원주민은 영어라는 언어의 자원이 그토록 풍부함에 크게 경탄할 만했으련만, 이윽고 크라바트를 매도록 순순히 몸을 맡겼다."], ["and his right-hand man.", "그리고 옷을 다 차려입고, 이 운동 뒤에 기분이 활기차게 솟아오름을 느끼고는, \"돔비\"와 그의 오른팔에게 흥을 돋우어 주러 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["Dombey was not yet in the room, but the right-hand man was there, and his dental treasures were, as usual, ready for the Major.", "돔비는 아직 방에 없었으나, 오른팔은 거기 있었고, 그의 치아라는 보물은 여느 때처럼 소령을 맞을 준비가 되어 있었다."]], [["“Well, Sir!”", "\"자, 선생!\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“How have you passed the time since I had the happiness of meeting you?", "\"선생을 뵙는 기쁨을 누린 이래로 어떻게 시간을 보내셨소?"], ["Have you walked at all?”", "산책이라도 좀 하셨소?\""]], [["“A saunter of barely half an hour’s duration,”", "\"고작 반 시간 남짓 거닐었지요,\""], ["returned Carker.", "카커가 응수했다."], ["“We have been so much occupied.”", "\"워낙 일이 많아서요.\""]], [["“Business, eh?”", "\"사업이오, 응?\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."]], [["“A variety of little matters necessary to be gone through,”", "\"꼭 거쳐야 할 자잘한 일들이 여러 가지 있었지요,\""], ["replied Carker.", "카커가 대답했다."], ["“But do you know—this is quite unusual with me, educated in a distrustful school, and who am not generally disposed to be communicative,”", "\"하지만 아십니까—이건 불신을 가르치는 학교에서 자란 데다 대체로 속마음을 잘 터놓지 않는 저로서는 자못 드문 일입니다만,\""], ["he said, breaking off, and speaking in a charming tone of frankness—“but I feel quite confidential with you, Major Bagstock.”", "그가 말을 끊고는, 매혹적인 솔직함의 어조로 말했다, \"소령께는 어쩐지 마음을 다 털어놓고 싶군요, 배그스톡 소령.\""]], [["“You do me honour, Sir,”", "\"제게 영광을 베푸시는구려, 선생,\""], ["returned the Major.", "소령이 응수했다."], ["“You may be.”", "\"그러셔도 좋소이다.\""]], [["“Do you know, then,”", "\"그러면 아십니까,\""], ["pursued Carker, “that I have not found my friend—our friend, I ought rather to call him—”", "카커가 이었다, \"제가 제 친구를—우리 친구라고 불러야겠지요—\""]], [["“Meaning Dombey, Sir?”", "\"돔비 말이오, 선생?\""], ["cried the Major.", "소령이 외쳤다."], ["“You see me, Mr Carker, standing here!", "\"여기 서 있는 저를 보십시오, 카커 씨!"], ["J. B.?”", "제이 비를?\""]], [["He was puffy enough to see, and blue enough; and Mr Carker intimated the he had that pleasure.", "그는 보기에 충분히 부어 있었고, 충분히 푸르렀다. 그래서 카커 씨는 그러한 기쁨을 누리고 있노라고 넌지시 일렀다."]], [["“Then you see a man, Sir, who would go through fire and water to serve Dombey,”", "\"그렇다면 선생은, 돔비를 섬기기 위해서라면 물불을 가리지 않을 사내를 보고 계신 거요,\""], ["returned Major Bagstock.", "배그스톡 소령이 응수했다."]], [["Mr Carker smiled, and said he was sure of it.", "카커 씨는 미소 지으며, 그 점을 확신한다고 말했다."], ["“Do you know, Major,”", "\"아십니까, 소령,\""], ["he proceeded: “to resume where I left off: that I have not found our friend so attentive to business today, as usual?”", "그가 이었다, \"하던 말을 다시 잇자면, 제가 오늘 우리 친구가 평소만큼 사업에 마음을 쓰지 않는다는 걸 알게 되었답니다.\""]], [["“No?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["observed the delighted Major.", "흡족해진 소령이 말했다."]], [["“I have found him a little abstracted, and with his attention disposed to wander,”", "\"그가 좀 정신이 딴 데 가 있고, 주의가 흩어지기 쉬운 상태인 걸 봤어요,\""], ["said Carker.", "카커가 말했다."]], [["“By Jove, Sir,”", "\"이런 맙소사, 선생,\""], ["cried the Major, “there’s a lady in the case.”", "소령이 외쳤다, \"거기엔 여자가 끼어 있소이다.\""]], [["“Indeed, I begin to believe there really is,”", "\"정말이지, 저도 실은 그런 게 아닌가 믿기 시작했습니다,\" 카커가 응수했다."], ["returned Carker; “I thought you might be jesting when you seemed to hint at it; for I know you military men”—", "\"소령께서 그걸 넌지시 비치시는 듯했을 때는 농담이신가 했지요. 군인 양반들이란 워낙\"—"]], [["The Major gave the horse’s cough, and shook his head and shoulders, as much as to say, “Well!", "소령은 말이 콜록거리는 듯한 그 기침 소리를 내고는, \"허! 우리야 한량들이지, 그건 부정할 수 없지\""], ["we are gay dogs, there’s no denying.”", "하는 듯이 머리와 어깨를 흔들었다."], ["He then seized Mr Carker by the button-hole, and with starting eyes whispered in his ear, that she was a woman of extraordinary charms, Sir.", "그러더니 카커 씨의 단춧구멍을 움켜잡고, 눈을 부릅뜬 채 그의 귀에 대고 속삭이기를, 그 여자는 비범한 매력을 지닌 여인이라고, 선생. 젊은 과부라고, 선생."], ["That she was a young widow, Sir.", "명문가 출신이라고, 선생."], ["That she was of a fine family, Sir.", "돔비가 그녀에게 머리끝까지 푹 빠져 있다고, 선생."], ["That Dombey was over head and ears in love with her, Sir, and that it would be a good match on both sides; for she had beauty, blood, and talent, and Dombey had fortune; and what more could any couple have?", "그리고 그것은 양쪽 모두에게 좋은 짝이 될 것이라고. 왜냐하면 그녀에게는 미모와 혈통과 재능이 있고, 돔비에게는 재산이 있으니, 어느 한 쌍인들 그 이상 무엇을 더 바라겠소?"], ["Hearing Mr Dombey’s footsteps without, the Major cut himself short by saying, that Mr Carker would see her tomorrow morning, and would judge for himself; and between his mental excitement, and the exertion of saying all this in wheezy whispers, the Major sat gurgling in the throat and watering at the eyes, until dinner was ready.", "돔비 씨의 발소리가 바깥에서 들려오자, 소령은 카커 씨가 내일 아침이면 그녀를 보고 스스로 판단하게 될 거라고 말하며 말을 뚝 끊었다. 그리고 정신적 흥분과 이 모든 말을 쌕쌕거리는 속삭임으로 해내느라 들인 애 사이에서, 소령은 만찬 준비가 될 때까지 목구멍을 그르렁거리고 눈에서 눈물을 글썽이며 앉아 있었다."]], [["The Major, like some other noble animals, exhibited himself to great advantage at feeding-time.", "소령은 다른 어떤 고귀한 짐승들이 그러하듯, 먹이 먹는 시간에 자기 모습을 한껏 유리하게 드러내 보였다."], ["On this occasion, he shone resplendent at one end of the table, supported by the milder lustre of Mr Dombey at the other; while Carker on one side lent his ray to either light, or suffered it to merge into both, as occasion arose.", "이번 자리에서, 그는 식탁 한쪽 끝에서 휘황하게 빛을 발했고, 다른 쪽 끝에서는 돔비 씨가 한결 부드러운 광채로 그를 뒷받침했다. 한편 카커는 한쪽에서 두 빛에 제 빛을 보태기도 하고, 경우에 따라 그것이 두 빛 모두에 녹아들도록 두기도 했다."]], [["During the first course or two, the Major was usually grave; for the Native, in obedience to general orders, secretly issued, collected every sauce and cruet round him, and gave him a great deal to do, in taking out the stoppers, and mixing up the contents in his plate.", "첫 두어 코스가 나오는 동안, 소령은 으레 근엄했다. 원주민이, 은밀히 내려진 일반 명령에 따라, 그의 주위에 온갖 소스와 양념병을 죄다 모아 놓아, 그에게 마개를 빼내고 그 내용물을 접시 위에서 뒤섞느라 할 일을 잔뜩 안겼기 때문이다."], ["Besides which, the Native had private zests and flavours on a side-table, with which the Major daily scorched himself; to say nothing of strange machines out of which he spirited unknown liquids into the Major’s drink.", "그뿐 아니라, 원주민은 곁탁자에 소령이 날마다 제 입을 데어 가며 즐기는 독특한 풍미와 양념들을 마련해 두었고, 거기에 더해 정체 모를 액체를 소령의 음료에 뿜어 넣는 묘한 기계들도 있었다."], ["But on this occasion, Major Bagstock, even amidst these many occupations, found time to be social; and his sociality consisted in excessive slyness for the behoof of Mr Carker, and the betrayal of Mr Dombey’s state of mind.", "그러나 이번 자리에서 배그스톡 소령은, 이 숱한 일거리 한가운데서도 사교적일 시간을 냈다. 그리고 그의 사교성이란, 카커 씨를 위해 지나치게 능청을 떨고 돔비 씨의 심리 상태를 폭로하는 것으로 이루어졌다."]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\""], ["said the Major, “you don’t eat; what’s the matter?”", "소령이 말했다, \"자네 안 먹는군. 무슨 일인가?\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙소,\""], ["returned the gentleman, “I am doing very well; I have no great appetite today.”", "그 신사가 응수했다, \"저는 아주 잘 지내고 있습니다. 오늘은 그리 입맛이 없군요.\""]], [["“Why, Dombey, what’s become of it?”", "\"아니, 돔비, 그게 어디로 갔나?\""], ["asked the Major.", "소령이 물었다."], ["“Where’s it gone?", "\"어디로 사라졌어?"], ["You haven’t left it with our friends, I’ll swear, for I can answer for their having none today at luncheon.", "우리 친구분들에게 두고 온 건 아닐 테지, 내 장담하건대. 오늘 점심에 그분들에게도 입맛이 없었다고 내가 보증할 수 있으니까."], ["I can answer for one of ’em, at least: I won’t say which.”", "적어도 그 가운데 한 분에 대해서는 보증할 수 있어. 누구라고는 말 안 하겠네만.\""]], [["Then the Major winked at Carker, and became so frightfully sly, that his dark attendant was obliged to pat him on the back, without orders, or he would probably have disappeared under the table.", "그러더니 소령은 카커에게 눈을 찡긋했고, 어찌나 무섭도록 능청맞아졌던지, 그의 검은 시중꾼이 명령도 없이 그의 등을 두드려 주지 않았다면 그는 아마 탁자 밑으로 사라져 버렸을 것이다."]], [["In a later stage of the dinner: that is to say, when the Native stood at the Major’s elbow ready to serve the first bottle of champagne: the Major became still slyer.", "만찬이 더 진행된 단계에서—그러니까 원주민이 첫 번째 샴페인 병을 따라 줄 채비를 하고 소령의 팔꿈치 곁에 섰을 때—소령은 한층 더 능청맞아졌다."]], [["“Fill this to the brim, you scoundrel,”", "\"이걸 가장자리까지 가득 채워라, 이 악당아,\""], ["said the Major, holding up his glass.", "소령이 자기 잔을 치켜들며 말했다."], ["“Fill Mr Carker’s to the brim too.", "\"카커 씨의 잔도 가장자리까지 가득 채우고."], ["And Mr Dombey’s too.", "돔비 씨의 잔도."], ["By Gad, gentlemen,”", "이런, 신사 여러분,\""], ["said the Major, winking at his new friend, while Mr Dombey looked into his plate with a conscious air, “we’ll consecrate this glass of wine to a Divinity whom Joe is proud to know, and at a distance humbly and reverently to admire.", "소령이 새 친구에게 눈을 찡긋하며 말했고, 그동안 돔비 씨는 속내를 들킨 듯한 표정으로 자기 접시를 들여다보았다, \"이 포도주 잔을 한 여신께 바칩시다. 조가 그분을 아는 것을 자랑스러워하고, 멀찍이서 겸허하고 경건하게 흠모하는 여신이지요."], ["Edith,”", "에디스,\""], ["said the Major, “is her name; angelic Edith!”", "소령이 말했다, \"그것이 그분의 이름이오. 천사 같은 에디스!\""]], [["“To angelic Edith!”", "\"천사 같은 에디스를 위하여!\""], ["cried the smiling Carker.", "미소 짓는 카커가 외쳤다."]], [["“Edith, by all means,”", "\"좋습니다, 에디스를 위하여,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["The entrance of the waiters with new dishes caused the Major to be slyer yet, but in a more serious vein.", "새 요리를 든 시중꾼들이 들어오는 바람에 소령은 한층 더 능청맞아졌으나, 더 진지한 어조로 그러했다."], ["“For though among ourselves, Joe Bagstock mingles jest and earnest on this subject, Sir,”", "\"우리끼리야, 조 배그스톡이 이 화제를 두고 농담과 진담을 섞기는 합니다만, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, laying his finger on his lips, and speaking half apart to Carker, “he holds that name too sacred to be made the property of these fellows, or of any fellows.", "소령이 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대고 카커에게 반쯤 떨어져서 말했다, \"그는 그 이름을 너무 신성하게 여기는지라 이런 작자들이나 그 어떤 작자들의 입에 올리도록 두지 않지요."], ["Not a word, Sir, while they are here!”", "한마디도 안 됩니다, 선생, 저들이 여기 있는 동안은!\""]], [["This was respectful and becoming on the Major’s part, and Mr Dombey plainly felt it so.", "이것은 소령으로서는 정중하고 어울리는 처신이었고, 돔비 씨도 분명히 그렇게 느꼈다."], ["Although embarrassed in his own frigid way, by the Major’s allusions, Mr Dombey had no objection to such rallying, it was clear, but rather courted it.", "소령의 암시에 자기 나름의 그 싸늘한 방식으로 거북해하긴 했어도, 돔비 씨는 그러한 놀림에 반대하지 않았던 것이 분명하며, 오히려 그것을 은근히 바랐다."], ["Perhaps the Major had been pretty near the truth, when he had divined that morning that the great man who was too haughty formally to consult with, or confide in his prime minister, on such a matter, yet wished him to be fully possessed of it.", "어쩌면 소령이, 그날 아침에, 그토록 거만하여 이런 문제를 자기 재상과 격식 차려 의논하거나 속내를 털어놓지는 못하면서도 그가 그 일을 속속들이 알아주기를 바라는 위인이라고 헤아렸을 때, 그는 진실에 자못 가까이 다가갔던 것인지도 모른다."], ["Let this be how it may, he often glanced at Mr Carker while the Major plied his light artillery, and seemed watchful of its effect upon him.", "그것이 어떠하든, 소령이 자기의 가벼운 포격을 퍼붓는 동안 그는 자주 카커 씨를 흘끗거렸고, 그것이 그에게 미치는 효과를 살피는 듯했다."]], [["But the Major, having secured an attentive listener, and a smiler who had not his match in all the world—“in short, a devilish intelligent and able fellow,” as he often afterwards declared—was not going to let him off with a little slyness personal to Mr Dombey.", "그러나 소령은, 주의 깊은 청자와, 세상에 둘도 없는 미소꾼을—\"한마디로 지독하게 영리하고 유능한 친구\"라고 그가 훗날 자주 단언했듯이—붙잡아 둔 터라, 그를 돔비 씨에게만 관련된 약간의 능청으로 놓아줄 생각이 없었다."], ["Therefore, on the removal of the cloth, the Major developed himself as a choice spirit in the broader and more comprehensive range of narrating regimental stories, and cracking regimental jokes, which he did with such prodigal exuberance, that Carker was (or feigned to be) quite exhausted with laughter and admiration: while Mr Dombey looked on over his starched cravat, like the Major’s proprietor, or like a stately showman who was glad to see his bear dancing well.", "그리하여 식탁보를 걷어 낸 뒤, 소령은 연대 이야기를 늘어놓고 연대 농담을 터뜨리는, 한층 폭넓고 두루 미치는 영역에서 자기를 빼어난 익살꾼으로 펼쳐 보였는데, 어찌나 헤프도록 흥이 넘쳤던지 카커는 웃음과 감탄에 자못 기진맥진했다(혹은 그런 척했다). 한편 돔비 씨는 빳빳한 크라바트 너머로 그 광경을 바라보았으니, 마치 소령의 소유주처럼, 혹은 자기 곰이 춤을 잘 추는 것을 보고 흐뭇해하는 위엄 있는 흥행사처럼 보였다."]], [["When the Major was too hoarse with meat and drink, and the display of his social powers, to render himself intelligible any longer, they adjourned to coffee.", "소령이 먹고 마시고 또 사교적 재간을 펼친 통에 목이 너무 쉬어 더는 알아들을 만하게 말할 수 없게 되자, 그들은 커피를 마시러 자리를 옮겼다."], ["After which, the Major inquired of Mr Carker the Manager, with little apparent hope of an answer in the affirmative, if he played picquet.", "그런 다음 소령은 지배인 카커 씨에게, 긍정의 대답을 들으리라는 기대는 별로 없이, 피켓을 할 줄 아느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I play picquet a little,”", "\"네, 피켓이라면 좀 합니다,\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Backgammon, perhaps?”", "\"백개먼은, 혹시?\""], ["observed the Major, hesitating.", "소령이 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I play backgammon a little too,”", "\"네, 백개먼도 좀 합니다,\""], ["replied the man of teeth.", "그 치아의 사내가 대답했다."]], [["“Carker plays at all games, I believe,”", "\"카커는 어떤 놀이든 다 할 줄 알 걸세,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, laying himself on a sofa like a man of wood, without a hinge or a joint in him; “and plays them well.”", "돔비 씨가 마치 경첩도 관절도 없는 목각 인형처럼 소파에 몸을 누이며 말했다. \"그것도 잘하지.\""]], [["In sooth, he played the two in question, to such perfection, that the Major was astonished, and asked him, at random, if he played chess.", "과연 그는 문제의 그 두 가지를 어찌나 완벽하게 해냈던지 소령이 깜짝 놀라, 무심코 그에게 체스도 둘 줄 아느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I play chess a little,”", "\"네, 체스도 좀 둡니다,\""], ["answered Carker.", "카커가 대답했다."], ["“I have sometimes played, and won a game—it’s a mere trick—without seeing the board.”", "\"더러는 판을 보지 않고도 둬서 한 판 이긴 적도 있지요—그저 잔재주일 뿐이지만요.\""]], [["“By Gad, Sir!”", "\"이런 맙소사, 선생!\""], ["said the Major, staring, “you are a contrast to Dombey, who plays nothing.”", "소령이 빤히 쳐다보며 말했다, \"선생은 아무 놀이도 안 하는 돔비와는 대조적이로구려.\""]], [["“Oh! He!”", "\"오!"], ["returned the Manager.", "저 사람이요!\" 지배인이 응수했다."], ["“He has never had occasion to acquire such little arts.", "\"저분은 그런 자잘한 재간을 익힐 일이 한 번도 없었으니까요."], ["To men like me, they are sometimes useful.", "저 같은 사람에게는 그것들이 더러 쓸모가 있지요."], ["As at present, Major Bagstock, when they enable me to take a hand with you.”", "지금처럼, 배그스톡 소령, 그것들 덕분에 소령과 한 판 둘 수 있게 될 때 말입니다.\""]], [["It might be only the false mouth, so smooth and wide; and yet there seemed to lurk beneath the humility and subserviency of this short speech, a something like a snarl; and, for a moment, one might have thought that the white teeth were prone to bite the hand they fawned upon.", "그것은 단지 그 거짓된 입, 그토록 매끄럽고 널찍한 입 때문이었는지도 모른다. 그런데도 이 짤막한 말의 겸손함과 비굴함 밑에는 무언가 으르렁거림 같은 것이 도사려 있는 듯했고, 한순간 그 하얀 이들이 자기가 아첨하는 그 손을 깨물고 싶어 한다고 여길 만도 했다."], ["But the Major thought nothing about it; and Mr Dombey lay meditating with his eyes half shut, during the whole of the play, which lasted until bed-time.", "그러나 소령은 그것에 대해 아무 생각도 하지 않았다. 그리고 돔비 씨는, 잠자리에 들 시간까지 이어진 그 놀이 내내, 눈을 반쯤 감은 채 사색에 잠겨 누워 있었다."]], [["By that time, Mr Carker, though the winner, had mounted high into the Major’s good opinion, insomuch that when he left the Major at his own room before going to bed, the Major as a special attention, sent the Native—who always rested on a mattress spread upon the ground at his master’s door—along the gallery, to light him to his room in state.", "그 무렵, 카커 씨는 비록 이긴 쪽이었으나, 소령의 호감 속에 높이 올라가 있었다. 어찌나 그러했던지, 카커 씨가 잠자리에 들기 전에 소령을 그 방에 두고 떠날 때, 소령은 각별한 배려로, 늘 자기 방문 앞 바닥에 깔린 매트리스 위에서 쉬는 원주민을 회랑을 따라 보내, 위풍당당하게 그의 방까지 불을 밝혀 가게 했다."]], [["There was a faint blur on the surface of the mirror in Mr Carker’s chamber, and its reflection was, perhaps, a false one.", "카커 씨의 침실에 있는 거울의 표면에는 희미한 얼룩이 끼어 있었고, 그 비친 모습은 어쩌면 거짓된 것이었는지도 모른다."], ["But it showed, that night, the image of a man, who saw, in his fancy, a crowd of people slumbering on the ground at his feet, like the poor Native at his master’s door: who picked his way among them: looking down, maliciously enough: but trod upon no upturned face—as yet.", "그러나 그 거울은, 그날 밤, 한 사내의 모습을 비추어 주었으니, 그는 제 공상 속에서, 자기 발치 바닥에 잠들어 있는 한 무리의 사람들을—마치 주인 방문 앞의 가엾은 원주민처럼—보고 있었다. 그는 그 사이로 길을 골라 디뎌 나아가며, 자못 악의에 차 내려다보았으나, 위를 향한 어느 얼굴도 밟지는 않았다—아직은."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVII. Deeper Shadows", "# CHAPTER XXVII. Deeper Shadows"]], [["Mr Carker the Manager rose with the lark, and went out, walking in the summer day.", "카커 지배인은 종달새와 함께 일어나, 여름날 아침 산책에 나섰다."], ["His meditations—and he meditated with contracted brows while he strolled along—hardly seemed to soar as high as the lark, or to mount in that direction; rather they kept close to their nest upon the earth, and looked about, among the dust and worms.", "그의 사색은—그는 거니는 동안 미간을 찌푸린 채 사색에 잠겼다—종달새만큼 높이 솟아오르거나 그 방향으로 떠오르는 것 같지는 않았다. 오히려 그것은 땅 위 둥지에 바싹 붙어, 먼지와 벌레들 사이를 둘러보는 듯했다."], ["But there was not a bird in the air, singing unseen, farther beyond the reach of human eye than Mr Carker’s thoughts.", "그러나 보이지 않는 곳에서 노래하며 인간의 눈이 닿지 못할 만큼 멀리 떠 있는 새도, 카커의 생각보다 더 멀리 가 있지는 못했다."], ["He had his face so perfectly under control, that few could say more, in distinct terms, of its expression, than that it smiled or that it pondered.", "그는 얼굴을 완벽하게 통제하고 있어서, 그 표정에 대해 분명한 말로 할 수 있는 것이라곤 그저 미소를 짓고 있다거나 깊이 생각에 잠겨 있다는 정도가 고작이었다."], ["It pondered now, intently.", "지금 그는 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["As the lark rose higher, he sank deeper in thought.", "종달새가 더 높이 솟아오를수록 그는 생각 속으로 더 깊이 가라앉았다."], ["As the lark poured out her melody clearer and stronger, he fell into a graver and profounder silence.", "종달새가 더 맑고 힘차게 가락을 쏟아낼수록 그는 더 진중하고 깊은 침묵에 빠져들었다. 마침내 종달새가 노래의 물결을 점점 더 쌓아 올리며 곤두박질쳐 내려와, 강물처럼 아침 숨결에 잔물결 일렁이는 그의 곁 푸른 밀밭 사이로 떨어졌을 때, 그는 몽상에서 벌떡 깨어나 갑작스러운 미소를 지으며 주위를 둘러보았는데, 그 미소는 마치 비위를 맞춰야 할 구경꾼이 수없이 둘러서 있기라도 한 듯 정중하고 부드러웠다."], ["At length, when the lark came headlong down, with an accumulating stream of song, and dropped among the green wheat near him, rippling in the breath of the morning like a river, he sprang up from his reverie, and looked round with a sudden smile, as courteous and as soft as if he had had numerous observers to propitiate; nor did he relapse, after being thus awakened; but clearing his face, like one who bethought himself that it might otherwise wrinkle and tell tales, went smiling on, as if for practice.", "또 그렇게 깨어난 뒤에도 그는 다시 가라앉지 않았다. 오히려 그러지 않으면 얼굴이 주름지며 속을 들킬지도 모른다고 생각한 사람처럼 얼굴을 말끔히 펴고는, 연습이라도 하듯 미소를 지으며 걸음을 이어 갔다."]], [["Perhaps with an eye to first impressions, Mr Carker was very carefully and trimly dressed, that morning.", "아마도 첫인상을 의식한 탓이었는지, 카커는 그날 아침 매우 세심하고 단정하게 차려입었다."], ["Though always somewhat formal, in his dress, in imitation of the great man whom he served, he stopped short of the extent of Mr Dombey’s stiffness: at once perhaps because he knew it to be ludicrous, and because in doing so he found another means of expressing his sense of the difference and distance between them.", "그는 자기가 모시는 그 위대한 인물을 흉내 내어 늘 옷차림이 다소 격식을 갖춘 편이었으나, 돔비 씨의 그 빳빳함의 극단에는 미치지 못하게 멈추었다. 그것이 우스꽝스럽다는 것을 알기 때문이기도 했고, 또 그렇게 함으로써 두 사람 사이의 차이와 거리에 대한 자기 인식을 표현하는 또 다른 수단을 찾았기 때문이기도 했다."], ["Some people quoted him indeed, in this respect, as a pointed commentary, and not a flattering one, on his icy patron—but the world is prone to misconstruction, and Mr Carker was not accountable for its bad propensity.", "실제로 어떤 이들은 이 점에서 그를, 그 얼음장 같은 후원자에 대한 날카롭고도 결코 아첨이 아닌 논평이라고 인용하기도 했다—그러나 세상이란 오해하기 쉬운 법이고, 카커가 세상의 그 나쁜 성향에 책임이 있는 것은 아니었다."]], [["Clean and florid: with his light complexion, fading as it were, in the sun, and his dainty step enhancing the softness of the turf: Mr Carker the Manager strolled about meadows, and green lanes, and glided among avenues of trees, until it was time to return to breakfast.", "말끔하고 혈색 좋은 모습으로, 햇빛 속에서 마치 옅어지는 듯한 밝은 안색과, 잔디의 부드러움을 한층 돋우는 정갈한 걸음걸이로, 카커 지배인은 초원과 푸른 오솔길을 거닐었고, 나무들이 늘어선 가로수길 사이를 미끄러지듯 지나며, 아침 식사로 돌아갈 시간이 될 때까지 산책했다."], ["Taking a nearer way back, Mr Carker pursued it, airing his teeth, and said aloud as he did so, “Now to see the second Mrs Dombey!”", "돌아오는 더 가까운 길을 잡아 그 길을 따라가며, 카커는 이를 드러내어 바람을 쐬더니 그렇게 하면서 소리 내어 말했다, \"자, 이제 두 번째 돔비 부인을 만나러 가 볼까!\""]], [["He had strolled beyond the town, and re-entered it by a pleasant walk, where there was a deep shade of leafy trees, and where there were a few benches here and there for those who chose to rest.", "그는 읍내를 벗어나 거닐었다가, 잎이 무성한 나무들이 짙은 그늘을 드리우고, 쉬고 싶은 이들을 위해 여기저기 벤치가 몇 개 놓인 기분 좋은 산책로를 따라 다시 읍내로 들어왔다."], ["It not being a place of general resort at any hour, and wearing at that time of the still morning the air of being quite deserted and retired, Mr Carker had it, or thought he had it, all to himself.", "그곳은 어느 시간에도 사람들이 흔히 모이는 장소가 아니었고, 고요한 아침의 그 시각에는 완전히 버려져 외진 분위기를 풍기고 있었으므로, 카커는 그곳을 온통 제 차지로 삼았다, 혹은 그렇게 여겼다."], ["So, with the whim of an idle man, to whom there yet remained twenty minutes for reaching a destination easily able in ten, Mr Carker threaded the great boles of the trees, and went passing in and out, before this one and behind that, weaving a chain of footsteps on the dewy ground.", "그래서, 십 분이면 넉넉히 닿을 목적지에 닿는 데 아직 이십 분이 남아 있는 한가한 사내의 변덕으로, 카커는 커다란 나무 둥치들 사이를 누비며, 이 나무 앞으로 저 나무 뒤로 들락날락 지나다니며, 이슬 맺힌 땅 위에 발자국의 사슬을 엮어 갔다."]], [["But he found he was mistaken in supposing there was no one in the grove, for as he softly rounded the trunk of one large tree, on which the obdurate bark was knotted and overlapped like the hide of a rhinoceros or some kindred monster of the ancient days before the Flood, he saw an unexpected figure sitting on a bench near at hand, about which, in another moment, he would have wound the chain he was making.", "그러나 그 숲에 아무도 없다고 여긴 것이 착각이었음을 그는 깨달았다. 어느 큰 나무의 둥치를 살그머니 돌아갈 때—그 나무의 거친 껍질은 마치 코뿔소나 대홍수 이전 고대의 어떤 비슷한 괴물의 가죽처럼 옹이지고 겹겹이 포개져 있었는데—그가 막 엮어 가던 그 사슬을 다음 순간 두르게 될 바로 그 부근의 벤치에, 뜻밖의 인물이 앉아 있는 것을 보았기 때문이다."]], [["It was that of a lady, elegantly dressed and very handsome, whose dark proud eyes were fixed upon the ground, and in whom some passion or struggle was raging.", "그것은 한 부인의 모습이었는데, 우아하게 차려입고 매우 아름다웠으며, 그 검고 도도한 두 눈은 땅바닥에 고정되어 있었고, 그 안에서는 어떤 격정이나 갈등이 들끓고 있었다."], ["For as she sat looking down, she held a corner of her under lip within her mouth, her bosom heaved, her nostril quivered, her head trembled, indignant tears were on her cheek, and her foot was set upon the moss as though she would have crushed it into nothing.", "앉아서 고개를 숙인 채, 그녀는 아랫입술 한 귀퉁이를 입안으로 깨물고 있었고, 가슴은 들썩였으며, 콧방울은 떨렸고, 고개는 흔들렸으며, 뺨에는 분한 눈물이 어려 있었고, 발은 이끼를 짓밟아 흔적도 없이 으스러뜨리려는 듯 그 위에 놓여 있었다."], ["And yet almost the self-same glance that showed him this, showed him the self-same lady rising with a scornful air of weariness and lassitude, and turning away with nothing expressed in face or figure but careless beauty and imperious disdain.", "그런데도 그에게 이 모습을 보여 준 거의 똑같은 그 눈길이, 바로 그 똑같은 부인이 경멸 어린 권태와 나른함의 기색으로 일어서서, 얼굴이나 자태에 무심한 아름다움과 거만한 멸시 외에는 아무것도 드러내지 않은 채 돌아서는 모습을 그에게 보여 주었다."]], [["A withered and very ugly old woman, dressed not so much like a gipsy as like any of that medley race of vagabonds who tramp about the country, begging, and stealing, and tinkering, and weaving rushes, by turns, or all together, had been observing the lady, too; for, as she rose, this second figure strangely confronting the first, scrambled up from the ground—out of it, it almost appeared—and stood in the way.", "말라비틀어지고 몹시 추한 노파 하나가—집시라기보다는 시골을 떠돌며 구걸하고 훔치고 땜질하고 골풀을 엮는 일을 번갈아, 혹은 한꺼번에 하며 돌아다니는 그 잡다한 부랑자 무리의 누구라도 닮은 차림으로—이 부인을 역시 지켜보고 있었던 것이다. 부인이 일어서자, 첫 번째 인물과 기이하게 맞서는 이 두 번째 인물이 땅바닥에서—거의 땅속에서 솟아난 듯—기어올라 와 그 앞을 가로막고 섰기 때문이다."]], [["“Let me tell your fortune, my pretty lady,”", "\"점을 봐 드릴게요, 어여쁜 아씨,\""], ["said the old woman, munching with her jaws, as if the Death’s Head beneath her yellow skin were impatient to get out.", "노파가, 마치 그 누런 살가죽 밑의 해골 머리가 어서 튀어나오고 싶어 안달이라도 난 듯 턱을 우물거리며 말했다."]], [["“I can tell it for myself,”", "\"점이라면 내가 직접 봐도 돼요,\""], ["was the reply.", "하는 것이 대답이었다."]], [["“Ay, ay, pretty lady; but not right.", "\"아이고, 아이고, 어여쁜 아씨."], ["You didn’t tell it right when you were sitting there.", "하지만 제대로는 못 보지요. 거기 앉아 계실 때도 제대로 못 보셨잖아요."], ["I see you!", "내가 다 봤다고요!"], ["Give me a piece of silver, pretty lady, and I’ll tell your fortune true.", "은전 한 닢만 주시면, 어여쁜 아씨, 점을 제대로 봐 드릴게요."], ["There’s riches, pretty lady, in your face.”", "어여쁜 아씨, 그 얼굴엔 부귀가 깃들어 있어요.\""]], [["“I know,”", "\"알아요,\""], ["returned the lady, passing her with a dark smile, and a proud step.", "부인이 어두운 미소를 띠고 도도한 걸음으로 노파를 지나치며 대꾸했다."], ["“I knew it before.", "\"전부터 알고 있었어요.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["You won’t give me nothing?”", "아무것도 안 주시겠다고요?\""], ["cried the old woman.", "노파가 외쳤다."], ["“You won’t give me nothing to tell your fortune, pretty lady?", "\"점을 봐 줘도 아무것도 안 주시겠다고요, 어여쁜 아씨?"], ["How much will you give me to tell it, then?", "그럼 점을 봐 주면 얼마나 주실 건데요?"], ["Give me something, or I’ll call it after you!”", "뭐라도 좀 주세요, 안 그러면 아씨 뒤에 대고 외쳐 댈 거예요!\""], ["croaked the old woman, passionately.", "노파가 격하게 쉰 목소리로 깍깍거렸다."]], [["Mr Carker, whom the lady was about to pass close, slinking against his tree as she crossed to gain the path, advanced so as to meet her, and pulling off his hat as she went by, bade the old woman hold her peace.", "부인이 막 바싹 지나치려 하던 카커는, 그녀가 길로 들어서려고 가로질러 가는 동안 자기 나무에 몸을 숨기고 있다가, 그녀를 마주하도록 앞으로 나서서, 그녀가 지나갈 때 모자를 벗으며 노파에게 입을 다물라고 일렀다."], ["The lady acknowledged his interference with an inclination of the head, and went her way.", "부인은 고갯짓으로 그의 개입에 답례하고는 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["“You give me something then, or I’ll call it after her!”", "\"그럼 당신이 뭐라도 좀 주구려, 안 그러면 저 여자 뒤에 대고 외쳐 댈 테니!\""], ["screamed the old woman, throwing up her arms, and pressing forward against his outstretched hand.", "노파가 두 팔을 치켜들고, 내민 그의 손에 맞서 앞으로 밀고 들며 비명을 질렀다. \"아니면 이러지,\""], ["“Or come,” she added, dropping her voice suddenly, looking at him earnestly, and seeming in a moment to forget the object of her wrath, “give me something, or I’ll call it after you!”", "그녀가 갑자기 목소리를 낮추고, 그를 진지하게 바라보며, 순간 분노의 대상을 잊은 듯 덧붙였다, \"뭐라도 좀 주구려, 안 그러면 당신 뒤에 대고 외쳐 댈 테니!\""]], [["“After me, old lady!”", "\"내 뒤에 대고라고, 할멈!\""], ["returned the Manager, putting his hand in his pocket.", "지배인이 주머니에 손을 넣으며 대꾸했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said the woman, steadfast in her scrutiny, and holding out her shrivelled hand.", "노파가 한결같이 그를 뜯어보며, 쪼그라든 손을 내밀고 말했다."], ["“I know!”", "\"내가 다 안다고!\""]], [["“What do you know?”", "\"뭘 안다는 거지?\""], ["demanded Carker, throwing her a shilling.", "카커가 그녀에게 일 실링을 던져 주며 따져 물었다."], ["“Do you know who the handsome lady is?”", "\"저 아름다운 부인이 누군지 안단 말인가?\""]], [["Munching like that sailor’s wife of yore, who had chestnuts in her lap, and scowling like the witch who asked for some in vain, the old woman picked the shilling up, and going backwards, like a crab, or like a heap of crabs: for her alternately expanding and contracting hands might have represented two of that species, and her creeping face, some half-a-dozen more: crouched on the veinous root of an old tree, pulled out a short black pipe from within the crown of her bonnet, lighted it with a match, and smoked in silence, looking fixedly at her questioner.", "옛적에 무릎에 밤을 한가득 담고 우물거리던 그 뱃사람의 아내처럼 입을 우물거리고, 밤을 좀 달라고 청했다가 헛걸음한 그 마녀처럼 험상궂은 표정을 지으며, 노파는 일 실링을 집어 들고, 게처럼, 아니 한 무더기의 게처럼 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 번갈아 펴졌다 오므라드는 그녀의 두 손은 그 종류의 게 두 마리를, 기어가는 듯한 그녀의 얼굴은 또 대여섯 마리를 나타냈을 법했으니. 그녀는 늙은 나무의 울퉁불퉁한 뿌리 위에 웅크려 앉아, 보닛 꼭대기 속에서 짧고 검은 파이프를 꺼내, 성냥으로 불을 붙이고는, 질문한 사내를 뚫어지게 바라보며 말없이 담배를 피웠다."]], [["Mr Carker laughed, and turned upon his heel.", "카커는 웃으며 발꿈치를 돌렸다."]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["said the old woman.", "노파가 말했다."], ["“One child dead, and one child living: one wife dead, and one wife coming.", "\"자식 하나 죽고, 자식 하나 살아 있고. 아내 하나 죽고, 아내 하나 다가오고."], ["Go and meet her!”", "가서 그 여자를 맞으시지!\""]], [["In spite of himself, the Manager looked round again, and stopped.", "자기도 모르게 지배인은 다시 돌아보며 멈춰 섰다."], ["The old woman, who had not removed her pipe, and was munching and mumbling while she smoked, as if in conversation with an invisible familiar, pointed with her finger in the direction he was going, and laughed.", "파이프를 입에서 떼지 않은 노파는, 담배를 피우면서 보이지 않는 정령과 대화라도 나누듯 우물거리고 중얼거리며, 그가 가던 방향을 손가락으로 가리키고는 웃었다."]], [["“What was that you said, Bedlamite?”", "\"방금 뭐라고 했나, 미친 할멈?\""], ["he demanded.", "그가 따져 물었다."]], [["The woman mumbled, and chattered, and smoked, and still pointed before him; but remained silent Muttering a farewell that was not complimentary, Mr Carker pursued his way; but as he turned out of that place, and looked over his shoulder at the root of the old tree, he could yet see the finger pointing before him, and thought he heard the woman screaming, “Go and meet her!”", "여인은 중얼거리고, 재잘대고, 담배를 피우며, 여전히 그의 앞쪽을 가리켰다. 그러나 더는 말이 없었다. 칭찬과는 거리가 먼 작별 인사를 웅얼거리며 카커는 제 갈 길을 갔다. 그러나 그곳을 빠져나오면서 어깨 너머로 그 늙은 나무 뿌리 쪽을 돌아보았을 때, 그는 여전히 자기 앞쪽을 가리키는 손가락을 볼 수 있었고, \"가서 그 여자를 맞으시지!\" 하고 외치는 여인의 소리를 들은 듯했다."]], [["Preparations for a choice repast were completed, he found, at the hotel; and Mr Dombey, and the Major, and the breakfast, were awaiting the ladies.", "호텔에 가 보니 정성스러운 식사 준비가 끝나 있었고, 돔비 씨와 소령과 아침 식사가 부인들을 기다리고 있었다."], ["Individual constitution has much to do with the development of such facts, no doubt; but in this case, appetite carried it hollow over the tender passion; Mr Dombey being very cool and collected, and the Major fretting and fuming in a state of violent heat and irritation.", "그런 사실들이 어떻게 드러나는지에는 개인의 기질이 큰 몫을 한다는 데 의심의 여지가 없으나, 이번 경우에는 식욕이 다정한 연정을 완전히 압도했다. 돔비 씨는 매우 냉정하고 침착했고, 소령은 격렬한 열기와 짜증 속에서 안달복달하며 부글거리고 있었다."], ["At length the door was thrown open by the Native, and, after a pause, occupied by her languishing along the gallery, a very blooming, but not very youthful lady, appeared.", "마침내 그 원주민이 문을 활짝 열었고, 그녀가 회랑을 따라 나른하게 걸어오느라 잠시 뜸을 들인 끝에, 매우 화사하지만 그리 젊지는 않은 한 부인이 나타났다."]], [["“My dear Mr Dombey,”", "\"이보세요, 돔비 씨,\""], ["said the lady, “I am afraid we are late, but Edith has been out already looking for a favourable point of view for a sketch, and kept me waiting for her.", "부인이 말했다, \"우리가 늦은 것 같아 걱정이군요. 하지만 에디스가 벌써 나가서 스케치하기 좋은 경치를 찾고 있어서, 그 애를 기다리느라 그만."], ["Falsest of Majors,”", "더없이 거짓된 소령님,\""], ["giving him her little finger, “how do you do?”", "그에게 새끼손가락을 내밀며, \"안녕하세요?\""]], [["“Mrs Skewton,”", "\"스큐턴 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “let me gratify my friend Carker:”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"제 친구 카커에게 기쁨을 안겨 드리고자 합니다.\""], ["Mr Dombey unconsciously emphasised the word friend, as saying ‘no really; I do allow him to take credit for that distinction:’ “by presenting him to you.", "돔비 씨는 자기도 모르게 친구라는 말에 힘을 주었는데, 마치 '아니, 정말로, 나는 그가 그런 영예를 자처하도록 허락하는 거요'라고 말하는 듯했다. \"이 사람을 부인께 소개해 드림으로써요."], ["You have heard me mention Mr Carker.”", "제가 카커 씨를 언급하는 것을 들으셨지요.\""]], [["“I am charmed, I am sure,”", "\"정말이지 황홀하군요,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, graciously.", "스큐턴 부인이 우아하게 말했다."]], [["Mr Carker was charmed, of course.", "물론 카커는 황홀했다."], ["Would he have been more charmed on Mr Dombey’s behalf, if Mrs Skewton had been (as he at first supposed her) the Edith whom they had toasted overnight?", "만일 스큐턴 부인이 (처음에 그가 짐작했던 대로) 그들이 간밤에 건배했던 그 에디스였더라면, 그는 돔비 씨를 위하여 더 황홀해했을까?"]], [["“Why, where, for Heaven’s sake, is Edith?”", "\"아니, 도대체, 맙소사, 에디스는 어디 있담?\""], ["exclaimed Mrs Skewton, looking round.", "스큐턴 부인이 주위를 둘러보며 외쳤다."], ["“Still at the door, giving Withers orders about the mounting of those drawings!", "\"아직도 문간에서 위더스한테 그 그림들 표구에 대해 지시를 내리고 있다니!"], ["My dear Mr Dombey, will you have the kindness”—", "이보세요 돔비 씨, 친절을 좀—\""]], [["Mr Dombey was already gone to seek her.", "돔비 씨는 이미 그녀를 찾으러 나간 뒤였다."], ["Next moment he returned, bearing on his arm the same elegantly dressed and very handsome lady whom Mr Carker had encountered underneath the trees.", "다음 순간 그는, 카커가 나무 아래에서 마주쳤던 바로 그 우아하게 차려입고 매우 아름다운 부인을 팔에 끼고 돌아왔다."]], [["“Carker—”", "\"카커—\""], ["began Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 입을 열었다."], ["But their recognition of each other was so manifest, that Mr Dombey stopped surprised.", "그러나 둘이 서로 알아보는 기색이 어찌나 역력했던지 돔비 씨는 놀라서 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“I am obliged to the gentleman,”", "\"이분께 신세를 졌어요,\""], ["said Edith, with a stately bend, “for sparing me some annoyance from an importunate beggar just now.”", "에디스가 위엄 있게 고개를 숙이며 말했다, \"방금 끈질긴 거지에게 시달릴 뻔한 걸 면하게 해 주셨거든요.\""]], [["“I am obliged to my good fortune,”", "\"제 행운에 신세를 진 셈입니다,\""], ["said Mr Carker, bowing low, “for the opportunity of rendering so slight a service to one whose servant I am proud to be.”", "카커가 깊이 절하며 말했다, \"그토록 사소한 봉사나마, 기꺼이 그 종이 되고 싶은 분께 해 드릴 기회를 얻었으니 말입니다.\""]], [["As her eye rested on him for an instant, and then lighted on the ground, he saw in its bright and searching glance a suspicion that he had not come up at the moment of his interference, but had secretly observed her sooner.", "그녀의 눈이 한순간 그에게 머물렀다가 땅바닥으로 내려앉을 때, 그는 그 밝고 살피는 듯한 눈길 속에서, 그가 개입한 그 순간에 비로소 다가온 것이 아니라 그보다 먼저 은밀히 그녀를 지켜보고 있었으리라는 의심을 읽었다."], ["As he saw that, she saw in his eye that her distrust was not without foundation.", "그가 그것을 알아챈 순간, 그녀는 그의 눈에서, 자기 의심이 근거 없는 것이 아니었음을 읽었다."]], [["“Really,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["cried Mrs Skewton, who had taken this opportunity of inspecting Mr Carker through her glass, and satisfying herself (as she lisped audibly to the Major) that he was all heart; “really now, this is one of the most enchanting coincidences that I ever heard of.", "이 틈을 타 안경 너머로 카커 씨를 살펴보고는, (소령에게 들릴 만큼 혀짧은 소리로 속삭이며) 그가 온통 마음씨뿐인 사람이라고 흡족해하던 스큐턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 이거야말로 내 평생 들어 본 것 중에 가장 매혹적인 우연의 일치 가운데 하나로군요."], ["The idea!", "어쩜 이럴 수가!"], ["My dearest Edith, there is such an obvious destiny in it, that really one might almost be induced to cross one’s arms upon one’s frock, and say, like those wicked Turks, there is no What’s-his-name but Thingummy, and What-you-may-call-it is his prophet!”", "사랑하는 에디스야, 여기엔 너무도 명백한 운명이 깃들어 있어서, 정말이지 사람이 옷 위로 팔짱을 끼고서, 그 못된 터키 사람들처럼, 거시기 말고는 어쩌고가 없으며 무어라더라가 그분의 예언자라고 말하고 싶어질 정도라니까!\""]], [["Edith designed no revision of this extraordinary quotation from the Koran, but Mr Dombey felt it necessary to offer a few polite remarks.", "에디스는 코란에서 따온 이 기이한 인용문을 바로잡으려 들지 않았으나, 돔비 씨는 정중한 말 몇 마디를 건네야 할 필요를 느꼈다."]], [["“It gives me great pleasure,”", "\"저로서는 대단히 기쁩니다,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with cumbrous gallantry, “that a gentleman so nearly connected with myself as Carker is, should have had the honour and happiness of rendering the least assistance to Mrs Granger.”", "돔비 씨가 굼뜬 정중함으로 말했다, \"카커처럼 저와 그토록 가까이 연결된 신사분이, 그레인저 부인께 사소하나마 도움을 드리는 영예와 행복을 누리셨다니 말입니다.\""], ["Mr Dombey bowed to her.", "돔비 씨는 그녀에게 절을 했다."], ["“But it gives me some pain, and it occasions me to be really envious of Carker;”", "\"허나 그것은 제게 다소의 고통을 주며, 카커를 정말로 부럽게 만드는군요.\""], ["he unconsciously laid stress on these words, as sensible that they must appear to involve a very surprising proposition; “envious of Carker, that I had not that honour and that happiness myself.”", "그는 자기도 모르게 이 말들에 힘을 주었는데, 그것이 매우 놀라운 명제를 담고 있는 듯 보이리라는 것을 의식했기 때문이다. \"그 영예와 그 행복을 제 자신이 누리지 못한 것에 대해, 카커가 부럽다는 말입니다.\""], ["Mr Dombey bowed again.", "돔비 씨는 다시 절을 했다."], ["Edith, saving for a curl of her lip, was motionless.", "에디스는 입술을 한 번 비죽인 것 말고는 미동도 없었다."]], [["“By the Lord, Sir,”", "\"맙소사, 선생,\""], ["cried the Major, bursting into speech at sight of the waiter, who was come to announce breakfast, “it’s an extraordinary thing to me that no one can have the honour and happiness of shooting all such beggars through the head without being brought to book for it.", "아침 식사를 알리러 온 종업원을 보자 불쑥 입을 연 소령이 외쳤다, \"나로선 참 이상한 일이오, 그런 거지들을 죄다 머리에 총알을 박아 줘도 책잡히지 않을 수 있는 영예와 행복을 아무도 누릴 수 없다니 말이오. 하지만 여기 그레인저 부인을 위한 팔이 있소이다, 부인께서 J."], ["But here’s an arm for Mrs Granger if she’ll do J. B.", "B.에게 이 팔을 받아 주시는 영예를 베푸신다면 말이오."], ["the honour to accept it; and the greatest service Joe can render you, Ma’am, just now, is, to lead you into table!”", "그리고 부인, 지금 이 조가 부인께 해 드릴 수 있는 가장 큰 봉사는, 부인을 식탁으로 모셔 가는 일이오!\""]], [["With this, the Major gave his arm to Edith; Mr Dombey led the way with Mrs Skewton; Mr Carker went last, smiling on the party.", "이렇게 말하며 소령은 에디스에게 팔을 내밀었다. 돔비 씨는 스큐턴 부인과 함께 앞장섰고, 카커는 일행에게 미소를 지으며 맨 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“I am quite rejoiced, Mr Carker,”", "\"정말이지 기쁘답니다, 카커 씨,\""], ["said the lady-mother, at breakfast, after another approving survey of him through her glass, “that you have timed your visit so happily, as to go with us today.", "아침 식사 자리에서, 안경 너머로 그를 다시 한번 흡족하게 살펴본 뒤, 그 어머니 되는 부인이 말했다, \"방문 시기를 어찌나 절묘하게 맞추셨는지, 오늘 우리와 함께 가시게 되었으니 말이에요."], ["It is the most enchanting expedition!”", "더없이 매혹적인 나들이랍니다!\""]], [["“Any expedition would be enchanting in such society,”", "\"이런 모임에서라면 어떤 나들이든 매혹적이겠지요,\""], ["returned Carker; “but I believe it is, in itself, full of interest.”", "카커가 대꾸했다, \"허나 제 생각엔 그 자체로도 흥미가 가득하리라 봅니다.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""], ["cried Mrs Skewton, with a faded little scream of rapture, “the Castle is charming!—associations of the Middle Ages—and all that—which is so truly exquisite.", "스큐턴 부인이 빛바랜 작은 환희의 비명을 지르며 외쳤다, \"그 성(城)은 매혹적이에요!—중세의 정취며—그런 온갖 것이—참으로 절묘하다니까요."], ["Don’t you dote upon the Middle Ages, Mr Carker?”", "카커 씨도 중세를 끔찍이 사랑하시지 않나요?\""]], [["“Very much, indeed,”", "\"정말이지 무척이나요,\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커가 말했다."]], [["“Such charming times!”", "\"그 얼마나 매혹적인 시대였던지!\""], ["cried Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 외쳤다."], ["“So full of faith!", "\"그토록 신앙이 충만하고!"], ["So vigorous and forcible!", "그토록 활기차고 힘찼으며!"], ["So picturesque!", "그토록 그림 같고!"], ["So perfectly removed from commonplace!", "평범함과는 그토록 완벽하게 동떨어졌으니!"], ["Oh dear!", "오 이런!"], ["If they would only leave us a little more of the poetry of existence in these terrible days!”", "이 끔찍한 시절에 그들이 우리에게 존재의 시정(詩情)을 조금만 더 남겨 주었더라면!\""]], [["Mrs Skewton was looking sharp after Mr Dombey all the time she said this, who was looking at Edith: who was listening, but who never lifted up her eyes.", "스큐턴 부인은 이 말을 하는 내내 돔비 씨를 날카롭게 살피고 있었는데, 그는 에디스를 보고 있었고, 에디스는 듣고는 있었으나 결코 눈을 들지 않았다."]], [["“We are dreadfully real, Mr Carker,”", "\"우리는 끔찍하게 현실적이에요, 카커 씨,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton; “are we not?”", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다. \"그렇지 않나요?\""]], [["Few people had less reason to complain of their reality than Cleopatra, who had as much that was false about her as could well go to the composition of anybody with a real individual existence.", "자기의 현실성을 불평할 이유가 클레오파트라보다 적은 사람은 거의 없었으니, 실제로 개별적 존재를 지닌 누군가를 구성하는 데 들어갈 수 있는 만큼의 거짓을 그녀는 몸에 지니고 있었다."], ["But Mr Carker commiserated our reality nevertheless, and agreed that we were very hardly used in that regard.", "그러나 카커는 그럼에도 우리의 현실성을 가엾이 여겼고, 그 점에서 우리가 참으로 가혹한 대접을 받는다는 데 동의했다."]], [["“Pictures at the Castle, quite divine!”", "\"그 성의 그림들은 정말이지 거룩하답니다!\""], ["said Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 말했다."], ["“I hope you dote upon pictures?”", "\"그림을 끔찍이 사랑하시면 좋겠는데요?\""]], [["“I assure you, Mrs Skewton,”", "\"장담하건대, 스큐턴 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with solemn encouragement of his Manager, “that Carker has a very good taste for pictures; quite a natural power of appreciating them.", "돔비 씨가 자기 지배인을 엄숙하게 격려하며 말했다, \"카커는 그림에 대해 매우 훌륭한 안목을 지녔습니다. 그림을 감상하는 타고난 능력이 아주 대단하지요."], ["He is a very creditable artist himself.", "그 자신이 꽤 평판 있는 화가이기도 하답니다."], ["He will be delighted, I am sure, with Mrs Granger’s taste and skill.”", "그레인저 부인의 안목과 솜씨에 분명 크게 기뻐할 겁니다.\""]], [["“Damme, Sir!”", "\"제기랄, 선생!\""], ["cried Major Bagstock, “my opinion is, that you’re the admirable Carker, and can do anything.”", "백스톡 소령이 외쳤다, \"내 의견으론, 당신은 감탄스러운 카커요, 못 하는 게 없는 사람이란 말이오.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["smiled Carker, with humility, “you are much too sanguine, Major Bagstock.", "카커가 겸손하게 미소 지었다, \"너무 후하게 보시는군요, 백스톡 소령님."], ["I can do very little.", "저는 할 줄 아는 게 아주 적습니다."], ["But Mr Dombey is so generous in his estimation of any trivial accomplishment a man like myself may find it almost necessary to acquire, and to which, in his very different sphere, he is far superior, that—”", "하지만 돔비 씨께선 저 같은 사람이 거의 부득이하게 익혀야 하는 하찮은 재주에 대해서도 어찌나 후하게 평가해 주시는지요—그리고 그분 자신은 전혀 다른 영역에서 그런 재주를 훨씬 능가하시는데도 말입니다—그러니—\""], ["Mr Carker shrugged his shoulders, deprecating further praise, and said no more.", "카커는 더 이상의 칭찬을 사양하듯 어깨를 으쓱하고는 입을 다물었다."]], [["All this time, Edith never raised her eyes, unless to glance towards her mother when that lady’s fervent spirit shone forth in words.", "이 내내 에디스는, 그 어머니의 열렬한 기질이 말로 빛을 발할 때 어머니 쪽을 흘끗 보는 것 말고는, 결코 눈을 들지 않았다."], ["But as Carker ceased, she looked at Mr Dombey for a moment.", "그러나 카커가 말을 멈추자, 그녀는 잠시 돔비 씨를 바라보았다."], ["For a moment only; but with a transient gleam of scornful wonder on her face, not lost on one observer, who was smiling round the board.", "그저 한순간이었으나, 그녀의 얼굴에는 경멸 어린 의아함의 빛이 스쳐 지나갔고, 그것은 식탁을 빙 둘러보며 미소 짓던 한 관찰자의 눈을 벗어나지 못했다."]], [["Mr Dombey caught the dark eyelash in its descent, and took the opportunity of arresting it.", "돔비 씨는 그 검은 속눈썹이 내려앉는 것을 포착하고는, 그것을 붙잡을 기회로 삼았다."]], [["“You have been to Warwick often, unfortunately?”", "\"안타깝게도 워릭에는 자주 다녀오셨겠지요?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Several times.”", "\"여러 번요.\""]], [["“The visit will be tedious to you, I am afraid.”", "\"이번 방문이 지루하실까 봐 걱정입니다.\""]], [["“Oh no; not at all.”", "\"오 아니에요, 전혀요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["You are like your cousin Feenix, my dearest Edith,”", "너는 네 사촌 피닉스를 닮았구나, 사랑하는 에디스야,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다."], ["“He has been to Warwick Castle fifty times, if he has been there once; yet if he came to Leamington to-morrow—I wish he would, dear angel!—he would make his fifty-second visit next day.”", "\"그 애는 워릭 성에 한 번 가 봤다 하면 쉰 번은 가 봤을 거예요. 그러면서도 내일 리밍턴에 온다면—그래 주면 좋으련만, 사랑스러운 천사 같으니!—그다음 날엔 쉰두 번째 방문을 하고 말 거라니까요.\""]], [["“We are all enthusiastic, are we not, Mama?”", "\"우린 다들 열정적이잖아요, 그렇지 않아요, 엄마?\""], ["said Edith, with a cold smile.", "에디스가 싸늘한 미소를 띠고 말했다."]], [["“Too much so, for our peace, perhaps, my dear,”", "\"우리 마음의 평안을 위해서는 어쩌면 지나칠 만큼이지, 얘야,\""], ["returned her mother; “but we won’t complain.", "그녀의 어머니가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 불평하진 말자꾸나."], ["Our own emotions are our recompense.", "우리 자신의 감정이 곧 우리의 보상이니까."], ["If, as your cousin Feenix says, the sword wears out the what’s-its-name—”", "네 사촌 피닉스 말마따나, 칼이 그 뭐라더라를 닳게 한다면—\""]], [["“The scabbard, perhaps,”", "\"칼집이겠지요, 아마,\""], ["said Edith.", "에디스가 말했다."]], [["“Exactly—a little too fast, it is because it is bright and glowing, you know, my dearest love.”", "\"바로 그거야—좀 너무 빨리 닳지. 그건 칼이 환하게 빛나며 타오르기 때문이란다, 알겠니, 사랑스러운 내 보물아.\""]], [["Mrs Skewton heaved a gentle sigh, supposed to cast a shadow on the surface of that dagger of lath, whereof her susceptible bosom was the sheath: and leaning her head on one side, in the Cleopatra manner, looked with pensive affection on her darling child.", "스큐턴 부인은 부드러운 한숨을 내쉬었는데, 그것은 그 예민한 가슴이 칼집 노릇을 하는 나무토막 단검의 표면에 그늘을 드리우기라도 하는 듯했다. 그러고는 클레오파트라의 자태로 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고, 수심에 잠긴 애정으로 사랑하는 자식을 바라보았다."]], [["Edith had turned her face towards Mr Dombey when he first addressed her, and had remained in that attitude, while speaking to her mother, and while her mother spoke to her, as though offering him her attention, if he had anything more to say.", "에디스는 돔비 씨가 처음 말을 건넸을 때 그를 향해 얼굴을 돌렸고, 어머니에게 말하는 동안에도, 또 어머니가 그녀에게 말하는 동안에도, 마치 그에게 더 할 말이 있거든 들어 주겠다는 듯 그 자세를 그대로 유지했다."], ["There was something in the manner of this simple courtesy: almost defiant, and giving it the character of being rendered on compulsion, or as a matter of traffic to which she was a reluctant party again not lost upon that same observer who was smiling round the board.", "이 단순한 예의의 태도에는 무언가가 있었으니—거의 도전적이라 할 만하고, 마치 강요에 의해, 혹은 자기가 마지못해 끌려든 어떤 거래의 일환으로 베풀어진다는 성격을 띠게 하는 것이—그것 또한 식탁을 빙 둘러보며 미소 짓던 바로 그 관찰자의 눈을 벗어나지 못했다."], ["It set him thinking of her as he had first seen her, when she had believed herself to be alone among the trees.", "그것은 그로 하여금, 그녀가 나무들 사이에서 홀로 있다고 믿고 있을 때 처음 보았던 그 모습으로 그녀를 떠올리게 했다."]], [["Mr Dombey having nothing else to say, proposed—the breakfast being now finished, and the Major gorged, like any Boa Constrictor—that they should start.", "더 할 말이 없는 돔비 씨는—식사가 이제 끝났고 소령은 보아 뱀처럼 잔뜩 포식한 터라—이제 출발하자고 제안했다. 그 신사의 분부에 따라 사륜마차 한 대가 대기하고 있어서, 두 부인과 소령과 그 자신이 그 안에 자리를 잡았다."], ["A barouche being in waiting, according to the orders of that gentleman, the two ladies, the Major and himself, took their seats in it; the Native and the wan page mounted the box, Mr Towlinson being left behind; and Mr Carker, on horseback, brought up the rear.", "원주민과 핏기 없는 시동이 마부석에 올랐고, 타울린슨 씨는 남겨졌다. 그리고 말을 탄 카커가 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["Mr Carker cantered behind the carriage at the distance of a hundred yards or so, and watched it, during all the ride, as if he were a cat, indeed, and its four occupants, mice.", "카커는 백 야드쯤 거리를 두고 마차 뒤를 천천히 따라 달리며, 가는 내내 그것을 지켜보았는데, 그 모습은 정말이지 그가 고양이이고 그 안의 네 사람이 생쥐이기라도 한 듯했다."], ["Whether he looked to one side of the road, or to the other—over distant landscape, with its smooth undulations, wind-mills, corn, grass, bean fields, wild-flowers, farm-yards, hayricks, and the spire among the wood—or upwards in the sunny air, where butterflies were sporting round his head, and birds were pouring out their songs—or downward, where the shadows of the branches interlaced, and made a trembling carpet on the road—or onward, where the overhanging trees formed aisles and arches, dim with the softened light that steeped through leaves—one corner of his eye was ever on the formal head of Mr Dombey, addressed towards him, and the feather in the bonnet, drooping so neglectfully and scornfully between them; much as he had seen the haughty eyelids droop; not least so, when the face met that now fronting it.", "그가 길 이쪽을 보든 저쪽을 보든—멀리 펼쳐진 풍경, 그 완만한 기복이며 풍차며 곡식이며 풀이며 콩밭이며 들꽃이며 농가 마당이며 건초 더미며 숲 사이로 솟은 첨탑을 보든—아니면 위로, 나비들이 그의 머리 둘레에서 노닐고 새들이 노래를 쏟아 내는 햇살 가득한 공중을 보든—아니면 아래로, 가지들의 그림자가 서로 얽혀 길 위에 일렁이는 양탄자를 만든 곳을 보든—아니면 앞으로, 머리 위로 드리운 나무들이 잎새 사이로 스며 부드러워진 빛으로 어슴푸레한 회랑과 아치를 이룬 곳을 보든—그의 한쪽 눈가는 언제나, 그를 향해 돌려진 돔비 씨의 격식 차린 머리와, 두 사람 사이에서 그토록 무심하고 경멸하듯 늘어진 보닛의 깃털 위에 가 있었다. 그것은 그가 본 그 도도한 눈꺼풀이 내리깔리던 모습과 다를 바 없었으니, 특히 지금 마주한 그 얼굴과 부딪칠 때 더욱 그러했다."], ["Once, and once only, did his wary glance release these objects; and that was, when a leap over a low hedge, and a gallop across a field, enabled him to anticipate the carriage coming by the road, and to be standing ready, at the journey’s end, to hand the ladies out.", "단 한 번, 오직 한 번 그의 경계하는 눈길이 이 대상들을 놓아주었으니, 그것은 낮은 울타리를 뛰어넘고 들판을 가로질러 질주함으로써, 길로 오는 마차를 앞질러 가, 여정의 끝에서 부인들이 내리는 것을 거들 채비를 갖추고 서 있게 되었을 때였다."], ["Then, and but then, he met her glance for an instant in her first surprise; but when he touched her, in alighting, with his soft white hand, it overlooked him altogether as before.", "그때, 오직 그때, 그녀가 처음 놀라움에 휩싸인 순간 그는 그녀의 눈길을 잠시 마주쳤다. 그러나 그녀가 내릴 때 그의 부드럽고 흰 손으로 그녀를 잡았을 때, 그녀는 이전과 마찬가지로 그를 완전히 못 본 척 지나쳤다."]], [["Mrs Skewton was bent on taking charge of Mr Carker herself, and showing him the beauties of the Castle.", "스큐턴 부인은 자기가 직접 카커를 맡아 성의 아름다움을 보여 주리라 작정했다."], ["She was determined to have his arm, and the Major’s too.", "그녀는 그의 팔을, 그리고 소령의 팔도 함께 차지하기로 결심했다."], ["It would do that incorrigible creature: who was the most barbarous infidel in point of poetry: good to be in such company.", "그것이 그 구제 불능의 작자에게—시정에 관한 한 가장 야만적인 무신론자인 자에게—그런 동행 속에 있는 것이 좋을 터였다."], ["This chance arrangement left Mr Dombey at liberty to escort Edith: which he did: stalking before them through the apartments with a gentlemanly solemnity.", "이 우연한 배치 덕분에 돔비 씨는 에디스를 호위할 자유를 얻었고, 그는 그렇게 했으니, 신사다운 엄숙함으로 여러 방을 가로질러 그녀 앞에서 거드름을 피우며 걸었다."]], [["“Those darling byegone times, Mr Carker,”", "\"그 사랑스러운 지난날들이여, 카커 씨,\""], ["said Cleopatra, “with their delicious fortresses, and their dear old dungeons, and their delightful places of torture, and their romantic vengeances, and their picturesque assaults and sieges, and everything that makes life truly charming!", "클레오파트라가 말했다, \"그 황홀한 요새들과, 그 정겨운 옛 지하 감옥들과, 그 즐거운 고문 장소들과, 그 낭만적인 복수들과, 그 그림 같은 습격과 포위 공격들과, 삶을 진정 매혹적으로 만드는 그 온갖 것들이라니!"], ["How dreadfully we have degenerated!”", "우리는 어쩜 이렇게 끔찍하게 타락해 버렸을까요!\""]], [["“Yes, we have fallen off deplorably,”", "\"그렇습니다, 우리는 한탄스러울 만큼 떨어져 버렸지요,\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커가 말했다."]], [["The peculiarity of their conversation was, that Mrs Skewton, in spite of her ecstasies, and Mr Carker, in spite of his urbanity, were both intent on watching Mr Dombey and Edith.", "그들 대화의 특이한 점은, 스큐턴 부인이 그 황홀경에도 불구하고, 또 카커가 그 정중함에도 불구하고, 둘 다 돔비 씨와 에디스를 지켜보는 데 골몰해 있었다는 것이다."], ["With all their conversational endowments, they spoke somewhat distractedly, and at random, in consequence.", "온갖 화술의 재능을 갖추고 있으면서도, 그 결과 그들은 다소 산만하게, 또 되는대로 말했다."]], [["“We have no Faith left, positively,”", "\"우리에겐 이제 신앙이라곤 전혀 안 남았어요, 정말이지,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, advancing her shrivelled ear; for Mr Dombey was saying something to Edith.", "스큐턴 부인이 쪼그라든 귀를 앞으로 들이밀며 말했다. 돔비 씨가 에디스에게 뭐라고 말하고 있었던 것이다."], ["“We have no Faith in the dear old Barons, who were the most delightful creatures—or in the dear old Priests, who were the most warlike of men—or even in the days of that inestimable Queen Bess, upon the wall there, which were so extremely golden.", "\"우리에겐 그 정겨운 옛 남작들에 대한 신앙도 없지요, 더없이 즐거운 자들이었는데—아니면 그 정겨운 옛 사제들에 대해서도요, 더없이 호전적인 사내들이었는데—심지어 저 벽에 걸린 저 더없이 소중한 여왕 베스의 시절, 그토록 더없이 황금 같던 그 시절에 대해서도요."], ["Dear creature!", "사랑스러운 분이시지!"], ["She was all Heart And that charming father of hers!", "온통 마음씨뿐이었답니다. 게다가 그 매혹적인 아버님이라니!"], ["I hope you dote on Harry the Eighth!”", "헨리 8세를 끔찍이 사랑하시면 좋겠는데요!\""]], [["“I admire him very much,”", "\"저는 그를 무척 흠모합니다,\""], ["said Carker.", "카커가 말했다."]], [["“So bluff!”", "\"그토록 우락부락하지요!\""], ["cried Mrs Skewton, “wasn’t he? So burly.", "스큐턴 부인이 외쳤다, \"안 그래요?"], ["So truly English.", "그토록 떡 벌어지고."], ["Such a picture, too, he makes, with his dear little peepy eyes, and his benevolent chin!”", "그토록 진정 영국인답고요. 게다가 그 사랑스러운 작은 눈하며, 그 인자한 턱하며, 어쩜 그렇게 멋진 그림이 되는지요!\""]], [["“Ah, Ma’am!”", "\"아, 부인!\""], ["said Carker, stopping short; “but if you speak of pictures, there’s a composition!", "카커가 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"허나 그림을 말씀하신다면, 저기 한 폭의 구도가 있군요!"], ["What gallery in the world can produce the counterpart of that?”", "세상의 어느 화랑이 저것과 견줄 만한 것을 내놓을 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["As the smiling gentleman thus spake, he pointed through a doorway to where Mr Dombey and Edith were standing alone in the centre of another room.", "미소 짓는 신사는 이렇게 말하며, 돔비 씨와 에디스가 다른 방 한가운데 단둘이 서 있는 곳을 문간 너머로 가리켰다."]], [["They were not interchanging a word or a look.", "그들은 말 한마디, 눈길 한 번 주고받지 않고 있었다."], ["Standing together, arm in arm, they had the appearance of being more divided than if seas had rolled between them.", "팔짱을 끼고 함께 서 있었으나, 그들 사이에 바다가 굽이쳐 흐르고 있는 것보다 더 동떨어져 보였다."], ["There was a difference even in the pride of the two, that removed them farther from each other, than if one had been the proudest and the other the humblest specimen of humanity in all creation.", "두 사람의 자존심에는 차이마저 있어서, 그것이 그들을 서로에게서 더욱 멀어지게 했으니, 마치 한쪽은 천지 만물 가운데 가장 거만한 인간 표본이고 다른 한쪽은 가장 비천한 표본이기라도 한 것 같았다."], ["He, self-important, unbending, formal, austere.", "그는 거드름을 피우고, 굽힐 줄 모르며, 격식을 차리고, 근엄했다."], ["She, lovely and graceful, in an uncommon degree, but totally regardless of herself and him and everything around, and spurning her own attractions with her haughty brow and lip, as if they were a badge or livery she hated.", "그녀는 보기 드물 만큼 사랑스럽고 우아했으나, 자기 자신도 그도 주위의 그 무엇도 전혀 아랑곳하지 않았고, 자기 자신의 매력을 도도한 이마와 입술로 마치 자기가 증오하는 표지나 제복이라도 되는 듯 경멸했다."], ["So unmatched were they, and opposed, so forced and linked together by a chain which adverse hazard and mischance had forged: that fancy might have imagined the pictures on the walls around them, startled by the unnatural conjunction, and observant of it in their several expressions.", "그들은 그토록 어울리지 않았고 대립되었으며, 불운한 우연과 재앙이 벼려 낸 사슬에 그토록 억지로 함께 묶여 있어서, 그들을 둘러싼 벽의 그림들이 이 부자연스러운 결합에 깜짝 놀라 저마다의 표정으로 그것을 바라보고 있는 듯 상상할 만했다."], ["Grim knights and warriors looked scowling on them.", "험상궂은 기사들과 전사들이 그들을 노려보았다."], ["A churchman, with his hand upraised, denounced the mockery of such a couple coming to God’s altar.", "손을 치켜든 한 성직자는 이런 한 쌍이 하느님의 제단으로 나아오는 그 우롱을 규탄했다."], ["Quiet waters in landscapes, with the sun reflected in their depths, asked, if better means of escape were not at hand, was there no drowning left?", "풍경화 속의 잔잔한 물은, 깊은 곳에 해를 비추며, 더 나은 도피 수단이 가까이에 없다면 빠져 죽을 곳이라도 남아 있지 않으냐고 물었다."], ["Ruins cried, “Look here, and see what We are, wedded to uncongenial Time!”", "폐허들은 외쳤다, \"여길 보라, 어울리지 않는 세월에 짝지어진 우리 꼴을 보라!\""], ["Animals, opposed by nature, worried one another, as a moral to them.", "본성상 서로 적대하는 짐승들은 그들에게 교훈이라도 주듯 서로 물어뜯었다."], ["Loves and Cupids took to flight afraid, and Martyrdom had no such torment in its painted history of suffering.", "사랑의 신들과 큐피드들은 겁을 먹고 달아났고, 순교조차도 그 채색된 수난의 역사 속에 이런 고통은 담고 있지 않았다."]], [["Nevertheless, Mrs Skewton was so charmed by the sight to which Mr Carker invoked her attention, that she could not refrain from saying, half aloud, how sweet, how very full of soul it was!", "그럼에도 스큐턴 부인은 카커 씨가 주의를 기울이라고 청한 그 광경에 어찌나 매혹되었던지, 절반쯤 소리 내어, 그것이 얼마나 아름답고, 얼마나 영혼으로 가득 차 있는지 말하지 않을 수 없었다!"], ["Edith, overhearing, looked round, and flushed indignant scarlet to her hair.", "그 말을 엿들은 에디스는 돌아보았고, 머리끝까지 분한 진홍빛으로 발갛게 달아올랐다."]], [["“My dearest Edith knows I was admiring her!”", "\"사랑하는 에디스는 내가 자기를 흠모하고 있었다는 걸 알 거야!\""], ["said Cleopatra, tapping her, almost timidly, on the back with her parasol.", "클레오파트라가 거의 머뭇거리듯, 양산으로 그녀의 등을 톡톡 두드리며 말했다."], ["“Sweet pet!”", "\"사랑스러운 내 새끼!\""]], [["Again Mr Carker saw the strife he had witnessed so unexpectedly among the trees.", "또다시 카커는 나무들 사이에서 그토록 뜻밖에 목격했던 그 갈등을 보았다."], ["Again he saw the haughty languor and indifference come over it, and hide it like a cloud.", "또다시 그는 그 위에 도도한 나른함과 무심함이 덮여 와, 구름처럼 그것을 가리는 것을 보았다."]], [["She did not raise her eyes to him; but with a slight peremptory motion of them, seemed to bid her mother come near.", "그녀는 그에게로 눈을 들지는 않았으나, 가벼우면서도 단호한 눈짓으로 어머니더러 가까이 오라고 명하는 듯했다."], ["Mrs Skewton thought it expedient to understand the hint, and advancing quickly, with her two cavaliers, kept near her daughter from that time.", "스큐턴 부인은 그 암시를 알아채는 것이 상책이라 여기고, 두 호위 신사를 거느린 채 얼른 다가와, 그때부터 딸 곁에 바싹 붙어 있었다."]], [["Mr Carker now, having nothing to distract his attention, began to discourse upon the pictures and to select the best, and point them out to Mr Dombey: speaking with his usual familiar recognition of Mr Dombey’s greatness, and rendering homage by adjusting his eye-glass for him, or finding out the right place in his catalogue, or holding his stick, or the like.", "이제 주의를 흩뜨릴 것이 없어진 카커는 그림들에 대해 논하기 시작하여, 가장 좋은 것들을 골라 돔비 씨에게 짚어 주었다. 그는 평소처럼 돔비 씨의 위대함을 친근하게 인정하는 투로 말하며, 그를 위해 안경을 맞추어 주거나, 목록에서 알맞은 자리를 찾아 주거나, 그의 지팡이를 들어 주는 따위로 경의를 표했다."], ["These services did not so much originate with Mr Carker, in truth, as with Mr Dombey himself, who was apt to assert his chieftainship by saying, with subdued authority, and in an easy way—for him—“Here, Carker, have the goodness to assist me, will you?”", "사실 이런 시중들은 카커에게서 비롯되었다기보다 돔비 씨 자신에게서 비롯된 것이었으니, 돔비 씨는 억눌린 권위로, 그로서는 편안한 투로 \"여기, 카커, 좀 거들어 주겠나?\""], ["which the smiling gentleman always did with pleasure.", "하고 말함으로써 자기의 우두머리 됨을 곧잘 내세웠고, 미소 짓는 신사는 늘 기꺼이 그렇게 했던 것이다."]], [["They made the tour of the pictures, the walls, crow’s nest, and so forth; and as they were still one little party, and the Major was rather in the shade: being sleepy during the process of digestion: Mr Carker became communicative and agreeable.", "그들은 그림들이며 벽이며 망루 따위를 두루 돌아보았다. 여전히 단출한 일행이었고 소령은 다소 뒷전으로 물러나 있었으므로—소화가 진행되는 동안 졸고 있었던 까닭에—카커는 말이 많아지고 상냥해졌다."], ["At first, he addressed himself for the most part to Mrs Skewton; but as that sensitive lady was in such ecstasies with the works of art, after the first quarter of an hour, that she could do nothing but yawn (they were such perfect inspirations, she observed as a reason for that mark of rapture), he transferred his attentions to Mr Dombey.", "처음에 그는 주로 스큐턴 부인을 상대로 말을 건넸다. 그러나 그 예민한 부인이 미술품들에 어찌나 황홀해했던지, 십오 분이 지나자 하품하는 것밖에는 아무것도 할 수 없게 되어(그것들이 어찌나 완벽한 영감을 주는지, 라고 그녀는 그 황홀의 표시에 대한 핑계로 한마디 했다), 그는 관심을 돔비 씨에게로 옮겼다."], ["Mr Dombey said little beyond an occasional “Very true, Carker,”", "돔비 씨는 이따금 \"지당하네, 카커\""], ["or “Indeed, Carker,”", "혹은 \"정말 그렇군, 카커\""], ["but he tacitly encouraged Carker to proceed, and inwardly approved of his behaviour very much: deeming it as well that somebody should talk, and thinking that his remarks, which were, as one might say, a branch of the parent establishment, might amuse Mrs Granger.", "하는 정도 외에는 말이 거의 없었으나, 카커가 계속하도록 은근히 부추겼고, 속으로는 그의 처신을 매우 흡족해했다. 누군가는 말을 하는 편이 낫겠다고 여겼고, 또 말하자면 본점에서 갈라져 나온 한 가지에 불과한 그의 말이 그레인저 부인을 즐겁게 해 줄지도 모른다고 생각했기 때문이다."], ["Mr Carker, who possessed an excellent discretion, never took the liberty of addressing that lady, direct; but she seemed to listen, though she never looked at him; and once or twice, when he was emphatic in his peculiar humility, the twilight smile stole over her face, not as a light, but as a deep black shadow.", "빼어난 분별력을 지닌 카커는 결코 그 부인에게 직접 말을 건네는 무례를 범하지 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 한 번도 쳐다보지 않으면서도 귀를 기울이는 듯했고, 한두 번, 그가 그 특유의 겸손을 힘주어 드러낼 때면, 어스름 같은 미소가 그녀의 얼굴 위로 스쳤으니, 그것은 빛이 아니라 짙고 검은 그림자로서였다."]], [["Warwick Castle being at length pretty well exhausted, and the Major very much so: to say nothing of Mrs Skewton, whose peculiar demonstrations of delight had become very frequent Indeed: the carriage was again put in requisition, and they rode to several admired points of view in the neighbourhood.", "워릭 성을 마침내 거의 다 둘러보고, 소령은 진이 다 빠졌으며—스큐턴 부인은 더 말할 것도 없었으니, 그 특유의 환희의 표현이 실로 매우 잦아져 있었다—마차가 다시 동원되었고, 그들은 인근의 여러 이름난 경승지로 달려갔다."], ["Mr Dombey ceremoniously observed of one of these, that a sketch, however slight, from the fair hand of Mrs Granger, would be a remembrance to him of that agreeable day: though he wanted no artificial remembrance, he was sure (here Mr Dombey made another of his bows), which he must always highly value.", "돔비 씨는 그중 한 곳을 두고 격식을 갖추어, 그레인저 부인의 고운 손으로 그린 스케치라면, 아무리 간략한 것이라도, 이 유쾌한 날의 기념이 되리라고 말했다. 비록 인위적인 기념물 따위는 필요 없으나—그는 확신하건대(여기서 돔비 씨는 또 한 번 절을 했다) 그것을 늘 높이 간직하리라고 했다."], ["Withers the lean having Edith’s sketch-book under his arm, was immediately called upon by Mrs Skewton to produce the same: and the carriage stopped, that Edith might make the drawing, which Mr Dombey was to put away among his treasures.", "비쩍 마른 위더스가 에디스의 스케치북을 팔에 끼고 있다가, 스큐턴 부인에게서 즉시 그것을 내놓으라는 분부를 받았다. 그리고 에디스가 그림을 그릴 수 있도록 마차가 멈추었으니, 그것을 돔비 씨가 자기 보물들 가운데 간직하기로 한 것이었다."]], [["“But I am afraid I trouble you too much,”", "\"하지만 제가 너무 폐를 끼치는 게 아닐까 걱정입니다,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“By no means.", "\"천만에요."], ["Where would you wish it taken from?”", "어디서 보고 그렸으면 하세요?\""], ["she answered, turning to him with the same enforced attention as before.", "그녀가 전과 똑같이 억지스러운 주의를 기울이며 그를 향해 돌아서서 대답했다."]], [["Mr Dombey, with another bow, which cracked the starch in his cravat, would beg to leave that to the Artist.", "돔비 씨는, 크라바트의 풀 먹인 부분이 빠지직 소리를 낼 만큼 또 한 번 절을 하고는, 그것은 화가에게 맡기겠노라고 청했다."]], [["“I would rather you chose for yourself,”", "\"당신이 직접 고르시면 좋겠어요,\""], ["said Edith.", "에디스가 말했다."]], [["“Suppose then,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “we say from here.", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"여기서 보고 그리는 게 어떻겠습니까."], ["It appears a good spot for the purpose, or—Carker, what do you think?”", "그 목적에는 좋은 자리로 보이는데, 아니면—카커, 자넨 어떻게 생각하나?\""]], [["There happened to be in the foreground, at some little distance, a grove of trees, not unlike that in which Mr Carker had made his chain of footsteps in the morning, and with a seat under one tree, greatly resembling, in the general character of its situation, the point where his chain had broken.", "마침 조금 떨어진 전경에 작은 나무 숲이 하나 있었는데, 카커가 아침에 발자국의 사슬을 엮었던 그 숲과 사뭇 비슷했고, 한 나무 아래에 놓인 의자는, 그 위치의 전반적인 성격에 있어 그의 사슬이 끊어졌던 바로 그 지점과 무척이나 닮아 있었다."]], [["“Might I venture to suggest to Mrs Granger,”", "\"그레인저 부인께 한 말씀 감히 권해 드려도 될까요,\""], ["said Carker, “that that is an interesting—almost a curious—point of view?”", "카커가 말했다, \"저곳이 흥미로운—거의 진기하다 할 만한—경치입니다.\""]], [["She followed the direction of his riding-whip with her eyes, and raised them quickly to his face.", "그녀는 그의 승마 채찍이 가리키는 방향을 눈으로 좇았다가, 재빨리 그의 얼굴로 눈을 들었다."], ["It was the second glance they had exchanged since their introduction; and would have been exactly like the first, but that its expression was plainer.", "그것은 소개받은 이후 둘이 주고받은 두 번째 눈길이었고, 그 표정이 더 분명했다는 것만 빼면 첫 번째와 똑같았을 터였다."]], [["“Will you like that?”", "\"이게 마음에 드세요?\""], ["said Edith to Mr Dombey.", "에디스가 돔비 씨에게 말했다."]], [["“I shall be charmed,”", "\"황홀하겠습니다,\""], ["said Mr Dombey to Edith.", "돔비 씨가 에디스에게 말했다."]], [["Therefore the carriage was driven to the spot where Mr Dombey was to be charmed; and Edith, without moving from her seat, and opening her sketch-book with her usual proud indifference, began to sketch.", "그리하여 마차는 돔비 씨가 황홀해할 그 지점으로 몰렸고, 에디스는 자리에서 움직이지도 않고 평소의 도도한 무심함으로 스케치북을 펼치더니 그림을 그리기 시작했다."]], [["“My pencils are all pointless,”", "\"제 연필이 다 뭉툭하네요,\""], ["she said, stopping and turning them over.", "그녀가 멈추고 연필들을 뒤적이며 말했다."]], [["“Pray allow me,”", "\"부디 제게 맡기시지요,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“Or Carker will do it better, as he understands these things.", "\"아니면 카커가 더 잘할 겁니다, 이런 일을 잘 아니까요."], ["Carker, have the goodness to see to these pencils for Mrs Granger.”", "카커, 수고스럽지만 그레인저 부인의 연필들을 좀 봐 드리게.\""]], [["Mr Carker rode up close to the carriage-door on Mrs Granger’s side, and letting the rein fall on his horse’s neck, took the pencils from her hand with a smile and a bow, and sat in the saddle leisurely mending them.", "카커는 그레인저 부인 쪽 마차 문에 바싹 다가가 말을 세우고, 고삐를 말의 목덜미에 떨군 채, 미소와 절과 함께 그녀의 손에서 연필들을 받아 들고는, 안장에 앉아 느긋하게 그것들을 깎았다."], ["Having done so, he begged to be allowed to hold them, and to hand them to her as they were required; and thus Mr Carker, with many commendations of Mrs Granger’s extraordinary skill—especially in trees—remained—close at her side, looking over the drawing as she made it.", "그러고는 자기가 그것들을 들고 있다가 필요할 때마다 그녀에게 건네드리게 해 달라고 청했다. 그리하여 카커는, 그레인저 부인의 비범한 솜씨를—특히 나무를 그리는 솜씨를—여러 번 칭송하며, 그녀의 곁에 바싹 붙어, 그녀가 그림 그리는 것을 들여다보고 있었다."], ["Mr Dombey in the meantime stood bolt upright in the carriage like a highly respectable ghost, looking on too; while Cleopatra and the Major dallied as two ancient doves might do.", "그동안 돔비 씨는 더없이 점잖은 유령처럼 마차 안에 꼿꼿이 서서 역시 지켜보고 있었고, 한편 클레오파트라와 소령은 늙은 비둘기 두 마리가 그러하듯 시시덕거렸다."]], [["“Are you satisfied with that, or shall I finish it a little more?”", "\"이걸로 만족하시나요, 아니면 좀 더 마무리할까요?\""], ["said Edith, showing the sketch to Mr Dombey.", "에디스가 돔비 씨에게 스케치를 보여 주며 말했다."]], [["Mr Dombey begged that it might not be touched; it was perfection.", "돔비 씨는 손도 대지 말아 달라고 청했다. 그것은 완벽 그 자체였다."]], [["“It is most extraordinary,”", "\"참으로 놀랍습니다,\""], ["said Carker, bringing every one of his red gums to bear upon his praise.", "카커가 그 붉은 잇몸을 하나도 빠짐없이 동원하여 칭찬하며 말했다."], ["“I was not prepared for anything so beautiful, and so unusual altogether.”", "\"이토록 아름답고, 또 전체적으로 이토록 범상치 않은 것은 미처 예상치 못했습니다.\""]], [["This might have applied to the sketcher no less than to the sketch; but Mr Carker’s manner was openness itself—not as to his mouth alone, but as to his whole spirit.", "이 말은 그림에 못지않게 그림을 그린 사람에게도 해당될 법했다. 그러나 카커 씨의 태도는 솔직함 그 자체였으니—입만이 아니라 그 전 영혼이 그러했다."], ["So it continued to be while the drawing was laid aside for Mr Dombey, and while the sketching materials were put up; then he handed in the pencils (which were received with a distant acknowledgment of his help, but without a look), and tightening his rein, fell back, and followed the carriage again.", "그림이 돔비 씨를 위해 한쪽에 치워지고, 스케치 도구들이 챙겨지는 동안에도 그 태도는 그대로였다. 그러고 나서 그는 연필들을 건넸고(그것들은 그의 도움에 대한 데면데면한 인사와 함께, 그러나 눈길 한 번 없이 받아들여졌다), 고삐를 죄어 뒤로 물러나 다시 마차를 따랐다."]], [["Thinking, perhaps, as he rode, that even this trivial sketch had been made and delivered to its owner, as if it had been bargained for and bought.", "말을 달리며 어쩌면 이런 생각을 했을지도 모른다. 이 하찮은 스케치조차도 마치 흥정되어 사들여진 것인 양 그려져 그 주인에게 넘겨졌다고."], ["Thinking, perhaps, that although she had assented with such perfect readiness to his request, her haughty face, bent over the drawing, or glancing at the distant objects represented in it, had been the face of a proud woman, engaged in a sordid and miserable transaction.", "어쩌면 이런 생각을 했을지도. 비록 그녀가 그토록 선뜻 그의 청에 응하기는 했으나, 그림 위로 숙인 그 도도한 얼굴, 혹은 그림 속에 담긴 멀리 있는 대상들을 흘끗 보던 그 얼굴은, 추잡하고 비참한 거래에 매여 있는 도도한 여인의 얼굴이었다고."], ["Thinking, perhaps, of such things: but smiling certainly, and while he seemed to look about him freely, in enjoyment of the air and exercise, keeping always that sharp corner of his eye upon the carriage.", "어쩌면 그런 것들을 생각했을지도. 그러나 분명 미소를 짓고 있었고, 바람과 운동을 즐기며 거리낌 없이 주위를 둘러보는 듯하면서도, 그 날카로운 눈가는 언제나 마차에 두고 있었다."]], [["A stroll among the haunted ruins of Kenilworth, and more rides to more points of view: most of which, Mrs Skewton reminded Mr Dombey, Edith had already sketched, as he had seen in looking over her drawings: brought the day’s expedition to a close.", "케닐워스의 유령 깃든 폐허 사이를 거닐고, 더 많은 경승지로 더 많이 말을 달리고—그 대부분은, 스큐턴 부인이 돔비 씨에게 일러 주었듯이, 에디스가 이미 스케치해 둔 것들이어서, 그가 그녀의 그림들을 넘겨 보다가 본 것들이었다—그렇게 그날의 나들이는 막을 내렸다."], ["Mrs Skewton and Edith were driven to their own lodgings; Mr Carker was graciously invited by Cleopatra to return thither with Mr Dombey and the Major, in the evening, to hear some of Edith’s music; and the three gentlemen repaired to their hotel to dinner.", "스큐턴 부인과 에디스는 마차로 자기네 숙소까지 모셔졌고, 카커는 클레오파트라로부터 저녁에 돔비 씨와 소령과 함께 그곳으로 다시 와서 에디스의 음악을 좀 들으라는 정중한 초대를 받았다. 그리고 세 신사는 만찬을 위해 자기네 호텔로 돌아갔다."]], [["The dinner was the counterpart of yesterday’s, except that the Major was twenty-four hours more triumphant and less mysterious.", "만찬은 어제 것과 판박이였는데, 다만 소령이 스물네 시간만큼 더 의기양양하고 덜 알쏭달쏭했다는 점이 달랐다."], ["Edith was toasted again.", "에디스를 위해 다시 건배가 올랐다."], ["Mr Dombey was again agreeably embarrassed.", "돔비 씨는 다시 기분 좋게 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다."], ["And Mr Carker was full of interest and praise.", "그리고 카커는 흥미와 찬사로 가득했다."]], [["There were no other visitors at Mrs Skewton’s.", "스큐턴 부인 댁에는 다른 손님이 없었다."], ["Edith’s drawings were strewn about the room, a little more abundantly than usual perhaps; and Withers, the wan page, handed round a little stronger tea.", "에디스의 그림들이 방 안에 흩어져 있었는데, 어쩌면 평소보다 조금 더 푸짐했다. 그리고 핏기 없는 시동 위더스가 조금 더 진한 차를 돌렸다."], ["The harp was there; the piano was there; and Edith sang and played.", "하프가 거기 있었다. 피아노가 거기 있었다."], ["But even the music was played by Edith to Mr Dombey’s order, as it were, in the same uncompromising way.", "그리고 에디스는 노래하고 연주했다. 그러나 그 음악조차도 에디스가 돔비 씨의 분부에 따라, 말하자면, 똑같이 한 치도 양보하지 않는 방식으로 연주한 것이었다."], ["As thus.", "이런 식으로였다."]], [["“Edith, my dearest love,”", "\"에디스, 사랑하는 내 새끼,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, half an hour after tea, “Mr Dombey is dying to hear you, I know.”", "차를 마시고 반 시간쯤 지나 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"돔비 씨가 네 연주를 듣고 싶어 죽을 지경이실 거다, 난 알아.\""]], [["“Mr Dombey has life enough left to say so for himself, Mama, I have no doubt.”", "\"돔비 씨께서 그 정도는 직접 말씀하실 만큼의 기력은 남아 있으시겠지요, 엄마, 틀림없이요.\""]], [["“I shall be immensely obliged,”", "\"그래 주신다면 한없이 고맙겠습니다,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“What do you wish?”", "\"무얼 원하세요?\""]], [["“Piano?”", "\"피아노로?\""], ["hesitated Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“Whatever you please.", "\"무엇이든 좋으실 대로요."], ["You have only to choose.”", "그저 고르시기만 하면 돼요.\""]], [["Accordingly, she began with the piano.", "그리하여 그녀는 피아노로 시작했다."], ["It was the same with the harp; the same with her singing; the same with the selection of the pieces that she sang and played.", "하프도 마찬가지였고, 그녀의 노래도 마찬가지였으며, 그녀가 부르고 연주한 곡목의 선택도 마찬가지였다."], ["Such frigid and constrained, yet prompt and pointed acquiescence with the wishes he imposed upon her, and on no one else, was sufficiently remarkable to penetrate through all the mysteries of picquet, and impress itself on Mr Carker’s keen attention.", "그가 그녀에게만, 다른 누구에게도 아닌 그녀에게만 부과한 소망에 대한 이토록 차갑고 강요된, 그러면서도 즉각적이고 정확한 순응은, 피켓 놀이의 온갖 신경전을 뚫고 들어가 카커 씨의 예리한 주의에 새겨질 만큼 충분히 두드러진 것이었다."], ["Nor did he lose sight of the fact that Mr Dombey was evidently proud of his power, and liked to show it.", "또한 그는 돔비 씨가 자기 권력을 자랑스러워하며 그것을 과시하기를 좋아한다는 사실도 놓치지 않았다."]], [["Nevertheless, Mr Carker played so well—some games with the Major, and some with Cleopatra, whose vigilance of eye in respect of Mr Dombey and Edith no lynx could have surpassed—that he even heightened his position in the lady-mother’s good graces; and when on taking leave he regretted that he would be obliged to return to London next morning, Cleopatra trusted: community of feeling not being met with every day: that it was far from being the last time they would meet.", "그럼에도 카커는 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 굴었던지—몇 판은 소령과, 몇 판은 클레오파트라와 했는데, 돔비 씨와 에디스에 관한 그녀의 눈초리의 경계심은 어떤 스라소니도 능가하지 못했을 것이다—그는 심지어 그 어머니 되는 부인의 호감 속에서 자기 입지를 한층 더 높였다. 그리고 작별을 고하면서 내일 아침 런던으로 돌아가야 함을 아쉬워하자, 클레오파트라는—마음이 통하는 사람을 날마다 만나는 것은 아니므로—이것이 결코 마지막 만남이 아니기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["“I hope so,”", "\"저도 그러기를 바랍니다,\""], ["said Mr Carker, with an expressive look at the couple in the distance, as he drew towards the door, following the Major.", "카커가, 소령을 따라 문 쪽으로 다가가며, 멀찍이 있는 한 쌍을 의미심장하게 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I think so.”", "\"그러리라 생각합니다.\""]], [["Mr Dombey, who had taken a stately leave of Edith, bent, or made some approach to a bend, over Cleopatra’s couch, and said, in a low voice:", "에디스에게 위엄 있게 작별을 고한 돔비 씨는, 클레오파트라의 긴 의자 위로 몸을 굽혔다, 혹은 굽히는 시늉 비슷한 것을 하고는, 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“I have requested Mrs Granger’s permission to call on her to-morrow morning—for a purpose—and she has appointed twelve o’clock.", "\"제가 그레인저 부인께 내일 아침에 찾아뵐 허락을 청했는데요—어떤 목적으로 말입니다—그리고 부인께서 열두 시로 정해 주셨습니다."], ["May I hope to have the pleasure of finding you at home, Madam, afterwards?”", "그 뒤에 부인을 댁에서 뵐 기쁨을 누릴 수 있기를 바라도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["Cleopatra was so much fluttered and moved, by hearing this, of course, incomprehensible speech, that she could only shut her eyes, and shake her head, and give Mr Dombey her hand; which Mr Dombey, not exactly knowing what to do with, dropped.", "이 물론 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 말을 듣고 클레오파트라는 어찌나 마음이 들뜨고 동요했던지, 그저 눈을 감고, 고개를 가로젓고, 돔비 씨에게 손을 내미는 수밖에 없었는데, 돔비 씨는 그것을 어찌해야 할지 정확히 알지 못해 그냥 놓아 버렸다."]], [["“Dombey, come along!”", "\"돔비, 어서 갑시다!\""], ["cried the Major, looking in at the door.", "소령이 문 안을 들여다보며 외쳤다."], ["“Damme, Sir, old Joe has a great mind to propose an alteration in the name of the Royal Hotel, and that it should be called the Three Jolly Bachelors, in honour of ourselves and Carker.”", "\"제기랄, 선생, 이 늙은 조는 로열 호텔의 이름을 바꾸자고 제안하고 싶어 죽겠소이다, 우리 자신과 카커를 기리는 뜻으로 '유쾌한 세 독신남'이라고 부르자고 말이오.\""], ["With this, the Major slapped Mr Dombey on the back, and winking over his shoulder at the ladies, with a frightful tendency of blood to the head, carried him off.", "이렇게 말하며 소령은 돔비 씨의 등을 철썩 치고는, 어깨 너머로 부인들에게 윙크를 했는데, 무시무시하게 피가 머리로 쏠리는 기색으로, 그를 데리고 나가 버렸다."]], [["Mrs Skewton reposed on her sofa, and Edith sat apart, by her harp, in silence.", "스큐턴 부인은 소파에 기대 누웠고, 에디스는 떨어져 앉아, 자기 하프 곁에서 말없이 있었다."], ["The mother, trifling with her fan, looked stealthily at the daughter more than once, but the daughter, brooding gloomily with downcast eyes, was not to be disturbed.", "어머니는 부채를 만지작거리며 한 번 이상 딸을 몰래 훔쳐보았으나, 눈을 내리깐 채 음울하게 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 딸은 방해할 수 없는 모습이었다."]], [["Thus they remained for a long hour, without a word, until Mrs Skewton’s maid appeared, according to custom, to prepare her gradually for night.", "그렇게 그들은 한 마디 말도 없이 긴 한 시간을 머물러 있었는데, 마침내 스큐턴 부인의 하녀가 관례에 따라, 그녀를 차츰차츰 밤을 위해 채비시키려고 나타났다. 밤이 되면 이 시중드는 여인은 여자라기보다 차라리 화살과 모래시계를 든 해골이 되어 마땅했으니, 그 손길이 죽음의 손길과 같았기 때문이다."], ["At night, she should have been a skeleton, with dart and hour-glass, rather than a woman, this attendant; for her touch was as the touch of Death.", "그 손 아래에서 그 채색된 물건은 쪼그라들었다. 형체가 무너지고, 머리카락이 떨어져 나가고, 아치 모양의 검은 눈썹은 듬성듬성한 잿빛 솜털로 변했다."], ["The painted object shrivelled underneath her hand; the form collapsed, the hair dropped off, the arched dark eyebrows changed to scanty tufts of grey; the pale lips shrunk, the skin became cadaverous and loose; an old, worn, yellow, nodding woman, with red eyes, alone remained in Cleopatra’s place, huddled up, like a slovenly bundle, in a greasy flannel gown.", "창백한 입술은 오그라들고, 살갗은 송장 같고 축 늘어졌다. 늙고, 닳고, 누렇고, 꾸벅거리는, 붉은 눈의 여인 하나만이 클레오파트라의 자리에 홀로 남아, 기름때 낀 플란넬 가운을 걸친 채 지저분한 보따리처럼 웅크리고 있었다."]], [["The very voice was changed, as it addressed Edith, when they were alone again.", "둘만 다시 남았을 때 에디스에게 건네는 그 목소리마저 변해 있었다."]], [["“Why don’t you tell me,”", "\"왜 나한테 말 안 했니,\""], ["it said sharply, “that he is coming here to-morrow by appointment?”", "그것이 날카롭게 말했다, \"그 사람이 내일 약속을 잡고 여기 온다는 걸 말이야?\""]], [["“Because you know it,”", "\"엄마는 알고 있으니까요,\""], ["returned Edith, “Mother.”", "에디스가 대꾸했다, \"엄마.\""]], [["The mocking emphasis she laid on that one word!", "그 한 단어에 그녀가 실은 그 비웃는 듯한 강세라니!"]], [["“You know he has bought me,”", "\"엄마도 알다시피 그 사람은 날 산 거예요,\""], ["she resumed.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“Or that he will, to-morrow.", "\"아니면 내일 살 거고요."], ["He has considered of his bargain; he has shown it to his friend; he is even rather proud of it; he thinks that it will suit him, and may be had sufficiently cheap; and he will buy to-morrow.", "그 사람은 자기 거래를 곰곰이 따져 봤고, 친구한테 보여 줬으며, 심지어 그걸 꽤 자랑스러워해요. 그 사람은 그게 자기한테 어울리겠다고, 충분히 싼값에 손에 넣을 수 있겠다고 생각해요."], ["God, that I have lived for this, and that I feel it!”", "그래서 내일 살 거예요. 하느님, 제가 이러려고 살아왔다니, 그리고 그걸 제가 느끼고 있다니!\""]], [["Compress into one handsome face the conscious self-abasement, and the burning indignation of a hundred women, strong in passion and in pride; and there it hid itself with two white shuddering arms.", "격정과 자존심으로 강인한 백 명의 여인이 지닌, 의식적인 자기 비하와 타오르는 분노를 한 폭의 아름다운 얼굴 속에 응축해 보라. 그것이 바로 거기, 부들부들 떠는 두 흰 팔 속에 자신을 숨겼다."]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 소리니?\""], ["returned the angry mother.", "화가 난 어머니가 대꾸했다."], ["“Haven’t you from a child—”", "\"넌 어릴 적부터—\""]], [["“A child!”", "\"어릴 적이라고요!\""], ["said Edith, looking at her, “when was I a child?", "에디스가 그녀를 바라보며 말했다, \"제게 어릴 적이 언제 있었나요?"], ["What childhood did you ever leave to me?", "엄마가 제게 무슨 어린 시절을 남겨 줬어요?"], ["I was a woman—artful, designing, mercenary, laying snares for men—before I knew myself, or you, or even understood the base and wretched aim of every new display I learnt You gave birth to a woman.", "저는 여자였어요—교활하고, 술수에 능하고, 돈만 밝히며, 남자들에게 덫을 놓는—제 자신을, 엄마를, 아니 제가 배운 새로운 재주의 그 천하고 비참한 목적조차 알기도 전에 말이에요. 엄마가 한 여자를 낳았어요."], ["Look upon her.", "그 여자를 보세요."], ["She is in her pride tonight.”", "오늘 밤 그 여자는 한껏 도도하군요.\""]], [["And as she spoke, she struck her hand upon her beautiful bosom, as though she would have beaten down herself.", "그렇게 말하며 그녀는 마치 자기 자신을 후려쳐 쓰러뜨리기라도 하려는 듯, 그 아름다운 가슴을 손으로 쳤다."]], [["“Look at me,”", "\"날 봐요,\""], ["she said, “who have never known what it is to have an honest heart, and love.", "그녀가 말했다, \"정직한 마음을, 그리고 사랑을 가진다는 게 어떤 건지 한 번도 알아 본 적 없는 이 여자를요."], ["Look at me, taught to scheme and plot when children play; and married in my youth—an old age of design—to one for whom I had no feeling but indifference.", "날 봐요, 아이들이 노는 나이에 술수를 부리고 음모를 꾸미도록 배웠고, 젊은 나이에—이미 늙어 빠진 계략 속에서—제겐 무관심 외에 아무 감정도 없는 사람에게 시집간 이 여자를요."], ["Look at me, whom he left a widow, dying before his inheritance descended to him—a judgment on you!", "날 봐요, 그 사람이 자기 유산을 물려받기도 전에 죽어 버려 저를 과부로 남긴—엄마에 대한 천벌이지요!"], ["well deserved!—and tell me what has been my life for ten years since.”", "마땅한 벌이고요!—그 여자를요. 그리고 그 이후 십 년간 제 삶이 어땠는지 말해 보세요.\""]], [["“We have been making every effort to endeavour to secure to you a good establishment,”", "\"우리는 너한테 좋은 자리를 마련해 주려고 온갖 애를 써 왔어,\" 그녀의 어머니가 응수했다."], ["rejoined her mother.", "\"그게 네 삶이었지."], ["“That has been your life. And now you have got it.”", "그리고 이제 넌 그걸 손에 넣었고.\""]], [["“There is no slave in a market: there is no horse in a fair: so shown and offered and examined and paraded, Mother, as I have been, for ten shameful years,”", "\"시장의 어떤 노예도, 장터의 어떤 말도, 제가 그래 왔던 것만큼 이렇게 내보여지고 내놓아지고 살펴지고 행진당하지는 않았어요, 엄마, 십 년이라는 부끄러운 세월 동안 말이에요,\""], ["cried Edith, with a burning brow, and the same bitter emphasis on the one word.", "에디스가 타오르는 이마로, 그 한 단어에 똑같이 쓰디쓴 강세를 실어 외쳤다."], ["“Is it not so?", "\"그렇지 않아요?"], ["Have I been made the bye-word of all kinds of men?", "제가 온갖 부류의 남자들의 입방아거리가 되지 않았나요?"], ["Have fools, have profligates, have boys, have dotards, dangled after me, and one by one rejected me, and fallen off, because you were too plain with all your cunning: yes, and too true, with all those false pretences: until we have almost come to be notorious?", "바보들이, 난봉꾼들이, 풋내기들이, 망령 든 늙은이들이 제 꽁무니를 쫓아다니다가, 하나씩 하나씩 절 퇴짜 놓고 떨어져 나가지 않았나요. 엄마가 그 온갖 술수를 부리면서도 너무 속이 빤히 들여다보였고, 그 온갖 거짓 핑계를 대면서도 너무 속이 들여다보여서요."], ["The licence of look and touch,”", "그러다 우리가 거의 악명을 떨치게 될 지경에 이르렀잖아요?"], ["she said, with flashing eyes, “have I submitted to it, in half the places of resort upon the map of England?", "눈으로 더듬고 손으로 만지는 그 방종을,\" 그녀가 번득이는 눈으로 말했다, \"제가 잉글랜드 지도 위 휴양지의 절반에서 다 받아 내지 않았나요?"], ["Have I been hawked and vended here and there, until the last grain of self-respect is dead within me, and I loathe myself?", "제 안의 마지막 한 톨의 자존심마저 죽어 버리고, 제가 제 자신을 혐오하게 될 때까지, 제가 여기저기서 호객당하고 팔려 다니지 않았나요? 그게 제 뒤늦은 어린 시절이었나요?"], ["Has been my late childhood?", "그 전엔 어린 시절이 아예 없었고요."], ["I had none before. Do not tell me that I had, tonight of all nights in my life!”", "제게 어린 시절이 있었다는 말은 하지 마세요, 제 평생의 그 어느 밤도 아닌 바로 오늘 밤에는요!\""]], [["“You might have been well married,”", "\"넌 좋은 곳에 시집갈 수도 있었어,\""], ["said her mother, “twenty times at least, Edith, if you had given encouragement enough.”", "그녀의 어머니가 말했다, \"적어도 스무 번은 말이야, 에디스, 네가 좀 더 부추기기만 했더라면.\""]], [["“No! Who takes me, refuse that I am, and as I well deserve to be,”", "\"안 돼요!"], ["she answered, raising her head, and trembling in her energy of shame and stormy pride, “shall take me, as this man does, with no art of mine put forth to lure him.", "폐물이나 다름없는 저를, 그리고 제가 마땅히 그래야 할 그런 저를 데려갈 사람은,\" 그녀가 고개를 들고, 부끄러움과 격한 자존심의 격정에 떨며 대답했다, \"이 사람이 그러는 것처럼, 그를 꾀려고 제가 부린 어떤 술수도 없이 저를 데려가야 해요."], ["He sees me at the auction, and he thinks it well to buy me.", "그는 경매에서 저를 보고, 저를 사 두는 게 좋겠다고 생각하지요."], ["Let him!", "그러라죠!"], ["When he came to view me—perhaps to bid—he required to see the roll of my accomplishments.", "그가 저를 살펴보러—어쩌면 값을 부르러—왔을 때, 제 재주의 목록을 보여 달라고 요구했어요."], ["I gave it to him.", "저는 그걸 줬어요."], ["When he would have me show one of them, to justify his purchase to his men, I require of him to say which he demands, and I exhibit it.", "그가 자기 부하들에게 자기 매입을 정당화하려고 그중 하나를 보여 달라고 하면, 저는 그에게 어느 것을 원하는지 말하라고 요구하고, 그러면 그걸 펼쳐 보이지요."], ["I will do no more.", "그 이상은 하지 않을 거예요."], ["He makes the purchase of his own will, and with his own sense of its worth, and the power of his money; and I hope it may never disappoint him.", "그는 자기 뜻대로, 자기 나름의 그 값어치에 대한 판단과 자기 돈의 힘으로 그 매입을 하는 거고요. 그게 그를 결코 실망시키지 않기를 바라요."], ["I have not vaunted and pressed the bargain; neither have you, so far as I have been able to prevent you.", "저는 그 거래를 떠벌리거나 밀어붙인 적 없어요. 엄마도 안 그랬고요, 제가 막을 수 있는 한에서는요.\""]], [["“You talk strangely tonight, Edith, to your own Mother.”", "\"넌 오늘 밤 이상하게 말하는구나, 에디스, 네 친엄마한테 말이다.\""]], [["“It seems so to me; stranger to me than you,”", "\"제게도 그렇게 느껴져요. 엄마보다 제게 더 낯설게요,\""], ["said Edith.", "에디스가 말했다."], ["“But my education was completed long ago.", "\"하지만 제 교육은 오래전에 끝났어요."], ["I am too old now, and have fallen too low, by degrees, to take a new course, and to stop yours, and to help myself.", "저는 이제 너무 나이 들었고, 차츰차츰 너무 낮게 떨어져 버려서, 새로운 길을 가거나, 엄마의 길을 멈추게 하거나, 제 자신을 도울 수가 없어요."], ["The germ of all that purifies a woman’s breast, and makes it true and good, has never stirred in mine, and I have nothing else to sustain me when I despise myself.”", "여자의 가슴을 정화하고 그것을 진실되고 선하게 만드는 모든 것의 싹이, 제 안에서는 한 번도 꿈틀거린 적 없고, 그래서 제 자신을 경멸할 때 저를 지탱해 줄 다른 무엇도 제겐 없어요.\""], ["There had been a touching sadness in her voice, but it was gone, when she went on to say, with a curled lip, “So, as we are genteel and poor, I am content that we should be made rich by these means; all I say is, I have kept the only purpose I have had the strength to form—I had almost said the power, with you at my side, Mother—and have not tempted this man on.”", "그녀의 목소리에는 가슴을 울리는 슬픔이 어려 있었으나, 입술을 비죽이며 말을 이을 때 그것은 사라지고 없었다, \"그러니, 우리가 점잖으면서도 가난하니까, 저는 우리가 이런 수단으로 부유해지는 것에 만족해요. 제가 하고 싶은 말은 오직, 제가 짜낼 힘이 있었던 단 하나의 목적을—거의 능력이라고까지 말할 뻔했네요, 엄마가 제 곁에 있는데도요—지켜 왔다는 것, 그리고 이 사람을 꾀지 않았다는 거예요.\""]], [["“This man!", "\"이 사람이라니!"], ["You speak,”", "넌 말하는 게,\""], ["said her mother, “as if you hated him.”", "그녀의 어머니가 말했다, \"마치 그를 미워하기라도 하는 것 같구나.\""]], [["“And you thought I loved him, did you not?”", "\"그리고 엄만 제가 그를 사랑한다고 생각했죠, 안 그래요?\""], ["she answered, stopping on her way across the room, and looking round.", "그녀가 방을 가로질러 가던 길을 멈추고 둘러보며 대답했다."], ["“Shall I tell you,”", "\"엄마한테 말해 드릴까요,\""], ["she continued, with her eyes fixed on her mother, “who already knows us thoroughly, and reads us right, and before whom I have even less of self-respect or confidence than before my own inward self; being so much degraded by his knowledge of me?”", "그녀가 어머니에게 눈을 고정한 채 말을 이었다, \"이미 우리를 속속들이 알고, 우리를 정확히 꿰뚫어 보며, 제 자신의 내면의 자아 앞에서보다도 그 앞에서 제가 자존심도 자신감도 더 적게 느끼는 사람이 누군지요? 그가 저를 너무 잘 알아서 그토록 비참하게 비참해진 까닭에요.\""]], [["“This is an attack, I suppose,”", "\"이건 아마 공격이로구나,\""], ["returned her mother coldly, “on poor, unfortunate what’s-his-name—Mr Carker!", "그녀의 어머니가 차갑게 응수했다, \"그 가엾고 불운한 그 뭐냐—카커 씨에 대한 말이지!"], ["Your want of self-respect and confidence, my dear, in reference to that person (who is very agreeable, it strikes me), is not likely to have much effect on your establishment.", "그 사람에 관한 한(꽤 호감 가는 사람으로 보이던데) 네 자존심이나 자신감의 결핍 따위는, 얘야, 네 혼사에 별 영향을 끼치지는 않을 거다."], ["Why do you look at me so hard?", "왜 날 그렇게 빤히 노려보니?"], ["Are you ill?”", "어디 아픈 거냐?\""]], [["Edith suddenly let fall her face, as if it had been stung, and while she pressed her hands upon it, a terrible tremble crept over her whole frame.", "에디스는 마치 무언가에 쏘이기라도 한 듯 갑자기 얼굴을 떨구었고, 두 손으로 얼굴을 누르는 동안, 끔찍한 전율이 온몸으로 기어올랐다."], ["It was quickly gone; and with her usual step, she passed out of the room.", "그것은 금세 사라졌다. 그러고는 평소의 걸음걸이로 그녀는 방을 나섰다."]], [["The maid who should have been a skeleton, then reappeared, and giving one arm to her mistress, who appeared to have taken off her manner with her charms, and to have put on paralysis with her flannel gown, collected the ashes of Cleopatra, and carried them away in the other, ready for tomorrow’s revivification.", "해골이 되어 마땅했던 그 하녀가 그제야 다시 나타나, 자기 안주인에게—매력과 함께 그 거동도 벗어 버리고, 플란넬 가운과 함께 중풍이라도 걸친 듯한 안주인에게—한쪽 팔을 내주고, 클레오파트라의 잿더미를 그러모아 다른 팔에 안고 데리고 나갔으니, 내일의 소생(蘇生)을 위한 채비였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVIII. Alterations", "# CHAPTER XXVIII. Alterations"]], [["So the day has come at length, Susan,”", "드디어 그날이 왔어요, 수전,\""], ["said Florence to the excellent Nipper, “when we are going back to our quiet home!”", "플로렌스가 훌륭한 니퍼에게 말했다. \"우리가 그 조용한 집으로 돌아가는 날 말이에요!\""]], [["Susan drew in her breath with an amount of expression not easily described, further relieving her feelings with a smart cough, answered, “Very quiet indeed, Miss Floy, no doubt.", "수전은 쉽게 표현하기 어려운 정도의 의미를 담아 숨을 들이쉬더니, 날카로운 헛기침으로 제 감정을 더 풀어내고 나서 대답했다. \"정말 무척이나 조용하지요, 플로이 아가씨, 의심할 여지가 없어요."], ["Excessive so.”", "지나치다 싶을 만큼요.\""]], [["“When I was a child,”", "\"내가 어렸을 때,\""], ["said Florence, thoughtfully, and after musing for some moments, “did you ever see that gentleman who has taken the trouble to ride down here to speak to me, now three times—three times, I think, Susan?”", "플로렌스가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다. \"나한테 말을 하려고 일부러 여기까지 말을 타고 내려오는 수고를 한 그 신사분을, 이제 세 번이나—세 번이었지, 아마, 수전?—본 적이 있니?\""]], [["“Three times, Miss,”", "\"세 번이에요, 아가씨,\""], ["returned the Nipper.", "니퍼가 대답했다."], ["“Once when you was out a walking with them Sket—”", "\"한 번은 아가씨가 그 스켓 어쩌고 하는 사람들과 산책을 나가셨을 때—\""]], [["Florence gently looked at her, and Miss Nipper checked herself.", "플로렌스가 가만히 그녀를 바라보자, 니퍼 양은 말을 멈췄다."]], [["“With Sir Barnet and his lady, I mean to say, Miss, and the young gentleman.", "\"바넷 경 내외분과 함께였다는 뜻이에요, 아가씨, 그리고 그 젊은 도련님하고요."], ["And two evenings since then.”", "그리고 그 뒤로 두 번의 저녁 때였고요.\""]], [["“When I was a child, and when company used to come to visit Papa, did you ever see that gentleman at home, Susan?”", "\"내가 어렸을 때, 그리고 손님들이 아빠를 찾아오곤 했을 때, 그 신사분을 집에서 본 적이 있니, 수전?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“Well, Miss,”", "\"글쎄요, 아가씨,\""], ["returned her maid, after considering, “I really couldn’t say I ever did.", "하녀가 한참 생각한 끝에 대답했다. \"정말이지 본 적이 있다고는 말씀 못 드리겠어요."], ["When your poor dear Ma died, Miss Floy, I was very new in the family, you see, and my element:”", "가엾게도 사랑스러운 어머님께서 돌아가셨을 때, 플로이 아가씨, 저는 그 댁에 들어온 지 얼마 안 된 신참이었거든요, 아시잖아요. 그리고 제가 있던 자리는,\""], ["the Nipper bridled, as opining that her merits had been always designedly extinguished by Mr Dombey: “was the floor below the attics.”", "니퍼는 자기 공로를 돔비 씨가 늘 일부러 묻어 버렸다고 여기는 듯 거드름을 피웠다. \"다락방 바로 아래층이었어요.\""]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"하긴 그렇지,\""], ["said Florence, still thoughtfully; “you are not likely to have known who came to the house.", "플로렌스가 여전히 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"네가 누가 집에 드나들었는지 알았을 리가 없겠구나."], ["I quite forgot.”", "깜빡했어.\""]], [["“Not, Miss, but what we talked about the family and visitors,” said Susan, “and but what I heard much said, although the nurse before Mrs Richards make unpleasant remarks when I was in company, and hint at little Pitchers, but that could only be attributed, poor thing,” observed Susan, with composed forbearance, “to habits of intoxication, for which she was required to leave, and did.”", "\"그렇긴 해도, 아가씨, 저희끼리 그 댁 식구들이며 손님들 얘기를 안 한 건 아니었어요,\" 수전이 말했다. \"그리고 이런저런 말을 많이 들었답니다. 리처즈 부인 전에 있던 유모가, 제가 사람들 틈에 끼어 있으면 듣기 싫은 소리를 하면서 '작은 항아리도 귀가 있다'느니 어쩌니 빈정대긴 했지만요. 하지만 그건 다, 가엾은 사람,\" 수전이 차분히 너그러운 태도로 말했다. \"술버릇 탓으로밖에 볼 수 없는 일이었어요. 그 술버릇 때문에 그만두라는 말을 들었고, 결국 그만뒀으니까요.\""]], [["Florence, who was seated at her chamber window, with her face resting on her hand, sat looking out, and hardly seemed to hear what Susan said, she was so lost in thought.", "플로렌스는 침실 창가에 앉아 한 손에 얼굴을 기댄 채 밖을 내다보고 있었는데, 어찌나 생각에 잠겨 있던지 수전이 하는 말이 거의 귀에 들어오지 않는 듯했다."]], [["“At all events, Miss,”", "\"어쨌든 말이에요, 아가씨,\" 수전이 말했다."], ["said Susan, “I remember very well that this same gentleman, Mr Carker, was almost, if not quite, as great a gentleman with your Papa then, as he is now.", "\"저는 아주 잘 기억해요. 이 카커 씨라는 분이 그때도 지금 못지않게, 아니 거의 그만큼 아가씨 아빠한테 대단한 신사 노릇을 했다는 걸요."], ["It used to be said in the house then, Miss, that he was at the head of all your Pa’s affairs in the City, and managed the whole, and that your Pa minded him more than anybody, which, begging your pardon, Miss Floy, he might easy do, for he never minded anybody else.", "그때 집안에서는 그 양반이 시티에 있는 아빠 일을 죄다 도맡아서 전부 관리하고, 아빠가 누구보다도 그 양반 말을 잘 들으신다는 얘기가 돌았어요. 죄송하지만, 플로이 아가씨, 그거야 어렵지 않은 일이었죠, 아빠는 다른 누구 말도 도통 안 들으셨으니까요."], ["I knew that, Pitcher as I might have been.”", "저는 그걸 알았어요, 제가 '항아리'였을지언정요.\""]], [["Susan Nipper, with an injured remembrance of the nurse before Mrs Richards, emphasised “Pitcher” strongly.", "수전 니퍼는 리처즈 부인 전에 있던 유모에 대한 상처 입은 기억을 떠올리며 '항아리'라는 말에 힘껏 힘을 주었다."]], [["“And that Mr Carker has not fallen off, Miss,” she pursued, “but has stood his ground, and kept his credit with your Pa, I know from what is always said among our people by that Perch, whenever he comes to the house; and though he’s the weakest weed in the world, Miss Floy, and no one can have a moment’s patience with the man, he knows what goes on in the City tolerable well, and says that your Pa does nothing without Mr Carker, and leaves all to Mr Carker, and acts according to Mr Carker, and has Mr Carker always at his elbow, and I do believe that he believes (that washiest of Perches!) that after your Pa, the Emperor of India is the child unborn to Mr Carker.”", "\"그리고 카커 씨가 떨어져 나가기는커녕, 아가씨,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"자기 자리를 굳건히 지키고 아빠의 신임을 계속 받고 있다는 건, 저 퍼치라는 작자가 이 집에 올 때마다 저희 식구들 사이에서 늘 하는 얘기로 알고 있어요. 그 사람이 세상에서 제일 줏대 없는 잡초 같은 인간이라, 플로이 아가씨, 아무도 그 사람을 한순간도 못 견뎌 하긴 하지만요, 시티에서 무슨 일이 돌아가는지는 꽤 잘 알거든요. 그 사람 말로는, 아가씨 아빠가 카커 씨 없이는 아무것도 안 하시고, 모든 걸 카커 씨한테 맡기시고, 카커 씨가 하라는 대로 하시고, 카커 씨를 늘 곁에 두신다는 거예요. 그래서 제 생각엔 그 사람이(그 더없이 멀건 퍼치가요!) 아가씨 아빠 다음으로는 인도 황제도 카커 씨한테 비하면 아직 태어나지도 않은 갓난애나 다름없다고 믿는 것 같아요.\""]], [["Not a word of this was lost on Florence, who, with an awakened interest in Susan’s speech, no longer gazed abstractedly on the prospect without, but looked at her, and listened with attention.", "이 말 가운데 한마디도 플로렌스에게서 놓치지 않았으니, 그녀는 수전의 이야기에 흥미가 깨어나, 더 이상 바깥 풍경을 멍하니 바라보지 않고 그녀를 쳐다보며 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Yes, Susan,”", "\"그래, 수전,\""], ["she said, when that young lady had concluded.", "그 젊은 아가씨가 이야기를 마치자 그녀가 말했다."], ["“He is in Papa’s confidence, and is his friend, I am sure.”", "\"그분은 아빠의 신임을 받고 계셔. 그리고 아빠의 친구야, 분명히.\""]], [["Florence’s mind ran high on this theme, and had done for some days.", "이 화제에 대한 플로렌스의 마음은 며칠째 높이 일렁이고 있었다."], ["Mr Carker, in the two visits with which he had followed up his first one, had assumed a confidence between himself and her—a right on his part to be mysterious and stealthy, in telling her that the ship was still unheard of—a kind of mildly restrained power and authority over her—that made her wonder, and caused her great uneasiness.", "카커 씨는 처음 찾아온 데 이어 두 번 더 방문하면서, 자기와 그녀 사이에 어떤 은밀한 친분이 있는 것처럼—그 배의 소식이 여전히 들리지 않는다고 전하면서 자기 쪽에서 무언가 신비롭고 은근하게 굴 권리가 있는 것처럼—그녀에 대해 일종의 부드럽게 절제된 힘과 권위를 지닌 것처럼 행세했고, 그것은 그녀를 의아하게 만들고 큰 불안을 안겨 주었다."], ["She had no means of repelling it, or of freeing herself from the web he was gradually winding about her; for that would have required some art and knowledge of the world, opposed to such address as his; and Florence had none.", "그녀로서는 그것을 물리치거나, 그가 차츰 그녀 주위에 둘러치는 거미줄에서 빠져나올 방도가 없었다. 그러려면 그의 그런 능란함에 맞설 만한 약간의 기교와 세상 물정에 대한 지식이 필요했을 텐데, 플로렌스에게는 그런 것이 전혀 없었다."], ["True, he had said no more to her than that there was no news of the ship, and that he feared the worst; but how he came to know that she was interested in the ship, and why he had the right to signify his knowledge to her, so insidiously and darkly, troubled Florence very much.", "그가 그녀에게 한 말이라곤 배의 소식이 없다는 것, 그리고 최악의 경우를 우려한다는 것뿐이었으나, 그가 어떻게 그녀가 그 배에 마음을 쓰고 있다는 것을 알게 되었는지, 또 무슨 권리로 자기가 그것을 안다는 사실을 그토록 음흉하고 어둡게 그녀에게 내비치는지가 플로렌스를 몹시 괴롭혔다."]], [["This conduct on the part of Mr Carker, and her habit of often considering it with wonder and uneasiness, began to invest him with an uncomfortable fascination in Florence’s thoughts.", "카커 씨의 이런 행동, 그리고 그것을 의아함과 불안 속에 거듭 곱씹는 그녀의 버릇은, 플로렌스의 생각 속에서 그를 불편한 매혹으로 둘러싸기 시작했다."], ["A more distinct remembrance of his features, voice, and manner: which she sometimes courted, as a means of reducing him to the level of a real personage, capable of exerting no greater charm over her than another: did not remove the vague impression.", "그를 자기에게 다른 누구보다 더한 마력을 행사할 수 없는 실재의 인물 수준으로 끌어내리는 한 방편으로서, 그녀가 이따금 일부러 떠올려 보는 그의 이목구비와 목소리와 태도에 대한 더 또렷한 기억조차도, 그 어렴풋한 인상을 지워 내지는 못했다."], ["And yet he never frowned, or looked upon her with an air of dislike or animosity, but was always smiling and serene.", "그런데도 그는 결코 눈살을 찌푸리거나 싫어하거나 적의 어린 기색으로 그녀를 바라보는 일이 없었고, 늘 미소 짓고 평온했다."]], [["Again, Florence, in pursuit of her strong purpose with reference to her father, and her steady resolution to believe that she was herself unwittingly to blame for their so cold and distant relations, would recall to mind that this gentleman was his confidential friend, and would think, with an anxious heart, could her struggling tendency to dislike and fear him be a part of that misfortune in her, which had turned her father’s love adrift, and left her so alone?", "또한 플로렌스는 아버지에 대해 품은 그 강한 결심을 좇으며, 그리고 두 사람의 그토록 차갑고 서먹한 관계가 자기도 모르게 자기 잘못 때문이라고 굳게 믿으려는 한결같은 결의로, 이 신사가 아버지의 막역한 친구라는 사실을 떠올리고는 불안한 마음으로 생각하곤 했다. 그를 싫어하고 두려워하려는 이 안간힘 같은 성향이, 아버지의 사랑을 떠내려가게 하고 자기를 이토록 외롭게 만든 그 자기 안의 불행의 한 부분은 아닐까?"], ["She dreaded that it might be; sometimes believed it was: then she resolved that she would try to conquer this wrong feeling; persuaded herself that she was honoured and encouraged by the notice of her father’s friend; and hoped that patient observation of him and trust in him would lead her bleeding feet along that stony road which ended in her father’s heart.", "그녀는 그럴까 봐 두려웠고, 때로는 그렇다고 믿었다. 그러다가는 이 그릇된 감정을 정복해 보리라 마음먹고, 아버지의 친구가 자기를 눈여겨봐 주는 것이 자기에게는 영광이고 격려라고 스스로를 타일렀으며, 그를 참을성 있게 살피고 그를 신뢰하다 보면 자기의 피 흘리는 발걸음이 아버지의 마음으로 이어지는 그 돌투성이 길을 따라 나아갈 수 있으리라 바랐다."]], [["Thus, with no one to advise her—for she could advise with no one without seeming to complain against him—gentle Florence tossed on an uneasy sea of doubt and hope; and Mr Carker, like a scaly monster of the deep, swam down below, and kept his shining eye upon her.", "이렇게, 조언을 구할 사람도 하나 없이—그에 대해 불평하는 꼴이 되지 않고서는 누구와도 의논할 수 없었으므로—온순한 플로렌스는 의심과 희망의 불안한 바다 위에서 이리저리 흔들렸다. 그리고 카커 씨는 심해의 비늘 덮인 괴물처럼 그 아래를 헤엄치며, 번뜩이는 눈을 그녀에게서 떼지 않았다."]], [["Florence had a new reason in all this for wishing to be at home again.", "플로렌스에게는 이 모든 것 속에 다시 집으로 돌아가고 싶은 새로운 이유가 있었다."], ["Her lonely life was better suited to her course of timid hope and doubt; and she feared sometimes, that in her absence she might miss some hopeful chance of testifying her affection for her father.", "그녀의 외로운 생활은 소심한 희망과 의심의 그 행로에 더 잘 어울렸다. 그리고 그녀는 이따금, 자기가 자리를 비운 사이에 아버지에 대한 애정을 드러낼 어떤 희망찬 기회를 놓칠지도 모른다고 두려워했다."], ["Heaven knows, she might have set her mind at rest, poor child!", "가엾은 아이여, 하늘은 알리라, 그녀가 이 마지막 점에 대해서는 마음을 놓아도 좋았으련만!"], ["on this last point; but her slighted love was fluttering within her, and, even in her sleep, it flew away in dreams, and nestled, like a wandering bird come home, upon her father’s neck.", "그러나 그녀의 푸대접받은 사랑은 그녀 안에서 파닥거리고 있었으니, 잠든 중에도 그것은 꿈속에서 날아올라, 집으로 돌아온 길 잃은 새처럼 아버지의 목에 깃들었다."]], [["Of Walter she thought often.", "월터에 대해서는 자주 생각했다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["how often, when the night was gloomy, and the wind was blowing round the house!", "밤이 음울하고 바람이 집 둘레로 윙윙 불 때면 얼마나 자주 생각했던가!"], ["But hope was strong in her breast.", "그러나 희망은 그녀의 가슴속에서 강했다."], ["It is so difficult for the young and ardent, even with such experience as hers, to imagine youth and ardour quenched like a weak flame, and the bright day of life merging into night, at noon, that hope was strong yet.", "젊고 열정적인 이에게는, 그녀만큼의 경험을 가졌더라도, 젊음과 열정이 가냘픈 불꽃처럼 꺼지고 삶의 환한 대낮이 한낮에 밤으로 잠겨 드는 것을 상상하기란 너무도 어려운 일이어서, 희망은 아직 강했다."], ["Her tears fell frequently for Walter’s sufferings; but rarely for his supposed death, and never long.", "그녀의 눈물은 월터의 고통을 위해 자주 흘렀으나, 그가 죽었으리라는 짐작 때문에 흘린 적은 드물었고, 오래간 적도 결코 없었다."]], [["She had written to the old Instrument-maker, but had received no answer to her note: which indeed required none.", "그녀는 그 늙은 기구 제작자에게 편지를 썼으나 답장을 받지 못했다. 사실 답장이 필요한 편지도 아니었다."], ["Thus matters stood with Florence on the morning when she was going home, gladly, to her old secluded life.", "집으로, 그 옛 은둔의 생활로 기쁘게 돌아가던 그 아침의 플로렌스의 사정이 이러했다."]], [["Doctor and Mrs Blimber, accompanied (much against his will) by their valued charge, Master Barnet, were already gone back to Brighton, where that young gentleman and his fellow-pilgrims to Parnassus were then, no doubt, in the continual resumption of their studies.", "블림버 박사 내외는, (몹시 마지못해하는) 그 소중한 보호 대상인 바넷 도련님을 데리고 이미 브라이턴으로 돌아간 뒤였다. 거기서 그 젊은 도련님과 그의 파르나소스 순례 동무들은 분명 끊임없이 학업을 다시 이어 가고 있을 터였다."], ["The holiday time was past and over; most of the juvenile guests at the villa had taken their departure; and Florence’s long visit was come to an end.", "방학 기간은 다 지나가 버렸고, 별장에 머물던 어린 손님들은 대개 떠났으며, 플로렌스의 긴 방문도 끝에 이르렀다."]], [["There was one guest, however, albeit not resident within the house, who had been very constant in his attentions to the family, and who still remained devoted to them.", "그러나 비록 집 안에 거주하지는 않았으나, 이 가족에 대한 살뜰한 정성이 한결같고 여전히 그들에게 헌신적인 손님이 하나 있었다. 바로 투츠 씨였다."], ["This was Mr Toots, who after renewing, some weeks ago, the acquaintance he had had the happiness of forming with Skettles Junior, on the night when he burst the Blimberian bonds and soared into freedom with his ring on, called regularly every other day, and left a perfect pack of cards at the hall-door; so many indeed, that the ceremony was quite a deal on the part of Mr Toots, and a hand at whist on the part of the servant.", "몇 주 전, 블림버 식의 속박을 깨뜨리고 반지를 낀 채 자유를 향해 날아오르던 그날 밤에 다행히도 맺게 된 스켓츠 주니어와의 친분을 다시 새롭게 한 이래, 그는 하루걸러 어김없이 찾아와 현관문에 트럼프 한 벌이 통째로 다 될 만큼 명함을 남겨 두고 갔다. 정말이지 어찌나 많았던지, 그 명함 의례는 투츠 씨 쪽에서는 한 벌 떼어 주는 셈이었고, 하인 쪽에서는 휘스트 한 판 칠 만큼이었다."]], [["Mr Toots, likewise, with the bold and happy idea of preventing the family from forgetting him (but there is reason to suppose that this expedient originated in the teeming brain of the Chicken), had established a six-oared cutter, manned by aquatic friends of the Chicken’s and steered by that illustrious character in person, who wore a bright red fireman’s coat for the purpose, and concealed the perpetual black eye with which he was afflicted, beneath a green shade.", "투츠 씨는 또한, 이 가족이 자기를 잊지 못하게 하겠다는 대담하고도 흐뭇한 발상으로(다만 이 묘책은 치킨의 넘쳐나는 머릿속에서 나온 것이라 짐작할 만한 근거가 있다), 노 여섯 개짜리 커터 한 척을 마련했다. 그 배는 치킨의 물놀이 친구들이 노를 젓고, 그 저명한 인물이 친히 키를 잡았는데, 그는 그 목적을 위해 새빨간 소방수 외투를 걸쳤고, 늘 앓고 있는 멍든 눈은 초록색 차양 아래 가렸다."], ["Previous to the institution of this equipage, Mr Toots sounded the Chicken on a hypothetical case, as, supposing the Chicken to be enamoured of a young lady named Mary, and to have conceived the intention of starting a boat of his own, what would he call that boat?", "이 배를 마련하기에 앞서, 투츠 씨는 치킨에게 가상의 경우를 들어 떠보았다. 가령 치킨이 메리라는 이름의 젊은 아가씨한테 반해서 자기 배를 한 척 마련할 작정이라면, 그 배 이름을 뭐라고 부르겠느냐고 말이다."], ["The Chicken replied, with divers strong asseverations, that he would either christen it Poll or The Chicken’s Delight.", "치킨은 갖가지 굳센 맹세를 곁들이며, '폴'이라고 짓거나 '치킨의 기쁨'이라고 짓겠다고 대답했다."], ["Improving on this idea, Mr Toots, after deep study and the exercise of much invention, resolved to call his boat The Toots’s Joy, as a delicate compliment to Florence, of which no man knowing the parties, could possibly miss the appreciation.", "이 발상을 더 갈고닦아, 투츠 씨는 깊이 궁리하고 숱하게 머리를 짜낸 끝에 자기 배를 '투츠의 기쁨'이라 부르기로 결정했으니, 이는 플로렌스에게 바치는 섬세한 찬사로서, 두 당사자를 아는 사람이라면 누구도 그 함의를 놓칠 리 없는 것이었다."]], [["Stretched on a crimson cushion in his gallant bark, with his shoes in the air, Mr Toots, in the exercise of his project, had come up the river, day after day, and week after week, and had flitted to and fro, near Sir Barnet’s garden, and had caused his crew to cut across and across the river at sharp angles, for his better exhibition to any lookers-out from Sir Barnet’s windows, and had had such evolutions performed by the Toots’s Joy as had filled all the neighbouring part of the water-side with astonishment.", "진홍색 방석 위에 두 발을 허공에 띄운 채 비스듬히 누워, 투츠 씨는 자기 계획을 수행하느라 멋진 배를 타고 날이면 날마다, 주가 가고 또 주가 가도록 강을 거슬러 올라왔고, 바넷 경의 정원 근처를 이리저리 떠돌았으며, 바넷 경의 창가에서 내다보는 누군가에게 더 잘 보이도록 선원들에게 강을 가로질러 날카로운 각도로 이쪽저쪽 누비게 했으니, '투츠의 기쁨'이 펼친 그런 곡예는 강가의 이웃 동네 전체를 경탄으로 가득 채웠다."], ["But whenever he saw anyone in Sir Barnet’s garden on the brink of the river, Mr Toots always feigned to be passing there, by a combination of coincidences of the most singular and unlikely description.", "그러나 바넷 경의 정원 강가 가장자리에 누군가가 보이기만 하면, 투츠 씨는 늘 더없이 기묘하고 있을 법하지 않은 우연들이 겹쳐서 마침 그곳을 지나가던 것처럼 시치미를 뗐다."]], [["“How are you, Toots?”", "\"잘 지내시오, 투츠?\""], ["Sir Barnet would say, waving his hand from the lawn, while the artful Chicken steered close in shore.", "바넷 경이 잔디밭에서 손을 흔들며 말하곤 했고, 그러면 교활한 치킨은 강기슭 가까이로 배를 몰았다."]], [["“How de do, Sir Barnet?”", "\"안녕하십니까, 바넷 경?\""], ["Mr Toots would answer, “What a surprising thing that I should see you here!”", "투츠 씨가 대답하곤 했다. \"여기서 경을 뵙다니, 이 무슨 놀라운 일입니까!\""]], [["Mr Toots, in his sagacity, always said this, as if, instead of that being Sir Barnet’s house, it were some deserted edifice on the banks of the Nile, or Ganges.", "투츠 씨는 그 빈틈없는 머리로, 마치 거기가 바넷 경의 저택이 아니라 나일강이나 갠지스강 강둑의 어느 버려진 건물이기라도 한 듯 늘 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“I never was so surprised!”", "\"이렇게 놀란 적이 없습니다!\""], ["Mr Toots would exclaim.—“Is Miss Dombey there?”", "투츠 씨가 외치곤 했다.—\"돔비 아가씨도 거기 계신가요?\""]], [["Whereupon Florence would appear, perhaps.", "그러면 플로렌스가, 어쩌면, 나타났다."]], [["“Oh, Diogenes is quite well, Miss Dombey,”", "\"오, 디오게네스는 아주 잘 있습니다, 돔비 아가씨,\""], ["Toots would cry.", "투츠가 소리치곤 했다."], ["“I called to ask this morning.”", "\"오늘 아침에 들러서 안부를 물었거든요.\""]], [["“Thank you very much!”", "\"정말 고마워요!\""], ["the pleasant voice of Florence would reply.", "플로렌스의 상냥한 목소리가 대답하곤 했다."]], [["“Won’t you come ashore, Toots?”", "\"뭍으로 올라오지 않겠소, 투츠?\""], ["Sir Barnet would say then.", "그러면 바넷 경이 말했다."], ["“Come!", "\"어서!"], ["you’re in no hurry.", "바쁠 것 없잖소."], ["Come and see us.”", "와서 우리랑 좀 봅시다.\""]], [["“Oh, it’s of no consequence, thank you!”", "\"오, 별것 아닙니다, 고맙습니다만!\""], ["Mr Toots would blushingly rejoin.", "투츠 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 대꾸하곤 했다."], ["“I thought Miss Dombey might like to know, that’s all. Good-bye!”", "\"그저 돔비 아가씨가 알고 싶어 하실까 해서요, 그게 다입니다."], ["And poor Mr Toots, who was dying to accept the invitation, but hadn’t the courage to do it, signed to the Chicken, with an aching heart, and away went the Joy, cleaving the water like an arrow.", "안녕히 계십시오!\" 그러고는 그 초대를 받아들이고 싶어 죽을 지경이면서도 그럴 용기가 없는 가엾은 투츠 씨는 쓰라린 마음으로 치킨에게 신호를 보냈고, 그러면 '기쁨'호는 화살처럼 물살을 가르며 멀어져 갔다."]], [["The Joy was lying in a state of extraordinary splendour, at the garden steps, on the morning of Florence’s departure.", "플로렌스가 떠나던 날 아침, '기쁨'호는 정원의 층계참에 더없이 화려한 자태로 떠 있었다."], ["When she went downstairs to take leave, after her talk with Susan, she found Mr Toots awaiting her in the drawing-room.", "수전과 이야기를 나눈 뒤 작별 인사를 하러 아래층으로 내려가니, 응접실에서 자기를 기다리고 있는 투츠 씨가 보였다."]], [["“Oh, how de do, Miss Dombey?” said the stricken Toots, always dreadfully disconcerted when the desire of his heart was gained, and he was speaking to her; “thank you, I’m very well indeed, I hope you’re the same, so was Diogenes yesterday.”", "\"오, 안녕하십니까, 돔비 아가씨?\" 마음의 소원이 이루어져 그녀에게 말을 건넬 때면 늘 끔찍이도 허둥대는, 그 상사병 든 투츠가 말했다. \"고맙습니다, 저는 아주 잘 지냅니다. 아가씨도 그러시기를 바라고요. 어제 디오게네스도 잘 있었습니다.\""]], [["“You are very kind,”", "\"참 친절하시네요,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Thank you, it’s of no consequence,”", "\"고맙습니다, 별것 아닙니다,\""], ["retorted Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["“I thought perhaps you wouldn’t mind, in this fine weather, coming home by water, Miss Dombey.", "\"혹시 이렇게 날씨가 좋으니, 돔비 아가씨, 배로 집에 돌아가시는 것도 괜찮지 않으실까 생각했어요."], ["There’s plenty of room in the boat for your maid.”", "배에는 하녀 분이 탈 자리도 넉넉하답니다.\""]], [["“I am very much obliged to you,”", "\"정말 고마워요,\""], ["said Florence, hesitating.", "플로렌스가 망설이며 말했다."], ["“I really am—but I would rather not.”", "\"정말이에요—하지만 사양할게요.\""]], [["“Oh, it’s of no consequence,”", "\"오, 별것 아닙니다,\""], ["retorted Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["“Good morning.”", "\"그럼 안녕히 계십시오.\""]], [["“Won’t you wait and see Lady Skettles?”", "\"기다렸다가 스켓츠 부인을 뵙고 가지 않으실래요?\""], ["asked Florence, kindly.", "플로렌스가 다정하게 물었다."]], [["“Oh no, thank you,”", "\"오, 아닙니다, 고맙습니다,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, “it’s of no consequence at all.”", "투츠 씨가 대답했다. \"그건 정말 아무것도 아닙니다.\""]], [["So shy was Mr Toots on such occasions, and so flurried!", "이런 자리에서 투츠 씨는 어찌나 수줍어하고 또 허둥대던지! 그러나 마침 그때 스켓츠 부인이 들어오자, 투츠 씨는 별안간 부인에게 안부를 묻고 아주 잘 지내시기를 바란다고 말하고 싶은 열정에 사로잡혔다."], ["But Lady Skettles entering at the moment, Mr Toots was suddenly seized with a passion for asking her how she did, and hoping she was very well; nor could Mr Toots by any possibility leave off shaking hands with her, until Sir Barnet appeared: to whom he immediately clung with the tenacity of desperation.", "그리고 바넷 경이 나타나기 전까지는 도무지 부인의 손을 놓고 악수를 끝낼 줄을 몰랐다. 바넷 경이 나타나자, 그는 곧바로 절박한 자의 끈질김으로 그에게 매달렸다."]], [["“We are losing, today, Toots,”", "\"우리는 오늘,\""], ["said Sir Barnet, turning towards Florence, “the light of our house, I assure you”", "바넷 경이 플로렌스 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"우리 집의 빛을 잃는답니다, 정말이지요, 투츠.\""]], [["“Oh, it’s of no conseq—I mean yes, to be sure,”", "\"오, 별것 아—제 말은, 그렇습니다, 정말로요,\""], ["faltered the embarrassed Mr Toots.", "당황한 투츠 씨가 더듬거렸다."], ["“Good morning!”", "\"안녕히 계십시오!\""]], [["Notwithstanding the emphatic nature of this farewell, Mr Toots, instead of going away, stood leering about him, vacantly.", "이 작별 인사가 단호하기 짝이 없었음에도, 투츠 씨는 떠나기는커녕 멍하니 주위를 두리번거리며 서 있었다."], ["Florence, to relieve him, bade adieu, with many thanks, to Lady Skettles, and gave her arm to Sir Barnet.", "플로렌스는 그를 거들어 주려고, 거듭 감사하며 스켓츠 부인에게 작별을 고하고는 바넷 경에게 팔을 내밀었다."]], [["“May I beg of you, my dear Miss Dombey,”", "\"청을 하나 드려도 되겠소, 나의 다정한 돔비 아가씨,\""], ["said her host, as he conducted her to the carriage, “to present my best compliments to your dear Papa?”", "마차로 그녀를 안내하며 주인이 말했다. \"댁의 다정한 아빠께 내 최고의 안부를 전해 주겠소?\""]], [["It was distressing to Florence to receive the commission, for she felt as if she were imposing on Sir Barnet by allowing him to believe that a kindness rendered to her, was rendered to her father.", "이 부탁을 받는 것이 플로렌스에게는 괴로운 일이었다. 자기에게 베풀어진 친절이 곧 아버지에게 베풀어진 것이라고 바넷 경이 믿게 내버려 둠으로써, 그를 속이고 있는 것만 같았기 때문이다."], ["As she could not explain, however, she bowed her head and thanked him; and again she thought that the dull home, free from such embarrassments, and such reminders of her sorrow, was her natural and best retreat.", "그러나 사정을 설명할 수 없었으므로, 그녀는 고개를 숙여 그에게 감사했다. 그리고 다시 한번, 그런 거북함도 없고 자기 슬픔을 일깨우는 것도 없는 그 음울한 집이야말로 자기에게 타고난, 가장 좋은 안식처라고 생각했다."]], [["Such of her late friends and companions as were yet remaining at the villa, came running from within, and from the garden, to say good-bye.", "별장에 아직 남아 있던 그녀의 옛 친구들과 벗들이 집 안에서, 또 정원에서 작별 인사를 하러 달려 나왔다."], ["They were all attached to her, and very earnest in taking leave of her.", "그들은 모두 그녀를 따랐고, 진심으로 절절하게 그녀와 작별했다."], ["Even the household were sorry for her going, and the servants came nodding and curtseying round the carriage door.", "하인들조차 그녀가 떠나는 것을 아쉬워하며 마차 문 둘레로 와서 고개를 끄덕이고 무릎을 굽혀 절했다."], ["As Florence looked round on the kind faces, and saw among them those of Sir Barnet and his lady, and of Mr Toots, who was chuckling and staring at her from a distance, she was reminded of the night when Paul and she had come from Doctor Blimber’s: and when the carriage drove away, her face was wet with tears.", "플로렌스가 그 다정한 얼굴들을 둘러보다가, 그 가운데서 바넷 경 내외분과, 멀찍이서 낄낄거리며 그녀를 빤히 바라보는 투츠 씨의 얼굴을 보았을 때, 그녀는 폴과 함께 블림버 박사 댁에서 돌아오던 그날 밤을 떠올렸다. 그리고 마차가 떠나갈 때, 그녀의 얼굴은 눈물로 젖어 있었다."]], [["Sorrowful tears, but tears of consolation, too; for all the softer memories connected with the dull old house to which she was returning made it dear to her, as they rose up.", "서글픈 눈물이었으나, 위안의 눈물이기도 했다. 그녀가 돌아가는 그 음울하고 오래된 집과 얽힌 더 부드러운 기억들이 모두 떠오르면서 그 집을 그녀에게 소중하게 만들었기 때문이다."], ["How long it seemed since she had wandered through the silent rooms: since she had last crept, softly and afraid, into those her father occupied: since she had felt the solemn but yet soothing influence of the beloved dead in every action of her daily life!", "그 고요한 방들을 거닐던 때로부터, 아버지가 쓰는 방들 안으로 부드럽게, 두려운 마음으로 마지막으로 살그머니 들어갔던 때로부터, 일상의 모든 행동 속에서 사랑하는 고인의 엄숙하면서도 위안을 주는 영향을 느끼던 때로부터 얼마나 오래된 일처럼 여겨지던지!"], ["This new farewell reminded her, besides, of her parting with poor Walter: of his looks and words that night: and of the gracious blending she had noticed in him, of tenderness for those he left behind, with courage and high spirit.", "이번의 새로운 작별은 더욱이 가엾은 월터와의 헤어짐을 떠올리게 했다. 그날 밤 그의 표정과 말, 그리고 그에게서 보았던, 뒤에 남겨 두고 가는 이들에 대한 다정함이 용기와 드높은 기개와 우아하게 어우러진 모습을 떠올리게 했다."], ["His little history was associated with the old house too, and gave it a new claim and hold upon her heart.", "그의 작은 이야기 또한 그 오래된 집과 결부되어, 그 집에 그녀의 마음에 대한 새로운 권리와 끈을 부여했다."]], [["Even Susan Nipper softened towards the home of so many years, as they were on their way towards it.", "수전 니퍼조차도, 집으로 향하는 길에서는 그 여러 해의 보금자리를 향해 마음이 누그러졌다."], ["Gloomy as it was, and rigid justice as she rendered to its gloom, she forgave it a great deal.", "그 집이 음울했고, 또 그 음울함에 그녀가 가차 없이 정당한 평가를 내리긴 했어도, 그녀는 그 집의 많은 것을 용서했다."], ["“I shall be glad to see it again, I don’t deny, Miss,”", "\"다시 보면 반가울 거예요, 부정 못 하겠어요, 아가씨,\""], ["said the Nipper.", "니퍼가 말했다."], ["“There ain’t much in it to boast of, but I wouldn’t have it burnt or pulled down, neither!”", "\"자랑할 거리야 별로 없는 집이지만, 그렇다고 불타거나 헐려 버리는 건 저도 싫네요!\""]], [["“You’ll be glad to go through the old rooms, won’t you, Susan?”", "\"옛 방들을 둘러보면 반갑겠지, 그렇지 수전?\""], ["said Florence, smiling.", "플로렌스가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Well, Miss,”", "\"글쎄요, 아가씨,\""], ["returned the Nipper, softening more and more towards the house, as they approached it nearer, “I won’t deny but what I shall, though I shall hate ’em again, to-morrow, very likely.”", "니퍼가, 집에 가까워질수록 점점 더 마음이 누그러지며 대답했다. \"반갑긴 할 거예요, 부정 안 할게요, 비록 내일이면 또 그 방들이 죄다 싫어질 가능성이 아주 높긴 하지만요.\""]], [["Florence felt that, for her, there was greater peace within it than elsewhere.", "플로렌스는 자기에게 있어서는 다른 어디보다 그 집 안에 더 큰 평화가 있음을 느꼈다."], ["It was better and easier to keep her secret shut up there, among the tall dark walls, than to carry it abroad into the light, and try to hide it from a crowd of happy eyes.", "그 키 높은 어두운 벽들 사이에 자기 비밀을 가두어 두는 편이, 그것을 밝은 데로 들고 나가 행복한 눈들의 무리로부터 감추려 애쓰는 것보다 더 낫고 더 수월했다."], ["It was better to pursue the study of her loving heart, alone, and find no new discouragements in loving hearts about her.", "자기 둘레의 사랑하는 마음들 속에서 새로운 낙담을 겪느니, 홀로 자기의 사랑하는 마음을 탐구하는 편이 더 나았다."], ["It was easier to hope, and pray, and love on, all uncared for, yet with constancy and patience, in the tranquil sanctuary of such remembrances: although it mouldered, rusted, and decayed about her: than in a new scene, let its gaiety be what it would.", "새로운 무대가 아무리 즐거운 것이라 한들, 거기서보다는, 비록 자기 둘레에서 곰팡이 피고 녹슬고 썩어 가더라도, 그런 기억들의 고요한 성소 안에서 아무에게도 보살핌받지 못한 채, 그러나 한결같이 참을성 있게 희망하고 기도하고 사랑하기를 이어 가는 편이 더 수월했다."], ["She welcomed back her old enchanted dream of life, and longed for the old dark door to close upon her, once again.", "그녀는 마법에 걸린 듯한 자기의 옛 삶의 꿈을 다시 반겨 맞으며, 그 옛 어두운 문이 자기 위로 다시 한번 닫히기를 갈망했다."]], [["Full of such thoughts, they turned into the long and sombre street.", "이런 생각들로 가득 찬 채, 그들은 길고 어둑한 거리로 접어들었다."], ["Florence was not on that side of the carriage which was nearest to her home, and as the distance lessened between them and it, she looked out of her window for the children over the way.", "플로렌스는 집에 가장 가까운 쪽 마차 자리에 앉아 있지 않았으므로, 마차와 집 사이의 거리가 줄어들자 길 건너편 아이들을 보려고 창밖을 내다보았다."]], [["She was thus engaged, when an exclamation from Susan caused her to turn quickly round.", "그녀가 그러고 있을 때, 수전의 탄성에 그녀는 재빨리 돌아보았다."]], [["“Why, Gracious me!”", "\"어머나, 세상에!\""], ["cried Susan, breathless, “where’s our house!”", "수전이 숨을 헐떡이며 외쳤다. \"우리 집이 어디 갔지!\""]], [["“Our house!”", "\"우리 집이!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["Susan, drawing in her head from the window, thrust it out again, drew it in again as the carriage stopped, and stared at her mistress in amazement.", "수전은 창밖에서 머리를 들이밀었다가 다시 내밀고, 마차가 멈춰 서자 다시 들이밀더니, 어리둥절한 눈으로 자기 아가씨를 빤히 바라보았다."]], [["There was a labyrinth of scaffolding raised all round the house, from the basement to the roof.", "집 둘레로 지하층에서 지붕까지 비계가 미로처럼 둘러쳐져 있었다."], ["Loads of bricks and stones, and heaps of mortar, and piles of wood, blocked up half the width and length of the broad street at the side.", "벽돌과 돌을 잔뜩 쌓아 둔 것이며, 회반죽 더미며, 목재 무더기가 옆쪽의 그 넓은 거리의 폭과 길이를 절반이나 가로막고 있었다. 사다리가 벽에 기대 세워져 있었고, 일꾼들이 그 위를 오르락내리락했으며, 사람들이 비계 디딤판 위에서 일하고 있었다."], ["Ladders were raised against the walls; labourers were climbing up and down; men were at work upon the steps of the scaffolding; painters and decorators were busy inside; great rolls of ornamental paper were being delivered from a cart at the door; an upholsterer’s waggon also stopped the way; no furniture was to be seen through the gaping and broken windows in any of the rooms; nothing but workmen, and the implements of their several trades, swarming from the kitchens to the garrets.", "안에서는 칠장이와 도배공들이 분주했고, 큼직한 장식 벽지 두루마리가 문 앞 수레에서 배달되고 있었으며, 가구 운반 마차 한 대도 길을 막고 있었다. 어느 방을 봐도 휑하니 깨진 창문 너머로는 가구가 하나도 보이지 않았고, 오로지 일꾼들과 그들의 갖가지 연장들만이 부엌에서 다락방까지 우글거렸다."], ["Inside and outside alike: bricklayers, painters, carpenters, masons: hammer, hod, brush, pickaxe, saw, and trowel: all at work together, in full chorus!", "안이나 밖이나 똑같이, 벽돌공, 칠장이, 목수, 석공—망치, 흙받기, 솔, 곡괭이, 톱, 흙손—이 모두가 한꺼번에, 일제히 합창하듯 일하고 있었다!"]], [["Florence descended from the coach, half doubting if it were, or could be the right house, until she recognised Towlinson, with a sun-burnt face, standing at the door to receive her.", "플로렌스는 마차에서 내렸는데, 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴로 그녀를 맞으러 문간에 서 있는 타울린슨을 알아보기 전까지는, 여기가 제대로 찾아온 집이 맞는지, 맞을 수 있는지 반신반의했다."]], [["“There is nothing the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이 있는 건 아니지요?\""], ["inquired Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“Oh no, Miss.”", "\"오, 아닙니다, 아가씨.\""]], [["“There are great alterations going on.”", "\"굉장한 개조가 진행되고 있군요.\""]], [["“Yes, Miss, great alterations,”", "\"네, 아가씨, 굉장한 개조지요,\""], ["said Towlinson.", "타울린슨이 말했다."]], [["Florence passed him as if she were in a dream, and hurried upstairs.", "플로렌스는 꿈속에 있는 듯이 그를 지나쳐 서둘러 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["The garish light was in the long-darkened drawing-room and there were steps and platforms, and men in paper caps, in the high places.", "오랫동안 어두웠던 응접실에는 야단스러운 빛이 들어차 있었고, 높은 자리에는 디딤대와 발판이 있고 종이 모자를 쓴 사람들이 있었다."], ["Her mother’s picture was gone with the rest of the moveables, and on the mark where it had been, was scrawled in chalk, “this room in panel. Green and gold.”", "어머니의 초상화는 다른 가재도구들과 함께 사라지고 없었으며, 그것이 걸려 있던 자국 위에는 분필로 \"이 방은 판벽."], ["The staircase was a labyrinth of posts and planks like the outside of the house, and a whole Olympus of plumbers and glaziers was reclining in various attitudes, on the skylight.", "초록과 금색\"이라고 휘갈겨져 있었다. 계단은 집 바깥처럼 기둥과 널빤지의 미로였고, 채광창 위에는 배관공과 유리공들의 올림포스 신들이 통째로 갖가지 자세로 비스듬히 누워 있었다."], ["Her own room was not yet touched within, but there were beams and boards raised against it without, baulking the daylight.", "그녀 자신의 방은 아직 안쪽은 손대지 않았으나, 바깥에는 들보와 판자가 세워져 있어 햇빛을 가로막고 있었다."], ["She went up swiftly to that other bedroom, where the little bed was; and a dark giant of a man with a pipe in his mouth, and his head tied up in a pocket-handkerchief, was staring in at the window.", "그녀는 그 작은 침대가 있던 또 다른 침실로 빠르게 올라갔다. 그런데 입에 파이프를 물고 머리를 손수건으로 동여맨 거인 같은 시커먼 사내 하나가 창문으로 안을 들여다보고 있었다."]], [["It was here that Susan Nipper, who had been in quest of Florence, found her, and said, would she go downstairs to her Papa, who wished to speak to her.", "플로렌스를 찾아다니던 수전 니퍼가 그녀를 발견한 곳이 바로 여기였다. 수전은, 아가씨더러 아빠께 내려가 보시라고, 아빠가 말씀을 나누고 싶어 하신다고 말했다."]], [["“At home!", "\"집에 계셔!"], ["and wishing to speak to me!”", "게다가 나랑 이야기를 하고 싶어 하셔!\""], ["cried Florence, trembling.", "플로렌스가 떨면서 외쳤다."]], [["Susan, who was infinitely more distraught than Florence herself, repeated her errand; and Florence, pale and agitated, hurried down again, without a moment’s hesitation.", "플로렌스 자신보다 한없이 더 정신이 산란해진 수전이 자기 심부름을 거듭 전하자, 플로렌스는 창백하게 동요하면서도 한순간의 망설임도 없이 다시 서둘러 내려갔다."], ["She thought upon the way down, would she dare to kiss him?", "그녀는 내려가는 길에 생각했다. 감히 아빠께 입을 맞춰 봐도 될까?"], ["The longing of her heart resolved her, and she thought she would.", "가슴속 갈망이 그녀의 결심을 굳혔고, 그녀는 그래도 되겠다고 생각했다."]], [["Her father might have heard that heart beat, when it came into his presence.", "그녀의 심장이 그의 앞에 이르렀을 때, 아버지는 그 고동 소리를 들을 수 있었을지도 모른다."], ["One instant, and it would have beat against his breast.", "한순간만 더 있었으면, 그 심장은 그의 가슴에 맞닿아 뛰었을 것이다."]], [["But he was not alone.", "그러나 그는 혼자가 아니었다."], ["There were two ladies there; and Florence stopped.", "거기에는 두 부인이 있었고, 플로렌스는 멈춰 섰다."], ["Striving so hard with her emotion, that if her brute friend Di had not burst in and overwhelmed her with his caresses as a welcome home—at which one of the ladies gave a little scream, and that diverted her attention from herself—she would have swooned upon the floor.", "어찌나 안간힘을 다해 감정을 억눌렀던지, 만일 그 짐승 같은 친구 디가 뛰어들어 집에 온 것을 환영하듯 그녀에게 애정 표시를 퍼붓지 않았더라면—그 바람에 부인 가운데 하나가 작게 비명을 질렀고, 그것이 그녀 자신에게서 주의를 돌려놓았는데—그녀는 바닥에 그대로 까무러쳤을 것이다."]], [["“Florence,”", "\"플로렌스,\" 아버지가 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["said her father, putting out his hand: so stiffly that it held her off: “how do you do?”", "어찌나 뻣뻣하게 내밀었던지 그것은 그녀를 떼어 놓는 듯했다. \"잘 지냈느냐?\""]], [["Florence took the hand between her own, and putting it timidly to her lips, yielded to its withdrawal.", "플로렌스는 두 손으로 그 손을 받아 들고 머뭇머뭇 입술에 가져다 댔다가, 그 손이 물러나는 대로 놓아주었다."], ["It touched the door in shutting it, with quite as much endearment as it had touched her.", "그 손은 문을 닫으며 그것에 닿았는데, 그녀에게 닿았을 때만큼이나 다정하게 닿았다."]], [["“What dog is that?”", "\"저 개는 무슨 개냐?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, displeased.", "돔비 씨가 못마땅한 듯 말했다."]], [["“It is a dog, Papa—from Brighton.”", "\"개예요, 아빠—브라이턴에서 온 거예요.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"그래!\""], ["said Mr Dombey; and a cloud passed over his face, for he understood her.", "돔비 씨가 말했다. 그러고는 그녀의 말뜻을 알아차렸기에, 그의 얼굴 위로 구름이 스쳐 지나갔다."]], [["“He is very good-tempered,”", "\"성질이 아주 순해요,\""], ["said Florence, addressing herself with her natural grace and sweetness to the two lady strangers.", "플로렌스가 타고난 우아함과 다정함으로 두 낯선 부인을 향해 말했다. \"그저 저를 보고 반가워하는 것뿐이에요."], ["“He is only glad to see me. Pray forgive him.”", "부디 너그러이 봐주세요.\""]], [["She saw in the glance they interchanged, that the lady who had screamed, and who was seated, was old; and that the other lady, who stood near her Papa, was very beautiful, and of an elegant figure.", "그녀는 두 사람이 주고받는 눈길에서, 비명을 지른, 앉아 있는 부인은 나이가 많고, 아빠 가까이에 서 있는 다른 부인은 매우 아름다우며 우아한 자태를 지녔음을 알아보았다."]], [["“Mrs Skewton,”", "\"스큐튼 부인,\""], ["said her father, turning to the first, and holding out his hand, “this is my daughter Florence.”", "아버지가 첫 번째 부인을 향해 돌아서서 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"이 아이가 제 딸 플로렌스입니다.\""]], [["“Charming, I am sure,”", "\"정말 매력적이군요,\""], ["observed the lady, putting up her glass.", "그 부인이 안경을 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["“So natural!", "\"참으로 꾸밈이 없어요!"], ["My darling Florence, you must kiss me, if you please.”", "사랑스러운 플로렌스, 괜찮다면 내게 입을 맞춰 다오.\""]], [["Florence having done so, turned towards the other lady, by whom her father stood waiting.", "플로렌스는 그렇게 한 뒤, 아버지가 곁에 서서 기다리고 있는 다른 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“Edith,”", "\"이디스,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “this is my daughter Florence.", "돔비 씨가 말했다. \"이 아이가 제 딸 플로렌스요."], ["Florence, this lady will soon be your Mama.”", "플로렌스, 이 부인이 곧 네 엄마가 되실 거다.\""]], [["Florence started, and looked up at the beautiful face in a conflict of emotions, among which the tears that name awakened, struggled for a moment with surprise, interest, admiration, and an indefinable sort of fear.", "플로렌스는 흠칫 놀라며, 갖가지 감정이 뒤섞인 채 그 아름다운 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그 가운데서 그 이름이 일깨운 눈물이 한순간 놀라움과 호기심과 감탄과 형언할 수 없는 종류의 두려움과 다투었다."], ["Then she cried out, “Oh, Papa, may you be happy!", "그러다 그녀는 외쳤다. \"오, 아빠, 부디 행복하세요!"], ["may you be very, very happy all your life!”", "평생토록 아주, 아주 행복하시기를 바라요!\""], ["and then fell weeping on the lady’s bosom.", "그러더니 그 부인의 가슴에 엎드려 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["There was a short silence.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["The beautiful lady, who at first had seemed to hesitate whether or no she should advance to Florence, held her to her breast, and pressed the hand with which she clasped her, close about her waist, as if to reassure her and comfort her.", "처음에는 플로렌스에게 다가갈지 말지 망설이는 듯하던 그 아름다운 부인은, 그녀를 가슴에 끌어안고, 그녀를 감싼 손으로 그녀의 허리를 꼭 죄어 안았다. 마치 그녀를 안심시키고 위로하려는 듯이."], ["Not one word passed the lady’s lips.", "그 부인의 입술에서는 한마디 말도 새어 나오지 않았다."], ["She bent her head down over Florence, and she kissed her on the cheek, but she said no word.", "그녀는 플로렌스 위로 고개를 숙여 그녀의 뺨에 입을 맞추었으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Shall we go on through the rooms,”", "\"우리, 방들을 둘러보며,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “and see how our workmen are doing?", "돔비 씨가 말했다. \"일꾼들이 어떻게 하고 있는지 가서 볼까요?"], ["Pray allow me, my dear madam.”", "부디 제가 모시지요, 친애하는 부인.\""]], [["He said this in offering his arm to Mrs Skewton, who had been looking at Florence through her glass, as though picturing to herself what she might be made, by the infusion—from her own copious storehouse, no doubt—of a little more Heart and Nature.", "그는 안경 너머로 플로렌스를 바라보고 있던 스큐튼 부인에게 팔을 내밀며 이렇게 말했다. 그 부인은, 마치 자기 자신의 풍부한 곳간에서—틀림없이—약간의 마음과 자연스러움을 부어 넣으면 그 아이를 어떤 모습으로 만들 수 있을지 그려 보기라도 하는 듯한 눈빛이었다."], ["Florence was still sobbing on the lady’s breast, and holding to her, when Mr Dombey was heard to say from the Conservatory:", "플로렌스는 여전히 그 부인의 가슴에 엎드려 흐느끼며 그녀에게 매달려 있었는데, 그때 온실에서 돔비 씨가 이렇게 말하는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Let us ask Edith.", "\"이디스에게 물어봅시다."], ["Dear me, where is she?”", "아니, 그 사람이 어디 있지?\""]], [["“Edith, my dear!”", "\"이디스, 얘야!\""], ["cried Mrs Skewton, “where are you?", "스큐튼 부인이 외쳤다. \"너 어디 있니?"], ["Looking for Mr Dombey somewhere, I know.", "어딘가에서 돔비 씨를 찾고 있구나, 내 다 알지."], ["We are here, my love.”", "우리 여기 있단다, 얘야.\""]], [["The beautiful lady released her hold of Florence, and pressing her lips once more upon her face, withdrew hurriedly, and joined them.", "그 아름다운 부인은 플로렌스를 안았던 팔을 풀고, 그녀의 얼굴에 다시 한번 입술을 누른 뒤, 서둘러 물러나 그들과 합류했다. 플로렌스는 그 자리에 그대로 서 있었다."], ["Florence remained standing in the same place: happy, sorry, joyful, and in tears, she knew not how, or how long, but all at once: when her new Mama came back, and took her in her arms again.", "행복하면서도 슬프고, 기쁘면서도 눈물에 젖은 채, 어찌 된 영문인지, 얼마나 오래인지도 모른 채, 그러나 일순간에. 그때 그녀의 새 엄마가 돌아와 다시 그녀를 품에 안았다."]], [["“Florence,”", "\"플로렌스,\""], ["said the lady, hurriedly, and looking into her face with great earnestness.", "그 부인이 서둘러, 그러면서도 더없이 진지하게 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“You will not begin by hating me?”", "\"넌 나를 미워하는 것으로 시작하지는 않을 거지?\""]], [["“By hating you, Mama?”", "\"미워하다니요, 엄마?\""], ["cried Florence, winding her arm round her neck, and returning the look.", "플로렌스가 그녀의 목에 팔을 두르고 그 눈길을 마주 돌려주며 외쳤다."]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Begin by thinking well of me,”", "나를 좋게 생각하는 것으로 시작하렴,\""], ["said the beautiful lady.", "그 아름다운 부인이 말했다."], ["“Begin by believing that I will try to make you happy, and that I am prepared to love you, Florence.", "\"내가 너를 행복하게 해 주려 애쓰겠다는 것, 그리고 내가 너를 사랑할 준비가 되어 있다는 것을 믿는 것으로 시작하렴, 플로렌스."], ["Good-bye.", "잘 있어."], ["We shall meet again soon.", "우리 곧 다시 만나자."], ["Good-bye!", "잘 있어!"], ["Don’t stay here, now.”", "이제 여기 있지 말고.\""]], [["Again she pressed her to her breast she had spoken in a rapid manner, but firmly—and Florence saw her rejoin them in the other room.", "그녀는 다시 한번 그녀를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안았는데—재빠른 말투였으나, 단호했다—그리고 플로렌스는 그녀가 다른 방에서 그들과 합류하는 것을 지켜보았다."]], [["And now Florence began to hope that she would learn from her new and beautiful Mama, how to gain her father’s love; and in her sleep that night, in her lost old home, her own Mama smiled radiantly upon the hope, and blessed it.", "그리고 이제 플로렌스는 그 새롭고 아름다운 엄마에게서 아버지의 사랑을 얻는 법을 배울 수 있으리라는 희망을 품기 시작했다. 그리고 그날 밤, 잃어버린 옛집에서의 잠 속에서, 그녀 자신의 엄마가 그 희망을 향해 환하게 미소 지으며 축복을 내려 주었다."], ["Dreaming Florence!", "꿈꾸는 플로렌스여!"]], [["# CHAPTER XXIX. The Opening of the Eyes of Mrs Chick", "# 제24장"]], [["Miss Tox, all unconscious of any such rare appearances in connexion with Mr Dombey’s house, as scaffoldings and ladders, and men with their heads tied up in pocket-handkerchiefs, glaring in at the windows like flying genii or strange birds,—having breakfasted one morning at about this eventful period of time, on her customary viands; to wit, one French roll rasped, one egg new laid (or warranted to be), and one little pot of tea, wherein was infused one little silver scoopful of that herb on behalf of Miss Tox, and one little silver scoopful on behalf of the teapot—a flight of fancy in which good housekeepers delight; went upstairs to set forth the bird waltz on the harpsichord, to water and arrange the plants, to dust the nick-nacks, and, according to her daily custom, to make her little drawing-room the garland of Princess’s Place.", "칙 부인의 눈이 뜨이다\n돔비 씨 집과 관련하여 그토록 진귀한 광경들—비계와 사다리, 그리고 머리에 손수건을 동여맨 사내들이 날아다니는 정령이나 기이한 새들처럼 창문 안을 노려보는 광경—이 벌어지고 있는 줄은 전혀 모르는 채, 톡스 양은 이 파란만장한 시기의 어느 날 아침에 늘 먹는 음식으로 식사를 마쳤다. 즉, 겉을 긁어낸 프랑스 빵 한 개, 갓 낳은(혹은 그렇다고 보증된) 달걀 한 개, 그리고 작은 찻주전자 하나였는데, 그 안에는 톡스 양 몫으로 그 찻잎을 작은 은수저로 한 술, 또 찻주전자 몫으로 작은 은수저로 한 술 우려 넣은 것이었다—이는 알뜰한 살림꾼들이 즐기는 공상적 발상이었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 위층으로 올라가, 하프시코드로 새 왈츠를 연주하고, 화초에 물을 주고 손질하며, 자질구레한 장식품의 먼지를 털고, 매일의 습관대로 자신의 작은 응접실을 프린세스 플레이스의 화환으로 만들었다."]], [["Miss Tox endued herself with a pair of ancient gloves, like dead leaves, in which she was accustomed to perform these avocations—hidden from human sight at other times in a table drawer—and went methodically to work; beginning with the bird waltz; passing, by a natural association of ideas, to her bird—a very high-shouldered canary, stricken in years, and much rumpled, but a piercing singer, as Princess’s Place well knew; taking, next in order, the little china ornaments, paper fly-cages, and so forth; and coming round, in good time, to the plants, which generally required to be snipped here and there with a pair of scissors, for some botanical reason that was very powerful with Miss Tox.", "톡스 양은 죽은 나뭇잎처럼 보이는 낡은 장갑 한 켤레를 끼었는데, 그 일들을 할 때면 늘 끼던 것으로—다른 때에는 탁자 서랍 속에 사람들 눈에 띄지 않게 숨겨 두었다—그러고는 차근차근 일에 착수했다. 새 왈츠로 시작해서, 자연스러운 연상 작용에 따라 자기 새로 넘어갔는데, 그 새는 어깨가 몹시 솟은, 나이를 먹어 깃이 많이 헝클어진 카나리아였으나, 프린세스 플레이스가 익히 아는 대로 째지게 잘 우는 가수였다. 다음 차례로는 작은 도자기 장식품, 종이로 만든 파리채 같은 것들을 손질하고, 적당한 때가 되자 화초로 돌아왔는데, 그 화초들은 톡스 양에게 매우 설득력 있는 어떤 식물학적 이유로 여기저기 가위로 잘라 주어야 하는 것이 보통이었다."]], [["Miss Tox was slow in coming to the plants, this morning.", "이날 아침 톡스 양은 화초에 좀처럼 다가가지 못했다."], ["The weather was warm, the wind southerly; and there was a sigh of the summer-time in Princess’s Place, that turned Miss Tox’s thoughts upon the country.", "날씨는 따뜻했고, 바람은 남풍이었으며, 프린세스 플레이스에는 여름철의 한숨 같은 것이 감돌아 톡스 양의 생각을 시골로 돌려놓았다."], ["The pot-boy attached to the Princess’s Arms had come out with a can and trickled water, in a flowering pattern, all over Princess’s Place, and it gave the weedy ground a fresh scent—quite a growing scent, Miss Tox said.", "프린세스 암스 술집에 딸린 술 따르는 소년이 물통을 들고 나와 프린세스 플레이스 전체에 꽃무늬를 그리며 물을 흩뿌렸는데, 그것이 잡초 무성한 땅에 신선한 향기를—톡스 양의 말로는, 그야말로 무언가 자라나는 향기를—풍겨 주었다."], ["There was a tiny blink of sun peeping in from the great street round the corner, and the smoky sparrows hopped over it and back again, brightening as they passed: or bathed in it, like a stream, and became glorified sparrows, unconnected with chimneys.", "모퉁이 너머 큰길에서 햇살이 자그맣게 비쳐 들었고, 그을음투성이 참새들이 그 위를 폴짝폴짝 건너갔다 돌아왔다 하면서, 지나칠 때마다 환해졌다. 아니, 그 햇살을 시냇물처럼 멱 감고는, 굴뚝과는 아무 상관 없는 영광스러운 참새가 되었다."], ["Legends in praise of Ginger-Beer, with pictorial representations of thirsty customers submerged in the effervescence, or stunned by the flying corks, were conspicuous in the window of the Princess’s Arms.", "프린세스 암스의 창문에는 진저비어를 찬미하는 문구들이, 목마른 손님들이 거품 속에 잠겨 있거나 날아드는 코르크 마개에 얻어맞아 정신을 잃은 그림과 더불어 눈에 띄게 걸려 있었다."], ["They were making late hay, somewhere out of town; and though the fragrance had a long way to come, and many counter fragrances to contend with among the dwellings of the poor (may God reward the worthy gentlemen who stickle for the Plague as part and parcel of the wisdom of our ancestors, and who do their little best to keep those dwellings miserable!), yet it was wafted faintly into Princess’s Place, whispering of Nature and her wholesome air, as such things will, even unto prisoners and captives, and those who are desolate and oppressed, in very spite of aldermen and knights to boot: at whose sage nod—and how they nod!—the rolling world stands still!", "어딘가 도시 밖에서는 늦건초를 만들고 있었는데, 그 향기가 멀리서 오느라 가난한 이들의 거처 사이에서 수많은 반대편 냄새와 싸워야 했음에도(역병을 우리 조상들의 지혜의 일부로 떠받들며, 그 거처들을 비참하게 유지하려고 제 나름의 보잘것없는 애를 쓰는 갸륵한 양반들에게 하느님께서 갚아 주시기를!), 그래도 그것은 프린세스 플레이스로 희미하게 실려 와, 그런 것들이 으레 그러하듯, 자연과 그 건강한 공기를 속삭여 주었으니—참사회원이며 거기에 기사들까지, 그 현명한 끄덕임에—그것도 어찌나 끄덕여 대는지!—구르는 세상이 멈춰 서는 그자들에게도 아랑곳없이, 갇힌 자들과 사로잡힌 자들에게도, 황폐하고 억눌린 자들에게도 그러했다."]], [["Miss Tox sat down upon the window-seat, and thought of her good Papa deceased—Mr Tox, of the Customs Department of the public service; and of her childhood, passed at a seaport, among a considerable quantity of cold tar, and some rusticity.", "톡스 양은 창가 의자에 앉아, 고인이 된 갸륵한 아버지를—공직의 세관부에 계셨던 톡스 씨를 떠올렸다. 그리고 항구 도시에서 상당량의 차가운 콜타르와 약간의 시골티 속에서 보낸 자기 어린 시절을 떠올렸다."], ["She fell into a softened remembrance of meadows, in old time, gleaming with buttercups, like so many inverted firmaments of golden stars; and how she had made chains of dandelion-stalks for youthful vowers of eternal constancy, dressed chiefly in nankeen; and how soon those fetters had withered and broken.", "그녀는 옛 시절의 초원에 대한 부드러운 회상에 잠겨 들었는데, 그 초원은 미나리아재비로 반짝여서, 마치 거꾸로 뒤집힌 황금별 하늘이 수없이 펼쳐진 것 같았다. 그리고 자기가 주로 면포 옷차림을 한 어린 영원한 정절의 맹세자들을 위해 민들레 줄기로 사슬을 엮어 주던 일과, 그 족쇄들이 얼마나 빨리 시들어 끊어졌는지를 떠올렸다."]], [["Sitting on the window-seat, and looking out upon the sparrows and the blink of sun, Miss Tox thought likewise of her good Mama deceased—sister to the owner of the powdered head and pigtail—of her virtues and her rheumatism.", "창가 의자에 앉아 참새들과 비쳐 드는 햇살을 내다보면서, 톡스 양은 또한 고인이 된 갸륵한 어머니를—분 바른 머리에 변발을 한 그자의 누이를—그분의 미덕과 류머티즘을 떠올렸다."], ["And when a man with bulgy legs, and a rough voice, and a heavy basket on his head that crushed his hat into a mere black muffin, came crying flowers down Princess’s Place, making his timid little roots of daisies shudder in the vibration of every yell he gave, as though he had been an ogre, hawking little children, summer recollections were so strong upon Miss Tox, that she shook her head, and murmured she would be comparatively old before she knew it—which seemed likely.", "그리고 다리가 불룩하고 목소리가 거친 사내가, 머리에 인 무거운 바구니에 눌려 모자가 그저 검은 머핀처럼 찌부러진 채로, \"꽃 사세요\" 하고 외치며 프린세스 플레이스를 내려왔을 때—그가 내지르는 고함 소리의 진동마다 그의 겁먹은 자그마한 데이지 뿌리들이 부르르 떨었으니, 마치 어린아이들을 팔러 다니는 식인귀라도 되는 것 같았다—그때 여름의 추억이 어찌나 강하게 톡스 양을 사로잡았던지, 그녀는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 자기도 모르는 사이에 비교적 늙어 버릴 거라고 중얼거렸다—그럴 법한 일이었다."]], [["In her pensive mood, Miss Tox’s thoughts went wandering on Mr Dombey’s track; probably because the Major had returned home to his lodgings opposite, and had just bowed to her from his window.", "수심에 잠긴 기분 속에서, 톡스 양의 생각은 돔비 씨의 자취를 따라 떠돌아갔다. 아마도 소령이 맞은편 자기 하숙으로 돌아와, 막 자기 창문에서 그녀에게 인사를 건넸기 때문이리라."], ["What other reason could Miss Tox have for connecting Mr Dombey with her summer days and dandelion fetters?", "그렇지 않고서야 톡스 양이 무슨 이유로 돔비 씨를 자기의 여름날이며 민들레 족쇄와 결부시켰겠는가?"], ["Was he more cheerful?", "그분은 좀 더 명랑해지셨을까?"], ["thought Miss Tox.", "톡스 양은 생각했다."], ["Was he reconciled to the decrees of fate?", "그분은 운명의 명령과 화해하셨을까?"], ["Would he ever marry again?", "그분은 다시 결혼하실까?"], ["and if yes, whom?", "만일 그렇다면, 누구와?"], ["What sort of person now!", "대체 어떤 사람과 말인가!"]], [["A flush—it was warm weather—overspread Miss Tox’s face, as, while entertaining these meditations, she turned her head, and was surprised by the reflection of her thoughtful image in the chimney-glass.", "이런 명상을 즐기던 중에, 그녀가 고개를 돌리다가 벽난로 거울에 비친 자신의 골똘한 모습에 놀랐을 때, 얼굴에 홍조가—날씨가 따뜻한 탓에—번졌다."], ["Another flush succeeded when she saw a little carriage drive into Princess’s Place, and make straight for her own door.", "작은 마차 한 대가 프린세스 플레이스로 들어와 곧장 자기 집 문을 향해 오는 것을 보았을 때, 또 한 번 홍조가 뒤따랐다."], ["Miss Tox arose, took up her scissors hastily, and so coming, at last, to the plants, was very busy with them when Mrs Chick entered the room.", "톡스 양은 일어서서 황급히 가위를 집어 들었고, 그리하여 마침내 화초로 다가가, 칙 부인이 방에 들어왔을 때는 화초를 손질하느라 무척 분주한 척했다."]], [["“How is my sweetest friend!”", "\"이렇게 더없이 사랑스러운 내 친구는 안녕하신가요!\""], ["exclaimed Miss Tox, with open arms.", "톡스 양이 두 팔을 벌리며 외쳤다."]], [["A little stateliness was mingled with Miss Tox’s sweetest friend’s demeanour, but she kissed Miss Tox, and said, “Lucretia, thank you, I am pretty well.", "톡스 양의 더없이 사랑스러운 친구의 거동에는 약간의 위엄이 섞여 있었으나, 그녀는 톡스 양에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"루크리셔, 고마워요, 나는 그럭저럭 잘 지내요."], ["I hope you are the same.", "당신도 그러기를 바라요."], ["Hem!”", "흠!\""]], [["Mrs Chick was labouring under a peculiar little monosyllabic cough; a sort of primer, or easy introduction to the art of coughing.", "칙 부인은 독특한 단음절의 작은 기침을 앓고 있었다. 일종의 입문서랄까, 기침 기술의 쉬운 길잡이 같은 것이었다."]], [["“You call very early, and how kind that is, my dear!”", "\"이렇게 일찍 오시다니, 얼마나 다정한지 몰라요, 그래요!\""], ["pursued Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말을 이었다."], ["“Now, have you breakfasted?”", "\"그래, 아침은 드셨나요?\""]], [["“Thank you, Lucretia,”", "\"고마워요, 루크리셔,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “I have.", "칙 부인이 말했다."], ["I took an early breakfast”—the good lady seemed curious on the subject of Princess’s Place, and looked all round it as she spoke—“with my brother, who has come home.”", "\"먹었어요. 일찍 아침을 들었지요\"—이 갸륵한 부인은 프린세스 플레이스에 대해 호기심이 인 듯, 말하는 동안 사방을 둘러보았다—\"집에 돌아온 오라비와 함께요.\""]], [["“He is better, I trust, my love,”", "\"좀 나아지셨으면 좋겠네요, 그렇죠?\""], ["faltered Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“He is greatly better, thank you.", "\"많이 나아졌어요, 고마워요."], ["Hem!”", "흠!\""]], [["“My dear Louisa must be careful of that cough”", "\"우리 루이자는 그 기침을 조심해야 해요.\""], ["remarked Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 한마디 했다."]], [["“It’s nothing,”", "\"아무것도 아니에요,\""], ["returned Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["“It’s merely change of weather.", "\"그저 날씨가 바뀌어서 그래요."], ["We must expect change.”", "우리는 변화를 각오해야 하지요.\""]], [["“Of weather?”", "\"날씨의요?\""], ["asked Miss Tox, in her simplicity.", "톡스 양이 천진하게 물었다."]], [["“Of everything,”", "\"모든 것의요,\""], ["returned Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["“Of course we must.", "\"당연히 그래야지요."], ["It’s a world of change.", "이건 변화의 세상이에요."], ["Anyone would surprise me very much, Lucretia, and would greatly alter my opinion of their understanding, if they attempted to contradict or evade what is so perfectly evident.", "누구라도 이렇게 명백한 사실을 반박하거나 회피하려 든다면, 루크리셔, 나는 무척 놀랄 거고, 그 사람의 이해력에 대한 내 평가가 크게 달라질 거예요."], ["Change!”", "변화라니!\""], ["exclaimed Mrs Chick, with severe philosophy.", "칙 부인이 준엄한 철학을 담아 외쳤다."], ["“Why, my gracious me, what is there that does not change!", "\"아니, 어머나 세상에, 변하지 않는 것이 대체 무어가 있나요!"], ["even the silkworm, who I am sure might be supposed not to trouble itself about such subjects, changes into all sorts of unexpected things continually.”", "누에조차도—그런 문제 따위는 신경 쓰지 않을 거라고 다들 짐작할 법한 그 누에조차도—온갖 뜻밖의 것들로 끊임없이 변하잖아요.\""]], [["“My Louisa,”", "\"우리 루이자는,\""], ["said the mild Miss Tox, “is ever happy in her illustrations.”", "온순한 톡스 양이 말했다. \"예를 드는 데 늘 능하시다니까요.\""]], [["“You are so kind, Lucretia,”", "\"당신은 어찌나 다정한지요, 루크리셔,\""], ["returned Mrs Chick, a little softened, “as to say so, and to think so, I believe.", "칙 부인이 다소 누그러져서 대꾸했다. \"그렇게 말해 주고, 또 정말 그렇게 생각해 주니 말이에요."], ["I hope neither of us may ever have any cause to lessen our opinion of the other, Lucretia.”", "우리 둘 중 누구도 서로에 대한 평가를 낮출 이유가 결코 생기지 않기를 바라요, 루크리셔.\""]], [["“I am sure of it,”", "\"틀림없이 그럴 거예요,\""], ["returned Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 대꾸했다."]], [["Mrs Chick coughed as before, and drew lines on the carpet with the ivory end of her parasol.", "칙 부인은 앞서처럼 기침을 하고는, 파라솔의 상아 끝으로 카펫에 줄을 그었다."], ["Miss Tox, who had experience of her fair friend, and knew that under the pressure of any slight fatigue or vexation she was prone to a discursive kind of irritability, availed herself of the pause, to change the subject.", "자기의 어여쁜 친구를 잘 알고, 그녀가 조금이라도 피로하거나 언짢은 압박을 받으면 이리저리 두서없는 짜증으로 빠지기 쉽다는 것을 경험으로 아는 톡스 양은, 이 잠깐의 틈을 이용해 화제를 바꾸었다."]], [["“Pardon me, my dear Louisa,”", "\"미안하지만, 루이자,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “but have I caught sight of the manly form of Mr Chick in the carriage?”", "톡스 양이 말했다. \"혹시 내가 마차 안에서 칙 씨의 늠름한 모습을 본 게 맞나요?\""]], [["“He is there,”", "\"그이는 거기 있어요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “but pray leave him there.", "칙 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 제발 거기 그냥 두세요."], ["He has his newspaper, and would be quite contented for the next two hours.", "신문을 가지고 있으니, 앞으로 두 시간은 아주 만족스러워할 거예요."], ["Go on with your flowers, Lucretia, and allow me to sit here and rest.”", "꽃이나 계속 손질하세요, 루크리셔, 나는 여기 앉아서 좀 쉬게 해 주고요.\""]], [["“My Louisa knows,”", "\"우리 루이자도 알다시피,\""], ["observed Miss Tox, “that between friends like ourselves, any approach to ceremony would be out of the question.", "톡스 양이 말했다. \"우리 같은 친구 사이에서는 격식 비슷한 거라면 무엇이든 어림도 없는 일이지요."], ["Therefore—”", "그러니까—\""], ["Therefore Miss Tox finished the sentence, not in words but action; and putting on her gloves again, which she had taken off, and arming herself once more with her scissors, began to snip and clip among the leaves with microscopic industry.", "그러니까 톡스 양은 그 문장을 말이 아닌 행동으로 끝맺었다. 벗어 두었던 장갑을 다시 끼고, 가위로 한 번 더 무장하고는, 현미경을 들여다보는 듯한 부지런함으로 잎사귀 사이를 싹둑싹둑 잘라 내기 시작했다."]], [["“Florence has returned home also,”", "\"플로렌스도 집에 돌아왔어요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, after sitting silent for some time, with her head on one side, and her parasol sketching on the floor; “and really Florence is a great deal too old now, to continue to lead that solitary life to which she has been accustomed.", "한동안 고개를 한쪽으로 기울인 채, 파라솔로 바닥에 그림을 그리며 말없이 앉아 있던 칙 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 정말이지 플로렌스는 이제 너무 컸어요, 늘 익숙해져 있던 그 외톨이 생활을 계속하기에는 말이지요."], ["Of course she is.", "물론 그렇고말고요."], ["There can be no doubt about it.", "그건 의심할 여지가 없어요."], ["I should have very little respect, indeed, for anybody who could advocate a different opinion.", "정말이지 나는 다른 의견을 옹호할 수 있는 사람이라면 거의 존경하지 못할 거예요."], ["Whatever my wishes might be, I could not respect them.", "내 바람이 어떻든 간에, 나 자신의 바람조차도 존경할 수 없을 거예요."], ["We cannot command our feelings to such an extent as that.”", "우리는 감정을 그 정도까지 제멋대로 명령할 수는 없으니까요.\""]], [["Miss Tox assented, without being particular as to the intelligibility of the proposition.", "톡스 양은 그 명제가 알아들을 만한 것인지 따지지 않고 동의했다."]], [["“If she’s a strange girl,”", "\"그 애가 이상한 아이고,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “and if my brother Paul cannot feel perfectly comfortable in her society, after all the sad things that have happened, and all the terrible disappointments that have been undergone, then, what is the reply?", "칙 부인이 말했다. \"또 그 모든 슬픈 일들이 벌어지고, 그 모든 끔찍한 실망을 겪은 뒤에, 오라비 폴이 그 애와 함께 있으면서 도무지 편안함을 느낄 수 없다면, 그렇다면, 그 대답은 무엇일까요?"], ["That he must make an effort.", "오라비가 노력을 해야 한다는 거죠."], ["That he is bound to make an effort.", "노력해야 할 의무가 있다는 거예요."], ["We have always been a family remarkable for effort.", "우리 집안은 늘 노력으로 유명한 집안이었어요."], ["Paul is at the head of the family; almost the only representative of it left—for what am I—I am of no consequence—”", "폴은 그 집안의 가장이고, 거의 유일하게 남은 대표자예요—나야 뭐겠어요—나는 아무 대수롭지도 않은 사람이지만—\""]], [["“My dearest love,”", "\"우리 더없이 사랑스러운 분,\""], ["remonstrated Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 만류했다."]], [["Mrs Chick dried her eyes, which were, for the moment, overflowing; and proceeded:", "칙 부인은 잠시 넘쳐흐르던 눈을 닦고 말을 이었다."]], [["“And consequently he is more than ever bound to make an effort.", "\"그러니까 오라비는 그 어느 때보다도 노력을 해야 할 의무가 있는 거예요. 그리고 오라비가 그렇게 했다는 사실이 나에게는 일종의 충격으로 다가오긴 하지만—나는 워낙 약하고 어리석은 천성이라서요."], ["And though his having done so, comes upon me with a sort of shock—for mine is a very weak and foolish nature; which is anything but a blessing I am sure; I often wish my heart was a marble slab, or a paving-stone—”", "그게 결코 축복이 아닌 건 분명하고요. 내 심장이 대리석 판이나 포석이었으면 하고 자주 바란다니까요—\""]], [["“My sweet Louisa,”", "\"우리 어여쁜 루이자,\""], ["remonstrated Miss Tox again.", "톡스 양이 다시 만류했다."]], [["“Still, it is a triumph to me to know that he is so true to himself, and to his name of Dombey; although, of course, I always knew he would be.", "\"그래도, 오라비가 자기 자신에게, 또 돔비라는 이름에 그토록 충실하다는 걸 아는 건 나에게 승리예요. 물론, 나는 늘 오라비가 그러리라는 걸 알고 있었지만요."], ["I only hope,”", "다만 바라기로는,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, after a pause, “that she may be worthy of the name too.”", "칙 부인이 잠시 멈추었다가 말했다. \"그 여자도 그 이름에 어울리는 사람이었으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["Miss Tox filled a little green watering-pot from a jug, and happening to look up when she had done so, was so surprised by the amount of expression Mrs Chick had conveyed into her face, and was bestowing upon her, that she put the little watering-pot on the table for the present, and sat down near it.", "톡스 양은 작은 초록색 물뿌리개를 주전자에서 채우고는, 그것을 마친 뒤 우연히 고개를 들었는데, 칙 부인이 자기 얼굴에 담아 자기에게 보내고 있는 표정의 분량에 어찌나 놀랐던지, 그 작은 물뿌리개를 일단 탁자에 내려놓고 그 곁에 앉았다."]], [["“My dear Louisa,”", "\"루이자,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “will it be the least satisfaction to you, if I venture to observe in reference to that remark, that I, as a humble individual, think your sweet niece in every way most promising?”", "톡스 양이 말했다. \"그 말씀에 관해서, 한낱 보잘것없는 사람으로서 제가 감히 한마디 하자면, 당신의 그 사랑스러운 조카가 모든 면에서 더없이 장래가 촉망된다고 생각하는데요, 혹시 조금이라도 위안이 되실까요?\""]], [["“What do you mean, Lucretia?”", "\"무슨 말이에요, 루크리셔?\""], ["returned Mrs Chick, with increased stateliness of manner.", "칙 부인이 한층 위엄을 더한 태도로 대꾸했다."], ["“To what remark of mine, my dear, do you refer?”", "\"내 어느 말을 두고 하는 말이지요?\""]], [["“Her being worthy of her name, my love,”", "\"그 애가 그 이름에 어울린다는 말씀이요,\""], ["replied Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 대답했다."]], [["“If,”", "\"만일,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with solemn patience, “I have not expressed myself with clearness, Lucretia, the fault of course is mine.", "칙 부인이 엄숙한 인내심을 담아 말했다. \"내가 분명히 표현하지 못했다면, 루크리셔, 물론 그건 내 잘못이지요."], ["There is, perhaps, no reason why I should express myself at all, except the intimacy that has subsisted between us, and which I very much hope, Lucretia—confidently hope—nothing will occur to disturb.", "어쩌면 우리 사이에 이어져 온 친밀함이 아니라면, 내가 굳이 무어라 표현할 이유 자체가 없을지도 모르지요. 그리고 그 친밀함을, 루크리셔, 나는 진심으로 바라기를—굳게 바라기를—그 무엇도 흩뜨리지 않았으면 해요."], ["Because, why should I do anything else?", "그렇지 않다면, 내가 무엇 때문에 달리 굴겠어요?"], ["There is no reason; it would be absurd.", "이유가 없지요. 터무니없는 일일 테고요."], ["But I wish to express myself clearly, Lucretia; and therefore to go back to that remark, I must beg to say that it was not intended to relate to Florence, in any way.”", "하지만 나는 분명히 표현하고 싶어요, 루크리셔. 그러니 그 말로 돌아가자면, 그건 어떤 식으로도 플로렌스와 관련된 것이 아니었다고 말해야겠어요.\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"정말요!\""], ["returned Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 대꾸했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick shortly and decisively.", "칙 부인이 짤막하고 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“Pardon me, my dear,”", "\"미안해요,\""], ["rejoined her meek friend; “but I cannot have understood it.", "그 온순한 친구가 응수했다. \"하지만 내가 도무지 이해를 못 했나 봐요."], ["I fear I am dull.”", "내가 둔한가 봐요.\""]], [["Mrs Chick looked round the room and over the way; at the plants, at the bird, at the watering-pot, at almost everything within view, except Miss Tox; and finally dropping her glance upon Miss Tox, for a moment, on its way to the ground, said, looking meanwhile with elevated eyebrows at the carpet:", "칙 부인은 방 안과 길 건너편을 둘러보았다. 화초며, 새며, 물뿌리개며, 톡스 양만 빼고 시야에 들어오는 거의 모든 것을 보고는, 마침내 시선이 바닥으로 향하던 길에 잠시 톡스 양에게 떨어뜨렸다가, 눈썹을 치켜올린 채 카펫을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“When I speak, Lucretia, of her being worthy of the name, I speak of my brother Paul’s second wife.", "\"내가, 루크리셔, 그 여자가 이름에 어울린다고 말할 때, 그건 오라비 폴의 두 번째 아내를 두고 하는 말이에요."], ["I believe I have already said, in effect, if not in the very words I now use, that it is his intention to marry a second wife.”", "똑같은 말은 아니더라도 사실상 그런 뜻으로, 오라비가 두 번째 아내를 맞을 작정이라는 걸 이미 말한 것 같은데요.\""]], [["Miss Tox left her seat in a hurry, and returned to her plants; clipping among the stems and leaves, with as little favour as a barber working at so many pauper heads of hair.", "톡스 양은 황급히 자리를 떠나 다시 화초로 돌아갔다. 마치 수많은 극빈자들의 머리털을 손질하는 이발사만큼이나 인정사정없이 줄기와 잎사귀 사이를 싹둑싹둑 잘라 댔다."]], [["“Whether she will be fully sensible of the distinction conferred upon her,”", "\"그 여자가 자기에게 베풀어진 영예를 충분히 알아챌 수 있을지,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, in a lofty tone, “is quite another question.", "칙 부인이 고고한 어조로 말했다. \"그건 또 별개의 문제지요."], ["I hope she may be.", "그러기를 바라요."], ["We are bound to think well of one another in this world, and I hope she may be.", "이 세상에서 우리는 서로를 좋게 생각해야 할 의무가 있으니, 나도 그러기를 바라요."], ["I have not been advised with myself.", "나와는 상의도 없었어요."], ["If I had been advised with, I have no doubt my advice would have been cavalierly received, and therefore it is infinitely better as it is.", "만일 상의했다면, 내 조언은 틀림없이 무례하게 받아들여졌을 테니, 그러니 지금 이대로가 한없이 나아요."], ["I much prefer it as it is.”", "나는 이대로가 훨씬 좋아요.\""]], [["Miss Tox, with head bent down, still clipped among the plants.", "톡스 양은 고개를 숙인 채로 여전히 화초 사이를 잘라 댔다."], ["Mrs Chick, with energetic shakings of her own head from time to time, continued to hold forth, as if in defiance of somebody.", "칙 부인은 이따금 자기 머리를 힘차게 절레절레 흔들며, 마치 누군가에게 도전이라도 하듯 일장연설을 계속했다."]], [["“If my brother Paul had consulted with me, which he sometimes does—or rather, sometimes used to do; for he will naturally do that no more now, and this is a circumstance which I regard as a relief from responsibility,”", "\"만일 오라비 폴이 나와 의논했다면—이따금 그러기도 하니까요—아니, 차라리, 이따금 그러곤 했지요. 이제 자연히 더는 그러지 않을 테니까요."], ["said Mrs Chick, hysterically, “for I thank Heaven I am not jealous—”", "그리고 이건 내가 책임에서 벗어나는 일로 여기는 일이에요.\" 칙 부인이 신경질적으로 말했다."], ["here Mrs Chick again shed tears: “if my brother Paul had come to me, and had said, ‘Louisa, what kind of qualities would you advise me to look out for, in a wife?’ I should certainly have answered, ‘Paul, you must have family, you must have beauty, you must have dignity, you must have connexion.’ Those are the words I should have used.", "\"왜냐하면 다행히도 나는 질투하는 사람이 아니거든요—\" 여기서 칙 부인은 다시 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["You might have led me to the block immediately afterwards,”", "\"만일 오라비 폴이 나에게 와서, '루이자, 아내에게서 어떤 종류의 자질을 찾아보라고 충고하겠소?'라고 물었다면, 나는 분명 이렇게 대답했을 거예요. '폴, 가문이 있어야 해요."], ["said Mrs Chick, as if that consequence were highly probable, “but I should have used them.", "미모가 있어야 해요. 품위가 있어야 해요."], ["I should have said, ‘Paul!", "연줄이 있어야 해요.' 내가 썼을 말이 바로 그거예요."], ["You to marry a second time without family!", "그 직후에 나를 단두대로 끌고 갔다 해도,\" 마치 그런 결과가 충분히 있을 법한 일이라는 듯 칙 부인이 말했다."], ["You to marry without beauty!", "\"나는 그 말을 썼을 거예요. 나는 이렇게 말했을 거예요."], ["You to marry without dignity!", "'폴! 오라비가 가문도 없는 사람과 재혼을 한다고요!"], ["You to marry without connexion!", "미모도 없는 사람과 결혼한다고요! 품위도 없는 사람과 결혼한다고요!"], ["There is nobody in the world, not mad, who could dream of daring to entertain such a preposterous idea!’”", "연줄도 없는 사람과 결혼한다고요! 이 세상에, 미치지 않고서야, 그런 터무니없는 생각을 감히 품을 꿈조차 꿀 사람은 아무도 없어요!'\""]], [["Miss Tox stopped clipping; and with her head among the plants, listened attentively.", "톡스 양은 자르던 손을 멈추고, 화초 사이로 머리를 숙인 채 귀 기울여 들었다."], ["Perhaps Miss Tox thought there was hope in this exordium, and the warmth of Mrs Chick.", "어쩌면 톡스 양은 이 서두와 칙 부인의 열기 속에 희망이 있다고 생각했는지도 모른다."]], [["“I should have adopted this course of argument,”", "\"내가 이런 논법을 택했을 거예요,\""], ["pursued the discreet lady, “because I trust I am not a fool.", "신중한 부인이 말을 이었다. \"왜냐하면 나는 바보가 아니라고 믿으니까요."], ["I make no claim to be considered a person of superior intellect—though I believe some people have been extraordinary enough to consider me so; one so little humoured as I am, would very soon be disabused of any such notion; but I trust I am not a downright fool.", "내가 뛰어난 지성을 지닌 사람이라고 주장하는 건 아니에요—물론 어떤 사람들은 별나게도 나를 그렇게 여겨 왔지만요. 나처럼 응석받이로 자라지 못한 사람이라면 그런 생각은 이내 깨우쳐 버릴 테지만요—그래도 내가 완전히 바보는 아니라고 믿어요."], ["And to tell ME,”", "그리고 나에게,\""], ["said Mrs Chick with ineffable disdain, “that my brother Paul Dombey could ever contemplate the possibility of uniting himself to anybody—I don’t care who”—she was more sharp and emphatic in that short clause than in any other part of her discourse—“not possessing these requisites, would be to insult what understanding I have got, as much as if I was to be told that I was born and bred an elephant, which I may be told next,”", "칙 부인이 형언할 수 없는 경멸을 담아 말했다. \"오라비 폴 돔비가 그런 자질을 갖추지 못한—누구든 상관없어요\"—이 짧은 구절에서 그녀는 연설의 다른 어느 부분에서보다 날카롭고 강조하는 어조였다—\"누구든 그런 자와 결합할 가능성을 한순간이라도 떠올릴 수 있다고 말하는 것은, 내가 코끼리로 태어나 코끼리로 길러졌다고 말하는 것만큼이나 내 알량한 이해력을 모욕하는 일이에요."], ["said Mrs Chick, with resignation.", "뭐, 다음번엔 그런 말도 듣겠지요.\" 칙 부인이 체념하듯 말했다."], ["“It wouldn’t surprise me at all.", "\"나는 조금도 놀라지 않을 거예요."], ["I expect it.”", "으레 그러려니 하고 있으니까요.\""]], [["In the moment’s silence that ensued, Miss Tox’s scissors gave a feeble clip or two; but Miss Tox’s face was still invisible, and Miss Tox’s morning gown was agitated.", "뒤이은 한순간의 침묵 속에서, 톡스 양의 가위가 힘없이 한두 번 싹둑거렸다. 그러나 톡스 양의 얼굴은 여전히 보이지 않았고, 톡스 양의 아침 가운이 들썩이고 있었다."], ["Mrs Chick looked sideways at her, through the intervening plants, and went on to say, in a tone of bland conviction, and as one dwelling on a point of fact that hardly required to be stated:", "칙 부인은 사이에 놓인 화초 너머로 그녀를 곁눈질하고는, 부드럽게 확신에 찬 어조로, 굳이 입에 올릴 필요조차 없는 명백한 사실 하나를 곱씹는 사람처럼 말을 이었다."]], [["“Therefore, of course my brother Paul has done what was to be expected of him, and what anybody might have foreseen he would do, if he entered the marriage state again.", "\"그러니, 물론 오라비 폴은 마땅히 그에게 기대되었을 일을, 또 그가 다시 결혼 상태에 들어선다면 누구라도 예견했을 법한 일을 한 거예요. 솔직히 좀 뜻밖이긴 해요."], ["I confess it takes me rather by surprise, however gratifying; because when Paul went out of town I had no idea at all that he would form any attachment out of town, and he certainly had no attachment when he left here.", "아무리 흐뭇한 일이라도 말이에요. 왜냐하면 폴이 시외로 나갈 때, 나는 그이가 시외에서 무슨 애정을 맺으리라고는 전혀 생각도 못 했고, 여기를 떠날 때 그이에게는 분명 어떤 애정도 없었으니까요."], ["However, it seems to be extremely desirable in every point of view.", "그래도 모든 면에서 더없이 바람직한 일인 것 같아요."], ["I have no doubt the mother is a most genteel and elegant creature, and I have no right whatever to dispute the policy of her living with them: which is Paul’s affair, not mine—and as to Paul’s choice, herself, I have only seen her picture yet, but that is beautiful indeed.", "그 어머니라는 분이 더없이 점잖고 우아한 사람일 거라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없고, 그분이 그들과 함께 산다는 방침을 두고 내가 왈가왈부할 권리는 조금도 없어요. 그건 내 일이 아니라 폴의 일이니까요—그리고 폴이 고른 그 여자 본인으로 말하자면, 나는 아직 초상화밖에 못 봤지만, 그건 정말이지 아름다워요."], ["Her name is beautiful too,”", "이름도 아름답고요.\""], ["said Mrs Chick, shaking her head with energy, and arranging herself in her chair; “Edith is at once uncommon, as it strikes me, and distinguished.", "칙 부인이 힘차게 머리를 흔들며, 의자에 자세를 고쳐 앉으며 말했다. \"에디스는 내가 보기에 흔치 않으면서도 동시에 기품이 있어요."], ["Consequently, Lucretia, I have no doubt you will be happy to hear that the marriage is to take place immediately—of course, you will:”", "그러니, 루크리셔, 당신도 그 결혼이 곧 거행될 거라는 소식을 기쁘게 들을 게 분명해요—물론, 그러겠지요.\""], ["great emphasis again: “and that you are delighted with this change in the condition of my brother, who has shown you a great deal of pleasant attention at various times.”", "다시 한번 잔뜩 힘을 주어 말했다. \"그리고 여러 차례 당신에게 많은 유쾌한 호의를 베풀어 온 오라비의 처지에 일어난 이 변화를 당신도 기뻐할 게 분명해요.\""]], [["Miss Tox made no verbal answer, but took up the little watering-pot with a trembling hand, and looked vacantly round as if considering what article of furniture would be improved by the contents.", "톡스 양은 말로 대답하지는 않고, 떨리는 손으로 작은 물뿌리개를 집어 들더니, 마치 그 안의 물로 어떤 가구를 더 보기 좋게 만들 수 있을까 궁리하는 사람처럼 멍하니 둘러보았다."], ["The room door opening at this crisis of Miss Tox’s feelings, she started, laughed aloud, and fell into the arms of the person entering; happily insensible alike of Mrs Chick’s indignant countenance and of the Major at his window over the way, who had his double-barrelled eye-glass in full action, and whose face and figure were dilated with Mephistophelean joy.", "톡스 양의 감정이 이런 고비에 이른 바로 그때 방문이 열렸고, 그녀는 흠칫 놀라 큰 소리로 웃으며, 들어오는 사람의 품에 쓰러졌다. 다행히도 칙 부인의 분개한 얼굴도, 길 건너편 자기 창가의 소령도 알아채지 못한 채였는데, 소령은 쌍안경 안경을 한껏 작동시키며, 메피스토펠레스 같은 환희로 얼굴과 몸뚱이를 한껏 부풀리고 있었다."]], [["Not so the expatriated Native, amazed supporter of Miss Tox’s swooning form, who, coming straight upstairs, with a polite inquiry touching Miss Tox’s health (in exact pursuance of the Major’s malicious instructions), had accidentally arrived in the very nick of time to catch the delicate burden in his arms, and to receive the contents of the little watering-pot in his shoe; both of which circumstances, coupled with his consciousness of being closely watched by the wrathful Major, who had threatened the usual penalty in regard of every bone in his skin in case of any failure, combined to render him a moving spectacle of mental and bodily distress.", "쓰러지는 톡스 양의 몸을 떠받친 망연자실한 외국 추방민은 그렇지 못했다. 그는 (소령의 짓궂은 지시를 정확히 따라) 톡스 양의 건강을 정중히 묻기 위해 곧장 위층으로 올라오다가, 마침 딱 그 순간에 우연히 도착하여 그 가냘픈 짐을 두 팔로 받아 들고, 동시에 작은 물뿌리개의 내용물을 제 구두에 받아 내게 되었던 것이다. 이 두 가지 사정이, 혹시라도 실수하면 그의 살갗 속 뼈 하나하나에 늘 하던 벌을 내리겠다고 위협한 노기등등한 소령의 감시를 바짝 받고 있다는 의식과 어우러져, 그를 정신적으로나 육체적으로나 고통받는 움직이는 구경거리로 만들어 놓았다."]], [["For some moments, this afflicted foreigner remained clasping Miss Tox to his heart, with an energy of action in remarkable opposition to his disconcerted face, while that poor lady trickled slowly down upon him the very last sprinklings of the little watering-pot, as if he were a delicate exotic (which indeed he was), and might be almost expected to blow while the gentle rain descended.", "이 고통받는 외국인은 몇 순간 동안 톡스 양을 제 가슴에 꼭 끌어안은 채 있었는데, 그 행동의 힘참은 당황한 얼굴과는 놀랄 만큼 대조적이었다. 그러는 동안 그 가엾은 부인은 작은 물뿌리개의 맨 마지막 물방울들을 그의 위로 천천히 흘려보냈으니, 마치 그가 섬세한 외래 식물이라도 되어(사실 그렇기도 했다), 그 부드러운 비가 내리는 동안 거의 꽃이라도 피우리라 기대되는 것 같았다."], ["Mrs Chick, at length recovering sufficient presence of mind to interpose, commanded him to drop Miss Tox upon the sofa and withdraw; and the exile promptly obeying, she applied herself to promote Miss Tox’s recovery.", "마침내 끼어들 만큼 정신을 차린 칙 부인이 그에게 톡스 양을 소파에 내려놓고 물러가라고 명령했고, 그 추방민이 즉각 복종하자, 그녀는 톡스 양을 회복시키는 일에 착수했다."]], [["But none of that gentle concern which usually characterises the daughters of Eve in their tending of each other; none of that freemasonry in fainting, by which they are generally bound together in a mysterious bond of sisterhood; was visible in Mrs Chick’s demeanour.", "그러나 이브의 딸들이 서로를 돌볼 때 으레 보이는 그 부드러운 염려는—기절했을 때 그들을 신비로운 자매애의 끈으로 으레 묶어 주는 그 비밀결사의 의리는—칙 부인의 거동에서는 조금도 보이지 않았다."], ["Rather like the executioner who restores the victim to sensation previous to proceeding with the torture (or was wont to do so, in the good old times for which all true men wear perpetual mourning), did Mrs Chick administer the smelling-bottle, the slapping on the hands, the dashing of cold water on the face, and the other proved remedies.", "그보다는, 고문을 진행하기에 앞서 희생자의 감각을 되돌려 놓는 사형 집행인처럼(진정한 사내라면 모두가 영원한 애도의 상복을 입는 그 좋았던 옛 시절에 으레 그러했듯이), 칙 부인은 냄새 맡는 약병이며, 손바닥 때리기며, 얼굴에 찬물 끼얹기며, 그 밖의 입증된 처치들을 시행했다."], ["And when, at length, Miss Tox opened her eyes, and gradually became restored to animation and consciousness, Mrs Chick drew off as from a criminal, and reversing the precedent of the murdered king of Denmark, regarded her more in anger than in sorrow.”", "그리고 마침내 톡스 양이 눈을 뜨고 차츰 생기와 의식을 되찾았을 때, 칙 부인은 죄인에게서 물러나듯 뒤로 물러나며, 살해당한 덴마크 왕의 선례를 뒤집어, 그녀를 슬픔보다는 노여움으로 바라보았다."]], [["“Lucretia!”", "\"루크리셔!\""], ["said Mrs Chick “I will not attempt to disguise what I feel.", "칙 부인이 말했다. \"내가 느끼는 바를 숨기려 들지 않겠어요."], ["My eyes are opened, all at once.", "단번에 내 눈이 뜨였어요."], ["I wouldn’t have believed this, if a Saint had told it to me.”", "성인이 나에게 이런 이야기를 해 주었다 해도 나는 믿지 않았을 거예요.\""]], [["“I am foolish to give way to faintness,”", "\"기절 따위에 굴복하다니 내가 어리석었어요,\""], ["Miss Tox faltered.", "톡스 양이 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["“I shall be better presently.”", "\"곧 나아질 거예요.\""]], [["“You will be better presently, Lucretia!”", "\"곧 나아질 거라고요, 루크리셔!\""], ["repeated Mrs Chick, with exceeding scorn.", "칙 부인이 지독한 경멸을 담아 되풀이했다."], ["“Do you suppose I am blind?", "\"내가 눈먼 사람인 줄 알아요?"], ["Do you imagine I am in my second childhood?", "내가 노망이라도 든 줄 알아요?"], ["No, Lucretia!", "천만에요, 루크리셔!"], ["I am obliged to you!”", "신세 한번 잘 졌네요!\""]], [["Miss Tox directed an imploring, helpless kind of look towards her friend, and put her handkerchief before her face.", "톡스 양은 애원하는, 어쩔 줄 모르는 눈길을 친구에게 보내고는, 손수건으로 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["“If anyone had told me this yesterday,”", "\"누군가 어제 나에게 이 이야기를 했다면,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with majesty, “or even half-an-hour ago, I should have been tempted, I almost believe, to strike them to the earth.", "칙 부인이 위엄을 담아 말했다. \"아니, 반 시간 전에만 했어도, 나는 거의, 그 사람을 땅바닥에 후려쳐 넘기고 싶은 유혹을 받았을 거예요."], ["Lucretia Tox, my eyes are opened to you all at once.", "루크리셔 톡스, 당신에 대해 단번에 내 눈이 뜨였어요."], ["The scales:”", "비늘이,\""], ["here Mrs Chick cast down an imaginary pair, such as are commonly used in grocers”", "여기서 칙 부인은 식료품 가게에서 흔히 쓰는 그런 저울 한 쌍을 떨어뜨리는 시늉을 했다."], ["shops: “have fallen from my sight.", "\"내 시야에서 떨어져 나갔어요."], ["The blindness of my confidence is past, Lucretia.", "내 신뢰의 눈멂은 지나갔어요, 루크리셔."], ["It has been abused and played, upon, and evasion is quite out of the question now, I assure you.”", "그것은 악용되고 농락당했고, 이제 발뺌은 어림도 없는 일이에요, 정말이지.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["to what do you allude so cruelly, my love?”", "무얼 그렇게 잔인하게 빗대시는 거예요?\""], ["asked Miss Tox, through her tears.", "톡스 양이 눈물을 흘리며 물었다."]], [["“Lucretia,”", "\"루크리셔,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “ask your own heart.", "칙 부인이 말했다."], ["I must entreat you not to address me by any such familiar term as you have just used, if you please.", "\"당신 자신의 양심에게 물어봐요. 그리고 부탁하건대, 방금 쓴 그런 허물없는 호칭으로 나를 부르지 말아 줘요."], ["I have some self-respect left, though you may think otherwise.”", "당신은 어떻게 생각할지 몰라도, 나에게는 아직 약간의 자존심이 남아 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, Louisa!”", "\"어머나, 루이자!\""], ["cried Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 외쳤다."], ["“How can you speak to me like that?”", "\"어떻게 나에게 그런 식으로 말할 수 있어요?\""]], [["“How can I speak to you like that?”", "\"어떻게 당신에게 그런 식으로 말할 수 있느냐고요?\""], ["retorted Mrs Chick, who, in default of having any particular argument to sustain herself upon, relied principally on such repetitions for her most withering effects.", "칙 부인이 응수했는데, 그녀는 자신을 뒷받침할 딱히 특별한 논거가 없을 때면 가장 신랄한 효과를 내기 위해 주로 이런 되풀이에 기댔다. \"그런 식으로라니!"], ["“Like that! You may well say like that, indeed!”", "그래요, 그런 식으로라고 말할 만도 하지요, 정말!\""]], [["Miss Tox sobbed pitifully.", "톡스 양은 가엾게 흐느꼈다."]], [["“The idea!”", "\"생각해 봐요!\""], ["said Mrs Chick, “of your having basked at my brother’s fireside, like a serpent, and wound yourself, through me, almost into his confidence, Lucretia, that you might, in secret, entertain designs upon him, and dare to aspire to contemplate the possibility of his uniting himself to you!", "칙 부인이 말했다. \"당신이 우리 오라비의 난롯가에 뱀처럼 들러붙어 햇볕을 쬐며, 나를 통해 그이의 신임 속으로 거의 파고들어 갔다는 것을, 그것도 남몰래 그이에게 흑심을 품고, 감히 그이가 당신과 결합할 가능성을 그려 볼 만큼 야심을 부리려고 그랬다는 것을요!"], ["Why, it is an idea,”", "아니, 그건,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with sarcastic dignity, “the absurdity of which almost relieves its treachery.”", "칙 부인이 빈정대는 위엄을 담아 말했다. \"그 터무니없음이 그 배신을 거의 덜어 줄 지경인 생각이에요.\""]], [["“Pray, Louisa,”", "\"제발, 루이자,\""], ["urged Miss Tox, “do not say such dreadful things.”", "톡스 양이 호소했다. \"그런 끔찍한 말은 하지 말아요.\""]], [["“Dreadful things!”", "\"끔찍한 말이라니!\""], ["repeated Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 되풀이했다."], ["“Dreadful things!", "\"끔찍한 말!"], ["Is it not a fact, Lucretia, that you have just now been unable to command your feelings even before me, whose eyes you had so completely closed?”", "루크리셔, 당신이 방금, 그토록 완전히 눈을 가려 놓았던 나 앞에서조차 감정을 다스리지 못했다는 게 사실이 아닌가요?\""]], [["“I have made no complaint,” sobbed Miss Tox.", "\"나는 아무 불평도 하지 않았어요,\""], ["“I have said nothing.", "톡스 양이 흐느꼈다."], ["If I have been a little overpowered by your news, Louisa, and have ever had any lingering thought that Mr Dombey was inclined to be particular towards me, surely you will not condemn me.”", "\"아무 말도 하지 않았어요. 만일 내가 당신의 소식에 조금 압도되었고, 돔비 씨가 나에게 각별한 마음을 두는 듯하다는 어떤 미련을 품은 적이 있다 한들, 설마 당신이 나를 나무라지는 않겠지요.\""]], [["“She is going to say,”", "\"이 사람이 무슨 말을 하려는지,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, addressing herself to the whole of the furniture, in a comprehensive glance of resignation and appeal, “She is going to say—I know it—that I have encouraged her!”", "칙 부인이 체념과 호소가 담긴 포괄적인 눈길로 가구 전체를 향해 말했다. \"이 사람이 무슨 말을 하려는지—나는 알아요—내가 자기를 부추겼다고 말하려는 거예요!\""]], [["“I don’t wish to exchange reproaches, dear Louisa,”", "\"비난을 주고받고 싶지 않아요, 사랑하는 루이자,\""], ["sobbed Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 흐느꼈다."], ["“Nor do I wish to complain.", "\"불평을 하고 싶지도 않고요."], ["But, in my own defence—”", "하지만 나 자신을 변호하기 위해서는—\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["cried Mrs Chick, looking round the room with a prophetic smile, “that’s what she’s going to say.", "칙 부인이 예언자 같은 미소를 지으며 방 안을 둘러보며 외쳤다. \"저게 바로 이 사람이 하려는 말이에요."], ["I knew it.", "내가 알았다니까요."], ["You had better say it.", "차라리 말하는 게 낫겠어요."], ["Say it openly!", "터놓고 말해 봐요!"], ["Be open, Lucretia Tox,”", "솔직해져요, 루크리셔 톡스,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with desperate sternness, “whatever you are.”", "칙 부인이 절박한 준엄함을 담아 말했다. \"당신이 어떤 사람이든 간에 말이에요.\""]], [["“In my own defence,”", "\"나 자신을 변호하기 위해서,\" 톡스 양이 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["faltered Miss Tox, “and only in my own defence against your unkind words, my dear Louisa, I would merely ask you if you haven’t often favoured such a fancy, and even said it might happen, for anything we could tell?”", "\"그리고 오직 당신의 매정한 말로부터 나 자신을 변호하기 위해서만, 사랑하는 루이자, 그저 한 가지만 묻고 싶어요. 당신이야말로 그런 공상을 자주 부추기지 않았던가요, 아니, 우리가 알 수 없는 일이니 그런 일이 일어날 수도 있다고 말하기까지 하지 않았던가요?\""]], [["“There is a point,”", "\"한 가지,\" 칙 부인이 일어서며 말했다."], ["said Mrs Chick, rising, not as if she were going to stop at the floor, but as if she were about to soar up, high, into her native skies, “beyond which endurance becomes ridiculous, if not culpable.", "그것은 바닥에서 멈추려는 것이 아니라, 마치 자신이 타고난 하늘로 높이 솟아오르려는 듯한 모습이었다. \"참는 데에도 한도가 있어요, 그 선을 넘으면 참는다는 게 우스꽝스러워지고, 심하면 죄가 되기까지 하지요."], ["I can bear much; but not too much.", "나는 많은 것을 견딜 수 있어요."], ["What spell was on me when I came into this house this day, I don’t know; but I had a presentiment—a dark presentiment,”", "하지만 너무 지나친 건 안 돼요. 오늘 이 집에 들어올 때 무슨 마력이 나를 사로잡았는지는 모르겠지만, 나에게는 예감이—어두운 예감이,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, with a shiver, “that something was going to happen.", "칙 부인이 몸서리를 치며 말했다. \"무언가 일어나려 한다는 예감이 있었어요."], ["Well may I have had that foreboding, Lucretia, when my confidence of many years is destroyed in an instant, when my eyes are opened all at once, and when I find you revealed in your true colours.", "그런 불길한 예감이 들 만도 했지요, 루크리셔, 수년간의 내 신뢰가 한순간에 무너지고, 단번에 내 눈이 뜨이고, 당신의 본색이 드러난 걸 알게 되었으니 말이에요."], ["Lucretia, I have been mistaken in you.", "루크리셔, 나는 당신을 잘못 봤어요."], ["It is better for us both that this subject should end here.", "이 이야기는 여기서 끝내는 게 우리 둘 다에게 나아요."], ["I wish you well, and I shall ever wish you well.", "나는 당신이 잘되기를 바라고, 앞으로도 늘 잘되기를 바랄 거예요."], ["But, as an individual who desires to be true to herself in her own poor position, whatever that position may be, or may not be—and as the sister of my brother—and as the sister-in-law of my brother’s wife—and as a connexion by marriage of my brother’s wife’s mother—may I be permitted to add, as a Dombey?—I can wish you nothing else but good morning.”", "하지만, 자기 자신의 보잘것없는 처지에—그 처지가 무엇이든, 아니든 간에—진실하고자 하는 한 개인으로서, 또 내 오라비의 누이로서, 또 내 오라비 아내의 시누이로서, 또 내 오라비 아내의 어머니의 인척으로서—덧붙이는 걸 허락해 준다면, 돔비 집안의 한 사람으로서—나는 당신에게 그저 안녕히 계시라는 인사밖에는 빌어 줄 게 없어요.\""]], [["These words, delivered with cutting suavity, tempered and chastened by a lofty air of moral rectitude, carried the speaker to the door.", "도덕적 올바름의 고고한 분위기로 누그러지고 다듬어진 채, 베일 듯한 상냥함으로 내뱉어진 이 말들은, 화자를 문간까지 데려갔다."], ["There she inclined her head in a ghostly and statue-like manner, and so withdrew to her carriage, to seek comfort and consolation in the arms of Mr Chick, her lord.", "거기서 그녀는 유령처럼, 또 조각상처럼 고개를 기울이고는, 자기의 주군인 칙 씨의 품에서 위로와 위안을 구하고자 마차로 물러났다."]], [["Figuratively speaking, that is to say; for the arms of Mr Chick were full of his newspaper.", "비유적으로 말하자면 그렇다는 것이다. 칙 씨의 품은 신문으로 가득 차 있었으니 말이다."], ["Neither did that gentleman address his eyes towards his wife otherwise than by stealth.", "또 이 신사는 자기 아내에게 눈길을 주되 몰래 곁눈질하는 것 말고는 달리 주지 않았다."], ["Neither did he offer any consolation whatever.", "또 그는 어떤 위로도 전혀 건네지 않았다."], ["In short, he sat reading, and humming fag ends of tunes, and sometimes glancing furtively at her without delivering himself of a word, good, bad, or indifferent.", "요컨대 그는 앉아서 신문을 읽고, 노래의 토막토막을 흥얼거리며, 이따금 좋은 말이든 나쁜 말이든 무덤덤한 말이든 한마디도 내뱉지 않고 슬그머니 아내를 흘끔거렸을 뿐이다."]], [["In the meantime Mrs Chick sat swelling and bridling, and tossing her head, as if she were still repeating that solemn formula of farewell to Lucretia Tox.", "그동안 칙 부인은 잔뜩 부풀어 오른 채, 거드름을 피우며, 머리를 홱홱 젖히고 앉아 있었으니, 마치 루크리셔 톡스에게 그 엄숙한 작별 문구를 아직도 되뇌고 있는 듯했다."], ["At length, she said aloud, “Oh the extent to which her eyes had been opened that day!”", "마침내 그녀는 소리 내어 말했다. \"오, 그날 그토록 내 눈이 뜨이게 되다니!\""]], [["“To which your eyes have been opened, my dear!”", "\"당신의 눈이 뜨이게 되다니 말이지요, 여보!\""], ["repeated Mr Chick.", "칙 씨가 되풀이했다."]], [["“Oh, don’t talk to me!”", "\"오, 나한테 말 걸지 마세요!\""], ["said Mrs Chic “if you can bear to see me in this state, and not ask me what the matter is, you had better hold your tongue for ever.”", "칙 부인이 말했다. \"내가 이런 꼴인 걸 보고도 무슨 일이냐고 묻지 못할 사람이라면, 차라리 영원히 입을 다무는 게 나아요.\""]], [["“What is the matter, my dear?”", "\"무슨 일이오, 여보?\""], ["asked Mr Chick", "칙 씨가 물었다."]], [["“To think,”", "\"생각해 봐요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, in a state of soliloquy, “that she should ever have conceived the base idea of connecting herself with our family by a marriage with Paul!", "칙 부인이 혼잣말하듯이 말했다. \"그 여자가 폴과의 결혼으로 우리 집안과 인연을 맺겠다는 비열한 생각을 다 품다니!"], ["To think that when she was playing at horses with that dear child who is now in his grave—I never liked it at the time—she should have been hiding such a double-faced design!", "지금은 무덤 속에 있는 그 사랑스러운 아이와 말놀이를 하면서—나는 그때도 그게 영 마음에 들지 않았어요—그런 두 얼굴의 속셈을 숨기고 있었다니!"], ["I wonder she was never afraid that something would happen to her.", "그 여자에게 무슨 일이 닥치지 않을까 한 번도 두렵지 않았다니 신기하네요."], ["She is fortunate if nothing does.”", "아무 일도 일어나지 않는다면 운이 좋은 거지요.\""]], [["“I really thought, my dear,”", "\"솔직히 말이오, 여보,\" 칙 씨가 신문으로 한참 콧대를 문지르고는 천천히 말했다."], ["said Mr Chick slowly, after rubbing the bridge of his nose for some time with his newspaper, “that you had gone on the same tack yourself, all along, until this morning; and had thought it would be a convenient thing enough, if it could have been brought about.”", "\"나는 오늘 아침 전까지는 당신도 줄곧 똑같은 노선을 타고 있었던 줄 알았소. 그 일이 성사될 수만 있다면 그것도 꽤 편리한 일이겠다고 여기고 있었던 줄 알았단 말이오.\""]], [["Mrs Chick instantly burst into tears, and told Mr Chick that if he wished to trample upon her with his boots, he had better do It.", "칙 부인은 즉시 울음을 터뜨리며, 칙 씨에게, 부츠로 자기를 짓밟고 싶거든 차라리 그렇게 하는 게 낫겠다고 말했다."]], [["“But with Lucretia Tox I have done,”", "\"하지만 루크리셔 톡스와는 이제 끝이에요,\" 칙 씨를 몹시 떨게 하며 몇 분간 감정에 몸을 내맡긴 끝에 칙 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs Chick, after abandoning herself to her feelings for some minutes, to Mr Chick’s great terror.", "\"그럴 자격이 있기를 내가 바라고 또 믿는 어떤 사람을 위해서라면, 폴의 신임을 양보하는 것쯤은 견딜 수 있어요. 폴이 원한다면 가엾은 패니의 자리를 그 사람으로 채울 완벽한 권리가 있는 그 사람을 위해서라면 말이에요."], ["“I can bear to resign Paul’s confidence in favour of one who, I hope and trust, may be deserving of it, and with whom he has a perfect right to replace poor Fanny if he chooses; I can bear to be informed, in Paul’s cool manner, of such a change in his plans, and never to be consulted until all is settled and determined; but deceit I can not bear, and with Lucretia Tox I have done.", "폴의 그 냉랭한 태도로 자기 계획의 그런 변화를 통고받고, 모든 게 정해지고 결정될 때까지 한 번도 의논받지 못하는 것쯤은 견딜 수 있어요. 하지만 기만은 견딜 수 없고, 그래서 루크리셔 톡스와는 이제 끝이에요."], ["It is better as it is,”", "이대로가 나아요,\" 칙 부인이 경건하게 말했다."], ["said Mrs Chick, piously; “much better.", "\"훨씬 나아요. 이런 일이 있고서 그 여자와 편안하게 잘 어울려 지내려면 한참 걸렸을 거예요."], ["It would have been a long time before I could have accommodated myself comfortably with her, after this; and I really don’t know, as Paul is going to be very grand, and these are people of condition, that she would have been quite presentable, and might not have compromised myself.", "게다가 폴이 아주 거창해질 참이고, 그쪽은 지체 높은 사람들인데, 그 여자가 과연 사람들 앞에 내놓을 만했을지, 나까지 망신시키지 않았을지 정말 모르겠다니까요. 모든 일에는 섭리가 있어요."], ["There’s a providence in everything; everything works for the best; I have been tried today but on the whole I do not regret it.”", "모든 일은 가장 좋은 쪽으로 풀려요. 오늘 나는 시험을 당했지만, 전체적으로 보면 후회하지 않아요.\""]], [["In which Christian spirit, Mrs Chick dried her eyes and smoothed her lap, and sat as became a person calm under a great wrong.", "이런 기독교적 정신으로, 칙 부인은 눈물을 닦고 무릎 위를 매만지고는, 큰 부당함 속에서도 평온한 사람답게 앉아 있었다."], ["Mr Chick feeling his unworthiness no doubt, took an early opportunity of being set down at a street corner and walking away whistling, with his shoulders very much raised, and his hands in his pockets.", "칙 씨는 분명 자신의 보잘것없음을 느꼈는지, 일찌감치 어느 길모퉁이에서 내려, 어깨를 잔뜩 추켜올리고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러넣은 채 휘파람을 불며 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["While poor excommunicated Miss Tox, who, if she were a fawner and toad-eater, was at least an honest and a constant one, and had ever borne a faithful friendship towards her impeacher and had been truly absorbed and swallowed up in devotion to the magnificence of Mr Dombey—while poor excommunicated Miss Tox watered her plants with her tears, and felt that it was winter in Princess’s Place.", "한편 가엾게 파문당한 톡스 양은, 비록 아첨꾼이요 알랑쇠였을지언정, 적어도 정직하고 한결같은 아첨꾼이었으며, 자기를 탄핵한 그 사람에게 늘 충실한 우정을 지녀 왔고, 돔비 씨의 위풍당당함에 대한 헌신에 참으로 빠져들어 송두리째 삼켜져 있었거니와—그 가엾게 파문당한 톡스 양은 눈물로 화초에 물을 주며, 프린세스 플레이스에 겨울이 찾아왔음을 느꼈다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXX. The interval before the Marriage", "# CHAPTER XXX. The interval before the Marriage"]], [["Although the enchanted house was no more, and the working world had broken into it, and was hammering and crashing and tramping up and down stairs all day long keeping Diogenes in an incessant paroxysm of barking, from sunrise to sunset—evidently convinced that his enemy had got the better of him at last, and was then sacking the premises in triumphant defiance—there was, at first, no other great change in the method of Florence’s life.", "마법에 걸린 듯하던 그 집은 이제 더는 존재하지 않았고, 일하는 세상이 그 안으로 밀고 들어와 하루 종일 망치질을 하고 부수고 계단을 오르내리며 쿵쿵거렸으니, 디오게네스는 해 뜰 때부터 해 질 때까지 쉴 새 없이 발작하듯 짖어 댔다—제 원수가 마침내 자기를 이겨 먹고는 이제 의기양양하게 도전장을 던지며 집을 약탈하고 있다고 확신하는 게 분명했다—그러나 처음에는 플로렌스의 생활 방식에 그 밖의 큰 변화는 없었다."], ["At night, when the workpeople went away, the house was dreary and deserted again; and Florence, listening to their voices echoing through the hall and staircase as they departed, pictured to herself the cheerful homes to which they were returning, and the children who were waiting for them, and was glad to think that they were merry and well pleased to go.", "밤이 되어 일꾼들이 떠나면, 집은 다시금 음산하고 적막해졌다. 그리고 플로렌스는 그들이 떠나면서 현관과 계단을 통해 메아리치는 목소리에 귀 기울이며, 그들이 돌아갈 정겨운 집들과 그들을 기다리는 아이들을 마음속에 그려 보았고, 그들이 명랑하게, 기꺼이 떠나간다고 생각하니 기뻤다."]], [["She welcomed back the evening silence as an old friend, but it came now with an altered face, and looked more kindly on her.", "플로렌스는 저녁의 고요함을 옛 벗처럼 반겼지만, 이제 그것은 달라진 얼굴로 찾아와 그녀를 한결 다정하게 바라보았다."], ["Fresh hope was in it.", "그 안에는 새로운 희망이 깃들어 있었다."], ["The beautiful lady who had soothed and carressed her, in the very room in which her heart had been so wrung, was a spirit of promise to her.", "그녀의 마음이 그토록 쥐어짜였던 바로 그 방에서 자기를 달래고 어루만져 주었던 그 아름다운 부인은, 그녀에게 약속의 정령과도 같았다."], ["Soft shadows of the bright life dawning, when her father’s affection should be gradually won, and all, or much should be restored, of what she had lost on the dark day when a mother’s love had faded with a mother’s last breath on her cheek, moved about her in the twilight and were welcome company.", "아버지의 애정을 차츰 얻어 내고, 어머니의 사랑이 어머니의 마지막 숨결과 함께 뺨 위에서 스러져 가던 그 어두운 날에 잃어버린 것을, 전부 혹은 많은 부분 되찾게 될, 동트는 밝은 삶의 부드러운 그림자들이 황혼 속에서 그녀의 둘레를 맴돌며 반가운 벗이 되어 주었다."], ["Peeping at the rosy children her neighbours, it was a new and precious sensation to think that they might soon speak together and know each other; when she would not fear, as of old, to show herself before them, lest they should be grieved to see her in her black dress sitting there alone!", "이웃의 발그레한 아이들을 살며시 엿보면서, 머지않아 그 아이들과 서로 말을 나누고 알게 될지도 모른다고 생각하는 것은 새롭고 귀한 감각이었다. 그때가 되면 그 아이들이 검은 옷을 입고 홀로 앉아 있는 자기를 보고 슬퍼할까 봐 그들 앞에 나서기를 두려워하던 예전과는 달리, 더는 두려워하지 않으리라!"]], [["In her thoughts of her new mother, and in the love and trust overflowing her pure heart towards her, Florence loved her own dead mother more and more.", "새 어머니를 생각하면서, 그리고 그분을 향해 순수한 마음에서 흘러넘치는 사랑과 신뢰 속에서, 플로렌스는 세상을 떠난 자기 어머니를 점점 더 사랑하게 되었다."], ["She had no fear of setting up a rival in her breast.", "그녀는 제 가슴속에 경쟁자를 세우는 것이 아닐까 하는 두려움이 없었다."], ["The new flower sprang from the deep-planted and long-cherished root, she knew.", "새 꽃은 깊이 심기고 오래도록 가꾸어 온 그 뿌리에서 돋아난 것임을, 그녀는 알고 있었다."], ["Every gentle word that had fallen from the lips of the beautiful lady, sounded to Florence like an echo of the voice long hushed and silent.", "그 아름다운 부인의 입술에서 떨어진 다정한 말 한 마디 한 마디가 플로렌스에게는 오래전에 잠잠해지고 침묵한 그 목소리의 메아리처럼 들렸다."], ["How could she love that memory less for living tenderness, when it was her memory of all parental tenderness and love!", "그것이 모든 부모의 다정함과 사랑에 대한 그녀의 기억인데, 어찌 살아 있는 다정함 때문에 그 기억을 덜 사랑할 수 있겠는가!"]], [["Florence was, one day, sitting reading in her room, and thinking of the lady and her promised visit soon—for her book turned on a kindred subject—when, raising her eyes, she saw her standing in the doorway.", "어느 날 플로렌스는 자기 방에서 책을 읽으며 앉아 그 부인과 머지않다고 약속된 방문을 생각하고 있었다—그녀가 읽던 책이 마침 그와 비슷한 주제를 다루고 있었기 때문이다—그런데 눈을 들어 보니, 그 부인이 문간에 서 있는 것이었다."]], [["“Mama!”", "\"어머니!\""], ["cried Florence, joyfully meeting her.", "플로렌스가 기쁘게 맞으며 외쳤다."], ["“Come again!”", "\"또 오셨네요!\""]], [["“Not Mama yet,”", "\"아직은 어머니가 아니란다.\""], ["returned the lady, with a serious smile, as she encircled Florence’s neck with her arm.", "부인이 진지한 미소를 띠며, 한 팔로 플로렌스의 목을 감싸 안으며 대답했다."]], [["“But very soon to be,”", "\"하지만 곧 되실 거잖아요.\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."]], [["“Very soon now, Florence: very soon.”", "\"이제 곧이지, 플로렌스. 아주 곧이야.\""]], [["Edith bent her head a little, so as to press the blooming cheek of Florence against her own, and for some few moments remained thus silent.", "이디스는 플로렌스의 발그레한 뺨을 제 뺨에 갖다 댈 수 있게끔 고개를 약간 숙였고, 그러고는 몇 분 동안 그렇게 말없이 있었다."], ["There was something so very tender in her manner, that Florence was even more sensible of it than on the first occasion of their meeting.", "그 태도에 어찌나 다정한 데가 있었던지, 플로렌스는 처음 만났을 때보다도 더 깊이 그것을 느꼈다."]], [["She led Florence to a chair beside her, and sat down: Florence looking in her face, quite wondering at its beauty, and willingly leaving her hand in hers.", "부인은 플로렌스를 제 곁의 의자로 이끌어 앉혔다. 플로렌스는 그 얼굴을 들여다보며 그 아름다움에 못내 경탄하면서, 기꺼이 제 손을 부인의 손안에 맡겨 두었다."]], [["“Have you been alone, Florence, since I was here last?”", "\"플로렌스, 내가 지난번에 다녀간 뒤로 줄곧 혼자 있었니?\""]], [["“Oh yes!”", "\"아, 네!\""], ["smiled Florence, hastily.", "플로렌스가 얼른 미소 지었다."]], [["She hesitated and cast down her eyes; for her new Mama was very earnest in her look, and the look was intently and thoughtfully fixed upon her face.", "플로렌스는 망설이며 눈을 내리깔았다. 새 어머니의 시선이 무척이나 진지했고, 그 시선이 골똘하고 사려 깊게 자기 얼굴에 박혀 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“I—I—am used to be alone,”", "\"저는—저는—혼자 있는 데 익숙해요.\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“I don’t mind it at all.", "\"전혀 개의치 않아요."], ["Di and I pass whole days together, sometimes.”", "디하고 저는 가끔 온종일 함께 지내기도 해요.\""], ["Florence might have said, whole weeks and months.", "플로렌스는 온 몇 주, 온 몇 달이라고 말할 수도 있었으리라."]], [["“Is Di your maid, love?”", "\"디라는 게 네 하녀니, 얘야?\""]], [["“My dog, Mama,”", "\"제 개예요, 어머니.\""], ["said Florence, laughing.", "플로렌스가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Susan is my maid.”", "\"수전이 제 하녀예요.\""]], [["“And these are your rooms,”", "\"그리고 여기가 네 방이로구나.\""], ["said Edith, looking round.", "이디스가 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“I was not shown these rooms the other day.", "\"지난번에는 이 방들을 보여 주지 않더구나."], ["We must have them improved, Florence.", "플로렌스, 이 방들을 손봐야겠다."], ["They shall be made the prettiest in the house.”", "집에서 가장 예쁜 방으로 만들어 주마.\""]], [["“If I might change them, Mama,”", "\"이 방들을 바꿀 수 있다면, 어머니,\""], ["returned Florence; “there is one upstairs I should like much better.”", "플로렌스가 대답했다, \"위층에 제가 훨씬 더 좋아할 방이 하나 있어요.\""]], [["“Is this not high enough, dear girl?”", "\"여기는 충분히 높지 않니, 얘야?\""], ["asked Edith, smiling.", "이디스가 미소 지으며 물었다."]], [["“The other was my brother’s room,”", "\"그 방은 제 남동생 방이었어요.\""], ["said Florence, “and I am very fond of it.", "플로렌스가 말했다. \"저는 그 방이 무척 좋아요."], ["I would have spoken to Papa about it when I came home, and found the workmen here, and everything changing; but—”", "집에 돌아와서 일꾼들이 와 있고 모든 게 바뀌고 있는 걸 보았을 때 아빠께 그 얘기를 드리려 했지만—\""]], [["Florence dropped her eyes, lest the same look should make her falter again.", "플로렌스는 그 똑같은 시선이 또다시 자기를 머뭇거리게 할까 봐 눈을 내리깔았다."]], [["“but I was afraid it might distress him; and as you said you would be here again soon, Mama, and are the mistress of everything, I determined to take courage and ask you.”", "\"하지만 아빠가 마음 상하실까 봐 두려웠어요. 그리고 어머니가 곧 다시 오겠다고 하셨고, 또 모든 것의 안주인이시니까, 제가 용기를 내어 어머니께 여쭤 보기로 마음먹었어요.\""]], [["Edith sat looking at her, with her brilliant eyes intent upon her face, until Florence raising her own, she, in her turn, withdrew her gaze, and turned it on the ground.", "이디스는 그 빛나는 두 눈을 플로렌스의 얼굴에 골똘히 박은 채 그녀를 바라보고 앉아 있다가, 플로렌스가 제 눈을 들자 이번에는 자기가 시선을 거두어 바닥으로 떨어뜨렸다."], ["It was then that Florence thought how different this lady’s beauty was, from what she had supposed.", "그제야 플로렌스는 이 부인의 아름다움이 자기가 짐작했던 것과 얼마나 다른지를 생각했다."], ["She had thought it of a proud and lofty kind; yet her manner was so subdued and gentle, that if she had been of Florence’s own age and character, it scarcely could have invited confidence more.", "그녀는 그것을 도도하고 고고한 종류의 것이라 여겼었다. 그런데 그 태도가 어찌나 다소곳하고 부드러운지, 만일 이 부인이 플로렌스와 동갑이고 같은 성격이었다 해도 이보다 더 마음을 터놓고 싶게 만들지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["Except when a constrained and singular reserve crept over her; and then she seemed (but Florence hardly understood this, though she could not choose but notice it, and think about it) as if she were humbled before Florence, and ill at ease.", "다만 어떤 어색하고 묘한 거리낌이 그녀에게 슬며시 깃들 때만은 예외였다. 그럴 때면 그녀는 (플로렌스로서는 이를 거의 이해하지 못했지만, 알아채고 그에 대해 생각하지 않을 수가 없었다) 마치 플로렌스 앞에서 주눅이 들어 불편해하는 것처럼 보였다."], ["When she had said that she was not her Mama yet, and when Florence had called her the mistress of everything there, this change in her was quick and startling; and now, while the eyes of Florence rested on her face, she sat as though she would have shrunk and hidden from her, rather than as one about to love and cherish her, in right of such a near connexion.", "자기는 아직 어머니가 아니라고 말했을 때, 그리고 플로렌스가 그녀를 거기 모든 것의 안주인이라 불렀을 때, 그녀의 이런 변화는 빠르고 깜짝 놀랄 만한 것이었다. 그리고 지금도, 플로렌스의 눈이 자기 얼굴에 머물러 있는 동안, 그녀는 그토록 가까운 인연을 명분으로 플로렌스를 사랑하고 아끼려는 사람이라기보다는, 오히려 움츠러들어 그녀에게서 숨고 싶어 하는 사람처럼 앉아 있었다."]], [["She gave Florence her ready promise, about her new room, and said she would give directions about it herself.", "그녀는 새 방에 대해 플로렌스에게 흔쾌히 약속했고, 그 일은 자기가 직접 지시해 주겠다고 말했다."], ["She then asked some questions concerning poor Paul; and when they had sat in conversation for some time, told Florence she had come to take her to her own home.", "그러고는 가엾은 폴에 관해 몇 가지를 물었다. 한동안 대화를 나누며 앉아 있다가, 그녀는 플로렌스를 자기 집으로 데려가려고 왔다고 말했다."]], [["“We have come to London now, my mother and I,”", "\"이제 어머니와 나는 런던으로 왔단다.\""], ["said Edith, “and you shall stay with us until I am married.", "이디스가 말했다. \"그러니 내가 혼인할 때까지 우리와 함께 지내자."], ["I wish that we should know and trust each other, Florence.”", "플로렌스, 우리가 서로를 알고 믿게 되었으면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["“You are very kind to me,”", "\"제게 정말 잘해 주시네요.\""], ["said Florence, “dear Mama.", "플로렌스가 말했다. \"친애하는 어머니."], ["How much I thank you!”", "얼마나 감사한지요!\""]], [["“Let me say now, for it may be the best opportunity,”", "\"지금 말해 두마, 이게 가장 좋은 기회일지 모르니.\""], ["continued Edith, looking round to see that they were quite alone, and speaking in a lower voice, “that when I am married, and have gone away for some weeks, I shall be easier at heart if you will come home here.", "이디스가 둘만 있는지 확인하려고 둘러보고는 더 낮은 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"내가 혼인하고 몇 주 동안 떠나 있게 되면, 네가 이 집으로 돌아와 있어야 마음이 더 놓일 것 같구나."], ["No matter who invites you to stay elsewhere. Come home here.", "누가 다른 데 묵으라고 청하든 상관없이, 이 집으로 돌아오너라."], ["It is better to be alone than—what I would say is,”", "혼자 있는 편이 차라리—내 말은,\""], ["she added, checking herself, “that I know well you are best at home, dear Florence.”", "그녀가 스스로 말을 멈추고 덧붙였다, \"네가 집에 있는 게 가장 좋다는 걸 내가 잘 안다는 거야, 친애하는 플로렌스.\""]], [["“I will come home on the very day, Mama”", "\"바로 그날 집으로 돌아올게요, 어머니.\""]], [["“Do so.", "\"그래라."], ["I rely on that promise.", "그 약속을 믿으마."], ["Now, prepare to come with me, dear girl.", "자, 이제 나와 함께 갈 채비를 하렴, 얘야."], ["You will find me downstairs when you are ready.”", "준비가 되면 아래층에서 나를 찾으면 된다.\""]], [["Slowly and thoughtfully did Edith wander alone through the mansion of which she was so soon to be the lady: and little heed took she of all the elegance and splendour it began to display.", "이디스는 머지않아 자기가 안주인이 될 그 저택을 홀로 천천히, 생각에 잠겨 거닐었다. 그러면서 그 집이 드러내기 시작한 그 모든 우아함과 화려함에는 거의 눈길도 주지 않았다."], ["The same indomitable haughtiness of soul, the same proud scorn expressed in eye and lip, the same fierce beauty, only tamed by a sense of its own little worth, and of the little worth of everything around it, went through the grand saloons and halls, that had got loose among the shady trees, and raged and rent themselves.", "그 길들일 수 없는 영혼의 오만함, 눈과 입술에 어린 그 도도한 경멸, 그 사나운 아름다움이—다만 자기 자신의 보잘것없음과 주위 모든 것의 보잘것없음을 깨달은 마음에 의해서만 누그러진 채—그 웅장한 객실들과 홀들을 누비고 다녔으니, 마치 그늘진 나무들 사이로 풀려나 미쳐 날뛰며 제 몸을 갈가리 찢던 그 무엇과도 같았다."], ["The mimic roses on the walls and floors were set round with sharp thorns, that tore her breast; in every scrap of gold so dazzling to the eye, she saw some hateful atom of her purchase-money; the broad high mirrors showed her, at full length, a woman with a noble quality yet dwelling in her nature, who was too false to her better self, and too debased and lost, to save herself.", "벽과 바닥의 모조 장미들에는 그녀의 가슴을 찢는 날카로운 가시들이 둘러져 있었고, 눈을 어지럽히는 황금 조각 하나하나에서 그녀는 자기 몸값의 가증스러운 부스러기를 보았다. 넓고 높은 거울들은 본성 안에 아직 고귀한 자질이 머물러 있으나 더 나은 자아에 너무도 거짓되고, 또 너무도 타락하고 길을 잃어 스스로를 구할 수조차 없는 여인의 전신을 그녀에게 비춰 주었다."], ["She believed that all this was so plain, more or less, to all eyes, that she had no resource or power of self-assertion but in pride: and with this pride, which tortured her own heart night and day, she fought her fate out, braved it, and defied it.", "그녀는 이 모든 것이, 정도의 차이는 있을지언정, 모든 이의 눈에 그토록 빤히 드러나 있어서, 자기에게는 오만 말고는 어떤 의지처도, 자기를 내세울 힘도 없다고 믿었다. 그리고 밤낮으로 제 마음을 괴롭히는 그 오만으로, 그녀는 제 운명과 끝까지 싸우고, 맞서고, 거역했다."]], [["Was this the woman whom Florence—an innocent girl, strong only in her earnestness and simple truth—could so impress and quell, that by her side she was another creature, with her tempest of passion hushed, and her very pride itself subdued?", "이 여인이, 순진한 소녀에 지나지 않으며 오직 그 진실함과 소박한 진심에서만 굳센 플로렌스가, 그토록 깊이 감화시키고 압도해서, 그녀 곁에서는 격정의 폭풍이 잠잠해지고 그 오만마저도 길들여진 또 다른 존재가 되게 한 바로 그 여인이란 말인가?"], ["Was this the woman who now sat beside her in a carriage, with her arms entwined, and who, while she courted and entreated her to love and trust her, drew her fair head to nestle on her breast, and would have laid down life to shield it from wrong or harm?", "지금 마차 안에서 두 팔을 그녀에게 휘감은 채 그녀 곁에 앉아, 자기를 사랑하고 믿어 달라고 간청하고 졸라 대면서, 그 고운 머리를 끌어다 제 가슴에 기대게 하고, 그것을 어떤 해악이나 위해로부터 지키기 위해서라면 목숨이라도 내놓았을 그 여인이 바로 이 여인이란 말인가?"]], [["Oh, Edith!", "오, 이디스!"], ["it were well to die, indeed, at such a time!", "정녕 이런 때에 죽는다면 차라리 나으리라!"], ["Better and happier far, perhaps, to die so, Edith, than to live on to the end!", "어쩌면 끝까지 살아가느니, 이디스, 이렇게 죽는 편이 훨씬 더 낫고 더 행복하리라!"]], [["The Honourable Mrs Skewton, who was thinking of anything rather than of such sentiments—for, like many genteel persons who have existed at various times, she set her face against death altogether, and objected to the mention of any such low and levelling upstart—had borrowed a house in Brook Street, Grosvenor Square, from a stately relative (one of the Feenix brood), who was out of town, and who did not object to lending it, in the handsomest manner, for nuptial purposes, as the loan implied his final release and acquittance from all further loans and gifts to Mrs Skewton and her daughter.", "명예로운 스큐턴 부인은 그런 감상과는 전혀 동떨어진 생각을 하고 있었으니—여러 시대에 존재해 온 많은 점잖은 사람들이 그러하듯, 그녀는 죽음이라는 것에 아예 등을 돌렸고, 그런 저속하고 만물을 평준화하는 벼락출세자를 입에 올리는 것조차 못마땅해했다—그리시너 스큐어 광장의 브룩 가에 있는 집 한 채를, 시내에 없는 어느 위엄 있는 친척(피닉스 가문의 한 핏줄)에게서 빌려 왔다. 그 친척은 더없이 후한 태도로 혼례를 위해 집을 빌려 주는 데 반대하지 않았는데, 이 대여가 곧 스큐턴 부인과 그 딸에게 앞으로 일체의 대여나 증여를 더는 하지 않아도 된다는, 그의 최종적인 해방과 면제를 뜻했기 때문이다."], ["It being necessary for the credit of the family to make a handsome appearance at such a time, Mrs Skewton, with the assistance of an accommodating tradesman resident in the parish of Mary-le-bone, who lent out all sorts of articles to the nobility and gentry, from a service of plate to an army of footmen, clapped into this house a silver-headed butler (who was charged extra on that account, as having the appearance of an ancient family retainer), two very tall young men in livery, and a select staff of kitchen-servants; so that a legend arose, downstairs, that Withers the page, released at once from his numerous household duties, and from the propulsion of the wheeled-chair (inconsistent with the metropolis), had been several times observed to rub his eyes and pinch his limbs, as if he misdoubted his having overslept himself at the Leamington milkman’s, and being still in a celestial dream.", "이런 때에 가문의 체면을 위해 번듯한 모양새를 갖출 필요가 있었으므로, 스큐턴 부인은 메릴번 교구에 거주하면서 은제 식기 한 벌에서 하인 한 부대에 이르기까지 온갖 물품을 귀족과 신사 계급에게 빌려 주는 한 편의적인 상인의 도움을 받아, 이 집에 은빛 머리의 집사 한 명(고풍스러운 가문의 늙은 하인 같은 풍채라 하여 그만큼 추가 요금이 붙었다)과 제복 차림의 키 큰 젊은이 둘, 그리고 엄선된 주방 하인들을 들여놓았다. 그래서 아래층에서는 이런 전설이 생겨났다."], ["A variety of requisites in plate and china being also conveyed to the same establishment from the same convenient source, with several miscellaneous articles, including a neat chariot and a pair of bays, Mrs Skewton cushioned herself on the principal sofa, in the Cleopatra attitude, and held her court in fair state.", "시동 위더스가, 그 수많은 집안일과 (대도시에는 어울리지 않는) 바퀴 의자 미는 일에서 단번에 풀려난 나머지, 자기가 리밍턴 우유 장수 집에서 늦잠을 자다가 아직도 천상의 꿈속에 있는 게 아닌가 의심하기라도 하듯, 여러 번 눈을 비비고 팔다리를 꼬집는 모습이 목격되었다는 것이다. 은제품과 도자기의 갖가지 필수품들도 같은 편리한 출처로부터 같은 살림집으로 운반되어 왔고, 그 밖에 말끔한 사륜마차 한 대와 밤색 말 두 필을 포함한 여러 잡다한 물품들도 함께 들여왔으니, 스큐턴 부인은 주된 소파에 클레오파트라 자세로 몸을 기대고 점잖은 위풍 속에서 자기 궁정을 열었다."]], [["“And how,” said Mrs Skewton, on the entrance of her daughter and her charge, “is my charming Florence?", "\"그래, 우리 매력적인 플로렌스는,\""], ["You must come and kiss me, Florence, if you please, my love.”", "딸과 그녀가 맡은 아이가 들어오자 스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"어떻게 지내니? 이리 와서 내게 입 맞춰 주렴, 플로렌스, 미안하지만, 얘야.\""]], [["Florence was timidly stooping to pick out a place in the white part of Mrs Skewton’s face, when that lady presented her ear, and relieved her of her difficulty.", "플로렌스가 스큐턴 부인 얼굴의 흰 부분에서 입 맞출 자리를 고르려고 머뭇머뭇 몸을 굽히는데, 그 부인이 귀를 내밀어 그녀의 곤혹스러움을 덜어 주었다."]], [["“Edith, my dear,”", "\"이디스, 얘야,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “positively, I—stand a little more in the light, my sweetest Florence, for a moment.”", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"정말이지 나는—잠깐만 빛이 좀 더 드는 쪽에 서 보렴, 우리 사랑스러운 플로렌스, 잠깐이면 된다.\""]], [["Florence blushingly complied.", "플로렌스는 얼굴을 붉히며 그 말에 따랐다."]], [["“You don’t remember, dearest Edith,”", "\"기억나지 않니, 사랑하는 이디스,\""], ["said her mother, “what you were when you were about the same age as our exceedingly precious Florence, or a few years younger?”", "그녀의 어머니가 말했다, \"네가 우리 더없이 소중한 플로렌스만 한 나이였을 때, 아니면 몇 살 더 어렸을 때 어떤 모습이었는지?\""]], [["“I have long forgotten, mother.”", "\"오래전에 잊어버렸어요, 어머니.\""]], [["“For positively, my dear,”", "\"왜냐하면 정말로, 얘야,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “I do think that I see a decided resemblance to what you were then, in our extremely fascinating young friend.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"우리의 더없이 매혹적인 어린 벗에게서, 그때 네 모습과 뚜렷이 닮은 데가 보이는 것 같거든."], ["And it shows,”", "그리고 그건,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, in a lower voice, which conveyed her opinion that Florence was in a very unfinished state, “what cultivation will do.”", "스큐턴 부인이 더 낮은 목소리로 말했는데, 그 목소리는 플로렌스가 아직 한참 다듬어지지 않은 상태라는 자기 견해를 담고 있었다, \"교양이 무엇을 해낼 수 있는지를 보여 주는 거란다.\""]], [["“It does, indeed,”", "\"정말 그렇네요.\""], ["was Edith’s stern reply.", "이디스의 매정한 대답이었다."]], [["Her mother eyed her sharply for a moment, and feeling herself on unsafe ground, said, as a diversion:", "그녀의 어머니는 잠시 딸을 날카롭게 쳐다보다가, 자기가 위태로운 땅에 서 있음을 느끼고는 화제를 돌리려고 말했다."]], [["“My charming Florence, you must come and kiss me once more, if you please, my love.”", "\"우리 매력적인 플로렌스, 한 번 더 와서 내게 입 맞춰 주렴, 미안하지만, 얘야.\""]], [["Florence complied, of course, and again imprinted her lips on Mrs Skewton’s ear.", "플로렌스는 물론 그 말을 따랐고, 또다시 스큐턴 부인의 귀에 입술을 갖다 댔다."]], [["“And you have heard, no doubt, my darling pet,”", "\"그리고 너도 분명 들었겠지, 우리 귀여운 아가,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, detaining her hand, “that your Papa, whom we all perfectly adore and dote upon, is to be married to my dearest Edith this day week.”", "스큐턴 부인이 그녀의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다, \"우리 모두가 더없이 흠모하고 애지중지하는 네 아빠가, 이날로 일주일 뒤에 내 더없이 사랑하는 이디스와 혼인하시리라는 걸 말이다.\""]], [["“I knew it would be very soon,”", "\"아주 곧이라는 건 알았어요.\""], ["returned Florence, “but not exactly when.”", "플로렌스가 대답했다, \"하지만 정확히 언제인지는 몰랐어요.\""]], [["“My darling Edith,”", "\"내 사랑하는 이디스,\""], ["urged her mother, gaily, “is it possible you have not told Florence?”", "그녀의 어머니가 명랑하게 다그쳤다, \"설마 네가 플로렌스에게 말해 주지 않았다는 거니?\""]], [["“Why should I tell Florence?”", "\"제가 왜 플로렌스에게 말해야 하죠?\""], ["she returned, so suddenly and harshly, that Florence could scarcely believe it was the same voice.", "그녀가 어찌나 갑작스럽고 매몰차게 받아쳤던지, 플로렌스는 그것이 같은 목소리라고는 거의 믿을 수 없었다."]], [["Mrs Skewton then told Florence, as another and safer diversion, that her father was coming to dinner, and that he would no doubt be charmingly surprised to see her; as he had spoken last night of dressing in the City, and had known nothing of Edith’s design, the execution of which, according to Mrs Skewton’s expectation, would throw him into a perfect ecstasy.", "그러자 스큐턴 부인은 또 다른, 더 안전한 화제로 돌리려고, 플로렌스에게 그녀의 아버지가 저녁 식사에 올 것이며, 그녀를 보면 분명 기분 좋게 깜짝 놀랄 거라고 말했다. 그가 어젯밤 시티에서 옷을 차려입겠다고 말한 데다 이디스의 계획을 전혀 모르고 있었으니, 스큐턴 부인의 예상으로는 그 계획의 실행이 그를 완전한 황홀경에 빠뜨리리라는 것이었다."], ["Florence was troubled to hear this; and her distress became so keen, as the dinner-hour approached, that if she had known how to frame an entreaty to be suffered to return home, without involving her father in her explanation, she would have hurried back on foot, bareheaded, breathless, and alone, rather than incur the risk of meeting his displeasure.", "플로렌스는 이 말을 듣고 괴로웠다. 그리고 저녁 식사 시간이 다가올수록 그 괴로움이 어찌나 사무쳐 갔던지, 만일 자기 설명에 아버지를 끌어들이지 않으면서 집으로 돌아가게 해 달라고 간청하는 방법을 알았더라면, 그의 노여움을 살 위험을 무릅쓰느니 차라리 모자도 쓰지 않고 숨 가쁘게 홀로 걸어서라도 급히 돌아갔으리라."]], [["As the time drew nearer, she could hardly breathe.", "시간이 가까워질수록 그녀는 거의 숨을 쉴 수 없었다."], ["She dared not approach a window, lest he should see her from the street.", "그녀는 창가에 다가갈 엄두를 내지 못했으니, 그가 길에서 자기를 볼까 두려웠던 것이다."], ["She dared not go upstairs to hide her emotion, lest, in passing out at the door, she should meet him unexpectedly; besides which dread, she felt as though she never could come back again if she were summoned to his presence.", "위층으로 올라가 자기 감정을 숨길 엄두도 내지 못했으니, 문으로 나가다가 뜻하지 않게 그와 마주칠까 두려웠던 것이다. 게다가 그 두려움에 더하여, 만일 그의 앞으로 불려 가게 된다면 다시는 돌아오지 못할 것만 같은 느낌이 들었다."], ["In this conflict of fears; she was sitting by Cleopatra’s couch, endeavouring to understand and to reply to the bald discourse of that lady, when she heard his foot upon the stair.", "이런 두려움의 갈등 속에서, 그녀는 클레오파트라의 긴 의자 곁에 앉아 그 부인의 시시한 이야기를 알아듣고 대답해 보려 애쓰고 있다가, 계단에 울리는 그의 발소리를 들었다."]], [["“I hear him now!”", "\"이제 들려요!\""], ["cried Florence, starting.", "플로렌스가 화들짝 놀라며 외쳤다."], ["“He is coming!”", "\"오고 계세요!\""]], [["Cleopatra, who in her juvenility was always playfully disposed, and who in her self-engrossment did not trouble herself about the nature of this agitation, pushed Florence behind her couch, and dropped a shawl over her, preparatory to giving Mr Dombey a rapture of surprise.", "클레오파트라는 그 젊은 척하는 기질로 늘 장난기 어린 태도였고, 또 제 일에만 골몰해 있어서 이 동요가 어떤 성질의 것인지 굳이 신경 쓰지 않은 채, 돔비 씨에게 황홀한 놀라움을 안겨 줄 채비로 플로렌스를 자기 긴 의자 뒤로 밀어 넣고 그 위에 숄을 덮어 버렸다."], ["It was so quickly done, that in a moment Florence heard his awful step in the room.", "어찌나 잽싸게 해치웠던지, 순식간에 플로렌스는 방 안에 울리는 그의 무시무시한 발소리를 들었다."]], [["He saluted his intended mother-in-law, and his intended bride.", "그는 장차 장모가 될 사람과 장차 신부가 될 사람에게 인사를 건넸다."], ["The strange sound of his voice thrilled through the whole frame of his child.", "그 목소리의 낯선 울림이 그의 아이의 온몸을 전율케 했다."]], [["“My dear Dombey,”", "\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\""], ["said Cleopatra, “come here and tell me how your pretty Florence is.”", "클레오파트라가 말했다, \"이리 와서 당신의 예쁜 플로렌스가 어떻게 지내는지 말해 줘요.\""]], [["“Florence is very well,”", "\"플로렌스는 아주 잘 지냅니다.\""], ["said Mr Dombey, advancing towards the couch.", "돔비 씨가 긴 의자 쪽으로 다가서며 말했다."]], [["“At home?”", "\"집에서요?\""]], [["“At home,”", "\"집에서요.\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“My dear Dombey,”", "\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\""], ["returned Cleopatra, with bewitching vivacity; “now are you sure you are not deceiving me?", "클레오파트라가 사람을 흘리는 활기를 띠며 받았다, \"자, 정말 나를 속이는 게 아니라고 장담할 수 있어요?"], ["I don’t know what my dearest Edith will say to me when I make such a declaration, but upon my honour I am afraid you are the falsest of men, my dear Dombey.”", "내가 이런 단언을 하면 우리 더없이 사랑하는 이디스가 내게 뭐라고 할지 모르겠지만, 명예를 걸고 말하건대 당신이 남자들 가운데 가장 거짓된 사람일까 봐 두렵네요, 내 사랑하는 돔비.\""]], [["Though he had been; and had been detected on the spot, in the most enormous falsehood that was ever said or done; he could hardly have been more disconcerted than he was, when Mrs Skewton plucked the shawl away, and Florence, pale and trembling, rose before him like a ghost.", "설령 그가 정말로 그러했고, 일찍이 입에 담거나 저질러진 적 없는 가장 엄청난 거짓말을 그 자리에서 들켰다 한들, 스큐턴 부인이 숄을 홱 걷어 내고 플로렌스가 창백하게 떨면서 유령처럼 그의 앞에 일어섰을 때만큼 당황하지는 못했을 것이다."], ["He had not yet recovered his presence of mind, when Florence had run up to him, clasped her hands round his neck, kissed his face, and hurried out of the room.", "그가 미처 침착을 되찾기도 전에, 플로렌스는 그에게 달려와 두 손을 그의 목에 둘러 감고 그의 얼굴에 입 맞추고는, 황급히 방을 나가 버렸다."], ["He looked round as if to refer the matter to somebody else, but Edith had gone after Florence, instantly.", "그는 이 일을 누군가 다른 사람에게 물어보기라도 하려는 듯 둘러보았지만, 이디스는 이미 플로렌스를 따라 나가 버린 뒤였다."]], [["“Now, confess, my dear Dombey,”", "\"자, 인정해요, 내 사랑하는 돔비,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, giving him her hand, “that you never were more surprised and pleased in your life.”", "스큐턴 부인이 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다, \"평생 이보다 더 놀라고 기뻤던 적이 없다고 말이에요.\""]], [["“I never was more surprised,”", "\"이보다 더 놀란 적은 없었습니다.\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Nor pleased, my dearest Dombey?”", "\"더 기뻤던 적도 없죠, 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비?\""], ["returned Mrs Skewton, holding up her fan.", "스큐턴 부인이 부채를 치켜들며 받았다."]], [["“I—yes, I am exceedingly glad to meet Florence here,”", "\"나는—그렇습니다, 플로렌스를 여기서 만나서 대단히 기쁩니다.\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["He appeared to consider gravely about it for a moment, and then said, more decidedly, “Yes, I really am very glad indeed to meet Florence here.”", "그는 잠시 그것에 대해 진지하게 따져 보는 듯하더니, 한결 단호하게 말했다, \"그래요, 플로렌스를 여기서 만나니 정말이지 무척 기쁩니다.\""]], [["“You wonder how she comes here?”", "\"저 애가 여기 어떻게 왔는지 궁금하죠?\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “don’t you?”", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"안 그래요?\""]], [["“Edith, perhaps—”", "\"아마도 이디스가—\""], ["suggested Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 넌지시 말했다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["wicked guesser!”", "짓궂은 추측쟁이 같으니!\""], ["replied Cleopatra, shaking her head.", "클레오파트라가 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["cunning, cunning man!", "약은, 약은 양반 같으니라고! 이런 건 말하면 안 되는 거예요."], ["One shouldn’t tell these things; your sex, my dear Dombey, are so vain, and so apt to abuse our weakness; but you know my open soul—very well; immediately.”", "당신네 남자들은, 내 사랑하는 돔비, 어찌나 허영심이 많고 우리 여자들의 약점을 잘도 이용해 먹는지. 하지만 당신은 내 솔직한 영혼을 알잖아요—아주 좋아요, 곧 갑니다.\""]], [["This was addressed to one of the very tall young men who announced dinner.", "이 말은 저녁 식사가 준비되었음을 알리는 그 키 큰 젊은이들 가운데 하나에게 한 것이었다."]], [["“But Edith, my dear Dombey,”", "\"하지만 이디스는, 내 사랑하는 돔비,\""], ["she continued in a whisper, “when she cannot have you near her—and as I tell her, she cannot expect that always—will at least have near her something or somebody belonging to you.", "그녀가 속삭이며 말을 이었다, \"당신을 곁에 둘 수 없을 때면—내가 늘 그 애에게 말하듯, 그걸 늘 기대할 수는 없는 노릇이지요—적어도 당신에게 속한 무언가나 누군가를 곁에 두려 한답니다."], ["Well, how extremely natural that is!", "자, 그게 얼마나 지극히 자연스러운 일인가요!"], ["And in this spirit, nothing would keep her from riding off today to fetch our darling Florence.", "그래서 이런 마음으로, 그 무엇도 그 애가 오늘 우리의 사랑스러운 플로렌스를 데리러 마차를 몰고 나가는 걸 막을 수 없었던 거예요."], ["Well, how excessively charming that is!”", "자, 그게 얼마나 더없이 사랑스러운 일인가요!\""]], [["As she waited for an answer, Mr Dombey answered, “Eminently so.”", "그녀가 대답을 기다리자, 돔비 씨가 대답했다, \"지극히 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Bless you, my dear Dombey, for that proof of heart!”", "\"당신에게 축복을, 내 사랑하는 돔비, 그토록 진심을 보여 주다니!\""], ["cried Cleopatra, squeezing his hand.", "클레오파트라가 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며 외쳤다."], ["“But I am growing too serious!", "\"하지만 내가 너무 심각해지고 있군요!"], ["Take me downstairs, like an angel, and let us see what these people intend to give us for dinner.", "천사처럼 나를 아래층으로 데려가서, 이 사람들이 저녁으로 우리에게 뭘 내올 작정인지 봅시다."], ["Bless you, dear Dombey!”", "당신에게 축복을, 사랑하는 돔비!\""]], [["Cleopatra skipping off her couch with tolerable briskness, after the last benediction, Mr Dombey took her arm in his and led her ceremoniously downstairs; one of the very tall young men on hire, whose organ of veneration was imperfectly developed, thrusting his tongue into his cheek, for the entertainment of the other very tall young man on hire, as the couple turned into the dining-room.", "마지막 축복을 마친 뒤, 클레오파트라가 제법 날렵하게 긴 의자에서 폴짝 내려서자, 돔비 씨는 그녀의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼고 격식을 차려 아래층으로 안내했다. 그 두 사람이 식당으로 접어드는데, 고용된 키 큰 젊은이들 가운데 하나, 공경심이라는 기관이 덜 발달한 그자가, 또 다른 고용된 키 큰 젊은이를 즐겁게 해 주려고 혀로 제 볼을 불룩하게 밀어 보였다."]], [["Florence and Edith were already there, and sitting side by side.", "플로렌스와 이디스는 이미 거기 와서 나란히 앉아 있었다."], ["Florence would have risen when her father entered, to resign her chair to him; but Edith openly put her hand upon her arm, and Mr Dombey took an opposite place at the round table.", "플로렌스는 아버지가 들어오자 일어나 자기 의자를 그에게 양보하려 했지만, 이디스가 대놓고 그녀의 팔에 손을 얹었고, 돔비 씨는 둥근 식탁의 맞은편 자리에 앉았다."]], [["The conversation was almost entirely sustained by Mrs Skewton.", "대화는 거의 전적으로 스큐턴 부인이 이끌었다."], ["Florence hardly dared to raise her eyes, lest they should reveal the traces of tears; far less dared to speak; and Edith never uttered one word, unless in answer to a question.", "플로렌스는 눈물 자국이 드러날까 봐 감히 눈을 들지도 못했고, 말을 할 엄두는 더더욱 내지 못했다. 그리고 이디스는 질문에 대답할 때를 빼고는 한 마디도 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["Verily, Cleopatra worked hard, for the establishment that was so nearly clutched; and verily it should have been a rich one to reward her!", "참으로 클레오파트라는, 거의 손아귀에 넣은 그 가문을 위하여 부지런히 애를 썼다. 그리고 참으로 그 가문은, 그녀에게 보답하기에 충분히 풍족한 것이어야 마땅했으리라!"]], [["“And so your preparations are nearly finished at last, my dear Dombey?”", "\"그래서 당신의 준비도 이제 거의 다 끝났나요, 내 사랑하는 돔비?\""], ["said Cleopatra, when the dessert was put upon the table, and the silver-headed butler had withdrawn.", "후식이 식탁에 올라오고 은빛 머리의 집사가 물러간 뒤, 클레오파트라가 말했다."], ["“Even the lawyers” preparations!”", "\"변호사들의 준비까지도요!\""]], [["“Yes, madam,”", "\"그렇습니다, 부인.\" 돔비 씨가 대답했다."], ["replied Mr Dombey; “the deed of settlement, the professional gentlemen inform me, is now ready, and as I was mentioning to you, Edith has only to do us the favour to suggest her own time for its execution.”", "\"재산 양도 증서는, 그 전문가 양반들이 알려 주기로, 이제 준비가 되었답니다. 그리고 말씀드렸다시피, 이디스가 그것을 작성할 날짜를 직접 정해 주는 호의만 베풀어 주면 됩니다.\""]], [["Edith sat like a handsome statue; as cold, as silent, and as still.", "이디스는 아름다운 조각상처럼 앉아 있었다. 그만큼 차갑게, 그만큼 말없이, 그만큼 미동도 없이."]], [["“My dearest love,”", "\"내 더없이 사랑하는 얘야,\""], ["said Cleopatra, “do you hear what Mr Dombey says?", "클레오파트라가 말했다, \"돔비 씨가 하는 말 들리니?"], ["Ah, my dear Dombey!”", "아, 내 사랑하는 돔비!\""], ["aside to that gentleman, “how her absence, as the time approaches, reminds me of the days, when that most agreeable of creatures, her Papa, was in your situation!”", "그 신사에게 곁말로, \"시간이 다가올수록 저 애가 저렇게 멍하니 있는 모습이, 저 애의 더없이 유쾌한 아빠가 당신과 같은 처지에 있던 그 시절을 어찌나 떠오르게 하는지요!\""]], [["“I have nothing to suggest.", "\"저는 정할 게 없어요."], ["It shall be when you please,”", "당신이 좋으실 때로 하세요.\""], ["said Edith, scarcely looking over the table at Mr Dombey.", "이디스가 식탁 너머 돔비 씨를 거의 쳐다보지도 않고 말했다."]], [["“To-morrow?”", "\"내일은요?\""], ["suggested Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 넌지시 말했다."]], [["“If you please.”", "\"좋으실 대로요.\""]], [["“Or would next day,”", "\"아니면 그다음 날이,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “suit your engagements better?”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"당신의 일정에 더 맞을까요?\""]], [["“I have no engagements.", "\"저는 일정이 없어요."], ["I am always at your disposal.", "저는 늘 당신 뜻대로 따를 수 있습니다."], ["Let it be when you like.”", "당신이 원하실 때로 하세요.\""]], [["“No engagements, my dear Edith!”", "\"일정이 없다니, 내 사랑하는 이디스!\""], ["remonstrated her mother, “when you are in a most terrible state of flurry all day long, and have a thousand and one appointments with all sorts of trades-people!”", "그녀의 어머니가 항변했다, \"온종일 더없이 정신없이 분주한 상태에다, 온갖 상인들과 천 가지 만 가지 약속이 잡혀 있으면서!\""]], [["“They are of your making,”", "\"그건 어머니가 만드신 거예요.\""], ["returned Edith, turning on her with a slight contraction of her brow.", "이디스가 살짝 미간을 찌푸리며 어머니에게 돌아서서 받았다."], ["“You and Mr Dombey can arrange between you.”", "\"어머니랑 돔비 씨랑 둘이서 정하시면 되잖아요.\""]], [["“Very true indeed, my love, and most considerate of you!”", "\"정말 그렇구나, 얘야, 참으로 사려 깊기도 하지!\""], ["said Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 말했다."], ["“My darling Florence, you must really come and kiss me once more, if you please, my dear!”", "\"우리 사랑하는 플로렌스, 한 번만 더 와서 내게 입 맞춰 주렴, 미안하지만, 얘야!\""]], [["Singular coincidence, that these gushes of interest in Florence hurried Cleopatra away from almost every dialogue in which Edith had a share, however trifling!", "이상한 우연이지, 플로렌스에 대한 이런 관심의 분출이 클레오파트라를 이디스가 끼어 있는 거의 모든 대화에서, 그것이 아무리 사소한 것이라도, 황급히 빠져나오게 하다니!"], ["Florence had certainly never undergone so much embracing, and perhaps had never been, unconsciously, so useful in her life.", "플로렌스는 분명 평생 이토록 많은 포옹을 받아 본 적이 없었고, 또 아마 자기도 모르는 사이에 이토록 쓸모 있어진 적도 없었으리라."]], [["Mr Dombey was far from quarrelling, in his own breast, with the manner of his beautiful betrothed.", "돔비 씨는 자기 아름다운 약혼녀의 태도에 대해 마음속으로 조금도 트집을 잡지 않았다."], ["He had that good reason for sympathy with haughtiness and coldness, which is found in a fellow-feeling.", "그는 오만함과 차가움에 공감할 만한 그 충분한 이유를 지니고 있었으니, 그것은 동류의식에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["It flattered him to think how these deferred to him, in Edith’s case, and seemed to have no will apart from his.", "이디스의 경우, 이런 것들이 자기에게 고개를 숙이고 자기와 별개의 의지란 없는 듯이 보인다고 생각하니 우쭐했다."], ["It flattered him to picture to himself, this proud and stately woman doing the honours of his house, and chilling his guests after his own manner.", "이 도도하고 위엄 있는 여인이 자기 집안의 접대를 맡아, 자기 방식 그대로 자기 손님들을 싸늘하게 대하는 모습을 상상하니 우쭐했다."], ["The dignity of Dombey and Son would be heightened and maintained, indeed, in such hands.", "돔비와 아들의 위엄은, 정녕 그런 손에 의해 더욱 드높여지고 지켜질 것이었다."]], [["So thought Mr Dombey, when he was left alone at the dining-table, and mused upon his past and future fortunes: finding no uncongeniality in an air of scant and gloomy state that pervaded the room, in colour a dark brown, with black hatchments of pictures blotching the walls, and twenty-four black chairs, with almost as many nails in them as so many coffins, waiting like mutes, upon the threshold of the Turkey carpet; and two exhausted negroes holding up two withered branches of candelabra on the sideboard, and a musty smell prevailing as if the ashes of ten thousand dinners were entombed in the sarcophagus below it. The owner of the house lived much abroad; the air of England seldom agreed long with a member of the Feenix family; and the room had gradually put itself into deeper and still deeper mourning for him, until it was become so funereal as to want nothing but a body in it to be quite complete.", "돔비 씨는 식탁에 혼자 남아, 자기의 지난날과 앞날의 운수를 곰곰이 헤아리며 그렇게 생각했다. 그러면서 그는 그 방을 감도는, 인색하고 음울한 위세의 분위기에서 자기와 어긋나는 데를 조금도 느끼지 못했다. 짙은 갈색을 띤 그 방은, 벽에 검은 문장(紋章) 그림들이 얼룩처럼 박혀 있었고, 검은 의자 스물네 개가, 관(棺)에 박힌 만큼이나 많은 못이 박힌 채, 마치 장례 조객처럼 터키 양탄자의 문턱에서 대기하고 있었다. 그리고 찬장 위에는 지친 흑인상(像) 둘이 시들어 빠진 촛대 가지 둘을 떠받치고 있었고, 마치 만 끼니 식사의 재가 그 아래 석관에 매장되기라도 한 듯 곰팡내가 진동했다. 그 집 주인은 외국에서 지내는 일이 많았다. 잉글랜드의 공기는 피닉스 가문 사람에게 오래도록 잘 맞는 일이 드물었던 것이다. 그래서 그 방은 그를 위해 차츰 더 깊고 더 깊은 상복을 차려입어, 마침내 어찌나 장례식 같아졌던지 완전무결해지는 데에 시신 하나만이 모자랄 뿐이었다."]], [["No bad representation of the body, for the nonce, in his unbending form, if not in his attitude, Mr Dombey looked down into the cold depths of the dead sea of mahogany on which the fruit dishes and decanters lay at anchor: as if the subjects of his thoughts were rising towards the surface one by one, and plunging down again.", "당장은, 자세는 아닐지언정 그 뻣뻣한 형체로 보아 시신의 표상으로 그리 나쁘지 않은 돔비 씨가, 과일 접시들과 유리병들이 닻을 내리고 있는 그 죽은 마호가니 바다의 차가운 심연을 내려다보았다. 마치 그의 상념의 대상들이 하나씩 하나씩 수면 위로 떠올랐다가 다시 가라앉기라도 하는 듯이."], ["Edith was there in all her majesty of brow and figure; and close to her came Florence, with her timid head turned to him, as it had been, for an instant, when she left the room; and Edith’s eyes upon her, and Edith’s hand put out protectingly.", "이디스가 그 이마와 자태의 온갖 위엄을 갖춘 채 거기 있었다. 그리고 그녀 가까이로, 방을 나설 때 한순간 그러했던 것처럼 겁먹은 고개를 그에게 돌린 플로렌스가 다가왔다."], ["A little figure in a low arm-chair came springing next into the light, and looked upon him wonderingly, with its bright eyes and its old-young face, gleaming as in the flickering of an evening fire.", "그리고 이디스의 눈이 그녀에게 머물러 있었고, 이디스의 손이 보호하듯 뻗쳐 있었다. 다음으로는 낮은 안락의자에 앉은 자그마한 형체 하나가 빛 속으로 튀어 오르더니, 그 초롱초롱한 눈과 늙은-어린 얼굴로, 마치 저녁 불빛이 일렁이는 가운데서처럼 반짝이며 그를 의아한 듯 바라보았다."], ["Again came Florence close upon it, and absorbed his whole attention.", "다시 플로렌스가 그것에 바짝 다가와 그의 온 관심을 사로잡았다."], ["Whether as a fore-doomed difficulty and disappointment to him; whether as a rival who had crossed him in his way, and might again; whether as his child, of whom, in his successful wooing, he could stoop to think as claiming, at such a time, to be no more estranged; or whether as a hint to him that the mere appearance of caring for his own blood should be maintained in his new relations; he best knew.", "그녀가 그에게 미리 정해진 골칫거리이자 실망거리로서인지, 자기 길을 가로막았고 또다시 가로막을지 모르는 경쟁자로서인지, 혹은 성공적인 구애를 마친 마당에 이런 때에 더는 멀어지지 않기를 요구할 만한 자기 자식으로서 너그러이 생각해 줄 만한 존재로서인지, 아니면 자기의 새로운 관계 속에서 적어도 제 핏줄을 위하는 시늉만이라도 유지해야 한다는 암시로서인지—그것은 그 자신이 가장 잘 알았다."], ["Indifferently well, perhaps, at best; for marriage company and marriage altars, and ambitious scenes—still blotted here and there with Florence—always Florence—turned up so fast, and so confusedly, that he rose, and went upstairs to escape them.", "어쩌면 기껏해야 어중간하게나 알았을 것이다. 혼례 하객들과 혼례 제단들과 야심 찬 장면들이—여전히 여기저기 플로렌스로, 언제나 플로렌스로 얼룩진 채—어찌나 빠르게, 또 어찌나 어지러이 떠오르던지, 그는 그것들에게서 벗어나려고 자리에서 일어나 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["It was quite late at night before candles were brought; for at present they made Mrs Skewton’s head ache, she complained; and in the meantime Florence and Mrs Skewton talked together (Cleopatra being very anxious to keep her close to herself), or Florence touched the piano softly for Mrs Skewton’s delight; to make no mention of a few occasions in the course of the evening, when that affectionate lady was impelled to solicit another kiss, and which always happened after Edith had said anything. They were not many, however, for Edith sat apart by an open window during the whole time (in spite of her mother’s fears that she would take cold), and remained there until Mr Dombey took leave. He was serenely gracious to Florence when he did so; and Florence went to bed in a room within Edith’s, so happy and hopeful, that she thought of her late self as if it were some other poor deserted girl who was to be pitied for her sorrow; and in her pity, sobbed herself to sleep.", "촛불을 들여올 때쯤에는 밤이 꽤 깊어 있었다. 지금은 촛불이 머리를 아프게 한다고 스큐턴 부인이 투덜댔기 때문이다. 그러는 동안 플로렌스와 스큐턴 부인은 함께 이야기를 나누었고(클레오파트라가 그녀를 바짝 자기 곁에 두고 싶어 안달했으므로), 혹은 플로렌스가 스큐턴 부인을 즐겁게 해 주려고 피아노를 가만히 어루만지기도 했다. 그 저녁 내내 그 다정한 부인이 한 번 더 입맞춤을 졸라 대지 않을 수 없었던 몇 번의 경우는 말할 것도 없는데, 그것은 늘 이디스가 무슨 말이든 한 뒤에 일어났다. 그러나 그런 경우가 많지는 않았으니, 이디스가 (감기에 걸릴까 봐 걱정하는 어머니의 우려에도 아랑곳없이) 줄곧 열린 창가에 따로 떨어져 앉아, 돔비 씨가 작별 인사를 할 때까지 거기 머물러 있었기 때문이다. 그는 작별할 때 플로렌스에게 차분하고 정중했다. 그리고 플로렌스는 이디스의 방 안쪽에 딸린 방에서 잠자리에 들었는데, 어찌나 행복하고 희망에 차 있었던지, 자기의 지난 모습을 마치 슬픔 때문에 가엾이 여겨야 할 어떤 다른 가련하고 버림받은 소녀인 양 떠올렸고, 그 가엾음 속에서 흐느끼다 잠들었다."]], [["The week fled fast.", "그 한 주는 빠르게 흘러갔다."], ["There were drives to milliners, dressmakers, jewellers, lawyers, florists, pastry-cooks; and Florence was always of the party.", "모자 가게며 양장점이며 보석상이며 변호사며 꽃집이며 제과점으로 가는 마차 행차가 있었고, 플로렌스는 늘 그 일행에 끼어 있었다."], ["Florence was to go to the wedding.", "플로렌스는 결혼식에 가기로 되어 있었다."], ["Florence was to cast off her mourning, and to wear a brilliant dress on the occasion.", "플로렌스는 상복을 벗고 그날을 위해 화려한 드레스를 입기로 되어 있었다."], ["The milliner’s intentions on the subject of this dress—the milliner was a Frenchwoman, and greatly resembled Mrs Skewton—were so chaste and elegant, that Mrs Skewton bespoke one like it for herself.", "이 드레스에 관한 모자 장수의 구상은—그 모자 장수는 프랑스 여자였고, 스큐턴 부인과 무척 닮았다—어찌나 고상하고 우아했던지, 스큐턴 부인이 그것과 똑같은 것을 자기 것으로 주문했다."], ["The milliner said it would become her to admiration, and that all the world would take her for the young lady’s sister.", "모자 장수는 그것이 부인에게 감탄스러울 만큼 잘 어울릴 것이며, 온 세상이 그녀를 그 아가씨의 언니로 여길 거라고 말했다."]], [["The week fled faster.", "그 한 주는 더 빠르게 흘러갔다."], ["Edith looked at nothing and cared for nothing.", "이디스는 아무것도 쳐다보지 않았고 아무것도 마음에 두지 않았다."], ["Her rich dresses came home, and were tried on, and were loudly commended by Mrs Skewton and the milliners, and were put away without a word from her.", "그녀의 호사스러운 드레스들이 집으로 배달되어 와서, 입어 보았고, 스큐턴 부인과 모자 장수들에게서 요란한 칭찬을 받았고, 그녀에게서는 한 마디 말도 없이 치워졌다."], ["Mrs Skewton made their plans for every day, and executed them.", "스큐턴 부인은 날마다의 계획을 세웠고 그것을 실행에 옮겼다."], ["Sometimes Edith sat in the carriage when they went to make purchases; sometimes, when it was absolutely necessary, she went into the shops.", "물건을 사러 갈 때면 이디스는 더러 마차 안에 앉아 있었고, 더러 꼭 필요할 때면 가게 안으로 들어가기도 했다."], ["But Mrs Skewton conducted the whole business, whatever it happened to be; and Edith looked on as uninterested and with as much apparent indifference as if she had no concern in it.", "그러나 무슨 일이든 그 전체를 처리한 것은 스큐턴 부인이었다. 그리고 이디스는 마치 자기와는 아무 상관도 없다는 듯, 흥미라곤 없이, 겉보기에 그토록 무관심하게 지켜보고만 있었다."], ["Florence might perhaps have thought she was haughty and listless, but that she was never so to her.", "플로렌스는 어쩌면 그녀가 오만하고 무기력하다고 여겼을지도 모르나, 자기에게는 결코 그러는 법이 없었다."], ["So Florence quenched her wonder in her gratitude whenever it broke out, and soon subdued it.", "그래서 플로렌스는 의아함이 고개를 들 때마다 그것을 감사함으로 잠재웠고, 곧 가라앉혔다."]], [["The week fled faster.", "그 한 주는 더 빠르게 흘러갔다."], ["It had nearly winged its flight away.", "그 주는 거의 날아가 버릴 듯했다."], ["The last night of the week, the night before the marriage, was come.", "그 주의 마지막 밤, 곧 혼례 전날 밤이 왔다."], ["In the dark room—for Mrs Skewton’s head was no better yet, though she expected to recover permanently to-morrow—were that lady, Edith, and Mr Dombey.", "어두운 방 안에는—스큐턴 부인의 머리는 아직 나아지지 않았으나, 내일이면 완전히 회복하리라 기대하고 있었다—그 부인과 이디스와 돔비 씨가 있었다."], ["Edith was at her open window looking out into the street; Mr Dombey and Cleopatra were talking softly on the sofa.", "이디스는 열린 창가에서 거리를 내다보고 있었고, 돔비 씨와 클레오파트라는 소파에서 나직이 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["It was growing late; and Florence, being fatigued, had gone to bed.", "밤이 깊어 가고 있었다. 그리고 플로렌스는 지친 나머지 잠자리에 든 뒤였다."]], [["“My dear Dombey,”", "\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\""], ["said Cleopatra, “you will leave me Florence to-morrow, when you deprive me of my sweetest Edith.”", "클레오파트라가 말했다, \"내일 내 더없이 사랑하는 이디스를 내게서 앗아 갈 때, 플로렌스는 내게 남겨 두고 가겠죠.\""]], [["Mr Dombey said he would, with pleasure.", "돔비 씨는 기꺼이 그러겠다고 말했다."]], [["“To have her about me, here, while you are both at Paris, and to think at her age, I am assisting in the formation of her mind, my dear Dombey,”", "\"두 사람이 다 파리에 가 있는 동안 저 애를 여기 내 곁에 두고서, 저 애 나이에 내가 그 마음을 빚어 내는 일을 거들고 있다고 생각하면, 내 사랑하는 돔비,\""], ["said Cleopatra, “will be a perfect balm to me in the extremely shattered state to which I shall be reduced.”", "클레오파트라가 말했다, \"내가 빠지게 될 그 극도로 산산조각 난 상태에 더없는 위안이 될 거예요.\""]], [["Edith turned her head suddenly.", "이디스가 불쑥 고개를 돌렸다."], ["Her listless manner was exchanged, in a moment, to one of burning interest, and, unseen in the darkness, she attended closely to their conversation.", "그 무기력한 태도가 한순간에 불타는 듯한 관심의 태도로 바뀌었고, 어둠 속에 가려진 채 그녀는 두 사람의 대화에 바짝 귀를 기울였다."]], [["Mr Dombey would be delighted to leave Florence in such admirable guardianship.", "돔비 씨는 플로렌스를 그토록 훌륭한 보호 아래 맡겨 두게 되어 기쁘기 그지없다고 했다."]], [["“My dear Dombey,”", "\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\""], ["returned Cleopatra, “a thousand thanks for your good opinion.", "클레오파트라가 받았다, \"그 좋은 평가에 천 번 감사드려요."], ["I feared you were going, with malice aforethought, as the dreadful lawyers say—those horrid prosers!—to condemn me to utter solitude.”", "나는 당신이, 그 끔찍한 변호사들 말마따나—그 지긋지긋한 수다쟁이들 같으니!—'미리 작심한 악의를 품고서', 나를 완전한 고독에 처하게 하려는 줄 알고 두려웠답니다.\""]], [["“Why do me so great an injustice, my dear madam?”", "\"어찌하여 나를 그토록 부당하게 보십니까, 부인?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Because my charming Florence tells me so positively she must go home tomorrow, returned Cleopatra, that I began to be afraid, my dearest Dombey, you were quite a Bashaw.”", "\"우리 매력적인 플로렌스가 내일 꼭 집에 가야 한다고 어찌나 단호하게 말하던지,\" 클레오파트라가 받았다, \"내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비, 나는 당신이 완전히 폭군인가 보다 하고 두려워지기 시작했거든요.\""]], [["“I assure you, madam!”", "\"분명히 말씀드립니다, 부인!\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “I have laid no commands on Florence; and if I had, there are no commands like your wish.”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"나는 플로렌스에게 아무런 명령도 내린 적이 없습니다. 그리고 설령 내렸다 한들, 부인의 바람만 한 명령은 없지요.\""]], [["“My dear Dombey,”", "\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\""], ["replied Cleopatra, what a courtier you are!", "클레오파트라가 대답했다, \"당신은 어쩌면 그렇게 아첨꾼인지!"], ["Though I’ll not say so, either; for courtiers have no heart, and yours pervades your farming life and character.", "하지만 그렇게 말하지는 않겠어요. 아첨꾼에게는 마음이 없는 법인데, 당신의 마음은 당신의 일하는 삶과 인품에 두루 배어 있으니까요."], ["And are you really going so early, my dear Dombey!”", "그래, 정말 이렇게 일찍 가려는 거예요, 내 사랑하는 돔비!\""]], [["Oh, indeed!", "아, 그렇다마다요!"], ["it was late, and Mr Dombey feared he must.", "늦은 시각이었고, 돔비 씨는 가야만 할 것 같았다."]], [["“Is this a fact, or is it all a dream!”", "\"이게 사실인가요, 아니면 다 꿈인가요!\""], ["lisped Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 혀짤배기소리로 말했다."], ["“Can I believe, my dearest Dombey, that you are coming back tomorrow morning to deprive me of my sweet companion; my own Edith!”", "\"믿을 수 있을까요, 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비, 당신이 내일 아침에 다시 와서 내 사랑스러운 벗, 나만의 이디스를 내게서 앗아 가리라는 것을!\""]], [["Mr Dombey, who was accustomed to take things literally, reminded Mrs Skewton that they were to meet first at the church.", "만사를 곧이곧대로 받아들이는 데 익숙한 돔비 씨는, 스큐턴 부인에게 두 사람이 먼저 교회에서 만나기로 했음을 일깨워 주었다."]], [["“The pang,”", "\"자식을, 비록 당신에게라 해도, 내 사랑하는 돔비, 내주어야 하는 그 고통은,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “of consigning a child, even to you, my dear Dombey, is one of the most excruciating imaginable, and combined with a naturally delicate constitution, and the extreme stupidity of the pastry-cook who has undertaken the breakfast, is almost too much for my poor strength.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"상상할 수 있는 가장 가슴 에이는 일 가운데 하나예요. 게다가 타고나기를 섬약한 체질인 데다, 아침 식사를 맡은 제과점 주인의 극심한 우둔함까지 겹치면, 내 가련한 기력으로는 거의 감당하기 어려울 지경이랍니다."], ["But I shall rally, my dear Dombey, in the morning; do not fear for me, or be uneasy on my account.", "하지만 아침이 되면 기운을 차릴 거예요, 내 사랑하는 돔비. 나에 대해서는 염려 말고, 나 때문에 마음 쓰지 마세요."], ["Heaven bless you!", "당신에게 하늘의 축복을!"], ["My dearest Edith!”", "내 더없이 사랑하는 이디스!\""], ["she cried archly.", "그녀가 짓궂게 외쳤다."], ["“Somebody is going, pet.”", "\"누가 가려는구나, 얘야.\""]], [["Edith, who had turned her head again towards the window, and whose interest in their conversation had ceased, rose up in her place, but made no advance towards him, and said nothing.", "다시 창문 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸고 두 사람의 대화에 대한 관심이 식어 버린 이디스는, 제자리에서 일어섰지만 그에게로 다가가지도, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Mr Dombey, with a lofty gallantry adapted to his dignity and the occasion, betook his creaking boots towards her, put her hand to his lips, said, “Tomorrow morning I shall have the happiness of claiming this hand as Mrs Dombey’s,”", "돔비 씨는 자기 위엄과 그 자리에 걸맞은 고고한 정중함으로, 삐걱거리는 부츠를 끌고 그녀에게로 가서, 그녀의 손을 입술에 갖다 대고는, \"내일 아침이면 이 손을 돔비 부인의 손으로서 차지하는 행복을 누리겠군요\""], ["and bowed himself solemnly out.", "하고 말한 뒤, 엄숙하게 절하고 물러났다."]], [["Mrs Skewton rang for candles as soon as the house-door had closed upon him.", "스큐턴 부인은 현관문이 그의 뒤로 닫히자마자 촛불을 가져오라고 종을 울렸다."], ["With the candles appeared her maid, with the juvenile dress that was to delude the world to-morrow.", "촛불과 함께 그녀의 하녀가, 내일 온 세상을 속일 그 젊어 보이는 드레스를 들고 나타났다."], ["The dress had savage retribution in it, as such dresses ever have, and made her infinitely older and more hideous than her greasy flannel gown.", "그 드레스에는, 그런 드레스가 으레 그렇듯, 잔인한 응보가 깃들어 있어서, 그녀를 기름때 낀 플란넬 잠옷보다 한없이 더 늙고 더 흉하게 만들었다. 그러나 스큐턴 부인은 새침한 만족감으로 그것을 입어 보았다."], ["But Mrs Skewton tried it on with mincing satisfaction; smirked at her cadaverous self in the glass, as she thought of its killing effect upon the Major; and suffering her maid to take it off again, and to prepare her for repose, tumbled into ruins like a house of painted cards.", "그것이 소령에게 미칠 죽여주는 효과를 떠올리며, 거울 속 자기의 송장 같은 모습을 향해 히죽거렸다. 그러고는 하녀가 다시 그것을 벗기고 잠자리에 들 채비를 해 주게 한 뒤, 그림 그려진 카드로 지은 집처럼 폭삭 무너져 내렸다."]], [["All this time, Edith remained at the dark window looking out into the street.", "이 모든 동안 이디스는 어두운 창가에 남아 거리를 내다보고 있었다."], ["When she and her mother were at last left alone, she moved from it for the first time that evening, and came opposite to her.", "그녀와 어머니가 마침내 단둘이 남게 되자, 그녀는 그 저녁 들어 처음으로 창가에서 물러나 어머니 맞은편으로 와 섰다."], ["The yawning, shaking, peevish figure of the mother, with her eyes raised to confront the proud erect form of the daughter, whose glance of fire was bent downward upon her, had a conscious air upon it, that no levity or temper could conceal.", "하품하고 떨고 짜증스러운 어머니의 형체가, 자기를 마주 보려고 눈을 치켜뜬 채로, 불꽃 같은 시선을 아래로 떨구고 있는 딸의 오만하고 꼿꼿한 자태와 마주하니, 그 위에는 어떤 경박함이나 성마름으로도 감출 수 없는 켕기는 기색이 어려 있었다."]], [["“I am tired to death,”", "\"나는 죽도록 지쳤다.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You can’t be trusted for a moment.", "\"너는 한순간도 믿고 맡길 수가 없어."], ["You are worse than a child.", "어린애보다도 못해. 어린애라니!"], ["Child! No child would be half so obstinate and undutiful.”", "어떤 어린애도 반만큼이라도 이렇게 고집스럽고 도리를 모르지는 않을 거다.\""]], [["“Listen to me, mother,”", "\"제 말 들으세요, 어머니.\""], ["returned Edith, passing these words by with a scorn that would not descend to trifle with them.", "이디스가, 이런 말들과 시시한 입씨름을 하기에는 격이 떨어진다는 듯한 경멸로 그 말들을 흘려보내며 받았다."], ["“You must remain alone here until I return.”", "\"제가 돌아올 때까지 어머니는 여기 혼자 계셔야 해요.\""]], [["“Must remain alone here, Edith, until you return!”", "\"내가 돌아올 때까지 여기 혼자 있어야 한다니, 이디스!\""], ["repeated her mother.", "그녀의 어머니가 되뇌었다."]], [["“Or in that name upon which I shall call to-morrow to witness what I do, so falsely: and so shamefully, I swear I will refuse the hand of this man in the church.", "\"안 그러면, 내일 제가 그토록 거짓되이, 그토록 부끄럽게 행하는 것을 지켜보라고 불러낼 그 이름을 걸고 맹세컨대, 저는 교회에서 이 남자의 손을 거절하겠어요."], ["If I do not, may I fall dead upon the pavement!”", "그러지 않으면, 제가 포석 위에 쓰러져 죽기를!\""]], [["The mother answered with a look of quick alarm, in no degree diminished by the look she met.", "어머니는 다급한 경악의 눈빛으로 대꾸했고, 그 눈빛은 자기가 마주한 눈빛 때문에 조금도 누그러지지 않았다."]], [["“It is enough,” said Edith, steadily, “that we are what we are.", "\"우리가 우리 같은 존재라는 것으로 족해요.\""], ["I will have no youth and truth dragged down to my level.", "이디스가 단호하게 말했다. \"저는 어떤 젊음과 진실함도 제 수준으로 끌어내리지 않을 거예요."], ["I will have no guileless nature undermined, corrupted, and perverted, to amuse the leisure of a world of mothers.", "어떤 순진무구한 천성도, 어미들로 가득 찬 이 세상의 한가한 소일거리를 위해, 좀먹히고 더럽혀지고 비틀어지게 두지 않겠어요."], ["You know my meaning.", "제 말뜻을 아시잖아요."], ["Florence must go home.”", "플로렌스는 집으로 가야 해요.\""]], [["“You are an idiot, Edith,”", "\"너는 바보로구나, 이디스.\""], ["cried her angry mother.", "그녀의 화난 어머니가 외쳤다."], ["“Do you expect there can ever be peace for you in that house, till she is married, and away?”", "\"저 애가 시집가서 떠나기 전에는, 저 집에서 너한테 평화란 게 있을 성싶으냐?\""]], [["“Ask me, or ask yourself, if I ever expect peace in that house,”", "\"제가 저 집에서 평화를 바라기나 하는지, 저에게든 어머니 자신에게든 물어보세요.\""], ["said her daughter, “and you know the answer.”", "딸이 말했다. \"그러면 답을 아실 거예요.\""]], [["“And am I to be told tonight, after all my pains and labour, and when you are going, through me, to be rendered independent,”", "\"그리고 그 온갖 수고와 노고를 다 들이고, 또 네가 나를 통해 독립된 처지가 되려는 마당에, 오늘 밤 내가 이런 소리를 들어야 한단 말이냐,\""], ["her mother almost shrieked in her passion, while her palsied head shook like a leaf, “that there is corruption and contagion in me, and that I am not fit company for a girl!", "그녀의 어머니가 격정에 차 거의 비명을 지르다시피 했고, 그러는 동안 그 중풍 든 머리가 나뭇잎처럼 떨렸다, \"내 안에 부패와 전염병이 있어서, 내가 한 소녀에게 어울릴 만한 동무가 못 된다는 소리를!"], ["What are you, pray?", "그럼 도대체 너는 뭐냐?"], ["What are you?”", "너는 뭐냔 말이다?\""]], [["“I have put the question to myself,”", "\"저는 그 물음을 제 자신에게 던져 봤어요.\""], ["said Edith, ashy pale, and pointing to the window, “more than once when I have been sitting there, and something in the faded likeness of my sex has wandered past outside; and God knows I have met with my reply.", "이디스가, 잿빛으로 창백해진 채 창문을 가리키며 말했다, \"저기 앉아 있을 때, 그리고 빛바랜 내 성(性)의 모습을 한 무언가가 바깥을 지나쳐 갈 때, 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번 던져 봤어요. 그리고 하느님은 아시지요, 제가 어떤 답을 만났는지를."], ["Oh mother, mother, if you had but left me to my natural heart when I too was a girl—a younger girl than Florence—how different I might have been!”", "오 어머니, 어머니, 저도 한낱 소녀였을 때—플로렌스보다 더 어린 소녀였을 때—저를 제 본래의 마음에 그저 내버려 두기만 하셨더라면, 저는 얼마나 달라졌을까요!\""]], [["Sensible that any show of anger was useless here, her mother restrained herself, and fell a whimpering, and bewailed that she had lived too long, and that her only child had cast her off, and that duty towards parents was forgotten in these evil days, and that she had heard unnatural taunts, and cared for life no longer.", "이 자리에서는 어떤 노여움의 표시도 소용없음을 깨달은 그녀의 어머니는, 스스로를 억누르고 훌쩍훌쩍 울기 시작하더니, 자기가 너무 오래 살았다고, 하나뿐인 자식이 자기를 내쳤다고, 부모에 대한 도리란 이 악한 시절에는 잊혀 버렸다고, 자기가 인륜에 어긋나는 빈정거림을 들었으니 더는 사는 데 미련이 없다고 한탄했다."]], [["“If one is to go on living through continual scenes like this,”", "\"이런 일을 끊임없이 겪으며 계속 살아가야 한다면,\""], ["she whined, “I am sure it would be much better for me to think of some means of putting an end to my existence.", "그녀가 칭얼거렸다, \"차라리 내 목숨을 끊을 무슨 방도를 궁리하는 편이 훨씬 나을 거야."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["The idea of your being my daughter, Edith, and addressing me in such a strain!”", "네가 내 딸이라는 생각이, 이디스, 그러고도 내게 그런 투로 말을 하다니!\""]], [["“Between us, mother,”", "\"우리 사이에는, 어머니,\""], ["returned Edith, mournfully, “the time for mutual reproaches is past.”", "이디스가 애처롭게 받았다, \"서로를 책망할 때는 지났어요.\""]], [["“Then why do you revive it?”", "\"그럼 왜 그걸 다시 들춰내는 거냐?\""], ["whimpered her mother.", "그녀의 어머니가 훌쩍였다."], ["“You know that you are lacerating me in the cruellest manner.", "\"네가 더없이 잔인하게 나를 갈가리 찢고 있는 걸 너도 알잖니."], ["You know how sensitive I am to unkindness.", "내가 매정한 소리에 얼마나 예민한지도 알면서."], ["At such a moment, too, when I have so much to think of, and am naturally anxious to appear to the best advantage!", "게다가 이런 때에, 생각할 게 그토록 많고, 또 당연히 가장 좋은 모습으로 보이고 싶어 안달인 이런 때에!"], ["I wonder at you, Edith.", "너한테 기가 막힐 따름이다, 이디스."], ["To make your mother a fright upon your wedding-day!”", "네 결혼식 날에 네 어미를 흉물로 만들다니!\""]], [["Edith bent the same fixed look upon her, as she sobbed and rubbed her eyes; and said in the same low steady voice, which had neither risen nor fallen since she first addressed her, “I have said that Florence must go home.”", "이디스는, 그녀가 흐느끼며 눈을 비비는 동안, 똑같은 한결같은 시선을 그녀에게 붙박은 채, 처음 말을 건넨 이래로 높아지지도 낮아지지도 않은 그 똑같이 낮고 한결같은 목소리로 말했다. \"플로렌스는 집으로 가야 한다고 제가 말했어요.\""]], [["“Let her go!”", "\"가게 해라!\""], ["cried the afflicted and affrighted parent, hastily.", "그 고통받고 겁먹은 어미가 황급히 외쳤다."], ["“I am sure I am willing she should go.", "\"당연히 나도 저 애가 가는 게 좋다."], ["What is the girl to me?”", "그 계집애가 나한테 뭐라고?\""]], [["“She is so much to me, that rather than communicate, or suffer to be communicated to her, one grain of the evil that is in my breast, mother, I would renounce you, as I would (if you gave me cause) renounce him in the church to-morrow,”", "\"제게는 너무도 소중한 사람이라, 제 가슴속에 있는 악의 한 톨이라도 그 애에게 전하거나 전해지게 두느니, 어머니, 차라리 저는 어머니를 버리겠어요. 내일 교회에서 (어머니가 제게 빌미를 준다면) 그 사람을 버리겠듯이 말이에요.\""], ["replied Edith.", "이디스가 대답했다."], ["“Leave her alone.", "\"그 애를 가만히 두세요."], ["She shall not, while I can interpose, be tampered with and tainted by the lessons I have learned.", "제가 가로막을 수 있는 한, 그 애는 제가 배운 가르침에 농락당하거나 더럽혀지지 않을 거예요."], ["This is no hard condition on this bitter night.”", "이건 이 쓰라린 밤에 가혹한 조건이 아니에요.\""]], [["“If you had proposed it in a filial manner, Edith,”", "\"네가 이걸 자식다운 태도로 청했더라면, 이디스,\""], ["whined her mother, “perhaps not; very likely not.", "그녀의 어머니가 칭얼거렸다, \"어쩌면 안 그랬을지도 모르지. 십중팔구 안 그랬을 거야."], ["But such extremely cutting words—”", "하지만 그토록 극도로 매서운 말들이라니—\""]], [["“They are past and at an end between us now,”", "\"그건 이제 우리 사이에서는 지나갔고 끝난 일이에요.\" 이디스가 말했다."], ["said Edith.", "\"어머니 방식대로 하세요."], ["“Take your own way, mother; share as you please in what you have gained; spend, enjoy, make much of it; and be as happy as you will.", "어머니가 얻은 것을 어머니 마음껏 나누어 가지세요. 쓰고, 누리고, 한껏 즐기시고, 원하시는 만큼 행복하세요."], ["The object of our lives is won.", "우리 삶의 목적은 이루어졌어요."], ["Henceforth let us wear it silently.", "이제부터는 그것을 말없이 지니고 가요."], ["My lips are closed upon the past from this hour.", "이 시각부터 제 입술은 지난 일에 대해 닫혔어요."], ["I forgive you your part in to-morrow’s wickedness.", "내일의 악행에서 어머니가 맡으신 몫은 용서할게요."], ["May God forgive my own!”", "제 몫은 하느님이 용서해 주시기를!\""]], [["Without a tremor in her voice, or frame, and passing onward with a foot that set itself upon the neck of every soft emotion, she bade her mother good-night, and repaired to her own room.", "목소리에도 몸에도 떨림 하나 없이, 온갖 여린 감정의 목덜미를 짓밟는 발걸음으로 앞으로 나아가며, 그녀는 어머니에게 안녕히 주무시라 인사하고 자기 방으로 물러났다."]], [["But not to rest; for there was no rest in the tumult of her agitation when alone to and fro, and to and fro, and to and fro again, five hundred times, among the splendid preparations for her adornment on the morrow; with her dark hair shaken down, her dark eyes flashing with a raging light, her broad white bosom red with the cruel grasp of the relentless hand with which she spurned it from her, pacing up and down with an averted head, as if she would avoid the sight of her own fair person, and divorce herself from its companionship.", "그러나 쉬기 위해서는 아니었다. 혼자가 되니 그 격동의 소란 속에는 안식이라곤 없었던 것이다."], ["Thus, in the dead time of the night before her bridal, Edith Granger wrestled with her unquiet spirit, tearless, friendless, silent, proud, and uncomplaining.", "내일 자기를 단장할 그 화려한 준비물들 사이를, 어두운 머리를 풀어 헤친 채, 검은 눈에 사나운 빛을 번득이며, 무자비한 손으로 그것을 제 몸에서 뿌리치느라 그 손아귀의 잔인한 움켜쥠으로 발갛게 된 넓고 흰 가슴으로, 마치 제 고운 몸을 보기조차 싫다는 듯, 또 그 동반에서 자기를 떼어 놓기라도 하려는 듯 고개를 돌린 채로, 오백 번이나 이리저리, 또 이리저리, 또다시 이리저리 거닐었다. 그렇게, 혼례 전날 밤 죽은 듯한 시각에, 이디스 그레인저는 자기의 불안한 영혼과 씨름했으니, 눈물도 없이, 벗도 없이, 말도 없이, 도도하게, 그리고 아무런 하소연도 없이."]], [["At length it happened that she touched the open door which led into the room where Florence lay.", "마침내 그녀는 플로렌스가 누워 있는 방으로 통하는 열린 문을 만지게 되었다."]], [["She started, stopped, and looked in.", "그녀는 흠칫 놀라 멈춰 서서, 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["A light was burning there, and showed her Florence in her bloom of innocence and beauty, fast asleep.", "거기에는 등불 하나가 타고 있었고, 순결함과 아름다움이 한창인 플로렌스가 깊이 잠들어 있는 모습을 비춰 주었다."], ["Edith held her breath, and felt herself drawn on towards her.", "이디스는 숨을 죽였고, 자기가 그녀에게로 이끌려 가는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Drawn nearer, nearer, nearer yet; at last, drawn so near, that stooping down, she pressed her lips to the gentle hand that lay outside the bed, and put it softly to her neck.", "더 가까이, 더 가까이, 더더욱 가까이 이끌려 갔다. 마침내 어찌나 가까이 이끌려 갔던지, 몸을 굽혀, 침대 밖으로 내놓인 그 가녀린 손에 입술을 갖다 대고, 그것을 가만히 제 목으로 가져갔다."], ["Its touch was like the prophet’s rod of old upon the rock.", "그 손길은 옛적 바위를 친 예언자의 지팡이와도 같았다."], ["Her tears sprung forth beneath it, as she sunk upon her knees, and laid her aching head and streaming hair upon the pillow by its side.", "그 아래에서 그녀의 눈물이 솟구쳐 나왔고, 그녀는 무릎을 꿇으며 그 손 곁의 베개 위에 자기의 욱신거리는 머리와 흐르는 머리채를 뉘었다."]], [["Thus Edith Granger passed the night before her bridal.", "그렇게 이디스 그레인저는 혼례 전날 밤을 보냈다."], ["Thus the sun found her on her bridal morning.", "그렇게 혼례 아침의 해는 그녀를 그 모습으로 맞이했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXI. The Wedding", "# CHAPTER XXXI. The Wedding"]], [["Dawn with its passionless blank face, steals shivering to the church beneath which lies the dust of little Paul and his mother, and looks in at the windows.", "무심하고 텅 빈 얼굴을 한 새벽이 어린 폴과 그 어머니의 유해가 잠든 교회 쪽으로 떨면서 살그머니 다가와, 창문 너머로 안을 들여다본다."], ["It is cold and dark.", "춥고 어둡다."], ["Night crouches yet, upon the pavement, and broods, sombre and heavy, in nooks and corners of the building.", "밤은 아직도 포석 위에 웅크린 채, 건물의 구석구석과 모퉁이에 음울하고 무겁게 도사리고 있다."], ["The steeple-clock, perched up above the houses, emerging from beneath another of the countless ripples in the tide of time that regularly roll and break on the eternal shore, is greyly visible, like a stone beacon, recording how the sea flows on; but within doors, dawn, at first, can only peep at night, and see that it is there.", "집들 위로 우뚝 솟은 첨탑 시계는, 영원한 기슭에 규칙적으로 밀려와 부서지는 시간의 조수, 그 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 물결 가운데 또 하나의 물결 밑에서 솟아나오며, 마치 바다가 어떻게 흘러가는지를 기록하는 돌 봉화처럼 잿빛으로 어렴풋이 보인다. 그러나 문 안쪽에서는, 새벽이 처음에는 그저 밤을 엿볼 수 있을 뿐이고, 밤이 거기 있다는 것을 알아챌 따름이다."]], [["Hovering feebly round the church, and looking in, dawn moans and weeps for its short reign, and its tears trickle on the window-glass, and the trees against the church-wall bow their heads, and wring their many hands in sympathy.", "교회 둘레를 힘없이 맴돌며 안을 들여다보면서, 새벽은 제 짧은 통치를 두고 신음하며 운다. 그 눈물이 창유리 위로 흘러내리고, 교회 벽에 기댄 나무들은 머리를 숙인 채 동정하여 그 수많은 손을 비틀어 짠다."], ["Night, growing pale before it, gradually fades out of the church, but lingers in the vaults below, and sits upon the coffins.", "밤은 그 앞에서 점점 창백해지더니, 차츰 교회에서 스러져 나가지만, 아래쪽 납골당에는 머뭇거리며 남아, 관 위에 앉아 있다."], ["And now comes bright day, burnishing the steeple-clock, and reddening the spire, and drying up the tears of dawn, and stifling its complaining; and the dawn, following the night, and chasing it from its last refuge, shrinks into the vaults itself and hides, with a frightened face, among the dead, until night returns, refreshed, to drive it out.", "그리고 이제 밝은 낮이 찾아와, 첨탑 시계를 광나게 닦고, 뾰족탑을 붉게 물들이며, 새벽의 눈물을 말리고, 그 하소연을 잠재운다. 그러자 밤을 뒤따라 그 마지막 피난처에서 밤을 쫓아내던 새벽은, 이번에는 제가 납골당 속으로 움츠러들어, 겁먹은 얼굴로 죽은 자들 틈에 숨어, 밤이 새 기운을 얻어 돌아와 저를 몰아낼 때까지 기다린다."]], [["And now, the mice, who have been busier with the prayer-books than their proper owners, and with the hassocks, more worn by their little teeth than by human knees, hide their bright eyes in their holes, and gather close together in affright at the resounding clashing of the church-door.", "그리고 이제, 기도서를 그 본래 임자들보다 더 부지런히 들쑤셔 댔던, 그리고 사람 무릎보다 제 자그마한 이빨로 더 닳게 한 무릎방석들을 들쑤셔 댔던 쥐들이, 교회 문이 쾅 하고 울리는 소리에 깜짝 놀라, 반짝이는 눈을 제 구멍 속에 감추고 한데 바싹 모여든다."], ["For the beadle, that man of power, comes early this morning with the sexton; and Mrs Miff, the wheezy little pew-opener—a mighty dry old lady, sparely dressed, with not an inch of fulness anywhere about her—is also here, and has been waiting at the church-gate half-an-hour, as her place is, for the beadle.", "권세 있는 자, 교회지기가 오늘 아침 일찍 묘지기와 함께 오기 때문이다. 그리고 쌕쌕거리는 자그마한 좌석 안내인 미프 부인—몸 어디에도 한 치의 군더더기 살이라곤 없이, 검소하게 차려입은, 무척이나 메마른 노부인—도 여기 와 있는데, 그녀는 제 직분대로, 교회 문에서 반 시간 동안이나 교회지기를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["A vinegary face has Mrs Miff, and a mortified bonnet, and eke a thirsty soul for sixpences and shillings.", "미프 부인은 식초처럼 시큼한 얼굴에, 풀이 죽은 보닛을 쓰고 있으며, 게다가 육 펜스와 한 실링짜리 동전에 목마른 영혼을 지녔다."], ["Beckoning to stray people to come into pews, has given Mrs Miff an air of mystery; and there is reservation in the eye of Mrs Miff, as always knowing of a softer seat, but having her suspicions of the fee.", "길 잃은 사람들을 좌석으로 들어오라고 손짓해 부르는 일은 미프 부인에게 어딘가 신비로운 분위기를 주었다. 또한 미프 부인의 눈에는 언제나 더 푹신한 자리를 알고 있으면서도 사례비에 대해서는 미심쩍어하는, 그런 유보의 빛이 어려 있다."], ["There is no such fact as Mr Miff, nor has there been, these twenty years, and Mrs Miff would rather not allude to him.", "미프 씨라는 사람은 존재하지도 않거니와, 지난 이십 년 동안 존재한 적도 없는데, 미프 부인은 차라리 그 사람 이야기는 꺼내지 않으려 한다."], ["He held some bad opinions, it would seem, about free seats; and though Mrs Miff hopes he may be gone upwards, she couldn’t positively undertake to say so.", "듣자 하니 그는 무료 좌석에 관하여 좋지 못한 견해를 품고 있었던 모양인데, 미프 부인은 그가 위로 올라갔기를 바라기는 하나, 그렇다고 단언해서 그렇다고 말할 수는 없는 것이다."]], [["Busy is Mrs Miff this morning at the church-door, beating and dusting the altar-cloth, the carpet, and the cushions; and much has Mrs Miff to say, about the wedding they are going to have.", "오늘 아침 미프 부인은 교회 문에서, 제단보와 양탄자와 방석들을 두드리고 먼지를 털어 내느라 바쁘다. 그리고 미프 부인은 이제 곧 있을 결혼식에 대해 할 말이 많다."], ["Mrs Miff is told, that the new furniture and alterations in the house cost full five thousand pound if they cost a penny; and Mrs Miff has heard, upon the best authority, that the lady hasn’t got a sixpence wherewithal to bless herself.", "미프 부인이 듣기로는, 그 댁의 새 가구와 개조에 든 비용이 한 푼이라도 들었다면 족히 오천 파운드는 들었다고 하며, 미프 부인은 가장 믿을 만한 소식통으로부터, 그 신붓감 여인에게는 제 한 몸 축복할 만한 육 펜스 한 닢도 없다는 말을 들었다."], ["Mrs Miff remembers, like wise, as if it had happened yesterday, the first wife’s funeral, and then the christening, and then the other funeral; and Mrs Miff says, by-the-by she’ll soap-and-water that “ere tablet presently, against the company arrive.", "미프 부인은 또한, 마치 어제 일이라도 되는 양, 첫 부인의 장례식을, 그다음 세례식을, 또 그다음 다른 장례식을 기억하고 있다. 그리고 미프 부인은, 참 그러고 보니, 손님들이 도착하기 전에 이제 곧 저 명판을 비누와 물로 닦아 두어야겠다고 말한다."], ["Mr Sownds the Beadle, who is sitting in the sun upon the church steps all this time (and seldom does anything else, except, in cold weather, sitting by the fire), approves of Mrs Miff’s discourse, and asks if Mrs Miff has heard it said, that the lady is uncommon handsome?", "이 내내 교회 계단에 앉아 햇볕을 쬐고 있던(그리고 추운 날씨에 난롯가에 앉는 것 말고는 좀체 다른 일을 하는 법이 없는) 교회지기 사운즈 씨가, 미프 부인의 이야기에 동의하면서, 그 여인이 보기 드물게 미인이라는 말을 미프 부인도 들었느냐고 묻는다."], ["The information Mrs Miff has received, being of this nature, Mr Sownds the Beadle, who, though orthodox and corpulent, is still an admirer of female beauty, observes, with unction, yes, he hears she is a spanker—an expression that seems somewhat forcible to Mrs Miff, or would, from any lips but those of Mr Sownds the Beadle.", "미프 부인이 받은 정보가 그런 종류의 것인지라, 비록 정통 신앙을 지녔고 살집이 두둑하긴 해도 여전히 여성의 아름다움을 흠모하는 교회지기 사운즈 씨는, 입맛을 다시듯 이렇게 말한다. 그럼요, 그 여인이 끝내주는 미인이라는 소리를 들었지요—이는 미프 부인에게는 다소 노골적으로 들리는 표현인데, 교회지기 사운즈 씨가 아닌 다른 누구의 입에서 나왔더라면 분명 그러했을 것이다."]], [["In Mr Dombey’s house, at this same time, there is great stir and bustle, more especially among the women: not one of whom has had a wink of sleep since four o’clock, and all of whom were fully dressed before six.", "바로 이 같은 시각, 돔비 씨의 댁에서는 큰 소동과 부산함이 일고 있는데, 특히 여자들 사이에서 그러하다. 그들 가운데 누구도 네 시 이후로는 한숨도 자지 못했고, 모두가 여섯 시도 되기 전에 옷을 다 차려입었다."], ["Mr Towlinson is an object of greater consideration than usual to the housemaid, and the cook says at breakfast time that one wedding makes many, which the housemaid can’t believe, and don’t think true at all.", "타울린슨 씨는 평소보다 더 하녀의 관심거리가 되어 있고, 요리사는 아침 식사 때 결혼식 하나가 여러 결혼식을 부른다고 말하지만, 하녀는 이를 믿지 못하며, 전혀 사실이 아니라고 생각한다."], ["Mr Towlinson reserves his sentiments on this question; being rendered something gloomy by the engagement of a foreigner with whiskers (Mr Towlinson is whiskerless himself), who has been hired to accompany the happy pair to Paris, and who is busy packing the new chariot.", "타울린슨 씨는 이 문제에 대한 제 소감을 아껴 둔다. 그가 다소 침울해진 까닭은, 행복한 부부를 따라 파리로 가도록 고용되어 새 사두마차를 꾸리느라 바쁜, 구레나룻 기른 외국인(타울린슨 씨 자신은 구레나룻이 없다)이 채용되었기 때문이다."], ["In respect of this personage, Mr Towlinson admits, presently, that he never knew of any good that ever come of foreigners; and being charged by the ladies with prejudice, says, look at Bonaparte who was at the head of ’em, and see what he was always up to!", "이 인물에 관하여, 타울린슨 씨는 이내, 외국인에게서 좋은 일이 생긴 걸 본 적이 한 번도 없다고 인정한다. 그리고 여자들에게 편견이라고 책망을 듣자, 그 우두머리였던 보나파르트를 보라, 그가 늘 무슨 짓을 꾸몄는지 보라고 말한다."], ["Which the housemaid says is very true.", "이에 하녀는 그거 참 옳은 말이라고 한다."]], [["The pastry-cook is hard at work in the funereal room in Brook Street, and the very tall young men are busy looking on.", "브룩 가의 그 장례식장 같은 방에서는 제과사가 한창 일에 몰두해 있고, 키가 무척 큰 젊은이들이 바삐 그 일을 거들어 본다."], ["One of the very tall young men already smells of sherry, and his eyes have a tendency to become fixed in his head, and to stare at objects without seeing them.", "키 큰 젊은이 가운데 하나는 벌써 셰리주 냄새를 풍기고, 그의 눈은 머리 속에 고정되어, 사물을 보면서도 보지 못한 채 멍하니 바라보는 경향이 있다."], ["The very tall young man is conscious of this failing in himself; and informs his comrade that it’s his “exciseman.”", "그 키 큰 젊은이는 제게 이런 결함이 있음을 의식하고 있어, 동료에게 그건 제 \"주세관\" 탓이라고 일러 준다."], ["The very tall young man would say excitement, but his speech is hazy.", "그 키 큰 젊은이는 흥분이라고 말하려는 것이지만, 그의 발음이 흐릿하다."]], [["The men who play the bells have got scent of the marriage; and the marrow-bones and cleavers too; and a brass band too.", "종을 울리는 사람들이 이 결혼식의 낌새를 알아챘고, 골수뼈와 식칼을 두드리는 패거리도 그러했으며, 금관 악대 또한 그러했다. 첫 번째 패거리는 배틀브리지 근처의 뒷동네에서 연습 중이고, 두 번째 패거리는 제 우두머리를 통해 타울린슨 씨와 연락을 트고는, 손을 떼는 대가로 돈을 받겠다는 조건을 제시한다."], ["The first, are practising in a back settlement near Battlebridge; the second, put themselves in communication, through their chief, with Mr Towlinson, to whom they offer terms to be bought off; and the third, in the person of an artful trombone, lurks and dodges round the corner, waiting for some traitor tradesman to reveal the place and hour of breakfast, for a bribe.", "그리고 세 번째 패거리는, 능청맞은 트롬본 연주자의 모습으로, 모퉁이 주위를 어슬렁대며 요리조리 피해 다니면서, 뇌물을 받고 아침 만찬의 장소와 시각을 일러바칠 배신자 상인이 나타나기를 기다린다. 기대와 흥분은 그보다 더 멀리까지 뻗어 나가, 한층 넓은 범위에 미친다."], ["Expectation and excitement extend further yet, and take a wider range.", "볼스 폰드에서 퍼치 씨가 퍼치 부인을 데려와, 돔비 씨네 하인들과 함께 하루를 보내며, 몰래 그들을 따라가 결혼식을 구경하게 한다. 투츠 씨의 하숙집에서는, 투츠 씨가 적어도 자기가 신랑이기라도 한 양 차려입는다."], ["From Balls Pond, Mr Perch brings Mrs Perch to spend the day with Mr Dombey’s servants, and accompany them, surreptitiously, to see the wedding.", "그는 회랑의 은밀한 구석에서 그 장관을 화려하게 지켜보기로, 그리고 그곳으로 치킨을 데려가기로 작정한다. 투츠 씨에게는 필사적인 의도가 있으니, 바로 그때 그 자리에서 치킨에게 플로렌스를 가리켜 보이며, 대놓고 이렇게 말하려는 것이다."], ["In Mr Toots’s lodgings, Mr Toots attires himself as if he were at least the Bridegroom; determined to behold the spectacle in splendour from a secret corner of the gallery, and thither to convey the Chicken: for it is Mr Toots’s desperate intent to point out Florence to the Chicken, then and there, and openly to say, “Now, Chicken, I will not deceive you any longer; the friend I have sometimes mentioned to you is myself; Miss Dombey is the object of my passion; what are your opinions, Chicken, in this state of things, and what, on the spot, do you advise?", "\"자, 치킨, 나는 더 이상 자넬 속이지 않겠네. 내가 가끔 자네에게 말하던 그 친구가 바로 나일세."], ["The so-much-to-be-astonished Chicken, in the meanwhile, dips his beak into a tankard of strong beer, in Mr Toots’s kitchen, and pecks up two pounds of beefsteaks.", "돔비 아가씨가 내 열정의 대상이라네. 이런 상황에서 자네 의견은 어떤가, 치킨, 그리고 당장 자네는 무얼 권하겠나?\""], ["In Princess’s Place, Miss Tox is up and doing; for she too, though in sore distress, is resolved to put a shilling in the hands of Mrs Miff, and see the ceremony which has a cruel fascination for her, from some lonely corner.", "한편, 그토록 놀랄 일이 많은 치킨은, 투츠 씨의 부엌에서 독한 맥주가 든 큰 잔에 부리를 처박고, 비프스테이크 두 파운드를 쪼아 먹는다. 프린세스 플레이스에서는 톡스 양이 일어나 분주하다."], ["The quarters of the wooden Midshipman are all alive; for Captain Cuttle, in his ankle-jacks and with a huge shirt-collar, is seated at his breakfast, listening to Rob the Grinder as he reads the marriage service to him beforehand, under orders, to the end that the Captain may perfectly understand the solemnity he is about to witness: for which purpose, the Captain gravely lays injunctions on his chaplain, from time to time, to “put about,”", "그녀 또한, 비록 쓰라린 괴로움 속에 있긴 하나, 미프 부인의 손에 한 실링을 쥐여 주고, 어느 외로운 구석에서, 자기에게는 잔인한 매혹으로 다가오는 그 예식을 지켜보기로 결심했기 때문이다. 나무 항해사 인형이 지키는 거처는 온통 활기에 차 있다."], ["or to “overhaul that “ere article again,”", "커틀 선장이, 발목까지 오는 부츠를 신고 커다란 셔츠 깃을 세운 채, 아침 식사 자리에 앉아, 분부에 따라 그라인더 롭이 그에게 결혼식 예문을 미리 읽어 주는 것을 듣고 있기 때문이다. 그가 곧 목격하게 될 그 엄숙한 의식을 선장이 완벽하게 이해할 수 있도록 하려는 것이었다."], ["or to stick to his own duty, and leave the Amens to him, the Captain; one of which he repeats, whenever a pause is made by Rob the Grinder, with sonorous satisfaction.", "이를 위하여 선장은 이따금 제 사제에게 엄숙하게 명을 내려, \"뱃머리를 돌려라\", 혹은 \"그 대목을 한 번 더 점검해 보라\"고 하거나, 아니면 네 본분에나 충실하고 아멘은 자기, 즉 선장에게 맡기라고 이른다. 그라인더 롭이 잠시 멈출 때마다 선장은 그 아멘 가운데 하나를 우렁차게, 흡족한 기색으로 되뇐다."]], [["Besides all this, and much more, twenty nursery-maids in Mr Dombey’s street alone, have promised twenty families of little women, whose instinctive interest in nuptials dates from their cradles, that they shall go and see the marriage.", "이 모든 것과 그 밖의 훨씬 더 많은 일에 더하여, 돔비 씨의 거리 하나에서만도 스무 명의 보모가, 요람에 누워 있을 적부터 본능적으로 결혼식에 관심을 두는 자그마한 여자아이들로 이루어진 스무 가족에게, 결혼식을 구경하러 가게 해 주겠노라 약속해 두었다."], ["Truly, Mr Sownds the Beadle has good reason to feel himself in office, as he suns his portly figure on the church steps, waiting for the marriage hour.", "참으로, 교회 계단에서 자신의 우람한 몸을 햇볕에 쬐며 결혼식 시각을 기다리는 교회지기 사운즈 씨가 스스로 직무를 수행하고 있다고 느낄 만한 충분한 이유가 있는 것이다."], ["Truly, Mrs Miff has cause to pounce on an unlucky dwarf child, with a giant baby, who peeps in at the porch, and drive her forth with indignation!", "참으로, 미프 부인이, 거인 같은 갓난아기를 안고 현관에서 안을 기웃거리는 운 나쁜 난쟁이 아이에게 와락 달려들어, 분개하며 그 아이를 쫓아낼 만한 까닭이 있는 것이다!"]], [["Cousin Feenix has come over from abroad, expressly to attend the marriage.", "사촌 피닉스는 이 결혼식에 참석하려고 일부러 외국에서 건너왔다."], ["Cousin Feenix was a man about town, forty years ago; but he is still so juvenile in figure and in manner, and so well got up, that strangers are amazed when they discover latent wrinkles in his lordship’s face, and crows’ feet in his eyes: and first observe him, not exactly certain when he walks across a room, of going quite straight to where he wants to go.", "사촌 피닉스는 사십 년 전 한량으로 이름을 떨치던 사내였다. 그러나 그는 아직도 몸매며 거동이 어찌나 젊어 보이고, 또 어찌나 잘 차려입었던지, 낯선 이들은 그분 얼굴에 숨은 주름을, 두 눈가의 까마귀 발 같은 잔주름을 발견하고는 깜짝 놀라며, 또 그가 방을 가로질러 걸어갈 때 가고자 하는 곳으로 곧장 가는지 어쩐지 도무지 확신할 수 없다는 것을 처음으로 알아챈다."], ["But Cousin Feenix, getting up at half-past seven o’clock or so, is quite another thing from Cousin Feenix got up; and very dim, indeed, he looks, while being shaved at Long’s Hotel, in Bond Street.", "그러나 일곱 시 반쯤 막 일어난 사촌 피닉스는 말끔히 단장한 사촌 피닉스와는 전혀 딴판이라, 본드 가의 롱스 호텔에서 면도를 받고 있을 적의 그는 정말이지 무척이나 흐리멍덩해 보인다."]], [["Mr Dombey leaves his dressing-room, amidst a general whisking away of the women on the staircase, who disperse in all directions, with a great rustling of skirts, except Mrs Perch, who, being (but that she always is) in an interesting situation, is not nimble, and is obliged to face him, and is ready to sink with confusion as she curtesys;—may Heaven avert all evil consequences from the house of Perch!", "돔비 씨가 화장실에서 나서니, 계단의 여자들이 일제히 휙휙 흩어진다. 그들은 치맛자락을 요란하게 바스락거리며 사방으로 흩어지는데, 다만 퍼치 부인만은—(언제나 그렇긴 하지만) 흥미로운 처지에 놓여 있는지라—민첩하지 못하여, 어쩔 수 없이 그를 마주하게 되고, 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 당혹감에 그만 주저앉을 듯하다."], ["Mr Dombey walks up to the drawing-room, to bide his time.", "부디 하늘이 퍼치 가문에 닥칠 온갖 불행을 막아 주시기를! 돔비 씨는 응접실로 올라가 제 때를 기다린다."], ["Gorgeous are Mr Dombey’s new blue coat, fawn-coloured pantaloons, and lilac waistcoat; and a whisper goes about the house, that Mr Dombey’s hair is curled.", "돔비 씨의 새 청색 외투며, 담갈색 바지며, 연보라색 조끼가 휘황찬란하다. 그리고 돔비 씨가 머리를 곱슬곱슬 지졌다는 수군거림이 집 안에 떠돈다."]], [["A double knock announces the arrival of the Major, who is gorgeous too, and wears a whole geranium in his button-hole, and has his hair curled tight and crisp, as well the Native knows.", "두 번의 노크 소리가 소령의 도착을 알린다. 그 역시 휘황찬란하게 차려입었고, 단춧구멍에 제라늄 한 송이를 통째로 꽂았으며, 머리를 꼬불꼬불 바싹 지졌으니, 토박이 하인이 그것을 잘 알고 있다."]], [["“Dombey!”", "\"돔비!\""], ["says the Major, putting out both hands, “how are you?”", "소령이 두 손을 내밀며 말한다, \"그동안 어떻게 지내셨소?\""]], [["“Major,”", "\"소령,\""], ["says Mr Dombey, “how are You?”", "돔비 씨가 말한다, \"댁은 어떻게 지내셨소?\""]], [["“By Jove, Sir,”", "\"맙소사, 선생,\""], ["says the Major, “Joey B. is in such case this morning, Sir,”—and here he hits himself hard upon the breast—“In such case this morning, Sir, that, damme, Dombey, he has half a mind to make a double marriage of it, Sir, and take the mother.”", "소령이 말한다, \"조이 비는 오늘 아침 이런 처지라오, 선생,\"—여기서 그는 제 가슴을 세게 친다—\"오늘 아침 이런 처지라서 말이오, 선생, 빌어먹을, 돔비, 그가 이참에 결혼을 두 쌍으로 하여 그 어머니를 차지할까 하는 마음이 반쯤 들지 뭐요, 선생.\""]], [["Mr Dombey smiles; but faintly, even for him; for Mr Dombey feels that he is going to be related to the mother, and that, under those circumstances, she is not to be joked about.", "돔비 씨는 미소 짓는다. 그러나 그로서도 희미하기 짝이 없는 미소다. 돔비 씨는 자기가 그 어머니와 친척이 될 참이고, 그런 사정에서 그 어머니를 두고 농담을 해서는 안 된다고 느끼기 때문이다."]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\""], ["says the Major, seeing this, “I give you joy.", "이를 본 소령이 말한다, \"축하드리오."], ["I congratulate you, Dombey.", "경하드리오, 돔비."], ["By the Lord, Sir,”", "맹세컨대, 선생,\""], ["says the Major, “you are more to be envied, this day, than any man in England!”", "소령이 말한다, \"오늘만큼은 영국의 그 어떤 사내보다도 댁이 더 부러움을 받을 만하오!\""]], [["Here again Mr Dombey’s assent is qualified; because he is going to confer a great distinction on a lady; and, no doubt, she is to be envied most.", "여기서 또다시 돔비 씨의 동의에는 단서가 붙는다. 그가 한 부인에게 크나큰 영예를 베풀어 줄 참인 까닭에, 의심할 바 없이 가장 부러움을 받을 사람은 그 부인일 터이기 때문이다."]], [["“As to Edith Granger, Sir,”", "\"에디스 그레인저 양으로 말하자면, 선생,\""], ["pursues the Major, “there is not a woman in all Europe but might—and would, Sir, you will allow Bagstock to add—and would—give her ears, and her earrings, too, to be in Edith Granger’s place.”", "소령이 말을 잇는다, \"온 유럽을 통틀어 어떤 여인이라도—그러고도 남을 것이오, 선생, 배그스톡이 한마디 덧붙이도록 허락하신다면—그러고도 남을 것이오—에디스 그레인저 양의 자리에 설 수만 있다면 제 두 귀라도, 게다가 귀고리까지도 내놓을 것이오.\""]], [["“You are good enough to say so, Major,”", "\"그렇게 말해 주시다니 친절하시구려, 소령,\""], ["says Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\""], ["returns the Major, “you know it.", "소령이 응수한다, \"댁도 그걸 알고 있질 않소."], ["Let us have no false delicacy.", "거짓된 점잔은 빼지 맙시다."], ["You know it.", "댁도 알고 있소."], ["Do you know it, or do you not, Dombey?”", "알고 있소, 아니면 모르고 있소, 돔비?\""], ["says the Major, almost in a passion.", "소령이 거의 발끈하며 말한다."]], [["“Oh, really, Major—”", "\"이런, 정말이지, 소령—\""]], [["“Damme, Sir,”", "\"빌어먹을, 선생,\""], ["retorts the Major, “do you know that fact, or do you not?", "소령이 받아친다, \"그 사실을 알고 있소, 모르고 있소?"], ["Dombey!", "돔비!"], ["Is old Joe your friend?", "늙은 조가 댁의 친구요?"], ["Are we on that footing of unreserved intimacy, Dombey, that may justify a man—a blunt old Joseph B., Sir—in speaking out; or am I to take open order, Dombey, and to keep my distance, and to stand on forms?”", "우리가, 한 사내—무뚝뚝한 늙은 조지프 비, 선생—가 거리낌 없이 솔직히 말할 만한 그런 허물없는 친밀한 사이요, 돔비? 아니면 내가 격식을 차려, 거리를 두고, 예법에 매여야겠소, 돔비?\""]], [["“My dear Major Bagstock,”", "\"이보시오, 배그스톡 소령,\""], ["says Mr Dombey, with a gratified air, “you are quite warm.”", "돔비 씨가 흐뭇한 기색으로 말한다, \"댁은 정말 흥분하셨구려.\""]], [["“By Gad, Sir,”", "\"맹세코, 선생,\""], ["says the Major, “I am warm.", "소령이 말한다, \"내가 흥분했소. 조지프 비는 그걸 부인하지 않소, 돔비."], ["Joseph B.", "그는 흥분했소."], ["does not deny it, Dombey. He is warm.", "이건, 선생, 늙고 지긋지긋하고 만신창이가 되고 닳아빠지고 병든 제이 비의 잔해 속에 남아 있는 온갖 진실한 정을 불러일으키는 자리란 말이오."], ["This is an occasion, Sir, that calls forth all the honest sympathies remaining in an old, infernal, battered, used-up, invalided, J. B.", "그리고 내 댁께 한마디 하리다, 돔비—이런 때에 사내라면 제가 느끼는 바를 불쑥 털어놓든지, 아니면 입에 재갈을 물려야 하는 법이오."], ["carcase. And I tell you what, Dombey—at such a time a man must blurt out what he feels, or put a muzzle on; and Joseph Bagstock tells you to your face, Dombey, as he tells his club behind your back, that he never will be muzzled when Paul Dombey is in question.", "그리고 조지프 배그스톡은 댁의 면전에서 말하겠소, 돔비, 댁의 등 뒤에서 제 클럽 사람들에게 말하듯이, 폴 돔비가 화제에 오르는 한 그는 결코 입에 재갈을 물지 않을 것이라고."], ["Now, damme, Sir,”", "자, 빌어먹을, 선생,\""], ["concludes the Major, with great firmness, “what do you make of that?”", "소령이 자못 단호하게 결론짓는다, \"그 점을 어떻게 생각하시오?\""]], [["“Major,”", "\"소령,\""], ["says Mr Dombey, “I assure you that I am really obliged to you.", "돔비 씨가 말한다, \"내 진심으로 댁께 신세를 졌소이다."], ["I had no idea of checking your too partial friendship.”", "댁의 지나치게 편향된 우정을 막아설 생각은 추호도 없었소.\""]], [["“Not too partial, Sir!”", "\"지나치게 편향된 것이 아니오, 선생!\""], ["exclaims the choleric Major.", "성마른 소령이 외친다."], ["“Dombey, I deny it.”", "\"돔비, 난 그걸 부인하오.\""]], [["“Your friendship I will say then,”", "\"그렇다면 댁의 우정이라고 해 둡시다,\""], ["pursues Mr Dombey, “on any account.", "돔비 씨가 말을 잇는다, \"어쨌든 말이오."], ["Nor can I forget, Major, on such an occasion as the present, how much I am indebted to it.”", "또한 소령, 오늘 같은 자리에서, 내가 그 우정에 얼마나 큰 빚을 졌는지 잊을 수도 없소.\""]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\""], ["says the Major, with appropriate action, “that is the hand of Joseph Bagstock: of plain old Joey B., Sir, if you like that better!", "소령이 그에 걸맞은 동작을 곁들여 말한다, \"이것이 조지프 배그스톡의 손이오. 댁이 그편이 더 좋으시다면, 수수한 늙은 조이 비의 손이오, 선생!"], ["That is the hand, of which His Royal Highness the late Duke of York, did me the honour to observe, Sir, to His Royal Highness the late Duke of Kent, that it was the hand of Josh: a rough and tough, and possibly an up-to-snuff, old vagabond.", "이것이 바로, 작고하신 요크 공작 전하께서 친히, 작고하신 켄트 공작 전하께 영광스럽게도 평하시기를, 이건 조시의 손이라, 거칠고 단단하며, 어쩌면 닳고 닳았을지도 모를 늙은 떠돌이의 손이라 하셨던 그 손이오."], ["Dombey, may the present moment be the least unhappy of our lives.", "돔비, 부디 지금 이 순간이 우리 생애에서 가장 덜 불행한 순간이기를."], ["God bless you!”", "신의 가호가 있기를!\""]], [["Now enters Mr Carker, gorgeous likewise, and smiling like a wedding-guest indeed.", "이제 카커 씨가 들어오는데, 그 역시 휘황찬란하게 차려입었고, 정말로 결혼식 하객답게 미소 짓고 있다."], ["He can scarcely let Mr Dombey’s hand go, he is so congratulatory; and he shakes the Major’s hand so heartily at the same time, that his voice shakes too, in accord with his arms, as it comes sliding from between his teeth.", "그는 돔비 씨의 손을 좀처럼 놓지 못할 만큼 축하의 말을 늘어놓는다. 그러면서 동시에 소령의 손을 어찌나 진심으로 흔들어 대는지, 그의 목소리마저 이빨 사이로 미끄러져 나올 때 그 두 팔에 발맞추어 떨려 나온다."]], [["“The very day is auspicious,”", "\"날부터가 상서롭구려,\""], ["says Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말한다."], ["“The brightest and most genial weather!", "\"더없이 화창하고 더없이 온화한 날씨라니!"], ["I hope I am not a moment late?”", "제가 조금도 늦지 않았기를 바랍니다만?\""]], [["“Punctual to your time, Sir,”", "\"시간을 꼭 맞추셨소이다, 선생,\""], ["says the Major.", "소령이 말한다."]], [["“I am rejoiced, I am sure,”", "\"정말 기쁩니다, 진심으로,\""], ["says Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말한다."], ["“I was afraid I might be a few seconds after the appointed time, for I was delayed by a procession of waggons; and I took the liberty of riding round to Brook Street”—this to Mr Dombey—“to leave a few poor rarities of flowers for Mrs Dombey.", "\"약속된 시각보다 몇 초쯤 늦지나 않을까 걱정했지요. 짐마차 행렬에 발이 묶이는 바람에요."], ["A man in my position, and so distinguished as to be invited here, is proud to offer some homage in acknowledgment of his vassalage: and as I have no doubt Mrs Dombey is overwhelmed with what is costly and magnificent;”", "그래서 외람되이 브룩 가로 돌아 말을 달려\"—이 말은 돔비 씨에게 하는 것이다—\"돔비 부인께 변변찮은 꽃 몇 송이를 두고 왔습니다. 저 같은 처지의 사람이, 이 자리에 초대받는 영광을 누릴 만큼 분에 넘치는 대접을 받았으니, 제 종속의 신분을 인정하는 뜻에서 무언가 경의를 표하는 것을 자랑으로 여기지요."], ["with a strange glance at his patron; “I hope the very poverty of my offering, may find favour for it.”", "그리고 돔비 부인께서야 의심할 바 없이 값지고 호화로운 것들에 파묻혀 계실 터이니\"—후원자를 향해 묘한 눈길을 던지며—\"제 보잘것없는 선물의 그 보잘것없음이 오히려 호의를 얻기를 바랄 따름입니다.\""]], [["“Mrs Dombey, that is to be,”", "\"머지않아 돔비 부인이 되실 분께서,\""], ["returns Mr Dombey, condescendingly, “will be very sensible of your attention, Carker, I am sure.”", "돔비 씨가 너그러이 응수한다, \"댁의 마음씀에 매우 감동하실 것이오, 카커, 틀림없이.\""]], [["“And if she is to be Mrs Dombey this morning, Sir,”", "\"그리고 만일 그분이 오늘 아침 돔비 부인이 되실 거라면, 선생,\""], ["says the Major, putting down his coffee-cup, and looking at his watch, “it’s high time we were off!”", "소령이 제 커피잔을 내려놓고 회중시계를 보며 말한다, \"이제 우리가 길을 나설 때가 다 됐소이다!\""]], [["Forth, in a barouche, ride Mr Dombey, Major Bagstock, and Mr Carker, to the church.", "사륜마차에 올라, 돔비 씨와 배그스톡 소령과 카커 씨가 교회로 마차를 달린다."], ["Mr Sownds the Beadle has long risen from the steps, and is in waiting with his cocked hat in his hand.", "교회지기 사운즈 씨는 진작 계단에서 일어나, 삼각모를 손에 든 채 대기하고 있다."], ["Mrs Miff curtseys and proposes chairs in the vestry.", "미프 부인이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 제의실에 의자를 권한다."], ["Mr Dombey prefers remaining in the church.", "돔비 씨는 교회 안에 그대로 머무는 편을 택한다."], ["As he looks up at the organ, Miss Tox in the gallery shrinks behind the fat leg of a cherubim on a monument, with cheeks like a young Wind.", "그가 오르간을 올려다보자, 회랑에 있던 톡스 양이 어린 바람 신처럼 부푼 뺨을 한, 기념비 위 천사상의 통통한 다리 뒤로 움츠러든다."], ["Captain Cuttle, on the contrary, stands up and waves his hook, in token of welcome and encouragement.", "그와 반대로 커틀 선장은 벌떡 일어나, 환영과 격려의 표시로 제 갈고리 손을 흔든다."], ["Mr Toots informs the Chicken, behind his hand, that the middle gentleman, he in the fawn-coloured pantaloons, is the father of his love.", "투츠 씨는 손으로 입을 가린 채, 가운데 신사, 곧 담갈색 바지를 입은 저분이 제가 연모하는 이의 아버지라고 치킨에게 일러 준다."], ["The Chicken hoarsely whispers Mr Toots that he’s as stiff a cove as ever he see, but that it is within the resources of Science to double him up, with one blow in the waistcoat.", "치킨은 쉰 목소리로 투츠 씨에게 속삭이기를, 저 양반은 여태껏 본 중에 가장 뻣뻣한 작자이긴 하나, 조끼에 한 방만 먹이면 저 양반을 둘로 접어 버리는 것쯤은 과학의 능력으로 충분하다고 한다."]], [["Mr Sownds and Mrs Miff are eyeing Mr Dombey from a little distance, when the noise of approaching wheels is heard, and Mr Sownds goes out.", "사운즈 씨와 미프 부인이 조금 떨어진 데서 돔비 씨를 눈여겨보고 있는데, 다가오는 마차 바퀴 소리가 들려오자, 사운즈 씨가 밖으로 나간다."], ["Mrs Miff, meeting Mr Dombey’s eye as it is withdrawn from the presumptuous maniac upstairs, who salutes him with so much urbanity, drops a curtsey, and informs him that she believes his “good lady” is come.", "위층에서 그토록 정중하게 인사를 건네는 그 주제넘은 미치광이에게서 거두어들인 돔비 씨의 눈길과 마주친 미프 부인은, 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 그분의 \"고운 마님\"이 오신 것 같다고 일러 준다."], ["Then there is a crowding and a whispering at the door, and the good lady enters, with a haughty step.", "그러자 문가에 사람들이 몰려들며 수군거리고, 그 고운 마님이 도도한 걸음으로 들어선다."]], [["There is no sign upon her face, of last night’s suffering; there is no trace in her manner, of the woman on the bended knees, reposing her wild head, in beautiful abandonment, upon the pillow of the sleeping girl.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 간밤의 고통의 흔적이 없다. 그 거동에는 잠든 처녀의 베개 위에 아름답게 몸을 내맡긴 채 흐트러진 머리를 누이고 무릎 꿇었던 그 여인의 자취가 조금도 없다."], ["That girl, all gentle and lovely, is at her side—a striking contrast to her own disdainful and defiant figure, standing there, composed, erect, inscrutable of will, resplendent and majestic in the zenith of its charms, yet beating down, and treading on, the admiration that it challenges.", "더없이 온순하고 사랑스러운 그 처녀가 그녀의 곁에 서 있다—거기 침착하게, 꼿꼿하게, 그 의지를 헤아릴 수 없이, 매력의 절정에서 찬란하고 위풍당당하게 서 있으면서도, 제가 불러일으키는 그 찬미를 짓밟고 깔아뭉개는, 그 경멸과 반항으로 가득 찬 자신의 자태와는 두드러진 대조를 이루며."]], [["There is a pause while Mr Sownds the Beadle glides into the vestry for the clergyman and clerk.", "교회지기 사운즈 씨가 성직자와 서기를 부르러 제의실로 미끄러져 들어간 사이, 잠시 멈춤이 있다."], ["At this juncture, Mrs Skewton speaks to Mr Dombey: more distinctly and emphatically than her custom is, and moving at the same time, close to Edith.", "이 대목에서, 스큐턴 부인이 돔비 씨에게 말을 건넨다. 평소의 버릇보다 더 또렷하고 더 힘주어 말하면서, 동시에 에디스 가까이로 다가선다."]], [["“My dear Dombey,”", "\"이보시오, 돔비,\""], ["said the good Mama, “I fear I must relinquish darling Florence after all, and suffer her to go home, as she herself proposed.", "그 자애로운 어머니가 말했다, \"내 아무래도 결국 사랑하는 플로렌스를 놓아주어, 그 애 스스로 청한 대로 집에 돌아가게 해야 할까 보오."], ["After my loss of today, my dear Dombey, I feel I shall not have spirits, even for her society.”", "오늘 이렇게 그 애를 잃고 나면, 이보시오 돔비, 그 애와 함께 지낼 기운조차 없을 것 같구려.\""]], [["“Had she not better stay with you?”", "\"그 애가 댁과 함께 있는 편이 낫지 않겠소?\""], ["returns the Bridegroom.", "신랑이 응수한다."]], [["“I think not, my dear Dombey.", "\"아닌 것 같소, 이보시오 돔비."], ["No, I think not.", "아니, 아닌 것 같소."], ["I shall be better alone.", "나 혼자 있는 편이 나을 게요."], ["Besides, my dearest Edith will be her natural and constant guardian when you return, and I had better not encroach upon her trust, perhaps.", "게다가 댁이 돌아오면 내 사랑하는 에디스가 그 애의 당연하고도 한결같은 보호자가 될 테니, 내가 괜히 그 애의 소임을 침범하지 않는 편이 나을 게요, 아마."], ["She might be jealous.", "그 애가 질투할지도 모르니."], ["Eh, dear Edith?”", "그렇잖니, 사랑하는 에디스?\""]], [["The affectionate Mama presses her daughter’s arm, as she says this; perhaps entreating her attention earnestly.", "그 애정 어린 어머니는 이렇게 말하며 딸의 팔을 꼭 누른다. 아마도 진지하게 그 주의를 끌려는 듯이."]], [["“To be serious, my dear Dombey,”", "\"진지하게 말하자면, 이보시오 돔비,\""], ["she resumes, “I will relinquish our dear child, and not inflict my gloom upon her.", "그녀가 다시 말을 잇는다, \"내 우리 사랑하는 아이를 놓아주어, 그 애에게 내 우울함을 안기지 않으리다."], ["We have settled that, just now.", "방금 우리끼리 그렇게 정했소."], ["She fully understands, dear Dombey.", "그 애도 잘 알고 있다오, 이보시오 돔비."], ["Edith, my dear,—she fully understands.”", "에디스, 얘야—그 애도 다 알고 있단다.\""]], [["Again, the good mother presses her daughter’s arm.", "또다시, 그 자애로운 어머니가 딸의 팔을 꼭 누른다. 돔비 씨는 더는 이의를 달지 않는다."], ["Mr Dombey offers no additional remonstrance; for the clergyman and clerk appear; and Mrs Miff, and Mr Sownds the Beadle, group the party in their proper places at the altar rails.", "성직자와 서기가 나타났기 때문이다. 그리고 미프 부인과 교회지기 사운즈 씨가 제단 난간 앞 제자리에 사람들을 늘어세운다."]], [["The sun is shining down, upon the golden letters of the ten commandments.", "햇살이 십계명의 금빛 글자 위로 내리쬔다."], ["Why does the Bride’s eye read them, one by one?", "어찌하여 신부의 눈은 그것을 하나하나 읽어 내려가는가?"], ["Which one of all the ten appears the plainest to her in the glare of light?", "그 열 가지 가운데 어느 것이 눈부신 빛 속에서 그녀에게 가장 또렷하게 떠오르는가?"], ["False Gods; murder; theft; the honour that she owes her mother;—which is it that appears to leave the wall, and printing itself in glowing letters, on her book!", "거짓 신들, 살인, 도둑질, 어머니께 마땅히 드려야 할 공경—그중 어느 것이 벽에서 떨어져 나와, 그녀의 책 위에 이글거리는 글자로 스스로를 새기는 듯한가!"]], [["“Who giveth this woman to be married to this man?”", "\"누가 이 여인을 이 남자에게 시집보내려 내어 주십니까?\""]], [["Cousin Feenix does that.", "사촌 피닉스가 그 일을 한다."], ["He has come from Baden-Baden on purpose.", "그는 일부러 바덴바덴에서 건너왔다."], ["“Confound it,”", "\"젠장,\""], ["Cousin Feenix says—good-natured creature, Cousin Feenix—“when we do get a rich City fellow into the family, let us show him some attention; let us do something for him.”", "사촌 피닉스가 말한다—사람 좋은 위인이지, 사촌 피닉스란—\"기왕에 돈 많은 도회지 양반을 집안에 들이게 되었으니, 그자에게 좀 정성을 보이자. 그자를 위해 뭐라도 해 주자.\""]], [["“I give this woman to be married to this man,”", "\"내가 이 여인을 이 남자에게 시집보내려 내어 줍니다.\""], ["saith Cousin Feenix therefore.", "그리하여 사촌 피닉스가 말한다."], ["Cousin Feenix, meaning to go in a straight line, but turning off sideways by reason of his wilful legs, gives the wrong woman to be married to this man, at first—to wit, a brides—maid of some condition, distantly connected with the family, and ten years Mrs Skewton’s junior —but Mrs Miff, interposing her mortified bonnet, dexterously turns him back, and runs him, as on castors, full at the “good lady:”", "사촌 피닉스는 곧장 직선으로 가려 했으나, 제멋대로 구는 두 다리 탓에 옆으로 비스듬히 빗나가는 바람에, 처음에는 엉뚱한 여인을 이 남자에게 시집보내려 내어 준다—다름 아니라, 집안과 멀찍이 친척뻘이고 스큐턴 부인보다 열 살은 손아래인, 제법 지체 있는 신부 들러리를 말이다—그러나 미프 부인이 풀 죽은 보닛을 들이밀며, 능숙하게 그를 도로 돌려세워, 마치 바퀴 위에 올려놓은 듯 \"그 고운 마님\" 쪽으로 똑바로 밀어붙인다."], ["whom Cousin Feenix giveth to married to this man accordingly.", "이에 사촌 피닉스는 그 마님을 이 남자에게 시집보내려 내어 준다."]], [["And will they in the sight of heaven—?", "그리고 두 사람은 하늘이 보시는 앞에서—?"]], [["Ay, that they will: Mr Dombey says he will.", "암, 그러고말고. 돔비 씨가 그러하겠노라 말한다."], ["And what says Edith?", "그러면 에디스는 무어라 하는가?"], ["She will.", "그러하겠노라 한다."]], [["So, from that day forward, for better for worse, for richer for poorer, in sickness and in health, to love and to cherish, till death do them part, they plight their troth to one another, and are married.", "그리하여, 그날 이후로, 좋을 때나 나쁠 때나, 부유할 때나 가난할 때나, 아플 때나 건강할 때나, 죽음이 두 사람을 갈라놓을 때까지, 서로 사랑하고 아끼겠노라, 두 사람은 서로에게 백년가약을 맺고, 부부가 된다."]], [["In a firm, free hand, the Bride subscribes her name in the register, when they adjourn to the vestry.", "제의실로 자리를 옮기자, 신부는 굳건하고 거침없는 필치로 결혼 등록부에 제 이름을 적어 넣는다."], ["“There ain’t a many ladies come here,”", "\"여기 오시는 마님네가 그리 많지는 않지요,\""], ["Mrs Miff says with a curtsey—to look at Mrs Miff, at such a season, is to make her mortified bonnet go down with a dip—“writes their names like this good lady!”", "미프 부인이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 말한다—이런 자리에서 미프 부인을 본다는 것은 그녀의 풀 죽은 보닛이 까딱 숙여지는 것을 본다는 뜻이다—\"이 고운 마님처럼 이렇게 이름을 쓰시는 분은요!\""], ["Mr Sownds the Beadle thinks it is a truly spanking signature, and worthy of the writer—this, however, between himself and conscience.", "교회지기 사운즈 씨는 그것이 참으로 끝내주는 서명이며, 쓴 이에게 걸맞다고 여긴다—허나 이는 그저 자기 양심에게나 하는 말이다."]], [["Florence signs too, but unapplauded, for her hand shakes.", "플로렌스도 서명하지만, 갈채는 없다. 그녀의 손이 떨리기 때문이다."], ["All the party sign; Cousin Feenix last; who puts his noble name into a wrong place, and enrols himself as having been born that morning.", "일행이 모두 서명한다. 마지막으로 사촌 피닉스가 하는데, 그는 자신의 고귀한 이름을 엉뚱한 칸에 적어 넣어, 마치 자기가 그날 아침에 태어난 것으로 등록해 버린다."]], [["The Major now salutes the Bride right gallantly, and carries out that branch of military tactics in reference to all the ladies: notwithstanding Mrs Skewton’s being extremely hard to kiss, and squeaking shrilly in the sacred edifice.", "이제 소령이 자못 늠름하게 신부에게 인사를 건네고, 그 군사 전술의 한 갈래를 모든 부인들에게도 적용한다. 스큐턴 부인은 입 맞추기가 여간 까다롭지 않은 데다, 그 성스러운 건물 안에서 날카롭게 꽥꽥 소리를 질러 대는데도 말이다."], ["The example is followed by Cousin Feenix and even by Mr Dombey.", "그 본보기를 사촌 피닉스가, 심지어 돔비 씨마저 뒤따른다."], ["Lastly, Mr Carker, with his white teeth glistening, approaches Edith, more as if he meant to bite her, than to taste the sweets that linger on her lips.", "마지막으로 카커 씨가, 하얀 이를 번득이며 에디스에게 다가가는데, 그 입술에 어린 단맛을 맛보려는 것이라기보다는, 차라리 그녀를 물어뜯으려는 것 같다."]], [["There is a glow upon her proud cheek, and a flashing in her eyes, that may be meant to stay him; but it does not, for he salutes her as the rest have done, and wishes her all happiness.", "그녀의 도도한 뺨에는 홍조가 어리고, 두 눈에는 그를 멈춰 세우려는 듯한 번득임이 인다. 그러나 그것은 그를 멈추지 못한다. 그가 다른 이들처럼 그녀에게 인사하며 온갖 행복을 빌어 주기 때문이다."]], [["“If wishes,”", "\"빈다는 것이,\""], ["says he in a low voice, “are not superfluous, applied to such a union.”", "그가 낮은 목소리로 말한다, \"이런 결합에 갖다 붙이기에 군더더기가 아니라면 말입니다만.\""]], [["“I thank you, Sir,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선생,\""], ["she answers, with a curled lip, and a heaving bosom.", "그녀가 입술을 비틀고 가슴을 들썩이며 대답한다."]], [["But, does Edith feel still, as on the night when she knew that Mr Dombey would return to offer his alliance, that Carker knows her thoroughly, and reads her right, and that she is more degraded by his knowledge of her, than by aught else?", "그러나 에디스는, 돔비 씨가 청혼하러 돌아오리라는 것을 알았던 그 밤처럼 여전히 느끼는 것일까? 카커가 자기를 속속들이 알고 있고, 자기를 정확히 꿰뚫어 보며, 그가 자기를 안다는 사실로 인해 다른 그 무엇보다도 더 자기가 비천해진다고?"], ["Is it for this reason that her haughtiness shrinks beneath his smile, like snow within the hands that grasps it firmly, and that her imperious glance droops in meeting his, and seeks the ground?", "그를 안다는 사실 때문에 그녀의 도도함이 그의 미소 앞에서, 단단히 움켜쥔 손안의 눈처럼 움츠러들고, 그녀의 거만한 눈길이 그의 눈길과 마주치자 풀이 죽어 땅바닥을 찾는 것은, 바로 그 까닭인가?"]], [["“I am proud to see,”", "\"제 자랑스럽기 그지없습니다,\""], ["said Mr Carker, with a servile stooping of his neck, which the revelations making by his eyes and teeth proclaim to be a lie, “I am proud to see that my humble offering is graced by Mrs Dombey’s hand, and permitted to hold so favoured a place in so joyful an occasion.”", "카커 씨가 비굴하게 목을 굽히며 말했는데, 그 눈과 이가 드러내는 바는 그것이 거짓임을 천명하고 있다, \"제 변변찮은 선물이 돔비 부인의 손에 들려 빛을 입고, 이토록 기쁜 자리에서 이토록 영광스러운 자리를 차지하도록 허락받았으니 자랑스럽기 그지없습니다.\""]], [["Though she bends her head, in answer, there is something in the momentary action of her hand, as if she would crush the flowers it holds, and fling them, with contempt, upon the ground.", "비록 그녀가 답례로 고개를 숙이긴 하나, 그 손이 순간적으로 움직이는 모양에는, 마치 그 들고 있는 꽃을 짓이겨 경멸하듯 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐 버리려는 듯한 무언가가 있다."], ["But, she puts the hand through the arm of her new husband, who has been standing near, conversing with the Major, and is proud again, and motionless, and silent.", "그러나 그녀는 그 손을, 가까이 서서 소령과 이야기를 나누고 있던 새 남편의 팔에 끼고는, 다시금 도도해져, 미동도 없이, 말없이 서 있다."]], [["The carriages are once more at the church door.", "마차들이 다시 한번 교회 문 앞에 와 있다."], ["Mr Dombey, with his bride upon his arm, conducts her through the twenty families of little women who are on the steps, and every one of whom remembers the fashion and the colour of her every article of dress from that moment, and reproduces it on her doll, who is for ever being married.", "돔비 씨가 신부를 팔에 끼고서, 계단 위에 늘어선 자그마한 여자아이들의 스무 가족 사이로 그녀를 이끌고 지나간다. 그 아이들 하나하나가 그 순간부터 그녀의 옷가지란 옷가지의 모양새와 색깔을 죄다 기억해 두었다가, 영영 시집만 가게 될 제 인형에게 고스란히 본떠 입힌다."], ["Cleopatra and Cousin Feenix enter the same carriage.", "클레오파트라와 사촌 피닉스가 같은 마차에 오른다."], ["The Major hands into a second carriage, Florence, and the bridesmaid who so narrowly escaped being given away by mistake, and then enters it himself, and is followed by Mr Carker.", "소령은 두 번째 마차에 플로렌스를, 그리고 하마터면 실수로 시집보내질 뻔했던 그 신부 들러리를 태워 주고는, 자기도 그 마차에 오르고, 카커 씨가 그 뒤를 따른다."], ["Horses prance and caper; coachmen and footmen shine in fluttering favours, flowers, and new-made liveries.", "말들이 껑충거리고 뛰논다. 마부와 시종들이 펄럭이는 장식 리본과 꽃과 새로 지은 제복으로 번쩍거린다."], ["Away they dash and rattle through the streets; and as they pass along, a thousand heads are turned to look at them, and a thousand sober moralists revenge themselves for not being married too, that morning, by reflecting that these people little think such happiness can’t last.", "그들은 거리를 가로질러 휙휙 내달리며 덜커덩거린다. 그들이 지나갈 때, 천 개의 머리가 그들을 보려 돌아가고, 천 명의 점잖은 도덕가들이, 자기네는 그날 아침 결혼하지 못한 것에 대한 앙갚음으로, 저 사람들은 이런 행복이 오래갈 수 없다는 걸 거의 생각지도 못하리라며 곱씹는다."]], [["Miss Tox emerges from behind the cherubim’s leg, when all is quiet, and comes slowly down from the gallery.", "모든 것이 잠잠해지자, 톡스 양은 천사상의 다리 뒤에서 모습을 드러내, 회랑에서 천천히 내려온다."], ["Miss Tox’s eyes are red, and her pocket-handkerchief is damp.", "톡스 양의 두 눈은 붉게 충혈되었고, 손수건은 축축하다."], ["She is wounded, but not exasperated, and she hopes they may be happy.", "그녀는 상처 입었으나 분개하지는 않았으며, 부디 그들이 행복하기를 바란다."], ["She quite admits to herself the beauty of the bride, and her own comparatively feeble and faded attractions; but the stately image of Mr Dombey in his lilac waistcoat, and his fawn-coloured pantaloons, is present to her mind, and Miss Tox weeps afresh, behind her veil, on her way home to Princess’s Place.", "그녀는 신부의 아름다움을, 그리고 그에 비하면 보잘것없이 빛바랜 자신의 매력을 속으로 순순히 인정한다. 그러나 연보라색 조끼에 담갈색 바지를 입은 돔비 씨의 위풍당당한 모습이 마음속에 떠오르니, 톡스 양은 프린세스 플레이스의 집으로 돌아가는 길에 베일 뒤에서 또다시 운다."], ["Captain Cuttle, having joined in all the amens and responses, with a devout growl, feels much improved by his religious exercises; and in a peaceful frame of mind pervades the body of the church, glazed hat in hand, and reads the tablet to the memory of little Paul.", "커틀 선장은, 경건한 으르렁거림으로 온갖 아멘과 응답을 함께 외쳤는지라, 제 신앙적 수련으로 한결 나아진 기분이다. 그리하여 평화로운 마음 상태로, 번들거리는 모자를 손에 든 채 교회의 본당을 거닐며, 어린 폴을 기리는 명판을 읽는다."], ["The gallant Mr Toots, attended by the faithful Chicken, leaves the building in torments of love.", "그 늠름한 투츠 씨는, 충직한 치킨을 거느린 채, 사랑의 고뇌에 시달리며 건물을 떠난다. 치킨은 아직 플로렌스를 손에 넣을 계략을 짜내지 못했으나, 처음에 떠올린 생각이 그를 사로잡고 있어, 그는 돔비 씨를 둘로 접어 버리는 것이 옳은 방향으로 나아가는 한 수가 되리라고 여긴다."], ["The Chicken is as yet unable to elaborate a scheme for winning Florence, but his first idea has gained possession of him, and he thinks the doubling up of Mr Dombey would be a move in the right direction.", "돔비 씨의 하인들이 숨었던 곳에서 나와 브룩 가로 내달릴 채비를 하는데, 퍼치 부인이 몸이 좋지 않은 기색을 보이는 바람에 발이 묶인다. 그녀는 물 한 잔을 청하며 사람들을 놀라게 한다."], ["Mr Dombey’s servants come out of their hiding-places, and prepare to rush to Brook Street, when they are delayed by symptoms of indisposition on the part of Mrs Perch, who entreats a glass of water, and becomes alarming; Mrs Perch gets better soon, however, and is borne away; and Mrs Miff, and Mr Sownds the Beadle, sit upon the steps to count what they have gained by the affair, and talk it over, while the sexton tolls a funeral.", "그러나 퍼치 부인은 곧 나아져서 부축을 받아 떠나간다. 그리고 미프 부인과 교회지기 사운즈 씨는 계단에 앉아, 이 일로 얼마나 벌었는지 셈하며 그 일을 두고 이야기를 나누고, 그동안 묘지기는 장례를 알리는 조종을 울린다."]], [["Now, the carriages arrive at the Bride’s residence, and the players on the bells begin to jingle, and the band strikes up, and Mr Punch, that model of connubial bliss, salutes his wife.", "이제, 마차들이 신부의 거처에 당도하고, 종을 울리는 사람들이 딸랑딸랑 종을 울리기 시작하며, 악대가 연주를 시작하고, 부부 행복의 본보기인 펀치 씨가 제 아내에게 인사한다."], ["Now, the people run, and push, and press round in a gaping throng, while Mr Dombey, leading Mrs Dombey by the hand, advances solemnly into the Feenix Halls.", "이제, 사람들이 입을 떡 벌린 무리를 이루어 우르르 몰려와 밀고 떠밀며 둘러싸는 가운데, 돔비 씨가 돔비 부인의 손을 잡아 이끌며 엄숙하게 피닉스 저택으로 들어선다."], ["Now, the rest of the wedding party alight, and enter after them.", "이제, 나머지 결혼식 일행이 마차에서 내려, 그들을 뒤따라 들어간다."], ["And why does Mr Carker, passing through the people to the hall-door, think of the old woman who called to him in the Grove that morning?", "그런데 어찌하여 카커 씨는 사람들 사이를 헤치고 현관문 쪽으로 가면서, 그날 아침 그로브에서 자기를 불러 세웠던 그 노파를 떠올리는 걸까?"], ["Or why does Florence, as she passes, think, with a tremble, of her childhood, when she was lost, and of the visage of Good Mrs Brown?", "또 어찌하여 플로렌스는 지나가면서, 길을 잃었던 어린 시절을, 그리고 착한 브라운 부인의 얼굴을 부르르 떨며 떠올리는 걸까?"]], [["Now, there are more congratulations on this happiest of days, and more company, though not much; and now they leave the drawing-room, and range themselves at table in the dark-brown dining-room, which no confectioner can brighten up, let him garnish the exhausted negroes with as many flowers and love-knots as he will.", "이제, 이 더없이 행복한 날에 더 많은 축하의 말이 오가고, 그리 많지는 않으나 더 많은 손님들이 모인다. 그리고 이제 그들은 응접실을 나와, 짙은 갈색의 식당에 차려진 식탁에 늘어앉는다. 그 식당은 제과사가 그 시들어 버린 무어인 조각상들을 제아무리 많은 꽃과 사랑의 매듭으로 치장한들, 도무지 밝게 꾸밀 도리가 없는 곳이다."]], [["The pastry-cook has done his duty like a man, though, and a rich breakfast is set forth.", "하기야 제과사는 사내답게 제 소임을 다했고, 푸짐한 아침 만찬이 차려져 있다."], ["Mr and Mrs Chick have joined the party, among others.", "칙 씨 부부도 여러 손님들 틈에 끼어 합류했다."], ["Mrs Chick admires that Edith should be, by nature, such a perfect Dombey; and is affable and confidential to Mrs Skewton, whose mind is relieved of a great load, and who takes her share of the champagne.", "칙 부인은 에디스가 천성적으로 그토록 완벽한 돔비 사람이라는 것에 감탄해 마지않으며, 큰 짐을 덜어 낸 듯한 스큐턴 부인에게 다정하고도 은근하게 군다. 그 스큐턴 부인은 샴페인을 제 몫만큼 든다."], ["The very tall young man who suffered from excitement early, is better; but a vague sentiment of repentance has seized upon him, and he hates the other very tall young man, and wrests dishes from him by violence, and takes a grim delight in disobliging the company.", "아까 일찌감치 흥분으로 고생하던 그 키 큰 젊은이는 좀 나아졌다. 그러나 어렴풋한 후회의 감정에 사로잡혀, 그는 다른 키 큰 젊은이를 미워하며, 그에게서 접시들을 우격다짐으로 빼앗고, 손님들의 비위를 거스르는 데서 음울한 즐거움을 느낀다."], ["The company are cool and calm, and do not outrage the black hatchments of pictures looking down upon them, by any excess of mirth.", "손님들은 차분하고 침착하여, 그들을 내려다보고 있는 그림들의 검은 문장(紋章)들을, 지나친 흥취로 모욕하는 법이 없다."], ["Cousin Feenix and the Major are the gayest there; but Mr Carker has a smile for the whole table.", "사촌 피닉스와 소령이 거기서 가장 흥겨운 이들이다. 그러나 카커 씨는 식탁 전체를 향한 미소를 머금고 있다."], ["He has an especial smile for the Bride, who very, very seldom meets it.", "그는 신부를 위한 각별한 미소를 지니고 있는데, 신부는 그 미소를 정말이지 좀처럼 마주하는 법이 없다."]], [["Cousin Feenix rises, when the company have breakfasted, and the servants have left the room; and wonderfully young he looks, with his white wristbands almost covering his hands (otherwise rather bony), and the bloom of the champagne in his cheeks.", "손님들이 아침 만찬을 마치고 하인들이 방을 나가자, 사촌 피닉스가 일어선다. 하얀 소맷동이 그의 손을(그러잖아도 다소 앙상한 손이지만) 거의 다 덮을 만큼 길고, 두 뺨에 샴페인의 발그레함이 피어올라, 그는 놀랍도록 젊어 보인다."]], [["“Upon my honour,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix, “although it’s an unusual sort of thing in a private gentleman’s house, I must beg leave to call upon you to drink what is usually called a—in fact a toast.”", "사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"비록 이게 한 개인 신사의 댁에서는 흔치 않은 일이긴 하나, 외람되이 여러분께 청하여, 흔히 말하는 그—사실은 건배를 들어 주십사 부탁드려야겠소이다.\""]], [["The Major very hoarsely indicates his approval.", "소령이 자못 쉰 목소리로 그 찬동을 나타낸다."], ["Mr Carker, bending his head forward over the table in the direction of Cousin Feenix, smiles and nods a great many times.", "카커 씨는 사촌 피닉스 쪽으로 식탁 위에 고개를 앞으로 숙이며, 미소 지으며 여러 번 고개를 끄덕인다."]], [["“A—in fact it’s not a—”", "\"그—사실 이게 무슨—\""], ["Cousin Feenix beginning again, thus, comes to a dead stop.", "사촌 피닉스가 이렇게 다시 시작하더니, 딱 멈추어 버린다."]], [["“Hear, hear!”", "\"옳소, 옳소!\""], ["says the Major, in a tone of conviction.", "소령이 확신에 찬 어조로 말한다."]], [["Mr Carker softly claps his hands, and bending forward over the table again, smiles and nods a great many more times than before, as if he were particularly struck by this last observation, and desired personally to express his sense of the good it has done.", "카커 씨는 부드럽게 손뼉을 치고는, 다시 식탁 위로 고개를 앞으로 숙이며, 마치 이 마지막 말에 각별히 감동받기라도 한 듯, 그리고 그것이 가져다준 좋은 효과에 대한 제 느낌을 몸소 표하고 싶기라도 한 듯, 아까보다 훨씬 더 여러 번 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕인다."]], [["“It is,”", "\"이는,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix, “an occasion in fact, when the general usages of life may be a little departed from, without impropriety; and although I never was an orator in my life, and when I was in the House of Commons, and had the honour of seconding the address, was—in fact, was laid up for a fortnight with the consciousness of failure—”", "사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"사실 인생의 일반적인 관례를, 무례를 범하지 않고 조금 벗어나도 좋은 그런 자리외다. 그리고 비록 내 평생 한 번도 연설가였던 적이 없고, 하원에 있을 적에, 답사를 재청하는 영광을 누렸을 적에도, 사실은—실패했다는 의식에 사로잡혀 보름이나 드러누워 있었습니다만—\""]], [["The Major and Mr Carker are so much delighted by this fragment of personal history, that Cousin Feenix laughs, and addressing them individually, goes on to say:", "소령과 카커 씨가 이 개인적 내력의 한 토막에 어찌나 즐거워하던지, 사촌 피닉스가 웃음을 터뜨리며, 두 사람을 각각 돌아보며 말을 이어 간다."]], [["“And in point of fact, when I was devilish ill—still, you know, I feel that a duty devolves upon me.", "\"그리고 사실, 내가 지독히 앓아누웠을 적에—그래도, 아시다시피, 나는 내게 어떤 의무가 지워져 있음을 느낍니다."], ["And when a duty devolves upon an Englishman, he is bound to get out of it, in my opinion, in the best way he can.", "그리고 한 영국인에게 의무가 지워질 적에는, 내 생각에는, 그가 할 수 있는 한 최선의 방도로 그것을 벗어나야 마땅하지요."], ["Well!", "자!"], ["our family has had the gratification, today, of connecting itself, in the person of my lovely and accomplished relative, whom I now see—in point of fact, present—”", "우리 집안은 오늘, 내가 지금 보고 있는—사실, 자리에 와 계신—내 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 친척의 모습으로, 그분과 인연을 맺는 기쁨을 누렸소이다—\""]], [["Here there is general applause.", "여기서 두루 박수갈채가 인다."]], [["“Present,”", "\"와 계신,\""], ["repeats Cousin Feenix, feeling that it is a neat point which will bear repetition,—“with one who—that is to say, with a man, at whom the finger of scorn can never—in fact, with my honourable friend Dombey, if he will allow me to call him so.”", "사촌 피닉스는, 그것이 거듭 말할 만한 산뜻한 표현이라 느끼며 되뇐다—\"그분과, 한 사람과—다시 말해, 한 사내와 인연을 맺었으니, 그 사내에게는 경멸의 손가락이 결코—사실은, 내 영예로운 벗 돔비, 그를 그렇게 부르도록 허락하신다면 말이오만, 그와 인연을 맺은 것이외다.\""]], [["Cousin Feenix bows to Mr Dombey; Mr Dombey solemnly returns the bow; everybody is more or less gratified and affected by this extraordinary, and perhaps unprecedented, appeal to the feelings.", "사촌 피닉스가 돔비 씨에게 절을 한다. 돔비 씨는 엄숙하게 그 절에 답례한다. 모두가, 감정에 호소하는 이 비범한, 어쩌면 전례 없는 이 인사말에 다소간 흐뭇해하며 감동을 받는다."]], [["“I have not,”", "\"나는,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix, “enjoyed those opportunities which I could have desired, of cultivating the acquaintance of my friend Dombey, and studying those qualities which do equal honour to his head, and, in point of fact, to his heart; for it has been my misfortune to be, as we used to say in my time in the House of Commons, when it was not the custom to allude to the Lords, and when the order of parliamentary proceedings was perhaps better observed than it is now—to be in—in point of fact,”", "사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"내 벗 돔비와 교분을 쌓고, 그의 머리에—그리고 사실은, 그의 가슴에 똑같이 영예를 안기는 그 자질들을 익히 살필, 그토록 바라 마지않던 기회를 누리지는 못했소이다. 내가, 우리가 하원에 있던 시절에 곧잘 하던 말마따나—상원을 들먹이는 것이 관례가 아니던 시절, 그리고 의회 절차의 질서가 어쩌면 지금보다 더 잘 지켜지던 시절에 하던 말마따나—내가 불운하게도—사실은,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix, cherishing his joke, with great slyness, and finally bringing it out with a jerk, ‘“in another place!’”", "사촌 피닉스가 자못 능청맞게 제 농담을 매만지다가, 마침내 불쑥 내뱉으며 말한다, \"'다른 곳에' 있었던 까닭이외다!\""]], [["The Major falls into convulsions, and is recovered with difficulty.", "소령이 발작적으로 웃음을 터뜨리다가, 가까스로 정신을 추스른다."]], [["“But I know sufficient of my friend Dombey,”", "\"하나 나는 내 벗 돔비에 대해 충분히 알고 있어서,\""], ["resumes Cousin Feenix in a graver tone, as if he had suddenly become a sadder and wiser man, “to know that he is, in point of fact, what may be emphatically called a—a merchant—a British merchant—and a—and a man.", "사촌 피닉스가, 마치 별안간 더 슬프고 더 지혜로운 사람이 되기라도 한 듯, 한층 진지한 어조로 다시 말을 잇는다, \"그가, 사실은, 힘주어 일컬어 마땅한 바—한 상인이며—한 영국 상인이며—그리고 한 사내라는 것을 알고 있소이다."], ["And although I have been resident abroad, for some years (it would give me great pleasure to receive my friend Dombey, and everybody here, at Baden-Baden, and to have an opportunity of making ’em known to the Grand Duke), still I know enough, I flatter myself, of my lovely and accomplished relative, to know that she possesses every requisite to make a man happy, and that her marriage with my friend Dombey is one of inclination and affection on both sides.”", "그리고 비록 내가 몇 해 동안 외국에 머물러 살았다고는 하나(내 벗 돔비를, 그리고 여기 계신 모든 분을 바덴바덴에서 맞이하여, 그분들을 대공께 소개해 드릴 기회를 누린다면 더없는 기쁨이겠소이다만), 그래도 나는, 외람되이 자부하건대, 내 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 친척에 대해 충분히 알고 있어서, 그분이 한 사내를 행복하게 해 줄 온갖 자질을 두루 갖추었다는 것, 그리고 그분과 내 벗 돔비의 혼인이 양쪽 모두의 끌림과 애정에서 비롯된 것임을 알고 있소이다.\""]], [["Many smiles and nods from Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 여러 번 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕인다."]], [["“Therefore,”", "\"하여,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix, “I congratulate the family of which I am a member, on the acquisition of my friend Dombey.", "사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"나는 내가 그 일원으로 속한 이 집안이 내 벗 돔비를 얻은 것을 경하하는 바이외다."], ["I congratulate my friend Dombey on his union with my lovely and accomplished relative who possesses every requisite to make a man happy; and I take the liberty of calling on you all, in point of fact, to congratulate both my friend Dombey and my lovely and accomplished relative, on the present occasion.”", "나는 내 벗 돔비가, 한 사내를 행복하게 해 줄 온갖 자질을 두루 갖춘 내 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 친척과 맺어진 것을 경하하는 바이외다. 그리고 외람되이 여러분 모두에게 청하노니, 사실, 이 자리에서 내 벗 돔비와 내 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 친척, 두 분 모두를 경하해 주시기를 바라는 바이외다.\""]], [["The speech of Cousin Feenix is received with great applause, and Mr Dombey returns thanks on behalf of himself and Mrs Dombey. J.", "사촌 피닉스의 인사말이 큰 갈채를 받고, 돔비 씨가 자기 자신과 돔비 부인을 대신하여 사례한다."], ["B. shortly afterwards proposes Mrs Skewton.", "제이 비가 곧이어 스큐턴 부인을 위한 건배를 제의한다."], ["The breakfast languishes when that is done, the violated hatchments are avenged, and Edith rises to assume her travelling dress.", "그 일이 끝나자 아침 만찬은 시들해지고, 모욕당했던 문장들은 앙갚음을 받으며, 에디스가 여행복으로 갈아입으려 일어선다."]], [["All the servants in the meantime, have been breakfasting below.", "그러는 사이, 모든 하인들은 아래층에서 아침을 먹고 있었다."], ["Champagne has grown too common among them to be mentioned, and roast fowls, raised pies, and lobster-salad, have become mere drugs.", "그들 사이에서 샴페인은 입에 올리기에는 너무 흔한 것이 되어 버렸고, 통닭구이며, 켜를 올린 파이며, 바닷가재 샐러드도 한낱 흔해 빠진 것이 되어 버렸다."], ["The very tall young man has recovered his spirits, and again alludes to the exciseman.", "아까 일찍 고생하던 그 키 큰 젊은이는 기운을 되찾아, 또다시 주세관 이야기를 들먹인다."], ["His comrade’s eye begins to emulate his own, and he, too, stares at objects without taking cognizance thereof.", "그의 동료의 눈도 그를 본떠 가기 시작하여, 그 역시 사물을 보면서도 그것을 알아보지 못한 채 멍하니 바라본다."], ["There is a general redness in the faces of the ladies; in the face of Mrs Perch particularly, who is joyous and beaming, and lifted so far above the cares of life, that if she were asked just now to direct a wayfarer to Ball’s Pond, where her own cares lodge, she would have some difficulty in recalling the way.", "부인들의 얼굴에는 두루 붉은 기운이 돈다. 특히 퍼치 부인의 얼굴이 그러한데, 그녀는 기쁨에 들떠 환히 빛나며, 인생의 근심걱정에서 한참 위로 들려 올라가, 만일 지금 누가 그녀에게 제 근심걱정이 깃들어 있는 볼스 폰드로 가는 길을 나그네에게 일러 주라고 청한다면, 그 길을 떠올리는 데 적잖이 애를 먹을 정도다."], ["Mr Towlinson has proposed the happy pair; to which the silver-headed butler has responded neatly, and with emotion; for he half begins to think he is an old retainer of the family, and that he is bound to be affected by these changes.", "타울린슨 씨가 행복한 부부를 위한 건배를 제의했고, 이에 은발의 집사가 깔끔하게, 그리고 감격에 겨워 답했다. 그가 어렴풋이나마 자신이 이 집안의 오랜 가신이라 여기기 시작했고, 이런 변화에 마땅히 감격해야 한다고 여기기 시작했기 때문이다."], ["The whole party, and especially the ladies, are very frolicsome.", "일행 전체가, 특히 부인들이 자못 들떠 있다."], ["Mr Dombey’s cook, who generally takes the lead in society, has said, it is impossible to settle down after this, and why not go, in a party, to the play?", "사교계에서 으레 앞장서곤 하는 돔비 씨의 요리사가, 이러고 나서 가만히 주저앉아 있을 수는 없으니, 다 함께 패를 지어 연극 구경이라도 가는 게 어떻겠느냐고 말했다."], ["Everybody (Mrs Perch included) has agreed to this; even the Native, who is tigerish in his drink, and who alarms the ladies (Mrs Perch particularly) by the rolling of his eyes.", "모두가(퍼치 부인을 포함하여) 이에 동의했다. 술에 취하면 호랑이처럼 사나워져, 부인들을(특히 퍼치 부인을) 두 눈을 부라려 놀라게 하는 토박이 하인마저도 그러했다."], ["One of the very tall young men has even proposed a ball after the play, and it presents itself to no one (Mrs Perch included) in the light of an impossibility.", "키 큰 젊은이 가운데 하나는 심지어 연극이 끝난 뒤 무도회를 열자고까지 제안했는데, 그것을 (퍼치 부인을 포함하여) 누구도 불가능한 일로 여기지 않았다. 하녀와 타울린슨 씨 사이에 말다툼이 일었다."], ["Words have arisen between the housemaid and Mr Towlinson; she, on the authority of an old saw, asserting marriages to be made in Heaven: he, affecting to trace the manufacture elsewhere; he, supposing that she says so, because she thinks of being married her own self: she, saying, Lord forbid, at any rate, that she should ever marry him.", "그녀는 어느 옛 격언을 근거로 혼인은 하늘이 맺어 주는 것이라 주장했고, 그는 그것이 다른 곳에서 만들어지는 것이라 우기는 척했으며, 그녀가 그렇게 말하는 것은 제 자신이 시집갈 생각을 하기 때문이라고 넘겨짚었다. 그녀는 어쨌든, 자기가 그와 결혼하는 일만은 하느님께서 부디 막아 주시기를 바란다고 말했다."], ["To calm these flying taunts, the silver-headed butler rises to propose the health of Mr Towlinson, whom to know is to esteem, and to esteem is to wish well settled in life with the object of his choice, wherever (here the silver-headed butler eyes the housemaid) she may be.", "이 어지러이 오가는 비아냥을 가라앉히고자, 은발의 집사가 일어서서 타울린슨 씨의 건강을 위한 건배를 제의하니, 그를 아는 것은 곧 그를 존경하는 것이요, 그를 존경하는 것은 곧 그가, 그가 택한 사람과 함께—그 사람이 어디에 있든(여기서 은발의 집사가 하녀를 흘끔 본다)—인생에 잘 자리 잡기를 바라는 것이라 하였다."], ["Mr Towlinson returns thanks in a speech replete with feeling, of which the peroration turns on foreigners, regarding whom he says they may find favour, sometimes, with weak and inconstant intellects that can be led away by hair, but all he hopes, is, he may never hear of no foreigner never boning nothing out of no travelling chariot.", "타울린슨 씨는 감정이 듬뿍 담긴 답사로 사례하는데, 그 결말은 외국인들에 관한 것으로 흘러갔다. 그가 말하기를, 그자들이 때로는, 머리카락에 홀려 넘어가 버리는 줏대 없고 변덕스러운 지각머리에게는 환심을 살지 모르나, 바라는 바는, 어떤 외국인이 어떤 여행용 마차에서 무엇이든 슬쩍 훔쳐 냈다는 소리를 결코 듣지 않는 것이라 하였다."], ["The eye of Mr Towlinson is so severe and so expressive here, that the housemaid is turning hysterical, when she and all the rest, roused by the intelligence that the Bride is going away, hurry upstairs to witness her departure.", "여기서 타울린슨 씨의 눈이 어찌나 매섭고 또 의미심장하던지, 하녀가 막 신경질을 부리려는 참인데, 신부가 떠난다는 소식에 그녀를 비롯한 다른 모두가 정신이 번쩍 들어, 그 출발을 지켜보려 부랴부랴 위층으로 올라간다."]], [["The chariot is at the door; the Bride is descending to the hall, where Mr Dombey waits for her.", "마차가 문 앞에 와 있다. 신부가 현관으로 내려오는데, 거기서 돔비 씨가 그녀를 기다리고 있다."], ["Florence is ready on the staircase to depart too; and Miss Nipper, who has held a middle state between the parlour and the kitchen, is prepared to accompany her.", "플로렌스도 떠날 채비를 하고 계단에 서 있고, 응접실과 부엌 사이의 중간 위치를 지켜 온 니퍼 양이 그녀를 따라나설 채비를 하고 있다."], ["As Edith appears, Florence hastens towards her, to bid her farewell.", "에디스가 모습을 드러내자, 플로렌스가 작별 인사를 하려고 그녀에게로 서둘러 다가간다."]], [["Is Edith cold, that she should tremble!", "에디스가 차갑기에 저리 떠는 것일까!"], ["Is there anything unnatural or unwholesome in the touch of Florence, that the beautiful form recedes and contracts, as if it could not bear it!", "플로렌스의 손길에 무언가 부자연스럽거나 해로운 것이라도 있어, 저 아름다운 자태가 견딜 수 없다는 듯 뒷걸음치며 움츠러드는 것일까!"], ["Is there so much hurry in this going away, that Edith, with a wave of her hand, sweeps on, and is gone!", "이 떠남이 그토록 황급한 것이기에, 에디스가 손짓 한 번으로 휙 지나쳐 가 버리고 마는 것일까!"]], [["Mrs Skewton, overpowered by her feelings as a mother, sinks on her sofa in the Cleopatra attitude, when the clatter of the chariot wheels is lost, and sheds several tears.", "마차 바퀴 소리가 멀어져 사라지자, 어머니로서의 감정에 북받친 스큐턴 부인은 클레오파트라 자세로 소파에 쓰러져 눈물을 몇 방울 흘린다."], ["The Major, coming with the rest of the company from table, endeavours to comfort her; but she will not be comforted on any terms, and so the Major takes his leave.", "소령이 나머지 손님들과 함께 식탁에서 와서 그녀를 달래 보려 애쓰지만, 그녀는 무슨 수를 써도 좀처럼 달래지지 않으니, 그래서 소령은 작별을 고한다."], ["Cousin Feenix takes his leave, and Mr Carker takes his leave.", "사촌 피닉스도 작별을 고하고, 카커 씨도 작별을 고한다."], ["The guests all go away.", "손님들이 모두 떠나간다."], ["Cleopatra, left alone, feels a little giddy from her strong emotion, and falls asleep.", "홀로 남겨진 클레오파트라는, 격한 감정에 다소 어질어질해져, 잠이 든다."]], [["Giddiness prevails below stairs too.", "아래층에서도 어질어질함이 만연하다."], ["The very tall young man whose excitement came on so soon, appears to have his head glued to the table in the pantry, and cannot be detached from it.", "그토록 일찍 흥분이 도졌던 그 키 큰 젊은이는, 식기실 식탁에 머리를 풀로 붙여 놓은 듯하여, 도무지 거기서 떼어 낼 수가 없다."], ["A violent revulsion has taken place in the spirits of Mrs Perch, who is low on account of Mr Perch, and tells cook that she fears he is not so much attached to his home, as he used to be, when they were only nine in family.", "퍼치 부인의 기분에는 격렬한 반전이 일어났는데, 그녀는 퍼치 씨 일로 풀이 죽어, 요리사에게 말하기를, 식구가 아홉뿐이던 시절만큼 그이가 이제는 제 집에 정을 붙이지 않는 것 같아 걱정이라고 한다."], ["Mr Towlinson has a singing in his ears and a large wheel going round and round inside his head.", "타울린슨 씨는 귓속에서 노랫소리가 울리고 머릿속에서 커다란 바퀴 하나가 빙글빙글 돌아간다."], ["The housemaid wishes it wasn’t wicked to wish that one was dead.", "하녀는 누군가가 죽었으면 좋겠다고 바라는 것이 죄가 아니었으면 한다."]], [["There is a general delusion likewise, in these lower regions, on the subject of time; everybody conceiving that it ought to be, at the earliest, ten o’clock at night, whereas it is not yet three in the afternoon.", "이 아래쪽 영역에서는 시간을 두고도 마찬가지로 두루 착각이 일고 있으니, 다들 적어도 밤 열 시는 되었으리라 여기지만, 아직 오후 세 시도 채 되지 않았다."], ["A shadowy idea of wickedness committed, haunts every individual in the party; and each one secretly thinks the other a companion in guilt, whom it would be agreeable to avoid.", "무언가 못된 짓을 저질렀다는 어렴풋한 생각이 그 일행 한 사람 한 사람을 따라다니고, 저마다 속으로는 상대방을, 가능하면 피하는 편이 좋을 죄의 동무라 여긴다."], ["No man or woman has the hardihood to hint at the projected visit to the play.", "그 누구도 연극 구경을 가기로 한 계획을 입에 올릴 만한 뱃심이 없다."], ["Anyone reviving the notion of the ball, would be scouted as a malignant idiot.", "누구든 무도회 이야기를 다시 꺼냈다가는 악의에 찬 멍청이라고 손가락질을 받았을 것이다."]], [["Mrs Skewton sleeps upstairs, two hours afterwards, and naps are not yet over in the kitchen.", "두 시간 뒤, 스큐턴 부인은 위층에서 잠들어 있고, 부엌에서도 낮잠은 아직 끝나지 않았다."], ["The hatchments in the dining-room look down on crumbs, dirty plates, spillings of wine, half-thawed ice, stale discoloured heel-taps, scraps of lobster, drumsticks of fowls, and pensive jellies, gradually resolving themselves into a lukewarm gummy soup.", "식당의 문장들이, 부스러기며, 더러운 접시며, 흘린 포도주며, 반쯤 녹은 얼음이며, 변색되어 김빠진 술 찌꺼기며, 바닷가재 부스러기며, 닭 다리며, 그리고 점차 미적지근하고 끈적한 수프로 변해 가는 침울한 젤리들을 내려다본다."], ["The marriage is, by this time, almost as denuded of its show and garnish as the breakfast.", "결혼식은 이제 아침 만찬이 그러하듯, 그 화려함과 장식을 거의 다 벗어 버렸다."], ["Mr Dombey’s servants moralise so much about it, and are so repentant over their early tea, at home, that by eight o’clock or so, they settle down into confirmed seriousness; and Mr Perch, arriving at that time from the City, fresh and jocular, with a white waistcoat and a comic song, ready to spend the evening, and prepared for any amount of dissipation, is amazed to find himself coldly received, and Mrs Perch but poorly, and to have the pleasing duty of escorting that lady home by the next omnibus.", "돔비 씨의 하인들은 그것을 두고 어찌나 도덕을 따져 대는지, 그리고 집에서 이른 저녁 차를 들며 어찌나 뉘우치는지, 여덟 시쯤 되자 굳어진 진지함에 빠져들고 만다. 그리고 그때쯤, 흰 조끼를 입고 익살스러운 노래를 흥얼대며, 도회지에서 산뜻하고 쾌활하게 도착한 퍼치 씨가, 저녁 한때를 보낼 채비로 어떤 흥청거림에도 응할 만반의 준비를 갖추고 왔다가, 자기가 냉랭한 대접을 받는 것을, 그리고 퍼치 부인이 영 시원찮은 것을 알고는 어리둥절해하며, 다음 합승마차 편으로 그 부인을 집까지 바래다주어야 하는 즐거운 소임을 떠맡게 된다."]], [["Night closes in.", "밤이 깃든다."], ["Florence, having rambled through the handsome house, from room to room, seeks her own chamber, where the care of Edith has surrounded her with luxuries and comforts; and divesting herself of her handsome dress, puts on her old simple mourning for dear Paul, and sits down to read, with Diogenes winking and blinking on the ground beside her.", "플로렌스는 그 으리으리한 저택을 이 방 저 방 거닐다가, 자기 방을 찾는데, 그 방은 에디스의 보살핌으로 온갖 호사와 안락에 둘러싸여 있다. 그녀는 그 고운 옷을 벗고, 사랑하는 폴을 위한 낡고 수수한 상복으로 갈아입고는, 디오게네스가 곁의 바닥에서 눈을 끔벅이며 깜박이는 가운데, 책을 읽으려 자리에 앉는다."], ["But Florence cannot read tonight.", "그러나 플로렌스는 오늘 밤 책을 읽지 못한다."], ["The house seems strange and new, and there are loud echoes in it.", "집은 낯설고 새롭게만 보이고, 그 안에는 요란한 메아리가 울린다."], ["There is a shadow on her heart: she knows not why or what: but it is heavy.", "그녀의 가슴에는 그림자가 드리워져 있다. 왜인지 무엇 때문인지는 알지 못하나, 그것은 무겁기만 하다."], ["Florence shuts her book, and gruff Diogenes, who takes that for a signal, puts his paws upon her lap, and rubs his ears against her caressing hands.", "플로렌스가 책을 덮자, 무뚝뚝한 디오게네스가 그것을 신호로 알아채고는, 그녀의 무릎에 앞발을 올려놓고, 어루만져 주는 그녀의 손에 제 귀를 비벼 댄다."], ["But Florence cannot see him plainly, in a little time, for there is a mist between her eyes and him, and her dead brother and dead mother shine in it like angels.", "그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 플로렌스는 그 개를 또렷이 볼 수가 없으니, 그녀의 두 눈과 그 사이에 안개가 끼어, 그 안개 속에서 죽은 오라비와 죽은 어머니가 천사처럼 빛나기 때문이다."], ["Walter, too, poor wandering shipwrecked boy, oh, where is he?", "월터도, 그 가엾고 떠돌다 난파한 소년도, 아, 그는 어디에 있는가?"]], [["The Major don’t know; that’s for certain; and don’t care.", "소령은 모른다. 그건 분명하다."], ["The Major, having choked and slumbered, all the afternoon, has taken a late dinner at his club, and now sits over his pint of wine, driving a modest young man, with a fresh-coloured face, at the next table (who would give a handsome sum to be able to rise and go away, but cannot do it) to the verge of madness, by anecdotes of Bagstock, Sir, at Dombey’s wedding, and Old Joe’s devilish gentle manly friend, Lord Feenix.", "그리고 관심도 없다. 소령은 오후 내내 숨이 막히고 졸기를 거듭하다가, 클럽에서 늦은 저녁을 들었다."], ["While Cousin Feenix, who ought to be at Long’s, and in bed, finds himself, instead, at a gaming-table, where his wilful legs have taken him, perhaps, in his own despite.", "그리고 이제 포도주 한 파인트를 앞에 놓고 앉아, 옆 식탁에 있는, 혈색 좋은 얼굴을 한 점잖은 젊은이(일어나 자리를 뜰 수만 있다면 상당한 액수라도 내놓고 싶지만 그러지 못하는)를, 돔비의 결혼식에서의 배그스톡, 선생, 이야기며, 늙은 조의 그 지독히도 점잖은 벗 피닉스 경 이야기로, 미칠 지경까지 몰아붙이고 있다. 한편 마땅히 롱스 호텔에서 잠자리에 들어 있어야 할 사촌 피닉스는, 그러기는커녕, 어쩌면 제 본의와는 어긋나게, 제멋대로 구는 두 다리가 그를 데려간 도박장에 가 있다."]], [["Night, like a giant, fills the church, from pavement to roof, and holds dominion through the silent hours.", "밤이, 거인처럼, 교회를 포석에서 지붕까지 가득 채우고, 고요한 시간 내내 그 위에 군림한다."], ["Pale dawn again comes peeping through the windows: and, giving place to day, sees night withdraw into the vaults, and follows it, and drives it out, and hides among the dead.", "창백한 새벽이 다시금 창문 너머로 살그머니 들여다본다. 그리고 낮에게 자리를 내주며, 밤이 납골당으로 물러나는 것을 보고, 그것을 뒤따라가 몰아내고는, 죽은 자들 틈에 숨는다."], ["The timid mice again cower close together, when the great door clashes, and Mr Sownds and Mrs Miff treading the circle of their daily lives, unbroken as a marriage ring, come in.", "커다란 문이 쾅 하고 부딪치고, 결혼반지처럼 끊임없이 이어지는 제 일상의 굴레를 밟아 나가는 사운즈 씨와 미프 부인이 들어오자, 겁 많은 쥐들이 또다시 한데 바싹 웅크린다."], ["Again, the cocked hat and the mortified bonnet stand in the background at the marriage hour; and again this man taketh this woman, and this woman taketh this man, on the solemn terms:", "또다시, 삼각모와 풀 죽은 보닛이 결혼식 시각에 뒤편에 서 있다. 그리고 또다시 이 남자가 이 여인을 맞이하고, 이 여인이 이 남자를 맞이하니, 그 엄숙한 언약은 이러하다."]], [["“To have and to hold, from this day forward, for better for worse, for richer for poorer, in sickness and in health, to love and to cherish, until death do them part.”", "\"오늘 이후로, 좋을 때나 나쁠 때나, 부유할 때나 가난할 때나, 아플 때나 건강할 때나, 죽음이 두 사람을 갈라놓을 때까지, 서로 맞이하여 지키며, 사랑하고 아끼겠노라.\""]], [["The very words that Mr Carker rides into town repeating, with his mouth stretched to the utmost, as he picks his dainty way.", "그것은 바로 카커 씨가, 입을 한껏 벌린 채, 깔끔하게 길을 골라 가며 말을 달려 도회지로 들어가면서 되뇌는 바로 그 말이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXII. The Wooden Midshipman goes to Pieces", "# CHAPTER XXXII. The Wooden Midshipman goes to Pieces"]], [["Honest Captain Cuttle, as the weeks flew over him in his fortified retreat, by no means abated any of his prudent provisions against surprise, because of the non-appearance of the enemy.", "나무 항해사가 산산조각 나다\n정직한 커틀 선장은 자기 요새화된 은신처에서 몇 주가 휙휙 흘러가는 동안에도, 적이 나타나지 않는다고 해서 기습에 대비한 신중한 방비를 조금도 늦추지 않았다. 선장은 지금 자기가 누리는 안전이 너무도 깊고 신기해서 오래 갈 리가 없다고 따져 보았다."], ["The Captain argued that his present security was too profound and wonderful to endure much longer; he knew that when the wind stood in a fair quarter, the weathercock was seldom nailed there; and he was too well acquainted with the determined and dauntless character of Mrs MacStinger, to doubt that that heroic woman had devoted herself to the task of his discovery and capture.", "바람이 순풍 쪽에 서 있을 때라도 풍향계가 거기에 못 박혀 있는 일은 드물다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 또 맥스팅거 부인의 단호하고 두려움 없는 성격을 너무도 잘 알고 있었기에, 그 영웅적인 여인이 자기를 찾아내어 사로잡는 과업에 몸을 바쳤으리라는 것을 의심하지 않았다."], ["Trembling beneath the weight of these reasons, Captain Cuttle lived a very close and retired life; seldom stirring abroad until after dark; venturing even then only into the obscurest streets; never going forth at all on Sundays; and both within and without the walls of his retreat, avoiding bonnets, as if they were worn by raging lions.", "이런 이유들의 무게에 짓눌려 부들부들 떨면서, 커틀 선장은 매우 폐쇄적이고 은둔적인 생활을 했다. 어두워진 다음에야 좀처럼 바깥출입을 하지 않았고, 그때에도 가장 으슥한 거리로만 나섰으며, 일요일에는 아예 한 발짝도 나가지 않았고, 은신처 담장 안에서나 밖에서나 보닛이라면 마치 그것을 성난 사자가 쓰고 있기라도 한 듯 피해 다녔다."]], [["The Captain never dreamed that in the event of his being pounced upon by Mrs MacStinger, in his walks, it would be possible to offer resistance.", "선장은 산책길에 맥스팅거 부인에게 덮쳐졌을 경우 저항을 시도한다는 것은 가능하지도 않은 일이라고 꿈에도 생각지 않았다."], ["He felt that it could not be done.", "그는 그런 짓은 할 수 없는 일이라고 느꼈다."], ["He saw himself, in his mind’s eye, put meekly in a hackney-coach, and carried off to his old lodgings.", "마음의 눈으로, 그는 자기가 고분고분하게 삯마차에 태워져 옛 하숙집으로 끌려가는 광경을 보았다."], ["He foresaw that, once immured there, he was a lost man: his hat gone; Mrs MacStinger watchful of him day and night; reproaches heaped upon his head, before the infant family; himself the guilty object of suspicion and distrust; an ogre in the children’s eyes, and in their mother’s a detected traitor.", "일단 그곳에 갇히고 나면 자기는 끝장난 사람임을 예견했다. 모자는 빼앗기고, 맥스팅거 부인이 밤낮으로 자기를 감시하며, 어린 자식들 앞에서 비난이 자기 머리 위로 쏟아지고, 자기는 의심과 불신의 죄인이 되며, 아이들 눈에는 도깨비요, 그 어미 눈에는 발각된 배신자가 될 터였다."]], [["A violent perspiration, and a lowness of spirits, always came over the Captain as this gloomy picture presented itself to his imagination.", "이 음울한 그림이 그의 상상 속에 떠오를 때면 언제나 격렬한 식은땀과 의기소침이 선장을 덮쳤다."], ["It generally did so previous to his stealing out of doors at night for air and exercise.", "그것은 대개 밤에 바람과 운동을 위해 슬그머니 문밖으로 빠져나가기 직전에 그랬다."], ["Sensible of the risk he ran, the Captain took leave of Rob, at those times, with the solemnity which became a man who might never return: exhorting him, in the event of his (the Captain’s) being lost sight of, for a time, to tread in the paths of virtue, and keep the brazen instruments well polished.", "자기가 무릅쓰는 위험을 의식하면서, 선장은 그럴 때마다 다시는 돌아오지 못할지도 모르는 사람에게 어울리는 엄숙함으로 롭에게 작별을 고했다. 만일 자기(즉 선장)가 한동안 보이지 않게 되거든, 미덕의 길을 걷고 놋쇠 기구들을 잘 닦아 두라고 신신당부하면서."]], [["But not to throw away a chance; and to secure to himself a means, in case of the worst, of holding communication with the external world; Captain Cuttle soon conceived the happy idea of teaching Rob the Grinder some secret signal, by which that adherent might make his presence and fidelity known to his commander, in the hour of adversity.", "그러나 기회를 헛되이 놓치지 않기 위해, 그리고 최악의 경우에 대비하여 바깥세상과 연락을 주고받을 수단을 확보해 두기 위해, 커틀 선장은 곧 그라인더 롭에게 어떤 비밀 신호를 가르친다는 좋은 생각을 떠올렸다. 그 추종자가 역경의 시각에 자기 상관에게 자기 존재와 충성을 알릴 수 있는 신호 말이다."], ["After much cogitation, the Captain decided in favour of instructing him to whistle the marine melody, “Oh cheerily, cheerily!”", "한참 궁리한 끝에, 선장은 \"오 즐겁게, 즐겁게!\"라는 뱃노래를 휘파람으로 부는 법을 가르치기로 결정했다."], ["and Rob the Grinder attaining a point as near perfection in that accomplishment as a landsman could hope to reach, the Captain impressed these mysterious instructions on his mind:", "그리하여 그라인더 롭이 뭍사람으로서 바랄 수 있는 한 거의 완벽에 가까운 경지까지 그 재주에 이르자, 선장은 이 신비로운 지시를 그의 머릿속에 새겨 넣었다."]], [["“Now, my lad, stand by!", "\"자, 얘야, 대기하고 있어라!"], ["If ever I’m took—”", "혹시라도 내가 잡히면—\""]], [["“Took, Captain!”", "\"잡히다니요, 선장님!\""], ["interposed Rob, with his round eyes wide open.", "롭이 동그란 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 끼어들었다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Captain Cuttle darkly, “if ever I goes away, meaning to come back to supper, and don’t come within hail again, twenty-four hours arter my loss, go you to Brig Place and whistle that “ere tune near my old moorings—not as if you was a meaning of it, you understand, but as if you’d drifted there, promiscuous.", "커틀 선장이 음울하게 말했다. \"혹시라도 내가 저녁을 먹으러 돌아올 작정으로 나갔다가, 사라진 지 스물네 시간이 지나도록 다시 부르는 소리가 들리는 거리 안으로 돌아오지 않거든, 너는 브리그 플레이스로 가서 내 옛 정박지 근처에서 그 곡조를 휘파람으로 불어라—작정하고 그러는 것처럼 말고, 알겠느냐, 그저 우연히 거기로 흘러든 것처럼 말이다."], ["If I answer in that tune, you sheer off, my lad, and come back four-and-twenty hours arterwards; if I answer in another tune, do you stand off and on, and wait till I throw out further signals.", "내가 그 곡조로 답하거든, 너는 멀리 떠나거라, 얘야, 그리고 스물네 시간 후에 다시 돌아오너라. 내가 다른 곡조로 답하거든, 멀어졌다 다가왔다 하면서 내가 다음 신호를 내보낼 때까지 기다려라."], ["Do you understand them orders, now?”", "자, 이 명령들을 알아들었느냐?\""]], [["“What am I to stand off and on of, Captain?”", "\"제가 뭘 멀어졌다 다가왔다 하라는 건가요, 선장님?\""], ["inquired Rob.", "롭이 물었다."], ["“The horse-road?”", "\"찻길을요?\""]], [["“Here’s a smart lad for you!”", "\"여기 똘똘한 녀석 한번 보게나!\""], ["cried the Captain eyeing him sternly, “as don’t know his own native alphabet!", "선장이 그를 엄하게 쏘아보며 외쳤다. \"제 모국어 알파벳도 모르다니!"], ["Go away a bit and come back again alternate—d’ye understand that?”", "좀 멀어졌다가 다시 돌아왔다가 번갈아 하라는 말이다—그건 알아듣겠느냐?\""]], [["“Yes, Captain,”", "\"네, 선장님,\""], ["said Rob.", "롭이 말했다."]], [["“Very good my lad, then,”", "\"아주 좋다 그럼, 얘야,\""], ["said the Captain, relenting.", "선장이 누그러지며 말했다."], ["“Do it!”", "\"그렇게 하거라!\""]], [["That he might do it the better, Captain Cuttle sometimes condescended, of an evening after the shop was shut, to rehearse this scene: retiring into the parlour for the purpose, as into the lodgings of a supposititious MacStinger, and carefully observing the behaviour of his ally, from the hole of espial he had cut in the wall.", "롭이 그것을 더 잘 해낼 수 있도록, 커틀 선장은 가게 문을 닫은 어느 저녁이면 가끔 이 장면을 예행연습하는 일까지 마다하지 않았다. 그러기 위해 거실로 물러나—마치 가상의 맥스팅거 하숙집으로 들어가듯이—벽에 뚫어 둔 정탐 구멍으로 자기 동맹자의 거동을 주의 깊게 관찰했다."], ["Rob the Grinder discharged himself of his duty with so much exactness and judgment, when thus put to the proof, that the Captain presented him, at divers times, with seven sixpences, in token of satisfaction; and gradually felt stealing over his spirit the resignation of a man who had made provision for the worst, and taken every reasonable precaution against an unrelenting fate.", "이렇게 시험에 부쳐졌을 때 그라인더 롭이 어찌나 정확하고 분별 있게 자기 임무를 수행해 내던지, 선장은 만족의 표시로 그에게 여러 번에 걸쳐 도합 6펜스짜리 일곱 닢을 주었다. 그러면서 선장은 차츰, 최악에 대비하고 가차 없는 운명에 맞서 합당한 예방책을 다 강구해 둔 사람의 체념이 자기 마음속에 슬며시 깃드는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Nevertheless, the Captain did not tempt ill-fortune, by being a whit more venturesome than before.", "그렇긴 해도 선장은 전보다 조금이라도 더 무모하게 굴어서 불운을 자초하는 짓은 하지 않았다."], ["Though he considered it a point of good breeding in himself, as a general friend of the family, to attend Mr Dombey’s wedding (of which he had heard from Mr Perch), and to show that gentleman a pleasant and approving countenance from the gallery, he had repaired to the church in a hackney cabriolet with both windows up; and might have scrupled even to make that venture, in his dread of Mrs MacStinger, but that the lady’s attendance on the ministry of the Reverend Melchisedech rendered it peculiarly unlikely that she would be found in communion with the Establishment.", "비록 그는 집안의 전반적인 벗으로서 돔비 씨의 결혼식에 (그 소식은 퍼치 씨에게서 들었다) 참석하여 회랑에서 그 신사에게 흐뭇하고 흡족해하는 표정을 보여 주는 것이 자기 예의범절상 옳은 일이라고 여겼지만, 양쪽 창문을 다 올린 삯마차를 타고 교회로 갔다. 게다가 맥스팅거 부인에 대한 공포 때문에 그 모험조차 망설였을 법했으나, 그 여인이 멜기세덱 목사의 설교에 다니는 처지였기에 그녀가 국교회 예배에 있을 가능성은 유난히 희박하여, 그나마 마음을 놓을 수 있었다."]], [["The Captain got safe home again, and fell into the ordinary routine of his new life, without encountering any more direct alarm from the enemy, than was suggested to him by the daily bonnets in the street.", "선장은 다시 무사히 집으로 돌아왔고, 거리에서 날마다 보이는 보닛이 일러 주는 것 외에는 적으로부터 더 직접적인 경보를 마주치는 일 없이 새 생활의 평범한 일상에 빠져들었다."], ["But other subjects began to lay heavy on the Captain’s mind.", "그러나 다른 문제들이 선장의 마음을 무겁게 짓누르기 시작했다."], ["Walter’s ship was still unheard of.", "월터의 배는 여전히 소식이 없었다."], ["No news came of old Sol Gills.", "늙은 솔 길스에게서도 아무런 기별이 오지 않았다."], ["Florence did not even know of the old man’s disappearance, and Captain Cuttle had not the heart to tell her.", "플로렌스는 그 노인이 사라진 것조차 알지 못했고, 커틀 선장은 차마 그녀에게 말할 마음이 나지 않았다."], ["Indeed the Captain, as his own hopes of the generous, handsome, gallant-hearted youth, whom he had loved, according to his rough manner, from a child, began to fade, and faded more and more from day to day, shrunk with instinctive pain from the thought of exchanging a word with Florence.", "사실 선장은, 자기 식의 투박한 방식으로 어린 시절부터 사랑해 온 그 너그럽고 잘생기고 용감한 마음을 지닌 청년에 대한 자기 희망이 시들기 시작하여, 날이 갈수록 점점 더 시들어 가자, 본능적인 아픔에 움찔하며 플로렌스와 한마디 나눈다는 생각조차 멀리했다."], ["If he had had good news to carry to her, the honest Captain would have braved the newly decorated house and splendid furniture—though these, connected with the lady he had seen at church, were awful to him—and made his way into her presence.", "만일 그녀에게 전할 좋은 소식이 있었더라면, 정직한 선장은 새로 단장한 집과 호화로운 가구들을—교회에서 본 그 부인과 결부되어 그것들이 그에게는 무시무시한 것이었지만—무릅쓰고서라도 그녀 앞으로 나아갔을 것이다."], ["With a dark horizon gathering around their common hopes, however, that darkened every hour, the Captain almost felt as if he were a new misfortune and affliction to her; and was scarcely less afraid of a visit from Florence, than from Mrs MacStinger herself.", "그러나 그들 공통의 희망 둘레로 어두운 지평선이 몰려들어 시시각각 더 어두워지고 있었기에, 선장은 자기가 그녀에게 새로운 불행이요 고통이기라도 한 양 느껴졌고, 맥스팅거 부인이 찾아오는 것 못지않게 플로렌스가 찾아오는 것을 두려워했다."]], [["It was a chill dark autumn evening, and Captain Cuttle had ordered a fire to be kindled in the little back parlour, now more than ever like the cabin of a ship.", "쌀쌀하고 어두운 가을 저녁이었고, 커틀 선장은 이제 그 어느 때보다도 배의 선실 같아진 자그마한 뒤채 거실에 불을 지피라고 일러 두었다. 비가 세차게 쏟아지고 바람이 거세게 불었다."], ["The rain fell fast, and the wind blew hard; and straying out on the house-top by that stormy bedroom of his old friend, to take an observation of the weather, the Captain’s heart died within him, when he saw how wild and desolate it was.", "옛 친구의 그 폭풍우 몰아치는 침실 곁 지붕 위로 나가 날씨를 살피던 선장은, 그것이 얼마나 거칠고 황량한지를 보고 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다. 그 시각의 날씨를 가엾은 월터의 운명과 연관 지었다거나, 만일 섭리가 그를 실종되고 난파될 운명에 처하게 했다면 그 일은 이미 오래전에 끝났으리라는 것을 의심해서가 아니었다."], ["Not that he associated the weather of that time with poor Walter’s destiny, or doubted that if Providence had doomed him to be lost and shipwrecked, it was over, long ago; but that beneath an outward influence, quite distinct from the subject-matter of his thoughts, the Captain’s spirits sank, and his hopes turned pale, as those of wiser men had often done before him, and will often do again.", "다만 자기 생각의 주제와는 전혀 별개인 어떤 외부의 영향 아래, 선장의 기운은 가라앉았고 그의 희망은 창백해졌다. 그보다 현명한 사람들도 이전에 흔히 그러했고, 앞으로도 흔히 그럴 것처럼."]], [["Captain Cuttle, addressing his face to the sharp wind and slanting rain, looked up at the heavy scud that was flying fast over the wilderness of house-tops, and looked for something cheery there in vain.", "커틀 선장은 매서운 바람과 비스듬히 들이치는 비에 얼굴을 향한 채, 지붕들의 황야 위로 빠르게 날아가는 묵직한 비구름을 올려다보며 거기서 무언가 기운 나는 것을 찾았지만 헛수고였다."], ["The prospect near at hand was no better.", "가까이 있는 광경도 더 나을 것이 없었다."], ["In sundry tea-chests and other rough boxes at his feet, the pigeons of Rob the Grinder were cooing like so many dismal breezes getting up.", "발치에 놓인 여러 차 상자며 거친 궤짝들 속에서 그라인더 롭의 비둘기들이 마치 음산한 미풍이 일어나는 것처럼 구구거리고 있었다."], ["A crazy weathercock of a midshipman, with a telescope at his eye, once visible from the street, but long bricked out, creaked and complained upon his rusty pivot as the shrill blast spun him round and round, and sported with him cruelly.", "망원경을 눈에 댄 채, 한때는 거리에서도 보였으나 오래전에 벽돌로 막혀 버린 풍향계 모양의 낡아빠진 항해사가, 날카로운 돌풍이 그를 빙글빙글 돌리며 잔인하게 가지고 노는 통에 녹슨 축 위에서 삐걱대며 신음했다."], ["Upon the Captain’s coarse blue vest the cold raindrops started like steel beads; and he could hardly maintain himself aslant against the stiff Nor’-Wester that came pressing against him, importunate to topple him over the parapet, and throw him on the pavement below.", "선장의 거친 푸른 조끼 위로 차가운 빗방울들이 강철 구슬처럼 튀어 올랐다. 그는 자기를 난간 너머로 넘어뜨려 아래 포도로 내동댕이치려고 끈질기게 밀어붙이는 뻣뻣한 북서풍에 맞서 몸을 비스듬히 가누기조차 힘들었다."], ["If there were any Hope alive that evening, the Captain thought, as he held his hat on, it certainly kept house, and wasn’t out of doors; so the Captain, shaking his head in a despondent manner, went in to look for it.", "만일 그날 저녁 살아 있는 희망이라는 것이 어디 있다면, 모자를 누르며 선장은 생각했다, 그것은 분명 집 안에 틀어박혀 있을 뿐 밖에 나와 있지는 않으리라고. 그래서 선장은 풀 죽은 모습으로 고개를 가로저으며 그것을 찾으러 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["Captain Cuttle descended slowly to the little back parlour, and, seated in his accustomed chair, looked for it in the fire; but it was not there, though the fire was bright.", "커틀 선장은 천천히 자그마한 뒤채 거실로 내려갔고, 늘 앉던 의자에 앉아 난롯불 속에서 그것을 찾았다. 그러나 불은 환히 타올랐어도 거기에는 그것이 없었다."], ["He took out his tobacco-box and pipe, and composing himself to smoke, looked for it in the red glow from the bowl, and in the wreaths of vapour that curled upward from his lips; but there was not so much as an atom of the rust of Hope’s anchor in either.", "담배 상자와 파이프를 꺼내 차분히 담배를 피울 채비를 하고는, 대통의 붉은 불빛 속에서, 그리고 입술에서 위로 굽이쳐 오르는 연기 자락 속에서 그것을 찾았다. 그러나 둘 중 어느 쪽에도 희망의 닻에 슨 녹조차 한 점 없었다."], ["He tried a glass of grog; but melancholy truth was at the bottom of that well, and he couldn’t finish it.", "그로그 한 잔을 들이켜 보았지만 그 우물 밑바닥에는 우울한 진실이 깔려 있어서 끝내 다 마시지 못했다."], ["He made a turn or two in the shop, and looked for Hope among the instruments; but they obstinately worked out reckonings for the missing ship, in spite of any opposition he could offer, that ended at the bottom of the lone sea.", "가게 안을 한두 바퀴 돌면서 기구들 사이에서 희망을 찾았다. 그러나 그것들은 그가 아무리 반대해 보아도 막무가내로 사라진 배에 대한 계산을 풀어 나갔고, 그 계산은 외로운 바다 밑바닥에서 끝나고 말았다."]], [["The wind still rushing, and the rain still pattering, against the closed shutters, the Captain brought to before the wooden Midshipman upon the counter, and thought, as he dried the little officer’s uniform with his sleeve, how many years the Midshipman had seen, during which few changes—hardly any—had transpired among his ship’s company; how the changes had come all together, one day, as it might be; and of what a sweeping kind they were.", "닫힌 덧문에 여전히 바람이 몰아치고 비가 후두두 두드리는 가운데, 선장은 계산대 위의 나무 항해사 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["Here was the little society of the back parlour broken up, and scattered far and wide.", "그러고는 소맷자락으로 그 자그마한 장교의 제복을 닦아 주면서 생각했다."], ["Here was no audience for Lovely Peg, even if there had been anybody to sing it, which there was not; for the Captain was as morally certain that nobody but he could execute that ballad, as he was that he had not the spirit, under existing circumstances, to attempt it.", "이 항해사가 그 오랜 세월을 지켜보는 동안 그의 선원들 사이에는 변화가—거의 아무런 변화도—일어나지 않았건만, 그 변화가 어느 날 한꺼번에 닥쳐왔으며, 더구나 얼마나 송두리째 쓸어 가는 변화였는지를. 여기 뒤채 거실의 그 자그마한 모임이 깨어져 사방으로 흩어져 버렸다."], ["There was no bright face of “Wal”r”", "여기 \"사랑스러운 페그\"를 들어 줄 청중이 없었다. 설령 그것을 불러 줄 사람이 있다 한들—그런 사람도 없었지만."], ["in the house;—here the Captain transferred his sleeve for a moment from the Midshipman’s uniform to his own cheek;—the familiar wig and buttons of Sol Gills were a vision of the past; Richard Whittington was knocked on the head; and every plan and project in connexion with the Midshipman, lay drifting, without mast or rudder, on the waste of waters.", "선장 자신밖에는 아무도 그 노래를 불러낼 수 없다고 그는 도덕적으로 확신하다시피 했는데, 그것은 지금 같은 처지에서는 자기에게 그것을 시도할 기운이 없다는 것을 확신하는 것과 마찬가지였다. 집 안에는 \"월\"의 환한 얼굴이 없었다—여기서 선장은 잠시 소맷자락을 항해사의 제복에서 자기 뺨으로 옮겼다—솔 길스의 낯익은 가발과 단추들은 과거의 환영이었고, 리처드 휘팅턴은 머리를 얻어맞아 쓰러졌으며, 항해사와 관련된 온갖 계획과 구상은 돛대도 키도 없이 물의 황야 위를 떠다니고 있었다."]], [["As the Captain, with a dejected face, stood revolving these thoughts, and polishing the Midshipman, partly in the tenderness of old acquaintance, and partly in the absence of his mind, a knocking at the shop-door communicated a frightful start to the frame of Rob the Grinder, seated on the counter, whose large eyes had been intently fixed on the Captain’s face, and who had been debating within himself, for the five hundredth time, whether the Captain could have done a murder, that he had such an evil conscience, and was always running away.", "* 리처드 휘팅턴: 가난뱅이에서 런던 시장이 된 입신출세의 전설적 인물로, 월터의 출세에 빗대 자주 거론되던 비유. 선장이 풀 죽은 얼굴로 이런 생각들을 곱씹으며, 한편으로는 오랜 사귐의 정으로, 또 한편으로는 정신이 딴 데 가 있어서, 항해사를 닦고 서 있을 때, 가게 문을 두드리는 소리가 계산대 위에 앉아 있던 그라인더 롭의 몸뚱이에 무시무시한 깜짝 놀람을 안겨 주었다. 그의 커다란 눈은 줄곧 선장의 얼굴에 골똘히 박혀 있었고, 그는 속으로, 오백 번째쯤, 선장이 무슨 살인이라도 저질렀기에 저토록 양심에 가책을 느끼며 늘 도망치려 하는 것은 아닐까 따져 보던 참이었다."]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"저게 뭐냐?\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, softly.", "커틀 선장이 나직이 말했다."]], [["“Somebody’s knuckles, Captain,”", "\"누가 주먹으로 두드린 겁니다, 선장님,\""], ["answered Rob the Grinder.", "그라인더 롭이 대답했다."]], [["The Captain, with an abashed and guilty air, immediately walked on tiptoe to the little parlour and locked himself in.", "선장은 무안하고 죄지은 듯한 기색으로 즉시 발끝으로 살금살금 자그마한 거실로 걸어가 안에 틀어박혔다. 롭은 문을 열면서, 만일 방문객이 여자 모습으로 왔더라면 문지방에서 담판을 지으려 했을 것이다."], ["Rob, opening the door, would have parleyed with the visitor on the threshold if the visitor had come in female guise; but the figure being of the male sex, and Rob’s orders only applying to women, Rob held the door open and allowed it to enter: which it did very quickly, glad to get out of the driving rain.", "그러나 그 형체가 남자였고, 롭의 명령은 여자에게만 적용되는 것이었으므로, 롭은 문을 열어 둔 채 그것이 들어오도록 했다. 그것은 휘몰아치는 비를 벗어난 것을 반기며 아주 잽싸게 들어왔다."]], [["“A job for Burgess and Co. at any rate,”", "\"하여간 버지스 상회에 일감 하나는 생기겠군,\""], ["said the visitor, looking over his shoulder compassionately at his own legs, which were very wet and covered with splashes.", "방문객이 어깨너머로 자기 다리를 안쓰럽게 내려다보며 말했다. 다리는 흠뻑 젖어 흙탕물 자국투성이였다."], ["“Oh, how-de-do, Mr Gills?”", "\"아, 안녕하십니까, 길스 씨?\""]], [["The salutation was addressed to the Captain, now emerging from the back parlour with a most transparent and utterly futile affectation of coming out by accidence.", "그 인사는 선장을 향한 것이었는데, 선장은 마침 우연히 나오는 척, 더없이 빤히 들여다보이고 전혀 통하지도 않는 시늉을 하며 뒤채 거실에서 나오던 참이었다."]], [["“Thankee,”", "\"고맙습니다,\""], ["the gentleman went on to say in the same breath; “I’m very well indeed, myself, I’m much obliged to you.", "그 신사가 같은 숨에 말을 이었다. \"저야 정말이지 아주 잘 지냅니다, 대단히 감사합니다."], ["My name is Toots,—Mister Toots.”", "제 이름은 투츠입니다—투츠 씨라고 합니다.\""]], [["The Captain remembered to have seen this young gentleman at the wedding, and made him a bow.", "선장은 이 젊은 신사를 결혼식에서 본 기억이 나서 그에게 절을 했다."], ["Mr Toots replied with a chuckle; and being embarrassed, as he generally was, breathed hard, shook hands with the Captain for a long time, and then falling on Rob the Grinder, in the absence of any other resource, shook hands with him in a most affectionate and cordial manner.", "투츠 씨는 킥킥 웃으며 답했다. 그리고 평소처럼 어색해져서는 거친 숨을 몰아쉬며 한참 동안 선장과 악수를 하더니, 달리 의지할 데가 없는지라 그라인더 롭에게 다가가 더없이 다정하고 친근한 태도로 그와도 악수를 했다."]], [["“I say!", "\"저, 있잖아요!"], ["I should like to speak a word to you, Mr Gills, if you please,”", "괜찮으시다면 길스 씨께 한마디 드리고 싶은데요,\""], ["said Toots at length, with surprising presence of mind.", "투츠 씨가 마침내, 놀라운 침착함을 발휘하며 말했다. \"저, 있잖아요!"], ["“I say!", "디.오.엠."], ["Miss D.O.M. you know!”", "아가씨 말입니다, 아시죠!\""]], [["The Captain, with responsive gravity and mystery, immediately waved his hook towards the little parlour, whither Mr Toots followed him.", "선장은 그에 화답하듯 엄숙하고 신비스러운 태도로 즉시 자그마한 거실 쪽으로 갈고리를 휘저었고, 투츠 씨가 그 뒤를 따라갔다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I beg your pardon though,”", "그런데 죄송합니다만,\""], ["said Mr Toots, looking up in the Captain’s face as he sat down in a chair by the fire, which the Captain placed for him; “you don’t happen to know the Chicken at all; do you, Mr Gills?”", "투츠 씨가, 선장이 자기를 위해 놓아 준 난롯가 의자에 앉으면서 선장의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다. \"혹시 치킨이라고 전혀 모르시지요, 길스 씨?\""]], [["“The Chicken?”", "\"치킨이라뇨?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“The Game Chicken,”", "\"게임 치킨 말입니다,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["The Captain shaking his head, Mr Toots explained that the man alluded to was the celebrated public character who had covered himself and his country with glory in his contest with the Nobby Shropshire One; but this piece of information did not appear to enlighten the Captain very much.", "선장이 고개를 가로젓자, 투츠 씨는 자기가 언급한 사람이 노비 슈롭셔원과의 대결에서 자신과 조국에 영광을 안긴 그 유명한 공인이라고 설명했다. 그러나 이 정보는 선장을 그다지 깨우쳐 주는 것 같지 않았다."]], [["“Because he’s outside: that’s all,”", "\"왜냐면 그가 밖에 있거든요. 그게 다입니다,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“But it’s of no consequence; he won’t get very wet, perhaps.”", "\"하지만 별일 아닙니다. 아마 그렇게 많이 젖지는 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“I can pass the word for him in a moment,”", "\"제가 금세 그분을 불러올 수 있습니다,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Well, if you would have the goodness to let him sit in the shop with your young man,”", "\"음, 그가 댁의 젊은이와 함께 가게에 좀 앉아 있게 해 주신다면,\" 투츠 씨가 킥킥 웃으며 말했다."], ["chuckled Mr Toots, “I should be glad; because, you know, he’s easily offended, and the damp’s rather bad for his stamina.", "\"고맙겠습니다. 아시다시피, 그는 쉽게 발끈하는 데다, 습기가 그의 체력에 좀 나쁘거든요."], ["I’ll call him in, Mr Gills.”", "제가 그를 불러들이겠습니다, 길스 씨.\""]], [["With that, Mr Toots repairing to the shop-door, sent a peculiar whistle into the night, which produced a stoical gentleman in a shaggy white great-coat and a flat-brimmed hat, with very short hair, a broken nose, and a considerable tract of bare and sterile country behind each ear.", "그 말과 함께 투츠 씨는 가게 문으로 가서 밤을 향해 독특한 휘파람을 불어 보냈고, 그러자 텁수룩한 흰 외투에 챙이 납작한 모자를 쓴, 머리가 아주 짧고 코가 부러졌으며 양쪽 귀 뒤로 상당히 넓은 헐벗고 메마른 땅뙈기를 거느린 한 무뚝뚝한 신사가 나타났다."]], [["“Sit down, Chicken,”", "\"앉게, 치킨,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["The compliant Chicken spat out some small pieces of straw on which he was regaling himself, and took in a fresh supply from a reserve he carried in his hand.", "고분고분한 치킨은 입에 물고 즐기던 지푸라기 몇 가닥을 뱉어 내고는, 손에 들고 있던 비축분에서 새것 한 줌을 다시 입에 넣었다."]], [["“There ain’t no drain of nothing short handy, is there?”", "\"여기 짧게라도 한잔 걸칠 거 뭐 없겠수?\""], ["said the Chicken, generally.", "치킨이 막연히 말했다."], ["“This here sluicing night is hard lines to a man as lives on his condition.”", "\"이렇게 물벼락 치는 밤은 제 컨디션으로 먹고사는 놈한테는 고생길이라우.\""]], [["Captain Cuttle proffered a glass of rum, which the Chicken, throwing back his head, emptied into himself, as into a cask, after proposing the brief sentiment, “Towards us!”", "커틀 선장이 럼주 한 잔을 권하자, 치킨은 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 \"우리를 위하여!\"라는 짤막한 건배사를 던진 뒤, 마치 통 속에 붓듯이 그것을 자기 안으로 비워 넣었다."], ["Mr Toots and the Captain returning then to the parlour, and taking their seats before the fire, Mr Toots began:", "그러고 나서 투츠 씨와 선장은 거실로 돌아와 난롯가에 자리를 잡았고, 투츠 씨가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Mr Gills—”", "\"길스 씨—\""]], [["“Awast!”", "\"잠깐!\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“My name’s Cuttle.”", "\"내 이름은 커틀이오.\""]], [["Mr Toots looked greatly disconcerted, while the Captain proceeded gravely.", "투츠 씨는 몹시 당황한 기색이었고, 선장은 엄숙하게 말을 이었다."]], [["“Cap’en Cuttle is my name, and England is my nation, this here is my dwelling-place, and blessed be creation—Job,”", "\"커틀 선장이 내 이름이고, 잉글랜드가 내 나라요, 여기가 내 거처라, 창조에 복이 있을진저—욥기,\""], ["said the Captain, as an index to his authority.", "선장이 자기 권위의 근거를 제시하듯 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I couldn’t see Mr Gills, could I?”", "길스 씨는 못 뵙겠지요, 그렇죠?\""], ["said Mr Toots; “because—”", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"왜냐면—\""]], [["“If you could see Sol Gills, young gen’l’m’n,”", "\"솔 길스를 볼 수만 있다면, 젊은 양반,\""], ["said the Captain, impressively, and laying his heavy hand on Mr Toots’s knee, “old Sol, mind you—with your own eyes—as you sit there—you’d be welcomer to me, than a wind astern, to a ship becalmed.", "선장이 묵직한 손을 투츠 씨의 무릎에 얹으며 인상 깊게 말했다. \"늙은 솔을 말이오, 알겠소—당신 두 눈으로—지금 거기 앉아 있는 그대로—그렇다면 당신은 나에게, 바람 한 점 없이 멈춘 배에 불어오는 순풍보다 더 반가운 손님일 거요."], ["But you can’t see Sol Gills.", "하지만 당신은 솔 길스를 볼 수 없소."], ["And why can’t you see Sol Gills?”", "그런데 왜 솔 길스를 볼 수 없느냐?\""], ["said the Captain, apprised by the face of Mr Toots that he was making a profound impression on that gentleman’s mind.", "선장이, 투츠 씨의 얼굴을 보고 자기가 그 신사의 마음에 깊은 인상을 새기고 있음을 알아채고서 말했다."], ["“Because he’s inwisible.”", "\"그가 안 보이게 됐기 때문이오.\""]], [["Mr Toots in his agitation was going to reply that it was of no consequence at all.", "투츠 씨는 동요한 나머지 그런 건 전혀 상관없다고 대답하려 했다."], ["But he corrected himself, and said, “Lor bless me!”", "그러나 곧 고쳐서, \"이런, 맙소사!\"라고 말했다."]], [["“That there man,” said the Captain, “has left me in charge here by a piece of writing, but though he was a’most as good as my sworn brother, I know no more where he’s gone, or why he’s gone; if so be to seek his nevy, or if so be along of being not quite settled in his mind; than you do. One morning at daybreak, he went over the side,” said the Captain, “without a splash, without a ripple I have looked for that man high and low, and never set eyes, nor ears, nor nothing else, upon him from that hour.”", "\"바로 저 사람이,\" 선장이 말했다. \"글로 써서 여기를 나한테 맡겨 두고 떠났소. 하지만 그가 거의 내 의형제나 다름없는 사람이었는데도, 그가 어디로 갔는지, 왜 갔는지는 당신만큼이나 나도 도무지 모르겠소. 조카를 찾으러 갔는지, 아니면 마음이 영 진정되지 않아서 그랬는지 말이오. 어느 날 동틀 녘에, 그는 뱃전을 넘어갔소,\" 선장이 말했다. \"물 한 방울 튀기지 않고, 잔물결 하나 없이. 나는 그 사람을 높은 데 낮은 데 다 뒤지며 찾았지만, 그때부터 그를 눈으로도 귀로도, 그 어떤 것으로도 도무지 보지 못했소.\""]], [["“But, good Gracious, Miss Dombey don’t know—”", "\"하지만, 세상에, 돔비 아가씨는 모르시지요—\""], ["Mr Toots began.", "투츠 씨가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Why, I ask you, as a feeling heart,”", "\"자, 내가 당신한테 묻겠소, 정 많은 사람으로서,\""], ["said the Captain, dropping his voice, “why should she know?", "선장이 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"왜 그분이 알아야 하오?"], ["why should she be made to know, until such time as there wam’t any help for it?", "어쩔 도리가 없게 될 때까지, 왜 그분에게 알게 만들어야 하오?"], ["She took to old Sol Gills, did that sweet creetur, with a kindness, with a affability, with a—what’s the good of saying so?", "그 사랑스러운 분이 늙은 솔 길스를 어찌나 다정하게, 어찌나 상냥하게, 어찌나—이런 말이 다 무슨 소용이오?"], ["you know her.”", "당신도 그분을 알지 않소.\""]], [["“I should hope so,”", "\"그러길 바랍니다,\""], ["chuckled Mr Toots, with a conscious blush that suffused his whole countenance.", "투츠 씨가, 온 얼굴에 번지는 의식적인 홍조와 함께 킥킥 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“And you come here from her?”", "\"그런데 당신은 그분 심부름으로 여기 온 거요?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“I should think so,”", "\"그렇다고 봐야지요,\""], ["chuckled Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 킥킥 웃었다."]], [["“Then all I need observe, is,”", "\"그렇다면 내가 한마디만 하겠소,\""], ["said the Captain, “that you know a angel, and are chartered a angel.”", "선장이 말했다. \"당신은 천사를 알고 있고, 천사의 위임을 받은 사람이오.\""]], [["Mr Toots instantly seized the Captain’s hand, and requested the favour of his friendship.", "투츠 씨는 즉시 선장의 손을 붙들고 그와 벗이 되는 영광을 청했다."]], [["“Upon my word and honour,”", "\"제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데,\""], ["said Mr Toots, earnestly, “I should be very much obliged to you if you’d improve my acquaintance.", "투츠 씨가 진지하게 말했다. \"저와의 교분을 두텁게 해 주신다면 정말 대단히 감사하겠습니다."], ["I should like to know you, Captain, very much.", "선장님과 아주 많이 가까워지고 싶어요."], ["I really am in want of a friend, I am.", "저는 정말이지 친구가 필요합니다, 정말로요."], ["Little Dombey was my friend at old Blimber’s, and would have been now, if he’d have lived.", "어린 돔비는 옛날 블림버 댁에서 제 친구였고, 만일 살아 있었더라면 지금도 그랬을 겁니다. 치킨은,\""], ["The Chicken,”", "투츠 씨가 처량한 속삭임으로 말했다."], ["said Mr Toots, in a forlorn whisper, “is very well—admirable in his way—the sharpest man perhaps in the world; there’s not a move he isn’t up to, everybody says so—but I don’t know—he’s not everything.", "\"아주 괜찮아요—제 방면에선 훌륭하지요—아마 세상에서 가장 약삭빠른 사람일 겁니다. 그가 모르는 수가 없거든요, 다들 그렇게 말합니다—하지만 글쎄요—그가 전부는 아니거든요."], ["So she is an angel, Captain.", "그러니까 그분은 천사예요, 선장님."], ["If there is an angel anywhere, it’s Miss Dombey.", "어디엔가 천사가 있다면, 그건 돔비 아가씨입니다."], ["That’s what I’ve always said.", "제가 늘 그렇게 말해 왔지요."], ["Really though, you know,”", "하지만 정말이지, 아시다시피,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “I should be very much obliged to you if you’d cultivate my acquaintance.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"저와의 교분을 길러 주신다면 정말 대단히 감사하겠습니다.\""]], [["Captain Cuttle received this proposal in a polite manner, but still without committing himself to its acceptance; merely observing, “Ay, ay, my lad.", "커틀 선장은 이 제안을 정중한 태도로 받아들였으나, 그것을 수락하겠다고 확실히 약속하지는 않은 채, 그저 \"그래, 그래, 얘야."], ["We shall see, we shall see;”", "두고 봅시다, 두고 봐요\"라고만 말했다."], ["and reminding Mr Toots of his immediate mission, by inquiring to what he was indebted for the honour of that visit.", "그러고는 무슨 일로 이번 방문의 영광을 누리게 되었는지 물어, 투츠 씨에게 그가 온 본래의 용건을 일깨워 주었다."]], [["“Why the fact is,”", "\"실은 말이지요,\""], ["replied Mr Toots, “that it’s the young woman I come from.", "투츠 씨가 대답했다. \"그 젊은 여자 심부름으로 온 겁니다."], ["Not Miss Dombey—Susan, you know.", "돔비 아가씨가 아니라—수전 말입니다, 아시죠.\""]], [["The Captain nodded his head once, with a grave expression of face indicative of his regarding that young woman with serious respect.", "선장은 한 번 고개를 끄덕였는데, 그 진지한 표정은 자기가 그 젊은 여자를 깊이 존경하고 있음을 나타냈다."]], [["“And I’ll tell you how it happens,”", "\"어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지 말씀드리지요,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“You know, I go and call sometimes, on Miss Dombey.", "\"아시다시피, 저는 가끔 돔비 아가씨를 찾아가 뵙거든요."], ["I don’t go there on purpose, you know, but I happen to be in the neighbourhood very often; and when I find myself there, why—why I call.”", "일부러 가는 건 아니에요, 아시겠지만, 그저 그 동네에 아주 자주 있게 되는데, 거기 있다 보면, 그러니까—그러니까 들르는 거지요.\""]], [["“Nat’rally,”", "\"그야 당연한 일이오,\""], ["observed the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“I called this afternoon.", "\"오늘 오후에 들렀어요."], ["Upon my word and honour, I don’t think it’s possible to form an idea of the angel Miss Dombey was this afternoon.”", "제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데, 오늘 오후 돔비 아가씨가 얼마나 천사 같으셨는지 도무지 상상도 못 하실 겁니다.\""]], [["The Captain answered with a jerk of his head, implying that it might not be easy to some people, but was quite so to him.", "선장은 고개를 한 번 홱 젖혀 답했는데, 그것은 어떤 사람들에게는 쉽지 않을지 몰라도 자기에게는 충분히 가능한 일이라는 뜻이었다."]], [["“As I was coming out,”", "\"제가 나오려는데,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “the young woman, in the most unexpected manner, took me into the pantry.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"그 젊은 여자가 더없이 뜻밖으로 저를 식료품실로 데려가는 거예요.\""]], [["The Captain seemed, for the moment, to object to this proceeding; and leaning back in his chair, looked at Mr Toots with a distrustful, if not threatening visage.", "선장은 잠시 그 처사에 이의가 있는 듯, 의자에 등을 기대며 미심쩍은, 위협적이지는 않다 해도 그런 표정으로 투츠 씨를 바라보았다."]], [["“Where she brought out,”", "\"거기서 그 여자가,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “this newspaper.", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"이 신문을 꺼내더군요."], ["She told me that she had kept it from Miss Dombey all day, on account of something that was in it, about somebody that she and Dombey used to know; and then she read the passage to me.", "거기에 실린 어떤 내용 때문에 하루 종일 그걸 돔비 아가씨에게서 숨겨 두었다고 하면서요. 그 여자와 돔비 아가씨가 전에 알던 누군가에 관한 거라고요."], ["Very well.", "그러고는 그 대목을 저한테 읽어 주었어요."], ["Then she said—wait a minute; what was it she said, though!”", "아주 좋아요. 그런 다음 그 여자가 말하길—잠깐만요, 뭐라고 했더라!\""]], [["Mr Toots, endeavouring to concentrate his mental powers on this question, unintentionally fixed the Captain’s eye, and was so much discomposed by its stern expression, that his difficulty in resuming the thread of his subject was enhanced to a painful extent.", "투츠 씨는 이 문제에 자기 정신력을 집중하려 애쓰다가 본의 아니게 선장의 눈에 시선이 박혔고, 그 엄한 표정에 너무도 마음이 흐트러진 나머지, 이야기의 실마리를 다시 잡는 어려움이 괴로울 지경으로 커졌다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Mr Toots after long consideration.", "한참 궁리한 끝에 투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“Oh, ah!", "\"아, 그래!"], ["Yes!", "맞다!"], ["She said that she hoped there was a bare possibility that it mightn’t be true; and that as she couldn’t very well come out herself, without surprising Miss Dombey, would I go down to Mr Solomon Gills the Instrument-maker’s in this street, who was the party’s Uncle, and ask whether he believed it was true, or had heard anything else in the City.", "그 여자가 말하길, 그게 사실이 아닐 가능성이 조금이라도 있기를 바란다고 했어요. 그리고 자기는 돔비 아가씨를 놀라게 하지 않고서는 직접 나오기가 어려우니, 제가 이 거리에 있는 기구 제작자 솔로몬 길스 씨—그 당사자의 외삼촌이라는—께 내려가서, 그분이 그게 사실이라고 믿는지, 아니면 시내에서 뭔가 다른 걸 들었는지 여쭤봐 달라고 했어요."], ["She said, if he couldn’t speak to me, no doubt Captain Cuttle could.", "그분이 저한테 말씀하지 못하시면, 틀림없이 커틀 선장님이 해 주실 거라고요."], ["By the bye!”", "참 그렇지!\""], ["said Mr Toots, as the discovery flashed upon him, “you, you know!”", "투츠 씨가, 그 사실이 퍼뜩 떠오르자 말했다. \"당신, 바로 당신이로군요!\""]], [["The Captain glanced at the newspaper in Mr Toots’s hand, and breathed short and hurriedly.", "선장은 투츠 씨 손에 든 신문을 흘끗 보았고, 숨을 짧고 가쁘게 몰아쉬었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["pursued Mr Toots, “the reason why I’m rather late is, because I went up as far as Finchley first, to get some uncommonly fine chickweed that grows there, for Miss Dombey’s bird.", "투츠 씨가 말을 이었다. \"제가 좀 늦은 이유는, 먼저 핀칠리까지 올라갔다 왔기 때문이에요."], ["But I came on here, directly afterwards.", "거기서 자라는 아주 보기 드물게 좋은 별꽃을 좀 구하려고요, 돔비 아가씨 새한테 주려고요."], ["You’ve seen the paper, I suppose?”", "하지만 그 길로 곧장 여기로 왔어요. 신문은 보셨겠지요?\""]], [["The Captain, who had become cautious of reading the news, lest he should find himself advertised at full length by Mrs MacStinger, shook his head.", "맥스팅거 부인에게 자기에 관한 광고가 길게 실린 것을 발견하게 될까 봐 신문 읽기를 조심하게 된 선장은 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“Shall I read the passage to you?”", "\"제가 그 대목을 읽어 드릴까요?\""], ["inquired Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 물었다."]], [["The Captain making a sign in the affirmative, Mr Toots read as follows, from the Shipping Intelligence:", "선장이 그러라는 표시를 하자, 투츠 씨가 선박 소식란에서 다음과 같이 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["“‘Southampton.", "\"'사우샘프턴."], ["The barque Defiance, Henry James, Commander, arrived in this port today, with a cargo of sugar, coffee, and rum, reports that being becalmed on the sixth day of her passage home from Jamaica, in’—in such and such a latitude, you know,”", "바크선 디파이언스호, 선장 헨리 제임스, 설탕과 커피와 럼을 싣고 오늘 본 항에 입항, 자메이카에서 귀항하던 항해 엿새째에 바람이 멎어 멈춰 선 곳이'—이러저러한 위도라는데, 아시죠,\""], ["said Mr Toots, after making a feeble dash at the figures, and tumbling over them.", "투츠 씨가 숫자에 한번 힘없이 덤벼들었다가 거기서 고꾸라진 뒤 말했다."]], [["“Ay!”", "\"이런!\""], ["cried the Captain, striking his clenched hand on the table.", "선장이 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 탁자를 치며 외쳤다."], ["“Heave ahead, my lad!”", "\"어서 계속 읽어라, 얘야!\""]], [["“—latitude,”", "\"—위도,\""], ["repeated Mr Toots, with a startled glance at the Captain, “and longitude so-and-so,—‘the look-out observed, half an hour before sunset, some fragments of a wreck, drifting at about the distance of a mile.", "투츠 씨가 선장을 깜짝 놀란 눈길로 흘끗 보며 되풀이했다. \"그리고 경도 이러저러한 곳에서—'망보던 자가, 해 지기 반 시간 전에, 약 1마일쯤 떨어진 거리에서 어떤 난파선 잔해가 떠다니는 것을 발견했다."], ["The weather being clear, and the barque making no way, a boat was hoisted out, with orders to inspect the same, when they were found to consist of sundry large spars, and a part of the main rigging of an English brig, of about five hundred tons burden, together with a portion of the stem on which the words and letters “Son and H-” were yet plainly legible.", "날씨가 맑고 바크선이 나아가지 못하던 터라, 그것을 조사하라는 명령과 함께 보트 한 척을 내렸는데, 그 잔해는 여러 개의 큰 원목과, 적재량 약 5백 톤가량 되는 영국 브리그선의 주삭구 일부, 그리고 \"선과 ㅎ—\"이라는 글자가 아직 또렷이 읽히는 선수재의 일부로 이루어져 있음이 밝혀졌다."], ["No vestige of any dead body was to be seen upon the floating fragments.", "떠다니는 잔해 위에는 어떤 시신의 흔적도 보이지 않았다."], ["Log of the Defiance states, that a breeze springing up in the night, the wreck was seen no more.", "디파이언스호의 항해일지에는, 밤중에 미풍이 일면서 그 난파선 잔해가 더는 보이지 않게 되었다고 기록되어 있다."], ["There can be no doubt that all surmises as to the fate of the missing vessel, the Son and Heir, port of London, bound for Barbados, are now set at rest for ever; that she broke up in the last hurricane; and that every soul on board perished.’”", "런던 항 소속, 바베이도스행이던 실종 선박 선과 상속자호의 운명에 관한 온갖 추측은 이제 영원히 잠재워졌음이 분명하다. 그 배는 지난 허리케인에 부서졌으며, 배에 탄 모든 사람이 목숨을 잃었다.'\""]], [["Captain Cuttle, like all mankind, little knew how much hope had survived within him under discouragement, until he felt its death-shock.", "커틀 선장은, 모든 인간이 그러하듯, 낙담 속에서도 자기 안에 얼마나 많은 희망이 살아남아 있었는지를, 그것의 죽음의 충격을 느끼기 전까지는 거의 알지 못했다."], ["During the reading of the paragraph, and for a minute or two afterwards, he sat with his gaze fixed on the modest Mr Toots, like a man entranced; then, suddenly rising, and putting on his glazed hat, which, in his visitor’s honour, he had laid upon the table, the Captain turned his back, and bent his head down on the little chimneypiece.", "그 단락을 읽는 동안, 그리고 그 후 한두 분 동안, 그는 마치 넋이 나간 사람처럼 시선을 그 겸손한 투츠 씨에게 고정한 채 앉아 있었다. 그러더니 갑자기 일어나, 손님에 대한 경의로 탁자 위에 놓아 두었던 번들거리는 모자를 쓰고서, 선장은 등을 돌려 자그마한 벽난로 선반 위로 머리를 숙였다."]], [["“Oh”", "\"오!"], ["upon my word and honour,”", "제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데,\""], ["cried Mr Toots, whose tender heart was moved by the Captain’s unexpected distress, “this is a most wretched sort of affair this world is!", "선장의 뜻밖의 비탄에 다정한 마음이 움직인 투츠 씨가 외쳤다. \"이 세상이라는 건 참으로 비참한 곳이로군요!"], ["Somebody’s always dying, or going and doing something uncomfortable in it.", "늘 누군가는 죽어 가거나, 무슨 거북한 일을 저지르고 있어요."], ["I’m sure I never should have looked forward so much, to coming into my property, if I had known this.", "정말이지 이런 걸 미리 알았더라면, 제가 제 재산을 물려받게 되는 걸 그렇게 고대하지는 않았을 거예요."], ["I never saw such a world.", "이런 세상은 본 적이 없어요."], ["It’s a great deal worse than Blimber’s.”", "블림버 댁보다 훨씬 더 못해요.\""]], [["Captain Cuttle, without altering his position, signed to Mr Toots not to mind him; and presently turned round, with his glazed hat thrust back upon his ears, and his hand composing and smoothing his brown face.", "커틀 선장은 자세를 바꾸지 않은 채 투츠 씨에게 자기는 신경 쓰지 말라고 손짓했다. 그러더니 곧 몸을 돌렸는데, 번들거리는 모자는 귀 쪽으로 밀려나 있었고, 손으로는 자기 갈색 얼굴을 진정시키며 쓰다듬고 있었다."]], [["“Wal”r, my dear lad,”", "\"월, 내 사랑하는 아이야,\""], ["said the Captain, “farewell!", "선장이 말했다. \"잘 가거라!"], ["Wal”r my child, my boy, and man, I loved you!", "월, 내 아이, 내 사내, 내 어른아, 나는 너를 사랑했단다!"], ["He warn’t my flesh and blood,”", "그 애는 내 피붙이가 아니었소,\""], ["said the Captain, looking at the fire—“I ain’t got none—but something of what a father feels when he loses a son, I feel in losing Wal”r.", "선장이 난롯불을 바라보며 말했다. \"나한테는 피붙이라곤 없으니까—하지만 아비가 자식을 잃을 때 느끼는 그 무언가를, 나는 월을 잃으면서 느끼고 있소."], ["For why?”", "왜냐고?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Because it ain’t one loss, but a round dozen.", "\"그건 하나만 잃은 게 아니라, 자그마치 한 다스나 잃었기 때문이오."], ["Where’s that there young school-boy with the rosy face and curly hair, that used to be as merry in this here parlour, come round every week, as a piece of music?", "발그레한 얼굴에 곱슬머리를 하고, 한 곡조 음악처럼 매주 이 거실에 들러 즐겁게 굴던 그 어린 학생 녀석은 어디 갔소?"], ["Gone down with Wal”r.", "월과 함께 가라앉았소."], ["Where’s that there fresh lad, that nothing couldn’t tire nor put out, and that sparkled up and blushed so, when we joked him about Heart’s Delight, that he was beautiful to look at?", "무엇으로도 지치게 하거나 풀죽게 할 수 없던, '마음의 기쁨' 얘기로 놀려 대면 환히 빛나며 얼굴을 붉히던, 보기에 그렇게도 아름답던 그 싱싱한 청년은 어디 있소?"], ["Gone down with Wal”r.", "월과 함께 가라앉았소."], ["Where’s that there man’s spirit, all afire, that wouldn’t see the old man hove down for a minute, and cared nothing for itself?", "노인이 일 분이라도 짓눌리는 걸 두고 보지 못하던, 자기 자신은 조금도 돌보지 않던, 온통 불타오르던 그 사내의 기상은 어디 있소?"], ["Gone down with Wal”r.", "월과 함께 가라앉았소."], ["It ain’t one Wal”r.", "그건 월 하나가 아니오."], ["There was a dozen Wal”rs that I know’d and loved, all holding round his neck when he went down, and they’re a-holding round mine now!”", "내가 알고 사랑한 월이 한 다스나 있었는데, 그가 가라앉을 때 모두 그의 목에 매달려 있었고, 지금은 그들이 내 목에 매달려 있소!\""]], [["Mr Toots sat silent: folding and refolding the newspaper as small as possible upon his knee.", "투츠 씨는 말없이 앉아, 무릎 위에서 신문을 되도록 작게 접고 또 접었다."]], [["“And Sol Gills,” said the Captain, gazing at the fire, “poor nevyless old Sol, where are you got to! you was left in charge of me; his last words was, ‘Take care of my Uncle!’ What came over you, Sol, when you went and gave the go-bye to Ned Cuttle; and what am I to put in my accounts that he’s a looking down upon, respecting you! Sol Gills, Sol Gills!” said the Captain, shaking his head slowly, “catch sight of that there newspaper, away from home, with no one as know’d Wal”r by, to say a word; and broadside to you broach, and down you pitch, head foremost!”", "\"그리고 솔 길스,\" 선장이 난롯불을 응시하며 말했다. \"가엾게도 조카 잃은 늙은 솔, 자넨 어디로 갔나! 자넨 나한테 맡겨졌었네. 자네 마지막 말은 '내 조카를 돌봐 주게!'였지. 솔, 자네 대체 무슨 마음이 들어서 네드 커틀을 모른 척하고 떠난 건가. 그리고 그 애가 내려다보고 있는 내 장부에, 자네에 관해선 대체 뭐라고 적어 넣어야 한단 말인가! 솔 길스, 솔 길스!\" 선장이 천천히 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"집을 떠나 어딘가에서, 곁에 월을 아는 사람도 없이, 한마디 거들 사람도 없이 저 신문을 보다니. 옆구리로 파도를 정통으로 맞고, 곤두박질치며 머리부터 처박히고 마는구나!\""]], [["Drawing a heavy sigh, the Captain turned to Mr Toots, and roused himself to a sustained consciousness of that gentleman’s presence.", "무거운 한숨을 내쉬며, 선장은 투츠 씨를 향해 돌아서서, 그 신사가 거기 있다는 것을 다시 또렷이 의식하도록 마음을 추슬렀다."]], [["“My lad,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["said the Captain, “you must tell the young woman honestly that this here fatal news is too correct.", "선장이 말했다. \"그 젊은 여자한테 솔직히 전해 주어야 하오."], ["They don’t romance, you see, on such pints.", "이 끔찍한 소식은 너무도 정확하다고."], ["It’s entered on the ship’s log, and that’s the truest book as a man can write.", "이런 일에 대해선, 알다시피, 사람들이 꾸며 대지 않거든. 그건 배의 항해일지에 기록된 거고, 항해일지는 사람이 쓸 수 있는 가장 진실한 책이오."], ["To-morrow morning,”", "내일 아침,\" 선장이 말했다."], ["said the Captain, “I’ll step out and make inquiries; but they’ll lead to no good.", "\"내가 나가서 알아보겠소. 하지만 좋은 결과로 이어지진 않을 거요."], ["They can’t do it.", "그럴 리가 없소."], ["If you’ll give me a look-in in the forenoon, you shall know what I have heerd; but tell the young woman from Cap’en Cuttle, that it’s over.", "오전 중에 한번 들러 주면, 내가 들은 걸 알려 주리다. 하지만 그 젊은 여자한테는 커틀 선장이 그러더라고 전해 주오, 다 끝났다고."], ["Over!”", "끝났다고!\""], ["And the Captain, hooking off his glazed hat, pulled his handkerchief out of the crown, wiped his grizzled head despairingly, and tossed the handkerchief in again, with the indifference of deep dejection.", "그러고 선장은 번들거리는 모자를 갈고리로 벗겨 내고, 모자 꼭대기 안에서 손수건을 꺼내, 절망적으로 자기 희끗한 머리를 닦고는, 깊은 의기소침의 무심함으로 손수건을 도로 그 안에 던져 넣었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I assure you,”", "정말이지,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “really I am dreadfully sorry.", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"정말로 더없이 마음이 아픕니다."], ["Upon my word I am, though I wasn’t acquainted with the party.", "명예를 걸고 그렇습니다, 비록 제가 그분과 아는 사이는 아니었지만요."], ["Do you think Miss Dombey will be very much affected, Captain Gills—I mean Mr Cuttle?”", "돔비 아가씨가 무척 충격을 받으실까요, 길스 선장님—아니, 커틀 씨 말입니다?\""]], [["“Why, Lord love you,”", "\"이런, 자네도 참,\""], ["returned the Captain, with something of compassion for Mr Toots’s innocence.", "선장이 투츠 씨의 순진함에 다소 측은해하며 응수했다."], ["“When she warn’t no higher than that, they were as fond of one another as two young doves.”", "\"그분이 요만할 때부터, 두 사람은 어린 멧비둘기 한 쌍처럼 서로 좋아했다네.\""]], [["“Were they though!”", "\"정말 그랬군요!\""], ["said Mr Toots, with a considerably lengthened face.", "투츠 씨가 상당히 길어진 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["“They were made for one another,”", "\"두 사람은 천생연분이었소,\""], ["said the Captain, mournfully; “but what signifies that now!”", "선장이 애절하게 말했다. \"하지만 이제 와서 그게 다 무슨 소용이오!\""]], [["“Upon my word and honour,”", "\"제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데,\""], ["cried Mr Toots, blurting out his words through a singular combination of awkward chuckles and emotion, “I’m even more sorry than I was before.", "투츠 씨가 어색한 킥킥거림과 격정이 묘하게 뒤섞인 채로 말을 토해 내며 외쳤다. \"아까보다도 더 마음이 아픕니다."], ["You know, Captain Gills, I—I positively adore Miss Dombey;—I—I am perfectly sore with loving her;”", "아시다시피, 길스 선장님, 저는—저는 정말로 돔비 아가씨를 흠모합니다—저는—저는 그분을 사랑하느라 완전히 쓰라립니다.\""], ["the burst with which this confession forced itself out of the unhappy Mr Toots, bespoke the vehemence of his feelings; “but what would be the good of my regarding her in this manner, if I wasn’t truly sorry for her feeling pain, whatever was the cause of it.", "이 고백이 불행한 투츠 씨에게서 터져 나오는 그 격렬함은 그의 감정의 맹렬함을 말해 주었다. \"하지만 그 원인이 무엇이든 그분이 고통받는 것에 제가 진정으로 가슴 아파하지 않는다면, 제가 그분을 이런 식으로 우러르는 게 무슨 소용이겠습니까."], ["Mine ain’t a selfish affection, you know,”", "제 애정은 이기적인 게 아니거든요, 아시겠지만,\""], ["said Mr Toots, in the confidence engendered by his having been a witness of the Captain’s tenderness.", "투츠 씨가, 선장의 다정함을 목격한 데서 생겨난 신뢰감으로 말했다."], ["“It’s the sort of thing with me, Captain Gills, that if I could be run over—or—or trampled upon—or—or thrown off a very high place-or anything of that sort—for Miss Dombey’s sake, it would be the most delightful thing that could happen to me.”", "\"저는요, 길스 선장님, 만일 제가 돔비 아가씨를 위해서 마차에 치이거나—아니면—아니면 짓밟히거나—아니면—아니면 아주 높은 데서 떨어지거나—뭐 그런 종류의 일을 당할 수 있다면, 그게 저한테 일어날 수 있는 가장 기쁜 일일 것 같은, 그런 사람입니다.\""]], [["All this, Mr Toots said in a suppressed voice, to prevent its reaching the jealous ears of the Chicken, who objected to the softer emotions; which effort of restraint, coupled with the intensity of his feelings, made him red to the tips of his ears, and caused him to present such an affecting spectacle of disinterested love to the eyes of Captain Cuttle, that the good Captain patted him consolingly on the back, and bade him cheer up.", "이 모든 말을 투츠 씨는 부드러운 감정을 못마땅해하는 치킨의 시샘 어린 귀에 들어가지 않게끔 낮은 목소리로 했다. 그 자제의 노력이 그의 감정의 강렬함과 어우러져 그를 귀 끝까지 빨갛게 만들었고, 커틀 선장의 눈에 그토록 감동적인 사심 없는 사랑의 광경을 펼쳐 보이게 했으니, 마음씨 좋은 선장은 위로하듯 그의 등을 토닥이며 힘내라고 일렀다."]], [["“Thankee, Captain Gills,”", "\"고맙습니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “it’s kind of you, in the midst of your own troubles, to say so.", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"선장님 자신도 괴로우신 와중에 그렇게 말씀해 주시다니 친절하시네요."], ["I’m very much obliged to you.", "정말 대단히 감사합니다."], ["As I said before, I really want a friend, and should be glad to have your acquaintance.", "아까도 말씀드렸지만, 저는 정말로 친구가 필요하고, 선장님과 가까이 지냈으면 좋겠습니다."], ["Although I am very well off,”", "제가 형편은 아주 넉넉하지만,\""], ["said Mr Toots, with energy, “you can’t think what a miserable Beast I am.", "투츠 씨가 힘주어 말했다. \"제가 얼마나 비참한 짐승인지 모르실 겁니다."], ["The hollow crowd, you know, when they see me with the Chicken, and characters of distinction like that, suppose me to be happy; but I’m wretched.", "그 속 빈 군중들은, 아시겠지만, 제가 치킨이나 그런 명사들과 함께 있는 걸 보면 제가 행복한 줄 압니다. 하지만 저는 비참합니다."], ["I suffer for Miss Dombey, Captain Gills.", "저는 돔비 아가씨 때문에 괴롭습니다, 길스 선장님."], ["I can’t get through my meals; I have no pleasure in my tailor; I often cry when I’m alone.", "밥도 제대로 못 넘기고, 양복 짓는 즐거움도 없고, 혼자 있을 때면 자주 웁니다."], ["I assure you it’ll be a satisfaction to me to come back to-morrow, or to come back fifty times.”", "정말이지 내일 다시 오는 것이, 아니 쉰 번을 다시 오는 것이 저에게는 기쁨일 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr Toots, with these words, shook the Captain’s hand; and disguising such traces of his agitation as could be disguised on so short a notice, before the Chicken’s penetrating glance, rejoined that eminent gentleman in the shop.", "이 말과 함께 투츠 씨는 선장의 손을 잡았다. 그리고 치킨의 날카로운 눈길 앞에서 그렇게 짧은 시간에 감출 수 있는 만큼의 동요의 흔적은 감춘 채, 그 저명한 신사가 있는 가게로 돌아갔다."], ["The Chicken, who was apt to be jealous of his ascendancy, eyed Captain Cuttle with anything but favour as he took leave of Mr Toots, but followed his patron without being otherwise demonstrative of his ill-will: leaving the Captain oppressed with sorrow; and Rob the Grinder elevated with joy, on account of having had the honour of staring for nearly half an hour at the conqueror of the Nobby Shropshire One.", "자기 우위를 시샘하기 쉬운 치킨은, 투츠 씨가 작별을 고하는 동안 커틀 선장을 결코 호의적이라고는 할 수 없는 눈으로 쏘아보았으나, 달리 자기 악감정을 드러내지는 않고 자기 후원자를 따라 나갔다. 그리하여 선장은 슬픔에 짓눌린 채 남았고, 그라인더 롭은 노비 슈롭셔원의 정복자를 거의 반 시간이나 빤히 쳐다보는 영광을 누린 덕에 기쁨에 들떠 남았다."]], [["Long after Rob was fast asleep in his bed under the counter, the Captain sat looking at the fire; and long after there was no fire to look at, the Captain sat gazing on the rusty bars, with unavailing thoughts of Walter and old Sol crowding through his mind.", "롭이 계산대 밑 자기 잠자리에서 곤히 잠든 지 한참 후에도, 선장은 앉아서 난롯불을 바라보았다. 그리고 바라볼 불이라곤 없어진 지 한참 후에도, 선장은 녹슨 살창을 응시하며 앉아 있었고, 월터와 늙은 솔에 대한 부질없는 생각들이 그의 마음속으로 몰려들었다."], ["Retirement to the stormy chamber at the top of the house brought no rest with it; and the Captain rose up in the morning, sorrowful and unrefreshed.", "집 꼭대기 폭풍우 치는 방으로 물러나도 안식은 따라오지 않았고, 선장은 아침에 슬픔에 잠긴 채 개운치 않은 몸으로 일어났다."]], [["As soon as the City offices were opened, the Captain issued forth to the counting-house of Dombey and Son.", "시내 사무소들이 문을 열자마자, 선장은 돔비와 아들 회사의 회계실로 나섰다."], ["But there was no opening of the Midshipman’s windows that morning.", "그러나 그날 아침 항해사의 창문은 열리지 않았다."], ["Rob the Grinder, by the Captain’s orders, left the shutters closed, and the house was as a house of death.", "그라인더 롭은 선장의 명령에 따라 덧문을 닫아 두었고, 그 집은 마치 초상집 같았다."]], [["It chanced that Mr Carker was entering the office, as Captain Cuttle arrived at the door.", "때마침 커틀 선장이 문에 다다랐을 때 카커 씨가 사무소로 들어서고 있었다."], ["Receiving the Manager’s benison gravely and silently, Captain Cuttle made bold to accompany him into his own room.", "그 지배인의 인사를 엄숙하고 말없이 받은 다음, 커틀 선장은 대담하게도 그를 따라 그의 방으로 들어갔다."]], [["“Well, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"자, 커틀 선장,\""], ["said Mr Carker, taking up his usual position before the fireplace, and keeping on his hat, “this is a bad business.”", "카커 씨가 벽난로 앞 자기 평소 자리를 잡고 모자를 쓴 채로 말했다. \"이거 안된 일이로군요.\""]], [["“You have received the news as was in print yesterday, Sir?”", "\"어제 인쇄물에 났던 소식을 받으셨습니까, 선생?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “we have received it!", "카커 씨가 말했다. \"우리도 받았소!"], ["It was accurately stated.", "정확하게 보도된 거였소."], ["The underwriters suffer a considerable loss.", "보험업자들이 상당한 손실을 입겠지요. 우리도 무척 유감이오."], ["We are very sorry.", "어쩔 수 없지요!"], ["No help! Such is life!”", "인생이란 게 그렇소!\""]], [["Mr Carker pared his nails delicately with a penknife, and smiled at the Captain, who was standing by the door looking at him.", "카커 씨는 주머니칼로 손톱을 섬세하게 다듬으며, 문가에 서서 자기를 바라보고 있는 선장에게 미소를 지었다."]], [["“I excessively regret poor Gay,”", "\"가엾은 게이는 정말 안타깝소,\""], ["said Carker, “and the crew.", "카커가 말했다. \"선원들도 그렇고요."], ["I understand there were some of our very best men among ’em.", "그중에 우리 회사 최고의 일꾼들이 몇 있었다고 들었소."], ["It always happens so.", "늘 이런 식이지요."], ["Many men with families too.", "게다가 식솔을 거느린 자들도 많았고요."], ["A comfort to reflect that poor Gay had no family, Captain Cuttle!”", "가엾은 게이에게는 식솔이 없었던 게 위안이라면 위안이오, 커틀 선장!\""]], [["The Captain stood rubbing his chin, and looking at the Manager.", "선장은 턱을 문지르며 서서 지배인을 바라보았다."], ["The Manager glanced at the unopened letters lying on his desk, and took up the newspaper.", "지배인은 책상 위에 놓인 뜯지 않은 편지들을 흘끗 보고는 신문을 집어 들었다."]], [["“Is there anything I can do for you, Captain Cuttle?”", "\"내가 도와줄 일이라도 있소, 커틀 선장?\""], ["he asked looking off it, with a smiling and expressive glance at the door.", "그가 신문에서 눈을 떼며, 미소 띤 의미심장한 눈길로 문 쪽을 보며 물었다."]], [["“I wish you could set my mind at rest, Sir, on something it’s uneasy about,”", "\"선생, 제 마음이 한 가지 불안해하는 게 있는데, 그걸 좀 가라앉혀 주셨으면 합니다,\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 응수했다."]], [["“Ay!”", "\"이런!\""], ["exclaimed the Manager, “what’s that?", "지배인이 외쳤다. \"그게 뭐요?"], ["Come, Captain Cuttle, I must trouble you to be quick, if you please.", "자, 커틀 선장, 미안하지만 좀 빨리 말해 주시오."], ["I am much engaged.”", "내가 일이 많이 밀려 있어서요.\""]], [["“Lookee here, Sir,”", "\"이걸 좀 보십쇼, 선생,\""], ["said the Captain, advancing a step.", "선장이 한 발짝 다가서며 말했다."], ["“Afore my friend Wal”r went on this here disastrous voyage—”", "\"제 친구 월이 이번 그 재앙 같은 항해를 떠나기 전에—\""]], [["“Come, come, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"자, 자, 커틀 선장,\""], ["interposed the smiling Manager, “don’t talk about disastrous voyages in that way.", "미소 띤 지배인이 끼어들었다. \"그런 식으로 재앙 같은 항해 운운하지 마시오."], ["We have nothing to do with disastrous voyages here, my good fellow.", "여기서 우리는 재앙 같은 항해와는 아무 상관이 없소, 이 양반아."], ["You must have begun very early on your day’s allowance, Captain, if you don’t remember that there are hazards in all voyages, whether by sea or land.", "바다든 뭍이든 모든 항해에는 위험이 따른다는 걸 기억 못 한다면, 선장, 당신은 오늘치 술 배급을 아주 일찍부터 시작한 게 틀림없소."], ["You are not made uneasy by the supposition that young what’s-his-name was lost in bad weather that was got up against him in these offices—are you?", "그 뭐라더라 하는 젊은이가 이 사무소에서 그에게 불리하게 꾸며진 험한 날씨에 죽은 거라는 억측 때문에 불안한 건 아니겠지요—그렇소?"], ["Fie, Captain!", "쳇, 선장!"], ["Sleep, and soda-water, are the best cures for such uneasiness as that.”", "그런 종류의 불안에는 잠과 소다수가 가장 좋은 약이오.\""]], [["“My lad,”", "\"이보시오,\""], ["returned the Captain, slowly—“you are a’most a lad to me, and so I don’t ask your pardon for that slip of a word,—if you find any pleasure in this here sport, you ain’t the gentleman I took you for.", "선장이 천천히 응수했다. \"당신은 나한테는 거의 애송이뻘이니, 그 말이 좀 헛나갔어도 양해를 구하진 않겠소—만일 당신이 이런 장난에서 무슨 재미를 느낀다면, 당신은 내가 생각했던 그런 신사가 아니오."], ["And if you ain’t the gentleman I took you for, may be my mind has call to be uneasy.", "그리고 만일 당신이 내가 생각했던 그런 신사가 아니라면, 어쩌면 내 마음이 불안해할 만한 까닭이 있는 거요."], ["Now this is what it is, Mr Carker.—Afore that poor lad went away, according to orders, he told me that he warn’t a going away for his own good, or for promotion, he know’d.", "자, 그게 뭐냐면 이렇소, 카커 씨—그 가엾은 녀석이 명령대로 떠나기 전에, 그가 나한테 말하길, 자기는 자기 잘되라고 가는 것도, 승진하러 가는 것도 아니라는 걸 알고 있다고 했소."], ["It was my belief that he was wrong, and I told him so, and I come here, your head governor being absent, to ask a question or two of you in a civil way, for my own satisfaction.", "그건 그 애 생각이 틀린 거라는 게 내 믿음이었고, 그래서 내가 그에게 그렇게 말했소. 그리고 내가 여기로 와서, 당신네 우두머리 총책임자가 안 계셨기에, 내 마음 편하자고 점잖게 당신한테 한두 가지를 물었소."], ["Them questions you answered—free.", "그 물음들에 당신은—거리낌 없이 답해 주었지요."], ["Now it’ll ease my mind to know, when all is over, as it is, and when what can’t be cured must be endoored—for which, as a scholar, you’ll overhaul the book it’s in, and thereof make a note—to know once more, in a word, that I warn’t mistaken; that I warn’t back’ard in my duty when I didn’t tell the old man what Wal”r told me; and that the wind was truly in his sail, when he highsted of it for Barbados Harbour.", "자 이제, 모든 게 다 끝나 버린 지금—정말 끝났으니—그리고 고칠 수 없는 것은 견뎌야만 하는 지금—그건, 학식 있는 양반이니, 그 말이 든 책을 찾아보고 거기에 한마디 적어 두시오—내가 틀리지 않았었다는 걸, 월이 나한테 한 말을 노인에게 전하지 않았을 때 내가 내 의무에 소홀했던 게 아니라는 걸, 그리고 그가 바베이도스 항을 향해 돛을 올렸을 때 바람이 정말로 그의 돛에 들어 있었다는 걸, 한 번 더 한마디로 아는 것이 내 마음을 놓이게 할 거요."], ["Mr Carker,”", "카커 씨,\""], ["said the Captain, in the goodness of his nature, “when I was here last, we was very pleasant together.", "선장이 천성의 선량함으로 말했다. \"지난번에 내가 여기 왔을 때, 우리는 아주 유쾌하게 지냈지요."], ["If I ain’t been altogether so pleasant myself this morning, on account of this poor lad, and if I have chafed again any observation of yours that I might have fended off, my name is Ed’ard Cuttle, and I ask your pardon.”", "만일 이 가엾은 녀석 때문에 오늘 아침 내가 그만큼 유쾌하지 못했다면, 그리고 내가 피해 갈 수도 있었을 당신의 어떤 말에 다시 발끈했다면, 내 이름은 에드워드 커틀이오, 그리고 양해를 구하겠소.\""]], [["“Captain Cuttle,”", "\"커틀 선장,\""], ["returned the Manager, with all possible politeness, “I must ask you to do me a favour.”", "지배인이 더없이 정중하게 응수했다. \"내 부탁 하나 들어주시오.\""]], [["“And what is it, Sir?”", "\"그게 뭡니까, 선생?\""], ["inquired the Captain.", "선장이 물었다."]], [["“To have the goodness to walk off, if you please,”", "\"미안하지만 나가 주시오,\""], ["rejoined the Manager, stretching forth his arm, “and to carry your jargon somewhere else.”", "지배인이 팔을 쭉 뻗으며 응수했다. \"그리고 그 횡설수설은 다른 데 가서 늘어놓으시오.\""]], [["Every knob in the Captain’s face turned white with astonishment and indignation; even the red rim on his forehead faded, like a rainbow among the gathering clouds.", "선장 얼굴의 마디마디가 놀라움과 분노로 하얗게 질렸다. 이마의 붉은 테두리마저, 몰려드는 구름 사이의 무지개처럼 빛이 바랬다."]], [["“I tell you what, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"내 한마디 하겠소, 커틀 선장,\""], ["said the Manager, shaking his forefinger at him, and showing him all his teeth, but still amiably smiling, “I was much too lenient with you when you came here before.", "지배인이 그에게 집게손가락을 흔들고 이를 죄다 드러내 보이면서도 여전히 상냥하게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"지난번 당신이 여기 왔을 때 나는 당신한테 너무 너그러웠소."], ["You belong to an artful and audacious set of people.", "당신은 교활하고 뻔뻔한 무리에 속한 자요."], ["In my desire to save young what’s-his-name from being kicked out of this place, neck and crop, my good Captain, I tolerated you; but for once, and only once.", "그 뭐라더라 하는 젊은이가 이곳에서 목덜미를 잡혀 쫓겨나는 걸 막아 주고 싶은 마음에서, 이 양반아, 내가 당신을 봐준 거요. 하지만 그건 한 번, 단 한 번뿐이오."], ["Now, go, my friend!”", "자, 가시오, 친구!\""]], [["The Captain was absolutely rooted to the ground, and speechless—", "선장은 완전히 땅에 뿌리박힌 듯 꼼짝 못 하고, 말문이 막혔다—"]], [["“Go,”", "\"가시오,\""], ["said the good-humoured Manager, gathering up his skirts, and standing astride upon the hearth-rug, “like a sensible fellow, and let us have no turning out, or any such violent measures.", "기분 좋아진 지배인이 옷자락을 추슬러 잡고 난로 깔개 위에 두 다리를 벌리고 서서 말했다. \"분별 있는 사람답게 말이오, 그리고 끌어내는 일이라든가 뭐 그런 난폭한 수단까지 가지는 맙시다."], ["If Mr Dombey were here, Captain, you might be obliged to leave in a more ignominious manner, possibly.", "만일 돔비 씨가 여기 계셨다면, 선장, 당신은 어쩌면 더 망신스러운 꼴로 떠나야 했을지도 모르오."], ["I merely say, Go!”", "나는 그저, 가라고 말할 뿐이오!\""]], [["The Captain, laying his ponderous hand upon his chest, to assist himself in fetching a deep breath, looked at Mr Carker from head to foot, and looked round the little room, as if he did not clearly understand where he was, or in what company.", "선장은 깊은 숨을 들이쉬는 데 보탬이 되도록 묵직한 손을 가슴에 얹고, 카커 씨를 머리부터 발끝까지 훑어보고는, 자기가 지금 어디에 있는지, 어떤 무리 속에 있는지 도무지 분명히 이해되지 않는 듯 그 자그마한 방을 둘러보았다."]], [["“You are deep, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"속이 깊은 양반이로군, 커틀 선장,\""], ["pursued Carker, with the easy and vivacious frankness of a man of the world who knew the world too well to be ruffled by any discovery of misdoing, when it did not immediately concern himself, “but you are not quite out of soundings, either—neither you nor your absent friend, Captain.", "카커가, 세상을 너무도 잘 알아서 자기와 직접 관련되지 않는 한 어떤 비행이 드러나도 흐트러지지 않는 세상물정 밝은 사람의 느긋하고 활기찬 솔직함으로 말을 이었다. \"하지만 당신도 수심을 다 벗어난 건 아니오—당신도, 당신의 그 사라진 친구도 말이오, 선장."], ["What have you done with your absent friend, hey?”", "그 사라진 친구를 당신은 어떻게 한 거요, 응?\""]], [["Again the Captain laid his hand upon his chest.", "다시 선장은 가슴에 손을 얹었다."], ["After drawing another deep breath, he conjured himself to “stand by!”", "또 한 번 깊은 숨을 들이쉰 뒤, 그는 자기 자신에게 \"대기하라!\"고 다짐했다."], ["But in a whisper.", "그러나 속삭임으로."]], [["“You hatch nice little plots, and hold nice little councils, and make nice little appointments, and receive nice little visitors, too, Captain, hey?”", "\"앙증맞은 음모도 꾸미고, 앙증맞은 회의도 열고, 앙증맞은 약속도 잡고, 앙증맞은 손님도 맞이하지요, 선장, 응?\""], ["said Carker, bending his brows upon him, without showing his teeth any the less: “but it’s a bold measure to come here afterwards.", "카커가, 이를 여전히 드러낸 채로 그에게 눈썹을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"하지만 그러고 나서 여기로 오다니, 대담한 짓이오."], ["Not like your discretion!", "당신답지 않게 경솔하구려!"], ["You conspirators, and hiders, and runners-away, should know better than that.", "당신네 음모꾼들, 숨는 자들, 도망치는 자들은 그보다 잘 알아야 마땅한 법이오."], ["Will you oblige me by going?”", "좀 나가 주시겠소?\""]], [["“My lad,”", "\"이보게나,\""], ["gasped the Captain, in a choked and trembling voice, and with a curious action going on in the ponderous fist; “there’s a many words I could wish to say to you, but I don’t rightly know where they’re stowed just at present.", "선장이 목멘 떨리는 목소리로, 그 묵직한 주먹에 묘한 움직임이 일어나는 채로 헐떡였다. \"자네한테 하고 싶은 말이 많네만, 그것들이 지금 당장은 어디 처박혀 있는지 영 모르겠네."], ["My young friend, Wal”r, was drownded only last night, according to my reckoning, and it puts me out, you see.", "내 젊은 친구 월이, 내 셈으로는 바로 지난밤에 물에 빠져 죽었으니, 그래서 내가 정신이 없네, 알겠나."], ["But you and me will come alongside o’one another again, my lad,”", "하지만 자네와 나는 다시 한번 나란히 마주 서게 될 걸세, 이 사람아,\""], ["said the Captain, holding up his hook, “if we live.”", "선장이 갈고리를 치켜들며 말했다. \"우리가 살아 있다면 말이야.\""]], [["“It will be anything but shrewd in you, my good fellow, if we do,”", "\"그렇게 된다면, 이 양반아, 그건 당신한테 결코 영리한 일이 못 될 거요,\""], ["returned the Manager, with the same frankness; “for you may rely, I give you fair warning, upon my detecting and exposing you.", "지배인이 똑같은 솔직함으로 응수했다. \"장담컨대, 분명히 경고하는데, 내가 반드시 당신을 적발하고 폭로할 테니까."], ["I don’t pretend to be a more moral man than my neighbours, my good Captain; but the confidence of this House, or of any member of this House, is not to be abused and undermined while I have eyes and ears.", "나는 내 이웃들보다 더 도덕적인 사람인 척하지는 않소, 이 양반아. 하지만 내 눈과 귀가 살아 있는 한, 이 회사의, 또는 이 회사 구성원 누구의 신뢰가 악용되고 잠식당하게 두지는 않을 거요."], ["Good day!”", "그럼 안녕히 가시오!\""], ["said Mr Carker, nodding his head.", "카커 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["Captain Cuttle, looking at him steadily (Mr Carker looked full as steadily at the Captain), went out of the office and left him standing astride before the fire, as calm and pleasant as if there were no more spots upon his soul than on his pure white linen, and his smooth sleek skin.", "커틀 선장은 그를 똑바로 바라보며(카커 씨도 꼭 그만큼 똑바로 선장을 바라보았다) 사무실 밖으로 나갔고, 그를 난롯불 앞에 두 다리를 벌리고 선 채로 남겨 두었다. 그는 마치 자기 영혼에도, 자기의 순백 아마포나 매끄럽고 윤기 흐르는 살갗만큼이나 한 점 얼룩도 없다는 듯 태연하고 흐뭇한 모습이었다."]], [["The Captain glanced, in passing through the outer counting-house, at the desk where he knew poor Walter had been used to sit, now occupied by another young boy, with a face almost as fresh and hopeful as his on the day when they tapped the famous last bottle but one of the old Madeira, in the little back parlour.", "바깥 회계실을 지나면서 선장은, 가엾은 월터가 늘 앉곤 하던 책상을 흘끗 보았는데, 이제 거기는 다른 어린 소년이 차지하고 있었다. 그 얼굴은 자그마한 뒤채 거실에서 옛 마데이라 술 마지막에서 두 번째 그 유명한 병의 마개를 따던 날의 월터 얼굴만큼이나 싱싱하고 희망에 차 있었다."], ["The nation of ideas, thus awakened, did the Captain a great deal of good; it softened him in the very height of his anger, and brought the tears into his eyes.", "이렇게 깨어난 한 무리의 생각들은 선장에게 큰 위안이 되었다. 그것은 분노가 한창 치솟은 순간에도 그를 누그러뜨렸고, 그의 눈에 눈물이 차오르게 했다."]], [["Arrived at the wooden Midshipman’s again, and sitting down in a corner of the dark shop, the Captain’s indignation, strong as it was, could make no head against his grief.", "다시 나무 항해사에게로 돌아와, 어두운 가게 한구석에 앉으니, 선장의 분노는, 그토록 강했건만, 그의 슬픔에 도무지 맞설 수가 없었다."], ["Passion seemed not only to do wrong and violence to the memory of the dead, but to be infected by death, and to droop and decline beside it.", "격정은 죽은 이의 기억에 부당하고 난폭한 짓을 하는 듯할 뿐 아니라, 죽음에 감염되어 그 곁에서 시들고 사그라드는 것 같았다."], ["All the living knaves and liars in the world, were nothing to the honesty and truth of one dead friend.", "세상의 살아 있는 온갖 악당과 거짓말쟁이들도, 죽은 친구 한 사람의 정직과 진실에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다."]], [["The only thing the honest Captain made out clearly, in this state of mind, besides the loss of Walter, was, that with him almost the whole world of Captain Cuttle had been drowned.", "이런 마음 상태에서 정직한 선장이 월터를 잃었다는 것 말고 분명히 깨달은 단 한 가지는, 그와 함께 커틀 선장의 세계가 거의 통째로 물에 빠져 버렸다는 것이었다."], ["If he reproached himself sometimes, and keenly too, for having ever connived at Walter’s innocent deceit, he thought at least as often of the Mr Carker whom no sea could ever render up; and the Mr Dombey, whom he now began to perceive was as far beyond human recall; and the “Heart’s Delight,” with whom he must never foregather again; and the Lovely Peg, that teak-built and trim ballad, that had gone ashore upon a rock, and split into mere planks and beams of rhyme.", "월터의 그 천진한 속임수를 한때나마 묵인했던 것을 두고 가끔, 그것도 통렬하게 자책하긴 했지만, 그는 적어도 그만큼 자주, 어떤 바다도 결코 돌려보내 줄 수 없는 카커 씨를 생각했고, 이제 그도 마찬가지로 인간의 부름이 닿지 않을 만큼 멀리 가 버렸음을 깨닫기 시작한 돔비 씨를 생각했으며, 다시는 함께 어울릴 수 없게 된 \"마음의 기쁨\"을 생각했고, 바위에 좌초하여 운율의 널빤지와 들보로 쪼개져 버린, 티크로 단단히 짜인 그 말끔한 노래 \"사랑스러운 페그\"를 생각했다."], ["The Captain sat in the dark shop, thinking of these things, to the entire exclusion of his own injury; and looking with as sad an eye upon the ground, as if in contemplation of their actual fragments, as they floated past.", "선장은 어두운 가게에 앉아, 자기가 입은 상처는 전혀 안중에 두지 않은 채 이런 것들을 생각했고, 마치 떠내려가는 그 실제 잔해들을 바라보기라도 하듯 슬픈 눈으로 바닥을 내려다보았다."]], [["But the Captain was not unmindful, for all that, of such decent and rest observances in memory of poor Walter, as he felt within his power.", "그러나 그러면서도 선장은, 가엾은 월터를 기리는 점잖고 차분한 추모의 의식을, 자기 힘이 닿는 한 잊지 않았다."], ["Rousing himself, and rousing Rob the Grinder (who in the unnatural twilight was fast asleep), the Captain sallied forth with his attendant at his heels, and the door-key in his pocket, and repairing to one of those convenient slop-selling establishments of which there is abundant choice at the eastern end of London, purchased on the spot two suits of mourning—one for Rob the Grinder, which was immensely too small, and one for himself, which was immensely too large.", "기운을 차리고, 그라인더 롭(이 부자연스러운 황혼 속에서 곤히 잠들어 있던)을 깨워서, 선장은 자기 시종을 뒤에 거느리고 문 열쇠를 주머니에 넣은 채 밖으로 나섰다. 그리고 런던 동쪽 끝에 가면 얼마든지 골라잡을 수 있는 그 편리한 헌옷 가게들 가운데 한 곳으로 가서, 그 자리에서 상복 두 벌을 샀다—하나는 그라인더 롭의 것으로 엄청나게 작았고, 하나는 자기 것으로 엄청나게 컸다."], ["He also provided Rob with a species of hat, greatly to be admired for its symmetry and usefulness, as well as for a happy blending of the mariner with the coal-heaver; which is usually termed a sou’wester; and which was something of a novelty in connexion with the instrument business.", "그는 또 롭에게 모자 비슷한 것을 마련해 주었는데, 균형미와 실용성으로 보나, 뱃사람과 석탄 운반부가 절묘하게 어우러진 점으로 보나 크게 감탄할 만한 것으로, 흔히 사우웨스터라 불리는 것이었다. 그것은 기구 장사와 관련해서는 다소 색다른 물건이었다."], ["In their several garments, which the vendor declared to be such a miracle in point of fit as nothing but a rare combination of fortuitous circumstances ever brought about, and the fashion of which was unparalleled within the memory of the oldest inhabitant, the Captain and Grinder immediately arrayed themselves: presenting a spectacle fraught with wonder to all who beheld it.", "각자의 옷을 차려입으니—파는 자가 단언하기로는, 어쩌다 운 좋은 사정들이 드물게 맞아떨어진 것 외에는 도무지 빚어질 수 없는 맞음새의 기적이요, 그 맵시로 말하자면 가장 나이 든 주민의 기억으로도 견줄 데 없는 것이라 했다—선장과 그라인더는 즉시 그것들을 떨쳐입었고, 그것을 바라보는 모든 이에게 경이로 가득 찬 광경을 연출했다. * 사우웨스터: 차양이 뒤로 길게 늘어진, 방수 처리된 뱃사람용 모자."]], [["In this altered form, the Captain received Mr Toots.", "이렇게 달라진 모습으로 선장은 투츠 씨를 맞이했다."], ["“I’m took aback, my lad, at present,”", "\"지금은 내가 어찌할 바를 모르겠네, 이 사람아,\" 선장이 말했다."], ["said the Captain, “and will only confirm that there ill news.", "\"그러니 그저 그 나쁜 소식을 확실하다고 일러 줄 수밖에. 그 젊은 여자한테 아가씨에게 부드럽게 알리라고 전해 주게."], ["Tell the young woman to break it gentle to the young lady, and for neither of ’em never to think of me no more—“special, mind you, that is—though I will think of them, when night comes on a hurricane and seas is mountains rowling, for which overhaul your Doctor Watts, brother, and when found make a note on.”", "그리고 둘 다 다시는 나를 생각하지 말라고—특히, 잘 듣게, 그게 중요한데—하지만 밤이 허리케인으로 닥쳐오고 바다가 산처럼 굽이칠 때면 나는 그들을 생각할 걸세. 그건 자네 닥터 와츠를 찾아보게, 형제여, 그리고 찾거든 거기에 한마디 적어 두게.\""]], [["The Captain reserved, until some fitter time, the consideration of Mr Toots’s offer of friendship, and thus dismissed him.", "선장은 투츠 씨의 우정 제안에 대한 검토는 좀 더 적당한 때로 미뤄 두고서, 그를 그렇게 돌려보냈다."], ["Captain Cuttle’s spirits were so low, in truth, that he half determined, that day, to take no further precautions against surprise from Mrs MacStinger, but to abandon himself recklessly to chance, and be indifferent to what might happen.", "사실 커틀 선장의 기운이 어찌나 가라앉았던지, 그날 그는 맥스팅거 부인의 기습에 맞선 더 이상의 예방책은 일절 취하지 않고, 오히려 무모하게 운에 자기 자신을 내맡긴 채 무슨 일이 일어나든 개의치 않기로 거의 마음을 굳히다시피 했다."], ["As evening came on, he fell into a better frame of mind, however; and spoke much of Walter to Rob the Grinder, whose attention and fidelity he likewise incidentally commended.", "그러나 저녁이 다가오자 그는 한결 나은 마음 상태가 되었고, 그라인더 롭에게 월터에 관해 많은 이야기를 했다. 그러면서 곁들여 롭의 주의 깊음과 충실함도 칭찬했다."], ["Rob did not blush to hear the Captain earnest in his praises, but sat staring at him, and affecting to snivel with sympathy, and making a feint of being virtuous, and treasuring up every word he said (like a young spy as he was) with very promising deceit.", "롭은 선장이 진지하게 자기를 칭찬하는 것을 듣고도 부끄러워하기는커녕, 그를 빤히 쳐다보며 앉아, 공감하는 척 훌쩍이고, 착한 척 시늉을 하면서, (어린 첩자답게) 그가 하는 말 한마디 한마디를 매우 장래가 촉망되는 교활함으로 차곡차곡 쌓아 두었다."]], [["When Rob had turned in, and was fast asleep, the Captain trimmed the candle, put on his spectacles—he had felt it appropriate to take to spectacles on entering into the Instrument Trade, though his eyes were like a hawk’s—and opened the prayer-book at the Burial Service.", "롭이 잠자리에 들어 곤히 잠들자, 선장은 촛불 심지를 다듬고 안경을 썼다—기구 장사에 들어선 마당에 안경을 쓰는 것이 어울린다고 여겼던 것이다, 비록 그의 눈은 매의 눈 같았지만—그러고는 기도서를 장례 예식 부분에서 펼쳤다."], ["And reading softly to himself, in the little back parlour, and stopping now and then to wipe his eyes, the Captain, in a true and simple spirit, committed Walter’s body to the deep.", "그리하여 자그마한 뒤채 거실에서 혼자 나직이 읽어 내려가며, 이따금 멈추어 눈물을 훔치면서, 선장은 참되고 소박한 마음으로 월터의 몸을 깊은 바다에 맡겼다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIII. Contrasts", "# CHAPTER XXXIII. Contrasts"]], [["Turn we our eyes upon two homes; not lying side by side, but wide apart, though both within easy range and reach of the great city of London.", "우리의 눈을 두 집으로 돌려 보자. 나란히 붙어 있지는 않고 멀리 떨어져 있으나, 둘 다 런던이라는 대도시에서 어렵지 않게 닿고 미칠 수 있는 거리에 있는 집들이다."]], [["The first is situated in the green and wooded country near Norwood.", "첫 번째 집은 노우드 근처의 푸르고 숲이 우거진 시골에 자리하고 있다."], ["It is not a mansion; it is of no pretensions as to size; but it is beautifully arranged, and tastefully kept.", "그것은 대저택은 아니며, 크기에 있어 거창함을 내세우지도 않는다. 그러나 아름답게 꾸며져 있고, 품위 있게 관리되고 있다."], ["The lawn, the soft, smooth slope, the flower-garden, the clumps of trees where graceful forms of ash and willow are not wanting, the conservatory, the rustic verandah with sweet-smelling creeping plants entwined about the pillars, the simple exterior of the house, the well-ordered offices, though all upon the diminutive scale proper to a mere cottage, bespeak an amount of elegant comfort within, that might serve for a palace.", "잔디밭, 부드럽고 매끄러운 비탈, 화원, 우아한 자태의 물푸레나무와 버드나무가 빠지지 않는 나무 무리, 온실, 향기로운 덩굴식물이 기둥을 휘감은 시골풍 베란다, 소박한 집의 외관, 잘 정돈된 부속 건물들—이 모든 것이 그저 한 채의 오두막에나 어울릴 자그마한 규모이긴 하나, 안에는 궁전에나 쓰일 법한 우아한 안락함이 어느 정도 갖추어져 있음을 말해 준다."], ["This indication is not without warrant; for, within, it is a house of refinement and luxury.", "이 짐작은 근거 없는 것이 아니니, 안으로 들어가 보면 그곳은 세련됨과 호사로 가득한 집이다."], ["Rich colours, excellently blended, meet the eye at every turn; in the furniture—its proportions admirably devised to suit the shapes and sizes of the small rooms; on the walls; upon the floors; tingeing and subduing the light that comes in through the odd glass doors and windows here and there.", "훌륭하게 어우러진 풍부한 색채가 어느 쪽으로 눈을 돌려도 마주친다. 작은 방들의 형태와 크기에 꼭 맞게 빼어나게 고안된 가구에서, 벽에서, 바닥에서, 그리고 여기저기 별난 유리문과 창문으로 들어오는 빛에 색을 입히고 그 빛을 누그러뜨리는 데서도."], ["There are a few choice prints and pictures too; in quaint nooks and recesses there is no want of books; and there are games of skill and chance set forth on tables—fantastic chessmen, dice, backgammon, cards, and billiards.", "정선된 판화와 그림도 몇 점 있고, 고풍스러운 구석진 곳과 우묵한 자리에는 책이 부족하지 않으며, 탁자 위에는 기술과 운을 겨루는 놀이들이 펼쳐져 있다—기이한 체스 말들, 주사위, 백개먼, 카드, 그리고 당구."]], [["And yet amidst this opulence of comfort, there is something in the general air that is not well.", "그러나 이런 안락의 풍요로움 가운데에도, 전체적인 분위기에는 어딘가 온당치 않은 무언가가 있다."], ["Is it that the carpets and the cushions are too soft and noiseless, so that those who move or repose among them seem to act by stealth?", "양탄자와 방석이 너무 부드럽고 소리 없는 탓에, 그 사이를 거닐거나 그 위에서 쉬는 이들이 마치 몰래 행동하는 듯 보여서일까?"], ["Is it that the prints and pictures do not commemorate great thoughts or deeds, or render nature in the Poetry of landscape, hall, or hut, but are of one voluptuous cast—mere shows of form and colour—and no more?", "판화와 그림들이 위대한 사상이나 행적을 기리지도 않고, 풍경이나 저택이나 오두막의 시정(詩情)으로 자연을 담아내지도 않으며, 오로지 관능적인 한 가지 색조로만—그저 형태와 색채의 과시에 불과하고 그 이상은 아닌 것으로만—이루어져서일까?"], ["Is it that the books have all their gold outside, and that the titles of the greater part qualify them to be companions of the prints and pictures?", "책들이 전부 겉만 금박을 입혔고, 그 대부분의 제목이 판화와 그림의 동무가 되기에나 알맞아서일까?"], ["Is it that the completeness and the beauty of the place are here and there belied by an affectation of humility, in some unimportant and inexpensive regard, which is as false as the face of the too truly painted portrait hanging yonder, or its original at breakfast in his easy chair below it?", "이곳의 완전함과 아름다움이 군데군데, 대수롭지 않고 돈도 들지 않는 어떤 면에서 겸손을 가장함으로써 배반당하고 있어서일까—그 가장이 저기 걸려 있는, 너무도 진실되게 그려진 초상화의 얼굴만큼이나, 아니 그 아래 안락의자에 앉아 아침을 들고 있는 그 초상의 실제 인물만큼이나 거짓되어서?"], ["Or is it that, with the daily breath of that original and master of all here, there issues forth some subtle portion of himself, which gives a vague expression of himself to everything about him?", "아니면, 이곳의 그 실제 인물이자 이 모든 것의 주인이 날마다 내쉬는 숨결과 더불어, 그 자신의 어떤 미묘한 일부가 흘러나와, 주위의 모든 것에 그 자신을 어렴풋이 드러내고 있어서일까?"]], [["It is Mr Carker the Manager who sits in the easy chair.", "안락의자에 앉아 있는 이는 지배인 카커 씨다."], ["A gaudy parrot in a burnished cage upon the table tears at the wires with her beak, and goes walking, upside down, in its dome-top, shaking her house and screeching; but Mr Carker is indifferent to the bird, and looks with a musing smile at a picture on the opposite wall.", "탁자 위의 번쩍이는 새장 안에서 화려한 앵무새 한 마리가 부리로 철사를 물어뜯고, 둥근 천장 꼭대기에서 거꾸로 매달려 걷다가 제 집을 흔들며 꽥꽥거린다. 그러나 카커 씨는 그 새에 무심한 채, 사색에 잠긴 미소를 머금고 맞은편 벽의 그림을 바라본다."]], [["“A most extraordinary accidental likeness, certainly,”", "\"참으로 기이한 우연의 닮음이로군, 그래.\""], ["says he.", "그가 말한다."]], [["Perhaps it is a Juno; perhaps a Potiphar’s Wife”; perhaps some scornful Nymph—according as the Picture Dealers found the market, when they christened it.", "아마도 유노일 것이다. 아마도 \"보디발의 아내\"이거나, 아마도 어떤 경멸 어린 님프일 것이다—그림 상인들이 시장 형편을 보아 그것에 이름을 붙였을 테니까."], ["It is the figure of a woman, supremely handsome, who, turning away, but with her face addressed to the spectator, flashes her proud glance upon him.", "그것은 더없이 아름다운 한 여인의 모습으로, 몸은 돌아서 있으나 얼굴은 보는 이를 향한 채, 그에게 거만한 시선을 번뜩이고 있다."]], [["It is like Edith.", "그것은 이디스를 닮았다."]], [["With a passing gesture of his hand at the picture—what!", "그가 그 그림을 향해 한 손으로 스치듯 몸짓을 한다—저것이 무엇인가!"], ["a menace?", "위협인가?"], ["No; yet something like it.", "아니다. 그러나 그것과 비슷한 무엇이다."], ["A wave as of triumph?", "승리의 손짓 같은 것인가?"], ["No; yet more like that.", "아니다. 그러나 그것에 더 가깝다."], ["An insolent salute wafted from his lips?", "그의 입술에서 띄워 보낸 무례한 인사인가? 아니다."], ["No; yet like that too—he resumes his breakfast, and calls to the chafing and imprisoned bird, who coming down into a pendant gilded hoop within the cage, like a great wedding-ring, swings in it, for his delight.", "그러나 그것과도 비슷하다—그는 다시 아침을 들며, 안달하며 갇혀 있는 새를 부른다. 새는 새장 안에 매달린, 커다란 결혼반지 같은 금빛 고리 속으로 내려와 그 안에서 그네를 타며 그를 즐겁게 한다."]], [["The second home is on the other side of London, near to where the busy great north road of bygone days is silent and almost deserted, except by wayfarers who toil along on foot.", "두 번째 집은 런던의 반대편에 있는데, 지난날의 분주하던 큰 북부 가도가 이제는 조용하고, 발로 터덜터덜 걸어가는 나그네들 말고는 거의 버려진 곳 가까이에 있다. 그것은 빈약하고 자그마한 집으로, 가구도 변변찮고 듬성듬성하나 매우 깨끗하다."], ["It is a poor small house, barely and sparely furnished, but very clean; and there is even an attempt to decorate it, shown in the homely flowers trained about the porch and in the narrow garden.", "게다가 현관 둘레와 좁은 정원에 정성껏 가꾼 소박한 꽃들에서 보이듯, 그곳을 꾸미려는 시도마저 엿보인다. 그 집이 서 있는 동네는 도시다운 면이 적은 만큼이나 시골다운 면도 추천할 것이 별로 없다."], ["The neighbourhood in which it stands has as little of the country to recommend it, as it has of the town.", "그곳은 도시도 아니고 시골도 아니다. 도시는, 여행용 장화를 신은 거인처럼, 성큼 한 걸음을 내디뎌 그곳을 지나쳐 버렸고, 벽돌과 회반죽으로 된 발뒤꿈치를 한참 앞에 디뎌 놓았다."], ["It is neither of the town nor country.", "그러나 그 거인의 두 발 사이에 낀 중간 지대는 아직 황폐해진 시골일 뿐 도시는 아니다."], ["The former, like the giant in his travelling boots, has made a stride and passed it, and has set his brick-and-mortar heel a long way in advance; but the intermediate space between the giant’s feet, as yet, is only blighted country, and not town; and, here, among a few tall chimneys belching smoke all day and night, and among the brick-fields and the lanes where turf is cut, and where the fences tumble down, and where the dusty nettles grow, and where a scrap or two of hedge may yet be seen, and where the bird-catcher still comes occasionally, though he swears every time to come no more—this second home is to be found.”", "그리고 이곳, 밤낮으로 종일 연기를 토해 내는 몇 개의 높다란 굴뚝들 사이에, 벽돌 가마터와 잔디를 떠낸 좁은 길들 사이에, 울타리가 무너져 내리고, 먼지 낀 쐐기풀이 자라고, 산울타리 자투리 한두 조각이 아직 보이며, 매번 다시는 오지 않겠노라 맹세하면서도 여전히 이따금 찾아오는 새잡이가 드나드는 그 사이에—이 두 번째 집이 자리하고 있다."]], [["She who inhabits it, is she who left the first in her devotion to an outcast brother.", "그곳에 사는 여인은, 버림받은 오라비를 향한 헌신으로 첫 번째 집을 떠난 바로 그 여인이다."], ["She withdrew from that home its redeeming spirit, and from its master’s breast his solitary angel: but though his liking for her is gone, after this ungrateful slight as he considers it; and though he abandons her altogether in return, an old idea of her is not quite forgotten even by him.", "그녀는 그 집에서 그곳을 구원하던 영혼을 거두어 갔고, 그 집 주인의 가슴에서 그의 유일한 천사를 거두어 갔다. 그러나 그가 배은망덕한 모욕이라 여기는 그 일이 있은 뒤로 그녀를 향한 그의 애정은 사라지고, 그 보복으로 그가 그녀를 완전히 저버리긴 했어도, 그녀에 대한 옛 정념마저 그에게서 아주 잊힌 것은 아니다."], ["Let her flower-garden, in which he never sets his foot, but which is yet maintained, among all his costly alterations, as if she had quitted it but yesterday, bear witness!", "그가 결코 발을 들이지는 않으면서도, 그의 그 값비싼 온갖 개조 가운데에서도, 마치 그녀가 어제 막 떠나기라도 한 듯 여전히 가꾸어지고 있는 그녀의 화원이 그 증거가 되게 하라!"]], [["Harriet Carker has changed since then, and on her beauty there has fallen a heavier shade than Time of his unassisted self can cast, all-potent as he is—the shadow of anxiety and sorrow, and the daily struggle of a poor existence.", "해리엇 카커는 그때 이후로 변했고, 그녀의 아름다움에는, 세월이 제아무리 전능하다 한들 그 홀로의 힘만으로 드리울 수 있는 것보다 더 무거운 그늘이 내려앉았다—근심과 슬픔의 그늘, 그리고 가난한 삶의 나날의 분투라는 그늘이."], ["But it is beauty still; and still a gentle, quiet, and retiring beauty that must be sought out, for it cannot vaunt itself; if it could, it would be what it is, no more.", "그러나 그것은 여전히 아름다움이며, 여전히 온화하고 조용하며 수줍은 아름다움이어서, 스스로를 자랑할 수 없기에 찾아내야만 하는 아름다움이다. 만일 그것이 자랑할 수 있었다면, 그것은 지금의 그것에 지나지 않았을 것이다."]], [["Yes.", "그렇다."], ["This slight, small, patient figure, neatly dressed in homely stuffs, and indicating nothing but the dull, household virtues, that have so little in common with the received idea of heroism and greatness, unless, indeed, any ray of them should shine through the lives of the great ones of the earth, when it becomes a constellation and is tracked in Heaven straightway—this slight, small, patient figure, leaning on the man still young but worn and grey, is she, his sister, who, of all the world, went over to him in his shame and put her hand in his, and with a sweet composure and determination, led him hopefully upon his barren way.", "이 가냘프고, 작고, 참을성 있는 모습은, 소박한 천으로 단정하게 차려입고, 영웅적이거나 위대하다는 통념과는 거의 공통점이 없는 따분한 살림의 미덕 말고는 아무것도 드러내지 않는다—다만 그러한 미덕의 한 줄기 빛이 이 땅의 위인들의 삶을 뚫고 비쳐 들 때, 그것은 하나의 별자리가 되어 곧장 하늘에서 자취가 좇아지긴 하지만 말이다—이 가냘프고, 작고, 참을성 있는 모습이, 아직 젊으나 지치고 머리가 센 한 남자에게 기대어 선 채, 온 세상 가운데 오직 그녀만이 그가 치욕에 빠졌을 때 그에게로 건너가 그의 손에 자기 손을 얹고, 달콤한 침착함과 결연함으로 그 메마른 길을 따라 희망차게 그를 이끌어 온, 그의 누이다."]], [["“It is early, John,”", "\"이르네요, 존.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Why do you go so early?”", "\"어째서 이렇게 일찍 나가세요?\""]], [["“Not many minutes earlier than usual, Harriet.", "\"평소보다 그리 많이 이른 건 아니야, 해리엇."], ["If I have the time to spare, I should like, I think—it’s a fancy—to walk once by the house where I took leave of him.”", "시간이 좀 남는다면, 내 생각엔—그저 변덕이지만—그와 작별을 나눈 그 집 앞을 한 번 걸어 보고 싶어서.\""]], [["“I wish I had ever seen or known him, John.”", "\"저도 그분을 한 번이라도 뵙거나 알았더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 존.\""]], [["“It is better as it is, my dear, remembering his fate.”", "\"이대로가 더 낫단다, 얘야, 그의 운명을 생각하면.\""]], [["“But I could not regret it more, though I had known him.", "\"하지만 그분을 알았다 한들 제가 더 안타까워할 수도 없었을 거예요."], ["Is not your sorrow mine?", "오라버니의 슬픔이 제 슬픔이 아닌가요?"], ["And if I had, perhaps you would feel that I was a better companion to you in speaking about him, than I may seem now.”", "그리고 제가 그분을 알았더라면, 어쩌면 그분에 대해 이야기할 때 제가 지금보다는 오라버니에게 더 나은 동무가 되어 드린다고 느끼셨을지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“My dearest sister!", "\"내 더없이 사랑하는 누이야!"], ["Is there anything within the range of rejoicing or regret, in which I am not sure of your companionship?”", "기쁨이든 회한이든, 그 어떤 것에서든 네가 함께해 주리라는 것을 내가 확신하지 못하는 것이 있겠느냐?\""]], [["“I hope you think not, John, for surely there is nothing!”", "\"없다고 생각하시길 바라요, 존. 정말로 아무것도 없으니까요!\""]], [["“How could you be better to me, or nearer to me then, than you are in this, or anything?”", "\"그러니 어떻게 네가 지금 이 일에서, 아니 어떤 일에서든, 지금의 너보다 내게 더 잘해 주거나 더 가까워질 수 있겠느냐?\""], ["said her brother.", "그녀의 오라비가 말했다."], ["“I feel that you did know him, Harriet, and that you shared my feelings towards him.”", "\"나는 네가 그를 알았다고, 그리고 그를 향한 내 감정을 함께 나누었다고 느낀단다, 해리엇.\""]], [["She drew the hand which had been resting on his shoulder, round his neck, and answered, with some hesitation:", "그녀는 그의 어깨에 얹혀 있던 손을 그의 목으로 둘러 감으며, 다소 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"아니요, 꼭 그렇지는 않아요.\""]], [["“No, not quite.”", "\"맞아, 맞아!\""], ["“True, true!”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said; “you think I might have done him no harm if I had allowed myself to know him better?”", "\"내가 나 자신에게 그를 더 잘 알도록 허락했더라면, 내가 그에게 해를 끼치지 않았으리라고 생각하는 거지?\""]], [["“Think!", "\"생각이라뇨!"], ["I know it.”", "저는 알아요.\""]], [["“Designedly, Heaven knows I would not,”", "\"일부러였다면, 하늘이 알겠지만 나는 결코 그러지 않았을 거다.\""], ["he replied, shaking his head mournfully; “but his reputation was too precious to be perilled by such association.", "그가 슬프게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"그러나 그의 평판은 너무도 소중해서, 그런 교제로 위태롭게 할 수는 없었어."], ["Whether you share that knowledge, or do not, my dear—”", "네가 그 생각을 함께하든 안 하든, 얘야—\""]], [["“I do not,”", "\"저는 함께하지 않아요.\""], ["she said quietly.", "그녀가 조용히 말했다."]], [["“It is still the truth, Harriet, and my mind is lighter when I think of him for that which made it so much heavier then.”", "\"그래도 그건 여전히 진실이란다, 해리엇. 그리고 그때 내 마음을 그토록 무겁게 했던 바로 그 일을 두고 그를 생각하면, 이제 내 마음은 한결 가벼워진다.\""], ["He checked himself in his tone of melancholy, and smiled upon her as he said “Good-bye!”", "그는 우울한 어조를 거두고, \"잘 있어!\" 하고 말하며 그녀에게 미소 지었다."]], [["“Good-bye, dear John!", "\"잘 다녀오세요, 사랑하는 존!"], ["In the evening, at the old time and place, I shall meet you as usual on your way home.", "저녁에, 늘 그 시간 그 자리에서, 평소처럼 돌아오시는 길에 오라버니를 마중할게요."], ["Good-bye.”", "다녀오세요.\""]], [["The cordial face she lifted up to his to kiss him, was his home, his life, his universe, and yet it was a portion of his punishment and grief; for in the cloud he saw upon it—though serene and calm as any radiant cloud at sunset—and in the constancy and devotion of her life, and in the sacrifice she had made of ease, enjoyment, and hope, he saw the bitter fruits of his old crime, for ever ripe and fresh.", "그에게 입을 맞추려 들어 올린 그녀의 다정한 얼굴은 그의 집이요, 그의 삶이요, 그의 우주였으나, 동시에 그것은 그가 받는 벌과 슬픔의 한 부분이기도 했다. 그가 그 얼굴 위에서 본 구름 속에서—해 질 녘의 어떤 빛나는 구름만큼이나 고요하고 잔잔한 구름이었지만—그리고 그녀의 삶의 한결같음과 헌신 속에서, 또 그녀가 안락과 즐거움과 희망을 희생한 데서, 그는 자신의 옛 죄가 맺은 쓰디쓴 열매가 영원히 익어 가고 신선하게 맺혀 있는 것을 보았던 것이다."]], [["She stood at the door looking after him, with her hands loosely clasped in each other, as he made his way over the frowzy and uneven patch of ground which lay before their house, which had once (and not long ago) been a pleasant meadow, and was now a very waste, with a disorderly crop of beginnings of mean houses, rising out of the rubbish, as if they had been unskilfully sown there.", "그가 그들 집 앞에 놓인, 칙칙하고 울퉁불퉁한 땅뙈기를 가로질러 가는 동안, 그녀는 두 손을 헐겁게 맞잡은 채 문가에 서서 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 그 땅은 한때(그리 오래지 않은 옛날) 기분 좋은 풀밭이었으나, 이제는 완전한 황무지가 되어, 누추한 집들이 쓰레기 더미에서 솟아나기 시작한 어수선한 무리를 이루고 있었으니, 마치 거기에 서투르게 씨를 뿌려 놓은 것만 같았다."], ["Whenever he looked back—as once or twice he did—her cordial face shone like a light upon his heart; but when he plodded on his way, and saw her not, the tears were in her eyes as she stood watching him.", "그가 뒤돌아볼 때마다—한두 번 그랬다—그녀의 다정한 얼굴은 그의 가슴을 비추는 한 줄기 빛처럼 빛났다. 그러나 그가 다시 터덜터덜 제 길을 가며 그녀를 보지 못할 때면, 그를 지켜보며 서 있는 그녀의 눈에는 눈물이 고였다."]], [["Her pensive form was not long idle at the door.", "그녀의 시름에 잠긴 모습은 오래도록 문가에서 한가로이 머물러 있지는 않았다."], ["There was daily duty to discharge, and daily work to do—for such commonplace spirits that are not heroic, often work hard with their hands—and Harriet was soon busy with her household tasks.", "날마다 해야 할 의무가 있었고, 날마다 해야 할 일이 있었으니—영웅적이지 못한 그런 평범한 영혼들은 흔히 제 손으로 고되게 일하는 법이다—해리엇은 곧 집안일로 분주해졌다."], ["These discharged, and the poor house made quite neat and orderly, she counted her little stock of money, with an anxious face, and went out thoughtfully to buy some necessaries for their table, planning and conniving, as she went, how to save.", "그 일들을 다 마치고 가난한 집을 아주 말끔하고 정연하게 해 놓은 뒤, 그녀는 근심 어린 얼굴로 얼마 안 되는 돈을 헤아리고는, 식탁에 올릴 필수품 몇 가지를 사러 생각에 잠긴 채 나갔다. 가는 길에 어떻게 아낄지 궁리하고 꾀를 짜내면서."], ["So sordid are the lives of such low natures, who are not only not heroic to their valets and waiting-women, but have neither valets nor waiting-women to be heroic to withal!", "그런 미천한 천성을 지닌 이들의 삶이란 이토록 구차하니, 그들은 제 하인이나 시녀에게 영웅이 못 될 뿐 아니라, 영웅 노릇을 할 하인도 시녀도 애초에 두지 못한 것이다!"]], [["While she was absent, and there was no one in the house, there approached it by a different way from that the brother had taken, a gentleman, a very little past his prime of life perhaps, but of a healthy florid hue, an upright presence, and a bright clear aspect, that was gracious and good-humoured.", "그녀가 집을 비우고 안에 아무도 없는 동안, 오라비가 갔던 길과는 다른 길로 그 집에 한 신사가 다가왔다. 한창때를 어쩌면 조금 지난 듯하나, 건강하게 혈색 좋은 안색에, 꼿꼿한 자태와, 밝고 맑은 풍모를 지녔으니, 그것은 우아하고 쾌활한 인상을 주었다."], ["His eyebrows were still black, and so was much of his hair; the sprinkling of grey observable among the latter, graced the former very much, and showed his broad frank brow and honest eyes to great advantage.", "그의 눈썹은 아직 검었고 머리카락도 상당 부분이 검었는데, 머리카락 사이로 군데군데 비치는 흰빛이 눈썹을 한결 돋보이게 했고, 그의 넓고 솔직한 이마와 정직한 눈을 크게 살려 주었다."]], [["After knocking once at the door, and obtaining no response, this gentleman sat down on a bench in the little porch to wait.", "이 신사는 문을 한 번 두드렸으나 아무 응답이 없자, 자그마한 현관의 긴 의자에 앉아 기다렸다."], ["A certain skilful action of his fingers as he hummed some bars, and beat time on the seat beside him, seemed to denote the musician; and the extraordinary satisfaction he derived from humming something very slow and long, which had no recognisable tune, seemed to denote that he was a scientific one.", "그가 몇 소절을 흥얼거리며 옆자리의 좌석을 두드려 박자를 맞출 때, 손가락을 놀리는 어떤 능숙한 동작은 그가 음악가임을 드러내는 듯했다. 그리고 알아챌 만한 곡조라곤 없는, 매우 느리고 긴 무언가를 흥얼거리는 데서 그가 얻는 그 유별난 만족감은, 그가 학식 있는 음악가임을 드러내는 듯했다."]], [["The gentleman was still twirling a theme, which seemed to go round and round and round, and in and in and in, and to involve itself like a corkscrew twirled upon a table, without getting any nearer to anything, when Harriet appeared returning.", "그 신사가 여전히 어떤 선율을 빙빙 돌리고 있을 때—그것은 빙빙 돌고 또 돌고, 안으로 들고 또 들며, 탁자 위에서 돌려진 타래송곳처럼 제 몸을 휘감을 뿐, 어디에도 조금도 가까이 다가가지 못하는 듯했다—해리엇이 돌아오는 모습이 보였다."], ["He rose up as she advanced, and stood with his head uncovered.", "그녀가 다가오자 그는 일어서서, 모자를 벗은 채로 섰다."]], [["“You are come again, Sir!”", "\"또 오셨군요, 선생님!\""], ["she said, faltering.", "그녀가 더듬거리며 말했다."]], [["“I take that liberty,”", "\"외람된 줄 압니다.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“May I ask for five minutes of your leisure?”", "\"잠시 오 분만 시간을 내주실 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["After a moment’s hesitation, she opened the door, and gave him admission to the little parlour.", "잠시 망설인 뒤, 그녀는 문을 열고 그를 자그마한 응접실로 들였다."], ["The gentleman sat down there, drew his chair to the table over against her, and said, in a voice that perfectly corresponded to his appearance, and with a simplicity that was very engaging:", "신사는 거기 앉아 의자를 끌어다 그녀 맞은편 탁자 곁에 두고, 그의 외모와 완벽하게 어울리는 목소리로, 매우 마음을 끄는 꾸밈없음으로 말했다."]], [["“Miss Harriet, you cannot be proud.", "\"해리엇 양, 당신은 오만할 수가 없는 분입니다."], ["You signified to me, when I called t’other morning, that you were.", "지난번 아침에 제가 찾아왔을 때, 당신은 제게 오만하다고 내비치셨지요."], ["Pardon me if I say that I looked into your face while you spoke, and that it contradicted you.", "하지만 용서하십시오, 당신이 말씀하시는 동안 저는 당신의 얼굴을 들여다보았는데, 그 얼굴이 당신의 말을 반박하고 있었습니다."], ["I look into it again,”", "다시 들여다보아도,\""], ["he added, laying his hand gently on her arm, for an instant, “and it contradicts you more and more.”", "그가 그녀의 팔에 잠시 한 손을 부드럽게 얹으며 덧붙였다. \"그 얼굴은 당신의 말을 점점 더 반박하는군요.\""]], [["She was somewhat confused and agitated, and could make no ready answer.", "그녀는 다소 당황하고 동요하여, 선뜻 대답을 하지 못했다."]], [["“It is the mirror of truth,”", "\"그것은 진실의 거울입니다.\""], ["said her visitor, “and gentleness.", "그녀의 방문객이 말했다. \"그리고 온화함의 거울이지요."], ["Excuse my trusting to it, and returning.”", "제가 그것을 믿고 다시 찾아온 것을 용서해 주십시오.\""]], [["His manner of saying these words, divested them entirely of the character of compliments.", "이 말을 하는 그의 태도는 그 말에서 칭찬이라는 성격을 완전히 벗겨 냈다."], ["It was so plain, grave, unaffected, and sincere, that she bent her head, as if at once to thank him, and acknowledge his sincerity.", "그것은 너무도 꾸밈없고, 진지하며, 가식 없고, 진실되어서, 그녀는 그에게 감사하는 동시에 그의 진심을 인정하기라도 하듯 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“The disparity between our ages,”", "\"우리 둘의 나이 차이와,\" 신사가 말했다."], ["said the gentleman, “and the plainness of my purpose, empower me, I am glad to think, to speak my mind.", "\"제 용건의 솔직함이, 제 마음을 그대로 말할 수 있게 해 주니, 다행스럽게 생각합니다. 그것이 제 마음입니다."], ["That is my mind; and so you see me for the second time.”", "그래서 이렇게 두 번째로 저를 보시는 것이지요.\""]], [["“There is a kind of pride, Sir,”", "\"선생님, 한 가지 종류의 오만이 있어요,\" 그녀가 잠시 침묵한 뒤 대답했다."], ["she returned, after a moment’s silence, “or what may be supposed to be pride, which is mere duty.", "\"혹은 오만이라 여겨질 만한 것이지만, 그저 의무일 뿐인 것이지요. 저는 그 밖에 다른 오만은 품지 않았기를 바랍니다.\""], ["I hope I cherish no other.”", "\"당신 자신을 위한 것 말이지요.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“For yourself,” he said.", "\"제 자신을 위한 것이에요.\""]], [["“For myself.” “But—pardon me—”", "\"하지만—용서하십시오—\""], ["suggested the gentleman.", "신사가 넌지시 말했다."], ["“For your brother John?”", "\"당신의 오라버니 존을 위한 것은요?\""]], [["“Proud of his love, I am,”", "\"그분의 사랑을 자랑스러워한답니다,\""], ["said Harriet, looking full upon her visitor, and changing her manner on the instant—not that it was less composed and quiet, but that there was a deep impassioned earnestness in it that made the very tremble in her voice a part of her firmness, “and proud of him.", "해리엇이 방문객을 똑바로 바라보며, 순식간에 태도를 바꾸어 말했다—덜 침착하거나 덜 조용해진 것이 아니라, 거기에 깊고 격정적인 진지함이 담겨, 그녀의 떨리는 목소리마저 그녀의 굳건함의 일부가 되었다—\"그리고 그분을 자랑스러워해요."], ["Sir, you who strangely know the story of his life, and repeated it to me when you were here last—”", "선생님, 이상하게도 그분의 인생 이야기를 아시고, 지난번에 오셨을 때 그것을 제게 되풀이해 들려주신 분이여—\""]], [["“Merely to make my way into your confidence,”", "\"그저 당신의 신뢰 속으로 들어가는 길을 찾으려는 것이었을 뿐입니다.\""], ["interposed the gentleman.", "신사가 끼어들었다."], ["“For heaven’s sake, don’t suppose—”", "\"제발, 그렇게 생각지 마십시오—\""]], [["“I am sure,”", "\"분명히,\""], ["she said, “you revived it, in my hearing, with a kind and good purpose.", "그녀가 말했다. \"선생님은 친절하고 선한 뜻으로 그 이야기를 제 앞에서 되살려 주셨어요."], ["I am quite sure of it.”", "저는 그것을 확신해요.\""]], [["“I thank you,”", "\"감사합니다.\""], ["returned her visitor, pressing her hand hastily.", "그녀의 방문객이 그녀의 손을 황급히 쥐며 대답했다."], ["“I am much obliged to you.", "\"정말 고맙습니다."], ["You do me justice, I assure you.", "당신은 저를 공정하게 봐 주시는군요, 정말로요."], ["You were going to say, that I, who know the story of John Carker’s life—”", "당신은, 존 카커의 인생 이야기를 아는 제가—라고 말씀하시려던 참이었지요.\""]], [["“May think it pride in me,”", "\"제가 그분을 자랑스러워한다고 말할 때, 그것을 제 오만이라 여기실지도 모른다고요!\""], ["she continued, “when I say that I am proud of him!", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"저는 자랑스러워요."], ["I am. You know the time was, when I was not—when I could not be—but that is past.", "그렇지 못했던 때가 있었음을 아실 거예요—그럴 수 없었던 때가요—하지만 그건 지나갔어요."], ["The humility of many years, the uncomplaining expiation, the true repentance, the terrible regret, the pain I know he has even in my affection, which he thinks has cost me dear, though Heaven knows I am happy, but for his sorrow I—oh, Sir, after what I have seen, let me conjure you, if you are in any place of power, and are ever wronged, never, for any wrong, inflict a punishment that cannot be recalled; while there is a GOD above us to work changes in the hearts He made.”", "여러 해에 걸친 겸허함, 불평 없는 속죄, 참된 뉘우침, 끔찍한 회한, 제 애정 속에서마저 그분이 느끼는 고통—그분은 그 애정이 제게 큰 대가를 치르게 했다고 여기지만, 하늘은 아실 거예요, 저는 행복하다는 것을, 다만 그분의 슬픔이 아니었다면요—오, 선생님, 제가 보아 온 것을 겪고 나서 간곡히 청하건대, 혹 선생님께서 어떤 권력의 자리에 계시고 언젠가 부당한 일을 당하시더라도, 그 어떤 잘못에 대해서도, 다시는 되돌릴 수 없는 벌은 결코 내리지 마세요. 그분이 지으신 마음에 변화를 일으키실 하느님이 우리 위에 계신 동안에는요.\""]], [["“Your brother is an altered man,”", "\"당신의 오라버니는 변한 사람입니다.\""], ["returned the gentleman, compassionately.", "신사가 동정 어린 어조로 대답했다."], ["“I assure you I don’t doubt it.”", "\"그것을 의심치 않는다고 분명히 말씀드립니다.\""]], [["“He was an altered man when he did wrong,”", "\"잘못을 저질렀을 때도 그분은 변한 사람이었어요.\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."], ["“He is an altered man again, and is his true self now, believe me, Sir.”", "\"그분은 다시 변한 사람이 되었고, 이제는 본래의 참된 자신이에요, 믿어 주세요, 선생님.\""]], [["“But we go on,”", "\"하지만 우리는 그저 살아갈 뿐이지요.\""], ["said her visitor, rubbing his forehead, in an absent manner, with his hand, and then drumming thoughtfully on the table, “we go on in our clockwork routine, from day to day, and can’t make out, or follow, these changes.", "그녀의 방문객이 한 손으로 멍하니 이마를 문지르더니, 생각에 잠겨 탁자를 두드리며 말했다. \"우리는 날마다 시계태엽 같은 일상 속에서 그저 살아가며, 이런 변화를 알아채지도, 좇아가지도 못합니다."], ["They—they’re a metaphysical sort of thing.", "그것은—그것은 형이상학적인 종류의 일이지요."], ["We—we haven’t leisure for it.", "우리에겐—우리에겐 그럴 여유가 없습니다."], ["We—we haven’t courage.", "우리에겐—우리에겐 용기가 없어요."], ["They’re not taught at schools or colleges, and we don’t know how to set about it.", "그런 건 학교나 대학에서 가르쳐 주지도 않고, 우리는 그것을 어떻게 시작해야 할지도 모릅니다."], ["In short, we are so d——d business-like,”", "요컨대, 우리는 지독히도 사무적인 인간들이라서요.\""], ["said the gentleman, walking to the window, and back, and sitting down again, in a state of extreme dissatisfaction and vexation.", "신사가 창가로 갔다가 돌아와, 극도의 불만과 짜증에 휩싸인 채 다시 앉으며 말했다."]], [["“I am sure,” said the gentleman, rubbing his forehead again; and drumming on the table as before, “I have good reason to believe that a jog-trot life, the same from day to day, would reconcile one to anything. One don’t see anything, one don’t hear anything, one don’t know anything; that’s the fact. We go on taking everything for granted, and so we go on, until whatever we do, good, bad, or indifferent, we do from habit. Habit is all I shall have to report, when I am called upon to plead to my conscience, on my death-bed. ‘Habit,’ says I; ‘I was deaf, dumb, blind, and paralytic, to a million things, from habit.’ ‘Very business-like indeed, Mr What’s-your-name,’ says Conscience, ‘but it won’t do here!’”", "\"분명히,\" 신사가 다시 이마를 문지르며, 앞서처럼 탁자를 두드리며 말했다. \"날마다 똑같은, 터덜터덜 굴러가는 인생이라면 무엇에든 사람을 길들여 버리리라고 믿을 만한 충분한 까닭이 제겐 있습니다. 아무것도 보지 못하고, 아무것도 듣지 못하고, 아무것도 알지 못하지요. 그게 사실입니다. 우리는 모든 걸 당연한 것으로 여기며 그저 살아가고, 그렇게 살아가다 보면, 우리가 무엇을 하든, 선하든 악하든 그저 그렇든, 습관에서 하게 됩니다. 제가 임종의 자리에서 제 양심 앞에 변론하라는 요구를 받을 때, 보고할 것이라고는 습관뿐일 겁니다. '습관 때문이오,' 하고 저는 말하겠지요. '나는 백만 가지 일에 대해 습관 때문에 귀먹고, 벙어리가 되고, 눈멀고, 마비되어 있었소.' 그러면 양심은 이렇게 말하겠지요. '참으로 사무적이시군요, 댁의 이름이 뭐든 간에 양반. 하지만 여기서는 그게 통하지 않아요!'\""]], [["The gentleman got up and walked to the window again and back: seriously uneasy, though giving his uneasiness this peculiar expression.", "신사는 일어나 다시 창가로 갔다가 돌아왔다. 자기의 불안을 이런 독특한 방식으로 표현하기는 했어도, 진지하게 마음이 편치 않은 것이었다."]], [["“Miss Harriet,”", "\"해리엇 양,\" 그가 다시 의자로 돌아오며 말했다."], ["he said, resuming his chair, “I wish you would let me serve you.", "\"당신께 도움이 되도록 허락해 주셨으면 합니다. 저를 보십시오."], ["Look at me; I ought to look honest, for I know I am so, at present.", "저는 정직해 보일 겁니다. 적어도 지금만큼은 제가 정직하다는 걸 아니까요."], ["Do I?”", "그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요.\""], ["she answered with a smile.", "그녀가 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“I believe every word you have said,”", "\"당신이 하신 말씀을 한마디 한마디 다 믿습니다.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“I am full of self-reproach that I might have known this and seen this, and known you and seen you, any time these dozen years, and that I never have.", "\"저는 자책으로 가득합니다. 지난 십수 년 동안 언제든 이것을 알고 이것을 볼 수 있었을 텐데, 당신을 알고 당신을 볼 수 있었을 텐데, 한 번도 그러지 못했으니까요."], ["I hardly know how I ever got here—creature that I am, not only of my own habit, but of other people’s!", "제가 어떻게 여기까지 왔는지조차 잘 모르겠습니다—저라는 인간은, 제 자신의 습관뿐 아니라 남들의 습관에 매인 피조물이니 말입니다!"], ["But having done so, let me do something.", "하지만 이렇게 온 이상, 무언가 하게 해 주십시오."], ["I ask it in all honour and respect.", "온전한 경의와 존중을 담아 청합니다."], ["You inspire me with both, in the highest degree.", "당신은 그 두 가지를 제게 더없이 깊이 불러일으키십니다."], ["Let me do something.”", "무언가 하게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["“We are contented, Sir.”", "\"저희는 만족하고 있어요, 선생님.\""]], [["“No, no, not quite,” returned the gentleman.", "\"아닙니다, 아니에요, 꼭 그렇지는 않지요.\""], ["“I think not quite.", "신사가 대답했다."], ["There are some little comforts that might smooth your life, and his.", "\"꼭 그렇지는 않다고 생각합니다. 당신의 삶과 그분의 삶을 한결 편하게 해 줄 자그마한 안락들이 있지요."], ["And his!”", "그분의 삶도요!\""], ["he repeated, fancying that had made some impression on her.", "그가 그 말이 그녀에게 어떤 인상을 준 듯하다고 여기며 되풀이했다."], ["“I have been in the habit of thinking that there was nothing wanting to be done for him; that it was all settled and over; in short, of not thinking at all about it.", "\"저는 그분을 위해 해 줄 일이라곤 아무것도 없다고, 다 끝나고 결판난 일이라고 여기는 데 익숙해 있었습니다. 요컨대 그것에 대해 아예 생각하지 않는 데 익숙했지요."], ["I am different now.", "이제는 다릅니다."], ["Let me do something for him.", "그분을 위해 무언가 하게 해 주십시오. 당신도요.\""], ["You too,”", "방문객이 조심스러운 섬세함으로 말했다."], ["said the visitor, with careful delicacy, “have need to watch your health closely, for his sake, and I fear it fails.”", "\"당신도 그분을 위해 건강을 면밀히 살피셔야 합니다. 한데 제가 보기엔 그 건강이 쇠해 가고 있군요.\""]], [["“Whoever you may be, Sir,”", "\"선생님이 어떤 분이시든,\""], ["answered Harriet, raising her eyes to his face, “I am deeply grateful to you.", "해리엇이 그의 얼굴로 눈을 들어 올리며 대답했다. \"저는 선생님께 깊이 감사드려요."], ["I feel certain that in all you say, you have no object in the world but kindness to us.", "선생님이 하시는 모든 말씀에 저희를 향한 친절 말고는 이 세상 어떤 다른 목적도 없다는 것을 확신해요."], ["But years have passed since we began this life; and to take from my brother any part of what has so endeared him to me, and so proved his better resolution—any fragment of the merit of his unassisted, obscure, and forgotten reparation—would be to diminish the comfort it will be to him and me, when that time comes to each of us, of which you spoke just now.", "하지만 저희가 이 삶을 시작한 지도 여러 해가 지났어요. 그리고 그분을 제게 그토록 사랑스럽게 만들고, 그분의 더 나은 결심을 그토록 증명해 준 것의 어느 한 부분이라도 제 오라버니에게서 빼앗는 것—그 누구의 도움도 없이, 남모르게, 잊힌 채로 이룬 속죄의 그 공의 한 조각이라도 덜어 내는 것—그것은, 방금 선생님께서 말씀하신 그때가 저희 각자에게 닥쳐올 때, 그분과 제게 위안이 될 그것을 줄어들게 하는 일이 될 거예요."], ["I thank you better with these tears than any words.", "저는 어떤 말보다 이 눈물로 더 잘 감사드립니다."], ["Believe it, pray.”", "부디 그것을 믿어 주세요.\""]], [["The gentleman was moved, and put the hand she held out, to his lips, much as a tender father might kiss the hand of a dutiful child.", "신사는 감동하여, 그녀가 내민 손을 입술로 가져갔으니, 자상한 아버지가 효성스러운 자식의 손에 입 맞추는 것과도 같았다."], ["But more reverently.", "다만 그보다 더 경건하게."]], [["“If the day should ever come,”", "\"만일 언젠가,\" 해리엇이 말했다."], ["said Harriet, “when he is restored, in part, to the position he lost—”", "\"그분이 잃었던 지위에 얼마간이라도 회복되는 날이 온다면—\""]], [["“Restored!”", "\"회복이라뇨!\""], ["cried the gentleman, quickly.", "신사가 재빨리 외쳤다."], ["“How can that be hoped for?", "\"어떻게 그것을 바랄 수 있겠습니까?"], ["In whose hands does the power of any restoration lie?", "그 어떤 회복의 권한이 누구의 손에 있단 말입니까?"], ["It is no mistake of mine, surely, to suppose that his having gained the priceless blessing of his life, is one cause of the animosity shown to him by his brother.”", "그분이 자기 인생의 그 값을 매길 수 없는 축복을 얻었다는 것이, 그분의 형이 그분에게 보이는 적의의 한 가지 원인이라고 짐작하는 것이, 분명 제 착각은 아니겠지요.\""]], [["“You touch upon a subject that is never breathed between us; not even between us,”", "\"우리 사이에서, 우리 둘 사이에서조차 결코 입에 올린 적 없는 일을 건드리시는군요.\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."]], [["“I beg your forgiveness,”", "\"용서를 구합니다.\""], ["said the visitor.", "방문객이 말했다."], ["“I should have known it.", "\"제가 그것을 알았어야 했는데요."], ["I entreat you to forget that I have done so, inadvertently.", "부디 제가 무심코 그랬던 것을 잊어 주시기를 간청합니다."], ["And now, as I dare urge no more—as I am not sure that I have a right to do so—though Heaven knows, even that doubt may be habit,”", "그리고 이제, 더는 감히 졸라서는 안 되니—제게 그럴 권리가 있는지도 확신하지 못하니—하기야 하늘은 아시겠지만, 그 의구심마저도 습관일지 모르지요.\""], ["said the gentleman, rubbing his head, as despondently as before, “let me; though a stranger, yet no stranger; ask two favours.”", "신사가 앞서처럼 낙심한 듯 머리를 문지르며 말했다. \"이방인이긴 하나 이방인이 아닌 사람으로서, 두 가지 청을 드리게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["“What are they?”", "\"무엇인가요?\""], ["she inquired.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“The first, that if you should see cause to change your resolution, you will suffer me to be as your right hand.", "\"첫째는, 혹 당신이 결심을 바꿀 까닭이 생긴다면, 저를 당신의 오른팔로 삼아 주시는 겁니다."], ["My name shall then be at your service; it is useless now, and always insignificant.”", "그때는 제 이름이 당신께 쓰일 것입니다. 지금은 쓸모없고, 늘 보잘것없는 이름이지만요.\""]], [["“Our choice of friends,”", "\"저희가 벗을 고를 폭이,\""], ["she answered, smiling faintly, “is not so great, that I need any time for consideration.", "그녀가 희미하게 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"그리 넓지 않으니, 따로 생각할 시간이 필요치 않아요."], ["I can promise that.”", "그것은 약속드릴 수 있어요.\""]], [["“The second, that you will allow me sometimes, say every Monday morning, at nine o’clock—habit again—I must be businesslike,”", "\"둘째는, 이따금, 이를테면 월요일 아침마다 아홉 시에—또 습관이군요—저는 사무적이어야 하니까요,\""], ["said the gentleman, with a whimsical inclination to quarrel with himself on that head, “in walking past, to see you at the door or window.", "신사가 그 점을 두고 자신과 다투려는 익살스러운 마음으로 말했다. \"지나가는 길에, 당신을 문가나 창가에서 보게 해 주시는 겁니다."], ["I don’t ask to come in, as your brother will be gone out at that hour.", "들어가게 해 달라는 게 아닙니다, 그 시간이면 당신 오라버니는 나가고 없을 테니까요."], ["I don’t ask to speak to you.", "당신과 말을 나누게 해 달라는 것도 아닙니다."], ["I merely ask to see, for the satisfaction of my own mind, that you are well, and without intrusion to remind you, by the sight of me, that you have a friend—an elderly friend, grey-haired already, and fast growing greyer—whom you may ever command.”", "저는 그저, 제 마음 한 자락이 편안하도록, 당신이 잘 지내고 있음을 보고 싶을 뿐이고, 거슬리지 않게, 저를 봄으로써 당신께 한 사람의 벗이—이미 머리가 세고 점점 더 빠르게 세어 가는, 나이 든 벗이, 당신이 언제든 부릴 수 있는 그런 벗이 있음을 일깨워 드리고 싶을 뿐입니다.\""]], [["The cordial face looked up in his; confided in it; and promised.", "그 다정한 얼굴이 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았고, 그것을 믿었고, 그러겠노라 약속했다."]], [["“I understand, as before,”", "\"앞서와 마찬가지로,\" 신사가 일어서며 말했다."], ["said the gentleman, rising, “that you purpose not to mention my visit to John Carker, lest he should be at all distressed by my acquaintance with his history.", "\"당신은 제 방문을 존 카커에게 말하지 않으시려는 것으로 압니다. 제가 그의 내력을 안다는 것에 그가 조금이라도 마음이 상할까 봐서요."], ["I am glad of it, for it is out of the ordinary course of things, and—habit again!”", "그것이 다행입니다, 그것이 통상적인 일의 순서에서 벗어난 것이니까요. 또—이게 다 습관이군요!\""], ["said the gentleman, checking himself impatiently, “as if there were no better course than the ordinary course!”", "신사가 짜증스럽게 자제하며 말했다. \"통상적인 순서보다 더 나은 순서란 없기라도 한 듯이 말이지요!\""]], [["With that he turned to go, and walking, bareheaded, to the outside of the little porch, took leave of her with such a happy mixture of unconstrained respect and unaffected interest, as no breeding could have taught, no truth mistrusted, and nothing but a pure and single heart expressed.", "그 말과 함께 그는 가려고 돌아서, 모자를 벗은 채로 자그마한 현관 밖으로 걸어 나가, 그 어떤 교양으로도 가르칠 수 없고, 그 어떤 진실됨으로도 의심할 수 없으며, 오로지 순수하고 한결같은 마음만이 드러낼 수 있는, 거리낌 없는 경의와 가식 없는 관심이 어우러진 그 행복한 태도로 그녀에게 작별을 고했다."]], [["Many half-forgotten emotions were awakened in the sister’s mind by this visit.", "이 방문으로 그 누이의 마음속에는 반쯤 잊혔던 숱한 감정이 깨어났다."], ["It was so very long since any other visitor had crossed their threshold; it was so very long since any voice of apathy had made sad music in her ears; that the stranger’s figure remained present to her, hours afterwards, when she sat at the window, plying her needle; and his words seemed newly spoken, again and again.", "다른 어떤 방문객이 그들의 문턱을 넘어온 지가 너무도 오래되었고, 그 어떤 무심함 아닌 목소리가 그녀의 귀에 슬픈 가락을 들려준 지도 너무도 오래되어서, 그 이방인의 모습은 몇 시간이 지나 그녀가 창가에 앉아 바늘을 놀릴 때에도 여전히 그녀 앞에 어른거렸고, 그의 말은 거듭거듭 새로이 들려오는 듯했다."], ["He had touched the spring that opened her whole life; and if she lost him for a short space, it was only among the many shapes of the one great recollection of which that life was made.", "그는 그녀의 온 삶을 열어 주는 용수철을 건드렸던 것이다. 그래서 그녀가 잠시 그를 놓친다 해도, 그것은 다만 그 삶을 이루어 온 하나의 위대한 회상의 숱한 형상들 가운데서일 뿐이었다."]], [["Musing and working by turns; now constraining herself to be steady at her needle for a long time together, and now letting her work fall, unregarded, on her lap, and straying wheresoever her busier thoughts led, Harriet Carker found the hours glide by her, and the day steal on.", "사색하다가 일하다가를 번갈아 하면서—이제는 오래도록 한결같이 바느질에 마음을 다잡으려 애쓰다가, 이제는 일감이 무릎 위에 떨어지도록 내버려 둔 채 거들떠보지도 않고 더 분주한 생각이 이끄는 대로 헤매면서—해리엇 카커는 시간이 곁을 미끄러져 가고 하루가 슬그머니 흘러가는 것을 깨달았다."], ["The morning, which had been bright and clear, gradually became overcast; a sharp wind set in; the rain fell heavily; and a dark mist drooping over the distant town, hid it from the view.", "밝고 맑던 아침은 차츰 흐려졌고, 매서운 바람이 일었으며, 비가 세차게 쏟아졌고, 어두운 안개가 멀리 도시 위에 드리워 그것을 시야에서 가려 버렸다."]], [["She often looked with compassion, at such a time, upon the stragglers who came wandering into London, by the great highway hard by, and who, footsore and weary, and gazing fearfully at the huge town before them, as if foreboding that their misery there would be but as a drop of water in the sea, or as a grain of sea-sand on the shore, went shrinking on, cowering before the angry weather, and looking as if the very elements rejected them.", "그녀는 그런 때면 자주, 바로 곁의 큰길을 따라 런던으로 흘러드는 떠돌이들을 동정 어린 눈으로 바라보곤 했다. 발이 부르트고 지친 그들은, 앞에 펼쳐진 거대한 도시를 두려운 듯 바라보며, 마치 그곳에서의 자기네 비참함이 바다에 떨어진 물 한 방울이나 해변의 모래 한 알에 지나지 않으리라 예감하기라도 한 듯, 성난 날씨 앞에 움츠리며 잔뜩 웅크린 채, 마치 만물이 자기네를 거부하기라도 하는 양 위축되어 걸어갔다."], ["Day after day, such travellers crept past, but always, as she thought, in one direction—always towards the town.", "날이면 날마다 그런 나그네들이 곁을 슬며시 지나갔으나, 그녀가 생각하기로는 늘 한 방향으로—언제나 도시를 향해서였다."], ["Swallowed up in one phase or other of its immensity, towards which they seemed impelled by a desperate fascination, they never returned.", "어떤 절망적인 매혹에 떠밀리는 듯 보이는 그 도시의 광대함, 그 어느 한 모습에 삼켜진 채, 그들은 결코 돌아오지 않았다."], ["Food for the hospitals, the churchyards, the prisons, the river, fever, madness, vice, and death,—they passed on to the monster, roaring in the distance, and were lost.", "병원과 묘지와 감옥과 강과 열병과 광기와 악덕과 죽음의 먹이가 되어—그들은 멀리서 으르렁대는 그 괴물을 향해 나아갔고, 그렇게 사라져 갔다."]], [["The chill wind was howling, and the rain was falling, and the day was darkening moodily, when Harriet, raising her eyes from the work on which she had long since been engaged with unremitting constancy, saw one of these travellers approaching.", "차디찬 바람이 울부짖고, 비가 내리고, 날이 침울하게 어두워져 갈 무렵, 해리엇이 오래도록 끊임없이 매달려 온 일감에서 눈을 들어 올렸을 때, 그런 나그네들 가운데 하나가 다가오는 것을 보았다."]], [["A woman.", "한 여인이었다. 서른쯤 되어 보이는 외로운 여인으로, 키가 크고, 몸매가 반듯하고, 잘생겼으나, 비참하게 차려입고 있었다."], ["A solitary woman of some thirty years of age; tall; well-formed; handsome; miserably dressed; the soil of many country roads in varied weather—dust, chalk, clay, gravel—clotted on her grey cloak by the streaming wet; no bonnet on her head, nothing to defend her rich black hair from the rain, but a torn handkerchief; with the fluttering ends of which, and with her hair, the wind blinded her so that she often stopped to push them back, and look upon the way she was going.", "온갖 날씨 속 숱한 시골길의 흙—먼지, 백악, 진흙, 자갈—이 흘러내리는 빗물에 그녀의 회색 망토 위에 엉겨 붙어 있었다. 머리에는 보닛도 없이, 풍성한 검은 머리칼을 비로부터 막아 줄 것이라고는 찢어진 손수건 하나뿐이었는데, 그 펄럭이는 손수건 자락과 머리칼 때문에 바람이 그녀의 눈을 가려서, 그녀는 그것들을 자꾸 밀어내고 자기가 가는 길을 살피느라 걸음을 멈추곤 했다."]], [["She was in the act of doing so, when Harriet observed her.", "해리엇이 그녀를 보았을 때, 그녀는 마침 그렇게 하고 있던 참이었다."], ["As her hands, parting on her sunburnt forehead, swept across her face, and threw aside the hindrances that encroached upon it, there was a reckless and regardless beauty in it: a dauntless and depraved indifference to more than weather: a carelessness of what was cast upon her bare head from Heaven or earth: that, coupled with her misery and loneliness, touched the heart of her fellow-woman.", "그녀의 두 손이 햇볕에 그을린 이마 위에서 갈라지며 얼굴을 가로질러 훑어, 얼굴을 가리던 것들을 옆으로 젖혔을 때, 그 얼굴에는 무모하고 거리낌 없는 아름다움이 있었다. 날씨 따위보다 더한 것에도 굴하지 않는 대담하고 타락한 무관심이, 하늘에서든 땅에서든 자기 맨머리 위로 쏟아지는 것에 대한 무신경함이 있었으니, 그것이 그녀의 비참함과 외로움과 어우러져, 같은 여자의 마음을 움직였다."], ["She thought of all that was perverted and debased within her, no less than without: of modest graces of the mind, hardened and steeled, like these attractions of the person; of the many gifts of the Creator flung to the winds like the wild hair; of all the beautiful ruin upon which the storm was beating and the night was coming.", "해리엇은 그 여인의 겉모습 못지않게 그 속에서 비뚤어지고 타락한 모든 것을 떠올렸다. 그 몸의 매력처럼 굳어지고 강철처럼 단단해진 마음의 정숙한 우아함들을, 거친 머리칼처럼 바람에 내던져진 창조주의 숱한 선물들을, 폭풍이 들이치고 밤이 다가오고 있는 그 모든 아름다운 폐허를."]], [["Thinking of this, she did not turn away with a delicate indignation—too many of her own compassionate and tender sex too often do—but pitied her.", "이것을 생각하며, 그녀는 섬세한 분노에 차서 외면하지 않았으니—저 자신도 동정심 많고 다정한 같은 성(性)의 여인들이 너무도 자주 그러하건만—오히려 그녀를 가엾이 여겼다."]], [["Her fallen sister came on, looking far before her, trying with her eager eyes to pierce the mist in which the city was enshrouded, and glancing, now and then, from side to side, with the bewildered—and uncertain aspect of a stranger.", "타락한 그녀의 자매는 멀리 앞을 바라보며, 도시를 휘감은 안개를 간절한 눈으로 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰면서, 이따금 이쪽저쪽을 곁눈질하며, 낯선 이의 어리둥절하고 미덥지 못한 표정으로 다가왔다."], ["Though her tread was bold and courageous, she was fatigued, and after a moment of irresolution,—sat down upon a heap of stones; seeking no shelter from the rain, but letting it rain on her as it would.", "그녀의 걸음걸이는 대담하고 용감했으나, 그녀는 지쳐 있었고, 잠시 망설인 끝에—돌무더기 위에 주저앉았다. 비를 피할 곳을 찾지도 않은 채, 비가 내리는 대로 자기 위에 내리도록 내버려 두면서."]], [["She was now opposite the house; raising her head after resting it for a moment on both hands, her eyes met those of Harriet.", "이제 그녀는 그 집 맞은편에 있었다. 두 손에 머리를 잠시 묻고 쉬다가 고개를 들었을 때, 그녀의 눈이 해리엇의 눈과 마주쳤다."]], [["In a moment, Harriet was at the door; and the other, rising from her seat at her beck, came slowly, and with no conciliatory look, towards her.", "순식간에 해리엇은 문가로 나왔고, 다른 여인은 그녀의 손짓에 자리에서 일어나, 누그러진 기색이라곤 없이 천천히 그녀에게로 다가왔다."]], [["“Why do you rest in the rain?”", "\"왜 빗속에서 쉬시나요?\""], ["said Harriet, gently.", "해리엇이 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“Because I have no other resting-place,”", "\"달리 쉴 곳이 없으니까요.\""], ["was the reply.", "대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“But there are many places of shelter near here.", "\"하지만 이 근처에 비를 피할 곳이 많이 있어요."], ["This,”", "여기가,\""], ["referring to the little porch, “is better than where you were.", "그녀가 자그마한 현관을 가리키며 말했다. \"당신이 있던 데보다는 나아요."], ["You are very welcome to rest here.”", "여기서 편히 쉬세요.\""]], [["The wanderer looked at her, in doubt and surprise, but without any expression of thankfulness; and sitting down, and taking off one of her worn shoes to beat out the fragments of stone and dust that were inside, showed that her foot was cut and bleeding.", "떠돌이 여인은 의아함과 놀라움에 차서 그녀를 바라보았으나, 고마워하는 기색이라곤 전혀 없었다. 그리고 자리에 앉아, 닳아빠진 신발 한 짝을 벗어 그 안에 든 돌조각과 먼지를 털어 내려다가, 그녀의 발이 찢기고 피 흘리고 있는 것이 드러났다."]], [["Harriet uttering an expression of pity, the traveller looked up with a contemptuous and incredulous smile.", "해리엇이 가엾다는 말을 내뱉자, 나그네는 경멸하듯 믿기 어렵다는 듯 미소 지으며 올려다보았다."]], [["“Why, what’s a torn foot to such as me?”", "\"흥, 나 같은 것한테 찢긴 발이 뭐 대수라고요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“And what’s a torn foot in such as me, to such as you?”", "\"그리고 나 같은 것의 찢긴 발이, 당신 같은 분한테 뭐 대수겠어요?\""]], [["“Come in and wash it,”", "\"들어와서 씻으세요,\""], ["answered Harriet, mildly, “and let me give you something to bind it up.”", "해리엇이 부드럽게 대답했다. \"그리고 동여맬 것을 좀 드릴게요.\""]], [["The woman caught her arm, and drawing it before her own eyes, hid them against it, and wept.", "여인은 그녀의 팔을 붙잡아 제 눈앞으로 끌어당기더니, 거기에 눈을 묻고 울었다."], ["Not like a woman, but like a stern man surprised into that weakness; with a violent heaving of her breast, and struggle for recovery, that showed how unusual the emotion was with her.", "여자처럼이 아니라, 그런 나약함에 불시에 사로잡힌 엄격한 사내처럼, 가슴을 거칠게 들썩이며, 그리고 다시 추스르려 안간힘을 쓰며 울었으니, 그것은 그런 감정이 그녀에게 얼마나 드문 것인지를 보여 주었다."]], [["She submitted to be led into the house, and, evidently more in gratitude than in any care for herself, washed and bound the injured place.", "그녀는 집 안으로 이끌려 들어가는 데에 순순히 따랐고, 분명 제 자신을 위해서라기보다는 감사하는 마음에서, 다친 곳을 씻고 동여맸다."], ["Harriet then put before her fragments of her own frugal dinner, and when she had eaten of them, though sparingly, besought her, before resuming her road (which she showed her anxiety to do), to dry her clothes before the fire.", "그러자 해리엇은 자기의 검소한 식사에서 남은 음식 부스러기를 그녀 앞에 내놓았고, 그녀가 비록 조금이긴 하나 그것을 먹고 나자, 길을 다시 떠나기 전에(그녀는 떠나려고 안달하는 기색을 보였다) 불 앞에서 옷을 말리라고 간곡히 청했다."], ["Again, more in gratitude than with any evidence of concern in her own behalf, she sat down in front of it, and unbinding the handkerchief about her head, and letting her thick wet hair fall down below her waist, sat drying it with the palms of her hands, and looking at the blaze.", "다시금, 제 자신을 염려하는 기색이라기보다는 감사하는 마음에서, 그녀는 불 앞에 앉아, 머리를 동여맨 손수건을 풀고, 두텁고 젖은 머리칼을 허리 아래까지 늘어뜨린 채, 손바닥으로 그것을 말리며 타오르는 불길을 바라보고 앉아 있었다."]], [["“I daresay you are thinking,”", "\"이런 생각을 하고 계시겠지요,\""], ["she said, lifting her head suddenly, “that I used to be handsome, once.", "그녀가 불쑥 고개를 들며 말했다. \"내가 한때는 어여뻤겠거니 하고요."], ["I believe I was—I know I was—Look here!”", "정말 그랬을 거예요—분명 그랬어요—여기 좀 보세요!\""]], [["She held up her hair roughly with both hands; seizing it as if she would have torn it out; then, threw it down again, and flung it back as though it were a heap of serpents.", "그녀는 두 손으로 머리칼을 거칠게 쳐들었다. 마치 그것을 잡아 뜯기라도 할 듯 움켜쥐었다가, 다시 그것을 떨어뜨리고는, 마치 그것이 한 무더기 뱀이기라도 한 양 뒤로 홱 내던졌다."]], [["“Are you a stranger in this place?”", "\"이곳이 낯선 곳인가요?\""], ["asked Harriet.", "해리엇이 물었다."]], [["“A stranger!”", "\"낯선 곳이냐고요!\""], ["she returned, stopping between each short reply, and looking at the fire.", "그녀가 짧은 대답마다 사이를 두고 멈추면서, 불을 바라보며 되받았다. \"그래요."], ["“Yes. Ten or a dozen years a stranger.", "십 년이나 십이 년쯤 낯선 곳이었지요."], ["I have had no almanack where I have been.", "내가 있던 데에는 달력이라곤 없었어요."], ["Ten or a dozen years.", "십 년인지 십이 년인지."], ["I don’t know this part.", "이 동네를 모르겠어요."], ["It’s much altered since I went away.”", "내가 떠난 뒤로 많이 변했네요.\""]], [["“Have you been far?”", "\"멀리 다녀오셨나요?\""]], [["“Very far.", "\"아주 멀리요."], ["Months upon months over the sea, and far away even then.", "몇 달이고 몇 달이고 바다를 건너서, 거기서도 또 멀리요."], ["I have been where convicts go,”", "나는 죄수들이 가는 데에 가 있었어요.\""], ["she added, looking full upon her entertainer.", "그녀가 자기를 거두어 준 여인을 똑바로 바라보며 덧붙였다."], ["“I have been one myself.”", "\"나 자신이 죄수였으니까요.\""]], [["“Heaven help you and forgive you!”", "\"하느님께서 당신을 도우시고 용서하시길!\""], ["was the gentle answer.", "부드러운 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Heaven help me and forgive me!”", "하느님께서 나를 도우시고 용서하시길이라!\""], ["she returned, nodding her head at the fire.", "그녀가 불을 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 되받았다."], ["“If man would help some of us a little more, God would forgive us all the sooner perhaps.”", "\"사람들이 우리 중 누군가를 조금만 더 도와준다면, 어쩌면 하느님께서 우리 모두를 그만큼 더 빨리 용서해 주실지도 모르지요.\""]], [["But she was softened by the earnest manner, and the cordial face so full of mildness and so free from judgment, of her, and said, less hardily:", "그러나 그녀는, 그 진지한 태도와, 온화함으로 가득하고 심판이라곤 없는 그 여인의 다정한 얼굴에 마음이 누그러져, 한결 덜 거칠게 말했다."]], [["“We may be about the same age, you and me.", "\"당신과 나, 우리 둘은 나이가 비슷할 거예요."], ["If I am older, it is not above a year or two.", "내가 더 많다 해도, 한두 살 위는 못 될 거예요."], ["Oh think of that!”", "오, 그걸 생각해 봐요!\""]], [["She opened her arms, as though the exhibition of her outward form would show the moral wretch she was; and letting them drop at her sides, hung down her head.", "그녀는, 마치 제 겉모습을 내보이면 자기가 얼마나 도덕적으로 비참한 인간인지가 드러나기라도 할 양 두 팔을 벌렸다가, 그것을 옆구리에 떨어뜨리고 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["“There is nothing we may not hope to repair; it is never too late to amend,”", "\"우리가 고치기를 바라지 못할 것은 아무것도 없어요. 바로잡기에 너무 늦은 때란 결코 없고요.\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."], ["“You are penitent?”", "\"당신은 뉘우치고 있나요?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요,\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“I am not!", "\"안 해요!"], ["I can’t be.", "못 해요."], ["I am no such thing.", "그런 거 안 한다고요."], ["Why should I be penitent, and all the world go free?", "내가 왜 뉘우쳐야 하지요, 온 세상이 멀쩡히 풀려나 있는데?"], ["They talk to me of my penitence.", "사람들은 내게 내 뉘우침을 운운하지요."], ["Who’s penitent for the wrongs that have been done to me?”", "나한테 저질러진 잘못들은 대체 누가 뉘우친답니까?\""]], [["She rose up, bound her handkerchief about her head, and turned to move away.", "그녀는 일어나, 머리에 손수건을 동여매고, 떠나려고 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Where are you going?”", "\"어디로 가시나요?\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."]], [["“Yonder,”", "\"저쪽이요,\""], ["she answered, pointing with her hand.", "그녀가 손으로 가리키며 대답했다."], ["“To London.”", "\"런던으로요.\""]], [["“Have you any home to go to?”", "\"갈 만한 집이라도 있나요?\""]], [["“I think I have a mother.", "\"어머니가 있는 것 같긴 해요."], ["She’s as much a mother, as her dwelling is a home,”", "그 여자가 어머니인 만큼이나, 그 여자가 사는 데도 집이지요.\""], ["she answered with a bitter laugh.", "그녀가 쓴웃음을 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“Take this,”", "\"이걸 받으세요,\""], ["cried Harriet, putting money in her hand.", "해리엇이 그녀의 손에 돈을 쥐여 주며 외쳤다."], ["“Try to do well.", "\"잘 살아 보려고 애써 봐요."], ["It is very little, but for one day it may keep you from harm.”", "얼마 안 되지만, 하루쯤은 당신을 화에서 지켜 줄 수 있을 거예요.\""]], [["“Are you married?”", "\"결혼하셨나요?\""], ["said the other, faintly, as she took it.", "다른 여인이 그것을 받으며 가냘프게 말했다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["I live here with my brother.", "저는 여기서 오라버니와 함께 살아요."], ["We have not much to spare, or I would give you more.”", "저희도 여유가 많지 않아서요, 그렇지 않았다면 더 드렸을 거예요.\""]], [["“Will you let me kiss you?”", "\"입맞춤해도 될까요?\""]], [["Seeing no scorn or repugnance in her face, the object of her charity bent over her as she asked the question, and pressed her lips against her cheek.", "그 얼굴에 경멸이나 혐오가 없음을 보고, 그녀의 자선을 받은 그 여인은 그렇게 물으며 그녀에게로 몸을 굽혀, 그녀의 뺨에 입술을 댔다."], ["Once more she caught her arm, and covered her eyes with it; and then was gone.", "다시 한번 그녀는 그녀의 팔을 붙잡아 거기에 눈을 가렸다. 그러고는 가 버렸다."]], [["Gone into the deepening night, and howling wind, and pelting rain; urging her way on towards the mist-enshrouded city where the blurred lights gleamed; and with her black hair, and disordered head-gear, fluttering round her reckless face.", "짙어 가는 밤과, 울부짖는 바람과, 퍼붓는 비 속으로 가 버렸다. 흐릿한 불빛이 희미하게 빛나는, 안개에 휘감긴 도시를 향해 길을 재촉하면서, 검은 머리칼과 흐트러진 머릿수건을 그 무모한 얼굴 둘레에 펄럭이면서."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIV. Another Mother and Daughter", "# 제29장"]], [["In an ugly and dark room, an old woman, ugly and dark too, sat listening to the wind and rain, and crouching over a meagre fire.", "추하고 어두운 방 안에, 역시 추하고 어두운 늙은 여인이 앉아 바람과 빗소리에 귀를 기울이며 변변찮은 불 위로 웅크리고 있었다. 앞엣것보다는 뒤엣것에 더 한결같이 매달려, 그녀는 자세를 바꾸는 일이 없었다."], ["More constant to the last-named occupation than the first, she never changed her attitude, unless, when any stray drops of rain fell hissing on the smouldering embers, to raise her head with an awakened attention to the whistling and pattering outside, and gradually to let it fall again lower and lower and lower as she sunk into a brooding state of thought, in which the noises of the night were as indistinctly regarded as is the monotonous rolling of a sea by one who sits in contemplation on its shore.", "다만 빗방울이 어쩌다 사그라드는 잿불 위로 떨어져 쉭쉭 소리를 낼 때면, 정신이 번쩍 깨어나 바깥의 휘파람 소리와 후두두 떨어지는 소리에 귀 기울이려 고개를 들었다가, 차츰차츰 점점 더 낮게, 더 낮게, 더 낮게 고개를 떨구며 골똘한 상념에 잠겨 들어가곤 했다. 그 상태에서는 밤의 소음들도, 마치 바닷가에 앉아 사색에 잠긴 이에게 단조롭게 굽이치는 바다의 소리가 그러하듯, 흐릿하게만 느껴졌다."]], [["There was no light in the room save that which the fire afforded.", "방 안에는 불빛 외에 아무 빛도 없었다."], ["Glaring sullenly from time to time like the eye of a fierce beast half asleep, it revealed no objects that needed to be jealous of a better display.", "반쯤 잠든 사나운 짐승의 눈처럼 이따금 음침하게 번득이는 그 빛은, 더 나은 모습을 보여 주려고 애써 가릴 필요가 있는 물건이라곤 아무것도 비추지 않았다."], ["A heap of rags, a heap of bones, a wretched bed, two or three mutilated chairs or stools, the black walls and blacker ceiling, were all its winking brightness shone upon.", "누더기 한 무더기, 뼈다귀 한 무더기, 초라한 침대, 망가진 의자나 걸상 두세 개, 검은 벽과 그보다 더 검은 천장—그 깜박이는 광채가 비추는 것이라곤 그게 전부였다. 늙은 여인은 자기 자신의 거대하고 일그러진 그림자를 반쯤은 뒤편 벽에, 반쯤은 위쪽 천장에 드리운 채, 불을 가둔 몇 장의 헐거운 벽돌 위로—난로가 없었으므로 굴뚝의 축축한 화덕 위로—몸을 굽히고 앉아 있었는데, 마치 어느 마녀의 제단에서 길조를 기다리며 지켜보는 듯한 모습이었다."], ["As the old woman, with a gigantic and distorted image of herself thrown half upon the wall behind her, half upon the roof above, sat bending over the few loose bricks within which it was pent, on the damp hearth of the chimney—for there was no stove—she looked as if she were watching at some witch’s altar for a favourable token; and but that the movement of her chattering jaws and trembling chin was too frequent and too fast for the slow flickering of the fire, it would have seemed an illusion wrought by the light, as it came and went, upon a face as motionless as the form to which it belonged.", "다만 그녀의 달싹이는 턱과 떨리는 아래턱의 움직임이, 더디게 일렁이는 불빛에 비해 너무 잦고 너무 빨랐기에 망정이지, 그렇지 않았다면 그 빛이 왔다 갔다 하며 만들어 낸 환영처럼 보였을 것이다. 그 빛이 비추는 얼굴은, 거기 딸린 형상만큼이나 미동도 없었으니까."]], [["If Florence could have stood within the room and looked upon the original of the shadow thrown upon the wall and roof as it cowered thus over the fire, a glance might have sufficed to recall the figure of Good Mrs Brown; notwithstanding that her childish recollection of that terrible old woman was as grotesque and exaggerated a presentment of the truth, perhaps, as the shadow on the wall.", "만일 플로렌스가 그 방 안에 서서, 그렇게 불 위로 웅크린 채 벽과 천장에 그림자를 드리운 그 그림자의 실물을 바라볼 수 있었다면, 단 한 번의 눈길로도 착한 브라운 부인의 모습을 떠올리기에 충분했으리라. 비록 그 무서운 늙은 여인에 대한 어린 시절의 기억이, 아마도 벽에 비친 그림자만큼이나 기괴하고 과장되게 진실을 그려 낸 것이었다 해도 말이다."], ["But Florence was not there to look on; and Good Mrs Brown remained unrecognised, and sat staring at her fire, unobserved.", "그러나 플로렌스는 거기서 보고 있지 않았다. 그리하여 착한 브라운 부인은 알아보는 이 없이, 보는 이 없이, 자기 불을 응시하며 앉아 있었다."]], [["Attracted by a louder sputtering than usual, as the rain came hissing down the chimney in a little stream, the old woman raised her head, impatiently, to listen afresh.", "빗물이 가느다란 줄기를 이루어 굴뚝을 타고 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 떨어지자 평소보다 요란한 탁탁 소리가 났고, 거기에 이끌려 늙은 여인은 다시 귀 기울이려 짜증스럽게 고개를 들었다."], ["And this time she did not drop it again; for there was a hand upon the door, and a footstep in the room.", "그리고 이번에는 고개를 다시 떨구지 않았다. 문에 손이 닿았고, 방 안에 발소리가 들렸기 때문이다."]], [["“Who’s that?”", "\"거 누구냐?\""], ["she said, looking over her shoulder.", "그녀가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“One who brings you news, was the answer, in a woman’s voice.", "\"소식을 가져온 사람이오.\" 여자의 목소리로 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“News?", "\"소식?"], ["Where from?”", "어디서?\""]], [["“From abroad.”", "\"외지에서.\""]], [["“From beyond seas?”", "\"바다 건너에서?\""], ["cried the old woman, starting up.", "늙은 여인이 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."]], [["“Ay, from beyond seas.”", "\"그래요, 바다 건너에서.\""]], [["The old woman raked the fire together, hurriedly, and going close to her visitor who had entered, and shut the door, and who now stood in the middle of the room, put her hand upon the drenched cloak, and turned the unresisting figure, so as to have it in the full light of the fire.", "늙은 여인은 황급히 불을 긁어모으고는, 방 안으로 들어와 문을 닫고 이제 방 한가운데 서 있는 방문객에게 바짝 다가가, 흠뻑 젖은 망토 위에 손을 얹고, 저항하지 않는 그 형체를 돌려 불빛이 온전히 비치도록 했다."], ["She did not find what she had expected, whatever that might be; for she let the cloak go again, and uttered a querulous cry of disappointment and misery.", "그게 무엇이든 간에, 그녀는 자기가 기대한 것을 찾지 못했다. 망토를 다시 놓아 버리고는, 실망과 비탄의 칭얼대는 비명을 내질렀으니까."]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["asked her visitor.", "방문객이 물었다."]], [["“Oho! Oho!”", "\"오호!"], ["cried the old woman, turning her face upward, with a terrible howl.", "오호!\" 늙은 여인이 끔찍한 울부짖음과 함께 얼굴을 위로 쳐들며 외쳤다."]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이냐고요?\""], ["asked the visitor again.", "방문객이 다시 물었다."]], [["“It’s not my gal!”", "\"내 딸이 아니잖아!\""], ["cried the old woman, tossing up her arms, and clasping her hands above her head.", "늙은 여인이 두 팔을 내던지듯 쳐들고는 머리 위로 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다."], ["“Where’s my Alice?", "\"내 앨리스는 어디 있어?"], ["Where’s my handsome daughter?", "내 어여쁜 딸은 어디 있어?"], ["They’ve been the death of her!”", "그놈들이 그 애를 죽여 버렸어!\""]], [["“They’ve not been the death of her yet, if your name’s Marwood,”", "\"댁 이름이 마우드라면, 그 사람들이 아직 따님을 죽이진 않았소.\""], ["said the visitor.", "방문객이 말했다."]], [["“Have you seen my gal, then?”", "\"그럼 내 딸을 봤단 말이오?\""], ["cried the old woman.", "늙은 여인이 외쳤다."], ["“Has she wrote to me?”", "\"그 애가 내게 편지를 썼어?\""]], [["“She said you couldn’t read,”", "\"댁은 글을 못 읽는다고 하던데요.\""], ["returned the other.", "상대가 대답했다."]], [["“No more I can!”", "\"맞아, 나는 못 읽지!\""], ["exclaimed the old woman, wringing her hands.", "늙은 여인이 두 손을 비틀며 소리쳤다."]], [["“Have you no light here?”", "\"여기 불은 없소?\""], ["said the other, looking round the room.", "상대가 방 안을 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["The old woman, mumbling and shaking her head, and muttering to herself about her handsome daughter, brought a candle from a cupboard in the corner, and thrusting it into the fire with a trembling hand, lighted it with some difficulty and set it on the table.", "늙은 여인은 우물거리며 고개를 흔들고, 어여쁜 딸 이야기를 혼자 중얼거리면서, 구석의 찬장에서 초 하나를 가져와 떨리는 손으로 불 속에 들이밀어 간신히 붙인 뒤 탁자 위에 세웠다."], ["Its dirty wick burnt dimly at first, being choked in its own grease; and when the bleared eyes and failing sight of the old woman could distinguish anything by its light, her visitor was sitting with her arms folded, her eyes turned downwards, and a handkerchief she had worn upon her head lying on the table by her side.", "더러운 심지는 처음엔 제 기름에 막혀 흐릿하게 탔다. 그리고 늙은 여인의 흐린 눈과 침침해진 시력이 그 불빛으로 무언가를 분간할 수 있게 되었을 때, 방문객은 팔짱을 끼고 눈을 내리깐 채 앉아 있었고, 그녀가 머리에 쓰고 있던 손수건은 옆 탁자 위에 놓여 있었다."]], [["“She sent to me by word of mouth then, my gal, Alice?”", "\"그럼 그 애가, 내 딸 앨리스가 말로 전갈을 보냈단 말이지?\""], ["mumbled the old woman, after waiting for some moments.", "늙은 여인이 잠시 기다린 뒤 우물거렸다."], ["“What did she say?”", "\"그 애가 뭐라고 했소?\""]], [["“Look,”", "\"보세요.\""], ["returned the visitor.", "방문객이 대답했다."]], [["The old woman repeated the word in a scared uncertain way; and, shading her eyes, looked at the speaker, round the room, and at the speaker once again.", "늙은 여인은 겁먹은 듯 불안하게 그 말을 되뇌었다. 그러고는 눈에 그늘을 드리우며, 말한 이를 보고, 방 안을 둘러보고, 다시 한번 말한 이를 보았다."]], [["“Alice said look again, mother;”", "\"앨리스가 다시 보라고 했어요, 어머니.\""], ["and the speaker fixed her eyes upon her.", "그러고는 말한 이가 그녀에게 눈길을 고정했다."]], [["Again the old woman looked round the room, and at her visitor, and round the room once more.", "다시 늙은 여인은 방 안을 둘러보고, 방문객을 보고, 다시 한번 방 안을 둘러보았다."], ["Hastily seizing the candle, and rising from her seat, she held it to the visitor’s face, uttered a loud cry, set down the light, and fell upon her neck!", "황급히 초를 집어 들고 자리에서 일어나, 그것을 방문객의 얼굴에 들이대더니, 큰 비명을 지르고는, 초를 내려놓고, 그녀의 목에 와락 안겼다!"]], [["“It’s my gal!", "\"내 딸이야!"], ["It’s my Alice!", "내 앨리스야!"], ["It’s my handsome daughter, living and come back!”", "살아서 돌아온 내 어여쁜 딸이야!\""], ["screamed the old woman, rocking herself to and fro upon the breast that coldly suffered her embrace.", "늙은 여인이, 그 포옹을 차갑게 견디는 가슴에 매달려 앞뒤로 몸을 흔들며 비명처럼 외쳤다."], ["“It’s my gal!", "\"내 딸이야!"], ["It’s my Alice!", "내 앨리스야!"], ["It’s my handsome daughter, living and come back!”", "살아서 돌아온 내 어여쁜 딸이야!\""], ["she screamed again, dropping on the floor before her, clasping her knees, laying her head against them, and still rocking herself to and fro with every frantic demonstration of which her vitality was capable.", "그녀는 다시 비명처럼 외치며, 딸 앞 바닥에 주저앉아 그 무릎을 끌어안고, 거기에 머리를 기대고는, 자기 생명력이 낼 수 있는 온갖 미친 듯한 몸짓으로 여전히 앞뒤로 몸을 흔들었다."]], [["“Yes, mother,”", "\"네, 어머니.\""], ["returned Alice, stooping forward for a moment and kissing her, but endeavouring, even in the act, to disengage herself from her embrace.", "앨리스가, 잠시 몸을 앞으로 숙여 어머니에게 입을 맞추면서도, 그러는 와중에도 그 포옹에서 벗어나려 애쓰며 대답했다."], ["“I am here, at last.", "\"마침내 돌아왔어요."], ["Let go, mother; let go.", "놓으세요, 어머니. 놔요."], ["Get up, and sit in your chair.", "일어나서 의자에 앉으세요."], ["What good does this do?”", "이런다고 뭐가 좋아지나요?\""]], [["“She’s come back harder than she went!”", "\"이 애가 떠날 때보다 더 모질어져서 돌아왔어!\""], ["cried the mother, looking up in her face, and still holding to her knees.", "어머니가 딸의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 여전히 그 무릎을 붙든 채 외쳤다."], ["“She don’t care for me!", "\"나 따위는 아랑곳도 안 해!"], ["after all these years, and all the wretched life I’ve led!”", "이 세월이 다 지나도록, 내가 끌어온 그 비참한 삶이 다 지나도록!\""]], [["“Why, mother!”", "\"아니, 어머니!\""], ["said Alice, shaking her ragged skirts to detach the old woman from them: “there are two sides to that.", "앨리스가 누더기 치마를 흔들어 늙은 여인을 떼어 내며 말했다. \"거기엔 양면이 있는 법이에요."], ["There have been years for me as well as you, and there has been wretchedness for me as well as you.", "어머니뿐 아니라 내게도 세월이 있었고, 어머니뿐 아니라 내게도 비참함이 있었어요."], ["Get up, get up!”", "일어나세요, 일어나요!\""]], [["Her mother rose, and cried, and wrung her hands, and stood at a little distance gazing on her.", "어머니는 일어나서 울고, 두 손을 비틀며, 조금 떨어진 거리에 서서 딸을 응시했다."], ["Then she took the candle again, and going round her, surveyed her from head to foot, making a low moaning all the time.", "그러더니 다시 초를 집어 들고 딸 주위를 돌며 머리끝부터 발끝까지 살펴보면서, 내내 나직이 신음했다."], ["Then she put the candle down, resumed her chair, and beating her hands together to a kind of weary tune, and rolling herself from side to side, continued moaning and wailing to herself.", "그러고는 초를 내려놓고 다시 의자에 앉아, 두 손을 마주쳐 무슨 지친 가락을 두드리듯 하며 몸을 좌우로 흔들면서, 혼자 신음하고 흐느끼기를 계속했다."]], [["Alice got up, took off her wet cloak, and laid it aside.", "앨리스는 일어나 젖은 망토를 벗어 한쪽에 치워 놓았다."], ["That done, she sat down as before, and with her arms folded, and her eyes gazing at the fire, remained silently listening with a contemptuous face to her old mother’s inarticulate complainings.", "그러고는 아까처럼 다시 앉아, 팔짱을 끼고 불을 응시하면서, 경멸하는 얼굴로 늙은 어머니의 두서없는 푸념에 말없이 귀 기울이고 있었다."]], [["“Did you expect to see me return as youthful as I went away, mother?”", "\"내가 떠날 때처럼 젊은 모습으로 돌아오리라 기대하셨어요, 어머니?\""], ["she said at length, turning her eyes upon the old woman.", "마침내 그녀가 늙은 여인에게 눈길을 돌리며 말했다."], ["“Did you think a foreign life, like mine, was good for good looks?", "\"내가 산 것 같은 외지 생활이 미모에 좋을 거라 생각하셨나요?"], ["One would believe so, to hear you!”", "어머니 말씀 들으면 누구라도 그렇게 믿겠네!\""]], [["“It ain’t that!”", "\"그게 아니다!\""], ["cried the mother.", "어머니가 외쳤다."], ["“She knows it!”", "\"이 애도 알면서!\""]], [["“What is it then?”", "\"그럼 뭔데요?\""], ["returned the daughter.", "딸이 대답했다."], ["“It had best be something that don’t last, mother, or my way out is easier than my way in.”", "\"오래가지 않는 거라야 좋을 거예요, 어머니. 안 그러면 내가 나가는 길이 들어온 길보다 더 쉬울 테니.\""]], [["“Hear that!” exclaimed the mother.", "\"저 말 좀 들어 봐!\""], ["“After all these years she threatens to desert me in the moment of her coming back again!”", "어머니가 소리쳤다. \"이 세월이 다 지나, 돌아온 바로 그 순간에 나를 버리겠다고 협박을 하다니!\""]], [["“I tell you, mother, for the second time, there have been years for me as well as you,”", "\"두 번째로 말씀드리는데요, 어머니, 어머니뿐 아니라 내게도 세월이 있었어요.\" 앨리스가 말했다."], ["said Alice. “Come back harder?", "\"더 모질어져서 돌아왔다고요?"], ["Of course I have come back harder.", "물론 더 모질어져서 돌아왔죠."], ["What else did you expect?”", "그럼 달리 뭘 기대하셨어요?\""]], [["“Harder to me!", "\"나한테 더 모질다니!"], ["To her own dear mother!”", "자기 친어미한테!\""], ["cried the old woman", "늙은 여인이 외쳤다."]], [["“I don’t know who began to harden me, if my own dear mother didn’t,”", "\"내 친어머니가 나를 모질게 만들지 않았다면, 누가 나를 모질게 만들기 시작했는지 나는 모르겠네요.\""], ["she returned, sitting with her folded arms, and knitted brows, and compressed lips as if she were bent on excluding, by force, every softer feeling from her breast.", "그녀가, 팔짱을 끼고 미간을 찌푸리고 입술을 앙다문 채, 마치 어떤 부드러운 감정이든 힘으로 제 가슴에서 몰아내려 작정한 듯이 앉아서 대답했다."], ["“Listen, mother, to a word or two.", "\"어머니, 한두 마디만 들어 보세요."], ["If we understand each other now, we shall not fall out any more, perhaps.", "우리가 지금 서로 이해하게 되면, 어쩌면 다시는 다투지 않을지도 몰라요."], ["I went away a girl, and have come back a woman.", "나는 계집애로 떠났다가 여자가 되어 돌아왔어요."], ["I went away undutiful enough, and have come back no better, you may swear.", "도리를 모르는 채 떠났다가, 나아진 것 없이 돌아왔죠, 그건 맹세해도 좋아요."], ["But have you been very dutiful to me?”", "그런데 어머니는 내게 도리를 다하셨던가요?\""]], [["“I!”", "\"내가!\""], ["cried the old woman.", "늙은 여인이 외쳤다."], ["“To my gal!", "\"내 딸한테!"], ["A mother dutiful to her own child!”", "제 자식한테 도리를 다하는 어미라니!\""]], [["“It sounds unnatural, don’t it?”", "\"부자연스럽게 들리죠, 안 그래요?\""], ["returned the daughter, looking coldly on her with her stern, regardless, hardy, beautiful face; “but I have thought of it sometimes, in the course of my lone years, till I have got used to it.", "딸이, 매섭고 무심하고 강인하고 아름다운 얼굴로 그녀를 차갑게 바라보며 대답했다. \"하지만 나는 혼자였던 그 세월 동안 가끔 그 생각을 하다가, 끝내는 익숙해져 버렸어요."], ["I have heard some talk about duty first and last; but it has always been of my duty to other people.", "처음부터 끝까지 도리에 관한 이야기는 좀 들어 봤죠. 하지만 그건 늘 내가 남들에게 다해야 할 도리에 관한 거였어요."], ["I have wondered now and then—to pass away the time—whether no one ever owed any duty to me.”", "나는 가끔, 시간이나 때울 겸, 누구도 내게 다해야 할 도리는 없었던 건가 하고 곰곰 생각했어요.\""]], [["Her mother sat mowing, and mumbling, and shaking her head, but whether angrily or remorsefully, or in denial, or only in her physical infirmity, did not appear.", "어머니는 입을 우물거리고, 중얼거리고, 고개를 흔들며 앉아 있었으나, 그게 화가 나서인지 후회 때문인지, 부정하는 것인지, 아니면 그저 몸이 쇠약해서인지는 드러나지 않았다."]], [["“There was a child called Alice Marwood,”", "\"앨리스 마우드라는 아이가 있었어요.\""], ["said the daughter, with a laugh, and looking down at herself in terrible derision of herself, “born, among poverty and neglect, and nursed in it.", "딸이 웃으며, 자기 자신을 끔찍하게 비웃듯 제 몸을 내려다보며 말했다. \"가난과 방치 속에서 태어나, 그 속에서 길러졌죠."], ["Nobody taught her, nobody stepped forward to help her, nobody cared for her.”", "아무도 그 애를 가르치지 않았고, 아무도 나서서 도와주지 않았고, 아무도 그 애를 돌보지 않았어요.\""]], [["“Nobody!”", "\"아무도 안 돌봤다고!\""], ["echoed the mother, pointing to herself, and striking her breast.", "어머니가 자기 자신을 가리키고 가슴을 치며 따라 외쳤다."]], [["“The only care she knew,”", "\"그 애가 받은 유일한 보살핌이라곤,\""], ["returned the daughter, “was to be beaten, and stinted, and abused sometimes; and she might have done better without that.", "딸이 대답했다, \"맞고, 굶주리고, 이따금 욕을 먹는 거였어요. 차라리 그런 게 없는 편이 나았을 거예요."], ["She lived in homes like this, and in the streets, with a crowd of little wretches like herself; and yet she brought good looks out of this childhood.", "그 애는 이런 집들에서, 또 거리에서, 자기와 같은 처지의 가엾은 꼬마들 한 무리와 함께 살았어요. 그런데도 이런 어린 시절에서 어여쁜 외모를 가지고 나왔죠."], ["So much the worse for her.", "그러니 더더욱 안된 일이었어요."], ["She had better have been hunted and worried to death for ugliness.”", "차라리 못생겨서 사냥감처럼 시달리다 죽임을 당하는 편이 나았을 거예요.\""]], [["“Go on!", "\"계속해!"], ["go on!”", "계속해 봐!\""], ["exclaimed the mother.", "어머니가 외쳤다."]], [["“I am going on,”", "\"계속하고 있어요.\""], ["returned the daughter.", "딸이 대답했다."], ["“There was a girl called Alice Marwood.", "\"앨리스 마우드라는 처녀가 있었어요."], ["She was handsome.", "그 애는 예뻤죠."], ["She was taught too late, and taught all wrong.", "너무 늦게, 그리고 죄다 잘못된 방향으로 배웠어요."], ["She was too well cared for, too well trained, too well helped on, too much looked after.", "너무 잘 보살핌을 받고, 너무 잘 길러지고, 너무 잘 부추겨지고, 너무 많은 손길을 탔죠."], ["You were very fond of her—you were better off then.", "어머니는 그 애를 무척 아끼셨어요—그때는 형편이 좀 나았으니까요."], ["What came to that girl comes to thousands every year.", "그 처녀에게 닥친 일은 해마다 수천 명에게 닥치는 일이에요."], ["It was only ruin, and she was born to it.”", "그저 파멸이었고, 그 애는 그 파멸을 타고난 거였어요.\""]], [["“After all these years!”", "\"이 세월이 다 지났는데!\""], ["whined the old woman.", "늙은 여인이 흐느꼈다."], ["“My gal begins with this.”", "\"내 딸이 이런 말로 시작하다니.\""]], [["“She’ll soon have ended,”", "\"곧 끝낼 거예요.\""], ["said the daughter.", "딸이 말했다."], ["“There was a criminal called Alice Marwood—a girl still, but deserted and an outcast.", "\"앨리스 마우드라는 죄인이 있었어요—아직 처녀였지만, 버림받고 내쳐진 처지였죠."], ["And she was tried, and she was sentenced.", "그리고 그 애는 재판을 받았고, 형을 선고받았어요."], ["And lord, how the gentlemen in the Court talked about it!", "아이고, 법정의 그 양반들이 그걸 두고 어찌나 떠들어 대던지!"], ["and how grave the judge was on her duty, and on her having perverted the gifts of nature—as if he didn’t know better than anybody there, that they had been made curses to her!—and how he preached about the strong arm of the Law—so very strong to save her, when she was an innocent and helpless little wretch!—and how solemn and religious it all was!", "그 판사는 그 애의 도리에 대해, 그 애가 타고난 천부의 재능을 그르친 데 대해 어찌나 근엄하게 굴던지—마치 그 재능이 그 애에게는 저주가 되어 버렸다는 걸, 그 자리의 누구보다도 자기가 더 잘 알지 못한다는 듯이!—그리고 법의 강한 팔에 대해 어찌나 설교를 하던지—그 애가 죄 없고 의지가지없는 가엾은 어린것이었을 때는 그 강한 팔이 그 애를 구하는 데는 그토록 강하더니!—그리고 그 모든 게 어찌나 엄숙하고 신앙심 깊던지!"], ["I have thought of that, many times since, to be sure!”", "정말이지, 그 뒤로 나는 그 생각을 여러 번 했어요!\""]], [["She folded her arms tightly on her breast, and laughed in a tone that made the howl of the old woman musical.", "그녀는 두 팔을 가슴에 단단히 모으고는, 늙은 여인의 울부짖음을 음악처럼 들리게 만드는 어조로 웃었다."]], [["“So Alice Marwood was transported, mother,”", "\"그래서 앨리스 마우드는 유배되었어요, 어머니.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["she pursued, “and was sent to learn her duty, where there was twenty times less duty, and more wickedness, and wrong, and infamy, than here.", "\"그리고 자기 도리를 배우라며 보내졌는데, 거기엔 여기보다 도리가 스무 배는 더 적고, 사악함과 그릇됨과 치욕은 더 많았죠. 그리고 앨리스 마우드는 여자가 되어 돌아왔어요."], ["And Alice Marwood is come back a woman.", "이 모든 일을 겪고 났으니 마땅히 될 만한 그런 여자가 되어서요."], ["Such a woman as she ought to be, after all this.", "머지않아 더 엄숙하고, 더 그럴듯한 말이 오가고, 십중팔구 그 강한 팔도 또 등장하겠죠."], ["In good time, there will be more solemnity, and more fine talk, and more strong arm, most likely, and there will be an end of her; but the gentlemen needn’t be afraid of being thrown out of work.", "그러면 그 애도 끝장날 거예요. 하지만 그 양반들이 일자리를 잃을까 봐 걱정할 필요는 없어요."], ["There’s crowds of little wretches, boy and girl, growing up in any of the streets they live in, that’ll keep them to it till they’ve made their fortunes.”", "그들이 사는 거리 어디에서나, 사내애든 계집애든, 가엾은 꼬마들이 떼를 지어 자라나고 있으니까요. 그 양반들이 재산을 다 모을 때까지 일감을 대 줄 거예요.\""]], [["The old woman leaned her elbows on the table, and resting her face upon her two hands, made a show of being in great distress—or really was, perhaps.", "늙은 여인은 탁자에 두 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻으며 몹시 괴로워하는 시늉을 했다—아니, 어쩌면 정말로 괴로웠는지도 모른다."]], [["“There!", "\"자!"], ["I have done, mother,”", "이제 다 했어요, 어머니.\""], ["said the daughter, with a motion of her head, as if in dismissal of the subject.", "딸이, 그 이야기를 떨쳐 버리듯 고갯짓을 하며 말했다."], ["“I have said enough.", "\"할 만큼 했어요."], ["Don’t let you and I talk of being dutiful, whatever we do.", "우리가 뭘 하든, 어머니랑 나랑 도리를 다한다는 이야기는 하지 맙시다."], ["Your childhood was like mine, I suppose.", "어머니의 어린 시절도 내 것과 같았겠죠, 아마."], ["So much the worse for both of us.", "그러니 우리 둘 다 더더욱 안된 일이고요."], ["I don’t want to blame you, or to defend myself; why should I?", "어머니를 탓하거나 내 변명을 하고 싶은 게 아니에요. 그럴 이유가 어디 있겠어요?"], ["That’s all over long ago.", "그건 다 오래전에 끝난 일이에요."], ["But I am a woman—not a girl, now—and you and I needn’t make a show of our history, like the gentlemen in the Court.", "하지만 나는 이제 여자예요—더는 계집애가 아니라고요—그러니 어머니랑 나랑 법정의 그 양반들처럼 우리 내력을 늘어놓을 필요는 없어요."], ["We know all about it, well enough.”", "그건 우리가 충분히 잘 알고 있으니까요.\""]], [["Lost and degraded as she was, there was a beauty in her, both of face and form, which, even in its worst expression, could not but be recognised as such by anyone regarding her with the least attention.", "길을 잃고 타락한 처지였으나, 그녀에게는 얼굴에도 자태에도 아름다움이 있었으니, 그것은 가장 흉한 표정을 지을 때조차 조금이라도 주의 깊게 바라보는 이라면 누구든 아름다움으로 인정하지 않을 수 없는 것이었다."], ["As she subsided into silence, and her face which had been harshly agitated, quieted down; while her dark eyes, fixed upon the fire, exchanged the reckless light that had animated them, for one that was softened by something like sorrow; there shone through all her wayworn misery and fatigue, a ray of the departed radiance of the fallen angel.", "그녀가 침묵 속으로 가라앉으며, 거칠게 동요했던 얼굴이 잔잔해질 때, 불을 응시하던 그 검은 눈동자가 거기 활기를 불어넣던 무모한 빛을 슬픔 같은 무언가로 누그러진 빛으로 바꿀 때, 길에 지친 그 모든 비참함과 피로를 뚫고, 타락한 천사의 사라진 광채의 한 줄기 빛이 비쳐 들었다."]], [["Her mother, after watching her for some time without speaking, ventured to steal her withered hand a little nearer to her across the table; and finding that she permitted this, to touch her face, and smooth her hair.", "어머니는 한동안 말없이 그녀를 지켜보다가, 탁자 너머로 시든 손을 그녀 쪽으로 조금 슬그머니 내미는 모험을 감행했다. 그리고 딸이 이를 허락하는 것을 알자, 그 얼굴을 만지고 머리카락을 쓰다듬어 보았다."], ["With the feeling, as it seemed, that the old woman was at least sincere in this show of interest, Alice made no movement to check her; so, advancing by degrees, she bound up her daughter’s hair afresh, took off her wet shoes, if they deserved the name, spread something dry upon her shoulders, and hovered humbly about her, muttering to herself, as she recognised her old features and expression more and more.", "적어도 이 관심의 표시만큼은 늙은 여인이 진심인 듯한 느낌이 들었던지, 앨리스는 그녀를 제지하려는 어떤 움직임도 보이지 않았다. 그리하여 어머니는 차츰 다가가 딸의 머리를 새로 묶어 주고, 신발이라 부를 수 있다면 그 젖은 신발을 벗겨 주고, 어깨에 마른 무언가를 둘러 주고는, 딸의 옛 이목구비와 표정을 점점 더 알아보며 혼자 중얼거리면서, 겸손히 그 곁을 맴돌았다."]], [["“You are very poor, mother, I see,”", "\"어머니, 보아하니 무척 가난하시네요.\""], ["said Alice, looking round, when she had sat thus for some time.", "한동안 그렇게 앉아 있던 앨리스가 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["“Bitter poor, my deary,”", "\"지독히 가난하지, 얘야.\""], ["replied the old woman.", "늙은 여인이 대답했다."]], [["She admired her daughter, and was afraid of her.", "그녀는 딸을 감탄하면서도 딸을 두려워했다."], ["Perhaps her admiration, such as it was, had originated long ago, when she first found anything that was beautiful appearing in the midst of the squalid fight of her existence.", "어쩌면 그 감탄은, 그게 어떤 것이든, 오래전 그녀의 누추한 생존 투쟁 한가운데 처음으로 아름다운 무언가가 나타나는 것을 발견했을 때 비롯된 것이었으리라."], ["Perhaps her fear was referable, in some sort, to the retrospect she had so lately heard.", "어쩌면 그 두려움은, 어느 정도는, 그녀가 방금 들은 그 회고와 관련된 것이었으리라."], ["Be this as it might, she stood, submissively and deferentially, before her child, and inclined her head, as if in a pitiful entreaty to be spared any further reproach.", "어쨌거나, 그녀는 자식 앞에 순종적이고 공손하게 서서, 더는 책망받지 않게 해 달라고 애처롭게 간청하기라도 하듯 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“How have you lived?”", "\"어떻게 사셨어요?\""]], [["“By begging, my deary.", "\"구걸로 살았지, 얘야.\""]], [["“And pilfering, mother?”", "\"그리고 좀도둑질로요, 어머니?\""]], [["“Sometimes, Ally—in a very small way.", "\"가끔, 앨리—아주 소소하게."], ["I am old and timid.", "나는 늙었고 겁이 많아."], ["I have taken trifles from children now and then, my deary, but not often.", "가끔 아이들한테서 자질구레한 걸 슬쩍하긴 했다, 얘야, 그래도 자주는 아니야."], ["I have tramped about the country, pet, and I know what I know.", "시골을 떠돌아다녔지, 우리 아가, 그래서 내가 아는 건 알아."], ["I have watched.”", "나는 지켜봤어.\""]], [["“Watched?”", "\"지켜봤다고요?\""], ["returned the daughter, looking at her.", "딸이 그녀를 바라보며 되물었다."]], [["“I have hung about a family, my deary,”", "\"어떤 집안 둘레를 맴돌았단다, 얘야.\""], ["said the mother, even more humbly and submissively than before.", "어머니가 아까보다 한층 더 겸손하고 순종적으로 말했다."]], [["“What family?”", "\"무슨 집안요?\""]], [["“Hush, darling.", "\"쉿, 얘야."], ["Don’t be angry with me.", "화내지 마라."], ["I did it for the love of you.", "다 너를 사랑해서 한 일이야."], ["In memory of my poor gal beyond seas.”", "바다 건너에 있는 내 가엾은 딸을 생각하면서.\""], ["She put out her hand deprecatingly, and drawing it back again, laid it on her lips.", "그녀는 변명하듯 손을 내밀었다가, 다시 거두어들여 입술에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“Years ago, my deary,”", "\"여러 해 전에, 얘야,\""], ["she pursued, glancing timidly at the attentive and stern face opposed to her, “I came across his little child, by chance.”", "그녀가, 자기와 마주한 주의 깊고 매서운 얼굴을 겁먹은 듯 흘끔거리며 말을 이었다, \"우연히 그 사람의 어린 자식을 마주쳤단다.\""]], [["“Whose child?”", "\"누구의 자식이요?\""]], [["“Not his, Alice deary; don’t look at me like that; not his.", "\"그 사람 자식은 아니야, 앨리스 얘야. 그렇게 날 쳐다보지 마라."], ["How could it be his?", "그 사람 자식이 아니라고. 어떻게 그 사람 자식이겠니?"], ["You know he has none.”", "그 사람한텐 자식이 없는 걸 너도 알잖니.\""]], [["“Whose then?”", "\"그럼 누구의 자식인데요?\""], ["returned the daughter.", "딸이 대꾸했다."], ["“You said his.”", "\"어머니가 '그 사람'이라고 하셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Hush, Ally; you frighten me, deary.", "\"쉿, 앨리. 무섭다, 얘야."], ["Mr Dombey’s—only Mr Dombey’s.", "돔비 씨—그저 돔비 씨 말이야."], ["Since then, darling, I have seen them often.", "그 뒤로, 우리 아가, 그 사람들을 자주 봤어."], ["I have seen him.”", "그 사람도 봤단다.\""]], [["In uttering this last word, the old woman shrunk and recoiled, as if with sudden fear that her daughter would strike her.", "이 마지막 말을 입에 올리면서, 늙은 여인은 딸이 자기를 때리기라도 할까 봐 별안간 겁이 난 듯, 움츠러들며 뒷걸음질했다."], ["But though the daughter’s face was fixed upon her, and expressed the most vehement passion, she remained still: except that she clenched her arms tighter and tighter within each other, on her bosom, as if to restrain them by that means from doing an injury to herself, or someone else, in the blind fury of the wrath that suddenly possessed her.", "그러나 딸의 얼굴은 그녀에게 고정된 채 더없이 격렬한 격정을 드러내면서도, 그녀는 가만히 있었다. 다만 두 팔을 가슴 위로 점점 더 단단히 맞잡았는데, 마치 갑자기 자기를 사로잡은 그 분노의 눈먼 광기 속에서, 자기 자신이나 누군가에게 해를 입히지 못하도록 그렇게 해서 두 팔을 억누르려는 듯했다."]], [["“Little he thought who I was!”", "\"내가 누군지는 꿈에도 생각 못 했지!\""], ["said the old woman, shaking her clenched hand.", "늙은 여인이 맞잡은 손을 흔들며 말했다."]], [["“And little he cared!”", "\"신경도 안 썼겠죠!\""], ["muttered her daughter, between her teeth.", "딸이 이를 악물고 중얼거렸다."]], [["“But there we were, said the old woman, “face to face.", "\"하지만 우리가 거기서,\" 늙은 여인이 말했다, \"얼굴을 맞대고 있었단다."], ["I spoke to him, and he spoke to me.", "내가 그 사람한테 말을 걸었고, 그 사람도 내게 말을 했어."], ["I sat and watched him as he went away down a long grove of trees: and at every step he took, I cursed him soul and body.”", "나는 그가 긴 나무숲 길을 따라 내려가는 걸 앉아서 지켜봤지. 그리고 그가 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다, 나는 그 영혼과 육신에 저주를 퍼부었어.\""]], [["“He will thrive in spite of that,”", "\"그래도 그는 잘만 번창할 거예요.\""], ["returned the daughter disdainfully.", "딸이 경멸하듯 대꾸했다."]], [["“Ay, he is thriving,”", "\"그래, 잘 번창하고 있지.\""], ["said the mother.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["She held her peace; for the face and form before her were unshaped by rage.", "그녀는 입을 다물었다. 자기 앞의 얼굴과 형체가 분노로 일그러져 있었으니까."], ["It seemed as if the bosom would burst with the emotions that strove within it.", "마치 그 안에서 다투는 격정들로 가슴이 터져 버릴 것만 같았다."], ["The effort that constrained and held it pent up, was no less formidable than the rage itself: no less bespeaking the violent and dangerous character of the woman who made it.", "그것을 억누르고 가두어 두려는 안간힘은 그 분노 자체에 못지않게 무시무시했고, 그것을 행하는 여인의 격렬하고 위험한 성정을 못지않게 드러내 보였다."], ["But it succeeded, and she asked, after a silence:", "그러나 그 안간힘은 성공했고, 그녀는 침묵 끝에 물었다."]], [["“Is he married?”", "\"그는 결혼했어요?\""]], [["“No, deary,”", "\"아니, 얘야.\""], ["said the mother.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["“Going to be?”", "\"하려고 해요?\""]], [["“Not that I know of, deary.", "\"내가 알기론 아니다, 얘야."], ["But his master and friend is married.", "하지만 그 사람의 주인이자 친구는 결혼했어."], ["Oh, we may give him joy! We may give ’em all joy!”", "오, 우리가 그에게 축하를 빌어 줘도 되겠구나!"], ["cried the old woman, hugging herself with her lean arms in her exultation.", "우리가 그들 모두에게 축하를 빌어 줘도 되겠어!\" 늙은 여인이 의기양양해서 야윈 두 팔로 제 몸을 끌어안으며 외쳤다."], ["“Nothing but joy to us will come of that marriage.", "\"그 결혼에서 우리에겐 기쁨밖에 올 게 없어."], ["Mind me!”", "내 말 명심해라!\""]], [["The daughter looked at her for an explanation.", "딸은 설명을 바라며 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“But you are wet and tired; hungry and thirsty,”", "\"그런데 너 젖었고 지쳤구나. 배고프고 목도 마르겠지.\""], ["said the old woman, hobbling to the cupboard; “and there’s little here, and little”—diving down into her pocket, and jingling a few half—pence on the table—“little here.", "늙은 여인이 절뚝거리며 찬장으로 가면서 말했다. \"그런데 여기엔 가진 게 별로 없고, 또 별로\"—주머니 속을 뒤져 반 페니짜리 몇 닢을 탁자 위에 짤랑 떨어뜨리며—\"여기도 별로 없구나."], ["Have you any money, Alice, deary?”", "너 돈 좀 있니, 앨리스 얘야?\""]], [["The covetous, sharp, eager face, with which she asked the question and looked on, as her daughter took out of her bosom the little gift she had so lately received, told almost as much of the history of this parent and child as the child herself had told in words.", "탐욕스럽고 날카롭고 간절한 그 얼굴로 그녀가 그 질문을 던지며 지켜보는 동안, 딸이 가슴에서 그토록 얼마 전에 받은 그 작은 선물을 꺼내는 모습은, 딸 자신이 말로 들려준 것 못지않게 이 어미와 자식의 내력을 거의 다 말해 주었다."]], [["“Is that all?”", "\"그게 다니?\""], ["said the mother.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["“I have no more.", "\"더는 없어요."], ["I should not have this, but for charity.”", "적선이 아니었다면 이것도 없었을 거예요.\""]], [["“But for charity, eh, deary?”", "\"적선이 아니었다면, 응, 얘야?\""], ["said the old woman, bending greedily over the table to look at the money, which she appeared distrustful of her daughter’s still retaining in her hand, and gazing on.", "늙은 여인이, 딸이 손에 쥔 채 여전히 가지고 있는 게 미덥지 못한 듯, 그 돈을 보려고 탐욕스럽게 탁자 위로 몸을 굽히며 응시한 채 말했다."], ["“Humph!", "\"흠!"], ["six and six is twelve, and six eighteen—so—we must make the most of it.", "여섯에 여섯이면 열둘, 거기에 여섯이면 열여덟이라—그러니—우리가 이걸 최대한 알뜰히 써야겠다."], ["I’ll go buy something to eat and drink.”", "내가 가서 먹고 마실 걸 좀 사 올게.\""]], [["With greater alacrity than might have been expected in one of her appearance—for age and misery seemed to have made her as decrepit as ugly—she began to occupy her trembling hands in tying an old bonnet on her head, and folding a torn shawl about herself: still eyeing the money in her daughter’s hand, with the same sharp desire.", "겉모습에서 기대할 법한 것보다 훨씬 재빠른 동작으로—나이와 비참함이 그녀를 추한 만큼이나 쇠약하게 만들어 놓은 듯했으니까—그녀는 떨리는 손을 놀리기 시작해, 낡은 보닛을 머리에 매고 찢어진 숄을 몸에 두르기 시작했다. 그러면서도 여전히 똑같은 날카로운 욕망으로 딸의 손에 든 돈을 곁눈질했다."]], [["“What joy is to come to us of this marriage, mother?”", "\"이 결혼에서 우리에게 무슨 기쁨이 온다는 거예요, 어머니?\""], ["asked the daughter.", "딸이 물었다."], ["“You have not told me that.”", "\"그건 말씀 안 하셨잖아요.\""]], [["“The joy,”", "\"기쁨이란,\""], ["she replied, attiring herself, with fumbling fingers, “of no love at all, and much pride and hate, my deary.", "그녀가 더듬거리는 손가락으로 옷을 차려입으며 대답했다, \"사랑이라곤 전혀 없고, 자만과 증오만 그득한 데서 오는 기쁨이지, 얘야."], ["The joy of confusion and strife among ’em, proud as they are, and of danger—danger, Alice!”", "그들 사이의 혼란과 다툼에서 오는 기쁨, 그렇게 거만한 자들이니까. 그리고 위험에서 오는 기쁨—위험이야, 앨리스!\""]], [["“What danger?”", "\"무슨 위험요?\""]], [["“I have seen what I have seen.", "\"나는 내가 본 걸 봤어."], ["I know what I know!”", "나는 내가 아는 걸 안다고!\""], ["chuckled the mother.", "어머니가 키득거렸다."], ["“Let some look to it.", "\"누군가는 조심해야 할 게야."], ["Let some be upon their guard.", "누군가는 경계해야지."], ["My gal may keep good company yet!”", "내 딸도 아직 좋은 사람들과 어울리게 될지 몰라!\""]], [["Then, seeing that in the wondering earnestness with which her daughter regarded her, her hand involuntarily closed upon the money, the old woman made more speed to secure it, and hurriedly added, “but I’ll go buy something; I’ll go buy something.”", "그러더니, 딸이 자기를 바라보는 그 의아해하는 진지함 속에서 딸의 손이 저도 모르게 돈을 그러쥐는 것을 보고는, 늙은 여인은 그것을 손에 넣으려 한층 서두르며 황급히 덧붙였다. \"하지만 가서 뭘 좀 사 올게. 가서 뭘 좀 사 올 거야.\""]], [["As she stood with her hand stretched out before her daughter, her daughter, glancing again at the money, put it to her lips before parting with it.", "그녀가 딸 앞에 손을 내민 채 서 있자, 딸은 그 돈을 다시 한번 흘끗 보고는, 그것을 내주기 전에 입술에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“What, Ally!", "\"아니, 앨리!"], ["Do you kiss it?”", "거기다 입을 맞추는 거니?\""], ["chuckled the old woman.", "늙은 여인이 키득거렸다."], ["“That’s like me—I often do.", "\"꼭 나 같구나—나도 자주 그런단다."], ["Oh, it’s so good to us!”", "오, 이건 우리한테 참 고마운 거지!\""], ["squeezing her own tarnished halfpence up to her bag of a throat, “so good to us in everything but not coming in heaps!”", "그녀는 변색된 제 반 페니 동전들을 자루 같은 목에 꼭 눌러 대며 말했다, \"모든 면에서 우리한테 참 고맙지, 다만 무더기로 들어오지 않는다는 것만 빼면!\""]], [["“I kiss it, mother,”", "\"내가 여기 입을 맞춘 건, 어머니,\""], ["said the daughter, “or I did then—I don’t know that I ever did before—for the giver’s sake.”", "딸이 말했다, \"아니, 그때 입을 맞춘 건—전엔 그래 본 적이 있는지 모르겠지만—이걸 준 사람을 봐서예요.\""]], [["“The giver, eh, deary?”", "\"준 사람이라, 응, 얘야?\""], ["retorted the old woman, whose dimmed eyes glistened as she took it.", "늙은 여인이 응수했고, 돈을 받아 들 때 그 흐릿한 눈이 번득였다. \"그래!"], ["“Ay! I’ll kiss it for the giver’s sake, too, when the giver can make it go farther.", "준 사람이 이걸 더 멀리 가게 해 줄 수 있을 때면, 나도 준 사람을 봐서 입을 맞춰 주마."], ["But I’ll go spend it, deary.", "하지만 가서 이걸 쓰고 올게, 얘야."], ["I’ll be back directly.”", "곧 돌아오마.\""]], [["“You seem to say you know a great deal, mother,”", "\"어머니, 무척 많은 걸 아시는 것처럼 말씀하시네요.\""], ["said the daughter, following her to the door with her eyes.", "딸이 눈으로 그녀를 문까지 좇으며 말했다."], ["“You have grown very wise since we parted.”", "\"우리가 헤어진 뒤로 무척 영리해지셨어요.\""]], [["“Know!”", "\"알다마다!\""], ["croaked the old woman, coming back a step or two, “I know more than you think I know more than he thinks, deary, as I’ll tell you by and bye.", "늙은 여인이 한두 걸음 되돌아오며 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"네가 생각하는 것보다 많이 알아, 그가 생각하는 것보다 많이 알지, 얘야, 그건 차차 말해 주마."], ["I know all.”", "나는 다 알아.\""]], [["The daughter smiled incredulously.", "딸은 못 믿겠다는 듯 미소 지었다."]], [["“I know of his brother, Alice,”", "\"그의 동생에 대해서도 안다, 앨리스.\""], ["said the old woman, stretching out her neck with a leer of malice absolutely frightful, “who might have been where you have been—for stealing money—and who lives with his sister, over yonder, by the north road out of London.”", "늙은 여인이, 정말로 소름 끼치는 악의의 곁눈질로 목을 길게 빼며 말했다, \"그 동생은 너 있던 곳에 갔을 수도 있는 사람이야—돈을 훔쳐서 말이지—그리고 지금은 제 누이랑 같이 저 너머, 런던을 벗어나는 북쪽 길가에 살고 있지.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디라고요?\""]], [["“By the north road out of London, deary.", "\"런던을 벗어나는 북쪽 길가야, 얘야. 보고 싶으면 그 집을 보여 주마."], ["You shall see the house if you like.", "자기 형 집처럼 점잖긴 해도, 자랑할 만한 데는 못 돼."], ["It ain’t much to boast of, genteel as his own is.", "아니, 아니, 안 돼,\" 늙은 여인이, 딸이 벌떡 일어났으므로, 고개를 흔들고 웃으며 외쳤다, \"지금은 안 돼."], ["No, no, no,”", "너무 멀어."], ["cried the old woman, shaking her head and laughing; for her daughter had started up, “not now; it’s too far off; it’s by the milestone, where the stones are heaped;—to-morrow, deary, if it’s fine, and you are in the humour.", "돌무더기가 쌓여 있는 이정표 옆이야—내일, 얘야, 날이 좋고 네 마음이 내키면."], ["But I’ll go spend—”", "하지만 나는 가서 이걸 쓰고—\""]], [["“Stop!”", "\"멈춰요!\""], ["and the daughter flung herself upon her, with her former passion raging like a fire.", "그러더니 딸이, 아까의 그 격정을 불처럼 타오르게 하며 그녀에게 와락 달려들었다."], ["“The sister is a fair-faced Devil, with brown hair?”", "\"그 누이라는 게 갈색 머리에, 고운 얼굴을 한 악마예요?\""]], [["The old woman, amazed and terrified, nodded her head.", "늙은 여인은 놀라고 겁에 질려 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“I see the shadow of him in her face!", "\"그 여자 얼굴에서 그의 그림자를 봤어요!"], ["It’s a red house standing by itself.", "따로 외따로 서 있는 붉은 집이죠."], ["Before the door there is a small green porch.”", "문 앞에는 작은 초록색 현관이 있고요.\""]], [["Again the old woman nodded.", "다시 늙은 여인이 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“In which I sat today!", "\"내가 오늘 그 안에 앉아 있었어요!"], ["Give me back the money.”", "그 돈 도로 줘요.\""]], [["“Alice!", "\"앨리스!"], ["Deary!”", "얘야!\""]], [["“Give me back the money, or you’ll be hurt.”", "\"그 돈 도로 줘요, 안 그러면 다칠 거예요.\""]], [["She forced it from the old woman’s hand as she spoke, and utterly indifferent to her complainings and entreaties, threw on the garments she had taken off, and hurried out, with headlong speed.", "그녀는 말하면서 늙은 여인의 손에서 그것을 빼앗았고, 어머니의 푸념과 애원에는 아랑곳도 않은 채, 벗어 두었던 옷가지를 걸치고는, 쏜살같은 속도로 서둘러 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["The mother followed, limping after her as she could, and expostulating with no more effect upon her than upon the wind and rain and darkness that encompassed them.", "어머니는 할 수 있는 한 절뚝거리며 뒤따랐고, 그들을 에워싼 비와 바람과 어둠을 상대로 떠드는 것이나 다름없이, 딸을 상대로 항변해 보았지만 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["Obdurate and fierce in her own purpose, and indifferent to all besides, the daughter defied the weather and the distance, as if she had known no travel or fatigue, and made for the house where she had been relieved.", "제 목적에 완강하고 사나우며, 그 밖의 모든 것에 무심한 채, 딸은 마치 여행도 피로도 모르는 사람처럼 날씨와 거리를 무릅쓰고, 자기가 도움을 받았던 그 집을 향해 나아갔다."], ["After some quarter of an hour’s walking, the old woman, spent and out of breath, ventured to hold by her skirts; but she ventured no more, and they travelled on in silence through the wet and gloom.", "십오 분쯤 걸은 뒤, 기진맥진해 숨이 가빠진 늙은 여인은 딸의 치맛자락을 붙드는 모험을 감행했다. 그러나 그 이상은 감히 하지 못했고, 그들은 비와 어둠을 뚫고 말없이 나아갔다."], ["If the mother now and then uttered a word of complaint, she stifled it lest her daughter should break away from her and leave her behind; and the daughter was dumb.", "어머니는 이따금 푸념 한마디를 내뱉으면서도, 딸이 떨쳐 버리고 자기를 뒤에 남겨 둘까 봐 그것을 삼켰다. 그리고 딸은 묵묵부답이었다."]], [["It was within an hour or so of midnight, when they left the regular streets behind them, and entered on the deeper gloom of that neutral ground where the house was situated.", "자정이 한 시간쯤 남았을 무렵, 그들은 반듯한 거리들을 뒤로하고, 그 집이 자리한 그 중립 지대의 한층 깊은 어둠 속으로 들어섰다."], ["The town lay in the distance, lurid and lowering; the bleak wind howled over the open space; all around was black, wild, desolate.", "도시는 멀리, 음산하고 험상궂게 누워 있었고, 황량한 바람이 그 트인 공간 위로 울부짖었으며, 사방은 검고 거칠고 적막했다."]], [["“This is a fit place for me!”", "\"여긴 나한테 딱 어울리는 곳이야!\""], ["said the daughter, stopping to look back.", "딸이 멈춰 서서 뒤돌아보며 말했다."], ["“I thought so, when I was here before, today.”", "\"오늘 전에 여기 왔을 때도 그렇게 생각했어.\""]], [["“Alice, my deary,”", "\"앨리스, 얘야,\""], ["cried the mother, pulling her gently by the skirt.", "어머니가 치맛자락을 살며시 잡아당기며 외쳤다."], ["“Alice!”", "\"앨리스!\""]], [["“What now, mother?”", "\"이번엔 또 뭐예요, 어머니?\""]], [["“Don’t give the money back, my darling; please don’t.", "\"그 돈 돌려주지 마라, 우리 아가. 제발 그러지 마."], ["We can’t afford it.", "우리 형편에 그럴 여유 없어."], ["We want supper, deary.", "우리한텐 저녁거리가 필요해, 얘야."], ["Money is money, whoever gives it.", "돈은 돈이지, 누가 주든."], ["Say what you will, but keep the money.”", "네가 무슨 말을 하든, 그 돈은 그냥 가지고 있어.\""]], [["“See there!”", "\"저기 봐요!\""], ["was all the daughter’s answer.", "딸의 대답은 그게 다였다."], ["“That is the house I mean.", "\"저게 내가 말한 그 집이에요."], ["Is that it?”", "저거예요?\""]], [["The old woman nodded in the affirmative; and a few more paces brought them to the threshold.", "늙은 여인은 그렇다고 고개를 끄덕였다. 그리고 몇 걸음 더 가자 문턱에 이르렀다."], ["There was the light of fire and candle in the room where Alice had sat to dry her clothes; and on her knocking at the door, John Carker appeared from that room.", "앨리스가 옷을 말리려 앉아 있었던 그 방에는 난롯불과 촛불의 빛이 있었다. 그녀가 문을 두드리자, 존 카커가 그 방에서 나타났다."]], [["He was surprised to see such visitors at such an hour, and asked Alice what she wanted.", "그는 이런 시각에 그런 방문객을 보고 놀라, 앨리스에게 무슨 일이냐고 물었다."]], [["“I want your sister,”", "\"당신 누이를 만나고 싶어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“The woman who gave me money today.”", "\"오늘 내게 돈을 준 그 여자요.\""]], [["At the sound of her raised voice, Harriet came out.", "그녀의 높아진 목소리에, 해리엇이 나왔다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["said Alice.", "앨리스가 말했다."], ["“You are here!", "\"당신 여기 있군요!"], ["Do you remember me?”", "나를 기억해요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she answered, wondering.", "그녀가 의아해하며 대답했다."]], [["The face that had humbled itself before her, looked on her now with such invincible hatred and defiance; and the hand that had gently touched her arm, was clenched with such a show of evil purpose, as if it would gladly strangle her; that she drew close to her brother for protection.", "그녀 앞에서 자신을 낮추었던 그 얼굴이, 이제는 도무지 꺾을 수 없는 증오와 적의로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 팔을 부드럽게 만졌던 그 손은, 마치 기꺼이 그녀의 목을 졸라 버리기라도 하려는 듯, 그토록 사악한 의도를 드러내며 그러쥐어져 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 보호를 구하듯 오라비에게 바싹 다가섰다."]], [["“That I could speak with you, and not know you!", "\"내가 당신과 말을 나누면서도 당신을 못 알아봤다니!"], ["That I could come near you, and not feel what blood was running in your veins, by the tingling of my own!”", "내가 당신 곁에 다가가면서도, 내 피가 따끔거리는 걸로 당신 핏줄에 무슨 피가 흐르는지 느끼지 못했다니!\""], ["said Alice, with a menacing gesture.", "앨리스가 위협하는 몸짓으로 말했다."]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 말씀이세요?"], ["What have I done?”", "내가 뭘 어쨌다고요?\""]], [["“Done!”", "\"어쨌느냐고!\""], ["returned the other.", "상대가 대꾸했다. \"당신은 나를 당신 난롯가에 앉혔어."], ["“You have sat me by your fire; you have given me food and money; you have bestowed your compassion on me!", "내게 먹을 것과 돈을 줬지. 내게 당신의 동정을 베풀었어!"], ["You!", "당신이!"], ["whose name I spit upon!”", "내가 이름만 들어도 침을 뱉을 그 당신이!\""]], [["The old woman, with a malevolence that made her ugliness quite awful, shook her withered hand at the brother and sister in confirmation of her daughter, but plucked her by the skirts again, nevertheless, imploring her to keep the money.", "늙은 여인은, 자기 추함을 사뭇 끔찍하게 만드는 악의로, 딸의 말을 확인해 주듯 오라비와 누이를 향해 시든 손을 흔들었지만, 그러면서도 딸의 치맛자락을 다시 잡아당기며 그 돈을 가지고 있으라고 애원했다."]], [["“If I dropped a tear upon your hand, may it wither it up!", "\"내가 당신 손에 눈물 한 방울이라도 떨어뜨렸다면, 그 손이 말라 비틀어지기를!"], ["If I spoke a gentle word in your hearing, may it deafen you!", "내가 당신이 듣는 데서 부드러운 말 한마디라도 했다면, 그게 당신 귀를 멀게 하기를!"], ["If I touched you with my lips, may the touch be poison to you!", "내가 내 입술로 당신을 만졌다면, 그 닿음이 당신에게 독이 되기를!"], ["A curse upon this roof that gave me shelter!", "내게 거처를 내준 이 지붕에 저주가 있기를!"], ["Sorrow and shame upon your head!", "당신 머리 위에 슬픔과 수치가 있기를!"], ["Ruin upon all belonging to you!”", "당신에게 속한 모든 것에 파멸이 있기를!\""]], [["As she said the words, she threw the money down upon the ground, and spurned it with her foot.", "그 말을 하면서, 그녀는 돈을 땅바닥에 내던지고는 발로 걷어차 버렸다."]], [["“I tread it in the dust: I wouldn’t take it if it paved my way to Heaven!", "\"이걸 흙먼지 속에 짓밟아 버리겠어. 이게 천국으로 가는 내 길을 깔아 준다 해도 받지 않을 거야!"], ["I would the bleeding foot that brought me here today, had rotted off, before it led me to your house!”", "오늘 나를 여기로 데려온 이 피 흐르는 발이, 당신 집으로 나를 이끌기 전에 차라리 썩어 문드러져 떨어졌더라면!\""]], [["Harriet, pale and trembling, restrained her brother, and suffered her to go on uninterrupted.", "해리엇은 새파랗게 질려 떨면서도 오라비를 제지하고, 그녀가 끊김 없이 말을 이어 가게 두었다."]], [["“It was well that I should be pitied and forgiven by you, or anyone of your name, in the first hour of my return!", "\"내가 돌아온 첫 순간에, 당신이든 당신 이름을 가진 누구에게든 동정받고 용서받았다니, 참 잘된 일이야!"], ["It was well that you should act the kind good lady to me!", "당신이 내게 친절하고 착한 귀부인 노릇을 해 줬다니, 참 잘된 일이고!"], ["I’ll thank you when I die; I’ll pray for you, and all your race, you may be sure!”", "죽을 때 당신에게 고마워하지. 당신과 당신 일족 모두를 위해 기도해 주겠어, 그건 틀림없으니까!\""]], [["With a fierce action of her hand, as if she sprinkled hatred on the ground, and with it devoted those who were standing there to destruction, she looked up once at the black sky, and strode out into the wild night.", "그녀는 마치 땅바닥에 증오를 흩뿌리듯, 그리고 그것으로 거기 서 있는 이들을 파멸에 바치듯, 손을 사납게 내젓고는, 검은 하늘을 한 번 올려다보고, 거친 밤 속으로 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔다."]], [["The mother, who had plucked at her skirts again and again in vain, and had eyed the money lying on the threshold with an absorbing greed that seemed to concentrate her faculties upon it, would have prowled about, until the house was dark, and then groped in the mire on the chance of repossessing herself of it.", "어머니는 몇 번이고 헛되이 딸의 치맛자락을 잡아당겼고, 문턱에 떨어진 돈을 자기 정신을 온통 거기 쏟아붓는 듯한 탐욕으로 노려보고 있었던지라, 집이 어두워질 때까지 근처를 어슬렁거리다가 그것을 도로 손에 넣을 요량으로 진창을 더듬어 보았으련만."], ["But the daughter drew her away, and they set forth, straight, on their return to their dwelling; the old woman whimpering and bemoaning their loss upon the road, and fretfully bewailing, as openly as she dared, the undutiful conduct of her handsome girl in depriving her of a supper, on the very first night of their reunion.", "그러나 딸이 그녀를 끌고 갔고, 둘은 곧장 자기들 거처로 돌아가는 길에 올랐다. 늙은 여인은 길에서 그 손실을 두고 흐느끼며 한탄했고, 재회한 바로 그 첫날 밤에 자기에게서 저녁거리를 앗아 간 어여쁜 딸의 도리 없는 행실을, 감히 드러낼 수 있는 만큼 짜증스럽게 한탄했다."]], [["Supperless to bed she went, saving for a few coarse fragments; and those she sat mumbling and munching over a scrap of fire, long after her undutiful daughter lay asleep.", "그녀는 거친 부스러기 몇 점을 빼면 저녁도 못 먹고 잠자리에 들었다. 그리고 도리 없는 딸이 잠든 지 한참이 지나서까지, 자투리 불 위에 앉아 그 부스러기를 우물우물 씹으며 중얼거렸다."]], [["Were this miserable mother, and this miserable daughter, only the reduction to their lowest grade, of certain social vices sometimes prevailing higher up?", "이 비참한 어머니와 이 비참한 딸은, 그저 때로 더 높은 곳에서 횡행하는 어떤 사회적 악덕이 가장 낮은 등급으로 끌어내려진 모습일 뿐일까?"], ["In this round world of many circles within circles, do we make a weary journey from the high grade to the low, to find at last that they lie close together, that the two extremes touch, and that our journey’s end is but our starting-place?", "원 안에 또 원이 겹겹이 든 이 둥근 세상에서, 우리는 높은 등급에서 낮은 등급으로 지친 여정을 떠나, 끝내 그 둘이 바싹 붙어 있음을, 양극단이 서로 맞닿아 있음을, 그리고 우리 여정의 끝이 다름 아닌 우리의 출발점임을 알게 되는 것일까?"], ["Allowing for great difference of stuff and texture, was the pattern of this woof repeated among gentle blood at all?", "재료와 결의 큰 차이를 감안하더라도, 이 씨실의 무늬가 고귀한 혈통 사이에서도 되풀이되고 있었던 것은 아닐까?"]], [["Say, Edith Dombey!", "말해 보라, 이디스 돔비여!"], ["And Cleopatra, best of mothers, let us have your testimony!", "그리고 어머니 중의 으뜸인 클레오파트라여, 그대의 증언을 들려 다오!"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXV. The Happy Pair", "# CHAPTER XXXV. The Happy Pair"]], [["The dark blot on the street is gone.", "거리에 드리웠던 어두운 얼룩은 사라졌다."], ["Mr Dombey’s mansion, if it be a gap among the other houses any longer, is only so because it is not to be vied with in its brightness, and haughtily casts them off.", "돔비 씨의 저택은, 설령 다른 집들 사이에서 여전히 하나의 빈틈으로 남아 있다 하더라도, 그것은 오직 그 광채에 있어 견줄 데가 없어 다른 집들을 오만하게 밀쳐 내기 때문일 뿐이다."], ["The saying is, that home is home, be it never so homely.", "흔히들 집은 아무리 초라해도 집이라고 한다."], ["If it hold good in the opposite contingency, and home is home be it never so stately, what an altar to the Household Gods is raised up here!", "그 반대의 경우에도 이 말이 들어맞아 집은 아무리 위풍당당해도 집이라면, 여기 가정의 신들을 모실 얼마나 대단한 제단이 세워진 것인가!"]], [["Lights are sparkling in the windows this evening, and the ruddy glow of fires is warm and bright upon the hangings and soft carpets, and the dinner waits to be served, and the dinner-table is handsomely set forth, though only for four persons, and the side board is cumbrous with plate.", "오늘 저녁 창문마다 불빛이 반짝이고, 난롯불의 불그레한 광채가 휘장과 부드러운 양탄자 위에 따뜻하고 환하게 비치며, 만찬은 차려지기를 기다리고 있고, 만찬 식탁은 비록 단 네 사람을 위한 것이지만 보기 좋게 차려져 있으며, 식기대는 은그릇으로 묵직하다."], ["It is the first time that the house has been arranged for occupation since its late changes, and the happy pair are looked for every minute.", "최근의 변화 이후 이 집이 사람이 살 수 있도록 꾸며진 것은 이번이 처음이며, 그 행복한 부부는 시시각각 기다려지고 있다."]], [["Only second to the wedding morning, in the interest and expectation it engenders among the household, is this evening of the coming home.", "집안 식구들 사이에서 자아내는 흥미와 기대에 있어, 결혼식 아침에 버금가는 것이 바로 이 귀가의 저녁이다."], ["Mrs Perch is in the kitchen taking tea; and has made the tour of the establishment, and priced the silks and damasks by the yard, and exhausted every interjection in the dictionary and out of it expressive of admiration and wonder.", "퍼치 부인은 부엌에서 차를 마시고 있다. 그녀는 저택을 한 바퀴 둘러보고, 비단과 다마스크 천을 마(碼) 단위로 값을 매겨 보았으며, 감탄과 경이를 표현할 수 있는 사전 안팎의 감탄사를 죄다 써먹어 버렸다."], ["The upholsterer’s foreman, who has left his hat, with a pocket-handkerchief in it, both smelling strongly of varnish, under a chair in the hall, lurks about the house, gazing upwards at the cornices, and downward at the carpets, and occasionally, in a silent transport of enjoyment, taking a rule out of his pocket, and skirmishingly measuring expensive objects, with unutterable feelings.", "가구상의 십장은 모자를—그 안에는 손수건이 들어 있었는데 둘 다 니스 냄새를 강하게 풍겼다—현관 의자 밑에 두고는, 집 안을 어슬렁거리며 위로는 처마 장식을 올려다보고 아래로는 양탄자를 내려다보며, 이따금 말없는 황홀경에 빠져 주머니에서 자를 꺼내, 값나가는 물건들을 형언할 수 없는 감회로 살금살금 재어 보곤 한다."], ["Cook is in high spirits, and says give her a place where there’s plenty of company (as she’ll bet you sixpence there will be now), for she is of a lively disposition, and she always was from a child, and she don’t mind who knows it; which sentiment elicits from the breast of Mrs Perch a responsive murmur of support and approbation.", "요리사는 한껏 기분이 들떠서, 사람이 많은 자리에 자기를 두라고 한다(이제 그렇게 될 거라고 육 펜스 내기를 걸 수도 있다면서). 자기는 쾌활한 성미이며, 어릴 적부터 늘 그래 왔고, 누가 알든 상관없다는 것인데, 이 말에 퍼치 부인의 가슴에서는 지지와 찬동의 맞장구치는 중얼거림이 새어 나온다."], ["All the housemaid hopes is, happiness for ’em—but marriage is a lottery, and the more she thinks about it, the more she feels the independence and the safety of a single life.", "가정부가 바라는 것이라곤 그저 두 분의 행복뿐이다—하지만 결혼이란 복권 같은 것이라, 생각하면 생각할수록 그녀는 독신 생활의 독립과 안전을 더욱 절감한다."], ["Mr Towlinson is saturnine and grim, and says that’s his opinion too, and give him War besides, and down with the French—for this young man has a general impression that every foreigner is a Frenchman, and must be by the laws of nature.", "타울린슨 씨는 침울하고 무뚝뚝하게, 자기 생각도 그러하다며, 거기에 전쟁이나 한판 붙여 주고 프랑스 놈들이나 쳐부수자고 말한다—이 젊은이는 외국인이라면 누구나 프랑스인이며 자연의 법칙상 그럴 수밖에 없다는 막연한 생각을 품고 있기 때문이다."]], [["At each new sound of wheels, they all stop, whatever they are saying, and listen; and more than once there is a general starting up and a cry of “Here they are!” But here they are not yet; and Cook begins to mourn over the dinner, which has been put back twice, and the upholsterer’s foreman still goes lurking about the rooms, undisturbed in his blissful reverie!", "바퀴 소리가 새로 날 때마다, 그들은 무슨 말을 하던 중이든 멈추고 귀를 기울인다. 한 번 이상, 다들 벌떡 일어나 \"오셨다!\" 하고 외친다. 그러나 아직 오신 게 아니다. 그래서 요리사는 두 번이나 미뤄진 만찬을 두고 슬퍼하기 시작하고, 가구상의 십장은 여전히 더없이 행복한 몽상에 방해받지 않은 채 방마다 어슬렁거린다!"]], [["Florence is ready to receive her father and her new Mama.", "플로렌스는 아버지와 새어머니를 맞이할 채비를 하고 있다."], ["Whether the emotions that are throbbing in her breast originate in pleasure or in pain, she hardly knows.", "가슴속에서 두근거리는 감정이 기쁨에서 비롯된 것인지 고통에서 비롯된 것인지, 그녀는 잘 알지 못한다."], ["But the fluttering heart sends added colour to her cheeks, and brightness to her eyes; and they say downstairs, drawing their heads together—for they always speak softly when they speak of her—how beautiful Miss Florence looks tonight, and what a sweet young lady she has grown, poor dear!", "그러나 설레는 마음이 그녀의 뺨에 더한 홍조를, 두 눈에 더한 광채를 보태 준다. 아래층에서는—그녀에 대해 이야기할 때면 그들은 늘 나직이 말하므로—서로 머리를 맞대고, 오늘 밤 플로렌스 아가씨가 얼마나 아름다워 보이는지, 가엾게도 얼마나 사랑스러운 숙녀로 자라셨는지 이야기한다."], ["A pause succeeds; and then Cook, feeling, as president, that her sentiments are waited for, wonders whether—and there stops.", "잠시 침묵이 흐르고, 그러다 요리사가 좌장(座長)으로서 자기 소감이 기다려지고 있음을 느끼고, ……일지 궁금하다고 운을 떼고는—거기서 멈춘다."], ["The housemaid wonders too, and so does Mrs Perch, who has the happy social faculty of always wondering when other people wonder, without being at all particular what she wonders at.", "가정부도 궁금해하고, 퍼치 부인도 궁금해하는데, 이 부인은 무엇을 궁금해하는지는 전혀 가리지 않으면서도 남들이 궁금해할 때면 늘 따라서 궁금해하는 행복한 사교적 재능을 지녔다."], ["Mr Towlinson, who now descries an opportunity of bringing down the spirits of the ladies to his own level, says wait and see; he wishes some people were well out of this.", "이제 부인들의 기분을 자기 수준으로 끌어내릴 기회를 포착한 타울린슨 씨는, 두고 보면 안다고 말한다. 어떤 사람들은 이 일에서 무사히 빠져나왔으면 좋겠다고 한다."], ["Cook leads a sigh then, and a murmur of “Ah, it’s a strange world, it is indeed!”", "그러자 요리사가 한숨을 앞장세우며 \"아, 참 이상한 세상이야, 정말로!\""], ["and when it has gone round the table, adds persuasively, “but Miss Florence can’t well be the worse for any change, Tom.”", "하고 중얼거리고, 그 말이 식탁을 한 바퀴 돌고 나자, 달래는 투로 덧붙인다. \"하지만 플로렌스 아가씨야 무슨 변화가 있다 한들 더 나빠질 리는 없잖아요, 톰.\""], ["Mr Towlinson’s rejoinder, pregnant with frightful meaning, is “Oh, can’t she though!” and sensible that a mere man can scarcely be more prophetic, or improve upon that, he holds his peace.", "타울린슨 씨의 응수는 무시무시한 의미를 잔뜩 머금은 채 \"오, 과연 그럴까!\"라는 것이며, 한낱 사내가 그보다 더 예언적일 수도, 그 말을 더 보탤 수도 없으리라는 것을 의식하고는, 입을 다문다."]], [["Mrs Skewton, prepared to greet her darling daughter and dear son-in-law with open arms, is appropriately attired for that purpose in a very youthful costume, with short sleeves.", "스큐턴 부인은 사랑하는 딸과 사랑하는 사위를 두 팔 벌려 맞이할 채비를 하고서, 그 목적에 걸맞게 매우 젊은이다운 차림에, 소매가 짧은 옷을 입고 있다."], ["At present, however, her ripe charms are blooming in the shade of her own apartments, whence she had not emerged since she took possession of them a few hours ago, and where she is fast growing fretful, on account of the postponement of dinner.", "그러나 지금 그녀의 무르익은 매력은 자기 처소의 그늘 속에서 피어나고 있는데, 몇 시간 전 그곳을 차지한 이후로 한 번도 나오지 않았으며, 만찬이 미뤄진 탓에 그곳에서 점점 안달이 나고 있다."], ["The maid who ought to be a skeleton, but is in truth a buxom damsel, is, on the other hand, in a most amiable state: considering her quarterly stipend much safer than heretofore, and foreseeing a great improvement in her board and lodging.", "해골처럼 비쩍 말랐어야 마땅하나 실은 통통한 처녀인 하녀는, 반대로 더없이 상냥한 상태이다. 자기 분기별 봉급이 전보다 훨씬 안전하다고 여기며, 숙식이 크게 나아지리라 내다보고 있기 때문이다."]], [["Where are the happy pair, for whom this brave home is waiting?", "이 멋들어진 집이 기다리고 있는 그 행복한 부부는 대체 어디에 있는가?"], ["Do steam, tide, wind, and horses, all abate their speed, to linger on such happiness?", "증기와 조류와 바람과 말들이, 그런 행복 앞에서 머뭇거리느라 죄다 속력을 늦추기라도 하는가?"], ["Does the swarm of loves and graces hovering about them retard their progress by its numbers?", "그들 주위를 맴도는 사랑과 우아의 무리가 그 수가 너무 많아 진행을 더디게 하는가?"], ["Are there so many flowers in their happy path, that they can scarcely move along, without entanglement in thornless roses, and sweetest briar?", "그들의 행복한 길 위에 꽃이 어찌나 많은지, 가시 없는 장미와 더없이 향기로운 들장미에 얽히지 않고서는 좀처럼 나아갈 수 없기라도 한가?"]], [["They are here at last!", "마침내 그들이 왔다!"], ["The noise of wheels is heard, grows louder, and a carriage drives up to the door!", "바퀴 소리가 들리고, 점점 커지더니, 마차 한 대가 현관 앞으로 다가온다!"], ["A thundering knock from the obnoxious foreigner anticipates the rush of Mr Towlinson and party to open it; and Mr Dombey and his bride alight, and walk in arm in arm.", "그 얄미운 외국인이 우레 같은 노크 소리를 울려, 문을 열려고 달려가는 타울린슨 씨 일행보다 한발 앞선다. 돔비 씨와 그의 신부가 내려서, 팔짱을 끼고 안으로 걸어 들어온다."]], [["“My sweetest Edith!”", "\"내 더없이 사랑스러운 에디스!\""], ["cries an agitated voice upon the stairs.", "계단에서 떨리는 목소리가 외친다."], ["“My dearest Dombey!”", "\"내 더없이 사랑스러운 돔비!\""], ["and the short sleeves wreath themselves about the happy couple in turn, and embrace them.", "그러고는 짧은 소매가 그 행복한 부부를 차례로 휘감으며 끌어안는다."]], [["Florence had come down to the hall too, but did not advance: reserving her timid welcome until these nearer and dearer transports should subside.", "플로렌스도 현관까지 내려와 있었으나, 앞으로 나서지는 않았다. 더 가깝고 더 사랑하는 이들의 격정이 가라앉기를 기다리며 자기의 수줍은 환영을 미뤄 둔 것이었다."], ["But the eyes of Edith sought her out, upon the threshold; and dismissing her sensitive parent with a slight kiss on the cheek, she hurried on to Florence and embraced her.", "그러나 에디스의 눈은 문턱에서 그녀를 찾아냈다. 그리고 자기의 예민한 어머니를 뺨에 가벼운 입맞춤으로 떼어 내고는, 서둘러 플로렌스에게로 다가가 그녀를 끌어안았다."]], [["“How do you do, Florence?”", "\"잘 지냈니, 플로렌스?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, putting out his hand.", "돔비 씨가 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["As Florence, trembling, raised it to her lips, she met his glance.", "플로렌스가 떨면서 그 손을 들어 입술로 가져갈 때, 그녀는 그의 시선과 마주쳤다."], ["The look was cold and distant enough, but it stirred her heart to think that she observed in it something more of interest than he had ever shown before.", "그 눈길은 충분히 차갑고 멀었지만, 그 안에서 그가 전에 보인 적 없는 무언가의 관심 같은 것이 엿보인다고 생각하니 그녀의 마음이 일렁였다."], ["It even expressed a kind of faint surprise, and not a disagreeable surprise, at sight of her.", "그것은 심지어 그녀를 보고 일종의 옅은 놀라움을, 그것도 불쾌하지 않은 놀라움을 드러내기까지 했다."], ["She dared not raise her eyes to his any more; but she felt that he looked at her once again, and not less favourably.", "그녀는 더는 감히 그의 눈으로 시선을 들지 못했지만, 그가 다시 한번, 그에 못지않게 호의적으로 자기를 바라보았음을 느꼈다."], ["Oh what a thrill of joy shot through her, awakened by even this intangible and baseless confirmation of her hope that she would learn to win him, through her new and beautiful Mama!", "오, 그녀가 새롭고 아름다운 어머니를 통해 그의 마음을 얻는 법을 배우게 되리라는 희망이, 비록 만질 수도 없고 근거도 없는 이런 확인에 의해서나마 일깨워졌을 때, 얼마나 큰 기쁨의 전율이 그녀를 꿰뚫고 지나갔던가!"]], [["“You will not be long dressing, Mrs Dombey, I presume?”", "\"옷 갈아입는 데 오래 걸리지는 않으리라 보오, 돔비 부인?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I shall be ready immediately.”", "\"곧 준비될 거예요.\""]], [["“Let them send up dinner in a quarter of an hour.”", "\"십오 분 안에 만찬을 올리라고 하시오.\""]], [["With that Mr Dombey stalked away to his own dressing-room, and Mrs Dombey went upstairs to hers.", "그 말과 함께 돔비 씨는 자기 의상실로 성큼성큼 걸어갔고, 돔비 부인은 자기 방으로 위층에 올라갔다."], ["Mrs Skewton and Florence repaired to the drawing-room, where that excellent mother considered it incumbent on her to shed a few irrepressible tears, supposed to be forced from her by her daughter’s felicity; and which she was still drying, very gingerly, with a laced corner of her pocket-handkerchief, when her son-in-law appeared.", "스큐턴 부인과 플로렌스는 응접실로 갔는데, 그곳에서 그 훌륭한 어머니는 억누를 수 없는 눈물 몇 방울을 흘리는 것이 자기 도리라 여겼으니, 그 눈물은 딸의 행복에 못 이겨 자아내진 것으로 여겨졌다. 사위가 나타났을 때도 그녀는 여전히 그 눈물을, 손수건의 레이스 달린 모서리로, 매우 조심스럽게 닦아 내고 있었다."]], [["“And how, my dearest Dombey, did you find that delightfullest of cities, Paris?”", "\"그래, 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비, 그 더없이 즐거운 도시 파리는 어떠하셨나요?\""], ["she asked, subduing her emotion.", "그녀가 감정을 가라앉히며 물었다."]], [["“It was cold,”", "\"추웠습니다,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대꾸했다."]], [["“Gay as ever,”", "\"여전히 흥겹지요,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “of course.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"당연히.\""]], [["“Not particularly.", "\"딱히 그렇지도 않았습니다."], ["I thought it dull,”", "따분하더군요,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Fie, my dearest Dombey!”", "\"어머, 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비!\""], ["archly; “dull!”", "짐짓 장난스럽게. \"따분하다니요!\""]], [["“It made that impression upon me, Madam,”", "\"제게는 그런 인상이었습니다, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with grave politeness.", "돔비 씨가 점잖은 정중함으로 말했다."], ["“I believe Mrs Dombey found it dull too.", "\"돔비 부인도 그곳을 따분하게 여긴 듯합니다."], ["She mentioned once or twice that she thought it so.”", "한두 번 그렇게 생각한다고 말하더군요.\""]], [["“Why, you naughty girl!”", "\"아니, 이런 못된 것 같으니!\""], ["cried Mrs Skewton, rallying her dear child, who now entered, “what dreadfully heretical things have you been saying about Paris?”", "스큐턴 부인이, 이제 막 들어선 사랑하는 딸을 놀려 대며 외쳤다, \"파리에 대해 그렇게 끔찍하게 불경한 말들을 하고 다녔단 말이냐?\""]], [["Edith raised her eyebrows with an air of weariness; and passing the folding-doors which were thrown open to display the suite of rooms in their new and handsome garniture, and barely glancing at them as she passed, sat down by Florence.", "에디스는 지친 기색으로 눈썹을 치켜올렸다. 그러고는 새롭고 멋진 장식으로 단장한 방들을 펼쳐 보이려고 활짝 열어 둔 접이문을 지나면서, 지나가며 그것들을 흘끗 보는 둥 마는 둥 하고는, 플로렌스 곁에 앉았다."]], [["“My dear Dombey,”", "\"내 사랑하는 돔비,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “how charmingly these people have carried out every idea that we hinted.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"이 사람들이 우리가 넌지시 일러 준 생각을 하나하나 얼마나 멋지게 구현해 놓았는지요."], ["They have made a perfect palace of the house, positively.”", "이 집을 그야말로 완벽한 궁전으로 만들어 놓았어요, 정말로.\""]], [["“It is handsome,”", "\"훌륭합니다,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, looking round.", "돔비 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“I directed that no expense should be spared; and all that money could do, has been done, I believe.”", "\"비용을 아끼지 말라고 지시했습니다. 돈으로 할 수 있는 일은 모두 다 했으리라 봅니다.\""]], [["“And what can it not do, dear Dombey?”", "\"그런데 돈으로 못 할 게 무엇이 있겠어요, 사랑하는 돔비?\""], ["observed Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 말했다."]], [["“It is powerful, Madam,”", "\"강력한 것이지요, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["He looked in his solemn way towards his wife, but not a word said she.", "그는 엄숙한 태도로 아내 쪽을 바라보았으나, 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["“I hope, Mrs Dombey,”", "\"바라건대, 돔비 부인,\""], ["addressing her after a moment’s silence, with especial distinctness; “that these alterations meet with your approval?”", "그가 잠시 침묵한 뒤, 특히 또렷하게 그녀에게 말을 건넸다, \"이 개조가 당신 마음에 드셨으면 하오?\""]], [["“They are as handsome as they can be,”", "\"더 바랄 수 없을 만큼 훌륭하군요,\""], ["she returned, with haughty carelessness.", "그녀가 오만한 무심함으로 대꾸했다."], ["“They should be so, of course.", "\"당연히 그래야겠지요."], ["And I suppose they are.”", "그리고 그러리라 봅니다.\""]], [["An expression of scorn was habitual to the proud face, and seemed inseparable from it; but the contempt with which it received any appeal to admiration, respect, or consideration on the ground of his riches, no matter how slight or ordinary in itself, was a new and different expression, unequalled in intensity by any other of which it was capable.", "그 도도한 얼굴에는 경멸의 표정이 늘 어려 있어 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 듯 보였다. 그러나 그의 재력을 근거로 한 감탄이나 존경이나 배려의 요구—그 자체로는 아무리 사소하고 평범하든—를 받아들일 때의 그 경멸은, 새롭고 다른 표정이었으니, 그녀가 지을 수 있는 다른 어떤 표정도 그 강렬함에서 견줄 바가 못 되었다."], ["Whether Mr Dombey, wrapped in his own greatness, was at all aware of this, or no, there had not been wanting opportunities already for his complete enlightenment; and at that moment it might have been effected by the one glance of the dark eye that lighted on him, after it had rapidly and scornfully surveyed the theme of his self-glorification.", "자기의 위대함에 싸여 있던 돔비 씨가 이를 조금이라도 알아챘는지 어떤지는 모르나, 그가 완전히 깨우칠 만한 기회는 이미 모자라지 않았다. 그리고 바로 그 순간, 자기 자랑의 화제를 재빠르고 경멸스럽게 훑어본 뒤 그에게 떨어진 그 검은 눈의 한 번의 눈길로도 그것은 이루어질 수 있었으리라."], ["He might have read in that one glance that nothing that his wealth could do, though it were increased ten thousand fold, could win him for its own sake, one look of softened recognition from the defiant woman, linked to him, but arrayed with her whole soul against him.", "그는 그 한 번의 눈길에서 읽을 수 있었으리라—그의 재력이 만 배로 불어난다 한들, 재력 그 자체를 위해서는, 그에게 매여 있으나 온 영혼을 다해 그에게 맞선 그 반항적인 여인에게서 누그러진 인정의 눈길 하나조차 얻어 낼 수 없으리라는 것을."], ["He might have read in that one glance that even for its sordid and mercenary influence upon herself, she spurned it, while she claimed its utmost power as her right, her bargain—as the base and worthless recompense for which she had become his wife.", "그는 그 한 번의 눈길에서 읽을 수 있었으리라—그녀 자신에게 미치는 그 천하고 돈에 매인 영향력마저도 그녀가 멸시하면서, 동시에 그 최대한의 위력을 자기의 권리로, 자기의 거래로—그녀가 그의 아내가 된 대가인 그 비천하고 하찮은 보수로—주장하고 있다는 것을."], ["He might have read in it that, ever baring her own head for the lightning of her own contempt and pride to strike, the most innocent allusion to the power of his riches degraded her anew, sunk her deeper in her own respect, and made the blight and waste within her more complete.", "그는 그 한 번의 눈길에서 읽을 수 있었으리라—제 머리를 늘 제 경멸과 자존심의 번개에 맞도록 드러내 놓는 그녀에게, 그의 재력의 위세를 향한 더없이 순진무구한 암시조차도 그녀를 새로이 욕보이고, 자기 존중에서 그녀를 더 깊이 가라앉히며, 그녀 내면의 황폐와 폐허를 더욱 완전하게 만든다는 것을."]], [["But dinner was announced, and Mr Dombey led down Cleopatra; Edith and his daughter following.", "그러나 만찬이 알려졌고, 돔비 씨가 클레오파트라를 모시고 내려갔다. 에디스와 그의 딸이 뒤따랐다."], ["Sweeping past the gold and silver demonstration on the sideboard as if it were heaped-up dirt, and deigning to bestow no look upon the elegancies around her, she took her place at his board for the first time, and sat, like a statue, at the feast.", "그녀는 식기대 위의 금은 과시물을 마치 쌓아 놓은 흙더미라도 되는 양 휙 지나치며, 주위의 우아한 것들에 눈길 한 번 주는 것조차 마다하고는, 처음으로 그의 식탁에 자기 자리를 잡고서, 그 향연에 조각상처럼 앉았다."]], [["Mr Dombey, being a good deal in the statue way himself, was well enough pleased to see his handsome wife immovable and proud and cold.", "돔비 씨는 자신도 조각상 같은 면이 다분한지라, 자기의 아름다운 아내가 꿈쩍 않고 도도하고 차가운 것을 보고 흡족해했다."], ["Her deportment being always elegant and graceful, this as a general behaviour was agreeable and congenial to him.", "그녀의 거동이 늘 우아하고 기품 있는 만큼, 전반적인 처신으로서 이런 태도는 그에게 흐뭇하고 마음에 맞는 것이었다."], ["Presiding, therefore, with his accustomed dignity, and not at all reflecting on his wife by any warmth or hilarity of his own, he performed his share of the honours of the table with a cool satisfaction; and the installation dinner, though not regarded downstairs as a great success, or very promising beginning, passed off, above, in a sufficiently polite, genteel, and frosty manner.", "따라서 그는 늘 그러던 위엄으로 좌장 노릇을 하면서, 자기 쪽에서 어떤 따스함이나 유쾌함으로도 아내를 비추는 일 없이, 냉정한 만족으로 식탁의 주인 노릇을 했다. 그리하여 이 입주(入住) 만찬은, 비록 아래층에서는 대단한 성공이라거나 매우 전도유망한 시작이라고 여겨지지 않았을지언정, 위층에서는 충분히 정중하고 점잖고 서릿발 같은 분위기로 지나갔다."]], [["Soon after tea, Mrs Skewton, who affected to be quite overcome and worn out by her emotions of happiness, arising in the contemplation of her dear child united to the man of her heart, but who, there is reason to suppose, found this family party somewhat dull, as she yawned for one hour continually behind her fan, retired to bed.", "차를 마신 직후, 스큐턴 부인은—사랑하는 딸이 자기 마음의 남자와 맺어진 것을 바라보는 데서 솟아난 행복의 감정에 그만 완전히 압도되어 기진맥진한 척했으나, 한 시간 내내 부채 뒤에서 줄곧 하품을 한 것으로 보아 이 가족 모임이 다소 따분하다고 여겼으리라 짐작할 만한 이유가 있다—잠자리에 들었다."], ["Edith, also, silently withdrew and came back no more.", "에디스 또한 말없이 물러가서는 다시 돌아오지 않았다."], ["Thus, it happened that Florence, who had been upstairs to have some conversation with Diogenes, returning to the drawing-room with her little work-basket, found no one there but her father, who was walking to and fro, in dreary magnificence.", "그리하여, 위층에 올라가 디오게네스와 잠시 이야기를 나누고 온 플로렌스가 자그마한 반짇고리를 들고 응접실로 돌아왔을 때, 그곳에는 음울한 장엄함 속에 이리저리 거닐고 있는 아버지 말고는 아무도 없었다."]], [["“I beg your pardon.", "\"죄송해요."], ["Shall I go away, Papa?”", "저 나갈까요, 아빠?\""], ["said Florence faintly, hesitating at the door.", "플로렌스가 문가에서 망설이며 가냘프게 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니다,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, looking round over his shoulder; “you can come and go here, Florence, as you please.", "돔비 씨가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 대꾸했다. \"여기는 네가 마음대로 드나들어도 된다, 플로렌스."], ["This is not my private room.”", "여긴 내 개인 방이 아니니까.\""]], [["Florence entered, and sat down at a distant little table with her work: finding herself for the first time in her life—for the very first time within her memory from her infancy to that hour—alone with her father, as his companion.", "플로렌스는 들어와서 일감을 들고 멀찍이 떨어진 작은 탁자에 앉았다. 그녀는 난생처음으로—아주 어린 시절부터 그 순간까지 기억하는 한 그야말로 처음으로—아버지와 단둘이, 그의 동무로서 함께 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["She, his natural companion, his only child, who in her lonely life and grief had known the suffering of a breaking heart; who, in her rejected love, had never breathed his name to God at night, but with a tearful blessing, heavier on him than a curse; who had prayed to die young, so she might only die in his arms; who had, all through, repaid the agony of slight and coldness, and dislike, with patient unexacting love, excusing him, and pleading for him, like his better angel!", "그녀, 그의 타고난 동무이자 그의 유일한 자식, 외로운 삶과 슬픔 속에서 가슴이 찢어지는 고통을 겪어 온 아이, 거부당한 사랑 속에서도 밤이면 결코 눈물 어린 축복 없이는 하느님께 그의 이름을 읊조린 적이 없었으니 그 축복은 저주보다도 그에게 더 무거웠던 아이, 오직 그의 품 안에서 죽을 수만 있다면 하고 어려서 죽기를 기도했던 아이, 그 모든 세월 내내 무시와 냉대와 미움의 고통을, 그를 변명하고 그를 두둔하며, 마치 그의 수호천사처럼, 참을성 있고 아무것도 바라지 않는 사랑으로 갚아 온 아이가!"]], [["She trembled, and her eyes were dim.", "그녀는 떨었고, 두 눈은 흐려졌다."], ["His figure seemed to grow in height and bulk before her as he paced the room: now it was all blurred and indistinct; now clear again, and plain; and now she seemed to think that this had happened, just the same, a multitude of years ago.", "그가 방 안을 거니는 동안 그의 모습은 그녀 앞에서 키도 덩치도 자라나는 듯했다. 어느 순간엔 온통 흐릿하고 또렷하지 않았다가, 또 어느 순간엔 다시 선명하고 또렷해졌다."], ["She yearned towards him, and yet shrunk from his approach.", "그러다가 지금 이 일이, 꼭 이대로, 수많은 세월 전에 일어났던 것 같다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Unnatural emotion in a child, innocent of wrong!", "그녀는 그를 향해 애틋이 다가가고 싶으면서도, 그가 다가오는 것은 움츠러들며 피했다."], ["Unnatural the hand that had directed the sharp plough, which furrowed up her gentle nature for the sowing of its seeds!", "잘못이라곤 모르는 순진한 아이에게는 부자연스러운 감정이었다! 그 부드러운 천성을, 그 씨앗을 뿌리려고 날카로운 쟁기로 갈아엎도록 이끈 그 손이야말로 부자연스러웠다!"]], [["Bent upon not distressing or offending him by her distress, Florence controlled herself, and sat quietly at her work.", "플로렌스는 자기 괴로움으로 그를 괴롭히거나 거스르지 않으려 작정하고, 마음을 다잡으며 조용히 일감에 매달려 앉았다."], ["After a few more turns across and across the room, he left off pacing it; and withdrawing into a shadowy corner at some distance, where there was an easy chair, covered his head with a handkerchief, and composed himself to sleep.", "그는 방을 몇 번 더 이리저리 가로질러 거닐다가, 거니는 것을 멈추었다. 그러고는 안락의자가 놓인, 얼마쯤 떨어진 어둑한 구석으로 물러나, 손수건으로 머리를 덮고 잠을 청하려 자리를 잡았다."]], [["It was enough for Florence to sit there watching him; turning her eyes towards his chair from time to time; watching him with her thoughts, when her face was intent upon her work; and sorrowfully glad to think that he could sleep, while she was there, and that he was not made restless by her strange and long-forbidden presence.", "플로렌스에게는 그저 거기 앉아 그를 지켜보는 것으로 족했다. 이따금 그의 의자 쪽으로 눈길을 돌리고, 얼굴은 일감에 골몰해 있을 때면 마음으로 그를 지켜보았다. 그리고 자기가 거기 있는데도 그가 잠들 수 있다는 것, 자기의 낯설고 오랫동안 금지되어 온 존재가 그를 안절부절못하게 만들지 않는다는 것을 생각하니 서글프면서도 기뻤다."]], [["What would have been her thoughts if she had known that he was steadily regarding her; that the veil upon his face, by accident or by design, was so adjusted that his sight was free, and that it never wandered from her face an instant.", "만일 그녀가 알았더라면 그녀의 생각은 어떠했을까—그가 한결같이 그녀를 바라보고 있었다는 것을, 그의 얼굴을 덮은 그 천이, 우연이든 의도이든, 시야가 트이도록 맞추어져 있어, 그의 시선이 한순간도 그녀의 얼굴을 떠난 적이 없었다는 것을."], ["That when she looked towards him, in the obscure dark corner, her speaking eyes, more earnest and pathetic in their voiceless speech than all the orators of all the world, and impeaching him more nearly in their mute address, met his, and did not know it!", "그녀가 그 어둑하고 컴컴한 구석으로 그를 바라볼 때, 그 어떤 세상의 웅변가들보다도 그 소리 없는 말 속에서 더 간곡하고 더 애처로운, 그리고 그 말 없는 호소로 그를 더 가까이 질책하는 그녀의 그 말하는 듯한 눈이, 그의 눈과 마주쳤건만 정작 그것을 알지 못했다는 것을!"], ["That when she bent her head again over her work, he drew his breath more easily, but with the same attention looked upon her still—upon her white brow and her falling hair, and busy hands; and once attracted, seemed to have no power to turn his eyes away!", "그녀가 다시 일감 위로 고개를 숙였을 때, 그가 한결 편안하게 숨을 내쉬면서도, 여전히 똑같은 주의로 그녀를—그녀의 흰 이마와 흘러내린 머리카락과 바삐 움직이는 두 손을—바라보고 있었으며, 한번 끌린 뒤로는 도무지 눈을 돌릴 힘이 없는 듯했다는 것을!"]], [["And what were his thoughts meanwhile?", "그러는 동안 그의 생각은 어떠했을까?"], ["With what emotions did he prolong the attentive gaze covertly directed on his unknown daughter?", "그는 어떤 감정으로, 알지 못하는 딸에게 은밀히 던진 그 주의 깊은 응시를 그토록 길게 끌었을까?"], ["Was there reproach to him in the quiet figure and the mild eyes?", "그 고요한 자태와 온순한 눈에 그를 향한 책망이 담겨 있었던가?"], ["Had he begun to feel her disregarded claims and did they touch him home at last, and waken him to some sense of his cruel injustice?", "그가 등한시해 온 그녀의 권리를 느끼기 시작했던가, 그리고 그것이 마침내 그의 마음 깊이 와닿아, 자기의 잔인한 불의에 대한 어떤 자각으로 그를 일깨웠던가?"]], [["There are yielding moments in the lives of the sternest and harshest men, though such men often keep their secret well.", "더없이 엄혹하고 모진 사내들의 삶에도 마음이 누그러지는 순간들이 있는 법이다, 비록 그런 사내들이 흔히 그 비밀을 잘 감추기는 하지만."], ["The sight of her in her beauty, almost changed into a woman without his knowledge, may have struck out some such moments even in his life of pride.", "자기도 모르는 새 거의 한 사람의 여인으로 변모한 그녀의 아름다운 모습이, 그의 그 오만한 삶 속에서도 그런 순간들을 불러일으켰는지도 모른다."], ["Some passing thought that he had had a happy home within his reach—had had a household spirit bending at his feet—had overlooked it in his stiffnecked sullen arrogance, and wandered away and lost himself, may have engendered them.", "손 닿는 곳에 행복한 가정을 두고 있었음을—제 발치에 엎드린 가정의 정령을 두고 있었음을—그것을 그 목이 뻣뻣하고 음울한 오만 속에서 못 본 체하고는, 멀리 헤매다가 길을 잃었음을 깨닫는 어떤 스쳐 가는 생각이, 그것들을 자아냈는지도 모른다."], ["Some simple eloquence distinctly heard, though only uttered in her eyes, unconscious that he read them as “By the death-beds I have tended, by the childhood I have suffered, by our meeting in this dreary house at midnight, by the cry wrung from me in the anguish of my heart, oh, father, turn to me and seek a refuge in my love before it is too late!” may have arrested them.", "오직 그녀의 눈으로만 말해졌을 뿐, 그가 그것을 읽고 있는 줄도 모르는 채, 또렷이 들려오는 어떤 소박한 웅변이—\"제가 지켜본 임종의 자리들에 걸고, 제가 겪은 어린 시절에 걸고, 이 음울한 집에서 한밤중에 이렇게 만난 것에 걸고, 가슴의 고통 속에서 제게서 짜내어진 그 부르짖음에 걸고, 오, 아버지, 너무 늦기 전에 저를 돌아보시고 제 사랑 안에서 안식처를 찾으세요!\"라고 말하는 그 웅변이—그것들을 붙들어 두었는지도 모른다."], ["Meaner and lower thoughts, as that his dead boy was now superseded by new ties, and he could forgive the having been supplanted in his affection, may have occasioned them.", "죽은 아들이 이제 새로운 인연들로 대신되었으니, 자기의 애정에서 밀려난 것쯤은 용서할 수 있다는 식의, 더 비루하고 더 낮은 생각들이 그것들을 일으켰는지도 모른다."], ["The mere association of her as an ornament, with all the ornament and pomp about him, may have been sufficient.", "그녀를 하나의 장식물로, 자기 주위의 모든 장식과 호사와 연결 짓는 것만으로 충분했는지도 모른다."], ["But as he looked, he softened to her, more and more.", "그러나 그는 바라보면 바라볼수록 그녀에게 점점 더 마음이 누그러졌다."], ["As he looked, she became blended with the child he had loved, and he could hardly separate the two.", "바라보고 있노라니, 그녀는 그가 사랑했던 그 아이와 뒤섞여, 그는 좀처럼 그 둘을 갈라 놓을 수가 없었다."], ["As he looked, he saw her for an instant by a clearer and a brighter light, not bending over that child’s pillow as his rival—monstrous thought—but as the spirit of his home, and in the action tending himself no less, as he sat once more with his bowed-down head upon his hand at the foot of the little bed.", "바라보고 있노라니, 그는 한순간 더 맑고 더 환한 빛 속에서 그녀를 보았으니, 그녀가 그 아이의 베갯머리에 몸을 굽힌 모습이—그의 경쟁자로서가 아니라—그 무슨 끔찍한 생각이런가—그의 가정의 정령으로서, 그리고 그 행위 속에서 자기 자신을 못지않게 돌보아 주는 모습으로 보였으니, 그동안 그는 다시금 그 자그마한 침대 발치에서 손에 머리를 떨군 채 앉아 있었다."], ["He felt inclined to speak to her, and call her to him.", "그는 그녀에게 말을 걸어, 그녀를 자기 곁으로 부르고 싶은 마음이 들었다."], ["The words “Florence, come here!” were rising to his lips—but slowly and with difficulty, they were so very strange—when they were checked and stifled by a footstep on the stair.", "\"플로렌스, 이리 오너라!\"라는 말이 그의 입술로 올라오고 있었다—그러나 더디게, 그리고 어렵사리, 그 말은 너무도 낯설었으니—그러다가 계단 위의 발소리에 막혀 목구멍에서 삼켜졌다."]], [["It was his wife’s.", "그의 아내의 발소리였다."], ["She had exchanged her dinner dress for a loose robe, and unbound her hair, which fell freely about her neck.", "그녀는 만찬용 드레스를 헐렁한 가운으로 갈아입고, 머리를 풀어 목덜미를 따라 자유로이 늘어뜨리고 있었다."], ["But this was not the change in her that startled him.", "그러나 그를 놀라게 한 것은 이런 변화가 아니었다."]], [["“Florence, dear,”", "\"플로렌스, 얘야,\""], ["she said, “I have been looking for you everywhere.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"너를 사방으로 찾아다녔단다.\""]], [["As she sat down by the side of Florence, she stooped and kissed her hand.", "플로렌스 곁에 앉으면서 그녀는 몸을 굽혀 그 손에 입을 맞추었다."], ["He hardly knew his wife. She was so changed.", "그는 자기 아내를 거의 알아보지 못했다."], ["It was not merely that her smile was new to him—though that he had never seen; but her manner, the tone of her voice, the light of her eyes, the interest, and confidence, and winning wish to please, expressed in all-this was not Edith.", "그녀가 너무도 달라져 있었다. 그것은 단지 그 미소가 그에게 낯설어서만은 아니었으니—하기야 그런 미소는 본 적도 없었지만—그녀의 태도, 목소리의 어조, 두 눈의 빛, 그 모든 것에 깃든 관심과 신뢰와 기쁘게 해 주려는 사랑스러운 바람—이것은 에디스가 아니었다."]], [["“Softly, dear Mama.", "\"가만히요, 엄마."], ["Papa is asleep.”", "아빠가 주무세요.\""]], [["It was Edith now.", "이제는 에디스였다."], ["She looked towards the corner where he was, and he knew that face and manner very well.", "그녀는 그가 있는 구석 쪽을 바라보았고, 그는 그 얼굴과 태도를 아주 잘 알았다."]], [["“I scarcely thought you could be here, Florence.”", "\"네가 여기 있으리라고는 거의 생각지 못했단다, 플로렌스.\""]], [["Again, how altered and how softened, in an instant!", "다시, 한순간에 얼마나 달라지고 얼마나 부드러워졌는지!"]], [["“I left here early,”", "\"여기서 일찍 자리를 떴단다,\""], ["pursued Edith, “purposely to sit upstairs and talk with you.", "에디스가 이어 말했다, \"일부러 위층에 올라가 너와 이야기를 나누려고."], ["But, going to your room, I found my bird was flown, and I have been waiting there ever since, expecting its return.", "그런데 네 방에 가 보니 내 작은 새가 날아가 버렸기에, 그때부터 줄곧 거기서, 새가 돌아오기를 기다리고 있었지.\""]], [["If it had been a bird, indeed, she could not have taken it more tenderly and gently to her breast, than she did Florence.", "설령 정말로 새였다 한들, 그녀가 플로렌스를 가슴에 안은 것보다 더 다정하고 더 부드럽게 안을 수는 없었으리라."]], [["“Come, dear!”", "\"가자, 얘야!\""]], [["“Papa will not expect to find me, I suppose, when he wakes,”", "\"아빠가 깨어나셨을 때 제가 있을 거라고는 기대하지 않으시겠지요, 아마,\""], ["hesitated Florence.", "플로렌스가 망설이며 말했다."]], [["“Do you think he will, Florence?”", "\"네 생각엔 기대하실 것 같니, 플로렌스?\""], ["said Edith, looking full upon her.", "에디스가 그녀를 똑바로 바라보며 말했다."]], [["Florence drooped her head, and rose, and put up her work-basket.", "플로렌스는 고개를 떨구고, 일어서서, 반짇고리를 챙겼다."], ["Edith drew her hand through her arm, and they went out of the room like sisters.", "에디스가 자기 팔로 그녀의 팔을 끼었고, 둘은 자매처럼 방을 나갔다."], ["Her very step was different and new to him, Mr Dombey thought, as his eyes followed her to the door.", "그녀의 발걸음마저도 자기에게는 사뭇 다르고 낯설게 느껴진다고, 돔비 씨는 그녀가 문께로 가는 것을 눈으로 좇으며 생각했다."]], [["He sat in his shadowy corner so long, that the church clocks struck the hour three times before he moved that night.", "그는 그 어둑한 구석에 어찌나 오래 앉아 있었던지, 그날 밤 그가 몸을 움직이기 전에 교회 시계가 세 번이나 시각을 알렸다."], ["All that while his face was still intent upon the spot where Florence had been seated.", "그동안 내내 그의 얼굴은 여전히 플로렌스가 앉아 있던 자리에 골똘히 향해 있었다."], ["The room grew darker, as the candles waned and went out; but a darkness gathered on his face, exceeding any that the night could cast, and rested there.", "촛불이 사위어 꺼져 가면서 방은 점점 어두워졌다. 그러나 그의 얼굴 위에는, 밤이 드리울 수 있는 그 어떤 어둠보다도 짙은 어둠이 모여들어, 거기 머물렀다."]], [["Florence and Edith, seated before the fire in the remote room where little Paul had died, talked together for a long time.", "플로렌스와 에디스는, 어린 폴이 숨을 거둔 그 외딴방에서 난롯불 앞에 앉아, 오래도록 함께 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Diogenes, who was of the party, had at first objected to the admission of Edith, and, even in deference to his mistress’s wish, had only permitted it under growling protest.", "그 자리에 함께한 디오게네스는 처음에는 에디스가 들어오는 것을 반대했고, 제 여주인의 바람에 마지못해 따르면서도 으르렁대는 항의 속에서야 그것을 허락했을 뿐이었다."], ["But, emerging by little and little from the ante-room, whither he had retired in dudgeon, he soon appeared to comprehend, that with the most amiable intentions he had made one of those mistakes which will occasionally arise in the best-regulated dogs’ minds; as a friendly apology for which he stuck himself up on end between the two, in a very hot place in front of the fire, and sat panting at it, with his tongue out, and a most imbecile expression of countenance, listening to the conversation.", "그러나 골이 나서 물러나 있던 대기실에서 조금씩 조금씩 다시 나오더니, 곧 깨달은 듯했다—더없이 좋은 의도로 한 일이긴 하나, 아무리 잘 길든 개의 마음에도 이따금 생기곤 하는 그런 실수를 하나 저질렀음을. 그 우호적인 사과의 표시로 디오게네스는 난롯불 바로 앞 몹시 뜨거운 자리에서 두 사람 사이에 꼿꼿이 버티고 앉아, 혀를 빼물고 더없이 멍청한 표정을 지은 채, 헐떡거리며 대화에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["It turned, at first, on Florence’s books and favourite pursuits, and on the manner in which she had beguiled the interval since the marriage.", "이야기는 처음엔 플로렌스의 책과 그녀가 좋아하는 소일거리에 관한 것으로, 그리고 결혼식 이후의 시간을 그녀가 어떻게 보냈는지에 관한 것으로 흘렀다."], ["The last theme opened up to her a subject which lay very near her heart, and she said, with the tears starting to her eyes:", "마지막 화제는 그녀의 가슴 가장 깊은 곳에 자리한 어떤 일을 열어젖혔고, 그녀는 두 눈에 눈물이 솟구치는 채로 말했다."]], [["“Oh, Mama!", "\"오, 엄마!"], ["I have had a great sorrow since that day.”", "그날 이후로 저는 커다란 슬픔을 겪었어요.\""]], [["“You a great sorrow, Florence!”", "\"네가 커다란 슬픔을, 플로렌스!\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Poor Walter is drowned.”", "가엾은 월터가 물에 빠져 죽었어요.\""]], [["Florence spread her hands before her face, and wept with all her heart.", "플로렌스는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고, 마음을 다해 흐느껴 울었다."], ["Many as were the secret tears which Walter’s fate had cost her, they flowed yet, when she thought or spoke of him.", "월터의 운명이 그녀에게 치르게 한 남모를 눈물이 아무리 많았어도, 그를 생각하거나 그에 대해 이야기할 때면 그 눈물은 여전히 흘렀다."]], [["“But tell me, dear,”", "\"하지만 말해 주렴, 얘야,\""], ["said Edith, soothing her.", "에디스가 그녀를 달래며 말했다."], ["“Who was Walter?", "\"월터가 누구였니?"], ["What was he to you?”", "너에게 어떤 사람이었지?\""]], [["“He was my brother, Mama.", "\"제 오라버니였어요, 엄마."], ["After dear Paul died, we said we would be brother and sister.", "사랑하는 폴이 죽은 뒤에, 우리는 오누이가 되자고 했어요. 저는 그를 오래—아주 어릴 적부터 알고 지냈어요."], ["I had known him a long time—from a little child.", "그는 폴을 알았고, 폴도 그를 무척 좋아했어요."], ["He knew Paul, who liked him very much; Paul said, almost at the last, ‘Take care of Walter, dear Papa!", "폴은 거의 마지막에 이렇게 말했어요. '월터를 잘 보살펴 주세요, 사랑하는 아빠!"], ["I was fond of him!’ Walter had been brought in to see him, and was there then—in this room.”", "저는 그를 좋아했어요!' 월터는 폴을 보러 들여보내져서, 그때 거기에—이 방에 있었어요.\""]], [["“And did he take care of Walter?”", "\"그래서 그분이 월터를 보살폈니?\""], ["inquired Edith, sternly.", "에디스가 매섭게 물었다."]], [["“Papa?", "\"아빠가요?"], ["He appointed him to go abroad.", "아빠는 그를 외국으로 보내도록 지명하셨어요."], ["He was drowned in shipwreck on his voyage,”", "그는 항해 중에 난파를 당해 물에 빠져 죽었어요,\""], ["said Florence, sobbing.", "플로렌스가 흐느끼며 말했다."]], [["“Does he know that he is dead?”", "\"그분은 그가 죽은 걸 아시니?\""], ["asked Edith.", "에디스가 물었다."]], [["“I cannot tell, Mama.", "\"모르겠어요, 엄마."], ["I have no means of knowing.", "알 길이 없어요. 사랑하는 엄마!\""], ["Dear Mama!” cried Florence, clinging to her as for help, and hiding her face upon her bosom, “I know that you have seen—”", "플로렌스가 도움을 청하듯 그녀에게 매달리며, 그 가슴에 얼굴을 묻고 외쳤다, \"저는 엄마가 보셨다는 걸 알아요—\""]], [["“Stay!", "\"잠깐!"], ["Stop, Florence.”", "멈추렴, 플로렌스.\""], ["Edith turned so pale, and spoke so earnestly, that Florence did not need her restraining hand upon her lips.", "에디스가 어찌나 창백해지며, 또 어찌나 간곡하게 말했던지, 플로렌스는 자기 입술을 막는 그녀의 손이 필요치 않았다."], ["“Tell me all about Walter first; let me understand this history all through.”", "\"먼저 월터에 대해 다 말해 주렴. 이 이야기를 처음부터 끝까지 다 이해하게 해 주렴.\""]], [["Florence related it, and everything belonging to it, even down to the friendship of Mr Toots, of whom she could hardly speak in her distress without a tearful smile, although she was deeply grateful to him.", "플로렌스는 그 이야기를, 그리고 거기 얽힌 모든 것을 다 들려주었으니, 심지어 투츠 씨의 우정에 이르기까지—비록 그에게 깊이 감사하면서도, 그녀는 슬픔에 잠긴 와중에 눈물 어린 미소 없이는 좀처럼 그에 대해 말할 수가 없었다."], ["When she had concluded her account, to the whole of which Edith, holding her hand, listened with close attention, and when a silence had succeeded, Edith said:", "그녀의 손을 잡은 채 그 이야기 전부를 골똘히 귀 기울여 들은 에디스가, 그녀가 이야기를 마치고 나서 침묵이 뒤따랐을 때, 말했다."]], [["“What is it that you know I have seen, Florence?”", "\"내가 무엇을 보았다는 거니, 플로렌스?\""]], [["“That I am not,”", "\"제가,\""], ["said Florence, with the same mute appeal, and the same quick concealment of her face as before, “that I am not a favourite child, Mama.", "플로렌스가 아까와 똑같이 말없이 호소하며, 아까와 똑같이 얼른 얼굴을 가리고 말했다, \"제가 사랑받는 자식이 아니라는 거요, 엄마. 저는 한 번도 그래 본 적이 없어요."], ["I never have been. I have never known how to be.", "어떻게 하면 되는지도 도무지 알지 못했어요."], ["I have missed the way, and had no one to show it to me.", "저는 그 길을 놓쳤고, 제게 그 길을 일러 줄 사람도 없었어요."], ["Oh, let me learn from you how to become dearer to Papa Teach me!", "오, 어떻게 하면 아빠에게 더 사랑받을 수 있는지 엄마에게서 배우게 해 주세요."], ["you, who can so well!”", "가르쳐 주세요!"], ["and clinging closer to her, with some broken fervent words of gratitude and endearment, Florence, relieved of her sad secret, wept long, but not as painfully as of yore, within the encircling arms of her new mother.", "엄마야말로 그토록 잘하실 수 있잖아요!\" 그러고는 더 바싹 그녀에게 매달리며, 감사와 애정의 끊어질 듯한 뜨거운 말 몇 마디와 함께, 그 슬픈 비밀을 덜어 낸 플로렌스는, 자기 새어머니의 감싸 안은 두 팔 안에서, 오래도록, 그러나 예전만큼 괴롭지는 않게 울었다."]], [["Pale even to her lips, and with a face that strove for composure until its proud beauty was as fixed as death, Edith looked down upon the weeping girl, and once kissed her.", "입술까지 창백해진 채, 그리고 그 도도한 아름다움이 죽음처럼 굳어질 때까지 평정을 지키려 애쓰는 얼굴로, 에디스는 우는 소녀를 내려다보며, 한 번 입을 맞추었다."], ["Then gradually disengaging herself, and putting Florence away, she said, stately, and quiet as a marble image, and in a voice that deepened as she spoke, but had no other token of emotion in it:", "그러고는 차츰 몸을 빼내, 플로렌스를 떼어 놓으며, 위엄 있게, 그리고 대리석상처럼 고요하게, 말하는 동안 점점 깊어지되 그 밖에 감정의 기색이라곤 없는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Florence, you do not know me!", "\"플로렌스, 너는 나를 모른단다!"], ["Heaven forbid that you should learn from me!”", "네가 나에게서 배우다니, 하늘이 막아 주시기를!\""]], [["“Not learn from you?”", "\"엄마에게서 배우면 안 된다고요?\""], ["repeated Florence, in surprise.", "플로렌스가 놀라서 되물었다."]], [["“That I should teach you how to love, or be loved, Heaven forbid!”", "\"사랑하는 법이나, 사랑받는 법을 내가 네게 가르치다니, 하늘이 막아 주시기를!\""], ["said Edith.", "에디스가 말했다."], ["“If you could teach me, that were better; but it is too late.", "\"차라리 네가 나를 가르칠 수 있다면, 그게 나으련만. 하지만 이젠 너무 늦었구나."], ["You are dear to me, Florence.", "너는 내게 소중하단다, 플로렌스."], ["I did not think that anything could ever be so dear to me, as you are in this little time.”", "이 짧은 동안에 네가 내게 소중해진 만큼 그 무엇이 내게 그토록 소중해질 수 있으리라고는 생각지 못했어.\""]], [["She saw that Florence would have spoken here, so checked her with her hand, and went on.", "그녀는 여기서 플로렌스가 무슨 말을 하려는 것을 보고는, 손으로 그것을 막고서, 말을 이었다."]], [["“I will be your true friend always.", "\"나는 언제까지나 너의 참된 벗이 되어 주마."], ["I will cherish you, as much, if not as well as anyone in this world could.", "이 세상 누구 못지않게 너를, 아무리 잘은 못 한다 해도, 그만큼 아끼마."], ["You may trust in me—I know it and I say it, dear,—with the whole confidence even of your pure heart.", "너는 나를 믿어도 된단다—나는 그것을 알기에 말하는 거야, 얘야—네 순결한 마음의 온전한 믿음으로 그래도 된단다."], ["There are hosts of women whom he might have married, better and truer in all other respects than I am, Florence; but there is not one who could come here, his wife, whose heart could beat with greater truth to you than mine does.”", "다른 모든 점에서 나보다 더 낫고 더 진실한, 그가 결혼할 수도 있었던 여인들이야 수두룩하지, 플로렌스. 하지만 그의 아내로 이곳에 와서, 나만큼 진실하게 너를 향해 두근거리는 마음을 지닐 수 있는 여인은 단 한 사람도 없을 거야.\""]], [["“I know it, dear Mama!”", "\"저도 알아요, 사랑하는 엄마!\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."], ["“From that first most happy day I have known it.”", "\"그 첫날, 그 더없이 행복했던 날부터 저는 알고 있었어요.\""]], [["“Most happy day!”", "\"더없이 행복했던 날이라니!\""], ["Edith seemed to repeat the words involuntarily, and went on.", "에디스는 그 말을 저도 모르게 되뇌는 듯하다가, 말을 이었다."], ["“Though the merit is not mine, for I thought little of you until I saw you, let the undeserved reward be mine in your trust and love.", "\"비록 그 공이 내 것은 아니지만—너를 보기 전까지는 너를 거의 마음에 두지 않았으니까—그 받을 자격 없는 보답일랑 너의 믿음과 사랑 속에서 내 것이 되게 해 다오."], ["And in this—in this, Florence; on the first night of my taking up my abode here; I am led on as it is best I should be, to say it for the first and last time.”", "그리고 이 일에서는—이 일에서는, 플로렌스, 내가 이곳에 거처를 잡은 첫날 밤이니, 그러는 게 가장 좋을 터이기에, 처음이자 마지막으로 이 말을 하도록 이끌리는구나.\""]], [["Florence, without knowing why, felt almost afraid to hear her proceed, but kept her eyes riveted on the beautiful face so fixed upon her own.", "플로렌스는 영문도 모른 채, 그녀가 말을 이어 가는 것을 듣기가 거의 두려웠지만, 자기에게 그토록 골똘히 향해 있는 그 아름다운 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 못했다."]], [["“Never seek to find in me,”", "\"결코 내게서 찾으려 들지 마라,\""], ["said Edith, laying her hand upon her breast, “what is not here.", "에디스가 자기 가슴에 손을 얹으며 말했다, \"여기 없는 것을."], ["Never if you can help it, Florence, fall off from me because it is not here.", "결코, 네가 어쩔 수 있는 한, 그것이 여기 없다고 해서 나에게서 멀어지지 마라, 플로렌스."], ["Little by little you will know me better, and the time will come when you will know me, as I know myself.", "조금씩 조금씩 너는 나를 더 잘 알게 될 것이고, 내가 나 자신을 아는 것처럼 너도 나를 알게 될 날이 올 거야."], ["Then, be as lenient to me as you can, and do not turn to bitterness the only sweet remembrance I shall have.”", "그때, 할 수 있는 한 내게 너그러워 다오. 그리고 내가 갖게 될 단 하나의 달콤한 추억을 쓰디쓴 것으로 만들지 말아 다오.\""]], [["The tears that were visible in her eyes as she kept them fixed on Florence, showed that the composed face was but as a handsome mask; but she preserved it, and continued:", "플로렌스에게 눈길을 고정한 채 그 눈에 어린 눈물은, 그 차분한 얼굴이 한낱 아름다운 가면에 지나지 않음을 드러냈다. 그러나 그녀는 그 얼굴을 그대로 지키며, 말을 이었다."]], [["“I have seen what you say, and know how true it is.", "\"나는 네가 말한 것을 보아 왔고, 그것이 얼마나 사실인지도 안단다."], ["But believe me—you will soon, if you cannot now—there is no one on this earth less qualified to set it right or help you, Florence, than I.", "하지만 나를 믿으렴—지금은 못 한다 해도, 너는 곧 믿게 될 거야—이 세상에서 그것을 바로잡거나 너를 도울 자격이 나보다 없는 사람은 없단다, 플로렌스."], ["Never ask me why, or speak to me about it or of my husband, more.", "결코 그 이유를 내게 묻지 말고, 그것에 대해서도, 내 남편에 대해서도, 더는 내게 말하지 마라."], ["There should be, so far, a division, and a silence between us two, like the grave itself.”", "우리 둘 사이에는, 그만큼은, 무덤 그 자체와도 같은 단절과 침묵이 있어야 한단다.\""]], [["She sat for some time silent; Florence scarcely venturing to breathe meanwhile, as dim and imperfect shadows of the truth, and all its daily consequences, chased each other through her terrified, yet incredulous imagination.", "그녀는 한동안 말없이 앉아 있었다. 그동안 플로렌스는 감히 숨도 거의 쉬지 못했으니, 진실의, 그리고 그것이 날마다 가져올 온갖 결과의 어렴풋하고 불완전한 그림자들이, 두려움에 떨면서도 좀처럼 믿기지 않아 하는 그녀의 상상 속에서 서로 꼬리를 물고 뒤쫓았던 것이다."], ["Almost as soon as she had ceased to speak, Edith’s face began to subside from its set composure to that quieter and more relenting aspect, which it usually wore when she and Florence were alone together.", "에디스가 말을 멈추자마자, 그녀의 얼굴은 그 굳은 평정에서, 그녀와 플로렌스가 단둘이 있을 때면 으레 띠곤 하는 그 한결 잔잔하고 한결 누그러진 표정으로 가라앉기 시작했다."], ["She shaded it, after this change, with her hands; and when she arose, and with an affectionate embrace bade Florence good-night, went quickly, and without looking round.", "이렇게 표정이 바뀐 뒤, 그녀는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 그러고는 일어서서, 다정한 포옹으로 플로렌스에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건네고는, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 빠르게 나갔다."]], [["But when Florence was in bed, and the room was dark except for the glow of the fire, Edith returned, and saying that she could not sleep, and that her dressing-room was lonely, drew a chair upon the hearth, and watched the embers as they died away.", "그러나 플로렌스가 잠자리에 들고, 난롯불의 잔광 말고는 방이 어두워졌을 때, 에디스가 돌아와, 잠을 이룰 수 없다고, 자기 의상실이 적적하다고 말하며, 난롯가로 의자 하나를 끌어다 놓고, 잦아드는 잉걸불을 지켜보았다."], ["Florence watched them too from her bed, until they, and the noble figure before them, crowned with its flowing hair, and in its thoughtful eyes reflecting back their light, became confused and indistinct, and finally were lost in slumber.", "플로렌스도 자기 침대에서 그 불을 지켜보았으니, 마침내 그 불이며, 그 앞에 앉은 그 고결한 자태가—흘러내린 머리카락을 왕관처럼 두르고, 사색에 잠긴 두 눈으로 그 불빛을 되비추는—흐릿하고 어렴풋해지더니, 끝내 잠 속으로 스러져 갔다."]], [["In her sleep, however, Florence could not lose an undefined impression of what had so recently passed.", "그러나 잠 속에서도 플로렌스는 그토록 방금 일어난 일의 어떤 막연한 인상을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다."], ["It formed the subject of her dreams, and haunted her; now in one shape, now in another; but always oppressively; and with a sense of fear.", "그것은 그녀의 꿈의 소재가 되어, 그녀를 따라다녔으니, 어떤 때는 이런 모습으로, 어떤 때는 저런 모습으로, 그러나 언제나 짓누르듯이, 그리고 두려움의 감각과 더불어 그러했다."], ["She dreamed of seeking her father in wildernesses, of following his track up fearful heights, and down into deep mines and caverns; of being charged with something that would release him from extraordinary suffering—she knew not what, or why—yet never being able to attain the goal and set him free.", "그녀는 황야에서 아버지를 찾아 헤매는 꿈을, 무시무시한 높은 곳까지 그의 자취를 좇아 올라가고 또 깊은 광산과 동굴 속으로 내려가는 꿈을, 무언가 그를 비상한 고통에서 풀어 줄 무엇을—그것이 무엇인지도, 왜인지도 모른 채—맡았으면서도, 끝내 그 목표에 다다라 그를 풀어 주지는 못하는 꿈을 꾸었다."], ["Then she saw him dead, upon that very bed, and in that very room, and knew that he had never loved her to the last, and fell upon his cold breast, passionately weeping.", "그러다가 그녀는 바로 그 침대 위에서, 바로 그 방에서 그가 죽어 있는 것을 보았고, 그가 끝끝내 자기를 사랑하지 않았음을 알고는, 그 차가운 가슴 위에 엎드려 미친 듯이 울었다."], ["Then a prospect opened, and a river flowed, and a plaintive voice she knew, cried, “It is running on, Floy!", "그러다가 어떤 풍경이 펼쳐지고, 강물이 흘렀으며, 그녀가 아는 어떤 구슬픈 목소리가 외쳤다. \"흘러가고 있어, 플로이!"], ["It has never stopped!", "한 번도 멈춘 적이 없어!"], ["You are moving with it!”", "너도 그것과 함께 움직이고 있어!\""], ["And she saw him at a distance stretching out his arms towards her, while a figure such as Walter’s used to be, stood near him, awfully serene and still.", "그리고 그녀는 멀리서 그가 자기를 향해 두 팔을 뻗고 있는 것을 보았는데, 그동안 예전의 월터를 닮은 어떤 모습이 그의 곁에, 섬뜩하리만치 고요하고 잔잔하게 서 있었다."], ["In every vision, Edith came and went, sometimes to her joy, sometimes to her sorrow, until they were alone upon the brink of a dark grave, and Edith pointing down, she looked and saw—what!—another Edith lying at the bottom.", "모든 환영 속에서 에디스가 오갔으니, 어떤 때는 그녀를 기쁘게, 어떤 때는 그녀를 슬프게 했고, 마침내 둘은 어두운 무덤가에 단둘이 서게 되었으며, 에디스가 아래를 가리키기에, 그녀가 들여다보니 보였다—무엇이었던가!—또 다른 에디스가 그 바닥에 누워 있는 것이."]], [["In the terror of this dream, she cried out and awoke, she thought.", "이 꿈의 공포 속에서, 그녀는 비명을 지르며 깨어났다고, 그녀는 생각했다."], ["A soft voice seemed to whisper in her ear, “Florence, dear Florence, it is nothing but a dream!”", "어떤 부드러운 목소리가 그녀의 귓가에 속삭이는 듯했다. \"플로렌스, 사랑하는 플로렌스, 그건 한낱 꿈일 뿐이란다!\""], ["and stretching out her arms, she returned the caress of her new Mama, who then went out at the door in the light of the grey morning.", "그리고 두 팔을 뻗으며, 그녀는 새어머니의 어루만짐에 화답했고, 새어머니는 그러고 나서 잿빛 아침의 빛 속에서 문밖으로 나갔다."], ["In a moment, Florence sat up wondering whether this had really taken place or not; but she was only certain that it was grey morning indeed, and that the blackened ashes of the fire were on the hearth, and that she was alone.", "잠시 후, 플로렌스는 일어나 앉아 이 일이 정말로 일어난 것인지 아닌지 의아해했다. 그러나 그녀가 확실히 아는 것이라곤, 정말로 잿빛 아침이라는 것, 그리고 난로 위에 거뭇거뭇 타 버린 잿더미가 놓여 있다는 것, 그리고 자기가 혼자라는 것뿐이었다."]], [["So passed the night on which the happy pair came home.", "그 행복한 부부가 집으로 돌아온 그날 밤은 이렇게 지나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVI. Housewarming", "# CHAPTER XXXVI. Housewarming"]], [["Many succeeding days passed in like manner; except that there were numerous visits received and paid, and that Mrs Skewton held little levees in her own apartments, at which Major Bagstock was a frequent attendant, and that Florence encountered no second look from her father, although she saw him every day.", "여러 날이 그렇게 비슷하게 흘러갔다. 다만 받고 또 갚는 방문이 무수히 많았다는 점, 스큐턴 부인이 자기 거처에서 작은 접견 모임을 열었고 거기에 백스톡 소령이 자주 참석했다는 점, 그리고 플로렌스가 날마다 아버지를 보면서도 두 번 다시 그의 눈길을 받지 못했다는 점은 달랐다."], ["Nor had she much communication in words with her new Mama, who was imperious and proud to all the house but her—Florence could not but observe that—and who, although she always sent for her or went to her when she came home from visiting, and would always go into her room at night, before retiring to rest, however late the hour, and never lost an opportunity of being with her, was often her silent and thoughtful companion for a long time together.", "새어머니와도 말을 많이 주고받지는 못했는데, 그녀는 플로렌스를 제외한 온 집안 사람에게는 거만하고 도도했으나—플로렌스로서는 그 점을 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다—방문에서 돌아오면 늘 플로렌스를 부르러 보내거나 직접 찾아왔고, 밤이면 아무리 늦은 시각이라도 쉬러 들기 전에 언제나 플로렌스의 방에 들렀으며, 함께 있을 기회를 결코 놓치는 법이 없었으나, 종종 오랫동안 말없이 생각에 잠긴 동무가 되곤 했다."]], [["Florence, who had hoped for so much from this marriage, could not help sometimes comparing the bright house with the faded dreary place out of which it had arisen, and wondering when, in any shape, it would begin to be a home; for that it was no home then, for anyone, though everything went on luxuriously and regularly, she had always a secret misgiving.", "이 결혼에서 그토록 많은 것을 바랐던 플로렌스는, 이따금 이 화려한 집을, 그것이 생겨난 바탕인 그 빛바래고 음울한 곳과 견주어 보지 않을 수 없었고, 도대체 언제쯤이면 이곳이 어떤 모습으로든 가정이 되기 시작할까 궁금해하지 않을 수 없었다. 모든 것이 호사스럽고 규칙적으로 돌아가고 있었음에도, 지금은 그 누구에게도 이곳이 가정이 아니라는 은밀한 불안을 늘 품고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Many an hour of sorrowful reflection by day and night, and many a tear of blighted hope, Florence bestowed upon the assurance her new Mama had given her so strongly, that there was no one on the earth more powerless than herself to teach her how to win her father’s heart.", "낮으로 밤으로 슬픔에 잠겨 곱씹는 수많은 시간을, 그리고 시들어 버린 희망의 수많은 눈물을, 플로렌스는 새어머니가 그토록 단호하게 자기에게 못 박아 준 그 말 위에 쏟았다. 곧 아버지의 마음을 얻는 법을 가르쳐 줄 사람으로 치자면 이 세상에서 자기 자신만큼 무력한 이가 없다는 그 말이었다."], ["And soon Florence began to think—resolved to think would be the truer phrase—that as no one knew so well, how hopeless of being subdued or changed her father’s coldness to her was, so she had given her this warning, and forbidden the subject in very compassion.", "그리고 머지않아 플로렌스는 이렇게 생각하기 시작했다—생각하기로 마음먹었다고 하는 편이 더 옳은 표현이리라—자기에 대한 아버지의 냉정함이 누그러지거나 변하리라는 가망이 얼마나 없는지를 그녀만큼 잘 아는 이가 없기에, 바로 그 연민에서 자기에게 그렇게 경고하고 그 화제를 금했던 것이라고."], ["Unselfish here, as in her every act and fancy, Florence preferred to bear the pain of this new wound, rather than encourage any faint foreshadowings of the truth as it concerned her father; tender of him, even in her wandering thoughts.", "자기의 모든 행동과 공상에서 그러하듯 여기서도 사심이 없었던 플로렌스는, 아버지에 관한 진실의 어렴풋한 전조를 조금이라도 키우느니, 차라리 이 새로운 상처의 아픔을 견디는 편을 택했다. 떠도는 생각 속에서조차 아버지를 다정히 감쌌던 것이다."], ["As for his home, she hoped it would become a better one, when its state of novelty and transition should be over; and for herself, thought little and lamented less.", "아버지의 집으로 말하자면, 새것이라는 상태와 과도기가 끝나고 나면 더 나은 집이 되리라고 그녀는 바랐다. 그리고 자기 자신에 대해서는 거의 생각하지 않았고, 한탄은 더더욱 하지 않았다."]], [["If none of the new family were particularly at home in private, it was resolved that Mrs Dombey at least should be at home in public, without delay.", "새 가족 가운데 사적으로 특별히 집에서 편안한 사람이 아무도 없다 하더라도, 적어도 돔비 부인만큼은 지체 없이 공적으로 손님을 맞이하기로 결정되었다."], ["A series of entertainments in celebration of the late nuptials, and in cultivation of society, were arranged, chiefly by Mr Dombey and Mrs Skewton; and it was settled that the festive proceedings should commence by Mrs Dombey’s being at home upon a certain evening, and by Mr and Mrs Dombey’s requesting the honour of the company of a great many incongruous people to dinner on the same day.", "지난 혼례를 축하하고 사교계를 다지기 위한 일련의 연회가, 주로 돔비 씨와 스큐턴 부인에 의해 마련되었다. 그리하여 축제의 절차는 어느 저녁 돔비 부인이 손님을 맞이하는 것으로 시작하고, 같은 날 돔비 씨 부부가 서로 어울리지 않는 수많은 사람을 만찬에 초대하는 영광을 청하는 것으로 시작하기로 정해졌다."]], [["Accordingly, Mr Dombey produced a list of sundry eastern magnates who were to be bidden to this feast on his behalf; to which Mrs Skewton, acting for her dearest child, who was haughtily careless on the subject, subjoined a western list, comprising Cousin Feenix, not yet returned to Baden-Baden, greatly to the detriment of his personal estate; and a variety of moths of various degrees and ages, who had, at various times, fluttered round the light of her fair daughter, or herself, without any lasting injury to their wings.", "그리하여 돔비 씨는 자기 몫으로 이 연회에 초청할 동방의 여러 거물들의 명단을 내놓았다. 거기에 스큐턴 부인은, 이 일에 도도하게 무관심한 자기의 더없이 사랑하는 자식을 대신하여, 서쪽 명단을 덧붙였다."], ["Florence was enrolled as a member of the dinner-party, by Edith’s command—elicited by a moment’s doubt and hesitation on the part of Mrs Skewton; and Florence, with a wondering heart, and with a quick instinctive sense of everything that grated on her father in the least, took her silent share in the proceedings of the day.", "거기에는 아직 바덴바덴으로 돌아가지 않아 자기 재산에 큰 손해를 끼치고 있는 피닉스 사촌과, 여러 시절에 그녀의 아리따운 딸이나 그녀 자신의 불빛 둘레를 날개에 영구적인 손상은 입지 않은 채 펄럭이며 맴돌았던, 갖가지 등급과 나이의 나방 같은 무리가 포함되었다. 플로렌스는 에디스의 명령으로 만찬 손님의 일원으로 이름이 올랐는데—이는 스큐턴 부인 쪽의 한순간 의심과 망설임에서 비롯된 명령이었다—플로렌스는 의아한 마음으로, 그리고 아버지의 신경을 조금이라도 거스르는 모든 것에 대한 빠르고 본능적인 감각으로, 그날의 절차에서 말없이 제 몫을 했다."]], [["The proceedings commenced by Mr Dombey, in a cravat of extraordinary height and stiffness, walking restlessly about the drawing-room until the hour appointed for dinner; punctual to which, an East India Director, of immense wealth, in a waistcoat apparently constructed in serviceable deal by some plain carpenter, but really engendered in the tailor’s art, and composed of the material called nankeen, arrived and was received by Mr Dombey alone. The next stage of the proceedings was Mr Dombey’s sending his compliments to Mrs Dombey, with a correct statement of the time; and the next, the East India Director’s falling prostrate, in a conversational point of view, and as Mr Dombey was not the man to pick him up, staring at the fire until rescue appeared in the shape of Mrs Skewton; whom the director, as a pleasant start in life for the evening, mistook for Mrs Dombey, and greeted with enthusiasm.", "절차는 돔비 씨가 유난히 높고 빳빳한 크라바트를 두른 채 만찬 시간으로 정해진 시각까지 응접실을 안절부절못하고 거니는 것으로 시작되었다. 그 시각에 정확히 맞춰, 막대한 재력을 지닌 동인도회사 이사 한 사람이 도착했다. 그는 보아하니 어느 평범한 목수가 쓸 만한 전나무 판자로 짜 맞춘 듯한, 그러나 실은 재단사의 솜씨로 빚어낸, 난킨이라 불리는 천으로 지은 조끼를 입고 있었고, 돔비 씨 혼자서 그를 맞이했다. 다음 단계의 절차는 돔비 씨가 돔비 부인에게 정확한 시각을 알리는 인사말을 보내는 것이었고, 그다음은 동인도회사 이사가 대화라는 관점에서 보면 그만 엎어져 버린 것이었다. 돔비 씨는 그를 일으켜 세워 줄 사람이 아니었으므로, 그는 스큐턴 부인의 모습으로 구원이 나타날 때까지 난롯불만 바라보고 있었다. 그 이사는 저녁을 시작하는 즐거운 출발로서 그녀를 돔비 부인으로 착각하고는 열렬히 인사했다."]], [["The next arrival was a Bank Director, reputed to be able to buy up anything—human Nature generally, if he should take it in his head to influence the money market in that direction—but who was a wonderfully modest-spoken man, almost boastfully so, and mentioned his “little place”", "다음에 도착한 이는 은행 이사였는데, 그는 무엇이든 사들일 수 있다는—만일 그 방향으로 금융 시장을 움직일 마음만 먹는다면 인간 본성까지도 통째로 사들일 수 있다는—평판을 지닌 사람이었으나, 놀라우리만치 겸손하게 말하는, 거의 자랑하듯 겸손한 사람이었다."], ["at Kingston-upon-Thames, and its just being barely equal to giving Dombey a bed and a chop, if he would come and visit it.", "그는 킹스턴어폰템스에 있는 자기의 \"조그만 거처\"를 입에 올리며, 돔비가 와서 방문해 준다면 잠자리 하나와 고기 한 점을 겨우 내줄 수 있을 정도밖에 안 된다고 했다."], ["Ladies, he said, it was not for a man who lived in his quiet way to take upon himself to invite—but if Mrs Skewton and her daughter, Mrs Dombey, should ever find themselves in that direction, and would do him the honour to look at a little bit of a shrubbery they would find there, and a poor little flower-bed or so, and a humble apology for a pinery, and two or three little attempts of that sort without any pretension, they would distinguish him very much.", "부인들로 말하자면, 자기처럼 조용히 사는 사람이 감히 초대를 한다는 것은 가당치 않으나, 만일 스큐턴 부인과 그 따님 돔비 부인이 언젠가 그쪽으로 오게 되어, 거기서 보게 될 자그마한 관목 숲과, 가련하게 작은 화단 몇 개와, 온실이랍시고 내세우기도 민망한 보잘것없는 것과, 별 자랑할 것 없는 그런 자잘한 시도 두세 가지를 보아 주는 영광을 베풀어 준다면, 자기로서는 더없는 영예가 되겠다고 했다."], ["Carrying out his character, this gentleman was very plainly dressed, in a wisp of cambric for a neckcloth, big shoes, a coat that was too loose for him, and a pair of trousers that were too spare; and mention being made of the Opera by Mrs Skewton, he said he very seldom went there, for he couldn’t afford it.", "자기 성격에 걸맞게, 이 신사는 매우 수수하게 차려입었으니, 목수건이랍시고 한 줌의 케임브릭 천을 두르고, 큼직한 신발에, 헐렁한 외투에, 너무 빈약한 바지를 입고 있었다. 스큐턴 부인이 오페라 이야기를 꺼내자, 그는 그곳에 거의 가지 못한다고, 그럴 형편이 못 된다고 말했다."], ["It seemed greatly to delight and exhilarate him to say so: and he beamed on his audience afterwards, with his hands in his pockets, and excessive satisfaction twinkling in his eyes.", "그렇게 말하는 것이 그를 무척 기쁘게 하고 들뜨게 하는 듯했다. 그러고는 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채, 눈에 지나친 만족이 반짝이는 가운데, 청중을 향해 환하게 웃었다."]], [["Now Mrs Dombey appeared, beautiful and proud, and as disdainful and defiant of them all as if the bridal wreath upon her head had been a garland of steel spikes put on to force concession from her which she would die sooner than yield.", "이제 돔비 부인이 나타났다. 아름답고 도도하게, 그리고 머리에 쓴 신부의 화관이 마치 죽으면 죽었지 절대 양보하지 않을 굴복을 강요하려고 씌워 놓은 강철 가시 화환이라도 되는 양, 그들 모두를 멸시하고 거역하는 모습이었다."], ["With her was Florence.", "그녀와 함께 플로렌스가 있었다."], ["When they entered together, the shadow of the night of the return again darkened Mr Dombey’s face.", "둘이 함께 들어서자, 돌아오던 그 밤의 그림자가 다시 돔비 씨의 얼굴을 어둡게 했다."], ["But unobserved; for Florence did not venture to raise her eyes to his, and Edith’s indifference was too supreme to take the least heed of him.", "그러나 아무도 알아차리지 못했다. 플로렌스는 감히 아버지를 향해 눈을 들지 못했고, 에디스의 무관심은 너무도 지극하여 그를 조금도 안중에 두지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["The arrivals quickly became numerous.", "도착하는 손님들이 곧 많아졌다."], ["More directors, chairmen of public companies, elderly ladies carrying burdens on their heads for full dress, Cousin Feenix, Major Bagstock, friends of Mrs Skewton, with the same bright bloom on their complexion, and very precious necklaces on very withered necks.", "더 많은 이사들, 공공 회사의 회장들, 정장이라며 머리에 짐을 인 노부인들, 피닉스 사촌, 백스톡 소령, 그리고 똑같이 환한 화색을 얼굴에 띠고 몹시 시든 목에 몹시 값진 목걸이를 두른 스큐턴 부인의 친구들."], ["Among these, a young lady of sixty-five, remarkably coolly dressed as to her back and shoulders, who spoke with an engaging lisp, and whose eyelids wouldn’t keep up well, without a great deal of trouble on her part, and whose manners had that indefinable charm which so frequently attaches to the giddiness of youth.", "그 가운데 예순다섯 살의 한 젊은 아가씨가 있었는데, 등과 어깨를 유난히 시원하게 드러낸 차림에, 사람의 마음을 끄는 혀짧은 소리로 말했고, 본인이 어지간히 애쓰지 않으면 눈꺼풀이 도무지 제대로 올라가 있지 않았으며, 젊음의 들뜸에 그토록 흔히 따라붙는 그 형언할 수 없는 매력을 거동에 지니고 있었다."], ["As the greater part of Mr Dombey’s list were disposed to be taciturn, and the greater part of Mrs Dombey’s list were disposed to be talkative, and there was no sympathy between them, Mrs Dombey’s list, by magnetic agreement, entered into a bond of union against Mr Dombey’s list, who, wandering about the rooms in a desolate manner, or seeking refuge in corners, entangled themselves with company coming in, and became barricaded behind sofas, and had doors opened smartly from without against their heads, and underwent every sort of discomfiture.", "돔비 씨의 명단에 든 사람들은 대개 과묵한 편이었고, 돔비 부인의 명단에 든 사람들은 대개 말이 많은 편이었으며, 둘 사이에는 아무런 공감이 없었으므로, 돔비 부인의 명단은 자석처럼 끌리는 합의에 따라 돔비 씨의 명단에 맞서는 동맹을 맺었다. 돔비 씨의 명단은 황량하게 방들을 떠돌거나 구석에서 피난처를 찾다가, 들어오는 손님들과 뒤엉키고, 소파 뒤에 갇혀 버리고, 바깥에서 문이 홱 열리며 머리를 얻어맞고, 온갖 낭패를 다 겪었다."]], [["When dinner was announced, Mr Dombey took down an old lady like a crimson velvet pincushion stuffed with bank notes, who might have been the identical old lady of Threadneedle Street, she was so rich, and looked so unaccommodating; Cousin Feenix took down Mrs Dombey; Major Bagstock took down Mrs Skewton; the young thing with the shoulders was bestowed, as an extinguisher, upon the East India Director; and the remaining ladies were left on view in the drawing-room by the remaining gentlemen, until a forlorn hope volunteered to conduct them downstairs, and those brave spirits with their captives blocked up the dining-room door, shutting out seven mild men in the stony-hearted hall. When all the rest were got in and were seated, one of these mild men still appeared, in smiling confusion, totally destitute and unprovided for, and, escorted by the butler, made the complete circuit of the table twice before his chair could be found, which it finally was, on Mrs Dombey’s left hand; after which the mild man never held up his head again.", "만찬이 알려지자, 돔비 씨는 지폐로 속을 채운 진홍색 벨벳 바늘꽂이 같은 노부인을 이끌고 내려갔다. 그녀는 어찌나 부유하고 또 어찌나 다루기 까다로워 보였던지, 스레드니들 거리의 바로 그 노부인이라 해도 좋을 정도였다. 피닉스 사촌은 돔비 부인을 이끌고 내려갔고, 백스톡 소령은 스큐턴 부인을 이끌고 내려갔으며, 어깨를 드러낸 그 젊은 것은 동인도회사 이사에게 입막음용으로 배정되었다. 그리고 남은 부인들은, 남은 신사들에 의해 응접실에 진열된 채 남겨졌다가, 한 결사대가 자원하여 그들을 아래층으로 안내해 주었다. 그 용맹한 정신의 소유자들은 포로들을 거느린 채 식당 문을 가로막아, 무정한 마음의 현관에 일곱 명의 온순한 사내들을 가두어 버렸다. 나머지 사람들이 다 들어가 자리에 앉았는데도, 이 온순한 사내들 가운데 하나가 여전히 웃음 띤 혼란 속에, 완전히 의지가지없고 자리도 마련되지 않은 채로 나타나서는, 집사의 호위를 받으며 자기 의자를 찾을 때까지 식탁을 두 바퀴나 온전히 돌았다. 마침내 의자는 돔비 부인의 왼편에서 찾아졌고, 그 뒤로 그 온순한 사내는 두 번 다시 고개를 들지 못했다."]], [["Now, the spacious dining-room, with the company seated round the glittering table, busy with their glittering spoons, and knives and forks, and plates, might have been taken for a grown-up exposition of Tom Tiddler’s ground, where children pick up gold and silver.", "이제, 그 널찍한 식당은, 반짝이는 식탁 둘레에 앉아 반짝이는 숟가락과 나이프와 포크와 접시로 분주한 손님들과 더불어, 아이들이 금과 은을 주워 모으는 톰 티들러의 땅을 어른용으로 펼쳐 놓은 것이라 해도 좋을 광경이었다. 티들러 역을 맡은 돔비 씨는 자기 배역에 감탄스러우리만치 잘 어울렸다."], ["Mr Dombey, as Tiddler, looked his character to admiration; and the long plateau of precious metal frosted, separating him from Mrs Dombey, whereon frosted Cupids offered scentless flowers to each of them, was allegorical to see.", "그리고 그와 돔비 부인을 갈라놓은, 서리처럼 뿌옇게 세공한 귀금속의 기다란 장식 받침대—그 위에서 서리처럼 뿌연 큐피드들이 둘에게 각기 향기 없는 꽃을 바치고 있던—는 보기에 우의적이었다."]], [["Cousin Feenix was in great force, and looked astonishingly young.", "피닉스 사촌은 기운이 넘쳤고, 놀랍도록 젊어 보였다."], ["But he was sometimes thoughtless in his good humour—his memory occasionally wandering like his legs—and on this occasion caused the company to shudder.", "그러나 그는 이따금 그 좋은 기분 속에서 무심했으니—그의 기억이 그의 다리처럼 가끔 갈피를 못 잡고 헤맸으므로—이번에도 좌중을 오싹하게 만들었다."], ["It happened thus.", "사정은 이러했다."], ["The young lady with the back, who regarded Cousin Feenix with sentiments of tenderness, had entrapped the East India Director into leading her to the chair next him; in return for which good office, she immediately abandoned the Director, who, being shaded on the other side by a gloomy black velvet hat surmounting a bony and speechless female with a fan, yielded to a depression of spirits and withdrew into himself.", "등을 드러낸 그 젊은 아가씨는 피닉스 사촌에 대해 애틋한 정을 품고 있어서, 동인도회사 이사를 꾀어 자기를 그의 옆자리로 이끌게 했다. 그 친절에 대한 보답으로, 그녀는 곧장 그 이사를 버려두었고, 다른 한쪽으로는 부채를 든 뼈만 앙상하고 말 없는 여인 위에 음울한 검은 벨벳 모자가 얹혀 그를 가리고 있던 터라, 그 이사는 기운이 가라앉아 제 안으로 움츠러들고 말았다."], ["Cousin Feenix and the young lady were very lively and humorous, and the young lady laughed so much at something Cousin Feenix related to her, that Major Bagstock begged leave to inquire on behalf of Mrs Skewton (they were sitting opposite, a little lower down), whether that might not be considered public property.", "피닉스 사촌과 그 젊은 아가씨는 무척 활기차고 익살스러웠는데, 피닉스 사촌이 들려준 어떤 이야기에 젊은 아가씨가 어찌나 크게 웃던지, 백스톡 소령이 스큐턴 부인을 대신하여(둘은 맞은편에, 조금 아래쪽에 앉아 있었다) 그것이 공공의 재산으로 여겨질 수는 없는지 여쭙는 것을 허락해 달라고 청했다."]], [["“Why, upon my life,”", "\"아, 이런, 정말이지,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, “there’s nothing in it; it really is not worth repeating: in point of fact, it’s merely an anecdote of Jack Adams.", "피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"별것 아닙니다. 정말 되풀이할 만한 가치가 없어요."], ["I dare say my friend Dombey;”", "사실 그저 잭 애덤스에 관한 일화일 뿐입니다. 아마 내 친구 돔비는,\""], ["for the general attention was concentrated on Cousin Feenix; “may remember Jack Adams, Jack Adams, not Joe; that was his brother.", "좌중의 관심이 피닉스 사촌에게 집중되어 있었으므로, \"잭 애덤스를 기억할지도 모르겠소. 조가 아니라 잭 애덤스 말이오."], ["Jack—little Jack—man with a cast in his eye, and slight impediment in his speech—man who sat for somebody’s borough.", "조는 그 형이었지. 잭—꼬마 잭—한쪽 눈이 사시이고, 말투에 약간 더듬는 데가 있던 사람, 어느 선거구를 대표해 의석에 앉았던 사람 말이오."], ["We used to call him in my parliamentary time W.", "내가 의원이던 시절에 우리는 그를 W."], ["P.", "P."], ["Adams, in consequence of his being Warming Pan for a young fellow who was in his minority.", "애덤스라고 부르곤 했는데, 그가 아직 미성년인 어느 젊은이를 위한 보온 그릇* 노릇을 하고 있었기 때문이오. 어쩌면 내 친구 돔비가 그 사람을 알고 있었을지도 모르겠소.\""], ["Perhaps my friend Dombey may have known the man?”", "* 보온 그릇(Warming Pan): 미성년자가 자격을 갖출 때까지 자리를 대신 채워 두는 임시 대리인을 뜻하는 당대의 비유적 표현."]], [["Mr Dombey, who was as likely to have known Guy Fawkes, replied in the negative.", "기 포크스를 알고 있었을 가능성이나 매한가지였던 돔비 씨는 모른다고 대답했다."], ["But one of the seven mild men unexpectedly leaped into distinction, by saying he had known him, and adding—“always wore Hessian boots!”", "그러나 일곱 명의 온순한 사내들 가운데 하나가 뜻밖에도 두각을 드러내며, 자기는 그를 알고 있었다고 말하고는 이렇게 덧붙였다—\"늘 헤시안 부츠를 신고 다녔지요!\""]], [["“Exactly,”", "\"바로 그렇소,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, bending forward to see the mild man, and smile encouragement at him down the table.", "피닉스 사촌이 그 온순한 사내를 보려고 몸을 앞으로 굽히며, 식탁 너머로 그에게 격려의 미소를 지어 보이며 말했다."], ["“That was Jack.", "\"그게 바로 잭이었소."], ["Joe wore—”", "조는—\""]], [["“Tops!”", "\"톱부츠를 신었지요!\""], ["cried the mild man, rising in public estimation every Instant.", "그 온순한 사내가 매 순간 세간의 평가를 높여 가며 외쳤다."]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론이오,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, “you were intimate with em?”", "피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"두 사람과 친하셨던 모양이지요?\""]], [["“I knew them both,”", "\"둘 다 알고 있었습니다,\""], ["said the mild man.", "그 온순한 사내가 말했다."], ["With whom Mr Dombey immediately took wine.", "돔비 씨는 즉시 그와 함께 술잔을 들었다."]], [["“Devilish good fellow, Jack!”", "\"기막히게 좋은 친구였지, 잭은!\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, again bending forward, and smiling.", "피닉스 사촌이 다시 몸을 앞으로 굽히며 미소 지었다."]], [["“Excellent,”", "\"훌륭한 사람이었지요,\""], ["returned the mild man, becoming bold on his success.", "그 온순한 사내가 성공에 힘입어 대담해지며 응수했다."], ["“One of the best fellows I ever knew.”", "\"제가 알던 사람 중에 가장 좋은 친구 가운데 하나였습니다.\""]], [["“No doubt you have heard the story?”", "\"그 이야기는 분명 들으셨겠지요?\""], ["said Cousin Feenix.", "피닉스 사촌이 말했다."]], [["“I shall know,” replied the bold mild man, “when I have heard your Ludship tell it.”", "\"각하께서 들려주시는 걸 듣고 나면,\""], ["With that, he leaned back in his chair and smiled at the ceiling, as knowing it by heart, and being already tickled.", "대담해진 온순한 사내가 대답했다, \"알게 되겠지요.\" 그 말과 함께 그는 의자에 등을 기대고는, 이미 다 외고 있어 벌써부터 즐거워하는 사람처럼 천장을 향해 미소 지었다."]], [["“In point of fact, it’s nothing of a story in itself,”", "\"사실 그 자체로는 이렇다 할 이야기도 아닙니다,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, addressing the table with a smile, and a gay shake of his head, “and not worth a word of preface.", "피닉스 사촌이 미소를 지으며, 머리를 유쾌하게 흔들며 좌중을 향해 말했다, \"서두를 한마디 붙일 가치도 없지요."], ["But it’s illustrative of the neatness of Jack’s humour.", "다만 잭의 재치가 얼마나 깔끔한지를 잘 보여 주는 이야기랍니다."], ["The fact is, that Jack was invited down to a marriage—which I think took place in Berkshire?”", "사정인즉, 잭이 어느 결혼식에 초대를 받았는데—내 생각엔 버크셔에서 열렸던 것 같소만?\""]], [["“Shropshire,”", "\"슈롭셔입니다,\""], ["said the bold mild man, finding himself appealed to.", "대담해진 온순한 사내가 자기에게 묻는 것임을 알아채고 말했다."]], [["“Was it?", "\"그랬소?"], ["Well!", "뭐!"], ["In point of fact it might have been in any shire,”", "사실 어느 셔에서 열렸든 마찬가지였겠지요,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix.", "피닉스 사촌이 말했다."], ["“So my friend being invited down to this marriage in Anyshire,”", "\"그래, 내 친구가 어느셔에서 열리는 이 결혼식에 초대를 받아,\""], ["with a pleasant sense of the readiness of this joke, “goes.", "이 농담이 잘 떠오른 것에 기분 좋아하며, \"갑니다."], ["Just as some of us, having had the honour of being invited to the marriage of my lovely and accomplished relative with my friend Dombey, didn’t require to be asked twice, and were devilish glad to be present on so interesting an occasion.—Goes—Jack goes.", "마치 우리 가운데 더러가, 나의 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 친척과 내 친구 돔비의 결혼식에 초대받는 영광을 누리고는, 두 번 청할 것도 없이, 그토록 흥미로운 자리에 참석하게 되어 기막히게 기뻐했던 것처럼 말이오.—갑니다—잭이 갑니다."], ["Now, this marriage was, in point of fact, the marriage of an uncommonly fine girl with a man for whom she didn’t care a button, but whom she accepted on account of his property, which was immense.", "그런데 이 결혼이라는 게, 사실은, 보기 드물게 고운 아가씨가, 단추 한 알만큼도 마음에 두지 않는 사내와 하는 결혼이었소. 다만 그 막대한 재산 때문에 그를 받아들인 거였지요."], ["When Jack returned to town, after the nuptials, a man he knew, meeting him in the lobby of the House of Commons, says, ‘Well, Jack, how are the ill-matched couple?’ ‘Ill-matched,’ says Jack ‘Not at all.", "혼례를 마치고 잭이 런던으로 돌아왔을 때, 그가 아는 사내 하나가 하원의 로비에서 그를 만나 말합니다, '이보게, 잭, 그 어울리지 않는 한 쌍은 어떻던가?' '어울리지 않는다고?'"], ["It’s a perfectly and equal transaction.", "잭이 말합니다. '천만에."], ["She is regularly bought, and you may take your oath he is as regularly sold!’”", "더없이 완벽하게 대등한 거래였네. 그 여자는 제대로 사들여졌고, 장담하건대 그 사내는 제대로 팔려 나간 게지!'\""]], [["In his full enjoyment of this culminating point of his story, the shudder, which had gone all round the table like an electric spark, struck Cousin Feenix, and he stopped.", "이 이야기의 절정을 한껏 즐기던 차에, 전기 불꽃처럼 식탁을 한 바퀴 돌았던 그 오싹함이 피닉스 사촌에게 닿았고, 그는 말을 멈추었다."], ["Not a smile occasioned by the only general topic of conversation broached that day, appeared on any face.", "그날 입에 올린 유일한 일반적 화제가 자아낸 미소는 그 어느 얼굴에도 떠오르지 않았다."], ["A profound silence ensued; and the wretched mild man, who had been as innocent of any real foreknowledge of the story as the child unborn, had the exquisite misery of reading in every eye that he was regarded as the prime mover of the mischief.", "깊은 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 그리고 그 이야기를 미리 알았던 것이 갓 태어나지 않은 아기만큼이나 결백했던 그 가엾은 온순한 사내는, 모든 눈동자 속에서 자기가 이 사달의 주모자로 여겨지고 있음을 읽어 내는 더없는 고통을 맛보았다."]], [["Mr Dombey’s face was not a changeful one, and being cast in its mould of state that day, showed little other apprehension of the story, if any, than that which he expressed when he said solemnly, amidst the silence, that it was “Very good.”", "돔비 씨의 얼굴은 잘 변하는 얼굴이 아니었고, 그날은 위엄이라는 거푸집에 부어 넣은 채였으므로, 그 이야기에 대해서는 설령 무언가를 느꼈다 한들, 침묵 가운데 엄숙하게 \"참 좋군요\"라고 말했을 때 드러낸 정도 외에는 거의 아무런 기색도 보이지 않았다."], ["There was a rapid glance from Edith towards Florence, but otherwise she remained, externally, impassive and unconscious.", "에디스가 플로렌스 쪽으로 재빠른 눈길을 던졌으나, 그 밖에는 겉으로 무표정하고 무심한 채로 있었다."]], [["Through the various stages of rich meats and wines, continual gold and silver, dainties of earth, air, fire, and water, heaped-up fruits, and that unnecessary article in Mr Dombey’s banquets—ice—the dinner slowly made its way: the later stages being achieved to the sonorous music of incessant double knocks, announcing the arrival of visitors, whose portion of the feast was limited to the smell thereof.", "진귀한 고기와 포도주, 끊임없는 금과 은, 땅과 공기와 불과 물에서 나온 별미들, 수북이 쌓아 올린 과일들, 그리고 돔비 씨의 연회에서는 불필요한 그 물건—얼음—의 여러 단계를 거치며, 만찬은 더디게 나아갔다. 후반 단계는, 손님들의 도착을 알리는 끊임없는 두 번 두드리는 노크 소리의 우렁찬 음악에 맞추어 이루어졌는데, 그 손님들에게 이 연회에서 돌아갈 몫이라곤 그 냄새뿐이었다."], ["When Mrs Dombey rose, it was a sight to see her lord, with stiff throat and erect head, hold the door open for the withdrawal of the ladies; and to see how she swept past him with his daughter on her arm.", "돔비 부인이 일어섰을 때, 그 부군이 빳빳한 목과 꼿꼿한 머리로 부인들이 물러나도록 문을 잡고 서 있던 모습은, 그리고 그녀가 자기 딸을 팔에 끼고 그의 곁을 휙 스쳐 지나가던 모습은, 가히 볼만한 광경이었다."]], [["Mr Dombey was a grave sight, behind the decanters, in a state of dignity; and the East India Director was a forlorn sight near the unoccupied end of the table, in a state of solitude; and the Major was a military sight, relating stories of the Duke of York to six of the seven mild men (the ambitious one was utterly quenched); and the Bank Director was a lowly sight, making a plan of his little attempt at a pinery, with dessert-knives, for a group of admirers; and Cousin Feenix was a thoughtful sight, as he smoothed his long wristbands and stealthily adjusted his wig.", "돔비 씨는 술병들 뒤에서 위엄을 갖춘 채 엄숙한 광경이었고, 동인도회사 이사는 비어 있는 식탁 끄트머리 가까이에서 고독에 잠긴 처량한 광경이었으며, 소령은 일곱 온순한 사내들 가운데 여섯에게 요크 공작의 이야기를 들려주는 군인다운 광경이었고(야심을 품었던 한 명은 완전히 꺼져 버렸다), 은행 이사는 후식용 나이프로 자기의 보잘것없는 온실 시도의 도면을 그려 한 무리의 찬미자들에게 보여 주는 겸손한 광경이었으며, 피닉스 사촌은 긴 소맷부리를 매만지고 슬그머니 가발을 바로잡으며 생각에 잠긴 광경이었다."], ["But all these sights were of short duration, being speedily broken up by coffee, and the desertion of the room.", "그러나 이 모든 광경은 잠깐뿐이었으니, 커피가 나오고 사람들이 방을 비우면서 이내 흩어져 버렸다."]], [["There was a throng in the state-rooms upstairs, increasing every minute; but still Mr Dombey’s list of visitors appeared to have some native impossibility of amalgamation with Mrs Dombey’s list, and no one could have doubted which was which.", "위층의 응접실들에는 사람이 북적였고, 매 순간 그 수가 불어났다. 그러나 여전히 돔비 씨의 손님 명단은 돔비 부인의 명단과 어우러지는 것이 천성적으로 불가능한 무언가가 있는 듯하여, 어느 쪽이 어느 쪽인지를 누구도 의심할 수 없었다."], ["The single exception to this rule perhaps was Mr Carker, who now smiled among the company, and who, as he stood in the circle that was gathered about Mrs Dombey—watchful of her, of them, his chief, Cleopatra and the Major, Florence, and everything around—appeared at ease with both divisions of guests, and not marked as exclusively belonging to either.", "이 규칙의 유일한 예외라면 아마 카커 씨였을 것이다. 그는 이제 손님들 사이에서 미소 짓고 있었고, 돔비 부인을 둘러싸고 모여든 무리 안에 서서—그녀를, 그들을, 자기 우두머리를, 클레오파트라와 소령을, 플로렌스를, 그리고 주위의 모든 것을 살피며—두 패의 손님 양쪽과 다 편안해 보였고, 어느 한쪽에만 오로지 속한 것으로 두드러지지도 않았다."]], [["Florence had a dread of him, which made his presence in the room a nightmare to her.", "플로렌스는 그에 대한 두려움이 있어서, 그가 방에 있다는 것 자체가 그녀에게는 악몽이었다."], ["She could not avoid the recollection of it, for her eyes were drawn towards him every now and then, by an attraction of dislike and distrust that she could not resist.", "그녀는 그것을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었으니, 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 혐오와 불신의 끌림에 이끌려 그녀의 눈이 이따금 그쪽으로 향했기 때문이다. 그렇지만 그녀의 생각은 다른 일들로 분주했다."], ["Yet her thoughts were busy with other things; for as she sat apart—not unadmired or unsought, but in the gentleness of her quiet spirit—she felt how little part her father had in what was going on, and saw, with pain, how ill at ease he seemed to be, and how little regarded he was as he lingered about near the door, for those visitors whom he wished to distinguish with particular attention, and took them up to introduce them to his wife, who received them with proud coldness, but showed no interest or wish to please, and never, after the bare ceremony of reception, in consultation of his wishes, or in welcome of his friends, opened her lips.", "한쪽에 떨어져 앉은 채—흠모받지 못한 것도, 청함을 받지 못한 것도 아니었으나, 그 고요한 영혼의 온화함 속에 앉아—그녀는 아버지가 지금 벌어지는 일에 얼마나 발붙일 데가 없는지를 느꼈고, 그가 얼마나 마음 불편해 보이는지를, 그리고 그가 특별한 관심으로 대접하고 싶어 하는 손님들을 맞으려고 문 가까이에서 서성이며 얼마나 무시당하고 있는지를 아프게 지켜보았다. 그는 그 손님들을 데려가 자기 아내에게 소개했는데, 아내는 그들을 도도한 냉랭함으로 맞이할 뿐, 흥미를 보이거나 기쁘게 해 주려는 기색은 없었고, 형식적인 맞이 인사를 마친 다음에는, 남편의 뜻을 헤아리거나 그의 친구들을 환영하느라 입술을 여는 일이 결코 없었다."], ["It was not the less perplexing or painful to Florence, that she who acted thus, treated her so kindly and with such loving consideration, that it almost seemed an ungrateful return on her part even to know of what was passing before her eyes.", "그렇게 처신하는 그녀가 정작 플로렌스 자신에게는 그토록 다정하게, 그토록 사랑 어린 배려로 대해 주었기에, 자기 눈앞에서 벌어지는 일을 알고 있는 것조차 거의 배은망덕한 보답처럼 여겨질 지경이었으니, 플로렌스로서는 그 점이 더욱 당혹스럽고 가슴 아팠다."]], [["Happy Florence would have been, might she have ventured to bear her father company, by so much as a look; and happy Florence was, in little suspecting the main cause of his uneasiness.", "눈길 하나만큼이라도 감히 아버지의 동무가 되어 줄 수 있었더라면 플로렌스는 행복했으리라. 그리고 그의 불안의 주된 원인을 거의 의심하지 못하고 있다는 점에서 플로렌스는 행복했다."], ["But afraid of seeming to know that he was placed at any disadvantage, lest he should be resentful of that knowledge; and divided between her impulse towards him, and her grateful affection for Edith; she scarcely dared to raise her eyes towards either.", "그러나 그가 어떤 불리한 처지에 놓여 있음을 자기가 안다는 것을 그가 분하게 여길까 두려워, 그것을 아는 듯이 보이는 것이 겁났고, 그를 향한 충동과 에디스를 향한 고마운 애정 사이에서 갈라진 채, 그녀는 어느 쪽으로도 감히 눈을 들지 못했다."], ["Anxious and unhappy for them both, the thought stole on her through the crowd, that it might have been better for them if this noise of tongues and tread of feet had never come there,—if the old dulness and decay had never been replaced by novelty and splendour,—if the neglected child had found no friend in Edith, but had lived her solitary life, unpitied and forgotten.", "두 사람 다를 위해 마음 졸이고 가슴 아파하던 중에, 사람들의 무리를 뚫고 이런 생각이 그녀에게 슬며시 스며들었다. 어쩌면 이 혀들의 소란과 발들의 디딤 소리가 애초에 이곳에 찾아들지 않았더라면, 옛 침울함과 쇠락함이 새로움과 화려함으로 결코 바뀌지 않았더라면, 그 버림받은 아이가 에디스에게서 아무런 벗도 찾지 못한 채, 가엾이 여겨지지도 기억되지도 못하며 외로운 삶을 살았더라면, 두 사람에게는 차라리 더 나았을지도 모른다는 생각이었다."]], [["Mrs Chick had some such thoughts too, but they were not so quietly developed in her mind.", "칙 부인에게도 그와 비슷한 생각이 들었으나, 그녀의 마음속에서는 그렇게 조용히 펼쳐지지 않았다."], ["This good matron had been outraged in the first instance by not receiving an invitation to dinner.", "이 선량한 부인은 우선 만찬 초대를 받지 못한 것에 모욕을 느꼈다."], ["That blow partially recovered, she had gone to a vast expense to make such a figure before Mrs Dombey at home, as should dazzle the senses of that lady, and heap mortification, mountains high, on the head of Mrs Skewton.", "그 타격에서 어느 정도 회복하고 나자, 그녀는 손님을 맞이하는 돔비 부인 앞에서 그 부인의 감각을 어지럽힐 만큼, 그리고 스큐턴 부인의 머리 위에 산더미 같은 굴욕을 쌓아 올릴 만큼의 위용을 갖추려고 막대한 비용을 들였다."]], [["“But I am made,”", "\"하지만 나는,\""], ["said Mrs Chick to Mr Chick, “of no more account than Florence!", "칙 부인이 칙 씨에게 말했다, \"플로렌스만큼도 대접을 못 받는 신세라니까!"], ["Who takes the smallest notice of me?", "누가 나를 조금이라도 거들떠보기나 해?"], ["No one!”", "아무도 안 봐!\""]], [["“No one, my dear,”", "\"아무도 안 보지, 여보,\""], ["assented Mr Chick, who was seated by the side of Mrs Chick against the wall, and could console himself, even there, by softly whistling.", "벽에 기대 칙 부인 곁에 앉아 있던 칙 씨가 맞장구를 쳤다. 그는 거기서나마 가만히 휘파람을 불며 스스로를 위로할 수 있었다."]], [["“Does it at all appear as if I was wanted here?”", "\"내가 여기 있어 줬으면 하는 기색이 조금이라도 보여?\""], ["exclaimed Mrs Chick, with flashing eyes.", "칙 부인이 눈을 번뜩이며 외쳤다."]], [["“No, my dear, I don’t think it does,”", "\"아니, 여보, 그런 것 같지는 않구려,\""], ["said Mr Chick.", "칙 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Paul’s mad!”", "\"폴이 미쳤어!\""], ["said Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."]], [["Mr Chick whistled.", "칙 씨가 휘파람을 불었다."]], [["“Unless you are a monster, which I sometimes think you are,”", "\"당신이 괴물이 아니라면—가끔은 그렇지 싶다만—\""], ["said Mrs Chick with candour, “don’t sit there humming tunes.", "칙 부인이 솔직하게 말했다, \"거기 앉아서 콧노래나 흥얼대고 있지 좀 마요."], ["How anyone with the most distant feelings of a man, can see that mother-in-law of Paul’s, dressed as she is, going on like that, with Major Bagstock, for whom, among other precious things, we are indebted to your Lucretia Tox.”", "사내로서의 감정이 손톱만큼이라도 있는 사람이라면, 어떻게 폴의 저 장모가, 저런 차림으로, 백스톡 소령이랑 저 모양으로 놀아나는 꼴을 보고 있을 수가 있어? 그 소령이라는 작자도, 다른 귀한 것들과 더불어, 당신의 그 루크리셔 톡스 덕에 우리가 떠안은 거잖아.\""]], [["“My Lucretia Tox, my dear!”", "\"내 루크리셔 톡스라니, 여보!\""], ["said Mr Chick, astounded.", "칙 씨가 어안이 벙벙해서 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["retorted Mrs Chick, with great severity, “your Lucretia Tox—I say how anybody can see that mother-in-law of Paul’s, and that haughty wife of Paul’s, and these indecent old frights with their backs and shoulders, and in short this at home generally, and hum—”", "칙 부인이 매우 매섭게 받아쳤다, \"당신의 루크리셔 톡스 말이야—그러니까 내 말은, 어떻게 누가 폴의 저 장모를, 그리고 폴의 저 오만한 아내를, 그리고 등이며 어깨며 드러낸 저 꼴사나운 늙은 허깨비들을, 한마디로 대체로 이 집들이 전부를 보고 있으면서 콧노래나 흥—\""], ["on which word Mrs Chick laid a scornful emphasis that made Mr Chick start, “is, I thank Heaven, a mystery to me!”", "이 말에 칙 부인이 어찌나 경멸 어린 힘을 주었던지 칙 씨가 흠칫 놀랐다, \"흥얼댈 수 있는지, 정말 하느님께 감사하게도, 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없는 노릇이라고!\""]], [["Mr Chick screwed his mouth into a form irreconcilable with humming or whistling, and looked very contemplative.", "칙 씨는 콧노래나 휘파람과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 모양으로 입을 비틀어 오므리고는, 무척 골똘한 표정을 지었다."]], [["“But I hope I know what is due to myself,”", "\"하지만 나는 내가 마땅히 받아야 할 게 무엇인지는 알고 있다고 봐요,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, swelling with indignation, “though Paul has forgotten what is due to me.", "칙 부인이 분노로 부풀어 오르며 말했다, \"폴은 나에게 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 잊어버렸지만 말이야."], ["I am not going to sit here, a member of this family, to be taken no notice of.", "나는 이 집안의 일원으로서, 여기 앉아 거들떠봐지지도 않는 신세로 있지는 않겠어."], ["I am not the dirt under Mrs Dombey’s feet, yet—not quite yet,”", "나는 아직 돔비 부인의 발밑에 깔린 흙은 아니라고—아직은 아니지,\""], ["said Mrs Chick, as if she expected to become so, about the day after to-morrow.", "칙 부인이, 마치 모레쯤이면 그렇게 되리라 예상하는 듯이 말했다."], ["“And I shall go.", "\"그러니 나는 가겠어."], ["I will not say (whatever I may think) that this affair has been got up solely to degrade and insult me.", "이 자리가 오로지 나를 깎아내리고 모욕하려고 마련된 거라고는 (속으로야 어떻게 생각하든) 말하지 않겠어. 그냥 가겠다고."], ["I shall merely go. I shall not be missed!”", "아무도 내가 없어진 걸 아쉬워하지도 않을 테니!\""]], [["Mrs Chick rose erect with these words, and took the arm of Mr Chick, who escorted her from the room, after half an hour’s shady sojourn there.", "칙 부인은 이 말과 함께 꼿꼿이 일어나 칙 씨의 팔을 잡았고, 칙 씨는 거기서 반 시간 남짓 음울하게 머문 끝에 그녀를 데리고 방을 나섰다."], ["And it is due to her penetration to observe that she certainly was not missed at all.", "그리고 그녀의 통찰력에 마땅히 돌려야 할 말을 하자면, 정말로 그녀가 없어진 것을 아쉬워한 사람은 전혀 없었다는 사실을 적어 두어야겠다."]], [["But she was not the only indignant guest; for Mr Dombey’s list (still constantly in difficulties) were, as a body, indignant with Mrs Dombey’s list, for looking at them through eyeglasses, and audibly wondering who all those people were; while Mrs Dombey’s list complained of weariness, and the young thing with the shoulders, deprived of the attentions of that gay youth Cousin Feenix (who went away from the dinner-table), confidentially alleged to thirty or forty friends that she was bored to death.", "그러나 분개한 손님이 그녀 하나만은 아니었다. 돔비 씨의 명단은(여전히 끊임없이 곤경에 처해 있던 채) 한 덩어리가 되어 돔비 부인의 명단에 분개하고 있었으니, 저쪽이 외알 안경 너머로 자기들을 쳐다보며 저 사람들이 다 누구냐고 들으라는 듯이 의아해했기 때문이다."], ["All the old ladies with the burdens on their heads, had greater or less cause of complaint against Mr Dombey; and the Directors and Chairmen coincided in thinking that if Dombey must marry, he had better have married somebody nearer his own age, not quite so handsome, and a little better off.", "한편 돔비 부인의 명단은 지루하다고 불평했고, 어깨를 드러낸 그 젊은 것은 저 명랑한 젊은이 피닉스 사촌(만찬 식탁을 떠나 가 버렸다)의 관심을 빼앗긴 채, 서른 명인지 마흔 명인지 되는 친구들에게 자기는 지루해 죽을 지경이라고 은밀히 털어놓았다. 머리에 짐을 인 노부인들은 모두 크든 작든 돔비 씨에 대해 불평할 거리가 있었고, 이사들과 회장들은 돔비가 굳이 결혼을 해야 했다면 차라리 제 나이에 좀 더 가깝고, 그렇게까지 잘나지는 않았으며, 형편이 조금 더 나은 사람과 결혼하는 편이 나았으리라는 데에 의견이 일치했다."], ["The general opinion among this class of gentlemen was, that it was a weak thing in Dombey, and he’d live to repent it.", "이 부류의 신사들 사이에서 일반적인 견해는, 그것이 돔비로서는 우둔한 짓이었으며, 그가 두고두고 후회하며 살게 되리라는 것이었다."], ["Hardly anybody there, except the mild men, stayed, or went away, without considering himself or herself neglected and aggrieved by Mr Dombey or Mrs Dombey; and the speechless female in the black velvet hat was found to have been stricken mute, because the lady in the crimson velvet had been handed down before her.", "그 자리에 있던 거의 모든 사람이, 온순한 사내들을 빼고는, 머무르든 떠나든 자기가 돔비 씨나 돔비 부인에게 무시당하고 푸대접받았다고 여기지 않은 이가 없었다. 검은 벨벳 모자를 쓴 그 말 없는 여인은, 진홍색 벨벳을 입은 부인이 자기보다 먼저 안내받아 내려갔다는 이유로 그만 벙어리가 되어 버린 것으로 밝혀졌다."], ["The nature even of the mild men got corrupted, either from their curdling it with too much lemonade, or from the general inoculation that prevailed; and they made sarcastic jokes to one another, and whispered disparagement on stairs and in bye-places.", "온순한 사내들마저도 그 천성이 변질되고 말았으니, 레모네이드를 너무 많이 마셔 천성이 응어리진 탓인지, 아니면 만연한 전반적인 감염 탓인지, 그들은 서로에게 빈정대는 농담을 던지고, 계단에서며 으슥한 구석에서며 험담을 속삭였다."], ["The general dissatisfaction and discomfort so diffused itself, that the assembled footmen in the hall were as well acquainted with it as the company above.", "전반적인 불만과 불편함이 어찌나 널리 퍼졌던지, 현관에 모여 있던 하인들도 위층의 손님들 못지않게 그것을 환히 알고 있었다."], ["Nay, the very linkmen outside got hold of it, and compared the party to a funeral out of mourning, with none of the company remembered in the will.", "아니, 바깥에 있던 횃불잡이들까지 그것을 알아채고는, 이 파티를 상복도 입지 않은 장례식에, 그것도 유언장에 한 사람도 기억된 이가 없는 장례식에 빗대었다."]], [["At last, the guests were all gone, and the linkmen too; and the street, crowded so long with carriages, was clear; and the dying lights showed no one in the rooms, but Mr Dombey and Mr Carker, who were talking together apart, and Mrs Dombey and her mother: the former seated on an ottoman; the latter reclining in the Cleopatra attitude, awaiting the arrival of her maid. Mr Dombey having finished his communication to Carker, the latter advanced obsequiously to take leave.", "마침내 손님들이 모두 떠났고, 횃불잡이들도 떠났다. 그토록 오래 마차로 붐비던 거리도 한산해졌다. 그리고 꺼져 가는 불빛은 방 안에 아무도 비추지 않았으니, 다만 따로 떨어져 이야기를 나누던 돔비 씨와 카커 씨, 그리고 돔비 부인과 그 어머니뿐이었다. 부인은 오토만에 앉아 있었고, 그 어머니는 시녀가 오기를 기다리며 클레오파트라의 자세로 비스듬히 기대어 있었다. 돔비 씨가 카커에게 할 말을 마치자, 카커는 굽실거리며 작별을 고하러 나아갔다."]], [["“I trust,”", "\"바라건대,\""], ["he said, “that the fatigues of this delightful evening will not inconvenience Mrs Dombey to-morrow.”", "그가 말했다, \"이 즐거운 저녁의 노고가 내일 돔비 부인께 폐가 되지 않기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Mrs Dombey,”", "\"돔비 부인은,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, advancing, “has sufficiently spared herself fatigue, to relieve you from any anxiety of that kind.", "돔비 씨가 앞으로 나서며 말했다, \"그런 종류의 걱정에서 자네를 놓아 줄 만큼 충분히 노고를 아꼈네."], ["I regret to say, Mrs Dombey, that I could have wished you had fatigued yourself a little more on this occasion.", "유감스럽게도, 돔비 부인, 나는 이번 자리에서 당신이 좀 더 수고를 해 주었더라면 좋았으리라 생각하오.\""]], [["She looked at him with a supercilious glance, that it seemed not worth her while to protract, and turned away her eyes without speaking.", "그녀는 그를 향해 거만한 눈길을 던졌으나, 그것을 길게 끌 가치도 없다는 듯, 아무 말 없이 눈을 돌려 버렸다."]], [["“I am sorry, Madam,”", "\"유감입니다, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “that you should not have thought it your duty—”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"당신이 그것을 당신의 도리라고 여기지 않았다는 것이—\""]], [["She looked at him again.", "그녀는 다시 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Your duty, Madam,”", "\"당신의 도리 말이오, 부인,\""], ["pursued Mr Dombey, “to have received my friends with a little more deference.", "돔비 씨가 말을 이었다, \"내 친구들을 좀 더 정중하게 맞이하는 것이."], ["Some of those whom you have been pleased to slight tonight in a very marked manner, Mrs Dombey, confer a distinction upon you, I must tell you, in any visit they pay you.”", "오늘 밤 당신이 아주 유난스럽게 홀대하기를 즐긴 그 사람들 가운데 더러는, 분명히 말해 두건대, 당신을 찾아오는 것만으로도 당신에게 영예를 베푸는 것이오.\""]], [["“Do you know that there is someone here?”", "\"여기 누가 있다는 걸 아시나요?\""], ["she returned, now looking at him steadily.", "그녀가 이제 그를 똑바로 바라보며 받아쳤다."]], [["“No! Carker!", "\"안 되네!"], ["I beg that you do not.", "카커! 부디 그러지 말게."], ["I insist that you do not,”", "단단히 이르건대 그러지 말게,\""], ["cried Mr Dombey, stopping that noiseless gentleman in his withdrawal.", "돔비 씨가, 소리도 없이 물러나려던 그 신사를 멈춰 세우며 외쳤다."], ["“Mr Carker, Madam, as you know, possesses my confidence.", "\"카커 씨는, 부인, 당신도 알다시피, 나의 신임을 받는 사람이오."], ["He is as well acquainted as myself with the subject on which I speak.", "그는 내가 말하고 있는 이 문제를 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있소."], ["I beg to tell you, for your information, Mrs Dombey, that I consider these wealthy and important persons confer a distinction upon me:”", "당신에게 일러두건대, 돔비 부인, 알아 두시오, 나는 이 부유하고 중요한 인사들이 나에게 영예를 베푸는 것이라 여기오.\""], ["and Mr Dombey drew himself up, as having now rendered them of the highest possible importance.", "그러고는 돔비 씨가, 이제 그들을 더없이 중요한 존재로 만들어 놓았다는 듯이, 몸을 한껏 곧추세웠다."]], [["“I ask you,”", "\"다시 묻겠어요,\""], ["she repeated, bending her disdainful, steady gaze upon him, “do you know that there is someone here, Sir?”", "그녀가 멸시 어린 흔들림 없는 시선을 그에게 못 박으며 되풀이했다, \"여기 누가 있다는 걸 아시나요, 여보?\""]], [["“I must entreat,”", "\"부디 청하건대,\""], ["said Mr Carker, stepping forward, “I must beg, I must demand, to be released.", "카커 씨가 앞으로 나서며 말했다, \"부디 빌건대, 부디 요구하건대, 저를 놓아주십시오."], ["Slight and unimportant as this difference is—”", "이 차이가 아무리 사소하고 하찮은 것이라 해도—\""]], [["Mrs Skewton, who had been intent upon her daughter’s face, took him up here.", "딸의 얼굴을 골똘히 살피고 있던 스큐턴 부인이 여기서 그 말을 받았다."]], [["“My sweetest Edith,”", "\"내 더없이 사랑스러운 에디스,\""], ["she said, “and my dearest Dombey; our excellent friend Mr Carker, for so I am sure I ought to mention him—”", "그녀가 말했다, \"그리고 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비. 우리의 훌륭한 벗 카커 씨가—분명 그렇게 불러야 마땅하니까—\""]], [["Mr Carker murmured, “Too much honour.”", "카커 씨가 \"과분한 영광입니다\"라고 중얼거렸다."]], [["“—has used the very words that were in my mind, and that I have been dying, these ages, for an opportunity of introducing.", "\"—내 마음속에 있던 바로 그 말을, 내가 그동안 한없이, 꺼낼 기회를 애타게 바라던 그 말을 해 주었어요."], ["Slight and unimportant!", "사소하고 하찮은 것이라고!"], ["My sweetest Edith, and my dearest Dombey, do we not know that any difference between you two—No, Flowers; not now.”", "내 더없이 사랑스러운 에디스, 그리고 내 더없이 사랑하는 돔비, 너희 둘 사이의 어떤 차이라도—아니, 플라워스, 지금은 아니야.\""]], [["Flowers was the maid, who, finding gentlemen present, retreated with precipitation.", "플라워스는 시녀였는데, 신사들이 있는 것을 보고는 황급히 물러났다."]], [["“That any difference between you two,”", "\"너희 둘 사이의 어떤 차이라도,\""], ["resumed Mrs Skewton, “with the Heart you possess in common, and the excessively charming bond of feeling that there is between you, must be slight and unimportant?", "스큐턴 부인이 말을 이었다, \"너희가 함께 지닌 그 마음과, 너희 사이에 있는 그 더없이 매혹적인 정의 끈을 생각하면, 사소하고 하찮은 것일 수밖에 없지 않겠니? 그 사실을 이보다 더 잘 일러 줄 말이 어디 있겠어?"], ["What words could better define the fact?", "없지. 그러니 나는 이 사소한 기회를—이토록 천성과, 너희 각자의 인품과, 그 모든 것으로 가득 찬, 어버이의 눈에 참으로 눈물을 고이게 할 만한 이 하찮은 기회를—기꺼이 빌려 이렇게 말하련다."], ["None.", "나는 그런 것들에 추호도 무게를 두지 않는단다."], ["Therefore I am glad to take this slight occasion—this trifling occasion, that is so replete with Nature, and your individual characters, and all that—so truly calculated to bring the tears into a parent’s eyes—to say that I attach no importance to them in the least, except as developing these minor elements of Soul; and that, unlike most Mamas-in-law (that odious phrase, dear Dombey!) as they have been represented to me to exist in this I fear too artificial world, I never shall attempt to interpose between you, at such a time, and never can much regret, after all, such little flashes of the torch of What’s-his-name—not Cupid, but the other delightful creature.”", "다만 영혼의 이런 자잘한 요소들이 드러나는 것으로 볼 뿐이지. 그리고, 이 너무도 가식적인 세상에—유감스럽게도 그렇지—존재한다고 내가 들어 온 대부분의 장모들(그 끔찍한 표현이라니, 사랑하는 돔비!)과는 달리, 나는 그런 때에 결코 너희 사이에 끼어들려 하지 않을 것이며, 그 뭐라더라—큐피드가 아니라, 그 다른 사랑스러운 녀석의 횃불이 그렇게 잠깐씩 번쩍이는 것을, 따지고 보면 그리 크게 안타까워하지도 않을 거야.\""]], [["There was a sharpness in the good mother’s glance at both her children as she spoke, that may have been expressive of a direct and well-considered purpose hidden between these rambling words.", "그 선량한 어머니가 이렇게 말하면서 자기 두 자식을 향해 던진 눈길에는 날카로움이 있었으니, 그것은 이 두서없는 말 속에 감추어진 어떤 직접적이고 잘 궁리된 의도를 드러내는 것이었는지도 모른다."], ["That purpose, providently to detach herself in the beginning from all the clankings of their chain that were to come, and to shelter herself with the fiction of her innocent belief in their mutual affection, and their adaptation to each other.", "그 의도란, 앞으로 들이닥칠 그들 사슬의 온갖 절거덕거리는 소리로부터 애초에 자기 자신을 떼어 놓고, 둘의 상호 애정과 서로에 대한 어울림을 결백하게 믿고 있다는 허구로 자기 몸을 감싸는 것이었다."]], [["“I have pointed out to Mrs Dombey,”", "\"나는 돔비 부인에게 지적해 두었습니다,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, in his most stately manner, “that in her conduct thus early in our married life, to which I object, and which, I request, may be corrected.", "돔비 씨가 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 말했다, \"우리 결혼 생활의 이토록 이른 시기에 보이는 그 행실에 대해 내가 이의를 제기한다는 것을, 그리고 그것이 시정되기를 내가 요구한다는 것을 말이오."], ["Carker,”", "카커,\""], ["with a nod of dismissal, “good-night to you!”", "물러가라는 끄덕임과 함께, \"잘 가게!\""]], [["Mr Carker bowed to the imperious form of the Bride, whose sparkling eye was fixed upon her husband; and stopping at Cleopatra’s couch on his way out, raised to his lips the hand she graciously extended to him, in lowly and admiring homage.", "카커 씨는 그 거만한 신부의 자태를 향해 절을 했는데, 그녀의 반짝이는 눈은 자기 남편에게 못 박혀 있었다. 그는 나가는 길에 클레오파트라의 긴 의자 앞에 멈춰 서서, 그녀가 우아하게 내미는 손을, 낮고도 흠모 어린 경의를 담아 입술로 들어 올렸다."]], [["If his handsome wife had reproached him, or even changed countenance, or broken the silence in which she remained, by one word, now that they were alone (for Cleopatra made off with all speed), Mr Dombey would have been equal to some assertion of his case against her.", "만일 그의 아름다운 아내가 그를 나무라기라도 했다면, 아니 안색을 바꾸기라도 했다면, 혹은 그녀가 잠겨 있던 그 침묵을, 이제 둘만 남은 마당에(클레오파트라가 부리나케 사라졌으므로) 단 한마디로라도 깨뜨려 주기라도 했다면, 돔비 씨는 그녀에 맞서 자기 주장을 어느 정도 내세울 수 있었으리라."], ["But the intense, unutterable, withering scorn, with which, after looking upon him, she dropped her eyes, as if he were too worthless and indifferent to her to be challenged with a syllable—the ineffable disdain and haughtiness in which she sat before him—the cold inflexible resolve with which her every feature seemed to bear him down, and put him by—these, he had no resource against; and he left her, with her whole overbearing beauty concentrated on despising him.", "그러나 그를 한번 바라본 다음, 마치 그가 너무도 하찮고 자기에게 아무래도 좋은 존재라 한 음절로도 따질 가치가 없다는 듯이 눈을 떨어뜨리던 그 강렬하고 형언할 수 없는, 사람을 시들게 하는 경멸—그를 앞에 두고 앉아 있던 그 이루 말할 수 없는 멸시와 오만함—제 얼굴의 모든 이목구비로 그를 짓누르고 밀쳐 내는 듯하던 그 차갑고 굽힐 줄 모르는 결의—이런 것들에 대해 그는 맞설 방도가 없었다. 그리하여 그는, 그를 멸시하는 데에 그 위압적인 아름다움을 온통 집중하고 있는 그녀를 남겨 둔 채 떠났다."]], [["Was he coward enough to watch her, an hour afterwards, on the old well staircase, where he had once seen Florence in the moonlight, toiling up with Paul?", "그가 한 시간쯤 뒤에, 일찍이 달빛 속에서 폴을 데리고 힘겹게 올라오던 플로렌스를 본 적이 있는 그 낡은 회랑식 계단에서, 그녀를 몰래 지켜볼 만큼 비겁했던가?"], ["Or was he in the dark by accident, when, looking up, he saw her coming, with a light, from the room where Florence lay, and marked again the face so changed, which he could not subdue?", "아니면 우연히 어둠 속에 있다가, 눈을 들어, 플로렌스가 누워 있는 방에서 그녀가 등불을 들고 나오는 것을 보고, 그가 끝내 굴복시키지 못한 그토록 달라진 그 얼굴을 다시금 똑똑히 보았던가?"]], [["But it could never alter as his own did.", "그러나 그 얼굴은 그 자신의 얼굴처럼 변할 수는 없었으리라."], ["It never, in its uttermost pride and passion, knew the shadow that had fallen on his, in the dark corner, on the night of the return; and often since; and which deepened on it now, as he looked up.", "그 얼굴은, 그 극도의 자긍심과 격정 속에서도, 돌아오던 그날 밤 어두운 구석에서 그의 얼굴 위로 내려앉았던 그 그림자를, 그리고 그 뒤로도 자주 내려앉았던 그 그림자를, 그리고 지금 그가 눈을 들어 올렸을 때 그 위로 더욱 짙어진 그 그림자를, 결코 알지 못했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVII. More Warnings than One", "# 제32장"]], [["Florence, Edith, and Mrs Skewton were together next day, and the carriage was waiting at the door to take them out.", "이튿날 플로렌스와 이디스, 그리고 스큐턴 부인이 함께 있었고, 마차는 그들을 데리고 나가려고 문 앞에서 기다리고 있었다."], ["For Cleopatra had her galley again now, and Withers, no longer the wan, stood upright in a pigeon-breasted jacket and military trousers, behind her wheel-less chair at dinner-time and butted no more.", "클레오파트라가 이제 다시 자기 유람선을 갖게 되었기 때문이었으니, 더는 핼쑥하지 않은 위더스가 가슴을 비둘기처럼 내민 재킷에 군용 바지를 입고 식사 때 바퀴 없는 그녀의 의자 뒤에 꼿꼿이 서서, 더는 머리로 들이받는 일도 없었다."], ["The hair of Withers was radiant with pomatum, in these days of down, and he wore kid gloves and smelt of the water of Cologne.", "위더스의 머리카락은 이 솜털 같은 나날에 포마드로 윤이 났고, 그는 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 오드콜로뉴 향을 풍겼다."]], [["They were assembled in Cleopatra’s room.", "그들은 클레오파트라의 방에 모여 있었다."], ["The Serpent of old Nile (not to mention her disrespectfully) was reposing on her sofa, sipping her morning chocolate at three o’clock in the afternoon, and Flowers the Maid was fastening on her youthful cuffs and frills, and performing a kind of private coronation ceremony on her, with a peach-coloured velvet bonnet; the artificial roses in which nodded to uncommon advantage, as the palsy trifled with them, like a breeze.", "오래된 나일강의 뱀이(그분을 무례하게 일컫자는 뜻은 아니다) 소파에 기대어 오후 세 시에 아침 초콜릿을 홀짝이고 있었고, 하녀 플라워스는 그녀에게 젊은이다운 소맷동과 주름 장식을 채워 주며, 복숭아색 벨벳 보닛으로 일종의 사적인 대관식을 거행하고 있었다. 그 보닛에 달린 조화 장미는 미풍에 흔들리듯 마비된 떨림이 희롱하는 통에 유난히 보기 좋게 까딱거렸다."]], [["“I think I am a little nervous this morning, Flowers,”", "\"오늘 아침엔 내가 좀 신경이 곤두선 것 같구나, 플라워스,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton.", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다."], ["“My hand quite shakes.”", "\"손이 영 떨려.\""]], [["“You were the life of the party last night, Ma’am, you know,”", "\"어젯밤엔 마님이 좌중의 활력이셨잖아요,\""], ["returned Flowers, “and you suffer for it today, you see.”", "플라워스가 응수했다, \"그러니 오늘 그 대가를 치르시는 거지요.\""]], [["Edith, who had beckoned Florence to the window, and was looking out, with her back turned on the toilet of her esteemed mother, suddenly withdrew from it, as if it had lightened.", "이디스는 플로렌스를 창가로 손짓해 부른 뒤, 존경스러운 어머니의 화장하는 모습에 등을 돌리고 밖을 내다보고 있었는데, 마치 번개가 친 듯 갑자기 그 자리에서 물러섰다."]], [["“My darling child,”", "\"내 사랑스러운 아가,\""], ["cried Cleopatra, languidly, “you are not nervous?", "클레오파트라가 나른하게 외쳤다, \"너까지 신경이 곤두선 건 아니겠지?"], ["Don’t tell me, my dear Edith, that you, so enviably self-possessed, are beginning to be a martyr too, like your unfortunately constituted mother!", "내 사랑 이디스, 너처럼 그렇게 부러우리만큼 침착한 아이가, 불행한 체질을 타고난 네 어미처럼 너도 순교자가 되어 가고 있다는 말은 제발 하지 말아 다오!"], ["Withers, someone at the door.”", "위더스, 문에 누가 왔다.\""]], [["“Card, Ma’am,”", "\"명함입니다, 마님,\""], ["said Withers, taking it towards Mrs Dombey.", "위더스가 명함을 돔비 부인 쪽으로 가져가며 말했다."]], [["“I am going out,”", "\"나는 나갈 거예요,\""], ["she said without looking at it.", "그녀가 명함을 쳐다보지도 않고 말했다."]], [["“My dear love,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["drawled Mrs Skewton, “how very odd to send that message without seeing the name!", "스큐턴 부인이 느릿느릿 말했다, \"이름도 보지 않고 그런 전갈을 보내다니 어찌나 이상한지!"], ["Bring it here, Withers.", "이리 가져오너라, 위더스."], ["Dear me, my love; Mr Carker, too!", "어머나 얘야, 카커 씨로구나!"], ["That very sensible person!”", "그 아주 분별 있는 분 말이야!\""]], [["“I am going out,”", "\"나는 나갈 거예요,\""], ["repeated Edith, in so imperious a tone that Withers, going to the door, imperiously informed the servant who was waiting, “Mrs Dombey is going out.", "이디스가 어찌나 위압적인 어조로 되풀이했던지, 문으로 가던 위더스도 위압적으로 기다리던 하인에게 일렀다, \"돔비 부인께서는 나가신다. 어서 물러가게나,\""], ["Get along with you,” and shut it on him.", "그러고는 그의 면전에서 문을 닫았다."]], [["But the servant came back after a short absence, and whispered to Withers again, who once more, and not very willingly, presented himself before Mrs Dombey.", "그러나 하인은 잠깐 자리를 비웠다가 돌아와 다시 위더스에게 속삭였고, 위더스는 한 번 더, 그것도 그리 내키지 않는 기색으로 돔비 부인 앞에 나섰다."]], [["“If you please, Ma’am, Mr Carker sends his respectful compliments, and begs you would spare him one minute, if you could—for business, Ma’am, if you please.”", "\"황송하옵니다, 마님, 카커 씨께서 정중한 안부를 전하시며, 가능하시다면 단 일 분만 시간을 내어 주십사 청하옵니다—사업 때문이라고 하옵니다, 마님, 괜찮으시다면요.\""]], [["“Really, my love,”", "\"정말이지 얘야,\" 스큐턴 부인이 더없이 부드러운 태도로 말했다."], ["said Mrs Skewton in her mildest manner; for her daughter’s face was threatening; “if you would allow me to offer a word, I should recommend—”", "딸의 얼굴이 험악해지고 있었기 때문이다. \"내가 한마디 거들어도 된다면, 권하건대—\""]], [["“Show him this way,”", "\"이리로 모셔라,\""], ["said Edith.", "이디스가 말했다."], ["As Withers disappeared to execute the command, she added, frowning on her mother, “As he comes at your recommendation, let him come to your room.”", "위더스가 그 명을 수행하려 사라지자, 그녀는 어머니에게 눈살을 찌푸리며 덧붙였다, \"어머니 권유로 오는 분이니, 어머니 방으로 오시게 하지요.\""]], [["“May I—shall I go away?”", "\"제가—제가 나가 있을까요?\""], ["asked Florence, hurriedly.", "플로렌스가 황급히 물었다."]], [["Edith nodded yes, but on her way to the door Florence met the visitor coming in.", "이디스가 그렇게 하라고 고개를 끄덕였으나, 문으로 가던 플로렌스는 들어오는 방문객과 마주쳤다."], ["With the same disagreeable mixture of familiarity and forbearance, with which he had first addressed her, he addressed her now in his softest manner—hoped she was quite well—needed not to ask, with such looks to anticipate the answer—had scarcely had the honour to know her, last night, she was so greatly changed—and held the door open for her to pass out; with a secret sense of power in her shrinking from him, that all the deference and politeness of his manner could not quite conceal.", "처음 그녀에게 말을 걸 때와 똑같은, 친근함과 너그러운 자제가 뒤섞인 그 불쾌한 태도로, 그는 이제 더없이 부드러운 모습으로 그녀에게 말을 건넸다—그녀가 아주 건강하기를 바란다고—그런 안색을 보니 대답을 미리 알 수 있어 굳이 물을 것도 없다고—어젯밤에는 그녀를 알아뵙는 영광을 거의 누리지 못했는데, 그토록 크게 달라지셨다고—그러고는 그녀가 지나갈 수 있도록 문을 잡아 주었다. 그녀가 자기 앞에서 움츠러드는 데서 은밀한 권력의 감각을 느끼는 듯했는데, 그것은 그의 온갖 공손함과 정중한 태도로도 끝내 다 감추지 못했다."]], [["He then bowed himself for a moment over Mrs Skewton’s condescending hand, and lastly bowed to Edith.", "그런 다음 그는 스큐턴 부인이 황송하게 내미는 손 위로 잠시 몸을 숙여 절했고, 마지막으로 이디스에게 절했다."], ["Coldly returning his salute without looking at him, and neither seating herself nor inviting him to be seated, she waited for him to speak.", "그를 쳐다보지도 않은 채 차갑게 그 인사에 답례한 그녀는, 자리에 앉지도, 그에게 앉기를 권하지도 않은 채, 그가 입을 열기를 기다렸다."]], [["Entrenched in her pride and power, and with all the obduracy of her spirit summoned about her, still her old conviction that she and her mother had been known by this man in their worst colours, from their first acquaintance; that every degradation she had suffered in her own eyes was as plain to him as to herself; that he read her life as though it were a vile book, and fluttered the leaves before her in slight looks and tones of voice which no one else could detect; weakened and undermined her.", "자기의 자존심과 권력 안에 단단히 틀어박혀, 그 정신의 온갖 완강함을 불러 모았음에도, 그녀와 그녀의 어머니가 이 사내에게 처음 알게 된 순간부터 가장 흉한 본색까지 다 들켰다는 오래된 확신이, 그녀가 자기 눈에 겪어 온 모든 굴욕이 자기 자신에게 그러하듯 그에게도 빤히 드러나 있다는 확신이, 그가 마치 그녀의 인생을 비열한 책 한 권 읽듯 읽어 내며, 다른 누구도 알아채지 못할 가벼운 눈짓과 말투로 그 책장을 그녀 앞에서 펄럭여 보인다는 확신이, 그녀를 약하게 하고 무너뜨렸다."], ["Proudly as she opposed herself to him, with her commanding face exacting his humility, her disdainful lip repulsing him, her bosom angry at his intrusion, and the dark lashes of her eyes sullenly veiling their light, that no ray of it might shine upon him—and submissively as he stood before her, with an entreating injured manner, but with complete submission to her will—she knew, in her own soul, that the cases were reversed, and that the triumph and superiority were his, and that he knew it full well.", "그녀가 자랑스레 그에게 맞서 명령하는 듯한 얼굴로 그의 비굴함을 요구하고, 멸시하는 입술로 그를 물리치고, 가슴이 그의 침입에 분노하고, 그 눈의 검은 속눈썹이 빛이 그에게 한 줄기도 비치지 못하도록 그 빛을 음울하게 가려 보아도—또 그가 애원하는 듯 상처 입은 태도로, 그러나 그녀의 뜻에는 완전히 복종하며 그녀 앞에 순종하듯 서 있어도—그녀는 제 영혼 속에서 알고 있었다, 처지가 뒤바뀌어 있다는 것을, 승리와 우위는 그의 것임을, 그리고 그가 그것을 너무도 잘 알고 있다는 것을."]], [["“I have presumed,”", "\"제가 외람되이,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “to solicit an interview, and I have ventured to describe it as being one of business, because—”", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"면담을 청했고, 또 외람되이 그것을 사업상의 일이라 일컬었습니다. 왜냐하면—\""]], [["“Perhaps you are charged by Mr Dombey with some message of reproof,”", "\"아마 돔비 씨에게서 무슨 책망의 전갈이라도 받아 오셨나 보군요,\""], ["said Edith “You possess Mr Dombey’s confidence in such an unusual degree, Sir, that you would scarcely surprise me if that were your business.”", "이디스가 말했다. \"당신은 돔비 씨의 신임을 워낙 유별나게 받고 계시니, 만일 그것이 당신의 용건이라 해도 저로서는 별로 놀랍지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“I have no message to the lady who sheds a lustre upon his name,”", "\"그분의 이름에 광채를 더해 주시는 부인께 전할 전갈 따위는 없습니다,\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."], ["“But I entreat that lady, on my own behalf, to be just to a very humble claimant for justice at her hands—a mere dependant of Mr Dombey’s—which is a position of humility; and to reflect upon my perfect helplessness last night, and the impossibility of my avoiding the share that was forced upon me in a very painful occasion.”", "\"다만 제 자신을 위하여, 부인의 손에 정의를 구하는 더없이 미천한 청원자에게—한낱 돔비 씨에게 딸린 자에게—그것은 미천한 처지이지요—공정하게 대해 주시기를, 그리고 어젯밤 제가 얼마나 완전히 무력했는지, 또 매우 고통스러운 자리에서 제게 떠넘겨진 몫을 도무지 피할 수 없었음을 헤아려 주시기를 간청합니다.\""]], [["“My dearest Edith,”", "\"내 사랑 이디스,\""], ["hinted Cleopatra in a low voice, as she held her eye-glass aside, “really very charming of Mr What’s-his-name.", "클레오파트라가 안경을 비스듬히 들면서 낮은 목소리로 귀띔했다, \"이름이 뭐라더라 하는 저 양반, 정말이지 매우 매력적이구나."], ["And full of heart!”", "게다가 마음이 풍부해!\""]], [["“For I do,”", "\"왜냐하면 저는,\""], ["said Mr Carker, appealing to Mrs Skewton with a look of grateful deference,—“I do venture to call it a painful occasion, though merely because it was so to me, who had the misfortune to be present.", "카커 씨가 고마운 듯 공손한 눈빛으로 스큐턴 부인에게 호소하며 말했다, \"감히 그것을 고통스러운 자리였다고 부릅니다, 다만 그 자리에 있는 불운을 겪은 제게 그러했기 때문일 뿐입니다."], ["So slight a difference, as between the principals—between those who love each other with disinterested devotion, and would make any sacrifice of self in such a cause—is nothing.", "당사자들 사이의—사심 없는 헌신으로 서로 사랑하며, 그런 일을 위해서라면 어떤 자기희생도 마다하지 않을 두 분 사이의—그토록 사소한 의견 차이는 아무것도 아닙니다."], ["As Mrs Skewton herself expressed, with so much truth and feeling last night, it is nothing.”", "스큐턴 부인께서 어젯밤 친히 그토록 진실하고 절절하게 말씀하셨듯이, 그것은 아무것도 아닙니다.\""]], [["Edith could not look at him, but she said after a few moments.", "이디스는 그를 쳐다볼 수 없었으나, 잠시 후 입을 열었다."]], [["“And your business, Sir—”", "\"그래서 용건은 무엇이지요, 선생—\""]], [["“Edith, my pet,”", "\"이디스, 얘야,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “all this time Mr Carker is standing!", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"이렇게 내내 카커 씨를 서 계시게 하다니!"], ["My dear Mr Carker, take a seat, I beg.”", "카커 씨, 자, 부디 자리에 앉으세요.\""]], [["He offered no reply to the mother, but fixed his eyes on the proud daughter, as though he would only be bidden by her, and was resolved to be bidden by her.", "그는 어머니에게는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 오직 그녀에게서만 권유받기를 바라며, 또 기어이 그녀에게서 권유받고야 말겠다는 듯, 거만한 딸에게 눈길을 고정했다."], ["Edith, in spite of herself, sat down, and slightly motioned with her hand to him to be seated too.", "이디스는 자기도 모르게 자리에 앉았고, 그에게도 앉으라고 손으로 살짝 신호했다."], ["No action could be colder, haughtier, more insolent in its air of supremacy and disrespect, but she had struggled against even that concession ineffectually, and it was wrested from her.", "그보다 더 차갑고, 더 오만하고, 우월함과 무례함을 풍기는 데 있어 그보다 더 건방진 행동은 없었으나, 그녀는 그 양보조차 하지 않으려 안간힘을 썼지만 헛수고였고, 그것은 그녀에게서 억지로 빼앗긴 것이었다."], ["That was enough!", "그것으로 충분했다!"], ["Mr Carker sat down.", "카커 씨는 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“May I be allowed, Madam,”", "\"부인, 허락해 주신다면,\""], ["said Carker, turning his white teeth on Mrs Skewton like a light—“a lady of your excellent sense and quick feeling will give me credit, for good reason, I am sure—to address what I have to say, to Mrs Dombey, and to leave her to impart it to you who are her best and dearest friend—next to Mr Dombey?”", "카커가 빛을 내듯 스큐턴 부인을 향해 흰 이를 드러내며 말했다—\"부인처럼 빼어난 분별력과 예민한 감수성을 지니신 분이라면, 분명 그럴 만한 이유가 있어서라고 저를 믿어 주시리라 확신합니다—드릴 말씀을 돔비 부인께만 아뢰고, 그것을—돔비 씨 다음으로—그분의 가장 좋고 가장 소중한 벗이신 부인께 전하는 일은 부인께 맡겨도 되겠는지요?\""]], [["Mrs Skewton would have retired, but Edith stopped her.", "스큐턴 부인은 물러가려 했으나, 이디스가 그녀를 붙들었다."], ["Edith would have stopped him too, and indignantly ordered him to speak openly or not at all, but that he said, in a low Voice—“Miss Florence—the young lady who has just left the room—”", "이디스는 그도 막아서서, 떳떳이 말하든지 아예 말하지 말든지 하라고 분연히 명령하려 했으나, 그가 낮은 목소리로 이렇게 말했다—\"플로렌스 아가씨—방금 이 방을 나가신 그 젊은 분 말입니다—\""]], [["Edith suffered him to proceed.", "이디스는 그가 말을 이어 가도록 내버려 두었다."], ["She looked at him now.", "이제 그녀는 그를 쳐다보았다."], ["As he bent forward, to be nearer, with the utmost show of delicacy and respect, and with his teeth persuasively arrayed, in a self-depreciating smile, she felt as if she could have struck him dead.", "그가 더 가까이 다가오려고 극진히 정중하고 공손한 태도로, 자기를 낮추는 미소 속에 이를 설득하듯 가지런히 드러내며 몸을 앞으로 굽혔을 때, 그녀는 그를 때려죽일 수도 있을 것 같은 심정이었다."]], [["“Miss Florence’s position,”", "\"플로렌스 아가씨의 처지는,\""], ["he began, “has been an unfortunate one.", "그가 말문을 열었다, \"불운한 것이었습니다."], ["I have a difficulty in alluding to it to you, whose attachment to her father is naturally watchful and jealous of every word that applies to him.”", "아버님에 대한 부인의 애착이 본래 그분에게 닿는 말 한마디 한마디에도 경계하고 민감하실 터이니, 그분 앞에서 이 이야기를 꺼내기가 저로서는 망설여집니다.\""], ["Always distinct and soft in speech, no language could describe the extent of his distinctness and softness, when he said these words, or came to any others of a similar import.", "언제나 또렷하고 부드러운 말씨였으나, 그가 이 말을, 또 그와 비슷한 다른 어떤 말을 할 때의 그 또렷함과 부드러움은 어떤 언어로도 그 정도를 다 표현할 수 없었다."], ["“But, as one who is devoted to Mr Dombey in his different way, and whose life is passed in admiration of Mr Dombey’s character, may I say, without offence to your tenderness as a wife, that Miss Florence has unhappily been neglected—by her father.", "\"그러나 다른 방식으로나마 돔비 씨에게 헌신하는 사람으로서, 또 돔비 씨의 인품을 흠모하며 일생을 보내는 사람으로서, 아내로서의 부인의 다정함을 거스르지 않는 한도에서 말씀드려도 된다면, 플로렌스 아가씨는 불행히도 방치되어 왔습니다—그 아버님에 의해서요."], ["May I say by her father?”", "아버님에 의해서라고 말씀드려도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["Edith replied, “I know it.”", "이디스가 대답했다, \"알고 있어요.\""]], [["“You know it!”", "\"알고 계신다고요!\""], ["said Mr Carker, with a great appearance of relief.", "카커 씨가 크게 안도하는 기색으로 말했다."], ["“It removes a mountain from my breast.", "\"제 가슴에서 산 하나가 치워지는군요."], ["May I hope you know how the neglect originated; in what an amiable phase of Mr Dombey’s pride—character I mean?”", "그 방치가 어떻게 비롯되었는지도 알고 계시기를 바라도 되겠습니까. 돔비 씨의 자존심—그러니까 인품의—그 사랑스러운 한 단면에서 비롯되었다는 것을요?\""]], [["“You may pass that by, Sir,”", "\"그 대목은 넘어가셔도 됩니다, 선생,\""], ["she returned, “and come the sooner to the end of what you have to say.”", "그녀가 응수했다, \"하실 말씀의 결론으로 더 빨리 들어가세요.\""]], [["“Indeed, I am sensible, Madam,”", "\"실로, 저는 잘 알고 있습니다, 부인,\""], ["replied Carker,—“trust me, I am deeply sensible, that Mr Dombey can require no justification in anything to you.", "카커가 응수했다, \"믿어 주십시오, 저는 깊이 알고 있습니다, 돔비 씨가 부인 앞에서 그 무엇에 대해서도 변명할 필요가 없다는 것을요."], ["But, kindly judge of my breast by your own, and you will forgive my interest in him, if in its excess, it goes at all astray.”", "그러나 부디 부인 자신의 가슴으로 제 가슴을 헤아려 주시면, 그분을 향한 제 관심이, 혹 그 지나침으로 조금 빗나가더라도, 용서해 주실 것입니다.\""]], [["What a stab to her proud heart, to sit there, face to face with him, and have him tendering her false oath at the altar again and again for her acceptance, and pressing it upon her like the dregs of a sickening cup she could not own her loathing of, or turn away from!", "자기 자존심 강한 가슴에 얼마나 깊은 비수였던가, 그와 얼굴을 맞대고 앉아, 그가 제단에서 한 거짓 맹세를 거듭거듭 그녀에게 받아들이라 들이밀며, 그녀가 그 역겨움을 인정할 수도, 외면할 수도 없는 구역질 나는 잔의 찌꺼기처럼 그것을 강요하는 것을 견뎌야 한다는 것은!"], ["How shame, remorse, and passion raged within her, when, upright and majestic in her beauty before him, she knew that in her spirit she was down at his feet!", "그녀가 그 아름다움 속에 꼿꼿하고 위엄 있게 그의 앞에 서 있으면서도, 제 영혼 속에서는 그의 발치에 엎드려 있음을 알았을 때, 그녀 안에서 수치와 회한과 격정이 얼마나 들끓었던가!"]], [["“Miss Florence,”", "\"플로렌스 아가씨는,\""], ["said Carker, “left to the care—if one may call it care—of servants and mercenary people, in every way her inferiors, necessarily wanted some guide and compass in her younger days, and, naturally, for want of them, has been indiscreet, and has in some degree forgotten her station.", "카커가 말했다, \"모든 면에서 자기보다 못한 하인들과 돈에 움직이는 사람들의 보살핌에—그것을 보살핌이라 부를 수 있다면—맡겨진 채, 어린 시절 필연적으로 무슨 길잡이도 나침반도 없었고, 그래서 당연히 그것이 없는 탓에 경솔하게 굴어 왔으며, 어느 정도는 자기 신분을 잊어버렸습니다."], ["There was some folly about one Walter, a common lad, who is fortunately dead now: and some very undesirable association, I regret to say, with certain coasting sailors, of anything but good repute, and a runaway old bankrupt.”", "월터라는 어떤 자에 대한 어리석은 일이 있었는데, 흔해 빠진 청년으로 다행히도 이제는 죽었지요. 그리고, 말씀드리기 송구하오나, 평판이라곤 도무지 좋지 않은 어떤 연안 뱃사람들과, 또 달아난 늙은 파산자와의 아주 바람직하지 못한 교제도 좀 있었습니다.\""]], [["“I have heard the circumstances, Sir,”", "\"그 사정은 저도 들어 알고 있어요, 선생,\""], ["said Edith, flashing her disdainful glance upon him, “and I know that you pervert them.", "이디스가 멸시하는 눈빛을 그에게 번뜩이며 말했다, \"그리고 당신이 그것을 왜곡하고 있다는 것도 알아요."], ["You may not know it.", "당신은 모르고 그러는지도 모르지요."], ["I hope so.”", "그러기를 바라요.\""]], [["“Pardon me,”", "\"용서하십시오,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “I believe that nobody knows them so well as I.", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"저는 그 누구도 저만큼 그 사정을 잘 아는 사람은 없다고 믿습니다."], ["Your generous and ardent nature, Madam—the same nature which is so nobly imperative in vindication of your beloved and honoured husband, and which has blessed him as even his merits deserve—I must respect, defer to, bow before.", "부인의 너그럽고 열렬한 천성—사랑하고 존경하는 남편을 변호하는 데 그토록 고귀하게 단호하며, 그 미덕에 합당한 만큼 그분을 축복해 온 바로 그 천성—을 저는 존경하고, 따르며, 그 앞에 머리를 숙이지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["But, as regards the circumstances, which is indeed the business I presumed to solicit your attention to, I can have no doubt, since, in the execution of my trust as Mr Dombey’s confidential—I presume to say—friend, I have fully ascertained them.", "그러나 그 사정에 관해서라면—이것이야말로 제가 외람되이 부인의 주의를 청한 용건입니다만—저는 추호도 의심할 수 없습니다. 왜냐하면 돔비 씨의 비밀스러운—감히 말씀드리건대—벗으로서 맡은 소임을 수행하는 과정에서 그것들을 빠짐없이 확인했기 때문입니다."], ["In my execution of that trust; in my deep concern, which you can so well understand, for everything relating to him, intensified, if you will (for I fear I labour under your displeasure), by the lower motive of desire to prove my diligence, and make myself the more acceptable; I have long pursued these circumstances by myself and trustworthy instruments, and have innumerable and most minute proofs.”", "그 소임을 수행하면서, 또 그분과 관련된 모든 일에 대한, 부인께서 그토록 잘 헤아리실 저의 깊은 관심으로—굳이 말하자면 제 부지런함을 입증하고 저를 좀 더 받아들여지게 만들려는 더 낮은 동기로 더욱 강해진(부인의 불쾌함을 사고 있는 듯하여 두렵습니다만)—저는 오랫동안 저 자신과 믿을 만한 수단을 통해 이 사정을 추적해 왔고, 헤아릴 수 없이 많고도 더없이 세세한 증거를 가지고 있습니다.\""]], [["She raised her eyes no higher than his mouth, but she saw the means of mischief vaunted in every tooth it contained.", "그녀는 눈을 그의 입 높이보다 더 높이 들지 않았으나, 그 입에 담긴 이 하나하나에서 해악의 수단이 으스대는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Pardon me, Madam,”", "\"용서하십시오, 부인,\""], ["he continued, “if in my perplexity, I presume to take counsel with you, and to consult your pleasure.", "그가 말을 이었다, \"제가 곤혹스러운 나머지, 외람되이 부인과 의논하고 부인의 뜻을 여쭙고자 하더라도요."], ["I think I have observed that you are greatly interested in Miss Florence?”", "제가 보기에 부인께서는 플로렌스 아가씨에게 무척 관심을 두고 계신 듯한데, 제가 그것을 알아챈 것 같습니다만?\""]], [["What was there in her he had not observed, and did not know?", "그가 그녀에게서 알아채지 못한 것이, 알지 못하는 것이 무엇이 있었던가?"], ["Humbled and yet maddened by the thought, in every new presentment of it, however faint, she pressed her teeth upon her quivering lip to force composure on it, and distantly inclined her head in reply.", "그 생각이, 아무리 희미하게라도 새롭게 떠오를 때마다, 굴욕스러우면서도 미칠 것 같아, 그녀는 떨리는 입술 위에 이를 꾹 눌러 억지로 침착을 강요하고는, 쌀쌀맞게 고개를 숙여 답했다."]], [["“This interest, Madam—so touching an evidence of everything associated with Mr Dombey being dear to you—induces me to pause before I make him acquainted with these circumstances, which, as yet, he does not know.", "\"이 관심은요, 부인—돔비 씨와 연관된 모든 것이 부인께 소중하다는 그토록 가슴 뭉클한 증거인 그 관심은—제가 이 사정을, 아직 그분이 모르고 계신 이 사정을 그분께 알려 드리기에 앞서 잠시 멈추게 만듭니다."], ["It so shakes me, if I may make the confession, in my allegiance, that on the intimation of the least desire to that effect from you, I would suppress them.”", "고백해도 된다면, 그것이 제 충성심을 어찌나 흔드는지, 부인께서 그러기를 바란다는 기색을 조금이라도 비치시면, 저는 그것들을 묻어 두겠습니다.\""]], [["Edith raised her head quickly, and starting back, bent her dark glance upon him.", "이디스가 재빨리 고개를 들고, 흠칫 물러나며, 그 검은 눈길을 그에게 쏟았다."], ["He met it with his blandest and most deferential smile, and went on.", "그는 더없이 상냥하고 더없이 공손한 미소로 그것을 받아 내며 말을 이었다."]], [["“You say that as I describe them, they are perverted.", "\"제가 묘사하는 그대로라면 그것은 왜곡된 것이라고 부인께서는 말씀하십니다. 저는 그렇지 않으리라 두렵습니다—그렇지 않으리라 두렵습니다."], ["I fear not—I fear not: but let us assume that they are.", "그러나 왜곡되었다고 쳐 봅시다. 제가 한동안 이 문제로 느껴 온 불안은 이 점에서 비롯됩니다."], ["The uneasiness I have for some time felt on the subject, arises in this: that the mere circumstance of such association often repeated, on the part of Miss Florence, however innocently and confidingly, would be conclusive with Mr Dombey, already predisposed against her, and would lead him to take some step (I know he has occasionally contemplated it) of separation and alienation of her from his home.", "곧, 플로렌스 아가씨 쪽에서 그런 교제를 여러 번 거듭한 그 단순한 정황만으로도, 그것이 아무리 순진하고 거리낌 없는 것이었다 한들, 이미 그녀에게 좋지 않은 마음을 품고 계신 돔비 씨에게는 결정적인 빌미가 될 것이며, 그래서 그분이 그녀를 자기 집에서 갈라놓고 떼어 놓는 어떤 조치를(이따금 그것을 염두에 두셨다는 걸 저는 압니다) 취하시게 만들리라는 것입니다. 부인, 저를 참고 들어 주시고, 제가 돔비 씨와 맺어 온 교분과, 그분에 대한 저의 이해와, 거의 어린 시절부터의 그분에 대한 저의 경외심을 기억해 주십시오."], ["Madam, bear with me, and remember my intercourse with Mr Dombey, and my knowledge of him, and my reverence for him, almost from childhood, when I say that if he has a fault, it is a lofty stubbornness, rooted in that noble pride and sense of power which belong to him, and which we must all defer to; which is not assailable like the obstinacy of other characters; and which grows upon itself from day to day, and year to year.”", "그분께 흠이 있다면, 그것은 그분께 속한, 또 우리 모두가 따르지 않을 수 없는 그 고귀한 자존심과 권력의 감각에 뿌리내린 드높은 고집입니다. 그것은 다른 사람들의 옹고집처럼 무너뜨릴 수 있는 것이 아니며, 날마다, 해마다 제 스스로 자라나는 것입니다.\""]], [["She bent her glance upon him still; but, look as steadfast as she would, her haughty nostrils dilated, and her breath came somewhat deeper, and her lip would slightly curl, as he described that in his patron to which they must all bow down.", "그녀는 여전히 그에게 눈길을 쏟고 있었으나, 아무리 흔들림 없이 바라보려 해도, 그가 그녀가 모두 머리를 숙여야 한다고 묘사하는 그 후원자의 그것을 묘사할 때, 그녀의 거만한 콧방울이 벌름거렸고, 숨이 어딘가 깊어졌으며, 입술이 살짝 비틀렸다. 그는 그것을 보았다."], ["He saw it; and though his expression did not change, she knew he saw it.", "그리고 그의 표정은 변하지 않았지만, 그녀는 그가 보았음을 알았다."]], [["“Even so slight an incident as last night’s,”", "\"어젯밤의 일처럼 그토록 사소한 사건조차도,\""], ["he said, “if I might refer to it once more, would serve to illustrate my meaning, better than a greater one.", "그가 말했다, \"한 번 더 그것을 언급해도 된다면요, 더 큰 사건보다 오히려 제 뜻을 더 잘 보여 줄 것입니다."], ["Dombey and Son know neither time, nor place, nor season, but bear them all down.", "돔비와 아들은 때도, 장소도, 철도 가리지 않으며, 그 모두를 짓눌러 버립니다."], ["But I rejoice in its occurrence, for it has opened the way for me to approach Mrs Dombey with this subject today, even if it has entailed upon me the penalty of her temporary displeasure.", "그러나 저는 그 일이 일어난 것을 기뻐합니다. 비록 그것이 제게 부인의 일시적인 불쾌라는 벌을 안겨 주었을지언정, 오늘 제가 이 문제로 돔비 부인께 다가갈 길을 열어 주었으니까요."], ["Madam, in the midst of my uneasiness and apprehension on this subject, I was summoned by Mr Dombey to Leamington.", "부인, 이 문제에 대한 불안과 염려가 한창이던 와중에, 저는 돔비 씨의 부름을 받고 리밍턴으로 갔습니다. 거기서 저는 부인을 뵈었습니다."], ["There I saw you. There I could not help knowing what relation you would shortly occupy towards him—to his enduring happiness and yours.", "거기서 저는 부인께서 머지않아 그분과 어떤 관계를 맺으시게 될지—그분과 부인의 변함없는 행복에 어떤 관계를 맺으시게 될지 알지 않을 수 없었습니다."], ["There I resolved to await the time of your establishment at home here, and to do as I have now done.", "거기서 저는 부인께서 이곳 가정에 자리 잡으실 때를 기다렸다가, 지금 제가 한 대로 하기로 결심했습니다."], ["I have, at heart, no fear that I shall be wanting in my duty to Mr Dombey, if I bury what I know in your breast; for where there is but one heart and mind between two persons—as in such a marriage—one almost represents the other.", "제가 알고 있는 바를 부인의 가슴속에 묻어 둔다 해도, 돔비 씨에 대한 제 의무를 저버리는 것은 아니라고 저는 진심으로 두려워하지 않습니다. 왜냐하면 두 사람 사이에 단 하나의 마음과 정신만이 있는 곳에서는—이런 결혼이 그러하듯이—한쪽이 다른 쪽을 거의 대신하기 때문입니다."], ["I can acquit my conscience therefore, almost equally, by confidence, on such a theme, in you or him.", "그러므로 이런 주제에서 부인을 신뢰하든 그분을 신뢰하든, 저는 거의 똑같이 제 양심에 떳떳할 수 있습니다."], ["For the reasons I have mentioned I would select you.", "앞서 말씀드린 이유로 저는 부인을 택하겠습니다."], ["May I aspire to the distinction of believing that my confidence is accepted, and that I am relieved from my responsibility?”", "제 신뢰가 받아들여졌고, 제가 책임에서 벗어났다고 믿어도 되는 그 영광을 감히 바라도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["He long remembered the look she gave him—who could see it, and forget it?—and the struggle that ensued within her.", "그는 그녀가 그에게 보낸 그 눈빛을—누가 그것을 보고 잊을 수 있겠는가?—그리고 그녀 안에서 뒤이은 그 갈등을 오래도록 기억했다."], ["At last she said:", "마침내 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I accept it, Sir You will please to consider this matter at an end, and that it goes no farther.”", "\"받아들이겠어요, 선생. 이 일은 이것으로 끝난 것으로, 그리고 더는 나아가지 않는 것으로 여겨 주세요.\""]], [["He bowed low, and rose.", "그는 깊이 절하고 일어섰다."], ["She rose too, and he took leave with all humility.", "그녀도 일어섰고, 그는 더없이 겸손하게 작별했다."], ["But Withers, meeting him on the stairs, stood amazed at the beauty of his teeth, and at his brilliant smile; and as he rode away upon his white-legged horse, the people took him for a dentist, such was the dazzling show he made.", "그러나 계단에서 그와 마주친 위더스는 그의 이의 아름다움과 그 눈부신 미소에 놀라 멍해졌고, 그가 다리가 흰 말을 타고 떠나갈 때, 사람들은 그를 치과 의사로 여겼으니, 그가 보이는 광경이 그토록 눈부셨던 것이다."], ["The people took her, when she rode out in her carriage presently, for a great lady, as happy as she was rich and fine.", "잠시 후 그녀가 마차를 타고 나갔을 때, 사람들은 그녀를 부유하고 고운 만큼이나 행복한 귀부인으로 여겼다."], ["But they had not seen her, just before, in her own room with no one by; and they had not heard her utterance of the three words, “Oh Florence, Florence!”", "그러나 그들은 바로 그 직전 아무도 곁에 없는 자기 방에서의 그녀를 보지 못했고, 그녀가 내뱉은 세 마디, \"오 플로렌스, 플로렌스!\"를 듣지 못했다."]], [["Mrs Skewton, reposing on her sofa, and sipping her chocolate, had heard nothing but the low word business, for which she had a mortal aversion, insomuch that she had long banished it from her vocabulary, and had gone nigh, in a charming manner and with an immense amount of heart, to say nothing of soul, to ruin divers milliners and others in consequence.", "소파에 기대어 초콜릿을 홀짝이던 스큐턴 부인은 사업이라는 그 낮은 말 한마디 말고는 아무것도 듣지 못했는데, 그녀는 그 말을 끔찍이도 싫어하여 진작에 자기 어휘에서 추방해 버린 터라, 그 결과로—매력적인 태도와 엄청난 마음, 영혼은 말할 것도 없거니와—여러 모자 가게 주인이며 그 밖의 사람들을 망하게 만들 뻔하기까지 했다."], ["Therefore Mrs Skewton asked no questions, and showed no curiosity.", "그러므로 스큐턴 부인은 아무것도 묻지 않았고, 아무런 호기심도 보이지 않았다."], ["Indeed, the peach-velvet bonnet gave her sufficient occupation out of doors; for being perched on the back of her head, and the day being rather windy, it was frantic to escape from Mrs Skewton’s company, and would be coaxed into no sort of compromise.", "사실, 그 복숭아색 벨벳 보닛만으로도 그녀는 바깥에서 충분히 분주했으니, 그것이 머리 뒤쪽에 얹혀 있는 데다 그날따라 바람이 제법 부는 통에, 그것은 스큐턴 부인의 곁을 떠나려고 미친 듯이 발버둥 쳤고, 어떤 식의 타협에도 도무지 구슬려지지 않으려 했다."], ["When the carriage was closed, and the wind shut out, the palsy played among the artificial roses again like an almshouse-full of superannuated zephyrs; and altogether Mrs Skewton had enough to do, and got on but indifferently.", "마차가 닫히고 바람이 차단되자, 그 마비된 떨림이 다시 조화 장미 사이에서 노쇠한 미풍들로 가득 찬 양로원처럼 희롱했고, 도무지 스큐턴 부인은 할 일이 많았으나 그저 그럭저럭 해 나갈 뿐이었다."]], [["She got on no better towards night; for when Mrs Dombey, in her dressing-room, had been dressed and waiting for her half an hour, and Mr Dombey, in the drawing-room, had paraded himself into a state of solemn fretfulness (they were all three going out to dinner), Flowers the Maid appeared with a pale face to Mrs Dombey, saying:", "저녁이 되어 가도 그녀는 별로 나아지지 않았으니, 돔비 부인이 화장실에서 단장을 마치고 그녀를 반 시간이나 기다리고, 돔비 씨가 응접실에서 엄숙한 짜증의 상태로 거닐며 시간을 보내고 있을 때(세 사람 모두 만찬에 나가려던 참이었다), 하녀 플라워스가 창백한 얼굴로 돔비 부인 앞에 나타나 말했다."]], [["“If you please, Ma’am, I beg your pardon, but I can’t do nothing with Missis!”", "\"황송하옵니다, 마님, 죄송하지만, 큰 마님을 도무지 어찌할 수가 없어요!\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 말이냐?\""], ["asked Edith.", "이디스가 물었다."]], [["“Well, Ma’am,”", "\"저어, 마님,\""], ["replied the frightened maid, “I hardly know.", "겁먹은 하녀가 응수했다, \"저도 잘 모르겠어요."], ["She’s making faces!”", "자꾸 얼굴을 일그러뜨리세요!\""]], [["Edith hurried with her to her mother’s room.", "이디스는 하녀와 함께 어머니의 방으로 서둘러 갔다."], ["Cleopatra was arrayed in full dress, with the diamonds, short sleeves, rouge, curls, teeth, and other juvenility all complete; but Paralysis was not to be deceived, had known her for the object of its errand, and had struck her at her glass, where she lay like a horrible doll that had tumbled down.", "클레오파트라는 정장을 갖추어 입고, 다이아몬드며 짧은 소매며 연지며 곱슬머리며 이며 그 밖의 온갖 젊은이다움까지 완벽하게 차린 채였다. 그러나 중풍은 속아 넘어가지 않았으니, 그녀가 제 임무의 대상임을 진작에 알아보고는 거울 앞에서 그녀를 후려쳤고, 그녀는 그 앞에서 굴러떨어진 끔찍한 인형처럼 쓰러져 있었다."]], [["They took her to pieces in very shame, and put the little of her that was real on a bed.", "그들은 수치심 속에서 그녀를 한 조각씩 해체하여, 그녀에게서 진짜인 그 자그마한 부분을 침대에 뉘었다. 의사들이 호출되어 곧 왔다."], ["Doctors were sent for, and soon came.", "강력한 치료제가 동원되었고, 그녀가 이번 충격에서는 회복하겠으나 또 한 번은 견디지 못하리라는 소견이 나왔다."], ["Powerful remedies were resorted to; opinions given that she would rally from this shock, but would not survive another; and there she lay speechless, and staring at the ceiling, for days; sometimes making inarticulate sounds in answer to such questions as did she know who were present, and the like: sometimes giving no reply either by sign or gesture, or in her unwinking eyes.", "그렇게 그녀는 며칠 동안 말도 못 한 채 천장을 빤히 응시하며 누워 있었다. 때로는 여기 누가 와 있는지 아느냐는 따위의 물음에 또렷하지 않은 소리를 내어 답했고, 때로는 신호로도 몸짓으로도, 깜박이지 않는 눈으로도 아무 대답을 하지 않았다."]], [["At length she began to recover consciousness, and in some degree the power of motion, though not yet of speech.", "마침내 그녀는 의식을, 그리고 어느 정도 운동 능력을 되찾기 시작했으나, 아직 말하는 능력은 아니었다."], ["One day the use of her right hand returned; and showing it to her maid who was in attendance on her, and appearing very uneasy in her mind, she made signs for a pencil and some paper.", "어느 날 오른손의 쓰임이 돌아왔다. 그것을 시중들고 있던 하녀에게 내보이며, 마음속이 몹시 불안한 듯한 기색으로, 그녀는 연필과 종이를 달라고 신호했다."], ["This the maid immediately provided, thinking she was going to make a will, or write some last request; and Mrs Dombey being from home, the maid awaited the result with solemn feelings.", "하녀는 즉시 그것을 마련해 주며, 그녀가 유언장을 쓰거나 무슨 마지막 부탁을 적으려는 줄로 생각했다. 돔비 부인이 외출 중이었으므로, 하녀는 엄숙한 심정으로 그 결과를 기다렸다."]], [["After much painful scrawling and erasing, and putting in of wrong characters, which seemed to tumble out of the pencil of their own accord, the old woman produced this document:", "고통스럽게 갈겨쓰고 지우고, 제멋대로 연필에서 굴러떨어지는 듯한 엉뚱한 글자들을 써 넣은 끝에, 노부인은 이런 문서를 만들어 냈다."]], [["“Rose-coloured curtains.”", "\"장밋빛 커튼.\""]], [["The maid being perfectly transfixed, and with tolerable reason, Cleopatra amended the manuscript by adding two words more, when it stood thus:", "하녀가 완전히 얼어붙어 있자, 그것도 그럴 만한 까닭이 충분했으나, 클레오파트라는 두 단어를 더 보태어 원고를 고쳤고, 그러자 그것은 이렇게 되었다."]], [["“Rose-coloured curtains for doctors.”", "\"의사들을 위한 장밋빛 커튼.\""]], [["The maid now perceived remotely that she wished these articles to be provided for the better presentation of her complexion to the faculty; and as those in the house who knew her best, had no doubt of the correctness of this opinion, which she was soon able to establish for herself, the rose-coloured curtains were added to her bed, and she mended with increased rapidity from that hour.", "하녀는 이제 어렴풋이나마, 그녀가 의사들에게 자기 안색을 더 좋게 내보이려고 그 물건들을 마련받고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘다. 그리고 그 집에서 그녀를 가장 잘 아는 사람들도 이 의견의 정확함을 의심치 않았으니, 머지않아 그녀 스스로 그것을 입증할 수 있게 되었거니와, 장밋빛 커튼이 그녀의 침대에 더해졌고, 그녀는 그때부터 한층 빠르게 차도를 보였다."], ["She was soon able to sit up, in curls and a laced cap and nightgown, and to have a little artificial bloom dropped into the hollow caverns of her cheeks.", "그녀는 곧 곱슬머리에 레이스 달린 모자와 잠옷 차림으로 일어나 앉아, 두 뺨의 움푹 팬 동굴 속에 약간의 인공 홍조를 떨어뜨리게 할 수 있었다."]], [["It was a tremendous sight to see this old woman in her finery leering and mincing at Death, and playing off her youthful tricks upon him as if he had been the Major; but an alteration in her mind that ensued on the paralytic stroke was fraught with as much matter for reflection, and was quite as ghastly.", "이 노부인이 화려하게 차려입고 죽음을 향해 추파를 던지며 점잔을 빼고, 마치 그가 소령이라도 되는 양 그에게 자기 젊은 시절의 수작을 부려 대는 광경은 대단한 볼거리였다. 그러나 그 중풍 발작 뒤에 그녀의 정신에 뒤따른 어떤 변화는, 그에 못지않게 곱씹어 볼 거리로 가득했고, 또 그만큼이나 섬뜩했다."]], [["Whether the weakening of her intellect made her more cunning and false than before, or whether it confused her between what she had assumed to be and what she really had been, or whether it had awakened any glimmering of remorse, which could neither struggle into light nor get back into total darkness, or whether, in the jumble of her faculties, a combination of these effects had been shaken up, which is perhaps the more likely supposition, the result was this:—That she became hugely exacting in respect of Edith’s affection and gratitude and attention to her; highly laudatory of herself as a most inestimable parent; and very jealous of having any rival in Edith’s regard.", "그녀의 지력이 약해진 탓에 그녀가 전보다 더 교활하고 거짓되어진 것인지, 아니면 그녀가 자기가 그런 척해 온 모습과 자기가 정말로 그러했던 모습 사이에서 그녀를 헷갈리게 만든 것인지, 아니면 그것이, 빛 속으로 솟아나지도, 완전한 어둠 속으로 되돌아가지도 못하는 어떤 회한의 가물거림을 일깨운 것인지, 아니면 그녀의 정신 기능들이 뒤죽박죽된 가운데 이 영향들이 한데 뒤섞여 흔들린 것인지—아마 이쪽이 더 그럴듯한 추측일 텐데—그 결과는 이러했다. 곧 그녀는 이디스의 애정과 감사와 자기를 향한 관심에 대해 엄청나게 까다로워졌고, 자기 자신을 더없이 값을 매길 수 없는 어버이라고 한껏 추켜세웠으며, 이디스의 마음에 누가 경쟁자로 끼어드는 것을 몹시 시기했다."], ["Further, in place of remembering that compact made between them for an avoidance of the subject, she constantly alluded to her daughter’s marriage as a proof of her being an incomparable mother; and all this, with the weakness and peevishness of such a state, always serving for a sarcastic commentary on her levity and youthfulness.", "더 나아가, 그 주제를 피하기로 둘 사이에 맺은 그 약속을 기억하기는커녕, 그녀는 끊임없이 딸의 결혼을 자기가 비할 데 없는 어머니라는 증거로 들먹였다. 그리고 이 모든 것은, 그런 상태 특유의 약함과 까탈스러움과 더불어, 늘 그녀 자신의 경박함과 젊은 척하는 태도에 대한 풍자적인 주석 노릇을 했다."]], [["“Where is Mrs Dombey?”", "\"돔비 부인은 어디 있느냐?\""], ["she would say to her maid.", "그녀는 하녀에게 묻곤 했다."]], [["“Gone out, Ma’am.”", "\"외출하셨어요, 마님.\""]], [["“Gone out!", "\"외출했다고!"], ["Does she go out to shun her Mama, Flowers?”", "그 애가 제 어미를 피하려고 나간 게냐, 플라워스?\""]], [["“La bless you, no, Ma’am.", "\"어머나 천만에요, 마님."], ["Mrs Dombey has only gone out for a ride with Miss Florence.”", "돔비 부인께서는 그저 플로렌스 아가씨와 함께 마차 산책을 나가셨을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Miss Florence.", "\"플로렌스 아가씨라."], ["Who’s Miss Florence?", "플로렌스 아가씨가 누구냐?"], ["Don’t tell me about Miss Florence.", "나한테 플로렌스 아가씨 얘기는 하지 마라."], ["What’s Miss Florence to her, compared to me?”", "플로렌스 아가씨가 나에 비하면 그 애한테 뭐란 말이냐?\""]], [["The apposite display of the diamonds, or the peach-velvet bonnet (she sat in the bonnet to receive visitors, weeks before she could stir out of doors), or the dressing of her up in some gaud or other, usually stopped the tears that began to flow hereabouts; and she would remain in a complacent state until Edith came to see her; when, at a glance of the proud face, she would relapse again.", "다이아몬드를 적절히 내보이거나, 복숭아색 벨벳 보닛을(그녀는 바깥출입을 할 수 있게 되기 몇 주 전부터 손님을 맞으려고 그 보닛을 쓰고 앉아 있었다) 내보이거나, 그녀를 무슨 장신구로 치장해 주거나 하면, 이 대목에서 흐르기 시작한 눈물이 대개 멎었다. 그러면 그녀는 이디스가 보러 올 때까지 흐뭇한 상태로 있다가, 그 거만한 얼굴을 한 번 보는 순간 다시 도지곤 했다."]], [["“Well, I am sure, Edith!”", "\"원, 정말이지, 이디스!\""], ["she would cry, shaking her head.", "그녀는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 외치곤 했다."]], [["“What is the matter, mother?”", "\"무슨 일이세요, 어머니?\""]], [["“Matter!", "\"무슨 일이냐고!"], ["I really don’t know what is the matter.", "정말이지 무슨 일인지 나도 모르겠다."], ["The world is coming to such an artificial and ungrateful state, that I begin to think there’s no Heart—or anything of that sort—left in it, positively.", "세상이 어찌나 가식적이고 배은망덕한 꼴이 되어 가는지, 이제는 거기에 마음이라곤—혹은 그런 따위라곤—단 한 톨도 남아 있지 않은 것 같다는 생각이 들기 시작하는구나, 정말로."], ["Withers is more a child to me than you are.", "위더스가 너보다 더 내 자식 같다니까."], ["He attends to me much more than my own daughter.", "그 애가 내 친딸보다 훨씬 더 나를 살뜰히 챙겨 준단 말이다."], ["I almost wish I didn’t look so young—and all that kind of thing—and then perhaps I should be more considered.”", "차라리 내가 이렇게 젊어 보이지 않았으면—이런저런 것들도 다 없었으면—싶을 지경이야, 그러면 좀 더 대접받을지도 모르지.\""]], [["“What would you have, mother?”", "\"무엇을 바라세요, 어머니?\""]], [["“Oh, a great deal, Edith,”", "\"오, 아주 많은 것을 바란다, 이디스,\""], ["impatiently.", "짜증스럽게."]], [["“Is there anything you want that you have not?", "\"가지지 못한 것 중에 바라는 게 있으세요?"], ["It is your own fault if there be.”", "만약 있다면 그건 어머니 탓이에요.\""]], [["“My own fault!”", "\"내 탓이라고!\""], ["beginning to whimper.", "훌쩍이기 시작하며."], ["“The parent I have been to you, Edith: making you a companion from your cradle!", "\"내가 너에게 어떤 어버이였는데, 이디스. 너를 요람에서부터 동무로 삼았는데!"], ["And when you neglect me, and have no more natural affection for me than if I was a stranger—not a twentieth part of the affection that you have for Florence—but I am only your mother, and should corrupt her in a day!—you reproach me with its being my own fault.”", "그런데 네가 나를 소홀히 하고, 마치 내가 남이라도 되는 양 나한테 자연스러운 애정이라곤 조금도 없으면서—네가 플로렌스에게 품은 애정의 스무 분의 일도 안 되게 하면서—하지만 나는 그저 네 어미일 뿐이고, 그 애를 하루 만에 타락시키기라도 할 사람이지!—나더러 그게 내 탓이라고 나무라는구나.\""]], [["“Mother, mother, I reproach you with nothing.", "\"어머니, 어머니, 저는 어머니를 아무것도 나무라지 않아요."], ["Why will you always dwell on this?”", "어찌하여 늘 이 일을 곱씹으세요?\""]], [["“Isn’t it natural that I should dwell on this, when I am all affection and sensitiveness, and am wounded in the cruellest way, whenever you look at me?”", "\"내가 온통 애정과 감수성으로 이루어진 사람이고, 네가 나를 쳐다볼 때마다 가장 잔인하게 상처 입는데, 내가 이 일을 곱씹는 게 당연하지 않으냐?\""]], [["“I do not mean to wound you, mother.", "\"저는 어머니를 상처 입힐 생각이 없어요, 어머니."], ["Have you no remembrance of what has been said between us?", "우리 사이에 오갔던 말을 기억 못 하세요?"], ["Let the Past rest.”", "지난 일은 가만 묻어 두세요.\""]], [["“Yes, rest!", "\"그래, 묻어 두라지!"], ["And let gratitude to me rest; and let affection for me rest; and let me rest in my out-of-the-way room, with no society and no attention, while you find new relations to make much of, who have no earthly claim upon you!", "그리고 나에 대한 감사도 묻어 두고, 나에 대한 애정도 묻어 두라지. 그리고 너는 너한테 손톱만큼의 연고도 없는 새 친척들을 찾아내어 떠받드는 동안, 나는 동무도 없고 보살핌도 없이 외진 방구석에 처박혀 묻혀 지내라지!"], ["Good gracious, Edith, do you know what an elegant establishment you are at the head of?”", "어쩜 세상에, 이디스, 네가 어떤 우아한 가문의 안주인 자리에 앉아 있는지 알기나 하느냐?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"알아요."], ["Hush!”", "쉿!\""]], [["“And that gentlemanly creature, Dombey?", "\"그리고 그 신사다운 양반, 돔비 말이다."], ["Do you know that you are married to him, Edith, and that you have a settlement and a position, and a carriage, and I don’t know what?”", "네가 그이와 결혼했고, 너한테 재산 양도와 지위와 마차와, 또 뭔지도 모를 것들까지 다 있다는 걸 알기나 하느냐, 이디스?\""]], [["“Indeed, I know it, mother; well.”", "\"정말이지 알아요, 어머니. 잘 알아요.\""]], [["“As you would have had with that delightful good soul—what did they call him?—Granger—if he hadn’t died.", "\"그 어여쁘고 착한 양반—그 사람 이름이 뭐였더라?—그레인저—그 사람이 죽지만 않았던들 너는 그 사람한테서도 그만큼 받았을 테지."], ["And who have you to thank for all this, Edith?”", "그런데 이 모든 것을 누구한테 고마워해야 하느냐, 이디스?\""]], [["“You, mother; you.”", "\"어머니께요, 어머니. 어머니께.\""]], [["“Then put your arms round my neck, and kiss me; and show me, Edith, that you know there never was a better Mama than I have been to you.", "\"그러면 네 팔을 내 목에 두르고 입을 맞춰 다오. 그리고 보여 다오, 이디스, 내가 너에게 더할 나위 없이 좋은 어미였다는 걸 네가 안다는 걸."], ["And don’t let me become a perfect fright with teasing and wearing myself at your ingratitude, or when I’m out again in society no soul will know me, not even that hateful animal, the Major.”", "그리고 내가 너의 배은망덕에 안달하고 애태우다가 영락없는 흉물이 되지 않게 해 다오, 아니면 내가 다시 사교계에 나갔을 때 단 한 사람도 나를 못 알아볼 게다, 그 가증스러운 짐승, 소령조차도 말이야.\""]], [["But, sometimes, when Edith went nearer to her, and bending down her stately head, put her cold cheek to hers, the mother would draw back as If she were afraid of her, and would fall into a fit of trembling, and cry out that there was a wandering in her wits.", "그러나 때때로, 이디스가 그녀에게 더 가까이 다가가, 위엄 있는 고개를 숙여 자기의 차가운 뺨을 그녀의 뺨에 댈 때면, 어머니는 마치 그녀가 두렵기라도 한 듯 뒤로 물러서며, 부들부들 떠는 발작에 빠져, 정신이 오락가락한다고 외치곤 했다."], ["And sometimes she would entreat her, with humility, to sit down on the chair beside her bed, and would look at her (as she sat there brooding) with a face that even the rose-coloured curtains could not make otherwise than scared and wild.", "또 때로는 겸손하게 그녀에게 자기 침대 곁 의자에 앉아 달라고 애원하고는, (그녀가 거기 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있을 때) 장밋빛 커튼으로도 겁에 질리고 사나운 빛을 도무지 가릴 수 없는 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보곤 했다."]], [["The rose-coloured curtains blushed, in course of time, on Cleopatra’s bodily recovery, and on her dress—more juvenile than ever, to repair the ravages of illness—and on the rouge, and on the teeth, and on the curls, and on the diamonds, and the short sleeves, and the whole wardrobe of the doll that had tumbled down before the mirror.", "장밋빛 커튼은 시간이 흐르며 클레오파트라의 신체적 회복 위에서, 그리고 병이 할퀴고 간 자국을 메우려고 그 어느 때보다 젊은이다워진 그녀의 옷차림 위에서, 또 연지와 이와 곱슬머리와 다이아몬드와 짧은 소매 위에서, 그리고 거울 앞에서 굴러떨어졌던 그 인형의 옷장 전체 위에서 붉게 물들었다."], ["They blushed, too, now and then, upon an indistinctness in her speech which she turned off with a girlish giggle, and on an occasional failing in her memory, that had no rule in it, but came and went fantastically, as if in mockery of her fantastic self.", "그것은 또한 이따금, 그녀가 소녀 같은 킥킥 웃음으로 얼버무리는 어눌해진 말씨 위에서, 그리고 아무런 규칙도 없이 변덕스럽게 오락가락하는 가끔씩의 기억의 결함 위에서—마치 그녀의 변덕스러운 자아를 조롱하듯—붉게 물들었다."]], [["But they never blushed upon a change in the new manner of her thought and speech towards her daughter.", "그러나 그것은 딸을 향한 그녀의 새로워진 생각과 말투의 변화 위에서는 결코 붉게 물들지 않았다."], ["And though that daughter often came within their influence, they never blushed upon her loveliness irradiated by a smile, or softened by the light of filial love, in its stern beauty.", "그리고 그 딸이 이따금 그것의 영향권 안으로 들어왔어도, 그것은 미소로 환히 빛나거나 자식 된 사랑의 빛으로 부드러워진, 그 준엄한 아름다움 속 그녀의 사랑스러움 위에서는 결코 붉게 물들지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVIII. Miss Tox improves an Old Acquaintance", "# 제33장"]], [["The forlorn Miss Tox, abandoned by her friend Louisa Chick, and bereft of Mr Dombey’s countenance—for no delicate pair of wedding cards, united by a silver thread, graced the chimney-glass in Princess’s Place, or the harpsichord, or any of those little posts of display which Lucretia reserved for holiday occupation—became depressed in her spirits, and suffered much from melancholy.", "톡스 양이 옛 지인과 다시 가까워지다\n쓸쓸한 톡스 양은 친구 루이자 칙에게 버림받고, 돔비 씨의 호의마저 잃은 처지였다—은실로 이어진 우아한 결혼 청첩장 한 쌍이 프린세스 플레이스의 벽난로 거울이나 하프시코드, 혹은 루크리셔가 휴일 소일거리로 남겨 두던 그 자그마한 전시용 자리들 가운데 어느 곳도 장식하지 못했으니—기운이 가라앉고 우울함에 무척 시달렸다."], ["For a time the Bird Waltz was unheard in Princess’s Place, the plants were neglected, and dust collected on the miniature of Miss Tox’s ancestor with the powdered head and pigtail.", "한동안 프린세스 플레이스에서는 새의 왈츠가 들리지 않았고, 화초들은 방치되었으며, 분칠한 머리에 변발을 한 톡스 양 조상의 세밀화 위에는 먼지가 쌓였다."]], [["Miss Tox, however, was not of an age or of a disposition long to abandon herself to unavailing regrets.", "그러나 톡스 양은 부질없는 회한에 오래 자신을 내맡길 나이도, 그런 성정도 아니었다. 새의 왈츠가 그 비뚜름한 응접실에서 다시 지저귀며 떨림음을 낼 무렵에는, 쓰지 않아 소리 죽은 하프시코드 건반은 단 두 개뿐이었다."], ["Only two notes of the harpsichord were dumb from disuse when the Bird Waltz again warbled and trilled in the crooked drawing-room: only one slip of geranium fell a victim to imperfect nursing, before she was gardening at her green baskets again, regularly every morning; the powdered-headed ancestor had not been under a cloud for more than six weeks, when Miss Tox breathed on his benignant visage, and polished him up with a piece of wash-leather.", "그녀가 매일 아침 어김없이 초록 바구니 화단을 가꾸기 시작하기 전까지 어설픈 손질의 희생물이 된 제라늄 가지는 단 하나뿐이었다. 분칠한 머리의 조상이 먹구름 아래 놓인 지 여섯 주가 채 못 되었을 때, 톡스 양은 그 인자한 얼굴에 입김을 불어 가죽 헝겊으로 윤이 나도록 닦아 주었다."]], [["Still, Miss Tox was lonely, and at a loss.", "그래도 톡스 양은 외로웠고, 어찌할 바를 몰랐다."], ["Her attachments, however ludicrously shown, were real and strong; and she was, as she expressed it, “deeply hurt by the unmerited contumely she had met with from Louisa.”", "그녀의 애정은, 우스꽝스럽게 드러나기는 했어도, 진실하고 강했다. 그래서 그녀는 스스로 표현한 대로 \"루이자에게서 받은 부당한 모욕에 깊이 상처받았다.\""], ["But there was no such thing as anger in Miss Tox’s composition.", "그러나 톡스 양의 성품에는 분노라는 것이 없었다."], ["If she had ambled on through life, in her soft spoken way, without any opinions, she had, at least, got so far without any harsh passions.", "부드럽게 말하는 태도로, 아무런 의견도 없이 인생을 느릿느릿 걸어왔다면, 적어도 그녀는 거친 격정도 없이 여기까지 온 셈이었다."], ["The mere sight of Louisa Chick in the street one day, at a considerable distance, so overpowered her milky nature, that she was fain to seek immediate refuge in a pastrycook’s, and there, in a musty little back room usually devoted to the consumption of soups, and pervaded by an ox-tail atmosphere, relieve her feelings by weeping plentifully.", "어느 날 길에서 꽤 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에 있는 루이자 칙을 그저 본 것만으로도 그녀의 우유 같은 천성은 그토록 압도당하여, 그녀는 곧장 어느 제과점으로 피신할 수밖에 없었다. 그러고는 보통 수프를 마시는 데 쓰이는, 소꼬리 냄새가 자욱한 곰팡내 나는 자그마한 뒷방에서, 실컷 눈물을 흘리며 감정을 달랬다."]], [["Against Mr Dombey Miss Tox hardly felt that she had any reason of complaint.", "돔비 씨에 대해서는 톡스 양은 불평할 이유가 있다고는 거의 느끼지 않았다."], ["Her sense of that gentleman’s magnificence was such, that once removed from him, she felt as if her distance always had been immeasurable, and as if he had greatly condescended in tolerating her at all.", "그 신사의 위풍에 대한 그녀의 감각이 어찌나 대단했던지, 일단 그에게서 멀어지고 나니, 자기와 그의 거리는 늘 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 멀었던 것처럼, 그리고 그가 자기를 조금이라도 너그러이 봐준 것이 대단한 겸양이었던 것처럼 느껴졌다."], ["No wife could be too handsome or too stately for him, according to Miss Tox’s sincere opinion.", "톡스 양의 진심 어린 견해로는, 어떤 아내도 그에게는 지나치게 아름답거나 지나치게 위엄 있을 수 없었다."], ["It was perfectly natural that in looking for one, he should look high.", "그가 아내를 구할 때 눈을 높이 두는 것은 더없이 당연했다."], ["Miss Tox with tears laid down this proposition, and fully admitted it, twenty times a day.", "톡스 양은 눈물을 흘리며 이 명제를 내려놓았고, 하루에도 스무 번씩 그것을 온전히 받아들였다."], ["She never recalled the lofty manner in which Mr Dombey had made her subservient to his convenience and caprices, and had graciously permitted her to be one of the nurses of his little son.", "돔비 씨가 자기를 자신의 편의와 변덕에 종속시키고, 어린 아들의 유모 가운데 하나가 되도록 은혜로이 허락했던 그 거만한 태도를, 그녀는 결코 떠올리지 않았다."], ["She only thought, in her own words, “that she had passed a great many happy hours in that house, which she must ever remember with gratification, and that she could never cease to regard Mr Dombey as one of the most impressive and dignified of men.”", "그녀는 다만 자기 말로 \"그 집에서 무척 행복한 시간을 많이 보냈고, 그것을 늘 흐뭇하게 기억할 수밖에 없으며, 돔비 씨를 가장 인상 깊고 위엄 있는 사람 중 하나로 여기는 마음을 결코 거둘 수 없다\"고만 생각했다."]], [["Cut off, however, from the implacable Louisa, and being shy of the Major (whom she viewed with some distrust now), Miss Tox found it very irksome to know nothing of what was going on in Mr Dombey’s establishment.", "그러나 가차 없는 루이자에게서 단절되고, 소령에게는 (이제 그를 다소 미심쩍게 보았으므로) 수줍은 처지가 되니, 톡스 양은 돔비 씨네 집안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 전혀 알지 못하는 것이 무척 괴로웠다."], ["And as she really had got into the habit of considering Dombey and Son as the pivot on which the world in general turned, she resolved, rather than be ignorant of intelligence which so strongly interested her, to cultivate her old acquaintance, Mrs Richards, who she knew, since her last memorable appearance before Mr Dombey, was in the habit of sometimes holding communication with his servants.", "그녀는 정말로 돔비와 아들을 온 세상이 도는 중심축으로 여기는 버릇이 들어 있었으므로, 그토록 강렬하게 자신의 관심을 끄는 소식에 깜깜한 채로 있느니, 옛 지인인 리처즈 부인과 다시 가까이 지내기로 마음먹었다. 그녀는 리처즈 부인이 돔비 씨 앞에 마지막으로 나타났던 그 기억할 만한 일 이후로 때때로 그 집 하인들과 연락을 주고받는 버릇이 있음을 알고 있었다."], ["Perhaps Miss Tox, in seeking out the Toodle family, had the tender motive hidden in her breast of having somebody to whom she could talk about Mr Dombey, no matter how humble that somebody might be.", "어쩌면 툴 가족을 찾아 나선 톡스 양의 가슴속에는, 그 상대가 아무리 보잘것없을지라도 돔비 씨에 관해 이야기를 나눌 누군가가 있었으면 하는 애틋한 동기가 숨어 있었는지도 모른다."]], [["At all events, towards the Toodle habitation Miss Tox directed her steps one evening, what time Mr Toodle, cindery and swart, was refreshing himself with tea, in the bosom of his family.", "아무튼 어느 날 저녁, 톡스 양은 툴의 거처를 향해 발걸음을 옮겼는데, 그때 마침 석탄재투성이에 검게 그을린 툴 씨는 가족의 품에서 차를 마시며 원기를 회복하고 있었다."], ["Mr Toodle had only three stages of existence.", "툴 씨의 삶에는 단 세 가지 양상만 있었다."], ["He was either taking refreshment in the bosom just mentioned, or he was tearing through the country at from twenty-five to fifty miles an hour, or he was sleeping after his fatigues.", "그는 방금 말한 그 품 안에서 원기를 회복하거나, 시속 이십오 마일에서 오십 마일의 속도로 시골을 가로질러 내달리거나, 아니면 피로를 푼 뒤 잠을 자거나, 셋 중 하나였다."], ["He was always in a whirlwind or a calm, and a peaceable, contented, easy-going man Mr Toodle was in either state, who seemed to have made over all his own inheritance of fuming and fretting to the engines with which he was connected, which panted, and gasped, and chafed, and wore themselves out, in a most unsparing manner, while Mr Toodle led a mild and equable life.", "그는 늘 회오리바람 속에 있거나 고요함 속에 있었으며, 어느 상태에서든 평화롭고 만족스럽고 느긋한 사람이었다. 그는 안달하고 안절부절못하는 자기 몫의 천성을 모조리 자신이 연결된 기관차들에게 넘겨버린 듯했고, 그 기관차들은 가차 없이 헐떡이고 숨차하고 씩씩대며 자신을 닳아 없애는 동안, 정작 툴 씨는 온화하고 한결같은 삶을 살았다."]], [["“Polly, my gal,”", "\"폴리, 여보,\""], ["said Mr Toodle, with a young Toodle on each knee, and two more making tea for him, and plenty more scattered about—Mr Toodle was never out of children, but always kept a good supply on hand—“you ain’t seen our Biler lately, have you?”", "양 무릎에 어린 툴 하나씩을 앉히고, 둘은 차를 따라 주고, 나머지 여럿은 여기저기 흩어져 있는 가운데 툴 씨가 말했다—툴 씨에게는 아이가 떨어지는 법이 없었고, 늘 넉넉한 재고를 갖추고 있었다—\"우리 바일러 요새 못 봤지, 응?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"못 봤어요,\""], ["replied Polly, “but he’s almost certain to look in tonight.", "폴리가 대답했다. \"하지만 오늘 밤엔 거의 틀림없이 들를 거예요."], ["It’s his right evening, and he’s very regular.”", "오늘이 걔가 오는 저녁이고, 워낙 규칙적이니까요.\""]], [["“I suppose,”", "\"내 짐작에는,\""], ["said Mr Toodle, relishing his meal infinitely, “as our Biler is a doin’ now about as well as a boy can do, eh, Polly?”", "툴 씨가 더할 나위 없이 식사를 음미하며 말했다, \"우리 바일러가 요새 사내아이로서 할 수 있는 만큼은 잘하고 있는 모양이지, 응, 폴리?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아유!"], ["he’s a doing beautiful!”", "아주 훌륭하게 잘하고 있어요!\""], ["responded Polly.", "폴리가 대답했다."]], [["“He ain’t got to be at all secret-like—has he, Polly?”", "\"걔가 통 비밀스럽게 굴거나 그러진 않지—그렇지, 폴리?\""], ["inquired Mr Toodle.", "툴 씨가 물었다."]], [["“No!”", "\"안 그래요!\""], ["said Mrs Toodle, plumply.", "툴 부인이 딱 잘라 말했다."]], [["“I’m glad he ain’t got to be at all secret-like, Polly,”", "\"걔가 통 비밀스럽게 굴지 않는다니 다행이군, 폴리,\" 툴 씨가 느릿느릿 신중한 어조로 말하며, 마치 제 몸에 석탄을 퍼 넣기라도 하듯 접칼로 빵과 버터를 떠 넣었다."], ["observed Mr Toodle in his slow and measured way, and shovelling in his bread and butter with a clasp knife, as if he were stoking himself, “because that don’t look well; do it, Polly?”", "\"그런 건 보기에 안 좋거든. 안 그래, 폴리?\""]], [["“Why, of course it don’t, father.", "\"아, 당연히 안 좋죠, 여보."], ["How can you ask!”", "그걸 왜 물어요!\""]], [["“You see, my boys and gals,”", "\"얘들아 봐라,\""], ["said Mr Toodle, looking round upon his family, “wotever you’re up to in a honest way, it’s my opinion as you can’t do better than be open.", "툴 씨가 가족을 둘러보며 말했다, \"정직한 방식으로 무슨 일을 하든 간에, 내 생각엔 터놓고 하는 것보다 더 나은 건 없단다."], ["If you find yourselves in cuttings or in tunnels, don’t you play no secret games.", "깎아낸 길이나 터널 안에 들어서더라도, 비밀스러운 장난은 하지 마라."], ["Keep your whistles going, and let’s know where you are.”", "기적을 계속 울리고, 너희가 어디 있는지 알리란 말이다.\""]], [["The rising Toodles set up a shrill murmur, expressive of their resolution to profit by the paternal advice.", "떠오르는 새싹 같은 툴 아이들은, 아버지의 충고를 따르겠다는 결의를 드러내며 날카로운 웅얼거림을 일으켰다."]], [["“But what makes you say this along of Rob, father?”", "\"한데 어째서 그걸 롭한테다 대고 하는 말씀이세요, 여보?\""], ["asked his wife, anxiously.", "아내가 걱정스레 물었다."]], [["“Polly, old “ooman,”", "\"폴리, 여보,\" 툴 씨가 말했다, \"내가 딱히 롭을 두고 한 말인지는 나도 잘 모르겠어, 정말로."], ["said Mr Toodle, “I don’t know as I said it partickler along o’ Rob, I’m sure.", "난 그저 롭에서 가볍게 출발했을 뿐이야. 그러다 분기점에 다다르지."], ["I starts light with Rob only; I comes to a branch; I takes on what I finds there; and a whole train of ideas gets coupled on to him, afore I knows where I am, or where they comes from.", "거기서 마주친 걸 싣고 가는 거야. 그러다 보면 내가 어디 있는지, 그것들이 어디서 왔는지 알기도 전에 온 한 줄의 생각 기차가 거기에 연결돼 버린다고."], ["What a Junction a man’s thoughts is,”", "사람 생각이란 게 참 대단한 환승역이지,\""], ["said Mr Toodle, “to-be-sure!”", "툴 씨가 말했다, \"정말이지!\""]], [["This profound reflection Mr Toodle washed down with a pint mug of tea, and proceeded to solidify with a great weight of bread and butter; charging his young daughters meanwhile, to keep plenty of hot water in the pot, as he was uncommon dry, and should take the indefinite quantity of “a sight of mugs,”", "이 심오한 성찰을 툴 씨는 차 한 파인트 잔으로 씻어 내리고는, 묵직한 빵과 버터로 단단히 다졌다."], ["before his thirst was appeased.", "그러는 한편 어린 딸들에게는 주전자에 뜨거운 물을 넉넉히 채워 두라고 일렀으니, 자기가 유난히 목이 말라서 갈증이 풀리기 전까지 \"잔이 한 무더기\"라는 막연한 양을 들이켤 참이었기 때문이다."]], [["In satisfying himself, however, Mr Toodle was not regardless of the younger branches about him, who, although they had made their own evening repast, were on the look-out for irregular morsels, as possessing a relish.", "하지만 툴 씨는 제 배를 채우면서도 주변의 어린 가지들을 소홀히 하지 않았다. 그 아이들은 저녁 식사를 이미 마쳤음에도, 별미라며 정해진 분량 밖의 부스러기를 노리고 있었다."], ["These he distributed now and then to the expectant circle, by holding out great wedges of bread and butter, to be bitten at by the family in lawful succession, and by serving out small doses of tea in like manner with a spoon; which snacks had such a relish in the mouths of these young Toodles, that, after partaking of the same, they performed private dances of ecstasy among themselves, and stood on one leg apiece, and hopped, and indulged in other saltatory tokens of gladness.", "그는 이따금 그 부스러기를 기다리는 무리에게 나눠 주었는데, 커다란 빵과 버터 덩이를 내밀어 정당한 순서대로 가족이 한 입씩 베어 물게 하고, 마찬가지로 숟가락으로 차를 조금씩 떠 주었다. 이 간식들이 어린 툴들의 입에 어찌나 맛있던지, 그것을 맛본 뒤에는 자기들끼리 환희의 사사로운 춤을 추었고, 저마다 한 발로 서서 깡충깡충 뛰며 그 밖에도 갖가지 도약하는 기쁨의 표시를 마음껏 드러냈다."], ["These vents for their excitement found, they gradually closed about Mr Toodle again, and eyed him hard as he got through more bread and butter and tea; affecting, however, to have no further expectations of their own in reference to those viands, but to be conversing on foreign subjects, and whispering confidentially.", "이렇게 흥분을 발산할 통로를 찾고 나자, 그들은 차츰 다시 툴 씨 주위로 모여들어, 그가 빵과 버터와 차를 더 해치우는 모습을 뚫어지게 지켜보았다. 하지만 그 음식들에 대해 저희 나름의 더 이상의 기대는 없는 척, 다른 화제로 이야기를 나누며 은밀히 속삭이는 척했다."]], [["Mr Toodle, in the midst of this family group, and setting an awful example to his children in the way of appetite, was conveying the two young Toodles on his knees to Birmingham by special engine, and was contemplating the rest over a barrier of bread and butter, when Rob the Grinder, in his sou’wester hat and mourning slops, presented himself, and was received with a general rush of brothers and sisters.", "이 가족 무리 한가운데서, 식욕으로 자식들에게 무시무시한 본보기를 보이며, 툴 씨는 두 무릎에 앉힌 어린 툴 둘을 특별 기관차로 버밍엄까지 실어 나르고 있었고, 빵과 버터의 방벽 너머로 나머지를 바라보고 있었다. 그때 그라인더 롭이 남서풍 모자를 쓰고 상복 작업복 차림으로 모습을 드러냈고, 형제자매들이 우르르 몰려들어 그를 맞이했다."]], [["“Well, mother!”", "\"안녕하세요, 어머니!\""], ["said Rob, dutifully kissing her; “how are you, mother?”", "롭이 공손히 어머니에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"잘 지내셨어요, 어머니?\""]], [["“There’s my boy!”", "\"내 아들이로구나!\""], ["cried Polly, giving him a hug and a pat on the back.", "폴리가 그를 꼭 껴안고 등을 토닥이며 외쳤다. \"비밀이라뇨!"], ["“Secret! Bless you, father, not he!”", "천만에요, 여보, 우리 애는 안 그래요!\""]], [["This was intended for Mr Toodle’s private edification, but Rob the Grinder, whose withers were not unwrung, caught the words as they were spoken.", "이 말은 툴 씨에게만 들으라고 한 것이었지만, 그라인더 롭은—제 양심에 찔리는 데가 없지 않았으므로—그 말이 떨어지자마자 알아들었다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["father’s been a saying something more again me, has he?”", "아버지가 또 나에 대해 뭔가 말씀하셨다는 거예요?\""], ["cried the injured innocent.", "억울한 죄 없는 사내가 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, what a hard thing it is that when a cove has once gone a little wrong, a cove’s own father should be always a throwing it in his face behind his back!", "\"아, 한 번 좀 빗나갔다고 해서, 제 친아버지가 등 뒤에서 늘 그걸 면전에 던져대는 게 얼마나 모진 일이람!"], ["It’s enough,”", "정말이지,\""], ["cried Rob, resorting to his coat-cuff in anguish of spirit, “to make a cove go and do something, out of spite!”", "롭이 비통한 심정으로 외투 소맷자락에 의지하며 외쳤다, \"사람을 홧김에 무슨 일이라도 저지르게 만들기에 충분하다고요!\""]], [["“My poor boy!”", "\"가엾은 내 아들!\""], ["cried Polly, “father didn’t mean anything.”", "폴리가 외쳤다. \"아버지는 아무 뜻도 없이 하신 말씀이야.\""]], [["“If father didn’t mean anything,”", "\"아버지가 아무 뜻도 없으셨다면,\""], ["blubbered the injured Grinder, “why did he go and say anything, mother?", "억울한 그라인더가 엉엉 울며 말했다, \"왜 가서 무슨 말씀을 하셨던 거예요, 어머니?"], ["Nobody thinks half so bad of me as my own father does.", "친아버지만큼 저를 나쁘게 여기는 사람은 세상에 절반도 없을 거예요."], ["What a unnatural thing!", "이런 인정머리 없는 일이 어디 있어요!"], ["I wish somebody’d take and chop my head off.", "누가 와서 제 목을 싹둑 잘라 줬으면 좋겠네요."], ["Father wouldn’t mind doing it, I believe, and I’d much rather he did that than t’other.”", "아버지라면 그러는 것도 마다하지 않으실 거예요, 정말로. 차라리 그게 다른 것보단 훨씬 낫겠어요.\""]], [["At these desperate words all the young Toodles shrieked; a pathetic effect, which the Grinder improved by ironically adjuring them not to cry for him, for they ought to hate him, they ought, if they was good boys and girls; and this so touched the youngest Toodle but one, who was easily moved, that it touched him not only in his spirit but in his wind too; making him so purple that Mr Toodle in consternation carried him out to the water-butt, and would have put him under the tap, but for his being recovered by the sight of that instrument.", "이 절망적인 말에 어린 툴들이 모두 비명을 질렀다. 그 가련한 효과를 그라인더는, 자기를 위해 울지 말라고, 너희가 착한 아이들이라면 마땅히 나를 미워해야 한다고 비꼬듯 엄숙히 당부하여 더욱 키웠다. 이것이 둘째로 어린 툴—쉽게 마음이 동하는 아이였다—의 마음을 어찌나 흔들었던지, 그 아이의 영혼뿐 아니라 숨통까지 건드려, 얼굴이 새파래지는 바람에 툴 씨가 기겁하여 그를 물통으로 데려가 수도꼭지 아래에 넣으려 했으나, 그 도구를 본 순간 아이가 정신을 차린 덕에 그만두었다."]], [["Matters having reached this point, Mr Toodle explained, and the virtuous feelings of his son being thereby calmed, they shook hands, and harmony reigned again.", "사태가 이 지경에 이르자 툴 씨가 해명을 했고, 그로써 아들의 갸륵한 감정이 가라앉아, 둘은 악수를 나누었으며, 다시 화목함이 자리 잡았다."]], [["“Will you do as I do, Biler, my boy?”", "\"나처럼 좀 들겠니, 바일러야?\""], ["inquired his father, returning to his tea with new strength.", "아버지가 새로운 기운으로 차로 돌아가며 물었다."]], [["“No, thank’ee, father.", "\"아뇨, 괜찮아요, 아버지."], ["Master and I had tea together.”", "주인 어른이랑 같이 차 마셨어요.\""]], [["“And how is master, Rob?”", "\"그래, 주인 어른은 어떠시냐, 롭?\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“Well, I don’t know, mother; not much to boast on. There ain’t no bis’ness done, you see. He don’t know anything about it—the Cap’en don’t. There was a man come into the shop this very day, and says, ‘I want a so-and-so,’ he says—some hard name or another. ‘A which?’ says the Cap’en. ‘A so-and-so,’ says the man. ‘Brother,’ says the Cap’en, ‘will you take a observation round the shop.’ ‘Well,’ says the man, ‘I’ve done.’ ‘Do you see wot you want?’ says the Cap’en ‘No, I don’t,’ says the man. ‘Do you know it wen you do see it?’ says the Cap’en. ‘No, I don’t,’ says the man. ‘Why, then I tell you wot, my lad,’ says the Cap’en, ‘you’d better go back and ask wot it’s like, outside, for no more don’t I!’”", "\"글쎄요, 모르겠어요, 어머니. 그리 자랑할 게 없어요. 보다시피 장사가 안 돼요. 그분은 그런 걸 통 모르세요—선장님 말이에요. 바로 오늘 어떤 사람이 가게에 들어와서는, '이러저러한 걸 주시오' 하더라고요—무슨 어려운 이름이었어요. '뭘?' 하고 선장님이 그러셨죠. '이러저러한 거요' 하고 그 사람이 그랬고요. '여보게,' 하고 선장님이 그러시더니, '가게를 한번 둘러보겠나' 하셨어요. '예,' 하고 그 사람이 '둘러봤소' 하니까, '찾는 게 보이나?' 하고 선장님이 물으셨죠. '아니, 안 보이오' 하고 그 사람이 그러고, '보면 그게 그건 줄 알겠나?' 하고 선장님이 또 물으셨어요. '아니, 모르겠소' 하고 그 사람이 그러니까, '그럼 이보게, 자네한테 한마디 하지,' 하고 선장님이 그러셨어요, '밖에 나가서 그게 어떻게 생겼는지 물어보고 다시 오는 게 낫겠네. 나도 더는 모르니까!' 하시더라고요.\""]], [["“That ain’t the way to make money, though, is it?”", "\"그래도 그게 돈 버는 방법은 아니잖니, 그렇지?\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“Money, mother!", "\"돈요, 어머니!"], ["He’ll never make money.", "그분은 절대 돈 못 버실 거예요."], ["He has such ways as I never see.", "그렇게 별난 방식은 제 평생 본 적이 없어요."], ["He ain’t a bad master though, I’ll say that for him.", "그래도 나쁜 주인은 아니세요, 그건 제가 말씀드릴 수 있어요."], ["But that ain’t much to me, for I don’t think I shall stop with him long.”", "하지만 그게 저한테 큰 의미는 없어요, 그분 밑에 오래 있을 것 같진 않으니까요.\""]], [["“Not stop in your place, Rob!”", "\"네 자리를 떠나지는 말아라, 롭!\""], ["cried his mother; while Mr Toodle opened his eyes.", "어머니가 외쳤다. 그 사이 툴 씨는 눈을 휘둥그레 떴다."]], [["“Not in that place, p’raps,”", "\"그 자리는 아닐지도 몰라요, 어쩌면,\""], ["returned the Grinder, with a wink.", "그라인더가 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 대꾸했다."], ["“I shouldn’t wonder—friends at court you know—but never you mind, mother, just now; I’m all right, that’s all.”", "\"이상할 것도 없죠—윗자리에 친구가 있으면 말이에요—하지만 어머니, 지금은 신경 쓰지 마세요. 저 잘 지내고 있어요, 그게 전부예요.\""]], [["The indisputable proof afforded in these hints, and in the Grinder’s mysterious manner, of his not being subject to that failing which Mr Toodle had, by implication, attributed to him, might have led to a renewal of his wrongs, and of the sensation in the family, but for the opportune arrival of another visitor, who, to Polly’s great surprise, appeared at the door, smiling patronage and friendship on all there.", "이런 암시와 그라인더의 알 수 없는 태도가, 툴 씨가 넌지시 그에게 돌렸던 그 결점에 자기가 빠져 있지 않다는 반박할 수 없는 증거를 보여 주었기에, 자칫 그의 억울함과 가족의 소동이 되살아날 뻔했다. 그러나 마침맞게 또 다른 손님이 도착했는데, 폴리가 무척 놀랍게도, 그 사람은 문간에 나타나 그 자리의 모두에게 자애로운 후원과 우정의 미소를 지어 보였다."]], [["“How do you do, Mrs Richards?”", "\"안녕하세요, 리처즈 부인?\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“I have come to see you.", "\"댁을 보러 왔답니다."], ["May I come in?”", "들어가도 될까요?\""]], [["The cheery face of Mrs Richards shone with a hospitable reply, and Miss Tox, accepting the proffered chair, and gracefully recognising Mr Toodle on her way to it, untied her bonnet strings, and said that in the first place she must beg the dear children, one and all, to come and kiss her.", "리처즈 부인의 명랑한 얼굴이 환대의 대답으로 빛났고, 톡스 양은 권하는 의자를 받아들이며 그리로 가는 길에 툴 씨에게 우아하게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 보닛 끈을 풀고는, 우선 사랑스러운 아이들 모두에게, 하나도 빠짐없이 와서 자기에게 입을 맞춰 달라고 청해야겠다고 말했다."]], [["The ill-starred youngest Toodle but one, who would appear, from the frequency of his domestic troubles, to have been born under an unlucky planet, was prevented from performing his part in this general salutation by having fixed the sou’wester hat (with which he had been previously trifling) deep on his head, hind side before, and being unable to get it off again; which accident presenting to his terrified imagination a dismal picture of his passing the rest of his days in darkness, and in hopeless seclusion from his friends and family, caused him to struggle with great violence, and to utter suffocating cries.", "불운한 별 아래 태어난 듯이 보이는—집안에서 곤욕을 자주 치르는 것으로 미루어—둘째로 어린 툴은, 이 전체 인사에서 제 몫을 다하지 못했다. 그가 (앞서 만지작거리던) 남서풍 모자를 머리에 앞뒤를 거꾸로 하여 푹 눌러쓰고는 다시 벗지 못했기 때문이다."], ["Being released, his face was discovered to be very hot, and red, and damp; and Miss Tox took him on her lap, much exhausted.", "이 사고는 겁에 질린 그의 상상 속에, 남은 평생을 어둠 속에서, 그리고 친구와 가족에게서 가망 없이 격리된 채 보내야 한다는 음울한 그림을 그려 보여, 그로 하여금 격렬하게 몸부림치며 숨 막히는 비명을 지르게 만들었다. 풀려나고 보니 그의 얼굴은 무척 뜨겁고 벌겋고 축축한 것이 드러났고, 톡스 양은 몹시 기진맥진한 그 아이를 자기 무릎에 앉혔다."]], [["“You have almost forgotten me, Sir, I daresay,”", "\"저를 거의 잊으셨겠지요, 선생님, 아마도요,\""], ["said Miss Tox to Mr Toodle.", "톡스 양이 툴 씨에게 말했다."]], [["“No, Ma’am, no,”", "\"아닙니다, 부인, 아니에요,\""], ["said Toodle.", "툴이 말했다."], ["“But we’ve all on us got a little older since then.”", "\"하지만 그때 이후로 우리 모두가 조금씩 나이를 먹었지요.\""]], [["“And how do you find yourself, Sir?”", "\"그래, 어떻게 지내세요, 선생님?\""], ["inquired Miss Tox, blandly.", "톡스 양이 상냥하게 물었다."]], [["“Hearty, Ma’am, thank’ee,”", "\"정정합니다, 부인, 고맙습니다,\""], ["replied Toodle.", "툴이 대답했다."], ["“How do you find yourself, Ma’am?", "\"부인께서는 어떻게 지내십니까?"], ["Do the rheumaticks keep off pretty well, Ma’am?", "류머티즘은 그런대로 잘 안 오나요, 부인?"], ["We must all expect to grow into ’em, as we gets on.”", "우리 모두 나이가 들면 그걸 안고 살게 되리라 각오해야지요.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙습니다,\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“I have not felt any inconvenience from that disorder yet.”", "\"그 병으로 아직 불편을 느낀 적은 없답니다.\""]], [["“You’re wery fortunate, Ma’am,”", "\"참 운이 좋으십니다, 부인,\""], ["returned Mr Toodle.", "툴 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["“Many people at your time of life, Ma’am, is martyrs to it.", "\"부인 연배의 많은 분들이 그것 때문에 고생하시지요."], ["There was my mother—”", "우리 어머니도—\""], ["But catching his wife’s eye here, Mr Toodle judiciously buried the rest in another mug of tea.", "그러나 여기서 아내의 눈길을 알아챈 툴 씨는, 슬기롭게도 그 나머지 말을 차 한 잔에 묻어 버렸다."]], [["“You never mean to say, Mrs Richards,”", "\"설마, 리처즈 부인,\""], ["cried Miss Tox, looking at Rob, “that that is your—”", "톡스 양이 롭을 바라보며 외쳤다, \"저 애가 댁의—\""]], [["“Eldest, Ma’am,”", "\"맏이예요, 부인,\""], ["said Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."], ["“Yes, indeed, it is.", "\"네, 정말 그렇답니다."], ["That’s the little fellow, Ma’am, that was the innocent cause of so much.”", "저 애가, 부인, 그 많은 일의 죄 없는 발단이 된 그 꼬마예요.\""]], [["“This here, Ma’am,”", "\"이 녀석이요, 부인,\""], ["said Toodle, “is him with the short legs—and they was,”", "툴이 말했다, \"다리가 짧은 그 녀석입니다—그리고 그 다리가,\""], ["said Mr Toodle, with a touch of poetry in his tone, “unusual short for leathers—as Mr Dombey made a Grinder on.”", "툴 씨가 어조에 시적인 운치를 살짝 담아 말했다, \"가죽바지에 비해 유난히 짧았는데—돔비 씨가 그라인더로 만들어 주신 녀석이지요.\""]], [["The recollection almost overpowered Miss Tox.", "그 회상에 톡스 양은 거의 압도당했다."], ["The subject of it had a peculiar interest for her directly.", "그 회상의 대상은 그녀에게 단번에 각별한 흥미를 자아냈다."], ["She asked him to shake hands, and congratulated his mother on his frank, ingenuous face.", "그녀는 그에게 악수를 청했고, 그의 솔직하고 천진한 얼굴을 두고 어머니에게 축하의 말을 건넸다."], ["Rob, overhearing her, called up a look, to justify the eulogium, but it was hardly the right look.", "롭은 그 말을 엿듣고는 그 칭찬에 걸맞은 표정을 지어 보였으나, 그것은 도무지 적절한 표정이 아니었다."]], [["“And now, Mrs Richards,”", "\"자, 그럼, 리처즈 부인,\""], ["said Miss Tox,—“and you too, Sir,”", "톡스 양이 말했다,—\"그리고 선생님도요,\""], ["addressing Toodle—“I’ll tell you, plainly and truly, what I have come here for.", "그녀가 툴에게 말했다—\"제가 여기 무엇 때문에 왔는지를 솔직하고 진솔하게 말씀드리지요."], ["You may be aware, Mrs Richards—and, possibly, you may be aware too, Sir—that a little distance has interposed itself between me and some of my friends, and that where I used to visit a good deal, I do not visit now.”", "리처즈 부인께서는 아실 테고—혹시 선생님께서도 아실지 모르겠지만—저와 제 친구들 가운데 몇몇 사이에 약간의 거리가 생겨서, 예전에는 꽤 자주 드나들던 곳을 지금은 드나들지 않게 되었답니다.\""]], [["Polly, who, with a woman’s tact, understood this at once, expressed as much in a little look.", "여자다운 눈치로 이것을 단번에 알아들은 폴리는, 그만큼을 살짝 눈짓으로 드러냈다."], ["Mr Toodle, who had not the faintest idea of what Miss Tox was talking about, expressed that also, in a stare.", "톡스 양이 무슨 말을 하는지 도무지 짐작도 못 한 툴 씨도, 그만큼을 멍한 눈빛으로 드러냈다."]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “how our little coolness has arisen is of no moment, and does not require to be discussed.", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"우리의 자그마한 냉랭함이 어떻게 생겨났는지는 중요하지 않고, 굳이 따질 필요도 없지요."], ["It is sufficient for me to say, that I have the greatest possible respect for, and interest in, Mr Dombey;”", "제가 돔비 씨를 더할 나위 없이 존경하고, 그분께 더없는 관심을 두고 있다는 것만 말씀드리면 충분하겠어요.\""], ["Miss Tox’s voice faltered; “and everything that relates to him.”", "톡스 양의 목소리가 떨렸다. \"그리고 그분과 관련된 모든 것에 대해서도요.\""]], [["Mr Toodle, enlightened, shook his head, and said he had heerd it said, and, for his own part, he did think, as Mr Dombey was a difficult subject.", "사정을 알아차린 툴 씨는 고개를 가로저으며, 그런 말을 들은 적이 있다고, 그리고 자기로서도 돔비 씨가 까다로운 양반이라고 생각한다고 말했다."]], [["“Pray don’t say so, Sir, if you please,”", "\"제발 그렇게 말씀하지 말아 주세요, 선생님, 부탁드려요,\""], ["returned Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 대꾸했다."], ["“Let me entreat you not to say so, Sir, either now, or at any future time.", "\"지금이든 앞으로 어느 때든, 그런 말씀은 하지 말아 주시길 간청드려요."], ["Such observations cannot but be very painful to me; and to a gentleman, whose mind is constituted as, I am quite sure, yours is, can afford no permanent satisfaction.”", "그런 말씀은 제게 매우 고통스러울 수밖에 없고, 제가 확신하건대 선생님처럼 마음이 곧은 분께도 결코 두고두고 흡족한 일이 될 수 없으니까요.\""]], [["Mr Toodle, who had not entertained the least doubt of offering a remark that would be received with acquiescence, was greatly confounded.", "수긍을 얻으리라 추호도 의심치 않고 한마디 던졌던 툴 씨는, 크게 당황했다."]], [["“All that I wish to say, Mrs Richards,”", "\"제가 드리고 싶은 말씀은, 리처즈 부인,\""], ["resumed Miss Tox,—“and I address myself to you too, Sir,—is this.", "톡스 양이 다시 말을 이었다,—\"그리고 선생님께도 드리는 말씀이지만—바로 이것이에요."], ["That any intelligence of the proceedings of the family, of the welfare of the family, of the health of the family, that reaches you, will be always most acceptable to me.", "그 댁의 일이며, 그 댁의 안녕이며, 그 댁의 건강에 관한 어떤 소식이든 두 분께 들려온다면, 제게는 늘 더없이 반가우리라는 것이지요."], ["That I shall be always very glad to chat with Mrs Richards about the family, and about old time.", "리처즈 부인과 그 댁에 관해, 그리고 옛 시절에 관해 이야기를 나누는 것이 저는 늘 무척 기쁠 거예요."], ["And as Mrs Richards and I never had the least difference (though I could wish now that we had been better acquainted, but I have no one but myself to blame for that), I hope she will not object to our being very good friends now, and to my coming backwards and forwards here, when I like, without being a stranger.", "그리고 리처즈 부인과 저 사이에는 조금의 다툼도 없었으니(이제 와서는 우리가 더 가깝게 지냈더라면 하고 바라지만, 그건 저 자신 말고는 탓할 사람이 없지요), 이제 우리가 아주 좋은 친구가 되는 것을, 그리고 제가 내키는 대로 남처럼 느끼지 않고 이리로 드나드는 것을 부인께서 마다하지 않으시기를 바라요."], ["Now, I really hope, Mrs Richards,”", "자, 정말이지 바라건대, 리처즈 부인,\""], ["said Miss Tox, earnestly, “that you will take this, as I mean it, like a good-humoured creature, as you always were.”", "톡스 양이 진지하게 말했다, \"이걸 제 뜻 그대로, 늘 그러셨듯이 그 너그러운 마음으로 받아들여 주시기를요.\""]], [["Polly was gratified, and showed it.", "폴리는 흐뭇했고, 그것을 내비쳤다."], ["Mr Toodle didn’t know whether he was gratified or not, and preserved a stolid calmness.", "툴 씨는 자기가 흐뭇한지 아닌지도 모른 채, 둔중한 침착함을 유지했다."]], [["“You see, Mrs Richards,”", "\"보세요, 리처즈 부인,\""], ["said Miss Tox—“and I hope you see too, Sir—there are many little ways in which I can be slightly useful to you, if you will make no stranger of me; and in which I shall be delighted to be so.", "톡스 양이 말했다—\"그리고 선생님도 알아주셨으면 해요—저를 남처럼 여기지만 않으신다면, 제가 두 분께 자그맣게나마 도움이 될 만한 소소한 방법들이 많이 있고, 저는 기꺼이 그렇게 하고 싶답니다."], ["For instance, I can teach your children something.", "이를테면, 댁의 아이들에게 뭔가를 가르칠 수 있어요."], ["I shall bring a few little books, if you’ll allow me, and some work, and of an evening now and then, they’ll learn—dear me, they’ll learn a great deal, I trust, and be a credit to their teacher.”", "허락해 주신다면 자그마한 책 몇 권과 바느질거리를 가져와서, 저녁에 이따금씩 아이들이 배우면—세상에, 아주 많이 배우게 될 거예요, 분명히요, 그래서 가르친 선생에게 자랑거리가 될 거예요.\""]], [["Mr Toodle, who had a great respect for learning, jerked his head approvingly at his wife, and moistened his hands with dawning satisfaction.", "배움을 무척 존중하던 툴 씨는, 아내를 향해 찬성하듯 고개를 까딱였고, 어렴풋이 차오르는 만족감에 손을 비비며 침을 발랐다."]], [["“Then, not being a stranger, I shall be in nobody’s way,”", "\"그러면, 남이 아니니까, 저는 누구에게도 걸리적거리지 않을 거예요,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “and everything will go on just as if I were not here.", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"그리고 모든 일이 제가 여기 없는 것과 똑같이 굴러갈 거예요."], ["Mrs Richards will do her mending, or her ironing, or her nursing, whatever it is, without minding me: and you’ll smoke your pipe, too, if you’re so disposed, Sir, won’t you?”", "리처즈 부인은 저를 신경 쓰지 않고 깁기나 다림질이나 아이 돌보기나, 뭐든 하던 일을 하시면 되고요. 그리고 선생님도 그러고 싶으시면 파이프 담배를 피우시면 되지요, 그렇죠, 선생님?\""]], [["“Thank’ee, Mum,”", "\"고맙습니다, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Toodle.", "툴 씨가 말했다."], ["“Yes; I’ll take my bit of backer.”", "\"예, 담배 한 대 피우겠습니다.\""]], [["“Very good of you to say so, Sir,”", "\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 참 고마워요, 선생님,\""], ["rejoined Miss Tox, “and I really do assure you now, unfeignedly, that it will be a great comfort to me, and that whatever good I may be fortunate enough to do the children, you will more than pay back to me, if you’ll enter into this little bargain comfortably, and easily, and good-naturedly, without another word about it.”", "톡스 양이 응수했다, \"그리고 이제 정말이지 진심으로 장담하건대, 그게 제게는 큰 위안이 될 거예요. 그리고 운 좋게 제가 아이들에게 무슨 좋은 일을 해 주든 간에, 두 분이 이 자그마한 약속을 편안하고 수월하고 너그럽게, 더는 한마디도 보태지 말고 받아들여 주신다면, 그것만으로도 제게 차고 넘치게 갚아 주시는 셈이에요.\""]], [["The bargain was ratified on the spot; and Miss Tox found herself so much at home already, that without delay she instituted a preliminary examination of the children all round—which Mr Toodle much admired—and booked their ages, names, and acquirements, on a piece of paper.", "그 약속은 그 자리에서 비준되었다. 그리고 톡스 양은 이미 어찌나 마음이 편안해졌던지, 지체 없이 아이들을 하나하나 둘러보며 예비 시험을 실시했고—이것을 툴 씨는 무척 감탄해 마지않았다—종이 한 장에 아이들의 나이며 이름이며 익힌 것들을 적어 넣었다."], ["This ceremony, and a little attendant gossip, prolonged the time until after their usual hour of going to bed, and detained Miss Tox at the Toodle fireside until it was too late for her to walk home alone.", "이 의식과, 그에 딸린 약간의 잡담이 시간을 끌어, 평소 잠자리에 드는 시각을 넘겼고, 톡스 양은 혼자 집까지 걸어가기엔 너무 늦은 시각이 될 때까지 툴네 난롯가에 붙들려 있었다."], ["The gallant Grinder, however, being still there, politely offered to attend her to her own door; and as it was something to Miss Tox to be seen home by a youth whom Mr Dombey had first inducted into those manly garments which are rarely mentioned by name, she very readily accepted the proposal.", "그러나 의젓한 그라인더가 아직 그 자리에 있었으므로, 그녀를 댁의 문 앞까지 모셔다 드리겠다고 공손히 제안했다. 그리고 돔비 씨가 처음으로 그 사내다운 옷—좀처럼 이름을 입에 올리지 않는 그 옷—을 입혀 준 청년에게 집까지 바래다받는 것이 톡스 양에게는 적잖이 흐뭇한 일이었으므로, 그녀는 그 제안을 선뜻 받아들였다."]], [["After shaking hands with Mr Toodle and Polly, and kissing all the children, Miss Tox left the house, therefore, with unlimited popularity, and carrying away with her so light a heart that it might have given Mrs Chick offence if that good lady could have weighed it.", "이리하여 톡스 양은 툴 씨와 폴리와 악수를 나누고 아이들에게 모두 입을 맞춘 뒤, 더할 나위 없는 인기를 누리며 그 집을 나섰고, 어찌나 가벼워진 마음을 안고 갔던지, 만일 그 어진 칙 부인이 그것을 저울질해 볼 수 있었더라면 마음이 상했을 법했다."]], [["Rob the Grinder, in his modesty, would have walked behind, but Miss Tox desired him to keep beside her, for conversational purposes; and, as she afterwards expressed it to his mother, “drew him out,”", "그라인더 롭은 겸손한 마음에 뒤따라 걷고자 했으나, 톡스 양은 이야기를 나눌 요량으로 그에게 곁에 붙어 걸으라 청했다. 그리고 그녀가 나중에 그의 어머니에게 표현한 대로, 길을 가며 그를 \"끌어내\""], ["upon the road.", "보았다."]], [["He drew out so bright, and clear, and shining, that Miss Tox was charmed with him.", "그가 어찌나 밝고 또렷하고 반짝이게 끌려 나오던지, 톡스 양은 그에게 매료되었다."], ["The more Miss Tox drew him out, the finer he came—like wire.", "톡스 양이 그를 끌어낼수록, 그는—철사처럼—더 곱게 뽑혀 나왔다."], ["There never was a better or more promising youth—a more affectionate, steady, prudent, sober, honest, meek, candid young man—than Rob drew out, that night.", "그날 밤 롭이 끌려 나온 모습보다 더 훌륭하고 더 장래가 촉망되는 청년은—더 다정하고 한결같고 신중하고 진중하고 정직하고 온순하고 솔직한 젊은이는—일찍이 없었다."]], [["“I am quite glad,”", "\"정말 기쁘네요,\""], ["said Miss Tox, arrived at her own door, “to know you.", "톡스 양이 자기 집 문 앞에 이르러 말했다, \"너를 알게 돼서."], ["I hope you’ll consider me your friend, and that you’ll come and see me as often as you like.", "나를 친구로 여겨 주었으면 좋겠고, 내키는 대로 자주 나를 보러 와 주었으면 해."], ["Do you keep a money-box?”", "너 저금통 있니?\""]], [["“Yes, Ma’am,”", "\"네, 있어요, 부인,\""], ["returned Rob; “I’m saving up, against I’ve got enough to put in the Bank, Ma’am.", "롭이 대답했다. \"은행에 넣을 만큼 충분히 모일 때까지 저축하고 있어요, 부인.\""]], [["“Very laudable indeed,”", "\"정말 기특하구나,\""], ["said Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말했다."], ["“I’m glad to hear it.", "\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘다."], ["Put this half-crown into it, if you please.”", "괜찮으면 이 반 크라운을 거기 넣으렴.\""]], [["“Oh thank you, Ma’am,”", "\"아유, 고맙습니다, 부인,\""], ["replied Rob, “but really I couldn’t think of depriving you.”", "롭이 대답했다, \"하지만 정말이지 부인 것을 빼앗을 수는 없어요.\""]], [["“I commend your independent spirit,”", "\"네 독립심을 칭찬한다,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “but it’s no deprivation, I assure you.", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"하지만 빼앗는 게 아니란다, 정말로."], ["I shall be offended if you don’t take it, as a mark of my good-will.", "내 호의의 표시로 받지 않으면 내가 섭섭할 거야."], ["Good-night, Robin.”", "잘 자거라, 로빈.\""]], [["“Good-night, Ma’am,”", "\"안녕히 주무세요, 부인,\""], ["said Rob, “and thank you!”", "롭이 말했다, \"그리고 고맙습니다!\""]], [["Who ran sniggering off to get change, and tossed it away with a pieman.", "그러고는 키득거리며 잔돈을 바꾸러 달려가, 파이 장수에게 그것을 탕진해 버렸다."], ["But they never taught honour at the Grinders’ School, where the system that prevailed was particularly strong in the engendering of hypocrisy.", "그러나 그라인더 학교에서는 명예라는 것을 가르치는 법이 없었고, 거기서 횡행하던 제도는 위선을 길러 내는 데에 유달리 효험이 있었다."], ["Insomuch, that many of the friends and masters of past Grinders said, if this were what came of education for the common people, let us have none.", "그래서 과거 그라인더 출신들의 친구와 스승들 중 다수가, 평민을 위한 교육이 이런 결과를 낳는 것이라면 차라리 그런 교육은 없는 편이 낫겠다고 말했다."], ["Some more rational said, let us have a better one.", "좀 더 이성적인 이들은, 더 나은 교육을 하자고 말했다."], ["But the governing powers of the Grinders’ Company were always ready for them, by picking out a few boys who had turned out well in spite of the system, and roundly asserting that they could have only turned out well because of it.", "그러나 그라인더 회사의 운영진은 늘 그들에게 대응할 준비가 되어 있었으니, 그 제도에도 불구하고 잘 자란 소년 몇몇을 골라내어, 그들이 잘 자란 것은 오로지 그 제도 덕분일 수밖에 없다고 딱 잘라 단언하는 식이었다."], ["Which settled the business of those objectors out of hand, and established the glory of the Grinders’ Institution.", "이것으로 그 반대자들의 문제는 단번에 처리되었고, 그라인더 학원의 영광은 확립되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIX. Further Adventures of Captain Edward Cuttle, Mariner", "# CHAPTER XXXIX. Further Adventures of Captain Edward Cuttle, Mariner"]], [["Time, sure of foot and strong of will, had so pressed onward, that the year enjoined by the old Instrument-maker, as the term during which his friend should refrain from opening the sealed packet accompanying the letter he had left for him, was now nearly expired, and Captain Cuttle began to look at it, of an evening, with feelings of mystery and uneasiness.", "발걸음이 확실하고 의지가 굳센 세월은 어찌나 부지런히 앞으로 밀고 나아갔던지, 늙은 기구 제작자가 자기 친구더러 자기가 남겨 둔 편지에 딸린 봉인된 꾸러미를 열지 말라고 당부한 기한인 일 년이 이제 거의 다 차 가고 있었고, 커틀 선장은 저녁이면 그것을 신비롭고 불안한 심정으로 바라보기 시작했다."]], [["The Captain, in his honour, would as soon have thought of opening the parcel one hour before the expiration of the term, as he would have thought of opening himself, to study his own anatomy.", "선장은, 그 명예를 걸고, 기한이 다 차기 한 시간 전에 그 꾸러미를 여느니 차라리 자기 자신을 갈라 제 해부 구조를 들여다보겠다고 생각했을 사람이었다."], ["He merely brought it out, at a certain stage of his first evening pipe, laid it on the table, and sat gazing at the outside of it, through the smoke, in silent gravity, for two or three hours at a spell.", "그는 그저 첫 저녁 파이프를 어느 단계쯤 피울 무렵에 그것을 꺼내 탁자 위에 올려놓고, 한 번에 두세 시간씩 말없이 진중하게 연기 사이로 그 겉면을 응시하며 앉아 있을 뿐이었다. 이따금 꽤 오랫동안 그렇게 그것을 바라보고 나면, 선장은 마치 그 매혹의 사정거리 밖으로 벗어나려는 듯, 자기 의자를 조금씩 점점 더 멀리 끌어당기곤 했다."], ["Sometimes, when he had contemplated it thus for a pretty long while, the Captain would hitch his chair, by degrees, farther and farther off, as if to get beyond the range of its fascination; but if this were his design, he never succeeded: for even when he was brought up by the parlour wall, the packet still attracted him; or if his eyes, in thoughtful wandering, roved to the ceiling or the fire, its image immediately followed, and posted itself conspicuously among the coals, or took up an advantageous position on the whitewash.", "그러나 그것이 그의 의도였다면, 그는 결코 성공하지 못했다. 거실 벽에 부딪혀 더는 물러설 수 없게 되었을 때조차 꾸러미는 여전히 그를 끌어당겼으며, 혹은 그의 눈이 생각에 잠겨 천장이나 난롯불로 헤매어 가면, 그것의 형상이 곧바로 따라와 석탄 사이에 눈에 띄게 자리 잡거나 회칠한 벽 위에 유리한 위치를 차지하곤 했다."]], [["In respect of Heart’s Delight, the Captain’s parental and admiration knew no change.", "마음의 기쁨(플로렌스)을 향한 선장의 아버지 같은 애정과 찬탄에는 변함이 없었다."], ["But since his last interview with Mr Carker, Captain Cuttle had come to entertain doubts whether his former intervention in behalf of that young lady and his dear boy Wal”r, had proved altogether so favourable as he could have wished, and as he at the time believed.", "그러나 카커 씨와의 마지막 면담 이후로, 커틀 선장은 그 젊은 아가씨와 사랑하는 소년 월터를 위해 전에 자기가 끼어들었던 일이 자기가 바랐던 만큼, 그리고 당시에 믿었던 만큼 과연 그렇게 호의적인 결과를 낳았던가 하는 의구심을 품게 되었다."], ["The Captain was troubled with a serious misgiving that he had done more harm than good, in short; and in his remorse and modesty he made the best atonement he could think of, by putting himself out of the way of doing any harm to anyone, and, as it were, throwing himself overboard for a dangerous person.", "한마디로 선장은 자기가 득보다 실을 더 많이 끼친 것은 아닐까 하는 심각한 걱정으로 괴로워했다. 그리하여 그는 회한과 겸허함 속에서, 누구에게도 해를 끼치는 길에서 자기 자신을 비켜 두는 것으로, 말하자면 위험한 인물인 자기 자신을 배 밖으로 내던지는 것으로, 자기가 생각해 낼 수 있는 최선의 속죄를 행했다."]], [["Self-buried, therefore, among the instruments, the Captain never went near Mr Dombey’s house, or reported himself in any way to Florence or Miss Nipper.", "그리하여 기구들 사이에 자기 자신을 파묻은 채, 선장은 돔비 씨의 집 근처에는 결코 가지 않았고, 플로렌스나 니퍼 양에게도 어떤 식으로든 소식을 전하지 않았다."], ["He even severed himself from Mr Perch, on the occasion of his next visit, by dryly informing that gentleman, that he thanked him for his company, but had cut himself adrift from all such acquaintance, as he didn’t know what magazine he mightn’t blow up, without meaning of it.", "그는 퍼치 씨가 다음번에 찾아왔을 때조차, 함께해 주어 고맙기는 하나, 자기가 무심코 어떤 화약고를 터뜨리게 될지 모르는 처지라 그런 친분 일체에서 손을 떼었노라고 무뚝뚝하게 알리며, 그와도 인연을 끊었다."], ["In this self-imposed retirement, the Captain passed whole days and weeks without interchanging a word with anyone but Rob the Grinder, whom he esteemed as a pattern of disinterested attachment and fidelity.", "이 스스로 부과한 은둔 속에서 선장은 그라인더 롭 말고는 누구와도 한마디 나누지 않은 채 며칠이고 몇 주고 보냈는데, 롭을 그는 사심 없는 애착과 충실함의 본보기로 여겼다."], ["In this retirement, the Captain, gazing at the packet of an evening, would sit smoking, and thinking of Florence and poor Walter, until they both seemed to his homely fancy to be dead, and to have passed away into eternal youth, the beautiful and innocent children of his first remembrance.", "이 은둔 속에서 선장은 저녁이면 꾸러미를 응시하면서 담배를 피우며 플로렌스와 가엾은 월터를 생각하며 앉아 있었고, 마침내 그 둘은 그의 소박한 상상 속에서 죽어 버린 것처럼, 그리고 그가 처음 기억하던 아름답고 순진한 아이들로서 영원한 젊음 속으로 떠나가 버린 것처럼 여겨지곤 했다."]], [["The Captain did not, however, in his musings, neglect his own improvement, or the mental culture of Rob the Grinder.", "그러나 선장은 그렇게 사색에 잠기는 와중에도 자기 자신의 향상이나 그라인더 롭의 정신적 도야를 게을리하지는 않았다."], ["That young man was generally required to read out of some book to the Captain, for one hour, every evening; and as the Captain implicitly believed that all books were true, he accumulated, by this means, many remarkable facts.", "그 젊은이는 대체로 매일 저녁 한 시간씩 어떤 책을 골라 선장에게 소리 내어 읽어 주어야 했다. 그리고 선장은 모든 책이 진실이라고 무조건 믿었으므로, 이런 방법으로 수많은 놀라운 사실들을 쌓아 갔다."], ["On Sunday nights, the Captain always read for himself, before going to bed, a certain Divine Sermon once delivered on a Mount; and although he was accustomed to quote the text, without book, after his own manner, he appeared to read it with as reverent an understanding of its heavenly spirit, as if he had got it all by heart in Greek, and had been able to write any number of fierce theological disquisitions on its every phrase.", "일요일 밤이면 선장은 잠자리에 들기 전에 늘 산상에서 한 번 행해진 어떤 거룩한 설교를 직접 읽었다. 그리고 비록 그는 그 본문을 책 없이 제 나름의 방식으로 인용하는 데 익숙했지만, 마치 그 모든 것을 그리스어로 통째로 외워 두고 그 모든 구절에 대해 얼마든지 격렬한 신학적 논고를 써낼 수 있는 사람이기라도 한 것처럼, 그 천상의 정신을 경건하게 이해하며 읽는 듯이 보였다."]], [["Rob the Grinder, whose reverence for the inspired writings, under the admirable system of the Grinders’ School, had been developed by a perpetual bruising of his intellectual shins against all the proper names of all the tribes of Judah, and by the monotonous repetition of hard verses, especially by way of punishment, and by the parading of him at six years old in leather breeches, three times a Sunday, very high up, in a very hot church, with a great organ buzzing against his drowsy head, like an exceedingly busy bee—Rob the Grinder made a mighty show of being edified when the Captain ceased to read, and generally yawned and nodded while the reading was in progress.", "그라인더 롭으로 말하자면, 그라인더 학교의 그 훌륭한 제도 아래서 영감받은 성서에 대한 그의 경외심이란, 유다의 모든 지파의 온갖 고유명사에 자기 지적 정강이를 끊임없이 부딪쳐 멍들이고, 특히 벌로써 어려운 구절들을 단조롭게 되풀이하고, 여섯 살 적에 가죽 반바지를 입은 채 일요일이면 세 번씩, 아주 무덥고 아주 높은 교회 좌석에 끌려가, 졸음이 쏟아지는 그의 머리에 대고 마치 더없이 바쁜 벌처럼 웅웅거리는 커다란 오르간 소리를 들으며 키워진 것이었으니—그라인더 롭은 선장이 읽기를 멈추면 크게 감화받은 듯한 시늉을 부렸으나, 읽기가 진행되는 동안에는 대체로 하품을 하고 꾸벅꾸벅 졸았다."], ["The latter fact being never so much as suspected by the good Captain.", "다만 후자의 사실은 그 선량한 선장이 의심조차 한 적이 없었다."]], [["Captain Cuttle, also, as a man of business; took to keeping books.", "커틀 선장은 또한 사업가로서 장부를 적기 시작했다."], ["In these he entered observations on the weather, and on the currents of the waggons and other vehicles: which he observed, in that quarter, to set westward in the morning and during the greater part of the day, and eastward towards the evening.", "거기에 그는 날씨에 관한 관찰과, 짐마차며 그 밖의 탈것들의 흐름에 관한 관찰을 기록해 두었는데, 그 동네에서 그것들이 아침과 낮의 대부분 동안에는 서쪽으로, 저녁 무렵에는 동쪽으로 향한다는 것을 관찰했다."], ["Two or three stragglers appearing in one week, who “spoke him”—so the Captain entered it—on the subject of spectacles, and who, without positively purchasing, said they would look in again, the Captain decided that the business was improving, and made an entry in the day-book to that effect: the wind then blowing (which he first recorded) pretty fresh, west and by north; having changed in the night.", "한 주에 두세 사람의 길손이 나타나 안경에 관해 \"그에게 말을 걸었고\"—선장은 그렇게 적었다—확실히 사기로 정한 것은 아니었으나 다시 들러 보겠노라고 했기에, 선장은 사업이 나아지고 있다고 판단하고 일지에 그런 취지의 기록을 남겼다. 그때 바람은 (그가 제일 먼저 기록한 바) 꽤 신선하게, 서북서로 불고 있었으며, 밤사이에 바뀐 것이었다."]], [["One of the Captain’s chief difficulties was Mr Toots, who called frequently, and who without saying much seemed to have an idea that the little back parlour was an eligible room to chuckle in, as he would sit and avail himself of its accommodations in that regard by the half-hour together, without at all advancing in intimacy with the Captain.", "선장의 주된 골칫거리 가운데 하나는 투츠 씨였는데, 그는 자주 찾아왔고, 별말은 없으면서도 그 작은 안쪽 거실이 낄낄대기에 적합한 방이라는 생각을 품고 있는 듯, 선장과의 친분은 조금도 진전시키지 못한 채, 그 점에서 그 방의 편의를 누리며 한 번에 삼십 분씩 죽치고 앉아 있곤 했다."], ["The Captain, rendered cautious by his late experience, was unable quite to satisfy his mind whether Mr Toots was the mild subject he appeared to be, or was a profoundly artful and dissimulating hypocrite.", "최근의 경험으로 신중해진 선장은, 투츠 씨가 겉으로 보이는 대로 온순한 위인인지, 아니면 깊이 교활하게 본심을 감추는 위선자인지를 도무지 마음으로 가늠할 수가 없었다."], ["His frequent reference to Miss Dombey was suspicious; but the Captain had a secret kindness for Mr Toots’s apparent reliance on him, and forbore to decide against him for the present; merely eyeing him, with a sagacity not to be described, whenever he approached the subject that was nearest to his heart.", "그가 돔비 양을 자주 입에 올리는 것은 수상쩍었으나, 선장은 투츠 씨가 겉으로 자기를 의지하는 데 대해 남모를 호의를 품고 있었기에, 당분간은 그에게 불리하게 단정하기를 삼갔다. 그저 그가 자기 마음에 가장 가까운 그 화제에 다가설 때마다, 이루 형언할 수 없는 명민함으로 그를 곁눈질할 뿐이었다."]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["blurted out Mr Toots, one day all at once, as his manner was, “do you think you could think favourably of that proposition of mine, and give me the pleasure of your acquaintance?”", "투츠 씨가 어느 날 늘 그러듯 느닷없이 불쑥 내뱉었다, \"제 그 제안을 호의적으로 생각해 주셔서, 저에게 선장님과 친분을 맺는 기쁨을 베풀어 주실 수 있겠는지요?\""]], [["“Why, I tell you what it is, my lad,”", "\"음, 이보게, 내 사정이 이러하네,\""], ["replied the Captain, who had at length concluded on a course of action; “I’ve been turning that there, over.”", "마침내 행동 방침을 정한 선장이 대답했다, \"내가 그 문제를 곰곰이 뒤집어 생각해 봤네.\""]], [["“Captain Gills, it’s very kind of you,”", "\"길스 선장님, 정말 친절하십니다,\""], ["retorted Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 응수했다."], ["“I’m much obliged to you.", "\"대단히 감사합니다."], ["Upon my word and honour, Captain Gills, it would be a charity to give me the pleasure of your acquaintance.", "맹세코 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데, 길스 선장님, 저에게 선장님과 친분을 맺는 기쁨을 베풀어 주시는 것은 자선 행위나 다름없을 것입니다."], ["It really would.”", "정말 그렇고말고요.\""]], [["“You see, brother,”", "\"이보게, 형제,\""], ["argued the Captain slowly, “I don’t know you.”", "선장이 천천히 따져 말했다, \"나는 자네를 모른다네.\""]], [["“But you never can know me, Captain Gills,”", "\"하지만 선장님, 선장님과 친분을 맺는 기쁨을 저에게 베풀어 주시지 않는다면,\""], ["replied Mr Toots, steadfast to his point, “if you don’t give me the pleasure of your acquaintance.”", "투츠 씨가 제 요지를 굳건히 지키며 대답했다, \"저를 결코 아실 수가 없잖습니까.\""]], [["The Captain seemed struck by the originality and power of this remark, and looked at Mr Toots as if he thought there was a great deal more in him than he had expected.", "선장은 이 말의 독창성과 위력에 충격을 받은 듯, 자기가 기대했던 것보다 그 안에 훨씬 더 많은 것이 들어 있다고 생각하는 양 투츠 씨를 바라보았다."]], [["“Well said, my lad,”", "\"옳은 말일세, 이보게,\""], ["observed the Captain, nodding his head thoughtfully; “and true.", "선장이 생각에 잠겨 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다, \"또 사실이고. 자, 이걸 보게."], ["Now look’ee here: You’ve made some observations to me, which gives me to understand as you admire a certain sweet creetur.", "자네가 나에게 어떤 말을 몇 마디 했는데, 그걸로 보아 자네가 어떤 사랑스러운 여인을 흠모한다는 걸 내가 알게 됐단 말일세."], ["Hey?”", "그렇지?\""]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, gesticulating violently with the hand in which he held his hat, “Admiration is not the word.", "모자를 든 손으로 격렬하게 손짓하며 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"흠모라는 말로는 부족합니다."], ["Upon my honour, you have no conception what my feelings are.", "명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데, 제 심정이 어떠한지는 선장님께서 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다."], ["If I could be dyed black, and made Miss Dombey’s slave, I should consider it a compliment.", "만약 제가 검게 물들여져 돔비 양의 노예가 될 수 있다면, 저는 그것을 영광으로 여길 것입니다."], ["If, at the sacrifice of all my property, I could get transmigrated into Miss Dombey’s dog—I—I really think I should never leave off wagging my tail.", "만약 제 전 재산을 희생해서 돔비 양의 개로 환생할 수 있다면, 저는—저는 정말이지 결코 꼬리 흔들기를 멈추지 않을 것 같습니다."], ["I should be so perfectly happy, Captain Gills!”", "너무도 완벽하게 행복할 테니까요, 길스 선장님!\""]], [["Mr Toots said it with watery eyes, and pressed his hat against his bosom with deep emotion.", "투츠 씨는 눈물 어린 눈으로 그렇게 말하며, 깊은 감정에 사로잡혀 모자를 가슴에 꼭 눌렀다."]], [["“My lad,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["returned the Captain, moved to compassion, “if you’re in arnest—”", "연민에 마음이 움직인 선장이 응수했다, \"자네가 진심이라면—\""]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["cried Mr Toots, “I’m in such a state of mind, and am so dreadfully in earnest, that if I could swear to it upon a hot piece of iron, or a live coal, or melted lead, or burning sealing-wax, Or anything of that sort, I should be glad to hurt myself, as a relief to my feelings.”", "투츠 씨가 외쳤다, \"저는 마음이 이런 상태이고, 또 너무도 끔찍이 진심이라서, 만약 시뻘겋게 단 쇳조각이나 시뻘건 숯불이나 녹은 납이나 타는 봉랍이나 그런 종류의 어떤 것에 대고 그걸 맹세할 수 있다면, 제 심정을 달래기 위해서라도 기꺼이 제 몸을 다치게 하고 싶을 정도입니다.\""], ["And Mr Toots looked hurriedly about the room, as if for some sufficiently painful means of accomplishing his dread purpose.", "그러고는 투츠 씨가 그 무서운 목적을 이룰 만큼 충분히 고통스러운 수단이라도 찾는 양, 방 안을 황급히 둘러보았다."]], [["The Captain pushed his glazed hat back upon his head, stroked his face down with his heavy hand—making his nose more mottled in the process—and planting himself before Mr Toots, and hooking him by the lapel of his coat, addressed him in these words, while Mr Toots looked up into his face, with much attention and some wonder.", "선장은 번들거리는 모자를 머리 뒤로 밀어 올리고, 묵직한 손으로 얼굴을 아래로 쓸어내리며—그 과정에서 코를 더욱 얼룩덜룩하게 만들면서—투츠 씨 앞에 떡 버티고 서서, 그의 외투 옷깃을 거머쥐고는, 다음과 같은 말로 그에게 일렀다. 투츠 씨는 자못 주의를 기울이며, 또 다소 의아한 듯, 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."]], [["“If you’re in arnest, you see, my lad,”", "\"이보게, 자네가 진심이라면 말일세,\""], ["said the Captain, “you’re a object of clemency, and clemency is the brightest jewel in the crown of a Briton’s head, for which you’ll overhaul the constitution as laid down in Rule Britannia, and, when found, that is the charter as them garden angels was a singing of, so many times over.", "선장이 말했다, \"자네는 자비를 받을 대상인데, 자비란 영국인의 머리 위 왕관에 박힌 가장 빛나는 보석이라네. 그건 자네가 '룰 브리타니아'에 정해진 헌장을 샅샅이 뒤져 보면 나오는 거고, 찾고 보면 그게 바로 그 천국의 천사들이 그토록 여러 번 거듭 노래하던 바로 그 헌장이지."], ["Stand by!", "자, 잠깐!"], ["This here proposal o’ you’rn takes me a little aback.", "자네의 그 제안이라는 게 나를 조금 당황하게 만든단 말일세."], ["And why?", "어째서냐?"], ["Because I holds my own only, you understand, in these here waters, and haven’t got no consort, and may be don’t wish for none.", "왜냐하면 알다시피 나는 이 수역에서 오직 내 한 몸만 건사할 뿐, 동행하는 배도 없고, 어쩌면 그런 걸 바라지도 않으니까."], ["Steady!", "침착하게!"], ["You hailed me first, along of a certain young lady, as you was chartered by.", "자네가 먼저 나를 불러 세운 건 자네가 위임을 받은 어떤 젊은 아가씨 일 때문이었지."], ["Now if you and me is to keep one another’s company at all, that there young creetur’s name must never be named nor referred to.", "그런데 만약 자네와 내가 조금이라도 서로 동행하는 사이가 되려면, 그 젊은 여인의 이름은 결코 입에 올려서도 안 되고 언급해서도 안 되네."], ["I don’t know what harm mayn’t have been done by naming of it too free, afore now, and thereby I brings up short.", "그 이름을 너무 함부로 입에 올린 탓에 지금까지 무슨 해가 끼쳐졌는지 내가 알 수 없는 노릇이라, 그래서 나는 거기서 딱 멈춘다네."], ["D’ye make me out pretty clear, brother?”", "내 말 알아듣겠나, 형제?\""]], [["“Well, you’ll excuse me, Captain Gills,”", "\"음, 양해해 주십시오, 길스 선장님,\""], ["replied Mr Toots, “if I don’t quite follow you sometimes.", "투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"제가 가끔 선장님 말씀을 잘 따라가지 못하더라도요."], ["But upon my word I—it’s a hard thing, Captain Gills, not to be able to mention Miss Dombey.", "하지만 정말이지 저는—돔비 양을 입에 올릴 수 없다는 건 괴로운 일입니다, 길스 선장님."], ["I really have got such a dreadful load here!”—Mr Toots pathetically touched his shirt-front with both hands—“that I feel night and day, exactly as if somebody was sitting upon me.”", "제가 정말이지 여기에 이렇게 끔찍한 짐을 지고 있어서요!\"—투츠 씨가 두 손으로 애처롭게 셔츠 앞자락을 만졌다—\"밤이고 낮이고 꼭 누가 제 위에 올라앉아 있는 것처럼 느껴진단 말입니다.\""]], [["“Them,”", "\"그게,\""], ["said the Captain, “is the terms I offer.", "선장이 말했다, \"내가 내거는 조건일세."], ["If they’re hard upon you, brother, as mayhap they are, give ’em a wide berth, sheer off, and part company cheerily!”", "그게 자네에게 가혹하다면, 형제, 아마 그럴지도 모르지만, 그건 멀찍이 피해서 방향을 돌려, 유쾌하게 갈라서면 되네!\""]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, “I hardly know how it is, but after what you told me when I came here, for the first time, I—I feel that I’d rather think about Miss Dombey in your society than talk about her in almost anybody else’s.", "투츠 씨가 응수했다, \"어찌 된 영문인지 저도 잘 모르겠습니다만, 제가 처음 여기 왔을 때 선장님이 저에게 해 주신 말씀 이후로, 저는—저는 다른 거의 누구와 함께 돔비 양에 대해 이야기하느니, 차라리 선장님과 함께 있으면서 그분을 생각하는 편이 낫겠다는 느낌이 듭니다."], ["Therefore, Captain Gills, if you’ll give me the pleasure of your acquaintance, I shall be very happy to accept it on your own conditions.", "그러니, 길스 선장님, 만약 저에게 선장님과 친분을 맺는 기쁨을 베풀어 주신다면, 저는 선장님이 내거신 조건대로 그것을 매우 기쁘게 받아들이겠습니다."], ["I wish to be honourable, Captain Gills,”", "저는 정직하고 싶습니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, holding back his extended hand for a moment, “and therefore I am obliged to say that I can not help thinking about Miss Dombey.", "내밀었던 손을 잠시 거두며 투츠 씨가 말했다, \"그래서 말씀드리지 않을 수 없는데, 저는 돔비 양을 생각하지 않을 수가 없습니다."], ["It’s impossible for me to make a promise not to think about her.”", "그분을 생각하지 않겠다고 약속하는 것은 저로서는 불가능한 일입니다.\""]], [["“My lad,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said the Captain, whose opinion of Mr Toots was much improved by this candid avowal, “a man’s thoughts is like the winds, and nobody can’t answer for ’em for certain, any length of time together.", "이 솔직한 고백으로 투츠 씨에 대한 평가가 한껏 좋아진 선장이 말했다, \"사람의 생각이란 바람과 같아서, 누구도 그걸 오랜 기간 한꺼번에 확실하게 책임질 수는 없는 법일세."], ["Is it a treaty as to words?”", "그럼 말에 관한 약조로 하지?\""]], [["“As to words, Captain Gills,”", "\"말에 관해서라면, 길스 선장님,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, “I think I can bind myself.”", "투츠 씨가 응수했다, \"제가 저 자신을 묶어 둘 수 있을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["Mr Toots gave Captain Cuttle his hand upon it, then and there; and the Captain with a pleasant and gracious show of condescension, bestowed his acquaintance upon him formally.", "투츠 씨는 즉석에서 그 자리에서 커틀 선장에게 그 약조의 표시로 손을 내밀었고, 선장은 흐뭇하고 너그럽게 겸양을 베푸는 태도로 정식으로 그에게 자기 친분을 허락했다."], ["Mr Toots seemed much relieved and gladdened by the acquisition, and chuckled rapturously during the remainder of his visit.", "투츠 씨는 그 친분을 얻은 것에 무척 마음을 놓고 기뻐하는 듯, 방문이 끝날 때까지 황홀하게 낄낄거렸다."], ["The Captain, for his part, was not ill pleased to occupy that position of patronage, and was exceedingly well satisfied by his own prudence and foresight.", "선장은 선장대로 그 후견인의 위치를 차지하게 된 것이 싫지 않았고, 자기 자신의 신중함과 선견지명에 더없이 흡족했다."]], [["But rich as Captain Cuttle was in the latter quality, he received a surprise that same evening from a no less ingenuous and simple youth, than Rob the Grinder.", "그러나 커틀 선장이 후자의 자질에 아무리 풍부했다 한들, 바로 그날 저녁 그는 그라인더 롭이라는, 그에 못지않게 순진하고 단순한 젊은이로부터 놀라움을 받았다."], ["That artless lad, drinking tea at the same table, and bending meekly over his cup and saucer, having taken sidelong observations of his master for some time, who was reading the newspaper with great difficulty, but much dignity, through his glasses, broke silence by saying—", "그 천진한 녀석은 같은 탁자에서 차를 마시며 찻잔과 받침 위로 얌전히 고개를 숙인 채, 안경 너머로 무척 힘겹게, 그러나 매우 위엄 있게 신문을 읽고 있는 자기 주인을 한동안 곁눈질로 살피더니, 이렇게 말하며 침묵을 깼다—"]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I beg your pardon, Captain, but you mayn’t be in want of any pigeons, may you, Sir?”", "죄송합니다만, 선장님, 혹시 비둘기가 좀 필요하지는 않으시겠지요, 나리?\""]], [["“No, my lad,”", "\"아니다, 이놈아,\""], ["replied the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."]], [["“Because I was wishing to dispose of mine, Captain,”", "\"제 것을 처분하고 싶어서요, 선장님,\""], ["said Rob.", "롭이 말했다."]], [["“Ay, ay?”", "\"오, 그래?\""], ["cried the Captain, lifting up his bushy eyebrows a little.", "선장이 텁수룩한 눈썹을 조금 치켜올리며 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes; I’m going, Captain, if you please,”", "\"네. 저 갑니다, 선장님, 괜찮으시다면요,\""], ["said Rob.", "롭이 말했다."]], [["“Going?", "\"가?"], ["Where are you going?”", "어디를 간단 말이냐?\""], ["asked the Captain, looking round at him over the glasses.", "선장이 안경 너머로 그를 돌아보며 물었다."]], [["“What?", "\"네?"], ["didn’t you know that I was going to leave you, Captain?”", "제가 선장님을 떠나려 한다는 걸 모르셨어요, 선장님?\""], ["asked Rob, with a sneaking smile.", "롭이 음흉한 미소를 지으며 물었다."]], [["The Captain put down the paper, took off his spectacles, and brought his eyes to bear on the deserter.", "선장은 신문을 내려놓고, 안경을 벗고는, 그 탈주병에게로 시선을 모았다."]], [["“Oh yes, Captain, I am going to give you warning.", "\"아, 네, 선장님, 저는 미리 알려드리는 겁니다."], ["I thought you’d have known that beforehand, perhaps,”", "선장님께서 진작에 아셨을 줄 알았는데요, 아마도요,\""], ["said Rob, rubbing his hands, and getting up.", "롭이 두 손을 비비며 일어서서 말했다."], ["“If you could be so good as provide yourself soon, Captain, it would be a great convenience to me.", "\"선장님, 곧 사람을 좀 구해 주시면 저로서는 큰 편의가 되겠습니다."], ["You couldn’t provide yourself by to-morrow morning, I am afraid, Captain: could you, do you think?”", "내일 아침까지는 못 구하실 것 같긴 한데요, 선장님. 어떠세요, 가능하실 것 같으세요?\""]], [["“And you’re a going to desert your colours, are you, my lad?”", "\"그래, 너는 네 깃발을 버리고 떠날 작정이구나, 이놈아?\""], ["said the Captain, after a long examination of his face.", "그의 얼굴을 한참 살핀 뒤 선장이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, it’s very hard upon a cove, Captain,”", "\"오, 이건 너무 가혹하잖아요, 선장님,\""], ["cried the tender Rob, injured and indignant in a moment, “that he can’t give lawful warning, without being frowned at in that way, and called a deserter.", "마음 여린 롭이 순식간에 상처받고 분개하여 외쳤다, \"정당하게 미리 알려드리는 것뿐인데 그렇게 인상을 쓰며 탈주병이라고 불리다니요."], ["You haven’t any right to call a poor cove names, Captain.", "선장님은 가엾은 놈을 그런 이름으로 부를 권리가 없어요, 선장님."], ["It ain’t because I’m a servant and you’re a master, that you’re to go and libel me.", "제가 하인이고 선장님이 주인이라고 해서, 가서 저를 그렇게 모함하셔도 되는 건 아니잖아요."], ["What wrong have I done?", "제가 무슨 잘못을 했는데요?"], ["Come, Captain, let me know what my crime is, will you?”", "자, 선장님, 제 죄가 뭔지 좀 알려 주시겠어요?\""]], [["The stricken Grinder wept, and put his coat-cuff in his eye.", "상심한 그라인더는 울면서 외투 소맷동으로 눈을 가렸다."]], [["“Come, Captain,”", "\"자, 선장님,\""], ["cried the injured youth, “give my crime a name!", "상처받은 젊은이가 외쳤다, \"제 죄에 이름을 붙여 보세요!"], ["What have I been and done?", "제가 뭘 어쨌다는 거예요?"], ["Have I stolen any of the property?", "제가 무슨 재산이라도 훔쳤나요?"], ["have I set the house a-fire?", "집에 불이라도 질렀나요?"], ["If I have, why don’t you give me in charge, and try it?", "그랬다면 어째서 저를 고발해서 재판에 부치지 않으세요?"], ["But to take away the character of a lad that’s been a good servant to you, because he can’t afford to stand in his own light for your good, what a injury it is, and what a bad return for faithful service!", "하지만 가엾은 놈이, 선장님 좋으라고 제 앞길을 막을 형편이 못 된다는 이유로, 선장님께 좋은 하인 노릇을 해 온 놈의 인격을 빼앗으시다니, 이게 얼마나 큰 상처이며, 충실한 봉사에 대한 얼마나 못된 보답인가요!"], ["This is the way young coves is spiled and drove wrong.", "이런 식으로 젊은 놈들이 망가지고 그릇된 길로 내몰리는 거라고요."], ["I wonder at you, Captain, I do.”", "선장님이 놀라워요, 정말로요.\""]], [["All of which the Grinder howled forth in a lachrymose whine, and backing carefully towards the door.", "이 모든 것을 그라인더는 눈물 어린 칭얼대는 소리로 울부짖으며, 조심조심 문 쪽으로 물러섰다."]], [["“And so you’ve got another berth, have you, my lad?”", "\"그래서 너는 다른 일자리를 구한 거로구나, 이놈아?\""], ["said the Captain, eyeing him intently.", "선장이 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Captain, since you put it in that shape, I have got another berth,”", "\"네, 선장님, 그렇게 말씀하시니 말인데, 저는 다른 일자리를 구했어요,\" 점점 더 뒤로 물러서며 롭이 외쳤다."], ["cried Rob, backing more and more; “a better berth than I’ve got here, and one where I don’t so much as want your good word, Captain, which is fort’nate for me, after all the dirt you’ve throw’d at me, because I’m poor, and can’t afford to stand in my own light for your good.", "\"여기 일자리보다 더 나은 일자리고, 거기서는 선장님의 칭찬 같은 건 아쉬울 것도 없는 데죠, 선장님. 그게 저로서는 다행이에요."], ["Yes, I have got another berth; and if it wasn’t for leaving you unprovided, Captain, I’d go to it now, sooner than I’d take them names from you, because I’m poor, and can’t afford to stand in my own light for your good.", "제가 가난하고, 선장님 좋으라고 제 앞길을 막을 형편이 못 된다고 선장님이 저에게 온갖 진흙을 끼얹으신 마당에 말이에요. 네, 저는 다른 일자리를 구했어요."], ["Why do you reproach me for being poor, and not standing in my own light for your good, Captain?", "그리고 선장님을 사람도 없이 내버려 두는 게 마음에 걸리지만 않았다면, 선장님, 가난하다고, 또 선장님 좋으라고 제 앞길을 막을 형편이 못 된다고 그런 이름들을 들으며 견디느니, 차라리 지금 당장 거기로 가 버렸을 거예요. 어째서 제가 가난하다고, 또 선장님 좋으라고 제 앞길을 막지 않는다고 저를 나무라시는 거예요, 선장님?"], ["How can you so demean yourself?”", "어떻게 그렇게 자신을 깎아내리실 수가 있어요?\""]], [["“Look ye here, my boy,”", "\"이보게, 잘 들어,\""], ["replied the peaceful Captain.", "평화로운 선장이 대답했다."], ["“Don’t you pay out no more of them words.”", "\"그런 말들을 더는 풀어내지 말게.\""]], [["“Well, then, don’t you pay in no more of your words, Captain,”", "\"그럼 선장님도 선장님 말들을 더는 풀어 넣지 마세요,\""], ["retorted the roused innocent, getting louder in his whine, and backing into the shop.", "격앙된 순진둥이가 칭얼대는 소리를 더 높이며, 가게 안으로 물러서면서 응수했다."], ["“I’d sooner you took my blood than my character.”", "\"제 인격을 빼앗기느니 차라리 제 피를 빼앗기는 게 낫겠어요.\""]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["pursued the Captain calmly, “you have heerd, may be, of such a thing as a rope’s end.”", "선장이 침착하게 이어 말했다, \"자네도 어쩌면 밧줄 끄트머리라는 게 있다는 걸 들어 봤을 테니까.\""]], [["“Oh, have I though, Captain?”", "\"오, 제가 그런 걸 들어 봤다고요, 선장님?\""], ["cried the taunting Grinder.", "비아냥대는 그라인더가 외쳤다."], ["“No I haven’t.", "\"아뇨, 못 들어 봤는데요."], ["I never heerd of any such a article!”", "그런 물건은 들어 본 적도 없어요!\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["said the Captain, “it’s my belief as you’ll know more about it pretty soon, if you don’t keep a bright look-out.", "선장이 말했다, \"자네가 눈을 똑바로 뜨고 살피지 않으면, 머잖아 그것에 대해 더 잘 알게 되리라는 게 내 생각일세."], ["I can read your signals, my lad.", "나는 자네 신호를 읽을 줄 안다네, 이놈아."], ["You may go.”", "가도 좋아.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I may go at once, may I, Captain?”", "제가 당장 가도 된다고요, 선장님?\""], ["cried Rob, exulting in his success.", "롭이 자기의 성공에 우쭐대며 외쳤다. \"하지만 명심하세요!"], ["“But mind! I never asked to go at once, Captain.", "저는 당장 가게 해 달라고 한 적 없어요, 선장님."], ["You are not to take away my character again, because you send me off of your own accord.", "선장님이 제 발로 내쫓으신 거니까, 또다시 제 인격을 빼앗으시면 안 돼요."], ["And you’re not to stop any of my wages, Captain!”", "그리고 제 품삯을 한 푼도 떼시면 안 돼요, 선장님!\""]], [["His employer settled the last point by producing the tin canister and telling the Grinder’s money out in full upon the table.", "그의 고용주는 양철통을 꺼내 탁자 위에 그라인더의 돈을 한 푼도 빠짐없이 세어 놓는 것으로 마지막 문제를 해결했다. 롭은 훌쩍거리고 흐느끼며, 그 심정에 몹시 상처를 입은 채, 동전을 하나하나 집어 들면서 그때마다 한 번씩 흐느끼고 훌쩍거리고는, 손수건 안에 매듭을 지어 따로따로 묶었다."], ["Rob, snivelling and sobbing, and grievously wounded in his feelings, took up the pieces one by one, with a sob and a snivel for each, and tied them up separately in knots in his pockethandkerchief; then he ascended to the roof of the house and filled his hat and pockets with pigeons; then, came down to his bed under the counter and made up his bundle, snivelling and sobbing louder, as if he were cut to the heart by old associations; then he whined, “Good-night, Captain.", "그런 다음 그는 지붕 위로 올라가 모자와 주머니에 비둘기를 가득 채웠다. 그러고는 계산대 밑 자기 잠자리로 내려와, 마치 옛 정에 가슴이 미어지기라도 하는 양 한층 더 큰 소리로 훌쩍이고 흐느끼며 짐 보따리를 꾸렸다."], ["I leave you without malice!”", "그런 다음 \"안녕히 계세요, 선장님."], ["and then, going out upon the door-step, pulled the little Midshipman’s nose as a parting indignity, and went away down the street grinning triumphantly.", "저는 악의 없이 떠납니다!\" 하고 칭얼대고는, 문간으로 나가더니 작별의 모욕으로 어린 항해사 인형의 코를 잡아당기고, 거리를 따라 의기양양하게 히죽거리며 떠나갔다."]], [["The Captain, left to himself, resumed his perusal of the news as if nothing unusual or unexpected had taken place, and went reading on with the greatest assiduity.", "홀로 남은 선장은 무슨 별나거나 뜻밖의 일이 일어나기라도 했냐는 듯 다시 신문 읽기를 이어 가, 더없이 부지런히 읽어 내려갔다."], ["But never a word did Captain Cuttle understand, though he read a vast number, for Rob the Grinder was scampering up one column and down another all through the newspaper.", "그러나 커틀 선장은 수많은 단어를 읽었으되, 단 한 마디도 이해하지 못했다. 그라인더 롭이 신문의 이 단을 타고 올라갔다 저 단을 타고 내려갔다 하며 온통 휘젓고 다녔기 때문이다."]], [["It is doubtful whether the worthy Captain had ever felt himself quite abandoned until now; but now, old Sol Gills, Walter, and Heart’s Delight were lost to him indeed, and now Mr Carker deceived and jeered him cruelly. They were all represented in the false Rob, to whom he had held forth many a time on the recollections that were warm within him; he had believed in the false Rob, and had been glad to believe in him; he had made a companion of him as the last of the old ship’s company; he had taken the command of the little Midshipman with him at his right hand; he had meant to do his duty by him, and had felt almost as kindly towards the boy as if they had been shipwrecked and cast upon a desert place together. And now, that the false Rob had brought distrust, treachery, and meanness into the very parlour, which was a kind of sacred place, Captain Cuttle felt as if the parlour might have gone down next, and not surprised him much by its sinking, or given him any very great concern.", "이 훌륭한 선장이 지금까지 자신이 완전히 버림받았다고 느낀 적이 있었는지는 의심스럽다. 그러나 이제 늙은 솔 길스도, 월터도, 마음의 기쁨도 그에게서 정말로 사라졌고, 이제 카커 씨는 그를 속이고 잔인하게 조롱했다. 그것들은 모두 그 거짓된 롭 안에 대표되어 있었으니, 그는 자기 안에 따뜻이 살아 있던 추억들을 그 롭에게 여러 번 늘어놓았었다. 그는 그 거짓된 롭을 믿었고, 또 기꺼이 믿었었다. 그는 그 롭을 옛 선원들 가운데 마지막으로 남은 동료로 삼았었다. 그는 그를 오른팔로 삼아 어린 항해사 인형의 지휘를 맡았었다. 그는 그에게 자기 의무를 다하려 했고, 마치 둘이 함께 난파를 당해 어느 무인지경에 던져진 사이라도 되는 양 그 소년에게 거의 다정한 마음을 품었었다. 그런데 이제 그 거짓된 롭이 일종의 성소나 다름없던 바로 그 거실에까지 불신과 배신과 비열함을 들여놓고 보니, 커틀 선장은 그다음에는 거실 자체가 가라앉아 버린다 해도, 그것이 가라앉는다고 해서 자기가 그리 크게 놀라거나 별다른 큰 근심을 느낄 것 같지도 않았다."]], [["Therefore Captain Cuttle read the newspaper with profound attention and no comprehension, and therefore Captain Cuttle said nothing whatever about Rob to himself, or admitted to himself that he was thinking about him, or would recognise in the most distant manner that Rob had anything to do with his feeling as lonely as Robinson Crusoe.", "그리하여 커틀 선장은 깊은 주의를 기울이되 아무런 이해 없이 신문을 읽었고, 그리하여 커틀 선장은 롭에 대해 혼잣말로도 한마디도 하지 않았으며, 자기가 그를 생각하고 있음을 스스로 인정하지도 않았고, 자기가 로빈슨 크루소만큼이나 외롭게 느끼는 것에 롭이 무슨 관련이 있다는 것을 아주 멀리서나마 인정하려 들지도 않았다."]], [["In the same composed, business-like way, the Captain stepped over to Leadenhall Market in the dusk, and effected an arrangement with a private watchman on duty there, to come and put up and take down the shutters of the wooden Midshipman every night and morning.", "그와 똑같이 차분하고 사무적인 태도로, 선장은 땅거미가 질 무렵 리든홀 시장으로 건너가, 거기서 근무 중인 사설 야경꾼과 약정을 맺어, 매일 밤낮으로 와서 나무 항해사의 덧문을 올리고 내려 주도록 했다."], ["He then called in at the eating-house to diminish by one half the daily rations theretofore supplied to the Midshipman, and at the public-house to stop the traitor’s beer.", "그런 다음 그는 음식점에 들러 그때까지 항해사에게 공급되던 일일 식량을 절반으로 줄였고, 술집에 들러 그 배신자의 맥주를 끊었다. \"내 젊은 친구가요,\""], ["“My young man,” said the Captain, in explanation to the young lady at the bar, “my young man having bettered himself, Miss.”", "선장이 술집 여종업원에게 설명하며 말했다, \"내 젊은 친구가 더 나은 자리로 갔답니다, 아가씨.\""], ["Lastly, the Captain resolved to take possession of the bed under the counter, and to turn in there o’ nights instead of upstairs, as sole guardian of the property.", "끝으로 선장은 계산대 밑의 잠자리를 차지하여, 재산의 유일한 수호자로서 위층 대신 밤마다 거기서 자기로 결심했다."]], [["From this bed Captain Cuttle daily rose thenceforth, and clapped on his glazed hat at six o’clock in the morning, with the solitary air of Crusoe finishing his toilet with his goat-skin cap; and although his fears of a visitation from the savage tribe, MacStinger, were somewhat cooled, as similar apprehensions on the part of that lone mariner used to be by the lapse of a long interval without any symptoms of the cannibals, he still observed a regular routine of defensive operations, and never encountered a bonnet without previous survey from his castle of retreat.", "그날 이후로 커틀 선장은 매일 그 잠자리에서 일어나, 마치 염소 가죽 모자로 몸단장을 마무리하는 크루소처럼 고독한 모습으로 아침 여섯 시에 번들거리는 모자를 척 눌러썼다. 그리고 비록 그 야만족, 즉 맥스팅거 부족의 습격에 대한 두려움은 다소 식어 있긴 했으나—마치 그 외로운 선원이 식인종들의 기척 하나 없이 긴 시간이 흐르면서 비슷한 우려를 누그러뜨리곤 했던 것처럼—그는 여전히 규칙적인 방어 작전의 일과를 지켰고, 자기 피난처 성에서 먼저 정찰하지 않고서는 결코 모자 쓴 여인과 마주치지 않았다."], ["In the meantime (during which he received no call from Mr Toots, who wrote to say he was out of town) his own voice began to have a strange sound in his ears; and he acquired such habits of profound meditation from much polishing and stowing away of the stock, and from much sitting behind the counter reading, or looking out of window, that the red rim made on his forehead by the hard glazed hat, sometimes ached again with excess of reflection.", "그러는 동안(이 기간에 그는 시외에 나와 있다고 편지로 알려 온 투츠 씨로부터 아무런 방문도 받지 못했다) 자기 자신의 목소리가 귀에 이상하게 들리기 시작했다. 그리고 그는 재고품을 닦고 정리하는 데, 또 계산대 뒤에 앉아 책을 읽거나 창밖을 내다보는 데 어찌나 많은 시간을 들였던지 깊은 명상에 잠기는 버릇이 들어, 딱딱한 번들거리는 모자가 이마에 남긴 그 붉은 자국이 이따금 지나친 사색으로 다시 욱신거리곤 했다."]], [["The year being now expired, Captain Cuttle deemed it expedient to open the packet; but as he had always designed doing this in the presence of Rob the Grinder, who had brought it to him, and as he had an idea that it would be regular and ship-shape to open it in the presence of somebody, he was sadly put to it for want of a witness.", "이제 일 년이 다 차자, 커틀 선장은 그 꾸러미를 여는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼다. 그러나 그는 그것을 자기에게 가져다준 그라인더 롭이 보는 앞에서 늘 열 작정이었던 데다, 누군가가 보는 앞에서 여는 것이 격식에 맞고 선박답게 단정한 일이라는 생각을 품고 있었으므로, 증인이 없는 탓에 딱하게도 곤란해졌다."], ["In this difficulty, he hailed one day with unusual delight the announcement in the Shipping Intelligence of the arrival of the Cautious Clara, Captain John Bunsby, from a coasting voyage; and to that philosopher immediately dispatched a letter by post, enjoining inviolable secrecy as to his place of residence, and requesting to be favoured with an early visit, in the evening season.", "이 곤경 속에서, 그는 어느 날 선박 소식란에 존 번스비 선장이 모는 코셔스 클라라호가 연안 항해를 마치고 도착했다는 공지가 실린 것을 유난히 반가워하며 맞이했다. 그러고는 그 철학자에게 즉시 우편으로 편지를 보내, 자기 거처에 관해서는 절대적인 비밀을 지켜 달라고 당부하며, 저녁 무렵에 일찌감치 방문해 주는 호의를 베풀어 달라고 청했다."]], [["Bunsby, who was one of those sages who act upon conviction, took some days to get the conviction thoroughly into his mind, that he had received a letter to this effect.", "확신에 따라 움직이는 부류의 현자 가운데 하나인 번스비는, 자기가 그런 취지의 편지를 받았다는 확신을 머릿속에 철저히 새기는 데 며칠이 걸렸다."], ["But when he had grappled with the fact, and mastered it, he promptly sent his boy with the message, “He’s a coming tonight.”", "그러나 일단 그 사실과 씨름하여 그것을 장악하고 나자, 그는 곧바로 자기 소년을 시켜 \"오늘 밤 가신답니다\"라는 전갈을 보냈다."], ["Who being instructed to deliver those words and disappear, fulfilled his mission like a tarry spirit, charged with a mysterious warning.", "그 말을 전하고 사라지라는 지시를 받은 소년은, 마치 신비로운 경고를 띤 콜타르 묻은 유령처럼 자기 임무를 완수했다."]], [["The Captain, well pleased to receive it, made preparation of pipes and rum and water, and awaited his visitor in the back parlour.", "그것을 받고 무척 기뻐한 선장은 파이프와 럼주와 물을 마련해 놓고, 안쪽 거실에서 손님을 기다렸다."], ["At the hour of eight, a deep lowing, as of a nautical Bull, outside the shop-door, succeeded by the knocking of a stick on the panel, announced to the listening ear of Captain Cuttle, that Bunsby was alongside; whom he instantly admitted, shaggy and loose, and with his stolid mahogany visage, as usual, appearing to have no consciousness of anything before it, but to be attentively observing something that was taking place in quite another part of the world.", "여덟 시 무렵, 가게 문밖에서 바다의 황소가 우는 듯한 깊고 낮은 울음소리가 나더니, 이어 지팡이로 문짝을 두드리는 소리가 나, 커틀 선장의 귀 기울인 귀에 번스비가 곁에 와 있음을 알렸다. 그는 즉시 그를 들였는데, 번스비는 여느 때처럼 텁수룩하고 헐렁한 차림에, 그 무뚝뚝한 마호가니 빛 얼굴은 자기 앞에 있는 것은 아무것도 의식하지 못한 채, 세상의 전혀 다른 어느 구석에서 벌어지고 있는 무언가를 주의 깊게 관찰하고 있는 듯이 보였다."]], [["“Bunsby,”", "\"번스비,\""], ["said the Captain, grasping him by the hand, “what cheer, my lad, what cheer?”", "선장이 그의 손을 움켜쥐며 말했다, \"어떤가, 이 사람아, 어떤가?\""]], [["“Shipmet,”", "\"동지,\""], ["replied the voice within Bunsby, unaccompanied by any sign on the part of the Commander himself, “hearty, hearty.”", "번스비 내부의 목소리가, 사령관 본인 쪽에서는 아무런 기색도 없이 대답했다, \"정정하다네, 정정해.\""]], [["“Bunsby!”", "\"번스비!\""], ["said the Captain, rendering irrepressible homage to his genius, “here you are!", "선장이 그의 천재성에 대한 억누를 수 없는 경의를 바치며 말했다, \"자네가 왔구먼!"], ["a man as can give an opinion as is brighter than di’monds—and give me the lad with the tarry trousers as shines to me like di’monds bright, for which you’ll overhaul the Stanfell’s Budget, and when found make a note.", "다이아몬드보다도 더 빛나는 의견을 내놓을 수 있는 사람이 말이야—그러니 콜타르 묻은 바지를 입고 빛나는 다이아몬드처럼 내게 빛나는 사내를 데려오라, 그건 자네가 스탠펠 예산서를 샅샅이 뒤져 보면 나오는 거고, 찾거든 적어 두게."], ["Here you are, a man as gave an opinion in this here very place, that has come true, every letter on it,”", "자네가 왔어, 바로 이 자리에서 의견을 하나 내놓았던 사람이, 그게 한 글자 한 글자 다 들어맞았던 그 사람이.\""], ["which the Captain sincerely believed.", "선장은 그것을 진심으로 믿었다."]], [["“Ay, ay?”", "\"오, 그래?\""], ["growled Bunsby.", "번스비가 으르렁댔다."]], [["“Every letter,”", "\"한 글자 한 글자 다,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“For why?”", "\"어째서?\""], ["growled Bunsby, looking at his friend for the first time.", "번스비가 처음으로 자기 친구를 바라보며 으르렁댔다. \"어느 쪽으로?"], ["“Which way? If so, why not?", "만약 그렇다면, 왜 아니겠나?"], ["Therefore.”", "그러므로.\""], ["With these oracular words—they seemed almost to make the Captain giddy; they launched him upon such a sea of speculation and conjecture—the sage submitted to be helped off with his pilot-coat, and accompanied his friend into the back parlour, where his hand presently alighted on the rum-bottle, from which he brewed a stiff glass of grog; and presently afterwards on a pipe, which he filled, lighted, and began to smoke.", "이 신탁 같은 말들과 함께—그 말들은 선장을 거의 어지럽게 만들 지경이었고, 그를 그토록 광활한 사색과 추측의 바다로 띄워 보냈다—그 현자는 자기 도선사 외투를 벗는 것을 순순히 도움받고는, 친구를 따라 안쪽 거실로 들어갔다. 거기서 그의 손은 이내 럼주 병에 내려앉아 독한 그로그 한 잔을 빚어 냈고, 곧이어 파이프에 내려앉아 그것을 채우고 불을 붙여 피우기 시작했다."]], [["Captain Cuttle, imitating his visitor in the matter of these particulars, though the rapt and imperturbable manner of the great Commander was far above his powers, sat in the opposite corner of the fireside, observing him respectfully, and as if he waited for some encouragement or expression of curiosity on Bunsby’s part which should lead him to his own affairs.", "커틀 선장은 이 세부적인 일에서 손님을 흉내 냈으나, 그 위대한 사령관의 황홀하고 흔들림 없는 태도는 그의 능력으로는 도무지 따라갈 수 없는 경지였으므로, 난롯가 맞은편 구석에 앉아 그를 공손히 지켜보며, 마치 번스비 쪽에서 무슨 격려나 호기심의 표명이 나와 자기를 자기 용건으로 이끌어 주기를 기다리는 듯했다."], ["But as the mahogany philosopher gave no evidence of being sentient of anything but warmth and tobacco, except once, when taking his pipe from his lips to make room for his glass, he incidentally remarked with exceeding gruffness, that his name was Jack Bunsby—a declaration that presented but small opening for conversation—the Captain bespeaking his attention in a short complimentary exordium, narrated the whole history of Uncle Sol’s departure, with the change it had produced in his own life and fortunes; and concluded by placing the packet on the table.", "그러나 그 마호가니 철학자는 한 번을 빼고는 따뜻함과 담배 말고는 아무것도 느끼는 기색을 보이지 않았으니—그 한 번이란, 잔을 들이려고 입에서 파이프를 떼면서 자기 이름이 잭 번스비라고 지나가는 말처럼 지독히 퉁명스럽게 한 것이었는데, 이는 대화의 실마리를 거의 열어 주지 못하는 선언이었다—선장은 짤막한 찬사의 머리말로 그의 주의를 청한 뒤, 솔 삼촌이 떠난 전말을, 그것이 자기 자신의 삶과 처지에 일으킨 변화와 더불어 죄다 이야기했다. 그리고 그 꾸러미를 탁자 위에 올려놓는 것으로 끝을 맺었다."]], [["After a long pause, Mr Bunsby nodded his head.", "오랜 침묵 끝에, 번스비 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“Open?”", "\"열까?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["Bunsby nodded again.", "번스비가 다시 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["The Captain accordingly broke the seal, and disclosed to view two folded papers, of which he severally read the endorsements, thus: “Last Will and Testament of Solomon Gills.”", "그리하여 선장은 봉인을 뜯어, 접힌 종이 두 장을 드러냈다. 그는 그 각각의 겉면에 적힌 글을 이렇게 읽었다."], ["“Letter for Ned Cuttle.”", "\"솔로몬 길스의 마지막 유언장.\" \"네드 커틀에게 보내는 편지.\""]], [["Bunsby, with his eye on the coast of Greenland, seemed to listen for the contents.", "번스비는 그린란드 해안에 눈길을 둔 채, 그 내용에 귀 기울이는 듯이 보였다."], ["The Captain therefore hemmed to clear his throat, and read the letter aloud.", "그래서 선장은 목청을 가다듬으려 헛기침을 하고는, 편지를 소리 내어 읽었다."]], [["“‘My dear Ned Cuttle.", "\"'사랑하는 네드 커틀에게."], ["When I left home for the West Indies’—”", "내가 서인도제도로 떠나려고 집을 나섰을 때—'\""]], [["Here the Captain stopped, and looked hard at Bunsby, who looked fixedly at the coast of Greenland.", "여기서 선장은 멈추어, 번스비를 뚫어지게 바라보았는데, 번스비는 그린란드 해안을 한결같이 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“—‘in forlorn search of intelligence of my dear boy, I knew that if you were acquainted with my design, you would thwart it, or accompany me; and therefore I kept it secret.", "\"—'사랑하는 내 아이의 소식을 절망적으로 찾아 나섰을 때, 나는 만약 자네가 내 계획을 알게 된다면 그것을 막거나 나와 동행하리라는 것을 알았네. 그래서 나는 그것을 비밀에 부쳤지."], ["If you ever read this letter, Ned, I am likely to be dead.", "만약 자네가 언젠가 이 편지를 읽는다면, 네드, 나는 십중팔구 죽었을 걸세."], ["You will easily forgive an old friend’s folly then, and will feel for the restlessness and uncertainty in which he wandered away on such a wild voyage.", "그때면 자네는 옛 친구의 어리석음을 쉬이 용서해 줄 테고, 그가 그런 무모한 항해에 나서며 헤매던 그 불안과 막막함을 헤아려 주겠지."], ["So no more of that.", "그러니 그 이야기는 그만하세."], ["I have little hope that my poor boy will ever read these words, or gladden your eyes with the sight of his frank face any more.’ No, no; no more,”", "나는 가엾은 내 아이가 언젠가 이 글을 읽거나, 그 솔직한 얼굴을 다시 보여 주어 자네 눈을 기쁘게 해 주리라는 희망은 거의 없네.' 그래, 그래, 더는 없지,\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, sorrowfully meditating; “no more.", "커틀 선장이 슬프게 골똘히 생각하며 말했다. \"더는 없어."], ["There he lays, all his days—”", "거기 그가 누워 있구나, 그 모든 날들을—\""]], [["Mr Bunsby, who had a musical ear, suddenly bellowed, “In the Bays of Biscay, O!”", "음악적인 귀를 가진 번스비 씨가 갑자기 \"비스케이 만 깊은 곳에, 오!\""], ["which so affected the good Captain, as an appropriate tribute to departed worth, that he shook him by the hand in acknowledgment, and was fain to wipe his eyes.", "하고 우렁차게 외쳤는데, 이것이 떠나간 이의 가치에 바치는 적절한 헌사로서 그 선량한 선장을 어찌나 감동시켰던지, 선장은 답례로 그의 손을 흔들며 눈물을 훔치지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Well, well!”", "\"그래, 그래!\""], ["said the Captain with a sigh, as the Lament of Bunsby ceased to ring and vibrate in the skylight.", "번스비의 비가가 채광창에서 울리고 떨리기를 그치자, 선장이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“Affliction sore, long time he bore, and let us overhaul the wollume, and there find it.”", "\"쓰라린 고난을, 오랜 세월 그는 견뎠으니, 자, 그 두루마리를 샅샅이 뒤져 거기서 찾아보세.\""]], [["“Physicians,”", "\"의사들도,\""], ["observed Bunsby, “was in vain.”", "번스비가 한마디 했다, \"헛수고였다.\""]], [["“Ay, ay, to be sure,”", "\"그래, 그래, 그렇고말고,\""], ["said the Captain, “what’s the good o’ them in two or three hundred fathoms o’ water!”", "선장이 말했다, \"이삼백 길 물속에서 그들이 무슨 소용이 있겠나!\""], ["Then, returning to the letter, he read on:—“\"But if he should be by, when it is opened;’”", "그러고는 다시 편지로 돌아가 읽어 내려갔다. \"'그러나 만약 이것을 열 때 그 애가 곁에 있다면,'\""], ["the Captain involuntarily looked round, and shook his head; “‘or should know of it at any other time;’”", "선장이 자기도 모르게 주위를 둘러보고는 고개를 저었다. \"'혹은 다른 어느 때에든 이를 알게 된다면,'\""], ["the Captain shook his head again; “‘my blessing on him!", "선장이 다시 고개를 저었다. \"'그 애에게 내 축복이 있기를!"], ["In case the accompanying paper is not legally written, it matters very little, for there is no one interested but you and he, and my plain wish is, that if he is living he should have what little there may be, and if (as I fear) otherwise, that you should have it, Ned.", "동봉한 서류가 법적으로 제대로 작성되지 않았다 해도, 그것은 거의 문제가 되지 않네. 이해관계가 있는 사람은 자네와 그 애뿐이고, 내 소박한 바람은, 만약 그 애가 살아 있다면 얼마 안 되는 그것을 그 애가 갖는 것이고, 만약 (내가 두려워하듯) 그렇지 않다면 자네가 그것을 갖는 것이네, 네드."], ["You will respect my wish, I know.", "자네가 내 바람을 존중해 주리라는 걸 나는 아네."], ["God bless you for it, and for all your friendliness besides, to Solomon Gills.’ Bunsby!”", "그 일로, 또 그 밖에 자네의 온갖 우정으로 하여, 솔로몬 길스에게 하느님의 축복이 있기를.'"], ["said the Captain, appealing to him solemnly, “what do you make of this?", "번스비!\" 선장이 그에게 엄숙하게 호소하며 말했다, \"이걸 자네는 어떻게 생각하나?"], ["There you sit, a man as has had his head broke from infancy up’ards, and has got a new opinion into it at every seam as has been opened.", "자네는 거기 앉아 있는, 어린 시절부터 줄곧 머리가 깨져 온 사람이고, 그렇게 터진 솔기마다 새 의견이 들어찬 사람이지."], ["Now, what do you make o’ this?”", "자, 이걸 자네는 어떻게 생각하나?\""]], [["“If so be,”", "\"만약에,\""], ["returned Bunsby, with unusual promptitude, “as he’s dead, my opinion is he won’t come back no more.", "번스비가 평소답지 않게 즉각 대답했다, \"그가 죽었다면, 내 의견으로는 그는 더는 돌아오지 않을 걸세."], ["If so be as he’s alive, my opinion is he will.", "만약에 그가 살아 있다면, 내 의견으로는 그는 돌아올 걸세."], ["Do I say he will? No.", "내가 그가 돌아온다고 말하는가?"], ["Why not?", "아니지."], ["Because the bearings of this obserwation lays in the application on it.”", "어째서 아닌가? 왜냐하면 이 관찰의 방위는 그 적용에 달려 있기 때문일세.\""]], [["“Bunsby!”", "\"번스비!\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, who would seem to have estimated the value of his distinguished friend’s opinions in proportion to the immensity of the difficulty he experienced in making anything out of them; “Bunsby,”", "그 저명한 친구의 의견에서 무언가를 알아내는 데 겪는 어려움의 막대함에 비례하여 그 가치를 매긴 듯한 커틀 선장이 말했다. \"번스비,\""], ["said the Captain, quite confounded by admiration, “you carry a weight of mind easy, as would swamp one of my tonnage soon.", "선장이 감탄으로 완전히 어리둥절해져서 말했다, \"자네는 머릿속 무게를 가볍게도 지고 다니는구먼, 그게 나 같은 톤수의 사람은 금세 가라앉히고도 남을 텐데 말이야."], ["But in regard o’ this here will, I don’t mean to take no steps towards the property—Lord forbid!—except to keep it for a more rightful owner; and I hope yet as the rightful owner, Sol Gills, is living and’ll come back, strange as it is that he ain’t forwarded no dispatches.", "하지만 이 유언에 관해서라면, 나는 그 재산에 손을 댈 생각이 조금도 없네—천만의 말씀이지!—다만 더 정당한 임자를 위해 그것을 지켜 둘 뿐이고, 나는 여전히 그 정당한 임자인 솔 길스가 살아서 돌아오기를 바라네. 그가 아무런 소식도 보내오지 않은 게 이상하긴 하지만 말이야."], ["Now, what is your opinion, Bunsby, as to stowing of these here papers away again, and marking outside as they was opened, such a day, in the presence of John Bunsby and Ed’ard Cuttle?”", "자, 자네 의견은 어떤가, 번스비, 이 서류들을 다시 챙겨 넣고, 겉면에 이를 아무 날 존 번스비와 에드워드 커틀이 보는 앞에서 열었노라고 표시해 두는 것에 대해서?\""]], [["Bunsby, descrying no objection, on the coast of Greenland or elsewhere, to this proposal, it was carried into execution; and that great man, bringing his eye into the present for a moment, affixed his sign-manual to the cover, totally abstaining, with characteristic modesty, from the use of capital letters.", "번스비가 그린란드 해안에서든 다른 어디서든 이 제안에 아무런 이의를 발견하지 못했으므로, 그것은 실행에 옮겨졌다. 그리하여 그 위대한 인물은 잠시 눈을 현재로 가져와, 그 특유의 겸손함으로 대문자 사용은 일체 삼간 채, 겉장에 자기 자필 서명을 적어 넣었다."], ["Captain Cuttle, having attached his own left-handed signature, and locked up the packet in the iron safe, entreated his guest to mix another glass and smoke another pipe; and doing the like himself, fell a musing over the fire on the possible fortunes of the poor old Instrument-maker.", "커틀 선장도 자기의 왼손잡이 서명을 덧붙인 다음, 꾸러미를 철제 금고에 넣어 잠그고는, 손님에게 한 잔 더 타고 파이프를 한 대 더 피우라고 청했다. 그리고 자기도 똑같이 하면서, 가엾은 늙은 기구 제작자의 있음직한 운명을 두고 난롯가에서 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["And now a surprise occurred, so overwhelming and terrific that Captain Cuttle, unsupported by the presence of Bunsby, must have sunk beneath it, and been a lost man from that fatal hour.", "그런데 이제 어찌나 압도적이고 끔찍한 놀라움이 닥쳐왔던지, 번스비가 곁에 있어 주지 않았더라면 커틀 선장은 그 아래 가라앉아, 그 운명의 시각부터 영영 끝장난 사람이 되고 말았을 것이다."]], [["How the Captain, even in the satisfaction of admitting such a guest, could have only shut the door, and not locked it, of which negligence he was undoubtedly guilty, is one of those questions that must for ever remain mere points of speculation, or vague charges against destiny.", "선장이, 그런 손님을 들이는 만족 속에서나마, 어떻게 문을 닫기만 하고 잠그지는 않았는지—그가 그런 태만을 범한 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다—그것은 영영 한낱 추측의 문제로, 혹은 운명을 향한 막연한 비난으로 남을 수밖에 없는 수수께끼 가운데 하나다."], ["But by that unlocked door, at this quiet moment, did the fell MacStinger dash into the parlour, bringing Alexander MacStinger in her parental arms, and confusion and vengeance (not to mention Juliana MacStinger, and the sweet child’s brother, Charles MacStinger, popularly known about the scenes of his youthful sports, as Chowley) in her train.", "그러나 바로 그 잠그지 않은 문으로, 이 고요한 순간에, 그 무시무시한 맥스팅거가 거실 안으로 돌진해 들어왔으니, 알렉산더 맥스팅거를 어머니의 두 팔에 안고, 혼란과 복수를 (줄리아나 맥스팅거와, 그 귀여운 아이의 오라비이자 자기 어린 시절 놀이판에서 흔히 차울리로 통하던 찰스 맥스팅거는 말할 것도 없이) 뒤에 거느린 채였다."], ["She came so swiftly and so silently, like a rushing air from the neighbourhood of the East India Docks, that Captain Cuttle found himself in the very act of sitting looking at her, before the calm face with which he had been meditating, changed to one of horror and dismay.", "그녀가 어찌나 빠르고도 소리 없이, 마치 동인도 부두 근방에서 몰아치는 한 줄기 바람처럼 들이닥쳤던지, 커틀 선장은 명상에 잠겨 짓고 있던 그 평온한 얼굴이 공포와 경악의 얼굴로 바뀌기도 전에, 어느새 자기가 그녀를 바라보며 앉아 있는 바로 그 와중에 처해 있는 자신을 발견했다."]], [["But the moment Captain Cuttle understood the full extent of his misfortune, self-preservation dictated an attempt at flight.", "그러나 커틀 선장은 자기 불행의 전모를 깨닫는 순간, 자기 보존 본능이 도주의 시도를 명했다."], ["Darting at the little door which opened from the parlour on the steep little range of cellar-steps, the Captain made a rush, head-foremost, at the latter, like a man indifferent to bruises and contusions, who only sought to hide himself in the bowels of the earth.", "거실에서 가파른 지하실 계단으로 이어지는 그 작은 문으로 돌진하여, 선장은 마치 멍이며 타박상 따위는 아랑곳 않고 오직 땅속 깊은 곳으로 몸을 숨기려는 사람처럼, 머리부터 들이밀며 그 계단으로 돌진해 갔다."], ["In this gallant effort he would probably have succeeded, but for the affectionate dispositions of Juliana and Chowley, who pinning him by the legs—one of those dear children holding on to each—claimed him as their friend, with lamentable cries.", "이 용감한 분투로 그는 아마 성공했을 테지만, 줄리아나와 차울리의 애정 어린 성향이 문제였다. 그 둘이 그의 다리에 매달려—그 사랑스러운 아이들이 한 다리씩 붙잡고서—구슬프게 울부짖으며 그를 자기네 친구라고 우겨 댔던 것이다."], ["In the meantime, Mrs MacStinger, who never entered upon any action of importance without previously inverting Alexander MacStinger, to bring him within the range of a brisk battery of slaps, and then sitting him down to cool as the reader first beheld him, performed that solemn rite, as if on this occasion it were a sacrifice to the Furies; and having deposited the victim on the floor, made at the Captain with a strength of purpose that appeared to threaten scratches to the interposing Bunsby.", "그러는 사이, 알렉산더 맥스팅거를 미리 거꾸로 뒤집어 잰 따귀 세례의 사정거리 안에 들여놓고, 그런 다음 독자가 그를 처음 보았던 그대로 식히려고 앉혀 두지 않고서는 어떤 중대한 행동에도 결코 착수하는 법이 없는 맥스팅거 부인은, 이번에는 마치 복수의 여신들에게 바치는 제물이라도 되는 양 그 엄숙한 의식을 거행했다. 그러고는 그 제물을 바닥에 내려놓고, 끼어든 번스비에게 할퀴기를 위협하는 듯한 결연한 목적의 기세로 선장에게 덤벼들었다."]], [["The cries of the two elder MacStingers, and the wailing of young Alexander, who may be said to have passed a piebald childhood, forasmuch as he was black in the face during one half of that fairy period of existence, combined to make this visitation the more awful.", "두 손위 맥스팅거 아이의 울음소리와, 어린 알렉산더의 통곡—그는 그 동화 같은 존재의 시기 중 절반 동안 얼굴이 시퍼렜으니, 가히 얼룩덜룩한 유년기를 보냈다 할 만했다—이 어우러져 이 습격을 한층 더 무시무시하게 만들었다."], ["But when silence reigned again, and the Captain, in a violent perspiration, stood meekly looking at Mrs MacStinger, its terrors were at their height.", "그러나 다시 정적이 깃들고, 선장이 심한 식은땀을 흘리며 맥스팅거 부인을 얌전히 바라보며 서 있게 되었을 때, 그 공포는 절정에 달했다."]], [["“Oh, Cap’en Cuttle, Cap’en Cuttle!”", "\"오, 커틀 선장님, 커틀 선장님!\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger, making her chin rigid, and shaking it in unison with what, but for the weakness of her sex, might be described as her fist.", "맥스팅거 부인이 턱을 빳빳이 굳히고, 그것을—만약 그 성별의 약함만 아니었다면 그녀의 주먹이라고 묘사했을 만한 것과 더불어—흔들어 대며 말했다."], ["“Oh, Cap’en Cuttle, Cap’en Cuttle, do you dare to look me in the face, and not be struck down in the berth!”", "\"오, 커틀 선장님, 커틀 선장님, 감히 내 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보면서 그 자리에서 거꾸러지지 않을 수가 있어요!\""]], [["The Captain, who looked anything but daring, feebly muttered “Stand by!”", "도무지 대담해 보이지는 않는 선장이 힘없이 \"침착하게!\" 하고 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Oh I was a weak and trusting Fool when I took you under my roof, Cap’en Cuttle, I was!”", "\"오, 내가 당신을 내 지붕 아래 들였을 때 나는 약하고 잘 믿는 바보였어요, 커틀 선장님, 그랬다고요!\""], ["cried Mrs MacStinger.", "맥스팅거 부인이 외쳤다."], ["“To think of the benefits I’ve showered on that man, and the way in which I brought my children up to love and honour him as if he was a father to ’em, when there ain’t a housekeeper, no nor a lodger in our street, don’t know that I lost money by that man, and by his guzzlings and his muzzlings”—Mrs MacStinger used the last word for the joint sake of alliteration and aggravation, rather than for the expression of any idea—“and when they cried out one and all, shame upon him for putting upon an industrious woman, up early and late for the good of her young family, and keeping her poor place so clean that a individual might have ate his dinner, yes, and his tea too, if he was so disposed, off any one of the floors or stairs, in spite of all his guzzlings and his muzzlings, such was the care and pains bestowed upon him!”", "\"내가 그 사람에게 퍼부은 은혜며, 내 아이들을 마치 그 사람이 제 친아비라도 되는 양 그 사람을 사랑하고 존경하도록 길러 낸 것을 생각하면! 우리 동네에 가정부든 하숙인이든, 내가 그 사람 때문에, 또 그 사람의 퍼마시기며 처마시기 때문에 돈을 잃었다는 걸 모르는 사람이 하나도 없는데 말이에요\"—맥스팅거 부인은 어떤 뜻을 표현하기 위해서라기보다는 두운과 가중을 노려 마지막 말을 썼다—\"또 모두가 하나같이, 부지런한 여자한테, 제 어린 식솔들을 위해 일찍 일어나고 늦게까지 일하는 여자한테 폐를 끼친다고, 또 그 사람의 온갖 퍼마시기며 처마시기에도 불구하고, 내가 그 사람한테 쏟은 정성과 수고가 얼마나 대단했던지, 누구든 마음만 먹으면 그 마룻바닥이나 계단 어느 한 군데에서 저녁밥은 물론이고 그래요, 차까지도 먹을 수 있을 만큼 내 누추한 집을 깨끗이 건사해 놓은 여자한테 폐를 끼친다고, 부끄러운 줄 알라고 외쳐 댔을 때 말이에요!\""]], [["Mrs MacStinger stopped to fetch her breath; and her face flushed with triumph in this second happy introduction of Captain Cuttle’s muzzlings.", "맥스팅거 부인은 숨을 고르려고 멈추었다. 그리고 커틀 선장의 처마시기를 이렇게 두 번째로 멋들어지게 끌어들인 의기양양함에 그녀의 얼굴이 상기되었다."]], [["“And he runs awa-a-a-y!”", "\"그런데 그 사람은 달아나아아아 버렸어요!\""], ["cried Mrs MacStinger, with a lengthening out of the last syllable that made the unfortunate Captain regard himself as the meanest of men; “and keeps away a twelve-month!", "맥스팅거 부인이 마지막 음절을 길게 늘여 외치는 바람에, 가엾은 선장은 자기 자신을 사내들 가운데 가장 비열한 자로 여기게 되었다. \"그러고는 일 년 내내 코빼기도 안 비쳐요!"], ["From a woman!", "여자 하나를 피해서요!"], ["Such is his conscience!", "그 사람 양심이라는 게 고작 이래요!"], ["He hasn’t the courage to meet her hi-i-igh;” long syllable again; “but steals away, like a fellon.", "그 여자를 떳떳이 마아아안날 용기는 없는 거죠\"—다시 길게 늘인 음절—\"그저 흉악범처럼 슬그머니 달아나는 거예요."], ["Why, if that baby of mine,”", "글쎄, 만약 우리 이 아기가,\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger, with sudden rapidity, “was to offer to go and steal away, I’d do my duty as a mother by him, till he was covered with wales!”", "맥스팅거 부인이 갑자기 빠르게 말했다, \"달아날 생각을 품기라도 한다면, 나는 그 애가 온통 채찍 자국으로 뒤덮일 때까지 어미로서 내 의무를 다할 거예요!\""]], [["The young Alexander, interpreting this into a positive promise, to be shortly redeemed, tumbled over with fear and grief, and lay upon the floor, exhibiting the soles of his shoes and making such a deafening outcry, that Mrs MacStinger found it necessary to take him up in her arms, where she quieted him, ever and anon, as he broke out again, by a shake that seemed enough to loosen his teeth.", "어린 알렉산더는 이것을 머잖아 이행될 확실한 약속으로 해석하고는, 두려움과 슬픔에 겨워 나뒹굴며 바닥에 누워, 제 신발 밑창을 드러낸 채 어찌나 귀가 멍멍하도록 울부짖었던지, 맥스팅거 부인은 그를 두 팔에 안아 올릴 수밖에 없었고, 거기서 그가 거듭 울음을 터뜨릴 때마다 그의 이를 죄다 흔들어 빼놓을 듯한 흔들기로 이따금 그를 달랬다."]], [["“A pretty sort of a man is Cap’en Cuttle,”", "\"커틀 선장이라는 이 작자가 참 잘난 사내라니까요,\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger, with a sharp stress on the first syllable of the Captain’s name, “to take on for—and to lose sleep for—and to faint along of—and to think dead forsooth—and to go up and down the blessed town like a madwoman, asking questions after!", "맥스팅거 부인이 선장의 이름 첫 음절에 날카롭게 힘을 주며 말했다, \"그런 사람을 위해 애를 태우고—잠을 설치고—기절까지 하고—심지어 죽었다고 여기고—그 사람 소식을 캐물으며 미친 여자처럼 온 도시를 헤매고 다녔다니!"], ["Oh, a pretty sort of a man!", "오, 참 잘난 사내야!"], ["Ha ha ha ha!", "하 하 하 하!"], ["He’s worth all that trouble and distress of mind, and much more.", "그 사람이야말로 그 모든 수고와 마음고생을 받아 마땅하고, 그보다 더한 것도 받아 마땅하지요."], ["That’s nothing, bless you! Ha ha ha ha!", "그까짓 건 아무것도 아니에요, 천만에!"], ["Cap’en Cuttle,”", "하 하 하 하!"], ["said Mrs MacStinger, with severe reaction in her voice and manner, “I wish to know if you’re a-coming home.”", "커틀 선장님,\" 맥스팅거 부인이 목소리와 태도가 심하게 표변하며 말했다, \"당신이 집으로 돌아올 건지 알고 싶군요.\""]], [["The frightened Captain looked into his hat, as if he saw nothing for it but to put it on, and give himself up.", "겁에 질린 선장은 마치 모자를 도로 써서 자수하는 것밖에는 달리 도리가 없다고 여기는 듯, 자기 모자 속을 들여다보았다."]], [["“Cap’en Cuttle,”", "\"커틀 선장님,\""], ["repeated Mrs MacStinger, in the same determined manner, “I wish to know if you’re a-coming home, Sir.”", "맥스팅거 부인이 똑같이 단호한 태도로 되풀이했다, \"당신이 집으로 돌아올 건지 알고 싶다고요, 선생.\""]], [["The Captain seemed quite ready to go, but faintly suggested something to the effect of “not making so much noise about it.”", "선장은 갈 채비가 자못 되어 있는 듯했으나, \"그 일로 그렇게 큰 소동을 피우지는 말자\"는 취지의 말을 힘없이 내비쳤다."]], [["“Ay, ay, ay,”", "\"그래, 그래, 그래,\""], ["said Bunsby, in a soothing tone.", "번스비가 달래는 어조로 말했다."], ["“Awast, my lass, awast!”", "\"잠깐 멈추쇼, 아주머니, 멈추라고!\""]], [["“And who may you be, if you please!”", "\"실례지만, 당신은 대체 누구셔요!\""], ["retorted Mrs MacStinger, with chaste loftiness.", "맥스팅거 부인이 정숙한 도도함으로 응수했다."], ["“Did you ever lodge at Number Nine, Brig Place, Sir?", "\"당신은 브리그 플레이스 9번지에 하숙한 적이 있나요, 선생?"], ["My memory may be bad, but not with me, I think.", "내 기억력이 시원찮을지는 몰라도, 그 점에선 아니라고 봐요."], ["There was a Mrs Jollson lived at Number Nine before me, and perhaps you’re mistaking me for her.", "내 앞에 9번지에 졸슨 부인이라는 사람이 살았는데, 어쩌면 당신이 나를 그 여자로 착각하나 보군요."], ["That is my only ways of accounting for your familiarity, Sir.”", "당신의 그 허물없는 태도를 내가 설명할 길은 그것밖에 없네요, 선생.\""]], [["“Come, come, my lass, awast, awast!”", "\"자, 자, 아주머니, 멈추쇼, 멈춰!\""], ["said Bunsby.", "번스비가 말했다."]], [["Captain Cuttle could hardly believe it, even of this great man, though he saw it done with his waking eyes; but Bunsby, advancing boldly, put his shaggy blue arm round Mrs MacStinger, and so softened her by his magic way of doing it, and by these few words—he said no more—that she melted into tears, after looking upon him for a few moments, and observed that a child might conquer her now, she was so low in her courage.", "커틀 선장은 깨어 있는 두 눈으로 그것이 행해지는 것을 보면서도, 그 위대한 인물이 하는 일이라 해도 도무지 믿기지 않았다. 그러나 번스비는 대담하게 다가가, 그 텁수룩한 푸른 팔을 맥스팅거 부인의 허리에 두르고는, 그 일을 해내는 마법 같은 솜씨로, 그리고 그 몇 마디 말로—그는 더는 말하지 않았다—그녀를 어찌나 부드럽게 누그러뜨렸던지, 그녀는 잠시 그를 바라보더니 눈물에 녹아들며, 이제는 어린아이조차 자기를 이겨먹을 수 있을 만큼 기가 푹 꺾였노라고 말했다."]], [["Speechless and utterly amazed, the Captain saw him gradually persuade this inexorable woman into the shop, return for rum and water and a candle, take them to her, and pacify her without appearing to utter one word.", "말문이 막히고 완전히 얼이 빠진 채, 선장은 번스비가 이 가차 없는 여인을 차츰차츰 구슬려 가게로 데려가고, 럼주와 물과 양초를 가지러 돌아왔다가 그것들을 그녀에게 가져다주며, 단 한마디도 입에 올리는 기색 없이 그녀를 달래는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["Presently he looked in with his pilot-coat on, and said, “Cuttle, I’m a-going to act as convoy home;”", "이윽고 번스비가 도선사 외투를 걸친 채 안을 들여다보며 말했다, \"커틀, 내가 집까지 호위선 노릇을 해 주려 가네.\""], ["and Captain Cuttle, more to his confusion than if he had been put in irons himself, for safe transport to Brig Place, saw the family pacifically filing off, with Mrs MacStinger at their head.", "그러자 커틀 선장은, 차라리 자기가 브리그 플레이스로 무사히 호송되도록 직접 쇠고랑을 차는 편이 덜 당황스러웠을 만큼 어리둥절해하며, 맥스팅거 부인을 선두로 그 가족이 평화롭게 줄지어 빠져나가는 것을 보았다. 그는 가까스로 양철통을 내려, 옛 총애를 받던 줄리아나 맥스팅거와, 자기가 천성적으로 항해사 체격이라는 명분으로 그에게 권리를 주장하던 차울리의 손에 몰래 돈을 좀 쥐여 줄 틈밖에 없었다."], ["He had scarcely time to take down his canister, and stealthily convey some money into the hands of Juliana MacStinger, his former favourite, and Chowley, who had the claim upon him that he was naturally of a maritime build, before the Midshipman was abandoned by them all; and Bunsby whispering that he’d carry on smart, and hail Ned Cuttle again before he went aboard, shut the door upon himself, as the last member of the party.", "그러고 나자 항해사 인형은 그들 모두에게 버림받았다. 번스비는 자기가 빈틈없이 일을 처리하고, 배에 오르기 전에 다시 네드 커틀을 불러 보겠노라고 속삭이고는, 일행 가운데 마지막 사람으로서 자기 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다."]], [["Some uneasy ideas that he must be walking in his sleep, or that he had been troubled with phantoms, and not a family of flesh and blood, beset the Captain at first, when he went back to the little parlour, and found himself alone.", "작은 거실로 돌아와 홀로 남은 자신을 발견했을 때, 선장은 처음에는 자기가 잠결에 걸어다니고 있는 것은 아닐까, 아니면 피와 살을 지닌 한 가족이 아니라 환영에 시달린 것은 아닐까 하는 어수선한 생각에 사로잡혔다."], ["Illimitable faith in, and immeasurable admiration of, the Commander of the Cautious Clara, succeeded, and threw the Captain into a wondering trance.", "뒤이어 코셔스 클라라호의 사령관에 대한 한없는 신뢰와 헤아릴 수 없는 찬탄이 밀려와, 선장을 경이로운 황홀경에 빠뜨렸다."]], [["Still, as time wore on, and Bunsby failed to reappear, the Captain began to entertain uncomfortable doubts of another kind.", "그러나 시간이 흐르고 번스비가 다시 나타나지 않자, 선장은 또 다른 종류의 불편한 의구심을 품기 시작했다."], ["Whether Bunsby had been artfully decoyed to Brig Place, and was there detained in safe custody as hostage for his friend; in which case it would become the Captain, as a man of honour, to release him, by the sacrifice of his own liberty.", "번스비가 교묘히 브리그 플레이스로 꾀여 가, 거기서 자기 친구를 대신할 인질로 단단히 붙잡혀 있는 것은 아닐까. 그렇다면 명예를 아는 사내로서 선장은 자기 자신의 자유를 희생하여 그를 풀어 주는 것이 마땅할 터였다."], ["Whether he had been attacked and defeated by Mrs MacStinger, and was ashamed to show himself after his discomfiture.", "혹은 그가 맥스팅거 부인에게 공격당해 패하고는, 그 낭패를 당한 뒤라 차마 모습을 드러내기가 부끄러운 것은 아닐까."], ["Whether Mrs MacStinger, thinking better of it, in the uncertainty of her temper, had turned back to board the Midshipman again, and Bunsby, pretending to conduct her by a short cut, was endeavouring to lose the family amid the wilds and savage places of the City.", "혹은 맥스팅거 부인이 그 변덕스러운 성미로 마음을 고쳐먹고 다시 항해사 인형으로 쳐들어오려고 발길을 돌렸는데, 번스비가 지름길로 그녀를 안내하는 척하며 시내의 황량하고 외진 곳들 사이에서 그 가족을 따돌리려 애쓰고 있는 것은 아닐까."], ["Above all, what it would behove him, Captain Cuttle, to do, in case of his hearing no more, either of the MacStingers or of Bunsby, which, in these wonderful and unforeseen conjunctions of events, might possibly happen.", "무엇보다도, 만약 맥스팅거 가족이든 번스비든 아무 소식도 더 듣지 못하게 된다면—이런 놀랍고 예측 불가능한 사건들의 얽힘 속에서라면 능히 그럴 법도 했다—자기, 커틀 선장이 어떻게 처신하는 것이 마땅할 것인가."]], [["He debated all this until he was tired; and still no Bunsby.", "그는 지칠 때까지 이 모든 것을 곱씹었으나, 여전히 번스비는 오지 않았다."], ["He made up his bed under the counter, all ready for turning in; and still no Bunsby.", "그는 언제든 잠자리에 들 수 있도록 계산대 밑에 잠자리를 다 마련해 두었으나, 여전히 번스비는 오지 않았다."], ["At length, when the Captain had given him up, for that night at least, and had begun to undress, the sound of approaching wheels was heard, and, stopping at the door, was succeeded by Bunsby’s hail.", "마침내 선장이, 적어도 그날 밤만은 그를 단념하고 옷을 벗기 시작했을 때, 다가오는 바퀴 소리가 들리더니, 문 앞에서 멈추고는, 뒤이어 번스비의 외침이 들려왔다."]], [["The Captain trembled to think that Mrs MacStinger was not to be got rid of, and had been brought back in a coach.", "선장은 맥스팅거 부인을 떨쳐 낼 수 없어, 마차에 태워 도로 데려온 것은 아닐까 하는 생각에 몸서리쳤다."]], [["But no.", "그러나 아니었다."], ["Bunsby was accompanied by nothing but a large box, which he hauled into the shop with his own hands, and as soon as he had hauled in, sat upon.", "번스비는 커다란 상자 하나 말고는 아무것도 동반하지 않았는데, 그것을 제 손으로 가게 안으로 끌어들이고는, 다 끌어들이자마자 그 위에 걸터앉았다."], ["Captain Cuttle knew it for the chest he had left at Mrs MacStinger’s house, and looking, candle in hand, at Bunsby more attentively, believed that he was three sheets in the wind, or, in plain words, drunk.", "커틀 선장은 그것이 맥스팅거 부인의 집에 두고 온 자기 궤짝임을 알아보았고, 양초를 손에 든 채 번스비를 더 주의 깊게 살펴보고는, 그가 돛을 셋이나 펄럭이고 있다고, 쉬운 말로 하자면 취해 있다고 믿었다."], ["It was difficult, however, to be sure of this; the Commander having no trace of expression in his face when sober.", "그러나 그것을 확신하기는 어려웠으니, 그 사령관은 멀쩡할 때조차 얼굴에 아무런 표정의 흔적이 없는 사람이었다."]], [["“Cuttle,”", "\"커틀,\""], ["said the Commander, getting off the chest, and opening the lid, “are these here your traps?”", "사령관이 궤짝에서 내려와 뚜껑을 열며 말했다, \"이게 자네 살림살이인가?\""]], [["Captain Cuttle looked in and identified his property.", "커틀 선장은 안을 들여다보고 자기 물건임을 확인했다."]], [["“Done pretty taut and trim, hey, shipmet?”", "\"꽤 팽팽하고 말끔하게 챙겨 왔지, 응, 동지?\""], ["said Bunsby.", "번스비가 말했다."]], [["The grateful and bewildered Captain grasped him by the hand, and was launching into a reply expressive of his astonished feelings, when Bunsby disengaged himself by a jerk of his wrist, and seemed to make an effort to wink with his revolving eye, the only effect of which attempt, in his condition, was nearly to over-balance him.", "고마워하면서도 어리둥절한 선장이 그의 손을 움켜쥐고, 자기의 놀란 심정을 담은 대답을 막 쏟아 내려는데, 번스비가 손목을 한번 홱 비틀어 빠져나가고는, 그 빙빙 도는 눈으로 윙크를 해 보이려고 애쓰는 듯했다. 그러나 그 상태에서 그 시도가 빚어낸 유일한 결과란 그가 하마터면 균형을 잃을 뻔한 것뿐이었다."], ["He then abruptly opened the door, and shot away to rejoin the Cautious Clara with all speed—supposed to be his invariable custom, whenever he considered he had made a point.", "그런 다음 그는 불쑥 문을 열고, 코셔스 클라라호에 다시 합류하려고 전속력으로 쏜살같이 사라졌으니—이는 자기가 한 건 했다고 여길 때마다 그가 으레 보이는 습관으로 짐작되었다."]], [["As it was not his humour to be often sought, Captain Cuttle decided not to go or send to him next day, or until he should make his gracious pleasure known in such wise, or failing that, until some little time should have lapsed.", "번스비가 자주 찾아오는 것을 즐기는 성미가 아니었으므로, 커틀 선장은 이튿날, 혹은 그가 자기의 너그러운 뜻을 그런 식으로 알려 오기 전까지는, 그것이 아니라면 얼마간의 시간이 흐를 때까지는 그에게 가거나 사람을 보내지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["The Captain, therefore, renewed his solitary life next morning, and thought profoundly, many mornings, noons, and nights, of old Sol Gills, and Bunsby’s sentiments concerning him, and the hopes there were of his return.", "그리하여 선장은 이튿날 아침 자기의 고독한 생활을 다시 시작했고, 여러 날의 아침과 한낮과 밤에 걸쳐 늙은 솔 길스에 대해, 또 그를 두고 번스비가 내놓은 견해에 대해, 또 그가 돌아오리라는 희망에 대해 깊이 생각했다."], ["Much of such thinking strengthened Captain Cuttle’s hopes; and he humoured them and himself by watching for the Instrument-maker at the door—as he ventured to do now, in his strange liberty—and setting his chair in its place, and arranging the little parlour as it used to be, in case he should come home unexpectedly.", "그런 생각을 많이 한 끝에 커틀 선장의 희망은 더욱 굳건해졌다. 그리고 그는 문간에서 기구 제작자를 기다리며—이제 그 묘한 자유 속에서 그렇게 해 볼 엄두를 냈으니—그 희망과 자기 자신을 다독였고, 그가 뜻밖에 집에 돌아올 경우에 대비해 그의 의자를 제자리에 놓고 작은 거실을 예전 그대로 정돈해 두었다."], ["He likewise, in his thoughtfulness, took down a certain little miniature of Walter as a schoolboy, from its accustomed nail, lest it should shock the old man on his return.", "또한 그는 사려 깊게도, 노인이 돌아왔을 때 충격받지 않도록, 학생 시절 월터의 어떤 작은 초상화를 늘 걸려 있던 못에서 내려 두었다."], ["The Captain had his presentiments, too, sometimes, that he would come on such a day; and one particular Sunday, even ordered a double allowance of dinner, he was so sanguine.", "선장은 또 이따금 그가 바로 어느 날 돌아오리라는 예감을 품기도 했다. 그리고 어느 특별한 일요일에는 너무도 자신만만한 나머지 저녁밥을 갑절로 차리라고 시키기까지 했다."], ["But come, old Solomon did not; and still the neighbours noticed how the seafaring man in the glazed hat, stood at the shop-door of an evening, looking up and down the street.", "그러나 늙은 솔로몬은 오지 않았다. 그리고 이웃들은 여전히, 번들거리는 모자를 쓴 그 뱃사람이 저녁이면 가게 문간에 서서 거리를 위아래로 두리번거리는 것을 눈여겨보았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XL. Domestic Relations", "# CHAPTER XL. Domestic Relations"]], [["It was not in the nature of things that a man of Mr Dombey’s mood, opposed to such a spirit as he had raised against himself, should be softened in the imperious asperity of his temper; or that the cold hard armour of pride in which he lived encased, should be made more flexible by constant collision with haughty scorn and defiance.", "돔비 씨 같은 기질의 사내가, 자신이 스스로에게 불러일으킨 그런 정신과 맞부딪쳤을 때 그 거만하고 가혹한 성미가 누그러진다거나, 그가 갑옷처럼 두르고 살아온 차갑고 단단한 자존심이 도도한 멸시며 반항과 끊임없이 충돌함으로써 한층 유연해진다는 것은, 사물의 이치에 맞지 않는 일이었다. 그런 천성이 짊어진 저주란—그것이 제 안에 품고 있는 무거운 응보의 주된 부분이란—이런 것이다."], ["It is the curse of such a nature—it is a main part of the heavy retribution on itself it bears within itself—that while deference and concession swell its evil qualities, and are the food it grows upon, resistance and a questioning of its exacting claims, foster it too, no less.", "곧 굴종과 양보가 그 악한 성질을 부풀려 그것이 자라나는 양식이 되는 한편, 저항과, 그 가혹한 요구에 대한 의문 제기 또한 그것을 못지않게 키운다는 것. 그 안의 악은 정반대의 것들 속에서도 똑같이 성장과 번식의 수단을 찾아낸다."], ["The evil that is in it finds equally its means of growth and propagation in opposites.", "그것은 단것에서도 쓴것에서도 지지와 생명을 끌어낸다."], ["It draws support and life from sweets and bitters; bowed down before, or unacknowledged, it still enslaves the breast in which it has its throne; and, worshipped or rejected, is as hard a master as the Devil in dark fables.", "떠받들리든 인정받지 못하든, 그것은 여전히 자기가 옥좌를 차지한 가슴을 노예로 삼는다. 그리고 숭배받든 거부당하든, 어두운 우화 속의 악마만큼이나 모진 주인이다."]], [["Towards his first wife, Mr Dombey, in his cold and lofty arrogance, had borne himself like the removed Being he almost conceived himself to be.", "첫 번째 아내를 대할 때, 돔비 씨는 그 차갑고 드높은 오만 속에서, 거의 스스로를 그렇다고 여기던 저 초연한 존재처럼 처신했다."], ["He had been “Mr Dombey” with her when she first saw him, and he was “Mr Dombey”", "그녀가 그를 처음 보았을 때 그는 \"돔비 씨\"였고, 그녀가 죽었을 때에도 그는 \"돔비 씨\"였다."], ["when she died. He had asserted his greatness during their whole married life, and she had meekly recognised it.", "그는 결혼 생활 내내 자신의 위대함을 내세웠고, 그녀는 온순하게 그것을 인정했다."], ["He had kept his distant seat of state on the top of his throne, and she her humble station on its lowest step; and much good it had done him, so to live in solitary bondage to his one idea.", "그는 옥좌 꼭대기에 자신의 멀찍한 위엄의 자리를 지켰고, 그녀는 그 가장 낮은 계단에 자신의 비천한 자리를 지켰다. 그렇게 자신의 단 하나의 관념에 외로이 매여 사는 것이 그에게 얼마나 큰 도움이 되었던가."], ["He had imagined that the proud character of his second wife would have been added to his own—would have merged into it, and exalted his greatness.", "그는 두 번째 아내의 그 도도한 성품이 자신의 것에 더해지리라고—자신의 것에 녹아들어 자신의 위대함을 한층 드높여 주리라고—상상했었다."], ["He had pictured himself haughtier than ever, with Edith’s haughtiness subservient to his.", "그는 에디스의 도도함이 자기 도도함에 복종하는 가운데, 자신이 어느 때보다도 더 거만해지는 모습을 그려 보았다."], ["He had never entertained the possibility of its arraying itself against him.", "그는 그것이 자기에게 맞서 떨쳐 일어날 가능성 따위는 단 한 번도 마음에 두지 않았다."], ["And now, when he found it rising in his path at every step and turn of his daily life, fixing its cold, defiant, and contemptuous face upon him, this pride of his, instead of withering, or hanging down its head beneath the shock, put forth new shoots, became more concentrated and intense, more gloomy, sullen, irksome, and unyielding, than it had ever been before.", "그런데 이제, 일상의 매 걸음과 매 굽이마다 그것이 자기 앞길에 솟아올라, 차갑고 반항적이며 경멸에 찬 얼굴을 자기에게 고정시키는 것을 발견하자, 그의 이 자존심은 그 충격 앞에 시들거나 고개를 떨어뜨리기는커녕 새로운 싹을 틔워, 어느 때보다도 더 응축되고 격렬해졌으며, 더 음울하고 뚱하고 성가시며 굽힘 없는 것이 되었다."]], [["Who wears such armour, too, bears with him ever another heavy retribution.", "그런 갑옷을 두른 자 또한, 늘 또 다른 무거운 응보를 지니고 다니는 법이다. 그것은 회유와 사랑과 신뢰를 막아 내고, 바깥에서 오는 온갖 부드러운 공감, 온갖 믿음, 온갖 다정함, 온갖 부드러운 감정을 막아 내는 데에는 끄떡없다."], ["It is of proof against conciliation, love, and confidence; against all gentle sympathy from without, all trust, all tenderness, all soft emotion; but to deep stabs in the self-love, it is as vulnerable as the bare breast to steel; and such tormenting festers rankle there, as follow on no other wounds, no, though dealt with the mailed hand of Pride itself, on weaker pride, disarmed and thrown down.", "그러나 자존심에 깊이 박히는 칼날에는, 강철 앞의 맨가슴만큼이나 무방비하다. 그리고 거기에는, 다른 어떤 상처에도—아니, 설령 자존심 그 자체의 갑옷 두른 손이 무장 해제되어 쓰러진 더 약한 자존심에 입힌 상처에도—뒤따르지 않는 그런 괴로운 곪음이 사무쳐 든다."]], [["Such wounds were his.", "그런 상처가 바로 그의 것이었다."], ["He felt them sharply, in the solitude of his old rooms; whither he now began often to retire again, and pass long solitary hours.", "그는 옛 방의 고독 속에서 그 상처를 날카롭게 느꼈다. 그는 이제 다시 그곳으로 자주 물러나 길고 외로운 시간을 보내기 시작했다."], ["It seemed his fate to be ever proud and powerful; ever humbled and powerless where he would be most strong.", "늘 거만하고 강력하면서도, 자기가 가장 강하고자 하는 곳에서는 늘 무력해지고 굴욕당하는 것이 그의 운명인 듯했다."], ["Who seemed fated to work out that doom?", "그 운명을 이루어 내도록 정해진 자는 누구인 듯했던가?"]], [["Who?", "누구?"], ["Who was it who could win his wife as she had won his boy?", "자기가 아들의 마음을 사로잡았듯 자기 아내의 마음을 사로잡을 수 있었던 자는 누구였던가?"], ["Who was it who had shown him that new victory, as he sat in the dark corner?", "그가 어두운 구석에 앉아 있을 때, 그 새로운 승리를 그에게 보여 준 자는 누구였던가?"], ["Who was it whose least word did what his utmost means could not?", "그의 온갖 수단으로도 하지 못한 일을 사소한 한마디로 해낸 자는 누구였던가?"], ["Who was it who, unaided by his love, regard or notice, thrived and grew beautiful when those so aided died?", "그의 사랑도 존중도 주목도 받지 못하면서, 그런 것을 받았던 이들이 죽어 갈 때 무성히 자라나 아름다워진 자는 누구였던가?"], ["Who could it be, but the same child at whom he had often glanced uneasily in her motherless infancy, with a kind of dread, lest he might come to hate her; and of whom his foreboding was fulfilled, for he DID hate her in his heart?", "어미 잃은 갓난아이였던 그 애를, 자기가 그 애를 미워하게 될까 두려워하며 일종의 불안과 함께 자주 곁눈질하던 바로 그 아이가 아니라면 누구일 수 있었겠는가. 그리고 그 불길한 예감은 이루어졌으니, 그는 정녕 마음속으로 그 애를 미워했던 것이다."]], [["Yes, and he would have it hatred, and he made it hatred, though some sparkles of the light in which she had appeared before him on the memorable night of his return home with his Bride, occasionally hung about her still.", "그렇다, 그는 그것을 증오로 삼고자 했고, 또 증오로 만들었다. 비록 신부를 데리고 귀가하던 그 잊을 수 없는 밤에 그 애가 그 앞에 나타났을 때의 그 빛의 불꽃 몇 점이 이따금 여전히 그 애 주위에 어른거렸음에도."], ["He knew now that she was beautiful; he did not dispute that she was graceful and winning, and that in the bright dawn of her womanhood she had come upon him, a surprise.", "이제 그는 그 애가 아름답다는 것을 알았다. 그 애가 우아하고 사람의 마음을 끈다는 것, 그리고 그 애가 여인됨의 환한 새벽녘에 자신에게 하나의 놀라움으로 다가왔다는 것을 그는 부정하지 않았다."], ["But he turned even this against her.", "그러나 그는 이것조차 그 애에게 불리하게 돌렸다."], ["In his sullen and unwholesome brooding, the unhappy man, with a dull perception of his alienation from all hearts, and a vague yearning for what he had all his life repelled, made a distorted picture of his rights and wrongs, and justified himself with it against her.", "자신이 모든 마음으로부터 소외되었다는 둔한 자각과, 평생 밀쳐 내 온 것에 대한 막연한 갈망 속에서, 이 불행한 사내는 뚱하고 병적인 곱씹음에 잠겨, 자기 권리와 억울함을 일그러진 그림으로 그려 내고는, 그것으로 그 애에 맞서 스스로를 정당화했다."], ["The worthier she promised to be of him, the greater claim he was disposed to antedate upon her duty and submission.", "그 애가 자기에게 더 어울리는 사람이 될 듯할수록, 그는 그 애의 의무와 복종에 대해 미리 권리를 앞당겨 주장하려는 마음이 더욱 커졌다."], ["When had she ever shown him duty and submission?", "대체 그 애가 언제 그에게 의무와 복종을 보인 적이 있던가?"], ["Did she grace his life—or Edith’s?", "그 애가 우아하게 꾸며 준 것이 그의 삶이던가—아니면 에디스의 삶이던가?"], ["Had her attractions been manifested first to him—or Edith?", "그 애가 그 매력을 처음 드러낸 상대가 그였던가—아니면 에디스였던가?"], ["Why, he and she had never been, from her birth, like father and child!", "아니, 그와 그 애는 그 애가 태어난 이래로 아비와 자식 같았던 적이 한 번도 없었다!"], ["They had always been estranged.", "그들은 늘 서로 소원했다."], ["She had crossed him every way and everywhere.", "그 애는 사사건건 어디서나 그의 뜻을 거슬렀다."], ["She was leagued against him now.", "이제 그 애는 그에게 맞서 한패가 되어 있었다."], ["Her very beauty softened natures that were obdurate to him, and insulted him with an unnatural triumph.", "바로 그 애의 아름다움이 그에게 완강하던 성품들을 누그러뜨려, 부자연스러운 승리로 그를 모욕했다."]], [["It may have been that in all this there were mutterings of an awakened feeling in his breast, however selfishly aroused by his position of disadvantage, in comparison with what she might have made his life.", "이 모든 것 가운데에는, 비록 자신이 불리한 처지에 놓였다는 데서 이기적으로 일깨워진 것이라 할지라도, 그 애가 그의 삶을 어떤 것으로 만들어 줄 수 있었을지에 견주어 자기 가슴속에서 깨어난 어떤 감정의 웅얼거림이 있었는지도 모른다."], ["But he silenced the distant thunder with the rolling of his sea of pride.", "그러나 그는 자기 자존심의 바다가 출렁이는 소리로 그 멀리서 울리는 천둥을 잠재웠다."], ["He would bear nothing but his pride.", "그는 자기 자존심 외에는 아무것도 견뎌 내려 하지 않았다."], ["And in his pride, a heap of inconsistency, and misery, and self-inflicted torment, he hated her.", "그리고 모순과 비참함과 스스로 자초한 고통의 더미인 그 자존심 속에서, 그는 그 애를 미워했다."]], [["To the moody, stubborn, sullen demon, that possessed him, his wife opposed her different pride in its full force.", "자기를 사로잡은 그 침울하고 완고하며 뚱한 악령에 맞서, 그의 아내는 종류가 다른 자기 자존심을 온 힘을 다해 들이댔다."], ["They never could have led a happy life together; but nothing could have made it more unhappy, than the wilful and determined warfare of such elements.", "그들은 결코 함께 행복한 삶을 이끌어 갈 수 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그런 요소들의 고집스럽고 단호한 싸움보다 그 삶을 더 불행하게 만들 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["His pride was set upon maintaining his magnificent supremacy, and forcing recognition of it from her.", "그의 자존심은 자신의 장엄한 우위를 지키고 그녀로 하여금 그것을 인정하게 만드는 데에 매여 있었다."], ["She would have been racked to death, and turned but her haughty glance of calm inflexible disdain upon him, to the last.", "그녀는 끝까지, 차라리 죽도록 고문당할지언정, 차분하고 굽힘 없는 멸시의 도도한 시선만을 그에게 돌릴 뿐이었을 것이다."], ["Such recognition from Edith!", "에디스에게서 그런 인정을 받겠다니!"], ["He little knew through what a storm and struggle she had been driven onward to the crowning honour of his hand.", "그는 그녀가 그의 손을 잡는 그 최고의 영예에 이르기까지 어떤 폭풍과 몸부림을 거쳐 떠밀려 왔는지 거의 알지 못했다."], ["He little knew how much she thought she had conceded, when she suffered him to call her wife.", "그가 자기를 아내라 부르도록 허락했을 때 그녀가 얼마나 많은 것을 양보했다고 여겼는지 그는 거의 알지 못했다."]], [["Mr Dombey was resolved to show her that he was supreme.", "돔비 씨는 그녀에게 자기가 최고임을 보여 주기로 작정했다."], ["There must be no will but his.", "그의 뜻 외에는 어떤 뜻도 있어서는 안 되었다."], ["Proud he desired that she should be, but she must be proud for, not against him.", "그는 그녀가 도도하기를 바랐지만, 그것은 자기에 맞서서가 아니라 자기를 위한 도도함이어야 했다."], ["As he sat alone, hardening, he would often hear her go out and come home, treading the round of London life with no more heed of his liking or disliking, pleasure or displeasure, than if he had been her groom.", "그가 홀로 앉아 점점 더 굳어 가는 동안, 그는 그녀가 외출했다가 돌아오는 소리를, 마치 자기가 그녀의 마부라도 되는 양 그의 호불호나 만족이나 불만 따위에는 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고 런던 사교 생활의 굴레를 밟는 소리를 자주 듣곤 했다."], ["Her cold supreme indifference—his own unquestioned attribute usurped—stung him more than any other kind of treatment could have done; and he determined to bend her to his magnificent and stately will.", "그녀의 차갑고 도도한 무관심은—그 자신의, 누구도 넘볼 수 없던 속성을 빼앗긴 것이었기에—다른 어떤 종류의 대우보다도 더 그를 찔렀다. 그리하여 그는 그녀를 자신의 장엄하고 위엄 있는 뜻에 굽히게 만들겠다고 결심했다."]], [["He had been long communing with these thoughts, when one night he sought her in her own apartment, after he had heard her return home late.", "그는 오랫동안 이런 생각들과 씨름하던 끝에, 어느 날 밤 그녀가 늦게 귀가하는 소리를 듣고서 그녀를 그녀 자신의 방으로 찾아갔다."], ["She was alone, in her brilliant dress, and had but that moment come from her mother’s room.", "그녀는 화사한 드레스 차림으로 혼자 있었고, 방금 막 어머니의 방에서 나온 참이었다. 그가 그녀에게 다가갔을 때 그녀의 얼굴은 우울하고 수심에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Her face was melancholy and pensive, when he came upon her; but it marked him at the door; for, glancing at the mirror before it, he saw immediately, as in a picture-frame, the knitted brow, and darkened beauty that he knew so well.", "그러나 그 얼굴은 문간의 그를 알아챘다. 그녀 앞에 놓인 거울을 흘끗 본 그는, 그가 너무도 잘 아는, 찌푸린 이마와 어두워진 아름다움을 마치 액자 속 그림처럼 즉시 보았기 때문이다."]], [["“Mrs Dombey,”", "\"돔비 부인,\""], ["he said, entering, “I must beg leave to have a few words with you.”", "그가 들어서며 말했다, \"당신과 몇 마디 나눌 수 있도록 양해를 구해야겠소.\""]], [["“To-morrow,”", "\"내일 하시죠,\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“There is no time like the present, Madam,”", "\"지금만 한 때가 없소, 부인,\""], ["he returned.", "그가 응수했다."], ["“You mistake your position.", "\"당신은 자기 처지를 잘못 알고 있소."], ["I am used to choose my own times; not to have them chosen for me.", "나는 내 때를 내가 고르는 데 익숙하지, 남이 골라 주는 데 익숙하지 않소."], ["I think you scarcely understand who and what I am, Mrs Dombey.”", "당신은 내가 누구이고 어떤 사람인지 거의 모르는 모양이오, 돔비 부인.\""]], [["“I think,”", "\"제 생각엔,\""], ["she answered, “that I understand you very well.”", "그녀가 대답했다, \"당신을 아주 잘 알고 있는 것 같은데요.\""]], [["She looked upon him as she said so, and folding her white arms, sparkling with gold and gems, upon her swelling breast, turned away her eyes.", "이렇게 말하면서 그녀는 그를 바라보았고, 금과 보석으로 반짝이는 하얀 팔을 부풀어 오른 가슴 위에 포갠 채 눈길을 돌렸다."]], [["If she had been less handsome, and less stately in her cold composure, she might not have had the power of impressing him with the sense of disadvantage that penetrated through his utmost pride.", "그녀가 덜 아름답고, 그 차가운 침착함 속에서 덜 위엄 있었더라면, 그의 더없는 자존심을 꿰뚫고 들어오는 그 불리함의 감각을 그에게 심어 줄 힘은 없었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀에게는 그 힘이 있었고, 그는 그것을 날카롭게 느꼈다."], ["But she had the power, and he felt it keenly.", "그는 방 안을 둘러보았다."], ["He glanced round the room: saw how the splendid means of personal adornment, and the luxuries of dress, were scattered here and there, and disregarded; not in mere caprice and carelessness (or so he thought), but in a steadfast haughty disregard of costly things: and felt it more and more.", "화려한 몸치장의 도구들이며 사치스러운 옷가지들이 여기저기 흩어진 채 함부로 방치되어 있는 것을 보았다. 그저 변덕이나 부주의에서가 아니라(적어도 그는 그렇게 생각했다), 값비싼 물건들에 대한 한결같고 도도한 무시에서 그러한 것을."], ["Chaplets of flowers, plumes of feathers, jewels, laces, silks and satins; look where he would, he saw riches, despised, poured out, and made of no account.", "그리고 그는 그것을 점점 더 절감했다. 화관, 깃털 장식, 보석, 레이스, 비단과 공단—어디를 보든, 그는 멸시받고 내팽개쳐지고 하찮게 여겨진 부를 보았다."], ["The very diamonds—a marriage gift—that rose and fell impatiently upon her bosom, seemed to pant to break the chain that clasped them round her neck, and roll down on the floor where she might tread upon them.", "그녀의 가슴 위에서 초조하게 오르내리는 그 다이아몬드들조차—결혼 선물이었다—목을 두른 사슬을 끊고 그녀가 밟고 다닐 수 있는 바닥으로 굴러떨어지려고 헐떡이는 것만 같았다."]], [["He felt his disadvantage, and he showed it.", "그는 자신의 불리함을 느꼈고, 또 그것을 드러냈다."], ["Solemn and strange among this wealth of colour and voluptuous glitter, strange and constrained towards its haughty mistress, whose repellent beauty it repeated, and presented all around him, as in so many fragments of a mirror, he was conscious of embarrassment and awkwardness.", "이 빛깔의 풍요로움과 관능적인 광채 속에서 엄숙하고 어색하게, 그 도도한 안주인을 향해 어색하고 거북하게—그 안주인의 사람을 밀쳐 내는 아름다움을, 그 모든 것이 마치 수많은 거울 조각 속처럼 그의 사방에 되비추어 내고 있었다—그는 당혹과 어색함을 의식했다."], ["Nothing that ministered to her disdainful self-possession could fail to gall him.", "그녀의 멸시에 찬 침착함에 보탬이 되는 것이라면 무엇이든 그를 짜증나게 하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Galled and irritated with himself, he sat down, and went on, in no improved humour:", "자기 자신에게 짜증이 나고 화가 난 채, 그는 자리에 앉아, 조금도 나아지지 않은 기분으로 말을 이었다."]], [["“Mrs Dombey, it is very necessary that there should be some understanding arrived at between us.", "\"돔비 부인, 우리 둘 사이에 어떤 이해에 이르는 것이 매우 필요하오."], ["Your conduct does not please me, Madam.”", "당신의 행실이 나는 마음에 들지 않소, 부인.\""]], [["She merely glanced at him again, and again averted her eyes; but she might have spoken for an hour, and expressed less.", "그녀는 그저 다시 한번 그를 흘끗 보고는, 다시 눈길을 돌렸다. 그러나 한 시간을 떠들었어도 그보다 적게 말하지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["“I repeat, Mrs Dombey, does not please me.", "\"다시 말하지만, 돔비 부인, 마음에 들지 않소."], ["I have already taken occasion to request that it may be corrected.", "나는 이미 그것이 시정되기를 요청할 기회를 가진 바 있소."], ["I now insist upon it.”", "이제 나는 그것을 강력히 요구하는 바요.\""]], [["“You chose a fitting occasion for your first remonstrance, Sir, and you adopt a fitting manner, and a fitting word for your second.", "\"당신은 첫 번째 항의에 알맞은 때를 골랐고, 두 번째 항의에는 알맞은 태도와 알맞은 말을 골라 쓰시는군요. 강력히 요구하신다!"], ["You insist! To me!”", "저에게요!\""]], [["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with his most offensive air of state, “I have made you my wife.", "돔비 씨가 더없이 비위 상하게 하는 위엄을 부리며 말했다, \"나는 당신을 내 아내로 삼았소."], ["You bear my name.", "당신은 내 이름을 지니고 있소."], ["You are associated with my position and my reputation.", "당신은 내 지위와 내 명성에 결부되어 있소. 세상 사람들이 대체로 당신이 그 결연으로 영예를 입었다고 여기는 쪽이라고까지는 말하지 않겠소."], ["I will not say that the world in general may be disposed to think you honoured by that association; but I will say that I am accustomed to ‘insist,’ to my connexions and dependents.”", "그러나 나는 내 인척과 내게 딸린 자들에게 '강력히 요구하는' 데 익숙하다는 것은 말해 두겠소.\""]], [["“Which may you be pleased to consider me?", "\"저는 그 둘 중 어느 쪽으로 여기시겠어요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Possibly I may think that my wife should partake—or does partake, and cannot help herself—of both characters, Mrs Dombey.”", "\"아마도 나는 내 아내가 그 두 가지 성격을 모두 함께 지녀야 한다고—혹은 함께 지니고 있으며, 어쩔 도리가 없다고—생각하는지도 모르겠소, 돔비 부인.\""]], [["She bent her eyes upon him steadily, and set her trembling lips.", "그녀는 그에게 한결같은 눈길을 떨구고, 떨리는 입술을 다물었다."], ["He saw her bosom throb, and saw her face flush and turn white.", "그는 그녀의 가슴이 두근거리는 것을 보았고, 그녀의 얼굴이 붉어졌다가 하얗게 질리는 것을 보았다."], ["All this he could know, and did: but he could not know that one word was whispering in the deep recesses of her heart, to keep her quiet; and that the word was Florence.", "이 모든 것을 그는 알 수 있었고 또 알았다. 그러나 한마디 말이 그녀의 마음 깊은 구석에서 그녀를 잠잠하게 하려고 속삭이고 있다는 것은, 그리고 그 말이 플로렌스라는 것은, 그는 알 수 없었다."]], [["Blind idiot, rushing to a precipice!", "눈먼 바보가, 벼랑을 향해 돌진하고 있었구나!"], ["He thought she stood in awe of him.", "그는 그녀가 자기를 두려워하며 서 있다고 생각했다."]], [["“You are too expensive, Madam,”", "\"당신은 너무 돈이 많이 드오, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“You are extravagant.", "\"당신은 낭비가 심하오."], ["You waste a great deal of money—or what would be a great deal in the pockets of most gentlemen—in cultivating a kind of society that is useless to me, and, indeed, that upon the whole is disagreeable to me.", "당신은 나에게 쓸모없고, 사실 전체적으로 보아 나에게 불쾌한, 그런 종류의 교제를 가꾸는 데에 막대한 돈을—혹은 대부분의 신사들 주머니에서라면 막대한 액수가 될 돈을—허비하고 있소."], ["I have to insist upon a total change in all these respects.", "나는 이 모든 면에서 전면적인 변화가 있어야 한다고 강력히 요구하는 바요."], ["I know that in the novelty of possessing a tithe of such means as Fortune has placed at your disposal, ladies are apt to run into a sudden extreme.", "운명이 당신의 처분에 맡겨 둔 만큼의 그런 재력의 십분의 일이라도 가지게 된 그 신기함 속에서, 부인들이 흔히 갑작스러운 극단으로 치닫는다는 것은 나도 알고 있소."], ["There has been more than enough of that extreme.", "그런 극단은 이미 차고 넘쳤소."], ["I beg that Mrs Granger’s very different experiences may now come to the instruction of Mrs Dombey.”", "이제는 그레인저 부인의 사뭇 다른 경험들이 돔비 부인의 교훈이 되어 주기를 바라오.\""]], [["Still the fixed look, the trembling lips, the throbbing breast, the face now crimson and now white; and still the deep whisper Florence, Florence, speaking to her in the beating of her heart.", "여전히 고정된 눈길, 떨리는 입술, 두근거리는 가슴, 이제 진홍빛이었다가 하얗게 질리는 얼굴. 그리고 여전히 그 깊은 속삭임, 플로렌스, 플로렌스가, 그녀의 심장 박동 속에서 그녀에게 말을 걸고 있었다."]], [["His insolence of self-importance dilated as he saw this alteration in her.", "그녀에게서 이런 변화를 보면서 그의 그 거만한 자만심은 한껏 부풀어 올랐다."], ["Swollen no less by her past scorn of him, and his so recent feeling of disadvantage, than by her present submission (as he took it to be), it became too mighty for his breast, and burst all bounds.", "그녀가 지난날 자기를 멸시했던 것과, 자기가 방금 느낀 그 불리함의 감각으로도 모자라, 이제 그녀가 (그가 그렇게 받아들이기로는) 굴복하는 것까지 더해져, 그것은 그의 가슴이 담아 두기에 너무도 거대해져서 온갖 한계를 터뜨려 버렸다."], ["Why, who could long resist his lofty will and pleasure!", "아니, 그의 드높은 뜻과 의향에 누가 오래 맞설 수 있단 말인가!"], ["He had resolved to conquer her, and look here!", "그는 그녀를 정복하기로 결심했고, 자, 보라, 이 꼴이렷다!"]], [["“You will further please, Madam,”", "\"또한 부탁하건대, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, in a tone of sovereign command, “to understand distinctly, that I am to be deferred to and obeyed.", "돔비 씨가 군주의 명령 같은 어조로 말했다, \"내가 떠받들어지고 복종받아야 할 사람이라는 것을 분명히 이해해 주시오."], ["That I must have a positive show and confession of deference before the world, Madam.", "세상 앞에서 명백한 경의의 표시와 고백이 내게 있어야 한다는 것을 말이오, 부인."], ["I am used to this.", "나는 이것에 익숙하오."], ["I require it as my right.", "나는 그것을 내 권리로서 요구하오."], ["In short I will have it.", "한마디로, 나는 그것을 가져야겠소."], ["I consider it no unreasonable return for the worldly advancement that has befallen you; and I believe nobody will be surprised, either at its being required from you, or at your making it.—To Me—To Me!”", "나는 그것을, 당신에게 닥쳐온 세속적 출세에 대한 부당하지 않은 보답이라고 여기오. 그리고 그것이 당신에게 요구되는 것에도, 당신이 그것을 행하는 것에도, 아무도 놀라지 않으리라 믿소.—나에게—나에게 말이오!\""], ["he added, with emphasis.", "그가 힘주어 덧붙였다."]], [["No word from her.", "그녀에게서는 한마디도 없었다."], ["No change in her.", "그녀에게서는 아무 변화도 없었다."], ["Her eyes upon him.", "그녀의 눈은 그에게 고정되어 있었다."]], [["“I have learnt from your mother, Mrs Dombey,”", "\"나는 당신 어머니에게서 들었소, 돔비 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with magisterial importance, “what no doubt you know, namely, that Brighton is recommended for her health.", "돔비 씨가 위압적인 무게를 실어 말했다, \"당신도 분명 알고 있는 것을 말이오. 다름 아니라, 어머니의 건강에는 브라이턴이 권장된다는 것 말이오."], ["Mr Carker has been so good.”", "카커 씨가 친절을 베풀어 주었소.\""]], [["She changed suddenly.", "그녀가 갑자기 변했다."], ["Her face and bosom glowed as if the red light of an angry sunset had been flung upon them.", "그녀의 얼굴과 가슴이, 마치 성난 석양의 붉은 빛이 거기 던져지기라도 한 듯 달아올랐다."], ["Not unobservant of the change, and putting his own interpretation upon it, Mr Dombey resumed:", "그 변화를 놓치지 않고 거기에 자기 나름의 해석을 붙이며, 돔비 씨가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Mr Carker has been so good as to go down and secure a house there, for a time.", "\"카커 씨가 친절을 베풀어, 거기 내려가 한동안 지낼 집을 한 채 마련해 주었소."], ["On the return of the establishment to London, I shall take such steps for its better management as I consider necessary.", "식솔들이 런던으로 돌아오면, 나는 그 집안을 더 잘 관리하기 위해 내가 필요하다고 여기는 조치들을 취할 것이오."], ["One of these, will be the engagement at Brighton (if it is to be effected), of a very respectable reduced person there, a Mrs Pipchin, formerly employed in a situation of trust in my family, to act as housekeeper.", "그중 하나는, 브라이턴에서 (그것이 성사된다면) 매우 점잖게 영락한 사람 하나를, 곧 전에 우리 집안에서 막중한 자리를 맡았던 핍친 부인이라는 이를 가정부로 고용하는 일이 될 것이오."], ["An establishment like this, presided over but nominally, Mrs Dombey, requires a competent head.”", "이 정도 규모의 집안이 그저 명목상으로만 관리되어서는, 돔비 부인, 유능한 우두머리가 필요한 법이오.\""]], [["She had changed her attitude before he arrived at these words, and now sat—still looking at him fixedly—turning a bracelet round and round upon her arm; not winding it about with a light, womanly touch, but pressing and dragging it over the smooth skin, until the white limb showed a bar of red.", "그가 이 말에 이르기 전에 그녀는 이미 자세를 바꾸어, 이제는—여전히 그를 한결같이 바라보면서—제 팔에 두른 팔찌를 빙글빙글 돌리며 앉아 있었다. 여자다운 가벼운 손길로 돌리는 것이 아니라, 매끄러운 살갗 위로 누르고 끌어당겨, 마침내 그 하얀 팔에 붉은 줄이 드러날 때까지였다."]], [["“I observed,”", "\"내가 보아하니,\""], ["said Mr Dombey—“and this concludes what I deem it necessary to say to you at present, Mrs Dombey—I observed a moment ago, Madam, that my allusion to Mr Carker was received in a peculiar manner.", "돔비 씨가 말했다—\"그리고 이로써 지금 당신에게 말해 둘 필요가 있다고 여기는 것을 끝맺겠소, 돔비 부인—내가 방금 보아하니, 부인, 카커 씨에 대한 내 언급이 묘한 태도로 받아들여졌소. 언젠가 내가 우연히, 그 믿을 만한 대리인 앞에서, 당신이 내 손님들을 맞이하는 방식에 대해 내가 품은 반대를 당신에게 지적했을 때, 당신은 그가 그 자리에 있는 것에 이의를 제기하는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼소."], ["On the occasion of my happening to point out to you, before that confidential agent, the objection I had to your mode of receiving my visitors, you were pleased to object to his presence.", "당신은 그 이의를 극복해야 할 것이오, 부인, 그리고 십중팔구는 그와 비슷한 여러 자리에서 그것에 익숙해져야 할 것이오. 당신 손안에 있는 처방, 곧 나에게 불평의 빌미를 주지 않는 처방을 택하지 않는다면 말이오."], ["You will have to get the better of that objection, Madam, and to accustom yourself to it very probably on many similar occasions; unless you adopt the remedy which is in your own hands, of giving me no cause of complaint.", "카커 씨는,\" 돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["Mr Carker,”", "방금 본 그 감정의 동요 끝에, 그는 자기의 도도한 아내를 굴복시킬 이 수단을 매우 소중히 여기게 되었고, 또 어쩌면 그 신사에게 새롭고 의기양양한 모습으로 자신의 힘을 과시하고 싶은 마음이 얼마간 있었던 것이다."], ["said Mr Dombey, who, after the emotion he had just seen, set great store by this means of reducing his proud wife, and who was perhaps sufficiently willing to exhibit his power to that gentleman in a new and triumphant aspect, “Mr Carker being in my confidence, Mrs Dombey, may very well be in yours to such an extent.", "\"카커 씨가 내 신임을 받고 있으니, 돔비 부인, 그가 그 정도까지는 당신의 신임도 충분히 받을 수 있을 것이오. 바라건대, 돔비 부인,\""], ["I hope, Mrs Dombey,”", "그가 잠시 뒤 말을 이었다. 그 잠깐 사이에, 점점 더 거만해지면서 그는 자기 생각을 더욱 다듬어 두었다."], ["he continued, after a few moments, during which, in his increasing haughtiness, he had improved on his idea, “I may not find it necessary ever to entrust Mr Carker with any message of objection or remonstrance to you; but as it would be derogatory to my position and reputation to be frequently holding trivial disputes with a lady upon whom I have conferred the highest distinction that it is in my power to bestow, I shall not scruple to avail myself of his services if I see occasion.”", "\"나로서는 카커 씨에게 당신에 대한 그 어떤 반대나 항의의 전갈을 맡길 필요가 없게 되기를 바라오. 그러나 내가 베풀 수 있는 최고의 영예를 수여한 부인을 상대로 자주 사소한 언쟁을 벌이는 것은 내 지위와 명성을 깎아내리는 일일 터이므로, 필요하다고 여겨지면 나는 거리낌 없이 그의 도움을 빌릴 것이오.\""]], [["“And now,”", "\"자, 이제,\""], ["he thought, rising in his moral magnificence, and rising a stiffer and more impenetrable man than ever, “she knows me and my resolution.”", "그는 자신의 도덕적 장엄함 속에서 일어서며—어느 때보다도 더 뻣뻣하고 더 뚫고 들어갈 수 없는 사내가 되어 일어서며—생각했다. \"그녀는 나를, 그리고 나의 결심을 알았겠지.\""]], [["The hand that had so pressed the bracelet was laid heavily upon her breast, but she looked at him still, with an unaltered face, and said in a low voice:", "그토록 팔찌를 눌러 대던 손이 그녀의 가슴 위에 무겁게 얹혔다. 그러나 그녀는 변함없는 얼굴로 여전히 그를 바라보았고, 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Wait! For God’s sake!", "\"기다리세요!"], ["I must speak to you.”", "제발! 당신에게 할 말이 있어요.\""]], [["Why did she not, and what was the inward struggle that rendered her incapable of doing so, for minutes, while, in the strong constraint she put upon her face, it was as fixed as any statue’s—looking upon him with neither yielding nor unyielding, liking nor hatred, pride not humility: nothing but a searching gaze?", "어찌하여 그녀는 그러지 못했던가. 그리고 그녀로 하여금 몇 분 동안 그러지 못하게 만든 그 내면의 몸부림은 무엇이었던가. 그동안 그녀가 제 얼굴에 가한 그 강한 억제 속에서, 그 얼굴은 어떤 조각상처럼이나 굳어 있었다—굽힘도 굽히지 않음도, 호의도 증오도, 자존심도 비굴함도 없이, 오직 무언가를 찾는 응시만으로 그를 바라보면서."]], [["“Did I ever tempt you to seek my hand?", "\"제가 언제 당신더러 제 손을 구하라고 꾄 적이 있나요?"], ["Did I ever use any art to win you?", "제가 언제 당신을 얻으려고 무슨 술책이라도 부린 적이 있나요?"], ["Was I ever more conciliating to you when you pursued me, than I have been since our marriage?", "당신이 저를 쫓아다닐 때, 제가 결혼한 뒤보다 당신에게 더 살갑게 군 적이 있나요?"], ["Was I ever other to you than I am?”", "제가 지금의 저와 다른 모습으로 당신을 대한 적이 있나요?\""]], [["“It is wholly unnecessary, Madam,”", "\"그런 논의에 들어가는 것은, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “to enter upon such discussions.”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"전혀 불필요하오.\""]], [["“Did you think I loved you?", "\"제가 당신을 사랑한다고 생각하셨나요?"], ["Did you know I did not?", "제가 사랑하지 않는다는 걸 아셨나요?"], ["Did you ever care, Man!", "당신은, 이 사람아!"], ["for my heart, or propose to yourself to win the worthless thing?", "제 마음 따위에 마음 쓴 적이라도 있나요, 아니면 그 하찮은 것을 얻겠다고 스스로 마음먹은 적이라도 있나요?"], ["Was there any poor pretence of any in our bargain?", "우리 거래에 사랑인 척하는 가련한 시늉이라도 있었던가요?"], ["Upon your side, or on mine?”", "당신 쪽에든, 제 쪽에든?\""]], [["“These questions,”", "\"이런 질문들은,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “are all wide of the purpose, Madam.”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"모두 핵심에서 한참 벗어난 것이오, 부인.\""]], [["She moved between him and the door to prevent his going away, and drawing her majestic figure to its height, looked steadily upon him still.", "그녀는 그가 떠나지 못하도록 그와 문 사이로 옮겨 서서, 자기 위풍당당한 자태를 한껏 곧추세우고는, 여전히 그를 한결같이 바라보았다."]], [["“You answer each of them.", "\"당신은 그 질문들 하나하나에 답을 하고 있어요."], ["You answer me before I speak, I see.", "제가 말하기도 전에 당신은 제게 답하고 있어요, 다 보여요."], ["How can you help it; you who know the miserable truth as well as I?", "어찌 안 그럴 수 있겠어요. 저만큼이나 그 비참한 진실을 잘 아는 당신인데."], ["Now, tell me.", "자, 말해 보세요."], ["If I loved you to devotion, could I do more than render up my whole will and being to you, as you have just demanded?", "만일 제가 당신을 헌신적으로 사랑한다 한들, 방금 당신이 요구한 대로 제 모든 의지와 존재를 당신에게 바치는 것보다 더 할 수 있는 게 있겠어요?"], ["If my heart were pure and all untried, and you its idol, could you ask more; could you have more?”", "만일 제 마음이 순결하고 한 번도 시험받지 않은 것이며 당신이 그 마음의 우상이라 한들, 당신이 그 이상을 요구할 수 있겠어요. 그 이상을 가질 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["“Possibly not, Madam,”", "\"아마 그럴 수 없겠지,\""], ["he returned coolly.", "그가 차갑게 응수했다."]], [["“You know how different I am.", "\"당신은 제가 얼마나 다른지 알고 있어요."], ["You see me looking on you now, and you can read the warmth of passion for you that is breathing in my face.”", "당신은 지금 제가 당신을 바라보는 것을 보고 있고, 제 얼굴에 어려 숨 쉬는 당신을 향한 정열의 뜨거움을 읽을 수 있을 거예요.\""], ["Not a curl of the proud lip, not a flash of the dark eye, nothing but the same intent and searching look, accompanied these words.", "도도한 입술의 단 한 번의 비틀림도, 검은 눈동자의 단 한 번의 번득임도 없이, 오직 그 똑같이 골똘하고 무언가를 찾는 눈길만이 이 말들과 함께했다."], ["“You know my general history.", "\"당신은 제 지난 내력을 다 알아요."], ["You have spoken of my mother.", "당신은 제 어머니에 대해 말했죠."], ["Do you think you can degrade, or bend or break, me to submission and obedience?”", "당신은 저를 깎아내리거나, 굽히거나, 꺾어서 순종과 복종에 이르게 할 수 있다고 생각하나요?\""]], [["Mr Dombey smiled, as he might have smiled at an inquiry whether he thought he could raise ten thousand pounds.", "돔비 씨는, 자기가 만 파운드를 마련할 수 있다고 생각하느냐는 물음에 지을 법한 그런 미소를 지었다."]], [["“If there is anything unusual here,”", "\"만일 여기 무언가 예사롭지 않은 것이 있다면,\""], ["she said, with a slight motion of her hand before her brow, which did not for a moment flinch from its immovable and otherwise expressionless gaze, “as I know there are unusual feelings here,”", "그녀가, 그 미동도 없고 그 밖에는 아무런 표정도 없는 응시에서 한순간도 움츠러들지 않은 채, 이마 앞에서 손을 살짝 움직이며 말했다, \"여기 예사롭지 않은 감정들이 있다는 것을 제가 아는 것처럼,\""], ["raising the hand she pressed upon her bosom, and heavily returning it, “consider that there is no common meaning in the appeal I am going to make you.", "가슴 위에 누르고 있던 손을 들어 올렸다가 다시 무겁게 내려놓으며, \"제가 지금 당신에게 하려는 호소에 예사로운 의미가 담겨 있지 않다는 것을 헤아려 주세요."], ["Yes, for I am going;”", "그래요, 제가 하려고 하니까요.\""], ["she said it as in prompt reply to something in his face; “to appeal to you.”", "그녀는 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 무언가에 즉각 대답이라도 하듯 이렇게 말했다. \"당신에게 호소하려고요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey, with a slightly condescending bend of his chin that rustled and crackled his stiff cravat, sat down on a sofa that was near him, to hear the appeal.", "돔비 씨는 빳빳한 크라바트가 사각거리고 바스락거리도록 턱을 조금 거들먹거리며 숙이고는, 그 호소를 들으려고 가까이 있던 소파에 앉았다."]], [["“If you can believe that I am of such a nature now,”—he fancied he saw tears glistening in her eyes, and he thought, complacently, that he had forced them from her, though none fell on her cheek, and she regarded him as steadily as ever,—“as would make what I now say almost incredible to myself, said to any man who had become my husband, but, above all, said to you, you may, perhaps, attach the greater weight to it.", "\"만일 당신이, 제가 지금 이런 천성을 지닌 사람이라고 믿어 줄 수 있다면,\"—그는 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 반짝이는 것을 본 것 같았고, 비록 그녀의 뺨에는 한 방울도 떨어지지 않았고 그녀가 여전히 한결같이 그를 바라보았음에도, 자기가 그녀에게서 그 눈물을 끌어냈다고 흐뭇하게 생각했다—\"곧 제가 지금 하는 이 말을 저 자신조차 거의 믿을 수 없게 만들 그런 천성을, 그러니까 제 남편이 된 어떤 남자에게라도, 그러나 그 누구보다도 당신에게 하는 이 말을 거의 믿을 수 없게 만들 그런 천성을 지닌 사람이라고 믿어 준다면, 당신은 어쩌면 이 말에 더 무게를 둘 수 있을 거예요."], ["In the dark end to which we are tending, and may come, we shall not involve ourselves alone (that might not be much) but others.”", "우리가 향해 가고 있고, 또 닥칠지도 모를 그 어두운 끝에서, 우리는 우리 자신만 끌어들이는 게 아니라(그건 그리 대단치 않을지도 모르죠) 다른 이들까지 끌어들이게 될 거예요.\""]], [["Others!", "다른 이들이라!"], ["He knew at whom that word pointed, and frowned heavily.", "그는 그 말이 누구를 가리키는지 알았고, 무겁게 미간을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“I speak to you for the sake of others.", "\"저는 다른 이들을 위해 당신에게 말하는 거예요."], ["Also your own sake; and for mine.", "또한 당신 자신을 위해, 그리고 저를 위해서요."], ["Since our marriage, you have been arrogant to me; and I have repaid you in kind.", "결혼한 이래로 당신은 저에게 거만하게 굴었고, 저는 똑같이 갚아 주었어요."], ["You have shown to me and everyone around us, every day and hour, that you think I am graced and distinguished by your alliance.", "당신은 저에게, 그리고 우리 주위의 모든 이에게, 날마다 시시각각, 당신이 저와 맺어진 것으로 제가 우아해지고 격이 높아졌다고 여긴다는 것을 보여 주었어요."], ["I do not think so, and have shown that too.", "저는 그렇게 생각하지 않고, 그것 또한 보여 주었죠."], ["It seems you do not understand, or (so far as your power can go) intend that each of us shall take a separate course; and you expect from me instead, a homage you will never have.”", "당신은 우리 둘이 각자의 길을 가는 것을 이해하지 못하거나, (당신 힘이 미치는 한) 그러도록 둘 작정이 없는 모양이에요. 그러면서 그 대신 저에게서, 당신이 결코 얻지 못할 그런 경의를 기대하고 있죠.\""]], [["Although her face was still the same, there was emphatic confirmation of this “Never” in the very breath she drew.", "비록 그녀의 얼굴은 여전히 그대로였지만, 그녀가 들이쉰 바로 그 숨결 속에 이 \"결코\"라는 말의 강조된 확언이 담겨 있었다."]], [["“I feel no tenderness towards you; that you know.", "\"저는 당신에게 아무런 다정함도 느끼지 않아요. 그건 당신도 알죠."], ["You would care nothing for it, if I did or could.", "제가 그것을 느끼거나 느낄 수 있다 한들, 당신은 거기에 조금도 마음 쓰지 않을 거예요."], ["I know as well that you feel none towards me.", "당신이 저에게 아무런 다정함도 느끼지 않는다는 것 또한 저는 잘 알고요."], ["But we are linked together; and in the knot that ties us, as I have said, others are bound up.", "그러나 우리는 함께 묶여 있고, 우리를 묶고 있는 그 매듭 속에는, 제가 말했듯, 다른 이들도 함께 얽혀 있어요."], ["We must both die; we are both connected with the dead already, each by a little child.", "우리는 둘 다 죽어야 하고, 우리는 둘 다 이미 죽은 이와, 저마다 한 어린 자식으로 이어져 있어요."], ["Let us forbear.”", "우리 서로 삼가도록 해요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey took a long respiration, as if he would have said, Oh!", "돔비 씨는, 마치 오!"], ["was this all!", "고작 이게 전부였단 말인가, 하고 말하려는 듯, 길게 숨을 들이쉬었다."]], [["“There is no wealth,” she went on, turning paler as she watched him, while her eyes grew yet more lustrous in their earnestness, “that could buy these words of me, and the meaning that belongs to them.", "\"이 말들과, 거기에 담긴 그 의미를 저에게서 사 갈 수 있는 부는 세상에 없어요,\""], ["Once cast away as idle breath, no wealth or power can bring them back.", "그녀가, 그를 지켜보면서 한층 창백해지는 한편 그 진지함 속에서 두 눈은 더욱 빛을 더하며 말을 이었다. \"한번 부질없는 숨결로 던져 버리고 나면, 어떤 부나 권력으로도 그것들을 되불러 올 수는 없어요."], ["I mean them; I have weighed them; and I will be true to what I undertake.", "저는 진심이에요. 저는 이 말들을 저울질해 보았고, 제가 떠맡는 것에 충실할 거예요."], ["If you will promise to forbear on your part, I will promise to forbear on mine.", "만일 당신이 당신 쪽에서 삼가겠다고 약속한다면, 저도 제 쪽에서 삼가겠다고 약속하겠어요. 우리는 더없이 불행한 한 쌍이에요."], ["We are a most unhappy pair, in whom, from different causes, every sentiment that blesses marriage, or justifies it, is rooted out; but in the course of time, some friendship, or some fitness for each other, may arise between us.", "서로 다른 까닭에서, 결혼을 축복하거나 정당화하는 온갖 정서가 우리 안에서 뿌리째 뽑혀 버린 한 쌍이죠. 그러나 시간이 흐르면, 우리 사이에 얼마간의 우정이나, 얼마간 서로 어울림이 생겨날지도 몰라요."], ["I will try to hope so, if you will make the endeavour too; and I will look forward to a better and a happier use of age than I have made of youth or prime.”", "만일 당신도 그 노력을 기울인다면, 저도 그렇게 되기를 바라 보려고 애쓰겠어요. 그리고 저는, 제가 젊음이나 한창때를 써 온 것보다 더 낫고 더 행복하게 노년을 쓰게 되기를 바라보겠어요.\""]], [["Throughout she had spoken in a low plain voice, that neither rose nor fell; ceasing, she dropped the hand with which she had enforced herself to be so passionless and distinct, but not the eyes with which she had so steadily observed him.", "처음부터 끝까지 그녀는 오르지도 내리지도 않는, 낮고 담담한 목소리로 말했다. 말을 멈추면서, 그녀는 스스로를 그토록 정념 없고 또렷하게 다스리려고 동원했던 그 손은 떨어뜨렸으나, 그토록 한결같이 그를 살펴 온 그 눈만은 거두지 않았다."]], [["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with his utmost dignity, “I cannot entertain any proposal of this extraordinary nature.”", "돔비 씨가 더없는 위엄을 부리며 말했다, \"나는 이런 별스러운 종류의 어떤 제안도 받아들일 수 없소.\""]], [["She looked at him yet, without the least change.", "그녀는 여전히, 조금의 변화도 없이 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“I cannot,”", "\"나는,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, rising as he spoke, “consent to temporise or treat with you, Mrs Dombey, upon a subject as to which you are in possession of my opinions and expectations.", "돔비 씨가 말하면서 일어섰다, \"당신이 내 의견과 기대를 이미 알고 있는 사안을 두고 당신과 시간을 끌거나 흥정하는 데에 동의할 수 없소, 돔비 부인."], ["I have stated my ultimatum, Madam, and have only to request your very serious attention to it.”", "나는 내 최후통첩을 밝혔으니, 부인, 그것에 매우 진지하게 주의를 기울여 주기를 요청할 따름이오.\""]], [["To see the face change to its old expression, deepened in intensity!", "그 얼굴이 예전의 표정으로, 한층 짙어진 강렬함으로 변하는 것을 보다니!"], ["To see the eyes droop as from some mean and odious object!", "그 눈이 무언가 비천하고 역겨운 대상에게서 그러듯 떨구어지는 것을 보다니!"], ["To see the lighting of the haughty brow!", "그 도도한 이마에 불이 켜지는 것을 보다니!"], ["To see scorn, anger, indignation, and abhorrence starting into sight, and the pale blank earnestness vanish like a mist!", "멸시와 분노와 격분과 혐오가 솟아나고, 창백하고 텅 빈 진지함이 안개처럼 사라지는 것을 보다니!"], ["He could not choose but look, although he looked to his dismay.", "그는 보지 않을 도리가 없었다. 비록 보면서 경악했음에도."]], [["“Go, Sir!”", "\"가세요!\""], ["she said, pointing with an imperious hand towards the door.", "그녀가 도도한 손으로 문 쪽을 가리키며 말했다."], ["“Our first and last confidence is at an end.", "\"우리의 처음이자 마지막인 흉금을 트는 일은 끝났어요."], ["Nothing can make us stranger to each other than we are henceforth.”", "그 무엇으로도 우리는 이제부터의 우리보다 더 서로 남남이 될 수는 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“I shall take my rightful course, Madam,”", "\"나는 내 정당한 길을 갈 것이오, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “undeterred, you may be sure, by any general declamation.”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"어떤 일반적인 열변에도 흔들리지 않고 말이오, 부디 그리 아시오.\""]], [["She turned her back upon him, and, without reply, sat down before her glass.", "그녀는 그에게 등을 돌리고는, 아무 대꾸 없이 거울 앞에 앉았다."]], [["“I place my reliance on your improved sense of duty, and more correct feeling, and better reflection, Madam,”", "\"나는 당신의 한층 나아진 의무감과, 더 올바른 감정과, 더 나은 성찰을 믿겠소, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["She answered not one word.", "그녀는 단 한마디도 대답하지 않았다."], ["He saw no more expression of any heed of him, in the mirror, than if he had been an unseen spider on the wall, or beetle on the floor, or rather, than if he had been the one or other, seen and crushed when she last turned from him, and forgotten among the ignominious and dead vermin of the ground.", "그는 거울 속에서, 마치 자기가 벽에 붙은 보이지 않는 거미나 바닥에 기는 딱정벌레라도 된 양—아니, 차라리 그녀가 마지막으로 그에게서 등을 돌렸을 때 보였다가 짓밟혀, 땅바닥의 치욕스럽고 죽은 벌레들 틈에 잊힌 그 거미나 딱정벌레라도 된 양—그를 조금이라도 거들떠보는 기색을 더는 보지 못했다."]], [["He looked back, as he went out at the door, upon the well-lighted and luxurious room, the beautiful and glittering objects everywhere displayed, the shape of Edith in its rich dress seated before her glass, and the face of Edith as the glass presented it to him; and betook himself to his old chamber of cogitation, carrying away with him a vivid picture in his mind of all these things, and a rambling and unaccountable speculation (such as sometimes comes into a man’s head) how they would all look when he saw them next.", "그는 문으로 나가면서, 환히 밝혀진 사치스러운 방을, 사방에 펼쳐진 아름답고 반짝이는 물건들을, 거울 앞에 앉은 화려한 드레스 차림의 에디스의 자태를, 그리고 거울이 그에게 비추어 보인 에디스의 얼굴을 돌아보았다. 그러고는 이 모든 것의 생생한 그림과, (이따금 사람의 머릿속에 떠오르곤 하는) 종잡을 수 없고 까닭 모를 어떤 상념을—다음번에 이것들을 볼 때 모두 어떤 모습일까 하는 상념을—마음에 담은 채, 사색에 잠기던 자기 옛 방으로 향했다."]], [["For the rest, Mr Dombey was very taciturn, and very dignified, and very confident of carrying out his purpose; and remained so.", "그 밖에는, 돔비 씨는 매우 과묵했고, 매우 위엄 있었으며, 자기 목적을 관철하리라고 매우 자신만만했다. 그리고 그는 줄곧 그러했다."]], [["He did not design accompanying the family to Brighton; but he graciously informed Cleopatra at breakfast, on the morning of departure, which arrived a day or two afterwards, that he might be expected down, soon.", "그는 식솔들을 따라 브라이턴에 갈 작정은 아니었다. 그러나 하루 이틀 뒤 출발하던 날 아침, 그는 식사 자리에서 클레오파트라에게 자기가 곧 내려갈 것으로 기대해도 좋다고 너그럽게 알려 주었다."], ["There was no time to be lost in getting Cleopatra to any place recommended as being salutary; for, indeed, she seemed upon the wane, and turning of the earth, earthy.", "클레오파트라를 어디든 몸에 좋다고 권장되는 곳으로 데려가는 데에는 지체할 겨를이 없었다. 사실 그녀는 기우는 듯했고, 흙으로, 흙답게 돌아가고 있었으니까."]], [["Without having undergone any decided second attack of her malady, the old woman seemed to have crawled backward in her recovery from the first.", "이렇다 할 두 번째 발작을 겪지는 않았지만, 그 늙은 여인은 첫 번째 발작에서 회복하던 것이 도리어 뒷걸음질 친 듯 보였다."], ["She was more lean and shrunken, more uncertain in her imbecility, and made stranger confusions in her mind and memory.", "그녀는 더 야위고 쪼그라들었으며, 그 치매가 더 종잡을 수 없어지고, 마음과 기억 속에서 더 기묘한 혼란을 빚어냈다."], ["Among other symptoms of this last affliction, she fell into the habit of confounding the names of her two sons-in-law, the living and the deceased; and in general called Mr Dombey, either “Grangeby,”", "이 마지막 병증의 여러 징후 가운데 하나로, 그녀는 살아 있는 사위와 죽은 사위, 두 사위의 이름을 뒤섞는 버릇에 빠져들었다."], ["or “Domber,” or indifferently, both.", "그래서 대개 돔비 씨를 \"그레인지비\"라거나 \"돔버\"라거나, 또는 그 둘을 가리지 않고 함께 불렀다."]], [["But she was youthful, very youthful still; and in her youthfulness appeared at breakfast, before going away, in a new bonnet made express, and a travelling robe that was embroidered and braided like an old baby’s.", "그러나 그녀는 젊었다, 여전히 매우 젊었다. 그리고 그 젊음을 뽐내며 그녀는 떠나기 전 아침 식사 자리에, 특별히 맞춘 새 보닛에, 늙은 갓난아이의 것처럼 수놓고 장식 끈을 두른 여행복 차림으로 나타났다."], ["It was not easy to put her into a fly-away bonnet now, or to keep the bonnet in its place on the back of her poor nodding head, when it was got on.", "이제는 그녀에게 날아갈 듯한 보닛을 씌우는 일도, 일단 씌워 놓은 보닛을 그 가엾게 끄덕이는 머리 뒤에 제자리에 붙들어 두는 일도 쉽지 않았다."], ["In this instance, it had not only the extraneous effect of being always on one side, but of being perpetually tapped on the crown by Flowers the maid, who attended in the background during breakfast to perform that duty.", "이번 경우 그것은 늘 한쪽으로 기울어져 있다는 별스러운 효과뿐 아니라, 하녀 플라워스가 끊임없이 그 꼭대기를 톡톡 두드린다는 효과까지 자아냈는데, 플라워스는 그 임무를 수행하느라 아침 식사 내내 뒤편에 시중들고 서 있었다."]], [["“Now, my dearest Grangeby,”", "\"자, 내 사랑하는 그레인지비,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, “you must posively prom,”", "스큐턴 부인이 말했다, \"너 꼭 약속해 줘야 해,\""], ["she cut some of her words short, and cut out others altogether, “come down very soon.”", "그녀는 어떤 말은 토막 내고 어떤 말은 아예 잘라먹었다, \"아주 빨리 내려오겠다고.\""]], [["“I said just now, Madam,”", "\"방금 말씀드렸습니다만, 부인,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, loudly and laboriously, “that I am coming in a day or two.”", "돔비 씨가 큰 소리로 힘들여 응수했다, \"하루 이틀 안에 가겠습니다.\""]], [["“Bless you, Domber!”", "\"고맙구나, 돔버!\""]], [["Here the Major, who was come to take leave of the ladies, and who was staring through his apoplectic eyes at Mrs Skewton’s face with the disinterested composure of an immortal being, said:", "이때, 부인들에게 작별 인사를 하러 온 소령이, 불사의 존재가 지을 법한 초연한 침착함으로 졸중에 걸린 두 눈을 부릅뜨고 스큐턴 부인의 얼굴을 빤히 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Begad, Ma’am, you don’t ask old Joe to come!”", "\"이런, 마님, 이 늙은 조에게는 오라고 안 하시는군요!\""]], [["“Sterious wretch, who’s he?”", "\"수상쩍은 인간 같으니, 저게 누구지?\""], ["lisped Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 혀 짧은 소리로 말했다."], ["But a tap on the bonnet from Flowers seeming to jog her memory, she added, “Oh!", "그러나 플라워스가 보닛을 톡 두드린 것이 그녀의 기억을 흔들어 깨운 듯, 그녀가 덧붙였다, \"오!"], ["You mean yourself, you naughty creature!”", "당신 자신을 말하는 거구려, 이 짓궂은 양반아!\""]], [["“Devilish queer, Sir,”", "\"고약하리만치 이상하군요, 선생,\""], ["whispered the Major to Mr Dombey.", "소령이 돔비 씨에게 속삭였다. \"안 좋은 경우예요."], ["“Bad case. Never did wrap up enough;”", "결코 충분히 싸매질 않으셨거든요.\""], ["the Major being buttoned to the chin.", "소령은 턱까지 단추를 채우고 있었다."], ["“Why who should J.", "\"아니, J."], ["B. mean by Joe, but old Joe Bagstock—Joseph—your slave—Joe, Ma’am?", "B.가 조라고 하면 늙은 조 백스톡 말고 누구를 가리키겠소—조지프—마님의 종—조 말이오, 마님?"], ["Here!", "여기!"], ["Here’s the man!", "바로 이 사람이오!"], ["Here are the Bagstock bellows, Ma’am!”", "여기 백스톡의 풀무가 있소이다, 마님!\""], ["cried the Major, striking himself a sounding blow on the chest.", "소령이 자기 가슴을 쩌렁쩌렁 울리도록 한 대 치며 외쳤다."]], [["“My dearest Edith—Grangeby—it’s most trordinry thing,”", "\"내 사랑하는 에디스—그레인지비—참 별스러운 일이지,\""], ["said Cleopatra, pettishly, “that Major—”", "클레오파트라가 짜증스럽게 말했다, \"저 소령이—\""]], [["“Bagstock!", "\"백스톡! J."], ["J. B.!”", "B.!\""], ["cried the Major, seeing that she faltered for his name.", "소령이, 그녀가 자기 이름을 더듬는 것을 보고 외쳤다."]], [["“Well, it don’t matter,”", "\"뭐, 아무려면 어때,\""], ["said Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 말했다."], ["“Edith, my love, you know I never could remember names—what was it?", "\"에디스, 얘야, 너도 알다시피 나는 통 이름을 못 외운다니까—그게 뭐였더라?"], ["oh!—most trordinry thing that so many people want to come down to see me.", "오!—참 별스러운 일이야, 그렇게 많은 사람들이 나를 보러 내려오고 싶어 하다니."], ["I’m not going for long.", "나 오래 가는 거 아니야."], ["I’m coming back.", "돌아올 거라고."], ["Surely they can wait, till I come back!”", "그 사람들도 내가 돌아올 때까지는 분명 기다릴 수 있겠지!\""]], [["Cleopatra looked all round the table as she said it, and appeared very uneasy.", "클레오파트라는 이렇게 말하면서 식탁을 죽 둘러보았고, 매우 불안해 보였다."]], [["“I won’t have visitors—really don’t want visitors,”", "\"손님들은 안 받을 거야—정말이지 손님은 바라지 않아,\""], ["she said; “little repose—and all that sort of thing—is what I quire.", "그녀가 말했다. \"약간의 휴식—그런 종류의 것 말이야—그게 내가 필요로 하는 거라고."], ["No odious brutes must proach me till I’ve shaken off this numbness;”", "이 마비가 떨어져 나갈 때까지는 어떤 역겨운 짐승들도 나한테 접근해선 안 돼.\""], ["and in a grisly resumption of her coquettish ways, she made a dab at the Major with her fan, but overset Mr Dombey’s breakfast cup instead, which was in quite a different direction.", "그러고는 그 교태 부리던 버릇을 으스스하게 되살려 부채로 소령을 톡 치는 시늉을 했지만, 엉뚱하게도 사뭇 다른 방향에 있던 돔비 씨의 찻잔을 엎질러 버렸다."]], [["Then she called for Withers, and charged him to see particularly that word was left about some trivial alterations in her room, which must be all made before she came back, and which must be set about immediately, as there was no saying how soon she might come back; for she had a great many engagements, and all sorts of people to call upon. Withers received these directions with becoming deference, and gave his guarantee for their execution; but when he withdrew a pace or two behind her, it appeared as if he couldn’t help looking strangely at the Major, who couldn’t help looking strangely at Mr Dombey, who couldn’t help looking strangely at Cleopatra, who couldn’t help nodding her bonnet over one eye, and rattling her knife and fork upon her plate in using them, as if she were playing castanets.", "그러더니 그녀는 위더스를 불러, 자기 방의 사소한 손질 몇 가지에 대해 말을 남겨 두도록 각별히 챙기라고 분부했다. 그것은 자기가 돌아오기 전에 모두 끝나 있어야 하고, 또 자기가 얼마나 빨리 돌아올지 알 수 없으니 당장 착수해야 한다는 것이었다. 약속이 매우 많고, 찾아가 보아야 할 온갖 사람들이 있다면서. 위더스는 합당한 공손함으로 이 지시들을 받아들이고, 그것을 이행하겠노라 보증했다. 그러나 그가 그녀에게서 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러나자, 그는 소령을 묘하게 바라보지 않을 수 없는 듯했고, 소령은 돔비 씨를 묘하게 바라보지 않을 수 없는 듯했으며, 돔비 씨는 클레오파트라를 묘하게 바라보지 않을 수 없는 듯했고, 클레오파트라는 보닛을 한쪽 눈 위로 끄덕이지 않을 수 없는 듯, 또 나이프와 포크를 쓰면서 접시에 달그락거리며 마치 캐스터네츠라도 치는 듯하지 않을 수 없는 듯했다."]], [["Edith alone never lifted her eyes to any face at the table, and never seemed dismayed by anything her mother said or did.", "오직 에디스만이 식탁의 어느 얼굴에도 눈을 들지 않았고, 어머니가 무슨 말을 하거나 무슨 짓을 해도 결코 당혹스러워하는 기색이 없었다. 그녀는 어머니의 두서없는 이야기에 귀를 기울이거나, 적어도 말을 걸어오면 어머니 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다."], ["She listened to her disjointed talk, or at least, turned her head towards her when addressed; replied in a few low words when necessary; and sometimes stopped her when she was rambling, or brought her thoughts back with a monosyllable, to the point from which they had strayed.", "필요할 때면 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디 대답했고, 때로는 어머니가 횡설수설하면 멈추게 하거나, 한 음절의 말로 그 빗나간 생각을 본디 자리로 되돌려 놓았다. 그러나 어머니는, 다른 일에는 아무리 종잡을 수 없어도, 이 한 가지에는 한결같았으니—언제나 딸을 눈여겨본다는 것이었다."], ["The mother, however unsteady in other things, was constant in this—that she was always observant of her.", "그녀는 그 대리석 같은 고요함과 준엄함을 띤 아름다운 얼굴을, 어떤 때는 일종의 두려운 감탄으로, 어떤 때는 그 얼굴을 미소로 움직여 보려는 실없고 어리석은 노력으로, 어떤 때는 그 얼굴에게 자기가 무시당하고 있다고 여기며 변덕스러운 눈물과 질투 어린 머리 흔듦으로 바라보았다."], ["She would look at the beautiful face, in its marble stillness and severity, now with a kind of fearful admiration; now in a giggling foolish effort to move it to a smile; now with capricious tears and jealous shakings of her head, as imagining herself neglected by it; always with an attraction towards it, that never fluctuated like her other ideas, but had constant possession of her.", "늘 그 얼굴을 향한 어떤 끌림과 더불어서였는데, 그 끌림은 그녀의 다른 생각들처럼 출렁이는 법이 없이 한결같이 그녀를 사로잡고 있었다. 에디스에게서 그녀는 때때로 플로렌스에게로 눈을 옮겼다가, 다시 에디스에게로 되돌아오곤 했는데, 그 모양이 자못 거칠었다."], ["From Edith she would sometimes look at Florence, and back again at Edith, in a manner that was wild enough; and sometimes she would try to look elsewhere, as if to escape from her daughter’s face; but back to it she seemed forced to come, although it never sought hers unless sought, or troubled her with one single glance.", "또 때로는 딸의 얼굴에서 벗어나려는 듯 다른 데를 보려고 애쓰기도 했다. 그러나 그 얼굴은 청하지 않는 한 어머니의 얼굴을 찾지 않았고 단 한 번의 눈길로도 어머니를 성가시게 한 적이 없었음에도, 그녀는 다시 그 얼굴로 되돌아오지 않을 수 없는 듯했다."]], [["The breakfast concluded, Mrs Skewton, affecting to lean girlishly upon the Major’s arm, but heavily supported on the other side by Flowers the maid, and propped up behind by Withers the page, was conducted to the carriage, which was to take her, Florence, and Edith to Brighton.", "아침 식사가 끝나자, 스큐턴 부인은 소령의 팔에 소녀처럼 기대는 척했으나 반대쪽에서는 하녀 플라워스가 무겁게 부축하고 뒤에서는 시동 위더스가 떠받친 채로, 자신과 플로렌스와 에디스를 브라이턴으로 데려갈 마차로 안내되었다."]], [["“And is Joseph absolutely banished?”", "\"그러면 조지프는 정녕 추방당한 겁니까?\""], ["said the Major, thrusting in his purple face over the steps.", "소령이 보랏빛 얼굴을 마차 발판 너머로 들이밀며 말했다."], ["“Damme, Ma’am, is Cleopatra so hard-hearted as to forbid her faithful Antony Bagstock to approach the presence?”", "\"이런, 마님, 클레오파트라께서는 그토록 매정하시어, 충실한 안토니우스 백스톡이 어전에 다가오는 것을 금하신단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Go along!”", "\"저리 가요!\""], ["said Cleopatra, “I can’t bear you.", "클레오파트라가 말했다. \"당신은 못 견디겠어."], ["You shall see me when I come back, if you are very good.”", "당신이 아주 얌전히 굴면, 내가 돌아왔을 때 만나 줄게.\""]], [["“Tell Joseph, he may live in hope, Ma’am,”", "\"조지프에게 희망을 품고 살아도 된다고 전해 주십시오, 마님,\""], ["said the Major; “or he’ll die in despair.”", "소령이 말했다. \"안 그러면 절망 속에 죽을 테니까요.\""]], [["Cleopatra shuddered, and leaned back.", "클레오파트라가 몸을 떨며 등을 기댔다."], ["“Edith, my dear,”", "\"에디스, 얘야,\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Tell him—”", "\"저이에게 전해 줘—\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭘요?\""]], [["“Such dreadful words,”", "\"저렇게 끔찍한 말들을 한다니까,\""], ["said Cleopatra.", "클레오파트라가 말했다."], ["“He uses such dreadful words!”", "\"저이는 저렇게 끔찍한 말을 쓴단 말이야!\""]], [["Edith signed to him to retire, gave the word to go on, and left the objectionable Major to Mr Dombey.", "에디스는 소령에게 물러가라는 신호를 보내고, 출발하라는 분부를 내린 뒤, 그 못마땅한 소령을 돔비 씨에게 떠넘겼다."], ["To whom he returned, whistling.", "소령은 휘파람을 불며 돔비 씨에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["“I’ll tell you what, Sir,”", "\"내 한마디 하리다, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, with his hands behind him, and his legs very wide asunder, “a fair friend of ours has removed to Queer Street.”", "소령이 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 두 다리를 한껏 벌리고 서서 말했다, \"우리의 어여쁜 벗 한 분이 곤경의 거리로 옮겨 갔습니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean, Major?”", "\"무슨 말씀이오, 소령?\""], ["inquired Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 물었다."]], [["“I mean to say, Dombey,”", "\"내 말인즉슨, 돔비,\""], ["returned the Major, “that you’ll soon be an orphan-in-law.”", "소령이 응수했다, \"당신이 곧 인척 잃은 고아가 되리란 거지요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey appeared to relish this waggish description of himself so very little, that the Major wound up with the horse’s cough, as an expression of gravity.", "돔비 씨는 자신을 두고 이렇게 익살맞게 묘사한 것이 어찌나 마뜩잖았던지, 소령은 엄숙함의 표시로 그 말 기침 같은 소리로 끝을 맺었다."]], [["“Damme, Sir,”", "\"이런, 선생,\""], ["said the Major, “there is no use in disguising a fact.", "소령이 말했다, \"사실을 감추어 봐야 소용없소."], ["Joe is blunt, Sir.", "조는 무뚝뚝한 사람이오, 선생."], ["That’s his nature.", "그게 그의 천성이지요."], ["If you take old Josh at all, you take him as you find him; and a devilish rusty, old rasper, of a close-toothed, J.", "늙은 조시를 받아들이려거든, 있는 그대로 받아들여야 합니다. 그러면 당신은 그가 지독히도 녹슨, 이가 촘촘히 박힌 늙은 줄칼, J."], ["B. file, you do find him.", "B.라는 줄칼임을 알게 될 거요."], ["Dombey,”", "돔비,\""], ["said the Major, “your wife’s mother is on the move, Sir.”", "소령이 말했다, \"당신 부인의 어머니가 떠나가고 계십니다, 선생.\""]], [["“I fear,”", "\"내 우려하건대,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, with much philosophy, “that Mrs Skewton is shaken.”", "돔비 씨가 꽤나 철학적인 태도로 응수했다, \"스큐턴 부인이 흔들리고 계시지요.\""]], [["“Shaken, Dombey!”", "\"흔들린다고요, 돔비!\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["“Smashed!”", "\"박살 났지요!\""]], [["“Change, however,”", "\"그러나 환경의 변화와,\""], ["pursued Mr Dombey, “and attention, may do much yet.”", "돔비 씨가 말을 이었다, \"보살핌이 있으면 아직 많은 것을 할 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["“Don’t believe it, Sir,”", "\"믿지 마시오, 선생,\""], ["returned the Major.", "소령이 응수했다."], ["“Damme, Sir, she never wrapped up enough.", "\"이런, 선생, 그분은 결코 충분히 싸매질 않으셨소."], ["If a man don’t wrap up,”", "사람이 싸매지 않으면,\""], ["said the Major, taking in another button of his buff waistcoat, “he has nothing to fall back upon.", "소령이 자기 담황색 조끼의 단추 하나를 더 채우며 말했다, \"기댈 것이 아무것도 없는 법이오. 그러나 어떤 사람들은 기어이 죽으려 들지요."], ["But some people will die.", "기어이 그러고 마는 거요. 이런, 정말 그런다니까."], ["They will do it.", "고집을 부리는 거지요."], ["Damme, they will.", "내 한마디 하리다, 돔비, 그것이 화려하지는 않을지도 모르오."], ["They’re obstinate.", "세련되지 않을지도 모르오."], ["I tell you what, Dombey, it may not be ornamental; it may not be refined; it may be rough and tough; but a little of the genuine old English Bagstock stamina, Sir, would do all the good in the world to the human breed.”", "거칠고 투박할지도 모르오. 그러나 진짜배기 옛 잉글랜드 백스톡의 끈기가 조금만 있으면, 선생, 인간이라는 종족에게 세상에 더없는 좋은 일을 해 줄 거외다.\""]], [["After imparting this precious piece of information, the Major, who was certainly true-blue, whatever other endowments he may have had or wanted, coming within the “genuine old English” classification, which has never been exactly ascertained, took his lobster-eyes and his apoplexy to the club, and choked there all day.", "이 귀중한 정보를 전한 뒤, 다른 어떤 자질을 갖추었든 못 갖추었든 간에 분명 골수 보수파였고, 정확히 규정된 적이 한 번도 없는 그 \"진짜배기 옛 잉글랜드\"라는 분류에 드는 소령은, 그 가재 같은 눈알과 졸중을 데리고 클럽으로 가서, 거기서 온종일 음식에 켁켁거렸다."]], [["Cleopatra, at one time fretful, at another self-complacent, sometimes awake, sometimes asleep, and at all times juvenile, reached Brighton the same night, fell to pieces as usual, and was put away in bed; where a gloomy fancy might have pictured a more potent skeleton than the maid, who should have been one, watching at the rose-coloured curtains, which were carried down to shed their bloom upon her.", "클레오파트라는 어떤 때는 안달복달하다가, 어떤 때는 자기만족에 젖고, 때로는 깨어 있다가, 때로는 잠들고, 어느 때나 어린아이 같은 채로, 그날 밤 브라이턴에 닿아, 여느 때처럼 무너져 내려 침대에 뉘어졌다. 음울한 공상을 부린다면, 마땅히 그래야 할 하녀보다 더 강력한 해골 하나가, 그녀를 위해 그 위에 화사함을 드리우려고 내려진 장밋빛 휘장 곁에서 지켜보고 있는 모습을 그려 볼 법도 했다."]], [["It was settled in high council of medical authority that she should take a carriage airing every day, and that it was important she should get out every day, and walk if she could.", "그녀가 날마다 마차를 타고 바람을 쐬어야 하고, 또 날마다 밖에 나가, 가능하면 걷는 것이 중요하다는 것이 의료 권위자들의 고위 회의에서 결정되었다."], ["Edith was ready to attend her—always ready to attend her, with the same mechanical attention and immovable beauty—and they drove out alone; for Edith had an uneasiness in the presence of Florence, now that her mother was worse, and told Florence, with a kiss, that she would rather they two went alone.", "에디스는 그녀를 모실 채비가 되어 있었다—언제나, 그 똑같은 기계적인 보살핌과 미동 없는 아름다움으로 그녀를 모실 채비가 되어 있었다—그리고 그들은 둘이서만 마차를 타고 나갔다. 어머니의 병세가 더 나빠진 지금, 에디스는 플로렌스가 있으면 마음이 불편했고, 그래서 입맞춤과 함께 플로렌스에게, 자기들 둘이서만 가는 편이 낫겠다고 말했던 것이다."]], [["Mrs Skewton, on one particular day, was in the irresolute, exacting, jealous temper that had developed itself on her recovery from her first attack.", "스큐턴 부인은 어느 특정한 날, 첫 번째 발작에서 회복하면서 드러났던 그 우유부단하고 까다롭고 질투 어린 기분에 잠겨 있었다."], ["After sitting silent in the carriage watching Edith for some time, she took her hand and kissed it passionately.", "마차 안에 한동안 말없이 앉아 에디스를 지켜보던 그녀가, 딸의 손을 잡고 정열적으로 입을 맞추었다."], ["The hand was neither given nor withdrawn, but simply yielded to her raising of it, and being released, dropped down again, almost as if it were insensible.", "그 손은 내밀어지지도 거두어지지도 않은 채, 그저 그녀가 들어 올리는 대로 맡겨졌다가, 놓아 주자 거의 감각이 없는 것처럼 다시 떨어져 내렸다."], ["At this she began to whimper and moan, and say what a mother she had been, and how she was forgotten!", "이에 그녀는 훌쩍이고 신음하기 시작하여, 자기가 어떤 어미였는데, 또 어찌 이리 잊혔는가 하고 말했다!"], ["This she continued to do at capricious intervals, even when they had alighted: when she herself was halting along with the joint support of Withers and a stick, and Edith was walking by her side, and the carriage slowly following at a little distance.", "그녀는 마차에서 내린 뒤에도 변덕스러운 간격으로 이 짓을 계속했다. 위더스와 지팡이의 양쪽 부축을 받아 그녀가 절뚝절뚝 걷고, 에디스가 그 곁을 걸으며, 마차가 조금 뒤에서 천천히 따라오는 그때에도."]], [["It was a bleak, lowering, windy day, and they were out upon the Downs with nothing but a bare sweep of land between them and the sky.", "음산하고 잔뜩 찌푸린, 바람 부는 날이었고, 그들은 다운스의 언덕 위에 나와 있었는데, 그들과 하늘 사이에는 헐벗고 휑한 땅의 비탈뿐이었다."], ["The mother, with a querulous satisfaction in the monotony of her complaint, was still repeating it in a low voice from time to time, and the proud form of her daughter moved beside her slowly, when there came advancing over a dark ridge before them, two other figures, which in the distance, were so like an exaggerated imitation of their own, that Edith stopped.", "어머니는 자기 푸념의 단조로움에서 짜증스러운 만족을 느끼며, 여전히 이따금 낮은 목소리로 그것을 되풀이하고 있었고, 딸의 도도한 자태가 그 곁에서 천천히 움직이고 있었다. 그때 그들 앞의 어두운 능선을 넘어 두 형상이 다가오는 것이 보였는데, 멀리서 보니 그것이 어찌나 자기들 둘을 과장되게 흉내 낸 듯하던지, 에디스는 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["Almost as she stopped, the two figures stopped; and that one which to Edith’s thinking was like a distorted shadow of her mother, spoke to the other, earnestly, and with a pointing hand towards them.", "그녀가 멈춤과 거의 동시에 그 두 형상도 멈추었다. 그리고 에디스가 보기에 마치 제 어머니의 일그러진 그림자 같던 그 하나가, 손으로 그들 쪽을 가리키며 다른 하나에게 간곡히 무어라 말했다."], ["That one seemed inclined to turn back, but the other, in which Edith recognised enough that was like herself to strike her with an unusual feeling, not quite free from fear, came on; and then they came on together.", "그 하나는 되돌아가고 싶어 하는 듯했으나, 다른 하나는—그 안에서 에디스는 자기와 닮은 무언가를, 두려움이 아주 없지는 않은 어떤 예사롭지 않은 느낌으로 그녀를 찌를 만큼 충분히 알아보았다—다가왔다. 그러고는 두 형상이 함께 다가왔다."]], [["The greater part of this observation, she made while walking towards them, for her stoppage had been momentary.", "이 관찰의 대부분을 그녀는 그들 쪽으로 걸어가면서 해냈으니, 그녀가 멈췄던 것은 한순간이었기 때문이다."], ["Nearer observation showed her that they were poorly dressed, as wanderers about the country; that the younger woman carried knitted work or some such goods for sale; and that the old one toiled on empty-handed.", "더 가까이서 살펴보니 그들은 시골을 떠도는 이들처럼 초라하게 차려입었고, 젊은 여자 쪽은 뜨개질감인지 그와 비슷한 팔 물건들을 들고 있었으며, 늙은 여자는 빈손으로 힘겹게 걷고 있었다."]], [["And yet, however far removed she was in dress, in dignity, in beauty, Edith could not but compare the younger woman with herself, still.", "그러나 차림새로 보나, 품위로 보나, 아름다움으로 보나 아무리 동떨어져 있을지언정, 에디스는 그 젊은 여자를 여전히 자기 자신과 견주어 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["It may have been that she saw upon her face some traces which she knew were lingering in her own soul, if not yet written on that index; but, as the woman came on, returning her gaze, fixing her shining eyes upon her, undoubtedly presenting something of her own air and stature, and appearing to reciprocate her own thoughts, she felt a chill creep over her, as if the day were darkening, and the wind were colder.", "어쩌면 그것은, 아직 그 얼굴이라는 표지에는 쓰여 있지 않을지언정 자기 영혼 속에 도사리고 있음을 그녀가 아는 어떤 흔적들을, 그 여자의 얼굴에서 보았기 때문인지도 몰랐다. 그러나 그 여자가 다가오면서, 시선을 마주 돌려 빛나는 두 눈을 그녀에게 고정시키고, 틀림없이 그녀 자신의 분위기와 풍채를 얼마간 풍기며, 그녀 자신의 생각을 똑같이 되비추는 듯하자, 그녀는 마치 날이 어두워지고 바람이 차가워지기라도 한 듯, 한기가 몸을 타고 기어오르는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["They had now come up.", "이제 그들은 가까이 다가와 있었다."], ["The old woman, holding out her hand importunately, stopped to beg of Mrs Skewton.", "늙은 여자는 손을 끈질기게 내밀며, 스큐턴 부인에게 구걸하려고 멈춰 섰다."], ["The younger one stopped too, and she and Edith looked in one another’s eyes.", "젊은 여자도 멈추었고, 그녀와 에디스는 서로의 눈을 들여다보았다."]], [["“What is it that you have to sell?”", "\"팔 게 무엇이오?\""], ["said Edith.", "에디스가 말했다."]], [["“Only this,”", "\"이것뿐이에요,\""], ["returned the woman, holding out her wares, without looking at them.", "그 여자가, 자기 물건들을 쳐다보지도 않고 그것들을 내밀며 응수했다."], ["“I sold myself long ago.”", "\"저 자신은 오래전에 팔아 버렸고요.\""]], [["“My Lady, don’t believe her,”", "\"마님, 저 애 말을 믿지 마세요,\""], ["croaked the old woman to Mrs Skewton; “don’t believe what she says.", "늙은 여자가 스큐턴 부인에게 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"저 애가 하는 말을 믿지 마세요."], ["She loves to talk like that.", "저 애는 저렇게 말하는 걸 좋아한답니다."], ["She’s my handsome and undutiful daughter.", "제 잘난, 못된 딸년이지요."], ["She gives me nothing but reproaches, my Lady, for all I have done for her.", "제가 저 애한테 해 준 게 얼만데, 마님, 저 애가 제게 주는 거라곤 비난뿐이에요."], ["Look at her now, my Lady, how she turns upon her poor old mother with her looks.”", "저것 좀 보세요, 마님, 저 애가 그 눈초리로 제 불쌍한 늙은 어미한테 어떻게 대드는지를요.\""]], [["As Mrs Skewton drew her purse out with a trembling hand, and eagerly fumbled for some money, which the other old woman greedily watched for—their heads all but touching, in their hurry and decrepitude—Edith interposed:", "스큐턴 부인이 떨리는 손으로 지갑을 꺼내, 돈을 좀 찾으려고 다급히 더듬는 동안—다른 늙은 여자가 그것을 게걸스레 지켜보았고, 그 다급함과 노쇠함 속에서 둘의 머리가 거의 맞닿을 지경이었다—에디스가 끼어들었다."]], [["“I have seen you,”", "\"당신을 본 적이 있소,\""], ["addressing the old woman, “before.”", "늙은 여자를 향해 그녀가 말했다, \"전에.\""]], [["“Yes, my Lady,”", "\"네, 마님,\""], ["with a curtsey.", "무릎을 굽혀 인사하며."], ["“Down in Warwickshire.", "\"저 아래 워릭셔에서요."], ["The morning among the trees.", "나무들 사이에서 맞던 그 아침에요."], ["When you wouldn’t give me nothing.", "마님이 제게 아무것도 안 주려 하셨을 때요."], ["But the gentleman, he give me something!", "하지만 그 신사분은, 그분은 제게 뭔가를 주셨답니다!"], ["Oh, bless him, bless him!”", "오, 그분께 복을, 그분께 복을 내려 주소서!\""], ["mumbled the old woman, holding up her skinny hand, and grinning frightfully at her daughter.", "늙은 여자가 그 뼈만 남은 손을 치켜들고, 제 딸을 향해 소름 끼치게 이를 드러내며 우물거렸다."]], [["“It’s of no use attempting to stay me, Edith!”", "\"나를 말려 봐야 소용없다, 에디스!\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, angrily anticipating an objection from her.", "스큐턴 부인이, 딸에게서 나올 반대를 성나게 앞질러 막으며 말했다."], ["“You know nothing about it.", "\"넌 이 일에 대해 아무것도 몰라."], ["I won’t be dissuaded.", "난 말려도 안 들을 거다."], ["I am sure this is an excellent woman, and a good mother.”", "이 사람은 분명 훌륭한 여자이고 좋은 어미야.\""]], [["“Yes, my Lady, yes,”", "\"네, 마님, 그럼요,\""], ["chattered the old woman, holding out her avaricious hand.", "늙은 여자가 그 탐욕스러운 손을 내밀며 재잘거렸다."], ["“Thankee, my Lady.", "\"고맙습니다, 마님."], ["Lord bless you, my Lady.", "주님의 복이 함께하시길, 마님."], ["Sixpence more, my pretty Lady, as a good mother yourself.”", "육 펜스만 더요, 어여쁜 마님, 마님도 좋은 어머니이시니까요.\""]], [["“And treated undutifully enough, too, my good old creature, sometimes, I assure you,”", "\"그리고 나 역시 가끔은 충분히 불효를 당하지요, 정말이오, 우리 착한 노친네,\""], ["said Mrs Skewton, whimpering.", "스큐턴 부인이 훌쩍이며 말했다."], ["“There!", "\"자!"], ["Shake hands with me.", "나랑 악수합시다."], ["You’re a very good old creature—full of what’s-his-name—and all that.", "당신은 참 착한 노친네야—그 뭐랄까가 가득한 사람—그런 것 말이야."], ["You’re all affection and et cetera, ain’t you?”", "당신은 온통 애정이며 기타 등등이지, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, my Lady!”", "\"오, 그럼요, 마님!\""]], [["“Yes, I’m sure you are; and so’s that gentlemanly creature Grangeby.", "\"그럼, 분명 그럴 테지. 그리고 그 신사 같은 사람 그레인지비도 그렇고."], ["I must really shake hands with you again.", "정말이지 당신과 또 한 번 악수를 해야겠어."], ["And now you can go, you know; and I hope,”", "그럼 이제 가도 돼요, 알겠지. 그리고 바라건대,\""], ["addressing the daughter, “that you’ll show more gratitude, and natural what’s-its-name, and all the rest of it—but I never remember names—for there never was a better mother than the good old creature’s been to you.", "딸을 향해 말하며, \"넌 더 많은 고마움과, 타고난 그 뭐라더라와, 그 나머지 것들을 보여 드리거라—난 도통 이름을 못 외우니—이 착한 노친네가 네게 해 준 것보다 더 나은 어미는 세상에 없었으니 말이다."], ["Come, Edith!”", "가자, 에디스!\""]], [["As the ruin of Cleopatra tottered off whimpering, and wiping its eyes with a gingerly remembrance of rouge in their neighbourhood, the old woman hobbled another way, mumbling and counting her money.", "클레오파트라의 폐허가 훌쩍이며 비틀비틀 걸어가, 눈가에 연지가 있을지 모른다는 것을 조심스레 떠올리며 눈물을 훔치는 동안, 늙은 여자는 우물거리며 제 돈을 세면서 다른 쪽으로 절뚝절뚝 걸어갔다."], ["Not one word more, nor one other gesture, had been exchanged between Edith and the younger woman, but neither had removed her eyes from the other for a moment.", "에디스와 그 젊은 여자 사이에는 더는 한마디 말도, 다른 어떤 몸짓도 오가지 않았으나, 둘 다 한순간도 서로에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["They had remained confronted until now, when Edith, as awakening from a dream, passed slowly on.", "그들은 지금까지 마주 선 채로 있었는데, 이윽고 에디스가, 마치 꿈에서 깨어나기라도 하듯 천천히 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["“You’re a handsome woman,”", "\"넌 예쁜 여자야,\""], ["muttered her shadow, looking after her; “but good looks won’t save us.", "그녀의 그림자가 그녀의 뒷모습을 바라보며 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 예쁜 얼굴이 우리를 구해 주진 못하지."], ["And you’re a proud woman; but pride won’t save us.", "그리고 넌 도도한 여자야. 하지만 도도함도 우리를 구해 주진 못해."], ["We had need to know each other when we meet again!”", "우리가 다시 만날 때는, 서로를 알아 둘 필요가 있겠어!\""]], [["# CHAPTER XLI. New Voices in the Waves", "# CHAPTER XLI. New Voices in the Waves"]], [["All is going on as it was wont.", "모든 것이 늘 그러했듯이 흘러간다."], ["The waves are hoarse with repetition of their mystery; the dust lies piled upon the shore; the sea-birds soar and hover; the winds and clouds go forth upon their trackless flight; the white arms beckon, in the moonlight, to the invisible country far away.", "파도는 제 신비를 거듭거듭 되뇌느라 목이 쉬어 있고, 모래밭에는 먼지가 쌓여 있으며, 바닷새들은 솟구쳐 올랐다 맴돌고, 바람과 구름은 자취 없는 비행에 나선다. 하얀 팔들은 달빛 속에서, 저 멀리 보이지 않는 나라를 향해 손짓한다."]], [["With a tender melancholy pleasure, Florence finds herself again on the old ground so sadly trodden, yet so happily, and thinks of him in the quiet place, where he and she have many and many a time conversed together, with the water welling up about his couch.", "플로렌스는 그토록 슬프게, 그러나 그토록 행복하게 밟았던 옛 자리에 다시 서서, 애틋하고 우수에 잠긴 즐거움을 느끼며, 그 고요한 곳에 있던 동생을 떠올린다. 그곳에서 그와 그녀는 그의 침상 둘레로 물이 솟아오르는 가운데 몇 번이고 몇 번이고 함께 이야기를 나누었다."], ["And now, as she sits pensive there, she hears in the wild low murmur of the sea, his little story told again, his very words repeated; and finds that all her life and hopes, and griefs, since—in the solitary house, and in the pageant it has changed to—have a portion in the burden of the marvellous song.", "그리고 이제, 그녀가 그곳에 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있노라니, 바다의 거칠고 나직한 속삭임 속에서 동생의 짧은 이야기가 다시 들려오고, 동생이 한 바로 그 말들이 되풀이되는 것을 듣는다. 그리고 그 이후로—외로운 집에서, 또 그 집이 변모한 화려한 행렬 속에서—자기의 모든 삶과 희망과 슬픔이 그 신비로운 노래의 후렴 속에 한 자락을 차지하고 있음을 깨닫는다."]], [["And gentle Mr Toots, who wanders at a distance, looking wistfully towards the figure that he dotes upon, and has followed there, but cannot in his delicacy disturb at such a time, likewise hears the requiem of little Dombey on the waters, rising and falling in the lulls of their eternal madrigal in praise of Florence. Yes! and he faintly understands, poor Mr Toots, that they are saying something of a time when he was sensible of being brighter and not addle-brained; and the tears rising in his eyes when he fears that he is dull and stupid now, and good for little but to be laughed at, diminish his satisfaction in their soothing reminder that he is relieved from present responsibility to the Chicken, by the absence of that game head of poultry in the country, training (at Toots’s cost) for his great mill with the Larkey Boy.", "그리고 멀찍이서 거니는 상냥한 투츠 씨는, 자기가 그토록 흠모하여 여기까지 뒤따라온 그 모습을 애틋하게 바라보면서도 섬세한 마음 탓에 이런 때에 차마 방해하지 못하는데, 그 역시 물결 위에 떠오르는 어린 돔비의 진혼곡을 듣는다. 플로렌스를 찬미하는 그 영원한 마드리갈의 잔잔한 틈새에서 솟았다 가라앉는 그 노래를. 그렇다! 그리고 가엾은 투츠 씨는 어렴풋이나마 깨닫는다. 그 노래가 자기가 더 총명했고 머리가 흐리멍덩하지 않다고 느끼던 시절에 관한 무언가를 말하고 있음을. 그리고 자기가 지금은 둔하고 어리석으며 남의 웃음거리가 되는 것 말고는 별 쓸모가 없지 않은가 두려워질 때 눈에 차오르는 눈물이, 그가 시골에서 (투츠의 비용으로) 라키 보이와의 큰 시합을 대비해 훈련 중인 저 닭대가리 같은 사내가 없는 덕분에 당장은 치킨에 대한 책임에서 풀려나 있다는, 위로가 되는 그 깨우침이 주는 만족을 줄어들게 한다. * 치킨: '게임 치킨(Game Chicken)'은 투츠가 고용한 권투 선생을 가리키는 별명이다."]], [["But Mr Toots takes courage, when they whisper a kind thought to him; and by slow degrees and with many indecisive stoppages on the way, approaches Florence.", "그러나 투츠 씨는 그 노래가 다정한 생각을 그에게 속삭여 줄 때 용기를 얻고, 더디게, 그리고 가는 길에 여러 번 망설이며 멈춰 서면서, 플로렌스에게 다가간다."], ["Stammering and blushing, Mr Toots affects amazement when he comes near her, and says (having followed close on the carriage in which she travelled, every inch of the way from London, loving even to be choked by the dust of its wheels) that he never was so surprised in all his life.", "더듬거리고 얼굴을 붉히며, 투츠 씨는 그녀 가까이 다가오자 놀란 척한다. 그러고는 (그녀가 타고 온 마차를 런던에서부터 한 치도 빠짐없이 바짝 뒤따라왔으며, 그 바퀴가 일으킨 먼지에 숨이 막히는 것조차 사랑스러웠으면서) 평생 이렇게 놀란 적이 없었다고 말한다."]], [["“And you’ve brought Diogenes, too, Miss Dombey!”", "\"게다가 다이오지니스도 데려오셨군요, 돔비 아가씨!\""], ["says Mr Toots, thrilled through and through by the touch of the small hand so pleasantly and frankly given him.", "투츠 씨가 말한다. 그토록 다정하고 스스럼없이 내밀어진 그 작은 손의 감촉에 온몸이 짜릿하다."]], [["No doubt Diogenes is there, and no doubt Mr Toots has reason to observe him, for he comes straightway at Mr Toots’s legs, and tumbles over himself in the desperation with which he makes at him, like a very dog of Montargis.", "다이오지니스가 거기 있는 것은 의심할 바 없고, 투츠 씨가 그 녀석을 주목할 이유가 있는 것도 의심할 바 없다. 녀석이 곧장 투츠 씨의 다리로 달려들어, 마치 몽타르지의 그 개라도 되는 양 필사적으로 덤벼들다 제풀에 나뒹굴기 때문이다."], ["But he is checked by his sweet mistress.", "그러나 녀석은 사랑스러운 여주인에게 제지당한다."]], [["“Down, Di, down. Don’t you remember who first made us friends, Di? For shame!”", "* 몽타르지의 개: 주인을 살해한 자를 끝까지 물어 정의를 밝혔다는 중세 프랑스 전설 속의 충견."]], [["Oh!", "\"엎드려, 다이, 엎드려."], ["Well may Di lay his loving cheek against her hand, and run off, and run back, and run round her, barking, and run headlong at anybody coming by, to show his devotion.", "누가 우리를 처음 친구로 만들어 줬는지 기억 안 나니, 다이? 부끄러운 줄 알아야지!\""], ["Mr Toots would run headlong at anybody, too.", "오! 다이가 사랑스러운 뺨을 그녀의 손에 비비고, 달아났다 되돌아오고, 그녀 주위를 빙빙 돌며 짖고, 지나가는 누구에게든 곤두박질치듯 달려들어 제 헌신을 내보일 만도 하다."], ["A military gentleman goes past, and Mr Toots would like nothing better than to run at him, full tilt.", "투츠 씨도 누구에게든 곤두박질치듯 달려들고 싶은 심정이다. 군인 신사 하나가 지나가는데, 투츠 씨는 그를 향해 전속력으로 돌진하는 것보다 더 좋은 일이 없을 것만 같다."]], [["“Diogenes is quite in his native air, isn’t he, Miss Dombey?”", "\"다이오지니스는 제 고향 공기 속에 있는 셈이지요, 그렇지 않습니까, 돔비 아가씨?\""], ["says Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말한다."]], [["Florence assents, with a grateful smile.", "플로렌스가 고마운 미소로 동의한다."]], [["“Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 아가씨,\""], ["says Mr Toots, “beg your pardon, but if you would like to walk to Blimber’s, I—I’m going there.”", "투츠 씨가 말한다, \"실례입니다만, 혹시 블림버 댁까지 걸어가고 싶으시다면, 제가—제가 그리로 가는 길이라서요.\""]], [["Florence puts her arm in that of Mr Toots without a word, and they walk away together, with Diogenes going on before.", "플로렌스는 말없이 투츠 씨의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼고, 둘은 다이오지니스를 앞세운 채 함께 걸어간다. 투츠 씨의 다리는 후들거린다."], ["Mr Toots’s legs shake under him; and though he is splendidly dressed, he feels misfits, and sees wrinkles, in the masterpieces of Burgess and Co., and wishes he had put on that brightest pair of boots.", "그리고 그는 화려하게 차려입었으면서도, 버지스 상회의 그 걸작품들이 몸에 맞지 않는 것 같고 주름이 잡힌 것 같은 느낌이 들어, 차라리 그 가장 번쩍이는 부츠를 신고 올 걸 그랬다고 아쉬워한다."]], [["Doctor Blimber’s house, outside, has as scholastic and studious an air as ever; and up there is the window where she used to look for the pale face, and where the pale face brightened when it saw her, and the wasted little hand waved kisses as she passed.", "블림버 박사의 집은, 겉모습이 여전히 학구적이고 사색에 잠긴 듯한 분위기를 풍긴다. 그리고 저 위에는, 그녀가 창백한 얼굴을 찾아 올려다보곤 하던 창문이 있다."], ["The door is opened by the same weak-eyed young man, whose imbecility of grin at sight of Mr Toots is feebleness of character personified.", "그 창백한 얼굴은 그녀를 보면 환해졌고, 여위어 가던 작은 손은 그녀가 지나갈 때 입맞춤을 보내며 흔들리곤 했다. 문을 열어 주는 것은 여전히 그 눈이 약한 젊은이인데, 투츠 씨를 보자 짓는 그 얼빠진 웃음은 나약한 성품을 그대로 형상화한 듯하다."], ["They are shown into the Doctor’s study, where blind Homer and Minerva give them audience as of yore, to the sober ticking of the great clock in the hall; and where the globes stand still in their accustomed places, as if the world were stationary too, and nothing in it ever perished in obedience to the universal law, that, while it keeps it on the roll, calls everything to earth.", "그들은 박사의 서재로 안내되는데, 거기서는 눈먼 호메로스와 미네르바가 예전처럼 그들을 맞이하고, 현관의 큰 시계가 차분히 똑딱거리는 소리가 들린다. 그리고 지구본들은 늘 놓이던 자리에 그대로 서 있어서, 마치 세상도 가만히 멈춰 있고, 그 안의 어떤 것도, 만물을 굴러가게 하는 동시에 모든 것을 땅으로 불러들이는 보편의 법칙을 따라 스러진 적이 없는 것만 같다."]], [["And here is Doctor Blimber, with his learned legs; and here is Mrs Blimber, with her sky-blue cap; and here Cornelia, with her sandy little row of curls, and her bright spectacles, still working like a sexton in the graves of languages.", "그리고 여기, 학구적인 다리를 한 블림버 박사가 있다. 그리고 여기, 하늘색 모자를 쓴 블림버 부인이 있다."], ["Here is the table upon which he sat forlorn and strange, the “new boy”", "그리고 여기, 모래빛의 작은 곱슬머리 줄과 반짝이는 안경을 쓴 코닐리어가, 여전히 죽은 언어들의 무덤 속에서 묘지기처럼 일하고 있다."], ["of the school; and hither comes the distant cooing of the old boys, at their old lives in the old room on the old principle!", "여기, 그가 학교의 \"새 학생\"으로서 쓸쓸하고 낯설게 앉아 있던 그 책상이 있다. 그리고 저쪽에서, 옛 방에서 옛 원칙대로 옛 생활을 영위하는 옛 학생들의 멀찍한 웅성거림이 들려온다!"]], [["“Toots,”", "\"투츠,\""], ["says Doctor Blimber, “I am very glad to see you, Toots.”", "블림버 박사가 말한다, \"자네를 보니 참 반갑네, 투츠.\""]], [["Mr Toots chuckles in reply.", "투츠 씨가 답례로 빙긋 웃는다."]], [["“Also to see you, Toots, in such good company,”", "\"또한 이렇게 좋은 분과 함께 있는 자네를 보니 반갑군, 투츠,\""], ["says Doctor Blimber.", "블림버 박사가 말한다."]], [["Mr Toots, with a scarlet visage, explains that he has met Miss Dombey by accident, and that Miss Dombey wishing, like himself, to see the old place, they have come together.", "투츠 씨는, 얼굴이 새빨개진 채로, 돔비 아가씨를 우연히 만났으며, 돔비 아가씨가 자기처럼 옛 장소를 보고 싶어 해서 함께 오게 되었노라고 설명한다."]], [["“You will like,”", "\"틀림없이,\""], ["says Doctor Blimber, “to step among our young friends, Miss Dombey, no doubt.", "블림버 박사가 말한다, \"우리 어린 벗들 사이를 거닐어 보고 싶으실 테지요, 돔비 아가씨."], ["All fellow-students of yours, Toots, once.", "한때는 모두 자네의 동문들이었지, 투츠."], ["I think we have no new disciples in our little portico, my dear,”", "우리의 작은 주랑(柱廊)에는 투츠 군이 떠난 이래 새 제자가 들어온 일이 없는 것 같구려, 여보,\""], ["says Doctor Blimber to Cornelia, “since Mr Toots left us.”", "블림버 박사가 코닐리어에게 말한다."]], [["“Except Bitherstone,”", "\"비더스톤만 빼면요,\""], ["returns Cornelia.", "코닐리어가 응수한다."]], [["“Ay, truly,”", "\"아, 그렇지,\""], ["says the Doctor.", "박사가 말한다."], ["“Bitherstone is new to Mr Toots.”", "\"비더스톤은 투츠 군에게는 새 얼굴이지.\""]], [["New to Florence, too, almost; for, in the schoolroom, Bitherstone—no longer Master Bitherstone of Mrs Pipchin’s—shows in collars and a neckcloth, and wears a watch.", "플로렌스에게도 거의 새 얼굴이다. 교실에서 비더스톤은—더 이상 핍친 부인 댁의 비더스톤 도련님이 아닌—깃과 넥타이를 두르고 시계를 차고 있는 모습이다."], ["But Bitherstone, born beneath some Bengal star of ill-omen, is extremely inky; and his Lexicon has got so dropsical from constant reference, that it won’t shut, and yawns as if it really could not bear to be so bothered.", "그러나 어떤 불길한 벵골의 별 아래 태어난 비더스톤은 잉크투성이가 되어 있다. 그리고 그의 사전은 끊임없이 들춰진 탓에 잔뜩 부풀어 올라 닫히지가 않고, 정말이지 이렇게 시달리는 걸 도무지 못 견디겠다는 듯이 하품을 하듯 입을 벌리고 있다."], ["So does Bitherstone its master, forced at Doctor Blimber’s highest pressure; but in the yawn of Bitherstone there is malice and snarl, and he has been heard to say that he wishes he could catch “old Blimber” in India.", "그 주인 비더스톤도 블림버 박사의 최고 압박을 받아 그렇게 하품을 하는데, 다만 비더스톤의 하품에는 악의와 으르렁거림이 서려 있어서, 그가 \"늙은 블림버\"를 인도에서 붙잡을 수 있었으면 좋겠다고 말하는 것을 사람들이 들은 적이 있다."], ["He’d precious soon find himself carried up the country by a few of his (Bitherstone’s) Coolies, and handed over to the Thugs; he can tell him that.", "그러면 블림버는 머지않아 자기(비더스톤)네 막일꾼 몇 명에게 들려 내륙으로 끌려가서, 교살단에게 넘겨지게 될 거다. 그가 장담할 수 있다는 거다."]], [["Briggs is still grinding in the mill of knowledge; and Tozer, too; and Johnson, too; and all the rest; the older pupils being principally engaged in forgetting, with prodigious labour, everything they knew when they were younger. All are as polite and as pale as ever; and among them, Mr Feeder, B.A., with his bony hand and bristly head, is still hard at it; with his Herodotus stop on just at present, and his other barrels on a shelf behind him.", "브리그스는 여전히 지식의 맷돌을 돌리고 있다. 토저도 그렇고, 존슨도 그렇고, 나머지 전부가 그렇다. 나이가 더 든 학생들은 주로, 어렸을 때 알던 것을 죄다 엄청난 수고를 들여 잊어버리는 일에 매달려 있다. 모두가 여전히 예전처럼 공손하고 창백하다. 그리고 그들 사이에서, 학사 학위를 지닌 피더 씨가, 그 뼈마디 굵은 손과 빳빳한 머리칼로, 여전히 열심히 일하고 있다. 마침 지금은 헤로도토스 음전(音栓)을 당겨 놓은 채이고, 그의 다른 음전들은 등 뒤 선반 위에 놓여 있다."]], [["A mighty sensation is created, even among these grave young gentlemen, by a visit from the emancipated Toots; who is regarded with a kind of awe, as one who has passed the Rubicon, and is pledged never to come back, and concerning the cut of whose clothes, and fashion of whose jewellery, whispers go about, behind hands; the bilious Bitherstone, who is not of Mr Toots’s time, affecting to despise the latter to the smaller boys, and saying he knows better, and that he should like to see him coming that sort of thing in Bengal, where his mother had got an emerald belonging to him that was taken out of the footstool of a Rajah. Come now!", "* 음전: 피더가 기계처럼 정해진 교과만 되풀이하는 것을 풍금 음전에 빗댄 표현. 이 진중한 젊은 신사들 사이에서조차, 해방된 투츠의 방문은 엄청난 파문을 일으킨다. 그는 루비콘강을 건너 다시는 돌아오지 않겠노라 맹세한 사람으로서 일종의 경외심으로 떠받들어지고, 그의 옷의 재단과 장신구의 유행을 두고 손으로 입을 가린 채 수군거림이 오간다. 투츠 씨와 같은 시절을 보내지 않은 그 신경질적인 비더스톤은, 어린 학생들 앞에서 투츠를 경멸하는 척하며, 자기는 더 잘 안다고, 그가 벵골에 와서 그딴 짓을 하는 걸 한번 보고 싶다고, 거기서는 자기 어머니가 어느 라자의 발받침에서 꺼낸, 자기 소유의 에메랄드를 갖고 있다고 말한다. 어디 해 봐!"]], [["Bewildering emotions are awakened also by the sight of Florence, with whom every young gentleman immediately falls in love, again; except, as aforesaid, the bilious Bitherstone, who declines to do so, out of contradiction.", "플로렌스의 모습에도 어리둥절한 감정이 일깨워지는데, 모든 젊은 신사가 그녀를 보자마자 다시금 사랑에 빠진다. 앞서 말한 그 신경질적인 비더스톤만은 예외인데, 그는 청개구리 심사로 사랑에 빠지기를 거부한다."], ["Black jealousies of Mr Toots arise, and Briggs is of opinion that he ain’t so very old after all.", "투츠 씨에 대한 시커먼 질투심이 일고, 브리그스는 그자가 결국 그렇게까지 나이가 많은 건 아니라는 의견을 내놓는다."], ["But this disparaging insinuation is speedily made nought by Mr Toots saying aloud to Mr Feeder, B.A., “How are you, Feeder?”", "그러나 이 깎아내리는 빈정거림은, 투츠 씨가 학사 학위를 지닌 피더 씨에게 큰 소리로 \"어떻게 지내나, 피더?\""], ["and asking him to come and dine with him today at the Bedford; in right of which feats he might set up as Old Parr, if he chose, unquestioned.", "하고 인사하며 오늘 베드퍼드에서 함께 저녁을 들자고 청하는 통에 금세 무너지고 만다. 그런 위업을 행한 권리로 보아, 그는 마음만 먹으면 아무 이의 없이 올드 파 노릇이라도 할 수 있을 것이다."]], [["There is much shaking of hands, and much bowing, and a great desire on the part of each young gentleman to take Toots down in Miss Dombey’s good graces; and then, Mr Toots having bestowed a chuckle on his old desk, Florence and he withdraw with Mrs Blimber and Cornelia; and Doctor Blimber is heard to observe behind them as he comes out last, and shuts the door, “Gentlemen, we will now resume our studies,” For that and little else is what the Doctor hears the sea say, or has heard it saying all his life.", "* 올드 파: 152세까지 살았다고 전해지는 전설적 장수 노인 토머스 파. 악수가 무수히 오가고, 절도 무수히 오가며, 젊은 신사들은 저마다 돔비 아가씨의 호감 속에서 투츠를 끌어내리고 싶어 안달이다. 그러다가, 투츠 씨가 자기 옛 책상에 빙긋 웃음을 한 번 선사하고 나서, 플로렌스와 그는 블림버 부인 및 코닐리어와 함께 물러난다. 그리고 맨 마지막에 나와 문을 닫는 블림버 박사가 그들 뒤에서 이렇게 말하는 것이 들린다. \"여러분, 이제 우리는 학업을 재개합시다.\" 그것이, 그리고 그 밖에 거의 아무것도 아닌 것이, 박사가 바다더러 하는 말이라고 듣는 것이요, 평생토록 들어 온 말이다."]], [["Florence then steals away and goes upstairs to the old bedroom with Mrs Blimber and Cornelia; Mr Toots, who feels that neither he nor anybody else is wanted there, stands talking to the Doctor at the study-door, or rather hearing the Doctor talk to him, and wondering how he ever thought the study a great sanctuary, and the Doctor, with his round turned legs, like a clerical pianoforte, an awful man. Florence soon comes down and takes leave; Mr Toots takes leave; and Diogenes, who has been worrying the weak-eyed young man pitilessly all the time, shoots out at the door, and barks a glad defiance down the cliff; while Melia, and another of the Doctor’s female domestics, looks out of an upper window, laughing “at that there Toots,” and saying of Miss Dombey, “But really though, now—ain’t she like her brother, only prettier?”", "이윽고 플로렌스는 슬그머니 빠져나와 블림버 부인 및 코닐리어와 함께 위층의 옛 침실로 올라간다. 거기서는 자기도 다른 누구도 필요치 않다고 느끼는 투츠 씨는, 서재 문간에 서서 박사와 이야기를 나눈다. 아니, 오히려 박사가 자기에게 하는 이야기를 듣고 서서, 자기가 어쩌다 이 서재를 대단한 성소로, 또 둥글게 휜 다리를 한 박사를, 마치 성직자풍의 피아노포르테처럼 생긴 그 박사를 어찌하여 무시무시한 사람이라고 여겼던가 의아해한다. 플로렌스가 곧 내려와 작별을 고한다. 투츠 씨도 작별을 고한다. 그리고 그동안 내내 눈이 약한 젊은이를 사정없이 괴롭혀 온 다이오지니스는 문밖으로 튀어 나가, 절벽 아래를 향해 기쁜 도전의 짖음을 짖어 댄다. 한편 밀리어와 박사의 또 다른 여자 하인이 위층 창밖으로 내다보며, \"저 투츠 양반 좀 봐\" 하고 웃고는, 돔비 아가씨에 대해서 이렇게 말한다. \"근데 정말이지, 안 그래—저 아가씨 자기 동생 닮지 않았어, 좀 더 예쁘기만 하고?\""]], [["Mr Toots, who saw when Florence came down that there were tears upon her face, is desperately anxious and uneasy, and at first fears that he did wrong in proposing the visit.", "플로렌스가 내려왔을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 눈물 자국이 있는 것을 본 투츠 씨는, 미칠 듯이 걱정스럽고 불안해져서, 처음에는 자기가 방문을 제안한 것이 잘못이 아니었나 두려워한다."], ["But he is soon relieved by her saying she is very glad to have been there again, and by her talking quite cheerfully about it all, as they walked on by the sea.", "그러나 그녀가 다시 그곳에 와 보게 되어 무척 기쁘다고 말하고, 바닷가를 따라 걸으면서 그 모든 일에 대해 아주 명랑하게 이야기하는 통에, 그는 곧 마음을 놓는다."], ["What with the voices there, and her sweet voice, when they come near Mr Dombey’s house, and Mr Toots must leave her, he is so enslaved that he has not a scrap of free-will left; when she gives him her hand at parting, he cannot let it go.", "거기 그 목소리들과, 그녀의 그 감미로운 목소리가 어우러진 가운데, 돔비 씨의 집 가까이에 이르러 투츠 씨가 그녀와 헤어져야 할 때가 되자, 그는 자유의지라고는 한 조각도 남지 않을 만큼 사로잡혀 버린다. 그녀가 작별로 손을 내밀자, 그는 그 손을 차마 놓지 못한다."]], [["“Miss Dombey, I beg your pardon,”", "\"돔비 아가씨, 실례입니다만,\""], ["says Mr Toots, in a sad fluster, “but if you would allow me to—to—”", "투츠 씨가 슬픈 안절부절못하며 말한다, \"혹시 제가—제가—해도 괜찮으시다면—\""]], [["The smiling and unconscious look of Florence brings him to a dead stop.", "플로렌스의 미소 띤, 아무것도 모르는 표정에 그는 그만 딱 멈추어 버린다."]], [["“If you would allow me to—if you would not consider it a liberty, Miss Dombey, if I was to—without any encouragement at all, if I was to hope, you know,”", "\"제가—해도 괜찮으시다면—그러니까, 만일 제가, 그게 주제넘은 짓이라고 여기지 않으신다면요, 돔비 아가씨, 만일 제가—아무런 격려도 받지 않은 채, 만일 제가 희망을 품어도 된다면 말입니다, 그러니까,\""], ["says Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말한다."]], [["Florence looks at him inquiringly.", "플로렌스가 미심쩍은 듯 그를 바라본다."]], [["“Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 아가씨,\" 투츠 씨가 말한다."], ["says Mr Toots, who feels that he is in for it now, “I really am in that state of adoration of you that I don’t know what to do with myself.", "이제 발을 뺄 수 없게 되었다고 느끼면서. \"저는 정말이지 아가씨를 흠모하는 그런 상태라서, 제 자신을 어찌해야 할지 모르겠습니다."], ["I am the most deplorable wretch.", "저는 가장 한심한 가련뱅이입니다."], ["If it wasn’t at the corner of the Square at present, I should go down on my knees, and beg and entreat of you, without any encouragement at all, just to let me hope that I may—may think it possible that you—”", "지금 여기가 광장 모퉁이만 아니라면, 저는 무릎을 꿇고, 아무런 격려도 받지 못한 채라도, 그저 제가—제가, 아가씨가 어쩌면—하고 가능하다고 생각해 봐도 좋을 거라고 희망하게만 해 달라고 빌고 또 애원할 것입니다—\""]], [["“Oh, if you please, don’t!”", "\"오, 제발, 그러지 마세요!\""], ["cries Florence, for the moment quite alarmed and distressed.", "플로렌스가 외친다. 순간 무척 놀라고 괴로워하며."], ["“Oh, pray don’t, Mr Toots.", "\"오, 제발 그러지 마세요, 투츠 씨."], ["Stop, if you please.", "멈춰 주세요, 부디."], ["Don’t say any more.", "더는 말씀하지 마세요."], ["As a kindness and a favour to me, don’t.”", "제게 베푸는 친절이자 호의로 여기고, 그러지 말아 주세요.\""]], [["Mr Toots is dreadfully abashed, and his mouth opens.", "투츠 씨는 끔찍하게 당황하여, 입이 떡 벌어진다."]], [["“You have been so good to me,”", "\"당신은 제게 그토록 잘해 주셨어요,\""], ["says Florence, “I am so grateful to you, I have such reason to like you for being a kind friend to me, and I do like you so much;”", "플로렌스가 말한다, \"저는 당신께 정말 감사하고, 제게 다정한 벗이 되어 주신 당신을 좋아할 이유가 충분하고, 또 정말 당신을 무척 좋아해요.\""], ["and here the ingenuous face smiles upon him with the pleasantest look of honesty in the world; “that I am sure you are only going to say good-bye!”", "그러면서 그 천진한 얼굴이 세상에서 가장 정직하고 다정스러운 표정으로 그를 향해 미소 짓는다. \"그러니 분명 당신은 그저 작별 인사를 하려던 거지요!\""]], [["“Certainly, Miss Dombey,”", "\"물론입니다, 돔비 아가씨,\""], ["says Mr Toots, “I—I—that’s exactly what I mean.", "투츠 씨가 말한다, \"제가—제가—바로 그 뜻이었습니다."], ["It’s of no consequence.”", "별일 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Good-bye!”", "\"안녕히 가세요!\""], ["cries Florence.", "플로렌스가 외친다."]], [["“Good-bye, Miss Dombey!”", "\"안녕히 계세요, 돔비 아가씨!\""], ["stammers Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 더듬거린다."], ["“I hope you won’t think anything about it.", "\"부디 그 일에 대해서는 아무것도 마음에 두지 마시기 바랍니다."], ["It’s—it’s of no consequence, thank you.", "그건—그건 별일 아니에요, 고맙습니다."], ["It’s not of the least consequence in the world.”", "세상에 조금도 대수로울 게 없는 일입니다.\""]], [["Poor Mr Toots goes home to his hotel in a state of desperation, locks himself into his bedroom, flings himself upon his bed, and lies there for a long time; as if it were of the greatest consequence, nevertheless.", "가엾은 투츠 씨는 절망에 빠진 채 호텔로 돌아가, 침실 문을 안에서 잠그고, 침대에 몸을 내던지고는, 오랫동안 거기 누워 있다. 그래도 그것이 마치 세상에서 가장 대수로운 일이라도 되는 듯이."], ["But Mr Feeder, B.A., is coming to dinner, which happens well for Mr Toots, or there is no knowing when he might get up again.", "그러나 학사 학위를 지닌 피더 씨가 저녁을 먹으러 오기로 되어 있는데, 이것이 투츠 씨에게는 잘된 일이다. 안 그랬다면 그가 언제나 다시 일어날지 알 수 없었을 테니까."], ["Mr Toots is obliged to get up to receive him, and to give him hospitable entertainment.", "투츠 씨는 그를 맞이하고 후하게 대접하기 위해 일어나지 않을 수 없다."]], [["And the generous influence of that social virtue, hospitality (to make no mention of wine and good cheer), opens Mr Toots’s heart, and warms him to conversation.", "그리고 그 사교적 미덕인 환대의 너그러운 영향력은 (포도주와 진수성찬은 말할 것도 없고) 투츠 씨의 마음을 열고, 그를 데워 대화로 이끈다."], ["He does not tell Mr Feeder, B.A., what passed at the corner of the Square; but when Mr Feeder asks him “When it is to come off?”", "그는 학사 학위를 지닌 피더 씨에게 광장 모퉁이에서 무슨 일이 있었는지는 말하지 않는다. 그러나 피더 씨가 \"그게 언제 성사되는 건가?\""], ["Mr Toots replies, “that there are certain subjects”—which brings Mr Feeder down a peg or two immediately.", "하고 묻자, 투츠 씨는 \"어떤 주제들이라는 게 있는 법이지\" 하고 대꾸하는데, 이 말은 피더 씨를 즉각 한두 단계 풀이 죽게 만든다."], ["Mr Toots adds, that he don’t know what right Blimber had to notice his being in Miss Dombey’s company, and that if he thought he meant impudence by it, he’d have him out, Doctor or no Doctor; but he supposes its only his ignorance.", "투츠 씨는 덧붙여서, 블림버가 무슨 권리로 자기가 돔비 아가씨와 함께 있었던 것을 들먹였는지 모르겠다고, 만일 그가 그걸로 무례하게 굴 작정이었다고 생각된다면, 박사든 뭐든 가만두지 않았을 거라고 말한다. 다만 그저 그자가 무지해서 그런 거려니 짐작한다고 한다."], ["Mr Feeder says he has no doubt of it.", "피더 씨는 의심할 여지가 없다고 말한다."]], [["Mr Feeder, however, as an intimate friend, is not excluded from the subject.", "그러나 피더 씨는 절친한 벗인지라, 그 주제에서 배제되지는 않는다."], ["Mr Toots merely requires that it should be mentioned mysteriously, and with feeling.", "투츠 씨가 요구하는 것은 그저, 그 이야기를 은밀하게, 또 감정을 담아서 거론해야 한다는 것뿐이다."], ["After a few glasses of wine, he gives Miss Dombey’s health, observing, “Feeder, you have no idea of the sentiments with which I propose that toast.”", "포도주를 몇 잔 들고 나서, 그는 돔비 아가씨의 건강을 위해 건배하며 한마디 한다. \"피더, 자네는 내가 이 건배를 제안하는 심정이 어떤 건지 짐작도 못 할 거야.\""], ["Mr Feeder replies, “Oh, yes, I have, my dear Toots; and greatly they redound to your honour, old boy.”", "피더 씨가 대답한다. \"아니, 짐작하고말고, 친애하는 투츠."], ["Mr Feeder is then agitated by friendship, and shakes hands; and says, if ever Toots wants a brother, he knows where to find him, either by post or parcel.", "그게 자네 명예를 크게 빛내 주는 거라네, 이 사람아.\" 그러고 나서 피더 씨는 우정에 북받쳐 악수를 청하고는, 혹시라도 투츠에게 형제가 필요해지면 어디서 자기를 찾을 수 있는지 안다고, 우편으로든 소포로든 말이라고 한다."], ["Mr Feeder like-wise says, that if he may advise, he would recommend Mr Toots to learn the guitar, or, at least the flute; for women like music, when you are paying your addresses to ’em, and he has found the advantage of it himself.", "피더 씨는 또, 조언을 해도 된다면, 투츠 씨에게 기타를, 적어도 플루트라도 배우라고 권하고 싶다고 한다. 여자들은 구애를 받을 때 음악을 좋아하는 법이고, 자기도 그 이로움을 몸소 겪어 봤다는 것이다."]], [["This brings Mr Feeder, B.A., to the confession that he has his eye upon Cornelia Blimber.", "이 말이 학사 학위를 지닌 피더 씨로 하여금, 자기가 코닐리어 블림버에게 눈독을 들이고 있다는 고백에 이르게 한다."], ["He informs Mr Toots that he don’t object to spectacles, and that if the Doctor were to do the handsome thing and give up the business, why, there they are—provided for.", "그는 투츠 씨에게, 자기는 안경에 거부감이 없으며, 만일 박사가 점잖게 처신하여 일을 그만둔다면, 자, 그러면 그들은—생계가 보장되는 셈이라고 알려 준다."], ["He says it’s his opinion that when a man has made a handsome sum by his business, he is bound to give it up; and that Cornelia would be an assistance in it which any man might be proud of.", "그가 보기에는, 사람이 일을 해서 상당한 돈을 모았으면 마땅히 그 일을 그만두어야 한다는 것이고, 그러면 코닐리어가 그 일에 어떤 사내라도 자랑스러워할 만한 조력자가 되어 줄 거라고 한다."], ["Mr Toots replies by launching wildly out into Miss Dombey’s praises, and by insinuations that sometimes he thinks he should like to blow his brains out.", "투츠 씨는 돔비 아가씨에 대한 찬사를 미친 듯이 쏟아 내고, 가끔은 자기 머리통을 날려 버리고 싶은 심정이라는 암시로 대답한다."], ["Mr Feeder strongly urges that it would be a rash attempt, and shows him, as a reconcilement to existence, Cornelia’s portrait, spectacles and all.", "피더 씨는 그것이 경솔한 짓이 될 거라고 강력히 타이르며, 삶과 화해하는 방편으로 코닐리어의 초상화를, 안경까지 그려진 그것을 그에게 내보인다."]], [["Thus these quiet spirits pass the evening; and when it has yielded place to night, Mr Toots walks home with Mr Feeder, and parts with him at Doctor Blimber’s door.", "이렇게 이 조용한 영혼들은 저녁을 보낸다. 그리고 저녁이 밤에게 자리를 내주자, 투츠 씨는 피더 씨와 함께 걸어서 집으로 가, 블림버 박사의 문 앞에서 그와 헤어진다."], ["But Mr Feeder only goes up the steps, and when Mr Toots is gone, comes down again, to stroll upon the beach alone, and think about his prospects.", "그러나 피더 씨는 계단을 오르기만 했다가, 투츠 씨가 가 버리자 다시 내려와, 홀로 바닷가를 거닐며 자기 앞날을 생각한다."], ["Mr Feeder plainly hears the waves informing him, as he loiters along, that Doctor Blimber will give up the business; and he feels a soft romantic pleasure in looking at the outside of the house, and thinking that the Doctor will first paint it, and put it into thorough repair.", "피더 씨는, 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어가는 동안, 블림버 박사가 일을 그만둘 거라고 일러 주는 파도 소리를 또렷이 듣는다. 그리고 그 집의 겉모습을 바라보며, 박사가 먼저 거기에 칠을 새로 하고 말끔히 수리해 두리라고 생각하면서, 부드럽고 낭만적인 즐거움을 느낀다."]], [["Mr Toots is likewise roaming up and down, outside the casket that contains his jewel; and in a deplorable condition of mind, and not unsuspected by the police, gazes at a window where he sees a light, and which he has no doubt is Florence’s.", "투츠 씨도 마찬가지로, 자기 보석을 담고 있는 그 함 바깥을 이리저리 헤매고 있다. 그리고 한심하기 그지없는 마음 상태로, 경찰의 의심도 사 가면서, 불빛이 보이는 어느 창문을 응시하는데, 그곳이 플로렌스의 방임에 틀림없다고 여긴다."], ["But it is not, for that is Mrs Skewton’s room; and while Florence, sleeping in another chamber, dreams lovingly, in the midst of the old scenes, and their old associations live again, the figure which in grim reality is substituted for the patient boy’s on the same theatre, once more to connect it—but how differently!—with decay and death, is stretched there, wakeful and complaining.", "그러나 그게 아니다. 그곳은 스큐턴 부인의 방이니까."], ["Ugly and haggard it lies upon its bed of unrest; and by it, in the terror of her unimpassioned loveliness—for it has terror in the sufferer’s failing eyes—sits Edith.", "그리고 다른 방에서 잠든 플로렌스가 옛 정경들 한가운데서 사랑스러운 꿈을 꾸고, 그 옛 추억들이 다시 살아나는 동안, 그 인내심 강했던 소년의 자리를 같은 무대 위에서 냉혹한 현실로 대신하여, 그 무대를 다시금—그러나 얼마나 다르게!—쇠락과 죽음에 결부시키는 그 인물이, 그곳에 잠 못 든 채 불평하며 누워 있다. 그것은 흉하고 초췌하게, 안식 없는 침상 위에 누워 있다."], ["What do the waves say, in the stillness of the night, to them?", "그리고 그 곁에는, 자기의 무정한 아리따움이 자아내는 그 두려움 속에서—병자의 흐려져 가는 눈에는 그것이 두려움이기에—에디스가 앉아 있다. 밤의 적막 속에서 파도는 그들에게 무슨 말을 하는가?"]], [["“Edith, what is that stone arm raised to strike me?", "\"에디스, 나를 치려고 쳐든 저 돌 팔뚝은 뭐냐?"], ["Don’t you see it?”", "너는 안 보이느냐?\""]], [["“There is nothing, mother, but your fancy.”", "\"아무것도 없어요, 어머니, 어머니의 헛것일 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“But my fancy!", "\"하지만 내 헛것이라니!"], ["Everything is my fancy.", "모든 게 내 헛것이라는 거지."], ["Look!", "봐라!"], ["Is it possible that you don’t see it?”", "네가 저걸 못 본다는 게 가당키나 하냐?\""]], [["“Indeed, mother, there is nothing.", "\"정말로, 어머니, 아무것도 없어요."], ["Should I sit unmoved, if there were any such thing there?”", "만약 그런 게 있다면 제가 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있겠어요?\""]], [["“Unmoved?”", "\"꼼짝 않고?\""], ["looking wildly at her—“it’s gone now—and why are you so unmoved?", "사납게 딸을 노려보며—\"이제 사라졌구나—그런데 너는 어쩌면 그렇게 꼼짝도 않느냐?"], ["That is not my fancy, Edith.", "저건 내 헛것이 아니다, 에디스."], ["It turns me cold to see you sitting at my side.”", "네가 내 곁에 앉아 있는 걸 보고 있자니 몸이 다 서늘해진다.\""]], [["“I am sorry, mother.”", "\"죄송해요, 어머니.\""]], [["“Sorry!", "\"죄송하다고!"], ["You seem always sorry.", "너는 늘 죄송해하는 것 같구나."], ["But it is not for me!”", "하지만 그게 나를 위한 건 아니지!\""]], [["With that, she cries; and tossing her restless head from side to side upon her pillow, runs on about neglect, and the mother she has been, and the mother the good old creature was, whom they met, and the cold return the daughters of such mothers make.", "그 말과 함께 그녀가 운다. 그리고 베개 위에서 가만있지 못하는 머리를 이쪽저쪽 뒤척이면서, 홀대받았다느니, 자기가 어떤 어미였느니, 그들이 만났던 그 착한 노파가 어떤 어미였느니, 그런 어미를 둔 딸자식들이 얼마나 차갑게 보답하는지를 늘어놓는다."], ["In the midst of her incoherence, she stops, looks at her daughter, cries out that her wits are going, and hides her face upon the bed.", "그 두서없는 넋두리 한가운데서 그녀는 멈추고, 딸을 바라보고는, 자기 정신이 나가고 있다고 외치며, 침대에 얼굴을 파묻는다."]], [["Edith, in compassion, bends over her and speaks to her.", "에디스는 가엾은 마음에 그녀에게 몸을 굽혀 말을 건넨다."], ["The sick old woman clutches her round the neck, and says, with a look of horror,", "병든 노파는 그녀의 목을 끌어안고는, 공포에 질린 표정으로 말한다."]], [["“Edith!", "\"에디스!"], ["we are going home soon; going back.", "우리 곧 집에 가는 거지. 돌아가는 거지."], ["You mean that I shall go home again?”", "내가 다시 집으로 가게 된다는 뜻이지?\""]], [["“Yes, mother, yes.”", "\"네, 어머니, 네.\""]], [["“And what he said—what’s-his-name, I never could remember names—Major—that dreadful word, when we came away—it’s not true?", "\"그리고 그 사람이 한 말—그 사람 이름이 뭐더라, 나는 도무지 이름을 못 외운다니까—소령—우리가 떠나올 때 했던 그 끔찍한 말—그건 사실이 아니지?"], ["Edith!”", "에디스!\""], ["with a shriek and a stare, “it’s not that that is the matter with me.”", "비명을 지르며 멍하니 응시하며, \"나한테 그게 문제인 게 아니지.\""]], [["Night after night, the lights burn in the window, and the figure lies upon the bed, and Edith sits beside it, and the restless waves are calling to them both the whole night long.", "밤마다 창가에는 불빛이 타오르고, 그 인물은 침대에 누워 있고, 에디스는 그 곁에 앉아 있고, 잠들지 못하는 파도는 밤새도록 그 둘을 부른다."], ["Night after night, the waves are hoarse with repetition of their mystery; the dust lies piled upon the shore; the sea-birds soar and hover; the winds and clouds are on their trackless flight; the white arms beckon, in the moonlight, to the invisible country far away.", "밤마다 파도는 제 신비를 거듭거듭 되뇌느라 목이 쉬어 있고, 모래밭에는 먼지가 쌓여 있으며, 바닷새들은 솟구쳐 올랐다 맴돌고, 바람과 구름은 자취 없는 비행에 나서 있다. 하얀 팔들은 달빛 속에서, 저 멀리 보이지 않는 나라를 향해 손짓한다."]], [["And still the sick old woman looks into the corner, where the stone arm—part of a figure of some tomb, she says—is raised to strike her.", "그리고 여전히 병든 노파는 구석을 들여다보는데, 그곳에는 돌 팔뚝이—어느 무덤의 조상(彫像) 일부라고 그녀는 말한다—자기를 치려고 쳐들려 있다."], ["At last it falls; and then a dumb old woman lies upon the bed, and she is crooked and shrunk up, and half of her is dead.", "마침내 그것이 떨어진다. 그러고 나자 말을 잃은 한 노파가 침대에 누워 있고, 그녀는 몸이 굽고 오그라들었으며, 그 절반이 죽어 있다."]], [["Such is the figure, painted and patched for the sun to mock, that is drawn slowly through the crowd from day to day; looking, as it goes, for the good old creature who was such a mother, and making mouths as it peers among the crowd in vain.", "그것이 바로, 해가 비웃도록 분칠하고 덧대 꾸민 그 인물의 모습이니, 날마다 사람들 틈을 헤치며 천천히 끌려 다니고, 그렇게 가면서 그토록 좋은 어미였던 그 착한 노파를 찾으며, 사람들 사이를 헛되이 들여다보면서 얼굴을 일그러뜨린다."], ["Such is the figure that is often wheeled down to the margin of the sea, and stationed there; but on which no wind can blow freshness, and for which the murmur of the ocean has no soothing word.", "그것이 바로, 자주 바다의 가장자리로 수레에 실려 내려가 그곳에 세워지는 그 인물이니, 그러나 그 위로 어떤 바람도 신선함을 불어넣지 못하고, 그것을 위해 대양의 속삭임도 위로의 말 한마디 건네지 못한다."], ["She lies and listens to it by the hour; but its speech is dark and gloomy to her, and a dread is on her face, and when her eyes wander over the expanse, they see but a broad stretch of desolation between earth and heaven.", "그녀는 누운 채 몇 시간이고 그 소리에 귀를 기울인다. 그러나 그 말은 그녀에게 어둡고 음울하기만 하고, 그녀의 얼굴에는 두려움이 어려 있으며, 그 눈이 드넓은 바다 위를 헤맬 때면, 하늘과 땅 사이에 펼쳐진 황량함의 너른 자락밖에 보이지 않는다."]], [["Florence she seldom sees, and when she does, is angry with and mows at.", "그녀는 플로렌스를 좀처럼 보지 못하고, 보게 되더라도, 화를 내며 얼굴을 일그러뜨려 보인다."], ["Edith is beside her always, and keeps Florence away; and Florence, in her bed at night, trembles at the thought of death in such a shape, and often wakes and listens, thinking it has come.", "에디스는 늘 그녀 곁에 있으면서 플로렌스를 가까이 오지 못하게 한다. 그리고 플로렌스는 밤에 자기 침대에서, 그런 모습의 죽음을 생각하며 몸서리치고, 죽음이 찾아온 것은 아닌가 하여 자주 잠에서 깨어 귀를 기울인다."], ["No one attends on her but Edith.", "에디스 말고는 아무도 그녀를 돌보지 않는다."], ["It is better that few eyes should see her; and her daughter watches alone by the bedside.", "그녀를 보는 눈이 적을수록 좋다. 그래서 딸이 홀로 침상 곁을 지킨다."]], [["A shadow even on that shadowed face, a sharpening even of the sharpened features, and a thickening of the veil before the eyes into a pall that shuts out the dim world, is come.", "그 그늘진 얼굴에 그늘이 더해지고, 그 날카로운 이목구비가 한층 더 날카로워지며, 두 눈 앞의 베일이 두꺼워져, 흐릿한 세상을 가리는 관보(棺褓)가 되는 때가 왔다. 이불 위를 헤매던 그녀의 두 손이 힘없이 손바닥을 맞대 모이고는 딸을 향해 움직인다."], ["Her wandering hands upon the coverlet join feebly palm to palm, and move towards her daughter; and a voice not like hers, not like any voice that speaks our mortal language—says, “For I nursed you!”", "그리고 그녀의 것 같지 않은, 우리 인간의 언어를 말하는 그 어떤 목소리 같지도 않은 목소리가 말한다. \"내가 너를 길렀으니까!\""]], [["Edith, without a tear, kneels down to bring her voice closer to the sinking head, and answers:", "에디스는 눈물 한 방울 없이, 가라앉아 가는 그 머리에 자기 목소리를 더 가까이 가져가려 무릎을 꿇고, 대답한다."]], [["“Mother, can you hear me?”", "\"어머니, 제 말 들리세요?\""]], [["Staring wide, she tries to nod in answer.", "눈을 한껏 크게 뜬 채, 그녀는 대답으로 고개를 끄덕이려 애쓴다."]], [["“Can you recollect the night before I married?”", "\"제가 결혼하기 전날 밤을 기억하세요?\""]], [["The head is motionless, but it expresses somehow that she does.", "머리는 미동도 없지만, 어쩐지 그렇다는 뜻을 드러낸다."]], [["“I told you then that I forgave your part in it, and prayed God to forgive my own.", "\"그때 저는 어머니께, 거기서 어머니가 한 몫을 용서하며, 제 몫은 하느님께서 용서해 주시기를 빈다고 말했어요."], ["I told you that time past was at an end between us.", "우리 사이에 지나간 시간은 끝났다고 말했지요."], ["I say so now, again.", "지금 다시 그렇게 말할게요."], ["Kiss me, mother.”", "입을 맞춰 주세요, 어머니.\""]], [["Edith touches the white lips, and for a moment all is still.", "에디스가 그 하얀 입술에 입을 대자, 잠시 모든 것이 고요하다."], ["A moment afterwards, her mother, with her girlish laugh, and the skeleton of the Cleopatra manner, rises in her bed.", "한순간 뒤, 그녀의 어머니가, 소녀 같은 웃음과 클레오파트라 같은 자태의 해골을 하고서, 침대에서 몸을 일으킨다."]], [["Draw the rose-coloured curtains.", "장밋빛 휘장을 쳐라."], ["There is something else upon its flight besides the wind and clouds.", "바람과 구름 말고도 무언가가 날아오르고 있다."], ["Draw the rose-coloured curtains close!", "장밋빛 휘장을 바짝 쳐라!"]], [["Intelligence of the event is sent to Mr Dombey in town, who waits upon Cousin Feenix (not yet able to make up his mind for Baden-Baden), who has just received it too.", "그 사건의 소식이 시내에 있는 돔비 씨에게 전해지고, 돔비 씨는 (아직 바덴바덴으로 갈 마음을 정하지 못한) 사촌 피닉스를 찾아가는데, 그 역시 방금 그 소식을 받은 참이다."], ["A good-natured creature like Cousin Feenix is the very man for a marriage or a funeral, and his position in the family renders it right that he should be consulted.", "사촌 피닉스처럼 사람 좋은 인물은 결혼식이나 장례식에 안성맞춤인 사람이고, 집안에서 그가 차지하는 위치로 보아 그와 의논하는 것이 마땅하다."]], [["“Dombey,”", "\"돔비,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, “upon my soul, I am very much shocked to see you on such a melancholy occasion.", "사촌 피닉스가 말했다, \"맹세코, 이런 우울한 일로 자네를 보게 되어 정말 가슴이 아프네."], ["My poor aunt!", "가엾은 우리 이모님!"], ["She was a devilish lively woman.”", "정말이지 지독히도 활기찬 분이셨는데.\""]], [["Mr Dombey replies, “Very much so.”", "돔비 씨가 대답한다, \"정말 그러셨지요.\""]], [["“And made up,”", "\"그리고 화장을 하시면,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix, “really young, you know, considering.", "사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"정말이지 젊어 보이셨거든, 나이를 생각하면 말이야."], ["I am sure, on the day of your marriage, I thought she was good for another twenty years.", "분명히 자네 결혼식 날, 나는 그분이 앞으로 이십 년은 거뜬하실 거라고 생각했었지."], ["In point of fact, I said so to a man at Brooks’s—little Billy Joper—you know him, no doubt—man with a glass in his eye?”", "실은, 브룩스 클럽에서 어떤 사람한테 그렇게 말했었네—꼬마 빌리 조퍼라고—자네도 분명 알 거야—눈에 외알 안경을 낀 사람 말이야.\""]], [["Mr Dombey bows a negative.", "돔비 씨는 모른다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙인다."], ["“In reference to the obsequies,”", "\"장례 절차에 관해서,\""], ["he hints, “whether there is any suggestion—”", "그가 넌지시 말한다, \"혹시 무슨 의견이 있으신지—\""]], [["“Well, upon my life,”", "\"음, 맹세코,\" 사촌 피닉스가 턱을 쓰다듬으며 말한다."], ["says Cousin Feenix, stroking his chin, which he has just enough of hand below his wristbands to do; “I really don’t know.", "소맷부리 아래로 손이 딱 그만큼 나와 있어서 겨우 쓰다듬을 수 있다. \"나도 정말 모르겠네."], ["There’s a Mausoleum down at my place, in the park, but I’m afraid it’s in bad repair, and, in point of fact, in a devil of a state.", "우리 시골 영지의 정원에 영묘(靈廟)가 하나 있긴 한데, 아무래도 손볼 데가 많을 거야. 실은 형편없는 꼴이지."], ["But for being a little out at elbows, I should have had it put to rights; but I believe the people come and make pic-nic parties there inside the iron railings.”", "내 주머니 사정이 좀 쪼들리지만 않았어도 손을 좀 봐 뒀을 텐데. 게다가 사람들이 그 철책 안으로 들어와 소풍 놀이를 벌인다지 뭔가.\""]], [["Mr Dombey is clear that this won’t do.", "돔비 씨는 그건 안 되겠다고 분명히 생각한다."]], [["“There’s an uncommon good church in the village,” says Cousin Feenix, thoughtfully; “pure specimen of the Anglo-Norman style, and admirably well sketched too by Lady Jane Finchbury—woman with tight stays—but they’ve spoilt it with whitewash, I understand, and it’s a long journey.”", "\"마을에 유난히 좋은 교회가 하나 있긴 해,\" 사촌 피닉스가 생각에 잠겨 말한다. \"앵글로노르만 양식의 순수한 표본이고, 레이디 제인 핀치베리가—코르셋을 꽉 졸라매는 여자 말이야—아주 훌륭하게 스케치해 두기도 했지. 그런데 듣자니 회칠을 해서 다 망쳐 놨다더군. 게다가 길이 멀어.\""]], [["“Perhaps Brighton itself,”", "\"차라리 브라이턴 자체가 어떨까요,\""], ["Mr Dombey suggests.", "돔비 씨가 제안한다."]], [["“Upon my honour, Dombey, I don’t think we could do better,”", "\"내 명예를 걸고, 돔비, 그보다 나은 데가 없을 것 같네,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix.", "사촌 피닉스가 말한다."], ["“It’s on the spot, you see, and a very cheerful place.”", "\"보다시피 바로 이 자리이고, 아주 명랑한 곳이지.\""]], [["“And when,”", "\"그러면 언제가,\""], ["hints Mr Dombey, “would it be convenient?”", "돔비 씨가 넌지시 말한다, \"편하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I shall make a point,”", "\"나는 꼭,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix, “of pledging myself for any day you think best.", "사촌 피닉스가 말한다, \"자네가 가장 좋다고 생각하는 날이면 어느 날이든 맞추겠노라 약속하겠네."], ["I shall have great pleasure (melancholy pleasure, of course) in following my poor aunt to the confines of the—in point of fact, to the grave,”", "가엾은 우리 이모님을—그 경계까지—실은, 무덤까지 모시는 일에 큰 기쁨을(물론 우울한 기쁨이지만) 느낄 걸세,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix, failing in the other turn of speech.", "사촌 피닉스가 다른 식의 표현을 떠올리지 못한 채 말한다."]], [["“Would Monday do for leaving town?”", "\"월요일이면 시내를 떠나기에 괜찮겠습니까?\""], ["says Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Monday would suit me to perfection,”", "\"월요일이면 나한테 더할 나위 없이 좋네,\""], ["replies Cousin Feenix.", "사촌 피닉스가 대답한다."], ["Therefore Mr Dombey arranges to take Cousin Feenix down on that day, and presently takes his leave, attended to the stairs by Cousin Feenix, who says, at parting, “I’m really excessively sorry, Dombey, that you should have so much trouble about it;”", "그리하여 돔비 씨는 그날 사촌 피닉스를 모시고 내려가기로 정하고, 곧 작별을 고하는데, 사촌 피닉스가 계단까지 배웅하며 헤어질 즈음에 이렇게 말한다. \"정말이지 자네가 이 일로 그토록 수고를 하게 되어 무척 미안하네, 돔비.\""], ["to which Mr Dombey answers, “Not at all.”", "이에 돔비 씨는 대답한다. \"천만에요.\""]], [["At the appointed time, Cousin Feenix and Mr Dombey meet, and go down to Brighton, and representing, in their two selves, all the other mourners for the deceased lady’s loss, attend her remains to their place of rest.", "정해진 시각에 사촌 피닉스와 돔비 씨가 만나, 브라이턴으로 내려가, 그 둘만으로 고인을 잃고 애도하는 다른 모든 조문객을 대신하여, 고인의 유해를 안식처로 따라간다."], ["Cousin Feenix, sitting in the mourning-coach, recognises innumerable acquaintances on the road, but takes no other notice of them, in decorum, than checking them off aloud, as they go by, for Mr Dombey’s information, as “Tom Johnson.", "사촌 피닉스는 장례 마차에 앉아 길에서 수많은 지인을 알아보지만, 예의를 차려 그들에게 다른 내색은 하지 않고, 그저 그들이 지나갈 때마다 돔비 씨에게 알려 주려고 소리 내어 하나씩 짚어 나간다. \"톰 존슨이로군."], ["Man with cork leg, from White’s.", "화이트 클럽 사람으로, 의족을 한 사내지."], ["What, are you here, Tommy?", "아니, 여기 웬일인가, 토미?"], ["Foley on a blood mare.", "혈통 좋은 암말을 탄 폴리로군."], ["The Smalder girls”—and so forth.", "스몰더 자매들이야\"—그런 식이다."], ["At the ceremony Cousin Feenix is depressed, observing, that these are the occasions to make a man think, in point of fact, that he is getting shaky; and his eyes are really moistened, when it is over.", "의식이 진행되는 동안 사촌 피닉스는 의기소침해져서, 이런 일들을 겪으면 사람이 정말이지 자기도 흔들리기 시작했구나 생각하게 된다고 말하고, 의식이 끝났을 즈음에는 그의 눈도 정말로 촉촉이 젖는다."], ["But he soon recovers; and so do the rest of Mrs Skewton’s relatives and friends, of whom the Major continually tells the club that she never did wrap up enough; while the young lady with the back, who has so much trouble with her eyelids, says, with a little scream, that she must have been enormously old, and that she died of all kinds of horrors, and you mustn’t mention it.", "그러나 그는 곧 기운을 회복한다. 스큐턴 부인의 다른 친척과 친구들도 마찬가지인데, 그들 가운데 소령은 클럽에서 그녀가 도무지 몸을 충분히 싸매는 법이 없었다고 줄곧 떠벌리고, 한편 그 등을 드러낸, 눈꺼풀 때문에 그토록 애를 먹는 젊은 아가씨는, 작은 비명을 지르며, 그분이 엄청나게 나이가 많았던 게 틀림없으며, 온갖 끔찍한 병으로 돌아가셨고, 그 얘기는 꺼내선 안 된다고 말한다."]], [["So Edith’s mother lies unmentioned of her dear friends, who are deaf to the waves that are hoarse with repetition of their mystery, and blind to the dust that is piled upon the shore, and to the white arms that are beckoning, in the moonlight, to the invisible country far away.", "이리하여 에디스의 어머니는 자기 소중한 친구들에게 입에 오르내리지도 못한 채 누워 있다. 그 친구들은 제 신비를 거듭거듭 되뇌느라 목이 쉰 파도 소리에 귀가 먹었고, 모래밭에 쌓인 먼지에도, 달빛 속에서 저 멀리 보이지 않는 나라를 향해 손짓하는 하얀 팔들에도 눈이 멀어 있다."], ["But all goes on, as it was wont, upon the margin of the unknown sea; and Edith standing there alone, and listening to its waves, has dank weed cast up at her feet, to strew her path in life withal.", "그러나 모든 것이 늘 그러했듯이, 그 미지의 바다 가장자리에서 흘러간다. 그리고 그곳에 홀로 서서 파도 소리에 귀 기울이는 에디스의 발치에는, 그녀가 인생의 길을 위에 흩뿌리며 걸어갈, 축축한 해초가 밀려와 던져진다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLII. Confidential and Accidental", "# CHAPTER XLII. Confidential and Accidental"]], [["Attired no more in Captain Cuttle’s sable slops and sou’-wester hat, but dressed in a substantial suit of brown livery, which, while it affected to be a very sober and demure livery indeed, was really as self-satisfied and confident a one as tailor need desire to make, Rob the Grinder, thus transformed as to his outer man, and all regardless within of the Captain and the Midshipman, except when he devoted a few minutes of his leisure time to crowing over those inseparable worthies, and recalling, with much applauding music from that brazen instrument, his conscience, the triumphant manner in which he had disembarrassed himself of their company, now served his patron, Mr Carker.", "더 이상 커틀 선장의 거무튀튀한 작업복과 차양 모자 차림이 아니라, 갈색 제복 한 벌을 든든히 차려입은—그 제복은 무척 점잖고 얌전한 척하면서도 실은 재단사가 만들고 싶어 할 만큼 흡족하고 자신만만한 물건이었다—그라인더 롭은, 겉모습이 이렇게 탈바꿈한 데다, 자기 양심이라는 그 뻔뻔스러운 악기에서 갈채의 음악을 한껏 울려 가며, 그 둘도 없는 호인들의 곁을 어떻게 의기양양하게 떨쳐 냈는지를 떠올리며 여가의 몇 분을 그들을 향해 거들먹대는 일에 쏟을 때를 제외하고는, 속으로는 선장이며 미드십맨이며 전혀 안중에도 없이, 이제는 자기 후원자인 카커 씨를 섬기고 있었다."], ["Inmate of Mr Carker’s house, and serving about his person, Rob kept his round eyes on the white teeth with fear and trembling, and felt that he had need to open them wider than ever.", "카커 씨의 집에 들어앉아 그의 시중을 들면서, 롭은 그 흰 이를 두려움과 떨림으로 둥근 눈으로 지켜보았으며, 그 눈을 어느 때보다 더 크게 떠야 할 필요가 있다고 느꼈다."]], [["He could not have quaked more, through his whole being, before the teeth, though he had come into the service of some powerful enchanter, and they had been his strongest spells.", "그가 어떤 강력한 마법사의 부림을 받게 되어 그 이들이 그의 가장 강한 주문이었다 한들, 온몸을 통째로 그 이들 앞에서 이보다 더 떨 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["The boy had a sense of power and authority in this patron of his that engrossed his whole attention and exacted his most implicit submission and obedience.", "소년은 이 후원자에게서 어떤 힘과 권위를 느꼈는데, 그것이 그의 온 신경을 빨아들이고 가장 무조건적인 복종과 순종을 강요했다."], ["He hardly considered himself safe in thinking about him when he was absent, lest he should feel himself immediately taken by the throat again, as on the morning when he first became bound to him, and should see every one of the teeth finding him out, and taxing him with every fancy of his mind.", "그는 카커 씨가 없을 때조차 그에 대해 생각하는 것만으로도 자신이 안전하다고 여기지 못했으니, 그에게 처음 매이게 된 그날 아침처럼 당장 멱살이 잡히기라도 할까, 그리고 그 이가 하나하나 자기를 찾아내어 마음속 온갖 공상을 추궁하기라도 할까 두려웠던 것이다."], ["Face to face with him, Rob had no more doubt that Mr Carker read his secret thoughts, or that he could read them by the least exertion of his will if he were so inclined, than he had that Mr Carker saw him when he looked at him.", "면전에서는, 카커 씨가 자기의 은밀한 생각을 읽어 낸다는 것, 혹은 마음만 먹으면 의지를 조금만 발휘해도 읽어 낼 수 있다는 것을, 카커 씨가 자기를 쳐다볼 때 자기를 보고 있다는 것 못지않게 의심하지 않았다."], ["The ascendancy was so complete, and held him in such enthralment, that, hardly daring to think at all, but with his mind filled with a constantly dilating impression of his patron’s irresistible command over him, and power of doing anything with him, he would stand watching his pleasure, and trying to anticipate his orders, in a state of mental suspension, as to all other things.", "그 지배력은 어찌나 완벽했고 그를 어찌나 사로잡았던지, 그는 도무지 생각이라곤 거의 하지 못한 채, 후원자가 자기에게 행사하는 그 거역할 수 없는 명령과 자기를 마음대로 부릴 수 있는 권능에 대한 인상이 끊임없이 부풀어 오르는 것으로 마음이 가득 찬 채, 다른 모든 것에 대해서는 정신이 정지된 상태로 그의 비위를 살피며 서서 그의 명령을 미리 헤아리려 애쓰곤 했다."]], [["Rob had not informed himself perhaps—in his then state of mind it would have been an act of no common temerity to inquire—whether he yielded so completely to this influence in any part, because he had floating suspicions of his patron’s being a master of certain treacherous arts in which he had himself been a poor scholar at the Grinders’ School.", "롭은 어쩌면—당시의 마음 상태로는 그것을 캐묻는 것 자체가 여간한 무모함이 아니었을 것이다—자기가 이 영향력에 이토록 완전히 굴복하는 것이 어느 부분이라도, 그 후원자가 자기도 그라인더 학교에서 변변찮은 학생으로 배운 어떤 음흉한 술수의 대가일지 모른다는 막연한 의심이 떠다녔기 때문은 아닌지 스스로 알아보지 않았던 모양이다."], ["But certainly Rob admired him, as well as feared him.", "그러나 롭이 그를 두려워하는 만큼이나 그를 우러러보았던 것만은 분명하다."], ["Mr Carker, perhaps, was better acquainted with the sources of his power, which lost nothing by his management of it.", "어쩌면 카커 씨가 자기 권능의 원천을 더 잘 알고 있었을 터인데, 그 권능은 그가 그것을 다루는 방식에 의해 조금도 손상되지 않았다."]], [["On the very night when he left the Captain’s service, Rob, after disposing of his pigeons, and even making a bad bargain in his hurry, had gone straight down to Mr Carker’s house, and hotly presented himself before his new master with a glowing face that seemed to expect commendation.", "선장의 부림에서 벗어난 바로 그날 밤, 롭은 비둘기를 처분하고—급한 마음에 손해 보는 흥정까지 해 가며—곧장 카커 씨의 집으로 내려가, 칭찬을 기대하는 듯 상기된 얼굴로 새 주인 앞에 기세 좋게 나타났다."]], [["“What, scapegrace!”", "\"이런, 망나니 녀석!\""], ["said Mr Carker, glancing at his bundle “Have you left your situation and come to me?”", "카커 씨가 그의 보따리를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"네 일자리를 버리고 나한테로 왔단 말이냐?\""]], [["“Oh if you please, Sir,”", "\"오, 죄송합니다만, 나리,\""], ["faltered Rob, “you said, you know, when I come here last—”", "롭이 더듬거렸다, \"지난번에 제가 여기 왔을 때, 나리께서 말씀하셨잖습니까—\""]], [["“I said,”", "\"내가 말했다고,\""], ["returned Mr Carker, “what did I say?”", "카커 씨가 응수했다, \"내가 뭐라고 했더냐?\""]], [["“If you please, Sir, you didn’t say nothing at all, Sir,”", "\"죄송합니다만, 나리, 아무 말씀도 안 하셨습니다, 나리,\""], ["returned Rob, warned by the manner of this inquiry, and very much disconcerted.", "롭이 이 물음의 어조에 경계심이 들어, 잔뜩 당황한 채 대답했다."]], [["His patron looked at him with a wide display of gums, and shaking his forefinger, observed:", "그의 후원자는 잇몸을 활짝 드러내며 그를 바라보았고, 집게손가락을 흔들며 말했다."]], [["“You’ll come to an evil end, my vagabond friend, I foresee.", "\"넌 험한 끝을 보게 될 거다, 내 떠돌이 친구야. 내 눈에 다 보인다."], ["There’s ruin in store for you.", "네 앞에는 파멸이 기다리고 있어.\""]], [["“Oh if you please, don’t, Sir!”", "\"오, 제발 그러지 마십시오, 나리!\""], ["cried Rob, with his legs trembling under him.", "롭이 두 다리를 후들거리며 외쳤다."], ["“I’m sure, Sir, I only want to work for you, Sir, and to wait upon you, Sir, and to do faithful whatever I’m bid, Sir.”", "\"정말이지, 나리, 저는 그저 나리를 위해 일하고, 나리를 시중들고, 시키시는 건 무엇이든 충실히 하고 싶을 뿐입니다, 나리.\""]], [["“You had better do faithfully whatever you are bid,”", "\"네가 나하고 무슨 관계라도 맺을 거라면,\""], ["returned his patron, “if you have anything to do with me.”", "그의 후원자가 응수했다, \"시키는 건 무엇이든 충실히 하는 게 좋을 거다.\""]], [["“Yes, I know that, Sir,”", "\"네, 그건 압니다, 나리,\""], ["pleaded the submissive Rob; “I’m sure of that, Sir.", "고분고분한 롭이 애원했다. \"확실히 알고 있습니다, 나리."], ["If you’ll only be so good as try me, Sir!", "한 번만 저를 시험 삼아 써 주신다면요, 나리!"], ["And if ever you find me out, Sir, doing anything against your wishes, I give you leave to kill me.”", "그리고 만에 하나라도 제가 나리 뜻을 거스르는 짓을 하는 걸 발견하시거든, 저를 죽이셔도 좋습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“You dog!”", "\"이 개 같은 놈아!\""], ["said Mr Carker, leaning back in his chair, and smiling at him serenely.", "카커 씨가 의자에 등을 기대며, 그를 향해 태연히 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“That’s nothing to what I’d do to you, if you tried to deceive me.”", "\"네가 나를 속이려 들기라도 하면, 내가 너한테 할 짓에 비하면 그건 아무것도 아니다.\""]], [["“Yes, Sir,”", "\"네, 나리,\""], ["replied the abject Grinder, “I’m sure you would be down upon me dreadful, Sir.", "비굴한 그라인더가 대답했다, \"확실히 무섭게 저를 짓밟으시겠지요, 나리."], ["I wouldn’t attempt for to go and do it, Sir, not if I was bribed with golden guineas.”", "황금 기니로 매수당한다 해도, 저는 그런 짓을 하려 들지 않겠습니다, 나리.\""]], [["Thoroughly checked in his expectations of commendation, the crestfallen Grinder stood looking at his patron, and vainly endeavouring not to look at him, with the uneasiness which a cur will often manifest in a similar situation.", "칭찬에 대한 기대가 완전히 꺾인 채, 풀이 죽은 그라인더는 후원자를 바라보며, 그러면서도 보지 않으려 헛되이 애쓰며 서 있었으니, 그것은 비슷한 처지의 똥개가 흔히 드러내는 그런 불안이었다."]], [["“So you have left your old service, and come here to ask me to take you into mine, eh?”", "\"그래, 옛 일자리를 버리고 여기 와서 나더러 너를 거두어 달라고 청하는 거란 말이지, 응?\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, if you please, Sir,”", "\"네, 죄송합니다만, 나리,\""], ["returned Rob, who, in doing so, had acted on his patron’s own instructions, but dared not justify himself by the least insinuation to that effect.", "롭이 대답했다. 사실 그렇게 한 것은 후원자 자신의 지시에 따른 것이었으나, 그는 그런 취지를 조금이라도 내비쳐 자기를 변호할 엄두를 내지 못했다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"좋다!\""], ["said Mr Carker.", "카커 씨가 말했다."], ["“You know me, boy?”", "\"넌 나를 알지, 얘야?\""]], [["“Please, Sir, yes, Sir,”", "\"죄송합니다만, 네, 압니다, 나리,\""], ["returned Rob, tumbling with his hat, and still fixed by Mr Carker’s eye, and fruitlessly endeavouring to unfix himself.", "롭이 모자를 만지작거리며, 여전히 카커 씨의 눈에 붙들린 채, 거기서 벗어나려 헛되이 애쓰며 대답했다."]], [["Mr Carker nodded.", "카커 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“Take care, then!”", "\"그럼 조심해라!\""]], [["Rob expressed in a number of short bows his lively understanding of this caution, and was bowing himself back to the door, greatly relieved by the prospect of getting on the outside of it, when his patron stopped him.", "롭은 여러 차례 짤막한 절로 이 경고를 똑똑히 알아들었음을 표시하고는, 문밖으로 나갈 수 있다는 전망에 크게 안도하며 절을 하면서 문 쪽으로 물러나고 있었는데, 그때 후원자가 그를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"이봐!\""], ["he cried, calling him roughly back.", "그가 거칠게 그를 도로 불러 외쳤다."], ["“You have been—shut that door.”", "\"넌 말이지—그 문 닫아라.\""]], [["Rob obeyed as if his life had depended on his alacrity.", "롭은 자기 목숨이 그 민첩함에 달리기라도 한 듯 복종했다."]], [["“You have been used to eaves-dropping.", "\"넌 엿듣는 데 익숙하지."], ["Do you know what that means?”", "그게 무슨 뜻인지 아느냐?\""]], [["“Listening, Sir?”", "\"엿듣는 거, 말씀입니까, 나리?\""], ["Rob hazarded, after some embarrassed reflection.", "롭이 당황해서 잠시 생각한 끝에 어림짐작으로 말했다."]], [["His patron nodded.", "그의 후원자가 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“And watching, and so forth.”", "\"그리고 엿보고, 그런 것들 말이다.\""]], [["“I wouldn’t do such a thing here, Sir,”", "\"전 여기서는 그런 짓 안 하겠습니다, 나리,\""], ["answered Rob; “upon my word and honour, I wouldn’t, Sir, I wish I may die if I would, Sir, for anything that could be promised to me.", "롭이 대답했다. \"맹세코, 정말로 안 하겠습니다, 나리, 만약 한다면 죽어도 좋습니다, 나리, 무슨 대가를 약속받든 말입니다."], ["I should consider it is as much as all the world was worth, to offer to do such a thing, unless I was ordered, Sir.”", "시키지 않으신 한, 그런 짓을 하겠다고 나서는 건 온 세상을 다 준대도 못 할 일이라 여기겠습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“You had better not”", "\"안 하는 게 좋을 거다."], ["You have been used, too, to babbling and tattling,”", "넌 지껄이고 고자질하는 데도 익숙하지,\""], ["said his patron with perfect coolness.", "그의 후원자가 더없이 냉정하게 말했다."], ["“Beware of that here, or you’re a lost rascal,”", "\"여기선 그걸 조심해라, 안 그러면 넌 영락없는 망종이다.\""], ["and he smiled again, and again cautioned him with his forefinger.", "그러고는 다시 미소 지으며, 다시 집게손가락으로 그에게 경고했다."]], [["The Grinder’s breath came short and thick with consternation.", "그라인더의 숨은 경악으로 짧고 가빠졌다."], ["He tried to protest the purity of his intentions, but could only stare at the smiling gentleman in a stupor of submission, with which the smiling gentleman seemed well enough satisfied, for he ordered him downstairs, after observing him for some moments in silence, and gave him to understand that he was retained in his employment.", "그는 자기 의도의 결백함을 항변하려 애썼으나, 멍한 복종 상태에서 미소 짓는 신사를 멀거니 쳐다볼 수밖에 없었다. 미소 짓는 신사는 그것으로 꽤 만족한 듯, 잠시 말없이 그를 살펴본 뒤 아래층으로 내려가라 명하고는, 그가 자기에게 고용되었음을 알려 주었다."]], [["This was the manner of Rob the Grinder’s engagement by Mr Carker, and his awe-stricken devotion to that gentleman had strengthened and increased, if possible, with every minute of his service.", "이것이 카커 씨가 그라인더 롭을 들인 경위였고, 그 신사에 대한 외경에 찬 그의 헌신은, 가능하다면, 섬기는 시간이 한 분 한 분 흐를수록 더 굳어지고 자라났다."]], [["It was a service of some months’ duration, when early one morning, Rob opened the garden gate to Mr Dombey, who was come to breakfast with his master, by appointment.", "몇 달간 이어진 섬김의 끝 무렵, 어느 이른 아침, 롭은 약속에 따라 자기 주인과 아침을 함께하러 온 돔비 씨에게 정원 문을 열어 주었다."], ["At the same moment his master himself came, hurrying forth to receive the distinguished guest, and give him welcome with all his teeth.", "바로 그 순간 주인 자신도, 그 귀하신 손님을 맞이하러, 또 온 이를 다 드러내며 환대하러 서둘러 나왔다."]], [["“I never thought,”", "\"여기서 뵙게 될 줄은,\""], ["said Carker, when he had assisted him to alight from his horse, “to see you here, I’m sure.", "카커가 그가 말에서 내리는 것을 거든 뒤 말했다, \"정말이지 꿈에도 생각 못 했습니다."], ["This is an extraordinary day in my calendar.", "제 달력에서는 참으로 특별한 날이군요."], ["No occasion is very special to a man like you, who may do anything; but to a man like me, the case is widely different.”", "무엇이든 마음대로 하실 수 있는 분에게야 어떤 일도 그리 특별할 게 없겠지만, 저 같은 사람에게는 사정이 사뭇 다르지요.\""]], [["“You have a tasteful place here, Carker,”", "\"여긴 취향이 좋은 곳이군, 카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, condescending to stop upon the lawn, to look about him.", "돔비 씨가, 잔디밭에 황송하게 멈춰 서서 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["“You can afford to say so,”", "\"그렇게 말씀해 주실 형편이 되시는 분이지요,\""], ["returned Carker.", "카커가 응수했다."], ["“Thank you.”", "\"감사합니다.\""]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"실은,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, in his lofty patronage, “anyone might say so.", "돔비 씨가 도도한 생색을 내며 말했다, \"누구라도 그렇게 말할 걸세."], ["As far as it goes, it is a very commodious and well-arranged place—quite elegant.”", "적어도 그만한 정도로는, 아주 널찍하고 짜임새 있게 잘 꾸민 곳이야—꽤 우아하네.\""]], [["“As far as it goes, truly,”", "\"적어도 그만한 정도로는, 참으로 그렇지요,\""], ["returned Carker, with an air of disparagement.", "카커가 깎아내리는 투로 응수했다."], ["“It wants that qualification.", "\"그 단서가 필요하지요."], ["Well!", "뭐!"], ["we have said enough about it; and though you can afford to praise it, I thank you nonetheless.", "이 얘긴 이만하면 됐고, 칭찬해 주실 형편이 되시긴 하나, 그래도 감사드립니다."], ["Will you walk in?”", "안으로 드시겠습니까?\""]], [["Mr Dombey, entering the house, noticed, as he had reason to do, the complete arrangement of the rooms, and the numerous contrivances for comfort and effect that abounded there.", "집 안으로 들어선 돔비 씨는, 그럴 만한 이유가 있었기에, 방들의 완벽한 배치와, 거기 넘쳐 나는 안락과 효과를 위한 수많은 장치들을 눈여겨보았다."], ["Mr Carker, in his ostentation of humility, received this notice with a deferential smile, and said he understood its delicate meaning, and appreciated it, but in truth the cottage was good enough for one in his position—better, perhaps, than such a man should occupy, poor as it was.", "카커 씨는 겸손을 과시하느라 이 주목을 공손한 미소로 받으며, 그 미묘한 의미를 이해하고 고맙게 여긴다고, 그러나 사실 이 오두막은 자기 처지의 사람에게는 과분할 만큼 훌륭한—어쩌면 이렇게 누추하긴 해도 자기 같은 사람이 차지하기에는 너무 좋은—곳이라고 말했다."]], [["“But perhaps to you, who are so far removed, it really does look better than it is,”", "\"하지만 어쩌면 이렇게 멀리 동떨어져 계신 나리께는, 실제보다 더 좋아 보이는 모양입니다,\""], ["he said, with his false mouth distended to its fullest stretch.", "그가 거짓된 입을 한껏 늘여 가며 말했다."], ["“Just as monarchs imagine attractions in the lives of beggars.”", "\"마치 군주들이 거지의 삶에서 매력을 상상하듯 말이지요.\""]], [["He directed a sharp glance and a sharp smile at Mr Dombey as he spoke, and a sharper glance, and a sharper smile yet, when Mr Dombey, drawing himself up before the fire, in the attitude so often copied by his second in command, looked round at the pictures on the walls.", "그는 그렇게 말하며 돔비 씨에게 날카로운 시선과 날카로운 미소를 던졌고, 돔비 씨가 난롯불 앞에서 몸을 한껏 곧추세우는—그의 부관이 그토록 자주 흉내 내던 그 자세로—벽에 걸린 그림들을 돌아보았을 때는, 더욱 날카로운 시선과 더욱 날카로운 미소를 보냈다."], ["Cursorily as his cold eye wandered over them, Carker’s keen glance accompanied his, and kept pace with his, marking exactly where it went, and what it saw.", "돔비 씨의 차가운 눈이 그것들 위를 무심히 훑었지만, 카커의 예리한 눈길은 그의 눈길과 동행하며, 그 보조를 맞추어, 그 눈이 어디로 가서 무엇을 보는지 정확히 짚어 냈다."], ["As it rested on one picture in particular, Carker hardly seemed to breathe, his sidelong scrutiny was so cat-like and vigilant, but the eye of his great chief passed from that, as from the others, and appeared no more impressed by it than by the rest.", "그 눈이 특히 한 그림 위에 머물렀을 때, 카커는 거의 숨도 쉬지 않는 듯했으니, 그 곁눈질의 살핌이 그토록 고양이처럼 빈틈없고 경계로 차 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그 위대한 우두머리의 눈은 다른 그림들에서와 마찬가지로 그 그림에서 지나갔고, 나머지 것들보다 더 감명받은 기색도 없어 보였다."]], [["Carker looked at it—it was the picture that resembled Edith—as if it were a living thing; and with a wicked, silent laugh upon his face, that seemed in part addressed to it, though it was all derisive of the great man standing so unconscious beside him.", "카커는 그것을—그것은 이디스를 닮은 그림이었다—마치 살아 있는 것인 양 바라보았다. 그러면서 그의 얼굴에는 사악하고 소리 없는 웃음이 떠올랐는데, 그것은 한편으로는 그 그림을 향한 것 같았으나, 사실은 그토록 아무것도 모른 채 그 옆에 서 있는 위대한 인물을 모조리 비웃는 것이었다."], ["Breakfast was soon set upon the table; and, inviting Mr Dombey to a chair which had its back towards this picture, he took his own seat opposite to it as usual.", "곧 아침 식사가 식탁에 차려졌고, 돔비 씨를 이 그림에 등을 진 의자로 청한 뒤, 카커는 평소처럼 그 맞은편 자기 자리에 앉았다."]], [["Mr Dombey was even graver than it was his custom to be, and quite silent.", "돔비 씨는 평소 버릇보다도 더 근엄했으며, 아주 말이 없었다."], ["The parrot, swinging in the gilded hoop within her gaudy cage, attempted in vain to attract notice, for Carker was too observant of his visitor to heed her; and the visitor, abstracted in meditation, looked fixedly, not to say sullenly, over his stiff neckcloth, without raising his eyes from the table-cloth.", "화려한 새장 안 도금된 고리에 매달려 흔들거리는 앵무새가 헛되이 주의를 끌려 애썼으나, 카커는 손님을 살피느라 새에게 신경 쓸 겨를이 없었다. 그리고 손님은, 골똘한 명상에 빠진 채, 식탁보에서 눈을 떼지 않고, 그 빳빳한 목도리 너머로, 시무룩하다 해도 좋을 만큼 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["As to Rob, who was in attendance, all his faculties and energies were so locked up in observation of his master, that he scarcely ventured to give shelter to the thought that the visitor was the great gentleman before whom he had been carried as a certificate of the family health, in his childhood, and to whom he had been indebted for his leather smalls.", "시중을 들던 롭으로 말하자면, 그의 온 기능과 정력이 주인을 살피는 데 너무도 사로잡혀 있어서, 이 손님이 바로 자기가 어린 시절 집안의 건강 증명서로서 안겨 가 보여졌던 그 위대한 신사이며, 자기가 가죽 반바지를 신세 진 분이라는 생각을 마음속에 들일 엄두조차 거의 내지 못했다."]], [["“Allow me,”", "\"실례지만,\""], ["said Carker suddenly, “to ask how Mrs Dombey is?”", "카커가 불쑥 말했다, \"돔비 부인께서는 어떠신지 여쭤도 될까요?\""]], [["He leaned forward obsequiously, as he made the inquiry, with his chin resting on his hand; and at the same time his eyes went up to the picture, as if he said to it, “Now, see, how I will lead him on!”", "그는 이 물음을 던지며, 턱을 손에 괸 채 비굴하게 몸을 앞으로 기울였다. 그와 동시에 그의 눈은 그림 쪽으로 올라갔으니, 마치 그 그림에게 \"자, 봐라, 내가 어떻게 이 사람을 끌고 가는지!\" 하고 말하는 듯했다."]], [["Mr Dombey reddened as he answered:", "돔비 씨는 대답하며 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“Mrs Dombey is quite well.", "\"돔비 부인은 아주 잘 지내네."], ["You remind me, Carker, of some conversation that I wish to have with you.”", "카커, 자네 말을 들으니 자네와 나누고 싶던 어떤 이야기가 생각나는군.\""]], [["“Robin, you can leave us,”", "\"로빈, 우린 좀 나가 있어도 좋다,\""], ["said his master, at whose mild tones Robin started and disappeared, with his eyes fixed on his patron to the last.", "주인이 말하자, 그 부드러운 어조에도 로빈은 흠칫 놀라더니, 마지막 순간까지 후원자에게 눈을 고정한 채 사라졌다."], ["“You don’t remember that boy, of course?”", "\"물론 자네는 저 아이를 기억하지 못하겠지?\""], ["he added, when the enmeshed Grinder was gone.", "그 옭아매인 그라인더가 나가자 그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“No,”", "\"못하네,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with magnificent indifference.", "돔비 씨가 더없이 도도한 무관심으로 말했다."]], [["“Not likely that a man like you would.", "\"나리 같으신 분이 기억하실 리가요."], ["Hardly possible,”", "거의 불가능한 일이지요,\""], ["murmured Carker.", "카커가 중얼거렸다."], ["“But he is one of that family from whom you took a nurse.", "\"하지만 저 아이는 나리께서 유모를 데려오셨던 그 집안 출신입니다."], ["Perhaps you may remember having generously charged yourself with his education?”", "어쩌면 나리께서 너그럽게도 저 아이의 교육을 맡으셨던 일을 기억하실지도 모르겠군요?\""]], [["“Is it that boy?”", "\"그 아이인가?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with a frown.", "돔비 씨가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["“He does little credit to his education, I believe.”", "\"그 교육에 별로 보답이 못 되는 아이인 모양이군.\""]], [["“Why, he is a young rip, I am afraid,”", "\"글쎄요, 변변찮은 망나니 녀석이지요,\""], ["returned Carker, with a shrug.", "카커가 어깨를 으쓱하며 응수했다."], ["“He bears that character.", "\"그런 평판을 듣습니다."], ["But the truth is, I took him into my service because, being able to get no other employment, he conceived (had been taught at home, I daresay) that he had some sort of claim upon you, and was constantly trying to dog your heels with his petition.", "하지만 사실인즉, 제가 저 아이를 거둔 건, 다른 일자리를 도무지 못 구하던 차에, 저 아이가 나리께 무슨 청구권 같은 게 있다고 여기고는(틀림없이 집에서 그렇게 배웠겠지요), 줄곧 그 청원으로 나리 뒤꽁무니를 쫓아다니려 했기 때문입니다."], ["And although my defined and recognised connexion with your affairs is merely of a business character, still I have that spontaneous interest in everything belonging to you, that—”", "그리고 나리의 일과 관련한 저의 명확하고 공인된 관계가 단지 사무적인 성격일 뿐이라 해도, 저는 나리께 속한 모든 것에 자연스레 우러나오는 관심을 가지고 있어서—\""]], [["He stopped again, as if to discover whether he had led Mr Dombey far enough yet.", "그는 다시 멈추었으니, 마치 자기가 돔비 씨를 충분히 멀리까지 끌고 왔는지 알아보려는 듯했다."], ["And again, with his chin resting on his hand, he leered at the picture.", "그리고 다시, 턱을 손에 괸 채, 그 그림을 곁눈질로 흘겼다."]], [["“Carker,”", "\"카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “I am sensible that you do not limit your—”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"나는 자네가 자네의—한정 짓지 않는다는 걸 잘 알고 있네—\""]], [["“Service,”", "\"섬김을,\""], ["suggested his smiling entertainer.", "미소 짓는 주인이 거들었다."]], [["“No; I prefer to say your regard,”", "\"아니, 나는 차라리 자네의 마음 씀이라고 하겠네,\""], ["observed Mr Dombey; very sensible, as he said so, that he was paying him a handsome and flattering compliment, “to our mere business relations.", "돔비 씨가 말하며, 그렇게 말하는 것이 그에게 후하고 우쭐한 찬사를 베푸는 것임을 잘 의식한 채, \"단지 우리의 사무적 관계에만 한정 짓지 않는다는 걸 말이지."], ["Your consideration for my feelings, hopes, and disappointments, in the little instance you have just now mentioned, is an example in point.", "방금 자네가 언급한 그 사소한 일에서, 나의 감정과 희망과 실망에 대한 자네의 배려가 바로 그 한 예일세."], ["I am obliged to you, Carker.”", "고맙네, 카커.\""]], [["Mr Carker bent his head slowly, and very softly rubbed his hands, as if he were afraid by any action to disturb the current of Mr Dombey’s confidence.", "카커 씨는 천천히 고개를 숙이고, 마치 어떤 행동으로든 돔비 씨의 신뢰의 물줄기를 흩뜨릴까 두려운 듯, 두 손을 아주 부드럽게 비볐다."]], [["“Your allusion to it is opportune,”", "\"자네가 그 일을 언급한 건 때맞춤일세,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, after a little hesitation; “for it prepares the way to what I was beginning to say to you, and reminds me that that involves no absolutely new relations between us, although it may involve more personal confidence on my part than I have hitherto—”", "돔비 씨가 잠시 망설인 뒤 말했다, \"그것이 내가 자네에게 말하려던 것으로 가는 길을 열어 주고, 또 그 일이 우리 사이에 전혀 새로운 관계를 끌어들이지는 않는다는 걸 일깨워 주니까. 비록 그것이 지금까지 내가—\""]], [["“Distinguished me with,”", "\"저를 영예롭게 해 주신 것보다,\" 카커가 다시 고개를 숙이며 거들었다."], ["suggested Carker, bending his head again: “I will not say to you how honoured I am; for a man like you well knows how much honour he has in his power to bestow at pleasure.”", "\"제가 얼마나 영광스러운지는 말씀드리지 않겠습니다. 나리 같으신 분이라면 마음 내키는 대로 베풀 수 있는 영예가 얼마나 큰지를 잘 아실 테니까요.\""]], [["“Mrs Dombey and myself,”", "\"돔비 부인과 나는,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, passing this compliment with august self-denial, “are not quite agreed upon some points.", "돔비 씨가, 그 찬사를 위엄 어린 극기로 흘려넘기며 말했다, \"몇몇 점에서 의견이 그리 일치하지 않네."], ["We do not appear to understand each other yet.", "우리는 아직 서로를 이해하지 못하는 듯해."], ["Mrs Dombey has something to learn.”", "돔비 부인은 배워야 할 것이 있어.\""]], [["“Mrs Dombey is distinguished by many rare attractions; and has been accustomed, no doubt, to receive much adulation,”", "\"돔비 부인께서는 보기 드문 매력을 여럿 지니셨고, 의심할 바 없이 많은 찬미를 받는 데 익숙하셨겠지요,\""], ["said the smooth, sleek watcher of his slightest look and tone.", "그의 사소한 눈짓과 어조 하나하나를 살피는 그 매끄럽고 반드러운 자가 말했다."], ["“But where there is affection, duty, and respect, any little mistakes engendered by such causes are soon set right.”", "\"하지만 애정과 의무와 존경이 있는 곳에서는, 그런 까닭에서 빚어진 자잘한 잘못들은 곧 바로잡히기 마련입니다.\""]], [["Mr Dombey’s thoughts instinctively flew back to the face that had looked at him in his wife’s dressing-room when an imperious hand was stretched towards the door; and remembering the affection, duty, and respect, expressed in it, he felt the blood rush to his own face quite as plainly as the watchful eyes upon him saw it there.", "돔비 씨의 생각은 본능적으로, 거만한 손이 문 쪽으로 뻗어지던 그때 아내의 화장실에서 자기를 바라보던 그 얼굴로 거슬러 날아갔다. 그리고 거기에 드러나 있던 애정과 의무와 존경을 떠올리며, 그는 자기를 살피는 그 눈이 분명히 본 것만큼이나 또렷이, 자기 얼굴로 피가 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Mrs Dombey and myself,”", "\"돔비 부인과 나는,\""], ["he went on to say, “had some discussion, before Mrs Skewton’s death, upon the causes of my dissatisfaction; of which you will have formed a general understanding from having been a witness of what passed between Mrs Dombey and myself on the evening when you were at our—at my house.”", "그가 말을 이었다, \"스큐턴 부인이 작고하기 전에, 내 불만의 원인을 두고 다소 의논을 한 적이 있네. 그 일에 대해서는, 자네가 우리—내 집에 와 있던 그날 저녁, 돔비 부인과 나 사이에 오간 일을 목격했으니, 대강은 짐작했을 테지.\""]], [["“When I so much regretted being present,”", "\"제가 그토록 그 자리에 있던 것을 후회했던 그날 말씀이군요,\""], ["said the smiling Carker.", "미소 짓는 카커가 말했다."], ["“Proud as a man in my position necessarily must be of your familiar notice—though I give you no credit for it; you may do anything you please without losing caste—and honoured as I was by an early presentation to Mrs Dombey, before she was made eminent by bearing your name, I almost regretted that night, I assure you, that I had been the object of such especial good fortune.”", "\"저 같은 처지의 사람이 나리의 허물없는 눈길을 받는 것을 반드시 자랑스러워해야 하겠지만—그렇다고 그것을 제 공으로 돌리지는 않습니다. 나리야 무엇을 하셔도 체통이 깎이지 않으시지요—또 돔비 부인이 나리의 성함을 받아 높여지시기 전에 일찍이 그분께 소개되는 영예를 누리긴 했습니다만, 정말이지, 그날 밤만은 제가 그런 각별한 행운의 대상이 되었던 것을 거의 후회했습니다.\""]], [["That any man could, under any possible circumstances, regret the being distinguished by his condescension and patronage, was a moral phenomenon which Mr Dombey could not comprehend.", "어떤 사람이 어떤 상황에서든, 자기의 황송한 자비와 후원으로 인해 두드러진 것을 후회할 수 있다는 것은, 돔비 씨로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 도덕적 진기함이었다."], ["He therefore responded, with a considerable accession of dignity.", "그리하여 그는 위엄을 한층 더 끌어올려 응수했다. \"그래!"], ["“Indeed! And why, Carker?”", "그건 또 왜인가, 카커?\""]], [["“I fear,”", "\"제가 염려하는 것은,\""], ["returned the confidential agent, “that Mrs Dombey, never very much disposed to regard me with favourable interest—one in my position could not expect that, from a lady naturally proud, and whose pride becomes her so well—may not easily forgive my innocent part in that conversation.", "그 심복이 응수했다, \"저를 호의 어린 관심으로 보아 주실 마음이 도통 없으시던 돔비 부인께서—저 같은 처지로서야, 본래 도도하시고 그 도도함이 그토록 잘 어울리시는 부인께 그런 걸 기대할 수야 없습니다만—그 대화에서의 제 죄 없는 역할을 쉬이 용서하지 않으실지도 모른다는 점입니다."], ["Your displeasure is no light matter, you must remember; and to be visited with it before a third party—”", "나리의 노여움은 결코 가벼운 일이 아니니까요, 잊지 마십시오. 더구나 제삼자 앞에서 그것을 받게 된다는 건—\""]], [["“Carker,”", "\"카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, arrogantly; “I presume that I am the first consideration?”", "돔비 씨가 오만하게 말했다, \"내가 제일가는 고려 대상이라고 봐도 되겠지?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Can there be a doubt about it?”", "거기에 무슨 의심이 있겠습니까?\""], ["replied the other, with the impatience of a man admitting a notorious and incontrovertible fact.", "상대방이, 악명 높고 반박할 수 없는 사실을 인정하는 사람의 조바심으로 대답했다."]], [["“Mrs Dombey becomes a secondary consideration, when we are both in question, I imagine,”", "\"우리 둘이 함께 거론될 때면, 돔비 부인은 부차적인 고려 대상이 된다고 생각하네만,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“Is that so?”", "\"그렇지 않은가?\""]], [["“Is it so?”", "\"그렇지 않냐고요?\""], ["returned Carker.", "카커가 응수했다."], ["“Do you know better than anyone, that you have no need to ask?”", "\"물어보실 필요조차 없다는 걸 누구보다 잘 아시지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Then I hope, Carker,”", "\"그렇다면, 카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “that your regret in the acquisition of Mrs Dombey’s displeasure, may be almost counterbalanced by your satisfaction in retaining my confidence and good opinion.”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"돔비 부인의 노여움을 사게 된 데 대한 자네의 후회가, 내 신뢰와 호의를 간직한다는 자네의 만족으로 거의 상쇄되기를 바라네.\""]], [["“I have the misfortune, I find,”", "\"제가 보니, 불행히도,\""], ["returned Carker, “to have incurred that displeasure.", "카커가 응수했다, \"그 노여움을 사 버린 모양입니다."], ["Mrs Dombey has expressed it to you?”", "돔비 부인께서 그것을 나리께 표하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Mrs Dombey has expressed various opinions,”", "\"돔비 부인은 여러 의견을 표했네,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with majestic coldness and indifference, “in which I do not participate, and which I am not inclined to discuss, or to recall.", "돔비 씨가 위엄 있는 냉담함과 무관심으로 말했다, \"내가 동조하지 않는, 또 논하거나 떠올리고 싶지도 않은 의견들을 말이지."], ["I made Mrs Dombey acquainted, some time since, as I have already told you, with certain points of domestic deference and submission on which I felt it necessary to insist.", "나는 얼마 전, 이미 자네에게 말한 대로, 내가 고집할 필요가 있다고 느낀 가정 내 공경과 복종의 몇 가지 점을 돔비 부인에게 알려 주었네."], ["I failed to convince Mrs Dombey of the expediency of her immediately altering her conduct in those respects, with a view to her own peace and welfare, and my dignity; and I informed Mrs Dombey that if I should find it necessary to object or remonstrate again, I should express my opinion to her through yourself, my confidential agent.”", "나는 부인의 평안과 안녕, 그리고 내 위엄을 위해, 그 점들에서 즉시 행실을 고치는 것이 마땅하다고 부인을 납득시키지 못했네. 그래서 나는 돔비 부인에게, 만약 내가 다시 이의를 제기하거나 항의할 필요가 생긴다면, 내 심복인 자네를 통해 내 의견을 부인에게 전하겠다고 알려 주었지.\""]], [["Blended with the look that Carker bent upon him, was a devilish look at the picture over his head, that struck upon it like a flash of lightning.", "카커가 그에게 던지는 시선에는, 그 머리 위 그림을 향한 악마 같은 시선이 섞여 들어, 한 줄기 번개처럼 그 그림에 내리꽂혔다."]], [["“Now, Carker,”", "\"자, 카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “I do not hesitate to say to you that I will carry my point.", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"나는 망설임 없이 자네에게 말하네, 나는 내 뜻을 관철하겠어."], ["I am not to be trifled with.", "나는 가지고 놀 만한 사람이 아닐세."], ["Mrs Dombey must understand that my will is law, and that I cannot allow of one exception to the whole rule of my life.", "돔비 부인은, 내 뜻이 곧 법이며, 내 평생의 규율 전체에 단 하나의 예외도 허락할 수 없다는 걸 알아야 해. 자네는 이 임무를 떠맡아 주는 호의를 베풀게나."], ["You will have the goodness to undertake this charge, which, coming from me, is not unacceptable to you, I hope, whatever regret you may politely profess—for which I am obliged to you on behalf of Mrs Dombey; and you will have the goodness, I am persuaded, to discharge it as exactly as any other commission.”", "나한테서 나온 일이니, 자네가 점잖게 어떤 후회를 내비치든—그건 돔비 부인을 위해 내가 고맙게 여기는 바지만—그리 달갑지 않은 일은 아니리라 믿네. 그리고 자네는, 분명히 믿건대, 다른 어떤 용무 못지않게 그것을 정확히 수행하는 호의를 베풀어 주겠지.\""]], [["“You know,”", "\"아시지 않습니까,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “that you have only to command me.”", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"그저 명령만 하시면 됩니다.\""]], [["“I know,”", "\"알지,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with a majestic indication of assent, “that I have only to command you.", "돔비 씨가 위엄 있게 수긍의 뜻을 표하며 말했다, \"그저 명령만 하면 된다는 걸."], ["It is necessary that I should proceed in this.", "이 일을 밀고 나가는 것이 필요해."], ["Mrs Dombey is a lady undoubtedly highly qualified, in many respects, to—”", "돔비 부인은 의심할 바 없이 여러 면에서 매우 자격 있는 부인이니—\""]], [["“To do credit even to your choice,”", "\"나리의 선택에 영예까지 안겨 드릴 만한 분이시지요,\""], ["suggested Carker, with a yawning show of teeth.", "카커가 하품하듯 이를 드러내며 거들었다."]], [["“Yes; if you please to adopt that form of words,”", "\"그래, 자네가 굳이 그런 식으로 표현하겠다면,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, in his tone of state; “and at present I do not conceive that Mrs Dombey does that credit to it, to which it is entitled.", "돔비 씨가 자기의 점잔 빼는 어조로 말했다. \"그런데 지금으로서는, 돔비 부인이 그 선택에 마땅히 돌아가야 할 영예를 돌리고 있다고는 보지 않네."], ["There is a principle of opposition in Mrs Dombey that must be eradicated; that must be overcome: Mrs Dombey does not appear to understand,”", "돔비 부인에게는 뿌리 뽑아야 할 반항의 기질이 있어. 반드시 극복해야 할 기질이지."], ["said Mr Dombey, forcibly, “that the idea of opposition to Me is monstrous and absurd.”", "돔비 부인은 도무지 이해하지 못하는 모양이야,\" 돔비 씨가 힘주어 말했다, \"이 나에게 반항한다는 생각 자체가 가당치 않고 터무니없다는 것을 말일세.\""]], [["“We, in the City, know you better,”", "\"우리 시티 사람들은 나리를 더 잘 알지요,\""], ["replied Carker, with a smile from ear to ear.", "카커가 입이 귀에 걸리도록 미소 지으며 응수했다."]], [["“You know me better,”", "\"자네는 나를 더 잘 알지,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["“I hope so.", "\"그러기를 바라네."], ["Though, indeed, I am bound to do Mrs Dombey the justice of saying, however inconsistent it may seem with her subsequent conduct (which remains unchanged), that on my expressing my disapprobation and determination to her, with some severity, on the occasion to which I have referred, my admonition appeared to produce a very powerful effect.”", "하지만 사실, 나는 돔비 부인에게 정당한 평가를 해 줄 의무가 있네. 비록 부인의 그 뒤 행실(그건 변함이 없네만)과는 모순돼 보일지 몰라도, 내가 앞서 말한 그 일에서 다소 엄하게 나의 못마땅함과 결의를 표했을 때, 내 훈계가 매우 강력한 효과를 낸 듯 보였단 말이지.\""], ["Mr Dombey delivered himself of those words with most portentous stateliness.", "돔비 씨는 더없이 거창한 위엄으로 그 말을 토해 냈다."], ["“I wish you to have the goodness, then, to inform Mrs Dombey, Carker, from me, that I must recall our former conversation to her remembrance, in some surprise that it has not yet had its effect.", "\"그러니, 카커, 나를 대신해 돔비 부인에게 이렇게 알리는 호의를 베풀어 주게. 우리의 지난 대화가 아직 그 효과를 보이지 않은 데 다소 놀라며, 나는 그것을 부인에게 다시 상기시키지 않을 수 없다고 말일세."], ["That I must insist upon her regulating her conduct by the injunctions laid upon her in that conversation.", "그 대화에서 부인에게 내린 명령에 따라 행실을 단속할 것을 내가 고집한다고."], ["That I am not satisfied with her conduct.", "부인의 행실에 나는 만족하지 못한다고."], ["That I am greatly dissatisfied with it.", "크게 불만이라고."], ["And that I shall be under the very disagreeable necessity of making you the bearer of yet more unwelcome and explicit communications, if she has not the good sense and the proper feeling to adapt herself to my wishes, as the first Mrs Dombey did, and, I believe I may add, as any other lady in her place would.”", "그리고 만약 부인이, 첫 번째 돔비 부인이 그러했고, 또 감히 덧붙이건대 부인의 자리에 있는 어떤 부인이라도 그러했을 것처럼, 내 뜻에 맞추어 처신할 분별과 합당한 감정을 갖지 못한다면, 나는 자네를 통해 한층 더 달갑지 않고 노골적인 통보를 전하게 하는, 매우 불쾌한 부득이함에 처하게 되리라고 말일세.\""]], [["“The first Mrs Dombey lived very happily,”", "\"첫 번째 돔비 부인은 매우 행복하게 사셨지요,\""], ["said Carker.", "카커가 말했다."]], [["“The first Mrs Dombey had great good sense,”", "\"첫 번째 돔비 부인은 분별이 대단했지,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, in a gentlemanly toleration of the dead, “and very correct feeling.”", "돔비 씨가 죽은 이에 대한 신사다운 관용으로 말했다, \"그리고 감정도 매우 올발랐고.\""]], [["“Is Miss Dombey like her mother, do you think?”", "\"돔비 아가씨는 어머니를 닮았다고 보십니까?\""], ["said Carker.", "카커가 말했다."]], [["Swiftly and darkly, Mr Dombey’s face changed.", "순식간에, 어둡게, 돔비 씨의 얼굴이 변했다."], ["His confidential agent eyed it keenly.", "그의 심복이 그것을 날카롭게 살폈다."]], [["“I have approached a painful subject,”", "\"제가 괴로운 화제에 다가갔군요,\""], ["he said, in a soft regretful tone of voice, irreconcilable with his eager eye.", "그가, 자신의 열띤 눈과는 도무지 어울리지 않는, 부드럽고 후회 어린 어조로 말했다."], ["“Pray forgive me.", "\"부디 용서해 주십시오."], ["I forget these chains of association in the interest I have.", "제가 가진 관심 탓에 이런 연상의 사슬을 그만 잊었습니다."], ["Pray forgive me.”", "부디 용서해 주십시오.\""]], [["But for all he said, his eager eye scanned Mr Dombey’s downcast face none the less closely; and then it shot a strange triumphant look at the picture, as appealing to it to bear witness how he led him on again, and what was coming.", "그러나 그렇게 말하면서도, 그의 열띤 눈은 그에 못지않게 바싹 돔비 씨의 떨군 얼굴을 훑었다. 그러고는 그 그림을 향해 이상하리만치 의기양양한 시선을 쏘았으니, 마치 자기가 어떻게 다시 그를 끌고 가는지, 또 무슨 일이 닥쳐오는지 증언해 달라고 호소하는 듯했다."]], [["“Carker,”", "\"카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, looking here and there upon the table, and saying in a somewhat altered and more hurried voice, and with a paler lip, “there is no occasion for apology.", "돔비 씨가 식탁 여기저기를 둘러보며, 다소 달라지고 한결 다급해진 목소리로, 그리고 한층 핼쑥해진 입술로 말했다, \"사과할 일은 없네. 자네가 잘못 짚었어."], ["You mistake. The association is with the matter in hand, and not with any recollection, as you suppose.", "그 연상은 자네가 짐작하는 것 같은 어떤 회상이 아니라, 지금 다루는 일과 결부된 거야."], ["I do not approve of Mrs Dombey’s behaviour towards my daughter.”", "나는 내 딸을 대하는 돔비 부인의 태도가 못마땅하네.\""]], [["“Pardon me,”", "\"실례지만,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “I don’t quite understand.”", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"잘 이해가 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“Understand then,”", "\"그럼 이렇게 알아 두게,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, “that you may make that—that you will make that, if you please—matter of direct objection from me to Mrs Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 응수했다, \"자네가 그것을—그것을, 원한다면—나로부터 돔비 부인에게 직접 제기하는 이의의 거리로 삼아도 좋다는 걸 말이지."], ["You will please to tell her that her show of devotion for my daughter is disagreeable to me.", "부디 부인에게 일러 주게, 부인이 내 딸에게 헌신을 과시하는 것이 나에게 거슬린다고."], ["It is likely to be noticed.", "그건 사람들 눈에 띄기 십상이야."], ["It is likely to induce people to contrast Mrs Dombey in her relation towards my daughter, with Mrs Dombey in her relation towards myself.", "그건 사람들로 하여금, 내 딸을 대하는 돔비 부인과, 나 자신을 대하는 돔비 부인을 견주어 보게 하기 십상이지."], ["You will have the goodness to let Mrs Dombey know, plainly, that I object to it; and that I expect her to defer, immediately, to my objection.", "부디 돔비 부인에게 분명히 알려 주게, 내가 그것에 반대한다고. 그리고 부인이 즉시 내 반대에 따르기를 내가 기대한다고."], ["Mrs Dombey may be in earnest, or she may be pursuing a whim, or she may be opposing me; but I object to it in any case, and in every case.", "돔비 부인이 진심일 수도 있고, 변덕을 부리는 것일 수도 있고, 나에게 맞서는 것일 수도 있겠지."], ["If Mrs Dombey is in earnest, so much the less reluctant should she be to desist; for she will not serve my daughter by any such display.", "하지만 어느 경우든, 그리고 모든 경우든, 나는 그것에 반대하네. 만약 돔비 부인이 진심이라면, 그만두기를 더더욱 꺼려선 안 되지."], ["If my wife has any superfluous gentleness, and duty over and above her proper submission to me, she may bestow them where she pleases, perhaps; but I will have submission first!—Carker,”", "그런 과시로는 내 딸에게 보탬이 되지 않을 테니까. 만약 내 아내에게 나에 대한 마땅한 복종을 넘어 넘쳐나는 어떤 다정함과 의무가 있다면, 어쩌면 그건 제 마음 가는 데 베풀어도 좋겠지."], ["said Mr Dombey, checking the unusual emotion with which he had spoken, and falling into a tone more like that in which he was accustomed to assert his greatness, “you will have the goodness not to omit or slur this point, but to consider it a very important part of your instructions.”", "하지만 나는 먼저 복종을 받겠어!—카커,\" 돔비 씨가, 말하면서 솟구쳤던 그 예사롭지 않은 격정을 억누르고, 자기의 위대함을 단언할 때 으레 쓰던 어조에 가까운 투로 돌아가며 말했다, \"부디 이 점을 빠뜨리거나 얼버무리지 말고, 자네 지시의 매우 중대한 일부로 여겨 주게.\""]], [["Mr Carker bowed his head, and rising from the table, and standing thoughtfully before the fire, with his hand to his smooth chin, looked down at Mr Dombey with the evil slyness of some monkish carving, half human and half brute; or like a leering face on an old water-spout.", "카커 씨는 고개를 숙이고, 식탁에서 일어나, 매끈한 턱에 손을 댄 채 난롯불 앞에 생각에 잠긴 듯 서서, 절반은 인간이고 절반은 짐승인 어떤 수도원의 조각상 같은, 혹은 낡은 빗물받이 위에서 흘겨보는 얼굴 같은 사악한 교활함으로 돔비 씨를 내려다보았다."], ["Mr Dombey, recovering his composure by degrees, or cooling his emotion in his sense of having taken a high position, sat gradually stiffening again, and looking at the parrot as she swung to and fro, in her great wedding ring.", "돔비 씨는 차츰 평정을 되찾으며, 혹은 자기가 고고한 자리를 차지했다는 의식 속에 격정을 식히며, 점점 다시 뻣뻣해져 앉아서는, 거대한 결혼반지 같은 고리 안에서 이리저리 흔들리는 앵무새를 바라보았다."]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "\"실례합니다만,\""], ["said Carker, after a silence, suddenly resuming his chair, and drawing it opposite to Mr Dombey’s, “but let me understand.", "침묵 끝에 카커가 갑자기 다시 자기 의자에 앉아, 그것을 돔비 씨의 맞은편으로 끌어당기며 말했다, \"제가 분명히 이해하게 해 주십시오."], ["Mrs Dombey is aware of the probability of your making me the organ of your displeasure?”", "돔비 부인께서는 나리가 저를 노여움의 전령으로 삼으실 가능성을 알고 계십니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇네,\""], ["replied Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대답했다."], ["“I have said so.”", "\"내가 그렇게 말했네.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇군요,\""], ["rejoined Carker, quickly; “but why?”", "카커가 재빨리 응수했다. \"그런데 왜죠?\""]], [["“Why!”", "\"왜라니!\""], ["Mr Dombey repeated, not without hesitation.", "돔비 씨가 다소 망설이며 되풀이했다."], ["“Because I told her.”", "\"내가 부인에게 말했으니까.\""]], [["“Ay,”", "\"아,\""], ["replied Carker.", "카커가 응수했다."], ["“But why did you tell her? You see,”", "\"하지만 왜 부인에게 말씀하셨습니까?"], ["he continued with a smile, and softly laying his velvet hand, as a cat might have laid its sheathed claws, on Mr Dombey’s arm; “if I perfectly understand what is in your mind, I am so much more likely to be useful, and to have the happiness of being effectually employed.", "보십시오,\" 그는 미소 지으며, 고양이가 발톱을 감춘 발을 얹듯, 부드러운 벨벳 같은 손을 돔비 씨의 팔에 살며시 얹으며 말을 이었다, \"제가 나리의 마음속을 완벽히 이해할수록, 그만큼 더 쓸모가 있고, 효과적으로 부려지는 행복을 누릴 가능성이 높아지니까요."], ["I think I do understand. I have not the honour of Mrs Dombey’s good opinion.", "제가 이해하기로는—제가 돔비 부인의 호의는 받지 못하고 있지요."], ["In my position, I have no reason to expect it; but I take the fact to be, that I have not got it?”", "제 처지에서야 그걸 바랄 까닭이 없습니다만, 제가 그걸 받지 못한 것이 사실이지요?\""]], [["“Possibly not,”", "\"아마 그럴 테지,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Consequently,”", "\"따라서,\""], ["pursued Carker, “your making the communications to Mrs Dombey through me, is sure to be particularly unpalatable to that lady?”", "카커가 이어 갔다, \"나리가 저를 통해 돔비 부인께 통보를 하시는 것이, 그 부인께는 특히나 입에 쓴 일이 되겠군요?\""]], [["“It appears to me,”", "\"내가 보기엔,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, with haughty reserve, and yet with some embarrassment, “that Mrs Dombey’s views upon the subject form no part of it as it presents itself to you and me, Carker.", "돔비 씨가 도도한 신중함으로, 그러면서도 다소 거북하게 말했다, \"그 일에 대한 돔비 부인의 생각은, 그것이 자네와 나에게 보이는 모습으로는, 그 일의 일부가 아닐세, 카커."], ["But it may be so.”", "하지만 그럴 수도 있겠지.\""]], [["“And—pardon me—do I misconceive you,”", "\"그리고—실례입니다만—제가 나리를 오해하는 걸까요,\""], ["said Carker, “when I think you descry in this, a likely means of humbling Mrs Dombey’s pride—I use the word as expressive of a quality which, kept within due bounds, adorns and graces a lady so distinguished for her beauty and accomplishments—and, not to say of punishing her, but of reducing her to the submission you so naturally and justly require?”", "카커가 말했다, \"나리가 이 일에서, 돔비 부인의 도도함을 꺾을 유력한 수단을—저는 이 말을, 적정한 선 안에 두면 그분처럼 미모와 교양이 출중한 부인을 꾸미고 빛내 주는 어떤 자질을 뜻하는 것으로 씁니다—그러니까, 부인을 벌하려는 게 아니라, 나리가 그토록 자연스럽고 정당하게 요구하시는 복종으로 끌어내릴 유력한 수단을 보고 계신다고 제가 생각한다면 말입니다?\""]], [["“I am not accustomed, Carker, as you know,”", "\"카커, 알다시피 나는,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “to give such close reasons for any course of conduct I think proper to adopt, but I will gainsay nothing of this.", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"내가 적절하다고 여기는 어떤 행동 방침에 대해 이렇게 속속들이 이유를 대는 데 익숙지 않네만, 이 점에 대해서는 아무것도 부정하지 않겠네."], ["If you have any objection to found upon it, that is indeed another thing, and the mere statement that you have one will be sufficient.", "자네가 거기에 무슨 이의를 두고 있다면, 그건 정말 별개의 문제고, 자네에게 이의가 있다는 말만 해도 충분하네."], ["But I have not supposed, I confess, that any confidence I could entrust to you, would be likely to degrade you—”", "하지만 솔직히 나는, 내가 자네에게 맡길 수 있는 어떤 신뢰가 자네를 깎아내리리라고는 생각하지 않았네—\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I degraded!”", "제가 깎인다고요!\""], ["exclaimed Carker.", "카커가 외쳤다."], ["“In your service!”", "\"나리를 섬기는 일에!\""]], [["“—or to place you,”", "\"—혹은 자네를,\""], ["pursued Mr Dombey, “in a false position.”", "돔비 씨가 이어 갔다, \"난처한 처지에 놓으리라고도 말일세.\""]], [["“I in a false position!”", "\"제가 난처한 처지에요!\""], ["exclaimed Carker.", "카커가 외쳤다."], ["“I shall be proud—delighted—to execute your trust.", "\"저는 자랑스럽게—기쁘게—나리의 신임을 받들겠습니다."], ["I could have wished, I own, to have given the lady at whose feet I would lay my humble duty and devotion—for is she not your wife!—no new cause of dislike; but a wish from you is, of course, paramount to every other consideration on earth.", "솔직히 고백건대, 제가 보잘것없는 의무와 헌신을 그 발치에 바치고 싶은 부인께—그분이 나리의 아내가 아니십니까!—새로운 미움의 빌미를 드리지 않았으면 했습니다만, 나리의 바람이야 물론 세상 그 어떤 고려보다 으뜸이지요."], ["Besides, when Mrs Dombey is converted from these little errors of judgment, incidental, I would presume to say, to the novelty of her situation, I shall hope that she will perceive in the slight part I take, only a grain—my removed and different sphere gives room for little more—of the respect for you, and sacrifice of all considerations to you, of which it will be her pleasure and privilege to garner up a great store every day.”", "게다가, 돔비 부인께서 이런 자잘한 판단의 잘못—감히 말씀드리자면, 그 처지의 새로움에서 비롯된 잘못—에서 돌아서시면, 제가 떠맡는 그 사소한 역할에서, 한낱 한 톨의—제 멀고 다른 처지로는 그 이상은 어림없습니다—나리에 대한 존경과, 나리께 모든 것을 바치는 희생, 즉 부인께서 날마다 큰 곳간에 그득 쌓아 두는 것을 기쁨이요 특권으로 여기실 그 존경과 희생의 한 톨만을 알아봐 주시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Mr Dombey seemed, at the moment, again to see her with her hand stretched out towards the door, and again to hear through the mild speech of his confidential agent an echo of the words, “Nothing can make us stranger to each other than we are henceforth!”", "돔비 씨에게는, 그 순간, 다시금 손을 문 쪽으로 뻗은 그녀가 보이는 듯했고, 또 자기 심복의 부드러운 말소리를 뚫고 \"이후로 우리가 지금보다 더 남남이 될 수는 없어요!\"라는 말의 메아리가 들리는 듯했다."], ["But he shook off the fancy, and did not shake in his resolution, and said, “Certainly, no doubt.”", "그러나 그는 그 환상을 떨쳐 냈고, 결의는 흔들리지 않았으며, 이렇게 말했다, \"물론이지, 의심할 바 없네.\""]], [["“There is nothing more,”", "\"더는 없습니다,\""], ["quoth Carker, drawing his chair back to its old place—for they had taken little breakfast as yet—and pausing for an answer before he sat down.", "카커가, 의자를 원래 자리로 도로 끌어당기며—아직 둘 다 아침을 거의 들지 못했던 것이다—앉기 전에 대답을 기다리며 잠시 멈춰 말했다."]], [["“Nothing,”", "\"없네,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “but this.", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"이것 말고는."], ["You will be good enough to observe, Carker, that no message to Mrs Dombey with which you are or may be charged, admits of reply.", "부디 유념해 주게, 카커, 돔비 부인에게 보내는 어떤 전갈이든, 자네가 맡았거나 맡게 될 그 어떤 전갈이든, 답변을 허용하지 않는다는 걸."], ["You will be good enough to bring me no reply.", "부디 나에게 어떤 답변도 가져오지 말게."], ["Mrs Dombey is informed that it does not become me to temporise or treat upon any matter that is at issue between us, and that what I say is final.”", "돔비 부인에게는, 우리 사이에 걸린 어떤 문제를 두고 미적거리거나 타협하는 것은 내 체통에 맞지 않으며, 내가 한 말이 곧 결정이라고 통고해 두었네.\""]], [["Mr Carker signified his understanding of these credentials, and they fell to breakfast with what appetite they might.", "카커 씨는 이 신임장을 이해했다는 뜻을 표했고, 둘은 입맛 닿는 대로 아침 식사에 들어갔다."], ["The Grinder also, in due time reappeared, keeping his eyes upon his master without a moment’s respite, and passing the time in a reverie of worshipful tenor.", "그라인더 역시 때맞춰 다시 나타나, 한순간도 쉬지 않고 주인에게 눈을 고정한 채, 경배에 찬 몽상으로 시간을 보냈다."], ["Breakfast concluded, Mr Dombey’s horse was ordered out again, and Mr Carker mounting his own, they rode off for the City together.", "식사가 끝나자, 돔비 씨의 말이 다시 끌려 나왔고, 카커 씨도 자기 말에 올라, 둘은 함께 시티를 향해 말을 몰았다."]], [["Mr Carker was in capital spirits, and talked much.", "카커 씨는 기분이 더없이 좋았고, 말도 많았다."], ["Mr Dombey received his conversation with the sovereign air of a man who had a right to be talked to, and occasionally condescended to throw in a few words to carry on the conversation.", "돔비 씨는 자기가 응당 이야기를 들을 권리가 있는 사람이라는 군주 같은 태도로 그의 말을 받았고, 이따금 대화를 이어 가도록 몇 마디 던지는 황송함을 베풀었다."], ["So they rode on characteristically enough.", "그렇게 둘은 자못 제 격에 맞게 말을 달렸다."], ["But Mr Dombey, in his dignity, rode with very long stirrups, and a very loose rein, and very rarely deigned to look down to see where his horse went.", "그러나 돔비 씨는, 그 위엄에 걸맞게, 등자를 아주 길게 늘이고, 고삐를 아주 느슨히 쥔 채, 자기 말이 어디로 가는지 굽어볼 황송함을 좀처럼 베풀지 않았다."], ["In consequence of which it happened that Mr Dombey’s horse, while going at a round trot, stumbled on some loose stones, threw him, rolled over him, and lashing out with his iron-shod feet, in his struggles to get up, kicked him.", "그 결과로, 돔비 씨의 말이 빠른 속보로 달리다가 흩어진 돌멩이에 발이 걸려 비틀거리며 그를 내동댕이쳤고, 그 위로 뒹굴었으며, 일어나려고 발버둥 치면서 쇠를 박은 발굽으로 발길질을 해 그를 걷어찼다."]], [["Mr Carker, quick of eye, steady of hand, and a good horseman, was afoot, and had the struggling animal upon his legs and by the bridle, in a moment.", "눈이 빠르고, 손이 야무지며, 솜씨 좋은 기수였던 카커 씨는 순식간에 땅에 내려, 발버둥 치는 짐승을 제 발로 세우고 굴레를 잡았다."], ["Otherwise that morning’s confidence would have been Mr Dombey’s last.", "그러지 않았더라면 그날 아침의 그 신임은 돔비 씨의 마지막 신임이 되었을 것이다."], ["Yet even with the flush and hurry of this action red upon him, he bent over his prostrate chief with every tooth disclosed, and muttered as he stooped down, “I have given good cause of offence to Mrs Dombey now, if she knew it!”", "그러나 그 행동의 상기됨과 분주함이 얼굴에 붉게 남아 있는 와중에도, 그는 이 하나하나를 다 드러낸 채 엎어진 우두머리 위로 몸을 굽혔고, 허리를 숙이며 중얼거렸다, \"이제 돔비 부인에게 단단히 노여움 살 빌미를 주었군, 그녀가 안다면 말이지!\""]], [["Mr Dombey being insensible, and bleeding from the head and face, was carried by certain menders of the road, under Carker’s direction, to the nearest public-house, which was not far off, and where he was soon attended by divers surgeons, who arrived in quick succession from all parts, and who seemed to come by some mysterious instinct, as vultures are said to gather about a camel who dies in the desert.", "의식을 잃고 머리와 얼굴에서 피를 흘리는 돔비 씨는, 카커의 지시에 따라, 도로를 손질하던 몇몇 인부들에 의해 멀지 않은 가장 가까운 선술집으로 옮겨졌고, 거기서 곧 사방에서 잇따라 도착한 여러 외과의사들의 진료를 받았는데, 그들은 사막에서 죽어 가는 낙타 주위로 독수리가 모여든다는 말처럼, 어떤 신비로운 본능에 이끌려 온 듯했다."], ["After being at some pains to restore him to consciousness, these gentlemen examined into the nature of his injuries.", "그를 의식으로 되돌려 놓느라 적잖이 애쓴 뒤, 이 신사들은 그 부상의 성질을 살폈다."], ["One surgeon who lived hard by was strong for a compound fracture of the leg, which was the landlord’s opinion also; but two surgeons who lived at a distance, and were only in that neighbourhood by accident, combated this opinion so disinterestedly, that it was decided at last that the patient, though severely cut and bruised, had broken no bones but a lesser rib or so, and might be carefully taken home before night.", "바로 가까이 사는 외과의사 하나는 다리의 복합 골절이라고 강력히 주장했고, 그것은 선술집 주인의 의견이기도 했다. 그러나 멀리 살면서 우연히 그 동네에 와 있던 외과의사 둘이 어찌나 사심 없이 그 의견에 맞섰던지, 결국 환자는 심하게 베이고 멍들기는 했어도, 갈비뼈 몇 대 말고는 어떤 뼈도 부러지지 않았으며, 밤이 되기 전에 조심해서 집으로 옮겨도 좋다는 결론이 내려졌다."], ["His injuries being dressed and bandaged, which was a long operation, and he at length left to repose, Mr Carker mounted his horse again, and rode away to carry the intelligence home.", "그의 부상을 처치하고 붕대로 감는 데, 그것은 긴 작업이었는데, 그러고 나서 마침내 그가 안정을 취하게 남겨지자, 카커 씨는 다시 말에 올라, 그 소식을 집에 전하러 말을 달렸다."]], [["Crafty and cruel as his face was at the best of times, though it was a sufficiently fair face as to form and regularity of feature, it was at its worst when he set forth on this errand; animated by the craft and cruelty of thoughts within him, suggestions of remote possibility rather than of design or plot, that made him ride as if he hunted men and women.", "가장 좋을 때조차도—생김새와 이목구비의 반듯함으로 말하자면 제법 준수한 얼굴이긴 했으나—교활하고 잔인했던 그의 얼굴은, 이 심부름을 나설 때 가장 험악했다. 마음속 생각들의 교활함과 잔인함이—계략이나 음모라기보다는 아득한 가능성의 암시들이—그를 부추겨, 마치 남자와 여자를 사냥하는 듯 말을 몰게 했다."], ["Drawing rein at length, and slackening in his speed, as he came into the more public roads, he checked his white-legged horse into picking his way along as usual, and hid himself beneath his sleek, hushed, crouched manner, and his ivory smile, as he best could.", "마침내 고삐를 당기고, 사람들 다니는 큰길로 접어들면서 속도를 늦추어, 그는 흰 발의 말을 평소처럼 조심조심 길을 골라 걷게 다잡았고, 그 매끄럽고 잠잠하며 웅크린 태도와 상아 같은 미소 아래로 최대한 자기를 감추었다."]], [["He rode direct to Mr Dombey’s house, alighted at the door, and begged to see Mrs Dombey on an affair of importance.", "그는 곧장 돔비 씨의 집으로 말을 몰아, 문 앞에서 내려, 긴한 용무로 돔비 부인을 뵙기를 청했다."], ["The servant who showed him to Mr Dombey’s own room, soon returned to say that it was not Mrs Dombey’s hour for receiving visitors, and that he begged pardon for not having mentioned it before.", "그를 돔비 씨 자신의 방으로 안내한 하인은, 곧 돌아와, 지금은 돔비 부인이 방문객을 맞이하시는 시간이 아니라고, 그리고 진작 그 말씀을 드리지 못해 죄송하다고 전했다."]], [["Mr Carker, who was quite prepared for a cold reception, wrote upon a card that he must take the liberty of pressing for an interview, and that he would not be so bold as to do so, for the second time (this he underlined), if he were not equally sure of the occasion being sufficient for his justification.", "차가운 응대를 충분히 예상했던 카커 씨는, 명함에다, 무례를 무릅쓰고 면담을 졸라야겠으며, 만약 이번 일이 자기 처사를 충분히 정당화할 만한 것이라고 똑같이 확신하지 않았더라면 두 번씩이나(이 대목에 그는 밑줄을 그었다) 이렇게 대담하게 나서지는 않았을 것이라고 적었다."], ["After a trifling delay, Mrs Dombey’s maid appeared, and conducted him to a morning room upstairs, where Edith and Florence were together.", "잠깐의 지체 끝에, 돔비 부인의 하녀가 나타나, 그를 위층의 한 거실로 안내했는데, 거기에는 이디스와 플로렌스가 함께 있었다."]], [["He had never thought Edith half so beautiful before.", "그는 일찍이 이디스가 이토록 절반만큼이라도 아름답다고 생각한 적이 없었다."], ["Much as he admired the graces of her face and form, and freshly as they dwelt within his sensual remembrance, he had never thought her half so beautiful.", "그녀의 얼굴과 자태의 우아함을 그토록 흠모해 왔고, 그것이 그의 육감적인 기억 속에 그토록 생생히 깃들어 있었음에도, 그는 그녀가 이토록 절반만큼이라도 아름답다고 여긴 적이 없었다."]], [["Her glance fell haughtily upon him in the doorway; but he looked at Florence—though only in the act of bending his head, as he came in—with some irrepressible expression of the new power he held; and it was his triumph to see the glance droop and falter, and to see that Edith half rose up to receive him.", "문간에 선 그에게 그녀의 시선이 도도하게 떨어졌다. 그러나 그는—들어서며 고개를 숙이는 동작 중이었을 뿐인데도—자기가 쥔 새로운 권능을 어쩔 수 없이 드러내는 어떤 표정으로 플로렌스를 바라보았다. 그리고 그 시선이 처지며 흔들리는 것을, 또 이디스가 그를 맞으려 반쯤 몸을 일으키는 것을 보는 것이 그의 승리였다."]], [["He was very sorry, he was deeply grieved; he couldn’t say with what unwillingness he came to prepare her for the intelligence of a very slight accident.", "그는 무척 안타깝다고, 깊이 비통하다고 했다. 매우 사소한 사고의 소식에 마음의 준비를 시켜 드리려 어떤 내키지 않는 심정으로 왔는지 모르겠다고 했다."], ["He entreated Mrs Dombey to compose herself.", "그는 돔비 부인에게 진정하시기를 간청했다."], ["Upon his sacred word of honour, there was no cause of alarm.", "자기의 신성한 명예를 걸고, 놀랄 까닭은 없다고 했다."], ["But Mr Dombey—", "그러나 돔비 씨가—"]], [["Florence uttered a sudden cry.", "플로렌스가 갑자기 비명을 질렀다."], ["He did not look at her, but at Edith.", "그는 그녀가 아니라 이디스를 바라보았다."], ["Edith composed and reassured her.", "이디스가 그녀를 진정시키고 안심시켰다."], ["She uttered no cry of distress.", "그녀는 비탄의 비명을 지르지 않았다."], ["No, no.", "아니, 아니었다."]], [["Mr Dombey had met with an accident in riding.", "돔비 씨가 말을 타다가 사고를 당했다고."], ["His horse had slipped, and he had been thrown.", "말이 미끄러져 그가 내동댕이쳐졌다고."]], [["Florence wildly exclaimed that he was badly hurt; that he was killed!", "플로렌스가 미친 듯이 외쳤다, 아버지가 크게 다쳤다고, 돌아가셨다고!"]], [["No.", "아닙니다."], ["Upon his honour, Mr Dombey, though stunned at first, was soon recovered, and though certainly hurt was in no kind of danger.", "명예를 걸고, 돔비 씨는 처음에는 정신을 잃으셨으나 곧 회복하셨으며, 다치긴 했어도 결코 어떤 위험에 처하시지는 않았다고."], ["If this were not the truth, he, the distressed intruder, never could have had the courage to present himself before Mrs Dombey.", "만약 이것이 사실이 아니었다면, 이 괴로운 불청객인 자기로서는 결코 돔비 부인 앞에 나설 용기를 내지 못했을 것이라고."], ["It was the truth indeed, he solemnly assured her.", "정말로 사실이라고, 그가 엄숙히 그녀에게 장담했다."]], [["All this he said as if he were answering Edith, and not Florence, and with his eyes and his smile fastened on Edith.", "이 모든 것을 그는, 마치 플로렌스가 아니라 이디스에게 대답하듯, 그리고 자기 눈과 미소를 이디스에게 고정한 채 말했다."]], [["He then went on to tell her where Mr Dombey was lying, and to request that a carriage might be placed at his disposal to bring him home.", "그러고 나서 그는 돔비 씨가 어디에 누워 있는지를 그녀에게 일러 주고, 그를 집으로 모셔 오도록 마차 한 대를 자기에게 내어 달라고 청했다."]], [["“Mama,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["faltered Florence in tears, “if I might venture to go!”", "플로렌스가 눈물 속에 더듬거렸다, \"제가 감히 가도 된다면!\""]], [["Mr Carker, having his eyes on Edith when he heard these words, gave her a secret look and slightly shook his head.", "이 말을 들었을 때 이디스에게 눈을 두고 있던 카커 씨는, 그녀에게 은밀한 눈짓을 보내며 가볍게 고개를 저었다."], ["He saw how she battled with herself before she answered him with her handsome eyes, but he wrested the answer from her—he showed her that he would have it, or that he would speak and cut Florence to the heart—and she gave it to him.", "그는 그녀가 자기에게 그 아름다운 눈으로 대답하기 전에 스스로와 얼마나 싸우는지를 보았다. 그러나 그는 그녀에게서 그 대답을 비틀어 짜냈다—자기가 그것을 받아 내리라는 것을, 아니면 입을 열어 플로렌스의 가슴을 도려내리라는 것을 그녀에게 보였다—그리고 그녀는 그것을 그에게 내주었다."], ["As he had looked at the picture in the morning, so he looked at her afterwards, when she turned her eyes away.", "아침에 그 그림을 바라보았던 것처럼, 그녀가 눈길을 돌렸을 때 그는 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“I am directed to request,”", "\"이렇게 청하라는 지시를 받았습니다,\""], ["he said, “that the new housekeeper—Mrs Pipchin, I think, is the name—”", "그가 말했다, \"새 가정부—핍친 부인이라는 이름인 것 같습니다만—에게—\""]], [["Nothing escaped him.", "그에게서 빠져나가는 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["He saw, in an instant, that she was another slight of Mr Dombey’s on his wife.", "그는 그녀 또한 돔비 씨가 아내에게 가하는 또 하나의 무시거리임을 한순간에 알아챘다."]], [["“—may be informed that Mr Dombey wishes to have his bed prepared in his own apartments downstairs, as he prefers those rooms to any other.", "\"—돔비 씨께서 아래층 당신의 처소에 잠자리를 마련해 두기를 바라신다고 알리라는 것이었지요. 다른 어떤 방보다 그 방들을 선호하신다고요."], ["I shall return to Mr Dombey almost immediately.", "저는 거의 곧바로 돔비 씨께 돌아가겠습니다."], ["That every possible attention has been paid to his comfort, and that he is the object of every possible solicitude, I need not assure you, Madam.", "그분의 안락을 위해 온갖 주의가 기울여졌으며, 그분이 온갖 정성의 대상이심을, 부인, 굳이 장담드릴 필요는 없겠지요."], ["Let me again say, there is no cause for the least alarm.", "다시 한번 말씀드립니다만, 조금도 놀라실 까닭이 없습니다."], ["Even you may be quite at ease, believe me.”", "부인조차도 아주 마음을 놓으셔도 됩니다, 믿어 주십시오.\""]], [["He bowed himself out, with his extremest show of deference and conciliation; and having returned to Mr Dombey’s room, and there arranged for a carriage being sent after him to the City, mounted his horse again, and rode slowly thither.", "그는 더없이 극진한 공경과 회유의 태도로 절하며 물러갔다. 그러고는 돔비 씨의 방으로 돌아가, 거기서 마차 한 대를 시티의 자기에게로 뒤따라 보내도록 조처한 뒤, 다시 말에 올라 천천히 그리로 달렸다."], ["He was very thoughtful as he went along, and very thoughtful there, and very thoughtful in the carriage on his way back to the place where Mr Dombey had been left.", "그는 가는 내내 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 거기서도 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었으며, 돔비 씨를 남겨 둔 곳으로 돌아가는 마차 안에서도 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["It was only when sitting by that gentleman’s couch that he was quite himself again, and conscious of his teeth.", "그가 다시 온전히 제정신으로 돌아와 자기 이를 의식하게 된 것은, 오직 그 신사의 긴 의자 곁에 앉아 있을 때뿐이었다."]], [["About the time of twilight, Mr Dombey, grievously afflicted with aches and pains, was helped into his carriage, and propped with cloaks and pillows on one side of it, while his confidential agent bore him company upon the other.", "땅거미 질 무렵, 욱신거림과 통증으로 심하게 시달리던 돔비 씨는, 부축을 받아 마차에 태워졌고, 그 한쪽에 외투와 베개로 받쳐졌으며, 다른 한쪽에는 그의 심복이 동행했다."], ["As he was not to be shaken, they moved at little more than a foot pace; and hence it was quite dark when he was brought home.", "그는 흔들려서는 안 되었기에, 그들은 걸음걸이보다 조금 빠른 정도로 움직였다. 그래서 그가 집에 당도했을 때는 어둠이 완연했다."], ["Mrs Pipchin, bitter and grim, and not oblivious of the Peruvian mines, as the establishment in general had good reason to know, received him at the door, and freshened the domestics with several little sprinklings of wordy vinegar, while they assisted in conveying him to his room.", "핍친 부인이, 신랄하고 험상궂게, 또—온 식솔이 익히 알고 있듯—페루의 광산을 잊지 않은 채, 문에서 그를 맞이했고, 그를 방으로 옮기는 것을 거드는 하인들에게 입심 사나운 식초를 몇 차례 잘게 흩뿌려 정신이 번쩍 들게 했다."], ["Mr Carker remained in attendance until he was safe in bed, and then, as he declined to receive any female visitor, but the excellent Ogress who presided over his household, waited on Mrs Dombey once more, with his report on her lord’s condition.", "카커 씨는 그가 무사히 잠자리에 들 때까지 곁에 머물렀고, 그러고 나서, 그가 자기 집안을 다스리는 그 빼어난 식인귀 말고는 어떤 여자 방문객도 받기를 마다했으므로, 그 주인의 상태에 대한 보고를 가지고 다시 한번 돔비 부인을 찾아갔다."]], [["He again found Edith alone with Florence, and he again addressed the whole of his soothing speech to Edith, as if she were a prey to the liveliest and most affectionate anxieties.", "그는 다시 이디스가 플로렌스와 단둘이 있는 것을 보았고, 다시 자기의 위로의 말을 통째로 이디스에게, 마치 그녀가 더없이 절절하고 애정 어린 근심에 사로잡힌 사람이라도 되는 양 건넸다."], ["So earnest he was in his respectful sympathy, that on taking leave, he ventured—with one more glance towards Florence at the moment—to take her hand, and bending over it, to touch it with his lips.", "그는 그 공손한 동정에 어찌나 진지했던지, 작별 인사를 할 때, 그 순간 플로렌스 쪽으로 한 번 더 눈길을 보내며—감히 그녀의 손을 잡고, 그 위로 몸을 굽혀 입술을 대었다."]], [["Edith did not withdraw the hand, nor did she strike his fair face with it, despite the flush upon her cheek, the bright light in her eyes, and the dilation of her whole form.", "이디스는 손을 빼지도, 뺨에 솟은 홍조와 두 눈의 형형한 빛, 그리고 온몸의 팽팽한 긴장에도 불구하고, 그 손으로 그의 준수한 얼굴을 치지도 않았다."], ["But when she was alone in her own room, she struck it on the marble chimney-shelf, so that, at one blow, it was bruised, and bled; and held it from her, near the shining fire, as if she could have thrust it in and burned it.", "그러나 자기 방에 홀로 있게 되었을 때, 그녀는 그 손을 대리석 벽난로 선반에 내리쳤고, 그리하여 단번에 그것은 멍이 들고 피가 났다. 그러고는 그것을 자기에게서 멀리 떼어, 환히 타오르는 불 가까이에 댄 채, 마치 그것을 불 속에 처넣어 태워 버리고도 싶다는 듯이 했다."]], [["Far into the night she sat alone, by the sinking blaze, in dark and threatening beauty, watching the murky shadows looming on the wall, as if her thoughts were tangible, and cast them there.", "밤이 이슥하도록 그녀는, 사그라드는 불꽃 곁에, 어둡고 위협적인 아름다움으로, 홀로 앉아, 마치 자기 생각이 만져지기라도 하는 것인 양, 그것을 벽에 드리우기라도 한 것인 양, 벽 위에 어른거리는 거뭇한 그림자들을 지켜보았다."], ["Whatever shapes of outrage and affront, and black foreshadowings of things that might happen, flickered, indistinct and giant-like, before her, one resented figure marshalled them against her.", "능욕과 모욕의 온갖 형상들이며, 닥쳐올지 모를 일들의 시커먼 예고들이, 그녀 앞에서 흐릿하고 거인처럼 일렁였는데, 그 가운데 분노에 찬 한 형상이 그것들을 그녀에게 맞서도록 도열시켰다."], ["And that figure was her husband.", "그리고 그 형상은 그녀의 남편이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIII. The Watches of the Night", "# 제38장"]], [["Florence, long since awakened from her dream, mournfully observed the estrangement between her father and Edith, and saw it widen more and more, and knew that there was greater bitterness between them every day.", "오래전에 꿈에서 깨어난 플로렌스는 아버지와 에디스 사이의 소원함을 비통한 심정으로 지켜보았다. 그 거리가 점점 더 벌어지는 것을 보았고, 두 사람 사이에 날마다 더 큰 쓰라림이 자라나는 것을 알았다."], ["Each day’s added knowledge deepened the shade upon her love and hope, roused up the old sorrow that had slumbered for a little time, and made it even heavier to bear than it had been before.", "날이 갈수록 늘어 가는 깨달음은 그녀의 사랑과 희망 위에 드리운 그늘을 짙게 했고, 잠시 잠들어 있던 옛 슬픔을 다시 일깨워, 그것을 이전보다 한층 견디기 힘든 것으로 만들었다."]], [["It had been hard—how hard may none but Florence ever know!—to have the natural affection of a true and earnest nature turned to agony; and slight, or stern repulse, substituted for the tenderest protection and the dearest care.", "진실하고 진지한 천성에서 우러난 자연스러운 애정이 고통으로 변해 버리고, 가장 부드러운 보호와 가장 소중한 보살핌 대신 무심하거나 매정한 거절이 들어서는 일은—얼마나 괴로운 일이었는지는 플로렌스 말고는 누구도 알지 못하리라!—참으로 괴로웠다."], ["It had been hard to feel in her deep heart what she had felt, and never know the happiness of one touch of response.", "깊은 가슴속으로 그토록 느껴 왔던 것을 느끼면서, 단 한 번의 응답의 손길이 주는 행복조차 알지 못하는 것은 괴로운 일이었다."], ["But it was much more hard to be compelled to doubt either her father or Edith, so affectionate and dear to her, and to think of her love for each of them, by turns, with fear, distrust, and wonder.", "그러나 그토록 다정하고 소중한 아버지든 에디스든 그 어느 쪽을 의심하지 않을 수 없게 되고, 두 사람 각각에 대한 자신의 사랑을 번갈아 두려움과 불신과 의아함으로 곱씹어야 하는 것은 훨씬 더 괴로운 일이었다."]], [["Yet Florence now began to do so; and the doing of it was a task imposed upon her by the very purity of her soul, as one she could not fly from.", "그러나 플로렌스는 이제 그렇게 하기 시작했다. 그리고 그렇게 하는 것은 도무지 달아날 수 없는 과업으로서, 바로 그녀 영혼의 순수함 자체가 그녀에게 지운 것이었다."], ["She saw her father cold and obdurate to Edith, as to her; hard, inflexible, unyielding.", "그녀는 아버지가 자기에게 그러하듯 에디스에게도 차갑고 완고하며, 단단하고 굽힐 줄 모르고 양보가 없는 것을 보았다."], ["Could it be, she asked herself with starting tears, that her own dear mother had been made unhappy by such treatment, and had pined away and died?", "그녀는 솟구치는 눈물과 함께 스스로에게 물었다. 혹시 사랑하던 자기 어머니도 그런 대접 때문에 불행해져 시들어 죽어 간 것은 아니었을까?"], ["Then she would think how proud and stately Edith was to everyone but her, with what disdain she treated him, how distantly she kept apart from him, and what she had said on the night when they came home; and quickly it would come on Florence, almost as a crime, that she loved one who was set in opposition to her father, and that her father knowing of it, must think of her in his solitary room as the unnatural child who added this wrong to the old fault, so much wept for, of never having won his fatherly affection from her birth.", "그러다가 그녀는 에디스가 자기를 제외한 모든 사람에게 얼마나 도도하고 위엄을 부렸는지, 아버지를 얼마나 경멸하며 대했는지, 얼마나 멀찌감치 거리를 두었는지, 그리고 집에 돌아온 그날 밤에 무슨 말을 했는지를 떠올리곤 했다. 그러면 곧 플로렌스에게 거의 죄악처럼 다가오는 생각이 있었으니, 자기가 아버지와 대립하는 사람을 사랑하고 있다는 것, 그리고 아버지가 그것을 알면 자기를 고독한 방 안에서 떠올리며 천성을 거스른 자식—태어날 때부터 아버지의 애정을 끝내 얻지 못했다는, 그토록 눈물지어 온 그 옛 잘못에 이 새로운 잘못까지 더한 자식—으로 여길 것이 틀림없다는 생각이었다."], ["The next kind word from Edith, the next kind glance, would shake these thoughts again, and make them seem like black ingratitude; for who but she had cheered the drooping heart of Florence, so lonely and so hurt, and been its best of comforters!", "에디스의 다음번 다정한 말 한마디, 다음번 다정한 눈길이 이 생각들을 다시 흔들어 놓아, 그것들이 마치 시커먼 배은망덕처럼 여겨지게 했다. 그토록 외롭고 상처 입어 풀이 죽은 플로렌스의 마음을 북돋워 주고, 가장 훌륭한 위로자가 되어 준 사람이 그녀 말고 또 누가 있었던가!"], ["Thus, with her gentle nature yearning to them both, feeling for the misery of both, and whispering doubts of her own duty to both, Florence in her wider and expanded love, and by the side of Edith, endured more than when she had hoarded up her undivided secret in the mournful house, and her beautiful Mama had never dawned upon it.", "이렇듯 그 온화한 천성으로 두 사람 모두를 그리워하고, 두 사람 모두의 비참함을 함께 아파하며, 두 사람 모두에 대한 자기 도리를 의심하는 속삭임을 품은 채로, 플로렌스는 더 넓어지고 확장된 사랑 속에서, 에디스 곁에서, 그 슬픔에 잠긴 집 안에서 나뉘지 않은 비밀을 홀로 쌓아 두고 아름다운 어머니가 그 위에 한 줄기 빛으로 떠오르지 않았던 시절보다 더 많은 것을 견뎌 냈다."]], [["One exquisite unhappiness that would have far outweighed this, Florence was spared.", "이 모든 것을 훨씬 능가했을 단 하나의 지독한 불행만은 플로렌스에게서 비껴갔다."], ["She never had the least suspicion that Edith by her tenderness for her widened the separation from her father, or gave him new cause of dislike.", "에디스가 자기를 향한 다정함으로 인해 아버지와의 사이를 더 벌려 놓는다거나, 아버지에게 새로운 미움의 빌미를 준다는 것을 그녀는 추호도 의심한 적이 없었다."], ["If Florence had conceived the possibility of such an effect being wrought by such a cause, what grief she would have felt, what sacrifice she would have tried to make, poor loving girl, how fast and sure her quiet passage might have been beneath it to the presence of that higher Father who does not reject his children’s love, or spurn their tried and broken hearts, Heaven knows!", "만약 플로렌스가 그런 원인에서 그런 결과가 빚어질 수 있음을 짐작이라도 했더라면, 가엾고 사랑이 많은 그 소녀가 얼마나 큰 슬픔을 느꼈을지, 얼마나 큰 희생을 치르려 했을지, 그리고 그 무게에 짓눌려 자식들의 사랑을 물리치지 않으시고 그들의 시달리고 부서진 마음을 내치지 않으시는 더 높으신 아버지의 곁으로 그녀가 얼마나 빠르고 확실하게 조용히 건너갔을지는 오직 하늘만이 아실 것이다!"], ["But it was otherwise, and that was well.", "그러나 사정은 그렇지 않았고, 그것은 다행한 일이었다."]], [["No word was ever spoken between Florence and Edith now, on these subjects.", "이제 플로렌스와 에디스 사이에서는 이런 문제들에 관해 단 한마디도 오가지 않았다."], ["Edith had said there ought to be between them, in that wise, a division and a silence like the grave itself: and Florence felt she was right.", "에디스는 그런 점에 있어서는 두 사람 사이에 무덤 그 자체와도 같은 단절과 침묵이 있어야 한다고 말했고, 플로렌스도 그 말이 옳다고 느꼈다."]], [["In this state of affairs her father was brought home, suffering and disabled; and gloomily retired to his own rooms, where he was tended by servants, not approached by Edith, and had no friend or companion but Mr Carker, who withdrew near midnight.", "이러한 형편 속에서 그녀의 아버지가 다치고 거동을 못 하는 몸으로 집에 실려 왔다. 그는 침울하게 자기 방으로 틀어박혔고, 하인들의 시중을 받았을 뿐 에디스는 다가가지 않았으며, 자정 무렵에 물러간 카커 씨 말고는 벗도 동무도 없었다."]], [["“And nice company he is, Miss Floy,”", "\"게다가 그자가 어찌나 좋은 동무인지요, 플로이 아가씨,\""], ["said Susan Nipper.", "수전 닙퍼가 말했다."], ["“Oh, he’s a precious piece of goods!", "\"어휴, 그자는 정말 귀하신 물건이라니까요!"], ["If ever he wants a character don’t let him come to me whatever he does, that’s all I tell him.”", "혹시라도 그자가 추천서 한 장이 필요해진대도 저한테는 절대 오지 말라지, 무슨 짓을 하든 간에요. 제가 그자한테 할 말은 그게 전부예요.\""]], [["“Dear Susan,”", "\"수전, 제발,\""], ["urged Florence, “don’t!”", "플로렌스가 타일렀다, \"그러지 마.\""]], [["“Oh, it’s very well to say ‘don’t’ Miss Floy,”", "\"오, '그러지 마'라고 말씀하시는 거야 쉽지요, 플로이 아가씨,\""], ["returned the Nipper, much exasperated; “but raly begging your pardon we’re coming to such passes that it turns all the blood in a person’s body into pins and needles, with their pints all ways.", "닙퍼가 잔뜩 격분해서 대꾸했다. \"하지만 정말이지 죄송한 말씀이지만 우리 신세가 어찌나 한심한 지경에 이르렀는지 사람 온몸의 피가 죄다 바늘방석으로 변해 버릴 지경이라고요, 사방에서 그렇게 콕콕 찔러 대니."], ["Don’t mistake me, Miss Floy, I don’t mean nothing again your ma-in-law who has always treated me as a lady should though she is rather high I must say not that I have any right to object to that particular, but when we come to Mrs Pipchinses and having them put over us and keeping guard at your Pa’s door like crocodiles (only make us thankful that they lay no eggs!) we are a growing too outrageous!”", "오해는 마세요, 플로이 아가씨, 새어머님을 두고 하는 말은 전혀 아니에요, 그분이야 좀 콧대가 높으시긴 해도—그 점에 대해 제가 뭐라 할 자격은 없지만요—늘 저를 숙녀에게 하듯이 대해 주셨으니까요. 하지만 핍친 부인 같은 사람들이며, 그런 사람들을 우리 머리 위에 앉혀 놓고 아가씨 아버님 방문 앞에서 악어처럼 보초나 서게 하는 데에 이르면(그래도 그것들이 알을 안 까는 것만은 고맙게 여겨야지요!) 우리 신세가 너무나도 기가 막혀지는 거예요!\""]], [["“Papa thinks well of Mrs Pipchin, Susan,”", "\"아빠는 핍친 부인을 좋게 여기시잖니, 수전,\""], ["returned Florence, “and has a right to choose his housekeeper, you know.", "플로렌스가 대꾸했다, \"그리고 가정부를 고르실 권리야 아빠한테 있는 거고."], ["Pray don’t!”", "그러니 제발 그러지 마.\""]], [["“Well Miss Floy,”", "\"그럼요 플로이 아가씨,\""], ["returned the Nipper, “when you say don’t, I never do I hope but Mrs Pipchin acts like early gooseberries upon me Miss, and nothing less.”", "닙퍼가 대꾸했다, \"아가씨가 그러지 말라고 하시면 저는 절대로 안 합니다, 그러길 바라고요. 하지만 핍친 부인은 저한테 풋내 나는 산딸기처럼 시큰거리게 만든다니까요, 아가씨, 딱 그 짝이에요.\""]], [["Susan was unusually emphatic and destitute of punctuation in her discourse on this night, which was the night of Mr Dombey’s being brought home, because, having been sent downstairs by Florence to inquire after him, she had been obliged to deliver her message to her mortal enemy Mrs Pipchin; who, without carrying it in to Mr Dombey, had taken upon herself to return what Miss Nipper called a huffish answer, on her own responsibility.", "수전은 이날 밤, 즉 돔비 씨가 집으로 실려 온 그 밤에 평소답지 않게 격앙되고 마침표라곤 없는 말투로 떠들어 댔는데, 플로렌스가 아버지의 안부를 알아보라고 그녀를 아래층으로 내려보냈을 때, 천적인 핍친 부인에게 전갈을 전달할 수밖에 없었던 탓이었다. 그 부인은 그것을 돔비 씨에게 들고 들어가지도 않고, 제멋대로 닙퍼 아가씨가 \"거만한 대답\"이라고 부르는 것을 자기 책임하에 돌려보낸 것이다."], ["This, Susan Nipper construed into presumption on the part of that exemplary sufferer by the Peruvian mines, and a deed of disparagement upon her young lady, that was not to be forgiven; and so far her emphatic state was special.", "수전 닙퍼는 이것을, 페루의 광산으로 고통받는 그 모범적인 수난자 측의 주제넘은 짓이자, 자기 아가씨를 깔보는 도저히 용서할 수 없는 처사로 해석했다. 그래서 그만큼 그녀의 격앙된 상태는 각별했다."], ["But she had been in a condition of greatly increased suspicion and distrust, ever since the marriage; for, like most persons of her quality of mind, who form a strong and sincere attachment to one in the different station which Florence occupied, Susan was very jealous, and her jealousy naturally attached to Edith, who divided her old empire, and came between them.", "그러나 그녀는 그 결혼 이후로 줄곧 의심과 불신이 크게 부풀어 오른 상태였다. 플로렌스가 차지한 것과 같은 다른 신분의 누군가에게 강하고 진실한 애착을 품는, 그녀와 같은 부류의 사람들이 대개 그렇듯이, 수전은 질투가 무척 심했고, 그 질투는 자연히 에디스를 향했으니, 에디스가 그녀의 옛 영토를 나누어 가지며 두 사람 사이에 끼어들었기 때문이다."], ["Proud and glad as Susan Nipper truly was, that her young mistress should be advanced towards her proper place in the scene of her old neglect, and that she should have her father’s handsome wife for her companion and protectress, she could not relinquish any part of her own dominion to the handsome wife, without a grudge and a vague feeling of ill-will, for which she did not fail to find a disinterested justification in her sharp perception of the pride and passion of the lady’s character.", "자기 어린 여주인이 그동안 천대받던 무대에서 마땅한 제자리를 향해 올라서고, 아버지의 아름다운 부인을 동무이자 보호자로 두게 된 것을 수전 닙퍼가 진심으로 자랑스럽고 기뻐하면서도, 그녀는 자기 영역의 어떤 부분도 그 아름다운 부인에게 넘겨주는 데에는 못마땅함과 막연한 적의가 없을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 적의에 대해, 그 부인의 성품에 깃든 오만과 격정을 예리하게 간파해 낸 덕분에 사심 없는 정당한 구실을 어김없이 찾아내곤 했다."], ["From the background to which she had necessarily retired somewhat, since the marriage, Miss Nipper looked on, therefore, at domestic affairs in general, with a resolute conviction that no good would come of Mrs Dombey: always being very careful to publish on all possible occasions, that she had nothing to say against her.", "그러므로 결혼 이후로 다소 물러설 수밖에 없었던 뒷전에서 닙퍼 아가씨는 집안일 전반을, 돔비 부인에게서는 결코 좋은 일이 나오지 않으리라는 단호한 확신을 품은 채 지켜보았다. 다만 가능한 모든 기회마다, 자기는 그 부인을 험담할 생각이 조금도 없다는 것을 공언하는 데에 항상 무척 신경을 썼다."]], [["“Susan,”", "\"수전,\""], ["said Florence, who was sitting thoughtfully at her table, “it is very late.", "탁자에 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있던 플로렌스가 말했다, \"아주 늦었구나."], ["I shall want nothing more tonight.”", "오늘 밤엔 더 필요한 게 없어.\""]], [["“Ah, Miss Floy!”", "\"아아, 플로이 아가씨!\" 닙퍼가 대꾸했다."], ["returned the Nipper, “I’m sure I often wish for them old times when I sat up with you hours later than this and fell asleep through being tired out when you was as broad awake as spectacles, but you’ve ma’s-in-law to come and sit with you now Miss Floy and I’m thankful for it I’m sure.", "\"정말이지 저는 그 옛 시절이 자주 그리워져요, 제가 이보다 훨씬 더 늦은 시각까지 아가씨 곁을 지키다가 지쳐서 잠들어 버리고 아가씨는 안경알처럼 말똥말똥 깨어 있던 그 시절이요. 하지만 이젠 새어머님이 오셔서 아가씨랑 함께 앉아 계시니, 정말 그게 고마운 일이지요."], ["I’ve not a word to say against ’em.”", "그분들에 대해선 한마디도 흠잡을 게 없고요.\""]], [["“I shall not forget who was my old companion when I had none, Susan,”", "\"동무가 하나도 없을 때 내 옛 동무가 누구였는지 나는 잊지 않을 거야, 수전,\""], ["returned Florence, gently, “never!”", "플로렌스가 부드럽게 대꾸했다, \"절대로!\""], ["And looking up, she put her arm round the neck of her humble friend, drew her face down to hers, and bidding her good-night, kissed it; which so mollified Miss Nipper, that she fell a sobbing.", "그러고는 고개를 들어, 그 미천한 벗의 목에 팔을 두르고, 그 얼굴을 자기 얼굴 쪽으로 끌어당겨, 잘 자라는 인사와 함께 거기에 입을 맞추었다. 그러자 닙퍼 아가씨는 어찌나 마음이 누그러졌는지 흐느껴 울기 시작했다."]], [["“Now my dear Miss Floy,”", "\"자 플로이 아가씨,\""], ["said Susan, “let me go downstairs again and see how your Pa is, I know you’re wretched about him, do let me go downstairs again and knock at his door my own self.”", "수전이 말했다, \"제가 다시 한번 아래층에 내려가서 아버님이 어떠신지 보고 올게요, 아가씨가 그분 때문에 마음 졸이시는 거 다 알아요, 제발 제가 다시 아래층에 내려가서 제 손으로 직접 그 방문을 두드려 보게 해 주세요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Florence, “go to bed.", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"가서 자."], ["We shall hear more in the morning.", "아침이면 더 알게 될 거야."], ["I will inquire myself in the morning.", "아침에 내가 직접 알아볼게."], ["Mama has been down, I daresay;”", "어머니가 다녀가셨을 거야, 아마도.\""], ["Florence blushed, for she had no such hope; “or is there now, perhaps.", "플로렌스는 얼굴을 붉혔으니, 사실 그런 기대는 품지 않았기 때문이다. \"아니면 지금 거기 계실지도 모르고."], ["Good-night!”", "잘 자!\""]], [["Susan was too much softened to express her private opinion on the probability of Mrs Dombey’s being in attendance on her husband, and silently withdrew.", "수전은 너무나 마음이 누그러진 터라 돔비 부인이 남편 곁에 붙어 있을 가능성에 대한 자기 속내를 입에 올리지 못한 채 말없이 물러갔다."], ["Florence left alone, soon hid her head upon her hands as she had often done in other days, and did not restrain the tears from coursing down her face.", "홀로 남은 플로렌스는 이내 지난날 자주 그랬던 것처럼 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었고, 눈물이 뺨을 타고 흘러내리는 것을 막지 않았다."], ["The misery of this domestic discord and unhappiness; the withered hope she cherished now, if hope it could be called, of ever being taken to her father’s heart; her doubts and fears between the two; the yearning of her innocent breast to both; the heavy disappointment and regret of such an end as this, to what had been a vision of bright hope and promise to her; all crowded on her mind and made her tears flow fast.", "이 집안의 불화와 불행이 주는 비참함, 언젠가는 아버지의 가슴에 받아들여지리라는, 그것을 희망이라 부를 수 있다면, 이제 시들어 버린 그 희망, 두 사람 사이에서 오가는 의심과 두려움, 두 사람 모두를 향한 그 순결한 가슴의 그리움, 한때 밝은 희망과 약속의 환영이었던 것이 이런 식으로 끝나 버린 데서 오는 묵직한 실망과 회한—이 모든 것이 그녀의 마음속으로 한꺼번에 몰려와 눈물을 빠르게 쏟게 했다."], ["Her mother and her brother dead, her father unmoved towards her, Edith opposed to him and casting him away, but loving her, and loved by her, it seemed as if her affection could never prosper, rest where it would.", "어머니와 남동생은 죽었고, 아버지는 그녀에게 무심하며, 에디스는 아버지와 맞서 그를 내치면서도 그녀를 사랑하고 그녀의 사랑을 받으니, 마치 그녀의 애정은 어디에 깃들든 결코 잘 자라날 수 없을 것만 같았다."], ["That weak thought was soon hushed, but the thoughts in which it had arisen were too true and strong to be dismissed with it; and they made the night desolate.", "그 미약한 생각은 곧 잠잠해졌지만, 그것이 솟아난 바탕이 된 생각들은 너무나 진실하고 강해서 그 생각과 함께 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없었다. 그리고 그것들이 그 밤을 황량하게 만들었다."]], [["Among such reflections there rose up, as there had risen up all day, the image of her father, wounded and in pain, alone in his own room, untended by those who should be nearest to him, and passing the tardy hours in lonely suffering.", "그러한 상념들 가운데, 하루 종일 떠올랐던 것처럼, 아버지의 모습이 솟아올랐다. 상처를 입고 고통 속에서, 가장 가까이 있어야 할 이들의 보살핌도 받지 못한 채 자기 방에 홀로 있으며, 더디 가는 시간을 외로운 고통 속에서 보내고 있는 아버지의 모습이."], ["A frightened thought which made her start and clasp her hands—though it was not a new one in her mind—that he might die, and never see her or pronounce her name, thrilled her whole frame.", "그녀를 깜짝 놀라게 하여 두 손을 마주 잡게 한 무서운 생각—비록 그녀의 마음속에 새로운 것은 아니었지만—즉 아버지가 죽어 버려, 그녀를 보지도 못하고 그녀의 이름을 부르지도 못할지 모른다는 생각이 온몸에 전율을 일으켰다."], ["In her agitation she thought, and trembled while she thought, of once more stealing downstairs, and venturing to his door.", "그 동요 속에서 그녀는 다시 한번 살그머니 아래층으로 내려가 그 방문 앞까지 가 볼까 하는 생각을 했고, 그 생각을 하며 몸을 떨었다."]], [["She listened at her own.", "그녀는 자기 방문 앞에서 귀를 기울였다."], ["The house was quiet, and all the lights were out.", "집 안은 고요했고 모든 불이 꺼져 있었다."], ["It was a long, long time, she thought, since she used to make her nightly pilgrimages to his door!", "밤마다 그 방문 앞으로 순례를 다니곤 하던 때로부터 참으로 오랜, 오랜 시간이 흘렀구나, 하고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["It was a long, long time, she tried to think, since she had entered his room at midnight, and he had led her back to the stair-foot!", "한밤중에 그 방에 들어갔다가 아버지가 그녀를 계단 아래까지 데려다주던 때로부터 참으로 오랜, 오랜 시간이 흘렀구나, 하고 그녀는 애써 떠올렸다!"]], [["With the same child’s heart within her, as of old: even with the child’s sweet timid eyes and clustering hair: Florence, as strange to her father in her early maiden bloom, as in her nursery time, crept down the staircase listening as she went, and drew near to his room.", "옛날과 똑같은 어린아이의 마음을 가슴속에 간직한 채, 그 어린아이의 다정하고 수줍은 눈빛과 곱슬곱슬 풍성한 머리카락마저 그대로 지닌 채, 이른 처녀의 꽃봉오리로 피어난 지금에도 어린 시절 못지않게 아버지에게는 낯선 존재인 플로렌스는, 걸음마다 귀를 기울이며 계단을 살금살금 내려가 그의 방으로 다가갔다."], ["No one was stirring in the house.", "집 안에서는 아무도 움직이지 않았다."], ["The door was partly open to admit air; and all was so still within, that she could hear the burning of the fire, and count the ticking of the clock that stood upon the chimney-piece.", "방문은 바람이 통하도록 반쯤 열려 있었고, 안은 어찌나 고요한지 불 타는 소리가 들리고 벽난로 선반 위에 놓인 시계의 똑딱이는 소리를 헤아릴 수 있을 정도였다."]], [["She looked in.", "그녀는 안을 들여다보았다."], ["In that room, the housekeeper wrapped in a blanket was fast asleep in an easy chair before the fire.", "그 방에서는 담요를 두른 가정부가 난롯불 앞 안락의자에 깊이 잠들어 있었다."], ["The doors between it and the next were partly closed, and a screen was drawn before them; but there was a light there, and it shone upon the cornice of his bed.", "그 방과 옆방 사이의 문은 반쯤 닫혀 있었고 그 앞에는 가리개가 쳐져 있었다. 그러나 거기에는 불빛이 있었고, 그 빛이 그의 침대 처마 장식을 비추고 있었다."], ["All was so very still that she could hear from his breathing that he was asleep.", "모든 것이 어찌나 고요했는지 그녀는 그의 숨소리만으로도 그가 잠들어 있음을 알 수 있었다."], ["This gave her courage to pass round the screen, and look into his chamber.", "이것이 그녀에게 가리개를 돌아 그의 침실 안을 들여다볼 용기를 주었다."]], [["It was as great a start to come upon his sleeping face as if she had not expected to see it.", "잠든 그의 얼굴과 마주치는 것은, 마치 그것을 보게 되리라 예상치 못했던 것처럼 큰 놀라움이었다."], ["Florence stood arrested on the spot, and if he had awakened then, must have remained there.", "플로렌스는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 멈춰 섰고, 만약 그가 그때 깨어났더라면 그녀는 그대로 거기 있을 수밖에 없었을 것이다."]], [["There was a cut upon his forehead, and they had been wetting his hair, which lay bedabbled and entangled on the pillow.", "그의 이마에는 베인 상처가 있었고, 사람들이 그의 머리를 적셔 두어서, 머리카락이 베개 위에 흠뻑 젖은 채 헝클어져 있었다."], ["One of his arms, resting outside the bed, was bandaged up, and he was very white.", "침대 밖으로 내놓은 한쪽 팔은 붕대가 감겨 있었고, 그는 몹시 창백했다."], ["But it was not this, that after the first quick glance, and first assurance of his sleeping quietly, held Florence rooted to the ground.", "그러나 첫 번째 재빠른 일별 뒤에, 그리고 그가 조용히 잠들어 있다는 첫 확인 뒤에, 플로렌스를 그 자리에 뿌리내리게 한 것은 이것이 아니었다."], ["It was something very different from this, and more than this, that made him look so solemn in her eye.", "그녀의 눈에 그를 그토록 엄숙해 보이게 한 것은, 이것과는 아주 다른, 이것을 넘어서는 무언가였다."]], [["She had never seen his face in all her life, but there had been upon it—or she fancied so—some disturbing consciousness of her.", "그녀는 평생 그의 얼굴을 본 적이 없었으나, 그 얼굴에는—혹은 그녀가 그렇게 여긴 것인지도 모르지만—언제나 그녀를 의식하는 어떤 거북한 기색이 어려 있었다."], ["She had never seen his face in all her life, but hope had sunk within her, and her timid glance had dropped before its stern, unloving, and repelling harshness.", "그녀는 평생 그의 얼굴을 본 적이 없었으나, 그럴 때마다 그녀의 마음속 희망은 가라앉았고, 그 수줍은 눈길은 그 매정하고 사랑 없고 밀쳐 내는 듯한 냉혹함 앞에서 떨어지곤 했다."], ["As she looked upon it now, she saw it, for the first time, free from the cloud that had darkened her childhood.", "그러나 지금 그 얼굴을 바라보면서, 그녀는 자기 어린 시절을 어둡게 했던 그 먹구름에서 벗어난 그 얼굴을 난생처음 보았다."], ["Calm, tranquil night was reigning in its stead.", "그 자리에는 잔잔하고 평온한 밤이 깃들어 있었다."], ["He might have gone to sleep, for anything she saw there, blessing her.", "그녀가 거기서 본 것만으로 판단한다면, 그는 그녀를 축복하며 잠든 것이라 해도 좋을 정도였다."]], [["Awake, unkind father!", "깨어나세요, 매정한 아버지여!"], ["Awake, now, sullen man!", "깨어나세요, 지금, 시무룩한 사람이여!"], ["The time is flitting by; the hour is coming with an angry tread.", "시간이 흘러가고 있어요. 시각이 성난 발걸음으로 다가오고 있어요."], ["Awake!", "깨어나세요!"]], [["There was no change upon his face; and as she watched it, awfully, its motionless response recalled the faces that were gone.", "그의 얼굴에는 아무런 변화가 없었다. 그리고 그녀가 그것을 두려운 마음으로 지켜보고 있을 때, 그 미동도 없는 모습은 이미 떠나간 이들의 얼굴을 떠올리게 했다."], ["So they looked, so would he; so she, his weeping child, who should say when!", "그들도 저렇게 보였고, 그도 저렇게 될 것이었다. 그리고 울고 있는 그의 자식인 그녀도, 그때가 언제일지는 누가 말할 수 있으랴!"], ["so all the world of love and hatred and indifference around them!", "그리고 그들을 둘러싼 사랑과 미움과 무관심으로 가득한 온 세상의 사람들도 모두 그렇게 될 것이었다."], ["When that time should come, it would not be the heavier to him, for this that she was going to do; and it might fall something lighter upon her.", "그때가 닥쳐오면, 지금 그녀가 하려는 이 일 때문에 그것이 그에게 더 무겁지는 않을 것이며, 그녀에게는 그것이 어쩌면 조금은 더 가볍게 내려앉을지도 몰랐다."]], [["She stole close to the bed, and drawing in her breath, bent down, and softly kissed him on the face, and laid her own for one brief moment by its side, and put the arm, with which she dared not touch him, round about him on the pillow.", "그녀는 살그머니 침대로 다가가, 숨을 들이마시고, 몸을 굽혀, 그의 얼굴에 가만히 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 자기 얼굴을 잠시 그 곁에 가져다 댔으며, 차마 그에게 대지 못하던 그 팔을 베개 위로 둘러 그를 감쌌다."]], [["Awake, doomed man, while she is near!", "깨어나요, 운명 지어진 사람이여, 그녀가 곁에 있을 때! 시간이 흘러가고 있어요."], ["The time is flitting by; the hour is coming with an angry tread; its foot is in the house.", "시각이 성난 발걸음으로 다가오고 있어요. 그 발이 이미 이 집 안에 들어와 있어요."], ["Awake!", "깨어나요!"]], [["In her mind, she prayed to God to bless her father, and to soften him towards her, if it might be so; and if not, to forgive him if he was wrong, and pardon her the prayer which almost seemed impiety.", "그녀는 마음속으로 하느님께 기도했다. 아버지를 축복해 주시고, 만약 가능하다면 그를 자기에게로 누그러지게 해 주시고, 그렇지 못하더라도 그가 잘못이 있다면 그를 용서해 주시고, 거의 불경처럼 여겨지는 이 기도를 드리는 자신을 용서해 주십사고."], ["And doing so, and looking back at him with blinded eyes, and stealing timidly away, passed out of his room, and crossed the other, and was gone.", "그렇게 기도하며, 눈물로 흐려진 눈으로 그를 돌아보고, 수줍게 살그머니 빠져나와, 그녀는 그의 방을 나서서 다른 방을 가로질러 사라졌다."]], [["He may sleep on now.", "이제 그는 계속 잠들어도 좋다."], ["He may sleep on while he may.", "잠들 수 있는 동안 계속 잠들어도 좋다."], ["But let him look for that slight figure when he wakes, and find it near him when the hour is come!", "그러나 깨어났을 때 그 가냘픈 모습을 찾을 것이며, 그때가 닥쳐왔을 때 그것이 자기 곁에 있음을 발견하기를!"]], [["Sad and grieving was the heart of Florence, as she crept upstairs.", "플로렌스가 살금살금 위층으로 올라갈 때, 그 마음은 슬프고 비통했다."], ["The quiet house had grown more dismal since she came down.", "그 고요한 집은 그녀가 내려갔을 때보다 한층 더 음산해져 있었다."], ["The sleep she had been looking on, in the dead of night, had the solemnity to her of death and life in one.", "그녀가 한밤중에 지켜보고 온 그 잠은, 그녀에게는 죽음과 삶이 하나로 어우러진 듯한 엄숙함을 띠고 있었다."], ["The secrecy and silence of her own proceeding made the night secret, silent, and oppressive.", "그녀 자신이 한 일의 은밀함과 침묵은 그 밤을 은밀하고 고요하며 짓누르는 것으로 만들었다. 그녀는 자기 방으로 계속 가기가 내키지 않았고, 거의 갈 수가 없을 듯한 심정이었다."], ["She felt unwilling, almost unable, to go on to her own chamber; and turning into the drawing-rooms, where the clouded moon was shining through the blinds, looked out into the empty streets.", "그래서 응접실로 발길을 돌렸는데, 그곳에는 구름에 가린 달빛이 블라인드 사이로 비쳐 들고 있었다. 그녀는 텅 빈 거리를 내다보았다."]], [["The wind was blowing drearily.", "바람이 음울하게 불고 있었다."], ["The lamps looked pale, and shook as if they were cold.", "가로등들은 핼쑥해 보였고, 추워서 떠는 것처럼 흔들렸다."], ["There was a distant glimmer of something that was not quite darkness, rather than of light, in the sky; and foreboding night was shivering and restless, as the dying are who make a troubled end.", "하늘에는 빛이라기보다는 완전한 어둠이라고는 할 수 없는 무언가가 멀리서 어슴푸레 깜박이고 있었다. 그리고 불길한 밤은, 괴로운 임종을 맞는 죽어 가는 이들이 그러하듯, 떨며 안절부절못하고 있었다."], ["Florence remembered how, as a watcher, by a sick-bed, she had noted this bleak time, and felt its influence, as if in some hidden natural antipathy to it; and now it was very, very gloomy.", "플로렌스는 병상 곁의 불침번을 서던 때에 이 황량한 시각을 눈여겨보고, 마치 그것에 대한 어떤 숨은 본능적 반감이라도 있는 듯 그 영향을 느끼던 것을 떠올렸다. 그리고 지금은 그것이 너무나도, 너무나도 음울했다."]], [["Her Mama had not come to her room that night, which was one cause of her having sat late out of her bed.", "그날 밤 어머니는 그녀의 방에 오지 않았는데, 그것이 그녀가 잠자리에 들지 않고 늦도록 앉아 있었던 한 가지 까닭이었다."], ["In her general uneasiness, no less than in her ardent longing to have somebody to speak to, and to break the spell of gloom and silence, Florence directed her steps towards the chamber where she slept.", "전반적인 불안 못지않게, 누군가와 이야기를 나누고 싶고 그 음울함과 침묵의 마법을 깨고 싶은 간절한 그리움에서, 플로렌스는 어머니가 잠자는 방을 향해 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["The door was not fastened within, and yielded smoothly to her hesitating hand.", "방문은 안에서 잠겨 있지 않아서, 망설이는 그녀의 손길에 부드럽게 열렸다."], ["She was surprised to find a bright light burning; still more surprised, on looking in, to see that her Mama, but partially undressed, was sitting near the ashes of the fire, which had crumbled and dropped away.", "그녀는 밝은 불빛이 타오르고 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 안을 들여다보고는 더욱 놀랐으니, 어머니가 옷을 채 다 벗지도 않은 차림으로, 무너져 부스러져 내린 난롯불의 잿더미 곁에 앉아 있었던 것이다."], ["Her eyes were intently bent upon the air; and in their light, and in her face, and in her form, and in the grasp with which she held the elbows of her chair as if about to start up, Florence saw such fierce emotion that it terrified her.", "그녀의 두 눈은 허공을 뚫어지게 응시하고 있었고, 그 눈빛에, 그 얼굴에, 그 자태에, 그리고 금방이라도 벌떡 일어설 듯이 의자 팔걸이를 움켜쥔 그 손아귀에, 플로렌스는 자기를 섬뜩하게 할 만큼 격렬한 감정을 보았다."]], [["“Mama!”", "\"어머니!\""], ["she cried, “what is the matter?”", "그녀가 외쳤다, \"무슨 일이세요?\""]], [["Edith started; looking at her with such a strange dread in her face, that Florence was more frightened than before.", "에디스가 흠칫 놀랐다. 그녀가 너무나 기이한 두려움을 얼굴에 담은 채 플로렌스를 바라보는 바람에, 플로렌스는 아까보다 더 겁이 났다."]], [["“Mama!”", "\"어머니!\""], ["said Florence, hurriedly advancing.", "플로렌스가 황급히 다가서며 말했다."], ["“Dear Mama!", "\"사랑하는 어머니!"], ["what is the matter?”", "무슨 일이세요?\""]], [["“I have not been well,”", "\"몸이 좀 안 좋았단다,\""], ["said Edith, shaking, and still looking at her in the same strange way.", "에디스가 몸을 떨면서도 여전히 똑같은 기이한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I have had bad dreams, my love.”", "\"흉한 꿈을 꾸었어, 얘야.\""]], [["“And not yet been to bed, Mama?”", "\"그럼 아직 주무시지도 않으신 거예요, 어머니?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"그래,\""], ["she returned.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“Half-waking dreams.”", "\"반쯤 깨어 있는 꿈이었지.\""]], [["Her features gradually softened; and suffering Florence to come closer to her, within her embrace, she said in a tender manner, “But what does my bird do here?", "그녀의 표정이 차츰 부드러워졌다. 그리고 플로렌스가 더 가까이, 자기 품 안으로 다가오게 두면서, 그녀는 다정한 태도로 말했다."], ["What does my bird do here?”", "\"그런데 내 작은 새가 여기서 무얼 하고 있을까? 내 작은 새가 여기서 무얼 하고 있는 거니?\""]], [["“I have been uneasy, Mama, in not seeing you tonight, and in not knowing how Papa was; and I—”", "\"어머니, 오늘 밤 어머니를 뵙지 못해서, 그리고 아빠가 어떠신지 알 수가 없어서 마음이 편치 않았어요. 그래서 저는—\""]], [["Florence stopped there, and said no more.", "플로렌스는 거기서 말을 멈추고 더는 말하지 않았다."]], [["“Is it late?”", "\"늦었니?\""], ["asked Edith, fondly putting back the curls that mingled with her own dark hair, and strayed upon her face.", "에디스가, 자기 검은 머리카락에 뒤섞여 얼굴 위로 흘러내린 곱슬머리를 다정하게 쓸어 넘겨 주며 물었다."]], [["“Very late.", "\"아주 늦었어요."], ["Near day.”", "거의 동틀 무렵이에요.\""]], [["“Near day!”", "\"동틀 무렵이라고!\""], ["she repeated in surprise.", "그녀가 놀라서 되뇌었다."]], [["“Dear Mama, what have you done to your hand?”", "\"사랑하는 어머니, 손을 어쩌다 그러셨어요?\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["Edith drew it suddenly away, and, for a moment, looked at her with the same strange dread (there was a sort of wild avoidance in it) as before; but she presently said, “Nothing, nothing.", "에디스는 손을 갑자기 거두어들였고, 한순간 아까와 똑같은 기이한 두려움(그 안에는 일종의 거친 회피가 깃들어 있었다)으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그러나 곧 이렇게 말했다, \"아무것도 아니야, 아무것도."], ["A blow.” And then she said, “My Florence!”", "부딪힌 거란다.\""], ["and then her bosom heaved, and she was weeping passionately.", "그러고는 \"내 플로렌스!\"라고 말했고, 이어 가슴이 들썩이더니 격정적으로 흐느껴 울었다."]], [["“Mama!”", "\"어머니!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“Oh Mama, what can I do, what should I do, to make us happier?", "\"오 어머니, 제가 무얼 할 수 있을까요, 제가 무얼 해야 우리가 더 행복해질까요?"], ["Is there anything?”", "뭔가 있나요?\""]], [["“Nothing,”", "\"아무것도 없어,\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Are you sure of that?", "\"정말 그래요?"], ["Can it never be?", "정녕 그럴 수는 없는 건가요?"], ["If I speak now of what is in my thoughts, in spite of what we have agreed,”", "우리가 약속한 것에도 불구하고 제가 지금 제 마음속에 있는 것을 말한다 해도,\""], ["said Florence, “you will not blame me, will you?”", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"저를 탓하지 않으실 거죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“It is useless,”", "\"소용없는 일이야,\""], ["she replied, “useless.", "그녀가 대답했다, \"소용없어."], ["I have told you, dear, that I have had bad dreams.", "내가 말했잖니, 얘야, 흉한 꿈을 꾸었다고."], ["Nothing can change them, or prevent them coming back.”", "그 무엇도 그 꿈들을 바꾸거나, 다시 찾아오는 걸 막을 수는 없어.\""]], [["“I do not understand,”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠어요,\""], ["said Florence, gazing on her agitated face which seemed to darken as she looked.", "플로렌스가, 바라볼수록 어두워지는 듯한 그 동요하는 얼굴을 응시하며 말했다."]], [["“I have dreamed,”", "\"꿈을 꾸었단다,\" 에디스가 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"선을 행하는 데는 전혀 무력하고 악을 행하는 데는 온통 강력한 어떤 오만의 꿈을."], ["said Edith in a low voice, “of a pride that is all powerless for good, all powerful for evil; of a pride that has been galled and goaded, through many shameful years, and has never recoiled except upon itself; a pride that has debased its owner with the consciousness of deep humiliation, and never helped its owner boldly to resent it or avoid it, or to say, ‘This shall not be!’ a pride that, rightly guided, might have led perhaps to better things, but which, misdirected and perverted, like all else belonging to the same possessor, has been self-contempt, mere hardihood and ruin.”", "여러 부끄러운 세월 동안 채찍질당하고 들볶이면서도 결코 제 자신을 향한 것 말고는 반격해 본 적이 없는 오만, 깊은 굴욕을 의식하게 하여 그 주인을 비천하게 만들었으면서도, 정작 그 주인이 그것에 당당히 분노하거나 그것을 피하거나 '이래서는 안 돼!'라고 말하도록 결코 도와준 적이 없는 오만. 올바로 인도되었더라면 어쩌면 더 나은 것으로 이어졌을지도 모르나, 그 같은 소유자에게 속한 다른 모든 것과 마찬가지로 그릇 인도되고 비뚤어진 나머지, 자기 경멸과 한낱 무모함과 파멸이 되어 버린 오만의 꿈을.\""]], [["She neither looked nor spoke to Florence now, but went on as if she were alone.", "이제 그녀는 플로렌스를 바라보지도, 그녀에게 말하지도 않고, 마치 혼자 있는 것처럼 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“I have dreamed,”", "\"꿈을 꾸었단다,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"이 자기 경멸에서, 이 비참하고 무력하고 가련한 오만에서 비롯된 그런 무관심과 무감각의 꿈을."], ["she said, “of such indifference and callousness, arising from this self-contempt; this wretched, inefficient, miserable pride; that it has gone on with listless steps even to the altar, yielding to the old, familiar, beckoning finger,—oh mother, oh mother!—while it spurned it; and willing to be hateful to itself for once and for all, rather than to be stung daily in some new form.", "그것이 옛날의, 익숙한, 손짓하는 그 손가락에 굴복한 채로—오 어머니, 오 어머니!—그러면서도 그것을 경멸하면서, 무기력한 걸음으로 제단 앞까지 나아간 꿈을. 그래서 날마다 어떤 새로운 형태로 찔리느니 차라리 단번에 영영 제 자신에게 가증스러운 존재가 되기를 마다하지 않은 꿈을."], ["Mean, poor thing!”", "비루하고 가련한 것!\""]], [["And now with gathering and darkening emotion, she looked as she had looked when Florence entered.", "그러고는 이제 점점 차오르고 어두워지는 감정과 함께, 그녀는 플로렌스가 들어왔을 때와 똑같은 표정을 지었다."]], [["“And I have dreamed,”", "\"그리고 꿈을 꾸었단다,\""], ["she said, “that in a first late effort to achieve a purpose, it has been trodden on, and trodden down by a base foot, but turns and looks upon him.", "그녀가 말했다, \"그 오만이 어떤 목적을 이루려는 뒤늦은 첫 시도에서, 어느 비천한 발에 짓밟히고 짓이겨졌지만, 돌아서서 그를 노려보는 꿈을. 그것이 상처 입고, 쫓기고, 개들에게 물어뜯기면서도, 막다른 데서 맞서며 굴복하지 않는 꿈을."], ["I have dreamed that it is wounded, hunted, set upon by dogs, but that it stands at bay, and will not yield; no, that it cannot if it would; but that it is urged on to hate.”", "아니, 굴복하려 한들 그럴 수가 없는 꿈을. 그러나 그것이 증오로 내몰리고 있는 꿈을.\""]], [["Her clenched hand tightened on the trembling arm she had in hers, and as she looked down on the alarmed and wondering face, frown subsided.", "그녀의 움켜쥔 손이 자기가 붙들고 있던 떨리는 팔을 더욱 세게 죄었다. 그리고 그 놀라고 의아해하는 얼굴을 내려다보면서, 찌푸린 미간이 가라앉았다."], ["“Oh Florence!”", "\"오 플로렌스!\""], ["she said, “I think I have been nearly mad tonight!”", "그녀가 말했다, \"내가 오늘 밤 거의 미쳐 버린 것 같구나!\""], ["and humbled her proud head upon her neck and wept again.", "그러고는 그 도도한 머리를 그녀의 목에 기대고 다시 울었다."]], [["“Don’t leave me!", "\"나를 떠나지 마!"], ["be near me!", "내 곁에 있어 줘!"], ["I have no hope but in you!”", "나에겐 너 말고는 희망이 없어!\""], ["These words she said a score of times.", "이 말을 그녀는 수십 번이나 되풀이했다."]], [["Soon she grew calmer, and was full of pity for the tears of Florence, and for her waking at such untimely hours.", "이윽고 그녀는 차츰 차분해졌고, 플로렌스의 눈물과 이런 때아닌 시각에 그녀가 깨어 있는 것에 대한 연민으로 가득 찼다."], ["And the day now dawning, Edith folded her in her arms and laid her down upon her bed, and, not lying down herself, sat by her, and bade her try to sleep.", "그리고 이제 날이 밝아 오자, 에디스는 그녀를 품에 안아 침대에 눕히고, 자기는 눕지 않은 채 그 곁에 앉아 잠을 청해 보라고 일렀다."]], [["“For you are weary, dearest, and unhappy, and should rest.”", "\"너는 지쳤으니까, 얘야, 그리고 슬프니까, 쉬어야 해.\""]], [["“I am indeed unhappy, dear Mama, tonight,”", "\"저는 정말이지 오늘 밤 슬퍼요, 사랑하는 어머니,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“But you are weary and unhappy, too.”", "\"하지만 어머니도 지치고 슬프시잖아요.\""]], [["“Not when you lie asleep so near me, sweet.”", "\"네가 이렇게 내 가까이서 잠들어 있으면 그렇지 않단다, 얘야.\""]], [["They kissed each other, and Florence, worn out, gradually fell into a gentle slumber; but as her eyes closed on the face beside her, it was so sad to think upon the face downstairs, that her hand drew closer to Edith for some comfort; yet, even in the act, it faltered, lest it should be deserting him.", "둘은 서로 입을 맞추었고, 지칠 대로 지친 플로렌스는 차츰 부드러운 잠 속으로 빠져들었다. 그러나 그 곁에 있는 얼굴 위로 눈이 감기는 순간, 아래층에 있는 그 얼굴을 떠올리는 것이 너무나 슬퍼서, 그녀의 손은 위안을 구하듯 에디스에게로 더 가까이 다가갔다."], ["So, in her sleep, she tried to reconcile the two together, and to show them that she loved them both, but could not do it, and her waking grief was part of her dreams.", "하지만 그 와중에도, 혹시 아버지를 저버리는 것은 아닐까 하여 그 손은 멈칫거렸다. 그래서 그녀는 잠 속에서 그 둘을 서로 화해시키려, 그리고 자기가 둘 다 사랑한다는 것을 그들에게 보여 주려 애썼지만 그러지 못했고, 깨어 있을 때의 슬픔이 그녀의 꿈의 일부가 되었다."]], [["Edith, sitting by, looked down at the dark eyelashes lying wet on the flushed cheeks, and looked with gentleness and pity, for she knew the truth.", "곁에 앉은 에디스는, 발그레한 뺨 위에 젖은 채 드리운 그 검은 속눈썹을 내려다보았다. 진실을 알고 있었기에, 그녀는 다정함과 연민으로 내려다보았다."], ["But no sleep hung upon her own eyes.", "그러나 그녀 자신의 눈에는 잠이 깃들지 않았다."], ["As the day came on she still sat watching and waking, with the placid hand in hers, and sometimes whispered, as she looked at the hushed face, “Be near me, Florence.", "날이 밝아 오는 동안에도 그녀는 그 평온한 손을 자기 손에 쥔 채 여전히 깨어 지켜보고 앉아 있었다. 그리고 이따금, 그 잠든 얼굴을 바라보며 속삭였다, \"내 곁에 있어 줘, 플로렌스."], ["I have no hope but in you!”", "나에겐 너 말고는 희망이 없어!\""]], [["# CHAPTER XLIV. A Separation", "# 제39장"]], [["With the day, though not so early as the sun, uprose Miss Susan Nipper.", "날이 밝아 오자, 비록 해만큼 이른 것은 아니었지만, 수전 닙퍼 양이 자리에서 일어났다."], ["There was a heaviness in this young maiden’s exceedingly sharp black eyes, that abated somewhat of their sparkling, and suggested—which was not their usual character—the possibility of their being sometimes shut.", "이 젊은 처녀의 유달리 날카로운 검은 두 눈에는 어딘가 무거운 기색이 어려 있어, 그 반짝임이 다소 누그러졌고, 또—평소의 그 눈답지 않게—이따금 감겨 있을 수도 있겠다는 가능성을 내비쳤다."], ["There was likewise a swollen look about them, as if they had been crying over-night.", "게다가 두 눈가가 부어 있어, 마치 간밤에 울기라도 한 것 같았다."], ["But the Nipper, so far from being cast down, was singularly brisk and bold, and all her energies appeared to be braced up for some great feat.", "그러나 닙퍼는 풀이 죽기는커녕 유난히 활기차고 대담했으며, 그녀의 온 기운이 어떤 큰일을 치르려고 단단히 벼려진 듯했다."], ["This was noticeable even in her dress, which was much more tight and trim than usual; and in occasional twitches of her head as she went about the house, which were mightily expressive of determination.", "이는 그녀의 옷차림에서도 드러났는데, 평소보다 훨씬 더 꼭 끼고 단정했다. 또 그녀가 집 안을 돌아다닐 때 이따금 머리를 홱홱 젖히는 모습에서도 드러났는데, 그것은 결연한 의지를 어마어마하게 잘 나타내고 있었다."]], [["In a word, she had formed a determination, and an aspiring one: it being nothing less than this—to penetrate to Mr Dombey’s presence, and have speech of that gentleman alone.", "한마디로, 그녀는 어떤 결심을, 그것도 야심 찬 결심을 굳혀 둔 터였다. 그 결심이란 다름이 아니라—돔비 씨 앞까지 뚫고 들어가, 그 신사와 단둘이 이야기를 나누겠다는 것이었다."], ["“I have often said I would,”", "\"그러겠다고 노상 말해 왔지,\""], ["she remarked, in a threatening manner, to herself, that morning, with many twitches of her head, “and now I will!”", "그녀는 그날 아침 머리를 여러 번 홱홱 젖히며 위협하는 투로 혼잣말을 했다, \"이제는 정말 해 보겠어!\""]], [["Spurring herself on to the accomplishment of this desperate design, with a sharpness that was peculiar to herself, Susan Nipper haunted the hall and staircase during the whole forenoon, without finding a favourable opportunity for the assault.", "이 필사적인 계획을 이루려고 스스로를 다그치며, 자기 특유의 날카로움으로 수전 닙퍼는 오전 내내 현관과 계단을 맴돌았으나, 돌격하기에 알맞은 기회를 찾지 못했다. 그러나 이 좌절에도 그녀는 조금도 꺾이지 않았으니, 그것은 오히려 자극이 되어 그녀의 투지를 돋우었다."], ["Not at all baffled by this discomfiture, which indeed had a stimulating effect, and put her on her mettle, she diminished nothing of her vigilance; and at last discovered, towards evening, that her sworn foe Mrs Pipchin, under pretence of having sat up all night, was dozing in her own room, and that Mr Dombey was lying on his sofa, unattended.", "그녀는 경계를 조금도 늦추지 않았고, 마침내 저녁 무렵에 알아냈다. 그녀의 철천지원수 핍친 부인이 밤새 깨어 있었노라는 핑계를 대고 자기 방에서 졸고 있다는 것을, 그리고 돔비 씨가 시중드는 이도 없이 소파에 누워 있다는 것을."]], [["With a twitch—not of her head merely, this time, but of her whole self—the Nipper went on tiptoe to Mr Dombey’s door, and knocked.", "이번에는 머리만이 아니라 온몸을 한 번 홱 떨고 나서, 닙퍼는 돔비 씨의 방문까지 발끝으로 다가가 노크했다."], ["“Come in!”", "\"들어오시오!\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."], ["Susan encouraged herself with a final twitch, and went in.", "수전은 마지막으로 한 번 더 몸을 떨며 스스로를 다독이고는 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["Mr Dombey, who was eyeing the fire, gave an amazed look at his visitor, and raised himself a little on his arm.", "난롯불을 바라보고 있던 돔비 씨는 방문객을 보고 깜짝 놀란 시선을 던지며, 팔을 짚고 몸을 조금 일으켰다."], ["The Nipper dropped a curtsey.", "닙퍼는 무릎을 굽혀 절을 했다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무슨 일인가?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“If you please, Sir, I wish to speak to you,”", "\"송구하오나, 나리, 나리께 드릴 말씀이 있사옵니다,\""], ["said Susan.", "수전이 말했다."]], [["Mr Dombey moved his lips as if he were repeating the words, but he seemed so lost in astonishment at the presumption of the young woman as to be incapable of giving them utterance.", "돔비 씨는 그 말을 되뇌듯 입술을 움직였으나, 이 젊은 여자의 주제넘음에 너무도 놀란 나머지 그 말을 소리 내어 입 밖에 낼 수가 없는 모양이었다."]], [["“I have been in your service, Sir,” said Susan Nipper, with her usual rapidity, “now twelve “year a waiting on Miss Floy my own young lady who couldn’t speak plain when I first come here and I was old in this house when Mrs Richards was new, I may not be Meethosalem, but I am not a child in arms.”", "\"제가 나리를 모신 지,\" 수전 닙퍼가 평소의 빠른 말투로 말했다, \"이제 십이 년이 되었습니다. 플로이 아가씨, 제 아가씨를 모셔 왔지요. 제가 이 댁에 처음 왔을 때 아가씨는 아직 말도 또렷이 못 하셨고, 리처즈 부인이 이 집에 갓 들어왔을 때 저는 이미 고참이었습니다. 제가 므두셀라는 아닐지 몰라도, 품에 안긴 갓난아이는 아니옵니다.\""]], [["Mr Dombey, raised upon his arm and looking at her, offered no comment on this preparatory statement of fact.", "* 므두셀라: 구약 성경에 나오는 가장 오래 산 인물. 돔비 씨는 팔을 짚고 몸을 일으킨 채 그녀를 바라보았으나, 이 예비 진술에 아무런 토를 달지 않았다."]], [["“There never was a dearer or a blesseder young lady than is my young lady, Sir,” said Susan, “and I ought to know a great deal better than some for I have seen her in her grief and I have seen her in her joy (there’s not been much of it) and I have seen her with her brother and I have seen her in her loneliness and some have never seen her, and I say to some and all—I do!” and here the black-eyed shook her head, and slightly stamped her foot; “that she’s the blessedest and dearest angel is Miss Floy that ever drew the breath of life, the more that I was torn to pieces Sir the more I’d say it though I may not be a Fox’s Martyr.”", "\"제 아가씨보다 더 사랑스럽고 더 복된 아가씨는 세상에 없었습니다, 나리,\" 수전이 말했다, \"그리고 저는 어떤 이들보다 훨씬 더 잘 알고 있어야 마땅하지요, 왜냐하면 저는 아가씨가 슬퍼하시는 것도 보았고 기뻐하시는 것도 보았으니까요(기뻐하실 일이 많지는 않았습니다만). 동생분과 함께 계신 것도 보았고 홀로 계신 것도 보았습니다. 어떤 이들은 아가씨를 도통 본 적도 없지요. 그래서 저는 누구에게든 말합니다—정말로요!\" 여기서 그 검은 눈의 처녀는 머리를 흔들고 발을 가볍게 굴렀다. \"플로이 아가씨야말로 이 세상에 숨을 쉰 이들 가운데 가장 복되고 가장 사랑스러운 천사라고요. 제가 갈가리 찢기는 한이 있어도 그렇게 말하겠습니다, 나리, 제가 폭스의 순교자는 못 될지언정 말입니다.\" * 폭스의 순교자: 존 폭스의 「순교자 열전」에 등장하는 신앙을 위해 목숨을 바친 인물들."]], [["Mr Dombey turned yet paler than his fall had made him, with indignation and astonishment; and kept his eyes upon the speaker as if he accused them, and his ears too, of playing him false.", "돔비 씨는 분노와 놀라움으로, 쓰러졌을 때보다 한층 더 창백해졌다. 그리고 자기 두 눈을, 또한 두 귀를, 자기를 속이는 것이라 탓하기라도 하듯 그 말하는 여자에게서 시선을 떼지 않았다."]], [["“No one could be anything but true and faithful to Miss Floy, Sir,”", "\"플로이 아가씨께 진실하고 충실하지 않을 사람은 아무도 없을 겁니다, 나리,\" 수전이 말을 이었다, \"그리고 저는 십이 년을 모셨다 해서 무슨 공을 내세우려는 것이 아닙니다."], ["pursued Susan, “and I take no merit for my service of twelve year, for I love her—yes, I say to some and all I do!”—and here the black-eyed shook her head again, and slightly stamped her foot again, and checked a sob; “but true and faithful service gives me right to speak I hope, and speak I must and will now, right or wrong.”", "제가 아가씨를 사랑하니까요—그래요, 저는 누구에게든 그렇게 말합니다!\"—여기서 그 검은 눈의 처녀는 다시 머리를 흔들고 다시 발을 가볍게 구르며 흐느낌을 삼켰다—\"하지만 진실하고 충실한 봉사가 제게 말할 권리를 준다고 생각합니다. 그러니 지금 옳든 그르든, 저는 말해야 하고 또 말하겠습니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean, woman?”", "\"무슨 소리냐, 이 여자야?\""], ["said Mr Dombey, glaring at her.", "돔비 씨가 그녀를 노려보며 말했다."], ["“How do you dare?”", "\"어찌 감히?\""]], [["“What I mean, Sir, is to speak respectful and without offence, but out, and how I dare I know not but I do!”", "\"제가 드리려는 말씀은, 나리, 공손하게, 모욕 없이, 그러나 거리낌 없이 드리는 것이옵니다. 어찌 감히 그러는지는 저도 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그렇게 합니다!\""], ["said Susan.", "수전이 말했다."], ["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["you don’t know my young lady Sir you don’t indeed, you’d never know so little of her, if you did.”", "나리께서는 제 아가씨를 모르십니다, 나리, 정말 모르세요. 아셨다면 결코 그렇게까지 모르실 리가 없지요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey, in a fury, put his hand out for the bell-rope; but there was no bell-rope on that side of the fire, and he could not rise and cross to the other without assistance.", "돔비 씨는 노발대발하여 종 끈을 잡으려고 손을 뻗었다. 그러나 난롯불 그쪽 편에는 종 끈이 없었고, 그는 부축 없이는 일어나 반대편으로 건너갈 수가 없었다."], ["The quick eye of the Nipper detected his helplessness immediately, and now, as she afterwards observed, she felt she had got him.", "닙퍼의 재빠른 눈은 그의 무력함을 즉시 알아챘고, 나중에 그녀가 술회한 대로, 이제 그를 손아귀에 넣었다는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["“Miss Floy,”", "\"플로이 아가씨는,\""], ["said Susan Nipper, “is the most devoted and most patient and most dutiful and beautiful of daughters, there ain’t no gentleman, no Sir, though as great and rich as all the greatest and richest of England put together, but might be proud of her and would and ought.", "수전 닙퍼가 말했다, \"가장 헌신적이고 가장 인내심 많고 가장 효성스럽고 아름다운 따님이십니다. 영국에서 가장 위대하고 가장 부유한 이들을 다 합친 것만큼 위대하고 부유한 신사라 할지라도, 그 어떤 신사도, 그 누구도, 아가씨를 자랑스러워하지 않을 수가 없고, 또 자랑스러워하는 것이 마땅합니다."], ["If he knew her value right, he’d rather lose his greatness and his fortune piece by piece and beg his way in rags from door to door, I say to some and all, he would!”", "그가 아가씨의 가치를 제대로 안다면, 차라리 자기 위엄과 재산을 한 조각씩 잃고 누더기를 걸친 채 이 집 저 집 빌어먹는 한이 있더라도—누구에게든 말하지만, 정말 그럴 겁니다!\""], ["cried Susan Nipper, bursting into tears, “than bring the sorrow on her tender heart that I have seen it suffer in this house!”", "수전 닙퍼가 울음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"이 집에서 제가 그토록 고통받는 것을 보아 온 그 여린 가슴에 슬픔을 안겨 주느니 그렇게 하겠지요!\""]], [["“Woman,”", "\"여자야,\""], ["cried Mr Dombey, “leave the room.”", "돔비 씨가 외쳤다, \"방에서 나가라.\""]], [["“Begging your pardon, not even if I am to leave the situation, Sir,”", "\"송구하오나, 나리, 설령 일자리를 떠나야 한다 해도 나가지 않겠습니다,\""], ["replied the steadfast Nipper, “in which I have been so many years and seen so much—although I hope you’d never have the heart to send me from Miss Floy for such a cause—will I go now till I have said the rest, I may not be a Indian widow Sir and I am not and I would not so become but if I once made up my mind to burn myself alive, I’d do it!", "흔들림 없는 닙퍼가 대답했다, \"제가 그토록 오랜 세월을 보내고 그토록 많은 것을 보아 온 이 자리를요—나리께서 그런 이유로 저를 플로이 아가씨 곁에서 내쫓으실 만큼 매정하지는 않으시리라 바라옵니다만—나머지 할 말을 다 할 때까지는 지금 나가지 않겠습니다. 저는 인도의 과부가 아니고, 그렇지도 않고, 그렇게 되고 싶지도 않습니다만, 일단 제 몸을 산 채로 태우겠다고 마음을 먹는다면, 정말 그렇게 할 겁니다!"], ["And I’ve made my mind up to go on.”", "그리고 저는 끝까지 말하기로 마음을 먹었습니다.\""]], [["Which was rendered no less clear by the expression of Susan Nipper’s countenance, than by her words.", "* 인도의 과부: 남편의 화장 장작더미에 함께 몸을 던져 순사하는 인도의 풍습(수티)을 따른 여인."]], [["“There ain’t a person in your service, Sir,” pursued the black-eyed, “that has always stood more in awe of you than me and you may think how true it is when I make so bold as say that I have hundreds and hundreds of times thought of speaking to you and never been able to make my mind up to it till last night, but last night decided of me.”", "그 말이 사실임은, 수전 닙퍼의 말 못지않게 그녀의 얼굴 표정으로도 또렷이 드러났다. \"나리를 모시는 이들 가운데,\" 그 검은 눈의 처녀가 말을 이었다, \"저보다 더 나리를 두려워해 온 사람은 없습니다. 그것이 얼마나 진실인지는, 제가 감히 이런 말씀을 드리는 것을 보시면 아실 겁니다. 저는 나리께 말씀드리려고 수백 번도 더 생각했지만, 어젯밤까지는 도무지 마음을 굳히지 못했습니다. 하지만 어젯밤이 저를 결심하게 만들었지요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey, in a paroxysm of rage, made another grasp at the bell-rope that was not there, and, in its absence, pulled his hair rather than nothing.", "돔비 씨는 격분의 발작 속에서, 그 자리에 있지도 않은 종 끈을 다시 한번 움켜잡으려 했고, 그것이 없으니 차라리 자기 머리카락을 쥐어뜯었다."]], [["“I have seen,” said Susan Nipper, “Miss Floy strive and strive when nothing but a child so sweet and patient that the best of women might have copied from her, I’ve seen her sitting nights together half the night through to help her delicate brother with his learning, I’ve seen her helping him and watching him at other times—some well know when—I’ve seen her, with no encouragement and no help, grow up to be a lady, thank God! that is the grace and pride of every company she goes in, and I’ve always seen her cruelly neglected and keenly feeling of it—I say to some and all, I have!—and never said one word, but ordering one’s self lowly and reverently towards one’s betters, is not to be a worshipper of graven images, and I will and must speak!”", "\"저는 보았습니다,\" 수전 닙퍼가 말했다, \"플로이 아가씨가 어렸을 적, 그토록 상냥하고 인내심 깊어 가장 훌륭한 여인들도 본받을 만한 그 어린것이 애쓰고 또 애쓰는 것을요. 여린 동생분의 공부를 돕느라 밤마다 거의 한밤중까지 앉아 계신 것을 보았고, 다른 때에도 동생분을 돕고 지켜보시는 것을 보았습니다—누구는 잘 알지요 언제인지를—저는 보았습니다, 격려도 도움도 없이, 아가씨가 자라나 숙녀가 되시는 것을요, 고맙게도! 어느 모임에 가든 그 모임의 품격이요 자랑이 되는 분으로 말입니다. 그리고 저는 늘 보았습니다, 아가씨가 모질게 외면당하면서도 그것을 뼈저리게 느끼시는 것을요—누구에게든 말하지만, 정말 그랬습니다!—그러면서도 한마디도 하지 않았지요. 하지만 윗사람에게 겸손하고 공손히 처신한다는 것이 새긴 우상을 숭배하는 것은 아니지 않습니까. 그러니 저는 말하겠고, 또 말해야만 합니다!\""]], [["“Is there anybody there?”", "\"거기 누구 없느냐?\""], ["cried Mr Dombey, calling out.", "돔비 씨가 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Where are the men?", "\"사내들은 어디 있느냐?"], ["where are the women?", "여자들은 어디 있느냐?"], ["Is there no one there?”", "거기 아무도 없느냐?\""]], [["“I left my dear young lady out of bed late last night,”", "\"저는 간밤 늦도록 제 사랑하는 아가씨를 잠자리에 들지 못하게 두었습니다,\" 수전이 조금도 멈추지 않고 말했다, \"그리고 저는 그 까닭을 알지요, 나리께서 편찮으셨고 아가씨는 얼마나 편찮으신지 모르셨으니까요."], ["said Susan, nothing checked, “and I knew why, for you was ill Sir and she didn’t know how ill and that was enough to make her wretched as I saw it did.", "그것만으로도 아가씨를 비참하게 만들기에 충분했고, 저는 정말 그러신 것을 보았습니다. 저는 공작새는 아닐지언정, 두 눈은 있습니다—그래서 저는 제 방에서 잠시 앉아 있었지요."], ["I may not be a Peacock; but I have my eyes—and I sat up a little in my own room thinking she might be lonesome and might want me, and I saw her steal downstairs and come to this door as if it was a guilty thing to look at her own Pa, and then steal back again and go into them lonely drawing-rooms, a-crying so, that I could hardly bear to hear it.", "아가씨가 외로워하시며 저를 찾으실지도 모른다는 생각에서요. 그리고 저는 아가씨가 살그머니 아래층으로 내려와, 마치 친아버지를 바라보는 것이 죄라도 되는 양 이 방문 앞에 왔다가, 다시 살그머니 돌아가 저 적막한 응접실로 들어가서는, 차마 들을 수가 없을 만큼 울고 또 우시는 것을 보았습니다."], ["I can not bear to hear it,”", "저는 그것을 차마 들을 수가 없습니다,\""], ["said Susan Nipper, wiping her black eyes, and fixing them undauntingly on Mr Dombey’s infuriated face.", "수전 닙퍼가 검은 눈을 훔치며, 돔비 씨의 격노한 얼굴에 그 눈을 굽힘 없이 고정한 채 말했다. \"그 소리를 들은 것이 이번이 처음도 아닙니다, 헤아릴 수 없이 여러 번 들었지요."], ["“It’s not the first time I have heard it, not by many and many a time you don’t know your own daughter, Sir, you don’t know what you’re doing, Sir, I say to some and all,”", "나리께서는 친따님을 모르십니다, 나리. 나리께서는 지금 무슨 짓을 하고 계신지 모르십니다, 나리."], ["cried Susan Nipper, in a final burst, “that it’s a sinful shame!”", "누구에게든 말하지만,\" 수전 닙퍼가 마지막으로 폭발하며 외쳤다, \"이것은 죄스러운 수치입니다!\""]], [["“Why, hoity toity!”", "\"아니, 이런 발칙한 것 봤나!\""], ["cried the voice of Mrs Pipchin, as the black bombazeen garments of that fair Peruvian Miner swept into the room.", "핍친 부인의 목소리가 외쳤다. 동시에 그 어여쁜 페루 광산 마님의 검은 봄바진 옷자락이 방 안으로 휩쓸려 들어왔다."], ["“What’s this, indeed?”", "\"이게 대체 무슨 일이람?\""]], [["Susan favoured Mrs Pipchin with a look she had invented expressly for her when they first became acquainted, and resigned the reply to Mr Dombey.", "* 봄바진: 견사와 모직을 섞어 짠 검은빛 천으로, 흔히 상복에 쓰였다. 수전은 핍친 부인을 위해, 둘이 처음 알게 되었을 때 그녀에게만 쓰려고 따로 고안해 둔 눈빛을 베풀어 주고는, 대답할 일은 돔비 씨에게 넘겼다."]], [["“What’s this?”", "\"이게 무슨 일이냐고?\""], ["repeated Mr Dombey, almost foaming.", "돔비 씨가 거의 거품을 물고 되뇌었다."], ["“What’s this, Madam?", "\"이게 무슨 일이냐, 부인?"], ["You who are at the head of this household, and bound to keep it in order, have reason to inquire.", "이 집안을 통솔하며 질서를 유지할 의무를 진 당신이야말로 물어볼 자격이 있겠지."], ["Do you know this woman?”", "이 여자를 아시오?\""]], [["“I know very little good of her, Sir,”", "\"저 애에 대해서는 좋은 점이라곤 거의 모릅니다, 나리,\""], ["croaked Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 쉰 목소리로 말했다."], ["“How dare you come here, you hussy?", "\"어찌 감히 여기 왔느냐, 이 계집애야?"], ["Go along with you!”", "썩 물러가지 못해!\""]], [["But the inflexible Nipper, merely honouring Mrs Pipchin with another look, remained.", "그러나 굽힐 줄 모르는 닙퍼는, 핍친 부인에게 또 한 번 눈길을 베풀어 줄 뿐, 그대로 머물러 있었다."]], [["“Do you call it managing this establishment, Madam,”", "\"이 집안을 관리한다는 게 고작 이런 거요, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “to leave a person like this at liberty to come and talk to me!", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"이런 자가 멋대로 와서 내게 말을 늘어놓게 내버려 두는 것이?"], ["A gentleman—in his own house—in his own room—assailed with the impertinences of women-servants!”", "신사가—제 집에서—제 방에서—하녀들의 무례에 시달리다니!\""]], [["“Well, Sir,”", "\"글쎄요, 나리,\""], ["returned Mrs Pipchin, with vengeance in her hard grey eye, “I exceedingly deplore it; nothing can be more irregular; nothing can be more out of all bounds and reason; but I regret to say, Sir, that this young woman is quite beyond control.", "핍친 부인이 그 단단한 잿빛 눈에 앙심을 담고 응수했다, \"저로서도 지극히 통탄스럽습니다. 이보다 더 도리에 어긋난 일도 없고, 이보다 더 분수와 이치를 벗어난 일도 없지요."], ["She has been spoiled by Miss Dombey, and is amenable to nobody.", "하지만 송구하게도 말씀드리자면, 나리, 이 젊은 계집은 도무지 통제가 안 됩니다. 돔비 아가씨가 버릇을 잘못 들여서, 그 누구의 말도 듣지 않습니다."], ["You know you’re not,”", "너도 알지, 안 그러냐,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, sharply, and shaking her head at Susan Nipper.", "핍친 부인이 날카롭게, 수전 닙퍼를 향해 머리를 흔들며 말했다."], ["“For shame, you hussy!", "\"부끄러운 줄 알아라, 이 계집애야!"], ["Go along with you!”", "썩 물러가!\""]], [["“If you find people in my service who are not to be controlled, Mrs Pipchin,”", "\"내 밑에 통제할 수 없는 자들이 있다면, 핍친 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, turning back towards the fire, “you know what to do with them, I presume.", "돔비 씨가 다시 난롯불 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다, \"그런 자들을 어떻게 처리해야 할지는 당신이 알 거라 짐작하오."], ["You know what you are here for?", "당신이 여기 무엇 때문에 있는지는 아시겠지?"], ["Take her away!”", "데리고 나가시오!\""]], [["“Sir, I know what to do,”", "\"나리, 저도 어찌해야 할지 압니다,\""], ["retorted Mrs Pipchin, “and of course shall do it.", "핍친 부인이 받아쳤다, \"물론 그리하겠습니다."], ["Susan Nipper,”", "수전 닙퍼,\""], ["snapping her up particularly short, “a month’s warning from this hour.”", "그녀는 유난히 매몰차게 그녀의 말을 끊었다, \"지금 이 시각부터 한 달 뒤에 그만두는 거다.\""]], [["“Oh indeed!”", "\"오, 그러시겠다고요!\""], ["cried Susan, loftily.", "수전이 거만하게 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["returned Mrs Pipchin, “and don’t smile at me, you minx, or I’ll know the reason why!", "핍친 부인이 응수했다, \"그리고 내게 웃지 마라, 이 깍쟁이야, 안 그러면 무슨 영문인지 내가 알아낼 테니!"], ["Go along with you this minute!”", "당장 물러가지 못해!\""]], [["“I intend to go this minute, you may rely upon it,”", "\"당장 나가려던 참이에요, 그건 믿으셔도 됩니다,\""], ["said the voluble Nipper.", "수다스러운 닙퍼가 말했다."], ["“I have been in this house waiting on my young lady a dozen year and I won’t stop in it one hour under notice from a person owning to the name of Pipchin trust me, Mrs P.”", "\"저는 이 집에서 제 아가씨를 모신 지 십이 년인데, 핍친이라는 이름을 가진 사람이 통보했다고 해서 단 한 시간도 더 머물지 않을 겁니다, 그건 믿으세요, 핍친 여사님.\""]], [["“A good riddance of bad rubbish!”", "\"못된 쓰레기 잘 치웠다!\""], ["said that wrathful old lady.", "그 노발대발한 노부인이 말했다."], ["“Get along with you, or I’ll have you carried out!”", "\"냉큼 나가, 안 그러면 끌어내게 할 테니!\""]], [["“My comfort is,”", "\"제게 위안이 되는 건,\""], ["said Susan, looking back at Mr Dombey, “that I have told a piece of truth this day which ought to have been told long before and can’t be told too often or too plain and that no amount of Pipchinses—I hope the number of ’em mayn’t be great”", "수전이 돔비 씨를 돌아보며 말했다, \"오늘 제가 진작 했어야 마땅하고 아무리 자주, 아무리 분명하게 해도 지나치지 않을 한 가지 진실을 말했다는 거예요."], ["(here Mrs Pipchin uttered a very sharp “Go along with you!”", "그리고 어떤 핍친들도—그 수가 부디 많지 않기를 바랍니다만\""], ["and Miss Nipper repeated the look) “can unsay what I have said, though they gave a whole year full of warnings beginning at ten o’clock in the forenoon and never leaving off till twelve at night and died of the exhaustion which would be a Jubilee!”", "(여기서 핍친 부인이 매우 날카롭게 \"썩 물러가!\"라고 내뱉었고, 닙퍼 양은 그 눈빛을 되풀이했다) \"제가 한 말을 거두게 할 수는 없을 거예요, 설령 그들이 오전 열 시에 시작해서 밤 열두 시까지 한순간도 쉬지 않고 통보를 해 대다가 기진맥진해 죽는 일이 일 년 내내 이어진다 해도 말이죠—그렇게만 된다면 정말 경사일 텐데요!\""]], [["With these words, Miss Nipper preceded her foe out of the room; and walking upstairs to her own apartments in great state, to the choking exasperation of the ireful Pipchin, sat down among her boxes and began to cry.", "이 말과 함께, 닙퍼 양은 자기 원수를 앞세워 방에서 나갔다. 그리고 분기탱천한 핍친의 숨 막히는 분노를 뒤로하고, 더없이 위풍당당하게 자기 방으로 올라가, 자기 짐 상자들 사이에 주저앉아 울기 시작했다."]], [["From this soft mood she was soon aroused, with a very wholesome and refreshing effect, by the voice of Mrs Pipchin outside the door.", "이 가냘픈 심사에서 그녀는, 매우 건전하고 상쾌한 효과와 함께, 문밖에서 들려온 핍친 부인의 목소리에 곧 깨어났다."]], [["“Does that bold-faced slut,”", "\"저 뻔뻔스러운 계집이,\""], ["said the fell Pipchin, “intend to take her warning, or does she not?”", "흉포한 핍친이 말했다, \"통보를 받아들이겠다는 거냐, 아니냐?\""]], [["Miss Nipper replied from within that the person described did not inhabit that part of the house, but that her name was Pipchin, and she was to be found in the housekeeper’s room.", "닙퍼 양은 안에서 대답하기를, 방금 말한 그런 사람은 이쪽 부분에 살지 않으며, 그 이름은 핍친이고, 가정부 방에 가면 찾을 수 있을 거라고 했다."]], [["“You saucy baggage!”", "\"이 건방진 화냥년!\""], ["retorted Mrs Pipchin, rattling at the handle of the door.", "핍친 부인이 문 손잡이를 덜컹덜컹 흔들며 받아쳤다."], ["“Go along with you this minute.", "\"당장 물러가. 짐을 즉시 싸!"], ["Pack up your things directly! How dare you talk in this way to a gentle-woman who has seen better days?”", "어떻게 감히 옛날에는 더 좋은 시절을 보냈던 양가집 여인에게 이런 식으로 말을 해?\""]], [["To which Miss Nipper rejoined from her castle, that she pitied the better days that had seen Mrs Pipchin; and that for her part she considered the worst days in the year to be about that lady’s mark, except that they were much too good for her.", "이에 닙퍼 양은 자기 성채 안에서 대꾸하기를, 핍친 부인을 겪어야 했던 그 좋은 시절이 가엾기 짝이 없다고, 그리고 자기로 말할 것 같으면 일 년 중 가장 나쁜 날이 저 마님 수준에 딱 맞다고 생각한다고, 다만 그런 날조차 저 마님에게는 너무 과분하다고 했다."]], [["“But you needn’t trouble yourself to make a noise at my door,”", "\"하지만 굳이 수고스럽게 제 방문 앞에서 소란을 피우실 것도,\""], ["said Susan Nipper, “nor to contaminate the key-hole with your eye, I’m packing up and going you may take your affidavit.”", "수전 닙퍼가 말했다, \"그 눈으로 열쇠 구멍을 더럽히실 것도 없어요. 저는 짐을 싸서 나갈 테니까요, 맹세하셔도 좋아요.\""]], [["The Dowager expressed her lively satisfaction at this intelligence, and with some general opinions upon young hussies as a race, and especially upon their demerits after being spoiled by Miss Dombey, withdrew to prepare the Nipper’s wages.", "이 소식에 노마님은 활기찬 만족을 표하고는, 젊은 계집들이라는 족속 일반에 대한, 특히 돔비 아가씨에게 버릇이 잘못 들여진 뒤의 그들의 결함에 대한 몇 가지 일반론을 늘어놓은 다음, 닙퍼의 품삯을 마련하러 물러갔다."], ["Susan then bestirred herself to get her trunks in order, that she might take an immediate and dignified departure; sobbing heartily all the time, as she thought of Florence.", "그러자 수전은 즉각 위엄 있게 떠날 수 있도록 짐 상자들을 정돈하느라 분주히 움직였다. 그러면서 내내, 플로렌스를 생각하며 진심으로 흐느꼈다."]], [["The object of her regret was not long in coming to her, for the news soon spread over the house that Susan Nipper had had a disturbance with Mrs Pipchin, and that they had both appealed to Mr Dombey, and that there had been an unprecedented piece of work in Mr Dombey’s room, and that Susan was going.", "그녀가 애석해하는 바로 그 대상은 오래지 않아 그녀에게 찾아왔다. 수전 닙퍼가 핍친 부인과 한바탕 소동을 벌였다는 것, 둘 다 돔비 씨에게 호소했다는 것, 돔비 씨 방에서 전례 없는 일이 벌어졌다는 것, 그리고 수전이 떠난다는 것이 곧 온 집안에 퍼졌기 때문이다."], ["The latter part of this confused rumour, Florence found to be so correct, that Susan had locked the last trunk and was sitting upon it with her bonnet on, when she came into her room.", "이 어수선한 소문의 뒷부분이 정확하다는 것을 플로렌스는 알게 되었으니, 그녀가 방에 들어섰을 때 수전은 마지막 짐 상자를 잠그고 보닛을 쓴 채 그 위에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Susan!”", "\"수전!\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."], ["“Going to leave me!", "\"나를 떠난다고!"], ["You!”", "네가!\""]], [["“Oh for goodness gracious sake, Miss Floy,”", "\"오, 제발 부탁이에요, 플로이 아가씨,\""], ["said Susan, sobbing, “don’t speak a word to me or I shall demean myself before them Pi-i-pchinses, and I wouldn’t have ’em see me cry Miss Floy for worlds!”", "수전이 흐느끼며 말했다, \"제게 한마디도 하지 마세요, 안 그러면 저 핍-친들 앞에서 추태를 부리고 말 거예요. 무슨 일이 있어도 그들에게 제가 우는 꼴은 보이고 싶지 않다고요, 플로이 아가씨!\""]], [["“Susan!”", "\"수전!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“My dear girl, my old friend!", "\"내 사랑하는 아이, 내 오랜 벗아!"], ["What shall I do without you!", "너 없이 나는 어떻게 하니!"], ["Can you bear to go away so?”", "어떻게 그렇게 떠날 수가 있어?\""]], [["“No-n-o-o, my darling dear Miss Floy, I can’t indeed,”", "\"못, 못, 못 해요, 제 사랑하는 플로이 아가씨, 정말 못 합니다,\""], ["sobbed Susan.", "수전이 흐느꼈다."], ["“But it can’t be helped, I’ve done my duty, Miss, I have indeed.", "\"하지만 어쩔 도리가 없어요. 저는 제 도리를 다했어요, 아가씨, 정말로요."], ["It’s no fault of mine.", "제 잘못이 아니랍니다."], ["I am quite resigned.", "저는 다 체념했어요."], ["I couldn’t stay my month or I could never leave you then my darling and I must at last as well as at first, don’t speak to me Miss Floy, for though I’m pretty firm I’m not a marble doorpost, my own dear.”", "한 달을 더 머물 수가 없어요, 안 그러면 그때 가서는 도저히 사랑하는 아가씨를 떠날 수 없을 테니까요. 처음에나 마지막에나 어차피 떠나야 한다면, 제게 말을 걸지 마세요, 플로이 아가씨, 제가 제법 굳세긴 해도 대리석 문기둥은 아니거든요, 사랑하는 아가씨.\""]], [["“What is it?", "\"무슨 일이니?"], ["Why is it?”", "왜 그래?\""], ["said Florence, “Won’t you tell me?”", "플로렌스가 말했다. \"내게 말해 주지 않을 거야?\""], ["For Susan was shaking her head.", "수전이 머리를 흔들고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“No-n-no, my darling,”", "\"안, 안 돼요, 사랑하는 아가씨,\" 수전이 대답했다."], ["returned Susan.", "\"묻지 마세요, 저는 말씀드릴 수 없으니까요."], ["“Don’t ask me, for I mustn’t, and whatever you do don’t put in a word for me to stop, for it couldn’t be and you’d only wrong yourself, and so God bless you my own precious and forgive me any harm I have done, or any temper I have showed in all these many years!”", "그리고 무슨 일이 있어도 저를 붙들겠다는 말씀은 하지 마세요, 그럴 수가 없고 아가씨 스스로를 그르치실 뿐이니까요. 그러니 부디 신의 가호가 있기를, 제 소중한 아가씨, 그리고 이 오랜 세월 동안 제가 끼친 어떤 해악이든, 제가 부린 어떤 성깔이든 용서해 주세요!\""]], [["With which entreaty, very heartily delivered, Susan hugged her mistress in her arms.", "이 간청을 진심으로 토해 내며, 수전은 두 팔로 자기 아가씨를 끌어안았다."]], [["“My darling there’s a many that may come to serve you and be glad to serve you and who’ll serve you well and true,”", "\"사랑하는 아가씨, 아가씨를 모시러 올 사람도, 기꺼이 모실 사람도, 또 잘되고 진실하게 모실 사람도 많을 거예요,\""], ["said Susan, “but there can’t be one who’ll serve you so affectionate as me or love you half as dearly, that’s my comfort.", "수전이 말했다, \"하지만 저처럼 다정히 모시거나 저만큼 절반이라도 애틋이 사랑할 사람은 없을 거예요, 그게 제 위안이랍니다."], ["Go-ood-bye, sweet Miss Floy!”", "안, 안녕히, 어여쁜 플로이 아가씨!\""]], [["“Where will you go, Susan?”", "\"어디로 갈 거니, 수전?\""], ["asked her weeping mistress.", "눈물짓는 아가씨가 물었다."]], [["“I’ve got a brother down in the country Miss—a farmer in Essex,”", "\"시골에 오빠가 하나 있어요, 아가씨—에식스에서 농사를 짓지요,\" 가슴이 미어지는 닙퍼가 말했다, \"소도, 돼지도 아주 많이 기른답니다."], ["said the heart-broken Nipper, “that keeps ever so many co-o-ows and pigs and I shall go down there by the coach and sto-op with him, and don’t mind me, for I’ve got money in the Savings Banks my dear, and needn’t take another service just yet, which I couldn’t, couldn’t, couldn’t do, my heart’s own mistress!”", "저는 거기 마차를 타고 내려가 오빠랑 지낼 거예요. 저는 신경 쓰지 마세요, 저축은행에 돈도 좀 있으니까 당분간은 다른 일자리를 구할 필요도 없어요, 그럴 수도, 그럴 수도, 그럴 수도 없고요, 제 마음의 주인이신 아가씨!\""], ["Susan finished with a burst of sorrow, which was opportunely broken by the voice of Mrs Pipchin talking downstairs; on hearing which, she dried her red and swollen eyes, and made a melancholy feint of calling jauntily to Mr Towlinson to fetch a cab and carry down her boxes.", "수전은 슬픔의 폭발로 말을 맺었는데, 마침 아래층에서 핍친 부인이 말하는 목소리가 들려 그 슬픔이 알맞게 끊겼다. 그 소리를 듣자 그녀는 붉게 부어오른 눈을 닦고, 토울린슨 씨에게 마차를 불러 짐 상자들을 내려 달라고 짐짓 쾌활한 척 부르는 시늉을 우울하게 해 보였다."]], [["Florence, pale and hurried and distressed, but withheld from useless interference even here, by her dread of causing any new division between her father and his wife (whose stern, indignant face had been a warning to her a few moments since), and by her apprehension of being in some way unconsciously connected already with the dismissal of her old servant and friend, followed, weeping, downstairs to Edith’s dressing-room, whither Susan betook herself to make her parting curtsey.", "플로렌스는 창백하고 황급하고 괴로워하면서도, 아버지와 그 부인 사이에 새로운 불화를 일으킬까 하는 두려움에(몇 분 전 그 부인의 엄하고 분노한 얼굴이 그녀에게 경고가 되었다), 또 자기가 어떤 식으로든 자기도 모르는 새 이미 오랜 하녀이자 벗의 해고에 얽혀 있는 것은 아닐까 하는 우려에, 여기서마저 쓸데없이 끼어드는 것을 삼간 채, 울면서 아래층 이디스의 화장실로 따라 내려갔다. 수전이 작별 인사로 무릎을 굽혀 절을 하려고 그리로 간 것이었다."]], [["“Now, here’s the cab, and here’s the boxes, get along with you, do!”", "\"자, 마차도 왔고, 짐 상자도 여기 있으니, 어서 물러가지 못해!\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, presenting herself at the same moment.", "마침 그 순간 모습을 드러내며 핍친 부인이 말했다."], ["“I beg your pardon, Ma’am, but Mr Dombey’s orders are imperative.”", "\"송구합니다, 마님, 그러나 돔비 씨의 명령이 지엄하셔서요.\""]], [["Edith, sitting under the hands of her maid—she was going out to dinner—preserved her haughty face, and took not the least notice.", "이디스는 하녀의 손길에 몸을 맡긴 채—그녀는 만찬에 나가려던 참이었다—오만한 얼굴을 그대로 지키며 조금도 아랑곳하지 않았다."]], [["“There’s your money,”", "\"여기 네 품삯이다,\" 핍친 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs Pipchin, who, in pursuance of her system, and in recollection of the Mines, was accustomed to rout the servants about, as she had routed her young Brighton boarders; to the everlasting acidulation of Master Bitherstone, “and the sooner this house sees your back the better.”", "그녀는 자기 방식에 따라, 또 광산 시절을 떠올리며, 하인들을 브라이턴의 어린 하숙생들을 닦달하던 식으로 닦달하는 데 익숙해져 있었으니, 그 탓에 비더스톤 도련님은 영영 시큰둥해지고 말았던 것이다. \"그리고 이 집이 네 뒷모습을 보는 게 빠르면 빠를수록 좋겠구나.\""]], [["Susan had no spirits even for the look that belonged to Ma Pipchin by right; so she dropped her curtsey to Mrs Dombey (who inclined her head without one word, and whose eye avoided everyone but Florence), and gave one last parting hug to her young mistress, and received her parting embrace in return.", "수전은 마 핍친에게 마땅히 베풀어 주어야 할 그 눈빛조차 보낼 기운이 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 돔비 부인에게 무릎을 굽혀 절을 하고(부인은 한마디 말도 없이 고개를 끄덕였고, 그 눈은 플로렌스 외에는 누구도 피했다), 자기 어린 아가씨에게 마지막 작별의 포옹을 한 번 안기고, 그 답례로 마지막 포옹을 받았다."], ["Poor Susan’s face at this crisis, in the intensity of her feelings and the determined suffocation of her sobs, lest one should become audible and be a triumph to Mrs Pipchin, presented a series of the most extraordinary physiognomical phenomena ever witnessed.", "이 위기의 순간, 가엾은 수전의 얼굴은, 그 감정의 격렬함과, 흐느낌 소리 하나라도 새어 나가 핍친 부인에게 승리감을 안겨 줄세라 기를 쓰고 그것을 틀어막는 통에, 일찍이 목격된 적 없는 가장 기상천외한 일련의 관상학적 현상을 펼쳐 보였다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Miss, I’m sure,”", "\"송구합니다, 아가씨, 정말로요,\""], ["said Towlinson, outside the door with the boxes, addressing Florence, “but Mr Toots is in the drawing-room, and sends his compliments, and begs to know how Diogenes and Master is.”", "짐 상자들과 함께 문밖에 있던 토울린슨이 플로렌스에게 말을 건넸다, \"하지만 투츠 도련님이 응접실에 와 계시는데, 안부를 전하시며 디오게네스와 도련님이 어찌 지내시는지 여쭙고 싶다 하십니다.\""]], [["Quick as thought, Florence glided out and hastened downstairs, where Mr Toots, in the most splendid vestments, was breathing very hard with doubt and agitation on the subject of her coming.", "생각만큼이나 잽싸게, 플로렌스는 슬며시 빠져나와 아래층으로 서둘러 내려갔다. 거기에는 더없이 화려한 옷을 차려입은 투츠 씨가, 그녀가 올지 어떨지 마음을 졸이며 몹시 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬고 있었다."]], [["“Oh, how de do, Miss Dombey,”", "\"오, 안녕하세요, 돔비 아가씨,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “God bless my soul!”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"맙소사!\""]], [["This last ejaculation was occasioned by Mr Toots’s deep concern at the distress he saw in Florence’s face; which caused him to stop short in a fit of chuckles, and become an image of despair.", "이 마지막 외침은, 플로렌스의 얼굴에 어린 괴로움을 보고 깊이 염려한 투츠 씨에게서 비롯된 것이었다. 그 바람에 그는 한바탕 킬킬거리려던 것을 뚝 멈추고, 절망의 화신이 되고 말았다."]], [["“Dear Mr Toots,”", "\"투츠 씨,\""], ["said Florence, “you are so friendly to me, and so honest, that I am sure I may ask a favour of you.”", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"당신은 제게 참으로 다정하고 참으로 정직하셔서, 제가 부탁 하나쯤 드려도 되겠다 싶어요.\""]], [["“Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 아가씨,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, “if you’ll only name one, you’ll—you’ll give me an appetite.", "투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"부디 하나만 말씀해 주시면, 그것이—그것이 제게 식욕을 돋워 줄 겁니다."], ["To which,”", "그 식욕이라는 것을,\""], ["said Mr Toots, with some sentiment, “I have long been a stranger.”", "투츠 씨가 약간의 감회를 담아 말했다, \"저는 오래도록 낯설게 지내 왔답니다.\""]], [["“Susan, who is an old friend of mine, the oldest friend I have,”", "\"제 오랜 벗인 수전이, 제가 둔 가장 오랜 벗인 수전이,\""], ["said Florence, “is about to leave here suddenly, and quite alone, poor girl.", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"갑자기 이 집을 떠나게 되었어요, 그것도 외톨이로, 가엾은 아이가요."], ["She is going home, a little way into the country.", "집으로 돌아가는데, 시골로 좀 들어가는 거예요."], ["Might I ask you to take care of her until she is in the coach?”", "그 애가 마차에 탈 때까지 보살펴 주시기를 부탁드려도 될까요?\""]], [["“Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 아가씨,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, “you really do me an honour and a kindness.", "투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"참으로 제게 영광이고 은혜로운 일입니다."], ["This proof of your confidence, after the manner in which I was Beast enough to conduct myself at Brighton—”", "제가 브라이턴에서 그토록 짐승처럼 굴었는데도 이렇게 저를 믿어 주시다니—\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Florence, hurriedly—“no—don’t think of that.", "플로렌스가 황급히 말했다—\"아니에요—그 일은 생각지 마세요."], ["Then would you have the kindness to—to go?", "그럼 친절을 베푸셔서—가 주시겠어요?"], ["and to be ready to meet her when she comes out?", "그리고 그 애가 나오면 맞이할 준비를 하고 계셔 주시겠어요?"], ["Thank you a thousand times!", "천번만번 고맙습니다!"], ["You ease my mind so much.", "덕분에 제 마음이 한결 놓여요."], ["She doesn’t seem so desolate.", "그 애가 그렇게까지 외롭지는 않겠지요."], ["You cannot think how grateful I feel to you, or what a good friend I am sure you are!”", "제가 당신께 얼마나 고마운지, 당신이 얼마나 좋은 벗인지, 짐작도 못 하실 거예요!\""], ["and Florence in her earnestness thanked him again and again; and Mr Toots, in his earnestness, hurried away—but backwards, that he might lose no glimpse of her.", "플로렌스는 간절한 마음으로 그에게 거듭거듭 고마워했다. 그러자 투츠 씨도 간절한 마음으로 서둘러 자리를 떴다—다만 뒷걸음으로, 그녀의 모습을 한순간도 놓치지 않으려고 그렇게 했다."]], [["Florence had not the courage to go out, when she saw poor Susan in the hall, with Mrs Pipchin driving her forth, and Diogenes jumping about her, and terrifying Mrs Pipchin to the last degree by making snaps at her bombazeen skirts, and howling with anguish at the sound of her voice—for the good duenna was the dearest and most cherished aversion of his breast.", "핍친 부인이 가엾은 수전을 몰아내고, 디오게네스가 그녀 주위를 깡충거리며 그 봄바진 치맛자락에 덥석 물어 대는 통에 핍친 부인을 극도로 질겁하게 만들고, 그녀의 목소리만 들려도 괴로워 울부짖는—그 선량한 시중꾼 노파야말로 디오게네스의 가슴속에서 가장 애지중지하는 혐오의 대상이었으니—그 광경을 현관에서 본 플로렌스는, 차마 나갈 용기가 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 수전이 하인들과 일일이 악수하고, 한 번 돌아서서 자기 옛집을 바라보는 것을 보았다."], ["But she saw Susan shake hands with the servants all round, and turn once to look at her old home; and she saw Diogenes bound out after the cab, and want to follow it, and testify an impossibility of conviction that he had no longer any property in the fare; and the door was shut, and the hurry over, and her tears flowed fast for the loss of an old friend, whom no one could replace.", "또 디오게네스가 마차를 뒤쫓아 뛰쳐나가 따라가고 싶어 하며, 그 마차 삯에 더는 자기 몫이 없다는 사실을 도무지 믿지 못하겠다는 듯 항변하는 것을 보았다. 그러고는 문이 닫혔고, 소동이 끝났으며, 그 누구도 대신할 수 없는 오랜 벗을 잃은 슬픔에 그녀의 눈물이 빠르게 흘렀다."], ["No one.", "그 누구도."], ["No one.", "그 누구도."]], [["Mr Toots, like the leal and trusty soul he was, stopped the cabriolet in a twinkling, and told Susan Nipper of his commission, at which she cried more than before.", "충실하고 미더운 사람인 투츠 씨는, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 그 마차를 세우고 수전 닙퍼에게 자기가 맡은 임무를 일러 주었는데, 그 말에 그녀는 전보다 더 울었다."]], [["“Upon my soul and body!”", "\"맹세코, 정말이지!\""], ["said Mr Toots, taking his seat beside her.", "투츠 씨가 그녀 곁에 자리를 잡으며 말했다."], ["“I feel for you.", "\"당신 심정이 절절히 와닿습니다."], ["Upon my word and honour I think you can hardly know your own feelings better than I imagine them.", "명예를 걸고 말하건대, 제가 짐작하는 것만큼이나 당신 자신도 자기 심정을 더 잘 알 수는 없을 거라 봅니다."], ["I can conceive nothing more dreadful than to have to leave Miss Dombey.”", "돔비 아가씨를 떠나야만 하는 것보다 더 끔찍한 일은 상상할 수가 없어요.\""]], [["Susan abandoned herself to her grief now, and it really was touching to see her.", "이제 수전은 슬픔에 자기를 온전히 내맡겼고, 그 모습은 정말로 가슴 뭉클했다."]], [["“I say,”", "\"저기요,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “now, don’t!", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"그러지 말아요!"], ["at least I mean now do, you know!”", "아니, 그러니까, 좀 그렇게 해요, 아시겠죠!\""]], [["“Do what, Mr Toots!”", "\"무얼 그렇게 하라는 거예요, 투츠 씨!\""], ["cried Susan.", "수전이 외쳤다."]], [["“Why, come home to my place, and have some dinner before you start,”", "\"그러니까, 제 집으로 가서, 출발하기 전에 식사를 좀 하자는 거예요,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“My cook’s a most respectable woman—one of the most motherly people I ever saw—and she’ll be delighted to make you comfortable.", "\"제 요리사는 더없이 점잖은 여인이거든요—제가 본 사람 중에 가장 자애로운 사람이지요—당신을 편안하게 해 주는 걸 기뻐할 겁니다. 그 사람 아들은,\""], ["Her son,” said Mr Toots, as an additional recommendation, “was educated in the Bluecoat School, and blown up in a powder-mill.”", "투츠 씨가 덧붙이는 추천의 말로 말했다, \"블루코트 학교에서 교육을 받았고, 화약 공장에서 폭발에 휩쓸린 적이 있답니다.\""]], [["Susan accepting this kind offer, Mr Toots conducted her to his dwelling, where they were received by the Matron in question who fully justified his character of her, and by the Chicken who at first supposed, on seeing a lady in the vehicle, that Mr Dombey had been doubled up, agreeably to his old recommendation, and Miss Dombey abducted.", "* 블루코트 학교: 가난한 아이들을 위해 세워진 자선 교육기관 크라이스트 호스피털의 별칭. 수전이 이 친절한 제안을 받아들이자, 투츠 씨는 그녀를 자기 거처로 데려갔고, 거기서 두 사람은 앞서 말한 그 가정부의 영접을 받았는데, 그 가정부는 투츠 씨가 평한 그대로의 사람임을 톡톡히 입증했다."], ["This gentleman awakened in Miss Nipper some considerable astonishment; for, having been defeated by the Larkey Boy, his visage was in a state of such great dilapidation, as to be hardly presentable in society with comfort to the beholders.", "또 치킨의 영접도 받았는데, 치킨은 마차에 부인이 한 사람 타고 있는 것을 보고는, 처음에는 자기가 늘 권하던 대로 돔비 씨가 두 동강 나고 돔비 아가씨가 납치된 줄로 알았다."], ["The Chicken himself attributed this punishment to his having had the misfortune to get into Chancery early in the proceedings, when he was severely fibbed by the Larkey one, and heavily grassed.", "이 신사는 닙퍼 양에게 상당한 놀라움을 불러일으켰으니, 라키 보이에게 패한 탓에 그의 얼굴이 어찌나 처참하게 망가져 있었던지, 보는 이들이 마음 편히 사교 자리에 내놓기 어려울 지경이었기 때문이다."], ["But it appeared from the published records of that great contest that the Larkey Boy had had it all his own way from the beginning, and that the Chicken had been tapped, and bunged, and had received pepper, and had been made groggy, and had come up piping, and had endured a complication of similar strange inconveniences, until he had been gone into and finished.", "치킨 본인은 이 응징을, 시합 초반에 운수 사납게도 코너에 몰려 라키 녀석에게 호되게 두들겨 맞고 보기 좋게 매트에 나뒹군 탓이라고 돌렸다. 그러나 그 대접전의 공개된 기록에 따르면, 라키 보이가 처음부터 끝까지 일방적으로 경기를 주도했고, 치킨은 얻어맞고, 코피가 터지고, 매운맛을 보고, 비틀거리게 되고, 헐떡이며 일어나고, 그와 비슷한 갖가지 기묘한 곤욕을 줄줄이 견디다가, 끝내 결딴이 나고 마무리되었다는 것이었다."]], [["After a good repast, and much hospitality, Susan set out for the coach-office in another cabriolet, with Mr Toots inside, as before, and the Chicken on the box, who, whatever distinction he conferred on the little party by the moral weight and heroism of his character, was scarcely ornamental to it, physically speaking, on account of his plasters; which were numerous.", "푸짐한 식사와 융숭한 대접을 마친 뒤, 수전은 또 다른 이륜마차를 타고 역마차 정류소로 출발했는데, 이번에도 투츠 씨가 안에 타고, 치킨이 마부석에 앉았다. 치킨은 그 인품의 도덕적 무게와 영웅적 기개로 이 작은 일행에 어떤 위엄을 더해 주기는 했으되, 물리적으로 말하자면 그 무수히 붙인 반창고들 때문에 일행을 꾸며 주는 구실은 거의 하지 못했다."], ["But the Chicken had registered a vow, in secret, that he would never leave Mr Toots (who was secretly pining to get rid of him), for any less consideration than the good-will and fixtures of a public-house; and being ambitious to go into that line, and drink himself to death as soon as possible, he felt it his cue to make his company unacceptable.", "그러나 치킨은 은밀히 한 가지 맹세를 새겨 두었으니, 어느 술집의 영업권과 비품 일체보다 못한 대가로는 결코 투츠 씨를 떠나지 않겠다는 것이었다(투츠 씨는 은밀히 그를 떨쳐 내고 싶어 안달이 나 있었다). 그리고 그 방면으로 진출해 되도록 빨리 술로 자멸하고픈 야심을 품고 있었기에, 그는 자기 곁을 달갑잖게 만드는 것이 자기가 취할 수단이라 여겼다."]], [["The night-coach by which Susan was to go, was on the point of departure.", "수전이 타고 떠날 야간 역마차가 막 출발하려던 참이었다."], ["Mr Toots having put her inside, lingered by the window, irresolutely, until the driver was about to mount; when, standing on the step, and putting in a face that by the light of the lamp was anxious and confused, he said abruptly:", "투츠 씨는 그녀를 안에 태우고 나서, 마부가 마차에 오르려 할 때까지 우물쭈물 창가에서 머뭇거렸다. 그러다가 발판에 올라서서, 등불 빛에 비친 불안하고 어수선한 얼굴을 들이밀며 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“I say, Susan!", "\"저기요, 수전!"], ["Miss Dombey, you know—”", "돔비 아가씨 말인데요—\""]], [["“Yes, Sir.”", "\"네, 도련님.\""]], [["“Do you think she could—you know—eh?”", "\"혹시 그분이—그러니까—응?\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, Mr Toots,”", "\"송구하지만, 투츠 씨,\""], ["said Susan, “but I don’t hear you.”", "수전이 말했다, \"무슨 말씀인지 안 들려요.\""]], [["“Do you think she could be brought, you know—not exactly at once, but in time—in a long time—to—to love me, you know?", "\"혹시 그분이, 그러니까—당장은 아니더라도, 세월이 지나면—오랜 세월이 지나면—저를—저를 사랑하게 될 수 있을까요? 그게 말이죠!\""], ["There!” said poor Mr Toots.", "가엾은 투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Oh dear no!”", "\"오, 어림없어요!\""], ["returned Susan, shaking her head.", "수전이 머리를 흔들며 대답했다."], ["“I should say, never.", "\"결코 안 될 거라고, 저는 말씀드리겠어요."], ["Never!”", "결코요!\""]], [["“Thank’ee!” said Mr Toots.", "\"고마워요!\""], ["“It’s of no consequence.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["Good-night.", "\"괜찮습니다."], ["It’s of no consequence, thank’ee!”", "안녕히 가세요. 괜찮아요, 고맙습니다!\""]], [["# CHAPTER XLV. The Trusty Agent", "# CHAPTER XLV. The Trusty Agent"]], [["Edith went out alone that day, and returned home early.", "그날 이디스는 혼자 외출했다가 일찍 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["It was but a few minutes after ten o’clock, when her carriage rolled along the street in which she lived.", "그녀의 마차가 그녀가 사는 거리를 굴러올 때는 열 시를 겨우 몇 분 넘긴 시각이었다."]], [["There was the same enforced composure on her face, that there had been when she was dressing; and the wreath upon her head encircled the same cold and steady brow.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 옷을 차려입을 때 보였던 것과 똑같은 억지스러운 침착함이 어려 있었고, 머리에 두른 화관은 똑같이 차갑고 흔들림 없는 이마를 둘러싸고 있었다."], ["But it would have been better to have seen its leaves and flowers reft into fragments by her passionate hand, or rendered shapeless by the fitful searches of a throbbing and bewildered brain for any resting-place, than adorning such tranquillity.", "그러나 그 화관의 잎과 꽃이 격정에 찬 손에 갈가리 찢겨 조각나거나, 두근거리고 갈피를 잃은 머리가 어디든 쉴 곳을 찾아 변덕스레 더듬는 통에 형체를 잃은 모습을 보는 편이, 그런 평정을 장식하고 있는 것보다는 차라리 나았을 것이다."], ["So obdurate, so unapproachable, so unrelenting, one would have thought that nothing could soften such a woman’s nature, and that everything in life had hardened it.", "그토록 완고하고, 그토록 다가갈 수 없으며, 그토록 가차 없으니, 그런 여인의 본성은 무엇으로도 부드럽게 할 수 없을 것이며, 삶의 모든 것이 그것을 단단하게 굳혀 놓았으리라고 사람들은 생각했을 것이다."]], [["Arrived at her own door, she was alighting, when some one coming quietly from the hall, and standing bareheaded, offered her his arm.", "자기 집 문 앞에 이르러 그녀가 마차에서 내리려 할 때, 누군가가 현관에서 조용히 나와 모자를 벗은 채 그녀에게 팔을 내밀었다."], ["The servant being thrust aside, she had no choice but to touch it; and she then knew whose arm it was.", "하인이 옆으로 밀려난 터라 그녀는 그 팔을 잡는 것 외에 다른 도리가 없었고, 그제야 그것이 누구의 팔인지를 알았다."]], [["“How is your patient, Sir?”", "\"환자분은 좀 어떠신가요, 선생?\""], ["she asked, with a curled lip.", "그녀가 입술을 비틀며 물었다."]], [["“He is better,”", "\"좋아지셨습니다,\""], ["returned Carker.", "카커가 대답했다."], ["“He is doing very well.", "\"아주 잘 회복되고 계십니다."], ["I have left him for the night.”", "밤이라 저는 그분 곁을 떠나왔지요.\""]], [["She bent her head, and was passing up the staircase, when he followed and said, speaking at the bottom:", "그녀는 고개를 숙이고 계단을 올라가려 했는데, 그가 뒤를 따라오며 계단 아래에서 말했다."]], [["“Madam!", "\"부인!"], ["May I beg the favour of a minute’s audience?”", "잠시 뵙는 영광을 청해도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["She stopped and turned her eyes back “It is an unseasonable time, Sir, and I am fatigued.", "그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 눈길을 뒤로 돌렸다. \"지금은 때가 아닙니다, 선생."], ["Is your business urgent?”", "게다가 저는 피곤합니다. 급한 용무인가요?\""]], [["“It is very urgent, returned Carker.", "\"매우 급합니다,\" 카커가 대답했다."], ["“As I am so fortunate as to have met you, let me press my petition.”", "\"이렇게 운 좋게 부인을 뵙게 되었으니, 제 청을 밀어붙이도록 허락해 주십시오.\""]], [["She looked down for a moment at his glistening mouth; and he looked up at her, standing above him in her stately dress, and thought, again, how beautiful she was.", "그녀는 잠시 그의 번들거리는 입을 내려다보았고, 그는 위쪽 위풍당당한 차림으로 서 있는 그녀를 올려다보며, 다시 한번 그녀가 얼마나 아름다운지를 생각했다."]], [["“Where is Miss Dombey?”", "\"돔비 아가씨는 어디 있느냐?\""], ["she asked the servant, aloud.", "그녀가 하인에게 큰 소리로 물었다."]], [["“In the morning room, Ma’am.”", "\"오전 거실에 계십니다, 마님.\""]], [["“Show the way there!”", "\"그리로 안내해라!\""], ["Turning her eyes again on the attentive gentleman at the bottom of the stairs, and informing him with a slight motion of her head, that he was at liberty to follow, she passed on.", "계단 아래에서 주의 깊게 기다리는 신사에게 다시 눈길을 돌리고, 따라와도 좋다는 것을 가벼운 고갯짓으로 알린 뒤, 그녀는 앞서 나아갔다."]], [["“I beg your pardon!", "\"실례합니다!"], ["Madam!", "부인!"], ["Mrs Dombey!”", "돔비 부인!\""], ["cried the soft and nimble Carker, at her side in a moment.", "부드럽고 날렵한 카커가 어느새 그녀의 옆에 와서 외쳤다."], ["“May I be permitted to entreat that Miss Dombey is not present?”", "\"돔비 아가씨가 자리에 없도록 해 주시기를 간청해도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["She confronted him, with a quick look, but with the same self-possession and steadiness.", "그녀는 재빠른 눈길로 그를 마주 보았으나, 똑같이 자제하며 흔들림이 없었다."]], [["“I would spare Miss Dombey,” said Carker, in a low voice, “the knowledge of what I have to say.", "\"제가 드릴 말씀을 돔비 아가씨가 알지 못하도록 해 드리고 싶습니다,\""], ["At least, Madam, I would leave it to you to decide whether she shall know of it or not.", "카커가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"적어도, 부인, 아가씨가 그것을 알게 할지 말지는 부인의 결정에 맡기고자 합니다."], ["I owe that to you. It is my bounden duty to you.", "그것이 제가 부인께 마땅히 해야 할 도리입니다."], ["After our former interview, it would be monstrous in me if I did otherwise.”", "부인께 진 빚이지요. 지난번 우리가 만난 일을 생각하면, 제가 달리 행동한다면 그것은 끔찍한 일이 될 것입니다.\""]], [["She slowly withdrew her eyes from his face, and turning to the servant, said, “Some other room.”", "그녀는 천천히 그의 얼굴에서 눈을 거두고, 하인에게로 돌아서서 말했다. \"다른 방으로.\""], ["He led the way to a drawing-room, which he speedily lighted up and then left them.", "하인은 어느 응접실로 안내하여 재빨리 불을 밝히고는 두 사람을 남겨 두고 나갔다."], ["While he remained, not a word was spoken.", "그가 머무는 동안에는 한마디도 오가지 않았다."], ["Edith enthroned herself upon a couch by the fire; and Mr Carker, with his hat in his hand and his eyes bent upon the carpet, stood before her, at some little distance.", "이디스는 난롯가 긴 의자에 옥좌에 오르듯 앉았고, 카커 씨는 모자를 손에 든 채 양탄자에 눈을 떨군 채로, 그녀에게서 조금 떨어진 자리에 섰다."]], [["“Before I hear you, Sir,”", "\"선생의 말씀을 듣기 전에,\""], ["said Edith, when the door was closed, “I wish you to hear me.”", "문이 닫히자 이디스가 말했다, \"제 말을 먼저 들어주시기 바랍니다.\""]], [["“To be addressed by Mrs Dombey,”", "\"돔비 부인의 말씀을 듣는 것은,\""], ["he returned, “even in accents of unmerited reproach, is an honour I so greatly esteem, that although I were not her servant in all things, I should defer to such a wish, most readily.”", "그가 대답했다, \"비록 마땅치 않은 책망의 어조라 하더라도, 제가 그토록 크게 여기는 영광이라, 설령 제가 만사에 부인의 종이 아니라 해도, 그런 바람에는 기꺼이 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["“If you are charged by the man whom you have just now left, Sir;”", "\"만일 방금 두고 온 그 사람에게서,\""], ["Mr Carker raised his eyes, as if he were going to counterfeit surprise, but she met them, and stopped him, if such were his intention; “with any message to me, do not attempt to deliver it, for I will not receive it.", "카커 씨는 마치 놀란 척을 꾸미려는 듯 눈을 들었으나, 그녀가 그 눈을 마주쳐 만약 그럴 의도였다면 그를 막아 버렸다. \"저에게 전할 어떤 전갈이라도 받으셨다면, 그것을 전하려 들지 마십시오."], ["I need scarcely ask you if you are come on such an errand.", "저는 받지 않겠으니까요. 선생이 그런 심부름으로 오셨는지 굳이 물을 것도 없겠지요."], ["I have expected you some time.”", "저는 한동안 선생을 기다려 왔습니다.\""]], [["“It is my misfortune,” he replied, “to be here, wholly against my will, for such a purpose.", "\"이런 목적으로, 전적으로 제 뜻에 반하여 이곳에 있게 된 것은,\""], ["Allow me to say that I am here for two purposes.", "그가 대답했다, \"저의 불운입니다. 제가 두 가지 목적으로 여기 왔다는 것을 말씀드리도록 허락해 주십시오."], ["That is one.”", "그것은 그중 하나입니다.\""]], [["“That one, Sir,”", "\"그 하나는, 선생,\""], ["she returned, “is ended.", "그녀가 대답했다, \"끝났습니다."], ["Or, if you return to it—”", "혹시 다시 그 이야기로 돌아오신다면—\""]], [["“Can Mrs Dombey believe,”", "\"돔비 부인께서,\""], ["said Carker, coming nearer, “that I would return to it in the face of her prohibition?", "카커가 더 가까이 다가오며 말했다, \"금하신 것을 무릅쓰고 제가 그 이야기로 돌아오리라 여기실 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Is it possible that Mrs Dombey, having no regard to my unfortunate position, is so determined to consider me inseparable from my instructor as to do me great and wilful injustice?”", "돔비 부인께서, 저의 불운한 처지는 헤아리지 않으시고, 저를 제 지시자와 떼어 놓을 수 없는 사람으로만 여기시어 저에게 크고도 고의적인 부당함을 안기시려 그토록 마음을 굳히셨단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["returned Edith, bending her dark gaze full upon him, and speaking with a rising passion that inflated her proud nostril and her swelling neck, and stirred the delicate white down upon a robe she wore, thrown loosely over shoulders that could bear its snowy neighbourhood, “Why do you present yourself to me, as you have done, and speak to me of love and duty to my husband, and pretend to think that I am happily married, and that I honour him?", "이디스가 어두운 눈길을 그에게 가득 쏟으며, 점점 차오르는 격정으로 그녀의 도도한 콧방울과 부풀어 오른 목을 한껏 일으키고, 눈처럼 흰 깃의 이웃을 감당할 수 있는 어깨 위로 헐겁게 걸친 옷의 섬세한 흰 솜털을 흔들리게 하면서 말했다, \"어찌하여 선생은 지금까지 해 온 것처럼 제 앞에 나타나, 남편에 대한 사랑이며 의무를 운운하고, 제가 행복한 결혼 생활을 하고 있으며 그를 존경한다고 믿는 척하십니까?"], ["How dare you venture so to affront me, when you know—I do not know better, Sir: I have seen it in your every glance, and heard it in your every word—that in place of affection between us there is aversion and contempt, and that I despise him hardly less than I despise myself for being his!", "선생이 알면서—저도 그보다 더 잘은 모릅니다, 선생. 저는 선생의 모든 눈길에서 그것을 보았고 모든 말에서 그것을 들었으니까요—우리 사이에 애정이 있기는커녕 혐오와 경멸만이 있으며, 그의 아내가 된 저 자신을 경멸하는 것 못지않게 제가 그를 경멸한다는 것을 알면서, 어찌 감히 저를 그렇게 모욕하시는 겁니까?"], ["Injustice!", "부당함이라!"], ["If I had done justice to the torment you have made me feel, and to my sense of the insult you have put upon me, I should have slain you!”", "선생이 제게 겪게 한 그 고통과, 선생이 제게 안긴 모욕에 대한 저의 감정에 정당한 응답을 했더라면, 저는 선생을 죽여 버렸을 것입니다!\""]], [["She had asked him why he did this.", "그녀는 그가 왜 이러는지 물었다."], ["Had she not been blinded by her pride and wrath, and self-humiliation,—which she was, fiercely as she bent her gaze upon him,—she would have seen the answer in his face.", "그녀가 자신의 자존심과 분노, 그리고 자기비하에 눈이 멀지만 않았더라면—그토록 사납게 그에게 눈길을 쏟고 있었으니 실제로 눈이 멀어 있었지만—그의 얼굴에서 그 답을 보았을 것이다."], ["To bring her to this declaration.", "그녀를 이런 고백에 이르게 하는 것, 그것이 답이었다."]], [["She saw it not, and cared not whether it was there or no.", "그녀는 그것을 보지 못했고, 그것이 거기 있든 없든 개의치 않았다."], ["She saw only the indignities and struggles she had undergone and had to undergo, and was writhing under them.", "그녀는 오직 자신이 겪었고 또 겪어야 할 모욕과 몸부림만을 보았으며, 그 아래에서 괴로워 몸을 뒤틀었다."], ["As she sat looking fixedly at them, rather than at him, she plucked the feathers from a pinion of some rare and beautiful bird, which hung from her wrist by a golden thread, to serve her as a fan, and rained them on the ground.", "그를 본다기보다 그것들을 응시하며 앉아 있는 동안, 그녀는 황금 실로 손목에 매달려 부채 노릇을 하던, 어느 진귀하고 아름다운 새의 날개에서 깃털을 뜯어내어 바닥에 비처럼 흩뿌렸다."]], [["He did not shrink beneath her gaze, but stood, until such outward signs of her anger as had escaped her control subsided, with the air of a man who had his sufficient reply in reserve and would presently deliver it.", "그는 그녀의 눈길 아래에서 움츠러들지 않고, 그녀의 통제를 벗어난 분노의 겉 표시들이 가라앉을 때까지, 충분한 대답을 마련해 두고 곧 그것을 내놓으려는 사람의 태도로 서 있었다."], ["And he then spoke, looking straight into her kindling eyes.", "그러고는 타오르는 그녀의 두 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["he said, “I know, and knew before today, that I have found no favour with you; and I knew why. Yes.", "그가 말했다, \"저는 오늘 이전부터, 제가 부인의 호의를 얻지 못했다는 것을 알고 있었고, 그 까닭도 알고 있었습니다."], ["I knew why.", "그렇습니다."], ["You have spoken so openly to me; I am so relieved by the possession of your confidence—”", "저는 그 까닭을 알았지요. 부인께서 그토록 솔직하게 말씀해 주셨으니, 부인의 신임을 얻게 되어 이렇게 마음이 놓이는지라—\""]], [["“Confidence!”", "\"신임이라니!\""], ["she repeated, with disdain.", "그녀가 경멸하며 되받았다."]], [["He passed it over.", "그는 그 말을 그냥 흘려 넘겼다."]], [["“—that I will make no pretence of concealment.", "\"—저는 아무것도 숨기는 척하지 않겠습니다."], ["I did see from the first, that there was no affection on your part for Mr Dombey—how could it possibly exist between such different subjects?", "저는 처음부터, 부인 쪽에 돔비 씨에 대한 애정이 없다는 것을 알았습니다—그토록 다른 두 사람 사이에 어찌 애정이 있을 수 있겠습니까?"], ["And I have seen, since, that stronger feelings than indifference have been engendered in your breast—how could that possibly be otherwise, either, circumstanced as you have been?", "그리고 그 이후로, 무관심보다 더 강한 감정이 부인의 가슴속에 생겨났다는 것도 알았습니다—부인께서 처하신 상황에서 어찌 그렇지 않을 수 있겠습니까?"], ["But was it for me to presume to avow this knowledge to you in so many words?”", "그러나 제가 이 앎을 부인께 그토록 노골적인 말로 감히 털어놓을 처지였겠습니까?\""]], [["“Was it for you, Sir,”", "\"선생이,\""], ["she replied, “to feign that other belief, and audaciously to thrust it on me day by day?”", "그녀가 대꾸했다, \"그와 다른 믿음을 가진 척 꾸미고, 그것을 뻔뻔스럽게 날마다 저에게 들이밀 처지였단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Madam, it was,”", "\"부인, 그러했습니다,\""], ["he eagerly retorted.", "그가 간절히 받아쳤다."], ["“If I had done less, if I had done anything but that, I should not be speaking to you thus; and I foresaw—who could better foresee, for who has had greater experience of Mr Dombey than myself?—that unless your character should prove to be as yielding and obedient as that of his first submissive lady, which I did not believe—”", "\"제가 그보다 덜 했더라면, 그것 말고 다른 무엇이라도 했더라면, 저는 지금 이렇게 부인께 말씀드리고 있지 못했을 것입니다. 그리고 저는 내다보았습니다—누군들 저보다 더 잘 내다볼 수 있겠습니까, 저보다 돔비 씨를 더 오래 겪은 사람이 누가 있겠습니까?—부인의 성품이, 제가 믿지 않았던 바이지만, 그의 첫 부인의 그 순종적이고 고분고분한 성품처럼 무르고 순순한 것으로 드러나지 않는 한—\""]], [["A haughty smile gave him reason to observe that he might repeat this.", "도도한 미소가 떠오르자, 그는 이 말을 다시 되풀이해도 좋다는 빌미를 얻었다."]], [["“I say, which I did not believe,—the time was likely to come, when such an understanding as we have now arrived at, would be serviceable.”", "\"제가 믿지 않았다고 말씀드린 바이지만—우리가 지금 이르른 것과 같은 이해가 쓸모 있게 될 때가 올 법하다고요.\""]], [["“Serviceable to whom, Sir?”", "\"누구에게 쓸모 있다는 겁니까, 선생?\""], ["she demanded scornfully.", "그녀가 경멸하며 따져 물었다."]], [["“To you.", "\"부인께요."], ["I will not add to myself, as warning me to refrain even from that limited commendation of Mr Dombey, in which I can honestly indulge, in order that I may not have the misfortune of saying anything distasteful to one whose aversion and contempt,”", "제 자신에게도라고 덧붙이지는 않겠습니다. 그러면 제가 정직하게 베풀 수 있는 그 한정된 돔비 씨 칭찬마저 삼가라고 스스로에게 경고하는 셈이 될 테니까요."], ["with great expression, “are so keen.”", "혐오와 경멸이,\" 대단한 표정으로, \"그토록 날카로운 분에게 거슬리는 말을 하는 불운을 겪지 않으려고 말입니다.\""]], [["“Is it honest in you, Sir,”", "\"선생이 '한정된 칭찬'을 고백하고, 그를—그분의 으뜸가는 조언자이자 아첨꾼이면서—그런 폄하의 어조로 말하는 것이,\""], ["said Edith, “to confess to your ‘limited commendation,’ and to speak in that tone of disparagement, even of him: being his chief counsellor and flatterer!”", "이디스가 말했다, \"정직한 일입니까?\""]], [["“Counsellor,—yes,”", "\"조언자라—그렇습니다,\""], ["said Carker.", "카커가 말했다."], ["“Flatterer,—no.", "\"아첨꾼이라—아닙니다."], ["A little reservation I fear I must confess to.", "약간의 사양은 인정해야 할 듯하여 두렵습니다만."], ["But our interest and convenience commonly oblige many of us to make professions that we cannot feel.", "그러나 우리의 이해관계와 편의는 우리 가운데 많은 이로 하여금 느끼지도 않는 것을 공언하게 만들곤 하지요."], ["We have partnerships of interest and convenience, friendships of interest and convenience, dealings of interest and convenience, marriages of interest and convenience, every day.”", "우리는 날마다 이해와 편의에 따른 동업을, 이해와 편의에 따른 우정을, 이해와 편의에 따른 거래를, 이해와 편의에 따른 결혼을 하고 있습니다.\""]], [["She bit her blood-red lip; but without wavering in the dark, stern watch she kept upon him.", "그녀는 핏빛처럼 붉은 입술을 깨물었으나, 그를 향해 지켜보는 어둡고 준엄한 감시는 흔들리지 않았다."]], [["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Mr Carker, sitting down in a chair that was near her, with an air of the most profound and most considerate respect, “why should I hesitate now, being altogether devoted to your service, to speak plainly?", "카커 씨가 그녀 가까이에 있는 의자에 앉으며, 더없이 깊고 더없이 사려 깊은 존경의 태도로 말했다, \"이제 전적으로 부인을 섬기는 데 바쳐진 몸인데, 솔직하게 말씀드리기를 어찌 머뭇거리겠습니까?"], ["It was natural that a lady, endowed as you are, should think it feasible to change her husband’s character in some respects, and mould him to a better form.”", "부인처럼 타고나신 분이라면, 남편의 성품을 어떤 점에서 바꾸어 더 나은 형태로 빚어낼 수 있으리라 여기시는 것도 당연한 일이었습니다.\""]], [["“It was not natural to me, Sir,”", "\"저에게는 당연하지 않았습니다, 선생,\""], ["she rejoined.", "그녀가 되받았다."], ["“I had never any expectation or intention of that kind.”", "\"저는 그런 기대도 의도도 결코 품은 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["The proud undaunted face showed him it was resolute to wear no mask he offered, but was set upon a reckless disclosure of itself, indifferent to any aspect in which it might present itself to such as he.", "도도하고 굴하지 않는 얼굴은, 그가 내미는 어떤 가면도 쓰지 않기로 굳게 마음먹었으며, 그런 자에게 자신이 어떤 모습으로 비치든 개의치 않고 거리낌 없이 자신을 드러내기로 작정했음을 그에게 보여 주었다."]], [["“At least it was natural,”", "\"적어도,\""], ["he resumed, “that you should deem it quite possible to live with Mr Dombey as his wife, at once without submitting to him, and without coming into such violent collision with him.", "그가 다시 말을 이었다, \"돔비 씨에게 굴복하지도 않으면서, 또 그와 그렇게 격렬하게 충돌하지도 않으면서, 그의 아내로서 함께 살아가는 것이 능히 가능하다고 여기신 것은 당연한 일이었습니다."], ["But, Madam, you did not know Mr Dombey (as you have since ascertained), when you thought that.", "그러나 부인, 그렇게 생각하셨을 때 부인은 (그 후로 확인하셨듯이) 돔비 씨를 알지 못하셨습니다."], ["You did not know how exacting and how proud he is, or how he is, if I may say so, the slave of his own greatness, and goes yoked to his own triumphal car like a beast of burden, with no idea on earth but that it is behind him and is to be drawn on, over everything and through everything.”", "그분이 얼마나 까다롭고 거만한지, 또, 이렇게 말씀드려도 된다면, 그분이 얼마나 자신의 위대함의 노예가 되어, 짐 부리는 짐승처럼 자신의 개선 수레에 매여, 그 수레가 뒤에 있어 무엇이든 짓밟고 무엇이든 헤치며 끌려가야 한다는 생각밖에는 이 세상에 아무 생각이 없는지를 알지 못하셨습니다.\""]], [["His teeth gleamed through his malicious relish of this conceit, as he went on talking:", "이 자만의 비유를 음흉하게 음미하는 동안, 그의 이가 번뜩였고, 그는 계속 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“Mr Dombey is really capable of no more true consideration for you, Madam, than for me.", "\"돔비 씨는, 부인, 정말이지 저를 위해서만큼이나 부인을 위해서도 진정한 배려를 할 줄 모르는 분입니다."], ["The comparison is an extreme one; I intend it to be so; but quite just.", "지나친 비교이지요. 일부러 그렇게 한 것입니다만, 더없이 정당한 비교이지요."], ["Mr Dombey, in the plenitude of his power, asked me—I had it from his own lips yesterday morning—to be his go-between to you, because he knows I am not agreeable to you, and because he intends that I shall be a punishment for your contumacy; and besides that, because he really does consider, that I, his paid servant, am an ambassador whom it is derogatory to the dignity—not of the lady to whom I have the happiness of speaking; she has no existence in his mind—but of his wife, a part of himself, to receive.", "돔비 씨는, 자기 권세가 한껏 충만한 가운데, 저에게—어제 아침 그분 입으로 직접 들은 바입니다—부인과 자기 사이의 중개인이 되어 달라고 청했습니다. 제가 부인께 달갑지 않은 사람임을 알기 때문이고, 또 제가 부인의 고집에 대한 벌이 되기를 의도하기 때문이지요."], ["You may imagine how regardless of me, how obtuse to the possibility of my having any individual sentiment or opinion he is, when he tells me, openly, that I am so employed.", "그리고 그 외에도, 그분은 정말로, 자기의 봉급 받는 종인 제가, 부인—제가 말씀 나누는 영광을 누리는 이 부인이 아니라, 그분 마음속에는 그런 분이 존재하지 않으니까요—그분의 일부인 자기 아내가 맞이하기에는 그 위엄을 떨어뜨리는 사절이라고 여기는 것입니다. 그분이 저더러 그렇게 부려진다고 대놓고 말할 때, 그분이 저를 얼마나 안중에도 두지 않는지, 제게도 어떤 개인적 감정이나 의견이 있을 수 있다는 가능성에 얼마나 무딘지를 부인께서는 짐작하실 수 있을 것입니다."], ["You know how perfectly indifferent to your feelings he is, when he threatens you with such a messenger.", "그분이 그런 심부름꾼으로 부인을 위협할 때, 그분이 부인의 감정에 얼마나 완전히 무관심한지도 아실 것입니다."], ["As you, of course, have not forgotten that he did.”", "물론 그분이 그러셨다는 것을 부인께서는 잊지 않으셨을 테니까요.\""]], [["She watched him still attentively.", "그녀는 여전히 그를 주의 깊게 지켜보았다."], ["But he watched her too; and he saw that this indication of a knowledge on his part, of something that had passed between herself and her husband, rankled and smarted in her haughty breast, like a poisoned arrow.", "그러나 그 역시 그녀를 지켜보았고, 그녀와 남편 사이에 오간 어떤 일을 자기가 알고 있다는 이 암시가, 마치 독화살처럼 그녀의 도도한 가슴속에 박혀 쓰라리게 욱신거린다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“I do not recall all this to widen the breach between yourself and Mr Dombey, Madam—Heaven forbid!", "\"제가 이 모든 것을 다시 끄집어내는 것은, 부인, 부인과 돔비 씨 사이의 골을 더 넓히려는 것이 아닙니다—천만에요!"], ["what would it profit me?—but as an example of the hopelessness of impressing Mr Dombey with a sense that anybody is to be considered when he is in question.", "그것이 저에게 무슨 득이 되겠습니까?—다만, 자기가 관련된 일에서는 누구든 헤아려져야 한다는 인식을 돔비 씨에게 심어 주는 것이 얼마나 가망 없는 일인지를 보여 드리는 본보기로 그러는 것입니다."], ["We who are about him, have, in our various positions, done our part, I daresay, to confirm him in his way of thinking; but if we had not done so, others would—or they would not have been about him; and it has always been, from the beginning, the very staple of his life.", "그분 곁에 있는 우리는, 저마다 다른 처지에서, 그분을 그런 사고방식 속에 굳어지게 하는 데 우리 몫을 다했다고 감히 말할 수 있습니다. 그러나 우리가 그러지 않았더라도 다른 이들이 그랬을 것입니다—아니면 그들은 그분 곁에 있지 못했겠지요."], ["Mr Dombey has had to deal, in short, with none but submissive and dependent persons, who have bowed the knee, and bent the neck, before him.", "그리고 그것은 처음부터 늘 그분 삶의 바로 그 근간이었습니다. 요컨대 돔비 씨는, 그분 앞에서 무릎을 꿇고 목을 굽힌, 순종적이고 의존적인 사람들 외에는 상대해 본 적이 없습니다."], ["He has never known what it is to have angry pride and strong resentment opposed to him.”", "그분은 성난 자존심과 강한 분노가 자기에게 맞선다는 것이 어떤 것인지 결코 알아 본 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“But he will know it now!”", "\"하지만 이제 그것을 알게 되겠지!\""], ["she seemed to say; though her lips did not part, nor her eyes falter.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하는 듯했다. 입술은 벌어지지 않았고 눈도 흔들리지 않았지만."], ["He saw the soft down tremble once again, and he saw her lay the plumage of the beautiful bird against her bosom for a moment; and he unfolded one more ring of the coil into which he had gathered himself.", "그는 부드러운 솜털이 다시 한번 떨리는 것을 보았고, 그녀가 그 아름다운 새의 깃털을 한순간 자기 가슴에 갖다 대는 것을 보았다. 그러고는 자기가 몸을 사리고 똬리 틀었던 그 고리를 한 겹 더 풀어냈다."]], [["“Mr Dombey, though a most honourable gentleman,” he said, “is so prone to pervert even facts to his own view, when he is at all opposed, in consequence of the warp in his mind, that he—can I give a better instance than this!—he sincerely believes (you will excuse the folly of what I am about to say; it not being mine) that his severe expression of opinion to his present wife, on a certain special occasion she may remember, before the lamented death of Mrs Skewton, produced a withering effect, and for the moment quite subdued her!”", "\"돔비 씨는, 더없이 명예로운 신사이긴 하지만,\" 그가 말했다, \"조금이라도 반대에 부딪히면, 그 마음의 뒤틀림 탓에, 사실마저도 자기 견해에 맞추어 곡해하는 경향이 어찌나 강한지요. 그가—이보다 더 좋은 예를 들 수 있겠습니까!—그는 진심으로 믿고 있습니다(이제 제가 드리려는 말의 어리석음을 너그러이 보아 주십시오. 제 생각이 아니니까요). 스큐턴 부인의 애통한 별세 전, 그분이 기억하실지 모를 어떤 특별한 자리에서, 그가 지금의 아내에게 자기 의견을 엄하게 표명한 것이 시들어 버리게 하는 효과를 내어, 그 순간 그녀를 완전히 굴복시켰다고 말입니다!\""]], [["Edith laughed.", "이디스가 웃었다."], ["How harshly and unmusically need not be described.", "그 웃음이 얼마나 거칠고 가락이 없었는지는 굳이 묘사할 필요가 없다."], ["It is enough that he was glad to hear her.", "그가 그것을 듣고 기뻐했다는 것으로 충분하다."]], [["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["he resumed, “I have done with this.", "그가 다시 말을 이었다, \"이 이야기는 이만하겠습니다."], ["Your own opinions are so strong, and, I am persuaded, so unalterable,”", "부인 자신의 의견은 어찌나 강하고, 또, 제가 확신컨대, 어찌나 변할 수 없는지,\""], ["he repeated those words slowly and with great emphasis, “that I am almost afraid to incur your displeasure anew, when I say that in spite of these defects and my full knowledge of them, I have become habituated to Mr Dombey, and esteem him.", "그는 그 말들을 천천히 그리고 대단히 힘주어 되풀이했다, \"이런 결점들에도 불구하고, 그리고 그것들을 제가 속속들이 알고 있으면서도, 제가 돔비 씨에게 익숙해졌고 그분을 존경하게 되었다고 말씀드리면, 또다시 부인의 노여움을 살까 거의 두렵습니다."], ["But when I say so, it is not, believe me, for the mere sake of vaunting a feeling that is so utterly at variance with your own, and for which you can have no sympathy”—oh how distinct and plain and emphasized this was!—“but to give you an assurance of the zeal with which, in this unhappy matter, I am yours, and the indignation with which I regard the part I am to fill!”", "그러나 제가 그렇게 말하는 것은, 부디 믿어 주십시오, 부인 자신의 감정과는 그토록 완전히 어긋나고 부인께서 공감하실 수 없는 어떤 감정을 그저 뽐내기 위해서가 아니라\"—오, 이 말이 얼마나 또렷하고 분명하며 힘주어진 것이었던가!—\"이 불행한 일에서 제가 얼마나 열성으로 부인의 편인지, 그리고 제가 맡아야 할 역할을 얼마나 분개하며 여기는지를 부인께 확신시켜 드리기 위해서입니다!\""]], [["She sat as if she were afraid to take her eyes from his face.", "그녀는 그의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼기가 두려운 듯이 앉아 있었다."]], [["And now to unwind the last ring of the coil!", "그리고 이제 그 똬리의 마지막 고리를 풀 차례였다!"]], [["“It is growing late,”", "\"시간이 늦어 가는군요,\""], ["said Carker, after a pause, “and you are, as you said, fatigued.", "카커가 잠시 사이를 두고 말했다, \"그리고 부인께서는, 말씀하셨듯이, 피곤하시고요."], ["But the second object of this interview, I must not forget.", "그러나 이 만남의 두 번째 목적을 잊어서는 안 되겠습니다."], ["I must recommend you, I must entreat you in the most earnest manner, for sufficient reasons that I have, to be cautious in your demonstrations of regard for Miss Dombey.”", "제가 가진 충분한 까닭이 있어, 돔비 아가씨에 대한 부인의 애정 표현에 신중하시기를 권해 드려야겠습니다, 아니, 더없이 간곡하게 간청해야겠습니다.\""]], [["“Cautious!", "\"신중하라니!"], ["What do you mean?”", "무슨 뜻이지요?\""]], [["“To be careful how you exhibit too much affection for that young lady.”", "\"그 젊은 아가씨에게 너무 큰 애정을 드러내지 않도록 조심하시라는 말씀입니다.\""]], [["“Too much affection, Sir!”", "\"너무 큰 애정이라니요, 선생!\""], ["said Edith, knitting her broad brow and rising.", "이디스가 넓은 이마를 찌푸리며 자리에서 일어나 말했다."], ["“Who judges my affection, or measures it out?", "\"누가 제 애정을 판단하고, 누가 그것을 재단한단 말입니까?"], ["You?”", "선생이요?\""]], [["“It is not I who do so.”", "\"제가 그러는 것이 아닙니다.\""], ["He was, or feigned to be, perplexed.", "그는 당혹스러워하거나, 당혹스러운 척했다."]], [["“Who then?”", "\"그럼 누가요?\""]], [["“Can you not guess who then?”", "\"누구일지 짐작이 안 가십니까?\""]], [["“I do not choose to guess,”", "\"저는 짐작하고 싶지 않습니다,\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\" 그가 잠시 머뭇거린 뒤에 말했다."], ["he said after a little hesitation; meantime they had been, and still were, regarding each other as before; “I am in a difficulty here.", "그러는 동안에도 두 사람은 여전히 아까처럼 서로를 바라보고 있었다. \"저는 여기서 곤경에 처해 있습니다."], ["You have told me you will receive no message, and you have forbidden me to return to that subject; but the two subjects are so closely entwined, I find, that unless you will accept this vague caution from one who has now the honour to possess your confidence, though the way to it has been through your displeasure, I must violate the injunction you have laid upon me.”", "부인께서는 어떤 전갈도 받지 않으시겠다 하셨고, 그 화제로 되돌아가는 것을 저에게 금하셨습니다. 그러나 그 두 화제는 제가 보기에 어찌나 긴밀히 얽혀 있는지, 부인의 노여움을 거쳐 오긴 했어도 이제 부인의 신임을 얻는 영광을 누리게 된 한 사람에게서 이 막연한 경고를 받아들여 주지 않으신다면, 저는 부인께서 제게 내리신 명을 어길 수밖에 없습니다.\""]], [["“You know that you are free to do so, Sir,”", "\"선생이 그렇게 해도 좋다는 것을 아시지요, 선생,\""], ["said Edith.", "이디스가 말했다."], ["“Do it.”", "\"그렇게 하세요.\""]], [["So pale, so trembling, so impassioned!", "그토록 창백하고, 그토록 떨며, 그토록 격정에 차 있다니!"], ["He had not miscalculated the effect then!", "그렇다면 그는 그 효과를 잘못 계산하지 않은 것이었다!"]], [["“His instructions were,”", "\"그분의 지시는,\""], ["he said, in a low voice, “that I should inform you that your demeanour towards Miss Dombey is not agreeable to him.", "그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"돔비 아가씨에 대한 부인의 태도가 그분에게 달갑지 않다는 것을 제가 부인께 알리라는 것이었습니다."], ["That it suggests comparisons to him which are not favourable to himself.", "그것이 그분 자신에게 불리한 비교를 떠올리게 한다는 것이지요."], ["That he desires it may be wholly changed; and that if you are in earnest, he is confident it will be; for your continued show of affection will not benefit its object.”", "그분은 그것이 완전히 바뀌기를 바라며, 만일 부인께서 진심이라면, 반드시 그렇게 되리라 확신한다는 것입니다. 부인의 끊임없는 애정 표현이 그 대상에게 이로울 것이 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["“That is a threat,”", "\"그것은 협박이군요,\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“That is a threat,”", "\"그것은 협박입니다,\""], ["he answered, in his voiceless manner of assent: adding aloud, “but not directed against you.”", "그가 동의하는 그 소리 없는 방식으로 대답하고는, 큰 소리로 덧붙였다, \"그러나 부인을 겨눈 것은 아닙니다.\""]], [["Proud, erect, and dignified, as she stood confronting him; and looking through him as she did, with her full bright flashing eye; and smiling, as she was, with scorn and bitterness; she sunk as if the ground had dropped beneath her, and in an instant would have fallen on the floor, but that he caught her in his arms.", "도도하고 꼿꼿하며 위엄 있게, 그녀는 그를 마주하고 서 있었고, 가득 빛나는 형형한 눈으로 그를 꿰뚫어 보았으며, 경멸과 쓰라림으로 미소 짓고 있었다. 그러던 그녀가, 마치 발밑의 땅이 꺼지기라도 한 듯 주저앉았고, 그가 두 팔로 붙들지 않았더라면 한순간에 바닥에 쓰러졌을 것이다."], ["As instantaneously she threw him off, the moment that he touched her, and, drawing back, confronted him again, immoveable, with her hand stretched out.", "그러나 그가 그녀에게 손을 대는 그 순간, 그녀는 그만큼이나 순식간에 그를 뿌리치고는, 뒤로 물러나 손을 내뻗은 채 꼼짝 않고 다시 그를 마주했다."]], [["“Please to leave me.", "\"저를 두고 가 주세요."], ["Say no more tonight.”", "오늘 밤은 더 말하지 마세요.\""]], [["“I feel the urgency of this,”", "\"저는 이 일의 시급함을 느낍니다,\""], ["said Mr Carker, “because it is impossible to say what unforeseen consequences might arise, or how soon, from your being unacquainted with his state of mind.", "카커 씨가 말했다, \"부인께서 그분의 마음 상태를 모르고 계신 데서, 어떤 예기치 못한 결과가, 또 얼마나 빨리 일어날지 알 수 없기 때문입니다."], ["I understand Miss Dombey is concerned, now, at the dismissal of her old servant, which is likely to have been a minor consequence in itself.", "돔비 아가씨가 지금, 자기의 오랜 하녀가 쫓겨난 일로 마음 아파하고 있다고 알고 있습니다. 그 자체로는 사소한 결과였을지 모르나 말이지요."], ["You don’t blame me for requesting that Miss Dombey might not be present.", "돔비 아가씨가 자리에 없게 해 달라고 청한 것을 부인께서는 나무라지 않으시겠지요."], ["May I hope so?”", "그러기를 바라도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“I do not.", "\"나무라지 않습니다."], ["Please to leave me, Sir.”", "저를 두고 가 주세요, 선생.\""]], [["“I knew that your regard for the young lady, which is very sincere and strong, I am well persuaded, would render it a great unhappiness to you, ever to be a prey to the reflection that you had injured her position and ruined her future hopes,”", "\"그 젊은 아가씨에 대한 부인의 애정이, 제가 확신컨대 매우 진실하고 강하기에, 부인께서 그 아가씨의 처지를 해치고 그 미래의 희망을 망쳐 버렸다는 생각에 시달리게 되는 것은 부인께 크나큰 불행이 되리라는 것을 저는 알았습니다,\""], ["said Carker hurriedly, but eagerly.", "카커가 서둘렀으나 간절하게 말했다."]], [["“No more tonight.", "\"오늘 밤은 더는 안 됩니다."], ["Leave me, if you please.”", "부디 저를 두고 가세요.\""]], [["“I shall be here constantly in my attendance upon him, and in the transaction of business matters.", "\"저는 그분을 시중드는 일로, 또 업무를 처리하는 일로 늘 이곳에 와 있을 것입니다."], ["You will allow me to see you again, and to consult what should be done, and learn your wishes?”", "부인께서 다시 뵙도록, 그리고 어찌해야 할지를 의논하고 부인의 뜻을 알도록 허락해 주시겠지요?\""]], [["She motioned him towards the door.", "그녀는 문 쪽을 가리켰다."]], [["“I cannot even decide whether to tell him I have spoken to you yet; or to lead him to suppose that I have deferred doing so, for want of opportunity, or for any other reason.", "\"제가 부인께 말씀을 나누었다는 것을 그분께 아직 알릴지, 아니면 기회가 없어서, 또는 다른 어떤 이유로 그러기를 미루었다고 그분이 여기도록 할지조차 저는 결정하지 못하겠습니다."], ["It will be necessary that you should enable me to consult with you very soon.”", "부인께서 저로 하여금 아주 빠른 시일 안에 부인과 의논할 수 있게 해 주셔야 하겠습니다.\""]], [["“At any time but now,”", "\"지금만 아니라면 언제든지요,\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“You will understand, when I wish to see you, that Miss Dombey is not to be present; and that I seek an interview as one who has the happiness to possess your confidence, and who comes to render you every assistance in his power, and, perhaps, on many occasions, to ward off evil from her?”", "\"제가 부인을 뵙고자 할 때는, 돔비 아가씨가 자리에 없어야 한다는 것을, 그리고 제가, 부인의 신임을 누리는 영광을 가진 사람으로서, 또 제 힘이 닿는 모든 도움을 부인께 드리러, 그리고 어쩌면 여러 경우에 그 아가씨에게서 화를 막아 드리러 오는 사람으로서 만남을 청한다는 것을 부인께서는 헤아려 주시겠지요?\""]], [["Looking at him still with the same apparent dread of releasing him for a moment from the influence of her steady gaze, whatever that might be, she answered, “Yes!” and once more bade him go.", "그녀는 그 흔들림 없는 눈길의 영향이 무엇이든 간에, 한순간이라도 그 영향에서 그를 풀어 주기를 두려워하는 듯한 똑같은 모습으로 여전히 그를 바라보면서 \"그러지요!\"라고 대답하고는, 다시 한번 그에게 가라고 일렀다."]], [["He bowed, as if in compliance; but turning back, when he had nearly reached the door, said:", "그는 따르는 듯 고개를 숙였으나, 문에 거의 다다랐을 때 돌아서서 말했다."]], [["“I am forgiven, and have explained my fault.", "\"저는 용서받았고, 제 잘못을 해명했습니다."], ["May I—for Miss Dombey’s sake, and for my own—take your hand before I go?”", "돔비 아가씨를 위해서, 그리고 저 자신을 위해서—가기 전에 부인의 손을 잡아 보아도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["She gave him the gloved hand she had maimed last night.", "그녀는 어젯밤 자기가 상하게 한 그 장갑 낀 손을 그에게 내밀었다."], ["He took it in one of his, and kissed it, and withdrew.", "그는 그것을 한 손에 쥐고 입을 맞춘 뒤 물러났다."], ["And when he had closed the door, he waved the hand with which he had taken hers, and thrust it in his breast.", "그리고 문을 닫고 나서, 그녀의 손을 잡았던 그 손을 흔들어 보이고는, 그것을 자기 가슴에 갖다 넣었다."]], [["Edith saw no one that night, but locked her door, and kept herself alone.", "이디스는 그날 밤 아무도 만나지 않고, 문을 잠근 채 홀로 있었다."]], [["She did not weep; she showed no greater agitation, outwardly, than when she was riding home.", "그녀는 울지 않았다. 겉으로는 집으로 마차를 타고 올 때보다 더한 동요를 보이지 않았다."], ["She laid as proud a head upon her pillow as she had borne in her carriage; and her prayer ran thus:", "그녀는 마차 안에서 떠받치던 만큼이나 도도한 머리를 베개에 누였고, 그녀의 기도는 이러했다."]], [["“May this man be a liar!", "\"이 사람이 거짓말쟁이이게 하소서!"], ["For if he has spoken truth, she is lost to me, and I have no hope left!”", "만일 그가 진실을 말했다면, 그 애는 저에게서 사라진 것이며, 저에게는 더 이상 희망이 남아 있지 않으니까요!\""]], [["This man, meanwhile, went home musing to bed, thinking, with a dainty pleasure, how imperious her passion was, how she had sat before him in her beauty, with the dark eyes that had never turned away but once; how the white down had fluttered; how the bird’s feathers had been strewn upon the ground.", "한편 이 사람은 잠자리로 향하며 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼는데, 그녀의 격정이 얼마나 도도했는지, 그녀가 단 한 번을 빼고는 결코 돌리지 않던 그 검은 눈으로 자기 앞에 아름답게 앉아 있던 모습을, 그 흰 솜털이 어떻게 떨렸는지, 그 새의 깃털이 어떻게 바닥에 흩뿌려졌는지를 곱씹으며 야릇한 쾌감에 젖어 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVI. Recognizant and Reflective", "# CHAPTER XLVI. Recognizant and Reflective"]], [["Among sundry minor alterations in Mr Carker’s life and habits that began to take place at this time, none was more remarkable than the extraordinary diligence with which he applied himself to business, and the closeness with which he investigated every detail that the affairs of the House laid open to him.", "이 무렵 카커 씨의 생활과 습관에 일어나기 시작한 자잘한 변화들 가운데, 그가 사업에 매달리는 그 비상한 근면함과, 회사의 업무가 그에게 드러내 보이는 모든 세부 사항을 파고드는 그 철저함만큼 두드러진 것은 없었다."], ["Always active and penetrating in such matters, his lynx-eyed vigilance now increased twenty-fold.", "그런 일에서 늘 활동적이고 예리했던 그였지만, 이제 그 스라소니 같은 경계심은 스무 배로 늘어났다."], ["Not only did his weary watch keep pace with every present point that every day presented to him in some new form, but in the midst of these engrossing occupations he found leisure—that is, he made it—to review the past transactions of the Firm, and his share in them, during a long series of years.", "그의 지칠 줄 모르는 감시는 날마다 새로운 형태로 그에게 닥쳐오는 현재의 온갖 사안과 보조를 맞추었을 뿐 아니라, 이렇게 사람을 온통 사로잡는 일들 한가운데서도 그는 짬을 냈다—아니, 짬을 만들어 냈다—회사가 지난 오랜 세월 동안 벌여 온 거래들과, 그 속에서 자기가 맡아 온 몫을 되짚어 보기 위해서였다."], ["Frequently when the clerks were all gone, the offices dark and empty, and all similar places of business shut up, Mr Carker, with the whole anatomy of the iron room laid bare before him, would explore the mysteries of books and papers, with the patient progress of a man who was dissecting the minutest nerves and fibres of his subject.", "점원들이 모두 가 버리고, 사무실은 어둡고 텅 비었으며, 비슷한 영업소들도 죄다 문을 닫은 뒤에, 카커 씨는 철제 방의 해부 구조를 송두리째 자기 앞에 펼쳐 놓은 채, 자기 대상의 가장 미세한 신경과 섬유까지 해부해 가는 사람의 끈기 있는 진척으로 장부와 서류의 비밀들을 탐색하곤 했다."], ["Perch, the messenger, who usually remained on these occasions, to entertain himself with the perusal of the Price Current by the light of one candle, or to doze over the fire in the outer office, at the imminent risk every moment of diving head foremost into the coal-box, could not withhold the tribute of his admiration from this zealous conduct, although it much contracted his domestic enjoyments; and again, and again, expatiated to Mrs Perch (now nursing twins) on the industry and acuteness of their managing gentleman in the City.", "이런 때면 대개 남아 있던 전령 퍼치는 촛불 하나에 의지해 시세표를 읽으며 시간을 보내거나, 바깥 사무실에서 난롯불 앞에 졸다가 시시각각 석탄 통 속으로 머리부터 처박힐 위험을 무릅쓰곤 했는데, 비록 그 때문에 가정의 즐거움이 적잖이 줄어들기는 했어도, 이 열성적인 처신에 감탄의 헌사를 바치지 않을 수 없었다. 그리하여 그는 (이제 쌍둥이에게 젖을 먹이고 있는) 퍼치 부인에게, 자기네 시티의 지배인 양반이 얼마나 부지런하고 명민한지를 거듭거듭 늘어놓았다."]], [["The same increased and sharp attention that Mr Carker bestowed on the business of the House, he applied to his own personal affairs.", "카커 씨가 회사 업무에 쏟는 그 늘어나고 날카로워진 주의력을, 그는 자기 개인의 일에도 똑같이 적용했다."], ["Though not a partner in the concern—a distinction hitherto reserved solely to inheritors of the great name of Dombey—he was in the receipt of some percentage on its dealings; and, participating in all its facilities for the employment of money to advantage, was considered, by the minnows among the tritons of the East, a rich man.", "비록 그가 그 사업의 동업자는 아니었지만—그 명예는 지금껏 오로지 위대한 돔비라는 이름의 상속자들에게만 주어진 것이었다—그는 회사 거래에서 얼마간의 보합을 받고 있었고, 돈을 유리하게 굴릴 수 있는 회사의 온갖 편의를 함께 누렸기에, 동방의 트리톤들 사이에 끼인 송사리들 눈에는 부자로 여겨졌다."], ["It began to be said, among these shrewd observers, that Jem Carker, of Dombey’s, was looking about him to see what he was worth; and that he was calling in his money at a good time, like the long-headed fellow he was; and bets were even offered on the Stock Exchange that Jem was going to marry a rich widow.", "이 약삭빠른 관찰자들 사이에서는, 돔비 회사의 젬 카커가 자기 재산이 얼마나 되는지 둘러보고 있다는 둥, 빈틈없는 사내답게 좋은 때를 골라 돈을 거둬들이고 있다는 둥 하는 말이 나돌기 시작했다. 심지어 증권 거래소에서는 젬이 부유한 과부와 결혼하려 한다는 데 내기를 거는 일까지 있었다."]], [["Yet these cares did not in the least interfere with Mr Carker’s watching of his chief, or with his cleanness, neatness, sleekness, or any cat-like quality he possessed.", "그러나 이런 신경 쓸 일들도 카커 씨가 자기 우두머리를 살피는 일을, 또 그의 청결함이며 단정함이며 매끈함이며 그가 지닌 그 고양이 같은 온갖 특성을 조금도 방해하지 못했다."], ["It was not so much that there was a change in him, in reference to any of his habits, as that the whole man was intensified.", "그것은 그의 어떤 습관에 변화가 생겼다기보다, 그 사람 전체가 한층 강렬해졌다고 하는 편이 옳았다."], ["Everything that had been observable in him before, was observable now, but with a greater amount of concentration.", "전에 그에게서 눈에 띄었던 모든 것이 지금도 눈에 띄었으되, 다만 더 큰 집중의 양을 띠고 있었다."], ["He did each single thing, as if he did nothing else—a pretty certain indication in a man of that range of ability and purpose, that he is doing something which sharpens and keeps alive his keenest powers.", "그는 무슨 일이든, 마치 그것 말고는 아무 일도 하지 않는 것처럼 하나하나 해치웠다—그 정도의 능력과 목적을 지닌 사람에게서 이는, 자기의 가장 예리한 능력을 벼리고 살려 두는 어떤 일을 하고 있다는 꽤 확실한 표시였다."]], [["The only decided alteration in him was, that as he rode to and fro along the streets, he would fall into deep fits of musing, like that in which he had come away from Mr Dombey’s house, on the morning of that gentleman’s disaster.", "그에게 일어난 단 하나의 뚜렷한 변화는, 거리를 따라 이리저리 말을 타고 갈 때면, 그가 깊은 명상에 빠져들곤 한다는 것이었다. 그것은 돔비 씨가 변을 당한 그날 아침, 그 신사의 집에서 나오며 빠져들었던 바로 그런 명상이었다."], ["At such times, he would keep clear of the obstacles in his way, mechanically; and would appear to see and hear nothing until arrival at his destination, or some sudden chance or effort roused him.", "이런 때면 그는 기계적으로 앞에 놓인 장애물을 피해 갔고, 목적지에 다다르거나, 무슨 갑작스러운 우연이나 충격이 그를 일깨우기 전까지는 아무것도 보지도 듣지도 못하는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["Walking his white-legged horse thus, to the counting-house of Dombey and Son one day, he was as unconscious of the observation of two pairs of women’s eyes, as of the fascinated orbs of Rob the Grinder, who, in waiting a street’s length from the appointed place, as a demonstration of punctuality, vainly touched and retouched his hat to attract attention, and trotted along on foot, by his master’s side, prepared to hold his stirrup when he should alight.", "어느 날 흰 다리의 말을 이렇게 타고 돔비와 아들 회사의 회계 사무소로 가던 그는, 어느 두 쌍의 여자 눈이 자기를 지켜보고 있다는 것을, 또 그라인더 롭의 홀린 듯한 눈동자도 의식하지 못했다. 롭은 시간 엄수를 과시하느라 약속된 장소에서 한 거리쯤 떨어진 곳에서 기다리다가, 주인의 주의를 끌려고 헛되이 모자를 만지작거리고 또 만지작거렸으며, 주인이 말에서 내릴 때 등자를 잡아 줄 채비를 한 채 주인 곁에서 종종걸음으로 따라 걸었다."]], [["“See where he goes!”", "\"저기 가는 것 좀 봐!\" 이 두 여자 가운데 하나가 외쳤다."], ["cried one of these two women, an old creature, who stretched out her shrivelled arm to point him out to her companion, a young woman, who stood close beside her, withdrawn like herself into a gateway.", "늙은 여자였는데, 쪼글쪼글한 팔을 뻗어 곁에 바싹 붙어 선 동행에게 그를 가리켜 보였다. 동행은 젊은 여자였고, 자기처럼 어느 출입문 안으로 물러나 있었다."]], [["Mrs Brown’s daughter looked out, at this bidding on the part of Mrs Brown; and there were wrath and vengeance in her face.", "브라운 부인의 딸은, 브라운 부인의 이런 지시에 따라 밖을 내다보았다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 분노와 복수심이 어려 있었다."]], [["“I never thought to look at him again,”", "\"저 사람을 다시 볼 일은 없을 줄 알았는데,\""], ["she said, in a low voice; “but it’s well I should, perhaps.", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"하지만 보는 편이 나을지도 모르지, 어쩌면."], ["I see.", "보이네."], ["I see!”", "보여!\""]], [["“Not changed!”", "\"안 변했지!\""], ["said the old woman, with a look of eager malice.", "늙은 여자가 게걸스러운 악의의 빛을 띠고 말했다."]], [["“He changed!”", "\"저 사람이 변해!\""], ["returned the other.", "다른 여자가 받아쳤다."], ["“What for?", "\"뭐 때문에?"], ["What has he suffered?", "저 사람이 무슨 고생을 했다고?"], ["There is change enough for twenty in me.", "스무 사람 몫의 변화가 내 안에 있어."], ["Isn’t that enough?”", "그걸로 충분하지 않아?\""]], [["“See where he goes!”", "\"저기 가는 것 좀 봐!\""], ["muttered the old woman, watching her daughter with her red eyes; “so easy and so trim a-horseback, while we are in the mud.”", "늙은 여자가 붉은 눈으로 딸을 지켜보며 중얼거렸다. \"저렇게 편안하고 말끔하게 말 위에 앉아 있는데, 우리는 진창 속에 있구나.\""]], [["“And of it,”", "\"진창 그 자체지,\""], ["said her daughter impatiently.", "딸이 안달하며 말했다."], ["“We are mud, underneath his horse’s feet.", "\"우린 저 사람 말발굽 밑의 진흙이야."], ["What should we be?”", "우리가 뭐가 더 되겠어?\""]], [["In the intentness with which she looked after him again, she made a hasty gesture with her hand when the old woman began to reply, as if her view could be obstructed by mere sound.", "다시 그를 바라보는 그 골똘함 속에서, 늙은 여자가 대꾸하려 들자 그녀는 마치 소리 따위에 시야가 가려지기라도 하는 양 손으로 황급히 제지하는 시늉을 했다."], ["Her mother watching her, and not him, remained silent; until her kindling glance subsided, and she drew a long breath, as if in the relief of his being gone.", "어머니는 그가 아니라 딸을 지켜보며 잠자코 있었다. 마침내 딸의 타오르던 눈빛이 가라앉고, 그가 가 버린 것에 안도한 듯 길게 숨을 내쉴 때까지."]], [["“Deary!”", "\"얘야!\""], ["said the old woman then.", "그제야 늙은 여자가 말했다."], ["“Alice!", "\"앨리스!"], ["Handsome gall Ally!”", "잘난 우리 앨리!\""], ["She gently shook her sleeve to arouse her attention.", "그녀는 딸의 주의를 끌려고 소맷자락을 살그머니 흔들었다."], ["“Will you let him go like that, when you can wring money from him?", "\"저 사람한테서 돈을 짜낼 수 있는데, 저렇게 그냥 보내 버릴 셈이냐?"], ["Why, it’s a wickedness, my daughter.”", "아니, 그건 죄악이야, 내 딸아.\""]], [["“Haven’t I told you, that I will not have money from him?”", "\"내가 저 사람한테서 돈을 받지 않겠다고 말하지 않았어요?\""], ["she returned.", "그녀가 받아쳤다."], ["“And don’t you yet believe me?", "\"아직도 내 말을 못 믿어요?"], ["Did I take his sister’s money?", "내가 저 사람 누이의 돈을 받았어요?"], ["Would I touch a penny, if I knew it, that had gone through his white hands—unless it was, indeed, that I could poison it, and send it back to him?", "저 사람의 그 흰 손을 거쳐 온 줄 알면서 단돈 한 푼이라도 손댈 것 같아요?—정말로 거기에 독을 넣어서 도로 저 사람한테 돌려보낼 수 있는 거라면 또 모를까."], ["Peace, mother, and come away.”", "조용히 해요, 어머니, 그리고 갑시다.\""]], [["“And him so rich?”", "\"그런데 저 사람은 저렇게 부자고?\""], ["murmured the old woman.", "늙은 여자가 중얼거렸다."], ["“And us so poor!”", "\"우리는 이렇게 가난한데!\""]], [["“Poor in not being able to pay him any of the harm we owe him,” returned her daughter.", "\"우리가 저 사람한테 갚아야 할 해코지를 조금도 갚아 줄 수 없으니 가난하지,\""], ["“Let him give me that sort of riches, and I’ll take them from him, and use them.", "딸이 받아쳤다. \"저 사람이 나한테 그런 종류의 재물을 준다면, 받아서 쓰겠어요."], ["Come away.", "갑시다."], ["Its no good looking at his horse.", "저 사람 말이나 본다고 무슨 소용 있어요."], ["Come away, mother!”", "가자고요, 어머니!\""]], [["But the old woman, for whom the spectacle of Rob the Grinder returning down the street, leading the riderless horse, appeared to have some extraneous interest that it did not possess in itself, surveyed that young man with the utmost earnestness; and seeming to have whatever doubts she entertained, resolved as he drew nearer, glanced at her daughter with brightened eyes and with her finger on her lip, and emerging from the gateway at the moment of his passing, touched him on the shoulder.", "그러나 그라인더 롭이 주인 없는 말을 끌고 거리를 도로 내려오는 광경에, 그 자체에는 없는 어떤 외적인 흥미라도 느낀 듯한 늙은 여자는, 그 젊은이를 더없이 진지하게 살펴보았다. 그러더니 마음에 품고 있던 의심이 무엇이든 그가 가까이 올수록 풀리는 듯, 두 눈을 빛내며 손가락을 입술에 댄 채 딸을 흘끗 보고는, 그가 지나가는 바로 그 순간에 출입문에서 나와 그의 어깨를 두드렸다."]], [["“Why, where’s my sprightly Rob been, all this time!”", "\"아니, 우리 발랄한 롭은 그동안 내내 어디 가 있었누!\""], ["she said, as he turned round.", "그가 돌아서자 그녀가 말했다."]], [["The sprightly Rob, whose sprightliness was very much diminished by the salutation, looked exceedingly dismayed, and said, with the water rising in his eyes:", "그 발랄한 롭은, 이 인사에 발랄함이 어지간히 줄어든 채, 몹시 낭패한 얼굴로 눈에 물기가 차오르며 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아이고!"], ["why can’t you leave a poor cove alone, Misses Brown, when he’s getting an honest livelihood and conducting himself respectable?", "가난한 녀석이 정직하게 밥벌이하면서 점잖게 처신하고 있는데, 좀 가만 내버려 둘 순 없소, 브라운 부인?"], ["What do you come and deprive a cove of his character for, by talking to him in the streets, when he’s taking his master’s horse to a honest stable—a horse you’d go and sell for cats’ and dogs’ meat if you had your way!", "녀석이 주인 말을 정직한 마구간으로 데려가는 중인데, 길거리에서 말을 걸어 녀석한테서 평판을 앗아가는 건 또 뭐요—당신 마음대로 했으면 고양이며 개 먹이로 팔아넘겼을 그런 말을 말이오!"], ["Why, I thought,”", "아니, 난,\""], ["said the Grinder, producing his concluding remark as if it were the climax of all his injuries, “that you was dead long ago!”", "그라인더는 마치 자기가 입은 온갖 피해의 절정이라도 되는 양 마지막 말을 꺼내며 말했다, \"당신이 진작에 죽은 줄로만 알았소!\""]], [["“This is the way,”", "\"이것 봐라,\""], ["cried the old woman, appealing to her daughter, “that he talks to me, who knew him weeks and months together, my deary, and have stood his friend many and many a time among the pigeon-fancying tramps and bird-catchers.”", "늙은 여자가 딸에게 호소하며 외쳤다, \"이게 나한테 말하는 꼴이라니, 몇 주 몇 달을 같이 알고 지냈고, 얘야, 비둘기 좋아하는 떠돌이며 새잡이들 틈에서 여러 번 이 녀석 편을 들어 줬던 나한테 말이다.\""]], [["“Let the birds be, will you, Misses Brown?”", "\"새 얘긴 그만 좀 하시오, 브라운 부인?\""], ["retorted Rob, in a tone of the acutest anguish.", "롭이 더없이 고통스러운 어조로 받아쳤다."], ["“I think a cove had better have to do with lions than them little creeturs, for they’re always flying back in your face when you least expect it.", "\"녀석 생각엔 그런 쪼그만 것들보다는 차라리 사자를 상대하는 편이 낫겠소, 그것들은 당신이 가장 방심한 순간에 늘 얼굴로 도로 날아드니까."], ["Well, how d’ye do and what do you want?”", "자, 안녕하시오, 그래 뭘 바라는 거요?\""], ["These polite inquiries the Grinder uttered, as it were under protest, and with great exasperation and vindictiveness.", "그라인더는 이 정중한 안부를, 마치 마지못해서인 듯, 그리고 잔뜩 분개하고 앙심을 품은 채 내뱉었다."]], [["“Hark how he speaks to an old friend, my deary!”", "\"옛 친구한테 말하는 꼴 좀 들어 봐라, 얘야!\""], ["said Mrs Brown, again appealing to her daughter.", "브라운 부인이 다시 딸에게 호소하며 말했다."], ["“But there’s some of his old friends not so patient as me.", "\"하지만 이 녀석 옛 친구들 중엔 나만큼 참을성 있는 사람들이 아닌 이들도 있지."], ["If I was to tell some that he knows, and has spotted and cheated with, where to find him—”", "내가 만약 이 녀석이 아는 사람들, 함께 점찍어 두고 사기 쳤던 그런 자들한테 이 녀석이 어디 있는지 일러바치기라도 한다면—\""]], [["“Will you hold your tongue, Misses Brown?”", "\"입 좀 다물 수 없소, 브라운 부인?\""], ["interrupted the miserable Grinder, glancing quickly round, as though he expected to see his master’s teeth shining at his elbow.", "비참한 그라인더가, 마치 주인의 이가 자기 팔꿈치 옆에서 번뜩이는 걸 볼 거라 예상하기라도 한 듯 황급히 주위를 둘러보며 끼어들었다."], ["“What do you take a pleasure in ruining a cove for?", "\"녀석을 파멸시키는 게 뭐가 그리 즐겁소?"], ["At your time of life too!", "그 연세에!"], ["when you ought to be thinking of a variety of things!”", "이런저런 일들을 생각하셔야 할 나이에 말이오!\""]], [["“What a gallant horse!”", "\"참 멋진 말이로구나!\""], ["said the old woman, patting the animal’s neck.", "늙은 여자가 짐승의 목을 토닥이며 말했다."]], [["“Let him alone, will you, Misses Brown?”", "\"그냥 좀 놔두시오, 브라운 부인?\""], ["cried Rob, pushing away her hand.", "롭이 그녀의 손을 밀어내며 외쳤다."], ["“You’re enough to drive a penitent cove mad!”", "\"당신은 회개한 녀석을 미치게 만들 작정이오!\""]], [["“Why, what hurt do I do him, child?”", "\"아니, 내가 이 녀석한테 무슨 해를 끼친다고 그러냐, 얘야?\""], ["returned the old woman.", "늙은 여자가 받아쳤다."]], [["“Hurt?” said Rob.", "\"해라뇨?\""], ["“He’s got a master that would find it out if he was touched with a straw.”", "롭이 말했다. \"지푸라기 하나라도 닿으면 알아챌 주인을 둔 녀석이오.\""], ["And he blew upon the place where the old woman’s hand had rested for a moment, and smoothed it gently with his finger, as if he seriously believed what he said.", "그러고는 늙은 여자의 손이 잠시 닿았던 자리에 입김을 불고, 손가락으로 부드럽게 쓸어 주었는데, 마치 자기가 한 말을 진심으로 믿는 듯했다."]], [["The old woman looking back to mumble and mouth at her daughter, who followed, kept close to Rob’s heels as he walked on with the bridle in his hand; and pursued the conversation.", "늙은 여자는 뒤따라오는 딸을 향해 우물거리고 입을 놀려 가며 뒤를 돌아보면서도, 롭이 고삐를 손에 쥐고 걸어가는 동안 그의 발꿈치에 바짝 붙어 따라가며 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“A good place, Rob, eh?”", "\"좋은 자리지, 롭, 응?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You’re in luck, my child.”", "\"운이 좋구나, 내 새끼.\""]], [["“Oh don’t talk about luck, Misses Brown,”", "\"아이고, 운 얘긴 마시오, 브라운 부인,\""], ["returned the wretched Grinder, facing round and stopping.", "비참한 그라인더가 휙 돌아서서 멈춰 서며 받아쳤다."], ["“If you’d never come, or if you’d go away, then indeed a cove might be considered tolerable lucky.", "\"당신이 아예 안 왔거나, 가 버리기만 한다면, 그땐 정말 녀석이 그럭저럭 운 좋다고 할 수 있겠지."], ["Can’t you go along, Misses Brown, and not foller me!”", "좀 갈 수 없소, 브라운 부인, 날 따라오지 말고!\""], ["blubbered Rob, with sudden defiance.", "롭이 별안간 반항하며 흐느꼈다."], ["“If the young woman’s a friend of yours, why don’t she take you away, instead of letting you make yourself so disgraceful!”", "\"저 젊은 여자가 당신 친구라면, 당신이 이렇게 망신스럽게 굴게 놔두지 말고 데려가는 게 어떻소!\""]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["croaked the old woman, putting her face close to his, with a malevolent grin upon it that puckered up the loose skin down in her very throat.", "늙은 여자가 얼굴을 그의 얼굴에 바싹 들이대며 악에 받친 히죽거림을 지었는데, 그것이 목 아래까지 늘어진 살갗을 온통 주름지게 했다."], ["“Do you deny your old chum!", "\"네가 옛 동무를 모른 척해?"], ["Have you lurked to my house fifty times, and slept sound in a corner when you had no other bed but the paving-stones, and do you talk to me like this!", "다른 잠자리라곤 포장석밖에 없을 때 내 집에 쉰 번이나 숨어들어 구석에서 곤히 잤으면서, 나한테 이렇게 말한단 말이냐?"], ["Have I bought and sold with you, and helped you in my way of business, schoolboy, sneak, and what not, and do you tell me to go along?", "너랑 같이 사고팔고, 내 장사 방식대로 너를 도와줬건만, 이 학생 녀석아, 좀도둑 같은 놈아, 별의별 짓을 다 했는데, 나더러 가라고 해?"], ["Could I raise a crowd of old company about you to-morrow morning, that would follow you to ruin like copies of your own shadow, and do you turn on me with your bold looks!", "내일 아침이면 너를 둘러싸고 네 그림자 복사판처럼 너를 파멸로 따라다닐 옛 패거리를 불러 모을 수도 있는 나한테, 그 뻔뻔한 낯짝으로 대들어?"], ["I’ll go.", "가마."], ["Come, Alice.”", "가자, 앨리스.\""]], [["“Stop, Misses Brown!”", "\"멈춰요, 브라운 부인!\""], ["cried the distracted Grinder.", "정신이 나간 그라인더가 외쳤다."], ["“What are you doing of?", "\"뭣들 하는 거요?"], ["Don’t put yourself in a passion!", "화내지 마시오!"], ["Don’t let her go, if you please.", "제발 저 사람 보내지 말아요."], ["I haven’t meant any offence.", "난 아무 무례도 끼칠 생각 없었소."], ["I said ‘how d’ye do,’ at first, didn’t I?", "처음에 '안녕하시오' 하고 인사했잖소?"], ["But you wouldn’t answer.", "그런데 당신이 대답을 안 했지."], ["How you do?", "안녕하시오?"], ["Besides,”", "게다가,\""], ["said Rob piteously, “look here!", "롭이 가련하게 말했다, \"이것 좀 보쇼!"], ["How can a cove stand talking in the street with his master’s prad a-wanting to be took to be rubbed down, and his master up to every individgle thing that happens!”", "녀석이 주인 말을 끌고 가서 솔질을 시켜야 하는데, 게다가 주인은 일어나는 일 하나하나를 다 꿰고 있는데, 길거리에 서서 이야기나 하고 있을 수 있겠소!\""]], [["The old woman made a show of being partially appeased, but shook her head, and mouthed and muttered still.", "늙은 여자는 어느 정도 화가 누그러진 시늉을 했지만, 여전히 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 입을 놀리고 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Come along to the stables, and have a glass of something that’s good for you, Misses Brown, can’t you?”", "\"마구간으로 같이 가서, 당신 몸에 좋은 걸로 한 잔 들지 그러시오, 브라운 부인?\" 롭이 말했다."], ["said Rob, “instead of going on, like that, which is no good to you, nor anybody else.", "\"그렇게 떼쓰지 말고 말이오, 그래 봤자 당신한테도 누구한테도 좋을 거 없소."], ["Come along with her, will you be so kind?” said Rob.", "같이 갑시다, 좀 그래 주겠소?\""], ["“I’m sure I’m delighted to see her, if it wasn’t for the horse!”", "롭이 말했다. \"말만 아니었으면, 정말이지 만나서 반갑소!\""]], [["With this apology, Rob turned away, a rueful picture of despair, and walked his charge down a bye street.", "이 변명과 함께, 롭은 절망의 처량한 그림처럼 돌아서서, 자기가 맡은 말을 골목길로 끌고 내려갔다."], ["The old woman, mouthing at her daughter, followed close upon him.", "늙은 여자는 딸을 향해 입을 놀려 가며 그의 뒤를 바짝 따랐다."], ["The daughter followed.", "딸도 뒤따랐다."]], [["Turning into a silent little square or court-yard that had a great church tower rising above it, and a packer’s warehouse, and a bottle-maker’s warehouse, for its places of business, Rob the Grinder delivered the white-legged horse to the hostler of a quaint stable at the corner; and inviting Mrs Brown and her daughter to seat themselves upon a stone bench at the gate of that establishment, soon reappeared from a neighbouring public-house with a pewter measure and a glass.", "위로 커다란 교회 탑이 솟아 있고, 영업소라고는 포장업자 창고와 병 제조업자 창고뿐인 어느 조용한 작은 광장, 아니 안마당으로 접어들어, 그라인더 롭은 모퉁이에 있는 예스러운 마구간의 마부에게 흰 다리의 말을 넘겼다. 그러고는 브라운 부인과 그 딸에게 그 마구간 문간의 돌 의자에 앉으라고 청하고는, 곧 이웃 술집에서 백랍 됫박과 잔을 들고 다시 나타났다."]], [["“Here’s master—Mr Carker, child!”", "\"여기 주인 양반—카커 씨를 위하여다, 얘야!\""], ["said the old woman, slowly, as her sentiment before drinking.", "늙은 여자가, 술 마시기 전의 건배사인 양 천천히 말했다."], ["“Lord bless him!”", "\"그분께 신의 축복을!\""]], [["“Why, I didn’t tell you who he was,”", "\"아니, 난 그분이 누군지 말한 적 없는데,\""], ["observed Rob, with staring eyes.", "롭이 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 말했다."]], [["“We know him by sight,”", "\"우리는 얼굴은 알지,\""], ["said Mrs Brown, whose working mouth and nodding head stopped for the moment, in the fixedness of her attention.", "브라운 부인이 말했다. 그 우물거리는 입과 끄덕이는 머리가 골똘한 주의의 고정 속에 잠시 멈추었다."], ["“We saw him pass this morning, afore he got off his horse; when you were ready to take it.”", "\"오늘 아침에 그분이 말에서 내리기 전에 지나가는 걸 봤다. 네가 말을 받으려고 채비하고 있을 때 말이야.\""]], [["“Ay, ay,”", "\"아이, 아이,\""], ["returned Rob, appearing to wish that his readiness had carried him to any other place.—“What’s the matter with her?", "롭이 받아쳤다, 자기의 그 채비성이 차라리 다른 데로 데려다주었더라면 싶은 표정이었다.—\"이 여자는 왜 이러오?"], ["Won’t she drink?”", "안 마시려는 거요?\""]], [["This inquiry had reference to Alice, who, folded in her cloak, sat a little apart, profoundly inattentive to his offer of the replenished glass.", "이 물음은 앨리스를 두고 한 것이었다. 그녀는 망토를 두른 채 조금 떨어져 앉아, 그가 다시 채워 내미는 잔에는 도통 관심을 두지 않았다."]], [["The old woman shook her head.", "늙은 여자는 고개를 저었다."], ["“Don’t mind her,”", "\"신경 쓰지 마라,\""], ["she said; “she’s a strange creetur, if you know’d her, Rob.", "그녀가 말했다. \"네가 알고 나면, 별난 애란다, 롭."], ["But Mr Carker—”", "그건 그렇고 카커 씨는—\""]], [["“Hush!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["said Rob, glancing cautiously up at the packer’s, and at the bottle-maker’s, as if, from any one of the tiers of warehouses, Mr Carker might be looking down.", "롭이 말했다. 마치 그 층층이 쌓인 창고들 어디에선가 카커 씨가 내려다보고 있기라도 한 양, 포장업자 창고와 병 제조업자 창고를 조심스레 올려다보며."], ["“Softly.”", "\"조용히.\""]], [["“Why, he ain’t here!”", "\"아니, 그분은 여기 없잖니!\""], ["cried Mrs Brown.", "브라운 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“I don’t know that,”", "\"그건 모르는 일이오,\""], ["muttered Rob, whose glance even wandered to the church tower, as if he might be there, with a supernatural power of hearing.", "롭이 중얼거렸다. 그의 시선은 교회 탑까지 떠돌았는데, 마치 그가 초자연적인 청력을 지니고 거기 있을지도 모른다는 듯이."]], [["“Good master?”", "\"좋은 주인이냐?\""], ["inquired Mrs Brown.", "브라운 부인이 물었다."]], [["Rob nodded; and added, in a low voice, “precious sharp.”", "롭은 고개를 끄덕이고는, 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"지독하게 예리하지.\""]], [["“Lives out of town, don’t he, lovey?”", "\"도시 밖에서 사시지, 그렇지, 아가?\""], ["said the old woman.", "늙은 여자가 말했다."]], [["“When he’s at home,”", "\"집에 계실 땐 그렇소,\""], ["returned Rob; “but we don’t live at home just now.”", "롭이 받아쳤다. \"하지만 지금은 우리가 집에서 안 살아요.\""]], [["“Where then?”", "\"그럼 어디서?\""], ["asked the old woman.", "늙은 여자가 물었다."]], [["“Lodgings; up near Mr Dombey’s,”", "\"하숙집이오. 돔비 씨 댁 근처에,\""], ["returned Rob.", "롭이 받아쳤다."]], [["The younger woman fixed her eyes so searchingly upon him, and so suddenly, that Rob was quite confounded, and offered the glass again, but with no more effect upon her than before.", "젊은 여자가 어찌나 캐묻듯이, 또 어찌나 느닷없이 그에게 눈길을 박았던지, 롭은 그만 당황해서 다시 잔을 내밀었으나, 전과 마찬가지로 그녀에게는 아무런 효과가 없었다."]], [["“Mr Dombey—you and I used to talk about him, sometimes, you know,”", "\"돔비 씨—당신이랑 나랑 가끔 그분 얘기를 하곤 했잖소, 알지,\""], ["said Rob to Mrs Brown.", "롭이 브라운 부인에게 말했다."], ["“You used to get me to talk about him.”", "\"당신이 나더러 그분 얘기를 하게 만들곤 했지.\""]], [["The old woman nodded.", "늙은 여자가 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“Well, Mr Dombey, he’s had a fall from his horse,”", "\"그게, 돔비 씨가 말에서 떨어지셨소,\" 롭이 마지못해 말했다."], ["said Rob, unwillingly; “and my master has to be up there, more than usual, either with him, or Mrs Dombey, or some of ’em; and so we’ve come to town.”", "\"그래서 우리 주인이 평소보다 더 자주 거기 가 계셔야 하오, 그분하고든, 돔비 부인하고든, 그 집 누구하고든 말이오. 그래서 우리가 도시로 나온 거요.\""]], [["“Are they good friends, lovey?”", "\"그 두 사람은 사이가 좋으냐, 아가?\" 늙은 여자가 물었다."], ["asked the old woman.", "\"누구요?\" 롭이 받아쳤다."]], [["“Who?” retorted Rob. “He and she?”", "\"그 사람이랑 그 여자 말이다.\""]], [["“What, Mr and Mrs Dombey?”", "\"뭐, 돔비 씨랑 돔비 부인 말이오?\""], ["said Rob.", "롭이 말했다."], ["“How should I know!”", "\"내가 그걸 어찌 알겠소!\""]], [["“Not them—Master and Mrs Dombey, chick,”", "\"그들 말고—주인 양반이랑 돔비 부인 말이다, 아가,\""], ["replied the old woman, coaxingly.", "늙은 여자가 어르듯 받았다."]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"모르오,\""], ["said Rob, looking round him again.", "롭이 다시 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“I suppose so.", "\"그렇겠지요."], ["How curious you are, Misses Brown!", "별걸 다 궁금해하시네, 브라운 부인!"], ["Least said, soonest mended.”", "말은 적게 할수록 탈이 적은 법이오.\""]], [["“Why there’s no harm in it!”", "\"아니, 그게 무슨 해가 된다고!\""], ["exclaimed the old woman, with a laugh, and a clap of her hands.", "늙은 여자가 웃음을 터뜨리고 손뼉을 치며 외쳤다."], ["“Sprightly Rob, has grown tame since he has been well off!", "\"발랄한 롭이 형편이 펴니까 순해졌구나!"], ["There’s no harm in it.”", "그게 무슨 해가 된다고.\""]], [["“No, there’s no harm in it, I know,”", "\"그래, 해 될 거 없는 줄 나도 알아요,\""], ["returned Rob, with the same distrustful glance at the packer’s and the bottle-maker’s, and the church; “but blabbing, if it’s only about the number of buttons on my master’s coat, won’t do.", "롭이 포장업자 창고와 병 제조업자 창고, 그리고 교회를 향한 그 미심쩍은 눈길을 다시 보내며 받아쳤다. \"하지만 입을 놀리는 건, 그게 우리 주인 외투 단추 수에 관한 거라 해도 안 될 일이오."], ["I tell you it won’t do with him.", "단언컨대 그분한테는 안 통해요."], ["A cove had better drown himself. He says so.", "차라리 녀석이 물에 빠져 죽는 게 낫지."], ["I shouldn’t have so much as told you what his name was, if you hadn’t known it.", "그분이 그렇게 말하오. 당신이 그분 이름을 이미 알고 있지 않았다면, 그게 뭔지조차 말 안 했을 거요."], ["Talk about somebody else.”", "다른 사람 얘기나 합시다.\""]], [["As Rob took another cautious survey of the yard, the old woman made a secret motion to her daughter.", "롭이 다시 한번 마당을 조심스레 둘러보는 사이, 늙은 여자는 딸에게 은밀히 신호를 보냈다."], ["It was momentary, but the daughter, with a slight look of intelligence, withdrew her eyes from the boy’s face, and sat folded in her cloak as before.", "순간적이었지만, 딸은 알아챘다는 가벼운 기색으로 소년의 얼굴에서 눈을 거두고는, 전처럼 망토에 싸인 채 앉았다."]], [["“Rob, lovey!”", "\"롭, 아가!\""], ["said the old woman, beckoning him to the other end of the bench.", "늙은 여자가 그를 의자 반대쪽 끝으로 손짓해 부르며 말했다."], ["“You were always a pet and favourite of mine.", "\"넌 늘 내가 아끼고 귀여워하던 애였지."], ["Now, weren’t you?", "안 그러냐?"], ["Don’t you know you were?”", "그런 거 알지?\""]], [["“Yes, Misses Brown,”", "\"네, 브라운 부인,\""], ["replied the Grinder, with a very bad grace.", "그라인더가 아주 마지못한 태도로 대답했다."]], [["“And you could leave me!”", "\"그런데도 날 떠날 수 있었구나!\""], ["said the old woman, flinging her arms about his neck.", "늙은 여자가 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안으며 말했다."], ["“You could go away, and grow almost out of knowledge, and never come to tell your poor old friend how fortunate you were, proud lad!", "\"넌 떠나 버려서, 거의 알아보지도 못할 만큼 자라 놓고는, 가엾은 옛 친구한테 네가 얼마나 잘됐는지 말하러 한 번을 안 왔어, 이 잘난 녀석아!"], ["Oho, Oho!”", "어허, 어허!\""]], [["“Oh here’s a dreadful go for a cove that’s got a master wide awake in the neighbourhood!”", "\"아이고, 동네에 빠삭하게 깨어 있는 주인을 둔 녀석한테 이건 정말 끔찍한 일이로구나!\""], ["exclaimed the wretched Grinder.", "비참한 그라인더가 외쳤다."], ["“To be howled over like this here!”", "\"이렇게 통곡 세례를 받다니!\""]], [["“Won’t you come and see me, Robby?”", "\"날 보러 안 올 테냐, 로비?\""], ["cried Mrs Brown.", "브라운 부인이 외쳤다."], ["“Oho, won’t you ever come and see me?”", "\"어허, 날 보러 영영 안 올 테냐?\""]], [["“Yes, I tell you!", "\"온다잖소!"], ["Yes, I will!”", "그래, 가겠소!\""], ["returned the Grinder.", "그라인더가 받아쳤다."]], [["“That’s my own Rob!", "\"그래야 내 롭이지!"], ["That’s my lovey!”", "그래야 내 아가지!\""], ["said Mrs Brown, drying the tears upon her shrivelled face, and giving him a tender squeeze.", "브라운 부인이 쪼글쪼글한 얼굴의 눈물을 닦으며, 그를 다정하게 꼭 끌어안고 말했다."], ["“At the old place, Rob?”", "\"옛날 그 자리로, 롭?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["replied the Grinder.", "그라인더가 대답했다."]], [["“Soon, Robby dear?”", "\"곧이지, 로비야?\""], ["cried Mrs Brown; “and often?”", "브라운 부인이 외쳤다. \"그리고 자주?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Yes.", "네."], ["Yes,”", "네,\""], ["replied Rob.", "롭이 대답했다."], ["“I will indeed, upon my soul and body.”", "\"정말 그러겠소, 내 영혼과 몸을 걸고.\""]], [["“And then,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["said Mrs Brown, with her arms uplifted towards the sky, and her head thrown back and shaking, “if he’s true to his word, I’ll never come a-near him though I know where he is, and never breathe a syllable about him!", "브라운 부인이 두 팔을 하늘로 쳐들고, 머리를 뒤로 젖혀 흔들며 말했다, \"이 녀석이 약속만 지킨다면, 내가 이 녀석이 어디 있는지 알아도 절대로 근처에 안 가고, 이 녀석에 대해선 한 마디도 입 밖에 안 내겠다!"], ["Never!”", "절대로!\""]], [["This ejaculation seemed a drop of comfort to the miserable Grinder, who shook Mrs Brown by the hand upon it, and implored her with tears in his eyes, to leave a cove and not destroy his prospects.", "이 외침이 비참한 그라인더에게는 위안의 한 방울처럼 여겨졌던지, 그는 그 말에 브라운 부인의 손을 잡아 흔들고는, 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 녀석을 좀 놔두고 자기 앞날을 망치지 말아 달라고 그녀에게 애원했다."], ["Mrs Brown, with another fond embrace, assented; but in the act of following her daughter, turned back, with her finger stealthily raised, and asked in a hoarse whisper for some money.", "브라운 부인은 또 한 번 정답게 끌어안으며 그러마고 했다. 그러나 딸의 뒤를 따라가려다가, 손가락을 슬그머니 치켜든 채 돌아서서, 쉰 목소리로 속삭이며 돈을 좀 달라고 청했다."]], [["“A shilling, dear!”", "\"한 실링만, 아가!\""], ["she said, with her eager avaricious face, “or sixpence!", "그녀가 게걸스럽고 탐욕스러운 얼굴로 말했다."], ["For old acquaintance sake.", "\"아니면 육 펜스라도!"], ["I’m so poor.", "옛정을 봐서."], ["And my handsome gal”—looking over her shoulder—“she’s my gal, Rob—half starves me.”", "내가 너무 가난해서. 게다가 내 잘난 딸년이\"—어깨너머로 돌아보며—\"저게 내 딸이다, 롭—날 거의 굶겨 죽인단다.\""]], [["But as the reluctant Grinder put it in her hand, her daughter, coming quietly back, caught the hand in hers, and twisted out the coin.", "그러나 마지못한 그라인더가 그것을 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주는 순간, 딸이 조용히 돌아와 그 손을 자기 손으로 붙잡고는 동전을 비틀어 빼냈다."]], [["“What,”", "\"뭐예요,\""], ["she said, “mother! always money!", "그녀가 말했다, \"어머니!"], ["money from the first, and to the last.", "늘 돈이야! 처음부터 끝까지 돈이라니."], ["Do you mind so little what I said but now?", "내가 방금 한 말을 그렇게도 안 새겨들어요?"], ["Here.", "자."], ["Take it!”", "받아요!\""]], [["The old woman uttered a moan as the money was restored, but without in any other way opposing its restoration, hobbled at her daughter’s side out of the yard, and along the by-street upon which it opened.", "늙은 여자는 돈이 되돌려지자 신음 소리를 냈지만, 그 밖에 어떤 식으로도 그 반환에 맞서지는 않고, 딸의 곁에서 절뚝거리며 마당을 빠져나가, 그 마당이 이어진 골목길을 따라갔다."], ["The astonished and dismayed Rob staring after them, saw that they stopped, and fell to earnest conversation very soon; and more than once observed a darkly threatening action of the younger woman’s hand (obviously having reference to someone of whom they spoke), and a crooning feeble imitation of it on the part of Mrs Brown, that made him earnestly hope he might not be the subject of their discourse.", "놀라고 낭패한 롭은 그들을 멍하니 바라보다가, 그들이 멈춰 서서 이내 진지한 대화에 빠져드는 것을 보았다. 그리고 젊은 여자의 손이 (분명 그들이 이야기하는 누군가를 가리키는) 어둡고 위협적인 동작을 하는 것과, 브라운 부인 쪽에서 그것을 힘없이 흥얼대며 흉내 내는 것을 한두 번이 아니게 목격했는데, 그 때문에 그는 부디 자기가 그들의 화제가 아니기를 간절히 바랐다."]], [["With the present consolation that they were gone, and with the prospective comfort that Mrs Brown could not live for ever, and was not likely to live long to trouble him, the Grinder, not otherwise regretting his misdeeds than as they were attended with such disagreeable incidental consequences, composed his ruffled features to a more serene expression by thinking of the admirable manner in which he had disposed of Captain Cuttle (a reflection that seldom failed to put him in a flow of spirits), and went to the Dombey Counting House to receive his master’s orders.", "그들이 가 버렸다는 당장의 위안과, 브라운 부인이 영원히 살 수는 없으며 자기를 괴롭힐 만큼 오래 살 것 같지도 않다는 앞날의 위안과 함께, 그라인더는—자기 비행을 후회하되 그저 그런 불쾌한 부수적 결과가 따른다는 점에서만 후회하면서—커틀 선장을 처리해 버린 그 감탄할 만한 솜씨를 떠올리며(이 회상은 어김없이 그를 신바람 나게 했다) 헝클어진 표정을 한결 평온한 빛으로 가다듬고는, 주인의 분부를 받으러 돔비 회계 사무소로 갔다."]], [["There his master, so subtle and vigilant of eye, that Rob quaked before him, more than half expecting to be taxed with Mrs Brown, gave him the usual morning’s box of papers for Mr Dombey, and a note for Mrs Dombey: merely nodding his head as an enjoinder to be careful, and to use dispatch—a mysterious admonition, fraught in the Grinder’s imagination with dismal warnings and threats; and more powerful with him than any words.", "거기서 주인은, 눈매가 어찌나 예민하고 빈틈없었던지, 롭은 브라운 부인 일로 추궁당하는 게 아닌가 절반 넘게 예상하며 그 앞에서 벌벌 떨었다. 주인은 그에게 돔비 씨에게 보낼 여느 아침의 서류 상자와, 돔비 부인에게 보낼 쪽지를 건네면서, 그저 조심하고 신속하게 하라는 당부로 고개를 한 번 끄덕였을 뿐이었다—그라인더의 상상 속에서는 음울한 경고와 위협으로 가득 찬 그 알 수 없는 훈계는, 그에게는 어떤 말보다도 더 위력이 있었다."]], [["Alone again, in his own room, Mr Carker applied himself to work, and worked all day.", "다시 혼자가 되어 제 방에 든 카커 씨는 일에 몰두하여 온종일 일했다."], ["He saw many visitors; overlooked a number of documents; went in and out, to and from, sundry places of mercantile resort; and indulged in no more abstraction until the day’s business was done.", "그는 많은 방문객을 만났고, 숱한 서류를 검토했으며, 상인들이 모이는 여러 장소를 들고 났다. 그러고는 그날의 업무가 끝날 때까지 더는 어떤 상념에도 빠지지 않았다."], ["But, when the usual clearance of papers from his table was made at last, he fell into his thoughtful mood once more.", "그러나 마침내 책상에서 여느 때처럼 서류를 다 치우고 나자, 그는 다시금 생각에 잠긴 기분에 빠져들었다."]], [["He was standing in his accustomed place and attitude, with his eyes intently fixed upon the ground, when his brother entered to bring back some letters that had been taken out in the course of the day.", "그가 평소의 자리에 평소의 자세로 서서, 두 눈을 바닥에 골똘히 고정한 채 있을 때, 그의 동생이 그날 동안 가지고 나갔던 편지 몇 통을 도로 가져오려고 들어왔다."], ["He put them quietly on the table, and was going immediately, when Mr Carker the Manager, whose eyes had rested on him, on his entrance, as if they had all this time had him for the subject of their contemplation, instead of the office-floor, said:", "동생은 그것들을 조용히 책상에 놓고 곧장 나가려 했는데, 그때 지배인 카커 씨—그의 두 눈은 동생이 들어올 때부터 그에게 머물러, 마치 사무실 바닥이 아니라 이 시간 내내 그를 사색의 대상으로 삼아 온 것 같았다—가 말했다."]], [["“Well, John Carker, and what brings you here?”", "\"그래, 존 카커, 무슨 일로 여기 왔나?\""]], [["His brother pointed to the letters, and was again withdrawing.", "동생은 편지들을 가리키고는, 다시 물러나려 했다."]], [["“I wonder,”", "\"이상하군,\""], ["said the Manager, “that you can come and go, without inquiring how our master is”.", "지배인이 말했다, \"우리 주인께서 어떠신지 묻지도 않고 오갈 수 있다니.\""]], [["“We had word this morning in the Counting House, that Mr Dombey was doing well,”", "\"오늘 아침 회계 사무소에서, 돔비 씨가 잘 회복하고 계시다는 소식을 들었어,\""], ["replied his brother.", "동생이 대답했다."]], [["“You are such a meek fellow,”", "\"넌 참 온순한 친구지,\""], ["said the Manager, with a smile,—“but you have grown so, in the course of years—that if any harm came to him, you’d be miserable, I dare swear now.”", "지배인이 미소 지으며 말했다.—\"하지만 세월이 흐르면서 그렇게 된 거지만—그분께 무슨 해라도 닥치면, 넌 비참해할 거다, 지금 단언컨대.\""]], [["“I should be truly sorry, James,”", "\"진심으로 슬퍼할 거야, 제임스,\""], ["returned the other.", "다른 사람이 받았다."]], [["“He would be sorry!”", "\"슬퍼하겠지!\""], ["said the Manager, pointing at him, as if there were some other person present to whom he was appealing.", "지배인이, 마치 호소할 다른 누군가가 그 자리에 있기라도 한 양 그를 가리키며 말했다."], ["“He would be truly sorry!", "\"진심으로 슬퍼하겠지!"], ["This brother of mine!", "이 내 동생이 말이야!"], ["This junior of the place, this slighted piece of lumber, pushed aside with his face to the wall, like a rotten picture, and left so, for Heaven knows how many years he’s all gratitude and respect, and devotion too, he would have me believe!”", "이 회사의 말단이, 이 천대받는 폐물 조각이, 썩은 그림짝처럼 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌린 채 옆으로 밀쳐져, 하늘만이 아실 몇 년 동안 그렇게 방치된 이 친구가—그래도 온통 감사와 존경, 게다가 헌신까지 바치고 있다고, 날더러 믿으라는 거지!\""]], [["“I would have you believe nothing, James,”", "\"난 자네더러 아무것도 믿으라고 안 해, 제임스,\""], ["returned the other.", "다른 사람이 받았다."], ["“Be as just to me as you would to any other man below you.", "\"자네보다 아래에 있는 다른 누구에게 하듯, 나한테도 공정하게 대해 줘."], ["You ask a question, and I answer it.”", "자네가 물었으니, 내가 대답한 거야.\""]], [["“And have you nothing, Spaniel,”", "\"그런데 넌, 이 비굴한 개야,\""], ["said the Manager, with unusual irascibility, “to complain of in him?", "지배인이 유난스러운 짜증을 내며 말했다, \"그분한테 불평할 거리가 하나도 없나?"], ["No proud treatment to resent, no insolence, no foolery of state, no exaction of any sort!", "분개할 만한 오만한 처사도, 무례도, 어리석은 위세도, 어떤 종류의 강요도 없었어?"], ["What the devil!", "도대체 뭐야!"], ["are you man or mouse?”", "넌 사람이냐 쥐냐?\""]], [["“It would be strange if any two persons could be together for so many years, especially as superior and inferior, without each having something to complain of in the other—as he thought, at all events,”", "\"두 사람이 그토록 오랜 세월을 함께 지내면서, 더구나 윗사람과 아랫사람으로 지내면서, 서로에게 불평할 거리가—적어도 본인 생각으로는—하나도 없다면, 그게 오히려 이상한 일이지,\""], ["replied John Carker.", "존 카커가 대답했다."], ["“But apart from my history here—”", "\"하지만 여기서의 내 이력을 제쳐 둔다면—\""]], [["“His history here!”", "\"여기서의 자기 이력이라!\""], ["exclaimed the Manager.", "지배인이 외쳤다."], ["“Why, there it is.", "\"그래, 바로 그거야."], ["The very fact that makes him an extreme case, puts him out of the whole chapter!", "너를 극단적인 경우로 만드는 바로 그 사실이, 너를 이야기 전체에서 빼 버리는 거지!"], ["Well?”", "그래서?\""]], [["“Apart from that, which, as you hint, gives me a reason to be thankful that I alone (happily for all the rest) possess, surely there is no one in the House who would not say and feel at least as much.", "\"그건 제쳐 두고—자네가 넌지시 말한 대로, 나만이 (다른 모든 이에게는 다행스럽게도) 그것을 지녔다는 점에서 내가 감사할 이유가 되는 그 이력은 제쳐 두고—이 회사에서 적어도 그만큼은 말하고 느끼지 않을 사람은 분명 아무도 없어."], ["You do not think that anybody here would be indifferent to a mischance or misfortune happening to the head of the House, or anything than truly sorry for it?”", "자넨 여기 누구라도 회사 우두머리에게 닥치는 불행이나 변고에 무심하리라고, 또 그것을 진심으로 슬퍼하지 않으리라고 생각하나?\""]], [["“You have good reason to be bound to him too!”", "\"너야말로 그분께 매여 있을 만한 충분한 이유가 있지!\""], ["said the Manager, contemptuously.", "지배인이 경멸하듯 말했다."], ["“Why, don’t you believe that you are kept here, as a cheap example, and a famous instance of the clemency of Dombey and Son, redounding to the credit of the illustrious House?”", "\"아니, 넌 네가 여기 붙어 있는 게, 값싼 본보기로, 그 빛나는 회사의 명예를 드높여 주는 돔비와 아들의 너그러움을 보여 주는 유명한 사례로 쓰이기 위해서라는 걸 모르겠나?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니,\""], ["replied his brother, mildly, “I have long believed that I am kept here for more kind and disinterested reasons.”", "동생이 온화하게 대답했다, \"난 오래전부터, 더 친절하고 사심 없는 이유로 내가 여기 붙어 있는 거라고 믿어 왔어.\""]], [["“But you were going,”", "\"하지만 넌 무슨 기독교 교훈이라도,\""], ["said the Manager, with the snarl of a tiger-cat, “to recite some Christian precept, I observed.”", "지배인이 살쾡이의 으르렁거림처럼 말했다, \"읊으려던 참이었지, 내가 봤어.\""]], [["“Nay, James,”", "\"아니야, 제임스,\""], ["returned the other, “though the tie of brotherhood between us has been long broken and thrown away—”", "다른 사람이 받았다, \"우리 사이의 형제라는 끈이 오래전에 끊겨 내버려진 것이긴 하지만—\""]], [["“Who broke it, good Sir?”", "\"누가 그걸 끊었지, 선생?\""], ["said the Manager.", "지배인이 말했다."]], [["“I, by my misconduct.", "\"내가, 내 비행으로."], ["I do not charge it upon you.”", "자네 탓으로 돌리는 게 아니야.\""]], [["The Manager replied, with that mute action of his bristling mouth, “Oh, you don’t charge it upon me!”", "지배인은 그 곤두선 입의 그 말없는 동작으로 \"오, 내 탓으로 안 돌리신다!\""], ["and bade him go on.", "하고 답하고는, 계속하라고 했다."]], [["“I say, though there is not that tie between us, do not, I entreat, assail me with unnecessary taunts, or misinterpret what I say, or would say.", "\"내 말은, 우리 사이에 그런 끈이 없다 해도, 부디 불필요한 조롱으로 날 공격하거나, 내가 한 말, 또는 하려는 말을 곡해하지 말아 달라는 거야."], ["I was only going to suggest to you that it would be a mistake to suppose that it is only you, who have been selected here, above all others, for advancement, confidence and distinction (selected, in the beginning, I know, for your great ability and trustfulness), and who communicate more freely with Mr Dombey than anyone, and stand, it may be said, on equal terms with him, and have been favoured and enriched by him—that it would be a mistake to suppose that it is only you who are tender of his welfare and reputation.", "난 그저 자네한테 한 가지를 일러 주려던 거야. 여기서 다른 누구보다 위로 발탁되어 승진과 신임과 영예를 받은(처음에 자네의 뛰어난 능력과 신뢰성 때문에 발탁된 거지, 나도 알아) 사람, 그 누구보다 돔비 씨와 더 허물없이 소통하고, 말하자면 그분과 대등한 처지에 서 있으며, 그분의 총애와 부유함을 입은 사람이 오직 자네뿐이라고—그분의 안녕과 명성을 아끼는 게 오직 자네뿐이라고 생각하는 건 잘못이라는 거야."], ["There is no one in the House, from yourself down to the lowest, I sincerely believe, who does not participate in that feeling.”", "이 회사엔, 자네부터 가장 낮은 자리까지, 그 감정에 함께하지 않을 사람은 아무도 없다고, 난 진심으로 믿어.\""]], [["“You lie!”", "\"거짓말!\""], ["said the Manager, red with sudden anger.", "지배인이 별안간 화가 치밀어 벌게진 얼굴로 말했다."], ["“You’re a hypocrite, John Carker, and you lie.”", "\"넌 위선자야, 존 카커, 거짓말을 하고 있어.\""]], [["“James!”", "\"제임스!\""], ["cried the other, flushing in his turn.", "다른 사람이 이번엔 자기가 벌게지며 외쳤다."], ["“What do you mean by these insulting words?", "\"이런 모욕적인 말로 무슨 소리를 하는 거야?"], ["Why do you so basely use them to me, unprovoked?”", "왜 도발하지도 않은 나한테 그렇게 비열하게 그런 말을 쓰는 거야?\""]], [["“I tell you,”", "\"내 말은,\""], ["said the Manager, “that your hypocrisy and meekness—that all the hypocrisy and meekness of this place—is not worth that to me,”", "지배인이 말했다, \"네 위선과 온순함이—이곳의 온갖 위선과 온순함이—나한테는 이만큼의 가치도 없다는 거야,\""], ["snapping his thumb and finger, “and that I see through it as if it were air!", "엄지와 손가락을 딱 튕기며, \"그리고 내가 그걸 마치 공기를 들여다보듯 꿰뚫어 본다는 거지!"], ["There is not a man employed here, standing between myself and the lowest in place (of whom you are very considerate, and with reason, for he is not far off), who wouldn’t be glad at heart to see his master humbled: who does not hate him, secretly: who does not wish him evil rather than good: and who would not turn upon him, if he had the power and boldness.", "여기 고용된 자들 가운데, 나와 가장 낮은 자리에 있는 자(너는 그자를 무척 배려하지, 그럴 만도 해, 멀지 않은 곳에 있으니까) 사이에 있는 자들 중에, 제 주인이 깎아내려지는 걸 보고 속으로 기뻐하지 않을 자는 하나도 없어. 몰래 그를 미워하지 않는 자, 그가 잘되기보다 못되기를 바라지 않는 자, 힘과 대담함만 있다면 그에게 등을 돌리지 않을 자는 하나도 없다고."], ["The nearer to his favour, the nearer to his insolence; the closer to him, the farther from him.", "그의 총애에 가까울수록, 그의 오만에도 가까운 거고, 그에게 바싹 다가갈수록, 그에게서 멀어지는 거야."], ["That’s the creed here!”", "그게 여기서의 신조다!\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"누가 그런 말로 자네 귀를 더럽혔는지,\" 동생이 말했다."], ["said his brother, whose roused feelings had soon yielded to surprise, “who may have abused your ear with such representations; or why you have chosen to try me, rather than another.", "그의 격앙된 감정은 이내 놀라움에 자리를 내준 터였다. \"혹은 왜 다른 사람이 아니라 날 시험하기로 했는지, 난 모르겠어."], ["But that you have been trying me, and tampering with me, I am now sure.", "하지만 자네가 날 시험하고, 날 떠보고 있었다는 건, 이제 확실해."], ["You have a different manner and a different aspect from any that I ever saw in you.", "자넨 내가 자네한테서 본 적 없는 다른 태도와 다른 안색을 하고 있어."], ["I will only say to you, once more, you are deceived.”", "단 한 번만 더 자네한테 말하지, 자넨 속고 있어.\""]], [["“I know I am,”", "\"내가 속고 있다는 거 알아,\""], ["said the Manager.", "지배인이 말했다."], ["“I have told you so.”", "\"내가 그렇게 말했잖나.\""]], [["“Not by me,”", "\"내게가 아니라,\""], ["returned his brother.", "동생이 받았다."], ["“By your informant, if you have one.", "\"자네한테 그 일러 준 자가 있다면, 그자한테 속는 거지."], ["If not, by your own thoughts and suspicions.”", "없다면, 자네 자신의 생각과 의심에 속는 거고.\""]], [["“I have no suspicions,”", "\"난 의심은 없어,\""], ["said the Manager.", "지배인이 말했다."], ["“Mine are certainties.", "\"내 건 확신이야."], ["You pusillanimous, abject, cringing dogs!", "너희 겁쟁이에 비굴하고 굽실거리는 개들!"], ["All making the same show, all canting the same story, all whining the same professions, all harbouring the same transparent secret.”", "다들 똑같은 시늉을 하고, 다들 똑같은 이야기를 위선으로 떠벌리고, 다들 똑같은 다짐을 징징대고, 다들 똑같은 빤한 비밀을 품고 있지.\""]], [["His brother withdrew, without saying more, and shut the door as he concluded.", "동생은 더 말하지 않고 물러나, 말을 맺으며 문을 닫았다."], ["Mr Carker the Manager drew a chair close before the fire, and fell to beating the coals softly with the poker.", "지배인 카커 씨는 의자를 난롯불 바로 앞으로 끌어다 놓고, 부지깽이로 석탄을 살살 두드리기 시작했다."]], [["“The faint-hearted, fawning knaves,”", "\"이 비겁하고 알랑거리는 악당들,\""], ["he muttered, with his two shining rows of teeth laid bare.", "그는 두 줄의 반짝이는 이를 드러낸 채 중얼거렸다."], ["“There’s not one among them, who wouldn’t feign to be so shocked and outraged—!", "\"이렇게나 충격받고 분개한 척하지 않을 자가 그중에 하나도 없지—!"], ["Bah!", "흥!"], ["There’s not one among them, but if he had at once the power, and the wit and daring to use it, would scatter Dombey’s pride and lay it low, as ruthlessly as I rake out these ashes.”", "하지만 그중에, 만약 힘과, 그것을 쓸 재치와 대담함을 동시에 가졌다면, 돔비의 오만을 흩어 놓고 내가 이 재를 긁어내듯 무자비하게 그것을 짓밟아 놓지 않을 자도 하나도 없어.\""]], [["As he broke them up and strewed them in the grate, he looked on with a thoughtful smile at what he was doing.", "석탄을 부수어 화격자에 흩뿌리면서, 그는 자기가 하는 짓을 생각에 잠긴 미소로 바라보았다."], ["“Without the same queen beckoner too!”", "\"게다가 똑같이 손짓하는 여왕도 없이 말이지!\""], ["he added presently; “and there is pride there, not to be forgotten—witness our own acquaintance!”", "그는 곧 덧붙였다. \"거기엔 오만이 있어, 잊을 수 없는 오만이—우리 자신의 그 안면이 증인이지!\""], ["With that he fell into a deeper reverie, and sat pondering over the blackening grate, until he rose up like a man who had been absorbed in a book, and looking round him took his hat and gloves, went to where his horse was waiting, mounted, and rode away through the lighted streets, for it was evening.", "그러고는 더 깊은 몽상에 빠져들어, 거뭇해져 가는 화격자를 골똘히 들여다보며 앉아 있다가, 책에 몰두해 있던 사람처럼 일어나 주위를 둘러보더니, 모자와 장갑을 집어 들고 말이 기다리는 곳으로 가서, 올라타고는, 불 밝힌 거리들을 지나 말을 몰았다. 저녁이었기 때문이다."]], [["He rode near Mr Dombey’s house; and falling into a walk as he approached it, looked up at the windows The window where he had once seen Florence sitting with her dog attracted his attention first, though there was no light in it; but he smiled as he carried his eyes up the tall front of the house, and seemed to leave that object superciliously behind.", "그는 돔비 씨의 집 가까이 말을 몰았다. 그러고는 거기에 다가가며 속보로 늦추고는, 창문들을 올려다보았다. 한때 플로렌스가 개와 함께 앉아 있는 것을 보았던 그 창문이, 불빛은 없었지만 가장 먼저 그의 주의를 끌었다. 그러나 그는 그 집의 높다란 정면 위로 시선을 옮겨 가며 미소 지었고, 그 대상을 거만하게 뒤로 흘려보내는 듯했다."]], [["“Time was,”", "\"한때는,\""], ["he said, “when it was well to watch even your rising little star, and know in what quarter there were clouds, to shadow you if needful.", "그가 말했다, \"떠오르는 너의 작은 별까지도 지켜보고, 필요하다면 너를 가릴 구름이 어느 방위에 있는지 알아 두는 게 잘하는 일이었지."], ["But a planet has arisen, and you are lost in its light.”", "하지만 하나의 행성이 떠올랐고, 너는 그 빛 속에 사라져 버렸어.\""]], [["He turned the white-legged horse round the street corner, and sought one shining window from among those at the back of the house.", "그는 흰 다리의 말을 거리 모퉁이로 돌려, 집 뒤편의 창문들 가운데서 빛나는 창 하나를 찾았다."], ["Associated with it was a certain stately presence, a gloved hand, the remembrance how the feathers of a beautiful bird’s wing had been showered down upon the floor, and how the light white down upon a robe had stirred and rustled, as in the rising of a distant storm.", "그것에는 어떤 위엄 있는 자태와, 장갑 낀 손과, 아름다운 새의 날개 깃털이 바닥에 흩뿌려지던 기억, 그리고 예복 위의 가볍고 흰 솜털이 마치 먼 폭풍이 일어날 때처럼 흔들리고 바스락거리던 기억이 결부되어 있었다."], ["These were the things he carried with him as he turned away again, and rode through the darkening and deserted Parks at a quick rate.", "이것들이, 그가 다시 돌아서서 어두워지고 인적 없는 공원들을 빠른 속도로 가로질러 달릴 때 그가 지니고 간 것들이었다."]], [["In fatal truth, these were associated with a woman, a proud woman, who hated him, but who by slow and sure degrees had been led on by his craft, and her pride and resentment, to endure his company, and little by little to receive him as one who had the privilege to talk to her of her own defiant disregard of her own husband, and her abandonment of high consideration for herself.", "치명적인 진실로, 이것들은 한 여인과, 그를 미워하는 오만한 여인과 결부되어 있었다. 그러나 그 여인은 그의 교활함과, 자신의 오만함과 분노에 이끌려, 더디지만 확실한 정도로, 그의 동행을 견디게 되고, 조금씩 그를 받아들여, 자기 자신의 남편에 대한 도전적인 무시며, 자기 자신에 대한 높은 존중을 내던진 일을 자기에게 이야기할 특권을 가진 자로 여기게 된 터였다."], ["They were associated with a woman who hated him deeply, and who knew him, and who mistrusted him because she knew him, and because he knew her; but who fed her fierce resentment by suffering him to draw nearer and yet nearer to her every day, in spite of the hate she cherished for him.", "이것들은 그를 깊이 미워하는 한 여인과 결부되어 있었으니, 그녀는 그를 알았고, 그를 알기에 그를 불신했으며, 또 그가 자기를 알기에 그를 불신했다. 그러나 그녀는, 그에게 품은 미움에도 불구하고, 그가 날마다 자기에게 더, 더 가까이 다가오도록 허락함으로써 그 사나운 분노를 키워 갔다."], ["In spite of it!", "미움에도 불구하고!"], ["For that very reason; since in its depths, too far down for her threatening eye to pierce, though she could see into them dimly, lay the dark retaliation, whose faintest shadow seen once and shuddered at, and never seen again, would have been sufficient stain upon her soul.", "바로 그 이유 때문에. 왜냐하면 그 미움의 밑바닥에, 그녀의 위협하는 눈으로도 꿰뚫기엔 너무 깊은 곳에—희미하게나마 그녀가 그 속을 들여다볼 수는 있었으나—어두운 보복이 놓여 있었으니, 그 가장 희미한 그림자조차 한 번 보고 몸서리치고 다시는 보지 못했더라도, 그것은 그녀의 영혼에 충분한 오점이 되었을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["Did the phantom of such a woman flit about him on his ride; true to the reality, and obvious to him?", "그런 여인의 환영이 그가 말을 모는 동안 그의 주위를 떠돌았던가, 그 실체에 충실하게, 그에게 또렷이?"]], [["Yes.", "그렇다."], ["He saw her in his mind, exactly as she was.", "그는 마음속으로 그녀를, 그녀의 모습 그대로 정확히 보았다."], ["She bore him company with her pride, resentment, hatred, all as plain to him as her beauty; with nothing plainer to him than her hatred of him.", "그녀는 자기의 오만과 분노와 미움을 지닌 채 그와 동행했으니, 그 모든 것이 그녀의 아름다움만큼이나 그에게 또렷했다. 그리고 그에게 그녀의 미움보다 더 또렷한 것은 없었다."], ["He saw her sometimes haughty and repellent at his side, and some times down among his horse’s feet, fallen and in the dust.", "그는 때로 그녀가 자기 곁에서 도도하고 쌀쌀맞게 있는 것을 보았고, 때로는 자기 말발굽 사이에 쓰러져 먼지를 뒤집어쓴 것을 보았다."], ["But he always saw her as she was, without disguise, and watched her on the dangerous way that she was going.", "그러나 그는 언제나 그녀를 있는 그대로, 가식 없이 보았고, 그녀가 가고 있는 그 위험한 길 위에서 그녀를 지켜보았다."]], [["And when his ride was over, and he was newly dressed, and came into the light of her bright room with his bent head, soft voice, and soothing smile, he saw her yet as plainly.", "그리고 말 타기가 끝나고, 그가 새로 옷을 차려입고, 고개를 숙인 채 부드러운 목소리와 어르는 미소를 띠고 그녀의 환한 방의 불빛 속으로 들어섰을 때에도, 그는 여전히 그녀를 또렷이 보았다."], ["He even suspected the mystery of the gloved hand, and held it all the longer in his own for that suspicion.", "그는 심지어 그 장갑 낀 손의 비밀까지 짐작했고, 그 짐작 때문에 그 손을 자기 손 안에 그만큼 더 오래 쥐고 있었다."], ["Upon the dangerous way that she was going, he was, still; and not a footprint did she mark upon it, but he set his own there, straight.", "그녀가 가고 있는 그 위험한 길 위에, 그는 여전히 있었다. 그리고 그녀가 그 위에 발자국 하나를 찍을 때마다, 그는 자기 발자국을 그 바로 옆에 똑바로 찍었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVII. The Thunderbolt", "# CHAPTER XLVII. The Thunderbolt"]], [["The barrier between Mr Dombey and his wife was not weakened by time.", "돔비 씨와 그의 아내 사이의 장벽은 세월이 흘러도 약해지지 않았다."], ["Ill-assorted couple, unhappy in themselves and in each other, bound together by no tie but the manacle that joined their fettered hands, and straining that so harshly, in their shrinking asunder, that it wore and chafed to the bone, Time, consoler of affliction and softener of anger, could do nothing to help them.", "어울리지 않는 부부, 제 자신에게도 서로에게도 불행한 두 사람, 묶인 손을 잇는 수갑 외에는 그 어떤 끈으로도 이어져 있지 않았고, 서로 떨어지려 발버둥 칠 때마다 그 수갑을 어찌나 모질게 잡아당겼던지 살갗을 뚫고 뼈에까지 쓸려 들어갔으니, 고통을 달래고 분노를 누그러뜨리는 위로자인 세월도 그들에게는 아무 도움이 되지 못했다."], ["Their pride, however different in kind and object, was equal in degree; and, in their flinty opposition, struck out fire between them which might smoulder or might blaze, as circumstances were, but burned up everything within their mutual reach, and made their marriage way a road of ashes.", "그들의 자존심은, 종류와 대상은 서로 달랐어도 그 정도에서는 대등했다. 그리고 부싯돌 같은 그들의 대립 속에서 불꽃이 튀어 올랐으니, 그 불은 사정에 따라 연기만 피우기도 하고 활활 타오르기도 했지만, 둘이 함께 닿을 수 있는 범위 안의 모든 것을 태워 버렸고, 그들의 결혼 길을 잿더미의 길로 만들어 버렸다."]], [["Let us be just to him. In the monstrous delusion of his life, swelling with every grain of sand that shifted in its glass, he urged her on, he little thought to what, or considered how; but still his feeling towards her, such as it was, remained as at first. She had the grand demerit of unaccountably putting herself in opposition to the recognition of his vast importance, and to the acknowledgment of her complete submission to it, and so far it was necessary to correct and reduce her; but otherwise he still considered her, in his cold way, a lady capable of doing honour, if she would, to his choice and name, and of reflecting credit on his proprietorship.", "그에게도 공정하기로 하자. 자기 생애라는 그 괴물 같은 망상은, 모래시계 속에서 흘러내리는 모래알 한 톨 한 톨마다 부풀어 오르며, 그로 하여금 그녀를 몰아붙이게 했지만, 그는 그것이 무엇을 향한 것인지도 거의 생각하지 않았고, 어떻게 하는지도 헤아리지 않았다. 그러나 그녀를 향한 그의 감정은, 그것이 어떤 것이었든 간에, 처음 그대로 남아 있었다. 그녀에게는 영문도 모를 큰 결함이 있었으니, 자기의 막대한 중요성을 인정하는 것에, 그리고 거기에 완전히 복종한다는 것을 시인하는 것에 스스로를 대립시킨다는 점이었다. 그 점에서는 그녀를 바로잡고 굴복시키는 것이 필요했다. 그러나 그 밖에는, 그는 여전히 그 특유의 차가운 방식으로 그녀를 이렇게 여겼다. 그녀가 마음만 먹는다면 자기의 선택과 이름에 영예를 더해 줄 수 있고, 자기 소유권에 위신을 비춰 줄 수 있는 귀부인이라고."]], [["Now, she, with all her might of passionate and proud resentment, bent her dark glance from day to day, and hour to hour—from that night in her own chamber, when she had sat gazing at the shadows on the wall, to the deeper night fast coming—upon one figure directing a crowd of humiliations and exasperations against her; and that figure, still her husband’s.", "이제 그녀는, 격정적이고 도도한 원한의 온 힘을 다해, 날마다 시시각각으로—벽에 비친 그림자들을 바라보며 앉아 있던 자기 방의 그 밤부터, 빠르게 다가오는 더 깊은 밤에 이르도록—자기를 겨냥해 갖가지 굴욕과 격분을 지휘하는 한 사람의 형상에 어두운 시선을 던졌다. 그리고 그 형상은, 여전히 그녀의 남편이었다."]], [["Was Mr Dombey’s master-vice, that ruled him so inexorably, an unnatural characteristic?", "돔비 씨를 그토록 가차 없이 지배하는 그 주된 악덕은 부자연스러운 특성이었을까?"], ["It might be worthwhile, sometimes, to inquire what Nature is, and how men work to change her, and whether, in the enforced distortions so produced, it is not natural to be unnatural.", "더러는 따져 볼 만한 일이리라. 자연이란 무엇이며, 인간이 그것을 어떻게 바꾸려 드는지, 그리고 그렇게 강제로 빚어진 일그러짐 속에서는 부자연스러운 것이야말로 자연스러운 것이 아닐는지를."], ["Coop any son or daughter of our mighty mother within narrow range, and bind the prisoner to one idea, and foster it by servile worship of it on the part of the few timid or designing people standing round, and what is Nature to the willing captive who has never risen up upon the wings of a free mind—drooping and useless soon—to see her in her comprehensive truth!", "우리의 위대한 어머니의 그 어떤 아들이나 딸이든 좁은 울타리 안에 가두어 놓고, 그 갇힌 자를 단 하나의 관념에 묶어 두고, 주위에 둘러선 소수의 소심하거나 음흉한 사람들이 그것을 노예처럼 떠받들어 길러 준다면, 자유로운 정신의 날개를 펴고—그 날개는 곧 시들어 쓸모없어지지만—솟아올라 자연을 그 포괄적인 진실 속에서 본 적이 한 번도 없는, 기꺼이 갇혀 있는 그 포로에게 자연이란 도대체 무엇이겠는가!"]], [["Alas!", "아아!"], ["are there so few things in the world, about us, most unnatural, and yet most natural in being so?", "우리 주위 세상에는, 더없이 부자연스러우면서도, 그렇게 부자연스럽다는 점에서 더없이 자연스러운 것들이 그리도 적단 말인가?"], ["Hear the magistrate or judge admonish the unnatural outcasts of society; unnatural in brutal habits, unnatural in want of decency, unnatural in losing and confounding all distinctions between good and evil; unnatural in ignorance, in vice, in recklessness, in contumacy, in mind, in looks, in everything.", "치안판사나 재판관이 사회의 부자연스러운 버림받은 자들을 훈계하는 것을 들어 보라. 야만스러운 습성에서 부자연스럽고, 염치의 결여에서 부자연스럽고, 선과 악의 모든 구별을 잃고 뒤섞어 버린 점에서 부자연스러우며, 무지에서, 악덕에서, 무모함에서, 완고함에서, 마음에서, 외모에서, 모든 것에서 부자연스러운 자들을."], ["But follow the good clergyman or doctor, who, with his life imperilled at every breath he draws, goes down into their dens, lying within the echoes of our carriage wheels and daily tread upon the pavement stones.", "그러나 저 선량한 목사나 의사를 따라가 보라. 숨을 들이쉴 때마다 목숨을 걸고서, 우리의 마차 바퀴 소리와 보도의 돌을 밟는 날마다의 발길이 메아리치는 바로 그 안쪽에 자리한 그들의 소굴로 내려가는 그 사람을."], ["Look round upon the world of odious sights—millions of immortal creatures have no other world on earth—at the lightest mention of which humanity revolts, and dainty delicacy living in the next street, stops her ears, and lisps “I don’t believe it!”", "그 혐오스러운 광경의 세상을 둘러보라—수백만의 불멸하는 피조물들에게는 이 땅 위에 다른 세상이라곤 없으니—그것을 아주 가볍게 입에 올리기만 해도 인간성이 반발하고, 바로 옆 거리에 사는 까다로운 우아함은 귀를 막고 혀 짧은 소리로 \"난 안 믿어!\"라고 말하는 그런 세상을."], ["Breathe the polluted air, foul with every impurity that is poisonous to health and life; and have every sense, conferred upon our race for its delight and happiness, offended, sickened and disgusted, and made a channel by which misery and death alone can enter.", "건강과 생명에 독이 되는 온갖 불순물로 더럽혀진 그 오염된 공기를 들이마셔 보라. 그리고 우리 인류에게 기쁨과 행복을 누리라고 주어진 모든 감각이 모욕당하고 병들고 역겨워져, 오직 비참과 죽음만이 들어올 수 있는 통로가 되는 것을 겪어 보라."], ["Vainly attempt to think of any simple plant, or flower, or wholesome weed, that, set in this foetid bed, could have its natural growth, or put its little leaves off to the sun as GOD designed it.", "이 악취 나는 자리에 심긴 어떤 소박한 풀이나 꽃이나 멀쩡한 잡초가, 신께서 마련하신 대로 제 자연스러운 성장을 이루거나 작은 잎사귀들을 햇빛을 향해 펼칠 수 있으리라고 생각해 보려 해도 부질없다."], ["And then, calling up some ghastly child, with stunted form and wicked face, hold forth on its unnatural sinfulness, and lament its being, so early, far away from Heaven—but think a little of its having been conceived, and born and bred, in Hell!", "그러고 나서, 발육이 멈춘 몸과 사악한 얼굴을 한 어떤 끔찍한 아이를 불러 세우고, 그 아이의 부자연스러운 죄악을 늘어놓으며, 그토록 일찍부터 천국에서 멀리 떨어져 있음을 한탄해 보라—그러나 그 아이가 지옥에서 잉태되고, 태어나고, 길러졌다는 사실을 조금이라도 생각해 보라!"]], [["Those who study the physical sciences, and bring them to bear upon the health of Man, tell us that if the noxious particles that rise from vitiated air were palpable to the sight, we should see them lowering in a dense black cloud above such haunts, and rolling slowly on to corrupt the better portions of a town.", "물리 과학을 연구하여 그것을 인간의 건강에 적용하는 사람들은 우리에게 이렇게 말한다. 만일 오염된 공기에서 피어오르는 유독한 미립자들이 눈에 보이는 것이라면, 우리는 그것들이 그런 소굴 위에 짙은 검은 구름이 되어 드리워 있고, 도시의 더 나은 부분들을 더럽히려 천천히 굴러가는 것을 보게 되리라고."], ["But if the moral pestilence that rises with them, and in the eternal laws of our Nature, is inseparable from them, could be made discernible too, how terrible the revelation!", "그러나 만일 그것들과 함께 피어오르며, 우리 자연의 영원한 법칙에 따라 그것들과 떼어 놓을 수 없는 그 도덕적 역병까지도 눈에 보이게 할 수 있다면, 그 폭로는 얼마나 끔찍할 것인가!"], ["Then should we see depravity, impiety, drunkenness, theft, murder, and a long train of nameless sins against the natural affections and repulsions of mankind, overhanging the devoted spots, and creeping on, to blight the innocent and spread contagion among the pure.", "그렇게 된다면 우리는 타락과 불경과 만취와 절도와 살인, 그리고 인류의 자연스러운 애정과 반감을 거스르는 이름 없는 죄들의 긴 행렬이, 그 저주받은 장소들 위에 드리운 채 기어 나와, 무고한 자들을 시들게 하고 순결한 자들 사이에 전염을 퍼뜨리는 것을 보게 되리라."], ["Then should we see how the same poisoned fountains that flow into our hospitals and lazar-houses, inundate the jails, and make the convict-ships swim deep, and roll across the seas, and over-run vast continents with crime.", "그렇게 된다면 우리는, 우리의 병원과 나병원으로 흘러드는 바로 그 독 묻은 샘물이 감옥을 범람시키고, 죄수 호송선을 깊이 가라앉히며, 바다를 건너 굴러가, 광대한 대륙들을 범죄로 뒤덮는 것을 보게 되리라."], ["Then should we stand appalled to know, that where we generate disease to strike our children down and entail itself on unborn generations, there also we breed, by the same certain process, infancy that knows no innocence, youth without modesty or shame, maturity that is mature in nothing but in suffering and guilt, blasted old age that is a scandal on the form we bear, unnatural humanity!", "그렇게 된다면 우리는 이 사실을 알고 소스라치게 놀라 서 있게 되리라. 우리 아이들을 쓰러뜨리고 아직 태어나지 않은 세대에까지 대물림될 질병을 우리가 만들어 내는 바로 그곳에서, 똑같은 확실한 과정을 통해, 순결이라곤 모르는 어린것과, 정숙함도 부끄러움도 없는 젊은이와, 고통과 죄밖에는 아무것도 무르익지 않은 장년과, 우리가 지닌 형상에 대한 치욕인 시들어 버린 노년, 곧 부자연스러운 인간성을 우리가 똑같이 길러 낸다는 것을!"], ["When we shall gather grapes from thorns, and figs from thistles; when fields of grain shall spring up from the offal in the bye-ways of our wicked cities, and roses bloom in the fat churchyards that they cherish; then we may look for natural humanity, and find it growing from such seed.", "우리가 가시나무에서 포도를, 엉겅퀴에서 무화과를 거두게 될 때, 우리의 사악한 도시 뒷골목의 오물에서 곡식의 들판이 솟아나고, 그것이 키워 내는 기름진 묘지에서 장미가 피어날 때, 그제야 우리는 자연스러운 인간성을 찾아볼 수 있을 것이며, 그것이 그런 씨앗에서 자라나는 것을 발견하게 되리라."]], [["Oh for a good spirit who would take the house-tops off, with a more potent and benignant hand than the lame demon in the tale, and show a Christian people what dark shapes issue from amidst their homes, to swell the retinue of the Destroying Angel as he moves forth among them!", "오, 그 이야기 속의 절름발이 악마보다 더 강력하고 자비로운 손으로 집들의 지붕을 걷어 내고, 그리스도교를 믿는 백성들에게, 파괴의 천사가 그들 사이를 누비며 나아갈 때 그 행렬을 불려 주려고 그들의 가정 한복판에서 어떤 어두운 형상들이 빠져나오는지를 보여 줄 선한 영혼이 하나 있다면!"], ["For only one night’s view of the pale phantoms rising from the scenes of our too-long neglect; and from the thick and sullen air where Vice and Fever propagate together, raining the tremendous social retributions which are ever pouring down, and ever coming thicker!", "단 하룻밤만이라도, 우리가 너무 오래 방치해 온 그 현장들에서 솟아오르는 창백한 유령들을, 악덕과 열병이 함께 번식하는 그 짙고 음울한 공기에서—언제나 쏟아져 내리고 언제나 더 자욱하게 닥쳐오는 그 무서운 사회적 응보를 비처럼 퍼부으며—솟아오르는 그것들을 보여 줄 영혼이 있다면!"], ["Bright and blest the morning that should rise on such a night: for men, delayed no more by stumbling-blocks of their own making, which are but specks of dust upon the path between them and eternity, would then apply themselves, like creatures of one common origin, owing one duty to the Father of one family, and tending to one common end, to make the world a better place!", "그런 밤 위에 밝아 오는 아침은 환하고 복될 것이다. 사람들이 자기 자신이 만들어 놓은 걸림돌들—그것들은 자기와 영원 사이의 길 위에 놓인 한낱 먼지 티끌에 불과하건만—에 더 이상 가로막히지 않고서, 한 공통된 기원을 지닌 피조물답게, 한 가족의 아버지께 한 가지 의무를 지고, 하나의 공통된 목적을 향해 나아가, 세상을 더 나은 곳으로 만드는 데 스스로를 바칠 것이기 때문이다!"]], [["Not the less bright and blest would that day be for rousing some who never have looked out upon the world of human life around them, to a knowledge of their own relation to it, and for making them acquainted with a perversion of nature in their own contracted sympathies and estimates; as great, and yet as natural in its development when once begun, as the lowest degradation known.", "자기 주위의 인간 삶의 세계를 한 번도 내다본 적 없는 이들 가운데 더러를 흔들어 깨워, 그 세계와 자기 자신의 관계를 깨닫게 하고, 자기 자신의 옹졸한 공감과 평가 속에 깃든 자연의 왜곡—한번 시작되고 나면 그 전개에 있어, 알려진 가장 비천한 타락만큼이나 크면서도 또한 그만큼 자연스러운 그 왜곡—을 알게 해 주는 것만으로도, 그날은 못지않게 환하고 복될 것이다."]], [["But no such day had ever dawned on Mr Dombey, or his wife; and the course of each was taken.", "그러나 그런 날은 돔비 씨에게도, 그의 아내에게도 한 번도 밝아 온 적이 없었다. 그리고 두 사람 각자의 길은 이미 정해져 있었다."]], [["Through six months that ensued upon his accident, they held the same relations one towards the other.", "그가 사고를 당한 뒤 이어진 여섯 달 동안, 그들은 서로에 대해 똑같은 관계를 유지했다."], ["A marble rock could not have stood more obdurately in his way than she; and no chilled spring, lying uncheered by any ray of light in the depths of a deep cave, could be more sullen or more cold than he.", "대리석 바위라도 그녀만큼 완강하게 그의 앞을 가로막지는 못했을 것이며, 깊은 동굴 속 한 줄기 빛도 들지 않는 곳에 고인 어떤 차디찬 샘물도 그만큼 음울하거나 차갑지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["The hope that had fluttered within her when the promise of her new home dawned, was quite gone from the heart of Florence now.", "새 보금자리의 약속이 밝아 올 때 그녀의 가슴속에서 팔랑이던 희망은, 이제 플로렌스의 마음에서 완전히 사라지고 없었다."], ["That home was nearly two years old; and even the patient trust that was in her, could not survive the daily blight of such experience.", "그 보금자리는 거의 두 해가 되었다. 그리고 그녀 안에 깃든 그 끈기 있는 믿음조차도 그런 경험의 날마다의 시듦을 견뎌 내지는 못했다."], ["If she had any lingering fancy in the nature of hope left, that Edith and her father might be happier together, in some distant time, she had none, now, that her father would ever love her.", "만일 그녀에게 희망 비슷한 어떤 어렴풋한 공상이라도 남아 있었다면, 그것은 이디스와 자기 아버지가 먼 훗날 어느 때엔가 함께 더 행복해질지도 모른다는 것이었지, 자기 아버지가 자기를 언젠가 사랑해 주리라는 것은 이제 조금도 없었다."], ["The little interval in which she had imagined that she saw some small relenting in him, was forgotten in the long remembrance of his coldness since and before, or only remembered as a sorrowful delusion.", "그에게서 무언가 작은 누그러짐을 본 것 같다고 상상했던 그 짧은 동안은, 그 전후의 오랜 냉담함에 대한 긴 기억 속에 묻혀 잊히고 말았고, 혹은 그저 서글픈 망상으로만 기억될 뿐이었다."]], [["Florence loved him still, but, by degrees, had come to love him rather as some dear one who had been, or who might have been, than as the hard reality before her eyes.", "플로렌스는 여전히 그를 사랑했다. 그러나 차츰차츰, 눈앞의 그 모진 현실로서가 아니라, 오히려 한때 그러했던, 혹은 그러했을 수도 있었을 어떤 소중한 사람으로서 그를 사랑하게 되었다."], ["Something of the softened sadness with which she loved the memory of little Paul, or of her mother, seemed to enter now into her thoughts of him, and to make them, as it were, a dear remembrance.", "어린 폴이나 자기 어머니의 추억을 사랑할 때 깃들던 그 누그러진 슬픔 같은 것이, 이제는 그를 떠올리는 그녀의 생각 속에도 스며들어, 그 생각들을 말하자면 하나의 소중한 추억으로 만들어 가는 듯했다. 그가 그녀에게는 죽은 것이나 다름없기 때문인지, 또 그 까닭의 일부는 그가 그녀의 옛 애정의 대상들과 한몫 얽혀 있기 때문인지, 또 일부는 그를 시들어 버린 희망들 및 그가 얼어붙게 한 다정함들과 오래 결부 지어 왔기 때문인지, 그녀로서는 말할 수 없었다."], ["Whether it was that he was dead to her, and that partly for this reason, partly for his share in those old objects of her affection, and partly for the long association of him with hopes that were withered and tendernesses he had frozen, she could not have told; but the father whom she loved began to be a vague and dreamy idea to her: hardly more substantially connected with her real life, than the image she would sometimes conjure up, of her dear brother yet alive, and growing to be a man, who would protect and cherish her.", "그러나 그녀가 사랑했던 아버지는 그녀에게 점점 막연하고 꿈결 같은 관념이 되어 가기 시작했다. 자기가 사랑하던 오라비가 아직 살아 있어 어른으로 자라나 자기를 지켜 주고 아껴 주는 모습을, 때때로 마음속에 떠올려 보던 그 영상보다도 그녀의 실제 삶과 거의 더 실질적으로 이어져 있지 못했다."]], [["The change, if it may be called one, had stolen on her like the change from childhood to womanhood, and had come with it.", "그 변화는, 그것을 변화라 부를 수 있다면, 어린 시절에서 여인 시절로 넘어가는 변화처럼 그녀에게 슬며시 다가왔고, 또 그 변화와 함께 찾아왔다."], ["Florence was almost seventeen, when, in her lonely musings, she was conscious of these thoughts.", "플로렌스가 외로운 사색에 잠겨 이런 생각들을 의식하게 되었을 때, 그녀는 거의 열일곱 살이었다."]], [["She was often alone now, for the old association between her and her Mama was greatly changed.", "이제 그녀는 자주 혼자였다. 그녀와 어머니 사이의 옛 정겨움이 크게 달라졌기 때문이다."], ["At the time of her father’s accident, and when he was lying in his room downstairs, Florence had first observed that Edith avoided her.", "아버지가 사고를 당했을 때, 그리고 그가 아래층 자기 방에 누워 있던 무렵, 플로렌스는 이디스가 자기를 피한다는 것을 처음으로 알아챘다."], ["Wounded and shocked, and yet unable to reconcile this with her affection when they did meet, she sought her in her own room at night, once more.", "상처받고 충격받았으면서도, 둘이 정작 만났을 때 보이는 그녀의 애정과는 이것을 도무지 조화시킬 수 없었던 플로렌스는, 한 번 더, 밤에 그녀의 방으로 그녀를 찾아갔다."]], [["“Mama,”", "\"엄마,\""], ["said Florence, stealing softly to her side, “have I offended you?”", "플로렌스가 가만히 그녀 곁으로 다가가며 말했다, \"제가 엄마를 화나게 했나요?\""]], [["Edith answered “No.”", "이디스는 \"아니\"라고 대답했다."]], [["“I must have done something,”", "\"제가 무슨 짓을 했나 봐요,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“Tell me what it is.", "\"그게 뭔지 말씀해 주세요."], ["You have changed your manner to me, dear Mama.", "엄마는 저를 대하는 태도가 달라지셨어요, 사랑하는 엄마."], ["I cannot say how instantly I feel the least change; for I love you with my whole heart.”", "저는 아주 작은 변화라도 얼마나 곧바로 느끼는지 몰라요. 저는 온 마음을 다해 엄마를 사랑하니까요.\""]], [["“As I do you,”", "\"나도 너를 그렇게 사랑한단다,\""], ["said Edith.", "이디스가 말했다."], ["“Ah, Florence, believe me never more than now!”", "\"아, 플로렌스, 지금 이 순간보다 더한 적이 없었음을 믿어 다오!\""]], [["“Why do you go away from me so often, and keep away?”", "\"그런데 왜 그렇게 자주 제게서 멀어지시고, 멀찍이 떨어져 계세요?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."], ["“And why do you sometimes look so strangely on me, dear Mama?", "\"그리고 왜 가끔 저를 그렇게 이상하게 바라보세요, 사랑하는 엄마?"], ["You do so, do you not?”", "그러시잖아요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["Edith signified assent with her dark eyes.", "이디스는 어두운 눈으로 그렇다는 뜻을 나타냈다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""], ["returned Florence imploringly.", "플로렌스가 애원하듯 되물었다."], ["“Tell me why, that I may know how to please you better; and tell me this shall not be so any more.”", "\"왜 그러시는지 말씀해 주세요, 제가 엄마를 어떻게 하면 더 기쁘게 해 드릴 수 있는지 알 수 있게요. 그리고 다시는 이러지 않겠다고 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“My Florence,”", "\"내 플로렌스야,\""], ["answered Edith, taking the hand that embraced her neck, and looking into the eyes that looked into hers so lovingly, as Florence knelt upon the ground before her; “why it is, I cannot tell you.", "이디스가, 자기 목을 감싼 손을 잡고, 자기 앞 바닥에 무릎 꿇은 플로렌스가 그토록 사랑스럽게 들여다보는 그 눈을 들여다보며 대답했다. \"왜 그런지는 네게 말해 줄 수가 없단다."], ["It is neither for me to say, nor you to hear; but that it is, and that it must be, I know.", "내가 말할 일도, 네가 들을 일도 아니야. 하지만 그것이 그렇다는 것, 그리고 그래야만 한다는 것은 내가 안단다."], ["Should I do it if I did not?”", "그렇지 않다면 내가 이러겠니?\""]], [["“Are we to be estranged, Mama?”", "\"우리는 멀어지게 되는 건가요, 엄마?\""], ["asked Florence, gazing at her like one frightened.", "플로렌스가 겁먹은 사람처럼 그녀를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["Edith’s silent lips formed “Yes.”", "이디스의 말없는 입술이 \"그래\"라는 모양을 지었다."]], [["Florence looked at her with increasing fear and wonder, until she could see her no more through the blinding tears that ran down her face.", "플로렌스는 점점 커지는 두려움과 의아함으로 그녀를 바라보았고, 마침내 얼굴을 타고 흘러내리는 눈물에 눈이 가려져 더는 그녀를 볼 수 없었다."]], [["“Florence!", "\"플로렌스!"], ["my life!”", "내 생명아!\""], ["said Edith, hurriedly, “listen to me.", "이디스가 황급히 말했다, \"내 말 좀 들어 보렴."], ["I cannot bear to see this grief.", "나는 이 슬픔을 보고 있을 수가 없구나."], ["Be calmer.", "좀 진정하렴."], ["You see that I am composed, and is it nothing to me?”", "너도 보다시피 나는 차분하지 않니, 그렇다면 이게 나한테는 아무것도 아니란 말이냐?\""]], [["She resumed her steady voice and manner as she said the latter words, and added presently:", "뒤의 말을 할 때 그녀는 다시 한결같은 목소리와 태도로 돌아갔고, 이내 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Not wholly estranged.", "\"완전히 멀어지는 건 아니야. 어느 정도만."], ["Partially: and only that, in appearance, Florence, for in my own breast I am still the same to you, and ever will be.", "그것도 겉으로만 그런 거란다, 플로렌스. 내 가슴속에서는 나는 여전히 네게 똑같고, 앞으로도 영원히 그럴 테니까."], ["But what I do is not done for myself.”", "하지만 내가 하는 일은 나 자신을 위해 하는 게 아니란다.\""]], [["“Is it for me, Mama?”", "\"저를 위해서인가요, 엄마?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“It is enough,”", "\"그것이 무엇인지를 아는 것으로 충분하단다,\""], ["said Edith, after a pause, “to know what it is; why, matters little.", "이디스가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. \"왜 그런지는 별로 중요하지 않아."], ["Dear Florence, it is better—it is necessary—it must be—that our association should be less frequent.", "사랑하는 플로렌스, 우리가 덜 자주 만나는 편이 더 낫단다—그래야만 하고—꼭 그래야만 해."], ["The confidence there has been between us must be broken off.”", "우리 사이에 있었던 그 다정한 신뢰는 끊어져야만 한단다.\""]], [["“When?”", "\"언제요?\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, Mama, when?”", "\"오, 엄마, 언제요?\""]], [["“Now,”", "\"지금부터,\""], ["said Edith.", "이디스가 말했다."]], [["“For all time to come?”", "\"앞으로 영영요?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“I do not say that,”", "\"그렇다고 말하는 건 아니야,\""], ["answered Edith.", "이디스가 대답했다."], ["“I do not know that.", "\"나도 그건 모르겠구나."], ["Nor will I say that companionship between us is, at the best, an ill-assorted and unholy union, of which I might have known no good could come.", "또 우리 사이의 가까움이라는 것이, 아무리 좋게 봐도, 어울리지 않고 거룩하지 못한 결합이어서, 거기서 좋은 것이 나올 리 없다는 걸 내가 미리 알았어야 했다고는 말하지 않으마."], ["My way here has been through paths that you will never tread, and my way henceforth may lie—God knows—I do not see it—”", "내가 여기까지 온 길은 네가 결코 밟지 않을 길들을 지나온 것이고, 앞으로 내 길이 어디로 뻗어 있을지는—하느님은 아시겠지—나는 보이지 않는구나—\""]], [["Her voice died away into silence; and she sat, looking at Florence, and almost shrinking from her, with the same strange dread and wild avoidance that Florence had noticed once before.", "그녀의 목소리는 사그라들어 침묵으로 잦아들었다. 그리고 그녀는, 플로렌스가 전에 한 번 알아챈 적 있던 그 똑같은 기이한 두려움과 격렬한 회피의 빛을 띤 채, 플로렌스를 바라보며, 거의 그녀에게서 움츠러들 듯이 앉아 있었다."], ["The same dark pride and rage succeeded, sweeping over her form and features like an angry chord across the strings of a wild harp.", "곧이어 그 똑같은 어두운 자존심과 분노가 뒤를 이어, 거친 하프의 현을 가로지르는 성난 화음처럼, 그녀의 몸과 얼굴 위를 휩쓸고 지나갔다."], ["But no softness or humility ensued on that.", "그러나 거기에는 그 어떤 부드러움도 겸손함도 뒤따르지 않았다."], ["She did not lay her head down now, and weep, and say that she had no hope but in Florence.", "이번에는 그녀가 고개를 떨구지도, 울지도, 플로렌스 말고는 아무 희망도 없다고 말하지도 않았다."], ["She held it up as if she were a beautiful Medusa, looking on him, face to face, to strike him dead.", "그녀는 마치 아름다운 메두사가 그를 정면으로 마주 보며 죽음으로 쓰러뜨리려는 듯, 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들었다."], ["Yes, and she would have done it, if she had had the charm.", "그렇다, 만일 그녀에게 그런 마력이 있었더라면 그녀는 정말로 그렇게 했으리라."]], [["“Mama,”", "\"엄마,\""], ["said Florence, anxiously, “there is a change in you, in more than what you say to me, which alarms me.", "플로렌스가 불안하게 말했다, \"엄마한테는 무언가 변화가 있어요, 제게 하시는 말씀 그 이상의, 저를 겁나게 하는 변화가요."], ["Let me stay with you a little.”", "잠시만 엄마 곁에 있게 해 주세요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"안 돼,\""], ["said Edith, “no, dearest.", "이디스가 말했다, \"안 된다, 사랑하는 아이야."], ["I am best left alone now, and I do best to keep apart from you, of all else.", "나는 지금 혼자 있는 게 가장 낫고, 무엇보다도 너에게서 떨어져 있는 게 가장 옳단다."], ["Ask me no questions, but believe that what I am when I seem fickle or capricious to you, I am not of my own will, or for myself.", "내게 아무것도 묻지 말고, 다만 이것만은 믿어 다오. 내가 너에게 변덕스럽거나 종잡을 수 없어 보일 때, 그것은 내 뜻으로도 아니고 나 자신을 위해서도 아니라는 것을."], ["Believe, though we are stranger to each other than we have been, that I am unchanged to you within.", "우리가 전보다 서로 더 남남이 되었어도, 내 마음속에서는 너에게 변함이 없다는 것을 믿어 다오."], ["Forgive me for having ever darkened your dark home—I am a shadow on it, I know well—and let us never speak of this again.”", "일찍이 너의 어두운 집을 더 어둡게 한 것을 용서해 다오—내가 그 위에 드리운 그림자라는 걸 나도 잘 안다—그리고 이 이야기는 다시는 하지 말자꾸나.\""]], [["“Mama,”", "\"엄마,\""], ["sobbed Florence, “we are not to part?”", "플로렌스가 흐느꼈다, \"우리 헤어지는 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“We do this that we may not part,”", "\"우리는 헤어지지 않으려고 이렇게 하는 거란다,\""], ["said Edith.", "이디스가 말했다."], ["“Ask no more.", "\"더는 묻지 마."], ["Go, Florence!", "가렴, 플로렌스!"], ["My love and my remorse go with you!”", "내 사랑과 내 회한이 너와 함께 갈 거야!\""]], [["She embraced her, and dismissed her; and as Florence passed out of her room, Edith looked on the retiring figure, as if her good angel went out in that form, and left her to the haughty and indignant passions that now claimed her for their own, and set their seal upon her brow.", "그녀는 플로렌스를 껴안고, 그녀를 내보냈다. 그리고 플로렌스가 방을 나설 때, 이디스는 마치 자기의 선한 천사가 저 모습으로 떠나가, 이제 자기를 제 것으로 차지하고 이마에 제 봉인을 찍는 그 오만하고 분에 찬 격정들에게 자기를 내맡기는 것을 보듯이, 멀어져 가는 그 모습을 바라보았다."]], [["From that hour, Florence and she were, as they had been, no more.", "그때부터, 플로렌스와 그녀는 예전과 같은 사이가 더는 아니었다."], ["For days together, they would seldom meet, except at table, and when Mr Dombey was present.", "며칠씩 잇따라, 그들은 식탁에서, 그것도 돔비 씨가 있을 때 말고는 좀처럼 마주치지 않았다."], ["Then Edith, imperious, inflexible, and silent, never looked at her.", "그럴 때면 이디스는 오만하고, 굽힐 줄 모르며, 말이 없었고, 결코 그녀를 쳐다보지 않았다."], ["Whenever Mr Carker was of the party, as he often was, during the progress of Mr Dombey’s recovery, and afterwards, Edith held herself more removed from her, and was more distant towards her, than at other times.", "돔비 씨가 회복해 가는 동안에도, 또 그 후에도 자주 그랬듯이, 카커 씨가 그 자리에 있을 때면 이디스는 평소보다도 한층 더 그녀에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 지냈고, 한층 더 그녀에게 쌀쌀맞게 대했다."], ["Yet she and Florence never encountered, when there was no one by, but she would embrace her as affectionately as of old, though not with the same relenting of her proud aspect; and often, when she had been out late, she would steal up to Florence’s room, as she had been used to do, in the dark, and whisper “Good-night,”", "그러나 그녀와 플로렌스는, 곁에 아무도 없을 때 마주치면, 비록 오만한 표정이 예전처럼 누그러지지는 않았어도, 옛날처럼 다정하게 서로를 껴안곤 했다. 그리고 그녀가 밤늦게 외출했다 돌아온 날이면, 으레 그랬듯이 어둠 속에서 플로렌스의 방으로 살그머니 올라가, 베개 위에 대고 \"잘 자거라\""], ["on her pillow.", "하고 속삭이곤 했다."], ["When unconscious, in her slumber, of such visits, Florence would sometimes awake, as from a dream of those words, softly spoken, and would seem to feel the touch of lips upon her face.", "잠결에 그런 방문을 의식하지 못하면서도, 플로렌스는 때때로 그 부드럽게 속삭인 말들의 꿈에서 깨어나듯 깨어나, 얼굴에 닿는 입술의 감촉을 느끼는 것만 같았다."], ["But less and less often as the months went on.", "그러나 달이 갈수록 그런 일은 점점 드물어졌다."]], [["And now the void in Florence’s own heart began again, indeed, to make a solitude around her.", "이제 플로렌스 자신의 가슴속 공허함이 정말로 다시금 그녀의 둘레에 외로움을 빚어내기 시작했다."], ["As the image of the father whom she loved had insensibly become a mere abstraction, so Edith, following the fate of all the rest about whom her affections had entwined themselves, was fleeting, fading, growing paler in the distance, every day.", "그녀가 사랑하던 아버지의 영상이 어느새 한낱 추상으로 변해 버렸듯이, 이디스 역시, 그녀의 애정이 휘감겨 있던 다른 모든 이들의 운명을 따라, 날마다 멀어지고, 흐려지고, 점점 더 창백해져 갔다. 조금씩 조금씩, 그녀는 자기가 한때 그러했던 모습의 물러가는 유령처럼 플로렌스에게서 멀어져 갔다."], ["Little by little, she receded from Florence, like the retiring ghost of what she had been; little by little, the chasm between them widened and seemed deeper; little by little, all the power of earnestness and tenderness she had shown, was frozen up in the bold, angry hardihood with which she stood, upon the brink of a deep precipice unseen by Florence, daring to look down.", "조금씩 조금씩, 둘 사이의 틈은 벌어지고 더 깊어 보였다. 조금씩 조금씩, 그녀가 보여 주었던 진지함과 다정함의 온갖 힘은, 플로렌스에게는 보이지 않는 깊은 벼랑 끝에 서서 감히 아래를 내려다보는, 대담하고 성난 강인함 속에 얼어붙어 갔다."]], [["There was but one consideration to set against the heavy loss of Edith, and though it was slight comfort to her burdened heart, she tried to think it some relief.", "이디스를 잃는다는 그 무거운 상실에 맞세울 만한 위안은 단 하나뿐이었고, 비록 그것이 그녀의 짓눌린 가슴에 변변찮은 위로일지라도, 그녀는 그것을 얼마간의 위안으로 여겨 보려 애썼다."], ["No longer divided between her affection and duty to the two, Florence could love both and do no injustice to either.", "더는 두 사람을 향한 애정과 의무 사이에서 갈라지지 않게 되었으니, 플로렌스는 둘 다를 사랑하면서도 어느 쪽에게도 부당하지 않을 수 있었다."], ["As shadows of her fond imagination, she could give them equal place in her own bosom, and wrong them with no doubts.", "자기의 애틋한 상상 속 그림자로서, 그녀는 두 사람을 자기 가슴속에 똑같은 자리에 놓고, 아무런 의혹으로도 그들을 그르치지 않을 수 있었다."]], [["So she tried to do.", "그래서 그녀는 그렇게 하려 애썼다."], ["At times, and often too, wondering speculations on the cause of this change in Edith, would obtrude themselves upon her mind and frighten her; but in the calm of its abandonment once more to silent grief and loneliness, it was not a curious mind.", "때때로, 그것도 자주, 이디스의 이 변화의 원인에 대한 의아한 추측들이 그녀의 마음속에 비집고 들어와 그녀를 겁나게 했다. 그러나 다시 말없는 슬픔과 외로움에 자기를 내맡긴 그 고요함 속에서, 그녀의 마음은 캐묻기를 좋아하는 마음이 아니었다."], ["Florence had only to remember that her star of promise was clouded in the general gloom that hung upon the house, and to weep and be resigned.", "플로렌스로서는 다만, 자기의 약속의 별이 이 집을 뒤덮은 전체적인 침울함 속에 흐려졌음을 떠올리며, 눈물 흘리고 체념하는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Thus living, in a dream wherein the overflowing love of her young heart expended itself on airy forms, and in a real world where she had experienced little but the rolling back of that strong tide upon itself, Florence grew to be seventeen.", "이렇게, 어린 가슴의 넘쳐흐르는 사랑을 덧없는 형상들에 쏟아붓는 꿈속에서, 그리고 그 거센 물결이 도로 자기에게로 밀려오는 것 말고는 거의 겪어 본 것이 없는 현실 세계에서 살아가며, 플로렌스는 열일곱 살이 되었다."], ["Timid and retiring as her solitary life had made her, it had not embittered her sweet temper, or her earnest nature.", "그 외로운 삶이 그녀를 소심하고 내성적으로 만들기는 했어도, 그녀의 다정한 성품이나 진실한 천성을 모질게 만들지는 못했다."], ["A child in innocent simplicity; a woman in her modest self-reliance, and her deep intensity of feeling; both child and woman seemed at once expressed in her face and fragile delicacy of shape, and gracefully to mingle there;—as if the spring should be unwilling to depart when summer came, and sought to blend the earlier beauties of the flowers with their bloom.", "천진한 순박함에 있어서는 아이였고, 정숙한 자립심과 깊고 강렬한 감정에 있어서는 여인이었으니, 아이와 여인이 동시에 그녀의 얼굴과 가냘프고 여린 자태에 함께 드러나, 거기서 우아하게 어우러지는 듯했다—마치 봄이 여름이 와도 차마 떠나려 하지 않고, 꽃들의 이른 아름다움을 그들의 만개와 뒤섞으려 하는 것처럼."], ["But in her thrilling voice, in her calm eyes, sometimes in a sage ethereal light that seemed to rest upon her head, and always in a certain pensive air upon her beauty, there was an expression, such as had been seen in the dead boy; and the council in the Servants’ Hall whispered so among themselves, and shook their heads, and ate and drank the more, in a closer bond of good-fellowship.", "그러나 그녀의 떨리는 목소리에, 그녀의 고요한 눈에, 때로는 그녀의 머리 위에 깃든 듯한 어떤 슬기롭고 영묘한 빛에, 그리고 언제나 그녀의 아름다움 위에 어린 어떤 수심 어린 분위기에는, 죽은 소년에게서 보였던 것과 같은 어떤 표정이 있었다. 하인 방의 회의는 자기들끼리 그렇게 수군거리고, 고개를 가로저으며, 더욱 끈끈한 우의의 결속 속에서 더 많이 먹고 마셨다."]], [["This observant body had plenty to say of Mr and Mrs Dombey, and of Mr Carker, who appeared to be a mediator between them, and who came and went as if he were trying to make peace, but never could.", "이 눈썰미 좋은 무리에게는 돔비 씨와 돔비 부인에 대해, 그리고 두 사람 사이의 중재자로 보이는, 화해를 붙여 보려는 듯 오가면서도 끝내 그러지 못하는 카커 씨에 대해 할 말이 많았다."], ["They all deplored the uncomfortable state of affairs, and all agreed that Mrs Pipchin (whose unpopularity was not to be surpassed) had some hand in it; but, upon the whole, it was agreeable to have so good a subject for a rallying point, and they made a great deal of it, and enjoyed themselves very much.", "그들은 모두 이 불편한 사태를 개탄했고, 핍친 부인(그 인기 없음으로 말하자면 따를 자가 없었다)이 여기에 어느 정도 손을 댔다는 데 모두 의견을 같이했다. 그러나 대체로, 그토록 좋은 화제를 집결점으로 삼게 된 것은 즐거운 일이었고, 그들은 그것을 한껏 부풀려 가며 무척 재미있어했다."]], [["The general visitors who came to the house, and those among whom Mr and Mrs Dombey visited, thought it a pretty equal match, as to haughtiness, at all events, and thought nothing more about it.", "이 집을 찾아오는 일반 방문객들, 그리고 돔비 씨 부부가 찾아가는 사람들은, 적어도 오만함이라는 점에서는 어지간히 어울리는 짝이라고 여겼고, 그 이상은 더 생각하지 않았다. 등을 드러낸 그 젊은 아가씨는 스큐턴 부인이 죽은 뒤로 한동안 모습을 보이지 않았다."], ["The young lady with the back did not appear for some time after Mrs Skewton’s death; observing to some particular friends, with her usual engaging little scream, that she couldn’t separate the family from a notion of tombstones, and horrors of that sort; but when she did come, she saw nothing wrong, except Mr Dombey’s wearing a bunch of gold seals to his watch, which shocked her very much, as an exploded superstition.", "그녀는 특별히 친한 몇몇 벗들에게 평소처럼 매혹적인 작은 비명을 지르며, 자기는 그 집안을 묘비라느니 그런 끔찍한 것들에 대한 관념과 떼어 놓을 수가 없다고 말했다. 그러나 막상 다시 찾아왔을 때, 그녀는 돔비 씨가 시계에 금 인장 한 묶음을 차고 다니는 것 말고는 잘못된 데를 하나도 보지 못했는데, 그것만은 이미 타파된 미신이라며 그녀를 몹시 충격에 빠뜨렸다."], ["This youthful fascinator considered a daughter-in-law objectionable in principle; otherwise, she had nothing to say against Florence, but that she sadly wanted “style”—which might mean back, perhaps.", "이 젊은 매혹꾼은 며느리란 원칙상 못마땅한 존재라고 여겼다. 그것 말고는 플로렌스에 대해 흠잡을 것이 없었으나, 다만 \"맵시\"가 가엾을 만큼 모자란다는 것뿐이었으니—이는 어쩌면 등을 두고 하는 말이었을 것이다."], ["Many, who only came to the house on state occasions, hardly knew who Florence was, and said, going home, “Indeed, was that Miss Dombey, in the corner?", "격식 차린 행사 때만 이 집에 오는 많은 이들은 플로렌스가 누구인지조차 거의 알지 못했고, 집으로 돌아가며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"아니, 저 구석에 있던 사람이 돔비 양이었어?"], ["Very pretty, but a little delicate and thoughtful in appearance!”", "아주 예쁘긴 한데, 좀 가냘프고 수심에 잠긴 모습이더군!\""]], [["None the less so, certainly, for her life of the last six months.", "지난 여섯 달의 그런 삶을 생각하면, 확실히 그럴 만도 했다."], ["Florence took her seat at the dinner-table, on the day before the second anniversary of her father’s marriage to Edith (Mrs Skewton had been lying stricken with paralysis when the first came round), with an uneasiness, amounting to dread.", "플로렌스는 아버지가 이디스와 결혼한 지 두 돌이 되기 전날(첫 돌이 돌아왔을 때 스큐턴 부인은 마비에 걸려 누워 있었다), 두려움에 가까운 불안을 안고 만찬 식탁에 앉았다."], ["She had no other warrant for it, than the occasion, the expression of her father’s face, in the hasty glance she caught of it, and the presence of Mr Carker, which, always unpleasant to her, was more so on this day, than she had ever felt it before.", "그렇게 느낄 근거라곤, 그 날짜라는 것과, 흘끗 스쳐 본 아버지 얼굴의 표정과, 늘 그녀에게 불쾌했지만 이날따라 그 어느 때보다도 더 불쾌하게 느껴지는 카커 씨의 존재밖에는 없었다."]], [["Edith was richly dressed, for she and Mr Dombey were engaged in the evening to some large assembly, and the dinner-hour that day was late.", "이디스는 화려하게 차려입고 있었다. 그녀와 돔비 씨는 저녁에 어느 큰 모임에 가기로 되어 있었고, 그날 만찬 시간은 늦었기 때문이다."], ["She did not appear until they were seated at table, when Mr Carker rose and led her to her chair.", "그녀는 모두가 식탁에 앉을 때까지 모습을 보이지 않았는데, 그때 카커 씨가 일어나 그녀를 의자로 안내했다."], ["Beautiful and lustrous as she was, there was that in her face and air which seemed to separate her hopelessly from Florence, and from everyone, for ever more.", "아름답고 눈부신 그녀였지만, 그녀의 얼굴과 분위기에는 그녀를 플로렌스로부터, 또 모든 이로부터 영영 가망 없이 떼어 놓는 듯한 무언가가 있었다."], ["And yet, for an instant, Florence saw a beam of kindness in her eyes, when they were turned on her, that made the distance to which she had withdrawn herself a greater cause of sorrow and regret than ever.", "그런데도, 한순간, 플로렌스는 그녀의 눈이 자기에게로 향했을 때 거기 어린 한 줄기 다정함의 빛을 보았으니, 그것은 그녀가 물러나 가 있는 그 거리를 그 어느 때보다도 더 큰 슬픔과 회한의 원인으로 만들었다."]], [["There was very little said at dinner.", "만찬 중에는 이야기가 거의 오가지 않았다."], ["Florence heard her father speak to Mr Carker sometimes on business matters, and heard him softly reply, but she paid little attention to what they said, and only wished the dinner at an end.", "플로렌스는 아버지가 이따금 카커 씨에게 사업 문제를 이야기하는 것을, 그리고 그가 나직이 대답하는 것을 들었으나, 그들이 무슨 말을 하는지에는 거의 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 그저 만찬이 끝나기만을 바랐다."], ["When the dessert was placed upon the table, and they were left alone, with no servant in attendance, Mr Dombey, who had been several times clearing his throat in a manner that augured no good, said:", "후식이 식탁에 차려지고, 시중드는 하인 하나 없이 그들만 남게 되자, 아까부터 몇 번이나 불길한 징조 같은 헛기침을 해 대던 돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Mrs Dombey, you know, I suppose, that I have instructed the housekeeper that there will be some company to dinner here to-morrow.”", "\"돔비 부인, 아마 알고 있을 텐데, 내가 가정부에게 내일 이곳에 손님 몇이 만찬에 올 거라고 일러 두었소.\""]], [["“I do not dine at home,”", "\"저는 집에서 만찬을 들지 않겠어요,\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Not a large party,”", "\"큰 모임은 아니오,\""], ["pursued Mr Dombey, with an indifferent assumption of not having heard her; “merely some twelve or fourteen.", "돔비 씨가, 그녀의 말을 듣지 못한 척 무심한 태도를 가장하며 말을 이었다. \"그저 열둘이나 열넷 정도요."], ["My sister, Major Bagstock, and some others whom you know but slightly.”", "내 누이와 배그스톡 소령, 그리고 당신이 잘은 모르는 몇몇 사람들이오.\""]], [["“I do not dine at home,”", "\"저는 집에서 만찬을 들지 않겠어요,\""], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되풀이했다."]], [["“However doubtful reason I may have, Mrs Dombey,”", "\"내가 지금 이 날을 그리 즐거운 기억으로 간직할 이유가 아무리 미심쩍다 한들, 돔비 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, still going majestically on, as if she had not spoken, “to hold the occasion in very pleasant remembrance just now, there are appearances in these things which must be maintained before the world.", "돔비 씨가, 그녀가 말하지 않기라도 한 듯 여전히 위엄 있게 말을 이어 갔다, \"세상 앞에서 지켜야 할 체면이라는 것이 이런 일에는 있는 법이오."], ["If you have no respect for yourself, Mrs Dombey—”", "당신이 자기 자신에 대한 존중심이 없다 하더라도, 돔비 부인—\""]], [["“I have none,”", "\"저는 그런 거 없어요,\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["cried Mr Dombey, striking his hand upon the table, “hear me if you please.", "돔비 씨가 손으로 식탁을 치며 외쳤다, \"제발 내 말을 들으시오."], ["I say, if you have no respect for yourself—”", "내 말은, 당신이 자기 자신에 대한 존중심이 없다 하더라도—\""]], [["“And I say I have none,”", "\"그리고 제 말은, 그런 게 없다는 거예요,\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["He looked at her; but the face she showed him in return would not have changed, if death itself had looked.", "그는 그녀를 바라보았다. 그러나 그 대답으로 그녀가 그에게 내보인 얼굴은, 죽음 그 자체가 들여다본다 해도 달라지지 않았으리라."]], [["“Carker,”", "\"카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, turning more quietly to that gentleman, “as you have been my medium of communication with Mrs Dombey on former occasions, and as I choose to preserve the decencies of life, so far as I am individually concerned, I will trouble you to have the goodness to inform Mrs Dombey that if she has no respect for herself, I have some respect for myself, and therefore insist on my arrangements for to-morrow.”", "돔비 씨가 한결 차분하게 그 신사 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다, \"전에도 자네가 나와 돔비 부인 사이의 전달 매개가 되어 준 적이 있고, 또 나로서는 적어도 나 개인에 관한 한 삶의 예의를 지키려 하니, 수고스럽겠지만 돔비 부인에게 이렇게 알려 주게. 부인이 자기 자신에 대한 존중심이 없다 하더라도, 나는 나 자신에 대한 존중심은 좀 있으니, 따라서 내일 일에 대한 내 결정을 고수하겠노라고.\""]], [["“Tell your sovereign master, Sir,”", "\"당신의 군주이신 주인 나리께 전하세요,\""], ["said Edith, “that I will take leave to speak to him on this subject by-and-bye, and that I will speak to him alone.”", "이디스가 말했다, \"제가 이 문제에 대해서는 이따가 말씀드릴 것이며, 그것도 그분과 단둘이서 말씀드리겠노라고요.\""]], [["“Mr Carker, Madam,”", "\"카커 씨는, 부인,\""], ["said her husband, “being in possession of the reason which obliges me to refuse you that privilege, shall be absolved from the delivery of any such message.”", "그녀의 남편이 말했다, \"내가 당신에게 그 특권을 거절하지 않을 수 없게 하는 이유를 알고 있으니, 그런 전갈은 전하지 않아도 되네.\""], ["He saw her eyes move, while he spoke, and followed them with his own.", "그는 말하면서 그녀의 눈이 움직이는 것을 보았고, 자기 눈으로 그 시선을 좇았다."]], [["“Your daughter is present, Sir,”", "\"당신 딸이 여기 있어요,\""], ["said Edith.", "이디스가 말했다."]], [["“My daughter will remain present,”", "\"내 딸은 여기 그대로 있을 거요,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["Florence, who had risen, sat down again, hiding her face in her hands, and trembling.", "일어서 있던 플로렌스가 다시 자리에 앉아, 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 떨었다."]], [["“My daughter, Madam”—began Mr Dombey.", "\"내 딸은, 부인—\" 돔비 씨가 입을 열었다."]], [["But Edith stopped him, in a voice which, although not raised in the least, was so clear, emphatic, and distinct, that it might have been heard in a whirlwind.", "그러나 이디스가, 조금도 높이지 않았는데도 어찌나 또렷하고 힘 있고 분명한지 회오리바람 속에서도 들렸을 법한 목소리로 그를 가로막았다."]], [["“I tell you I will speak to you alone,”", "\"단둘이서 말씀드리겠다고 했잖아요,\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“If you are not mad, heed what I say.”", "\"정신이 나간 게 아니라면, 제 말을 새겨들으세요.\""]], [["“I have authority to speak to you, Madam,”", "\"나는 당신에게 말할 권한이 있소, 부인,\""], ["returned her husband, “when and where I please; and it is my pleasure to speak here and now.”", "그녀의 남편이 응수했다, \"언제든 어디서든 내가 원하는 대로. 그리고 여기서 지금 말하는 것이 내 뜻이오.\""]], [["She rose up as if to leave the room; but sat down again, and looking at him with all outward composure, said, in the same voice:", "그녀는 방을 나가려는 듯 몸을 일으켰다. 그러나 다시 자리에 앉아, 겉으로는 더없이 침착하게 그를 바라보며, 똑같은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“You shall!”", "\"그렇게 하세요!\""]], [["“I must tell you first, that there is a threatening appearance in your manner, Madam,”", "\"먼저 말해 두어야겠는데, 당신의 태도에는 위협적인 기색이 있소, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, “which does not become you.”", "돔비 씨가 말했다, \"그건 당신에게 어울리지 않소.\""]], [["She laughed.", "그녀가 웃었다."], ["The shaken diamonds in her hair started and trembled.", "머리에 꽂힌 다이아몬드들이 흔들리며 떨려 반짝였다."], ["There are fables of precious stones that would turn pale, their wearer being in danger.", "보석을 두른 이가 위험에 처하면 빛이 바랜다는 보석에 관한 우화들이 있다."], ["Had these been such, their imprisoned rays of light would have taken flight that moment, and they would have been as dull as lead.", "만일 이것들이 그런 보석이었더라면, 그 갇혀 있던 빛줄기들이 그 순간 달아나 버려, 그것들은 납덩이처럼 칙칙해졌으리라."]], [["Carker listened, with his eyes cast down.", "카커는 눈을 내리깐 채 듣고 있었다."]], [["“As to my daughter, Madam,”", "\"내 딸로 말하자면, 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, resuming the thread of his discourse, “it is by no means inconsistent with her duty to me, that she should know what conduct to avoid.", "돔비 씨가 자기 이야기의 실마리를 다시 이어 가며 말했다, \"그 애가 피해야 할 행실이 무엇인지를 아는 것은 나에 대한 그 애의 의무와 결코 어긋나지 않소."], ["At present you are a very strong example to her of this kind, and I hope she may profit by it.”", "지금 당신은 그 애에게 그런 종류의 아주 강력한 본보기를 보여 주고 있으니, 그 애가 거기서 교훈을 얻기를 바라오.\""]], [["“I would not stop you now,”", "\"저는 지금 당신을 막지 않겠어요,\""], ["returned his wife, immoveable in eye, and voice, and attitude; “I would not rise and go away, and save you the utterance of one word, if the room were burning.”", "그의 아내가, 눈도 목소리도 자세도 미동 없이 응수했다, \"설령 이 방이 불타고 있다 한들, 일어나 나가서 당신이 한마디라도 입에 올리는 수고를 덜어 드리지는 않겠어요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey moved his head, as if in a sarcastic acknowledgment of the attention, and resumed.", "돔비 씨는, 그 경청에 대한 비꼬는 답례라도 하듯 고개를 까딱하고는, 말을 이어 갔다."], ["But not with so much self-possession as before; for Edith’s quick uneasiness in reference to Florence, and Edith’s indifference to him and his censure, chafed and galled him like a stiffening wound.", "그러나 아까만큼 자제력을 갖추지는 못했으니, 플로렌스를 두고 보이는 이디스의 그 재빠른 불안과, 그와 그의 질책을 향한 이디스의 무관심이, 곪아 가는 상처처럼 그를 쓰라리게 하고 화나게 했기 때문이다."]], [["“Mrs Dombey,”", "\"돔비 부인,\""], ["said he, “it may not be inconsistent with my daughter’s improvement to know how very much to be lamented, and how necessary to be corrected, a stubborn disposition is, especially when it is indulged in—unthankfully indulged in, I will add—after the gratification of ambition and interest.", "그가 말했다, \"완고한 기질이라는 것이 얼마나 개탄스러운 것이며 얼마나 마땅히 고쳐져야 하는 것인지를—특히 그것이, 야심과 잇속이 충족된 뒤에 부려질 때, 그것도 은혜를 모른 채 부려질 때 더더욱 그러하다는 것을—내 딸이 아는 것이 그 애의 향상에 어긋나지는 않을 것이오."], ["Both of which, I believe, had some share in inducing you to occupy your present station at this board.”", "내 생각에는, 그 야심과 잇속 둘 다가, 당신이 지금 이 식탁에서 차지하고 있는 자리에 앉도록 당신을 부추기는 데 얼마간 한몫을 했을 거요.\""]], [["“No!", "\"아니에요!"], ["I would not rise, and go away, and save you the utterance of one word,”", "저는 일어나 나가서, 당신이 한마디라도 입에 올리는 수고를 덜어 드리지는 않겠어요,\""], ["she repeated, exactly as before, “if the room were burning.”", "그녀가, 앞서와 똑같이 되풀이했다, \"설령 이 방이 불타고 있다 한들요.\""]], [["“It may be natural enough, Mrs Dombey,”", "\"당신이 이런 불쾌한 진실들을 듣는 사람들 앞에서 거북해하는 것도 어쩌면 충분히 자연스러운 일일 것이오, 돔비 부인,\""], ["he pursued, “that you should be uneasy in the presence of any auditors of these disagreeable truths; though why”—he could not hide his real feeling here, or keep his eyes from glancing gloomily at Florence—“why anyone can give them greater force and point than myself, whom they so nearly concern, I do not pretend to understand.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 왜\"—그는 여기서 자기의 진짜 감정을 숨기지 못했고, 음울하게 플로렌스를 흘끔거리는 눈길을 어쩌지 못했다—\"왜 다른 누군가가, 이 일과 그토록 깊이 관련된 나 자신보다도 그것들에 더한 무게와 날카로움을 더할 수 있다는 건지, 나로서는 도무지 이해하는 척조차 못 하겠소."], ["It may be natural enough that you should object to hear, in anybody’s presence, that there is a rebellious principle within you which you cannot curb too soon; which you must curb, Mrs Dombey; and which, I regret to say, I remember to have seen manifested—with some doubt and displeasure, on more than one occasion before our marriage—towards your deceased mother.", "당신 안에 당신이 한시바삐 억눌러야 할 반항적인 기질이 있다는 것을—당신이 반드시 억눌러야만 하는 거요, 돔비 부인—누군가의 면전에서 듣기 싫어하는 것도 어쩌면 충분히 자연스러운 일일 거요. 그리고 유감스럽게도, 나는 그 기질이—우리가 결혼하기 전에도 한 번 이상, 다소의 의구심과 불쾌감을 품고 본 적이 있는데—당신의 돌아가신 어머니를 향해 드러나는 것을 기억하고 있소."], ["But you have the remedy in your own hands.", "하지만 그 해법은 당신 자신의 손안에 있소."], ["I by no means forgot, when I began, that my daughter was present, Mrs Dombey.", "시작할 때, 나는 내 딸이 여기 있다는 것을 결코 잊지 않았소, 돔비 부인."], ["I beg you will not forget, to-morrow, that there are several persons present; and that, with some regard to appearances, you will receive your company in a becoming manner.”", "내일은, 부디 잊지 마시오, 여러 사람이 자리에 있다는 것을. 그리고 체면을 얼마쯤은 차려서, 당신의 손님들을 합당한 태도로 맞아 주기를 바라오.\""]], [["“So it is not enough,” said Edith, “that you know what has passed between yourself and me; it is not enough that you can look here,”", "\"그러니까 당신은 그것으로도 모자란다는 거군요,\""], ["pointing at Carker, who still listened, with his eyes cast down, “and be reminded of the affronts you have put upon me; it is not enough that you can look here,” pointing to Florence with a hand that slightly trembled for the first and only time, “and think of what you have done, and of the ingenious agony, daily, hourly, constant, you have made me feel in doing it; it is not enough that this day, of all others in the year, is memorable to me for a struggle (well-deserved, but not conceivable by such as you) in which I wish I had died!", "이디스가 말했다, \"당신과 나 사이에 있었던 일을 당신이 아는 것으로도 모자라고, 여기를 바라보며\"—여전히 눈을 내리깐 채 듣고 있는 카커를 가리키며—\"당신이 내게 가한 모욕들을 떠올릴 수 있는 것으로도 모자라고, 여기를 바라보며\"—처음이자 마지막으로 살짝 떨리는 손으로 플로렌스를 가리키며—\"당신이 한 짓을, 그리고 그 짓을 하면서 날마다, 시시각각, 끊임없이 내게 안겨 준 그 교묘한 고통을 떠올릴 수 있는 것으로도 모자라고, 한 해의 그 어느 날보다도 내게는, 차라리 그때 죽었더라면 싶은 어떤 몸부림(마땅히 치러야 할 것이었으나, 당신 같은 사람으로서는 짐작도 못 할 그런 몸부림) 때문에 잊지 못할 바로 이날이라는 것으로도 모자란다는 거군요!"], ["You add to all this, do you, the last crowning meanness of making her a witness of the depth to which I have fallen; when you know that you have made me sacrifice to her peace, the only gentle feeling and interest of my life, when you know that for her sake, I would now if I could—but I can not, my soul recoils from you too much—submit myself wholly to your will, and be the meekest vassal that you have!”", "이 모든 것에 더해서, 당신은 내가 얼마나 깊이 떨어졌는지를 저 아이에게 똑똑히 보게 하는 그 마지막 극치의 비열함까지 보태는 거예요. 당신은 알면서도—내가 저 아이의 평안을 위해, 내 생애의 단 하나뿐인 다정한 감정과 애착을 희생하게 만든 것을 알면서도—내가 저 아이를 위해서라면, 할 수만 있다면 지금이라도—하지만 나는 할 수 없어요, 내 영혼이 당신에게서 너무도 움츠러드니까—내 자신을 온전히 당신의 뜻에 맡기고, 당신이 거느린 가장 온순한 종이 되었으리라는 것을 알면서도요!\""]], [["This was not the way to minister to Mr Dombey’s greatness.", "이것은 돔비 씨의 위대함에 봉사하는 길이 아니었다."], ["The old feeling was roused by what she said, into a stronger and fiercer existence than it had ever had.", "그녀의 말에 그 해묵은 감정이, 그것이 일찍이 지녀 본 적 없는 더 강하고 더 사나운 존재로 일깨워졌다."], ["Again, his neglected child, at this rough passage of his life, put forth by even this rebellious woman, as powerful where he was powerless, and everything where he was nothing!", "다시 한번, 그의 생애의 이 거친 길목에서, 방치된 자기 자식이, 이 반항적인 여자의 입을 통해서까지도, 그가 무력한 곳에서 강력한 존재로, 그가 아무것도 아닌 곳에서 전부인 존재로 내세워진 것이다!"]], [["He turned on Florence, as if it were she who had spoken, and bade her leave the room.", "그는 마치 말을 한 것이 플로렌스이기라도 한 듯 그녀에게로 홱 돌아서서, 방에서 나가라고 명했다."], ["Florence with her covered face obeyed, trembling and weeping as she went.", "플로렌스는 얼굴을 가린 채, 나가면서 떨며 울며 그 말에 따랐다."]], [["“I understand, Madam,”", "\"이제 알겠소, 부인,\" 돔비 씨가 성난 승리의 홍조를 띠며 말했다, \"당신의 애정을 그쪽으로 돌려놓은 그 반항의 정신을 말이오."], ["said Mr Dombey, with an angry flush of triumph, “the spirit of opposition that turned your affections in that channel, but they have been met, Mrs Dombey; they have been met, and turned back!”", "하지만 그것은 막혔소, 돔비 부인. 그것은 막혔고, 도로 돌려보내졌소!\""]], [["“The worse for you!”", "\"당신에게는 더 안된 일이죠!\""], ["she answered, with her voice and manner still unchanged.", "그녀가, 여전히 변함없는 목소리와 태도로 대답했다. \"암요!\""], ["“Ay!” for he turned sharply when she said so, “what is the worse for me, is twenty million times the worse for you.", "그녀가 그렇게 말하자 그가 홱 돌아섰으므로 그녀가 덧붙였다, \"내게 안된 일은, 당신에게는 그 이천만 배로 안된 일이에요."], ["Heed that, if you heed nothing else.”", "다른 건 다 흘려듣더라도 그것만은 새겨들으세요.\""]], [["The arch of diamonds spanning her dark hair, flashed and glittered like a starry bridge.", "그녀의 검은 머리 위에 걸린 다이아몬드의 아치가 별들의 다리처럼 번쩍이며 반짝였다."], ["There was no warning in them, or they would have turned as dull and dim as tarnished honour.", "거기에는 아무런 경고도 없었으니, 그렇지 않았더라면 그것들은 빛바랜 명예처럼 칙칙하고 흐릿하게 변했으리라."], ["Carker still sat and listened, with his eyes cast down.", "카커는 여전히 눈을 내리깐 채 앉아 듣고 있었다."]], [["“Mrs Dombey,”", "\"돔비 부인,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, resuming as much as he could of his arrogant composure, “you will not conciliate me, or turn me from any purpose, by this course of conduct.”", "돔비 씨가, 자기의 오만한 침착함을 되도록 추스르며 말했다, \"당신은 이런 식의 행동으로는 나를 회유하지도, 어떤 목적에서든 나를 돌려세우지도 못할 거요.\""]], [["“It is the only true although it is a faint expression of what is within me,”", "\"이건 내 안에 있는 것에 비하면 희미한 표현일망정, 그래도 유일하게 참된 표현이에요,\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“But if I thought it would conciliate you, I would repress it, if it were repressible by any human effort.", "\"하지만 만일 이것이 당신을 회유하리라고 생각한다면, 인간의 어떤 노력으로든 억누를 수 있는 것이라면, 나는 이것을 억눌렀을 거예요."], ["I will do nothing that you ask.”", "나는 당신이 청하는 건 아무것도 하지 않겠어요.\""]], [["“I am not accustomed to ask, Mrs Dombey,”", "\"나는 청하는 데는 익숙지 않소, 돔비 부인,\""], ["he observed; “I direct.”", "그가 말했다. \"나는 명하는 거요.\""]], [["“I will hold no place in your house to-morrow, or on any recurrence of to-morrow.", "\"나는 내일도, 또 앞으로 그 어떤 내일이 돌아와도, 당신의 집에서 아무 자리도 지키지 않겠어요."], ["I will be exhibited to no one, as the refractory slave you purchased, such a time.", "나는 그 누구에게도, 당신이 어느 때에 사들인 말 안 듣는 노예라고 전시되지 않겠어요."], ["If I kept my marriage day, I would keep it as a day of shame.", "내가 내 결혼 기념일을 지킨다면, 그것을 치욕의 날로 지킬 거예요. 자기 자신에 대한 존중심이라니!"], ["Self-respect!", "세상 앞에서의 체면이라니!"], ["appearances before the world!", "그런 것들이 내게 무슨 의미가 있나요?"], ["what are these to me? You have done all you can to make them nothing to me, and they are nothing.”", "당신은 그것들이 내게 아무 의미도 없게 만들려고 할 수 있는 짓을 다 했고, 그래서 그것들은 아무 의미도 없어요.\""]], [["“Carker,”", "\"카커,\""], ["said Mr Dombey, speaking with knitted brows, and after a moment’s consideration, “Mrs Dombey is so forgetful of herself and me in all this, and places me in a position so unsuited to my character, that I must bring this state of matters to a close.”", "돔비 씨가, 미간을 찌푸린 채, 한순간 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다, \"돔비 부인은 이 모든 일에서 자기 자신과 나를 어찌나 잊어버리고, 또 나를 내 성품에 도무지 어울리지 않는 처지에 놓아두는지, 나는 이 사태를 끝맺지 않을 수 없네.\""]], [["“Release me, then,”", "\"그렇다면 나를 풀어 주세요,\""], ["said Edith, immoveable in voice, in look, and bearing, as she had been throughout, “from the chain by which I am bound.", "이디스가, 줄곧 그래 왔듯이 목소리도 표정도 거동도 미동 없이 말했다, \"나를 묶고 있는 그 사슬에서."], ["Let me go.”", "나를 가게 해 주세요.\""]], [["“Madam?”", "\"부인?\""], ["exclaimed Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Loose me.", "\"나를 풀어 줘요."], ["Set me free!”", "나를 자유롭게 해 줘요!\""]], [["“Madam?”", "\"부인?\""], ["he repeated, “Mrs Dombey?”", "그가 되풀이했다, \"돔비 부인?\""]], [["“Tell him,”", "\"그에게 전하세요,\""], ["said Edith, addressing her proud face to Carker, “that I wish for a separation between us.", "이디스가, 자기의 도도한 얼굴을 카커에게 향하며 말했다, \"내가 우리 사이의 별거를 바란다고요."], ["That there had better be one.", "그러는 편이 낫겠다고요."], ["That I recommend it to him.", "내가 그에게 그것을 권한다고요."], ["Tell him it may take place on his own terms—his wealth is nothing to me—but that it cannot be too soon.”", "그것이 그가 내거는 조건대로 이루어져도 좋다고—그의 재산 따위 내게는 아무것도 아니니까—다만 빠를수록 좋다고 전하세요.\""]], [["“Good Heaven, Mrs Dombey!”", "\"맙소사, 돔비 부인!\""], ["said her husband, with supreme amazement, “do you imagine it possible that I could ever listen to such a proposition?", "그녀의 남편이 더없는 경악을 띠며 말했다, \"내가 그런 제안에 귀를 기울이는 일이 가능하리라고 생각하는 거요?"], ["Do you know who I am, Madam?", "내가 누구인지 알기나 하오, 부인?"], ["Do you know what I represent?", "내가 무엇을 대표하는지 알기나 하오?"], ["Did you ever hear of Dombey and Son?", "돔비와 아들이라는 이름을 들어 본 적이나 있소?"], ["People to say that Mr Dombey—Mr Dombey!—was separated from his wife!", "사람들이 돔비 씨가—돔비 씨가!—제 아내와 별거했다고 입에 올린다는 거요!"], ["Common people to talk of Mr Dombey and his domestic affairs!", "미천한 것들이 돔비 씨와 그의 집안일을 떠들어 댄다는 거요!"], ["Do you seriously think, Mrs Dombey, that I would permit my name to be banded about in such connexion?", "정말로 생각하는 거요, 돔비 부인, 내가 내 이름이 그런 일에 얽혀 떠돌아다니도록 내버려 두리라고? 흥, 흥, 부인!"], ["Pooh, pooh, Madam!", "부끄러운 줄 아시오!"], ["Fie for shame! You’re absurd.”", "당신은 어처구니없는 사람이오.\""], ["Mr Dombey absolutely laughed.", "돔비 씨는 그야말로 소리 내어 웃었다."]], [["But not as she did.", "그러나 그녀가 웃은 식으로는 아니었다."], ["She had better have been dead than laugh as she did, in reply, with her intent look fixed upon him.", "그를 똑바로 응시하며 그렇게 웃어 보일 바에는, 그녀는 차라리 죽어 버리는 편이 나았으리라."], ["He had better have been dead, than sitting there, in his magnificence, to hear her.", "그리고 그는, 자기의 영화 속에 거기 앉아 그 웃음을 듣고 있을 바에는, 차라리 죽어 버리는 편이 나았으리라."]], [["“No, Mrs Dombey,”", "\"아니오, 돔비 부인,\""], ["he resumed.", "그가 다시 말을 이었다."], ["“No, Madam.", "\"아니오, 부인."], ["There is no possibility of separation between you and me, and therefore I the more advise you to be awakened to a sense of duty.", "당신과 나 사이에 별거란 있을 수 없는 일이오. 그러니 나는 당신이 더더욱 의무감을 깨닫게 되기를 권하는 바요."], ["And, Carker, as I was about to say to you—”", "그리고, 카커, 자네에게 막 말하려던 것인데—\""]], [["Mr Carker, who had sat and listened all this time, now raised his eyes, in which there was a bright unusual light.", "이제껏 줄곧 앉아 듣고 있던 카커 씨가, 이제 눈을 들었는데, 그 안에는 어떤 밝고 예사롭지 않은 빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["“—As I was about to say to you,”", "\"—자네에게 막 말하려던 것인데,\""], ["resumed Mr Dombey, “I must beg you, now that matters have come to this, to inform Mrs Dombey, that it is not the rule of my life to allow myself to be thwarted by anybody—anybody, Carker—or to suffer anybody to be paraded as a stronger motive for obedience in those who owe obedience to me than I am my self.", "돔비 씨가 다시 이었다, \"이제 사태가 이 지경에 이르렀으니, 부탁하건대 돔비 부인에게 알려 주게. 그 누구에게도—그 누구에게도 말일세, 카커—내가 가로막히도록 내버려 두는 것은, 또 나에게 복종할 의무가 있는 자들에게, 나 자신보다 더 강력한 복종의 동기로 그 누군가가 떠받들어지도록 내버려 두는 것은, 내 삶의 방식이 아니라고."], ["The mention that has been made of my daughter, and the use that is made of my daughter, in opposition to me, are unnatural.", "내 딸이 입에 오르내린 것, 그리고 내 딸이 나에게 맞서는 데 이용되고 있는 것은, 부자연스러운 일이오. 내 딸이 실제로 돔비 부인과 한패인지 아닌지, 나는 알지 못하고, 또 알 바도 아니오."], ["Whether my daughter is in actual concert with Mrs Dombey, I do not know, and do not care; but after what Mrs Dombey has said today, and my daughter has heard today, I beg you to make known to Mrs Dombey, that if she continues to make this house the scene of contention it has become, I shall consider my daughter responsible in some degree, on that lady’s own avowal, and shall visit her with my severe displeasure.", "하지만 오늘 돔비 부인이 한 말과, 오늘 내 딸이 들은 것으로 미루어, 부탁하건대 돔비 부인에게 알려 주게. 만일 그녀가 이 집을, 지금처럼 다툼의 무대로 계속 만든다면, 나는 저 부인 자신의 자백으로 보아 내 딸에게도 어느 정도 책임이 있다고 여길 것이며, 내 딸에게 나의 준엄한 노여움을 내릴 것이라고."], ["Mrs Dombey has asked ‘whether it is not enough,’ that she had done this and that.", "돔비 부인은 자기가 이러저러한 일을 한 것으로 '모자라느냐'고 물었소."], ["You will please to answer no, it is not enough.”", "부디 그녀에게 답해 주게, 아니다, 모자란다고.\""]], [["“A moment!”", "\"잠깐만요!\""], ["cried Carker, interposing, “permit me!", "카커가 끼어들며 외쳤다, \"허락해 주십시오!"], ["painful as my position is, at the best, and unusually painful in seeming to entertain a different opinion from you,”", "제 처지가 아무리 좋게 봐도 괴로운 것이고, 더욱이 어른과 다른 의견을 품은 것처럼 보이는 일이 유난히 괴로운 것입니다만,\""], ["addressing Mr Dombey, “I must ask, had you not better reconsider the question of a separation.", "돔비 씨에게 말하면서, \"여쭙지 않을 수 없습니다. 별거 문제를 다시 한번 생각해 보시는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까."], ["I know how incompatible it appears with your high public position, and I know how determined you are when you give Mrs Dombey to understand”—the light in his eyes fell upon her as he separated his words each from each, with the distinctness of so many bells—“that nothing but death can ever part you.", "그것이 어른의 드높은 공적 지위와 얼마나 양립하기 어려운지를 저도 압니다. 또 어른이 돔비 부인에게 이렇게 일러 주실 때—\"그의 눈 속의 그 빛이, 그가 한마디 한마디를 마치 여러 개의 종소리처럼 또렷이 떼어 가며 말할 때, 그녀에게 떨어졌다—\"오직 죽음만이 두 분을 갈라놓을 수 있다고, 그 외에는 아무것도 그럴 수 없다고 일러 주실 때, 그 결심이 얼마나 단호한지도 압니다."], ["Nothing else. But when you consider that Mrs Dombey, by living in this house, and making it as you have said, a scene of contention, not only has her part in that contention, but compromises Miss Dombey every day (for I know how determined you are), will you not relieve her from a continual irritation of spirit, and a continual sense of being unjust to another, almost intolerable?", "하지만 돔비 부인이 이 집에 살면서, 어른의 말씀대로 이 집을 다툼의 무대로 만드는 한, 그녀가 그 다툼에 자기 몫을 담당할 뿐 아니라, 날마다 돔비 양을 곤란하게 한다는 것을(어른의 결심이 얼마나 단호한지를 제가 아니까요) 헤아려 보시면, 그녀를 그 끊임없는 심령의 자극에서, 그리고 또 다른 사람에게 부당하게 굴고 있다는, 거의 견딜 수 없는 그 끊임없는 자각에서 풀어 주지 않으시렵니까?"], ["Does this not seem like—I do not say it is—sacrificing Mrs Dombey to the preservation of your preeminent and unassailable position?”", "이것은—그렇다고 말하는 건 아닙니다만—어른의 탁월하고 범할 수 없는 지위의 보존을 위해 돔비 부인을 희생시키는 것처럼 보이지 않습니까?\""]], [["Again the light in his eyes fell upon her, as she stood looking at her husband: now with an extraordinary and awful smile upon her face.", "다시 그의 눈 속의 빛이, 이제 자기 얼굴에 어떤 기이하고 무시무시한 미소를 띤 채 남편을 바라보며 서 있는 그녀에게 떨어졌다."]], [["“Carker,”", "\"카커,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, with a supercilious frown, and in a tone that was intended to be final, “you mistake your position in offering advice to me on such a point, and you mistake me (I am surprised to find) in the character of your advice.", "돔비 씨가, 오만하게 미간을 찌푸리며, 단호함을 의도한 어조로 응수했다, \"자네는 그런 문제에 대해 내게 조언을 함으로써 자네의 처지를 잘못 알고 있고, 또 (놀랍게도 알게 된 바이지만) 자네 조언의 성격에서 나를 잘못 알고 있네."], ["I have no more to say.”", "나는 더 할 말이 없네.\""]], [["“Perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면,\""], ["said Carker, with an unusual and indefinable taunt in his air, “you mistook my position, when you honoured me with the negotiations in which I have been engaged here”—with a motion of his hand towards Mrs Dombey.", "카커가, 어딘가 예사롭지 않고 형언키 어려운 조롱을 분위기에 담아 말했다, \"어른께서 제게 이곳에서 종사해 온 그 교섭을 맡기는 영예를 베푸셨을 때, 제 처지를 잘못 아셨는지도 모르지요\"—돔비 부인 쪽으로 손짓하며."]], [["“Not at all, Sir, not at all,”", "\"전혀, 그렇지 않네, 전혀,\""], ["returned the other haughtily.", "상대가 오만하게 응수했다."], ["“You were employed—”", "\"자네가 부려진 것은—\""]], [["“Being an inferior person, for the humiliation of Mrs Dombey.", "\"미천한 사람으로서, 돔비 부인을 욕보이기 위해서였지요. 제가 잊었군요."], ["I forgot. Oh, yes, it was expressly understood!”", "오, 그렇지요, 그것은 분명히 합의된 것이었습니다!\""], ["said Carker.", "카커가 말했다."], ["“I beg your pardon!”", "\"용서하십시오!\""]], [["As he bent his head to Mr Dombey, with an air of deference that accorded ill with his words, though they were humbly spoken, he moved it round towards her, and kept his watching eyes that way.", "그는, 비록 겸손하게 말하기는 했어도 그 말과는 어울리지 않는 공손한 태도로 돔비 씨에게 고개를 숙이면서, 그것을 그녀 쪽으로 돌리고, 살피는 눈길을 그쪽으로 둔 채로 있었다."]], [["She had better have turned hideous and dropped dead, than have stood up with such a smile upon her face, in such a fallen spirit’s majesty of scorn and beauty.", "그런 미소를 얼굴에 띤 채, 추락한 영혼의 그런 경멸과 아름다움의 위엄으로 일어서 있을 바에는, 그녀는 차라리 흉측해져 쓰러져 죽는 편이 나았으리라."], ["She lifted her hand to the tiara of bright jewels radiant on her head, and, plucking it off with a force that dragged and strained her rich black hair with heedless cruelty, and brought it tumbling wildly on her shoulders, cast the gems upon the ground.", "그녀는 머리 위에서 찬란히 빛나는 그 보석들의 보관(寶冠)으로 손을 들어 올려, 그것을 한 번에 잡아채는 그 힘에 풍성한 검은 머리가 인정사정없이 끌려 당겨져 어깨 위로 마구 흘러내리도록 하면서, 그 보석들을 바닥에 내던졌다."], ["From each arm, she unclasped a diamond bracelet, flung it down, and trod upon the glittering heap.", "양팔에서 그녀는 다이아몬드 팔찌를 풀어 내던지고, 반짝이는 그 무더기를 짓밟았다."], ["Without a word, without a shadow on the fire of her bright eye, without abatement of her awful smile, she looked on Mr Dombey to the last, in moving to the door; and left him.", "한마디 말도 없이, 그 빛나는 눈의 불길에 한 점 그늘도 없이, 그 무시무시한 미소를 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않은 채, 그녀는 문 쪽으로 움직여 가면서 마지막 순간까지 돔비 씨를 바라보았다. 그리고 그를 남겨 둔 채 떠났다."]], [["Florence had heard enough before quitting the room, to know that Edith loved her yet; that she had suffered for her sake; and that she had kept her sacrifices quiet, lest they should trouble her peace.", "플로렌스는 방을 나가기 전에, 이디스가 자기를 여전히 사랑한다는 것, 자기를 위해 괴로워해 왔다는 것, 그리고 자기의 평안을 어지럽힐까 두려워 그 희생들을 조용히 감춰 왔다는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 들었다."], ["She did not want to speak to her of this—she could not, remembering to whom she was opposed—but she wished, in one silent and affectionate embrace, to assure her that she felt it all, and thanked her.", "그녀는 그것에 대해 이디스에게 말하고 싶지는 않았다—그녀가 누구와 맞서고 있는지를 떠올리면 그럴 수도 없었다—그러나, 말없는 다정한 포옹 한 번으로, 자기가 그 모든 것을 다 느끼고 있으며 고마워한다는 것을 그녀에게 전하고 싶었다."]], [["Her father went out alone, that evening, and Florence issuing from her own chamber soon afterwards, went about the house in search of Edith, but unavailingly.", "그날 저녁 그녀의 아버지는 혼자 외출했고, 곧이어 자기 방에서 나온 플로렌스는 이디스를 찾아 온 집안을 돌아다녔으나 헛수고였다."], ["She was in her own rooms, where Florence had long ceased to go, and did not dare to venture now, lest she should unconsciously engender new trouble.", "이디스는 자기 방에 있었는데, 플로렌스는 오래전부터 그곳에 발을 끊은 터였고, 자기도 모르게 새로운 분란을 일으킬까 두려워 이제는 감히 들어가 볼 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["Still Florence hoping to meet her before going to bed, changed from room to room, and wandered through the house so splendid and so dreary, without remaining anywhere.", "그래도 플로렌스는 잠자리에 들기 전에 그녀를 만나기를 바라며, 이 방 저 방을 옮겨 다니고, 그토록 으리으리하면서도 그토록 음산한 그 집을 헤매었으나, 어디에도 머무르지 못했다."]], [["She was crossing a gallery of communication that opened at some little distance on the staircase, and was only lighted on great occasions, when she saw, through the opening, which was an arch, the figure of a man coming down some few stairs opposite.", "그녀가, 계단에서 조금 떨어진 곳으로 열려 있고 큰 행사 때만 불을 밝히는 어느 연결 회랑을 가로질러 가고 있을 때, 아치로 된 그 트인 곳 너머로, 맞은편 몇 계단을 내려오는 한 남자의 모습이 보였다."], ["Instinctively apprehensive of her father, whom she supposed it was, she stopped, in the dark, gazing through the arch into the light.", "그것이 자기 아버지라고 짐작하여 본능적으로 겁이 난 그녀는, 어둠 속에 멈춰 서서, 아치 너머의 불빛 속을 응시했다."], ["But it was Mr Carker coming down alone, and looking over the railing into the hall.", "그러나 그것은 혼자 내려오며 난간 너머로 현관 홀을 내려다보는 카커 씨였다."], ["No bell was rung to announce his departure, and no servant was in attendance.", "그의 떠남을 알리는 종도 울리지 않았고, 시중드는 하인도 없었다."], ["He went down quietly, opened the door for himself, glided out, and shut it softly after him.", "그는 조용히 내려가, 손수 문을 열고, 미끄러지듯 빠져나가, 뒤로 살며시 문을 닫았다."]], [["Her invincible repugnance to this man, and perhaps the stealthy act of watching anyone, which, even under such innocent circumstances, is in a manner guilty and oppressive, made Florence shake from head to foot.", "이 남자에 대한 떨칠 수 없는 혐오감과, 또 어쩌면 그런 무고한 상황에서조차 어딘가 죄스럽고 짓누르는 듯한, 누군가를 몰래 엿보는 그 행위 자체가, 플로렌스를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨게 만들었다."], ["Her blood seemed to run cold.", "그녀의 피가 차갑게 식는 것만 같았다."], ["As soon as she could—for at first she felt an insurmountable dread of moving—she went quickly to her own room and locked her door; but even then, shut in with her dog beside her, felt a chill sensation of horror, as if there were danger brooding somewhere near her.", "그녀는 될 수 있는 한 빨리—처음에는 움직인다는 것에 넘어설 수 없는 공포를 느꼈으므로—얼른 자기 방으로 가서 문을 잠갔다. 그러나 그러고 나서도, 개를 곁에 두고 방 안에 틀어박혀서도, 어딘가 가까이에서 위험이 도사리고 있기라도 한 듯, 오싹한 공포의 느낌을 떨치지 못했다."]], [["It invaded her dreams and disturbed the whole night.", "그것은 그녀의 꿈속까지 파고들어 밤새 그녀를 어지럽혔다."], ["Rising in the morning, unrefreshed, and with a heavy recollection of the domestic unhappiness of the preceding day, she sought Edith again in all the rooms, and did so, from time to time, all the morning.", "아침에 일어났을 때, 그녀는 개운하지 못했고, 전날의 가정의 불행에 대한 무거운 기억을 안고 있었으며, 다시 모든 방에서 이디스를 찾았고, 아침 내내 이따금 그렇게 했다."], ["But she remained in her own chamber, and Florence saw nothing of her.", "그러나 이디스는 자기 방에 머물러 있었고, 플로렌스는 그녀를 전혀 보지 못했다."], ["Learning, however, that the projected dinner at home was put off, Florence thought it likely that she would go out in the evening to fulfil the engagement she had spoken of; and resolved to try and meet her, then, upon the staircase.", "하지만 집에서 하려던 만찬이 미뤄졌다는 것을 알게 된 플로렌스는, 그녀가 이야기했던 약속을 지키러 저녁에 외출할 것 같다고 생각했고, 그때 계단에서 그녀를 만나 보려고 마음먹었다."]], [["When the evening had set in, she heard, from the room in which she sat on purpose, a footstep on the stairs that she thought to be Edith’s.", "저녁이 깃들었을 때, 그녀는 일부러 와 앉아 있던 방에서, 계단을 밟는 발소리를 들었는데, 그것이 이디스의 발소리라고 생각했다."], ["Hurrying out, and up towards her room, Florence met her immediately, coming down alone.", "황급히 나와 그녀의 방 쪽으로 올라가던 플로렌스는, 혼자 내려오는 그녀와 곧바로 마주쳤다."]], [["What was Florence’s affright and wonder when, at sight of her, with her tearful face, and outstretched arms, Edith recoiled and shrieked!", "눈물 어린 얼굴에 두 팔을 내민 자기를 보자, 이디스가 흠칫 물러서며 비명을 지르는 것을 보았을 때, 플로렌스의 그 놀라움과 의아함이란 어떠했겠는가!"]], [["“Don’t come near me!”", "\"내게 다가오지 마!\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["“Keep away!", "\"저리 비켜!"], ["Let me go by!”", "나를 지나가게 해 줘!\""]], [["“Mama!”", "\"엄마!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Don’t call me by that name!", "\"그 이름으로 나를 부르지 마!"], ["Don’t speak to me!", "내게 말 걸지 마!"], ["Don’t look at me!—Florence!”", "나를 보지 마!—플로렌스!\""], ["shrinking back, as Florence moved a step towards her, “don’t touch me!”", "플로렌스가 그녀 쪽으로 한 걸음 다가서자 뒷걸음치며, \"나를 만지지 마!\""]], [["As Florence stood transfixed before the haggard face and staring eyes, she noted, as in a dream, that Edith spread her hands over them, and shuddering through all her form, and crouching down against the wall, crawled by her like some lower animal, sprang up, and fled away.", "플로렌스가 그 초췌한 얼굴과 빤히 응시하는 눈 앞에 못 박힌 듯 서 있는 동안, 그녀는, 꿈결에서처럼, 이디스가 두 손을 자기 눈 위로 펼치고는, 온몸을 부르르 떨며, 벽에 바짝 붙어 웅크린 채, 무슨 하등한 짐승처럼 자기 곁을 기어 지나가, 벌떡 일어나, 달아나 버리는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["Florence dropped upon the stairs in a swoon; and was found there by Mrs Pipchin, she supposed.", "플로렌스는 정신을 잃고 계단 위에 쓰러졌다. 그리고 거기서, 핍친 부인에게 발견된 것이라고 그녀는 짐작했다."], ["She knew nothing more, until she found herself lying on her own bed, with Mrs Pipchin and some servants standing round her.", "그녀는 그 이상 아무것도 알지 못했고, 정신을 차려 보니 자기 침대에 누워 있었으며, 핍친 부인과 하인 몇이 그녀를 둘러싸고 서 있었다."]], [["“Where is Mama?” was her first question.", "\"엄마는 어디 계세요?\"가 그녀의 첫 질문이었다."]], [["“Gone out to dinner,”", "\"만찬에 나가셨소,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 말했다."]], [["“And Papa?”", "\"그럼 아빠는요?\""]], [["“Mr Dombey is in his own room, Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 씨는 자기 방에 계시오, 돔비 양,\""], ["said Mrs Pipchin, “and the best thing you can do, is to take off your things and go to bed this minute.”", "핍친 부인이 말했다, \"그리고 아가씨가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일은, 옷을 벗고 지금 당장 잠자리에 드는 거요.\""], ["This was the sagacious woman’s remedy for all complaints, particularly lowness of spirits, and inability to sleep; for which offences, many young victims in the days of the Brighton Castle had been committed to bed at ten o’clock in the morning.", "이것이 모든 병에 대한 그 약삭빠른 여인의 처방이었으니, 특히 기분의 침울함과 잠 못 이룸에 대해서 그러했다. 브라이턴 캐슬 시절에는 이런 죄목으로 수많은 어린 희생자들이 아침 열 시에 잠자리로 보내지곤 했다."]], [["Without promising obedience, but on the plea of desiring to be very quiet, Florence disengaged herself, as soon as she could, from the ministration of Mrs Pipchin and her attendants.", "복종을 약속하지는 않았으나, 아주 조용히 있고 싶다는 핑계를 대고, 플로렌스는 될 수 있는 한 빨리 핍친 부인과 그 시중꾼들의 보살핌에서 벗어났다."], ["Left alone, she thought of what had happened on the staircase, at first in doubt of its reality; then with tears; then with an indescribable and terrible alarm, like that she had felt the night before.", "혼자 남게 되자, 그녀는 계단에서 일어난 일을 떠올렸다. 처음에는 그것이 정말이었는지를 의심하면서, 그다음에는 눈물을 흘리면서, 그다음에는 전날 밤 느꼈던 것 같은, 형언할 수 없고 끔찍한 경악을 느끼면서."]], [["She determined not to go to bed until Edith returned, and if she could not speak to her, at least to be sure that she was safe at home.", "그녀는 이디스가 돌아올 때까지는 잠자리에 들지 않기로, 그리고 설령 그녀와 말을 나눌 수 없더라도, 적어도 그녀가 무사히 집에 돌아왔음을 확인하기로 결심했다."], ["What indistinct and shadowy dread moved Florence to this resolution, she did not know, and did not dare to think.", "어떤 막연하고 어렴풋한 두려움이 플로렌스를 이런 결심으로 이끌었는지, 그녀는 알지 못했고, 또 감히 생각해 보려 하지도 않았다."], ["She only knew that until Edith came back, there was no repose for her aching head or throbbing heart.", "그녀는 다만, 이디스가 돌아올 때까지는 자기의 욱신거리는 머리에도 두근거리는 가슴에도 안식이 없으리라는 것만 알 뿐이었다."]], [["The evening deepened into night; midnight came; no Edith.", "저녁은 깊어 밤이 되었고, 자정이 왔으나, 이디스는 오지 않았다."]], [["Florence could not read, or rest a moment.", "플로렌스는 책을 읽을 수도, 한순간도 쉴 수도 없었다."], ["She paced her own room, opened the door and paced the staircase-gallery outside, looked out of window on the night, listened to the wind blowing and the rain falling, sat down and watched the faces in the fire, got up and watched the moon flying like a storm-driven ship through the sea of clouds.", "그녀는 자기 방 안을 거닐다가, 문을 열고 바깥 계단 회랑을 거닐다가, 창밖으로 밤을 내다보다가, 바람이 부는 소리와 비가 내리는 소리에 귀 기울이다가, 앉아서 난롯불 속의 형상들을 바라보다가, 일어나서 구름의 바다를 가로질러 폭풍에 떠밀린 배처럼 내달리는 달을 바라보았다."]], [["All the house was gone to bed, except two servants who were waiting the return of their mistress, downstairs.", "온 집안이 잠자리에 들었고, 다만 아래층에서 안주인의 귀가를 기다리는 하인 둘만 깨어 있었다."]], [["One o’clock.", "한 시."], ["The carriages that rumbled in the distance, turned away, or stopped short, or went past; the silence gradually deepened, and was more and more rarely broken, save by a rush of wind or sweep of rain.", "멀리서 덜컹거리던 마차들이 방향을 돌리거나, 갑자기 멈추거나, 지나쳐 갔다. 정적은 차츰 깊어졌고, 한바탕 휘몰아치는 바람이나 한차례 쏟아지는 비를 빼고는 점점 더 드물게만 깨어졌다."], ["Two o’clock.", "두 시."], ["No Edith!", "이디스는 오지 않았다!"]], [["Florence, more agitated, paced her room; and paced the gallery outside; and looked out at the night, blurred and wavy with the raindrops on the glass, and the tears in her own eyes; and looked up at the hurry in the sky, so different from the repose below, and yet so tranquil and solitary.", "한층 더 마음을 졸이며, 플로렌스는 자기 방을 거닐었고, 바깥 회랑을 거닐었으며, 유리에 맺힌 빗방울과 자기 눈에 어린 눈물로 흐릿하고 일렁이는 밤을 내다보았고, 아래의 그 안식과는 그토록 다르면서도 또 그토록 고요하고 외로운, 하늘의 그 분주함을 올려다보았다."], ["Three o’clock!", "세 시!"], ["There was a terror in every ash that dropped out of the fire.", "난롯불에서 떨어지는 재 한 톨 한 톨에마다 어떤 공포가 서려 있었다."], ["No Edith yet.", "이디스는 아직도 오지 않았다."]], [["More and more agitated, Florence paced her room, and paced the gallery, and looked out at the moon with a new fancy of her likeness to a pale fugitive hurrying away and hiding her guilty face.", "점점 더 마음을 졸이며, 플로렌스는 자기 방을 거닐고, 회랑을 거닐었으며, 죄지은 얼굴을 감춘 채 황급히 달아나는 어떤 창백한 도망자를 닮았다는 새로운 공상을 품고서 달을 내다보았다. 네 시가 쳤다!"], ["Four struck!", "다섯 시!"], ["Five! No Edith yet.", "이디스는 아직도 오지 않았다."]], [["But now there was some cautious stir in the house; and Florence found that Mrs Pipchin had been awakened by one of those who sat up, had risen and had gone down to her father’s door.", "그러나 이제 집안에 어떤 조심스러운 움직임이 일었다. 그리고 플로렌스는, 깨어 지키고 있던 사람 가운데 하나에게 잠을 깬 핍친 부인이, 일어나 아래층 자기 아버지의 방문으로 내려갔다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Stealing lower down the stairs, and observing what passed, she saw her father come out in his morning gown, and start when he was told his wife had not come home.", "계단을 더 아래로 살금살금 내려가, 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 살피던 그녀는, 아버지가 잠옷 차림으로 나오는 것을, 그리고 아내가 집에 돌아오지 않았다는 말을 듣고 흠칫 놀라는 것을 보았다."], ["He dispatched a messenger to the stables to inquire whether the coachman was there; and while the man was gone, dressed himself very hurriedly.", "그는 마부가 거기 있는지 물어보라고 마구간으로 사람을 보냈고, 그 사람이 간 사이에 몹시 황급히 옷을 차려입었다."]], [["The man came back, in great haste, bringing the coachman with him, who said he had been at home and in bed, since ten o’clock.", "그 사람이, 마부를 데리고, 무척 서둘러 돌아왔다. 마부는 자기가 열 시 이후로 집에서 잠자리에 들어 있었다고 말했다."], ["He had driven his mistress to her old house in Brook Street, where she had been met by Mr Carker—", "그는 안주인을 그녀의 옛 집이 있는 브룩 가로 모셔다 드렸는데, 거기서 그녀를 카커 씨가 맞이했다고—"]], [["Florence stood upon the very spot where she had seen him coming down.", "플로렌스는 그가 내려오는 것을 보았던 바로 그 자리에 서 있었다."], ["Again she shivered with the nameless terror of that sight, and had hardly steadiness enough to hear and understand what followed.", "다시 그녀는 그 광경의 이름 없는 공포로 부르르 떨었고, 그다음에 이어지는 말을 듣고 알아들을 만한 침착함도 거의 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["—Who had told him, the man went on to say, that his mistress would not want the carriage to go home in; and had dismissed him.", "—그 카커 씨가, 마부가 말을 이어 가기를, 자기에게 안주인은 집으로 돌아가는 데 마차가 필요 없을 거라고 일러 주고, 자기를 돌려보냈다고 했다."]], [["She saw her father turn white in the face, and heard him ask in a quick, trembling voice, for Mrs Dombey’s maid.", "그녀는 아버지의 얼굴이 새하얗게 질리는 것을 보았고, 그가 빠르고 떨리는 목소리로 돔비 부인의 하녀를 찾는 것을 들었다."], ["The whole house was roused; for she was there, in a moment, very pale too, and speaking incoherently.", "온 집안이 깨어났다. 하녀가 곧 거기 와 있었으니, 그녀 역시 무척 창백했고, 횡설수설 말하고 있었다."]], [["She said she had dressed her mistress early—full two hours before she went out—and had been told, as she often was, that she would not be wanted at night.", "그녀는 자기가 안주인의 옷을 일찍—안주인이 외출하기 꼬박 두 시간 전에—입혀 드렸으며, 늘 그랬듯이, 밤에는 부르지 않을 거라는 말을 들었다고 했다."], ["She had just come from her mistress’s rooms, but—", "그녀는 방금 안주인의 방에서 나오는 길인데, 다만—"]], [["“But what!", "\"다만 뭔가!"], ["what was it?”", "그게 뭐였느냐?\""], ["Florence heard her father demand like a madman.", "플로렌스는 아버지가 미친 사람처럼 다그치는 것을 들었다."]], [["“But the inner dressing-room was locked and the key gone.”", "\"다만 안쪽 화장실이 잠겨 있고 열쇠가 없어졌어요.\""]], [["Her father seized a candle that was flaming on the ground—someone had put it down there, and forgotten it—and came running upstairs with such fury, that Florence, in her fear, had hardly time to fly before him.", "그녀의 아버지는 바닥에서 타고 있던 촛불 하나를—누군가가 거기 내려놓고 잊어버린 것이었다—움켜쥐고, 어찌나 사나운 기세로 계단을 뛰어 올라왔던지, 플로렌스는 겁에 질려 그의 앞에서 달아날 겨를도 거의 없었다."], ["She heard him striking in the door, as she ran on, with her hands widely spread, and her hair streaming, and her face like a distracted person’s, back to her own room.", "그녀가, 두 손을 활짝 벌리고, 머리를 휘날리며, 얼굴은 넋 나간 사람 같은 모습으로 자기 방으로 도로 달려가는 동안, 그가 그 문을 두들겨 부수는 소리를 들었다."]], [["When the door yielded, and he rushed in, what did he see there?", "문이 부서지고, 그가 안으로 뛰어들었을 때, 그는 거기서 무엇을 보았던가?"], ["No one knew.", "아무도 알지 못했다."], ["But thrown down in a costly mass upon the ground, was every ornament she had had, since she had been his wife; every dress she had worn; and everything she had possessed.", "그러나 값비싼 무더기를 이룬 채 바닥에 내던져져 있는 것은, 그녀가 그의 아내가 된 이후로 가졌던 온갖 장신구였고, 그녀가 입었던 온갖 드레스였으며, 그녀가 소유했던 온갖 것이었다."], ["This was the room in which he had seen, in yonder mirror, the proud face discard him.", "이 방은 그가, 저 거울 속에서, 그 도도한 얼굴이 자기를 내치는 것을 보았던 그 방이었다."], ["This was the room in which he had wondered, idly, how these things would look when he should see them next!", "이 방은 그가, 다음에 자기가 이것들을 보게 될 때면 어떻게 보일까를 하릴없이 궁금해했던 그 방이었다!"]], [["Heaping them back into the drawers, and locking them up in a rage of haste, he saw some papers on the table.", "그것들을 서랍 속으로 도로 쓸어 담아, 황급한 분노로 잠가 버리던 그는, 탁자 위에서 서류 몇 장을 보았다."], ["The deed of settlement he had executed on their marriage, and a letter.", "결혼할 때 그가 작성했던 재산 양도 증서와, 편지 한 통이었다."], ["He read that she was gone.", "그는 그녀가 떠났다는 것을 읽었다."], ["He read that he was dishonoured.", "그는 자기가 명예를 더럽혔다는 것을 읽었다."], ["He read that she had fled, upon her shameful wedding-day, with the man whom he had chosen for her humiliation; and he tore out of the room, and out of the house, with a frantic idea of finding her yet, at the place to which she had been taken, and beating all trace of beauty out of the triumphant face with his bare hand.", "그는 그녀가, 자기의 그 치욕스러운 결혼기념일에, 자기가 그녀를 욕보이려고 골라 둔 바로 그 남자와 함께 달아났다는 것을 읽었다. 그리고 그는, 아직도 그녀가 끌려간 그곳에서 그녀를 찾아내, 그 의기양양한 얼굴에서 아름다움의 흔적을 죄다 맨손으로 두들겨 없애 버리겠다는 광기 어린 생각에 사로잡혀, 방에서, 또 집에서 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["Florence, not knowing what she did, put on a shawl and bonnet, in a dream of running through the streets until she found Edith, and then clasping her in her arms, to save and bring her back.", "플로렌스는, 자기가 무엇을 하는지도 모른 채, 거리를 헤매며 이디스를 찾아내, 그러고는 그녀를 품에 끌어안아, 구해 내어 도로 데려오겠다는 꿈결 같은 생각으로, 숄을 두르고 보닛을 썼다."], ["But when she hurried out upon the staircase, and saw the frightened servants going up and down with lights, and whispering together, and falling away from her father as he passed down, she awoke to a sense of her own powerlessness; and hiding in one of the great rooms that had been made gorgeous for this, felt as if her heart would burst with grief.", "그러나 그녀가 황급히 계단으로 나와, 겁에 질린 하인들이 불을 들고 오르내리며 자기들끼리 속삭이고, 자기 아버지가 내려갈 때 슬금슬금 물러서는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀는 자기 자신의 무력함을 깨달았다. 그리고 이 일을 위해 화려하게 꾸며진 그 큰 방들 가운데 하나에 숨어, 슬픔으로 가슴이 터질 것만 같았다."]], [["Compassion for her father was the first distinct emotion that made head against the flood of sorrow which overwhelmed her.", "아버지에 대한 연민이, 그녀를 휩쓴 슬픔의 홍수에 맞서 처음으로 또렷하게 떠오른 감정이었다."], ["Her constant nature turned to him in his distress, as fervently and faithfully, as if, in his prosperity, he had been the embodiment of that idea which had gradually become so faint and dim.", "그녀의 한결같은 천성은, 그가 번창하던 시절에, 점점 그토록 희미하고 어렴풋해진 그 관념의 화신이기라도 했던 것처럼, 곤경에 처한 그를 향해 그토록 뜨겁고 그토록 변함없이 돌아섰다."], ["Although she did not know, otherwise than through the suggestions of a shapeless fear, the full extent of his calamity, he stood before her, wronged and deserted; and again her yearning love impelled her to his side.", "비록 그녀는, 어떤 형체 없는 두려움의 암시를 통하지 않고서는, 그가 겪은 재난의 전모를 알지 못했으나, 그는 부당한 일을 당하고 버림받은 채 그녀 앞에 서 있었다. 그리고 다시 한번 그녀의 애타는 사랑이 그녀를 그의 곁으로 떠밀었다."]], [["He was not long away; for Florence was yet weeping in the great room and nourishing these thoughts, when she heard him come back.", "그는 오래 나가 있지 않았다. 플로렌스가 아직 그 큰 방에서 울며 이런 생각들을 곱씹고 있을 때, 그녀는 그가 돌아오는 소리를 들었다."], ["He ordered the servants to set about their ordinary occupations, and went into his own apartment, where he trod so heavily that she could hear him walking up and down from end to end.", "그는 하인들에게 평소의 일을 보라고 명하고는, 자기 방으로 들어갔는데, 어찌나 무겁게 발을 디뎠던지 그가 이 끝에서 저 끝까지 오락가락 걷는 소리가 그녀에게 들렸다."]], [["Yielding at once to the impulse of her affection, timid at all other times, but bold in its truth to him in his adversity, and undaunted by past repulse, Florence, dressed as she was, hurried downstairs.", "다른 모든 때에는 겁이 많았으나, 역경에 처한 그를 향한 진심에 있어서는 대담했고, 지난날의 거절에도 굴하지 않은 채, 자기 애정의 충동에 곧바로 굴복하여, 플로렌스는 입은 차림 그대로 황급히 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["As she set her light foot in the hall, he came out of his room.", "그녀가 가벼운 발을 현관 홀에 들여놓았을 때, 그가 자기 방에서 나왔다."], ["She hastened towards him unchecked, with her arms stretched out, and crying “Oh dear, dear Papa!”", "그녀는, 마치 그의 목을 끌어안기라도 할 것처럼, 두 팔을 뻗고 \"오, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아빠!\""], ["as if she would have clasped him round the neck.", "하고 외치며, 거침없이 그에게로 서둘러 다가갔다."]], [["And so she would have done.", "그리고 그녀는 정말 그렇게 했으리라."], ["But in his frenzy, he lifted up his cruel arm, and struck her, crosswise, with that heaviness, that she tottered on the marble floor; and as he dealt the blow, he told her what Edith was, and bade her follow her, since they had always been in league.", "그러나 광기에 휩싸인 그는 자기의 그 잔인한 팔을 쳐들어, 어찌나 모질게 그녀를 가로질러 후려쳤던지, 그녀가 대리석 바닥 위에서 비틀거렸다. 그리고 그 일격을 가하면서, 그는 그녀에게 이디스가 어떤 여자인지를 말하고, 너희가 늘 한패였으니 그녀를 따라가라고 명했다."]], [["She did not sink down at his feet; she did not shut out the sight of him with her trembling hands; she did not weep; she did not utter one word of reproach.", "그녀는 그의 발치에 주저앉지 않았다. 그녀는 떨리는 두 손으로 그를 보는 시선을 가리지 않았다."], ["But she looked at him, and a cry of desolation issued from her heart.", "그녀는 울지 않았다. 그녀는 단 한마디 책망의 말도 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["For as she looked, she saw him murdering that fond idea to which she had held in spite of him.", "그러나 그녀는 그를 바라보았고, 그녀의 가슴에서 황량한 절규가 터져 나왔다. 바라보는 동안, 그녀는 그가, 자기가 그토록 그에 맞서 지켜 온 그 애틋한 관념을 죽이는 것을 보았기 때문이다."], ["She saw his cruelty, neglect, and hatred dominant above it, and stamping it down.", "그녀는 그의 잔인함과 무관심과 증오가 그것 위에 군림하며 그것을 짓밟아 버리는 것을 보았다."], ["She saw she had no father upon earth, and ran out, orphaned, from his house.", "그녀는 자기에게 이 땅 위에 아버지가 없다는 것을 보았고, 고아가 되어, 그의 집에서 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["Ran out of his house.", "그의 집에서 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["A moment, and her hand was on the lock, the cry was on her lips, his face was there, made paler by the yellow candles hastily put down and guttering away, and by the daylight coming in above the door.", "한순간, 그녀의 손이 자물쇠에 닿았고, 절규가 그녀의 입술에 맺혔으며, 황급히 내려놓아져 녹아 사라져 가는 누런 촛불들과, 문 위로 들어오는 햇빛에 한층 더 창백해진 그의 얼굴이 거기 있었다."], ["Another moment, and the close darkness of the shut-up house (forgotten to be opened, though it was long since day) yielded to the unexpected glare and freedom of the morning; and Florence, with her head bent down to hide her agony of tears, was in the streets.", "또 한순간, 닫혀 있던 그 집의 짙은 어둠이(날이 밝은 지 오래였건만 누구도 열어젖힐 생각을 하지 못한 그 어둠이) 뜻밖의 환한 빛과 아침의 트인 자유에 자리를 내주었다. 그리고 플로렌스는, 눈물의 고통을 감추려 고개를 푹 숙인 채, 거리에 나와 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVIII. The Flight of Florence", "# 제43장"]], [["In the wildness of her sorrow, shame, and terror, the forlorn girl hurried through the sunshine of a bright morning, as if it were the darkness of a winter night.", "비탄과 수치와 공포의 광란 속에서, 의지할 데 없는 그 소녀는 밝은 아침의 햇살 속을 마치 그것이 겨울밤의 어둠이기라도 한 듯 허둥지둥 헤쳐 나갔다."], ["Wringing her hands and weeping bitterly, insensible to everything but the deep wound in her breast, stunned by the loss of all she loved, left like the sole survivor on a lonely shore from the wreck of a great vessel, she fled without a thought, without a hope, without a purpose, but to fly somewhere anywhere.", "두 손을 쥐어짜고 비통하게 울면서, 가슴속 깊은 상처 말고는 아무것에도 감각이 없이, 사랑하던 모든 것을 잃은 충격으로 멍해진 채, 거대한 배가 난파한 외딴 해안에 홀로 살아남은 단 한 사람처럼, 그녀는 아무 생각도, 아무 희망도, 아무 목적도 없이 그저 어디든 도망쳐야 한다는 일념으로 달아났다."]], [["The cheerful vista of the long street, burnished by the morning light, the sight of the blue sky and airy clouds, the vigorous freshness of the day, so flushed and rosy in its conquest of the night, awakened no responsive feelings in her so hurt bosom.", "아침 빛에 반짝이는 긴 거리의 명랑한 풍경도, 푸른 하늘과 가벼운 구름의 모습도, 밤을 정복하여 그토록 발그레하고 장밋빛으로 물든 하루의 힘찬 신선함도, 그토록 상처 입은 그녀의 가슴에는 아무런 호응의 감정도 일깨우지 못했다."], ["Somewhere, anywhere, to hide her head!", "어디든, 어디라도, 머리를 숨길 곳!"], ["somewhere, anywhere, for refuge, never more to look upon the place from which she fled!", "어디든, 어디라도, 피신할 곳, 다시는 자기가 도망쳐 나온 그곳을 바라보지 않을 곳!"]], [["But there were people going to and fro; there were opening shops, and servants at the doors of houses; there was the rising clash and roar of the day’s struggle.", "그러나 사람들은 이리저리 오갔고, 가게들은 문을 열고 있었으며, 하인들은 집집의 문간에 나와 있었고, 하루의 분투가 점점 부딪치고 으르렁대며 일어나고 있었다."], ["Florence saw surprise and curiosity in the faces flitting past her; saw long shadows coming back upon the pavement; and heard voices that were strange to her asking her where she went, and what the matter was; and though these frightened her the more at first, and made her hurry on the faster, they did her the good service of recalling her in some degree to herself, and reminding her of the necessity of greater composure.", "플로렌스는 자기 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 얼굴들에서 놀라움과 호기심을 보았고, 포장도로 위로 되돌아오는 긴 그림자들을 보았으며, 낯선 목소리들이 어디로 가느냐고, 무슨 일이냐고 묻는 것을 들었다. 처음에는 그 일들이 그녀를 더욱 겁먹게 하여 발걸음을 더 빨리하게 만들었지만, 그것들은 그녀를 어느 정도 제정신으로 돌아오게 하고 좀 더 침착해야 할 필요를 일깨워 주는 좋은 역할을 해 주었다."]], [["Where to go?", "어디로 갈 것인가?"], ["Still somewhere, anywhere!", "여전히 어디든, 어디라도!"], ["still going on; but where!", "여전히 앞으로 나아가면서, 그러나 어디로!"], ["She thought of the only other time she had been lost in the wild wilderness of London—though not lost as now—and went that way.", "그녀는 자기가 런던이라는 거친 황야 속에서 길을 잃었던 단 한 번의 다른 때를—지금처럼 길을 잃은 것은 아니었지만—떠올렸고, 그 길로 갔다."], ["To the home of Walter’s Uncle.", "월터의 외삼촌 댁으로."]], [["Checking her sobs, and drying her swollen eyes, and endeavouring to calm the agitation of her manner, so as to avoid attracting notice, Florence, resolving to keep to the more quiet streets as long as she could, was going on more quietly herself, when a familiar little shadow darted past upon the sunny pavement, stopped short, wheeled about, came close to her, made off again, bounded round and round her, and Diogenes, panting for breath, and yet making the street ring with his glad bark, was at her feet.", "흐느낌을 억누르고, 퉁퉁 부은 눈을 닦으며, 남의 눈에 띄지 않으려고 들뜬 거동을 가라앉히려 애쓰면서, 플로렌스는 할 수 있는 한 오래 좀 더 조용한 거리로만 다니기로 마음먹고 자기도 좀 더 차분하게 걷고 있었는데, 그때 햇살 비치는 포장도로 위로 낯익은 작은 그림자가 휙 지나가더니, 우뚝 멈춰 서고, 빙 돌아서서, 그녀에게 바짝 다가왔다가, 다시 달아나고, 그녀의 둘레를 빙글빙글 돌았다. 그러더니 디오게네스가, 숨을 헐떡이면서도 기쁜 짖음으로 거리를 쩌렁쩌렁 울리며, 그녀의 발치에 와 있었다."]], [["“Oh, Di!", "\"오, 디!"], ["oh, dear, true, faithful Di, how did you come here?", "오, 사랑스럽고, 참되고, 충직한 디, 어떻게 여기까지 왔니?"], ["How could I ever leave you, Di, who would never leave me?”", "내가 어떻게 너를 두고 떠날 수 있었을까, 디, 너는 결코 나를 떠나지 않을 텐데?\""]], [["Florence bent down on the pavement, and laid his rough, old, loving, foolish head against her breast, and they got up together, and went on together; Di more off the ground than on it, endeavouring to kiss his mistress flying, tumbling over and getting up again without the least concern, dashing at big dogs in a jocose defiance of his species, terrifying with touches of his nose young housemaids who were cleaning doorsteps, and continually stopping, in the midst of a thousand extravagances, to look back at Florence, and bark until all the dogs within hearing answered, and all the dogs who could come out, came out to stare at him.", "플로렌스는 포장도로 위로 몸을 굽혀, 그 거칠고 늙고 다정하고 어수룩한 머리를 자기 가슴에 끌어안았고, 둘은 함께 일어나 함께 걸어갔다. 디는 땅에 붙어 있다기보다 땅에서 떠 있는 시간이 더 많았는데, 달려가는 주인에게 입을 맞추려 애쓰다가, 데굴데굴 굴러 넘어졌다가도 조금도 개의치 않고 다시 일어났고, 자기 종족에 대한 짓궂은 도전으로 큰 개들에게 달려들었으며, 문간 계단을 닦고 있는 어린 하녀들을 코끝으로 슬쩍 건드려 질겁하게 했고, 수천 가지 야단법석을 떠는 와중에도 끊임없이 멈춰 서서 플로렌스를 돌아보며, 들리는 거리 안의 모든 개들이 응답할 때까지, 그리고 밖으로 나올 수 있는 모든 개들이 그를 빤히 쳐다보러 나올 때까지 짖어 댔다."]], [["With this last adherent, Florence hurried away in the advancing morning, and the strengthening sunshine, to the City.", "이 마지막 추종자와 함께, 플로렌스는 밝아 오는 아침과 점점 강해지는 햇살 속을 서둘러 도심을 향해 갔다."], ["The roar soon grew more loud, the passengers more numerous, the shops more busy, until she was carried onward in a stream of life setting that way, and flowing, indifferently, past marts and mansions, prisons, churches, market-places, wealth, poverty, good, and evil, like the broad river side by side with it, awakened from its dreams of rushes, willows, and green moss, and rolling on, turbid and troubled, among the works and cares of men, to the deep sea.", "으르렁대는 소리는 곧 더 커졌고, 행인들은 더 많아졌으며, 가게들은 더 분주해졌고, 마침내 그녀는 그쪽으로 흘러가는 생명의 물결에 떠밀려 가게 되었다. 그 물결은 시장과 저택, 감옥과 교회, 장터, 부와 빈곤, 선과 악을 무심히 흘러 지나갔으니, 마치 그 옆에 나란히 흐르는 넓은 강이 갈대와 버드나무와 푸른 이끼의 꿈에서 깨어나, 흐리고 어지러운 모습으로, 사람들의 일과 근심 사이를 굽이쳐 깊은 바다로 흘러가는 것과도 같았다."]], [["At length the quarters of the little Midshipman arose in view.", "마침내 작은 항해사가 있는 곳이 시야에 들어왔다."], ["Nearer yet, and the little Midshipman himself was seen upon his post, intent as ever on his observations.", "좀 더 가까이 가자, 작은 항해사 자신이 자기 자리에 서서, 여느 때처럼 관측에 골몰해 있는 것이 보였다."], ["Nearer yet, and the door stood open, inviting her to enter.", "좀 더 가까이 가자, 문이 열려 있어 그녀에게 들어오라고 손짓하고 있었다."], ["Florence, who had again quickened her pace, as she approached the end of her journey, ran across the road (closely followed by Diogenes, whom the bustle had somewhat confused), ran in, and sank upon the threshold of the well-remembered little parlour.", "여정의 끝에 다가가면서 다시 걸음을 빨리한 플로렌스는, 길을 가로질러 달려가(법석에 다소 어리둥절해진 디오게네스가 바짝 뒤따랐다), 안으로 뛰어들어, 잘 기억하고 있는 그 작은 응접실의 문지방에 쓰러져 버렸다."]], [["The Captain, in his glazed hat, was standing over the fire, making his morning’s cocoa, with that elegant trifle, his watch, upon the chimney-piece, for easy reference during the progress of the cookery.", "니스 칠한 모자를 쓴 선장은 난롯불 위에 몸을 굽혀 아침 코코아를 끓이고 있었는데, 요리가 진행되는 동안 손쉽게 참고하려고, 그 우아한 장신구인 시계를 벽난로 선반 위에 올려놓은 참이었다."], ["Hearing a footstep and the rustle of a dress, the Captain turned with a palpitating remembrance of the dreadful Mrs MacStinger, at the instant when Florence made a motion with her hand towards him, reeled, and fell upon the floor.", "발소리와 옷자락 스치는 소리를 듣자, 선장은 그 무시무시한 맥스틴저 부인을 떠올리며 가슴이 두근거려 몸을 돌렸는데, 바로 그 순간 플로렌스가 그를 향해 손짓을 하다가, 비틀거리며 바닥에 쓰러졌다."]], [["The Captain, pale as Florence, pale in the very knobs upon his face, raised her like a baby, and laid her on the same old sofa upon which she had slumbered long ago.", "플로렌스만큼이나 창백한, 얼굴의 혹마디까지 새하얗게 질린 선장은, 그녀를 갓난아기처럼 들어 올려, 오래전 그녀가 잠들었던 바로 그 낡은 소파에 눕혔다."]], [["“It’s Heart’s Delight!”", "\"우리 '마음의 기쁨'이로구나!\""], ["said the Captain, looking intently in her face.", "선장이 그녀의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“It’s the sweet creetur grow’d a woman!”", "\"그 귀여운 아가씨가 어엿한 여인이 다 되었구먼!\""]], [["Captain Cuttle was so respectful of her, and had such a reverence for her, in this new character, that he would not have held her in his arms, while she was unconscious, for a thousand pounds.", "커틀 선장은 이 새로운 모습의 그녀를 어찌나 깍듯이 존중하고, 어찌나 우러러보았던지, 그녀가 의식을 잃은 동안 천 파운드를 준대도 그녀를 자기 팔에 안고 있으려 하지 않았다."]], [["“My Heart’s Delight!”", "\"우리 '마음의 기쁨'!\""], ["said the Captain, withdrawing to a little distance, with the greatest alarm and sympathy depicted on his countenance.", "선장이 얼굴에 더없는 놀라움과 동정을 가득 담은 채, 조금 뒤로 물러서며 말했다."], ["“If you can hail Ned Cuttle with a finger, do it!”", "\"네드 커틀을 손가락 하나로라도 부를 수 있거든, 그렇게 해 주오!\""]], [["But Florence did not stir.", "그러나 플로렌스는 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."]], [["“My Heart’s Delight!”", "\"우리 '마음의 기쁨'!\""], ["said the trembling Captain.", "선장이 떨면서 말했다."], ["“For the sake of Wal”r drownded in the briny deep, turn to, and histe up something or another, if able!”", "\"짠 바다 깊은 곳에 빠져 죽은 월터를 봐서라도, 정신을 차리고, 할 수 있거든 뭐든 좀 끌어올려 주오!\""]], [["Finding her insensible to this impressive adjuration also, Captain Cuttle snatched from his breakfast-table a basin of cold water, and sprinkled some upon her face.", "이 감동적인 호소에도 그녀가 아무 반응이 없는 것을 보고, 커틀 선장은 아침 식탁에서 찬물이 든 대야를 집어 들어, 그녀의 얼굴에 조금 뿌렸다."], ["Yielding to the urgency of the case, the Captain then, using his immense hand with extraordinary gentleness, relieved her of her bonnet, moistened her lips and forehead, put back her hair, covered her feet with his own coat which he pulled off for the purpose, patted her hand—so small in his, that he was struck with wonder when he touched it—and seeing that her eyelids quivered, and that her lips began to move, continued these restorative applications with a better heart.", "사태의 급박함에 못 이겨, 선장은 그 거대한 손을 보기 드물게 부드럽게 놀려, 그녀의 보닛을 벗겨 주고, 입술과 이마를 적셔 주고, 머리카락을 뒤로 넘겨 주고, 그러려고 벗어 든 자기 외투로 그녀의 발을 덮어 주고, 그녀의 손을—자기 손안에 들어가니 어찌나 작은지 만져 보고 깜짝 놀랄 정도였다—토닥여 주었으며, 그녀의 눈꺼풀이 떨리고 입술이 움직이기 시작하는 것을 보고, 한결 마음을 놓고 이 회복 조치들을 계속했다."]], [["“Cheerily,”", "\"기운 차려요,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Cheerily!", "\"기운을 차려!"], ["Stand by, my pretty one, stand by!", "버텨요, 우리 예쁜이, 버텨!"], ["There!", "자!"], ["You’re better now.", "이제 좀 나아졌소."], ["Steady’s the word, and steady it is.", "침착, 그게 핵심이고, 잘 침착하고 있소."], ["Keep her so!", "그대로 유지해요!"], ["Drink a little drop o’ this here,”", "이거 한 모금만 마셔 봐요,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“There you are!", "\"옳지!"], ["What cheer now, my pretty, what cheer now?”", "이제 좀 어떻소, 우리 예쁜이, 이제 좀 어떻소?\""]], [["At this stage of her recovery, Captain Cuttle, with an imperfect association of a Watch with a Physician’s treatment of a patient, took his own down from the mantel-shelf, and holding it out on his hook, and taking Florence’s hand in his, looked steadily from one to the other, as expecting the dial to do something.", "그녀의 회복이 이 단계에 이르자, 커틀 선장은 시계를 의사가 환자를 진료하는 일과 어렴풋이 연결지어 떠올리며, 자기 시계를 벽난로 선반에서 내려, 갈고리에 매달아 들고, 플로렌스의 손을 자기 손에 잡은 채, 마치 그 시계판이 무언가 해 주기를 기대하듯 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“What cheer, my pretty?”", "\"좀 어떻소, 우리 예쁜이?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“What cheer now?", "\"이제 좀 어떻소?"], ["You’ve done her some good, my lad, I believe,”", "자네가 이 아가씨한테 효험을 좀 보인 것 같군그래,\""], ["said the Captain, under his breath, and throwing an approving glance upon his watch.", "선장이 숨죽인 소리로, 자기 시계에 흐뭇한 눈길을 던지며 말했다."], ["“Put you back half-an-hour every morning, and about another quarter towards the arternoon, and you’re a watch as can be ekalled by few and excelled by none.", "\"아침마다 자넬 반 시간씩 뒤로 돌려놓고, 오후 무렵에 또 한 십오 분쯤 돌려놓으면, 자네는 견줄 자 거의 없고 능가할 자 하나도 없는 시계가 되지."], ["What cheer, my lady lass!”", "좀 어떻소, 우리 아가씨!\""]], [["“Captain Cuttle!", "\"커틀 선장님!"], ["Is it you?”", "선장님이세요?\""], ["exclaimed Florence, raising herself a little.", "플로렌스가 몸을 조금 일으키며 소리쳤다."]], [["“Yes, yes, my lady lass,”", "\"그렇소, 그렇소, 우리 아가씨,\""], ["said the Captain, hastily deciding in his own mind upon the superior elegance of that form of address, as the most courtly he could think of.", "선장이 말했다. 자기가 생각해 낼 수 있는 가장 정중한 호칭으로서 그 표현이 한결 우아하다고 속으로 얼른 단정 지으며."]], [["“Is Walter’s Uncle here?”", "\"월터의 외삼촌이 여기 계세요?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“Here, pretty?”", "\"여기, 예쁜이?\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 응수했다."], ["“He ain’t been here this many a long day.", "\"그분은 여기 안 계신 지 한참 됐소."], ["He ain’t been heerd on, since he sheered off arter poor Wal”r.", "가엾은 월터를 찾아 떠난 뒤로는 소식도 끊겼지. 하지만,\""], ["But,” said the Captain, as a quotation, “Though lost to sight, to memory dear, and England, Home, and Beauty!”", "선장은 인용구를 읊듯 말했다, \"비록 눈앞에서는 사라졌어도, 기억 속에 소중하니, 잉글랜드여, 고향이여, 그리고 아름다움이여!\""]], [["“Do you live here?”", "\"여기서 사세요?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, my lady lass,”", "\"그렇소, 우리 아가씨,\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, Captain Cuttle!”", "\"오, 커틀 선장님!\""], ["cried Florence, putting her hands together, and speaking wildly.", "플로렌스가 두 손을 맞잡고, 정신없이 외쳤다. \"저를 구해 주세요!"], ["“Save me! keep me here!", "저를 여기 있게 해 주세요!"], ["Let no one know where I am!", "아무도 제가 어디 있는지 모르게 해 주세요!"], ["I’ll tell you what has happened by-and-by, when I can.", "무슨 일이 있었는지는 차차, 제가 할 수 있게 되면 말씀드릴게요."], ["I have no one in the world to go to.", "저는 이 세상에 찾아갈 사람이 아무도 없어요."], ["Do not send me away!”", "저를 내쫓지 마세요!\""]], [["“Send you away, my lady lass!”", "\"널 내쫓다니, 우리 아가씨!\""], ["exclaimed the Captain.", "선장이 소리쳤다."], ["“You, my Heart’s Delight! Stay a bit!", "\"너를, 우리 '마음의 기쁨'을!"], ["We’ll put up this here deadlight, and take a double turn on the key!”", "잠깐만 기다려요! 이 덧문을 닫고, 열쇠를 두 번 돌려 잠가 버립시다!\""]], [["With these words, the Captain, using his one hand and his hook with the greatest dexterity, got out the shutter of the door, put it up, made it all fast, and locked the door itself.", "이 말과 함께, 선장은 한 손과 갈고리를 더없이 능숙하게 놀려, 문의 덧문을 꺼내 와 끼우고, 단단히 고정하고, 문 자체도 잠갔다."]], [["When he came back to the side of Florence, she took his hand, and kissed it.", "그가 플로렌스 곁으로 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 그의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추었다."], ["The helplessness of the action, the appeal it made to him, the confidence it expressed, the unspeakable sorrow in her face, the pain of mind she had too plainly suffered, and was suffering then, his knowledge of her past history, her present lonely, worn, and unprotected appearance, all so rushed upon the good Captain together, that he fairly overflowed with compassion and gentleness.", "그 행동의 가련함, 그것이 그에게 보내는 호소, 그것이 드러내는 신뢰, 그녀 얼굴에 어린 말할 수 없는 슬픔, 그녀가 너무도 분명히 겪어 왔고 그때도 겪고 있는 마음의 고통, 그녀의 지난 내력에 대한 그의 앎, 지금의 외롭고 지치고 보호받지 못하는 그녀의 모습, 이 모든 것이 한꺼번에 마음씨 고운 선장에게 밀려들어, 그는 그야말로 연민과 다정함으로 넘쳐흘렀다."]], [["“My lady lass,”", "\"우리 아가씨,\""], ["said the Captain, polishing the bridge of his nose with his arm until it shone like burnished copper, “don’t you say a word to Ed’ard Cuttle, until such times as you finds yourself a riding smooth and easy; which won’t be today, nor yet to-morrow.", "선장이 콧날을 팔로 문질러 닦아 광낸 구리처럼 반짝이게 하며 말했다, \"에드워드 커틀한테는 한마디도 하지 말아요, 네 처지가 잔잔하고 편안하게 흘러가게 됐다고 느낄 그런 때가 올 때까지는. 오늘도 아니고, 내일도 아닐 거요."], ["And as to giving of you up, or reporting where you are, yes verily, and by God’s help, so I won’t, Church catechism, make a note on!”", "그리고 널 넘겨준다거나, 네가 어디 있는지 일러바친다거나 하는 일은, 그래, 진실로, 하느님의 도우심을 빌어, 결단코 하지 않겠소, 교회 교리문답을 걸고, 명심해 두시오!\""]], [["This the Captain said, reference and all, in one breath, and with much solemnity; taking off his hat at “yes verily,”", "이 말을 선장은, 인용까지 죄다, 한 숨에, 매우 엄숙하게 했다. \"그래, 진실로\""], ["and putting it on again, when he had quite concluded.", "하는 대목에서 모자를 벗었다가, 말을 다 끝맺고 나서야 다시 썼다."]], [["Florence could do but one thing more to thank him, and to show him how she trusted in him; and she did it.", "플로렌스가 그에게 고마움을 표하고, 자기가 얼마나 그를 믿는지 보여 주기 위해 할 수 있는 일은 단 한 가지뿐이었고, 그녀는 그것을 했다."], ["Clinging to this rough creature as the last asylum of her bleeding heart, she laid her head upon his honest shoulder, and clasped him round his neck, and would have kneeled down to bless him, but that he divined her purpose, and held her up like a true man.", "피 흘리는 자기 마음의 마지막 피난처인 이 거친 사내에게 매달려, 그녀는 그의 정직한 어깨에 머리를 기대고, 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안았으며, 무릎을 꿇고 그를 축복하려 했으나, 그가 그 뜻을 알아채고 참된 사내답게 그녀를 부축해 일으켰다."]], [["“Steady!”", "\"침착!\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Steady!", "\"침착!"], ["You’re too weak to stand, you see, my pretty, and must lie down here again.", "보다시피 넌 서 있기엔 너무 기운이 없으니, 우리 예쁜이, 여기 다시 누워야 해요."], ["There, there!”", "자, 자!\""], ["To see the Captain lift her on the sofa, and cover her with his coat, would have been worth a hundred state sights. “And now,”", "선장이 그녀를 소파에 들어 올려 눕히고 자기 외투로 덮어 주는 모습은, 화려한 구경거리 백 개를 합친 것만큼 볼 만한 광경이었다."], ["said the Captain, “you must take some breakfast, lady lass, and the dog shall have some too.", "\"그리고 이제,\" 선장이 말했다, \"아침을 좀 들어야 해요, 아가씨, 그리고 개한테도 좀 줘야지."], ["And arter that you shall go aloft to old Sol Gills’s room, and fall asleep there, like a angel.”", "그러고 나서는 솔 길스 영감의 방으로 올라가, 천사처럼 거기서 잠이 들어요.\""]], [["Captain Cuttle patted Diogenes when he made allusion to him, and Diogenes met that overture graciously, half-way.", "커틀 선장은 디오게네스를 언급하면서 그를 토닥여 주었고, 디오게네스도 그 호의를 정중히 절반쯤 받아들였다."], ["During the administration of the restoratives he had clearly been in two minds whether to fly at the Captain or to offer him his friendship; and he had expressed that conflict of feeling by alternate waggings of his tail, and displays of his teeth, with now and then a growl or so.", "회복 조치가 진행되는 동안 그는 선장에게 달려들지, 우정을 내밀지를 두고 분명 마음이 두 갈래였던 터라, 꼬리를 흔들었다가 이빨을 드러냈다가, 이따금 으르렁거리기까지 하며 그 갈등을 표현했다."], ["But by this time, his doubts were all removed.", "그러나 이제는 그 의심이 모두 사라졌다."], ["It was plain that he considered the Captain one of the most amiable of men, and a man whom it was an honour to a dog to know.", "그가 선장을 가장 상냥한 사람 중 하나로, 개로서 알고 지내는 것이 영광인 사람으로 여기게 된 것이 분명했다."]], [["In evidence of these convictions, Diogenes attended on the Captain while he made some tea and toast, and showed a lively interest in his housekeeping.", "이러한 확신의 증거로, 디오게네스는 선장이 차와 토스트를 만드는 동안 시중을 들며, 그의 살림살이에 활기찬 관심을 보였다."], ["But it was in vain for the kind Captain to make such preparations for Florence, who sorely tried to do some honour to them, but could touch nothing, and could only weep and weep again.", "그러나 마음씨 고운 선장이 플로렌스를 위해 그렇게 차린 음식은 헛수고였으니, 그녀는 그것들에 대접을 표하려 무던히 애썼지만 아무것도 입에 댈 수 없었고, 그저 울고 또 울 뿐이었다."]], [["“Well, well!”", "\"자, 자!\""], ["said the compassionate Captain, “arter turning in, my Heart’s Delight, you’ll get more way upon you.", "동정심 많은 선장이 말했다, \"한숨 자고 나면, 우리 '마음의 기쁨'아, 좀 더 기운이 날 거요."], ["Now, I’ll serve out your allowance, my lad.”", "자, 이제 자네 몫을 나눠 주마, 이 녀석.\""], ["To Diogenes.", "디오게네스에게 한 말이었다."], ["“And you shall keep guard on your mistress aloft.”", "\"그리고 자네는 위층에서 네 주인을 지켜야 해.\""]], [["Diogenes, however, although he had been eyeing his intended breakfast with a watering mouth and glistening eyes, instead of falling to, ravenously, when it was put before him, pricked up his ears, darted to the shop-door, and barked there furiously: burrowing with his head at the bottom, as if he were bent on mining his way out.", "그런데 디오게네스는, 군침을 흘리고 눈을 번득이며 차려진 자기 아침밥을 노려보고 있었으면서도, 그것이 앞에 놓이자 게걸스럽게 달려들기는커녕, 귀를 쫑긋 세우더니, 가게 문으로 쏜살같이 달려가 거기서 사납게 짖어 댔다. 마치 굴을 파서 밖으로 빠져나가기로 작정한 듯, 문 밑바닥을 머리로 파헤치면서."]], [["“Can there be anybody there!”", "\"거기 누가 있는 건 아닐까요!\""], ["asked Florence, in alarm.", "플로렌스가 놀라 물었다."]], [["“No, my lady lass,”", "\"아니오, 우리 아가씨,\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."], ["“Who’d stay there, without making any noise!", "\"아무 소리도 안 내고 거기 있을 사람이 누가 있겠소!"], ["Keep up a good heart, pretty.", "마음 단단히 먹어요, 예쁜이."], ["It’s only people going by.”", "그냥 지나가는 사람들일 뿐이오.\""]], [["But for all that, Diogenes barked and barked, and burrowed and burrowed, with pertinacious fury; and whenever he stopped to listen, appeared to receive some new conviction into his mind, for he set to, barking and burrowing again, a dozen times.", "그래도 디오게네스는 짖고 또 짖었고, 파고 또 팠으니, 끈질긴 광기로 그러했다. 그리고 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울일 때마다, 무언가 새로운 확신을 마음속에 받아들이는 듯, 다시 짖고 파헤치기를 열두 번도 더 했다."], ["Even when he was persuaded to return to his breakfast, he came jogging back to it, with a very doubtful air; and was off again, in another paroxysm, before touching a morsel.", "가까스로 달래져 아침밥으로 돌아올 때조차, 그는 매우 미심쩍은 기색으로 어슬렁어슬렁 돌아왔고, 한 입도 대기 전에 또다시 발작을 일으키며 달려가 버렸다."]], [["“If there should be someone listening and watching,”", "\"누군가 엿듣고 지켜보는 사람이 있을지도 몰라요,\""], ["whispered Florence.", "플로렌스가 속삭였다."], ["“Someone who saw me come—who followed me, perhaps.”", "\"제가 들어오는 걸 본 사람이—어쩌면 저를 따라온 사람이.\""]], [["“It ain’t the young woman, lady lass, is it?”", "\"그 아가씨는 아니겠지요, 우리 아가씨, 그렇소?\""], ["said the Captain, taken with a bright idea.", "선장이 번뜩이는 생각이 떠올라 말했다."]], [["“Susan?”", "\"수전이요?\""], ["said Florence, shaking her head. “Ah no!", "플로렌스가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["Susan has been gone from me a long time.”", "\"아, 아니에요! 수전은 제 곁을 떠난 지 오래됐어요.\""]], [["“Not deserted, I hope?”", "\"버림받은 건 아니길 바라오?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Don’t say that that there young woman’s run, my pretty!”", "\"그 아가씨가 달아났다는 말은 말아 주오, 우리 예쁜이!\""]], [["“Oh, no, no!”", "\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요!\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."], ["“She is one of the truest hearts in the world!”", "\"그 애는 세상에서 가장 참된 마음을 가진 사람 중 하나예요!\""]], [["The Captain was greatly relieved by this reply, and expressed his satisfaction by taking off his hard glazed hat, and dabbing his head all over with his handkerchief, rolled up like a ball, observing several times, with infinite complacency, and with a beaming countenance, that he know’d it.", "선장은 이 대답에 크게 안도하여, 만족스러운 마음을 표하느라 그 딱딱한 니스 칠 모자를 벗고, 공처럼 둘둘 만 손수건으로 머리 전체를 톡톡 두드렸으며, 무한히 흐뭇한 표정으로, 환한 얼굴로, 자기도 그럴 줄 알았노라고 여러 번 되뇌었다."]], [["“So you’re quiet now, are you, brother?”", "\"자, 이제 좀 잠잠해졌느냐, 형제?\""], ["said the Captain to Diogenes.", "선장이 디오게네스에게 말했다."], ["“There warn’t nobody there, my lady lass, bless you!”", "\"아무도 거기 없었소, 우리 아가씨, 안심해요!\""]], [["Diogenes was not so sure of that.", "디오게네스는 그것을 그리 확신하지 못했다."], ["The door still had an attraction for him at intervals; and he went snuffing about it, and growling to himself, unable to forget the subject.", "그 문은 여전히 이따금씩 그의 관심을 끌었고, 그는 그 문제를 잊지 못한 채, 문 둘레를 킁킁대며 혼자 으르렁거렸다."], ["This incident, coupled with the Captain’s observation of Florence’s fatigue and faintness, decided him to prepare Sol Gills’s chamber as a place of retirement for her immediately.", "이 사건과 더불어, 플로렌스의 피로와 쇠약함을 살핀 선장은, 솔 길스의 방을 그녀의 휴식처로 당장 마련해 두기로 결심했다."], ["He therefore hastily betook himself to the top of the house, and made the best arrangement of it that his imagination and his means suggested.", "그리하여 그는 부랴부랴 집 꼭대기로 올라가, 자기 상상력과 형편이 허락하는 한 최선을 다해 그 방을 꾸몄다."]], [["It was very clean already; and the Captain, being an orderly man, and accustomed to make things ship-shape, converted the bed into a couch, by covering it all over with a clean white drapery.", "그 방은 이미 매우 깨끗했다. 게다가 선장은 단정한 사람이고 매사를 배 안처럼 말끔히 갖추는 데 익숙한 터라, 침대를 깨끗한 흰 천으로 죄다 덮어 긴 의자로 탈바꿈시켰다."], ["By a similar contrivance, the Captain converted the little dressing-table into a species of altar, on which he set forth two silver teaspoons, a flower-pot, a telescope, his celebrated watch, a pocket-comb, and a song-book, as a small collection of rarities, that made a choice appearance.", "비슷한 궁리로, 선장은 작은 화장대를 일종의 제단으로 만들어, 그 위에 은 찻숟가락 두 개, 화분 하나, 망원경 하나, 그 유명한 자기 시계, 휴대용 빗 하나, 노래책 한 권을 진귀한 물건들의 작은 수집품인 양 늘어놓았는데, 그 모양새가 그럴듯해 보였다."], ["Having darkened the window, and straightened the pieces of carpet on the floor, the Captain surveyed these preparations with great delight, and descended to the little parlour again, to bring Florence to her bower.", "창문을 어둡게 가리고, 바닥에 깔린 양탄자 조각들을 반듯하게 편 뒤, 선장은 이 준비물들을 크게 흡족해하며 둘러보고는, 플로렌스를 그녀의 안식처로 데려오려고 다시 작은 응접실로 내려갔다."]], [["Nothing would induce the Captain to believe that it was possible for Florence to walk upstairs.", "선장은 무슨 일이 있어도 플로렌스가 계단을 걸어 올라갈 수 있으리라고는 믿으려 하지 않았다."], ["If he could have got the idea into his head, he would have considered it an outrageous breach of hospitality to allow her to do so.", "설령 그런 생각이 머릿속에 들어왔다 해도, 그녀에게 그렇게 하도록 내버려 두는 것은 손님맞이 도리에 어긋나는 괘씸한 일이라 여겼을 것이다."], ["Florence was too weak to dispute the point, and the Captain carried her up out of hand, laid her down, and covered her with a great watch-coat.", "플로렌스는 너무 기운이 없어 그 점을 따질 수도 없었고, 선장은 그녀를 단번에 들쳐 안고 올라가, 눕히고, 커다란 방수 외투로 덮어 주었다."]], [["“My lady lass!”", "\"우리 아가씨!\""], ["said the Captain, “you’re as safe here as if you was at the top of St Paul’s Cathedral, with the ladder cast off.", "선장이 말했다, \"여긴 사다리를 치워 버린 세인트폴 대성당 꼭대기에 있는 것만큼이나 안전하오."], ["Sleep is what you want, afore all other things, and may you be able to show yourself smart with that there balsam for the still small woice of a wounded mind!", "무엇보다도 잠이 너에게 필요한 것이고, 부디 상처 입은 마음의 가냘프고 작은 목소리를 달래 줄 그 향유 덕에 네가 거뜬해진 모습을 보여 줄 수 있기를!"], ["When there’s anything you want, my Heart’s Delight, as this here humble house or town can offer, pass the word to Ed’ard Cuttle, as’ll stand off and on outside that door, and that there man will wibrate with joy.”", "무엇이든 필요한 게 있거든, 우리 '마음의 기쁨'아, 이 누추한 집이나 거리가 내놓을 수 있는 것이라면, 저 문밖에서 가까이 떠돌며 망보고 있을 에드워드 커틀한테 한마디만 전해 주오, 그러면 그 사내는 기쁨에 겨워 부르르 떨 거요.\""], ["The Captain concluded by kissing the hand that Florence stretched out to him, with the chivalry of any old knight-errant, and walking on tiptoe out of the room.", "선장은 플로렌스가 내민 손에, 어느 옛 편력 기사 못지않은 의협심으로 입을 맞추고는, 발끝으로 살금살금 방을 나가는 것으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["Descending to the little parlour, Captain Cuttle, after holding a hasty council with himself, decided to open the shop-door for a few minutes, and satisfy himself that now, at all events, there was no one loitering about it.", "작은 응접실로 내려와, 커틀 선장은 혼자서 황급히 의논을 한 끝에, 가게 문을 잠시 열어 두고, 적어도 지금은 그 주위를 어슬렁거리는 사람이 아무도 없다는 것을 스스로 확인해 두기로 결심했다."], ["Accordingly he set it open, and stood upon the threshold, keeping a bright look-out, and sweeping the whole street with his spectacles.", "그래서 그는 문을 열어 놓고 문지방에 서서, 날카롭게 망을 보며, 안경 너머로 온 거리를 훑어보았다."]], [["“How de do, Captain Gills?”", "\"안녕하시오, 길스 선장?\""], ["said a voice beside him.", "곁에서 한 목소리가 말했다."], ["The Captain, looking down, found that he had been boarded by Mr Toots while sweeping the horizon.", "선장이 내려다보니, 자기가 수평선을 훑고 있는 사이에 투츠 씨가 자기 곁에 올라타 있었던 것이다."]], [["“How are, you, my lad?”", "\"잘 지냈나, 이 사람아?\""], ["replied the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."]], [["“Well, I’m pretty well, thank’ee, Captain Gills,”", "\"네, 저는 꽤 잘 지냅니다, 고맙습니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“You know I’m never quite what I could wish to be, now.", "\"아시다시피 저는 이제 결코 제가 바라는 만큼의 상태가 못 됩니다."], ["I don’t expect that I ever shall be any more.”", "앞으로도 더 나아지리라고는 기대하지 않고요.\""]], [["Mr Toots never approached any nearer than this to the great theme of his life, when in conversation with Captain Cuttle, on account of the agreement between them.", "투츠 씨는 커틀 선장과 대화할 때면, 둘 사이의 약조 때문에 자기 인생의 그 위대한 주제에 이 이상으로는 결코 가까이 다가가지 않았다."]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “if I could have the pleasure of a word with you, it’s—it’s rather particular.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"선장님과 한마디 나누는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있다면 좋겠는데요, 그게—그게 좀 특별한 일이라서요.\""]], [["“Why, you see, my lad,”", "\"그게 말일세, 이 사람아,\""], ["replied the Captain, leading the way into the parlour, “I ain’t what you may call exactly free this morning; and therefore if you can clap on a bit, I should take it kindly.”", "선장이 응접실로 앞장서 가며 대답했다, \"내가 오늘 아침엔 딱히 한가하다 할 처지가 못 되거든. 그러니 자네가 좀 서둘러 줄 수 있다면, 고맙게 여기겠네.\""]], [["“Certainly, Captain Gills,”", "\"물론입죠, 길스 선장님,\""], ["replied Mr Toots, who seldom had any notion of the Captain’s meaning.", "투츠 씨가 대답했다. 선장의 말뜻을 좀처럼 알아듣지 못하는 사람이었다."], ["“To clap on, is exactly what I could wish to do.", "\"서두르는 거야말로 제가 딱 바라는 바입니다."], ["Naturally.”", "당연하고말고요.\""]], [["“If so be, my lad,”", "\"그렇다면, 이 사람아,\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 응수했다."], ["“Do it!”", "\"그렇게 하게!\""]], [["The Captain was so impressed by the possession of his tremendous secret—by the fact of Miss Dombey being at that moment under his roof, while the innocent and unconscious Toots sat opposite to him—that a perspiration broke out on his forehead, and he found it impossible, while slowly drying the same, glazed hat in hand, to keep his eyes off Mr Toots’s face.", "선장은 자기가 품은 그 어마어마한 비밀에 어찌나 짓눌려 있었던지—바로 그 순간 돔비 양이 자기 지붕 아래 있는데, 그것도 모르는 순진한 투츠가 자기 맞은편에 앉아 있다는 사실에—이마에 진땀이 솟았고, 니스 칠한 모자를 손에 든 채 그것을 천천히 닦으면서도 투츠 씨의 얼굴에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다."], ["Mr Toots, who himself appeared to have some secret reasons for being in a nervous state, was so unspeakably disconcerted by the Captain’s stare, that after looking at him vacantly for some time in silence, and shifting uneasily on his chair, he said:", "자기 나름으로 무언가 마음 졸일 비밀스러운 사정이 있는 듯한 투츠 씨는, 선장의 응시에 어찌나 말할 수 없이 당황했던지, 한동안 말없이 멍하니 그를 바라보다가, 의자에서 불안하게 몸을 들썩이더니 말했다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Captain Gills, but you don’t happen to see anything particular in me, do you?”", "\"실례합니다만, 길스 선장님, 혹시 저한테서 뭔가 이상한 점이라도 보이시는 건 아니지요?\""]], [["“No, my lad,”", "\"아닐세, 이 사람아,\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."], ["“No.”", "\"아닐세.\""]], [["“Because you know,”", "\"왜냐하면 말이죠,\""], ["said Mr Toots with a chuckle, “I know I’m wasting away.", "투츠 씨가 낄낄 웃으며 말했다, \"제가 점점 야위어 가고 있다는 거 저도 압니다."], ["You needn’t at all mind alluding to that.", "그 얘기를 꺼내셔도 전혀 개의치 않으셔도 돼요."], ["I—I should like it. Burgess and Co.", "저는—저는 오히려 그게 좋습니다."], ["have altered my measure, I’m in that state of thinness.", "버지스 상회에서 제 치수를 다시 쟀어요, 제가 그 정도로 말랐거든요."], ["It’s a gratification to me.", "그게 저한테는 만족스럽습니다."], ["I—I’m glad of it.", "저는—저는 그게 기쁩니다."], ["I—I’d a great deal rather go into a decline, if I could.", "저는—할 수만 있다면 차라리 쇠약해져 갔으면 좋겠어요."], ["I’m a mere brute you know, grazing upon the surface of the earth, Captain Gills.”", "아시다시피 저는 그저 한 마리 짐승일 뿐이니까요, 땅거죽이나 뜯어 먹고 사는, 길스 선장님.\""]], [["The more Mr Toots went on in this way, the more the Captain was weighed down by his secret, and stared at him.", "투츠 씨가 이런 식으로 말을 이어 갈수록, 선장은 자기 비밀에 더욱 짓눌리며 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["What with this cause of uneasiness, and his desire to get rid of Mr Toots, the Captain was in such a scared and strange condition, indeed, that if he had been in conversation with a ghost, he could hardly have evinced greater discomposure.", "이 불안의 원인에다, 투츠 씨를 떼어 내고 싶은 마음까지 겹쳐, 선장은 실로 어찌나 겁먹고 묘한 상태였던지, 설령 유령과 대화를 나누고 있었다 한들 이보다 더 큰 동요를 드러내기도 어려웠을 것이다."]], [["“But I was going to say, Captain Gills,”", "\"그런데 제가 말씀드리려던 건요, 길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“Happening to be this way early this morning—to tell you the truth, I was coming to breakfast with you.", "\"오늘 아침 일찍 마침 이쪽으로 오던 길이었는데요—사실대로 말씀드리면, 선장님과 아침을 함께하러 오는 길이었습니다."], ["As to sleep, you know, I never sleep now.", "잠으로 말할 것 같으면, 아시다시피 저는 이제 도통 잠을 못 잡니다."], ["I might be a Watchman, except that I don’t get any pay, and he’s got nothing on his mind.”", "야경꾼이나 다름없는데, 다만 저는 삯을 못 받고, 그 사람은 마음에 걸리는 게 아무것도 없다는 점이 다르지요.\""]], [["“Carry on, my lad!”", "\"계속하게, 이 사람아!\""], ["said the Captain, in an admonitory voice.", "선장이 타이르는 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Certainly, Captain Gills,”", "\"물론입죠, 길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“Perfectly true!", "\"지당하신 말씀!"], ["Happening to be this way early this morning (an hour or so ago), and finding the door shut—”", "오늘 아침 일찍(한 시간쯤 전에) 마침 이쪽으로 오다가, 문이 닫힌 걸 보고는—\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐!"], ["were you waiting there, brother?”", "자네가 거기서 기다리고 있었나, 형제?\""], ["demanded the Captain.", "선장이 캐물었다."]], [["“Not at all, Captain Gills,”", "\"전혀 아닙니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["returned Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 대답했다."], ["“I didn’t stop a moment.", "\"잠시도 멈추지 않았어요."], ["I thought you were out.", "선장님이 외출하신 줄 알았거든요."], ["But the person said—by the bye, you don’t keep a dog, you, Captain Gills?”", "그런데 그 사람이 말하길—그런데 말입니다, 선장님은 개를 안 기르시지요, 길스 선장님?\""]], [["The Captain shook his head.", "선장은 고개를 저었다."]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"그렇고말고요,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “that’s exactly what I said.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"제가 한 말이 바로 그겁니다."], ["I knew you didn’t.", "안 기르시는 줄 제가 알고 있었죠."], ["There is a dog, Captain Gills, connected with—but excuse me.", "어떤 개 한 마리가, 길스 선장님, 어떤 분과 관련이 있는데—하지만 죄송합니다."], ["That’s forbidden ground.”", "그건 금지된 영역이죠.\""]], [["The Captain stared at Mr Toots until he seemed to swell to twice his natural size; and again the perspiration broke out on the Captain’s forehead, when he thought of Diogenes taking it into his head to come down and make a third in the parlour.", "선장은 투츠 씨가 제 몸집의 두 배로 부풀어 오르는 것처럼 보일 때까지 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그리고 디오게네스가 문득 아래로 내려와 응접실에 제삼자로 끼어들 생각을 할까 봐 떠올리자, 선장의 이마에는 또다시 진땀이 솟았다."]], [["“The person said,”", "\"그 사람이 말하길,\""], ["continued Mr Toots, “that he had heard a dog barking in the shop: which I knew couldn’t be, and I told him so.", "투츠 씨가 계속했다, \"가게 안에서 개 짖는 소리를 들었다는 거예요. 그럴 리 없다는 걸 제가 아니까, 그렇게 말해 줬죠."], ["But he was as positive as if he had seen the dog.”", "그런데도 그 사람은 마치 그 개를 직접 보기라도 한 것처럼 단언하더군요.\""]], [["“What person, my lad?”", "\"어떤 사람 말인가, 이 사람아?\""], ["inquired the Captain.", "선장이 물었다."]], [["“Why, you see there it is, Captain Gills,”", "\"그게 말이죠, 바로 그게 문제입니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, with a perceptible increase in the nervousness of his manner.", "투츠 씨가 거동의 불안함이 눈에 띄게 더해지며 말했다."], ["“It’s not for me to say what may have taken place, or what may not have taken place.", "\"무슨 일이 있었는지, 또는 무슨 일이 없었는지 말하는 건 제 몫이 아니에요."], ["Indeed, I don’t know.", "사실 저도 모르겠고요."], ["I get mixed up with all sorts of things that I don’t quite understand, and I think there’s something rather weak in my—in my head, in short.”", "저는 도무지 잘 이해되지 않는 온갖 일에 휘말리곤 하는데, 제 생각엔 제—제 머리에 좀 모자란 구석이 있는 것 같아요, 요컨대.\""]], [["The Captain nodded his own, as a mark of assent.", "선장은 동의의 표시로 자기 머리를 끄덕였다."]], [["“But the person said, as we were walking away,”", "\"하지만 그 사람이, 우리가 걸어 나오는데 말하길,\""], ["continued Mr Toots, “that you knew what, under existing circumstances, might occur—he said ‘might,’ very strongly—and that if you were requested to prepare yourself, you would, no doubt, come prepared.”", "투츠 씨가 계속했다, \"현재의 상황에서 무슨 일이 일어날지도 모른다는 걸 선장님이 아신다고—'모른다'를 아주 힘주어 말하더군요—그리고 만일 선장님더러 채비를 갖춰 두라고 부탁하면, 분명히 채비를 하고 오실 거라고요.\""]], [["“Person, my lad”", "\"사람이라니, 이 사람아.\""], ["the Captain repeated.", "선장이 되뇌었다."]], [["“I don’t know what person, I’m sure, Captain Gills,”", "\"어떤 사람인지는 정말 저도 모르겠어요, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"전혀 짐작이 안 갑니다."], ["replied Mr Toots, “I haven’t the least idea.", "그런데 문간에 와 보니, 그 사람이 거기서 기다리고 있더군요. 그러더니 제가 다시 돌아갈 거냐고 묻길래, 그렇다고 했죠."], ["But coming to the door, I found him waiting there; and he said was I coming back again, and I said yes; and he said did I know you, and I said, yes, I had the pleasure of your acquaintance—you had given me the pleasure of your acquaintance, after some persuasion; and he said, if that was the case, would I say to you what I have said, about existing circumstances and coming prepared, and as soon as ever I saw you, would I ask you to step round the corner, if it was only for one minute, on most important business, to Mr Brogley’s the Broker’s.", "그리고 선장님을 아느냐고 묻길래, 그렇다고, 선장님과 친분을 나누는 기쁨을 누리고 있다고—선장님이 얼마간 권유를 하신 끝에 저한테 친분을 나누는 기쁨을 주셨다고 했어요. 그랬더니 그 사람이, 사정이 그렇다면, 제가 방금 말씀드린 현재의 상황이며 채비를 하고 오시라는 얘기를 선장님께 전해 주고, 선장님을 뵙는 즉시, 단 일 분이라도 좋으니 아주 중요한 용무로 모퉁이를 돌아 브로글리 중개인 가게로 잠깐 들러 주십사 부탁드려 달라더군요."], ["Now, I tell you what, Captain Gills—whatever it is, I am convinced it’s very important; and if you like to step round, now, I’ll wait here till you come back.”", "자, 그래서 말씀인데요, 길스 선장님—그게 무엇이든 간에, 저는 그게 아주 중요한 일이라고 확신합니다. 그러니 지금 잠깐 다녀오시겠다면, 선장님이 돌아오실 때까지 제가 여기서 기다리고 있겠습니다.\""]], [["The Captain, divided between his fear of compromising Florence in some way by not going, and his horror of leaving Mr Toots in possession of the house with a chance of finding out the secret, was a spectacle of mental disturbance that even Mr Toots could not be blind to.", "선장은, 가지 않음으로써 어떤 식으로든 플로렌스를 위태롭게 할까 봐 하는 두려움과, 투츠 씨에게 집을 맡겨 두었다가 비밀을 들킬지도 모른다는 끔찍함 사이에서 갈팡질팡했으니, 그 마음의 동요는 투츠 씨조차 못 알아챌 수 없을 만한 광경이었다."], ["But that young gentleman, considering his nautical friend as merely in a state of preparation for the interview he was going to have, was quite satisfied, and did not review his own discreet conduct without chuckle.", "그러나 그 젊은 신사는, 자기의 항해사 친구가 그저 자기가 가질 면담에 대비해 채비를 하는 상태일 뿐이라고 여겨, 아주 흡족해했고, 자기 자신의 신중한 처신을 낄낄 웃지 않고는 되돌아볼 수 없었다."]], [["At length the Captain decided, as the lesser of two evils, to run round to Brogley’s the Broker’s: previously locking the door that communicated with the upper part of the house, and putting the key in his pocket.", "마침내 선장은, 두 악 가운데 덜한 쪽으로, 브로글리 중개인 가게로 잠깐 다녀오기로 결심했다. 그에 앞서 집 위층으로 통하는 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 주머니에 넣었다."], ["“If so be,”", "\"그게 말일세,\""], ["said the Captain to Mr Toots, with not a little shame and hesitation, “as you’ll excuse my doing of it, brother.”", "선장이 적잖은 부끄러움과 망설임을 담아 투츠 씨에게 말했다, \"내가 이렇게 하는 걸 양해해 준다면 말이지, 형제.\""]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, “whatever you do, is satisfactory to me.”", "투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"선장님이 무엇을 하시든, 저로서는 흡족합니다.\""]], [["The Captain thanked him heartily, and promising to come back in less than five minutes, went out in quest of the person who had entrusted Mr Toots with this mysterious message.", "선장은 진심으로 그에게 고마워하고, 오 분 안에 돌아오겠다고 약속한 뒤, 투츠 씨에게 그 알 수 없는 전갈을 맡긴 사람을 찾아 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Poor Mr Toots, left to himself, lay down upon the sofa, little thinking who had reclined there last, and, gazing up at the skylight and resigning himself to visions of Miss Dombey, lost all heed of time and place.", "가엾은 투츠 씨는 홀로 남겨지자 소파에 드러누웠으니, 바로 거기에 마지막으로 누웠던 이가 누구였는지는 꿈에도 모른 채, 천창을 올려다보며 돔비 양에 대한 환상에 자신을 내맡기고는, 시간이며 장소며 다 잊어버렸다."]], [["It was as well that he did so; for although the Captain was not gone long, he was gone much longer than he had proposed.", "그가 그렇게 한 것은 잘된 일이었다. 선장은 오래 가 있지는 않았지만, 자기가 말했던 것보다는 훨씬 오래 가 있었기 때문이다."], ["When he came back, he was very pale indeed, and greatly agitated, and even looked as if he had been shedding tears.", "돌아왔을 때 그는 실로 몹시 창백했고, 크게 동요해 있었으며, 심지어 눈물을 흘린 것처럼 보이기까지 했다."], ["He seemed to have lost the faculty of speech, until he had been to the cupboard and taken a dram of rum from the case-bottle, when he fetched a deep breath, and sat down in a chair with his hand before his face.", "그는 찬장으로 가서 됫병에서 럼주를 한 모금 들이켤 때까지 말하는 능력을 잃어버린 듯하더니, 깊이 숨을 한 번 내쉬고는, 한 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 의자에 앉았다."]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["said Toots, kindly, “I hope and trust there’s nothing wrong?”", "투츠가 다정하게 말했다, \"아무 일도 없기를 바라고 또 믿습니다만?\""]], [["“Thank’ee, my lad, not a bit,”", "\"고맙네, 이 사람아, 아무 일도 없네,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Quite contrairy.”", "\"오히려 정반대일세.\""]], [["“You have the appearance of being overcome, Captain Gills,”", "\"넋이 나가신 것 같은 모습이신데요, 길스 선장님,\""], ["observed Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Why, my lad, I am took aback,”", "\"아닌 게 아니라, 이 사람아, 내가 좀 얼이 빠졌네,\""], ["the Captain admitted.", "선장이 인정했다."], ["“I am.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Is there anything I can do, Captain Gills?”", "\"제가 할 수 있는 일이 있을까요, 길스 선장님?\""], ["inquired Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 물었다."], ["“If there is, make use of me.”", "\"있다면, 저를 부려 주세요.\""]], [["The Captain removed his hand from his face, looked at him with a remarkable expression of pity and tenderness, and took him by the hand, and shook it hard.", "선장은 얼굴에서 손을 떼고, 보기 드문 연민과 다정함의 표정으로 그를 바라보더니, 그의 손을 잡아 힘껏 흔들었다."]], [["“No, thank’ee,”", "\"아니, 고맙네,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Nothing.", "\"아무것도 없네."], ["Only I’ll take it as a favour if you’ll part company for the present.", "다만 지금은 자네가 자리를 비켜 준다면 고맙겠네."], ["I believe, brother,”", "내 생각에, 형제,\""], ["wringing his hand again, “that, after Wal”r, and on a different model, you’re as good a lad as ever stepped.”", "그의 손을 다시 한번 꼭 쥐며, \"월터 다음으로는, 그리고 다른 본보기로 치자면, 자네는 여태껏 세상에 나온 그 누구 못지않은 좋은 사람일세.\""]], [["“Upon my word and honour, Captain Gills,”", "\"맹세코 그리고 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데요, 길스 선장님,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, giving the Captain’s hand a preliminary slap before shaking it again, “it’s delightful to me to possess your good opinion.", "투츠 씨가 선장의 손을 다시 흔들기에 앞서 한 번 툭 치며 대답했다, \"선장님의 좋은 평을 얻게 되어 저로서는 기쁘기 그지없습니다."], ["Thank’ee.”", "고맙습니다.\""]], [["“And bear a hand and cheer up,”", "\"그러니 기운을 내고 힘을 좀 내게,\""], ["said the Captain, patting him on the back.", "선장이 그의 등을 토닥이며 말했다."], ["“What!", "\"아니!"], ["There’s more than one sweet creetur in the world!”", "세상에 귀여운 아가씨가 한 사람만 있는 건 아니잖나!\""]], [["“Not to me, Captain Gills,” replied Mr Toots gravely.", "\"저한테는 그렇지 않습니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["“Not to me, I assure you.", "투츠 씨가 엄숙하게 대답했다."], ["The state of my feelings towards Miss Dombey is of that unspeakable description, that my heart is a desert island, and she lives in it alone.", "\"저한테는 그렇지 않아요, 정말입니다. 돔비 양을 향한 제 감정의 상태는 말로 다 할 수 없는 그런 것이어서, 제 마음은 하나의 무인도이고, 그분이 그곳에 홀로 사십니다."], ["I’m getting more used up every day, and I’m proud to be so.", "저는 날마다 더 쇠약해져 가는데, 저는 그게 자랑스럽습니다."], ["If you could see my legs when I take my boots off, you’d form some idea of what unrequited affection is.", "제가 부츠를 벗었을 때 제 다리를 보신다면, 보답받지 못한 사랑이라는 게 어떤 건지 얼마쯤 짐작이 가실 겁니다."], ["I have been prescribed bark, but I don’t take it, for I don’t wish to have any tone whatever given to my constitution.", "의사가 키나나무 껍질을 처방했는데, 저는 안 먹습니다, 제 체질에 어떤 식으로든 기력이 붙는 건 바라지 않으니까요."], ["I’d rather not.", "차라리 안 그러는 게 낫지요."], ["This, however, is forbidden ground.", "하지만 이건 금지된 영역이죠."], ["Captain Gills, goodbye!”", "길스 선장님, 안녕히 계십시오!\""]], [["Captain Cuttle cordially reciprocating the warmth of Mr Toots’s farewell, locked the door behind him, and shaking his head with the same remarkable expression of pity and tenderness as he had regarded him with before, went up to see if Florence wanted him.", "커틀 선장은 투츠 씨의 따뜻한 작별 인사에 진심으로 화답하고는, 그의 등 뒤로 문을 잠그고, 앞서 그를 바라보던 것과 똑같은 보기 드문 연민과 다정함의 표정으로 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 플로렌스가 자기를 찾는지 보려고 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["There was an entire change in the Captain’s face as he went upstairs.", "위층으로 올라가는 선장의 얼굴에는 완전한 변화가 일어나 있었다."], ["He wiped his eyes with his handkerchief, and he polished the bridge of his nose with his sleeve as he had done already that morning, but his face was absolutely changed.", "그는 손수건으로 눈을 닦고, 그날 아침 이미 한 번 그랬던 것처럼 소매로 콧날을 문질러 닦았지만, 그의 얼굴은 그야말로 달라져 있었다."], ["Now, he might have been thought supremely happy; now, he might have been thought sad; but the kind of gravity that sat upon his features was quite new to them, and was as great an improvement to them as if they had undergone some sublimating process.", "지금 그는 더없이 행복해 보인다고 여길 수도 있었고, 지금 그는 슬퍼 보인다고 여길 수도 있었다. 그러나 그의 이목구비에 깃든 그런 종류의 진지함은 거기에는 아주 새로운 것이었으며, 마치 그 얼굴이 어떤 승화의 과정을 거치기라도 한 듯 크나큰 개선을 이루어 놓은 것이었다."]], [["He knocked softly, with his hook, at Florence’s door, twice or thrice; but, receiving no answer, ventured first to peep in, and then to enter: emboldened to take the latter step, perhaps, by the familiar recognition of Diogenes, who, stretched upon the ground by the side of her couch, wagged his tail, and winked his eyes at the Captain, without being at the trouble of getting up.", "그는 갈고리로 플로렌스의 방문을 부드럽게 두세 번 두드렸다. 그러나 아무 대답이 없자, 우선 살그머니 안을 들여다보았고, 그다음에는 들어가 보았다. 그 두 번째 걸음을 감행하게 된 것은, 아마도 디오게네스가 낯익게 알아봐 준 덕분이었을 것이다. 디오게네스는 그녀의 긴 의자 옆 바닥에 길게 누운 채, 굳이 일어나는 수고는 하지 않고, 선장을 향해 꼬리를 흔들며 눈을 끔벅여 주었다."]], [["She was sleeping heavily, and moaning in her sleep; and Captain Cuttle, with a perfect awe of her youth, and beauty, and her sorrow, raised her head, and adjusted the coat that covered her, where it had fallen off, and darkened the window a little more that she might sleep on, and crept out again, and took his post of watch upon the stairs.", "그녀는 깊이 잠들어 있었고, 잠결에 신음하고 있었다. 커틀 선장은 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움과 슬픔에 완전히 외경심을 품고서, 그녀의 머리를 받쳐 주고, 흘러내린 그녀를 덮은 외투를 고쳐 덮어 주고, 그녀가 계속 잘 수 있도록 창문을 조금 더 어둡게 가리고는, 다시 살그머니 빠져나와 계단에서 자기 망보는 자리를 잡았다."], ["All this, with a touch and tread as light as Florence’s own.", "이 모든 것을, 플로렌스 자신의 그것만큼이나 가벼운 손길과 발걸음으로 했다."]], [["Long may it remain in this mixed world a point not easy of decision, which is the more beautiful evidence of the Almighty’s goodness—the delicate fingers that are formed for sensitiveness and sympathy of touch, and made to minister to pain and grief, or the rough hard Captain Cuttle hand, that the heart teaches, guides, and softens in a moment!", "이 뒤섞인 세상에서, 전능하신 분의 선하심을 보여 주는 더 아름다운 증거가 어느 쪽인지—섬세한 감수성과 어루만지는 공감을 위해 빚어져, 아픔과 슬픔을 돌보도록 만들어진 그 가냘픈 손가락들인지, 아니면 마음이 한순간에 가르치고 이끌고 부드럽게 만드는, 커틀 선장의 그 거칠고 단단한 손인지—오래도록 가리기 쉽지 않은 문제로 남아 있어도 좋으리라!"]], [["Florence slept upon her couch, forgetful of her homelessness and orphanage, and Captain Cuttle watched upon the stairs.", "플로렌스는 자기 긴 의자에서, 갈 곳 없는 처지도 부모 없는 신세도 잊은 채 잠들었고, 커틀 선장은 계단에서 망을 보았다."], ["A louder sob or moan than usual, brought him sometimes to her door; but by degrees she slept more peacefully, and the Captain’s watch was undisturbed.", "여느 때보다 더 큰 흐느낌이나 신음이 이따금 그를 그녀의 방문 앞으로 데려갔지만, 차츰 그녀는 더 평온히 잠들었고, 선장의 망보기는 방해받지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIX. The Midshipman makes a Discovery", "# 제44장"]], [["It was long before Florence awoke.", "플로렌스가 잠에서 깨어나기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸렸다."], ["The day was in its prime, the day was in its wane, and still, uneasy in mind and body, she slept on; unconscious of her strange bed, of the noise and turmoil in the street, and of the light that shone outside the shaded window.", "한낮이 되었다가, 다시 저물어 가는 시각이 되도록, 마음도 몸도 편치 못한 채 그녀는 줄곧 잠들어 있었다. 낯선 침대도, 거리의 소음과 소란도, 가려진 창밖에서 비쳐 드는 빛도 의식하지 못했다."], ["Perfect unconsciousness of what had happened in the home that existed no more, even the deep slumber of exhaustion could not produce.", "이제는 존재하지 않는 그 집에서 일어난 일에 대한 완전한 무의식만은, 기진맥진한 깊은 잠으로도 만들어 낼 수 없었다."], ["Some undefined and mournful recollection of it, dozing uneasily but never sleeping, pervaded all her rest.", "그 일에 대한 어떤 흐릿하고 슬픈 기억이, 불안하게 졸뿐 결코 잠들지는 않은 채, 그녀의 모든 휴식에 스며 있었다."], ["A dull sorrow, like a half-lulled sense of pain, was always present to her; and her pale cheek was oftener wet with tears than the honest Captain, softly putting in his head from time to time at the half-closed door, could have desired to see it.", "반쯤 잠재워진 고통의 감각 같은 둔중한 슬픔이 늘 그녀 곁에 있었고, 그녀의 창백한 뺨은 반쯤 닫힌 문 사이로 이따금 머리를 들이밀어 살펴보던 정직한 선장이 보고 싶어 했을 것보다 더 자주 눈물에 젖어 있었다."]], [["The sun was getting low in the west, and, glancing out of a red mist, pierced with its rays opposite loopholes and pieces of fretwork in the spires of city churches, as if with golden arrows that struck through and through them—and far away athwart the river and its flat banks, it was gleaming like a path of fire—and out at sea it was irradiating sails of ships—and, looked towards, from quiet churchyards, upon hill-tops in the country, it was steeping distant prospects in a flush and glow that seemed to mingle earth and sky together in one glorious suffusion—when Florence, opening her heavy eyes, lay at first, looking without interest or recognition at the unfamiliar walls around her, and listening in the same regardless manner to the noises in the street.", "해가 서쪽으로 낮게 기울며, 붉은 안개 사이로 비스듬히 비쳐 나와, 도시 교회 첨탑의 맞은편 총안과 격자 세공들을 그 빛살로 꿰뚫었으니, 마치 그것들을 관통하여 박히는 황금 화살 같았고—멀리 강과 그 평평한 둑을 가로질러 불의 길처럼 번쩍였으며—바다 멀리 나가서는 배들의 돛을 환히 비추었고—시골 언덕마루의 고요한 묘지에서 바라보면, 먼 풍경을 붉은 기운과 노을빛에 잠기게 하여 마치 땅과 하늘을 하나의 찬란한 충만 속에 뒤섞어 놓은 듯했다—바로 그때 플로렌스가 무거운 눈을 떠, 처음에는 누운 채 주위의 낯선 벽들을 흥미도 알아봄도 없이 바라보았고, 거리의 소음에도 똑같이 무심한 태도로 귀를 기울였다."], ["But presently she started up upon her couch, gazed round with a surprised and vacant look, and recollected all.", "그러나 곧 그녀는 침상에서 벌떡 일어나, 놀라고 멍한 눈빛으로 주위를 둘러보고는, 모든 것을 기억해 냈다."]], [["“My pretty,”", "\"우리 예쁜 아가씨,\""], ["said the Captain, knocking at the door, “what cheer?”", "선장이 문을 두드리며 말했다, \"기분이 좀 어떻수?\""]], [["“Dear friend,”", "\"그리운 친구분,\""], ["cried Florence, hurrying to him, “is it you?”", "플로렌스가 그에게로 황급히 다가서며 외쳤다, \"선장님이세요?\""]], [["The Captain felt so much pride in the name, and was so pleased by the gleam of pleasure in her face, when she saw him, that he kissed his hook, by way of reply, in speechless gratification.", "선장은 그 호칭에 너무도 큰 자부심을 느꼈고, 그녀가 자기를 보았을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 스치는 기쁨의 빛에 너무도 흐뭇하여, 대답 대신 말없는 흡족함으로 자기 갈고리에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“What cheer, bright di’mond?”", "\"기분이 좀 어떻수, 빛나는 다이아몬드 같은 아가씨?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“I have surely slept very long,”", "\"제가 정말 오래 잤나 봐요,\""], ["returned Florence.", "플로렌스가 대답했다."], ["“When did I come here?", "\"제가 언제 여기 왔죠?"], ["Yesterday?”", "어제였나요?\""]], [["“This here blessed day, my lady lass,”", "\"바로 오늘이라우, 복받은 오늘 말이우, 아가씨,\""], ["replied the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."]], [["“Has there been no night?", "\"밤이 없었나요?"], ["Is it still day?”", "아직도 낮인가요?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“Getting on for evening now, my pretty,”", "\"이제 저녁으로 접어드는 참이라우, 예쁜 아가씨,\""], ["said the Captain, drawing back the curtain of the window.", "선장이 창문 커튼을 젖히며 말했다."], ["“See!”", "\"보시구려!\""]], [["Florence, with her hand upon the Captain’s arm, so sorrowful and timid, and the Captain with his rough face and burly figure, so quietly protective of her, stood in the rosy light of the bright evening sky, without saying a word.", "플로렌스는 선장의 팔에 손을 얹은 채, 그토록 서글프고 수줍어하며, 선장은 거친 얼굴과 우람한 몸집으로 그토록 조용히 그녀를 감싸 보호하며, 밝은 저녁 하늘의 장밋빛 속에 한마디 말도 없이 서 있었다."], ["However strange the form of speech into which he might have fashioned the feeling, if he had had to give it utterance, the Captain felt, as sensibly as the most eloquent of men could have done, that there was something in the tranquil time and in its softened beauty that would make the wounded heart of Florence overflow; and that it was better that such tears should have their way.", "만일 그 감정을 입 밖에 내어야 했더라면 그것을 어떤 기묘한 말투로 빚어냈을지 몰라도, 선장은 그 고요한 시간 속에, 그리고 그 부드러워진 아름다움 속에 플로렌스의 상처 입은 마음을 넘쳐흐르게 할 무언가가 있음을, 가장 언변 좋은 사람만큼이나 또렷하게 느꼈다. 그리고 그런 눈물은 흐르도록 내버려 두는 편이 낫다는 것도."], ["So not a word spake Captain Cuttle.", "그래서 커틀 선장은 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["But when he felt his arm clasped closer, and when he felt the lonely head come nearer to it, and lay itself against his homely coarse blue sleeve, he pressed it gently with his rugged hand, and understood it, and was understood.", "그러나 그녀의 팔이 더 가까이 자기 팔을 부여잡는 것을 느끼고, 그 외로운 머리가 더 다가와 자기의 투박한 거친 푸른 소맷자락에 기대는 것을 느끼자, 그는 우툴두툴한 손으로 그것을 부드럽게 눌러 주었고, 그것을 이해했고, 또 이해받았다."]], [["“Better now, my pretty!”", "\"이제 좀 나아졌수, 예쁜 아가씨!\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Cheerily, cheerily, I’ll go down below, and get some dinner ready.", "\"기운 내우, 기운 내우, 내 아래층으로 내려가서 저녁을 좀 차려 보겠수."], ["Will you come down of your own self, arterwards, pretty, or shall Ed’ard Cuttle come and fetch you?”", "나중에 혼자서 내려올 수 있겠수, 예쁜 아가씨, 아니면 에드워드 커틀이 데리러 올라올까?\""]], [["As Florence assured him that she was quite able to walk downstairs, the Captain, though evidently doubtful of his own hospitality in permitting it, left her to do so, and immediately set about roasting a fowl at the fire in the little parlour.", "플로렌스가 혼자서 충분히 계단을 내려갈 수 있다고 그를 안심시키자, 선장은 그것을 허락하는 것이 자기 접대의 도리에 어긋나는 게 아닐까 미심쩍어하면서도 그녀가 그렇게 하도록 내버려 두고, 곧장 작은 거실 난롯불에 닭 한 마리를 굽기 시작했다."], ["To achieve his cookery with the greater skill, he pulled off his coat, tucked up his wristbands, and put on his glazed hat, without which assistant he never applied himself to any nice or difficult undertaking.", "더 솜씨 좋게 요리를 해내려고 그는 외투를 벗고, 소맷부리를 걷어 올리고, 번들거리는 모자를 썼는데, 그는 무언가 정교하거나 까다로운 일에 착수할 때면 이 조력자 없이는 결코 손을 대는 법이 없었다."]], [["After cooling her aching head and burning face in the fresh water which the Captain’s care had provided for her while she slept, Florence went to the little mirror to bind up her disordered hair.", "선장이 그녀가 잠든 동안 마련해 둔 맑은 물에 욱신거리는 머리와 화끈거리는 얼굴을 식힌 후, 플로렌스는 헝클어진 머리를 매만지러 작은 거울 앞으로 갔다."], ["Then she knew—in a moment, for she shunned it instantly, that on her breast there was the darkening mark of an angry hand.", "그때 그녀는 알아챘다—한순간에, 곧바로 외면해 버렸으니까, 자기 가슴에 성난 손이 남긴 거무스름해져 가는 자국이 있다는 것을."]], [["Her tears burst forth afresh at the sight; she was ashamed and afraid of it; but it moved her to no anger against him. Homeless and fatherless, she forgave him everything; hardly thought that she had need to forgive him, or that she did; but she fled from the idea of him as she had fled from the reality, and he was utterly gone and lost. There was no such Being in the world.", "그것을 본 순간 그녀의 눈물이 다시 터져 나왔다. 그녀는 그것이 부끄럽고 두려웠다. 그러나 그것이 그에 대한 어떤 분노로 그녀를 이끌지는 않았다. 집도 없고 아버지도 없게 된 그녀는 그에게 모든 것을 용서했다. 자기가 그를 용서할 필요가 있다거나 용서하고 있다는 생각조차 거의 하지 않았다. 다만 그녀는 그 실체로부터 달아났듯이 그에 대한 생각으로부터도 달아났고, 그는 완전히 사라져 없어졌다. 세상에 그런 사람은 없었다."]], [["What to do, or where to live, Florence—poor, inexperienced girl!—could not yet consider.", "무엇을 해야 할지, 어디서 살아야 할지, 플로렌스는—가엾고 세상 물정 모르는 소녀!—아직 헤아릴 수가 없었다."], ["She had indistinct dreams of finding, a long way off, some little sisters to instruct, who would be gentle with her, and to whom, under some feigned name, she might attach herself, and who would grow up in their happy home, and marry, and be good to their old governess, and perhaps entrust her, in time, with the education of their own daughters.", "그녀에게는 흐릿한 꿈이 있었다. 아주 먼 어딘가에서 가르칠 어린 자매들을 찾아, 그 아이들이 자기에게 다정히 굴고, 어떤 가명으로 그 아이들에게 정을 붙이고, 아이들이 행복한 가정에서 자라나 시집을 가서는 자기들의 늙은 가정교사에게 잘해 주고, 어쩌면 때가 되면 자기들의 딸들 교육을 그녀에게 맡길지도 모른다는 꿈."], ["And she thought how strange and sorrowful it would be, thus to become a grey-haired woman, carrying her secret to the grave, when Florence Dombey was forgotten.", "그리고 그녀는 생각했다. 플로렌스 돔비가 잊힌 뒤에, 자기 비밀을 무덤까지 안고 가는 백발의 여인이 되어 간다는 것이 얼마나 기이하고 서글픈 일일까 하고."], ["But it was all dim and clouded to her now.", "그러나 지금은 그 모든 것이 그녀에게 흐릿하고 어슴푸레했다."], ["She only knew that she had no Father upon earth, and she said so, many times, with her suppliant head hidden from all, but her Father who was in Heaven.", "그녀는 다만 이 땅 위에 자기에게는 아버지가 없다는 것만을 알았고, 모두에게 가려 숨긴 채 오직 하늘에 계신 아버지께만 드러낸 그 간구하는 머리로, 그 말을 여러 번 되뇌었다."]], [["Her little stock of money amounted to but a few guineas.", "그녀가 지닌 얼마 안 되는 돈은 몇 기니에 지나지 않았다."], ["With a part of this, it would be necessary to buy some clothes, for she had none but those she wore.", "그 일부로 옷가지를 좀 사야 했으니, 지금 입고 있는 것 말고는 가진 옷이 없었기 때문이다."], ["She was too desolate to think how soon her money would be gone—too much a child in worldly matters to be greatly troubled on that score yet, even if her other trouble had been less.", "그녀는 너무도 막막하여 자기 돈이 얼마나 빨리 바닥날지 헤아릴 마음의 여유가 없었다—세상일에는 너무도 어린아이 같아서, 설령 다른 근심이 덜했더라도 그 문제로 크게 마음 졸이지는 못했을 것이다."], ["She tried to calm her thoughts and stay her tears; to quiet the hurry in her throbbing head, and bring herself to believe that what had happened were but the events of a few hours ago, instead of weeks or months, as they appeared; and went down to her kind protector.", "그녀는 생각을 가라앉히고 눈물을 그치려 애썼고, 지끈거리는 머릿속의 어수선함을 진정시키며, 일어난 일이 몇 주나 몇 달 전이 아니라—그렇게만 여겨졌지만—그저 몇 시간 전의 사건이라고 스스로 믿어 보려 했다. 그러고는 그 친절한 보호자에게로 내려갔다."]], [["The Captain had spread the cloth with great care, and was making some egg-sauce in a little saucepan: basting the fowl from time to time during the process with a strong interest, as it turned and browned on a string before the fire.", "선장은 식탁보를 무척 정성스럽게 펴 놓고, 작은 냄비에 달걀 소스를 만들고 있었다. 그러는 동안 끈에 매달려 난롯불 앞에서 돌아가며 노릇노릇 구워지는 닭에 대단한 관심을 기울이며 이따금 기름을 끼얹어 주었다."], ["Having propped Florence up with cushions on the sofa, which was already wheeled into a warm corner for her greater comfort, the Captain pursued his cooking with extraordinary skill, making hot gravy in a second little saucepan, boiling a handful of potatoes in a third, never forgetting the egg-sauce in the first, and making an impartial round of basting and stirring with the most useful of spoons every minute.", "더 편안하라고 이미 따뜻한 구석으로 밀어다 놓은 소파에 쿠션을 받쳐 플로렌스를 기대 앉히고 나서, 선장은 비상한 솜씨로 요리를 이어 갔다. 두 번째 작은 냄비에 뜨거운 그레이비를 만들고, 세 번째 냄비에 감자 한 줌을 삶고, 첫 번째 냄비의 달걀 소스도 결코 잊지 않으며, 더없이 요긴한 숟가락으로 일 분마다 골고루 기름을 끼얹고 휘저었다."], ["Besides these cares, the Captain had to keep his eye on a diminutive frying-pan, in which some sausages were hissing and bubbling in a most musical manner; and there was never such a radiant cook as the Captain looked, in the height and heat of these functions: it being impossible to say whether his face or his glazed hat shone the brighter.", "이런 수고들에 더해 선장은 자그마한 프라이팬도 살펴야 했는데, 그 안에서는 소시지 몇 개가 더없이 음악적인 소리로 지글지글 보글보글 끓고 있었다. 그리고 이 일들이 한창 무르익어 열기를 더할 때의 선장만큼 환히 빛나는 요리사도 없었으니, 그의 얼굴과 번들거리는 모자 중 어느 것이 더 환하게 빛나는지 가려낼 수 없을 정도였다."]], [["The dinner being at length quite ready, Captain Cuttle dished and served it up, with no less dexterity than he had cooked it.", "마침내 저녁 식사가 완전히 준비되자, 커틀 선장은 요리할 때 못지않은 능숙함으로 음식을 접시에 담아 차려 냈다."], ["He then dressed for dinner, by taking off his glazed hat and putting on his coat.", "그러고는 번들거리는 모자를 벗고 외투를 입음으로써 저녁 식사를 위한 정장을 갖추었다."], ["That done, he wheeled the table close against Florence on the sofa, said grace, unscrewed his hook, screwed his fork into its place, and did the honours of the table.", "그 일을 마치자 그는 소파에 앉은 플로렌스에게 바싹 식탁을 밀어다 붙이고, 식전 기도를 올리고, 갈고리를 풀어 내고 그 자리에 포크를 끼워 넣고는, 식탁의 주인 노릇을 했다."]], [["“My lady lass,”", "\"아가씨,\""], ["said the Captain, “cheer up, and try to eat a deal.", "선장이 말했다, \"기운 내우, 그리고 많이 좀 들어 보구려."], ["Stand by, my deary! Liver wing it is.", "자, 옆에서 대기하겠수, 우리 아가씨!"], ["Sarse it is.", "닭 다릿살이라우."], ["Sassage it is.", "소스라우. 소시지라우."], ["And potato!”", "그리고 감자!\""], ["all which the Captain ranged symmetrically on a plate, and pouring hot gravy on the whole with the useful spoon, set before his cherished guest.", "이 모든 것을 선장은 접시 위에 가지런히 늘어놓고, 그 요긴한 숟가락으로 뜨거운 그레이비를 전부에 부어, 자기가 애지중지하는 손님 앞에 차려 놓았다."]], [["“The whole row o’ dead lights is up, for’ard, lady lass,”", "\"앞쪽 죽은 등(燈) 덮개도 한 줄로 죄다 올려놨고, 아가씨,\""], ["observed the Captain, encouragingly, “and everythink is made snug.", "선장이 격려하듯 말했다, \"모든 게 다 아늑하게 준비됐수."], ["Try and pick a bit, my pretty.", "좀 들어 보구려, 예쁜 아가씨."], ["If Wal”r was here—”", "월터가 여기 있었다면—\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["If I had him for my brother now!”", "지금 그분이 제 오라버니이기만 했다면!\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."]], [["“Don’t!", "\"그러지!"], ["don’t take on, my pretty!”", "그렇게 상심하지 마우, 예쁜 아가씨!\""], ["said the Captain, “awast, to obleege me!", "선장이 말했다, \"이 늙은이 봐서라도, 멈추구려!"], ["He was your nat’ral born friend like, warn’t he, Pet?”", "그 애는 아가씨한테 타고난 천생의 벗 같은 사람이었지, 안 그렇수, 아가씨?\""]], [["Florence had no words to answer with.", "플로렌스는 대답할 말이 없었다."], ["She only said, “Oh, dear, dear Paul!", "그녀는 다만 \"오, 그리운, 그리운 폴!"], ["oh, Walter!”", "오, 월터!\"라고만 말했다."]], [["“The wery planks she walked on,”", "\"그 애가 밟고 다니던 바로 그 널빤지마저,\""], ["murmured the Captain, looking at her drooping face, “was as high esteemed by Wal”r, as the water brooks is by the hart which never rejices!", "선장이 그녀의 수그린 얼굴을 바라보며 중얼거렸다, \"월터한테는 결코 만족할 줄 모르는 수사슴이 시냇물을 귀히 여기는 만큼이나 높이 떠받들어졌다우!"], ["I see him now, the wery day as he was rated on them Dombey books, a speaking of her with his face a glistening with doo—leastways with his modest sentiments—like a new blowed rose, at dinner.", "지금도 그 애가 눈에 선하우, 바로 그 돔비 회사 장부에 이름이 올라가던 날, 저녁 식사 자리에서 얼굴이 이슬로 반짝이며—아니 그 애의 겸손한 마음씨로, 갓 피어난 장미처럼 반짝이며—아가씨 얘기를 하던 모습이."], ["Well, well!", "그래, 그래!"], ["If our poor Wal”r was here, my lady lass—or if he could be—for he’s drownded, ain’t he?”", "우리 가엾은 월터가 여기 있다면, 아가씨—아니, 있을 수만 있다면—그 애는 물에 빠져 죽었으니까, 안 그렇수?\""]], [["Florence shook her head.", "플로렌스는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“Yes, yes; drownded,”", "\"그래, 그래, 물에 빠져 죽었지,\""], ["said the Captain, soothingly; “as I was saying, if he could be here he’d beg and pray of you, my precious, to pick a leetle bit, with a look-out for your own sweet health.", "선장이 달래듯 말했다, \"내 말했듯이, 그 애가 여기 있을 수만 있다면 아가씨한테 빌고 또 빌었을 거라우, 우리 귀한 아가씨, 자기 고운 건강을 챙겨서 조금이라도 좀 들라고 말이우."], ["Whereby, hold your own, my lady lass, as if it was for Wal”r’s sake, and lay your pretty head to the wind.”", "그러니 버텨 내우, 아가씨, 월터를 위해서라고 여기고, 그 고운 머리를 바람 쪽으로 들어 올리구려.\""]], [["Florence essayed to eat a morsel, for the Captain’s pleasure.", "플로렌스는 선장을 기쁘게 해 주려고 한 입 먹어 보려 애썼다."], ["The Captain, meanwhile, who seemed to have quite forgotten his own dinner, laid down his knife and fork, and drew his chair to the sofa.", "그동안 선장은 자기 저녁밥은 까맣게 잊은 듯, 칼과 포크를 내려놓고 의자를 소파 쪽으로 끌어당겼다."]], [["“Wal”r was a trim lad, warn’t he, precious?”", "\"월터는 단정한 녀석이었지, 안 그렇수, 귀한 아가씨?\""], ["said the Captain, after sitting for some time silently rubbing his chin, with his eyes fixed upon her, “and a brave lad, and a good lad?”", "선장이 한동안 말없이 앉아 그녀에게 눈길을 고정한 채 턱을 문지르고 나서 말했다, \"그리고 용감한 녀석이었고, 착한 녀석이었지?\""]], [["Florence tearfully assented.", "플로렌스는 눈물을 글썽이며 그렇다고 했다."]], [["“And he’s drownded, Beauty, ain’t he?”", "\"그리고 그 애는 물에 빠져 죽었지, 어여쁜 아가씨, 안 그렇수?\""], ["said the Captain, in a soothing voice.", "선장이 달래는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["Florence could not but assent again.", "플로렌스는 또다시 그렇다고 할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“He was older than you, my lady lass,”", "\"그 애는 아가씨보다 나이가 많았지, 아가씨,\""], ["pursued the Captain, “but you was like two children together, at first; wam’t you?”", "선장이 이어 갔다, \"하지만 처음엔 아가씨네 둘이 꼭 어린아이 둘 같았지, 안 그렇수?\""]], [["Florence answered “Yes.”", "플로렌스는 \"네\"라고 대답했다."]], [["“And Wal”r’s drownded,”", "\"그리고 월터는 물에 빠져 죽었지,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Ain’t he?”", "\"안 그렇수?\""]], [["The repetition of this inquiry was a curious source of consolation, but it seemed to be one to Captain Cuttle, for he came back to it again and again.", "이 물음을 거듭하는 것이 위안의 기묘한 원천이었으나, 커틀 선장에게는 그것이 하나의 위안인 듯했으니, 그는 몇 번이고 다시 그 물음으로 되돌아왔다."], ["Florence, fain to push from her her untasted dinner, and to lie back on her sofa, gave him her hand, feeling that she had disappointed him, though truly wishing to have pleased him after all his trouble, but he held it in his own (which shook as he held it), and appearing to have quite forgotten all about the dinner and her want of appetite, went on growling at intervals, in a ruminating tone of sympathy, “Poor Wal”r.", "플로렌스는 손도 대지 않은 저녁밥을 밀어 내고 소파에 다시 기대고 싶은 마음에, 자기가 그를 실망시켰다고 느끼면서도—그 온갖 수고 끝에 진심으로 그를 기쁘게 해 주고 싶었으나—그에게 손을 내밀었고, 그는 그것을 자기 손에(그 손은 잡으면서 떨렸다) 쥐고는, 저녁 식사며 그녀가 입맛이 없다는 것이며 죄다 까맣게 잊어버린 듯, 곱씹는 듯한 연민의 어조로 이따금 중얼중얼 이어 갔다, \"가엾은 월터."], ["Ay, ay!", "아아!"], ["Drownded. Ain’t he?”", "물에 빠져 죽었지."], ["And always waited for her answer, in which the great point of these singular reflections appeared to consist.", "안 그렇수?\" 그리고 늘 그녀의 대답을 기다렸으니, 이 별난 사색의 큰 요점이 거기에 있는 듯했다."]], [["The fowl and sausages were cold, and the gravy and the egg-sauce stagnant, before the Captain remembered that they were on the board, and fell to with the assistance of Diogenes, whose united efforts quickly dispatched the banquet.", "선장이 닭고기와 소시지가 식탁에 차려져 있다는 것을 떠올려 디오게네스의 도움을 받아 먹어 치우기 시작했을 무렵에는 그것들은 차게 식었고 그레이비와 달걀 소스는 굳어 있었다. 둘이 힘을 합치자 잔치는 금세 끝났다."], ["The Captain’s delight and wonder at the quiet housewifery of Florence in assisting to clear the table, arrange the parlour, and sweep up the hearth—only to be equalled by the fervency of his protest when she began to assist him—were gradually raised to that degree, that at last he could not choose but do nothing himself, and stand looking at her as if she were some Fairy, daintily performing these offices for him; the red rim on his forehead glowing again, in his unspeakable admiration.", "플로렌스가 식탁을 치우고 거실을 정돈하고 난로를 쓸어 내는 일을 거드는 그 조용한 살림 솜씨에 선장이 느낀 기쁨과 경탄은—그녀가 자기를 거들기 시작하자 그가 내뱉은 항의의 열렬함에나 견줄 만한 것이었는데—차츰 그 정도가 높아져, 마침내 그는 자기는 아무것도 하지 못하고, 마치 그녀가 자기를 위해 그 일들을 우아하게 해 주는 어떤 요정이기라도 한 듯 그녀를 바라보며 서 있을 수밖에 없었다. 그의 이마에 난 붉은 자국이 형언할 수 없는 감탄 속에서 다시 벌겋게 달아올랐다."]], [["But when Florence, taking down his pipe from the mantel-shelf gave it into his hand, and entreated him to smoke it, the good Captain was so bewildered by her attention that he held it as if he had never held a pipe, in all his life.", "그러나 플로렌스가 벽난로 선반에서 그의 파이프를 내려 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 피우라고 권하자, 선량한 선장은 그녀의 그 배려에 너무도 어리둥절하여, 마치 평생 파이프라고는 한 번도 쥐어 본 적이 없는 사람처럼 그것을 쥐고 있었다."], ["Likewise, when Florence, looking into the little cupboard, took out the case-bottle and mixed a perfect glass of grog for him, unasked, and set it at his elbow, his ruddy nose turned pale, he felt himself so graced and honoured.", "마찬가지로, 플로렌스가 작은 찬장을 들여다보고 술병을 꺼내 청하지도 않았는데 그를 위해 완벽한 그로그 한 잔을 타서 그의 팔꿈치 곁에 놓아 주자, 그의 불그레한 코가 창백해졌으니, 그토록 큰 영광과 대접을 받았다고 느꼈던 것이다."], ["When he had filled his pipe in an absolute reverie of satisfaction, Florence lighted it for him—the Captain having no power to object, or to prevent her—and resuming her place on the old sofa, looked at him with a smile so loving and so grateful, a smile that showed him so plainly how her forlorn heart turned to him, as her face did, through grief, that the smoke of the pipe got into the Captain’s throat and made him cough, and got into the Captain’s eyes, and made them blink and water.", "그가 완전한 만족의 황홀경에 빠져 파이프에 담배를 채워 넣자 플로렌스가 그를 위해 불을 붙여 주었고—선장은 그것을 거절하거나 막을 힘이 없었다—그러고는 낡은 소파의 제자리로 돌아가 어찌나 다정하고 고마운 미소를 띠며 그를 바라보았던지, 그녀의 쓸쓸한 마음이 그녀의 얼굴이 그렇듯 슬픔을 헤치고 그에게로 향하고 있음을 그토록 또렷이 보여 주는 그 미소에, 파이프 연기가 선장의 목구멍으로 들어가 기침을 하게 만들고, 선장의 눈으로 들어가 눈을 깜박이고 눈물 짓게 만들었다."]], [["The manner in which the Captain tried to make believe that the cause of these effects lay hidden in the pipe itself, and the way in which he looked into the bowl for it, and not finding it there, pretended to blow it out of the stem, was wonderfully pleasant.", "선장이 이 효과의 원인이 파이프 자체에 숨어 있는 척하려고 애쓰던 모습, 그 원인을 찾으려고 파이프 통을 들여다보다가 거기서 찾지 못하자 대를 통해 후 불어 내는 척하던 그 꼴이란 놀라우리만치 흐뭇한 광경이었다. 파이프가 곧 좀 나은 상태가 되자, 그는 능숙한 흡연가에게 어울리는 그 안식의 경지에 빠져들었다."], ["The pipe soon getting into better condition, he fell into that state of repose becoming a good smoker; but sat with his eyes fixed on Florence, and, with a beaming placidity not to be described, and stopping every now and then to discharge a little cloud from his lips, slowly puffed it forth, as if it were a scroll coming out of his mouth, bearing the legend “Poor Wal”r, ay, ay.", "그러나 눈은 플로렌스에게 고정한 채로, 이루 형언할 수 없이 환하게 빛나는 평온함으로, 이따금 멈춰 입술에서 작은 구름을 내뿜으며, 천천히 연기를 뿜어냈으니, 마치 그것이 그의 입에서 흘러나오는 두루마리이고 거기에 \"가엾은 월터, 아아. 물에 빠져 죽었지, 안 그렇수?\"라는 글귀가 적혀 있기라도 한 듯했다."], ["Drownded, ain’t he?” after which he would resume his smoking with infinite gentleness.", "그러고 나서는 더없이 부드럽게 다시 담배를 피우곤 했다."]], [["Unlike as they were externally—and there could scarcely be a more decided contrast than between Florence in her delicate youth and beauty, and Captain Cuttle with his knobby face, his great broad weather-beaten person, and his gruff voice—in simple innocence of the world’s ways and the world’s perplexities and dangers, they were nearly on a level.", "겉모습으로는 그토록 달랐지만—여리고 젊은 아름다움을 지닌 플로렌스와 울퉁불퉁한 얼굴에 풍상에 시달린 크고 떡 벌어진 몸집과 거친 목소리를 지닌 커틀 선장보다 더 뚜렷한 대조도 좀처럼 없었으리라—세상의 방식이며 세상의 곤혹과 위험에 대한 천진난만한 무지에 있어서는 그 둘이 거의 같은 수준이었다."], ["No child could have surpassed Captain Cuttle in inexperience of everything but wind and weather; in simplicity, credulity, and generous trustfulness.", "바람과 날씨를 빼고는 모든 일에서의 무경험에 있어, 그 순박함과 잘 믿는 마음과 너그럽게 신뢰하는 마음에 있어, 어떤 아이도 커틀 선장을 능가할 수 없었으리라."], ["Faith, hope, and charity, shared his whole nature among them.", "믿음과 소망과 사랑이 그의 천성 전체를 나누어 차지하고 있었다."], ["An odd sort of romance, perfectly unimaginative, yet perfectly unreal, and subject to no considerations of worldly prudence or practicability, was the only partner they had in his character.", "한 가지 기묘한 종류의 낭만, 도무지 상상력이라곤 없으면서도 완벽하게 비현실적이고, 세속적인 분별이나 실행 가능성 따위는 전혀 고려하지 않는 그 낭만만이 그의 성품 속에서 그것들과 동무하는 유일한 것이었다."], ["As the Captain sat, and smoked, and looked at Florence, God knows what impossible pictures, in which she was the principal figure, presented themselves to his mind.", "선장이 앉아 담배를 피우며 플로렌스를 바라보는 동안, 그녀가 주인공으로 등장하는 어떤 있을 수 없는 그림들이 그의 마음속에 떠올랐는지는 하느님만 아실 일이었다."], ["Equally vague and uncertain, though not so sanguine, were her own thoughts of the life before her; and even as her tears made prismatic colours in the light she gazed at, so, through her new and heavy grief, she already saw a rainbow faintly shining in the far-off sky.", "그녀 자신의, 앞에 놓인 삶에 대한 생각들도 그만큼 막연하고 불확실했으나, 그처럼 낙관적이지는 않았다. 그리고 그녀의 눈물이 그녀가 바라보는 빛 속에서 프리즘의 빛깔을 자아내듯이, 그녀는 그 새롭고 무거운 슬픔을 헤치고서, 이미 저 먼 하늘에 희미하게 빛나는 무지개를 보고 있었다."], ["A wandering princess and a good monster in a storybook might have sat by the fireside, and talked as Captain Cuttle and poor Florence talked—and not have looked very much unlike them.", "어느 이야기책 속 떠도는 공주와 착한 괴물이 난롯가에 앉아, 커틀 선장과 가엾은 플로렌스가 나눈 것처럼 이야기를 나눈다 해도—그 둘과 그리 달라 보이지는 않았을 것이다."]], [["The Captain was not troubled with the faintest idea of any difficulty in retaining Florence, or of any responsibility thereby incurred.", "선장은 플로렌스를 곁에 둔다는 것에 무슨 곤란이 있다거나, 그로 인해 어떤 책임이 따른다는 생각 따위로는 조금도 마음 졸이지 않았다."], ["Having put up the shutters and locked the door, he was quite satisfied on this head.", "덧문을 올려 닫고 문을 잠그고 나니, 그 점에 대해서는 아주 흡족했다."], ["If she had been a Ward in Chancery, it would have made no difference at all to Captain Cuttle.", "설령 그녀가 형평법 법원의 피후견인이었다 한들 커틀 선장에게는 아무런 차이가 없었을 것이다."], ["He was the last man in the world to be troubled by any such considerations.", "그는 세상에서 그런 따위 고려로 마음 졸일 사람과는 가장 거리가 먼 사내였다."]], [["So the Captain smoked his pipe very comfortably, and Florence and he meditated after their own manner.", "그리하여 선장은 파이프를 아주 편안하게 피웠고, 플로렌스와 그는 저마다의 방식으로 사색에 잠겼다. 파이프가 다 타자 둘은 차를 좀 마셨다."], ["When the pipe was out, they had some tea; and then Florence entreated him to take her to some neighbouring shop, where she could buy the few necessaries she immediately wanted.", "그러고 나서 플로렌스는 그에게 자기가 당장 필요한 몇 가지 물건을 살 수 있는 이웃 가게로 데려다 달라고 청했다. 이미 날이 꽤 어두웠으므로 선장은 승낙했다."], ["It being quite dark, the Captain consented: peeping carefully out first, as he had been wont to do in his time of hiding from Mrs MacStinger; and arming himself with his large stick, in case of an appeal to arms being rendered necessary by any unforeseen circumstance.", "다만 먼저 조심스레 밖을 내다보았으니, 그가 맥스팅거 부인에게서 숨어 지내던 시절에 늘 하던 버릇이었다. 그리고 어떤 예기치 못한 상황으로 무력에 호소할 필요가 생길 경우에 대비해 자기의 큼직한 지팡이로 무장했다."]], [["The pride Captain Cuttle had, in giving his arm to Florence, and escorting her some two or three hundred yards, keeping a bright look-out all the time, and attracting the attention of everyone who passed them, by his great vigilance and numerous precautions, was extreme.", "커틀 선장이 플로렌스에게 자기 팔을 내어 주고, 이삼백 야드쯤 그녀를 호위하며, 내내 환히 경계의 눈초리를 늦추지 않고, 그 대단한 조심성과 수많은 예방책으로 지나가는 모든 이의 이목을 끌면서 느낀 그 자부심은 이루 말할 수 없었다."], ["Arrived at the shop, the Captain felt it a point of delicacy to retire during the making of the purchases, as they were to consist of wearing apparel; but he previously deposited his tin canister on the counter, and informing the young lady of the establishment that it contained fourteen pound two, requested her, in case that amount of property should not be sufficient to defray the expenses of his niece’s little outfit—at the word “niece,”", "가게에 다다르자, 선장은 사들일 물건이 입을 옷가지인 만큼, 그 구매가 이루어지는 동안 자리를 비켜 주는 것이 섬세한 도리라고 여겼다. 그러나 그 전에 그는 양철통을 계산대 위에 올려놓고, 가게의 젊은 아가씨에게 그 안에 십사 파운드 이 실링이 들어 있노라고 알려 주며, 만일 그 액수로 자기 조카딸의 자그마한 차림새 비용을 치르기에 모자라거든—\"조카딸\"이라는 말에서 그는 더없이 의미심장한 눈길을 플로렌스에게 던지고, 거기에 영리함과 비밀스러움을 담은 손짓을 곁들였다—부디 친절히 \"크게 외쳐\""], ["he bestowed a most significant look on Florence, accompanied with pantomime, expressive of sagacity and mystery—to have the goodness to “sing out,”", "알려 달라고, 그러면 자기 주머니에서 부족한 만큼을 채우겠노라고 청했다."], ["and he would make up the difference from his pocket. Casually consulting his big watch, as a deep means of dazzling the establishment, and impressing it with a sense of property, the Captain then kissed his hook to his niece, and retired outside the window, where it was a choice sight to see his great face looking in from time to time, among the silks and ribbons, with an obvious misgiving that Florence had been spirited away by a back door.", "그러고는 가게에 깊은 인상을 심고 재력을 과시하는 한 수로, 짐짓 자기의 큼직한 시계를 들여다보고서, 선장은 갈고리에 입을 맞춰 조카딸에게 인사하고 창밖으로 물러났는데, 거기서 그의 큰 얼굴이 이따금 비단과 리본 사이로 안을 들여다보는 모습이 볼 만한 구경거리였으니, 플로렌스가 뒷문으로 감쪽같이 채여 가 버린 게 아닐까 하는 역력한 근심이 어려 있었다."]], [["“Dear Captain Cuttle,”", "\"고마우신 커틀 선장님,\""], ["said Florence, when she came out with a parcel, the size of which greatly disappointed the Captain, who had expected to see a porter following with a bale of goods, “I don’t want this money, indeed.", "플로렌스가 꾸러미 하나를 들고 나오며 말했다. 그 크기가 어찌나 작던지 일꾼 하나가 짐짝 한 더미를 따라 들고 나오리라 기대했던 선장은 크게 실망했다."], ["I have not spent any of it.", "\"저는 이 돈 정말 필요 없어요. 한 푼도 안 썼답니다."], ["I have money of my own.”", "저한테 제 돈이 있는걸요.\""]], [["“My lady lass,”", "\"아가씨,\""], ["returned the baffled Captain, looking straight down the street before them, “take care on it for me, will you be so good, till such time as I ask ye for it?”", "당황한 선장이 둘 앞으로 곧게 뻗은 거리를 똑바로 바라보며 대꾸했다, \"이 늙은이 대신 그걸 좀 맡아 주겠수, 그렇게 좀 해 주시구려, 내가 그걸 달라고 할 때까지 말이우?\""]], [["“May I put it back in its usual place,”", "\"제가 늘 두던 자리에 도로 넣어 두고,\""], ["said Florence, “and keep it there?”", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"거기 보관하면 될까요?\""]], [["The Captain was not at all gratified by this proposal, but he answered, “Ay, ay, put it anywheres, my lady lass, so long as you know where to find it again.", "선장은 이 제안에 조금도 흡족해하지 않았으나, 이렇게 대답했다, \"그래, 그래, 아무 데나 두구려, 아가씨, 다시 어디서 찾을지만 알고 있다면 말이우."], ["It ain’t o’ no use to me,”", "그건 나한테는 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없수,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“I wonder I haven’t chucked it away afore now.", "\"내가 진작 그걸 안 내던진 게 이상할 정도라우.\""]], [["The Captain was quite disheartened for the moment, but he revived at the first touch of Florence’s arm, and they returned with the same precautions as they had come; the Captain opening the door of the little Midshipman’s berth, and diving in, with a suddenness which his great practice only could have taught him.", "선장은 그 순간 적잖이 풀이 죽었으나, 플로렌스의 팔이 닿자마자 다시 기운을 되찾았고, 둘은 올 때와 똑같은 예방책을 갖추고 돌아왔다. 선장은 작은 미드십맨 가게의 문을 열고, 오랜 숙련만이 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 그 민첩함으로 안으로 쑥 들어갔다."], ["During Florence’s slumber in the morning, he had engaged the daughter of an elderly lady who usually sat under a blue umbrella in Leadenhall Market, selling poultry, to come and put her room in order, and render her any little services she required; and this damsel now appearing, Florence found everything about her as convenient and orderly, if not as handsome, as in the terrible dream she had once called Home.", "아침에 플로렌스가 잠들어 있는 동안, 그는 리든홀 시장에서 으레 파란 우산 아래 앉아 닭을 파는 어느 나이 든 부인의 딸을 고용해, 와서 그녀의 방을 정돈하고 그녀가 필요로 하는 자잘한 시중을 들어 주게 해 두었다. 이제 이 처녀가 모습을 나타내자, 플로렌스는 자기 주변의 모든 것이, 비록 한때 자기가 집이라 불렀던 그 끔찍한 꿈에서만큼 멋지지는 않을지언정, 그만큼 편리하고 정돈되어 있음을 알았다."]], [["When they were alone again, the Captain insisted on her eating a slice of dry toast, and drinking a glass of spiced negus (which he made to perfection); and, encouraging her with every kind word and inconsequential quotation he could possibly think of, led her upstairs to her bedroom.", "다시 둘만 남게 되자, 선장은 그녀에게 마른 토스트 한 조각을 먹고 향료 넣은 니거스 한 잔(그가 완벽하게 만들어 낸 것이었다)을 마셔야 한다고 우겼다. 그리고 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 다정한 말과 앞뒤도 안 맞는 인용구로 그녀를 북돋우며, 위층 침실로 데려다주었다."], ["But he too had something on his mind, and was not easy in his manner.", "그러나 그 역시 마음에 무언가 걸리는 것이 있어, 태도가 편치 못했다."]], [["“Good-night, dear heart,”", "\"잘 자우, 소중한 아가씨,\""], ["said Captain Cuttle to her at her chamber-door.", "커틀 선장이 그녀의 방문 앞에서 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["Florence raised her lips to his face, and kissed him.", "플로렌스는 그의 얼굴로 입술을 들어 올려, 그에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["At any other time the Captain would have been overbalanced by such a token of her affection and gratitude; but now, although he was very sensible of it, he looked in her face with even more uneasiness than he had testified before, and seemed unwilling to leave her.", "다른 때였다면 선장은 그녀의 그런 애정과 감사의 표시에 그만 마음의 평정을 잃었을 것이다. 그러나 지금은, 그것을 매우 절절히 느끼면서도, 앞서 드러냈던 것보다 한층 더 불안한 빛으로 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 그녀를 떠나기를 꺼리는 듯했다."]], [["“Poor Wal”r!”", "\"가엾은 월터!\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Poor, poor Walter!”", "\"가엾은, 가엾은 월터!\""], ["sighed Florence.", "플로렌스가 한숨지었다."]], [["“Drownded, ain’t he?”", "\"물에 빠져 죽었지, 안 그렇수?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["Florence shook her head, and sighed.", "플로렌스는 고개를 가로젓고 한숨지었다."]], [["“Good-night, my lady lass!”", "\"잘 자우, 아가씨!\""], ["said Captain Cuttle, putting out his hand.", "커틀 선장이 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“God bless you, dear, kind friend!”", "\"하느님의 가호가 있으시길, 그립고 친절하신 친구분!\""]], [["But the Captain lingered still.", "그러나 선장은 여전히 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“Is anything the matter, dear Captain Cuttle?”", "\"무슨 일 있으세요, 고마우신 커틀 선장님?\""], ["said Florence, easily alarmed in her then state of mind.", "플로렌스가, 그때의 심정으로는 쉬이 놀라며 말했다."], ["“Have you anything to tell me?”", "\"제게 하실 말씀이라도 있으세요?\""]], [["“To tell you, lady lass!”", "\"아가씨한테 할 말이라니!\""], ["replied the Captain, meeting her eyes in confusion.", "선장이 당황하여 그녀의 눈길을 마주하며 대답했다. \"아니, 아니우."], ["“No, no; what should I have to tell you, pretty!", "내가 무슨 아가씨한테 할 말이 있겠수, 예쁜 아가씨!"], ["You don’t expect as I’ve got anything good to tell you, sure?”", "설마 내가 무슨 좋은 소식을 들려줄 게 있으리라 기대하는 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["“No!”", "\"아니에요!\""], ["said Florence, shaking her head.", "플로렌스가 고개를 가로저으며 말했다."]], [["The Captain looked at her wistfully, and repeated “No,”— still lingering, and still showing embarrassment.", "선장은 안타까운 듯 그녀를 바라보고는 \"아니우\"라고 되풀이했다—여전히 머뭇거리며, 여전히 어쩔 줄 몰라 하면서."]], [["“Poor Wal”r!”", "\"가엾은 월터!\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“My Wal”r, as I used to call you!", "\"내 월터, 내가 너를 그렇게 부르곤 했지!"], ["Old Sol Gills’s nevy!", "늙은 솔 길스의 조카!"], ["Welcome to all as knowed you, as the flowers in May!", "너를 알던 모두에게 오월의 꽃처럼 반가운 녀석!"], ["Where are you got to, brave boy?", "어디로 가 버렸느냐, 용감한 녀석아?"], ["Drownded, ain’t he?”", "물에 빠져 죽었지, 안 그렇수?\""]], [["Concluding his apostrophe with this abrupt appeal to Florence, the Captain bade her good-night, and descended the stairs, while Florence remained at the top, holding the candle out to light him down.", "이 돈호의 끝을 플로렌스에게 던지는 이 느닷없는 물음으로 맺고, 선장은 그녀에게 잘 자라고 인사하고는 계단을 내려갔다. 그동안 플로렌스는 계단 꼭대기에 남아, 그가 내려가는 길을 비추려고 촛불을 내밀고 있었다."], ["He was lost in the obscurity, and, judging from the sound of his receding footsteps, was in the act of turning into the little parlour, when his head and shoulders unexpectedly emerged again, as from the deep, apparently for no other purpose than to repeat, “Drownded, ain’t he, pretty?”", "그는 어둠 속으로 사라졌고, 멀어지는 발소리로 미루어 보아 작은 거실로 막 접어드는 참이었는데, 그때 그의 머리와 어깨가 마치 깊은 물속에서처럼 뜻밖에 다시 불쑥 솟아올랐으니, 보아하니 \"물에 빠져 죽었지, 안 그렇수, 예쁜 아가씨?\"라는 말을 되풀이하는 것 말고는 다른 목적이라곤 없는 듯했다."], ["For when he had said that in a tone of tender condolence, he disappeared.", "다정한 위로의 어조로 그 말을 하고 나서 그는 사라졌다."]], [["Florence was very sorry that she should unwittingly, though naturally, have awakened these associations in the mind of her protector, by taking refuge there; and sitting down before the little table where the Captain had arranged the telescope and song-book, and those other rarities, thought of Walter, and of all that was connected with him in the past, until she could have almost wished to lie down on her bed and fade away.", "플로렌스는 자기가 그곳으로 피신함으로써, 비록 자연스러운 일이긴 했어도 무심코 자기 보호자의 마음속에 이런 연상들을 일깨워 버린 것이 무척 미안했다. 그래서 선장이 망원경과 노래책과 그 밖의 진귀한 물건들을 늘어놓은 작은 탁자 앞에 앉아, 월터와 지난날 그와 얽힌 모든 일을 떠올리다가, 거의 침대에 누워 그대로 스러져 사라지고 싶다는 마음마저 들 지경이었다."], ["But in her lonely yearning to the dead whom she had loved, no thought of home—no possibility of going back—no presentation of it as yet existing, or as sheltering her father—once entered her thoughts.", "그러나 자기가 사랑했던 죽은 이를 향한 그 외로운 그리움 속에서도, 집에 대한 생각은—돌아갈 가능성도—아직 그런 곳이 존재한다거나 자기 아버지를 품어 줄 곳이라는 떠올림도—단 한 번도 그녀의 마음속에 끼어들지 않았다."], ["She had seen the murder done.", "그녀는 그 살해가 저질러지는 것을 보았다."], ["In the last lingering natural aspect in which she had cherished him through so much, he had been torn out of her heart, defaced, and slain.", "그토록 많은 것을 견디며 그를 소중히 품어 온 그 마지막 남은 자연스러운 모습 속에서, 그는 그녀의 마음에서 뜯겨 나가고, 짓이겨지고, 살해되었다."], ["The thought of it was so appalling to her, that she covered her eyes, and shrunk trembling from the least remembrance of the deed, or of the cruel hand that did it.", "그 생각이 너무도 끔찍하여, 그녀는 두 눈을 가리고, 그 행위에 대한, 혹은 그 짓을 저지른 잔인한 손에 대한 가장 작은 기억으로부터도 부르르 떨며 움츠러들었다. 만일 그녀의 사랑하는 마음이 그 일 이후에도 그의 모습을 간직할 수 있었다면, 그 마음은 산산이 부서졌을 것이다."], ["If her fond heart could have held his image after that, it must have broken; but it could not; and the void was filled with a wild dread that fled from all confronting with its shattered fragments—with such a dread as could have risen out of nothing but the depths of such a love, so wronged.", "그러나 그럴 수가 없었다. 그리고 그 빈자리는, 자기의 부서진 조각들과 마주하는 모든 것으로부터 달아나는 사나운 두려움으로 채워졌으니—그토록 깊고, 그토록 짓밟힌 사랑의 심연이 아니고서는 결코 솟아날 수 없었을 그런 두려움이었다."]], [["She dared not look into the glass; for the sight of the darkening mark upon her bosom made her afraid of herself, as if she bore about her something wicked.", "그녀는 거울을 들여다볼 엄두가 나지 않았다. 가슴 위의 거무스름해져 가는 자국을 보면, 마치 자기가 무언가 사악한 것을 몸에 지니고 다니기라도 하는 듯 자기 자신이 두려워졌기 때문이다."], ["She covered it up, with a hasty, faltering hand, and in the dark; and laid her weary head down, weeping.", "그녀는 황급하고 떨리는 손으로, 그것도 어둠 속에서 그것을 가렸다. 그러고는 지친 머리를 누이고, 울었다."]], [["The Captain did not go to bed for a long time.", "선장은 오랫동안 잠자리에 들지 않았다."], ["He walked to and fro in the shop and in the little parlour, for a full hour, and, appearing to have composed himself by that exercise, sat down with a grave and thoughtful face, and read out of a Prayer-book the forms of prayer appointed to be used at sea.", "그는 꼬박 한 시간을 가게와 작은 거실을 오락가락 거닐었고, 그 운동으로 마음을 가다듬은 듯, 진지하고 사려 깊은 얼굴로 앉아, 기도서에서 바다에서 쓰도록 정해진 기도문들을 소리 내어 읽었다."], ["These were not easily disposed of; the good Captain being a mighty slow, gruff reader, and frequently stopping at a hard word to give himself such encouragement as “Now, my lad!", "이것들은 쉽게 처리되지 않았으니, 선량한 선장이 대단히 더디고 거친 낭독자인 데다, 어려운 단어에서 자주 멈추어, \"자, 이놈아!"], ["With a will!” or, “Steady, Ed’ard Cuttle, steady!”", "기운차게!\"라거나 \"침착해, 에드워드 커틀, 침착해!\""], ["which had a great effect in helping him out of any difficulty.", "따위로 스스로를 북돋우곤 했는데, 그것이 어떤 난관에서 벗어나는 데 큰 효력이 있었다."], ["Moreover, his spectacles greatly interfered with his powers of vision.", "게다가 그의 안경이 시력을 크게 방해했다."], ["But notwithstanding these drawbacks, the Captain, being heartily in earnest, read the service to the very last line, and with genuine feeling too; and approving of it very much when he had done, turned in, under the counter (but not before he had been upstairs, and listened at Florence’s door), with a serene breast, and a most benevolent visage.", "그러나 이런 장애에도 불구하고, 선장은 진심으로 골똘했던지라 그 예배문을 맨 마지막 줄까지, 그것도 진정한 감정을 담아 읽었다. 그리고 다 읽고 나서 그것을 무척 흡족해하며, 평온한 가슴과 더없이 자애로운 얼굴로 계산대 밑으로 들어가 잠자리에 들었다(다만 그 전에 위층으로 올라가 플로렌스의 방문 앞에서 귀를 기울였다)."]], [["The Captain turned out several times in the course of the night, to assure himself that his charge was resting quietly; and once, at daybreak, found that she was awake: for she called to know if it were he, on hearing footsteps near her door.", "선장은 밤사이 여러 번 일어나, 자기가 맡은 아가씨가 조용히 쉬고 있는지 확인했다. 그리고 한 번은, 동틀 무렵에, 그녀가 깨어 있는 것을 알게 되었으니, 그녀가 자기 방문 가까이에서 발소리를 듣고는 그게 그인지 알려고 불렀던 것이다."]], [["“Yes, my lady lass,”", "\"그렇수, 아가씨,\""], ["replied the Captain, in a growling whisper.", "선장이 으르렁대는 듯한 속삭임으로 대답했다."], ["“Are you all right, di’mond?”", "\"다 괜찮수, 다이아몬드 같은 아가씨?\""]], [["Florence thanked him, and said “Yes.”", "플로렌스는 그에게 고맙다고 하고 \"네\"라고 말했다."]], [["The Captain could not lose so favourable an opportunity of applying his mouth to the keyhole, and calling through it, like a hoarse breeze, “Poor Wal”r!", "선장은 이토록 좋은 기회를 놓칠 수 없어, 입을 열쇠 구멍에 대고는 쉰 듯한 미풍처럼 그 사이로 외쳤다, \"가엾은 월터!"], ["Drownded, ain’t he?”", "물에 빠져 죽었지, 안 그렇수?\""], ["after which he withdrew, and turning in again, slept till seven o’clock.", "그러고 나서 그는 물러나, 다시 잠자리에 들어 일곱 시까지 잤다."]], [["Nor was he free from his uneasy and embarrassed manner all that day; though Florence, being busy with her needle in the little parlour, was more calm and tranquil than she had been on the day preceding.", "그날 하루 종일 그는 그 불안하고 어쩔 줄 모르는 태도에서 벗어나지 못했다. 다만 플로렌스는 작은 거실에서 바느질에 여념이 없어, 전날보다는 한결 차분하고 평온했다."], ["Almost always when she raised her eyes from her work, she observed the captain looking at her, and thoughtfully stroking his chin; and he so often hitched his arm-chair close to her, as if he were going to say something very confidential, and hitched it away again, as not being able to make up his mind how to begin, that in the course of the day he cruised completely round the parlour in that frail bark, and more than once went ashore against the wainscot or the closet door, in a very distressed condition.", "그녀가 일감에서 눈을 들 때면 거의 언제나, 선장이 자기를 바라보며 사려 깊게 턱을 쓰다듬고 있는 것이 눈에 띄었다. 그리고 그는 무언가 매우 은밀한 이야기라도 하려는 듯 안락의자를 그녀에게 바싹 끌어당겼다가, 어떻게 말문을 떼야 할지 마음을 정하지 못하겠다는 듯 다시 끌어다 멀어지기를 어찌나 자주 했던지, 하루가 지나는 동안 그는 그 위태로운 작은 배를 타고 거실을 완전히 한 바퀴 돌았으며, 매우 곤란한 처지에 빠진 채 한 번 이상이나 징두리 판자나 벽장 문에 부딪쳐 좌초하기도 했다."]], [["It was not until the twilight that Captain Cuttle, fairly dropping anchor, at last, by the side of Florence, began to talk at all connectedly.", "마침내 커틀 선장이 플로렌스 곁에 닻을 제대로 내리고 조금이라도 조리 있게 이야기하기 시작한 것은 땅거미가 질 무렵이 되어서였다."], ["But when the light of the fire was shining on the walls and ceiling of the little room, and on the tea-board and the cups and saucers that were ranged upon the table, and on her calm face turned towards the flame, and reflecting it in the tears that filled her eyes, the Captain broke a long silence thus:", "그러나 난롯불 빛이 그 작은 방의 벽과 천장에, 그리고 탁자 위에 늘어놓은 찻쟁반과 찻잔과 받침에, 그리고 불꽃을 향해 돌린 그녀의 고요한 얼굴에 어리어, 그 빛이 그녀의 눈에 그렁그렁한 눈물에 비치고 있을 때, 선장은 긴 침묵을 이렇게 깨뜨렸다."]], [["“You never was at sea, my own?” “No,” replied Florence.", "\"아가씨는 바다에 나가 본 적이 한 번도 없지요, 우리 아가씨?\""]], [["“Ay,”", "\"네,\""], ["said the Captain, reverentially; “it’s a almighty element.", "플로렌스가 대답했다. \"아아,\""], ["There’s wonders in the deep, my pretty.", "선장이 경건하게 말했다, \"그건 전능하신 원소라우."], ["Think on it when the winds is roaring and the waves is rowling.", "깊은 바다에는 경이가 가득하다우, 예쁜 아가씨. 바람이 울부짖고 파도가 굽이칠 때 그걸 떠올려 보구려."], ["Think on it when the stormy nights is so pitch dark,”", "폭풍우 치는 밤이 어찌나 캄캄한지,\""], ["said the Captain, solemnly holding up his hook, “as you can’t see your hand afore you, excepting when the wiwid lightning reweals the same; and when you drive, drive, drive through the storm and dark, as if you was a driving, head on, to the world without end, evermore, amen, and when found making a note of.", "선장이 엄숙하게 갈고리를 치켜들며 말했다, \"번쩍이는 번개가 그걸 드러내 줄 때 말고는 눈앞에 든 제 손도 안 보일 지경일 때 그걸 떠올려 보구려. 그리고 폭풍과 어둠을 뚫고 달리고, 달리고, 또 달릴 때, 마치 끝없는 세상을 향해 곧장 머리부터 들이박으며 달려가는 것 같을 때, 영원무궁토록, 아멘, 그리고 발견될 때는 그걸 기록으로 남겨 둘지어다."], ["Them’s the times, my beauty, when a man may say to his messmate (previously a overhauling of the wollume), ‘A stiff nor’wester’s blowing, Bill; hark, don’t you hear it roar now!", "바로 그런 때라우, 우리 아름다운 아가씨, 사내가 (먼저 책을 한번 죽 훑어보고서) 자기 동료한테 이렇게 말할 만한 때가 말이우. '북서풍이 거세게 부는구나, 빌."], ["Lord help ’em, how I pitys all unhappy folks ashore now!’”", "들어 봐, 지금 저 으르렁대는 소리 안 들리나!"], ["Which quotation, as particularly applicable to the terrors of the ocean, the Captain delivered in a most impressive manner, concluding with a sonorous “Stand by!”", "주여 저들을 도우소서, 지금 뭍에 있는 가엾은 이들이 다 어찌나 측은한지!'\" 바다의 공포에 특히 들어맞는 이 인용구를 선장은 더없이 인상 깊은 태도로 읊었고, 우렁찬 \"대기하라!\"로 끝맺었다."]], [["“Were you ever in a dreadful storm?”", "\"무서운 폭풍을 겪어 보신 적 있으세요?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“Why ay, my lady lass, I’ve seen my share of bad weather,”", "\"아 그럼이지, 아가씨, 나도 궂은 날씨라면 겪을 만큼 겪었수,\""], ["said the Captain, tremulously wiping his head, “and I’ve had my share of knocking about; but—but it ain’t of myself as I was a meaning to speak.", "선장이 떨리는 손으로 머리를 훔치며 말했다, \"그리고 험한 일도 당할 만큼 당했고. 하지만—하지만 내가 말하려던 건 내 얘기가 아니라우."], ["Our dear boy,”", "우리 사랑하는 녀석,\""], ["drawing closer to her, “Wal”r, darling, as was drownded.”", "그녀에게 더 가까이 다가서며, \"월터, 그 귀한 녀석, 물에 빠져 죽은.\""]], [["The Captain spoke in such a trembling voice, and looked at Florence with a face so pale and agitated, that she clung to his hand in affright.", "선장이 어찌나 떨리는 목소리로 말하고, 어찌나 창백하고 동요된 얼굴로 플로렌스를 바라보았던지, 그녀는 겁에 질려 그의 손에 매달렸다."]], [["“Your face is changed,”", "\"얼굴이 달라지셨어요,\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."], ["“You are altered in a moment.", "\"한순간에 변하셨어요."], ["What is it?", "무슨 일이세요?"], ["Dear Captain Cuttle, it turns me cold to see you!”", "고마우신 커틀 선장님, 선장님을 보니 등골이 서늘해져요!\""]], [["“What!", "\"아니!"], ["Lady lass,”", "아가씨,\""], ["returned the Captain, supporting her with his hand, “don’t be took aback.", "선장이 손으로 그녀를 부축하며 대답했다, \"당황하지 마우. 아니, 아니우!"], ["No, no! All’s well, all’s well, my dear.", "다 괜찮수, 다 괜찮수, 우리 아가씨."], ["As I was a saying—Wal”r—he’s—he’s drownded.", "내가 말하던 대로—월터—그 애가—그 애가 물에 빠져 죽었지."], ["Ain’t he?”", "안 그렇수?\""]], [["Florence looked at him intently; her colour came and went; and she laid her hand upon her breast.", "플로렌스는 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 핏기가 들었다 가셨다 했고, 그녀는 한 손을 가슴에 얹었다."]], [["“There’s perils and dangers on the deep, my beauty,”", "\"깊은 바다에는 위험과 위난이 도사리고 있다우, 우리 아름다운 아가씨,\""], ["said the Captain; “and over many a brave ship, and many and many a bould heart, the secret waters has closed up, and never told no tales.", "선장이 말했다. \"그리고 수많은 용감한 배와, 수많고 수많은 대담한 가슴 위로, 그 은밀한 바닷물이 덮어 버리고는, 아무 이야기도 들려준 적이 없다우."], ["But there’s escapes upon the deep, too, and sometimes one man out of a score,—ah!", "하지만 깊은 바다에는 살아남는 일도 있는 법이라우, 그래서 때로는 스무 명 가운데 한 사람이—아!"], ["maybe out of a hundred, pretty,—has been saved by the mercy of God, and come home after being given over for dead, and told of all hands lost.", "어쩌면 백 명 가운데 한 사람이, 예쁜 아가씨—하느님의 자비로 구원을 받아, 죽은 것으로 단념된 끝에 집으로 돌아와, 모두가 죽었더라는 이야기를 전하기도 한다우."], ["I—I know a story, Heart’s Delight,”", "내가—내가 이런 종류의 이야기를 하나 안다우, 내 마음의 기쁨인 아가씨,\""], ["stammered the Captain, “o’ this natur, as was told to me once; and being on this here tack, and you and me sitting alone by the fire, maybe you’d like to hear me tell it.", "선장이 더듬거리며 말했다, \"언젠가 내가 들은 이야기지. 그리고 이런 화제로 접어든 김에, 아가씨하고 나하고 난롯가에 단둘이 앉아 있으니, 어쩌면 내가 그 이야기를 들려주는 걸 듣고 싶을지도 모르겠수."], ["Would you, deary?”", "그러겠수, 아가씨?\""]], [["Florence, trembling with an agitation which she could not control or understand, involuntarily followed his glance, which went behind her into the shop, where a lamp was burning.", "플로렌스는 자기로서도 억누르거나 이해할 수 없는 동요에 떨면서, 자기도 모르게 그의 눈길을 따라갔다. 그 눈길은 그녀의 등 뒤로, 등불이 켜져 있는 가게 쪽을 향했다."], ["The instant that she turned her head, the Captain sprung out of his chair, and interposed his hand.", "그녀가 머리를 돌리는 순간, 선장이 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 손을 내밀어 가로막았다."]], [["“There’s nothing there, my beauty,”", "\"저기엔 아무것도 없수, 우리 아름다운 아가씨,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Don’t look there.”", "\"저쪽은 보지 마우.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 안 되나요?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["The Captain murmured something about its being dull that way, and about the fire being cheerful.", "선장은 저쪽은 침침하다느니, 난롯불이 더 정겹다느니 하고 무어라 중얼거렸다."], ["He drew the door ajar, which had been standing open until now, and resumed his seat.", "그는 지금까지 열려 있던 문을 빠끔히 닫고, 다시 자리에 앉았다."], ["Florence followed him with her eyes, and looked intently in his face.", "플로렌스는 눈으로 그를 좇으며, 그의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“The story was about a ship, my lady lass,”", "\"그 이야기는 어떤 배에 관한 것이었수, 아가씨,\""], ["began the Captain, “as sailed out of the Port of London, with a fair wind and in fair weather, bound for—don’t be took aback, my lady lass, she was only out’ard bound, pretty, only out’ard bound!”", "선장이 입을 뗐다, \"런던 항을 떠나, 순풍을 받고 좋은 날씨에, 어디로 향하던 배냐면—당황하지 마우, 아가씨, 그 배는 그저 바깥으로 나가는 길이었수, 예쁜 아가씨, 그저 바깥으로 나가는 길이었다우!\""]], [["The expression on Florence’s face alarmed the Captain, who was himself very hot and flurried, and showed scarcely less agitation than she did.", "플로렌스의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정이 선장을 불안하게 했다. 선장 자신도 무척 달아오르고 허둥대어, 그녀 못지않은 동요를 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“Shall I go on, Beauty?”", "\"계속할까요, 아름다운 아가씨?\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes, pray!”", "\"네, 네, 어서요!\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."]], [["The Captain made a gulp as if to get down something that was sticking in his throat, and nervously proceeded:", "선장은 목구멍에 걸린 무언가를 삼키기라도 하려는 듯 꿀꺽 침을 삼키고, 초조하게 이어 갔다."]], [["“That there unfort’nate ship met with such foul weather, out at sea, as don’t blow once in twenty year, my darling.", "\"그 불운한 배는 바다 한복판에서 그런 험악한 날씨를 만났는데, 우리 사랑하는 아가씨, 그런 날씨는 이십 년에 한 번도 불지 않는 거라우."], ["There was hurricanes ashore as tore up forests and blowed down towns, and there was gales at sea in them latitudes, as not the stoutest wessel ever launched could live in.", "뭍에서는 숲을 뿌리째 뽑고 마을을 쓰러뜨리는 폭풍이 일었고, 그 위도의 바다에서는 여태껏 진수된 그 어떤 튼튼한 배도 살아남지 못할 강풍이 일었다우. 날이면 날마다 그 불운한 배는 장하게도 버티며 용감히 제 도리를 다했다고 들었수, 예쁜 아가씨."], ["Day arter day that there unfort’nate ship behaved noble, I’m told, and did her duty brave, my pretty, but at one blow a’most her bulwarks was stove in, her masts and rudder carved away, her best man swept overboard, and she left to the mercy of the storm as had no mercy but blowed harder and harder yet, while the waves dashed over her, and beat her in, and every time they come a thundering at her, broke her like a shell.", "하지만 거의 한 방에 뱃전이 부서지고, 돛대와 키가 깎여 나가고, 으뜸가는 일꾼이 바다로 휩쓸려 가고, 자비라곤 없이 점점 더 거세게 불어 대는 폭풍의 처분에 내맡겨졌다우. 그동안 파도는 배 위로 들이치고, 배를 두들겨 부수고, 천둥 치듯 들이닥칠 때마다 조개껍데기처럼 배를 박살 냈지."], ["Every black spot in every mountain of water that rolled away was a bit o’ the ship’s life or a living man, and so she went to pieces, Beauty, and no grass will never grow upon the graves of them as manned that ship.”", "굽이쳐 밀려가는 산더미 같은 물마다 그 위의 검은 점 하나하나가 그 배의 목숨 한 조각이거나 살아 있는 사람 하나였다우. 그렇게 배는 산산조각이 났수, 아름다운 아가씨, 그 배에 탔던 이들의 무덤 위에는 결코 풀 한 포기 돋지 않을 거라우.\""]], [["“They were not all lost!”", "\"모두 죽은 건 아니었죠!\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."], ["“Some were saved!—Was one?”", "\"몇 사람은 구조됐죠!—한 사람이라도요?\""]], [["“Aboard o’ that there unfort’nate wessel,”", "\"그 불운한 배에는,\""], ["said the Captain, rising from his chair, and clenching his hand with prodigious energy and exultation, “was a lad, a gallant lad—as I’ve heerd tell—that had loved, when he was a boy, to read and talk about brave actions in shipwrecks—I’ve heerd him!", "선장이 의자에서 일어나, 엄청난 기운과 환희로 주먹을 불끈 쥐며 말했다, \"한 젊은이가 있었다우, 늠름한 젊은이가—내가 전해 듣기로는—소년 시절에 난파선에서의 용감한 행적에 관해 읽고 이야기하기를 좋아하던—내가 직접 들었수!"], ["I’ve heerd him!—and he remembered of ’em in his hour of need; for when the stoutest and oldest hands was hove down, he was firm and cheery.", "내가 직접 들었다니까!—그래서 위기의 순간에 그걸 기억해 냈지. 가장 억세고 노련한 뱃사람들이 쓰러져 나뒹굴 때, 그는 의연하고 명랑했다우."], ["It warn’t the want of objects to like and love ashore that gave him courage, it was his nat’ral mind.", "그에게 용기를 준 건 뭍에 좋아하고 사랑할 사람이 없어서가 아니라, 바로 그의 타고난 마음이었수."], ["I’ve seen it in his face, when he was no more than a child—ay, many a time!—and when I thought it nothing but his good looks, bless him!”", "나는 그게 그 애 얼굴에 서린 걸 봤다우, 그 애가 어린아이에 지나지 않았을 때부터—아, 여러 번이나!—그땐 나는 그저 그 애가 잘생겨서 그런 줄로만 알았지, 하느님 그 애를 축복하소서!\""]], [["“And was he saved!”", "\"그래서 그는 구조됐나요!\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."], ["“Was he saved!”", "\"구조됐나요!\""]], [["“That brave lad,”", "\"그 용감한 젊은이는,\""], ["said the Captain,—“look at me, pretty!", "선장이 말했다,—\"나를 보구려, 예쁜 아가씨!"], ["Don’t look round—”", "뒤돌아보지 말고—\""]], [["Florence had hardly power to repeat, “Why not?”", "플로렌스는 \"왜 안 되나요?\"라고 되풀이할 힘조차 거의 없었다."]], [["“Because there’s nothing there, my deary,”", "\"저기엔 아무것도 없으니까 그렇수, 우리 아가씨,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“Don’t be took aback, pretty creetur!", "\"당황하지 마우, 예쁜 사람!"], ["Don’t, for the sake of Wal”r, as was dear to all on us!", "그러지 마우, 우리 모두에게 소중했던 월터를 봐서라도!"], ["That there lad,”", "그 젊은이는,\""], ["said the Captain, “arter working with the best, and standing by the faint-hearted, and never making no complaint nor sign of fear, and keeping up a spirit in all hands that made ’em honour him as if he’d been a admiral—that lad, along with the second-mate and one seaman, was left, of all the beatin’ hearts that went aboard that ship, the only living creeturs—lashed to a fragment of the wreck, and driftin’ on the stormy sea.”", "선장이 말했다, \"가장 뛰어난 이들과 함께 일하고, 겁먹은 이들 곁을 지키고, 불평 한마디 두려운 기색 하나 내비치지 않고, 모두에게 기운을 북돋아 주어, 마치 자기가 제독이라도 되는 양 그들이 그를 떠받들게 했다우—그 젊은이는, 이등 항해사와 선원 한 사람과 함께, 그 배에 올랐던 그 모든 뛰는 가슴 가운데 유일하게 살아남은 자가 되어—난파선 조각에 몸을 묶은 채, 폭풍우 치는 바다 위를 떠돌았다우.\""]], [["“Were they saved?”", "\"그들은 구조됐나요?\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."]], [["“Days and nights they drifted on them endless waters,”", "\"여러 날 여러 밤을 그들은 그 끝없는 물 위를 떠돌았수,\""], ["said the Captain, “until at last—No!", "선장이 말했다, \"그러다 마침내—아니!"], ["Don’t look that way, pretty!—a sail bore down upon ’em, and they was, by the Lord’s mercy, took aboard: two living and one dead.”", "그쪽은 보지 마우, 예쁜 아가씨!—돛단배 한 척이 그들을 향해 다가왔고, 주님의 자비로 그들은 배에 실렸다우. 둘은 산 채로, 하나는 죽은 채로.\""]], [["“Which of them was dead?”", "\"그중 누가 죽었나요?\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 외쳤다."]], [["“Not the lad I speak on,”", "\"내가 말하는 그 젊은이는 아니라우,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“Thank God!", "\"하느님 감사합니다!"], ["oh thank God!”", "오, 하느님 감사합니다!\""]], [["“Amen!”", "\"아멘!\""], ["returned the Captain hurriedly.", "선장이 황급히 응수했다."], ["“Don’t be took aback!", "\"당황하지 마우!"], ["A minute more, my lady lass!", "잠깐만 더, 아가씨!"], ["with a good heart!—aboard that ship, they went a long voyage, right away across the chart (for there warn’t no touching nowhere), and on that voyage the seaman as was picked up with him died.", "굳센 마음으로!—그 배를 타고 그들은 긴 항해를 했수, 해도를 가로질러 곧장 멀리 나아갔지(어디에도 들를 데가 없었으니까). 그리고 그 항해 중에 그와 함께 구조됐던 그 선원은 죽었수."], ["But he was spared, and—”", "하지만 그는 목숨을 건졌고, 그리고—\""]], [["The Captain, without knowing what he did, had cut a slice of bread from the loaf, and put it on his hook (which was his usual toasting-fork), on which he now held it to the fire; looking behind Florence with great emotion in his face, and suffering the bread to blaze and burn like fuel.", "선장은 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 모른 채, 빵 덩어리에서 한 조각을 잘라 자기 갈고리(그것이 그의 평소 토스트 굽는 포크였다)에 꿰어, 이제 그것을 난롯불에 갖다 대고 있었다. 얼굴에 큰 감정을 담고 플로렌스의 등 뒤를 바라보면서, 빵이 땔감처럼 활활 타들어 가도록 내버려 둔 채로."]], [["“Was spared,”", "\"목숨을 건졌고,\""], ["repeated Florence, “and—?”", "플로렌스가 되풀이했다, \"그리고요—?\""]], [["“And come home in that ship,”", "\"그리고 그 배를 타고 집으로 돌아왔수,\""], ["said the Captain, still looking in the same direction, “and—don’t be frightened, pretty—and landed; and one morning come cautiously to his own door to take a obserwation, knowing that his friends would think him drownded, when he sheered off at the unexpected—”", "선장이 여전히 같은 방향을 바라보며 말했다, \"그리고—겁먹지 마우, 예쁜 아가씨—그리고 뭍에 내렸수. 그리고 어느 날 아침, 자기 친구들이 자기를 물에 빠져 죽은 줄로 알 거란 걸 알기에, 슬쩍 살펴보려고 조심스레 제 집 문 앞으로 다가왔다가, 뜻밖의—그것에 그만 비켜 달아났는데—\""]], [["“At the unexpected barking of a dog?”", "\"개가 뜻밖에 짖는 소리에요?\""], ["cried Florence, quickly.", "플로렌스가 재빨리 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇수,\""], ["roared the Captain.", "선장이 우렁차게 외쳤다."], ["“Steady, darling!", "\"침착하우, 우리 아가씨!"], ["courage!", "용기를 내우!"], ["Don’t look round yet.", "아직 뒤돌아보지 말고."], ["See there!", "저길 보우!"], ["upon the wall!”", "벽 위를!\""]], [["There was the shadow of a man upon the wall close to her.", "그녀 바로 가까이 벽 위에 한 사내의 그림자가 있었다."], ["She started up, looked round, and with a piercing cry, saw Walter Gay behind her!", "그녀는 벌떡 일어나 둘러보았고, 날카로운 외마디 비명과 함께, 자기 등 뒤에 선 월터 게이를 보았다!"]], [["She had no thought of him but as a brother, a brother rescued from the grave; a shipwrecked brother saved and at her side; and rushed into his arms.", "그녀는 그를 오라버니로밖에, 무덤에서 구해 낸 오라버니로밖에, 난파에서 살아남아 자기 곁에 있는 오라버니로밖에 생각하지 못했다. 그래서 그의 품으로 달려들었다."], ["In all the world, he seemed to be her hope, her comfort, refuge, natural protector.", "온 세상에서 그가 자기의 희망, 위안, 피난처, 천생의 보호자인 것만 같았다."], ["“Take care of Walter, I was fond of Walter!”", "\"월터를 잘 보살펴 다오, 나는 월터를 좋아했단다!\""], ["The dear remembrance of the plaintive voice that said so, rushed upon her soul, like music in the night.", "그렇게 말하던 그 애처로운 목소리에 대한 그리운 기억이, 밤중의 음악처럼 그녀의 영혼으로 밀려들었다."], ["“Oh welcome home, dear Walter!", "\"오, 어서 와요, 그리운 월터!"], ["Welcome to this stricken breast!”", "이 상처 입은 가슴으로 어서 와요!\""], ["She felt the words, although she could not utter them, and held him in her pure embrace.", "그녀는 그 말을 입 밖에 낼 수는 없었으나 그 말을 느꼈고, 순결한 포옹으로 그를 끌어안았다."]], [["Captain Cuttle, in a fit of delirium, attempted to wipe his head with the blackened toast upon his hook: and finding it an uncongenial substance for the purpose, put it into the crown of his glazed hat, put the glazed hat on with some difficulty, essayed to sing a verse of Lovely Peg, broke down at the first word, and retired into the shop, whence he presently came back express, with a face all flushed and besmeared, and the starch completely taken out of his shirt-collar, to say these words:", "커틀 선장은 일종의 정신착란에 빠져, 갈고리에 꿰인 새까매진 토스트로 자기 머리를 닦으려 들었다. 그러나 그것이 그 목적에는 영 어울리지 않는 물건임을 알고는, 그것을 번들거리는 모자 안에 넣고, 그 번들거리는 모자를 간신히 머리에 쓰고, 「사랑스러운 페그」 한 소절을 불러 보려다 첫 마디에서 막혀 버리고는, 가게로 물러갔다가, 이내 얼굴이 온통 벌게지고 시커멓게 얼룩진 채, 셔츠 깃의 풀기는 완전히 빠져 버린 채로 부랴부랴 돌아와, 이 말을 했다."]], [["“Wal”r, my lad, here is a little bit of property as I should wish to make over, jintly!”", "\"월터, 이놈아, 여기 내가 너한테 공동으로 넘겨주고 싶은 자그마한 재산이 좀 있다!\""]], [["The Captain hastily produced the big watch, the teaspoons, the sugar-tongs, and the canister, and laying them on the table, swept them with his great hand into Walter’s hat; but in handing that singular strong box to Walter, he was so overcome again, that he was fain to make another retreat into the shop, and absent himself for a longer space of time than on his first retirement.", "선장은 황급히 큼직한 시계와 찻숟가락들과 설탕 집게와 양철통을 꺼내 탁자 위에 늘어놓고는, 자기 큰 손으로 그것들을 쓸어 월터의 모자 안에 담았다. 그러나 그 별난 튼튼한 금고를 월터에게 건네면서, 그는 다시금 어찌나 북받쳤던지, 또 한 번 가게로 물러나야만 했고, 처음 물러났을 때보다 더 오랫동안 모습을 감추었다."]], [["But Walter sought him out, and brought him back; and then the Captain’s great apprehension was, that Florence would suffer from this new shock.", "그러나 월터가 그를 찾아내어 데려왔다. 그러자 선장의 큰 걱정거리는, 플로렌스가 이 새로운 충격으로 탈이 나지 않을까 하는 것이었다."], ["He felt it so earnestly, that he turned quite rational, and positively interdicted any further allusion to Walter’s adventures for some days to come.", "그것이 어찌나 진지하게 마음에 걸렸던지, 그는 아주 멀쩡하게 제정신을 차려, 앞으로 며칠간은 월터의 모험에 대해 더 이상 언급하는 것을 단호히 금했다."], ["Captain Cuttle then became sufficiently composed to relieve himself of the toast in his hat, and to take his place at the tea-board; but finding Walter’s grasp upon his shoulder, on one side, and Florence whispering her tearful congratulations on the other, the Captain suddenly bolted again, and was missing for a good ten minutes.", "그러고 나서 커틀 선장은 모자 속 토스트를 치워 낼 만큼은 충분히 마음을 가다듬고 찻쟁반 앞 자기 자리에 앉았다. 그러나 한쪽에서는 월터가 자기 어깨를 잡고, 다른 한쪽에서는 플로렌스가 눈물 어린 축하의 말을 속삭이는 것을 알아채자, 선장은 또 별안간 줄행랑을 쳐, 족히 십 분간 행방이 묘연했다."]], [["But never in all his life had the Captain’s face so shone and glistened, as when, at last, he sat stationary at the tea-board, looking from Florence to Walter, and from Walter to Florence.", "그러나 평생토록 선장의 얼굴이, 마침내 찻쟁반 앞에 가만히 앉아 플로렌스에게서 월터로, 월터에게서 플로렌스로 눈길을 옮길 때만큼 환히 빛나고 반들거린 적은 없었다."], ["Nor was this effect produced or at all heightened by the immense quantity of polishing he had administered to his face with his coat-sleeve during the last half-hour.", "그리고 이 효과는, 그가 지난 반 시간 동안 외투 소맷자락으로 자기 얼굴에 베푼 엄청난 양의 광내기로 생겨난 것도, 조금이라도 더해진 것도 아니었다."], ["It was solely the effect of his internal emotions.", "그것은 오로지 그의 내면의 감정이 빚어낸 효과였다."], ["There was a glory and delight within the Captain that spread itself over his whole visage, and made a perfect illumination there.", "선장의 내면에는 영광과 기쁨이 있어, 그것이 그의 얼굴 전체로 번져 거기에 완벽한 광휘를 이루어 놓았다."]], [["The pride with which the Captain looked upon the bronzed cheek and the courageous eyes of his recovered boy; with which he saw the generous fervour of his youth, and all its frank and hopeful qualities, shining once more, in the fresh, wholesome manner, and the ardent face, would have kindled something of this light in his countenance.", "선장이, 되찾은 자기 녀석의 햇볕에 그을린 뺨과 용감한 눈을 바라보며 느낀 자부심, 그 젊음의 너그러운 열정과 그 모든 솔직하고 희망찬 자질이 그 싱그럽고 건강한 태도와 그 열의에 찬 얼굴에 다시 한번 빛나는 것을 바라보며 느낀 자부심만으로도, 그의 얼굴에 이 빛 비슷한 것이 지펴졌을 것이다."], ["The admiration and sympathy with which he turned his eyes on Florence, whose beauty, grace, and innocence could have won no truer or more zealous champion than himself, would have had an equal influence upon him.", "그가 플로렌스에게로 눈길을 돌릴 때의 그 감탄과 공감만으로도—그녀의 아름다움과 우아함과 순결함은 그 자신보다 더 진실하고 더 열성적인 옹호자를 얻을 수 없었을 것인데—그에게 똑같은 영향을 미쳤을 것이다."], ["But the fulness of the glow he shed around him could only have been engendered in his contemplation of the two together, and in all the fancies springing out of that association, that came sparkling and beaming into his head, and danced about it.", "그러나 그가 사방에 두른 그 빛의 충만함은, 오직 그 둘을 함께 바라보는 가운데, 그리고 그 결합에서 솟아나 반짝이며 빛을 내며 그의 머릿속으로 들어와 그 둘레를 춤추던 그 모든 공상 속에서만 자라날 수 있었던 것이다."]], [["How they talked of poor old Uncle Sol, and dwelt on every little circumstance relating to his disappearance; how their joy was moderated by the old man’s absence and by the misfortunes of Florence; how they released Diogenes, whom the Captain had decoyed upstairs some time before, lest he should bark again; the Captain, though he was in one continual flutter, and made many more short plunges into the shop, fully comprehended.", "가엾은 늙은 솔 아저씨에 대해 둘이 어떻게 이야기를 나누고, 그의 실종에 관한 자잘한 사정 하나하나에 어떻게 머물렀는지. 그 노인의 부재와 플로렌스의 불행으로 그들의 기쁨이 어떻게 누그러졌는지."], ["But he no more dreamed that Walter looked on Florence, as it were, from a new and far-off place; that while his eyes often sought the lovely face, they seldom met its open glance of sisterly affection, but withdrew themselves when hers were raised towards him; than he believed that it was Walter’s ghost who sat beside him.", "다시 짖어 댈까 봐 선장이 얼마 전 위층으로 꾀어 올려 두었던 디오게네스를 어떻게 풀어 주었는지—그 모든 것을 선장은, 비록 끊임없이 안절부절못하며 가게로 몇 번이고 더 짧게 뛰어들기는 했어도, 충분히 알아들었다. 그러나 그는, 월터가 플로렌스를 말하자면 새롭고 아득히 먼 자리에서 바라보고 있다는 것을, 그의 눈이 그 사랑스러운 얼굴을 자주 찾으면서도 그 누이다운 애정의 솔직한 눈길과는 좀처럼 마주치지 못하고, 그녀의 눈이 그를 향해 들릴 때면 슬그머니 거두어진다는 것을—자기 곁에 앉은 것이 월터의 유령이라고 믿지 않은 것만큼이나—꿈에도 알아채지 못했다."], ["He saw them together in their youth and beauty, and he knew the story of their younger days, and he had no inch of room beneath his great blue waistcoat for anything save admiration of such a pair, and gratitude for their being reunited.", "그는 그 둘이 그 젊음과 아름다움 속에 함께 있는 것을 보았고, 그들의 어린 시절 이야기를 알고 있었으며, 그의 그 큰 푸른 조끼 아래에는 그런 한 쌍에 대한 감탄과 그들이 다시 만나게 된 것에 대한 감사 말고는 다른 무엇이 들어앉을 자리가 한 치도 없었다."]], [["They sat thus, until it grew late.", "둘은 이렇게 밤이 깊도록 앉아 있었다."], ["The Captain would have been content to sit so for a week.", "선장은 일주일이라도 이렇게 앉아 있어 흡족했을 것이다."], ["But Walter rose, to take leave for the night.", "그러나 월터가 그날 밤 작별을 고하려고 일어섰다."]], [["“Going, Walter!”", "\"가시려고요, 월터!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“Where?”", "\"어디로요?\""]], [["“He slings his hammock for the present, lady lass,”", "\"그 앤 당분간 브로글리네 가게에 가서 해먹을 매단다우, 아가씨,\" 커틀 선장이 말했다."], ["said Captain Cuttle, “round at Brogley’s. Within hail, Heart’s Delight.”", "\"부르면 들리는 데에 있는 거지요, 내 마음의 기쁨인 아가씨.\""]], [["“I am the cause of your going away, Walter,”", "\"제가 당신을 떠나게 만든 거예요, 월터,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“There is a houseless sister in your place.”", "\"당신 자리에 집 없는 누이가 들어앉은 거니까요.\""]], [["“Dear Miss Dombey,”", "\"그리운 돔비 아가씨,\""], ["replied Walter, hesitating—“if it is not too bold to call you so!—”", "월터가 머뭇거리며 대답했다—\"이렇게 부르는 게 너무 외람된 게 아니라면요!—\""]], [["“Walter!”", "\"월터!\""], ["she exclaimed, surprised.", "그녀가 놀라서 소리쳤다."]], [["“—If anything could make me happier in being allowed to see and speak to you, would it not be the discovery that I had any means on earth of doing you a moment’s service!", "\"—제가 당신을 보고 당신과 이야기할 수 있도록 허락받은 것보다 저를 더 행복하게 만들 수 있는 것이 있다면, 그건 제가 이 땅 위에서 당신께 단 한순간이라도 도움이 될 수 있는 무슨 방도가 있다는 것을 발견하는 일이 아닐까요!"], ["Where would I not go, what would I not do, for your sake?”", "당신을 위해서라면 제가 어디인들 못 가겠으며, 무엇인들 못 하겠습니까?\""]], [["She smiled, and called him brother.", "그녀는 미소 지으며 그를 오라버니라 불렀다."]], [["“You are so changed,”", "\"당신은 너무 달라졌어요,\""], ["said Walter—", "월터가 말했다—"]], [["“I changed!”", "\"제가 달라졌다고요!\""], ["she interrupted.", "그녀가 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“—To me,”", "\"—제게요,\""], ["said Walter, softly, as if he were thinking aloud, “changed to me.", "월터가 마치 소리 내어 생각하는 듯 부드럽게 말했다, \"제게 달라졌어요."], ["I left you such a child, and find you—oh!", "제가 떠날 땐 당신은 그런 어린아이였는데, 이제 보니 당신은—오!"], ["something so different—”", "너무도 다른 무언가가—\""]], [["“But your sister, Walter.", "\"하지만 당신의 누이잖아요, 월터."], ["You have not forgotten what we promised to each other, when we parted?”", "우리가 헤어질 때 서로에게 무엇을 약속했는지 잊지 않으셨죠?\""]], [["“Forgotten!”", "\"잊다니요!\""], ["But he said no more.", "그러나 그는 더 이상 말하지 않았다."]], [["“And if you had—if suffering and danger had driven it from your thoughts—which it has not—you would remember it now, Walter, when you find me poor and abandoned, with no home but this, and no friends but the two who hear me speak!”", "\"그리고 설령 잊으셨다 해도—설령 고난과 위험이 그것을 당신 마음에서 몰아냈다 해도—그렇지 않았지만요—당신은 이제 그것을 기억해 내실 거예요, 월터, 제가 이렇게 가난하고 버림받아, 이곳 말고는 집도 없고, 제 말을 들어 주는 이 두 분 말고는 벗도 없는 처지가 된 것을 보셨으니!\""]], [["“I would!", "\"기억해 내고말고요!"], ["Heaven knows I would!”", "하느님이 아십니다, 그러고말고요!\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, Walter,”", "\"오, 월터,\""], ["exclaimed Florence, through her sobs and tears.", "플로렌스가 흐느낌과 눈물 사이로 외쳤다."], ["“Dear brother!", "\"그리운 오라버니!"], ["Show me some way through the world—some humble path that I may take alone, and labour in, and sometimes think of you as one who will protect and care for me as for a sister!", "저에게 이 세상을 헤쳐 갈 무슨 길을 좀 알려 주세요—제가 혼자 걸어가, 그 안에서 애써 일하며, 때때로 당신을 누이를 지켜 주고 돌봐 줄 사람으로 떠올릴 수 있는, 어떤 누추한 오솔길이라도요!"], ["Oh, help me, Walter, for I need help so much!”", "오, 저를 도와주세요, 월터, 저는 도움이 너무도 절실하답니다!\""]], [["“Miss Dombey!", "\"돔비 아가씨! 플로렌스!"], ["Florence! I would die to help you.", "당신을 돕기 위해서라면 저는 죽을 수도 있어요."], ["But your friends are proud and rich.", "하지만 당신의 가족은 자존심 높고 부유한 분들이에요."], ["Your father—”", "당신의 아버님께서—\""]], [["“No, no! Walter!”", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼요!"], ["She shrieked, and put her hands up to her head, in an attitude of terror that transfixed him where he stood.", "월터!\" 그녀가 비명을 지르며, 그를 그 자리에 못 박아 버린 공포의 자세로 두 손을 들어 머리를 감쌌다."], ["“Don’t say that word!”", "\"그 말은 하지 마세요!\""]], [["He never, from that hour, forgot the voice and look with which she stopped him at the name.", "그는 그 시각 이후로, 그녀가 그 이름에서 자기를 막아서던 그 목소리와 그 표정을 결코 잊지 못했다."], ["He felt that if he were to live a hundred years, he never could forget it.", "그는 자기가 백 년을 산다 해도 결코 그것을 잊지 못하리란 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Somewhere—anywhere—but never home!", "어디든지—어디든 좋으나—다만 집만은 결코 안 된다!"], ["All past, all gone, all lost, and broken up!", "모든 것이 지나갔고, 모든 것이 사라졌고, 모든 것이 잃어졌고, 산산이 부서졌다!"], ["The whole history of her untold slight and suffering was in the cry and look; and he felt he never could forget it, and he never did.", "그녀가 입 밖에 내지 못한 그 멸시와 고통의 온 내력이 그 외침과 표정 속에 담겨 있었다. 그리고 그는 자기가 결코 그것을 잊지 못하리란 것을 느꼈고, 정말로 결코 잊지 못했다."]], [["She laid her gentle face upon the Captain’s shoulder, and related how and why she had fled.", "그녀는 그 부드러운 얼굴을 선장의 어깨에 기대고, 자기가 어떻게, 왜 도망쳐 나왔는지를 들려주었다."], ["If every sorrowing tear she shed in doing so, had been a curse upon the head of him she never named or blamed, it would have been better for him, Walter thought, with awe, than to be renounced out of such a strength and might of love.", "월터는 경외감에 잠겨 생각했다. 만일 그녀가 그렇게 이야기하며 흘린 슬픔의 눈물 한 방울 한 방울이, 그녀가 결코 이름 부르지도 탓하지도 않는 그 사람의 머리 위에 떨어지는 저주였더라면, 차라리 그 사람에게는 그편이, 그토록 강하고 위력 있는 사랑으로부터 버림받는 것보다는 나았으리라고."]], [["“There, precious!”", "\"자, 됐수, 우리 귀한 아가씨!\""], ["said the Captain, when she ceased; and deep attention the Captain had paid to her while she spoke; listening, with his glazed hat all awry and his mouth wide open.", "그녀가 말을 그치자 선장이 말했다. 그녀가 이야기하는 동안 선장은 깊은 주의를 기울였으니, 번들거리는 모자를 온통 비뚜름하게 쓰고 입을 떡 벌린 채 귀를 기울이고 있었던 것이다."], ["“Awast, awast, my eyes!", "\"그쳐요, 그쳐요, 내 눈물아!"], ["Wal”r, dear lad, sheer off for tonight, and leave the pretty one to me!”", "월터, 이 사랑하는 녀석아, 오늘 밤은 물러가서, 이 예쁜 아가씨는 이 늙은이한테 맡겨 다오!\""]], [["Walter took her hand in both of his, and put it to his lips, and kissed it.", "월터는 그녀의 손을 자기 두 손으로 잡아, 입술로 가져가 입을 맞추었다."], ["He knew now that she was, indeed, a homeless wandering fugitive; but, richer to him so, than in all the wealth and pride of her right station, she seemed farther off than even on the height that had made him giddy in his boyish dreams.", "이제 그는 그녀가 정말로 집 없이 떠도는 도망자임을 알았다. 그러나 그렇기에 그에게는 더욱 귀하면서도, 그녀가 마땅히 누릴 신분의 온갖 부와 자긍심 속에 있을 때보다도 더 멀게 느껴졌으니, 소년 시절의 꿈속에서 그를 어질어질하게 만들었던 그 높은 곳에 있을 때보다도 더 아득해 보였다."]], [["Captain Cuttle, perplexed by no such meditations, guarded Florence to her room, and watched at intervals upon the charmed ground outside her door—for such it truly was to him—until he felt sufficiently easy in his mind about her, to turn in under the counter.", "그런 사색에 조금도 시달리지 않는 커틀 선장은, 플로렌스를 그녀의 방까지 호위해 주고, 그녀의 방문 밖 그 마법에 걸린 땅에서—그에게는 정말로 그러했으니—이따금씩 망을 보다가, 마음이 충분히 놓일 만큼 그녀에 대해 안심이 된 후에야 계산대 밑으로 들어가 잠자리에 들었다."], ["On abandoning his watch for that purpose, he could not help calling once, rapturously, through the keyhole, “Drownded.", "그러려고 망보기를 단념하면서도, 그는 황홀한 마음에 또 한 번 열쇠 구멍에 대고 \"물에 빠져 죽었지."], ["Ain’t he, pretty?”—or, when he got downstairs, making another trial at that verse of Lovely Peg.", "안 그렇수, 예쁜 아가씨?\"라고 외치지 않을 수 없었고—혹은 아래층으로 내려가서는 「사랑스러운 페그」의 그 소절을 또 한 번 시도해 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["But it stuck in his throat somehow, and he could make nothing of it; so he went to bed, and dreamed that old Sol Gills was married to Mrs MacStinger, and kept prisoner by that lady in a secret chamber on a short allowance of victuals.", "그러나 그것이 어쩐지 목구멍에 걸려 도무지 제대로 부를 수가 없었다. 그래서 그는 잠자리에 들었고, 늙은 솔 길스가 맥스팅거 부인과 결혼하여, 그 부인에게 어느 비밀의 방에 갇힌 채 변변찮은 식량으로 연명하는 포로 신세가 되어 있는 꿈을 꾸었다."]], [["# CHAPTER L. Mr Toots’s Complaint", "# CHAPTER L. Mr Toots’s Complaint"]], [["There was an empty room above-stairs at the wooden Midshipman’s, which, in days of yore, had been Walter’s bedroom.", "나무로 만든 항해사 가게의 위층에는 빈방이 하나 있었는데, 옛날에는 월터의 침실이었던 곳이다."], ["Walter, rousing up the Captain betimes in the morning, proposed that they should carry thither such furniture out of the little parlour as would grace it best, so that Florence might take possession of it when she rose.", "월터는 아침 일찍 선장을 깨워, 작은 응접실에서 그 방을 가장 잘 꾸며 줄 만한 가구를 그리로 옮기자고 제안했다. 그러면 플로렌스가 일어났을 때 그 방을 차지할 수 있을 터였다."], ["As nothing could be more agreeable to Captain Cuttle than making himself very red and short of breath in such a cause, he turned to (as he himself said) with a will; and, in a couple of hours, this garret was transformed into a species of land-cabin, adorned with all the choicest moveables out of the parlour, inclusive even of the Tartar frigate, which the Captain hung up over the chimney-piece with such extreme delight, that he could do nothing for half-an-hour afterwards but walk backward from it, lost in admiration.", "그런 일로 얼굴이 새빨개지고 숨이 차도록 애쓰는 것보다 커틀 선장에게 더 기쁜 일은 없었으므로, 그는 (자기 말마따나) 의욕을 가지고 달려들었고, 두어 시간 만에 이 다락방은 일종의 육상 선실로 탈바꿈했다. 응접실에서 가장 멋진 세간들로 꾸며졌는데, 거기에는 그 타타르 프리깃함마저 포함되어 있었으니, 선장은 그것을 굴뚝 선반 위에 걸어 놓고는 어찌나 기뻐했던지, 그 뒤 반 시간 동안은 감탄에 넋을 잃은 채 그것을 보며 뒷걸음질 치는 일밖에 아무것도 하지 못했다."]], [["The Captain could be induced by no persuasion of Walter’s to wind up the big watch, or to take back the canister, or to touch the sugar-tongs and teaspoons.", "선장은 월터가 아무리 설득해도 큰 시계의 태엽을 감거나, 차통을 도로 받거나, 설탕집게와 찻숟가락에 손을 대려 하지 않았다."], ["“No, no, my lad;”", "\"아니, 아니다, 얘야.\""], ["was the Captain’s invariable reply to any solicitation of the kind, “I’ve made that there little property over, jintly.”", "그런 권유가 있을 때마다 선장은 한결같이 대답했다. \"내가 그 자그마한 재산을 공동 명의로 양도해 버렸느니라.\""], ["These words he repeated with great unction and gravity, evidently believing that they had the virtue of an Act of Parliament, and that unless he committed himself by some new admission of ownership, no flaw could be found in such a form of conveyance.", "이 말을 그는 더없는 진지함과 엄숙함을 담아 되풀이했는데, 분명 그 말에 의회 법령과 같은 효력이 있다고 믿는 듯했고, 또 자기가 무슨 새로운 소유권 인정으로 스스로를 옭아매지 않는 한 그런 양도 형식에는 흠을 찾을 수 없으리라고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["It was an advantage of the new arrangement, that besides the greater seclusion it afforded Florence, it admitted of the Midshipman being restored to his usual post of observation, and also of the shop shutters being taken down.", "새 배치의 이점은, 플로렌스에게 더 큰 은둔처를 제공해 주는 것 외에도, 항해사 인형을 평소의 관측 위치로 되돌려 놓을 수 있고, 또 가게 덧문도 내릴 수 있게 된 것이었다. 후자의 의식에는, 아무것도 모르는 선장이 아무리 그것을 대수롭지 않게 여겼다 하더라도, 전혀 쓸데없는 일은 아니었다."], ["The latter ceremony, however little importance the unconscious Captain attached to it, was not wholly superfluous; for, on the previous day, so much excitement had been occasioned in the neighbourhood, by the shutters remaining unopened, that the Instrument-maker’s house had been honoured with an unusual share of public observation, and had been intently stared at from the opposite side of the way, by groups of hungry gazers, at any time between sunrise and sunset.", "전날, 덧문이 닫힌 채로 있는 바람에 동네에 어찌나 큰 소동이 일었던지, 이 계기 제작자의 집은 유난히 많은 대중의 주목을 받는 영광을 누렸고, 해 뜰 때부터 해 질 때까지 아무 때나 길 건너편에서 굶주린 구경꾼들 무리가 뚫어지게 쳐다보았던 것이다. 한가한 자들과 부랑자들은 선장의 운명에 특히 흥미를 느꼈다."], ["The idlers and vagabonds had been particularly interested in the Captain’s fate; constantly grovelling in the mud to apply their eyes to the cellar-grating, under the shop-window, and delighting their imaginations with the fancy that they could see a piece of his coat as he hung in a corner; though this settlement of him was stoutly disputed by an opposite faction, who were of opinion that he lay murdered with a hammer, on the stairs.", "그들은 끊임없이 진창에 엎드려 가게 창문 아래 지하실 쇠창살에 눈을 갖다 대고는, 그가 한쪽 구석에 매달려 있는데 그 외투 자락이 보인다는 공상으로 상상을 즐겼다. 그러나 이렇게 그를 자리매김하는 것은 반대편 일파에게 거세게 반박당했으니, 이들은 그가 계단에서 망치에 맞아 살해되어 누워 있다는 의견이었다."], ["It was not without exciting some discontent, therefore, that the subject of these rumours was seen early in the morning standing at his shop-door as hale and hearty as if nothing had happened; and the beadle of that quarter, a man of an ambitious character, who had expected to have the distinction of being present at the breaking open of the door, and of giving evidence in full uniform before the coroner, went so far as to say to an opposite neighbour, that the chap in the glazed hat had better not try it on there—without more particularly mentioning what—and further, that he, the beadle, would keep his eye upon him.", "그러니 이런 소문들의 주인공이 아침 일찍 자기 가게 문 앞에 마치 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 멀쩡하고 정정하게 서 있는 것이 발견되었을 때, 다소간의 불만을 일으키지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 구역의 교구 관리는 야심 찬 성격의 사내였는데, 문을 부수고 들어가는 자리에 참석하여 정복 차림으로 검시관 앞에서 증언하는 영예를 기대하고 있던 터라, 길 건너 이웃에게 저 번들거리는 모자를 쓴 작자가 여기서 그런 짓을 안 하는 게 좋을 거라고—무엇을 안 하는 게 좋다는 건지는 더 구체적으로 언급하지 않은 채—말하기까지 했고, 더 나아가 자기, 곧 교구 관리가 그자를 눈여겨보고 있겠노라고 했다."]], [["“Captain Cuttle,” said Walter, musing, when they stood resting from their labours at the shop-door, looking down the old familiar street; it being still early in the morning; “nothing at all of Uncle Sol, in all that time!”", "\"커틀 선장님,\" 월터가 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 말했다. 두 사람이 일을 끝낸 뒤 가게 문 앞에 서서 쉬며, 그 낯익은 옛 거리를 내려다보고 있을 때였다. 아직 이른 아침이었다. \"그 오랜 시간 동안 솔 외삼촌 소식이 전혀 없었군요!\""]], [["“Nothing at all, my lad,”", "\"전혀 없었지, 얘야.\""], ["replied the Captain, shaking his head.", "선장이 고개를 저으며 대답했다."]], [["“Gone in search of me, dear, kind old man,”", "\"저를 찾으러 가신 거예요, 친절하고 다정한 노인이.\" 월터가 말했다."], ["said Walter: “yet never write to you!", "\"그런데도 선장님께 편지 한 장 안 보내시다니!"], ["But why not?", "하지만 왜 안 보내실까요?"], ["He says, in effect, in this packet that you gave me,”", "선장님이 제게 주신 이 봉투에서, 외삼촌은 사실상 이렇게 말씀하셨잖아요.\""], ["taking the paper from his pocket, which had been opened in the presence of the enlightened Bunsby, “that if you never hear from him before opening it, you may believe him dead.", "그러며 주머니에서 종이를 꺼냈는데, 그것은 식견 높은 번스비가 있는 자리에서 개봉되었던 것이다. \"이 봉투를 열기 전까지 자기 소식을 전혀 듣지 못한다면, 자기가 죽은 줄로 믿으셔도 좋다고요."], ["Heaven forbid!", "천만에!"], ["But you would have heard of him, even if he were dead!", "하지만 외삼촌이 돌아가셨다 해도 선장님은 그 소식을 들으셨을 거예요! 분명 누군가가, 외삼촌이 직접 못 쓰셨다면 그분 부탁으로 대신 편지를 써서, '아무 날, 제 집에서'"], ["Someone would have written, surely, by his desire, if he could not; and have said, ‘on such a day, there died in my house,’ or ‘under my care,’ or so forth, ‘Mr Solomon Gills of London, who left this last remembrance and this last request to you’.”", "혹은 '제 보살핌 아래' 혹은 그 비슷하게, '런던의 솔로몬 길스 씨가 세상을 떠났으며, 이것이 그분의 마지막 기념물이요 마지막 당부입니다'라고 전했을 거예요.\""]], [["The Captain, who had never climbed to such a clear height of probability before, was greatly impressed by the wide prospect it opened, and answered, with a thoughtful shake of his head, “Well said, my lad; wery well said.”", "선장은 전에 이런 명료한 개연성의 높이에 올라본 적이 없었으므로, 그것이 열어 보이는 드넓은 전망에 크게 감명받아, 사려 깊게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"잘 말했다, 얘야. 아주 잘 말했어.\""]], [["“I have been thinking of this, or, at least,” said Walter, colouring, “I have been thinking of one thing and another, all through a sleepless night, and I cannot believe, Captain Cuttle, but that my Uncle Sol (Lord bless him!) is alive, and will return. I don’t so much wonder at his going away, because, leaving out of consideration that spice of the marvellous which was always in his character, and his great affection for me, before which every other consideration of his life became nothing, as no one ought to know so well as I who had the best of fathers in him,”—Walter’s voice was indistinct and husky here, and he looked away, along the street,—“leaving that out of consideration, I say, I have often read and heard of people who, having some near and dear relative, who was supposed to be shipwrecked at sea, have gone down to live on that part of the sea-shore where any tidings of the missing ship might be expected to arrive, though only an hour or two sooner than elsewhere, or have even gone upon her track to the place whither she was bound, as if their going would create intelligence. I think I should do such a thing myself, as soon as another, or sooner than many, perhaps. But why my Uncle shouldn’t write to you, when he so clearly intended to do so, or how he should die abroad, and you not know it through some other hand, I cannot make out.”", "\"저는 이 일을 곰곰이 생각해 봤어요, 아니, 적어도,\" 월터가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"잠 못 이룬 밤 내내 이런저런 것을 생각해 봤는데, 커틀 선장님, 저는 우리 솔 외삼촌(부디 축복받으시길!)이 살아 계시고, 또 돌아오시리라고 믿지 않을 수가 없어요. 외삼촌이 떠나신 것 자체는 그리 이상하게 여기지 않아요. 그분 성격에 늘 있던 그 신비로운 기질이라든가, 저에 대한 그 깊은 애정—그분의 다른 모든 생애의 고려가 그 앞에서는 아무것도 아니게 되어 버리던 그 애정, 그분에게서 더없이 훌륭한 아버지를 얻었던 저보다 그것을 더 잘 알 사람은 없겠지요—\" 여기서 월터의 목소리는 흐릿하고 잠겨 들었고, 그는 거리를 따라 시선을 돌렸다. \"그런 것을 빼고 보더라도, 제 말은, 저는 종종 읽고 들었어요. 가까이 사랑하는 친척이 바다에서 난파당한 것으로 여겨질 때, 사람들이 그 실종된 배의 소식이 닿을 만한 해변 그곳으로 내려가 살러 간다는 것을요. 비록 다른 곳보다 겨우 한두 시간 먼저 소식을 들을 뿐인데도 말이에요. 심지어 그 배가 향하던 목적지까지 그 항로를 따라가는 사람들도 있다지요, 마치 자기가 가면 소식이 생겨나기라도 할 것처럼요. 저도 그런 일이라면 남들 못지않게, 아니 어쩌면 많은 이들보다 더 빨리 할 것 같아요. 하지만 외삼촌이 그토록 분명히 편지하시려 했으면서 왜 선장님께 안 쓰셨는지, 또 어떻게 타지에서 돌아가셨는데 선장님이 다른 누군가의 손을 통해서도 그걸 모르실 수 있는지, 그건 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없어요.\""]], [["Captain Cuttle observed, with a shake of his head, that Jack Bunsby himself hadn’t made it out, and that he was a man as could give a pretty taut opinion too.", "커틀 선장은 고개를 저으며, 잭 번스비 자신도 그것을 헤아리지 못했다고, 게다가 그 사람은 꽤 팽팽한 의견을 내놓을 줄 아는 인물이라고 말했다."]], [["“If my Uncle had been a heedless young man, likely to be entrapped by jovial company to some drinking-place, where he was to be got rid of for the sake of what money he might have about him,”", "\"만약 외삼촌이 분별없는 젊은이여서, 흥겨운 패거리한테 꾀여 어느 술집으로 끌려가, 몸에 지닌 돈푼 때문에 처치당할 만한 사람이었다면,\""], ["said Walter; “or if he had been a reckless sailor, going ashore with two or three months’ pay in his pocket, I could understand his disappearing, and leaving no trace behind.", "월터가 말했다. \"아니면 무모한 뱃사람이어서, 두세 달 치 봉급을 주머니에 넣고 상륙했더라면, 저는 그분이 사라져 흔적도 없이 떠나신 걸 이해할 수 있을 거예요."], ["But, being what he was—and is, I hope—I can’t believe it.”", "하지만 그분이 그런 분이었으니까—또 지금도 그러시길 바라니까—저는 그걸 믿을 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“Wal”r, my lad,”", "\"월터, 얘야,\""], ["inquired the Captain, wistfully eyeing him as he pondered and pondered, “what do you make of it, then?”", "선장이 줄곧 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 월터를 안타까운 눈으로 바라보며 물었다. \"그럼 너는 이 일을 어떻게 보느냐?\""]], [["“Captain Cuttle,”", "\"커틀 선장님,\""], ["returned Walter, “I don’t know what to make of it.", "월터가 대답했다. \"저도 어떻게 봐야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["I suppose he never has written!", "외삼촌이 편지를 전혀 안 쓰셨다고 봐야겠지요!"], ["There is no doubt about that?”", "그건 의심할 여지가 없겠죠?\""]], [["“If so be as Sol Gills wrote, my lad,”", "\"솔 길스가 편지를 썼다 치면, 얘야,\""], ["replied the Captain, argumentatively, “where’s his dispatch?”", "선장이 따지듯 대답했다. \"그 편지는 어디 있겠느냐?\""]], [["“Say that he entrusted it to some private hand,”", "\"외삼촌이 그 편지를 누군가 개인의 손에 맡기셨다고 쳐 봐요,\""], ["suggested Walter, “and that it has been forgotten, or carelessly thrown aside, or lost.", "월터가 의견을 냈다. \"그런데 그것이 잊혔거나, 무심히 내던져졌거나, 분실되었다고요."], ["Even that is more probable to me, than the other event.", "저로서는 그게 다른 경우보다 더 그럴듯하기까지 해요."], ["In short, I not only cannot bear to contemplate that other event, Captain Cuttle, but I can’t, and won’t.”", "한마디로, 커틀 선장님, 저는 그 다른 경우는 떠올리는 것조차 견딜 수 없을 뿐만 아니라, 떠올릴 수도 없고, 떠올리지도 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Hope, you see, Wal”r,”", "\"희망이란다, 알겠느냐, 월터,\""], ["said the Captain, sagely, “Hope.", "선장이 슬기롭게 말했다. \"희망 말이다."], ["It’s that as animates you.", "너를 살아 움직이게 하는 게 바로 그거야."], ["Hope is a buoy, for which you overhaul your Little Warbler, sentimental diwision, but Lord, my lad, like any other buoy, it only floats; it can’t be steered nowhere.", "희망은 부표(浮標)란다, 네 『꼬마 가수』 감상 부문을 샅샅이 뒤지면 나오는 그것 말이다. 하지만 얘야, 다른 부표들과 마찬가지로 그건 떠 있기만 할 뿐, 어디로 조종할 수는 없는 거란다."], ["Along with the figure-head of Hope,”", "희망의 선수상(船首像) 옆에는,\" 선장이 말했다."], ["said the Captain, “there’s a anchor; but what’s the good of my having a anchor, if I can’t find no bottom to let it go in?”", "\"닻도 하나 있지. 그렇지만 닻을 내려 박을 바닥을 찾지 못한다면 닻을 가진들 무슨 소용이겠느냐?\""]], [["Captain Cuttle said this rather in his character of a sagacious citizen and householder, bound to impart a morsel from his stores of wisdom to an inexperienced youth, than in his own proper person.", "커틀 선장이 이 말을 한 것은 자기 본연의 모습에서라기보다는, 슬기로운 시민이자 가장(家長)으로서 경험 없는 젊은이에게 지혜의 창고에서 한 조각을 나누어 주어야 한다는 자격에서였다."], ["Indeed, his face was quite luminous as he spoke, with new hope, caught from Walter; and he appropriately concluded by slapping him on the back; and saying, with enthusiasm, “Hooroar, my lad!", "사실 그가 이 말을 하는 동안 그의 얼굴은 월터에게서 옮아온 새로운 희망으로 환히 빛나고 있었다. 그리고 그는 적절하게도 월터의 등을 한 대 치며, 열의를 담아 이렇게 말함으로써 결론을 맺었다."], ["Indiwidually, I’m o’ your opinion.”", "\"만세, 얘야! 개인적으로 말하자면, 나도 네 의견과 같다.\""]], [["Walter, with his cheerful laugh, returned the salutation, and said:", "월터는 명랑하게 웃으며 그 인사를 되돌려 주고는 말했다."]], [["“Only one word more about my Uncle at present, Captain Cuttle.", "\"지금으로서는 외삼촌에 대해 딱 한마디만 더 하겠어요, 커틀 선장님."], ["I suppose it is impossible that he can have written in the ordinary course—by mail packet, or ship letter, you understand—”", "외삼촌이 보통의 경로로—우편선이나 선편 편지로요, 무슨 말씀인지 아시죠—편지를 쓰셨을 가능성은 없겠죠—\""]], [["“Ay, ay, my lad,”", "\"그래, 그래, 얘야.\""], ["said the Captain approvingly.", "선장이 동의하며 말했다."]], [["“—And that you have missed the letter, anyhow?”", "\"—그런데 선장님이 어쩌다 그 편지를 못 받으셨을 가능성은요?\""]], [["“Why, Wal”r,”", "\"이런, 월터,\""], ["said the Captain, turning his eyes upon him with a faint approach to a severe expression, “ain’t I been on the look-out for any tidings of that man o’ science, old Sol Gills, your Uncle, day and night, ever since I lost him?", "선장이 살짝 엄한 표정에 가까운 기색으로 그에게 눈을 돌리며 말했다. \"내가 그 과학자 양반, 늙은 솔 길스, 네 외삼촌을 잃은 그날 이후로, 그분 소식이라면 무엇이든 밤낮으로 살피지 않았겠느냐?"], ["Ain’t my heart been heavy and watchful always, along of him and you?", "늘 그분과 너를 생각하느라 내 마음이 무겁고 경계를 늦추지 않았겠느냐?"], ["Sleeping and waking, ain’t I been upon my post, and wouldn’t I scorn to quit it while this here Midshipman held together!”", "자나 깨나 내가 내 자리를 지키지 않았겠느냐, 또 이 항해사 인형이 멀쩡히 붙어 있는 한 내가 그 자리를 떠나는 걸 떳떳잖게 여기지 않았겠느냐!\""]], [["“Yes, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"네, 커틀 선장님,\""], ["replied Walter, grasping his hand, “I know you would, and I know how faithful and earnest all you say and feel is.", "월터가 그의 손을 꼭 잡으며 대답했다. \"그러셨다는 거 알아요, 또 선장님이 하시는 말씀과 느끼시는 모든 것이 얼마나 충직하고 진실한지도 알아요."], ["I am sure of it.", "확신해요."], ["You don’t doubt that I am as sure of it as I am that my foot is again upon this door-step, or that I again have hold of this true hand.", "제가 그것을 확신하는 게, 제 발이 다시 이 문지방을 밟고 있다는 것이나 제가 다시 이 참된 손을 잡고 있다는 것만큼이나 확실하다는 걸, 선장님은 의심하지 않으시죠."], ["Do you?”", "그렇죠?\""]], [["“No, no, Wal”r,”", "\"아니, 아니다, 월터.\""], ["returned the Captain, with his beaming", "선장이 환한 얼굴로 대답했다."]], [["“I’ll hazard no more conjectures,”", "\"더는 어떤 억측도 하지 않겠어요,\""], ["said Walter, fervently shaking the hard hand of the Captain, who shook his with no less goodwill.", "월터가 선장의 거친 손을 뜨겁게 흔들며 말했고, 선장도 그에 못지않은 선의로 그의 손을 마주 흔들었다."], ["“All I will add is, Heaven forbid that I should touch my Uncle’s possessions, Captain Cuttle!", "\"다만 한마디 덧붙일 건, 제가 외삼촌의 재산에 손을 댄다면 하늘이 용서치 않으실 거예요, 커틀 선장님!"], ["Everything that he left here, shall remain in the care of the truest of stewards and kindest of men—and if his name is not Cuttle, he has no name!", "그분이 여기 남겨 두신 모든 것은, 가장 참된 관리인이자 가장 친절한 분의 보살핌 아래 그대로 있을 거예요—그리고 그분 이름이 커틀이 아니라면, 그분에겐 이름이 없는 거지요!"], ["Now, best of friends, about—Miss Dombey.”", "자, 둘도 없는 친구분, 이제—돔비 양에 대해서요.\""]], [["There was a change in Walter’s manner, as he came to these two words; and when he uttered them, all his confidence and cheerfulness appeared to have deserted him.", "월터가 이 마지막 두 마디에 이르렀을 때 그의 태도에 변화가 있었다. 그리고 그가 그 말을 입에 올렸을 때, 그의 자신감과 명랑함이 모두 그를 떠나 버린 듯했다."]], [["“I thought, before Miss Dombey stopped me when I spoke of her father last night,”", "\"어젯밤 제가 돔비 양의 아버지 이야기를 꺼냈을 때 돔비 양이 저를 막기 전까지,\" 월터가 말했다."], ["said Walter, “—you remember how?”", "\"—어떻게 막았는지 기억하시죠?\""]], [["The Captain well remembered, and shook his head.", "선장은 잘 기억했고, 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“I thought,”", "\"저는 그 전까지는,\" 월터가 말했다."], ["said Walter, “before that, that we had but one hard duty to perform, and that it was, to prevail upon her to communicate with her friends, and to return home.”", "\"우리가 해야 할 어려운 의무가 하나뿐이라고 생각했어요. 그건 그분을 설득해서 친지들과 연락하게 하고, 집으로 돌아가게 하는 거였지요.\""]], [["The Captain muttered a feeble “Awast!”", "선장은 힘없이 \"잠깐!\""], ["or a “Stand by!”", "혹은 \"대기!\""], ["or something or other, equally pertinent to the occasion; but it was rendered so extremely feeble by the total discomfiture with which he received this announcement, that what it was, is mere matter of conjecture.", "혹은 그 상황에 마찬가지로 들어맞는 무언가를 중얼거렸다. 그러나 그 말은 이 통고를 받은 충격으로 인한 완전한 낭패감 탓에 어찌나 힘없게 나왔던지, 그게 무슨 말이었는지는 그저 추측에 맡길 따름이다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Walter, “that is over.", "월터가 말했다."], ["I think so, no longer.", "\"그건 끝났어요."], ["I would sooner be put back again upon that piece of wreck, on which I have so often floated, since my preservation, in my dreams, and there left to drift, and drive, and die!”", "저는 더 이상 그렇게 생각하지 않아요. 차라리 저는, 살아남은 뒤로 꿈속에서 그토록 자주 떠다니던 그 난파선 조각 위로 다시 던져져, 거기 표류하고 떠밀리다 죽는 편을 택하겠어요!\""]], [["“Hooroar, my lad!”", "\"만세, 얘야!\""], ["exclaimed the Captain, in a burst of uncontrollable satisfaction.", "선장이 걷잡을 수 없는 만족감에 터져 외쳤다."], ["“Hooroar!", "\"만세!"], ["hooroar!", "만세!"], ["hooroar!”", "만세!\""]], [["“To think that she, so young, so good, and beautiful,”", "\"저토록 젊고, 저토록 착하고, 아름다운 분이,\""], ["said Walter, “so delicately brought up, and born to such a different fortune, should strive with the rough world!", "월터가 말했다. \"저토록 곱게 자라고, 그토록 다른 운명을 타고난 분이, 거친 세상과 맞서 싸워야 한다니!"], ["But we have seen the gulf that cuts off all behind her, though no one but herself can know how deep it is; and there is no return.”", "하지만 우리는 그분의 뒤에 있는 모든 것을 끊어 놓은 그 심연을 봤어요, 비록 그것이 얼마나 깊은지는 오직 그분 자신밖에 모르겠지만요. 그리고 돌아갈 길은 없어요.\""]], [["Captain Cuttle, without quite understanding this, greatly approved of it, and observed in a tone of strong corroboration, that the wind was quite abaft.", "커틀 선장은 이 말을 온전히 이해하지는 못했으나 크게 찬동했고, 바람이 완전히 고물 쪽에서 분다고 강하게 맞장구치는 어조로 말했다."]], [["“She ought not to be alone here; ought she, Captain Cuttle?”", "\"그분이 여기 혼자 계셔서는 안 되겠죠, 그렇죠, 커틀 선장님?\""], ["said Walter, anxiously.", "월터가 근심스럽게 말했다."]], [["“Well, my lad,”", "\"글쎄다, 얘야,\""], ["replied the Captain, after a little sagacious consideration.", "선장이 잠시 슬기롭게 궁리한 뒤 대답했다."], ["“I don’t know.", "\"나도 모르겠다."], ["You being here to keep her company, you see, and you two being jintly—”", "네가 여기서 그분 말동무가 되어 드리고, 또 너희 둘이 공동으로—\""]], [["“Dear Captain Cuttle!”", "\"커틀 선장님!\""], ["remonstrated Walter.", "월터가 항의했다."], ["“I being here!", "\"제가 여기 있다니요!"], ["Miss Dombey, in her guileless innocent heart, regards me as her adopted brother; but what would the guile and guilt of my heart be, if I pretended to believe that I had any right to approach her, familiarly, in that character—if I pretended to forget that I am bound, in honour, not to do it?”", "돔비 양은 그 순결하고 천진한 마음으로 저를 양오라비로 여기고 있어요. 하지만 만약 제가 그런 자격으로 그분에게 허물없이 다가갈 권리가 있다고 믿는 척한다면—제가 명예를 걸고 그래선 안 된다는 사실을 잊은 척한다면, 제 마음의 간계와 죄가 어떻겠어요?\""]], [["“Wal”r, my lad,”", "\"월터, 얘야,\""], ["hinted the Captain, with some revival of his discomfiture, “ain’t there no other character as—”", "선장이 다소간 낭패감을 되살리며 넌지시 말했다. \"다른 자격은 없는 게냐, 가령—\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\" 월터가 대답했다."], ["returned Walter, “would you have me die in her esteem—in such esteem as hers—and put a veil between myself and her angel’s face for ever, by taking advantage of her being here for refuge, so trusting and so unprotected, to endeavour to exalt myself into her lover?", "\"선장님은 제가 그분의 존경—그분이 품은 것과 같은 그런 존경—을 잃기를 바라세요? 그리고 그분이 이토록 믿고 이토록 무방비한 채로 여기 피신해 와 계신 것을 이용해, 저 자신을 그분의 연인으로 높이려 애써서, 저와 그분의 천사 같은 얼굴 사이에 영원히 장막을 치기를 바라세요?"], ["What do I say?", "제가 무슨 말을 하고 있는 거죠?"], ["There is no one in the world who would be more opposed to me if I could do so, than you.”", "제가 그럴 수 있다면, 세상에 선장님보다 더 저를 반대하실 분은 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“Wal”r, my lad,”", "\"월터, 얘야,\""], ["said the Captain, drooping more and more, “prowiding as there is any just cause or impediment why two persons should not be jined together in the house of bondage, for which you’ll overhaul the place and make a note, I hope I should declare it as promised and wowed in the banns.", "선장이 점점 더 풀이 죽으며 말했다. \"두 사람이 속박의 집에서 맺어지면 안 될 정당한 사유나 장애가 있다면—그건 네가 그 대목을 샅샅이 뒤져 적어 둘 일이지만—나는 그것을 결혼 예고에서 약속되고 맹세된 대로 선언해야 한다고 믿는다."], ["So there ain’t no other character; ain’t there, my lad?”", "그래, 다른 자격은 없는 게로구나, 그렇지, 얘야?\""]], [["Walter briskly waved his hand in the negative.", "월터는 활기차게 손을 저어 아니라는 뜻을 표했다."]], [["“Well, my lad,”", "\"음, 얘야,\""], ["growled the Captain slowly, “I won’t deny but what I find myself wery much down by the head, along o’ this here, or but what I’ve gone clean about.", "선장이 천천히 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"이 일로 내가 뱃머리가 잔뜩 가라앉은 기분이라는 걸, 또 완전히 방향을 잃었다는 걸 부인하진 않으마."], ["But as to Lady lass, Wal”r, mind you, wot’s respect and duty to her, is respect and duty in my articles, howsumever disapinting; and therefore I follows in your wake, my lad, and feel as you are, no doubt, acting up to yourself.", "하지만 그 아씨로 말할 것 같으면, 월터, 잘 들어라, 그분에 대한 존중과 의무라면, 그건 제아무리 실망스럽다 해도 내 규약에서도 존중이요 의무다. 그러니 나는 네 항적을 따르마, 얘야, 또 너는 분명 네 본분에 맞게 행동하고 있다고 느낀다."], ["And there ain’t no other character, ain’t there?”", "그래, 다른 자격은 없는 게지, 그렇지?\""], ["said the Captain, musing over the ruins of his fallen castle, with a very despondent face.", "선장은 무너져 내린 자기 성(城)의 폐허를 곱씹으며, 몹시 낙담한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["“Now, Captain Cuttle,”", "\"자, 커틀 선장님,\""], ["said Walter, starting a fresh point with a gayer air, to cheer the Captain up—but nothing could do that; he was too much concerned—“I think we should exert ourselves to find someone who would be a proper attendant for Miss Dombey while she remains here, and who may be trusted.", "월터가 선장의 기운을 북돋우려고 한결 명랑한 기색으로 새 화제를 꺼냈다—그러나 그 무엇도 그것을 해낼 수 없었으니, 선장은 너무도 마음이 무거웠던 것이다—\"제 생각엔 우리가 애를 써서, 돔비 양이 여기 머무시는 동안 그분의 적절한 시중을 들어 드릴 만하고 믿을 수 있는 누군가를 찾아야 할 것 같아요."], ["None of her relations may.", "그분의 친척들은 안 될 거예요."], ["It’s clear Miss Dombey feels that they are all subservient to her father.", "돔비 양은 그들 모두가 자기 아버지에게 굽실거린다고 느끼는 게 분명하니까요."], ["What has become of Susan?”", "수전은 어떻게 됐을까요?\""]], [["“The young woman?”", "\"그 젊은 여자 말이냐?\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."], ["“It’s my belief as she was sent away again the will of Heart’s Delight.", "\"내가 보기엔 그 아가씨가 '마음의 기쁨'의 뜻을 거슬러 다시 떠나보내진 것 같다."], ["I made a signal for her when Lady lass first come, and she rated of her wery high, and said she had been gone a long time.”", "아씨가 처음 왔을 때 내가 그 여자 얘기를 슬쩍 꺼냈더니, 그 여자를 아주 높이 평가하면서, 떠난 지 오래됐다고 하더구나.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["said Walter, “do you ask Miss Dombey where she’s gone, and we’ll try to find her.", "월터가 말했다. \"돔비 양에게 그 여자가 어디로 갔는지 여쭤 봐 주세요, 우리가 찾아볼게요."], ["The morning’s getting on, and Miss Dombey will soon be rising.", "아침이 깊어 가니, 돔비 양도 곧 일어나실 거예요."], ["You are her best friend.", "선장님이 그분의 가장 좋은 친구잖아요."], ["Wait for her upstairs, and leave me to take care of all down here.”", "위층에서 그분을 기다리세요, 여기 아래 일은 다 제게 맡기시고요.\""]], [["The Captain, very crest-fallen indeed, echoed the sigh with which Walter said this, and complied.", "선장은 정말이지 몹시 풀이 죽어, 월터가 이 말을 하며 내쉰 한숨을 따라 쉬고는 그대로 따랐다."], ["Florence was delighted with her new room, anxious to see Walter, and overjoyed at the prospect of greeting her old friend Susan.", "플로렌스는 자기 새 방이 무척 마음에 들었고, 월터를 보고 싶어 했으며, 옛 친구 수전을 다시 맞이할 수 있다는 전망에 기뻐 어쩔 줄 몰랐다."], ["But Florence could not say where Susan was gone, except that it was in Essex, and no one could say, she remembered, unless it were Mr Toots.", "그러나 플로렌스는 수전이 어디로 갔는지 말할 수 없었으니, 다만 에식스 어디라는 것뿐, 또 자기 기억으로는 투츠 씨 말고는 아무도 말할 수 없으리라고 했다."]], [["With this information the melancholy Captain returned to Walter, and gave him to understand that Mr Toots was the young gentleman whom he had encountered on the door-step, and that he was a friend of his, and that he was a young gentleman of property, and that he hopelessly adored Miss Dombey.", "이 정보를 가지고 침울한 선장은 월터에게 돌아가, 투츠 씨가 바로 문지방에서 마주쳤던 그 젊은 신사이며, 자기 친구이고, 재산이 있는 젊은 신사이며, 돔비 양을 가망 없이 흠모하고 있다는 사실을 알려 주었다."], ["The Captain also related how the intelligence of Walter’s supposed fate had first made him acquainted with Mr Toots, and how there was solemn treaty and compact between them, that Mr Toots should be mute upon the subject of his love.", "선장은 또한 월터가 죽었다고 여겨졌다는 소식이 어떻게 처음으로 자기를 투츠 씨와 알게 만들었는지, 또 투츠 씨가 자기 연정에 대해 입을 다물기로 그들 사이에 엄숙한 조약과 협약이 있다는 것도 이야기해 주었다."]], [["The question then was, whether Florence could trust Mr Toots; and Florence saying, with a smile, “Oh, yes, with her whole heart!”", "그렇다면 문제는 플로렌스가 투츠 씨를 믿을 수 있느냐였는데, 플로렌스가 미소 지으며 \"오, 그럼요, 진심으로요!\""], ["it became important to find out where Mr Toots lived.", "하고 말하자, 이제 투츠 씨가 어디 사는지 알아내는 것이 중요해졌다."], ["This, Florence didn’t know, and the Captain had forgotten; and the Captain was telling Walter, in the little parlour, that Mr Toots was sure to be there soon, when in came Mr Toots himself.", "이것은 플로렌스도 몰랐고, 선장도 잊어버렸다. 선장이 작은 응접실에서 월터에게 투츠 씨가 분명 곧 이리 올 거라고 말하고 있는데, 바로 그때 투츠 씨 본인이 들어왔다."]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, rushing into the parlour without any ceremony, “I’m in a state of mind bordering on distraction!”", "투츠 씨가 아무런 격식도 없이 응접실로 뛰어들며 말했다. \"저는 지금 거의 정신착란에 가까운 심정입니다!\""]], [["Mr Toots had discharged those words, as from a mortar, before he observed Walter, whom he recognised with what may be described as a chuckle of misery.", "투츠 씨는 마치 박격포에서 쏘아 내듯 그 말을 발사한 뒤에야 월터를 알아보았고, 비참함의 낄낄거림이라고 할 만한 것으로 그를 알아보았다."]], [["“You’ll excuse me, Sir,”", "\"실례합니다, 선생,\""], ["said Mr Toots, holding his forehead, “but I’m at present in that state that my brain is going, if not gone, and anything approaching to politeness in an individual so situated would be a hollow mockery.", "투츠 씨가 이마를 짚으며 말했다. \"하지만 제가 지금 제정신이 나가려는, 아니 이미 나간 상태라서, 이런 처지에 놓인 사람이 예의 비슷한 걸 차린다면 그건 공허한 조롱일 따름입니다."], ["Captain Gills, I beg to request the favour of a private interview.”", "길스 선장님, 단둘이 만나는 호의를 청하고 싶습니다.\""]], [["“Why, Brother,”", "\"이런, 형제여,\""], ["returned the Captain, taking him by the hand, “you are the man as we was on the look-out for.”", "선장이 그의 손을 잡으며 대답했다. \"자네야말로 우리가 찾고 있던 사람일세.\""]], [["“Oh, Captain Gills,”", "\"오, 길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “what a look-out that must be, of which I am the object!", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"제가 찾는 대상이 되다니, 그게 대체 무슨 찾기란 말입니까!"], ["I haven’t dared to shave, I’m in that rash state.", "저는 면도할 엄두도 못 냈습니다, 이렇게 무모한 상태라서요."], ["I haven’t had my clothes brushed.", "옷도 솔질 못 했고요."], ["My hair is matted together.", "머리는 떡이 졌습니다."], ["I told the Chicken that if he offered to clean my boots, I’d stretch him a Corpse before me!”", "치킨한테 만약 내 부츠를 닦겠다고 들면 네놈을 내 앞에 시체로 뻗게 만들겠다고 했지요!\""]], [["All these indications of a disordered mind were verified in Mr Toots’s appearance, which was wild and savage.", "이렇게 마음이 흐트러졌다는 온갖 표시가 투츠 씨의 모습에서 확인되었으니, 그것은 거칠고 사납기 그지없었다."]], [["“See here, Brother,”", "\"여길 보게, 형제여,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“This here’s old Sol Gills’s nevy Wal”r.", "\"여기 이 사람이 늙은 솔 길스의 조카 월터일세."], ["Him as was supposed to have perished at sea.”", "바다에서 죽었다고 여겨졌던 바로 그 사람 말이야.\""]], [["Mr Toots took his hand from his forehead, and stared at Walter.", "투츠 씨는 이마에서 손을 떼고 월터를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Good gracious me!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["stammered Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 더듬거렸다."], ["“What a complication of misery!", "\"이 무슨 비참함의 뒤엉킴이람!"], ["How-de-do?", "안녕하십니까?"], ["I—I—I’m afraid you must have got very wet.", "저—저—저는 당신이 무척 흠뻑 젖으셨겠다 싶어 걱정입니다."], ["Captain Gills, will you allow me a word in the shop?”", "길스 선장님, 가게에서 한마디 나누는 걸 허락해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["He took the Captain by the coat, and going out with him whispered:", "그는 선장의 외투를 붙잡고 그와 함께 나가며 속삭였다."]], [["“That then, Captain Gills, is the party you spoke of, when you said that he and Miss Dombey were made for one another?”", "\"그러니까, 길스 선장님, 저 사람이 바로, 그 사람과 돔비 양이 천생연분이라고 선장님이 말씀하셨던 그 당사자입니까?\""]], [["“Why, ay, my lad,”", "\"이런, 그래, 얘야,\""], ["replied the disconsolate Captain; “I was of that mind once.”", "비탄에 잠긴 선장이 대답했다. \"한때 나도 그런 마음이었지.\""]], [["“And at this time!”", "\"그런데 이런 때에!\""], ["exclaimed Mr Toots, with his hand to his forehead again.", "투츠 씨가 다시 이마에 손을 대며 외쳤다."], ["“Of all others!—a hated rival!", "\"하필이면!—증오스러운 연적이라니!"], ["At least, he ain’t a hated rival,”", "적어도, 저 사람은 증오스러운 연적은 아니지,\""], ["said Mr Toots, stopping short, on second thoughts, and taking away his hand; “what should I hate him for?", "투츠 씨가 다시 생각하며 딱 멈추고 손을 떼며 말했다. \"내가 저 사람을 미워할 게 뭐람?"], ["No.", "아니지."], ["If my affection has been truly disinterested, Captain Gills, let me prove it now!”", "만약 내 애정이 정말로 사심 없는 거였다면, 길스 선장님, 지금 그것을 증명하게 해 주십시오!\""]], [["Mr Toots shot back abruptly into the parlour, and said, wringing Walter by the hand:", "투츠 씨는 별안간 응접실로 도로 쏜살같이 들어가, 월터의 손을 꽉 쥐고 흔들며 말했다."]], [["“How-de-do?", "\"안녕하십니까?"], ["I hope you didn’t take any cold.", "감기 안 걸리셨길 바랍니다."], ["I—I shall be very glad if you’ll give me the pleasure of your acquaintance.", "저는—저는 당신과 알고 지내는 기쁨을 주신다면 무척 기쁘겠습니다."], ["I wish you many happy returns of the day.", "이날을 여러 번 행복하게 맞이하시길 빕니다."], ["Upon my word and honour,”", "맹세코, 진심으로,\""], ["said Mr Toots, warming as he became better acquainted with Walter’s face and figure, “I’m very glad to see you!”", "투츠 씨가 월터의 얼굴과 풍채와 더 잘 알게 되면서 점점 다정해지며 말했다. \"당신을 만나서 정말 기쁩니다!\""]], [["“Thank you, heartily,”", "\"진심으로 고맙습니다,\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."], ["“I couldn’t desire a more genuine and genial welcome.”", "\"이보다 더 참되고 따뜻한 환영은 바랄 수도 없겠어요.\""]], [["“Couldn’t you, though?”", "\"그렇게 생각하세요, 정말로?\""], ["said Mr Toots, still shaking his hand.", "투츠 씨가 여전히 그의 손을 흔들며 말했다."], ["“It’s very kind of you.", "\"참 친절하십니다."], ["I’m much obliged to you.", "정말 감사합니다."], ["How-de-do?", "안녕하십니까?"], ["I hope you left everybody quite well over the—that is, upon the—I mean wherever you came from last, you know.”", "저—거기 다들 안녕히 잘 계시던가요, 그—그러니까, 저—제 말은, 당신이 마지막으로 오신 곳이 어디든 거기 사람들 말입니다.\""]], [["All these good wishes, and better intentions, Walter responded to manfully.", "이 모든 좋은 인사와 더 좋은 의도에 월터는 의젓하게 응답했다."]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “I should wish to be strictly honourable; but I trust I may be allowed now, to allude to a certain subject that—”", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"저는 엄격히 명예를 지키고 싶습니다만, 이제는 어떤 일정한 주제에 대해 언급하는 것이 허락되리라 믿습니다, 그러니까—\""]], [["“Ay, ay, my lad,”", "\"그래, 그래, 얘야,\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."], ["“Freely, freely.”", "\"거리낌 없이, 거리낌 없이.\""]], [["“Then, Captain Gills,”", "\"그러면, 길스 선장님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr Toots, “and Lieutenant Walters—are you aware that the most dreadful circumstances have been happening at Mr Dombey’s house, and that Miss Dombey herself has left her father, who, in my opinion,”", "\"그리고 월터스 중위님—혹시 돔비 씨 댁에서 더없이 끔찍한 사건들이 벌어졌다는 걸, 또 돔비 양 본인이 자기 아버지를 떠났다는 걸 아십니까? 제 생각으로 그 아버지란,\""], ["said Mr Toots, with great excitement, “is a Brute, that it would be a flattery to call a—a marble monument, or a bird of prey,—and that she is not to be found, and has gone no one knows where?”", "투츠 씨가 잔뜩 흥분하여 말했다. \"—대리석 기념비라거나 맹금이라거나 하고 부르는 게 차라리 칭찬일 만한, 그런 짐승입니다—그리고 그분이 어디 계신지 찾을 수가 없고, 아무도 모르는 곳으로 가 버리셨다는 걸요?\""]], [["“May I ask how you heard this?”", "\"이 소식을 어떻게 들으셨는지 여쭤 봐도 될까요?\""], ["inquired Walter.", "월터가 물었다."]], [["“Lieutenant Walters,”", "\"월터스 중위님,\" 투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr Toots, who had arrived at that appellation by a process peculiar to himself; probably by jumbling up his Christian name with the seafaring profession, and supposing some relationship between him and the Captain, which would extend, as a matter of course, to their titles; “Lieutenant Walters, I can have no objection to make a straightforward reply.", "그는 자기 나름의 독특한 과정을 거쳐 이 호칭에 이른 것인데, 아마도 월터의 세례명을 뱃사람 직업과 뒤섞고는, 그와 선장 사이에 어떤 관계가 있어서 그게 당연히 둘의 직함에까지 미친다고 짐작한 모양이었다. \"월터스 중위님, 저는 솔직하게 대답하는 데 아무 거리낌이 없습니다."], ["The fact is, that feeling extremely interested in everything that relates to Miss Dombey—not for any selfish reason, Lieutenant Walters, for I am well aware that the most able thing I could do for all parties would be to put an end to my existence, which can only be regarded as an inconvenience—I have been in the habit of bestowing a trifle now and then upon a footman; a most respectable young man, of the name of Towlinson, who has lived in the family some time; and Towlinson informed me, yesterday evening, that this was the state of things.", "사실인즉, 돔비 양과 관련된 모든 일에 제가 지극히 관심을 두는 까닭에—무슨 사심이 있어서가 아닙니다, 월터스 중위님, 저로서는 모두를 위해 제가 할 수 있는 가장 유능한 일이 제 존재를 끝내는 것임을 잘 알고 있고, 제 존재란 그저 거추장스러운 것으로만 여겨질 수 있으니까요—저는 이따금 하인 하나에게 약간의 돈을 쥐여 주는 버릇이 있었습니다. 타울린슨이라는 이름의 더없이 점잖은 젊은이로, 그 집안에 한동안 살아온 사람이지요."], ["Since which, Captain Gills—and Lieutenant Walters—I have been perfectly frantic, and have been lying down on the sofa all night, the Ruin you behold.”", "그 타울린슨이 어제저녁 저에게 이런 사정을 알려 주었습니다. 그 뒤로, 길스 선장님—그리고 월터스 중위님—저는 완전히 미칠 지경이었고, 밤새 소파에 누워 있었지요, 지금 보시는 이 폐허가 된 몰골로요.\""]], [["“Mr Toots,”", "\"투츠 씨,\""], ["said Walter, “I am happy to be able to relieve your mind.", "월터가 말했다. \"제가 당신 마음을 덜어 드릴 수 있어서 기쁩니다."], ["Pray calm yourself.", "부디 진정하세요."], ["Miss Dombey is safe and well.”", "돔비 양은 무사하고 안녕하십니다.\""]], [["“Sir!”", "\"선생!\""], ["cried Mr Toots, starting from his chair and shaking hands with him anew, “the relief is so excessive, and unspeakable, that if you were to tell me now that Miss Dombey was married even, I could smile.", "투츠 씨가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 그와 새로이 악수하며 외쳤다. \"그 안도감이 어찌나 넘치고 이루 말할 수 없는지, 만약 지금 당신이 돔비 양이 결혼했다고 말씀하신대도 저는 미소 지을 수 있을 겁니다."], ["Yes, Captain Gills,”", "그래요, 길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, appealing to him, “upon my soul and body, I really think, whatever I might do to myself immediately afterwards, that I could smile, I am so relieved.”", "투츠 씨가 선장에게 호소하며 말했다. \"제 영혼과 육신을 걸고, 정말로 그렇게 생각합니다, 그 직후에 제가 저 자신에게 무슨 짓을 하든 간에, 저는 미소 지을 수 있을 것 같습니다, 이렇게나 안도가 되니까요.\""]], [["“It will be a greater relief and delight still, to such a generous mind as yours,”", "\"당신처럼 너그러운 마음을 가진 분께는, 돔비 양에게 도움을 드릴 수 있다는 걸 알게 되시면 더욱 큰 안도이자 기쁨일 겁니다,\""], ["said Walter, not at all slow in returning his greeting, “to find that you can render service to Miss Dombey.", "월터가 조금도 더디지 않게 인사를 되돌려 주며 말했다."], ["Captain Cuttle, will you have the kindness to take Mr Toots upstairs?”", "\"커틀 선장님, 투츠 씨를 위층으로 좀 모셔다 주시겠어요?\""]], [["The Captain beckoned to Mr Toots, who followed him with a bewildered countenance, and, ascending to the top of the house, was introduced, without a word of preparation from his conductor, into Florence’s new retreat.", "선장이 투츠 씨에게 손짓하자, 그는 어리둥절한 얼굴로 그 뒤를 따랐고, 집 꼭대기로 올라가, 안내인의 사전 설명 한마디 없이 플로렌스의 새 은신처로 안내되었다."]], [["Poor Mr Toots’s amazement and pleasure at sight of her were such, that they could find a vent in nothing but extravagance.", "가엾은 투츠 씨가 그녀를 보고 느낀 놀라움과 기쁨은 어찌나 컸던지, 오로지 격에 넘치는 행동으로밖에는 분출구를 찾을 수 없었다."], ["He ran up to her, seized her hand, kissed it, dropped it, seized it again, fell upon one knee, shed tears, chuckled, and was quite regardless of his danger of being pinned by Diogenes, who, inspired by the belief that there was something hostile to his mistress in these demonstrations, worked round and round him, as if only undecided at what particular point to go in for the assault, but quite resolved to do him a fearful mischief.", "그는 그녀에게 달려가, 손을 붙잡고, 입 맞추고, 떨어뜨렸다가, 다시 붙잡고, 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 눈물을 흘리고, 낄낄거렸다. 그러느라 디오게네스에게 물어뜯길 위험은 도통 아랑곳하지 않았는데, 디오게네스는 이런 거동에 자기 여주인을 해치려는 무언가가 있다는 믿음에 사로잡혀 그의 주위를 빙빙 돌았으니, 마치 어느 지점을 노려 공격에 들어갈지만 정하지 못했을 뿐, 그에게 끔찍한 해를 끼치기로는 단단히 작정한 듯했다."]], [["“Oh Di, you bad, forgetful dog!", "\"오 디, 이 못된, 잊어버린 강아지야!"], ["Dear Mr Toots, I am so rejoiced to see you!”", "친애하는 투츠 씨, 당신을 뵈니 정말 기뻐요!\""]], [["“Thankee,”", "\"고맙습니다,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “I am pretty well, I’m much obliged to you, Miss Dombey.", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"저는 꽤 잘 지냅니다, 정말 감사합니다, 돔비 양."], ["I hope all the family are the same.”", "댁의 온 가족분들도 다 그러시길 바랍니다.\""]], [["Mr Toots said this without the least notion of what he was talking about, and sat down on a chair, staring at Florence with the liveliest contention of delight and despair going on in his face that any face could exhibit.", "투츠 씨는 자기가 무슨 말을 하고 있는지 전혀 모르는 채 이렇게 말하고는, 의자에 앉아 플로렌스를 빤히 바라보았는데, 그 얼굴에는 어떤 얼굴이 보일 수 있는 것 중에서도 가장 생생한 기쁨과 절망의 다툼이 벌어지고 있었다."]], [["“Captain Gills and Lieutenant Walters have mentioned, Miss Dombey,”", "\"길스 선장님과 월터스 중위님이 말씀하시기를, 돔비 양,\" 투츠 씨가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["gasped Mr Toots, “that I can do you some service.", "\"제가 당신께 무슨 도움을 드릴 수 있다더군요."], ["If I could by any means wash out the remembrance of that day at Brighton, when I conducted myself—much more like a Parricide than a person of independent property,”", "만약 제가 무슨 수로든, 제가 어버이를 죽인 자처럼—독립된 재산을 가진 사람이라기보다 훨씬 더 그렇게 처신했던 그 브라이턴에서의 그날의 기억을 씻어 낼 수 있다면,\""], ["said Mr Toots, with severe self-accusation, “I should sink into the silent tomb with a gleam of joy.”", "투츠 씨가 매서운 자책으로 말했다. \"저는 한 줄기 기쁨을 띤 채 고요한 무덤 속으로 가라앉을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Pray, Mr Toots,”", "\"부디, 투츠 씨,\""], ["said Florence, “do not wish me to forget anything in our acquaintance.", "플로렌스가 말했다. \"우리가 알고 지낸 어떤 일도 제가 잊기를 바라지 마세요."], ["I never can, believe me.", "저는 결코 잊을 수 없어요, 믿어 주세요."], ["You have been far too kind and good to me always.”", "당신은 늘 제게 너무도 친절하고 좋은 분이셨어요.\""]], [["“Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 양,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, “your consideration for my feelings is a part of your angelic character.", "투츠 씨가 대답했다. \"제 감정을 헤아려 주시는 것이야말로 당신의 천사 같은 성품의 일부지요."], ["Thank you a thousand times.", "천 번이라도 감사드립니다."], ["It’s of no consequence at all.”", "그건 전혀 대수롭지 않은 일입니다.\""]], [["“What we thought of asking you,”", "\"우리가 당신께 여쭤 보려던 건,\""], ["said Florence, “is, whether you remember where Susan, whom you were so kind as to accompany to the coach-office when she left me, is to be found.”", "플로렌스가 말했다. \"수전이, 제 곁을 떠날 때 당신이 친절하게도 마차 사무소까지 바래다주셨던 그 수전이 어디서 찾아질지 기억하시느냐 하는 거예요.\""]], [["“Why I do not certainly, Miss Dombey,”", "\"글쎄요, 분명히는 기억이 안 나는군요, 돔비 양,\""], ["said Mr Toots, after a little consideration, “remember the exact name of the place that was on the coach; and I do recollect that she said she was not going to stop there, but was going farther on.", "투츠 씨가 잠시 궁리한 뒤 말했다. \"마차에 적혀 있던 곳의 정확한 이름이 말입니다."], ["But, Miss Dombey, if your object is to find her, and to have her here, myself and the Chicken will produce her with every dispatch that devotion on my part, and great intelligence on the Chicken’s, can ensure.”", "다만 그 여자가 거기서 멈추지 않고 더 멀리 갈 거라고 말했던 건 기억이 납니다. 하지만, 돔비 양, 만약 당신의 목적이 그 여자를 찾아 이리로 데려오는 것이라면, 저와 치킨이 그 여자를 데려오겠습니다, 제 헌신과 치킨의 대단한 지력이 보장할 수 있는 온갖 신속함을 다해서요.\""]], [["Mr Toots was so manifestly delighted and revived by the prospect of being useful, and the disinterested sincerity of his devotion was so unquestionable, that it would have been cruel to refuse him.", "투츠 씨가 쓸모 있는 사람이 될 수 있다는 전망에 어찌나 분명히 기뻐하고 생기를 되찾던지, 또 그 헌신의 사심 없는 진심이 어찌나 의심할 여지 없던지, 그를 거절한다면 잔인한 일이 되었으리라."], ["Florence, with an instinctive delicacy, forbore to urge the least obstacle, though she did not forbear to overpower him with thanks; and Mr Toots proudly took the commission upon himself for immediate execution.", "플로렌스는 본능적인 섬세함으로 조금의 방해도 두지 않으려 삼갔으나, 그를 감사로 압도하는 것만은 삼가지 않았다. 그리고 투츠 씨는 자랑스럽게 그 임무를 즉각 수행할 것으로 떠맡았다."]], [["“Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 양,\""], ["said Mr Toots, touching her proffered hand, with a pang of hopeless love visibly shooting through him, and flashing out in his face, “Good-bye!", "투츠 씨가 그녀가 내민 손에 손을 대며 말했는데, 가망 없는 사랑의 아픔이 그를 꿰뚫고 지나가 얼굴에 번뜩여 드러났다. \"안녕히 계세요!"], ["Allow me to take the liberty of saying, that your misfortunes make me perfectly wretched, and that you may trust me, next to Captain Gills himself.", "외람되지만 한 말씀 드리자면, 당신의 불행이 저를 더없이 비참하게 만들며, 또 당신은 길스 선장님 바로 다음으로 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다."], ["I am quite aware, Miss Dombey, of my own deficiencies—they’re not of the least consequence, thank you—but I am entirely to be relied upon, I do assure you, Miss Dombey.”", "저는 제 자신의 부족함을 잘 압니다, 돔비 양—그건 조금도 대수롭지 않은 일이지요, 고맙습니다—하지만 저는 전적으로 의지하셔도 좋은 사람입니다, 정말이지 그렇습니다, 돔비 양.\""]], [["With that Mr Toots came out of the room, again accompanied by the Captain, who, standing at a little distance, holding his hat under his arm and arranging his scattered locks with his hook, had been a not uninterested witness of what passed.", "그 말과 함께 투츠 씨는 방에서 나왔는데, 다시 선장이 동행했다. 선장은 조금 떨어진 곳에 서서, 모자를 겨드랑이에 끼고 갈고리손으로 흐트러진 머리칼을 매만지며, 방금 오간 일을 자못 흥미롭게 지켜보고 있던 참이었다."], ["And when the door closed behind them, the light of Mr Toots’s life was darkly clouded again.", "그리고 그들 뒤로 문이 닫히자, 투츠 씨 인생의 빛은 다시 어둡게 흐려졌다."]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["said that gentleman, stopping near the bottom of the stairs, and turning round, “to tell you the truth, I am not in a frame of mind at the present moment, in which I could see Lieutenant Walters with that entirely friendly feeling towards him that I should wish to harbour in my breast.", "그 신사가 계단 거의 맨 아래쯤에서 멈춰 서 돌아보며 말했다. \"솔직히 말씀드리면, 저는 지금 이 순간 월터스 중위님을 향해, 제 가슴속에 품고 싶은 그런 전적으로 우호적인 감정으로 그분을 대할 만한 심정이 아닙니다."], ["We cannot always command our feelings, Captain Gills, and I should take it as a particular favour if you’d let me out at the private door.”", "우리가 늘 자기 감정을 다스릴 수는 없는 법이지요, 길스 선장님, 그러니 저를 옆문으로 내보내 주신다면 각별한 호의로 알겠습니다.\""]], [["“Brother,”", "\"형제여,\""], ["returned the Captain, “you shall shape your own course.", "선장이 대답했다. \"자네 항로는 자네가 정하게."], ["Wotever course you take, is plain and seamanlike, I’m wery sure.”", "어떤 항로를 택하든 그건 떳떳하고 뱃사람다운 일일 걸세, 나는 아주 확신하네.\""]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “you’re extremely kind.", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"정말 친절하십니다."], ["Your good opinion is a consolation to me.", "선장님이 좋게 봐주시는 게 제겐 위안입니다."], ["There is one thing,”", "한 가지,\""], ["said Mr Toots, standing in the passage, behind the half-opened door, “that I hope you’ll bear in mind, Captain Gills, and that I should wish Lieutenant Walters to be made acquainted with.", "투츠 씨가 반쯤 열린 문 뒤 통로에 서서 말했다. \"선장님이 명심해 주시길 바라는 게 있는데, 또 월터스 중위님께도 알려 주셨으면 하는 것입니다."], ["I have quite come into my property now, you know, and—and I don’t know what to do with it.", "저는 이제 제 재산을 온전히 물려받았습니다, 아시다시피요, 그런데—그런데 저는 그걸 어찌해야 할지 모르겠습니다."], ["If I could be at all useful in a pecuniary point of view, I should glide into the silent tomb with ease and smoothness.”", "만약 제가 금전적인 면에서 조금이라도 쓸모가 될 수 있다면, 저는 편안하고 매끄럽게 고요한 무덤 속으로 미끄러져 들 텐데요.\""]], [["Mr Toots said no more, but slipped out quietly and shut the door upon himself, to cut the Captain off from any reply.", "투츠 씨는 더 말하지 않고 조용히 빠져나가, 선장이 어떤 대꾸도 못 하게 자기 뒤로 문을 닫아 버렸다."]], [["Florence thought of this good creature, long after he had left her, with mingled emotions of pain and pleasure.", "플로렌스는 이 착한 사람이 떠난 뒤로도 오래도록 그를 생각했으니, 아픔과 기쁨이 뒤섞인 감정으로 그러했다."], ["He was so honest and warm-hearted, that to see him again and be assured of his truth to her in her distress, was a joy and comfort beyond all price; but for that very reason, it was so affecting to think that she caused him a moment’s unhappiness, or ruffled, by a breath, the harmless current of his life, that her eyes filled with tears, and her bosom overflowed with pity.", "그는 너무도 정직하고 따뜻한 마음을 지녀서, 그를 다시 보고 자기가 곤경에 처했을 때 그가 진실하다는 것을 확인받는 것은 무엇과도 바꿀 수 없는 기쁨이요 위안이었다. 그러나 바로 그 까닭에, 자기가 그에게 한순간의 불행을 안기거나, 한 가닥 입김으로 그 무해한 삶의 흐름을 흩뜨린다고 생각하니 너무도 가슴이 아파, 그녀의 두 눈에 눈물이 가득 차고 가슴이 연민으로 넘쳤다."], ["Captain Cuttle, in his different way, thought much of Mr Toots too; and so did Walter; and when the evening came, and they were all sitting together in Florence’s new room, Walter praised him in a most impassioned manner, and told Florence what he had said on leaving the house, with every graceful setting-off in the way of comment and appreciation that his own honesty and sympathy could surround it with.", "커틀 선장도 자기 나름의 방식으로 투츠 씨를 많이 생각했고, 월터도 그러했다. 저녁이 되어 모두가 플로렌스의 새 방에 함께 앉아 있을 때, 월터는 그를 더없이 열렬하게 칭찬했고, 그가 집을 나서며 했던 말을 플로렌스에게 전했는데, 자기 자신의 정직함과 공감이 그 말을 둘러쌀 수 있는 온갖 우아한 논평과 찬사의 꾸밈을 곁들였다."]], [["Mr Toots did not return upon the next day, or the next, or for several days; and in the meanwhile Florence, without any new alarm, lived like a quiet bird in a cage, at the top of the old Instrument-maker’s house.", "투츠 씨는 이튿날에도, 그다음 날에도, 또 며칠 동안 다시 오지 않았다. 그동안 플로렌스는 새로운 불안 없이, 늙은 계기 제작자 집 꼭대기에서 새장 속의 조용한 새처럼 살았다."], ["But Florence drooped and hung her head more and more plainly, as the days went on; and the expression that had been seen in the face of the dead child, was often turned to the sky from her high window, as if it sought his angel out, on the bright shore of which he had spoken: lying on his little bed.", "그러나 날이 갈수록 플로렌스는 점점 더 또렷이 기운을 잃고 고개를 떨구었고, 죽은 아이의 얼굴에서 보였던 그 표정을 그녀는 자주 높은 창문에서 하늘로 향했으니, 마치 그 아이가 밝은 기슭에 대해 이야기했던 그 천사를 찾는 듯했다. 아이는 자기 작은 침대에 누워 그렇게 말했었다."]], [["Florence had been weak and delicate of late, and the agitation she had undergone was not without its influences on her health.", "플로렌스는 최근 들어 약하고 여위었으며, 자기가 겪은 동요도 건강에 영향을 미치지 않은 것은 아니었다."], ["But it was no bodily illness that affected her now.", "그러나 지금 그녀를 괴롭히는 것은 육신의 병이 아니었다."], ["She was distressed in mind; and the cause of her distress was Walter.", "그녀는 마음이 괴로웠고, 그 괴로움의 원인은 월터였다."]], [["Interested in her, anxious for her, proud and glad to serve her, and showing all this with the enthusiasm and ardour of his character, Florence saw that he avoided her.", "자기에게 관심을 두고, 자기를 염려하고, 자기를 위해 봉사하는 것을 자랑스럽고 기뻐하며, 그 모든 것을 자기 성격다운 열의와 열정으로 드러내면서도, 플로렌스는 그가 자기를 피한다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["All the long day through, he seldom approached her room.", "긴긴 하루 내내 그는 좀처럼 그녀의 방에 다가오지 않았다."], ["If she asked for him, he came, again for the moment as earnest and as bright as she remembered him when she was a lost child in the staring streets; but he soon became constrained—her quick affection was too watchful not to know it—and uneasy, and soon left her.", "그녀가 그를 찾으면 그는 왔고, 그 순간만큼은 다시, 그녀가 사람들이 빤히 쳐다보던 거리에서 길 잃은 아이였을 때 기억하던 그대로 진지하고 환했다. 그러나 그는 곧 어색해졌고—그녀의 예민한 애정은 그것을 모르기에는 너무도 살피고 있었다—불안해졌으며, 곧 그녀를 떠났다."], ["Unsought, he never came, all day, between the morning and the night.", "부르지 않으면 그는 아침부터 밤까지 온종일 결코 오지 않았다."], ["When the evening closed in, he was always there, and that was her happiest time, for then she half believed that the old Walter of her childhood was not changed.", "저녁 어스름이 깃들면 그는 늘 거기 있었고, 그때가 그녀에게는 가장 행복한 시간이었으니, 그때만큼은 어린 시절의 그 옛 월터가 변하지 않았다고 반쯤은 믿을 수 있었던 까닭이다."], ["But, even then, some trivial word, look, or circumstance would show her that there was an indefinable division between them which could not be passed.", "그러나 그때조차도, 어떤 사소한 말이나 눈빛이나 정황이, 둘 사이에는 넘을 수 없는 형언키 어려운 간극이 있다는 것을 그녀에게 보여 주곤 했다."]], [["And she could not but see that these revealings of a great alteration in Walter manifested themselves in despite of his utmost efforts to hide them.", "그리고 그녀는, 월터에게 일어난 이 큰 변화의 이런 드러남이, 그것을 감추려는 그의 온갖 안간힘에도 불구하고 나타난다는 것을 알아채지 않을 수 없었다."], ["In his consideration for her, she thought, and in the earnestness of his desire to spare her any wound from his kind hand, he resorted to innumerable little artifices and disguises.", "그녀는 생각했다. 자기를 헤아리는 마음에서, 또 자기 친절한 손으로 그녀에게 어떤 상처도 입히지 않으려는 간절함에서, 그가 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 자잘한 꾀와 위장에 기대고 있다고."], ["So much the more did Florence feel the greatness of the alteration in him; so much the oftener did she weep at this estrangement of her brother.", "그럴수록 플로렌스는 그에게 일어난 변화의 크기를 더욱더 절감했고, 그럴수록 오라비가 이렇게 멀어지는 것에 더욱더 자주 눈물지었다."]], [["The good Captain—her untiring, tender, ever zealous friend—saw it, too, Florence thought, and it pained him.", "마음씨 착한 선장—지칠 줄 모르고, 다정하고, 한결같이 열성적인 그녀의 친구도—그것을 알아챘다고 플로렌스는 생각했고, 그것이 그를 아프게 했다."], ["He was less cheerful and hopeful than he had been at first, and would steal looks at her and Walter, by turns, when they were all three together of an evening, with quite a sad face.", "그는 처음만큼 명랑하고 희망에 차 있지 못했고, 저녁에 셋이 함께 있을 때면 자못 서글픈 얼굴로 그녀와 월터를 번갈아 가며 슬쩍슬쩍 바라보곤 했다."]], [["Florence resolved, at last, to speak to Walter.", "마침내 플로렌스는 월터에게 말하기로 마음먹었다."], ["She believed she knew now what the cause of his estrangement was, and she thought it would be a relief to her full heart, and would set him more at ease, if she told him she had found it out, and quite submitted to it, and did not reproach him.", "이제 그가 멀어지는 까닭을 알 것 같았고, 자기가 그것을 알아챘으며 거기에 온전히 따르고 그를 책망하지 않는다고 말한다면, 그것이 자기의 벅찬 마음에 위안이 되고 또 그의 마음도 더 편하게 해 주리라고 생각했다."]], [["It was on a certain Sunday afternoon, that Florence took this resolution.", "플로렌스가 이 결심을 한 것은 어느 일요일 오후였다."], ["The faithful Captain, in an amazing shirt-collar, was sitting by her, reading with his spectacles on, and she asked him where Walter was.", "충직한 선장은 놀라우리만치 큰 셔츠 깃을 단 채로 그녀 곁에 앉아, 안경을 쓰고 책을 읽고 있었는데, 그녀가 그에게 월터가 어디 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“I think he’s down below, my lady lass,”", "\"아래층에 있는 것 같다, 아씨.\""], ["returned the Captain.", "선장이 대답했다."]], [["“I should like to speak to him,”", "\"그분과 이야기를 좀 하고 싶어요,\""], ["said Florence, rising hurriedly as if to go downstairs.", "플로렌스가 마치 아래층으로 내려가려는 듯 황급히 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“I’ll rouse him up here, Beauty,”", "\"이리로 불러올리마, 아가씨,\""], ["said the Captain, “in a trice.”", "선장이 말했다. \"눈 깜짝할 새에.\""]], [["Thereupon the Captain, with much alacrity, shouldered his book—for he made it a point of duty to read none but very large books on a Sunday, as having a more staid appearance: and had bargained, years ago, for a prodigious volume at a book-stall, five lines of which utterly confounded him at any time, insomuch that he had not yet ascertained of what subject it treated—and withdrew.", "그러고는 선장이 무척 민첩하게 자기 책을 어깨에 둘러멨다—그는 일요일에는 더 점잖아 보인다는 이유로 아주 큼직한 책만 읽는 것을 자기 의무로 삼았던 것인데, 여러 해 전에 헌책 노점에서 어마어마한 책 한 권을 흥정해 사 두었으나, 언제든 다섯 줄만 읽어도 도무지 갈피를 못 잡을 지경이어서, 그 책이 무슨 내용을 다루는지조차 아직 알아내지 못한 터였다—그러고는 물러갔다."], ["Walter soon appeared.", "곧 월터가 나타났다."]], [["“Captain Cuttle tells me, Miss Dombey,”", "\"커틀 선장님이 말씀하시기를, 돔비 양,\""], ["he eagerly began on coming in—but stopped when he saw her face.", "그가 들어오며 열심히 운을 떼다가—그녀의 얼굴을 보고는 멈추었다."]], [["“You are not so well today.", "\"오늘은 몸이 좋지 않으시군요."], ["You look distressed.", "괴로워 보이세요."], ["You have been weeping.”", "우셨군요.\""]], [["He spoke so kindly, and with such a fervent tremor in his voice, that the tears gushed into her eyes at the sound of his words.", "그가 어찌나 다정하게, 또 목소리에 어찌나 뜨거운 떨림을 담아 말했던지, 그 말소리에 그녀의 두 눈에 눈물이 쏟아졌다."]], [["“Walter,”", "\"월터,\""], ["said Florence, gently, “I am not quite well, and I have been weeping.", "플로렌스가 부드럽게 말했다. \"저는 몸이 좀 안 좋고, 또 울고 있었어요."], ["I want to speak to you.”", "당신에게 할 말이 있어요.\""]], [["He sat down opposite to her, looking at her beautiful and innocent face; and his own turned pale, and his lips trembled.", "그는 그녀의 맞은편에 앉아, 아름답고 순결한 그녀의 얼굴을 바라보았다. 그러자 그의 얼굴이 창백해지고 입술이 떨렸다."]], [["“You said, upon the night when I knew that you were saved—and oh! dear Walter, what I felt that night, and what I hoped!—”", "\"당신은, 당신이 살아 돌아왔다는 걸 제가 알게 된 그날 밤에—아, 사랑하는 월터, 그날 밤 제가 느꼈던 것, 또 제가 바랐던 것이라니!—말씀하셨어요.\""]], [["He put his trembling hand upon the table between them, and sat looking at her.", "그가 둘 사이의 탁자 위에 떨리는 손을 얹고, 앉아서 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“—that I was changed.", "\"—제가 변했다고요."], ["I was surprised to hear you say so, but I understand, now, that I am.", "당신이 그렇게 말하는 걸 듣고 저는 놀랐지만, 이제는 제가 변했다는 걸 알아요."], ["Don’t be angry with me, Walter.", "저한테 화내지 마세요, 월터."], ["I was too much overjoyed to think of it, then.”", "그때는 제가 너무 기뻐서 그것을 생각할 겨를이 없었어요.\""]], [["She seemed a child to him again.", "그녀는 그에게 다시 어린아이처럼 보였다."], ["It was the ingenuous, confiding, loving child he saw and heard.", "그가 보고 들은 것은 그 천진하고, 믿음 가득하고, 사랑스러운 아이였다."], ["Not the dear woman, at whose feet he would have laid the riches of the earth.", "그가 그 발치에 이 세상의 부귀를 다 바쳤을 그 사랑하는 여인이 아니었다."]], [["“You remember the last time I saw you, Walter, before you went away?”", "\"제가 당신을 마지막으로 봤던 때를 기억하세요, 월터, 당신이 떠나기 전에요?\""]], [["He put his hand into his breast, and took out a little purse.", "그가 가슴속에 손을 넣어 작은 돈주머니 하나를 꺼냈다."]], [["“I have always worn it round my neck!", "\"저는 늘 이걸 목에 걸고 다녔어요!"], ["If I had gone down in the deep, it would have been with me at the bottom of the sea.”", "만약 제가 깊은 바닷속으로 가라앉았더라도, 이건 바다 밑바닥까지 저와 함께였을 거예요.\""]], [["“And you will wear it still, Walter, for my old sake?”", "\"그럼 앞으로도 이걸 지니실 거죠, 월터, 옛정을 봐서라도요?\""]], [["“Until I die!”", "\"제가 죽을 때까지요!\""]], [["She laid her hand on his, as fearlessly and simply, as if not a day had intervened since she gave him the little token of remembrance.", "그녀는, 마치 그 작은 기념의 정표를 그에게 건넨 그날로부터 단 하루도 지나지 않은 듯, 두려움 없이 또 꾸밈없이 자기 손을 그의 손 위에 얹었다."]], [["“I am glad of that.", "\"그게 기뻐요."], ["I shall be always glad to think so, Walter.", "저는 늘 그렇게 생각하면 기쁠 거예요, 월터."], ["Do you recollect that a thought of this change seemed to come into our minds at the same time that evening, when we were talking together?”", "그날 저녁 우리가 함께 이야기할 때, 이 변화의 생각이 우리 마음에 동시에 떠오르는 듯했던 걸 기억하세요?\""]], [["“No!”", "\"아뇨!\""], ["he answered, in a wondering tone.", "그가 의아한 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“Yes, Walter.", "\"그래요, 월터."], ["I had been the means of injuring your hopes and prospects even then.", "그때 이미 저는 당신의 희망과 앞날을 해치는 원인이 되어 있었어요."], ["I feared to think so, then, but I know it now.", "그때는 그렇게 생각하기가 두려웠지만, 이제는 알아요."], ["If you were able, then, in your generosity, to hide from me that you knew it too, you cannot do so now, although you try as generously as before. You do.", "그때 당신이 너그러이도 자기 역시 그걸 알고 있다는 걸 제게 감출 수 있었다 해도, 지금은 그러지 못해요, 아무리 전처럼 너그럽게 애쓰셔도요."], ["I thank you for it, Walter, deeply, truly; but you cannot succeed.", "당신은 애쓰고 계세요. 그것에 대해 깊이, 진심으로 감사드려요, 월터."], ["You have suffered too much in your own hardships, and in those of your dearest relation, quite to overlook the innocent cause of all the peril and affliction that has befallen you.", "하지만 당신은 성공하지 못해요. 당신은 자기 자신의 고난 속에서, 또 가장 사랑하는 친척의 고난 속에서 너무도 많이 고통받았기에, 당신에게 닥친 그 모든 위험과 고통의 무고한 원인을 도저히 못 본 척할 수가 없어요."], ["You cannot quite forget me in that character, and we can be brother and sister no longer.", "당신은 그런 처지의 저를 도무지 잊을 수 없고, 그래서 우리는 더 이상 오누이가 될 수 없어요."], ["But, dear Walter, do not think that I complain of you in this.", "하지만, 사랑하는 월터, 제가 이 일로 당신을 탓한다고 생각하지는 마세요."], ["I might have known it—ought to have known it—but forgot it in my joy.", "저는 그것을 알 수 있었어야—알았어야 했지만—기쁨에 겨워 잊었던 거예요."], ["All I hope is that you may think of me less irksomely when this feeling is no more a secret one; and all I ask is, Walter, in the name of the poor child who was your sister once, that you will not struggle with yourself, and pain yourself, for my sake, now that I know all!”", "제가 바라는 건 단 하나, 이 감정이 더 이상 비밀이 아니게 되었을 때 당신이 저를 좀 덜 괴롭게 떠올렸으면 하는 거예요. 그리고 제가 청하는 건 단 하나, 월터, 한때 당신의 누이였던 그 가엾은 아이의 이름으로, 이제 제가 다 알게 되었으니, 저 때문에 자기 자신과 씨름하며 스스로를 괴롭히지 말아 달라는 거예요!\""]], [["Walter had looked upon her while she said this, with a face so full of wonder and amazement, that it had room for nothing else.", "월터는 그녀가 이 말을 하는 동안 그녀를 바라보았는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 경이와 놀라움으로 가득 찼던지 다른 것이 들어설 자리가 없었다."], ["Now he caught up the hand that touched his, so entreatingly, and held it between his own.", "이제 그는 그토록 애원하듯 자기 손에 닿아 있던 그 손을 붙잡아, 자기 두 손 사이에 쥐었다."]], [["“Oh, Miss Dombey,”", "\"오, 돔비 양,\""], ["he said, “is it possible that while I have been suffering so much, in striving with my sense of what is due to you, and must be rendered to you, I have made you suffer what your words disclose to me?", "그가 말했다. \"제가 당신께 마땅히 돌려 드려야 할 것이 무엇인가 하는 의식과 씨름하느라 그토록 많이 괴로워하는 동안, 정작 당신께는 방금 그 말씀으로 제게 드러내신 그런 고통을 안겨 드렸다는 게 정말일까요?"], ["Never, never, before Heaven, have I thought of you but as the single, bright, pure, blessed recollection of my boyhood and my youth.", "결단코, 하늘에 맹세코, 저는 당신을 제 소년 시절과 청년 시절의 유일하고, 빛나고, 순결하고, 축복받은 추억으로밖에는 생각한 적이 없습니다."], ["Never have I from the first, and never shall I to the last, regard your part in my life, but as something sacred, never to be lightly thought of, never to be esteemed enough, never, until death, to be forgotten.", "처음부터 결코, 또 마지막까지 결코, 저는 제 삶 속에서 당신이 차지한 그 자리를, 신성한 것, 결코 가벼이 여겨선 안 될 것, 아무리 귀히 여겨도 모자랄 것, 죽을 때까지 결코 잊어선 안 될 것으로밖에는 여기지 않을 것입니다."], ["Again to see you look, and hear you speak, as you did on that night when we parted, is happiness to me that there are no words to utter; and to be loved and trusted as your brother, is the next gift I could receive and prize!”", "우리가 헤어지던 그날 밤처럼 당신이 바라보고 또 말하는 것을 다시 보고 듣는 것은, 제게 무어라 말로 다 할 수 없는 행복입니다. 그리고 당신의 오라비로서 사랑받고 신뢰받는 것은, 제가 받아 소중히 간직할 수 있는 그다음으로 큰 선물입니다!\""]], [["“Walter,”", "\"월터,\""], ["said Florence, looking at him earnestly, but with a changing face, “what is that which is due to me, and must be rendered to me, at the sacrifice of all this?”", "플로렌스가 그를 진지하게, 그러나 빛이 변해 가는 얼굴로 바라보며 말했다. \"이 모든 것을 희생하면서까지 제게 마땅히 돌려야 한다는 그것이 뭐죠?\""]], [["“Respect,”", "\"존경입니다,\""], ["said Walter, in a low tone.", "월터가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Reverence.”", "\"경의지요.\""]], [["The colour dawned in her face, and she timidly and thoughtfully withdrew her hand; still looking at him with unabated earnestness.", "그녀의 얼굴에 홍조가 떠올랐고, 그녀는 수줍게 또 생각에 잠겨 자기 손을 거두었다. 그러면서도 여전히 조금도 수그러들지 않은 진지함으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“I have not a brother’s right,”", "\"저에게는 오라비의 권리가 없습니다,\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."], ["“I have not a brother’s claim.", "\"저에게는 오라비의 자격이 없습니다."], ["I left a child.", "저는 한 아이를 두고 떠났습니다."], ["I find a woman.”", "그런데 한 여인을 만났습니다.\""]], [["The colour overspread her face.", "홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 온통 번졌다."], ["She made a gesture as if of entreaty that he would say no more, and her face dropped upon her hands.", "그녀는 그가 더 말하지 말기를 애원하는 듯한 몸짓을 했고, 얼굴을 두 손에 묻었다."]], [["They were both silent for a time; she weeping.", "둘은 한동안 말이 없었다. 그녀는 울고 있었다."]], [["“I owe it to a heart so trusting, pure, and good,”", "\"저는 그토록 믿음 가득하고, 순결하고, 착한 마음에 마땅히 그래야 합니다,\""], ["said Walter, “even to tear myself from it, though I rend my own.", "월터가 말했다. \"비록 제 마음이 찢어진다 해도, 그 마음에서 저 자신을 떼어 내기까지 해야 합니다."], ["How dare I say it is my sister’s!”", "어찌 감히 그것이 제 누이의 마음이라고 말하겠습니까!\""]], [["She was weeping still.", "그녀는 여전히 울고 있었다."]], [["“If you had been happy; surrounded as you should be by loving and admiring friends, and by all that makes the station you were born to enviable,”", "\"만약 당신이 행복했더라면, 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 사랑하고 흠모하는 친구들에 둘러싸이고, 당신이 태어나 누렸어야 할 그 신분을 부러움 사게 만드는 온갖 것에 둘러싸여 있었더라면,\""], ["said Walter; “and if you had called me brother, then, in your affectionate remembrance of the past, I could have answered to the name from my distant place, with no inward assurance that I wronged your spotless truth by doing so.", "월터가 말했다. \"그리고 그때 당신이 지난날을 다정하게 추억하며 저를 오라비라 불러 주었더라면, 저는 제 멀리 떨어진 자리에서도 그 이름에 응답할 수 있었을 겁니다, 그렇게 함으로써 당신의 티 없는 진실을 욕보인다는 마음의 거리낌 없이요."], ["But here—and now!”", "하지만 여기서—지금은!\""]], [["“Oh thank you, thank you, Walter!", "\"오, 고마워요, 고마워요, 월터!"], ["Forgive my having wronged you so much.", "제가 당신을 그토록 잘못 본 것을 용서해 주세요."], ["I had no one to advise me.", "제겐 조언해 줄 사람이 아무도 없었어요."], ["I am quite alone.”", "저는 완전히 혼자예요.\""]], [["“Florence!”", "\"플로렌스!\""], ["said Walter, passionately.", "월터가 격정에 차 말했다."], ["“I am hurried on to say, what I thought, but a few moments ago, nothing could have forced from my lips.", "\"저는 불과 몇 분 전만 해도 그 무엇도 제 입에서 끄집어내지 못하리라 여겼던 말을 내뱉도록 떠밀리고 있습니다. 만약 제가 형편이 넉넉했더라면, 만약 언젠가 당신을 당신의 본래 신분에 가까운 자리로 되돌려 드릴 만한 재력이나 가망이 조금이라도 제게 있었더라면, 저는 당신께 말씀드렸을 겁니다."], ["If I had been prosperous; if I had any means or hope of being one day able to restore you to a station near your own; I would have told you that there was one name you might bestow upon—me—a right above all others, to protect and cherish you—that I was worthy of in nothing but the love and honour that I bore you, and in my whole heart being yours.", "당신이 제게—단 하나의 이름을, 다른 무엇보다 앞서는 권리를, 곧 당신을 지키고 보살필 권리를 내려 주실 수 있다고요—제가 당신께 품은 사랑과 경의, 그리고 제 온 마음이 당신의 것이라는 사실 말고는 어느 것 하나 그럴 자격이 없는 저이지만요. 저는 당신께 말씀드렸을 겁니다."], ["I would have told you that it was the only claim that you could give me to defend and guard you, which I dare accept and dare assert; but that if I had that right, I would regard it as a trust so precious and so priceless, that the undivided truth and fervour of my life would poorly acknowledge its worth.”", "그것이야말로 당신이 제게 주실 수 있는 유일한 권리, 곧 제가 감히 받아들이고 감히 주장할 수 있는, 당신을 지키고 수호할 그 권리라고요. 그러나 만약 제게 그 권리가 있다면, 저는 그것을 너무도 귀하고 값을 매길 수 없는 신뢰로 여길 것이기에, 제 삶의 온전한 진심과 열정을 다 바쳐도 그 값어치를 보잘것없이밖에 못 갚을 거라고요.\""]], [["The head was still bent down, the tears still falling, and the bosom swelling with its sobs.", "머리는 여전히 수그러져 있었고, 눈물은 여전히 떨어졌으며, 가슴은 흐느낌으로 부풀어 올랐다."]], [["“Dear Florence!", "\"사랑하는 플로렌스!"], ["Dearest Florence!", "더없이 사랑하는 플로렌스!"], ["whom I called so in my thoughts before I could consider how presumptuous and wild it was.", "그렇게 부르는 것이 얼마나 주제넘고 무모한 일인지 헤아릴 겨를도 없이, 저는 마음속으로 당신을 그렇게 불렀습니다."], ["One last time let me call you by your own dear name, and touch this gentle hand in token of your sisterly forgetfulness of what I have said.”", "마지막으로 한 번만, 당신의 그 사랑스러운 이름으로 당신을 부르고, 또 제가 한 말을 누이로서 잊어 주신다는 표시로 이 부드러운 손에 손을 대게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["She raised her head, and spoke to him with such a solemn sweetness in her eyes; with such a calm, bright, placid smile shining on him through her tears; with such a low, soft tremble in her frame and voice; that the innermost chords of his heart were touched, and his sight was dim as he listened.", "그녀가 고개를 들어, 두 눈에 그토록 엄숙한 다정함을 담고, 눈물 너머로 그에게 그토록 고요하고 밝고 잔잔한 미소를 비추며, 그 몸과 목소리에 그토록 낮고 부드러운 떨림을 띠고 그에게 말했으니, 그의 마음 가장 깊은 현(絃)이 울렸고, 그것을 듣는 그의 눈앞이 흐려졌다."]], [["“No, Walter, I cannot forget it.", "\"아니에요, 월터, 저는 그것을 잊을 수 없어요."], ["I would not forget it, for the world.", "세상을 다 준다 해도 그것을 잊지 않겠어요."], ["Are you—are you very poor?”", "당신은—당신은 무척 가난한가요?\""]], [["“I am but a wanderer,”", "\"저는 그저 떠도는 사람일 뿐입니다,\""], ["said Walter, “making voyages to live, across the sea.", "월터가 말했다. \"먹고살려고 바다를 건너 항해를 다니는 거지요."], ["That is my calling now.”", "그게 지금 제 천직입니다.\""]], [["“Are you soon going away again, Walter?”", "\"또 곧 다시 떠나시나요, 월터?\""]], [["“Very soon.”", "\"아주 곧요.\""]], [["She sat looking at him for a moment; then timidly put her trembling hand in his.", "그녀는 잠시 그를 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 그러더니 수줍게 자기 떨리는 손을 그의 손에 얹었다."]], [["“If you will take me for your wife, Walter, I will love you dearly.", "\"저를 아내로 맞아 주신다면, 월터, 저는 당신을 진심으로 사랑할 거예요."], ["If you will let me go with you, Walter, I will go to the world’s end without fear.", "저를 당신과 함께 가게 해 주신다면, 월터, 저는 두려움 없이 세상 끝까지라도 가겠어요."], ["I can give up nothing for you—I have nothing to resign, and no one to forsake; but all my love and life shall be devoted to you, and with my last breath I will breathe your name to God if I have sense and memory left.”", "저는 당신을 위해 포기할 게 아무것도 없어요—내놓을 것도 없고, 버릴 사람도 없으니까요. 하지만 제 모든 사랑과 삶을 당신께 바치겠어요, 그리고 제게 분별과 기억이 남아 있는 한, 마지막 숨으로 당신의 이름을 하느님께 불러 올리겠어요.\""]], [["He caught her to his heart, and laid her cheek against his own, and now, no more repulsed, no more forlorn, she wept indeed, upon the breast of her dear lover.", "그가 그녀를 가슴으로 끌어당겨, 자기 뺨을 그녀의 뺨에 갖다 댔다. 그리고 이제 더는 밀쳐지지도, 더는 외롭지도 않은 채, 그녀는 정말로 울었다, 자기가 사랑하는 연인의 가슴에 기대어."]], [["Blessed Sunday Bells, ringing so tranquilly in their entranced and happy ears!", "축복받은 일요일의 종소리여, 넋을 잃고 행복에 겨운 그 두 사람의 귀에 그토록 평온하게 울려 퍼지누나!"], ["Blessed Sunday peace and quiet, harmonising with the calmness in their souls, and making holy air around them!", "축복받은 일요일의 평화와 고요여, 그들 영혼 속의 잔잔함과 어우러져 그들 둘레에 거룩한 공기를 자아내누나!"], ["Blessed twilight stealing on, and shading her so soothingly and gravely, as she falls asleep, like a hushed child, upon the bosom she has clung to!", "축복받은 황혼이 살며시 깃들어, 그녀가 잠들 때 그토록 다독이듯 또 진중하게 그녀를 그늘 지우누나, 마치 잠재운 아이처럼, 자기가 매달린 그 가슴 위에서 잠드는 그녀를!"]], [["Oh load of love and trustfulness that lies to lightly there!", "오, 그토록 가벼이 거기 놓인 사랑과 믿음의 무게여!"], ["Ay, look down on the closed eyes, Walter, with a proudly tender gaze; for in all the wide wide world they seek but thee now—only thee!", "그래, 감긴 두 눈을 내려다보아라, 월터, 자랑스럽고 다정한 눈길로. 이 넓디넓은 온 세상에서 그 눈이 지금 찾는 것은 오직 너뿐이니—오직 너뿐이니!"]], [["The Captain remained in the little parlour until it was quite dark.", "선장은 사위가 아주 캄캄해질 때까지 작은 응접실에 남아 있었다."], ["He took the chair on which Walter had been sitting, and looked up at the skylight, until the day, by little and little, faded away, and the stars peeped down.", "그는 월터가 앉아 있던 의자에 앉아, 채광창을 올려다보았는데, 그렇게 하는 사이 낮이 조금씩 조금씩 스러지고 별들이 빼꼼 내려다보았다."], ["He lighted a candle, lighted a pipe, smoked it out, and wondered what on earth was going on upstairs, and why they didn’t call him to tea.", "그는 초에 불을 붙이고, 파이프에 불을 붙여 다 태우고는, 도대체 위층에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는 건지, 또 왜 자기를 차 마시러 부르지 않는 건지 의아해했다."]], [["Florence came to his side while he was in the height of his wonderment.", "그가 한창 의아해하고 있을 때 플로렌스가 그의 곁으로 왔다."]], [["“Ay!", "\"오!"], ["lady lass!”", "아씨!\""], ["cried the Captain.", "선장이 외쳤다."], ["“Why, you and Wal”r have had a long spell o’ talk, my beauty.”", "\"이런, 아씨와 월터가 한참 동안 이야기를 길게 나누셨군요, 우리 아가씨.\""]], [["Florence put her little hand round one of the great buttons of his coat, and said, looking down into his face:", "플로렌스가 작은 손을 그의 외투의 커다란 단추 하나에 둘러 감고는, 그의 얼굴을 내려다보며 말했다."]], [["“Dear Captain, I want to tell you something, if you please.", "\"친애하는 선장님, 괜찮으시다면 한 가지 말씀드릴 게 있어요.\""]], [["The Captain raised his head pretty smartly, to hear what it was.", "선장은 그게 무슨 말인지 들으려고 꽤 잽싸게 고개를 들었다."], ["Catching by this means a more distinct view of Florence, he pushed back his chair, and himself with it, as far as they could go.", "그 덕에 플로렌스를 좀 더 또렷이 보게 되자, 그는 의자를 뒤로 밀고, 자기 몸도 의자째 갈 수 있는 데까지 한껏 물렸다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["Heart’s Delight!”", "마음의 기쁨이여!\""], ["cried the Captain, suddenly elated, “Is it that?”", "선장이 별안간 의기양양해져 외쳤다. \"그거예요?\""]], [["“Yes!”", "\"네!\""], ["said Florence, eagerly.", "플로렌스가 간절히 말했다."]], [["“Wal”r!", "\"월터! 신랑!"], ["Husband! THAT?”", "그거란 말이오?\""], ["roared the Captain, tossing up his glazed hat into the skylight.", "선장이 자기 번들거리는 모자를 채광창 쪽으로 휙 던져 올리며 우렁차게 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes!”", "\"네!\""], ["cried Florence, laughing and crying together.", "플로렌스가 웃으면서 동시에 울면서 외쳤다."]], [["The Captain immediately hugged her; and then, picking up the glazed hat and putting it on, drew her arm through his, and conducted her upstairs again; where he felt that the great joke of his life was now to be made.", "선장은 즉시 그녀를 와락 끌어안았다. 그러고는 번들거리는 모자를 주워 머리에 쓰고, 그녀의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼우고는 그녀를 다시 위층으로 데려갔다. 거기서 그는 이제 자기 인생 최대의 농담을 펼칠 때가 되었다고 느꼈다."]], [["“What, Wal”r my lad!”", "\"이런, 월터 얘야!\""], ["said the Captain, looking in at the door, with his face like an amiable warming-pan.", "선장이 더없이 흐뭇한 보온 냄비 같은 얼굴로 문 안을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“So there ain’t NO other character, ain’t there?”", "\"그래, 다른 자격은 없는 게로구나, 그렇지?\""]], [["He had like to have suffocated himself with this pleasantry, which he repeated at least forty times during tea; polishing his radiant face with the sleeve of his coat, and dabbing his head all over with his pocket-handkerchief, in the intervals.", "그는 이 익살에 하마터면 숨이 막힐 뻔했는데, 차를 마시는 동안 적어도 마흔 번은 그 말을 되풀이했고, 그 사이사이 외투 소매로 환하게 빛나는 얼굴을 닦고 손수건으로 머리를 온통 두드려 댔다."], ["But he was not without a graver source of enjoyment to fall back upon, when so disposed, for he was repeatedly heard to say in an undertone, as he looked with ineffable delight at Walter and Florence:", "그러나 그에게는 마음이 내킬 때 기댈 만한 더 진중한 기쁨의 원천도 없지 않았으니, 그가 월터와 플로렌스를 이루 말할 수 없는 기쁨으로 바라보며 낮은 소리로 이렇게 말하는 것이 거듭 들렸기 때문이다."]], [["“Ed’ard Cuttle, my lad, you never shaped a better course in your life, than when you made that there little property over, jintly!”", "\"에드워드 커틀, 이 사람아, 자네가 그 자그마한 재산을 공동 명의로 양도해 버렸을 때만큼 자네 평생 더 나은 항로를 잡은 적은 없었네!\""]], [["# CHAPTER LI. Mr Dombey and the World", "# CHAPTER LI. Mr Dombey and the World"]], [["What is the proud man doing, while the days go by?", "돔비 씨는 날들이 흘러가는 동안 무엇을 하고 있는가?"], ["Does he ever think of his daughter, or wonder where she is gone?", "그는 한 번이라도 자기 딸을 생각하는가, 아니면 그 애가 어디로 갔는지 궁금해하기라도 하는가?"], ["Does he suppose she has come home, and is leading her old life in the weary house?", "그 애가 집으로 돌아와, 그 적막한 집에서 예전의 생활을 이어 가고 있다고 짐작하는가?"], ["No one can answer for him.", "누구도 그를 대신해 답할 수 없다."], ["He has never uttered her name, since.", "그는 그 이후로 한 번도 그 애의 이름을 입에 올린 적이 없다."], ["His household dread him too much to approach a subject on which he is resolutely dumb; and the only person who dares question him, he silences immediately.", "그의 집안 식구들은 그가 단호히 입을 다물고 있는 화제에 다가서기에는 그를 너무도 두려워하고 있으며, 그에게 감히 물어볼 수 있는 단 한 사람마저도 그는 즉시 입을 막아 버린다."]], [["“My dear Paul!”", "\"오, 폴!\""], ["murmurs his sister, sidling into the room, on the day of Florence’s departure, “your wife!", "그의 누이가 플로렌스가 떠난 날 방 안으로 슬그머니 들어오며 중얼거린다. \"네 아내!"], ["that upstart woman!", "그 벼락출세한 여자 말이야!"], ["Is it possible that what I hear confusedly, is true, and that this is her return for your unparalleled devotion to her; extending, I am sure, even to the sacrifice of your own relations, to her caprices and haughtiness?", "내가 어렴풋이 들은 게 사실이라는 게, 그게 가능한 일이니, 그 여자가 너의 그 유례없는 헌신에—내가 장담컨대, 그 여자의 변덕과 오만에 맞춰 네 친혈육마저 희생시키기에 이른 그 헌신에—이런 식으로 보답한다는 게?"], ["My poor brother!”", "가엾은 내 동생아!\""]], [["With this speech feelingly reminiscent of her not having been asked to dinner on the day of the first party, Mrs Chick makes great use of her pocket-handkerchief, and falls on Mr Dombey’s neck.", "첫 번째 만찬회 날 자기가 저녁 식사에 초대받지 못했던 것을 절절히 떠올리게 하는 이 말과 함께, 칙 부인은 손수건을 한껏 동원하며 돔비 씨의 목에 매달린다."], ["But Mr Dombey frigidly lifts her off, and hands her to a chair.", "그러나 돔비 씨는 냉랭하게 그녀를 떼어내어 의자로 안내한다."]], [["“I thank you, Louisa,”", "\"고맙소, 루이자,\""], ["he says, “for this mark of your affection; but desire that our conversation may refer to any other subject.", "그가 말한다, \"네 애정의 이런 표시는 말이오. 하지만 우리 대화가 다른 어떤 주제로 옮겨 가기를 바라오."], ["When I bewail my fate, Louisa, or express myself as being in want of consolation, you can offer it, if you will have the goodness.”", "내가 내 운명을 한탄하거나, 위로가 필요하다고 토로하거든, 그때 친절을 베풀어 위로해 주면 되오, 루이자.\""]], [["“My dear Paul,”", "\"오, 폴,\""], ["rejoins his sister, with her handkerchief to her face, and shaking her head, “I know your great spirit, and will say no more upon a theme so painful and revolting;”", "그의 누이가 손수건을 얼굴에 댄 채 고개를 가로저으며 대꾸한다, \"나는 네 그 대단한 기개를 잘 알아. 그러니 이토록 고통스럽고 역겨운 주제에 대해서는 더 이상 말하지 않으마.\""], ["on the heads of which two adjectives, Mrs Chick visits scathing indignation; “but pray let me ask you—though I dread to hear something that will shock and distress me—that unfortunate child Florence—”", "이 두 형용사의 머리 위로 칙 부인은 통렬한 분노를 쏟아붓는다. \"하지만 제발 한 가지만 묻게 해 줘—나를 충격에 빠뜨리고 비탄에 잠기게 할 무언가를 듣게 될까 두렵긴 하다만—그 불행한 아이 플로렌스가—\""]], [["“Louisa!”", "\"루이자!\""], ["says her brother, sternly, “silence!", "그녀의 동생이 매섭게 말한다, \"조용히 해!"], ["Not another word of this!”", "이 얘기는 한마디도 더 하지 마!\""]], [["Mrs Chick can only shake her head, and use her handkerchief, and moan over degenerate Dombeys, who are no Dombeys.", "칙 부인은 그저 고개를 가로젓고 손수건을 쓰며, 더 이상 돔비답지 않은 타락한 돔비들을 두고 신음할 수 있을 뿐이다."], ["But whether Florence has been inculpated in the flight of Edith, or has followed her, or has done too much, or too little, or anything, or nothing, she has not the least idea.", "그러나 플로렌스가 이디스의 도주에 연루된 것인지, 아니면 그녀를 따라간 것인지, 너무 많은 일을 한 것인지, 너무 적게 한 것인지, 무언가를 한 것인지 아무것도 하지 않은 것인지, 그녀로서는 도무지 알 길이 없다."]], [["He goes on, without deviation, keeping his thoughts and feelings close within his own breast, and imparting them to no one.", "그는 한 치의 일탈도 없이 나아가며, 자기 생각과 감정을 제 가슴속에 단단히 가두어 누구에게도 내보이지 않는다."], ["He makes no search for his daughter.", "그는 딸을 찾는 일을 하지 않는다."], ["He may think that she is with his sister, or that she is under his own roof.", "그 애가 누이와 함께 있다고 여길 수도, 제 지붕 아래 있다고 여길 수도 있다."], ["He may think of her constantly, or he may never think about her.", "그 애를 끊임없이 생각하고 있을 수도, 한 번도 생각하지 않을 수도 있다."], ["It is all one for any sign he makes.", "그가 보이는 어떤 기색으로도 그건 매한가지다."]], [["But this is sure; he does not think that he has lost her.", "그러나 이것만은 분명하다. 그는 자기가 그 애를 잃었다고는 생각하지 않는다."], ["He has no suspicion of the truth.", "그는 진실을 조금도 의심하지 않는다."], ["He has lived too long shut up in his towering supremacy, seeing her, a patient gentle creature, in the path below it, to have any fear of that.", "그는 자기의 우뚝 솟은 우월함 속에 너무 오래 갇혀 지내며, 그 아래 길에서 인내심 강하고 온순한 존재로 있는 그 애를 보아 왔기에, 그런 두려움 따위는 품지 않는다."], ["Shaken as he is by his disgrace, he is not yet humbled to the level earth.", "자기의 치욕으로 흔들리기는 했어도, 그는 아직 땅바닥까지 낮아지지는 않았다."], ["The root is broad and deep, and in the course of years its fibres have spread out and gathered nourishment from everything around it.", "뿌리는 넓고 깊으며, 세월이 흐르는 동안 그 잔뿌리들은 사방으로 뻗어 주위 온갖 것에서 양분을 빨아들였다."], ["The tree is struck, but not down.", "나무는 일격을 맞았으나, 쓰러지지는 않았다."]], [["Though he hide the world within him from the world without—which he believes has but one purpose for the time, and that, to watch him eagerly wherever he goes—he cannot hide those rebel traces of it, which escape in hollow eyes and cheeks, a haggard forehead, and a moody, brooding air.", "비록 그가 자기 안의 세계를 바깥세상으로부터 숨긴다 해도—그 바깥세상은 지금으로서는 단 하나의 목적만을 지녔으며, 그것은 그가 가는 곳마다 그를 열심히 지켜보는 일이라고 그는 믿거니와—움푹 꺼진 두 눈과 뺨, 초췌한 이마, 침울하고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 분위기로 새어 나오는 그 세계의 반항적인 흔적들만큼은 숨길 수가 없다."], ["Impenetrable as before, he is still an altered man; and, proud as ever, he is humbled, or those marks would not be there.", "예전과 다름없이 속을 꿰뚫어 볼 수 없는 사람이지만, 그는 여전히 달라진 사람이며, 예전과 다름없이 오만하지만, 그는 꺾인 것이다. 그렇지 않다면 그런 흔적이 거기 있을 리 없다."]], [["The world. What the world thinks of him, how it looks at him, what it sees in him, and what it says—this is the haunting demon of his mind. It is everywhere where he is; and, worse than that, it is everywhere where he is not. It comes out with him among his servants, and yet he leaves it whispering behind; he sees it pointing after him in the street; it is waiting for him in his counting-house; it leers over the shoulders of rich men among the merchants; it goes beckoning and babbling among the crowd; it always anticipates him, in every place; and is always busiest, he knows, when he has gone away. When he is shut up in his room at night, it is in his house, outside it, audible in footsteps on the pavement, visible in print upon the table, steaming to and fro on railroads and in ships; restless and busy everywhere, with nothing else but him.", "세상. 세상이 그를 어떻게 여기는지, 그를 어떻게 바라보는지, 그에게서 무엇을 보는지, 그리고 무엇이라고 말하는지—이것이 그의 마음을 떠나지 않고 괴롭히는 악령이다. 그것은 그가 있는 곳이라면 어디에나 있고, 그보다 더 나쁜 것은, 그가 없는 곳에도 어디에나 있다는 점이다. 그것은 그와 함께 하인들 사이로 나오면서도, 동시에 등 뒤에 남아 수군대게 내버려 둔다. 그는 그것이 거리에서 자기 뒤를 손가락질하는 것을 본다. 그것은 그의 회계 사무소에서 그를 기다린다. 그것은 상인들 가운데서 부유한 자들의 어깨너머로 곁눈질한다. 그것은 군중 사이를 손짓하며 재잘대고 돌아다닌다. 그것은 어느 곳에서든 늘 그를 앞질러 가 있다. 그리고 그가 떠나고 나면 늘 가장 분주해진다는 것을 그는 안다. 밤에 그가 제 방에 틀어박혀 있을 때면, 그것은 그의 집 안에도, 그 바깥에도 있어, 보도를 밟는 발소리로 들리고, 탁자 위 인쇄물 속에 보이며, 철길 위로 또 배 안에서 이리저리 김을 뿜으며 다닌다. 어디서나 잠시도 쉬지 않고 분주하며, 오직 그 한 사람만을 화제로 삼는다."]], [["It is not a phantom of his imagination.", "그것은 그의 상상이 빚어낸 허깨비가 아니다."], ["It is as active in other people’s minds as in his.", "그것은 그 자신의 마음속에서만큼이나 다른 사람들의 마음속에서도 활발하다."], ["Witness Cousin Feenix, who comes from Baden-Baden, purposely to talk to him.", "일부러 그와 이야기를 나누려고 바덴바덴에서 찾아온 피닉스 사촌이 그 증인이다."], ["Witness Major Bagstock, who accompanies Cousin Feenix on that friendly mission.", "그 우정 어린 임무에 피닉스 사촌과 동행한 백스톡 소령이 그 증인이다."]], [["Mr Dombey receives them with his usual dignity, and stands erect, in his old attitude, before the fire.", "돔비 씨는 평소의 위엄으로 그들을 맞이하며, 예전의 자세 그대로 난롯불 앞에 꼿꼿이 선다."], ["He feels that the world is looking at him out of their eyes.", "그는 세상이 그들의 두 눈을 통해 자기를 바라보고 있음을 느낀다."], ["That it is in the stare of the pictures.", "그것이 벽에 걸린 그림들의 응시 속에 있음을."], ["That Mr Pitt, upon the bookcase, represents it.", "책장 위의 피트 씨가 그것을 대변하고 있음을."], ["That there are eyes in its own map, hanging on the wall.", "벽에 걸린 그 자신의 지도 속에도 눈들이 있음을."]], [["“An unusually cold spring,”", "\"유난히 추운 봄이오,\""], ["says Mr Dombey—to deceive the world.", "돔비 씨가 말한다—세상을 속이려고."]], [["“Damme, Sir,”", "\"빌어먹을, 선생,\""], ["says the Major, in the warmth of friendship, “Joseph Bagstock is a bad hand at a counterfeit.", "소령이 우정의 열기에 차서 말한다, \"조지프 백스톡은 위선 따위엔 영 서툰 사람입니다."], ["If you want to hold your friends off, Dombey, and to give them the cold shoulder, J.", "친구들을 멀리하고 냉대하고 싶으시거든, 돔비, J. B.는 그런 용도에 맞는 사람이 못 됩니다."], ["B. is not the man for your purpose.", "조는 거칠고 억센 사람이외다, 선생."], ["Joe is rough and tough, Sir; blunt, Sir, blunt, is Joe.", "무뚝뚝하지요, 선생, 무뚝뚝한 게 바로 조올시다."], ["His Royal Highness the late Duke of York did me the honour to say, deservedly or undeservedly—never mind that—‘If there is a man in the service on whom I can depend for coming to the point, that man is Joe—Joe Bagstock.’”", "고(故) 요크 공작 전하께서 황공하게도 저에게 이런 말씀을 해 주셨소이다, 그럴 만한 자격이 있든 없든—그건 아무래도 좋소—'요점으로 곧장 파고드는 데 있어 내가 믿을 만한 사람이 군대에 하나 있다면, 그게 바로 조다—조 백스톡이지.'\""]], [["Mr Dombey intimates his acquiescence.", "돔비 씨는 동의의 뜻을 내비친다."]], [["“Now, Dombey,”", "\"자, 돔비,\""], ["says the Major, “I am a man of the world.", "소령이 말한다, \"나는 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이오."], ["Our friend Feenix—if I may presume to—”", "우리 친구 피닉스도—내가 감히 말해도 된다면—\""]], [["“Honoured, I am sure,”", "\"영광이지요, 그럼요,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix.", "피닉스 사촌이 말한다."]], [["“—is,”", "\"—역시,\""], ["proceeds the Major, with a wag of his head, “also a man of the world.", "소령이 고개를 끄덕이며 말을 잇는다, \"세상 물정에 밝은 사람입니다."], ["Dombey, you are a man of the world.", "돔비, 당신도 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이오."], ["Now, when three men of the world meet together, and are friends—as I believe—”", "자, 세상 물정에 밝은 세 사람이 한데 모이고, 게다가 친구 사이라면—나는 그렇게 믿거니와—\""], ["again appealing to Cousin Feenix.", "그가 다시 한번 피닉스 사촌에게 호소한다."]], [["“I am sure,”", "\"그럼요,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix, “most friendly.”", "피닉스 사촌이 말한다, \"더없이 다정한 사이지요.\""]], [["“—and are friends,”", "\"—친구 사이라면,\""], ["resumes the Major, “Old Joe’s opinion is (I may be wrong), that the opinion of the world on any particular subject, is very easily got at.”", "소령이 말을 잇는다, \"늙은 조의 견해로는(내가 틀릴 수도 있소만), 어떤 특정한 주제에 대한 세상의 의견이란 아주 쉽게 알아낼 수 있는 거올시다.\""]], [["“Undoubtedly,” says Cousin Feenix.", "\"의심할 여지가 없지요,\""], ["“In point of fact, it’s quite a self-evident sort of thing.", "피닉스 사촌이 말한다. \"사실상, 그건 아주 자명한 종류의 일입니다."], ["I am extremely anxious, Major, that my friend Dombey should hear me express my very great astonishment and regret, that my lovely and accomplished relative, who was possessed of every qualification to make a man happy, should have so far forgotten what was due to—in point of fact, to the world—as to commit herself in such a very extraordinary manner.", "소령, 나는 내 친구 돔비가 내 말을 꼭 들어주었으면 하는 간절한 바람이 있소이다. 사람을 행복하게 해 줄 온갖 자질을 두루 갖추었던 나의 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 친척이, 마땅히 지켜야 할 바를—사실상, 세상에 대해 마땅히 지켜야 할 바를—그토록 까맣게 잊고서, 그토록 유별난 방식으로 처신하고 말았다는 데 대해 내가 품은 지극한 경악과 유감을 표하고 싶다는 거올시다."], ["I have been in a devilish state of depression ever since; and said indeed to Long Saxby last night—man of six foot ten, with whom my friend Dombey is probably acquainted—that it had upset me in a confounded way, and made me bilious.", "나는 그 이후로 줄곧 지독한 우울 상태에 빠져 있었소이다. 실로 어젯밤에는 롱 색스비에게—키가 6피트 10인치나 되는 사내인데, 내 친구 돔비도 아마 아는 사이일 거요—그 일이 나를 형편없이 뒤흔들어 놓고 속을 뒤집어 놓았다고 말했소이다."], ["It induces a man to reflect, this kind of fatal catastrophe,”", "이런 종류의 치명적인 파국은 사람으로 하여금 사건이란 게 참으로 섭리적인 방식으로 일어난다는 걸 곱씹게 만든다오,\""], ["says Cousin Feenix, “that events do occur in quite a providential manner; for if my Aunt had been living at the time, I think the effect upon a devilish lively woman like herself, would have been prostration, and that she would have fallen, in point of fact, a victim.”", "피닉스 사촌이 말한다, \"왜냐하면 만일 내 숙모가 그때 살아 계셨더라면, 그분처럼 지독히도 활달한 여인에게 미쳤을 영향은 그야말로 쇠약일 테고, 그분은 사실상 희생자가 되어 쓰러지고 마셨을 테니 말이외다.\""]], [["“Now, Dombey!—”", "\"자, 돔비!—\""], ["says the Major, resuming his discourse with great energy.", "소령이 대단한 기세로 말을 다시 잇는다."]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "\"실례하오,\""], ["interposes Cousin Feenix.", "피닉스 사촌이 끼어든다."], ["“Allow me another word.", "\"한마디만 더 하게 해 주시오."], ["My friend Dombey will permit me to say, that if any circumstance could have added to the most infernal state of pain in which I find myself on this occasion, it would be the natural amazement of the world at my lovely and accomplished relative (as I must still beg leave to call her) being supposed to have so committed herself with a person—man with white teeth, in point of fact—of very inferior station to her husband.", "내 친구 돔비가 이런 말을 하도록 허락해 주리라 믿소만, 이번 일로 내가 처해 있는 그 지독히도 고통스러운 상태에 무언가가 더해질 수 있다면, 그건 바로 나의 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 친척이(여전히 그렇게 부르도록 허락을 구해야겠소만), 자기 남편보다 훨씬 신분이 낮은 사람—사실상, 이가 새하얀 사내 말이오—과 그토록 처신해 버렸다고 여겨지는 데 대한 세상의 당연한 경악일 거외다."], ["But while I must, rather peremptorily, request my friend Dombey not to criminate my lovely and accomplished relative until her criminality is perfectly established, I beg to assure my friend Dombey that the family I represent, and which is now almost extinct (devilish sad reflection for a man), will interpose no obstacle in his way, and will be happy to assent to any honourable course of proceeding, with a view to the future, that he may point out.", "하지만 나는 내 친구 돔비에게, 다소 단호하게나마, 나의 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 친척의 죄가 완전히 입증되기 전까지는 그녀를 죄인으로 몰지 말기를 청하는 한편, 내 친구 돔비에게 이것만은 확언해 두고자 하오. 내가 대표하는 가문, 이제 거의 절멸 직전에 이른 그 가문은(사내에게는 지독히도 서글픈 생각이오만) 그의 앞길에 어떤 장애도 놓지 않을 것이며, 앞날을 내다보아 그가 제시할 수 있는 어떤 명예로운 처리 방식에도 기꺼이 동의하리라는 것이오."], ["I trust my friend Dombey will give me credit for the intentions by which I am animated in this very melancholy affair, and—a—in point of fact, I am not aware that I need trouble my friend Dombey with any further observations.”", "나는 내 친구 돔비가 이 지극히 우울한 일에서 나를 움직이고 있는 그 의도를 믿어 주리라 믿소. 그리고—어—사실상, 내 친구 돔비를 더 이상의 소견으로 번거롭게 할 필요는 없을 듯하오.\""]], [["Mr Dombey bows, without raising his eyes, and is silent.", "돔비 씨는 눈을 들지 않은 채 고개를 숙이고는 침묵한다."]], [["“Now, Dombey,”", "\"자, 돔비,\""], ["says the Major, “our friend Feenix having, with an amount of eloquence that Old Joe B. has never heard surpassed—no, by the Lord, Sir!", "소령이 말한다, \"우리 친구 피닉스가, 늙은 조 B.가 여태껏 그보다 더 뛰어난 것을 들어본 적이 없는—아니, 맹세코, 선생!"], ["never!”—says the Major, very blue, indeed, and grasping his cane in the middle—“stated the case as regards the lady, I shall presume upon our friendship, Dombey, to offer a word on another aspect of it.", "한 번도 없소!\"—소령이 정말이지 시퍼래져서는 지팡이의 한가운데를 움켜쥐며 말한다—\"그 정도의 웅변으로 부인에 관한 사정을 진술했으니, 나는 우리의 우정을 빌려, 돔비, 이 일의 또 다른 측면에 대해 한마디 하겠소."], ["Sir,”", "선생,\""], ["says the Major, with the horse’s cough, “the world in these things has opinions, which must be satisfied.”", "소령이 그 말울음 같은 기침을 하며 말한다, \"이런 일에 있어 세상은 의견을 가지고 있고, 그건 반드시 충족되어야 하는 거올시다.\""]], [["“I know it,”", "\"나도 알고 있소,\""], ["rejoins Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대꾸한다."]], [["“Of course you know it, Dombey,”", "\"당연히 알고 계시지요, 돔비,\" 소령이 말한다."], ["says the Major, “Damme, Sir, I know you know it.", "\"빌어먹을, 선생, 당신이 알고 있다는 걸 나는 알고 있소."], ["A man of your calibre is not likely to be ignorant of it.”", "당신만 한 그릇의 사람이 그걸 모를 리가 없지요.\""]], [["“I hope not,”", "\"모르지 않기를 바라오,\""], ["replies Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 대답한다."]], [["“Dombey!”", "\"돔비!\""], ["says the Major, “you will guess the rest.", "소령이 말한다, \"나머지는 짐작하실 거외다."], ["I speak out—prematurely, perhaps—because the Bagstock breed have always spoke out.", "나는 솔직히 말하겠소—어쩌면 너무 이른 것일지 모르나—백스톡 집안은 늘 솔직히 말해 왔으니까."], ["Little, Sir, have they ever got by doing it; but it’s in the Bagstock blood.", "그래서 얻은 게 별로 없긴 합니다, 선생. 하지만 그건 백스톡의 피에 흐르는 거올시다."], ["A shot is to be taken at this man.", "이 사내에게 한 발 쏘아야 하오."], ["You have J. B.", "당신 곁엔 J."], ["at your elbow.", "B.가 있소이다."], ["He claims the name of friend.", "그는 친구라는 이름을 자처하는 사람이오."], ["God bless you!”", "하느님의 가호가 있기를!\""]], [["“Major,”", "\"소령,\""], ["returns Mr Dombey, “I am obliged.", "돔비 씨가 대꾸한다, \"고맙소."], ["I shall put myself in your hands when the time comes.", "때가 오면 당신 손에 나를 맡기겠소."], ["The time not being come, I have forborne to speak to you.”", "아직 그때가 오지 않았기에, 나는 당신에게 말을 삼가고 있었던 거요.\""]], [["“Where is the fellow, Dombey?”", "\"그 작자는 어디 있소, 돔비?\""], ["inquires the Major, after gasping and looking at him, for a minute.", "소령이 한동안 숨을 헐떡이며 그를 바라본 끝에 묻는다."]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"모르오.\""]], [["“Any intelligence of him?”", "\"그자에 관한 무슨 소식이라도 있소?\""], ["asks the Major.", "소령이 묻는다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"있소.\""]], [["“Dombey, I am rejoiced to hear it,”", "\"돔비, 그 말을 들으니 기쁘오,\""], ["says the Major.", "소령이 말한다."], ["“I congratulate you.”", "\"축하하오.\""]], [["“You will excuse—even you, Major,”", "\"양해해 주시오—소령, 당신마저도,\""], ["replies Mr Dombey, “my entering into any further detail at present.", "돔비 씨가 대답한다, \"지금으로서는 더 이상의 자세한 이야기로 들어가는 것을 말이오."], ["The intelligence is of a singular kind, and singularly obtained.", "그 소식은 기이한 종류의 것이고, 기이하게 얻은 것이오."], ["It may turn out to be valueless; it may turn out to be true; I cannot say at present.", "별 가치 없는 것으로 드러날 수도 있고, 사실로 드러날 수도 있소. 지금은 뭐라 말할 수 없소."], ["My explanation must stop here.”", "내 설명은 여기서 멈추어야겠소.\""]], [["Although this is but a dry reply to the Major’s purple enthusiasm, the Major receives it graciously, and is delighted to think that the world has such a fair prospect of soon receiving its due.", "이것이 소령의 시뻘건 열의에 비하면 무미건조한 답변이기는 하나, 소령은 그것을 너그럽게 받아들이며, 세상이 곧 마땅한 보상을 받게 될 그토록 좋은 전망이 있다는 생각에 흡족해한다."], ["Cousin Feenix is then presented with his meed of acknowledgment by the husband of his lovely and accomplished relative, and Cousin Feenix and Major Bagstock retire, leaving that husband to the world again, and to ponder at leisure on their representation of its state of mind concerning his affairs, and on its just and reasonable expectations.", "그러고는 피닉스 사촌이, 자신의 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 친척의 남편으로부터 그에 합당한 감사의 표시를 받고, 피닉스 사촌과 백스톡 소령은 물러가며, 그 남편을 다시 세상과 마주하도록, 그리고 자기 일에 관한 세상의 심중을 그들이 대변한 바와, 세상의 정당하고 합당한 기대를 한가로이 곱씹도록 남겨 둔다."]], [["But who sits in the housekeeper’s room, shedding tears, and talking to Mrs Pipchin in a low tone, with uplifted hands?", "그런데 가정부 방에 앉아 눈물을 흘리며, 두 손을 치켜든 채 핍친 부인에게 낮은 목소리로 이야기하고 있는 이는 누구인가?"], ["It is a lady with her face concealed in a very close black bonnet, which appears not to belong to her.", "자기 것이 아닌 듯한, 아주 꼭 끼는 검은 보닛 속에 얼굴을 감춘 한 부인이다."], ["It is Miss Tox, who has borrowed this disguise from her servant, and comes from Princess’s Place, thus secretly, to revive her old acquaintance with Mrs Pipchin, in order to get certain information of the state of Mr Dombey.", "톡스 양인데, 이 변장을 자기 하녀에게서 빌려, 프린세스 플레이스에서 이렇게 은밀히 찾아와, 돔비 씨의 상태에 관한 어떤 소식을 얻으려고 핍친 부인과의 옛 친분을 되살리려는 참이다."]], [["“How does he bear it, my dear creature?”", "\"그분은 이 일을 어떻게 견디고 계세요, 여보세요?\""], ["asks Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 묻는다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"뭐,\""], ["says Mrs Pipchin, in her snappish way, “he’s pretty much as usual.”", "핍친 부인이 그 퉁명스러운 투로 말한다, \"거의 평소랑 똑같아요.\""]], [["“Externally,”", "\"겉으로는 그렇겠지요,\""], ["suggests Miss Tox “But what he feels within!”", "톡스 양이 말을 보탠다. \"하지만 속으로 느끼시는 건!\""]], [["Mrs Pipchin’s hard grey eye looks doubtful as she answers, in three distinct jerks, “Ah!", "핍친 부인의 단단한 잿빛 눈이 미심쩍은 빛을 띠며, 세 번 또렷이 끊어서 대답한다. \"아!"], ["Perhaps.", "글쎄요."], ["I suppose so.”", "그렇겠지요.\""]], [["“To tell you my mind, Lucretia,”", "\"솔직히 내 생각을 말하자면, 루크리셔,\""], ["says Mrs Pipchin; she still calls Miss Tox Lucretia, on account of having made her first experiments in the child-quelling line of business on that lady, when an unfortunate and weazen little girl of tender years; “to tell you my mind, Lucretia, I think it’s a good riddance.", "핍친 부인이 말한다—그녀는 여전히 톡스 양을 루크리셔라고 부르는데, 톡스 양이 가엾고 비쩍 마른 어린 계집아이였던 시절에 아이를 휘어잡는 그 사업의 첫 실험을 바로 이 부인에게 했던 까닭이다—\"솔직히 내 생각을 말하자면, 루크리셔, 잘 떨어져 나갔다 싶어요."], ["I don’t want any of your brazen faces here, myself!”", "나 같으면 그런 뻔뻔한 낯짝들은 여기 두고 싶지 않으니까!\""]], [["“Brazen indeed!", "\"뻔뻔하다마다요!"], ["Well may you say brazen, Mrs Pipchin!”", "정말 잘 말씀하셨어요, 뻔뻔하다고, 핍친 부인!\""], ["returned Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 대꾸했다."], ["“To leave him!", "\"그분을 떠나다니!"], ["Such a noble figure of a man!”", "그렇게 고결한 풍채의 사내를!\""], ["And here Miss Tox is overcome.", "그러고는 여기서 톡스 양은 감정에 북받친다."]], [["“I don’t know about noble, I’m sure,”", "\"고결한지 어떤지는 난 잘 모르겠고, 정말이지,\""], ["observes Mrs Pipchin; irascibly rubbing her nose.", "핍친 부인이 짜증스레 코를 문지르며 말한다."], ["“But I know this—that when people meet with trials, they must bear ’em.", "\"이건 알지요—사람이 시련을 만나면 그걸 견뎌야 한다는 거요."], ["Hoity, toity!", "어이구, 참!"], ["I have had enough to bear myself, in my time!", "나도 내 평생에 견딜 만큼 견뎌 봤어요!"], ["What a fuss there is!", "무슨 야단법석이람!"], ["She’s gone, and well got rid of.", "그 여자는 가 버렸고, 잘 치워졌고요."], ["Nobody wants her back, I should think!”", "아무도 돌아오기를 바라지 않을 거예요, 내 생각엔!\""]], [["This hint of the Peruvian Mines, causes Miss Tox to rise to go away; when Mrs Pipchin rings the bell for Towlinson to show her out, Mr Towlinson, not having seen Miss Tox for ages, grins, and hopes she’s well; observing that he didn’t know her at first, in that bonnet.", "페루 광산을 암시하는 이 말에 톡스 양이 떠나려고 일어서자, 핍친 부인은 종을 울려 타울린슨을 불러 그녀를 바래다주게 한다. 타울린슨 씨는 톡스 양을 오랜만에 본지라 씨익 웃으며 안녕하시냐고 인사하고는, 그 보닛 차림 때문에 처음엔 못 알아봤다고 말한다."]], [["“Pretty well, Towlinson, I thank you,”", "\"그럭저럭 잘 지내요, 타울린슨, 고마워요,\""], ["says Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말한다."], ["“I beg you’ll have the goodness, when you happen to see me here, not to mention it.", "\"부탁이 있는데, 혹시 여기서 나를 보게 되더라도 그 얘긴 하지 말아 줘요."], ["My visits are merely to Mrs Pipchin.”", "내가 찾아오는 건 그저 핍친 부인을 뵈러 오는 것뿐이니까요.\""]], [["“Very good, Miss,”", "\"잘 알겠습니다, 아가씨,\""], ["says Towlinson.", "타울린슨이 말한다."]], [["“Shocking circumstances occur, Towlinson,”", "\"끔찍한 일들이 다 일어나는군요, 타울린슨,\""], ["says Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 말한다."]], [["“Very much so indeed, Miss,”", "\"정말이지 그렇습죠, 아가씨,\""], ["rejoins Towlinson.", "타울린슨이 대꾸한다."]], [["“I hope, Towlinson,”", "\"나는 바라요, 타울린슨,\""], ["says Miss Tox, who, in her instruction of the Toodle family, has acquired an admonitorial tone, and a habit of improving passing occasions, “that what has happened here, will be a warning to you, Towlinson.”", "투들 가족을 가르치는 동안 훈계조의 말투와 지나가는 기회를 놓치지 않고 교화하려는 버릇을 익힌 톡스 양이 말한다, \"여기서 일어난 일이 당신에게 하나의 경고가 되기를 말이에요, 타울린슨.\""]], [["“Thank you, Miss, I’m sure,”", "\"고맙습니다, 아가씨, 그럼요,\""], ["says Towlinson.", "타울린슨이 말한다."]], [["He appears to be falling into a consideration of the manner in which this warning ought to operate in his particular case, when the vinegary Mrs Pipchin, suddenly stirring him up with a “What are you doing?", "그는 이 경고가 자기 같은 처지에서는 어떤 식으로 작용해야 마땅할지 곰곰이 생각하기 시작하는 듯한데, 그때 식초처럼 시큼한 핍친 부인이 불쑥 그를 들쑤시며 \"뭘 하고 있는 거예요? 어째서 아가씨를 문까지 모셔다 드리지 않고?\""], ["Why don’t you show the lady to the door?” he ushers Miss Tox forth.", "하고 다그치자, 그는 톡스 양을 밖으로 안내한다."], ["As she passes Mr Dombey’s room, she shrinks into the inmost depths of the black bonnet, and walks, on tip-toe; and there is not another atom in the world which haunts him so, that feels such sorrow and solicitude about him, as Miss Tox takes out under the black bonnet into the street, and tries to carry home shadowed it from the newly-lighted lamps.", "돔비 씨의 방을 지날 때 그녀는 검은 보닛 속 가장 깊숙한 데로 움츠러들어 발끝으로 걷는다. 그리고 그를 그토록 떠나지 않고 따라다니면서, 그에 대해 그토록 큰 슬픔과 염려를 품은 존재는 세상에 또 없으니, 톡스 양은 그 마음을 검은 보닛 속에 담아 거리로 가지고 나가, 새로 켜진 가로등 불빛으로부터 그것을 그늘 속에 감춰 집으로 가져가려 애쓴다."]], [["But Miss Tox is not a part of Mr Dombey’s world. She comes back every evening at dusk; adding clogs and an umbrella to the bonnet on wet nights; and bears the grins of Towlinson, and the huffs and rebuffs of Mrs Pipchin, and all to ask how he does, and how he bears his misfortune: but she has nothing to do with Mr Dombey’s world. Exacting and harassing as ever, it goes on without her; and she, a by no means bright or particular star, moves in her little orbit in the corner of another system, and knows it quite well, and comes, and cries, and goes away, and is satisfied. Verily Miss Tox is easier of satisfaction than the world that troubles Mr Dombey so much!", "그러나 톡스 양은 돔비 씨의 세상의 일부가 아니다. 그녀는 저녁 어스름이면 매일 다시 찾아온다. 비 오는 밤에는 보닛에 덧신과 우산을 보태고서. 그리고 타울린슨의 씨익 웃는 낯과 핍친 부인의 핀잔과 매정한 거절을 견뎌 낸다. 모두가 그저 그분이 어떻게 지내시는지, 그분이 불행을 어떻게 견디시는지 물어보기 위함이다. 하지만 그녀는 돔비 씨의 세상과는 아무 상관이 없다. 그 세상은 예전처럼 까다롭고 들볶아 대며 그녀 없이도 굴러간다. 그리고 그녀는, 결코 밝지도 특출나지도 않은 하나의 별로서, 또 다른 어떤 체계의 한구석에서 제 자그마한 궤도를 돌며, 그것을 아주 잘 알고서, 찾아오고, 울고, 떠나가며, 그것으로 만족한다. 참으로 톡스 양은 돔비 씨를 그토록 괴롭히는 세상보다 훨씬 만족시키기 쉬운 사람이다!"]], [["At the Counting House, the clerks discuss the great disaster in all its lights and shades, but chiefly wonder who will get Mr Carker’s place.", "회계 사무소에서는 사무원들이 그 엄청난 사달을 온갖 명암으로 따져 보지만, 무엇보다도 카커 씨의 자리를 누가 차지할지를 궁금해한다."], ["They are generally of opinion that it will be shorn of some of its emoluments, and made uncomfortable by newly-devised checks and restrictions; and those who are beyond all hope of it are quite sure they would rather not have it, and don’t at all envy the person for whom it may prove to be reserved.", "그들은 대체로 그 자리가 보수의 일부를 깎이고, 새로 고안된 견제와 제약으로 불편해지리라는 의견이다. 그리고 그 자리에 대한 일말의 희망조차 없는 자들은 차라리 그 자리를 맡지 않는 편이 낫겠다고, 그것이 누구 차지가 되든 조금도 부럽지 않다고 단언한다."], ["Nothing like the prevailing sensation has existed in the Counting House since Mr Dombey’s little son died; but all such excitements there take a social, not to say a jovial turn, and lead to the cultivation of good fellowship.", "돔비 씨의 어린 아들이 죽은 이래로 회계 사무소에서 지금처럼 들끓는 동요는 없었다. 그러나 그곳의 그런 흥분은 모두 사교적인, 아니 흥겹다고까지 할 만한 방향으로 흘러, 좋은 친목을 다지는 데로 이어진다."], ["A reconciliation is established on this propitious occasion between the acknowledged wit of the Counting House and an aspiring rival, with whom he has been at deadly feud for months; and a little dinner being proposed, in commemoration of their happily restored amity, takes place at a neighbouring tavern; the wit in the chair; the rival acting as Vice-President.", "이 상서로운 계기에 회계 사무소에서 인정받는 익살꾼과, 몇 달째 그와 사생결단의 반목을 벌여 온 야심 찬 경쟁자 사이에 화해가 이루어진다. 그리고 다행히 되살아난 그들의 우정을 기념하여 조촐한 만찬이 제안되어, 이웃 선술집에서 열리는데, 익살꾼이 좌장석에 앉고 경쟁자가 부좌장 노릇을 한다."], ["The orations following the removal of the cloth are opened by the Chair, who says, Gentlemen, he can’t disguise from himself that this is not a time for private dissensions.", "식탁보가 치워진 뒤 이어지는 연설은 좌장이 열며, 그는 말하길, 여러분, 지금이 사사로운 불화를 다툴 때가 아님을 자기 스스로도 숨길 수 없노라고 한다."], ["Recent occurrences to which he need not more particularly allude, but which have not been altogether without notice in some Sunday Papers, and in a daily paper which he need not name (here every other member of the company names it in an audible murmur), have caused him to reflect; and he feels that for him and Robinson to have any personal differences at such a moment, would be for ever to deny that good feeling in the general cause, for which he has reason to think and hope that the gentlemen in Dombey’s House have always been distinguished.", "굳이 더 자세히 언급할 필요는 없으나 몇몇 일요 신문과, 굳이 이름을 댈 필요 없는 어떤 일간지에서도 더러 다루어진 바 있는 최근의 사건들이(여기서 좌중의 다른 모두가 알아들을 만한 웅얼거림으로 그 신문 이름을 댄다) 자기로 하여금 곰곰이 생각하게 만들었노라고 한다. 그리고 자기와 로빈슨이 이런 순간에 사사로운 갈등을 품는다면, 그것은 돔비 회사의 신사 여러분이 늘 두드러져 왔다고 자기가 생각하고 또 바랄 만한 그 대의에 대한 선량한 마음씨를 영원히 부정하는 일이 되리라고 느낀다는 것이다."], ["Robinson replies to this like a man and a brother; and one gentleman who has been in the office three years, under continual notice to quit on account of lapses in his arithmetic, appears in a perfectly new light, suddenly bursting out with a thrilling speech, in which he says, May their respected chief never again know the desolation which has fallen on his hearth!", "로빈슨은 이에 한 사람의 사내답게, 또 한 형제답게 화답한다. 그리고 산수 실수 탓에 끊임없이 해고 통보를 받아 가며 삼 년째 사무소에 있던 한 신사가 완전히 새로운 면모를 드러내어, 짜릿한 연설을 불쑥 터뜨리는데, 그 가운데 그는, 우리의 존경하는 우두머리께서 그분의 가정에 닥친 그 황폐함을 다시는 겪지 않으시기를!"], ["and says a great variety of things, beginning with “May he never again,”", "하고 말하고, \"그분께서 다시는\" 으로 시작하는 갖가지 말을 잇따라 늘어놓으니, 우레와 같은 박수갈채로 환영받는다."], ["which are received with thunders of applause. In short, a most delightful evening is passed, only interrupted by a difference between two juniors, who, quarrelling about the probable amount of Mr Carker’s late receipts per annum, defy each other with decanters, and are taken out greatly excited.", "요컨대 더없이 유쾌한 저녁이 흘러가는데, 다만 카커 씨의 지난 연간 수입이 대략 얼마나 되었을지를 두고 다투던 두 풋내기 사이의 언쟁에 잠시 가로막힐 뿐이니, 이들은 디캔터를 들고 서로를 위협하다가 잔뜩 흥분한 채로 끌려 나간다."], ["Soda water is in general request at the office next day, and most of the party deem the bill an imposition.", "이튿날 사무소에서는 소다수가 두루 찾는 물건이 되고, 그 일행 대다수는 청구서를 바가지라고 여긴다."]], [["As to Perch, the messenger, he is in a fair way of being ruined for life.", "심부름꾼 퍼치로 말하자면, 그는 평생 신세를 망칠 판이다."], ["He finds himself again constantly in bars of public-houses, being treated and lying dreadfully.", "그는 다시 또 노상 술집 카운터에 처박혀, 한턱을 얻어먹고는 끔찍하게 거짓말을 늘어놓는다."], ["It appears that he met everybody concerned in the late transaction, everywhere, and said to them, “Sir,”", "들리는 바로는, 그가 어디서든 지난 사달에 관련된 사람이라면 누구든 만났고, 그들에게 경우에 따라 \"선생님\""], ["or “Madam,”", "혹은 \"마님\""], ["as the case was, “why do you look so pale?”", "하고는 \"어째서 그리 안색이 창백하십니까?\""], ["at which each shuddered from head to foot, and said, “Oh, Perch!”", "하고 물었다는 것인데, 그러면 저마다 머리끝부터 발끝까지 부르르 떨며 \"오, 퍼치!\""], ["and ran away.", "하고는 달아났다는 것이다."], ["Either the consciousness of these enormities, or the reaction consequent on liquor, reduces Mr Perch to an extreme state of low spirits at that hour of the evening when he usually seeks consolation in the society of Mrs Perch at Balls Pond; and Mrs Perch frets a good deal, for she fears his confidence in woman is shaken now, and that he half expects on coming home at night to find her gone off with some Viscount—“which,”", "이런 엄청난 짓들에 대한 자각 탓인지, 아니면 술 마신 뒤의 반동 탓인지, 퍼치 씨는 평소 발스 폰드에서 퍼치 부인과 함께하며 위안을 찾던 저녁 그 시각이면 극도의 의기소침에 빠진다. 그리고 퍼치 부인은 적잖이 애를 태우니, 이제 그가 여자에 대한 믿음이 흔들려, 밤에 집에 돌아오면 자기가 어느 자작과 눈이 맞아 달아나 버린 것을 발견하리라 반쯤 예상하고 있는 게 아닌가 두려운 까닭이다—\"이게 말이에요,\""], ["as she observes to an intimate female friend, “is what these wretches in the form of woman have to answer for, Mrs P.", "그녀가 어느 막역한 여자 친구에게 말하듯, \"여자 탈을 쓴 그런 못된 것들이 책임져야 할 일이라니까요, 피 부인."], ["It ain’t the harm they do themselves so much as what they reflect upon us, Ma’am; and I see it in Perch’s eye.”", "그것들이 제 몸에 끼치는 해악보다도, 그것들이 우리한테 어떻게 비치게 만드느냐가 문제예요, 마님. 그리고 그게 퍼치의 눈에 보이거든요.\""]], [["Mr Dombey’s servants are becoming, at the same time, quite dissipated, and unfit for other service.", "같은 시기에 돔비 씨의 하인들은 아주 방탕해져서, 다른 일자리에는 쓸모없게 되어 가고 있다."], ["They have hot suppers every night, and “talk it over” with smoking drinks upon the board.", "그들은 밤마다 거한 야식을 들고, 식탁에 김 나는 술을 올려놓고 \"그 얘기를 곱씹는다.\""], ["Mr Towlinson is always maudlin after half-past ten, and frequently begs to know whether he didn’t say that no good would ever come of living in a corner house?", "타울린슨 씨는 열 시 반만 지나면 늘 눈물을 글썽이며, 모퉁이 집에 사는 것은 결코 좋은 일이 못 된다고 자기가 말하지 않았느냐고 거듭 묻곤 한다."], ["They whisper about Miss Florence, and wonder where she is; but agree that if Mr Dombey don’t know, Mrs Dombey does.", "그들은 플로렌스 아가씨에 대해 수군대며, 그녀가 어디 있을지 궁금해한다. 하지만 돔비 씨가 모른다면 돔비 부인은 알 거라는 데 의견을 모은다."], ["This brings them to the latter, of whom Cook says, She had a stately way though, hadn’t she?", "이 말이 그들을 그 부인 이야기로 이끄니, 요리사가 이르길, 그분은 그래도 위풍당당한 데가 있었잖아요, 안 그래요?"], ["But she was too high!", "하지만 너무 도도했죠!"], ["They all agree that she was too high, and Mr Towlinson’s old flame, the housemaid (who is very virtuous), entreats that you will never talk to her any more about people who hold their heads up, as if the ground wasn’t good enough for ’em.", "모두가 그녀는 너무 도도했다는 데 동의하고, 타울린슨 씨의 옛 연인이었던 (아주 정숙한) 하녀는, 마치 땅바닥이 제 발을 디디기에도 모자란다는 듯 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들고 다니는 사람들 얘기는 자기한테 두 번 다시 꺼내지 말아 달라고 간청한다."]], [["Everything that is said and done about it, except by Mr Dombey, is done in chorus.", "이 일에 관해 입에 오르고 행해지는 모든 것은, 돔비 씨를 제외하고는, 합창으로 이루어진다."], ["Mr Dombey and the world are alone together.", "돔비 씨와 세상만이 단둘이 마주하고 있다."]], [["# CHAPTER LII. Secret Intelligence", "# CHAPTER LII. Secret Intelligence"]], [["Good Mrs Brown and her daughter Alice kept silent company together, in their own dwelling.", "착한 브라운 부인과 그녀의 딸 앨리스는 자기네 거처에서 말없이 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["It was early in the evening, and late in the spring.", "이른 저녁이었고, 봄도 깊어 가는 무렵이었다."], ["But a few days had elapsed since Mr Dombey had told Major Bagstock of his singular intelligence, singularly obtained, which might turn out to be valueless, and might turn out to be true; and the world was not satisfied yet.", "돔비 씨가 배그스톡 소령에게 그 기묘한 정보에 관해—기묘하게 손에 넣은, 아무 가치도 없는 것으로 드러날 수도 있고 사실로 드러날 수도 있는 그 정보에 관해—이야기한 지 며칠 지나지 않았고, 세상은 아직 만족하지 못한 채였다."]], [["The mother and daughter sat for a long time without interchanging a word: almost without motion.", "어머니와 딸은 오랫동안 한마디도 주고받지 않은 채 앉아 있었다. 거의 미동조차 없었다."], ["The old woman’s face was shrewdly anxious and expectant; that of her daughter was expectant too, but in a less sharp degree, and sometimes it darkened, as if with gathering disappointment and incredulity.", "노파의 얼굴은 약삭빠르게 초조하고 기대에 차 있었다. 딸의 얼굴 역시 기대에 차 있었으나 그 정도는 덜 날카로웠고, 때때로 그것은 차츰 짙어지는 실망과 불신이라도 어린 듯 어두워지곤 했다."], ["The old woman, without heeding these changes in its expression, though her eyes were often turned towards it, sat mumbling and munching, and listening confidently.", "노파는 그 표정의 변화에 아랑곳하지 않았는데, 비록 눈은 자주 그쪽을 향했으나, 그저 입을 우물거리고 무언가를 씹으며 확신에 차서 귀를 기울이고 앉아 있었다."]], [["Their abode, though poor and miserable, was not so utterly wretched as in the days when only Good Mrs Brown inhabited it.", "그들의 거처는 비록 가난하고 비참했으나, 착한 브라운 부인 혼자 살던 시절만큼 완전히 처참하지는 않았다."], ["Some few attempts at cleanliness and order were manifest, though made in a reckless, gipsy way, that might have connected them, at a glance, with the younger woman.", "청결과 정돈을 위한 몇 가지 시도가 엿보였는데, 다만 그것이 무심하고 집시 같은 방식으로 이루어진 터라 한눈에 보아도 그 젊은 여인과 결부 지을 만한 것이었다."], ["The shades of evening thickened and deepened as the two kept silence, until the blackened walls were nearly lost in the prevailing gloom.", "두 사람이 침묵을 지키는 사이 저녁 그림자가 짙어지고 깊어져, 마침내 거뭇해진 벽이 사방에 깔린 어둠 속에 거의 묻혀 버렸다."]], [["Then Alice broke the silence which had lasted so long, and said:", "그때 앨리스가 그토록 오래 이어진 침묵을 깨고 말했다."]], [["“You may give him up, mother.", "\"포기하셔도 돼요, 어머니."], ["He’ll not come here.”", "그 사람 여기 안 와요.\""]], [["“Death give him up!”", "\"죽어도 안 포기해!\""], ["returned the old woman, impatiently.", "노파가 조바심을 내며 응수했다."], ["“He will come here.”", "\"그자는 여기 올 거야.\""]], [["“We shall see,”", "\"두고 보면 알겠죠,\""], ["said Alice.", "앨리스가 말했다."]], [["“We shall see him,”", "\"그자를 보게 될 거다,\""], ["returned her mother.", "어머니가 응수했다."]], [["“And doomsday,”", "\"심판의 날에나요,\""], ["said the daughter.", "딸이 말했다."]], [["“You think I’m in my second childhood, I know!”", "\"내가 노망이 들었다고 생각하는 게지, 다 안다!\""], ["croaked the old woman.", "노파가 쉰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“That’s the respect and duty that I get from my own gal, but I’m wiser than you take me for.", "\"그게 내 친딸한테서 받는 존경이고 효도란 말이지. 하지만 난 네가 생각하는 것보다 더 영리해."], ["He’ll come.", "그자는 올 거야."], ["T’other day when I touched his coat in the street, he looked round as if I was a toad.", "며칠 전에 길에서 내가 그자의 외투를 건드렸더니, 마치 내가 두꺼비라도 되는 양 돌아보더구나."], ["But Lord, to see him when I said their names, and asked him if he’d like to find out where they was!”", "하지만 세상에, 내가 그 이름들을 입에 올리며 그 둘이 어디 있는지 알아내고 싶지 않으냐고 물었을 때 그자의 얼굴을 봤어야 하는데!\""]], [["“Was it so angry?”", "\"그렇게 화를 냈어요?\""], ["asked her daughter, roused to interest in a moment.", "딸이 순식간에 흥미를 느껴 정신이 번쩍 들어 물었다."]], [["“Angry?", "\"화를 내?"], ["ask if it was bloody.", "피라도 봤느냐고 물어라."], ["That’s more like the word.", "그게 더 어울리는 말이지."], ["Angry?", "화를 내?"], ["Ha, ha!", "하, 하!"], ["To call that only angry!”", "그걸 고작 화났다고 하다니!\""], ["said the old woman, hobbling to the cupboard, and lighting a candle, which displayed the workings of her mouth to ugly advantage, as she brought it to the table.", "노파는 절뚝거리며 찬장으로 가서 초에 불을 붙였는데, 그 초를 탁자로 가져오자 그녀의 입놀림이 보기 흉할 정도로 또렷이 드러났다."], ["“I might as well call your face only angry, when you think or talk about ’em.”", "\"그 둘에 대해 네가 생각하거나 말할 때 그 얼굴을 두고도 그저 화났다고밖에 못 할 게다.\""]], [["It was something different from that, truly, as she sat as still as a crouched tigress, with her kindling eyes.", "사실 그것은 그저 화난 것과는 달랐으니, 그녀는 웅크린 암호랑이처럼 꼼짝 않고 앉아 두 눈을 이글거리고 있었다."]], [["“Hark!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["said the old woman, triumphantly.", "노파가 의기양양하게 말했다."], ["“I hear a step coming.", "\"발소리가 들려."], ["It’s not the tread of anyone that lives about here, or comes this way often.", "여기 사는 사람이나 이쪽으로 자주 오는 사람의 걸음걸이가 아니야."], ["We don’t walk like that.", "우리는 저렇게 안 걷지."], ["We should grow proud on such neighbours!", "저런 이웃이 있으면 우리도 우쭐해지련만!"], ["Do you hear him?”", "저 소리 들리니?\""]], [["“I believe you are right, mother,”", "\"어머니 말씀이 맞는 것 같아요,\""], ["replied Alice, in a low voice.", "앨리스가 낮은 목소리로 대답했다."], ["“Peace!", "\"조용히!"], ["open the door.”", "문을 여세요.\""]], [["As she drew herself within her shawl, and gathered it about her, the old woman complied; and peering out, and beckoning, gave admission to Mr Dombey, who stopped when he had set his foot within the door, and looked distrustfully around.", "앨리스가 숄을 끌어당겨 몸을 감싸자, 노파는 그 말에 따랐다. 그러고는 밖을 내다보며 손짓해 돔비 씨를 들였다. 돔비 씨는 문 안에 발을 들여놓고는 걸음을 멈추고 미심쩍은 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“It’s a poor place for a great gentleman like your worship,”", "\"나리 같은 훌륭한 신사분께는 누추한 곳입지요,\""], ["said the old woman, curtseying and chattering.", "노파가 절을 하며 재잘거렸다."], ["“I told you so, but there’s no harm in it.”", "\"그렇게 말씀드렸습니다만, 해될 건 없습죠.\""]], [["“Who is that?”", "\"저건 누구요?\""], ["asked Mr Dombey, looking at her companion.", "돔비 씨가 그녀의 동행을 보며 물었다."]], [["“That’s my handsome daughter,”", "\"제 어여쁜 딸이옵니다,\""], ["said the old woman.", "노파가 말했다."], ["“Your worship won’t mind her.", "\"나리께서 신경 쓰실 것 없습니다."], ["She knows all about it.”", "저 애는 다 알고 있으니까요.\""]], [["A shadow fell upon his face not less expressive than if he had groaned aloud, “Who does not know all about it!”", "그의 얼굴에 그림자가 드리웠으니, \"다 알지 못하는 자가 어디 있겠는가!\" 하고 소리 내어 신음한 것이나 다름없이 분명한 표정이었다."], ["but he looked at her steadily, and she, without any acknowledgment of his presence, looked at him.", "그러나 그는 그녀를 똑바로 바라보았고, 그녀 역시 그의 존재를 아랑곳하지 않고 그를 바라보았다."], ["The shadow on his face was darker when he turned his glance away from her; and even then it wandered back again, furtively, as if he were haunted by her bold eyes, and some remembrance they inspired.", "그가 그녀에게서 시선을 거두자 얼굴의 그림자는 한층 짙어졌고, 그러고 나서도 그 시선은 슬그머니 다시 그녀에게로 되돌아갔으니, 마치 그녀의 당돌한 눈빛과 그것이 불러일으키는 어떤 기억에 사로잡히기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["“Woman,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said Mr Dombey to the old witch who was chuckling and leering close at his elbow, and who, when he turned to address her, pointed stealthily at her daughter, and rubbed her hands, and pointed again, “Woman!", "돔비 씨가 자기 팔꿈치 곁에서 낄낄거리며 곁눈질하는 늙은 마녀에게 말했다. 그녀는 그가 몸을 돌려 말을 건네자 슬그머니 제 딸을 가리키고는 두 손을 비비더니 다시 가리켰다."], ["I believe that I am weak and forgetful of my station in coming here, but you know why I come, and what you offered when you stopped me in the street the other day.", "\"이보게! 내가 여기 온 것은 나 자신이 약해서, 내 신분을 잊은 짓임을 알고 있네."], ["What is it that you have to tell me concerning what I want to know; and how does it happen that I can find voluntary intelligence in a hovel like this,”", "그러나 자네는 내가 왜 왔는지, 그리고 며칠 전 길에서 나를 멈춰 세웠을 때 무엇을 제안했는지 알 게야. 내가 알고자 하는 것에 관하여 자네가 내게 들려줄 게 무엇인가."], ["with a disdainful glance about him, “when I have exerted my power and means to obtain it in vain?", "또 어찌하여 내가 권력과 재력을 다 쏟고도 헛되이 얻지 못한 정보를, 이런 누추한 오두막에서 제 발로 얻을 수 있단 말인가,\" 그는 경멸하는 눈길로 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["I do not think,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["he said, after a moment’s pause, during which he had observed her, sternly, “that you are so audacious as to mean to trifle with me, or endeavour to impose upon me.", "그는 그녀를 엄하게 살핀 뒤 잠시 사이를 두었다가 말했다, \"자네가 나를 가지고 장난을 치거나 속이려 들 만큼 뻔뻔하지는 않을 게야."], ["But if you have that purpose, you had better stop on the threshold of your scheme.", "그러나 만일 그런 속셈이라면, 그 계략의 문턱에서 멈추는 편이 나을 게야."], ["My humour is not a trifling one, and my acknowledgment will be severe.”", "내 기분은 장난칠 만한 것이 못 되고, 내 보답은 가혹할 테니까.\""]], [["“Oh a proud, hard gentleman!”", "\"오, 거만하고 모진 나리시여!\""], ["chuckled the old woman, shaking her head, and rubbing her shrivelled hands, “oh hard, hard, hard!", "노파가 고개를 가로젓고 쪼글쪼글한 손을 비비며 낄낄거렸다. \"오, 모질고 모질고 모질도다!"], ["But your worship shall see with your own eyes and hear with your own ears; not with ours—and if your worship’s put upon their track, you won’t mind paying something for it, will you, honourable deary?”", "하지만 나리께서는 우리 눈이 아니라 나리 두 눈으로 직접 보시고 나리 두 귀로 직접 들으실 겁니다. 그리고 나리께서 그들의 자취를 좇게 되시면, 그 대가로 뭐든 좀 치르시는 것쯤은 마다하지 않으시겠지요, 안 그렇습니까, 귀하신 나리?\""]], [["“Money,”", "\"돈이라면,\""], ["returned Mr Dombey, apparently relieved, and assured by this inquiry, “will bring about unlikely things, I know.", "돔비 씨가 이 물음에 안도하고 확신을 얻은 듯 응수했다, \"있을 법하지 않은 일도 이루어 낸다는 것을 나도 아네."], ["It may turn even means as unexpected and unpromising as these, to account.", "이처럼 뜻밖이고 가망 없어 보이는 수단조차 쓸모 있게 만들 수도 있겠지."], ["Yes.", "그래."], ["For any reliable information I receive, I will pay.", "믿을 만한 정보를 받는다면 무엇이든 값을 치르겠네."], ["But I must have the information first, and judge for myself of its value.”", "그러나 먼저 그 정보를 손에 넣고, 그 가치를 내 스스로 판단해야겠네.\""]], [["“Do you know nothing more powerful than money?”", "\"돈보다 더 강한 것을 모르시나요?\""], ["asked the younger woman, without rising, or altering her attitude.", "젊은 여인이 일어서지도 자세를 바꾸지도 않은 채 물었다."]], [["“Not here, I should imagine,”", "\"여기서는 없을 듯싶군,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“You should know of something that is more powerful elsewhere, as I judge,”", "\"다른 곳에서는 더 강한 무언가가 있다는 걸 아셔야 할 텐데요, 제가 보기엔,\""], ["she returned.", "그녀가 응수했다."], ["“Do you know nothing of a woman’s anger?”", "\"여자의 분노라는 걸 모르시나요?\""]], [["“You have a saucy tongue, Jade,”", "\"입버릇이 건방진 계집이로구나,\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Not usually,”", "\"평소엔 안 그래요,\""], ["she answered, without any show of emotion: “I speak to you now, that you may understand us better, and rely more on us.", "그녀가 아무 감정도 드러내지 않고 대답했다. \"지금 당신에게 말하는 것은, 우리를 더 잘 이해하고 우리를 더 믿게 하려는 거예요."], ["A woman’s anger is pretty much the same here, as in your fine house. I am angry.", "여자의 분노란 여기서나 당신의 훌륭한 저택에서나 거의 똑같답니다."], ["I have been so, many years.", "나는 화가 나 있어요."], ["I have as good cause for my anger as you have for yours, and its object is the same man.”", "여러 해 동안 그래 왔지요. 내가 화낼 까닭은 당신 못지않게 충분하고, 그 대상은 똑같은 남자예요.\""]], [["He started, in spite of himself, and looked at her with astonishment.", "그는 자기도 모르게 흠칫하며 놀란 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["she said, with a kind of laugh.", "그녀가 일종의 웃음과 함께 말했다."], ["“Wide as the distance may seem between us, it is so.", "\"우리 사이가 아무리 멀어 보여도, 사실이 그래요."], ["How it is so, is no matter; that is my story, and I keep my story to myself.", "어찌하여 그런지는 중요치 않아요. 그건 내 사연이고, 내 사연은 나 혼자 간직할 테니까."], ["I would bring you and him together, because I have a rage against him.", "나는 당신과 그자를 한자리에 끌어다 놓으려 해요. 그자에게 격렬한 분노를 품고 있으니까요."], ["My mother there, is avaricious and poor; and she would sell any tidings she could glean, or anything, or anybody, for money.", "저기 계신 우리 어머니는 욕심 많고 가난한 분이에요. 어머니라면 주워 모을 수 있는 어떤 소식이든, 어떤 것이든, 어떤 사람이든, 돈을 받고 팔아 치울 거예요."], ["It is fair enough, perhaps, that you should pay her some, if she can help you to what you want to know.", "어머니가 당신이 알고 싶어 하는 것을 손에 넣게 도와준다면, 어머니께 얼마쯤 치르는 게 온당하기는 하겠죠."], ["But that is not my motive.", "하지만 그건 내 동기가 아니에요."], ["I have told you what mine is, and it would be as strong and all-sufficient with me if you haggled and bargained with her for a sixpence.", "내 동기가 무엇인지는 이미 말했고, 설령 당신이 어머니와 육 펜스를 두고 흥정하고 깎아 댄다 해도 그 동기는 내게 똑같이 강하고 충분하답니다. 나는 할 말을 다 했어요."], ["I have done. My saucy tongue says no more, if you wait here till sunrise tomorrow.”", "내 건방진 혀는 더는 말하지 않을 거예요, 당신이 내일 해 뜰 때까지 여기서 기다린다 해도요.\""]], [["The old woman, who had shown great uneasiness during this speech, which had a tendency to depreciate her expected gains, pulled Mr Dombey softly by the sleeve, and whispered to him not to mind her.", "이 말이 자기가 기대하던 벌이를 깎아내리는 쪽으로 흐르자 적잖이 불안해하던 노파는, 돔비 씨의 소매를 살며시 잡아당기며 딸의 말은 신경 쓰지 말라고 속삭였다."], ["He glared at them both, by turns, with a haggard look, and said, in a deeper voice than was usual with him:", "그는 초췌한 얼굴로 두 사람을 번갈아 노려보더니, 평소보다 한층 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Go on—what do you know?”", "\"계속해 보게—무엇을 알고 있는가?\""]], [["“Oh, not so fast, your worship!", "\"오, 그리 서두르지 마십쇼, 나리!"], ["we must wait for someone,”", "누군가를 기다려야 합니다요,\""], ["answered the old woman.", "노파가 대답했다."], ["“It’s to be got from someone else—wormed out—screwed and twisted from him.”", "\"그건 다른 사람한테서 얻어 내야 하는 겁니다—파내야지요—쥐어짜고 비틀어서 그자한테서 끄집어내야 한단 말입니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 소린가?\""], ["said Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Patience,”", "\"인내심을 가지셔야지요,\""], ["she croaked, laying her hand, like a claw, upon his arm.", "그녀는 갈고리 같은 손을 그의 팔 위에 얹으며 쉰 소리로 말했다."], ["“Patience.", "\"인내심을요."], ["I’ll get at it.", "제가 알아내겠습니다."], ["I know I can!", "할 수 있다는 걸 압니다요!"], ["If he was to hold it back from me,”", "그자가 그걸 내게서 감추려 든다면,\""], ["said Good Mrs Brown, crooking her ten fingers, “I’d tear it out of him!”", "착한 브라운 부인이 열 손가락을 갈고리처럼 구부리며 말했다, \"그자한테서 뜯어내고야 말 겁니다!\""]], [["Mr Dombey followed her with his eyes as she hobbled to the door, and looked out again: and then his glance sought her daughter; but she remained impassive, silent, and regardless of him.", "돔비 씨는 그녀가 절뚝거리며 문으로 가서 다시 밖을 내다보는 동안 눈으로 그녀를 좇았다. 그러고는 그 시선이 그녀의 딸을 찾았다. 그러나 그녀는 무표정하고 말없이, 그에게 아랑곳하지 않은 채 그대로 있었다."]], [["“Do you tell me, woman,”", "\"이보게, 자네 말은,\""], ["he said, when the bent figure of Mrs Brown came back, shaking its head and chattering to itself, “that there is another person expected here?”", "그는 브라운 부인의 굽은 형체가 고개를 가로저으며 혼자 재잘거리며 돌아오자 말했다, \"여기 또 다른 사람이 오기로 되어 있다는 건가?\""]], [["“Yes!”", "\"그렇습니다!\""], ["said the old woman, looking up into his face, and nodding.", "노파가 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“From whom you are to exact the intelligence that is to be useful to me?”", "\"그자에게서 내게 쓸모 있을 정보를 캐낼 작정이고?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇습죠,\""], ["said the old woman, nodding again.", "노파가 다시 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["“A stranger?”", "\"낯선 자인가?\""]], [["“Chut!”", "\"체!\""], ["said the old woman, with a shrill laugh.", "노파가 날카로운 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다."], ["“What signifies!", "\"그게 무슨 상관이람!"], ["Well, well; no.", "그래, 그래, 아닙니다."], ["No stranger to your worship.", "나리께 낯선 자는 아닙죠."], ["But he won’t see you.", "하지만 그자는 나리를 못 봅니다."], ["He’d be afraid of you, and wouldn’t talk.", "나리를 무서워해서 입을 안 열 테니까요."], ["You’ll stand behind that door, and judge him for yourself.", "나리는 저 문 뒤에 서 계시면서, 그자를 직접 판단하시면 됩니다."], ["We don’t ask to be believed on trust What!", "우리는 그냥 믿어 달라고 청하는 게 아닙니다."], ["Your worship doubts the room behind the door?", "뭐라굽쇼! 나리께선 저 문 뒤의 방이 미심쩍으신 게로군요?"], ["Oh the suspicion of you rich gentlefolks!", "오, 부자 양반들의 의심이라니!"], ["Look at it, then.”", "그럼, 들여다보십쇼.\""]], [["Her sharp eye had detected an involuntary expression of this feeling on his part, which was not unreasonable under the circumstances.", "그녀의 날카로운 눈은 그가 본의 아니게 드러낸 이 감정의 기색을 알아챘는데, 그것은 이런 상황에서 무리도 아니었다."], ["In satisfaction of it she now took the candle to the door she spoke of.", "그녀는 그 감정을 풀어 주려고 자기가 말한 문으로 초를 가져갔다."], ["Mr Dombey looked in; assured himself that it was an empty, crazy room; and signed to her to put the light back in its place.", "돔비 씨는 안을 들여다보고 그것이 텅 비고 다 쓰러져 가는 방임을 확인하더니, 초를 제자리에 도로 갖다 놓으라고 손짓했다."]], [["“How long,”", "\"얼마나 있어야,\""], ["he asked, “before this person comes?”", "그가 물었다, \"이 사람이 오는가?\""]], [["“Not long,” she answered.", "\"오래 안 걸립니다,\""], ["“Would your worship sit down for a few odd minutes?”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"나리께서 잠깐 짬을 내어 앉아 계시겠습니까?\""]], [["He made no answer; but began pacing the room with an irresolute air, as if he were undecided whether to remain or depart, and as if he had some quarrel with himself for being there at all.", "그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 머무를지 떠날지 결단을 내리지 못한 듯, 또 애초에 여기 와 있다는 사실로 자기 자신과 다투기라도 하는 듯, 망설이는 기색으로 방 안을 거닐기 시작했다."], ["But soon his tread grew slower and heavier, and his face more sternly thoughtful; as the object with which he had come, fixed itself in his mind, and dilated there again.", "그러나 곧 그의 발걸음은 더 느리고 무거워졌고, 그의 얼굴은 한층 엄숙하게 생각에 잠겼다. 그가 여기 온 목적이 마음속에 또렷이 자리 잡고 다시금 부풀어 올랐기 때문이었다."]], [["While he thus walked up and down with his eyes on the ground, Mrs Brown, in the chair from which she had risen to receive him, sat listening anew.", "그가 이렇게 눈을 바닥에 두고 이리저리 거니는 동안, 그를 맞으려고 일어났던 의자에 다시 앉은 브라운 부인은 새로이 귀를 기울였다."], ["The monotony of his step, or the uncertainty of age, made her so slow of hearing, that a footfall without had sounded in her daughter’s ears for some moments, and she had looked up hastily to warn her mother of its approach, before the old woman was roused by it.", "그의 단조로운 발소리 탓인지, 늙은 탓에 흐릿해진 귀 탓인지, 그녀는 듣는 게 어찌나 더딘지, 바깥의 발소리가 딸의 귀에는 이미 몇 순간 동안 울렸고 딸이 황급히 고개를 들어 그 다가옴을 어머니에게 알리려 한 뒤에야 비로소 노파가 그 소리에 정신을 차렸다."], ["But then she started from her seat, and whispering “Here he is!”", "그러나 그제야 그녀는 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 \"그자가 왔다!\""], ["hurried her visitor to his place of observation, and put a bottle and glass upon the table, with such alacrity, as to be ready to fling her arms round the neck of Rob the Grinder on his appearance at the door.", "하고 속삭이고는 손님을 그 망보는 자리로 서둘러 데려갔고, 술병과 잔을 탁자 위에 놓되 어찌나 재빠른지 문간에 모습을 드러낸 연마공 롭의 목에 두 팔을 두를 채비까지 갖추었다."]], [["“And here’s my bonny boy,”", "\"드디어 내 어여쁜 아들이 왔구나,\""], ["cried Mrs Brown, “at last!—oho, oho!", "브라운 부인이 외쳤다, \"마침내!—오호, 오호!"], ["You’re like my own son, Robby!”", "넌 꼭 내 친아들 같다니까, 로비!\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Misses Brown!”", "브라운 아주머니!\""], ["remonstrated the Grinder. “Don’t!", "연마공이 항의했다."], ["Can’t you be fond of a cove without squeedging and throttling of him?", "\"이러지 마세요! 사람을 짓누르고 목을 조르지 않고선 좋아할 수가 없는 거예요?"], ["Take care of the birdcage in my hand, will you?”", "내 손에 든 새장 좀 조심하라니까요?\""]], [["“Thinks of a birdcage, afore me!”", "\"나보다 새장을 먼저 챙기다니!\""], ["cried the old woman, apostrophizing the ceiling.", "노파가 천장을 향해 부르짖었다."], ["“Me that feels more than a mother for him!”", "\"어미보다 더한 정을 이 애한테 쏟는 나를 두고!\""]], [["“Well, I’m sure I’m very much obliged to you, Misses Brown,”", "\"아니, 정말이지 저도 아주머니께 무척 감사해요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["said the unfortunate youth, greatly aggravated; “but you’re so jealous of a cove.", "불운한 청년이 잔뜩 짜증을 내며 말했다, \"하지만 아주머닌 사람한테 너무 집착하신다니까요."], ["I’m very fond of you myself, and all that, of course; but I don’t smother you, do I, Misses Brown?”", "저도 물론 아주머니를 무척 좋아하고, 다 그렇긴 하지만요. 그래도 전 아주머니를 숨 막히게 짓누르진 않잖아요, 안 그래요, 브라운 아주머니?\""]], [["He looked and spoke as if he would have been far from objecting to do so, however, on a favourable occasion.", "하긴 그 표정과 말투를 보면, 적당한 기회만 있다면 그렇게 짓누르는 것도 결코 마다하지 않을 듯했다."]], [["“And to talk about birdcages, too!”", "\"새장 얘기까지 꺼내다니!\""], ["whimpered the Grinder.", "연마공이 훌쩍거렸다."], ["“As if that was a crime!", "\"그게 무슨 죄라도 되는 양!"], ["Why, look’ee here!", "자, 여기 좀 보세요!"], ["Do you know who this belongs to?”", "이게 누구 건지 아세요?\""]], [["“To Master, dear?”", "\"주인 양반 거겠지, 얘야?\""], ["said the old woman with a grin.", "노파가 씩 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"맞아요!\""], ["replied the Grinder, lifting a large cage tied up in a wrapper, on the table, and untying it with his teeth and hands.", "연마공이 천으로 싼 커다란 새장을 탁자 위로 들어 올리며, 이로 물고 손으로 풀어 헤치며 대답했다."], ["“It’s our parrot, this is.”", "\"이건 우리 앵무새예요, 이게.\""]], [["“Mr Carker’s parrot, Rob?”", "\"카커 씨의 앵무새냐, 롭?\""]], [["“Will you hold your tongue, Misses Brown?”", "\"입 좀 다물어 주시겠어요, 브라운 아주머니?\""], ["returned the goaded Grinder.", "다그침에 시달린 연마공이 응수했다."], ["“What do you go naming names for?", "\"왜 자꾸 이름을 들먹이는 거예요?"], ["I’m blest,”", "정말이지,\""], ["said Rob, pulling his hair with both hands in the exasperation of his feelings, “if she ain’t enough to make a cove run wild!”", "롭이 격해진 감정에 두 손으로 머리를 쥐어뜯으며 말했다, \"이 아주머닌 사람을 미치게 만들기 딱이라니까!\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐!"], ["Do you snub me, thankless boy!”", "이 배은망덕한 놈, 나한테 핀잔을 줘!\""], ["cried the old woman, with ready vehemence.", "노파가 곧장 사납게 외쳤다."]], [["“Good gracious, Misses Brown, no!”", "\"맙소사, 브라운 아주머니, 아니에요!\""], ["returned the Grinder, with tears in his eyes.", "연마공이 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 응수했다."], ["“Was there ever such a—!", "\"이렇게 사람이—!"], ["Don’t I dote upon you, Misses Brown?”", "제가 아주머니를 끔찍이 아끼지 않나요, 브라운 아주머니?\""]], [["“Do you, sweet Rob?", "\"정말이냐, 귀여운 롭아?"], ["Do you truly, chickabiddy?”", "진짜로 그러냐, 우리 병아리야?\""], ["With that, Mrs Brown held him in her fond embrace once more; and did not release him until he had made several violent and ineffectual struggles with his legs, and his hair was standing on end all over his head.", "그 말과 함께 브라운 부인은 그를 다시 한번 다정한 포옹으로 끌어안았고, 그가 다리로 몇 차례 격렬하고도 헛된 발버둥을 치고 머리카락이 온통 곤두설 때까지 놓아주지 않았다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["returned the Grinder, “what a thing it is to be perfectly pitched into with affection like this here.", "연마공이 응수했다, \"이렇게 사랑 공세에 흠씬 두들겨 맞다니 이게 무슨 꼴이람."], ["I wish she was—How have you been, Misses Brown?”", "차라리 저 아주머니가—요즘 어떻게 지내셨어요, 브라운 아주머니?\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Not here since this night week!”", "지난주 오늘 밤 이후로 통 안 왔잖니!\""], ["said the old woman, contemplating him with a look of reproach.", "노파가 책망하는 눈길로 그를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Good gracious, Misses Brown,”", "\"맙소사, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["returned the Grinder, “I said tonight’s a week, that I’d come tonight, didn’t I?", "연마공이 응수했다, \"오늘이 일주일째니까 오늘 밤에 오겠다고 했잖아요, 안 그랬어요?"], ["And here I am.", "그래서 이렇게 왔잖아요."], ["How you do go on!", "어쩜 그러세요!"], ["I wish you’d be a little rational, Misses Brown.", "좀 사리에 맞게 구셨으면 좋겠어요, 브라운 아주머니."], ["I’m hoarse with saying things in my defence, and my very face is shiny with being hugged!”", "변명하느라 목이 다 쉬었고, 끌어안기는 통에 얼굴이 다 번들거린다니까요!\""], ["He rubbed it hard with his sleeve, as if to remove the tender polish in question.", "그는 마치 문제의 그 부드러운 윤기라도 닦아내려는 듯 소매로 얼굴을 박박 문질렀다."]], [["“Drink a little drop to comfort you, my Robin,”", "\"한 모금 마시고 기운 차려라, 우리 로빈,\""], ["said the old woman, filling the glass from the bottle and giving it to him.", "노파가 병에서 잔에 술을 따라 그에게 건네며 말했다."]], [["“Thank’ee, Misses Brown,”", "\"고마워요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["returned the Grinder.", "연마공이 응수했다."], ["“Here’s your health.", "\"아주머니 건강을 위해."], ["And long may you—et ceterer.”", "그리고 오래오래—기타 등등.\""], ["Which, to judge from the expression of his face, did not include any very choice blessings.", "그 표정으로 미루어 보아, 그 말에 그다지 정성스러운 축복이 담긴 것 같지는 않았다."], ["“And here’s her health,”", "\"그리고 이 아가씨 건강을 위해,\""], ["said the Grinder, glancing at Alice, who sat with her eyes fixed, as it seemed to him, on the wall behind him, but in reality on Mr Dombey’s face at the door, “and wishing her the same and many of ’em!”", "연마공이 앨리스를 흘끗 보며 말했는데, 그녀는 그의 눈에는 그의 뒤쪽 벽에 시선을 못 박고 앉아 있는 듯 보였으나 실은 문가의 돔비 씨 얼굴을 보고 있었다, \"이 아가씨께도 같은 걸, 그것도 듬뿍 빕니다!\""]], [["He drained the glass to these two sentiments, and set it down.", "그는 이 두 가지 축원에 잔을 비우고는 그것을 내려놓았다."]], [["“Well, I say, Misses Brown!”", "\"자, 이봐요, 브라운 아주머니!\""], ["he proceeded.", "그가 이어 말했다."], ["“To go on a little rational now.", "\"이제 좀 사리에 맞게 얘기해 봐요."], ["You’re a judge of birds, and up to their ways, as I know to my cost.”", "아주머닌 새에 대해 잘 아시고, 새 다루는 법에도 밝으시잖아요. 제가 톡톡히 대가를 치르고 알게 된 일이지만요.\""]], [["“Cost!”", "\"대가라고!\""], ["repeated Mrs Brown.", "브라운 부인이 되받았다."]], [["“Satisfaction, I mean,”", "\"만족 말이에요,\""], ["returned the Grinder.", "연마공이 응수했다."], ["“How you do take up a cove, Misses Brown!", "\"어쩜 그렇게 사람 말꼬리를 잡으세요, 브라운 아주머니!"], ["You’ve put it all out of my head again.”", "또 머릿속이 새하얘졌잖아요.\""]], [["“Judge of birds, Robby,”", "\"새에 밝다고 했지, 로비,\""], ["suggested the old woman.", "노파가 거들었다."]], [["“Ah!” said the Grinder.", "\"맞아요!\""], ["“Well, I’ve got to take care of this parrot—certain things being sold, and a certain establishment broke up—and as I don’t want no notice took at present, I wish you’d attend to her for a week or so, and give her board and lodging, will you?", "연마공이 말했다. \"그게요, 제가 이 앵무새를 돌봐야 하게 됐어요—이런저런 물건이 팔리고, 어떤 살림이 다 흩어지는 바람에요—그리고 지금은 남의 눈에 띄고 싶지가 않아서, 아주머니가 한 일주일쯤 이 녀석을 봐주시고 먹이고 재워 주셨으면 하는데, 그래 주시겠어요?"], ["If I must come backwards and forwards,”", "제가 어차피 들락날락해야 한다면,\""], ["mused the Grinder with a dejected face, “I may as well have something to come for.”", "연마공이 풀 죽은 얼굴로 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다, \"올 만한 구실이라도 있는 편이 낫겠죠.\""]], [["“Something to come for?”", "\"올 만한 구실이라고?\""], ["screamed the old woman.", "노파가 비명을 질렀다."]], [["“Besides you, I mean, Misses Brown,”", "\"아주머니 말고요, 제 말은요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["returned the craven Rob.", "비굴한 롭이 응수했다."], ["“Not that I want any inducement but yourself, Misses Brown, I’m sure.", "\"물론 아주머니 말고 다른 구실이 필요하다는 건 아니에요, 정말로요, 브라운 아주머니."], ["Don’t begin again, for goodness’ sake.”", "제발 또 시작하진 마세요.\""]], [["“He don’t care for me!", "\"이 애는 날 좋아하지 않아!"], ["He don’t care for me, as I care for him!”", "내가 이 애를 위하는 만큼 날 위해 주질 않아!\""], ["cried Mrs Brown, lifting up her skinny hands.", "브라운 부인이 앙상한 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다."], ["“But I’ll take care of his bird.”", "\"하지만 그 새는 잘 돌봐 주마.\""]], [["“Take good care of it too, you know, Mrs Brown,”", "\"그것도 정말 잘 돌봐 주셔야 해요, 아시겠죠, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["said Rob, shaking his head.", "롭이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다."], ["“If you was so much as to stroke its feathers once the wrong way, I believe it would be found out.”", "\"혹시라도 깃털을 한 번이라도 결 반대로 쓰다듬으셨다간, 들통날 거라고요.\""]], [["“Ah, so sharp as that, Rob?”", "\"아니, 그렇게나 빈틈없다더냐, 롭?\""], ["said Mrs Brown, quickly.", "브라운 부인이 잽싸게 물었다."]], [["“Sharp, Misses Brown!”", "\"빈틈없냐고요, 브라운 아주머니!\""], ["repeated Rob.", "롭이 되뇌었다."], ["“But this is not to be talked about.”", "\"하지만 이건 입에 올릴 게 아니에요.\""]], [["Checking himself abruptly, and not without a fearful glance across the room, Rob filled the glass again, and having slowly emptied it, shook his head, and began to draw his fingers across and across the wires of the parrot’s cage by way of a diversion from the dangerous theme that had just been broached.", "불쑥 말을 멈추고는, 방 건너편으로 겁먹은 눈길을 던지는 것도 잊지 않은 채, 롭은 다시 잔을 채워 천천히 비우고 고개를 가로저었다. 그러고는 방금 입에 올린 그 위험한 화제에서 벗어나 보려는 셈으로, 손가락을 앵무새 새장 철망에 대고 이리저리 긁기 시작했다."]], [["The old woman eyed him slily, and hitching her chair nearer his, and looking in at the parrot, who came down from the gilded dome at her call, said:", "노파는 그를 곁눈으로 슬쩍 살피더니, 의자를 그에게 더 가까이 끌어당기고는, 자기가 부르자 금빛 둥근 지붕에서 내려온 앵무새를 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“Out of place now, Robby?”", "\"이제 일자리가 없는 게냐, 로비?\""]], [["“Never you mind, Misses Brown,”", "\"신경 쓰지 마세요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["returned the Grinder, shortly.", "연마공이 퉁명스레 응수했다."]], [["“Board wages, perhaps, Rob?”", "\"그럼 그냥 식대만 받고 있는 거냐, 롭?\""], ["said Mrs Brown.", "브라운 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Pretty Polly!”", "\"예쁜 폴리야!\""], ["said the Grinder.", "연마공이 말했다."]], [["The old woman darted a glance at him that might have warned him to consider his ears in danger, but it was his turn to look in at the parrot now, and however expressive his imagination may have made her angry scowl, it was unseen by his bodily eyes.", "노파가 그에게 흘긴 눈길은, 귀때기가 위험하다는 걸 알아차리라는 경고일 법했으나, 이번엔 그가 앵무새를 들여다볼 차례라, 그의 상상 속에서 그녀의 성난 찌푸림이 아무리 생생했을지언정 그의 육안에는 보이지 않았다."]], [["“I wonder Master didn’t take you with him, Rob,”", "\"주인 양반이 너를 안 데려간 게 이상하구나, 롭,\""], ["said the old woman, in a wheedling voice, but with increased malignity of aspect.", "노파가 살살 구슬리는 목소리로, 그러나 한층 악의 어린 표정으로 말했다."]], [["Rob was so absorbed in contemplation of the parrot, and in trolling his forefinger on the wires, that he made no answer.", "롭은 앵무새를 들여다보고 철망에 집게손가락을 굴리는 데 어찌나 몰두했던지 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["The old woman had her clutch within a hair’s breadth of his shock of hair as it stooped over the table; but she restrained her fingers, and said, in a voice that choked with its efforts to be coaxing:", "노파는 탁자 위로 숙인 그의 더부룩한 머리채를 머리카락 한 올 차이로 움켜쥐려다가, 손가락을 거두고는, 어르려 애쓰느라 목이 메는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Robby, my child.”", "\"로비야, 우리 아가.\""]], [["“Well, Misses Brown,”", "\"왜요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["returned the Grinder.", "연마공이 응수했다."]], [["“I say I wonder Master didn’t take you with him, dear.”", "\"주인 양반이 너를 안 데려간 게 이상하다고 했다, 얘야.\""]], [["“Never you mind, Misses Brown,”", "\"신경 쓰지 마세요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["returned the Grinder.", "연마공이 응수했다."]], [["Mrs Brown instantly directed the clutch of her right hand at his hair, and the clutch of her left hand at his throat, and held on to the object of her fond affection with such extraordinary fury, that his face began to blacken in a moment.", "브라운 부인은 즉시 오른손으로는 그의 머리채를, 왼손으로는 그의 목을 움켜쥐고는, 그 애지중지하는 대상을 어찌나 무시무시한 기세로 붙들었던지, 그의 얼굴이 삽시간에 거뭇해지기 시작했다."]], [["“Misses Brown!”", "\"브라운 아주머니!\""], ["exclaimed the Grinder, “let go, will you?", "연마공이 소리쳤다, \"놓으세요, 좀!"], ["What are you doing of?", "뭐 하는 거예요?"], ["Help, young woman!", "사람 살려, 아가씨!"], ["Misses Brow—Brow—!”", "브라운 아주—주—!\""]], [["The young woman, however, equally unmoved by his direct appeal to her, and by his inarticulate utterance, remained quite neutral, until, after struggling with his assailant into a corner, Rob disengaged himself, and stood there panting and fenced in by his own elbows, while the old woman, panting too, and stamping with rage and eagerness, appeared to be collecting her energies for another swoop upon him.", "그러나 젊은 여인은 그의 직접적인 호소에도, 그의 알아들을 수 없는 발버둥에도 똑같이 꿈쩍하지 않은 채 그저 가만히 있었다. 마침내 롭이 자기를 덮친 노파와 실랑이하며 한쪽 구석으로 몰린 끝에 빠져나와, 두 팔꿈치로 제 몸을 막아 가며 숨을 헐떡이며 거기 섰고, 노파 역시 숨을 헐떡이며 분노와 조바심에 발을 구르며, 그에게 또 한 번 덮칠 기운을 그러모으는 듯 보였다."], ["At this crisis Alice interposed her voice, but not in the Grinder’s favour, by saying,", "이 위기의 순간에 앨리스가 끼어들어 한마디 했으나, 연마공 편을 드는 말은 아니었다."]], [["“Well done, mother.", "\"잘하셨어요, 어머니."], ["Tear him to pieces!”", "그자를 갈가리 찢어 버리세요!\""]], [["“What, young woman!”", "\"뭐예요, 아가씨!\""], ["blubbered Rob; “are you against me too?", "롭이 엉엉 울었다. \"아가씨까지 내 편이 아니란 거예요?"], ["What have I been and done?", "내가 뭘 어쨌다고요?"], ["What am I to be tore to pieces for, I should like to know?", "내가 왜 갈가리 찢겨야 하는지 알고나 싶네요?"], ["Why do you take and choke a cove who has never done you any harm, neither of you?", "두 분 다 아무 해도 끼친 적 없는 사람을 왜 붙잡고 목을 조르는 거예요?"], ["Call yourselves females, too!”", "그러고도 여자라고요!\""], ["said the frightened and afflicted Grinder, with his coat-cuff at his eye.", "겁에 질려 시달린 연마공이 외투 소맷부리를 눈에 갖다 대며 말했다."], ["“I’m surprised at you!", "\"정말 어이가 없네요!"], ["Where’s your feminine tenderness?”", "여자의 다정함은 어디 갔어요?\""]], [["“You thankless dog!”", "\"이 배은망덕한 개 같은 놈!\""], ["gasped Mrs Brown.", "브라운 부인이 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["“You impudent insulting dog!”", "\"건방지고 모욕적인 개 같은 놈!\""]], [["“What have I been and done to go and give you offence, Misses Brown?”", "\"제가 뭘 어쨌다고 아주머니를 화나게 했다는 거예요, 브라운 아주머니?\""], ["retorted the fearful Rob.", "겁먹은 롭이 받아쳤다."], ["“You was very much attached to me a minute ago.”", "\"조금 전만 해도 저한테 무척 정이 깊으셨잖아요.\""]], [["“To cut me off with his short answers and his sulky words,”", "\"퉁명스러운 대답에 골난 말로 내 말을 싹둑 잘라 버리다니,\""], ["said the old woman.", "노파가 말했다. \"이 나를!"], ["“Me! Because I happen to be curious to have a little bit of gossip about Master and the lady, to dare to play at fast and loose with me!", "내가 어쩌다 주인 양반하고 그 마님 얘기로 잡담이나 좀 나누고 싶어졌기로서니, 감히 이 나를 데리고 이랬다저랬다 장난을 쳐!"], ["But I’ll talk to you no more, my lad.", "하지만 이제 너랑은 더 말 안 한다, 이놈아."], ["Now go!”", "자, 가거라!\""]], [["“I’m sure, Misses Brown,”", "\"정말로요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["returned the abject Grinder, “I never insiniwated that I wished to go.", "비굴한 연마공이 응수했다, \"전 가고 싶다는 내색은 한 번도 안 했어요."], ["Don’t talk like that, Misses Brown, if you please.”", "제발 그렇게 말씀하지 마세요, 브라운 아주머니.\""]], [["“I won’t talk at all,”", "\"난 아예 말을 안 하겠다,\""], ["said Mrs Brown, with an action of her crooked fingers that made him shrink into half his natural compass in the corner.", "브라운 부인이 갈고리 같은 손가락을 놀리며 말했는데, 그 바람에 그는 구석에서 제 몸뚱이를 절반으로 움츠렸다."], ["“Not another word with him shall pass my lips.", "\"저 녀석하고는 더 이상 한마디도 안 한다."], ["He’s an ungrateful hound.", "배은망덕한 개 같은 놈."], ["I cast him off.", "내가 내쳤다."], ["Now let him go!", "자, 가게 둬라!"], ["And I’ll slip those after him that shall talk too much; that won’t be shook away; that’ll hang to him like leeches, and slink arter him like foxes.", "그리고 내가 그 뒤꽁무니에 말 많은 자들을 붙여 줄 테다. 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 자들, 거머리처럼 들러붙고 여우처럼 슬금슬금 뒤를 밟을 자들을."], ["What!", "뭐!"], ["He knows ’em.", "저 녀석도 그 작자들을 알지."], ["He knows his old games and his old ways.", "제 옛날 짓거리며 옛날 수법도 알고."], ["If he’s forgotten ’em, they’ll soon remind him.", "잊어버렸다 해도 그 작자들이 곧 일깨워 줄 거다."], ["Now let him go, and see how he’ll do Master’s business, and keep Master’s secrets, with such company always following him up and down.", "자, 이제 가게 둬라, 그리고 저 녀석이 그런 패거리한테 늘 이리저리 따라붙이며 주인 양반 일을 어떻게 해내고 주인 양반 비밀을 어떻게 지키는지 두고 보자고."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["He’ll find ’em a different sort from you and me, Ally; Close as he is with you and me.", "그 작자들은 너나 나랑은 영 다른 부류임을 알게 될 게다, 앨리. 저 녀석이 너나 나한테는 그렇게 입을 꼭 다물지만."], ["Now let him go, now let him go!”", "자, 이제 가게 둬라, 어서 가게 둬!\""]], [["The old woman, to the unspeakable dismay of the Grinder, walked her twisted figure round and round, in a ring of some four feet in diameter, constantly repeating these words, and shaking her fist above her head, and working her mouth about.", "노파는 연마공이 이루 말할 수 없이 당황하는 가운데, 그 비틀린 형체로 지름 사 피트쯤 되는 원을 빙글빙글 돌며, 끊임없이 이 말들을 되풀이하고, 머리 위로 주먹을 흔들고, 입을 우물거렸다."]], [["“Misses Brown,”", "\"브라운 아주머니,\""], ["pleaded Rob, coming a little out of his corner, “I’m sure you wouldn’t injure a cove, on second thoughts, and in cold blood, would you?”", "롭이 구석에서 조금 나오며 애원했다, \"다시 생각해 보시면, 냉정하게 보시면 사람을 해치진 않으실 거잖아요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“Don’t talk to me,”", "\"내게 말 걸지 마라,\""], ["said Mrs Brown, still wrathfully pursuing her circle.", "브라운 부인이 여전히 노여워하며 원을 돌며 말했다."], ["“Now let him go, now let him go!”", "\"자, 가게 둬라, 어서 가게 둬!\""]], [["“Misses Brown,”", "\"브라운 아주머니,\""], ["urged the tormented Grinder, “I didn’t mean to—Oh, what a thing it is for a cove to get into such a line as this!—I was only careful of talking, Misses Brown, because I always am, on account of his being up to everything; but I might have known it wouldn’t have gone any further.", "시달린 연마공이 호소했다, \"전 그럴 뜻이 없었어요—오, 사람이 이런 처지에 빠지다니 이게 무슨 꼴이람!—전 그저 말조심을 했을 뿐이에요, 브라운 아주머니. 그분이 모든 걸 빠삭하게 아시니까 늘 그러는 거라고요."], ["I’m sure I’m quite agreeable,” with a wretched face, “for any little bit of gossip, Misses Brown.", "하지만 더 멀리 새 나갈 일이 아니란 걸 알았어야 했는데."], ["Don’t go on like this, if you please.", "정말이지 저는 잡담쯤은 얼마든지 좋아요,\""], ["Oh, couldn’t you have the goodness to put in a word for a miserable cove, here?”", "비참한 얼굴로 그가 말했다, \"어떤 자질구레한 잡담이든요, 브라운 아주머니. 제발 이러지 마세요."], ["said the Grinder, appealing in desperation to the daughter.", "오, 여기 가엾은 놈을 위해 한마디만 거들어 주실 순 없어요?\" 연마공이 절망에 빠져 딸에게 호소하며 말했다."]], [["“Come, mother, you hear what he says,”", "\"자, 어머니, 저 애가 하는 말 들으셨죠,\" 그녀가 엄한 목소리로, 초조하게 고개를 까딱이며 끼어들었다."], ["she interposed, in her stern voice, and with an impatient action of her head; “try him once more, and if you fall out with him again, ruin him, if you like, and have done with him.”", "\"한 번만 더 시켜 보세요. 그래도 또 틀어지거든, 마음대로 망쳐 버리고 끝장내 버리세요.\""]], [["Mrs Brown, moved as it seemed by this very tender exhortation, presently began to howl; and softening by degrees, took the apologetic Grinder to her arms, who embraced her with a face of unutterable woe, and like a victim as he was, resumed his former seat, close by the side of his venerable friend, whom he suffered, not without much constrained sweetness of countenance, combating very expressive physiognomical revelations of an opposite character to draw his arm through hers, and keep it there.", "브라운 부인은 이 더없이 다정한 권고에 마음이 움직인 듯, 이내 울부짖기 시작하더니, 차츰 누그러져 사죄하는 연마공을 품에 끌어안았다. 그는 이루 말할 수 없는 비탄의 얼굴로 그녀를 끌어안고는, 희생물이 다 그렇듯, 자기의 그 존경스러운 벗 곁에 바싹 붙은 본래 자리로 돌아갔으며, 정반대 성격을 적나라하게 드러내려는 표정과 싸워 가며 억지로 짜낸 살가운 낯빛을 적잖이 띤 채, 그녀가 자기 팔짱을 끼고 그대로 두는 것을 가만히 받아들였다."]], [["“And how’s Master, deary dear?”", "\"그래, 주인 양반은 어떠시냐, 귀여운 우리 아가?\""], ["said Mrs Brown, when, sitting in this amicable posture, they had pledged each other.", "브라운 부인이 이렇게 화기애애한 자세로 앉아 서로 잔을 부딪친 뒤에 말했다."]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["If you’d be so good, Misses Brown, as to speak a little lower,”", "죄송하지만 좀 더 작게 말씀해 주실 수 없어요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["Rob implored.", "롭이 간청했다."], ["“Why, he’s pretty well, thank’ee, I suppose.”", "\"그게, 잘 지내시는 것 같아요, 고마워요.\""]], [["“You’re not out of place, Robby?”", "\"일자리에서 안 잘렸지, 로비?\""], ["said Mrs Brown, in a wheedling tone.", "브라운 부인이 구슬리는 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Why, I’m not exactly out of place, nor in,”", "\"그게, 잘린 것도 아니고, 그렇다고 있는 것도 아니고,\""], ["faltered Rob.", "롭이 우물거렸다."], ["“I—I’m still in pay, Misses Brown.”", "\"전—전 아직 봉급은 받고 있어요, 브라운 아주머니.\""]], [["“And nothing to do, Rob?”", "\"그런데 할 일은 없고, 롭?\""]], [["“Nothing particular to do just now, Misses Brown, but to—keep my eyes open,”", "\"지금은 딱히 할 일은 없어요, 브라운 아주머니, 그저—눈을 부릅뜨고 있는 것 말고는요,\""], ["said the Grinder, rolling them in a forlorn way.", "연마공이 처량하게 눈을 굴리며 말했다."]], [["“Master abroad, Rob?”", "\"주인 양반은 외국에 가셨고, 롭?\""]], [["“Oh, for goodness’ sake, Misses Brown, couldn’t you gossip with a cove about anything else?”", "\"오, 제발요, 브라운 아주머니, 다른 거로 잡담 좀 하시면 안 돼요?\""], ["cried the Grinder, in a burst of despair.", "연마공이 절망에 못 이겨 외쳤다."]], [["The impetuous Mrs Brown rising directly, the tortured Grinder detained her, stammering “Ye-es, Misses Brown, I believe he’s abroad.", "성마른 브라운 부인이 곧장 일어서자, 시달린 연마공이 그녀를 붙들고 더듬거렸다. \"네—네, 브라운 아주머니, 외국에 계신 것 같아요."], ["What’s she staring at?”", "저 아가씬 뭘 저렇게 빤히 보는 거예요?\""], ["he added, in allusion to the daughter, whose eyes were fixed upon the face that now again looked out behind.", "그가 딸을 가리키며 덧붙였는데, 그녀의 눈은 이제 다시 뒤쪽에서 내다보는 그 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다."]], [["“Don’t mind her, lad,”", "\"저 애는 신경 쓰지 마라, 얘야,\""], ["said the old woman, holding him closer to prevent his turning round.", "노파가 그가 돌아보지 못하도록 더 바짝 끌어안으며 말했다."], ["“It’s her way—her way.", "\"원래 저런다—저게 저 애 버릇이야."], ["Tell me, Rob.", "말해 봐라, 롭."], ["Did you ever see the lady, deary?”", "그 마님을 본 적 있느냐, 얘야?\""]], [["“Oh, Misses Brown, what lady?”", "\"오, 브라운 아주머니, 무슨 마님요?\""], ["cried the Grinder in a tone of piteous supplication.", "연마공이 가련히 애원하는 어조로 외쳤다."]], [["“What lady?”", "\"무슨 마님이라니?\""], ["she retorted.", "그녀가 받아쳤다."], ["“The lady; Mrs Dombey.”", "\"그 마님 말이다. 돔비 부인 말이야.\""]], [["“Yes, I believe I see her once,”", "\"네, 한 번 본 것 같아요,\""], ["replied Rob.", "롭이 대답했다."]], [["“The night she went away, Robby, eh?”", "\"그 마님이 떠나던 날 밤이지, 로비, 응?\""], ["said the old woman in his ear, and taking note of every change in his face.", "노파가 그의 귀에 대고 말하며, 그의 얼굴에 일어나는 변화 하나하나를 놓치지 않았다."], ["“Aha!", "\"아하!"], ["I know it was that night.”", "그날 밤이었다는 걸 알지.\""]], [["“Well, if you know it was that night, you know, Misses Brown,”", "\"그게 그날 밤이었다는 걸 아신다면, 아시잖아요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["replied Rob, “it’s no use putting pinchers into a cove to make him say so.", "롭이 대답했다, \"사람한테 집게를 들이대서 억지로 말하게 할 것까진 없잖아요.\""]], [["“Where did they go that night, Rob?", "\"그날 밤 그들이 어디로 갔지, 롭?"], ["Straight away?", "곧장 갔어?"], ["How did they go?", "어떻게 갔지?"], ["Where did you see her? Did she laugh?", "넌 그 마님을 어디서 봤니?"], ["Did she cry?", "웃던? 울던?"], ["Tell me all about it,”", "죄다 말해 봐라,\""], ["cried the old hag, holding him closer yet, patting the hand that was drawn through his arm against her other hand, and searching every line in his face with her bleared eyes. “Come!", "늙은 마귀할멈이 그를 더욱 바짝 끌어안고는, 자기 팔짱에 낀 그의 손을 다른 손바닥에 대고 토닥이며, 침침한 눈으로 그의 얼굴 주름 하나하나를 샅샅이 살피며 외쳤다."], ["Begin!", "\"자!"], ["I want to be told all about it.", "시작해라! 죄다 듣고 싶다."], ["What, Rob, boy!", "뭐냐, 롭, 얘야!"], ["You and me can keep a secret together, eh?", "너랑 나랑은 함께 비밀을 지킬 수 있잖니, 응?"], ["We’ve done so before now.", "전에도 그래 왔고."], ["Where did they go first, Rob?”", "그들이 먼저 어디로 갔지, 롭?\""]], [["The wretched Grinder made a gasp, and a pause.", "비참한 연마공은 숨을 헉 들이쉬고는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“Are you dumb?”", "\"벙어리가 됐냐?\""], ["said the old woman, angrily.", "노파가 성을 내며 말했다."]], [["“Lord, Misses Brown, no!", "\"맙소사, 브라운 아주머니, 아니에요!"], ["You expect a cove to be a flash of lightning.", "사람더러 번갯불이 되라는 거예요."], ["I wish I was the electric fluency,”", "차라리 제가 전기 유체였으면 좋겠네,\""], ["muttered the bewildered Grinder.", "어리둥절한 연마공이 중얼거렸다."], ["“I’d have a shock at somebody, that would settle their business.”", "\"누군가한테 한 방 먹여서 끝장을 내 버리게요.\""]], [["“What do you say?”", "\"뭐라고 하는 게냐?\""], ["asked the old woman, with a grin.", "노파가 씩 웃으며 물었다."]], [["“I’m wishing my love to you, Misses Brown,”", "\"아주머니께 제 사랑을 빈다고요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["returned the false Rob, seeking consolation in the glass.", "거짓말쟁이 롭이 잔에서 위안을 찾으며 응수했다."], ["“Where did they go to first was it?", "\"그들이 먼저 어디로 갔냐고요?"], ["Him and her, do you mean?”", "그분하고 그 마님 말씀이세요?\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"그래!\""], ["said the old woman, eagerly.", "노파가 간절히 말했다."], ["“Them two.”", "\"그 둘 말이다.\""]], [["“Why, they didn’t go nowhere—not together, I mean,”", "\"그게요, 그들은 아무 데도 안 갔어요—같이는, 그러니까요,\""], ["answered Rob.", "롭이 대답했다."]], [["The old woman looked at him, as though she had a strong impulse upon her to make another clutch at his head and throat, but was restrained by a certain dogged mystery in his face.", "노파는 그의 머리와 목을 다시 한번 움켜쥐고 싶은 강한 충동이라도 인 듯 그를 바라보았으나, 그의 얼굴에 어린 어떤 고집스러운 비밀스러움에 제지당했다."]], [["“That was the art of it,”", "\"그게 바로 그 수법이었어요,\""], ["said the reluctant Grinder; “that’s the way nobody saw ’em go, or has been able to say how they did go.", "마지못한 연마공이 말했다. \"그래서 아무도 그들이 가는 걸 못 봤고, 어떻게 갔는지 말할 수 있는 사람도 없는 거예요."], ["They went different ways, I tell you Misses Brown.”", "따로따로 갔다니까요, 브라운 아주머니.\""]], [["“Ay, ay, ay!", "\"그래, 그래, 그래!"], ["To meet at an appointed place,”", "정해 둔 곳에서 만나려고,\""], ["chuckled the old woman, after a moment’s silent and keen scrutiny of his face.", "노파가 잠시 말없이 그의 얼굴을 날카롭게 뜯어본 뒤 낄낄거렸다."]], [["“Why, if they weren’t a going to meet somewhere, I suppose they might as well have stayed at home, mightn’t they, Brown?”", "\"아니, 어디서 만나려는 게 아니었다면, 그냥 집에 있는 거나 마찬가지였겠죠, 안 그래요, 브라운 아주머니?\""], ["returned the unwilling Grinder.", "마지못한 연마공이 응수했다."]], [["“Well, Rob?", "\"그래, 롭?"], ["Well?”", "그래서?\""], ["said the old woman, drawing his arm yet tighter through her own, as if, in her eagerness, she were afraid of his slipping away.", "노파가 그의 팔을 자기 팔에 더욱 단단히 끼며 말했는데, 마치 간절한 나머지 그가 빠져나갈까 봐 두려워하는 듯했다."]], [["“What, haven’t we talked enough yet, Misses Brown?”", "\"아니, 우리 아직도 충분히 얘기 안 한 거예요, 브라운 아주머니?\""], ["returned the Grinder, who, between his sense of injury, his sense of liquor, and his sense of being on the rack, had become so lachrymose, that at almost every answer he scooped his coats into one or other of his eyes, and uttered an unavailing whine of remonstrance.", "연마공이 응수했는데, 그는 억울함과 취기와 고문당하는 듯한 기분 사이에서 어찌나 눈물이 그렁그렁해졌는지, 거의 모든 대답마다 외투 자락을 이쪽저쪽 눈에 갖다 대고는 부질없는 항의의 칭얼거림을 내뱉었다."], ["“Did she laugh that night, was it?", "\"그 마님이 그날 밤 웃었냐고요?"], ["Didn’t you ask if she laughed, Misses Brown?”", "웃었는지 물으신 거잖아요, 브라운 아주머니?\""]], [["“Or cried?”", "\"아니면 울었는지?\""], ["added the old woman, nodding assent.", "노파가 동의의 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕이며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Neither,” said the Grinder.", "\"둘 다 아니에요,\""], ["“She kept as steady when she and me—oh, I see you will have it out of me, Misses Brown!", "연마공이 말했다. \"그 마님하고 제가—오, 기어이 저한테서 캐내고야 마실 작정이군요, 브라운 아주머니!"], ["But take your solemn oath now, that you’ll never tell anybody.”", "그럼 지금 엄숙히 맹세하세요, 누구한테도 절대 말 안 하겠다고요.\""]], [["This Mrs Brown very readily did: being naturally Jesuitical; and having no other intention in the matter than that her concealed visitor should hear for himself.", "브라운 부인은 선뜻 그러마 했으니, 천성이 음흉한 데다, 이 일에서 다른 의도는 없고 그저 숨어 있는 손님이 직접 듣게 하려는 것뿐이었다."]], [["“She kept as steady, then, when she and me went down to Southampton,”", "\"그 마님은 그러니까, 저랑 같이 사우샘프턴까지 내려가는 동안,\""], ["said the Grinder, “as a image.", "연마공이 말했다, \"석상처럼 의연했어요."], ["In the morning she was just the same, Misses Brown.", "아침이 돼서도 한결같으셨고요, 브라운 아주머니."], ["And when she went away in the packet before daylight, by herself—me pretending to be her servant, and seeing her safe aboard—she was just the same.", "그리고 날이 밝기도 전에 혼자 정기선을 타고 떠나실 때—제가 그 마님 하인인 척하면서 무사히 배에 오르시는 걸 지켜봤는데—그때도 한결같으셨어요."], ["Now, are you contented, Misses Brown?”", "자, 이제 만족하세요, 브라운 아주머니?\""]], [["“No, Rob.", "\"아니, 롭."], ["Not yet,”", "아직 아니다,\""], ["answered Mrs Brown, decisively.", "브라운 부인이 단호하게 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, here’s a woman for you!”", "\"오, 이런 여자가 어디 있담!\""], ["cried the unfortunate Rob, in an outburst of feeble lamentation over his own helplessness.", "불운한 롭이 제 무력함을 두고 힘없이 한탄하며 터뜨렸다."], ["“What did you wish to know next, Misses Brown?”", "\"그다음엔 또 뭘 알고 싶으세요, 브라운 아주머니?\""]], [["“What became of Master?", "\"주인 양반은 어떻게 됐지?"], ["Where did he go?”", "어디로 갔어?\""], ["she inquired, still holding him tight, and looking close into his face, with her sharp eyes.", "그녀는 여전히 그를 단단히 붙든 채, 날카로운 눈으로 그의 얼굴을 바짝 들여다보며 물었다."]], [["“Upon my soul, I don’t know, Misses Brown,”", "\"맹세코, 모르겠어요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["answered Rob.", "롭이 대답했다."], ["“Upon my soul I don’t know what he did, nor where he went, nor anything about him I only know what he said to me as a caution to hold my tongue, when we parted; and I tell you this, Misses Brown, as a friend, that sooner than ever repeat a word of what we’re saying now, you had better take and shoot yourself, or shut yourself up in this house, and set it a-fire, for there’s nothing he wouldn’t do, to be revenged upon you.", "\"맹세코 그분이 뭘 했는지도, 어디로 갔는지도, 그분에 관한 어떤 것도 몰라요. 다만 우리가 헤어질 때 입조심하라고 제게 하신 말씀만 알 뿐이에요."], ["You don’t know him half as well as I do, Misses Brown.", "그리고 이건 친구로서 하는 말인데요, 브라운 아주머니, 지금 우리가 하는 말을 한마디라도 옮기느니, 차라리 권총으로 자결하시거나 이 집에 틀어박혀 불을 질러 버리시는 게 나아요. 그분은 아주머니께 복수하기 위해서라면 못 할 일이 없으니까요."], ["You’re never safe from him, I tell you.”", "아주머닌 그분을 저만큼도 잘 모르세요, 브라운 아주머니. 그분한테서는 결코 안전하지 못하다니까요, 정말이에요.\""]], [["“Haven’t I taken an oath,”", "\"내가 맹세하지 않았느냐,\""], ["retorted the old woman, “and won’t I keep it?”", "노파가 받아쳤다, \"그리고 그 맹세를 지키지 않겠느냐?\""]], [["“Well, I’m sure I hope you will, Misses Brown,”", "\"뭐, 정말 지켜 주시길 바라요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["returned Rob, somewhat doubtfully, and not without a latent threatening in his manner.", "롭이 다소 미심쩍어하며, 또 은근한 위협이 없지 않은 태도로 응수했다."], ["“For your own sake, quite as much as mine.”", "\"아주머니 자신을 위해서라도요, 저 못지않게 말이에요.\""]], [["He looked at her as he gave her this friendly caution, and emphasized it with a nodding of his head; but finding it uncomfortable to encounter the yellow face with its grotesque action, and the ferret eyes with their keen old wintry gaze, so close to his own, he looked down uneasily and sat skulking in his chair, as if he were trying to bring himself to a sullen declaration that he would answer no more questions.", "그는 이 우정 어린 경고를 하면서 그녀를 바라보고는 고개를 끄덕여 강조했다. 그러나 기괴하게 일그러지는 그 누런 얼굴과, 늙고 겨울처럼 싸늘한 매서운 눈빛을 가진 그 족제비 같은 눈을 제 얼굴에 그토록 바짝 마주하고 있기가 거북해, 그는 불편한 듯 시선을 떨구고 의자에 움츠려 앉았다."], ["The old woman, still holding him as before, took this opportunity of raising the forefinger of her right hand, in the air, as a stealthy signal to the concealed observer to give particular attention to what was about to follow.", "마치 더는 어떤 질문에도 대답하지 않겠노라 시무룩하게 선언하려고 마음을 다잡는 듯했다. 노파는 여전히 전처럼 그를 붙든 채, 이 틈을 타 오른손 집게손가락을 허공에 치켜들어, 이제 곧 이어질 것에 특히 주의를 기울이라고 숨어 있는 관찰자에게 은밀한 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“Rob,”", "\"롭,\""], ["she said, in her most coaxing tone.", "그녀가 더없이 어르는 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Good gracious, Misses Brown, what’s the matter now?”", "\"맙소사, 브라운 아주머니, 또 뭐예요?\""], ["returned the exasperated Grinder.", "격분한 연마공이 응수했다."]], [["“Rob!", "\"롭!"], ["where did the lady and Master appoint to meet?”", "그 마님하고 주인 양반이 어디서 만나기로 했지?\""]], [["Rob shuffled more and more, and looked up and looked down, and bit his thumb, and dried it on his waistcoat, and finally said, eyeing his tormentor askance, “How should I know, Misses Brown?”", "롭은 점점 더 안절부절못하며, 위를 봤다 아래를 봤다 하고, 엄지손가락을 깨물었다가 조끼에 닦더니, 마침내 자기를 괴롭히는 자를 곁눈으로 흘기며 말했다. \"제가 어떻게 알아요, 브라운 아주머니?\""]], [["The old woman held up her finger again, as before, and replying, “Come, lad!", "노파는 다시 아까처럼 손가락을 치켜들고 대꾸했다. \"이봐, 얘야!"], ["It’s no use leading me to that, and there leaving me.", "거기까지 끌고 와서 거기서 날 내버려 둬 봤자 소용없다."], ["I want to know”", "난 알아야겠어.\""], ["waited for his answer.", "그러고는 그의 대답을 기다렸다."], ["Rob, after a discomfited pause, suddenly broke out with, “How can I pronounce the names of foreign places, Mrs Brown?", "롭은 당황한 듯 잠시 멈췄다가, 불쑥 터뜨렸다. \"외국 지명을 제가 어떻게 발음해요, 브라운 아주머니?"], ["What an unreasonable woman you are!”", "어쩜 이렇게 사리에 안 맞는 분이세요!\""]], [["“But you have heard it said, Robby,”", "\"하지만 누가 말하는 걸 들었잖니, 로비,\""], ["she retorted firmly, “and you know what it sounded like.", "그녀가 단호하게 받아쳤다, \"그러니 그게 어떤 소리였는지는 알 게다."], ["Come!”", "자!\""]], [["“I never heard it said, Misses Brown,”", "\"누가 말하는 건 한 번도 못 들었어요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["returned the Grinder.", "연마공이 응수했다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["retorted the old woman quickly, “you have seen it written, and you can spell it.”", "노파가 잽싸게 받아쳤다, \"글로 쓴 걸 봤을 게고, 철자도 댈 수 있겠구나.\""]], [["Rob, with a petulant exclamation between laughing and crying—for he was penetrated with some admiration of Mrs Brown’s cunning, even through this persecution—after some reluctant fumbling in his waistcoat pocket, produced from it a little piece of chalk.", "롭은 웃음과 울음 사이의 짜증스러운 외마디 소리를 내며—이런 시달림 속에서도 그는 브라운 부인의 교활함에 다소 감탄하지 않을 수 없었던 것이다—마지못해 조끼 주머니를 뒤적이더니, 거기서 작은 분필 조각을 하나 꺼냈다."], ["The old woman’s eyes sparkled when she saw it between his thumb and finger, and hastily clearing a space on the deal table, that he might write the word there, she once more made her signal with a shaking hand.", "노파는 그것이 그의 엄지와 손가락 사이에 끼여 있는 것을 보자 눈을 반짝였고, 그가 거기에 단어를 쓸 수 있도록 전나무 탁자 위에 황급히 자리를 비우고는, 떨리는 손으로 다시 한번 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“Now I tell you beforehand what it is, Misses Brown,”", "\"자, 미리 말해 두는데요, 브라운 아주머니,\""], ["said Rob, “it’s no use asking me anything else.", "롭이 말했다, \"다른 건 물어봐야 소용없어요."], ["I won’t answer anything else; I can’t.", "다른 건 아무것도 대답 안 할 거예요."], ["How long it was to be before they met, or whose plan it was that they was to go away alone, I don’t know no more than you do.", "못 해요. 그들이 만나기로 한 게 얼마나 뒤였는지, 둘이서만 떠나기로 한 게 누구 계획이었는지, 저도 아주머니만큼이나 몰라요."], ["I don’t know any more about it.", "더 이상은 아무것도 몰라요."], ["If I was to tell you how I found out this word, you’d believe that.", "제가 이 단어를 어떻게 알아냈는지 말씀드리면, 믿으실 거예요."], ["Shall I tell you, Misses Brown?”", "말씀드릴까요, 브라운 아주머니?\""]], [["“Yes, Rob.”", "\"그래, 롭.\""]], [["“Well then, Misses Brown.", "\"그럼요, 브라운 아주머니."], ["The way—now you won’t ask any more, you know?”", "그 사정은—이제 더는 안 물으실 거죠, 아시죠?\""], ["said Rob, turning his eyes, which were now fast getting drowsy and stupid, upon her.", "롭이 이제 빠르게 졸리고 멍해지는 눈을 그녀에게 돌리며 말했다."]], [["“Not another word,”", "\"한마디도 더 안 묻는다,\""], ["said Mrs Brown.", "브라운 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Well then, the way was this.", "\"그럼요, 사정은 이래요."], ["When a certain person left the lady with me, he put a piece of paper with a direction written on it in the lady’s hand, saying it was in case she should forget.", "어떤 분이 그 마님을 제게 맡기고 떠날 때, 그분이 행선지를 적은 종잇조각을 마님 손에 쥐여 주면서, 혹시 잊으실까 봐 그런다고 하셨어요. 마님은 잊을까 봐 걱정하는 기색이 아니었어요."], ["She wasn’t afraid of forgetting, for she tore it up as soon as his back was turned, and when I put up the carriage steps, I shook out one of the pieces—she sprinkled the rest out of the window, I suppose, for there was none there afterwards, though I looked for ’em.", "그분이 등을 돌리자마자 그걸 찢어 버리셨으니까요. 그리고 제가 마차 발판을 접어 올릴 때, 그 조각 하나를 털어 냈어요—나머지는 창밖으로 뿌리신 모양이에요."], ["There was only one word on it, and that was this, if you must and will know.", "나중에 찾아봤지만 거기엔 한 조각도 없었거든요. 그 위엔 단어가 딱 하나만 적혀 있었는데, 정 알고 싶으시고 알아야겠다면, 그게 바로 이거예요."], ["But remember!", "하지만 명심하세요!"], ["You’re upon your oath, Misses Brown!”", "아주머닌 맹세하신 거예요, 브라운 아주머니!\""]], [["Mrs Brown knew that, she said.", "브라운 부인은 안다고 했다."], ["Rob, having nothing more to say, began to chalk, slowly and laboriously, on the table.", "롭은 더 할 말이 없자, 탁자 위에 천천히 힘들게 분필로 쓰기 시작했다."]], [["“‘D,’”", "\"'D'"], ["the old woman read aloud, when he had formed the letter.", "자네,\" 그가 그 글자를 다 쓰자 노파가 소리 내어 읽었다."]], [["“Will you hold your tongue, Misses Brown?”", "\"입 좀 다물어 주시겠어요, 브라운 아주머니?\""], ["he exclaimed, covering it with his hand, and turning impatiently upon her.", "그가 그것을 손으로 가리고는 짜증스레 그녀를 돌아보며 외쳤다."], ["“I won’t have it read out.", "\"소리 내어 읽는 건 싫어요."], ["Be quiet, will you!”", "좀 가만히 계세요!\""]], [["“Then write large, Rob,”", "\"그럼 크게 써라, 롭,\""], ["she returned, repeating her secret signal; “for my eyes are not good, even at print.”", "그녀가 은밀한 신호를 되풀이하며 응수했다. \"내 눈이 어두워서 활자라도 잘 안 보인다.\""]], [["Muttering to himself, and returning to his work with an ill will, Rob went on with the word.", "혼자 투덜대며 마지못해 다시 작업으로 돌아간 롭은 단어를 계속 써 나갔다."], ["As he bent his head down, the person for whose information he so unconsciously laboured, moved from the door behind him to within a short stride of his shoulder, and looked eagerly towards the creeping track of his hand upon the table.", "그가 고개를 숙이는 사이, 그가 자기도 모르게 그토록 애써 일러 주고 있는 그 사람이, 그의 등 뒤 문가에서 그의 어깨로부터 한 걸음 못 미치는 곳까지 다가와, 탁자 위로 기어가는 그 손의 자취를 향해 간절히 시선을 던졌다."], ["At the same time, Alice, from her opposite chair, watched it narrowly as it shaped the letters, and repeated each one on her lips as he made it, without articulating it aloud.", "동시에 앨리스는 맞은편 의자에서 그것이 글자를 이루어 가는 모양을 유심히 지켜보며, 그가 한 글자씩 만들 때마다 소리 내지 않고 입술로 따라 했다."], ["At the end of every letter her eyes and Mr Dombey’s met, as if each of them sought to be confirmed by the other; and thus they both spelt D.I.J.O.N.", "글자 하나가 끝날 때마다 그녀의 눈과 돔비 씨의 눈이 마주쳤으니, 마치 둘이 서로에게서 확인을 구하기라도 하는 듯했다. 그렇게 두 사람은 함께 D.I.J.O.N.이라고 철자를 읽어 냈다."]], [["“There!”", "\"자!\""], ["said the Grinder, moistening the palm of his hand hastily, to obliterate the word; and not content with smearing it out, rubbing and planing all trace of it away with his coat-sleeve, until the very colour of the chalk was gone from the table.", "연마공이 그 단어를 지우려고 황급히 손바닥에 침을 묻히며 말했다. 그러고는 문질러 지우는 것으로도 모자라, 외투 소매로 흔적을 죄다 박박 문대고 평평하게 밀어, 탁자에서 분필 색깔조차 사라질 때까지 닦아 냈다."], ["“Now, I hope you’re contented, Misses Brown!”", "\"자, 이제 만족하시죠, 브라운 아주머니!\""]], [["The old woman, in token of her being so, released his arm and patted his back; and the Grinder, overcome with mortification, cross-examination, and liquor, folded his arms on the table, laid his head upon them, and fell asleep.", "노파는 만족한다는 표시로 그의 팔을 놓아주고 등을 토닥였다. 그러자 연마공은 굴욕감과 닦달과 취기에 짓눌려, 탁자 위에 두 팔을 포개고 그 위에 머리를 얹더니 잠이 들었다."]], [["Not until he had been heavily asleep some time, and was snoring roundly, did the old woman turn towards the door where Mr Dombey stood concealed, and beckon him to come through the room, and pass out.", "그가 한참 깊이 잠들어 코를 우렁차게 골기 시작한 뒤에야, 노파는 돔비 씨가 숨어 있는 문 쪽을 돌아보며, 방을 가로질러 나가라고 손짓했다."], ["Even then, she hovered over Rob, ready to blind him with her hands, or strike his head down, if he should raise it while the secret step was crossing to the door.", "그러면서도 그녀는 롭 위로 맴돌며, 그 은밀한 발걸음이 문으로 건너가는 동안 그가 혹시 머리를 들면 두 손으로 눈을 가리거나 머리를 도로 눌러 버릴 채비를 했다."], ["But though her glance took sharp cognizance of the sleeper, it was sharp too for the waking man; and when he touched her hand with his, and in spite of all his caution, made a chinking, golden sound, it was as bright and greedy as a raven’s.", "그러나 그녀의 눈길이 잠든 자를 날카롭게 살피는 한편, 깨어 있는 자에게도 날카로웠으니, 그가 자기 손으로 그녀의 손을 건드리며 온갖 조심에도 불구하고 금화 부딪치는 짤랑 소리를 내자, 그 눈길은 까마귀의 그것처럼 번득이고 탐욕스러웠다."]], [["The daughter’s dark gaze followed him to the door, and noted well how pale he was, and how his hurried tread indicated that the least delay was an insupportable restraint upon him, and how he was burning to be active and away.", "딸의 어두운 시선은 그가 문까지 가는 것을 좇으며, 그가 얼마나 창백한지, 그의 황급한 발걸음이 조금의 지체도 그에게는 견딜 수 없는 속박임을 어떻게 드러내는지, 그가 얼마나 행동에 옮겨 떠나고 싶어 안달인지를 똑똑히 새겼다."], ["As he closed the door behind him, she looked round at her mother.", "그가 등 뒤로 문을 닫자, 그녀는 어머니를 돌아보았다."], ["The old woman trotted to her; opened her hand to show what was within; and, tightly closing it again in her jealousy and avarice, whispered:", "노파는 종종걸음으로 그녀에게 다가와, 손을 펴 그 안에 든 것을 보여 주고는, 시기심과 탐욕에 다시 손을 꼭 움켜쥐고 속삭였다."]], [["“What will he do, Ally?”", "\"저자가 무슨 짓을 할까, 앨리?\""]], [["“Mischief,”", "\"못된 짓이겠죠,\""], ["said the daughter.", "딸이 말했다."]], [["“Murder?”", "\"살인을?\""], ["asked the old woman.", "노파가 물었다."]], [["“He’s a madman, in his wounded pride, and may do that, for anything we can say, or he either.”", "\"상처 입은 자존심에 미쳐 버린 사람이니, 그럴지도 모르죠. 우리가 무슨 말을 하든, 그자 자신이 무슨 말을 하든 상관없이요.\""]], [["Her glance was brighter than her mother’s, and the fire that shone in it was fiercer; but her face was colourless, even to her lips.", "그녀의 눈길은 어머니의 것보다 더 밝았고, 거기서 타오르는 불길은 더 사나웠다. 그러나 그녀의 얼굴은 입술까지도 핏기가 없었다."]], [["They said no more, but sat apart; the mother communing with her money; the daughter with her thoughts; the glance of each, shining in the gloom of the feebly lighted room.", "두 사람은 더는 말이 없었고, 따로 떨어져 앉았다. 어머니는 제 돈과 마주하고, 딸은 제 생각과 마주한 채, 각자의 눈길이 희미하게 밝혀진 방의 어둠 속에서 빛났다."], ["Rob slept and snored.", "롭은 잠을 자며 코를 골았다."], ["The disregarded parrot only was in action.", "아랑곳받지 못한 앵무새만이 부산했다."], ["It twisted and pulled at the wires of its cage, with its crooked beak, and crawled up to the dome, and along its roof like a fly, and down again head foremost, and shook, and bit, and rattled at every slender bar, as if it knew its master’s danger, and was wild to force a passage out, and fly away to warn him of it.", "그것은 구부러진 부리로 새장 철망을 비틀고 잡아당기며, 둥근 지붕까지 기어올라 파리처럼 그 지붕을 따라 가다가 머리부터 거꾸로 다시 내려오고, 가느다란 창살 하나하나를 흔들고 물어뜯고 덜그럭거렸으니, 마치 제 주인의 위험을 알아채고 길을 뚫고 나가 날아가서 그에게 그것을 알리고 싶어 미칠 듯한 것만 같았다."]], [["# CHAPTER LIII. More Intelligence", "# CHAPTER LIII. More Intelligence"]], [["There were two of the traitor’s own blood—his renounced brother and sister—on whom the weight of his guilt rested almost more heavily, at this time, than on the man whom he had so deeply injured.", "배신자와 같은 피를 나눈 두 사람—그가 의절한 형과 누이—이 있었으니, 이즈음 그들에게는 그의 죄의 무게가, 그토록 깊이 해를 입은 그 사람에게보다도 오히려 더 무겁게 얹혀 있었다."], ["Prying and tormenting as the world was, it did Mr Dombey the service of nerving him to pursuit and revenge.", "세상은 캐묻기 좋아하고 사람을 들볶기 일쑤였지만, 돔비 씨에게는 그것이 추적과 복수를 향해 마음을 단단히 다잡게 해 주는 봉사가 되었다."], ["It roused his passion, stung his pride, twisted the one idea of his life into a new shape, and made some gratification of his wrath, the object into which his whole intellectual existence resolved itself.", "그것은 그의 격정을 자극했고, 그의 자존심을 쏘아 댔으며, 그의 생애의 단 하나뿐인 관념을 새로운 모양으로 비틀었고, 자신의 분노를 어느 정도 채우는 일을 그의 지적 존재 전체가 귀결되는 목표로 만들었다."], ["All the stubbornness and implacability of his nature, all its hard impenetrable quality, all its gloom and moroseness, all its exaggerated sense of personal importance, all its jealous disposition to resent the least flaw in the ample recognition of his importance by others, set this way like many streams united into one, and bore him on upon their tide.", "그의 본성에 깃든 모든 완고함과 무자비함, 그 단단하고 뚫리지 않는 성질, 그 음울함과 까다로움, 자기 자신의 중요성에 대한 과장된 자각, 남들이 그 중요성을 충분히 인정해 주는 가운데 조금이라도 흠이 보이면 분개하고 마는 그 시기심 어린 성벽—이 모든 것이 마치 여러 갈래의 물줄기가 하나로 합쳐지듯 이쪽으로 쏠렸고, 그 조류 위에 그를 실어 날랐다."], ["The most impetuously passionate and violently impulsive of mankind would have been a milder enemy to encounter than the sullen Mr Dombey wrought to this.", "인류 가운데 가장 충동적으로 격정에 휘둘리고 가장 폭력적으로 본능에 내몰리는 자라 해도, 이 지경에 이른 음침한 돔비 씨보다는 마주치기에 한결 부드러운 적이었을 것이다."], ["A wild beast would have been easier turned or soothed than the grave gentleman without a wrinkle in his starched cravat.", "빳빳한 크라바트에 주름 하나 없는 그 근엄한 신사보다는 차라리 야수가 돌려세우거나 달래기에 더 수월했을 것이다."]], [["But the very intensity of his purpose became almost a substitute for action in it.", "그러나 바로 그 목적의 강렬함이 거의 그 일에서의 행동을 대신하는 셈이 되었다."], ["While he was yet uninformed of the traitor’s retreat, it served to divert his mind from his own calamity, and to entertain it with another prospect.", "배신자가 어디로 달아났는지 아직 알지 못하는 동안, 그것은 자신의 재앙으로부터 마음을 돌려놓고 다른 전망으로 마음을 사로잡아 주는 구실을 했다."], ["The brother and sister of his false favourite had no such relief; everything in their history, past and present, gave his delinquency a more afflicting meaning to them.", "그가 잘못 총애했던 자의 형과 누이에게는 그런 위안이 없었다. 그들의 내력에서 지난날의 것이든 지금의 것이든 모든 것이, 그의 죄를 그들에게 한층 더 비통한 의미로 만들었다."]], [["The sister may have sometimes sadly thought that if she had remained with him, the companion and friend she had been once, he might have escaped the crime into which he had fallen.", "누이는 이따금 슬프게 생각했을지도 모른다. 자신이 그의 곁에 남아, 한때 그러했던 동무이자 벗으로 머물러 있었더라면, 그가 빠져든 그 죄를 면할 수 있었을지도 모른다고."], ["If she ever thought so, it was still without regret for what she had done, without the least doubt of her duty, without any pricing or enhancing of her self-devotion.", "설령 그녀가 그렇게 생각했다 하더라도, 그것은 자신이 한 일에 대한 후회도, 자기 의무에 대한 추호의 의심도, 자기 헌신을 값매기거나 부풀리는 마음도 없는 것이었다."], ["But when this possibility presented itself to the erring and repentant brother, as it sometimes did, it smote upon his heart with such a keen, reproachful touch as he could hardly bear.", "그러나 잘못을 저지르고 뉘우치는 형에게 이런 가능성이 떠오를 때면, 이따금 그러했듯이, 그것은 그가 거의 견딜 수 없을 만큼 날카롭고 책망하는 손길로 그의 가슴을 쳤다."], ["No idea of retort upon his cruel brother came into his mind.", "매정한 동생에게 되갚으려는 생각 따위는 그의 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["New accusation of himself, fresh inward lamentings over his own unworthiness, and the ruin in which it was at once his consolation and his self-reproach that he did not stand alone, were the sole kind of reflections to which the discovery gave rise in him.", "자기 자신에 대한 새로운 단죄, 자신의 못남에 대한 거듭되는 내면의 탄식, 그리고 자기 혼자만 그 폐허 속에 서 있는 것이 아니라는 사실—그것이 그에게는 위안이자 동시에 자책이었다—이런 부류의 성찰만이, 그 깨달음이 그의 안에서 불러일으키는 전부였다."]], [["It was on the very same day whose evening set upon the last chapter, and when Mr Dombey’s world was busiest with the elopement of his wife, that the window of the room in which the brother and sister sat at their early breakfast, was darkened by the unexpected shadow of a man coming to the little porch: which man was Perch the Messenger.", "돔비 씨의 세계가 그의 아내의 도주로 가장 분주하던 바로 그날, 그 저녁이 앞 장을 덮어 내린 바로 그날, 형과 누이가 이른 아침을 들며 앉아 있던 방의 창문이, 작은 현관으로 다가오는 한 사내의 뜻밖의 그림자로 어두워졌다. 그 사내는 사환 퍼치였다."]], [["“I’ve stepped over from Balls Pond at a early hour,”", "\"어젯밤 받은 지시대로, 이른 시간에 발스 폰드에서 건너왔습니다요,\""], ["said Mr Perch, confidentially looking in at the room door, and stopping on the mat to wipe his shoes all round, which had no mud upon them, “agreeable to my instructions last night.", "퍼치 씨가 은밀하게 방문 안을 들여다보며 말했고, 진흙 한 점 묻지 않은 신발을 매트 위에서 빙 둘러 가며 닦으려고 멈춰 섰다."], ["They was, to be sure and bring a note to you, Mr Carker, before you went out in the morning.", "\"지시인즉슨, 카커 나리, 아침에 외출하시기 전에 꼭 편지 한 장을 가져다드리라는 것이었습죠."], ["I should have been here a good hour and a half ago,”", "한 시간 반은 일찍 도착했어야 마땅했지만,\""], ["said Mr Perch, meekly, “but for the state of health of Mrs P., who I thought I should have lost in the night, I do assure you, five distinct times.”", "퍼치 씨가 풀죽은 투로 말했다, \"퍼치 부인의 건강 상태가 그래서요. 정말이지 간밤에 다섯 번이나 또렷이, 아내를 잃는 줄 알았습니다요.\""]], [["“Is your wife so ill?”", "\"부인이 그렇게 편찮으신가요?\""], ["asked Harriet.", "해리엇이 물었다."]], [["“Why, you see,”", "\"아, 그게 말입니다,\""], ["said Mr Perch, first turning round to shut the door carefully, “she takes what has happened in our House so much to heart, Miss.", "퍼치 씨가 먼저 돌아서서 문을 조심스레 닫고는 말했다, \"우리 회사에서 일어난 일을 아내가 어찌나 가슴에 사무치게 받아들이는지요, 아가씨."], ["Her nerves is so very delicate, you see, and soon unstrung.", "보시다시피 신경이 아주 섬세해서, 금세 풀어져 버린단 말씀입니다요."], ["Not but what the strongest nerves had good need to be shook, I’m sure.", "하기야 제아무리 강한 신경이라도 흔들릴 만한 일이긴 하지요, 암요."], ["You feel it very much yourself, no doubts.”", "아가씨께서도 무척 마음 아프실 테지요, 두말할 것 없이.\""]], [["Harriet repressed a sigh, and glanced at her brother.", "해리엇은 한숨을 억누르고 오라비를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“I’m sure I feel it myself, in my humble way,”", "\"저 자신도 정말이지 제 보잘것없는 방식으로나마 마음이 아픕니다요,\""], ["Mr Perch went on to say, with a shake of his head, “in a manner I couldn’t have believed if I hadn’t been called upon to undergo.", "퍼치 씨가 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말을 이었다, \"이런 일을 겪어 보라는 부름을 받지 않았더라면 도무지 믿지 못했을 정도로 말입죠."], ["It has almost the effect of drink upon me.", "거의 술기운 같은 효과가 저한테 나타난다니까요."], ["I literally feels every morning as if I had been taking more than was good for me over-night.”", "정말이지 매일 아침마다, 마치 간밤에 몸에 좋을 만큼보다 더 많이 들이켜기라도 한 것 같은 기분이 드는 거예요.\""]], [["Mr Perch’s appearance corroborated this recital of his symptoms.", "퍼치 씨의 행색이 자기 증상에 대한 이 설명을 입증해 주었다."], ["There was an air of feverish lassitude about it, that seemed referable to drams; and, which, in fact, might no doubt have been traced to those numerous discoveries of himself in the bars of public-houses, being treated and questioned, which he was in the daily habit of making.", "거기에는 술잔과 결부될 법한 열병 같은 나른함의 기색이 있었으니, 사실 그것은 그가 매일같이 술집 카운터에서 자신을 발견하게 되고, 거기서 술 대접을 받고 이런저런 질문에 답하던 그 숱한 일들로 거슬러 올라갈 수 있는 것이었으리라."]], [["“Therefore I can judge,”", "\"그래서 제가 헤아릴 수 있습죠,\""], ["said Mr Perch, shaking his head and speaking in a silvery murmur, “of the feelings of such as is at all peculiarly sitiwated in this most painful rewelation.”", "퍼치 씨가 고개를 저으며 은빛 속삭임 같은 목소리로 말했다, \"이 더없이 고통스러운 폭로의 일에서 특히나 묘하게 처지가 된 분들의 심정을 말입니다요.\""]], [["Here Mr Perch waited to be confided in; and receiving no confidence, coughed behind his hand.", "여기서 퍼치 씨는 자기에게 속내를 털어놓아 주기를 기다렸다. 그러나 아무런 속내도 받지 못하자, 손으로 입을 가리고 헛기침을 했다."], ["This leading to nothing, he coughed behind his hat; and that leading to nothing, he put his hat on the ground and sought in his breast pocket for the letter.", "그래도 아무 반응이 없자, 모자 뒤로 헛기침을 했고, 그것도 아무 소용이 없자, 모자를 바닥에 내려놓고 가슴팍 안주머니에서 편지를 찾았다."]], [["“If I rightly recollect, there was no answer,”", "\"제 기억이 옳다면 답장은 필요 없었습니다요,\""], ["said Mr Perch, with an affable smile; “but perhaps you’ll be so good as cast your eye over it, Sir.”", "퍼치 씨가 상냥한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그래도 한번 훑어봐 주신다면 고맙겠습니다요, 나리.\""]], [["John Carker broke the seal, which was Mr Dombey’s, and possessing himself of the contents, which were very brief, replied, “No.", "존 카커는 돔비 씨의 봉인을 뜯고, 매우 짤막한 내용을 다 읽고는 대답했다, \"아니요."], ["No answer is expected.”", "답장은 필요 없소.\""]], [["“Then I shall wish you good morning, Miss,”", "\"그럼 안녕히 계십시오, 아가씨,\""], ["said Perch, taking a step toward the door, and hoping, I’m sure, that you’ll not permit yourself to be more reduced in mind than you can help, by the late painful rewelation. The Papers,”", "퍼치가 문 쪽으로 한 걸음 옮기며 말했다, \"그리고 정말이지, 이번의 고통스러운 폭로로 인해 마음을 어쩔 수 없이 상하는 것 이상으로는 부디 상하지 않으시길 빕니다요."], ["said Mr Perch, taking two steps back again, and comprehensively addressing both the brother and sister in a whisper of increased mystery, “is more eager for news of it than you’d suppose possible.", "신문들은 말입니다,\" 퍼치 씨가 다시 두 걸음 물러서서, 한층 비밀스러운 속삭임으로 형과 누이를 한꺼번에 향해 말했다, \"여러분이 짐작도 못 하실 만큼 그 소식에 안달이 났습죠."], ["One of the Sunday ones, in a blue cloak and a white hat, that had previously offered for to bribe me—need I say with what success?—was dodging about our court last night as late as twenty minutes after eight o’clock.", "일요일판 신문 가운데 하나는, 파란 외투에 흰 모자를 쓴 작자였는데, 전에 저한테 뇌물을 주려고까지 했었지요—굳이 그게 어찌 됐는지 말씀드릴 필요는 없겠지요?—어젯밤 여덟 시 이십 분이나 되도록 우리 안마당을 어슬렁거리고 있더란 말입니다요."], ["I see him myself, with his eye at the counting-house keyhole, which being patent is impervious.", "제 눈으로 똑똑히 봤습죠, 경리실 열쇠 구멍에 눈을 갖다 대고 있는 걸요. 그 구멍이 특허품이라 안이 안 보이는데도 말입니다요."], ["Another one,”", "또 다른 작자는,\""], ["said Mr Perch, “with military frogs, is in the parlour of the King’s Arms all the blessed day.", "퍼치 씨가 말했다, \"군복 같은 장식 단추를 단 자였는데, 종일토록 킹스 암스의 응접실에 죽치고 있더군요."], ["I happened, last week, to let a little obserwation fall there, and next morning, which was Sunday, I see it worked up in print, in a most surprising manner.”", "제가 지난주에 거기서 우연히 사소한 한마디를 흘렸더니, 이튿날 아침, 그게 일요일이었습죠, 그게 인쇄물에 떡하니 실린 걸 봤습니다요. 정말 놀랄 만큼 잘도 부풀려져 있더라고요.\""]], [["Mr Perch resorted to his breast pocket, as if to produce the paragraph but receiving no encouragement, pulled out his beaver gloves, picked up his hat, and took his leave; and before it was high noon, Mr Perch had related to several select audiences at the King’s Arms and elsewhere, how Miss Carker, bursting into tears, had caught him by both hands, and said, “Oh!", "퍼치 씨는 그 단락을 꺼내 보이기라도 하려는 듯 다시 가슴팍 안주머니로 손을 가져갔으나, 아무런 격려도 받지 못하자 비버 가죽 장갑을 꺼내고 모자를 집어 든 뒤 작별을 고했다. 그리고 정오가 채 되기도 전에, 퍼치 씨는 킹스 암스를 비롯한 여러 곳에서 몇몇 엄선된 청중에게 이렇게 들려주었다."], ["dear dear Perch, the sight of you is all the comfort I have left!”", "카커 아가씨가 울음을 터뜨리며 자기 두 손을 덥석 붙잡고는 \"오! 다정하고 다정한 퍼치, 당신을 보는 것만이 제게 남은 유일한 위안이에요!\""], ["and how Mr John Carker had said, in an awful voice, “Perch, I disown him.", "하고 말했다고, 그리고 존 카커 씨는 무시무시한 목소리로 \"퍼치, 나는 그를 의절했소."], ["Never let me hear him mentioned as a brother more!”", "다시는 그를 내 동생이라 입에 올리지 마시오!\" 하고 말했다고."]], [["“Dear John,”", "\"존 오라버니,\""], ["said Harriet, when they were left alone, and had remained silent for some few moments.", "두 사람만 남아 몇 분간 말없이 있다가 해리엇이 말했다."], ["“There are bad tidings in that letter.”", "\"저 편지에 나쁜 소식이 들었군요.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["But nothing unexpected,”", "하지만 뜻밖의 일은 아니다,\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“I saw the writer yesterday.”", "\"어제 그분을 봤거든.\""]], [["“The writer?”", "\"그분이라뇨?\""]], [["“Mr Dombey.", "\"돔비 씨 말이다."], ["He passed twice through the Counting House while I was there.", "내가 거기 있는 동안 두 번이나 경리실을 지나가셨지."], ["I had been able to avoid him before, but of course could not hope to do that long.", "그 전까지는 그분을 피할 수 있었지만, 물론 그걸 오래 바랄 수는 없는 노릇이었어."], ["I know how natural it was that he should regard my presence as something offensive; I felt it must be so, myself.”", "그분이 내 존재를 무언가 거슬리는 것으로 여기는 게 얼마나 당연한 일인지 나는 안다. 나 스스로도 그럴 수밖에 없다고 느꼈으니까.\""]], [["“He did not say so?”", "\"그분이 그렇게 말씀하시던가요?\""]], [["“No; he said nothing: but I saw that his glance rested on me for a moment, and I was prepared for what would happen—for what has happened.", "\"아니, 아무 말도 하지 않으셨어. 하지만 그분의 시선이 잠시 내게 머무는 걸 봤고, 그래서 앞으로 닥칠 일에—이미 닥친 일에 마음의 준비를 했지."], ["I am dismissed!”", "나는 해고됐다!\""]], [["She looked as little shocked and as hopeful as she could, but it was distressing news, for many reasons.", "그녀는 될 수 있는 대로 충격받지 않은, 희망을 잃지 않은 표정을 지으려 애썼으나, 여러 가지 이유로 그것은 괴로운 소식이었다."]], [["“‘I need not tell you,’”", "\"'굳이 자네에게 말할 필요도 없겠지,'\""], ["said John Carker, reading the letter, “‘why your name would henceforth have an unnatural sound, in however remote a connexion with mine, or why the daily sight of anyone who bears it, would be unendurable to me.", "존 카커가 편지를 읽었다, \"'앞으로 자네 이름이, 내 이름과 아무리 동떨어진 관계로 엮인다 한들, 어찌하여 부자연스럽게 들리게 될지, 또 어찌하여 그 이름을 지닌 자를 날마다 보는 일이 내게 견딜 수 없는 것이 될지를."], ["I have to notify the cessation of all engagements between us, from this date, and to request that no renewal of any communication with me, or my establishment, be ever attempted by you.’—Enclosed is an equivalent in money to a generously long notice, and this is my discharge.", "나는 오늘부로 우리 사이의 모든 고용 관계가 끝났음을 통고하며, 앞으로 나 또는 내 회사와의 어떠한 교신도 자네 쪽에서 결코 새로이 시도하지 말 것을 요구하는 바이다.'—넉넉히 긴 통고 기간에 상당하는 액수가 돈으로 동봉되어 있고, 이것이 내 해고 통지다."], ["Heaven knows, Harriet, it is a lenient and considerate one, when we remember all!”", "하느님은 아시겠지, 해리엇, 우리가 모든 걸 떠올려 보면 이건 너그럽고 사려 깊은 처사야!\""]], [["“If it be lenient and considerate to punish you at all, John, for the misdeed of another,”", "\"남이 저지른 잘못을 두고 오라버니를 벌하는 것이 조금이라도 너그럽고 사려 깊은 일이라고 한다면요, 존 오라버니,\""], ["she replied gently, “yes.”", "그녀가 부드럽게 대답했다, \"그렇겠지요.\""]], [["“We have been an ill-omened race to him,”", "\"우리는 그분에게 불길한 핏줄이었어,\""], ["said John Carker.", "존 카커가 말했다."], ["“He has reason to shrink from the sound of our name, and to think that there is something cursed and wicked in our blood.", "\"그분이 우리 이름 소리만 들어도 움찔하고, 우리 피에 무언가 저주받고 사악한 것이 흐른다고 여기는 것도 무리가 아니야."], ["I should almost think it too, Harriet, but for you.”", "나도 거의 그렇게 생각할 뻔했다, 해리엇, 네가 없었더라면.\""]], [["“Brother, don’t speak like this.", "\"오라버니, 그렇게 말하지 마세요."], ["If you have any special reason, as you say you have, and think you have—though I say, No!—to love me, spare me the hearing of such wild mad words!”", "오라버니가 말하는 대로, 또 그렇게 믿는 대로, 나를 사랑할 어떤 특별한 이유가 있다 하더라도—나는 아니라고 하지만요!—제발 그런 거칠고 미친 말은 듣지 않게 해 주세요!\""]], [["He covered his face with both his hands; but soon permitted her, coming near him, to take one in her own.", "그는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 그러나 곧 그녀가 곁으로 다가와 그 한 손을 자기 손안에 쥐도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["“After so many years, this parting is a melancholy thing, I know,”", "\"이토록 오랜 세월 끝에 이렇게 헤어지는 건 서글픈 일이지, 나도 안다,\""], ["said his sister, “and the cause of it is dreadful to us both.", "누이가 말했다, \"그 까닭이 우리 둘 모두에게 끔찍하다는 것도."], ["We have to live, too, and must look about us for the means.", "게다가 우리는 살아가야 하고, 그러자면 살길을 둘러봐야지요. 자, 자!"], ["Well, well! We can do so, undismayed.", "우리는 그렇게 할 수 있어요, 기죽지 말고."], ["It is our pride, not our trouble, to strive, John, and to strive together!”", "애써 나아가는 것은, 그것도 함께 애써 나아가는 것은 우리의 괴로움이 아니라 우리의 자랑이에요, 존 오라버니!\""]], [["A smile played on her lips, as she kissed his cheek, and entreated him to be of good cheer.", "그녀의 입가에 미소가 어렸다. 그녀는 그의 뺨에 입을 맞추며 부디 기운을 내라고 간청했다."]], [["“Oh, dearest sister!", "\"오, 사랑하는 누이여!"], ["Tied, of your own noble will, to a ruined man!", "네 자신의 고귀한 뜻으로, 파멸한 사내에게 묶여 버리다니!"], ["whose reputation is blighted; who has no friend himself, and has driven every friend of yours away!”", "평판이 무너지고, 제 친구 하나 없으며, 네 친구마저 모조리 쫓아 버린 사내에게 말이다!\""]], [["“John!”", "\"존 오라버니!\""], ["she laid her hand hastily upon his lips, “for my sake!", "그녀가 황급히 그의 입술에 손을 갖다 댔다, \"날 위해서요! 우리가 함께 지내 온 그 오랜 세월을 떠올려서라도요!\""], ["In remembrance of our long companionship!”", "그는 입을 다물었다. \"자, 이제 말씀드릴게요, 오라버니,\""], ["He was silent “Now, let me tell you, dear,”", "조용히 그의 곁에 앉으며 그녀가 말했다, \"나도 오라버니처럼 이 일을 예상하고 있었어요."], ["quietly sitting by his side, “I have, as you have, expected this; and when I have been thinking of it, and fearing that it would happen, and preparing myself for it, as well as I could, I have resolved to tell you, if it should be so, that I have kept a secret from you, and that we have a friend.”", "그리고 이 일을 생각하며, 그것이 일어날까 두려워하며, 될 수 있는 대로 마음의 준비를 하면서, 만일 그렇게 된다면 오라버니께 이 말을 하기로 마음먹었지요. 내가 오라버니께 비밀 하나를 감추고 있었다고, 그리고 우리에게는 친구가 한 분 있다고요.\""]], [["“What’s our friend’s name, Harriet?”", "\"그 친구분 이름이 뭔데, 해리엇?\""], ["he answered with a sorrowful smile.", "그가 서글픈 미소를 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“Indeed, I don’t know, but he once made a very earnest protestation to me of his friendship and his wish to serve us: and to this day I believe him.”", "\"사실 모르겠어요. 하지만 그분은 언젠가 제게 자기 우정과, 우리를 돕고 싶다는 뜻을 아주 진지하게 밝히셨어요. 그리고 저는 오늘까지도 그분을 믿어요.\""]], [["“Harriet!”", "\"해리엇!\""], ["exclaimed her wondering brother, “where does this friend live?”", "의아해하는 오라비가 외쳤다, \"그 친구분이 어디 사시는데?\""]], [["“Neither do I know that,”", "\"그것도 모르겠어요,\""], ["she returned.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“But he knows us both, and our history—all our little history, John.", "\"하지만 그분은 우리 둘을 다 아시고, 우리 내력을—우리의 그 자잘한 내력을 전부 아세요, 존 오라버니."], ["That is the reason why, at his own suggestion, I have kept the secret of his coming, here, from you, lest his acquaintance with it should distress you.”", "그분이 직접 그렇게 하자고 하셔서, 제가 오라버니께 그분이 다녀가신 비밀을 감춰 온 거예요. 오라버니가 그 사실을 아시면 마음 아파하실까 봐요.\""]], [["“Here!", "\"여기를!"], ["Has he been here, Harriet?”", "그분이 여기에 다녀가셨다고, 해리엇?\""]], [["“Here, in this room.", "\"여기, 이 방에요."], ["Once.”", "한 번.\""]], [["“What kind of man?”", "\"어떤 분이시던?\""]], [["“Not young.", "\"젊은 분은 아니었어요. 그분 말씀으로는 '머리가 희끗하고, 점점 더 희어져 가는'"], ["‘Grey-headed,’ as he said, ‘and fast growing greyer.’ But generous, and frank, and good, I am sure.”", "분이라고요. 하지만 너그럽고, 솔직하고, 선량한 분인 게 분명해요.\""]], [["“And only seen once, Harriet?”", "\"한 번밖에 못 봤다고, 해리엇?\""]], [["“In this room only once,”", "\"이 방에서는 단 한 번이에요,\""], ["said his sister, with the slightest and most transient glow upon her cheek; “but when here, he entreated me to suffer him to see me once a week as he passed by, in token of our being well, and continuing to need nothing at his hands.", "누이가 뺨에 아주 희미하고 덧없는 홍조를 띠며 말했다, \"하지만 여기 계실 때, 그분은 지나는 길에 일주일에 한 번씩 저를 볼 수 있게 해 달라고 간청하셨어요. 우리가 잘 지내고 있다는, 그분 손에서 아무것도 필요로 하지 않는다는 표시로요."], ["For I told him, when he proffered us any service he could render—which was the object of his visit—that we needed nothing.”", "그분이 베풀 수 있는 봉사가 있으면 무엇이든 주겠다고 하셨을 때—그게 방문의 목적이었지요—제가 우리에게는 필요한 게 없다고 말씀드렸거든요.\""]], [["“And once a week—”", "\"그래서 일주일에 한 번씩—\""]], [["“Once every week since then, and always on the same day, and at the same hour, he his gone past; always on foot; always going in the same direction—towards London; and never pausing longer than to bow to me, and wave his hand cheerfully, as a kind guardian might.", "\"그 뒤로 매주 한 번씩, 늘 같은 날, 같은 시각에 그분이 지나가셨어요. 언제나 걸어서, 언제나 같은 방향으로요—런던 쪽으로요."], ["He made that promise when he proposed these curious interviews, and has kept it so faithfully and pleasantly, that if I ever felt any trifling uneasiness about them in the beginning (which I don’t think I did, John; his manner was so plain and true) it very soon vanished, and left me quite glad when the day was coming.", "그리고 잠깐 멈춰 서서 저에게 고개를 숙이고, 다정한 보호자처럼 명랑하게 손을 흔드시는 것 외에는 결코 더 머무는 법이 없으셨지요. 그 묘한 만남을 제안하셨을 때 그렇게 약속하셨고, 어찌나 충실하고 기분 좋게 지키셨던지, 처음에 제가 그 일을 두고 조금이라도 거북함을 느낀 적이 있었다 해도—그런 적이 있었던 것 같지도 않아요, 존 오라버니, 그분의 태도가 어찌나 꾸밈없고 진실했던지요—그건 곧 사라져 버리고, 그 날이 다가오면 오히려 기쁘기만 했답니다."], ["Last Monday—the first since this terrible event—he did not go by; and I have wondered whether his absence can have been in any way connected with what has happened.”", "그런데 지난 월요일에는—이 끔찍한 일이 있고 처음 맞는 월요일이었죠—그분이 지나가지 않으셨어요. 그래서 그분이 안 오신 게 혹시 이번에 일어난 일과 무슨 관련이 있는 건 아닐까 하고 궁금해하고 있었어요.\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어떻게 말이냐?\""], ["inquired her brother.", "오라비가 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know how.", "\"어떻게인지는 모르겠어요."], ["I have only speculated on the coincidence; I have not tried to account for it.", "그저 그 우연의 일치를 두고 이리저리 짐작만 해 봤을 뿐, 그걸 설명해 보려고 한 건 아니에요."], ["I feel sure he will return.", "그분이 분명 다시 오시리라는 건 확실해요."], ["When he does, dear John, let me tell him that I have at last spoken to you, and let me bring you together.", "그분이 오시면, 사랑하는 존 오라버니, 제가 마침내 오라버니께 이 이야기를 했다고 말씀드리고, 두 분을 만나게 해 드릴게요."], ["He will certainly help us to a new livelihood.", "그분은 분명 우리가 새 살길을 찾도록 도와주실 거예요."], ["His entreaty was that he might do something to smooth my life and yours; and I gave him my promise that if we ever wanted a friend, I would remember him.", "그분이 간청하신 건 제 삶과 오라버니의 삶을 조금이나마 편하게 해 줄 무언가를 하게 해 달라는 것이었어요. 그래서 저는 우리가 언젠가 친구를 필요로 하게 되면 그분을 떠올리겠다고 약속드렸지요."], ["Then his name was to be no secret.”", "그때가 되면 그분 이름도 더는 비밀이 아니게 되는 거고요.\""]], [["“Harriet,”", "\"해리엇,\""], ["said her brother, who had listened with close attention, “describe this gentleman to me.", "오라비가 골똘히 귀 기울여 듣고 있다가 말했다, \"그 신사분을 내게 그려 보아라."], ["I surely ought to know one who knows me so well.”", "나를 그토록 잘 아는 분이라면 나도 분명 알아봐야 할 텐데.\""]], [["His sister painted, as vividly as she could, the features, stature, and dress of her visitor; but John Carker, either from having no knowledge of the original, or from some fault in her description, or from some abstraction of his thoughts as he walked to and fro, pondering, could not recognise the portrait she presented to him.", "누이는 자기 손님의 이목구비며 체격이며 옷차림을 될 수 있는 한 생생하게 그려 보였다. 그러나 존 카커는, 본디 그 인물을 전혀 모르는 탓인지, 아니면 그녀의 묘사에 어떤 흠이 있어서인지, 아니면 그가 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 이리저리 거닐며 마음이 다른 데 가 있어서인지, 그녀가 내놓은 초상을 알아보지 못했다."]], [["However, it was agreed between them that he should see the original when he next appeared.", "그렇지만 두 사람은 그분이 다음번에 나타나면 오라비가 직접 그 실물을 보기로 합의했다."], ["This concluded, the sister applied herself, with a less anxious breast, to her domestic occupations; and the grey-haired man, late Junior of Dombey’s, devoted the first day of his unwonted liberty to working in the garden.", "이렇게 결말이 나자, 누이는 한결 가벼워진 마음으로 집안일에 몰두했다. 그리고 돔비 회사의 옛 차석이었던 그 머리 희끗한 사내는 뜻밖에 얻은 자유의 첫날을 정원 일을 하며 보냈다."]], [["It was quite late at night, and the brother was reading aloud while the sister plied her needle, when they were interrupted by a knocking at the door.", "밤이 꽤 깊어, 누이는 바늘을 놀리고 오라비는 소리 내어 책을 읽고 있을 때, 문을 두드리는 소리에 둘은 방해를 받았다."], ["In the atmosphere of vague anxiety and dread that lowered about them in connexion with their fugitive brother, this sound, unusual there, became almost alarming.", "달아난 동생과 관련하여 그들 주위로 짙게 드리워진 막연한 불안과 두려움의 분위기 속에서, 그곳에서는 흔치 않은 이 소리가 거의 섬뜩하게 느껴졌다."], ["The brother going to the door, the sister sat and listened timidly.", "오라비가 문으로 가는 동안, 누이는 앉은 채 겁먹은 듯 귀를 기울였다."], ["Someone spoke to him, and he replied and seemed surprised; and after a few words, the two approached together.", "누군가 그에게 말을 걸었고, 그는 대답하며 놀란 듯했다. 그리고 몇 마디 말이 오간 뒤, 두 사람이 함께 다가왔다."]], [["“Harriet,”", "\"해리엇,\""], ["said her brother, lighting in their late visitor, and speaking in a low voice, “Mr Morfin—the gentleman so long in Dombey’s House with James.”", "오라비가 늦은 손님을 데리고 들어오며 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"모핀 씨란다—제임스와 함께 돔비 회사에 그토록 오래 계시던 그분이야.\""]], [["His sister started back, as if a ghost had entered.", "누이는 마치 유령이라도 들어온 듯 흠칫 물러섰다."], ["In the doorway stood the unknown friend, with the dark hair sprinkled with grey, the ruddy face, the broad clear brow, and hazel eyes, whose secret she had kept so long!", "문간에는 그 낯 모를 친구가 서 있었으니, 희끗희끗 센 검은 머리에, 불그레한 얼굴, 넓고 훤한 이마, 그리고 그녀가 그토록 오래 비밀을 지켜 온 그 갈색 눈이었다!"]], [["“John!”", "\"존 오라버니!\""], ["she said, half-breathless.", "그녀가 거의 숨이 막힌 채 말했다."], ["“It is the gentleman I told you of, today!”", "\"오늘 제가 말씀드린 그 신사분이에요!\""]], [["“The gentleman, Miss Harriet,”", "\"이 신사가 말입니다, 해리엇 아가씨,\""], ["said the visitor, coming in—for he had stopped a moment in the doorway—“is greatly relieved to hear you say that: he has been devising ways and means, all the way here, of explaining himself, and has been satisfied with none.", "손님이 들어서며 말했다—그는 문간에서 잠시 멈춰 섰던 터였다—\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 무척 마음이 놓이는군요. 여기 오는 내내 제 처지를 어찌 설명할까 이런저런 방도를 궁리해 봤습니다만, 어느 것도 마음에 차질 않더군요."], ["Mr John, I am not quite a stranger here.", "존 군, 나는 여기서 아주 낯선 사람은 아니라네."], ["You were stricken with astonishment when you saw me at your door just now.", "방금 자네가 문간에서 나를 봤을 때 깜짝 놀랐지."], ["I observe you are more astonished at present.", "지금은 더 놀란 듯하군."], ["Well!", "뭐!"], ["That’s reasonable enough under existing circumstances.", "지금 사정으로 보면 그것도 무리는 아니지."], ["If we were not such creatures of habit as we are, we shouldn’t have reason to be astonished half so often.”", "우리가 이토록 습관의 피조물만 아니었다면, 이렇게 자주 놀랄 일도 없었을 텐데 말이야.\""]], [["By this time, he had greeted Harriet with that able mingling of cordiality and respect which she recollected so well, and had sat down near her, pulled off his gloves, and thrown them into his hat upon the table.", "이때쯤 그는, 그녀가 그토록 또렷이 기억하는 그 진심 어린 다정함과 정중함을 능숙하게 뒤섞은 태도로 해리엇에게 인사를 건넸고, 그녀 곁에 앉아 장갑을 벗어 탁자 위 모자 안에 던져 넣었다."]], [["“There’s nothing astonishing,” he said, “in my having conceived a desire to see your sister, Mr John, or in my having gratified it in my own way.", "\"내가 자네 누이를 보고 싶어졌다는 것, 존 군, 또 내 나름의 방식으로 그 소망을 채웠다는 것에는 조금도 놀랄 일이 없다네,\""], ["As to the regularity of my visits since (which she may have mentioned to you), there is nothing extraordinary in that.", "그가 말했다. \"그 뒤로 내 방문이 규칙적이었다는 것도—누이가 자네에게 말했을지 모르지만—거기에는 별스러운 게 없어."], ["They soon grew into a habit; and we are creatures of habit—creatures of habit!”", "그건 곧 습관이 되었거든. 우리는 습관의 피조물이니까—습관의 피조물이지!\""]], [["Putting his hands into his pockets, and leaning back in his chair, he looked at the brother and sister as if it were interesting to him to see them together; and went on to say, with a kind of irritable thoughtfulness: “It’s this same habit that confirms some of us, who are capable of better things, in Lucifer’s own pride and stubbornness—that confirms and deepens others of us in villainy—more of us in indifference —that hardens us from day to day, according to the temper of our clay, like images, and leaves us as susceptible as images to new impressions and convictions.", "두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 의자에 기대앉은 채, 그는 마치 형제와 누이가 함께 있는 모습을 보는 것이 흥미롭다는 듯 둘을 바라보았다. 그러고는 어딘가 짜증 섞인 사색의 어조로 말을 이었다."], ["You shall judge of its influence on me, John.", "\"바로 이 습관이라는 것이, 더 나은 일을 해낼 능력이 있는 우리 중 어떤 이들은 루시퍼 같은 오만과 완고함 속에 굳혀 버리고—또 어떤 이들은 악행 속에 굳히고 깊이 빠뜨리며—더 많은 이들을 무관심 속에 가둔다네—그것은 우리 흙의 기질에 따라 우리를 날마다 굳혀서 마치 조각상처럼 만들고, 그러면서도 새로운 인상과 신념에 대해서는 조각상처럼 무감각한 채로 우리를 내버려 두지."], ["For more years than I need name, I had my small, and exactly defined share, in the management of Dombey’s House, and saw your brother (who has proved himself a scoundrel!", "그것이 내게 끼친 영향을 자네가 판단해 보게, 존 군. 굳이 햇수를 댈 것도 없이 오랜 세월 동안, 나는 돔비 회사의 경영에서 작고도 정확히 정해진 내 몫만을 맡았고, 자네 동생이—그 자는 스스로 악당임을 입증했지!"], ["Your sister will forgive my being obliged to mention it) extending and extending his influence, until the business and its owner were his football; and saw you toiling at your obscure desk every day; and was quite content to be as little troubled as I might be, out of my own strip of duty, and to let everything about me go on, day by day, unquestioned, like a great machine—that was its habit and mine—and to take it all for granted, and consider it all right.", "누이께서 내가 부득이 이 말을 꺼내는 걸 용서하시겠지—제 영향력을 자꾸자꾸 넓혀, 마침내 사업도 그 주인도 그의 공이 되어 버리는 걸 지켜보았네. 또 자네가 그 후미진 책상에서 날마다 고되게 일하는 것도 보았지."], ["My Wednesday nights came regularly round, our quartette parties came regularly off, my violoncello was in good tune, and there was nothing wrong in my world—or if anything not much—or little or much, it was no affair of mine.”", "그러면서도 나는 내 의무의 그 한 가닥 밖으로는 될 수 있는 한 신경 쓰지 않는 데 만족했고, 내 주위의 모든 일이 마치 거대한 기계처럼 날마다 의문 없이 굴러가게 내버려 두었네—그게 그것의 습관이자 내 습관이었지—그리고 그 모든 걸 당연하게 여기고, 다 옳다고 치부했어. 내 수요일 밤은 어김없이 돌아왔고, 우리 사중주 모임은 어김없이 열렸으며, 내 첼로는 음이 잘 맞았고, 내 세계에는 아무런 잘못도 없었지—설령 무언가 있었다 해도 대수롭지 않았고—적든 많든 그건 내 알 바가 아니었던 거야.\""]], [["“I can answer for your being more respected and beloved during all that time than anybody in the House, Sir,”", "\"그 모든 세월 동안 회사의 그 누구보다도 선생님이 더 존경받고 사랑받으셨다는 건 제가 장담할 수 있습니다,\""], ["said John Carker.", "존 카커가 말했다."]], [["“Pooh!", "\"흥!"], ["Good-natured and easy enough, I daresay,”", "그저 마음씨 좋고 무던했을 뿐이겠지,\""], ["returned the other, “a habit I had.", "상대가 대답했다, \"그것도 내 습관이었어."], ["It suited the Manager; it suited the man he managed: it suited me best of all.", "그게 지배인의 마음에도 들었고, 그가 부린 사람들 마음에도 들었으며, 무엇보다 내게 가장 잘 맞았지."], ["I did what was allotted to me to do, made no court to either of them, and was glad to occupy a station in which none was required.", "나는 내게 할당된 일을 했고, 어느 쪽에도 아첨하지 않았으며, 그런 게 전혀 필요 없는 자리를 차지하고 있어서 기뻤다네."], ["So I should have gone on till now, but that my room had a thin wall.", "그렇게 지금껏 지내 왔을 거야, 내 방의 벽이 얇지만 않았더라면 말이지."], ["You can tell your sister that it was divided from the Manager’s room by a wainscot partition.”", "그게 지배인 방과는 판자 칸막이 하나로 나뉘어 있었다고 자네 누이에게 말해 주게나.\""]], [["“They were adjoining rooms; had been one, Perhaps, originally; and were separated, as Mr Morfin says,”", "\"두 방은 붙어 있었어요. 어쩌면 본래 한 방이었던 것을, 모핀 씨 말씀대로 나누어 놓은 것이겠지요,\""], ["said her brother, looking back to him for the resumption of his explanation.", "오라비가 설명을 마저 이어 가 주기를 바라며 그를 돌아보고 말했다."]], [["“I have whistled, hummed tunes, gone accurately through the whole of Beethoven’s Sonata in B, to let him know that I was within hearing,”", "\"나는 휘파람도 불고, 콧노래도 흥얼거리고, 베토벤의 B조 소나타를 처음부터 끝까지 정확히 연주해 보이기도 했네. 내가 다 듣고 있다는 걸 그에게 알리려고 말이야,\""], ["said Mr Morfin; “but he never heeded me.", "모핀 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 그는 결코 나를 개의치 않더군."], ["It happened seldom enough that I was within hearing of anything of a private nature, certainly.", "물론 내가 사적인 성격의 무언가를 엿들을 만한 거리에 있는 일은 드물었지."], ["But when I was, and couldn’t otherwise avoid knowing something of it, I walked out.", "하지만 그런 거리에 있게 되어, 무언가를 알아챌 수밖에 없을 때면, 나는 밖으로 나가 버렸네."], ["I walked out once, John, during a conversation between two brothers, to which, in the beginning, young Walter Gay was a party.", "한번은 두 형제가 나누는 대화 중에 나가 버린 적이 있어, 존 군. 그 대화의 처음에는 젊은 월터 게이도 끼어 있었지."], ["But I overheard some of it before I left the room.", "하지만 방을 나서기 전에 그 일부를 엿들었다네."], ["You remember it sufficiently, perhaps, to tell your sister what its nature was?”", "자네는 그게 어떤 내용이었는지 누이에게 말해 줄 만큼은 기억하고 있겠지?\""]], [["“It referred, Harriet,”", "\"그건 말이다, 해리엇,\""], ["said her brother in a low voice, “to the past, and to our relative positions in the House.”", "오라비가 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"지난날의 일과, 회사 안에서 우리 형제가 처한 상대적인 위치에 관한 것이었어.\""]], [["“Its matter was not new to me, but was presented in a new aspect.", "\"그 내용 자체는 내게 새로운 게 아니었지만, 새로운 면모로 다가왔다네."], ["It shook me in my habit—the habit of nine-tenths of the world—of believing that all was right about me, because I was used to it,”", "그것이 내 습관을 흔들었지—세상 사람 열에 아홉이 가진 습관, 즉 내가 익숙해져 있다는 이유만으로 내 주위의 모든 게 옳다고 믿어 버리는 그 습관 말일세,\""], ["said their visitor; “and induced me to recall the history of the two brothers, and to ponder on it.", "손님이 말했다. \"그래서 나는 두 형제의 내력을 되짚어 보고, 그것을 곰곰이 생각해 보게 되었어."], ["I think it was almost the first time in my life when I fell into this train of reflection—how will many things that are familiar, and quite matters of course to us now, look, when we come to see them from that new and distant point of view which we must all take up, one day or other?", "내 생각엔 그게 거의 내 평생 처음으로 이런 사색의 갈래에 빠져든 순간이었던 것 같네—지금 우리에게 익숙하고 너무도 당연한 그 많은 것들이, 언젠가 우리 모두가 반드시 서게 될 그 새롭고 아득한 시점에서 바라보면 과연 어떻게 보일까?"], ["I was something less good-natured, as the phrase goes, after that morning, less easy and complacent altogether.”", "그 아침 이후로 나는, 흔히 말하듯이, 좀 덜 마음씨 좋은 사람이 되었고, 전체적으로 덜 무던하고 덜 안일한 사람이 되었지.\""]], [["He sat for a minute or so, drumming with one hand on the table; and resumed in a hurry, as if he were anxious to get rid of his confession.", "그는 한 손으로 탁자를 톡톡 두드리며 한두 분쯤 앉아 있다가, 마치 제 고백을 어서 털어 버리고 싶어 안달이라도 난 듯 서둘러 말을 이었다."]], [["“Before I knew what to do, or whether I could do anything, there was a second conversation between the same two brothers, in which their sister was mentioned.", "\"내가 무엇을 해야 할지, 아니 무엇이든 할 수 있을지 미처 알기도 전에, 같은 두 형제 사이에 또 한 차례 대화가 있었고, 거기서 그들의 누이가 언급되었네."], ["I had no scruples of conscience in suffering all the waifs and strays of that conversation to float to me as freely as they would.", "나는 그 대화의 온갖 부스러기와 토막을 가리지 않고 마음껏 흘러들게 내버려 두면서도 양심에 거리낌이 없었어."], ["I considered them mine by right.", "그것들을 마땅히 내 것으로 여겼던 거지."], ["After that, I came here to see the sister for myself.", "그 뒤로 나는 직접 그 누이를 보려고 이곳에 왔네."], ["The first time I stopped at the garden gate, I made a pretext of inquiring into the character of a poor neighbour; but I wandered out of that tract, and I think Miss Harriet mistrusted me.", "처음에 정원 문 앞에 멈춰 섰을 때는, 어느 가난한 이웃의 사람됨을 알아보러 왔다는 핑계를 댔지. 하지만 그 핑계에서 벗어나 딴소리를 하고 말았고, 해리엇 아가씨가 나를 의심하셨던 것 같네."], ["The second time I asked leave to come in; came in; and said what I wished to say.", "두 번째에는 들어가게 해 달라고 청해서, 안으로 들어가, 하고 싶은 말을 했지. 자네 누이는 그때 내게서 도움을 받지 않을 이유들을 보여 주셨고, 나는 감히 그것을 반박할 수 없었어."], ["Your sister showed me reasons which I dared not dispute, for receiving no assistance from me then; but I established a means of communication between us, which remained unbroken until within these few days, when I was prevented, by important matters that have lately devolved upon me, from maintaining them.”", "다만 우리 사이에 연락할 방도를 마련해 두었는데, 그것이 며칠 전까지 끊어지지 않고 이어졌다네. 근래 내게 떨어진 중대한 일들 때문에 그걸 이어 가지 못하게 되기 전까지는 말이야.\""]], [["“How little I have suspected this,”", "\"제가 이걸 얼마나 까맣게 몰랐는지요,\""], ["said John Carker, “when I have seen you every day, Sir!", "존 카커가 말했다, \"선생님을 날마다 뵈면서도 말입니다!"], ["If Harriet could have guessed your name—”", "해리엇이 선생님 성함을 짐작이라도 할 수 있었다면—\""]], [["“Why, to tell you the truth, John,”", "\"실은 말일세, 존 군,\""], ["interposed the visitor, “I kept it to myself for two reasons.", "손님이 끼어들었다, \"내가 그걸 혼자 간직한 데는 두 가지 까닭이 있었네."], ["I don’t know that the first might have been binding alone; but one has no business to take credit for good intentions, and I made up my mind, at all events, not to disclose myself until I should be able to do you some real service or other.", "첫째 까닭만으로는 꼭 그래야 했을지 모르겠지만—사람이 좋은 의도를 가졌다고 공치사를 받을 일은 아니니까—어쨌든 나는 자네에게 무언가 진짜 도움이 될 만한 일을 할 수 있게 되기 전까지는 내 정체를 밝히지 않기로 마음먹었네."], ["My second reason was, that I always hoped there might be some lingering possibility of your brother’s relenting towards you both; and in that case, I felt that where there was the chance of a man of his suspicious, watchful character, discovering that you had been secretly befriended by me, there was the chance of a new and fatal cause of division.", "둘째 까닭은, 자네 동생이 두 사람에 대해 마음을 누그러뜨릴 가능성이 조금이라도 남아 있기를 늘 바랐기 때문이야. 그런 경우에, 그처럼 의심 많고 경계심 강한 성품을 가진 자가 자네들이 나에게 몰래 도움을 받았다는 걸 알아챌 위험이 있다면, 거기엔 새롭고도 치명적인 불화의 씨앗이 생길 위험이 있다고 느꼈지."], ["I resolved, to be sure, at the risk of turning his displeasure against myself—which would have been no matter—to watch my opportunity of serving you with the head of the House; but the distractions of death, courtship, marriage, and domestic unhappiness, have left us no head but your brother for this long, long time.", "그래서 나는, 그의 노여움을 차라리 내 쪽으로 돌리는 한이 있더라도—그건 아무래도 좋았네만—회사의 우두머리를 통해 자네들을 도울 기회를 엿보기로 단단히 작정했었네. 그러나 죽음, 구애, 결혼, 가정의 불행이라는 어지러운 일들 탓에, 이 오랜, 참으로 오랜 세월 동안 우리에게는 자네 동생 말고는 우두머리라 할 사람이 없었어."], ["And it would have been better for us,”", "그리고 우리에게는 차라리,\""], ["said the visitor, dropping his voice, “to have been a lifeless trunk.”", "손님이 목소리를 낮추며 말했다, \"생명 없는 몸뚱이가 우두머리였던 편이 더 나았을 걸세.\""]], [["He seemed conscious that these latter words had escaped him against his will, and stretching out a hand to the brother, and a hand to the sister, continued:", "그는 이 마지막 말이 본의 아니게 입 밖으로 새어 나왔음을 의식한 듯, 한 손은 오라비에게, 한 손은 누이에게 내밀며 말을 이었다."]], [["“All I could desire to say, and more, I have now said.", "\"내가 하고 싶었던 말은, 그리고 그 이상도, 이제 다 했네."], ["All I mean goes beyond words, as I hope you understand and believe.", "내가 뜻하는 바는 말로 다할 수 없는 것이지만, 자네들이 알아듣고 믿어 주리라 믿네."], ["The time has come, John—though most unfortunately and unhappily come—when I may help you without interfering with that redeeming struggle, which has lasted through so many years; since you were discharged from it today by no act of your own.", "때가 왔네, 존 군—참으로 불운하고 불행하게도 왔지만—내가 그 오랜 세월 이어진 속죄의 분투를 방해하지 않으면서 자네를 도울 수 있는 때가 말이야. 자네는 자네 자신의 어떤 행동 때문이 아니라, 오늘 그 분투에서 해고당한 셈이니까."], ["It is late; I need say no more tonight.", "이미 늦었군. 오늘 밤엔 더 말할 게 없네."], ["You will guard the treasure you have here, without advice or reminder from me.”", "자네는 내 조언이나 일깨움 없이도 여기 있는 이 보물을 지켜 낼 테지.\""]], [["With these words he rose to go.", "이 말과 함께 그는 떠나려고 일어섰다."]], [["“But go you first, John,”", "\"하지만 자네가 먼저 가게, 존 군,\""], ["he said goodhumouredly, “with a light, without saying what you want to say, whatever that may be;”", "그가 너그러운 투로 말했다, \"불을 들고서, 하고 싶은 말이 무엇이든 그건 말하지 말고 말일세.\""], ["John Carker’s heart was full, and he would have relieved it in speech, if he could; “and let me have a word with your sister.", "존 카커는 가슴이 벅차올라, 할 수만 있다면 말로 그것을 풀어내고 싶었다. \"그리고 내가 자네 누이와 한마디 나누게 해 주게."], ["We have talked alone before, and in this room too; though it looks more natural with you here.”", "우리는 전에도, 그것도 이 방에서, 단둘이 이야기를 나눈 적이 있다네. 자네가 여기 있는 편이 더 자연스러워 보이긴 하지만 말이야.\""]], [["Following him out with his eyes, he turned kindly to Harriet, and said in a lower voice, and with an altered and graver manner:", "그를 눈으로 좇으며 배웅하고 나서, 그는 다정하게 해리엇에게로 돌아서서, 한층 낮은 목소리로, 사뭇 달라진 더 진지한 태도로 말했다."]], [["“You wish to ask me something of the man whose sister it is your misfortune to be.”", "\"누이로 지내야 하는 것이 불행인 그 사내에 대해, 아가씨는 내게 무언가 묻고 싶으시겠지요.\""]], [["“I dread to ask,”", "\"여쭙기가 두려워요,\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."]], [["“You have looked so earnestly at me more than once,”", "\"아가씨가 한 번도 아니고 여러 번 그토록 간절하게 나를 바라보셨으니,\""], ["rejoined the visitor, “that I think I can divine your question.", "손님이 대답했다, \"그 물음을 알아맞힐 수 있을 것 같군요."], ["Has he taken money?", "그가 돈에 손을 댔느냐?"], ["Is it that?”", "그것이지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“He has not.”", "\"손대지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“I thank Heaven!”", "\"하느님 감사합니다!\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."], ["“For the sake of John.”", "\"존 오라버니를 위해서요.\""]], [["“That he has abused his trust in many ways,”", "\"그가 여러모로 직무상의 신뢰를 저버렸다는 것,\""], ["said Mr Morfin; “that he has oftener dealt and speculated to advantage for himself, than for the House he represented; that he has led the House on, to prodigious ventures, often resulting in enormous losses; that he has always pampered the vanity and ambition of his employer, when it was his duty to have held them in check, and shown, as it was in his power to do, to what they tended here or there; will not, perhaps, surprise you now.", "모핀 씨가 말했다, \"자기가 대표하던 회사를 위해서보다 제 자신을 위해서 거래하고 투기한 적이 더 잦았다는 것, 회사를 엄청난 모험으로 끌고 들어가 종종 막대한 손실로 이어지게 했다는 것, 고용주의 허영과 야심을 억눌러야 마땅했고 또 그렇게 할 힘이 있었음에도, 그것이 이리저리 어디로 향하는지를 보여 주기는커녕 늘 그 허영과 야심을 부추겼다는 것—이런 일들은 이제 아가씨께 그리 놀랍지 않으실 겁니다."], ["Undertakings have been entered on, to swell the reputation of the House for vast resources, and to exhibit it in magnificent contrast to other merchants’ Houses, of which it requires a steady head to contemplate the possibly—a few disastrous changes of affairs might render them the probably—ruinous consequences.", "회사가 막대한 자원을 지녔다는 평판을 부풀리고, 다른 상인들의 회사와 견주어 더없이 화려하게 돋보이게 하려고 여러 사업이 벌어졌는데, 그 사업들이 가져올 수도 있는—몇 차례 운수 나쁜 변동만 닥쳐도 가져오기 십상인—파멸적인 결과를 헤아리려면 침착한 머리가 필요할 정도지요."], ["In the midst of the many transactions of the House, in most parts of the world: a great labyrinth of which only he has held the clue: he has had the opportunity, and he seems to have used it, of keeping the various results afloat, when ascertained, and substituting estimates and generalities for facts.", "세계 곳곳에 걸친 회사의 그 숱한 거래들 한복판에서—오직 그만이 실마리를 쥐고 있는 거대한 미로 같은 것이지요—그는 기회를 잡았고, 또 그걸 이용한 듯합니다. 결과가 드러나도 그 갖가지 결과를 떠다니는 채로 내버려 두고, 사실 대신에 추정과 두루뭉술한 말로 채워 넣는 식으로 말이지요."], ["But latterly—you follow me, Miss Harriet?”", "그런데 근래에 들어서는—제 말을 따라오고 계십니까, 해리엇 아가씨?\""]], [["“Perfectly, perfectly,”", "\"완벽하게요, 완벽하게요,\""], ["she answered, with her frightened face fixed on his.", "그녀가 겁에 질린 얼굴을 그에게 고정한 채 대답했다."], ["“Pray tell me all the worst at once.”", "\"제발 가장 나쁜 것까지 한꺼번에 다 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“Latterly, he appears to have devoted the greatest pains to making these results so plain and clear, that reference to the private books enables one to grasp them, numerous and varying as they are, with extraordinary ease.", "\"근래에 들어 그는, 이 결과들을 아주 또렷하고 명료하게 만드는 데 가장 큰 공을 들인 듯합니다. 그 결과들이 아무리 많고 다양하다 해도, 비밀 장부만 들춰 보면 누구든 놀랄 만큼 손쉽게 그것들을 파악할 수 있도록 말이지요."], ["As if he had resolved to show his employer at one broad view what has been brought upon him by ministration to his ruling passion!", "마치 제 고용주에게, 그 지배적인 격정에 영합한 결과 무엇이 닥쳐왔는지를 단번에 넓게 펼쳐 보여 주기로 작정이라도 한 것처럼요!"], ["That it has been his constant practice to minister to that passion basely, and to flatter it corruptly, is indubitable.", "그가 그 격정에 비열하게 영합하고, 부패하게 아첨하는 것을 늘 일삼아 왔다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없습니다."], ["In that, his criminality, as it is connected with the affairs of the House, chiefly consists.”", "회사의 일과 관련된 그의 범죄성은 주로 거기에 있지요.\""]], [["“One other word before you leave me, dear Sir,”", "\"가시기 전에 한 마디만 더요, 선생님,\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."], ["“There is no danger in all this?”", "\"이 모든 일에 위험은 없는 건가요?\""]], [["“How danger?”", "\"위험이라니요?\""], ["he returned, with a little hesitation.", "그가 조금 머뭇거리며 되물었다."]], [["“To the credit of the House?”", "\"회사의 신용에 말이에요.\""]], [["“I cannot help answering you plainly, and trusting you completely,”", "\"아가씨께 솔직히 말씀드리고, 또 아가씨를 온전히 믿지 않을 수가 없군요,\""], ["said Mr Morfin, after a moment’s survey of her face.", "모핀 씨가 잠시 그녀의 얼굴을 살핀 뒤 말했다."]], [["“You may.", "\"믿으셔도 돼요."], ["Indeed you may!”", "정말로 믿으셔도 됩니다!\""]], [["“I am sure I may.", "\"믿어도 되겠지요."], ["Danger to the House’s credit?", "회사의 신용에 위험이 있느냐고요? 아니요, 전혀 없습니다."], ["No; none There may be difficulty, greater or less difficulty, but no danger, unless—unless, indeed—the head of the House, unable to bring his mind to the reduction of its enterprises, and positively refusing to believe that it is, or can be, in any position but the position in which he has always represented it to himself, should urge it beyond its strength.", "어려움은 있을지 모르지요, 더 크거나 더 작은 어려움이요. 하지만 위험은 없습니다—다만—참으로 다만—회사의 우두머리가 그 사업들을 줄이는 쪽으로 마음을 돌리지 못하고, 회사가 지금까지 자기가 늘 스스로에게 그려 보여 온 그 위치 말고 다른 어떤 위치에 있다는 것을, 아니 있을 수 있다는 것을 한사코 믿으려 들지 않은 채, 그것을 제 힘 이상으로 밀어붙인다면 모르지요."], ["Then it would totter.”", "그러면 휘청거리게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“But there is no apprehension of that?”", "\"하지만 그럴 염려는 없는 거지요?\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."]], [["“There shall be no half-confidence,”", "\"우리 사이에 어설픈 신뢰란 없어야지요,\""], ["he replied, shaking her hand, “between us.", "그가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 대답했다."], ["Mr Dombey is unapproachable by anyone, and his state of mind is haughty, rash, unreasonable, and ungovernable, now.", "\"돔비 씨는 지금 그 누구도 가까이 다가갈 수 없는 분이고, 그의 마음 상태는 오만하고, 무모하고, 비이성적이며, 걷잡을 수 없습니다."], ["But he is disturbed and agitated now beyond all common bounds, and it may pass.", "하지만 지금 그분은 온갖 정상의 범위를 넘어설 만큼 어지럽고 동요되어 있으니, 그것도 지나갈지 모르지요."], ["You now know all, both worst and best.", "이제 아가씨는 모든 걸 다 아셨습니다, 가장 나쁜 것도 가장 좋은 것도요."], ["No more tonight, and good-night!”", "오늘 밤은 더 말하지 맙시다, 안녕히 주무세요!\""]], [["With that he kissed her hand, and, passing out to the door where her brother stood awaiting his coming, put him cheerfully aside when he essayed to speak; told him that, as they would see each other soon and often, he might speak at another time, if he would, but there was no leisure for it then; and went away at a round pace, in order that no word of gratitude might follow him.", "그러고 나서 그는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추고, 오라비가 들어오기를 기다리며 서 있던 문 쪽으로 나가, 오라비가 무슨 말을 하려 하자 명랑하게 옆으로 비켜서게 하고는, 어차피 곧 자주 만나게 될 테니 말하고 싶으면 다음에 하라고, 지금은 그럴 겨를이 없다고 일렀다. 그리고 어떤 감사의 말도 자기를 뒤따르지 못하도록 빠른 걸음으로 떠나갔다."]], [["The brother and sister sat conversing by the fireside, until it was almost day; made sleepless by this glimpse of the new world that opened before them, and feeling like two people shipwrecked long ago, upon a solitary coast, to whom a ship had come at last, when they were old in resignation, and had lost all thought of any other home.", "형과 누이는 거의 동이 틀 무렵까지 난롯가에 앉아 이야기를 나누었다. 그들 앞에 열린 새로운 세계를 언뜻 본 탓에 잠을 이루지 못했으니, 마치 오래전 외딴 해안에서 난파당한 두 사람이, 이미 체념에 늙어 버리고 다른 집은 생각조차 잃어버렸을 무렵에야 마침내 배 한 척이 다가온 것 같은 심정이었다."], ["But another and different kind of disquietude kept them waking too.", "그러나 또 다른, 사뭇 다른 종류의 불안도 그들을 깨어 있게 했다."], ["The darkness out of which this light had broken on them gathered around; and the shadow of their guilty brother was in the house where his foot had never trod.", "이 빛이 그들에게 비쳐 든 그 어둠이 다시 주위로 모여들었다. 그리고 죄지은 동생의 그림자가, 그의 발이 한 번도 디딘 적 없는 그 집 안에 드리워 있었다."]], [["Nor was it to be driven out, nor did it fade before the sun.", "그 그림자는 쫓아낼 수도, 해가 떠도 사라지지도 않았다. 이튿날 아침에도 그것은 거기 있었다."], ["Next morning it was there; at noon; at night Darkest and most distinct at night, as is now to be told.", "정오에도, 밤에도. 밤이 가장 어둡고 가장 또렷했으니, 이제 이야기할 그대로다."]], [["John Carker had gone out, in pursuance of a letter of appointment from their friend, and Harriet was left in the house alone.", "존 카커는 친구가 보낸 약속 편지에 따라 외출했고, 해리엇은 홀로 집에 남았다. 그녀가 혼자 있은 지 몇 시간이 지났다."], ["She had been alone some hours. A dull, grave evening, and a deepening twilight, were not favourable to the removal of the oppression on her spirits.", "음울하고 무거운 저녁과 점점 깊어 가는 어스름은 그녀의 마음을 짓누르는 무게를 걷어 내기에 좋은 것이 못 되었다."], ["The idea of this brother, long unseen and unknown, flitted about her in frightful shapes.", "오래도록 보지 못해 알지도 못하는 그 동생의 모습이 무시무시한 형상으로 그녀 주위를 떠돌았다."], ["He was dead, dying, calling to her, staring at her, frowning on her.", "그는 죽어 있었고, 죽어 가고 있었고, 그녀를 부르고 있었고, 그녀를 노려보고 있었고, 그녀에게 얼굴을 찌푸리고 있었다."], ["The pictures in her mind were so obtrusive and exact that, as the twilight deepened, she dreaded to raise her head and look at the dark corners of the room, lest his wraith, the offspring of her excited imagination, should be waiting there, to startle her.", "마음속의 그 영상들이 어찌나 집요하고 또렷하던지, 어스름이 짙어질수록 그녀는 고개를 들어 방의 어두운 구석을 바라보기가 두려웠다. 그 흥분한 상상이 낳은 그의 망령이 거기서 자기를 놀래키려 기다리고 있을까 봐."], ["Once she had such a fancy of his being in the next room, hiding—though she knew quite well what a distempered fancy it was, and had no belief in it—that she forced herself to go there, for her own conviction.", "한번은 그가 옆방에 숨어 있다는 환상에 사로잡혀—그것이 얼마나 병적인 환상인지 잘 알았고 조금도 믿지 않았으면서도—제 마음을 다잡으려고 억지로 그리로 가 보았다. 그러나 헛일이었다."], ["But in vain. The room resumed its shadowy terrors, the moment she left it; and she had no more power to divest herself of these vague impressions of dread, than if they had been stone giants, rooted in the solid earth.", "방을 나서는 순간 그곳은 다시 어슴푸레한 공포에 휩싸였고, 그녀는 마치 그 막연한 두려움의 인상들이 단단한 땅에 뿌리박은 돌 거인이기라도 한 듯, 도무지 거기서 벗어날 힘이 없었다."]], [["It was almost dark, and she was sitting near the window, with her head upon her hand, looking down, when, sensible of a sudden increase in the gloom of the apartment, she raised her eyes, and uttered an involuntary cry.", "거의 어두워졌을 무렵, 그녀는 창가에 앉아 손으로 머리를 괴고 아래를 내려다보고 있었다. 그때 방 안의 어둠이 갑자기 짙어지는 것을 느끼고 눈을 들었다가, 자기도 모르게 비명을 질렀다."], ["Close to the glass, a pale scared face gazed in; vacantly, for an instant, as searching for an object; then the eyes rested on herself, and lighted up.", "유리에 바싹 붙어, 창백하고 겁에 질린 얼굴 하나가 안을 들여다보고 있었다. 한순간은 무언가를 찾는 듯 멍하니, 그러더니 그 눈이 그녀에게 머물면서 환히 빛났다."]], [["“Let me in!", "\"들여보내 줘요!"], ["Let me in!", "들여보내 줘요!"], ["I want to speak to you!”", "당신에게 할 말이 있어요!\""], ["and the hand rattled on the glass.", "그러면서 손이 유리를 두드려 댔다."]], [["She recognised immediately the woman with the long dark hair, to whom she had given warmth, food, and shelter, one wet night.", "그녀는 그 검고 긴 머리의 여자를 곧바로 알아보았다. 비 오던 어느 밤 자기가 따뜻함과 음식과 잠자리를 베풀어 주었던 여자였다."], ["Naturally afraid of her, remembering her violent behaviour, Harriet, retreating a little from the window, stood undecided and alarmed.", "그 여자의 사나운 행동을 떠올리며 자연히 두려운 마음이 든 해리엇은, 창가에서 조금 물러서서 어찌할 바를 모르고 겁에 질린 채 서 있었다."]], [["“Let me in!", "\"들여보내 줘요!"], ["Let me speak to you!", "당신에게 말하게 해 줘요!"], ["I am thankful—quiet—humble—anything you like.", "나는 고마워하고 있어요—조용히—겸손하게—당신이 바라는 대로 뭐든지 할게요."], ["But let me speak to you.”", "그저 당신에게 말하게만 해 줘요.\""]], [["The vehement manner of the entreaty, the earnest expression of the face, the trembling of the two hands that were raised imploringly, a certain dread and terror in the voice akin to her own condition at the moment, prevailed with Harriet.", "그 간청의 격렬한 태도, 얼굴에 어린 간절한 표정, 애원하듯 치켜든 두 손의 떨림, 그리고 그 순간 그녀 자신의 처지와도 닮은 어떤 두려움과 공포가 깃든 목소리—이런 것들이 해리엇의 마음을 움직였다."], ["She hastened to the door and opened it.", "그녀는 서둘러 문으로 가 문을 열었다."]], [["“May I come in, or shall I speak here?”", "\"들어가도 될까요, 아니면 여기서 말할까요?\""], ["said the woman, catching at her hand.", "여자가 그녀의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다."]], [["“What is it that you want?", "\"무엇을 원하는 거예요?"], ["What is it that you have to say?”", "할 말이 뭐예요?\""]], [["“Not much, but let me say it out, or I shall never say it.", "\"많지 않아요, 하지만 다 말하게 해 줘요. 안 그러면 영영 말하지 못할 거예요."], ["I am tempted now to go away.", "지금 나는 그냥 가 버리고 싶은 유혹이 들어요."], ["There seem to be hands dragging me from the door.", "무슨 손들이 나를 문에서 끌어내는 것만 같아요."], ["Let me come in, if you can trust me for this once!”", "이번 한 번만 나를 믿어 줄 수 있다면, 들어가게 해 줘요!\""]], [["Her energy again prevailed, and they passed into the firelight of the little kitchen, where she had before sat, and ate, and dried her clothes.", "그녀의 그 절박함이 또다시 통했고, 두 사람은 작은 부엌의 난롯불 빛 속으로 들어갔다. 전에 그녀가 앉아서 먹고 옷을 말리던 곳이었다."]], [["“Sit there,”", "\"거기 앉아요,\""], ["said Alice, kneeling down beside her, “and look at me.", "앨리스가 그녀 곁에 무릎 꿇으며 말했다, \"그리고 나를 봐요."], ["You remember me?”", "나를 기억하지요?\""]], [["“I do.”", "\"기억해요.\""]], [["“You remember what I told you I had been, and where I came from, ragged and lame, with the fierce wind and weather beating on my head?”", "\"내가 무엇이었는지, 또 어디서 왔는지, 누더기를 걸치고 다리를 절며, 사나운 바람과 비바람을 머리에 맞으며 왔다고 말한 것을 기억하지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“You know how I came back that night, and threw your money in the dirt, and you and your race.", "\"그날 밤 내가 돌아와서, 당신이 준 돈을 흙바닥에 내던지고, 당신과 당신 핏줄을 내던졌던 걸 알지요."], ["Now, see me here, upon my knees.", "자, 지금 여기 무릎 꿇은 나를 봐요."], ["Am I less earnest now, than I was then?”", "지금의 나는 그때의 나보다 덜 간절한가요?\""]], [["“If what you ask,”", "\"당신이 청하는 것이,\""], ["said Harriet, gently, “is forgiveness—”", "해리엇이 부드럽게 말했다, \"용서라면—\""]], [["“But it’s not!”", "\"하지만 그게 아니에요!\""], ["returned the other, with a proud, fierce look “What I ask is to be believed.", "상대가 자존심 어린, 사나운 눈빛으로 응수했다. \"내가 청하는 건 믿어 달라는 거예요."], ["Now you shall judge if I am worthy of belief, both as I was, and as I am.”", "자, 그때의 나로서도, 지금의 나로서도, 내가 믿을 만한 사람인지 당신이 판단해 봐요.\""]], [["Still upon her knees, and with her eyes upon the fire, and the fire shining on her ruined beauty and her wild black hair, one long tress of which she pulled over her shoulder, and wound about her hand, and thoughtfully bit and tore while speaking, she went on:", "여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채, 두 눈은 난롯불에 두고, 그 불빛이 그녀의 망가진 미모와 거칠게 헝클어진 검은 머리를 비추는 가운데, 그녀는 그 긴 머리 타래 한 가닥을 어깨 너머로 끌어다 손에 감고는, 말하는 동안 생각에 잠긴 듯 그것을 깨물고 잡아 뜯으며 말을 이었다."]], [["“When I was young and pretty, and this,”", "\"내가 젊고 예뻤을 때, 그리고 이게,\""], ["plucking contemptuously at the hair she held, “was only handled delicately, and couldn’t be admired enough, my mother, who had not been very mindful of me as a child, found out my merits, and was fond of me, and proud of me.", "손에 쥔 머리채를 경멸하듯 잡아당기며, \"그저 곱게 다루어지기만 하고 아무리 칭찬해도 모자랄 정도였을 때, 어릴 적 나를 그다지 마음에 두지 않던 우리 어머니가 내 장점을 알아채고는 나를 아끼고, 나를 자랑스러워했어요."], ["She was covetous and poor, and thought to make a sort of property of me.", "어머니는 탐욕스럽고 가난해서, 나를 일종의 재산으로 삼으려 했지요."], ["No great lady ever thought that of a daughter yet, I’m sure, or acted as if she did—it’s never done, we all know—and that shows that the only instances of mothers bringing up their daughters wrong, and evil coming of it, are among such miserable folks as us.”", "어떤 귀부인도 딸을 두고 그런 생각을 한 적이 없을 거예요, 분명히. 또 그런 듯이 행동한 적도 없겠지요—그런 일은 결코 없으니까요, 우리 다 알다시피—그러니 그건, 딸을 그릇되게 키워서 거기서 화가 비롯되는 어미의 사례란 오직 우리 같은 비참한 인간들 사이에서만 있다는 걸 보여 주는 거지요.\""]], [["Looking at the fire, as if she were forgetful, for the moment, of having any auditor, she continued in a dreamy way, as she wound the long tress of hair tight round and round her hand.", "마치 그 순간 듣는 이가 있다는 것조차 잊은 듯 난롯불을 바라보며, 그녀는 긴 머리 타래를 손에 단단히 칭칭 감으며 꿈결 같은 투로 말을 이었다."]], [["“What came of that, I needn’t say.", "\"그 끝에 무슨 일이 있었는지는 말할 것도 없지요."], ["Wretched marriages don’t come of such things, in our degree; only wretchedness and ruin.", "우리 같은 처지에서 그런 일들의 끝에 비참한 결혼 따위가 오지는 않아요. 그저 비참함과 파멸뿐이지요."], ["Wretchedness and ruin came on me—came on me.”", "비참함과 파멸이 내게 닥쳤어요—내게 닥쳤어요.\""]], [["Raising her eyes swiftly from their moody gaze upon the fire, to Harriet’s face, she said:", "난롯불을 음울하게 응시하던 눈을 재빨리 들어 해리엇의 얼굴을 향하며, 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I am wasting time, and there is none to spare; yet if I hadn’t thought of all, I shouldn’t be here now.", "\"내가 시간을 허비하고 있군요, 한시도 헛되이 할 틈이 없는데. 하지만 내가 모든 걸 다 생각해 보지 않았더라면, 지금 여기 와 있지도 않을 거예요."], ["Wretchedness and ruin came on me, I say.", "비참함과 파멸이 내게 닥쳤어요, 말했듯이."], ["I was made a short-lived toy, and flung aside more cruelly and carelessly than even such things are.", "나는 수명 짧은 노리개가 되었다가, 그런 물건들보다도 더 잔인하고 무심하게 내던져졌지요."], ["By whose hand do you think?”", "누구의 손에 그랬다고 생각해요?\""]], [["“Why do you ask me?”", "\"왜 내게 묻는 거예요?\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."]], [["“Why do you tremble?”", "\"왜 떨고 있어요?\""], ["rejoined Alice, with an eager look.", "앨리스가 간절한 눈빛으로 응수했다."], ["“His usage made a Devil of me.", "\"그자의 그 처사가 나를 악마로 만들었어요."], ["I sunk in wretchedness and ruin, lower and lower yet.", "나는 비참함과 파멸 속으로 자꾸자꾸 더 깊이 가라앉았지요."], ["I was concerned in a robbery—in every part of it but the gains—and was found out, and sent to be tried, without a friend, without a penny.", "어떤 강도질에 가담했는데—그 일의 모든 부분에 가담했지만 그 몫만은 받지 못했어요—발각되어, 친구 하나 없이, 한 푼도 없이 재판을 받게 됐어요."], ["Though I was but a girl, I would have gone to Death, sooner than ask him for a word, if a word of his could have saved me.", "비록 어린 계집애였지만, 그자의 말 한마디가 나를 구할 수 있었다 해도, 그자에게 한마디 청하느니 차라리 죽음을 택했을 거예요."], ["I would!", "정말이에요!"], ["To any death that could have been invented.", "어떤 식으로든 고안해 낼 수 있는 어떤 죽음이라도요."], ["But my mother, covetous always, sent to him in my name, told the true story of my case, and humbly prayed and petitioned for a small last gift—for not so many pounds as I have fingers on this hand.", "하지만 늘 탐욕스러운 우리 어머니가 내 이름으로 그자에게 사람을 보내, 내 처지의 진상을 죄다 일러바치고, 마지막으로 작은 선물 하나를 겸손히 빌고 간청했어요—이 손가락 수만큼도 안 되는 몇 파운드를요."], ["Who was it, do you think, who snapped his fingers at me in my misery, lying, as he believed, at his feet, and left me without even this poor sign of remembrance; well satisfied that I should be sent abroad, beyond the reach of farther trouble to him, and should die, and rot there?", "그자가 믿기로는 제 발치에 엎드린 셈인 나를, 그 비참함 속에서 손가락을 튕겨 내쫓고, 이 보잘것없는 기억의 표시 하나조차 남기지 않고 가 버린 게 누구라고 생각해요? 내가 외국으로 보내져, 자기를 더 성가시게 할 일이 없는 곳으로 가서, 죽어 거기서 썩어 버리리라는 데 흡족해하면서요."], ["Who was this, do you think?”", "이게 누구라고 생각해요?\""]], [["“Why do you ask me?”", "\"왜 내게 묻는 거예요?\""], ["repeated Harriet.", "해리엇이 되풀이했다."]], [["“Why do you tremble?”", "\"왜 떨고 있어요?\""], ["said Alice, laying her hand upon her arm, and looking in her face, “but that the answer is on your lips!", "앨리스가 그녀의 팔에 손을 얹고 그 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다, \"그 답이 당신 입술에 떠올라 있는데요!"], ["It was your brother James.”", "당신 오빠 제임스였어요.\""]], [["Harriet trembled more and more, but did not avert her eyes from the eager look that rested on them.", "해리엇은 점점 더 떨었지만, 자기에게 머무는 그 간절한 눈길에서 시선을 돌리지 않았다."]], [["“When I knew you were his sister—which was on that night—I came back, weary and lame, to spurn your gift.", "\"당신이 그자의 누이라는 걸 알았을 때—그건 그날 밤이었지요—나는 지치고 다리를 절며 돌아와, 당신의 선물을 내팽개쳤어요."], ["I felt that night as if I could have travelled, weary and lame, over the whole world, to stab him, if I could have found him in a lonely place with no one near.", "그날 밤 나는, 그자를 인적 없는 곳에서 곁에 아무도 없이 찾아낼 수만 있다면, 그를 찔러 죽이려고 지치고 절뚝거리는 몸으로 온 세상을 다 헤매고 다닐 수도 있을 것만 같았어요."], ["Do you believe that I was earnest in all that?”", "내가 그 모든 것에 진심이었다는 걸 믿어요?\""]], [["“I do!", "\"믿어요!"], ["Good Heaven, why are you come again?”", "맙소사, 그런데 왜 또 온 거예요?\""]], [["“Since then,”", "\"그 뒤로,\""], ["said Alice, with the same grasp of her arm, and the same look in her face, “I have seen him!", "앨리스가 여전히 그녀의 팔을 붙잡고, 여전히 같은 눈빛으로 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다, \"나는 그자를 봤어요!"], ["I have followed him with my eyes.", "내 눈으로 그자를 좇았어요."], ["In the broad day.", "환한 대낮에요."], ["If any spark of my resentment slumbered in my bosom, it sprung into a blaze when my eyes rested on him.", "내 가슴속에 잠들어 있던 원한의 불씨가 조금이라도 남아 있었다면, 그자에게 눈길이 닿는 순간 그게 활활 타올랐어요."], ["You know he has wronged a proud man, and made him his deadly enemy.", "그자가 어떤 오만한 사내에게 못된 짓을 해서, 그를 자기 철천지원수로 만들었다는 걸 당신도 알지요."], ["What if I had given information of him to that man?”", "만약 내가 그 사내에게 그자에 대한 정보를 일러바쳤다면 어떨까요?\""]], [["“Information!”", "\"정보라뇨!\""], ["repeated Harriet.", "해리엇이 되풀이했다."]], [["“What if I had found out one who knew your brother’s secret; who knew the manner of his flight, who knew where he and the companion of his flight were gone?", "\"만약 내가 당신 오빠의 비밀을 아는 자를 찾아냈다면 어떨까요. 그가 어떻게 달아났는지, 그와 그 도주의 동행이 어디로 갔는지를 아는 자를요."], ["What if I had made him utter all his knowledge, word by word, before his enemy, concealed to hear it?", "만약 내가 그자로 하여금 자기가 아는 것을 한마디 한마디 죄다, 그걸 들으려고 숨어 있는 원수 앞에서 털어놓게 만들었다면 어떨까요?"], ["What if I had sat by at the time, looking into this enemy’s face, and seeing it change till it was scarcely human?", "만약 내가 그때 곁에 앉아, 이 원수의 얼굴을 들여다보며, 그것이 거의 사람의 것이라 할 수 없을 만큼 변해 가는 걸 지켜보았다면요?"], ["What if I had seen him rush away, mad, in pursuit?", "만약 내가 그가 미쳐서 추격에 나서는 걸 봤다면요?"], ["What if I knew, now, that he was on his road, more fiend than man, and must, in so many hours, come up with him?”", "만약 내가 지금, 그가 사람이라기보다 악귀에 가까운 모습으로 길에 올라, 몇 시간 안에 그자를 따라잡고야 말리라는 걸 안다면요?\""]], [["“Remove your hand!”", "\"손을 치워요!\""], ["said Harriet, recoiling.", "해리엇이 움찔 물러서며 말했다."], ["“Go away!", "\"가 버려요!"], ["Your touch is dreadful to me!”", "당신 손길이 내겐 끔찍해요!\""]], [["“I have done this,”", "\"내가 이 일을 했어요,\""], ["pursued the other, with her eager look, regardless of the interruption.", "상대가 그 방해에도 아랑곳없이 간절한 눈빛으로 말을 이었다."], ["“Do I speak and look as if I really had?", "\"내가 정말로 그렇게 한 듯이 말하고, 또 그렇게 보이지 않나요?"], ["Do you believe what I am saying?”", "내가 하는 말을 믿어요?\""]], [["“I fear I must.", "\"믿을 수밖에 없을 것 같아요."], ["Let my arm go!”", "내 팔을 놓아 줘요!\""]], [["“Not yet.", "\"아직요."], ["A moment more.", "한순간만 더요."], ["You can think what my revengeful purpose must have been, to last so long, and urge me to do this?”", "이토록 오래 이어져, 나를 이 일을 하도록 내몬 내 복수심이 얼마나 컸을지 짐작이 갈 거예요.\""]], [["“Dreadful!”", "\"끔찍해요!\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."]], [["“Then when you see me now,”", "\"그러니 지금 당신이 나를 본다면,\""], ["said Alice hoarsely, “here again, kneeling quietly on the ground, with my touch upon your arm, with my eyes upon your face, you may believe that there is no common earnestness in what I say, and that no common struggle has been battling in my breast.", "앨리스가 쉰 목소리로 말했다, \"이렇게 다시, 땅에 조용히 무릎 꿇고, 내 손은 당신 팔에 얹고, 내 눈은 당신 얼굴에 두고 있는 나를 본다면, 내가 하는 이 말에 예사롭지 않은 간절함이 담겨 있다는 걸, 또 내 가슴속에서 예사롭지 않은 싸움이 벌어졌다는 걸 믿어 줄 거예요."], ["I am ashamed to speak the words, but I relent.", "이런 말을 입에 올리기가 부끄럽지만, 나는 마음이 누그러졌어요. 나 자신이 경멸스러워요."], ["I despise myself; I have fought with myself all day, and all last night; but I relent towards him without reason, and wish to repair what I have done, if it is possible.", "온종일, 그리고 어젯밤 내내 나 자신과 싸웠어요. 하지만 까닭도 없이 그자에게 마음이 누그러져서, 할 수만 있다면 내가 한 일을 돌이키고 싶어요."], ["I wouldn’t have them come together while his pursuer is so blind and headlong.", "그자를 쫓는 사람이 저토록 눈멀고 앞뒤 없는 동안에는 그 둘이 만나지 않게 하고 싶어요."], ["If you had seen him as he went out last night, you would know the danger better.”", "어젯밤 그가 나서는 걸 당신이 봤다면, 그 위험을 더 잘 알 거예요.\""]], [["“How can it be prevented?", "\"어떻게 막을 수 있어요?"], ["What can I do?”", "내가 뭘 할 수 있죠?\""], ["cried Harriet.", "해리엇이 외쳤다."]], [["“All night long,”", "\"밤새도록,\""], ["pursued the other, hurriedly, “I had dreams of him—and yet I didn’t sleep—in his blood.", "상대가 다급히 말을 이었다, \"나는 그자의 꿈을 꿨어요—그러면서도 잠은 자지 않았지만요—피투성이가 된 그자의 꿈을요."], ["All day, I have had him near me.”", "온종일 그자가 내 곁에 어른거렸어요.\""]], [["“What can I do?”", "\"내가 뭘 할 수 있죠?\""], ["cried Harriet, shuddering at these words.", "해리엇이 그 말에 몸서리치며 외쳤다."]], [["“If there is anyone who’ll write, or send, or go to him, let them lose no time.", "\"누구든 그자에게 편지를 쓰든, 사람을 보내든, 직접 가든 할 사람이 있다면, 한시도 지체하지 말게 하세요."], ["He is at Dijon.", "그는 디종에 있어요."], ["Do you know the name, and where it is?”", "그 지명을 알아요, 그게 어디 있는지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"알아요.\""]], [["“Warn him that the man he has made his enemy is in a frenzy, and that he doesn’t know him if he makes light of his approach.", "\"그가 원수로 만든 그 사내가 광란에 빠져 있다고, 만약 그자가 그 사내의 접근을 우습게 안다면 그 사내를 모르는 거라고 경고하세요."], ["Tell him that he is on the road—I know he is!—and hurrying on.", "그가 길에 올랐다고—분명히 그래요!—서둘러 다가오고 있다고 말하세요."], ["Urge him to get away while there is time—if there is time—and not to meet him yet.", "시간이 있는 동안—시간이 있다면요—어서 달아나라고, 아직은 그를 만나지 말라고 다그치세요."], ["A month or so will make years of difference.", "한 달쯤이면 몇 년만큼의 차이를 만들어 낼 거예요."], ["Let them not encounter, through me.", "나 때문에 그 둘이 마주치지 않게 하세요."], ["Anywhere but there!", "그곳만 아니면 어디든요!"], ["Any time but now!", "지금만 아니면 언제든요!"], ["Let his foe follow him, and find him for himself, but not through me!", "그자의 원수가 그자를 쫓아, 제 힘으로 그자를 찾아내게 하세요, 나를 통해서가 아니라요!"], ["There is enough upon my head without.”", "그러지 않아도 이미 내 머리에는 충분히 짊어진 게 있으니까요.\""]], [["The fire ceased to be reflected in her jet black hair, uplifted face, and eager eyes; her hand was gone from Harriet’s arm; and the place where she had been was empty.", "난롯불은 더 이상 그녀의 새까만 머리에도, 치켜든 얼굴에도, 간절한 눈에도 비치지 않았다. 그녀의 손은 해리엇의 팔에서 사라졌고, 그녀가 있던 자리는 텅 비어 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LIV. The Fugitives", "# CHAPTER LIV. The Fugitives"]], [["Tea-time, an hour short of midnight; the place, a French apartment, comprising some half-dozen rooms;—a dull cold hall or corridor, a dining-room, a drawing-room, a bed-room, and an inner drawingroom, or boudoir, smaller and more retired than the rest.", "차 마실 시간, 자정을 한 시간 앞둔 무렵. 장소는 프랑스식 아파트로, 대여섯 개쯤의 방으로 이루어져 있었다—음침하고 차가운 현관 혹은 복도, 식당, 응접실, 침실, 그리고 안쪽 응접실, 즉 부두아르가 있었는데, 이 부두아르는 다른 방들보다 작고 더 외진 곳이었다."], ["All these shut in by one large pair of doors on the main staircase, but each room provided with two or three pairs of doors of its own, establishing several means of communication with the remaining portion of the apartment, or with certain small passages within the wall, leading, as is not unusual in such houses, to some back stairs with an obscure outlet below.", "이 모든 방은 본계단 쪽으로 난 큰 문 한 쌍으로 막혀 있었으나, 각 방마다 두세 쌍씩 제 나름의 문을 갖추고 있어서, 아파트의 나머지 부분과, 혹은 벽 안쪽의 어떤 좁은 통로와 통하는 여러 갈래의 길을 만들고 있었다. 그 통로는 이런 집들에서 드물지 않은 일이지만, 아래쪽에 잘 드러나지 않는 출구가 있는 어떤 뒷계단으로 이어져 있었다."], ["The whole situated on the first floor of so large an Hotel, that it did not absorb one entire row of windows upon one side of the square court-yard in the centre, upon which the whole four sides of the mansion looked.", "이 모든 것이 어찌나 큰 호텔의 이층에 자리 잡고 있었던지, 한가운데의 네모진 안뜰 한 면에 늘어선 창문 한 줄을 다 차지하지도 못할 정도였는데, 그 안뜰은 저택의 네 면 전부가 굽어보고 있었다."]], [["An air of splendour, sufficiently faded to be melancholy, and sufficiently dazzling to clog and embarrass the details of life with a show of state, reigned in these rooms.", "이 방들에는, 우울하게 느껴질 만큼 충분히 빛이 바랜, 그러면서도 위세를 부리는 겉치레로 살림살이의 세세한 부분들을 막아 세우고 거추장스럽게 할 만큼 충분히 눈부신, 어떤 화려한 기운이 감돌고 있었다."], ["The walls and ceilings were gilded and painted; the floors were waxed and polished; crimson drapery hung in festoons from window, door, and mirror; and candelabra, gnarled and intertwisted like the branches of trees, or horns of animals, stuck out from the panels of the wall.", "벽과 천장에는 금박과 채색이 입혀져 있었고, 마룻바닥은 밀랍을 먹여 윤이 났으며, 진홍색 휘장이 창과 문과 거울에서 꽃줄 모양으로 늘어져 있었다. 또 나뭇가지나 짐승의 뿔처럼 옹이지고 서로 뒤얽힌 촛대가 벽 판자에서 튀어나와 있었다."], ["But in the day-time, when the lattice-blinds (now closely shut) were opened, and the light let in, traces were discernible among this finery, of wear and tear and dust, of sun and damp and smoke, and lengthened intervals of want of use and habitation, when such shows and toys of life seem sensitive like life, and waste as men shut up in prison do.", "그러나 낮 동안, (지금은 꼭 닫혀 있는) 격자 덧창을 열어 빛을 들이면, 이 호화로운 장식들 사이로 닳고 해진 자국, 먼지 자국, 햇볕과 습기와 연기의 흔적, 그리고 오랫동안 쓰이지도 거주되지도 않고 방치된 흔적이 눈에 띄었다. 그럴 때면 삶의 그런 구경거리와 장난감들도 마치 생명을 지닌 것처럼 예민해 보였고, 감옥에 갇힌 사람들이 그러하듯 시들어 가는 듯했다."], ["Even night, and clusters of burning candles, could not wholly efface them, though the general glitter threw them in the shade.", "밤이 와서 활활 타오르는 촛불 무리가 켜져도, 비록 전반적인 광채가 그것들을 그늘 속에 밀어 넣기는 했으나, 그 흔적을 온전히 지워 버리지는 못했다."]], [["The glitter of bright tapers, and their reflection in looking-glasses, scraps of gilding and gay colours, were confined, on this night, to one room—that smaller room within the rest, just now enumerated.", "이날 밤, 밝은 촛불의 광채와 거울에 비친 그 반사, 금박 조각들과 화려한 색채는 한 방에만—방금 헤아린 방들 가운데 그 안쪽의 더 작은 방에만 한정되어 있었다."], ["Seen from the hall, where a lamp was feebly burning, through the dark perspective of open doors, it looked as shining and precious as a gem.", "등불 하나가 희미하게 타고 있는 현관에서, 열린 문들의 어두운 원근을 통해 바라보면, 그 방은 보석처럼 빛나고 값져 보였다."], ["In the heart of its radiance sat a beautiful woman—Edith.", "그 광휘의 한가운데에 한 아름다운 여인이 앉아 있었다—이디스였다."]], [["She was alone.", "그녀는 혼자였다."], ["The same defiant, scornful woman still.", "여전히 똑같이 반항적이고 경멸에 찬 여인이었다."], ["The cheek a little worn, the eye a little larger in appearance, and more lustrous, but the haughty bearing just the same.", "뺨은 조금 야위었고, 눈은 보기에 조금 더 커지고 더 윤기가 돌았으나, 그 오만한 자태는 조금도 다름이 없었다."], ["No shame upon her brow; no late repentance bending her disdainful neck.", "그녀의 이마에는 수치의 그늘이 없었고, 뒤늦은 뉘우침이 그 도도한 목을 굽히지도 않았다."], ["Imperious and stately yet, and yet regardless of herself and of all else, she sat with her dark eyes cast down, waiting for someone.", "여전히 위풍당당하고 위엄에 차 있으면서도, 자기 자신과 그 밖의 모든 것에는 아랑곳하지 않은 채, 그녀는 검은 눈을 내리깔고 앉아 누군가를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["No book, no work, no occupation of any kind but her own thought, beguiled the tardy time.", "책도, 바느질거리도, 그 어떤 소일거리도 없이, 오로지 자기 생각만이 더디게 흐르는 시간을 달래 주었다."], ["Some purpose, strong enough to fill up any pause, possessed her.", "어떤 목적이, 어떤 빈틈이라도 채울 만큼 강력한 목적이 그녀를 사로잡고 있었다."], ["With her lips pressed together, and quivering if for a moment she released them from her control; with her nostril inflated; her hands clasped in one another; and her purpose swelling in her breast; she sat, and waited.", "입술을 꼭 다문 채로—잠시라도 그것을 통제에서 풀어 놓으면 그 입술은 파르르 떨렸다—콧구멍을 벌름거리며, 두 손을 맞잡은 채, 가슴속에서 부풀어 오르는 그 목적과 함께, 그녀는 앉아서 기다렸다."]], [["At the sound of a key in the outer door, and a footstep in the hall, she started up, and cried “Who’s that?”", "바깥문에서 열쇠 소리가 나고 현관에서 발소리가 들리자, 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 \"거기 누구냐?\"라고 외쳤다."], ["The answer was in French, and two men came in with jingling trays, to make preparation for supper.", "대답은 프랑스어였고, 두 남자가 짤랑거리는 쟁반을 들고 들어와 저녁 식사 준비를 했다."]], [["“Who had bade them to do so?”", "\"누가 그렇게 하라고 시켰지?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Monsieur had commanded it, when it was his pleasure to take the apartment.", "\"무슈께서 그렇게 분부하셨습니다, 이 아파트를 빌리시기로 한 그때에 말입니다."], ["Monsieur had said, when he stayed there for an hour, en route, and left the letter for Madame—Madame had received it surely?”", "무슈께서는, 도중에 한 시간 머무르시며 마담께 편지를 남기실 때 그렇게 말씀하셨습니다—마담께서는 그 편지를 분명 받으셨겠지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"받았네.\""]], [["“A thousand pardons!", "\"천 번 용서를 빕니다!"], ["The sudden apprehension that it might have been forgotten had struck him;” a bald man, with a large beard from a neighbouring restaurant; “with despair!", "혹시 그것이 잊혔을지도 모른다는 갑작스러운 걱정이 그를 덮쳤다는 것이었다\"—인근 식당에서 온, 큼직한 수염을 기른 대머리 사내였다—\"절망과 더불어 말입니다!"], ["Monsieur had said that supper was to be ready at that hour: also that he had forewarned Madame of the commands he had given, in his letter.", "무슈께서는 저녁 식사가 그 시각에 준비되어 있어야 한다고 말씀하셨습니다. 또한 편지에 적어 내리신 분부에 대해 마담께 미리 알려 두셨다고도 하셨습니다."], ["Monsieur had done the Golden Head the honour to request that the supper should be choice and delicate.", "무슈께서는 황금머리 식당에 영광을 베푸시어 저녁 식사를 정선되고 섬세하게 차려 달라 청하셨습니다."], ["Monsieur would find that his confidence in the Golden Head was not misplaced.”", "무슈께서는 황금머리 식당에 두신 그 신뢰가 잘못되지 않았음을 아시게 될 것입니다.\""]], [["Edith said no more, but looked on thoughtfully while they prepared the table for two persons, and set the wine upon it.", "이디스는 더는 말이 없었으나, 그들이 두 사람 몫으로 식탁을 차리고 그 위에 포도주를 놓는 동안 생각에 잠겨 바라보았다."], ["She arose before they had finished, and taking a lamp, passed into the bed-chamber and into the drawing-room, where she hurriedly but narrowly examined all the doors; particularly one in the former room that opened on the passage in the wall.", "그녀는 그들이 일을 끝내기도 전에 일어나, 등불을 하나 들고 침실로, 또 응접실로 들어가, 서두르면서도 면밀하게 모든 문을 살펴보았다. 특히 앞쪽 방에서 벽 안의 통로로 통하는 문 하나를 유심히 보았다."], ["From this she took the key, and put it on the outer side.", "그녀는 거기서 열쇠를 빼내어 바깥쪽에 꽂아 두었다."], ["She then came back.", "그러고는 다시 돌아왔다."]], [["The men—the second of whom was a dark, bilious subject, in a jacket, close shaved, and with a black head of hair close cropped—had completed their preparation of the table, and were standing looking at it.", "그 사내들은—둘 가운데 두 번째는 거무튀튀하고 신경질적인 얼굴에, 재킷을 걸치고, 말끔히 면도를 했으며, 새카만 머리를 바짝 깎은 자였다—식탁 준비를 끝내고 서서 그것을 바라보고 있었다."], ["He who had spoken before, inquired whether Madame thought it would be long before Monsieur arrived?", "앞서 말을 했던 자가, 마담께서는 무슈가 도착하시기까지 오래 걸리리라 보시느냐고 물었다."]], [["“She couldn’t say.", "\"모르겠네."], ["It was all one.”", "다 마찬가지야.\""]], [["“Pardon! There was the supper!", "\"용서를 빕니다!"], ["It should be eaten on the instant.", "저녁 식사가 있지 않습니까! 당장 드셔야 합니다."], ["Monsieur (who spoke French like an Angel—or a Frenchman—it was all the same) had spoken with great emphasis of his punctuality.", "무슈께서는(천사처럼—아니 프랑스 사람처럼—그게 그거지만, 아무튼 프랑스어를 하시는 분이) 당신의 시간 엄수에 대해 대단히 힘주어 말씀하셨습니다."], ["But the English nation had so grand a genius for punctuality.", "그런데 영국 민족은 시간 엄수에 그토록 대단한 재능이 있으니 말입니다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["what noise!", "무슨 소리지!"], ["Great Heaven, here was Monsieur.", "맙소사, 여기 무슈가 오셨습니다."], ["Behold him!”", "보십시오!\""]], [["In effect, Monsieur, admitted by the other of the two, came, with his gleaming teeth, through the dark rooms, like a mouth; and arriving in that sanctuary of light and colour, a figure at full length, embraced Madame, and addressed her in the French tongue as his charming wife.", "과연, 두 사내 가운데 다른 자가 들여보낸 무슈가, 번득이는 이를 드러내며 어두운 방들을 지나 마치 입처럼 다가왔다. 그러고는 그 빛과 색채의 성소에 다다라, 온몸을 곧추세운 채, 마담을 끌어안고 프랑스어로 그녀를 자기의 사랑스러운 아내라 불렀다."]], [["“My God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["Madame is going to faint.", "마담께서 기절하시려나 봅니다."], ["Madame is overcome with joy!”", "마담께서 기쁨에 겨워 어쩔 줄 모르시는군요!\""], ["The bald man with the beard observed it, and cried out.", "수염을 기른 대머리 사내가 그것을 알아채고 소리쳤다."]], [["Madame had only shrunk and shivered.", "마담은 다만 움츠리며 부르르 떨었을 뿐이다."], ["Before the words were spoken, she was standing with her hand upon the velvet back of a great chair; her figure drawn up to its full height, and her face immoveable.", "그 말이 채 끝나기도 전에, 그녀는 커다란 의자의 벨벳 등받이에 한 손을 얹고 서 있었다. 몸은 한껏 곧추세우고, 얼굴은 미동도 없었다."]], [["“Francois has flown over to the Golden Head for supper.", "\"프랑수아가 저녁 식사를 가지러 황금머리 식당으로 날아갔습니다."], ["He flies on these occasions like an angel or a bird.", "이런 때 그는 천사나 새처럼 날아다니지요."], ["The baggage of Monsieur is in his room.", "무슈의 짐은 그분 방에 있습니다."], ["All is arranged.", "모든 게 마련되었습니다."], ["The supper will be here this moment.”", "저녁 식사는 곧 도착할 것입니다.\""], ["These facts the bald man notified with bows and smiles, and presently the supper came.", "대머리 사내가 절을 하고 미소를 지으며 이런 사실들을 알렸고, 이윽고 저녁 식사가 도착했다."]], [["The hot dishes were on a chafing-dish; the cold already set forth, with the change of service on a sideboard.", "따뜻한 요리들은 보온 그릇에 담겨 있었고, 차가운 요리들은 이미 차려져 있었으며, 그릇을 바꿔 놓을 여분은 찬장 위에 마련되어 있었다."], ["Monsieur was satisfied with this arrangement.", "무슈는 이 배치에 만족했다."], ["The supper table being small, it pleased him very well.", "식탁이 작은 것이 그의 마음에 썩 들었다."], ["Let them set the chafing-dish upon the floor, and go.", "보온 그릇을 바닥에 내려놓고 물러가라고 했다."], ["He would remove the dishes with his own hands.", "접시는 자기 손으로 치우겠다는 것이었다."]], [["“Pardon!”", "\"용서를 빕니다!\""], ["said the bald man, politely.", "대머리 사내가 정중하게 말했다."], ["“It was impossible!”", "\"그건 안 될 말씀입니다!\""]], [["Monsieur was of another opinion.", "무슈의 생각은 달랐다."], ["He required no further attendance that night.", "그날 밤 더는 시중이 필요 없다고 했다."]], [["“But Madame—”", "\"하오나 마담께서는—\""], ["the bald man hinted.", "대머리 사내가 넌지시 운을 뗐다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"마담께는,\""], ["replied Monsieur, “had her own maid.", "무슈가 응수했다, \"당신의 하녀가 있네."], ["It was enough.”", "그것으로 충분해.\""]], [["“A million pardons!", "\"백만 번 용서를 빕니다! 아닙니다!"], ["No! Madame had no maid!”", "마담께는 하녀가 없으십니다!\""]], [["“I came here alone,”", "\"나는 혼자 여기 왔다네,\""], ["said Edith “It was my choice to do so.", "이디스가 말했다. \"내가 그렇게 하기로 한 거지."], ["I am well used to travelling; I want no attendance.", "나는 여행에 아주 익숙해. 시중은 필요 없네."], ["They need send nobody to me.", "내게 누구도 보낼 필요 없어.\""]], [["Monsieur accordingly, persevering in his first proposed impossibility, proceeded to follow the two attendants to the outer door, and secure it after them for the night.", "그리하여 무슈는, 처음에 안 된다고 했던 그 일을 끝내 고집하여, 두 시중꾼을 바깥문까지 뒤따라가서는, 그들이 나간 뒤 밤을 대비해 문을 단단히 잠갔다."], ["The bald man turning round to bow, as he went out, observed that Madame still stood with her hand upon the velvet back of the great chair, and that her face was quite regardless of him, though she was looking straight before her.", "대머리 사내는 나가면서 절을 하려고 돌아서다가, 마담이 여전히 커다란 의자의 벨벳 등받이에 한 손을 얹고 서 있는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 똑바로 앞만 바라보고 있을 뿐 자기에게는 전혀 아랑곳하지 않는다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["As the sound of Carker’s fastening the door resounded through the intermediate rooms, and seemed to come hushed and stilled into that last distant one, the sound of the Cathedral clock striking twelve mingled with it, in Edith’s ears.", "카커가 문을 잠그는 소리가 중간의 방들을 통해 울려 퍼지며, 그 가장 멀고 끝에 있는 방까지 죽은 듯 잦아들어 다다랐을 때, 그 소리에 대성당 시계가 열두 시를 치는 소리가 이디스의 귓가에 섞여 들었다."], ["She heard him pause, as if he heard it too and listened; and then came back towards her, laying a long train of footsteps through the silence, and shutting all the doors behind him as he came along.", "그녀는 그가, 마치 자기도 그 소리를 듣고 귀 기울이는 듯, 잠시 멈춰 서는 것을 들었다. 그러더니 그가 정적을 가로질러 긴 발소리의 행렬을 늘어놓으며, 지나오는 동안 등 뒤로 모든 문을 닫으면서 그녀에게로 돌아왔다."], ["Her hand, for a moment, left the velvet chair to bring a knife within her reach upon the table; then she stood as she had stood before.", "그녀의 손은 잠시 벨벳 의자를 떠나, 식탁 위에 놓인 칼을 손 닿는 데로 가져왔다. 그러고는 전과 똑같이 서 있었다."]], [["“How strange to come here by yourself, my love!”", "\"혼자서 여기 오다니 참 이상도 하지, 내 사랑!\""], ["he said as he entered.", "그가 들어서며 말했다."]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고?\""], ["she returned.", "그녀가 응수했다."]], [["Her tone was so harsh; the quick turn of her head so fierce; her attitude so repellent; and her frown so black; that he stood, with the lamp in his hand, looking at her, as if she had struck him motionless.", "그녀의 어조가 어찌나 거칠었던지, 고개를 홱 돌리는 것이 어찌나 사납고, 자태가 어찌나 거부에 차 있고, 찌푸린 이맛살이 어찌나 새카맸던지, 그는 등불을 손에 든 채 멈춰 서서, 마치 그녀가 자기를 꼼짝 못 하게 후려치기라도 한 듯 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“I say,”", "\"그러니까 내 말은,\""], ["he at length repeated, putting down the lamp, and smiling his most courtly smile, “how strange to come here alone!", "그가 마침내 등불을 내려놓으며, 더없이 정중한 미소를 지으며 되풀이했다, \"혼자서 여기 오다니 참 이상하다는 거요!"], ["It was unnecessary caution surely, and might have defeated itself.", "분명 쓸데없는 조심이었고, 자칫 일을 그르칠 뻔도 했소."], ["You were to have engaged an attendant at Havre or Rouen, and have had abundance of time for the purpose, though you had been the most capricious and difficult (as you are the most beautiful, my love) of women.”", "르아브르나 루앙에서 시중들 사람을 구하기로 되어 있었고, 또 (내 사랑, 당신이 여자들 가운데 가장 아름답듯이) 가장 변덕스럽고 까다로운 여자였다 한들, 그럴 시간은 넉넉했을 텐데.\""]], [["Her eyes gleamed strangely on him, but she stood with her hand resting on the chair, and said not a word.", "그녀의 눈이 그를 향해 이상하게 번득였으나, 그녀는 손을 의자에 얹은 채 서서 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["“I have never,”", "\"나는 한 번도,\""], ["resumed Carker, “seen you look so handsome, as you do tonight.", "카커가 다시 입을 열었다, \"오늘 밤만큼 당신이 아름다워 보이는 걸 본 적이 없소."], ["Even the picture I have carried in my mind during this cruel probation, and which I have contemplated night and day, is exceeded by the reality.”", "이 가혹한 시련의 시간 동안 내가 마음속에 품고 다녔던, 밤낮으로 응시했던 그 그림조차도 실물에는 미치지 못하는구려.\""]], [["Not a word.", "한마디도 없었다."], ["Not a look Her eyes completely hidden by their drooping lashes, but her head held up.", "눈길조차 없었다. 그녀의 눈은 내리깐 속눈썹에 완전히 가려져 있었으나, 고개는 곧추세우고 있었다."]], [["“Hard, unrelenting terms they were!”", "\"가혹하고 매정한 조건이었소!\""], ["said Carker, with a smile, “but they are all fulfilled and passed, and make the present more delicious and more safe.", "카커가 미소 지으며 말했다, \"하지만 그것도 다 채워지고 지나갔으니, 지금 이 순간을 더욱 달콤하고 더욱 안전하게 만들어 주는구려."], ["Sicily shall be the place of our retreat.", "시칠리아가 우리의 은신처가 될 거요."], ["In the idlest and easiest part of the world, my soul, we’ll both seek compensation for old slavery.”", "세상에서 가장 한가롭고 편한 그곳에서, 내 영혼이여, 우리 둘 다 옛 노예살이를 보상받읍시다.\""]], [["He was coming gaily towards her, when, in an instant, she caught the knife up from the table, and started one pace back.", "그가 즐거이 그녀에게로 다가가던 순간, 그녀가 별안간 식탁에서 칼을 낚아채고 한 발 뒤로 물러섰다."]], [["“Stand still!”", "\"가만히 있어!\""], ["she said, “or I shall murder you!”", "그녀가 말했다. \"안 그러면 당신을 죽여 버리겠어!\""]], [["The sudden change in her, the towering fury and intense abhorrence sparkling in her eyes and lighting up her brow, made him stop as if a fire had stopped him.", "그녀의 그 갑작스러운 변화, 그 눈에서 번득이고 그 이마를 환히 밝히는 치솟는 분노와 격렬한 혐오가, 마치 불길이 그를 막아 세우기라도 한 듯 그를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Stand still!”", "\"가만히 있어!\""], ["she said, “come no nearer me, upon your life!”", "그녀가 말했다. \"목숨이 아깝거든 한 발짝도 다가오지 마!\""]], [["They both stood looking at each other.", "둘은 서로를 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["Rage and astonishment were in his face, but he controlled them, and said lightly,", "그의 얼굴에는 분노와 경악이 어려 있었으나, 그는 그것을 억누르고 가볍게 말했다."]], [["“Come, come!", "\"이런, 이런!"], ["Tush, we are alone, and out of everybody’s sight and hearing.", "쳇, 우리뿐인데, 아무도 보지도 듣지도 못하는데."], ["Do you think to frighten me with these tricks of virtue?”", "이런 정숙한 척하는 수작으로 나를 겁줄 수 있을 거라 생각하오?\""]], [["“Do you think to frighten me,”", "\"이 외진 곳을, 가까이에 도움 청할 데가 없다는 걸 내게 상기시켜서,\""], ["she answered fiercely, “from any purpose that I have, and any course I am resolved upon, by reminding me of the solitude of this place, and there being no help near?", "그녀가 사납게 대꾸했다, \"내가 마음먹은 어떤 목적, 결심한 어떤 길에서 나를 겁주어 물러나게 할 수 있을 거라 생각해?"], ["Me, who am here alone, designedly?", "일부러 여기 혼자 와 있는 이 나를?"], ["If I feared you, should I not have avoided you?", "내가 당신을 두려워했다면 당신을 피하지 않았겠어?"], ["If I feared you, should I be here, in the dead of night, telling you to your face what I am going to tell?”", "당신을 두려워했다면, 이 깊은 밤에 여기 와서, 이제 말하려는 것을 당신 면전에 대고 말하려 하겠어?\""]], [["“And what is that,”", "\"그래서 그게 뭔데,\""], ["he said, “you handsome shrew?", "그가 말했다, \"이 아름다운 표독스러운 여인아?"], ["Handsomer so, than any other woman in her best humour?”", "다른 어떤 여자가 제일 기분 좋을 때보다도 이러는 게 더 아름답군그래?\""]], [["“I tell you nothing,”", "\"아무것도 말하지 않겠어,\""], ["she returned, until you go back to that chair—except this, once again—Don’t come near me!", "그녀가 응수했다, \"당신이 저 의자로 돌아갈 때까지는—다만 이것 한 번만 다시 말하지—내게 다가오지 마!"], ["Not a step nearer.", "한 발짝도 더 가까이 오지 마."], ["I tell you, if you do, as Heaven sees us, I shall murder you!”", "분명히 말하는데, 만약 그러면, 하늘이 우리를 굽어보는 가운데, 당신을 죽여 버리겠어!\""]], [["“Do you mistake me for your husband?”", "\"나를 당신 남편으로 착각한 거요?\""], ["he retorted, with a grin.", "그가 이를 드러내며 응수했다."]], [["Disdaining to reply, she stretched her arm out, pointing to the chair.", "대꾸할 가치도 없다는 듯, 그녀는 팔을 뻗어 의자를 가리켰다."], ["He bit his lip, frowned, laughed, and sat down in it, with a baffled, irresolute, impatient air, he was unable to conceal; and biting his nail nervously, and looking at her sideways, with bitter discomfiture, even while he feigned to be amused by her caprice.", "그는 입술을 깨물고, 이맛살을 찌푸리고, 웃더니, 좌절하고 우유부단하며 안달하는 기색—감추지 못하는 그 기색을 띤 채 거기 앉았다. 그러고는 신경질적으로 손톱을 깨물며, 그녀의 변덕을 재미있어하는 척하면서도, 쓰라린 낭패감을 안고 그녀를 곁눈질로 바라보았다."]], [["She put the knife down upon the table, and touching her bosom with her hand, said:", "그녀는 칼을 식탁 위에 내려놓고, 한 손으로 가슴께를 짚으며 말했다."]], [["“I have something lying here that is no love trinket, and sooner than endure your touch once more, I would use it on you—and you know it, while I speak—with less reluctance than I would on any other creeping thing that lives.”", "\"여기 사랑의 노리개 따위가 아닌 무언가가 들어 있어. 다시 한번 당신 손이 닿느니, 차라리 이걸 당신한테 쓰겠어—그리고 당신도 알지, 내가 말하는 이 순간에—이 세상에 사는 그 어떤 기어다니는 것에게보다도 덜 망설이며 말이야.\""]], [["He affected to laugh jestingly, and entreated her to act her play out quickly, for the supper was growing cold.", "그는 농담조로 웃는 시늉을 하며, 저녁 식사가 식어 가니 어서 연극을 끝내라고 그녀에게 청했다."], ["But the secret look with which he regarded her, was more sullen and lowering, and he struck his foot once upon the floor with a muttered oath.", "그러나 그녀를 바라보는 그 은밀한 눈길은 한층 더 음침하고 음울해졌고, 그는 한 번 발을 바닥에 구르며 욕설을 중얼거렸다."]], [["“How many times,”", "\"몇 번이나,\""], ["said Edith, bending her darkest glance upon him, “has your bold knavery assailed me with outrage and insult?", "이디스가 그에게 가장 어두운 눈길을 던지며 말했다, \"당신의 뻔뻔한 악행이 모욕과 능멸로 나를 덮쳤지?"], ["How many times in your smooth manner, and mocking words and looks, have I been twitted with my courtship and my marriage?", "그 매끄러운 태도로, 그 비웃는 말과 눈길로, 몇 번이나 나는 내 구애와 내 결혼을 두고 빈정거림을 당했지?"], ["How many times have you laid bare my wound of love for that sweet, injured girl and lacerated it?", "몇 번이나 당신은 그 사랑스럽고 상처 입은 아이를 향한 내 사랑의 상처를 헤집어 갈가리 찢어 놓았지?"], ["How often have you fanned the fire on which, for two years, I have writhed; and tempted me to take a desperate revenge, when it has most tortured me?”", "이 년 동안 내가 그 위에서 몸부림쳐 온 그 불길을, 그것이 나를 가장 고통스럽게 할 때, 몇 번이나 당신은 부채질하며, 절박한 복수를 하라고 나를 부추겼지?\""]], [["“I have no doubt, Ma’am,”", "\"틀림없이, 부인,\""], ["he replied, “that you have kept a good account, and that it’s pretty accurate.", "그가 응수했다, \"당신은 셈을 잘 적어 두었을 테고, 그것도 꽤 정확하겠지."], ["Come, Edith.", "자, 이디스."], ["To your husband, poor wretch, this was well enough—”", "당신의 가엾은 남편에게라면, 이 정도면 충분히 괜찮았겠지만—\""]], [["“Why, if,”", "\"흥, 만약,\""], ["she said, surveying him with a haughty contempt and disgust, that he shrunk under, let him brave it as he would, “if all my other reasons for despising him could have been blown away like feathers, his having you for his counsellor and favourite, would have almost been enough to hold their place.”", "그녀가, 그가 아무리 태연한 척해도 그 아래에서 움츠러들 만한, 오만한 경멸과 혐오의 눈길로 그를 훑어보며 말했다, \"그를 경멸할 다른 모든 이유가 깃털처럼 다 날아가 버린대도, 당신을 자기 고문이자 총신으로 삼았다는 사실 하나만으로도, 그 이유들의 자리를 거의 채우고도 남았을 거야.\""]], [["“Is that a reason why you have run away with me?”", "\"그래서 그게 당신이 나와 함께 달아난 이유요?\""], ["he asked her, tauntingly.", "그가 비아냥거리며 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes, and why we are face to face for the last time.", "\"그래, 그리고 우리가 마지막으로 얼굴을 맞대고 있는 이유지."], ["Wretch!", "비열한 인간!"], ["We meet tonight, and part tonight.", "우리는 오늘 밤 만났고, 오늘 밤 헤어져."], ["For not one moment after I have ceased to speak, will I stay here!”", "내가 말을 마친 그 순간부터 단 한 순간도 나는 여기 머무르지 않을 테니까!\""]], [["He turned upon her with his ugliest look, and gripped the table with his hand; but neither rose, nor otherwise answered or threatened her.", "그는 가장 흉악한 표정으로 그녀를 돌아보며, 손으로 식탁을 움켜쥐었다. 그러나 일어서지도, 달리 대꾸하거나 위협하지도 않았다."]], [["“I am a woman,”", "\"나는,\""], ["she said, confronting him steadfastly, “who from her childhood has been shamed and steeled.", "그녀가 그를 굳건히 마주하며 말했다, \"어릴 적부터 수치를 당하고 무감각해지도록 단련된 여자야."], ["I have been offered and rejected, put up and appraised, until my very soul has sickened.", "내 영혼이 진저리를 칠 때까지 나는 내놓아지고 퇴짜 맞고, 진열되고 값 매겨졌어."], ["I have not had an accomplishment or grace that might have been a resource to me, but it has been paraded and vended to enhance my value, as if the common crier had called it through the streets.", "내게 밑천이 될 만한 재주나 기품이 하나라도 있으면, 그것은 마치 동네 외치는 사람이 거리거리에서 떠벌리듯, 내 몸값을 올리려고 죄다 자랑되고 팔려 나갔지."], ["My poor, proud friends, have looked on and approved; and every tie between us has been deadened in my breast.", "나의 가엾고 오만한 가족들은 그것을 지켜보며 흡족해했고, 우리 사이의 모든 정은 내 가슴속에서 죽어 갔어."], ["There is not one of them for whom I care, as I could care for a pet dog.", "그들 가운데 애완견에게 쏟을 만큼이라도 내가 마음 쓰는 이는 하나도 없어."], ["I stand alone in the world, remembering well what a hollow world it has been to me, and what a hollow part of it I have been myself.", "나는 이 세상에 홀로 서 있어, 이 세상이 내게 얼마나 공허한 것이었는지, 그리고 나 자신이 그 안에서 얼마나 공허한 한 부분이었는지 똑똑히 기억하면서."], ["You know this, and you know that my fame with it is worthless to me.”", "당신은 이걸 알지, 그리고 그래서 내 평판 따위가 내게는 아무 가치도 없다는 것도 알지.\""]], [["“Yes; I imagined that,”", "\"그래, 나도 그럴 거라 짐작했소,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“And calculated on it,”", "\"그리고 그걸 계산에 넣었지,\""], ["she rejoined, “and so pursued me.", "그녀가 되받았다, \"그래서 나를 쫓아다닌 거고."], ["Grown too indifferent for any opposition but indifference, to the daily working of the hands that had moulded me to this; and knowing that my marriage would at least prevent their hawking of me up and down; I suffered myself to be sold, as infamously as any woman with a halter round her neck is sold in any market-place.", "나를 이 꼴로 빚어낸 그 손들이 날마다 부리는 짓에 대해, 무관심 말고는 어떤 저항도 하지 못할 만큼 무덤덤해져서, 또 내 결혼이 적어도 그들이 나를 이리저리 내다 파는 짓만은 막아 주리라는 것을 알았기에, 나는 목에 올가미를 두른 어떤 여자가 어느 장터에서 팔려 나가듯, 그처럼 치욕스럽게 내가 팔려 가도록 내버려 두었지."], ["You know that.”", "당신도 알잖아.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["he said, showing all his teeth “I know that.”", "그가 이를 죄다 드러내며 말했다, \"나도 알지.\""]], [["“And calculated on it,”", "\"그리고 그걸 계산에 넣었지,\""], ["she rejoined once more, “and so pursued me.", "그녀가 다시 한번 되받았다, \"그래서 나를 쫓아다닌 거고."], ["From my marriage day, I found myself exposed to such new shame—to such solicitation and pursuit (expressed as clearly as if it had been written in the coarsest words, and thrust into my hand at every turn) from one mean villain, that I felt as if I had never known humiliation till that time.", "결혼한 그날부터, 나는 한 비열한 악당으로부터 그처럼 새로운 수치에—그처럼 끈질긴 유혹과 추근거림에(마치 그것이 가장 천박한 말로 쓰여 길목마다 내 손에 떠밀어지기라도 한 듯 또렷이 드러난 그 추근거림에) 내몰린 나 자신을 발견했어. 그래서 나는 그때까지 진정한 굴욕을 알지 못했던 것처럼 느껴졌지."], ["This shame my husband fixed upon me; hemmed me round with, himself; steeped me in, with his own hands, and of his own act, repeated hundreds of times.", "이 수치를 내 남편이 내게 못 박았어. 자기가 직접 나를 그 안에 에워쌌고, 자기 손으로, 자기 행위로, 수백 번을 거듭하여 나를 그 안에 적셔 넣었지."], ["And thus—forced by the two from every point of rest I had—forced by the two to yield up the last retreat of love and gentleness within me, or to be a new misfortune on its innocent object—driven from each to each, and beset by one when I escaped the other—my anger rose almost to distraction against both.", "그리하여—그 두 사람에게 내가 쉴 수 있는 모든 자리에서 떠밀리고—그 두 사람에게, 내 안에 남은 사랑과 부드러움의 마지막 도피처를 내어 주거나, 아니면 그 죄 없는 대상에게 새로운 불행이 되거나 둘 중 하나로 내몰리고—이쪽에서 저쪽으로 쫓기며, 한쪽을 피하면 다른 쪽이 들러붙는 처지가 되어, 두 사람 다를 향한 내 분노는 거의 광기에 이르도록 치솟았어."], ["I do not know against which it rose higher—the master or the man!”", "어느 쪽을 향해 더 높이 치솟았는지는 나도 모르겠어—주인인지, 아니면 그 종인지!\""]], [["He watched her closely, as she stood before him in the very triumph of her indignant beauty. She was resolute, he saw; undauntable; with no more fear of him than of a worm.", "그는 그녀가 분개한 아름다움의 그 절정에 차서 자기 앞에 서 있는 것을 유심히 지켜보았다. 그녀는 단호했다. 그는 보았다. 굴할 줄 모르는 여인이었다. 벌레를 두려워하지 않듯 그를 조금도 두려워하지 않았다."]], [["“What should I say of honour or of chastity to you!”", "\"명예니 정조니 하는 것을 당신에게 무어라 말하겠어!\""], ["she went on.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“What meaning would it have to you; what meaning would it have from me!", "\"그게 당신에게 무슨 의미가 있겠으며, 내 입에서 나온들 무슨 의미가 있겠어!"], ["But if I tell you that the lightest touch of your hand makes my blood cold with antipathy; that from the hour when I first saw and hated you, to now, when my instinctive repugnance is enhanced by every minute’s knowledge of you I have since had, you have been a loathsome creature to me which has not its like on earth; how then?”", "하지만 만약 내가, 당신 손이 살짝만 닿아도 혐오감으로 내 피가 싸늘해진다고, 당신을 처음 보고 미워한 그 순간부터 지금까지—그 뒤로 매분 당신을 알아 갈수록 내 본능적인 역겨움이 한층 더해진 지금까지, 당신은 내게 이 땅에 둘도 없는 혐오스러운 존재였다고 말한다면, 그러면 어쩌겠어?\""]], [["He answered with a faint laugh, “Ay!", "그는 희미하게 웃으며 대꾸했다, \"허!"], ["How then, my queen?”", "그러면 어쩌겠소, 나의 여왕이여?\""]], [["“On that night, when, emboldened by the scene you had assisted at, you dared come to my room and speak to me,”", "\"그날 밤, 당신이 거들었던 그 장면에 대담해져서, 감히 내 방에 와서 내게 말을 걸었던 그날 밤,\""], ["she said, “what passed?”", "그녀가 말했다, \"무슨 일이 있었지?\""]], [["He shrugged his shoulders, and laughed", "그가 어깨를 으쓱하더니 웃었다."]], [["“What passed?”", "\"무슨 일이 있었지?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Your memory is so distinct,”", "\"당신 기억이 어찌나 또렷한지,\""], ["he said, “that I have no doubt you can recall it.”", "그가 말했다, \"당신이 다 떠올릴 수 있을 거라 믿어 의심치 않소.\""]], [["“I can,”", "\"떠올릴 수 있지,\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Hear it!", "\"들어 봐! 그때 당신은 이 도망을—이 도망이 아니라, 당신이 도망이라 여겼던 그 도망을 제안하면서, 내게 말했지."], ["Proposing then, this flight—not this flight, but the flight you thought it—you told me that in the having given you that meeting, and leaving you to be discovered there, if you so thought fit; and in the having suffered you to be alone with me many times before,—and having made the opportunities, you said,—and in the having openly avowed to you that I had no feeling for my husband but aversion, and no care for myself—I was lost; I had given you the power to traduce my name; and I lived, in virtuous reputation, at the pleasure of your breath.”", "당신에게 그 밀회를 허락하고, 당신이 그러는 게 좋다고 여기면 거기서 발각되도록 당신을 내버려 둠으로써, 또 그 전에도 여러 번 당신과 단둘이 있도록 내버려 둠으로써—그 기회를 만든 건 당신이었다고 했지만—또 내가 남편에게 혐오감 말고는 아무 감정도 없고 나 자신에 대해서도 아무 미련이 없다고 당신에게 공공연히 털어놓음으로써—나는 끝장났다고. 내 이름을 더럽힐 힘을 당신에게 내주었으며, 정숙하다는 평판 속에 사는 내 삶이 당신 입김 하나에 달려 있게 되었다고.\""]], [["“All stratagems in love—-”", "\"사랑에서는 어떤 책략도—\""], ["he interrupted, smiling.", "그가 미소 지으며 끼어들었다."], ["“The old adage—”", "\"옛 격언에도—\""]], [["“On that night,”", "\"그날 밤,\""], ["said Edith, “and then, the struggle that I long had had with something that was not respect for my good fame—that was I know not what—perhaps the clinging to that last retreat—was ended.", "이디스가 말했다, \"그리고 바로 그때, 내가 오랫동안 무언가와—내 좋은 평판에 대한 존중이 아닌 무언가와—그게 무엇인지 나는 알 수 없지만, 어쩌면 그 마지막 도피처에 매달리려는 마음이었을 그 무언가와 벌여 온 싸움이 끝나 버렸어."], ["On that night, and then, I turned from everything but passion and resentment.", "그날 밤, 그리고 바로 그때, 나는 격정과 원한 말고는 모든 것에서 등을 돌렸어."], ["I struck a blow that laid your lofty master in the dust, and set you there, before me, looking at me now, and knowing what I mean.”", "나는 일격을 가해 당신의 오만한 주인을 먼지 속에 처박았고, 당신을 거기, 내 앞에, 지금 나를 바라보며 내가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 아는 그 자리에 앉혀 놓았지.\""]], [["He sprung up from his chair with a great oath.", "그가 거센 욕설을 내뱉으며 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["She put her hand into her bosom, and not a finger trembled, not a hair upon her head was stirred.", "그녀는 손을 가슴께에 넣었고, 손가락 하나 떨지 않았으며, 머리카락 한 올도 흔들리지 않았다. 그가 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["He stood still: she too: the table and chair between them.", "그녀도 그러했다. 둘 사이에는 식탁과 의자가 놓여 있었다."]], [["“When I forget that this man put his lips to mine that night, and held me in his arms as he has done again tonight,”", "\"이 남자가 그날 밤 내 입술에 제 입술을 댔고, 오늘 밤 또다시 그랬듯이 나를 제 팔에 안았던 것을 내가 잊는다면,\""], ["said Edith, pointing at him; “when I forget the taint of his kiss upon my cheek—the cheek that Florence would have laid her guiltless face against—when I forget my meeting with her, while that taint was hot upon me, and in what a flood the knowledge rushed upon me when I saw her, that in releasing her from the persecution I had caused by my love, I brought a shame and degradation on her name through mine, and in all time to come should be the solitary figure representing in her mind her first avoidance of a guilty creature—then, Husband, from whom I stand divorced henceforth, I will forget these last two years, and undo what I have done, and undeceive you!”", "이디스가 그를 가리키며 말했다, \"내 뺨에 묻은 그 입맞춤의 오점을—플로렌스가 자기의 죄 없는 얼굴을 가져다 댔을 그 뺨에 묻은 오점을 내가 잊는다면—그 오점이 내게 뜨겁게 묻어 있는 동안 그 애를 만났던 일을, 그리고 그 애를 보았을 때 어떤 홍수처럼 내게 밀려들던 깨달음을—내 사랑이 불러온 박해에서 그 애를 풀어 주면서, 내 이름을 통해 그 애 이름에 수치와 오욕을 안겨 주었고, 또 앞으로 영원토록 나는 그 애 마음속에서 죄 지은 한 인간을 처음으로 피했던 일을 떠올리게 하는 외로운 형상이 되리라는 그 깨달음을 내가 잊는다면—그렇다면, 이제부터 내가 갈라선 남편이여, 나는 지난 이 년을 잊고, 내가 한 일을 되돌리며, 당신의 착각을 풀어 주겠어!\""]], [["Her flashing eyes, uplifted for a moment, lighted again on Carker, and she held some letters out in her left hand.", "번득이는 그녀의 눈이 잠시 치켜 올라갔다가 다시 카커에게로 떨어졌고, 그녀는 왼손에 편지 몇 통을 내밀었다."]], [["“See these!”", "\"이것들 봐!\""], ["she said, contemptuously.", "그녀가 경멸에 차서 말했다."], ["“You have addressed these to me in the false name you go by; one here, some elsewhere on my road.", "\"당신은 당신이 쓰고 다니는 그 가짜 이름으로 이것들을 내게 부쳤지. 하나는 여기로, 몇 통은 내가 오는 길의 다른 곳으로."], ["The seals are unbroken.", "봉인은 뜯지 않았어."], ["Take them back!”", "도로 가져가!\""]], [["She crunched them in her hand, and tossed them to his feet.", "그녀는 그것들을 손안에서 구겨 그의 발치에 내던졌다."], ["And as she looked upon him now, a smile was on her face.", "그리고 이제 그를 바라보는 그녀의 얼굴에는 미소가 어려 있었다."]], [["“We meet and part tonight,” she said.", "\"우리는 오늘 밤 만나고 오늘 밤 헤어져,\""], ["“You have fallen on Sicilian days and sensual rest, too soon.", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 시칠리아의 나날과 육욕의 안식에 너무 일찍 빠졌어."], ["You might have cajoled, and fawned, and played your traitor’s part, a little longer, and grown richer.", "당신은 좀 더 오래 알랑거리고, 아첨하고, 배신자의 노릇을 하면서, 더 부자가 될 수도 있었을 텐데."], ["You purchase your voluptuous retirement dear!”", "당신은 그 향락의 은둔을 비싼 값을 치르고 사는 거야!\""]], [["“Edith!”", "\"이디스!\""], ["he retorted, menacing her with his hand.", "그가 손으로 그녀를 위협하며 되받았다."], ["“Sit down!", "\"앉아!"], ["Have done with this!", "이 짓을 그만둬!"], ["What devil possesses you?”", "대체 무슨 악마가 당신한테 들린 거야?\""]], [["“Their name is Legion,”", "\"그것들의 이름은 군대처럼 많지,\""], ["she replied, uprearing her proud form as if she would have crushed him; “you and your master have raised them in a fruitful house, and they shall tear you both.", "그녀가 마치 그를 짓뭉개 버리기라도 할 듯 그 오만한 몸을 곧추세우며 대꾸했다. \"당신과 당신 주인이 풍요로운 한 집안에서 그것들을 불러일으켰으니, 그것들이 당신들 둘 다를 갈가리 찢어 놓을 거야."], ["False to him, false to his innocent child, false every way and everywhere, go forth and boast of me, and gnash your teeth, for once, to know that you are lying!”", "그에게 거짓되고, 그 죄 없는 아이에게 거짓되고, 어느 모로 보나 어디서나 거짓된 자여, 가서 나를 두고 떠벌리고, 그러면서 단 한 번이라도 당신이 거짓말을 하고 있음을 알아채고 이를 갈아 봐!\" * 군대처럼 많지: 신약성서(마가복음 5장)에서 예수가 귀신 들린 자에게 이름을 묻자 \"내 이름은 군대(Legion)니 우리가 많음이니이다\"라고 답한 데서 온 표현."]], [["He stood before her, muttering and menacing, and scowling round as if for something that would help him to conquer her; but with the same indomitable spirit she opposed him, without faltering.", "그는 그녀 앞에 서서, 무어라 중얼거리고 위협하며, 그녀를 제압할 수 있을 무언가라도 찾는 듯 사방을 노려보았다. 그러나 그녀는 똑같이 굴할 줄 모르는 기개로, 조금도 흔들림 없이 그에게 맞섰다."]], [["“In every vaunt you make,”", "\"당신이 떠벌리는 자랑 하나하나마다,\""], ["she said, “I have my triumph.", "그녀가 말했다, \"나는 나의 승리를 거두지."], ["I single out in you the meanest man I know, the parasite and tool of the proud tyrant, that his wound may go the deeper, and may rankle more.", "나는 당신 안에서 내가 아는 가장 비열한 인간을, 그 오만한 폭군의 기생충이자 도구를 골라내. 그래야 그자의 상처가 더 깊이 파이고, 더 쓰라리게 곪을 테니까."], ["Boast, and revenge me on him!", "떠벌려서 나를 위해 그자에게 복수해 줘! 당신은 오늘 밤 어떻게 여기 왔는지 알지."], ["You know how you came here tonight; you know how you stand cowering there; you see yourself in colours quite as despicable, if not as odious, as those in which I see you.", "거기 어떻게 움츠리고 서 있는지 알지. 내가 당신을 보는 빛깔만큼 가증스럽지는 않다 해도, 그만큼 경멸스러운 빛깔로 칠해진 당신 자신의 모습이 당신 눈에도 보이지."], ["Boast then, and revenge me on yourself.”", "그러니 떠벌려서, 당신 자신에게 나를 위한 복수를 해 줘.\""]], [["The foam was on his lips; the wet stood on his forehead.", "그의 입가에는 거품이 일었고, 이마에는 땀이 맺혔다."], ["If she would have faltered once for only one half-moment, he would have pinioned her; but she was as firm as rock, and her searching eyes never left him.", "만약 그녀가 단 한 번이라도, 단 반 순간만이라도 흔들렸더라면, 그는 그녀를 옴짝달싹 못 하게 붙들었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 바위처럼 단단했고, 그녀의 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈은 한 번도 그에게서 떠나지 않았다."]], [["“We don’t part so,” he said.", "\"우린 이렇게는 헤어지지 않아,\""], ["“Do you think I am drivelling, to let you go in your mad temper?”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 당신을 그 미친 성깔대로 가게 놔둘 만큼 얼간이인 줄 알아?\""]], [["“Do you think,”", "\"당신은,\""], ["she answered, “that I am to be stayed?”", "그녀가 대답했다, \"내가 붙들려 머물 사람인 줄 알아?\""]], [["“I’ll try, my dear,”", "\"한번 해 보지, 내 사랑,\""], ["he said with a ferocious gesture of his head.", "그가 사납게 고갯짓을 하며 말했다."]], [["“God’s mercy on you, if you try by coming near me!”", "\"가까이 와서 한번 해 보겠다면, 하느님의 자비가 당신에게 있기를!\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 응수했다."]], [["“And what,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["he said, “if there are none of these same boasts and vaunts on my part?", "그가 말했다, \"내가 그런 떠벌림이니 자랑이니 하는 짓을 안 한다면 어쩔 거요?"], ["What if I were to turn too?", "나도 돌아선다면?"], ["Come!” and his teeth fairly shone again.", "자!\"—그러더니 그의 이가 다시 번쩍 빛났다."], ["“We must make a treaty of this, or I may take some unexpected course.", "\"우린 이걸 두고 협정을 맺어야 해, 안 그러면 나도 무슨 뜻밖의 수를 쓸지 모르니까."], ["Sit down, sit down!”", "앉아, 앉으라고!\""]], [["“Too late!”", "\"너무 늦었어!\""], ["she cried, with eyes that seemed to sparkle fire.", "그녀가 불을 뿜는 듯한 눈으로 외쳤다."], ["“I have thrown my fame and good name to the winds!", "\"나는 내 명성과 좋은 이름을 바람결에 던져 버렸어!"], ["I have resolved to bear the shame that will attach to me—resolved to know that it attaches falsely—that you know it too—and that he does not, never can, and never shall.", "내게 들러붙을 그 수치를 견디기로 마음먹었지—그것이 거짓으로 들러붙은 것임을 안다는 것도—당신 역시 그걸 안다는 것도—그리고 그는 그것을 모르며, 결코 알 수 없고, 결코 알게 되지도 않으리라는 것도 알면서 말이야."], ["I’ll die, and make no sign.", "나는 죽어 가면서도 아무 내색도 하지 않을 거야."], ["For this, I am here alone with you, at the dead of night.", "이걸 위해서, 나는 이 깊은 밤에 여기 당신과 단둘이 와 있는 거야."], ["For this, I have met you here, in a false name, as your wife.", "이걸 위해서, 나는 가짜 이름으로, 당신의 아내인 척하며 여기서 당신을 만난 거야."], ["For this, I have been seen here by those men, and left here.", "이걸 위해서, 나는 저 사내들에게 여기 모습을 보였고, 여기 남겨졌던 거야."], ["Nothing can save you now.”", "이제 그 무엇도 당신을 구할 수 없어.\""]], [["He would have sold his soul to root her, in her beauty, to the floor, and make her arms drop at her sides, and have her at his mercy.", "그는 그녀를, 그 아름다움 그대로 마룻바닥에 못 박아, 두 팔을 옆구리에 떨구게 하여, 자기 손아귀에 넣을 수만 있다면 제 영혼이라도 팔았을 것이다."], ["But he could not look at her, and not be afraid of her.", "그러나 그는 그녀를 바라보면서 그녀가 두렵지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He saw a strength within her that was resistless.", "그는 그녀 안에서 도저히 거스를 수 없는 어떤 힘을 보았다."], ["He saw that she was desperate, and that her unquenchable hatred of him would stop at nothing.", "그는 그녀가 필사적이며, 그녀의 그 꺼지지 않는 증오가 그 무엇 앞에서도 멈추지 않으리라는 것을 보았다."], ["His eyes followed the hand that was put with such rugged uncongenial purpose into her white bosom, and he thought that if it struck at him, and failed, it would strike there, just as soon.", "그의 눈은 그처럼 거칠고 어울리지 않는 결의로 그녀의 흰 가슴께에 찔러 넣은 그 손을 좇았고, 만약 그 손이 자기를 겨누었다가 빗나간다면, 그 손은 그만큼 빠르게 거기, 그녀 자신을 겨눌 것이라는 생각이 들었다."]], [["He did not venture, therefore, to advance towards her; but the door by which he had entered was behind him, and he stepped back to lock it.", "그래서 그는 감히 그녀에게로 다가서지 못했다. 그러나 그가 들어왔던 문이 등 뒤에 있었기에, 그는 그것을 잠그려고 뒷걸음질했다."]], [["“Lastly, take my warning!", "\"마지막으로, 내 경고를 들어!"], ["Look to yourself!”", "당신 자신을 조심해!\""], ["she said, and smiled again.", "그녀가 말하며 다시 미소 지었다."], ["“You have been betrayed, as all betrayers are.", "\"당신은 배신당했어, 모든 배신자가 그러하듯."], ["It has been made known that you are in this place, or were to be, or have been.", "당신이 이곳에 있다는 것, 혹은 있을 예정이었다는 것, 혹은 있었다는 것이 알려졌어."], ["If I live, I saw my husband in a carriage in the street tonight!”", "내가 살아 있는 것이 분명하듯, 오늘 밤 거리에서 마차 안에 있는 내 남편을 보았어!\""]], [["“Strumpet, it’s false!”", "\"이 갈보 같은 년, 거짓말이야!\""], ["cried Carker.", "카커가 소리쳤다."]], [["At the moment, the bell rang loudly in the hall.", "바로 그 순간, 현관에서 종이 요란하게 울렸다."], ["He turned white, as she held her hand up like an enchantress, at whose invocation the sound had come.", "그가 새파랗게 질렸고, 그녀는 마치 그 부름에 그 소리가 응답해 온 마법사처럼 손을 치켜들었다."]], [["“Hark!", "\"들어 봐!"], ["do you hear it?”", "저 소리 들리지?\""]], [["He set his back against the door; for he saw a change in her, and fancied she was coming on to pass him.", "그는 등을 문에 기댔다. 그녀에게 어떤 변화가 이는 것을 보고, 그녀가 자기를 지나쳐 가려고 다가오는 줄 알았기 때문이다."], ["But, in a moment, she was gone through the opposite doors communicating with the bed-chamber, and they shut upon her.", "그러나 어느새 그녀는 침실로 통하는 맞은편 문을 통해 사라졌고, 그 문들은 그녀의 등 뒤로 닫혔다."]], [["Once turned, once changed in her inflexible unyielding look, he felt that he could cope with her.", "그녀가 일단 돌아서자, 그 굽힐 줄 모르고 양보할 줄 모르던 표정이 일단 바뀌자, 그는 자기가 그녀를 능히 다룰 수 있겠다고 느꼈다."], ["He thought a sudden terror, occasioned by this night-alarm, had subdued her; not the less readily, for her overwrought condition.", "그는 이 한밤의 경보로 일어난 갑작스러운 공포가 그녀를 굴복시켰다고 생각했고, 그녀의 그 극도로 지친 상태를 생각하면 더욱 그러기 쉬우리라 여겼다."], ["Throwing open the doors, he followed, almost instantly.", "그는 문을 활짝 열어젖히고, 거의 곧바로 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["But the room was dark; and as she made no answer to his call, he was fain to go back for the lamp.", "그러나 방은 어두웠고, 그녀가 그의 부름에 아무런 대답도 하지 않자, 그는 하는 수 없이 등불을 가지러 돌아갔다."], ["He held it up, and looked round, everywhere, expecting to see her crouching in some corner; but the room was empty.", "그는 등불을 치켜들고, 그녀가 어느 구석엔가 웅크리고 있는 것을 보게 되리라 기대하며 사방을 두루 둘러보았으나, 방은 비어 있었다."], ["So, into the drawing-room and dining-room he went, in succession, with the uncertain steps of a man in a strange place; looking fearfully about, and prying behind screens and couches; but she was not there.", "그래서 그는 낯선 곳에 있는 사람의 미덥지 못한 걸음걸이로 응접실로, 또 식당으로 차례차례 들어가, 겁에 질려 두리번거리며 칸막이와 긴 의자 뒤를 들여다보았다. 그러나 그녀는 거기에 없었다."], ["No, nor in the hall, which was so bare that he could see that, at a glance.", "아니, 현관에도 없었으니, 그곳은 어찌나 휑한지 한눈에 다 볼 수 있었다."]], [["All this time, the ringing at the bell was constantly renewed, and those without were beating at the door.", "이 내내, 종 울리는 소리는 끊임없이 거듭되었고, 바깥에 있는 자들은 문을 두들기고 있었다."], ["He put his lamp down at a distance, and going near it, listened.", "그는 등불을 멀찍이 내려놓고, 그쪽으로 다가가 귀를 기울였다. 여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 떠들고 있었다."], ["There were several voices talking together: at least two of them in English; and though the door was thick, and there was great confusion, he knew one of these too well to doubt whose voice it was.", "적어도 그 가운데 둘은 영어였다. 문이 두꺼웠고 몹시 어수선했지만, 그는 그 가운데 한 목소리를 너무도 잘 알고 있었기에 그것이 누구의 목소리인지 의심할 여지가 없었다."]], [["He took up his lamp again, and came back quickly through all the rooms, stopping as he quitted each, and looking round for her, with the light raised above his head.", "그는 다시 등불을 집어 들고, 모든 방을 빠르게 되짚어 오면서, 방을 하나씩 나설 때마다 멈춰 서서, 불을 머리 위로 치켜든 채 그녀를 찾아 둘러보았다."], ["He was standing thus in the bed-chamber, when the door, leading to the little passage in the wall, caught his eye.", "그가 그렇게 침실에 서 있을 때, 벽 안의 작은 통로로 통하는 문이 그의 눈에 들어왔다."], ["He went to it, and found it fastened on the other side; but she had dropped a veil in going through, and shut it in the door.", "그가 그리로 가 보니 문은 반대편에서 잠겨 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 빠져나가면서 베일을 떨어뜨렸고, 그것이 문에 끼인 채였다."]], [["All this time the people on the stairs were ringing at the bell, and knocking with their hands and feet.", "이 내내 계단에 있는 사람들은 종을 울리고, 손과 발로 두드려 댔다."]], [["He was not a coward: but these sounds; what had gone before; the strangeness of the place, which had confused him, even in his return from the hall; the frustration of his schemes (for, strange to say, he would have been much bolder, if they had succeeded); the unseasonable time; the recollection of having no one near to whom he could appeal for any friendly office; above all, the sudden sense, which made even his heart beat like lead, that the man whose confidence he had outraged, and whom he had so treacherously deceived, was there to recognise and challenge him with his mask plucked off his face; struck a panic through him.", "그는 겁쟁이는 아니었다. 그러나 이 소리들, 그 전에 벌어진 일, 현관에서 돌아오는 길에도 그를 어리둥절하게 만든 이 장소의 낯섦, 그의 계략이 좌절된 것(이상한 말이지만, 만약 그 계략이 성공했더라면 그는 훨씬 더 대담했을 것이다), 때아닌 시각, 어떤 우호적인 도움이라도 청할 만한 사람이 가까이 아무도 없다는 기억, 무엇보다도, 자기가 그 신뢰를 짓밟고 그토록 배신하여 속여 넘긴 그 사내가, 자기 얼굴에서 가면이 벗겨진 채 자기를 알아보고 도전하려고 거기 와 있다는 갑작스러운 깨달음—그의 심장마저 납덩이처럼 쿵쾅거리게 만든 그 깨달음이, 그의 온몸을 공포로 꿰뚫었다."], ["He tried the door in which the veil was shut, but couldn’t force it.", "그는 베일이 끼인 그 문을 열어 보려 했으나, 억지로 열 수가 없었다."], ["He opened one of the windows, and looked down through the lattice of the blind, into the court-yard; but it was a high leap, and the stones were pitiless.", "그는 창문 하나를 열고, 덧창의 격자 사이로 안뜰을 내려다보았다. 그러나 너무 높은 곳이었고, 바닥의 돌은 무자비했다."]], [["The ringing and knocking still continuing—his panic too—he went back to the door in the bed-chamber, and with some new efforts, each more stubborn than the last, wrenched it open.", "종소리와 두드리는 소리가 여전히 계속되고—그의 공포도 그러하여—그는 침실에 있는 그 문으로 돌아가, 거듭하여 새로 힘을 쓰며, 한 번 한 번 앞선 것보다 더 끈질기게 애쓴 끝에, 그것을 비틀어 열었다."], ["Seeing the little staircase not far off, and feeling the night-air coming up, he stole back for his hat and coat, made the door as secure after him as he could, crept down lamp in hand, extinguished it on seeing the street, and having put it in a corner, went out where the stars were shining.", "작은 계단이 멀지 않은 곳에 있는 것을 보고, 밤공기가 올라오는 것을 느끼며, 그는 모자와 외투를 가지러 살그머니 돌아갔다가, 등 뒤로 그 문을 할 수 있는 한 단단히 닫고는, 등불을 손에 든 채 살금살금 내려가, 거리가 보이자 그것을 끄고, 한구석에 놓아둔 다음, 별들이 빛나고 있는 바깥으로 나섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER LV. Rob the Grinder loses his Place", "# CHAPTER LV. Rob the Grinder loses his Place"]], [["The Porter at the iron gate which shut the court-yard from the street, had left the little wicket of his house open, and was gone away; no doubt to mingle in the distant noise at the door of the great staircase.", "「분쇄기」 롭이 일자리를 잃다\n거리와 안뜰을 가르는 철문을 지키는 문지기는 자기 오두막의 작은 쪽문을 열어 둔 채 어디론가 가 버렸다."], ["Lifting the latch softly, Carker crept out, and shutting the jangling gate after him with as little noise as possible, hurried off.", "의심할 바 없이 큰 계단 입구에서 들려오는 멀리 떨어진 소란에 한몫 끼려고 간 것이리라. 카커는 빗장을 살그머니 들어 올리고 살금살금 빠져나가, 삐걱거리는 철문을 되도록 소리 나지 않게 등 뒤로 닫고는 서둘러 자리를 떴다."]], [["In the fever of his mortification and unavailing rage, the panic that had seized upon him mastered him completely.", "굴욕과 부질없는 분노의 열병 속에서, 그를 사로잡은 공포가 그를 완전히 지배해 버렸다."], ["It rose to such a height that he would have blindly encountered almost any risk, rather than meet the man of whom, two hours ago, he had been utterly regardless.", "그 공포가 어찌나 높이 치솟았던지, 그는 두 시간 전만 해도 전혀 안중에도 없던 그 사내와 마주치느니 차라리 거의 어떤 위험이든 눈먼 채 무릅쓰려 했을 것이다."], ["His fierce arrival, which he had never expected; the sound of his voice; their having been so near a meeting, face to face; he would have braved out this, after the first momentary shock of alarm, and would have put as bold a front upon his guilt as any villain.", "결코 예상하지 못한 그의 거센 도착, 그 목소리의 울림, 두 사람이 얼굴을 맞대고 마주칠 뻔했던 그 아슬아슬함—처음의 순간적인 경악의 충격이 지나간 뒤였다면, 그는 이런 것쯤은 끝까지 견뎌 내고, 그 어떤 악당 못지않게 자기 죄에 대담한 낯을 들이밀었을 것이다."], ["But the springing of his mine upon himself, seemed to have rent and shivered all his hardihood and self-reliance.", "그러나 자기가 묻어 둔 지뢰가 도리어 자기 발밑에서 터져 버린 듯한 일은, 그의 모든 강단과 자만을 갈가리 찢고 산산조각 낸 것 같았다."], ["Spurned like any reptile; entrapped and mocked; turned upon, and trodden down by the proud woman whose mind he had slowly poisoned, as he thought, until she had sunk into the mere creature of his pleasure; undeceived in his deceit, and with his fox’s hide stripped off, he sneaked away, abashed, degraded, and afraid.", "어떤 파충류처럼 걷어차이고, 덫에 걸려 조롱당하고, 자기가 천천히 그 마음에 독을 부어 넣어 마침내 자기 쾌락의 한낱 도구로 가라앉혀 놓았다고 여겼던 그 도도한 여인에게 도리어 역공당하고 짓밟혀, 자기 속임수에 자기가 속았음을 깨닫고 여우 가죽이 벗겨진 채, 그는 부끄럽고 천하고 두려운 몸으로 슬그머니 달아났다."]], [["Some other terror came upon him quite removed from this of being pursued, suddenly, like an electric shock, as he was creeping through the streets Some visionary terror, unintelligible and inexplicable, associated with a trembling of the ground,—a rush and sweep of something through the air, like Death upon the wing.", "거리를 살금살금 빠져나가는 동안, 추격당한다는 이 두려움과는 전혀 동떨어진 또 다른 공포가, 마치 전기 충격처럼 갑자기 그를 덮쳐 왔다. 알 수도 없고 설명할 수도 없는 어떤 환영 같은 공포였는데, 땅의 떨림과—날개 단 죽음처럼 무언가가 공중을 가르며 휙 쓸고 지나가는 것과 결부되어 있었다."], ["He shrunk, as if to let the thing go by.", "그는 그것을 지나가게 두려는 듯 움츠러들었다."], ["It was not gone, it never had been there, yet what a startling horror it had left behind.", "그것은 가 버린 것도 아니었고, 애초에 거기 있었던 적도 없었으나, 뒤에 남긴 그 소름 끼치는 공포란 얼마나 깜짝 놀랄 만한 것이었던가."]], [["He raised his wicked face so full of trouble, to the night sky, where the stars, so full of peace, were shining on him as they had been when he first stole out into the air; and stopped to think what he should do.", "그는 근심으로 가득 찬 그 사악한 얼굴을 밤하늘로 들어 올렸다. 그곳에서는, 그가 처음 바깥 공기 속으로 살그머니 나섰을 때와 똑같이, 평화로 가득 찬 별들이 그를 비추고 있었다."], ["The dread of being hunted in a strange remote place, where the laws might not protect him—the novelty of the feeling that it was strange and remote, originating in his being left alone so suddenly amid the ruins of his plans—his greater dread of seeking refuge now, in Italy or in Sicily, where men might be hired to assassinate him, he thought, at any dark street corner—the waywardness of guilt and fear—perhaps some sympathy of action with the turning back of all his schemes—impelled him to turn back too, and go to England.", "그는 멈춰 서서 무엇을 해야 할지 생각했다. 법이 그를 보호해 주지 않을지도 모르는 낯설고 외딴곳에서 사냥당할 것이라는 두려움—자기 계획의 폐허 한가운데 이토록 갑작스레 홀로 남겨진 데서 비롯된, 그곳이 낯설고 외딴곳이라는 느낌의 생경함—이탈리아나 시칠리아 같은, 어느 어두운 길모퉁이에서든 자기를 암살하라고 사람을 살 수도 있다고 생각되는 곳에 지금 피신처를 구하는 것에 대한 더 큰 두려움—죄와 공포의 변덕스러움—어쩌면 자기 모든 계략이 거꾸로 돌아선 것과 행동에서 어떤 공명을 이룬 까닭이었는지, 그것이 그로 하여금 자기도 발길을 돌려 영국으로 가도록 떠밀었다."]], [["“I am safer there, in any case. If I should not decide,”", "\"어쨌든 거기가 더 안전해."], ["he thought, “to give this fool a meeting, I am less likely to be traced there, than abroad here, now.", "만일 이 어리석은 놈에게 한판 맞붙기로 결심하지 않는다 해도,\" 그는 생각했다, \"지금 여기 외국에 있는 것보다는 거기서 추적당할 가능성이 더 적어."], ["And if I should (this cursed fit being over), at least I shall not be alone, without a soul to speak to, or advise with, or stand by me.", "그리고 만일 맞붙기로 한다면(이 빌어먹을 발작이 끝나고 나면), 적어도 나는 말 한마디 나눌 사람도, 의논할 사람도, 곁에 서 줄 사람도 없이 혼자는 아닐 거야."], ["I shall not be run in upon and worried like a rat.”", "쥐처럼 몰려서 들볶이지는 않을 거야.\""]], [["He muttered Edith’s name, and clenched his hand.", "그는 이디스의 이름을 중얼거리며 주먹을 꽉 쥐었다."], ["As he crept along, in the shadow of the massive buildings, he set his teeth, and muttered dreadful imprecations on her head, and looked from side to side, as if in search of her.", "육중한 건물들의 그늘 속을 살금살금 따라가면서, 그는 이를 악물고 그녀의 머리 위에 무시무시한 저주를 퍼붓듯 중얼거렸으며, 마치 그녀를 찾기라도 하듯 좌우를 두리번거렸다."], ["Thus, he stole on to the gate of an inn-yard.", "그렇게 해서 그는 어느 여관 안마당의 문 앞까지 슬그머니 다가갔다."], ["The people were a-bed; but his ringing at the bell soon produced a man with a lantern, in company with whom he was presently in a dim coach-house, bargaining for the hire of an old phaeton, to Paris.", "사람들은 잠자리에 들어 있었으나, 그가 종을 울리자 곧 등불을 든 사내 하나가 나타났고, 그와 함께 그는 이내 어둑한 마차 보관소 안에서 파리까지 가는 낡은 사륜마차를 빌리는 흥정을 하고 있었다."]], [["The bargain was a short one; and the horses were soon sent for.", "흥정은 짧게 끝났고, 곧 말을 데리러 사람을 보냈다."], ["Leaving word that the carriage was to follow him when they came, he stole away again, beyond the town, past the old ramparts, out on the open road, which seemed to glide away along the dark plain, like a stream.", "말이 도착하면 마차가 자기 뒤를 따라오게 하라는 말을 남기고, 그는 다시 슬그머니 빠져나가, 읍내를 지나, 오래된 성벽을 지나, 탁 트인 길로 나섰다. 그 길은 어두운 평원을 따라 한 줄기 시냇물처럼 미끄러져 사라지는 것 같았다."]], [["Whither did it flow?", "그 길은 어디로 흘러가는 것일까?"], ["What was the end of it?", "그 끝은 무엇일까?"], ["As he paused, with some such suggestion within him, looking over the gloomy flat where the slender trees marked out the way, again that flight of Death came rushing up, again went on, impetuous and resistless, again was nothing but a horror in his mind, dark as the scene and undefined as its remotest verge.", "그가 마음속에 그런 어떤 물음을 품은 채 멈춰 서서, 가느다란 나무들이 길을 표시해 주는 음울한 평지를 바라보고 있을 때, 다시 그 죽음의 비행이 휙 다가왔다가, 다시 격렬하고 거역할 수 없는 기세로 지나가더니, 다시 그의 마음속의 한낱 공포에 지나지 않게 되었다—그 풍경처럼 어둡고, 그 가장 먼 끝자락처럼 형체가 분명치 않은 공포였다."]], [["There was no wind; there was no passing shadow on the deep shade of the night; there was no noise.", "바람도 없었고, 밤의 깊은 그늘 위로 지나가는 그림자 하나 없었으며, 소리도 없었다."], ["The city lay behind him, lighted here and there, and starry worlds were hidden by the masonry of spire and roof that hardly made out any shapes against the sky.", "도시는 여기저기 불빛이 켜진 채 그의 뒤편에 놓여 있었고, 별이 총총한 세계들은 첨탑과 지붕의 석조 건축물에 가려져 있었는데, 그 건축물들은 하늘을 배경으로 거의 어떤 형체도 드러내지 못했다."], ["Dark and lonely distance lay around him everywhere, and the clocks were faintly striking two.", "어둡고 적막한 거리가 그의 사방 곳곳에 펼쳐져 있었고, 시계가 희미하게 두 시를 치고 있었다."]], [["He went forward for what appeared a long time, and a long way; often stopping to listen.", "그는 오랜 시간 같고 먼 길 같은 거리를 앞으로 나아갔다. 자주 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울이면서."], ["At last the ringing of horses’ bells greeted his anxious ears.", "마침내 말방울 소리가 그의 초조한 귀를 반겼다."], ["Now softer, and now louder, now inaudible, now ringing very slowly over bad ground, now brisk and merry, it came on; until with a loud shouting and lashing, a shadowy postillion muffled to the eyes, checked his four struggling horses at his side.", "어떤 때는 더 부드럽게, 어떤 때는 더 크게, 어떤 때는 들리지 않다가, 어떤 때는 험한 길 위에서 아주 느리게 울리고, 어떤 때는 경쾌하고 흥겹게 다가오더니, 마침내 큰 고함과 채찍질 소리와 함께, 눈까지 천으로 감싼 그림자 같은 마부가 발버둥 치는 네 마리 말을 그의 곁에 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Who goes there!", "\"거기 누구요!"], ["Monsieur?”", "무슈?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Monsieur has walked a long way in the dark midnight.”", "\"무슈께서 이 깜깜한 한밤중에 먼 길을 걸어오셨군요.\""]], [["“No matter.", "\"상관없소."], ["Everyone to his task.", "사람마다 제 할 일이 있는 법이지."], ["Were there any other horses ordered at the Post-house?”", "역참에 다른 말을 주문해 둔 사람이 있었소?\""]], [["“A thousand devils!—and pardons!", "\"천 마리 악마가!—아, 실례했습니다!"], ["other horses?", "다른 말이요?"], ["at this hour?", "이 시각에?"], ["No.”", "아니요.\""]], [["“Listen, my friend.", "\"이보게, 친구."], ["I am much hurried.", "나는 무척 급하다네."], ["Let us see how fast we can travel!", "우리 얼마나 빨리 달릴 수 있는지 한번 봅시다!"], ["The faster, the more money there will be to drink.", "빠르면 빠를수록 술값이 더 많아질 거요."], ["Off we go then!", "자, 그럼 출발하세!"], ["Quick!”", "어서!\""]], [["“Halloa!", "\"이랴!"], ["whoop!", "워!"], ["Halloa!", "이랴!"], ["Hi!”", "히!\""], ["Away, at a gallop, over the black landscape, scattering the dust and dirt like spray!", "검은 풍경 위로, 흙먼지를 물보라처럼 흩날리며, 전속력으로 내달렸다!"]], [["The clatter and commotion echoed to the hurry and discordance of the fugitive’s ideas.", "덜컹거리는 소리와 소란이 그 도망자의 다급하고 어긋난 생각들과 메아리쳐 울렸다."], ["Nothing clear without, and nothing clear within.", "바깥에도 분명한 것이 없었고, 안에도 분명한 것이 없었다."], ["Objects flitting past, merging into one another, dimly descried, confusedly lost sight of, gone!", "사물들이 휙휙 스쳐 지나가며, 서로 뒤섞이고, 어렴풋이 보이는가 싶더니, 어수선하게 시야에서 사라져, 가 버렸다!"], ["Beyond the changing scraps of fence and cottage immediately upon the road, a lowering waste.", "길 바로 옆으로 변해 가는 울타리와 오두막 조각들 너머에는, 음울하게 내려앉은 황무지가 있었다."], ["Beyond the shifting images that rose up in his mind and vanished as they showed themselves, a black expanse of dread and rage and baffled villainy.", "그의 마음속에 떠올랐다가 모습을 드러내자마자 사라져 버리는 변화무쌍한 영상들 너머에는, 공포와 분노와 좌절된 악행의 검은 광막함이 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["Occasionally, a sigh of mountain air came from the distant Jura, fading along the plain.", "이따금, 산의 바람 한 줄기가 멀리 쥐라산맥에서 불어와, 평원을 따라 잦아들었다."], ["Sometimes that rush which was so furious and horrible, again came sweeping through his fancy, passed away, and left a chill upon his blood.", "때로는, 그토록 사납고 끔찍하던 그 돌진이, 다시 그의 환상 속을 휩쓸고 지나갔다가, 사라지면서, 그의 피에 한기를 남겼다."]], [["The lamps, gleaming on the medley of horses’ heads, jumbled with the shadowy driver, and the fluttering of his cloak, made a thousand indistinct shapes, answering to his thoughts.", "말들의 머리가 뒤섞인 혼란 위로 어른거리는 등불은, 그림자 같은 마부와, 펄럭이는 그의 망토와 한데 어우러져, 그의 생각에 응답하듯 분명치 않은 형체를 수천 가지나 만들어 냈다."], ["Shadows of familiar people, stooping at their desks and books, in their remembered attitudes; strange apparitions of the man whom he was flying from, or of Edith; repetitions in the ringing bells and rolling wheels, of words that had been spoken; confusions of time and place, making last night a month ago, a month ago last night—home now distant beyond hope, now instantly accessible; commotion, discord, hurry, darkness, and confusion in his mind, and all around him.—Hallo!", "책상과 책에 몸을 숙인 채, 기억 속 자세 그대로 있는 낯익은 사람들의 그림자들, 그가 달아나고 있는 그 사내나 이디스의 기이한 환영들, 울리는 방울 소리와 구르는 바퀴 소리 속에서 되풀이되는, 입에 올려졌던 말들, 어젯밤을 한 달 전으로, 한 달 전을 어젯밤으로 만들어 버리는 시간과 장소의 혼란—이제 집은 희망 너머로 멀어졌다가, 이제 당장 닿을 수 있는 곳에 있고—그의 마음속과 그의 사방의 온갖 소란, 불협화, 다급함, 어둠, 혼란.—이랴!"], ["Hi!", "히!"], ["away at a gallop over the black landscape; dust and dirt flying like spray, the smoking horses snorting and plunging as if each of them were ridden by a demon, away in a frantic triumph on the dark road—whither?", "검은 풍경 위로 전속력으로 내달렸다. 흙먼지가 물보라처럼 날리고, 김을 내뿜는 말들이 마치 저마다 악마라도 태운 듯 콧김을 뿜으며 날뛰고, 어두운 길 위에서 미친 듯한 승리감에 휩싸여 달려갔다—어디로?"]], [["Again the nameless shock comes speeding up, and as it passes, the bells ring in his ears “whither?”", "다시 그 이름 없는 충격이 빠르게 다가왔고, 그것이 지나갈 때, 방울들이 그의 귓속에서 \"어디로?\" 하고 울렸다."], ["The wheels roar in his ears “whither?”", "바퀴들이 그의 귓속에서 \"어디로?\" 하고 으르렁거렸다."], ["All the noise and rattle shapes itself into that cry.", "그 모든 소음과 덜컹거림이 그 외침으로 형체를 갖추었다."], ["The lights and shadows dance upon the horses’ heads like imps.", "빛과 그림자가 마치 작은 도깨비들처럼 말들의 머리 위에서 춤추었다."], ["No stopping now: no slackening!", "이제 멈춤은 없었다. 늦춤도 없었다!"], ["On, on!", "가자, 가자!"], ["Away with him upon the dark road wildly!", "어두운 길 위로 그를 싣고 미친 듯이 달려라!"]], [["He could not think to any purpose.", "그는 무엇 하나 제대로 생각할 수가 없었다."], ["He could not separate one subject of reflection from another, sufficiently to dwell upon it, by itself, for a minute at a time.", "한 가지 사색의 주제를 다른 주제와 떼어 내어, 한 번에 일 분이라도 그것 하나에만 머무를 만큼 따로 떼어 둘 수가 없었다."], ["The crash of his project for the gaining of a voluptuous compensation for past restraint; the overthrow of his treachery to one who had been true and generous to him, but whose least proud word and look he had treasured up, at interest, for years—for false and subtle men will always secretly despise and dislike the object upon which they fawn and always resent the payment and receipt of homage that they know to be worthless; these were the themes uppermost in his mind.", "지난날의 억제에 대한 음탕한 보상을 얻으려던 그의 계획이 무너진 것, 자기에게 진실하고 너그러웠으나 그가 그 가장 사소한 도도한 말과 표정조차 수년간 이자까지 쳐서 마음속에 쌓아 둔 그 사람에게 저지른 배신이 뒤엎인 것—거짓되고 음흉한 사내들은 늘 자기가 아첨하는 대상을 속으로 멸시하고 싫어하며, 자기가 무가치하다고 아는 그 경의를 바치고 받는 일에 늘 분개하는 법이므로—이런 것들이 그의 마음속에 가장 두드러진 주제들이었다."], ["A lurking rage against the woman who had so entrapped him and avenged herself was always there; crude and misshapen schemes of retaliation upon her, floated in his brain; but nothing was distinct.", "자기를 그토록 덫에 빠뜨리고 복수를 한 그 여인을 향한 도사린 분노가 늘 거기 있었고, 그녀에게 보복할 거칠고 일그러진 계략들이 그의 머릿속을 떠다녔으나, 그 무엇도 또렷하지 않았다."], ["A hurry and contradiction pervaded all his thoughts.", "다급함과 모순이 그의 모든 생각에 배어 있었다."], ["Even while he was so busy with this fevered, ineffectual thinking, his one constant idea was, that he would postpone reflection until some indefinite time.", "이렇게 열에 들떠 부질없는 생각에 그토록 골몰하는 와중에도, 그의 한결같은 단 하나의 생각은, 어떤 막연한 때까지 사색을 미루겠다는 것이었다."]], [["Then, the old days before the second marriage rose up in his remembrance.", "그러다, 두 번째 결혼 이전의 옛 시절이 그의 기억 속에 떠올랐다. 그는 자기가 그 사내아이를 얼마나 질투했는지, 그 계집아이를 얼마나 질투했는지, 얼마나 교묘하게 침입자들을 멀찍이 떨어뜨려 놓고, 자기 말고는 아무도 넘지 못할 원을 자기 봉 둘레에 그어 놓았는지를 떠올렸다."], ["He thought how jealous he had been of the boy, how jealous he had been of the girl, how artfully he had kept intruders at a distance, and drawn a circle round his dupe that none but himself should cross; and then he thought, had he done all this to be flying now, like a scared thief, from only the poor dupe?", "그러고는 생각했다. 이 모든 짓을 한 것이, 고작 그 가엾은 봉 하나로부터, 겁먹은 도둑처럼 지금 달아나기 위함이었던가?"]], [["He could have laid hands upon himself for his cowardice, but it was the very shadow of his defeat, and could not be separated from it.", "그는 자기 비겁함을 두고 자기 자신에게 손찌검이라도 하고 싶었으나, 그것이야말로 그의 패배의 바로 그 그림자여서, 그것과 떼어 낼 수가 없었다."], ["To have his confidence in his own knavery so shattered at a blow—to be within his own knowledge such a miserable tool—was like being paralysed.", "자기 자신의 간계에 대한 확신이 단 한 방에 그토록 산산이 부서진 것—자기 자신이 알기에도 그토록 비참한 도구였다는 것—은 마치 마비된 것과도 같았다."], ["With an impotent ferocity he raged at Edith, and hated Mr Dombey and hated himself, but still he fled, and could do nothing else.", "그는 무력한 사나움으로 이디스에게 격노했고, 돔비 씨를 미워했고, 자기 자신을 미워했으나, 그래도 여전히 달아나고 있었고, 그 밖에 달리 아무것도 할 수가 없었다."]], [["Again and again he listened for the sound of wheels behind.", "거듭거듭 그는 뒤에서 바퀴 소리가 나는지 귀를 기울였다."], ["Again and again his fancy heard it, coming on louder and louder.", "거듭거듭 그의 환상은 그 소리가, 점점 더 크게 다가오는 것을 들었다."], ["At last he was so persuaded of this, that he cried out, “Stop”", "마침내 그는 이를 어찌나 확신했던지, 차라리 거리를 잃는 한이 있더라도 그런 불확실함보다는 낫다고 여겨, \"멈춰!\""], ["preferring even the loss of ground to such uncertainty.", "하고 외쳤다."]], [["The word soon brought carriage, horses, driver, all in a heap together, across the road.", "그 말에 곧 마차와 말들과 마부가, 한꺼번에 뒤엉켜 길을 가로질러 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“The devil!”", "\"제기랄!\""], ["cried the driver, looking over his shoulder, “what’s the matter?”", "마부가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 외쳤다, \"무슨 일이오?\""]], [["“Hark!", "\"쉿!"], ["What’s that?”", "저게 뭐요?\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐가요?\""]], [["“That noise?”", "\"저 소리?\""]], [["“Ah Heaven, be quiet, cursed brigand!”", "\"아, 맙소사, 조용히 해, 이 빌어먹을 도둑놈아!\""], ["to a horse who shook his bells “What noise?”", "방울을 흔드는 말 한 마리를 향해 그가 말했다. \"무슨 소리 말이오?\""]], [["“Behind.", "\"뒤에서."], ["Is it not another carriage at a gallop?", "또 다른 마차가 전속력으로 달려오는 게 아니오?"], ["There!", "저기!"], ["what’s that?”", "저게 뭐요?\""]], [["“Miscreant with a Pig’s head, stand still!”", "\"이 돼지대가리 악당아, 가만있어!\""], ["to another horse, who bit another, who frightened the other two, who plunged and backed.", "다른 말 한 마리를 향해 외쳤는데, 그놈이 또 다른 말을 물어, 그놈이 나머지 두 마리를 놀라게 해, 그것들이 날뛰며 뒷걸음질 쳤다."], ["“There is nothing coming.”", "\"오는 건 아무것도 없소.\""]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"아무것도.\""]], [["“No, nothing but the day yonder.”", "\"네, 아무것도 없습니다, 저기 동트는 것 말고는.\""]], [["“You are right, I think.", "\"자네 말이 맞는 것 같군."], ["I hear nothing now, indeed.", "정말이지 이제 아무 소리도 안 들려."], ["Go on!”", "가세!\""]], [["The entangled equipage, half hidden in the reeking cloud from the horses, goes on slowly at first, for the driver, checked unnecessarily in his progress, sulkily takes out a pocket-knife, and puts a new lash to his whip.", "뒤엉킨 마차는, 말들에게서 피어오르는 김 구름에 반쯤 가려진 채, 처음에는 천천히 나아갔다. 가는 길에 공연히 제지당한 마부가 뚱한 얼굴로 주머니칼을 꺼내, 채찍에 새 가죽끈을 매단 까닭이었다."], ["Then “Hallo, whoop!", "그러더니 \"이랴, 워!"], ["Hallo, hi!”", "이랴, 히!\""], ["Away once more, savagely.", "다시 한번, 사납게 내달렸다."]], [["And now the stars faded, and the day glimmered, and standing in the carriage, looking back, he could discern the track by which he had come, and see that there was no traveller within view, on all the heavy expanse.", "그리고 이제 별들이 흐려지고, 날이 어슴푸레 밝아 오자, 그는 마차 안에 서서 뒤를 돌아보며, 자기가 지나온 길을 알아볼 수 있었고, 그 묵직하게 펼쳐진 평원 전체에 시야에 들어오는 나그네가 하나도 없음을 볼 수 있었다."], ["And soon it was broad day, and the sun began to shine on cornfields and vineyards; and solitary labourers, risen from little temporary huts by heaps of stones upon the road, were, here and there, at work repairing the highway, or eating bread.", "곧 환한 대낮이 되었고, 해가 밀밭과 포도밭을 비추기 시작했다. 길가의 돌무더기 옆 작은 임시 오두막에서 일어난 외로운 일꾼들이, 여기저기서, 큰길을 보수하거나 빵을 먹으며 일하고 있었다."], ["By and by, there were peasants going to their daily labour, or to market, or lounging at the doors of poor cottages, gazing idly at him as he passed.", "이윽고, 하루의 노동이나 장터로 가는 농부들이 있었고, 가난한 오두막 문가에서 빈둥거리며 그가 지나가는 것을 멀거니 바라보는 이들도 있었다."], ["And then there was a postyard, ankle-deep in mud, with steaming dunghills and vast outhouses half ruined; and looking on this dainty prospect, an immense, old, shadeless, glaring, stone chateau, with half its windows blinded, and green damp crawling lazily over it, from the balustraded terrace to the taper tips of the extinguishers upon the turrets.", "그러고 나서는 발목까지 진창에 잠긴 역참 마당이 나왔는데, 김을 내뿜는 두엄 더미와 반쯤 허물어진 거대한 헛간들이 있었다. 그리고 이 우아한 광경을 굽어보는, 창문이 반쯤 가려지고, 푸른 곰팡이가 난간 두른 테라스에서부터 작은 탑들 위 촛불 끄개 모양의 가느다란 꼭대기에 이르기까지 게으르게 기어오른, 거대하고 오래된, 그늘 한 점 없이 휑하니 번쩍이는 석조 성관이 있었다."]], [["Gathered up moodily in a corner of the carriage, and only intent on going fast—except when he stood up, for a mile together, and looked back; which he would do whenever there was a piece of open country—he went on, still postponing thought indefinitely, and still always tormented with thinking to no purpose.", "마차 한구석에 침울하게 웅크린 채, 오로지 빨리 가는 데만 정신이 팔려—탁 트인 들판이 나올 때면 언제든, 일 마일을 내처 일어서서 뒤를 돌아보았는데, 그럴 때만은 예외였다—그는 여전히 사색을 막연히 미루며, 여전히 부질없는 생각에 늘 시달리며, 계속 나아갔다."]], [["Shame, disappointment, and discomfiture gnawed at his heart; a constant apprehension of being overtaken, or met—for he was groundlessly afraid even of travellers, who came towards him by the way he was going—oppressed him heavily.", "수치와 실망과 낭패가 그의 가슴을 갉아먹었다. 따라잡히거나 마주칠지 모른다는 끊임없는 불안이—그가 가고 있는 길을 따라 마주 오는 나그네들에게조차 까닭 없이 겁을 먹었으므로—그를 무겁게 짓눌렀다."], ["The same intolerable awe and dread that had come upon him in the night, returned unweakened in the day.", "밤중에 그를 덮쳐 왔던 그 견디기 힘든 두려움과 공포가, 낮에도 조금도 약해지지 않은 채 되돌아왔다."], ["The monotonous ringing of the bells and tramping of the horses; the monotony of his anxiety, and useless rage; the monotonous wheel of fear, regret, and passion, he kept turning round and round; made the journey like a vision, in which nothing was quite real but his own torment.", "단조로운 방울 소리와 말발굽 소리, 그의 불안과 부질없는 분노의 단조로움, 그가 빙글빙글 돌리고 또 돌린 공포와 후회와 격정의 단조로운 수레바퀴—이것들이 그 여정을, 자기 자신의 고통 말고는 무엇 하나 도무지 현실 같지 않은 하나의 환영처럼 만들어 버렸다."]], [["It was a vision of long roads, that stretched away to an horizon, always receding and never gained; of ill-paved towns, up hill and down, where faces came to dark doors and ill-glazed windows, and where rows of mudbespattered cows and oxen were tied up for sale in the long narrow streets, butting and lowing, and receiving blows on their blunt heads from bludgeons that might have beaten them in; of bridges, crosses, churches, postyards, new horses being put in against their wills, and the horses of the last stage reeking, panting, and laying their drooping heads together dolefully at stable doors; of little cemeteries with black crosses settled sideways in the graves, and withered wreaths upon them dropping away; again of long, long roads, dragging themselves out, up hill and down, to the treacherous horizon.", "그것은 이런 환영이었다. 지평선을 향해 멀리 뻗어 나가, 늘 물러나기만 할 뿐 결코 닿지 못하는 긴 길들의 환영, 오르막과 내리막으로 이어진 포장이 형편없는 읍내들—그곳에서는 얼굴들이 어두운 문간과 흐릿한 창문으로 다가왔고, 진흙투성이 암소와 황소들이 줄지어 좁고 긴 거리에 팔리려고 매여 있었는데, 들이받고 음매 울며, 그 무딘 머리에 들이박을 만한 곤봉으로 후려치는 매를 맞고 있었다—다리들, 십자가들, 교회들, 역참 마당들, 제 뜻을 거슬러 새로 매여지는 말들, 김을 내뿜고 헐떡이며, 마구간 문가에서 축 늘어진 머리를 처량하게 한데 모으는 지난 구간의 말들, 검은 십자가들이 무덤 속에 비스듬히 박히고, 그 위의 시든 화환들이 떨어져 내리는 작은 묘지들의 환영, 그리고 다시, 오르막과 내리막으로, 저 미덥지 못한 지평선을 향해 제 몸을 끌어가는 길고 긴 길들의 환영."]], [["Of morning, noon, and sunset; night, and the rising of an early moon. Of long roads temporarily left behind, and a rough pavement reached; of battering and clattering over it, and looking up, among house-roofs, at a great church-tower; of getting out and eating hastily, and drinking draughts of wine that had no cheering influence; of coming forth afoot, among a host of beggars—blind men with quivering eyelids, led by old women holding candles to their faces; idiot girls; the lame, the epileptic, and the palsied—of passing through the clamour, and looking from his seat at the upturned countenances and outstretched hands, with a hurried dread of recognising some pursuer pressing forward—of galloping away again, upon the long, long road, gathered up, dull and stunned, in his corner, or rising to see where the moon shone faintly on a patch of the same endless road miles away, or looking back to see who followed.", "아침과 정오와 해 질 녘의, 밤과 일찍 떠오르는 달의 환영. 긴 길들을 잠시 뒤로하고 거친 포장도로에 다다른 것의, 그 위를 덜컹덜컹 부딪치며 달리다가, 집 지붕들 사이로 우뚝 솟은 큰 교회 탑을 올려다본 것의 환영. 마차에서 내려 허겁지겁 음식을 먹고, 아무런 기운도 북돋워 주지 못하는 포도주를 들이켠 것의 환영. 걸어 나오니 거지 떼 한가운데였던 것의 환영—눈꺼풀을 떨어 대는 맹인들을, 그 얼굴에 촛불을 들이대는 노파들이 이끌고 있었고, 백치 소녀들, 절름발이들, 간질 환자들, 중풍 환자들이 있었다—그 아우성 속을 지나가며, 어떤 추격자가 앞으로 밀고 들어오는 것을 알아볼까 다급한 두려움을 안고, 자기 자리에서 치켜든 얼굴들과 내뻗은 손들을 내려다본 것의 환영. 길고 긴 길 위로 다시 전속력으로 달려가며, 멍하고 얼이 빠진 채 한구석에 웅크리거나, 몸을 일으켜 몇 마일 떨어진 곳에서 그 끝없는 길의 한 자락 위에 달이 희미하게 비치는 곳을 바라보거나, 누가 뒤따라오는지 보려고 뒤를 돌아본 것의 환영."]], [["Of never sleeping, but sometimes dozing with unclosed eyes, and springing up with a start, and a reply aloud to an imaginary voice.", "결코 잠들지 못한 것의, 그러나 때로는 눈을 감지 못한 채 꾸벅꾸벅 졸다가, 깜짝 놀라 벌떡 일어나, 상상 속 목소리에 소리 내어 대꾸한 것의 환영."], ["Of cursing himself for being there, for having fled, for having let her go, for not having confronted and defied him.", "자기가 거기 있는 것을, 달아난 것을, 그녀를 놓아 보낸 것을, 그와 맞서 대들지 않은 것을 두고 자기 자신을 저주한 것의 환영."], ["Of having a deadly quarrel with the whole world, but chiefly with himself.", "온 세상과, 그러나 무엇보다 자기 자신과 죽기 살기로 다툰 것의 환영."], ["Of blighting everything with his black mood as he was carried on and away.", "실려 가는 동안 자기 검은 심사로 모든 것을 시들게 한 것의 환영."]], [["It was a fevered vision of things past and present all confounded together; of his life and journey blended into one.", "그것은 과거와 현재의 일들이 모두 한데 뒤섞인, 열에 들뜬 환영이었다. 자기 인생과 여정이 하나로 뒤섞인 것의 환영."], ["Of being madly hurried somewhere, whither he must go.", "가야만 하는 어딘가로 미친 듯이 떠밀려 가는 것의 환영."], ["Of old scenes starting up among the novelties through which he travelled.", "그가 지나가는 새로운 것들 사이로 옛 장면들이 불쑥 솟아오르는 것의 환영."], ["Of musing and brooding over what was past and distant, and seeming to take no notice of the actual objects he encountered, but with a wearisome exhausting consciousness of being bewildered by them, and having their images all crowded in his hot brain after they were gone.", "지나간 일과 멀리 있는 일을 곱씹고 곰곰이 생각하며, 자기가 마주치는 실제 사물들에는 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않는 듯하면서도, 그것들에 어리둥절해하고 있다는, 그리고 그것들이 사라진 뒤에도 그 영상들이 모조리 자기 달아오른 머릿속에 빽빽이 들어차 있다는, 지치고 진을 빼는 의식에 사로잡혀 있는 것의 환영."]], [["A vision of change upon change, and still the same monotony of bells and wheels, and horses’ feet, and no rest.", "변화에 변화를 거듭하되, 여전히 그 똑같은 단조로운 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리, 그리고 휴식 없음의 환영."], ["Of town and country, postyards, horses, drivers, hill and valley, light and darkness, road and pavement, height and hollow, wet weather and dry, and still the same monotony of bells and wheels, and horses’ feet, and no rest.", "읍내와 시골, 역참 마당들, 말들, 마부들, 언덕과 골짜기, 빛과 어둠, 흙길과 포장길, 높은 곳과 움푹한 곳, 궂은 날씨와 갠 날씨, 그리고 여전히 그 똑같은 단조로운 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리, 그리고 휴식 없음의 환영."], ["A vision of tending on at last, towards the distant capital, by busier roads, and sweeping round, by old cathedrals, and dashing through small towns and villages, less thinly scattered on the road than formerly, and sitting shrouded in his corner, with his cloak up to his face, as people passing by looked at him.", "마침내 더 분주한 길들을 따라, 저 멀리 수도를 향해 나아가며, 오래된 대성당들을 돌아 휙 지나고, 예전보다 길 위에 덜 드문드문 흩어진 작은 읍내와 마을들을 쏜살같이 통과하며, 지나가는 사람들이 자기를 쳐다보는 가운데 망토를 얼굴까지 끌어올린 채 한구석에 가려 앉아 있는 것의 환영."]], [["Of rolling on and on, always postponing thought, and always racked with thinking; of being unable to reckon up the hours he had been upon the road, or to comprehend the points of time and place in his journey.", "자꾸자꾸 굴러가며, 늘 사색을 미루면서도 늘 생각에 시달린 것의 환영. 자기가 길 위에 있은 시간을 셈할 수도, 자기 여정의 시각과 장소의 지점들을 헤아릴 수도 없었던 것의 환영."], ["Of being parched and giddy, and half mad.", "목이 타고 어지러우며, 반쯤 미쳐 있는 것의 환영."], ["Of pressing on, in spite of all, as if he could not stop, and coming into Paris, where the turbid river held its swift course undisturbed, between two brawling streams of life and motion.", "이 모든 것에도 불구하고, 마치 멈출 수 없기라도 한 듯 앞으로 밀고 나아가, 파리로 들어선 것의 환영—그곳에서는 탁한 강이, 떠들썩한 두 줄기 삶과 움직임의 흐름 사이로, 거침없이 제 빠른 물길을 이어 가고 있었다."]], [["A troubled vision, then, of bridges, quays, interminable streets; of wine-shops, water-carriers, great crowds of people, soldiers, coaches, military drums, arcades.", "그러고는, 다리들과 부두들과 끝없이 이어지는 거리들의 어수선한 환영. 술집들, 물장수들, 거대한 인파, 군인들, 마차들, 군대의 북소리, 아케이드들의 환영."], ["Of the monotony of bells and wheels and horses’ feet being at length lost in the universal din and uproar.", "방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리의 단조로움이 마침내 천지를 뒤덮은 소음과 소란 속에 묻혀 버린 것의 환영."], ["Of the gradual subsidence of that noise as he passed out in another carriage by a different barrier from that by which he had entered.", "그가 들어왔던 관문과는 다른 관문을 통해 또 다른 마차를 타고 빠져나가면서 그 소음이 차츰 잦아든 것의 환영."], ["Of the restoration, as he travelled on towards the seacoast, of the monotony of bells and wheels, and horses’ feet, and no rest.", "바닷가를 향해 나아갈수록, 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리의 단조로움과, 휴식 없음이 되살아난 것의 환영."]], [["Of sunset once again, and nightfall.", "다시 한번 해 질 녘과, 밤이 내린 것의 환영."], ["Of long roads again, and dead of night, and feeble lights in windows by the roadside; and still the old monotony of bells and wheels, and horses’ feet, and no rest.", "다시 긴 길들과, 깊은 밤과, 길가 창문들의 희미한 불빛들의 환영. 그리고 여전히 그 옛 단조로운 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리, 그리고 휴식 없음의 환영."], ["Of dawn, and daybreak, and the rising of the sun.", "새벽과, 동틀 녘과, 해돋이의 환영."], ["Of tolling slowly up a hill, and feeling on its top the fresh sea-breeze; and seeing the morning light upon the edges of the distant waves.", "언덕을 천천히 올라, 그 꼭대기에서 상쾌한 바닷바람을 느끼고, 멀리 파도의 가장자리에 아침 햇살이 비치는 것을 본 것의 환영."], ["Of coming down into a harbour when the tide was at its full, and seeing fishing-boats float on, and glad women and children waiting for them.", "밀물이 가득 찼을 때 항구로 내려가, 고기잡이배들이 떠가는 것과, 그것들을 기다리는 기쁨에 찬 아낙들과 아이들을 본 것의 환영. 물가에 널려 말려지는 그물들과 뱃사람들의 옷가지의 환영."], ["Of nets and seamen’s clothes spread out to dry upon the shore; of busy sailors, and their voices high among ships’ masts and rigging; of the buoyancy and brightness of the water, and the universal sparkling.", "분주한 선원들과, 돛대와 삭구 사이로 높이 울리는 그들의 목소리의 환영. 물의 출렁임과 환함과, 천지에 가득한 반짝임의 환영."]], [["Of receding from the coast, and looking back upon it from the deck when it was a haze upon the water, with here and there a little opening of bright land where the Sun struck.", "해안에서 멀어지며, 그곳이 물 위의 안개가 되었을 때, 갑판에서 그곳을 돌아본 것의 환영—해가 내리쬐는 곳마다 환한 땅뙈기가 여기저기 조금씩 트여 있었다."], ["Of the swell, and flash, and murmur of the calm sea.", "잔잔한 바다의 너울과 번뜩임과 속삭임의 환영."], ["Of another grey line on the ocean, on the vessel’s track, fast growing clearer and higher.", "배의 항로 위, 대양에 또 다른 잿빛 선이, 빠르게 더 또렷하고 더 높이 자라나는 것의 환영."], ["Of cliffs and buildings, and a windmill, and a church, becoming more and more visible upon it.", "그 위로 절벽들과 건물들과, 풍차 하나와, 교회 하나가, 점점 더 또렷이 보이게 되는 것의 환영."], ["Of steaming on at last into smooth water, and mooring to a pier whence groups of people looked down, greeting friends on board.", "마침내 잔잔한 물속으로 김을 내뿜으며 들어가, 사람들이 무리 지어 내려다보며 배에 탄 벗들에게 인사를 건네는 부두에 정박한 것의 환영."], ["Of disembarking, passing among them quickly, shunning every one; and of being at last again in England.", "배에서 내려, 그들 사이를 빠르게 지나, 누구든 피하면서, 마침내 다시 영국에 와 있는 것의 환영."]], [["He had thought, in his dream, of going down into a remote country-place he knew, and lying quiet there, while he secretly informed himself of what transpired, and determined how to act, Still in the same stunned condition, he remembered a certain station on the railway, where he would have to branch off to his place of destination, and where there was a quiet Inn.", "그는 꿈결에, 자기가 아는 외딴 시골 마을로 내려가, 그곳에서 조용히 누워 지내면서, 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 남몰래 알아보고, 어떻게 행동할지 결정하리라 생각했었다. 여전히 그 똑같이 얼이 빠진 상태에서, 그는 철도의 어느 역을 떠올렸다."], ["Here, he indistinctly resolved to tarry and rest.", "거기서 자기 목적지로 갈라져 들어가야 했고, 거기에 조용한 여관이 하나 있었다. 그는 여기서 머물며 쉬리라고 어렴풋이 마음먹었다."]], [["With this purpose he slunk into a railway carriage as quickly as he could, and lying there wrapped in his cloak as if he were asleep, was soon borne far away from the sea, and deep into the inland green.", "이 작정을 품고, 그는 되도록 빨리 철도 객차 안으로 슬그머니 들어가, 거기 망토에 싸인 채 마치 잠든 듯 누워, 이내 바다에서 멀리 떨어져 내륙의 푸르름 깊숙이 실려 갔다."], ["Arrived at his destination he looked out, and surveyed it carefully.", "목적지에 다다르자 그는 밖을 내다보며, 그곳을 주의 깊게 살폈다."], ["He was not mistaken in his impression of the place.", "그곳에 대한 그의 인상은 틀리지 않았다."], ["It was a retired spot, on the borders of a little wood.", "작은 숲 가장자리에 자리한 외딴곳이었다."], ["Only one house, newly-built or altered for the purpose, stood there, surrounded by its neat garden; the small town that was nearest, was some miles away.", "이 목적에 맞게 새로 지었거나 개조한 집 단 한 채가, 산뜻한 정원에 둘러싸여 거기 서 있었다. 가장 가까운 작은 읍내는 몇 마일 떨어져 있었다."], ["Here he alighted then; and going straight into the tavern, unobserved by anyone, secured two rooms upstairs communicating with each other, and sufficiently retired.", "그렇게 해서 그는 여기서 내렸다. 그러고는 아무에게도 들키지 않고 곧장 여관 안으로 들어가, 서로 통하는, 충분히 외진 위층 방 두 개를 잡았다."]], [["His object was to rest, and recover the command of himself, and the balance of his mind.", "그의 목적은 쉬는 것, 그리고 자기 자신에 대한 통제와 마음의 평정을 되찾는 것이었다."], ["Imbecile discomfiture and rage—so that, as he walked about his room, he ground his teeth—had complete possession of him.", "얼빠진 낭패감과 분노가—그래서 방 안을 거닐 때면 그는 이를 갈았다—그를 완전히 사로잡고 있었다."], ["His thoughts, not to be stopped or directed, still wandered where they would, and dragged him after them.", "멈추게 할 수도 방향을 잡아 줄 수도 없는 그의 생각은, 여전히 제멋대로 떠돌며, 그를 그 뒤로 질질 끌고 다녔다."], ["He was stupefied, and he was wearied to death.", "그는 망연자실했고, 죽도록 지쳐 있었다."]], [["But, as if there were a curse upon him that he should never rest again, his drowsy senses would not lose their consciousness.", "그러나, 그가 다시는 결코 쉬어서는 안 된다는 저주라도 받은 듯, 졸음에 겨운 그의 감각은 의식을 놓아주지 않았다."], ["He had no more influence with them, in this regard, than if they had been another man’s.", "이 점에 관한 한, 그는 그것들에 대해, 마치 그것들이 다른 사람의 것이기라도 한 양, 아무런 영향력도 행사하지 못했다."], ["It was not that they forced him to take note of present sounds and objects, but that they would not be diverted from the whole hurried vision of his journey.", "그것들이 그로 하여금 현재의 소리와 사물에 주목하게끔 강요한 것은 아니었으나, 그의 여정의 그 온통 다급한 환영에서 떠나려 하지 않았다. 그것은 끊임없이 그의 눈앞에 한꺼번에 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["It was constantly before him all at once.", "그녀가 거기 서서, 그 어둡고 경멸 어린 눈으로 다시 그를 노려보았다."], ["She stood there, with her dark disdainful eyes again upon him; and he was riding on nevertheless, through town and country, light and darkness, wet weather and dry, over road and pavement, hill and valley, height and hollow, jaded and scared by the monotony of bells and wheels, and horses’ feet, and no rest.", "그런데도 그는 여전히, 읍내와 시골을, 빛과 어둠을, 궂은 날씨와 갠 날씨를, 흙길과 포장길을, 언덕과 골짜기를, 높은 곳과 움푹한 곳을 가로질러 달려가고 있었다. 단조로운 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리, 그리고 휴식 없음에 지치고 겁먹은 채로."]], [["“What day is this?”", "\"오늘이 무슨 요일인가?\""], ["he asked of the waiter, who was making preparations for his dinner.", "그가 저녁 식사 준비를 하고 있는 종업원에게 물었다. \"요일 말씀이십니까, 손님?\""]], [["“Day, Sir?”", "\"수요일인가?\""]], [["“Is it Wednesday?”", "\"수요일이요, 손님?"], ["“Wednesday, Sir?", "아닙니다, 손님."], ["No, Sir. Thursday, Sir.”", "목요일입니다, 손님.\""]], [["“I forgot.", "\"내가 잊었군."], ["How goes the time?", "시각은 어떻게 되나?"], ["My watch is unwound.”", "내 시계가 안 감겨 있어서.\""]], [["“Wants a few minutes of five o’clock, Sir.", "\"다섯 시 몇 분 전입니다, 손님."], ["Been travelling a long time, Sir, perhaps?”", "오랫동안 여행하셨나 봅니다, 손님?\" \"그렇다네.\""]], [["“By rail, Sir?” “Yes”", "\"기차로요, 손님?\""]], [["“Very confusing, Sir.", "\"그렇다네.\""], ["Not much in the habit of travelling by rail myself, Sir, but gentlemen frequently say so.”", "\"무척 정신이 어지러우시지요, 손님. 저야 기차로 여행하는 일이 그리 익숙지 않습니다만, 손님, 신사분들이 흔히들 그렇게 말씀하시더군요.\""]], [["“Do many gentlemen come here?", "\"이곳에 신사들이 많이 오나?\""]], [["“Pretty well, Sir, in general.", "\"대체로 그런대로 옵니다, 손님."], ["Nobody here at present.", "지금은 아무도 안 계십니다."], ["Rather slack just now, Sir.", "요즘은 좀 한산하지요, 손님."], ["Everything is slack, Sir.”", "모든 게 한산합니다, 손님.\""]], [["He made no answer; but had risen into a sitting posture on the sofa where he had been lying, and leaned forward with an arm on each knee, staring at the ground.", "그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그가 누워 있던 소파 위에 일어나 앉아, 두 팔을 각각 무릎에 얹고 앞으로 몸을 숙인 채, 바닥을 응시하고 있었다."], ["He could not master his own attention for a minute together.", "그는 단 일 분도 제 주의력을 다스릴 수가 없었다."], ["It rushed away where it would, but it never, for an instant, lost itself in sleep.", "그것은 제멋대로 내달았으나, 단 한순간도 잠 속에 빠져들지는 않았다."]], [["He drank a quantity of wine after dinner, in vain.", "그는 저녁 식사 후 포도주를 잔뜩 마셨으나, 헛일이었다."], ["No such artificial means would bring sleep to his eyes.", "그런 인위적인 수단으로는 그의 눈에 잠이 찾아들지 않았다."], ["His thoughts, more incoherent, dragged him more unmercifully after them—as if a wretch, condemned to such expiation, were drawn at the heels of wild horses.", "한층 더 갈피를 잡지 못하게 된 그의 생각은, 그를 한층 더 무자비하게 그 뒤로 끌고 다녔다—마치 그런 속죄형을 선고받은 가엾은 자가, 야생마들의 발꿈치에 매달려 끌려가기라도 하는 것처럼."], ["No oblivion, and no rest.", "망각도 없었고, 휴식도 없었다."]], [["How long he sat, drinking and brooding, and being dragged in imagination hither and thither, no one could have told less correctly than he.", "마시고 곰곰이 생각하며, 상상 속에서 이리저리 끌려다니면서 얼마나 오래 앉아 있었는지는, 그 자신보다 더 정확히 말할 수 있는 이가 없었을 것이다."], ["But he knew that he had been sitting a long time by candle-light, when he started up and listened, in a sudden terror.", "그러나 그가 갑작스러운 공포에 벌떡 일어나 귀를 기울였을 때, 자기가 촛불 곁에 오랫동안 앉아 있었다는 것만은 알고 있었다."]], [["For now, indeed, it was no fancy.", "이제는, 정말로, 환상이 아니었다."], ["The ground shook, the house rattled, the fierce impetuous rush was in the air!", "땅이 흔들렸고, 집이 덜컹거렸으며, 그 사납고 거센 돌진이 공중에 있었다!"], ["He felt it come up, and go darting by; and even when he had hurried to the window, and saw what it was, he stood, shrinking from it, as if it were not safe to look.", "그는 그것이 다가왔다가, 휙 쏜살같이 지나가는 것을 느꼈다. 창가로 황급히 달려가 그것이 무엇인지 보았을 때조차, 그는, 마치 바라보는 것이 안전치 않기라도 한 듯, 그것에서 움츠러든 채 서 있었다."]], [["A curse upon the fiery devil, thundering along so smoothly, tracked through the distant valley by a glare of light and lurid smoke, and gone!", "그토록 매끄럽게 우레처럼 내달려, 멀리 골짜기를 가로지르며 한 줄기 섬광과 음산한 연기로 자취를 남기고는, 가 버린 그 불의 악마에게 저주가 있기를!"], ["He felt as if he had been plucked out of its path, and saved from being torn asunder.", "그는 마치 자기가 그 진로에서 뽑혀 나와, 갈가리 찢기는 것을 면하기라도 한 듯한 기분이 들었다."], ["It made him shrink and shudder even now, when its faintest hum was hushed, and when the lines of iron road he could trace in the moonlight, running to a point, were as empty and as silent as a desert.", "그것의 가장 희미한 웅웅거림조차 잦아들고, 달빛 속에 한 점을 향해 뻗어 가는 것을 알아볼 수 있는 그 철길이 사막처럼 텅 비고 고요해진 지금까지도, 그것은 그를 움츠러들고 몸서리치게 만들었다."]], [["Unable to rest, and irresistibly attracted—or he thought so—to this road, he went out, and lounged on the brink of it, marking the way the train had gone, by the yet smoking cinders that were lying in its track.", "쉴 수가 없는 데다, 저항할 수 없이—혹은 그가 그렇게 여기기에—이 철길에 이끌려, 그는 밖으로 나가, 그 가장자리를 어슬렁거리며, 기차가 지나간 자취 위에 아직 김을 내뿜으며 놓여 있는 불씨들로 그것이 간 방향을 짚어 보았다."], ["After a lounge of some half hour in the direction by which it had disappeared, he turned and walked the other way—still keeping to the brink of the road—past the inn garden, and a long way down; looking curiously at the bridges, signals, lamps, and wondering when another Devil would come by.", "그것이 사라져 간 방향으로 반시간쯤 어슬렁거린 뒤, 그는 돌아서서 반대편으로 걸었다—여전히 철길 가장자리를 따라서—여관 정원을 지나, 한참을 내려가며, 다리들과 신호기들과 등불들을 신기한 듯 바라보고, 또 다른 악마가 언제 지나갈까 궁금해했다."]], [["A trembling of the ground, and quick vibration in his ears; a distant shriek; a dull light advancing, quickly changed to two red eyes, and a fierce fire, dropping glowing coals; an irresistible bearing on of a great roaring and dilating mass; a high wind, and a rattle—another come and gone, and he holding to a gate, as if to save himself!", "땅의 떨림과, 그의 귓속의 빠른 진동, 멀리서 들리는 날카로운 비명 소리, 빠르게 두 개의 붉은 눈으로 바뀌는 다가오는 희미한 빛, 그리고 이글거리는 석탄을 떨어뜨리는 사나운 불길, 으르렁대며 부풀어 오르는 거대한 덩어리가 저항할 수 없이 밀어닥치는 것, 거센 바람과 덜컹거림—또 한 대가 왔다가 가 버렸고, 그는 마치 제 몸을 지탱하려는 듯 문 하나를 붙들고 있었다!"]], [["He waited for another, and for another.", "그는 또 한 대를, 그리고 또 한 대를 기다렸다."], ["He walked back to his former point, and back again to that, and still, through the wearisome vision of his journey, looked for these approaching monsters.", "그는 아까 있던 지점으로 도로 걸어갔다가, 다시 그곳으로 돌아오기를 거듭하며, 여전히, 자기 여정의 그 지긋지긋한 환영을 통해, 다가오는 이 괴물들을 기다렸다."], ["He loitered about the station, waiting until one should stay to call there; and when one did, and was detached for water, he stood parallel with it, watching its heavy wheels and brazen front, and thinking what a cruel power and might it had.", "그는 어떤 기차가 그곳에 멈춰 서기를 기다리며 역 주변을 서성였다. 그러다 한 대가 정말로 멈춰 서서, 물을 받으려고 떼어 놓이자, 그는 그것과 나란히 서서, 그 묵직한 바퀴들과 놋쇠 전면을 지켜보며, 그것이 얼마나 잔인한 힘과 위력을 지녔는지 생각했다."], ["Ugh!", "으윽!"], ["To see the great wheels slowly turning, and to think of being run down and crushed!", "그 거대한 바퀴들이 천천히 도는 것을 보며, 그 아래 깔려 짓이겨지는 것을 생각하다니!"]], [["Disordered with wine and want of rest—that want which nothing, although he was so weary, would appease—these ideas and objects assumed a diseased importance in his thoughts.", "포도주와, 그리고 휴식의 결핍으로—그토록 지쳤건만 무엇으로도 가실 줄 모르는 그 결핍으로—어수선해진 채, 이 생각들과 사물들은 그의 머릿속에서 병적인 중요성을 띠어 갔다."], ["When he went back to his room, which was not until near midnight, they still haunted him, and he sat listening for the coming of another.", "자정이 다 되어서야 비로소 자기 방으로 돌아갔을 때에도, 그것들은 여전히 그를 따라다녔고, 그는 또 한 대가 다가오기를 귀 기울이며 앉아 있었다."]], [["So in his bed, whither he repaired with no hope of sleep.", "잠은 바라지도 않고 찾아든 침대에서도 마찬가지였다."], ["He still lay listening; and when he felt the trembling and vibration, got up and went to the window, to watch (as he could from its position) the dull light changing to the two red eyes, and the fierce fire dropping glowing coals, and the rush of the giant as it fled past, and the track of glare and smoke along the valley.", "그는 여전히 누워 귀를 기울였다. 떨림과 진동이 느껴지면, 일어나 창가로 가서, (그 위치에서 볼 수 있는 한) 희미한 빛이 두 개의 붉은 눈으로 바뀌는 것과, 이글거리는 석탄을 떨어뜨리는 사나운 불길과, 휙 지나가며 달아나는 그 거인의 돌진과, 골짜기를 따라 남는 섬광과 연기의 자취를 지켜보았다."], ["Then he would glance in the direction by which he intended to depart at sunrise, as there was no rest for him there; and would lie down again, to be troubled by the vision of his journey, and the old monotony of bells and wheels and horses’ feet, until another came.", "그러고는, 거기에는 그에게 휴식이 없으므로, 해돋이에 떠날 작정인 방향을 흘끗 바라보았다. 그러고는 다시 누워, 또 한 대가 올 때까지, 자기 여정의 환영과, 그 옛 단조로운 방울 소리와 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리에 시달렸다."], ["This lasted all night.", "이것이 밤새도록 이어졌다."], ["So far from resuming the mastery of himself, he seemed, if possible, to lose it more and more, as the night crept on.", "자기 자신에 대한 통제를 되찾기는커녕, 밤이 깊어 갈수록, 가능하다면 그것을 점점 더 잃어 가는 것 같았다."], ["When the dawn appeared, he was still tormented with thinking, still postponing thought until he should be in a better state; the past, present, and future all floated confusedly before him, and he had lost all power of looking steadily at any one of them.", "새벽이 밝아 올 때에도, 그는 여전히 생각에 시달리며, 여전히 더 나은 상태가 될 때까지 사색을 미루고 있었다. 과거와 현재와 미래가 모두 그의 앞에 어수선하게 떠다녔고, 그는 그중 어느 하나라도 차분히 바라볼 힘을 모조리 잃어버린 터였다."]], [["“At what time,”", "\"몇 시에,\""], ["he asked the man who had waited on him over-night, now entering with a candle, “do I leave here, did you say?”", "그가, 이제 촛불을 들고 들어오는, 간밤에 자기 시중을 들었던 사내에게 물었다, \"내가 여기서 떠난다고 했지?\""]], [["“About a quarter after four, Sir.", "\"네 시 십오 분쯤입니다, 손님."], ["Express comes through at four, Sir.—It don’t stop.”", "급행이 네 시에 통과합니다, 손님.—그건 안 섭니다.\""]], [["He passed his hand across his throbbing head, and looked at his watch.", "그는 욱신거리는 머리를 손으로 쓸어내리며, 자기 시계를 보았다."], ["Nearly half-past three.", "거의 세 시 반이었다."]], [["“Nobody going with you, Sir, probably,”", "\"손님과 함께 가는 분은 아마 아무도 없을 겁니다, 손님,\""], ["observed the man.", "사내가 말했다."], ["“Two gentlemen here, Sir, but they’re waiting for the train to London.”", "\"여기 신사 두 분이 계십니다만, 손님, 그분들은 런던행 기차를 기다리고 계십니다.\""]], [["“I thought you said there was nobody here,”", "\"아무도 없다고 했던 것 같은데,\""], ["said Carker, turning upon him with the ghost of his old smile, when he was angry or suspicious.", "카커가, 화가 나거나 의심스러울 때 짓던 그 옛 미소의 유령을 띠고 그를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“Not then, sir.", "\"그때는 없었습니다, 손님."], ["Two gentlemen came in the night by the short train that stops here, Sir.", "두 신사분이 밤중에, 여기 서는 단거리 기차로 오셨습니다, 손님."], ["Warm water, Sir?”", "따뜻한 물 드릴까요, 손님?\""]], [["“No; and take away the candle.", "\"아니, 그리고 촛불은 가져가게."], ["There’s day enough for me.”", "나한테는 날이 충분히 밝았으니까.\""]], [["Having thrown himself upon the bed, half-dressed he was at the window as the man left the room.", "옷을 반쯤 입은 채 침대에 몸을 던졌던 그는, 사내가 방을 나설 때 창가에 서 있었다."], ["The cold light of morning had succeeded to night and there was already, in the sky, the red suffusion of the coming sun.", "밤에 뒤이어 차가운 아침빛이 찾아들었고, 하늘에는 이미, 다가오는 해의 붉은 기운이 번져 있었다."], ["He bathed his head and face with water—there was no cooling influence in it for him—hurriedly put on his clothes, paid what he owed, and went out.", "그는 물로 머리와 얼굴을 씻었으나—그에게는 그 안에 어떤 식혀 주는 기운도 없었다—허둥지둥 옷을 입고, 셈을 치르고는, 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["The air struck chill and comfortless as it breathed upon him.", "공기가 그에게 숨결을 불어 올 때 한기 어리고 스산하게 느껴졌다."], ["There was a heavy dew; and, hot as he was, it made him shiver.", "이슬이 잔뜩 내려 있었고, 그토록 달아오른 몸인데도, 그것이 그를 떨게 했다."], ["After a glance at the place where he had walked last night, and at the signal-lights burning in the morning, and bereft of their significance, he turned to where the sun was rising, and beheld it, in its glory, as it broke upon the scene.", "간밤에 자기가 거닐던 곳과, 아침이 되어 그 의미를 잃은 채 타오르는 신호등들을 흘끗 본 뒤, 그는 해가 떠오르는 쪽으로 몸을 돌려, 그것이 풍경 위로 솟구쳐 오를 때, 그 영광 가운데서 그것을 바라보았다."]], [["So awful, so transcendent in its beauty, so divinely solemn.", "그토록 경외롭고, 그 아름다움이 그토록 초월적이며, 그토록 신성하리만치 장엄했다."], ["As he cast his faded eyes upon it, where it rose, tranquil and serene, unmoved by all the wrong and wickedness on which its beams had shone since the beginning of the world, who shall say that some weak sense of virtue upon Earth, and its in Heaven, did not manifest itself, even to him?", "그것이, 세상이 시작된 이래 제 광선이 비추어 온 그 모든 그릇됨과 사악함에도 아랑곳없이, 고요하고 평온하게 솟아오르는 곳을 향해, 그가 그 빛바랜 두 눈을 던졌을 때, 지상의 미덕과 천상의 미덕에 대한 어떤 미약한 감각이, 그에게마저, 모습을 드러내지 않았다고 누가 말할 수 있으랴?"], ["If ever he remembered sister or brother with a touch of tenderness and remorse, who shall say it was not then?", "그가 일찍이 누이나 형제를 한 가닥 다정함과 회한으로 떠올린 적이 있다면, 그것이 바로 그때가 아니었다고 누가 말할 수 있으랴?"]], [["He needed some such touch then.", "그는 그때 그런 한 가닥 감정이 필요했다."], ["Death was on him.", "죽음이 그에게 닥쳐 있었다."], ["He was marked off—the living world, and going down into his grave.", "그는 살아 있는 세상에서 떼어져—무덤 속으로 내려가고 있었다."]], [["He paid the money for his journey to the country-place he had thought of; and was walking to and fro, alone, looking along the lines of iron, across the valley in one direction, and towards a dark bridge near at hand in the other; when, turning in his walk, where it was bounded by one end of the wooden stage on which he paced up and down, he saw the man from whom he had fled, emerging from the door by which he himself had entered.", "그는 자기가 생각해 둔 그 시골 마을까지 가는 여비를 치렀다. 그러고는 홀로 이리저리 거닐며, 한쪽으로는 골짜기를 가로질러 뻗은 철길을 따라, 다른 쪽으로는 가까이 있는 어두운 다리 쪽을 바라보고 있었다."], ["And their eyes met.", "그러다, 자기가 오락가락 거니는 그 나무 단의 한쪽 끝에 막혀 있는 곳에서 걸음을 돌릴 때, 그는 자기가 달아나 온 그 사내가, 자기가 들어왔던 바로 그 문에서 나오는 것을 보았다. 그리고 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다."]], [["In the quick unsteadiness of the surprise, he staggered, and slipped on to the road below him.", "놀라움의 그 빠르고 불안정한 동요 속에서, 그는 비틀거리다, 아래쪽 선로로 미끄러져 떨어졌다."], ["But recovering his feet immediately, he stepped back a pace or two upon that road, to interpose some wider space between them, and looked at his pursuer, breathing short and quick.", "그러나 곧바로 발을 가누고는, 그 선로 위에서 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러나, 둘 사이에 좀 더 넓은 간격을 두려고, 짧고 빠르게 숨을 몰아쉬며 자기 추격자를 바라보았다."]], [["He heard a shout—another—saw the face change from its vindictive passion to a faint sickness and terror—felt the earth tremble—knew in a moment that the rush was come—uttered a shriek—looked round—saw the red eyes, bleared and dim, in the daylight, close upon him—was beaten down, caught up, and whirled away upon a jagged mill, that spun him round and round, and struck him limb from limb, and licked his stream of life up with its fiery heat, and cast his mutilated fragments in the air.", "그는 한 차례 외침을 들었고—또 한 차례—그 얼굴이 복수에 찬 격정에서 희미한 메스꺼움과 공포로 바뀌는 것을 보았다—땅이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다—그 돌진이 닥쳤음을 한순간에 알아챘다—날카로운 비명을 질렀다—주위를 둘러보았다—대낮 속에 흐릿하게 침침해진 그 붉은 눈이 바로 코앞에 다가온 것을 보았다—두들겨 쓰러뜨려져, 채여 올려져, 들쭉날쭉한 맷돌 위로 휘말려, 그것이 그를 빙글빙글 돌리며, 사지를 갈가리 찢어 내고, 제 불같은 열기로 그의 생명의 물줄기를 핥아 들이고는, 그의 토막 난 살점들을 공중으로 내던졌다."]], [["When the traveller, who had been recognised, recovered from a swoon, he saw them bringing from a distance something covered, that lay heavy and still, upon a board, between four men, and saw that others drove some dogs away that sniffed upon the road, and soaked his blood up, with a train of ashes.", "얼굴을 알아본 적 있던 그 나그네가 혼절에서 깨어났을 때, 그는 멀리서 그들이 무언가 덮인 것을, 묵직하고 미동도 없이 널빤지 위에 놓인 것을, 네 사내 사이에 끼워 들고 오는 것을 보았다. 그리고 다른 이들이, 길 위에서 킁킁대며 그의 피를 한 줄기 재와 함께 빨아들이던 개 몇 마리를 쫓아 버리는 것을 보았다."]], [["# CHAPTER LVI. Several People delighted, and the Game Chicken disgusted", "# CHAPTER LVI. Several People delighted, and the Game Chicken disgusted"]], [["The Midshipman was all alive.", "해군 후보생은 온통 생기로 넘쳤다."], ["Mr Toots and Susan had arrived at last.", "투츠 씨와 수전이 마침내 도착한 것이다."], ["Susan had run upstairs like a young woman bereft of her senses, and Mr Toots and the Chicken had gone into the Parlour.", "수전은 정신이 나간 처녀처럼 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔고, 투츠 씨와 치킨은 응접실로 들어갔다."]], [["“Oh my own pretty darling sweet Miss Floy!”", "\"오, 내 예쁘고 사랑스럽고 다정한 플로이 아가씨!\""], ["cried the Nipper, running into Florence’s room, “to think that it should come to this and I should find you here my own dear dove with nobody to wait upon you and no home to call your own but never never will I go away again Miss Floy for though I may not gather moss I’m not a rolling stone nor is my heart a stone or else it wouldn’t bust as it is busting now oh dear oh dear!”", "니퍼가 플로렌스의 방으로 뛰어 들어오며 외쳤다, \"일이 이 지경에 이르다니, 시중들 사람 하나 없이, 내 집이라 부를 곳 하나 없이 여기 계시다니 내 소중한 비둘기를 이렇게 만나다니 하지만 다시는 절대로 절대로 떠나지 않을 거예요 플로이 아가씨, 제가 이끼를 모으지는 못할지언정 굴러다니는 돌멩이는 아니고 제 마음도 돌멩이는 아니니까요 그렇지 않으면 지금처럼 이렇게 터지지도 않았을 거예요 오 이런 오 이런!\""]], [["Pouring out these words, without the faintest indication of a stop, of any sort, Miss Nipper, on her knees beside her mistress, hugged her close.", "* 이끼를 모으지 못해도 굴러다니는 돌멩이는 아니다: \"구르는 돌에는 이끼가 끼지 않는다\"는 속담을 비튼 말. 이 말들을 단 한 군데도 멈춤 없이 쏟아 내면서, 니퍼 양은 여주인 곁에 무릎을 꿇은 채 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았다."]], [["“Oh love!”", "\"오 내 사랑!\""], ["cried Susan, “I know all that’s past I know it all my tender pet and I’m a choking give me air!”", "수전이 외쳤다, \"지난 일은 다 알아요 다 알아요 내 가여운 아가 숨이 막혀요 숨 좀 쉬게 해 줘요!\""]], [["“Susan, dear good Susan!”", "\"수전, 착하고 다정한 수전!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Oh bless her!", "\"오 아가씨께 축복을!"], ["I that was her little maid when she was a little child!", "아가씨가 어린아이였을 때 제가 그 어린 하녀였는데!"], ["and is she really, really truly going to be married?”", "그런데 정말로, 정말로 진짜로 결혼을 하시는 거예요?\""], ["exclaimed Susan, in a burst of pain and pleasure, pride and grief, and Heaven knows how many other conflicting feelings.", "수전이 고통과 기쁨, 자랑스러움과 슬픔, 그 밖에 하느님만 아실 갖가지 뒤엉킨 감정에 북받쳐 외쳤다."]], [["“Who told you so?”", "\"누가 그러던?\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."]], [["“Oh gracious me!", "\"오 이런 맙소사!"], ["that innocentest creetur Toots,”", "그 더없이 순진한 토츠 씨가요,\""], ["returned Susan hysterically.", "수전이 히스테릭하게 대답했다."], ["“I knew he must be right my dear, because he took on so.", "\"제가 그분 말이 틀림없다는 걸 알았어요 아가씨, 그분이 그토록 상심하셨으니까요."], ["He’s the devotedest and innocentest infant!", "그분은 세상에서 제일 헌신적이고 제일 순진한 아기 같은 분이세요!"], ["And is my darling,”", "그래서 우리 아가씨가,\""], ["pursued Susan, with another close embrace and burst of tears, “really really going to be married!”", "수전은 다시 한번 꼭 끌어안고 눈물을 터뜨리며 말을 이었다, \"정말로 정말로 결혼을 하시는 거예요!\""]], [["The mixture of compassion, pleasure, tenderness, protection, and regret with which the Nipper constantly recurred to this subject, and at every such once, raised her head to look in the young face and kiss it, and then laid her head again upon her mistress’s shoulder, caressing her and sobbing, was as womanly and good a thing, in its way, as ever was seen in the world.", "니퍼가 이 주제로 끊임없이 되돌아올 때마다 거기에 어린 연민과 기쁨, 다정함과 보호하려는 마음과 안타까움이 뒤섞여 있었고, 그럴 때마다 고개를 들어 그 젊은 얼굴을 들여다보고 입을 맞추고는 다시 여주인의 어깨에 머리를 기대며 그녀를 어루만지고 흐느꼈으니, 그 나름으로는 이 세상에서 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 여성스럽고 갸륵한 모습이었다."]], [["“There, there!”", "\"자, 자!\""], ["said the soothing voice of Florence presently.", "곧 플로렌스의 달래는 목소리가 들렸다."], ["“Now you’re quite yourself, dear Susan!”", "\"이제 완전히 정신을 차렸구나, 다정한 수전!\""]], [["Miss Nipper, sitting down upon the floor, at her mistress’s feet, laughing and sobbing, holding her pocket-handkerchief to her eyes with one hand, and patting Diogenes with the other as he licked her face, confessed to being more composed, and laughed and cried a little more in proof of it.", "니퍼 양은 여주인의 발치 바닥에 주저앉아 웃고 흐느끼며, 한 손으로는 손수건을 눈에 갖다 대고 다른 손으로는 자기 얼굴을 핥는 다이오지니즈를 쓰다듬으면서, 한결 진정되었다고 고백했다. 그러고는 그 증거랍시고 조금 더 웃고 울었다."]], [["“I-I-I never did see such a creetur as that Toots,”", "\"제, 제, 제 평생 그 토츠 씨 같은 분은 본 적이 없어요,\""], ["said Susan, “in all my born days never!”", "수전이 말했다, \"태어나서 단 한 번도요!\""]], [["“So kind,”", "\"무척 친절하시지,\""], ["suggested Florence.", "플로렌스가 거들었다."]], [["“And so comic!”", "\"게다가 어찌나 우스운지요!\""], ["Susan sobbed.", "수전이 흐느꼈다."], ["“The way he’s been going on inside with me with that disrespectable Chicken on the box!”", "\"그 무례한 치킨을 마부석에 앉혀 두고 마차 안에서 저한테 그렇게 떠들어 대시던 모습이라니!\""]], [["“About what, Susan?”", "\"무슨 얘기를, 수전?\""], ["inquired Florence, timidly.", "플로렌스가 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["“Oh about Lieutenant Walters, and Captain Gills, and you my dear Miss Floy, and the silent tomb,”", "\"오 월터스 중위님이며, 길스 선장님이며, 다정한 플로이 아가씨며, 그리고 적막한 무덤 얘기였어요,\""], ["said Susan.", "수전이 말했다."]], [["“The silent tomb!”", "\"적막한 무덤이라니!\""], ["repeated Florence.", "플로렌스가 되뇌었다."]], [["“He says,”", "\"그분 말씀이,\""], ["here Susan burst into a violent hysterical laugh, “that he’ll go down into it now immediately and quite comfortable, but bless your heart my dear Miss Floy he won’t, he’s a great deal too happy in seeing other people happy for that, he may not be a Solomon,”", "여기서 수전은 격렬한 히스테릭한 웃음을 터뜨렸다, \"이제 당장 거기로 내려가서 아주 편안히 누워 버리시겠다지만, 아이고 다정한 플로이 아가씨 그럴 리가 있겠어요, 그분은 남들 행복한 걸 보는 게 너무 좋아서 그럴 수가 없으세요, 솔로몬은 아닐지 몰라도,\""], ["pursued the Nipper, with her usual volubility, “nor do I say he is but this I do say a less selfish human creature human nature never knew!”", "니퍼가 여느 때처럼 거침없이 말을 이었다, \"그렇다고 제가 솔로몬이라고 하는 건 아니지만 이건 말할 수 있어요 그분보다 덜 이기적인 사람은 인간 본성이 일찍이 알지 못했다고요!\""]], [["Miss Nipper being still hysterical, laughed immoderately after making this energetic declaration, and then informed Florence that he was waiting below to see her; which would be a rich repayment for the trouble he had had in his late expedition.", "니퍼 양은 여전히 히스테릭한 상태라, 이 힘찬 선언을 마치고는 한참을 끝없이 웃어 댔다. 그러고는 플로렌스에게, 그분이 아래층에서 아가씨를 뵙기를 기다리고 계시다고 알렸다. 그것이 그분이 이번 원정길에 겪은 수고에 대한 풍성한 보답이 되리라고 했다."]], [["Florence entreated Susan to beg of Mr Toots as a favour that she might have the pleasure of thanking him for his kindness; and Susan, in a few moments, produced that young gentleman, still very much dishevelled in appearance, and stammering exceedingly.", "플로렌스는 수전에게, 그분의 친절에 감사를 드릴 기쁨을 자기에게 허락해 주십사 투츠 씨에게 청해 달라고 간곡히 부탁했다. 수전은 잠시 후 그 젊은 신사를 데려왔는데, 여전히 행색이 몹시 흐트러져 있었고 말도 심하게 더듬었다."]], [["“Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 아가씨,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“To be again permitted to—to—gaze—at least, not to gaze, but—I don’t exactly know what I was going to say, but it’s of no consequence.”", "\"다시금 허락을 받아—그—응시할—아니, 응시한다기보다는—제가 무슨 말을 하려던 건지 정확히 모르겠습니다만, 그건 아무래도 좋습니다.\""]], [["“I have to thank you so often,”", "\"제가 너무도 자주 감사를 드려야 하니,\""], ["returned Florence, giving him both her hands, with all her innocent gratitude beaming in her face, “that I have no words left, and don’t know how to do it.”", "플로렌스가 천진한 감사의 빛을 얼굴 가득 빛내며 두 손을 그에게 내밀고 대답했다, \"남은 말이 없어서, 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 아가씨,\""], ["said Mr Toots, in an awful voice, “if it was possible that you could, consistently with your angelic nature, Curse me, you would—if I may be allowed to say so—floor me infinitely less, than by these undeserved expressions of kindness Their effect upon me—is—but,”", "투츠 씨가 엄숙한 목소리로 말했다, \"만일 아가씨께서, 그 천사 같은 본성에 어긋나지 않게, 저를 저주하실 수 있다면, 그렇게 하시는 편이—이렇게 말씀드려도 된다면—이 분에 넘치는 친절한 말씀으로 저를 쓰러뜨리시는 것보다 한없이 덜 저를 무너뜨릴 것입니다. 그 말씀이 제게 미치는 영향은—말하자면—그런데,\""], ["said Mr Toots, abruptly, “this is a digression, and of no consequence at all.”", "투츠 씨가 불쑥 말했다, \"이건 곁가지로 새는 얘기이고, 아무래도 좋은 일입니다.\""]], [["As there seemed to be no means of replying to this, but by thanking him again, Florence thanked him again.", "이 말에 대답할 길이 다시 감사를 드리는 것밖에는 없는 듯하여, 플로렌스는 다시 한번 그에게 감사했다."]], [["“I could wish,”", "\"이 기회에,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “to take this opportunity, Miss Dombey, if I might, of entering into a word of explanation.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"괜찮으시다면, 돔비 아가씨, 한마디 해명을 드리고 싶습니다."], ["I should have had the pleasure of—of returning with Susan at an earlier period; but, in the first place, we didn’t know the name of the relation to whose house she had gone, and, in the second, as she had left that relation’s and gone to another at a distance, I think that scarcely anything short of the sagacity of the Chicken, would have found her out in the time.”", "저는 좀 더 일찍 수전과 함께 돌아오는 기쁨을 누렸어야 했지요. 그런데 첫째로, 수전이 찾아간 친척 집 이름을 우리가 몰랐고, 둘째로, 수전이 그 친척 집을 떠나 멀리 떨어진 또 다른 친척에게 가 버려서, 치킨의 명민함이 아니었다면 그 짧은 시간 안에 그녀를 찾아낼 길은 거의 없었으리라 생각합니다.\""]], [["Florence was sure of it.", "플로렌스도 분명 그러했으리라고 믿었다."]], [["“This, however,” said Mr Toots, “is not the point.", "\"하지만 그게 요점은 아닙니다,\""], ["The company of Susan has been, I assure you, Miss Dombey, a consolation and satisfaction to me, in my state of mind, more easily conceived than described.", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"수전과 함께한 길은, 정말이지, 돔비 아가씨, 제 마음 상태에서 말로 표현하기보다 짐작하시는 편이 더 쉬울 위로이자 만족이었습니다."], ["The journey has been its own reward.", "그 여정 자체가 보상이었지요."], ["That, however, still, is not the point.", "하지만 그것도 요점은 아닙니다."], ["Miss Dombey, I have before observed that I know I am not what is considered a quick person.", "돔비 아가씨, 제가 전에도 말씀드렸듯이 저는 머리 회전이 빠른 사람으로 여겨지지 않는다는 걸 압니다."], ["I am perfectly aware of that.", "그 점은 저도 잘 알고 있습니다."], ["I don’t think anybody could be better acquainted with his own—if it was not too strong an expression, I should say with the thickness of his own head—than myself.", "제 자신의—너무 심한 표현이 아니라면, 제 머리가 얼마나 둔한지를—저보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없으리라 생각합니다."], ["But, Miss Dombey, I do, notwithstanding, perceive the state of—of things—with Lieutenant Walters.", "하지만 돔비 아가씨, 그런데도 저는 월터스 중위님과 관련된—그—사정을 알아차리고 있습니다."], ["Whatever agony that state of things may have caused me (which is of no consequence at all), I am bound to say, that Lieutenant Walters is a person who appears to be worthy of the blessing that has fallen on his—on his brow.", "그 사정이 제게 어떤 고통을 안겨 주었든(그건 아무래도 좋은 일입니다만), 저는 말씀드리지 않을 수 없습니다, 월터스 중위님은 그의—그의 이마에 내려앉은 그 축복을 받기에 합당해 보이는 분이라고요."], ["May he wear it long, and appreciate it, as a very different, and very unworthy individual, that it is of no consequence to name, would have done!", "부디 그분이 그 축복을 오래도록 누리고, 또 그 가치를 알아보시기를, 굳이 이름을 댈 것도 없는 아주 다르고 아주 보잘것없는 어떤 사람이 그러했을 만큼이나!"], ["That, however, still, is not the point.", "하지만 그것도 요점은 아닙니다."], ["Miss Dombey, Captain Gills is a friend of mine; and during the interval that is now elapsing, I believe it would afford Captain Gills pleasure to see me occasionally coming backwards and forwards here.", "돔비 아가씨, 길스 선장님은 제 친구이십니다. 그래서 이제 흘러갈 그 기간 동안, 제가 이따금 이곳에 드나드는 것을 보시면 길스 선장님께서 기뻐하시리라 믿습니다."], ["It would afford me pleasure so to come.", "저로서도 그렇게 드나드는 것이 기쁠 것입니다."], ["But I cannot forget that I once committed myself, fatally, at the corner of the Square at Brighton; and if my presence will be, in the least degree, unpleasant to you, I only ask you to name it to me now, and assure you that I shall perfectly understand you.", "하지만 저는 한때 브라이턴의 광장 모퉁이에서 제 처지를 치명적으로 그르쳤던 일을 잊을 수가 없습니다. 만일 제 존재가 아가씨께 조금이라도 불쾌하다면, 부디 지금 제게 그 점을 말씀해 주십사 청할 뿐이고, 제가 아가씨의 뜻을 완벽히 이해하리라는 점을 약속드립니다."], ["I shall not consider it at all unkind, and shall only be too delighted and happy to be honoured with your confidence.”", "저는 그것을 조금도 매정하다 여기지 않을 것이며, 다만 아가씨의 솔직한 마음을 영광스럽게 들려주신 것에 더없이 기뻐하고 행복해할 따름입니다.\""]], [["“Mr Toots,”", "\"투츠 씨,\""], ["returned Florence, “if you, who are so old and true a friend of mine, were to stay away from this house now, you would make me very unhappy.", "플로렌스가 대답했다, \"이렇게 오래되고 진실한 제 친구이신 당신이 이제 이 집에 발길을 끊으신다면, 저를 무척 불행하게 만드실 거예요."], ["It can never, never, give me any feeling but pleasure to see you.", "당신을 뵙는 것은 결코, 결코 기쁨 말고는 다른 어떤 감정도 제게 주지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 아가씨,\""], ["said Mr Toots, taking out his pocket-handkerchief, “if I shed a tear, it is a tear of joy.", "투츠 씨가 손수건을 꺼내며 말했다, \"제가 눈물을 흘린다면, 그건 기쁨의 눈물입니다."], ["It is of no consequence, and I am very much obliged to you.", "아무래도 좋은 일이고, 정말 감사드립니다."], ["I may be allowed to remark, after what you have so kindly said, that it is not my intention to neglect my person any longer.”", "아가씨께서 그토록 친절히 말씀해 주셨으니, 한 말씀 드려도 된다면, 저는 더는 제 몸가짐을 소홀히 할 생각이 없습니다.\""]], [["Florence received this intimation with the prettiest expression of perplexity possible.", "플로렌스는 더없이 어여쁜 당혹스러운 표정으로 이 말을 받아들였다."]], [["“I mean,”", "\"제 말씀은,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “that I shall consider it my duty as a fellow-creature generally, until I am claimed by the silent tomb, to make the best of myself, and to—to have my boots as brightly polished, as—as—circumstances will admit of.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"적막한 무덤이 저를 부를 때까지, 대체로 한 인간으로서의 의무로 여겨, 제 자신을 최대한 가다듬고, 또—또 제 부츠를—형편이 허락하는 한—최대한 반짝반짝 닦아 두겠다는 뜻입니다."], ["This is the last time, Miss Dombey, of my intruding any observation of a private and personal nature.", "사적이고 개인적인 성격의 말씀을 들이미는 것은 이번이 마지막입니다, 돔비 아가씨."], ["I thank you very much indeed.", "정말 진심으로 감사드립니다."], ["If I am not, in a general way, as sensible as my friends could wish me to be, or as I could wish myself, I really am, upon my word and honour, particularly sensible of what is considerate and kind.", "제가 대체로 제 친구들이 바라는 만큼, 혹은 제 자신이 바라는 만큼 분별이 있지는 못할지라도, 정말이지, 제 명예를 걸고, 사려 깊고 친절한 것이 무엇인지는 각별히 분별할 줄 압니다."], ["I feel,”", "저는,\""], ["said Mr Toots, in an impassioned tone, “as if I could express my feelings, at the present moment, in a most remarkable manner, if—if—I could only get a start.”", "투츠 씨가 격정에 찬 어조로 말했다, \"지금 이 순간 제 감정을 더없이 놀라운 방식으로 표현할 수 있을 것만 같습니다, 만일—만일—그 첫머리를 떼기만 할 수 있다면요.\""]], [["Appearing not to get it, after waiting a minute or two to see if it would come, Mr Toots took a hasty leave, and went below to seek the Captain, whom he found in the shop.", "그러나 일이 분쯤 기다려도 그 첫머리가 떠오르지 않는 듯하자, 투츠 씨는 황급히 작별을 고하고 아래층으로 내려가 선장을 찾았는데, 가게에 있었다."]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “what is now to take place between us, takes place under the sacred seal of confidence.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"지금부터 우리 사이에 오갈 이야기는 신성한 비밀의 봉인 아래 이루어지는 것입니다."], ["It is the sequel, Captain Gills, of what has taken place between myself and Miss Dombey, upstairs.”", "이것은, 길스 선장님, 위층에서 저와 돔비 아가씨 사이에 오간 이야기의 후속편입니다.\""]], [["“Alow and aloft, eh, my lad?”", "\"아래위로 다 말이지, 응, 젊은이?\""], ["murmured the Captain.", "선장이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Exactly so, Captain Gills,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, whose fervour of acquiescence was greatly heightened by his entire ignorance of the Captain’s meaning.", "투츠 씨가 말했는데, 선장의 말뜻을 전혀 알지 못한 덕분에 동의하는 그의 열의는 한층 더 고조되었다."], ["“Miss Dombey, I believe, Captain Gills, is to be shortly united to Lieutenant Walters?”", "\"제가 알기로, 길스 선장님, 돔비 아가씨께서는 머지않아 월터스 중위님과 맺어지시는 거지요?\""]], [["“Why, ay, my lad.", "\"암, 그렇지, 젊은이."], ["We’re all shipmets here,—Wal”r and sweet—heart will be jined together in the house of bondage, as soon as the askings is over,”", "우리는 여기서 다 한배를 탄 동지일세,—월터하고 그 어여쁜 색시는 청혼 공고가 끝나는 대로 속박의 집에서 하나로 맺어질 걸세,\""], ["whispered Captain Cuttle, in his ear.", "커틀 선장이 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였다."]], [["“The askings, Captain Gills!”", "\"청혼 공고라니요, 길스 선장님!\""], ["repeated Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 되뇌었다."]], [["“In the church, down yonder,”", "\"저기, 저 아래 교회에서 말일세,\""], ["said the Captain, pointing his thumb over his shoulder.", "선장이 어깨 너머로 엄지손가락을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Yes!”", "네!\""], ["returned Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 대답했다."]], [["“And then,”", "\"그러고 나면,\""], ["said the Captain, in his hoarse whisper, and tapping Mr Toots on the chest with the back of his hand, and falling from him with a look of infinite admiration, “what follers?", "선장이 쉰 목소리로 속삭이며, 손등으로 투츠 씨의 가슴을 톡톡 치고는 한없는 감탄의 눈빛으로 그에게서 물러서며 말했다, \"그 뒤엔 뭐가 이어지겠나?"], ["That there pretty creetur, as delicately brought up as a foreign bird, goes away upon the roaring main with Wal”r on a woyage to China!”", "그 어여쁜 색시가, 외국에서 온 새처럼 곱게 자란 그 색시가, 월터하고 함께 포효하는 망망대해로 중국 가는 항해를 떠나는 거라네!\""]], [["“Lord, Captain Gills!”", "\"세상에, 길스 선장님!\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Ay!”", "\"그렇다네!\""], ["nodded the Captain.", "선장이 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“The ship as took him up, when he was wrecked in the hurricane that had drove her clean out of her course, was a China trader, and Wal”r made the woyage, and got into favour, aboard and ashore—being as smart and good a lad as ever stepped—and so, the supercargo dying at Canton, he got made (having acted as clerk afore), and now he’s supercargo aboard another ship, same owners.", "\"월터가 항로를 완전히 벗어나게 만든 그 허리케인 속에서 난파했을 때 그를 건져 올린 배가, 중국 무역선이었거든. 그래서 월터가 그 항해에 올랐고, 배 위에서나 뭍에서나 신임을 얻었네—여태 발 디딘 그 어떤 젊은이 못지않게 영리하고 착한 녀석이니까—그러다 광둥에서 화물 관리인이 죽는 바람에 (전에 서기 노릇을 했던 터라) 그 자리에 올랐고, 이제는 같은 선주의 다른 배에서 화물 관리인이 되었다네."], ["And so, you see,”", "그래서, 알겠나,\""], ["repeated the Captain, thoughtfully, “the pretty creetur goes away upon the roaring main with Wal”r, on a woyage to China.”", "선장이 생각에 잠겨 되뇌었다, \"그 어여쁜 색시가 월터하고 함께 포효하는 망망대해로 중국 가는 항해를 떠나는 거지.\""]], [["Mr Toots and Captain Cuttle heaved a sigh in concert.", "투츠 씨와 커틀 선장은 함께 한숨을 내쉬었다. \"그럼 어떤가?\""], ["“What then?”", "선장이 말했다."], ["said the Captain. “She loves him true.", "\"색시는 그를 진실로 사랑하지."], ["He loves her true.", "그도 색시를 진실로 사랑하고."], ["Them as should have loved and tended of her, treated of her like the beasts as perish.", "그 색시를 사랑하고 보살펴야 했을 사람들은, 그 색시를 멸망하는 짐승처럼 대했네."], ["When she, cast out of home, come here to me, and dropped upon them planks, her wownded heart was broke.", "집에서 쫓겨난 색시가 나한테 찾아와 저 판자 위에 쓰러졌을 때, 그 상처 입은 마음은 부서져 있었어."], ["I know it.", "나는 알아."], ["I, Ed’ard Cuttle, see it.", "나, 에드워드 커틀이, 그걸 봤네."], ["There’s nowt but true, kind, steady love, as can ever piece it up again.", "진실하고 다정하고 한결같은 사랑만이 그 마음을 다시 이어 붙일 수 있어."], ["If so be I didn’t know that, and didn’t know as Wal”r was her true love, brother, and she his, I’d have these here blue arms and legs chopped off, afore I’d let her go.", "만일 내가 그걸 모르고, 또 월터가 색시의 진실한 사랑이고, 형제여, 색시가 월터의 진실한 사랑인 걸 몰랐다면, 색시를 보내느니 차라리 이 파란 두 팔다리를 잘라 버렸을 걸세."], ["But I know it, and what then!", "하지만 나는 알아, 그러니 어떤가!"], ["Why, then, I say, Heaven go with ’em both, and so it will!", "그러니, 내 말은, 하느님께서 두 사람과 함께하시기를, 분명 그러실 거야!"], ["Amen!”", "아멘!\""]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “let me have the pleasure of shaking hands.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"악수하는 기쁨을 좀 누리게 해 주십시오."], ["You’ve a way of saying things, that gives me an agreeable warmth, all up my back.", "선장님은 말씀하시는 방식이, 등줄기를 타고 기분 좋은 온기가 쭉 올라오게 하는 데가 있습니다."], ["I say Amen.", "저도 아멘 하겠습니다."], ["You are aware, Captain Gills, that I, too, have adored Miss Dombey.”", "길스 선장님, 저 역시 돔비 아가씨를 흠모해 왔다는 걸 아실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Cheer up!”", "\"기운 내게!\""], ["said the Captain, laying his hand on Mr Toots’s shoulder.", "선장이 투츠 씨의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다."], ["“Stand by, boy!”", "\"버텨 내게, 젊은이!\""]], [["“It is my intention, Captain Gills,”", "\"제 뜻이 그렇습니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["returned the spirited Mr Toots, “to cheer up.", "기운을 차린 투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"기운을 내려고 합니다."], ["Also to standby, as much as possible.", "또 가능한 한 버텨 내려고도 합니다."], ["When the silent tomb shall yawn, Captain Gills, I shall be ready for burial; not before.", "적막한 무덤이 입을 벌릴 때, 길스 선장님, 저는 묻힐 준비가 되어 있을 겁니다. 그 전에는 아니고요."], ["But not being certain, just at present, of my power over myself, what I wish to say to you, and what I shall take it as a particular favour if you will mention to Lieutenant Walters, is as follows.”", "하지만 지금 당장은 제 자신을 다스릴 힘이 있다고 확신할 수 없으니, 제가 선장님께 드리고 싶은 말씀, 그리고 선장님께서 월터스 중위님께 전해 주신다면 특별한 은혜로 여길 말씀은 다음과 같습니다.\""]], [["“Is as follers,”", "\"다음과 같다 이거지,\""], ["echoed the Captain.", "선장이 따라 했다."], ["“Steady!”", "\"침착하게!\""]], [["“Miss Dombey being so inexpressably kind,”", "\"돔비 아가씨께서 너무도 말할 수 없이 친절하셔서,\""], ["continued Mr Toots with watery eyes, “as to say that my presence is the reverse of disagreeable to her, and you and everybody here being no less forbearing and tolerant towards one who—who certainly,”", "투츠 씨가 눈물 어린 눈으로 말을 이었다, \"제 존재가 자기에게 불쾌하기는커녕 그 반대라고 말씀해 주셨고, 또 선장님이며 여기 계신 모든 분이, 분명—,\""], ["said Mr Toots, with momentary dejection, “would appear to have been born by mistake, I shall come backwards and forwards of an evening, during the short time we can all be together.", "투츠 씨가 잠시 풀이 죽어 말했다, \"착오로 태어난 듯한 한 사람에게 못지않게 너그럽고 관대하시니, 저는 우리 모두가 함께 있을 수 있는 그 짧은 동안, 저녁마다 드나들겠습니다."], ["But what I ask is this.", "하지만 제가 청하는 건 이것입니다."], ["If, at any moment, I find that I cannot endure the contemplation of Lieutenant Walters’s bliss, and should rush out, I hope, Captain Gills, that you and he will both consider it as my misfortune and not my fault, or the want of inward conflict.", "만일 어느 순간엔가 제가 월터스 중위님의 행복을 차마 바라볼 수 없어 밖으로 뛰쳐나가게 되더라도, 부디, 길스 선장님, 선장님도 중위님도 그것을 제 잘못이 아니라 제 불운으로, 혹은 내적인 갈등이 부족해서가 아니라고 여겨 주시기를 바랍니다."], ["That you’ll feel convinced I bear no malice to any living creature-least of all to Lieutenant Walters himself—and that you’ll casually remark that I have gone out for a walk, or probably to see what o’clock it is by the Royal Exchange.", "제가 살아 있는 어떤 이에게도 악의를 품지 않는다는 걸—누구보다 월터스 중위님 자신에게는 더더욱—확신해 주시고, 또 제가 산책하러 나갔다거나, 아마 왕립 거래소에 가서 몇 시인지 보러 갔다거나 하고 무심히 말씀해 주시기를 바랍니다."], ["Captain Gills, if you could enter into this arrangement, and could answer for Lieutenant Walters, it would be a relief to my feelings that I should think cheap at the sacrifice of a considerable portion of my property.”", "길스 선장님, 만일 선장님께서 이 약속에 응해 주시고, 또 월터스 중위님을 대신해 보증해 주실 수 있다면, 제 재산의 상당 부분을 치르고서라도 헐값으로 여길 만큼 제 마음에 위안이 될 것입니다.\""]], [["“My lad,”", "\"젊은이,\""], ["returned the Captain, “say no more.", "선장이 대답했다, \"더 말 말게."], ["There ain’t a colour you can run up, as won’t be made out, and answered to, by Wal”r and self.”", "자네가 어떤 깃발을 올려도, 월터하고 내가 알아보고 응답하지 못할 일은 없을 걸세.\""]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “my mind is greatly relieved.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"제 마음이 크게 놓입니다."], ["I wish to preserve the good opinion of all here.", "저는 여기 계신 모든 분의 좋은 평판을 지키고 싶습니다."], ["I—I—mean well, upon my honour, however badly I may show it.", "저는—저는—제 명예를 걸고, 비록 서툴게 드러낼지라도, 진심에서 우러나옵니다."], ["You know,”", "아시다시피,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “it’s as exactly as Burgess and Co. wished to oblige a customer with a most extraordinary pair of trousers, and could not cut out what they had in their minds.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"그건 마치 버지스 상회가 어느 손님에게 더없이 비범한 바지 한 벌로 호의를 베풀고 싶었지만, 머릿속에 그린 대로 마름질해 내지 못한 것과 꼭 같지요.\""]], [["With this apposite illustration, of which he seemed a little Proud, Mr Toots gave Captain Cuttle his blessing and departed.", "이 적절한 비유를 든 것을 조금 자랑스러워하는 듯한 표정으로, 투츠 씨는 커틀 선장에게 축복을 빌고 떠나갔다."]], [["The honest Captain, with his Heart’s Delight in the house, and Susan tending her, was a beaming and a happy man.", "정직한 선장은, 자기 마음의 기쁨인 그 아가씨를 집에 두고 수전이 그녀를 보살피는 가운데, 환하게 빛나는 행복한 사람이었다."], ["As the days flew by, he grew more beaming and more happy, every day.", "날이 흘러갈수록 그는 날마다 더 환해지고 더 행복해졌다."], ["After some conferences with Susan (for whose wisdom the Captain had a profound respect, and whose valiant precipitation of herself on Mrs MacStinger he could never forget), he proposed to Florence that the daughter of the elderly lady who usually sat under the blue umbrella in Leadenhall Market, should, for prudential reasons and considerations of privacy, be superseded in the temporary discharge of the household duties, by someone who was not unknown to them, and in whom they could safely confide.", "수전과 몇 차례 의논을 한 끝에(선장은 수전의 지혜를 깊이 존경했고, 또 그녀가 맥스팅거 부인에게 용맹스럽게 자기 몸을 내던졌던 일을 결코 잊을 수 없었다), 그는 플로렌스에게, 리든홀 시장에서 평소 파란 우산 아래 앉아 있던 그 노부인의 딸을, 신중함을 기하고 사생활을 고려하는 차원에서, 그들이 모르지 않고 또 안심하고 믿을 수 있는 누군가로 갈음하여 집안일을 임시로 맡기자고 제안했다."], ["Susan, being present, then named, in furtherance of a suggestion she had previously offered to the Captain, Mrs Richards.", "그 자리에 있던 수전은, 전에 선장에게 미리 내놓았던 제안을 밀어붙여, 리처즈 부인의 이름을 댔다."], ["Florence brightened at the name.", "플로렌스는 그 이름에 얼굴이 환해졌다."], ["And Susan, setting off that very afternoon to the Toodle domicile, to sound Mrs Richards, returned in triumph the same evening, accompanied by the identical rosy-cheeked apple-faced Polly, whose demonstrations, when brought into Florence’s presence, were hardly less affectionate than those of Susan Nipper herself.", "그리고 수전은 바로 그날 오후 리처즈 부인의 의향을 떠보러 투들 댁으로 떠났다가, 바로 그날 저녁 의기양양하게 돌아왔는데, 그 곁에는 바로 그 발그레한 뺨에 사과 같은 얼굴을 한 폴리가 함께 있었다. 폴리가 플로렌스 앞에 나타났을 때 보인 애정 어린 모습은, 수전 니퍼 자신의 것에 못지않을 정도였다."]], [["This piece of generalship accomplished; from which the Captain derived uncommon satisfaction, as he did, indeed, from everything else that was done, whatever it happened to be; Florence had next to prepare Susan for their approaching separation.", "이 작전이 성취되자—선장은 여기서, 사실 무엇이 됐든 무슨 일이 이루어질 때마다 그러했듯이, 남다른 만족을 느꼈다—플로렌스는 다음으로 다가오는 이별을 위해 수전의 마음을 준비시켜야 했다."], ["This was a much more difficult task, as Miss Nipper was of a resolute disposition, and had fully made up her mind that she had come back never to be parted from her old mistress any more.", "이것은 훨씬 더 어려운 일이었으니, 니퍼 양은 결연한 성격이라, 옛 여주인과 두 번 다시 떨어지지 않으려고 돌아왔노라 단단히 마음먹고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“As to wages dear Miss Floy,”", "\"품삯 얘기라면요 다정한 플로이 아가씨,\""], ["she said, “you wouldn’t hint and wrong me so as think of naming them, for I’ve put money by and wouldn’t sell my love and duty at a time like this even if the Savings’ Banks and me were total strangers or the Banks were broke to pieces, but you’ve never been without me darling from the time your poor dear Ma was took away, and though I’m nothing to be boasted of you’re used to me and oh my own dear mistress through so many years don’t think of going anywhere without me, for it mustn’t and can’t be!”", "그녀가 말했다, \"그런 걸 입에 올릴 생각을 하셔서 저를 넌지시 떠보거나 서운하게 하지는 않으실 거예요, 전 돈을 모아 두었고 이런 때에 제 사랑과 의무를 팔지는 않을 테니까요 설령 저축은행하고 제가 생판 남남이라 해도, 아니 그 은행들이 산산조각이 났다 해도요, 그래도 아가씨께서는 가여운 어머님이 떠나가신 그때부터 한 번도 제 곁을 떠나신 적이 없으셨고, 또 제가 자랑할 만한 사람은 못 되어도 아가씨는 저한테 익숙하시잖아요 오 그 오랜 세월 동안 함께한 제 소중한 여주인님 저를 두고 어디로 가실 생각일랑 마세요, 그건 안 되고 또 그럴 수도 없으니까요!\""]], [["“Dear Susan, I am going on a long, long voyage.”", "\"다정한 수전, 나는 멀고 먼 항해를 떠나는 거야.\""]], [["“Well Miss Floy, and what of that?", "\"그래서요 플로이 아가씨, 그게 어쨌다는 거예요?"], ["the more you’ll want me.", "그러면 더더욱 제가 필요하시겠죠."], ["Lengths of voyages ain’t an object in my eyes, thank God!”", "항해가 멀고 가깝고는 제 눈엔 문제도 아니에요, 천만다행이지요!\""], ["said the impetuous Susan Nipper.", "성마른 수전 니퍼가 말했다."]], [["“But, Susan, I am going with Walter, and I would go with Walter anywhere—everywhere!", "\"하지만, 수전, 나는 월터와 함께 가는 거야, 그리고 나는 월터와 함께라면 어디든—어디든 갈 거야!"], ["Walter is poor, and I am very poor, and I must learn, now, both to help myself, and help him.”", "월터는 가난하고, 나는 무척 가난해, 그래서 나는 이제 스스로를 건사하는 법도, 그이를 돕는 법도 배워야 해.\""]], [["“Dear Miss Floy!”", "\"다정한 플로이 아가씨!\""], ["cried Susan, bursting out afresh, and shaking her head violently, “it’s nothing new to you to help yourself and others too and be the patientest and truest of noble hearts, but let me talk to Mr Walter Gay and settle it with him, for suffer you to go away across the world alone I cannot, and I won’t.”", "수전이 다시금 울음을 터뜨리고 격렬하게 고개를 흔들며 외쳤다, \"아가씨가 스스로를 건사하고 또 남들까지 돕는 거며, 더없이 인내심 많고 더없이 진실한 고귀한 마음을 지니신 거야 새삼스러운 일이 아니지만, 하지만 월터 게이 씨한테 제가 얘기해서 그분하고 매듭을 짓게 해 주세요, 아가씨를 혼자 세상 저 너머로 떠나보내는 건 저는 못해요, 절대 안 할 거예요.\""]], [["“Alone, Susan?”", "\"혼자라니, 수전?\""], ["returned Florence.", "플로렌스가 대답했다."], ["“Alone?", "\"혼자?"], ["and Walter taking me with him!”", "월터가 나를 데려가는데!\""], ["Ah, what a bright, amazed, enraptured smile was on her face!—He should have seen it.", "아, 그 얼굴에 어린 그 환하고 놀랍고 황홀한 미소라니!—그가 그것을 봤어야 했는데."], ["“I am sure you will not speak to Walter if I ask you not,”", "\"내가 부탁하지 않으면 분명 너는 월터한테 말하지 않을 거지,\""], ["she added tenderly; “and pray don’t, dear.”", "그녀가 다정하게 덧붙였다. \"그러니 제발 그러지 마, 응.\""]], [["Susan sobbed “Why not, Miss Floy?”", "수전이 흐느꼈다. \"왜 안 되나요, 플로이 아가씨?\""]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["said Florence, “I am going to be his wife, to give him up my whole heart, and to live with him and die with him.", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"나는 그이의 아내가 되어, 내 온 마음을 그이에게 바치고, 그이와 함께 살고 그이와 함께 죽으려는 거니까."], ["He might think, if you said to him what you have said to me, that I am afraid of what is before me, or that you have some cause to be afraid for me.", "만일 네가 나한테 한 말을 그이한테 한다면, 그이는 내가 앞날을 두려워한다거나, 너에게 나를 두고 두려워할 무슨 까닭이 있다고 생각할지도 몰라."], ["Why, Susan, dear, I love him!”", "아니, 수전, 다정한 수전, 나는 그이를 사랑하는걸!\""]], [["Miss Nipper was so much affected by the quiet fervour of these words, and the simple, heartfelt, all-pervading earnestness expressed in them, and making the speaker’s face more beautiful and pure than ever, that she could only cling to her again, crying.", "니퍼 양은 이 말들의 잔잔한 열정과, 거기에 담긴 소박하고 진심 어린, 온몸에 스며든 진지함—말하는 이의 얼굴을 그 어느 때보다 아름답고 순결하게 만든 그 진지함—에 너무도 감동한 나머지, 그저 다시 그녀에게 매달려 울 수밖에 없었다."], ["Was her little mistress really, really going to be married, and pitying, caressing, and protecting her, as she had done before.", "어린 여주인이 정말로, 정말로 결혼을 하시는구나, 하면서, 예전에 그랬듯이 그녀를 가엾어하고 어루만지고 지켜 주려 했다."]], [["But the Nipper, though susceptible of womanly weaknesses, was almost as capable of putting constraint upon herself as of attacking the redoubtable MacStinger.", "그러나 니퍼는, 비록 여성스러운 약한 면에 휘둘리기 쉬웠으되, 그 무시무시한 맥스팅거에게 맞서는 일만큼이나 자기 자신을 다잡는 일에도 거의 능했다. 그때부터 그녀는 다시는 그 주제로 돌아가지 않았고, 늘 명랑하고 활기차고 부산하고 희망에 차 있었다."], ["From that time, she never returned to the subject, but was always cheerful, active, bustling, and hopeful. She did, indeed, inform Mr Toots privately, that she was only “keeping up”", "다만 투츠 씨에게는 은밀히, 자기는 그저 그동안 버티고 있는 것뿐이며, 모든 일이 끝나고 돔비 아가씨가 떠나고 나면 보기에 안쓰러운 꼴이 되리라 예상해도 좋다고 일러 주기는 했다."], ["for the time, and that when it was all over, and Miss Dombey was gone, she might be expected to become a spectacle distressful; and Mr Toots did also express that it was his case too, and that they would mingle their tears together; but she never otherwise indulged her private feelings in the presence of Florence or within the precincts of the Midshipman.", "그러자 투츠 씨도 자기 처지 역시 그렇다고, 그러니 둘이서 함께 눈물을 섞으면 되겠다고 했다. 하지만 그녀는 그 밖에는 플로렌스 앞에서나 후보생관의 울타리 안에서 결코 자기 속내를 드러내지 않았다."]], [["Limited and plain as Florence’s wardrobe was—what a contrast to that prepared for the last marriage in which she had taken part!—there was a good deal to do in getting it ready, and Susan Nipper worked away at her side, all day, with the concentrated zeal of fifty sempstresses.", "플로렌스의 혼수가 비록 단출하고 수수했을지언정—그녀가 참여했던 지난번 결혼식을 위해 마련된 것과는 얼마나 대조적이던가!—그것을 갖추는 데에는 할 일이 꽤 많았고, 수전 니퍼는 하루 종일 그녀 곁에서 바느질꾼 쉰 명 몫의 열의를 모아 일했다."], ["The wonderful contributions Captain Cuttle would have made to this branch of the outfit, if he had been permitted—as pink parasols, tinted silk stockings, blue shoes, and other articles no less necessary on shipboard—would occupy some space in the recital.", "만일 커틀 선장이 이 혼수 마련에 허락만 받았다면 보탰을 그 놀라운 기여 품목들이란—분홍색 양산이며, 색색의 비단 양말이며, 파란 신발이며, 그 밖에 배 위에서도 못지않게 필요할 갖가지 물건들이며—다 늘어놓자면 적잖은 지면을 차지할 것이다."], ["He was induced, however, by various fraudulent representations, to limit his contributions to a work-box and dressing case, of each of which he purchased the very largest specimen that could be got for money.", "그러나 그는 여러 가지 그럴싸한 핑계에 넘어가, 자기 기여를 반짇고리와 화장 상자로 한정하게 되었는데, 그 둘 모두 돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 큰 것으로 구했다."], ["For ten days or a fortnight afterwards, he generally sat, during the greater part of the day, gazing at these boxes; divided between extreme admiration of them, and dejected misgivings that they were not gorgeous enough, and frequently diving out into the street to purchase some wild article that he deemed necessary to their completeness.", "그 후 열흘이나 보름 동안, 그는 대체로 하루의 대부분을 이 상자들을 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 그 상자들에 대한 극도의 감탄과, 그것들이 충분히 화려하지 못하다는 풀죽은 의구심 사이에서 갈팡질팡했고, 그것들을 완벽하게 하는 데 꼭 필요하다고 여긴 어떤 엉뚱한 물건을 사러 거리로 뛰쳐나가기 일쑤였다."], ["But his master-stroke was, the bearing of them both off, suddenly, one morning, and getting the two words FLORENCE GAY engraved upon a brass heart inlaid over the lid of each.", "그러나 그의 회심의 일격은, 어느 날 아침 그 두 상자를 갑작스레 들고 나가, 각 뚜껑 위에 박아 넣은 놋쇠 하트에 플로렌스 게이라는 두 단어를 새겨 넣은 것이었다."], ["After this, he smoked four pipes successively in the little parlour by himself, and was discovered chuckling, at the expiration of as many hours.", "그 후 그는 작은 응접실에서 혼자 연달아 파이프 네 대를 피웠고, 그만한 시간이 흐른 뒤에 혼자 낄낄거리고 있는 것이 발견되었다."]], [["Walter was busy and away all day, but came there every morning early to see Florence, and always passed the evening with her.", "월터는 하루 종일 바빠 밖에 나가 있었으나, 아침마다 일찍 와서 플로렌스를 보았고 늘 저녁을 그녀와 함께 보냈다."], ["Florence never left her high rooms but to steal downstairs to wait for him when it was his time to come, or, sheltered by his proud, encircling arm, to bear him company to the door again, and sometimes peep into the street.", "플로렌스는 자기 높은 방을 떠나는 일이 없었는데, 다만 그가 올 시각이 되면 살그머니 아래층으로 내려가 그를 기다리거나, 그의 자랑스럽게 감싸 안은 팔에 보호받으며 다시 문간까지 그를 배웅하고, 이따금 거리를 살짝 내다보러 갈 때만은 예외였다."], ["In the twilight they were always together.", "황혼 녘이면 그들은 늘 함께였다."], ["Oh blessed time!", "오 복된 시간이여!"], ["Oh wandering heart at rest!", "오 마침내 안식을 찾은 떠돌던 마음이여!"], ["Oh deep, exhaustless, mighty well of love, in which so much was sunk!", "오 그토록 많은 것이 잠겨 든, 깊고 마르지 않는 거대한 사랑의 샘이여!"]], [["The cruel mark was on her bosom yet.", "그 잔인한 자국은 아직도 그녀의 가슴에 남아 있었다."], ["It rose against her father with the breath she drew, it lay between her and her lover when he pressed her to his heart.", "그것은 그녀가 들이쉬는 숨결과 더불어 그녀의 아버지를 거슬러 일어섰고, 그가 그녀를 가슴에 끌어안을 때면 그녀와 연인 사이에 가로놓였다."], ["But she forgot it.", "그러나 그녀는 그것을 잊었다."], ["In the beating of that heart for her, and in the beating of her own for him, all harsher music was unheard, all stern unloving hearts forgotten.", "그녀를 향해 뛰는 그 가슴 속에서, 또 그를 향해 뛰는 자기 가슴 속에서, 더 거친 모든 가락은 들리지 않았고, 차갑고 사랑 없는 모든 마음은 잊혔다."], ["Fragile and delicate she was, but with a might of love within her that could, and did, create a world to fly to, and to rest in, out of his one image.", "그녀는 가냘프고 연약했으되, 그 안에는 그의 한결같은 모습 하나로부터 날아가 깃들 세계를 빚어낼 수 있고 또 실제로 빚어낸, 그런 사랑의 힘이 있었다."]], [["How often did the great house, and the old days, come before her in the twilight time, when she was sheltered by the arm, so proud, so fond, and, creeping closer to him, shrunk within it at the recollection!", "그 황혼 녘이면, 그토록 자랑스럽고 그토록 다정한 그 팔에 감싸여, 그에게 더 바싹 다가가 그 안에서 움츠러들 때면, 그 큰 저택과 옛 시절이 추억 속에서 그녀 앞에 떠오르는 일이 얼마나 잦았던가!"], ["How often, from remembering the night when she went down to that room and met the never-to-be forgotten look, did she raise her eyes to those that watched her with such loving earnestness, and weep with happiness in such a refuge!", "그녀가 그 방으로 내려갔다가 결코 잊을 수 없는 그 눈빛을 마주쳤던 그 밤을 떠올릴 때면, 그토록 사랑스러운 진지함으로 자기를 지켜보는 그 눈을 향해 눈을 들어 올리고, 그런 안식처에서 행복에 겨워 우는 일이 얼마나 잦았던가!"], ["The more she clung to it, the more the dear dead child was in her thoughts: but as if the last time she had seen her father, had been when he was sleeping and she kissed his face, she always left him so, and never, in her fancy, passed that hour.", "그녀가 그곳에 매달리면 매달릴수록, 세상을 떠난 그 소중한 아이가 그녀의 마음속에 더 많이 떠올랐다. 다만, 마치 아버지를 마지막으로 본 것이 그가 잠들어 있고 자기가 그 얼굴에 입 맞췄을 때이기라도 한 듯, 그녀는 늘 그를 그 모습 그대로 떠나보냈고, 공상 속에서 결코 그 시각을 넘어가지 않았다."]], [["“Walter, dear,”", "\"월터, 여보,\""], ["said Florence, one evening, when it was almost dark.", "어느 날 저녁, 거의 어두워졌을 무렵 플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“Do you know what I have been thinking today?”", "\"오늘 내가 무슨 생각을 했는지 알아요?\""]], [["“Thinking how the time is flying on, and how soon we shall be upon the sea, sweet Florence?”", "\"시간이 얼마나 빨리 흘러가는지, 또 우리가 얼마나 곧 바다 위에 떠 있을지 생각했겠지요, 사랑스러운 플로렌스?\""]], [["“I don’t mean that, Walter, though I think of that too.", "\"그건 아니에요, 월터, 그 생각도 하긴 했지만요."], ["I have been thinking what a charge I am to you.”", "내가 당신한테 얼마나 짐이 되는지 생각했어요.\""]], [["“A precious, sacred charge, dear heart!", "\"소중하고 거룩한 짐이지요, 사랑하는 사람!"], ["Why, I think that sometimes.”", "그야, 나도 가끔 그런 생각을 해요.\""]], [["“You are laughing, Walter.", "\"당신은 웃고 있군요, 월터."], ["I know that’s much more in your thoughts than mine.", "그게 나보다 당신 마음에 훨씬 더 자주 떠오르는 거 알아요."], ["But I mean a cost.", "하지만 내 말은 비용을 두고 하는 말이에요.\""]], [["“A cost, my own?”", "\"비용이라니, 내 사람?\""]], [["“In money, dear.", "\"돈 말이에요, 여보."], ["All these preparations that Susan and I are so busy with—I have been able to purchase very little for myself.", "수전하고 내가 이렇게 바쁘게 매달려 준비하는 이 모든 것들 말이에요—나는 나를 위해서는 거의 아무것도 살 수가 없었어요."], ["You were poor before.", "당신은 전에도 가난했지요."], ["But how much poorer I shall make you, Walter!”", "그런데 내가 당신을 얼마나 더 가난하게 만들게 될까요, 월터!\""]], [["“And how much richer, Florence!”", "\"그리고 얼마나 더 부유하게도 만들지요, 플로렌스!\""]], [["Florence laughed, and shook her head.", "플로렌스는 웃으며 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Besides,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["said Walter, “long ago—before I went to sea—I had a little purse presented to me, dearest, which had money in it.”", "월터가 말했다, \"오래전에—내가 바다로 나가기 전에—작은 지갑을 하나 선물받은 적이 있어요, 여보, 그 안에 돈이 들어 있었지요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["returned Florence, laughing sorrowfully, “very little!", "플로렌스가 서글프게 웃으며 대답했다, \"아주 조금이었죠!"], ["very little, Walter!", "아주 조금요, 월터!"], ["But, you must not think,”", "하지만, 당신은 생각해선 안 돼요,\""], ["and here she laid her light hand on his shoulder, and looked into his face, “that I regret to be this burden on you.", "그러면서 그녀는 가벼운 손을 그의 어깨에 얹고 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다, \"내가 당신에게 이렇게 짐이 되는 걸 후회한다고는요."], ["No, dear love, I am glad of it.", "아니에요, 사랑하는 이여, 나는 그게 기뻐요."], ["I am happy in it.", "그게 행복해요."], ["I wouldn’t have it otherwise for all the world!”", "온 세상을 다 준대도 다른 식으로는 싫어요!\""]], [["“Nor I, indeed, dear Florence.”", "\"나도 정말 그래요, 다정한 플로렌스.\""]], [["“Ay!", "\"네!"], ["but, Walter, you can never feel it as I do.", "하지만, 월터, 당신은 결코 나만큼 그것을 느낄 수는 없어요."], ["I am so proud of you!", "나는 당신이 너무 자랑스러워요!"], ["It makes my heart swell with such delight to know that those who speak of you must say you married a poor disowned girl, who had taken shelter here; who had no other home, no other friends; who had nothing—nothing!", "당신을 이야기하는 사람들이, 당신은 이곳에 몸을 의탁했던 가난하고 버림받은 처녀와, 다른 집도, 다른 친구도 없는, 아무것도—아무것도 없던 그런 처녀와 결혼했다고 말할 수밖에 없으리라는 걸 아는 것만으로도 내 마음은 그토록 큰 기쁨으로 부풀어 올라요!"], ["Oh, Walter, if I could have brought you millions, I never could have been so happy for your sake, as I am!”", "오, 월터, 내가 당신에게 수백만금을 가져다줄 수 있었다 한들, 당신을 위해 지금만큼 행복할 수는 없었을 거예요!\""]], [["“And you, dear Florence?", "\"그리고 당신은요, 다정한 플로렌스?"], ["are you nothing?”", "당신은 아무것도 아닌가요?\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“No, nothing, Walter.", "\"네, 아무것도 아니에요, 월터."], ["Nothing but your wife.”", "당신의 아내라는 것 말고는 아무것도요.\""], ["The light hand stole about his neck, and the voice came nearer—nearer.", "가벼운 손이 그의 목을 살며시 감았고, 목소리는 더 가까이—더 가까이 다가왔다."], ["“I am nothing any more, that is not you.", "\"나는 이제, 당신이 아닌 것은 더 이상 아무것도 아니에요."], ["I have no earthly hope any more, that is not you.", "나는 이제, 당신이 아닌 어떤 세속의 희망도 없어요."], ["I have nothing dear to me any more, that is not you.”", "나는 이제, 당신이 아닌 어떤 소중한 것도 없어요.\""]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["well might Mr Toots leave the little company that evening, and twice go out to correct his watch by the Royal Exchange, and once to keep an appointment with a banker which he suddenly remembered, and once to take a little turn to Aldgate Pump and back!", "그러니 투츠 씨가 그날 저녁 그 작은 모임을 떠나, 왕립 거래소에 가서 자기 시계를 맞춘다고 두 번이나 나가고, 또 문득 떠오른 은행가와의 약속을 지키러 한 번 나가고, 또 올드게이트 펌프까지 잠깐 한 바퀴 돌고 오느라 한 번 나간 것도 당연한 일이었다!"]], [["But before he went upon these expeditions, or indeed before he came, and before lights were brought, Walter said:", "그러나 그가 이런 원정길에 나서기 전에, 아니 사실 그가 오기 전, 또 불이 들어오기 전에, 월터가 말했다."]], [["“Florence, love, the lading of our ship is nearly finished, and probably on the very day of our marriage she will drop down the river.", "\"플로렌스, 여보, 우리 배의 짐 싣는 일이 거의 끝나가고, 아마 바로 우리 결혼식 날에 배가 강을 따라 내려갈 거예요."], ["Shall we go away that morning, and stay in Kent until we go on board at Gravesend within a week?”", "그날 아침 떠나서, 일주일 안에 그레이브젠드에서 배에 오를 때까지 켄트에 머물면 어떨까요?\""]], [["“If you please, Walter.", "\"당신 좋으실 대로요, 월터."], ["I shall be happy anywhere.", "나는 어디서든 행복할 거예요."], ["But—”", "하지만—\""]], [["“Yes, my life?”", "\"네, 내 생명?\""]], [["“You know,”", "\"알다시피,\""], ["said Florence, “that we shall have no marriage party, and that nobody will distinguish us by our dress from other people.", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"우리는 결혼식 잔치도 없을 테고, 아무도 우리 옷차림으로 우리를 다른 사람들과 구별하지 못할 거예요."], ["As we leave the same day, will you—will you take me somewhere that morning, Walter—early—before we go to church?”", "우리가 같은 날 떠나니까, 그날 아침—일찍—교회에 가기 전에, 나를—나를 어딘가로 데려다줄래요, 월터?\""]], [["Walter seemed to understand her, as so true a lover so truly loved should, and confirmed his ready promise with a kiss—with more than one perhaps, or two or three, or five or six; and in the grave, peaceful evening, Florence was very happy.", "월터는, 그토록 진실한 연인이 그토록 진실로 사랑받는 처지라면 마땅히 그러하듯, 그녀의 뜻을 알아차린 듯했고, 입맞춤으로 선뜻 그러마 하고 약속을 다졌다—아마 한 번이 아니라 두 번, 세 번, 혹은 다섯 번, 여섯 번이었으리라. 그리고 그 그윽하고 평온한 저녁에, 플로렌스는 무척 행복했다."]], [["Then into the quiet room came Susan Nipper and the candles; shortly afterwards, the tea, the Captain, and the excursive Mr Toots, who, as above mentioned, was frequently on the move afterwards, and passed but a restless evening.", "이윽고 그 고요한 방으로 수전 니퍼가 촛불을 들고 들어왔다. 곧이어 차와, 선장과, 들락날락하던 투츠 씨가 들어왔는데, 그는 앞서 말한 대로 그 후에도 자주 자리를 떴고 안절부절못한 저녁을 보냈다."], ["This, however, was not his habit: for he generally got on very well, by dint of playing at cribbage with the Captain under the advice and guidance of Miss Nipper, and distracting his mind with the calculations incidental to the game; which he found to be a very effectual means of utterly confounding himself.", "그러나 이것이 그의 평소 모습은 아니었다. 그는 대개 니퍼 양의 조언과 지도를 받으며 선장과 크리비지 놀이를 하는 데에 힘입어 아주 잘 지냈고, 그 놀이에 딸린 셈으로 마음을 다른 데로 돌렸으니, 그것이 자기 자신을 완전히 헷갈리게 만드는 데 아주 효과적인 방편임을 알게 되었던 것이다."]], [["The Captain’s visage on these occasions presented one of the finest examples of combination and succession of expression ever observed.", "이런 자리에서 선장의 얼굴은, 일찍이 관찰된 표정의 조합과 연속의 가장 빼어난 본보기 가운데 하나를 보여 주었다."], ["His instinctive delicacy and his chivalrous feeling towards Florence, taught him that it was not a time for any boisterous jollity, or violent display of satisfaction; floating reminiscences of Lovely Peg, on the other hand, were constantly struggling for a vent, and urging the Captain to commit himself by some irreparable demonstration.", "그가 본능적으로 지닌 섬세함과 플로렌스를 향한 기사도적인 마음씨는, 지금이 떠들썩한 흥겨움이나 격렬한 만족의 표출을 드러낼 때가 아니라고 그에게 일러 주었다. 한편 '어여쁜 페그'의 떠도는 추억은 끊임없이 새어 나올 출구를 찾아 다투며, 어떤 돌이킬 수 없는 표출로 선장이 그만 자기 처지를 그르치도록 부추겼다."], ["Anon, his admiration of Florence and Walter—well-matched, truly, and full of grace and interest in their youth, and love, and good looks, as they sat apart—would take such complete possession of him, that he would lay down his cards, and beam upon them, dabbing his head all over with his pocket-handkerchief; until warned, perhaps, by the sudden rushing forth of Mr Toots, that he had unconsciously been very instrumental, indeed, in making that gentleman miserable.", "그러다가, 따로 떨어져 앉은 플로렌스와 월터를 향한—그 젊음과 사랑과 준수함이 참으로 잘 어울리고 우아함과 흥미로 가득한 두 사람을 향한—감탄이 어찌나 그를 온통 사로잡던지, 그는 카드를 내려놓고 손수건으로 머리를 온통 톡톡 두드리며 두 사람을 향해 환히 빛나곤 했다."], ["This reflection would make the Captain profoundly melancholy, until the return of Mr Toots; when he would fall to his cards again, with many side winks and nods, and polite waves of his hook at Miss Nipper, importing that he wasn’t going to do so any more.", "그러다 어쩌면 투츠 씨가 갑자기 밖으로 뛰쳐나가는 것을 보고, 자기가 무심결에 정말이지 그 신사를 비참하게 만드는 데 단단히 한몫했음을 깨닫게 되곤 했다. 이 깨달음은 투츠 씨가 돌아올 때까지 선장을 깊은 우울에 빠뜨렸다."], ["The state that ensued on this, was, perhaps, his best; for then, endeavouring to discharge all expression from his face, he would sit staring round the room, with all these expressions conveyed into it at once, and each wrestling with the other.", "그가 돌아오면 선장은 다시 카드를 잡으며, 니퍼 양에게 곁눈질과 끄덕임을 여러 번 보내고 갈고리를 정중히 흔들어 보였는데, 그건 더는 그러지 않겠다는 뜻이었다. 그 뒤에 이어지는 상태야말로 아마 그의 최고의 모습이었으리라."], ["Delighted admiration of Florence and Walter always overthrew the rest, and remained victorious and undisguised, unless Mr Toots made another rush into the air, and then the Captain would sit, like a remorseful culprit, until he came back again, occasionally calling upon himself, in a low reproachful voice, to “Stand by!”", "그때 그는 얼굴에서 온갖 표정을 다 지워 버리려 애쓰면서, 방 안을 둘러보며 앉아 있었는데, 그 모든 표정이 한꺼번에 얼굴에 실려 서로 뒤엉켜 다투었다. 플로렌스와 월터를 향한 기쁨에 찬 감탄은 늘 나머지를 다 눌러 이기고, 투츠 씨가 다시 한번 바깥으로 뛰쳐나가지 않는 한, 승자로 남아 거리낌 없이 드러났다."], ["or growling some remonstrance to “Ed’ard Cuttle, my lad,” on the want of caution observable in his behaviour.", "투츠 씨가 뛰쳐나가면 선장은 죄를 뉘우치는 죄인처럼 그가 돌아올 때까지 앉아 있었고, 이따금 낮고 책망하는 목소리로 자기 자신에게 \"버텨 내라!\"고 명하거나, \"에드워드 커틀, 이 사람아\"에게 그 처신에서 보이는 조심성 부족을 두고 무어라 으르렁대며 타이르곤 했다."]], [["One of Mr Toots’s hardest trials, however, was of his own seeking.", "그러나 투츠 씨에게 가장 혹독한 시련 가운데 하나는 그가 스스로 자초한 것이었다."], ["On the approach of the Sunday which was to witness the last of those askings in church of which the Captain had spoken, Mr Toots thus stated his feelings to Susan Nipper.", "선장이 말했던 그 교회에서의 청혼 공고 가운데 마지막 것을 듣게 될 일요일이 다가오자, 투츠 씨는 수전 니퍼에게 자기 심정을 이렇게 털어놓았다."]], [["“Susan,”", "\"수전,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “I am drawn towards the building.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"나는 그 건물 쪽으로 끌려가는 것 같습니다."], ["The words which cut me off from Miss Dombey for ever, will strike upon my ears like a knell you know, but upon my word and honour, I feel that I must hear them.", "나를 돔비 아가씨에게서 영원히 갈라놓을 그 말들이, 알다시피, 조종(弔鐘)처럼 내 귀를 때리겠지만, 정말이지 내 명예를 걸고, 나는 그 말들을 꼭 들어야만 할 것 같습니다."], ["Therefore,”", "그러니,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “will you accompany me to-morrow, to the sacred edifice?”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"내일 나와 함께 그 성스러운 건물에 가 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["Miss Nipper expressed her readiness to do so, if that would be any satisfaction to Mr Toots, but besought him to abandon his idea of going.", "니퍼 양은, 그것이 투츠 씨에게 조금이라도 위안이 된다면 기꺼이 그러겠노라고 밝혔으나, 가겠다는 생각일랑 버리시라고 간청했다."]], [["“Susan,”", "\"수전,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, with much solemnity, “before my whiskers began to be observed by anybody but myself, I adored Miss Dombey.", "투츠 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 대답했다, \"내 구레나룻이 나 말고 다른 누구의 눈에 띄기 시작하기도 전부터, 나는 돔비 아가씨를 흠모했습니다."], ["While yet a victim to the thraldom of Blimber, I adored Miss Dombey.", "아직 블림버의 속박에 매인 희생양이던 시절에도, 나는 돔비 아가씨를 흠모했습니다."], ["When I could no longer be kept out of my property, in a legal point of view, and—and accordingly came into it—I adored Miss Dombey.", "법적인 관점에서 더는 내 재산에서 떨어져 있을 수 없게 되어—그래서—그래서 그것을 손에 넣게 되었을 때에도, 나는 돔비 아가씨를 흠모했습니다."], ["The banns which consign her to Lieutenant Walters, and me to—to Gloom, you know,”", "그녀를 월터스 중위님에게 맡기고, 나를—나를 암흑에 넘기는, 알다시피, 그 결혼 공고는,\""], ["said Mr Toots, after hesitating for a strong expression, “may be dreadful, will be dreadful; but I feel that I should wish to hear them spoken.", "투츠 씨가 강한 표현을 찾느라 머뭇거린 뒤 말했다, \"끔찍할 수도 있고, 끔찍할 것입니다만, 나는 그 말이 선포되는 것을 꼭 듣고 싶은 심정입니다."], ["I feel that I should wish to know that the ground was certainly cut from under me, and that I hadn’t a hope to cherish, or a—or a leg, in short, to—to go upon.”", "내 발밑의 땅이 확실히 잘려 나갔다는 것, 그리고 내게 품을 희망도, 그—혹은 그—한마디로, 딛고—딛고 설 다리 하나조차 남지 않았다는 것을 꼭 알고 싶은 심정입니다.\""]], [["Susan Nipper could only commiserate Mr Toots’s unfortunate condition, and agree, under these circumstances, to accompany him; which she did next morning.", "수전 니퍼는 그저 투츠 씨의 가련한 처지를 가엾이 여기고, 이런 형편이니 그와 함께 가겠노라 응할 수밖에 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 이튿날 아침 그렇게 했다."]], [["The church Walter had chosen for the purpose, was a mouldy old church in a yard, hemmed in by a labyrinth of back streets and courts, with a little burying-ground round it, and itself buried in a kind of vault, formed by the neighbouring houses, and paved with echoing stones.", "월터가 그 목적으로 골라 둔 교회는, 어느 안뜰에 자리한 곰팡내 나는 낡은 교회로, 뒷골목과 안마당이 미로처럼 둘러싸고 있었고, 둘레에는 작은 묘지가 딸려 있었으며, 그 교회 자체는 이웃집들이 이룬 일종의 지하 납골당 같은 곳에 파묻혀, 메아리치는 돌바닥이 깔려 있었다."], ["It was a great dim, shabby pile, with high old oaken pews, among which about a score of people lost themselves every Sunday; while the clergyman’s voice drowsily resounded through the emptiness, and the organ rumbled and rolled as if the church had got the colic, for want of a congregation to keep the wind and damp out.", "그것은 크고 어둑하고 초라한 더미 같은 건물로, 높다란 낡은 떡갈나무 신도석들이 있었는데, 일요일마다 스무 명 남짓한 사람들이 그 사이에서 길을 잃었다. 한편 목사의 목소리는 그 텅 빈 공간을 졸린 듯 울려 퍼졌고, 오르간은 마치 바람과 습기를 막아 줄 회중이 없어 교회가 배앓이라도 하는 양 우르릉거리고 굴러 댔다."], ["But so far was this city church from languishing for the company of other churches, that spires were clustered round it, as the masts of shipping cluster on the river.", "그러나 이 도시 교회는 다른 교회들과 함께하지 못해 시름에 잠긴 처지와는 거리가 멀어서, 강 위에 배의 돛대들이 빽빽이 모여 있듯 첨탑들이 그 둘레에 옹기종기 모여 있었다."], ["It would have been hard to count them from its steeple-top, they were so many.", "그 첨탑 꼭대기에서 세어 보려 해도, 너무 많아 헤아리기 어려웠을 것이다."], ["In almost every yard and blind-place near, there was a church.", "근처의 거의 모든 안뜰과 막다른 곳마다 교회가 하나씩 있었다."], ["The confusion of bells when Susan and Mr Toots betook themselves towards it on the Sunday morning, was deafening.", "일요일 아침 수전과 투츠 씨가 그 교회로 향했을 때, 어지러이 뒤섞인 종소리는 귀가 먹먹할 지경이었다."], ["There were twenty churches close together, clamouring for people to come in.", "가까이 붙은 스무 개의 교회가 사람들에게 들어오라고 아우성치고 있었다."]], [["The two stray sheep in question were penned by a beadle in a commodious pew, and, being early, sat for some time counting the congregation, listening to the disappointed bell high up in the tower, or looking at a shabby little old man in the porch behind the screen, who was ringing the same, like the Bull in Cock Robin, with his foot in a stirrup. Mr Toots, after a lengthened survey of the large books on the reading-desk, whispered Miss Nipper that he wondered where the banns were kept, but that young lady merely shook her head and frowned; repelling for the time all approaches of a temporal nature.", "문제의 그 길 잃은 두 마리 양은 교회지기에 의해 널찍한 신도석에 갇혔고, 일찍 온 터라 한동안 앉아서 회중을 세어 보거나, 탑 높은 곳에서 실망에 찬 종소리를 듣거나, 칸막이 뒤편 현관에서 어느 초라한 작은 노인이 한 발을 등자에 걸친 채 그 종을 울리는—마치 '울새 누가 죽였나' 노래 속 황소처럼—모습을 바라보았다. 투츠 씨는 독경대 위의 큰 책들을 한참 살펴보고는, 결혼 공고를 어디에 보관하는지 궁금하다고 니퍼 양에게 속삭였으나, 그 젊은 숙녀는 그저 고개를 가로젓고 눈살을 찌푸리며, 당분간 세속적인 종류의 모든 접근을 물리쳤다. * 울새 누가 죽였나: 영국의 전래 동요. 노래에 종을 울리는 황소가 나온다."]], [["Mr Toots, however, appearing unable to keep his thoughts from the banns, was evidently looking out for them during the whole preliminary portion of the service.", "그러나 투츠 씨는 결혼 공고에서 도무지 마음을 떼지 못하는 듯, 예배의 도입부 내내 줄곧 그것을 살피고 있는 게 분명했다."], ["As the time for reading them approached, the poor young gentleman manifested great anxiety and trepidation, which was not diminished by the unexpected apparition of the Captain in the front row of the gallery.", "공고를 읽을 시간이 다가오자, 그 가련한 젊은 신사는 큰 불안과 떨림을 드러냈는데, 회랑 맨 앞줄에 선장이 뜻밖에 나타난 일도 그 불안을 덜어 주지 못했다. 서기가 목사에게 명단을 올렸을 때, 그때 앉아 있던 투츠 씨는 신도석 좌석을 꽉 붙들었다."], ["When the clerk handed up a list to the clergyman, Mr Toots, being then seated, held on by the seat of the pew; but when the names of Walter Gay and Florence Dombey were read aloud as being in the third and last stage of that association, he was so entirley conquered by his feelings as to rush from the church without his hat, followed by the beadle and pew-opener, and two gentlemen of the medical profession, who happened to be present; of whom the first-named presently returned for that article, informing Miss Nipper in a whisper that she was not to make herself uneasy about the gentleman, as the gentleman said his indisposition was of no consequence.", "그러나 월터 게이와 플로렌스 돔비의 이름이, 그 결합의 세 번째이자 마지막 단계에 이르렀다고 큰 소리로 낭독되자, 그는 감정에 완전히 압도된 나머지 모자도 없이 교회 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔고, 그 뒤를 교회지기와 신도석 안내인, 그리고 마침 그 자리에 있던 두 명의 의사 신사가 뒤따랐다. 그중 먼저 말한 교회지기는 곧 그 물건(모자)을 가지러 돌아와, 니퍼 양에게 그 신사 일로 불안해하지 마시라고, 그 신사가 자기 불편은 아무래도 좋다고 말하더라고 속삭여 일러 주었다."]], [["Miss Nipper, feeling that the eyes of that integral portion of Europe which lost itself weekly among the high-backed pews, were upon her, would have been sufficient embarrassed by this incident, though it had terminated here; the more so, as the Captain in the front row of the gallery, was in a state of unmitigated consciousness which could hardly fail to express to the congregation that he had some mysterious connection with it.", "니퍼 양은, 매주 그 높은 등받이 신도석들 사이에서 길을 잃는 유럽의 그 한 부분을 이루는 사람들의 눈이 자기에게 쏠려 있음을 느꼈으니, 일이 여기서 끝났다 해도 이 사건만으로 충분히 난처했을 것이다. 더구나 회랑 맨 앞줄의 선장은 자기가 이 일과 무슨 알 수 없는 연관이 있음을 회중에게 드러내지 않을 도리가 거의 없을 만큼 노골적인 의식 상태에 있었으니 더욱 그러했다."], ["But the extreme restlessness of Mr Toots painfully increased and protracted the delicacy of her situation.", "그러나 투츠 씨의 극심한 안절부절못함이 그녀의 처지의 미묘함을 고통스럽게 키우고 또 질질 끌었다."], ["That young gentleman, incapable, in his state of mind, of remaining alone in the churchyard, a prey to solitary meditation, and also desirous, no doubt, of testifying his respect for the offices he had in some measure interrupted, suddenly returned—not coming back to the pew, but stationing himself on a free seat in the aisle, between two elderly females who were in the habit of receiving their portion of a weekly dole of bread then set forth on a shelf in the porch.", "그 젊은 신사는, 그런 심정으로는 묘지에 홀로 남아 적막한 명상에 잠겨 있을 수도 없었거니와, 또 자기가 어느 정도 방해한 그 예식에 대한 존경을 분명 표하고 싶기도 했던지, 갑자기 돌아왔다—다만 신도석으로 되돌아온 것이 아니라, 현관 선반에 놓아둔 매주 베푸는 빵 한 몫을 받아 가는 데 익숙한 두 노부인 사이의, 통로에 있는 빈 좌석에 자리를 잡았다. 이렇게 결합한 채로 투츠 씨는 머물러 있었고, 회중을 크게 어지럽혔으니, 그들로서는 그를 쳐다보지 않을 도리가 없었던 것이다."], ["In this conjunction Mr Toots remained, greatly disturbing the congregation, who felt it impossible to avoid looking at him, until his feelings overcame him again, when he departed silently and suddenly.", "그러다 감정이 다시 그를 짓누르자, 그는 소리 없이 갑작스레 떠나갔다. 더는 교회 안에서 자기 자신을 믿고 맡길 엄두를 못 내면서도, 그래도 그 안에서 벌어지는 일에 어떤 식으로든 함께하고 싶었던 투츠 씨는, 그 후 이따금 처량한 얼굴로 이 창문 저 창문을 들여다보는 모습으로 눈에 띄었다."], ["Not venturing to trust himself in the church any more, and yet wishing to have some social participation in what was going on there, Mr Toots was, after this, seen from time to time, looking in, with a lorn aspect, at one or other of the windows; and as there were several windows accessible to him from without, and as his restlessness was very great, it not only became difficult to conceive at which window he would appear next, but likewise became necessary, as it were, for the whole congregation to speculate upon the chances of the different windows, during the comparative leisure afforded them by the sermon.", "그가 밖에서 접근할 수 있는 창문이 여럿이었고 또 그의 안절부절못함이 워낙 컸던 탓에, 다음번에 그가 어느 창문에 나타날지 짐작하기 어려웠을 뿐 아니라, 설교가 베풀어 준 비교적 한가한 시간 동안 회중 전체가 이를테면 서로 다른 창문들의 확률을 점쳐 봐야 할 지경이 되었다. 투츠 씨가 묘지에서 움직이는 모양이 어찌나 별난지, 그는 대개 온갖 예측을 다 어그러뜨리는 듯했고, 마술사의 인형처럼 가장 예상치 못한 곳에 나타났다."], ["Mr Toots’s movements in the churchyard were so eccentric, that he seemed generally to defeat all calculation, and to appear, like the conjuror’s figure, where he was least expected; and the effect of these mysterious presentations was much increased by its being difficult to him to see in, and easy to everybody else to see out: which occasioned his remaining, every time, longer than might have been expected, with his face close to the glass, until he all at once became aware that all eyes were upon him, and vanished.", "그리고 이 알 수 없는 출몰의 효과는, 그가 안을 들여다보기는 어렵고 다른 모든 사람이 밖을 내다보기는 쉽다는 사정 때문에 한층 더 커졌다. 그런 까닭에 그는 번번이, 모든 눈이 자기에게 쏠려 있음을 문득 알아차리고 사라질 때까지, 얼굴을 유리에 바싹 대고 예상보다 더 오래 머물러 있곤 했다."]], [["These proceedings on the part of Mr Toots, and the strong individual consciousness of them that was exhibited by the Captain, rendered Miss Nipper’s position so responsible a one, that she was mightily relieved by the conclusion of the service; and was hardly so affable to Mr Toots as usual, when he informed her and the Captain, on the way back, that now he was sure he had no hope, you know, he felt more comfortable—at least not exactly more comfortable, but more comfortably and completely miserable.", "투츠 씨 쪽의 이런 행동거지와, 또 선장이 드러낸 그것에 대한 강한 개인적 의식 탓에, 니퍼 양의 처지는 그토록 책임이 막중해져서, 그녀는 예배가 끝난 것에 크게 안도했다. 돌아오는 길에 투츠 씨가 그녀와 선장에게, 이제 자기에게 희망이 없다는 걸 확실히 알고 나니, 알다시피, 한결 편안하다고—적어도 정확히 더 편안하다기보다는, 더 편안하고도 온전하게 비참하다고—일러 주었을 때, 그녀는 평소만큼 그에게 살갑지 못했다."]], [["Swiftly now, indeed, the time flew by until it was the evening before the day appointed for the marriage.", "이제 정말로 시간은 쏜살같이 흘러, 결혼식으로 정해진 날의 전날 저녁이 되었다."], ["They were all assembled in the upper room at the Midshipman’s, and had no fear of interruption; for there were no lodgers in the house now, and the Midshipman had it all to himself.", "그들은 모두 후보생관의 위층 방에 모여 있었고, 방해받을 걱정은 없었으니, 이제 그 집에는 세 든 사람이 하나도 없어 후보생이 그 집을 통째로 차지하고 있었던 것이다."], ["They were grave and quiet in the prospect of to-morrow, but moderately cheerful too.", "그들은 내일을 앞두고 진지하고 차분했으나, 그래도 어지간히 명랑하기도 했다."], ["Florence, with Walter close beside her, was finishing a little piece of work intended as a parting gift to the Captain.", "플로렌스는, 월터를 바로 곁에 둔 채, 선장에게 줄 작별 선물로 마련한 작은 바느질감을 마무리하고 있었다."], ["The Captain was playing cribbage with Mr Toots.", "선장은 투츠 씨와 크리비지를 하고 있었다."], ["Mr Toots was taking counsel as to his hand, of Susan Nipper.", "투츠 씨는 수전 니퍼에게 자기 패를 어떻게 낼지 조언을 구하고 있었다."], ["Miss Nipper was giving it, with all due secrecy and circumspection.", "니퍼 양은 마땅한 비밀과 신중함을 다해 그것을 일러 주고 있었다."], ["Diogenes was listening, and occasionally breaking out into a gruff half-smothered fragment of a bark, of which he afterwards seemed half-ashamed, as if he doubted having any reason for it.", "다이오지니즈는 귀를 기울이다가, 이따금 거친 데다 반쯤 짓눌린 짧은 짖음을 터뜨렸는데, 그러고 나서는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었나 스스로 의심하는 듯, 반쯤 부끄러워하는 기색이었다."]], [["“Steady, steady!”", "\"가만, 가만!\""], ["said the Captain to Diogenes, “what’s amiss with you?", "선장이 다이오지니즈에게 말했다, \"너 왜 그러느냐?"], ["You don’t seem easy in your mind tonight, my boy!”", "오늘 밤은 마음이 영 편치 않은 모양이구나, 이 녀석!\""]], [["Diogenes wagged his tail, but pricked up his ears immediately afterwards, and gave utterance to another fragment of a bark; for which he apologised to the Captain, by again wagging his tail.", "다이오지니즈는 꼬리를 흔들었으나, 곧바로 귀를 쫑긋 세우더니 또 한 차례 짧게 짖었다. 그러고는 다시 꼬리를 흔들어 선장에게 사과했다."]], [["“It’s my opinion, Di,”", "\"내 생각엔 말이다, 다이,\""], ["said the Captain, looking thoughtfully at his cards, and stroking his chin with his hook, “as you have your doubts of Mrs Richards; but if you’re the animal I take you to be, you’ll think better o’ that; for her looks is her commission.", "선장이 카드를 생각에 잠겨 들여다보고 갈고리로 턱을 쓰다듬으며 말했다, \"네가 리처즈 부인을 의심하는 게로구나. 하지만 네가 내가 생각하는 그런 짐승이라면, 그 생각을 고쳐먹는 게 좋을 게다."], ["Now, Brother:”", "그 부인의 인상이야말로 그 부인의 보증서니까."], ["to Mr Toots: “if so be as you’re ready, heave ahead.”", "자, 형제여,\" 투츠 씨에게 하는 말이었다, \"준비됐거든, 어서 패를 던지게.\""]], [["The Captain spoke with all composure and attention to the game, but suddenly his cards dropped out of his hand, his mouth and eyes opened wide, his legs drew themselves up and stuck out in front of his chair, and he sat staring at the door with blank amazement.", "선장은 더없이 침착하게, 또 놀이에 주의를 기울이며 말했다. 그런데 별안간 그의 손에서 카드가 떨어지고, 입과 눈이 휘둥그레지고, 두 다리가 끌어올려졌다가 의자 앞으로 쭉 뻗어 나오더니, 그는 멍하니 어리둥절한 채 문을 바라보며 앉아 있었다."], ["Looking round upon the company, and seeing that none of them observed him or the cause of his astonishment, the Captain recovered himself with a great gasp, struck the table a tremendous blow, cried in a stentorian roar, “Sol Gills ahoy!”", "둘러앉은 사람들을 돌아보니 아무도 자기를, 또 자기가 놀란 까닭을 알아채지 못한 것을 보고, 선장은 크게 헐떡이며 정신을 차리고는 탁자를 엄청나게 내리치면서 우렁찬 고함으로 \"솔 길스 어이!\""], ["and tumbled into the arms of a weather-beaten pea-coat that had come with Polly into the room.", "하고 외쳤고, 폴리와 함께 방으로 들어온 비바람에 닳은 더플코트의 품으로 와락 무너져 들었다."]], [["In another moment, Walter was in the arms of the weather-beaten pea-coat.", "다음 순간, 월터가 그 비바람에 닳은 더플코트의 품에 안겨 있었다."], ["In another moment, Florence was in the arms of the weather-beaten pea-coat.", "또 다음 순간, 플로렌스가 그 비바람에 닳은 더플코트의 품에 안겨 있었다."], ["In another moment, Captain Cuttle had embraced Mrs Richards and Miss Nipper, and was violently shaking hands with Mr Toots, exclaiming, as he waved his hook above his head, “Hooroar, my lad, hooroar!”", "또 다음 순간, 커틀 선장은 리처즈 부인과 니퍼 양을 끌어안고는, 투츠 씨와 격렬하게 악수를 하며, 갈고리를 머리 위로 흔들면서 외쳤다, \"만세, 젊은이, 만세!\""], ["To which Mr Toots, wholly at a loss to account for these proceedings, replied with great politeness, “Certainly, Captain Gills, whatever you think proper!”", "이에 투츠 씨는 이 일들을 도무지 어찌 된 영문인지 알 길이 없었던지라, 더없이 공손하게 대답했다, \"여부가 있겠습니까, 길스 선장님, 선장님 보시기에 옳은 대로 무엇이든지요!\""]], [["The weather-beaten pea-coat, and a no less weather-beaten cap and comforter belonging to it, turned from the Captain and from Florence back to Walter, and sounds came from the weather-beaten pea-coat, cap, and comforter, as of an old man sobbing underneath them; while the shaggy sleeves clasped Walter tight.", "그 비바람에 닳은 더플코트와, 거기 딸린 못지않게 비바람에 닳은 모자와 목도리는, 선장과 플로렌스에게서 돌아서 다시 월터에게로 향했고, 그 비바람에 닳은 더플코트와 모자와 목도리 아래에서 마치 한 노인이 흐느끼는 듯한 소리가 새어 나왔다. 그러는 동안 그 텁수룩한 소맷자락이 월터를 꼭 끌어안았다."], ["During this pause, there was an universal silence, and the Captain polished his nose with great diligence.", "이 잠깐 동안 사방이 온통 고요했고, 선장은 부지런히 코를 문질러 닦았다."], ["But when the pea-coat, cap, and comforter lifted themselves up again, Florence gently moved towards them; and she and Walter taking them off, disclosed the old Instrument-maker, a little thinner and more careworn than of old, in his old Welsh wig and his old coffee-coloured coat and basket buttons, with his old infallible chronometer ticking away in his pocket.", "그러나 그 더플코트와 모자와 목도리가 다시 몸을 일으키자, 플로렌스가 살며시 그쪽으로 다가갔다. 그녀와 월터가 그것들을 벗겨 내자, 그 늙은 기계 제작자가 모습을 드러냈는데, 예전보다 조금 야위고 더 근심에 찌든 채, 그 낡은 웨일스 가발에 낡은 커피색 외투에 바구니 모양 단추를 단 차림이었고, 주머니에서는 그 낡았으나 틀림없는 정밀시계가 째깍거리고 있었다."]], [["“Chock full o’ science,”", "\"여전히 학식이 가득하시군요,\""], ["said the radiant Captain, “as ever he was!", "환히 빛나는 선장이 말했다, \"예나 다름없이!"], ["Sol Gills, Sol Gills, what have you been up to, for this many a long day, my ould boy?”", "솔 길스, 솔 길스, 이 오랜 세월 동안 대체 뭘 하고 다니셨소, 내 늙은 친구?\""]], [["“I’m half blind, Ned,”", "\"눈이 반쯤 멀었네, 네드,\""], ["said the old man, “and almost deaf and dumb with joy.”", "노인이 말했다, \"그리고 기뻐서 거의 귀먹고 말도 못 할 지경일세.\""]], [["“His wery woice,”", "\"바로 그 목소리야,\""], ["said the Captain, looking round with an exultation to which even his face could hardly render justice—“his wery woice as chock full o’ science as ever it was!", "선장이, 자기 얼굴조차 미처 다 담아내지 못할 환희에 차서 둘러보며 말했다—\"학식이 예나 다름없이 가득한 바로 그 목소리야!"], ["Sol Gills, lay to, my lad, upon your own wines and fig-trees like a taut ould patriark as you are, and overhaul them there adwentures o’ yourn, in your own formilior woice.", "솔 길스, 닻을 내리게나, 이 사람아, 자네야말로 꼿꼿한 늙은 족장이니 자네 포도나무와 무화과나무 아래에 자리를 잡고, 그 자네 모험담을 자네 그 친근한 목소리로 한번 죽 풀어 보게나."], ["“Tis the woice,”", "'이는 게으름뱅이의 목소리로다,'\""], ["said the Captain, impressively, and announcing a quotation with his hook, “of the sluggard, I heerd him complain, you have woke me too soon, I must slumber again.", "선장이 자못 인상 깊게, 갈고리로 인용문임을 알리며 말했다, \"'그가 푸념하는 소리를 내 들었노라, 너희가 나를 너무 일찍 깨웠으니, 내 다시 졸아야겠도다. 그의 원수들을 흩으시고, 그들을 쓰러지게 하소서!'\""], ["Scatter his ene-mies, and make ’em fall!”", "* 게으름뱅이의 목소리: 아이작 와츠의 시 「게으름뱅이」와 시편 구절을 뒤섞은 인용. 선장은 늘 성구를 엉뚱하게 짜깁기한다."]], [["The Captain sat down with the air of a man who had happily expressed the feeling of everybody present, and immediately rose again to present Mr Toots, who was much disconcerted by the arrival of anybody, appearing to prefer a claim to the name of Gills.", "선장은 그 자리에 있는 모두의 심정을 흐뭇하게 대변하기라도 한 사람 같은 태도로 자리에 앉았다가, 곧바로 다시 일어나 투츠 씨를 소개하려 했다. 투츠 씨는 누군가 길스라는 이름의 권리를 주장하는 듯한 사람이 도착한 일로 적잖이 당혹스러워했다."]], [["“Although,”", "\"비록,\""], ["stammered Mr Toots, “I had not the pleasure of your acquaintance, Sir, before you were—you were—”", "투츠 씨가 더듬거렸다, \"선생님과 알고 지내는 기쁨은 누리지 못했습니다만, 선생님께서—선생님께서—\""]], [["“Lost to sight, to memory dear,”", "\"보이지 않는 곳으로 사라졌으나 기억 속에 소중히 남으셨던 동안에 말이지요,\""], ["suggested the Captain, in a low voice.", "선장이 낮은 목소리로 거들었다."]], [["“Exactly so, Captain Gills!”", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 길스 선장님!\""], ["assented Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 동의했다."], ["“Although I had not the pleasure of your acquaintance, Mr—Mr Sols,”", "\"비록 그 일이 있기 전에 선생님과—솔스 씨와,\""], ["said Toots, hitting on that name in the inspiration of a bright idea, “before that happened, I have the greatest pleasure, I assure you, in—you know, in knowing you.", "투츠 씨가 번뜩이는 착상에 힘입어 그 이름을 짚어 내며 말했다, \"알고 지내는 기쁨은 누리지 못했습니다만, 정말이지 저는—그러니까—선생님을 알게 되어 더없이 기쁩니다."], ["I hope,”", "부디,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “that you’re as well as can be expected.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"기대할 수 있는 만큼은 건강하시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["With these courteous words, Mr Toots sat down blushing and chuckling.", "이 정중한 말과 함께, 투츠 씨는 얼굴을 붉히고 낄낄거리며 자리에 앉았다."], ["The old Instrument-maker, seated in a corner between Walter and Florence, and nodding at Polly, who was looking on, all smiles and delight, answered the Captain thus:", "늙은 기계 제작자는, 월터와 플로렌스 사이의 한쪽 구석에 앉아, 온통 미소와 기쁨에 차 지켜보고 있는 폴리를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며, 선장에게 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“Ned Cuttle, my dear boy, although I have heard something of the changes of events here, from my pleasant friend there—what a pleasant face she has to be sure, to welcome a wanderer home!”", "\"네드 커틀, 내 소중한 친구, 비록 여기서 일어난 사건들의 변화에 관해서는 저기 저 유쾌한 내 친구한테서 조금 듣긴 했네만—정말이지 떠돌이가 집에 돌아온 걸 반겨 주는 얼굴치고 어쩜 저리 유쾌한 얼굴이람!\""], ["said the old man, breaking off, and rubbing his hands in his old dreamy way.", "노인이 말을 끊고는, 예의 그 꿈꾸는 듯한 버릇대로 두 손을 비비며 말했다."]], [["“Hear him!”", "\"저 말 좀 듣게나!\""], ["cried the Captain gravely.", "선장이 자못 엄숙하게 외쳤다."], ["“’Tis woman as seduces all mankind.", "\"온 인류를 호리는 것은 여자라네."], ["For which,”", "그러니,\""], ["aside to Mr Toots, “you’ll overhaul your Adam and Eve, brother.”", "투츠 씨에게 옆으로 살짝 덧붙이며, \"형제여, 자네 아담과 이브를 한번 살펴보게나.\""]], [["“I shall make a point of doing so, Captain Gills,”", "\"꼭 그렇게 하겠습니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Although I have heard something of the changes of events, from her,”", "\"비록 사건들의 변화에 관해 저 친구한테서 조금 듣긴 했네만,\""], ["resumed the Instrument-maker, taking his old spectacles from his pocket, and putting them on his forehead in his old manner, “they are so great and unexpected, and I am so overpowered by the sight of my dear boy, and by the,”—glancing at the downcast eyes of Florence, and not attempting to finish the sentence—“that I—I can’t say much tonight.", "기계 제작자가 주머니에서 낡은 안경을 꺼내 예의 그 버릇대로 이마에 얹으며 말을 이었다, \"워낙 크고 뜻밖의 일들인 데다, 내 소중한 아이를 본 것과, 또 저—,\" 플로렌스의 내리깐 눈을 흘끗 보고는, 문장을 마저 맺으려 하지 않은 채 말했다—\"그것 때문에 너무도 벅차서, 나는—나는 오늘 밤엔 별 말을 못 하겠네."], ["But my dear Ned Cuttle, why didn’t you write?”", "그런데 내 소중한 네드 커틀, 자네는 왜 편지를 안 했나?\""]], [["The astonishment depicted in the Captain’s features positively frightened Mr Toots, whose eyes were quite fixed by it, so that he could not withdraw them from his face.", "선장의 이목구비에 그려진 놀라움은 투츠 씨를 단단히 겁먹게 했고, 그의 눈은 그것에 완전히 못 박혀, 선장의 얼굴에서 도무지 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다."]], [["“Write!”", "\"편지라니!\""], ["echoed the Captain.", "선장이 따라 했다."], ["“Write, Sol Gills?”", "\"편지를 하라니, 솔 길스?\""]], [["“Ay,”", "\"그렇다네,\""], ["said the old man, “either to Barbados, or Jamaica, or Demerara, that was what I asked.”", "노인이 말했다, \"바베이도스로든, 자메이카로든, 데메라라로든, 그게 내가 부탁한 거였잖나.\""]], [["“What you asked, Sol Gills?”", "\"자네가 부탁했다니, 솔 길스?\""], ["repeated the Captain.", "선장이 되뇌었다."]], [["“Ay,” said the old man.", "\"그렇다네,\""], ["“Don’t you know, Ned?", "노인이 말했다."], ["Sure you have not forgotten?", "\"모르겠나, 네드? 설마 잊지는 않았겠지?"], ["Every time I wrote to you.”", "내가 자네한테 편지할 때마다 말일세.\""]], [["The Captain took off his glazed hat, hung it on his hook, and smoothing his hair from behind with his hand, sat gazing at the group around him: a perfect image of wondering resignation.", "선장은 번들거리는 모자를 벗어 갈고리에 걸고, 손으로 뒤에서부터 머리를 매만지며, 둘러앉은 사람들을 멍하니 바라보고 앉아 있었다. 영락없이 어리둥절한 체념의 화신이었다."]], [["“You don’t appear to understand me, Ned!”", "\"자네 내 말을 못 알아듣는 모양이군, 네드!\""], ["observed old Sol.", "늙은 솔이 일렀다."]], [["“Sol Gills,”", "\"솔 길스,\""], ["returned the Captain, after staring at him and the rest for a long time, without speaking, “I’m gone about and adrift.", "선장이 한참을 말없이 그와 다른 이들을 바라본 뒤에 대답했다, \"나는 갈피를 못 잡고 표류하는 중일세."], ["Pay out a word or two respecting them adwenturs, will you!", "그 모험담에 관해 한두 마디 풀어 주게나, 응!"], ["Can’t I bring up, nohows?", "내가 어떻게든 닻을 내릴 수가 없으니, 어찌하나?"], ["Nohows?”", "어찌하나?\""], ["said the Captain, ruminating, and staring all round.", "선장이 곰곰 생각에 잠겨, 사방을 두리번거리며 말했다."]], [["“You know, Ned,”", "\"자네도 알지 않나, 네드,\""], ["said Sol Gills, “why I left here.", "솔 길스가 말했다, \"내가 왜 여기를 떠났는지."], ["Did you open my packet, Ned?”", "자네 내 꾸러미를 열어 봤나, 네드?\""]], [["“Why, ay, ay,”", "\"암, 그럼, 그럼,\""], ["said the Captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“To be sure, I opened the packet.”", "\"물론, 그 꾸러미를 열어 봤네.\""]], [["“And read it?”", "\"그래서 읽어 봤나?\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."]], [["“And read it,”", "\"그래서 읽었네,\""], ["answered the Captain, eyeing him attentively, and proceeding to quote it from memory.", "선장이, 그를 유심히 바라보며 대답하고는, 기억을 더듬어 그것을 외워 보였다."], ["“‘My dear Ned Cuttle, when I left home for the West Indies in forlorn search of intelligence of my dear-’ There he sits!", "\"'내 소중한 네드 커틀, 내가 사랑하는—을 찾을 소식을 절망적으로 구하러 서인도제도로 집을 떠날 적에—' 저기 그 아이가 앉아 있군!"], ["There’s Wal”r!”", "저기 월터가 있어!\""], ["said the Captain, as if he were relieved by getting hold of anything that was real and indisputable.", "선장이 마치 무언가 실재하고 의심할 수 없는 것을 붙들게 되어 안도하는 듯이 말했다."]], [["“Well, Ned.", "\"자, 네드."], ["Now attend a moment!”", "이제 잠깐만 들어 보게!\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."], ["“When I wrote first—that was from Barbados—I said that though you would receive that letter long before the year was out, I should be glad if you would open the packet, as it explained the reason of my going away.", "\"내가 처음 편지를 썼을 때—그건 바베이도스에서였는데—자네가 그 편지를 받는 건 그해가 다 가기 한참 전이겠지만, 그래도 그 꾸러미를 열어 봐 주면 좋겠다고 했네, 내가 떠난 까닭을 거기 적어 두었으니까."], ["Very good, Ned.", "좋아, 네드."], ["When I wrote the second, third, and perhaps the fourth times—that was from Jamaica—I said I was in just the same state, couldn’t rest, and couldn’t come away from that part of the world, without knowing that my boy was lost or saved.", "두 번째, 세 번째, 아마 네 번째로 편지를 썼을 때는—그건 자메이카에서였는데—나는 여전히 같은 처지라, 도무지 안정을 찾을 수가 없고, 내 아이가 죽었는지 살았는지 알지 못하고는 그 지역을 떠날 수가 없다고 했네."], ["When I wrote next—that, I think, was from Demerara, wasn’t it?”", "그다음에 편지를 썼을 때는—그건, 내 생각에, 데메라라에서였지, 아니었나?\""]], [["“That he thinks was from Demerara, warn’t it!”", "\"그건 자기 생각에 데메라라에서였다는군, 아니었느냐고!\""], ["said the Captain, looking hopelessly round.", "선장이 막막하게 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["“—I said,” proceeded old Sol, “that still there was no certain information got yet.", "\"—나는 이렇게 썼네,\""], ["That I found many captains and others, in that part of the world, who had known me for years, and who assisted me with a passage here and there, and for whom I was able, now and then, to do a little in return, in my own craft.", "늙은 솔이 말을 이었다, \"여전히 확실한 소식은 아직 얻지 못했다고. 그 지역에서 나를 여러 해 알고 지낸 선장들과 그 밖의 사람들을 많이 만났는데, 그들이 여기저기 배편을 마련해 나를 도와주었고, 나도 이따금 내 솜씨로 그들에게 조금이나마 보답할 수 있었다고."], ["That everyone was sorry for me, and seemed to take a sort of interest in my wanderings; and that I began to think it would be my fate to cruise about in search of tidings of my boy, until I died.”", "다들 나를 딱하게 여기고, 내 떠돌이 신세에 일종의 관심을 가져 주는 듯하다고. 그리고 나는, 죽을 때까지 내 아이의 소식을 찾아 떠돌아다니는 게 내 운명이 되려나 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다고.\""]], [["“Began to think as how he was a scientific Flying Dutchman!”", "\"학식 있는 방황하는 네덜란드인이 다 된 것 같다는 생각이 들기 시작했다 이거지!\""], ["said the Captain, as before, and with great seriousness.", "선장이 앞서처럼, 그리고 자못 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“But when the news come one day, Ned,—that was to Barbados, after I got back there,—that a China trader home’ard bound had been spoke, that had my boy aboard, then, Ned, I took passage in the next ship and came home; arrived at home tonight to find it true, thank God!”", "* 방황하는 네덜란드인: 영원히 바다를 떠도는 저주받은 유령선 전설. \"하지만 어느 날 소식이 왔네, 네드,—그건 내가 다시 돌아간 뒤의 바베이도스로였는데,—본국으로 향하던 중국 무역선과 교신이 있었고, 그 배에 내 아이가 타고 있다는 소식이었지."], ["said the old man, devoutly.", "그래서, 네드, 나는 다음 배에 배편을 얻어 집으로 돌아왔고, 오늘 밤 집에 닿아 보니 그게 사실이었네, 천만다행하게도!\" 노인이 경건하게 말했다."]], [["The Captain, after bowing his head with great reverence, stared all round the circle, beginning with Mr Toots, and ending with the Instrument-maker; then gravely said:", "선장은 자못 경건하게 고개를 숙인 뒤, 투츠 씨를 시작으로 기계 제작자에 이르기까지 둘러앉은 무리를 죽 둘러보고는, 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“Sol Gills!", "\"솔 길스!"], ["The observation as I’m a-going to make is calc’lated to blow every stitch of sail as you can carry, clean out of the bolt-ropes, and bring you on your beam ends with a lurch.", "내가 지금 하려는 말은 말일세, 자네가 펼칠 수 있는 돛이란 돛은 죄다 바닥줄에서 깨끗이 날려 버리고, 자네를 휘청 옆구리째 자빠뜨리게 만들 만한 말일세. 그 편지들 가운데 단 한 통도 에드워드 커틀한테 전해진 적이 없네."], ["Not one of them letters was ever delivered to Ed’ard Cuttle.", "그 편지들 가운데 단 한 통도,\" 선장이 자기 선언을 한층 더 엄숙하고 인상 깊게 만들려고 되풀이했다, \"잉글랜드의 뱃사람, 집에서 편히 지내며 빛나는 시간을 한 시간 한 시간 알차게 보내는, 에드워드 커틀한테 전해진 적이 없단 말일세!\""], ["Not one o’ them letters,”", "* 빛나는 시간을 알차게 보내는: 아이작 와츠의 동시 「부지런한 꿀벌」"], ["repeated the Captain, to make his declaration the more solemn and impressive, “was ever delivered unto Ed’ard Cuttle, Mariner, of England, as lives at home at ease, and doth improve each shining hour!”", "구절을 따온 것."]], [["“And posted by my own hand!", "\"게다가 내 손으로 직접 부쳤다네!"], ["And directed by my own hand, Number nine Brig Place!”", "그리고 내 손으로 직접 주소를 적었어, 브리그 플레이스 9번지라고!\""], ["exclaimed old Sol.", "늙은 솔이 외쳤다."]], [["The colour all went out of the Captain’s face and all came back again in a glow.", "선장의 얼굴에서 핏기가 싹 가셨다가 다시 발갛게 확 돌아왔다."]], [["“What do you mean, Sol Gills, my friend, by Number nine Brig Place?”", "\"브리그 플레이스 9번지라니, 솔 길스, 그게 무슨 뜻인가, 이 사람아?\""], ["inquired the Captain.", "선장이 물었다."]], [["“Mean? Your lodgings, Ned,”", "\"무슨 뜻이냐고?"], ["returned the old man.", "자네 하숙집 말일세, 네드,\""], ["“Mrs What’s-her-name!", "노인이 대답했다."], ["I shall forget my own name next, but I am behind the present time—I always was, you recollect—and very much confused.", "\"그 무어라는 부인 말이야! 다음엔 내 이름도 잊어버리겠군, 하지만 나는 시류에 뒤처져 있으니—늘 그랬다는 거 자네도 알지 않나—그리고 정신이 무척 어수선해."], ["Mrs—”", "그—부인이—\""]], [["“Sol Gills!”", "\"솔 길스!\""], ["said the Captain, as if he were putting the most improbable case in the world, “it ain’t the name of MacStinger as you’re a trying to remember?”", "선장이 마치 세상에서 가장 있을 법하지 않은 경우를 들이대듯 말했다, \"자네가 기억해 내려는 게 설마 맥스팅거라는 이름은 아니겠지?\""]], [["“Of course it is!”", "\"바로 그거야!\""], ["exclaimed the Instrument-maker.", "기계 제작자가 외쳤다."], ["“To be sure Ned.", "\"그렇고말고, 네드."], ["Mrs MacStinger!”", "맥스팅거 부인!\""]], [["Captain Cuttle, whose eyes were now as wide open as they would be, and the knobs upon whose face were perfectly luminous, gave a long shrill whistle of a most melancholy sound, and stood gazing at everybody in a state of speechlessness.", "이제 더 커질 수 없을 만큼 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고, 얼굴의 혹들이 완전히 환하게 빛나는 커틀 선장은, 더없이 구슬픈 가락의 길고 날카로운 휘파람을 한 번 불고는, 말문이 막힌 채 모두를 멍하니 바라보며 서 있었다."]], [["“Overhaul that there again, Sol Gills, will you be so kind?”", "\"미안하지만 그걸 한 번만 더 죽 풀어 주겠나, 솔 길스?\""], ["he said at last.", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["“All these letters,”", "\"그 편지들을 전부,\""], ["returned Uncle Sol, beating time with the forefinger of his right hand upon the palm of his left, with a steadiness and distinctness that might have done honour, even to the infallible chronometer in his pocket, “I posted with my own hand, and directed with my own hand, to Captain Cuttle, at Mrs MacStinger’s, Number nine Brig Place.”", "솔 아저씨가, 오른손 집게손가락으로 왼손 손바닥을 두드리며 박자를 맞추었는데, 그 한결같음과 또렷함은 그의 주머니 속 틀림없는 정밀시계에마저 부끄럽지 않을 정도였다, \"내가 내 손으로 직접 부치고, 내 손으로 직접 주소를 적어, 브리그 플레이스 9번지 맥스팅거 부인 댁의 커틀 선장 앞으로 보냈다네.\""]], [["The Captain took his glazed hat off his hook, looked into it, put it on, and sat down.", "선장은 갈고리에 걸어 둔 번들거리는 모자를 벗어 들고 안을 들여다보더니, 도로 쓰고 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Why, friends all,”", "\"이거 참, 다들 들어 보게,\""], ["said the Captain, staring round in the last state of discomfiture, “I cut and run from there!”", "선장이 더없이 낭패한 꼴로 둘러보며 말했다, \"내가 거기서 줄행랑쳐 나왔단 말일세!\""]], [["“And no one knew where you were gone, Captain Cuttle?”", "\"그래서 선장님이 어디로 가셨는지 아무도 몰랐던 거군요, 커틀 선장님?\""], ["cried Walter hastily.", "월터가 황급히 외쳤다."]], [["“Bless your heart, Wal”r,”", "\"이것 보게나, 월터,\""], ["said the Captain, shaking his head, “she’d never have allowed o’ my coming to take charge o’ this here property.", "선장이 고개를 저으며 말했다, \"그 여자는 내가 이 가게를 맡으러 오는 걸 절대로 허락하지 않았을 걸세."], ["Nothing could be done but cut and run.", "줄행랑치는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었지."], ["Lord love you, Wal”r!”", "아이고 이 사람아, 월터!\""], ["said the Captain, “you’ve only seen her in a calm!", "선장이 말했다, \"자네는 그 여자가 잠잠할 때만 봤지!"], ["But see her when her angry passions rise—and make a note on!”", "하지만 그 여자가 성난 노여움이 솟구칠 때를 한번 봐 보게—그러곤 잘 새겨 두게나!\""]], [["“I’d give it her!”", "\"제가 본때를 보여 줬을 텐데요!\""], ["remarked the Nipper, softly.", "니퍼가 나직이 한마디 했다."]], [["“Would you, do you think, my dear?”", "\"그럴 것 같나, 정말로, 아가씨?\""], ["returned the Captain, with feeble admiration.", "선장이 힘없는 감탄을 담아 대답했다."], ["“Well, my dear, it does you credit.", "\"허, 아가씨, 참으로 갸륵한 말이로군."], ["But there ain’t no wild animal I wouldn’t sooner face myself.", "하지만 나라면 차라리 먼저 맞서고 싶은 사나운 짐승이 한 마리도 없을 지경이라네."], ["I only got my chest away by means of a friend as nobody’s a match for.", "나는 그 누구도 당해 낼 수 없는 친구 하나의 도움을 받아서야 겨우 내 궤짝을 빼낼 수 있었지."], ["It was no good sending any letter there.", "거기로 편지를 보내 봐야 아무 소용이 없었을 걸세."], ["She wouldn’t take in any letter, bless you,”", "그 여자는 편지 한 통도 받아 두지 않았을 거야, 정말로,\""], ["said the Captain, “under them circumstances!", "선장이 말했다, \"그런 형편에서는!"], ["Why, you could hardly make it worth a man’s while to be the postman!”", "허, 편지 배달부 노릇을 할 만한 값어치가 도무지 없었을 게야!\""]], [["“Then it’s pretty clear, Captain Cuttle, that all of us, and you and Uncle Sol especially,”", "\"그럼 분명한 건,\""], ["said Walter, “may thank Mrs MacStinger for no small anxiety.”", "월터가 말했다, \"우리 모두가, 그중에서도 특히 선장님과 솔 아저씨가, 적잖은 근심을 안은 데 대해 맥스팅거 부인께 고마워해도 좋겠다는 거네요, 커틀 선장님.\""]], [["The general obligation in this wise to the determined relict of the late Mr MacStinger, was so apparent, that the Captain did not contest the point; but being in some measure ashamed of his position, though nobody dwelt upon the subject, and Walter especially avoided it, remembering the last conversation he and the Captain had held together respecting it, he remained under a cloud for nearly five minutes—an extraordinary period for him when that sun, his face, broke out once more, shining on all beholders with extraordinary brilliancy; and he fell into a fit of shaking hands with everybody over and over again.", "이렇게 작고한 맥스팅거 씨의 그 단호한 미망인에게 다들 신세를 진 셈이라는 것이 너무도 분명했으므로, 선장은 그 점을 따지지 않았다. 다만 아무도 그 주제를 들먹이지 않았고 특히 월터는 그것을—전에 그와 선장이 그 일을 두고 나눴던 마지막 대화를 떠올리며—애써 피했음에도, 선장은 자기 처지가 다소 부끄러웠던지, 거의 오 분 동안이나 먹구름 속에 잠겨 있었다—그로서는 유례없이 긴 시간이었다. 이윽고 그의 얼굴이라는 태양이 다시 한번 솟아올라, 유난한 광채로 바라보는 모두를 비추었고, 그는 모두와 거듭거듭 악수하는 발작에 빠져들었다."]], [["At an early hour, but not before Uncle Sol and Walter had questioned each other at some length about their voyages and dangers, they all, except Walter, vacated Florence’s room, and went down to the parlour.", "이른 시각이었으나, 솔 아저씨와 월터가 서로의 항해와 위험에 관해 한참 동안 캐묻고 난 뒤에, 그들은 월터만 빼고 모두 플로렌스의 방을 비우고 응접실로 내려갔다. 곧이어 월터가 그들과 합류하여, 플로렌스가 조금 서글프고 마음이 무거워져 잠자리에 들었다고 일러 주었다."], ["Here they were soon afterwards joined by Walter, who told them Florence was a little sorrowful and heavy-hearted, and had gone to bed.", "아래층에서 목소리를 낸들 그녀를 방해할 리는 없었으련만, 그 후로 그들은 모두 속삭이는 소리로 이야기했다. 그리고 저마다 제 나름의 방식으로 월터의 아리따운 어린 신부를 향해 무척 다정하고 부드러운 마음을 느꼈다."], ["Though they could not have disturbed her with their voices down there, they all spoke in a whisper after this: and each, in his different way, felt very lovingly and gently towards Walter’s fair young bride: and a long explanation there was of everything relating to her, for the satisfaction of Uncle Sol; and very sensible Mr Toots was of the delicacy with which Walter made his name and services important, and his presence necessary to their little council.", "솔 아저씨가 흡족하도록 그녀에 관한 모든 일에 대한 긴 설명이 있었다. 또 투츠 씨는, 월터가 자기 이름과 봉사를 중요하게, 또 자기 존재를 그 작은 회의에 꼭 필요하게 만들어 준 그 섬세함을 무척이나 절감했다."]], [["“Mr Toots,”", "\"투츠 씨,\""], ["said Walter, on parting with him at the house door, “we shall see each other to-morrow morning?”", "월터가 그와 집 문간에서 작별하며 말했다, \"우리 내일 아침에 보는 거지요?\""]], [["“Lieutenant Walters,”", "\"월터스 중위님,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, grasping his hand fervently, “I shall certainly be present.”", "투츠 씨가 그의 손을 뜨겁게 부여잡으며 대답했다, \"분명 참석하겠습니다.\""]], [["“This is the last night we shall meet for a long time—the last night we may ever meet,”", "\"오랫동안 우리가 만나는 건 오늘 밤이 마지막입니다—어쩌면 영영 만나는 마지막 밤일 수도 있고요,\""], ["said Walter.", "월터가 말했다."], ["“Such a noble heart as yours, must feel, I think, when another heart is bound to it.", "\"당신처럼 고결한 마음이라면, 내 생각에, 또 다른 마음이 거기에 묶일 때 무언가를 느끼지 않을 수 없을 겁니다."], ["I hope you know that I am very grateful to you?”", "내가 당신에게 무척 감사하고 있다는 걸 부디 알아주시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Walters,”", "\"월터스 씨,\""], ["replied Mr Toots, quite touched, “I should be glad to feel that you had reason to be so.”", "투츠 씨가 자못 감동하여 대답했다, \"당신이 그럴 만한 까닭이 있다는 걸 느낄 수 있다면 나도 기쁘겠습니다.\""]], [["“Florence,”", "\"플로렌스가,\""], ["said Walter, “on this last night of her bearing her own name, has made me promise—it was only just now, when you left us together—that I would tell you—with her dear love—”", "월터가 말했다, \"자기 본래의 이름을 지니는 이 마지막 밤에, 내게 약속을 시켰습니다—바로 방금, 당신이 우리 둘만 남겨 두고 갔을 때였지요—당신에게 전해 달라고 말입니다—그녀의 다정한 사랑과 더불어—\""]], [["Mr Toots laid his hand upon the doorpost, and his eyes upon his hand.", "투츠 씨는 문기둥에 손을 얹고, 그 손에 눈길을 떨구었다."]], [["“—With her dear love,”", "\"—그녀의 다정한 사랑과 더불어,\""], ["said Walter, “that she can never have a friend whom she will value above you.", "월터가 말했다, \"그녀가 당신보다 더 소중히 여길 친구는 결코 가질 수 없으리라고요."], ["That the recollection of your true consideration for her always, can never be forgotten by her.", "늘 자기를 진심으로 헤아려 준 당신을 향한 기억은 결코 잊을 수 없으리라고요."], ["That she remembers you in her prayers tonight, and hopes that you will think of her when she is far away.", "오늘 밤 기도 속에서 당신을 기억하며, 자기가 멀리 떠난 뒤에도 당신이 자기를 생각해 주기를 바란다고요."], ["Shall I say anything for you?”", "당신을 위해 제가 무어라 전할까요?\""]], [["“Say, Walter,”", "\"이렇게 전해 주십시오, 월터,\""], ["replied Mr Toots indistinctly, “that I shall think of her every day, but never without feeling happy to know that she is married to the man she loves, and who loves her.", "투츠 씨가 또렷하지 못한 소리로 대답했다, \"나는 날마다 그녀를 생각할 테지만, 그녀가 사랑하는, 또 그녀를 사랑하는 사람과 결혼했다는 걸 아는 데서 오는 행복을 느끼지 않고서는 결코 그러지 않으리라고요."], ["Say, if you please, that I am sure her husband deserves her—even her!—and that I am glad of her choice.”", "부디 이렇게도 전해 주십시오, 그녀의 남편이 그녀를—그녀만큼이나!—받을 자격이 있다고 나는 확신하며, 그녀의 선택이 기쁘다고요.\""]], [["Mr Toots got more distinct as he came to these last words, and raising his eyes from the doorpost, said them stoutly.", "투츠 씨는 이 마지막 말에 이르자 한결 또렷해져서, 문기둥에서 눈을 들어, 그 말을 꿋꿋이 내뱉었다."], ["He then shook Walter’s hand again with a fervour that Walter was not slow to return and started homeward.", "그러고 나서 그는 다시 한번 월터의 손을 뜨겁게 흔들었고, 월터도 지체 없이 그것에 화답했으며, 그는 집을 향해 길을 나섰다."]], [["Mr Toots was accompanied by the Chicken, whom he had of late brought with him every evening, and left in the shop, with an idea that unforeseen circumstances might arise from without, in which the prowess of that distinguished character would be of service to the Midshipman.", "투츠 씨에게는 치킨이 따라붙었는데, 그는 근래 저녁마다 그를 데리고 와 가게에 남겨 두었으니, 바깥에서 무슨 예기치 못한 사정이 생길 경우 그 걸출한 인물의 무용이 후보생관에 도움이 되리라는 생각에서였다."], ["The Chicken did not appear to be in a particularly good humour on this occasion.", "그런데 치킨은 이번에는 영 기분이 좋아 보이지 않았다."], ["Either the gas-lamps were treacherous, or he cocked his eye in a hideous manner, and likewise distorted his nose, when Mr Toots, crossing the road, looked back over his shoulder at the room where Florence slept.", "가스등이 사람을 속인 것이거나, 아니면 정말로, 투츠 씨가 길을 건너다 플로렌스가 잠든 방을 어깨 너머로 돌아보았을 때, 그는 흉측하게 눈을 치켜뜨고 코까지 일그러뜨렸다."], ["On the road home, he was more demonstrative of aggressive intentions against the other foot-passengers, than comported with a professor of the peaceful art of self-defence.", "집으로 가는 길에 그는, 평화로운 호신술의 사범답지 않게, 다른 길손들을 향한 공격적인 의도를 자못 노골적으로 드러냈다."], ["Arrived at home, instead of leaving Mr Toots in his apartments when he had escorted him thither, he remained before him weighing his white hat in both hands by the brim, and twitching his head and nose (both of which had been many times broken, and but indifferently repaired), with an air of decided disrespect.", "집에 다다라서는, 투츠 씨를 거처까지 모셔다 드린 뒤 그 방에 남겨 두고 가는 대신, 그 앞에 버티고 서서 흰 모자챙을 두 손으로 잡고 무게를 가늠하듯 흔들며, 머리와 코를(둘 다 여러 번 부러져 시원찮게 고쳐진 것이었다) 씰룩거렸는데, 그 태도에는 단연 무례한 기색이 어려 있었다."]], [["His patron being much engaged with his own thoughts, did not observe this for some time, nor indeed until the Chicken, determined not to be overlooked, had made divers clicking sounds with his tongue and teeth, to attract attention.", "그의 후원자는 제 생각에 푹 잠겨 있던 터라 한동안 이것을 알아채지 못했고, 사실 치킨이 결코 무시당하지 않겠다고 작정하고 혀와 이로 여러 번 딱딱 소리를 내어 주의를 끌고 나서야 비로소 알아챘다."]], [["“Now, Master,”", "\"자, 나리,\""], ["said the Chicken, doggedly, when he, at length, caught Mr Toots’s eye, “I want to know whether this here gammon is to finish it, or whether you’re a going in to win?”", "치킨이 마침내 투츠 씨의 눈길을 붙들자 무뚝뚝하게 말했다, \"이 사기 노름을 여기서 끝장낼 작정이신지, 아니면 이겨 보러 들어가실 작정이신지 알고 싶습니다요.\""]], [["“Chicken,”", "\"치킨,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, “explain yourself.”", "투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"분명히 말해 보게.\""]], [["“Why then, here’s all about it, Master,”", "\"그럼, 사정인즉 이렇습니다요, 나리,\""], ["said the Chicken.", "치킨이 말했다."], ["“I ain’t a cove to chuck a word away.", "\"저는 말 한마디도 헛되이 버리는 위인이 아니거든요."], ["Here’s wot it is.", "사정은 이렇습죠."], ["Are any on ’em to be doubled up?”", "그놈들 중에 누구라도 떡이 되게 패 줄 작정이신지요?\""]], [["When the Chicken put this question he dropped his hat, made a dodge and a feint with his left hand, hit a supposed enemy a violent blow with his right, shook his head smartly, and recovered himself.", "이 질문을 던지면서 치킨은 모자를 떨어뜨리고, 왼손으로 슬쩍 피하고 찌르는 시늉을 하더니, 오른손으로 가상의 적에게 맹렬한 일격을 날리고, 머리를 날렵하게 흔든 뒤, 자세를 추슬렀다."]], [["“Come, Master,”", "\"자, 나리,\""], ["said the Chicken.", "치킨이 말했다."], ["“Is it to be gammon or pluck?", "\"사기로 가실 겁니까, 배짱으로 가실 겁니까?"], ["Which?”", "어느 쪽입니까?\""]], [["“Chicken,”", "\"치킨,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, “your expressions are coarse, and your meaning is obscure.”", "투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"자네 표현은 거칠고, 뜻은 알쏭달쏭하군.\""]], [["“Why, then, I tell you what, Master,”", "\"그럼, 이렇게 말씀드리죠, 나리,\" 치킨이 말했다."], ["said the Chicken.", "\"사정은 이렇다는 겁니다."], ["“This is where it is. It’s mean.”", "그건 치사한 짓이라고요.\""]], [["“What is mean, Chicken?”", "\"치사한 게 뭐란 말인가, 치킨?\""], ["asked Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 물었다."]], [["“It is,”", "\"이게 그렇다 이겁니다,\""], ["said the Chicken, with a frightful corrugation of his broken nose.", "치킨이 부러진 코를 무시무시하게 찡그리며 말했다. \"거 보십쇼!"], ["“There! Now, Master!", "자, 나리!"], ["Wot!", "아니!"], ["When you could go and blow on this here match to the stiff’un;”", "나리께서 이 혼사를 그 영감탱이한테,\""], ["by which depreciatory appellation it has been since supposed that the Game One intended to signify Mr Dombey; “and when you could knock the winner and all the kit of ’em dead out o’ wind and time, are you going to give in?", "이 깔보는 호칭으로 그 시합꾼이 돔비 씨를 가리키려 한 것이라고 그 후 짐작되었다, \"고자질해서 깨 버릴 수도 있는데다, 그 신랑 놈이며 그 패거리 죄다 숨도 못 쉬고 정신도 못 차리게 뻗어 버리게 만들 수도 있는데, 그냥 손 들고 마실 작정입니까?"], ["To give in?”", "손을 들어요?\""], ["said the Chicken, with contemptuous emphasis.", "치킨이 경멸하듯 힘주어 말했다."], ["“Wy, it’s mean!”", "\"허, 그건 치사한 짓이라고요!\""]], [["“Chicken,”", "\"치킨,\""], ["said Mr Toots, severely, “you’re a perfect Vulture!", "투츠 씨가 엄하게 말했다, \"자네는 영락없는 독수리로군!"], ["Your sentiments are atrocious.”", "자네 속내는 흉악하기 짝이 없네.\""]], [["“My sentiments is Game and Fancy, Master,”", "\"제 속내는 배짱과 멋입니다요, 나리,\""], ["returned the Chicken.", "치킨이 대답했다."], ["“That’s wot my sentiments is.", "\"그게 바로 제 속내라고요."], ["I can’t abear a meanness.", "저는 치사한 짓은 못 봐 줍니다."], ["I’m afore the public, I’m to be heerd on at the bar of the Little Helephant, and no Gov’ner o’ mine mustn’t go and do what’s mean.", "저는 대중 앞에 나서는 몸이고, 리틀 헬레펀트 술집 카운터에서 제 소문이 자자한 몸인데, 제 주인 양반이 가서 치사한 짓을 하면 안 되지요."], ["Wy, it’s mean,”", "허, 그건 치사한 짓이라니까요,\""], ["said the Chicken, with increased expression.", "치킨이 한층 힘주어 말했다. \"사정이 그렇다는 겁니다."], ["“That’s where it is. It’s mean.”", "그건 치사한 짓이에요.\""]], [["“Chicken,”", "\"치킨,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “you disgust me.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"자네는 정나미가 떨어지는군.\""]], [["“Master,”", "\"나리,\""], ["returned the Chicken, putting on his hat, “there’s a pair on us, then.", "치킨이 모자를 쓰며 대답했다, \"그럼 피차 마찬가지입니다요."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Here’s a offer!", "제안 하나 하죠!"], ["You’ve spoke to me more than once”t or twice’t about the public line.", "나리께서 술집 차리는 일을 두고 한두 번 저한테 말씀하신 적이 있잖습니까."], ["Never mind!", "됐고요!"], ["Give me a fi’typunnote to-morrow, and let me go.”", "내일 저한테 오십 파운드짜리 한 장 주시고, 절 보내 주십쇼.\""]], [["“Chicken,”", "\"치킨,\""], ["returned Mr Toots, “after the odious sentiments you have expressed, I shall be glad to part on such terms.”", "투츠 씨가 대답했다, \"자네가 토해 낸 그 역겨운 속내를 듣고 나니, 그런 조건으로 헤어지게 되어 차라리 기쁘군.\""]], [["“Done then,” said the Chicken.", "\"그럼 결정 났습니다,\""], ["“It’s a bargain.", "치킨이 말했다."], ["This here conduct of yourn won’t suit my book, Master.", "\"거래 성사입죠. 나리의 이런 처신은 제 장부에 맞지가 않아요."], ["Wy, it’s mean,”", "허, 그건 치사한 짓이라고요,\""], ["said the Chicken; who seemed equally unable to get beyond that point, and to stop short of it.", "치킨이 말했는데, 그는 그 대목을 넘어서지도, 또 그 못 미쳐 멈추지도 못하는 듯했다. \"사정이 그렇다는 겁니다."], ["“That’s where it is; it’s mean!”", "그건 치사한 짓이에요!\""]], [["So Mr Toots and the Chicken agreed to part on this incompatibility of moral perception; and Mr Toots lying down to sleep, dreamed happily of Florence, who had thought of him as her friend upon the last night of her maiden life, and who had sent him her dear love.", "이리하여 투츠 씨와 치킨은 이 도덕적 인식의 양립 불가를 이유로 헤어지기로 합의했다. 그리고 투츠 씨는 잠자리에 누워, 자기 처녀 시절의 마지막 밤에 자기를 친구로 생각해 주고, 또 자기에게 다정한 사랑을 보내 준 플로렌스의 행복한 꿈을 꾸었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LVII. Another Wedding", "# 제52장"]], [["Mr Sownds the beadle, and Mrs Miff the pew-opener, are early at their posts in the fine church where Mr Dombey was married.", "교구지기 사운즈 씨와 좌석 안내인 미프 부인은 돔비 씨가 결혼식을 올렸던 그 훌륭한 교회의 제자리에 일찌감치 와 있다."], ["A yellow-faced old gentleman from India, is going to take unto himself a young wife this morning, and six carriages full of company are expected, and Mrs Miff has been informed that the yellow-faced old gentleman could pave the road to church with diamonds and hardly miss them.", "인도에서 온 누런 얼굴의 노신사가 오늘 아침 젊은 아내를 맞아들이려 하고 있으며, 하객을 가득 태운 마차 여섯 대가 올 것으로 예상되는 데다, 미프 부인은 그 누런 얼굴의 노신사가 교회까지 가는 길을 다이아몬드로 포장하고도 그것을 거의 아쉬워하지 않을 정도라는 이야기를 들은 터다."]], [["The nuptial benediction is to be a superior one, proceeding from a very reverend, a dean, and the lady is to be given away, as an extraordinary present, by somebody who comes express from the Horse Guards.", "혼인의 축도는 한층 격이 높은 것으로, 매우 존귀하신 한 분, 즉 주임 사제께서 집전하실 예정이며, 신부는 특별한 선물처럼 호스 가즈에서 일부러 온 어떤 사람의 손에 이끌려 넘겨질 것이다. * 호스 가즈: 런던에 있던 영국 육군 본부."]], [["Mrs Miff is more intolerant of common people this morning, than she generally is; and she has always strong opinions on that subject, for it is associated with free sittings.", "미프 부인은 오늘 아침 평소보다 더 평범한 사람들을 못마땅해한다. 그리고 그녀는 그 문제에 늘 강경한 견해를 갖고 있는데, 그것이 무료 좌석과 결부되어 있기 때문이다."], ["Mrs Miff is not a student of political economy (she thinks the science is connected with dissenters; “Baptists or Wesleyans, or some o’ them,”", "미프 부인은 정치경제학을 공부한 사람은 아니지만(그 학문이 비국교도들과 관련 있다고 여긴다."], ["she says), but she can never understand what business your common folks have to be married. “Drat ’em,”", "\"침례교도나 웨슬리교도, 뭐 그런 부류들 말이지\"라고 그녀는 말한다), 도대체 평범한 사람들이 무슨 자격으로 결혼을 하는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없다."], ["says Mrs Miff “you read the same things over ’em and instead of sovereigns get sixpences!”", "\"쳇, 망할 것들,\" 미프 부인이 말한다, \"그 사람들 머리 위에서나 똑같은 말을 읽어 주는데, 받는 건 금화 대신 6펜스라니까!\""]], [["Mr Sownds the beadle is more liberal than Mrs Miff—but then he is not a pew-opener.", "교구지기 사운즈 씨는 미프 부인보다 너그러운 편이다—하기야 그는 좌석 안내인은 아니니까."], ["“It must be done, Ma’am,”", "\"해야 할 일이지요, 부인,\""], ["he says. “We must marry ’em.", "그가 말한다."], ["We must have our national schools to walk at the head of, and we must have our standing armies.", "\"우리가 그 사람들을 결혼시켜 줘야지요. 우리한테는 앞장서서 행진할 국민학교도 있어야 하고, 상비군도 있어야 하니까요."], ["We must marry ’em, Ma’am,”", "그 사람들을 결혼시켜 줘야지요, 부인,\""], ["says Mr Sownds, “and keep the country going.”", "사운즈 씨가 말한다, \"그래야 나라가 굴러가지요.\""]], [["Mr Sownds is sitting on the steps and Mrs Miff is dusting in the church, when a young couple, plainly dressed, come in.", "사운즈 씨가 계단에 앉아 있고 미프 부인이 교회 안에서 먼지를 털고 있을 때, 수수한 차림의 젊은 한 쌍이 들어온다."], ["The mortified bonnet of Mrs Miff is sharply turned towards them, for she espies in this early visit indications of a runaway match.", "미프 부인의 풀 죽은 보닛이 그들을 향해 매섭게 돌아간다. 이렇게 이른 방문에서 그녀는 야반도주 결혼의 낌새를 알아챈 것이다."], ["But they don’t want to be married—“Only,”", "그러나 그들은 결혼하려는 게 아니다—\"그저,\""], ["says the gentleman, “to walk round the church.”", "신사가 말한다, \"교회를 한 바퀴 둘러보고 싶을 뿐입니다.\""], ["And as he slips a genteel compliment into the palm of Mrs Miff, her vinegary face relaxes, and her mortified bonnet and her spare dry figure dip and crackle.", "그가 미프 부인의 손바닥에 점잖은 사례를 슬쩍 쥐여 주자, 그녀의 식초처럼 시큼한 얼굴이 누그러지고, 풀 죽은 보닛과 비쩍 마른 몸이 까딱거리며 바스락거린다."]], [["Mrs Miff resumes her dusting and plumps up her cushions—for the yellow-faced old gentleman is reported to have tender knees—but keeps her glazed, pew-opening eye on the young couple who are walking round the church.", "미프 부인은 다시 먼지를 털고 방석을 두툼하게 부풀린다—그 누런 얼굴의 노신사가 무릎이 약하다는 소문이 있기 때문이다—그러면서도 교회를 둘러보고 있는 젊은 한 쌍에게서 좌석 안내인 특유의 흐리멍덩한 눈길을 떼지 않는다. \"에헴,\""], ["“Ahem,”", "미프 부인이 헛기침을 한다."], ["coughs Mrs Miff whose cough is drier than the hay in any hassock in her charge, “you’ll come to us one of these mornings, my dears, unless I’m much mistaken!”", "그녀의 기침은 자기 관할의 어떤 무릎방석에 든 건초보다도 메마르다. \"조만간 어느 아침에 우리한테 오시겠구먼, 얘들아, 내 짐작이 크게 틀리지 않다면 말이지!\""]], [["They are looking at a tablet on the wall, erected to the memory of someone dead.", "그들은 죽은 누군가를 기리기 위해 세워진 벽의 명판을 바라보고 있다."], ["They are a long way off from Mrs Miff, but Mrs Miff can see with half an eye how she is leaning on his arm, and how his head is bent down over her.", "미프 부인에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 있지만, 미프 부인은 한쪽 눈으로도 그녀가 어떻게 그의 팔에 기대고 있는지, 그의 머리가 어떻게 그녀 위로 숙여져 있는지 알아볼 수 있다."], ["“Well, well,”", "\"그래, 그래,\""], ["says Mrs Miff, “you might do worse.", "미프 부인이 말한다, \"이보다 못한 짝을 만날 수도 있었지."], ["For you’re a tidy pair!”", "둘이 참 단정한 한 쌍이니까!\""]], [["There is nothing personal in Mrs Miff’s remark.", "미프 부인의 말에 사사로운 뜻은 없다."], ["She merely speaks of stock-in-trade.", "그녀는 그저 자기 장사 밑천에 대해 말할 뿐이다."], ["She is hardly more curious in couples than in coffins.", "그녀는 한 쌍에 대해 관을 두고 갖는 것 이상의 호기심을 거의 품지 않는다."], ["She is such a spare, straight, dry old lady—such a pew of a woman—that you should find as many individual sympathies in a chip.", "그녀는 어찌나 비쩍 마르고 꼿꼿하고 메마른 노부인인지—어찌나 좌석 같은 여인인지—나무토막에서 그만큼의 개인적 정감이라도 찾아내는 편이 나을 정도다."], ["Mr Sownds, now, who is fleshy, and has scarlet in his coat, is of a different temperament.", "그런데 살집이 있고 외투에 진홍색을 두른 사운즈 씨는 기질이 다르다."], ["He says, as they stand upon the steps watching the young couple away, that she has a pretty figure, hasn’t she, and as well as he could see (for she held her head down coming out), an uncommon pretty face.", "그는 둘이 계단에 서서 젊은 한 쌍이 멀어져 가는 모습을 지켜보며, 저 여자는 몸매가 예쁘지 않냐고, 그리고 자기가 본 한에서는(나오면서 고개를 숙이고 있었으니까) 보기 드물게 예쁜 얼굴이라고 말한다."], ["“Altogether, Mrs Miff,”", "\"통틀어 말이지요, 미프 부인,\""], ["says Mr Sownds with a relish, “she is what you may call a rose-bud.”", "사운즈 씨가 음미하듯 말한다, \"저 여자는 그야말로 막 피어나는 장미꽃봉오리라고 할 만하지요.\""]], [["Mrs Miff assents with a spare nod of her mortified bonnet; but approves of this so little, that she inwardly resolves she wouldn’t be the wife of Mr Sownds for any money he could give her, Beadle as he is.", "미프 부인은 풀 죽은 보닛을 비쩍 마르게 한 번 끄덕여 동의를 표하지만, 이 말이 어찌나 마음에 들지 않는지, 속으로는 사운즈 씨가 아무리 큰돈을 준다 해도, 교구지기이긴 하지만, 절대 그의 아내가 되지는 않겠다고 다짐한다."]], [["And what are the young couple saying as they leave the church, and go out at the gate?", "그러면 교회를 떠나 문밖으로 나서는 젊은 한 쌍은 무슨 이야기를 나누고 있는가?"]], [["“Dear Walter, thank you!", "\"고마워요, 월터!"], ["I can go away, now, happy.”", "이제 나는 행복하게 떠날 수 있어요.\""]], [["“And when we come back, Florence, we will come and see his grave again.”", "\"그리고 돌아오면, 플로렌스, 우리 다시 와서 그의 무덤을 보러 가요.\""]], [["Florence lifts her eyes, so bright with tears, to his kind face; and clasps her disengaged hand on that other modest little hand which clasps his arm.", "플로렌스는 눈물로 반짝이는 눈을 들어 그의 다정한 얼굴을 바라본다. 그러고는 그의 팔을 붙잡고 있는 그 다른 작고 얌전한 손 위에, 비어 있는 손을 포개어 꼭 쥔다."]], [["“It is very early, Walter, and the streets are almost empty yet.", "\"아직 이른 시간이에요, 월터, 거리도 거의 텅 비어 있고요."], ["Let us walk.”", "걸어가요.\""]], [["“But you will be so tired, my love.”", "\"하지만 당신이 너무 지칠 거예요, 내 사랑.\""]], [["“Oh no!", "\"오, 아니에요!"], ["I was very tired the first time that we ever walked together, but I shall not be so today.”", "우리가 맨 처음 함께 걸었을 때는 정말 많이 지쳤지만, 오늘은 그렇지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["And thus—not much changed—she, as innocent and earnest-hearted—he, as frank, as hopeful, and more proud of her—Florence and Walter, on their bridal morning, walk through the streets together.", "이렇게—그다지 변하지 않은 채—그녀는, 그때처럼 순수하고 진실한 마음으로—그는, 그때처럼 솔직하고 희망에 차서, 그리고 그녀를 더욱 자랑스러워하며—플로렌스와 월터는, 결혼식 아침에, 함께 거리를 걸어간다."]], [["Not even in that childish walk of long ago, were they so far removed from all the world about them as today.", "오래전 그 어린 시절의 산책에서조차, 그들이 주위의 온 세상으로부터 오늘만큼 멀리 떨어져 있던 적은 없었다."], ["The childish feet of long ago, did not tread such enchanted ground as theirs do now.", "오래전 그 어린 발들은 지금 그들의 발이 밟는 것 같은 마법에 걸린 땅을 밟아 본 적이 없었다."], ["The confidence and love of children may be given many times, and will spring up in many places; but the woman’s heart of Florence, with its undivided treasure, can be yielded only once, and under slight or change, can only droop and die.", "아이들의 신뢰와 사랑은 여러 번 주어질 수 있고, 여러 곳에서 돋아날 수 있다. 그러나 그 나뉘지 않은 보물을 간직한 플로렌스의 여인다운 마음은 단 한 번만 내어줄 수 있으며, 멸시나 변심을 만나면 시들어 죽어 갈 뿐이다."]], [["They take the streets that are the quietest, and do not go near that in which her old home stands.", "그들은 가장 한적한 거리를 골라, 그녀의 옛집이 서 있는 거리 근처로는 가지 않는다."], ["It is a fair, warm summer morning, and the sun shines on them, as they walk towards the darkening mist that overspreads the City.", "화창하고 따뜻한 여름 아침이라, 그들이 도심을 뒤덮은 어두워지는 안개를 향해 걸어가는 동안 해가 그들을 비춘다."], ["Riches are uncovering in shops; jewels, gold, and silver flash in the goldsmith’s sunny windows; and great houses cast a stately shade upon them as they pass.", "상점에서는 재물이 모습을 드러내고, 보석과 금과 은이 금세공인의 햇빛 드는 진열창에서 번득이며, 그들이 지나갈 때 큰 저택들이 위풍당당한 그늘을 그들 위로 드리운다."], ["But through the light, and through the shade, they go on lovingly together, lost to everything around; thinking of no other riches, and no prouder home, than they have now in one another.", "그러나 그 빛 속을 지나, 그 그늘 속을 지나, 그들은 주위의 모든 것을 잊은 채 다정히 함께 걸어간다. 지금 서로의 안에 지닌 것보다 더한 재물도, 더 자랑스러운 보금자리도 생각하지 않으면서."]], [["Gradually they come into the darker, narrower streets, where the sun, now yellow, and now red, is seen through the mist, only at street corners, and in small open spaces where there is a tree, or one of the innumerable churches, or a paved way and a flight of steps, or a curious little patch of garden, or a burying-ground, where the few tombs and tombstones are almost black.", "차츰 그들은 더 어둡고 더 좁은 거리로 접어드는데, 거기서는 이제 누렇기도 하고 붉기도 한 해가 안개 사이로, 오직 길모퉁이에서나, 나무 한 그루가 있거나 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 교회 가운데 하나가 있거나 포장된 길과 층계가 있거나 묘한 작은 텃밭 한 뙈기가 있거나 묘지가 있는 작고 트인 공간에서만 보인다. 그 묘지에는 몇 안 되는 무덤과 묘비가 거의 새카맣게 변해 있다."], ["Lovingly and trustfully, through all the narrow yards and alleys and the shady streets, Florence goes, clinging to his arm, to be his wife.", "다정하게 그리고 믿음으로, 그 모든 좁은 마당과 골목과 그늘진 거리를 지나, 플로렌스는 그의 아내가 되기 위해 그의 팔에 매달려 걸어간다."]], [["Her heart beats quicker now, for Walter tells her that their church is very near.", "이제 그녀의 가슴이 더 빨리 뛴다. 월터가 그들의 교회가 아주 가깝다고 말해 주기 때문이다."], ["They pass a few great stacks of warehouses, with waggons at the doors, and busy carmen stopping up the way—but Florence does not see or hear them—and then the air is quiet, and the day is darkened, and she is trembling in a church which has a strange smell like a cellar.", "그들은 문 앞에 짐마차가 늘어서고 분주한 짐꾼들이 길을 막고 있는 커다란 창고 무더기 몇 채를 지나는데—플로렌스는 그것을 보지도 듣지도 못한다—이윽고 공기가 고요해지고, 날이 어두워지며, 그녀는 지하실 같은 묘한 냄새가 나는 교회 안에서 떨고 있다."]], [["The shabby little old man, ringer of the disappointed bell, is standing in the porch, and has put his hat in the font—for he is quite at home there, being sexton.", "실망스러운 종을 울리는 종지기인 초라하고 작은 노인이 현관에 서서, 모자를 세례반 안에 넣어 두었다—그는 교회지기여서 그곳이 아주 제집처럼 편하기 때문이다."], ["He ushers them into an old brown, panelled, dusty vestry, like a corner-cupboard with the shelves taken out; where the wormy registers diffuse a smell like faded snuff, which has set the tearful Nipper sneezing.", "그는 그들을 낡고 갈색이며 판자를 덧댄 먼지투성이 제의실로 안내하는데, 그곳은 선반을 들어낸 모서리 찬장 같다. 거기 좀먹은 등록부들이 빛바랜 코담배 같은 냄새를 풍기는 바람에 눈물 글썽이던 니퍼가 재채기를 했다."]], [["Youthful, and how beautiful, the young bride looks, in this old dusty place, with no kindred object near her but her husband.", "이 낡고 먼지투성이인 곳에서, 곁에 자기 남편 말고는 마음 붙일 대상 하나 없이 선 젊은 신부는 어쩌면 그토록 앳되고 또 아름다운지."], ["There is a dusty old clerk, who keeps a sort of evaporated news shop underneath an archway opposite, behind a perfect fortification of posts.", "맞은편 아치 길 아래, 완벽한 기둥 요새 뒤편에서 김빠진 신문 가게 비슷한 것을 꾸려 가는 먼지투성이 늙은 사무원이 있다."], ["There is a dusty old pew-opener who only keeps herself, and finds that quite enough to do.", "오직 자기 한 몸만 건사하면서도 그것만으로도 할 일이 충분하다고 여기는 먼지투성이 늙은 좌석 안내인이 있다."], ["There is a dusty old beadle (these are Mr Toots’s beadle and pew-opener of last Sunday), who has something to do with a Worshipful Company who have got a Hall in the next yard, with a stained-glass window in it that no mortal ever saw.", "옆 마당에 회관을 가진 어느 명예로운 동업조합과 무슨 관련이 있는 먼지투성이 늙은 교구지기가 있는데(이들은 지난 일요일 토츠 씨가 만난 그 교구지기와 좌석 안내인이다), 그 회관에는 살아 있는 사람이라면 아무도 본 적 없는 스테인드글라스 창이 하나 있다."], ["There are dusty wooden ledges and cornices poked in and out over the altar, and over the screen and round the gallery, and over the inscription about what the Master and Wardens of the Worshipful Company did in one thousand six hundred and ninety-four.", "제단 위로, 칸막이 위로, 회랑 둘레로, 그리고 천육백구십사 년에 그 명예로운 동업조합의 회장과 간사들이 한 일에 관한 명문 위로, 먼지투성이 나무 선반과 처마 장식들이 들쭉날쭉 삐죽삐죽 끼어 있다."], ["There are dusty old sounding-boards over the pulpit and reading-desk, looking like lids to be let down on the officiating ministers in case of their giving offence.", "설교단과 독경대 위로는 먼지투성이 낡은 음향판들이 있는데, 마치 집전하는 목사들이 무례한 짓을 할 경우 그들 위로 떨어뜨릴 뚜껑처럼 보인다."], ["There is every possible provision for the accommodation of dust, except in the churchyard, where the facilities in that respect are very limited.", "먼지를 수용할 온갖 가능한 시설이 다 갖추어져 있는데, 다만 묘지만은 예외여서, 그 점에서는 시설이 매우 한정되어 있다."]], [["The Captain, Uncle Sol, and Mr Toots are come; the clergyman is putting on his surplice in the vestry, while the clerk walks round him, blowing the dust off it; and the bride and bridegroom stand before the altar. There is no bridesmaid, unless Susan Nipper is one; and no better father than Captain Cuttle. A man with a wooden leg, chewing a faint apple and carrying a blue bag in has hand, looks in to see what is going on; but finding it nothing entertaining, stumps off again, and pegs his way among the echoes out of doors.", "선장과 솔 삼촌, 그리고 토츠 씨가 왔다. 목사는 제의실에서 법의를 걸치고 있고, 사무원은 그 둘레를 돌며 거기 묻은 먼지를 불어 낸다. 그리고 신랑과 신부는 제단 앞에 선다. 수전 니퍼를 들러리로 친다면 모를까, 들러리는 없다. 그리고 커틀 선장보다 더 나은 신부 측 아버지 역도 없다. 나무다리를 단 한 사내가, 시들한 사과를 우물거리며 한 손에 파란 자루를 들고, 무슨 일이 벌어지나 보려고 안을 들여다본다. 그러나 별로 흥미로운 게 없다는 걸 알고는 다시 쿵쿵 걸어 나가, 문밖 메아리들 사이로 또각또각 길을 뚫고 간다."]], [["No gracious ray of light is seen to fall on Florence, kneeling at the altar with her timid head bowed down.", "제단 앞에 겁먹은 머리를 숙인 채 무릎 꿇은 플로렌스 위로는 자비로운 한 줄기 빛도 비치는 것이 보이지 않는다. 아침의 발광체는 가로막혀 있어 그곳을 비추지 않는다."], ["The morning luminary is built out, and don’t shine there.", "밖에는 참새들이 조금씩 짹짹거리는 앙상한 나무 한 그루가 있고, 창 맞은편 염색업자의 다락방, 햇빛이 작은 구멍처럼 든 곳에는 검은 새 한 마리가 있어 예식이 진행되는 내내 큰 소리로 휘파람을 분다."], ["There is a meagre tree outside, where the sparrows are chirping a little; and there is a blackbird in an eyelet-hole of sun in a dyer’s garret, over against the window, who whistles loudly whilst the service is performing; and there is the man with the wooden leg stumping away.", "그리고 나무다리를 단 사내가 쿵쿵 걸어 멀어져 간다. 먼지투성이 사무원의 아멘 소리는, 맥베스의 그것처럼, 목구멍에 조금 걸리는 듯하다."], ["The amens of the dusty clerk appear, like Macbeth’s, to stick in his throat a little; but Captain Cuttle helps him out, and does it with so much goodwill that he interpolates three entirely new responses of that word, never introduced into the service before.", "그러나 커틀 선장이 그를 거들어 주는데, 어찌나 선의가 넘치게 하는지 그는 그 단어로 된 전에 없던 응답을 셋이나 새로이 끼워 넣는다. 그런 응답은 이전까지 예식에 한 번도 도입된 적이 없는 것이다."]], [["They are married, and have signed their names in one of the old sneezy registers, and the clergyman’s surplice is restored to the dust, and the clergyman is gone home.", "그들은 혼인을 맺고, 재채기를 부르는 낡은 등록부 가운데 하나에 이름을 서명했으며, 목사의 법의는 다시 먼지 속으로 돌려졌고, 목사는 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["In a dark corner of the dark church, Florence has turned to Susan Nipper, and is weeping in her arms.", "어두운 교회의 어두운 구석에서, 플로렌스는 수전 니퍼에게로 돌아서서 그녀의 품에 안겨 흐느끼고 있다."], ["Mr Toots’s eyes are red.", "토츠 씨의 눈은 벌겋다."], ["The Captain lubricates his nose.", "선장은 자기 코를 문질러 댄다."], ["Uncle Sol has pulled down his spectacles from his forehead, and walked out to the door.", "솔 삼촌은 이마에 올려 두었던 안경을 끌어내리고는 문가로 걸어 나갔다."]], [["“God bless you, Susan; dearest Susan!", "\"네게 신의 가호가 있기를, 수전, 사랑하는 수전!"], ["If you ever can bear witness to the love I have for Walter, and the reason that I have to love him, do it for his sake.", "내가 월터를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 그리고 그를 사랑할 만한 까닭이 내게 얼마나 많은지 언젠가 네가 증언할 수 있게 된다면, 그를 위해 그렇게 해 주렴."], ["Good-bye!", "잘 있어!"], ["Good-bye!”", "잘 있어!\""]], [["They have thought it better not to go back to the Midshipman, but to part so; a coach is waiting for them, near at hand.", "그들은 미드십맨으로 돌아가기보다 이대로 헤어지는 편이 낫겠다고 생각해 둔 터다. 마차 한 대가 바로 근처에서 그들을 기다리고 있다."]], [["Miss Nipper cannot speak; she only sobs and chokes, and hugs her mistress.", "니퍼 양은 말을 잇지 못한다. 그저 흐느끼고 목이 메어, 아씨를 끌어안을 뿐이다."], ["Mr Toots advances, urges her to cheer up, and takes charge of her.", "토츠 씨가 다가와, 그녀에게 기운을 내라고 권하며 그녀를 맡아 돌본다."], ["Florence gives him her hand—gives him, in the fulness of her heart, her lips—kisses Uncle Sol, and Captain Cuttle, and is borne away by her young husband.", "플로렌스는 그에게 손을 내밀고—벅찬 마음에 그에게 입술까지 내어 주고—솔 삼촌과 커틀 선장에게 입을 맞춘 뒤, 젊은 남편에게 이끌려 떠나간다."]], [["But Susan cannot bear that Florence should go away with a mournful recollection of her.", "그러나 수전은 플로렌스가 자기에 대한 슬픈 기억을 안고 떠나는 것을 견딜 수가 없다."], ["She had meant to be so different, that she reproaches herself bitterly.", "자기는 이렇게가 아니라 전혀 다르게 굴려고 했었던 터라, 스스로를 모질게 자책한다."], ["Intent on making one last effort to redeem her character, she breaks from Mr Toots and runs away to find the coach, and show a parting smile.", "자기 체면을 만회하려고 마지막 한 번의 노력을 기울이기로 작정하고, 그녀는 토츠 씨에게서 벗어나 마차를 찾아내어 작별의 미소를 보여 주려고 달려간다."], ["The Captain, divining her object, sets off after her; for he feels it his duty also to dismiss them with a cheer, if possible.", "그녀의 의도를 알아챈 선장이 그녀를 뒤쫓아 나선다. 그 역시 가능하다면 그들을 환호로 배웅하는 것이 자기 의무라고 느끼기 때문이다."], ["Uncle Sol and Mr Toots are left behind together, outside the church, to wait for them.", "솔 삼촌과 토츠 씨는 둘이 함께 교회 밖에 남아 그들을 기다린다."]], [["The coach is gone, but the street is steep, and narrow, and blocked up, and Susan can see it at a stand-still in the distance, she is sure.", "마차는 떠났지만, 거리가 가파르고 좁고 막혀 있어서, 수전은 저 멀리 마차가 멈춰 서 있는 것을 볼 수 있다고, 그렇게 확신한다."], ["Captain Cuttle follows her as she flies down the hill, and waves his glazed hat as a general signal, which may attract the right coach and which may not.", "커틀 선장이 언덕을 내달리는 그녀를 뒤따르며, 광택 나는 모자를 흔들어 전체에게 보내는 신호로 삼는데, 그 신호가 맞는 마차를 끌어들일 수도 있고 그러지 못할 수도 있다."]], [["Susan outstrips the Captain, and comes up with it.", "수전이 선장을 앞질러, 마차를 따라잡는다."], ["She looks in at the window, sees Walter, with the gentle face beside him, and claps her hands and screams:", "그녀가 창 안을 들여다보니, 월터가 보이고, 그 곁에 다정한 얼굴이 보인다. 그녀는 두 손을 마주치며 외친다."]], [["“Miss Floy, my darling!", "\"플로이 아가씨, 내 사랑!"], ["look at me!", "저를 봐요!"], ["We are all so happy now, dear!", "우리 모두 이제 이렇게 행복해요, 아가씨!"], ["One more good-bye, my precious, one more!”", "한 번만 더 작별 인사를요, 내 보배 아가씨, 한 번만 더요!\""]], [["How Susan does it, she don’t know, but she reaches to the window, kisses her, and has her arms about her neck, in a moment.", "수전이 어떻게 그렇게 하는지는 자기도 모르지만, 어느새 창문에 닿아, 아가씨에게 입을 맞추고, 두 팔로 그 목을 감싸 안는다."]], [["“We are all so—so happy now, my dear Miss Floy!”", "\"우리 모두 이제 이렇게—이렇게 행복해요, 사랑하는 플로이 아가씨!\""], ["says Susan, with a suspicious catching in her breath.", "수전이 수상쩍게 숨을 들이켜며 말한다."], ["“You, you won’t be angry with me now.", "\"아가씨는, 아가씨는 이제 저한테 화나지 않으셨죠."], ["Now will you?”", "그렇죠?\""]], [["“Angry, Susan!”", "\"화라니, 수전!\""]], [["“No, no; I am sure you won’t.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요. 화 안 나신 거 알아요."], ["I say you won’t, my pet, my dearest!”", "화 안 나신 거 안다고요, 내 귀염둥이, 내 사랑하는 아가씨!\""], ["exclaims Susan; “and here’s the Captain too—your friend the Captain, you know—to say good-bye once more!”", "수전이 소리친다. \"그리고 여기 선장님도 오셨어요—아가씨 친구인 선장님 말이에요—한 번 더 작별 인사를 하러요!\""]], [["“Hooroar, my Heart’s Delight!”", "\"만세, 내 마음의 기쁨이여!\""], ["vociferates the Captain, with a countenance of strong emotion.", "선장이 격한 감정이 어린 얼굴로 목청껏 외친다."], ["“Hooroar, Wal”r my lad.", "\"만세, 월터 내 아들아."], ["Hooroar!", "만세!"], ["Hooroar!”", "만세!\""]], [["What with the young husband at one window, and the young wife at the other; the Captain hanging on at this door, and Susan Nipper holding fast by that; the coach obliged to go on whether it will or no, and all the other carts and coaches turbulent because it hesitates; there never was so much confusion on four wheels.", "한쪽 창에는 젊은 남편이, 다른 쪽 창에는 젊은 아내가 있고, 이쪽 문에는 선장이 매달려 있고, 저쪽은 수전 니퍼가 꼭 붙들고 있고, 마차는 가든 안 가든 가야만 하는 처지인 데다, 마차가 머뭇거리는 통에 다른 모든 짐마차와 마차들이 소란스러우니, 네 바퀴 위에 이만한 혼란이 일었던 적은 일찍이 없었다."], ["But Susan Nipper gallantly maintains her point.", "그러나 수전 니퍼는 용감하게 자기 자리를 지킨다."], ["She keeps a smiling face upon her mistress, smiling through her tears, until the last.", "그녀는 마지막 순간까지 눈물을 흘리면서도 미소를 지으며, 아씨를 향해 웃는 얼굴을 잃지 않는다."], ["Even when she is left behind, the Captain continues to appear and disappear at the door, crying “Hooroar, my lad!", "마침내 그녀가 뒤에 처졌을 때에도, 선장은 셔츠 깃을 격렬하게 펄럭이며, \"만세, 내 아들아!"], ["Hooroar, my Heart’s Delight!”", "만세, 내 마음의 기쁨이여!\""], ["with his shirt-collar in a violent state of agitation, until it is hopeless to attempt to keep up with the coach any longer.", "외치면서 계속 문가에 나타났다 사라졌다 하는데, 마침내 더는 마차를 따라가려는 것이 가망 없어질 때까지 그러기를 멈추지 않는다."], ["Finally, when the coach is gone, Susan Nipper, being rejoined by the Captain, falls into a state of insensibility, and is taken into a baker’s shop to recover.", "마침내 마차가 떠나 버리자, 수전 니퍼는 선장과 다시 합류한 뒤 인사불성 상태에 빠져, 정신을 차리도록 어느 빵집으로 들려 들어간다."]], [["Uncle Sol and Mr Toots wait patiently in the churchyard, sitting on the coping-stone of the railings, until Captain Cuttle and Susan come back.", "솔 삼촌과 토츠 씨는 교회 묘지에서 난간의 갓돌 위에 앉아, 커틀 선장과 수전이 돌아올 때까지 끈기 있게 기다린다."], ["Neither being at all desirous to speak, or to be spoken to, they are excellent company, and quite satisfied.", "둘 다 말을 하거나 말을 거는 것을 조금도 바라지 않으니, 그들은 더없이 좋은 동무이며 아주 만족스럽다."], ["When they all arrive again at the little Midshipman, and sit down to breakfast, nobody can touch a morsel.", "그들 모두 다시 작은 미드십맨에 도착해 아침 식탁에 둘러앉지만, 아무도 한 입도 먹지 못한다."], ["Captain Cuttle makes a feint of being voracious about toast, but gives it up as a swindle.", "커틀 선장은 토스트를 게걸스레 먹는 시늉을 하지만, 그것이 속임수임을 자인하고 그만둔다."], ["Mr Toots says, after breakfast, he will come back in the evening; and goes wandering about the town all day, with a vague sensation upon him as if he hadn’t been to bed for a fortnight.", "토츠 씨는 아침을 먹고 나서 저녁에 다시 오겠다고 말하고는, 마치 두 주 동안 잠자리에 들지 못한 듯한 막연한 느낌을 품은 채 온종일 시내를 떠돈다."]], [["There is a strange charm in the house, and in the room, in which they have been used to be together, and out of which so much is gone.", "그들이 함께 어울려 지내곤 했던, 그리고 이제 그 안에서 너무도 많은 것이 사라져 버린 그 집과 그 방에는 묘한 매력이 깃들어 있다."], ["It aggravates, and yet it soothes, the sorrow of the separation.", "그것은 이별의 슬픔을 더하면서도, 동시에 그 슬픔을 달래 준다."], ["Mr Toots tells Susan Nipper when he comes at night, that he hasn’t been so wretched all day long, and yet he likes it.", "토츠 씨는 밤에 와서 수전 니퍼에게, 온종일 이토록 비참한 적이 없었지만 그래도 이게 좋다고 말한다."], ["He confides in Susan Nipper, being alone with her, and tells her what his feelings were when she gave him that candid opinion as to the probability of Miss Dombey’s ever loving him.", "그는 수전 니퍼와 단둘이 있게 되자 속내를 털어놓으며, 그녀가 돔비 아가씨가 그를 사랑하게 될 가망에 대해 그 솔직한 견해를 들려주었을 때 자기 심정이 어땠는지를 이야기한다."], ["In the vein of confidence engendered by these common recollections, and their tears, Mr Toots proposes that they shall go out together, and buy something for supper.", "이런 공통된 추억과 그들의 눈물이 자아낸 흉금을 트는 분위기 속에서, 토츠 씨는 둘이 함께 나가 저녁거리를 좀 사 오자고 제안한다."], ["Miss Nipper assenting, they buy a good many little things; and, with the aid of Mrs Richards, set the supper out quite showily before the Captain and old Sol came home.", "니퍼 양이 동의하자, 그들은 자잘한 것들을 잔뜩 사고, 리처즈 부인의 도움을 받아, 선장과 늙은 솔이 돌아오기 전에 저녁상을 제법 그럴듯하게 차려 놓는다."]], [["The Captain and old Sol have been on board the ship, and have established Di there, and have seen the chests put aboard.", "선장과 늙은 솔은 배에 올라가, 거기에 다이를 자리 잡게 해 두고, 궤짝들이 실리는 것을 보고 왔다."], ["They have much to tell about the popularity of Walter, and the comforts he will have about him, and the quiet way in which it seems he has been working early and late, to make his cabin what the Captain calls “a picter,”", "그들은 월터의 인기에 대해, 그가 곁에 누리게 될 안락함에 대해, 그리고 그가 자기 작은 아내를 놀래 주려고 선장이 \"한 폭의 그림\"이라 부르는 것으로 선실을 꾸미느라 아침저녁으로 묵묵히 일해 온 듯하다는 것에 대해, 할 이야기가 많다."], ["to surprise his little wife.", "\"제독의 선실이라도 말이지,\""], ["“A admiral’s cabin, mind you,” says the Captain, “ain’t more trim.”", "선장이 말한다, \"이보다 더 말끔하진 않을 거요.\""]], [["But one of the Captain’s chief delights is, that he knows the big watch, and the sugar-tongs, and tea-spoons, are on board: and again and again he murmurs to himself, “Ed’ard Cuttle, my lad, you never shaped a better course in your life than when you made that there little property over jintly.", "그러나 선장의 가장 큰 기쁨 가운데 하나는, 그 큰 시계와 각설탕 집게와 찻숟가락들이 배에 실려 있음을 안다는 것이다. 그래서 그는 몇 번이고 거듭 혼잣말로 중얼거린다."], ["You see how the land bore, Ed’ard,”", "\"에드워드 커틀, 이 친구야, 자네 그 자그마한 재산을 공동 명의로 넘겨준 그때만큼 평생 더 나은 항로를 잡은 적이 없다네."], ["says the Captain, “and it does you credit, my lad.”", "자네는 땅이 어떻게 자리 잡혔는지 잘 봤어, 에드워드,\" 선장이 말한다, \"그리고 그건 자네 명예가 될 일이야, 이 친구야.\""]], [["The old Instrument-maker is more distraught and misty than he used to be, and takes the marriage and the parting very much to heart.", "늙은 계기 제작자는 예전보다 더 정신이 산만하고 흐리멍덩해져서, 이 결혼과 이별을 무척이나 마음 아파한다."], ["But he is greatly comforted by having his old ally, Ned Cuttle, at his side; and he sits down to supper with a grateful and contented face.", "그러나 그는 오랜 동지인 네드 커틀이 곁에 있어 크게 위로받고 있으며, 고맙고 흡족한 얼굴로 저녁 식탁에 앉는다."]], [["“My boy has been preserved and thrives,”", "\"내 아이는 무사히 지켜져서 잘 자라고 있어,\""], ["says old Sol Gills, rubbing his hands.", "늙은 솔 길스가 두 손을 비비며 말한다."], ["“What right have I to be otherwise than thankful and happy!”", "\"내가 감사하고 행복해하지 않을 무슨 까닭이 있겠나!\""]], [["The Captain, who has not yet taken his seat at the table, but who has been fidgeting about for some time, and now stands hesitating in his place, looks doubtfully at Mr Gills, and says:", "아직 식탁에 자리를 잡지 않고, 한동안 안절부절못하며 서성이다가 이제 제자리에 망설이며 서 있는 선장이, 미심쩍은 듯 길스 씨를 바라보며 말한다."]], [["“Sol!", "\"솔!"], ["There’s the last bottle of the old Madeira down below.", "저 아래에 그 묵은 마데이라가 마지막 한 병 있잖나."], ["Would you wish to have it up tonight, my boy, and drink to Wal”r and his wife?”", "오늘 밤 그걸 올려다가, 월터와 그 아내를 위해 마시고 싶나, 이 사람아?\""]], [["The Instrument-maker, looking wistfully at the Captain, puts his hand into the breast-pocket of his coffee-coloured coat, brings forth his pocket-book, and takes a letter out.", "계기 제작자는 선장을 애틋하게 바라보더니, 커피색 외투의 가슴 안주머니에 손을 넣어 수첩을 꺼내고는, 거기서 편지 한 장을 빼낸다."]], [["“To Mr Dombey,”", "\"돔비 씨께,\""], ["says the old man.", "노인이 말한다."], ["“From Walter.", "\"월터한테서 온 거야."], ["To be sent in three weeks’ time.", "삼 주 뒤에 부치라고."], ["I’ll read it.”", "읽어 주지.\""]], [["“‘Sir.", "\"'선생님."], ["I am married to your daughter.", "저는 선생님의 따님과 결혼했습니다."], ["She is gone with me upon a distant voyage.", "그녀는 저와 함께 먼 항해에 올랐습니다."], ["To be devoted to her is to have no claim on her or you, but God knows that I am.", "그녀에게 헌신한다는 것은 그녀나 선생님께 아무런 권리를 주장하지 않는다는 뜻이지만, 제가 그러하다는 것은 하느님께서 아십니다."]], [["“‘Why, loving her beyond all earthly things, I have yet, without remorse, united her to the uncertainties and dangers of my life, I will not say to you.", "\"'어찌하여, 그녀를 이 세상 그 무엇보다도 사랑하면서도, 저는 후회 없이 그녀를 제 삶의 불확실함과 위험에 묶어 두었는가, 그것을 선생님께 말씀드리지는 않겠습니다."], ["You know why, and you are her father.", "선생님께서는 그 까닭을 아시며, 또 그녀의 아버지이시니까요."]], [["“‘Do not reproach her.", "\"'그녀를 나무라지 마십시오."], ["She has never reproached you.", "그녀는 한 번도 선생님을 나무란 적이 없습니다."]], [["“‘I do not think or hope that you will ever forgive me.", "\"'선생님께서 저를 언젠가 용서해 주시리라고는 생각하지도, 바라지도 않습니다."], ["There is nothing I expect less.", "그것만큼 제가 기대하지 않는 일도 없습니다."], ["But if an hour should come when it will comfort you to believe that Florence has someone ever near her, the great charge of whose life is to cancel her remembrance of past sorrow, I solemnly assure you, you may, in that hour, rest in that belief.’”", "그러나 만일 플로렌스 곁에 늘 누군가가, 지난 슬픔의 기억을 지워 주는 것을 제 삶의 큰 사명으로 삼는 누군가가 있다고 믿는 것이 선생님께 위안이 될 그런 순간이 온다면, 저는 엄숙히 단언합니다, 선생님께서는 그 순간에 그 믿음 안에서 마음 놓으셔도 된다고요.'\""]], [["Solomon puts back the letter carefully in his pocket-book, and puts back his pocket-book in his coat.", "솔로몬은 편지를 조심스레 수첩에 도로 넣고, 수첩을 다시 외투에 집어넣는다."]], [["“We won’t drink the last bottle of the old Madeira yet, Ned,”", "\"그 묵은 마데이라 마지막 한 병은 아직 마시지 마세, 네드,\""], ["says the old man thoughtfully.", "노인이 생각에 잠겨 말한다."], ["“Not yet.", "\"아직은 아니야."]], [["“Not yet,”", "\"아직은 아니지,\""], ["assents the Captain.", "선장이 동의한다. \"그래."], ["“No. Not yet.”", "아직은 아니야.\""]], [["Susan and Mr Toots are of the same opinion.", "수전과 토츠 씨도 같은 생각이다."], ["After a silence they all sit down to supper, and drink to the young husband and wife in something else; and the last bottle of the old Madeira still remains among its dust and cobwebs, undisturbed.", "한동안의 침묵 끝에 그들은 모두 저녁 식탁에 둘러앉아, 다른 것으로 젊은 부부를 위해 잔을 든다. 그리고 그 묵은 마데이라 마지막 한 병은 여전히 그 먼지와 거미줄 속에, 건드려지지 않은 채 남아 있다."]], [["A few days have elapsed, and a stately ship is out at sea, spreading its white wings to the favouring wind.", "며칠이 흘렀고, 한 척의 위풍당당한 배가 순풍에 흰 날개를 펼친 채 먼바다에 나가 있다."]], [["Upon the deck, image to the roughest man on board of something that is graceful, beautiful, and harmless—something that it is good and pleasant to have there, and that should make the voyage prosperous—is Florence.", "갑판 위에는, 배에 탄 가장 거친 사내에게조차 우아하고 아름답고 해롭지 않은 무언가의 형상으로—거기 있어 좋고 기쁜 무언가, 항해를 순조롭게 해 줄 듯한 무언가의 형상으로—플로렌스가 있다."], ["It is night, and she and Walter sit alone, watching the solemn path of light upon the sea between them and the moon.", "때는 밤이고, 그녀와 월터는 단둘이 앉아, 자기들과 달 사이로 바다 위에 드리운 엄숙한 빛의 길을 바라보고 있다."]], [["At length she cannot see it plainly, for the tears that fill her eyes; and then she lays her head down on his breast, and puts her arms around his neck, saying, “Oh Walter, dearest love, I am so happy!”", "이윽고 그녀는 눈에 차오르는 눈물 때문에 그것을 또렷이 볼 수 없게 된다. 그러자 그녀는 그의 가슴에 머리를 묻고, 두 팔로 그의 목을 감싸며 말한다. \"오 월터, 사랑하는 이여, 나는 정말 행복해요!\""]], [["Her husband holds her to his heart, and they are very quiet, and the stately ship goes on serenely.", "남편은 그녀를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안고, 둘은 아주 고요하며, 위풍당당한 배는 잔잔히 나아간다."]], [["“As I hear the sea,”", "\"바닷소리를 들으며,\""], ["says Florence, “and sit watching it, it brings so many days into my mind.", "플로렌스가 말한다, \"그것을 바라보고 앉아 있으면, 참 많은 날들이 마음에 떠올라요."], ["It makes me think so much—”", "정말 많은 생각이 들어요—\""]], [["“Of Paul, my love.", "\"폴 생각이지, 내 사랑."], ["I know it does.”", "나도 알아요.\""]], [["Of Paul and Walter.", "폴과 월터 생각이다."], ["And the voices in the waves are always whispering to Florence, in their ceaseless murmuring, of love—of love, eternal and illimitable, not bounded by the confines of this world, or by the end of time, but ranging still, beyond the sea, beyond the sky, to the invisible country far away!", "그리고 파도 속의 목소리들은 그 끊임없는 속삭임으로, 늘 플로렌스에게 사랑을 속삭인다—이 세상의 경계로도, 시간의 끝으로도 한정되지 않고, 바다 너머로, 하늘 너머로, 저 멀리 보이지 않는 나라에까지 여전히 뻗어 가는, 영원하고 가없는 사랑을."]], [["# CHAPTER LVIII. After a Lapse", "# CHAPTER LVIII. After a Lapse"]], [["The sea had ebbed and flowed, through a whole year.", "바다는 한 해 내내 썰물과 밀물을 거듭했다."], ["Through a whole year, the winds and clouds had come and gone; the ceaseless work of Time had been performed, in storm and sunshine.", "한 해 내내 바람과 구름이 오고 갔으며, 시간의 쉼 없는 작업이 폭풍 속에서도 햇빛 속에서도 이루어졌다."], ["Through a whole year, the tides of human chance and change had set in their allotted courses.", "한 해 내내 인간사의 운수와 변천의 조류가 저마다 정해진 길로 흘러갔다."], ["Through a whole year, the famous House of Dombey and Son had fought a fight for life, against cross accidents, doubtful rumours, unsuccessful ventures, unpropitious times, and most of all, against the infatuation of its head, who would not contract its enterprises by a hair’s breadth, and would not listen to a word of warning that the ship he strained so hard against the storm, was weak, and could not bear it.", "한 해 내내 그 유명한 돔비와 아들 회사는 살아남기 위한 싸움을 벌였으니, 어긋난 사고들과, 불확실한 소문들과, 실패한 모험들과, 불리한 시운(時運)과, 그리고 무엇보다도 그 우두머리의 맹목에 맞선 싸움이었다. 그는 사업을 머리카락 한 올만큼도 줄이려 하지 않았고, 자기가 그토록 무리하게 폭풍에 맞세운 배가 약하여 그것을 견딜 수 없다는 한마디 경고도 들으려 하지 않았다."]], [["The year was out, and the great House was down.", "그 한 해가 다하자, 그 거대한 회사가 무너졌다."]], [["One summer afternoon; a year, wanting some odd days, after the marriage in the City church; there was a buzz and whisper upon “Change of a great failure.", "어느 여름날 오후, 시내 교회에서의 결혼식이 있은 지 며칠 모자라는 한 해가 지난 무렵, 거래소에는 큰 파산에 대한 웅성거림과 속삭임이 일었다."], ["A certain cold proud man, well known there, was not there, nor was he represented there.", "그곳에서 잘 알려진, 어떤 차갑고 거만한 사람이 그 자리에 없었고, 그를 대신할 사람도 없었다."], ["Next day it was noised abroad that Dombey and Son had stopped, and next night there was a List of Bankrupts published, headed by that name.", "이튿날 돔비와 아들이 거래를 중단했다는 소문이 널리 퍼졌고, 그다음 날 밤에는 파산자 명단이 발표되었는데, 그 첫머리에 바로 그 이름이 올라 있었다."]], [["The world was very busy now, in sooth, and had a deal to say.", "세상은 이제 정말이지 매우 분주했고, 할 말이 많았다."], ["It was an innocently credulous and a much ill-used world.", "그것은 순진하게 잘 믿고, 또 몹시 부당한 대우를 받는 세상이었다."], ["It was a world in which there was no other sort of bankruptcy whatever.", "그것은 다른 어떤 종류의 파산도 결코 존재하지 않는 세상이었다."], ["There were no conspicuous people in it, trading far and wide on rotten banks of religion, patriotism, virtue, honour.", "그 속에는 종교며 애국심이며 미덕이며 명예라는 썩은 둑 위에서 멀리까지 두루 장사를 하는 눈에 띄는 인물이라곤 하나도 없었다."], ["There was no amount worth mentioning of mere paper in circulation, on which anybody lived pretty handsomely, promising to pay great sums of goodness with no effects.", "누군가가 제법 그럴듯하게 살아가면서, 아무런 자산도 없이 막대한 선(善)의 액수를 갚아 주겠노라 약속하며 유통시키는, 거론할 만한 분량의 한낱 종잇조각이라곤 하나도 없었다."], ["There were no shortcomings anywhere, in anything but money.", "돈을 빼고는 그 어떤 것에서도, 그 어디에서도 부족함이라곤 없었다."], ["The world was very angry indeed; and the people especially, who, in a worse world, might have been supposed to be apt traders themselves in shows and pretences, were observed to be mightily indignant.", "세상은 참으로 몹시 노여워했다. 그리고 더 나쁜 세상이었다면 자기들이야말로 허울과 가식의 능란한 장사꾼이었으리라 짐작될 법한 사람들이, 특히나 대단히 분개하는 것이 눈에 띄었다."]], [["Here was a new inducement to dissipation, presented to that sport of circumstances, Mr Perch the Messenger!", "이 일은 환경의 노리갯감인 심부름꾼 퍼치 씨에게 방탕으로의 새로운 유혹을 안겨 주었다!"], ["It was apparently the fate of Mr Perch to be always waking up, and finding himself famous.", "퍼치 씨의 운명이란 늘 잠에서 깨어 보니 자기가 유명해져 있는 것이었던 모양이다."], ["He had but yesterday, as one might say, subsided into private life from the celebrity of the elopement and the events that followed it; and now he was made a more important man than ever, by the bankruptcy.", "그는, 말하자면 바로 어제, 사랑의 도피며 그에 뒤따른 사건들로 누리던 명성에서 사사로운 생활로 가라앉았는데, 이제 이 파산으로 그 어느 때보다 더 중요한 인물이 되었다."], ["Gliding from his bracket in the outer office where he now sat, watching the strange faces of accountants and others, who quickly superseded nearly all the old clerks, Mr Perch had but to show himself in the court outside, or, at farthest, in the bar of the King’s Arms, to be asked a multitude of questions, almost certain to include that interesting question, what would he take to drink?", "이제 그가 앉아서, 옛 사무원들을 거의 모조리 빠르게 갈아치운 회계사들과 그 밖의 사람들의 낯선 얼굴을 지켜보던 바깥 사무실의 자기 받침대 자리에서 슬며시 빠져나와, 바깥 안마당에 모습을 드러내거나, 멀어 봤자 킹스 암스 술집의 바에 모습을 드러내기만 하면, 그는 무수한 질문을 받았는데, 그 가운데에는 그가 무엇을 마시겠느냐는 흥미로운 질문이 거의 틀림없이 끼어 있었다."], ["Then would Mr Perch descant upon the hours of acute uneasiness he and Mrs Perch had suffered out at Balls Pond, when they first suspected “things was going wrong.”", "그러면 퍼치 씨는, 그와 퍼치 부인이 볼스 폰드 바깥의 집에서 \"일이 잘못되어 가고 있다\"고 처음 의심하기 시작했을 때 겪은 극심한 불안의 시간들을 장황하게 늘어놓곤 했다."], ["Then would Mr Perch relate to gaping listeners, in a low voice, as if the corpse of the deceased House were lying unburied in the next room, how Mrs Perch had first come to surmise that things was going wrong by hearing him (Perch) moaning in his sleep, “twelve and ninepence in the pound, twelve and ninepence in the pound!”", "그러면 퍼치 씨는, 마치 고인이 된 회사의 시신이 옆방에 묻히지 못한 채 누워 있기라도 한 듯 낮은 목소리로, 입을 떡 벌리고 듣는 청중에게, 퍼치 부인이 일이 잘못되어 가고 있음을 처음 짐작하게 된 것은 그(퍼치)가 잠결에 \"파운드당 십이 실링 구 펜스, 파운드당 십이 실링 구 펜스!\"라고 신음하는 소리를 들었기 때문이라고 이야기하곤 했다."], ["Which act of somnambulism he supposed to have originated in the impression made upon him by the change in Mr Dombey’s face.", "그는 그 몽유병적 행동이, 돔비 씨의 얼굴에 일어난 변화가 자기에게 남긴 인상에서 비롯되었으리라고 짐작했다."], ["Then would he inform them how he had once said, “Might I make so bold as ask, Sir, are you unhappy in your mind?” and how Mr Dombey had replied, “My faithful Perch—but no, it cannot be!”", "그러면 그는, 자기가 언젠가 \"감히 여쭙겠습니다만, 나리, 마음이 편치 않으신지요?\"라고 물었더니, 돔비 씨가 \"내 충직한 퍼치—아니, 그럴 수는 없네!\"라고 대답하고는, 이어서 손을 이마에 갖다 대며 \"나를 혼자 두게, 퍼치!\"라고 했다는 이야기를 들려주곤 했다."], ["and with that had struck his hand upon his forehead, and said, “Leave me, Perch!”", "그러면, 요컨대 퍼치 씨는, 자기 처지의 희생자로서, 온갖 거짓말을 늘어놓았다."], ["Then, in short, would Mr Perch, a victim to his position, tell all manner of lies; affecting himself to tears by those that were of a moving nature, and really believing that the inventions of yesterday had, on repetition, a sort of truth about them today.", "가슴 뭉클한 성질의 거짓말로는 스스로 눈물까지 흘렸고, 어제 지어낸 이야기가 되풀이되다 보니 오늘은 어딘가 진실 비슷한 것을 띠게 된다고 정말로 믿었다."]], [["Mr Perch always closed these conferences by meekly remarking, that, of course, whatever his suspicions might have been (as if he had ever had any!) it wasn’t for him to betray his trust, was it?", "퍼치 씨는 이런 회담을 늘 온순하게 한마디 덧붙이며 끝맺었으니, 물론 자기 의혹이 어떠했든 간에(마치 그가 무슨 의혹이라도 품은 적이 있었던 것처럼!) 자기가 맡은 신임을 저버리는 것은 자기 할 일이 아니지 않으냐는 것이었다. 그 정서는(채권자라곤 결코 한 사람도 그 자리에 없었으니) 그의 마음씨에 큰 영예가 되는 것으로 받아들여졌다."], ["Which sentiment (there never being any creditors present) was received as doing great honour to his feelings.", "그리하여 그는 대개 누그러진 양심을 안고 떠났으며, 자기 받침대 자리로 돌아갈 때면 흐뭇한 인상을 뒤에 남겼다."], ["Thus, he generally brought away a soothed conscience and left an agreeable impression behind him, when he returned to his bracket: again to sit watching the strange faces of the accountants and others, making so free with the great mysteries, the Books; or now and then to go on tiptoe into Mr Dombey’s empty room, and stir the fire; or to take an airing at the door, and have a little more doleful chat with any straggler whom he knew; or to propitiate, with various small attentions, the head accountant: from whom Mr Perch had expectations of a messengership in a Fire Office, when the affairs of the House should be wound up.", "그러고는 다시 앉아서, 그 큰 비밀인 장부를 그토록 함부로 다루는 회계사들과 그 밖의 사람들의 낯선 얼굴을 지켜보거나, 이따금 발끝으로 살금살금 돔비 씨의 텅 빈 방에 들어가 불을 쑤셔 돋우거나, 문간에 나가 바람을 쐬며 아는 떠돌이 누구하고든 조금 더 구슬픈 잡담을 나누거나, 혹은 갖가지 자잘한 친절로 수석 회계사의 비위를 맞추곤 했다. 퍼치 씨는 회사의 일이 청산되고 나면 그에게서 어느 화재보험회사 심부름꾼 자리를 얻을 수 있으리라 기대하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["To Major Bagstock, the bankruptcy was quite a calamity.", "배그스톡 소령에게 이 파산은 그야말로 하나의 재앙이었다."], ["The Major was not a sympathetic character—his attention being wholly concentrated on J.", "소령은 동정심 많은 인물도 아니었고—그의 관심은 온통 J."], ["B.—nor was he a man subject to lively emotions, except in the physical regards of gasping and choking.", "B.에게만 쏠려 있었으니—숨을 헐떡이고 목이 막히는 신체적 측면이 아니고서는 격한 감정에 잘 휩쓸리는 사람도 아니었다."], ["But he had so paraded his friend Dombey at the club; had so flourished him at the heads of the members in general, and so put them down by continual assertion of his riches; that the club, being but human, was delighted to retort upon the Major, by asking him, with a show of great concern, whether this tremendous smash had been at all expected, and how his friend Dombey bore it.", "그러나 그는 클럽에서 자기 친구 돔비를 어찌나 떠벌리고 다녔는지, 회원들 전체의 머리 위로 그를 어찌나 휘둘러 댔는지, 그의 부유함을 끊임없이 주장하며 그들을 어찌나 눌러 댔는지, 그러니 클럽도 인간인지라, 소령에게 되받아치는 것을 기뻐했다. 곧 대단히 걱정스러운 양하며, 이 엄청난 파국이 조금이라도 예상되었던 일이냐고, 그의 친구 돔비는 그것을 어떻게 견디고 있느냐고 물어 댄 것이다."], ["To such questions, the Major, waxing very purple, would reply that it was a bad world, Sir, altogether; that Joey knew a thing or two, but had been done, Sir, done like an infant; that if you had foretold this, Sir, to J.", "그런 질문에 소령은 얼굴이 시뻘게져서, 대체로 고약한 세상이라고, 조이가 한두 가지는 알지만 당했다고, 어린애처럼 당했다고 대답하곤 했다. 만일 이 일을 J."], ["Bagstock, when he went abroad with Dombey and was chasing that vagabond up and down France, J.", "배그스톡에게, 그가 돔비와 함께 외국으로 가서 그 부랑자를 프랑스 곳곳으로 쫓아다닐 때 예언했더라면, J."], ["Bagstock would have pooh-pooh’d you—would have pooh-pooh’d you, Sir, by the Lord!", "배그스톡은 당신을 코웃음으로 일축했을 거라고, 맹세코, 당신을 코웃음으로 일축했을 거라고 했다!"], ["That Joe had been deceived, Sir, taken in, hoodwinked, blindfolded, but was broad awake again and staring; insomuch, Sir, that if Joe’s father were to rise up from the grave to-morrow, he wouldn’t trust the old blade with a penny piece, but would tell him that his son Josh was too old a soldier to be done again, Sir.", "조는 속았고, 넘어갔고, 눈가림을 당했고, 눈을 가린 채 끌려다녔지만, 이제는 다시 활짝 깨어 두 눈 부릅뜨고 있다고. 그러니 만일 조의 아버지가 내일 무덤에서 일어난다 해도, 그 늙은 협잡꾼에게 단돈 한 푼도 맡기지 않을 것이며, 도리어 그에게 당신 아들 조시는 다시 당하기엔 너무 노련한 군인이라고 말해 줄 거라고."], ["That he was a suspicious, crabbed, cranky, used-up, J.", "자기는 의심 많고, 까칠하고, 괴팍하고, 닳고 닳은, J."], ["B.", "B."], ["infidel, Sir; and that if it were consistent with the dignity of a rough and tough old Major, of the old school, who had had the honour of being personally known to, and commended by, their late Royal Highnesses the Dukes of Kent and York, to retire to a tub and live in it, by Gad!", "불신자라고. 그리고 만일 켄트 공작과 요크 공작 두 분 전하께 친히 알현하여 칭찬을 들은 영예를 지닌, 구식의 거칠고 강인한 늙은 소령의 위엄에 어울리는 일이라면, 통 속에 들어앉아 그 안에서 살아도 좋다면, 천만에!"], ["Sir, he’d have a tub in Pall Mall to-morrow, to show his contempt for mankind!", "그는 인류를 향한 경멸을 보여 주려고 내일이라도 폴 몰 한복판에 통 하나를 갖다 놓겠노라고 했다!"]], [["Of all this, and many variations of the same tune, the Major would deliver himself with so many apoplectic symptoms, such rollings of his head, and such violent growls of ill usage and resentment, that the younger members of the club surmised he had invested money in his friend Dombey’s House, and lost it; though the older soldiers and deeper dogs, who knew Joe better, wouldn’t hear of such a thing.", "이 모든 것을, 그리고 같은 곡조의 무수한 변주를, 소령은 어찌나 많은 졸중(卒中) 증세를 보이며, 어찌나 머리를 휘저으며, 부당한 대우와 분노에 대해 어찌나 사납게 으르렁대며 쏟아 냈는지, 클럽의 젊은 회원들은 그가 친구 돔비의 회사에 돈을 투자했다가 잃은 것이라 짐작했다. 다만 조를 더 잘 아는 나이 든 군인들과 더 능구렁이 같은 자들은 그런 말을 들으려고도 하지 않았다."], ["The unfortunate Native, expressing no opinion, suffered dreadfully; not merely in his moral feelings, which were regularly fusilladed by the Major every hour in the day, and riddled through and through, but in his sensitiveness to bodily knocks and bumps, which was kept continually on the stretch.", "그 불운한 토인은 아무런 의견도 내놓지 않은 채 끔찍하게 시달렸다. 하루도 빠짐없이 매시간 소령에게 규칙적으로 일제 사격을 당하여 구멍이 숭숭 뚫린 그의 도덕적 감정에서만이 아니라, 끊임없이 팽팽하게 시달려 온 신체적 타격과 부딪힘에 대한 그의 예민함에서도 그러했다."], ["For six entire weeks after the bankruptcy, this miserable foreigner lived in a rainy season of boot-jacks and brushes.", "파산 후 꼬박 여섯 주 동안, 이 가엾은 이방인은 구둣주걱과 솔이 빗발치는 우기(雨期) 속에서 살았다."]], [["Mrs Chick had three ideas upon the subject of the terrible reverse.", "칙 부인은 그 끔찍한 역경의 문제에 대해 세 가지 생각을 품고 있었다."], ["The first was that she could not understand it.", "첫째는 자기로서는 그것을 도무지 이해할 수 없다는 것이었다."], ["The second, that her brother had not made an effort.", "둘째는 자기 오라비가 노력을 하지 않았다는 것이었다."], ["The third, that if she had been invited to dinner on the day of that first party, it never would have happened; and that she had said so, at the time.", "셋째는 만일 그 첫 번째 파티가 있던 날 자기가 만찬에 초대받았더라면 이런 일은 결코 일어나지 않았을 것이며, 자기가 그때 그렇게 말했었다는 것이었다."]], [["Nobody’s opinion stayed the misfortune, lightened it, or made it heavier. It was understood that the affairs of the House were to be wound up as they best could be; that Mr Dombey freely resigned everything he had, and asked for no favour from anyone. That any resumption of the business was out of the question, as he would listen to no friendly negotiation having that compromise in view; that he had relinquished every post of trust or distinction he had held, as a man respected among merchants; that he was dying, according to some; that he was going melancholy mad, according to others; that he was a broken man, according to all.", "그 누구의 의견도 그 불행을 막지 못했고, 덜어 주지도, 더 무겁게 하지도 못했다. 회사의 일은 형편이 닿는 한 최선으로 청산될 것이며, 돔비 씨는 자기가 가진 모든 것을 선뜻 내놓고 누구에게도 아무런 호의를 청하지 않는다는 것이 알려졌다. 사업을 재개하는 일은 논외인데, 그가 그러한 타협을 염두에 둔 어떠한 우호적 협상에도 귀를 기울이려 하지 않기 때문이라는 것. 상인들 사이에서 존경받는 사람으로서 그가 지녔던 신임과 명예의 자리를 모두 내놓았다는 것. 어떤 이들의 말로는 그가 죽어 가고 있다는 것. 또 다른 이들의 말로는 그가 우울증으로 미쳐 가고 있다는 것. 모든 이의 말로는 그가 폐인이 된 사람이라는 것이었다."]], [["The clerks dispersed after holding a little dinner of condolence among themselves, which was enlivened by comic singing, and went off admirably.", "사무원들은 자기들끼리 조촐한 위로의 만찬을 가진 뒤에 흩어졌는데, 그 만찬은 익살스러운 노래로 흥겨워졌고 더없이 훌륭하게 끝났다."], ["Some took places abroad, and some engaged in other Houses at home; some looked up relations in the country, for whom they suddenly remembered they had a particular affection; and some advertised for employment in the newspapers.", "더러는 외국에 자리를 얻었고, 더러는 본국의 다른 회사에 들어갔으며, 더러는 시골에 사는, 갑자기 각별한 애정이 떠오른 친척들을 찾아갔고, 더러는 신문에 구직 광고를 냈다."], ["Mr Perch alone remained of all the late establishment, sitting on his bracket looking at the accountants, or starting off it, to propitiate the head accountant, who was to get him into the Fire Office.", "그 옛 직원들 가운데 퍼치 씨만 홀로 남아, 받침대 자리에 앉아 회계사들을 바라보거나, 거기서 벌떡 일어나, 자기를 화재보험회사에 넣어 주기로 한 수석 회계사의 비위를 맞추러 가곤 했다."], ["The Counting House soon got to be dirty and neglected.", "회계 사무실은 이내 더럽고 방치된 모습이 되었다."], ["The principal slipper and dogs’ collar seller, at the corner of the court, would have doubted the propriety of throwing up his forefinger to the brim of his hat, any more, if Mr Dombey had appeared there now; and the ticket porter, with his hands under his white apron, moralised good sound morality about ambition, which (he observed) was not, in his opinion, made to rhyme to perdition, for nothing.", "안마당 모퉁이의 슬리퍼와 개 목걸이 파는 주인장은, 이제 돔비 씨가 그곳에 나타난다 해도 모자챙에 집게손가락을 갖다 대는 것이 과연 온당한 일일지 의심했을 것이다. 그리고 흰 앞치마 밑에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 표 운반인은, 야망에 관하여 그럴듯하고 건전한 교훈을 늘어놓았으니, 야망(ambition)이 파멸(perdition)과 운(韻)이 맞게 되어 있는 것은, 그가 보기에, 다 까닭이 있어서라고 했다."]], [["Mr Morfin, the hazel-eyed bachelor, with the hair and whiskers sprinkled with grey, was perhaps the only person within the atmosphere of the House—its head, of course, excepted—who was heartily and deeply affected by the disaster that had befallen it.", "개암빛 눈을 한 독신 남자로, 머리칼과 구레나룻에 희끗희끗 백발이 섞인 모핀 씨는, 어쩌면 그 회사의 분위기 안에서—물론 그 우두머리는 예외로 하고—그 회사에 닥친 재난으로 진심으로 깊이 마음 아파한 유일한 사람이었을 것이다."], ["He had treated Mr Dombey with due respect and deference through many years, but he had never disguised his natural character, or meanly truckled to him, or pampered his master passion for the advancement of his own purposes.", "그는 여러 해 동안 돔비 씨를 마땅한 존경과 경의로 대해 왔으나, 자기 본연의 성품을 감춘 적도, 비굴하게 그에게 아첨한 적도, 자기 목적의 출세를 위해 주인의 으뜸가는 정념에 영합한 적도 없었다. 그러므로 그에게는 복수해야 할 자기 모멸도 없었고, 빠르게 튕겨 나가며 풀어 줄, 오래 조여 둔 용수철도 없었다."], ["He had, therefore, no self-disrespect to avenge; no long-tightened springs to release with a quick recoil.", "그는 회사의 거래 기록 가운데 복잡하거나 어려운 것은 무엇이든 풀어내려고 이른 아침부터 늦은 밤까지 일했다."], ["He worked early and late to unravel whatever was complicated or difficult in the records of the transactions of the House; was always in attendance to explain whatever required explanation; sat in his old room sometimes very late at night, studying points by his mastery of which he could spare Mr Dombey the pain of being personally referred to; and then would go home to Islington, and calm his mind by producing the most dismal and forlorn sounds out of his violoncello before going to bed.", "설명이 필요한 것은 무엇이든 설명하려고 늘 대기하고 있었고, 때로는 밤늦도록 자기 옛 방에 앉아, 자기가 통달함으로써 돔비 씨가 직접 거론되는 고통을 덜어 줄 수 있는 사항들을 연구했다. 그러고는 이즐링턴의 집으로 돌아가, 잠자리에 들기 전에 첼로에서 더없이 음울하고 처량한 소리를 뽑아내며 마음을 가라앉히곤 했다."]], [["He was solacing himself with this melodious grumbler one evening, and, having been much dispirited by the proceedings of the day, was scraping consolation out of its deepest notes, when his landlady (who was fortunately deaf, and had no other consciousness of these performances than a sensation of something rumbling in her bones) announced a lady.", "어느 날 저녁 그는 이 가락 좋게 투덜대는 악기로 스스로를 달래고 있었는데, 그날의 일들로 무척 기운이 빠진 터라, 그 가장 낮은 음들에서 위안을 긁어내고 있었다. 그때 그의 하숙집 여주인이(다행히 귀가 먹어, 이런 연주에 대해 자기 뼛속에서 무언가 우르릉거리는 느낌 말고는 달리 의식하는 것이 없었다) 한 부인의 방문을 알렸다."]], [["“In mourning,”", "\"상복을 입으셨어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["The violoncello stopped immediately; and the performer, laying it on the sofa with great tenderness and care, made a sign that the lady was to come in.", "첼로가 즉시 멈췄다. 그리고 연주자는 그것을 더없이 부드럽고 조심스럽게 소파에 내려놓고는, 그 부인을 들여보내라는 신호를 했다."], ["He followed directly, and met Harriet Carker on the stair.", "그는 곧장 뒤따라 나갔고, 계단에서 해리엇 카커를 만났다."]], [["“Alone!”", "\"혼자시군요!\""], ["he said, “and John here this morning!", "그가 말했다, \"오늘 아침에는 존이 여기 왔었는데!"], ["Is there anything the matter, my dear?", "무슨 일이라도 있는 거요, 아가씨? 아니, 그게 아니로군요,\""], ["But no,” he added, “your face tells quite another story.”", "그가 덧붙였다, \"당신 얼굴이 전혀 다른 이야기를 들려주는걸요.\""]], [["“I am afraid it is a selfish revelation that you see there, then,”", "\"그렇다면 거기서 보시는 건 이기적인 속내가 드러난 것일까 봐 걱정스럽네요.\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“It is a very pleasant one,”", "\"그건 아주 유쾌한 속내인걸요,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “and, if selfish, a novelty too, worth seeing in you.", "\"그리고 설령 이기적이라 해도, 당신에게서는 보기 드문 새로운 것이니 볼 만한 가치가 있지요."], ["But I don’t believe that.”", "하지만 난 그렇게 믿지 않아요.\""]], [["He had placed a chair for her by this time, and sat down opposite; the violoncello lying snugly on the sofa between them.", "이 무렵 그는 그녀에게 의자를 권해 두었고, 자기는 맞은편에 앉았다. 두 사람 사이의 소파에는 첼로가 아늑하게 놓여 있었다."]], [["“You will not be surprised at my coming alone, or at John’s not having told you I was coming,”", "\"제가 혼자 온 것이나, 존이 제가 온다고 선생님께 말씀드리지 않은 것이나, 놀라실 일이 아니에요,\""], ["said Harriet; “and you will believe that, when I tell you why I have come.", "해리엇이 말했다. \"제가 왜 왔는지 말씀드리면 그것도 이해하실 거예요."], ["May I do so now?”", "지금 말씀드려도 될까요?\""]], [["“You can do nothing better.”", "\"그보다 더 나은 일을 하실 수는 없지요.\""]], [["“You were not busy?”", "\"바쁘진 않으셨어요?\""]], [["He pointed to the violoncello lying on the sofa, and said “I have been, all day.", "그는 소파에 놓인 첼로를 가리키며 말했다, \"하루 종일 바빴지요."], ["Here’s my witness.", "여기 내 증인이 있어요."], ["I have been confiding all my cares to it.", "내 온갖 근심을 다 저것에게 털어놓았답니다."], ["I wish I had none but my own to tell.”", "나 자신의 근심 말고는 털어놓을 게 없으면 좋으련만.\""]], [["“Is the House at an end?”", "\"회사는 끝난 건가요?\""], ["said Harriet, earnestly.", "해리엇이 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“Completely at an end.”", "\"완전히 끝났어요.\""]], [["“Will it never be resumed?”", "\"결코 재개되지 않을까요?\""]], [["“Never.”", "\"결코.\""]], [["The bright expression of her face was not overshadowed as her lips silently repeated the word.", "그녀의 입술이 소리 없이 그 말을 되뇔 때에도, 그녀 얼굴의 밝은 표정에는 그늘이 드리우지 않았다."], ["He seemed to observe this with some little involuntary surprise: and said again:", "그는 이를 다소 무심결에 살짝 놀라며 지켜보는 듯하다가, 다시 말했다."]], [["“Never.", "\"결코."], ["You remember what I told you.", "내가 당신에게 한 말을 기억하겠지요."], ["It has been, all along, impossible to convince him; impossible to reason with him; sometimes, impossible even to approach him.", "그를 납득시키는 일은 처음부터 줄곧 불가능했어요. 그와 도리를 따져 보는 일도 불가능했고, 때로는 그에게 다가가는 것조차 불가능했지요."], ["The worst has happened; and the House has fallen, never to be built up any more.”", "최악의 일이 일어났고, 회사는 무너졌으니, 다시는 일으켜 세워지지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“And Mr Dombey, is he personally ruined?”", "\"그러면 돔비 씨는, 본인은 파멸한 건가요?\""]], [["“Ruined.”", "\"파멸했어요.\""]], [["“Will he have no private fortune left?", "\"개인 재산은 하나도 남지 않을까요?"], ["Nothing?”", "아무것도?\""]], [["A certain eagerness in her voice, and something that was almost joyful in her look, seemed to surprise him more and more; to disappoint him too, and jar discordantly against his own emotions.", "그녀의 목소리에 담긴 어떤 간절함과, 거의 기쁨에 가까운 그녀의 눈빛이, 그를 점점 더 놀라게 하는 듯했다. 그를 실망시키기도 하고, 자기 자신의 감정과는 어긋나게 거슬리는 듯도 했다."], ["He drummed with the fingers of one hand on the table, looking wistfully at her, and shaking his head, said, after a pause:", "그는 한 손의 손가락으로 탁자를 두드리며, 안타깝게 그녀를 바라보고 고개를 저으며, 잠시 뒤에 말했다."]], [["“The extent of Mr Dombey’s resources is not accurately within my knowledge; but though they are doubtless very large, his obligations are enormous.", "\"돔비 씨가 가진 자산의 규모는 제가 정확히 알지 못해요. 하지만 그것이 분명 매우 크다 해도, 그의 채무는 어마어마하지요."], ["He is a gentleman of high honour and integrity.", "그는 높은 명예와 청렴을 지닌 신사예요."], ["Any man in his position could, and many a man in his position would, have saved himself, by making terms which would have very slightly, almost insensibly, increased the losses of those who had had dealings with him, and left him a remnant to live upon.", "그의 처지에 놓인 사람이라면 누구라도, 자기와 거래해 온 사람들의 손실을 아주 약간, 거의 느낄 수도 없을 만큼만 늘리는 조건을 내세움으로써 자기 자신을 구하고, 살아갈 만한 잔여 재산을 남겨 둘 수 있었을 거예요. 또 그의 처지에 놓인 많은 사람이 그렇게 했을 테고요."], ["But he is resolved on payment to the last farthing of his means.", "하지만 그는 자기 재산이 닿는 한 마지막 한 푼까지 갚겠다고 결심했어요."], ["His own words are, that they will clear, or nearly clear, the House, and that no one can lose much.", "그의 말로는, 그것으로 회사를 청산하거나 거의 청산할 것이며, 누구도 큰 손해를 보지 않으리라는 거예요."], ["Ah, Miss Harriet, it would do us no harm to remember oftener than we do, that vices are sometimes only virtues carried to excess!", "아, 해리엇 양, 악덕이라는 것이 때로는 지나치게 밀어붙인 미덕에 지나지 않는다는 것을, 우리가 지금보다 더 자주 떠올린다 해도 우리에게 해될 것은 없을 거예요!"], ["His pride shows well in this.”", "그의 자존심이 이 일에서는 훌륭하게 드러나는군요.\""]], [["She heard him with little or no change in her expression, and with a divided attention that showed her to be busy with something in her own mind.", "그녀는 표정에 거의, 아니 전혀 변화 없이, 그리고 자기 마음속의 무언가에 골몰해 있음을 보여 주는 분산된 주의로 그의 말을 들었다."], ["When he was silent, she asked him hurriedly:", "그가 입을 다물자, 그녀가 황급히 그에게 물었다."]], [["“Have you seen him lately?”", "\"그분을 요즘 뵌 적 있으세요?\""]], [["“No one sees him.", "\"아무도 그를 보지 못해요."], ["When this crisis of his affairs renders it necessary for him to come out of his house, he comes out for the occasion, and again goes home, and shuts himself up, and will see no one.", "그의 사정에 닥친 이번 위기로 인해 그가 집 밖으로 나와야 할 필요가 있으면, 그는 그 일을 위해 나왔다가 다시 집으로 돌아가 틀어박혀, 아무도 만나려 하지 않아요."], ["He has written me a letter, acknowledging our past connexion in higher terms than it deserved, and parting from me.", "그가 내게 편지를 한 통 보냈는데, 우리의 지난 인연을 그것이 받을 만한 것보다 더 높이 인정하면서, 내게 작별을 고하더군요."], ["I am delicate of obtruding myself upon him now, never having had much intercourse with him in better times; but I have tried to do so.", "형편이 더 나았던 시절에도 그와 그리 많이 왕래하지 못했던 처지라, 이제 와서 그에게 불쑥 끼어드는 것이 조심스럽지만, 그래도 그렇게 해 보려고 애썼어요."], ["I have written, gone there, entreated.", "편지도 쓰고, 거기 찾아가기도 하고, 간청도 했지요."], ["Quite in vain.”", "다 헛수고였어요.\""]], [["He watched her, as in the hope that she would testify some greater concern than she had yet shown; and spoke gravely and feelingly, as if to impress her the more; but there was no change in her.", "그는 그녀가 지금껏 보인 것보다 더 큰 관심을 무엇으로든 보여 주기를 바라는 듯 그녀를 지켜보았다. 그리고 그녀에게 더 깊은 인상을 주려는 듯 엄숙하고 절절하게 말했다. 그러나 그녀에게는 아무런 변화가 없었다."]], [["“Well, well, Miss Harriet,”", "\"자, 자, 해리엇 양,\""], ["he said, with a disappointed air, “this is not to the purpose.", "그가 실망한 기색으로 말했다, \"이건 본론이 아니로군요."], ["You have not come here to hear this.", "당신은 이런 이야기를 들으러 온 게 아니지요."], ["Some other and pleasanter theme is in your mind.", "무언가 다른, 더 즐거운 주제가 당신 마음속에 있군요."], ["Let it be in mine, too, and we shall talk upon more equal terms.", "그걸 내 마음속에도 있게 해 줘요, 그러면 우리가 좀 더 대등하게 이야기를 나눌 수 있을 테니."], ["Come!”", "자!\""]], [["“No, it is the same theme,”", "\"아니에요, 같은 주제예요,\""], ["returned Harriet, with frank and quick surprise.", "해리엇이 솔직하고 빠르게 놀라며 대답했다."], ["“Is it not likely that it should be?", "\"그게 같은 주제인 것이 당연하지 않겠어요?"], ["Is it not natural that John and I should have been thinking and speaking very much of late of these great changes?", "존과 제가 요즘 이 큰 변화에 대해 무척 많이 생각하고 이야기한 것이 자연스러운 일 아닌가요?"], ["Mr Dombey, whom he served so many years—you know upon what terms—reduced, as you describe; and we quite rich!”", "그분이 그토록 여러 해 섬긴 돔비 씨가—어떤 처지에서였는지는 아시잖아요—선생님이 말씀하신 대로 영락하셨는데, 우리는 이렇게 부유하니!\""]], [["Good, true face, as that face of hers was, and pleasant as it had been to him, Mr Morfin, the hazel-eyed bachelor, since the first time he had ever looked upon it, it pleased him less at that moment, lighted with a ray of exultation, than it had ever pleased him before.", "그녀의 얼굴은, 비록 그토록 선량하고 진실한 얼굴이었고, 또 개암빛 눈을 한 독신 남자 모핀 씨가 그것을 처음 본 그 순간부터 그에게 그토록 흐뭇한 얼굴이었지만, 환희의 빛으로 밝혀진 그 순간에는, 그 어느 때보다도 그를 덜 흐뭇하게 했다."]], [["“I need not remind you,” said Harriet, casting down her eyes upon her black dress, “through what means our circumstances changed.", "\"우리의 형편이 어떤 연유로 바뀌었는지 새삼 일깨워 드릴 필요는 없겠지요,\""], ["You have not forgotten that our brother James, upon that dreadful day, left no will, no relations but ourselves.”", "해리엇이 자기 검은 옷으로 눈길을 떨구며 말했다. \"우리 오빠 제임스가, 그 끔찍한 날, 유언장도 남기지 않고, 우리 말고는 아무 친척도 남기지 않았다는 것을 선생님은 잊지 않으셨을 거예요.\""]], [["The face was pleasanter to him now, though it was pale and melancholy, than it had been a moment since.", "이제 그 얼굴은, 비록 창백하고 우울했지만, 조금 전보다 그에게 더 흐뭇했다."], ["He seemed to breathe more cheerily.", "그는 한결 명랑하게 숨을 쉬는 듯했다."]], [["“You know,”", "\"선생님은 아시잖아요,\""], ["she said, “our history, the history of both my brothers, in connexion with the unfortunate, unhappy gentleman, of whom you have spoken so truly.", "그녀가 말했다, \"우리 집안의 내력을, 선생님이 그토록 진실하게 말씀하신 그 불운하고 불행한 신사분과 얽힌 제 두 오빠의 내력을요."], ["You know how few our wants are—John’s and mine—and what little use we have for money, after the life we have led together for so many years; and now that he is earning an income that is ample for us, through your kindness.", "선생님은 우리—존과 제가—얼마나 바라는 것이 적은지, 그리고 우리가 그토록 여러 해를 함께 살아온 끝에 돈이 얼마나 쓸데가 없는지, 그리고 이제 선생님의 친절 덕분에 오빠가 우리에게 넉넉한 수입을 벌고 있다는 것을 아시지요."], ["You are not unprepared to hear what favour I have come to ask of you?”", "제가 선생님께 청하러 온 부탁이 무엇인지 들으실 마음의 준비가 안 되신 건 아니지요?\""]], [["“I hardly know.", "\"잘 모르겠어요."], ["I was, a minute ago. Now, I think, I am not.”", "일 분 전만 해도 그랬는데, 지금은, 준비가 안 된 것 같군요.\""]], [["“Of my dead brother I say nothing.", "\"세상을 떠난 오빠에 대해서는 아무 말 않겠어요."], ["If the dead know what we do—but you understand me.", "죽은 이들이 우리가 하는 일을 안다면—하지만 제 말을 아시겠지요."], ["Of my living brother I could say much; but what need I say more, than that this act of duty, in which I have come to ask your indispensable assistance, is his own, and that he cannot rest until it is performed!”", "살아 있는 오빠에 대해서라면 많은 것을 말할 수 있겠지만, 제가 선생님의 없어서는 안 될 도움을 청하러 온 이 의무의 행위가 바로 오빠 자신의 것이며, 그것이 이루어지기까지 오빠가 마음 놓을 수 없으리라는 것 말고 무슨 말을 더 해야겠어요!\""]], [["She raised her eyes again; and the light of exultation in her face began to appear beautiful, in the observant eyes that watched her.", "그녀는 다시 눈을 들었다. 그러자 그녀를 지켜보던 그 관찰력 있는 눈에는, 그녀 얼굴의 환희의 빛이 아름다워 보이기 시작했다."]], [["“Dear Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["she went on to say, “it must be done very quietly and secretly.", "그녀가 말을 이었다, \"이 일은 아주 조용히, 비밀스럽게 이루어져야 해요."], ["Your experience and knowledge will point out a way of doing it.", "선생님의 경험과 지식이 그것을 해낼 방도를 일러 주겠지요."], ["Mr Dombey may, perhaps, be led to believe that it is something saved, unexpectedly, from the wreck of his fortunes; or that it is a voluntary tribute to his honourable and upright character, from some of those with whom he has had great dealings; or that it is some old lost debt repaid.", "어쩌면 돔비 씨가, 그것이 자기 재산의 난파 속에서 뜻밖에 건져 낸 무엇이라고 믿게 될 수도 있고, 혹은 그가 크게 거래해 온 사람들 가운데 누군가가 그의 명예롭고 올곧은 인품에 바치는 자발적인 헌사라고, 혹은 잊혀 있던 오래된 빚을 갚는 것이라고 믿게 될 수도 있겠지요. 그것을 해낼 방도는 많을 거예요."], ["There must be many ways of doing it.", "선생님이 가장 좋은 방도를 고르시리라는 걸 알아요. 제가 청하러 온 부탁은, 선생님이 그 친절하고 너그럽고 사려 깊은 방식으로 우리를 위해 그것을 해 주십사 하는 거예요."], ["I know you will choose the best.", "존에게는 결코 그 일을 말씀하지 마시고요."], ["The favour I have come to ask is, that you will do it for us in your own kind, generous, considerate manner.", "이 보상의 행위에서 오빠가 누리는 가장 큰 행복은, 그것을 비밀스럽게, 알려지지 않고, 인정받지 않은 채 하는 것이니까요. 또 상속 재산 가운데 아주 작은 부분만 우리에게 떼어 두시고, 돔비 씨가 살아가는 동안 나머지의 이자를 평생토록 받도록 해 주세요."], ["That you will never speak of it to John, whose chief happiness in this act of restitution is to do it secretly, unknown, and unapproved of: that only a very small part of the inheritance may be reserved to us, until Mr Dombey shall have possessed the interest of the rest for the remainder of his life; that you will keep our secret, faithfully—but that I am sure you will; and that, from this time, it may seldom be whispered, even between you and me, but may live in my thoughts only as a new reason for thankfulness to Heaven, and joy and pride in my brother.”", "우리의 비밀을 충실히 지켜 주시고요—하지만 그러시리라는 걸 확신해요. 그리고 이제부터는, 선생님과 저 사이에서조차 이 일이 좀처럼 입에 오르지 않고, 다만 제 생각 속에서, 하늘에 감사할 새로운 까닭으로, 그리고 오빠에 대한 기쁨과 자랑으로만 살아 있게 해 주세요.\""]], [["Such a look of exultation there may be on Angels’ faces when the one repentant sinner enters Heaven, among ninety-nine just men.", "의로운 사람 아흔아홉 명 가운데 회개한 죄인 하나가 천국에 들어설 때, 천사들의 얼굴에 떠오르는 환희의 빛이 바로 그러할 것이다."], ["It was not dimmed or tarnished by the joyful tears that filled her eyes, but was the brighter for them.", "그것은 그녀의 눈에 가득 차오른 기쁨의 눈물로도 흐려지거나 빛바래지 않았고, 오히려 그 눈물로 인해 더욱 밝게 빛났다."]], [["“My dear Harriet,”", "\"해리엇 양,\""], ["said Mr Morfin, after a silence, “I was not prepared for this.", "모핀 씨가 한동안 잠잠히 있다가 말했다, \"이건 미처 예상치 못한 일이군요."], ["Do I understand you that you wish to make your own part in the inheritance available for your good purpose, as well as John’s?”", "내가 제대로 이해한 거라면, 상속 재산에서 당신 자신의 몫도, 존의 몫과 마찬가지로, 당신의 그 선한 목적에 쓸 수 있도록 하고 싶다는 거지요?\""]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"오, 그럼요,\""], ["she returned “When we have shared everything together for so long a time, and have had no care, hope, or purpose apart, could I bear to be excluded from my share in this?", "그녀가 대답했다. \"우리가 그토록 오랜 세월 모든 것을 함께 나누어 왔고, 따로 떨어진 근심도, 희망도, 목적도 없었는데, 제가 이 일에서 제 몫에서 제외되는 것을 어찌 견딜 수 있겠어요?"], ["May I not urge a claim to be my brother’s partner and companion to the last?”", "끝까지 오빠의 동반자요 동무가 되겠다는 주장을 제가 내세우면 안 되는 건가요?\""]], [["“Heaven forbid that I should dispute it!”", "\"내가 그것을 두고 왈가왈부하다니, 천만의 말씀이에요!\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“We may rely on your friendly help?”", "\"선생님의 다정한 도움에 기대도 될까요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I knew we might!”", "\"그래도 된다는 걸 알았어요!\""]], [["“I should be a worse man than,—than I hope I am, or would willingly believe myself, if I could not give you that assurance from my heart and soul. You may, implicitly.", "\"내가 만일 마음과 영혼을 다해 당신에게 그 다짐을 드릴 수 없다면, 나는—내가 그러기를 바라는, 혹은 기꺼이 그렇다고 믿고 싶은 사람보다—더 못난 사람일 거예요."], ["Upon my honour, I will keep your secret.", "당신은 전적으로 기대도 좋아요."], ["And if it should be found that Mr Dombey is so reduced as I fear he will be, acting on a determination that there seem to be no means of influencing, I will assist you to accomplish the design, on which you and John are jointly resolved.”", "내 명예를 걸고, 당신의 비밀을 지키겠어요. 그리고 만일 돔비 씨가, 영향을 줄 방도라곤 없어 보이는 어떤 결심에 따라 행동하여, 내가 우려하는 만큼 영락한 것으로 드러난다면, 당신과 존이 함께 결심한 그 계획을 이루도록 내가 돕겠어요.\""]], [["She gave him her hand, and thanked him with a cordial, happy face.", "그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀고, 진심 어린 행복한 얼굴로 그에게 감사했다."]], [["“Harriet,”", "\"해리엇,\""], ["he said, detaining it in his.", "그가 그 손을 자기 손에 잡아 둔 채 말했다."], ["“To speak to you of the worth of any sacrifice that you can make now—above all, of any sacrifice of mere money—would be idle and presumptuous.", "\"당신이 이제 할 수 있는 어떤 희생이든—무엇보다도 한낱 돈의 어떤 희생이든—그 가치에 대해 당신에게 말한다는 것은 부질없고 주제넘은 일일 거예요."], ["To put before you any appeal to reconsider your purpose or to set narrow limits to it, would be, I feel, not less so.", "당신에게 그 목적을 다시 생각해 보라거나 거기에 좁은 한계를 두라고 호소하는 것은, 내 생각에, 그에 못지않게 그러하겠지요."], ["I have no right to mar the great end of a great history, by any obtrusion of my own weak self.", "나의 보잘것없는 자아를 들이밀어, 위대한 내력의 위대한 결말을 망칠 권리는 내게 없어요. 당신이 내게 맡기는 것 앞에 고개를 숙일 권리는 온전히 내게 있지요."], ["I have every right to bend my head before what you confide to me, satisfied that it comes from a higher and better source of inspiration than my poor worldly knowledge.", "그것이 내 빈약한 세속의 지식보다 더 높고 더 선한 영감의 원천에서 비롯된 것임을 확신하니까요. 단지 이 말만 하겠어요."], ["I will say only this: I am your faithful steward; and I would rather be so, and your chosen friend, than I would be anybody in the world, except yourself.”", "나는 당신의 충실한 집사예요. 그리고 나는 당신 자신을 빼고는, 세상의 그 누가 되기보다, 차라리 당신의 그 집사이자 당신이 택한 벗이 되고 싶어요.\""]], [["She thanked him again, cordially, and wished him good-night.", "그녀는 다시 진심으로 그에게 감사하고, 그에게 잘 자라는 인사를 했다."]], [["“Are you going home?”", "\"집에 가시는 거요?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Let me go with you.”", "\"내가 함께 가지요.\""]], [["“Not tonight.", "\"오늘 밤은 안 돼요."], ["I am not going home now; I have a visit to make alone.", "지금은 집에 가는 게 아니에요. 혼자 다녀와야 할 방문이 있거든요."], ["Will you come to-morrow?”", "내일 와 주시겠어요?\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"그래요, 그래,\""], ["said he, “I’ll come to-morrow.", "그가 말했다, \"내일 가지요."], ["In the meantime, I’ll think of this, and how we can best proceed.", "그동안 이 일을, 그리고 우리가 어떻게 하는 것이 가장 좋을지를 생각해 보겠어요."], ["And perhaps I’ll think of it, dear Harriet, and—and—think of me a little in connexion with it.”", "그리고 어쩌면 그것을, 사랑하는 해리엇, 그것을 생각하면서—그리고—그리고 그것과 더불어 나도 조금은 생각해 주시겠지요.\""]], [["He handed her down to a coach she had in waiting at the door; and if his landlady had not been deaf, she would have heard him muttering as he went back upstairs, when the coach had driven off, that we were creatures of habit, and it was a sorrowful habit to be an old bachelor.", "그는 그녀를 문간에 대기시켜 둔 마차까지 부축해 내려보냈다. 그리고 만일 그의 하숙집 여주인이 귀가 먹지 않았더라면, 마차가 떠난 뒤 그가 다시 위층으로 올라가며, 우리란 습관의 피조물이며, 늙은 독신으로 산다는 것은 서글픈 습관이라고 중얼거리는 소리를 들었을 것이다."]], [["The violoncello lying on the sofa between the two chairs, he took it up, without putting away the vacant chair, and sat droning on it, and slowly shaking his head at the vacant chair, for a long, long time.", "두 의자 사이 소파에 첼로가 놓여 있었는데, 그는 빈 의자를 치워 두지도 않은 채 그것을 집어 들고, 앉아서 거기에 대고 웅웅거리며, 빈 의자를 향해 천천히 고개를 저으면서, 오래오래 그렇게 있었다."], ["The expression he communicated to the instrument at first, though monstrously pathetic and bland, was nothing to the expression he communicated to his own face, and bestowed upon the empty chair: which was so sincere, that he was obliged to have recourse to Captain Cuttle’s remedy more than once, and to rub his face with his sleeve.", "그가 처음에 그 악기에 실어 보낸 표정은, 비록 끔찍하게 애처롭고 부드러웠지만, 그가 자기 자신의 얼굴에 실어 그 빈 의자에 베푼 표정에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그 표정이 어찌나 진실했던지, 그는 한 번 이상 커틀 선장의 처방에 의지하여, 소맷자락으로 얼굴을 문질러야 했다."], ["By degrees, however, the violoncello, in unison with his own frame of mind, glided melodiously into the Harmonious Blacksmith, which he played over and over again, until his ruddy and serene face gleamed like true metal on the anvil of a veritable blacksmith.", "그러나 차츰, 첼로는 그 자신의 심경과 어우러져, 가락 좋게 미끄러져 「조화로운 대장장이」로 들어갔고, 그는 그것을 거듭거듭 연주하다가, 마침내 혈색 좋고 평온한 그의 얼굴이 진짜 대장장이의 모루 위에 놓인 진짜 쇠붙이처럼 번득였다."], ["In fine, the violoncello and the empty chair were the companions of his bachelorhood until nearly midnight; and when he took his supper, the violoncello set up on end in the sofa corner, big with the latent harmony of a whole foundry full of harmonious blacksmiths, seemed to ogle the empty chair out of its crooked eyes, with unutterable intelligence.", "요컨대, 첼로와 그 빈 의자는 자정 무렵까지 그의 독신 생활의 동무가 되어 주었다. 그리고 그가 저녁을 들 때, 소파 구석에 곧추세워진 첼로는, 조화로운 대장장이들로 가득 찬 한 주물 공장의 잠재된 화음을 잔뜩 머금은 채, 그 비뚜름한 눈으로 빈 의자를 향해, 이루 말할 수 없는 총기로 추파를 던지는 듯했다."]], [["When Harriet left the house, the driver of her hired coach, taking a course that was evidently no new one to him, went in and out by bye-ways, through that part of the suburbs, until he arrived at some open ground, where there were a few quiet little old houses standing among gardens.", "해리엇이 그 집을 떠나자, 그녀가 빌린 마차의 마부는, 그에게는 분명 새로울 것 없는 길을 잡아, 그 교외 지역을 이리저리 샛길로 누비다가, 정원들 사이에 자그마하고 조용한 옛집 몇 채가 서 있는 어느 빈터에 이르렀다."], ["At the garden-gate of one of these he stopped, and Harriet alighted.", "그 가운데 한 집의 정원 문 앞에서 마부가 멈추자, 해리엇이 내렸다."]], [["Her gentle ringing at the bell was responded to by a dolorous-looking woman, of light complexion, with raised eyebrows, and head drooping on one side, who curtseyed at sight of her, and conducted her across the garden to the house.", "그녀가 종을 부드럽게 울리자, 살빛이 희고 눈썹을 치켜올린 채 머리를 한쪽으로 떨군, 비통해 보이는 여인이 응답했다. 여인은 그녀를 보자 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는, 정원을 가로질러 집으로 그녀를 안내했다."]], [["“How is your patient, nurse, tonight?”", "\"간호인, 오늘 밤 환자분은 좀 어떤가요?\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."]], [["“In a poor way, Miss, I am afraid.", "\"형편이 안 좋답니다, 아가씨, 그게 걱정이에요."], ["Oh how she do remind me, sometimes, of my Uncle’s Betsey Jane!”", "아, 저 사람을 보면 가끔 어쩜 그리도 우리 외삼촌네 베치 제인이 떠오르는지!\""], ["returned the woman of the light complexion, in a sort of doleful rapture.", "살빛 흰 여인이 일종의 구슬픈 황홀경에 잠겨 대답했다."]], [["“In what respect?”", "\"어떤 점에서요?\""], ["asked Harriet.", "해리엇이 물었다."]], [["“Miss, in all respects,”", "\"아가씨, 모든 점에서요,\""], ["replied the other, “except that she’s grown up, and Betsey Jane, when at death’s door, was but a child.”", "상대가 대답했다, \"다만 저 사람은 다 큰 어른이고, 베치 제인은 죽음의 문턱에 이르렀을 때 아직 어린애였다는 것만 빼고요.\""]], [["“But you have told me she recovered,”", "\"하지만 그 애가 회복되었다고 하지 않았나요,\""], ["observed Harriet mildly; “so there is the more reason for hope, Mrs Wickam.”", "해리엇이 부드럽게 말했다, \"그러니 더더욱 희망을 가질 까닭이 있는 거지요, 위컴 부인.\""]], [["“Ah, Miss, hope is an excellent thing for such as has the spirits to bear it!”", "\"아, 아가씨, 희망이란 그것을 견딜 만한 기운을 가진 이들에게나 좋은 것이지요!\""], ["said Mrs Wickam, shaking her head.", "위컴 부인이 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["“My own spirits is not equal to it, but I don’t owe it any grudge.", "\"제 기운은 그만 못하답니다만, 그렇다고 제가 그걸 원망하는 건 아니에요."], ["I envys them that is so blest!”", "그렇게 복받은 이들을 부러워할 뿐이지요!\""]], [["“You should try to be more cheerful,”", "\"좀 더 명랑해지려고 애써 보셔야지요.\""], ["remarked Harriet.", "해리엇이 일렀다."]], [["“Thank you, Miss, I’m sure,”", "\"고맙습니다, 아가씨, 정말요,\""], ["said Mrs Wickam grimly.", "위컴 부인이 침울하게 말했다."], ["“If I was so inclined, the loneliness of this situation—you’ll excuse my speaking so free—would put it out of my power, in four and twenty hours; but I ain’t at all.", "\"제가 설령 그러고 싶은 마음이 든다 해도, 이런 처지의 외로움이—이렇게 거리낌 없이 말씀드리는 걸 용서하세요—스물네 시간 안에 그럴 힘을 빼앗아 갈 거예요. 하지만 저는 그럴 마음이 전혀 없답니다."], ["I’d rather not.", "차라리 안 그러는 게 낫지요."], ["The little spirits that I ever had, I was bereaved of at Brighton some few years ago, and I think I feel myself the better for it.”", "제가 그나마 가지고 있던 쥐꼬리만 한 기운마저, 몇 해 전에 브라이튼에서 잃어버렸는데, 저는 그게 오히려 제게 나은 것 같아요.\""]], [["In truth, this was the very Mrs Wickam who had superseded Mrs Richards as the nurse of little Paul, and who considered herself to have gained the loss in question, under the roof of the amiable Pipchin.", "사실 이 사람은 바로, 어린 폴의 유모로 리처즈 부인을 대신했던 그 위컴 부인으로, 사람 좋은 핍친 부인의 지붕 아래서 바로 그 잃어버림을 얻었다고 스스로 여기던 이였다."], ["The excellent and thoughtful old system, hallowed by long prescription, which has usually picked out from the rest of mankind the most dreary and uncomfortable people that could possibly be laid hold of, to act as instructors of youth, finger-posts to the virtues, matrons, monitors, attendants on sick beds, and the like, had established Mrs Wickam in very good business as a nurse, and had led to her serious qualities being particularly commended by an admiring and numerous connexion.", "오래도록 관습으로 떠받들어진, 그 훌륭하고 사려 깊은 옛 제도—나머지 인류 가운데에서 손에 넣을 수 있는 가장 음울하고 마뜩잖은 사람들을 으레 골라내어, 젊은이를 가르치는 교사로, 미덕을 가리키는 이정표로, 사감으로, 감독으로, 병상의 간병인으로, 그리고 그 비슷한 일들을 맡게 하는 제도—는, 위컴 부인을 간호인으로서 아주 잘되는 일에 자리 잡게 해 주었고, 그녀의 진중한 자질이 그녀를 우러르는 수많은 연줄로부터 각별히 칭송받도록 이끌었다."]], [["Mrs Wickam, with her eyebrows elevated, and her head on one side, lighted the way upstairs to a clean, neat chamber, opening on another chamber dimly lighted, where there was a bed.", "위컴 부인은, 눈썹을 치켜올리고 머리를 한쪽으로 기울인 채, 깨끗하고 정갈한 방으로 이어지는 위층 길을 밝히며 올라갔다. 그 방은 침대가 놓인, 희미하게 불 밝힌 또 다른 방으로 통해 있었다."], ["In the first room, an old woman sat mechanically staring out at the open window, on the darkness.", "첫 번째 방에서는 한 노파가 열린 창밖의 어둠을 기계적으로 응시하며 앉아 있었다."], ["In the second, stretched upon the bed, lay the shadow of a figure that had spurned the wind and rain, one wintry night; hardly to be recognised now, but by the long black hair that showed so very black against the colourless face, and all the white things about it.", "두 번째 방에서는, 침대 위에 길게 누운 채, 어느 겨울밤 비바람을 마다하고 떨쳐 냈던 한 형체의 그림자가 누워 있었다. 이제는 알아보기 어려웠으니, 다만 핏기 없는 얼굴과 그 주위의 온통 흰 것들에 대비되어 그토록 새카맣게 두드러지는 긴 검은 머리칼로나 알아볼 수 있을 따름이었다."]], [["Oh, the strong eyes, and the weak frame!", "오, 그 강한 눈과, 그 쇠약한 몸뚱이!"], ["The eyes that turned so eagerly and brightly to the door when Harriet came in; the feeble head that could not raise itself, and moved so slowly round upon its pillow!", "해리엇이 들어왔을 때 그토록 간절하고 환하게 문 쪽으로 돌아간 그 눈, 스스로 들어 올리지도 못한 채 베개 위에서 그토록 느릿하게 돌아간 그 가냘픈 머리!"]], [["“Alice!”", "\"앨리스!\""], ["said the visitor’s mild voice, “am I late tonight?”", "방문객의 부드러운 목소리가 말했다, \"내가 오늘 밤은 늦었나요?\""]], [["“You always seem late, but are always early.”", "\"늘 늦은 것 같지만, 늘 일찍 오시지요.\""]], [["Harriet had sat down by the bedside now, and put her hand upon the thin hand lying there.", "해리엇은 이제 침대 곁에 앉아, 거기 놓인 야윈 손 위에 자기 손을 얹었다."]], [["“You are better?”", "\"좀 나아졌어요?\""]], [["Mrs Wickam, standing at the foot of the bed, like a disconsolate spectre, most decidedly and forcibly shook her head to negative this position.", "침대 발치에 서 있던 위컴 부인은, 비통한 망령처럼, 이 견해를 부정하느라 단호하고도 힘차게 고개를 저었다."]], [["“It matters very little!”", "\"그런 건 아무래도 상관없어요!\""], ["said Alice, with a faint smile.", "앨리스가 희미하게 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“Better or worse today, is but a day’s difference—perhaps not so much.”", "\"오늘 나아지든 나빠지든, 그래 봤자 하루 차이일 뿐인걸요—어쩌면 그만큼도 안 되고요.\""]], [["Mrs Wickam, as a serious character, expressed her approval with a groan; and having made some cold dabs at the bottom of the bedclothes, as feeling for the patient’s feet and expecting to find them stony; went clinking among the medicine bottles on the table, as who should say, “while we are here, let us repeat the mixture as before.”", "위컴 부인은, 진중한 인물답게, 신음 소리로 동의를 표했다. 그러고는 환자의 발을 더듬어 차디찬 돌처럼 굳어 있기를 기대하기라도 하듯, 이불 밑자락을 몇 번 싸늘하게 두드려 보고는, 마치 \"이왕 여기 와 있는 김에, 전에 했던 대로 약을 다시 한 번 먹입시다\"라고 말하기라도 하는 양, 탁자 위의 약병들 사이에서 쩔그렁거리며 다녔다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요,\""], ["said Alice, whispering to her visitor, “evil courses, and remorse, travel, want, and weather, storm within, and storm without, have worn my life away.", "앨리스가 방문객에게 속삭였다, \"사악한 행실과, 회한과, 떠돌이 생활과, 궁핍과, 모진 날씨와, 안의 폭풍과 밖의 폭풍이, 제 목숨을 닳아 없애 버렸어요."], ["It will not last much longer.", "그리 오래가지는 못할 거예요.\""]], [["She drew the hand up as she spoke, and laid her face against it.", "그녀는 말하면서 그 손을 끌어 올려, 거기에 자기 얼굴을 갖다 대었다."]], [["“I lie here, sometimes, thinking I should like to live until I had had a little time to show you how grateful I could be!", "\"제가 여기 누워서, 이따금 이런 생각을 해요. 제가 얼마나 고마워할 수 있는지 아가씨에게 보여 드릴 만큼만이라도 시간을 가질 때까지 살았으면 좋겠다고요!"], ["It is a weakness, and soon passes.", "그건 약한 마음이고, 이내 지나가 버려요."], ["Better for you as it is.", "이대로가 아가씨에게는 더 나아요."], ["Better for me!”", "제게도 더 낫고요!\""]], [["How different her hold upon the hand, from what it had been when she took it by the fireside on the bleak winter evening!", "황량한 겨울 저녁, 난롯가에서 그 손을 잡았을 때와는 그녀가 그 손을 붙잡은 모습이 어찌나 다른지!"], ["Scorn, rage, defiance, recklessness, look here!", "경멸, 분노, 반항, 무모함이여, 여기를 보라!"], ["This is the end.", "이것이 그 끝이다."]], [["Mrs Wickam having clinked sufficiently among the bottles, now produced the mixture.", "약병들 사이에서 충분히 쩔그렁거린 위컴 부인이, 이제 약을 내왔다."], ["Mrs Wickam looked hard at her patient in the act of drinking, screwed her mouth up tight, her eyebrows also, and shook her head, expressing that tortures shouldn’t make her say it was a hopeless case.", "위컴 부인은 환자가 약을 마시는 동안 그녀를 빤히 쏘아보고는, 입을 앙다물고, 눈썹도 함께 찡그리며, 고개를 저었으니, 고문을 당해도 자기 입으로 가망 없는 경우라고는 말하지 않겠다는 뜻이었다."], ["Mrs Wickam then sprinkled a little cooling-stuff about the room, with the air of a female grave-digger, who was strewing ashes on ashes, dust on dust—for she was a serious character—and withdrew to partake of certain funeral baked meats downstairs.", "그런 다음 위컴 부인은, 재 위에 재를, 흙 위에 흙을 흩뿌리는 여자 무덤지기 같은 태도로—그녀는 진중한 인물이었으니까—방 안에 식혀 주는 약을 조금 뿌리고는, 아래층에서 무슨 장례식 구운 고기라도 들러 물러갔다."]], [["“How long is it,”", "\"제가 아가씨를 찾아가, 제가 한 일을 말씀드린 게,\""], ["asked Alice, “since I went to you and told you what I had done, and when you were advised it was too late for anyone to follow?”", "앨리스가 물었다, \"그리고 이제는 누구도 뒤쫓기엔 너무 늦었다는 말씀을 아가씨가 들으신 게, 얼마나 되었지요?\""]], [["“It is a year and more,”", "\"일 년하고도 더 되었어요.\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."]], [["“A year and more,”", "\"일 년하고도 더,\""], ["said Alice, thoughtfully intent upon her face.", "앨리스가 그녀의 얼굴을 깊이 응시하며 생각에 잠겨 말했다."], ["“Months upon months since you brought me here!”", "\"아가씨가 저를 이리로 데려온 지도 여러 달이 지났군요!\""]], [["Harriet answered “Yes.”", "해리엇이 \"그래요\"라고 대답했다."]], [["“Brought me here, by force of gentleness and kindness. Me!”", "\"저를 이리로 데려오셨지요, 부드러움과 친절의 힘으로요."], ["said Alice, shrinking with her face behind her hand, “and made me human by woman’s looks and words, and angel’s deeds!”", "이런 저를요!\" 앨리스가 손 뒤로 얼굴을 움츠리며 말했다, \"그리고 여인의 눈길과 말로, 천사의 행실로, 저를 사람답게 만들어 주셨어요!\""]], [["Harriet bending over her, composed and soothed her.", "해리엇은 그녀에게 몸을 굽혀, 그녀를 가라앉히고 달랬다."], ["By and bye, Alice lying as before, with the hand against her face, asked to have her mother called.", "이윽고 앨리스는 아까처럼 손을 얼굴에 댄 채 누워, 자기 어머니를 불러 달라고 청했다."]], [["Harriet called to her more than once, but the old woman was so absorbed looking out at the open window on the darkness, that she did not hear.", "해리엇이 한 번 이상 그녀를 불렀으나, 노파는 열린 창밖의 어둠을 내다보는 데 어찌나 골몰해 있던지 듣지 못했다."], ["It was not until Harriet went to her and touched her, that she rose up, and came.", "해리엇이 그녀에게 가서 그녀를 건드리고 나서야, 그녀는 일어나 다가왔다."]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["said Alice, taking the hand again, and fixing her lustrous eyes lovingly upon her visitor, while she merely addressed a motion of her finger to the old woman, “tell her what you know.”", "앨리스가 그 손을 다시 잡고, 자기의 빛나는 눈을 다정스레 방문객에게 고정한 채, 노파에게는 그저 손가락으로 까딱 동작만 보내며 말했다, \"어머니가 아시는 걸 이분께 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“Tonight, my deary?”", "\"오늘 밤에, 우리 아가?\""]], [["“Ay, mother,”", "\"네, 어머니,\""], ["answered Alice, faintly and solemnly, “tonight!”", "앨리스가 희미하고 엄숙하게 대답했다, \"오늘 밤에요!\""]], [["The old woman, whose wits appeared disorderly by alarm, remorse, or grief, came creeping along the side of the bed, opposite to that on which Harriet sat; and kneeling down, so as to bring her withered face upon a level with the coverlet, and stretching out her hand, so as to touch her daughter’s arm, began:", "경악과 회한과 비탄으로 정신이 어지러워진 듯한 노파는, 해리엇이 앉은 쪽의 맞은편으로, 침대 곁을 따라 기어 왔다. 그러고는 무릎을 꿇어, 시든 얼굴을 이불보와 같은 높이로 가져오고, 손을 뻗어 딸의 팔에 닿게 하고는, 입을 열었다."]], [["“My handsome gal—”", "\"내 어여쁜 딸아—\""]], [["Heaven, what a cry was that, with which she stopped there, gazing at the poor form lying on the bed!", "오 하느님, 그녀가 거기서 말을 멈추고, 침대에 누운 그 가엾은 형체를 응시하며 내지른 그 비명이라니!"]], [["“Changed, long ago, mother!", "\"오래전에 변해 버렸어요, 어머니!"], ["Withered, long ago,”", "오래전에 시들어 버렸고요,\""], ["said Alice, without looking at her.", "앨리스가 그녀를 쳐다보지도 않은 채 말했다."], ["“Don’t grieve for that now.”", "\"이제 와서 그 일로 슬퍼하지 마세요.\""]], [["“—My daughter,”", "\"—내 딸,\""], ["faltered the old woman, “my gal who’ll soon get better, and shame ’em all with her good looks.”", "노파가 더듬더듬 말했다, \"곧 나아져서, 그 고운 얼굴로 그치들을 다 부끄럽게 만들 내 딸.\""]], [["Alice smiled mournfully at Harriet, and fondled her hand a little closer, but said nothing.", "앨리스는 해리엇을 향해 구슬프게 미소 짓고, 그녀의 손을 조금 더 가까이 어루만졌으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Who’ll soon get better, I say,”", "\"곧 나아질 거라니까, 내 말은,\""], ["repeated the old woman, menacing the vacant air with her shrivelled fist, “and who’ll shame ’em all with her good looks—she will.", "노파가 쪼그라든 주먹으로 텅 빈 허공을 위협하며 되풀이했다, \"그리고 그 고운 얼굴로 그치들을 다 부끄럽게 만들 거야—그럴 거야."], ["I say she will!", "내 말은 그럴 거라니까!"], ["she shall!”—as if she were in passionate contention with some unseen opponent at the bedside, who contradicted her—“my daughter has been turned away from, and cast out, but she could boast relationship to proud folks too, if she chose.", "그래야 해!\"—마치 그녀에게 반박하는, 침대 곁의 보이지 않는 어떤 적수와 격렬하게 다투기라도 하는 것처럼—\"내 딸은 외면당하고 내쫓겼지만, 마음만 먹으면 콧대 높은 양반들과의 친척 관계도 내세울 수 있다고."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["To proud folks!", "콧대 높은 양반들과 말이야!"], ["There’s relationship without your clergy and your wedding rings—they may make it, but they can’t break it—and my daughter’s well related.", "당신네 목사도 결혼반지도 없는 친척 관계라는 게 있는 법이야—그치들이 그걸 맺을 수는 있어도, 끊을 수는 없지—그리고 내 딸은 좋은 집안과 닿아 있다고."], ["Show me Mrs Dombey, and I’ll show you my Alice’s first cousin.”", "돔비 부인을 내게 보여 봐, 그러면 내 앨리스의 사촌을 보여 줄 테니.\""]], [["Harriet glanced from the old woman to the lustrous eyes intent upon her face, and derived corroboration from them.", "해리엇은 노파에게서 자기 얼굴을 골똘히 응시하는 그 빛나는 눈으로 시선을 옮겼고, 거기서 확증을 얻었다."]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["cried the old woman, her nodding head bridling with a ghastly vanity.", "노파가, 끄덕이는 머리를 섬뜩한 허영으로 빳빳이 세우며 외쳤다."], ["“Though I am old and ugly now,—much older by life and habit than years though,—I was once as young as any.", "\"내가 지금은 늙고 추하지만,—세월보다는 험한 삶과 버릇 탓에 훨씬 더 늙었지만,—나도 한때는 누구 못지않게 젊었다고."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["as pretty too, as many!", "누구 못지않게 예쁘기도 했지!"], ["I was a fresh country wench in my time, darling,”", "나도 한창때는 싱싱한 시골 처녀였단다, 아가,\""], ["stretching out her arm to Harriet, across the bed, “and looked it, too.", "침대 너머로 해리엇에게 팔을 뻗으며, \"그렇게 보이기도 했고."], ["Down in my country, Mrs Dombey’s father and his brother were the gayest gentlemen and the best-liked that came a visiting from London—they have long been dead, though!", "우리 시골 동네로 말이야, 돔비 부인의 아버지하고 그 형제분이, 런던에서 놀러 오던 신사들 가운데 제일 명랑하고 제일 인기 있는 분들이었지—하기야 두 분 다 돌아가신 지 오래됐지만!"], ["Lord, Lord, this long while!", "어이구, 어이구, 정말 오래됐어!"], ["The brother, who was my Ally’s father, longest of the two.”", "우리 앨리의 아비였던 그 형제분이, 둘 중에 제일 오래 사셨지.\""]], [["She raised her head a little, and peered at her daughter’s face; as if from the remembrance of her own youth, she had flown to the remembrance of her child’s.", "그녀는 머리를 조금 쳐들고, 딸의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 마치 제 젊은 날의 기억에서, 제 자식의 기억으로 날아가 버린 듯이."], ["Then, suddenly, she laid her face down on the bed, and shut her head up in her hands and arms.", "그러더니 갑자기, 그녀는 얼굴을 침대에 파묻고, 두 손과 두 팔로 머리를 감싸 가렸다."]], [["“They were as like,”", "\"두 분이 어찌나 닮았던지,\""], ["said the old woman, without looking up, as you could see two brothers, so near an age—there wasn’t much more than a year between them, as I recollect—and if you could have seen my gal, as I have seen her once, side by side with the other’s daughter, you’d have seen, for all the difference of dress and life, that they were like each other.", "노파가 고개도 들지 않은 채 말했다, \"나이 차이가 얼마 안 나는 형제 둘을 보고 그만큼 닮았다 할 만큼이었지—내 기억으로는 둘 사이가 한 살 남짓밖에 차이가 안 났어—그리고 만일 내 딸을, 내가 언젠가 한 번 본 것처럼, 그 다른 분의 딸과 나란히 세워 놓고 보았더라면, 옷차림과 살아온 처지가 아무리 달라도, 둘이 서로 닮았다는 걸 보았을 거야."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["is the likeness gone, and is it my gal—only my gal—that’s to change so!”", "그 닮음은 사라져 버리고, 이제 내 딸이—오직 내 딸만이—이렇게 변해 가야 한단 말인가!\""]], [["“We shall all change, mother, in our turn,”", "\"우리 모두 변할 거예요, 어머니, 차례차례,\""], ["said Alice.", "앨리스가 말했다."]], [["“Turn!”", "\"차례라고!\""], ["cried the old woman, “but why not hers as soon as my gal’s!", "노파가 외쳤다, \"한데 어째서 내 딸만큼 빨리 그 여자의 차례는 안 오느냔 말이야!"], ["The mother must have changed—she looked as old as me, and full as wrinkled through her paint—but she was handsome.", "그 어미도 변했을 테지—그 여자도 나만큼 늙어 보였고, 분칠을 했어도 주름이 자글자글했으니까—하지만 그래도 그 여자는 곱더라."], ["What have I done, I, what have I done worse than her, that only my gal is to lie there fading!”", "내가, 이 내가, 그 여자보다 뭘 더 못한 짓을 했길래, 내 딸만 저기 누워 시들어 가야 한단 말이냐!\""]], [["With another of those wild cries, she went running out into the room from which she had come; but immediately, in her uncertain mood, returned, and creeping up to Harriet, said:", "그녀는 또 한 번 그 거친 비명을 지르며, 자기가 나왔던 방으로 달려 들어갔다. 그러나 이내, 그 변덕스러운 기분에, 되돌아와서는 해리엇에게로 슬금슬금 다가와 말했다."]], [["“That’s what Alice bade me tell you, deary.", "\"저게 바로 앨리스가 아가씨께 전하라던 거예요, 아가."], ["That’s all.", "그게 다예요."], ["I found it out when I began to ask who she was, and all about her, away in Warwickshire there, one summer-time.", "내가 어느 여름철에, 저 멀리 워릭셔에서, 그 여자가 누군지, 그 여자에 관한 온갖 걸 캐묻기 시작했을 때 알아낸 거지요."], ["Such relations was no good to me, then.", "그런 친척이라야 그때 내겐 아무 쓸모가 없었어요."], ["They wouldn’t have owned me, and had nothing to give me.", "그치들은 나를 친척으로 인정하지도 않았을 테고, 내게 줄 것도 없었으니까."], ["I should have asked ’em, maybe, for a little money, afterwards, if it hadn’t been for my Alice; she’d a’most have killed me, if I had, I think.", "우리 앨리스만 아니었으면, 나중에 어쩌면 돈이나 좀 달라고 그치들에게 청했을지도 몰라요. 만일 내가 그랬더라면, 우리 애가 나를 거의 죽이려 들었을 거예요, 내 생각엔."], ["She was as proud as t’other in her way,”", "그 애도 제 나름으로는 그 여자만큼이나 콧대가 높았지요,\""], ["said the old woman, touching the face of her daughter fearfully, and withdrawing her hand, “for all she’s so quiet now; but she’ll shame ’em with her good looks yet.", "노파가 두려운 듯 딸의 얼굴을 만지고는 손을 거두며 말했다, \"지금은 저렇게 얌전하지만 말이에요. 하지만 그래도 그 고운 얼굴로 그치들을 부끄럽게 만들 거예요."], ["Ha, ha!", "하하!"], ["She’ll shame ’em, will my handsome daughter!”", "부끄럽게 만들고말고, 내 어여쁜 딸이!\""]], [["Her laugh, as she retreated, was worse than her cry; worse than the burst of imbecile lamentation in which it ended; worse than the doting air with which she sat down in her old seat, and stared out at the darkness.", "그녀가 물러나며 짓는 웃음은, 그녀의 비명보다 더 끔찍했다. 그 웃음이 끝내 잦아들며 터져 나온 백치 같은 비탄의 절규보다도 더 끔찍했고, 그녀가 제 옛 자리에 주저앉아 어둠을 응시하던 그 노망 든 기색보다도 더 끔찍했다."]], [["The eyes of Alice had all this time been fixed on Harriet, whose hand she had never released.", "앨리스의 눈은 이 내내 해리엇에게 고정되어 있었고, 그녀는 그 손을 한 번도 놓지 않았다."], ["She said now:", "이제 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I have felt, lying here, that I should like you to know this.", "\"여기 누워서, 아가씨가 이걸 아셨으면 좋겠다고 느꼈어요."], ["It might explain, I have thought, something that used to help to harden me.", "어쩌면 이게, 저를 모질게 만드는 데 일조하곤 했던 무언가를 설명해 줄지도 모른다고 생각했거든요."], ["I had heard so much, in my wrongdoing, of my neglected duty, that I took up with the belief that duty had not been done to me, and that as the seed was sown, the harvest grew.", "제가 나쁜 짓을 하는 동안, 제 저버린 의무에 대한 말을 어찌나 많이 들었던지, 저는 오히려 제게 마땅한 의무가 다해지지 않았다는, 그리고 씨를 뿌린 대로 거둔 거라는 믿음을 갖게 되었어요."], ["I somehow made it out that when ladies had bad homes and mothers, they went wrong in their way, too; but that their way was not so foul a one as mine, and they had need to bless God for it.", "어찌어찌 저는 이렇게 생각하게 됐지요. 귀부인들도 나쁜 가정과 나쁜 어머니를 두면 자기들 식으로 그릇된 길에 빠지지만, 다만 그들의 길은 제 길만큼 더럽지 않으며, 그러니 그들은 그것을 하느님께 감사해야 한다고요."], ["That is all past.", "그건 다 지나간 일이에요."], ["It is like a dream, now, which I cannot quite remember or understand.", "이제는 그게, 제가 잘 기억하거나 이해할 수 없는 꿈처럼 느껴져요."], ["It has been more and more like a dream, every day, since you began to sit here, and to read to me.", "아가씨가 여기 앉아 제게 글을 읽어 주기 시작한 뒤로, 날마다 점점 더 꿈처럼 되어 갔어요."], ["I only tell it you, as I can recollect it.", "저는 그저 제가 기억할 수 있는 대로 그걸 말씀드리는 거예요."], ["Will you read to me a little more?”", "제게 조금만 더 읽어 주시겠어요?\""]], [["Harriet was withdrawing her hand to open the book, when Alice detained it for a moment.", "해리엇이 책을 펴려고 손을 빼려는데, 앨리스가 잠시 그 손을 붙잡았다."]], [["“You will not forget my mother?", "\"제 어머니를 잊지 않으실 거지요?"], ["I forgive her, if I have any cause.", "제게 무슨 원망할 까닭이 있다 해도, 저는 어머니를 용서해요."], ["I know that she forgives me, and is sorry in her heart.", "어머니가 저를 용서하시고, 마음으로 미안해하신다는 걸 저는 알아요. 어머니를 잊지 않으실 거지요?\""], ["You will not forget her?”", "\"결코 잊지 않을게요, 앨리스!\""]], [["“Never, Alice!”", "\"잠깐만요."], ["“A moment yet. Lay your head so, dear, that as you read I may see the words in your kind face.”", "제가 읽어 주시는 동안 아가씨의 그 다정한 얼굴 속에서 그 말씀을 볼 수 있게, 머리를 이렇게 두어 주세요, 아가씨.\""]], [["Harriet complied and read—read the eternal book for all the weary, and the heavy-laden; for all the wretched, fallen, and neglected of this earth—read the blessed history, in which the blind lame palsied beggar, the criminal, the woman stained with shame, the shunned of all our dainty clay, has each a portion, that no human pride, indifference, or sophistry, through all the ages that this world shall last, can take away, or by the thousandth atom of a grain reduce—read the ministry of Him who, through the round of human life, and all its hopes and griefs, from birth to death, from infancy to age, had sweet compassion for, and interest in, its every scene and stage, its every suffering and sorrow.", "해리엇이 그대로 따라, 읽었다—지치고 무거운 짐을 진 모든 이를 위한, 이 땅의 모든 비참한 이, 타락한 이, 버림받은 이를 위한, 그 영원한 책을 읽었다—앞 못 보고, 다리 절고, 중풍 든 거지도, 죄인도, 수치로 더럽혀진 여인도, 우리네 고운 흙덩이들 모두에게 따돌림당하는 이도, 저마다 한 몫을 지니고 있어, 이 세상이 지속되는 모든 세월 동안 인간의 그 어떤 오만도, 무관심도, 궤변도 그것을 앗아 가거나 한 톨의 천분의 일만큼도 줄일 수 없는, 그 복된 이야기를 읽었다—인간 삶의 전 과정과, 그 모든 희망과 슬픔을 두루, 태어남에서 죽음까지, 갓난아이 적부터 노년까지, 그 삶의 모든 장면과 단계에, 그 모든 고통과 비애에 다정한 연민과 관심을 품으셨던 그분의 사역을 읽었다."]], [["“I shall come,”", "\"내일 아침,\""], ["said Harriet, when she shut the book, “very early in the morning.”", "해리엇이 책을 덮으며 말했다, \"아주 일찍 올게요.\""]], [["The lustrous eyes, yet fixed upon her face, closed for a moment, then opened; and Alice kissed and blest her.", "여전히 그녀의 얼굴에 고정되어 있던 그 빛나는 눈이, 잠시 감겼다가 다시 떠졌다. 그리고 앨리스는 그녀에게 입 맞추고 그녀를 축복했다."]], [["The same eyes followed her to the door; and in their light, and on the tranquil face, there was a smile when it was closed.", "그 같은 눈이 문간까지 그녀를 따라갔다. 그리고 그 눈빛 속에, 그리고 그 평온한 얼굴 위에, 문이 닫혔을 때 미소가 어려 있었다."]], [["They never turned away.", "그 눈은 결코 돌려지지 않았다."], ["She laid her hand upon her breast, murmuring the sacred name that had been read to her; and life passed from her face, like light removed.", "그녀는 한 손을 가슴에 얹고, 자기에게 읽어 준 그 거룩한 이름을 중얼거렸다. 그리고 생명이 그녀의 얼굴에서, 거두어진 빛처럼 떠나갔다."]], [["Nothing lay there, any longer, but the ruin of the mortal house on which the rain had beaten, and the black hair that had fluttered in the wintry wind.", "이제 거기에 남은 것이라곤, 비가 두드려 댄 그 죽을 운명의 집의 잔해와, 겨울바람에 나부끼던 그 검은 머리칼뿐이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LIX. Retribution", "# CHAPTER LIX. Retribution"]], [["Changes have come again upon the great house in the long dull street, once the scene of Florence’s childhood and loneliness.", "길고 음울한 거리의 그 큰 저택에 다시금 변화가 찾아왔다. 한때 플로렌스의 어린 시절과 외로움이 깃든 무대였던 그곳."], ["It is a great house still, proof against wind and weather, without breaches in the roof, or shattered windows, or dilapidated walls; but it is a ruin none the less, and the rats fly from it.", "그것은 여전히 큰 저택이었고, 바람과 비를 막아 내었으며, 지붕에 구멍이 뚫리지도, 창문이 깨지지도, 벽이 허물어지지도 않았다. 그러나 그것은 그럼에도 폐허였고, 쥐들이 그곳에서 달아났다."]], [["Mr Towlinson and company are, at first, incredulous in respect of the shapeless rumours that they hear.", "타울린슨 씨와 그 일행은 처음에는 자기들이 듣는 그 형체 없는 소문을 도무지 믿으려 하지 않았다."], ["Cook says our people’s credit ain’t so easy shook as that comes to, thank God; and Mr Towlinson expects to hear it reported next, that the Bank of England’s a-going to break, or the jewels in the Tower to be sold up.", "요리사는 우리 댁의 신용이 그깟 일로 그리 쉽게 흔들릴 만한 게 아니지, 감사하게도, 라고 말했고, 타울린슨 씨는 다음번에는 잉글랜드 은행이 무너진다든가 런던탑의 보석이 경매에 넘어간다든가 하는 소리를 듣게 될 것 같다고 했다."], ["But, next come the Gazette, and Mr Perch; and Mr Perch brings Mrs Perch to talk it over in the kitchen, and to spend a pleasant evening.", "그러나 다음에 관보가 나왔고, 퍼치 씨가 왔다. 퍼치 씨는 부엌에서 그 일을 함께 이야기하고 즐거운 저녁을 보내려고 퍼치 부인을 데려왔다."]], [["As soon as there is no doubt about it, Mr Towlinson’s main anxiety is that the failure should be a good round one—not less than a hundred thousand pound.", "그 일에 더 이상 의심의 여지가 없게 되자, 타울린슨 씨의 가장 큰 관심사는 그 파산이 그럴듯하게 큰 규모이기를—적어도 십만 파운드는 되기를—바라는 것이었다. 퍼치 씨는 십만 파운드로도 그것을 거의 다 메우지 못하리라 생각했다."], ["Mr Perch don’t think himself that a hundred thousand pound will nearly cover it. The women, led by Mrs Perch and Cook, often repeat “a hun-dred thou-sand pound!”", "퍼치 부인과 요리사를 필두로 한 여인들은 \"시-십만 파-운드!\"를 끔찍한 만족감과 함께 자주 되뇌었다—마치 그 말을 다루는 것이 그 돈을 다루는 것과 같기라도 한 듯이."], ["with awful satisfaction—as if handling the words were like handling the money; and the housemaid, who has her eye on Mr Towlinson, wishes she had only a hundredth part of the sum to bestow on the man of her choice.", "그리고 타울린슨 씨에게 눈독을 들이고 있는 하녀는 그 금액의 백분의 일이라도 자기가 골라잡은 사내에게 줄 수 있었으면 하고 바랐다."], ["Mr Towlinson, still mindful of his old wrong, opines that a foreigner would hardly know what to do with so much money, unless he spent it on his whiskers; which bitter sarcasm causes the housemaid to withdraw in tears.", "타울린슨 씨는 여전히 자기가 당한 옛 원한을 잊지 못하고, 외국인 같으면 그렇게 많은 돈을 가지고 도대체 무얼 할 줄 알겠느냐, 구레나룻에다 쏟아붓는 거나 빼면 말이지, 라고 한마디 했는데, 그 신랄한 비꼼에 하녀는 눈물을 흘리며 자리를 떴다."]], [["But not to remain long absent; for Cook, who has the reputation of being extremely good-hearted, says, whatever they do, let ’em stand by one another now, Towlinson, for there’s no telling how soon they may be divided.", "그러나 오래 자리를 비우지는 않았다. 마음씨가 무척 좋다는 평판을 듣는 요리사가, 무슨 일을 하든 이제는 서로 곁을 지켜야지, 타울린슨 씨, 우리가 얼마나 빨리 뿔뿔이 흩어질지 모르는 일이니까, 라고 말했기 때문이다."], ["They have been in that house (says Cook) through a funeral, a wedding, and a running-away; and let it not be said that they couldn’t agree among themselves at such a time as the present.", "우리는 이 집에서 (요리사가 말했다) 장례식 하나, 결혼식 하나, 그리고 야반도주 하나를 함께 겪었지, 그러니 지금 같은 이런 때에 자기들끼리 화목하지 못했다는 소리는 듣지 말자고."], ["Mrs Perch is immensely affected by this moving address, and openly remarks that Cook is an angel.", "퍼치 부인은 이 가슴 뭉클한 연설에 크게 감동하여, 요리사야말로 천사라고 대놓고 말했다."], ["Mr Towlinson replies to Cook, far be it from him to stand in the way of that good feeling which he could wish to see; and adjourning in quest of the housemaid, and presently returning with that young lady on his arm, informs the kitchen that foreigners is only his fun, and that him and Anne have now resolved to take one another for better for worse, and to settle in Oxford Market in the general greengrocery and herb and leech line, where your kind favours is particular requested.", "타울린슨 씨는 요리사에게, 자기가 보고 싶어 하는 그 좋은 정을 가로막는 일은 결코 없을 거라고 대답하고는, 하녀를 찾으러 잠시 자리를 떠났다가 이내 그 젊은 아가씨를 팔짱 끼고 돌아와서는, 부엌을 향해 외국인 운운한 것은 그저 농담이었으며, 자기와 앤은 이제 좋을 때나 나쁠 때나 서로를 맞이하기로 결심했고, 옥스퍼드 마켓에 자리를 잡아 청과물과 약초와 거머리를 두루 다루는 장사를 할 작정이니, 여러분의 후한 애고를 각별히 부탁드린다고 알렸다."], ["This announcement is received with acclamation; and Mrs Perch, projecting her soul into futurity, says, “girls,”", "이 발표는 환호로 맞아졌고, 퍼치 부인은 영혼을 미래로 던져 넣은 채, \"딸들 말이야,\""], ["in Cook’s ear, in a solemn whisper.", "하고 요리사의 귀에 엄숙한 속삭임으로 말했다."]], [["Misfortune in the family without feasting, in these lower regions, couldn’t be.", "이 아래쪽 영역에서는, 잔치 없이 집안의 불행이 지나갈 수 없었다."], ["Therefore Cook tosses up a hot dish or two for supper, and Mr Towlinson compounds a lobster salad to be devoted to the same hospitable purpose.", "그래서 요리사는 저녁거리로 따뜻한 요리 한두 가지를 후딱 만들어 냈고, 타울린슨 씨는 같은 환대의 목적에 바칠 바닷가재 샐러드를 버무렸다."], ["Even Mrs Pipchin, agitated by the occasion, rings her bell, and sends down word that she requests to have that little bit of sweetbread that was left, warmed up for her supper, and sent to her on a tray with about a quarter of a tumbler-full of mulled sherry; for she feels poorly.", "핍친 부인까지도 이 사태에 마음이 동하여 종을 울리고는, 남은 그 췌장 고기 조금을 데워서 데운 셰리주 한 잔의 사분의 일가량과 함께 쟁반에 담아 저녁으로 가져다 달라고, 자기가 몸이 좀 좋지 않으니 그리해 달라고 아래로 전갈을 보냈다."]], [["There is a little talk about Mr Dombey, but very little.", "돔비 씨에 대한 이야기도 조금 오갔지만, 아주 조금이었다."], ["It is chiefly speculation as to how long he has known that this was going to happen.", "주로 그가 이 일이 벌어지리란 것을 얼마나 오래전부터 알고 있었느냐 하는 추측이었다."], ["Cook says shrewdly, “Oh a long time, bless you!", "요리사가 빈틈없이 말했다, \"오, 오래됐고말고, 정말이지!"], ["Take your oath of that.”", "그건 내가 장담해.\""], ["And reference being made to Mr Perch, he confirms her view of the case.", "그리고 퍼치 씨에게 물어보니, 그도 요리사의 견해에 동의했다."], ["Somebody wonders what he’ll do, and whether he’ll go out in any situation.", "누군가는 그가 앞으로 어찌할지, 어디 자리라도 얻어 나갈지 궁금해했다."], ["Mr Towlinson thinks not, and hints at a refuge in one of them genteel almshouses of the better kind.", "타울린슨 씨는 그러지 않을 것 같다며, 좀 나은 축에 드는 점잖은 양로원 어딘가에 들어가 몸을 의탁하지 않겠느냐고 넌지시 말했다."], ["“Ah, where he’ll have his little garden, you know,”", "\"아, 거기서 자그마한 정원도 갖고 말이지, 아시잖아요,\""], ["says Cook plaintively, “and bring up sweet peas in the spring.”", "요리사가 애처롭게 말했다, \"봄이면 스위트피도 가꾸고.\""], ["“Exactly so,”", "\"바로 그렇지,\""], ["says Mr Towlinson, “and be one of the Brethren of something or another.”", "타울린슨 씨가 말했다, \"그리고 무슨무슨 형제회의 일원이 되는 거고.\""], ["“We are all brethren,”", "\"우린 다 형제지요,\""], ["says Mrs Perch, in a pause of her drink.", "퍼치 부인이 술잔을 들이켜다 잠시 멈추고 말했다."], ["“Except the sisters,”", "\"자매들만 빼고,\""], ["says Mr Perch.", "퍼치 씨가 말했다."], ["“How are the mighty fallen!”", "\"강하던 자들이 어쩜 이리 무너졌누!\""], ["remarks Cook.", "요리사가 한마디 했다."], ["“Pride shall have a fall, and it always was and will be so!”", "\"교만하면 넘어지는 법이지, 늘 그래 왔고 앞으로도 그럴 거야!\""], ["observes the housemaid.", "하녀가 한마디 보탰다."]], [["It is wonderful how good they feel, in making these reflections; and what a Christian unanimity they are sensible of, in bearing the common shock with resignation.", "이런 소회를 풀어놓으면서 그들이 얼마나 흐뭇해하는지, 그리고 그 공통의 충격을 체념으로 견뎌 내며 얼마나 기독교인다운 한마음을 느끼는지, 참으로 놀라운 일이었다."], ["There is only one interruption to this excellent state of mind, which is occasioned by a young kitchen-maid of inferior rank—in black stockings—who, having sat with her mouth open for a long time, unexpectedly discharges from it words to this effect, “Suppose the wages shouldn’t be paid!”", "이 더없이 훌륭한 심경 상태에 단 한 번의 훼방이 끼어들었는데, 그것은 신분이 낮은—검은 양말을 신은—어린 부엌 하녀가 일으킨 것이었다. 그 아이는 오랫동안 입을 벌린 채 앉아 있다가 별안간 그 입에서 이런 뜻의 말을 내뱉었다, \"혹시 품삯을 못 받게 되면 어쩌죠!\""], ["The company sit for a moment speechless; but Cook recovering first, turns upon the young woman, and requests to know how she dares insult the family, whose bread she eats, by such a dishonest supposition, and whether she thinks that anybody, with a scrap of honour left, could deprive poor servants of their pittance?", "일동은 잠시 말문이 막힌 채 앉아 있었다. 그러나 요리사가 가장 먼저 정신을 차리고 그 젊은 여자에게 돌아서서는, 네가 그 밥을 얻어먹는 집안을 어찌 감히 그런 부정직한 억측으로 모욕하느냐고, 그리고 명예의 한 조각이라도 남은 사람이라면 누가 가엾은 하인들의 쥐꼬리만 한 품삯을 떼어먹겠느냐고 따져 물었다."], ["“Because if that is your religious feelings, Mary Daws,”", "\"그게 네 신앙심에서 나온 생각이라면, 메리 도스,\""], ["says Cook warmly, “I don’t know where you mean to go to.”", "요리사가 발끈하여 말했다, \"넌 도대체 어디로 가려는 건지 모르겠구나.\""]], [["Mr Towlinson don’t know either; nor anybody; and the young kitchen-maid, appearing not to know exactly, herself, and scouted by the general voice, is covered with confusion, as with a garment.", "타울린슨 씨도 알지 못했다. 아무도 몰랐다. 그리고 그 어린 부엌 하녀는 정작 자기 자신도 정확히 모르는 듯한 데다 일동의 목소리에 매도당하여, 옷을 두르듯 부끄러움에 온통 휩싸였다."]], [["After a few days, strange people begin to call at the house, and to make appointments with one another in the dining-room, as if they lived there.", "며칠이 지나자, 낯선 사람들이 그 집에 드나들기 시작하더니, 마치 자기들이 거기 사는 양 식당에서 서로 약속을 잡았다."], ["Especially, there is a gentleman, of a Mosaic Arabian cast of countenance, with a very massive watch-guard, who whistles in the drawing-room, and, while he is waiting for the other gentleman, who always has pen and ink in his pocket, asks Mr Towlinson (by the easy name of “Old Cock,”) if he happens to know what the figure of them crimson and gold hangings might have been, when new bought.", "특히, 모자이크풍 아라비아 사람 같은 인상에 아주 묵직한 시곗줄을 두른 한 신사가 있었는데, 그는 응접실에서 휘파람을 불었고, 늘 펜과 잉크를 주머니에 넣고 다니는 다른 신사를 기다리는 동안 타울린슨 씨에게 (\"영감\"이라는 허물없는 호칭으로) 저 진홍색에 금빛 도는 휘장들이 새로 샀을 때 값이 얼마나 됐을 것 같으냐고 물었다."], ["The callers and appointments in the dining-room become more numerous every day, and every gentleman seems to have pen and ink in his pocket, and to have some occasion to use it.", "식당에 드나드는 사람과 약속은 날마다 더 많아졌고, 신사마다 주머니에 펜과 잉크를 지니고 있는 듯했으며, 또 저마다 그것을 쓸 일이 있는 듯했다."], ["At last it is said that there is going to be a Sale; and then more people arrive, with pen and ink in their pockets, commanding a detachment of men with carpet caps, who immediately begin to pull up the carpets, and knock the furniture about, and to print off thousands of impressions of their shoes upon the hall and staircase.", "마침내 경매가 있을 거라는 말이 나왔고, 그러자 펜과 잉크를 주머니에 넣은 사람들이 더 많이 도착했는데, 이들은 카펫 모자를 쓴 한 무리의 인부들을 지휘했다. 그 인부들은 곧장 카펫을 뜯어내고 가구를 마구 두들겨 옮기며, 현관과 계단에 자기네 신발 자국을 수천 개나 찍어 댔다."]], [["The council downstairs are in full conclave all this time, and, having nothing to do, perform perfect feats of eating.", "아래층의 회의단은 이 내내 한자리에 모여 있었고, 할 일이 없는지라 먹는 일로 가히 묘기를 부렸다."], ["At length, they are one day summoned in a body to Mrs Pipchin’s room, and thus addressed by the fair Peruvian:", "마침내 어느 날 그들은 한꺼번에 핍친 부인의 방으로 호출되었고, 그 어여쁜 페루 여인에게서 이런 말을 들었다."]], [["“Your master’s in difficulties,”", "\"너희 주인 어른이 곤경에 빠지셨다,\""], ["says Mrs Pipchin, tartly.", "핍친 부인이 톡 쏘듯 말했다."], ["“You know that, I suppose?”", "\"그건 다들 알고 있겠지?\""]], [["Mr Towlinson, as spokesman, admits a general knowledge of the fact.", "타울린슨 씨가 대변인으로서, 그 사실을 대체로 알고 있노라고 인정했다."]], [["“And you’re all on the look-out for yourselves, I warrant you,”", "\"그래서 너희들 죄다 제 앞가림이나 하려고 눈치만 보고 있는 게지, 내 장담하마,\""], ["says Mrs Pipchin, shaking her head at them.", "핍친 부인이 그들을 향해 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했다."]], [["A shrill voice from the rear exclaims, “No more than yourself!”", "뒤쪽에서 날카로운 목소리가 외쳤다, \"당신이나 마찬가지지!\""]], [["“That’s your opinion, Mrs Impudence, is it?”", "\"그게 네 생각이냐, 이 뻔뻔한 것아, 응?\""], ["says the ireful Pipchin, looking with a fiery eye over the intermediate heads.", "노기등등한 핍친이 사람들 머리 너머로 불같은 눈초리를 던지며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Mrs Pipchin, it is,”", "\"네, 핍친 부인, 그렇답니다,\""], ["replies Cook, advancing.", "요리사가 앞으로 나서며 응수했다."], ["“And what then, pray?”", "\"그래서 어쩌시겠다는 거죠, 네?\""]], [["“Why, then you may go as soon as you like,”", "\"흥, 그럼 네 멋대로 빨리 나가려무나,\""], ["says Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 말했다."], ["“The sooner the better; and I hope I shall never see your face again.”", "\"빠르면 빠를수록 좋지. 다시는 네 낯짝을 안 봤으면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["With this the doughty Pipchin produces a canvas bag; and tells her wages out to that day, and a month beyond it; and clutches the money tight, until a receipt for the same is duly signed, to the last upstroke; when she grudgingly lets it go.", "이 말과 함께 그 억센 핍친은 캔버스 천 자루를 꺼내더니, 그날까지의 품삯에 한 달 치를 더하여 헤아려 주었다. 그러고는 그 돈을 받았다는 영수증이 마지막 한 획까지 정식으로 서명될 때까지 돈을 꽉 움켜쥐고 있다가, 그제야 마지못해 놓아주었다."], ["This form of proceeding Mrs Pipchin repeats with every member of the household, until all are paid.", "이런 식의 절차를 핍친 부인은 집안 식구 한 사람 한 사람에게 되풀이하여, 마침내 모두에게 품삯을 치렀다."]], [["“Now those that choose, can go about their business,”", "\"이제 가고 싶은 자는 제 갈 길을 가도 좋고,\""], ["says Mrs Pipchin, “and those that choose can stay here on board wages for a week or so, and make themselves useful.", "핍친 부인이 말했다, \"남고 싶은 자는 일주일쯤 식대 받고 여기 남아 쓸모 있게 굴어도 좋다."], ["Except,”", "다만,\""], ["says the inflammable Pipchin, “that slut of a cook, who’ll go immediately.”", "발끈하기 잘하는 핍친이 말했다, \"저 칠칠치 못한 요리사만은 당장 나가야 해.\""]], [["“That,”", "\"그러고말고요,\""], ["says Cook, “she certainly will!", "요리사가 말했다, \"꼭 그러겠어요!"], ["I wish you good day, Mrs Pipchin, and sincerely wish I could compliment you on the sweetness of your appearance!”", "안녕히 계세요, 핍친 부인, 그리고 진심으로 부인의 그 어여쁜 인상에 대해 칭찬을 드릴 수 있었으면 좋겠네요!\""]], [["“Get along with you,”", "\"썩 꺼지지 못해,\""], ["says Mrs Pipchin, stamping her foot.", "핍친 부인이 발을 구르며 말했다."]], [["Cook sails off with an air of beneficent dignity, highly exasperating to Mrs Pipchin, and is shortly joined below stairs by the rest of the confederation.", "요리사는 인자한 위엄을 풍기며 미끄러지듯 나갔는데, 그것이 핍친 부인을 몹시 약 올렸다. 그리고 이내 아래층에서 나머지 동맹자들과 합류했다."]], [["Mr Towlinson then says that, in the first place, he would beg to propose a little snack of something to eat; and over that snack would desire to offer a suggestion which he thinks will meet the position in which they find themselves.", "그러자 타울린슨 씨는, 우선 먹을 것을 조금 간단히 들 것을 제안하고 싶으며, 그 간식을 들면서 지금 자기들이 처한 형편에 들어맞을 만한 제안을 하나 내놓고 싶다고 말했다."], ["The refreshment being produced, and very heartily partaken of, Mr Towlinson’s suggestion is, in effect, that Cook is going, and that if we are not true to ourselves, nobody will be true to us.", "다과가 마련되고 다들 아주 흔쾌히 나눠 먹은 뒤, 타울린슨 씨의 제안인즉슨, 요컨대 요리사가 떠나는 마당에, 우리가 우리 자신에게 진실하지 않으면 누구도 우리에게 진실하지 않을 거라는 것이었다."], ["That they have lived in that house a long time, and exerted themselves very much to be sociable together.", "우리는 이 집에서 오래 살았고, 서로 사이좋게 지내려고 무던히도 애를 썼다는 것이었다."], ["(At this, Cook says, with emotion, “Hear, hear!”", "(이 대목에서 요리사가 감격하여 \"옳소, 옳소!\""], ["and Mrs Perch, who is there again, and full to the throat, sheds tears.) And that he thinks, at the present time, the feeling ought to be “Go one, go all!”", "하고 외쳤고, 또다시 와 있던 데다 목까지 차오른 퍼치 부인은 눈물을 흘렸다.) 그리고 지금 같은 때에는 마땅히 \"하나가 가면 모두 간다!\"는 마음가짐이어야 한다고 그는 생각한다는 것이었다."], ["The housemaid is much affected by this generous sentiment, and warmly seconds it.", "하녀는 이 너그러운 정서에 크게 감동하여 따뜻하게 찬동했다."], ["Cook says she feels it’s right, and only hopes it’s not done as a compliment to her, but from a sense of duty.", "요리사는 그것이 옳다고 느낀다며, 다만 그것이 자기에게 바치는 인사치레가 아니라 의무감에서 나온 것이기를 바랄 뿐이라고 말했다."], ["Mr Towlinson replies, from a sense of duty; and that now he is driven to express his opinions, he will openly say, that he does not think it over-respectable to remain in a house where Sales and such-like are carrying forwards.", "타울린슨 씨는 의무감에서 나온 것이라고 대답하고, 또 이제 자기 의견을 밝히지 않을 수 없게 된 마당이니 터놓고 말하건대, 경매니 뭐니 하는 일이 진행되고 있는 집에 남아 있는 것이 그리 점잖은 일이라고는 생각지 않는다고 했다."], ["The housemaid is sure of it; and relates, in confirmation, that a strange man, in a carpet cap, offered, this very morning, to kiss her on the stairs.", "하녀도 그건 확실하다며, 그 증거로, 바로 오늘 아침 카펫 모자를 쓴 낯선 사내가 계단에서 자기에게 입을 맞추려 들었다고 이야기했다."], ["Hereupon, Mr Towlinson is starting from his chair, to seek and “smash”", "이 말에 타울린슨 씨는 그 무례한 자를 찾아내어 \"박살내려고\""], ["the offender; when he is laid hold on by the ladies, who beseech him to calm himself, and to reflect that it is easier and wiser to leave the scene of such indecencies at once.", "의자에서 벌떡 일어서려 했으나, 여인들에게 붙들리고 말았다. 여인들은 그에게 진정하라고, 그런 추잡한 일이 벌어지는 곳은 당장 떠나는 것이 더 쉽고 현명하다는 점을 헤아려 보라고 간곡히 청했다."], ["Mrs Perch, presenting the case in a new light, even shows that delicacy towards Mr Dombey, shut up in his own rooms, imperatively demands precipitate retreat.", "퍼치 부인은 그 일을 새로운 시각에서 내놓으며, 자기 방에 틀어박혀 있는 돔비 씨에 대한 배려를 보아서라도 황급히 물러나는 것이 마땅하다고까지 했다. \"그도 그럴 것이,\""], ["“For what,” says the good woman, “must his feelings be, if he was to come upon any of the poor servants that he once deceived into thinking him immensely rich!”", "그 착한 여인이 말했다, \"한때 자기가 어마어마한 부자인 줄 알게끔 속였던 그 가엾은 하인들 가운데 누구라도 마주친다면, 그 어른 심정이 어떠하겠어요!\""], ["Cook is so struck by this moral consideration, that Mrs Perch improves it with several pious axioms, original and selected.", "요리사는 이 도덕적 고려에 어찌나 크게 감명을 받았던지, 퍼치 부인은 거기에 더해 직접 지어낸 것이며 어디서 가려 뽑은 것이며 경건한 격언을 여럿 보탰다."], ["It becomes a clear case that they must all go.", "이리하여 모두가 떠나야 한다는 것이 분명한 일이 되었다."], ["Boxes are packed, cabs fetched, and at dusk that evening there is not one member of the party left.", "짐 상자가 꾸려지고, 삯마차가 불려 왔으며, 그날 저녁 땅거미가 질 무렵에는 그 무리 가운데 단 한 사람도 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["The house stands, large and weather-proof, in the long dull street; but it is a ruin, and the rats fly from it.", "그 집은 길고 음울한 거리에, 크고 풍우에 끄떡없이 서 있었다. 그러나 그것은 폐허였고, 쥐들이 그곳에서 달아났다."]], [["The men in the carpet caps go on tumbling the furniture about; and the gentlemen with the pens and ink make out inventories of it, and sit upon pieces of furniture never made to be sat upon, and eat bread and cheese from the public-house on other pieces of furniture never made to be eaten on, and seem to have a delight in appropriating precious articles to strange uses.", "카펫 모자를 쓴 인부들은 가구를 계속 함부로 굴려 댔고, 펜과 잉크를 지닌 신사들은 그 목록을 작성하며, 결코 앉으라고 만들어지지 않은 가구 위에 걸터앉고, 결코 그 위에서 먹으라고 만들어지지 않은 또 다른 가구 위에서 술집에서 가져온 빵과 치즈를 먹었으니, 귀한 물건들을 엉뚱한 용도에 갖다 쓰는 데서 즐거움을 느끼는 듯했다."], ["Chaotic combinations of furniture also take place.", "가구의 무질서한 뒤섞임 또한 벌어졌다."], ["Mattresses and bedding appear in the dining-room; the glass and china get into the conservatory; the great dinner service is set out in heaps on the long divan in the large drawing-room; and the stair-wires, made into fasces, decorate the marble chimneypieces.", "매트리스와 침구가 식당에 나타났고, 유리그릇과 도자기가 온실로 들어갔으며, 거창한 만찬 식기 일습이 큰 응접실의 긴 소파 위에 수북이 늘어놓였고, 다발로 묶인 계단 난간 줄이 대리석 벽난로 위를 장식했다."], ["Finally, a rug, with a printed bill upon it, is hung out from the balcony; and a similar appendage graces either side of the hall door.", "마침내 인쇄된 광고지를 붙인 양탄자 한 장이 발코니에 내걸렸고, 그와 비슷한 부착물이 현관문 양옆을 꾸몄다."]], [["Then, all day long, there is a retinue of mouldy gigs and chaise-carts in the street; and herds of shabby vampires, Jew and Christian, over-run the house, sounding the plate-glass mirrors with their knuckles, striking discordant octaves on the Grand Piano, drawing wet forefingers over the pictures, breathing on the blades of the best dinner-knives, punching the squabs of chairs and sofas with their dirty fists, touzling the feather beds, opening and shutting all the drawers, balancing the silver spoons and forks, looking into the very threads of the drapery and linen, and disparaging everything.", "그러더니 온종일, 거리에는 곰팡내 나는 이륜마차와 사륜 짐마차의 행렬이 늘어섰다. 그리고 유대인이며 기독교인이며 할 것 없이 남루한 흡혈귀 떼가 집 안을 휘젓고 다니며, 손가락 마디로 판유리 거울을 두드려 소리를 내보고, 그랜드 피아노로 불협화의 옥타브를 쾅쾅 쳐 보고, 젖은 집게손가락으로 그림 위를 쓱쓱 문지르고, 제일 좋은 식칼 날에 입김을 호호 불어 보고, 더러운 주먹으로 의자와 소파의 쿠션을 쿡쿡 찔러 보고, 깃털 침대를 마구 헝클어 놓고, 모든 서랍을 열었다 닫았다 하고, 은수저와 포크를 손으로 가늠해 보고, 휘장과 아마포의 올 하나하나까지 들여다보며 무엇이든 깎아내렸다."], ["There is not a secret place in the whole house.", "온 집 안에 은밀한 구석이라곤 없었다."], ["Fluffy and snuffy strangers stare into the kitchen-range as curiously as into the attic clothes-press.", "보풀투성이에 코담배 냄새 풍기는 낯선 자들이 다락방의 옷장을 들여다보듯 호기심 어린 눈으로 부엌의 화덕 속을 빤히 들여다보았다."], ["Stout men with napless hats on, look out of the bedroom windows, and cut jokes with friends in the street.", "보풀 빠진 모자를 쓴 뚱뚱한 사내들이 침실 창밖을 내다보며 거리의 친구들과 농지거리를 주고받았다."], ["Quiet, calculating spirits withdraw into the dressing-rooms with catalogues, and make marginal notes thereon, with stumps of pencils.", "조용히 계산속이 밝은 자들은 목록을 들고 화장실로 물러나, 몽당연필로 그 여백에 메모를 적었다."], ["Two brokers invade the very fire-escape, and take a panoramic survey of the neighbourhood from the top of the house.", "중개인 둘은 비상용 사다리까지 침범하여 집 꼭대기에서 동네를 한 바퀴 빙 둘러보았다."], ["The swarm and buzz, and going up and down, endure for days.", "그 우글거림과 웅성거림, 그리고 오르락내리락은 며칠이고 계속되었다."], ["The Capital Modern Household Furniture, &c., is on view.", "일급 현대식 가정용 가구 외 일습이 전시 중이었다."]], [["Then there is a palisade of tables made in the best drawing-room; and on the capital, french-polished, extending, telescopic range of Spanish mahogany dining-tables with turned legs, the pulpit of the Auctioneer is erected; and the herds of shabby vampires, Jew and Christian, the strangers fluffy and snuffy, and the stout men with the napless hats, congregate about it and sit upon everything within reach, mantel-pieces included, and begin to bid.", "그러더니 가장 좋은 응접실에는 탁자로 만든 울타리가 세워졌다. 그리고 돌려 깎은 다리가 달린, 일급의, 프랑스식으로 광을 낸, 펼침식의, 망원경처럼 늘어나는 스페인산 마호가니 만찬 탁자 위에 경매인의 강단이 세워졌다."], ["Hot, humming, and dusty are the rooms all day; and—high above the heat, hum, and dust—the head and shoulders, voice and hammer, of the Auctioneer, are ever at work.", "그리고 유대인이며 기독교인이며 할 것 없는 남루한 흡혈귀 떼와, 보풀투성이에 코담배 냄새 풍기는 낯선 자들과, 보풀 빠진 모자를 쓴 뚱뚱한 사내들이 그 둘레에 모여들어, 벽난로 선반까지 포함하여 손 닿는 것마다 죄다 걸터앉고는 값을 부르기 시작했다. 방들은 온종일 후끈하고, 웅웅거리고, 먼지투성이였다."], ["The men in the carpet caps get flustered and vicious with tumbling the Lots about, and still the Lots are going, going, gone; still coming on.", "그리고—그 열기와 웅성거림과 먼지를 훌쩍 넘어, 그 위로—경매인의 머리와 어깨, 목소리와 망치가 줄곧 분주히 움직였다. 카펫 모자를 쓴 인부들은 경매 물품을 이리저리 굴리느라 허둥대고 사나워졌으나, 물품은 여전히 나갑니다, 나갑니다, 낙찰!"], ["Sometimes there is joking and a general roar.", "여전히 새것이 올라왔다. 이따금 농담이 오가고 일제히 웃음보가 터졌다."], ["This lasts all day and three days following.", "이것이 그날 온종일, 그리고 뒤이은 사흘 동안 계속되었다."], ["The Capital Modern Household Furniture, &c., is on sale.", "일급 현대식 가정용 가구 외 일습이 판매 중이었다."]], [["Then the mouldy gigs and chaise-carts reappear; and with them come spring-vans and waggons, and an army of porters with knots.", "그러더니 곰팡내 나는 이륜마차와 사륜 짐마차가 다시 나타났고, 그와 더불어 용수철 짐마차와 짐수레, 그리고 짐을 묶는 밧줄을 든 짐꾼 부대가 왔다."], ["All day long, the men with carpet caps are screwing at screw-drivers and bed-winches, or staggering by the dozen together on the staircase under heavy burdens, or upheaving perfect rocks of Spanish mahogany, best rose-wood, or plate-glass, into the gigs and chaise-carts, vans and waggons.", "온종일, 카펫 모자를 쓴 인부들은 나사돌리개와 침대 죔쇠를 비틀어 대거나, 무거운 짐을 진 채 열댓 명씩 한 덩어리로 계단에서 비틀거리거나, 스페인산 마호가니며 최고급 자단이며 판유리로 된 바위 덩어리 같은 것들을 들어 올려 이륜마차와 사륜 짐마차, 용수철 짐마차와 짐수레에 실었다."], ["All sorts of vehicles of burden are in attendance, from a tilted waggon to a wheelbarrow.", "포장을 씌운 짐마차에서 외바퀴 손수레에 이르기까지, 온갖 종류의 짐 나르는 수레가 대기하고 있었다."], ["Poor Paul’s little bedstead is carried off in a donkey-tandem.", "가엾은 폴의 자그마한 침대는 당나귀 두 마리가 끄는 수레에 실려 나갔다."], ["For nearly a whole week, the Capital Modern Household Furniture, & c., is in course of removal.", "거의 일주일 내내, 일급 현대식 가정용 가구 외 일습이 운반되어 나가는 중이었다."]], [["At last it is all gone.", "마침내 그것이 죄다 사라졌다."], ["Nothing is left about the house but scattered leaves of catalogues, littered scraps of straw and hay, and a battery of pewter pots behind the hall-door.", "집 안에 남은 것이라곤 목록에서 흩어져 나온 낱장들과, 어수선하게 널린 짚과 건초 부스러기와, 현관문 뒤에 줄지어 선 백랍 단지들뿐이었다."], ["The men with the carpet-caps gather up their screw-drivers and bed-winches into bags, shoulder them, and walk off.", "카펫 모자를 쓴 인부들은 나사돌리개와 침대 죔쇠를 자루에 거두어 담고, 그것을 어깨에 메고 떠나갔다."], ["One of the pen-and-ink gentlemen goes over the house as a last attention; sticking up bills in the windows respecting the lease of this desirable family mansion, and shutting the shutters.", "펜과 잉크를 지닌 신사들 가운데 하나가 마지막 마무리로 집을 한 바퀴 돌며, 이 탐나는 가족용 저택의 임대에 관한 광고지를 창문마다 붙이고 덧창을 닫았다."], ["At length he follows the men with the carpet caps.", "마침내 그도 카펫 모자를 쓴 인부들의 뒤를 따라갔다."], ["None of the invaders remain.", "침입자는 한 사람도 남지 않았다."], ["The house is a ruin, and the rats fly from it.", "그 집은 폐허였고, 쥐들이 그곳에서 달아났다."]], [["Mrs Pipchin’s apartments, together with those locked rooms on the ground-floor where the window-blinds are drawn down close, have been spared the general devastation.", "핍친 부인의 거처는, 창문 블라인드가 빈틈없이 내려진 일층의 그 잠긴 방들과 함께, 이 전반적인 황폐화를 면했다."], ["Mrs Pipchin has remained austere and stony during the proceedings, in her own room; or has occasionally looked in at the sale to see what the goods are fetching, and to bid for one particular easy chair.", "핍친 부인은 그 절차가 진행되는 내내 자기 방에서 근엄하고 돌처럼 굳은 채로 있었다. 그러지 않으면 이따금 경매장을 들여다보며 물건들이 얼마에 팔리는지 살피고, 특정한 안락의자 하나에 값을 불렀다."], ["Mrs Pipchin has been the highest bidder for the easy chair, and sits upon her property when Mrs Chick comes to see her.", "핍친 부인은 그 안락의자의 최고가 응찰자가 되었고, 칙 부인이 찾아왔을 때 자기 소유물 위에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“How is my brother, Mrs Pipchin?”", "\"제 오라비는 좀 어떤가요, 핍친 부인?\""], ["says Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I don’t know any more than the deuce,”", "\"나야 도무지 알 길이 없지요,\""], ["says Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 말했다."], ["“He never does me the honour to speak to me.", "\"그분은 내게 말 한마디 건네는 영광을 베푸시는 법이 없으니."], ["He has his meat and drink put in the next room to his own; and what he takes, he comes out and takes when there’s nobody there.", "자기 방 바로 옆방에 음식과 술을 들여놓게 하고는, 아무도 없을 때 나와서 자기가 들 것을 들지요."], ["It’s no use asking me.", "내게 물어봐야 소용없어요."], ["I know no more about him than the man in the south who burnt his mouth by eating cold plum porridge.”", "식은 자두죽을 먹다 입을 덴 남쪽 나라 그 사내만큼도 그분에 대해 아는 게 없으니까요.\""]], [["This the acrimonious Pipchin says with a flounce.", "이것을 신랄한 핍친은 몸을 홱 비틀며 말했다."]], [["“But good gracious me!”", "\"하지만 어쩜 좋아!\""], ["cries Mrs Chick blandly.", "칙 부인이 부드럽게 외쳤다."], ["“How long is this to last!", "\"이게 얼마나 더 가야 한단 말이에요!"], ["If my brother will not make an effort, Mrs Pipchin, what is to become of him?", "제 오라비가 노력을 하지 않으면, 핍친 부인, 그분은 대체 어찌 되는 거죠?"], ["I am sure I should have thought he had seen enough of the consequences of not making an effort, by this time, to be warned against that fatal error.”", "이쯤 됐으면 노력하지 않은 결과가 어떤지 충분히 보았을 테니, 그 치명적인 잘못만은 경계할 줄 알았을 텐데요, 정말이지.\""]], [["“Hoity toity!”", "\"이런, 호들갑 좀 봐!\""], ["says Mrs Pipchin, rubbing her nose.", "핍친 부인이 코를 문지르며 말했다."], ["“There’s a great fuss, I think, about it.", "\"이걸 두고 야단법석이 어지간하군요, 내가 보기엔."], ["It ain’t so wonderful a case.", "그렇게 대단한 일도 아니구먼."], ["People have had misfortunes before now, and been obliged to part with their furniture.", "사람이 불행을 겪고 가구를 처분해야 하는 일이야 예전에도 있었지요."], ["I’m sure I have!”", "나야말로 분명 그랬고요!\""]], [["“My brother,”", "\"제 오라비는,\""], ["pursues Mrs Chick profoundly, “is so peculiar—so strange a man.", "칙 부인이 심오하게 말을 이었다, \"어찌나 별난지—어찌나 묘한 사람인지 몰라요."], ["He is the most peculiar man I ever saw.", "내가 본 중에 가장 별난 사람이에요."], ["Would anyone believe that when he received news of the marriage and emigration of that unnatural child—it’s a comfort to me, now, to remember that I always said there was something extraordinary about that child: but nobody minds me—would anybody believe, I say, that he should then turn round upon me and say he had supposed, from my manner, that she had come to my house?", "누가 믿겠어요, 그 자식답잖은 아이가 결혼해서 외국으로 떠났다는 소식을 받았을 때—이제 와선 내가 그 아이에겐 어딘가 심상찮은 데가 있다고 늘 말했던 걸 떠올리면 위안이 되긴 하지만요. 하긴 아무도 내 말엔 신경도 안 쓰지만—누가 믿겠냐고요, 글쎄, 그분이 그때 내게 홱 돌아서서는, 내 태도로 미루어 그 애가 우리 집에 와 있는 줄로 짐작했었다고 말하다니요?"], ["Why, my gracious!", "어머나 세상에!"], ["And would anybody believe that when I merely say to him, ‘Paul, I may be very foolish, and I have no doubt I am, but I cannot understand how your affairs can have got into this state,’ he should actually fly at me, and request that I will come to see him no more until he asks me!", "또 누가 믿겠어요, 내가 그분에게 그저, '폴, 내가 무척 어리석은지도 모르지, 분명 그렇겠지만, 도무지 네 사정이 어쩌다 이 지경이 됐는지 모르겠구나,' 하고 말했더니, 그분이 정말로 내게 버럭 화를 내며, 자기가 부르기 전에는 다시는 보러 오지 말라고 하다니요!"], ["Why, my goodness!”", "어머, 세상에나!\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["says Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 말했다."], ["“It’s a pity he hadn’t a little more to do with mines.", "\"그분이 광산 일하고 좀 더 인연이 있었더라면 좋았을 것을."], ["They’d have tried his temper for him.”", "그랬으면 그 성미를 한번 단단히 시험받았을 텐데.\""]], [["“And what,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["resumes Mrs Chick, quite regardless of Mrs Pipchin’s observations, “is it to end in?", "칙 부인이 핍친 부인의 말은 전혀 아랑곳하지 않고 다시 말을 이었다, \"이게 어떻게 끝나려는 걸까요?"], ["That’s what I want to know.", "그게 내가 알고 싶은 거예요."], ["What does my brother mean to do?", "제 오라비는 대체 어쩔 작정인 거죠?"], ["He must do something.", "뭐라도 해야 할 텐데."], ["It’s of no use remaining shut up in his own rooms.", "제 방에 틀어박혀 있어 봐야 아무 소용없잖아요."], ["Business won’t come to him. No.", "일거리가 제 발로 그분을 찾아오진 않을 테니까요."], ["He must go to it.", "안 되죠."], ["Then why don’t he go?", "그분이 일거리를 찾아가야 해요."], ["He knows where to go, I suppose, having been a man of business all his life.", "그런데 왜 안 가는 거죠? 평생 사업가로 살았으니 어디로 가야 할지는 알 거 아니에요."], ["Very good.", "좋아요."], ["Then why not go there?”", "그럼 왜 거기로 안 가는 거냐고요?\""]], [["Mrs Chick, after forging this powerful chain of reasoning, remains silent for a minute to admire it.", "칙 부인은 이 강력한 추론의 사슬을 벼려 낸 뒤, 그것을 감탄하느라 잠시 입을 다물고 있었다."]], [["“Besides,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["says the discreet lady, with an argumentative air, “who ever heard of such obstinacy as his staying shut up here through all these dreadful disagreeables?", "그 신중한 부인이 따지는 듯한 태도로 말했다, \"그분처럼 이 끔찍하게 거북한 일들이 다 지나가는 내내 여기 틀어박혀 있는 고집이 세상에 어디 있겠어요?"], ["It’s not as if there was no place for him to go to.", "갈 데가 없어서 그러는 것도 아닌데 말이죠."], ["Of course he could have come to our house.", "당연히 우리 집으로 올 수 있었잖아요."], ["He knows he is at home there, I suppose?", "거기선 제집처럼 편히 지낼 수 있다는 걸 그분도 알 텐데요?"], ["Mr Chick has perfectly bored about it, and I said with my own lips, ‘Why surely, Paul, you don’t imagine that because your affairs have got into this state, you are the less at home to such near relatives as ourselves?", "칙 씨는 그 얘기를 아주 지겹도록 했고, 나도 내 입으로 직접 말했다고요, '아니 폴, 설마 네 사정이 이 지경이 됐다고 해서 우리처럼 가까운 친척에게마저 더는 마음 편히 못 온다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지? 우리가 세상 다른 사람들과 똑같다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지?'"], ["You don’t imagine that we are like the rest of the world?’ But no; here he stays all through, and here he is.", "하지만 천만에요. 여기서 내내 버티고, 지금도 여기 있잖아요."], ["Why, good gracious me, suppose the house was to be let!", "어머, 세상에, 만약 이 집이 세놓이기라도 하면!"], ["What would he do then?", "그땐 그분이 어쩌려고요?"], ["He couldn’t remain here then.", "그땐 여기 못 있죠."], ["If he attempted to do so, there would be an ejectment, an action for Doe, and all sorts of things; and then he must go.", "그래도 버티려 들면, 퇴거 명령에, 부동산 점유 회복 소송에, 별의별 일이 다 벌어질 거고, 그러면 그분은 떠나야 해요."], ["Then why not go at first instead of at last?", "그럴 거면 마지막에 떠날 게 아니라 애초에 떠나는 게 낫지 않겠어요?"], ["And that brings me back to what I said just now, and I naturally ask what is to be the end of it?”", "그러니 다시 아까 내가 한 말로 돌아가서, 나는 당연히 묻게 되는 거죠, 이게 대체 어떻게 끝나려는 거냐고요?\""]], [["“I know what’s to be the end of it, as far as I am concerned,”", "\"적어도 나에 관한 한 이게 어떻게 끝날지는 내가 알지요,\""], ["replies Mrs Pipchin, “and that’s enough for me.", "핍친 부인이 대답했다, \"그거면 나한텐 충분하고요."], ["I’m going to take myself off in a jiffy.”", "나는 단숨에 떠날 참이에요.\""]], [["“In a which, Mrs Pipchin,”", "\"단, 무엇으로요, 핍친 부인?\""], ["says Mrs Chick.", "칙 부인이 말했다."]], [["“In a jiffy,”", "\"단숨에요,\""], ["retorts Mrs Pipchin sharply.", "핍친 부인이 날카롭게 되받았다."]], [["“Ah, well!", "\"아, 그래요!"], ["really I can’t blame you, Mrs Pipchin,”", "정말이지 부인을 탓할 순 없네요, 핍친 부인,\""], ["says Mrs Chick, with frankness.", "칙 부인이 솔직하게 말했다."]], [["“It would be pretty much the same to me, if you could,”", "\"부인이 탓할 수 있다 한들 내겐 매한가지지만요,\""], ["replies the sardonic Pipchin.", "빈정대는 핍친이 대답했다."], ["“At any rate I’m going.", "\"어쨌든 나는 떠나요."], ["I can’t stop here.", "여기 더는 못 있어요."], ["I should be dead in a week.", "일주일이면 죽고 말 거예요."], ["I had to cook my own pork chop yesterday, and I’m not used to it.", "어제는 내 손으로 직접 돼지갈비를 구워야 했는데, 그런 일엔 익숙지가 않거든요."], ["My constitution will be giving way next.", "다음엔 내 몸이 탈이 나고 말 거예요."], ["Besides, I had a very fair connexion at Brighton when I came here—little Pankey’s folks alone were worth a good eighty pounds a-year to me—and I can’t afford to throw it away.", "게다가 내가 여기 올 때 브라이턴에 꽤 괜찮은 거래처를 두고 왔는데—꼬마 팽키네 식구들만 해도 나한텐 일 년에 족히 팔십 파운드는 되는 자리였다고요—그걸 내버릴 형편이 못 돼요."], ["I’ve written to my niece, and she expects me by this time.”", "조카에게 편지를 써 뒀고, 지금쯤 나를 기다리고 있을 거예요.\""]], [["“Have you spoken to my brother?”", "\"제 오라비에게는 말씀하셨나요?\""], ["inquires Mrs Chick", "칙 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes, it’s very easy to say speak to him,”", "\"오, 그래요, 그분에게 말하라니 참 쉽게도 말하시네요,\""], ["retorts Mrs Pipchin.", "핍친 부인이 되받았다."], ["“How is it done?", "\"그게 어떻게 되는데요?"], ["I called out to him yesterday, that I was no use here, and that he had better let me send for Mrs Richards.", "어제 그분에게 큰 소리로 외쳤다고요, 내가 여기 있어 봐야 쓸모가 없으니, 차라리 리처즈 부인을 불러오게 하시는 게 낫겠다고요."], ["He grunted something or other that meant yes, and I sent.", "그분은 그렇다는 뜻으로 뭔가 끙 소리를 냈고, 그래서 내가 사람을 보냈지요."], ["Grunt indeed!", "끙 소리라니, 참!"], ["If he had been Mr Pipchin, he’d have had some reason to grunt.", "그분이 핍친 씨였더라면, 끙거릴 만한 까닭이라도 좀 있었을 텐데."], ["Yah!", "흥!"], ["I’ve no patience with it!”", "더는 못 참겠어요!\""]], [["Here this exemplary female, who has pumped up so much fortitude and virtue from the depths of the Peruvian mines, rises from her cushioned property to see Mrs Chick to the door.", "이 대목에서, 페루 광산의 밑바닥에서 그토록 많은 꿋꿋함과 미덕을 길어 올린 이 모범적인 여인은, 칙 부인을 문까지 배웅하려고 쿠션 깔린 자기 소유물에서 일어났다."], ["Mrs Chick, deploring to the last the peculiar character of her brother, noiselessly retires, much occupied with her own sagacity and clearness of head.", "칙 부인은 마지막까지 자기 오라비의 별난 성격을 한탄하며, 자기의 명민함과 명석한 두뇌에 잔뜩 마음을 빼앗긴 채 소리 없이 물러갔다."]], [["In the dusk of the evening Mr Toodle, being off duty, arrives with Polly and a box, and leaves them, with a sounding kiss, in the hall of the empty house, the retired character of which affects Mr Toodle’s spirits strongly.", "저녁 땅거미가 질 무렵, 비번이 된 투들 씨가 폴리와 짐 상자 하나를 데리고 도착하여, 쪽 소리 나는 입맞춤과 함께 그 둘을 텅 빈 집의 현관에 남겨 두었는데, 그 한적한 분위기가 투들 씨의 마음을 몹시 무겁게 했다."]], [["“I tell you what, Polly, me dear,”", "\"내 말 좀 들어 봐, 폴리, 여보,\""], ["says Mr Toodle, “being now an ingine-driver, and well to do in the world, I shouldn’t allow of your coming here, to be made dull-like, if it warn’t for favours past.", "투들 씨가 말했다, \"내가 이제 기관사가 되었고 세상살이도 그럭저럭 넉넉하니, 지난날의 은혜만 아니었다면 당신이 여기 와서 이렇게 우중충해지게 두진 않았을 거야."], ["But favours past, Polly, is never to be forgot.", "하지만 지난날의 은혜란 건, 폴리, 결코 잊어서는 안 되는 거지."], ["To them which is in adversity, besides, your face is a cord’l.", "게다가 역경에 처한 이들에겐 당신 얼굴이 강심제 같은 법이야."], ["So let’s have another kiss on it, my dear.", "그러니 한 번 더 입맞춤이나 하자고, 여보."], ["You wish no better than to do a right act, I know; and my views is, that it’s right and dutiful to do this.", "당신이 옳은 일 하는 것밖에 더 바라는 게 없다는 거 내가 알아. 그리고 내 생각엔, 이건 옳고 마땅한 일을 하는 거야."], ["Good-night, Polly!”", "잘 자, 폴리!\""]], [["Mrs Pipchin by this time looms dark in her black bombazeen skirts, black bonnet, and shawl; and has her personal property packed up; and has her chair (late a favourite chair of Mr Dombey’s and the dead bargain of the sale) ready near the street door; and is only waiting for a fly-van, going tonight to Brighton on private service, which is to call for her, by private contract, and convey her home.", "이 무렵 핍친 부인은 검은 봄버진 치마와 검은 보닛과 숄을 두르고 거뭇하게 모습을 드러냈다. 그리고 자기 소지품을 꾸려 놓았고, 자기 의자를(전에 돔비 씨가 아끼던 의자였고 경매에서 거저나 다름없이 산 것이었다) 현관문 가까이에 준비해 두었으며, 오늘 밤 개인 용무로 브라이턴에 가는 길에 개인적인 약조에 따라 자기를 들러 태우고 집까지 데려다줄 사륜 합승마차만을 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Presently it comes.", "이윽고 그것이 왔다."], ["Mrs Pipchin’s wardrobe being handed in and stowed away, Mrs Pipchin’s chair is next handed in, and placed in a convenient corner among certain trusses of hay; it being the intention of the amiable woman to occupy the chair during her journey.", "핍친 부인의 옷가지가 안으로 건네져 챙겨 넣어진 다음, 핍친 부인의 의자가 그다음으로 건네져, 건초 묶음 몇 단 사이의 알맞은 구석에 놓였다. 그 상냥한 여인이 여행하는 동안 그 의자에 앉아 갈 작정이었기 때문이다."], ["Mrs Pipchin herself is next handed in, and grimly takes her seat.", "핍친 부인 본인이 그다음으로 안으로 건네져, 험상궂게 자리를 잡았다."], ["There is a snaky gleam in her hard grey eye, as of anticipated rounds of buttered toast, relays of hot chops, worryings and quellings of young children, sharp snappings at poor Berry, and all the other delights of her Ogress’s castle.", "그 딱딱한 잿빛 눈에는 뱀처럼 번득이는 빛이 어렸으니, 버터 바른 토스트의 거듭되는 차례며, 잇따라 나올 뜨거운 갈비며, 어린아이들을 들볶고 윽박지르는 일이며, 가엾은 베리에게 매섭게 쏘아붙이는 일이며, 그 밖에 그 식인귀의 성에서 누리던 온갖 즐거움을 미리 그려 보는 듯했다."], ["Mrs Pipchin almost laughs as the fly-van drives off, and she composes her black bombazeen skirts, and settles herself among the cushions of her easy chair.", "핍친 부인은 합승마차가 출발하자 거의 웃음을 터뜨릴 뻔하면서, 검은 봄버진 치마를 가지런히 매만지고 안락의자의 쿠션 사이에 몸을 편안히 묻었다."]], [["The house is such a ruin that the rats have fled, and there is not one left.", "그 집은 어찌나 완전한 폐허인지 쥐들마저 달아나, 단 한 마리도 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["But Polly, though alone in the deserted mansion—for there is no companionship in the shut-up rooms in which its late master hides his head—is not alone long.", "그러나 폴리는, 그 버려진 저택에 홀로 있었으나—그곳의 옛 주인이 머리를 숨기고 있는 그 잠긴 방들 안에 동무가 있을 리 없으니—오래 홀로 있지는 않았다. 밤이었다."], ["It is night; and she is sitting at work in the housekeeper’s room, trying to forget what a lonely house it is, and what a history belongs to it; when there is a knock at the hall door, as loud sounding as any knock can be, striking into such an empty place.", "그녀는 가정부 방에 앉아 일을 하며, 이곳이 얼마나 외로운 집인지, 또 여기에 어떤 사연이 깃들어 있는지를 잊으려 애쓰고 있었다. 그때 현관문에서 노크 소리가 났는데, 이렇게 텅 빈 곳에 울려 퍼지는 노크 소리가 그렇듯 더없이 크게 울렸다."], ["Opening it, she returns across the echoing hall, accompanied by a female figure in a close black bonnet.", "문을 열고 그녀는, 꼭 맞는 검은 보닛을 쓴 여인의 모습을 동반한 채 메아리치는 현관을 가로질러 되돌아왔다."], ["It is Miss Tox, and Miss Tox’s eyes are red.", "톡스 양이었고, 톡스 양의 두 눈은 붉었다."]], [["“Oh, Polly,”", "\"오, 폴리,\""], ["says Miss Tox, “when I looked in to have a little lesson with the children just now, I got the message that you left for me; and as soon as I could recover my spirits at all, I came on after you.", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"방금 아이들과 짧은 수업을 좀 하려고 들렀더니, 당신이 나한테 남긴 전갈을 받았지 뭐예요. 그래서 마음을 조금이나마 추스르자마자 당신을 뒤따라왔어요."], ["Is there no one here but you?”", "여기 당신 말고는 아무도 없나요?\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"네!"], ["not a soul,”", "한 사람도 없어요,\""], ["says Polly.", "폴리가 말했다."]], [["“Have you seen him?”", "\"그분은 보셨어요?\""], ["whispers Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 속삭였다."]], [["“Bless you,”", "\"어머나,\""], ["returns Polly, “no; he has not been seen this many a day.", "폴리가 대답했다, \"아뇨. 그분은 여러 날째 모습을 안 보이셨어요."], ["They tell me he never leaves his room.”", "방에서 도통 나오시질 않는다고들 하더라고요.\""]], [["“Is he said to be ill?”", "\"어디 편찮으시다던가요?\""], ["inquires Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 물었다."]], [["“No, Ma’am, not that I know of,”", "\"아뇨, 마님, 제가 알기론 그렇진 않아요,\""], ["returns Polly, “except in his mind.", "폴리가 대답했다, \"마음만 빼면요."], ["He must be very bad there, poor gentleman!”", "거기가 단단히 안 좋으실 거예요, 가엾은 어른!\""]], [["Miss Tox’s sympathy is such that she can scarcely speak.", "톡스 양의 가엾어하는 마음이 어찌나 컸던지 그녀는 거의 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["She is no chicken, but she has not grown tough with age and celibacy.", "그녀는 이제 애송이는 아니었으나, 나이와 독신 생활로 매정해지지는 않았다."], ["Her heart is very tender, her compassion very genuine, her homage very real.", "그녀의 마음은 무척 다정했고, 그 연민은 무척 진실했으며, 그 경의는 무척 진심이었다."], ["Beneath the locket with the fishy eye in it, Miss Tox bears better qualities than many a less whimsical outside; such qualities as will outlive, by many courses of the sun, the best outsides and brightest husks that fall in the harvest of the great reaper.", "물고기 눈알이 박힌 로켓 목걸이 아래에, 톡스 양은 한결 덜 별난 겉모습을 한 많은 이들보다 나은 자질을 지니고 있었으니, 그 자질이란 저 위대한 추수꾼의 수확 때에 떨어지는 가장 훌륭한 겉모습이며 가장 빛나는 껍데기보다도, 여러 해의 해가 거듭 도는 동안 더 오래 살아남을 그런 것이었다."]], [["It is long before Miss Tox goes away, and before Polly, with a candle flaring on the blank stairs, looks after her, for company, down the street, and feels unwilling to go back into the dreary house, and jar its emptiness with the heavy fastenings of the door, and glide away to bed.", "톡스 양이 자리를 뜨기까지, 그리고 폴리가 휑한 계단에 일렁이는 촛불을 들고 동무 삼아 거리 저편으로 그녀의 뒷모습을 바라보고서, 그 음산한 집으로 되돌아가 무거운 문 빗장으로 그 텅 빔을 덜컹 울리고 미끄러지듯 잠자리에 들기를 내키지 않아 하기까지는, 오랜 시간이 걸렸다."], ["But all this Polly does; and in the morning sets in one of those darkened rooms such matters as she has been advised to prepare, and then retires and enters them no more until next morning at the same hour.", "그러나 이 모든 일을 폴리는 해냈다. 그리고 아침이면 그 어두컴컴한 방 가운데 한 곳에, 미리 준비하라고 일러 들은 물건들을 차려 놓고, 그런 다음 물러나 이튿날 아침 같은 시각이 되기까지는 다시 들어가지 않았다."], ["There are bells there, but they never ring; and though she can sometimes hear a footfall going to and fro, it never comes out.", "거기엔 종이 있었으나 결코 울리지 않았고, 이따금 오락가락하는 발소리가 들리긴 했어도 그것이 밖으로 나오는 법은 없었다."]], [["Miss Tox returns early in the day.", "톡스 양은 그날 일찌감치 다시 왔다."], ["It then begins to be Miss Tox’s occupation to prepare little dainties—or what are such to her—to be carried into these rooms next morning.", "그때부터 톡스 양의 일거리는 이 방들로 이튿날 아침 들여보낼 자그마한 별미를—혹은 그녀에게 별미인 것을—마련하는 것이 되었다."], ["She derives so much satisfaction from the pursuit, that she enters on it regularly from that time; and brings daily in her little basket, various choice condiments selected from the scanty stores of the deceased owner of the powdered head and pigtail.", "그녀는 그 일에서 어찌나 큰 만족을 얻었던지, 그때부터 꼬박꼬박 그 일에 매달렸다. 그리고 분 바른 머리에 변발을 했던 고인이 남긴 빈약한 살림에서 가려 뽑은 갖가지 별미를 자기 작은 바구니에 담아 날마다 가져왔다."], ["She likewise brings, in sheets of curl-paper, morsels of cold meats, tongues of sheep, halves of fowls, for her own dinner; and sharing these collations with Polly, passes the greater part of her time in the ruined house that the rats have fled from: hiding, in a fright at every sound, stealing in and out like a criminal; only desiring to be true to the fallen object of her admiration, unknown to him, unknown to all the world but one poor simple woman.", "그녀는 또한, 곱슬머리를 마는 종이에 싸서, 자기 끼니로 먹을 차가운 고기 조각이며 양의 혀며 닭 반 마리 따위를 가져왔고, 이 음식들을 폴리와 나누며, 쥐들마저 달아난 그 폐허가 된 집에서 거의 온종일을 보냈다. 무슨 소리만 나도 화들짝 놀라 몸을 숨기고, 죄인처럼 살그머니 드나들면서, 오직 자기가 흠모하는 그 몰락한 대상에게 진실하기만을 바랐으니—그에게도 알려지지 않고, 단 한 사람의 가난하고 소박한 여인을 빼고는 온 세상에 알려지지도 않은 채로."]], [["The Major knows it; but no one is the wiser for that, though the Major is much the merrier.", "소령은 그것을 알고 있었다. 그러나 그 일로 누가 더 현명해지는 일은 없었어도, 소령만은 훨씬 더 즐거워졌다."], ["The Major, in a fit of curiosity, has charged the Native to watch the house sometimes, and find out what becomes of Dombey.", "소령은 호기심이 발동하여, 원주민에게 이따금 그 집을 지켜보며 돔비가 어찌 되어 가는지 알아내라고 일렀다."], ["The Native has reported Miss Tox’s fidelity, and the Major has nearly choked himself dead with laughter.", "원주민이 톡스 양의 한결같음을 보고하자, 소령은 웃다가 거의 숨이 막혀 죽을 뻔했다."], ["He is permanently bluer from that hour, and constantly wheezes to himself, his lobster eyes starting out of his head, “Damme, Sir, the woman’s a born idiot!”", "그는 그때부터 줄곧 더 시퍼레져서는, 게 눈 같은 두 눈을 머리에서 툭 튀어나오게 한 채, 줄곧 혼자 씩씩거리며 말했다, \"제기랄, 그 여자는 타고난 멍청이로군!\""]], [["And the ruined man.", "그러면 그 몰락한 사내는."], ["How does he pass the hours, alone?", "그는 어떻게 시간을 보냈을까, 홀로?"]], [["“Let him remember it in that room, years to come!”", "\"훗날 이 방에서 그 일을 기억하게 하라!\""], ["He did remember it.", "그는 그 일을 기억했다."], ["It was heavy on his mind now; heavier than all the rest.", "그것이 이제 그의 마음을 짓눌렀으니, 다른 그 무엇보다도 더 무겁게."]], [["“Let him remember it in that room, years to come!", "\"훗날 이 방에서 그 일을 기억하게 하라!"], ["The rain that falls upon the roof, the wind that mourns outside the door, may have foreknowledge in their melancholy sound.", "지붕 위로 떨어지는 비, 문밖에서 슬피 우는 바람은, 그 구슬픈 소리 속에 어쩌면 앞날을 미리 아는 능력을 지니고 있을지도 모른다."], ["Let him remember it in that room, years to come!”", "훗날 이 방에서 그 일을 기억하게 하라!\""]], [["He did remember it.", "그는 그 일을 기억했다."], ["In the miserable night he thought of it; in the dreary day, the wretched dawn, the ghostly, memory-haunted twilight.", "비참한 밤에 그는 그것을 생각했고, 음산한 낮에, 처참한 새벽에, 유령 같고 기억에 시달리는 황혼녘에 그것을 생각했다."], ["He did remember it.", "그는 그 일을 기억했다."], ["In agony, in sorrow, in remorse, in despair!", "고뇌 속에서, 슬픔 속에서, 회한 속에서, 절망 속에서!"], ["“Papa!", "\"아빠!"], ["Papa!", "아빠!"], ["Speak to me, dear Papa!”", "저한테 말 좀 해 주세요, 사랑하는 아빠!\""], ["He heard the words again, and saw the face.", "그는 그 말을 다시 들었고, 그 얼굴을 보았다."], ["He saw it fall upon the trembling hands, and heard the one prolonged low cry go upward.", "그는 그것이 떨리는 두 손 위로 무너지는 것을 보았고, 한 줄기 길게 늘어진 나직한 울부짖음이 위로 올라가는 것을 들었다."]], [["He was fallen, never to be raised up any more.", "그는 쓰러졌고, 다시는 일으켜 세워질 수 없었다."], ["For the night of his worldly ruin there was no to-morrow’s sun; for the stain of his domestic shame there was no purification; nothing, thank Heaven, could bring his dead child back to life.", "그의 세속적 파멸의 그 밤에는 내일의 태양이 없었고, 그의 가정적 치욕의 그 오점에는 정화가 없었다. 다행히도, 그 무엇도 죽은 그의 아이를 되살려 낼 수는 없었다."], ["But that which he might have made so different in all the Past—which might have made the Past itself so different, though this he hardly thought of now—that which was his own work, that which he could so easily have wrought into a blessing, and had set himself so steadily for years to form into a curse: that was the sharp grief of his soul.", "그러나 지난 모든 시절에서 그토록 다르게 만들 수 있었던 것—지난날 그 자체를 그토록 다르게 만들 수 있었던 것, 비록 지금은 이것을 거의 생각지 못했지만—자기 자신이 빚어낸 것, 그토록 손쉽게 축복으로 빚어 낼 수 있었으나 여러 해에 걸쳐 그토록 한결같이 저주로 만들려고 작정했던 것, 그것이 그의 영혼의 날카로운 비탄이었다."]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["He did remember it!", "그는 그 일을 기억했다!"], ["The rain that fell upon the roof, the wind that mourned outside the door that night, had had foreknowledge in their melancholy sound.", "그날 밤 지붕 위로 떨어지던 비, 문밖에서 슬피 울던 바람은, 그 구슬픈 소리 속에 앞날을 미리 아는 능력을 지니고 있었던 것이다."], ["He knew, now, what he had done.", "그는 이제 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알았다."], ["He knew, now, that he had called down that upon his head, which bowed it lower than the heaviest stroke of fortune.", "그는 이제, 운명의 가장 가혹한 일격보다도 더 깊이 자기 고개를 숙이게 만든 그것을, 자기가 제 머리 위로 불러들였음을 알았다."], ["He knew, now, what it was to be rejected and deserted; now, when every loving blossom he had withered in his innocent daughter’s heart was snowing down in ashes on him.", "그는 이제, 거부당하고 버림받는다는 것이 무엇인지 알았다. 자기가 그 죄 없는 딸의 마음속에서 시들게 했던 사랑의 꽃송이 하나하나가 이제 잿가루가 되어 자기 위로 눈처럼 내려앉고 있는 지금에 와서."]], [["He thought of her, as she had been that night when he and his bride came home.", "그는 자기와 신부가 집으로 돌아오던 그날 밤의 모습 그대로의 딸을 떠올렸다."], ["He thought of her as she had been, in all the home-events of the abandoned house.", "그는 이제 버려진 그 집의 온갖 가정사 속에서의 그녀의 모습을 떠올렸다."], ["He thought, now, that of all around him, she alone had never changed.", "그는 이제, 자기 주위의 모든 것 가운데 오직 그녀만이 결코 변하지 않았다고 생각했다."], ["His boy had faded into dust, his proud wife had sunk into a polluted creature, his flatterer and friend had been transformed into the worst of villains, his riches had melted away, the very walls that sheltered him looked on him as a stranger; she alone had turned the same mild gentle look upon him always.", "그의 아들은 스러져 흙이 되었고, 그의 도도한 아내는 더럽혀진 인간으로 전락했으며, 그의 아첨꾼이자 친구는 가장 못된 악당으로 둔갑했고, 그의 재산은 녹아 사라졌으며, 그를 감싸 주던 바로 그 벽들조차 그를 낯선 이로 바라보았다. 오직 그녀만이 언제나 한결같이 그 온화하고 다정한 눈길을 그에게 돌렸다."], ["Yes, to the latest and the last.", "그렇다, 가장 나중까지, 그리고 마지막 순간까지."], ["She had never changed to him—nor had he ever changed to her—and she was lost.", "그녀는 결코 그에게 변한 적이 없었고—그 또한 그녀에게 결코 변한 적이 없었으며—그리고 그녀는 떠나 버렸다."]], [["As, one by one, they fell away before his mind—his baby—hope, his wife, his friend, his fortune—oh how the mist, through which he had seen her, cleared, and showed him her true self!", "그것들이 하나하나 그의 마음 앞에서 떨어져 나갈 때—그의 아기, 그의 희망, 그의 아내, 그의 친구, 그의 재산이—오, 그가 그녀를 보아 온 그 안개가 얼마나 걷혀, 그녀의 참모습을 그에게 드러내 보였던가!"], ["Oh, how much better than this that he had loved her as he had his boy, and lost her as he had his boy, and laid them in their early grave together!", "오, 이렇게 되느니 차라리 그가 그녀를 자기 아들처럼 사랑하고, 자기 아들을 잃듯 그녀를 잃어, 그 둘을 함께 그 이른 무덤에 뉘었더라면 얼마나 나았을까!"]], [["In his pride—for he was proud yet—he let the world go from him freely.", "그의 자존심 속에서—그는 아직도 자존심이 셌으니까—그는 세상이 자기에게서 거리낌 없이 떨어져 나가게 두었다."], ["As it fell away, he shook it off.", "세상이 떨어져 나가자 그는 그것을 떨쳐 냈다."], ["Whether he imagined its face as expressing pity for him, or indifference to him, he shunned it alike.", "세상의 얼굴이 자기에 대한 동정을 드러내는 것이라 여기든, 자기에 대한 무관심을 드러내는 것이라 여기든, 그는 그것을 똑같이 피했다."], ["It was in the same degree to be avoided, in either aspect.", "그것은 어느 쪽 모습이든 똑같은 정도로 피해야 할 것이었다."], ["He had no idea of any one companion in his misery, but the one he had driven away.", "그는 자기 비참함 속에서 어떤 동무도 떠올리지 못했으니, 오직 자기가 내쫓아 버린 그 한 사람을 빼고는."], ["What he would have said to her, or what consolation submitted to receive from her, he never pictured to himself.", "자기가 그녀에게 무슨 말을 했을지, 혹은 그녀에게서 어떤 위로를 기꺼이 받았을지, 그는 스스로 그려 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 그는, 자기가 그녀를 견뎌 내기만 했더라면 그녀가 자기에게 진실했으리란 것을 늘 알고 있었다."], ["But he always knew she would have been true to him, if he had suffered her.", "그는, 그녀가 다른 어느 때보다도 지금 자기를 더 사랑했으리란 것을 늘 알고 있었다."], ["He always knew she would have loved him better now, than at any other time; he was as certain that it was in her nature, as he was that there was a sky above him; and he sat thinking so, in his loneliness, from hour to hour.", "그는 그것이 그녀의 천성에 깃들어 있음을, 자기 머리 위에 하늘이 있음만큼이나 확신했다. 그리고 그는 외로움 속에서, 시간이 가고 또 가도록 그렇게 생각하며 앉아 있었다."], ["Day after day uttered this speech; night after night showed him this knowledge.", "날이면 날마다 이 말을 되뇌었고, 밤이면 밤마다 그에게 이 깨달음을 드러내 보였다."]], [["It began, beyond all doubt (however slow it advanced for some time), in the receipt of her young husband’s letter, and the certainty that she was gone.", "그것은, 의심할 바 없이 (한동안은 더디게 나아갔을지언정), 그녀의 젊은 남편이 보낸 편지를 받고, 그녀가 떠나 버렸음을 확실히 알게 된 데서 시작되었다."], ["And yet—so proud he was in his ruin, or so reminiscent of her only as something that might have been his, but was lost beyond redemption—that if he could have heard her voice in an adjoining room, he would not have gone to her.", "그럼에도—그는 자기 파멸 속에서도 그토록 자존심이 셌고, 혹은 그녀를 오직 자기 것이 될 수도 있었으나 돌이킬 길 없이 잃어버린 무언가로만 떠올렸기에—설령 옆방에서 그녀의 목소리를 들을 수 있었다 한들, 그는 그녀에게 가지 않았을 것이다."], ["If he could have seen her in the street, and she had done no more than look at him as she had been used to look, he would have passed on with his old cold unforgiving face, and not addressed her, or relaxed it, though his heart should have broken soon afterwards.", "설령 거리에서 그녀를 볼 수 있었고, 그녀가 늘 그러던 눈길로 자기를 바라보는 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않았다 한들, 그는 옛날의 그 차갑고 용서할 줄 모르는 얼굴로 지나쳐 갔을 것이며, 그녀에게 말을 건네지도, 그 얼굴을 누그러뜨리지도 않았을 것이다, 비록 그 직후에 자기 가슴이 찢어질지라도."], ["However turbulent his thoughts, or harsh his anger had been, at first, concerning her marriage, or her husband, that was all past now.", "처음에 그녀의 결혼이나 남편에 관하여 그의 생각이 아무리 격렬하고 그의 분노가 아무리 모질었던들, 그것은 이제 죄다 지나간 일이었다."], ["He chiefly thought of what might have been, and what was not.", "그는 주로 무엇이 될 수도 있었으나 되지 못한 것을 생각했다."], ["What was, was all summed up in this: that she was lost, and he bowed down with sorrow and remorse.", "무엇이 있었는가 하면, 그것은 죄다 이 한 가지로 요약되었으니, 곧 그녀는 떠나 버렸고, 그는 슬픔과 회한에 짓눌려 고개를 숙이고 있다는 것이었다."]], [["And now he felt that he had had two children born to him in that house, and that between him and the bare wide empty walls there was a tie, mournful, but hard to rend asunder, connected with a double childhood, and a double loss.", "그리고 이제 그는, 자기에게 그 집에서 두 아이가 태어났음을, 그리고 자기와 그 휑하니 넓고 텅 빈 벽들 사이에는, 슬프지만 끊어 내기 힘든, 두 번의 어린 시절과 두 번의 상실에 얽힌 어떤 끈이 있음을 느꼈다."], ["He had thought to leave the house—knowing he must go, not knowing whither—upon the evening of the day on which this feeling first struck root in his breast; but he resolved to stay another night, and in the night to ramble through the rooms once more.", "그는 이 감정이 처음 그의 가슴속에 뿌리를 내린 날 저녁에 그 집을 떠나려고—떠나야 한다는 것은 알았으나 어디로 갈지는 알지 못한 채—마음먹었었다. 그러나 그는 하룻밤을 더 머물기로, 그리고 그 밤에 다시 한번 방들을 돌아다녀 보기로 결심했다."]], [["He came out of his solitude when it was the dead of night, and with a candle in his hand went softly up the stairs.", "그는 깊은 밤이 되었을 때 자기 칩거에서 나와, 손에 촛불을 들고 살그머니 계단을 올라갔다."], ["Of all the footmarks there, making them as common as the common street, there was not one, he thought, but had seemed at the time to set itself upon his brain while he had kept close, listening.", "거기 찍힌 모든 발자국들은—그것들을 흔한 거리바닥만큼이나 흔한 것으로 만들어 놓았건만—그가 틀어박혀 귀를 기울이는 동안 그 하나하나가 당시 자기 뇌리에 박히는 듯했었다고 그는 생각했다."], ["He looked at their number, and their hurry, and contention—foot treading foot out, and upward track and downward jostling one another—and thought, with absolute dread and wonder, how much he must have suffered during that trial, and what a changed man he had cause to be.", "그는 그것들의 수효를, 그리고 그 분주함과 다툼을—발이 발을 짓밟고, 위로 난 자취와 아래로 난 자취가 서로 밀치는 것을—바라보며, 그 시련 동안 자기가 얼마나 많은 고통을 겪었음에 틀림없는지, 또 자기가 얼마나 변해 버린 사람이 될 수밖에 없었는지를 절대적인 두려움과 경이로움으로 생각했다."], ["He thought, besides, oh was there, somewhere in the world, a light footstep that might have worn out in a moment half those marks!—and bent his head, and wept as he went up.", "그는 또한 생각했다, 오, 이 세상 어딘가에, 한순간에 저 자국들의 절반쯤은 닳아 없애 버릴 수도 있었을 가벼운 발걸음이 있지는 않았을까!—그러고는 고개를 숙이고, 올라가면서 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["He almost saw it, going on before.", "그는 그 발걸음이 자기 앞에서 나아가는 것을 거의 본 듯했다."], ["He stopped, looking up towards the skylight; and a figure, childish itself, but carrying a child, and singing as it went, seemed to be there again.", "그는 멈춰 서서, 천창을 향해 위를 올려다보았다. 그러자 한 형상이, 그 자신이 어린아이였으나 한 아이를 안고서, 가면서 노래를 부르는 한 형상이, 다시 거기 있는 듯했다."], ["Anon, it was the same figure, alone, stopping for an instant, with suspended breath; the bright hair clustering loosely round its tearful face; and looking back at him.", "이윽고, 그것은 똑같은 그 형상이, 홀로, 숨을 멈춘 채 한순간 멈춰 서서, 눈물 젖은 얼굴 둘레로 빛나는 머리칼을 헐겁게 드리운 채, 그를 돌아보고 있었다."]], [["He wandered through the rooms: lately so luxurious; now so bare and dismal and so changed, apparently, even in their shape and size.", "그는 방들을 돌아다녔다. 얼마 전까지만 해도 그토록 호사스러웠으나, 이제는 그토록 휑하고 음산하며, 보아하니 그 형태와 크기마저 그토록 달라진 방들을."], ["The press of footsteps was as thick here; and the same consideration of the suffering he had had, perplexed and terrified him.", "발자국들이 여기에도 빼곡했고, 자기가 겪은 그 고통에 대한 똑같은 생각이 그를 어리둥절하게 하고 겁먹게 했다."], ["He began to fear that all this intricacy in his brain would drive him mad; and that his thoughts already lost coherence as the footprints did, and were pieced on to one another, with the same trackless involutions, and varieties of indistinct shapes.", "그는 자기 머릿속의 이 온갖 뒤엉킴이 자기를 미치게 하지나 않을까, 그리고 자기 생각들이 이미 저 발자국들처럼 갈피를 잃고서, 똑같이 종잡을 수 없는 뒤얽힘과 어렴풋한 형상들의 갖가지 변형으로 서로 이어 붙고 있지나 않은가 두려워지기 시작했다."]], [["He did not so much as know in which of these rooms she had lived, when she was alone.", "그는 그녀가 홀로 있을 때 이 방들 가운데 어디서 지냈는지조차 알지 못했다."], ["He was glad to leave them, and go wandering higher up.", "그는 그 방들을 떠나, 더 높은 곳으로 돌아다니러 가게 된 것이 다행스러웠다."], ["Abundance of associations were here, connected with his false wife, his false friend and servant, his false grounds of pride; but he put them all by now, and only recalled miserably, weakly, fondly, his two children.", "여기에는 그의 거짓된 아내, 그의 거짓된 친구이자 종복, 그의 거짓된 자존심의 근거에 얽힌 온갖 연상이 가득했다. 그러나 그는 이제 그것들을 죄다 밀쳐 두고, 오직 자기 두 아이만을 비참하게, 나약하게, 애틋하게 떠올렸다."]], [["Everywhere, the footsteps!", "어디에나, 그 발자국들!"], ["They had had no respect for the old room high up, where the little bed had been; he could hardly find a clear space there, to throw himself down, on the floor, against the wall, poor broken man, and let his tears flow as they would.", "그것들은 작은 침대가 있던 저 높은 곳 옛 방에도 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그는 거기서, 이 가엾고 망가진 사내가, 벽에 기대어 바닥에 몸을 던지고 눈물이 흐르는 대로 내버려 둘 만한 빈자리조차 좀처럼 찾기 어려웠다."], ["He had shed so many tears here, long ago, that he was less ashamed of his weakness in this place than in any other—perhaps, with that consciousness, had made excuses to himself for coming here.", "그는 오래전 여기서 그토록 많은 눈물을 흘렸기에, 다른 어느 곳에서보다 이곳에서 자기 나약함을 덜 부끄러워했다—어쩌면 그런 의식 때문에, 여기 오는 것에 대해 스스로에게 핑계를 댔는지도 몰랐다."], ["Here, with stooping shoulders, and his chin dropped on his breast, he had come.", "여기로, 어깨를 구부정하게 늘어뜨리고 턱을 가슴에 떨군 채, 그는 왔었다."], ["Here, thrown upon the bare boards, in the dead of night, he wept, alone—a proud man, even then; who, if a kind hand could have been stretched out, or a kind face could have looked in, would have risen up, and turned away, and gone down to his cell.", "여기서, 깊은 밤에 맨바닥에 몸을 내던지고, 그는 홀로 울었다—그때조차 자존심이 센 사내였으니, 만일 어떤 다정한 손길이 내밀어졌거나 어떤 다정한 얼굴이 들여다보았더라면, 그는 벌떡 일어나 돌아서서, 자기 골방으로 내려가 버렸을 그런 사내였다."]], [["When the day broke he was shut up in his rooms again.", "날이 밝자 그는 다시 자기 방에 틀어박혔다."], ["He had meant to go away today, but clung to this tie in the house as the last and only thing left to him.", "그는 오늘 떠나려고 마음먹었었으나, 자기에게 남은 마지막이자 유일한 것으로서 그 집의 이 끈에 매달렸다."], ["He would go to-morrow.", "내일 떠나야지."], ["To-morrow came.", "내일이 왔다."], ["He would go to-morrow.", "내일 떠나야지."], ["Every night, within the knowledge of no human creature, he came forth, and wandered through the despoiled house like a ghost.", "어떤 인간도 알지 못하는 가운데, 밤마다 그는 밖으로 나와, 약탈당한 그 집을 유령처럼 떠돌았다."], ["Many a morning when the day broke, his altered face, drooping behind the closed blind in his window, imperfectly transparent to the light as yet, pondered on the loss of his two children.", "날이 밝아 오는 많은 아침이면, 변해 버린 그의 얼굴이, 아직 빛에 어슴푸레하게밖에 비치지 않는 창문의 닫힌 블라인드 뒤로 늘어진 채, 자기 두 아이를 잃은 일을 곱씹었다."], ["It was one child no more.", "그것은 이제 아이 하나가 아니었다."], ["He reunited them in his thoughts, and they were never asunder.", "그는 생각 속에서 그 둘을 다시 합쳐 놓았고, 그 둘은 결코 갈라지지 않았다."], ["Oh, that he could have united them in his past love, and in death, and that one had not been so much worse than dead!", "오, 그가 그 둘을 지난날의 자기 사랑 속에서, 그리고 죽음 속에서 합쳐 놓을 수 있었더라면, 그리고 하나가 죽은 것보다도 그토록 더 못한 처지가 되지 않았더라면!"]], [["Strong mental agitation and disturbance was no novelty to him, even before his late sufferings.", "격렬한 정신적 동요와 혼란은, 최근의 고통을 겪기 전에도, 그에게 새삼스러운 것이 아니었다."], ["It never is, to obstinate and sullen natures; for they struggle hard to be such.", "그것은 고집스럽고 음울한 천성에게는 결코 새삼스러운 것이 아니다. 그런 천성은 그렇게 되려고 모질게 애쓰는 법이니까."], ["Ground, long undermined, will often fall down in a moment; what was undermined here in so many ways, weakened, and crumbled, little by little, more and more, as the hand moved on the dial.", "오래 밑이 파인 땅은 종종 한순간에 무너져 내리는 법이다. 여기서 그토록 여러 방식으로 밑이 파이고, 약해지고, 조금씩 부서져 내린 것은, 시곗바늘이 문자판 위를 움직여 감에 따라 점점 더, 점점 더 그러하였다."]], [["At last he began to think he need not go at all.", "마침내 그는 자기가 아예 떠날 필요가 없을지도 모른다는 생각을 하기 시작했다."], ["He might yet give up what his creditors had spared him (that they had not spared him more, was his own act), and only sever the tie between him and the ruined house, by severing that other link—", "그는 채권자들이 자기에게 남겨 준 것을(그들이 더 많이 남겨 주지 않은 것은 그 자신의 처사 탓이었다) 그래도 내어 줄 수 있었고, 자기와 그 폐허가 된 집 사이의 끈을, 오직 저 다른 한 가닥의 연결을 끊어 냄으로써만 끊어 낼 수 있었다—"]], [["It was then that his footfall was audible in the late housekeeper’s room, as he walked to and fro; but not audible in its true meaning, or it would have had an appalling sound.", "바로 그때 그의 발소리가, 그가 오락가락 거니는 동안, 옛 가정부 방에서 들려왔다. 그러나 그것이 지닌 참뜻으로 들린 것은 아니었으니, 그렇지 않았더라면 그것은 소름 끼치는 소리였으리라."]], [["The world was very busy and restless about him.", "세상은 그의 주위에서 무척 분주하고 부산스러웠다."], ["He became aware of that again.", "그는 그것을 다시금 의식하게 되었다."], ["It was whispering and babbling.", "그것은 소곤거리고 재잘거렸다."], ["It was never quiet.", "그것은 결코 잠잠하지 않았다."], ["This, and the intricacy and complication of the footsteps, harassed him to death.", "이것이, 그리고 발자국들의 뒤엉킴과 복잡함이, 그를 죽을 만큼 괴롭혔다."], ["Objects began to take a bleared and russet colour in his eyes.", "사물들이 그의 눈에 흐릿하고 적갈색을 띠기 시작했다."], ["Dombey and Son was no more—his children no more.", "돔비와 아들은 이제 없었다—그의 아이들도 이제 없었다."], ["This must be thought of, well, to-morrow.", "이것을 잘 생각해 봐야지, 내일."]], [["He thought of it to-morrow; and sitting thinking in his chair, saw in the glass, from time to time, this picture:", "그는 내일 그것을 생각했다. 그리고 자기 의자에 앉아 생각하다가, 이따금 거울 속에서 이런 그림을 보았다."]], [["A spectral, haggard, wasted likeness of himself, brooded and brooded over the empty fireplace.", "유령 같고, 초췌하고, 수척한 자기 자신의 형상이, 텅 빈 벽난로 위로 골똘히, 골똘히 잠겨 있었다."], ["Now it lifted up its head, examining the lines and hollows in its face; now hung it down again, and brooded afresh.", "이제 그것은 고개를 들어, 자기 얼굴의 주름과 패인 곳들을 살펴보았다. 이제 다시 고개를 떨구고는, 새로이 골똘히 잠겼다."], ["Now it rose and walked about; now passed into the next room, and came back with something from the dressing-table in its breast.", "이제 그것은 일어나 이리저리 거닐었다. 이제 옆방으로 들어가더니, 화장대에서 무언가를 가슴에 품고 돌아왔다."], ["Now, it was looking at the bottom of the door, and thinking.", "이제 그것은 문 아래쪽을 바라보며, 생각에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["—Hush!", "—쉿!"], ["what?", "무엇을?"]], [["It was thinking that if blood were to trickle that way, and to leak out into the hall, it must be a long time going so far.", "그것은, 만일 피가 그쪽으로 흘러나와 현관으로 새어 나간다면, 그토록 멀리까지 가는 데 오랜 시간이 걸리겠다고 생각하고 있었다."], ["It would move so stealthily and slowly, creeping on, with here a lazy little pool, and there a start, and then another little pool, that a desperately wounded man could only be discovered through its means, either dead or dying.", "그것은 너무도 은밀하고 더디게, 기어가듯 흘러서, 여기에 게으른 작은 웅덩이를 하나 짓고, 저기서 한 번 멈칫하고, 그러고는 또 다른 작은 웅덩이를 하나 짓다 보면, 치명상을 입은 사내는 오직 그것을 통해서만, 죽었거나 죽어 가는 채로 발견될 수 있겠다고."], ["When it had thought of this a long while, it got up again, and walked to and fro with its hand in its breast.", "그것은 이것을 오랫동안 생각한 끝에 다시 일어나, 한 손을 가슴에 품은 채 이리저리 거닐었다."], ["He glanced at it occasionally, very curious to watch its motions, and he marked how wicked and murderous that hand looked.", "그는 이따금 그것을 흘끔거리며, 그 동작을 지켜보는 것에 무척 호기심을 느꼈고, 그 손이 얼마나 사악하고 살기등등해 보이는지를 눈여겨보았다."]], [["Now it was thinking again!", "이제 그것은 다시 생각하고 있었다!"], ["What was it thinking?", "그것은 무엇을 생각하고 있었던가?"]], [["Whether they would tread in the blood when it crept so far, and carry it about the house among those many prints of feet, or even out into the street.", "사람들이, 그 피가 그토록 멀리까지 기어가면 그 피를 밟고서, 저 수많은 발자국들 사이로 그것을 온 집 안에, 아니 거리에까지 묻혀 다니지나 않을까 하는 것을."]], [["It sat down, with its eyes upon the empty fireplace, and as it lost itself in thought there shone into the room a gleam of light; a ray of sun.", "그것은 텅 빈 벽난로에 두 눈을 둔 채 앉았다. 그리고 그것이 생각 속에 빠져들었을 때, 방 안으로 한 줄기 빛이, 한 가닥 햇살이 비쳐 들었다."], ["It was quite unmindful, and sat thinking.", "그것은 전혀 개의치 않고, 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있었다."], ["Suddenly it rose, with a terrible face, and that guilty hand grasping what was in its breast.", "별안간 그것은, 무시무시한 얼굴로, 그 죄지은 손에 가슴속에 품은 것을 움켜쥐고 일어났다."], ["Then it was arrested by a cry—a wild, loud, piercing, loving, rapturous cry—and he only saw his own reflection in the glass, and at his knees, his daughter!", "그때 그것은 한 외침에 멈춰 섰다—거칠고, 크고, 가슴을 찌르는 듯한, 사랑이 넘치고, 환희에 찬 외침에—그리고 그는 거울 속에서 자기 자신의 모습만을, 그리고 자기 무릎 곁에서, 자기 딸을 보았다!"]], [["Yes.", "그렇다."], ["His daughter!", "그의 딸을!"], ["Look at her!", "그녀를 보라!"], ["Look here!", "여기를 보라!"], ["Down upon the ground, clinging to him, calling to him, folding her hands, praying to him.", "바닥에 엎드려, 그에게 매달려, 그를 부르며, 두 손을 모으고, 그에게 빌고 있는 그녀를."]], [["“Papa!", "\"아빠!"], ["Dearest Papa!", "사랑하는 아빠!"], ["Pardon me, forgive me!", "절 용서해 주세요, 용서해 주세요!"], ["I have come back to ask forgiveness on my knees.", "저는 무릎 꿇고 용서를 빌러 돌아왔어요."], ["I never can be happy more, without it!”", "그것 없이는 저는 다시는 행복할 수 없어요!\""]], [["Unchanged still.", "여전히 변함이 없었다."], ["Of all the world, unchanged.", "온 세상에서, 변함이 없었다."], ["Raising the same face to his, as on that miserable night.", "그 비참하던 밤과 똑같은 그 얼굴을 그에게 들어 올리며."], ["Asking his forgiveness!", "그의 용서를 빌면서!"]], [["“Dear Papa, oh don’t look strangely on me!", "\"사랑하는 아빠, 오, 저를 낯설게 바라보지 마세요!"], ["I never meant to leave you.", "저는 아빠를 떠날 생각이 결코 없었어요."], ["I never thought of it, before or afterwards.", "그 전에도 그 후에도 그런 건 한 번도 생각해 본 적 없어요."], ["I was frightened when I went away, and could not think.", "저는 떠나갈 때 겁에 질려서, 도무지 생각이란 걸 할 수가 없었어요."], ["Papa, dear, I am changed.", "아빠, 사랑하는 아빠, 저는 변했어요."], ["I am penitent.", "저는 뉘우치고 있어요."], ["I know my fault.", "제 잘못을 알아요."], ["I know my duty better now.", "이제는 제 도리를 더 잘 알아요."], ["Papa, don’t cast me off, or I shall die!”", "아빠, 저를 내치지 마세요, 안 그러면 저는 죽고 말 거예요!\""]], [["He tottered to his chair.", "그는 비틀거리며 자기 의자로 갔다."], ["He felt her draw his arms about her neck; he felt her put her own round his; he felt her kisses on his face; he felt her wet cheek laid against his own; he felt—oh, how deeply!—all that he had done.", "그는 그녀가 자기 두 팔을 자기 목에 둘러 감는 것을 느꼈고, 그녀가 자기 두 팔을 그의 목에 두르는 것을 느꼈으며, 그녀의 입맞춤이 자기 얼굴에 닿는 것을 느꼈고, 그녀의 젖은 뺨이 자기 뺨에 맞닿는 것을 느꼈으며, 느꼈다—오, 얼마나 사무치게!—자기가 저질러 온 그 모든 것을."]], [["Upon the breast that he had bruised, against the heart that he had almost broken, she laid his face, now covered with his hands, and said, sobbing:", "자기가 멍들게 했던 그 가슴에, 자기가 거의 부숴 놓았던 그 심장에, 그녀는 이제 두 손으로 가린 그의 얼굴을 뉘었고, 흐느끼며 말했다."]], [["“Papa, love, I am a mother.", "\"아빠, 사랑하는 아빠, 저는 어미가 되었어요."], ["I have a child who will soon call Walter by the name by which I call you.", "제게는 머지않아 월터를, 제가 아빠를 부르는 그 이름으로 부르게 될 아이가 있어요."], ["When it was born, and when I knew how much I loved it, I knew what I had done in leaving you.", "그 애가 태어났을 때, 그리고 제가 그 애를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알았을 때, 저는 아빠를 떠나면서 제가 무슨 짓을 한 것인지 알게 되었어요."], ["Forgive me, dear Papa!", "저를 용서해 주세요, 사랑하는 아빠!"], ["oh say God bless me, and my little child!”", "오 부디 저와 제 어린아이에게 하느님의 축복을 빌어 주세요!\""]], [["He would have said it, if he could.", "그는 할 수만 있었다면 그렇게 말했을 것이다."], ["He would have raised his hands and besought her for pardon, but she caught them in her own, and put them down, hurriedly.", "그는 두 손을 들어 그녀에게 용서를 빌었을 것이나, 그녀가 자기 두 손으로 그 손을 붙잡아, 황급히 끌어 내렸다."]], [["“My little child was born at sea, Papa I prayed to God (and so did Walter for me) to spare me, that I might come home.", "\"제 어린아이는 바다 위에서 태어났어요, 아빠. 저는 하느님께 (그리고 월터도 저를 위해 그랬어요) 제 목숨을 살려 주셔서 집에 돌아올 수 있게 해 달라고 빌었어요."], ["The moment I could land, I came back to you.", "뭍에 닿을 수 있게 되자마자, 저는 아빠에게로 돌아왔어요."], ["Never let us be parted any more, Papa.", "우리 다시는 갈라지지 말아요, 아빠."], ["Never let us be parted any more!”", "다시는 결코 갈라지지 말아요!\""]], [["His head, now grey, was encircled by her arm; and he groaned to think that never, never, had it rested so before.", "이제 백발이 된 그의 머리가 그녀의 팔에 감싸였다. 그리고 그는 그것이 전에는 한 번도, 결코 한 번도 그렇게 안긴 적이 없었음을 생각하며 신음했다."]], [["“You will come home with me, Papa, and see my baby.", "\"저랑 같이 집에 가요, 아빠, 그래서 제 아기를 보세요."], ["A boy, Papa.", "사내아이예요, 아빠."], ["His name is Paul.", "그 애 이름은 폴이에요."], ["I think—I hope—he’s like—”", "제 생각엔—제 바람으로는—그 애가 닮은 것 같아요—\""]], [["Her tears stopped her.", "그녀의 눈물이 그녀의 말을 막았다."]], [["“Dear Papa, for the sake of my child, for the sake of the name we have given him, for my sake, pardon Walter.", "\"사랑하는 아빠, 제 아이를 봐서, 우리가 그 애에게 지어 준 이름을 봐서, 저를 봐서, 월터를 용서해 주세요."], ["He is so kind and tender to me.", "그이는 제게 무척 다정하고 자상해요."], ["I am so happy with him.", "저는 그이와 너무나 행복해요."], ["It was not his fault that we were married.", "우리가 결혼한 건 그이 잘못이 아니었어요."], ["It was mine.", "제 잘못이었어요."], ["I loved him so much.”", "제가 그이를 너무나 사랑했거든요.\""]], [["She clung closer to him, more endearing and more earnest.", "그녀는 더한층 다정하게, 더한층 간절하게, 그에게 더 바싹 매달렸다."]], [["“He is the darling of my heart, Papa I would die for him.", "\"그이는 제 마음의 보배예요, 아빠. 저는 그이를 위해서라면 죽을 수도 있어요."], ["He will love and honour you as I will.", "그이는 제가 그러듯이 아빠를 사랑하고 공경할 거예요. 우리는 우리 어린아이에게 아빠를 사랑하고 공경하라고 가르칠 거예요."], ["We will teach our little child to love and honour you; and we will tell him, when he can understand, that you had a son of that name once, and that he died, and you were very sorry; but that he is gone to Heaven, where we all hope to see him when our time for resting comes.", "그리고 그 애가 알아들을 만하면, 아빠에게 한때 그 이름을 가진 아들이 있었는데 그 애가 죽어서 아빠가 무척 슬퍼하셨다고, 하지만 그 애는 하늘나라로 갔고 우리가 쉴 때가 오면 거기서 다들 그 애를 만나길 바란다고 그 애에게 말해 줄 거예요. 입맞춤해 주세요, 아빠, 월터와—제 더없이 소중한 남편과—저더러 돌아오라고 가르쳐 준 그 어린아이의 아버지와 화해하시겠다는 약속으로요."], ["Kiss me, Papa, as a promise that you will be reconciled to Walter—to my dearest husband—to the father of the little child who taught me to come back, Papa Who taught me to come back!”", "아빠. 저더러 돌아오라고 가르쳐 준 그 애 말이에요!\""]], [["As she clung closer to him, in another burst of tears, he kissed her on her lips, and, lifting up his eyes, said, “Oh my God, forgive me, for I need it very much!”", "그녀가 또 한차례 눈물을 쏟으며 그에게 더 바싹 매달릴 때, 그는 그녀의 입술에 입맞추고는, 두 눈을 들어 올리며 말했다, \"오 하느님, 저를 용서해 주소서, 제겐 그것이 너무도 절실하나이다!\""]], [["With that he dropped his head again, lamenting over and caressing her, and there was not a sound in all the house for a long, long time; they remaining clasped in one another’s arms, in the glorious sunshine that had crept in with Florence.", "그 말과 함께 그는 다시 고개를 떨구고, 그녀를 애통해하며 어루만졌다. 그리고 온 집 안에 오래도록, 오래도록 아무 소리도 없었다. 플로렌스와 함께 스며든 그 찬란한 햇살 속에서, 두 사람은 서로의 품에 꼭 안긴 채 있었다."]], [["He dressed himself for going out, with a docile submission to her entreaty; and walking with a feeble gait, and looking back, with a tremble, at the room in which he had been so long shut up, and where he had seen the picture in the glass, passed out with her into the hall.", "그는 그녀의 간청에 고분고분 따르며 외출 채비를 했다. 그리고 비실비실한 걸음걸이로 걸으며, 자기가 그토록 오래 틀어박혀 있던, 거울 속에서 그 그림을 보았던 그 방을 떨리는 몸으로 돌아보면서, 그녀와 함께 현관으로 나섰다."], ["Florence, hardly glancing round her, lest she should remind him freshly of their last parting—for their feet were on the very stones where he had struck her in his madness—and keeping close to him, with her eyes upon his face, and his arm about her, led him out to a coach that was waiting at the door, and carried him away.", "플로렌스는, 그가 마지막 이별을 새삼 떠올릴까 봐 주위를 거의 둘러보지도 않은 채—그도 그럴 것이 두 사람의 발은 그가 광기에 사로잡혀 그녀를 내리쳤던 바로 그 돌바닥 위에 있었으니—그에게 바싹 붙어, 그의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않고, 그의 팔을 자기에게 두른 채, 문 앞에 기다리고 있던 마차로 그를 이끌어, 그를 데리고 떠났다."]], [["Then, Miss Tox and Polly came out of their concealment, and exulted tearfully.", "그러자 톡스 양과 폴리가 숨어 있던 데서 나와, 눈물을 흘리며 기뻐 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다."], ["And then they packed his clothes, and books, and so forth, with great care; and consigned them in due course to certain persons sent by Florence, in the evening, to fetch them.", "그러고는 그의 옷가지며 책이며 그 밖의 것들을 무척 정성스레 꾸려, 저녁에 플로렌스가 그것들을 가지러 보낸 어떤 사람들에게 차례에 따라 넘겨주었다."], ["And then they took a last cup of tea in the lonely house.", "그러고서 그 두 사람은 외로운 그 집에서 마지막 차 한 잔을 들었다."]], [["“And so Dombey and Son, as I observed upon a certain sad occasion,”", "\"그러니 돔비와 아들은, 내가 어떤 슬픈 자리에서 말했듯이,\""], ["said Miss Tox, winding up a host of recollections, “is indeed a daughter, Polly, after all.”", "톡스 양이 숱한 회상을 마무리 지으며 말했다, \"결국, 폴리, 정말이지 딸이었던 거예요.\""]], [["“And a good one!”", "\"그것도 착한 딸이고요!\""], ["exclaimed Polly.", "폴리가 외쳤다."]], [["“You are right,”", "\"맞아요,\""], ["said Miss Tox; “and it’s a credit to you, Polly, that you were always her friend when she was a little child.", "톡스 양이 말했다. \"그리고 폴리, 당신이 그 애가 어린아이였을 때부터 늘 그 애의 동무가 되어 준 건 당신에게 명예로운 일이에요."], ["You were her friend long before I was, Polly,”", "당신은 나보다 훨씬 전부터 그 애의 동무였잖아요, 폴리,\""], ["said Miss Tox; “and you’re a good creature.", "톡스 양이 말했다. \"그리고 당신은 착한 사람이에요."], ["Robin!”", "로빈!\""]], [["Miss Tox addressed herself to a bullet-headed young man, who appeared to be in but indifferent circumstances, and in depressed spirits, and who was sitting in a remote corner.", "톡스 양은, 형편이 그저 그런 데다 풀 죽은 기색이 역력한, 외진 구석에 앉아 있던 머리통이 탄알처럼 둥근 젊은이에게 말을 건넸다."], ["Rising, he disclosed to view the form and features of the Grinder.", "일어서자 그는 그라인더의 몸매와 이목구비를 드러내 보였다."]], [["“Robin,”", "\"로빈,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “I have just observed to your mother, as you may have heard, that she is a good creature.”", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"방금 너희 어머니에게, 너도 들었겠지만, 그분이 착한 사람이라고 말했단다.\""]], [["“And so she is, Miss,”", "\"정말 그러세요, 아가씨,\""], ["quoth the Grinder, with some feeling.", "그라인더가 다소 감정을 담아 말했다."]], [["“Very well, Robin,”", "\"좋아, 로빈,\""], ["said Miss Tox, “I am glad to hear you say so.", "톡스 양이 말했다, \"네가 그렇게 말해 주니 기쁘구나."], ["Now, Robin, as I am going to give you a trial, at your urgent request, as my domestic, with a view to your restoration to respectability, I will take this impressive occasion of remarking that I hope you will never forget that you have, and have always had, a good mother, and that you will endeavour so to conduct yourself as to be a comfort to her.”", "자, 로빈, 네가 간곡히 청한 대로 너를 우리 집 하인으로 한번 시험 삼아 써 보면서 네가 다시 점잖은 신분을 되찾도록 해 줄 참이니, 나는 이 뜻깊은 자리를 빌려 한마디 하마. 네게는 착한 어머니가 있고 또 늘 있어 왔다는 것을 결코 잊지 말고, 그분께 위안이 되도록 처신하기를 부디 애쓰기 바란다.\""]], [["“Upon my soul I will, Miss,”", "\"맹세코 그러겠습니다, 아가씨,\""], ["returned the Grinder.", "그라인더가 대답했다."], ["“I have come through a good deal, and my intentions is now as straightfor’ard, Miss, as a cove’s—”", "\"저는 별의별 일을 다 겪었고, 이제 제 마음가짐은 한 사내 녀석이—\""]], [["“I must get you to break yourself of that word, Robin, if you please,”", "\"제발 그 말버릇은 고쳤으면 좋겠구나, 로빈,\""], ["interposed Miss Tox, politely.", "톡스 양이 정중하게 끼어들었다."]], [["“If you please, Miss, as a chap’s—”", "\"그럼, 아가씨, 한 사내놈이—\""]], [["“Thankee, Robin, no,”", "\"고맙다만, 로빈, 아니다,\""], ["returned Miss Tox, “I should prefer individual.”", "톡스 양이 대답했다, \"나는 한 개인이라고 하는 게 더 좋겠구나.\""]], [["“As a indiwiddle’s—,”", "\"한 개인의—,\""], ["said the Grinder.", "그라인더가 말했다."]], [["“Much better,”", "\"훨씬 낫구나,\""], ["remarked Miss Tox, complacently; “infinitely more expressive!”", "톡스 양이 흐뭇하게 한마디 했다. \"한없이 더 적절해!\""]], [["“—can be,” pursued Rob.", "\"—그것만큼 곧을 수 있습니다,\""], ["“If I hadn’t been and got made a Grinder on, Miss and Mother, which was a most unfortunate circumstance for a young co—indiwiddle—”", "로브가 말을 이었다. \"제가 그라인더가 돼 버린 게 아니었다면요, 아가씨, 어머니, 그게 어린 사—개인에게는 더없이 불운한 일이었지만요—\""]], [["“Very good indeed,”", "\"정말 아주 좋구나,\""], ["observed Miss Tox, approvingly.", "톡스 양이 흐뭇해하며 한마디 했다."]], [["“—and if I hadn’t been led away by birds, and then fallen into a bad service,”", "\"—그리고 제가 새들한테 홀려 빗나가지 않고, 그담에 못된 일자리에 빠져들지만 않았어도,\""], ["said the Grinder, “I hope I might have done better.", "그라인더가 말했다, \"저는 더 잘됐을 거예요."], ["But it’s never too late for a—”", "하지만 한 개—\""]], [["“Indi—”", "\"개인이—\""], ["suggested Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 거들었다."]], [["“—widdle,”", "\"—인이 고쳐 나가기엔 결코 늦지 않은 법이죠."], ["said the Grinder, “to mend; and I hope to mend, Miss, with your kind trial; and wishing, Mother, my love to father, and brothers and sisters, and saying of it.”", "그리고 저는 아가씨의 자비로운 시험 덕분에 고쳐 나가기를 바라고요, 아가씨. 그리고 어머니, 아버지랑 형제자매들에게 제 사랑을 전해 주시길 바라며, 그리 말씀드립니다.\""]], [["“I am very glad indeed to hear it,”", "\"그렇게 들으니 정말 무척 기쁘구나,\""], ["observed Miss Tox.", "톡스 양이 한마디 했다."], ["“Will you take a little bread and butter, and a cup of tea, before we go, Robin?”", "\"떠나기 전에 빵에 버터 바른 것 좀하고 차 한 잔 들겠니, 로빈?\""]], [["“Thankee, Miss,”", "\"고맙습니다, 아가씨,\""], ["returned the Grinder; who immediately began to use his own personal grinders in a most remarkable manner, as if he had been on very short allowance for a considerable period.", "그라인더가 대답했다. 그는 마치 상당 기간 아주 부족한 양만 배급받기라도 했던 것처럼, 곧장 자기 어금니를 더없이 놀라운 솜씨로 놀려 대기 시작했다."]], [["Miss Tox, being, in good time, bonneted and shawled, and Polly too, Rob hugged his mother, and followed his new mistress away; so much to the hopeful admiration of Polly, that something in her eyes made luminous rings round the gas-lamps as she looked after him.", "톡스 양이 때맞춰 보닛을 쓰고 숄을 두르고, 폴리도 그러자, 로브는 어머니를 꼭 끌어안고 새 여주인을 따라 떠나갔는데, 그것이 폴리에게 어찌나 희망에 찬 흐뭇함을 안겨 주었던지, 그 뒷모습을 바라보는 그녀의 두 눈에 어린 무언가가 가스등 둘레로 환한 둥근 빛 고리를 만들어 냈다."], ["Polly then put out her light, locked the house-door, delivered the key at an agent’s hard by, and went home as fast as she could go; rejoicing in the shrill delight that her unexpected arrival would occasion there.", "그러고서 폴리는 불을 끄고, 집 문을 잠그고, 가까운 곳의 대리인에게 열쇠를 건네주고는, 갈 수 있는 한 빠르게 집으로 갔다. 자기가 뜻밖에 돌아가면 거기서 자아낼 새된 환희를 기뻐하면서."], ["The great house, dumb as to all that had been suffered in it, and the changes it had witnessed, stood frowning like a dark mute on the street; baulking any nearer inquiries with the staring announcement that the lease of this desirable Family Mansion was to be disposed of.", "그 큰 저택은, 그 안에서 겪어 온 그 모든 일과 그것이 지켜본 그 모든 변화에 대해 벙어리가 된 채, 거리를 향해 어둡고 말없는 거인처럼 찌푸린 얼굴로 서 있었다. 이 탐나는 가족용 저택의 임대권을 처분한다는 빤히 노려보는 듯한 공고로, 그 어떤 가까운 캐물음도 가로막으면서."]], [["# CHAPTER LX. Chiefly Matrimonial", "# CHAPTER LX. Chiefly Matrimonial"]], [["The grand half-yearly festival holden by Doctor and Mrs Blimber, on which occasion they requested the pleasure of the company of every young gentleman pursuing his studies in that genteel establishment, at an early party, when the hour was half-past seven o’clock, and when the object was quadrilles, had duly taken place, about this time; and the young gentlemen, with no unbecoming demonstrations of levity, had betaken themselves, in a state of scholastic repletion, to their own homes.", "블림버 박사 부부가 반년마다 한 번씩 성대하게 여는 잔치—이 점잖은 학교에서 공부하는 모든 어린 신사들에게 일찌감치 참석해 주는 기쁨을 청하고, 시각은 일곱 시 반이며 목적은 카드리유 춤인 그 모임—은 이 무렵에 어김없이 열렸다. 그리고 어린 신사들은 경박하다 할 만한 꼴사나운 기색 없이, 학문으로 배가 잔뜩 부른 상태에서 저마다의 집으로 떠나갔다."], ["Mr Skettles had repaired abroad, permanently to grace the establishment of his father Sir Barnet Skettles, whose popular manners had obtained him a diplomatic appointment, the honours of which were discharged by himself and Lady Skettles, to the satisfaction even of their own countrymen and countrywomen: which was considered almost miraculous.", "스케틀즈 군은 외국으로 가서, 자기 아버지 바넷 스케틀즈 경의 가문을 영구히 빛내게 되었는데, 그 아버지의 인기 있는 처신은 그에게 외교관 직책을 안겨 주었고, 그 직무의 영예는 그 자신과 스케틀즈 부인에 의해, 심지어 자기 나라 사람들조차 만족할 만큼 잘 수행되었다—이는 거의 기적으로 여겨졌다."], ["Mr Tozer, now a young man of lofty stature, in Wellington boots, was so extremely full of antiquity as to be nearly on a par with a genuine ancient Roman in his knowledge of English: a triumph that affected his good parents with the tenderest emotions, and caused the father and mother of Mr Briggs (whose learning, like ill-arranged luggage, was so tightly packed that he couldn’t get at anything he wanted) to hide their diminished heads.", "토저 군은 이제 키 큰 청년이 되어 웰링턴 부츠를 신고 다녔는데, 고대에 어찌나 통달했던지 영어 지식에 있어서만큼은 진짜 고대 로마인에 거의 맞먹을 정도였다. 이는 그의 착한 부모의 마음을 더없이 애틋한 감정으로 적셔 놓았으며, 브릭스 군의 아버지와 어머니로 하여금(그의 학식은 어수선하게 꾸린 짐짝처럼 너무 빽빽이 채워져 있어서 정작 필요한 것은 무엇 하나 꺼내 쓸 수가 없었다) 의기소침하여 고개를 숙이게 만들었다."], ["The fruit laboriously gathered from the tree of knowledge by this latter young gentleman, in fact, had been subjected to so much pressure, that it had become a kind of intellectual Norfolk Biffin, and had nothing of its original form or flavour remaining.", "사실 이 후자의 어린 신사가 지식의 나무에서 애써 따 모은 열매는 어찌나 많은 압력을 받았던지, 일종의 지적인 노퍽 비핀이 되어 본래의 형태나 맛이라고는 하나도 남아 있지 않았다. 비더스톤 군으로 말하자면, 주입식 교육이 그에게는 더 다행스럽고도 드물지 않은 결과를 가져와, 주입 장치가 작동을 멈추자 아무런 흔적도 남기지 않았으므로, 훨씬 편안한 처지였다."], ["Master Bitherstone now, on whom the forcing system had the happier and not uncommon effect of leaving no impression whatever, when the forcing apparatus ceased to work, was in a much more comfortable plight; and being then on shipboard, bound for Bengal, found himself forgetting, with such admirable rapidity, that it was doubtful whether his declensions of noun-substantives would hold out to the end of the voyage.", "그리고 그는 그때 벵골로 가는 배에 올라 있었는데, 어찌나 감탄스러울 만큼 빠르게 잊어 가고 있던지, 그의 명사 변화법이 항해가 끝날 때까지 버텨 줄지조차 의심스러울 지경이었다. * 노퍽 비핀: 오래 눌러 말린 사과의 일종으로 본래 형태를 잃은 모양."]], [["When Doctor Blimber, in pursuance of the usual course, would have said to the young gentlemen, on the morning of the party, “Gentlemen, we will resume our studies on the twenty-fifth of next month,”", "블림버 박사는 으레 하던 대로, 잔치가 있는 날 아침이면 어린 신사들에게 \"여러분, 다음 달 이십오일에 학업을 재개하겠습니다\"라고 말했을 터인데, 이번에는 그 관례에서 벗어나 이렇게 말했다."], ["he departed from the usual course, and said, “Gentlemen, when our friend Cincinnatus retired to his farm, he did not present to the senate any Roman who he sought to nominate as his successor.", "\"여러분, 우리의 벗 킨킨나투스가 자기 농장으로 물러갔을 때, 그는 자기 후임으로 지명하고자 하는 로마인을 원로원에 내세우지 않았습니다. 그러나 여기 한 로마인이 있습니다.\""], ["But there is a Roman here,”", "블림버 박사는 피더 군 학사의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다."], ["said Doctor Blimber, laying his hand on the shoulder of Mr Feeder, B.A., “adolescens imprimis gravis et doctus, gentlemen, whom I, a retiring Cincinnatus, wish to present to my little senate, as their future Dictator.", "\"여러분, '아돌레스켄스 임프리미스 그라비스 엣 독투스'(특히 진중하고 학식 있는 젊은이)이니, 물러가는 킨킨나투스인 내가 나의 작은 원로원에 미래의 독재관으로 내세우고자 하는 사람입니다. 여러분, 우리는 다음 달 이십오일에 피더 군 학사의 지도 아래 학업을 재개하겠습니다.\""], ["Gentlemen, we will resume our studies on the twenty-fifth of next month, under the auspices of Mr Feeder, B.A.”", "이 말에(블림버 박사는 이에 앞서 모든 학부모를 일일이 찾아가 정중하게 설명해 둔 터였다) 어린 신사들은 환호했다."], ["At this (which Doctor Blimber had previously called upon all the parents, and urbanely explained), the young gentlemen cheered; and Mr Tozer, on behalf of the rest, instantly presented the Doctor with a silver inkstand, in a speech containing very little of the mother-tongue, but fifteen quotations from the Latin, and seven from the Greek, which moved the younger of the young gentlemen to discontent and envy: they remarking, “Oh, ah.", "그리고 토저 군이 나머지 모두를 대표하여 즉시 박사에게 은제 잉크병을 바쳤는데, 모국어는 거의 들어 있지 않고 라틴어 인용이 열다섯 군데, 그리스어 인용이 일곱 군데나 든 연설을 곁들였다. 이것은 더 어린 신사들의 불만과 시샘을 자아냈으니, 그들은 이렇게 투덜거렸다."], ["It was all very well for old Tozer, but they didn’t subscribe money for old Tozer to show off with, they supposed; did they?", "\"허, 참. 토저 영감이야 잘됐겠지만, 우리가 토저 영감 잘난 척하라고 돈을 모은 건 아니잖아, 안 그래?"], ["What business was it of old Tozer’s more than anybody else’s?", "토저 영감이 무슨 권리로 남보다 더 나서는 거야?"], ["It wasn’t his inkstand.", "그의 잉크병도 아닌데."], ["Why couldn’t he leave the boys’ property alone?”", "애들 물건을 왜 가만 놔두질 못해?\""], ["and murmuring other expressions of their dissatisfaction, which seemed to find a greater relief in calling him old Tozer, than in any other available vent.", "그러고는 다른 불만의 표현들을 중얼거렸는데, 그것들은 그를 토저 영감이라고 부르는 것 말고는 달리 풀 길을 찾지 못하는 듯했다. * 킨킨나투스: 로마의 정치가로, 위기 때 독재관이 되었다가 임무를 마치고 농장으로 돌아간 인물."]], [["Not a word had been said to the young gentlemen, nor a hint dropped, of anything like a contemplated marriage between Mr Feeder, B.A., and the fair Cornelia Blimber.", "피더 군 학사와 아리따운 코르넬리아 블림버 사이에 혼인을 염두에 두고 있다는 따위의 말은 어린 신사들에게 한마디도 새어 나간 적이 없었고, 암시조차 흘려진 적이 없었다."], ["Doctor Blimber, especially, seemed to take pains to look as if nothing would surprise him more; but it was perfectly well known to all the young gentlemen nevertheless, and when they departed for the society of their relations and friends, they took leave of Mr Feeder with awe.", "특히 블림버 박사는, 그 무엇도 자기를 그보다 더 놀라게 할 수는 없다는 듯한 표정을 지으려고 일부러 애쓰는 것 같았다. 그러나 그럼에도 불구하고 어린 신사들은 모두 그것을 아주 잘 알고 있었고, 친척과 친구들의 모임으로 떠날 때 그들은 경외감을 품고 피더 군에게 작별을 고했다."]], [["Mr Feeder’s most romantic visions were fulfilled.", "피더 군의 가장 낭만적인 꿈들이 이루어졌다."], ["The Doctor had determined to paint the house outside, and put it in thorough repair; and to give up the business, and to give up Cornelia.", "박사는 집 바깥을 새로 칠하고 구석구석 손보기로, 그리고 사업을 그만두고 코르넬리아도 내주기로 결심한 터였다."], ["The painting and repairing began upon the very day of the young gentlemen’s departure, and now behold!", "칠하고 수리하는 일은 어린 신사들이 떠난 바로 그날 시작되었는데, 이제 보라!"], ["the wedding morning was come, and Cornelia, in a new pair of spectacles, was waiting to be led to the hymeneal altar.", "혼례를 올리는 아침이 밝았고, 코르넬리아는 새 안경을 끼고서 혼인의 제단으로 인도되기를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["The Doctor with his learned legs, and Mrs Blimber in a lilac bonnet, and Mr Feeder, B.A., with his long knuckles and his bristly head of hair, and Mr Feeder’s brother, the Reverend Alfred Feeder, M.A., who was to perform the ceremony, were all assembled in the drawing-room, and Cornelia with her orange-flowers and bridesmaids had just come down, and looked, as of old, a little squeezed in appearance, but very charming, when the door opened, and the weak-eyed young man, in a loud voice, made the following proclamation:", "박식한 다리를 지닌 박사와, 라일락빛 보닛을 쓴 블림버 부인과, 긴 손가락 마디와 뻣뻣한 머리털을 한 피더 군 학사와, 예식을 집전할 피더 군의 형제 알프레드 피더 신부 석사가 모두 응접실에 모여 있었다. 오렌지꽃을 들고 들러리들을 거느린 코르넬리아가 막 내려왔는데, 옛날처럼 외양이 다소 죄어든 듯 보였으나 무척 매력적이었다. 그때 문이 열리고, 눈이 약한 청년이 큰 소리로 다음과 같이 알렸다."]], [["Upon which there entered Mr Toots, grown extremely stout, and on his arm a lady very handsomely and becomingly dressed, with very bright black eyes.", "\"투츠 부부 도착이옵니다!\" 이에 투츠 씨가 들어왔는데, 무척 뚱뚱해져 있었고, 그의 팔짱을 낀 부인은 아주 우아하고 어울리게 차려입었으며 새까만 눈이 무척 반짝였다."]], [["“Mrs Blimber,”", "\"블림버 부인,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “allow me to present my wife.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"제 아내를 소개하게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["Mrs Blimber was delighted to receive her.", "블림버 부인은 그녀를 맞이하게 되어 기뻐했다."], ["Mrs Blimber was a little condescending, but extremely kind.", "블림버 부인은 다소 점잔을 빼긴 했지만 더없이 친절했다."]], [["“And as you’ve known me for a long time, you know,”", "\"그리고 저를 오래 알고 계시니까,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “let me assure you that she is one of the most remarkable women that ever lived.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"단언하건대 이 사람은 여태껏 살았던 여인들 가운데 가장 비범한 여인 중 하나입니다.\""]], [["“My dear!”", "\"여보!\""], ["remonstrated Mrs Toots.", "투츠 부인이 나무라듯 말했다."]], [["“Upon my word and honour she is,”", "\"맹세코 정말 그렇습니다,\""], ["said Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“I—I assure you, Mrs Blimber, she’s a most extraordinary woman.”", "\"정말이지—정말이지, 블림버 부인, 이 사람은 더할 나위 없이 비범한 여인입니다.\""]], [["Mrs Toots laughed merrily, and Mrs Blimber led her to Cornelia.", "투츠 부인이 명랑하게 웃었고, 블림버 부인은 그녀를 코르넬리아에게로 데려갔다."], ["Mr Toots having paid his respects in that direction and having saluted his old preceptor, who said, in allusion to his conjugal state, “Well, Toots, well, Toots!", "투츠 씨는 그쪽을 향해 인사를 올리고, 자기의 옛 스승에게 경례를 했는데, 스승은 그의 부부 생활을 두고 이렇게 말했다. \"허, 투츠, 허, 투츠!"], ["So you are one of us, are you, Toots?”—retired with Mr Feeder, B.A., into a window.", "그래, 자네도 우리 같은 사람이 되었군그래, 응, 투츠?\" 그러고서 투츠 씨는 피더 군 학사와 함께 창가로 물러났다."]], [["Mr Feeder, B.A., being in great spirits, made a spar at Mr Toots, and tapped him skilfully with the back of his hand on the breastbone.", "피더 군 학사는 기분이 한껏 들떠서, 투츠 씨에게 권투하는 시늉을 하더니 손등으로 그의 가슴뼈를 솜씨 좋게 톡 쳤다."]], [["“Well, old Buck!”", "\"이봐, 친구!\""], ["said Mr Feeder with a laugh.", "피더 군이 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Well!", "\"이야!"], ["Here we are!", "우리도 드디어!"], ["Taken in and done for.", "걸려들어서 끝장났구먼."], ["Eh?”", "그렇지?\""]], [["“Feeder,”", "\"피더,\""], ["returned Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 응수했다."], ["“I give you joy.", "\"축하하네."], ["If you’re as—as—as perfectly blissful in a matrimonial life, as I am myself, you’ll have nothing to desire.”", "자네가 나만큼이나—나만큼이나—나만큼이나 부부 생활에서 더없이 행복하다면, 더 바랄 게 없을 걸세.\""]], [["“I don’t forget my old friends, you see,”", "\"보다시피 나는 옛 친구들을 잊지 않아,\""], ["said Mr Feeder.", "피더 군이 말했다."], ["“I ask em to my wedding, Toots.”", "\"내 혼례에 부른 거잖나, 투츠.\""]], [["“Feeder,”", "\"피더,\""], ["replied Mr Toots gravely, “the fact is, that there were several circumstances which prevented me from communicating with you until after my marriage had been solemnised.", "투츠 씨가 진지하게 응수했다, \"사실은 말이지, 내 혼례가 치러질 때까지는 자네에게 연락할 수 없게 만든 몇 가지 사정이 있었네."], ["In the first place, I had made a perfect brute of myself to you, on the subject of Miss Dombey; and I felt that if you were asked to any wedding of mine, you would naturally expect that it was with Miss Dombey, which involved explanations, that upon my word and honour, at that crisis, would have knocked me completely over.", "첫째로, 나는 돔비 양에 관한 일로 자네에게 완전히 짐승처럼 굴었지. 그래서 자네가 내 혼례에 초대받으면 당연히 돔비 양과 하는 혼례인 줄로 기대할 거라고 느꼈고, 그러면 설명이 필요해질 텐데, 맹세코 그 시점에서는 그 설명이 나를 완전히 무너뜨렸을 걸세."], ["In the second place, our wedding was strictly private; there being nobody present but one friend of myself and Mrs Toots’s, who is a Captain in—I don’t exactly know in what,”", "둘째로, 우리 혼례는 엄격하게 비공개였네. 참석한 사람은 나와 투츠 부인의 친구 한 사람뿐이었는데, 그는—어디 소속인지는 정확히 모르겠지만—함장이라네.\""], ["said Mr Toots, “but it’s of no consequence.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"하지만 그건 중요한 게 아니지."], ["I hope, Feeder, that in writing a statement of what had occurred before Mrs Toots and myself went abroad upon our foreign tour, I fully discharged the offices of friendship.”", "피더, 투츠 부인과 내가 해외 여행을 떠나기 전에 무슨 일이 있었는지 적어 보냈으니, 우정의 도리는 충분히 다했기를 바라네.\""]], [["“Toots, my boy,”", "\"투츠, 이 친구야,\""], ["said Mr Feeder, shaking his hands, “I was joking.”", "피더 군이 그의 두 손을 흔들며 말했다, \"농담이었어.\""]], [["“And now, Feeder,”", "\"그건 그렇고, 피더,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “I should be glad to know what you think of my union.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"내 혼인을 자네가 어떻게 생각하는지 들려준다면 기쁘겠네.\""]], [["“Capital!”", "\"훌륭해!\""], ["returned Mr Feeder.", "피더 군이 응수했다."]], [["“You think it’s capital, do you, Feeder?”", "\"훌륭하다고 생각한단 말이지, 피더?\""], ["said Mr Toots solemnly.", "투츠 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["“Then how capital must it be to Me!", "\"그렇다면 그게 내게는 얼마나 훌륭하겠나!"], ["For you can never know what an extraordinary woman that is.”", "자네는 저 사람이 얼마나 비범한 여인인지 결코 알 수 없을 테니까.\""]], [["Mr Feeder was willing to take it for granted.", "피더 군은 기꺼이 그렇다고 인정하려 했다."], ["But Mr Toots shook his head, and wouldn’t hear of that being possible.", "그러나 투츠 씨는 고개를 내저으며, 그것이 가능하다는 말은 들으려고 하지 않았다."]], [["“You see,”", "\"있잖나,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “what I wanted in a wife was—in short, was sense.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"내가 아내에게 바란 것은—한마디로, 지각이었네."], ["Money, Feeder, I had.", "돈은, 피더, 내게 있었지."], ["Sense I—I had not, particularly.”", "지각은—나는—특별히 없었어.\""]], [["Mr Feeder murmured, “Oh, yes, you had, Toots!”", "피더 군이 \"아, 아니, 자네도 있었지, 투츠!\" 하고 중얼거렸다."], ["But Mr Toots said:", "그러나 투츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["“No, Feeder, I had not.", "\"아니, 피더, 나는 없었네."], ["Why should I disguise it?", "왜 감추겠나? 없었어."], ["I had not. I knew that sense was There,”", "나는 지각이라는 게 바로 저기 있다는 걸 알았지.\""], ["said Mr Toots, stretching out his hand towards his wife, “in perfect heaps.", "투츠 씨가 자기 아내 쪽으로 손을 뻗으며 말했다, \"그것도 잔뜩."], ["I had no relation to object or be offended, on the score of station; for I had no relation.", "신분 문제로 반대하거나 기분 상해 할 친척도 내겐 없었네. 친척이라곤 없었으니까."], ["I have never had anybody belonging to me but my guardian, and him, Feeder, I have always considered as a Pirate and a Corsair.", "내겐 후견인 말고는 내게 딸린 사람이 평생 아무도 없었는데, 그 사람을, 피더, 나는 늘 해적이자 바다도둑이라고 여겨 왔지."], ["Therefore, you know it was not likely,”", "그러니, 알겠지만,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “that I should take his opinion.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"내가 그 사람의 의견을 받아들일 리는 없었지.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"그렇지,\""], ["said Mr Feeder.", "피더 군이 말했다."]], [["“Accordingly,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["resumed Mr Toots, “I acted on my own.", "투츠 씨가 이어 말했다, \"나는 내 뜻대로 행동했네."], ["Bright was the day on which I did so!", "그렇게 한 날은 빛나는 날이었지!"], ["Feeder!", "피더!"], ["Nobody but myself can tell what the capacity of that woman’s mind is.", "저 여인의 지력이 어느 정도인지는 나밖에 모를 걸세."], ["If ever the Rights of Women, and all that kind of thing, are properly attended to, it will be through her powerful intellect—Susan, my dear!”", "만일 언젠가 여성의 권리, 그런 부류의 모든 것이 제대로 다뤄진다면, 그건 저 사람의 강력한 지성을 통해서일 걸세—수전, 여보!\""], ["said Mr Toots, looking abruptly out of the windows “pray do not exert yourself!”", "투츠 씨가 갑자기 창밖을 내다보며 말했다, \"제발 무리하지 마!\""]], [["“My dear,”", "\"여보,\""], ["said Mrs Toots, “I was only talking.”", "투츠 부인이 말했다, \"그냥 얘기하고 있었을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“But, my love,”", "\"하지만, 여보,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “pray do not exert yourself.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"제발 무리하지 마."], ["You really must be careful.", "정말 조심해야 돼."], ["Do not, my dear Susan, exert yourself.", "그러지 마, 수전, 무리하지 마."], ["She’s so easily excited,”", "이 사람은 너무 쉽게 흥분해서,\""], ["said Mr Toots, apart to Mrs Blimber, “and then she forgets the medical man altogether.”", "투츠 씨가 블림버 부인에게만 들리도록 말했다, \"그러고 나면 의사 선생 말은 까맣게 잊어버린답니다.\""]], [["Mrs Blimber was impressing on Mrs Toots the necessity of caution, when Mr Feeder, B.A., offered her his arm, and led her down to the carriages that were waiting to go to church.", "블림버 부인이 투츠 부인에게 조심해야 한다고 일러 주고 있을 때, 피더 군 학사가 그녀에게 팔을 내밀어, 교회로 가려고 기다리고 있는 마차들로 데려갔다."], ["Doctor Blimber escorted Mrs Toots.", "블림버 박사는 투츠 부인을 모셨다."], ["Mr Toots escorted the fair bride, around whose lambent spectacles two gauzy little bridesmaids fluttered like moths.", "투츠 씨는 아리따운 신부를 모셨는데, 그녀의 반짝이는 안경 둘레로 얇은 드레스를 입은 두 작은 들러리들이 나방처럼 팔랑거렸다."], ["Mr Feeder’s brother, Mr Alfred Feeder, M.A., had already gone on, in advance, to assume his official functions.", "피더 군의 형제 알프레드 피더 석사는 이미 앞서가서 자기의 공식 직무를 맡고 있었다."]], [["The ceremony was performed in an admirable manner.", "예식은 훌륭하게 거행되었다."], ["Cornelia, with her crisp little curls, “went in,” as the Chicken might have said, with great composure; and Doctor Blimber gave her away, like a man who had quite made up his mind to it.", "곱슬곱슬 잔머리를 한 코르넬리아는, 치킨이라면 말했을 법한 표현대로, 아주 침착하게 \"입장했고\", 블림버 박사는 단단히 마음을 굳힌 사람처럼 그녀를 신랑에게 넘겨주었다."], ["The gauzy little bridesmaids appeared to suffer most.", "얇은 드레스를 입은 작은 들러리들이 가장 괴로워하는 것 같았다."], ["Mrs Blimber was affected, but gently so; and told the Reverend Mr Alfred Feeder, M.A., on the way home, that if she could only have seen Cicero in his retirement at Tusculum, she would not have had a wish, now, ungratified.", "블림버 부인은 감동했으나 그저 가만히 그러했다. 그녀는 돌아오는 길에 알프레드 피더 신부 석사에게, 키케로가 투스쿨룸에 은거해 있는 모습을 볼 수만 있었다면 이제 더는 이루지 못한 소원이 하나도 없었을 거라고 말했다."]], [["There was a breakfast afterwards, limited to the same small party; at which the spirits of Mr Feeder, B.A., were tremendous, and so communicated themselves to Mrs Toots that Mr Toots was several times heard to observe, across the table, “My dear Susan, don’t exert yourself!” The best of it was, that Mr Toots felt it incumbent on him to make a speech; and in spite of a whole code of telegraphic dissuasions from Mrs Toots, appeared on his legs for the first time in his life.", "* 키케로: 로마의 정치가·문인으로, 투스쿨룸의 별장에 은거했다. 그 뒤에는 아침 식사가 있었는데, 똑같은 소규모 일행으로 한정되었다. 거기서 피더 군 학사는 어마어마하게 흥이 올랐고, 그것이 투츠 부인에게도 옮아붙어서, 투츠 씨가 식탁 너머로 \"여보, 수전, 무리하지 마!\" 하고 말하는 소리가 여러 번 들렸다. 그중 압권은, 투츠 씨가 연설을 하는 것이 자기 책무라고 느낀 점이었다. 그래서 투츠 부인의 온갖 전신 신호식 만류에도 불구하고, 그는 난생처음 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["“I really,”", "\"저는 정말이지,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “in this house, where whatever was done to me in the way of—of any mental confusion sometimes—which is of no consequence and I impute to nobody—I was always treated like one of Doctor Blimber’s family, and had a desk to myself for a considerable period—can—not—allow—my friend Feeder to be—”", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"이 집에서—이따금 어떤—어떤 정신의 혼란이라 할 만한 일이 제게 가해지긴 했지만, 그건 중요하지 않고 누구를 탓하지도 않습니다—언제나 블림버 박사 댁 식구처럼 대접받았고, 상당 기간 제 책상도 따로 가졌던 터라—제 친구 피더가—이렇게 되는 것을—그냥—두고—볼—수가—없습니다—\""]], [["Mrs Toots suggested “married.”", "투츠 부인이 \"결혼하는 것을\" 하고 거들었다."]], [["“It may not be inappropriate to the occasion, or altogether uninteresting,”", "\"이 자리에 어울리지 않거나 아주 흥미가 없지는 않을 것 같습니다만,\""], ["said Mr Toots with a delighted face, “to observe that my wife is a most extraordinary woman, and would do this much better than myself—allow my friend Feeder to be married—especially to—”", "투츠 씨가 기뻐하는 얼굴로 말했다, \"제 아내가 더없이 비범한 여인이고, 저보다 이걸 훨씬 잘했으리라는 점을 말씀드리겠습니다—제 친구 피더가 결혼하는 것을—특히—\""]], [["Mrs Toots suggested “to Miss Blimber.”", "투츠 부인이 \"블림버 양과\" 하고 거들었다."]], [["“To Mrs Feeder, my love!”", "\"피더 부인과 말이오, 여보!\""], ["said Mr Toots, in a subdued tone of private discussion: ‘“whom God hath joined,’ you know, ‘let no man’—don’t you know?", "투츠 씨가 사적인 의논을 하듯 낮은 어조로 말했다. \"'하느님이 짝지어 주신 것을', 알다시피, '사람이'—알지 않소?"], ["I cannot allow my friend Feeder to be married—especially to Mrs Feeder—without proposing their—their—Toasts; and may,”", "저는 제 친구 피더가 결혼하는 것을—특히 피더 부인과 결혼하는 것을—그분들을 위한—그분들을 위한—건배를 제의하지 않고서는 그냥 둘 수가 없습니다."], ["said Mr Toots, fixing his eyes on his wife, as if for inspiration in a high flight, “may the torch of Hymen be the beacon of joy, and may the flowers we have this day strewed in their path, be the—the banishers of—of gloom!”", "그러니,\" 투츠 씨가 마치 드높은 비상에 필요한 영감을 구하듯 아내에게 눈을 고정한 채 말했다, \"혼인의 횃불이 기쁨의 봉화가 되기를, 그리고 오늘 우리가 그분들의 길에 뿌린 꽃들이—그—우울을—몰아내는 것이—되기를 빕니다!\""]], [["Doctor Blimber, who had a taste for metaphor, was pleased with this, and said, “Very good, Toots!", "은유를 좋아하는 블림버 박사가 이 말에 흡족하여 \"아주 좋아, 투츠!"], ["Very well said, indeed, Toots!”", "정말 잘 말했네, 투츠!\""], ["and nodded his head and patted his hands.", "하고는 고개를 끄덕이며 손뼉을 쳤다."], ["Mr Feeder made in reply, a comic speech chequered with sentiment.", "피더 군이 답례로 감상이 섞인 익살스러운 연설을 했다."], ["Mr Alfred Feeder, M.A., was afterwards very happy on Doctor and Mrs Blimber; Mr Feeder, B.A., scarcely less so, on the gauzy little bridesmaids.", "그 뒤 알프레드 피더 석사는 블림버 박사 부부를 두고 무척 기분 좋게 말했고, 피더 군 학사는 그에 못지않게 얇은 드레스를 입은 작은 들러리들을 두고 그러했다."], ["Doctor Blimber then, in a sonorous voice, delivered a few thoughts in the pastoral style, relative to the rushes among which it was the intention of himself and Mrs Blimber to dwell, and the bee that would hum around their cot.", "그러자 블림버 박사가 낭랑한 목소리로, 자기와 블림버 부인이 거하려는 골풀밭과 그들의 오두막 둘레를 윙윙 맴돌 벌에 관하여 목가적인 양식으로 몇 가지 생각을 펼쳤다."], ["Shortly after which, as the Doctor’s eyes were twinkling in a remarkable manner, and his son-in-law had already observed that time was made for slaves, and had inquired whether Mrs Toots sang, the discreet Mrs Blimber dissolved the sitting, and sent Cornelia away, very cool and comfortable, in a post-chaise, with the man of her heart.", "그 직후, 박사의 두 눈이 유난히 반짝이고, 그의 사위가 이미 시간이란 노예를 위해 만들어진 거라고 평하면서 투츠 부인이 노래를 부르느냐고 물어 왔으므로, 신중한 블림버 부인이 모임을 파하고 코르넬리아를 떠나보냈다. 코르넬리아는 더없이 차분하고 편안하게, 마음의 임과 함께 역마차에 올랐다."]], [["Mr and Mrs Toots withdrew to the Bedford (Mrs Toots had been there before in old times, under her maiden name of Nipper), and there found a letter, which it took Mr Toots such an enormous time to read, that Mrs Toots was frightened.", "투츠 부부는 베드퍼드 호텔로 물러났다(투츠 부인은 옛날 처녀 시절 니퍼라는 이름으로 그곳에 와 본 적이 있었다). 그리고 거기서 편지 한 통을 발견했는데, 투츠 씨가 그것을 읽는 데 어찌나 엄청난 시간이 걸렸던지 투츠 부인이 겁을 먹었다."]], [["“My dear Susan,”", "\"여보, 수전,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “fright is worse than exertion.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"겁먹는 건 무리하는 것보다 더 나빠."], ["Pray be calm!”", "제발 진정해!\""]], [["“Who is it from?”", "\"누구한테서 온 거예요?\""], ["asked Mrs Toots.", "투츠 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Why, my love,”", "\"아니, 여보,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “it’s from Captain Gills.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"길스 함장한테서야."], ["Do not excite yourself.", "흥분하지 마."], ["Walters and Miss Dombey are expected home!”", "월터스와 돔비 양이 귀국한다는군!\""]], [["“My dear,”", "\"여보,\""], ["said Mrs Toots, raising herself quickly from the sofa, very pale, “don’t try to deceive me, for it’s no use, they’re come home—I see it plainly in your face!”", "투츠 부인이 소파에서 재빨리 몸을 일으키며, 새하얗게 질린 채 말했다, \"날 속이려 하지 마요, 소용없으니까. 돌아오신 거죠—당신 얼굴에 환히 다 보여요!\""]], [["“She’s a most extraordinary woman!”", "\"이 사람은 더없이 비범한 여인이야!\""], ["exclaimed Mr Toots, in rapturous admiration.", "투츠 씨가 황홀한 감탄에 잠겨 외쳤다."], ["“You’re perfectly right, my love, they have come home.", "\"당신 말이 완벽하게 맞아, 여보, 그분들이 돌아왔어."], ["Miss Dombey has seen her father, and they are reconciled!”", "돔비 양이 아버지를 만났고, 두 분이 화해했어!\""]], [["“Reconciled!”", "\"화해했다고요!\""], ["cried Mrs Toots, clapping her hands.", "투츠 부인이 손뼉을 치며 외쳤다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"여보,\""], ["said Mr Toots; “pray do not exert yourself.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"제발 무리하지 마."], ["Do remember the medical man!", "의사 선생을 꼭 기억해!"], ["Captain Gills says—at least he don’t say, but I imagine, from what I can make out, he means—that Miss Dombey has brought her unfortunate father away from his old house, to one where she and Walters are living; that he is lying very ill there—supposed to be dying; and that she attends upon him night and day.”", "길스 함장 말로는—적어도 그렇게 말한 건 아니지만, 내가 헤아려 낼 수 있는 바로는, 그가 뜻하는 건—돔비 양이 불운한 아버지를 옛집에서 모셔 나와, 자기와 월터스가 사는 집으로 옮겼다는 거야. 그 댁에서 그분이 몹시 앓아누워 있는데—임종이 가깝다고 여겨지고—밤낮으로 그녀가 곁에서 간호한다는 거지.\""]], [["Mrs Toots began to cry quite bitterly.", "투츠 부인이 몹시 서럽게 울기 시작했다."]], [["“My dearest Susan,”", "\"내 사랑하는 수전,\""], ["replied Mr Toots, “do, do, if you possibly can, remember the medical man!", "투츠 씨가 응수했다, \"제발, 제발, 할 수 있는 한, 의사 선생을 기억해!"], ["If you can’t, it’s of no consequence—but do endeavour to!”", "못 하겠으면, 그건 중요하지 않으니까—그래도 꼭 애써 봐!\""]], [["His wife, with her old manner suddenly restored, so pathetically entreated him to take her to her precious pet, her little mistress, her own darling, and the like, that Mr Toots, whose sympathy and admiration were of the strongest kind, consented from his very heart of hearts; and they agreed to depart immediately, and present themselves in answer to the Captain’s letter.", "그의 아내는 옛 태도가 문득 되살아나서, 자기의 소중한 아가, 자기의 어린 아씨, 자기의 사랑하는 분에게 데려가 달라고 어찌나 애처롭게 애원하던지, 그런 식의 온갖 말로 빌어 대니, 동정심과 감탄이 누구보다 강렬한 투츠 씨는 진심으로 마음 깊은 곳에서 응낙했다. 그래서 그들은 즉시 떠나, 함장의 편지에 답하여 모습을 드러내기로 합의했다."]], [["Now some hidden sympathies of things, or some coincidences, had that day brought the Captain himself (toward whom Mr and Mrs Toots were soon journeying) into the flowery train of wedlock; not as a principal, but as an accessory.", "그런데 사물들 사이의 어떤 숨은 인연, 혹은 어떤 우연의 일치가, 바로 그날 함장 본인을(투츠 부부가 곧 그를 향해 떠날 참이었다) 혼인이라는 꽃길의 행렬에 끌어들였다. 주역으로서가 아니라 조역으로서였다."], ["It happened accidentally, and thus:", "그것은 우연히 일어났는데, 사연은 이러했다."]], [["The Captain, having seen Florence and her baby for a moment, to his unbounded content, and having had a long talk with Walter, turned out for a walk; feeling it necessary to have some solitary meditation on the changes of human affairs, and to shake his glazed hat profoundly over the fall of Mr Dombey, for whom the generosity and simplicity of his nature were awakened in a lively manner. The Captain would have been very low, indeed, on the unhappy gentleman’s account, but for the recollection of the baby; which afforded him such intense satisfaction whenever it arose, that he laughed aloud as he went along the street, and, indeed, more than once, in a sudden impulse of joy, threw up his glazed hat and caught it again; much to the amazement of the spectators. The rapid alternations of light and shade to which these two conflicting subjects of reflection exposed the Captain, were so very trying to his spirits, that he felt a long walk necessary to his composure; and as there is a great deal in the influence of harmonious associations, he chose, for the scene of this walk, his old neighbourhood, down among the mast, oar, and block makers, ship-biscuit bakers, coal-whippers, pitch-kettles, sailors, canals, docks, swing-bridges, and other soothing objects.", "함장은 플로렌스와 그녀의 아기를 잠시나마 보고 한없이 흐뭇해한 뒤, 월터와 긴 이야기를 나누고 나서 산책을 나섰다. 인간사의 변천을 두고 홀로 명상에 잠길 필요를 느꼈고, 돔비 씨의 몰락을 두고 자기의 번들거리는 모자를 깊이 흔들 필요를 느꼈기 때문이었다. 돔비 씨에 대해서는, 그의 천성의 너그러움과 순박함이 생생하게 일깨워진 터였다. 함장은 그 불행한 신사 때문에 정말이지 몹시 풀이 죽었을 터였으나, 아기 생각이 떠오르면 그렇지 않았다. 그 생각은 떠오를 때마다 그에게 어찌나 강렬한 만족을 주었던지, 그는 길을 걸으며 소리 내어 웃었고, 실로 한 번 이상, 갑작스러운 기쁨의 충동에 번들거리는 모자를 공중에 던졌다가 다시 받아 들곤 하여, 구경꾼들을 적잖이 놀라게 했다. 이 두 가지 상반된 명상거리가 함장에게 안긴 명암의 빠른 교차는 그의 마음에 어찌나 힘겨운 시련이던지, 그는 마음을 가라앉히기 위해 긴 산책이 필요하다고 느꼈다. 그리고 조화로운 연상의 영향에는 큰 힘이 있는 법이므로, 그는 이 산책의 무대로 자기 옛 동네를 골랐다. 돛대며 노며 도르래 만드는 이들, 배 비스킷 굽는 이들, 석탄 부리는 인부들, 역청 끓이는 가마들, 뱃사람들, 운하들, 부두들, 도개교들, 그 밖에 마음을 달래 주는 온갖 풍경 속으로."]], [["These peaceful scenes, and particularly the region of Limehouse Hole and thereabouts, were so influential in calming the Captain, that he walked on with restored tranquillity, and was, in fact, regaling himself, under his breath, with the ballad of Lovely Peg, when, on turning a corner, he was suddenly transfixed and rendered speechless by a triumphant procession that he beheld advancing towards him.", "이 평화로운 풍경들, 특히 라임하우스 홀과 그 일대는 함장의 마음을 가라앉히는 데 어찌나 효험이 있던지, 그는 평정을 되찾아 걸어갔고, 사실 숨죽인 소리로 '어여쁜 페그'의 발라드를 흥얼거리며 즐기고 있었다. 그런데 길모퉁이를 도는 순간, 그는 자기를 향해 다가오는 의기양양한 행렬을 보고는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 굳어져 말을 잃었다."]], [["This awful demonstration was headed by that determined woman Mrs MacStinger, who, preserving a countenance of inexorable resolution, and wearing conspicuously attached to her obdurate bosom a stupendous watch and appendages, which the Captain recognised at a glance as the property of Bunsby, conducted under her arm no other than that sagacious mariner; he, with the distraught and melancholy visage of a captive borne into a foreign land, meekly resigning himself to her will.", "이 무시무시한 광경의 선두에는 그 단호한 여인 맥스팅거 부인이 서 있었다. 그녀는 추호의 흔들림 없는 결의의 표정을 지킨 채, 자기의 완강한 가슴팍에 어마어마한 시계와 그 부속물들을 보란 듯이 매달고 있었는데, 함장은 그것이 번스비의 물건임을 한눈에 알아보았다."], ["Behind them appeared the young MacStingers, in a body, exulting.", "그녀는 다름 아닌 그 현명한 뱃사람을 팔짱에 끼고 인도하고 있었고, 그는 낯선 땅으로 끌려가는 포로의 넋 나가고 우울한 얼굴로, 그녀의 뜻에 고분고분 자신을 내맡기고 있었다."], ["Behind them, two ladies of a terrible and steadfast aspect, leading between them a short gentleman in a tall hat, who likewise exulted.", "그들 뒤로는 어린 맥스팅거들이 떼를 지어 의기양양하게 나타났다. 그 뒤로는 무섭고 굳센 인상의 두 부인이, 키 작은 신사 하나를 가운데 끼고 인도해 오고 있었는데, 그 신사는 높은 모자를 쓰고 역시 의기양양해했다."], ["In the wake, appeared Bunsby’s boy, bearing umbrellas.", "행렬 맨 뒤에는 우산을 든 번스비의 소년이 나타났다."], ["The whole were in good marching order; and a dreadful smartness that pervaded the party would have sufficiently announced, if the intrepid countenances of the ladies had been wanting, that it was a procession of sacrifice, and that the victim was Bunsby.", "일행 전체가 잘 갖춰진 행군 대형을 이루었고, 그 무리에 감도는 무시무시한 멋스러움은, 설령 부인들의 담대한 표정이 없었더라도, 이것이 제물을 바치는 행렬이며 그 제물이 번스비임을 충분히 알려 주었을 것이다."]], [["The first impulse of the Captain was to run away.", "함장의 첫 충동은 달아나는 것이었다."], ["This also appeared to be the first impulse of Bunsby, hopeless as its execution must have proved.", "이것은 번스비의 첫 충동이기도 한 듯했는데, 그 실행은 부질없을 것이 뻔했다."], ["But a cry of recognition proceeding from the party, and Alexander MacStinger running up to the Captain with open arms, the Captain struck.", "그러나 일행 쪽에서 알아보는 외침이 터져 나오고, 알렉산더 맥스팅거가 두 팔을 벌리고 함장에게 달려오니, 함장은 깃발을 내렸다."]], [["“Well, Cap’en Cuttle!”", "\"이런, 커틀 함장님!\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger.", "맥스팅거 부인이 말했다."], ["“This is indeed a meeting!", "\"이거야말로 정말 뜻밖의 만남이군요!"], ["I bear no malice now, Cap’en Cuttle—you needn’t fear that I’m a going to cast any reflections.", "이제 저는 아무 원한도 없어요, 커틀 함장님—제가 무슨 트집을 잡을까 봐 걱정하실 것 없어요."], ["I hope to go to the altar in another spirit.”", "저는 다른 마음으로 제단에 나아가기를 바라니까요.\""], ["Here Mrs MacStinger paused, and drawing herself up, and inflating her bosom with a long breath, said, in allusion to the victim, “My “usband, Cap’en Cuttle!”", "여기서 맥스팅거 부인은 잠시 멈추더니, 몸을 곧추세우고 긴 숨으로 가슴을 부풀리며, 제물을 가리켜 말했다. \"제 '남편'이옵니다, 커틀 함장님!\""]], [["The abject Bunsby looked neither to the right nor to the left, nor at his bride, nor at his friend, but straight before him at nothing.", "비굴해진 번스비는 오른쪽도 왼쪽도, 자기 신부도 자기 친구도 보지 않고, 그저 똑바로 앞을, 아무것도 아닌 곳을 응시했다."], ["The Captain putting out his hand, Bunsby put out his; but, in answer to the Captain’s greeting, spake no word.", "함장이 손을 내미니 번스비도 손을 내밀었으나, 함장의 인사에 답하여 한마디 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Cap’en Cuttle,”", "\"커틀 함장님,\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger, “if you would wish to heal up past animosities, and to see the last of your friend, my “usband, as a single person, we should be “appy of your company to chapel.", "맥스팅거 부인이 말했다, \"지난 적의를 풀고 싶으시다면, 그리고 제 '남편'인 함장님 친구가 독신으로서 마지막인 모습을 보고 싶으시다면, 저희와 함께 예배당에 와 주시면 '기쁘'겠어요."], ["Here is a lady here,”", "여기 한 부인이 계신데,\""], ["said Mrs MacStinger, turning round to the more intrepid of the two, “my bridesmaid, that will be glad of your protection, Cap’en Cuttle.”", "맥스팅거 부인이 둘 중 더 담대한 쪽을 향해 돌아서며 말했다, \"제 들러리예요, 함장님의 보호를 받으면 기뻐할 거예요, 커틀 함장님.\""]], [["The short gentleman in the tall hat, who it appeared was the husband of the other lady, and who evidently exulted at the reduction of a fellow creature to his own condition, gave place at this, and resigned the lady to Captain Cuttle.", "높은 모자를 쓴 키 작은 신사는, 알고 보니 다른 부인의 남편이었는데, 한 동료 인간이 자기와 같은 처지로 전락하는 것을 분명 고소해하는 듯, 이 말에 자리를 비켜 주며 그 부인을 커틀 함장에게 넘겼다."], ["The lady immediately seized him, and, observing that there was no time to lose, gave the word, in a strong voice, to advance.", "부인은 즉시 그를 붙들고는, 지체할 시간이 없다고 말하며, 우렁찬 목소리로 전진하라는 호령을 내렸다."]], [["The Captain’s concern for his friend, not unmingled, at first, with some concern for himself—for a shadowy terror that he might be married by violence, possessed him, until his knowledge of the service came to his relief, and remembering the legal obligation of saying, “I will,”", "함장이 자기 친구를 걱정하는 마음은, 처음에는 자기 자신에 대한 약간의 걱정과 뒤섞이지 않을 수 없었다—혹시 강제로 결혼당할지 모른다는 어렴풋한 공포가 그를 사로잡았던 것이다."], ["he felt himself personally safe so long as he resolved, if asked any question, distinctly to reply “I won’t”—threw him into a profuse perspiration; and rendered him, for a time, insensible to the movements of the procession, of which he now formed a feature, and to the conversation of his fair companion.", "그러다 예식 절차에 대한 자기 지식이 그를 구해 주었고, \"예, 하겠습니다\"라고 말해야 하는 법적 의무를 떠올린 그는, 무슨 질문을 받든 또렷이 \"안 하겠습니다\"라고 대답하기로 작정하는 한 자기는 안전하다고 느꼈다—이 모든 것이 그를 땀투성이로 만들었고, 한동안은 그가 이제 한 부분을 이루게 된 그 행렬의 움직임도, 자기의 아리따운 동행이 하는 이야기도 알아차리지 못하게 만들었다."], ["But as he became less agitated, he learnt from this lady that she was the widow of a Mr Bokum, who had held an employment in the Custom House; that she was the dearest friend of Mrs MacStinger, whom she considered a pattern for her sex; that she had often heard of the Captain, and now hoped he had repented of his past life; that she trusted Mr Bunsby knew what a blessing he had gained, but that she feared men seldom did know what such blessings were, until they had lost them; with more to the same purpose.", "그러나 마음이 덜 동요하게 되자, 그는 이 부인에게서 다음을 알게 되었다. 그녀는 세관에 근무했던 보컴 씨의 미망인이며, 맥스팅거 부인의 가장 절친한 벗으로 그녀를 자기 성별의 귀감으로 여기고 있고, 함장에 대해서는 자주 들어 왔으며 이제 그가 지난 삶을 뉘우쳤기를 바라고, 번스비 씨가 자기가 얼마나 큰 복을 얻었는지 알고 있기를 믿지만, 남자들이란 그런 복을 잃기 전에는 그것이 무엇인지 좀처럼 알지 못하지 않을까 두렵다는 등, 그 비슷한 이야기를 더 늘어놓았다."]], [["All this time, the Captain could not but observe that Mrs Bokum kept her eyes steadily on the bridegroom, and that whenever they came near a court or other narrow turning which appeared favourable for flight, she was on the alert to cut him off if he attempted escape.", "이러는 내내 함장은, 보컴 부인이 신랑에게서 줄곧 눈을 떼지 않는다는 것과, 골목길이나 달아나기에 알맞아 보이는 좁은 모퉁이에 가까워질 때마다 그녀가 그가 탈출을 시도하면 길을 끊으려고 바짝 경계한다는 것을 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The other lady, too, as well as her husband, the short gentleman with the tall hat, were plainly on guard, according to a preconcerted plan; and the wretched man was so secured by Mrs MacStinger, that any effort at self-preservation by flight was rendered futile.", "다른 부인 역시 자기 남편인 높은 모자 쓴 키 작은 신사와 마찬가지로, 미리 짜 둔 계획에 따라 분명히 경계 태세였다. 그리고 그 가련한 남자는 맥스팅거 부인에게 어찌나 단단히 붙들려 있던지, 달아나서 살아남으려는 어떤 노력도 부질없게 되어 버렸다."], ["This, indeed, was apparent to the mere populace, who expressed their perception of the fact by jeers and cries; to all of which, the dread MacStinger was inflexibly indifferent, while Bunsby himself appeared in a state of unconsciousness.", "이는 실로 한낱 구경꾼들에게도 빤히 보였으니, 그들은 야유와 고함으로 그 사실에 대한 자기들의 통찰을 드러냈다. 그 모든 것에 무서운 맥스팅거는 요지부동 무관심했고, 번스비 자신은 아무것도 의식하지 못하는 상태인 듯했다."]], [["The Captain made many attempts to accost the philosopher, if only in a monosyllable or a signal; but always failed, in consequence of the vigilance of the guard, and the difficulty, at all times peculiar to Bunsby’s constitution, of having his attention aroused by any outward and visible sign whatever.", "함장은 단음절로든 신호로든 그 철학자에게 말을 걸어 보려고 여러 번 시도했으나, 경계병의 빈틈없는 감시 탓에, 그리고 번스비의 기질에 늘 따라붙는 특유의 난점—어떤 외적이고 가시적인 표시로도 그의 주의를 일깨우기 어렵다는 점—탓에 번번이 실패했다. 그렇게 그들은 예배당에 다다랐다."], ["Thus they approached the chapel, a neat whitewashed edifice, recently engaged by the Reverend Melchisedech Howler, who had consented, on very urgent solicitation, to give the world another two years of existence, but had informed his followers that, then, it must positively go.", "말끔하게 회칠한 건물로, 멜키세데크 하울러 신부가 최근에 빌린 곳이었다. 그는 간곡한 청을 받고서, 세상이 이 년 더 존속하도록 허락하기로 했으나, 그다음에는 세상이 반드시 끝나야 한다고 자기 신도들에게 일러둔 터였다."]], [["While the Reverend Melchisedech was offering up some extemporary orisons, the Captain found an opportunity of growling in the bridegroom’s ear:", "멜키세데크 신부가 즉흥 기도를 올리고 있는 동안, 함장은 신랑의 귀에 대고 으르렁거릴 기회를 잡았다."]], [["“What cheer, my lad, what cheer?”", "\"기운 좀 어떤가, 이 사람아, 기운 좀 어때?\""]], [["To which Bunsby replied, with a forgetfulness of the Reverend Melchisedech, which nothing but his desperate circumstances could have excused:", "이에 번스비는 멜키세데크 신부의 존재 따위는 잊은 채—그의 절박한 처지가 아니고서는 무엇으로도 용서받을 수 없을 만큼—대답했다."]], [["“D——d bad,”", "\"빌어먹게 나빠.\""]], [["“Jack Bunsby,”", "\"잭 번스비,\""], ["whispered the Captain, “do you do this here, of your own free will?”", "함장이 속삭였다, \"자네 이걸 자네 자유 의지로 하는 건가?\""]], [["Mr Bunsby answered “No.”", "번스비 씨가 \"아니\" 하고 답했다."]], [["“Why do you do it, then, my lad?”", "\"그럼 왜 하는 건가, 이 사람아?\""], ["inquired the Captain, not unnaturally.", "함장이 자연스럽게 물었다."]], [["Bunsby, still looking, and always looking with an immovable countenance, at the opposite side of the world, made no reply.", "번스비는 여전히, 변함없는 표정으로 세상의 반대편을 응시한 채, 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Why not sheer off?”", "\"왜 줄행랑을 안 치나?\""], ["said the Captain.", "함장이 말했다."], ["“Eh?”", "\"응?\""], ["whispered Bunsby, with a momentary gleam of hope.", "번스비가 한순간 희망의 빛을 번득이며 속삭였다."]], [["“Sheer off,”", "\"줄행랑을 치게,\""], ["said the Captain.", "함장이 말했다."]], [["“Where’s the good?”", "\"무슨 소용이 있겠나?\""], ["retorted the forlorn sage.", "의기소침한 현자가 응수했다."], ["“She’d capter me agen.”", "\"또 날 붙잡고 말 텐데.\""]], [["“Try!”", "\"해 보게!\""], ["replied the Captain.", "함장이 응수했다."], ["“Cheer up!", "\"힘내!"], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Now’s your time.", "지금이 기회야."], ["Sheer off, Jack Bunsby!”", "줄행랑을 치게, 잭 번스비!\""]], [["Jack Bunsby, however, instead of profiting by the advice, said in a doleful whisper:", "그러나 잭 번스비는 그 충고를 따르기는커녕, 구슬픈 속삭임으로 말했다."]], [["“It all began in that there chest o’ yourn.", "\"이게 다 자네 그 궤짝에서 비롯됐어."], ["Why did I ever conwoy her into port that night?”", "그날 밤 내가 왜 그 여자를 항구로 데려다줬을까?\""]], [["“My lad,”", "\"이 사람아,\""], ["faltered the Captain, “I thought as you had come over her; not as she had come over you.", "함장이 더듬거렸다, \"나는 자네가 그 여자를 휘어잡은 줄 알았지, 그 여자가 자네를 휘어잡은 게 아니라."], ["A man as has got such opinions as you have!”", "자네처럼 그런 식견을 가진 사람이!\""]], [["Mr Bunsby merely uttered a suppressed groan.", "번스비 씨는 그저 억눌린 신음만 토해 냈다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"자!\""], ["said the Captain, nudging him with his elbow, “now’s your time!", "함장이 팔꿈치로 그를 쿡 찌르며 말했다, \"지금이 기회야!"], ["Sheer off!", "줄행랑을 치게!"], ["I’ll cover your retreat.", "내가 자네 퇴각을 엄호하지."], ["The time’s a flying.", "시간이 가고 있어."], ["Bunsby!", "번스비!"], ["It’s for liberty.", "자유를 위해서야."], ["Will you once?”", "한 번만 해 보겠나?\""]], [["Bunsby was immovable.", "번스비는 미동도 없었다."]], [["“Bunsby!”", "\"번스비!\""], ["whispered the Captain, “will you twice?”", "함장이 속삭였다, \"두 번째로 해 보겠나?\""]], [["Bunsby wouldn’t twice.", "번스비는 두 번째도 하지 않으려 했다."]], [["“Bunsby!”", "\"번스비!\""], ["urged the Captain, “it’s for liberty; will you three times?", "함장이 다그쳤다, \"자유를 위해서야. 세 번째로 해 보겠나?"], ["Now or never!”", "지금 아니면 영영 못 해!\""]], [["Bunsby didn’t then, and didn’t ever; for Mrs MacStinger immediately afterwards married him.", "번스비는 그때도 하지 않았고, 끝내 영영 하지 않았다. 맥스팅거 부인이 그 직후 곧바로 그와 결혼해 버렸기 때문이다."]], [["One of the most frightful circumstances of the ceremony to the Captain, was the deadly interest exhibited therein by Juliana MacStinger; and the fatal concentration of her faculties, with which that promising child, already the image of her parent, observed the whole proceedings.", "함장에게 이 예식에서 가장 끔찍했던 광경 중 하나는, 줄리아나 맥스팅거가 거기서 보인 섬뜩한 흥미와, 그 앞날이 촉망되는 아이—이미 자기 어미를 빼닮은—가 온 절차를 지켜보던 그 무서운 정신 집중이었다."], ["The Captain saw in this a succession of man-traps stretching out infinitely; a series of ages of oppression and coercion, through which the seafaring line was doomed.", "함장은 거기서, 끝없이 뻗어 나가는 일련의 덫을 보았다. 뱃사람의 혈통이 운명적으로 짊어진, 억압과 강제의 시대가 줄줄이 이어지는 것을 보았다."], ["It was a more memorable sight than the unflinching steadiness of Mrs Bokum and the other lady, the exultation of the short gentleman in the tall hat, or even the fell inflexibility of Mrs MacStinger.", "그것은 보컴 부인과 다른 부인의 흔들림 없는 굳건함이나, 높은 모자 쓴 키 작은 신사의 의기양양함, 심지어 맥스팅거 부인의 무시무시한 완강함보다도 더 잊지 못할 광경이었다. 어린 맥스팅거 사내아이들은 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 거의 이해하지 못했고 관심도 더더욱 없었다."], ["The Master MacStingers understood little of what was going on, and cared less; being chiefly engaged, during the ceremony, in treading on one another’s half-boots; but the contrast afforded by those wretched infants only set off and adorned the precocious woman in Juliana.", "그들은 예식 내내 주로 서로의 반장화를 밟는 데 몰두해 있었다. 그러나 그 한심한 아이들이 빚어낸 대조는, 줄리아나에게 깃든 조숙한 여인을 도리어 돋보이게 하고 장식해 줄 뿐이었다."], ["Another year or two, the Captain thought, and to lodge where that child was, would be destruction.", "일이 년만 더 지나면, 저 아이가 사는 곳에 머문다는 것은 파멸이리라고 함장은 생각했다."]], [["The ceremony was concluded by a general spring of the young family on Mr Bunsby, whom they hailed by the endearing name of father, and from whom they solicited half-pence.", "예식은 어린 식구들이 일제히 번스비 씨에게 달려드는 것으로 마무리되었는데, 그들은 그를 아버지라는 다정한 이름으로 부르며 그에게 동전을 졸라 댔다."], ["These gushes of affection over, the procession was about to issue forth again, when it was delayed for some little time by an unexpected transport on the part of Alexander MacStinger.", "이렇게 애정이 쏟아지는 일이 끝나고 행렬이 다시 출발하려는 참에, 알렉산더 맥스팅거의 뜻밖의 격정 탓에 잠시 지체되었다."], ["That dear child, it seemed, connecting a chapel with tombstones, when it was entered for any purpose apart from the ordinary religious exercises, could not be persuaded but that his mother was now to be decently interred, and lost to him for ever.", "그 귀여운 아이는, 보아하니 예배당이 평소의 종교 의식이 아닌 다른 목적으로 들어서는 곳일 때면 예배당과 묘비를 연결지어 생각했던 듯, 자기 어머니가 이제 점잖게 매장되어 자기에게서 영영 사라져 버린다고 굳게 믿었고 도무지 다른 말로는 설득되지 않았다."], ["In the anguish of this conviction, he screamed with astonishing force, and turned black in the face.", "이 확신의 고통 속에서 그는 놀라운 기세로 비명을 질러 댔고 얼굴이 시퍼레졌다."], ["However touching these marks of a tender disposition were to his mother, it was not in the character of that remarkable woman to permit her recognition of them to degenerate into weakness.", "다정한 성품을 보여 주는 이런 표시들이 어미에게 아무리 감동적이었다 한들, 그것을 알아주는 마음이 나약함으로 흐르도록 내버려 두는 것은 그 비범한 여인의 성격에 없는 일이었다."], ["Therefore, after vainly endeavouring to convince his reason by shakes, pokes, bawlings-out, and similar applications to his head, she led him into the air, and tried another method; which was manifested to the marriage party by a quick succession of sharp sounds, resembling applause, and subsequently, by their seeing Alexander in contact with the coolest paving-stone in the court, greatly flushed, and loudly lamenting.", "그리하여 그녀는 흔들고, 쿡쿡 찌르고, 고함치고, 그 비슷하게 그의 머리를 다루는 방법으로 그의 이성을 일깨우려 했으나 헛되자, 그를 바깥으로 데리고 나가 다른 방법을 써 보았다. 그것은 혼례 일행에게, 박수 소리를 닮은 날카로운 소리가 빠르게 연달아 들리고, 뒤이어 알렉산더가 안마당에서 가장 차가운 포석에 닿은 채 잔뜩 벌게진 얼굴로 큰 소리로 통탄하는 모습으로 드러났다."]], [["The procession being then in a condition to form itself once more, and repair to Brig Place, where a marriage feast was in readiness, returned as it had come; not without the receipt, by Bunsby, of many humorous congratulations from the populace on his recently-acquired happiness.", "그러고 나서 행렬이 다시 대형을 갖추어, 혼례 잔치가 차려진 브리그 플레이스로 갈 채비가 되자, 왔던 대로 돌아갔다. 가는 길에 번스비는 최근 얻은 행복을 두고 구경꾼들로부터 익살스러운 축하를 숱하게 받았다."], ["The Captain accompanied it as far as the house-door, but, being made uneasy by the gentler manner of Mrs Bokum, who, now that she was relieved from her engrossing duty—for the watchfulness and alacrity of the ladies sensibly diminished when the bridegroom was safely married—had greater leisure to show an interest in his behalf, there left it and the captive; faintly pleading an appointment, and promising to return presently.", "함장은 그 집 문 앞까지 동행했으나, 보컴 부인의 한결 부드러워진 태도에 불안해진 나머지—이제 그녀는 자기에게 맡겨졌던 막중한 임무에서 풀려나(부인들의 경계심과 민첩함은 신랑이 무사히 결혼하자 눈에 띄게 줄어들었다) 그를 위해 관심을 보일 여유가 더 많아진 터였다—그 집과 포로를 거기 두고 자리를 떴다. 약속이 있다고 가냘프게 둘러대고는, 곧 돌아오겠다고 약속했다."], ["The Captain had another cause for uneasiness, in remorsefully reflecting that he had been the first means of Bunsby’s entrapment, though certainly without intending it, and through his unbounded faith in the resources of that philosopher.", "함장에게는 불안할 또 다른 이유가 있었으니, 비록 분명 그럴 뜻은 없었고 그 철학자의 능력에 대한 끝없는 믿음 때문이긴 했으나, 자기가 번스비를 덫에 빠뜨린 첫 번째 원인이었다는 것을 후회스럽게 곱씹었기 때문이다."]], [["To go back to old Sol Gills at the wooden Midshipman’s, and not first go round to ask how Mr Dombey was—albeit the house where he lay was out of London, and away on the borders of a fresh heath—was quite out of the Captain’s course.", "목조 항해사 가게에 있는 노인 솔 길스에게로 돌아가면서, 돔비 씨가 어떤지 먼저 들러 묻지 않는 것은—비록 그가 누워 있는 집이 런던 바깥에, 멀리 어느 싱그러운 황야의 경계에 있긴 했으나—함장의 행로에서 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["So he got a lift when he was tired, and made out the journey gaily.", "그래서 그는 지치면 수레를 얻어 타며, 즐겁게 그 길을 헤쳐 나갔다."]], [["The blinds were pulled down, and the house so quiet, that the Captain was almost afraid to knock; but listening at the door, he heard low voices within, very near it, and, knocking softly, was admitted by Mr Toots.", "블라인드가 내려져 있고 집이 어찌나 고요하던지, 함장은 노크하기조차 거의 겁이 났다. 그러나 문에 귀를 대 보니, 안에서 바로 가까이 낮은 목소리들이 들렸고, 가만히 두드리자 투츠 씨가 그를 들여보내 주었다."], ["Mr Toots and his wife had, in fact, just arrived there; having been at the Midshipman’s to seek him, and having there obtained the address.", "사실 투츠 씨와 그의 아내는 막 도착한 참이었다. 항해사 가게로 함장을 찾으러 갔다가, 거기서 주소를 알아낸 것이었다."]], [["They were not so recently arrived, but that Mrs Toots had caught the baby from somebody, taken it in her arms, and sat down on the stairs, hugging and fondling it.", "그렇게 막 도착한 것치고는, 투츠 부인은 어느새 누군가에게서 아기를 받아 안고 계단에 앉아, 아기를 품에 안고 어르고 있었다. 플로렌스가 그녀 곁에 몸을 굽히고 있었다."], ["Florence was stooping down beside her; and no one could have said which Mrs Toots was hugging and fondling most, the mother or the child, or which was the tenderer, Florence of Mrs Toots, or Mrs Toots of her, or both of the baby; it was such a little group of love and agitation.", "투츠 부인이 어미와 아이 중 어느 쪽을 더 안고 어르는지, 또 플로렌스가 투츠 부인을 더 애틋해하는지 투츠 부인이 플로렌스를 더 애틋해하는지, 아니면 둘 다 아기를 더 애틋해하는지, 아무도 가려낼 수 없었을 것이다. 그것은 그토록 사랑과 격정이 어린 작은 무리였다."]], [["“And is your Pa very ill, my darling dear Miss Floy?”", "\"그래, 아버님께서 많이 편찮으세요, 사랑하는 플로이 아씨?\""], ["asked Susan.", "수전이 물었다."]], [["“He is very, very ill,”", "\"정말, 정말 편찮으셔,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“But, Susan, dear, you must not speak to me as you used to speak.", "\"하지만, 수전, 얘, 너 옛날처럼 나한테 그렇게 말하면 안 돼."], ["And what’s this?”", "그런데 이게 뭐야?\""], ["said Florence, touching her clothes, in amazement.", "플로렌스가 그녀의 옷을 만지며 놀라서 말했다."], ["“Your old dress, dear?", "\"옛날 네 옷이잖아, 얘?"], ["Your old cap, curls, and all?”", "옛날 네 모자랑 잔머리랑 다?\""]], [["Susan burst into tears, and showered kisses on the little hand that had touched her so wonderingly.", "수전이 울음을 터뜨리며, 자기를 그토록 신기해하며 만져 준 그 작은 손에 입맞춤을 쏟아부었다."]], [["“My dear Miss Dombey,”", "\"돔비 양,\""], ["said Mr Toots, stepping forward, “I’ll explain.", "투츠 씨가 앞으로 나서며 말했다, \"제가 설명하지요."], ["She’s the most extraordinary woman.", "이 사람은 더없이 비범한 여인입니다."], ["There are not many to equal her!", "이 사람에 견줄 만한 이는 많지 않아요!"], ["She has always said—she said before we were married, and has said to this day—that whenever you came home, she’d come to you in no dress but the dress she used to serve you in, for fear she might seem strange to you, and you might like her less.", "이 사람은 늘 말해 왔지요—우리가 결혼하기 전부터, 그리고 오늘까지도 말해 왔습니다—아씨가 집에 돌아오시면, 아씨를 모실 때 입던 옷 말고 다른 옷차림으로는 아씨께 가지 않겠다고요. 자기가 낯설어 보여서 아씨가 자기를 덜 좋아하실까 봐서요."], ["I admire the dress myself,”", "저로 말하면 그 옷이,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “of all things.", "투츠 씨가 말했다, \"무엇보다도 좋습니다."], ["I adore her in it!", "그 옷을 입은 이 사람을 흠모하지요!"], ["My dear Miss Dombey, she’ll be your maid again, your nurse, all that she ever was, and more.", "돔비 양, 이 사람은 다시 아씨의 하녀가, 아씨의 유모가, 예전에 그랬던 모든 것이, 아니 그 이상이 되어 드릴 겁니다."], ["There’s no change in her.", "이 사람은 조금도 변하지 않았어요."], ["But, Susan, my dear,”", "그런데, 수전, 여보,\""], ["said Mr Toots, who had spoken with great feeling and high admiration, “all I ask is, that you’ll remember the medical man, and not exert yourself too much!”", "투츠 씨가 깊은 감정과 드높은 감탄을 담아 말하더니 덧붙였다, \"내가 바라는 건 단 하나, 당신이 의사 선생을 기억하고, 너무 무리하지 않는 것뿐이야!\""]], [["# CHAPTER LXI. Relenting", "# CHAPTER LXI. Relenting"]], [["Florence had need of help.", "플로렌스에게는 도움이 필요했다."], ["Her father’s need of it was sore, and made the aid of her old friend invaluable.", "아버지에게도 그 필요가 절실했고, 그래서 옛 친구의 도움이 더없이 귀중해졌다."], ["Death stood at his pillow.", "죽음이 그의 머리맡에 서 있었다."], ["A shade, already, of what he had been, shattered in mind, and perilously sick in body, he laid his weary head down on the bed his daughter’s hands prepared for him, and had never raised it since.", "한때의 그가 남긴 그림자에 지나지 않게, 정신은 무너지고 몸은 위태롭게 병든 채로, 그는 딸의 손이 마련해 준 침대에 지친 머리를 뉘었고, 그 뒤로 다시는 머리를 들지 못했다."]], [["She was always with him.", "딸은 늘 그의 곁에 있었다."], ["He knew her, generally; though, in the wandering of his brain, he often confused the circumstances under which he spoke to her.", "그는 대체로 딸을 알아보았다. 다만 머릿속이 혼미해지면, 자기가 딸에게 말을 건네는 상황을 흔히 뒤죽박죽으로 만들곤 했다."], ["Thus he would address her, sometimes, as if his boy were newly dead; and would tell her, that although he had said nothing of her ministering at the little bedside, yet he had seen it—he had seen it; and then would hide his face and sob, and put out his worn hand.", "그래서 때로는 마치 자기 아들이 갓 죽은 것처럼 딸에게 말을 건넸다. 그러고는 비록 그 작은 침대 곁에서 딸이 시중든 일을 자기가 입에 올리지는 않았지만, 그래도 자기는 그것을 보았노라고—보았노라고 딸에게 말하고는, 얼굴을 가리고 흐느끼며 야윈 손을 내밀곤 했다."], ["Sometimes he would ask her for herself.", "때로는 딸 본인에 대해 그녀를 찾기도 했다."], ["“Where is Florence?”", "\"플로렌스는 어디 있느냐?\""], ["“I am here, Papa, I am here.”", "\"여기 있어요, 아빠, 여기 있어요.\""], ["“I don’t know her!”", "\"나는 그 애를 모른다!\""], ["he would cry.", "그는 부르짖곤 했다."], ["“We have been parted so long, that I don’t know her!”", "\"우리가 너무 오래 떨어져 있어서, 나는 그 애를 모른다!\""], ["and then a staring dread would be upon him, until she could soothe his perturbation; and recall the tears she tried so hard, at other times, to dry.", "그러고는 딸이 그의 동요를 달래 주고, 다른 때라면 그토록 애써 닦아 내려던 눈물을 다시 불러낼 때까지, 멍한 두려움이 그를 사로잡곤 했다."]], [["He rambled through the scenes of his old pursuits—through many where Florence lost him as she listened—sometimes for hours. He would repeat that childish question, “What is money?”", "그는 옛날 자기가 좇던 일들의 장면들을 두서없이 헤매었다—그 가운데 많은 곳에서 플로렌스는 귀 기울여 듣다가 그를 놓쳐 버렸다—때로는 몇 시간씩."], ["and ponder on it, and think about it, and reason with himself, more or less connectedly, for a good answer; as if it had never been proposed to him until that moment.", "그는 그 어린아이 같은 물음을, \"돈이 뭐야?\"를 되풀이하고는, 그것을 곰곰 따지고, 그것에 대해 생각하고, 좋은 답을 찾으려 스스로와 다소 조리 있게 따져 보았다."], ["He would go on with a musing repetition of the title of his old firm twenty thousand times, and at every one of them, would turn his head upon his pillow.", "마치 바로 그 순간까지 한 번도 그런 물음을 받아 본 적이 없는 것처럼. 그는 자기 옛 회사의 이름을 이만 번이나 골똘히 되풀이하면서, 그 한 번 한 번마다 베개 위에서 머리를 돌리곤 했다."], ["He would count his children—one—two—stop, and go back, and begin again in the same way.", "그는 자기 자식들을 세어 보곤 했다—하나—둘—멈추고, 다시 돌아가, 똑같은 식으로 다시 시작했다."]], [["But this was when his mind was in its most distracted state.", "그러나 이것은 그의 정신이 가장 혼란스러운 상태에 빠졌을 때의 일이었다."], ["In all the other phases of its illness, and in those to which it was most constant, it always turned on Florence.", "그 병의 다른 모든 국면에서, 그리고 가장 한결같이 머무는 국면에서, 그의 정신은 언제나 플로렌스에게로 향했다."], ["What he would oftenest do was this: he would recall that night he had so recently remembered, the night on which she came down to his room, and would imagine that his heart smote him, and that he went out after her, and up the stairs to seek her.", "그가 가장 자주 하는 것은 이러했다. 그는 그토록 최근에 떠올린 그 밤을, 딸이 자기 방으로 내려왔던 그 밤을 되새기면서, 자기 마음이 자기를 찔러 후회하게 만들었고 그래서 자기가 딸을 뒤따라 나가 계단을 올라 딸을 찾았다고 상상하곤 했다."], ["Then, confounding that time with the later days of the many footsteps, he would be amazed at their number, and begin to count them as he followed her.", "그러다가 그 시간을, 그 숱한 발소리가 나던 훗날과 뒤섞으면서, 그 발소리의 수에 놀라며 딸을 뒤따라가면서 그것을 세기 시작했다."], ["Here, of a sudden, was a bloody footstep going on among the others; and after it there began to be, at intervals, doors standing open, through which certain terrible pictures were seen, in mirrors, of haggard men, concealing something in their breasts.", "그러면 문득, 다른 발소리들 사이로 피 묻은 발자국 하나가 지나갔다. 그리고 그 뒤로는 이따금씩, 문들이 열린 채 서 있기 시작했는데, 그 너머로 거울 속에 어떤 끔찍한 그림들이 보였다—초췌한 사내들이 가슴속에 무언가를 감추고 있는 그림이었다."], ["Still, among the many footsteps and the bloody footsteps here and there, was the step of Florence.", "그래도 그 숱한 발소리와 여기저기의 피 묻은 발자국 사이로, 플로렌스의 발걸음이 있었다."], ["Still she was going on before.", "여전히 딸은 앞서 걸어가고 있었다."], ["Still the restless mind went, following and counting, ever farther, ever higher, as to the summit of a mighty tower that it took years to climb.", "여전히 그 잠 못 드는 정신은, 따라가고 세면서, 오르는 데 몇 해가 걸리는 거대한 탑의 꼭대기를 향하듯, 더 멀리, 더 높이 나아갔다."]], [["One day he inquired if that were not Susan who had spoken a long while ago.", "어느 날 그는 한참 전에 말한 사람이 수전이 아니었느냐고 물었다."]], [["Florence said “Yes, dear Papa;” and asked him would he like to see her?", "플로렌스는 \"네, 그래요, 아빠\"라고 말하고는, 그 애를 보고 싶으시냐고 물었다."]], [["He said “very much.”", "그는 \"무척\"이라고 말했다."], ["And Susan, with no little trepidation, showed herself at his bedside.", "그러자 수전이, 적잖이 떨면서, 그의 침대 곁에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["It seemed a great relief to him.", "그것은 그에게 큰 위안이 되는 듯했다."], ["He begged her not to go; to understand that he forgave her what she had said; and that she was to stay.", "그는 그녀에게 가지 말아 달라고, 그녀가 했던 말을 자기가 용서한다는 것을 알아 달라고, 그러니 머물러 달라고 청했다."], ["Florence and he were very different now, he said, and very happy.", "이제 플로렌스와 자기는 아주 달라졌으며, 아주 행복하다고 그는 말했다."], ["Let her look at this!", "이것을 좀 보라고!"], ["He meant his drawing the gentle head down to his pillow, and laying it beside him.", "그가 말한 것은, 그 부드러운 머리를 자기 베개로 끌어당겨 자기 곁에 뉘는 일이었다."]], [["He remained like this for days and weeks.", "그는 며칠, 몇 주씩 이렇게 지냈다."], ["At length, lying, the faint feeble semblance of a man, upon his bed, and speaking in a voice so low that they could only hear him by listening very near to his lips, he became quiet.", "마침내, 한 사내의 희미하고 가냘픈 형상이 되어 침대에 누운 채, 입술에 아주 가까이 귀를 대고 들어야만 알아들을 수 있을 만큼 낮은 목소리로 말하면서, 그는 평온해졌다."], ["It was dimly pleasant to him now, to lie there, with the window open, looking out at the summer sky and the trees: and, in the evening, at the sunset.", "이제 그에게는, 창문을 열어 두고 거기 누워 여름 하늘과 나무들을 내다보는 것이, 그리고 저녁에는 저무는 노을을 내다보는 것이 어렴풋이 기쁜 일이 되었다."], ["To watch the shadows of the clouds and leaves, and seem to feel a sympathy with shadows.", "구름과 잎사귀의 그림자를 지켜보며, 그림자에 어떤 공감을 느끼는 듯한 것이."], ["It was natural that he should.", "그가 그러는 것은 자연스러웠다."], ["To him, life and the world were nothing else.", "그에게 삶과 세상은 그 그림자 이외에 아무것도 아니었으니."]], [["He began to show now that he thought of Florence’s fatigue: and often taxed his weakness to whisper to her, “Go and walk, my dearest, in the sweet air.", "그는 이제 플로렌스의 피로를 헤아린다는 것을 보이기 시작했다. 그래서 자주 자기 쇠약함을 무릅쓰고 딸에게 속삭이곤 했다, \"가서 거닐어라, 내 가장 사랑하는 아이야, 향긋한 공기 속을."], ["Go to your good husband!”", "네 착한 남편에게 가렴!\""], ["One time when Walter was in his room, he beckoned him to come near, and to stoop down; and pressing his hand, whispered an assurance to him that he knew he could trust him with his child when he was dead.", "한번은 월터가 그의 방에 있을 때, 그는 가까이 와서 몸을 굽혀 달라고 손짓했다. 그러고는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며, 자기가 죽으면 자기 아이를 그에게 맡길 수 있음을 안다고 다짐의 말을 속삭였다."]], [["It chanced one evening, towards sunset, when Florence and Walter were sitting in his room together, as he liked to see them, that Florence, having her baby in her arms, began in a low voice to sing to the little fellow, and sang the old tune she had so often sung to the dead child: He could not bear it at the time; he held up his trembling hand, imploring her to stop; but next day he asked her to repeat it, and to do so often of an evening: which she did. He listening, with his face turned away.", "어느 날 저녁, 해 질 무렵, 그가 보기 좋아하던 대로 플로렌스와 월터가 함께 그의 방에 앉아 있을 때였다. 플로렌스가 품에 아기를 안고서, 낮은 목소리로 그 작은 녀석에게 노래를 불러 주기 시작했는데, 죽은 아이에게 그토록 자주 불러 주던 옛 가락을 불렀다. 그는 그때는 그것을 견디지 못했다. 그는 떨리는 손을 들어 올려 그만두라고 애원했다. 그러나 이튿날 그는 딸에게 그것을 다시 불러 달라고, 그리고 저녁마다 자주 그래 달라고 청했다. 딸은 그렇게 했다. 그는 얼굴을 돌린 채 귀를 기울였다."]], [["Florence was sitting on a certain time by his window, with her work-basket between her and her old attendant, who was still her faithful companion.", "어느 때 플로렌스는 그의 창가에 앉아 있었는데, 자기와 옛 시중꾼—여전히 그녀의 충실한 동무였다—사이에 반짇고리를 두고 있었다."], ["He had fallen into a doze.", "그는 졸음에 빠져들어 있었다."], ["It was a beautiful evening, with two hours of light to come yet; and the tranquillity and quiet made Florence very thoughtful.", "아름다운 저녁이었고, 아직 두 시간의 햇빛이 남아 있었다. 그 고요함과 정적이 플로렌스를 무척 생각에 잠기게 했다."], ["She was lost to everything for the moment, but the occasion when the so altered figure on the bed had first presented her to her beautiful Mama; when a touch from Walter leaning on the back of her chair, made her start.", "그녀는 한순간 모든 것을 잊었다—그 침대 위의 그토록 달라진 형상이 처음 자기를 아름다운 어머니께 인사시켜 주던 그때만을 빼고. 그때 의자 등받이에 기대어 있던 월터의 손길이 닿자, 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"여보,\""], ["said Walter, “there is someone downstairs who wishes to speak to you.”", "월터가 말했다, \"아래층에 당신과 이야기하고 싶어 하는 분이 와 계시오.\""]], [["She fancied Walter looked grave, and asked him if anything had happened.", "그녀는 월터가 심각해 보인다고 생각하고는, 무슨 일이라도 생겼느냐고 물었다."]], [["“No, no, my love!” said Walter.", "\"아니, 아니오, 여보!\""], ["“I have seen the gentleman myself, and spoken with him.", "월터가 말했다. \"내가 그분을 직접 뵙고 이야기도 나누었소."], ["Nothing has happened.", "아무 일도 없소."], ["Will you come?”", "가 보겠소?\""]], [["Florence put her arm through his; and confiding her father to the black-eyed Mrs Toots, who sat as brisk and smart at her work as black-eyed woman could, accompanied her husband downstairs.", "플로렌스는 그의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼우고는, 검은 눈의 토츠 부인에게—그녀는 검은 눈의 여인이 할 수 있는 한 가장 활기차고 야무지게 일감에 앉아 있었다—아버지를 맡기고 남편과 함께 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["In the pleasant little parlour opening on the garden, sat a gentleman, who rose to advance towards her when she came in, but turned off, by reason of some peculiarity in his legs, and was only stopped by the table.", "정원으로 통하는 아담한 작은 응접실에, 한 신사가 앉아 있다가 그녀가 들어서자 일어나 다가오려 했으나, 다리에 어떤 특이한 점이 있어 옆으로 빗나가다가 탁자에 가로막혀서야 겨우 멈춰 섰다."]], [["Florence then remembered Cousin Feenix, whom she had not at first recognised in the shade of the leaves.", "그제야 플로렌스는 피닉스 사촌을 알아보았는데, 처음에는 잎사귀들의 그늘 속이라 그를 알아보지 못했던 것이다."], ["Cousin Feenix took her hand, and congratulated her upon her marriage.", "피닉스 사촌은 그녀의 손을 잡고 결혼을 축하했다."]], [["“I could have wished, I am sure,”", "\"진작에,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, sitting down as Florence sat, “to have had an earlier opportunity of offering my congratulations; but, in point of fact, so many painful occurrences have happened, treading, as a man may say, on one another’s heels, that I have been in a devil of a state myself, and perfectly unfit for every description of society.", "피닉스 사촌이 플로렌스가 앉자 따라 앉으며 말했다, \"축하 인사를 드릴 기회가 더 일찍 있었더라면 하고 정말이지 바랐습니다만, 사실인즉슨, 워낙 많은 고통스러운 사건들이, 말하자면 줄줄이 꼬리를 물고 일어나는 바람에, 저 자신도 지독히도 끔찍한 처지에 빠져서, 온갖 종류의 사교에는 도무지 어울릴 수가 없었습니다."], ["The only description of society I have kept, has been my own; and it certainly is anything but flattering to a man’s good opinion of his own sources, to know that, in point of fact, he has the capacity of boring himself to a perfectly unlimited extent.”", "제가 유지한 유일한 사교라고는 저 자신과의 사교뿐이었지요. 그런데 사실인즉슨, 한 사람이 자기 자신을 무한정 지루하게 만들 능력이 있다는 걸 알게 되는 것은, 자기 자신의 밑천에 대한 좋은 평가에는 결코 우쭐할 만한 일이 못 됩니다.\""]], [["Florence divined, from some indefinable constraint and anxiety in this gentleman’s manner—which was always a gentleman’s, in spite of the harmless little eccentricities that attached to it—and from Walter’s manner no less, that something more immediately tending to some object was to follow this.", "플로렌스는 이 신사의 거동에—해롭지 않은 자잘한 기벽이 따라붙긴 했어도 그 거동은 언제나 신사다웠다—어떤 형언할 수 없는 거북함과 불안함이 깃든 것에서, 그리고 못지않게 월터의 거동에서, 어떤 목적을 향해 더 직접적으로 다가가는 무언가가 이 뒤에 이어지리라는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“I have been mentioning to my friend Mr Gay, if I may be allowed to have the honour of calling him so,”", "\"제 친구 게이 군에게—그를 그렇게 부르는 영광을 누려도 된다면 말입니다만—제가 말씀드린 바 있습니다,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, “that I am rejoiced to hear that my friend Dombey is very decidedly mending.", "피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"제 친구 돔비가 매우 확실하게 차도가 있다고 들으니 기쁘기 그지없다고요."], ["I trust my friend Dombey will not allow his mind to be too much preyed upon, by any mere loss of fortune.", "제 친구 돔비가 한낱 재산의 손실 따위에 마음을 너무 많이 잠식당하는 일이 없기를 바랍니다."], ["I cannot say that I have ever experienced any very great loss of fortune myself: never having had, in point of fact, any great amount of fortune to lose.", "저로서는 제가 큰 재산의 손실을 겪어 봤다고는 말할 수가 없습니다. 사실인즉슨, 애당초 잃을 만한 큰 재산을 가져 본 적이 없으니까요."], ["But as much as I could lose, I have lost; and I don’t find that I particularly care about it.", "그러나 잃을 수 있는 만큼은 제가 다 잃었지요. 그런데도 제가 그것을 딱히 마음에 두지는 않더라고요."], ["I know my friend Dombey to be a devilish honourable man; and it’s calculated to console my friend Dombey very much, to know, that this is the universal sentiment.", "저는 제 친구 돔비가 지독히도 명예로운 사람임을 압니다. 그리고 이것이 만인의 한결같은 생각임을 아는 것은, 제 친구 돔비에게 큰 위로가 될 만한 일입니다."], ["Even Tommy Screwzer,—a man of an extremely bilious habit, with whom my friend Gay is probably acquainted—cannot say a syllable in disputation of the fact.”", "심지어 토미 스크루저—극도로 신경질적인 기질의 사람으로, 아마 제 친구 게이도 아는 사람일 텐데—조차도 그 사실을 반박하는 말은 한마디도 못 할 겁니다.\""]], [["Florence felt, more than ever, that there was something to come; and looked earnestly for it.", "플로렌스는 그 어느 때보다도 더, 무언가 다가올 것이 있음을 느꼈다. 그래서 간절히 그것을 기다렸다."], ["So earnestly, that Cousin Feenix answered, as if she had spoken.", "어찌나 간절했던지, 마치 그녀가 입을 열기라도 한 것처럼 피닉스 사촌이 대답했다."]], [["“The fact is,” said Cousin Feenix, “that my friend Gay and myself have been discussing the propriety of entreating a favour at your hands; and that I have the consent of my friend Gay—who has met me in an exceedingly kind and open manner, for which I am very much indebted to him—to solicit it. I am sensible that so amiable a lady as the lovely and accomplished daughter of my friend Dombey will not require much urging; but I am happy to know, that I am supported by my friend Gay’s influence and approval. As in my parliamentary time, when a man had a motion to make of any sort—which happened seldom in those days, for we were kept very tight in hand, the leaders on both sides being regular Martinets, which was a devilish good thing for the rank and file, like myself, and prevented our exposing ourselves continually, as a great many of us had a feverish anxiety to do—as, in my parliamentary time, I was about to say, when a man had leave to let off any little private popgun, it was always considered a great point for him to say that he had the happiness of believing that his sentiments were not without an echo in the breast of Mr Pitt; the pilot, in point of fact, who had weathered the storm. Upon which, a devilish large number of fellows immediately cheered, and put him in spirits. Though the fact is, that these fellows, being under orders to cheer most excessively whenever Mr Pitt’s name was mentioned, became so proficient that it always woke ’em. And they were so entirely innocent of what was going on, otherwise, that it used to be commonly said by Conversation Brown—four-bottle man at the Treasury Board, with whom the father of my friend Gay was probably acquainted, for it was before my friend Gay’s time—that if a man had risen in his place, and said that he regretted to inform the house that there was an Honourable Member in the last stage of convulsions in the Lobby, and that the Honourable Member’s name was Pitt, the approbation would have been vociferous.”", "\"사실인즉슨,\" 피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"제 친구 게이와 제가, 당신께 한 가지 청을 드리는 것이 온당한지를 의논해 온 터입니다. 그리고 저는 제 친구 게이의—그는 저를 지극히 친절하고 솔직한 태도로 대해 주었고, 그 점에 대해 저는 그에게 큰 신세를 졌습니다—동의를 얻어 그 청을 드리려 합니다. 제 친구 돔비의 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 따님처럼 그토록 상냥한 부인이라면 굳이 많이 졸라 댈 필요가 없으리라는 것을 저도 잘 압니다만, 제 친구 게이의 영향력과 찬동에 힘입고 있음을 알게 되어 기쁩니다. 제가 의회에 몸담던 시절, 한 사람이 무슨 종류든 동의안을 내놓을 때—그 시절엔 그런 일이 드물었습니다. 우리는 아주 단단히 고삐가 잡혀 있었거든요. 양편의 지도자들이 다들 빈틈없는 규율가들이어서, 저 같은 평의원들에게는 그게 지독히도 좋은 일이었지요. 우리 중 상당수가 열에 들떠 끊임없이 제 꼴을 드러내고 싶어 했는데, 그걸 막아 주었으니까요—제가 의회에 몸담던 시절에, 말하자면, 한 사람이 무슨 자잘한 사적 콩알총이라도 한 방 쏘아 올릴 허락을 얻었을 때, 그가 자기 의견이 피트 씨의 가슴속에서 메아리 없이 사라지지는 않으리라 믿어 다행이라고 말하는 것이, 그에게는 늘 큰 점수를 따는 일로 여겨졌지요. 사실인즉슨, 폭풍을 뚫고 배를 몰아온 그 키잡이 말입니다. 그러면 지독히도 많은 친구들이 즉각 환호하며 그의 기운을 북돋아 주었지요. 하지만 사실인즉슨, 이 친구들은 피트 씨의 이름이 거론될 때마다 더없이 요란하게 환호하라는 명을 받고 있어서, 어찌나 능숙해졌던지 그 이름이 늘 그들을 잠에서 깨우곤 했답니다. 그리고 그들은 그것 말고 무슨 일이 벌어지는지에 대해서는 워낙 깜깜무식이어서, '담소가' 브라운이—재무위원회의 네 병들이 술꾼으로, 아마 제 친구 게이의 아버님은 알았을 사람입니다. 제 친구 게이의 시절보다는 전이니까요—이런 말을 흔히 하곤 했지요. 만일 누군가가 제자리에서 일어나, 로비에서 한 의원께서 발작의 마지막 단계에 빠져 있음을 의회에 알리게 되어 유감이며, 그 의원의 이름이 피트라고 말한다면, 갈채가 떠나갈 듯 쏟아졌을 거라고요.\""]], [["This postponement of the point, put Florence in a flutter; and she looked from Cousin Feenix to Walter, in increasing agitation.", "이렇게 본론이 미루어지자 플로렌스는 안절부절못하게 되었다. 그래서 점점 더해 가는 동요 속에 피닉스 사촌에게서 월터에게로 눈길을 옮겼다."]], [["“My love,”", "\"여보,\""], ["said Walter, “there is nothing the matter.”", "월터가 말했다, \"아무 일도 없소.\""]], [["“There is nothing the matter, upon my honour,”", "\"아무 일도 없습니다, 제 명예를 걸고,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix; “and I am deeply distressed at being the means of causing you a moment’s uneasiness.", "피닉스 사촌이 말했다. \"그리고 제가 당신께 한순간이나마 불안을 끼친 원인이 된 것이 몹시 송구스럽습니다."], ["I beg to assure you that there is nothing the matter.", "정말이지 아무 일도 없음을 단언해 드립니다."], ["The favour that I have to ask is, simply—but it really does seem so exceedingly singular, that I should be in the last degree obliged to my friend Gay if he would have the goodness to break the—in point of fact, the ice,”", "제가 드릴 청은 그저—그러나 정말이지 어찌나 지극히 별난 일로 보이는지, 제 친구 게이가 그—사실인즉슨, 그 얼음을 깨 주는 호의를 베풀어 준다면 저로서는 더없이 고맙겠습니다,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix.", "피닉스 사촌이 말했다."]], [["Walter thus appealed to, and appealed to no less in the look that Florence turned towards him, said:", "이렇게 부탁을 받고, 또 플로렌스가 그를 향해 돌린 눈길에서도 못지않게 부탁을 받은 월터가 말했다."]], [["“My dearest, it is no more than this.", "\"내 가장 사랑하는 사람, 이것뿐이오."], ["That you will ride to London with this gentleman, whom you know.”", "당신이 아는 이 신사분과 함께 런던으로 마차를 타고 가는 것이오.\""]], [["“And my friend Gay, also—I beg your pardon!”", "\"제 친구 게이도 함께—실례했습니다!\""], ["interrupted Cousin Feenix.", "피닉스 사촌이 끼어들었다."]], [["“—And with me—and make a visit somewhere.”", "\"—그리고 나와 함께—어딘가를 방문하는 거요.\""]], [["“To whom?”", "\"누구를요?\""], ["asked Florence, looking from one to the other.", "플로렌스가 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 보며 물었다."]], [["“If I might entreat,”", "\"감히 청하건대,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, “that you would not press for an answer to that question, I would venture to take the liberty of making the request.”", "피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"그 물음에 대한 답을 다그치지 말아 주신다면, 제가 외람되이 이 부탁을 드리는 모험을 해 보겠습니다.\""]], [["“Do you know, Walter?”", "\"당신은 알아요, 월터?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“And think it right?”", "\"그리고 옳다고 생각해요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["Only because I am sure that you would too.", "다만 당신도 그렇게 생각하리라 확신하기 때문이오."], ["Though there may be reasons I very well understand, which make it better that nothing more should be said beforehand.”", "비록 내가 아주 잘 이해하는 어떤 이유들이 있어서, 미리 더 말하지 않는 편이 낫긴 하지만 말이오.\""]], [["“If Papa is still asleep, or can spare me if he is awake, I will go immediately,”", "\"아빠가 아직 주무시거나, 깨어 계시더라도 저를 잠시 보내 주실 수 있다면, 곧바로 가겠어요,\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["And rising quietly, and glancing at them with a look that was a little alarmed but perfectly confiding, left the room.", "그러고는 조용히 일어나, 조금은 겁먹었지만 더없이 신뢰가 담긴 눈빛으로 그들을 흘끗 보고는 방을 나갔다."]], [["When she came back, ready to bear them company, they were talking together, gravely, at the window; and Florence could not but wonder what the topic was, that had made them so well acquainted in so short a time.", "그녀가 그들과 동행할 채비를 하고 돌아왔을 때, 그들은 창가에서 함께 진지하게 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 플로렌스는 그토록 짧은 시간에 두 사람을 그토록 가깝게 만든 화제가 무엇일까 궁금하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She did not wonder at the look of pride and love with which her husband broke off as she entered; for she never saw him, but that rested on her.", "그녀가 들어서자 남편이 자랑스러움과 사랑이 담긴 눈빛으로 말을 멈추는 것에는 궁금해하지 않았다. 그녀가 그를 볼 때면 늘 그 눈빛이 그녀에게 머물러 있었으니까."]], [["“I will leave,”", "\"제 친구 돔비에게,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, “a card for my friend Dombey, sincerely trusting that he will pick up health and strength with every returning hour.", "피닉스 사촌이 말했다, \"명함을 한 장 남기고 가겠습니다. 그가 시간이 갈수록 건강과 기력을 되찾기를 진심으로 빕니다."], ["And I hope my friend Dombey will do me the favour to consider me a man who has a devilish warm admiration of his character, as, in point of fact, a British merchant and a devilish upright gentleman.", "그리고 제 친구 돔비가, 저를 그의 인품을—사실인즉슨, 영국 상인으로서, 또 지독히도 곧은 신사로서의 그의 인품을—지독히도 따뜻하게 흠모하는 사람으로 여겨 주는 호의를 베풀어 주기를 바랍니다."], ["My place in the country is in a most confounded state of dilapidation, but if my friend Dombey should require a change of air, and would take up his quarters there, he would find it a remarkably healthy spot—as it need be, for it’s amazingly dull.", "제 시골 영지는 더없이 황폐하게 무너져 가는 형편입니다만, 만일 제 친구 돔비가 요양이 필요해서 그곳에 거처를 정한다면, 그는 그곳이 놀랍도록 건강에 좋은 곳임을 알게 될 겁니다—그럴 수밖에요, 놀랍도록 따분한 곳이니까요."], ["If my friend Dombey suffers from bodily weakness, and would allow me to recommend what has frequently done myself good, as a man who has been extremely queer at times, and who lived pretty freely in the days when men lived very freely, I should say, let it be in point of fact the yolk of an egg, beat up with sugar and nutmeg, in a glass of sherry, and taken in the morning with a slice of dry toast.", "만일 제 친구 돔비가 신체의 쇠약으로 고생하고 있다면, 그리고 제가 자주 제 자신에게 효험을 보았던 것을 추천하도록 허락해 준다면—저는 때때로 지독히도 몸이 안 좋은 사람이었고, 사람들이 아주 흥청망청 살던 시절에 꽤 자유분방하게 살았던 사람입니다만—사실인즉슨, 달걀노른자를 설탕과 육두구와 함께 셰리주 한 잔에 휘저어, 아침에 마른 토스트 한 조각과 함께 들라고 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Jackson, who kept the boxing-rooms in Bond Street—man of very superior qualifications, with whose reputation my friend Gay is no doubt acquainted—used to mention that in training for the ring they substituted rum for sherry.", "본드 스트리트에서 권투장을 운영하던 잭슨이—대단히 뛰어난 자질의 사람으로, 그 명성은 분명 제 친구 게이도 알 겁니다—말하기를, 링에 오를 훈련을 할 때는 셰리 대신 럼을 쓴다고 하더군요."], ["I should recommend sherry in this case, on account of my friend Dombey being in an invalided condition; which might occasion rum to fly—in point of fact to his head—and throw him into a devil of a state.”", "이 경우에는 제 친구 돔비가 병약한 상태이니 셰리를 추천하겠습니다. 럼은 그를—사실인즉슨, 그의 머리로—치고 올라가서 그를 지독히도 끔찍한 상태에 빠뜨릴 수도 있으니까요.\""]], [["Of all this, Cousin Feenix delivered himself with an obviously nervous and discomposed air.", "이 모든 것을 피닉스 사촌은 누가 봐도 초조하고 안절부절못하는 기색으로 늘어놓았다."], ["Then, giving his arm to Florence, and putting the strongest possible constraint upon his wilful legs, which seemed determined to go out into the garden, he led her to the door, and handed her into a carriage that was ready for her reception.", "그러고는 플로렌스에게 팔을 내주고, 한사코 정원으로 나가려 작정한 듯한 자기 고집스러운 다리에 있는 힘껏 제동을 걸면서, 그녀를 문간으로 이끌어, 그녀를 맞으려고 대기 중이던 마차에 태웠다."]], [["Walter entered after him, and they drove away.", "월터가 그 뒤를 따라 올라탔고, 마차는 출발했다."]], [["Their ride was six or eight miles long.", "마차로 가는 길은 육칠 마일쯤 되었다."], ["When they drove through certain dull and stately streets, lying westward in London, it was growing dusk.", "런던 서쪽에 자리한 어떤 칙칙하고 위엄 있는 거리들을 지날 무렵, 땅거미가 지고 있었다."], ["Florence had, by this time, put her hand in Walter’s; and was looking very earnestly, and with increasing agitation, into every new street into which they turned.", "플로렌스는 이때쯤 자기 손을 월터의 손에 넣고 있었다. 그리고 마차가 접어드는 새 거리마다, 점점 더해 가는 동요 속에 무척 간절히 들여다보고 있었다."]], [["When the carriage stopped, at last, before that house in Brook Street, where her father’s unhappy marriage had been celebrated, Florence said, “Walter, what is this?", "마침내 마차가, 아버지의 불행한 결혼식이 거행되었던 브룩 스트리트의 그 집 앞에 멈춰 섰을 때, 플로렌스가 말했다, \"월터, 여기가 어디예요? 누가 여기 있는 거예요?\""], ["Who is here?” Walter cheering her, and not replying, she glanced up at the house-front, and saw that all the windows were shut, as if it were uninhabited.", "월터가 그녀를 다독이며 대답하지 않자, 그녀는 집 정면을 올려다보았고, 마치 사람이 살지 않는 듯 창문이 모두 닫혀 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Cousin Feenix had by this time alighted, and was offering his hand.", "피닉스 사촌은 이때쯤 마차에서 내려, 손을 내밀고 있었다."]], [["“Are you not coming, Walter?”", "\"당신은 안 와요, 월터?\""]], [["“No, I will remain here.", "\"안 가오, 나는 여기 남아 있겠소."], ["Don’t tremble there is nothing to fear, dearest Florence.”", "떨지 마오, 두려워할 것 없소, 내 가장 사랑하는 플로렌스.\""]], [["“I know that, Walter, with you so near.", "\"알아요, 월터, 당신이 이렇게 가까이 있으니."], ["I am sure of that, but—”", "그건 확실해요, 하지만—\""]], [["The door was softly opened, without any knock, and Cousin Feenix led her out of the summer evening air into the close dull house.", "문이 노크도 없이 살며시 열렸고, 피닉스 사촌은 그녀를 여름 저녁 공기 속에서 그 답답하고 음울한 집 안으로 이끌었다."], ["More sombre and brown than ever, it seemed to have been shut up from the wedding-day, and to have hoarded darkness and sadness ever since.", "어느 때보다 더 침침하고 거무튀튀한 그 집은, 결혼식 날 이후로 줄곧 닫혀 있었던 듯, 그 뒤로 줄곧 어둠과 슬픔을 쌓아 둔 듯했다."]], [["Florence ascended the dusky staircase, trembling; and stopped, with her conductor, at the drawing-room door.", "플로렌스는 떨면서 어둑한 계단을 올라, 안내자와 함께 응접실 문 앞에서 멈춰 섰다."], ["He opened it, without speaking, and signed an entreaty to her to advance into the inner room, while he remained there.", "그는 말없이 문을 열고, 자기는 그 자리에 남은 채, 그녀에게 안쪽 방으로 들어가 달라고 간청하는 몸짓을 해 보였다."], ["Florence, after hesitating an instant, complied.", "플로렌스는 한순간 망설이다가 그대로 따랐다."]], [["Sitting by the window at a table, where she seemed to have been writing or drawing, was a lady, whose head, turned away towards the dying light, was resting on her hand.", "창가의 탁자 곁에, 무언가를 쓰거나 그리고 있었던 듯한 한 부인이 앉아 있었는데, 저무는 빛을 향해 돌린 그 머리는 한 손에 받쳐져 있었다."], ["Florence advancing, doubtfully, all at once stood still, as if she had lost the power of motion.", "머뭇거리며 다가가던 플로렌스는, 마치 움직이는 힘을 잃기라도 한 듯 문득 멈춰 섰다."], ["The lady turned her head.", "그 부인이 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["“Great Heaven!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["she said, “what is this?”", "그녀가 말했다, \"이게 어찌 된 일이냐?\""]], [["“No, no!”", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼요!\""], ["cried Florence, shrinking back as she rose up and putting out her hands to keep her off.", "플로렌스가 부르짖으며, 그녀가 일어서자 뒷걸음질 치며 두 손을 내밀어 그녀를 막으려 했다."], ["“Mama!”", "\"어머니!\""]], [["They stood looking at each other.", "두 사람은 서로를 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["Passion and pride had worn it, but it was the face of Edith, and beautiful and stately yet.", "격정과 자존심이 그것을 닳게 했으나, 그것은 에디스의 얼굴이었고, 여전히 아름답고 위엄 있었다."], ["It was the face of Florence, and through all the terrified avoidance it expressed, there was pity in it, sorrow, a grateful tender memory.", "그것은 플로렌스의 얼굴이었고, 그것이 드러내는 그 온갖 겁먹은 회피에도 불구하고, 거기에는 연민이, 슬픔이, 고마운 정겨운 추억이 깃들어 있었다."], ["On each face, wonder and fear were painted vividly; each so still and silent, looking at the other over the black gulf of the irrevocable past.", "두 얼굴에는 저마다 경이와 두려움이 선명하게 어려 있었다. 저마다 그토록 고요하고 말없이, 돌이킬 수 없는 과거의 검은 심연 너머로 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["Florence was the first to change.", "먼저 변한 것은 플로렌스였다."], ["Bursting into tears, she said from her full heart, “Oh, Mama, Mama!", "그녀는 울음을 터뜨리며, 벅찬 가슴으로 말했다, \"오, 어머니, 어머니!"], ["why do we meet like this?", "왜 우리가 이렇게 만나야 하나요?"], ["Why were you ever kind to me when there was no one else, that we should meet like this?”", "아무도 없을 때 어머니는 왜 제게 그토록 다정하셨나요, 우리가 이렇게 만나야 하는데?\""]], [["Edith stood before her, dumb and motionless.", "에디스는 그녀 앞에 말없이, 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."], ["Her eyes were fixed upon her face.", "그녀의 눈은 그 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다."]], [["“I dare not think of that,”", "\"저는 감히 그 일은 생각할 수가 없어요,\""], ["said Florence, “I am come from Papa’s sick bed.", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"저는 아빠의 병상에서 오는 길이에요."], ["We are never asunder now; we never shall be”", "우리는 이제 결코 떨어지지 않아요. 다시는 떨어지지 않을 거예요."], ["any more. If you would have me ask his pardon, I will do it, Mama.", "만일 제가 아빠에게 용서를 빌기를 원하신다면, 그렇게 할게요, 어머니."], ["I am almost sure he will grant it now, if I ask him.", "제가 청한다면 아빠가 이제는 거의 틀림없이 들어주실 거예요."], ["May Heaven grant it to you, too, and comfort you!”", "하늘도 어머니께 그것을 허락하시고, 어머니를 위로해 주시기를!\""]], [["She answered not a word.", "그녀는 한마디도 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“Walter—I am married to him, and we have a son,”", "\"월터가—저는 그와 결혼했고, 우리에게는 아들이 있어요,\""], ["said Florence, timidly—“is at the door, and has brought me here.", "플로렌스가 머뭇거리며 말했다—\"문간에 와 있어요, 저를 여기 데려다주었어요."], ["I will tell him that you are repentant; that you are changed,”", "어머니가 뉘우치고 계신다고, 어머니가 변하셨다고 그에게 말할게요,\""], ["said Florence, looking mournfully upon her; “and he will speak to Papa with me, I know.", "플로렌스가 애처롭게 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. \"그러면 그가 저와 함께 아빠에게 말해 줄 거예요, 분명히요."], ["Is there anything but this that I can do?”", "이것 말고 제가 할 수 있는 일이 또 있을까요?\""]], [["Edith, breaking her silence, without moving eye or limb, answered slowly:", "에디스가 침묵을 깨고, 눈도 손발도 움직이지 않은 채 천천히 대답했다."]], [["“The stain upon your name, upon your husband’s, on your child’s.", "\"네 이름에, 네 남편의 이름에, 네 아이의 이름에 묻은 오점은."], ["Will that ever be forgiven, Florence?”", "그것이 언젠가 용서받을 수 있겠느냐, 플로렌스?\""]], [["“Will it ever be, Mama? It is!", "\"그것이 언젠가 용서받겠느냐고요, 어머니?"], ["Freely, freely, both by Walter and by me.", "이미 용서되었어요! 아낌없이, 아낌없이, 월터에 의해서도 저에 의해서도요."], ["If that is any consolation to you, there is nothing that you may believe more certainly.", "그것이 어머니께 조금이라도 위안이 된다면, 그보다 더 확실히 믿으셔도 좋은 것은 없어요."], ["You do not—you do not,”", "어머니는—어머니는,\""], ["faltered Florence, “speak of Papa; but I am sure you wish that I should ask him for his forgiveness.", "플로렌스가 더듬거리며 말했다, \"아빠 이야기는 안 하시지만, 어머니가 제게 아빠께 용서를 빌어 달라고 바라시는 걸 저는 분명히 알아요."], ["I am sure you do.”", "분명히 그러실 거예요.\""]], [["She answered not a word.", "그녀는 한마디도 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“I will!”", "\"그렇게 할게요!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“I will bring it you, if you will let me; and then, perhaps, we may take leave of each other, more like what we used to be to one another.", "\"어머니가 허락하신다면 그것을 받아다 드릴게요. 그러면 어쩌면, 우리가 예전에 서로에게 그랬던 것에 좀 더 가깝게 작별할 수 있을 거예요."], ["I have not,”", "저는,\""], ["said Florence very gently, and drawing nearer to her, “I have not shrunk back from you, Mama, because I fear you, or because I dread to be disgraced by you.", "플로렌스가 아주 부드럽게, 그녀에게 더 가까이 다가서며 말했다, \"어머니가 두려워서, 또는 어머니로 인해 욕먹을까 겁이 나서 어머니에게서 뒷걸음질 친 게 아니에요."], ["I only wish to do my duty to Papa.", "저는 그저 아빠에 대한 제 도리를 다하고 싶을 뿐이에요."], ["I am very dear to him, and he is very dear to me.", "저는 아빠에게 무척 소중하고, 아빠는 제게 무척 소중해요."], ["But I never can forget that you were very good to me.", "하지만 어머니가 제게 무척 잘해 주셨다는 걸 저는 결코 잊을 수가 없어요."], ["Oh, pray to Heaven,”", "오, 하늘에 비세요,\""], ["cried Florence, falling on her bosom, “pray to Heaven, Mama, to forgive you all this sin and shame, and to forgive me if I cannot help doing this (if it is wrong), when I remember what you used to be!”", "플로렌스가 그녀의 품에 안기며 부르짖었다, \"하늘에 비세요, 어머니, 이 모든 죄와 수치를 어머니께 용서하시기를, 그리고 제가 옛날의 어머니를 떠올릴 때 이렇게 하지 않을 수 없는 것을 (만일 그것이 잘못이라면) 용서하시기를!\""]], [["Edith, as if she fell beneath her touch, sunk down on her knees, and caught her round the neck.", "에디스는, 마치 그 손길 아래 무너지듯, 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉아 그녀의 목을 끌어안았다."]], [["“Florence!”", "\"플로렌스!\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 부르짖었다."], ["“My better angel!", "\"내 더 나은 천사야!"], ["Before I am mad again, before my stubbornness comes back and strikes me dumb, believe me, upon my soul I am innocent!”", "내가 다시 미쳐 버리기 전에, 내 고집이 되돌아와 나를 벙어리로 만들기 전에, 나를 믿어 다오, 내 영혼을 걸고 나는 결백하다!\""]], [["“Mama!”", "\"어머니!\""]], [["“Guilty of much!", "\"많은 것에 죄가 있지!"], ["Guilty of that which sets a waste between us evermore.", "우리 사이에 영영 황무지를 갈라놓는 그 일에 죄가 있어."], ["Guilty of what must separate me, through the whole remainder of my life, from purity and innocence—from you, of all the earth.", "내 남은 평생 내내, 순결함과 무구함에서—이 세상 누구보다도 너에게서—나를 갈라놓을 수밖에 없는 그것에 죄가 있어."], ["Guilty of a blind and passionate resentment, of which I do not, cannot, will not, even now, repent; but not guilty with that dead man.", "눈멀고 격정에 찬 원한에 죄가 있어, 그것은 지금조차도 내가 뉘우치지 않고, 뉘우칠 수 없고, 뉘우치지 않을 거야. 하지만 그 죽은 자와의 죄는 없어."], ["Before God!”", "하느님 앞에 맹세코!\""]], [["Upon her knees upon the ground, she held up both her hands, and swore it.", "그녀는 땅바닥에 무릎을 꿇은 채, 두 손을 들어 올려 그것을 맹세했다."]], [["“Florence!”", "\"플로렌스!\""], ["she said, “purest and best of natures,—whom I love—who might have changed me long ago, and did for a time work some change even in the woman that I am,—believe me, I am innocent of that; and once more, on my desolate heart, let me lay this dear head, for the last time!”", "그녀가 말했다, \"더없이 순결하고 더없이 선한 사람아—내가 사랑하는—오래전에 나를 변화시킬 수도 있었고, 한때는 나 같은 여자에게서조차 얼마간의 변화를 정말로 이루어 낸 사람아—나를 믿어 다오, 나는 그 일에는 결백하다. 그리고 한 번 더, 내 황량한 가슴에, 이 사랑스러운 머리를 마지막으로 뉘게 해 다오!\""]], [["She was moved and weeping.", "그녀는 마음이 움직여 울고 있었다."], ["Had she been oftener thus in older days, she had been happier now.", "옛날에 더 자주 이러했더라면, 그녀는 지금 더 행복했을 것이다."]], [["“There is nothing else in all the world,”", "\"이 세상 그 무엇도,\""], ["she said, “that would have wrung denial from me.", "그녀가 말했다, \"나에게서 부인의 말을 쥐어짜 낼 수는 없었을 거야."], ["No love, no hatred, no hope, no threat.", "어떤 사랑도, 어떤 증오도, 어떤 희망도, 어떤 위협도."], ["I said that I would die, and make no sign.", "나는 입을 다문 채 죽겠다고 말했어."], ["I could have done so, and I would, if we had never met, Florence.”", "나는 그렇게 할 수 있었고, 그렇게 했을 거야, 만일 우리가 결코 만나지 않았더라면, 플로렌스.\""]], [["“I trust,”", "\"바라건대,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, ambling in at the door, and speaking, half in the room, and half out of it, “that my lovely and accomplished relative will excuse my having, by a little stratagem, effected this meeting.", "피닉스 사촌이 문간으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어서며, 몸은 절반은 방 안에, 절반은 방 밖에 둔 채로 말했다, \"제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척이, 제가 자그마한 계략을 써서 이 만남을 성사시킨 것을 너그러이 봐 주기를 바랍니다."], ["I cannot say that I was, at first, wholly incredulous as to the possibility of my lovely and accomplished relative having, very unfortunately, committed herself with the deceased person with white teeth; because in point of fact, one does see, in this world—which is remarkable for devilish strange arrangements, and for being decidedly the most unintelligible thing within a man’s experience—very odd conjunctions of that sort.", "처음부터 제가, 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척이 매우 불행하게도 그 하얀 이를 가진 고인과 부정한 관계를 맺었을 가능성에 대해 전적으로 의심하지 않았다고는 말할 수 없습니다. 사실인즉슨, 이 세상에서는—지독히도 기묘한 일들로 유명하고, 한 사람의 경험 안에서 단연 가장 이해 불가능한 것인 이 세상에서는—그런 종류의 매우 묘한 결합을 보게 되니까요."], ["But as I mentioned to my friend Dombey, I could not admit the criminality of my lovely and accomplished relative until it was perfectly established.", "하지만 제가 제 친구 돔비에게 말씀드린 대로, 저는 그것이 완전히 입증되기 전까지는 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척의 죄를 인정할 수가 없었습니다."], ["And feeling, when the deceased person was, in point of fact, destroyed in a devilish horrible manner, that her position was a very painful one—and feeling besides that our family had been a little to blame in not paying more attention to her, and that we are a careless family—and also that my aunt, though a devilish lively woman, had perhaps not been the very best of mothers—I took the liberty of seeking her in France, and offering her such protection as a man very much out at elbows could offer.", "그리고 그 고인이, 사실인즉슨, 지독히도 끔찍한 방식으로 목숨을 잃었을 때, 그녀의 처지가 매우 고통스러운 것임을 느끼고—게다가 우리 집안이 그녀에게 더 마음을 쓰지 않은 데 다소 잘못이 있고, 우리가 무심한 집안이라는 것을 느끼고—또한 제 이모님이, 비록 지독히도 활달한 분이긴 했어도, 아마도 가장 훌륭한 어머니는 아니었으리라는 것을 느끼고—저는 외람되이 프랑스로 그녀를 찾아가, 형편이 몹시 쪼들리는 사내가 베풀 수 있는 만큼의 보호를 베풀어 드렸습니다."], ["Upon which occasion, my lovely and accomplished relative did me the honour to express that she believed I was, in my way, a devilish good sort of fellow; and that therefore she put herself under my protection.", "그때 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척은, 제가 제 나름으로는 지독히도 괜찮은 사람이라고 믿는다는 영광스러운 말씀을 제게 해 주었습니다. 그래서 제 보호 아래 자기를 맡긴 것이지요."], ["Which in point of fact I understood to be a kind thing on the part of my lovely and accomplished relative, as I am getting extremely shaky, and have derived great comfort from her solicitude.”", "사실인즉슨, 저는 이것이 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척 편에서의 친절한 처사라고 이해했습니다. 저는 극도로 비실비실해져 가는 처지인지라, 그녀의 보살핌에서 큰 위안을 얻었으니까요.\""]], [["Edith, who had taken Florence to a sofa, made a gesture with her hand as if she would have begged him to say no more.", "에디스는, 플로렌스를 소파로 데려가 앉혔는데, 마치 그에게 더는 말하지 말아 달라고 청하는 듯한 손짓을 했다."]], [["“My lovely and accomplished relative,”", "\"제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척은,\""], ["resumed Cousin Feenix, still ambling about at the door, “will excuse me, if, for her satisfaction, and my own, and that of my friend Dombey, whose lovely and accomplished daughter we so much admire, I complete the thread of my observations.", "피닉스 사촌이 여전히 문간을 어슬렁거리며 말을 이었다, \"그녀의 마음의 평안을 위해, 또 저 자신의 평안과, 그 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 따님을 우리가 그토록 흠모하는 제 친구 돔비의 평안을 위해, 제가 제 소견의 실타래를 마저 풀어내더라도 너그러이 봐 줄 겁니다."], ["She will remember that, from the first, she and I never alluded to the subject of her elopement.", "그녀는, 처음부터 그녀와 제가 그녀의 가출 문제를 한 번도 입에 올린 적이 없음을 기억할 겁니다."], ["My impression, certainly, has always been, that there was a mystery in the affair which she could explain if so inclined.", "저의 인상은, 분명히, 그 일에는 그녀가 마음만 먹으면 설명할 수 있는 어떤 비밀이 있다는 것이었지요."], ["But my lovely and accomplished relative being a devilish resolute woman, I knew that she was not, in point of fact, to be trifled with, and therefore did not involve myself in any discussions.", "하지만 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척이 지독히도 단호한 여인인지라, 저는 그녀가 사실인즉슨 함부로 다룰 사람이 아님을 알았기에, 어떤 논의에도 끼어들지 않았습니다."], ["But, observing lately, that her accessible point did appear to be a very strong description of tenderness for the daughter of my friend Dombey, it occurred to me that if I could bring about a meeting, unexpected on both sides, it might lead to beneficial results.", "하지만 근래에, 그녀가 마음을 열 수 있는 지점이 제 친구 돔비의 따님에 대한 매우 강한 종류의 애정인 듯함을 알아차리고는, 만일 제가 양편 모두에게 뜻밖일 만남을 성사시킬 수 있다면, 그것이 이로운 결과로 이어질지도 모른다는 생각이 들었습니다."], ["Therefore, we being in London, in the present private way, before going to the South of Italy, there to establish ourselves, in point of fact, until we go to our long homes, which is a devilish disagreeable reflection for a man, I applied myself to the discovery of the residence of my friend Gay—handsome man of an uncommonly frank disposition, who is probably known to my lovely and accomplished relative—and had the happiness of bringing his amiable wife to the present place.", "그래서, 우리가 이렇게 은밀한 방식으로 런던에 와 있는 지금—이탈리아 남부로 가서, 사실인즉슨, 우리의 영원한 안식처로 갈 때까지 그곳에 자리를 잡기 전에 말입니다. 그건 한 사내에게는 지독히도 달갑잖은 생각이지요—저는 제 친구 게이의 거처를 찾아내는 일에 몰두했고—보기 드물게 솔직한 성품의 잘생긴 사내로, 아마 제 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 친척도 아는 사람일 텐데—그의 상냥한 아내를 이곳으로 모셔오는 기쁨을 누렸습니다."], ["And now,”", "그리고 이제,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, with a real and genuine earnestness shining through the levity of his manner and his slipshod speech, “I do conjure my relative, not to stop half way, but to set right, as far as she can, whatever she has done wrong—not for the honour of her family, not for her own fame, not for any of those considerations which unfortunate circumstances have induced her to regard as hollow, and in point of fact, as approaching to humbug—but because it is wrong, and not right.”", "피닉스 사촌이, 그 경박한 거동과 어수룩한 말투를 뚫고 빛나는 진실하고 진정한 간절함을 담아 말했다, \"저는 제 친척에게 간곡히 청합니다. 중도에 멈추지 말고, 자기가 잘못한 것이 무엇이든 할 수 있는 데까지 바로잡으라고요—집안의 명예를 위해서가 아니라, 자기 자신의 명성을 위해서가 아니라, 불행한 사정들 때문에 그녀가 공허하고, 사실인즉슨, 허튼소리에 가까운 것으로 여기게 된 그런 고려들 가운데 어느 것을 위해서도 아니라—그것이 옳은 게 아니라 그른 일이기 때문에 말입니다.\""]], [["Cousin Feenix’s legs consented to take him away after this; and leaving them alone together, he shut the door.", "이 말 뒤에 피닉스 사촌의 다리가 그를 데리고 나가는 데 동의했다. 그래서 두 사람을 함께 남겨 둔 채, 그는 문을 닫았다."]], [["Edith remained silent for some minutes, with Florence sitting close beside her.", "에디스는 몇 분 동안 말없이 있었고, 플로렌스는 그녀 곁에 바짝 붙어 앉아 있었다."], ["Then she took from her bosom a sealed paper.", "이윽고 그녀는 가슴에서 봉인된 종이 한 장을 꺼냈다."]], [["“I debated with myself a long time,”", "\"오랫동안 나 자신과 따져 보았어,\""], ["she said in a low voice, “whether to write this at all, in case of dying suddenly or by accident, and feeling the want of it upon me.", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다, \"이것을 아예 써야 할지를, 갑작스럽게 혹은 사고로 죽을 경우를 대비해, 그게 없어 곤란해질 것을 느끼면서."], ["I have deliberated, ever since, when and how to destroy it.", "그 뒤로 줄곧, 언제 어떻게 이것을 없앨까 곰곰이 생각해 왔지."], ["Take it, Florence.", "받아라, 플로렌스."], ["The truth is written in it.”", "거기에 진실이 적혀 있어.\""]], [["“Is it for Papa?”", "\"아빠께 드리는 건가요?\""], ["asked Florence.", "플로렌스가 물었다."]], [["“It is for whom you will,”", "\"네가 원하는 누구에게든 주는 거야,\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“It is given to you, and is obtained by you.", "\"그건 너에게 주는 것이고, 너에 의해 얻어진 거야."], ["He never could have had it otherwise.”", "그분은 달리 어떤 방법으로도 결코 그것을 가질 수 없었을 거야.\""]], [["Again they sat silent, in the deepening darkness.", "두 사람은 짙어 가는 어둠 속에서 다시 말없이 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Mama,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["said Florence, “he has lost his fortune; he has been at the point of death; he may not recover, even now.", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"아빠는 재산을 잃었어요. 죽음의 문턱까지 갔었고요."], ["Is there any word that I shall say to him from you?”", "지금도 회복하지 못하실지 몰라요. 어머니가 아빠께 전하라고 할 말이 있을까요?\""]], [["“Did you tell me,”", "\"네가 그분에게 무척 소중하다고,\""], ["asked Edith, “that you were very dear to him?”", "에디스가 물었다, \"내게 말했지?\""]], [["“Yes!”", "\"네!\""], ["said Florence, in a thrilling voice.", "플로렌스가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Tell him I am sorry that we ever met.”", "\"우리가 만났던 것이 유감이라고 그분에게 전해라.\""]], [["“No more?”", "\"그게 다인가요?\""], ["said Florence after a pause.", "플로렌스가 잠시 뒤에 말했다."]], [["“Tell him, if he asks, that I do not repent of what I have done—not yet—for if it were to do again to-morrow, I should do it.", "\"만일 그분이 묻거든, 내가 한 일을 뉘우치지 않는다고—아직은—전해라. 내일 다시 그 일을 해야 한다면, 나는 그렇게 할 테니까."], ["But if he is a changed man—-”", "하지만 만일 그분이 변한 사람이라면—\""]], [["She stopped.", "그녀는 멈췄다."], ["There was something in the silent touch of Florence’s hand that stopped her.", "플로렌스의 손길이 말없이 닿는 데에 그녀를 멈추게 하는 무언가가 있었다."]], [["“—But that being a changed man, he knows, now, it would never be.", "\"—하지만 변한 사람이 되었으니, 그분은 이제 알아, 결코 그렇지는 않으리라는 걸."], ["Tell him I wish it never had been.”", "차라리 그런 일이 아예 없었으면 한다고 내가 바란다고 전해라.\""]], [["“May I say,”", "\"제가 이렇게 말해도 될까요,\""], ["said Florence, “that you grieved to hear of the afflictions he has suffered?”", "플로렌스가 말했다, \"어머니가 아빠가 겪으신 고난을 듣고 슬퍼하셨다고요?\""]], [["“Not,”", "\"안 된다,\""], ["she replied, “if they have taught him that his daughter is very dear to him.", "그녀가 대답했다, \"만일 그 고난들이 그분에게 자기 딸이 무척 소중하다는 것을 가르쳐 주었다면."], ["He will not grieve for them himself, one day, if they have brought that lesson, Florence.”", "만일 그 고난들이 그 교훈을 가져다주었다면, 그분은 언젠가 그것들로 인해 스스로 슬퍼하지 않을 거야, 플로렌스.\""]], [["“You wish well to him, and would have him happy.", "\"어머니는 아빠가 잘되기를 바라고, 아빠가 행복하기를 바라시잖아요."], ["I am sure you would!”", "분명히 그러시잖아요!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["let me be able, if I have the occasion at some future time, to say so?”", "제가 언젠가 훗날 기회가 닿으면, 그렇게 말할 수 있게 해 주세요?\""]], [["Edith sat with her dark eyes gazing steadfastly before her, and did not reply until Florence had repeated her entreaty; when she drew her hand within her arm, and said, with the same thoughtful gaze upon the night outside:", "에디스는 검은 눈으로 앞을 한결같이 응시한 채 앉아, 플로렌스가 그 간청을 되풀이할 때까지 대답하지 않았다. 그러다가 그녀는 자기 팔 안에 플로렌스의 손을 끌어당기며, 바깥의 밤을 향한 그 사색에 잠긴 눈길 그대로, 말했다."]], [["“Tell him that if, in his own present, he can find any reason to compassionate my past, I sent word that I asked him to do so.", "\"그분에게 전해라, 만일 지금 자기 처지에서, 내 과거를 측은히 여길 어떤 까닭을 찾을 수 있다면, 내가 그렇게 해 달라고 청한다는 전갈을 보냈다고."], ["Tell him that if, in his own present, he can find a reason to think less bitterly of me, I asked him to do so.", "그분에게 전해라, 만일 지금 자기 처지에서, 나를 덜 모질게 생각할 어떤 까닭을 찾을 수 있다면, 내가 그렇게 해 달라고 청했다고."], ["Tell him, that, dead as we are to one another, never more to meet on this side of eternity, he knows there is one feeling in common between us now, that there never was before.”", "그분에게 전해라, 우리가 서로에게 죽은 것이나 다름없고, 영원의 이편에서는 다시 만나지 못할지언정, 이제 우리 사이에는 예전에는 결코 없었던 공통된 감정 하나가 있음을 그분이 안다고.\""]], [["Her sternness seemed to yield, and there were tears in her dark eyes.", "그녀의 매서움이 누그러지는 듯했고, 그 검은 눈에는 눈물이 어려 있었다."]], [["“I trust myself to that,”", "\"나는 거기에 나를 맡길 거야,\""], ["she said, “for his better thoughts of me, and mine of him.", "그녀가 말했다, \"그분이 나를 더 좋게 생각하도록, 또 내가 그분을 더 좋게 생각하도록."], ["When he loves his Florence most, he will hate me least.", "그분이 자기 플로렌스를 가장 사랑할 때, 나를 가장 덜 미워할 거야."], ["When he is most proud and happy in her and her children, he will be most repentant of his own part in the dark vision of our married life.", "그분이 그 애와 그 애의 아이들로 가장 자랑스럽고 행복할 때, 우리 결혼 생활의 어두운 환영 속 자기 몫에 대해 가장 뉘우치게 될 거야."], ["At that time, I will be repentant too—let him know it then—and think that when I thought so much of all the causes that had made me what I was, I needed to have allowed more for the causes that had made him what he was.", "그때는 나도 뉘우치고 있을 거야—그때 그분이 그것을 알게 해 다오—그리고 내가 나를 지금의 나로 만든 그 모든 까닭을 그토록 많이 생각했을 때, 그분을 지금의 그분으로 만든 까닭들에 대해서도 더 많이 헤아렸어야 했음을 생각할 거야."], ["I will try, then, to forgive him his share of blame.", "그때는 그분의 잘못의 몫을 용서하려 애쓰겠어."], ["Let him try to forgive me mine!”", "그분도 내 몫을 용서하려 애쓰게 해 다오!\""]], [["“Oh Mama!”", "\"오 어머니!\""], ["said Florence.", "플로렌스가 말했다."], ["“How it lightens my heart, even in such a strange meeting and parting, to hear this!”", "\"이런 기이한 만남과 헤어짐 속에서도, 이 말을 들으니 제 마음이 얼마나 가벼워지는지 몰라요!\""]], [["“Strange words in my own ears,”", "\"내 귀에는 기이한 말이로구나,\""], ["said Edith, “and foreign to the sound of my own voice!", "에디스가 말했다, \"내 목소리의 울림과는 동떨어진 말이야!"], ["But even if I had been the wretched creature I have given him occasion to believe me, I think I could have said them still, hearing that you and he were very dear to one another.", "하지만 내가 그분으로 하여금 나를 그렇게 믿게 만든 그 비참한 인간이었다 하더라도, 너와 그분이 서로에게 무척 소중하다는 걸 들었으니, 나는 여전히 이 말을 할 수 있었으리라 생각한다."], ["Let him, when you are dearest, ever feel that he is most forbearing in his thoughts of me—that I am most forbearing in my thoughts of him!", "네가 가장 소중할 때, 그분이 늘 느끼게 해 다오, 자기가 나를 생각하는 데 가장 너그럽다는 것을—내가 그분을 생각하는 데 가장 너그럽다는 것을!"], ["Those are the last words I send him!", "그것이 내가 그분에게 보내는 마지막 말이다!"], ["Now, goodbye, my life!”", "자, 잘 가거라, 내 생명아!\""]], [["She clasped her in her arms, and seemed to pour out all her woman’s soul of love and tenderness at once.", "그녀는 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안고, 자기 여인으로서의 사랑과 다정함의 영혼을 한꺼번에 모두 쏟아붓는 듯했다."]], [["“This kiss for your child!", "\"이 입맞춤은 네 아이에게!"], ["These kisses for a blessing on your head!", "이 입맞춤들은 네 머리 위에 축복이 되기를!"], ["My own dear Florence, my sweet girl, farewell!”", "내 사랑하는 플로렌스, 내 어여쁜 아이야, 잘 가거라!\""]], [["“To meet again!”", "\"다시 만나요!\""], ["cried Florence.", "플로렌스가 부르짖었다."]], [["“Never again!", "\"다시는!"], ["Never again!", "다시는 안 돼!"], ["When you leave me in this dark room, think that you have left me in the grave.", "이 어두운 방에 나를 두고 떠날 때, 나를 무덤에 두고 떠난다고 생각해라."], ["Remember only that I was once, and that I loved you!”", "다만 내가 한때 존재했다는 것을, 그리고 내가 너를 사랑했다는 것을 기억해 다오!\""]], [["And Florence left her, seeing her face no more, but accompanied by her embraces and caresses to the last.", "그리고 플로렌스는 그녀의 얼굴을 더는 보지 못한 채, 그러나 마지막까지 그녀의 포옹과 어루만짐을 받으며, 그녀를 떠났다."]], [["Cousin Feenix met her at the door, and took her down to Walter in the dingy dining room, upon whose shoulder she laid her head weeping.", "피닉스 사촌이 문간에서 그녀를 맞아, 그 칙칙한 식당으로 그녀를 데리고 내려가 월터에게 데려다주었고, 그녀는 그의 어깨에 머리를 기대고 울었다."]], [["“I am devilish sorry,”", "\"지독히도 안타깝습니다,\""], ["said Cousin Feenix, lifting his wristbands to his eyes in the simplest manner possible, and without the least concealment, “that the lovely and accomplished daughter of my friend Dombey and amiable wife of my friend Gay, should have had her sensitive nature so very much distressed and cut up by the interview which is just concluded.", "피닉스 사촌이, 더없이 꾸밈없는 태도로, 조금도 감추는 기색 없이 소맷부리를 들어 눈으로 가져가며 말했다, \"제 친구 돔비의 사랑스럽고 재능 있는 따님이자 제 친구 게이의 상냥한 아내가, 방금 끝난 그 면담으로 그 예민한 심성을 그토록 심히 상하고 마음 아파하게 되다니요."], ["But I hope and trust I have acted for the best, and that my honourable friend Dombey will find his mind relieved by the disclosures which have taken place.", "하지만 제가 최선을 다해 행동했기를, 그리고 제 명예로운 친구 돔비가 이번에 밝혀진 사실들로 마음의 짐을 덜기를 바라고 또 믿습니다."], ["I exceedingly lament that my friend Dombey should have got himself, in point of fact, into the devil’s own state of conglomeration by an alliance with our family; but am strongly of opinion that if it hadn’t been for the infernal scoundrel Barker—man with white teeth—everything would have gone on pretty smoothly.", "제 친구 돔비가 우리 집안과의 인연으로, 사실인즉슨, 지독히도 엉망진창인 상태에 빠지게 된 것을 저는 몹시 한탄합니다. 하지만 만일 그 흉악한 악당 바커—하얀 이를 가진 사내—만 아니었더라면, 모든 일이 꽤 순조롭게 흘러갔으리라고 굳게 믿습니다."], ["In regard to my relative who does me the honour to have formed an uncommonly good opinion of myself, I can assure the amiable wife of my friend Gay, that she may rely on my being, in point of fact, a father to her.", "저에 대해 보기 드물게 좋은 견해를 품어 주는 영광을 베푼 제 친척에 관해서는, 제 친구 게이의 상냥한 아내에게 단언할 수 있습니다. 그녀가, 사실인즉슨, 제가 그녀의 아버지가 되어 줄 것을 믿어도 좋다고요."], ["And in regard to the changes of human life, and the extraordinary manner in which we are perpetually conducting ourselves, all I can say is, with my friend Shakespeare—man who wasn’t for an age but for all time, and with whom my friend Gay is no doubt acquainted—that its like the shadow of a dream.”", "그리고 인생의 변화들, 또 우리가 끊임없이 자기 자신을 처신하는 그 기이한 방식에 관해서는, 제가 할 수 있는 말이라고는, 제 친구 셰익스피어와 더불어—한 시대를 위해서가 아니라 모든 시대를 위해 살았던 사람으로, 분명 제 친구 게이도 아는 사람입니다만—그것이 한낱 꿈의 그림자 같다는 것뿐입니다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER LXII. Final", "# 제57장"]], [["A bottle that has been long excluded from the light of day, and is hoary with dust and cobwebs, has been brought into the sunshine; and the golden wine within it sheds a lustre on the table.", "오랫동안 햇빛을 보지 못한 채 먼지와 거미줄로 허옇게 뒤덮여 있던 한 병이 양지로 옮겨져 나왔다. 그리고 그 안의 황금빛 포도주는 식탁 위로 광채를 흩뿌린다."]], [["It is the last bottle of the old Madiera.", "그것은 오래된 마데이라의 마지막 한 병이다."]], [["“You are quite right, Mr Gills,”", "\"길스 씨, 당신 말이 옳소,\""], ["says Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말한다."], ["“This is a very rare and most delicious wine.”", "\"이건 매우 진귀하고 더없이 감미로운 포도주요.\""]], [["The Captain, who is of the party, beams with joy.", "자리를 함께한 선장은 기쁨으로 환하게 빛난다."], ["There is a very halo of delight round his glowing forehead.", "그 달아오른 이마 둘레에는 그야말로 희열의 후광이 어려 있다."]], [["“We always promised ourselves, Sir,”", "\"우리는 늘 다짐했었습죠, 나리,\" 길스 씨가 말한다."], ["observes Mr Gills,” Ned and myself, I mean—”", "\"네드와 저, 그러니까 저희 둘이 말입니다요—\""]], [["Mr Dombey nods at the Captain, who shines more and more with speechless gratification.", "돔비 씨가 선장을 향해 고개를 끄덕이고, 선장은 말없는 만족감으로 점점 더 환히 빛난다."]], [["“—that we would drink this, one day or other, to Walter safe at home: though such a home we never thought of.", "\"—언젠가 어느 날엔가 이 술을, 무사히 집에 돌아온 월터를 위해 마시자고요. 비록 이런 집은 생각도 못 했습니다만."], ["If you don’t object to our old whim, Sir, let us devote this first glass to Walter and his wife.”", "나리, 저희의 오랜 변덕을 못마땅히 여기지 않으신다면, 이 첫 잔을 월터와 그의 아내에게 바치도록 하시지요.\""]], [["“To Walter and his wife!”", "\"월터와 그의 아내를 위하여!\""], ["says Mr Dombey.", "돔비 씨가 말한다."], ["“Florence, my child”—and turns to kiss her.", "\"플로렌스, 내 아가야\"—그러고는 몸을 돌려 딸에게 입을 맞춘다."]], [["“To Walter and his wife!”", "\"월터와 그의 아내를 위하여!\""], ["says Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말한다."]], [["“To Wal”r and his wife!”", "\"월터와 그의 아내를 위하여!\""], ["exclaims the Captain.", "선장이 외친다."], ["“Hooroar!”", "\"만세!\""], ["and the Captain exhibiting a strong desire to clink his glass against some other glass, Mr Dombey, with a ready hand, holds out his.", "그러고는 선장이 자기 잔을 다른 잔에 부딪치고 싶어 안달하는 기색을 보이자, 돔비 씨가 선뜻 손을 내밀어 잔을 갖다 댄다. 나머지 사람들도 뒤따른다."], ["The others follow; and there is a blithe and merry ringing, as of a little peal of marriage bells.", "그러자 마치 자그마한 결혼 종소리가 한바탕 울려 퍼지듯, 흥겹고 명랑한 쟁쟁거림이 인다."]], [["Other buried wine grows older, as the old Madeira did in its time; and dust and cobwebs thicken on the bottles.", "땅에 묻힌 다른 포도주들도, 그 옛날 마데이라가 그러했듯, 점점 더 오래되어 간다. 그리고 병마다 먼지와 거미줄이 두텁게 쌓여 간다."]], [["Mr Dombey is a white-haired gentleman, whose face bears heavy marks of care and suffering; but they are traces of a storm that has passed on for ever, and left a clear evening in its track.", "돔비 씨는 백발의 신사다. 그 얼굴에는 근심과 고통의 묵직한 자국이 새겨져 있다. 그러나 그것은 영영 지나가 버려 그 뒤에 맑은 저녁을 남긴 폭풍의 흔적이다."]], [["Ambitious projects trouble him no more.", "야심에 찬 계획들은 더 이상 그를 괴롭히지 않는다."], ["His only pride is in his daughter and her husband.", "그의 유일한 자랑거리는 딸과 사위다."], ["He has a silent, thoughtful, quiet manner, and is always with his daughter.", "그는 말이 적고 사려 깊고 조용한 태도를 지녔으며, 늘 딸과 함께 있다."], ["Miss Tox is not infrequently of the family party, and is quite devoted to it, and a great favourite.", "톡스 양도 심심찮게 이 가족 모임에 끼는데, 거기에 아주 헌신적이며 큰 사랑을 받는 사람이다."], ["Her admiration of her once stately patron is, and has been ever since the morning of her shock in Princess’s Place, platonic, but not weakened in the least.", "한때 위풍당당했던 그 후원자에 대한 그녀의 흠모는, 프린세스 플레이스에서 충격을 받았던 그 아침 이래로 줄곧 플라토닉한 것이지만, 조금도 약해지지 않았다."]], [["Nothing has drifted to him from the wreck of his fortunes, but a certain annual sum that comes he knows not how, with an earnest entreaty that he will not seek to discover, and with the assurance that it is a debt, and an act of reparation.", "그의 재산이 난파된 잔해에서 그에게 흘러들어 온 것은 아무것도 없다. 다만 어떻게 들어오는지 모를 일정한 연간 금액이 있는데, 그것은 출처를 캐려 들지 말아 달라는 간곡한 청과 함께, 그것이 한 빚이자 보상의 행위라는 다짐과 함께 들어온다."], ["He has consulted with his old clerk about this, who is clear it may be honourably accepted, and has no doubt it arises out of some forgotten transaction in the times of the old House.", "그는 이 일을 두고 자기의 옛 점원과 의논해 보았는데, 그 점원은 떳떳이 받아도 좋다고 단언하며, 옛 회사 시절의 잊힌 어떤 거래에서 비롯된 것임이 틀림없다고 했다."]], [["That hazel-eyed bachelor, a bachelor no more, is married now, and to the sister of the grey-haired Junior.", "그 개암빛 눈동자의 독신남은, 이제 더 이상 독신이 아니어서, 지금은 백발의 부(副)점원의 누이와 결혼한 몸이다."], ["He visits his old chief sometimes, but seldom.", "그는 가끔, 그러나 드물게, 옛 상관을 찾아간다."], ["There is a reason in the greyhaired Junior’s history, and yet a stronger reason in his name, why he should keep retired from his old employer; and as he lives with his sister and her husband, they participate in that retirement.", "그가 옛 고용주에게서 물러나 지내야 하는 데에는 백발 부점원의 내력에 한 가지 이유가 있고, 그의 이름에는 더 강한 이유가 있다. 그리고 그가 누이와 매부와 함께 살기에, 그들도 그 은둔 생활을 함께한다."], ["Walter sees them sometimes—Florence too—and the pleasant house resounds with profound duets arranged for the Piano-Forte and Violoncello, and with the labours of Harmonious Blacksmiths.", "월터는 가끔 그들을 보러 가고—플로렌스도 마찬가지인데—그 정겨운 집에는 피아노포르테와 첼로를 위해 편곡된 묵직한 이중주와, '화목한 대장장이들'의 노고가 울려 퍼진다."]], [["And how goes the wooden Midshipman in these changed days?", "그런데 이 달라진 시절에 나무로 만든 해군 사관후보생은 어떻게 지낼까? 그야, 여전히 이 자리에 있으니, 오른발을 앞으로 내민 채 삯마차들을 향해 부지런히 일하면서, 어느 때보다 더 빈틈없는 모습이다."], ["Why, here he still is, right leg foremost, hard at work upon the hackney coaches, and more on the alert than ever, being newly painted from his cocked hat to his buckled shoes; and up above him, in golden characters, these names shine refulgent, GILLS AND CUTTLE.", "삼각모에서 버클 달린 구두까지 새로 칠을 한 까닭이다. 그리고 그 위쪽에는 황금빛 글자로, 길스 앤드 커틀, 이라는 이 이름들이 눈부시게 빛난다."]], [["Not another stroke of business does the Midshipman achieve beyond his usual easy trade.", "사관후보생이 여느 때의 수월한 장사 너머로 무슨 큰 거래를 한 건 아니다."], ["But they do say, in a circuit of some half-mile round the blue umbrella in Leadenhall Market, that some of Mr Gills’s old investments are coming out wonderfully well; and that instead of being behind the time in those respects, as he supposed, he was, in truth, a little before it, and had to wait the fulness of the time and the design.", "그러나 리든홀 시장의 푸른 우산 둘레로 반 마일쯤 되는 일대에서는 이런 말들이 돈다. 길스 씨가 옛날에 해 둔 투자 가운데 몇 가지가 놀라우리만치 잘 풀리고 있다고."], ["The whisper is that Mr Gills’s money has begun to turn itself, and that it is turning itself over and over pretty briskly.", "그리고 그가 그런 면에서 시류에 뒤처졌다고 스스로 여겼던 것과는 달리, 실은 시류보다 조금 앞섰던 것이어서, 때와 계획이 무르익기를 기다려야 했을 뿐이라고."], ["Certain it is that, standing at his shop-door, in his coffee-coloured suit, with his chronometer in his pocket, and his spectacles on his forehead, he don’t appear to break his heart at customers not coming, but looks very jovial and contented, though full as misty as of yore.", "길스 씨의 돈이 스스로 굴러가기 시작했고, 그것도 꽤 활기차게 자꾸자꾸 불어나고 있다는 소문이다. 분명한 것은, 커피빛 양복 차림으로, 크로노미터를 주머니에 넣고, 안경을 이마에 걸친 채 가게 문간에 서 있는 그가, 손님이 오지 않는다고 애를 태우는 기색은 전혀 없이, 예나 지금이나 여전히 안개에 싸인 듯 흐리멍덩하면서도 무척 유쾌하고 만족스러워 보인다는 점이다."]], [["As to his partner, Captain Cuttle, there is a fiction of a business in the Captain’s mind which is better than any reality.", "그의 동업자 커틀 선장으로 말하자면, 선장의 마음속에는 어떤 현실보다도 나은, 장사라는 한 편의 허구가 자리 잡고 있다."], ["The Captain is as satisfied of the Midshipman’s importance to the commerce and navigation of the country, as he could possibly be, if no ship left the Port of London without the Midshipman’s assistance.", "선장은 마치 사관후보생의 도움 없이는 어떤 배도 런던 항을 떠나지 못하기라도 하는 양, 이 나라의 통상과 항해에 사관후보생이 얼마나 중요한지를 더없이 확신하고 있다. 문 위에 걸린 자기 이름을 향한 그의 기쁨은 다함이 없다."], ["His delight in his own name over the door, is inexhaustible.", "그는 하루에도 스무 번씩 길을 건너가 맞은편에서 그것을 바라본다."], ["He crosses the street, twenty times a day, to look at it from the other side of the way; and invariably says, on these occasions, “Ed’ard Cuttle, my lad, if your mother could ha’ know’d as you would ever be a man o’ science, the good old creetur would ha’ been took aback in-deed!”", "그리고 그럴 때마다 어김없이 이렇게 말한다. \"에드워드 커틀, 이 사람아, 자네 어머니가 자네가 언젠가 학문하는 사람이 될 줄 아셨더라면, 그 착한 노인네가 정말이지 깜짝 놀라 자빠지셨을 게야!\""]], [["But here is Mr Toots descending on the Midshipman with violent rapidity, and Mr Toots’s face is very red as he bursts into the little parlour.", "그런데 여기 투츠 씨가 무서운 속도로 사관후보생을 향해 들이닥치고 있고, 자그마한 응접실로 뛰어들어 오는 투츠 씨의 얼굴은 몹시 벌겋다."]], [["“Captain Gills,”", "\"길스 선장님,\""], ["says Mr Toots, “and Mr Sols, I am happy to inform you that Mrs Toots has had an increase to her family.”", "투츠 씨가 말한다. \"그리고 솔스 씨, 투츠 부인이 식구를 하나 더 늘렸다는 소식을 기쁘게 알려 드립니다.\""]], [["“And it does her credit!”", "\"그거 그 사람 면목이 서는 일이로군!\""], ["cries the Captain.", "선장이 외친다."]], [["“I give you joy, Mr Toots!”", "\"축하드립니다, 투츠 씨!\""], ["says old Sol.", "늙은 솔이 말한다."]], [["“Thank’ee,”", "\"고맙습니다,\""], ["chuckles Mr Toots, “I’m very much obliged to you.", "투츠 씨가 흐뭇하게 웃는다. \"정말 감사합니다."], ["I knew that you’d be glad to hear, and so I came down myself.", "두 분이 들으시면 기뻐하실 줄 알고, 그래서 제가 직접 내려온 겁니다."], ["We’re positively getting on, you know.", "우리가 정말이지 자꾸 늘어나고 있어요, 아시겠지만."], ["There’s Florence, and Susan, and now here’s another little stranger.”", "플로렌스가 있고, 수전이 있고, 이제 여기 또 자그마한 새 식구가 하나 생겼지요.\""]], [["“A female stranger?”", "\"여자 식구입니까?\""], ["inquires the Captain.", "선장이 묻는다."]], [["“Yes, Captain Gills,”", "\"네, 길스 선장님,\""], ["says Mr Toots, “and I’m glad of it.", "투츠 씨가 말한다. \"그래서 저는 기쁩니다."], ["The oftener we can repeat that most extraordinary woman, my opinion is, the better!”", "그 더없이 비범한 여인을 더 자주 되풀이해 낼 수 있을수록, 제 생각엔, 더 좋은 일이지요!\""]], [["“Stand by!”", "\"준비!\""], ["says the Captain, turning to the old case-bottle with no throat—for it is evening, and the Midshipman’s usual moderate provision of pipes and glasses is on the board.", "선장이 목이 없는 그 오래된 손잡이 술병 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말한다—저녁이라, 사관후보생 집에서 늘 적당히 차려 두는 파이프와 술잔들이 식탁 위에 놓여 있는 까닭이다."], ["“Here’s to her, and may she have ever so many more!”", "\"그 사람을 위하여, 부디 그 사람이 앞으로도 식구를 잔뜩 더 늘리기를!\""]], [["“Thank’ee, Captain Gills,”", "\"고맙습니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["says the delighted Mr Toots.", "기뻐하는 투츠 씨가 말한다."], ["“I echo the sentiment.", "\"저도 그 뜻에 한목소리를 내겠습니다."], ["If you’ll allow me, as my so doing cannot be unpleasant to anybody, under the circumstances, I think I’ll take a pipe.”", "허락해 주신다면, 이런 상황에서 제가 그렇게 하는 것이 누구에게도 거슬리지 않을 테니, 파이프를 하나 피워 볼까 합니다.\""]], [["Mr Toots begins to smoke, accordingly, and in the openness of his heart is very loquacious.", "그리하여 투츠 씨가 담배를 피우기 시작하고, 마음을 활짝 연 채 무척 수다스러워진다."]], [["“Of all the remarkable instances that that delightful woman has given of her excellent sense, Captain Gills and Mr Sols,”", "\"그 사랑스러운 여인이 자신의 빼어난 분별력을 보여 준 온갖 놀라운 사례들 가운데서도, 길스 선장님과 솔스 씨,\""], ["said Mr Toots, “I think none is more remarkable than the perfection with which she has understood my devotion to Miss Dombey.”", "투츠 씨가 말했다. \"돔비 양에 대한 제 헌신을 그토록 완벽하게 이해해 준 것보다 더 놀라운 일은 없다고 생각합니다.\""]], [["Both his auditors assent.", "그의 두 청중이 모두 동의한다."]], [["“Because you know,”", "\"왜냐하면 아시겠지만,\""], ["says Mr Toots, “I have never changed my sentiments towards Miss Dombey.", "투츠 씨가 말한다. \"저는 돔비 양에 대한 제 마음을 한 번도 바꾼 적이 없거든요."], ["They are the same as ever.", "예나 지금이나 똑같습니다."], ["She is the same bright vision to me, at present, that she was before I made Walters’s acquaintance.", "그분은 지금도 제게, 제가 월터스와 알고 지내기 전에 그랬던 것과 똑같이 변함없는 눈부신 환영입니다."], ["When Mrs Toots and myself first began to talk of—in short, of the tender passion, you know, Captain Gills.”", "투츠 부인과 제가 처음으로 그—한마디로, 그 애틋한 연정에 대해 이야기하기 시작했을 때 말입니다, 길스 선장님.\""]], [["“Ay, ay, my lad,”", "\"그럼, 그럼, 이 사람아,\""], ["says the Captain, “as makes us all slue round—for which you’ll overhaul the book—”", "선장이 말한다. \"우리 모두를 빙 돌게 만드는 그것 말이지—그건 자네가 그 책을 펼쳐 봐야 할 텐데—\""]], [["“I shall certainly do so, Captain Gills,”", "\"꼭 그러겠습니다, 길스 선장님,\""], ["says Mr Toots, with great earnestness; “when we first began to mention such subjects, I explained that I was what you may call a Blighted Flower, you know.”", "투츠 씨가 자못 진지하게 말한다. \"우리가 처음 그런 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했을 때, 저는 제가, 말하자면 시들어 버린 꽃이라는 걸 설명했지요, 아시겠지만.\""]], [["The Captain approves of this figure greatly; and murmurs that no flower as blows, is like the rose.", "선장은 이 비유를 크게 마음에 들어 하며, 활짝 피는 어떤 꽃도 장미만은 못하다고 중얼거린다."]], [["“But Lord bless me,”", "\"그런데 세상에나,\""], ["pursues Mr Toots, “she was as entirely conscious of the state of my feelings as I was myself.", "투츠 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"그 사람은 제 감정 상태를 저 자신만큼이나 훤히 알고 있었어요."], ["There was nothing I could tell her.", "제가 그 사람에게 따로 일러 줄 게 하나도 없었지요."], ["She was the only person who could have stood between me and the silent Tomb, and she did it, in a manner to command my everlasting admiration.", "그 사람은 저와 말 없는 무덤 사이를 가로막아 줄 수 있던 단 한 사람이었고, 실제로 그렇게 해 주었는데, 그 방식이 제 영원한 흠모를 자아낼 만한 것이었습니다."], ["She knows that there’s nobody in the world I look up to, as I do to Miss Dombey.", "그 사람은 이 세상에서 제가 돔비 양만큼 우러러보는 사람이 아무도 없다는 걸 압니다."], ["Knows that there’s nothing on earth I wouldn’t do for Miss Dombey.", "이 세상에서 제가 돔비 양을 위해서라면 못 할 일이 하나도 없다는 걸 압니다."], ["She knows that I consider Miss Dombey the most beautiful, the most amiable, the most angelic of her sex.", "제가 돔비 양을 그 성(性) 가운데 가장 아름답고, 가장 사랑스럽고, 가장 천사 같은 분으로 여긴다는 걸 압니다."], ["What is her observation upon that?", "그에 대한 그 사람의 소견이 뭔지 아십니까?"], ["The perfection of sense.", "분별력의 극치지요."], ["‘My dear, you’re right.", "'여보, 당신 말이 옳아요."], ["I think so too.’”", "나도 그렇게 생각해요.'\""]], [["“And so do I!”", "\"나도 그렇소!\""], ["says the Captain.", "선장이 말한다."]], [["“So do I,”", "\"나도 그렇소,\""], ["says Sol Gills.", "솔 길스가 말한다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그러고는,\""], ["resumes Mr Toots, after some contemplative pulling at his pipe, during which his visage has expressed the most contented reflection, “what an observant woman my wife is!", "투츠 씨가 파이프를 사색에 잠긴 듯 몇 번 빨고 나서 다시 말을 잇는데, 그동안 그의 얼굴에는 더없이 흡족한 상념이 어려 있었다. \"제 아내가 얼마나 관찰력이 뛰어난 여인인지요!"], ["What sagacity she possesses!", "얼마나 명민한지요!"], ["What remarks she makes!", "어떤 소견들을 내놓는지요!"], ["It was only last night, when we were sitting in the enjoyment of connubial bliss—which, upon my word and honour, is a feeble term to express my feelings in the society of my wife—that she said how remarkable it was to consider the present position of our friend Walters.", "바로 어젯밤이었어요, 우리가 부부의 행복을 누리며 앉아 있을 때—솔직히 맹세코, '행복'이라는 말로는 제 아내와 함께 있을 때의 제 감정을 표현하기엔 한참 모자랍니다만—그 사람이 우리 벗 월터스의 지금 처지를 생각하면 얼마나 놀라운 일이냐고 하더군요. '여기,'"], ["‘Here,’ observes my wife, ‘he is, released from sea-going, after that first long voyage with his young bride’—as you know he was, Mr Sols.”", "제 아내가 그러는데, '이이가 그 어린 신부와 함께한 첫 긴 항해를 마치고 바닷일에서 풀려나 있잖아요'—아시다시피 그랬지요, 솔스 씨.\""]], [["“Quite true,”", "\"맞고말고요,\""], ["says the old Instrument-maker, rubbing his hands.", "늙은 기계 제작자가 두 손을 비비며 말한다."]], [["“‘Here he is,’ says my wife, ‘released from that, immediately; appointed by the same establishment to a post of great trust and confidence at home; showing himself again worthy; mounting up the ladder with the greatest expedition; beloved by everybody; assisted by his uncle at the very best possible time of his fortunes’—which I think is the case, Mr Sols?", "\"'여기 이이가,' 제 아내가 그러기를, '그 일에서 곧바로 풀려나서, 같은 회사로부터 집에서 막중한 신임을 받는 자리에 임명되었고, 다시 한번 그럴 만한 사람임을 보여 주면서, 더없이 빠르게 사다리를 올라가고, 모두에게 사랑받고, 자기 운수가 가장 좋은 바로 그때에 삼촌의 도움을 받고 있잖아요'—제 생각에도 그렇지요, 솔스 씨?"], ["My wife is always correct.”", "제 아내는 늘 정확하거든요.\""]], [["“Why yes, yes—some of our lost ships, freighted with gold, have come home, truly,”", "\"아, 그렇지요, 그래요—황금을 실은 우리의 잃어버린 배 가운데 몇 척이 정말로 돌아왔답니다,\""], ["returns old Sol, laughing.", "늙은 솔이 웃으며 대답한다."], ["“Small craft, Mr Toots, but serviceable to my boy!”", "\"작은 배들이지요, 투츠 씨, 그래도 우리 아이한테는 쓸모가 있었어요!\""]], [["“Exactly so,”", "\"바로 그겁니다,\""], ["says Mr Toots.", "투츠 씨가 말한다."], ["“You’ll never find my wife wrong.", "\"제 아내가 틀린 걸 결코 못 보실 겁니다."], ["‘Here he is,’ says that most remarkable woman, ‘so situated,—and what follows?", "'여기 이이가,' 그 더없이 비범한 여인이 그러기를, '이런 처지에 놓여 있는데—그래서 뭐가 따라올까요?"], ["What follows?’ observed Mrs Toots.", "뭐가 따라올까요?' 하고 투츠 부인이 짚었지요."], ["Now pray remark, Captain Gills, and Mr Sols, the depth of my wife’s penetration.", "자, 길스 선장님, 그리고 솔스 씨, 제 아내의 통찰력이 얼마나 깊은지 잘 봐 주십시오. '바로 이거예요."], ["‘Why that, under the very eye of Mr Dombey, there is a foundation going on, upon which a—an Edifice;’ that was Mrs Toots’s word,”", "돔비 씨의 바로 그 눈앞에서, 한 토대가 닦이고 있는데, 그 위에 한 채의—한 채의 건축물이'—이게 투츠 부인이 쓴 말입니다,\" 투츠 씨가 의기양양하게 말한다."], ["says Mr Toots exultingly, ‘“is gradually rising, perhaps to equal, perhaps excel, that of which he was once the head, and the small beginnings of which (a common fault, but a bad one, Mrs Toots said) escaped his memory.", "\"'서서히 솟아오르고 있어서, 어쩌면 그이가 한때 우두머리였던 그 회사에 맞먹거나, 어쩌면 능가할지도 모르는데, 그 회사의 자그마한 시작은 (흔한 잘못이지만 나쁜 잘못이라고 투츠 부인이 그러더군요) 그이의 기억에서 빠져나가 버렸지요. 그리하여,'"], ["Thus,’ said my wife, ‘from his daughter, after all, another Dombey and Son will ascend’—no ‘rise;’ that was Mrs Toots’s word—‘triumphant!’”", "제 아내가 그러기를, '결국 그이의 딸로부터, 또 하나의 돔비 앤드 선이 떠오를'—아니 '솟아오를'이었네요. 그게 투츠 부인이 쓴 말입니다—'의기양양하게!'\""]], [["Mr Toots, with the assistance of his pipe—which he is extremely glad to devote to oratorical purposes, as its proper use affects him with a very uncomfortable sensation—does such grand justice to this prophetic sentence of his wife’s, that the Captain, throwing away his glazed hat in a state of the greatest excitement, cries:", "투츠 씨는 파이프의 도움을 받아—그것을 웅변의 목적에 쓰게 되어 무척 다행스러워하는데, 본래 용도대로 쓰면 매우 불편한 느낌이 드는 까닭이다—아내의 이 예언적인 문장을 어찌나 거창하게 살려 내는지, 선장은 잔뜩 흥분한 나머지 번들거리는 모자를 내던지며 외친다."]], [["“Sol Gills, you man of science and my ould pardner, what did I tell Wal”r to overhaul on that there night when he first took to business?", "\"솔 길스, 학문하는 사람이자 내 오랜 동무여, 월터가 처음 장사에 발을 들이던 그날 밤, 내가 그 친구더러 펼쳐 보라고 한 게 뭐였더라?"], ["Was it this here quotation, ‘Turn again Whittington, Lord Mayor of London, and when you are old you will never depart from it.’ Was it them words, Sol Gills?”", "바로 이 인용구 아니었나, '다시 돌아오라 휘팅턴, 런던 시장이여, 그리고 늙거든 그것에서 결코 떠나지 말지어다.' 그 말 아니었나, 솔 길스?\""]], [["“It certainly was, Ned,”", "\"분명 그랬지, 네드,\""], ["replied the old Instrument-maker.", "늙은 기계 제작자가 대답한다."], ["“I remember well.”", "\"잘 기억하고 있다네.\""]], [["“Then I tell you what,”", "\"그럼 내 한마디 함세,\""], ["says the Captain, leaning back in his chair, and composing his chest for a prodigious roar.", "선장이 의자에 등을 기대고는 어마어마한 고함을 내지를 채비로 가슴을 가다듬으며 말한다."], ["“I’ll give you Lovely Peg right through; and stand by, both on you, for the chorus!”", "\"내 자네들한테 '어여쁜 페그'를 처음부터 끝까지 불러 줄 테니, 두 사람 다 후렴 부를 준비를 단단히 하게나!\""]], [["Buried wine grows older, as the old Madeira did, in its time; and dust and cobwebs thicken on the bottles.", "땅에 묻힌 포도주는, 그 옛날 마데이라가 그러했듯, 점점 더 오래되어 간다. 그리고 병마다 먼지와 거미줄이 두텁게 쌓여 간다."]], [["Autumn days are shining, and on the sea-beach there are often a young lady, and a white-haired gentleman.", "가을날이 환히 빛나고, 바닷가 모래밭에는 종종 한 젊은 여인과 백발의 신사가 있다."], ["With them, or near them, are two children: boy and girl.", "그들과 함께, 혹은 그 가까이에 두 아이가 있으니, 사내아이와 계집아이다."], ["And an old dog is generally in their company.", "그리고 대개는 늙은 개 한 마리가 그들과 동무한다."]], [["The white-haired gentleman walks with the little boy, talks with him, helps him in his play, attends upon him, watches him as if he were the object of his life.", "백발의 신사는 그 어린 사내아이와 함께 걷고, 아이와 이야기를 나누고, 아이의 놀이를 거들고, 아이의 시중을 들고, 마치 아이가 자기 삶의 목적이기라도 한 양 아이를 지켜본다."], ["If he be thoughtful, the white-haired gentleman is thoughtful too; and sometimes when the child is sitting by his side, and looks up in his face, asking him questions, he takes the tiny hand in his, and holding it, forgets to answer.", "아이가 생각에 잠기면 백발의 신사도 생각에 잠긴다. 그리고 이따금 아이가 그의 곁에 앉아 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 이것저것 물을 때면, 그는 그 자그마한 손을 자기 손에 쥐고는, 그것을 잡은 채 대답하기를 잊는다."], ["Then the child says:", "그러면 아이가 말한다."]], [["“What, grandpa!", "\"왜 그러세요, 할아버지!"], ["Am I so like my poor little Uncle again?”", "제가 또 그 불쌍한 어린 외삼촌을 그렇게나 닮았나요?\""]], [["“Yes, Paul.", "\"그래, 폴."], ["But he was weak, and you are very strong.”", "하지만 그 아인 약했고, 너는 아주 튼튼하단다.\""]], [["“Oh yes, I am very strong.”", "\"네, 맞아요, 저는 아주 튼튼해요.\""]], [["“And he lay on a little bed beside the sea, and you can run about.”", "\"그리고 그 아인 바닷가 자그마한 침대에 누워 있었지만, 너는 뛰어다닐 수 있지.\""]], [["And so they range away again, busily, for the white-haired gentleman likes best to see the child free and stirring; and as they go about together, the story of the bond between them goes about, and follows them.", "그러고 나면 그들은 다시 부지런히 이리저리 거닐어 가는데, 백발의 신사는 아이가 자유롭게 뛰노는 모습을 보는 걸 가장 좋아하는 까닭이다. 그리고 그들이 함께 돌아다니는 동안, 그 둘 사이의 인연에 얽힌 이야기가 떠돌며 그들을 따라다닌다."]], [["But no one, except Florence, knows the measure of the white-haired gentleman’s affection for the girl.", "그러나 플로렌스 말고는 아무도, 그 계집아이를 향한 백발 신사의 애정이 얼마나 깊은지 알지 못한다."], ["That story never goes about.", "그 이야기만은 결코 떠돌지 않는다."], ["The child herself almost wonders at a certain secrecy he keeps in it.", "아이 본인조차도 그가 그 일에 대해 지키는 어떤 은밀함을 거의 의아해할 정도다."], ["He hoards her in his heart.", "그는 아이를 제 가슴속에 고이 간직한다."], ["He cannot bear to see a cloud upon her face.", "그는 아이의 얼굴에 그늘이 지는 것을 차마 보지 못한다."], ["He cannot bear to see her sit apart.", "그는 아이가 외따로 떨어져 앉아 있는 것을 차마 보지 못한다."], ["He fancies that she feels a slight, when there is none.", "그는 아이가 푸대접을 받는다고, 그런 일이 없는데도 공연히 짐작한다."], ["He steals away to look at her, in her sleep.", "그는 잠든 아이를 보려고 살그머니 다가간다."], ["It pleases him to have her come, and wake him in the morning.", "아침에 아이가 와서 자기를 깨워 주는 것을 그는 기뻐한다."], ["He is fondest of her and most loving to her, when there is no creature by.", "그는 곁에 아무도 없을 때, 아이를 가장 애틋이 여기고 가장 다정히 대한다."], ["The child says then, sometimes:", "그럴 때면 아이가 이따금 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“Dear grandpapa, why do you cry when you kiss me?”", "\"할아버지, 왜 저한테 입맞추실 때 우세요?\""]], [["He only answers, “Little Florence!", "그는 그저 \"어린 플로렌스! 어린 플로렌스!\""], ["little Florence!” and smooths away the curls that shade her earnest eyes.", "하고 대답할 뿐, 아이의 진지한 두 눈에 드리운 곱슬머리를 쓸어 넘겨 줄 뿐이다."]], [["The voices in the waves speak low to him of Florence, day and night—plainest when he, his blooming daughter, and her husband, beside them in the evening, or sit at an open window, listening to their roar.", "물결 속의 목소리들은 밤낮으로 그에게 플로렌스에 대해 나직이 속삭인다—저녁이면 그와, 활짝 핀 그의 딸과, 그녀의 남편이 그 물결 곁에 있을 때, 혹은 열린 창가에 앉아 그 노호를 귀 기울여 들을 때 가장 또렷하다."], ["They speak to him of Florence and his altered heart; of Florence and their ceaseless murmuring to her of the love, eternal and illimitable, extending still, beyond the sea, beyond the sky, to the invisible country far away.", "그것들은 그에게 플로렌스에 대해, 그리고 달라진 그의 마음에 대해 말한다. 플로렌스에 대해, 그리고 영원하고 가없는 사랑을, 바다 너머로, 하늘 너머로, 저 멀리 보이지 않는 나라에까지 여전히 뻗어 가는 그 사랑을 그녀에게 끊임없이 속삭이는 일에 대해 말한다."]], [["Never from the mighty sea may voices rise too late, to come between us and the unseen region on the other shore!", "저 거대한 바다로부터 솟아오르는 목소리들이, 너무 늦지 않게 우리와 저편 기슭의 보이지 않는 나라 사이에 깃들기를!"], ["Better, far better, that they whispered of that region in our childish ears, and the swift river hurried us away!", "차라리, 훨씬 더 차라리, 그 목소리들이 우리 어린 시절의 귓가에 그 나라에 대해 속삭여 주고, 그 빠른 강물이 우리를 서둘러 데려가 주기를!"]], [["I cannot forego my usual opportunity of saying farewell to my readers in this greeting-place, though I have only to acknowledge the unbounded warmth and earnestness of their sympathy in every stage of the journey we have just concluded.", "1848년 서문\n이 인사의 자리에서 독자들에게 작별을 고할 늘 누리던 기회를 나는 포기할 수가 없다. 비록 우리가 방금 마친 이 여정의 매 단계마다 그들이 보여 준 그지없이 따뜻하고 진지한 공감을 그저 감사히 받들 수 있을 따름이지만 말이다."]], [["If any of them have felt a sorrow in one of the principal incidents on which this fiction turns, I hope it may be a sorrow of that sort which endears the sharers in it, one to another.", "만일 그들 가운데 누군가가 이 소설의 굴대를 이루는 주요 사건 가운데 하나에서 슬픔을 느꼈다면, 그것이 그 슬픔을 함께 나눈 이들을 서로에게 더욱 정답게 묶어 주는 그런 부류의 슬픔이기를 나는 바란다."], ["This is not unselfish in me.", "이것은 나로서 사심 없는 마음만은 아니다."], ["I may claim to have felt it, at least as much as anybody else; and I would fain be remembered kindly for my part in the experience.", "적어도 그 누구 못지않게 나도 그 슬픔을 느꼈노라 주장할 수 있겠으며, 그 체험에서 내가 맡은 몫으로 하여 정겹게 기억되기를 진정 바라기 때문이다."]], [["DEVONSHIRE TERRACE, Twenty-Fourth March, 1848.", "데번셔 테라스, 1848년 3월 24일. 1867년 서문"]], [["I make so bold as to believe that the faculty (or the habit) of correctly observing the characters of men, is a rare one.", "나는 사람들의 성격을 올바르게 관찰하는 능력(혹은 습관)이 드문 것이라고 감히 믿는 바이다."], ["I have not even found, within my experience, that the faculty (or the habit) of correctly observing so much as the faces of men, is a general one by any means.", "나는 내 경험 안에서, 사람들의 얼굴을 올바르게 관찰하는 능력(혹은 습관)조차도 결코 흔한 것이 아님을 알게 되었다."], ["The two commonest mistakes in judgement that I suppose to arise from the former default, are, the confounding of shyness with arrogance—a very common mistake indeed—and the not understanding that an obstinate nature exists in a perpetual struggle with itself.", "앞엣것의 결함에서 비롯한다고 내가 짐작하는, 판단상의 가장 흔한 두 가지 착오는, 수줍음을 오만으로 혼동하는 것—이건 실로 매우 흔한 착오다—그리고 완고한 천성이란 끊임없이 자기 자신과 싸움을 벌이며 존재한다는 것을 이해하지 못하는 것이다."]], [["Mr Dombey undergoes no violent change, either in this book, or in real life.", "돔비 씨는 이 책에서도, 또 실제 삶에서도, 격렬한 변화를 겪지 않는다."], ["A sense of his injustice is within him, all along.", "자신의 부당함에 대한 자각이 그의 내면에 줄곧 자리하고 있다."], ["The more he represses it, the more unjust he necessarily is.", "그가 그것을 억누르면 억누를수록, 그는 그만큼 더 부당해질 수밖에 없다."], ["Internal shame and external circumstances may bring the contest to a close in a week, or a day; but, it has been a contest for years, and is only fought out after a long balance of victory.", "내면의 수치심과 외부의 상황이 그 싸움을 한 주, 혹은 하루 만에 끝장낼 수도 있겠으나, 그것은 여러 해에 걸친 싸움이었으며, 승부가 오래도록 팽팽히 저울질된 끝에야 비로소 끝까지 가려진다."]], [["I began this book by the Lake of Geneva, and went on with it for some months in France, before pursuing it in England.", "나는 이 책을 제네바 호숫가에서 쓰기 시작했고, 영국에서 이어 쓰기에 앞서 몇 달 동안 프랑스에서 써 나갔다."], ["The association between the writing and the place of writing is so curiously strong in my mind, that at this day, although I know, in my fancy, every stair in the little midshipman’s house, and could swear to every pew in the church in which Florence was married, or to every young gentleman’s bedstead in Doctor Blimber’s establishment, I yet confusedly imagine Captain Cuttle as secluding himself from Mrs MacStinger among the mountains of Switzerland.", "글쓰기와 그 글을 쓴 장소 사이의 연상이 내 마음속에 어찌나 묘하게 강한지, 오늘날까지도 나는, 비록 내 머릿속으로 그 자그마한 사관후보생 집의 계단을 하나하나 다 알고, 플로렌스가 혼인한 그 교회의 신도석 하나하나, 혹은 블림버 박사의 학교에 있던 젊은 신사들의 침대 하나하나를 두고 단언할 수 있으면서도, 여전히 어렴풋이 커틀 선장이 맥스팅거 부인을 피해 스위스의 산속에 틀어박혀 있는 모습을 떠올린다."], ["Similarly, when I am reminded by any chance of what it was that the waves were always saying, my remembrance wanders for a whole winter night about the streets of Paris—as I restlessly did with a heavy heart, on the night when I had written the chapter in which my little friend and I parted company.", "마찬가지로, 우연한 계기로 물결이 늘 무어라 말하던 것이 떠오를 때면, 내 기억은 겨우내 파리의 거리를 헤매고 다닌다—내 어린 벗과 내가 서로 헤어지는 그 장(章)을 다 쓰던 날 밤, 내가 무거운 마음으로 안절부절못하며 헤매고 다녔던 것처럼 말이다."]]];
